if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-766"]="Whether I shall turn out to be the hero of my own life, or whether that station will be held by anybody else, these pages must show. To begin my life with the beginning of my life, I record that I was born (as I have been informed and believe) on a Friday, at twelve o’clock at night. It was remarked that the clock began to strike, and I began to cry, simultaneously.\n\nIn consideration of the day and hour of my birth, it was declared by the nurse, and by some sage women in the neighbourhood who had taken a lively interest in me several months before there was any possibility of our becoming personally acquainted, first, that I was destined to be unlucky in life; and secondly, that I was privileged to see ghosts and spirits; both these gifts inevitably attaching, as they believed, to all unlucky infants of either gender, born towards the small hours on a Friday night.\n\nI need say nothing here, on the first head, because nothing can show better than my history whether that prediction was verified or falsified by the result. On the second branch of the question, I will only remark, that unless I ran through that part of my inheritance while I was still a baby, I have not come into it yet. But I do not at all complain of having been kept out of this property; and if anybody else should be in the present enjoyment of it, he is heartily welcome to keep it.\n\nI was born with a caul, which was advertised for sale, in the newspapers, at the low price of fifteen guineas. Whether sea-going people were short of money about that time, or were short of faith and preferred cork jackets, I don’t know; all I know is, that there was but one solitary bidding, and that was from an attorney connected with the bill-broking business, who offered two pounds in cash, and the balance in sherry, but declined to be guaranteed from drowning on any higher bargain. Consequently the advertisement was withdrawn at a dead loss--for as to sherry, my poor dear mother’s own sherry was in the market then--and ten years afterwards, the caul was put up in a raffle down in our part of the country, to fifty members at half-a-crown a head, the winner to spend five shillings. I was present myself, and I remember to have felt quite uncomfortable and confused, at a part of myself being disposed of in that way. The caul was won, I recollect, by an old lady with a hand-basket, who, very reluctantly, produced from it the stipulated five shillings, all in halfpence, and twopence halfpenny short--as it took an immense time and a great waste of arithmetic, to endeavour without any effect to prove to her. It is a fact which will be long remembered as remarkable down there, that she was never drowned, but died triumphantly in bed, at ninety-two. I have understood that it was, to the last, her proudest boast, that she never had been on the water in her life, except upon a bridge; and that over her tea (to which she was extremely partial) she, to the last, expressed her indignation at the impiety of mariners and others, who had the presumption to go ‘meandering’ about the world. It was in vain to represent to her that some conveniences, tea perhaps included, resulted from this objectionable practice. She always returned, with greater emphasis and with an instinctive knowledge of the strength of her objection, ‘Let us have no meandering.’\n\nNot to meander myself, at present, I will go back to my birth.\n\nI was born at Blunderstone, in Suffolk, or ‘there by’, as they say in Scotland. I was a posthumous child. My father’s eyes had closed upon the light of this world six months, when mine opened on it. There is something strange to me, even now, in the reflection that he never saw me; and something stranger yet in the shadowy remembrance that I have of my first childish associations with his white grave-stone in the churchyard, and of the indefinable compassion I used to feel for it lying out alone there in the dark night, when our little parlour was warm and bright with fire and candle, and the doors of our house were--almost cruelly, it seemed to me sometimes--bolted and locked against it.\n\nAn aunt of my father’s, and consequently a great-aunt of mine, of whom I shall have more to relate by and by, was the principal magnate of our family. Miss Trotwood, or Miss Betsey, as my poor mother always called her, when she sufficiently overcame her dread of this formidable personage to mention her at all (which was seldom), had been married to a husband younger than herself, who was very handsome, except in the sense of the homely adage, ‘handsome is, that handsome does’--for he was strongly suspected of having beaten Miss Betsey, and even of having once, on a disputed question of supplies, made some hasty but determined arrangements to throw her out of a two pair of stairs’ window. These evidences of an incompatibility of temper induced Miss Betsey to pay him off, and effect a separation by mutual consent. He went to India with his capital, and there, according to a wild legend in our family, he was once seen riding on an elephant, in company with a Baboon; but I think it must have been a Baboo--or a Begum. Anyhow, from India tidings of his death reached home, within ten years. How they affected my aunt, nobody knew; for immediately upon the separation, she took her maiden name again, bought a cottage in a hamlet on the sea-coast a long way off, established herself there as a single woman with one servant, and was understood to live secluded, ever afterwards, in an inflexible retirement.\n\nMy father had once been a favourite of hers, I believe; but she was mortally affronted by his marriage, on the ground that my mother was ‘a wax doll’. She had never seen my mother, but she knew her to be not yet twenty. My father and Miss Betsey never met again. He was double my mother’s age when he married, and of but a delicate constitution. He died a year afterwards, and, as I have said, six months before I came into the world.\n\nThis was the state of matters, on the afternoon of, what I may be excused for calling, that eventful and important Friday. I can make no claim therefore to have known, at that time, how matters stood; or to have any remembrance, founded on the evidence of my own senses, of what follows.\n\nMy mother was sitting by the fire, but poorly in health, and very low in spirits, looking at it through her tears, and desponding heavily about herself and the fatherless little stranger, who was already welcomed by some grosses of prophetic pins, in a drawer upstairs, to a world not at all excited on the subject of his arrival; my mother, I say, was sitting by the fire, that bright, windy March afternoon, very timid and sad, and very doubtful of ever coming alive out of the trial that was before her, when, lifting her eyes as she dried them, to the window opposite, she saw a strange lady coming up the garden.\n\nMy mother had a sure foreboding at the second glance, that it was Miss Betsey. The setting sun was glowing on the strange lady, over the garden-fence, and she came walking up to the door with a fell rigidity of figure and composure of countenance that could have belonged to nobody else.\n\nWhen she reached the house, she gave another proof of her identity. My father had often hinted that she seldom conducted herself like any ordinary Christian; and now, instead of ringing the bell, she came and looked in at that identical window, pressing the end of her nose against the glass to that extent, that my poor dear mother used to say it became perfectly flat and white in a moment.\n\nShe gave my mother such a turn, that I have always been convinced I am indebted to Miss Betsey for having been born on a Friday.\n\nMy mother had left her chair in her agitation, and gone behind it in the corner. Miss Betsey, looking round the room, slowly and inquiringly, began on the other side, and carried her eyes on, like a Saracen’s Head in a Dutch clock, until they reached my mother. Then she made a frown and a gesture to my mother, like one who was accustomed to be obeyed, to come and open the door. My mother went.\n\n‘Mrs. David Copperfield, I think,’ said Miss Betsey; the emphasis referring, perhaps, to my mother’s mourning weeds, and her condition.\n\n‘Yes,’ said my mother, faintly.\n\n‘Miss Trotwood,’ said the visitor. ‘You have heard of her, I dare say?’\n\nMy mother answered she had had that pleasure. And she had a disagreeable consciousness of not appearing to imply that it had been an overpowering pleasure.\n\n‘Now you see her,’ said Miss Betsey. My mother bent her head, and begged her to walk in.\n\nThey went into the parlour my mother had come from, the fire in the best room on the other side of the passage not being lighted--not having been lighted, indeed, since my father’s funeral; and when they were both seated, and Miss Betsey said nothing, my mother, after vainly trying to restrain herself, began to cry. ‘Oh tut, tut, tut!’ said Miss Betsey, in a hurry. ‘Don’t do that! Come, come!’\n\nMy mother couldn’t help it notwithstanding, so she cried until she had had her cry out.\n\n‘Take off your cap, child,’ said Miss Betsey, ‘and let me see you.’\n\nMy mother was too much afraid of her to refuse compliance with this odd request, if she had any disposition to do so. Therefore she did as she was told, and did it with such nervous hands that her hair (which was luxuriant and beautiful) fell all about her face.\n\n‘Why, bless my heart!’ exclaimed Miss Betsey. ‘You are a very Baby!’\n\nMy mother was, no doubt, unusually youthful in appearance even for her years; she hung her head, as if it were her fault, poor thing, and said, sobbing, that indeed she was afraid she was but a childish widow, and would be but a childish mother if she lived. In a short pause which ensued, she had a fancy that she felt Miss Betsey touch her hair, and that with no ungentle hand; but, looking at her, in her timid hope, she found that lady sitting with the skirt of her dress tucked up, her hands folded on one knee, and her feet upon the fender, frowning at the fire.\n\n‘In the name of Heaven,’ said Miss Betsey, suddenly, ‘why Rookery?’\n\n‘Do you mean the house, ma’am?’ asked my mother.\n\n‘Why Rookery?’ said Miss Betsey. ‘Cookery would have been more to the purpose, if you had had any practical ideas of life, either of you.’\n\n‘The name was Mr. Copperfield’s choice,’ returned my mother. ‘When he bought the house, he liked to think that there were rooks about it.’\n\nThe evening wind made such a disturbance just now, among some tall old elm-trees at the bottom of the garden, that neither my mother nor Miss Betsey could forbear glancing that way. As the elms bent to one another, like giants who were whispering secrets, and after a few seconds of such repose, fell into a violent flurry, tossing their wild arms about, as if their late confidences were really too wicked for their peace of mind, some weatherbeaten ragged old rooks’-nests, burdening their higher branches, swung like wrecks upon a stormy sea.\n\n‘Where are the birds?’ asked Miss Betsey.\n\n‘The--?’ My mother had been thinking of something else.\n\n‘The rooks--what has become of them?’ asked Miss Betsey.\n\n‘There have not been any since we have lived here,’ said my mother. ‘We thought--Mr. Copperfield thought--it was quite a large rookery; but the nests were very old ones, and the birds have deserted them a long while.’\n\n‘David Copperfield all over!’ cried Miss Betsey. ‘David Copperfield from head to foot! Calls a house a rookery when there’s not a rook near it, and takes the birds on trust, because he sees the nests!’\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ returned my mother, ‘is dead, and if you dare to speak unkindly of him to me--’\n\nMy poor dear mother, I suppose, had some momentary intention of committing an assault and battery upon my aunt, who could easily have settled her with one hand, even if my mother had been in far better training for such an encounter than she was that evening. But it passed with the action of rising from her chair; and she sat down again very meekly, and fainted.\n\nWhen she came to herself, or when Miss Betsey had restored her, whichever it was, she found the latter standing at the window. The twilight was by this time shading down into darkness; and dimly as they saw each other, they could not have done that without the aid of the fire.\n\n‘Well?’ said Miss Betsey, coming back to her chair, as if she had only been taking a casual look at the prospect; ‘and when do you expect--’\n\n‘I am all in a tremble,’ faltered my mother. ‘I don’t know what’s the matter. I shall die, I am sure!’\n\n‘No, no, no,’ said Miss Betsey. ‘Have some tea.’\n\n‘Oh dear me, dear me, do you think it will do me any good?’ cried my mother in a helpless manner.\n\n‘Of course it will,’ said Miss Betsey. ‘It’s nothing but fancy. What do you call your girl?’\n\n‘I don’t know that it will be a girl, yet, ma’am,’ said my mother innocently.\n\n‘Bless the Baby!’ exclaimed Miss Betsey, unconsciously quoting the second sentiment of the pincushion in the drawer upstairs, but applying it to my mother instead of me, ‘I don’t mean that. I mean your servant-girl.’\n\n‘Peggotty,’ said my mother.\n\n‘Peggotty!’ repeated Miss Betsey, with some indignation. ‘Do you mean to say, child, that any human being has gone into a Christian church, and got herself named Peggotty?’ ‘It’s her surname,’ said my mother, faintly. ‘Mr. Copperfield called her by it, because her Christian name was the same as mine.’\n\n‘Here! Peggotty!’ cried Miss Betsey, opening the parlour door. ‘Tea. Your mistress is a little unwell. Don’t dawdle.’\n\nHaving issued this mandate with as much potentiality as if she had been a recognized authority in the house ever since it had been a house, and having looked out to confront the amazed Peggotty coming along the passage with a candle at the sound of a strange voice, Miss Betsey shut the door again, and sat down as before: with her feet on the fender, the skirt of her dress tucked up, and her hands folded on one knee.\n\n‘You were speaking about its being a girl,’ said Miss Betsey. ‘I have no doubt it will be a girl. I have a presentiment that it must be a girl. Now child, from the moment of the birth of this girl--’\n\n‘Perhaps boy,’ my mother took the liberty of putting in.\n\n‘I tell you I have a presentiment that it must be a girl,’ returned Miss Betsey. ‘Don’t contradict. From the moment of this girl’s birth, child, I intend to be her friend. I intend to be her godmother, and I beg you’ll call her Betsey Trotwood Copperfield. There must be no mistakes in life with THIS Betsey Trotwood. There must be no trifling with HER affections, poor dear. She must be well brought up, and well guarded from reposing any foolish confidences where they are not deserved. I must make that MY care.’\n\nThere was a twitch of Miss Betsey’s head, after each of these sentences, as if her own old wrongs were working within her, and she repressed any plainer reference to them by strong constraint. So my mother suspected, at least, as she observed her by the low glimmer of the fire: too much scared by Miss Betsey, too uneasy in herself, and too subdued and bewildered altogether, to observe anything very clearly, or to know what to say.\n\n‘And was David good to you, child?’ asked Miss Betsey, when she had been silent for a little while, and these motions of her head had gradually ceased. ‘Were you comfortable together?’\n\n‘We were very happy,’ said my mother. ‘Mr. Copperfield was only too good to me.’\n\n‘What, he spoilt you, I suppose?’ returned Miss Betsey.\n\n‘For being quite alone and dependent on myself in this rough world again, yes, I fear he did indeed,’ sobbed my mother.\n\n‘Well! Don’t cry!’ said Miss Betsey. ‘You were not equally matched, child--if any two people can be equally matched--and so I asked the question. You were an orphan, weren’t you?’ ‘Yes.’\n\n‘And a governess?’\n\n‘I was nursery-governess in a family where Mr. Copperfield came to visit. Mr. Copperfield was very kind to me, and took a great deal of notice of me, and paid me a good deal of attention, and at last proposed to me. And I accepted him. And so we were married,’ said my mother simply.\n\n‘Ha! Poor Baby!’ mused Miss Betsey, with her frown still bent upon the fire. ‘Do you know anything?’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, ma’am,’ faltered my mother.\n\n‘About keeping house, for instance,’ said Miss Betsey.\n\n‘Not much, I fear,’ returned my mother. ‘Not so much as I could wish. But Mr. Copperfield was teaching me--’\n\n(‘Much he knew about it himself!’) said Miss Betsey in a parenthesis. --‘And I hope I should have improved, being very anxious to learn, and he very patient to teach me, if the great misfortune of his death’--my mother broke down again here, and could get no farther.\n\n‘Well, well!’ said Miss Betsey. --‘I kept my housekeeping-book regularly, and balanced it with Mr. Copperfield every night,’ cried my mother in another burst of distress, and breaking down again.\n\n‘Well, well!’ said Miss Betsey. ‘Don’t cry any more.’ --‘And I am sure we never had a word of difference respecting it, except when Mr. Copperfield objected to my threes and fives being too much like each other, or to my putting curly tails to my sevens and nines,’ resumed my mother in another burst, and breaking down again.\n\n‘You’ll make yourself ill,’ said Miss Betsey, ‘and you know that will not be good either for you or for my god-daughter. Come! You mustn’t do it!’\n\nThis argument had some share in quieting my mother, though her increasing indisposition had a larger one. There was an interval of silence, only broken by Miss Betsey’s occasionally ejaculating ‘Ha!’ as she sat with her feet upon the fender.\n\n‘David had bought an annuity for himself with his money, I know,’ said she, by and by. ‘What did he do for you?’\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ said my mother, answering with some difficulty, ‘was so considerate and good as to secure the reversion of a part of it to me.’\n\n‘How much?’ asked Miss Betsey.\n\n‘A hundred and five pounds a year,’ said my mother.\n\n‘He might have done worse,’ said my aunt.\n\nThe word was appropriate to the moment. My mother was so much worse that Peggotty, coming in with the teaboard and candles, and seeing at a glance how ill she was,--as Miss Betsey might have done sooner if there had been light enough,--conveyed her upstairs to her own room with all speed; and immediately dispatched Ham Peggotty, her nephew, who had been for some days past secreted in the house, unknown to my mother, as a special messenger in case of emergency, to fetch the nurse and doctor.\n\nThose allied powers were considerably astonished, when they arrived within a few minutes of each other, to find an unknown lady of portentous appearance, sitting before the fire, with her bonnet tied over her left arm, stopping her ears with jewellers’ cotton. Peggotty knowing nothing about her, and my mother saying nothing about her, she was quite a mystery in the parlour; and the fact of her having a magazine of jewellers’ cotton in her pocket, and sticking the article in her ears in that way, did not detract from the solemnity of her presence.\n\nThe doctor having been upstairs and come down again, and having satisfied himself, I suppose, that there was a probability of this unknown lady and himself having to sit there, face to face, for some hours, laid himself out to be polite and social. He was the meekest of his sex, the mildest of little men. He sidled in and out of a room, to take up the less space. He walked as softly as the Ghost in Hamlet, and more slowly. He carried his head on one side, partly in modest depreciation of himself, partly in modest propitiation of everybody else. It is nothing to say that he hadn’t a word to throw at a dog. He couldn’t have thrown a word at a mad dog. He might have offered him one gently, or half a one, or a fragment of one; for he spoke as slowly as he walked; but he wouldn’t have been rude to him, and he couldn’t have been quick with him, for any earthly consideration.\n\nMr. Chillip, looking mildly at my aunt with his head on one side, and making her a little bow, said, in allusion to the jewellers’ cotton, as he softly touched his left ear:\n\n‘Some local irritation, ma’am?’\n\n‘What!’ replied my aunt, pulling the cotton out of one ear like a cork.\n\nMr. Chillip was so alarmed by her abruptness--as he told my mother afterwards--that it was a mercy he didn’t lose his presence of mind. But he repeated sweetly:\n\n‘Some local irritation, ma’am?’\n\n‘Nonsense!’ replied my aunt, and corked herself again, at one blow.\n\nMr. Chillip could do nothing after this, but sit and look at her feebly, as she sat and looked at the fire, until he was called upstairs again. After some quarter of an hour’s absence, he returned.\n\n‘Well?’ said my aunt, taking the cotton out of the ear nearest to him.\n\n‘Well, ma’am,’ returned Mr. Chillip, ‘we are--we are progressing slowly, ma’am.’\n\n‘Ba--a--ah!’ said my aunt, with a perfect shake on the contemptuous interjection. And corked herself as before.\n\nReally--really--as Mr. Chillip told my mother, he was almost shocked; speaking in a professional point of view alone, he was almost shocked. But he sat and looked at her, notwithstanding, for nearly two hours, as she sat looking at the fire, until he was again called out. After another absence, he again returned.\n\n‘Well?’ said my aunt, taking out the cotton on that side again.\n\n‘Well, ma’am,’ returned Mr. Chillip, ‘we are--we are progressing slowly, ma’am.’\n\n‘Ya--a--ah!’ said my aunt. With such a snarl at him, that Mr. Chillip absolutely could not bear it. It was really calculated to break his spirit, he said afterwards. He preferred to go and sit upon the stairs, in the dark and a strong draught, until he was again sent for.\n\nHam Peggotty, who went to the national school, and was a very dragon at his catechism, and who may therefore be regarded as a credible witness, reported next day, that happening to peep in at the parlour-door an hour after this, he was instantly descried by Miss Betsey, then walking to and fro in a state of agitation, and pounced upon before he could make his escape. That there were now occasional sounds of feet and voices overhead which he inferred the cotton did not exclude, from the circumstance of his evidently being clutched by the lady as a victim on whom to expend her superabundant agitation when the sounds were loudest. That, marching him constantly up and down by the collar (as if he had been taking too much laudanum), she, at those times, shook him, rumpled his hair, made light of his linen, stopped his ears as if she confounded them with her own, and otherwise tousled and maltreated him. This was in part confirmed by his aunt, who saw him at half past twelve o’clock, soon after his release, and affirmed that he was then as red as I was.\n\nThe mild Mr. Chillip could not possibly bear malice at such a time, if at any time. He sidled into the parlour as soon as he was at liberty, and said to my aunt in his meekest manner:\n\n‘Well, ma’am, I am happy to congratulate you.’\n\n‘What upon?’ said my aunt, sharply.\n\nMr. Chillip was fluttered again, by the extreme severity of my aunt’s manner; so he made her a little bow and gave her a little smile, to mollify her.\n\n‘Mercy on the man, what’s he doing!’ cried my aunt, impatiently. ‘Can’t he speak?’\n\n‘Be calm, my dear ma’am,’ said Mr. Chillip, in his softest accents.\n\n‘There is no longer any occasion for uneasiness, ma’am. Be calm.’\n\nIt has since been considered almost a miracle that my aunt didn’t shake him, and shake what he had to say, out of him. She only shook her own head at him, but in a way that made him quail.\n\n‘Well, ma’am,’ resumed Mr. Chillip, as soon as he had courage, ‘I am happy to congratulate you. All is now over, ma’am, and well over.’\n\nDuring the five minutes or so that Mr. Chillip devoted to the delivery of this oration, my aunt eyed him narrowly.\n\n‘How is she?’ said my aunt, folding her arms with her bonnet still tied on one of them.\n\n‘Well, ma’am, she will soon be quite comfortable, I hope,’ returned Mr. Chillip. ‘Quite as comfortable as we can expect a young mother to be, under these melancholy domestic circumstances. There cannot be any objection to your seeing her presently, ma’am. It may do her good.’\n\n‘And SHE. How is SHE?’ said my aunt, sharply.\n\nMr. Chillip laid his head a little more on one side, and looked at my aunt like an amiable bird.\n\n‘The baby,’ said my aunt. ‘How is she?’\n\n‘Ma’am,’ returned Mr. Chillip, ‘I apprehended you had known. It’s a boy.’\n\nMy aunt said never a word, but took her bonnet by the strings, in the manner of a sling, aimed a blow at Mr. Chillip’s head with it, put it on bent, walked out, and never came back. She vanished like a discontented fairy; or like one of those supernatural beings, whom it was popularly supposed I was entitled to see; and never came back any more.\n\nNo. I lay in my basket, and my mother lay in her bed; but Betsey Trotwood Copperfield was for ever in the land of dreams and shadows, the tremendous region whence I had so lately travelled; and the light upon the window of our room shone out upon the earthly bourne of all such travellers, and the mound above the ashes and the dust that once was he, without whom I had never been.\n\n# CHAPTER 2. I OBSERVE\n\nThe first objects that assume a distinct presence before me, as I look far back, into the blank of my infancy, are my mother with her pretty hair and youthful shape, and Peggotty with no shape at all, and eyes so dark that they seemed to darken their whole neighbourhood in her face, and cheeks and arms so hard and red that I wondered the birds didn’t peck her in preference to apples.\n\nI believe I can remember these two at a little distance apart, dwarfed to my sight by stooping down or kneeling on the floor, and I going unsteadily from the one to the other. I have an impression on my mind which I cannot distinguish from actual remembrance, of the touch of Peggotty’s forefinger as she used to hold it out to me, and of its being roughened by needlework, like a pocket nutmeg-grater.\n\nThis may be fancy, though I think the memory of most of us can go farther back into such times than many of us suppose; just as I believe the power of observation in numbers of very young children to be quite wonderful for its closeness and accuracy. Indeed, I think that most grown men who are remarkable in this respect, may with greater propriety be said not to have lost the faculty, than to have acquired it; the rather, as I generally observe such men to retain a certain freshness, and gentleness, and capacity of being pleased, which are also an inheritance they have preserved from their childhood.\n\nI might have a misgiving that I am ‘meandering’ in stopping to say this, but that it brings me to remark that I build these conclusions, in part upon my own experience of myself; and if it should appear from anything I may set down in this narrative that I was a child of close observation, or that as a man I have a strong memory of my childhood, I undoubtedly lay claim to both of these characteristics.\n\nLooking back, as I was saying, into the blank of my infancy, the first objects I can remember as standing out by themselves from a confusion of things, are my mother and Peggotty. What else do I remember? Let me see.\n\nThere comes out of the cloud, our house--not new to me, but quite familiar, in its earliest remembrance. On the ground-floor is Peggotty’s kitchen, opening into a back yard; with a pigeon-house on a pole, in the centre, without any pigeons in it; a great dog-kennel in a corner, without any dog; and a quantity of fowls that look terribly tall to me, walking about, in a menacing and ferocious manner. There is one cock who gets upon a post to crow, and seems to take particular notice of me as I look at him through the kitchen window, who makes me shiver, he is so fierce. Of the geese outside the side-gate who come waddling after me with their long necks stretched out when I go that way, I dream at night: as a man environed by wild beasts might dream of lions.\n\nHere is a long passage--what an enormous perspective I make of it!--leading from Peggotty’s kitchen to the front door. A dark store-room opens out of it, and that is a place to be run past at night; for I don’t know what may be among those tubs and jars and old tea-chests, when there is nobody in there with a dimly-burning light, letting a mouldy air come out of the door, in which there is the smell of soap, pickles, pepper, candles, and coffee, all at one whiff. Then there are the two parlours: the parlour in which we sit of an evening, my mother and I and Peggotty--for Peggotty is quite our companion, when her work is done and we are alone--and the best parlour where we sit on a Sunday; grandly, but not so comfortably. There is something of a doleful air about that room to me, for Peggotty has told me--I don’t know when, but apparently ages ago--about my father’s funeral, and the company having their black cloaks put on. One Sunday night my mother reads to Peggotty and me in there, how Lazarus was raised up from the dead. And I am so frightened that they are afterwards obliged to take me out of bed, and show me the quiet churchyard out of the bedroom window, with the dead all lying in their graves at rest, below the solemn moon.\n\nThere is nothing half so green that I know anywhere, as the grass of that churchyard; nothing half so shady as its trees; nothing half so quiet as its tombstones. The sheep are feeding there, when I kneel up, early in the morning, in my little bed in a closet within my mother’s room, to look out at it; and I see the red light shining on the sun-dial, and think within myself, ‘Is the sun-dial glad, I wonder, that it can tell the time again?’\n\nHere is our pew in the church. What a high-backed pew! With a window near it, out of which our house can be seen, and IS seen many times during the morning’s service, by Peggotty, who likes to make herself as sure as she can that it’s not being robbed, or is not in flames. But though Peggotty’s eye wanders, she is much offended if mine does, and frowns to me, as I stand upon the seat, that I am to look at the clergyman. But I can’t always look at him--I know him without that white thing on, and I am afraid of his wondering why I stare so, and perhaps stopping the service to inquire--and what am I to do? It’s a dreadful thing to gape, but I must do something. I look at my mother, but she pretends not to see me. I look at a boy in the aisle, and he makes faces at me. I look at the sunlight coming in at the open door through the porch, and there I see a stray sheep--I don’t mean a sinner, but mutton--half making up his mind to come into the church. I feel that if I looked at him any longer, I might be tempted to say something out loud; and what would become of me then! I look up at the monumental tablets on the wall, and try to think of Mr. Bodgers late of this parish, and what the feelings of Mrs. Bodgers must have been, when affliction sore, long time Mr. Bodgers bore, and physicians were in vain. I wonder whether they called in Mr. Chillip, and he was in vain; and if so, how he likes to be reminded of it once a week. I look from Mr. Chillip, in his Sunday neckcloth, to the pulpit; and think what a good place it would be to play in, and what a castle it would make, with another boy coming up the stairs to attack it, and having the velvet cushion with the tassels thrown down on his head. In time my eyes gradually shut up; and, from seeming to hear the clergyman singing a drowsy song in the heat, I hear nothing, until I fall off the seat with a crash, and am taken out, more dead than alive, by Peggotty.\n\nAnd now I see the outside of our house, with the latticed bedroom-windows standing open to let in the sweet-smelling air, and the ragged old rooks’-nests still dangling in the elm-trees at the bottom of the front garden. Now I am in the garden at the back, beyond the yard where the empty pigeon-house and dog-kennel are--a very preserve of butterflies, as I remember it, with a high fence, and a gate and padlock; where the fruit clusters on the trees, riper and richer than fruit has ever been since, in any other garden, and where my mother gathers some in a basket, while I stand by, bolting furtive gooseberries, and trying to look unmoved. A great wind rises, and the summer is gone in a moment. We are playing in the winter twilight, dancing about the parlour. When my mother is out of breath and rests herself in an elbow-chair, I watch her winding her bright curls round her fingers, and straitening her waist, and nobody knows better than I do that she likes to look so well, and is proud of being so pretty.\n\nThat is among my very earliest impressions. That, and a sense that we were both a little afraid of Peggotty, and submitted ourselves in most things to her direction, were among the first opinions--if they may be so called--that I ever derived from what I saw.\n\nPeggotty and I were sitting one night by the parlour fire, alone. I had been reading to Peggotty about crocodiles. I must have read very perspicuously, or the poor soul must have been deeply interested, for I remember she had a cloudy impression, after I had done, that they were a sort of vegetable. I was tired of reading, and dead sleepy; but having leave, as a high treat, to sit up until my mother came home from spending the evening at a neighbour’s, I would rather have died upon my post (of course) than have gone to bed. I had reached that stage of sleepiness when Peggotty seemed to swell and grow immensely large. I propped my eyelids open with my two forefingers, and looked perseveringly at her as she sat at work; at the little bit of wax-candle she kept for her thread--how old it looked, being so wrinkled in all directions!--at the little house with a thatched roof, where the yard-measure lived; at her work-box with a sliding lid, with a view of St. Paul’s Cathedral (with a pink dome) painted on the top; at the brass thimble on her finger; at herself, whom I thought lovely. I felt so sleepy, that I knew if I lost sight of anything for a moment, I was gone.\n\n‘Peggotty,’ says I, suddenly, ‘were you ever married?’\n\n‘Lord, Master Davy,’ replied Peggotty. ‘What’s put marriage in your head?’\n\nShe answered with such a start, that it quite awoke me. And then she stopped in her work, and looked at me, with her needle drawn out to its thread’s length.\n\n‘But WERE you ever married, Peggotty?’ says I. ‘You are a very handsome woman, an’t you?’\n\nI thought her in a different style from my mother, certainly; but of another school of beauty, I considered her a perfect example. There was a red velvet footstool in the best parlour, on which my mother had painted a nosegay. The ground-work of that stool, and Peggotty’s complexion appeared to me to be one and the same thing. The stool was smooth, and Peggotty was rough, but that made no difference.\n\n‘Me handsome, Davy!’ said Peggotty. ‘Lawk, no, my dear! But what put marriage in your head?’\n\n‘I don’t know!--You mustn’t marry more than one person at a time, may you, Peggotty?’\n\n‘Certainly not,’ says Peggotty, with the promptest decision.\n\n‘But if you marry a person, and the person dies, why then you may marry another person, mayn’t you, Peggotty?’\n\n‘YOU MAY,’ says Peggotty, ‘if you choose, my dear. That’s a matter of opinion.’\n\n‘But what is your opinion, Peggotty?’ said I.\n\nI asked her, and looked curiously at her, because she looked so curiously at me.\n\n‘My opinion is,’ said Peggotty, taking her eyes from me, after a little indecision and going on with her work, ‘that I never was married myself, Master Davy, and that I don’t expect to be. That’s all I know about the subject.’\n\n‘You an’t cross, I suppose, Peggotty, are you?’ said I, after sitting quiet for a minute.\n\nI really thought she was, she had been so short with me; but I was quite mistaken: for she laid aside her work (which was a stocking of her own), and opening her arms wide, took my curly head within them, and gave it a good squeeze. I know it was a good squeeze, because, being very plump, whenever she made any little exertion after she was dressed, some of the buttons on the back of her gown flew off. And I recollect two bursting to the opposite side of the parlour, while she was hugging me.\n\n‘Now let me hear some more about the Crorkindills,’ said Peggotty, who was not quite right in the name yet, ‘for I an’t heard half enough.’\n\nI couldn’t quite understand why Peggotty looked so queer, or why she was so ready to go back to the crocodiles. However, we returned to those monsters, with fresh wakefulness on my part, and we left their eggs in the sand for the sun to hatch; and we ran away from them, and baffled them by constantly turning, which they were unable to do quickly, on account of their unwieldy make; and we went into the water after them, as natives, and put sharp pieces of timber down their throats; and in short we ran the whole crocodile gauntlet. I did, at least; but I had my doubts of Peggotty, who was thoughtfully sticking her needle into various parts of her face and arms, all the time.\n\nWe had exhausted the crocodiles, and begun with the alligators, when the garden-bell rang. We went out to the door; and there was my mother, looking unusually pretty, I thought, and with her a gentleman with beautiful black hair and whiskers, who had walked home with us from church last Sunday.\n\nAs my mother stooped down on the threshold to take me in her arms and kiss me, the gentleman said I was a more highly privileged little fellow than a monarch--or something like that; for my later understanding comes, I am sensible, to my aid here.\n\n‘What does that mean?’ I asked him, over her shoulder.\n\nHe patted me on the head; but somehow, I didn’t like him or his deep voice, and I was jealous that his hand should touch my mother’s in touching me--which it did. I put it away, as well as I could.\n\n‘Oh, Davy!’ remonstrated my mother.\n\n‘Dear boy!’ said the gentleman. ‘I cannot wonder at his devotion!’\n\nI never saw such a beautiful colour on my mother’s face before. She gently chid me for being rude; and, keeping me close to her shawl, turned to thank the gentleman for taking so much trouble as to bring her home. She put out her hand to him as she spoke, and, as he met it with his own, she glanced, I thought, at me.\n\n‘Let us say “good night”, my fine boy,’ said the gentleman, when he had bent his head--I saw him!--over my mother’s little glove.\n\n‘Good night!’ said I.\n\n‘Come! Let us be the best friends in the world!’ said the gentleman, laughing. ‘Shake hands!’\n\nMy right hand was in my mother’s left, so I gave him the other.\n\n‘Why, that’s the Wrong hand, Davy!’ laughed the gentleman.\n\nMy mother drew my right hand forward, but I was resolved, for my former reason, not to give it him, and I did not. I gave him the other, and he shook it heartily, and said I was a brave fellow, and went away.\n\nAt this minute I see him turn round in the garden, and give us a last look with his ill-omened black eyes, before the door was shut.\n\nPeggotty, who had not said a word or moved a finger, secured the fastenings instantly, and we all went into the parlour. My mother, contrary to her usual habit, instead of coming to the elbow-chair by the fire, remained at the other end of the room, and sat singing to herself. --‘Hope you have had a pleasant evening, ma’am,’ said Peggotty, standing as stiff as a barrel in the centre of the room, with a candlestick in her hand.\n\n‘Much obliged to you, Peggotty,’ returned my mother, in a cheerful voice, ‘I have had a VERY pleasant evening.’\n\n‘A stranger or so makes an agreeable change,’ suggested Peggotty.\n\n‘A very agreeable change, indeed,’ returned my mother.\n\nPeggotty continuing to stand motionless in the middle of the room, and my mother resuming her singing, I fell asleep, though I was not so sound asleep but that I could hear voices, without hearing what they said. When I half awoke from this uncomfortable doze, I found Peggotty and my mother both in tears, and both talking.\n\n‘Not such a one as this, Mr. Copperfield wouldn’t have liked,’ said Peggotty. ‘That I say, and that I swear!’\n\n‘Good Heavens!’ cried my mother, ‘you’ll drive me mad! Was ever any poor girl so ill-used by her servants as I am! Why do I do myself the injustice of calling myself a girl? Have I never been married, Peggotty?’\n\n‘God knows you have, ma’am,’ returned Peggotty. ‘Then, how can you dare,’ said my mother--‘you know I don’t mean how can you dare, Peggotty, but how can you have the heart--to make me so uncomfortable and say such bitter things to me, when you are well aware that I haven’t, out of this place, a single friend to turn to?’\n\n‘The more’s the reason,’ returned Peggotty, ‘for saying that it won’t do. No! That it won’t do. No! No price could make it do. No!’--I thought Peggotty would have thrown the candlestick away, she was so emphatic with it.\n\n‘How can you be so aggravating,’ said my mother, shedding more tears than before, ‘as to talk in such an unjust manner! How can you go on as if it was all settled and arranged, Peggotty, when I tell you over and over again, you cruel thing, that beyond the commonest civilities nothing has passed! You talk of admiration. What am I to do? If people are so silly as to indulge the sentiment, is it my fault? What am I to do, I ask you? Would you wish me to shave my head and black my face, or disfigure myself with a burn, or a scald, or something of that sort? I dare say you would, Peggotty. I dare say you’d quite enjoy it.’\n\nPeggotty seemed to take this aspersion very much to heart, I thought.\n\n‘And my dear boy,’ cried my mother, coming to the elbow-chair in which I was, and caressing me, ‘my own little Davy! Is it to be hinted to me that I am wanting in affection for my precious treasure, the dearest little fellow that ever was!’\n\n‘Nobody never went and hinted no such a thing,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘You did, Peggotty!’ returned my mother. ‘You know you did. What else was it possible to infer from what you said, you unkind creature, when you know as well as I do, that on his account only last quarter I wouldn’t buy myself a new parasol, though that old green one is frayed the whole way up, and the fringe is perfectly mangy? You know it is, Peggotty. You can’t deny it.’ Then, turning affectionately to me, with her cheek against mine, ‘Am I a naughty mama to you, Davy? Am I a nasty, cruel, selfish, bad mama? Say I am, my child; say “yes”, dear boy, and Peggotty will love you; and Peggotty’s love is a great deal better than mine, Davy. I don’t love you at all, do I?’\n\nAt this, we all fell a-crying together. I think I was the loudest of the party, but I am sure we were all sincere about it. I was quite heart-broken myself, and am afraid that in the first transports of wounded tenderness I called Peggotty a ‘Beast’. That honest creature was in deep affliction, I remember, and must have become quite buttonless on the occasion; for a little volley of those explosives went off, when, after having made it up with my mother, she kneeled down by the elbow-chair, and made it up with me.\n\nWe went to bed greatly dejected. My sobs kept waking me, for a long time; and when one very strong sob quite hoisted me up in bed, I found my mother sitting on the coverlet, and leaning over me. I fell asleep in her arms, after that, and slept soundly.\n\nWhether it was the following Sunday when I saw the gentleman again, or whether there was any greater lapse of time before he reappeared, I cannot recall. I don’t profess to be clear about dates. But there he was, in church, and he walked home with us afterwards. He came in, too, to look at a famous geranium we had, in the parlour-window. It did not appear to me that he took much notice of it, but before he went he asked my mother to give him a bit of the blossom. She begged him to choose it for himself, but he refused to do that--I could not understand why--so she plucked it for him, and gave it into his hand. He said he would never, never part with it any more; and I thought he must be quite a fool not to know that it would fall to pieces in a day or two.\n\nPeggotty began to be less with us, of an evening, than she had always been. My mother deferred to her very much--more than usual, it occurred to me--and we were all three excellent friends; still we were different from what we used to be, and were not so comfortable among ourselves. Sometimes I fancied that Peggotty perhaps objected to my mother’s wearing all the pretty dresses she had in her drawers, or to her going so often to visit at that neighbour’s; but I couldn’t, to my satisfaction, make out how it was.\n\nGradually, I became used to seeing the gentleman with the black whiskers. I liked him no better than at first, and had the same uneasy jealousy of him; but if I had any reason for it beyond a child’s instinctive dislike, and a general idea that Peggotty and I could make much of my mother without any help, it certainly was not THE reason that I might have found if I had been older. No such thing came into my mind, or near it. I could observe, in little pieces, as it were; but as to making a net of a number of these pieces, and catching anybody in it, that was, as yet, beyond me.\n\nOne autumn morning I was with my mother in the front garden, when Mr. Murdstone--I knew him by that name now--came by, on horseback. He reined up his horse to salute my mother, and said he was going to Lowestoft to see some friends who were there with a yacht, and merrily proposed to take me on the saddle before him if I would like the ride.\n\nThe air was so clear and pleasant, and the horse seemed to like the idea of the ride so much himself, as he stood snorting and pawing at the garden-gate, that I had a great desire to go. So I was sent upstairs to Peggotty to be made spruce; and in the meantime Mr. Murdstone dismounted, and, with his horse’s bridle drawn over his arm, walked slowly up and down on the outer side of the sweetbriar fence, while my mother walked slowly up and down on the inner to keep him company. I recollect Peggotty and I peeping out at them from my little window; I recollect how closely they seemed to be examining the sweetbriar between them, as they strolled along; and how, from being in a perfectly angelic temper, Peggotty turned cross in a moment, and brushed my hair the wrong way, excessively hard.\n\nMr. Murdstone and I were soon off, and trotting along on the green turf by the side of the road. He held me quite easily with one arm, and I don’t think I was restless usually; but I could not make up my mind to sit in front of him without turning my head sometimes, and looking up in his face. He had that kind of shallow black eye--I want a better word to express an eye that has no depth in it to be looked into--which, when it is abstracted, seems from some peculiarity of light to be disfigured, for a moment at a time, by a cast. Several times when I glanced at him, I observed that appearance with a sort of awe, and wondered what he was thinking about so closely. His hair and whiskers were blacker and thicker, looked at so near, than even I had given them credit for being. A squareness about the lower part of his face, and the dotted indication of the strong black beard he shaved close every day, reminded me of the wax-work that had travelled into our neighbourhood some half-a-year before. This, his regular eyebrows, and the rich white, and black, and brown, of his complexion--confound his complexion, and his memory!--made me think him, in spite of my misgivings, a very handsome man. I have no doubt that my poor dear mother thought him so too.\n\nWe went to an hotel by the sea, where two gentlemen were smoking cigars in a room by themselves. Each of them was lying on at least four chairs, and had a large rough jacket on. In a corner was a heap of coats and boat-cloaks, and a flag, all bundled up together.\n\nThey both rolled on to their feet in an untidy sort of manner, when we came in, and said, ‘Halloa, Murdstone! We thought you were dead!’\n\n‘Not yet,’ said Mr. Murdstone.\n\n‘And who’s this shaver?’ said one of the gentlemen, taking hold of me.\n\n‘That’s Davy,’ returned Mr. Murdstone.\n\n‘Davy who?’ said the gentleman. ‘Jones?’\n\n‘Copperfield,’ said Mr. Murdstone.\n\n‘What! Bewitching Mrs. Copperfield’s encumbrance?’ cried the gentleman. ‘The pretty little widow?’\n\n‘Quinion,’ said Mr. Murdstone, ‘take care, if you please. Somebody’s sharp.’\n\n‘Who is?’ asked the gentleman, laughing. I looked up, quickly; being curious to know.\n\n‘Only Brooks of Sheffield,’ said Mr. Murdstone.\n\nI was quite relieved to find that it was only Brooks of Sheffield; for, at first, I really thought it was I.\n\nThere seemed to be something very comical in the reputation of Mr. Brooks of Sheffield, for both the gentlemen laughed heartily when he was mentioned, and Mr. Murdstone was a good deal amused also. After some laughing, the gentleman whom he had called Quinion, said:\n\n‘And what is the opinion of Brooks of Sheffield, in reference to the projected business?’\n\n‘Why, I don’t know that Brooks understands much about it at present,’ replied Mr. Murdstone; ‘but he is not generally favourable, I believe.’\n\nThere was more laughter at this, and Mr. Quinion said he would ring the bell for some sherry in which to drink to Brooks. This he did; and when the wine came, he made me have a little, with a biscuit, and, before I drank it, stand up and say, ‘Confusion to Brooks of Sheffield!’ The toast was received with great applause, and such hearty laughter that it made me laugh too; at which they laughed the more. In short, we quite enjoyed ourselves.\n\nWe walked about on the cliff after that, and sat on the grass, and looked at things through a telescope--I could make out nothing myself when it was put to my eye, but I pretended I could--and then we came back to the hotel to an early dinner. All the time we were out, the two gentlemen smoked incessantly--which, I thought, if I might judge from the smell of their rough coats, they must have been doing, ever since the coats had first come home from the tailor’s. I must not forget that we went on board the yacht, where they all three descended into the cabin, and were busy with some papers. I saw them quite hard at work, when I looked down through the open skylight. They left me, during this time, with a very nice man with a very large head of red hair and a very small shiny hat upon it, who had got a cross-barred shirt or waistcoat on, with ‘Skylark’ in capital letters across the chest. I thought it was his name; and that as he lived on board ship and hadn’t a street door to put his name on, he put it there instead; but when I called him Mr. Skylark, he said it meant the vessel.\n\nI observed all day that Mr. Murdstone was graver and steadier than the two gentlemen. They were very gay and careless. They joked freely with one another, but seldom with him. It appeared to me that he was more clever and cold than they were, and that they regarded him with something of my own feeling. I remarked that, once or twice when Mr. Quinion was talking, he looked at Mr. Murdstone sideways, as if to make sure of his not being displeased; and that once when Mr. Passnidge (the other gentleman) was in high spirits, he trod upon his foot, and gave him a secret caution with his eyes, to observe Mr. Murdstone, who was sitting stern and silent. Nor do I recollect that Mr. Murdstone laughed at all that day, except at the Sheffield joke--and that, by the by, was his own.\n\nWe went home early in the evening. It was a very fine evening, and my mother and he had another stroll by the sweetbriar, while I was sent in to get my tea. When he was gone, my mother asked me all about the day I had had, and what they had said and done. I mentioned what they had said about her, and she laughed, and told me they were impudent fellows who talked nonsense--but I knew it pleased her. I knew it quite as well as I know it now. I took the opportunity of asking if she was at all acquainted with Mr. Brooks of Sheffield, but she answered No, only she supposed he must be a manufacturer in the knife and fork way.\n\nCan I say of her face--altered as I have reason to remember it, perished as I know it is--that it is gone, when here it comes before me at this instant, as distinct as any face that I may choose to look on in a crowded street? Can I say of her innocent and girlish beauty, that it faded, and was no more, when its breath falls on my cheek now, as it fell that night? Can I say she ever changed, when my remembrance brings her back to life, thus only; and, truer to its loving youth than I have been, or man ever is, still holds fast what it cherished then?\n\nI write of her just as she was when I had gone to bed after this talk, and she came to bid me good night. She kneeled down playfully by the side of the bed, and laying her chin upon her hands, and laughing, said:\n\n‘What was it they said, Davy? Tell me again. I can’t believe it.’\n\n‘“Bewitching--”’ I began.\n\nMy mother put her hands upon my lips to stop me.\n\n‘It was never bewitching,’ she said, laughing. ‘It never could have been bewitching, Davy. Now I know it wasn’t!’\n\n‘Yes, it was. “Bewitching Mrs. Copperfield”,’ I repeated stoutly. ‘And, “pretty.”’\n\n‘No, no, it was never pretty. Not pretty,’ interposed my mother, laying her fingers on my lips again.\n\n‘Yes it was. “Pretty little widow.”’\n\n‘What foolish, impudent creatures!’ cried my mother, laughing and covering her face. ‘What ridiculous men! An’t they? Davy dear--’\n\n‘Well, Ma.’\n\n‘Don’t tell Peggotty; she might be angry with them. I am dreadfully angry with them myself; but I would rather Peggotty didn’t know.’\n\nI promised, of course; and we kissed one another over and over again, and I soon fell fast asleep.\n\nIt seems to me, at this distance of time, as if it were the next day when Peggotty broached the striking and adventurous proposition I am about to mention; but it was probably about two months afterwards.\n\nWe were sitting as before, one evening (when my mother was out as before), in company with the stocking and the yard-measure, and the bit of wax, and the box with St. Paul’s on the lid, and the crocodile book, when Peggotty, after looking at me several times, and opening her mouth as if she were going to speak, without doing it--which I thought was merely gaping, or I should have been rather alarmed--said coaxingly:\n\n‘Master Davy, how should you like to go along with me and spend a fortnight at my brother’s at Yarmouth? Wouldn’t that be a treat?’\n\n‘Is your brother an agreeable man, Peggotty?’ I inquired, provisionally.\n\n‘Oh, what an agreeable man he is!’ cried Peggotty, holding up her hands. ‘Then there’s the sea; and the boats and ships; and the fishermen; and the beach; and Am to play with--’\n\nPeggotty meant her nephew Ham, mentioned in my first chapter; but she spoke of him as a morsel of English Grammar.\n\nI was flushed by her summary of delights, and replied that it would indeed be a treat, but what would my mother say?\n\n‘Why then I’ll as good as bet a guinea,’ said Peggotty, intent upon my face, ‘that she’ll let us go. I’ll ask her, if you like, as soon as ever she comes home. There now!’\n\n‘But what’s she to do while we’re away?’ said I, putting my small elbows on the table to argue the point. ‘She can’t live by herself.’\n\nIf Peggotty were looking for a hole, all of a sudden, in the heel of that stocking, it must have been a very little one indeed, and not worth darning.\n\n‘I say! Peggotty! She can’t live by herself, you know.’\n\n‘Oh, bless you!’ said Peggotty, looking at me again at last. ‘Don’t you know? She’s going to stay for a fortnight with Mrs. Grayper. Mrs. Grayper’s going to have a lot of company.’\n\nOh! If that was it, I was quite ready to go. I waited, in the utmost impatience, until my mother came home from Mrs. Grayper’s (for it was that identical neighbour), to ascertain if we could get leave to carry out this great idea. Without being nearly so much surprised as I had expected, my mother entered into it readily; and it was all arranged that night, and my board and lodging during the visit were to be paid for.\n\nThe day soon came for our going. It was such an early day that it came soon, even to me, who was in a fever of expectation, and half afraid that an earthquake or a fiery mountain, or some other great convulsion of nature, might interpose to stop the expedition. We were to go in a carrier’s cart, which departed in the morning after breakfast. I would have given any money to have been allowed to wrap myself up over-night, and sleep in my hat and boots.\n\nIt touches me nearly now, although I tell it lightly, to recollect how eager I was to leave my happy home; to think how little I suspected what I did leave for ever.\n\nI am glad to recollect that when the carrier’s cart was at the gate, and my mother stood there kissing me, a grateful fondness for her and for the old place I had never turned my back upon before, made me cry. I am glad to know that my mother cried too, and that I felt her heart beat against mine.\n\nI am glad to recollect that when the carrier began to move, my mother ran out at the gate, and called to him to stop, that she might kiss me once more. I am glad to dwell upon the earnestness and love with which she lifted up her face to mine, and did so.\n\nAs we left her standing in the road, Mr. Murdstone came up to where she was, and seemed to expostulate with her for being so moved. I was looking back round the awning of the cart, and wondered what business it was of his. Peggotty, who was also looking back on the other side, seemed anything but satisfied; as the face she brought back in the cart denoted.\n\nI sat looking at Peggotty for some time, in a reverie on this supposititious case: whether, if she were employed to lose me like the boy in the fairy tale, I should be able to track my way home again by the buttons she would shed.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. I HAVE A CHANGE\n\nThe carrier’s horse was the laziest horse in the world, I should hope, and shuffled along, with his head down, as if he liked to keep people waiting to whom the packages were directed. I fancied, indeed, that he sometimes chuckled audibly over this reflection, but the carrier said he was only troubled with a cough. The carrier had a way of keeping his head down, like his horse, and of drooping sleepily forward as he drove, with one of his arms on each of his knees. I say ‘drove’, but it struck me that the cart would have gone to Yarmouth quite as well without him, for the horse did all that; and as to conversation, he had no idea of it but whistling.\n\nPeggotty had a basket of refreshments on her knee, which would have lasted us out handsomely, if we had been going to London by the same conveyance. We ate a good deal, and slept a good deal. Peggotty always went to sleep with her chin upon the handle of the basket, her hold of which never relaxed; and I could not have believed unless I had heard her do it, that one defenceless woman could have snored so much.\n\nWe made so many deviations up and down lanes, and were such a long time delivering a bedstead at a public-house, and calling at other places, that I was quite tired, and very glad, when we saw Yarmouth. It looked rather spongy and soppy, I thought, as I carried my eye over the great dull waste that lay across the river; and I could not help wondering, if the world were really as round as my geography book said, how any part of it came to be so flat. But I reflected that Yarmouth might be situated at one of the poles; which would account for it.\n\nAs we drew a little nearer, and saw the whole adjacent prospect lying a straight low line under the sky, I hinted to Peggotty that a mound or so might have improved it; and also that if the land had been a little more separated from the sea, and the town and the tide had not been quite so much mixed up, like toast and water, it would have been nicer. But Peggotty said, with greater emphasis than usual, that we must take things as we found them, and that, for her part, she was proud to call herself a Yarmouth Bloater.\n\nWhen we got into the street (which was strange enough to me) and smelt the fish, and pitch, and oakum, and tar, and saw the sailors walking about, and the carts jingling up and down over the stones, I felt that I had done so busy a place an injustice; and said as much to Peggotty, who heard my expressions of delight with great complacency, and told me it was well known (I suppose to those who had the good fortune to be born Bloaters) that Yarmouth was, upon the whole, the finest place in the universe.\n\n‘Here’s my Am!’ screamed Peggotty, ‘growed out of knowledge!’\n\nHe was waiting for us, in fact, at the public-house; and asked me how I found myself, like an old acquaintance. I did not feel, at first, that I knew him as well as he knew me, because he had never come to our house since the night I was born, and naturally he had the advantage of me. But our intimacy was much advanced by his taking me on his back to carry me home. He was, now, a huge, strong fellow of six feet high, broad in proportion, and round-shouldered; but with a simpering boy’s face and curly light hair that gave him quite a sheepish look. He was dressed in a canvas jacket, and a pair of such very stiff trousers that they would have stood quite as well alone, without any legs in them. And you couldn’t so properly have said he wore a hat, as that he was covered in a-top, like an old building, with something pitchy.\n\nHam carrying me on his back and a small box of ours under his arm, and Peggotty carrying another small box of ours, we turned down lanes bestrewn with bits of chips and little hillocks of sand, and went past gas-works, rope-walks, boat-builders’ yards, shipwrights’ yards, ship-breakers’ yards, caulkers’ yards, riggers’ lofts, smiths’ forges, and a great litter of such places, until we came out upon the dull waste I had already seen at a distance; when Ham said,\n\n‘Yon’s our house, Mas’r Davy!’\n\nI looked in all directions, as far as I could stare over the wilderness, and away at the sea, and away at the river, but no house could I make out. There was a black barge, or some other kind of superannuated boat, not far off, high and dry on the ground, with an iron funnel sticking out of it for a chimney and smoking very cosily; but nothing else in the way of a habitation that was visible to me.\n\n‘That’s not it?’ said I. ‘That ship-looking thing?’\n\n‘That’s it, Mas’r Davy,’ returned Ham.\n\nIf it had been Aladdin’s palace, roc’s egg and all, I suppose I could not have been more charmed with the romantic idea of living in it. There was a delightful door cut in the side, and it was roofed in, and there were little windows in it; but the wonderful charm of it was, that it was a real boat which had no doubt been upon the water hundreds of times, and which had never been intended to be lived in, on dry land. That was the captivation of it to me. If it had ever been meant to be lived in, I might have thought it small, or inconvenient, or lonely; but never having been designed for any such use, it became a perfect abode.\n\nIt was beautifully clean inside, and as tidy as possible. There was a table, and a Dutch clock, and a chest of drawers, and on the chest of drawers there was a tea-tray with a painting on it of a lady with a parasol, taking a walk with a military-looking child who was trundling a hoop. The tray was kept from tumbling down, by a bible; and the tray, if it had tumbled down, would have smashed a quantity of cups and saucers and a teapot that were grouped around the book. On the walls there were some common coloured pictures, framed and glazed, of scripture subjects; such as I have never seen since in the hands of pedlars, without seeing the whole interior of Peggotty’s brother’s house again, at one view. Abraham in red going to sacrifice Isaac in blue, and Daniel in yellow cast into a den of green lions, were the most prominent of these. Over the little mantelshelf, was a picture of the ‘Sarah Jane’ lugger, built at Sunderland, with a real little wooden stern stuck on to it; a work of art, combining composition with carpentry, which I considered to be one of the most enviable possessions that the world could afford. There were some hooks in the beams of the ceiling, the use of which I did not divine then; and some lockers and boxes and conveniences of that sort, which served for seats and eked out the chairs.\n\nAll this I saw in the first glance after I crossed the threshold--child-like, according to my theory--and then Peggotty opened a little door and showed me my bedroom. It was the completest and most desirable bedroom ever seen--in the stern of the vessel; with a little window, where the rudder used to go through; a little looking-glass, just the right height for me, nailed against the wall, and framed with oyster-shells; a little bed, which there was just room enough to get into; and a nosegay of seaweed in a blue mug on the table. The walls were whitewashed as white as milk, and the patchwork counterpane made my eyes quite ache with its brightness. One thing I particularly noticed in this delightful house, was the smell of fish; which was so searching, that when I took out my pocket-handkerchief to wipe my nose, I found it smelt exactly as if it had wrapped up a lobster. On my imparting this discovery in confidence to Peggotty, she informed me that her brother dealt in lobsters, crabs, and crawfish; and I afterwards found that a heap of these creatures, in a state of wonderful conglomeration with one another, and never leaving off pinching whatever they laid hold of, were usually to be found in a little wooden outhouse where the pots and kettles were kept.\n\nWe were welcomed by a very civil woman in a white apron, whom I had seen curtseying at the door when I was on Ham’s back, about a quarter of a mile off. Likewise by a most beautiful little girl (or I thought her so) with a necklace of blue beads on, who wouldn’t let me kiss her when I offered to, but ran away and hid herself. By and by, when we had dined in a sumptuous manner off boiled dabs, melted butter, and potatoes, with a chop for me, a hairy man with a very good-natured face came home. As he called Peggotty ‘Lass’, and gave her a hearty smack on the cheek, I had no doubt, from the general propriety of her conduct, that he was her brother; and so he turned out--being presently introduced to me as Mr. Peggotty, the master of the house.\n\n‘Glad to see you, sir,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘You’ll find us rough, sir, but you’ll find us ready.’\n\nI thanked him, and replied that I was sure I should be happy in such a delightful place.\n\n‘How’s your Ma, sir?’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘Did you leave her pretty jolly?’\n\nI gave Mr. Peggotty to understand that she was as jolly as I could wish, and that she desired her compliments--which was a polite fiction on my part.\n\n‘I’m much obleeged to her, I’m sure,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘Well, sir, if you can make out here, fur a fortnut, ‘long wi’ her,’ nodding at his sister, ‘and Ham, and little Em’ly, we shall be proud of your company.’\n\nHaving done the honours of his house in this hospitable manner, Mr. Peggotty went out to wash himself in a kettleful of hot water, remarking that ‘cold would never get his muck off’. He soon returned, greatly improved in appearance; but so rubicund, that I couldn’t help thinking his face had this in common with the lobsters, crabs, and crawfish,--that it went into the hot water very black, and came out very red.\n\nAfter tea, when the door was shut and all was made snug (the nights being cold and misty now), it seemed to me the most delicious retreat that the imagination of man could conceive. To hear the wind getting up out at sea, to know that the fog was creeping over the desolate flat outside, and to look at the fire, and think that there was no house near but this one, and this one a boat, was like enchantment. Little Em’ly had overcome her shyness, and was sitting by my side upon the lowest and least of the lockers, which was just large enough for us two, and just fitted into the chimney corner. Mrs. Peggotty with the white apron, was knitting on the opposite side of the fire. Peggotty at her needlework was as much at home with St. Paul’s and the bit of wax-candle, as if they had never known any other roof. Ham, who had been giving me my first lesson in all-fours, was trying to recollect a scheme of telling fortunes with the dirty cards, and was printing off fishy impressions of his thumb on all the cards he turned. Mr. Peggotty was smoking his pipe. I felt it was a time for conversation and confidence.\n\n‘Mr. Peggotty!’ says I.\n‘Sir,’ says he.\n\n‘Did you give your son the name of Ham, because you lived in a sort of ark?’\n\nMr. Peggotty seemed to think it a deep idea, but answered:\n\n‘No, sir. I never giv him no name.’\n\n‘Who gave him that name, then?’ said I, putting question number two of the catechism to Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘Why, sir, his father giv it him,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘I thought you were his father!’\n\n‘My brother Joe was his father,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘Dead, Mr. Peggotty?’ I hinted, after a respectful pause.\n\n‘Drowndead,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\nI was very much surprised that Mr. Peggotty was not Ham’s father, and began to wonder whether I was mistaken about his relationship to anybody else there. I was so curious to know, that I made up my mind to have it out with Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘Little Em’ly,’ I said, glancing at her. ‘She is your daughter, isn’t she, Mr. Peggotty?’\n\n‘No, sir. My brother-in-law, Tom, was her father.’\n\nI couldn’t help it. ‘--Dead, Mr. Peggotty?’ I hinted, after another respectful silence.\n\n‘Drowndead,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\nI felt the difficulty of resuming the subject, but had not got to the bottom of it yet, and must get to the bottom somehow. So I said:\n\n‘Haven’t you ANY children, Mr. Peggotty?’\n\n‘No, master,’ he answered with a short laugh. ‘I’m a bacheldore.’\n\n‘A bachelor!’ I said, astonished. ‘Why, who’s that, Mr. Peggotty?’ pointing to the person in the apron who was knitting.\n\n‘That’s Missis Gummidge,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘Gummidge, Mr. Peggotty?’\n\nBut at this point Peggotty--I mean my own peculiar Peggotty--made such impressive motions to me not to ask any more questions, that I could only sit and look at all the silent company, until it was time to go to bed. Then, in the privacy of my own little cabin, she informed me that Ham and Em’ly were an orphan nephew and niece, whom my host had at different times adopted in their childhood, when they were left destitute: and that Mrs. Gummidge was the widow of his partner in a boat, who had died very poor. He was but a poor man himself, said Peggotty, but as good as gold and as true as steel--those were her similes. The only subject, she informed me, on which he ever showed a violent temper or swore an oath, was this generosity of his; and if it were ever referred to, by any one of them, he struck the table a heavy blow with his right hand (had split it on one such occasion), and swore a dreadful oath that he would be ‘Gormed’ if he didn’t cut and run for good, if it was ever mentioned again. It appeared, in answer to my inquiries, that nobody had the least idea of the etymology of this terrible verb passive to be gormed; but that they all regarded it as constituting a most solemn imprecation.\n\nI was very sensible of my entertainer’s goodness, and listened to the women’s going to bed in another little crib like mine at the opposite end of the boat, and to him and Ham hanging up two hammocks for themselves on the hooks I had noticed in the roof, in a very luxurious state of mind, enhanced by my being sleepy. As slumber gradually stole upon me, I heard the wind howling out at sea and coming on across the flat so fiercely, that I had a lazy apprehension of the great deep rising in the night. But I bethought myself that I was in a boat, after all; and that a man like Mr. Peggotty was not a bad person to have on board if anything did happen.\n\nNothing happened, however, worse than morning. Almost as soon as it shone upon the oyster-shell frame of my mirror I was out of bed, and out with little Em’ly, picking up stones upon the beach.\n\n‘You’re quite a sailor, I suppose?’ I said to Em’ly. I don’t know that I supposed anything of the kind, but I felt it an act of gallantry to say something; and a shining sail close to us made such a pretty little image of itself, at the moment, in her bright eye, that it came into my head to say this.\n\n‘No,’ replied Em’ly, shaking her head, ‘I’m afraid of the sea.’\n\n‘Afraid!’ I said, with a becoming air of boldness, and looking very big at the mighty ocean. ‘I an’t!’\n\n‘Ah! but it’s cruel,’ said Em’ly. ‘I have seen it very cruel to some of our men. I have seen it tear a boat as big as our house, all to pieces.’\n\n‘I hope it wasn’t the boat that--’\n\n‘That father was drownded in?’ said Em’ly. ‘No. Not that one, I never see that boat.’\n\n‘Nor him?’ I asked her.\n\nLittle Em’ly shook her head. ‘Not to remember!’\n\nHere was a coincidence! I immediately went into an explanation how I had never seen my own father; and how my mother and I had always lived by ourselves in the happiest state imaginable, and lived so then, and always meant to live so; and how my father’s grave was in the churchyard near our house, and shaded by a tree, beneath the boughs of which I had walked and heard the birds sing many a pleasant morning. But there were some differences between Em’ly’s orphanhood and mine, it appeared. She had lost her mother before her father; and where her father’s grave was no one knew, except that it was somewhere in the depths of the sea.\n\n‘Besides,’ said Em’ly, as she looked about for shells and pebbles, ‘your father was a gentleman and your mother is a lady; and my father was a fisherman and my mother was a fisherman’s daughter, and my uncle Dan is a fisherman.’\n\n‘Dan is Mr. Peggotty, is he?’ said I.\n\n‘Uncle Dan--yonder,’ answered Em’ly, nodding at the boat-house.\n\n‘Yes. I mean him. He must be very good, I should think?’\n\n‘Good?’ said Em’ly. ‘If I was ever to be a lady, I’d give him a sky-blue coat with diamond buttons, nankeen trousers, a red velvet waistcoat, a cocked hat, a large gold watch, a silver pipe, and a box of money.’\n\nI said I had no doubt that Mr. Peggotty well deserved these treasures. I must acknowledge that I felt it difficult to picture him quite at his ease in the raiment proposed for him by his grateful little niece, and that I was particularly doubtful of the policy of the cocked hat; but I kept these sentiments to myself.\n\nLittle Em’ly had stopped and looked up at the sky in her enumeration of these articles, as if they were a glorious vision. We went on again, picking up shells and pebbles.\n\n‘You would like to be a lady?’ I said.\n\nEmily looked at me, and laughed and nodded ‘yes’.\n\n‘I should like it very much. We would all be gentlefolks together, then. Me, and uncle, and Ham, and Mrs. Gummidge. We wouldn’t mind then, when there comes stormy weather.---Not for our own sakes, I mean. We would for the poor fishermen’s, to be sure, and we’d help ‘em with money when they come to any hurt.’ This seemed to me to be a very satisfactory and therefore not at all improbable picture. I expressed my pleasure in the contemplation of it, and little Em’ly was emboldened to say, shyly,\n\n‘Don’t you think you are afraid of the sea, now?’\n\nIt was quiet enough to reassure me, but I have no doubt if I had seen a moderately large wave come tumbling in, I should have taken to my heels, with an awful recollection of her drowned relations. However, I said ‘No,’ and I added, ‘You don’t seem to be either, though you say you are,’--for she was walking much too near the brink of a sort of old jetty or wooden causeway we had strolled upon, and I was afraid of her falling over.\n\n‘I’m not afraid in this way,’ said little Em’ly. ‘But I wake when it blows, and tremble to think of Uncle Dan and Ham and believe I hear ‘em crying out for help. That’s why I should like so much to be a lady. But I’m not afraid in this way. Not a bit. Look here!’\n\nShe started from my side, and ran along a jagged timber which protruded from the place we stood upon, and overhung the deep water at some height, without the least defence. The incident is so impressed on my remembrance, that if I were a draughtsman I could draw its form here, I dare say, accurately as it was that day, and little Em’ly springing forward to her destruction (as it appeared to me), with a look that I have never forgotten, directed far out to sea.\n\nThe light, bold, fluttering little figure turned and came back safe to me, and I soon laughed at my fears, and at the cry I had uttered; fruitlessly in any case, for there was no one near. But there have been times since, in my manhood, many times there have been, when I have thought, Is it possible, among the possibilities of hidden things, that in the sudden rashness of the child and her wild look so far off, there was any merciful attraction of her into danger, any tempting her towards him permitted on the part of her dead father, that her life might have a chance of ending that day? There has been a time since when I have wondered whether, if the life before her could have been revealed to me at a glance, and so revealed as that a child could fully comprehend it, and if her preservation could have depended on a motion of my hand, I ought to have held it up to save her. There has been a time since--I do not say it lasted long, but it has been--when I have asked myself the question, would it have been better for little Em’ly to have had the waters close above her head that morning in my sight; and when I have answered Yes, it would have been.\n\nThis may be premature. I have set it down too soon, perhaps. But let it stand.\n\nWe strolled a long way, and loaded ourselves with things that we thought curious, and put some stranded starfish carefully back into the water--I hardly know enough of the race at this moment to be quite certain whether they had reason to feel obliged to us for doing so, or the reverse--and then made our way home to Mr. Peggotty’s dwelling. We stopped under the lee of the lobster-outhouse to exchange an innocent kiss, and went in to breakfast glowing with health and pleasure.\n\n‘Like two young mavishes,’ Mr. Peggotty said. I knew this meant, in our local dialect, like two young thrushes, and received it as a compliment.\n\nOf course I was in love with little Em’ly. I am sure I loved that baby quite as truly, quite as tenderly, with greater purity and more disinterestedness, than can enter into the best love of a later time of life, high and ennobling as it is. I am sure my fancy raised up something round that blue-eyed mite of a child, which etherealized, and made a very angel of her. If, any sunny forenoon, she had spread a little pair of wings and flown away before my eyes, I don’t think I should have regarded it as much more than I had had reason to expect.\n\nWe used to walk about that dim old flat at Yarmouth in a loving manner, hours and hours. The days sported by us, as if Time had not grown up himself yet, but were a child too, and always at play. I told Em’ly I adored her, and that unless she confessed she adored me I should be reduced to the necessity of killing myself with a sword. She said she did, and I have no doubt she did.\n\nAs to any sense of inequality, or youthfulness, or other difficulty in our way, little Em’ly and I had no such trouble, because we had no future. We made no more provision for growing older, than we did for growing younger. We were the admiration of Mrs. Gummidge and Peggotty, who used to whisper of an evening when we sat, lovingly, on our little locker side by side, ‘Lor! wasn’t it beautiful!’ Mr. Peggotty smiled at us from behind his pipe, and Ham grinned all the evening and did nothing else. They had something of the sort of pleasure in us, I suppose, that they might have had in a pretty toy, or a pocket model of the Colosseum.\n\nI soon found out that Mrs. Gummidge did not always make herself so agreeable as she might have been expected to do, under the circumstances of her residence with Mr. Peggotty. Mrs. Gummidge’s was rather a fretful disposition, and she whimpered more sometimes than was comfortable for other parties in so small an establishment. I was very sorry for her; but there were moments when it would have been more agreeable, I thought, if Mrs. Gummidge had had a convenient apartment of her own to retire to, and had stopped there until her spirits revived.\n\nMr. Peggotty went occasionally to a public-house called The Willing Mind. I discovered this, by his being out on the second or third evening of our visit, and by Mrs. Gummidge’s looking up at the Dutch clock, between eight and nine, and saying he was there, and that, what was more, she had known in the morning he would go there.\n\nMrs. Gummidge had been in a low state all day, and had burst into tears in the forenoon, when the fire smoked. ‘I am a lone lorn creetur’,’ were Mrs. Gummidge’s words, when that unpleasant occurrence took place, ‘and everythink goes contrary with me.’\n\n‘Oh, it’ll soon leave off,’ said Peggotty--I again mean our Peggotty--‘and besides, you know, it’s not more disagreeable to you than to us.’\n\n‘I feel it more,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.\n\nIt was a very cold day, with cutting blasts of wind. Mrs. Gummidge’s peculiar corner of the fireside seemed to me to be the warmest and snuggest in the place, as her chair was certainly the easiest, but it didn’t suit her that day at all. She was constantly complaining of the cold, and of its occasioning a visitation in her back which she called ‘the creeps’. At last she shed tears on that subject, and said again that she was ‘a lone lorn creetur’ and everythink went contrary with her’.\n\n‘It is certainly very cold,’ said Peggotty. ‘Everybody must feel it so.’\n\n‘I feel it more than other people,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.\n\nSo at dinner; when Mrs. Gummidge was always helped immediately after me, to whom the preference was given as a visitor of distinction. The fish were small and bony, and the potatoes were a little burnt. We all acknowledged that we felt this something of a disappointment; but Mrs. Gummidge said she felt it more than we did, and shed tears again, and made that former declaration with great bitterness.\n\nAccordingly, when Mr. Peggotty came home about nine o’clock, this unfortunate Mrs. Gummidge was knitting in her corner, in a very wretched and miserable condition. Peggotty had been working cheerfully. Ham had been patching up a great pair of waterboots; and I, with little Em’ly by my side, had been reading to them. Mrs. Gummidge had never made any other remark than a forlorn sigh, and had never raised her eyes since tea.\n\n‘Well, Mates,’ said Mr. Peggotty, taking his seat, ‘and how are you?’\n\nWe all said something, or looked something, to welcome him, except Mrs. Gummidge, who only shook her head over her knitting.\n\n‘What’s amiss?’ said Mr. Peggotty, with a clap of his hands. ‘Cheer up, old Mawther!’ (Mr. Peggotty meant old girl.)\n\nMrs. Gummidge did not appear to be able to cheer up. She took out an old black silk handkerchief and wiped her eyes; but instead of putting it in her pocket, kept it out, and wiped them again, and still kept it out, ready for use.\n\n‘What’s amiss, dame?’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘Nothing,’ returned Mrs. Gummidge. ‘You’ve come from The Willing Mind, Dan’l?’\n\n‘Why yes, I’ve took a short spell at The Willing Mind tonight,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘I’m sorry I should drive you there,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.\n\n‘Drive! I don’t want no driving,’ returned Mr. Peggotty with an honest laugh. ‘I only go too ready.’\n\n‘Very ready,’ said Mrs. Gummidge, shaking her head, and wiping her eyes. ‘Yes, yes, very ready. I am sorry it should be along of me that you’re so ready.’\n\n‘Along o’ you! It an’t along o’ you!’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘Don’t ye believe a bit on it.’\n\n‘Yes, yes, it is,’ cried Mrs. Gummidge. ‘I know what I am. I know that I am a lone lorn creetur’, and not only that everythink goes contrary with me, but that I go contrary with everybody. Yes, yes. I feel more than other people do, and I show it more. It’s my misfortun’.’\n\nI really couldn’t help thinking, as I sat taking in all this, that the misfortune extended to some other members of that family besides Mrs. Gummidge. But Mr. Peggotty made no such retort, only answering with another entreaty to Mrs. Gummidge to cheer up.\n\n‘I an’t what I could wish myself to be,’ said Mrs. Gummidge. ‘I am far from it. I know what I am. My troubles has made me contrary. I feel my troubles, and they make me contrary. I wish I didn’t feel ‘em, but I do. I wish I could be hardened to ‘em, but I an’t. I make the house uncomfortable. I don’t wonder at it. I’ve made your sister so all day, and Master Davy.’\n\nHere I was suddenly melted, and roared out, ‘No, you haven’t, Mrs. Gummidge,’ in great mental distress.\n\n‘It’s far from right that I should do it,’ said Mrs. Gummidge. ‘It an’t a fit return. I had better go into the house and die. I am a lone lorn creetur’, and had much better not make myself contrary here. If thinks must go contrary with me, and I must go contrary myself, let me go contrary in my parish. Dan’l, I’d better go into the house, and die and be a riddance!’\n\nMrs. Gummidge retired with these words, and betook herself to bed. When she was gone, Mr. Peggotty, who had not exhibited a trace of any feeling but the profoundest sympathy, looked round upon us, and nodding his head with a lively expression of that sentiment still animating his face, said in a whisper:\n\n‘She’s been thinking of the old ‘un!’\n\nI did not quite understand what old one Mrs. Gummidge was supposed to have fixed her mind upon, until Peggotty, on seeing me to bed, explained that it was the late Mr. Gummidge; and that her brother always took that for a received truth on such occasions, and that it always had a moving effect upon him. Some time after he was in his hammock that night, I heard him myself repeat to Ham, ‘Poor thing! She’s been thinking of the old ‘un!’ And whenever Mrs. Gummidge was overcome in a similar manner during the remainder of our stay (which happened some few times), he always said the same thing in extenuation of the circumstance, and always with the tenderest commiseration.\n\nSo the fortnight slipped away, varied by nothing but the variation of the tide, which altered Mr. Peggotty’s times of going out and coming in, and altered Ham’s engagements also. When the latter was unemployed, he sometimes walked with us to show us the boats and ships, and once or twice he took us for a row. I don’t know why one slight set of impressions should be more particularly associated with a place than another, though I believe this obtains with most people, in reference especially to the associations of their childhood. I never hear the name, or read the name, of Yarmouth, but I am reminded of a certain Sunday morning on the beach, the bells ringing for church, little Em’ly leaning on my shoulder, Ham lazily dropping stones into the water, and the sun, away at sea, just breaking through the heavy mist, and showing us the ships, like their own shadows.\n\nAt last the day came for going home. I bore up against the separation from Mr. Peggotty and Mrs. Gummidge, but my agony of mind at leaving little Em’ly was piercing. We went arm-in-arm to the public-house where the carrier put up, and I promised, on the road, to write to her. (I redeemed that promise afterwards, in characters larger than those in which apartments are usually announced in manuscript, as being to let.) We were greatly overcome at parting; and if ever, in my life, I have had a void made in my heart, I had one made that day.\n\nNow, all the time I had been on my visit, I had been ungrateful to my home again, and had thought little or nothing about it. But I was no sooner turned towards it, than my reproachful young conscience seemed to point that way with a ready finger; and I felt, all the more for the sinking of my spirits, that it was my nest, and that my mother was my comforter and friend.\n\nThis gained upon me as we went along; so that the nearer we drew, the more familiar the objects became that we passed, the more excited I was to get there, and to run into her arms. But Peggotty, instead of sharing in those transports, tried to check them (though very kindly), and looked confused and out of sorts.\n\nBlunderstone Rookery would come, however, in spite of her, when the carrier’s horse pleased--and did. How well I recollect it, on a cold grey afternoon, with a dull sky, threatening rain!\n\nThe door opened, and I looked, half laughing and half crying in my pleasant agitation, for my mother. It was not she, but a strange servant.\n\n‘Why, Peggotty!’ I said, ruefully, ‘isn’t she come home?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, Master Davy,’ said Peggotty. ‘She’s come home. Wait a bit, Master Davy, and I’ll--I’ll tell you something.’\n\nBetween her agitation, and her natural awkwardness in getting out of the cart, Peggotty was making a most extraordinary festoon of herself, but I felt too blank and strange to tell her so. When she had got down, she took me by the hand; led me, wondering, into the kitchen; and shut the door.\n\n‘Peggotty!’ said I, quite frightened. ‘What’s the matter?’\n\n‘Nothing’s the matter, bless you, Master Davy dear!’ she answered, assuming an air of sprightliness.\n\n‘Something’s the matter, I’m sure. Where’s mama?’\n\n‘Where’s mama, Master Davy?’ repeated Peggotty.\n\n‘Yes. Why hasn’t she come out to the gate, and what have we come in here for? Oh, Peggotty!’ My eyes were full, and I felt as if I were going to tumble down.\n\n‘Bless the precious boy!’ cried Peggotty, taking hold of me. ‘What is it? Speak, my pet!’\n\n‘Not dead, too! Oh, she’s not dead, Peggotty?’\n\nPeggotty cried out No! with an astonishing volume of voice; and then sat down, and began to pant, and said I had given her a turn.\n\nI gave her a hug to take away the turn, or to give her another turn in the right direction, and then stood before her, looking at her in anxious inquiry.\n\n‘You see, dear, I should have told you before now,’ said Peggotty, ‘but I hadn’t an opportunity. I ought to have made it, perhaps, but I couldn’t azackly’--that was always the substitute for exactly, in Peggotty’s militia of words--‘bring my mind to it.’\n\n‘Go on, Peggotty,’ said I, more frightened than before.\n\n‘Master Davy,’ said Peggotty, untying her bonnet with a shaking hand, and speaking in a breathless sort of way. ‘What do you think? You have got a Pa!’\n\nI trembled, and turned white. Something--I don’t know what, or how--connected with the grave in the churchyard, and the raising of the dead, seemed to strike me like an unwholesome wind.\n\n‘A new one,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘A new one?’ I repeated.\n\nPeggotty gave a gasp, as if she were swallowing something that was very hard, and, putting out her hand, said:\n\n‘Come and see him.’\n\n‘I don’t want to see him.’\n\n--‘And your mama,’ said Peggotty.\n\nI ceased to draw back, and we went straight to the best parlour, where she left me. On one side of the fire, sat my mother; on the other, Mr. Murdstone. My mother dropped her work, and arose hurriedly, but timidly I thought.\n\n‘Now, Clara my dear,’ said Mr. Murdstone. ‘Recollect! control yourself, always control yourself! Davy boy, how do you do?’\n\nI gave him my hand. After a moment of suspense, I went and kissed my mother: she kissed me, patted me gently on the shoulder, and sat down again to her work. I could not look at her, I could not look at him, I knew quite well that he was looking at us both; and I turned to the window and looked out there, at some shrubs that were drooping their heads in the cold.\n\nAs soon as I could creep away, I crept upstairs. My old dear bedroom was changed, and I was to lie a long way off. I rambled downstairs to find anything that was like itself, so altered it all seemed; and roamed into the yard. I very soon started back from there, for the empty dog-kennel was filled up with a great dog--deep mouthed and black-haired like Him--and he was very angry at the sight of me, and sprang out to get at me.\n\n# CHAPTER 4. I FALL INTO DISGRACE\n\nIf the room to which my bed was removed were a sentient thing that could give evidence, I might appeal to it at this day--who sleeps there now, I wonder!--to bear witness for me what a heavy heart I carried to it. I went up there, hearing the dog in the yard bark after me all the way while I climbed the stairs; and, looking as blank and strange upon the room as the room looked upon me, sat down with my small hands crossed, and thought.\n\nI thought of the oddest things. Of the shape of the room, of the cracks in the ceiling, of the paper on the walls, of the flaws in the window-glass making ripples and dimples on the prospect, of the washing-stand being rickety on its three legs, and having a discontented something about it, which reminded me of Mrs. Gummidge under the influence of the old one. I was crying all the time, but, except that I was conscious of being cold and dejected, I am sure I never thought why I cried. At last in my desolation I began to consider that I was dreadfully in love with little Em’ly, and had been torn away from her to come here where no one seemed to want me, or to care about me, half as much as she did. This made such a very miserable piece of business of it, that I rolled myself up in a corner of the counterpane, and cried myself to sleep.\n\nI was awoke by somebody saying ‘Here he is!’ and uncovering my hot head. My mother and Peggotty had come to look for me, and it was one of them who had done it.\n\n‘Davy,’ said my mother. ‘What’s the matter?’\n\nI thought it was very strange that she should ask me, and answered, ‘Nothing.’ I turned over on my face, I recollect, to hide my trembling lip, which answered her with greater truth. ‘Davy,’ said my mother. ‘Davy, my child!’\n\nI dare say no words she could have uttered would have affected me so much, then, as her calling me her child. I hid my tears in the bedclothes, and pressed her from me with my hand, when she would have raised me up.\n\n‘This is your doing, Peggotty, you cruel thing!’ said my mother. ‘I have no doubt at all about it. How can you reconcile it to your conscience, I wonder, to prejudice my own boy against me, or against anybody who is dear to me? What do you mean by it, Peggotty?’\n\nPoor Peggotty lifted up her hands and eyes, and only answered, in a sort of paraphrase of the grace I usually repeated after dinner, ‘Lord forgive you, Mrs. Copperfield, and for what you have said this minute, may you never be truly sorry!’\n\n‘It’s enough to distract me,’ cried my mother. ‘In my honeymoon, too, when my most inveterate enemy might relent, one would think, and not envy me a little peace of mind and happiness. Davy, you naughty boy! Peggotty, you savage creature! Oh, dear me!’ cried my mother, turning from one of us to the other, in her pettish wilful manner, ‘what a troublesome world this is, when one has the most right to expect it to be as agreeable as possible!’\n\nI felt the touch of a hand that I knew was neither hers nor Peggotty’s, and slipped to my feet at the bed-side. It was Mr. Murdstone’s hand, and he kept it on my arm as he said:\n\n‘What’s this? Clara, my love, have you forgotten?--Firmness, my dear!’\n\n‘I am very sorry, Edward,’ said my mother. ‘I meant to be very good, but I am so uncomfortable.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ he answered. ‘That’s a bad hearing, so soon, Clara.’\n\n‘I say it’s very hard I should be made so now,’ returned my mother, pouting; ‘and it is--very hard--isn’t it?’\n\nHe drew her to him, whispered in her ear, and kissed her. I knew as well, when I saw my mother’s head lean down upon his shoulder, and her arm touch his neck--I knew as well that he could mould her pliant nature into any form he chose, as I know, now, that he did it.\n\n‘Go you below, my love,’ said Mr. Murdstone. ‘David and I will come down, together. My friend,’ turning a darkening face on Peggotty, when he had watched my mother out, and dismissed her with a nod and a smile; ‘do you know your mistress’s name?’\n\n‘She has been my mistress a long time, sir,’ answered Peggotty, ‘I ought to know it.’ ‘That’s true,’ he answered. ‘But I thought I heard you, as I came upstairs, address her by a name that is not hers. She has taken mine, you know. Will you remember that?’\n\nPeggotty, with some uneasy glances at me, curtseyed herself out of the room without replying; seeing, I suppose, that she was expected to go, and had no excuse for remaining. When we two were left alone, he shut the door, and sitting on a chair, and holding me standing before him, looked steadily into my eyes. I felt my own attracted, no less steadily, to his. As I recall our being opposed thus, face to face, I seem again to hear my heart beat fast and high.\n\n‘David,’ he said, making his lips thin, by pressing them together, ‘if I have an obstinate horse or dog to deal with, what do you think I do?’\n\n‘I don’t know.’\n\n‘I beat him.’\n\nI had answered in a kind of breathless whisper, but I felt, in my silence, that my breath was shorter now.\n\n‘I make him wince, and smart. I say to myself, “I’ll conquer that fellow”; and if it were to cost him all the blood he had, I should do it. What is that upon your face?’\n\n‘Dirt,’ I said.\n\nHe knew it was the mark of tears as well as I. But if he had asked the question twenty times, each time with twenty blows, I believe my baby heart would have burst before I would have told him so.\n\n‘You have a good deal of intelligence for a little fellow,’ he said, with a grave smile that belonged to him, ‘and you understood me very well, I see. Wash that face, sir, and come down with me.’\n\nHe pointed to the washing-stand, which I had made out to be like Mrs. Gummidge, and motioned me with his head to obey him directly. I had little doubt then, and I have less doubt now, that he would have knocked me down without the least compunction, if I had hesitated.\n\n‘Clara, my dear,’ he said, when I had done his bidding, and he walked me into the parlour, with his hand still on my arm; ‘you will not be made uncomfortable any more, I hope. We shall soon improve our youthful humours.’\n\nGod help me, I might have been improved for my whole life, I might have been made another creature perhaps, for life, by a kind word at that season. A word of encouragement and explanation, of pity for my childish ignorance, of welcome home, of reassurance to me that it was home, might have made me dutiful to him in my heart henceforth, instead of in my hypocritical outside, and might have made me respect instead of hate him. I thought my mother was sorry to see me standing in the room so scared and strange, and that, presently, when I stole to a chair, she followed me with her eyes more sorrowfully still--missing, perhaps, some freedom in my childish tread--but the word was not spoken, and the time for it was gone.\n\nWe dined alone, we three together. He seemed to be very fond of my mother--I am afraid I liked him none the better for that--and she was very fond of him. I gathered from what they said, that an elder sister of his was coming to stay with them, and that she was expected that evening. I am not certain whether I found out then, or afterwards, that, without being actively concerned in any business, he had some share in, or some annual charge upon the profits of, a wine-merchant’s house in London, with which his family had been connected from his great-grandfather’s time, and in which his sister had a similar interest; but I may mention it in this place, whether or no.\n\nAfter dinner, when we were sitting by the fire, and I was meditating an escape to Peggotty without having the hardihood to slip away, lest it should offend the master of the house, a coach drove up to the garden-gate and he went out to receive the visitor. My mother followed him. I was timidly following her, when she turned round at the parlour door, in the dusk, and taking me in her embrace as she had been used to do, whispered me to love my new father and be obedient to him. She did this hurriedly and secretly, as if it were wrong, but tenderly; and, putting out her hand behind her, held mine in it, until we came near to where he was standing in the garden, where she let mine go, and drew hers through his arm.\n\nIt was Miss Murdstone who was arrived, and a gloomy-looking lady she was; dark, like her brother, whom she greatly resembled in face and voice; and with very heavy eyebrows, nearly meeting over her large nose, as if, being disabled by the wrongs of her sex from wearing whiskers, she had carried them to that account. She brought with her two uncompromising hard black boxes, with her initials on the lids in hard brass nails. When she paid the coachman she took her money out of a hard steel purse, and she kept the purse in a very jail of a bag which hung upon her arm by a heavy chain, and shut up like a bite. I had never, at that time, seen such a metallic lady altogether as Miss Murdstone was.\n\nShe was brought into the parlour with many tokens of welcome, and there formally recognized my mother as a new and near relation. Then she looked at me, and said:\n\n‘Is that your boy, sister-in-law?’\n\nMy mother acknowledged me.\n\n‘Generally speaking,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘I don’t like boys. How d’ye do, boy?’\n\nUnder these encouraging circumstances, I replied that I was very well, and that I hoped she was the same; with such an indifferent grace, that Miss Murdstone disposed of me in two words:\n\n‘Wants manner!’\n\nHaving uttered which, with great distinctness, she begged the favour of being shown to her room, which became to me from that time forth a place of awe and dread, wherein the two black boxes were never seen open or known to be left unlocked, and where (for I peeped in once or twice when she was out) numerous little steel fetters and rivets, with which Miss Murdstone embellished herself when she was dressed, generally hung upon the looking-glass in formidable array.\n\nAs well as I could make out, she had come for good, and had no intention of ever going again. She began to ‘help’ my mother next morning, and was in and out of the store-closet all day, putting things to rights, and making havoc in the old arrangements. Almost the first remarkable thing I observed in Miss Murdstone was, her being constantly haunted by a suspicion that the servants had a man secreted somewhere on the premises. Under the influence of this delusion, she dived into the coal-cellar at the most untimely hours, and scarcely ever opened the door of a dark cupboard without clapping it to again, in the belief that she had got him.\n\nThough there was nothing very airy about Miss Murdstone, she was a perfect Lark in point of getting up. She was up (and, as I believe to this hour, looking for that man) before anybody in the house was stirring. Peggotty gave it as her opinion that she even slept with one eye open; but I could not concur in this idea; for I tried it myself after hearing the suggestion thrown out, and found it couldn’t be done.\n\nOn the very first morning after her arrival she was up and ringing her bell at cock-crow. When my mother came down to breakfast and was going to make the tea, Miss Murdstone gave her a kind of peck on the cheek, which was her nearest approach to a kiss, and said:\n\n‘Now, Clara, my dear, I am come here, you know, to relieve you of all the trouble I can. You’re much too pretty and thoughtless’--my mother blushed but laughed, and seemed not to dislike this character--‘to have any duties imposed upon you that can be undertaken by me. If you’ll be so good as give me your keys, my dear, I’ll attend to all this sort of thing in future.’\n\nFrom that time, Miss Murdstone kept the keys in her own little jail all day, and under her pillow all night, and my mother had no more to do with them than I had.\n\nMy mother did not suffer her authority to pass from her without a shadow of protest. One night when Miss Murdstone had been developing certain household plans to her brother, of which he signified his approbation, my mother suddenly began to cry, and said she thought she might have been consulted.\n\n‘Clara!’ said Mr. Murdstone sternly. ‘Clara! I wonder at you.’\n\n‘Oh, it’s very well to say you wonder, Edward!’ cried my mother, ‘and it’s very well for you to talk about firmness, but you wouldn’t like it yourself.’\n\nFirmness, I may observe, was the grand quality on which both Mr. and Miss Murdstone took their stand. However I might have expressed my comprehension of it at that time, if I had been called upon, I nevertheless did clearly comprehend in my own way, that it was another name for tyranny; and for a certain gloomy, arrogant, devil’s humour, that was in them both. The creed, as I should state it now, was this. Mr. Murdstone was firm; nobody in his world was to be so firm as Mr. Murdstone; nobody else in his world was to be firm at all, for everybody was to be bent to his firmness. Miss Murdstone was an exception. She might be firm, but only by relationship, and in an inferior and tributary degree. My mother was another exception. She might be firm, and must be; but only in bearing their firmness, and firmly believing there was no other firmness upon earth.\n\n‘It’s very hard,’ said my mother, ‘that in my own house--’\n\n‘My own house?’ repeated Mr. Murdstone. ‘Clara!’\n\n‘OUR own house, I mean,’ faltered my mother, evidently frightened--‘I hope you must know what I mean, Edward--it’s very hard that in YOUR own house I may not have a word to say about domestic matters. I am sure I managed very well before we were married. There’s evidence,’ said my mother, sobbing; ‘ask Peggotty if I didn’t do very well when I wasn’t interfered with!’\n\n‘Edward,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘let there be an end of this. I go tomorrow.’\n\n‘Jane Murdstone,’ said her brother, ‘be silent! How dare you to insinuate that you don’t know my character better than your words imply?’\n\n‘I am sure,’ my poor mother went on, at a grievous disadvantage, and with many tears, ‘I don’t want anybody to go. I should be very miserable and unhappy if anybody was to go. I don’t ask much. I am not unreasonable. I only want to be consulted sometimes. I am very much obliged to anybody who assists me, and I only want to be consulted as a mere form, sometimes. I thought you were pleased, once, with my being a little inexperienced and girlish, Edward--I am sure you said so--but you seem to hate me for it now, you are so severe.’\n\n‘Edward,’ said Miss Murdstone, again, ‘let there be an end of this. I go tomorrow.’\n\n‘Jane Murdstone,’ thundered Mr. Murdstone. ‘Will you be silent? How dare you?’\n\nMiss Murdstone made a jail-delivery of her pocket-handkerchief, and held it before her eyes.\n\n‘Clara,’ he continued, looking at my mother, ‘you surprise me! You astound me! Yes, I had a satisfaction in the thought of marrying an inexperienced and artless person, and forming her character, and infusing into it some amount of that firmness and decision of which it stood in need. But when Jane Murdstone is kind enough to come to my assistance in this endeavour, and to assume, for my sake, a condition something like a housekeeper’s, and when she meets with a base return--’\n\n‘Oh, pray, pray, Edward,’ cried my mother, ‘don’t accuse me of being ungrateful. I am sure I am not ungrateful. No one ever said I was before. I have many faults, but not that. Oh, don’t, my dear!’\n\n‘When Jane Murdstone meets, I say,’ he went on, after waiting until my mother was silent, ‘with a base return, that feeling of mine is chilled and altered.’\n\n‘Don’t, my love, say that!’ implored my mother very piteously. ‘Oh, don’t, Edward! I can’t bear to hear it. Whatever I am, I am affectionate. I know I am affectionate. I wouldn’t say it, if I wasn’t sure that I am. Ask Peggotty. I am sure she’ll tell you I’m affectionate.’\n\n‘There is no extent of mere weakness, Clara,’ said Mr. Murdstone in reply, ‘that can have the least weight with me. You lose breath.’\n\n‘Pray let us be friends,’ said my mother, ‘I couldn’t live under coldness or unkindness. I am so sorry. I have a great many defects, I know, and it’s very good of you, Edward, with your strength of mind, to endeavour to correct them for me. Jane, I don’t object to anything. I should be quite broken-hearted if you thought of leaving--’ My mother was too much overcome to go on.\n\n‘Jane Murdstone,’ said Mr. Murdstone to his sister, ‘any harsh words between us are, I hope, uncommon. It is not my fault that so unusual an occurrence has taken place tonight. I was betrayed into it by another. Nor is it your fault. You were betrayed into it by another. Let us both try to forget it. And as this,’ he added, after these magnanimous words, ‘is not a fit scene for the boy--David, go to bed!’\n\nI could hardly find the door, through the tears that stood in my eyes. I was so sorry for my mother’s distress; but I groped my way out, and groped my way up to my room in the dark, without even having the heart to say good night to Peggotty, or to get a candle from her. When her coming up to look for me, an hour or so afterwards, awoke me, she said that my mother had gone to bed poorly, and that Mr. and Miss Murdstone were sitting alone.\n\nGoing down next morning rather earlier than usual, I paused outside the parlour door, on hearing my mother’s voice. She was very earnestly and humbly entreating Miss Murdstone’s pardon, which that lady granted, and a perfect reconciliation took place. I never knew my mother afterwards to give an opinion on any matter, without first appealing to Miss Murdstone, or without having first ascertained by some sure means, what Miss Murdstone’s opinion was; and I never saw Miss Murdstone, when out of temper (she was infirm that way), move her hand towards her bag as if she were going to take out the keys and offer to resign them to my mother, without seeing that my mother was in a terrible fright.\n\nThe gloomy taint that was in the Murdstone blood, darkened the Murdstone religion, which was austere and wrathful. I have thought, since, that its assuming that character was a necessary consequence of Mr. Murdstone’s firmness, which wouldn’t allow him to let anybody off from the utmost weight of the severest penalties he could find any excuse for. Be this as it may, I well remember the tremendous visages with which we used to go to church, and the changed air of the place. Again, the dreaded Sunday comes round, and I file into the old pew first, like a guarded captive brought to a condemned service. Again, Miss Murdstone, in a black velvet gown, that looks as if it had been made out of a pall, follows close upon me; then my mother; then her husband. There is no Peggotty now, as in the old time. Again, I listen to Miss Murdstone mumbling the responses, and emphasizing all the dread words with a cruel relish. Again, I see her dark eyes roll round the church when she says ‘miserable sinners’, as if she were calling all the congregation names. Again, I catch rare glimpses of my mother, moving her lips timidly between the two, with one of them muttering at each ear like low thunder. Again, I wonder with a sudden fear whether it is likely that our good old clergyman can be wrong, and Mr. and Miss Murdstone right, and that all the angels in Heaven can be destroying angels. Again, if I move a finger or relax a muscle of my face, Miss Murdstone pokes me with her prayer-book, and makes my side ache.\n\nYes, and again, as we walk home, I note some neighbours looking at my mother and at me, and whispering. Again, as the three go on arm-in-arm, and I linger behind alone, I follow some of those looks, and wonder if my mother’s step be really not so light as I have seen it, and if the gaiety of her beauty be really almost worried away. Again, I wonder whether any of the neighbours call to mind, as I do, how we used to walk home together, she and I; and I wonder stupidly about that, all the dreary dismal day.\n\nThere had been some talk on occasions of my going to boarding-school. Mr. and Miss Murdstone had originated it, and my mother had of course agreed with them. Nothing, however, was concluded on the subject yet. In the meantime, I learnt lessons at home. Shall I ever forget those lessons! They were presided over nominally by my mother, but really by Mr. Murdstone and his sister, who were always present, and found them a favourable occasion for giving my mother lessons in that miscalled firmness, which was the bane of both our lives. I believe I was kept at home for that purpose. I had been apt enough to learn, and willing enough, when my mother and I had lived alone together. I can faintly remember learning the alphabet at her knee. To this day, when I look upon the fat black letters in the primer, the puzzling novelty of their shapes, and the easy good-nature of O and Q and S, seem to present themselves again before me as they used to do. But they recall no feeling of disgust or reluctance. On the contrary, I seem to have walked along a path of flowers as far as the crocodile-book, and to have been cheered by the gentleness of my mother’s voice and manner all the way. But these solemn lessons which succeeded those, I remember as the death-blow of my peace, and a grievous daily drudgery and misery. They were very long, very numerous, very hard--perfectly unintelligible, some of them, to me--and I was generally as much bewildered by them as I believe my poor mother was herself.\n\nLet me remember how it used to be, and bring one morning back again.\n\nI come into the second-best parlour after breakfast, with my books, and an exercise-book, and a slate. My mother is ready for me at her writing-desk, but not half so ready as Mr. Murdstone in his easy-chair by the window (though he pretends to be reading a book), or as Miss Murdstone, sitting near my mother stringing steel beads. The very sight of these two has such an influence over me, that I begin to feel the words I have been at infinite pains to get into my head, all sliding away, and going I don’t know where. I wonder where they do go, by the by?\n\nI hand the first book to my mother. Perhaps it is a grammar, perhaps a history, or geography. I take a last drowning look at the page as I give it into her hand, and start off aloud at a racing pace while I have got it fresh. I trip over a word. Mr. Murdstone looks up. I trip over another word. Miss Murdstone looks up. I redden, tumble over half-a-dozen words, and stop. I think my mother would show me the book if she dared, but she does not dare, and she says softly:\n\n‘Oh, Davy, Davy!’\n\n‘Now, Clara,’ says Mr. Murdstone, ‘be firm with the boy. Don’t say, “Oh, Davy, Davy!” That’s childish. He knows his lesson, or he does not know it.’\n\n‘He does NOT know it,’ Miss Murdstone interposes awfully.\n\n‘I am really afraid he does not,’ says my mother.\n\n‘Then, you see, Clara,’ returns Miss Murdstone, ‘you should just give him the book back, and make him know it.’\n\n‘Yes, certainly,’ says my mother; ‘that is what I intend to do, my dear Jane. Now, Davy, try once more, and don’t be stupid.’\n\nI obey the first clause of the injunction by trying once more, but am not so successful with the second, for I am very stupid. I tumble down before I get to the old place, at a point where I was all right before, and stop to think. But I can’t think about the lesson. I think of the number of yards of net in Miss Murdstone’s cap, or of the price of Mr. Murdstone’s dressing-gown, or any such ridiculous problem that I have no business with, and don’t want to have anything at all to do with. Mr. Murdstone makes a movement of impatience which I have been expecting for a long time. Miss Murdstone does the same. My mother glances submissively at them, shuts the book, and lays it by as an arrear to be worked out when my other tasks are done.\n\nThere is a pile of these arrears very soon, and it swells like a rolling snowball. The bigger it gets, the more stupid I get. The case is so hopeless, and I feel that I am wallowing in such a bog of nonsense, that I give up all idea of getting out, and abandon myself to my fate. The despairing way in which my mother and I look at each other, as I blunder on, is truly melancholy. But the greatest effect in these miserable lessons is when my mother (thinking nobody is observing her) tries to give me the cue by the motion of her lips. At that instant, Miss Murdstone, who has been lying in wait for nothing else all along, says in a deep warning voice:\n\n‘Clara!’\n\nMy mother starts, colours, and smiles faintly. Mr. Murdstone comes out of his chair, takes the book, throws it at me or boxes my ears with it, and turns me out of the room by the shoulders.\n\nEven when the lessons are done, the worst is yet to happen, in the shape of an appalling sum. This is invented for me, and delivered to me orally by Mr. Murdstone, and begins, ‘If I go into a cheesemonger’s shop, and buy five thousand double-Gloucester cheeses at fourpence-halfpenny each, present payment’--at which I see Miss Murdstone secretly overjoyed. I pore over these cheeses without any result or enlightenment until dinner-time, when, having made a Mulatto of myself by getting the dirt of the slate into the pores of my skin, I have a slice of bread to help me out with the cheeses, and am considered in disgrace for the rest of the evening.\n\nIt seems to me, at this distance of time, as if my unfortunate studies generally took this course. I could have done very well if I had been without the Murdstones; but the influence of the Murdstones upon me was like the fascination of two snakes on a wretched young bird. Even when I did get through the morning with tolerable credit, there was not much gained but dinner; for Miss Murdstone never could endure to see me untasked, and if I rashly made any show of being unemployed, called her brother’s attention to me by saying, ‘Clara, my dear, there’s nothing like work--give your boy an exercise’; which caused me to be clapped down to some new labour, there and then. As to any recreation with other children of my age, I had very little of that; for the gloomy theology of the Murdstones made all children out to be a swarm of little vipers (though there WAS a child once set in the midst of the Disciples), and held that they contaminated one another.\n\nThe natural result of this treatment, continued, I suppose, for some six months or more, was to make me sullen, dull, and dogged. I was not made the less so by my sense of being daily more and more shut out and alienated from my mother. I believe I should have been almost stupefied but for one circumstance.\n\nIt was this. My father had left a small collection of books in a little room upstairs, to which I had access (for it adjoined my own) and which nobody else in our house ever troubled. From that blessed little room, Roderick Random, Peregrine Pickle, Humphrey Clinker, Tom Jones, the Vicar of Wakefield, Don Quixote, Gil Blas, and Robinson Crusoe, came out, a glorious host, to keep me company. They kept alive my fancy, and my hope of something beyond that place and time,--they, and the Arabian Nights, and the Tales of the Genii,--and did me no harm; for whatever harm was in some of them was not there for me; I knew nothing of it. It is astonishing to me now, how I found time, in the midst of my porings and blunderings over heavier themes, to read those books as I did. It is curious to me how I could ever have consoled myself under my small troubles (which were great troubles to me), by impersonating my favourite characters in them--as I did--and by putting Mr. and Miss Murdstone into all the bad ones--which I did too. I have been Tom Jones (a child’s Tom Jones, a harmless creature) for a week together. I have sustained my own idea of Roderick Random for a month at a stretch, I verily believe. I had a greedy relish for a few volumes of Voyages and Travels--I forget what, now--that were on those shelves; and for days and days I can remember to have gone about my region of our house, armed with the centre-piece out of an old set of boot-trees--the perfect realization of Captain Somebody, of the Royal British Navy, in danger of being beset by savages, and resolved to sell his life at a great price. The Captain never lost dignity, from having his ears boxed with the Latin Grammar. I did; but the Captain was a Captain and a hero, in despite of all the grammars of all the languages in the world, dead or alive.\n\nThis was my only and my constant comfort. When I think of it, the picture always rises in my mind, of a summer evening, the boys at play in the churchyard, and I sitting on my bed, reading as if for life. Every barn in the neighbourhood, every stone in the church, and every foot of the churchyard, had some association of its own, in my mind, connected with these books, and stood for some locality made famous in them. I have seen Tom Pipes go climbing up the church-steeple; I have watched Strap, with the knapsack on his back, stopping to rest himself upon the wicket-gate; and I know that Commodore Trunnion held that club with Mr. Pickle, in the parlour of our little village alehouse.\n\nThe reader now understands, as well as I do, what I was when I came to that point of my youthful history to which I am now coming again.\n\nOne morning when I went into the parlour with my books, I found my mother looking anxious, Miss Murdstone looking firm, and Mr. Murdstone binding something round the bottom of a cane--a lithe and limber cane, which he left off binding when I came in, and poised and switched in the air.\n\n‘I tell you, Clara,’ said Mr. Murdstone, ‘I have been often flogged myself.’\n\n‘To be sure; of course,’ said Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘Certainly, my dear Jane,’ faltered my mother, meekly. ‘But--but do you think it did Edward good?’\n\n‘Do you think it did Edward harm, Clara?’ asked Mr. Murdstone, gravely.\n\n‘That’s the point,’ said his sister.\n\nTo this my mother returned, ‘Certainly, my dear Jane,’ and said no more.\n\nI felt apprehensive that I was personally interested in this dialogue, and sought Mr. Murdstone’s eye as it lighted on mine.\n\n‘Now, David,’ he said--and I saw that cast again as he said it--‘you must be far more careful today than usual.’ He gave the cane another poise, and another switch; and having finished his preparation of it, laid it down beside him, with an impressive look, and took up his book.\n\nThis was a good freshener to my presence of mind, as a beginning. I felt the words of my lessons slipping off, not one by one, or line by line, but by the entire page; I tried to lay hold of them; but they seemed, if I may so express it, to have put skates on, and to skim away from me with a smoothness there was no checking.\n\nWe began badly, and went on worse. I had come in with an idea of distinguishing myself rather, conceiving that I was very well prepared; but it turned out to be quite a mistake. Book after book was added to the heap of failures, Miss Murdstone being firmly watchful of us all the time. And when we came at last to the five thousand cheeses (canes he made it that day, I remember), my mother burst out crying.\n\n‘Clara!’ said Miss Murdstone, in her warning voice.\n\n‘I am not quite well, my dear Jane, I think,’ said my mother.\n\nI saw him wink, solemnly, at his sister, as he rose and said, taking up the cane:\n\n‘Why, Jane, we can hardly expect Clara to bear, with perfect firmness, the worry and torment that David has occasioned her today. That would be stoical. Clara is greatly strengthened and improved, but we can hardly expect so much from her. David, you and I will go upstairs, boy.’\n\nAs he took me out at the door, my mother ran towards us. Miss Murdstone said, ‘Clara! are you a perfect fool?’ and interfered. I saw my mother stop her ears then, and I heard her crying.\n\nHe walked me up to my room slowly and gravely--I am certain he had a delight in that formal parade of executing justice--and when we got there, suddenly twisted my head under his arm.\n\n‘Mr. Murdstone! Sir!’ I cried to him. ‘Don’t! Pray don’t beat me! I have tried to learn, sir, but I can’t learn while you and Miss Murdstone are by. I can’t indeed!’\n\n‘Can’t you, indeed, David?’ he said. ‘We’ll try that.’\n\nHe had my head as in a vice, but I twined round him somehow, and stopped him for a moment, entreating him not to beat me. It was only a moment that I stopped him, for he cut me heavily an instant afterwards, and in the same instant I caught the hand with which he held me in my mouth, between my teeth, and bit it through. It sets my teeth on edge to think of it.\n\nHe beat me then, as if he would have beaten me to death. Above all the noise we made, I heard them running up the stairs, and crying out--I heard my mother crying out--and Peggotty. Then he was gone; and the door was locked outside; and I was lying, fevered and hot, and torn, and sore, and raging in my puny way, upon the floor.\n\nHow well I recollect, when I became quiet, what an unnatural stillness seemed to reign through the whole house! How well I remember, when my smart and passion began to cool, how wicked I began to feel!\n\nI sat listening for a long while, but there was not a sound. I crawled up from the floor, and saw my face in the glass, so swollen, red, and ugly that it almost frightened me. My stripes were sore and stiff, and made me cry afresh, when I moved; but they were nothing to the guilt I felt. It lay heavier on my breast than if I had been a most atrocious criminal, I dare say.\n\nIt had begun to grow dark, and I had shut the window (I had been lying, for the most part, with my head upon the sill, by turns crying, dozing, and looking listlessly out), when the key was turned, and Miss Murdstone came in with some bread and meat, and milk. These she put down upon the table without a word, glaring at me the while with exemplary firmness, and then retired, locking the door after her.\n\nLong after it was dark I sat there, wondering whether anybody else would come. When this appeared improbable for that night, I undressed, and went to bed; and, there, I began to wonder fearfully what would be done to me. Whether it was a criminal act that I had committed? Whether I should be taken into custody, and sent to prison? Whether I was at all in danger of being hanged?\n\nI never shall forget the waking, next morning; the being cheerful and fresh for the first moment, and then the being weighed down by the stale and dismal oppression of remembrance. Miss Murdstone reappeared before I was out of bed; told me, in so many words, that I was free to walk in the garden for half an hour and no longer; and retired, leaving the door open, that I might avail myself of that permission.\n\nI did so, and did so every morning of my imprisonment, which lasted five days. If I could have seen my mother alone, I should have gone down on my knees to her and besought her forgiveness; but I saw no one, Miss Murdstone excepted, during the whole time--except at evening prayers in the parlour; to which I was escorted by Miss Murdstone after everybody else was placed; where I was stationed, a young outlaw, all alone by myself near the door; and whence I was solemnly conducted by my jailer, before any one arose from the devotional posture. I only observed that my mother was as far off from me as she could be, and kept her face another way so that I never saw it; and that Mr. Murdstone’s hand was bound up in a large linen wrapper.\n\nThe length of those five days I can convey no idea of to any one. They occupy the place of years in my remembrance. The way in which I listened to all the incidents of the house that made themselves audible to me; the ringing of bells, the opening and shutting of doors, the murmuring of voices, the footsteps on the stairs; to any laughing, whistling, or singing, outside, which seemed more dismal than anything else to me in my solitude and disgrace--the uncertain pace of the hours, especially at night, when I would wake thinking it was morning, and find that the family were not yet gone to bed, and that all the length of night had yet to come--the depressed dreams and nightmares I had--the return of day, noon, afternoon, evening, when the boys played in the churchyard, and I watched them from a distance within the room, being ashamed to show myself at the window lest they should know I was a prisoner--the strange sensation of never hearing myself speak--the fleeting intervals of something like cheerfulness, which came with eating and drinking, and went away with it--the setting in of rain one evening, with a fresh smell, and its coming down faster and faster between me and the church, until it and gathering night seemed to quench me in gloom, and fear, and remorse--all this appears to have gone round and round for years instead of days, it is so vividly and strongly stamped on my remembrance. On the last night of my restraint, I was awakened by hearing my own name spoken in a whisper. I started up in bed, and putting out my arms in the dark, said:\n\n‘Is that you, Peggotty?’\n\nThere was no immediate answer, but presently I heard my name again, in a tone so very mysterious and awful, that I think I should have gone into a fit, if it had not occurred to me that it must have come through the keyhole.\n\nI groped my way to the door, and putting my own lips to the keyhole, whispered: ‘Is that you, Peggotty dear?’\n\n‘Yes, my own precious Davy,’ she replied. ‘Be as soft as a mouse, or the Cat’ll hear us.’\n\nI understood this to mean Miss Murdstone, and was sensible of the urgency of the case; her room being close by.\n\n‘How’s mama, dear Peggotty? Is she very angry with me?’\n\nI could hear Peggotty crying softly on her side of the keyhole, as I was doing on mine, before she answered. ‘No. Not very.’\n\n‘What is going to be done with me, Peggotty dear? Do you know?’\n\n‘School. Near London,’ was Peggotty’s answer. I was obliged to get her to repeat it, for she spoke it the first time quite down my throat, in consequence of my having forgotten to take my mouth away from the keyhole and put my ear there; and though her words tickled me a good deal, I didn’t hear them.\n\n‘When, Peggotty?’\n\n‘Tomorrow.’\n\n‘Is that the reason why Miss Murdstone took the clothes out of my drawers?’ which she had done, though I have forgotten to mention it.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Peggotty. ‘Box.’\n\n‘Shan’t I see mama?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Peggotty. ‘Morning.’\n\nThen Peggotty fitted her mouth close to the keyhole, and delivered these words through it with as much feeling and earnestness as a keyhole has ever been the medium of communicating, I will venture to assert: shooting in each broken little sentence in a convulsive little burst of its own.\n\n‘Davy, dear. If I ain’t been azackly as intimate with you. Lately, as I used to be. It ain’t because I don’t love you. Just as well and more, my pretty poppet. It’s because I thought it better for you. And for someone else besides. Davy, my darling, are you listening? Can you hear?’\n\n‘Ye-ye-ye-yes, Peggotty!’ I sobbed.\n\n‘My own!’ said Peggotty, with infinite compassion. ‘What I want to say, is. That you must never forget me. For I’ll never forget you. And I’ll take as much care of your mama, Davy. As ever I took of you. And I won’t leave her. The day may come when she’ll be glad to lay her poor head. On her stupid, cross old Peggotty’s arm again. And I’ll write to you, my dear. Though I ain’t no scholar. And I’ll--I’ll--’ Peggotty fell to kissing the keyhole, as she couldn’t kiss me.\n\n‘Thank you, dear Peggotty!’ said I. ‘Oh, thank you! Thank you! Will you promise me one thing, Peggotty? Will you write and tell Mr. Peggotty and little Em’ly, and Mrs. Gummidge and Ham, that I am not so bad as they might suppose, and that I sent ‘em all my love--especially to little Em’ly? Will you, if you please, Peggotty?’\n\nThe kind soul promised, and we both of us kissed the keyhole with the greatest affection--I patted it with my hand, I recollect, as if it had been her honest face--and parted. From that night there grew up in my breast a feeling for Peggotty which I cannot very well define. She did not replace my mother; no one could do that; but she came into a vacancy in my heart, which closed upon her, and I felt towards her something I have never felt for any other human being. It was a sort of comical affection, too; and yet if she had died, I cannot think what I should have done, or how I should have acted out the tragedy it would have been to me.\n\nIn the morning Miss Murdstone appeared as usual, and told me I was going to school; which was not altogether such news to me as she supposed. She also informed me that when I was dressed, I was to come downstairs into the parlour, and have my breakfast. There, I found my mother, very pale and with red eyes: into whose arms I ran, and begged her pardon from my suffering soul.\n\n‘Oh, Davy!’ she said. ‘That you could hurt anyone I love! Try to be better, pray to be better! I forgive you; but I am so grieved, Davy, that you should have such bad passions in your heart.’\n\nThey had persuaded her that I was a wicked fellow, and she was more sorry for that than for my going away. I felt it sorely. I tried to eat my parting breakfast, but my tears dropped upon my bread-and-butter, and trickled into my tea. I saw my mother look at me sometimes, and then glance at the watchful Miss Murdstone, and than look down, or look away.\n\n‘Master Copperfield’s box there!’ said Miss Murdstone, when wheels were heard at the gate.\n\nI looked for Peggotty, but it was not she; neither she nor Mr. Murdstone appeared. My former acquaintance, the carrier, was at the door. The box was taken out to his cart, and lifted in.\n\n‘Clara!’ said Miss Murdstone, in her warning note.\n\n‘Ready, my dear Jane,’ returned my mother. ‘Good-bye, Davy. You are going for your own good. Good-bye, my child. You will come home in the holidays, and be a better boy.’\n\n‘Clara!’ Miss Murdstone repeated.\n\n‘Certainly, my dear Jane,’ replied my mother, who was holding me. ‘I forgive you, my dear boy. God bless you!’\n\n‘Clara!’ Miss Murdstone repeated.\n\nMiss Murdstone was good enough to take me out to the cart, and to say on the way that she hoped I would repent, before I came to a bad end; and then I got into the cart, and the lazy horse walked off with it.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. I AM SENT AWAY FROM HOME\n\nWe might have gone about half a mile, and my pocket-handkerchief was quite wet through, when the carrier stopped short. Looking out to ascertain for what, I saw, to My amazement, Peggotty burst from a hedge and climb into the cart. She took me in both her arms, and squeezed me to her stays until the pressure on my nose was extremely painful, though I never thought of that till afterwards when I found it very tender. Not a single word did Peggotty speak. Releasing one of her arms, she put it down in her pocket to the elbow, and brought out some paper bags of cakes which she crammed into my pockets, and a purse which she put into my hand, but not one word did she say. After another and a final squeeze with both arms, she got down from the cart and ran away; and, my belief is, and has always been, without a solitary button on her gown. I picked up one, of several that were rolling about, and treasured it as a keepsake for a long time.\n\nThe carrier looked at me, as if to inquire if she were coming back. I shook my head, and said I thought not. ‘Then come up,’ said the carrier to the lazy horse; who came up accordingly.\n\nHaving by this time cried as much as I possibly could, I began to think it was of no use crying any more, especially as neither Roderick Random, nor that Captain in the Royal British Navy, had ever cried, that I could remember, in trying situations. The carrier, seeing me in this resolution, proposed that my pocket-handkerchief should be spread upon the horse’s back to dry. I thanked him, and assented; and particularly small it looked, under those circumstances.\n\nI had now leisure to examine the purse. It was a stiff leather purse, with a snap, and had three bright shillings in it, which Peggotty had evidently polished up with whitening, for my greater delight. But its most precious contents were two half-crowns folded together in a bit of paper, on which was written, in my mother’s hand, ‘For Davy. With my love.’ I was so overcome by this, that I asked the carrier to be so good as to reach me my pocket-handkerchief again; but he said he thought I had better do without it, and I thought I really had, so I wiped my eyes on my sleeve and stopped myself.\n\nFor good, too; though, in consequence of my previous emotions, I was still occasionally seized with a stormy sob. After we had jogged on for some little time, I asked the carrier if he was going all the way.\n\n‘All the way where?’ inquired the carrier.\n\n‘There,’ I said.\n\n‘Where’s there?’ inquired the carrier.\n\n‘Near London,’ I said.\n\n‘Why that horse,’ said the carrier, jerking the rein to point him out, ‘would be deader than pork afore he got over half the ground.’\n\n‘Are you only going to Yarmouth then?’ I asked.\n\n‘That’s about it,’ said the carrier. ‘And there I shall take you to the stage-cutch, and the stage-cutch that’ll take you to--wherever it is.’\n\nAs this was a great deal for the carrier (whose name was Mr. Barkis) to say--he being, as I observed in a former chapter, of a phlegmatic temperament, and not at all conversational--I offered him a cake as a mark of attention, which he ate at one gulp, exactly like an elephant, and which made no more impression on his big face than it would have done on an elephant’s.\n\n‘Did SHE make ‘em, now?’ said Mr. Barkis, always leaning forward, in his slouching way, on the footboard of the cart with an arm on each knee.\n\n‘Peggotty, do you mean, sir?’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Barkis. ‘Her.’\n\n‘Yes. She makes all our pastry, and does all our cooking.’\n\n‘Do she though?’ said Mr. Barkis. He made up his mouth as if to whistle, but he didn’t whistle. He sat looking at the horse’s ears, as if he saw something new there; and sat so, for a considerable time. By and by, he said:\n\n‘No sweethearts, I b’lieve?’\n\n‘Sweetmeats did you say, Mr. Barkis?’ For I thought he wanted something else to eat, and had pointedly alluded to that description of refreshment.\n\n‘Hearts,’ said Mr. Barkis. ‘Sweet hearts; no person walks with her!’\n\n‘With Peggotty?’\n\n‘Ah!’ he said. ‘Her.’\n\n‘Oh, no. She never had a sweetheart.’\n\n‘Didn’t she, though!’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\nAgain he made up his mouth to whistle, and again he didn’t whistle, but sat looking at the horse’s ears.\n\n‘So she makes,’ said Mr. Barkis, after a long interval of reflection, ‘all the apple parsties, and doos all the cooking, do she?’\n\nI replied that such was the fact.\n\n‘Well. I’ll tell you what,’ said Mr. Barkis. ‘P’raps you might be writin’ to her?’\n\n‘I shall certainly write to her,’ I rejoined.\n\n‘Ah!’ he said, slowly turning his eyes towards me. ‘Well! If you was writin’ to her, p’raps you’d recollect to say that Barkis was willin’; would you?’\n\n‘That Barkis is willing,’ I repeated, innocently. ‘Is that all the message?’\n\n‘Ye-es,’ he said, considering. ‘Ye-es. Barkis is willin’.’\n\n‘But you will be at Blunderstone again tomorrow, Mr. Barkis,’ I said, faltering a little at the idea of my being far away from it then, and could give your own message so much better.’\n\nAs he repudiated this suggestion, however, with a jerk of his head, and once more confirmed his previous request by saying, with profound gravity, ‘Barkis is willin’. That’s the message,’ I readily undertook its transmission. While I was waiting for the coach in the hotel at Yarmouth that very afternoon, I procured a sheet of paper and an inkstand, and wrote a note to Peggotty, which ran thus: ‘My dear Peggotty. I have come here safe. Barkis is willing. My love to mama. Yours affectionately. P.S. He says he particularly wants you to know--BARKIS IS WILLING.’\n\nWhen I had taken this commission on myself prospectively, Mr. Barkis relapsed into perfect silence; and I, feeling quite worn out by all that had happened lately, lay down on a sack in the cart and fell asleep. I slept soundly until we got to Yarmouth; which was so entirely new and strange to me in the inn-yard to which we drove, that I at once abandoned a latent hope I had had of meeting with some of Mr. Peggotty’s family there, perhaps even with little Em’ly herself.\n\nThe coach was in the yard, shining very much all over, but without any horses to it as yet; and it looked in that state as if nothing was more unlikely than its ever going to London. I was thinking this, and wondering what would ultimately become of my box, which Mr. Barkis had put down on the yard-pavement by the pole (he having driven up the yard to turn his cart), and also what would ultimately become of me, when a lady looked out of a bow-window where some fowls and joints of meat were hanging up, and said:\n\n‘Is that the little gentleman from Blunderstone?’\n\n‘Yes, ma’am,’ I said.\n\n‘What name?’ inquired the lady.\n\n‘Copperfield, ma’am,’ I said.\n\n‘That won’t do,’ returned the lady. ‘Nobody’s dinner is paid for here, in that name.’\n\n‘Is it Murdstone, ma’am?’ I said.\n\n‘If you’re Master Murdstone,’ said the lady, ‘why do you go and give another name, first?’\n\nI explained to the lady how it was, who than rang a bell, and called out, ‘William! show the coffee-room!’ upon which a waiter came running out of a kitchen on the opposite side of the yard to show it, and seemed a good deal surprised when he was only to show it to me.\n\nIt was a large long room with some large maps in it. I doubt if I could have felt much stranger if the maps had been real foreign countries, and I cast away in the middle of them. I felt it was taking a liberty to sit down, with my cap in my hand, on the corner of the chair nearest the door; and when the waiter laid a cloth on purpose for me, and put a set of castors on it, I think I must have turned red all over with modesty.\n\nHe brought me some chops, and vegetables, and took the covers off in such a bouncing manner that I was afraid I must have given him some offence. But he greatly relieved my mind by putting a chair for me at the table, and saying, very affably, ‘Now, six-foot! come on!’\n\nI thanked him, and took my seat at the board; but found it extremely difficult to handle my knife and fork with anything like dexterity, or to avoid splashing myself with the gravy, while he was standing opposite, staring so hard, and making me blush in the most dreadful manner every time I caught his eye. After watching me into the second chop, he said:\n\n‘There’s half a pint of ale for you. Will you have it now?’\n\nI thanked him and said, ‘Yes.’ Upon which he poured it out of a jug into a large tumbler, and held it up against the light, and made it look beautiful.\n\n‘My eye!’ he said. ‘It seems a good deal, don’t it?’\n\n‘It does seem a good deal,’ I answered with a smile. For it was quite delightful to me, to find him so pleasant. He was a twinkling-eyed, pimple-faced man, with his hair standing upright all over his head; and as he stood with one arm a-kimbo, holding up the glass to the light with the other hand, he looked quite friendly.\n\n‘There was a gentleman here, yesterday,’ he said--‘a stout gentleman, by the name of Topsawyer--perhaps you know him?’\n\n‘No,’ I said, ‘I don’t think--’\n\n‘In breeches and gaiters, broad-brimmed hat, grey coat, speckled choker,’ said the waiter.\n\n‘No,’ I said bashfully, ‘I haven’t the pleasure--’\n\n‘He came in here,’ said the waiter, looking at the light through the tumbler, ‘ordered a glass of this ale--WOULD order it--I told him not--drank it, and fell dead. It was too old for him. It oughtn’t to be drawn; that’s the fact.’\n\nI was very much shocked to hear of this melancholy accident, and said I thought I had better have some water.\n\n‘Why you see,’ said the waiter, still looking at the light through the tumbler, with one of his eyes shut up, ‘our people don’t like things being ordered and left. It offends ‘em. But I’ll drink it, if you like. I’m used to it, and use is everything. I don’t think it’ll hurt me, if I throw my head back, and take it off quick. Shall I?’\n\nI replied that he would much oblige me by drinking it, if he thought he could do it safely, but by no means otherwise. When he did throw his head back, and take it off quick, I had a horrible fear, I confess, of seeing him meet the fate of the lamented Mr. Topsawyer, and fall lifeless on the carpet. But it didn’t hurt him. On the contrary, I thought he seemed the fresher for it.\n\n‘What have we got here?’ he said, putting a fork into my dish. ‘Not chops?’\n‘Chops,’ I said.\n\n‘Lord bless my soul!’ he exclaimed, ‘I didn’t know they were chops. Why, a chop’s the very thing to take off the bad effects of that beer! Ain’t it lucky?’\n\nSo he took a chop by the bone in one hand, and a potato in the other, and ate away with a very good appetite, to my extreme satisfaction. He afterwards took another chop, and another potato; and after that, another chop and another potato. When we had done, he brought me a pudding, and having set it before me, seemed to ruminate, and to become absent in his mind for some moments.\n\n‘How’s the pie?’ he said, rousing himself.\n\n‘It’s a pudding,’ I made answer.\n\n‘Pudding!’ he exclaimed. ‘Why, bless me, so it is! What!’ looking at it nearer. ‘You don’t mean to say it’s a batter-pudding!’\n\n‘Yes, it is indeed.’\n\n‘Why, a batter-pudding,’ he said, taking up a table-spoon, ‘is my favourite pudding! Ain’t that lucky? Come on, little ‘un, and let’s see who’ll get most.’\n\nThe waiter certainly got most. He entreated me more than once to come in and win, but what with his table-spoon to my tea-spoon, his dispatch to my dispatch, and his appetite to my appetite, I was left far behind at the first mouthful, and had no chance with him. I never saw anyone enjoy a pudding so much, I think; and he laughed, when it was all gone, as if his enjoyment of it lasted still.\n\nFinding him so very friendly and companionable, it was then that I asked for the pen and ink and paper, to write to Peggotty. He not only brought it immediately, but was good enough to look over me while I wrote the letter. When I had finished it, he asked me where I was going to school.\n\nI said, ‘Near London,’ which was all I knew.\n\n‘Oh! my eye!’ he said, looking very low-spirited, ‘I am sorry for that.’\n\n‘Why?’ I asked him.\n\n‘Oh, Lord!’ he said, shaking his head, ‘that’s the school where they broke the boy’s ribs--two ribs--a little boy he was. I should say he was--let me see--how old are you, about?’\n\nI told him between eight and nine.\n\n‘That’s just his age,’ he said. ‘He was eight years and six months old when they broke his first rib; eight years and eight months old when they broke his second, and did for him.’\n\nI could not disguise from myself, or from the waiter, that this was an uncomfortable coincidence, and inquired how it was done. His answer was not cheering to my spirits, for it consisted of two dismal words, ‘With whopping.’\n\nThe blowing of the coach-horn in the yard was a seasonable diversion, which made me get up and hesitatingly inquire, in the mingled pride and diffidence of having a purse (which I took out of my pocket), if there were anything to pay.\n\n‘There’s a sheet of letter-paper,’ he returned. ‘Did you ever buy a sheet of letter-paper?’\n\nI could not remember that I ever had.\n\n‘It’s dear,’ he said, ‘on account of the duty. Threepence. That’s the way we’re taxed in this country. There’s nothing else, except the waiter. Never mind the ink. I lose by that.’\n\n‘What should you--what should I--how much ought I to--what would it be right to pay the waiter, if you please?’ I stammered, blushing.\n\n‘If I hadn’t a family, and that family hadn’t the cowpock,’ said the waiter, ‘I wouldn’t take a sixpence. If I didn’t support a aged pairint, and a lovely sister,’--here the waiter was greatly agitated--‘I wouldn’t take a farthing. If I had a good place, and was treated well here, I should beg acceptance of a trifle, instead of taking of it. But I live on broken wittles--and I sleep on the coals’--here the waiter burst into tears.\n\nI was very much concerned for his misfortunes, and felt that any recognition short of ninepence would be mere brutality and hardness of heart. Therefore I gave him one of my three bright shillings, which he received with much humility and veneration, and spun up with his thumb, directly afterwards, to try the goodness of.\n\nIt was a little disconcerting to me, to find, when I was being helped up behind the coach, that I was supposed to have eaten all the dinner without any assistance. I discovered this, from overhearing the lady in the bow-window say to the guard, ‘Take care of that child, George, or he’ll burst!’ and from observing that the women-servants who were about the place came out to look and giggle at me as a young phenomenon. My unfortunate friend the waiter, who had quite recovered his spirits, did not appear to be disturbed by this, but joined in the general admiration without being at all confused. If I had any doubt of him, I suppose this half awakened it; but I am inclined to believe that with the simple confidence of a child, and the natural reliance of a child upon superior years (qualities I am very sorry any children should prematurely change for worldly wisdom), I had no serious mistrust of him on the whole, even then.\n\nI felt it rather hard, I must own, to be made, without deserving it, the subject of jokes between the coachman and guard as to the coach drawing heavy behind, on account of my sitting there, and as to the greater expediency of my travelling by waggon. The story of my supposed appetite getting wind among the outside passengers, they were merry upon it likewise; and asked me whether I was going to be paid for, at school, as two brothers or three, and whether I was contracted for, or went upon the regular terms; with other pleasant questions. But the worst of it was, that I knew I should be ashamed to eat anything, when an opportunity offered, and that, after a rather light dinner, I should remain hungry all night--for I had left my cakes behind, at the hotel, in my hurry. My apprehensions were realized. When we stopped for supper I couldn’t muster courage to take any, though I should have liked it very much, but sat by the fire and said I didn’t want anything. This did not save me from more jokes, either; for a husky-voiced gentleman with a rough face, who had been eating out of a sandwich-box nearly all the way, except when he had been drinking out of a bottle, said I was like a boa-constrictor who took enough at one meal to last him a long time; after which, he actually brought a rash out upon himself with boiled beef.\n\nWe had started from Yarmouth at three o’clock in the afternoon, and we were due in London about eight next morning. It was Mid-summer weather, and the evening was very pleasant. When we passed through a village, I pictured to myself what the insides of the houses were like, and what the inhabitants were about; and when boys came running after us, and got up behind and swung there for a little way, I wondered whether their fathers were alive, and whether they were happy at home. I had plenty to think of, therefore, besides my mind running continually on the kind of place I was going to--which was an awful speculation. Sometimes, I remember, I resigned myself to thoughts of home and Peggotty; and to endeavouring, in a confused blind way, to recall how I had felt, and what sort of boy I used to be, before I bit Mr. Murdstone: which I couldn’t satisfy myself about by any means, I seemed to have bitten him in such a remote antiquity.\n\nThe night was not so pleasant as the evening, for it got chilly; and being put between two gentlemen (the rough-faced one and another) to prevent my tumbling off the coach, I was nearly smothered by their falling asleep, and completely blocking me up. They squeezed me so hard sometimes, that I could not help crying out, ‘Oh! If you please!’--which they didn’t like at all, because it woke them. Opposite me was an elderly lady in a great fur cloak, who looked in the dark more like a haystack than a lady, she was wrapped up to such a degree. This lady had a basket with her, and she hadn’t known what to do with it, for a long time, until she found that on account of my legs being short, it could go underneath me. It cramped and hurt me so, that it made me perfectly miserable; but if I moved in the least, and made a glass that was in the basket rattle against something else (as it was sure to do), she gave me the cruellest poke with her foot, and said, ‘Come, don’t YOU fidget. YOUR bones are young enough, I’m sure!’\n\nAt last the sun rose, and then my companions seemed to sleep easier. The difficulties under which they had laboured all night, and which had found utterance in the most terrific gasps and snorts, are not to be conceived. As the sun got higher, their sleep became lighter, and so they gradually one by one awoke. I recollect being very much surprised by the feint everybody made, then, of not having been to sleep at all, and by the uncommon indignation with which everyone repelled the charge. I labour under the same kind of astonishment to this day, having invariably observed that of all human weaknesses, the one to which our common nature is the least disposed to confess (I cannot imagine why) is the weakness of having gone to sleep in a coach.\n\nWhat an amazing place London was to me when I saw it in the distance, and how I believed all the adventures of all my favourite heroes to be constantly enacting and re-enacting there, and how I vaguely made it out in my own mind to be fuller of wonders and wickedness than all the cities of the earth, I need not stop here to relate. We approached it by degrees, and got, in due time, to the inn in the Whitechapel district, for which we were bound. I forget whether it was the Blue Bull, or the Blue Boar; but I know it was the Blue Something, and that its likeness was painted up on the back of the coach.\n\nThe guard’s eye lighted on me as he was getting down, and he said at the booking-office door:\n\n‘Is there anybody here for a yoongster booked in the name of Murdstone, from Bloonderstone, Sooffolk, to be left till called for?’\n\nNobody answered.\n\n‘Try Copperfield, if you please, sir,’ said I, looking helplessly down.\n\n‘Is there anybody here for a yoongster, booked in the name of Murdstone, from Bloonderstone, Sooffolk, but owning to the name of Copperfield, to be left till called for?’ said the guard. ‘Come! IS there anybody?’\n\nNo. There was nobody. I looked anxiously around; but the inquiry made no impression on any of the bystanders, if I except a man in gaiters, with one eye, who suggested that they had better put a brass collar round my neck, and tie me up in the stable.\n\nA ladder was brought, and I got down after the lady, who was like a haystack: not daring to stir, until her basket was removed. The coach was clear of passengers by that time, the luggage was very soon cleared out, the horses had been taken out before the luggage, and now the coach itself was wheeled and backed off by some hostlers, out of the way. Still, nobody appeared, to claim the dusty youngster from Blunderstone, Suffolk.\n\nMore solitary than Robinson Crusoe, who had nobody to look at him and see that he was solitary, I went into the booking-office, and, by invitation of the clerk on duty, passed behind the counter, and sat down on the scale at which they weighed the luggage. Here, as I sat looking at the parcels, packages, and books, and inhaling the smell of stables (ever since associated with that morning), a procession of most tremendous considerations began to march through my mind. Supposing nobody should ever fetch me, how long would they consent to keep me there? Would they keep me long enough to spend seven shillings? Should I sleep at night in one of those wooden bins, with the other luggage, and wash myself at the pump in the yard in the morning; or should I be turned out every night, and expected to come again to be left till called for, when the office opened next day? Supposing there was no mistake in the case, and Mr. Murdstone had devised this plan to get rid of me, what should I do? If they allowed me to remain there until my seven shillings were spent, I couldn’t hope to remain there when I began to starve. That would obviously be inconvenient and unpleasant to the customers, besides entailing on the Blue Whatever-it-was, the risk of funeral expenses. If I started off at once, and tried to walk back home, how could I ever find my way, how could I ever hope to walk so far, how could I make sure of anyone but Peggotty, even if I got back? If I found out the nearest proper authorities, and offered myself to go for a soldier, or a sailor, I was such a little fellow that it was most likely they wouldn’t take me in. These thoughts, and a hundred other such thoughts, turned me burning hot, and made me giddy with apprehension and dismay. I was in the height of my fever when a man entered and whispered to the clerk, who presently slanted me off the scale, and pushed me over to him, as if I were weighed, bought, delivered, and paid for.\n\nAs I went out of the office, hand in hand with this new acquaintance, I stole a look at him. He was a gaunt, sallow young man, with hollow cheeks, and a chin almost as black as Mr. Murdstone’s; but there the likeness ended, for his whiskers were shaved off, and his hair, instead of being glossy, was rusty and dry. He was dressed in a suit of black clothes which were rather rusty and dry too, and rather short in the sleeves and legs; and he had a white neck-kerchief on, that was not over-clean. I did not, and do not, suppose that this neck-kerchief was all the linen he wore, but it was all he showed or gave any hint of.\n\n‘You’re the new boy?’ he said. ‘Yes, sir,’ I said.\n\nI supposed I was. I didn’t know.\n\n‘I’m one of the masters at Salem House,’ he said.\n\nI made him a bow and felt very much overawed. I was so ashamed to allude to a commonplace thing like my box, to a scholar and a master at Salem House, that we had gone some little distance from the yard before I had the hardihood to mention it. We turned back, on my humbly insinuating that it might be useful to me hereafter; and he told the clerk that the carrier had instructions to call for it at noon.\n\n‘If you please, sir,’ I said, when we had accomplished about the same distance as before, ‘is it far?’\n\n‘It’s down by Blackheath,’ he said.\n\n‘Is that far, sir?’ I diffidently asked.\n\n‘It’s a good step,’ he said. ‘We shall go by the stage-coach. It’s about six miles.’\n\nI was so faint and tired, that the idea of holding out for six miles more, was too much for me. I took heart to tell him that I had had nothing all night, and that if he would allow me to buy something to eat, I should be very much obliged to him. He appeared surprised at this--I see him stop and look at me now--and after considering for a few moments, said he wanted to call on an old person who lived not far off, and that the best way would be for me to buy some bread, or whatever I liked best that was wholesome, and make my breakfast at her house, where we could get some milk.\n\nAccordingly we looked in at a baker’s window, and after I had made a series of proposals to buy everything that was bilious in the shop, and he had rejected them one by one, we decided in favour of a nice little loaf of brown bread, which cost me threepence. Then, at a grocer’s shop, we bought an egg and a slice of streaky bacon; which still left what I thought a good deal of change, out of the second of the bright shillings, and made me consider London a very cheap place. These provisions laid in, we went on through a great noise and uproar that confused my weary head beyond description, and over a bridge which, no doubt, was London Bridge (indeed I think he told me so, but I was half asleep), until we came to the poor person’s house, which was a part of some alms-houses, as I knew by their look, and by an inscription on a stone over the gate which said they were established for twenty-five poor women.\n\nThe Master at Salem House lifted the latch of one of a number of little black doors that were all alike, and had each a little diamond-paned window on one side, and another little diamond--paned window above; and we went into the little house of one of these poor old women, who was blowing a fire to make a little saucepan boil. On seeing the master enter, the old woman stopped with the bellows on her knee, and said something that I thought sounded like ‘My Charley!’ but on seeing me come in too, she got up, and rubbing her hands made a confused sort of half curtsey.\n\n‘Can you cook this young gentleman’s breakfast for him, if you please?’ said the Master at Salem House.\n\n‘Can I?’ said the old woman. ‘Yes can I, sure!’\n\n‘How’s Mrs. Fibbitson today?’ said the Master, looking at another old woman in a large chair by the fire, who was such a bundle of clothes that I feel grateful to this hour for not having sat upon her by mistake.\n\n‘Ah, she’s poorly,’ said the first old woman. ‘It’s one of her bad days. If the fire was to go out, through any accident, I verily believe she’d go out too, and never come to life again.’\n\nAs they looked at her, I looked at her also. Although it was a warm day, she seemed to think of nothing but the fire. I fancied she was jealous even of the saucepan on it; and I have reason to know that she took its impressment into the service of boiling my egg and broiling my bacon, in dudgeon; for I saw her, with my own discomfited eyes, shake her fist at me once, when those culinary operations were going on, and no one else was looking. The sun streamed in at the little window, but she sat with her own back and the back of the large chair towards it, screening the fire as if she were sedulously keeping IT warm, instead of it keeping her warm, and watching it in a most distrustful manner. The completion of the preparations for my breakfast, by relieving the fire, gave her such extreme joy that she laughed aloud--and a very unmelodious laugh she had, I must say.\n\nI sat down to my brown loaf, my egg, and my rasher of bacon, with a basin of milk besides, and made a most delicious meal. While I was yet in the full enjoyment of it, the old woman of the house said to the Master:\n\n‘Have you got your flute with you?’\n\n‘Yes,’ he returned.\n\n‘Have a blow at it,’ said the old woman, coaxingly. ‘Do!’\n\nThe Master, upon this, put his hand underneath the skirts of his coat, and brought out his flute in three pieces, which he screwed together, and began immediately to play. My impression is, after many years of consideration, that there never can have been anybody in the world who played worse. He made the most dismal sounds I have ever heard produced by any means, natural or artificial. I don’t know what the tunes were--if there were such things in the performance at all, which I doubt--but the influence of the strain upon me was, first, to make me think of all my sorrows until I could hardly keep my tears back; then to take away my appetite; and lastly, to make me so sleepy that I couldn’t keep my eyes open. They begin to close again, and I begin to nod, as the recollection rises fresh upon me. Once more the little room, with its open corner cupboard, and its square-backed chairs, and its angular little staircase leading to the room above, and its three peacock’s feathers displayed over the mantelpiece--I remember wondering when I first went in, what that peacock would have thought if he had known what his finery was doomed to come to--fades from before me, and I nod, and sleep. The flute becomes inaudible, the wheels of the coach are heard instead, and I am on my journey. The coach jolts, I wake with a start, and the flute has come back again, and the Master at Salem House is sitting with his legs crossed, playing it dolefully, while the old woman of the house looks on delighted. She fades in her turn, and he fades, and all fades, and there is no flute, no Master, no Salem House, no David Copperfield, no anything but heavy sleep.\n\nI dreamed, I thought, that once while he was blowing into this dismal flute, the old woman of the house, who had gone nearer and nearer to him in her ecstatic admiration, leaned over the back of his chair and gave him an affectionate squeeze round the neck, which stopped his playing for a moment. I was in the middle state between sleeping and waking, either then or immediately afterwards; for, as he resumed--it was a real fact that he had stopped playing--I saw and heard the same old woman ask Mrs. Fibbitson if it wasn’t delicious (meaning the flute), to which Mrs. Fibbitson replied, ‘Ay, ay! yes!’ and nodded at the fire: to which, I am persuaded, she gave the credit of the whole performance.\n\nWhen I seemed to have been dozing a long while, the Master at Salem House unscrewed his flute into the three pieces, put them up as before, and took me away. We found the coach very near at hand, and got upon the roof; but I was so dead sleepy, that when we stopped on the road to take up somebody else, they put me inside where there were no passengers, and where I slept profoundly, until I found the coach going at a footpace up a steep hill among green leaves. Presently, it stopped, and had come to its destination.\n\nA short walk brought us--I mean the Master and me--to Salem House, which was enclosed with a high brick wall, and looked very dull. Over a door in this wall was a board with SALEM HOUSE upon it; and through a grating in this door we were surveyed when we rang the bell by a surly face, which I found, on the door being opened, belonged to a stout man with a bull-neck, a wooden leg, overhanging temples, and his hair cut close all round his head.\n\n‘The new boy,’ said the Master.\n\nThe man with the wooden leg eyed me all over--it didn’t take long, for there was not much of me--and locked the gate behind us, and took out the key. We were going up to the house, among some dark heavy trees, when he called after my conductor. ‘Hallo!’\n\nWe looked back, and he was standing at the door of a little lodge, where he lived, with a pair of boots in his hand.\n\n‘Here! The cobbler’s been,’ he said, ‘since you’ve been out, Mr. Mell, and he says he can’t mend ‘em any more. He says there ain’t a bit of the original boot left, and he wonders you expect it.’\n\nWith these words he threw the boots towards Mr. Mell, who went back a few paces to pick them up, and looked at them (very disconsolately, I was afraid), as we went on together. I observed then, for the first time, that the boots he had on were a good deal the worse for wear, and that his stocking was just breaking out in one place, like a bud.\n\nSalem House was a square brick building with wings; of a bare and unfurnished appearance. All about it was so very quiet, that I said to Mr. Mell I supposed the boys were out; but he seemed surprised at my not knowing that it was holiday-time. That all the boys were at their several homes. That Mr. Creakle, the proprietor, was down by the sea-side with Mrs. and Miss Creakle; and that I was sent in holiday-time as a punishment for my misdoing, all of which he explained to me as we went along.\n\nI gazed upon the schoolroom into which he took me, as the most forlorn and desolate place I had ever seen. I see it now. A long room with three long rows of desks, and six of forms, and bristling all round with pegs for hats and slates. Scraps of old copy-books and exercises litter the dirty floor. Some silkworms’ houses, made of the same materials, are scattered over the desks. Two miserable little white mice, left behind by their owner, are running up and down in a fusty castle made of pasteboard and wire, looking in all the corners with their red eyes for anything to eat. A bird, in a cage very little bigger than himself, makes a mournful rattle now and then in hopping on his perch, two inches high, or dropping from it; but neither sings nor chirps. There is a strange unwholesome smell upon the room, like mildewed corduroys, sweet apples wanting air, and rotten books. There could not well be more ink splashed about it, if it had been roofless from its first construction, and the skies had rained, snowed, hailed, and blown ink through the varying seasons of the year.\n\nMr. Mell having left me while he took his irreparable boots upstairs, I went softly to the upper end of the room, observing all this as I crept along. Suddenly I came upon a pasteboard placard, beautifully written, which was lying on the desk, and bore these words: ‘TAKE CARE OF HIM. HE BITES.’\n\nI got upon the desk immediately, apprehensive of at least a great dog underneath. But, though I looked all round with anxious eyes, I could see nothing of him. I was still engaged in peering about, when Mr. Mell came back, and asked me what I did up there?\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ says I, ‘if you please, I’m looking for the dog.’\n\n‘Dog?’ he says. ‘What dog?’\n\n‘Isn’t it a dog, sir?’\n\n‘Isn’t what a dog?’\n\n‘That’s to be taken care of, sir; that bites.’\n\n‘No, Copperfield,’ says he, gravely, ‘that’s not a dog. That’s a boy. My instructions are, Copperfield, to put this placard on your back. I am sorry to make such a beginning with you, but I must do it.’ With that he took me down, and tied the placard, which was neatly constructed for the purpose, on my shoulders like a knapsack; and wherever I went, afterwards, I had the consolation of carrying it.\n\nWhat I suffered from that placard, nobody can imagine. Whether it was possible for people to see me or not, I always fancied that somebody was reading it. It was no relief to turn round and find nobody; for wherever my back was, there I imagined somebody always to be. That cruel man with the wooden leg aggravated my sufferings. He was in authority; and if he ever saw me leaning against a tree, or a wall, or the house, he roared out from his lodge door in a stupendous voice, ‘Hallo, you sir! You Copperfield! Show that badge conspicuous, or I’ll report you!’ The playground was a bare gravelled yard, open to all the back of the house and the offices; and I knew that the servants read it, and the butcher read it, and the baker read it; that everybody, in a word, who came backwards and forwards to the house, of a morning when I was ordered to walk there, read that I was to be taken care of, for I bit, I recollect that I positively began to have a dread of myself, as a kind of wild boy who did bite.\n\nThere was an old door in this playground, on which the boys had a custom of carving their names. It was completely covered with such inscriptions. In my dread of the end of the vacation and their coming back, I could not read a boy’s name, without inquiring in what tone and with what emphasis HE would read, ‘Take care of him. He bites.’ There was one boy--a certain J. Steerforth--who cut his name very deep and very often, who, I conceived, would read it in a rather strong voice, and afterwards pull my hair. There was another boy, one Tommy Traddles, who I dreaded would make game of it, and pretend to be dreadfully frightened of me. There was a third, George Demple, who I fancied would sing it. I have looked, a little shrinking creature, at that door, until the owners of all the names--there were five-and-forty of them in the school then, Mr. Mell said--seemed to send me to Coventry by general acclamation, and to cry out, each in his own way, ‘Take care of him. He bites!’\n\nIt was the same with the places at the desks and forms. It was the same with the groves of deserted bedsteads I peeped at, on my way to, and when I was in, my own bed. I remember dreaming night after night, of being with my mother as she used to be, or of going to a party at Mr. Peggotty’s, or of travelling outside the stage-coach, or of dining again with my unfortunate friend the waiter, and in all these circumstances making people scream and stare, by the unhappy disclosure that I had nothing on but my little night-shirt, and that placard.\n\nIn the monotony of my life, and in my constant apprehension of the re-opening of the school, it was such an insupportable affliction! I had long tasks every day to do with Mr. Mell; but I did them, there being no Mr. and Miss Murdstone here, and got through them without disgrace. Before, and after them, I walked about--supervised, as I have mentioned, by the man with the wooden leg. How vividly I call to mind the damp about the house, the green cracked flagstones in the court, an old leaky water-butt, and the discoloured trunks of some of the grim trees, which seemed to have dripped more in the rain than other trees, and to have blown less in the sun! At one we dined, Mr. Mell and I, at the upper end of a long bare dining-room, full of deal tables, and smelling of fat. Then, we had more tasks until tea, which Mr. Mell drank out of a blue teacup, and I out of a tin pot. All day long, and until seven or eight in the evening, Mr. Mell, at his own detached desk in the schoolroom, worked hard with pen, ink, ruler, books, and writing-paper, making out the bills (as I found) for last half-year. When he had put up his things for the night he took out his flute, and blew at it, until I almost thought he would gradually blow his whole being into the large hole at the top, and ooze away at the keys.\n\nI picture my small self in the dimly-lighted rooms, sitting with my head upon my hand, listening to the doleful performance of Mr. Mell, and conning tomorrow’s lessons. I picture myself with my books shut up, still listening to the doleful performance of Mr. Mell, and listening through it to what used to be at home, and to the blowing of the wind on Yarmouth flats, and feeling very sad and solitary. I picture myself going up to bed, among the unused rooms, and sitting on my bed-side crying for a comfortable word from Peggotty. I picture myself coming downstairs in the morning, and looking through a long ghastly gash of a staircase window at the school-bell hanging on the top of an out-house with a weathercock above it; and dreading the time when it shall ring J. Steerforth and the rest to work: which is only second, in my foreboding apprehensions, to the time when the man with the wooden leg shall unlock the rusty gate to give admission to the awful Mr. Creakle. I cannot think I was a very dangerous character in any of these aspects, but in all of them I carried the same warning on my back.\n\nMr. Mell never said much to me, but he was never harsh to me. I suppose we were company to each other, without talking. I forgot to mention that he would talk to himself sometimes, and grin, and clench his fist, and grind his teeth, and pull his hair in an unaccountable manner. But he had these peculiarities: and at first they frightened me, though I soon got used to them.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. I ENLARGE MY CIRCLE OF ACQUAINTANCE\n\nI HAD led this life about a month, when the man with the wooden leg began to stump about with a mop and a bucket of water, from which I inferred that preparations were making to receive Mr. Creakle and the boys. I was not mistaken; for the mop came into the schoolroom before long, and turned out Mr. Mell and me, who lived where we could, and got on how we could, for some days, during which we were always in the way of two or three young women, who had rarely shown themselves before, and were so continually in the midst of dust that I sneezed almost as much as if Salem House had been a great snuff-box.\n\nOne day I was informed by Mr. Mell that Mr. Creakle would be home that evening. In the evening, after tea, I heard that he was come. Before bedtime, I was fetched by the man with the wooden leg to appear before him.\n\nMr. Creakle’s part of the house was a good deal more comfortable than ours, and he had a snug bit of garden that looked pleasant after the dusty playground, which was such a desert in miniature, that I thought no one but a camel, or a dromedary, could have felt at home in it. It seemed to me a bold thing even to take notice that the passage looked comfortable, as I went on my way, trembling, to Mr. Creakle’s presence: which so abashed me, when I was ushered into it, that I hardly saw Mrs. Creakle or Miss Creakle (who were both there, in the parlour), or anything but Mr. Creakle, a stout gentleman with a bunch of watch-chain and seals, in an arm-chair, with a tumbler and bottle beside him.\n\n‘So!’ said Mr. Creakle. ‘This is the young gentleman whose teeth are to be filed! Turn him round.’\n\nThe wooden-legged man turned me about so as to exhibit the placard; and having afforded time for a full survey of it, turned me about again, with my face to Mr. Creakle, and posted himself at Mr. Creakle’s side. Mr. Creakle’s face was fiery, and his eyes were small, and deep in his head; he had thick veins in his forehead, a little nose, and a large chin. He was bald on the top of his head; and had some thin wet-looking hair that was just turning grey, brushed across each temple, so that the two sides interlaced on his forehead. But the circumstance about him which impressed me most, was, that he had no voice, but spoke in a whisper. The exertion this cost him, or the consciousness of talking in that feeble way, made his angry face so much more angry, and his thick veins so much thicker, when he spoke, that I am not surprised, on looking back, at this peculiarity striking me as his chief one. ‘Now,’ said Mr. Creakle. ‘What’s the report of this boy?’\n\n‘There’s nothing against him yet,’ returned the man with the wooden leg. ‘There has been no opportunity.’\n\nI thought Mr. Creakle was disappointed. I thought Mrs. and Miss Creakle (at whom I now glanced for the first time, and who were, both, thin and quiet) were not disappointed.\n\n‘Come here, sir!’ said Mr. Creakle, beckoning to me.\n\n‘Come here!’ said the man with the wooden leg, repeating the gesture.\n\n‘I have the happiness of knowing your father-in-law,’ whispered Mr. Creakle, taking me by the ear; ‘and a worthy man he is, and a man of a strong character. He knows me, and I know him. Do YOU know me? Hey?’ said Mr. Creakle, pinching my ear with ferocious playfulness.\n\n‘Not yet, sir,’ I said, flinching with the pain.\n\n‘Not yet? Hey?’ repeated Mr. Creakle. ‘But you will soon. Hey?’\n\n‘You will soon. Hey?’ repeated the man with the wooden leg. I afterwards found that he generally acted, with his strong voice, as Mr. Creakle’s interpreter to the boys.\n\nI was very much frightened, and said, I hoped so, if he pleased. I felt, all this while, as if my ear were blazing; he pinched it so hard.\n\n‘I’ll tell you what I am,’ whispered Mr. Creakle, letting it go at last, with a screw at parting that brought the water into my eyes. ‘I’m a Tartar.’\n\n‘A Tartar,’ said the man with the wooden leg.\n\n‘When I say I’ll do a thing, I do it,’ said Mr. Creakle; ‘and when I say I will have a thing done, I will have it done.’\n\n‘--Will have a thing done, I will have it done,’ repeated the man with the wooden leg.\n\n‘I am a determined character,’ said Mr. Creakle. ‘That’s what I am. I do my duty. That’s what I do. My flesh and blood’--he looked at Mrs. Creakle as he said this--‘when it rises against me, is not my flesh and blood. I discard it. Has that fellow’--to the man with the wooden leg--‘been here again?’\n\n‘No,’ was the answer.\n\n‘No,’ said Mr. Creakle. ‘He knows better. He knows me. Let him keep away. I say let him keep away,’ said Mr. Creakle, striking his hand upon the table, and looking at Mrs. Creakle, ‘for he knows me. Now you have begun to know me too, my young friend, and you may go. Take him away.’\n\nI was very glad to be ordered away, for Mrs. and Miss Creakle were both wiping their eyes, and I felt as uncomfortable for them as I did for myself. But I had a petition on my mind which concerned me so nearly, that I couldn’t help saying, though I wondered at my own courage:\n\n‘If you please, sir--’\n\nMr. Creakle whispered, ‘Hah! What’s this?’ and bent his eyes upon me, as if he would have burnt me up with them.\n\n‘If you please, sir,’ I faltered, ‘if I might be allowed (I am very sorry indeed, sir, for what I did) to take this writing off, before the boys come back--’\n\nWhether Mr. Creakle was in earnest, or whether he only did it to frighten me, I don’t know, but he made a burst out of his chair, before which I precipitately retreated, without waiting for the escort of the man with the wooden leg, and never once stopped until I reached my own bedroom, where, finding I was not pursued, I went to bed, as it was time, and lay quaking, for a couple of hours.\n\nNext morning Mr. Sharp came back. Mr. Sharp was the first master, and superior to Mr. Mell. Mr. Mell took his meals with the boys, but Mr. Sharp dined and supped at Mr. Creakle’s table. He was a limp, delicate-looking gentleman, I thought, with a good deal of nose, and a way of carrying his head on one side, as if it were a little too heavy for him. His hair was very smooth and wavy; but I was informed by the very first boy who came back that it was a wig (a second-hand one HE said), and that Mr. Sharp went out every Saturday afternoon to get it curled.\n\nIt was no other than Tommy Traddles who gave me this piece of intelligence. He was the first boy who returned. He introduced himself by informing me that I should find his name on the right-hand corner of the gate, over the top-bolt; upon that I said, ‘Traddles?’ to which he replied, ‘The same,’ and then he asked me for a full account of myself and family.\n\nIt was a happy circumstance for me that Traddles came back first. He enjoyed my placard so much, that he saved me from the embarrassment of either disclosure or concealment, by presenting me to every other boy who came back, great or small, immediately on his arrival, in this form of introduction, ‘Look here! Here’s a game!’ Happily, too, the greater part of the boys came back low-spirited, and were not so boisterous at my expense as I had expected. Some of them certainly did dance about me like wild Indians, and the greater part could not resist the temptation of pretending that I was a dog, and patting and soothing me, lest I should bite, and saying, ‘Lie down, sir!’ and calling me Towzer. This was naturally confusing, among so many strangers, and cost me some tears, but on the whole it was much better than I had anticipated.\n\nI was not considered as being formally received into the school, however, until J. Steerforth arrived. Before this boy, who was reputed to be a great scholar, and was very good-looking, and at least half-a-dozen years my senior, I was carried as before a magistrate. He inquired, under a shed in the playground, into the particulars of my punishment, and was pleased to express his opinion that it was ‘a jolly shame’; for which I became bound to him ever afterwards.\n\n‘What money have you got, Copperfield?’ he said, walking aside with me when he had disposed of my affair in these terms. I told him seven shillings.\n\n‘You had better give it to me to take care of,’ he said. ‘At least, you can if you like. You needn’t if you don’t like.’\n\nI hastened to comply with his friendly suggestion, and opening Peggotty’s purse, turned it upside down into his hand.\n\n‘Do you want to spend anything now?’ he asked me.\n\n‘No thank you,’ I replied.\n\n‘You can, if you like, you know,’ said Steerforth. ‘Say the word.’\n\n‘No, thank you, sir,’ I repeated.\n\n‘Perhaps you’d like to spend a couple of shillings or so, in a bottle of currant wine by and by, up in the bedroom?’ said Steerforth. ‘You belong to my bedroom, I find.’\n\nIt certainly had not occurred to me before, but I said, Yes, I should like that.\n\n‘Very good,’ said Steerforth. ‘You’ll be glad to spend another shilling or so, in almond cakes, I dare say?’\n\nI said, Yes, I should like that, too.\n\n‘And another shilling or so in biscuits, and another in fruit, eh?’ said Steerforth. ‘I say, young Copperfield, you’re going it!’\n\nI smiled because he smiled, but I was a little troubled in my mind, too.\n\n‘Well!’ said Steerforth. ‘We must make it stretch as far as we can; that’s all. I’ll do the best in my power for you. I can go out when I like, and I’ll smuggle the prog in.’ With these words he put the money in his pocket, and kindly told me not to make myself uneasy; he would take care it should be all right. He was as good as his word, if that were all right which I had a secret misgiving was nearly all wrong--for I feared it was a waste of my mother’s two half-crowns--though I had preserved the piece of paper they were wrapped in: which was a precious saving. When we went upstairs to bed, he produced the whole seven shillings’ worth, and laid it out on my bed in the moonlight, saying:\n\n‘There you are, young Copperfield, and a royal spread you’ve got.’\n\nI couldn’t think of doing the honours of the feast, at my time of life, while he was by; my hand shook at the very thought of it. I begged him to do me the favour of presiding; and my request being seconded by the other boys who were in that room, he acceded to it, and sat upon my pillow, handing round the viands--with perfect fairness, I must say--and dispensing the currant wine in a little glass without a foot, which was his own property. As to me, I sat on his left hand, and the rest were grouped about us, on the nearest beds and on the floor.\n\nHow well I recollect our sitting there, talking in whispers; or their talking, and my respectfully listening, I ought rather to say; the moonlight falling a little way into the room, through the window, painting a pale window on the floor, and the greater part of us in shadow, except when Steerforth dipped a match into a phosphorus-box, when he wanted to look for anything on the board, and shed a blue glare over us that was gone directly! A certain mysterious feeling, consequent on the darkness, the secrecy of the revel, and the whisper in which everything was said, steals over me again, and I listen to all they tell me with a vague feeling of solemnity and awe, which makes me glad that they are all so near, and frightens me (though I feign to laugh) when Traddles pretends to see a ghost in the corner.\n\nI heard all kinds of things about the school and all belonging to it. I heard that Mr. Creakle had not preferred his claim to being a Tartar without reason; that he was the sternest and most severe of masters; that he laid about him, right and left, every day of his life, charging in among the boys like a trooper, and slashing away, unmercifully. That he knew nothing himself, but the art of slashing, being more ignorant (J. Steerforth said) than the lowest boy in the school; that he had been, a good many years ago, a small hop-dealer in the Borough, and had taken to the schooling business after being bankrupt in hops, and making away with Mrs. Creakle’s money. With a good deal more of that sort, which I wondered how they knew.\n\nI heard that the man with the wooden leg, whose name was Tungay, was an obstinate barbarian who had formerly assisted in the hop business, but had come into the scholastic line with Mr. Creakle, in consequence, as was supposed among the boys, of his having broken his leg in Mr. Creakle’s service, and having done a deal of dishonest work for him, and knowing his secrets. I heard that with the single exception of Mr. Creakle, Tungay considered the whole establishment, masters and boys, as his natural enemies, and that the only delight of his life was to be sour and malicious. I heard that Mr. Creakle had a son, who had not been Tungay’s friend, and who, assisting in the school, had once held some remonstrance with his father on an occasion when its discipline was very cruelly exercised, and was supposed, besides, to have protested against his father’s usage of his mother. I heard that Mr. Creakle had turned him out of doors, in consequence; and that Mrs. and Miss Creakle had been in a sad way, ever since.\n\nBut the greatest wonder that I heard of Mr. Creakle was, there being one boy in the school on whom he never ventured to lay a hand, and that boy being J. Steerforth. Steerforth himself confirmed this when it was stated, and said that he should like to begin to see him do it. On being asked by a mild boy (not me) how he would proceed if he did begin to see him do it, he dipped a match into his phosphorus-box on purpose to shed a glare over his reply, and said he would commence by knocking him down with a blow on the forehead from the seven-and-sixpenny ink-bottle that was always on the mantelpiece. We sat in the dark for some time, breathless.\n\nI heard that Mr. Sharp and Mr. Mell were both supposed to be wretchedly paid; and that when there was hot and cold meat for dinner at Mr. Creakle’s table, Mr. Sharp was always expected to say he preferred cold; which was again corroborated by J. Steerforth, the only parlour-boarder. I heard that Mr. Sharp’s wig didn’t fit him; and that he needn’t be so ‘bounceable’--somebody else said ‘bumptious’--about it, because his own red hair was very plainly to be seen behind.\n\nI heard that one boy, who was a coal-merchant’s son, came as a set-off against the coal-bill, and was called, on that account, ‘Exchange or Barter’--a name selected from the arithmetic book as expressing this arrangement. I heard that the table beer was a robbery of parents, and the pudding an imposition. I heard that Miss Creakle was regarded by the school in general as being in love with Steerforth; and I am sure, as I sat in the dark, thinking of his nice voice, and his fine face, and his easy manner, and his curling hair, I thought it very likely. I heard that Mr. Mell was not a bad sort of fellow, but hadn’t a sixpence to bless himself with; and that there was no doubt that old Mrs. Mell, his mother, was as poor as job. I thought of my breakfast then, and what had sounded like ‘My Charley!’ but I was, I am glad to remember, as mute as a mouse about it.\n\nThe hearing of all this, and a good deal more, outlasted the banquet some time. The greater part of the guests had gone to bed as soon as the eating and drinking were over; and we, who had remained whispering and listening half-undressed, at last betook ourselves to bed, too.\n\n‘Good night, young Copperfield,’ said Steerforth. ‘I’ll take care of you.’ ‘You’re very kind,’ I gratefully returned. ‘I am very much obliged to you.’\n\n‘You haven’t got a sister, have you?’ said Steerforth, yawning.\n\n‘No,’ I answered.\n\n‘That’s a pity,’ said Steerforth. ‘If you had had one, I should think she would have been a pretty, timid, little, bright-eyed sort of girl. I should have liked to know her. Good night, young Copperfield.’\n\n‘Good night, sir,’ I replied.\n\nI thought of him very much after I went to bed, and raised myself, I recollect, to look at him where he lay in the moonlight, with his handsome face turned up, and his head reclining easily on his arm. He was a person of great power in my eyes; that was, of course, the reason of my mind running on him. No veiled future dimly glanced upon him in the moonbeams. There was no shadowy picture of his footsteps, in the garden that I dreamed of walking in all night.\n\n# CHAPTER 7. MY ‘FIRST HALF’ AT SALEM HOUSE\n\nSchool began in earnest next day. A profound impression was made upon me, I remember, by the roar of voices in the schoolroom suddenly becoming hushed as death when Mr. Creakle entered after breakfast, and stood in the doorway looking round upon us like a giant in a story-book surveying his captives.\n\nTungay stood at Mr. Creakle’s elbow. He had no occasion, I thought, to cry out ‘Silence!’ so ferociously, for the boys were all struck speechless and motionless.\n\nMr. Creakle was seen to speak, and Tungay was heard, to this effect.\n\n‘Now, boys, this is a new half. Take care what you’re about, in this new half. Come fresh up to the lessons, I advise you, for I come fresh up to the punishment. I won’t flinch. It will be of no use your rubbing yourselves; you won’t rub the marks out that I shall give you. Now get to work, every boy!’\n\nWhen this dreadful exordium was over, and Tungay had stumped out again, Mr. Creakle came to where I sat, and told me that if I were famous for biting, he was famous for biting, too. He then showed me the cane, and asked me what I thought of THAT, for a tooth? Was it a sharp tooth, hey? Was it a double tooth, hey? Had it a deep prong, hey? Did it bite, hey? Did it bite? At every question he gave me a fleshy cut with it that made me writhe; so I was very soon made free of Salem House (as Steerforth said), and was very soon in tears also.\n\nNot that I mean to say these were special marks of distinction, which only I received. On the contrary, a large majority of the boys (especially the smaller ones) were visited with similar instances of notice, as Mr. Creakle made the round of the schoolroom. Half the establishment was writhing and crying, before the day’s work began; and how much of it had writhed and cried before the day’s work was over, I am really afraid to recollect, lest I should seem to exaggerate.\n\nI should think there never can have been a man who enjoyed his profession more than Mr. Creakle did. He had a delight in cutting at the boys, which was like the satisfaction of a craving appetite. I am confident that he couldn’t resist a chubby boy, especially; that there was a fascination in such a subject, which made him restless in his mind, until he had scored and marked him for the day. I was chubby myself, and ought to know. I am sure when I think of the fellow now, my blood rises against him with the disinterested indignation I should feel if I could have known all about him without having ever been in his power; but it rises hotly, because I know him to have been an incapable brute, who had no more right to be possessed of the great trust he held, than to be Lord High Admiral, or Commander-in-Chief--in either of which capacities it is probable that he would have done infinitely less mischief.\n\nMiserable little propitiators of a remorseless Idol, how abject we were to him! What a launch in life I think it now, on looking back, to be so mean and servile to a man of such parts and pretensions!\n\nHere I sit at the desk again, watching his eye--humbly watching his eye, as he rules a ciphering-book for another victim whose hands have just been flattened by that identical ruler, and who is trying to wipe the sting out with a pocket-handkerchief. I have plenty to do. I don’t watch his eye in idleness, but because I am morbidly attracted to it, in a dread desire to know what he will do next, and whether it will be my turn to suffer, or somebody else’s. A lane of small boys beyond me, with the same interest in his eye, watch it too. I think he knows it, though he pretends he don’t. He makes dreadful mouths as he rules the ciphering-book; and now he throws his eye sideways down our lane, and we all droop over our books and tremble. A moment afterwards we are again eyeing him. An unhappy culprit, found guilty of imperfect exercise, approaches at his command. The culprit falters excuses, and professes a determination to do better tomorrow. Mr. Creakle cuts a joke before he beats him, and we laugh at it,--miserable little dogs, we laugh, with our visages as white as ashes, and our hearts sinking into our boots.\n\nHere I sit at the desk again, on a drowsy summer afternoon. A buzz and hum go up around me, as if the boys were so many bluebottles. A cloggy sensation of the lukewarm fat of meat is upon me (we dined an hour or two ago), and my head is as heavy as so much lead. I would give the world to go to sleep. I sit with my eye on Mr. Creakle, blinking at him like a young owl; when sleep overpowers me for a minute, he still looms through my slumber, ruling those ciphering-books, until he softly comes behind me and wakes me to plainer perception of him, with a red ridge across my back.\n\nHere I am in the playground, with my eye still fascinated by him, though I can’t see him. The window at a little distance from which I know he is having his dinner, stands for him, and I eye that instead. If he shows his face near it, mine assumes an imploring and submissive expression. If he looks out through the glass, the boldest boy (Steerforth excepted) stops in the middle of a shout or yell, and becomes contemplative. One day, Traddles (the most unfortunate boy in the world) breaks that window accidentally, with a ball. I shudder at this moment with the tremendous sensation of seeing it done, and feeling that the ball has bounded on to Mr. Creakle’s sacred head.\n\nPoor Traddles! In a tight sky-blue suit that made his arms and legs like German sausages, or roly-poly puddings, he was the merriest and most miserable of all the boys. He was always being caned--I think he was caned every day that half-year, except one holiday Monday when he was only ruler’d on both hands--and was always going to write to his uncle about it, and never did. After laying his head on the desk for a little while, he would cheer up, somehow, begin to laugh again, and draw skeletons all over his slate, before his eyes were dry. I used at first to wonder what comfort Traddles found in drawing skeletons; and for some time looked upon him as a sort of hermit, who reminded himself by those symbols of mortality that caning couldn’t last for ever. But I believe he only did it because they were easy, and didn’t want any features.\n\nHe was very honourable, Traddles was, and held it as a solemn duty in the boys to stand by one another. He suffered for this on several occasions; and particularly once, when Steerforth laughed in church, and the Beadle thought it was Traddles, and took him out. I see him now, going away in custody, despised by the congregation. He never said who was the real offender, though he smarted for it next day, and was imprisoned so many hours that he came forth with a whole churchyard-full of skeletons swarming all over his Latin Dictionary. But he had his reward. Steerforth said there was nothing of the sneak in Traddles, and we all felt that to be the highest praise. For my part, I could have gone through a good deal (though I was much less brave than Traddles, and nothing like so old) to have won such a recompense.\n\nTo see Steerforth walk to church before us, arm-in-arm with Miss Creakle, was one of the great sights of my life. I didn’t think Miss Creakle equal to little Em’ly in point of beauty, and I didn’t love her (I didn’t dare); but I thought her a young lady of extraordinary attractions, and in point of gentility not to be surpassed. When Steerforth, in white trousers, carried her parasol for her, I felt proud to know him; and believed that she could not choose but adore him with all her heart. Mr. Sharp and Mr. Mell were both notable personages in my eyes; but Steerforth was to them what the sun was to two stars.\n\nSteerforth continued his protection of me, and proved a very useful friend; since nobody dared to annoy one whom he honoured with his countenance. He couldn’t--or at all events he didn’t--defend me from Mr. Creakle, who was very severe with me; but whenever I had been treated worse than usual, he always told me that I wanted a little of his pluck, and that he wouldn’t have stood it himself; which I felt he intended for encouragement, and considered to be very kind of him. There was one advantage, and only one that I know of, in Mr. Creakle’s severity. He found my placard in his way when he came up or down behind the form on which I sat, and wanted to make a cut at me in passing; for this reason it was soon taken off, and I saw it no more.\n\nAn accidental circumstance cemented the intimacy between Steerforth and me, in a manner that inspired me with great pride and satisfaction, though it sometimes led to inconvenience. It happened on one occasion, when he was doing me the honour of talking to me in the playground, that I hazarded the observation that something or somebody--I forget what now--was like something or somebody in Peregrine Pickle. He said nothing at the time; but when I was going to bed at night, asked me if I had got that book?\n\nI told him no, and explained how it was that I had read it, and all those other books of which I have made mention.\n\n‘And do you recollect them?’ Steerforth said.\n\n‘Oh yes,’ I replied; I had a good memory, and I believed I recollected them very well.\n\n‘Then I tell you what, young Copperfield,’ said Steerforth, ‘you shall tell ‘em to me. I can’t get to sleep very early at night, and I generally wake rather early in the morning. We’ll go over ‘em one after another. We’ll make some regular Arabian Nights of it.’\n\nI felt extremely flattered by this arrangement, and we commenced carrying it into execution that very evening. What ravages I committed on my favourite authors in the course of my interpretation of them, I am not in a condition to say, and should be very unwilling to know; but I had a profound faith in them, and I had, to the best of my belief, a simple, earnest manner of narrating what I did narrate; and these qualities went a long way.\n\nThe drawback was, that I was often sleepy at night, or out of spirits and indisposed to resume the story; and then it was rather hard work, and it must be done; for to disappoint or to displease Steerforth was of course out of the question. In the morning, too, when I felt weary, and should have enjoyed another hour’s repose very much, it was a tiresome thing to be roused, like the Sultana Scheherazade, and forced into a long story before the getting-up bell rang; but Steerforth was resolute; and as he explained to me, in return, my sums and exercises, and anything in my tasks that was too hard for me, I was no loser by the transaction. Let me do myself justice, however. I was moved by no interested or selfish motive, nor was I moved by fear of him. I admired and loved him, and his approval was return enough. It was so precious to me that I look back on these trifles, now, with an aching heart.\n\nSteerforth was considerate, too; and showed his consideration, in one particular instance, in an unflinching manner that was a little tantalizing, I suspect, to poor Traddles and the rest. Peggotty’s promised letter--what a comfortable letter it was!--arrived before ‘the half’ was many weeks old; and with it a cake in a perfect nest of oranges, and two bottles of cowslip wine. This treasure, as in duty bound, I laid at the feet of Steerforth, and begged him to dispense.\n\n‘Now, I’ll tell you what, young Copperfield,’ said he: ‘the wine shall be kept to wet your whistle when you are story-telling.’\n\nI blushed at the idea, and begged him, in my modesty, not to think of it. But he said he had observed I was sometimes hoarse--a little roopy was his exact expression--and it should be, every drop, devoted to the purpose he had mentioned. Accordingly, it was locked up in his box, and drawn off by himself in a phial, and administered to me through a piece of quill in the cork, when I was supposed to be in want of a restorative. Sometimes, to make it a more sovereign specific, he was so kind as to squeeze orange juice into it, or to stir it up with ginger, or dissolve a peppermint drop in it; and although I cannot assert that the flavour was improved by these experiments, or that it was exactly the compound one would have chosen for a stomachic, the last thing at night and the first thing in the morning, I drank it gratefully and was very sensible of his attention.\n\nWe seem, to me, to have been months over Peregrine, and months more over the other stories. The institution never flagged for want of a story, I am certain; and the wine lasted out almost as well as the matter. Poor Traddles--I never think of that boy but with a strange disposition to laugh, and with tears in my eyes--was a sort of chorus, in general; and affected to be convulsed with mirth at the comic parts, and to be overcome with fear when there was any passage of an alarming character in the narrative. This rather put me out, very often. It was a great jest of his, I recollect, to pretend that he couldn’t keep his teeth from chattering, whenever mention was made of an Alguazill in connexion with the adventures of Gil Blas; and I remember that when Gil Blas met the captain of the robbers in Madrid, this unlucky joker counterfeited such an ague of terror, that he was overheard by Mr. Creakle, who was prowling about the passage, and handsomely flogged for disorderly conduct in the bedroom. Whatever I had within me that was romantic and dreamy, was encouraged by so much story-telling in the dark; and in that respect the pursuit may not have been very profitable to me. But the being cherished as a kind of plaything in my room, and the consciousness that this accomplishment of mine was bruited about among the boys, and attracted a good deal of notice to me though I was the youngest there, stimulated me to exertion. In a school carried on by sheer cruelty, whether it is presided over by a dunce or not, there is not likely to be much learnt. I believe our boys were, generally, as ignorant a set as any schoolboys in existence; they were too much troubled and knocked about to learn; they could no more do that to advantage, than any one can do anything to advantage in a life of constant misfortune, torment, and worry. But my little vanity, and Steerforth’s help, urged me on somehow; and without saving me from much, if anything, in the way of punishment, made me, for the time I was there, an exception to the general body, insomuch that I did steadily pick up some crumbs of knowledge.\n\nIn this I was much assisted by Mr. Mell, who had a liking for me that I am grateful to remember. It always gave me pain to observe that Steerforth treated him with systematic disparagement, and seldom lost an occasion of wounding his feelings, or inducing others to do so. This troubled me the more for a long time, because I had soon told Steerforth, from whom I could no more keep such a secret, than I could keep a cake or any other tangible possession, about the two old women Mr. Mell had taken me to see; and I was always afraid that Steerforth would let it out, and twit him with it.\n\nWe little thought, any one of us, I dare say, when I ate my breakfast that first morning, and went to sleep under the shadow of the peacock’s feathers to the sound of the flute, what consequences would come of the introduction into those alms-houses of my insignificant person. But the visit had its unforeseen consequences; and of a serious sort, too, in their way.\n\nOne day when Mr. Creakle kept the house from indisposition, which naturally diffused a lively joy through the school, there was a good deal of noise in the course of the morning’s work. The great relief and satisfaction experienced by the boys made them difficult to manage; and though the dreaded Tungay brought his wooden leg in twice or thrice, and took notes of the principal offenders’ names, no great impression was made by it, as they were pretty sure of getting into trouble tomorrow, do what they would, and thought it wise, no doubt, to enjoy themselves today.\n\nIt was, properly, a half-holiday; being Saturday. But as the noise in the playground would have disturbed Mr. Creakle, and the weather was not favourable for going out walking, we were ordered into school in the afternoon, and set some lighter tasks than usual, which were made for the occasion. It was the day of the week on which Mr. Sharp went out to get his wig curled; so Mr. Mell, who always did the drudgery, whatever it was, kept school by himself. If I could associate the idea of a bull or a bear with anyone so mild as Mr. Mell, I should think of him, in connexion with that afternoon when the uproar was at its height, as of one of those animals, baited by a thousand dogs. I recall him bending his aching head, supported on his bony hand, over the book on his desk, and wretchedly endeavouring to get on with his tiresome work, amidst an uproar that might have made the Speaker of the House of Commons giddy. Boys started in and out of their places, playing at puss in the corner with other boys; there were laughing boys, singing boys, talking boys, dancing boys, howling boys; boys shuffled with their feet, boys whirled about him, grinning, making faces, mimicking him behind his back and before his eyes; mimicking his poverty, his boots, his coat, his mother, everything belonging to him that they should have had consideration for.\n\n‘Silence!’ cried Mr. Mell, suddenly rising up, and striking his desk with the book. ‘What does this mean! It’s impossible to bear it. It’s maddening. How can you do it to me, boys?’\n\nIt was my book that he struck his desk with; and as I stood beside him, following his eye as it glanced round the room, I saw the boys all stop, some suddenly surprised, some half afraid, and some sorry perhaps.\n\nSteerforth’s place was at the bottom of the school, at the opposite end of the long room. He was lounging with his back against the wall, and his hands in his pockets, and looked at Mr. Mell with his mouth shut up as if he were whistling, when Mr. Mell looked at him.\n\n‘Silence, Mr. Steerforth!’ said Mr. Mell.\n\n‘Silence yourself,’ said Steerforth, turning red. ‘Whom are you talking to?’\n\n‘Sit down,’ said Mr. Mell.\n\n‘Sit down yourself,’ said Steerforth, ‘and mind your business.’\n\nThere was a titter, and some applause; but Mr. Mell was so white, that silence immediately succeeded; and one boy, who had darted out behind him to imitate his mother again, changed his mind, and pretended to want a pen mended.\n\n‘If you think, Steerforth,’ said Mr. Mell, ‘that I am not acquainted with the power you can establish over any mind here’--he laid his hand, without considering what he did (as I supposed), upon my head--‘or that I have not observed you, within a few minutes, urging your juniors on to every sort of outrage against me, you are mistaken.’\n\n‘I don’t give myself the trouble of thinking at all about you,’ said Steerforth, coolly; ‘so I’m not mistaken, as it happens.’\n\n‘And when you make use of your position of favouritism here, sir,’ pursued Mr. Mell, with his lip trembling very much, ‘to insult a gentleman--’\n\n‘A what?--where is he?’ said Steerforth.\n\nHere somebody cried out, ‘Shame, J. Steerforth! Too bad!’ It was Traddles; whom Mr. Mell instantly discomfited by bidding him hold his tongue. --‘To insult one who is not fortunate in life, sir, and who never gave you the least offence, and the many reasons for not insulting whom you are old enough and wise enough to understand,’ said Mr. Mell, with his lips trembling more and more, ‘you commit a mean and base action. You can sit down or stand up as you please, sir. Copperfield, go on.’\n\n‘Young Copperfield,’ said Steerforth, coming forward up the room, ‘stop a bit. I tell you what, Mr. Mell, once for all. When you take the liberty of calling me mean or base, or anything of that sort, you are an impudent beggar. You are always a beggar, you know; but when you do that, you are an impudent beggar.’\n\nI am not clear whether he was going to strike Mr. Mell, or Mr. Mell was going to strike him, or there was any such intention on either side. I saw a rigidity come upon the whole school as if they had been turned into stone, and found Mr. Creakle in the midst of us, with Tungay at his side, and Mrs. and Miss Creakle looking in at the door as if they were frightened. Mr. Mell, with his elbows on his desk and his face in his hands, sat, for some moments, quite still.\n\n‘Mr. Mell,’ said Mr. Creakle, shaking him by the arm; and his whisper was so audible now, that Tungay felt it unnecessary to repeat his words; ‘you have not forgotten yourself, I hope?’\n\n‘No, sir, no,’ returned the Master, showing his face, and shaking his head, and rubbing his hands in great agitation. ‘No, sir. No. I have remembered myself, I--no, Mr. Creakle, I have not forgotten myself, I--I have remembered myself, sir. I--I--could wish you had remembered me a little sooner, Mr. Creakle. It--it--would have been more kind, sir, more just, sir. It would have saved me something, sir.’\n\nMr. Creakle, looking hard at Mr. Mell, put his hand on Tungay’s shoulder, and got his feet upon the form close by, and sat upon the desk. After still looking hard at Mr. Mell from his throne, as he shook his head, and rubbed his hands, and remained in the same state of agitation, Mr. Creakle turned to Steerforth, and said:\n\n‘Now, sir, as he don’t condescend to tell me, what is this?’\n\nSteerforth evaded the question for a little while; looking in scorn and anger on his opponent, and remaining silent. I could not help thinking even in that interval, I remember, what a noble fellow he was in appearance, and how homely and plain Mr. Mell looked opposed to him.\n\n‘What did he mean by talking about favourites, then?’ said Steerforth at length.\n\n‘Favourites?’ repeated Mr. Creakle, with the veins in his forehead swelling quickly. ‘Who talked about favourites?’\n\n‘He did,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘And pray, what did you mean by that, sir?’ demanded Mr. Creakle, turning angrily on his assistant.\n\n‘I meant, Mr. Creakle,’ he returned in a low voice, ‘as I said; that no pupil had a right to avail himself of his position of favouritism to degrade me.’\n\n‘To degrade YOU?’ said Mr. Creakle. ‘My stars! But give me leave to ask you, Mr. What’s-your-name’; and here Mr. Creakle folded his arms, cane and all, upon his chest, and made such a knot of his brows that his little eyes were hardly visible below them; ‘whether, when you talk about favourites, you showed proper respect to me? To me, sir,’ said Mr. Creakle, darting his head at him suddenly, and drawing it back again, ‘the principal of this establishment, and your employer.’\n\n‘It was not judicious, sir, I am willing to admit,’ said Mr. Mell. ‘I should not have done so, if I had been cool.’\n\nHere Steerforth struck in.\n\n‘Then he said I was mean, and then he said I was base, and then I called him a beggar. If I had been cool, perhaps I shouldn’t have called him a beggar. But I did, and I am ready to take the consequences of it.’\n\nWithout considering, perhaps, whether there were any consequences to be taken, I felt quite in a glow at this gallant speech. It made an impression on the boys too, for there was a low stir among them, though no one spoke a word.\n\n‘I am surprised, Steerforth--although your candour does you honour,’ said Mr. Creakle, ‘does you honour, certainly--I am surprised, Steerforth, I must say, that you should attach such an epithet to any person employed and paid in Salem House, sir.’\n\nSteerforth gave a short laugh.\n\n‘That’s not an answer, sir,’ said Mr. Creakle, ‘to my remark. I expect more than that from you, Steerforth.’\n\nIf Mr. Mell looked homely, in my eyes, before the handsome boy, it would be quite impossible to say how homely Mr. Creakle looked. ‘Let him deny it,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Deny that he is a beggar, Steerforth?’ cried Mr. Creakle. ‘Why, where does he go a-begging?’\n\n‘If he is not a beggar himself, his near relation’s one,’ said Steerforth. ‘It’s all the same.’\n\nHe glanced at me, and Mr. Mell’s hand gently patted me upon the shoulder. I looked up with a flush upon my face and remorse in my heart, but Mr. Mell’s eyes were fixed on Steerforth. He continued to pat me kindly on the shoulder, but he looked at him.\n\n‘Since you expect me, Mr. Creakle, to justify myself,’ said Steerforth, ‘and to say what I mean,--what I have to say is, that his mother lives on charity in an alms-house.’\n\nMr. Mell still looked at him, and still patted me kindly on the shoulder, and said to himself, in a whisper, if I heard right: ‘Yes, I thought so.’\n\nMr. Creakle turned to his assistant, with a severe frown and laboured politeness:\n\n‘Now, you hear what this gentleman says, Mr. Mell. Have the goodness, if you please, to set him right before the assembled school.’\n\n‘He is right, sir, without correction,’ returned Mr. Mell, in the midst of a dead silence; ‘what he has said is true.’\n\n‘Be so good then as declare publicly, will you,’ said Mr. Creakle, putting his head on one side, and rolling his eyes round the school, ‘whether it ever came to my knowledge until this moment?’\n\n‘I believe not directly,’ he returned.\n\n‘Why, you know not,’ said Mr. Creakle. ‘Don’t you, man?’\n\n‘I apprehend you never supposed my worldly circumstances to be very good,’ replied the assistant. ‘You know what my position is, and always has been, here.’\n\n‘I apprehend, if you come to that,’ said Mr. Creakle, with his veins swelling again bigger than ever, ‘that you’ve been in a wrong position altogether, and mistook this for a charity school. Mr. Mell, we’ll part, if you please. The sooner the better.’\n\n‘There is no time,’ answered Mr. Mell, rising, ‘like the present.’\n\n‘Sir, to you!’ said Mr. Creakle.\n\n‘I take my leave of you, Mr. Creakle, and all of you,’ said Mr. Mell, glancing round the room, and again patting me gently on the shoulders. ‘James Steerforth, the best wish I can leave you is that you may come to be ashamed of what you have done today. At present I would prefer to see you anything rather than a friend, to me, or to anyone in whom I feel an interest.’\n\nOnce more he laid his hand upon my shoulder; and then taking his flute and a few books from his desk, and leaving the key in it for his successor, he went out of the school, with his property under his arm. Mr. Creakle then made a speech, through Tungay, in which he thanked Steerforth for asserting (though perhaps too warmly) the independence and respectability of Salem House; and which he wound up by shaking hands with Steerforth, while we gave three cheers--I did not quite know what for, but I supposed for Steerforth, and so joined in them ardently, though I felt miserable. Mr. Creakle then caned Tommy Traddles for being discovered in tears, instead of cheers, on account of Mr. Mell’s departure; and went back to his sofa, or his bed, or wherever he had come from.\n\nWe were left to ourselves now, and looked very blank, I recollect, on one another. For myself, I felt so much self-reproach and contrition for my part in what had happened, that nothing would have enabled me to keep back my tears but the fear that Steerforth, who often looked at me, I saw, might think it unfriendly--or, I should rather say, considering our relative ages, and the feeling with which I regarded him, undutiful--if I showed the emotion which distressed me. He was very angry with Traddles, and said he was glad he had caught it.\n\nPoor Traddles, who had passed the stage of lying with his head upon the desk, and was relieving himself as usual with a burst of skeletons, said he didn’t care. Mr. Mell was ill-used.\n\n‘Who has ill-used him, you girl?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Why, you have,’ returned Traddles.\n\n‘What have I done?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘What have you done?’ retorted Traddles. ‘Hurt his feelings, and lost him his situation.’\n\n‘His feelings?’ repeated Steerforth disdainfully. ‘His feelings will soon get the better of it, I’ll be bound. His feelings are not like yours, Miss Traddles. As to his situation--which was a precious one, wasn’t it?--do you suppose I am not going to write home, and take care that he gets some money? Polly?’\n\nWe thought this intention very noble in Steerforth, whose mother was a widow, and rich, and would do almost anything, it was said, that he asked her. We were all extremely glad to see Traddles so put down, and exalted Steerforth to the skies: especially when he told us, as he condescended to do, that what he had done had been done expressly for us, and for our cause; and that he had conferred a great boon upon us by unselfishly doing it. But I must say that when I was going on with a story in the dark that night, Mr. Mell’s old flute seemed more than once to sound mournfully in my ears; and that when at last Steerforth was tired, and I lay down in my bed, I fancied it playing so sorrowfully somewhere, that I was quite wretched.\n\nI soon forgot him in the contemplation of Steerforth, who, in an easy amateur way, and without any book (he seemed to me to know everything by heart), took some of his classes until a new master was found. The new master came from a grammar school; and before he entered on his duties, dined in the parlour one day, to be introduced to Steerforth. Steerforth approved of him highly, and told us he was a Brick. Without exactly understanding what learned distinction was meant by this, I respected him greatly for it, and had no doubt whatever of his superior knowledge: though he never took the pains with me--not that I was anybody--that Mr. Mell had taken.\n\nThere was only one other event in this half-year, out of the daily school-life, that made an impression upon me which still survives. It survives for many reasons.\n\nOne afternoon, when we were all harassed into a state of dire confusion, and Mr. Creakle was laying about him dreadfully, Tungay came in, and called out in his usual strong way: ‘Visitors for Copperfield!’\n\nA few words were interchanged between him and Mr. Creakle, as, who the visitors were, and what room they were to be shown into; and then I, who had, according to custom, stood up on the announcement being made, and felt quite faint with astonishment, was told to go by the back stairs and get a clean frill on, before I repaired to the dining-room. These orders I obeyed, in such a flutter and hurry of my young spirits as I had never known before; and when I got to the parlour door, and the thought came into my head that it might be my mother--I had only thought of Mr. or Miss Murdstone until then--I drew back my hand from the lock, and stopped to have a sob before I went in.\n\nAt first I saw nobody; but feeling a pressure against the door, I looked round it, and there, to my amazement, were Mr. Peggotty and Ham, ducking at me with their hats, and squeezing one another against the wall. I could not help laughing; but it was much more in the pleasure of seeing them, than at the appearance they made. We shook hands in a very cordial way; and I laughed and laughed, until I pulled out my pocket-handkerchief and wiped my eyes.\n\nMr. Peggotty (who never shut his mouth once, I remember, during the visit) showed great concern when he saw me do this, and nudged Ham to say something.\n\n‘Cheer up, Mas’r Davy bor’!’ said Ham, in his simpering way. ‘Why, how you have growed!’\n\n‘Am I grown?’ I said, drying my eyes. I was not crying at anything in particular that I know of; but somehow it made me cry, to see old friends.\n\n‘Growed, Mas’r Davy bor’? Ain’t he growed!’ said Ham.\n\n‘Ain’t he growed!’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\nThey made me laugh again by laughing at each other, and then we all three laughed until I was in danger of crying again.\n\n‘Do you know how mama is, Mr. Peggotty?’ I said. ‘And how my dear, dear, old Peggotty is?’\n\n‘Oncommon,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘And little Em’ly, and Mrs. Gummidge?’\n\n‘On--common,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\nThere was a silence. Mr. Peggotty, to relieve it, took two prodigious lobsters, and an enormous crab, and a large canvas bag of shrimps, out of his pockets, and piled them up in Ham’s arms.\n\n‘You see,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘knowing as you was partial to a little relish with your wittles when you was along with us, we took the liberty. The old Mawther biled ‘em, she did. Mrs. Gummidge biled ‘em. Yes,’ said Mr. Peggotty, slowly, who I thought appeared to stick to the subject on account of having no other subject ready, ‘Mrs. Gummidge, I do assure you, she biled ‘em.’\n\nI expressed my thanks; and Mr. Peggotty, after looking at Ham, who stood smiling sheepishly over the shellfish, without making any attempt to help him, said:\n\n‘We come, you see, the wind and tide making in our favour, in one of our Yarmouth lugs to Gravesen’. My sister she wrote to me the name of this here place, and wrote to me as if ever I chanced to come to Gravesen’, I was to come over and inquire for Mas’r Davy and give her dooty, humbly wishing him well and reporting of the fam’ly as they was oncommon toe-be-sure. Little Em’ly, you see, she’ll write to my sister when I go back, as I see you and as you was similarly oncommon, and so we make it quite a merry-go-rounder.’\n\nI was obliged to consider a little before I understood what Mr. Peggotty meant by this figure, expressive of a complete circle of intelligence. I then thanked him heartily; and said, with a consciousness of reddening, that I supposed little Em’ly was altered too, since we used to pick up shells and pebbles on the beach?\n\n‘She’s getting to be a woman, that’s wot she’s getting to be,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘Ask HIM.’ He meant Ham, who beamed with delight and assent over the bag of shrimps.\n\n‘Her pretty face!’ said Mr. Peggotty, with his own shining like a light.\n\n‘Her learning!’ said Ham.\n\n‘Her writing!’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘Why it’s as black as jet! And so large it is, you might see it anywheres.’\n\nIt was perfectly delightful to behold with what enthusiasm Mr. Peggotty became inspired when he thought of his little favourite. He stands before me again, his bluff hairy face irradiating with a joyful love and pride, for which I can find no description. His honest eyes fire up, and sparkle, as if their depths were stirred by something bright. His broad chest heaves with pleasure. His strong loose hands clench themselves, in his earnestness; and he emphasizes what he says with a right arm that shows, in my pigmy view, like a sledge-hammer.\n\nHam was quite as earnest as he. I dare say they would have said much more about her, if they had not been abashed by the unexpected coming in of Steerforth, who, seeing me in a corner speaking with two strangers, stopped in a song he was singing, and said: ‘I didn’t know you were here, young Copperfield!’ (for it was not the usual visiting room) and crossed by us on his way out.\n\nI am not sure whether it was in the pride of having such a friend as Steerforth, or in the desire to explain to him how I came to have such a friend as Mr. Peggotty, that I called to him as he was going away. But I said, modestly--Good Heaven, how it all comes back to me this long time afterwards--!\n\n‘Don’t go, Steerforth, if you please. These are two Yarmouth boatmen--very kind, good people--who are relations of my nurse, and have come from Gravesend to see me.’\n\n‘Aye, aye?’ said Steerforth, returning. ‘I am glad to see them. How are you both?’\n\nThere was an ease in his manner--a gay and light manner it was, but not swaggering--which I still believe to have borne a kind of enchantment with it. I still believe him, in virtue of this carriage, his animal spirits, his delightful voice, his handsome face and figure, and, for aught I know, of some inborn power of attraction besides (which I think a few people possess), to have carried a spell with him to which it was a natural weakness to yield, and which not many persons could withstand. I could not but see how pleased they were with him, and how they seemed to open their hearts to him in a moment.\n\n‘You must let them know at home, if you please, Mr. Peggotty,’ I said, ‘when that letter is sent, that Mr. Steerforth is very kind to me, and that I don’t know what I should ever do here without him.’\n\n‘Nonsense!’ said Steerforth, laughing. ‘You mustn’t tell them anything of the sort.’\n\n‘And if Mr. Steerforth ever comes into Norfolk or Suffolk, Mr. Peggotty,’ I said, ‘while I am there, you may depend upon it I shall bring him to Yarmouth, if he will let me, to see your house. You never saw such a good house, Steerforth. It’s made out of a boat!’\n\n‘Made out of a boat, is it?’ said Steerforth. ‘It’s the right sort of a house for such a thorough-built boatman.’\n\n‘So ‘tis, sir, so ‘tis, sir,’ said Ham, grinning. ‘You’re right, young gen’l’m’n! Mas’r Davy bor’, gen’l’m’n’s right. A thorough-built boatman! Hor, hor! That’s what he is, too!’\n\nMr. Peggotty was no less pleased than his nephew, though his modesty forbade him to claim a personal compliment so vociferously.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ he said, bowing and chuckling, and tucking in the ends of his neckerchief at his breast: ‘I thankee, sir, I thankee! I do my endeavours in my line of life, sir.’\n\n‘The best of men can do no more, Mr. Peggotty,’ said Steerforth. He had got his name already.\n\n‘I’ll pound it, it’s wot you do yourself, sir,’ said Mr. Peggotty, shaking his head, ‘and wot you do well--right well! I thankee, sir. I’m obleeged to you, sir, for your welcoming manner of me. I’m rough, sir, but I’m ready--least ways, I hope I’m ready, you unnerstand. My house ain’t much for to see, sir, but it’s hearty at your service if ever you should come along with Mas’r Davy to see it. I’m a reg’lar Dodman, I am,’ said Mr. Peggotty, by which he meant snail, and this was in allusion to his being slow to go, for he had attempted to go after every sentence, and had somehow or other come back again; ‘but I wish you both well, and I wish you happy!’\n\nHam echoed this sentiment, and we parted with them in the heartiest manner. I was almost tempted that evening to tell Steerforth about pretty little Em’ly, but I was too timid of mentioning her name, and too much afraid of his laughing at me. I remember that I thought a good deal, and in an uneasy sort of way, about Mr. Peggotty having said that she was getting on to be a woman; but I decided that was nonsense.\n\nWe transported the shellfish, or the ‘relish’ as Mr. Peggotty had modestly called it, up into our room unobserved, and made a great supper that evening. But Traddles couldn’t get happily out of it. He was too unfortunate even to come through a supper like anybody else. He was taken ill in the night--quite prostrate he was--in consequence of Crab; and after being drugged with black draughts and blue pills, to an extent which Demple (whose father was a doctor) said was enough to undermine a horse’s constitution, received a caning and six chapters of Greek Testament for refusing to confess.\n\nThe rest of the half-year is a jumble in my recollection of the daily strife and struggle of our lives; of the waning summer and the changing season; of the frosty mornings when we were rung out of bed, and the cold, cold smell of the dark nights when we were rung into bed again; of the evening schoolroom dimly lighted and indifferently warmed, and the morning schoolroom which was nothing but a great shivering-machine; of the alternation of boiled beef with roast beef, and boiled mutton with roast mutton; of clods of bread-and-butter, dog’s-eared lesson-books, cracked slates, tear-blotted copy-books, canings, rulerings, hair-cuttings, rainy Sundays, suet-puddings, and a dirty atmosphere of ink, surrounding all.\n\nI well remember though, how the distant idea of the holidays, after seeming for an immense time to be a stationary speck, began to come towards us, and to grow and grow. How from counting months, we came to weeks, and then to days; and how I then began to be afraid that I should not be sent for and when I learnt from Steerforth that I had been sent for, and was certainly to go home, had dim forebodings that I might break my leg first. How the breaking-up day changed its place fast, at last, from the week after next to next week, this week, the day after tomorrow, tomorrow, today, tonight--when I was inside the Yarmouth mail, and going home.\n\nI had many a broken sleep inside the Yarmouth mail, and many an incoherent dream of all these things. But when I awoke at intervals, the ground outside the window was not the playground of Salem House, and the sound in my ears was not the sound of Mr. Creakle giving it to Traddles, but the sound of the coachman touching up the horses.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. MY HOLIDAYS. ESPECIALLY ONE HAPPY AFTERNOON\n\nWhen we arrived before day at the inn where the mail stopped, which was not the inn where my friend the waiter lived, I was shown up to a nice little bedroom, with DOLPHIN painted on the door. Very cold I was, I know, notwithstanding the hot tea they had given me before a large fire downstairs; and very glad I was to turn into the Dolphin’s bed, pull the Dolphin’s blankets round my head, and go to sleep.\n\nMr. Barkis the carrier was to call for me in the morning at nine o’clock. I got up at eight, a little giddy from the shortness of my night’s rest, and was ready for him before the appointed time. He received me exactly as if not five minutes had elapsed since we were last together, and I had only been into the hotel to get change for sixpence, or something of that sort.\n\nAs soon as I and my box were in the cart, and the carrier seated, the lazy horse walked away with us all at his accustomed pace.\n\n‘You look very well, Mr. Barkis,’ I said, thinking he would like to know it.\n\nMr. Barkis rubbed his cheek with his cuff, and then looked at his cuff as if he expected to find some of the bloom upon it; but made no other acknowledgement of the compliment.\n\n‘I gave your message, Mr. Barkis,’ I said: ‘I wrote to Peggotty.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\nMr. Barkis seemed gruff, and answered drily.\n\n‘Wasn’t it right, Mr. Barkis?’ I asked, after a little hesitation.\n\n‘Why, no,’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\n‘Not the message?’\n\n‘The message was right enough, perhaps,’ said Mr. Barkis; ‘but it come to an end there.’\n\nNot understanding what he meant, I repeated inquisitively: ‘Came to an end, Mr. Barkis?’\n\n‘Nothing come of it,’ he explained, looking at me sideways. ‘No answer.’\n\n‘There was an answer expected, was there, Mr. Barkis?’ said I, opening my eyes. For this was a new light to me.\n\n‘When a man says he’s willin’,’ said Mr. Barkis, turning his glance slowly on me again, ‘it’s as much as to say, that man’s a-waitin’ for a answer.’\n\n‘Well, Mr. Barkis?’\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Barkis, carrying his eyes back to his horse’s ears; ‘that man’s been a-waitin’ for a answer ever since.’\n\n‘Have you told her so, Mr. Barkis?’\n\n‘No--no,’ growled Mr. Barkis, reflecting about it. ‘I ain’t got no call to go and tell her so. I never said six words to her myself, I ain’t a-goin’ to tell her so.’\n\n‘Would you like me to do it, Mr. Barkis?’ said I, doubtfully. ‘You might tell her, if you would,’ said Mr. Barkis, with another slow look at me, ‘that Barkis was a-waitin’ for a answer. Says you--what name is it?’\n\n‘Her name?’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Barkis, with a nod of his head.\n\n‘Peggotty.’\n\n‘Chrisen name? Or nat’ral name?’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\n‘Oh, it’s not her Christian name. Her Christian name is Clara.’\n\n‘Is it though?’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\nHe seemed to find an immense fund of reflection in this circumstance, and sat pondering and inwardly whistling for some time.\n\n‘Well!’ he resumed at length. ‘Says you, “Peggotty! Barkis is waitin’ for a answer.” Says she, perhaps, “Answer to what?” Says you, “To what I told you.” “What is that?” says she. “Barkis is willin’,” says you.’\n\nThis extremely artful suggestion Mr. Barkis accompanied with a nudge of his elbow that gave me quite a stitch in my side. After that, he slouched over his horse in his usual manner; and made no other reference to the subject except, half an hour afterwards, taking a piece of chalk from his pocket, and writing up, inside the tilt of the cart, ‘Clara Peggotty’--apparently as a private memorandum.\n\nAh, what a strange feeling it was to be going home when it was not home, and to find that every object I looked at, reminded me of the happy old home, which was like a dream I could never dream again! The days when my mother and I and Peggotty were all in all to one another, and there was no one to come between us, rose up before me so sorrowfully on the road, that I am not sure I was glad to be there--not sure but that I would rather have remained away, and forgotten it in Steerforth’s company. But there I was; and soon I was at our house, where the bare old elm-trees wrung their many hands in the bleak wintry air, and shreds of the old rooks’-nests drifted away upon the wind.\n\nThe carrier put my box down at the garden-gate, and left me. I walked along the path towards the house, glancing at the windows, and fearing at every step to see Mr. Murdstone or Miss Murdstone lowering out of one of them. No face appeared, however; and being come to the house, and knowing how to open the door, before dark, without knocking, I went in with a quiet, timid step.\n\nGod knows how infantine the memory may have been, that was awakened within me by the sound of my mother’s voice in the old parlour, when I set foot in the hall. She was singing in a low tone. I think I must have lain in her arms, and heard her singing so to me when I was but a baby. The strain was new to me, and yet it was so old that it filled my heart brim-full; like a friend come back from a long absence.\n\nI believed, from the solitary and thoughtful way in which my mother murmured her song, that she was alone. And I went softly into the room. She was sitting by the fire, suckling an infant, whose tiny hand she held against her neck. Her eyes were looking down upon its face, and she sat singing to it. I was so far right, that she had no other companion.\n\nI spoke to her, and she started, and cried out. But seeing me, she called me her dear Davy, her own boy! and coming half across the room to meet me, kneeled down upon the ground and kissed me, and laid my head down on her bosom near the little creature that was nestling there, and put its hand to my lips.\n\nI wish I had died. I wish I had died then, with that feeling in my heart! I should have been more fit for Heaven than I ever have been since.\n\n‘He is your brother,’ said my mother, fondling me. ‘Davy, my pretty boy! My poor child!’ Then she kissed me more and more, and clasped me round the neck. This she was doing when Peggotty came running in, and bounced down on the ground beside us, and went mad about us both for a quarter of an hour.\n\nIt seemed that I had not been expected so soon, the carrier being much before his usual time. It seemed, too, that Mr. and Miss Murdstone had gone out upon a visit in the neighbourhood, and would not return before night. I had never hoped for this. I had never thought it possible that we three could be together undisturbed, once more; and I felt, for the time, as if the old days were come back.\n\nWe dined together by the fireside. Peggotty was in attendance to wait upon us, but my mother wouldn’t let her do it, and made her dine with us. I had my own old plate, with a brown view of a man-of-war in full sail upon it, which Peggotty had hoarded somewhere all the time I had been away, and would not have had broken, she said, for a hundred pounds. I had my own old mug with David on it, and my own old little knife and fork that wouldn’t cut.\n\nWhile we were at table, I thought it a favourable occasion to tell Peggotty about Mr. Barkis, who, before I had finished what I had to tell her, began to laugh, and throw her apron over her face.\n\n‘Peggotty,’ said my mother. ‘What’s the matter?’\n\nPeggotty only laughed the more, and held her apron tight over her face when my mother tried to pull it away, and sat as if her head were in a bag.\n\n‘What are you doing, you stupid creature?’ said my mother, laughing.\n\n‘Oh, drat the man!’ cried Peggotty. ‘He wants to marry me.’\n\n‘It would be a very good match for you; wouldn’t it?’ said my mother.\n\n‘Oh! I don’t know,’ said Peggotty. ‘Don’t ask me. I wouldn’t have him if he was made of gold. Nor I wouldn’t have anybody.’\n\n‘Then, why don’t you tell him so, you ridiculous thing?’ said my mother.\n\n‘Tell him so,’ retorted Peggotty, looking out of her apron. ‘He has never said a word to me about it. He knows better. If he was to make so bold as say a word to me, I should slap his face.’\n\nHer own was as red as ever I saw it, or any other face, I think; but she only covered it again, for a few moments at a time, when she was taken with a violent fit of laughter; and after two or three of those attacks, went on with her dinner.\n\nI remarked that my mother, though she smiled when Peggotty looked at her, became more serious and thoughtful. I had seen at first that she was changed. Her face was very pretty still, but it looked careworn, and too delicate; and her hand was so thin and white that it seemed to me to be almost transparent. But the change to which I now refer was superadded to this: it was in her manner, which became anxious and fluttered. At last she said, putting out her hand, and laying it affectionately on the hand of her old servant,\n\n‘Peggotty, dear, you are not going to be married?’\n\n‘Me, ma’am?’ returned Peggotty, staring. ‘Lord bless you, no!’\n\n‘Not just yet?’ said my mother, tenderly.\n\n‘Never!’ cried Peggotty.\n\nMy mother took her hand, and said:\n\n‘Don’t leave me, Peggotty. Stay with me. It will not be for long, perhaps. What should I ever do without you!’\n\n‘Me leave you, my precious!’ cried Peggotty. ‘Not for all the world and his wife. Why, what’s put that in your silly little head?’--For Peggotty had been used of old to talk to my mother sometimes like a child.\n\nBut my mother made no answer, except to thank her, and Peggotty went running on in her own fashion.\n\n‘Me leave you? I think I see myself. Peggotty go away from you? I should like to catch her at it! No, no, no,’ said Peggotty, shaking her head, and folding her arms; ‘not she, my dear. It isn’t that there ain’t some Cats that would be well enough pleased if she did, but they sha’n’t be pleased. They shall be aggravated. I’ll stay with you till I am a cross cranky old woman. And when I’m too deaf, and too lame, and too blind, and too mumbly for want of teeth, to be of any use at all, even to be found fault with, than I shall go to my Davy, and ask him to take me in.’\n\n‘And, Peggotty,’ says I, ‘I shall be glad to see you, and I’ll make you as welcome as a queen.’\n\n‘Bless your dear heart!’ cried Peggotty. ‘I know you will!’ And she kissed me beforehand, in grateful acknowledgement of my hospitality. After that, she covered her head up with her apron again and had another laugh about Mr. Barkis. After that, she took the baby out of its little cradle, and nursed it. After that, she cleared the dinner table; after that, came in with another cap on, and her work-box, and the yard-measure, and the bit of wax-candle, all just the same as ever.\n\nWe sat round the fire, and talked delightfully. I told them what a hard master Mr. Creakle was, and they pitied me very much. I told them what a fine fellow Steerforth was, and what a patron of mine, and Peggotty said she would walk a score of miles to see him. I took the little baby in my arms when it was awake, and nursed it lovingly. When it was asleep again, I crept close to my mother’s side according to my old custom, broken now a long time, and sat with my arms embracing her waist, and my little red cheek on her shoulder, and once more felt her beautiful hair drooping over me--like an angel’s wing as I used to think, I recollect--and was very happy indeed.\n\nWhile I sat thus, looking at the fire, and seeing pictures in the red-hot coals, I almost believed that I had never been away; that Mr. and Miss Murdstone were such pictures, and would vanish when the fire got low; and that there was nothing real in all that I remembered, save my mother, Peggotty, and I.\n\nPeggotty darned away at a stocking as long as she could see, and then sat with it drawn on her left hand like a glove, and her needle in her right, ready to take another stitch whenever there was a blaze. I cannot conceive whose stockings they can have been that Peggotty was always darning, or where such an unfailing supply of stockings in want of darning can have come from. From my earliest infancy she seems to have been always employed in that class of needlework, and never by any chance in any other.\n\n‘I wonder,’ said Peggotty, who was sometimes seized with a fit of wondering on some most unexpected topic, ‘what’s become of Davy’s great-aunt?’ ‘Lor, Peggotty!’ observed my mother, rousing herself from a reverie, ‘what nonsense you talk!’\n\n‘Well, but I really do wonder, ma’am,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘What can have put such a person in your head?’ inquired my mother. ‘Is there nobody else in the world to come there?’\n\n‘I don’t know how it is,’ said Peggotty, ‘unless it’s on account of being stupid, but my head never can pick and choose its people. They come and they go, and they don’t come and they don’t go, just as they like. I wonder what’s become of her?’\n\n‘How absurd you are, Peggotty!’ returned my mother. ‘One would suppose you wanted a second visit from her.’\n\n‘Lord forbid!’ cried Peggotty.\n\n‘Well then, don’t talk about such uncomfortable things, there’s a good soul,’ said my mother. ‘Miss Betsey is shut up in her cottage by the sea, no doubt, and will remain there. At all events, she is not likely ever to trouble us again.’\n\n‘No!’ mused Peggotty. ‘No, that ain’t likely at all.---I wonder, if she was to die, whether she’d leave Davy anything?’\n\n‘Good gracious me, Peggotty,’ returned my mother, ‘what a nonsensical woman you are! when you know that she took offence at the poor dear boy’s ever being born at all.’\n\n‘I suppose she wouldn’t be inclined to forgive him now,’ hinted Peggotty.\n\n‘Why should she be inclined to forgive him now?’ said my mother, rather sharply.\n\n‘Now that he’s got a brother, I mean,’ said Peggotty.\n\nMy mother immediately began to cry, and wondered how Peggotty dared to say such a thing.\n\n‘As if this poor little innocent in its cradle had ever done any harm to you or anybody else, you jealous thing!’ said she. ‘You had much better go and marry Mr. Barkis, the carrier. Why don’t you?’\n\n‘I should make Miss Murdstone happy, if I was to,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘What a bad disposition you have, Peggotty!’ returned my mother. ‘You are as jealous of Miss Murdstone as it is possible for a ridiculous creature to be. You want to keep the keys yourself, and give out all the things, I suppose? I shouldn’t be surprised if you did. When you know that she only does it out of kindness and the best intentions! You know she does, Peggotty--you know it well.’\n\nPeggotty muttered something to the effect of ‘Bother the best intentions!’ and something else to the effect that there was a little too much of the best intentions going on.\n\n‘I know what you mean, you cross thing,’ said my mother. ‘I understand you, Peggotty, perfectly. You know I do, and I wonder you don’t colour up like fire. But one point at a time. Miss Murdstone is the point now, Peggotty, and you sha’n’t escape from it. Haven’t you heard her say, over and over again, that she thinks I am too thoughtless and too--a--a--’\n\n‘Pretty,’ suggested Peggotty.\n\n‘Well,’ returned my mother, half laughing, ‘and if she is so silly as to say so, can I be blamed for it?’\n\n‘No one says you can,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘No, I should hope not, indeed!’ returned my mother. ‘Haven’t you heard her say, over and over again, that on this account she wished to spare me a great deal of trouble, which she thinks I am not suited for, and which I really don’t know myself that I AM suited for; and isn’t she up early and late, and going to and fro continually--and doesn’t she do all sorts of things, and grope into all sorts of places, coal-holes and pantries and I don’t know where, that can’t be very agreeable--and do you mean to insinuate that there is not a sort of devotion in that?’\n\n‘I don’t insinuate at all,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘You do, Peggotty,’ returned my mother. ‘You never do anything else, except your work. You are always insinuating. You revel in it. And when you talk of Mr. Murdstone’s good intentions--’\n\n‘I never talked of ‘em,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘No, Peggotty,’ returned my mother, ‘but you insinuated. That’s what I told you just now. That’s the worst of you. You WILL insinuate. I said, at the moment, that I understood you, and you see I did. When you talk of Mr. Murdstone’s good intentions, and pretend to slight them (for I don’t believe you really do, in your heart, Peggotty), you must be as well convinced as I am how good they are, and how they actuate him in everything. If he seems to have been at all stern with a certain person, Peggotty--you understand, and so I am sure does Davy, that I am not alluding to anybody present--it is solely because he is satisfied that it is for a certain person’s benefit. He naturally loves a certain person, on my account; and acts solely for a certain person’s good. He is better able to judge of it than I am; for I very well know that I am a weak, light, girlish creature, and that he is a firm, grave, serious man. And he takes,’ said my mother, with the tears which were engendered in her affectionate nature, stealing down her face, ‘he takes great pains with me; and I ought to be very thankful to him, and very submissive to him even in my thoughts; and when I am not, Peggotty, I worry and condemn myself, and feel doubtful of my own heart, and don’t know what to do.’\n\nPeggotty sat with her chin on the foot of the stocking, looking silently at the fire.\n\n‘There, Peggotty,’ said my mother, changing her tone, ‘don’t let us fall out with one another, for I couldn’t bear it. You are my true friend, I know, if I have any in the world. When I call you a ridiculous creature, or a vexatious thing, or anything of that sort, Peggotty, I only mean that you are my true friend, and always have been, ever since the night when Mr. Copperfield first brought me home here, and you came out to the gate to meet me.’\n\nPeggotty was not slow to respond, and ratify the treaty of friendship by giving me one of her best hugs. I think I had some glimpses of the real character of this conversation at the time; but I am sure, now, that the good creature originated it, and took her part in it, merely that my mother might comfort herself with the little contradictory summary in which she had indulged. The design was efficacious; for I remember that my mother seemed more at ease during the rest of the evening, and that Peggotty observed her less.\n\nWhen we had had our tea, and the ashes were thrown up, and the candles snuffed, I read Peggotty a chapter out of the Crocodile Book, in remembrance of old times--she took it out of her pocket: I don’t know whether she had kept it there ever since--and then we talked about Salem House, which brought me round again to Steerforth, who was my great subject. We were very happy; and that evening, as the last of its race, and destined evermore to close that volume of my life, will never pass out of my memory.\n\nIt was almost ten o’clock before we heard the sound of wheels. We all got up then; and my mother said hurriedly that, as it was so late, and Mr. and Miss Murdstone approved of early hours for young people, perhaps I had better go to bed. I kissed her, and went upstairs with my candle directly, before they came in. It appeared to my childish fancy, as I ascended to the bedroom where I had been imprisoned, that they brought a cold blast of air into the house which blew away the old familiar feeling like a feather.\n\nI felt uncomfortable about going down to breakfast in the morning, as I had never set eyes on Mr. Murdstone since the day when I committed my memorable offence. However, as it must be done, I went down, after two or three false starts half-way, and as many runs back on tiptoe to my own room, and presented myself in the parlour.\n\nHe was standing before the fire with his back to it, while Miss Murdstone made the tea. He looked at me steadily as I entered, but made no sign of recognition whatever. I went up to him, after a moment of confusion, and said: ‘I beg your pardon, sir. I am very sorry for what I did, and I hope you will forgive me.’\n\n‘I am glad to hear you are sorry, David,’ he replied.\n\nThe hand he gave me was the hand I had bitten. I could not restrain my eye from resting for an instant on a red spot upon it; but it was not so red as I turned, when I met that sinister expression in his face.\n\n‘How do you do, ma’am?’ I said to Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘Ah, dear me!’ sighed Miss Murdstone, giving me the tea-caddy scoop instead of her fingers. ‘How long are the holidays?’\n\n‘A month, ma’am.’\n\n‘Counting from when?’\n\n‘From today, ma’am.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Miss Murdstone. ‘Then here’s one day off.’\n\nShe kept a calendar of the holidays in this way, and every morning checked a day off in exactly the same manner. She did it gloomily until she came to ten, but when she got into two figures she became more hopeful, and, as the time advanced, even jocular.\n\nIt was on this very first day that I had the misfortune to throw her, though she was not subject to such weakness in general, into a state of violent consternation. I came into the room where she and my mother were sitting; and the baby (who was only a few weeks old) being on my mother’s lap, I took it very carefully in my arms. Suddenly Miss Murdstone gave such a scream that I all but dropped it.\n\n‘My dear Jane!’ cried my mother.\n\n‘Good heavens, Clara, do you see?’ exclaimed Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘See what, my dear Jane?’ said my mother; ‘where?’\n\n‘He’s got it!’ cried Miss Murdstone. ‘The boy has got the baby!’\n\nShe was limp with horror; but stiffened herself to make a dart at me, and take it out of my arms. Then, she turned faint; and was so very ill that they were obliged to give her cherry brandy. I was solemnly interdicted by her, on her recovery, from touching my brother any more on any pretence whatever; and my poor mother, who, I could see, wished otherwise, meekly confirmed the interdict, by saying: ‘No doubt you are right, my dear Jane.’\n\nOn another occasion, when we three were together, this same dear baby--it was truly dear to me, for our mother’s sake--was the innocent occasion of Miss Murdstone’s going into a passion. My mother, who had been looking at its eyes as it lay upon her lap, said:\n\n‘Davy! come here!’ and looked at mine.\n\nI saw Miss Murdstone lay her beads down.\n\n‘I declare,’ said my mother, gently, ‘they are exactly alike. I suppose they are mine. I think they are the colour of mine. But they are wonderfully alike.’\n\n‘What are you talking about, Clara?’ said Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘My dear Jane,’ faltered my mother, a little abashed by the harsh tone of this inquiry, ‘I find that the baby’s eyes and Davy’s are exactly alike.’\n\n‘Clara!’ said Miss Murdstone, rising angrily, ‘you are a positive fool sometimes.’\n\n‘My dear Jane,’ remonstrated my mother.\n\n‘A positive fool,’ said Miss Murdstone. ‘Who else could compare my brother’s baby with your boy? They are not at all alike. They are exactly unlike. They are utterly dissimilar in all respects. I hope they will ever remain so. I will not sit here, and hear such comparisons made.’ With that she stalked out, and made the door bang after her.\n\nIn short, I was not a favourite with Miss Murdstone. In short, I was not a favourite there with anybody, not even with myself; for those who did like me could not show it, and those who did not, showed it so plainly that I had a sensitive consciousness of always appearing constrained, boorish, and dull.\n\nI felt that I made them as uncomfortable as they made me. If I came into the room where they were, and they were talking together and my mother seemed cheerful, an anxious cloud would steal over her face from the moment of my entrance. If Mr. Murdstone were in his best humour, I checked him. If Miss Murdstone were in her worst, I intensified it. I had perception enough to know that my mother was the victim always; that she was afraid to speak to me or to be kind to me, lest she should give them some offence by her manner of doing so, and receive a lecture afterwards; that she was not only ceaselessly afraid of her own offending, but of my offending, and uneasily watched their looks if I only moved. Therefore I resolved to keep myself as much out of their way as I could; and many a wintry hour did I hear the church clock strike, when I was sitting in my cheerless bedroom, wrapped in my little great-coat, poring over a book.\n\nIn the evening, sometimes, I went and sat with Peggotty in the kitchen. There I was comfortable, and not afraid of being myself. But neither of these resources was approved of in the parlour. The tormenting humour which was dominant there stopped them both. I was still held to be necessary to my poor mother’s training, and, as one of her trials, could not be suffered to absent myself.\n\n‘David,’ said Mr. Murdstone, one day after dinner when I was going to leave the room as usual; ‘I am sorry to observe that you are of a sullen disposition.’\n\n‘As sulky as a bear!’ said Miss Murdstone.\n\nI stood still, and hung my head.\n\n‘Now, David,’ said Mr. Murdstone, ‘a sullen obdurate disposition is, of all tempers, the worst.’\n\n‘And the boy’s is, of all such dispositions that ever I have seen,’ remarked his sister, ‘the most confirmed and stubborn. I think, my dear Clara, even you must observe it?’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, my dear Jane,’ said my mother, ‘but are you quite sure--I am certain you’ll excuse me, my dear Jane--that you understand Davy?’\n\n‘I should be somewhat ashamed of myself, Clara,’ returned Miss Murdstone, ‘if I could not understand the boy, or any boy. I don’t profess to be profound; but I do lay claim to common sense.’\n\n‘No doubt, my dear Jane,’ returned my mother, ‘your understanding is very vigorous--’\n\n‘Oh dear, no! Pray don’t say that, Clara,’ interposed Miss Murdstone, angrily.\n\n‘But I am sure it is,’ resumed my mother; ‘and everybody knows it is. I profit so much by it myself, in many ways--at least I ought to--that no one can be more convinced of it than myself; and therefore I speak with great diffidence, my dear Jane, I assure you.’\n\n‘We’ll say I don’t understand the boy, Clara,’ returned Miss Murdstone, arranging the little fetters on her wrists. ‘We’ll agree, if you please, that I don’t understand him at all. He is much too deep for me. But perhaps my brother’s penetration may enable him to have some insight into his character. And I believe my brother was speaking on the subject when we--not very decently--interrupted him.’\n\n‘I think, Clara,’ said Mr. Murdstone, in a low grave voice, ‘that there may be better and more dispassionate judges of such a question than you.’\n\n‘Edward,’ replied my mother, timidly, ‘you are a far better judge of all questions than I pretend to be. Both you and Jane are. I only said--’\n\n‘You only said something weak and inconsiderate,’ he replied. ‘Try not to do it again, my dear Clara, and keep a watch upon yourself.’\n\nMy mother’s lips moved, as if she answered ‘Yes, my dear Edward,’ but she said nothing aloud.\n\n‘I was sorry, David, I remarked,’ said Mr. Murdstone, turning his head and his eyes stiffly towards me, ‘to observe that you are of a sullen disposition. This is not a character that I can suffer to develop itself beneath my eyes without an effort at improvement. You must endeavour, sir, to change it. We must endeavour to change it for you.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ I faltered. ‘I have never meant to be sullen since I came back.’\n\n‘Don’t take refuge in a lie, sir!’ he returned so fiercely, that I saw my mother involuntarily put out her trembling hand as if to interpose between us. ‘You have withdrawn yourself in your sullenness to your own room. You have kept your own room when you ought to have been here. You know now, once for all, that I require you to be here, and not there. Further, that I require you to bring obedience here. You know me, David. I will have it done.’\n\nMiss Murdstone gave a hoarse chuckle.\n\n‘I will have a respectful, prompt, and ready bearing towards myself,’ he continued, ‘and towards Jane Murdstone, and towards your mother. I will not have this room shunned as if it were infected, at the pleasure of a child. Sit down.’\n\nHe ordered me like a dog, and I obeyed like a dog.\n\n‘One thing more,’ he said. ‘I observe that you have an attachment to low and common company. You are not to associate with servants. The kitchen will not improve you, in the many respects in which you need improvement. Of the woman who abets you, I say nothing--since you, Clara,’ addressing my mother in a lower voice, ‘from old associations and long-established fancies, have a weakness respecting her which is not yet overcome.’\n\n‘A most unaccountable delusion it is!’ cried Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘I only say,’ he resumed, addressing me, ‘that I disapprove of your preferring such company as Mistress Peggotty, and that it is to be abandoned. Now, David, you understand me, and you know what will be the consequence if you fail to obey me to the letter.’\n\nI knew well--better perhaps than he thought, as far as my poor mother was concerned--and I obeyed him to the letter. I retreated to my own room no more; I took refuge with Peggotty no more; but sat wearily in the parlour day after day, looking forward to night, and bedtime.\n\nWhat irksome constraint I underwent, sitting in the same attitude hours upon hours, afraid to move an arm or a leg lest Miss Murdstone should complain (as she did on the least pretence) of my restlessness, and afraid to move an eye lest she should light on some look of dislike or scrutiny that would find new cause for complaint in mine! What intolerable dulness to sit listening to the ticking of the clock; and watching Miss Murdstone’s little shiny steel beads as she strung them; and wondering whether she would ever be married, and if so, to what sort of unhappy man; and counting the divisions in the moulding of the chimney-piece; and wandering away, with my eyes, to the ceiling, among the curls and corkscrews in the paper on the wall!\n\nWhat walks I took alone, down muddy lanes, in the bad winter weather, carrying that parlour, and Mr. and Miss Murdstone in it, everywhere: a monstrous load that I was obliged to bear, a daymare that there was no possibility of breaking in, a weight that brooded on my wits, and blunted them!\n\nWhat meals I had in silence and embarrassment, always feeling that there were a knife and fork too many, and that mine; an appetite too many, and that mine; a plate and chair too many, and those mine; a somebody too many, and that I!\n\nWhat evenings, when the candles came, and I was expected to employ myself, but, not daring to read an entertaining book, pored over some hard-headed, harder-hearted treatise on arithmetic; when the tables of weights and measures set themselves to tunes, as ‘Rule Britannia’, or ‘Away with Melancholy’; when they wouldn’t stand still to be learnt, but would go threading my grandmother’s needle through my unfortunate head, in at one ear and out at the other! What yawns and dozes I lapsed into, in spite of all my care; what starts I came out of concealed sleeps with; what answers I never got, to little observations that I rarely made; what a blank space I seemed, which everybody overlooked, and yet was in everybody’s way; what a heavy relief it was to hear Miss Murdstone hail the first stroke of nine at night, and order me to bed!\n\nThus the holidays lagged away, until the morning came when Miss Murdstone said: ‘Here’s the last day off!’ and gave me the closing cup of tea of the vacation.\n\nI was not sorry to go. I had lapsed into a stupid state; but I was recovering a little and looking forward to Steerforth, albeit Mr. Creakle loomed behind him. Again Mr. Barkis appeared at the gate, and again Miss Murdstone in her warning voice, said: ‘Clara!’ when my mother bent over me, to bid me farewell.\n\nI kissed her, and my baby brother, and was very sorry then; but not sorry to go away, for the gulf between us was there, and the parting was there, every day. And it is not so much the embrace she gave me, that lives in my mind, though it was as fervent as could be, as what followed the embrace.\n\nI was in the carrier’s cart when I heard her calling to me. I looked out, and she stood at the garden-gate alone, holding her baby up in her arms for me to see. It was cold still weather; and not a hair of her head, nor a fold of her dress, was stirred, as she looked intently at me, holding up her child.\n\nSo I lost her. So I saw her afterwards, in my sleep at school--a silent presence near my bed--looking at me with the same intent face--holding up her baby in her arms.\n\n# CHAPTER 9. I HAVE A MEMORABLE BIRTHDAY\n\nI PASS over all that happened at school, until the anniversary of my birthday came round in March. Except that Steerforth was more to be admired than ever, I remember nothing. He was going away at the end of the half-year, if not sooner, and was more spirited and independent than before in my eyes, and therefore more engaging than before; but beyond this I remember nothing. The great remembrance by which that time is marked in my mind, seems to have swallowed up all lesser recollections, and to exist alone.\n\nIt is even difficult for me to believe that there was a gap of full two months between my return to Salem House and the arrival of that birthday. I can only understand that the fact was so, because I know it must have been so; otherwise I should feel convinced that there was no interval, and that the one occasion trod upon the other’s heels.\n\nHow well I recollect the kind of day it was! I smell the fog that hung about the place; I see the hoar frost, ghostly, through it; I feel my rimy hair fall clammy on my cheek; I look along the dim perspective of the schoolroom, with a sputtering candle here and there to light up the foggy morning, and the breath of the boys wreathing and smoking in the raw cold as they blow upon their fingers, and tap their feet upon the floor. It was after breakfast, and we had been summoned in from the playground, when Mr. Sharp entered and said:\n\n‘David Copperfield is to go into the parlour.’\n\nI expected a hamper from Peggotty, and brightened at the order. Some of the boys about me put in their claim not to be forgotten in the distribution of the good things, as I got out of my seat with great alacrity.\n\n‘Don’t hurry, David,’ said Mr. Sharp. ‘There’s time enough, my boy, don’t hurry.’\n\nI might have been surprised by the feeling tone in which he spoke, if I had given it a thought; but I gave it none until afterwards. I hurried away to the parlour; and there I found Mr. Creakle, sitting at his breakfast with the cane and a newspaper before him, and Mrs. Creakle with an opened letter in her hand. But no hamper.\n\n‘David Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Creakle, leading me to a sofa, and sitting down beside me. ‘I want to speak to you very particularly. I have something to tell you, my child.’\n\nMr. Creakle, at whom of course I looked, shook his head without looking at me, and stopped up a sigh with a very large piece of buttered toast.\n\n‘You are too young to know how the world changes every day,’ said Mrs. Creakle, ‘and how the people in it pass away. But we all have to learn it, David; some of us when we are young, some of us when we are old, some of us at all times of our lives.’\n\nI looked at her earnestly.\n\n‘When you came away from home at the end of the vacation,’ said Mrs. Creakle, after a pause, ‘were they all well?’ After another pause, ‘Was your mama well?’\n\nI trembled without distinctly knowing why, and still looked at her earnestly, making no attempt to answer.\n\n‘Because,’ said she, ‘I grieve to tell you that I hear this morning your mama is very ill.’\n\nA mist rose between Mrs. Creakle and me, and her figure seemed to move in it for an instant. Then I felt the burning tears run down my face, and it was steady again.\n\n‘She is very dangerously ill,’ she added.\n\nI knew all now.\n\n‘She is dead.’\n\nThere was no need to tell me so. I had already broken out into a desolate cry, and felt an orphan in the wide world.\n\nShe was very kind to me. She kept me there all day, and left me alone sometimes; and I cried, and wore myself to sleep, and awoke and cried again. When I could cry no more, I began to think; and then the oppression on my breast was heaviest, and my grief a dull pain that there was no ease for.\n\nAnd yet my thoughts were idle; not intent on the calamity that weighed upon my heart, but idly loitering near it. I thought of our house shut up and hushed. I thought of the little baby, who, Mrs. Creakle said, had been pining away for some time, and who, they believed, would die too. I thought of my father’s grave in the churchyard, by our house, and of my mother lying there beneath the tree I knew so well. I stood upon a chair when I was left alone, and looked into the glass to see how red my eyes were, and how sorrowful my face. I considered, after some hours were gone, if my tears were really hard to flow now, as they seemed to be, what, in connexion with my loss, it would affect me most to think of when I drew near home--for I was going home to the funeral. I am sensible of having felt that a dignity attached to me among the rest of the boys, and that I was important in my affliction.\n\nIf ever child were stricken with sincere grief, I was. But I remember that this importance was a kind of satisfaction to me, when I walked in the playground that afternoon while the boys were in school. When I saw them glancing at me out of the windows, as they went up to their classes, I felt distinguished, and looked more melancholy, and walked slower. When school was over, and they came out and spoke to me, I felt it rather good in myself not to be proud to any of them, and to take exactly the same notice of them all, as before.\n\nI was to go home next night; not by the mail, but by the heavy night-coach, which was called the Farmer, and was principally used by country-people travelling short intermediate distances upon the road. We had no story-telling that evening, and Traddles insisted on lending me his pillow. I don’t know what good he thought it would do me, for I had one of my own: but it was all he had to lend, poor fellow, except a sheet of letter-paper full of skeletons; and that he gave me at parting, as a soother of my sorrows and a contribution to my peace of mind.\n\nI left Salem House upon the morrow afternoon. I little thought then that I left it, never to return. We travelled very slowly all night, and did not get into Yarmouth before nine or ten o’clock in the morning. I looked out for Mr. Barkis, but he was not there; and instead of him a fat, short-winded, merry-looking, little old man in black, with rusty little bunches of ribbons at the knees of his breeches, black stockings, and a broad-brimmed hat, came puffing up to the coach window, and said:\n\n‘Master Copperfield?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘Will you come with me, young sir, if you please,’ he said, opening the door, ‘and I shall have the pleasure of taking you home.’\n\nI put my hand in his, wondering who he was, and we walked away to a shop in a narrow street, on which was written OMER, DRAPER, TAILOR, HABERDASHER, FUNERAL FURNISHER, &c. It was a close and stifling little shop; full of all sorts of clothing, made and unmade, including one window full of beaver-hats and bonnets. We went into a little back-parlour behind the shop, where we found three young women at work on a quantity of black materials, which were heaped upon the table, and little bits and cuttings of which were littered all over the floor. There was a good fire in the room, and a breathless smell of warm black crape--I did not know what the smell was then, but I know now.\n\nThe three young women, who appeared to be very industrious and comfortable, raised their heads to look at me, and then went on with their work. Stitch, stitch, stitch. At the same time there came from a workshop across a little yard outside the window, a regular sound of hammering that kept a kind of tune: RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, without any variation.\n\n‘Well,’ said my conductor to one of the three young women. ‘How do you get on, Minnie?’\n\n‘We shall be ready by the trying-on time,’ she replied gaily, without looking up. ‘Don’t you be afraid, father.’\n\nMr. Omer took off his broad-brimmed hat, and sat down and panted. He was so fat that he was obliged to pant some time before he could say:\n\n‘That’s right.’\n\n‘Father!’ said Minnie, playfully. ‘What a porpoise you do grow!’\n\n‘Well, I don’t know how it is, my dear,’ he replied, considering about it. ‘I am rather so.’\n\n‘You are such a comfortable man, you see,’ said Minnie. ‘You take things so easy.’\n\n‘No use taking ‘em otherwise, my dear,’ said Mr. Omer.\n\n‘No, indeed,’ returned his daughter. ‘We are all pretty gay here, thank Heaven! Ain’t we, father?’\n\n‘I hope so, my dear,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘As I have got my breath now, I think I’ll measure this young scholar. Would you walk into the shop, Master Copperfield?’\n\nI preceded Mr. Omer, in compliance with his request; and after showing me a roll of cloth which he said was extra super, and too good mourning for anything short of parents, he took my various dimensions, and put them down in a book. While he was recording them he called my attention to his stock in trade, and to certain fashions which he said had ‘just come up’, and to certain other fashions which he said had ‘just gone out’.\n\n‘And by that sort of thing we very often lose a little mint of money,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘But fashions are like human beings. They come in, nobody knows when, why, or how; and they go out, nobody knows when, why, or how. Everything is like life, in my opinion, if you look at it in that point of view.’\n\nI was too sorrowful to discuss the question, which would possibly have been beyond me under any circumstances; and Mr. Omer took me back into the parlour, breathing with some difficulty on the way.\n\nHe then called down a little break-neck range of steps behind a door: ‘Bring up that tea and bread-and-butter!’ which, after some time, during which I sat looking about me and thinking, and listening to the stitching in the room and the tune that was being hammered across the yard, appeared on a tray, and turned out to be for me.\n\n‘I have been acquainted with you,’ said Mr. Omer, after watching me for some minutes, during which I had not made much impression on the breakfast, for the black things destroyed my appetite, ‘I have been acquainted with you a long time, my young friend.’\n\n‘Have you, sir?’\n\n‘All your life,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘I may say before it. I knew your father before you. He was five foot nine and a half, and he lays in five-and-twen-ty foot of ground.’\n\n‘RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat,’ across the yard.\n\n‘He lays in five and twen-ty foot of ground, if he lays in a fraction,’ said Mr. Omer, pleasantly. ‘It was either his request or her direction, I forget which.’\n\n‘Do you know how my little brother is, sir?’ I inquired.\n\nMr. Omer shook his head.\n\n‘RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat.’\n\n‘He is in his mother’s arms,’ said he.\n\n‘Oh, poor little fellow! Is he dead?’\n\n‘Don’t mind it more than you can help,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Yes. The baby’s dead.’\n\nMy wounds broke out afresh at this intelligence. I left the scarcely-tasted breakfast, and went and rested my head on another table, in a corner of the little room, which Minnie hastily cleared, lest I should spot the mourning that was lying there with my tears. She was a pretty, good-natured girl, and put my hair away from my eyes with a soft, kind touch; but she was very cheerful at having nearly finished her work and being in good time, and was so different from me!\n\nPresently the tune left off, and a good-looking young fellow came across the yard into the room. He had a hammer in his hand, and his mouth was full of little nails, which he was obliged to take out before he could speak.\n\n‘Well, Joram!’ said Mr. Omer. ‘How do you get on?’\n\n‘All right,’ said Joram. ‘Done, sir.’\n\nMinnie coloured a little, and the other two girls smiled at one another.\n\n‘What! you were at it by candle-light last night, when I was at the club, then? Were you?’ said Mr. Omer, shutting up one eye.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Joram. ‘As you said we could make a little trip of it, and go over together, if it was done, Minnie and me--and you.’\n\n‘Oh! I thought you were going to leave me out altogether,’ said Mr. Omer, laughing till he coughed.\n\n‘--As you was so good as to say that,’ resumed the young man, ‘why I turned to with a will, you see. Will you give me your opinion of it?’\n\n‘I will,’ said Mr. Omer, rising. ‘My dear’; and he stopped and turned to me: ‘would you like to see your--’\n\n‘No, father,’ Minnie interposed.\n\n‘I thought it might be agreeable, my dear,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘But perhaps you’re right.’\n\nI can’t say how I knew it was my dear, dear mother’s coffin that they went to look at. I had never heard one making; I had never seen one that I know of.--but it came into my mind what the noise was, while it was going on; and when the young man entered, I am sure I knew what he had been doing.\n\nThe work being now finished, the two girls, whose names I had not heard, brushed the shreds and threads from their dresses, and went into the shop to put that to rights, and wait for customers. Minnie stayed behind to fold up what they had made, and pack it in two baskets. This she did upon her knees, humming a lively little tune the while. Joram, who I had no doubt was her lover, came in and stole a kiss from her while she was busy (he didn’t appear to mind me, at all), and said her father was gone for the chaise, and he must make haste and get himself ready. Then he went out again; and then she put her thimble and scissors in her pocket, and stuck a needle threaded with black thread neatly in the bosom of her gown, and put on her outer clothing smartly, at a little glass behind the door, in which I saw the reflection of her pleased face.\n\nAll this I observed, sitting at the table in the corner with my head leaning on my hand, and my thoughts running on very different things. The chaise soon came round to the front of the shop, and the baskets being put in first, I was put in next, and those three followed. I remember it as a kind of half chaise-cart, half pianoforte-van, painted of a sombre colour, and drawn by a black horse with a long tail. There was plenty of room for us all.\n\nI do not think I have ever experienced so strange a feeling in my life (I am wiser now, perhaps) as that of being with them, remembering how they had been employed, and seeing them enjoy the ride. I was not angry with them; I was more afraid of them, as if I were cast away among creatures with whom I had no community of nature. They were very cheerful. The old man sat in front to drive, and the two young people sat behind him, and whenever he spoke to them leaned forward, the one on one side of his chubby face and the other on the other, and made a great deal of him. They would have talked to me too, but I held back, and moped in my corner; scared by their love-making and hilarity, though it was far from boisterous, and almost wondering that no judgement came upon them for their hardness of heart.\n\nSo, when they stopped to bait the horse, and ate and drank and enjoyed themselves, I could touch nothing that they touched, but kept my fast unbroken. So, when we reached home, I dropped out of the chaise behind, as quickly as possible, that I might not be in their company before those solemn windows, looking blindly on me like closed eyes once bright. And oh, how little need I had had to think what would move me to tears when I came back--seeing the window of my mother’s room, and next it that which, in the better time, was mine!\n\nI was in Peggotty’s arms before I got to the door, and she took me into the house. Her grief burst out when she first saw me; but she controlled it soon, and spoke in whispers, and walked softly, as if the dead could be disturbed. She had not been in bed, I found, for a long time. She sat up at night still, and watched. As long as her poor dear pretty was above the ground, she said, she would never desert her.\n\nMr. Murdstone took no heed of me when I went into the parlour where he was, but sat by the fireside, weeping silently, and pondering in his elbow-chair. Miss Murdstone, who was busy at her writing-desk, which was covered with letters and papers, gave me her cold finger-nails, and asked me, in an iron whisper, if I had been measured for my mourning.\n\nI said: ‘Yes.’\n\n‘And your shirts,’ said Miss Murdstone; ‘have you brought ‘em home?’\n\n‘Yes, ma’am. I have brought home all my clothes.’\n\nThis was all the consolation that her firmness administered to me. I do not doubt that she had a choice pleasure in exhibiting what she called her self-command, and her firmness, and her strength of mind, and her common sense, and the whole diabolical catalogue of her unamiable qualities, on such an occasion. She was particularly proud of her turn for business; and she showed it now in reducing everything to pen and ink, and being moved by nothing. All the rest of that day, and from morning to night afterwards, she sat at that desk, scratching composedly with a hard pen, speaking in the same imperturbable whisper to everybody; never relaxing a muscle of her face, or softening a tone of her voice, or appearing with an atom of her dress astray.\n\nHer brother took a book sometimes, but never read it that I saw. He would open it and look at it as if he were reading, but would remain for a whole hour without turning the leaf, and then put it down and walk to and fro in the room. I used to sit with folded hands watching him, and counting his footsteps, hour after hour. He very seldom spoke to her, and never to me. He seemed to be the only restless thing, except the clocks, in the whole motionless house.\n\nIn these days before the funeral, I saw but little of Peggotty, except that, in passing up or down stairs, I always found her close to the room where my mother and her baby lay, and except that she came to me every night, and sat by my bed’s head while I went to sleep. A day or two before the burial--I think it was a day or two before, but I am conscious of confusion in my mind about that heavy time, with nothing to mark its progress--she took me into the room. I only recollect that underneath some white covering on the bed, with a beautiful cleanliness and freshness all around it, there seemed to me to lie embodied the solemn stillness that was in the house; and that when she would have turned the cover gently back, I cried: ‘Oh no! oh no!’ and held her hand.\n\nIf the funeral had been yesterday, I could not recollect it better. The very air of the best parlour, when I went in at the door, the bright condition of the fire, the shining of the wine in the decanters, the patterns of the glasses and plates, the faint sweet smell of cake, the odour of Miss Murdstone’s dress, and our black clothes. Mr. Chillip is in the room, and comes to speak to me.\n\n‘And how is Master David?’ he says, kindly.\n\nI cannot tell him very well. I give him my hand, which he holds in his.\n\n‘Dear me!’ says Mr. Chillip, meekly smiling, with something shining in his eye. ‘Our little friends grow up around us. They grow out of our knowledge, ma’am?’ This is to Miss Murdstone, who makes no reply.\n\n‘There is a great improvement here, ma’am?’ says Mr. Chillip.\n\nMiss Murdstone merely answers with a frown and a formal bend: Mr. Chillip, discomfited, goes into a corner, keeping me with him, and opens his mouth no more.\n\nI remark this, because I remark everything that happens, not because I care about myself, or have done since I came home. And now the bell begins to sound, and Mr. Omer and another come to make us ready. As Peggotty was wont to tell me, long ago, the followers of my father to the same grave were made ready in the same room.\n\nThere are Mr. Murdstone, our neighbour Mr. Grayper, Mr. Chillip, and I. When we go out to the door, the Bearers and their load are in the garden; and they move before us down the path, and past the elms, and through the gate, and into the churchyard, where I have so often heard the birds sing on a summer morning.\n\nWe stand around the grave. The day seems different to me from every other day, and the light not of the same colour--of a sadder colour. Now there is a solemn hush, which we have brought from home with what is resting in the mould; and while we stand bareheaded, I hear the voice of the clergyman, sounding remote in the open air, and yet distinct and plain, saying: ‘I am the Resurrection and the Life, saith the Lord!’ Then I hear sobs; and, standing apart among the lookers-on, I see that good and faithful servant, whom of all the people upon earth I love the best, and unto whom my childish heart is certain that the Lord will one day say: ‘Well done.’\n\nThere are many faces that I know, among the little crowd; faces that I knew in church, when mine was always wondering there; faces that first saw my mother, when she came to the village in her youthful bloom. I do not mind them--I mind nothing but my grief--and yet I see and know them all; and even in the background, far away, see Minnie looking on, and her eye glancing on her sweetheart, who is near me.\n\nIt is over, and the earth is filled in, and we turn to come away. Before us stands our house, so pretty and unchanged, so linked in my mind with the young idea of what is gone, that all my sorrow has been nothing to the sorrow it calls forth. But they take me on; and Mr. Chillip talks to me; and when we get home, puts some water to my lips; and when I ask his leave to go up to my room, dismisses me with the gentleness of a woman.\n\nAll this, I say, is yesterday’s event. Events of later date have floated from me to the shore where all forgotten things will reappear, but this stands like a high rock in the ocean.\n\nI knew that Peggotty would come to me in my room. The Sabbath stillness of the time (the day was so like Sunday! I have forgotten that) was suited to us both. She sat down by my side upon my little bed; and holding my hand, and sometimes putting it to her lips, and sometimes smoothing it with hers, as she might have comforted my little brother, told me, in her way, all that she had to tell concerning what had happened.\n\n‘She was never well,’ said Peggotty, ‘for a long time. She was uncertain in her mind, and not happy. When her baby was born, I thought at first she would get better, but she was more delicate, and sunk a little every day. She used to like to sit alone before her baby came, and then she cried; but afterwards she used to sing to it--so soft, that I once thought, when I heard her, it was like a voice up in the air, that was rising away.\n\n‘I think she got to be more timid, and more frightened-like, of late; and that a hard word was like a blow to her. But she was always the same to me. She never changed to her foolish Peggotty, didn’t my sweet girl.’\n\nHere Peggotty stopped, and softly beat upon my hand a little while.\n\n‘The last time that I saw her like her own old self, was the night when you came home, my dear. The day you went away, she said to me, “I never shall see my pretty darling again. Something tells me so, that tells the truth, I know.”\n\n‘She tried to hold up after that; and many a time, when they told her she was thoughtless and light-hearted, made believe to be so; but it was all a bygone then. She never told her husband what she had told me--she was afraid of saying it to anybody else--till one night, a little more than a week before it happened, when she said to him: “My dear, I think I am dying.”\n\n‘“It’s off my mind now, Peggotty,” she told me, when I laid her in her bed that night. “He will believe it more and more, poor fellow, every day for a few days to come; and then it will be past. I am very tired. If this is sleep, sit by me while I sleep: don’t leave me. God bless both my children! God protect and keep my fatherless boy!”\n\n‘I never left her afterwards,’ said Peggotty. ‘She often talked to them two downstairs--for she loved them; she couldn’t bear not to love anyone who was about her--but when they went away from her bed-side, she always turned to me, as if there was rest where Peggotty was, and never fell asleep in any other way.\n\n‘On the last night, in the evening, she kissed me, and said: “If my baby should die too, Peggotty, please let them lay him in my arms, and bury us together.” (It was done; for the poor lamb lived but a day beyond her.) “Let my dearest boy go with us to our resting-place,” she said, “and tell him that his mother, when she lay here, blessed him not once, but a thousand times.”’\n\nAnother silence followed this, and another gentle beating on my hand.\n\n‘It was pretty far in the night,’ said Peggotty, ‘when she asked me for some drink; and when she had taken it, gave me such a patient smile, the dear!--so beautiful!\n\n‘Daybreak had come, and the sun was rising, when she said to me, how kind and considerate Mr. Copperfield had always been to her, and how he had borne with her, and told her, when she doubted herself, that a loving heart was better and stronger than wisdom, and that he was a happy man in hers. “Peggotty, my dear,” she said then, “put me nearer to you,” for she was very weak. “Lay your good arm underneath my neck,” she said, “and turn me to you, for your face is going far off, and I want it to be near.” I put it as she asked; and oh Davy! the time had come when my first parting words to you were true--when she was glad to lay her poor head on her stupid cross old Peggotty’s arm--and she died like a child that had gone to sleep!’\n\nThus ended Peggotty’s narration. From the moment of my knowing of the death of my mother, the idea of her as she had been of late had vanished from me. I remembered her, from that instant, only as the young mother of my earliest impressions, who had been used to wind her bright curls round and round her finger, and to dance with me at twilight in the parlour. What Peggotty had told me now, was so far from bringing me back to the later period, that it rooted the earlier image in my mind. It may be curious, but it is true. In her death she winged her way back to her calm untroubled youth, and cancelled all the rest.\n\nThe mother who lay in the grave, was the mother of my infancy; the little creature in her arms, was myself, as I had once been, hushed for ever on her bosom.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. I BECOME NEGLECTED, AND AM PROVIDED FOR\n\nThe first act of business Miss Murdstone performed when the day of the solemnity was over, and light was freely admitted into the house, was to give Peggotty a month’s warning. Much as Peggotty would have disliked such a service, I believe she would have retained it, for my sake, in preference to the best upon earth. She told me we must part, and told me why; and we condoled with one another, in all sincerity.\n\nAs to me or my future, not a word was said, or a step taken. Happy they would have been, I dare say, if they could have dismissed me at a month’s warning too. I mustered courage once, to ask Miss Murdstone when I was going back to school; and she answered dryly, she believed I was not going back at all. I was told nothing more. I was very anxious to know what was going to be done with me, and so was Peggotty; but neither she nor I could pick up any information on the subject.\n\nThere was one change in my condition, which, while it relieved me of a great deal of present uneasiness, might have made me, if I had been capable of considering it closely, yet more uncomfortable about the future. It was this. The constraint that had been put upon me, was quite abandoned. I was so far from being required to keep my dull post in the parlour, that on several occasions, when I took my seat there, Miss Murdstone frowned to me to go away. I was so far from being warned off from Peggotty’s society, that, provided I was not in Mr. Murdstone’s, I was never sought out or inquired for. At first I was in daily dread of his taking my education in hand again, or of Miss Murdstone’s devoting herself to it; but I soon began to think that such fears were groundless, and that all I had to anticipate was neglect.\n\nI do not conceive that this discovery gave me much pain then. I was still giddy with the shock of my mother’s death, and in a kind of stunned state as to all tributary things. I can recollect, indeed, to have speculated, at odd times, on the possibility of my not being taught any more, or cared for any more; and growing up to be a shabby, moody man, lounging an idle life away, about the village; as well as on the feasibility of my getting rid of this picture by going away somewhere, like the hero in a story, to seek my fortune: but these were transient visions, daydreams I sat looking at sometimes, as if they were faintly painted or written on the wall of my room, and which, as they melted away, left the wall blank again.\n\n‘Peggotty,’ I said in a thoughtful whisper, one evening, when I was warming my hands at the kitchen fire, ‘Mr. Murdstone likes me less than he used to. He never liked me much, Peggotty; but he would rather not even see me now, if he can help it.’\n\n‘Perhaps it’s his sorrow,’ said Peggotty, stroking my hair.\n\n‘I am sure, Peggotty, I am sorry too. If I believed it was his sorrow, I should not think of it at all. But it’s not that; oh, no, it’s not that.’\n\n‘How do you know it’s not that?’ said Peggotty, after a silence.\n\n‘Oh, his sorrow is another and quite a different thing. He is sorry at this moment, sitting by the fireside with Miss Murdstone; but if I was to go in, Peggotty, he would be something besides.’\n\n‘What would he be?’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘Angry,’ I answered, with an involuntary imitation of his dark frown. ‘If he was only sorry, he wouldn’t look at me as he does. I am only sorry, and it makes me feel kinder.’\n\nPeggotty said nothing for a little while; and I warmed my hands, as silent as she.\n\n‘Davy,’ she said at length.\n\n‘Yes, Peggotty?’ ‘I have tried, my dear, all ways I could think of--all the ways there are, and all the ways there ain’t, in short--to get a suitable service here, in Blunderstone; but there’s no such a thing, my love.’\n\n‘And what do you mean to do, Peggotty,’ says I, wistfully. ‘Do you mean to go and seek your fortune?’\n\n‘I expect I shall be forced to go to Yarmouth,’ replied Peggotty, ‘and live there.’\n\n‘You might have gone farther off,’ I said, brightening a little, ‘and been as bad as lost. I shall see you sometimes, my dear old Peggotty, there. You won’t be quite at the other end of the world, will you?’\n\n‘Contrary ways, please God!’ cried Peggotty, with great animation. ‘As long as you are here, my pet, I shall come over every week of my life to see you. One day, every week of my life!’\n\nI felt a great weight taken off my mind by this promise: but even this was not all, for Peggotty went on to say:\n\n‘I’m a-going, Davy, you see, to my brother’s, first, for another fortnight’s visit--just till I have had time to look about me, and get to be something like myself again. Now, I have been thinking that perhaps, as they don’t want you here at present, you might be let to go along with me.’\n\nIf anything, short of being in a different relation to every one about me, Peggotty excepted, could have given me a sense of pleasure at that time, it would have been this project of all others. The idea of being again surrounded by those honest faces, shining welcome on me; of renewing the peacefulness of the sweet Sunday morning, when the bells were ringing, the stones dropping in the water, and the shadowy ships breaking through the mist; of roaming up and down with little Em’ly, telling her my troubles, and finding charms against them in the shells and pebbles on the beach; made a calm in my heart. It was ruffled next moment, to be sure, by a doubt of Miss Murdstone’s giving her consent; but even that was set at rest soon, for she came out to take an evening grope in the store-closet while we were yet in conversation, and Peggotty, with a boldness that amazed me, broached the topic on the spot.\n\n‘The boy will be idle there,’ said Miss Murdstone, looking into a pickle-jar, ‘and idleness is the root of all evil. But, to be sure, he would be idle here--or anywhere, in my opinion.’\n\nPeggotty had an angry answer ready, I could see; but she swallowed it for my sake, and remained silent.\n\n‘Humph!’ said Miss Murdstone, still keeping her eye on the pickles; ‘it is of more importance than anything else--it is of paramount importance--that my brother should not be disturbed or made uncomfortable. I suppose I had better say yes.’\n\nI thanked her, without making any demonstration of joy, lest it should induce her to withdraw her assent. Nor could I help thinking this a prudent course, since she looked at me out of the pickle-jar, with as great an access of sourness as if her black eyes had absorbed its contents. However, the permission was given, and was never retracted; for when the month was out, Peggotty and I were ready to depart.\n\nMr. Barkis came into the house for Peggotty’s boxes. I had never known him to pass the garden-gate before, but on this occasion he came into the house. And he gave me a look as he shouldered the largest box and went out, which I thought had meaning in it, if meaning could ever be said to find its way into Mr. Barkis’s visage.\n\nPeggotty was naturally in low spirits at leaving what had been her home so many years, and where the two strong attachments of her life--for my mother and myself--had been formed. She had been walking in the churchyard, too, very early; and she got into the cart, and sat in it with her handkerchief at her eyes.\n\nSo long as she remained in this condition, Mr. Barkis gave no sign of life whatever. He sat in his usual place and attitude like a great stuffed figure. But when she began to look about her, and to speak to me, he nodded his head and grinned several times. I have not the least notion at whom, or what he meant by it.\n\n‘It’s a beautiful day, Mr. Barkis!’ I said, as an act of politeness.\n\n‘It ain’t bad,’ said Mr. Barkis, who generally qualified his speech, and rarely committed himself.\n\n‘Peggotty is quite comfortable now, Mr. Barkis,’ I remarked, for his satisfaction.\n\n‘Is she, though?’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\nAfter reflecting about it, with a sagacious air, Mr. Barkis eyed her, and said:\n\n‘ARE you pretty comfortable?’\n\nPeggotty laughed, and answered in the affirmative.\n\n‘But really and truly, you know. Are you?’ growled Mr. Barkis, sliding nearer to her on the seat, and nudging her with his elbow. ‘Are you? Really and truly pretty comfortable? Are you? Eh?’\n\nAt each of these inquiries Mr. Barkis shuffled nearer to her, and gave her another nudge; so that at last we were all crowded together in the left-hand corner of the cart, and I was so squeezed that I could hardly bear it.\n\nPeggotty calling his attention to my sufferings, Mr. Barkis gave me a little more room at once, and got away by degrees. But I could not help observing that he seemed to think he had hit upon a wonderful expedient for expressing himself in a neat, agreeable, and pointed manner, without the inconvenience of inventing conversation. He manifestly chuckled over it for some time. By and by he turned to Peggotty again, and repeating, ‘Are you pretty comfortable though?’ bore down upon us as before, until the breath was nearly edged out of my body. By and by he made another descent upon us with the same inquiry, and the same result. At length, I got up whenever I saw him coming, and standing on the foot-board, pretended to look at the prospect; after which I did very well.\n\nHe was so polite as to stop at a public-house, expressly on our account, and entertain us with broiled mutton and beer. Even when Peggotty was in the act of drinking, he was seized with one of those approaches, and almost choked her. But as we drew nearer to the end of our journey, he had more to do and less time for gallantry; and when we got on Yarmouth pavement, we were all too much shaken and jolted, I apprehend, to have any leisure for anything else.\n\nMr. Peggotty and Ham waited for us at the old place. They received me and Peggotty in an affectionate manner, and shook hands with Mr. Barkis, who, with his hat on the very back of his head, and a shame-faced leer upon his countenance, and pervading his very legs, presented but a vacant appearance, I thought. They each took one of Peggotty’s trunks, and we were going away, when Mr. Barkis solemnly made a sign to me with his forefinger to come under an archway.\n\n‘I say,’ growled Mr. Barkis, ‘it was all right.’\n\nI looked up into his face, and answered, with an attempt to be very profound: ‘Oh!’\n\n‘It didn’t come to a end there,’ said Mr. Barkis, nodding confidentially. ‘It was all right.’\n\nAgain I answered, ‘Oh!’\n\n‘You know who was willin’,’ said my friend. ‘It was Barkis, and Barkis only.’\n\n‘It’s all right,’ said Mr. Barkis, shaking hands; ‘I’m a friend of your’n. You made it all right, first. It’s all right.’\n\nIn his attempts to be particularly lucid, Mr. Barkis was so extremely mysterious, that I might have stood looking in his face for an hour, and most assuredly should have got as much information out of it as out of the face of a clock that had stopped, but for Peggotty’s calling me away. As we were going along, she asked me what he had said; and I told her he had said it was all right.\n\n‘Like his impudence,’ said Peggotty, ‘but I don’t mind that! Davy dear, what should you think if I was to think of being married?’\n\n‘Why--I suppose you would like me as much then, Peggotty, as you do now?’ I returned, after a little consideration.\n\nGreatly to the astonishment of the passengers in the street, as well as of her relations going on before, the good soul was obliged to stop and embrace me on the spot, with many protestations of her unalterable love.\n\n‘Tell me what should you say, darling?’ she asked again, when this was over, and we were walking on.\n\n‘If you were thinking of being married--to Mr. Barkis, Peggotty?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘I should think it would be a very good thing. For then you know, Peggotty, you would always have the horse and cart to bring you over to see me, and could come for nothing, and be sure of coming.’\n\n‘The sense of the dear!’ cried Peggotty. ‘What I have been thinking of, this month back! Yes, my precious; and I think I should be more independent altogether, you see; let alone my working with a better heart in my own house, than I could in anybody else’s now. I don’t know what I might be fit for, now, as a servant to a stranger. And I shall be always near my pretty’s resting-place,’ said Peggotty, musing, ‘and be able to see it when I like; and when I lie down to rest, I may be laid not far off from my darling girl!’\n\nWe neither of us said anything for a little while.\n\n‘But I wouldn’t so much as give it another thought,’ said Peggotty, cheerily ‘if my Davy was anyways against it--not if I had been asked in church thirty times three times over, and was wearing out the ring in my pocket.’\n\n‘Look at me, Peggotty,’ I replied; ‘and see if I am not really glad, and don’t truly wish it!’ As indeed I did, with all my heart.\n\n‘Well, my life,’ said Peggotty, giving me a squeeze, ‘I have thought of it night and day, every way I can, and I hope the right way; but I’ll think of it again, and speak to my brother about it, and in the meantime we’ll keep it to ourselves, Davy, you and me. Barkis is a good plain creature,’ said Peggotty, ‘and if I tried to do my duty by him, I think it would be my fault if I wasn’t--if I wasn’t pretty comfortable,’ said Peggotty, laughing heartily. This quotation from Mr. Barkis was so appropriate, and tickled us both so much, that we laughed again and again, and were quite in a pleasant humour when we came within view of Mr. Peggotty’s cottage.\n\nIt looked just the same, except that it may, perhaps, have shrunk a little in my eyes; and Mrs. Gummidge was waiting at the door as if she had stood there ever since. All within was the same, down to the seaweed in the blue mug in my bedroom. I went into the out-house to look about me; and the very same lobsters, crabs, and crawfish possessed by the same desire to pinch the world in general, appeared to be in the same state of conglomeration in the same old corner.\n\nBut there was no little Em’ly to be seen, so I asked Mr. Peggotty where she was.\n\n‘She’s at school, sir,’ said Mr. Peggotty, wiping the heat consequent on the porterage of Peggotty’s box from his forehead; ‘she’ll be home,’ looking at the Dutch clock, ‘in from twenty minutes to half-an-hour’s time. We all on us feel the loss of her, bless ye!’\n\nMrs. Gummidge moaned.\n\n‘Cheer up, Mawther!’ cried Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘I feel it more than anybody else,’ said Mrs. Gummidge; ‘I’m a lone lorn creetur’, and she used to be a’most the only thing that didn’t go contrary with me.’\n\nMrs. Gummidge, whimpering and shaking her head, applied herself to blowing the fire. Mr. Peggotty, looking round upon us while she was so engaged, said in a low voice, which he shaded with his hand: ‘The old ‘un!’ From this I rightly conjectured that no improvement had taken place since my last visit in the state of Mrs. Gummidge’s spirits.\n\nNow, the whole place was, or it should have been, quite as delightful a place as ever; and yet it did not impress me in the same way. I felt rather disappointed with it. Perhaps it was because little Em’ly was not at home. I knew the way by which she would come, and presently found myself strolling along the path to meet her.\n\nA figure appeared in the distance before long, and I soon knew it to be Em’ly, who was a little creature still in stature, though she was grown. But when she drew nearer, and I saw her blue eyes looking bluer, and her dimpled face looking brighter, and her whole self prettier and gayer, a curious feeling came over me that made me pretend not to know her, and pass by as if I were looking at something a long way off. I have done such a thing since in later life, or I am mistaken.\n\nLittle Em’ly didn’t care a bit. She saw me well enough; but instead of turning round and calling after me, ran away laughing. This obliged me to run after her, and she ran so fast that we were very near the cottage before I caught her.\n\n‘Oh, it’s you, is it?’ said little Em’ly.\n\n‘Why, you knew who it was, Em’ly,’ said I.\n\n‘And didn’t YOU know who it was?’ said Em’ly. I was going to kiss her, but she covered her cherry lips with her hands, and said she wasn’t a baby now, and ran away, laughing more than ever, into the house.\n\nShe seemed to delight in teasing me, which was a change in her I wondered at very much. The tea table was ready, and our little locker was put out in its old place, but instead of coming to sit by me, she went and bestowed her company upon that grumbling Mrs. Gummidge: and on Mr. Peggotty’s inquiring why, rumpled her hair all over her face to hide it, and could do nothing but laugh.\n\n‘A little puss, it is!’ said Mr. Peggotty, patting her with his great hand.\n\n‘So sh’ is! so sh’ is!’ cried Ham. ‘Mas’r Davy bor’, so sh’ is!’ and he sat and chuckled at her for some time, in a state of mingled admiration and delight, that made his face a burning red.\n\nLittle Em’ly was spoiled by them all, in fact; and by no one more than Mr. Peggotty himself, whom she could have coaxed into anything, by only going and laying her cheek against his rough whisker. That was my opinion, at least, when I saw her do it; and I held Mr. Peggotty to be thoroughly in the right. But she was so affectionate and sweet-natured, and had such a pleasant manner of being both sly and shy at once, that she captivated me more than ever.\n\nShe was tender-hearted, too; for when, as we sat round the fire after tea, an allusion was made by Mr. Peggotty over his pipe to the loss I had sustained, the tears stood in her eyes, and she looked at me so kindly across the table, that I felt quite thankful to her.\n\n‘Ah!’ said Mr. Peggotty, taking up her curls, and running them over his hand like water, ‘here’s another orphan, you see, sir. And here,’ said Mr. Peggotty, giving Ham a backhanded knock in the chest, ‘is another of ‘em, though he don’t look much like it.’\n\n‘If I had you for my guardian, Mr. Peggotty,’ said I, shaking my head, ‘I don’t think I should FEEL much like it.’\n\n‘Well said, Mas’r Davy bor’!’ cried Ham, in an ecstasy. ‘Hoorah! Well said! Nor more you wouldn’t! Hor! Hor!’--Here he returned Mr. Peggotty’s back-hander, and little Em’ly got up and kissed Mr. Peggotty. ‘And how’s your friend, sir?’ said Mr. Peggotty to me.\n\n‘Steerforth?’ said I.\n\n‘That’s the name!’ cried Mr. Peggotty, turning to Ham. ‘I knowed it was something in our way.’\n\n‘You said it was Rudderford,’ observed Ham, laughing.\n\n‘Well!’ retorted Mr. Peggotty. ‘And ye steer with a rudder, don’t ye? It ain’t fur off. How is he, sir?’\n\n‘He was very well indeed when I came away, Mr. Peggotty.’\n\n‘There’s a friend!’ said Mr. Peggotty, stretching out his pipe. ‘There’s a friend, if you talk of friends! Why, Lord love my heart alive, if it ain’t a treat to look at him!’\n\n‘He is very handsome, is he not?’ said I, my heart warming with this praise.\n\n‘Handsome!’ cried Mr. Peggotty. ‘He stands up to you like--like a--why I don’t know what he don’t stand up to you like. He’s so bold!’\n\n‘Yes! That’s just his character,’ said I. ‘He’s as brave as a lion, and you can’t think how frank he is, Mr. Peggotty.’\n\n‘And I do suppose, now,’ said Mr. Peggotty, looking at me through the smoke of his pipe, ‘that in the way of book-larning he’d take the wind out of a’most anything.’\n\n‘Yes,’ said I, delighted; ‘he knows everything. He is astonishingly clever.’\n\n‘There’s a friend!’ murmured Mr. Peggotty, with a grave toss of his head.\n\n‘Nothing seems to cost him any trouble,’ said I. ‘He knows a task if he only looks at it. He is the best cricketer you ever saw. He will give you almost as many men as you like at draughts, and beat you easily.’\n\nMr. Peggotty gave his head another toss, as much as to say: ‘Of course he will.’\n\n‘He is such a speaker,’ I pursued, ‘that he can win anybody over; and I don’t know what you’d say if you were to hear him sing, Mr. Peggotty.’\n\nMr. Peggotty gave his head another toss, as much as to say: ‘I have no doubt of it.’\n\n‘Then, he’s such a generous, fine, noble fellow,’ said I, quite carried away by my favourite theme, ‘that it’s hardly possible to give him as much praise as he deserves. I am sure I can never feel thankful enough for the generosity with which he has protected me, so much younger and lower in the school than himself.’\n\nI was running on, very fast indeed, when my eyes rested on little Em’ly’s face, which was bent forward over the table, listening with the deepest attention, her breath held, her blue eyes sparkling like jewels, and the colour mantling in her cheeks. She looked so extraordinarily earnest and pretty, that I stopped in a sort of wonder; and they all observed her at the same time, for as I stopped, they laughed and looked at her.\n\n‘Em’ly is like me,’ said Peggotty, ‘and would like to see him.’\n\nEm’ly was confused by our all observing her, and hung down her head, and her face was covered with blushes. Glancing up presently through her stray curls, and seeing that we were all looking at her still (I am sure I, for one, could have looked at her for hours), she ran away, and kept away till it was nearly bedtime.\n\nI lay down in the old little bed in the stern of the boat, and the wind came moaning on across the flat as it had done before. But I could not help fancying, now, that it moaned of those who were gone; and instead of thinking that the sea might rise in the night and float the boat away, I thought of the sea that had risen, since I last heard those sounds, and drowned my happy home. I recollect, as the wind and water began to sound fainter in my ears, putting a short clause into my prayers, petitioning that I might grow up to marry little Em’ly, and so dropping lovingly asleep.\n\nThe days passed pretty much as they had passed before, except--it was a great exception--that little Em’ly and I seldom wandered on the beach now. She had tasks to learn, and needle-work to do; and was absent during a great part of each day. But I felt that we should not have had those old wanderings, even if it had been otherwise. Wild and full of childish whims as Em’ly was, she was more of a little woman than I had supposed. She seemed to have got a great distance away from me, in little more than a year. She liked me, but she laughed at me, and tormented me; and when I went to meet her, stole home another way, and was laughing at the door when I came back, disappointed. The best times were when she sat quietly at work in the doorway, and I sat on the wooden step at her feet, reading to her. It seems to me, at this hour, that I have never seen such sunlight as on those bright April afternoons; that I have never seen such a sunny little figure as I used to see, sitting in the doorway of the old boat; that I have never beheld such sky, such water, such glorified ships sailing away into golden air.\n\nOn the very first evening after our arrival, Mr. Barkis appeared in an exceedingly vacant and awkward condition, and with a bundle of oranges tied up in a handkerchief. As he made no allusion of any kind to this property, he was supposed to have left it behind him by accident when he went away; until Ham, running after him to restore it, came back with the information that it was intended for Peggotty. After that occasion he appeared every evening at exactly the same hour, and always with a little bundle, to which he never alluded, and which he regularly put behind the door and left there. These offerings of affection were of a most various and eccentric description. Among them I remember a double set of pigs’ trotters, a huge pin-cushion, half a bushel or so of apples, a pair of jet earrings, some Spanish onions, a box of dominoes, a canary bird and cage, and a leg of pickled pork.\n\nMr. Barkis’s wooing, as I remember it, was altogether of a peculiar kind. He very seldom said anything; but would sit by the fire in much the same attitude as he sat in his cart, and stare heavily at Peggotty, who was opposite. One night, being, as I suppose, inspired by love, he made a dart at the bit of wax-candle she kept for her thread, and put it in his waistcoat-pocket and carried it off. After that, his great delight was to produce it when it was wanted, sticking to the lining of his pocket, in a partially melted state, and pocket it again when it was done with. He seemed to enjoy himself very much, and not to feel at all called upon to talk. Even when he took Peggotty out for a walk on the flats, he had no uneasiness on that head, I believe; contenting himself with now and then asking her if she was pretty comfortable; and I remember that sometimes, after he was gone, Peggotty would throw her apron over her face, and laugh for half-an-hour. Indeed, we were all more or less amused, except that miserable Mrs. Gummidge, whose courtship would appear to have been of an exactly parallel nature, she was so continually reminded by these transactions of the old one.\n\nAt length, when the term of my visit was nearly expired, it was given out that Peggotty and Mr. Barkis were going to make a day’s holiday together, and that little Em’ly and I were to accompany them. I had but a broken sleep the night before, in anticipation of the pleasure of a whole day with Em’ly. We were all astir betimes in the morning; and while we were yet at breakfast, Mr. Barkis appeared in the distance, driving a chaise-cart towards the object of his affections.\n\nPeggotty was dressed as usual, in her neat and quiet mourning; but Mr. Barkis bloomed in a new blue coat, of which the tailor had given him such good measure, that the cuffs would have rendered gloves unnecessary in the coldest weather, while the collar was so high that it pushed his hair up on end on the top of his head. His bright buttons, too, were of the largest size. Rendered complete by drab pantaloons and a buff waistcoat, I thought Mr. Barkis a phenomenon of respectability.\n\nWhen we were all in a bustle outside the door, I found that Mr. Peggotty was prepared with an old shoe, which was to be thrown after us for luck, and which he offered to Mrs. Gummidge for that purpose.\n\n‘No. It had better be done by somebody else, Dan’l,’ said Mrs. Gummidge. ‘I’m a lone lorn creetur’ myself, and everythink that reminds me of creetur’s that ain’t lone and lorn, goes contrary with me.’\n\n‘Come, old gal!’ cried Mr. Peggotty. ‘Take and heave it.’\n\n‘No, Dan’l,’ returned Mrs. Gummidge, whimpering and shaking her head. ‘If I felt less, I could do more. You don’t feel like me, Dan’l; thinks don’t go contrary with you, nor you with them; you had better do it yourself.’\n\nBut here Peggotty, who had been going about from one to another in a hurried way, kissing everybody, called out from the cart, in which we all were by this time (Em’ly and I on two little chairs, side by side), that Mrs. Gummidge must do it. So Mrs. Gummidge did it; and, I am sorry to relate, cast a damp upon the festive character of our departure, by immediately bursting into tears, and sinking subdued into the arms of Ham, with the declaration that she knowed she was a burden, and had better be carried to the House at once. Which I really thought was a sensible idea, that Ham might have acted on.\n\nAway we went, however, on our holiday excursion; and the first thing we did was to stop at a church, where Mr. Barkis tied the horse to some rails, and went in with Peggotty, leaving little Em’ly and me alone in the chaise. I took that occasion to put my arm round Em’ly’s waist, and propose that as I was going away so very soon now, we should determine to be very affectionate to one another, and very happy, all day. Little Em’ly consenting, and allowing me to kiss her, I became desperate; informing her, I recollect, that I never could love another, and that I was prepared to shed the blood of anybody who should aspire to her affections.\n\nHow merry little Em’ly made herself about it! With what a demure assumption of being immensely older and wiser than I, the fairy little woman said I was ‘a silly boy’; and then laughed so charmingly that I forgot the pain of being called by that disparaging name, in the pleasure of looking at her.\n\nMr. Barkis and Peggotty were a good while in the church, but came out at last, and then we drove away into the country. As we were going along, Mr. Barkis turned to me, and said, with a wink,--by the by, I should hardly have thought, before, that he could wink:\n\n‘What name was it as I wrote up in the cart?’\n\n‘Clara Peggotty,’ I answered.\n\n‘What name would it be as I should write up now, if there was a tilt here?’\n\n‘Clara Peggotty, again?’ I suggested.\n\n‘Clara Peggotty BARKIS!’ he returned, and burst into a roar of laughter that shook the chaise.\n\nIn a word, they were married, and had gone into the church for no other purpose. Peggotty was resolved that it should be quietly done; and the clerk had given her away, and there had been no witnesses of the ceremony. She was a little confused when Mr. Barkis made this abrupt announcement of their union, and could not hug me enough in token of her unimpaired affection; but she soon became herself again, and said she was very glad it was over.\n\nWe drove to a little inn in a by-road, where we were expected, and where we had a very comfortable dinner, and passed the day with great satisfaction. If Peggotty had been married every day for the last ten years, she could hardly have been more at her ease about it; it made no sort of difference in her: she was just the same as ever, and went out for a stroll with little Em’ly and me before tea, while Mr. Barkis philosophically smoked his pipe, and enjoyed himself, I suppose, with the contemplation of his happiness. If so, it sharpened his appetite; for I distinctly call to mind that, although he had eaten a good deal of pork and greens at dinner, and had finished off with a fowl or two, he was obliged to have cold boiled bacon for tea, and disposed of a large quantity without any emotion.\n\nI have often thought, since, what an odd, innocent, out-of-the-way kind of wedding it must have been! We got into the chaise again soon after dark, and drove cosily back, looking up at the stars, and talking about them. I was their chief exponent, and opened Mr. Barkis’s mind to an amazing extent. I told him all I knew, but he would have believed anything I might have taken it into my head to impart to him; for he had a profound veneration for my abilities, and informed his wife in my hearing, on that very occasion, that I was ‘a young Roeshus’--by which I think he meant prodigy.\n\nWhen we had exhausted the subject of the stars, or rather when I had exhausted the mental faculties of Mr. Barkis, little Em’ly and I made a cloak of an old wrapper, and sat under it for the rest of the journey. Ah, how I loved her! What happiness (I thought) if we were married, and were going away anywhere to live among the trees and in the fields, never growing older, never growing wiser, children ever, rambling hand in hand through sunshine and among flowery meadows, laying down our heads on moss at night, in a sweet sleep of purity and peace, and buried by the birds when we were dead! Some such picture, with no real world in it, bright with the light of our innocence, and vague as the stars afar off, was in my mind all the way. I am glad to think there were two such guileless hearts at Peggotty’s marriage as little Em’ly’s and mine. I am glad to think the Loves and Graces took such airy forms in its homely procession.\n\nWell, we came to the old boat again in good time at night; and there Mr. and Mrs. Barkis bade us good-bye, and drove away snugly to their own home. I felt then, for the first time, that I had lost Peggotty. I should have gone to bed with a sore heart indeed under any other roof but that which sheltered little Em’ly’s head.\n\nMr. Peggotty and Ham knew what was in my thoughts as well as I did, and were ready with some supper and their hospitable faces to drive it away. Little Em’ly came and sat beside me on the locker for the only time in all that visit; and it was altogether a wonderful close to a wonderful day.\n\nIt was a night tide; and soon after we went to bed, Mr. Peggotty and Ham went out to fish. I felt very brave at being left alone in the solitary house, the protector of Em’ly and Mrs. Gummidge, and only wished that a lion or a serpent, or any ill-disposed monster, would make an attack upon us, that I might destroy him, and cover myself with glory. But as nothing of the sort happened to be walking about on Yarmouth flats that night, I provided the best substitute I could by dreaming of dragons until morning.\n\nWith morning came Peggotty; who called to me, as usual, under my window as if Mr. Barkis the carrier had been from first to last a dream too. After breakfast she took me to her own home, and a beautiful little home it was. Of all the moveables in it, I must have been impressed by a certain old bureau of some dark wood in the parlour (the tile-floored kitchen was the general sitting-room), with a retreating top which opened, let down, and became a desk, within which was a large quarto edition of Foxe’s Book of Martyrs. This precious volume, of which I do not recollect one word, I immediately discovered and immediately applied myself to; and I never visited the house afterwards, but I kneeled on a chair, opened the casket where this gem was enshrined, spread my arms over the desk, and fell to devouring the book afresh. I was chiefly edified, I am afraid, by the pictures, which were numerous, and represented all kinds of dismal horrors; but the Martyrs and Peggotty’s house have been inseparable in my mind ever since, and are now.\n\nI took leave of Mr. Peggotty, and Ham, and Mrs. Gummidge, and little Em’ly, that day; and passed the night at Peggotty’s, in a little room in the roof (with the Crocodile Book on a shelf by the bed’s head) which was to be always mine, Peggotty said, and should always be kept for me in exactly the same state.\n\n‘Young or old, Davy dear, as long as I am alive and have this house over my head,’ said Peggotty, ‘you shall find it as if I expected you here directly minute. I shall keep it every day, as I used to keep your old little room, my darling; and if you was to go to China, you might think of it as being kept just the same, all the time you were away.’\n\nI felt the truth and constancy of my dear old nurse, with all my heart, and thanked her as well as I could. That was not very well, for she spoke to me thus, with her arms round my neck, in the morning, and I was going home in the morning, and I went home in the morning, with herself and Mr. Barkis in the cart. They left me at the gate, not easily or lightly; and it was a strange sight to me to see the cart go on, taking Peggotty away, and leaving me under the old elm-trees looking at the house, in which there was no face to look on mine with love or liking any more.\n\nAnd now I fell into a state of neglect, which I cannot look back upon without compassion. I fell at once into a solitary condition,--apart from all friendly notice, apart from the society of all other boys of my own age, apart from all companionship but my own spiritless thoughts,--which seems to cast its gloom upon this paper as I write.\n\nWhat would I have given, to have been sent to the hardest school that ever was kept!--to have been taught something, anyhow, anywhere! No such hope dawned upon me. They disliked me; and they sullenly, sternly, steadily, overlooked me. I think Mr. Murdstone’s means were straitened at about this time; but it is little to the purpose. He could not bear me; and in putting me from him he tried, as I believe, to put away the notion that I had any claim upon him--and succeeded.\n\nI was not actively ill-used. I was not beaten, or starved; but the wrong that was done to me had no intervals of relenting, and was done in a systematic, passionless manner. Day after day, week after week, month after month, I was coldly neglected. I wonder sometimes, when I think of it, what they would have done if I had been taken with an illness; whether I should have lain down in my lonely room, and languished through it in my usual solitary way, or whether anybody would have helped me out.\n\nWhen Mr. and Miss Murdstone were at home, I took my meals with them; in their absence, I ate and drank by myself. At all times I lounged about the house and neighbourhood quite disregarded, except that they were jealous of my making any friends: thinking, perhaps, that if I did, I might complain to someone. For this reason, though Mr. Chillip often asked me to go and see him (he was a widower, having, some years before that, lost a little small light-haired wife, whom I can just remember connecting in my own thoughts with a pale tortoise-shell cat), it was but seldom that I enjoyed the happiness of passing an afternoon in his closet of a surgery; reading some book that was new to me, with the smell of the whole Pharmacopoeia coming up my nose, or pounding something in a mortar under his mild directions.\n\nFor the same reason, added no doubt to the old dislike of her, I was seldom allowed to visit Peggotty. Faithful to her promise, she either came to see me, or met me somewhere near, once every week, and never empty-handed; but many and bitter were the disappointments I had, in being refused permission to pay a visit to her at her house. Some few times, however, at long intervals, I was allowed to go there; and then I found out that Mr. Barkis was something of a miser, or as Peggotty dutifully expressed it, was ‘a little near’, and kept a heap of money in a box under his bed, which he pretended was only full of coats and trousers. In this coffer, his riches hid themselves with such a tenacious modesty, that the smallest instalments could only be tempted out by artifice; so that Peggotty had to prepare a long and elaborate scheme, a very Gunpowder Plot, for every Saturday’s expenses.\n\nAll this time I was so conscious of the waste of any promise I had given, and of my being utterly neglected, that I should have been perfectly miserable, I have no doubt, but for the old books. They were my only comfort; and I was as true to them as they were to me, and read them over and over I don’t know how many times more.\n\nI now approach a period of my life, which I can never lose the remembrance of, while I remember anything: and the recollection of which has often, without my invocation, come before me like a ghost, and haunted happier times.\n\nI had been out, one day, loitering somewhere, in the listless, meditative manner that my way of life engendered, when, turning the corner of a lane near our house, I came upon Mr. Murdstone walking with a gentleman. I was confused, and was going by them, when the gentleman cried:\n\n‘What! Brooks!’\n‘No, sir, David Copperfield,’ I said.\n\n‘Don’t tell me. You are Brooks,’ said the gentleman. ‘You are Brooks of Sheffield. That’s your name.’\n\nAt these words, I observed the gentleman more attentively. His laugh coming to my remembrance too, I knew him to be Mr. Quinion, whom I had gone over to Lowestoft with Mr. Murdstone to see, before--it is no matter--I need not recall when.\n\n‘And how do you get on, and where are you being educated, Brooks?’ said Mr. Quinion.\n\nHe had put his hand upon my shoulder, and turned me about, to walk with them. I did not know what to reply, and glanced dubiously at Mr. Murdstone.\n\n‘He is at home at present,’ said the latter. ‘He is not being educated anywhere. I don’t know what to do with him. He is a difficult subject.’\n\nThat old, double look was on me for a moment; and then his eyes darkened with a frown, as it turned, in its aversion, elsewhere.\n\n‘Humph!’ said Mr. Quinion, looking at us both, I thought. ‘Fine weather!’\n\nSilence ensued, and I was considering how I could best disengage my shoulder from his hand, and go away, when he said:\n\n‘I suppose you are a pretty sharp fellow still? Eh, Brooks?’\n\n‘Aye! He is sharp enough,’ said Mr. Murdstone, impatiently. ‘You had better let him go. He will not thank you for troubling him.’\n\nOn this hint, Mr. Quinion released me, and I made the best of my way home. Looking back as I turned into the front garden, I saw Mr. Murdstone leaning against the wicket of the churchyard, and Mr. Quinion talking to him. They were both looking after me, and I felt that they were speaking of me.\n\nMr. Quinion lay at our house that night. After breakfast, the next morning, I had put my chair away, and was going out of the room, when Mr. Murdstone called me back. He then gravely repaired to another table, where his sister sat herself at her desk. Mr. Quinion, with his hands in his pockets, stood looking out of window; and I stood looking at them all.\n\n‘David,’ said Mr. Murdstone, ‘to the young this is a world for action; not for moping and droning in.’ --‘As you do,’ added his sister.\n\n‘Jane Murdstone, leave it to me, if you please. I say, David, to the young this is a world for action, and not for moping and droning in. It is especially so for a young boy of your disposition, which requires a great deal of correcting; and to which no greater service can be done than to force it to conform to the ways of the working world, and to bend it and break it.’\n\n‘For stubbornness won’t do here,’ said his sister ‘What it wants is, to be crushed. And crushed it must be. Shall be, too!’\n\nHe gave her a look, half in remonstrance, half in approval, and went on:\n\n‘I suppose you know, David, that I am not rich. At any rate, you know it now. You have received some considerable education already. Education is costly; and even if it were not, and I could afford it, I am of opinion that it would not be at all advantageous to you to be kept at school. What is before you, is a fight with the world; and the sooner you begin it, the better.’\n\nI think it occurred to me that I had already begun it, in my poor way: but it occurs to me now, whether or no.\n\n‘You have heard the “counting-house” mentioned sometimes,’ said Mr. Murdstone.\n\n‘The counting-house, sir?’ I repeated. ‘Of Murdstone and Grinby, in the wine trade,’ he replied.\n\nI suppose I looked uncertain, for he went on hastily:\n\n‘You have heard the “counting-house” mentioned, or the business, or the cellars, or the wharf, or something about it.’\n\n‘I think I have heard the business mentioned, sir,’ I said, remembering what I vaguely knew of his and his sister’s resources. ‘But I don’t know when.’\n\n‘It does not matter when,’ he returned. ‘Mr. Quinion manages that business.’\n\nI glanced at the latter deferentially as he stood looking out of window.\n\n‘Mr. Quinion suggests that it gives employment to some other boys, and that he sees no reason why it shouldn’t, on the same terms, give employment to you.’\n\n‘He having,’ Mr. Quinion observed in a low voice, and half turning round, ‘no other prospect, Murdstone.’\n\nMr. Murdstone, with an impatient, even an angry gesture, resumed, without noticing what he had said:\n\n‘Those terms are, that you will earn enough for yourself to provide for your eating and drinking, and pocket-money. Your lodging (which I have arranged for) will be paid by me. So will your washing--’\n\n‘--Which will be kept down to my estimate,’ said his sister.\n\n‘Your clothes will be looked after for you, too,’ said Mr. Murdstone; ‘as you will not be able, yet awhile, to get them for yourself. So you are now going to London, David, with Mr. Quinion, to begin the world on your own account.’\n\n‘In short, you are provided for,’ observed his sister; ‘and will please to do your duty.’\n\nThough I quite understood that the purpose of this announcement was to get rid of me, I have no distinct remembrance whether it pleased or frightened me. My impression is, that I was in a state of confusion about it, and, oscillating between the two points, touched neither. Nor had I much time for the clearing of my thoughts, as Mr. Quinion was to go upon the morrow.\n\nBehold me, on the morrow, in a much-worn little white hat, with a black crape round it for my mother, a black jacket, and a pair of hard, stiff corduroy trousers--which Miss Murdstone considered the best armour for the legs in that fight with the world which was now to come off. Behold me so attired, and with my little worldly all before me in a small trunk, sitting, a lone lorn child (as Mrs. Gummidge might have said), in the post-chaise that was carrying Mr. Quinion to the London coach at Yarmouth! See, how our house and church are lessening in the distance; how the grave beneath the tree is blotted out by intervening objects; how the spire points upwards from my old playground no more, and the sky is empty!\n\n# CHAPTER 11. I BEGIN LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT, AND DON’T LIKE IT\n\nI know enough of the world now, to have almost lost the capacity of being much surprised by anything; but it is matter of some surprise to me, even now, that I can have been so easily thrown away at such an age. A child of excellent abilities, and with strong powers of observation, quick, eager, delicate, and soon hurt bodily or mentally, it seems wonderful to me that nobody should have made any sign in my behalf. But none was made; and I became, at ten years old, a little labouring hind in the service of Murdstone and Grinby.\n\nMurdstone and Grinby’s warehouse was at the waterside. It was down in Blackfriars. Modern improvements have altered the place; but it was the last house at the bottom of a narrow street, curving down hill to the river, with some stairs at the end, where people took boat. It was a crazy old house with a wharf of its own, abutting on the water when the tide was in, and on the mud when the tide was out, and literally overrun with rats. Its panelled rooms, discoloured with the dirt and smoke of a hundred years, I dare say; its decaying floors and staircase; the squeaking and scuffling of the old grey rats down in the cellars; and the dirt and rottenness of the place; are things, not of many years ago, in my mind, but of the present instant. They are all before me, just as they were in the evil hour when I went among them for the first time, with my trembling hand in Mr. Quinion’s.\n\nMurdstone and Grinby’s trade was among a good many kinds of people, but an important branch of it was the supply of wines and spirits to certain packet ships. I forget now where they chiefly went, but I think there were some among them that made voyages both to the East and West Indies. I know that a great many empty bottles were one of the consequences of this traffic, and that certain men and boys were employed to examine them against the light, and reject those that were flawed, and to rinse and wash them. When the empty bottles ran short, there were labels to be pasted on full ones, or corks to be fitted to them, or seals to be put upon the corks, or finished bottles to be packed in casks. All this work was my work, and of the boys employed upon it I was one.\n\nThere were three or four of us, counting me. My working place was established in a corner of the warehouse, where Mr. Quinion could see me, when he chose to stand up on the bottom rail of his stool in the counting-house, and look at me through a window above the desk. Hither, on the first morning of my so auspiciously beginning life on my own account, the oldest of the regular boys was summoned to show me my business. His name was Mick Walker, and he wore a ragged apron and a paper cap. He informed me that his father was a bargeman, and walked, in a black velvet head-dress, in the Lord Mayor’s Show. He also informed me that our principal associate would be another boy whom he introduced by the--to me--extraordinary name of Mealy Potatoes. I discovered, however, that this youth had not been christened by that name, but that it had been bestowed upon him in the warehouse, on account of his complexion, which was pale or mealy. Mealy’s father was a waterman, who had the additional distinction of being a fireman, and was engaged as such at one of the large theatres; where some young relation of Mealy’s--I think his little sister--did Imps in the Pantomimes.\n\nNo words can express the secret agony of my soul as I sunk into this companionship; compared these henceforth everyday associates with those of my happier childhood--not to say with Steerforth, Traddles, and the rest of those boys; and felt my hopes of growing up to be a learned and distinguished man, crushed in my bosom. The deep remembrance of the sense I had, of being utterly without hope now; of the shame I felt in my position; of the misery it was to my young heart to believe that day by day what I had learned, and thought, and delighted in, and raised my fancy and my emulation up by, would pass away from me, little by little, never to be brought back any more; cannot be written. As often as Mick Walker went away in the course of that forenoon, I mingled my tears with the water in which I was washing the bottles; and sobbed as if there were a flaw in my own breast, and it were in danger of bursting.\n\nThe counting-house clock was at half past twelve, and there was general preparation for going to dinner, when Mr. Quinion tapped at the counting-house window, and beckoned to me to go in. I went in, and found there a stoutish, middle-aged person, in a brown surtout and black tights and shoes, with no more hair upon his head (which was a large one, and very shining) than there is upon an egg, and with a very extensive face, which he turned full upon me. His clothes were shabby, but he had an imposing shirt-collar on. He carried a jaunty sort of a stick, with a large pair of rusty tassels to it; and a quizzing-glass hung outside his coat,--for ornament, I afterwards found, as he very seldom looked through it, and couldn’t see anything when he did.\n\n‘This,’ said Mr. Quinion, in allusion to myself, ‘is he.’\n\n‘This,’ said the stranger, with a certain condescending roll in his voice, and a certain indescribable air of doing something genteel, which impressed me very much, ‘is Master Copperfield. I hope I see you well, sir?’\n\nI said I was very well, and hoped he was. I was sufficiently ill at ease, Heaven knows; but it was not in my nature to complain much at that time of my life, so I said I was very well, and hoped he was.\n\n‘I am,’ said the stranger, ‘thank Heaven, quite well. I have received a letter from Mr. Murdstone, in which he mentions that he would desire me to receive into an apartment in the rear of my house, which is at present unoccupied--and is, in short, to be let as a--in short,’ said the stranger, with a smile and in a burst of confidence, ‘as a bedroom--the young beginner whom I have now the pleasure to--’ and the stranger waved his hand, and settled his chin in his shirt-collar.\n\n‘This is Mr. Micawber,’ said Mr. Quinion to me.\n\n‘Ahem!’ said the stranger, ‘that is my name.’\n\n‘Mr. Micawber,’ said Mr. Quinion, ‘is known to Mr. Murdstone. He takes orders for us on commission, when he can get any. He has been written to by Mr. Murdstone, on the subject of your lodgings, and he will receive you as a lodger.’\n\n‘My address,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘is Windsor Terrace, City Road. I--in short,’ said Mr. Micawber, with the same genteel air, and in another burst of confidence--‘I live there.’\n\nI made him a bow.\n\n‘Under the impression,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘that your peregrinations in this metropolis have not as yet been extensive, and that you might have some difficulty in penetrating the arcana of the Modern Babylon in the direction of the City Road,--in short,’ said Mr. Micawber, in another burst of confidence, ‘that you might lose yourself--I shall be happy to call this evening, and install you in the knowledge of the nearest way.’\n\nI thanked him with all my heart, for it was friendly in him to offer to take that trouble.\n\n‘At what hour,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘shall I--’\n\n‘At about eight,’ said Mr. Quinion.\n\n‘At about eight,’ said Mr. Micawber. ‘I beg to wish you good day, Mr. Quinion. I will intrude no longer.’\n\nSo he put on his hat, and went out with his cane under his arm: very upright, and humming a tune when he was clear of the counting-house.\n\nMr. Quinion then formally engaged me to be as useful as I could in the warehouse of Murdstone and Grinby, at a salary, I think, of six shillings a week. I am not clear whether it was six or seven. I am inclined to believe, from my uncertainty on this head, that it was six at first and seven afterwards. He paid me a week down (from his own pocket, I believe), and I gave Mealy sixpence out of it to get my trunk carried to Windsor Terrace that night: it being too heavy for my strength, small as it was. I paid sixpence more for my dinner, which was a meat pie and a turn at a neighbouring pump; and passed the hour which was allowed for that meal, in walking about the streets.\n\nAt the appointed time in the evening, Mr. Micawber reappeared. I washed my hands and face, to do the greater honour to his gentility, and we walked to our house, as I suppose I must now call it, together; Mr. Micawber impressing the name of streets, and the shapes of corner houses upon me, as we went along, that I might find my way back, easily, in the morning.\n\nArrived at this house in Windsor Terrace (which I noticed was shabby like himself, but also, like himself, made all the show it could), he presented me to Mrs. Micawber, a thin and faded lady, not at all young, who was sitting in the parlour (the first floor was altogether unfurnished, and the blinds were kept down to delude the neighbours), with a baby at her breast. This baby was one of twins; and I may remark here that I hardly ever, in all my experience of the family, saw both the twins detached from Mrs. Micawber at the same time. One of them was always taking refreshment.\n\nThere were two other children; Master Micawber, aged about four, and Miss Micawber, aged about three. These, and a dark-complexioned young woman, with a habit of snorting, who was servant to the family, and informed me, before half an hour had expired, that she was ‘a Orfling’, and came from St. Luke’s workhouse, in the neighbourhood, completed the establishment. My room was at the top of the house, at the back: a close chamber; stencilled all over with an ornament which my young imagination represented as a blue muffin; and very scantily furnished.\n\n‘I never thought,’ said Mrs. Micawber, when she came up, twin and all, to show me the apartment, and sat down to take breath, ‘before I was married, when I lived with papa and mama, that I should ever find it necessary to take a lodger. But Mr. Micawber being in difficulties, all considerations of private feeling must give way.’\n\nI said: ‘Yes, ma’am.’\n\n‘Mr. Micawber’s difficulties are almost overwhelming just at present,’ said Mrs. Micawber; ‘and whether it is possible to bring him through them, I don’t know. When I lived at home with papa and mama, I really should have hardly understood what the word meant, in the sense in which I now employ it, but experientia does it,--as papa used to say.’\n\nI cannot satisfy myself whether she told me that Mr. Micawber had been an officer in the Marines, or whether I have imagined it. I only know that I believe to this hour that he WAS in the Marines once upon a time, without knowing why. He was a sort of town traveller for a number of miscellaneous houses, now; but made little or nothing of it, I am afraid.\n\n‘If Mr. Micawber’s creditors will not give him time,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘they must take the consequences; and the sooner they bring it to an issue the better. Blood cannot be obtained from a stone, neither can anything on account be obtained at present (not to mention law expenses) from Mr. Micawber.’\n\nI never can quite understand whether my precocious self-dependence confused Mrs. Micawber in reference to my age, or whether she was so full of the subject that she would have talked about it to the very twins if there had been nobody else to communicate with, but this was the strain in which she began, and she went on accordingly all the time I knew her.\n\nPoor Mrs. Micawber! She said she had tried to exert herself, and so, I have no doubt, she had. The centre of the street door was perfectly covered with a great brass-plate, on which was engraved ‘Mrs. Micawber’s Boarding Establishment for Young Ladies’: but I never found that any young lady had ever been to school there; or that any young lady ever came, or proposed to come; or that the least preparation was ever made to receive any young lady. The only visitors I ever saw, or heard of, were creditors. THEY used to come at all hours, and some of them were quite ferocious. One dirty-faced man, I think he was a boot-maker, used to edge himself into the passage as early as seven o’clock in the morning, and call up the stairs to Mr. Micawber--‘Come! You ain’t out yet, you know. Pay us, will you? Don’t hide, you know; that’s mean. I wouldn’t be mean if I was you. Pay us, will you? You just pay us, d’ye hear? Come!’ Receiving no answer to these taunts, he would mount in his wrath to the words ‘swindlers’ and ‘robbers’; and these being ineffectual too, would sometimes go to the extremity of crossing the street, and roaring up at the windows of the second floor, where he knew Mr. Micawber was. At these times, Mr. Micawber would be transported with grief and mortification, even to the length (as I was once made aware by a scream from his wife) of making motions at himself with a razor; but within half-an-hour afterwards, he would polish up his shoes with extraordinary pains, and go out, humming a tune with a greater air of gentility than ever. Mrs. Micawber was quite as elastic. I have known her to be thrown into fainting fits by the king’s taxes at three o’clock, and to eat lamb chops, breaded, and drink warm ale (paid for with two tea-spoons that had gone to the pawnbroker’s) at four. On one occasion, when an execution had just been put in, coming home through some chance as early as six o’clock, I saw her lying (of course with a twin) under the grate in a swoon, with her hair all torn about her face; but I never knew her more cheerful than she was, that very same night, over a veal cutlet before the kitchen fire, telling me stories about her papa and mama, and the company they used to keep.\n\nIn this house, and with this family, I passed my leisure time. My own exclusive breakfast of a penny loaf and a pennyworth of milk, I provided myself. I kept another small loaf, and a modicum of cheese, on a particular shelf of a particular cupboard, to make my supper on when I came back at night. This made a hole in the six or seven shillings, I know well; and I was out at the warehouse all day, and had to support myself on that money all the week. From Monday morning until Saturday night, I had no advice, no counsel, no encouragement, no consolation, no assistance, no support, of any kind, from anyone, that I can call to mind, as I hope to go to heaven!\n\nI was so young and childish, and so little qualified--how could I be otherwise?--to undertake the whole charge of my own existence, that often, in going to Murdstone and Grinby’s, of a morning, I could not resist the stale pastry put out for sale at half-price at the pastrycooks’ doors, and spent in that the money I should have kept for my dinner. Then, I went without my dinner, or bought a roll or a slice of pudding. I remember two pudding shops, between which I was divided, according to my finances. One was in a court close to St. Martin’s Church--at the back of the church,--which is now removed altogether. The pudding at that shop was made of currants, and was rather a special pudding, but was dear, twopennyworth not being larger than a pennyworth of more ordinary pudding. A good shop for the latter was in the Strand--somewhere in that part which has been rebuilt since. It was a stout pale pudding, heavy and flabby, and with great flat raisins in it, stuck in whole at wide distances apart. It came up hot at about my time every day, and many a day did I dine off it. When I dined regularly and handsomely, I had a saveloy and a penny loaf, or a fourpenny plate of red beef from a cook’s shop; or a plate of bread and cheese and a glass of beer, from a miserable old public-house opposite our place of business, called the Lion, or the Lion and something else that I have forgotten. Once, I remember carrying my own bread (which I had brought from home in the morning) under my arm, wrapped in a piece of paper, like a book, and going to a famous alamode beef-house near Drury Lane, and ordering a ‘small plate’ of that delicacy to eat with it. What the waiter thought of such a strange little apparition coming in all alone, I don’t know; but I can see him now, staring at me as I ate my dinner, and bringing up the other waiter to look. I gave him a halfpenny for himself, and I wish he hadn’t taken it.\n\nWe had half-an-hour, I think, for tea. When I had money enough, I used to get half-a-pint of ready-made coffee and a slice of bread and butter. When I had none, I used to look at a venison shop in Fleet Street; or I have strolled, at such a time, as far as Covent Garden Market, and stared at the pineapples. I was fond of wandering about the Adelphi, because it was a mysterious place, with those dark arches. I see myself emerging one evening from some of these arches, on a little public-house close to the river, with an open space before it, where some coal-heavers were dancing; to look at whom I sat down upon a bench. I wonder what they thought of me!\n\nI was such a child, and so little, that frequently when I went into the bar of a strange public-house for a glass of ale or porter, to moisten what I had had for dinner, they were afraid to give it me. I remember one hot evening I went into the bar of a public-house, and said to the landlord: ‘What is your best--your very best--ale a glass?’ For it was a special occasion. I don’t know what. It may have been my birthday.\n\n‘Twopence-halfpenny,’ says the landlord, ‘is the price of the Genuine Stunning ale.’\n\n‘Then,’ says I, producing the money, ‘just draw me a glass of the Genuine Stunning, if you please, with a good head to it.’\n\nThe landlord looked at me in return over the bar, from head to foot, with a strange smile on his face; and instead of drawing the beer, looked round the screen and said something to his wife. She came out from behind it, with her work in her hand, and joined him in surveying me. Here we stand, all three, before me now. The landlord in his shirt-sleeves, leaning against the bar window-frame; his wife looking over the little half-door; and I, in some confusion, looking up at them from outside the partition. They asked me a good many questions; as, what my name was, how old I was, where I lived, how I was employed, and how I came there. To all of which, that I might commit nobody, I invented, I am afraid, appropriate answers. They served me with the ale, though I suspect it was not the Genuine Stunning; and the landlord’s wife, opening the little half-door of the bar, and bending down, gave me my money back, and gave me a kiss that was half admiring and half compassionate, but all womanly and good, I am sure.\n\nI know I do not exaggerate, unconsciously and unintentionally, the scantiness of my resources or the difficulties of my life. I know that if a shilling were given me by Mr. Quinion at any time, I spent it in a dinner or a tea. I know that I worked, from morning until night, with common men and boys, a shabby child. I know that I lounged about the streets, insufficiently and unsatisfactorily fed. I know that, but for the mercy of God, I might easily have been, for any care that was taken of me, a little robber or a little vagabond.\n\nYet I held some station at Murdstone and Grinby’s too. Besides that Mr. Quinion did what a careless man so occupied, and dealing with a thing so anomalous, could, to treat me as one upon a different footing from the rest, I never said, to man or boy, how it was that I came to be there, or gave the least indication of being sorry that I was there. That I suffered in secret, and that I suffered exquisitely, no one ever knew but I. How much I suffered, it is, as I have said already, utterly beyond my power to tell. But I kept my own counsel, and I did my work. I knew from the first, that, if I could not do my work as well as any of the rest, I could not hold myself above slight and contempt. I soon became at least as expeditious and as skilful as either of the other boys. Though perfectly familiar with them, my conduct and manner were different enough from theirs to place a space between us. They and the men generally spoke of me as ‘the little gent’, or ‘the young Suffolker.’ A certain man named Gregory, who was foreman of the packers, and another named Tipp, who was the carman, and wore a red jacket, used to address me sometimes as ‘David’: but I think it was mostly when we were very confidential, and when I had made some efforts to entertain them, over our work, with some results of the old readings; which were fast perishing out of my remembrance. Mealy Potatoes uprose once, and rebelled against my being so distinguished; but Mick Walker settled him in no time.\n\nMy rescue from this kind of existence I considered quite hopeless, and abandoned, as such, altogether. I am solemnly convinced that I never for one hour was reconciled to it, or was otherwise than miserably unhappy; but I bore it; and even to Peggotty, partly for the love of her and partly for shame, never in any letter (though many passed between us) revealed the truth.\n\nMr. Micawber’s difficulties were an addition to the distressed state of my mind. In my forlorn state I became quite attached to the family, and used to walk about, busy with Mrs. Micawber’s calculations of ways and means, and heavy with the weight of Mr. Micawber’s debts. On a Saturday night, which was my grand treat,--partly because it was a great thing to walk home with six or seven shillings in my pocket, looking into the shops and thinking what such a sum would buy, and partly because I went home early,--Mrs. Micawber would make the most heart-rending confidences to me; also on a Sunday morning, when I mixed the portion of tea or coffee I had bought over-night, in a little shaving-pot, and sat late at my breakfast. It was nothing at all unusual for Mr. Micawber to sob violently at the beginning of one of these Saturday night conversations, and sing about Jack’s delight being his lovely Nan, towards the end of it. I have known him come home to supper with a flood of tears, and a declaration that nothing was now left but a jail; and go to bed making a calculation of the expense of putting bow-windows to the house, ‘in case anything turned up’, which was his favourite expression. And Mrs. Micawber was just the same.\n\nA curious equality of friendship, originating, I suppose, in our respective circumstances, sprung up between me and these people, notwithstanding the ludicrous disparity in our years. But I never allowed myself to be prevailed upon to accept any invitation to eat and drink with them out of their stock (knowing that they got on badly with the butcher and baker, and had often not too much for themselves), until Mrs. Micawber took me into her entire confidence. This she did one evening as follows:\n\n‘Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘I make no stranger of you, and therefore do not hesitate to say that Mr. Micawber’s difficulties are coming to a crisis.’\n\nIt made me very miserable to hear it, and I looked at Mrs. Micawber’s red eyes with the utmost sympathy.\n\n‘With the exception of the heel of a Dutch cheese--which is not adapted to the wants of a young family’--said Mrs. Micawber, ‘there is really not a scrap of anything in the larder. I was accustomed to speak of the larder when I lived with papa and mama, and I use the word almost unconsciously. What I mean to express is, that there is nothing to eat in the house.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ I said, in great concern.\n\nI had two or three shillings of my week’s money in my pocket--from which I presume that it must have been on a Wednesday night when we held this conversation--and I hastily produced them, and with heartfelt emotion begged Mrs. Micawber to accept of them as a loan. But that lady, kissing me, and making me put them back in my pocket, replied that she couldn’t think of it.\n\n‘No, my dear Master Copperfield,’ said she, ‘far be it from my thoughts! But you have a discretion beyond your years, and can render me another kind of service, if you will; and a service I will thankfully accept of.’\n\nI begged Mrs. Micawber to name it.\n\n‘I have parted with the plate myself,’ said Mrs. Micawber. ‘Six tea, two salt, and a pair of sugars, I have at different times borrowed money on, in secret, with my own hands. But the twins are a great tie; and to me, with my recollections, of papa and mama, these transactions are very painful. There are still a few trifles that we could part with. Mr. Micawber’s feelings would never allow him to dispose of them; and Clickett’--this was the girl from the workhouse--‘being of a vulgar mind, would take painful liberties if so much confidence was reposed in her. Master Copperfield, if I might ask you--’\n\nI understood Mrs. Micawber now, and begged her to make use of me to any extent. I began to dispose of the more portable articles of property that very evening; and went out on a similar expedition almost every morning, before I went to Murdstone and Grinby’s.\n\nMr. Micawber had a few books on a little chiffonier, which he called the library; and those went first. I carried them, one after another, to a bookstall in the City Road--one part of which, near our house, was almost all bookstalls and bird shops then--and sold them for whatever they would bring. The keeper of this bookstall, who lived in a little house behind it, used to get tipsy every night, and to be violently scolded by his wife every morning. More than once, when I went there early, I had audience of him in a turn-up bedstead, with a cut in his forehead or a black eye, bearing witness to his excesses over-night (I am afraid he was quarrelsome in his drink), and he, with a shaking hand, endeavouring to find the needful shillings in one or other of the pockets of his clothes, which lay upon the floor, while his wife, with a baby in her arms and her shoes down at heel, never left off rating him. Sometimes he had lost his money, and then he would ask me to call again; but his wife had always got some--had taken his, I dare say, while he was drunk--and secretly completed the bargain on the stairs, as we went down together. At the pawnbroker’s shop, too, I began to be very well known. The principal gentleman who officiated behind the counter, took a good deal of notice of me; and often got me, I recollect, to decline a Latin noun or adjective, or to conjugate a Latin verb, in his ear, while he transacted my business. After all these occasions Mrs. Micawber made a little treat, which was generally a supper; and there was a peculiar relish in these meals which I well remember.\n\nAt last Mr. Micawber’s difficulties came to a crisis, and he was arrested early one morning, and carried over to the King’s Bench Prison in the Borough. He told me, as he went out of the house, that the God of day had now gone down upon him--and I really thought his heart was broken and mine too. But I heard, afterwards, that he was seen to play a lively game at skittles, before noon.\n\nOn the first Sunday after he was taken there, I was to go and see him, and have dinner with him. I was to ask my way to such a place, and just short of that place I should see such another place, and just short of that I should see a yard, which I was to cross, and keep straight on until I saw a turnkey. All this I did; and when at last I did see a turnkey (poor little fellow that I was!), and thought how, when Roderick Random was in a debtors’ prison, there was a man there with nothing on him but an old rug, the turnkey swam before my dimmed eyes and my beating heart.\n\nMr. Micawber was waiting for me within the gate, and we went up to his room (top story but one), and cried very much. He solemnly conjured me, I remember, to take warning by his fate; and to observe that if a man had twenty pounds a-year for his income, and spent nineteen pounds nineteen shillings and sixpence, he would be happy, but that if he spent twenty pounds one he would be miserable. After which he borrowed a shilling of me for porter, gave me a written order on Mrs. Micawber for the amount, and put away his pocket-handkerchief, and cheered up.\n\nWe sat before a little fire, with two bricks put within the rusted grate, one on each side, to prevent its burning too many coals; until another debtor, who shared the room with Mr. Micawber, came in from the bakehouse with the loin of mutton which was our joint-stock repast. Then I was sent up to ‘Captain Hopkins’ in the room overhead, with Mr. Micawber’s compliments, and I was his young friend, and would Captain Hopkins lend me a knife and fork.\n\nCaptain Hopkins lent me the knife and fork, with his compliments to Mr. Micawber. There was a very dirty lady in his little room, and two wan girls, his daughters, with shock heads of hair. I thought it was better to borrow Captain Hopkins’s knife and fork, than Captain Hopkins’s comb. The Captain himself was in the last extremity of shabbiness, with large whiskers, and an old, old brown great-coat with no other coat below it. I saw his bed rolled up in a corner; and what plates and dishes and pots he had, on a shelf; and I divined (God knows how) that though the two girls with the shock heads of hair were Captain Hopkins’s children, the dirty lady was not married to Captain Hopkins. My timid station on his threshold was not occupied more than a couple of minutes at most; but I came down again with all this in my knowledge, as surely as the knife and fork were in my hand.\n\nThere was something gipsy-like and agreeable in the dinner, after all. I took back Captain Hopkins’s knife and fork early in the afternoon, and went home to comfort Mrs. Micawber with an account of my visit. She fainted when she saw me return, and made a little jug of egg-hot afterwards to console us while we talked it over.\n\nI don’t know how the household furniture came to be sold for the family benefit, or who sold it, except that I did not. Sold it was, however, and carried away in a van; except the bed, a few chairs, and the kitchen table. With these possessions we encamped, as it were, in the two parlours of the emptied house in Windsor Terrace; Mrs. Micawber, the children, the Orfling, and myself; and lived in those rooms night and day. I have no idea for how long, though it seems to me for a long time. At last Mrs. Micawber resolved to move into the prison, where Mr. Micawber had now secured a room to himself. So I took the key of the house to the landlord, who was very glad to get it; and the beds were sent over to the King’s Bench, except mine, for which a little room was hired outside the walls in the neighbourhood of that Institution, very much to my satisfaction, since the Micawbers and I had become too used to one another, in our troubles, to part. The Orfling was likewise accommodated with an inexpensive lodging in the same neighbourhood. Mine was a quiet back-garret with a sloping roof, commanding a pleasant prospect of a timberyard; and when I took possession of it, with the reflection that Mr. Micawber’s troubles had come to a crisis at last, I thought it quite a paradise.\n\nAll this time I was working at Murdstone and Grinby’s in the same common way, and with the same common companions, and with the same sense of unmerited degradation as at first. But I never, happily for me no doubt, made a single acquaintance, or spoke to any of the many boys whom I saw daily in going to the warehouse, in coming from it, and in prowling about the streets at meal-times. I led the same secretly unhappy life; but I led it in the same lonely, self-reliant manner. The only changes I am conscious of are, firstly, that I had grown more shabby, and secondly, that I was now relieved of much of the weight of Mr. and Mrs. Micawber’s cares; for some relatives or friends had engaged to help them at their present pass, and they lived more comfortably in the prison than they had lived for a long while out of it. I used to breakfast with them now, in virtue of some arrangement, of which I have forgotten the details. I forget, too, at what hour the gates were opened in the morning, admitting of my going in; but I know that I was often up at six o’clock, and that my favourite lounging-place in the interval was old London Bridge, where I was wont to sit in one of the stone recesses, watching the people going by, or to look over the balustrades at the sun shining in the water, and lighting up the golden flame on the top of the Monument. The Orfling met me here sometimes, to be told some astonishing fictions respecting the wharves and the Tower; of which I can say no more than that I hope I believed them myself. In the evening I used to go back to the prison, and walk up and down the parade with Mr. Micawber; or play casino with Mrs. Micawber, and hear reminiscences of her papa and mama. Whether Mr. Murdstone knew where I was, I am unable to say. I never told them at Murdstone and Grinby’s.\n\nMr. Micawber’s affairs, although past their crisis, were very much involved by reason of a certain ‘Deed’, of which I used to hear a great deal, and which I suppose, now, to have been some former composition with his creditors, though I was so far from being clear about it then, that I am conscious of having confounded it with those demoniacal parchments which are held to have, once upon a time, obtained to a great extent in Germany. At last this document appeared to be got out of the way, somehow; at all events it ceased to be the rock-ahead it had been; and Mrs. Micawber informed me that ‘her family’ had decided that Mr. Micawber should apply for his release under the Insolvent Debtors Act, which would set him free, she expected, in about six weeks.\n\n‘And then,’ said Mr. Micawber, who was present, ‘I have no doubt I shall, please Heaven, begin to be beforehand with the world, and to live in a perfectly new manner, if--in short, if anything turns up.’\n\nBy way of going in for anything that might be on the cards, I call to mind that Mr. Micawber, about this time, composed a petition to the House of Commons, praying for an alteration in the law of imprisonment for debt. I set down this remembrance here, because it is an instance to myself of the manner in which I fitted my old books to my altered life, and made stories for myself, out of the streets, and out of men and women; and how some main points in the character I shall unconsciously develop, I suppose, in writing my life, were gradually forming all this while.\n\nThere was a club in the prison, in which Mr. Micawber, as a gentleman, was a great authority. Mr. Micawber had stated his idea of this petition to the club, and the club had strongly approved of the same. Wherefore Mr. Micawber (who was a thoroughly good-natured man, and as active a creature about everything but his own affairs as ever existed, and never so happy as when he was busy about something that could never be of any profit to him) set to work at the petition, invented it, engrossed it on an immense sheet of paper, spread it out on a table, and appointed a time for all the club, and all within the walls if they chose, to come up to his room and sign it.\n\nWhen I heard of this approaching ceremony, I was so anxious to see them all come in, one after another, though I knew the greater part of them already, and they me, that I got an hour’s leave of absence from Murdstone and Grinby’s, and established myself in a corner for that purpose. As many of the principal members of the club as could be got into the small room without filling it, supported Mr. Micawber in front of the petition, while my old friend Captain Hopkins (who had washed himself, to do honour to so solemn an occasion) stationed himself close to it, to read it to all who were unacquainted with its contents. The door was then thrown open, and the general population began to come in, in a long file: several waiting outside, while one entered, affixed his signature, and went out. To everybody in succession, Captain Hopkins said: ‘Have you read it?’--‘No.’---‘Would you like to hear it read?’ If he weakly showed the least disposition to hear it, Captain Hopkins, in a loud sonorous voice, gave him every word of it. The Captain would have read it twenty thousand times, if twenty thousand people would have heard him, one by one. I remember a certain luscious roll he gave to such phrases as ‘The people’s representatives in Parliament assembled,’ ‘Your petitioners therefore humbly approach your honourable house,’ ‘His gracious Majesty’s unfortunate subjects,’ as if the words were something real in his mouth, and delicious to taste; Mr. Micawber, meanwhile, listening with a little of an author’s vanity, and contemplating (not severely) the spikes on the opposite wall.\n\nAs I walked to and fro daily between Southwark and Blackfriars, and lounged about at meal-times in obscure streets, the stones of which may, for anything I know, be worn at this moment by my childish feet, I wonder how many of these people were wanting in the crowd that used to come filing before me in review again, to the echo of Captain Hopkins’s voice! When my thoughts go back, now, to that slow agony of my youth, I wonder how much of the histories I invented for such people hangs like a mist of fancy over well-remembered facts! When I tread the old ground, I do not wonder that I seem to see and pity, going on before me, an innocent romantic boy, making his imaginative world out of such strange experiences and sordid things!\n\n# CHAPTER 12. LIKING LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT NO BETTER, I FORM A GREAT RESOLUTION\n\nIn due time, Mr. Micawber’s petition was ripe for hearing; and that gentleman was ordered to be discharged under the Act, to my great joy. His creditors were not implacable; and Mrs. Micawber informed me that even the revengeful boot-maker had declared in open court that he bore him no malice, but that when money was owing to him he liked to be paid. He said he thought it was human nature.\n\nMr. Micawber returned to the King’s Bench when his case was over, as some fees were to be settled, and some formalities observed, before he could be actually released. The club received him with transport, and held an harmonic meeting that evening in his honour; while Mrs. Micawber and I had a lamb’s fry in private, surrounded by the sleeping family.\n\n‘On such an occasion I will give you, Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘in a little more flip,’ for we had been having some already, ‘the memory of my papa and mama.’\n\n‘Are they dead, ma’am?’ I inquired, after drinking the toast in a wine-glass.\n\n‘My mama departed this life,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘before Mr. Micawber’s difficulties commenced, or at least before they became pressing. My papa lived to bail Mr. Micawber several times, and then expired, regretted by a numerous circle.’\n\nMrs. Micawber shook her head, and dropped a pious tear upon the twin who happened to be in hand.\n\nAs I could hardly hope for a more favourable opportunity of putting a question in which I had a near interest, I said to Mrs. Micawber:\n\n‘May I ask, ma’am, what you and Mr. Micawber intend to do, now that Mr. Micawber is out of his difficulties, and at liberty? Have you settled yet?’\n\n‘My family,’ said Mrs. Micawber, who always said those two words with an air, though I never could discover who came under the denomination, ‘my family are of opinion that Mr. Micawber should quit London, and exert his talents in the country. Mr. Micawber is a man of great talent, Master Copperfield.’\n\nI said I was sure of that.\n\n‘Of great talent,’ repeated Mrs. Micawber. ‘My family are of opinion, that, with a little interest, something might be done for a man of his ability in the Custom House. The influence of my family being local, it is their wish that Mr. Micawber should go down to Plymouth. They think it indispensable that he should be upon the spot.’\n\n‘That he may be ready?’ I suggested.\n\n‘Exactly,’ returned Mrs. Micawber. ‘That he may be ready--in case of anything turning up.’\n\n‘And do you go too, ma’am?’\n\nThe events of the day, in combination with the twins, if not with the flip, had made Mrs. Micawber hysterical, and she shed tears as she replied:\n\n‘I never will desert Mr. Micawber. Mr. Micawber may have concealed his difficulties from me in the first instance, but his sanguine temper may have led him to expect that he would overcome them. The pearl necklace and bracelets which I inherited from mama, have been disposed of for less than half their value; and the set of coral, which was the wedding gift of my papa, has been actually thrown away for nothing. But I never will desert Mr. Micawber. No!’ cried Mrs. Micawber, more affected than before, ‘I never will do it! It’s of no use asking me!’\n\nI felt quite uncomfortable--as if Mrs. Micawber supposed I had asked her to do anything of the sort!--and sat looking at her in alarm.\n\n‘Mr. Micawber has his faults. I do not deny that he is improvident. I do not deny that he has kept me in the dark as to his resources and his liabilities both,’ she went on, looking at the wall; ‘but I never will desert Mr. Micawber!’\n\nMrs. Micawber having now raised her voice into a perfect scream, I was so frightened that I ran off to the club-room, and disturbed Mr. Micawber in the act of presiding at a long table, and leading the chorus of\n\nGee up, Dobbin, Gee ho, Dobbin, Gee up, Dobbin, Gee up, and gee ho--o--o!\n\n--with the tidings that Mrs. Micawber was in an alarming state, upon which he immediately burst into tears, and came away with me with his waistcoat full of the heads and tails of shrimps, of which he had been partaking.\n\n‘Emma, my angel!’ cried Mr. Micawber, running into the room; ‘what is the matter?’\n\n‘I never will desert you, Micawber!’ she exclaimed.\n\n‘My life!’ said Mr. Micawber, taking her in his arms. ‘I am perfectly aware of it.’\n\n‘He is the parent of my children! He is the father of my twins! He is the husband of my affections,’ cried Mrs. Micawber, struggling; ‘and I ne--ver--will--desert Mr. Micawber!’\n\nMr. Micawber was so deeply affected by this proof of her devotion (as to me, I was dissolved in tears), that he hung over her in a passionate manner, imploring her to look up, and to be calm. But the more he asked Mrs. Micawber to look up, the more she fixed her eyes on nothing; and the more he asked her to compose herself, the more she wouldn’t. Consequently Mr. Micawber was soon so overcome, that he mingled his tears with hers and mine; until he begged me to do him the favour of taking a chair on the staircase, while he got her into bed. I would have taken my leave for the night, but he would not hear of my doing that until the strangers’ bell should ring. So I sat at the staircase window, until he came out with another chair and joined me.\n\n‘How is Mrs. Micawber now, sir?’ I said.\n\n‘Very low,’ said Mr. Micawber, shaking his head; ‘reaction. Ah, this has been a dreadful day! We stand alone now--everything is gone from us!’\n\nMr. Micawber pressed my hand, and groaned, and afterwards shed tears. I was greatly touched, and disappointed too, for I had expected that we should be quite gay on this happy and long-looked-for occasion. But Mr. and Mrs. Micawber were so used to their old difficulties, I think, that they felt quite shipwrecked when they came to consider that they were released from them. All their elasticity was departed, and I never saw them half so wretched as on this night; insomuch that when the bell rang, and Mr. Micawber walked with me to the lodge, and parted from me there with a blessing, I felt quite afraid to leave him by himself, he was so profoundly miserable.\n\nBut through all the confusion and lowness of spirits in which we had been, so unexpectedly to me, involved, I plainly discerned that Mr. and Mrs. Micawber and their family were going away from London, and that a parting between us was near at hand. It was in my walk home that night, and in the sleepless hours which followed when I lay in bed, that the thought first occurred to me--though I don’t know how it came into my head--which afterwards shaped itself into a settled resolution.\n\nI had grown to be so accustomed to the Micawbers, and had been so intimate with them in their distresses, and was so utterly friendless without them, that the prospect of being thrown upon some new shift for a lodging, and going once more among unknown people, was like being that moment turned adrift into my present life, with such a knowledge of it ready made as experience had given me. All the sensitive feelings it wounded so cruelly, all the shame and misery it kept alive within my breast, became more poignant as I thought of this; and I determined that the life was unendurable.\n\nThat there was no hope of escape from it, unless the escape was my own act, I knew quite well. I rarely heard from Miss Murdstone, and never from Mr. Murdstone: but two or three parcels of made or mended clothes had come up for me, consigned to Mr. Quinion, and in each there was a scrap of paper to the effect that J. M. trusted D. C. was applying himself to business, and devoting himself wholly to his duties--not the least hint of my ever being anything else than the common drudge into which I was fast settling down.\n\nThe very next day showed me, while my mind was in the first agitation of what it had conceived, that Mrs. Micawber had not spoken of their going away without warrant. They took a lodging in the house where I lived, for a week; at the expiration of which time they were to start for Plymouth. Mr. Micawber himself came down to the counting-house, in the afternoon, to tell Mr. Quinion that he must relinquish me on the day of his departure, and to give me a high character, which I am sure I deserved. And Mr. Quinion, calling in Tipp the carman, who was a married man, and had a room to let, quartered me prospectively on him--by our mutual consent, as he had every reason to think; for I said nothing, though my resolution was now taken.\n\nI passed my evenings with Mr. and Mrs. Micawber, during the remaining term of our residence under the same roof; and I think we became fonder of one another as the time went on. On the last Sunday, they invited me to dinner; and we had a loin of pork and apple sauce, and a pudding. I had bought a spotted wooden horse over-night as a parting gift to little Wilkins Micawber--that was the boy--and a doll for little Emma. I had also bestowed a shilling on the Orfling, who was about to be disbanded.\n\nWe had a very pleasant day, though we were all in a tender state about our approaching separation.\n\n‘I shall never, Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘revert to the period when Mr. Micawber was in difficulties, without thinking of you. Your conduct has always been of the most delicate and obliging description. You have never been a lodger. You have been a friend.’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Micawber; ‘Copperfield,’ for so he had been accustomed to call me, of late, ‘has a heart to feel for the distresses of his fellow-creatures when they are behind a cloud, and a head to plan, and a hand to--in short, a general ability to dispose of such available property as could be made away with.’\n\nI expressed my sense of this commendation, and said I was very sorry we were going to lose one another.\n\n‘My dear young friend,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘I am older than you; a man of some experience in life, and--and of some experience, in short, in difficulties, generally speaking. At present, and until something turns up (which I am, I may say, hourly expecting), I have nothing to bestow but advice. Still my advice is so far worth taking, that--in short, that I have never taken it myself, and am the’--here Mr. Micawber, who had been beaming and smiling, all over his head and face, up to the present moment, checked himself and frowned--‘the miserable wretch you behold.’\n\n‘My dear Micawber!’ urged his wife.\n\n‘I say,’ returned Mr. Micawber, quite forgetting himself, and smiling again, ‘the miserable wretch you behold. My advice is, never do tomorrow what you can do today. Procrastination is the thief of time. Collar him!’\n\n‘My poor papa’s maxim,’ Mrs. Micawber observed.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘your papa was very well in his way, and Heaven forbid that I should disparage him. Take him for all in all, we ne’er shall--in short, make the acquaintance, probably, of anybody else possessing, at his time of life, the same legs for gaiters, and able to read the same description of print, without spectacles. But he applied that maxim to our marriage, my dear; and that was so far prematurely entered into, in consequence, that I never recovered the expense.’ Mr. Micawber looked aside at Mrs. Micawber, and added: ‘Not that I am sorry for it. Quite the contrary, my love.’ After which, he was grave for a minute or so.\n\n‘My other piece of advice, Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘you know. Annual income twenty pounds, annual expenditure nineteen nineteen and six, result happiness. Annual income twenty pounds, annual expenditure twenty pounds ought and six, result misery. The blossom is blighted, the leaf is withered, the god of day goes down upon the dreary scene, and--and in short you are for ever floored. As I am!’\n\nTo make his example the more impressive, Mr. Micawber drank a glass of punch with an air of great enjoyment and satisfaction, and whistled the College Hornpipe.\n\nI did not fail to assure him that I would store these precepts in my mind, though indeed I had no need to do so, for, at the time, they affected me visibly. Next morning I met the whole family at the coach office, and saw them, with a desolate heart, take their places outside, at the back.\n\n‘Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘God bless you! I never can forget all that, you know, and I never would if I could.’\n\n‘Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘farewell! Every happiness and prosperity! If, in the progress of revolving years, I could persuade myself that my blighted destiny had been a warning to you, I should feel that I had not occupied another man’s place in existence altogether in vain. In case of anything turning up (of which I am rather confident), I shall be extremely happy if it should be in my power to improve your prospects.’\n\nI think, as Mrs. Micawber sat at the back of the coach, with the children, and I stood in the road looking wistfully at them, a mist cleared from her eyes, and she saw what a little creature I really was. I think so, because she beckoned to me to climb up, with quite a new and motherly expression in her face, and put her arm round my neck, and gave me just such a kiss as she might have given to her own boy. I had barely time to get down again before the coach started, and I could hardly see the family for the handkerchiefs they waved. It was gone in a minute. The Orfling and I stood looking vacantly at each other in the middle of the road, and then shook hands and said good-bye; she going back, I suppose, to St. Luke’s workhouse, as I went to begin my weary day at Murdstone and Grinby’s.\n\nBut with no intention of passing many more weary days there. No. I had resolved to run away.---To go, by some means or other, down into the country, to the only relation I had in the world, and tell my story to my aunt, Miss Betsey. I have already observed that I don’t know how this desperate idea came into my brain. But, once there, it remained there; and hardened into a purpose than which I have never entertained a more determined purpose in my life. I am far from sure that I believed there was anything hopeful in it, but my mind was thoroughly made up that it must be carried into execution.\n\nAgain, and again, and a hundred times again, since the night when the thought had first occurred to me and banished sleep, I had gone over that old story of my poor mother’s about my birth, which it had been one of my great delights in the old time to hear her tell, and which I knew by heart. My aunt walked into that story, and walked out of it, a dread and awful personage; but there was one little trait in her behaviour which I liked to dwell on, and which gave me some faint shadow of encouragement. I could not forget how my mother had thought that she felt her touch her pretty hair with no ungentle hand; and though it might have been altogether my mother’s fancy, and might have had no foundation whatever in fact, I made a little picture, out of it, of my terrible aunt relenting towards the girlish beauty that I recollected so well and loved so much, which softened the whole narrative. It is very possible that it had been in my mind a long time, and had gradually engendered my determination.\n\nAs I did not even know where Miss Betsey lived, I wrote a long letter to Peggotty, and asked her, incidentally, if she remembered; pretending that I had heard of such a lady living at a certain place I named at random, and had a curiosity to know if it were the same. In the course of that letter, I told Peggotty that I had a particular occasion for half a guinea; and that if she could lend me that sum until I could repay it, I should be very much obliged to her, and would tell her afterwards what I had wanted it for.\n\nPeggotty’s answer soon arrived, and was, as usual, full of affectionate devotion. She enclosed the half guinea (I was afraid she must have had a world of trouble to get it out of Mr. Barkis’s box), and told me that Miss Betsey lived near Dover, but whether at Dover itself, at Hythe, Sandgate, or Folkestone, she could not say. One of our men, however, informing me on my asking him about these places, that they were all close together, I deemed this enough for my object, and resolved to set out at the end of that week.\n\nBeing a very honest little creature, and unwilling to disgrace the memory I was going to leave behind me at Murdstone and Grinby’s, I considered myself bound to remain until Saturday night; and, as I had been paid a week’s wages in advance when I first came there, not to present myself in the counting-house at the usual hour, to receive my stipend. For this express reason, I had borrowed the half-guinea, that I might not be without a fund for my travelling-expenses. Accordingly, when the Saturday night came, and we were all waiting in the warehouse to be paid, and Tipp the carman, who always took precedence, went in first to draw his money, I shook Mick Walker by the hand; asked him, when it came to his turn to be paid, to say to Mr. Quinion that I had gone to move my box to Tipp’s; and, bidding a last good night to Mealy Potatoes, ran away.\n\nMy box was at my old lodging, over the water, and I had written a direction for it on the back of one of our address cards that we nailed on the casks: ‘Master David, to be left till called for, at the Coach Office, Dover.’ This I had in my pocket ready to put on the box, after I should have got it out of the house; and as I went towards my lodging, I looked about me for someone who would help me to carry it to the booking-office.\n\nThere was a long-legged young man with a very little empty donkey-cart, standing near the Obelisk, in the Blackfriars Road, whose eye I caught as I was going by, and who, addressing me as ‘Sixpenn’orth of bad ha’pence,’ hoped ‘I should know him agin to swear to’--in allusion, I have no doubt, to my staring at him. I stopped to assure him that I had not done so in bad manners, but uncertain whether he might or might not like a job.\n\n‘Wot job?’ said the long-legged young man.\n\n‘To move a box,’ I answered.\n\n‘Wot box?’ said the long-legged young man.\n\nI told him mine, which was down that street there, and which I wanted him to take to the Dover coach office for sixpence.\n\n‘Done with you for a tanner!’ said the long-legged young man, and directly got upon his cart, which was nothing but a large wooden tray on wheels, and rattled away at such a rate, that it was as much as I could do to keep pace with the donkey.\n\nThere was a defiant manner about this young man, and particularly about the way in which he chewed straw as he spoke to me, that I did not much like; as the bargain was made, however, I took him upstairs to the room I was leaving, and we brought the box down, and put it on his cart. Now, I was unwilling to put the direction-card on there, lest any of my landlord’s family should fathom what I was doing, and detain me; so I said to the young man that I would be glad if he would stop for a minute, when he came to the dead-wall of the King’s Bench prison. The words were no sooner out of my mouth, than he rattled away as if he, my box, the cart, and the donkey, were all equally mad; and I was quite out of breath with running and calling after him, when I caught him at the place appointed.\n\nBeing much flushed and excited, I tumbled my half-guinea out of my pocket in pulling the card out. I put it in my mouth for safety, and though my hands trembled a good deal, had just tied the card on very much to my satisfaction, when I felt myself violently chucked under the chin by the long-legged young man, and saw my half-guinea fly out of my mouth into his hand.\n\n‘Wot!’ said the young man, seizing me by my jacket collar, with a frightful grin. ‘This is a pollis case, is it? You’re a-going to bolt, are you? Come to the pollis, you young warmin, come to the pollis!’\n\n‘You give me my money back, if you please,’ said I, very much frightened; ‘and leave me alone.’\n\n‘Come to the pollis!’ said the young man. ‘You shall prove it yourn to the pollis.’\n\n‘Give me my box and money, will you,’ I cried, bursting into tears.\n\nThe young man still replied: ‘Come to the pollis!’ and was dragging me against the donkey in a violent manner, as if there were any affinity between that animal and a magistrate, when he changed his mind, jumped into the cart, sat upon my box, and, exclaiming that he would drive to the pollis straight, rattled away harder than ever.\n\nI ran after him as fast as I could, but I had no breath to call out with, and should not have dared to call out, now, if I had. I narrowly escaped being run over, twenty times at least, in half a mile. Now I lost him, now I saw him, now I lost him, now I was cut at with a whip, now shouted at, now down in the mud, now up again, now running into somebody’s arms, now running headlong at a post. At length, confused by fright and heat, and doubting whether half London might not by this time be turning out for my apprehension, I left the young man to go where he would with my box and money; and, panting and crying, but never stopping, faced about for Greenwich, which I had understood was on the Dover Road: taking very little more out of the world, towards the retreat of my aunt, Miss Betsey, than I had brought into it, on the night when my arrival gave her so much umbrage.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. THE SEQUEL OF MY RESOLUTION\n\nFor anything I know, I may have had some wild idea of running all the way to Dover, when I gave up the pursuit of the young man with the donkey-cart, and started for Greenwich. My scattered senses were soon collected as to that point, if I had; for I came to a stop in the Kent Road, at a terrace with a piece of water before it, and a great foolish image in the middle, blowing a dry shell. Here I sat down on a doorstep, quite spent and exhausted with the efforts I had already made, and with hardly breath enough to cry for the loss of my box and half-guinea.\n\nIt was by this time dark; I heard the clocks strike ten, as I sat resting. But it was a summer night, fortunately, and fine weather. When I had recovered my breath, and had got rid of a stifling sensation in my throat, I rose up and went on. In the midst of my distress, I had no notion of going back. I doubt if I should have had any, though there had been a Swiss snow-drift in the Kent Road.\n\nBut my standing possessed of only three-halfpence in the world (and I am sure I wonder how they came to be left in my pocket on a Saturday night!) troubled me none the less because I went on. I began to picture to myself, as a scrap of newspaper intelligence, my being found dead in a day or two, under some hedge; and I trudged on miserably, though as fast as I could, until I happened to pass a little shop, where it was written up that ladies’ and gentlemen’s wardrobes were bought, and that the best price was given for rags, bones, and kitchen-stuff. The master of this shop was sitting at the door in his shirt-sleeves, smoking; and as there were a great many coats and pairs of trousers dangling from the low ceiling, and only two feeble candles burning inside to show what they were, I fancied that he looked like a man of a revengeful disposition, who had hung all his enemies, and was enjoying himself.\n\nMy late experiences with Mr. and Mrs. Micawber suggested to me that here might be a means of keeping off the wolf for a little while. I went up the next by-street, took off my waistcoat, rolled it neatly under my arm, and came back to the shop door.\n\n‘If you please, sir,’ I said, ‘I am to sell this for a fair price.’\n\nMr. Dolloby--Dolloby was the name over the shop door, at least--took the waistcoat, stood his pipe on its head, against the door-post, went into the shop, followed by me, snuffed the two candles with his fingers, spread the waistcoat on the counter, and looked at it there, held it up against the light, and looked at it there, and ultimately said:\n\n‘What do you call a price, now, for this here little weskit?’\n\n‘Oh! you know best, sir,’ I returned modestly.\n\n‘I can’t be buyer and seller too,’ said Mr. Dolloby. ‘Put a price on this here little weskit.’\n\n‘Would eighteenpence be?’--I hinted, after some hesitation.\n\nMr. Dolloby rolled it up again, and gave it me back. ‘I should rob my family,’ he said, ‘if I was to offer ninepence for it.’\n\nThis was a disagreeable way of putting the business; because it imposed upon me, a perfect stranger, the unpleasantness of asking Mr. Dolloby to rob his family on my account. My circumstances being so very pressing, however, I said I would take ninepence for it, if he pleased. Mr. Dolloby, not without some grumbling, gave ninepence. I wished him good night, and walked out of the shop the richer by that sum, and the poorer by a waistcoat. But when I buttoned my jacket, that was not much. Indeed, I foresaw pretty clearly that my jacket would go next, and that I should have to make the best of my way to Dover in a shirt and a pair of trousers, and might deem myself lucky if I got there even in that trim. But my mind did not run so much on this as might be supposed. Beyond a general impression of the distance before me, and of the young man with the donkey-cart having used me cruelly, I think I had no very urgent sense of my difficulties when I once again set off with my ninepence in my pocket.\n\nA plan had occurred to me for passing the night, which I was going to carry into execution. This was, to lie behind the wall at the back of my old school, in a corner where there used to be a haystack. I imagined it would be a kind of company to have the boys, and the bedroom where I used to tell the stories, so near me: although the boys would know nothing of my being there, and the bedroom would yield me no shelter.\n\nI had had a hard day’s work, and was pretty well jaded when I came climbing out, at last, upon the level of Blackheath. It cost me some trouble to find out Salem House; but I found it, and I found a haystack in the corner, and I lay down by it; having first walked round the wall, and looked up at the windows, and seen that all was dark and silent within. Never shall I forget the lonely sensation of first lying down, without a roof above my head!\n\nSleep came upon me as it came on many other outcasts, against whom house-doors were locked, and house-dogs barked, that night--and I dreamed of lying on my old school-bed, talking to the boys in my room; and found myself sitting upright, with Steerforth’s name upon my lips, looking wildly at the stars that were glistening and glimmering above me. When I remembered where I was at that untimely hour, a feeling stole upon me that made me get up, afraid of I don’t know what, and walk about. But the fainter glimmering of the stars, and the pale light in the sky where the day was coming, reassured me: and my eyes being very heavy, I lay down again and slept--though with a knowledge in my sleep that it was cold--until the warm beams of the sun, and the ringing of the getting-up bell at Salem House, awoke me. If I could have hoped that Steerforth was there, I would have lurked about until he came out alone; but I knew he must have left long since. Traddles still remained, perhaps, but it was very doubtful; and I had not sufficient confidence in his discretion or good luck, however strong my reliance was on his good nature, to wish to trust him with my situation. So I crept away from the wall as Mr. Creakle’s boys were getting up, and struck into the long dusty track which I had first known to be the Dover Road when I was one of them, and when I little expected that any eyes would ever see me the wayfarer I was now, upon it.\n\nWhat a different Sunday morning from the old Sunday morning at Yarmouth! In due time I heard the church-bells ringing, as I plodded on; and I met people who were going to church; and I passed a church or two where the congregation were inside, and the sound of singing came out into the sunshine, while the beadle sat and cooled himself in the shade of the porch, or stood beneath the yew-tree, with his hand to his forehead, glowering at me going by. But the peace and rest of the old Sunday morning were on everything, except me. That was the difference. I felt quite wicked in my dirt and dust, with my tangled hair. But for the quiet picture I had conjured up, of my mother in her youth and beauty, weeping by the fire, and my aunt relenting to her, I hardly think I should have had the courage to go on until next day. But it always went before me, and I followed.\n\nI got, that Sunday, through three-and-twenty miles on the straight road, though not very easily, for I was new to that kind of toil. I see myself, as evening closes in, coming over the bridge at Rochester, footsore and tired, and eating bread that I had bought for supper. One or two little houses, with the notice, ‘Lodgings for Travellers’, hanging out, had tempted me; but I was afraid of spending the few pence I had, and was even more afraid of the vicious looks of the trampers I had met or overtaken. I sought no shelter, therefore, but the sky; and toiling into Chatham,--which, in that night’s aspect, is a mere dream of chalk, and drawbridges, and mastless ships in a muddy river, roofed like Noah’s arks,--crept, at last, upon a sort of grass-grown battery overhanging a lane, where a sentry was walking to and fro. Here I lay down, near a cannon; and, happy in the society of the sentry’s footsteps, though he knew no more of my being above him than the boys at Salem House had known of my lying by the wall, slept soundly until morning.\n\nVery stiff and sore of foot I was in the morning, and quite dazed by the beating of drums and marching of troops, which seemed to hem me in on every side when I went down towards the long narrow street. Feeling that I could go but a very little way that day, if I were to reserve any strength for getting to my journey’s end, I resolved to make the sale of my jacket its principal business. Accordingly, I took the jacket off, that I might learn to do without it; and carrying it under my arm, began a tour of inspection of the various slop-shops.\n\nIt was a likely place to sell a jacket in; for the dealers in second-hand clothes were numerous, and were, generally speaking, on the look-out for customers at their shop doors. But as most of them had, hanging up among their stock, an officer’s coat or two, epaulettes and all, I was rendered timid by the costly nature of their dealings, and walked about for a long time without offering my merchandise to anyone.\n\nThis modesty of mine directed my attention to the marine-store shops, and such shops as Mr. Dolloby’s, in preference to the regular dealers. At last I found one that I thought looked promising, at the corner of a dirty lane, ending in an enclosure full of stinging-nettles, against the palings of which some second-hand sailors’ clothes, that seemed to have overflowed the shop, were fluttering among some cots, and rusty guns, and oilskin hats, and certain trays full of so many old rusty keys of so many sizes that they seemed various enough to open all the doors in the world.\n\nInto this shop, which was low and small, and which was darkened rather than lighted by a little window, overhung with clothes, and was descended into by some steps, I went with a palpitating heart; which was not relieved when an ugly old man, with the lower part of his face all covered with a stubbly grey beard, rushed out of a dirty den behind it, and seized me by the hair of my head. He was a dreadful old man to look at, in a filthy flannel waistcoat, and smelling terribly of rum. His bedstead, covered with a tumbled and ragged piece of patchwork, was in the den he had come from, where another little window showed a prospect of more stinging-nettles, and a lame donkey.\n\n‘Oh, what do you want?’ grinned this old man, in a fierce, monotonous whine. ‘Oh, my eyes and limbs, what do you want? Oh, my lungs and liver, what do you want? Oh, goroo, goroo!’\n\nI was so much dismayed by these words, and particularly by the repetition of the last unknown one, which was a kind of rattle in his throat, that I could make no answer; hereupon the old man, still holding me by the hair, repeated:\n\n‘Oh, what do you want? Oh, my eyes and limbs, what do you want? Oh, my lungs and liver, what do you want? Oh, goroo!’--which he screwed out of himself, with an energy that made his eyes start in his head.\n\n‘I wanted to know,’ I said, trembling, ‘if you would buy a jacket.’\n\n‘Oh, let’s see the jacket!’ cried the old man. ‘Oh, my heart on fire, show the jacket to us! Oh, my eyes and limbs, bring the jacket out!’\n\nWith that he took his trembling hands, which were like the claws of a great bird, out of my hair; and put on a pair of spectacles, not at all ornamental to his inflamed eyes.\n\n‘Oh, how much for the jacket?’ cried the old man, after examining it. ‘Oh--goroo!--how much for the jacket?’\n\n‘Half-a-crown,’ I answered, recovering myself.\n\n‘Oh, my lungs and liver,’ cried the old man, ‘no! Oh, my eyes, no! Oh, my limbs, no! Eighteenpence. Goroo!’\n\nEvery time he uttered this ejaculation, his eyes seemed to be in danger of starting out; and every sentence he spoke, he delivered in a sort of tune, always exactly the same, and more like a gust of wind, which begins low, mounts up high, and falls again, than any other comparison I can find for it.\n\n‘Well,’ said I, glad to have closed the bargain, ‘I’ll take eighteenpence.’\n\n‘Oh, my liver!’ cried the old man, throwing the jacket on a shelf. ‘Get out of the shop! Oh, my lungs, get out of the shop! Oh, my eyes and limbs--goroo!--don’t ask for money; make it an exchange.’ I never was so frightened in my life, before or since; but I told him humbly that I wanted money, and that nothing else was of any use to me, but that I would wait for it, as he desired, outside, and had no wish to hurry him. So I went outside, and sat down in the shade in a corner. And I sat there so many hours, that the shade became sunlight, and the sunlight became shade again, and still I sat there waiting for the money.\n\nThere never was such another drunken madman in that line of business, I hope. That he was well known in the neighbourhood, and enjoyed the reputation of having sold himself to the devil, I soon understood from the visits he received from the boys, who continually came skirmishing about the shop, shouting that legend, and calling to him to bring out his gold. ‘You ain’t poor, you know, Charley, as you pretend. Bring out your gold. Bring out some of the gold you sold yourself to the devil for. Come! It’s in the lining of the mattress, Charley. Rip it open and let’s have some!’ This, and many offers to lend him a knife for the purpose, exasperated him to such a degree, that the whole day was a succession of rushes on his part, and flights on the part of the boys. Sometimes in his rage he would take me for one of them, and come at me, mouthing as if he were going to tear me in pieces; then, remembering me, just in time, would dive into the shop, and lie upon his bed, as I thought from the sound of his voice, yelling in a frantic way, to his own windy tune, the ‘Death of Nelson’; with an Oh! before every line, and innumerable Goroos interspersed. As if this were not bad enough for me, the boys, connecting me with the establishment, on account of the patience and perseverance with which I sat outside, half-dressed, pelted me, and used me very ill all day.\n\nHe made many attempts to induce me to consent to an exchange; at one time coming out with a fishing-rod, at another with a fiddle, at another with a cocked hat, at another with a flute. But I resisted all these overtures, and sat there in desperation; each time asking him, with tears in my eyes, for my money or my jacket. At last he began to pay me in halfpence at a time; and was full two hours getting by easy stages to a shilling.\n\n‘Oh, my eyes and limbs!’ he then cried, peeping hideously out of the shop, after a long pause, ‘will you go for twopence more?’\n\n‘I can’t,’ I said; ‘I shall be starved.’\n\n‘Oh, my lungs and liver, will you go for threepence?’\n\n‘I would go for nothing, if I could,’ I said, ‘but I want the money badly.’\n\n‘Oh, go-roo!’ (it is really impossible to express how he twisted this ejaculation out of himself, as he peeped round the door-post at me, showing nothing but his crafty old head); ‘will you go for fourpence?’\n\nI was so faint and weary that I closed with this offer; and taking the money out of his claw, not without trembling, went away more hungry and thirsty than I had ever been, a little before sunset. But at an expense of threepence I soon refreshed myself completely; and, being in better spirits then, limped seven miles upon my road.\n\nMy bed at night was under another haystack, where I rested comfortably, after having washed my blistered feet in a stream, and dressed them as well as I was able, with some cool leaves. When I took the road again next morning, I found that it lay through a succession of hop-grounds and orchards. It was sufficiently late in the year for the orchards to be ruddy with ripe apples; and in a few places the hop-pickers were already at work. I thought it all extremely beautiful, and made up my mind to sleep among the hops that night: imagining some cheerful companionship in the long perspectives of poles, with the graceful leaves twining round them.\n\nThe trampers were worse than ever that day, and inspired me with a dread that is yet quite fresh in my mind. Some of them were most ferocious-looking ruffians, who stared at me as I went by; and stopped, perhaps, and called after me to come back and speak to them, and when I took to my heels, stoned me. I recollect one young fellow--a tinker, I suppose, from his wallet and brazier--who had a woman with him, and who faced about and stared at me thus; and then roared to me in such a tremendous voice to come back, that I halted and looked round.\n\n‘Come here, when you’re called,’ said the tinker, ‘or I’ll rip your young body open.’\n\nI thought it best to go back. As I drew nearer to them, trying to propitiate the tinker by my looks, I observed that the woman had a black eye.\n\n‘Where are you going?’ said the tinker, gripping the bosom of my shirt with his blackened hand.\n\n‘I am going to Dover,’ I said.\n\n‘Where do you come from?’ asked the tinker, giving his hand another turn in my shirt, to hold me more securely.\n\n‘I come from London,’ I said.\n\n‘What lay are you upon?’ asked the tinker. ‘Are you a prig?’\n\n‘N-no,’ I said.\n\n‘Ain’t you, by G--? If you make a brag of your honesty to me,’ said the tinker, ‘I’ll knock your brains out.’\n\nWith his disengaged hand he made a menace of striking me, and then looked at me from head to foot.\n\n‘Have you got the price of a pint of beer about you?’ said the tinker. ‘If you have, out with it, afore I take it away!’\n\nI should certainly have produced it, but that I met the woman’s look, and saw her very slightly shake her head, and form ‘No!’ with her lips.\n\n‘I am very poor,’ I said, attempting to smile, ‘and have got no money.’\n\n‘Why, what do you mean?’ said the tinker, looking so sternly at me, that I almost feared he saw the money in my pocket.\n\n‘Sir!’ I stammered.\n\n‘What do you mean,’ said the tinker, ‘by wearing my brother’s silk handkerchief! Give it over here!’ And he had mine off my neck in a moment, and tossed it to the woman.\n\nThe woman burst into a fit of laughter, as if she thought this a joke, and tossed it back to me, nodded once, as slightly as before, and made the word ‘Go!’ with her lips. Before I could obey, however, the tinker seized the handkerchief out of my hand with a roughness that threw me away like a feather, and putting it loosely round his own neck, turned upon the woman with an oath, and knocked her down. I never shall forget seeing her fall backward on the hard road, and lie there with her bonnet tumbled off, and her hair all whitened in the dust; nor, when I looked back from a distance, seeing her sitting on the pathway, which was a bank by the roadside, wiping the blood from her face with a corner of her shawl, while he went on ahead.\n\nThis adventure frightened me so, that, afterwards, when I saw any of these people coming, I turned back until I could find a hiding-place, where I remained until they had gone out of sight; which happened so often, that I was very seriously delayed. But under this difficulty, as under all the other difficulties of my journey, I seemed to be sustained and led on by my fanciful picture of my mother in her youth, before I came into the world. It always kept me company. It was there, among the hops, when I lay down to sleep; it was with me on my waking in the morning; it went before me all day. I have associated it, ever since, with the sunny street of Canterbury, dozing as it were in the hot light; and with the sight of its old houses and gateways, and the stately, grey Cathedral, with the rooks sailing round the towers. When I came, at last, upon the bare, wide downs near Dover, it relieved the solitary aspect of the scene with hope; and not until I reached that first great aim of my journey, and actually set foot in the town itself, on the sixth day of my flight, did it desert me. But then, strange to say, when I stood with my ragged shoes, and my dusty, sunburnt, half-clothed figure, in the place so long desired, it seemed to vanish like a dream, and to leave me helpless and dispirited.\n\nI inquired about my aunt among the boatmen first, and received various answers. One said she lived in the South Foreland Light, and had singed her whiskers by doing so; another, that she was made fast to the great buoy outside the harbour, and could only be visited at half-tide; a third, that she was locked up in Maidstone jail for child-stealing; a fourth, that she was seen to mount a broom in the last high wind, and make direct for Calais. The fly-drivers, among whom I inquired next, were equally jocose and equally disrespectful; and the shopkeepers, not liking my appearance, generally replied, without hearing what I had to say, that they had got nothing for me. I felt more miserable and destitute than I had done at any period of my running away. My money was all gone, I had nothing left to dispose of; I was hungry, thirsty, and worn out; and seemed as distant from my end as if I had remained in London.\n\nThe morning had worn away in these inquiries, and I was sitting on the step of an empty shop at a street corner, near the market-place, deliberating upon wandering towards those other places which had been mentioned, when a fly-driver, coming by with his carriage, dropped a horsecloth. Something good-natured in the man’s face, as I handed it up, encouraged me to ask him if he could tell me where Miss Trotwood lived; though I had asked the question so often, that it almost died upon my lips.\n\n‘Trotwood,’ said he. ‘Let me see. I know the name, too. Old lady?’\n\n‘Yes,’ I said, ‘rather.’\n\n‘Pretty stiff in the back?’ said he, making himself upright.\n\n‘Yes,’ I said. ‘I should think it very likely.’\n\n‘Carries a bag?’ said he--‘bag with a good deal of room in it--is gruffish, and comes down upon you, sharp?’\n\nMy heart sank within me as I acknowledged the undoubted accuracy of this description.\n\n‘Why then, I tell you what,’ said he. ‘If you go up there,’ pointing with his whip towards the heights, ‘and keep right on till you come to some houses facing the sea, I think you’ll hear of her. My opinion is she won’t stand anything, so here’s a penny for you.’\n\nI accepted the gift thankfully, and bought a loaf with it. Dispatching this refreshment by the way, I went in the direction my friend had indicated, and walked on a good distance without coming to the houses he had mentioned. At length I saw some before me; and approaching them, went into a little shop (it was what we used to call a general shop, at home), and inquired if they could have the goodness to tell me where Miss Trotwood lived. I addressed myself to a man behind the counter, who was weighing some rice for a young woman; but the latter, taking the inquiry to herself, turned round quickly.\n\n‘My mistress?’ she said. ‘What do you want with her, boy?’\n\n‘I want,’ I replied, ‘to speak to her, if you please.’\n\n‘To beg of her, you mean,’ retorted the damsel.\n\n‘No,’ I said, ‘indeed.’ But suddenly remembering that in truth I came for no other purpose, I held my peace in confusion, and felt my face burn.\n\nMy aunt’s handmaid, as I supposed she was from what she had said, put her rice in a little basket and walked out of the shop; telling me that I could follow her, if I wanted to know where Miss Trotwood lived. I needed no second permission; though I was by this time in such a state of consternation and agitation, that my legs shook under me. I followed the young woman, and we soon came to a very neat little cottage with cheerful bow-windows: in front of it, a small square gravelled court or garden full of flowers, carefully tended, and smelling deliciously.\n\n‘This is Miss Trotwood’s,’ said the young woman. ‘Now you know; and that’s all I have got to say.’ With which words she hurried into the house, as if to shake off the responsibility of my appearance; and left me standing at the garden-gate, looking disconsolately over the top of it towards the parlour window, where a muslin curtain partly undrawn in the middle, a large round green screen or fan fastened on to the windowsill, a small table, and a great chair, suggested to me that my aunt might be at that moment seated in awful state.\n\nMy shoes were by this time in a woeful condition. The soles had shed themselves bit by bit, and the upper leathers had broken and burst until the very shape and form of shoes had departed from them. My hat (which had served me for a night-cap, too) was so crushed and bent, that no old battered handleless saucepan on a dunghill need have been ashamed to vie with it. My shirt and trousers, stained with heat, dew, grass, and the Kentish soil on which I had slept--and torn besides--might have frightened the birds from my aunt’s garden, as I stood at the gate. My hair had known no comb or brush since I left London. My face, neck, and hands, from unaccustomed exposure to the air and sun, were burnt to a berry-brown. From head to foot I was powdered almost as white with chalk and dust, as if I had come out of a lime-kiln. In this plight, and with a strong consciousness of it, I waited to introduce myself to, and make my first impression on, my formidable aunt.\n\nThe unbroken stillness of the parlour window leading me to infer, after a while, that she was not there, I lifted up my eyes to the window above it, where I saw a florid, pleasant-looking gentleman, with a grey head, who shut up one eye in a grotesque manner, nodded his head at me several times, shook it at me as often, laughed, and went away.\n\nI had been discomposed enough before; but I was so much the more discomposed by this unexpected behaviour, that I was on the point of slinking off, to think how I had best proceed, when there came out of the house a lady with her handkerchief tied over her cap, and a pair of gardening gloves on her hands, wearing a gardening pocket like a toll-man’s apron, and carrying a great knife. I knew her immediately to be Miss Betsey, for she came stalking out of the house exactly as my poor mother had so often described her stalking up our garden at Blunderstone Rookery.\n\n‘Go away!’ said Miss Betsey, shaking her head, and making a distant chop in the air with her knife. ‘Go along! No boys here!’\n\nI watched her, with my heart at my lips, as she marched to a corner of her garden, and stooped to dig up some little root there. Then, without a scrap of courage, but with a great deal of desperation, I went softly in and stood beside her, touching her with my finger.\n\n‘If you please, ma’am,’ I began.\n\nShe started and looked up.\n\n‘If you please, aunt.’\n\n‘EH?’ exclaimed Miss Betsey, in a tone of amazement I have never heard approached.\n\n‘If you please, aunt, I am your nephew.’\n\n‘Oh, Lord!’ said my aunt. And sat flat down in the garden-path.\n\n‘I am David Copperfield, of Blunderstone, in Suffolk--where you came, on the night when I was born, and saw my dear mama. I have been very unhappy since she died. I have been slighted, and taught nothing, and thrown upon myself, and put to work not fit for me. It made me run away to you. I was robbed at first setting out, and have walked all the way, and have never slept in a bed since I began the journey.’ Here my self-support gave way all at once; and with a movement of my hands, intended to show her my ragged state, and call it to witness that I had suffered something, I broke into a passion of crying, which I suppose had been pent up within me all the week.\n\nMy aunt, with every sort of expression but wonder discharged from her countenance, sat on the gravel, staring at me, until I began to cry; when she got up in a great hurry, collared me, and took me into the parlour. Her first proceeding there was to unlock a tall press, bring out several bottles, and pour some of the contents of each into my mouth. I think they must have been taken out at random, for I am sure I tasted aniseed water, anchovy sauce, and salad dressing. When she had administered these restoratives, as I was still quite hysterical, and unable to control my sobs, she put me on the sofa, with a shawl under my head, and the handkerchief from her own head under my feet, lest I should sully the cover; and then, sitting herself down behind the green fan or screen I have already mentioned, so that I could not see her face, ejaculated at intervals, ‘Mercy on us!’ letting those exclamations off like minute guns.\n\nAfter a time she rang the bell. ‘Janet,’ said my aunt, when her servant came in. ‘Go upstairs, give my compliments to Mr. Dick, and say I wish to speak to him.’\n\nJanet looked a little surprised to see me lying stiffly on the sofa (I was afraid to move lest it should be displeasing to my aunt), but went on her errand. My aunt, with her hands behind her, walked up and down the room, until the gentleman who had squinted at me from the upper window came in laughing.\n\n‘Mr. Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘don’t be a fool, because nobody can be more discreet than you can, when you choose. We all know that. So don’t be a fool, whatever you are.’\n\nThe gentleman was serious immediately, and looked at me, I thought, as if he would entreat me to say nothing about the window.\n\n‘Mr. Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘you have heard me mention David Copperfield? Now don’t pretend not to have a memory, because you and I know better.’\n\n‘David Copperfield?’ said Mr. Dick, who did not appear to me to remember much about it. ‘David Copperfield? Oh yes, to be sure. David, certainly.’\n\n‘Well,’ said my aunt, ‘this is his boy--his son. He would be as like his father as it’s possible to be, if he was not so like his mother, too.’\n\n‘His son?’ said Mr. Dick. ‘David’s son? Indeed!’\n\n‘Yes,’ pursued my aunt, ‘and he has done a pretty piece of business. He has run away. Ah! His sister, Betsey Trotwood, never would have run away.’ My aunt shook her head firmly, confident in the character and behaviour of the girl who never was born.\n\n‘Oh! you think she wouldn’t have run away?’ said Mr. Dick.\n\n‘Bless and save the man,’ exclaimed my aunt, sharply, ‘how he talks! Don’t I know she wouldn’t? She would have lived with her god-mother, and we should have been devoted to one another. Where, in the name of wonder, should his sister, Betsey Trotwood, have run from, or to?’\n\n‘Nowhere,’ said Mr. Dick.\n\n‘Well then,’ returned my aunt, softened by the reply, ‘how can you pretend to be wool-gathering, Dick, when you are as sharp as a surgeon’s lancet? Now, here you see young David Copperfield, and the question I put to you is, what shall I do with him?’\n\n‘What shall you do with him?’ said Mr. Dick, feebly, scratching his head. ‘Oh! do with him?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said my aunt, with a grave look, and her forefinger held up. ‘Come! I want some very sound advice.’\n\n‘Why, if I was you,’ said Mr. Dick, considering, and looking vacantly at me, ‘I should--’ The contemplation of me seemed to inspire him with a sudden idea, and he added, briskly, ‘I should wash him!’\n\n‘Janet,’ said my aunt, turning round with a quiet triumph, which I did not then understand, ‘Mr. Dick sets us all right. Heat the bath!’\n\nAlthough I was deeply interested in this dialogue, I could not help observing my aunt, Mr. Dick, and Janet, while it was in progress, and completing a survey I had already been engaged in making of the room.\n\nMy aunt was a tall, hard-featured lady, but by no means ill-looking. There was an inflexibility in her face, in her voice, in her gait and carriage, amply sufficient to account for the effect she had made upon a gentle creature like my mother; but her features were rather handsome than otherwise, though unbending and austere. I particularly noticed that she had a very quick, bright eye. Her hair, which was grey, was arranged in two plain divisions, under what I believe would be called a mob-cap; I mean a cap, much more common then than now, with side-pieces fastening under the chin. Her dress was of a lavender colour, and perfectly neat; but scantily made, as if she desired to be as little encumbered as possible. I remember that I thought it, in form, more like a riding-habit with the superfluous skirt cut off, than anything else. She wore at her side a gentleman’s gold watch, if I might judge from its size and make, with an appropriate chain and seals; she had some linen at her throat not unlike a shirt-collar, and things at her wrists like little shirt-wristbands.\n\nMr. Dick, as I have already said, was grey-headed, and florid: I should have said all about him, in saying so, had not his head been curiously bowed--not by age; it reminded me of one of Mr. Creakle’s boys’ heads after a beating--and his grey eyes prominent and large, with a strange kind of watery brightness in them that made me, in combination with his vacant manner, his submission to my aunt, and his childish delight when she praised him, suspect him of being a little mad; though, if he were mad, how he came to be there puzzled me extremely. He was dressed like any other ordinary gentleman, in a loose grey morning coat and waistcoat, and white trousers; and had his watch in his fob, and his money in his pockets: which he rattled as if he were very proud of it.\n\nJanet was a pretty blooming girl, of about nineteen or twenty, and a perfect picture of neatness. Though I made no further observation of her at the moment, I may mention here what I did not discover until afterwards, namely, that she was one of a series of protegees whom my aunt had taken into her service expressly to educate in a renouncement of mankind, and who had generally completed their abjuration by marrying the baker.\n\nThe room was as neat as Janet or my aunt. As I laid down my pen, a moment since, to think of it, the air from the sea came blowing in again, mixed with the perfume of the flowers; and I saw the old-fashioned furniture brightly rubbed and polished, my aunt’s inviolable chair and table by the round green fan in the bow-window, the drugget-covered carpet, the cat, the kettle-holder, the two canaries, the old china, the punchbowl full of dried rose-leaves, the tall press guarding all sorts of bottles and pots, and, wonderfully out of keeping with the rest, my dusty self upon the sofa, taking note of everything.\n\nJanet had gone away to get the bath ready, when my aunt, to my great alarm, became in one moment rigid with indignation, and had hardly voice to cry out, ‘Janet! Donkeys!’\n\nUpon which, Janet came running up the stairs as if the house were in flames, darted out on a little piece of green in front, and warned off two saddle-donkeys, lady-ridden, that had presumed to set hoof upon it; while my aunt, rushing out of the house, seized the bridle of a third animal laden with a bestriding child, turned him, led him forth from those sacred precincts, and boxed the ears of the unlucky urchin in attendance who had dared to profane that hallowed ground.\n\nTo this hour I don’t know whether my aunt had any lawful right of way over that patch of green; but she had settled it in her own mind that she had, and it was all the same to her. The one great outrage of her life, demanding to be constantly avenged, was the passage of a donkey over that immaculate spot. In whatever occupation she was engaged, however interesting to her the conversation in which she was taking part, a donkey turned the current of her ideas in a moment, and she was upon him straight. Jugs of water, and watering-pots, were kept in secret places ready to be discharged on the offending boys; sticks were laid in ambush behind the door; sallies were made at all hours; and incessant war prevailed. Perhaps this was an agreeable excitement to the donkey-boys; or perhaps the more sagacious of the donkeys, understanding how the case stood, delighted with constitutional obstinacy in coming that way. I only know that there were three alarms before the bath was ready; and that on the occasion of the last and most desperate of all, I saw my aunt engage, single-handed, with a sandy-headed lad of fifteen, and bump his sandy head against her own gate, before he seemed to comprehend what was the matter. These interruptions were of the more ridiculous to me, because she was giving me broth out of a table-spoon at the time (having firmly persuaded herself that I was actually starving, and must receive nourishment at first in very small quantities), and, while my mouth was yet open to receive the spoon, she would put it back into the basin, cry ‘Janet! Donkeys!’ and go out to the assault.\n\nThe bath was a great comfort. For I began to be sensible of acute pains in my limbs from lying out in the fields, and was now so tired and low that I could hardly keep myself awake for five minutes together. When I had bathed, they (I mean my aunt and Janet) enrobed me in a shirt and a pair of trousers belonging to Mr. Dick, and tied me up in two or three great shawls. What sort of bundle I looked like, I don’t know, but I felt a very hot one. Feeling also very faint and drowsy, I soon lay down on the sofa again and fell asleep.\n\nIt might have been a dream, originating in the fancy which had occupied my mind so long, but I awoke with the impression that my aunt had come and bent over me, and had put my hair away from my face, and laid my head more comfortably, and had then stood looking at me. The words, ‘Pretty fellow,’ or ‘Poor fellow,’ seemed to be in my ears, too; but certainly there was nothing else, when I awoke, to lead me to believe that they had been uttered by my aunt, who sat in the bow-window gazing at the sea from behind the green fan, which was mounted on a kind of swivel, and turned any way.\n\nWe dined soon after I awoke, off a roast fowl and a pudding; I sitting at table, not unlike a trussed bird myself, and moving my arms with considerable difficulty. But as my aunt had swathed me up, I made no complaint of being inconvenienced. All this time I was deeply anxious to know what she was going to do with me; but she took her dinner in profound silence, except when she occasionally fixed her eyes on me sitting opposite, and said, ‘Mercy upon us!’ which did not by any means relieve my anxiety.\n\nThe cloth being drawn, and some sherry put upon the table (of which I had a glass), my aunt sent up for Mr. Dick again, who joined us, and looked as wise as he could when she requested him to attend to my story, which she elicited from me, gradually, by a course of questions. During my recital, she kept her eyes on Mr. Dick, who I thought would have gone to sleep but for that, and who, whensoever he lapsed into a smile, was checked by a frown from my aunt.\n\n‘Whatever possessed that poor unfortunate Baby, that she must go and be married again,’ said my aunt, when I had finished, ‘I can’t conceive.’\n\n‘Perhaps she fell in love with her second husband,’ Mr. Dick suggested.\n\n‘Fell in love!’ repeated my aunt. ‘What do you mean? What business had she to do it?’\n\n‘Perhaps,’ Mr. Dick simpered, after thinking a little, ‘she did it for pleasure.’\n\n‘Pleasure, indeed!’ replied my aunt. ‘A mighty pleasure for the poor Baby to fix her simple faith upon any dog of a fellow, certain to ill-use her in some way or other. What did she propose to herself, I should like to know! She had had one husband. She had seen David Copperfield out of the world, who was always running after wax dolls from his cradle. She had got a baby--oh, there were a pair of babies when she gave birth to this child sitting here, that Friday night!--and what more did she want?’\n\nMr. Dick secretly shook his head at me, as if he thought there was no getting over this.\n\n‘She couldn’t even have a baby like anybody else,’ said my aunt. ‘Where was this child’s sister, Betsey Trotwood? Not forthcoming. Don’t tell me!’\n\nMr. Dick seemed quite frightened.\n\n‘That little man of a doctor, with his head on one side,’ said my aunt, ‘Jellips, or whatever his name was, what was he about? All he could do, was to say to me, like a robin redbreast--as he is--“It’s a boy.” A boy! Yah, the imbecility of the whole set of ‘em!’\n\nThe heartiness of the ejaculation startled Mr. Dick exceedingly; and me, too, if I am to tell the truth.\n\n‘And then, as if this was not enough, and she had not stood sufficiently in the light of this child’s sister, Betsey Trotwood,’ said my aunt, ‘she marries a second time--goes and marries a Murderer--or a man with a name like it--and stands in THIS child’s light! And the natural consequence is, as anybody but a baby might have foreseen, that he prowls and wanders. He’s as like Cain before he was grown up, as he can be.’\n\nMr. Dick looked hard at me, as if to identify me in this character.\n\n‘And then there’s that woman with the Pagan name,’ said my aunt, ‘that Peggotty, she goes and gets married next. Because she has not seen enough of the evil attending such things, she goes and gets married next, as the child relates. I only hope,’ said my aunt, shaking her head, ‘that her husband is one of those Poker husbands who abound in the newspapers, and will beat her well with one.’\n\nI could not bear to hear my old nurse so decried, and made the subject of such a wish. I told my aunt that indeed she was mistaken. That Peggotty was the best, the truest, the most faithful, most devoted, and most self-denying friend and servant in the world; who had ever loved me dearly, who had ever loved my mother dearly; who had held my mother’s dying head upon her arm, on whose face my mother had imprinted her last grateful kiss. And my remembrance of them both, choking me, I broke down as I was trying to say that her home was my home, and that all she had was mine, and that I would have gone to her for shelter, but for her humble station, which made me fear that I might bring some trouble on her--I broke down, I say, as I was trying to say so, and laid my face in my hands upon the table.\n\n‘Well, well!’ said my aunt, ‘the child is right to stand by those who have stood by him--Janet! Donkeys!’\n\nI thoroughly believe that but for those unfortunate donkeys, we should have come to a good understanding; for my aunt had laid her hand on my shoulder, and the impulse was upon me, thus emboldened, to embrace her and beseech her protection. But the interruption, and the disorder she was thrown into by the struggle outside, put an end to all softer ideas for the present, and kept my aunt indignantly declaiming to Mr. Dick about her determination to appeal for redress to the laws of her country, and to bring actions for trespass against the whole donkey proprietorship of Dover, until tea-time.\n\nAfter tea, we sat at the window--on the look-out, as I imagined, from my aunt’s sharp expression of face, for more invaders--until dusk, when Janet set candles, and a backgammon-board, on the table, and pulled down the blinds.\n\n‘Now, Mr. Dick,’ said my aunt, with her grave look, and her forefinger up as before, ‘I am going to ask you another question. Look at this child.’\n\n‘David’s son?’ said Mr. Dick, with an attentive, puzzled face.\n\n‘Exactly so,’ returned my aunt. ‘What would you do with him, now?’\n\n‘Do with David’s son?’ said Mr. Dick.\n\n‘Ay,’ replied my aunt, ‘with David’s son.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Mr. Dick. ‘Yes. Do with--I should put him to bed.’\n\n‘Janet!’ cried my aunt, with the same complacent triumph that I had remarked before. ‘Mr. Dick sets us all right. If the bed is ready, we’ll take him up to it.’\n\nJanet reporting it to be quite ready, I was taken up to it; kindly, but in some sort like a prisoner; my aunt going in front and Janet bringing up the rear. The only circumstance which gave me any new hope, was my aunt’s stopping on the stairs to inquire about a smell of fire that was prevalent there; and janet’s replying that she had been making tinder down in the kitchen, of my old shirt. But there were no other clothes in my room than the odd heap of things I wore; and when I was left there, with a little taper which my aunt forewarned me would burn exactly five minutes, I heard them lock my door on the outside. Turning these things over in my mind I deemed it possible that my aunt, who could know nothing of me, might suspect I had a habit of running away, and took precautions, on that account, to have me in safe keeping.\n\nThe room was a pleasant one, at the top of the house, overlooking the sea, on which the moon was shining brilliantly. After I had said my prayers, and the candle had burnt out, I remember how I still sat looking at the moonlight on the water, as if I could hope to read my fortune in it, as in a bright book; or to see my mother with her child, coming from Heaven, along that shining path, to look upon me as she had looked when I last saw her sweet face. I remember how the solemn feeling with which at length I turned my eyes away, yielded to the sensation of gratitude and rest which the sight of the white-curtained bed--and how much more the lying softly down upon it, nestling in the snow-white sheets!--inspired. I remember how I thought of all the solitary places under the night sky where I had slept, and how I prayed that I never might be houseless any more, and never might forget the houseless. I remember how I seemed to float, then, down the melancholy glory of that track upon the sea, away into the world of dreams.\n\n# CHAPTER 14. MY AUNT MAKES UP HER MIND ABOUT ME\n\nOn going down in the morning, I found my aunt musing so profoundly over the breakfast table, with her elbow on the tray, that the contents of the urn had overflowed the teapot and were laying the whole table-cloth under water, when my entrance put her meditations to flight. I felt sure that I had been the subject of her reflections, and was more than ever anxious to know her intentions towards me. Yet I dared not express my anxiety, lest it should give her offence.\n\nMy eyes, however, not being so much under control as my tongue, were attracted towards my aunt very often during breakfast. I never could look at her for a few moments together but I found her looking at me--in an odd thoughtful manner, as if I were an immense way off, instead of being on the other side of the small round table. When she had finished her breakfast, my aunt very deliberately leaned back in her chair, knitted her brows, folded her arms, and contemplated me at her leisure, with such a fixedness of attention that I was quite overpowered by embarrassment. Not having as yet finished my own breakfast, I attempted to hide my confusion by proceeding with it; but my knife tumbled over my fork, my fork tripped up my knife, I chipped bits of bacon a surprising height into the air instead of cutting them for my own eating, and choked myself with my tea, which persisted in going the wrong way instead of the right one, until I gave in altogether, and sat blushing under my aunt’s close scrutiny.\n\n‘Hallo!’ said my aunt, after a long time.\n\nI looked up, and met her sharp bright glance respectfully.\n\n‘I have written to him,’ said my aunt.\n‘To--?’\n\n‘To your father-in-law,’ said my aunt. ‘I have sent him a letter that I’ll trouble him to attend to, or he and I will fall out, I can tell him!’\n\n‘Does he know where I am, aunt?’ I inquired, alarmed.\n\n‘I have told him,’ said my aunt, with a nod.\n\n‘Shall I--be--given up to him?’ I faltered.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said my aunt. ‘We shall see.’\n\n‘Oh! I can’t think what I shall do,’ I exclaimed, ‘if I have to go back to Mr. Murdstone!’\n\n‘I don’t know anything about it,’ said my aunt, shaking her head. ‘I can’t say, I am sure. We shall see.’\n\nMy spirits sank under these words, and I became very downcast and heavy of heart. My aunt, without appearing to take much heed of me, put on a coarse apron with a bib, which she took out of the press; washed up the teacups with her own hands; and, when everything was washed and set in the tray again, and the cloth folded and put on the top of the whole, rang for Janet to remove it. She next swept up the crumbs with a little broom (putting on a pair of gloves first), until there did not appear to be one microscopic speck left on the carpet; next dusted and arranged the room, which was dusted and arranged to a hair’s breadth already. When all these tasks were performed to her satisfaction, she took off the gloves and apron, folded them up, put them in the particular corner of the press from which they had been taken, brought out her work-box to her own table in the open window, and sat down, with the green fan between her and the light, to work.\n\n‘I wish you’d go upstairs,’ said my aunt, as she threaded her needle, ‘and give my compliments to Mr. Dick, and I’ll be glad to know how he gets on with his Memorial.’\n\nI rose with all alacrity, to acquit myself of this commission.\n\n‘I suppose,’ said my aunt, eyeing me as narrowly as she had eyed the needle in threading it, ‘you think Mr. Dick a short name, eh?’\n\n‘I thought it was rather a short name, yesterday,’ I confessed.\n\n‘You are not to suppose that he hasn’t got a longer name, if he chose to use it,’ said my aunt, with a loftier air. ‘Babley--Mr. Richard Babley--that’s the gentleman’s true name.’\n\nI was going to suggest, with a modest sense of my youth and the familiarity I had been already guilty of, that I had better give him the full benefit of that name, when my aunt went on to say:\n\n‘But don’t you call him by it, whatever you do. He can’t bear his name. That’s a peculiarity of his. Though I don’t know that it’s much of a peculiarity, either; for he has been ill-used enough, by some that bear it, to have a mortal antipathy for it, Heaven knows. Mr. Dick is his name here, and everywhere else, now--if he ever went anywhere else, which he don’t. So take care, child, you don’t call him anything BUT Mr. Dick.’\n\nI promised to obey, and went upstairs with my message; thinking, as I went, that if Mr. Dick had been working at his Memorial long, at the same rate as I had seen him working at it, through the open door, when I came down, he was probably getting on very well indeed. I found him still driving at it with a long pen, and his head almost laid upon the paper. He was so intent upon it, that I had ample leisure to observe the large paper kite in a corner, the confusion of bundles of manuscript, the number of pens, and, above all, the quantity of ink (which he seemed to have in, in half-gallon jars by the dozen), before he observed my being present.\n\n‘Ha! Phoebus!’ said Mr. Dick, laying down his pen. ‘How does the world go? I’ll tell you what,’ he added, in a lower tone, ‘I shouldn’t wish it to be mentioned, but it’s a--’ here he beckoned to me, and put his lips close to my ear--‘it’s a mad world. Mad as Bedlam, boy!’ said Mr. Dick, taking snuff from a round box on the table, and laughing heartily.\n\nWithout presuming to give my opinion on this question, I delivered my message.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr. Dick, in answer, ‘my compliments to her, and I--I believe I have made a start. I think I have made a start,’ said Mr. Dick, passing his hand among his grey hair, and casting anything but a confident look at his manuscript. ‘You have been to school?’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ I answered; ‘for a short time.’\n\n‘Do you recollect the date,’ said Mr. Dick, looking earnestly at me, and taking up his pen to note it down, ‘when King Charles the First had his head cut off?’ I said I believed it happened in the year sixteen hundred and forty-nine.\n\n‘Well,’ returned Mr. Dick, scratching his ear with his pen, and looking dubiously at me. ‘So the books say; but I don’t see how that can be. Because, if it was so long ago, how could the people about him have made that mistake of putting some of the trouble out of his head, after it was taken off, into mine?’\n\nI was very much surprised by the inquiry; but could give no information on this point.\n\n‘It’s very strange,’ said Mr. Dick, with a despondent look upon his papers, and with his hand among his hair again, ‘that I never can get that quite right. I never can make that perfectly clear. But no matter, no matter!’ he said cheerfully, and rousing himself, ‘there’s time enough! My compliments to Miss Trotwood, I am getting on very well indeed.’\n\nI was going away, when he directed my attention to the kite.\n\n‘What do you think of that for a kite?’ he said.\n\nI answered that it was a beautiful one. I should think it must have been as much as seven feet high.\n\n‘I made it. We’ll go and fly it, you and I,’ said Mr. Dick. ‘Do you see this?’\n\nHe showed me that it was covered with manuscript, very closely and laboriously written; but so plainly, that as I looked along the lines, I thought I saw some allusion to King Charles the First’s head again, in one or two places.\n\n‘There’s plenty of string,’ said Mr. Dick, ‘and when it flies high, it takes the facts a long way. That’s my manner of diffusing ‘em. I don’t know where they may come down. It’s according to circumstances, and the wind, and so forth; but I take my chance of that.’\n\nHis face was so very mild and pleasant, and had something so reverend in it, though it was hale and hearty, that I was not sure but that he was having a good-humoured jest with me. So I laughed, and he laughed, and we parted the best friends possible.\n\n‘Well, child,’ said my aunt, when I went downstairs. ‘And what of Mr. Dick, this morning?’\n\nI informed her that he sent his compliments, and was getting on very well indeed.\n\n‘What do you think of him?’ said my aunt.\n\nI had some shadowy idea of endeavouring to evade the question, by replying that I thought him a very nice gentleman; but my aunt was not to be so put off, for she laid her work down in her lap, and said, folding her hands upon it:\n\n‘Come! Your sister Betsey Trotwood would have told me what she thought of anyone, directly. Be as like your sister as you can, and speak out!’\n\n‘Is he--is Mr. Dick--I ask because I don’t know, aunt--is he at all out of his mind, then?’ I stammered; for I felt I was on dangerous ground.\n\n‘Not a morsel,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ I observed faintly.\n\n‘If there is anything in the world,’ said my aunt, with great decision and force of manner, ‘that Mr. Dick is not, it’s that.’\n\nI had nothing better to offer, than another timid, ‘Oh, indeed!’\n\n‘He has been CALLED mad,’ said my aunt. ‘I have a selfish pleasure in saying he has been called mad, or I should not have had the benefit of his society and advice for these last ten years and upwards--in fact, ever since your sister, Betsey Trotwood, disappointed me.’\n\n‘So long as that?’ I said.\n\n‘And nice people they were, who had the audacity to call him mad,’ pursued my aunt. ‘Mr. Dick is a sort of distant connexion of mine--it doesn’t matter how; I needn’t enter into that. If it hadn’t been for me, his own brother would have shut him up for life. That’s all.’\n\nI am afraid it was hypocritical in me, but seeing that my aunt felt strongly on the subject, I tried to look as if I felt strongly too.\n\n‘A proud fool!’ said my aunt. ‘Because his brother was a little eccentric--though he is not half so eccentric as a good many people--he didn’t like to have him visible about his house, and sent him away to some private asylum-place: though he had been left to his particular care by their deceased father, who thought him almost a natural. And a wise man he must have been to think so! Mad himself, no doubt.’\n\nAgain, as my aunt looked quite convinced, I endeavoured to look quite convinced also.\n\n‘So I stepped in,’ said my aunt, ‘and made him an offer. I said, “Your brother’s sane--a great deal more sane than you are, or ever will be, it is to be hoped. Let him have his little income, and come and live with me. I am not afraid of him, I am not proud, I am ready to take care of him, and shall not ill-treat him as some people (besides the asylum-folks) have done.” After a good deal of squabbling,’ said my aunt, ‘I got him; and he has been here ever since. He is the most friendly and amenable creature in existence; and as for advice!--But nobody knows what that man’s mind is, except myself.’\n\nMy aunt smoothed her dress and shook her head, as if she smoothed defiance of the whole world out of the one, and shook it out of the other.\n\n‘He had a favourite sister,’ said my aunt, ‘a good creature, and very kind to him. But she did what they all do--took a husband. And HE did what they all do--made her wretched. It had such an effect upon the mind of Mr. Dick (that’s not madness, I hope!) that, combined with his fear of his brother, and his sense of his unkindness, it threw him into a fever. That was before he came to me, but the recollection of it is oppressive to him even now. Did he say anything to you about King Charles the First, child?’\n\n‘Yes, aunt.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose as if she were a little vexed. ‘That’s his allegorical way of expressing it. He connects his illness with great disturbance and agitation, naturally, and that’s the figure, or the simile, or whatever it’s called, which he chooses to use. And why shouldn’t he, if he thinks proper!’\n\nI said: ‘Certainly, aunt.’\n\n‘It’s not a business-like way of speaking,’ said my aunt, ‘nor a worldly way. I am aware of that; and that’s the reason why I insist upon it, that there shan’t be a word about it in his Memorial.’\n\n‘Is it a Memorial about his own history that he is writing, aunt?’\n\n‘Yes, child,’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose again. ‘He is memorializing the Lord Chancellor, or the Lord Somebody or other--one of those people, at all events, who are paid to be memorialized--about his affairs. I suppose it will go in, one of these days. He hasn’t been able to draw it up yet, without introducing that mode of expressing himself; but it don’t signify; it keeps him employed.’\n\nIn fact, I found out afterwards that Mr. Dick had been for upwards of ten years endeavouring to keep King Charles the First out of the Memorial; but he had been constantly getting into it, and was there now.\n\n‘I say again,’ said my aunt, ‘nobody knows what that man’s mind is except myself; and he’s the most amenable and friendly creature in existence. If he likes to fly a kite sometimes, what of that! Franklin used to fly a kite. He was a Quaker, or something of that sort, if I am not mistaken. And a Quaker flying a kite is a much more ridiculous object than anybody else.’\n\nIf I could have supposed that my aunt had recounted these particulars for my especial behoof, and as a piece of confidence in me, I should have felt very much distinguished, and should have augured favourably from such a mark of her good opinion. But I could hardly help observing that she had launched into them, chiefly because the question was raised in her own mind, and with very little reference to me, though she had addressed herself to me in the absence of anybody else.\n\nAt the same time, I must say that the generosity of her championship of poor harmless Mr. Dick, not only inspired my young breast with some selfish hope for myself, but warmed it unselfishly towards her. I believe that I began to know that there was something about my aunt, notwithstanding her many eccentricities and odd humours, to be honoured and trusted in. Though she was just as sharp that day as on the day before, and was in and out about the donkeys just as often, and was thrown into a tremendous state of indignation, when a young man, going by, ogled Janet at a window (which was one of the gravest misdemeanours that could be committed against my aunt’s dignity), she seemed to me to command more of my respect, if not less of my fear.\n\nThe anxiety I underwent, in the interval which necessarily elapsed before a reply could be received to her letter to Mr. Murdstone, was extreme; but I made an endeavour to suppress it, and to be as agreeable as I could in a quiet way, both to my aunt and Mr. Dick. The latter and I would have gone out to fly the great kite; but that I had still no other clothes than the anything but ornamental garments with which I had been decorated on the first day, and which confined me to the house, except for an hour after dark, when my aunt, for my health’s sake, paraded me up and down on the cliff outside, before going to bed. At length the reply from Mr. Murdstone came, and my aunt informed me, to my infinite terror, that he was coming to speak to her herself on the next day. On the next day, still bundled up in my curious habiliments, I sat counting the time, flushed and heated by the conflict of sinking hopes and rising fears within me; and waiting to be startled by the sight of the gloomy face, whose non-arrival startled me every minute.\n\nMy aunt was a little more imperious and stern than usual, but I observed no other token of her preparing herself to receive the visitor so much dreaded by me. She sat at work in the window, and I sat by, with my thoughts running astray on all possible and impossible results of Mr. Murdstone’s visit, until pretty late in the afternoon. Our dinner had been indefinitely postponed; but it was growing so late, that my aunt had ordered it to be got ready, when she gave a sudden alarm of donkeys, and to my consternation and amazement, I beheld Miss Murdstone, on a side-saddle, ride deliberately over the sacred piece of green, and stop in front of the house, looking about her.\n\n‘Go along with you!’ cried my aunt, shaking her head and her fist at the window. ‘You have no business there. How dare you trespass? Go along! Oh! you bold-faced thing!’\n\nMy aunt was so exasperated by the coolness with which Miss Murdstone looked about her, that I really believe she was motionless, and unable for the moment to dart out according to custom. I seized the opportunity to inform her who it was; and that the gentleman now coming near the offender (for the way up was very steep, and he had dropped behind), was Mr. Murdstone himself.\n\n‘I don’t care who it is!’ cried my aunt, still shaking her head and gesticulating anything but welcome from the bow-window. ‘I won’t be trespassed upon. I won’t allow it. Go away! Janet, turn him round. Lead him off!’ and I saw, from behind my aunt, a sort of hurried battle-piece, in which the donkey stood resisting everybody, with all his four legs planted different ways, while Janet tried to pull him round by the bridle, Mr. Murdstone tried to lead him on, Miss Murdstone struck at Janet with a parasol, and several boys, who had come to see the engagement, shouted vigorously. But my aunt, suddenly descrying among them the young malefactor who was the donkey’s guardian, and who was one of the most inveterate offenders against her, though hardly in his teens, rushed out to the scene of action, pounced upon him, captured him, dragged him, with his jacket over his head, and his heels grinding the ground, into the garden, and, calling upon Janet to fetch the constables and justices, that he might be taken, tried, and executed on the spot, held him at bay there. This part of the business, however, did not last long; for the young rascal, being expert at a variety of feints and dodges, of which my aunt had no conception, soon went whooping away, leaving some deep impressions of his nailed boots in the flower-beds, and taking his donkey in triumph with him.\n\nMiss Murdstone, during the latter portion of the contest, had dismounted, and was now waiting with her brother at the bottom of the steps, until my aunt should be at leisure to receive them. My aunt, a little ruffled by the combat, marched past them into the house, with great dignity, and took no notice of their presence, until they were announced by Janet.\n\n‘Shall I go away, aunt?’ I asked, trembling.\n\n‘No, sir,’ said my aunt. ‘Certainly not!’ With which she pushed me into a corner near her, and fenced Me in with a chair, as if it were a prison or a bar of justice. This position I continued to occupy during the whole interview, and from it I now saw Mr. and Miss Murdstone enter the room.\n\n‘Oh!’ said my aunt, ‘I was not aware at first to whom I had the pleasure of objecting. But I don’t allow anybody to ride over that turf. I make no exceptions. I don’t allow anybody to do it.’\n\n‘Your regulation is rather awkward to strangers,’ said Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘Is it!’ said my aunt.\n\nMr. Murdstone seemed afraid of a renewal of hostilities, and interposing began:\n\n‘Miss Trotwood!’\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ observed my aunt with a keen look. ‘You are the Mr. Murdstone who married the widow of my late nephew, David Copperfield, of Blunderstone Rookery!--Though why Rookery, I don’t know!’\n\n‘I am,’ said Mr. Murdstone.\n\n‘You’ll excuse my saying, sir,’ returned my aunt, ‘that I think it would have been a much better and happier thing if you had left that poor child alone.’\n\n‘I so far agree with what Miss Trotwood has remarked,’ observed Miss Murdstone, bridling, ‘that I consider our lamented Clara to have been, in all essential respects, a mere child.’\n\n‘It is a comfort to you and me, ma’am,’ said my aunt, ‘who are getting on in life, and are not likely to be made unhappy by our personal attractions, that nobody can say the same of us.’\n\n‘No doubt!’ returned Miss Murdstone, though, I thought, not with a very ready or gracious assent. ‘And it certainly might have been, as you say, a better and happier thing for my brother if he had never entered into such a marriage. I have always been of that opinion.’\n\n‘I have no doubt you have,’ said my aunt. ‘Janet,’ ringing the bell, ‘my compliments to Mr. Dick, and beg him to come down.’\n\nUntil he came, my aunt sat perfectly upright and stiff, frowning at the wall. When he came, my aunt performed the ceremony of introduction.\n\n‘Mr. Dick. An old and intimate friend. On whose judgement,’ said my aunt, with emphasis, as an admonition to Mr. Dick, who was biting his forefinger and looking rather foolish, ‘I rely.’\n\nMr. Dick took his finger out of his mouth, on this hint, and stood among the group, with a grave and attentive expression of face.\n\nMy aunt inclined her head to Mr. Murdstone, who went on:\n\n‘Miss Trotwood: on the receipt of your letter, I considered it an act of greater justice to myself, and perhaps of more respect to you--’\n\n‘Thank you,’ said my aunt, still eyeing him keenly. ‘You needn’t mind me.’\n\n‘To answer it in person, however inconvenient the journey,’ pursued Mr. Murdstone, ‘rather than by letter. This unhappy boy who has run away from his friends and his occupation--’\n\n‘And whose appearance,’ interposed his sister, directing general attention to me in my indefinable costume, ‘is perfectly scandalous and disgraceful.’\n\n‘Jane Murdstone,’ said her brother, ‘have the goodness not to interrupt me. This unhappy boy, Miss Trotwood, has been the occasion of much domestic trouble and uneasiness; both during the lifetime of my late dear wife, and since. He has a sullen, rebellious spirit; a violent temper; and an untoward, intractable disposition. Both my sister and myself have endeavoured to correct his vices, but ineffectually. And I have felt--we both have felt, I may say; my sister being fully in my confidence--that it is right you should receive this grave and dispassionate assurance from our lips.’\n\n‘It can hardly be necessary for me to confirm anything stated by my brother,’ said Miss Murdstone; ‘but I beg to observe, that, of all the boys in the world, I believe this is the worst boy.’\n\n‘Strong!’ said my aunt, shortly.\n\n‘But not at all too strong for the facts,’ returned Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘Ha!’ said my aunt. ‘Well, sir?’\n\n‘I have my own opinions,’ resumed Mr. Murdstone, whose face darkened more and more, the more he and my aunt observed each other, which they did very narrowly, ‘as to the best mode of bringing him up; they are founded, in part, on my knowledge of him, and in part on my knowledge of my own means and resources. I am responsible for them to myself, I act upon them, and I say no more about them. It is enough that I place this boy under the eye of a friend of my own, in a respectable business; that it does not please him; that he runs away from it; makes himself a common vagabond about the country; and comes here, in rags, to appeal to you, Miss Trotwood. I wish to set before you, honourably, the exact consequences--so far as they are within my knowledge--of your abetting him in this appeal.’\n\n‘But about the respectable business first,’ said my aunt. ‘If he had been your own boy, you would have put him to it, just the same, I suppose?’\n\n‘If he had been my brother’s own boy,’ returned Miss Murdstone, striking in, ‘his character, I trust, would have been altogether different.’\n\n‘Or if the poor child, his mother, had been alive, he would still have gone into the respectable business, would he?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘I believe,’ said Mr. Murdstone, with an inclination of his head, ‘that Clara would have disputed nothing which myself and my sister Jane Murdstone were agreed was for the best.’\n\nMiss Murdstone confirmed this with an audible murmur.\n\n‘Humph!’ said my aunt. ‘Unfortunate baby!’\n\nMr. Dick, who had been rattling his money all this time, was rattling it so loudly now, that my aunt felt it necessary to check him with a look, before saying:\n\n‘The poor child’s annuity died with her?’\n\n‘Died with her,’ replied Mr. Murdstone.\n\n‘And there was no settlement of the little property--the house and garden--the what’s-its-name Rookery without any rooks in it--upon her boy?’\n\n‘It had been left to her, unconditionally, by her first husband,’ Mr. Murdstone began, when my aunt caught him up with the greatest irascibility and impatience.\n\n‘Good Lord, man, there’s no occasion to say that. Left to her unconditionally! I think I see David Copperfield looking forward to any condition of any sort or kind, though it stared him point-blank in the face! Of course it was left to her unconditionally. But when she married again--when she took that most disastrous step of marrying you, in short,’ said my aunt, ‘to be plain--did no one put in a word for the boy at that time?’\n\n‘My late wife loved her second husband, ma’am,’ said Mr. Murdstone, ‘and trusted implicitly in him.’\n\n‘Your late wife, sir, was a most unworldly, most unhappy, most unfortunate baby,’ returned my aunt, shaking her head at him. ‘That’s what she was. And now, what have you got to say next?’\n\n‘Merely this, Miss Trotwood,’ he returned. ‘I am here to take David back--to take him back unconditionally, to dispose of him as I think proper, and to deal with him as I think right. I am not here to make any promise, or give any pledge to anybody. You may possibly have some idea, Miss Trotwood, of abetting him in his running away, and in his complaints to you. Your manner, which I must say does not seem intended to propitiate, induces me to think it possible. Now I must caution you that if you abet him once, you abet him for good and all; if you step in between him and me, now, you must step in, Miss Trotwood, for ever. I cannot trifle, or be trifled with. I am here, for the first and last time, to take him away. Is he ready to go? If he is not--and you tell me he is not; on any pretence; it is indifferent to me what--my doors are shut against him henceforth, and yours, I take it for granted, are open to him.’\n\nTo this address, my aunt had listened with the closest attention, sitting perfectly upright, with her hands folded on one knee, and looking grimly on the speaker. When he had finished, she turned her eyes so as to command Miss Murdstone, without otherwise disturbing her attitude, and said:\n\n‘Well, ma’am, have YOU got anything to remark?’\n\n‘Indeed, Miss Trotwood,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘all that I could say has been so well said by my brother, and all that I know to be the fact has been so plainly stated by him, that I have nothing to add except my thanks for your politeness. For your very great politeness, I am sure,’ said Miss Murdstone; with an irony which no more affected my aunt, than it discomposed the cannon I had slept by at Chatham.\n\n‘And what does the boy say?’ said my aunt. ‘Are you ready to go, David?’\n\nI answered no, and entreated her not to let me go. I said that neither Mr. nor Miss Murdstone had ever liked me, or had ever been kind to me. That they had made my mama, who always loved me dearly, unhappy about me, and that I knew it well, and that Peggotty knew it. I said that I had been more miserable than I thought anybody could believe, who only knew how young I was. And I begged and prayed my aunt--I forget in what terms now, but I remember that they affected me very much then--to befriend and protect me, for my father’s sake.\n\n‘Mr. Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘what shall I do with this child?’\n\nMr. Dick considered, hesitated, brightened, and rejoined, ‘Have him measured for a suit of clothes directly.’\n\n‘Mr. Dick,’ said my aunt triumphantly, ‘give me your hand, for your common sense is invaluable.’ Having shaken it with great cordiality, she pulled me towards her and said to Mr. Murdstone:\n\n‘You can go when you like; I’ll take my chance with the boy. If he’s all you say he is, at least I can do as much for him then, as you have done. But I don’t believe a word of it.’\n\n‘Miss Trotwood,’ rejoined Mr. Murdstone, shrugging his shoulders, as he rose, ‘if you were a gentleman--’\n\n‘Bah! Stuff and nonsense!’ said my aunt. ‘Don’t talk to me!’\n\n‘How exquisitely polite!’ exclaimed Miss Murdstone, rising. ‘Overpowering, really!’\n\n‘Do you think I don’t know,’ said my aunt, turning a deaf ear to the sister, and continuing to address the brother, and to shake her head at him with infinite expression, ‘what kind of life you must have led that poor, unhappy, misdirected baby? Do you think I don’t know what a woeful day it was for the soft little creature when you first came in her way--smirking and making great eyes at her, I’ll be bound, as if you couldn’t say boh! to a goose!’\n\n‘I never heard anything so elegant!’ said Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘Do you think I can’t understand you as well as if I had seen you,’ pursued my aunt, ‘now that I DO see and hear you--which, I tell you candidly, is anything but a pleasure to me? Oh yes, bless us! who so smooth and silky as Mr. Murdstone at first! The poor, benighted innocent had never seen such a man. He was made of sweetness. He worshipped her. He doted on her boy--tenderly doted on him! He was to be another father to him, and they were all to live together in a garden of roses, weren’t they? Ugh! Get along with you, do!’ said my aunt.\n\n‘I never heard anything like this person in my life!’ exclaimed Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘And when you had made sure of the poor little fool,’ said my aunt--‘God forgive me that I should call her so, and she gone where YOU won’t go in a hurry--because you had not done wrong enough to her and hers, you must begin to train her, must you? begin to break her, like a poor caged bird, and wear her deluded life away, in teaching her to sing YOUR notes?’\n\n‘This is either insanity or intoxication,’ said Miss Murdstone, in a perfect agony at not being able to turn the current of my aunt’s address towards herself; ‘and my suspicion is that it’s intoxication.’\n\nMiss Betsey, without taking the least notice of the interruption, continued to address herself to Mr. Murdstone as if there had been no such thing.\n\n‘Mr. Murdstone,’ she said, shaking her finger at him, ‘you were a tyrant to the simple baby, and you broke her heart. She was a loving baby--I know that; I knew it, years before you ever saw her--and through the best part of her weakness you gave her the wounds she died of. There is the truth for your comfort, however you like it. And you and your instruments may make the most of it.’\n\n‘Allow me to inquire, Miss Trotwood,’ interposed Miss Murdstone, ‘whom you are pleased to call, in a choice of words in which I am not experienced, my brother’s instruments?’\n\n‘It was clear enough, as I have told you, years before YOU ever saw her--and why, in the mysterious dispensations of Providence, you ever did see her, is more than humanity can comprehend--it was clear enough that the poor soft little thing would marry somebody, at some time or other; but I did hope it wouldn’t have been as bad as it has turned out. That was the time, Mr. Murdstone, when she gave birth to her boy here,’ said my aunt; ‘to the poor child you sometimes tormented her through afterwards, which is a disagreeable remembrance and makes the sight of him odious now. Aye, aye! you needn’t wince!’ said my aunt. ‘I know it’s true without that.’\n\nHe had stood by the door, all this while, observant of her with a smile upon his face, though his black eyebrows were heavily contracted. I remarked now, that, though the smile was on his face still, his colour had gone in a moment, and he seemed to breathe as if he had been running.\n\n‘Good day, sir,’ said my aunt, ‘and good-bye! Good day to you, too, ma’am,’ said my aunt, turning suddenly upon his sister. ‘Let me see you ride a donkey over my green again, and as sure as you have a head upon your shoulders, I’ll knock your bonnet off, and tread upon it!’\n\nIt would require a painter, and no common painter too, to depict my aunt’s face as she delivered herself of this very unexpected sentiment, and Miss Murdstone’s face as she heard it. But the manner of the speech, no less than the matter, was so fiery, that Miss Murdstone, without a word in answer, discreetly put her arm through her brother’s, and walked haughtily out of the cottage; my aunt remaining in the window looking after them; prepared, I have no doubt, in case of the donkey’s reappearance, to carry her threat into instant execution.\n\nNo attempt at defiance being made, however, her face gradually relaxed, and became so pleasant, that I was emboldened to kiss and thank her; which I did with great heartiness, and with both my arms clasped round her neck. I then shook hands with Mr. Dick, who shook hands with me a great many times, and hailed this happy close of the proceedings with repeated bursts of laughter.\n\n‘You’ll consider yourself guardian, jointly with me, of this child, Mr. Dick,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘I shall be delighted,’ said Mr. Dick, ‘to be the guardian of David’s son.’\n\n‘Very good,’ returned my aunt, ‘that’s settled. I have been thinking, do you know, Mr. Dick, that I might call him Trotwood?’\n\n‘Certainly, certainly. Call him Trotwood, certainly,’ said Mr. Dick. ‘David’s son’s Trotwood.’\n\n‘Trotwood Copperfield, you mean,’ returned my aunt.\n\n‘Yes, to be sure. Yes. Trotwood Copperfield,’ said Mr. Dick, a little abashed.\n\nMy aunt took so kindly to the notion, that some ready-made clothes, which were purchased for me that afternoon, were marked ‘Trotwood Copperfield’, in her own handwriting, and in indelible marking-ink, before I put them on; and it was settled that all the other clothes which were ordered to be made for me (a complete outfit was bespoke that afternoon) should be marked in the same way.\n\nThus I began my new life, in a new name, and with everything new about me. Now that the state of doubt was over, I felt, for many days, like one in a dream. I never thought that I had a curious couple of guardians, in my aunt and Mr. Dick. I never thought of anything about myself, distinctly. The two things clearest in my mind were, that a remoteness had come upon the old Blunderstone life--which seemed to lie in the haze of an immeasurable distance; and that a curtain had for ever fallen on my life at Murdstone and Grinby’s. No one has ever raised that curtain since. I have lifted it for a moment, even in this narrative, with a reluctant hand, and dropped it gladly. The remembrance of that life is fraught with so much pain to me, with so much mental suffering and want of hope, that I have never had the courage even to examine how long I was doomed to lead it. Whether it lasted for a year, or more, or less, I do not know. I only know that it was, and ceased to be; and that I have written, and there I leave it.\n\n# CHAPTER 15. I MAKE ANOTHER BEGINNING\n\nMr. Dick and I soon became the best of friends, and very often, when his day’s work was done, went out together to fly the great kite. Every day of his life he had a long sitting at the Memorial, which never made the least progress, however hard he laboured, for King Charles the First always strayed into it, sooner or later, and then it was thrown aside, and another one begun. The patience and hope with which he bore these perpetual disappointments, the mild perception he had that there was something wrong about King Charles the First, the feeble efforts he made to keep him out, and the certainty with which he came in, and tumbled the Memorial out of all shape, made a deep impression on me. What Mr. Dick supposed would come of the Memorial, if it were completed; where he thought it was to go, or what he thought it was to do; he knew no more than anybody else, I believe. Nor was it at all necessary that he should trouble himself with such questions, for if anything were certain under the sun, it was certain that the Memorial never would be finished. It was quite an affecting sight, I used to think, to see him with the kite when it was up a great height in the air. What he had told me, in his room, about his belief in its disseminating the statements pasted on it, which were nothing but old leaves of abortive Memorials, might have been a fancy with him sometimes; but not when he was out, looking up at the kite in the sky, and feeling it pull and tug at his hand. He never looked so serene as he did then. I used to fancy, as I sat by him of an evening, on a green slope, and saw him watch the kite high in the quiet air, that it lifted his mind out of its confusion, and bore it (such was my boyish thought) into the skies. As he wound the string in and it came lower and lower down out of the beautiful light, until it fluttered to the ground, and lay there like a dead thing, he seemed to wake gradually out of a dream; and I remember to have seen him take it up, and look about him in a lost way, as if they had both come down together, so that I pitied him with all my heart.\n\nWhile I advanced in friendship and intimacy with Mr. Dick, I did not go backward in the favour of his staunch friend, my aunt. She took so kindly to me, that, in the course of a few weeks, she shortened my adopted name of Trotwood into Trot; and even encouraged me to hope, that if I went on as I had begun, I might take equal rank in her affections with my sister Betsey Trotwood.\n\n‘Trot,’ said my aunt one evening, when the backgammon-board was placed as usual for herself and Mr. Dick, ‘we must not forget your education.’\n\nThis was my only subject of anxiety, and I felt quite delighted by her referring to it.\n\n‘Should you like to go to school at Canterbury?’ said my aunt.\n\nI replied that I should like it very much, as it was so near her.\n\n‘Good,’ said my aunt. ‘Should you like to go tomorrow?’\n\nBeing already no stranger to the general rapidity of my aunt’s evolutions, I was not surprised by the suddenness of the proposal, and said: ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Good,’ said my aunt again. ‘Janet, hire the grey pony and chaise tomorrow morning at ten o’clock, and pack up Master Trotwood’s clothes tonight.’\n\nI was greatly elated by these orders; but my heart smote me for my selfishness, when I witnessed their effect on Mr. Dick, who was so low-spirited at the prospect of our separation, and played so ill in consequence, that my aunt, after giving him several admonitory raps on the knuckles with her dice-box, shut up the board, and declined to play with him any more. But, on hearing from my aunt that I should sometimes come over on a Saturday, and that he could sometimes come and see me on a Wednesday, he revived; and vowed to make another kite for those occasions, of proportions greatly surpassing the present one. In the morning he was downhearted again, and would have sustained himself by giving me all the money he had in his possession, gold and silver too, if my aunt had not interposed, and limited the gift to five shillings, which, at his earnest petition, were afterwards increased to ten. We parted at the garden-gate in a most affectionate manner, and Mr. Dick did not go into the house until my aunt had driven me out of sight of it.\n\nMy aunt, who was perfectly indifferent to public opinion, drove the grey pony through Dover in a masterly manner; sitting high and stiff like a state coachman, keeping a steady eye upon him wherever he went, and making a point of not letting him have his own way in any respect. When we came into the country road, she permitted him to relax a little, however; and looking at me down in a valley of cushion by her side, asked me whether I was happy?\n\n‘Very happy indeed, thank you, aunt,’ I said.\n\nShe was much gratified; and both her hands being occupied, patted me on the head with her whip.\n\n‘Is it a large school, aunt?’ I asked.\n\n‘Why, I don’t know,’ said my aunt. ‘We are going to Mr. Wickfield’s first.’\n\n‘Does he keep a school?’ I asked.\n\n‘No, Trot,’ said my aunt. ‘He keeps an office.’\n\nI asked for no more information about Mr. Wickfield, as she offered none, and we conversed on other subjects until we came to Canterbury, where, as it was market-day, my aunt had a great opportunity of insinuating the grey pony among carts, baskets, vegetables, and huckster’s goods. The hair-breadth turns and twists we made, drew down upon us a variety of speeches from the people standing about, which were not always complimentary; but my aunt drove on with perfect indifference, and I dare say would have taken her own way with as much coolness through an enemy’s country.\n\nAt length we stopped before a very old house bulging out over the road; a house with long low lattice-windows bulging out still farther, and beams with carved heads on the ends bulging out too, so that I fancied the whole house was leaning forward, trying to see who was passing on the narrow pavement below. It was quite spotless in its cleanliness. The old-fashioned brass knocker on the low arched door, ornamented with carved garlands of fruit and flowers, twinkled like a star; the two stone steps descending to the door were as white as if they had been covered with fair linen; and all the angles and corners, and carvings and mouldings, and quaint little panes of glass, and quainter little windows, though as old as the hills, were as pure as any snow that ever fell upon the hills.\n\nWhen the pony-chaise stopped at the door, and my eyes were intent upon the house, I saw a cadaverous face appear at a small window on the ground floor (in a little round tower that formed one side of the house), and quickly disappear. The low arched door then opened, and the face came out. It was quite as cadaverous as it had looked in the window, though in the grain of it there was that tinge of red which is sometimes to be observed in the skins of red-haired people. It belonged to a red-haired person--a youth of fifteen, as I take it now, but looking much older--whose hair was cropped as close as the closest stubble; who had hardly any eyebrows, and no eyelashes, and eyes of a red-brown, so unsheltered and unshaded, that I remember wondering how he went to sleep. He was high-shouldered and bony; dressed in decent black, with a white wisp of a neckcloth; buttoned up to the throat; and had a long, lank, skeleton hand, which particularly attracted my attention, as he stood at the pony’s head, rubbing his chin with it, and looking up at us in the chaise.\n\n‘Is Mr. Wickfield at home, Uriah Heep?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Mr. Wickfield’s at home, ma’am,’ said Uriah Heep, ‘if you’ll please to walk in there’--pointing with his long hand to the room he meant.\n\nWe got out; and leaving him to hold the pony, went into a long low parlour looking towards the street, from the window of which I caught a glimpse, as I went in, of Uriah Heep breathing into the pony’s nostrils, and immediately covering them with his hand, as if he were putting some spell upon him. Opposite to the tall old chimney-piece were two portraits: one of a gentleman with grey hair (though not by any means an old man) and black eyebrows, who was looking over some papers tied together with red tape; the other, of a lady, with a very placid and sweet expression of face, who was looking at me.\n\nI believe I was turning about in search of Uriah’s picture, when, a door at the farther end of the room opening, a gentleman entered, at sight of whom I turned to the first-mentioned portrait again, to make quite sure that it had not come out of its frame. But it was stationary; and as the gentleman advanced into the light, I saw that he was some years older than when he had had his picture painted.\n\n‘Miss Betsey Trotwood,’ said the gentleman, ‘pray walk in. I was engaged for a moment, but you’ll excuse my being busy. You know my motive. I have but one in life.’\n\nMiss Betsey thanked him, and we went into his room, which was furnished as an office, with books, papers, tin boxes, and so forth. It looked into a garden, and had an iron safe let into the wall; so immediately over the mantelshelf, that I wondered, as I sat down, how the sweeps got round it when they swept the chimney.\n\n‘Well, Miss Trotwood,’ said Mr. Wickfield; for I soon found that it was he, and that he was a lawyer, and steward of the estates of a rich gentleman of the county; ‘what wind blows you here? Not an ill wind, I hope?’\n\n‘No,’ replied my aunt. ‘I have not come for any law.’\n\n‘That’s right, ma’am,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘You had better come for anything else.’ His hair was quite white now, though his eyebrows were still black. He had a very agreeable face, and, I thought, was handsome. There was a certain richness in his complexion, which I had been long accustomed, under Peggotty’s tuition, to connect with port wine; and I fancied it was in his voice too, and referred his growing corpulency to the same cause. He was very cleanly dressed, in a blue coat, striped waistcoat, and nankeen trousers; and his fine frilled shirt and cambric neckcloth looked unusually soft and white, reminding my strolling fancy (I call to mind) of the plumage on the breast of a swan.\n\n‘This is my nephew,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Wasn’t aware you had one, Miss Trotwood,’ said Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘My grand-nephew, that is to say,’ observed my aunt.\n\n‘Wasn’t aware you had a grand-nephew, I give you my word,’ said Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘I have adopted him,’ said my aunt, with a wave of her hand, importing that his knowledge and his ignorance were all one to her, ‘and I have brought him here, to put to a school where he may be thoroughly well taught, and well treated. Now tell me where that school is, and what it is, and all about it.’\n\n‘Before I can advise you properly,’ said Mr. Wickfield--‘the old question, you know. What’s your motive in this?’\n\n‘Deuce take the man!’ exclaimed my aunt. ‘Always fishing for motives, when they’re on the surface! Why, to make the child happy and useful.’\n\n‘It must be a mixed motive, I think,’ said Mr. Wickfield, shaking his head and smiling incredulously.\n\n‘A mixed fiddlestick,’ returned my aunt. ‘You claim to have one plain motive in all you do yourself. You don’t suppose, I hope, that you are the only plain dealer in the world?’\n\n‘Ay, but I have only one motive in life, Miss Trotwood,’ he rejoined, smiling. ‘Other people have dozens, scores, hundreds. I have only one. There’s the difference. However, that’s beside the question. The best school? Whatever the motive, you want the best?’\n\nMy aunt nodded assent.\n\n‘At the best we have,’ said Mr. Wickfield, considering, ‘your nephew couldn’t board just now.’\n\n‘But he could board somewhere else, I suppose?’ suggested my aunt.\n\nMr. Wickfield thought I could. After a little discussion, he proposed to take my aunt to the school, that she might see it and judge for herself; also, to take her, with the same object, to two or three houses where he thought I could be boarded. My aunt embracing the proposal, we were all three going out together, when he stopped and said:\n\n‘Our little friend here might have some motive, perhaps, for objecting to the arrangements. I think we had better leave him behind?’\n\nMy aunt seemed disposed to contest the point; but to facilitate matters I said I would gladly remain behind, if they pleased; and returned into Mr. Wickfield’s office, where I sat down again, in the chair I had first occupied, to await their return.\n\nIt so happened that this chair was opposite a narrow passage, which ended in the little circular room where I had seen Uriah Heep’s pale face looking out of the window. Uriah, having taken the pony to a neighbouring stable, was at work at a desk in this room, which had a brass frame on the top to hang paper upon, and on which the writing he was making a copy of was then hanging. Though his face was towards me, I thought, for some time, the writing being between us, that he could not see me; but looking that way more attentively, it made me uncomfortable to observe that, every now and then, his sleepless eyes would come below the writing, like two red suns, and stealthily stare at me for I dare say a whole minute at a time, during which his pen went, or pretended to go, as cleverly as ever. I made several attempts to get out of their way--such as standing on a chair to look at a map on the other side of the room, and poring over the columns of a Kentish newspaper--but they always attracted me back again; and whenever I looked towards those two red suns, I was sure to find them, either just rising or just setting.\n\nAt length, much to my relief, my aunt and Mr. Wickfield came back, after a pretty long absence. They were not so successful as I could have wished; for though the advantages of the school were undeniable, my aunt had not approved of any of the boarding-houses proposed for me.\n\n‘It’s very unfortunate,’ said my aunt. ‘I don’t know what to do, Trot.’\n\n‘It does happen unfortunately,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘But I’ll tell you what you can do, Miss Trotwood.’\n\n‘What’s that?’ inquired my aunt.\n\n‘Leave your nephew here, for the present. He’s a quiet fellow. He won’t disturb me at all. It’s a capital house for study. As quiet as a monastery, and almost as roomy. Leave him here.’\n\nMy aunt evidently liked the offer, though she was delicate of accepting it. So did I. ‘Come, Miss Trotwood,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘This is the way out of the difficulty. It’s only a temporary arrangement, you know. If it don’t act well, or don’t quite accord with our mutual convenience, he can easily go to the right-about. There will be time to find some better place for him in the meanwhile. You had better determine to leave him here for the present!’\n\n‘I am very much obliged to you,’ said my aunt; ‘and so is he, I see; but--’\n\n‘Come! I know what you mean,’ cried Mr. Wickfield. ‘You shall not be oppressed by the receipt of favours, Miss Trotwood. You may pay for him, if you like. We won’t be hard about terms, but you shall pay if you will.’\n\n‘On that understanding,’ said my aunt, ‘though it doesn’t lessen the real obligation, I shall be very glad to leave him.’\n\n‘Then come and see my little housekeeper,’ said Mr. Wickfield.\n\nWe accordingly went up a wonderful old staircase; with a balustrade so broad that we might have gone up that, almost as easily; and into a shady old drawing-room, lighted by some three or four of the quaint windows I had looked up at from the street: which had old oak seats in them, that seemed to have come of the same trees as the shining oak floor, and the great beams in the ceiling. It was a prettily furnished room, with a piano and some lively furniture in red and green, and some flowers. It seemed to be all old nooks and corners; and in every nook and corner there was some queer little table, or cupboard, or bookcase, or seat, or something or other, that made me think there was not such another good corner in the room; until I looked at the next one, and found it equal to it, if not better. On everything there was the same air of retirement and cleanliness that marked the house outside.\n\nMr. Wickfield tapped at a door in a corner of the panelled wall, and a girl of about my own age came quickly out and kissed him. On her face, I saw immediately the placid and sweet expression of the lady whose picture had looked at me downstairs. It seemed to my imagination as if the portrait had grown womanly, and the original remained a child. Although her face was quite bright and happy, there was a tranquillity about it, and about her--a quiet, good, calm spirit--that I never have forgotten; that I shall never forget. This was his little housekeeper, his daughter Agnes, Mr. Wickfield said. When I heard how he said it, and saw how he held her hand, I guessed what the one motive of his life was.\n\nShe had a little basket-trifle hanging at her side, with keys in it; and she looked as staid and as discreet a housekeeper as the old house could have. She listened to her father as he told her about me, with a pleasant face; and when he had concluded, proposed to my aunt that we should go upstairs and see my room. We all went together, she before us: and a glorious old room it was, with more oak beams, and diamond panes; and the broad balustrade going all the way up to it.\n\nI cannot call to mind where or when, in my childhood, I had seen a stained glass window in a church. Nor do I recollect its subject. But I know that when I saw her turn round, in the grave light of the old staircase, and wait for us, above, I thought of that window; and I associated something of its tranquil brightness with Agnes Wickfield ever afterwards.\n\nMy aunt was as happy as I was, in the arrangement made for me; and we went down to the drawing-room again, well pleased and gratified. As she would not hear of staying to dinner, lest she should by any chance fail to arrive at home with the grey pony before dark; and as I apprehend Mr. Wickfield knew her too well to argue any point with her; some lunch was provided for her there, and Agnes went back to her governess, and Mr. Wickfield to his office. So we were left to take leave of one another without any restraint.\n\nShe told me that everything would be arranged for me by Mr. Wickfield, and that I should want for nothing, and gave me the kindest words and the best advice.\n\n‘Trot,’ said my aunt in conclusion, ‘be a credit to yourself, to me, and Mr. Dick, and Heaven be with you!’\n\nI was greatly overcome, and could only thank her, again and again, and send my love to Mr. Dick.\n\n‘Never,’ said my aunt, ‘be mean in anything; never be false; never be cruel. Avoid those three vices, Trot, and I can always be hopeful of you.’\n\nI promised, as well as I could, that I would not abuse her kindness or forget her admonition.\n\n‘The pony’s at the door,’ said my aunt, ‘and I am off! Stay here.’ With these words she embraced me hastily, and went out of the room, shutting the door after her. At first I was startled by so abrupt a departure, and almost feared I had displeased her; but when I looked into the street, and saw how dejectedly she got into the chaise, and drove away without looking up, I understood her better and did not do her that injustice.\n\nBy five o’clock, which was Mr. Wickfield’s dinner-hour, I had mustered up my spirits again, and was ready for my knife and fork. The cloth was only laid for us two; but Agnes was waiting in the drawing-room before dinner, went down with her father, and sat opposite to him at table. I doubted whether he could have dined without her.\n\nWe did not stay there, after dinner, but came upstairs into the drawing-room again: in one snug corner of which, Agnes set glasses for her father, and a decanter of port wine. I thought he would have missed its usual flavour, if it had been put there for him by any other hands.\n\nThere he sat, taking his wine, and taking a good deal of it, for two hours; while Agnes played on the piano, worked, and talked to him and me. He was, for the most part, gay and cheerful with us; but sometimes his eyes rested on her, and he fell into a brooding state, and was silent. She always observed this quickly, I thought, and always roused him with a question or caress. Then he came out of his meditation, and drank more wine.\n\nAgnes made the tea, and presided over it; and the time passed away after it, as after dinner, until she went to bed; when her father took her in his arms and kissed her, and, she being gone, ordered candles in his office. Then I went to bed too.\n\nBut in the course of the evening I had rambled down to the door, and a little way along the street, that I might have another peep at the old houses, and the grey Cathedral; and might think of my coming through that old city on my journey, and of my passing the very house I lived in, without knowing it. As I came back, I saw Uriah Heep shutting up the office; and feeling friendly towards everybody, went in and spoke to him, and at parting, gave him my hand. But oh, what a clammy hand his was! as ghostly to the touch as to the sight! I rubbed mine afterwards, to warm it, AND TO RUB HIS OFF.\n\nIt was such an uncomfortable hand, that, when I went to my room, it was still cold and wet upon my memory. Leaning out of the window, and seeing one of the faces on the beam-ends looking at me sideways, I fancied it was Uriah Heep got up there somehow, and shut him out in a hurry.\n\n# CHAPTER 16. I AM A NEW BOY IN MORE SENSES THAN ONE\n\nNext morning, after breakfast, I entered on school life again. I went, accompanied by Mr. Wickfield, to the scene of my future studies--a grave building in a courtyard, with a learned air about it that seemed very well suited to the stray rooks and jackdaws who came down from the Cathedral towers to walk with a clerkly bearing on the grass-plot--and was introduced to my new master, Doctor Strong.\n\nDoctor Strong looked almost as rusty, to my thinking, as the tall iron rails and gates outside the house; and almost as stiff and heavy as the great stone urns that flanked them, and were set up, on the top of the red-brick wall, at regular distances all round the court, like sublimated skittles, for Time to play at. He was in his library (I mean Doctor Strong was), with his clothes not particularly well brushed, and his hair not particularly well combed; his knee-smalls unbraced; his long black gaiters unbuttoned; and his shoes yawning like two caverns on the hearth-rug. Turning upon me a lustreless eye, that reminded me of a long-forgotten blind old horse who once used to crop the grass, and tumble over the graves, in Blunderstone churchyard, he said he was glad to see me: and then he gave me his hand; which I didn’t know what to do with, as it did nothing for itself.\n\nBut, sitting at work, not far from Doctor Strong, was a very pretty young lady--whom he called Annie, and who was his daughter, I supposed--who got me out of my difficulty by kneeling down to put Doctor Strong’s shoes on, and button his gaiters, which she did with great cheerfulness and quickness. When she had finished, and we were going out to the schoolroom, I was much surprised to hear Mr. Wickfield, in bidding her good morning, address her as ‘Mrs. Strong’; and I was wondering could she be Doctor Strong’s son’s wife, or could she be Mrs. Doctor Strong, when Doctor Strong himself unconsciously enlightened me.\n\n‘By the by, Wickfield,’ he said, stopping in a passage with his hand on my shoulder; ‘you have not found any suitable provision for my wife’s cousin yet?’\n\n‘No,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘No. Not yet.’\n\n‘I could wish it done as soon as it can be done, Wickfield,’ said Doctor Strong, ‘for Jack Maldon is needy, and idle; and of those two bad things, worse things sometimes come. What does Doctor Watts say,’ he added, looking at me, and moving his head to the time of his quotation, ‘“Satan finds some mischief still, for idle hands to do.”’\n\n‘Egad, Doctor,’ returned Mr. Wickfield, ‘if Doctor Watts knew mankind, he might have written, with as much truth, “Satan finds some mischief still, for busy hands to do.” The busy people achieve their full share of mischief in the world, you may rely upon it. What have the people been about, who have been the busiest in getting money, and in getting power, this century or two? No mischief?’\n\n‘Jack Maldon will never be very busy in getting either, I expect,’ said Doctor Strong, rubbing his chin thoughtfully.\n\n‘Perhaps not,’ said Mr. Wickfield; ‘and you bring me back to the question, with an apology for digressing. No, I have not been able to dispose of Mr. Jack Maldon yet. I believe,’ he said this with some hesitation, ‘I penetrate your motive, and it makes the thing more difficult.’\n\n‘My motive,’ returned Doctor Strong, ‘is to make some suitable provision for a cousin, and an old playfellow, of Annie’s.’\n\n‘Yes, I know,’ said Mr. Wickfield; ‘at home or abroad.’\n\n‘Aye!’ replied the Doctor, apparently wondering why he emphasized those words so much. ‘At home or abroad.’\n\n‘Your own expression, you know,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘Or abroad.’\n\n‘Surely,’ the Doctor answered. ‘Surely. One or other.’\n\n‘One or other? Have you no choice?’ asked Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘No,’ returned the Doctor.\n\n‘No?’ with astonishment.\n\n‘Not the least.’\n\n‘No motive,’ said Mr. Wickfield, ‘for meaning abroad, and not at home?’\n\n‘No,’ returned the Doctor.\n\n‘I am bound to believe you, and of course I do believe you,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘It might have simplified my office very much, if I had known it before. But I confess I entertained another impression.’\n\nDoctor Strong regarded him with a puzzled and doubting look, which almost immediately subsided into a smile that gave me great encouragement; for it was full of amiability and sweetness, and there was a simplicity in it, and indeed in his whole manner, when the studious, pondering frost upon it was got through, very attractive and hopeful to a young scholar like me. Repeating ‘no’, and ‘not the least’, and other short assurances to the same purport, Doctor Strong jogged on before us, at a queer, uneven pace; and we followed: Mr. Wickfield, looking grave, I observed, and shaking his head to himself, without knowing that I saw him.\n\nThe schoolroom was a pretty large hall, on the quietest side of the house, confronted by the stately stare of some half-dozen of the great urns, and commanding a peep of an old secluded garden belonging to the Doctor, where the peaches were ripening on the sunny south wall. There were two great aloes, in tubs, on the turf outside the windows; the broad hard leaves of which plant (looking as if they were made of painted tin) have ever since, by association, been symbolical to me of silence and retirement. About five-and-twenty boys were studiously engaged at their books when we went in, but they rose to give the Doctor good morning, and remained standing when they saw Mr. Wickfield and me.\n\n‘A new boy, young gentlemen,’ said the Doctor; ‘Trotwood Copperfield.’\n\nOne Adams, who was the head-boy, then stepped out of his place and welcomed me. He looked like a young clergyman, in his white cravat, but he was very affable and good-humoured; and he showed me my place, and presented me to the masters, in a gentlemanly way that would have put me at my ease, if anything could.\n\nIt seemed to me so long, however, since I had been among such boys, or among any companions of my own age, except Mick Walker and Mealy Potatoes, that I felt as strange as ever I have done in my life. I was so conscious of having passed through scenes of which they could have no knowledge, and of having acquired experiences foreign to my age, appearance, and condition as one of them, that I half believed it was an imposture to come there as an ordinary little schoolboy. I had become, in the Murdstone and Grinby time, however short or long it may have been, so unused to the sports and games of boys, that I knew I was awkward and inexperienced in the commonest things belonging to them. Whatever I had learnt, had so slipped away from me in the sordid cares of my life from day to night, that now, when I was examined about what I knew, I knew nothing, and was put into the lowest form of the school. But, troubled as I was, by my want of boyish skill, and of book-learning too, I was made infinitely more uncomfortable by the consideration, that, in what I did know, I was much farther removed from my companions than in what I did not. My mind ran upon what they would think, if they knew of my familiar acquaintance with the King’s Bench Prison? Was there anything about me which would reveal my proceedings in connexion with the Micawber family--all those pawnings, and sellings, and suppers--in spite of myself? Suppose some of the boys had seen me coming through Canterbury, wayworn and ragged, and should find me out? What would they say, who made so light of money, if they could know how I had scraped my halfpence together, for the purchase of my daily saveloy and beer, or my slices of pudding? How would it affect them, who were so innocent of London life, and London streets, to discover how knowing I was (and was ashamed to be) in some of the meanest phases of both? All this ran in my head so much, on that first day at Doctor Strong’s, that I felt distrustful of my slightest look and gesture; shrunk within myself whensoever I was approached by one of my new schoolfellows; and hurried off the minute school was over, afraid of committing myself in my response to any friendly notice or advance.\n\nBut there was such an influence in Mr. Wickfield’s old house, that when I knocked at it, with my new school-books under my arm, I began to feel my uneasiness softening away. As I went up to my airy old room, the grave shadow of the staircase seemed to fall upon my doubts and fears, and to make the past more indistinct. I sat there, sturdily conning my books, until dinner-time (we were out of school for good at three); and went down, hopeful of becoming a passable sort of boy yet.\n\nAgnes was in the drawing-room, waiting for her father, who was detained by someone in his office. She met me with her pleasant smile, and asked me how I liked the school. I told her I should like it very much, I hoped; but I was a little strange to it at first.\n\n‘You have never been to school,’ I said, ‘have you?’ ‘Oh yes! Every day.’\n\n‘Ah, but you mean here, at your own home?’\n\n‘Papa couldn’t spare me to go anywhere else,’ she answered, smiling and shaking her head. ‘His housekeeper must be in his house, you know.’\n\n‘He is very fond of you, I am sure,’ I said.\n\nShe nodded ‘Yes,’ and went to the door to listen for his coming up, that she might meet him on the stairs. But, as he was not there, she came back again.\n\n‘Mama has been dead ever since I was born,’ she said, in her quiet way. ‘I only know her picture, downstairs. I saw you looking at it yesterday. Did you think whose it was?’\n\nI told her yes, because it was so like herself.\n\n‘Papa says so, too,’ said Agnes, pleased. ‘Hark! That’s papa now!’\n\nHer bright calm face lighted up with pleasure as she went to meet him, and as they came in, hand in hand. He greeted me cordially; and told me I should certainly be happy under Doctor Strong, who was one of the gentlest of men.\n\n‘There may be some, perhaps--I don’t know that there are--who abuse his kindness,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘Never be one of those, Trotwood, in anything. He is the least suspicious of mankind; and whether that’s a merit, or whether it’s a blemish, it deserves consideration in all dealings with the Doctor, great or small.’\n\nHe spoke, I thought, as if he were weary, or dissatisfied with something; but I did not pursue the question in my mind, for dinner was just then announced, and we went down and took the same seats as before.\n\nWe had scarcely done so, when Uriah Heep put in his red head and his lank hand at the door, and said:\n\n‘Here’s Mr. Maldon begs the favour of a word, sir.’\n\n‘I am but this moment quit of Mr. Maldon,’ said his master.\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ returned Uriah; ‘but Mr. Maldon has come back, and he begs the favour of a word.’\n\nAs he held the door open with his hand, Uriah looked at me, and looked at Agnes, and looked at the dishes, and looked at the plates, and looked at every object in the room, I thought,--yet seemed to look at nothing; he made such an appearance all the while of keeping his red eyes dutifully on his master. ‘I beg your pardon. It’s only to say, on reflection,’ observed a voice behind Uriah, as Uriah’s head was pushed away, and the speaker’s substituted--‘pray excuse me for this intrusion--that as it seems I have no choice in the matter, the sooner I go abroad the better. My cousin Annie did say, when we talked of it, that she liked to have her friends within reach rather than to have them banished, and the old Doctor--’\n\n‘Doctor Strong, was that?’ Mr. Wickfield interposed, gravely.\n\n‘Doctor Strong, of course,’ returned the other; ‘I call him the old Doctor; it’s all the same, you know.’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ returned Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘Well, Doctor Strong,’ said the other--‘Doctor Strong was of the same mind, I believed. But as it appears from the course you take with me he has changed his mind, why there’s no more to be said, except that the sooner I am off, the better. Therefore, I thought I’d come back and say, that the sooner I am off the better. When a plunge is to be made into the water, it’s of no use lingering on the bank.’\n\n‘There shall be as little lingering as possible, in your case, Mr. Maldon, you may depend upon it,’ said Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘Thank’ee,’ said the other. ‘Much obliged. I don’t want to look a gift-horse in the mouth, which is not a gracious thing to do; otherwise, I dare say, my cousin Annie could easily arrange it in her own way. I suppose Annie would only have to say to the old Doctor--’\n\n‘Meaning that Mrs. Strong would only have to say to her husband--do I follow you?’ said Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘Quite so,’ returned the other, ‘--would only have to say, that she wanted such and such a thing to be so and so; and it would be so and so, as a matter of course.’\n\n‘And why as a matter of course, Mr. Maldon?’ asked Mr. Wickfield, sedately eating his dinner.\n\n‘Why, because Annie’s a charming young girl, and the old Doctor--Doctor Strong, I mean--is not quite a charming young boy,’ said Mr. Jack Maldon, laughing. ‘No offence to anybody, Mr. Wickfield. I only mean that I suppose some compensation is fair and reasonable in that sort of marriage.’\n\n‘Compensation to the lady, sir?’ asked Mr. Wickfield gravely.\n\n‘To the lady, sir,’ Mr. Jack Maldon answered, laughing. But appearing to remark that Mr. Wickfield went on with his dinner in the same sedate, immovable manner, and that there was no hope of making him relax a muscle of his face, he added: ‘However, I have said what I came to say, and, with another apology for this intrusion, I may take myself off. Of course I shall observe your directions, in considering the matter as one to be arranged between you and me solely, and not to be referred to, up at the Doctor’s.’\n\n‘Have you dined?’ asked Mr. Wickfield, with a motion of his hand towards the table.\n\n‘Thank’ee. I am going to dine,’ said Mr. Maldon, ‘with my cousin Annie. Good-bye!’\n\nMr. Wickfield, without rising, looked after him thoughtfully as he went out. He was rather a shallow sort of young gentleman, I thought, with a handsome face, a rapid utterance, and a confident, bold air. And this was the first I ever saw of Mr. Jack Maldon; whom I had not expected to see so soon, when I heard the Doctor speak of him that morning.\n\nWhen we had dined, we went upstairs again, where everything went on exactly as on the previous day. Agnes set the glasses and decanters in the same corner, and Mr. Wickfield sat down to drink, and drank a good deal. Agnes played the piano to him, sat by him, and worked and talked, and played some games at dominoes with me. In good time she made tea; and afterwards, when I brought down my books, looked into them, and showed me what she knew of them (which was no slight matter, though she said it was), and what was the best way to learn and understand them. I see her, with her modest, orderly, placid manner, and I hear her beautiful calm voice, as I write these words. The influence for all good, which she came to exercise over me at a later time, begins already to descend upon my breast. I love little Em’ly, and I don’t love Agnes--no, not at all in that way--but I feel that there are goodness, peace, and truth, wherever Agnes is; and that the soft light of the coloured window in the church, seen long ago, falls on her always, and on me when I am near her, and on everything around.\n\nThe time having come for her withdrawal for the night, and she having left us, I gave Mr. Wickfield my hand, preparatory to going away myself. But he checked me and said: ‘Should you like to stay with us, Trotwood, or to go elsewhere?’\n\n‘To stay,’ I answered, quickly.\n\n‘You are sure?’\n\n‘If you please. If I may!’\n\n‘Why, it’s but a dull life that we lead here, boy, I am afraid,’ he said.\n\n‘Not more dull for me than Agnes, sir. Not dull at all!’\n\n‘Than Agnes,’ he repeated, walking slowly to the great chimney-piece, and leaning against it. ‘Than Agnes!’\n\nHe had drank wine that evening (or I fancied it), until his eyes were bloodshot. Not that I could see them now, for they were cast down, and shaded by his hand; but I had noticed them a little while before.\n\n‘Now I wonder,’ he muttered, ‘whether my Agnes tires of me. When should I ever tire of her! But that’s different, that’s quite different.’\n\nHe was musing, not speaking to me; so I remained quiet.\n\n‘A dull old house,’ he said, ‘and a monotonous life; but I must have her near me. I must keep her near me. If the thought that I may die and leave my darling, or that my darling may die and leave me, comes like a spectre, to distress my happiest hours, and is only to be drowned in--’\n\nHe did not supply the word; but pacing slowly to the place where he had sat, and mechanically going through the action of pouring wine from the empty decanter, set it down and paced back again.\n\n‘If it is miserable to bear, when she is here,’ he said, ‘what would it be, and she away? No, no, no. I cannot try that.’\n\nHe leaned against the chimney-piece, brooding so long that I could not decide whether to run the risk of disturbing him by going, or to remain quietly where I was, until he should come out of his reverie. At length he aroused himself, and looked about the room until his eyes encountered mine.\n\n‘Stay with us, Trotwood, eh?’ he said in his usual manner, and as if he were answering something I had just said. ‘I am glad of it. You are company to us both. It is wholesome to have you here. Wholesome for me, wholesome for Agnes, wholesome perhaps for all of us.’\n\n‘I am sure it is for me, sir,’ I said. ‘I am so glad to be here.’\n\n‘That’s a fine fellow!’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘As long as you are glad to be here, you shall stay here.’ He shook hands with me upon it, and clapped me on the back; and told me that when I had anything to do at night after Agnes had left us, or when I wished to read for my own pleasure, I was free to come down to his room, if he were there and if I desired it for company’s sake, and to sit with him. I thanked him for his consideration; and, as he went down soon afterwards, and I was not tired, went down too, with a book in my hand, to avail myself, for half-an-hour, of his permission.\n\nBut, seeing a light in the little round office, and immediately feeling myself attracted towards Uriah Heep, who had a sort of fascination for me, I went in there instead. I found Uriah reading a great fat book, with such demonstrative attention, that his lank forefinger followed up every line as he read, and made clammy tracks along the page (or so I fully believed) like a snail.\n\n‘You are working late tonight, Uriah,’ says I.\n\n‘Yes, Master Copperfield,’ says Uriah.\n\nAs I was getting on the stool opposite, to talk to him more conveniently, I observed that he had not such a thing as a smile about him, and that he could only widen his mouth and make two hard creases down his cheeks, one on each side, to stand for one.\n\n‘I am not doing office-work, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah.\n\n‘What work, then?’ I asked.\n\n‘I am improving my legal knowledge, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah. ‘I am going through Tidd’s Practice. Oh, what a writer Mr. Tidd is, Master Copperfield!’\n\nMy stool was such a tower of observation, that as I watched him reading on again, after this rapturous exclamation, and following up the lines with his forefinger, I observed that his nostrils, which were thin and pointed, with sharp dints in them, had a singular and most uncomfortable way of expanding and contracting themselves--that they seemed to twinkle instead of his eyes, which hardly ever twinkled at all.\n\n‘I suppose you are quite a great lawyer?’ I said, after looking at him for some time.\n\n‘Me, Master Copperfield?’ said Uriah. ‘Oh, no! I’m a very umble person.’\n\nIt was no fancy of mine about his hands, I observed; for he frequently ground the palms against each other as if to squeeze them dry and warm, besides often wiping them, in a stealthy way, on his pocket-handkerchief.\n\n‘I am well aware that I am the umblest person going,’ said Uriah Heep, modestly; ‘let the other be where he may. My mother is likewise a very umble person. We live in a numble abode, Master Copperfield, but have much to be thankful for. My father’s former calling was umble. He was a sexton.’\n\n‘What is he now?’ I asked.\n\n‘He is a partaker of glory at present, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah Heep. ‘But we have much to be thankful for. How much have I to be thankful for in living with Mr. Wickfield!’\n\nI asked Uriah if he had been with Mr. Wickfield long?\n\n‘I have been with him, going on four year, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah; shutting up his book, after carefully marking the place where he had left off. ‘Since a year after my father’s death. How much have I to be thankful for, in that! How much have I to be thankful for, in Mr. Wickfield’s kind intention to give me my articles, which would otherwise not lay within the umble means of mother and self!’\n\n‘Then, when your articled time is over, you’ll be a regular lawyer, I suppose?’ said I.\n\n‘With the blessing of Providence, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah.\n\n‘Perhaps you’ll be a partner in Mr. Wickfield’s business, one of these days,’ I said, to make myself agreeable; ‘and it will be Wickfield and Heep, or Heep late Wickfield.’\n\n‘Oh no, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, shaking his head, ‘I am much too umble for that!’\n\nHe certainly did look uncommonly like the carved face on the beam outside my window, as he sat, in his humility, eyeing me sideways, with his mouth widened, and the creases in his cheeks.\n\n‘Mr. Wickfield is a most excellent man, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah. ‘If you have known him long, you know it, I am sure, much better than I can inform you.’\n\nI replied that I was certain he was; but that I had not known him long myself, though he was a friend of my aunt’s.\n\n‘Oh, indeed, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah. ‘Your aunt is a sweet lady, Master Copperfield!’\n\nHe had a way of writhing when he wanted to express enthusiasm, which was very ugly; and which diverted my attention from the compliment he had paid my relation, to the snaky twistings of his throat and body.\n\n‘A sweet lady, Master Copperfield!’ said Uriah Heep. ‘She has a great admiration for Miss Agnes, Master Copperfield, I believe?’\n\nI said, ‘Yes,’ boldly; not that I knew anything about it, Heaven forgive me!\n\n‘I hope you have, too, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah. ‘But I am sure you must have.’\n\n‘Everybody must have,’ I returned.\n\n‘Oh, thank you, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah Heep, ‘for that remark! It is so true! Umble as I am, I know it is so true! Oh, thank you, Master Copperfield!’ He writhed himself quite off his stool in the excitement of his feelings, and, being off, began to make arrangements for going home.\n\n‘Mother will be expecting me,’ he said, referring to a pale, inexpressive-faced watch in his pocket, ‘and getting uneasy; for though we are very umble, Master Copperfield, we are much attached to one another. If you would come and see us, any afternoon, and take a cup of tea at our lowly dwelling, mother would be as proud of your company as I should be.’\n\nI said I should be glad to come.\n\n‘Thank you, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, putting his book away upon the shelf--‘I suppose you stop here, some time, Master Copperfield?’\n\nI said I was going to be brought up there, I believed, as long as I remained at school.\n\n‘Oh, indeed!’ exclaimed Uriah. ‘I should think YOU would come into the business at last, Master Copperfield!’\n\nI protested that I had no views of that sort, and that no such scheme was entertained in my behalf by anybody; but Uriah insisted on blandly replying to all my assurances, ‘Oh, yes, Master Copperfield, I should think you would, indeed!’ and, ‘Oh, indeed, Master Copperfield, I should think you would, certainly!’ over and over again. Being, at last, ready to leave the office for the night, he asked me if it would suit my convenience to have the light put out; and on my answering ‘Yes,’ instantly extinguished it. After shaking hands with me--his hand felt like a fish, in the dark--he opened the door into the street a very little, and crept out, and shut it, leaving me to grope my way back into the house: which cost me some trouble and a fall over his stool. This was the proximate cause, I suppose, of my dreaming about him, for what appeared to me to be half the night; and dreaming, among other things, that he had launched Mr. Peggotty’s house on a piratical expedition, with a black flag at the masthead, bearing the inscription ‘Tidd’s Practice’, under which diabolical ensign he was carrying me and little Em’ly to the Spanish Main, to be drowned.\n\nI got a little the better of my uneasiness when I went to school next day, and a good deal the better next day, and so shook it off by degrees, that in less than a fortnight I was quite at home, and happy, among my new companions. I was awkward enough in their games, and backward enough in their studies; but custom would improve me in the first respect, I hoped, and hard work in the second. Accordingly, I went to work very hard, both in play and in earnest, and gained great commendation. And, in a very little while, the Murdstone and Grinby life became so strange to me that I hardly believed in it, while my present life grew so familiar, that I seemed to have been leading it a long time.\n\nDoctor Strong’s was an excellent school; as different from Mr. Creakle’s as good is from evil. It was very gravely and decorously ordered, and on a sound system; with an appeal, in everything, to the honour and good faith of the boys, and an avowed intention to rely on their possession of those qualities unless they proved themselves unworthy of it, which worked wonders. We all felt that we had a part in the management of the place, and in sustaining its character and dignity. Hence, we soon became warmly attached to it--I am sure I did for one, and I never knew, in all my time, of any other boy being otherwise--and learnt with a good will, desiring to do it credit. We had noble games out of hours, and plenty of liberty; but even then, as I remember, we were well spoken of in the town, and rarely did any disgrace, by our appearance or manner, to the reputation of Doctor Strong and Doctor Strong’s boys.\n\nSome of the higher scholars boarded in the Doctor’s house, and through them I learned, at second hand, some particulars of the Doctor’s history--as, how he had not yet been married twelve months to the beautiful young lady I had seen in the study, whom he had married for love; for she had not a sixpence, and had a world of poor relations (so our fellows said) ready to swarm the Doctor out of house and home. Also, how the Doctor’s cogitating manner was attributable to his being always engaged in looking out for Greek roots; which, in my innocence and ignorance, I supposed to be a botanical furor on the Doctor’s part, especially as he always looked at the ground when he walked about, until I understood that they were roots of words, with a view to a new Dictionary which he had in contemplation. Adams, our head-boy, who had a turn for mathematics, had made a calculation, I was informed, of the time this Dictionary would take in completing, on the Doctor’s plan, and at the Doctor’s rate of going. He considered that it might be done in one thousand six hundred and forty-nine years, counting from the Doctor’s last, or sixty-second, birthday.\n\nBut the Doctor himself was the idol of the whole school: and it must have been a badly composed school if he had been anything else, for he was the kindest of men; with a simple faith in him that might have touched the stone hearts of the very urns upon the wall. As he walked up and down that part of the courtyard which was at the side of the house, with the stray rooks and jackdaws looking after him with their heads cocked slyly, as if they knew how much more knowing they were in worldly affairs than he, if any sort of vagabond could only get near enough to his creaking shoes to attract his attention to one sentence of a tale of distress, that vagabond was made for the next two days. It was so notorious in the house, that the masters and head-boys took pains to cut these marauders off at angles, and to get out of windows, and turn them out of the courtyard, before they could make the Doctor aware of their presence; which was sometimes happily effected within a few yards of him, without his knowing anything of the matter, as he jogged to and fro. Outside his own domain, and unprotected, he was a very sheep for the shearers. He would have taken his gaiters off his legs, to give away. In fact, there was a story current among us (I have no idea, and never had, on what authority, but I have believed it for so many years that I feel quite certain it is true), that on a frosty day, one winter-time, he actually did bestow his gaiters on a beggar-woman, who occasioned some scandal in the neighbourhood by exhibiting a fine infant from door to door, wrapped in those garments, which were universally recognized, being as well known in the vicinity as the Cathedral. The legend added that the only person who did not identify them was the Doctor himself, who, when they were shortly afterwards displayed at the door of a little second-hand shop of no very good repute, where such things were taken in exchange for gin, was more than once observed to handle them approvingly, as if admiring some curious novelty in the pattern, and considering them an improvement on his own.\n\nIt was very pleasant to see the Doctor with his pretty young wife. He had a fatherly, benignant way of showing his fondness for her, which seemed in itself to express a good man. I often saw them walking in the garden where the peaches were, and I sometimes had a nearer observation of them in the study or the parlour. She appeared to me to take great care of the Doctor, and to like him very much, though I never thought her vitally interested in the Dictionary: some cumbrous fragments of which work the Doctor always carried in his pockets, and in the lining of his hat, and generally seemed to be expounding to her as they walked about.\n\nI saw a good deal of Mrs. Strong, both because she had taken a liking for me on the morning of my introduction to the Doctor, and was always afterwards kind to me, and interested in me; and because she was very fond of Agnes, and was often backwards and forwards at our house. There was a curious constraint between her and Mr. Wickfield, I thought (of whom she seemed to be afraid), that never wore off. When she came there of an evening, she always shrunk from accepting his escort home, and ran away with me instead. And sometimes, as we were running gaily across the Cathedral yard together, expecting to meet nobody, we would meet Mr. Jack Maldon, who was always surprised to see us.\n\nMrs. Strong’s mama was a lady I took great delight in. Her name was Mrs. Markleham; but our boys used to call her the Old Soldier, on account of her generalship, and the skill with which she marshalled great forces of relations against the Doctor. She was a little, sharp-eyed woman, who used to wear, when she was dressed, one unchangeable cap, ornamented with some artificial flowers, and two artificial butterflies supposed to be hovering above the flowers. There was a superstition among us that this cap had come from France, and could only originate in the workmanship of that ingenious nation: but all I certainly know about it, is, that it always made its appearance of an evening, wheresoever Mrs. Markleham made HER appearance; that it was carried about to friendly meetings in a Hindoo basket; that the butterflies had the gift of trembling constantly; and that they improved the shining hours at Doctor Strong’s expense, like busy bees.\n\nI observed the Old Soldier--not to adopt the name disrespectfully--to pretty good advantage, on a night which is made memorable to me by something else I shall relate. It was the night of a little party at the Doctor’s, which was given on the occasion of Mr. Jack Maldon’s departure for India, whither he was going as a cadet, or something of that kind: Mr. Wickfield having at length arranged the business. It happened to be the Doctor’s birthday, too. We had had a holiday, had made presents to him in the morning, had made a speech to him through the head-boy, and had cheered him until we were hoarse, and until he had shed tears. And now, in the evening, Mr. Wickfield, Agnes, and I, went to have tea with him in his private capacity.\n\nMr. Jack Maldon was there, before us. Mrs. Strong, dressed in white, with cherry-coloured ribbons, was playing the piano, when we went in; and he was leaning over her to turn the leaves. The clear red and white of her complexion was not so blooming and flower-like as usual, I thought, when she turned round; but she looked very pretty, Wonderfully pretty.\n\n‘I have forgotten, Doctor,’ said Mrs. Strong’s mama, when we were seated, ‘to pay you the compliments of the day--though they are, as you may suppose, very far from being mere compliments in my case. Allow me to wish you many happy returns.’\n\n‘I thank you, ma’am,’ replied the Doctor.\n\n‘Many, many, many, happy returns,’ said the Old Soldier. ‘Not only for your own sake, but for Annie’s, and John Maldon’s, and many other people’s. It seems but yesterday to me, John, when you were a little creature, a head shorter than Master Copperfield, making baby love to Annie behind the gooseberry bushes in the back-garden.’\n\n‘My dear mama,’ said Mrs. Strong, ‘never mind that now.’\n\n‘Annie, don’t be absurd,’ returned her mother. ‘If you are to blush to hear of such things now you are an old married woman, when are you not to blush to hear of them?’\n\n‘Old?’ exclaimed Mr. Jack Maldon. ‘Annie? Come!’\n\n‘Yes, John,’ returned the Soldier. ‘Virtually, an old married woman. Although not old by years--for when did you ever hear me say, or who has ever heard me say, that a girl of twenty was old by years!--your cousin is the wife of the Doctor, and, as such, what I have described her. It is well for you, John, that your cousin is the wife of the Doctor. You have found in him an influential and kind friend, who will be kinder yet, I venture to predict, if you deserve it. I have no false pride. I never hesitate to admit, frankly, that there are some members of our family who want a friend. You were one yourself, before your cousin’s influence raised up one for you.’\n\nThe Doctor, in the goodness of his heart, waved his hand as if to make light of it, and save Mr. Jack Maldon from any further reminder. But Mrs. Markleham changed her chair for one next the Doctor’s, and putting her fan on his coat-sleeve, said:\n\n‘No, really, my dear Doctor, you must excuse me if I appear to dwell on this rather, because I feel so very strongly. I call it quite my monomania, it is such a subject of mine. You are a blessing to us. You really are a Boon, you know.’\n\n‘Nonsense, nonsense,’ said the Doctor.\n\n‘No, no, I beg your pardon,’ retorted the Old Soldier. ‘With nobody present, but our dear and confidential friend Mr. Wickfield, I cannot consent to be put down. I shall begin to assert the privileges of a mother-in-law, if you go on like that, and scold you. I am perfectly honest and outspoken. What I am saying, is what I said when you first overpowered me with surprise--you remember how surprised I was?--by proposing for Annie. Not that there was anything so very much out of the way, in the mere fact of the proposal--it would be ridiculous to say that!--but because, you having known her poor father, and having known her from a baby six months old, I hadn’t thought of you in such a light at all, or indeed as a marrying man in any way,--simply that, you know.’\n\n‘Aye, aye,’ returned the Doctor, good-humouredly. ‘Never mind.’\n\n‘But I DO mind,’ said the Old Soldier, laying her fan upon his lips. ‘I mind very much. I recall these things that I may be contradicted if I am wrong. Well! Then I spoke to Annie, and I told her what had happened. I said, “My dear, here’s Doctor Strong has positively been and made you the subject of a handsome declaration and an offer.” Did I press it in the least? No. I said, “Now, Annie, tell me the truth this moment; is your heart free?” “Mama,” she said crying, “I am extremely young”--which was perfectly true--“and I hardly know if I have a heart at all.” “Then, my dear,” I said, “you may rely upon it, it’s free. At all events, my love,” said I, “Doctor Strong is in an agitated state of mind, and must be answered. He cannot be kept in his present state of suspense.” “Mama,” said Annie, still crying, “would he be unhappy without me? If he would, I honour and respect him so much, that I think I will have him.” So it was settled. And then, and not till then, I said to Annie, “Annie, Doctor Strong will not only be your husband, but he will represent your late father: he will represent the head of our family, he will represent the wisdom and station, and I may say the means, of our family; and will be, in short, a Boon to it.” I used the word at the time, and I have used it again, today. If I have any merit it is consistency.’\n\nThe daughter had sat quite silent and still during this speech, with her eyes fixed on the ground; her cousin standing near her, and looking on the ground too. She now said very softly, in a trembling voice:\n\n‘Mama, I hope you have finished?’ ‘No, my dear Annie,’ returned the Old Soldier, ‘I have not quite finished. Since you ask me, my love, I reply that I have not. I complain that you really are a little unnatural towards your own family; and, as it is of no use complaining to you. I mean to complain to your husband. Now, my dear Doctor, do look at that silly wife of yours.’\n\nAs the Doctor turned his kind face, with its smile of simplicity and gentleness, towards her, she drooped her head more. I noticed that Mr. Wickfield looked at her steadily.\n\n‘When I happened to say to that naughty thing, the other day,’ pursued her mother, shaking her head and her fan at her, playfully, ‘that there was a family circumstance she might mention to you--indeed, I think, was bound to mention--she said, that to mention it was to ask a favour; and that, as you were too generous, and as for her to ask was always to have, she wouldn’t.’\n\n‘Annie, my dear,’ said the Doctor. ‘That was wrong. It robbed me of a pleasure.’\n\n‘Almost the very words I said to her!’ exclaimed her mother. ‘Now really, another time, when I know what she would tell you but for this reason, and won’t, I have a great mind, my dear Doctor, to tell you myself.’\n\n‘I shall be glad if you will,’ returned the Doctor.\n\n‘Shall I?’\n\n‘Certainly.’\n\n‘Well, then, I will!’ said the Old Soldier. ‘That’s a bargain.’ And having, I suppose, carried her point, she tapped the Doctor’s hand several times with her fan (which she kissed first), and returned triumphantly to her former station.\n\nSome more company coming in, among whom were the two masters and Adams, the talk became general; and it naturally turned on Mr. Jack Maldon, and his voyage, and the country he was going to, and his various plans and prospects. He was to leave that night, after supper, in a post-chaise, for Gravesend; where the ship, in which he was to make the voyage, lay; and was to be gone--unless he came home on leave, or for his health--I don’t know how many years. I recollect it was settled by general consent that India was quite a misrepresented country, and had nothing objectionable in it, but a tiger or two, and a little heat in the warm part of the day. For my own part, I looked on Mr. Jack Maldon as a modern Sindbad, and pictured him the bosom friend of all the Rajahs in the East, sitting under canopies, smoking curly golden pipes--a mile long, if they could be straightened out.\n\nMrs. Strong was a very pretty singer: as I knew, who often heard her singing by herself. But, whether she was afraid of singing before people, or was out of voice that evening, it was certain that she couldn’t sing at all. She tried a duet, once, with her cousin Maldon, but could not so much as begin; and afterwards, when she tried to sing by herself, although she began sweetly, her voice died away on a sudden, and left her quite distressed, with her head hanging down over the keys. The good Doctor said she was nervous, and, to relieve her, proposed a round game at cards; of which he knew as much as of the art of playing the trombone. But I remarked that the Old Soldier took him into custody directly, for her partner; and instructed him, as the first preliminary of initiation, to give her all the silver he had in his pocket.\n\nWe had a merry game, not made the less merry by the Doctor’s mistakes, of which he committed an innumerable quantity, in spite of the watchfulness of the butterflies, and to their great aggravation. Mrs. Strong had declined to play, on the ground of not feeling very well; and her cousin Maldon had excused himself because he had some packing to do. When he had done it, however, he returned, and they sat together, talking, on the sofa. From time to time she came and looked over the Doctor’s hand, and told him what to play. She was very pale, as she bent over him, and I thought her finger trembled as she pointed out the cards; but the Doctor was quite happy in her attention, and took no notice of this, if it were so.\n\nAt supper, we were hardly so gay. Everyone appeared to feel that a parting of that sort was an awkward thing, and that the nearer it approached, the more awkward it was. Mr. Jack Maldon tried to be very talkative, but was not at his ease, and made matters worse. And they were not improved, as it appeared to me, by the Old Soldier: who continually recalled passages of Mr. Jack Maldon’s youth.\n\nThe Doctor, however, who felt, I am sure, that he was making everybody happy, was well pleased, and had no suspicion but that we were all at the utmost height of enjoyment.\n\n‘Annie, my dear,’ said he, looking at his watch, and filling his glass, ‘it is past your cousin Jack’s time, and we must not detain him, since time and tide--both concerned in this case--wait for no man. Mr. Jack Maldon, you have a long voyage, and a strange country, before you; but many men have had both, and many men will have both, to the end of time. The winds you are going to tempt, have wafted thousands upon thousands to fortune, and brought thousands upon thousands happily back.’\n\n‘It’s an affecting thing,’ said Mrs. Markleham--‘however it’s viewed, it’s affecting, to see a fine young man one has known from an infant, going away to the other end of the world, leaving all he knows behind, and not knowing what’s before him. A young man really well deserves constant support and patronage,’ looking at the Doctor, ‘who makes such sacrifices.’\n\n‘Time will go fast with you, Mr. Jack Maldon,’ pursued the Doctor, ‘and fast with all of us. Some of us can hardly expect, perhaps, in the natural course of things, to greet you on your return. The next best thing is to hope to do it, and that’s my case. I shall not weary you with good advice. You have long had a good model before you, in your cousin Annie. Imitate her virtues as nearly as you can.’\n\nMrs. Markleham fanned herself, and shook her head.\n\n‘Farewell, Mr. Jack,’ said the Doctor, standing up; on which we all stood up. ‘A prosperous voyage out, a thriving career abroad, and a happy return home!’\n\nWe all drank the toast, and all shook hands with Mr. Jack Maldon; after which he hastily took leave of the ladies who were there, and hurried to the door, where he was received, as he got into the chaise, with a tremendous broadside of cheers discharged by our boys, who had assembled on the lawn for the purpose. Running in among them to swell the ranks, I was very near the chaise when it rolled away; and I had a lively impression made upon me, in the midst of the noise and dust, of having seen Mr. Jack Maldon rattle past with an agitated face, and something cherry-coloured in his hand.\n\nAfter another broadside for the Doctor, and another for the Doctor’s wife, the boys dispersed, and I went back into the house, where I found the guests all standing in a group about the Doctor, discussing how Mr. Jack Maldon had gone away, and how he had borne it, and how he had felt it, and all the rest of it. In the midst of these remarks, Mrs. Markleham cried: ‘Where’s Annie?’\n\nNo Annie was there; and when they called to her, no Annie replied. But all pressing out of the room, in a crowd, to see what was the matter, we found her lying on the hall floor. There was great alarm at first, until it was found that she was in a swoon, and that the swoon was yielding to the usual means of recovery; when the Doctor, who had lifted her head upon his knee, put her curls aside with his hand, and said, looking around:\n\n‘Poor Annie! She’s so faithful and tender-hearted! It’s the parting from her old playfellow and friend--her favourite cousin--that has done this. Ah! It’s a pity! I am very sorry!’\n\nWhen she opened her eyes, and saw where she was, and that we were all standing about her, she arose with assistance: turning her head, as she did so, to lay it on the Doctor’s shoulder--or to hide it, I don’t know which. We went into the drawing-room, to leave her with the Doctor and her mother; but she said, it seemed, that she was better than she had been since morning, and that she would rather be brought among us; so they brought her in, looking very white and weak, I thought, and sat her on a sofa.\n\n‘Annie, my dear,’ said her mother, doing something to her dress. ‘See here! You have lost a bow. Will anybody be so good as find a ribbon; a cherry-coloured ribbon?’\n\nIt was the one she had worn at her bosom. We all looked for it; I myself looked everywhere, I am certain--but nobody could find it.\n\n‘Do you recollect where you had it last, Annie?’ said her mother.\n\nI wondered how I could have thought she looked white, or anything but burning red, when she answered that she had had it safe, a little while ago, she thought, but it was not worth looking for.\n\nNevertheless, it was looked for again, and still not found. She entreated that there might be no more searching; but it was still sought for, in a desultory way, until she was quite well, and the company took their departure.\n\nWe walked very slowly home, Mr. Wickfield, Agnes, and I--Agnes and I admiring the moonlight, and Mr. Wickfield scarcely raising his eyes from the ground. When we, at last, reached our own door, Agnes discovered that she had left her little reticule behind. Delighted to be of any service to her, I ran back to fetch it.\n\nI went into the supper-room where it had been left, which was deserted and dark. But a door of communication between that and the Doctor’s study, where there was a light, being open, I passed on there, to say what I wanted, and to get a candle.\n\nThe Doctor was sitting in his easy-chair by the fireside, and his young wife was on a stool at his feet. The Doctor, with a complacent smile, was reading aloud some manuscript explanation or statement of a theory out of that interminable Dictionary, and she was looking up at him. But with such a face as I never saw. It was so beautiful in its form, it was so ashy pale, it was so fixed in its abstraction, it was so full of a wild, sleep-walking, dreamy horror of I don’t know what. The eyes were wide open, and her brown hair fell in two rich clusters on her shoulders, and on her white dress, disordered by the want of the lost ribbon. Distinctly as I recollect her look, I cannot say of what it was expressive, I cannot even say of what it is expressive to me now, rising again before my older judgement. Penitence, humiliation, shame, pride, love, and trustfulness--I see them all; and in them all, I see that horror of I don’t know what.\n\nMy entrance, and my saying what I wanted, roused her. It disturbed the Doctor too, for when I went back to replace the candle I had taken from the table, he was patting her head, in his fatherly way, and saying he was a merciless drone to let her tempt him into reading on; and he would have her go to bed.\n\nBut she asked him, in a rapid, urgent manner, to let her stay--to let her feel assured (I heard her murmur some broken words to this effect) that she was in his confidence that night. And, as she turned again towards him, after glancing at me as I left the room and went out at the door, I saw her cross her hands upon his knee, and look up at him with the same face, something quieted, as he resumed his reading.\n\nIt made a great impression on me, and I remembered it a long time afterwards; as I shall have occasion to narrate when the time comes.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. SOMEBODY TURNS UP\n\nIt has not occurred to me to mention Peggotty since I ran away; but, of course, I wrote her a letter almost as soon as I was housed at Dover, and another, and a longer letter, containing all particulars fully related, when my aunt took me formally under her protection. On my being settled at Doctor Strong’s I wrote to her again, detailing my happy condition and prospects. I never could have derived anything like the pleasure from spending the money Mr. Dick had given me, that I felt in sending a gold half-guinea to Peggotty, per post, enclosed in this last letter, to discharge the sum I had borrowed of her: in which epistle, not before, I mentioned about the young man with the donkey-cart.\n\nTo these communications Peggotty replied as promptly, if not as concisely, as a merchant’s clerk. Her utmost powers of expression (which were certainly not great in ink) were exhausted in the attempt to write what she felt on the subject of my journey. Four sides of incoherent and interjectional beginnings of sentences, that had no end, except blots, were inadequate to afford her any relief. But the blots were more expressive to me than the best composition; for they showed me that Peggotty had been crying all over the paper, and what could I have desired more?\n\nI made out, without much difficulty, that she could not take quite kindly to my aunt yet. The notice was too short after so long a prepossession the other way. We never knew a person, she wrote; but to think that Miss Betsey should seem to be so different from what she had been thought to be, was a Moral!--that was her word. She was evidently still afraid of Miss Betsey, for she sent her grateful duty to her but timidly; and she was evidently afraid of me, too, and entertained the probability of my running away again soon: if I might judge from the repeated hints she threw out, that the coach-fare to Yarmouth was always to be had of her for the asking.\n\nShe gave me one piece of intelligence which affected me very much, namely, that there had been a sale of the furniture at our old home, and that Mr. and Miss Murdstone were gone away, and the house was shut up, to be let or sold. God knows I had no part in it while they remained there, but it pained me to think of the dear old place as altogether abandoned; of the weeds growing tall in the garden, and the fallen leaves lying thick and wet upon the paths. I imagined how the winds of winter would howl round it, how the cold rain would beat upon the window-glass, how the moon would make ghosts on the walls of the empty rooms, watching their solitude all night. I thought afresh of the grave in the churchyard, underneath the tree: and it seemed as if the house were dead too, now, and all connected with my father and mother were faded away.\n\nThere was no other news in Peggotty’s letters. Mr. Barkis was an excellent husband, she said, though still a little near; but we all had our faults, and she had plenty (though I am sure I don’t know what they were); and he sent his duty, and my little bedroom was always ready for me. Mr. Peggotty was well, and Ham was well, and Mrs. Gummidge was but poorly, and little Em’ly wouldn’t send her love, but said that Peggotty might send it, if she liked.\n\nAll this intelligence I dutifully imparted to my aunt, only reserving to myself the mention of little Em’ly, to whom I instinctively felt that she would not very tenderly incline. While I was yet new at Doctor Strong’s, she made several excursions over to Canterbury to see me, and always at unseasonable hours: with the view, I suppose, of taking me by surprise. But, finding me well employed, and bearing a good character, and hearing on all hands that I rose fast in the school, she soon discontinued these visits. I saw her on a Saturday, every third or fourth week, when I went over to Dover for a treat; and I saw Mr. Dick every alternate Wednesday, when he arrived by stage-coach at noon, to stay until next morning.\n\nOn these occasions Mr. Dick never travelled without a leathern writing-desk, containing a supply of stationery and the Memorial; in relation to which document he had a notion that time was beginning to press now, and that it really must be got out of hand.\n\nMr. Dick was very partial to gingerbread. To render his visits the more agreeable, my aunt had instructed me to open a credit for him at a cake shop, which was hampered with the stipulation that he should not be served with more than one shilling’s-worth in the course of any one day. This, and the reference of all his little bills at the county inn where he slept, to my aunt, before they were paid, induced me to suspect that he was only allowed to rattle his money, and not to spend it. I found on further investigation that this was so, or at least there was an agreement between him and my aunt that he should account to her for all his disbursements. As he had no idea of deceiving her, and always desired to please her, he was thus made chary of launching into expense. On this point, as well as on all other possible points, Mr. Dick was convinced that my aunt was the wisest and most wonderful of women; as he repeatedly told me with infinite secrecy, and always in a whisper.\n\n‘Trotwood,’ said Mr. Dick, with an air of mystery, after imparting this confidence to me, one Wednesday; ‘who’s the man that hides near our house and frightens her?’\n\n‘Frightens my aunt, sir?’\n\nMr. Dick nodded. ‘I thought nothing would have frightened her,’ he said, ‘for she’s--’ here he whispered softly, ‘don’t mention it--the wisest and most wonderful of women.’ Having said which, he drew back, to observe the effect which this description of her made upon me.\n\n‘The first time he came,’ said Mr. Dick, ‘was--let me see--sixteen hundred and forty-nine was the date of King Charles’s execution. I think you said sixteen hundred and forty-nine?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘I don’t know how it can be,’ said Mr. Dick, sorely puzzled and shaking his head. ‘I don’t think I am as old as that.’\n\n‘Was it in that year that the man appeared, sir?’ I asked.\n\n‘Why, really’ said Mr. Dick, ‘I don’t see how it can have been in that year, Trotwood. Did you get that date out of history?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘I suppose history never lies, does it?’ said Mr. Dick, with a gleam of hope.\n\n‘Oh dear, no, sir!’ I replied, most decisively. I was ingenuous and young, and I thought so.\n\n‘I can’t make it out,’ said Mr. Dick, shaking his head. ‘There’s something wrong, somewhere. However, it was very soon after the mistake was made of putting some of the trouble out of King Charles’s head into my head, that the man first came. I was walking out with Miss Trotwood after tea, just at dark, and there he was, close to our house.’\n\n‘Walking about?’ I inquired.\n\n‘Walking about?’ repeated Mr. Dick. ‘Let me see, I must recollect a bit. N-no, no; he was not walking about.’\n\nI asked, as the shortest way to get at it, what he WAS doing.\n\n‘Well, he wasn’t there at all,’ said Mr. Dick, ‘until he came up behind her, and whispered. Then she turned round and fainted, and I stood still and looked at him, and he walked away; but that he should have been hiding ever since (in the ground or somewhere), is the most extraordinary thing!’\n\n‘HAS he been hiding ever since?’ I asked.\n\n‘To be sure he has,’ retorted Mr. Dick, nodding his head gravely. ‘Never came out, till last night! We were walking last night, and he came up behind her again, and I knew him again.’\n\n‘And did he frighten my aunt again?’\n\n‘All of a shiver,’ said Mr. Dick, counterfeiting that affection and making his teeth chatter. ‘Held by the palings. Cried. But, Trotwood, come here,’ getting me close to him, that he might whisper very softly; ‘why did she give him money, boy, in the moonlight?’\n\n‘He was a beggar, perhaps.’\n\nMr. Dick shook his head, as utterly renouncing the suggestion; and having replied a great many times, and with great confidence, ‘No beggar, no beggar, no beggar, sir!’ went on to say, that from his window he had afterwards, and late at night, seen my aunt give this person money outside the garden rails in the moonlight, who then slunk away--into the ground again, as he thought probable--and was seen no more: while my aunt came hurriedly and secretly back into the house, and had, even that morning, been quite different from her usual self; which preyed on Mr. Dick’s mind.\n\nI had not the least belief, in the outset of this story, that the unknown was anything but a delusion of Mr. Dick’s, and one of the line of that ill-fated Prince who occasioned him so much difficulty; but after some reflection I began to entertain the question whether an attempt, or threat of an attempt, might have been twice made to take poor Mr. Dick himself from under my aunt’s protection, and whether my aunt, the strength of whose kind feeling towards him I knew from herself, might have been induced to pay a price for his peace and quiet. As I was already much attached to Mr. Dick, and very solicitous for his welfare, my fears favoured this supposition; and for a long time his Wednesday hardly ever came round, without my entertaining a misgiving that he would not be on the coach-box as usual. There he always appeared, however, grey-headed, laughing, and happy; and he never had anything more to tell of the man who could frighten my aunt.\n\nThese Wednesdays were the happiest days of Mr. Dick’s life; they were far from being the least happy of mine. He soon became known to every boy in the school; and though he never took an active part in any game but kite-flying, was as deeply interested in all our sports as anyone among us. How often have I seen him, intent upon a match at marbles or pegtop, looking on with a face of unutterable interest, and hardly breathing at the critical times! How often, at hare and hounds, have I seen him mounted on a little knoll, cheering the whole field on to action, and waving his hat above his grey head, oblivious of King Charles the Martyr’s head, and all belonging to it! How many a summer hour have I known to be but blissful minutes to him in the cricket-field! How many winter days have I seen him, standing blue-nosed, in the snow and east wind, looking at the boys going down the long slide, and clapping his worsted gloves in rapture!\n\nHe was an universal favourite, and his ingenuity in little things was transcendent. He could cut oranges into such devices as none of us had an idea of. He could make a boat out of anything, from a skewer upwards. He could turn cramp-bones into chessmen; fashion Roman chariots from old court cards; make spoked wheels out of cotton reels, and bird-cages of old wire. But he was greatest of all, perhaps, in the articles of string and straw; with which we were all persuaded he could do anything that could be done by hands.\n\nMr. Dick’s renown was not long confined to us. After a few Wednesdays, Doctor Strong himself made some inquiries of me about him, and I told him all my aunt had told me; which interested the Doctor so much that he requested, on the occasion of his next visit, to be presented to him. This ceremony I performed; and the Doctor begging Mr. Dick, whensoever he should not find me at the coach office, to come on there, and rest himself until our morning’s work was over, it soon passed into a custom for Mr. Dick to come on as a matter of course, and, if we were a little late, as often happened on a Wednesday, to walk about the courtyard, waiting for me. Here he made the acquaintance of the Doctor’s beautiful young wife (paler than formerly, all this time; more rarely seen by me or anyone, I think; and not so gay, but not less beautiful), and so became more and more familiar by degrees, until, at last, he would come into the school and wait. He always sat in a particular corner, on a particular stool, which was called ‘Dick’, after him; here he would sit, with his grey head bent forward, attentively listening to whatever might be going on, with a profound veneration for the learning he had never been able to acquire.\n\nThis veneration Mr. Dick extended to the Doctor, whom he thought the most subtle and accomplished philosopher of any age. It was long before Mr. Dick ever spoke to him otherwise than bareheaded; and even when he and the Doctor had struck up quite a friendship, and would walk together by the hour, on that side of the courtyard which was known among us as The Doctor’s Walk, Mr. Dick would pull off his hat at intervals to show his respect for wisdom and knowledge. How it ever came about that the Doctor began to read out scraps of the famous Dictionary, in these walks, I never knew; perhaps he felt it all the same, at first, as reading to himself. However, it passed into a custom too; and Mr. Dick, listening with a face shining with pride and pleasure, in his heart of hearts believed the Dictionary to be the most delightful book in the world.\n\nAs I think of them going up and down before those schoolroom windows--the Doctor reading with his complacent smile, an occasional flourish of the manuscript, or grave motion of his head; and Mr. Dick listening, enchained by interest, with his poor wits calmly wandering God knows where, upon the wings of hard words--I think of it as one of the pleasantest things, in a quiet way, that I have ever seen. I feel as if they might go walking to and fro for ever, and the world might somehow be the better for it--as if a thousand things it makes a noise about, were not one half so good for it, or me.\n\nAgnes was one of Mr. Dick’s friends, very soon; and in often coming to the house, he made acquaintance with Uriah. The friendship between himself and me increased continually, and it was maintained on this odd footing: that, while Mr. Dick came professedly to look after me as my guardian, he always consulted me in any little matter of doubt that arose, and invariably guided himself by my advice; not only having a high respect for my native sagacity, but considering that I inherited a good deal from my aunt.\n\nOne Thursday morning, when I was about to walk with Mr. Dick from the hotel to the coach office before going back to school (for we had an hour’s school before breakfast), I met Uriah in the street, who reminded me of the promise I had made to take tea with himself and his mother: adding, with a writhe, ‘But I didn’t expect you to keep it, Master Copperfield, we’re so very umble.’\n\nI really had not yet been able to make up my mind whether I liked Uriah or detested him; and I was very doubtful about it still, as I stood looking him in the face in the street. But I felt it quite an affront to be supposed proud, and said I only wanted to be asked.\n\n‘Oh, if that’s all, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘and it really isn’t our umbleness that prevents you, will you come this evening? But if it is our umbleness, I hope you won’t mind owning to it, Master Copperfield; for we are well aware of our condition.’\n\nI said I would mention it to Mr. Wickfield, and if he approved, as I had no doubt he would, I would come with pleasure. So, at six o’clock that evening, which was one of the early office evenings, I announced myself as ready, to Uriah.\n\n‘Mother will be proud, indeed,’ he said, as we walked away together. ‘Or she would be proud, if it wasn’t sinful, Master Copperfield.’\n\n‘Yet you didn’t mind supposing I was proud this morning,’ I returned.\n\n‘Oh dear, no, Master Copperfield!’ returned Uriah. ‘Oh, believe me, no! Such a thought never came into my head! I shouldn’t have deemed it at all proud if you had thought US too umble for you. Because we are so very umble.’\n\n‘Have you been studying much law lately?’ I asked, to change the subject.\n\n‘Oh, Master Copperfield,’ he said, with an air of self-denial, ‘my reading is hardly to be called study. I have passed an hour or two in the evening, sometimes, with Mr. Tidd.’\n\n‘Rather hard, I suppose?’ said I. ‘He is hard to me sometimes,’ returned Uriah. ‘But I don’t know what he might be to a gifted person.’\n\nAfter beating a little tune on his chin as he walked on, with the two forefingers of his skeleton right hand, he added:\n\n‘There are expressions, you see, Master Copperfield--Latin words and terms--in Mr. Tidd, that are trying to a reader of my umble attainments.’\n\n‘Would you like to be taught Latin?’ I said briskly. ‘I will teach it you with pleasure, as I learn it.’\n\n‘Oh, thank you, Master Copperfield,’ he answered, shaking his head. ‘I am sure it’s very kind of you to make the offer, but I am much too umble to accept it.’\n\n‘What nonsense, Uriah!’\n\n‘Oh, indeed you must excuse me, Master Copperfield! I am greatly obliged, and I should like it of all things, I assure you; but I am far too umble. There are people enough to tread upon me in my lowly state, without my doing outrage to their feelings by possessing learning. Learning ain’t for me. A person like myself had better not aspire. If he is to get on in life, he must get on umbly, Master Copperfield!’\n\nI never saw his mouth so wide, or the creases in his cheeks so deep, as when he delivered himself of these sentiments: shaking his head all the time, and writhing modestly.\n\n‘I think you are wrong, Uriah,’ I said. ‘I dare say there are several things that I could teach you, if you would like to learn them.’\n\n‘Oh, I don’t doubt that, Master Copperfield,’ he answered; ‘not in the least. But not being umble yourself, you don’t judge well, perhaps, for them that are. I won’t provoke my betters with knowledge, thank you. I’m much too umble. Here is my umble dwelling, Master Copperfield!’\n\nWe entered a low, old-fashioned room, walked straight into from the street, and found there Mrs. Heep, who was the dead image of Uriah, only short. She received me with the utmost humility, and apologized to me for giving her son a kiss, observing that, lowly as they were, they had their natural affections, which they hoped would give no offence to anyone. It was a perfectly decent room, half parlour and half kitchen, but not at all a snug room. The tea-things were set upon the table, and the kettle was boiling on the hob. There was a chest of drawers with an escritoire top, for Uriah to read or write at of an evening; there was Uriah’s blue bag lying down and vomiting papers; there was a company of Uriah’s books commanded by Mr. Tidd; there was a corner cupboard: and there were the usual articles of furniture. I don’t remember that any individual object had a bare, pinched, spare look; but I do remember that the whole place had.\n\nIt was perhaps a part of Mrs. Heep’s humility, that she still wore weeds. Notwithstanding the lapse of time that had occurred since Mr. Heep’s decease, she still wore weeds. I think there was some compromise in the cap; but otherwise she was as weedy as in the early days of her mourning.\n\n‘This is a day to be remembered, my Uriah, I am sure,’ said Mrs. Heep, making the tea, ‘when Master Copperfield pays us a visit.’\n\n‘I said you’d think so, mother,’ said Uriah.\n\n‘If I could have wished father to remain among us for any reason,’ said Mrs. Heep, ‘it would have been, that he might have known his company this afternoon.’\n\nI felt embarrassed by these compliments; but I was sensible, too, of being entertained as an honoured guest, and I thought Mrs. Heep an agreeable woman.\n\n‘My Uriah,’ said Mrs. Heep, ‘has looked forward to this, sir, a long while. He had his fears that our umbleness stood in the way, and I joined in them myself. Umble we are, umble we have been, umble we shall ever be,’ said Mrs. Heep.\n\n‘I am sure you have no occasion to be so, ma’am,’ I said, ‘unless you like.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir,’ retorted Mrs. Heep. ‘We know our station and are thankful in it.’\n\nI found that Mrs. Heep gradually got nearer to me, and that Uriah gradually got opposite to me, and that they respectfully plied me with the choicest of the eatables on the table. There was nothing particularly choice there, to be sure; but I took the will for the deed, and felt that they were very attentive. Presently they began to talk about aunts, and then I told them about mine; and about fathers and mothers, and then I told them about mine; and then Mrs. Heep began to talk about fathers-in-law, and then I began to tell her about mine--but stopped, because my aunt had advised me to observe a silence on that subject. A tender young cork, however, would have had no more chance against a pair of corkscrews, or a tender young tooth against a pair of dentists, or a little shuttlecock against two battledores, than I had against Uriah and Mrs. Heep. They did just what they liked with me; and wormed things out of me that I had no desire to tell, with a certainty I blush to think of, the more especially, as in my juvenile frankness, I took some credit to myself for being so confidential and felt that I was quite the patron of my two respectful entertainers.\n\nThey were very fond of one another: that was certain. I take it, that had its effect upon me, as a touch of nature; but the skill with which the one followed up whatever the other said, was a touch of art which I was still less proof against. When there was nothing more to be got out of me about myself (for on the Murdstone and Grinby life, and on my journey, I was dumb), they began about Mr. Wickfield and Agnes. Uriah threw the ball to Mrs. Heep, Mrs. Heep caught it and threw it back to Uriah, Uriah kept it up a little while, then sent it back to Mrs. Heep, and so they went on tossing it about until I had no idea who had got it, and was quite bewildered. The ball itself was always changing too. Now it was Mr. Wickfield, now Agnes, now the excellence of Mr. Wickfield, now my admiration of Agnes; now the extent of Mr. Wickfield’s business and resources, now our domestic life after dinner; now, the wine that Mr. Wickfield took, the reason why he took it, and the pity that it was he took so much; now one thing, now another, then everything at once; and all the time, without appearing to speak very often, or to do anything but sometimes encourage them a little, for fear they should be overcome by their humility and the honour of my company, I found myself perpetually letting out something or other that I had no business to let out and seeing the effect of it in the twinkling of Uriah’s dinted nostrils.\n\nI had begun to be a little uncomfortable, and to wish myself well out of the visit, when a figure coming down the street passed the door--it stood open to air the room, which was warm, the weather being close for the time of year--came back again, looked in, and walked in, exclaiming loudly, ‘Copperfield! Is it possible?’\n\nIt was Mr. Micawber! It was Mr. Micawber, with his eye-glass, and his walking-stick, and his shirt-collar, and his genteel air, and the condescending roll in his voice, all complete!\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, putting out his hand, ‘this is indeed a meeting which is calculated to impress the mind with a sense of the instability and uncertainty of all human--in short, it is a most extraordinary meeting. Walking along the street, reflecting upon the probability of something turning up (of which I am at present rather sanguine), I find a young but valued friend turn up, who is connected with the most eventful period of my life; I may say, with the turning-point of my existence. Copperfield, my dear fellow, how do you do?’\n\nI cannot say--I really cannot say--that I was glad to see Mr. Micawber there; but I was glad to see him too, and shook hands with him, heartily, inquiring how Mrs. Micawber was.\n\n‘Thank you,’ said Mr. Micawber, waving his hand as of old, and settling his chin in his shirt-collar. ‘She is tolerably convalescent. The twins no longer derive their sustenance from Nature’s founts--in short,’ said Mr. Micawber, in one of his bursts of confidence, ‘they are weaned--and Mrs. Micawber is, at present, my travelling companion. She will be rejoiced, Copperfield, to renew her acquaintance with one who has proved himself in all respects a worthy minister at the sacred altar of friendship.’\n\nI said I should be delighted to see her.\n\n‘You are very good,’ said Mr. Micawber.\n\nMr. Micawber then smiled, settled his chin again, and looked about him.\n\n‘I have discovered my friend Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber genteelly, and without addressing himself particularly to anyone, ‘not in solitude, but partaking of a social meal in company with a widow lady, and one who is apparently her offspring--in short,’ said Mr. Micawber, in another of his bursts of confidence, ‘her son. I shall esteem it an honour to be presented.’\n\nI could do no less, under these circumstances, than make Mr. Micawber known to Uriah Heep and his mother; which I accordingly did. As they abased themselves before him, Mr. Micawber took a seat, and waved his hand in his most courtly manner.\n\n‘Any friend of my friend Copperfield’s,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘has a personal claim upon myself.’\n\n‘We are too umble, sir,’ said Mrs. Heep, ‘my son and me, to be the friends of Master Copperfield. He has been so good as take his tea with us, and we are thankful to him for his company, also to you, sir, for your notice.’\n\n‘Ma’am,’ returned Mr. Micawber, with a bow, ‘you are very obliging: and what are you doing, Copperfield? Still in the wine trade?’\n\nI was excessively anxious to get Mr. Micawber away; and replied, with my hat in my hand, and a very red face, I have no doubt, that I was a pupil at Doctor Strong’s.\n\n‘A pupil?’ said Mr. Micawber, raising his eyebrows. ‘I am extremely happy to hear it. Although a mind like my friend Copperfield’s’--to Uriah and Mrs. Heep--‘does not require that cultivation which, without his knowledge of men and things, it would require, still it is a rich soil teeming with latent vegetation--in short,’ said Mr. Micawber, smiling, in another burst of confidence, ‘it is an intellect capable of getting up the classics to any extent.’\n\nUriah, with his long hands slowly twining over one another, made a ghastly writhe from the waist upwards, to express his concurrence in this estimation of me.\n\n‘Shall we go and see Mrs. Micawber, sir?’ I said, to get Mr. Micawber away.\n\n‘If you will do her that favour, Copperfield,’ replied Mr. Micawber, rising. ‘I have no scruple in saying, in the presence of our friends here, that I am a man who has, for some years, contended against the pressure of pecuniary difficulties.’ I knew he was certain to say something of this kind; he always would be so boastful about his difficulties. ‘Sometimes I have risen superior to my difficulties. Sometimes my difficulties have--in short, have floored me. There have been times when I have administered a succession of facers to them; there have been times when they have been too many for me, and I have given in, and said to Mrs. Micawber, in the words of Cato, “Plato, thou reasonest well. It’s all up now. I can show fight no more.” But at no time of my life,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘have I enjoyed a higher degree of satisfaction than in pouring my griefs (if I may describe difficulties, chiefly arising out of warrants of attorney and promissory notes at two and four months, by that word) into the bosom of my friend Copperfield.’\n\nMr. Micawber closed this handsome tribute by saying, ‘Mr. Heep! Good evening. Mrs. Heep! Your servant,’ and then walking out with me in his most fashionable manner, making a good deal of noise on the pavement with his shoes, and humming a tune as we went.\n\nIt was a little inn where Mr. Micawber put up, and he occupied a little room in it, partitioned off from the commercial room, and strongly flavoured with tobacco-smoke. I think it was over the kitchen, because a warm greasy smell appeared to come up through the chinks in the floor, and there was a flabby perspiration on the walls. I know it was near the bar, on account of the smell of spirits and jingling of glasses. Here, recumbent on a small sofa, underneath a picture of a race-horse, with her head close to the fire, and her feet pushing the mustard off the dumb-waiter at the other end of the room, was Mrs. Micawber, to whom Mr. Micawber entered first, saying, ‘My dear, allow me to introduce to you a pupil of Doctor Strong’s.’\n\nI noticed, by the by, that although Mr. Micawber was just as much confused as ever about my age and standing, he always remembered, as a genteel thing, that I was a pupil of Doctor Strong’s.\n\nMrs. Micawber was amazed, but very glad to see me. I was very glad to see her too, and, after an affectionate greeting on both sides, sat down on the small sofa near her.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘if you will mention to Copperfield what our present position is, which I have no doubt he will like to know, I will go and look at the paper the while, and see whether anything turns up among the advertisements.’\n\n‘I thought you were at Plymouth, ma’am,’ I said to Mrs. Micawber, as he went out.\n\n‘My dear Master Copperfield,’ she replied, ‘we went to Plymouth.’\n\n‘To be on the spot,’ I hinted.\n\n‘Just so,’ said Mrs. Micawber. ‘To be on the spot. But, the truth is, talent is not wanted in the Custom House. The local influence of my family was quite unavailing to obtain any employment in that department, for a man of Mr. Micawber’s abilities. They would rather NOT have a man of Mr. Micawber’s abilities. He would only show the deficiency of the others. Apart from which,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘I will not disguise from you, my dear Master Copperfield, that when that branch of my family which is settled in Plymouth, became aware that Mr. Micawber was accompanied by myself, and by little Wilkins and his sister, and by the twins, they did not receive him with that ardour which he might have expected, being so newly released from captivity. In fact,’ said Mrs. Micawber, lowering her voice,--‘this is between ourselves--our reception was cool.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ I said.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mrs. Micawber. ‘It is truly painful to contemplate mankind in such an aspect, Master Copperfield, but our reception was, decidedly, cool. There is no doubt about it. In fact, that branch of my family which is settled in Plymouth became quite personal to Mr. Micawber, before we had been there a week.’\n\nI said, and thought, that they ought to be ashamed of themselves.\n\n‘Still, so it was,’ continued Mrs. Micawber. ‘Under such circumstances, what could a man of Mr. Micawber’s spirit do? But one obvious course was left. To borrow, of that branch of my family, the money to return to London, and to return at any sacrifice.’\n\n‘Then you all came back again, ma’am?’ I said.\n\n‘We all came back again,’ replied Mrs. Micawber. ‘Since then, I have consulted other branches of my family on the course which it is most expedient for Mr. Micawber to take--for I maintain that he must take some course, Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, argumentatively. ‘It is clear that a family of six, not including a domestic, cannot live upon air.’\n\n‘Certainly, ma’am,’ said I.\n\n‘The opinion of those other branches of my family,’ pursued Mrs. Micawber, ‘is, that Mr. Micawber should immediately turn his attention to coals.’\n\n‘To what, ma’am?’\n\n‘To coals,’ said Mrs. Micawber. ‘To the coal trade. Mr. Micawber was induced to think, on inquiry, that there might be an opening for a man of his talent in the Medway Coal Trade. Then, as Mr. Micawber very properly said, the first step to be taken clearly was, to come and see the Medway. Which we came and saw. I say “we”, Master Copperfield; for I never will,’ said Mrs. Micawber with emotion, ‘I never will desert Mr. Micawber.’\n\nI murmured my admiration and approbation.\n\n‘We came,’ repeated Mrs. Micawber, ‘and saw the Medway. My opinion of the coal trade on that river is, that it may require talent, but that it certainly requires capital. Talent, Mr. Micawber has; capital, Mr. Micawber has not. We saw, I think, the greater part of the Medway; and that is my individual conclusion. Being so near here, Mr. Micawber was of opinion that it would be rash not to come on, and see the Cathedral. Firstly, on account of its being so well worth seeing, and our never having seen it; and secondly, on account of the great probability of something turning up in a cathedral town. We have been here,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘three days. Nothing has, as yet, turned up; and it may not surprise you, my dear Master Copperfield, so much as it would a stranger, to know that we are at present waiting for a remittance from London, to discharge our pecuniary obligations at this hotel. Until the arrival of that remittance,’ said Mrs. Micawber with much feeling, ‘I am cut off from my home (I allude to lodgings in Pentonville), from my boy and girl, and from my twins.’\n\nI felt the utmost sympathy for Mr. and Mrs. Micawber in this anxious extremity, and said as much to Mr. Micawber, who now returned: adding that I only wished I had money enough, to lend them the amount they needed. Mr. Micawber’s answer expressed the disturbance of his mind. He said, shaking hands with me, ‘Copperfield, you are a true friend; but when the worst comes to the worst, no man is without a friend who is possessed of shaving materials.’ At this dreadful hint Mrs. Micawber threw her arms round Mr. Micawber’s neck and entreated him to be calm. He wept; but so far recovered, almost immediately, as to ring the bell for the waiter, and bespeak a hot kidney pudding and a plate of shrimps for breakfast in the morning.\n\nWhen I took my leave of them, they both pressed me so much to come and dine before they went away, that I could not refuse. But, as I knew I could not come next day, when I should have a good deal to prepare in the evening, Mr. Micawber arranged that he would call at Doctor Strong’s in the course of the morning (having a presentiment that the remittance would arrive by that post), and propose the day after, if it would suit me better. Accordingly I was called out of school next forenoon, and found Mr. Micawber in the parlour; who had called to say that the dinner would take place as proposed. When I asked him if the remittance had come, he pressed my hand and departed.\n\nAs I was looking out of window that same evening, it surprised me, and made me rather uneasy, to see Mr. Micawber and Uriah Heep walk past, arm in arm: Uriah humbly sensible of the honour that was done him, and Mr. Micawber taking a bland delight in extending his patronage to Uriah. But I was still more surprised, when I went to the little hotel next day at the appointed dinner-hour, which was four o’clock, to find, from what Mr. Micawber said, that he had gone home with Uriah, and had drunk brandy-and-water at Mrs. Heep’s.\n\n‘And I’ll tell you what, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘your friend Heep is a young fellow who might be attorney-general. If I had known that young man, at the period when my difficulties came to a crisis, all I can say is, that I believe my creditors would have been a great deal better managed than they were.’\n\nI hardly understood how this could have been, seeing that Mr. Micawber had paid them nothing at all as it was; but I did not like to ask. Neither did I like to say, that I hoped he had not been too communicative to Uriah; or to inquire if they had talked much about me. I was afraid of hurting Mr. Micawber’s feelings, or, at all events, Mrs. Micawber’s, she being very sensitive; but I was uncomfortable about it, too, and often thought about it afterwards.\n\nWe had a beautiful little dinner. Quite an elegant dish of fish; the kidney-end of a loin of veal, roasted; fried sausage-meat; a partridge, and a pudding. There was wine, and there was strong ale; and after dinner Mrs. Micawber made us a bowl of hot punch with her own hands.\n\nMr. Micawber was uncommonly convivial. I never saw him such good company. He made his face shine with the punch, so that it looked as if it had been varnished all over. He got cheerfully sentimental about the town, and proposed success to it; observing that Mrs. Micawber and himself had been made extremely snug and comfortable there and that he never should forget the agreeable hours they had passed in Canterbury. He proposed me afterwards; and he, and Mrs. Micawber, and I, took a review of our past acquaintance, in the course of which we sold the property all over again. Then I proposed Mrs. Micawber: or, at least, said, modestly, ‘If you’ll allow me, Mrs. Micawber, I shall now have the pleasure of drinking your health, ma’am.’ On which Mr. Micawber delivered an eulogium on Mrs. Micawber’s character, and said she had ever been his guide, philosopher, and friend, and that he would recommend me, when I came to a marrying time of life, to marry such another woman, if such another woman could be found.\n\nAs the punch disappeared, Mr. Micawber became still more friendly and convivial. Mrs. Micawber’s spirits becoming elevated, too, we sang ‘Auld Lang Syne’. When we came to ‘Here’s a hand, my trusty frere’, we all joined hands round the table; and when we declared we would ‘take a right gude Willie Waught’, and hadn’t the least idea what it meant, we were really affected.\n\nIn a word, I never saw anybody so thoroughly jovial as Mr. Micawber was, down to the very last moment of the evening, when I took a hearty farewell of himself and his amiable wife. Consequently, I was not prepared, at seven o’clock next morning, to receive the following communication, dated half past nine in the evening; a quarter of an hour after I had left him:--\n\n‘The die is cast--all is over. Hiding the ravages of care with a sickly mask of mirth, I have not informed you, this evening, that there is no hope of the remittance! Under these circumstances, alike humiliating to endure, humiliating to contemplate, and humiliating to relate, I have discharged the pecuniary liability contracted at this establishment, by giving a note of hand, made payable fourteen days after date, at my residence, Pentonville, London. When it becomes due, it will not be taken up. The result is destruction. The bolt is impending, and the tree must fall.\n\n‘Let the wretched man who now addresses you, my dear Copperfield, be a beacon to you through life. He writes with that intention, and in that hope. If he could think himself of so much use, one gleam of day might, by possibility, penetrate into the cheerless dungeon of his remaining existence--though his longevity is, at present (to say the least of it), extremely problematical.\n\n‘This is the last communication, my dear Copperfield, you will ever receive\n\n‘From\n‘The\n\n‘Beggared Outcast,\n\nI was so shocked by the contents of this heart-rending letter, that I ran off directly towards the little hotel with the intention of taking it on my way to Doctor Strong’s, and trying to soothe Mr. Micawber with a word of comfort. But, half-way there, I met the London coach with Mr. and Mrs. Micawber up behind; Mr. Micawber, the very picture of tranquil enjoyment, smiling at Mrs. Micawber’s conversation, eating walnuts out of a paper bag, with a bottle sticking out of his breast pocket. As they did not see me, I thought it best, all things considered, not to see them. So, with a great weight taken off my mind, I turned into a by-street that was the nearest way to school, and felt, upon the whole, relieved that they were gone; though I still liked them very much, nevertheless.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. A RETROSPECT\n\nMy school-days! The silent gliding on of my existence--the unseen, unfelt progress of my life--from childhood up to youth! Let me think, as I look back upon that flowing water, now a dry channel overgrown with leaves, whether there are any marks along its course, by which I can remember how it ran.\n\nA moment, and I occupy my place in the Cathedral, where we all went together, every Sunday morning, assembling first at school for that purpose. The earthy smell, the sunless air, the sensation of the world being shut out, the resounding of the organ through the black and white arched galleries and aisles, are wings that take me back, and hold me hovering above those days, in a half-sleeping and half-waking dream.\n\nI am not the last boy in the school. I have risen in a few months, over several heads. But the first boy seems to me a mighty creature, dwelling afar off, whose giddy height is unattainable. Agnes says ‘No,’ but I say ‘Yes,’ and tell her that she little thinks what stores of knowledge have been mastered by the wonderful Being, at whose place she thinks I, even I, weak aspirant, may arrive in time. He is not my private friend and public patron, as Steerforth was, but I hold him in a reverential respect. I chiefly wonder what he’ll be, when he leaves Doctor Strong’s, and what mankind will do to maintain any place against him.\n\nBut who is this that breaks upon me? This is Miss Shepherd, whom I love.\n\nMiss Shepherd is a boarder at the Misses Nettingalls’ establishment. I adore Miss Shepherd. She is a little girl, in a spencer, with a round face and curly flaxen hair. The Misses Nettingalls’ young ladies come to the Cathedral too. I cannot look upon my book, for I must look upon Miss Shepherd. When the choristers chaunt, I hear Miss Shepherd. In the service I mentally insert Miss Shepherd’s name--I put her in among the Royal Family. At home, in my own room, I am sometimes moved to cry out, ‘Oh, Miss Shepherd!’ in a transport of love.\n\nFor some time, I am doubtful of Miss Shepherd’s feelings, but, at length, Fate being propitious, we meet at the dancing-school. I have Miss Shepherd for my partner. I touch Miss Shepherd’s glove, and feel a thrill go up the right arm of my jacket, and come out at my hair. I say nothing to Miss Shepherd, but we understand each other. Miss Shepherd and myself live but to be united.\n\nWhy do I secretly give Miss Shepherd twelve Brazil nuts for a present, I wonder? They are not expressive of affection, they are difficult to pack into a parcel of any regular shape, they are hard to crack, even in room doors, and they are oily when cracked; yet I feel that they are appropriate to Miss Shepherd. Soft, seedy biscuits, also, I bestow upon Miss Shepherd; and oranges innumerable. Once, I kiss Miss Shepherd in the cloak-room. Ecstasy! What are my agony and indignation next day, when I hear a flying rumour that the Misses Nettingall have stood Miss Shepherd in the stocks for turning in her toes!\n\nMiss Shepherd being the one pervading theme and vision of my life, how do I ever come to break with her? I can’t conceive. And yet a coolness grows between Miss Shepherd and myself. Whispers reach me of Miss Shepherd having said she wished I wouldn’t stare so, and having avowed a preference for Master Jones--for Jones! a boy of no merit whatever! The gulf between me and Miss Shepherd widens. At last, one day, I meet the Misses Nettingalls’ establishment out walking. Miss Shepherd makes a face as she goes by, and laughs to her companion. All is over. The devotion of a life--it seems a life, it is all the same--is at an end; Miss Shepherd comes out of the morning service, and the Royal Family know her no more.\n\nI am higher in the school, and no one breaks my peace. I am not at all polite, now, to the Misses Nettingalls’ young ladies, and shouldn’t dote on any of them, if they were twice as many and twenty times as beautiful. I think the dancing-school a tiresome affair, and wonder why the girls can’t dance by themselves and leave us alone. I am growing great in Latin verses, and neglect the laces of my boots. Doctor Strong refers to me in public as a promising young scholar. Mr. Dick is wild with joy, and my aunt remits me a guinea by the next post.\n\nThe shade of a young butcher rises, like the apparition of an armed head in Macbeth. Who is this young butcher? He is the terror of the youth of Canterbury. There is a vague belief abroad, that the beef suet with which he anoints his hair gives him unnatural strength, and that he is a match for a man. He is a broad-faced, bull-necked, young butcher, with rough red cheeks, an ill-conditioned mind, and an injurious tongue. His main use of this tongue, is, to disparage Doctor Strong’s young gentlemen. He says, publicly, that if they want anything he’ll give it ‘em. He names individuals among them (myself included), whom he could undertake to settle with one hand, and the other tied behind him. He waylays the smaller boys to punch their unprotected heads, and calls challenges after me in the open streets. For these sufficient reasons I resolve to fight the butcher.\n\nIt is a summer evening, down in a green hollow, at the corner of a wall. I meet the butcher by appointment. I am attended by a select body of our boys; the butcher, by two other butchers, a young publican, and a sweep. The preliminaries are adjusted, and the butcher and myself stand face to face. In a moment the butcher lights ten thousand candles out of my left eyebrow. In another moment, I don’t know where the wall is, or where I am, or where anybody is. I hardly know which is myself and which the butcher, we are always in such a tangle and tussle, knocking about upon the trodden grass. Sometimes I see the butcher, bloody but confident; sometimes I see nothing, and sit gasping on my second’s knee; sometimes I go in at the butcher madly, and cut my knuckles open against his face, without appearing to discompose him at all. At last I awake, very queer about the head, as from a giddy sleep, and see the butcher walking off, congratulated by the two other butchers and the sweep and publican, and putting on his coat as he goes; from which I augur, justly, that the victory is his.\n\nI am taken home in a sad plight, and I have beef-steaks put to my eyes, and am rubbed with vinegar and brandy, and find a great puffy place bursting out on my upper lip, which swells immoderately. For three or four days I remain at home, a very ill-looking subject, with a green shade over my eyes; and I should be very dull, but that Agnes is a sister to me, and condoles with me, and reads to me, and makes the time light and happy. Agnes has my confidence completely, always; I tell her all about the butcher, and the wrongs he has heaped upon me; she thinks I couldn’t have done otherwise than fight the butcher, while she shrinks and trembles at my having fought him.\n\nTime has stolen on unobserved, for Adams is not the head-boy in the days that are come now, nor has he been this many and many a day. Adams has left the school so long, that when he comes back, on a visit to Doctor Strong, there are not many there, besides myself, who know him. Adams is going to be called to the bar almost directly, and is to be an advocate, and to wear a wig. I am surprised to find him a meeker man than I had thought, and less imposing in appearance. He has not staggered the world yet, either; for it goes on (as well as I can make out) pretty much the same as if he had never joined it.\n\nA blank, through which the warriors of poetry and history march on in stately hosts that seem to have no end--and what comes next! I am the head-boy, now! I look down on the line of boys below me, with a condescending interest in such of them as bring to my mind the boy I was myself, when I first came there. That little fellow seems to be no part of me; I remember him as something left behind upon the road of life--as something I have passed, rather than have actually been--and almost think of him as of someone else.\n\nAnd the little girl I saw on that first day at Mr. Wickfield’s, where is she? Gone also. In her stead, the perfect likeness of the picture, a child likeness no more, moves about the house; and Agnes--my sweet sister, as I call her in my thoughts, my counsellor and friend, the better angel of the lives of all who come within her calm, good, self-denying influence--is quite a woman.\n\nWhat other changes have come upon me, besides the changes in my growth and looks, and in the knowledge I have garnered all this while? I wear a gold watch and chain, a ring upon my little finger, and a long-tailed coat; and I use a great deal of bear’s grease--which, taken in conjunction with the ring, looks bad. Am I in love again? I am. I worship the eldest Miss Larkins.\n\nThe eldest Miss Larkins is not a little girl. She is a tall, dark, black-eyed, fine figure of a woman. The eldest Miss Larkins is not a chicken; for the youngest Miss Larkins is not that, and the eldest must be three or four years older. Perhaps the eldest Miss Larkins may be about thirty. My passion for her is beyond all bounds.\n\nThe eldest Miss Larkins knows officers. It is an awful thing to bear. I see them speaking to her in the street. I see them cross the way to meet her, when her bonnet (she has a bright taste in bonnets) is seen coming down the pavement, accompanied by her sister’s bonnet. She laughs and talks, and seems to like it. I spend a good deal of my own spare time in walking up and down to meet her. If I can bow to her once in the day (I know her to bow to, knowing Mr. Larkins), I am happier. I deserve a bow now and then. The raging agonies I suffer on the night of the Race Ball, where I know the eldest Miss Larkins will be dancing with the military, ought to have some compensation, if there be even-handed justice in the world.\n\nMy passion takes away my appetite, and makes me wear my newest silk neckerchief continually. I have no relief but in putting on my best clothes, and having my boots cleaned over and over again. I seem, then, to be worthier of the eldest Miss Larkins. Everything that belongs to her, or is connected with her, is precious to me. Mr. Larkins (a gruff old gentleman with a double chin, and one of his eyes immovable in his head) is fraught with interest to me. When I can’t meet his daughter, I go where I am likely to meet him. To say ‘How do you do, Mr. Larkins? Are the young ladies and all the family quite well?’ seems so pointed, that I blush.\n\nI think continually about my age. Say I am seventeen, and say that seventeen is young for the eldest Miss Larkins, what of that? Besides, I shall be one-and-twenty in no time almost. I regularly take walks outside Mr. Larkins’s house in the evening, though it cuts me to the heart to see the officers go in, or to hear them up in the drawing-room, where the eldest Miss Larkins plays the harp. I even walk, on two or three occasions, in a sickly, spoony manner, round and round the house after the family are gone to bed, wondering which is the eldest Miss Larkins’s chamber (and pitching, I dare say now, on Mr. Larkins’s instead); wishing that a fire would burst out; that the assembled crowd would stand appalled; that I, dashing through them with a ladder, might rear it against her window, save her in my arms, go back for something she had left behind, and perish in the flames. For I am generally disinterested in my love, and think I could be content to make a figure before Miss Larkins, and expire.\n\nGenerally, but not always. Sometimes brighter visions rise before me. When I dress (the occupation of two hours), for a great ball given at the Larkins’s (the anticipation of three weeks), I indulge my fancy with pleasing images. I picture myself taking courage to make a declaration to Miss Larkins. I picture Miss Larkins sinking her head upon my shoulder, and saying, ‘Oh, Mr. Copperfield, can I believe my ears!’ I picture Mr. Larkins waiting on me next morning, and saying, ‘My dear Copperfield, my daughter has told me all. Youth is no objection. Here are twenty thousand pounds. Be happy!’ I picture my aunt relenting, and blessing us; and Mr. Dick and Doctor Strong being present at the marriage ceremony. I am a sensible fellow, I believe--I believe, on looking back, I mean--and modest I am sure; but all this goes on notwithstanding. I repair to the enchanted house, where there are lights, chattering, music, flowers, officers (I am sorry to see), and the eldest Miss Larkins, a blaze of beauty. She is dressed in blue, with blue flowers in her hair--forget-me-nots--as if SHE had any need to wear forget-me-nots. It is the first really grown-up party that I have ever been invited to, and I am a little uncomfortable; for I appear not to belong to anybody, and nobody appears to have anything to say to me, except Mr. Larkins, who asks me how my schoolfellows are, which he needn’t do, as I have not come there to be insulted.\n\nBut after I have stood in the doorway for some time, and feasted my eyes upon the goddess of my heart, she approaches me--she, the eldest Miss Larkins!--and asks me pleasantly, if I dance?\n\nI stammer, with a bow, ‘With you, Miss Larkins.’\n\n‘With no one else?’ inquires Miss Larkins.\n\n‘I should have no pleasure in dancing with anyone else.’\n\nMiss Larkins laughs and blushes (or I think she blushes), and says, ‘Next time but one, I shall be very glad.’\n\nThe time arrives. ‘It is a waltz, I think,’ Miss Larkins doubtfully observes, when I present myself. ‘Do you waltz? If not, Captain Bailey--’\n\nBut I do waltz (pretty well, too, as it happens), and I take Miss Larkins out. I take her sternly from the side of Captain Bailey. He is wretched, I have no doubt; but he is nothing to me. I have been wretched, too. I waltz with the eldest Miss Larkins! I don’t know where, among whom, or how long. I only know that I swim about in space, with a blue angel, in a state of blissful delirium, until I find myself alone with her in a little room, resting on a sofa. She admires a flower (pink camellia japonica, price half-a-crown), in my button-hole. I give it her, and say:\n\n‘I ask an inestimable price for it, Miss Larkins.’\n\n‘Indeed! What is that?’ returns Miss Larkins.\n\n‘A flower of yours, that I may treasure it as a miser does gold.’\n\n‘You’re a bold boy,’ says Miss Larkins. ‘There.’\n\nShe gives it me, not displeased; and I put it to my lips, and then into my breast. Miss Larkins, laughing, draws her hand through my arm, and says, ‘Now take me back to Captain Bailey.’\n\nI am lost in the recollection of this delicious interview, and the waltz, when she comes to me again, with a plain elderly gentleman who has been playing whist all night, upon her arm, and says:\n\n‘Oh! here is my bold friend! Mr. Chestle wants to know you, Mr. Copperfield.’\n\nI feel at once that he is a friend of the family, and am much gratified.\n\n‘I admire your taste, sir,’ says Mr. Chestle. ‘It does you credit. I suppose you don’t take much interest in hops; but I am a pretty large grower myself; and if you ever like to come over to our neighbourhood--neighbourhood of Ashford--and take a run about our place,--we shall be glad for you to stop as long as you like.’\n\nI thank Mr. Chestle warmly, and shake hands. I think I am in a happy dream. I waltz with the eldest Miss Larkins once again. She says I waltz so well! I go home in a state of unspeakable bliss, and waltz in imagination, all night long, with my arm round the blue waist of my dear divinity. For some days afterwards, I am lost in rapturous reflections; but I neither see her in the street, nor when I call. I am imperfectly consoled for this disappointment by the sacred pledge, the perished flower.\n\n‘Trotwood,’ says Agnes, one day after dinner. ‘Who do you think is going to be married tomorrow? Someone you admire.’\n\n‘Not you, I suppose, Agnes?’\n\n‘Not me!’ raising her cheerful face from the music she is copying. ‘Do you hear him, Papa?--The eldest Miss Larkins.’\n\n‘To--to Captain Bailey?’ I have just enough power to ask.\n\n‘No; to no Captain. To Mr. Chestle, a hop-grower.’\n\nI am terribly dejected for about a week or two. I take off my ring, I wear my worst clothes, I use no bear’s grease, and I frequently lament over the late Miss Larkins’s faded flower. Being, by that time, rather tired of this kind of life, and having received new provocation from the butcher, I throw the flower away, go out with the butcher, and gloriously defeat him.\n\nThis, and the resumption of my ring, as well as of the bear’s grease in moderation, are the last marks I can discern, now, in my progress to seventeen.\n\n# CHAPTER 19. I LOOK ABOUT ME, AND MAKE A DISCOVERY\n\nI am doubtful whether I was at heart glad or sorry, when my school-days drew to an end, and the time came for my leaving Doctor Strong’s. I had been very happy there, I had a great attachment for the Doctor, and I was eminent and distinguished in that little world. For these reasons I was sorry to go; but for other reasons, unsubstantial enough, I was glad. Misty ideas of being a young man at my own disposal, of the importance attaching to a young man at his own disposal, of the wonderful things to be seen and done by that magnificent animal, and the wonderful effects he could not fail to make upon society, lured me away. So powerful were these visionary considerations in my boyish mind, that I seem, according to my present way of thinking, to have left school without natural regret. The separation has not made the impression on me, that other separations have. I try in vain to recall how I felt about it, and what its circumstances were; but it is not momentous in my recollection. I suppose the opening prospect confused me. I know that my juvenile experiences went for little or nothing then; and that life was more like a great fairy story, which I was just about to begin to read, than anything else.\n\nMy aunt and I had held many grave deliberations on the calling to which I should be devoted. For a year or more I had endeavoured to find a satisfactory answer to her often-repeated question, ‘What I would like to be?’ But I had no particular liking, that I could discover, for anything. If I could have been inspired with a knowledge of the science of navigation, taken the command of a fast-sailing expedition, and gone round the world on a triumphant voyage of discovery, I think I might have considered myself completely suited. But, in the absence of any such miraculous provision, my desire was to apply myself to some pursuit that would not lie too heavily upon her purse; and to do my duty in it, whatever it might be.\n\nMr. Dick had regularly assisted at our councils, with a meditative and sage demeanour. He never made a suggestion but once; and on that occasion (I don’t know what put it in his head), he suddenly proposed that I should be ‘a Brazier’. My aunt received this proposal so very ungraciously, that he never ventured on a second; but ever afterwards confined himself to looking watchfully at her for her suggestions, and rattling his money.\n\n‘Trot, I tell you what, my dear,’ said my aunt, one morning in the Christmas season when I left school: ‘as this knotty point is still unsettled, and as we must not make a mistake in our decision if we can help it, I think we had better take a little breathing-time. In the meanwhile, you must try to look at it from a new point of view, and not as a schoolboy.’\n\n‘I will, aunt.’\n\n‘It has occurred to me,’ pursued my aunt, ‘that a little change, and a glimpse of life out of doors, may be useful in helping you to know your own mind, and form a cooler judgement. Suppose you were to go down into the old part of the country again, for instance, and see that--that out-of-the-way woman with the savagest of names,’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose, for she could never thoroughly forgive Peggotty for being so called.\n\n‘Of all things in the world, aunt, I should like it best!’\n\n‘Well,’ said my aunt, ‘that’s lucky, for I should like it too. But it’s natural and rational that you should like it. And I am very well persuaded that whatever you do, Trot, will always be natural and rational.’\n\n‘I hope so, aunt.’\n\n‘Your sister, Betsey Trotwood,’ said my aunt, ‘would have been as natural and rational a girl as ever breathed. You’ll be worthy of her, won’t you?’\n\n‘I hope I shall be worthy of YOU, aunt. That will be enough for me.’\n\n‘It’s a mercy that poor dear baby of a mother of yours didn’t live,’ said my aunt, looking at me approvingly, ‘or she’d have been so vain of her boy by this time, that her soft little head would have been completely turned, if there was anything of it left to turn.’ (My aunt always excused any weakness of her own in my behalf, by transferring it in this way to my poor mother.) ‘Bless me, Trotwood, how you do remind me of her!’\n\n‘Pleasantly, I hope, aunt?’ said I.\n\n‘He’s as like her, Dick,’ said my aunt, emphatically, ‘he’s as like her, as she was that afternoon before she began to fret--bless my heart, he’s as like her, as he can look at me out of his two eyes!’\n\n‘Is he indeed?’ said Mr. Dick.\n\n‘And he’s like David, too,’ said my aunt, decisively.\n\n‘He is very like David!’ said Mr. Dick.\n\n‘But what I want you to be, Trot,’ resumed my aunt, ‘--I don’t mean physically, but morally; you are very well physically--is, a firm fellow. A fine firm fellow, with a will of your own. With resolution,’ said my aunt, shaking her cap at me, and clenching her hand. ‘With determination. With character, Trot--with strength of character that is not to be influenced, except on good reason, by anybody, or by anything. That’s what I want you to be. That’s what your father and mother might both have been, Heaven knows, and been the better for it.’\n\nI intimated that I hoped I should be what she described.\n\n‘That you may begin, in a small way, to have a reliance upon yourself, and to act for yourself,’ said my aunt, ‘I shall send you upon your trip, alone. I did think, once, of Mr. Dick’s going with you; but, on second thoughts, I shall keep him to take care of me.’\n\nMr. Dick, for a moment, looked a little disappointed; until the honour and dignity of having to take care of the most wonderful woman in the world, restored the sunshine to his face.\n\n‘Besides,’ said my aunt, ‘there’s the Memorial--’\n\n‘Oh, certainly,’ said Mr. Dick, in a hurry, ‘I intend, Trotwood, to get that done immediately--it really must be done immediately! And then it will go in, you know--and then--’ said Mr. Dick, after checking himself, and pausing a long time, ‘there’ll be a pretty kettle of fish!’\n\nIn pursuance of my aunt’s kind scheme, I was shortly afterwards fitted out with a handsome purse of money, and a portmanteau, and tenderly dismissed upon my expedition. At parting, my aunt gave me some good advice, and a good many kisses; and said that as her object was that I should look about me, and should think a little, she would recommend me to stay a few days in London, if I liked it, either on my way down into Suffolk, or in coming back. In a word, I was at liberty to do what I would, for three weeks or a month; and no other conditions were imposed upon my freedom than the before-mentioned thinking and looking about me, and a pledge to write three times a week and faithfully report myself.\n\nI went to Canterbury first, that I might take leave of Agnes and Mr. Wickfield (my old room in whose house I had not yet relinquished), and also of the good Doctor. Agnes was very glad to see me, and told me that the house had not been like itself since I had left it.\n\n‘I am sure I am not like myself when I am away,’ said I. ‘I seem to want my right hand, when I miss you. Though that’s not saying much; for there’s no head in my right hand, and no heart. Everyone who knows you, consults with you, and is guided by you, Agnes.’\n\n‘Everyone who knows me, spoils me, I believe,’ she answered, smiling.\n\n‘No. It’s because you are like no one else. You are so good, and so sweet-tempered. You have such a gentle nature, and you are always right.’\n\n‘You talk,’ said Agnes, breaking into a pleasant laugh, as she sat at work, ‘as if I were the late Miss Larkins.’\n\n‘Come! It’s not fair to abuse my confidence,’ I answered, reddening at the recollection of my blue enslaver. ‘But I shall confide in you, just the same, Agnes. I can never grow out of that. Whenever I fall into trouble, or fall in love, I shall always tell you, if you’ll let me--even when I come to fall in love in earnest.’\n\n‘Why, you have always been in earnest!’ said Agnes, laughing again.\n\n‘Oh! that was as a child, or a schoolboy,’ said I, laughing in my turn, not without being a little shame-faced. ‘Times are altering now, and I suppose I shall be in a terrible state of earnestness one day or other. My wonder is, that you are not in earnest yourself, by this time, Agnes.’\n\nAgnes laughed again, and shook her head.\n\n‘Oh, I know you are not!’ said I, ‘because if you had been you would have told me. Or at least’--for I saw a faint blush in her face, ‘you would have let me find it out for myself. But there is no one that I know of, who deserves to love you, Agnes. Someone of a nobler character, and more worthy altogether than anyone I have ever seen here, must rise up, before I give my consent. In the time to come, I shall have a wary eye on all admirers; and shall exact a great deal from the successful one, I assure you.’\n\nWe had gone on, so far, in a mixture of confidential jest and earnest, that had long grown naturally out of our familiar relations, begun as mere children. But Agnes, now suddenly lifting up her eyes to mine, and speaking in a different manner, said:\n\n‘Trotwood, there is something that I want to ask you, and that I may not have another opportunity of asking for a long time, perhaps--something I would ask, I think, of no one else. Have you observed any gradual alteration in Papa?’\n\nI had observed it, and had often wondered whether she had too. I must have shown as much, now, in my face; for her eyes were in a moment cast down, and I saw tears in them.\n\n‘Tell me what it is,’ she said, in a low voice.\n\n‘I think--shall I be quite plain, Agnes, liking him so much?’\n\n‘Yes,’ she said.\n\n‘I think he does himself no good by the habit that has increased upon him since I first came here. He is often very nervous--or I fancy so.’\n\n‘It is not fancy,’ said Agnes, shaking her head.\n\n‘His hand trembles, his speech is not plain, and his eyes look wild. I have remarked that at those times, and when he is least like himself, he is most certain to be wanted on some business.’\n\n‘By Uriah,’ said Agnes.\n\n‘Yes; and the sense of being unfit for it, or of not having understood it, or of having shown his condition in spite of himself, seems to make him so uneasy, that next day he is worse, and next day worse, and so he becomes jaded and haggard. Do not be alarmed by what I say, Agnes, but in this state I saw him, only the other evening, lay down his head upon his desk, and shed tears like a child.’\n\nHer hand passed softly before my lips while I was yet speaking, and in a moment she had met her father at the door of the room, and was hanging on his shoulder. The expression of her face, as they both looked towards me, I felt to be very touching. There was such deep fondness for him, and gratitude to him for all his love and care, in her beautiful look; and there was such a fervent appeal to me to deal tenderly by him, even in my inmost thoughts, and to let no harsh construction find any place against him; she was, at once, so proud of him and devoted to him, yet so compassionate and sorry, and so reliant upon me to be so, too; that nothing she could have said would have expressed more to me, or moved me more.\n\nWe were to drink tea at the Doctor’s. We went there at the usual hour; and round the study fireside found the Doctor, and his young wife, and her mother. The Doctor, who made as much of my going away as if I were going to China, received me as an honoured guest; and called for a log of wood to be thrown on the fire, that he might see the face of his old pupil reddening in the blaze.\n\n‘I shall not see many more new faces in Trotwood’s stead, Wickfield,’ said the Doctor, warming his hands; ‘I am getting lazy, and want ease. I shall relinquish all my young people in another six months, and lead a quieter life.’\n\n‘You have said so, any time these ten years, Doctor,’ Mr. Wickfield answered.\n\n‘But now I mean to do it,’ returned the Doctor. ‘My first master will succeed me--I am in earnest at last--so you’ll soon have to arrange our contracts, and to bind us firmly to them, like a couple of knaves.’\n\n‘And to take care,’ said Mr. Wickfield, ‘that you’re not imposed on, eh? As you certainly would be, in any contract you should make for yourself. Well! I am ready. There are worse tasks than that, in my calling.’\n\n‘I shall have nothing to think of then,’ said the Doctor, with a smile, ‘but my Dictionary; and this other contract-bargain--Annie.’\n\nAs Mr. Wickfield glanced towards her, sitting at the tea table by Agnes, she seemed to me to avoid his look with such unwonted hesitation and timidity, that his attention became fixed upon her, as if something were suggested to his thoughts.\n\n‘There is a post come in from India, I observe,’ he said, after a short silence.\n\n‘By the by! and letters from Mr. Jack Maldon!’ said the Doctor.\n\n‘Indeed!’ ‘Poor dear Jack!’ said Mrs. Markleham, shaking her head. ‘That trying climate!--like living, they tell me, on a sand-heap, underneath a burning-glass! He looked strong, but he wasn’t. My dear Doctor, it was his spirit, not his constitution, that he ventured on so boldly. Annie, my dear, I am sure you must perfectly recollect that your cousin never was strong--not what can be called ROBUST, you know,’ said Mrs. Markleham, with emphasis, and looking round upon us generally, ‘--from the time when my daughter and himself were children together, and walking about, arm-in-arm, the livelong day.’\n\nAnnie, thus addressed, made no reply.\n\n‘Do I gather from what you say, ma’am, that Mr. Maldon is ill?’ asked Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘Ill!’ replied the Old Soldier. ‘My dear sir, he’s all sorts of things.’\n\n‘Except well?’ said Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘Except well, indeed!’ said the Old Soldier. ‘He has had dreadful strokes of the sun, no doubt, and jungle fevers and agues, and every kind of thing you can mention. As to his liver,’ said the Old Soldier resignedly, ‘that, of course, he gave up altogether, when he first went out!’\n\n‘Does he say all this?’ asked Mr. Wickfield.\n\n‘Say? My dear sir,’ returned Mrs. Markleham, shaking her head and her fan, ‘you little know my poor Jack Maldon when you ask that question. Say? Not he. You might drag him at the heels of four wild horses first.’\n\n‘Mama!’ said Mrs. Strong.\n\n‘Annie, my dear,’ returned her mother, ‘once for all, I must really beg that you will not interfere with me, unless it is to confirm what I say. You know as well as I do that your cousin Maldon would be dragged at the heels of any number of wild horses--why should I confine myself to four! I WON’T confine myself to four--eight, sixteen, two-and-thirty, rather than say anything calculated to overturn the Doctor’s plans.’\n\n‘Wickfield’s plans,’ said the Doctor, stroking his face, and looking penitently at his adviser. ‘That is to say, our joint plans for him. I said myself, abroad or at home.’\n\n‘And I said’ added Mr. Wickfield gravely, ‘abroad. I was the means of sending him abroad. It’s my responsibility.’\n\n‘Oh! Responsibility!’ said the Old Soldier. ‘Everything was done for the best, my dear Mr. Wickfield; everything was done for the kindest and best, we know. But if the dear fellow can’t live there, he can’t live there. And if he can’t live there, he’ll die there, sooner than he’ll overturn the Doctor’s plans. I know him,’ said the Old Soldier, fanning herself, in a sort of calm prophetic agony, ‘and I know he’ll die there, sooner than he’ll overturn the Doctor’s plans.’\n\n‘Well, well, ma’am,’ said the Doctor cheerfully, ‘I am not bigoted to my plans, and I can overturn them myself. I can substitute some other plans. If Mr. Jack Maldon comes home on account of ill health, he must not be allowed to go back, and we must endeavour to make some more suitable and fortunate provision for him in this country.’\n\nMrs. Markleham was so overcome by this generous speech--which, I need not say, she had not at all expected or led up to--that she could only tell the Doctor it was like himself, and go several times through that operation of kissing the sticks of her fan, and then tapping his hand with it. After which she gently chid her daughter Annie, for not being more demonstrative when such kindnesses were showered, for her sake, on her old playfellow; and entertained us with some particulars concerning other deserving members of her family, whom it was desirable to set on their deserving legs.\n\nAll this time, her daughter Annie never once spoke, or lifted up her eyes. All this time, Mr. Wickfield had his glance upon her as she sat by his own daughter’s side. It appeared to me that he never thought of being observed by anyone; but was so intent upon her, and upon his own thoughts in connexion with her, as to be quite absorbed. He now asked what Mr. Jack Maldon had actually written in reference to himself, and to whom he had written?\n\n‘Why, here,’ said Mrs. Markleham, taking a letter from the chimney-piece above the Doctor’s head, ‘the dear fellow says to the Doctor himself--where is it? Oh!--“I am sorry to inform you that my health is suffering severely, and that I fear I may be reduced to the necessity of returning home for a time, as the only hope of restoration.” That’s pretty plain, poor fellow! His only hope of restoration! But Annie’s letter is plainer still. Annie, show me that letter again.’\n\n‘Not now, mama,’ she pleaded in a low tone.\n\n‘My dear, you absolutely are, on some subjects, one of the most ridiculous persons in the world,’ returned her mother, ‘and perhaps the most unnatural to the claims of your own family. We never should have heard of the letter at all, I believe, unless I had asked for it myself. Do you call that confidence, my love, towards Doctor Strong? I am surprised. You ought to know better.’\n\nThe letter was reluctantly produced; and as I handed it to the old lady, I saw how the unwilling hand from which I took it, trembled.\n\n‘Now let us see,’ said Mrs. Markleham, putting her glass to her eye, ‘where the passage is. “The remembrance of old times, my dearest Annie”--and so forth--it’s not there. “The amiable old Proctor”--who’s he? Dear me, Annie, how illegibly your cousin Maldon writes, and how stupid I am! “Doctor,” of course. Ah! amiable indeed!’ Here she left off, to kiss her fan again, and shake it at the Doctor, who was looking at us in a state of placid satisfaction. ‘Now I have found it. “You may not be surprised to hear, Annie,”--no, to be sure, knowing that he never was really strong; what did I say just now?--“that I have undergone so much in this distant place, as to have decided to leave it at all hazards; on sick leave, if I can; on total resignation, if that is not to be obtained. What I have endured, and do endure here, is insupportable.” And but for the promptitude of that best of creatures,’ said Mrs. Markleham, telegraphing the Doctor as before, and refolding the letter, ‘it would be insupportable to me to think of.’\n\nMr. Wickfield said not one word, though the old lady looked to him as if for his commentary on this intelligence; but sat severely silent, with his eyes fixed on the ground. Long after the subject was dismissed, and other topics occupied us, he remained so; seldom raising his eyes, unless to rest them for a moment, with a thoughtful frown, upon the Doctor, or his wife, or both.\n\nThe Doctor was very fond of music. Agnes sang with great sweetness and expression, and so did Mrs. Strong. They sang together, and played duets together, and we had quite a little concert. But I remarked two things: first, that though Annie soon recovered her composure, and was quite herself, there was a blank between her and Mr. Wickfield which separated them wholly from each other; secondly, that Mr. Wickfield seemed to dislike the intimacy between her and Agnes, and to watch it with uneasiness. And now, I must confess, the recollection of what I had seen on that night when Mr. Maldon went away, first began to return upon me with a meaning it had never had, and to trouble me. The innocent beauty of her face was not as innocent to me as it had been; I mistrusted the natural grace and charm of her manner; and when I looked at Agnes by her side, and thought how good and true Agnes was, suspicions arose within me that it was an ill-assorted friendship.\n\nShe was so happy in it herself, however, and the other was so happy too, that they made the evening fly away as if it were but an hour. It closed in an incident which I well remember. They were taking leave of each other, and Agnes was going to embrace her and kiss her, when Mr. Wickfield stepped between them, as if by accident, and drew Agnes quickly away. Then I saw, as though all the intervening time had been cancelled, and I were still standing in the doorway on the night of the departure, the expression of that night in the face of Mrs. Strong, as it confronted his.\n\nI cannot say what an impression this made upon me, or how impossible I found it, when I thought of her afterwards, to separate her from this look, and remember her face in its innocent loveliness again. It haunted me when I got home. I seemed to have left the Doctor’s roof with a dark cloud lowering on it. The reverence that I had for his grey head, was mingled with commiseration for his faith in those who were treacherous to him, and with resentment against those who injured him. The impending shadow of a great affliction, and a great disgrace that had no distinct form in it yet, fell like a stain upon the quiet place where I had worked and played as a boy, and did it a cruel wrong. I had no pleasure in thinking, any more, of the grave old broad-leaved aloe-trees, which remained shut up in themselves a hundred years together, and of the trim smooth grass-plot, and the stone urns, and the Doctor’s walk, and the congenial sound of the Cathedral bell hovering above them all. It was as if the tranquil sanctuary of my boyhood had been sacked before my face, and its peace and honour given to the winds.\n\nBut morning brought with it my parting from the old house, which Agnes had filled with her influence; and that occupied my mind sufficiently. I should be there again soon, no doubt; I might sleep again--perhaps often--in my old room; but the days of my inhabiting there were gone, and the old time was past. I was heavier at heart when I packed up such of my books and clothes as still remained there to be sent to Dover, than I cared to show to Uriah Heep; who was so officious to help me, that I uncharitably thought him mighty glad that I was going.\n\nI got away from Agnes and her father, somehow, with an indifferent show of being very manly, and took my seat upon the box of the London coach. I was so softened and forgiving, going through the town, that I had half a mind to nod to my old enemy the butcher, and throw him five shillings to drink. But he looked such a very obdurate butcher as he stood scraping the great block in the shop, and moreover, his appearance was so little improved by the loss of a front tooth which I had knocked out, that I thought it best to make no advances.\n\nThe main object on my mind, I remember, when we got fairly on the road, was to appear as old as possible to the coachman, and to speak extremely gruff. The latter point I achieved at great personal inconvenience; but I stuck to it, because I felt it was a grown-up sort of thing.\n\n‘You are going through, sir?’ said the coachman.\n\n‘Yes, William,’ I said, condescendingly (I knew him); ‘I am going to London. I shall go down into Suffolk afterwards.’\n\n‘Shooting, sir?’ said the coachman.\n\nHe knew as well as I did that it was just as likely, at that time of year, I was going down there whaling; but I felt complimented, too.\n\n‘I don’t know,’ I said, pretending to be undecided, ‘whether I shall take a shot or not.’ ‘Birds is got wery shy, I’m told,’ said William.\n\n‘So I understand,’ said I.\n\n‘Is Suffolk your county, sir?’ asked William.\n\n‘Yes,’ I said, with some importance. ‘Suffolk’s my county.’\n\n‘I’m told the dumplings is uncommon fine down there,’ said William.\n\nI was not aware of it myself, but I felt it necessary to uphold the institutions of my county, and to evince a familiarity with them; so I shook my head, as much as to say, ‘I believe you!’\n\n‘And the Punches,’ said William. ‘There’s cattle! A Suffolk Punch, when he’s a good un, is worth his weight in gold. Did you ever breed any Suffolk Punches yourself, sir?’\n\n‘N-no,’ I said, ‘not exactly.’\n\n‘Here’s a gen’lm’n behind me, I’ll pound it,’ said William, ‘as has bred ‘em by wholesale.’\n\nThe gentleman spoken of was a gentleman with a very unpromising squint, and a prominent chin, who had a tall white hat on with a narrow flat brim, and whose close-fitting drab trousers seemed to button all the way up outside his legs from his boots to his hips. His chin was cocked over the coachman’s shoulder, so near to me, that his breath quite tickled the back of my head; and as I looked at him, he leered at the leaders with the eye with which he didn’t squint, in a very knowing manner.\n\n‘Ain’t you?’ asked William.\n\n‘Ain’t I what?’ said the gentleman behind.\n\n‘Bred them Suffolk Punches by wholesale?’\n\n‘I should think so,’ said the gentleman. ‘There ain’t no sort of orse that I ain’t bred, and no sort of dorg. Orses and dorgs is some men’s fancy. They’re wittles and drink to me--lodging, wife, and children--reading, writing, and Arithmetic--snuff, tobacker, and sleep.’\n\n‘That ain’t a sort of man to see sitting behind a coach-box, is it though?’ said William in my ear, as he handled the reins.\n\nI construed this remark into an indication of a wish that he should have my place, so I blushingly offered to resign it.\n\n‘Well, if you don’t mind, sir,’ said William, ‘I think it would be more correct.’\n\nI have always considered this as the first fall I had in life. When I booked my place at the coach office I had had ‘Box Seat’ written against the entry, and had given the book-keeper half-a-crown. I was got up in a special great-coat and shawl, expressly to do honour to that distinguished eminence; had glorified myself upon it a good deal; and had felt that I was a credit to the coach. And here, in the very first stage, I was supplanted by a shabby man with a squint, who had no other merit than smelling like a livery-stables, and being able to walk across me, more like a fly than a human being, while the horses were at a canter!\n\nA distrust of myself, which has often beset me in life on small occasions, when it would have been better away, was assuredly not stopped in its growth by this little incident outside the Canterbury coach. It was in vain to take refuge in gruffness of speech. I spoke from the pit of my stomach for the rest of the journey, but I felt completely extinguished, and dreadfully young.\n\nIt was curious and interesting, nevertheless, to be sitting up there behind four horses: well educated, well dressed, and with plenty of money in my pocket; and to look out for the places where I had slept on my weary journey. I had abundant occupation for my thoughts, in every conspicuous landmark on the road. When I looked down at the trampers whom we passed, and saw that well-remembered style of face turned up, I felt as if the tinker’s blackened hand were in the bosom of my shirt again. When we clattered through the narrow street of Chatham, and I caught a glimpse, in passing, of the lane where the old monster lived who had bought my jacket, I stretched my neck eagerly to look for the place where I had sat, in the sun and in the shade, waiting for my money. When we came, at last, within a stage of London, and passed the veritable Salem House where Mr. Creakle had laid about him with a heavy hand, I would have given all I had, for lawful permission to get down and thrash him, and let all the boys out like so many caged sparrows.\n\nWe went to the Golden Cross at Charing Cross, then a mouldy sort of establishment in a close neighbourhood. A waiter showed me into the coffee-room; and a chambermaid introduced me to my small bedchamber, which smelt like a hackney-coach, and was shut up like a family vault. I was still painfully conscious of my youth, for nobody stood in any awe of me at all: the chambermaid being utterly indifferent to my opinions on any subject, and the waiter being familiar with me, and offering advice to my inexperience.\n\n‘Well now,’ said the waiter, in a tone of confidence, ‘what would you like for dinner? Young gentlemen likes poultry in general: have a fowl!’\n\nI told him, as majestically as I could, that I wasn’t in the humour for a fowl.\n\n‘Ain’t you?’ said the waiter. ‘Young gentlemen is generally tired of beef and mutton: have a weal cutlet!’\n\nI assented to this proposal, in default of being able to suggest anything else.\n\n‘Do you care for taters?’ said the waiter, with an insinuating smile, and his head on one side. ‘Young gentlemen generally has been overdosed with taters.’\n\nI commanded him, in my deepest voice, to order a veal cutlet and potatoes, and all things fitting; and to inquire at the bar if there were any letters for Trotwood Copperfield, Esquire--which I knew there were not, and couldn’t be, but thought it manly to appear to expect.\n\nHe soon came back to say that there were none (at which I was much surprised) and began to lay the cloth for my dinner in a box by the fire. While he was so engaged, he asked me what I would take with it; and on my replying ‘Half a pint of sherry,’ thought it a favourable opportunity, I am afraid, to extract that measure of wine from the stale leavings at the bottoms of several small decanters. I am of this opinion, because, while I was reading the newspaper, I observed him behind a low wooden partition, which was his private apartment, very busy pouring out of a number of those vessels into one, like a chemist and druggist making up a prescription. When the wine came, too, I thought it flat; and it certainly had more English crumbs in it, than were to be expected in a foreign wine in anything like a pure state, but I was bashful enough to drink it, and say nothing.\n\nBeing then in a pleasant frame of mind (from which I infer that poisoning is not always disagreeable in some stages of the process), I resolved to go to the play. It was Covent Garden Theatre that I chose; and there, from the back of a centre box, I saw Julius Caesar and the new Pantomime. To have all those noble Romans alive before me, and walking in and out for my entertainment, instead of being the stern taskmasters they had been at school, was a most novel and delightful effect. But the mingled reality and mystery of the whole show, the influence upon me of the poetry, the lights, the music, the company, the smooth stupendous changes of glittering and brilliant scenery, were so dazzling, and opened up such illimitable regions of delight, that when I came out into the rainy street, at twelve o’clock at night, I felt as if I had come from the clouds, where I had been leading a romantic life for ages, to a bawling, splashing, link-lighted, umbrella-struggling, hackney-coach-jostling, patten-clinking, muddy, miserable world.\n\nI had emerged by another door, and stood in the street for a little while, as if I really were a stranger upon earth: but the unceremonious pushing and hustling that I received, soon recalled me to myself, and put me in the road back to the hotel; whither I went, revolving the glorious vision all the way; and where, after some porter and oysters, I sat revolving it still, at past one o’clock, with my eyes on the coffee-room fire.\n\nI was so filled with the play, and with the past--for it was, in a manner, like a shining transparency, through which I saw my earlier life moving along--that I don’t know when the figure of a handsome well-formed young man dressed with a tasteful easy negligence which I have reason to remember very well, became a real presence to me. But I recollect being conscious of his company without having noticed his coming in--and my still sitting, musing, over the coffee-room fire.\n\nAt last I rose to go to bed, much to the relief of the sleepy waiter, who had got the fidgets in his legs, and was twisting them, and hitting them, and putting them through all kinds of contortions in his small pantry. In going towards the door, I passed the person who had come in, and saw him plainly. I turned directly, came back, and looked again. He did not know me, but I knew him in a moment.\n\nAt another time I might have wanted the confidence or the decision to speak to him, and might have put it off until next day, and might have lost him. But, in the then condition of my mind, where the play was still running high, his former protection of me appeared so deserving of my gratitude, and my old love for him overflowed my breast so freshly and spontaneously, that I went up to him at once, with a fast-beating heart, and said:\n\n‘Steerforth! won’t you speak to me?’\n\nHe looked at me--just as he used to look, sometimes--but I saw no recognition in his face.\n\n‘You don’t remember me, I am afraid,’ said I.\n\n‘My God!’ he suddenly exclaimed. ‘It’s little Copperfield!’\n\nI grasped him by both hands, and could not let them go. But for very shame, and the fear that it might displease him, I could have held him round the neck and cried.\n\n‘I never, never, never was so glad! My dear Steerforth, I am so overjoyed to see you!’\n\n‘And I am rejoiced to see you, too!’ he said, shaking my hands heartily. ‘Why, Copperfield, old boy, don’t be overpowered!’ And yet he was glad, too, I thought, to see how the delight I had in meeting him affected me.\n\nI brushed away the tears that my utmost resolution had not been able to keep back, and I made a clumsy laugh of it, and we sat down together, side by side.\n\n‘Why, how do you come to be here?’ said Steerforth, clapping me on the shoulder.\n\n‘I came here by the Canterbury coach, today. I have been adopted by an aunt down in that part of the country, and have just finished my education there. How do YOU come to be here, Steerforth?’\n\n‘Well, I am what they call an Oxford man,’ he returned; ‘that is to say, I get bored to death down there, periodically--and I am on my way now to my mother’s. You’re a devilish amiable-looking fellow, Copperfield. Just what you used to be, now I look at you! Not altered in the least!’\n\n‘I knew you immediately,’ I said; ‘but you are more easily remembered.’\n\nHe laughed as he ran his hand through the clustering curls of his hair, and said gaily:\n\n‘Yes, I am on an expedition of duty. My mother lives a little way out of town; and the roads being in a beastly condition, and our house tedious enough, I remained here tonight instead of going on. I have not been in town half-a-dozen hours, and those I have been dozing and grumbling away at the play.’\n\n‘I have been at the play, too,’ said I. ‘At Covent Garden. What a delightful and magnificent entertainment, Steerforth!’\n\nSteerforth laughed heartily.\n\n‘My dear young Davy,’ he said, clapping me on the shoulder again, ‘you are a very Daisy. The daisy of the field, at sunrise, is not fresher than you are. I have been at Covent Garden, too, and there never was a more miserable business. Holloa, you sir!’\n\nThis was addressed to the waiter, who had been very attentive to our recognition, at a distance, and now came forward deferentially.\n\n‘Where have you put my friend, Mr. Copperfield?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Beg your pardon, sir?’\n\n‘Where does he sleep? What’s his number? You know what I mean,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ said the waiter, with an apologetic air. ‘Mr. Copperfield is at present in forty-four, sir.’\n\n‘And what the devil do you mean,’ retorted Steerforth, ‘by putting Mr. Copperfield into a little loft over a stable?’\n\n‘Why, you see we wasn’t aware, sir,’ returned the waiter, still apologetically, ‘as Mr. Copperfield was anyways particular. We can give Mr. Copperfield seventy-two, sir, if it would be preferred. Next you, sir.’\n\n‘Of course it would be preferred,’ said Steerforth. ‘And do it at once.’ The waiter immediately withdrew to make the exchange. Steerforth, very much amused at my having been put into forty-four, laughed again, and clapped me on the shoulder again, and invited me to breakfast with him next morning at ten o’clock--an invitation I was only too proud and happy to accept. It being now pretty late, we took our candles and went upstairs, where we parted with friendly heartiness at his door, and where I found my new room a great improvement on my old one, it not being at all musty, and having an immense four-post bedstead in it, which was quite a little landed estate. Here, among pillows enough for six, I soon fell asleep in a blissful condition, and dreamed of ancient Rome, Steerforth, and friendship, until the early morning coaches, rumbling out of the archway underneath, made me dream of thunder and the gods.\n\n# CHAPTER 20. STEERFORTH’S HOME\n\nWhen the chambermaid tapped at my door at eight o’clock, and informed me that my shaving-water was outside, I felt severely the having no occasion for it, and blushed in my bed. The suspicion that she laughed too, when she said it, preyed upon my mind all the time I was dressing; and gave me, I was conscious, a sneaking and guilty air when I passed her on the staircase, as I was going down to breakfast. I was so sensitively aware, indeed, of being younger than I could have wished, that for some time I could not make up my mind to pass her at all, under the ignoble circumstances of the case; but, hearing her there with a broom, stood peeping out of window at King Charles on horseback, surrounded by a maze of hackney-coaches, and looking anything but regal in a drizzling rain and a dark-brown fog, until I was admonished by the waiter that the gentleman was waiting for me.\n\nIt was not in the coffee-room that I found Steerforth expecting me, but in a snug private apartment, red-curtained and Turkey-carpeted, where the fire burnt bright, and a fine hot breakfast was set forth on a table covered with a clean cloth; and a cheerful miniature of the room, the fire, the breakfast, Steerforth, and all, was shining in the little round mirror over the sideboard. I was rather bashful at first, Steerforth being so self-possessed, and elegant, and superior to me in all respects (age included); but his easy patronage soon put that to rights, and made me quite at home. I could not enough admire the change he had wrought in the Golden Cross; or compare the dull forlorn state I had held yesterday, with this morning’s comfort and this morning’s entertainment. As to the waiter’s familiarity, it was quenched as if it had never been. He attended on us, as I may say, in sackcloth and ashes.\n\n‘Now, Copperfield,’ said Steerforth, when we were alone, ‘I should like to hear what you are doing, and where you are going, and all about you. I feel as if you were my property.’ Glowing with pleasure to find that he had still this interest in me, I told him how my aunt had proposed the little expedition that I had before me, and whither it tended.\n\n‘As you are in no hurry, then,’ said Steerforth, ‘come home with me to Highgate, and stay a day or two. You will be pleased with my mother--she is a little vain and prosy about me, but that you can forgive her--and she will be pleased with you.’\n\n‘I should like to be as sure of that, as you are kind enough to say you are,’ I answered, smiling.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Steerforth, ‘everyone who likes me, has a claim on her that is sure to be acknowledged.’\n\n‘Then I think I shall be a favourite,’ said I.\n\n‘Good!’ said Steerforth. ‘Come and prove it. We will go and see the lions for an hour or two--it’s something to have a fresh fellow like you to show them to, Copperfield--and then we’ll journey out to Highgate by the coach.’\n\nI could hardly believe but that I was in a dream, and that I should wake presently in number forty-four, to the solitary box in the coffee-room and the familiar waiter again. After I had written to my aunt and told her of my fortunate meeting with my admired old schoolfellow, and my acceptance of his invitation, we went out in a hackney-chariot, and saw a Panorama and some other sights, and took a walk through the Museum, where I could not help observing how much Steerforth knew, on an infinite variety of subjects, and of how little account he seemed to make his knowledge.\n\n‘You’ll take a high degree at college, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘if you have not done so already; and they will have good reason to be proud of you.’\n\n‘I take a degree!’ cried Steerforth. ‘Not I! my dear Daisy--will you mind my calling you Daisy?’\n\n‘Not at all!’ said I.\n\n‘That’s a good fellow! My dear Daisy,’ said Steerforth, laughing. ‘I have not the least desire or intention to distinguish myself in that way. I have done quite sufficient for my purpose. I find that I am heavy company enough for myself as I am.’\n\n‘But the fame--’ I was beginning.\n\n‘You romantic Daisy!’ said Steerforth, laughing still more heartily: ‘why should I trouble myself, that a parcel of heavy-headed fellows may gape and hold up their hands? Let them do it at some other man. There’s fame for him, and he’s welcome to it.’\n\nI was abashed at having made so great a mistake, and was glad to change the subject. Fortunately it was not difficult to do, for Steerforth could always pass from one subject to another with a carelessness and lightness that were his own.\n\nLunch succeeded to our sight-seeing, and the short winter day wore away so fast, that it was dusk when the stage-coach stopped with us at an old brick house at Highgate on the summit of the hill. An elderly lady, though not very far advanced in years, with a proud carriage and a handsome face, was in the doorway as we alighted; and greeting Steerforth as ‘My dearest James,’ folded him in her arms. To this lady he presented me as his mother, and she gave me a stately welcome.\n\nIt was a genteel old-fashioned house, very quiet and orderly. From the windows of my room I saw all London lying in the distance like a great vapour, with here and there some lights twinkling through it. I had only time, in dressing, to glance at the solid furniture, the framed pieces of work (done, I supposed, by Steerforth’s mother when she was a girl), and some pictures in crayons of ladies with powdered hair and bodices, coming and going on the walls, as the newly-kindled fire crackled and sputtered, when I was called to dinner.\n\nThere was a second lady in the dining-room, of a slight short figure, dark, and not agreeable to look at, but with some appearance of good looks too, who attracted my attention: perhaps because I had not expected to see her; perhaps because I found myself sitting opposite to her; perhaps because of something really remarkable in her. She had black hair and eager black eyes, and was thin, and had a scar upon her lip. It was an old scar--I should rather call it seam, for it was not discoloured, and had healed years ago--which had once cut through her mouth, downward towards the chin, but was now barely visible across the table, except above and on her upper lip, the shape of which it had altered. I concluded in my own mind that she was about thirty years of age, and that she wished to be married. She was a little dilapidated--like a house--with having been so long to let; yet had, as I have said, an appearance of good looks. Her thinness seemed to be the effect of some wasting fire within her, which found a vent in her gaunt eyes.\n\nShe was introduced as Miss Dartle, and both Steerforth and his mother called her Rosa. I found that she lived there, and had been for a long time Mrs. Steerforth’s companion. It appeared to me that she never said anything she wanted to say, outright; but hinted it, and made a great deal more of it by this practice. For example, when Mrs. Steerforth observed, more in jest than earnest, that she feared her son led but a wild life at college, Miss Dartle put in thus:\n\n‘Oh, really? You know how ignorant I am, and that I only ask for information, but isn’t it always so? I thought that kind of life was on all hands understood to be--eh?’ ‘It is education for a very grave profession, if you mean that, Rosa,’ Mrs. Steerforth answered with some coldness.\n\n‘Oh! Yes! That’s very true,’ returned Miss Dartle. ‘But isn’t it, though?--I want to be put right, if I am wrong--isn’t it, really?’\n\n‘Really what?’ said Mrs. Steerforth.\n\n‘Oh! You mean it’s not!’ returned Miss Dartle. ‘Well, I’m very glad to hear it! Now, I know what to do! That’s the advantage of asking. I shall never allow people to talk before me about wastefulness and profligacy, and so forth, in connexion with that life, any more.’\n\n‘And you will be right,’ said Mrs. Steerforth. ‘My son’s tutor is a conscientious gentleman; and if I had not implicit reliance on my son, I should have reliance on him.’\n\n‘Should you?’ said Miss Dartle. ‘Dear me! Conscientious, is he? Really conscientious, now?’\n\n‘Yes, I am convinced of it,’ said Mrs. Steerforth.\n\n‘How very nice!’ exclaimed Miss Dartle. ‘What a comfort! Really conscientious? Then he’s not--but of course he can’t be, if he’s really conscientious. Well, I shall be quite happy in my opinion of him, from this time. You can’t think how it elevates him in my opinion, to know for certain that he’s really conscientious!’\n\nHer own views of every question, and her correction of everything that was said to which she was opposed, Miss Dartle insinuated in the same way: sometimes, I could not conceal from myself, with great power, though in contradiction even of Steerforth. An instance happened before dinner was done. Mrs. Steerforth speaking to me about my intention of going down into Suffolk, I said at hazard how glad I should be, if Steerforth would only go there with me; and explaining to him that I was going to see my old nurse, and Mr. Peggotty’s family, I reminded him of the boatman whom he had seen at school.\n\n‘Oh! That bluff fellow!’ said Steerforth. ‘He had a son with him, hadn’t he?’\n\n‘No. That was his nephew,’ I replied; ‘whom he adopted, though, as a son. He has a very pretty little niece too, whom he adopted as a daughter. In short, his house--or rather his boat, for he lives in one, on dry land--is full of people who are objects of his generosity and kindness. You would be delighted to see that household.’\n\n‘Should I?’ said Steerforth. ‘Well, I think I should. I must see what can be done. It would be worth a journey (not to mention the pleasure of a journey with you, Daisy), to see that sort of people together, and to make one of ‘em.’\n\nMy heart leaped with a new hope of pleasure. But it was in reference to the tone in which he had spoken of ‘that sort of people’, that Miss Dartle, whose sparkling eyes had been watchful of us, now broke in again.\n\n‘Oh, but, really? Do tell me. Are they, though?’ she said.\n\n‘Are they what? And are who what?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘That sort of people.---Are they really animals and clods, and beings of another order? I want to know SO much.’\n\n‘Why, there’s a pretty wide separation between them and us,’ said Steerforth, with indifference. ‘They are not to be expected to be as sensitive as we are. Their delicacy is not to be shocked, or hurt easily. They are wonderfully virtuous, I dare say--some people contend for that, at least; and I am sure I don’t want to contradict them--but they have not very fine natures, and they may be thankful that, like their coarse rough skins, they are not easily wounded.’\n\n‘Really!’ said Miss Dartle. ‘Well, I don’t know, now, when I have been better pleased than to hear that. It’s so consoling! It’s such a delight to know that, when they suffer, they don’t feel! Sometimes I have been quite uneasy for that sort of people; but now I shall just dismiss the idea of them, altogether. Live and learn. I had my doubts, I confess, but now they’re cleared up. I didn’t know, and now I do know, and that shows the advantage of asking--don’t it?’\n\nI believed that Steerforth had said what he had, in jest, or to draw Miss Dartle out; and I expected him to say as much when she was gone, and we two were sitting before the fire. But he merely asked me what I thought of her.\n\n‘She is very clever, is she not?’ I asked.\n\n‘Clever! She brings everything to a grindstone,’ said Steerforth, and sharpens it, as she has sharpened her own face and figure these years past. She has worn herself away by constant sharpening. She is all edge.’\n\n‘What a remarkable scar that is upon her lip!’ I said.\n\nSteerforth’s face fell, and he paused a moment.\n\n‘Why, the fact is,’ he returned, ‘I did that.’\n\n‘By an unfortunate accident!’\n\n‘No. I was a young boy, and she exasperated me, and I threw a hammer at her. A promising young angel I must have been!’ I was deeply sorry to have touched on such a painful theme, but that was useless now.\n\n‘She has borne the mark ever since, as you see,’ said Steerforth; ‘and she’ll bear it to her grave, if she ever rests in one--though I can hardly believe she will ever rest anywhere. She was the motherless child of a sort of cousin of my father’s. He died one day. My mother, who was then a widow, brought her here to be company to her. She has a couple of thousand pounds of her own, and saves the interest of it every year, to add to the principal. There’s the history of Miss Rosa Dartle for you.’\n\n‘And I have no doubt she loves you like a brother?’ said I.\n\n‘Humph!’ retorted Steerforth, looking at the fire. ‘Some brothers are not loved over much; and some love--but help yourself, Copperfield! We’ll drink the daisies of the field, in compliment to you; and the lilies of the valley that toil not, neither do they spin, in compliment to me--the more shame for me!’ A moody smile that had overspread his features cleared off as he said this merrily, and he was his own frank, winning self again.\n\nI could not help glancing at the scar with a painful interest when we went in to tea. It was not long before I observed that it was the most susceptible part of her face, and that, when she turned pale, that mark altered first, and became a dull, lead-coloured streak, lengthening out to its full extent, like a mark in invisible ink brought to the fire. There was a little altercation between her and Steerforth about a cast of the dice at backgammon--when I thought her, for one moment, in a storm of rage; and then I saw it start forth like the old writing on the wall.\n\nIt was no matter of wonder to me to find Mrs. Steerforth devoted to her son. She seemed to be able to speak or think about nothing else. She showed me his picture as an infant, in a locket, with some of his baby-hair in it; she showed me his picture as he had been when I first knew him; and she wore at her breast his picture as he was now. All the letters he had ever written to her, she kept in a cabinet near her own chair by the fire; and she would have read me some of them, and I should have been very glad to hear them too, if he had not interposed, and coaxed her out of the design.\n\n‘It was at Mr. Creakle’s, my son tells me, that you first became acquainted,’ said Mrs. Steerforth, as she and I were talking at one table, while they played backgammon at another. ‘Indeed, I recollect his speaking, at that time, of a pupil younger than himself who had taken his fancy there; but your name, as you may suppose, has not lived in my memory.’\n\n‘He was very generous and noble to me in those days, I assure you, ma’am,’ said I, ‘and I stood in need of such a friend. I should have been quite crushed without him.’\n\n‘He is always generous and noble,’ said Mrs. Steerforth, proudly.\n\nI subscribed to this with all my heart, God knows. She knew I did; for the stateliness of her manner already abated towards me, except when she spoke in praise of him, and then her air was always lofty.\n\n‘It was not a fit school generally for my son,’ said she; ‘far from it; but there were particular circumstances to be considered at the time, of more importance even than that selection. My son’s high spirit made it desirable that he should be placed with some man who felt its superiority, and would be content to bow himself before it; and we found such a man there.’\n\nI knew that, knowing the fellow. And yet I did not despise him the more for it, but thought it a redeeming quality in him if he could be allowed any grace for not resisting one so irresistible as Steerforth.\n\n‘My son’s great capacity was tempted on, there, by a feeling of voluntary emulation and conscious pride,’ the fond lady went on to say. ‘He would have risen against all constraint; but he found himself the monarch of the place, and he haughtily determined to be worthy of his station. It was like himself.’\n\nI echoed, with all my heart and soul, that it was like himself.\n\n‘So my son took, of his own will, and on no compulsion, to the course in which he can always, when it is his pleasure, outstrip every competitor,’ she pursued. ‘My son informs me, Mr. Copperfield, that you were quite devoted to him, and that when you met yesterday you made yourself known to him with tears of joy. I should be an affected woman if I made any pretence of being surprised by my son’s inspiring such emotions; but I cannot be indifferent to anyone who is so sensible of his merit, and I am very glad to see you here, and can assure you that he feels an unusual friendship for you, and that you may rely on his protection.’\n\nMiss Dartle played backgammon as eagerly as she did everything else. If I had seen her, first, at the board, I should have fancied that her figure had got thin, and her eyes had got large, over that pursuit, and no other in the world. But I am very much mistaken if she missed a word of this, or lost a look of mine as I received it with the utmost pleasure, and honoured by Mrs. Steerforth’s confidence, felt older than I had done since I left Canterbury.\n\nWhen the evening was pretty far spent, and a tray of glasses and decanters came in, Steerforth promised, over the fire, that he would seriously think of going down into the country with me. There was no hurry, he said; a week hence would do; and his mother hospitably said the same. While we were talking, he more than once called me Daisy; which brought Miss Dartle out again.\n\n‘But really, Mr. Copperfield,’ she asked, ‘is it a nickname? And why does he give it you? Is it--eh?--because he thinks you young and innocent? I am so stupid in these things.’\n\nI coloured in replying that I believed it was.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Miss Dartle. ‘Now I am glad to know that! I ask for information, and I am glad to know it. He thinks you young and innocent; and so you are his friend. Well, that’s quite delightful!’\n\nShe went to bed soon after this, and Mrs. Steerforth retired too. Steerforth and I, after lingering for half-an-hour over the fire, talking about Traddles and all the rest of them at old Salem House, went upstairs together. Steerforth’s room was next to mine, and I went in to look at it. It was a picture of comfort, full of easy-chairs, cushions and footstools, worked by his mother’s hand, and with no sort of thing omitted that could help to render it complete. Finally, her handsome features looked down on her darling from a portrait on the wall, as if it were even something to her that her likeness should watch him while he slept.\n\nI found the fire burning clear enough in my room by this time, and the curtains drawn before the windows and round the bed, giving it a very snug appearance. I sat down in a great chair upon the hearth to meditate on my happiness; and had enjoyed the contemplation of it for some time, when I found a likeness of Miss Dartle looking eagerly at me from above the chimney-piece.\n\nIt was a startling likeness, and necessarily had a startling look. The painter hadn’t made the scar, but I made it; and there it was, coming and going; now confined to the upper lip as I had seen it at dinner, and now showing the whole extent of the wound inflicted by the hammer, as I had seen it when she was passionate.\n\nI wondered peevishly why they couldn’t put her anywhere else instead of quartering her on me. To get rid of her, I undressed quickly, extinguished my light, and went to bed. But, as I fell asleep, I could not forget that she was still there looking, ‘Is it really, though? I want to know’; and when I awoke in the night, I found that I was uneasily asking all sorts of people in my dreams whether it really was or not--without knowing what I meant.\n\n# CHAPTER 21. LITTLE EM’LY\n\nThere was a servant in that house, a man who, I understood, was usually with Steerforth, and had come into his service at the University, who was in appearance a pattern of respectability. I believe there never existed in his station a more respectable-looking man. He was taciturn, soft-footed, very quiet in his manner, deferential, observant, always at hand when wanted, and never near when not wanted; but his great claim to consideration was his respectability. He had not a pliant face, he had rather a stiff neck, rather a tight smooth head with short hair clinging to it at the sides, a soft way of speaking, with a peculiar habit of whispering the letter S so distinctly, that he seemed to use it oftener than any other man; but every peculiarity that he had he made respectable. If his nose had been upside-down, he would have made that respectable. He surrounded himself with an atmosphere of respectability, and walked secure in it. It would have been next to impossible to suspect him of anything wrong, he was so thoroughly respectable. Nobody could have thought of putting him in a livery, he was so highly respectable. To have imposed any derogatory work upon him, would have been to inflict a wanton insult on the feelings of a most respectable man. And of this, I noticed--the women-servants in the household were so intuitively conscious, that they always did such work themselves, and generally while he read the paper by the pantry fire.\n\nSuch a self-contained man I never saw. But in that quality, as in every other he possessed, he only seemed to be the more respectable. Even the fact that no one knew his Christian name, seemed to form a part of his respectability. Nothing could be objected against his surname, Littimer, by which he was known. Peter might have been hanged, or Tom transported; but Littimer was perfectly respectable.\n\nIt was occasioned, I suppose, by the reverend nature of respectability in the abstract, but I felt particularly young in this man’s presence. How old he was himself, I could not guess--and that again went to his credit on the same score; for in the calmness of respectability he might have numbered fifty years as well as thirty.\n\nLittimer was in my room in the morning before I was up, to bring me that reproachful shaving-water, and to put out my clothes. When I undrew the curtains and looked out of bed, I saw him, in an equable temperature of respectability, unaffected by the east wind of January, and not even breathing frostily, standing my boots right and left in the first dancing position, and blowing specks of dust off my coat as he laid it down like a baby.\n\nI gave him good morning, and asked him what o’clock it was. He took out of his pocket the most respectable hunting-watch I ever saw, and preventing the spring with his thumb from opening far, looked in at the face as if he were consulting an oracular oyster, shut it up again, and said, if I pleased, it was half past eight.\n\n‘Mr. Steerforth will be glad to hear how you have rested, sir.’\n\n‘Thank you,’ said I, ‘very well indeed. Is Mr. Steerforth quite well?’\n\n‘Thank you, sir, Mr. Steerforth is tolerably well.’ Another of his characteristics--no use of superlatives. A cool calm medium always.\n\n‘Is there anything more I can have the honour of doing for you, sir? The warning-bell will ring at nine; the family take breakfast at half past nine.’\n\n‘Nothing, I thank you.’\n\n‘I thank YOU, sir, if you please’; and with that, and with a little inclination of his head when he passed the bed-side, as an apology for correcting me, he went out, shutting the door as delicately as if I had just fallen into a sweet sleep on which my life depended.\n\nEvery morning we held exactly this conversation: never any more, and never any less: and yet, invariably, however far I might have been lifted out of myself over-night, and advanced towards maturer years, by Steerforth’s companionship, or Mrs. Steerforth’s confidence, or Miss Dartle’s conversation, in the presence of this most respectable man I became, as our smaller poets sing, ‘a boy again’.\n\nHe got horses for us; and Steerforth, who knew everything, gave me lessons in riding. He provided foils for us, and Steerforth gave me lessons in fencing--gloves, and I began, of the same master, to improve in boxing. It gave me no manner of concern that Steerforth should find me a novice in these sciences, but I never could bear to show my want of skill before the respectable Littimer. I had no reason to believe that Littimer understood such arts himself; he never led me to suppose anything of the kind, by so much as the vibration of one of his respectable eyelashes; yet whenever he was by, while we were practising, I felt myself the greenest and most inexperienced of mortals.\n\nI am particular about this man, because he made a particular effect on me at that time, and because of what took place thereafter.\n\nThe week passed away in a most delightful manner. It passed rapidly, as may be supposed, to one entranced as I was; and yet it gave me so many occasions for knowing Steerforth better, and admiring him more in a thousand respects, that at its close I seemed to have been with him for a much longer time. A dashing way he had of treating me like a plaything, was more agreeable to me than any behaviour he could have adopted. It reminded me of our old acquaintance; it seemed the natural sequel of it; it showed me that he was unchanged; it relieved me of any uneasiness I might have felt, in comparing my merits with his, and measuring my claims upon his friendship by any equal standard; above all, it was a familiar, unrestrained, affectionate demeanour that he used towards no one else. As he had treated me at school differently from all the rest, I joyfully believed that he treated me in life unlike any other friend he had. I believed that I was nearer to his heart than any other friend, and my own heart warmed with attachment to him. He made up his mind to go with me into the country, and the day arrived for our departure. He had been doubtful at first whether to take Littimer or not, but decided to leave him at home. The respectable creature, satisfied with his lot whatever it was, arranged our portmanteaux on the little carriage that was to take us into London, as if they were intended to defy the shocks of ages, and received my modestly proffered donation with perfect tranquillity.\n\nWe bade adieu to Mrs. Steerforth and Miss Dartle, with many thanks on my part, and much kindness on the devoted mother’s. The last thing I saw was Littimer’s unruffled eye; fraught, as I fancied, with the silent conviction that I was very young indeed.\n\nWhat I felt, in returning so auspiciously to the old familiar places, I shall not endeavour to describe. We went down by the Mail. I was so concerned, I recollect, even for the honour of Yarmouth, that when Steerforth said, as we drove through its dark streets to the inn, that, as well as he could make out, it was a good, queer, out-of-the-way kind of hole, I was highly pleased. We went to bed on our arrival (I observed a pair of dirty shoes and gaiters in connexion with my old friend the Dolphin as we passed that door), and breakfasted late in the morning. Steerforth, who was in great spirits, had been strolling about the beach before I was up, and had made acquaintance, he said, with half the boatmen in the place. Moreover, he had seen, in the distance, what he was sure must be the identical house of Mr. Peggotty, with smoke coming out of the chimney; and had had a great mind, he told me, to walk in and swear he was myself grown out of knowledge.\n\n‘When do you propose to introduce me there, Daisy?’ he said. ‘I am at your disposal. Make your own arrangements.’\n\n‘Why, I was thinking that this evening would be a good time, Steerforth, when they are all sitting round the fire. I should like you to see it when it’s snug, it’s such a curious place.’\n\n‘So be it!’ returned Steerforth. ‘This evening.’\n\n‘I shall not give them any notice that we are here, you know,’ said I, delighted. ‘We must take them by surprise.’\n\n‘Oh, of course! It’s no fun,’ said Steerforth, ‘unless we take them by surprise. Let us see the natives in their aboriginal condition.’\n\n‘Though they ARE that sort of people that you mentioned,’ I returned.\n\n‘Aha! What! you recollect my skirmishes with Rosa, do you?’ he exclaimed with a quick look. ‘Confound the girl, I am half afraid of her. She’s like a goblin to me. But never mind her. Now what are you going to do? You are going to see your nurse, I suppose?’\n\n‘Why, yes,’ I said, ‘I must see Peggotty first of all.’\n\n‘Well,’ replied Steerforth, looking at his watch. ‘Suppose I deliver you up to be cried over for a couple of hours. Is that long enough?’\n\nI answered, laughing, that I thought we might get through it in that time, but that he must come also; for he would find that his renown had preceded him, and that he was almost as great a personage as I was.\n\n‘I’ll come anywhere you like,’ said Steerforth, ‘or do anything you like. Tell me where to come to; and in two hours I’ll produce myself in any state you please, sentimental or comical.’\n\nI gave him minute directions for finding the residence of Mr. Barkis, carrier to Blunderstone and elsewhere; and, on this understanding, went out alone. There was a sharp bracing air; the ground was dry; the sea was crisp and clear; the sun was diffusing abundance of light, if not much warmth; and everything was fresh and lively. I was so fresh and lively myself, in the pleasure of being there, that I could have stopped the people in the streets and shaken hands with them.\n\nThe streets looked small, of course. The streets that we have only seen as children always do, I believe, when we go back to them. But I had forgotten nothing in them, and found nothing changed, until I came to Mr. Omer’s shop. OMER AND Joram was now written up, where OMER used to be; but the inscription, DRAPER, TAILOR, HABERDASHER, FUNERAL FURNISHER, &c., remained as it was.\n\nMy footsteps seemed to tend so naturally to the shop door, after I had read these words from over the way, that I went across the road and looked in. There was a pretty woman at the back of the shop, dancing a little child in her arms, while another little fellow clung to her apron. I had no difficulty in recognizing either Minnie or Minnie’s children. The glass door of the parlour was not open; but in the workshop across the yard I could faintly hear the old tune playing, as if it had never left off.\n\n‘Is Mr. Omer at home?’ said I, entering. ‘I should like to see him, for a moment, if he is.’\n\n‘Oh yes, sir, he is at home,’ said Minnie; ‘the weather don’t suit his asthma out of doors. Joe, call your grandfather!’\n\nThe little fellow, who was holding her apron, gave such a lusty shout, that the sound of it made him bashful, and he buried his face in her skirts, to her great admiration. I heard a heavy puffing and blowing coming towards us, and soon Mr. Omer, shorter-winded than of yore, but not much older-looking, stood before me.\n\n‘Servant, sir,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘What can I do for you, sir?’ ‘You can shake hands with me, Mr. Omer, if you please,’ said I, putting out my own. ‘You were very good-natured to me once, when I am afraid I didn’t show that I thought so.’\n\n‘Was I though?’ returned the old man. ‘I’m glad to hear it, but I don’t remember when. Are you sure it was me?’\n\n‘Quite.’\n‘I think my memory has got as short as my breath,’ said Mr. Omer, looking at me and shaking his head; ‘for I don’t remember you.’\n\n‘Don’t you remember your coming to the coach to meet me, and my having breakfast here, and our riding out to Blunderstone together: you, and I, and Mrs. Joram, and Mr. Joram too--who wasn’t her husband then?’\n\n‘Why, Lord bless my soul!’ exclaimed Mr. Omer, after being thrown by his surprise into a fit of coughing, ‘you don’t say so! Minnie, my dear, you recollect? Dear me, yes; the party was a lady, I think?’\n\n‘My mother,’ I rejoined.\n\n‘To--be--sure,’ said Mr. Omer, touching my waistcoat with his forefinger, ‘and there was a little child too! There was two parties. The little party was laid along with the other party. Over at Blunderstone it was, of course. Dear me! And how have you been since?’\n\nVery well, I thanked him, as I hoped he had been too.\n\n‘Oh! nothing to grumble at, you know,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘I find my breath gets short, but it seldom gets longer as a man gets older. I take it as it comes, and make the most of it. That’s the best way, ain’t it?’\n\nMr. Omer coughed again, in consequence of laughing, and was assisted out of his fit by his daughter, who now stood close beside us, dancing her smallest child on the counter.\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Yes, to be sure. Two parties! Why, in that very ride, if you’ll believe me, the day was named for my Minnie to marry Joram. “Do name it, sir,” says Joram. “Yes, do, father,” says Minnie. And now he’s come into the business. And look here! The youngest!’\n\nMinnie laughed, and stroked her banded hair upon her temples, as her father put one of his fat fingers into the hand of the child she was dancing on the counter.\n\n‘Two parties, of course!’ said Mr. Omer, nodding his head retrospectively. ‘Ex-actly so! And Joram’s at work, at this minute, on a grey one with silver nails, not this measurement’--the measurement of the dancing child upon the counter--‘by a good two inches.---Will you take something?’\n\nI thanked him, but declined.\n\n‘Let me see,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Barkis’s the carrier’s wife--Peggotty’s the boatman’s sister--she had something to do with your family? She was in service there, sure?’\n\nMy answering in the affirmative gave him great satisfaction.\n\n‘I believe my breath will get long next, my memory’s getting so much so,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Well, sir, we’ve got a young relation of hers here, under articles to us, that has as elegant a taste in the dress-making business--I assure you I don’t believe there’s a Duchess in England can touch her.’\n\n‘Not little Em’ly?’ said I, involuntarily.\n\n‘Em’ly’s her name,’ said Mr. Omer, ‘and she’s little too. But if you’ll believe me, she has such a face of her own that half the women in this town are mad against her.’\n\n‘Nonsense, father!’ cried Minnie.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Omer, ‘I don’t say it’s the case with you,’ winking at me, ‘but I say that half the women in Yarmouth--ah! and in five mile round--are mad against that girl.’\n\n‘Then she should have kept to her own station in life, father,’ said Minnie, ‘and not have given them any hold to talk about her, and then they couldn’t have done it.’\n\n‘Couldn’t have done it, my dear!’ retorted Mr. Omer. ‘Couldn’t have done it! Is that YOUR knowledge of life? What is there that any woman couldn’t do, that she shouldn’t do--especially on the subject of another woman’s good looks?’\n\nI really thought it was all over with Mr. Omer, after he had uttered this libellous pleasantry. He coughed to that extent, and his breath eluded all his attempts to recover it with that obstinacy, that I fully expected to see his head go down behind the counter, and his little black breeches, with the rusty little bunches of ribbons at the knees, come quivering up in a last ineffectual struggle. At length, however, he got better, though he still panted hard, and was so exhausted that he was obliged to sit on the stool of the shop-desk.\n\n‘You see,’ he said, wiping his head, and breathing with difficulty, ‘she hasn’t taken much to any companions here; she hasn’t taken kindly to any particular acquaintances and friends, not to mention sweethearts. In consequence, an ill-natured story got about, that Em’ly wanted to be a lady. Now my opinion is, that it came into circulation principally on account of her sometimes saying, at the school, that if she was a lady she would like to do so-and-so for her uncle--don’t you see?--and buy him such-and-such fine things.’\n\n‘I assure you, Mr. Omer, she has said so to me,’ I returned eagerly, ‘when we were both children.’\n\nMr. Omer nodded his head and rubbed his chin. ‘Just so. Then out of a very little, she could dress herself, you see, better than most others could out of a deal, and that made things unpleasant. Moreover, she was rather what might be called wayward--I’ll go so far as to say what I should call wayward myself,’ said Mr. Omer; ‘didn’t know her own mind quite--a little spoiled--and couldn’t, at first, exactly bind herself down. No more than that was ever said against her, Minnie?’\n\n‘No, father,’ said Mrs. Joram. ‘That’s the worst, I believe.’\n\n‘So when she got a situation,’ said Mr. Omer, ‘to keep a fractious old lady company, they didn’t very well agree, and she didn’t stop. At last she came here, apprenticed for three years. Nearly two of ‘em are over, and she has been as good a girl as ever was. Worth any six! Minnie, is she worth any six, now?’\n\n‘Yes, father,’ replied Minnie. ‘Never say I detracted from her!’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘That’s right. And so, young gentleman,’ he added, after a few moments’ further rubbing of his chin, ‘that you may not consider me long-winded as well as short-breathed, I believe that’s all about it.’\n\nAs they had spoken in a subdued tone, while speaking of Em’ly, I had no doubt that she was near. On my asking now, if that were not so, Mr. Omer nodded yes, and nodded towards the door of the parlour. My hurried inquiry if I might peep in, was answered with a free permission; and, looking through the glass, I saw her sitting at her work. I saw her, a most beautiful little creature, with the cloudless blue eyes, that had looked into my childish heart, turned laughingly upon another child of Minnie’s who was playing near her; with enough of wilfulness in her bright face to justify what I had heard; with much of the old capricious coyness lurking in it; but with nothing in her pretty looks, I am sure, but what was meant for goodness and for happiness, and what was on a good and happy course.\n\nThe tune across the yard that seemed as if it never had left off--alas! it was the tune that never DOES leave off--was beating, softly, all the while.\n\n‘Wouldn’t you like to step in,’ said Mr. Omer, ‘and speak to her? Walk in and speak to her, sir! Make yourself at home!’\n\nI was too bashful to do so then--I was afraid of confusing her, and I was no less afraid of confusing myself.--but I informed myself of the hour at which she left of an evening, in order that our visit might be timed accordingly; and taking leave of Mr. Omer, and his pretty daughter, and her little children, went away to my dear old Peggotty’s.\n\nHere she was, in the tiled kitchen, cooking dinner! The moment I knocked at the door she opened it, and asked me what I pleased to want. I looked at her with a smile, but she gave me no smile in return. I had never ceased to write to her, but it must have been seven years since we had met.\n\n‘Is Mr. Barkis at home, ma’am?’ I said, feigning to speak roughly to her.\n\n‘He’s at home, sir,’ returned Peggotty, ‘but he’s bad abed with the rheumatics.’\n\n‘Don’t he go over to Blunderstone now?’ I asked.\n\n‘When he’s well he do,’ she answered.\n\n‘Do YOU ever go there, Mrs. Barkis?’\n\nShe looked at me more attentively, and I noticed a quick movement of her hands towards each other.\n\n‘Because I want to ask a question about a house there, that they call the--what is it?--the Rookery,’ said I.\n\nShe took a step backward, and put out her hands in an undecided frightened way, as if to keep me off.\n\n‘Peggotty!’ I cried to her.\n\nShe cried, ‘My darling boy!’ and we both burst into tears, and were locked in one another’s arms.\n\nWhat extravagances she committed; what laughing and crying over me; what pride she showed, what joy, what sorrow that she whose pride and joy I might have been, could never hold me in a fond embrace; I have not the heart to tell. I was troubled with no misgiving that it was young in me to respond to her emotions. I had never laughed and cried in all my life, I dare say--not even to her--more freely than I did that morning.\n\n‘Barkis will be so glad,’ said Peggotty, wiping her eyes with her apron, ‘that it’ll do him more good than pints of liniment. May I go and tell him you are here? Will you come up and see him, my dear?’\n\nOf course I would. But Peggotty could not get out of the room as easily as she meant to, for as often as she got to the door and looked round at me, she came back again to have another laugh and another cry upon my shoulder. At last, to make the matter easier, I went upstairs with her; and having waited outside for a minute, while she said a word of preparation to Mr. Barkis, presented myself before that invalid.\n\nHe received me with absolute enthusiasm. He was too rheumatic to be shaken hands with, but he begged me to shake the tassel on the top of his nightcap, which I did most cordially. When I sat down by the side of the bed, he said that it did him a world of good to feel as if he was driving me on the Blunderstone road again. As he lay in bed, face upward, and so covered, with that exception, that he seemed to be nothing but a face--like a conventional cherubim--he looked the queerest object I ever beheld.\n\n‘What name was it, as I wrote up in the cart, sir?’ said Mr. Barkis, with a slow rheumatic smile.\n\n‘Ah! Mr. Barkis, we had some grave talks about that matter, hadn’t we?’\n\n‘I was willin’ a long time, sir?’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\n‘A long time,’ said I.\n\n‘And I don’t regret it,’ said Mr. Barkis. ‘Do you remember what you told me once, about her making all the apple parsties and doing all the cooking?’\n\n‘Yes, very well,’ I returned.\n\n‘It was as true,’ said Mr. Barkis, ‘as turnips is. It was as true,’ said Mr. Barkis, nodding his nightcap, which was his only means of emphasis, ‘as taxes is. And nothing’s truer than them.’\n\nMr. Barkis turned his eyes upon me, as if for my assent to this result of his reflections in bed; and I gave it.\n\n‘Nothing’s truer than them,’ repeated Mr. Barkis; ‘a man as poor as I am, finds that out in his mind when he’s laid up. I’m a very poor man, sir!’\n\n‘I am sorry to hear it, Mr. Barkis.’\n\n‘A very poor man, indeed I am,’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\nHere his right hand came slowly and feebly from under the bedclothes, and with a purposeless uncertain grasp took hold of a stick which was loosely tied to the side of the bed. After some poking about with this instrument, in the course of which his face assumed a variety of distracted expressions, Mr. Barkis poked it against a box, an end of which had been visible to me all the time. Then his face became composed.\n\n‘Old clothes,’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\n‘Oh!’ said I.\n\n‘I wish it was Money, sir,’ said Mr. Barkis.\n\n‘I wish it was, indeed,’ said I.\n\n‘But it AIN’T,’ said Mr. Barkis, opening both his eyes as wide as he possibly could.\n\nI expressed myself quite sure of that, and Mr. Barkis, turning his eyes more gently to his wife, said:\n\n‘She’s the usefullest and best of women, C. P. Barkis. All the praise that anyone can give to C. P. Barkis, she deserves, and more! My dear, you’ll get a dinner today, for company; something good to eat and drink, will you?’\n\nI should have protested against this unnecessary demonstration in my honour, but that I saw Peggotty, on the opposite side of the bed, extremely anxious I should not. So I held my peace.\n\n‘I have got a trifle of money somewhere about me, my dear,’ said Mr. Barkis, ‘but I’m a little tired. If you and Mr. David will leave me for a short nap, I’ll try and find it when I wake.’\n\nWe left the room, in compliance with this request. When we got outside the door, Peggotty informed me that Mr. Barkis, being now ‘a little nearer’ than he used to be, always resorted to this same device before producing a single coin from his store; and that he endured unheard-of agonies in crawling out of bed alone, and taking it from that unlucky box. In effect, we presently heard him uttering suppressed groans of the most dismal nature, as this magpie proceeding racked him in every joint; but while Peggotty’s eyes were full of compassion for him, she said his generous impulse would do him good, and it was better not to check it. So he groaned on, until he had got into bed again, suffering, I have no doubt, a martyrdom; and then called us in, pretending to have just woke up from a refreshing sleep, and to produce a guinea from under his pillow. His satisfaction in which happy imposition on us, and in having preserved the impenetrable secret of the box, appeared to be a sufficient compensation to him for all his tortures.\n\nI prepared Peggotty for Steerforth’s arrival and it was not long before he came. I am persuaded she knew no difference between his having been a personal benefactor of hers, and a kind friend to me, and that she would have received him with the utmost gratitude and devotion in any case. But his easy, spirited good humour; his genial manner, his handsome looks, his natural gift of adapting himself to whomsoever he pleased, and making direct, when he cared to do it, to the main point of interest in anybody’s heart; bound her to him wholly in five minutes. His manner to me, alone, would have won her. But, through all these causes combined, I sincerely believe she had a kind of adoration for him before he left the house that night.\n\nHe stayed there with me to dinner--if I were to say willingly, I should not half express how readily and gaily. He went into Mr. Barkis’s room like light and air, brightening and refreshing it as if he were healthy weather. There was no noise, no effort, no consciousness, in anything he did; but in everything an indescribable lightness, a seeming impossibility of doing anything else, or doing anything better, which was so graceful, so natural, and agreeable, that it overcomes me, even now, in the remembrance.\n\nWe made merry in the little parlour, where the Book of Martyrs, unthumbed since my time, was laid out upon the desk as of old, and where I now turned over its terrific pictures, remembering the old sensations they had awakened, but not feeling them. When Peggotty spoke of what she called my room, and of its being ready for me at night, and of her hoping I would occupy it, before I could so much as look at Steerforth, hesitating, he was possessed of the whole case.\n\n‘Of course,’ he said. ‘You’ll sleep here, while we stay, and I shall sleep at the hotel.’\n\n‘But to bring you so far,’ I returned, ‘and to separate, seems bad companionship, Steerforth.’\n\n‘Why, in the name of Heaven, where do you naturally belong?’ he said. ‘What is “seems”, compared to that?’ It was settled at once.\n\nHe maintained all his delightful qualities to the last, until we started forth, at eight o’clock, for Mr. Peggotty’s boat. Indeed, they were more and more brightly exhibited as the hours went on; for I thought even then, and I have no doubt now, that the consciousness of success in his determination to please, inspired him with a new delicacy of perception, and made it, subtle as it was, more easy to him. If anyone had told me, then, that all this was a brilliant game, played for the excitement of the moment, for the employment of high spirits, in the thoughtless love of superiority, in a mere wasteful careless course of winning what was worthless to him, and next minute thrown away--I say, if anyone had told me such a lie that night, I wonder in what manner of receiving it my indignation would have found a vent! Probably only in an increase, had that been possible, of the romantic feelings of fidelity and friendship with which I walked beside him, over the dark wintry sands towards the old boat; the wind sighing around us even more mournfully, than it had sighed and moaned upon the night when I first darkened Mr. Peggotty’s door.\n\n‘This is a wild kind of place, Steerforth, is it not?’\n\n‘Dismal enough in the dark,’ he said: ‘and the sea roars as if it were hungry for us. Is that the boat, where I see a light yonder?’ ‘That’s the boat,’ said I.\n\n‘And it’s the same I saw this morning,’ he returned. ‘I came straight to it, by instinct, I suppose.’\n\nWe said no more as we approached the light, but made softly for the door. I laid my hand upon the latch; and whispering Steerforth to keep close to me, went in.\n\nA murmur of voices had been audible on the outside, and, at the moment of our entrance, a clapping of hands: which latter noise, I was surprised to see, proceeded from the generally disconsolate Mrs. Gummidge. But Mrs. Gummidge was not the only person there who was unusually excited. Mr. Peggotty, his face lighted up with uncommon satisfaction, and laughing with all his might, held his rough arms wide open, as if for little Em’ly to run into them; Ham, with a mixed expression in his face of admiration, exultation, and a lumbering sort of bashfulness that sat upon him very well, held little Em’ly by the hand, as if he were presenting her to Mr. Peggotty; little Em’ly herself, blushing and shy, but delighted with Mr. Peggotty’s delight, as her joyous eyes expressed, was stopped by our entrance (for she saw us first) in the very act of springing from Ham to nestle in Mr. Peggotty’s embrace. In the first glimpse we had of them all, and at the moment of our passing from the dark cold night into the warm light room, this was the way in which they were all employed: Mrs. Gummidge in the background, clapping her hands like a madwoman.\n\nThe little picture was so instantaneously dissolved by our going in, that one might have doubted whether it had ever been. I was in the midst of the astonished family, face to face with Mr. Peggotty, and holding out my hand to him, when Ham shouted:\n\n‘Mas’r Davy! It’s Mas’r Davy!’\n\nIn a moment we were all shaking hands with one another, and asking one another how we did, and telling one another how glad we were to meet, and all talking at once. Mr. Peggotty was so proud and overjoyed to see us, that he did not know what to say or do, but kept over and over again shaking hands with me, and then with Steerforth, and then with me, and then ruffling his shaggy hair all over his head, and laughing with such glee and triumph, that it was a treat to see him.\n\n‘Why, that you two gent’lmen--gent’lmen growed--should come to this here roof tonight, of all nights in my life,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘is such a thing as never happened afore, I do rightly believe! Em’ly, my darling, come here! Come here, my little witch! There’s Mas’r Davy’s friend, my dear! There’s the gent’lman as you’ve heerd on, Em’ly. He comes to see you, along with Mas’r Davy, on the brightest night of your uncle’s life as ever was or will be, Gorm the t’other one, and horroar for it!’\n\nAfter delivering this speech all in a breath, and with extraordinary animation and pleasure, Mr. Peggotty put one of his large hands rapturously on each side of his niece’s face, and kissing it a dozen times, laid it with a gentle pride and love upon his broad chest, and patted it as if his hand had been a lady’s. Then he let her go; and as she ran into the little chamber where I used to sleep, looked round upon us, quite hot and out of breath with his uncommon satisfaction.\n\n‘If you two gent’lmen--gent’lmen growed now, and such gent’lmen--’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘So th’ are, so th’ are!’ cried Ham. ‘Well said! So th’ are. Mas’r Davy bor’--gent’lmen growed--so th’ are!’\n\n‘If you two gent’lmen, gent’lmen growed,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘don’t ex-cuse me for being in a state of mind, when you understand matters, I’ll arks your pardon. Em’ly, my dear!--She knows I’m a going to tell,’ here his delight broke out again, ‘and has made off. Would you be so good as look arter her, Mawther, for a minute?’\n\nMrs. Gummidge nodded and disappeared.\n\n‘If this ain’t,’ said Mr. Peggotty, sitting down among us by the fire, ‘the brightest night o’ my life, I’m a shellfish--biled too--and more I can’t say. This here little Em’ly, sir,’ in a low voice to Steerforth, ‘--her as you see a blushing here just now--’\n\nSteerforth only nodded; but with such a pleased expression of interest, and of participation in Mr. Peggotty’s feelings, that the latter answered him as if he had spoken.\n\n‘To be sure,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘That’s her, and so she is. Thankee, sir.’\n\nHam nodded to me several times, as if he would have said so too.\n\n‘This here little Em’ly of ours,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘has been, in our house, what I suppose (I’m a ignorant man, but that’s my belief) no one but a little bright-eyed creetur can be in a house. She ain’t my child; I never had one; but I couldn’t love her more. You understand! I couldn’t do it!’\n\n‘I quite understand,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘I know you do, sir,’ returned Mr. Peggotty, ‘and thankee again. Mas’r Davy, he can remember what she was; you may judge for your own self what she is; but neither of you can’t fully know what she has been, is, and will be, to my loving art. I am rough, sir,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘I am as rough as a Sea Porkypine; but no one, unless, mayhap, it is a woman, can know, I think, what our little Em’ly is to me. And betwixt ourselves,’ sinking his voice lower yet, ‘that woman’s name ain’t Missis Gummidge neither, though she has a world of merits.’ Mr. Peggotty ruffled his hair again, with both hands, as a further preparation for what he was going to say, and went on, with a hand upon each of his knees:\n\n‘There was a certain person as had know’d our Em’ly, from the time when her father was drownded; as had seen her constant; when a babby, when a young gal, when a woman. Not much of a person to look at, he warn’t,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘something o’ my own build--rough--a good deal o’ the sou’-wester in him--wery salt--but, on the whole, a honest sort of a chap, with his art in the right place.’\n\nI thought I had never seen Ham grin to anything like the extent to which he sat grinning at us now.\n\n‘What does this here blessed tarpaulin go and do,’ said Mr. Peggotty, with his face one high noon of enjoyment, ‘but he loses that there art of his to our little Em’ly. He follers her about, he makes hisself a sort o’ servant to her, he loses in a great measure his relish for his wittles, and in the long-run he makes it clear to me wot’s amiss. Now I could wish myself, you see, that our little Em’ly was in a fair way of being married. I could wish to see her, at all ewents, under articles to a honest man as had a right to defend her. I don’t know how long I may live, or how soon I may die; but I know that if I was capsized, any night, in a gale of wind in Yarmouth Roads here, and was to see the town-lights shining for the last time over the rollers as I couldn’t make no head against, I could go down quieter for thinking “There’s a man ashore there, iron-true to my little Em’ly, God bless her, and no wrong can touch my Em’ly while so be as that man lives.”’\n\nMr. Peggotty, in simple earnestness, waved his right arm, as if he were waving it at the town-lights for the last time, and then, exchanging a nod with Ham, whose eye he caught, proceeded as before.\n\n‘Well! I counsels him to speak to Em’ly. He’s big enough, but he’s bashfuller than a little un, and he don’t like. So I speak. “What! Him!” says Em’ly. “Him that I’ve know’d so intimate so many years, and like so much. Oh, Uncle! I never can have him. He’s such a good fellow!” I gives her a kiss, and I says no more to her than, “My dear, you’re right to speak out, you’re to choose for yourself, you’re as free as a little bird.” Then I aways to him, and I says, “I wish it could have been so, but it can’t. But you can both be as you was, and wot I say to you is, Be as you was with her, like a man.” He says to me, a-shaking of my hand, “I will!” he says. And he was--honourable and manful--for two year going on, and we was just the same at home here as afore.’\n\nMr. Peggotty’s face, which had varied in its expression with the various stages of his narrative, now resumed all its former triumphant delight, as he laid a hand upon my knee and a hand upon Steerforth’s (previously wetting them both, for the greater emphasis of the action), and divided the following speech between us:\n\n‘All of a sudden, one evening--as it might be tonight--comes little Em’ly from her work, and him with her! There ain’t so much in that, you’ll say. No, because he takes care on her, like a brother, arter dark, and indeed afore dark, and at all times. But this tarpaulin chap, he takes hold of her hand, and he cries out to me, joyful, “Look here! This is to be my little wife!” And she says, half bold and half shy, and half a laughing and half a crying, “Yes, Uncle! If you please.”--If I please!’ cried Mr. Peggotty, rolling his head in an ecstasy at the idea; ‘Lord, as if I should do anythink else!--“If you please, I am steadier now, and I have thought better of it, and I’ll be as good a little wife as I can to him, for he’s a dear, good fellow!” Then Missis Gummidge, she claps her hands like a play, and you come in. Theer! the murder’s out!’ said Mr. Peggotty--‘You come in! It took place this here present hour; and here’s the man that’ll marry her, the minute she’s out of her time.’\n\nHam staggered, as well he might, under the blow Mr. Peggotty dealt him in his unbounded joy, as a mark of confidence and friendship; but feeling called upon to say something to us, he said, with much faltering and great difficulty:\n\n‘She warn’t no higher than you was, Mas’r Davy--when you first come--when I thought what she’d grow up to be. I see her grown up--gent’lmen--like a flower. I’d lay down my life for her--Mas’r Davy--Oh! most content and cheerful! She’s more to me--gent’lmen--than--she’s all to me that ever I can want, and more than ever I--than ever I could say. I--I love her true. There ain’t a gent’lman in all the land--nor yet sailing upon all the sea--that can love his lady more than I love her, though there’s many a common man--would say better--what he meant.’\n\nI thought it affecting to see such a sturdy fellow as Ham was now, trembling in the strength of what he felt for the pretty little creature who had won his heart. I thought the simple confidence reposed in us by Mr. Peggotty and by himself, was, in itself, affecting. I was affected by the story altogether. How far my emotions were influenced by the recollections of my childhood, I don’t know. Whether I had come there with any lingering fancy that I was still to love little Em’ly, I don’t know. I know that I was filled with pleasure by all this; but, at first, with an indescribably sensitive pleasure, that a very little would have changed to pain.\n\nTherefore, if it had depended upon me to touch the prevailing chord among them with any skill, I should have made a poor hand of it. But it depended upon Steerforth; and he did it with such address, that in a few minutes we were all as easy and as happy as it was possible to be.\n\n‘Mr. Peggotty,’ he said, ‘you are a thoroughly good fellow, and deserve to be as happy as you are tonight. My hand upon it! Ham, I give you joy, my boy. My hand upon that, too! Daisy, stir the fire, and make it a brisk one! and Mr. Peggotty, unless you can induce your gentle niece to come back (for whom I vacate this seat in the corner), I shall go. Any gap at your fireside on such a night--such a gap least of all--I wouldn’t make, for the wealth of the Indies!’\n\nSo Mr. Peggotty went into my old room to fetch little Em’ly. At first little Em’ly didn’t like to come, and then Ham went. Presently they brought her to the fireside, very much confused, and very shy,--but she soon became more assured when she found how gently and respectfully Steerforth spoke to her; how skilfully he avoided anything that would embarrass her; how he talked to Mr. Peggotty of boats, and ships, and tides, and fish; how he referred to me about the time when he had seen Mr. Peggotty at Salem House; how delighted he was with the boat and all belonging to it; how lightly and easily he carried on, until he brought us, by degrees, into a charmed circle, and we were all talking away without any reserve.\n\nEm’ly, indeed, said little all the evening; but she looked, and listened, and her face got animated, and she was charming. Steerforth told a story of a dismal shipwreck (which arose out of his talk with Mr. Peggotty), as if he saw it all before him--and little Em’ly’s eyes were fastened on him all the time, as if she saw it too. He told us a merry adventure of his own, as a relief to that, with as much gaiety as if the narrative were as fresh to him as it was to us--and little Em’ly laughed until the boat rang with the musical sounds, and we all laughed (Steerforth too), in irresistible sympathy with what was so pleasant and light-hearted. He got Mr. Peggotty to sing, or rather to roar, ‘When the stormy winds do blow, do blow, do blow’; and he sang a sailor’s song himself, so pathetically and beautifully, that I could have almost fancied that the real wind creeping sorrowfully round the house, and murmuring low through our unbroken silence, was there to listen.\n\nAs to Mrs. Gummidge, he roused that victim of despondency with a success never attained by anyone else (so Mr. Peggotty informed me), since the decease of the old one. He left her so little leisure for being miserable, that she said next day she thought she must have been bewitched.\n\nBut he set up no monopoly of the general attention, or the conversation. When little Em’ly grew more courageous, and talked (but still bashfully) across the fire to me, of our old wanderings upon the beach, to pick up shells and pebbles; and when I asked her if she recollected how I used to be devoted to her; and when we both laughed and reddened, casting these looks back on the pleasant old times, so unreal to look at now; he was silent and attentive, and observed us thoughtfully. She sat, at this time, and all the evening, on the old locker in her old little corner by the fire--Ham beside her, where I used to sit. I could not satisfy myself whether it was in her own little tormenting way, or in a maidenly reserve before us, that she kept quite close to the wall, and away from him; but I observed that she did so, all the evening.\n\nAs I remember, it was almost midnight when we took our leave. We had had some biscuit and dried fish for supper, and Steerforth had produced from his pocket a full flask of Hollands, which we men (I may say we men, now, without a blush) had emptied. We parted merrily; and as they all stood crowded round the door to light us as far as they could upon our road, I saw the sweet blue eyes of little Em’ly peeping after us, from behind Ham, and heard her soft voice calling to us to be careful how we went.\n\n‘A most engaging little Beauty!’ said Steerforth, taking my arm. ‘Well! It’s a quaint place, and they are quaint company, and it’s quite a new sensation to mix with them.’\n\n‘How fortunate we are, too,’ I returned, ‘to have arrived to witness their happiness in that intended marriage! I never saw people so happy. How delightful to see it, and to be made the sharers in their honest joy, as we have been!’\n\n‘That’s rather a chuckle-headed fellow for the girl; isn’t he?’ said Steerforth.\n\nHe had been so hearty with him, and with them all, that I felt a shock in this unexpected and cold reply. But turning quickly upon him, and seeing a laugh in his eyes, I answered, much relieved:\n\n‘Ah, Steerforth! It’s well for you to joke about the poor! You may skirmish with Miss Dartle, or try to hide your sympathies in jest from me, but I know better. When I see how perfectly you understand them, how exquisitely you can enter into happiness like this plain fisherman’s, or humour a love like my old nurse’s, I know that there is not a joy or sorrow, not an emotion, of such people, that can be indifferent to you. And I admire and love you for it, Steerforth, twenty times the more!’\n\nHe stopped, and, looking in my face, said, ‘Daisy, I believe you are in earnest, and are good. I wish we all were!’ Next moment he was gaily singing Mr. Peggotty’s song, as we walked at a round pace back to Yarmouth.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. SOME OLD SCENES, AND SOME NEW PEOPLE\n\nSteerforth and I stayed for more than a fortnight in that part of the country. We were very much together, I need not say; but occasionally we were asunder for some hours at a time. He was a good sailor, and I was but an indifferent one; and when he went out boating with Mr. Peggotty, which was a favourite amusement of his, I generally remained ashore. My occupation of Peggotty’s spare-room put a constraint upon me, from which he was free: for, knowing how assiduously she attended on Mr. Barkis all day, I did not like to remain out late at night; whereas Steerforth, lying at the Inn, had nothing to consult but his own humour. Thus it came about, that I heard of his making little treats for the fishermen at Mr. Peggotty’s house of call, ‘The Willing Mind’, after I was in bed, and of his being afloat, wrapped in fishermen’s clothes, whole moonlight nights, and coming back when the morning tide was at flood. By this time, however, I knew that his restless nature and bold spirits delighted to find a vent in rough toil and hard weather, as in any other means of excitement that presented itself freshly to him; so none of his proceedings surprised me.\n\nAnother cause of our being sometimes apart, was, that I had naturally an interest in going over to Blunderstone, and revisiting the old familiar scenes of my childhood; while Steerforth, after being there once, had naturally no great interest in going there again. Hence, on three or four days that I can at once recall, we went our several ways after an early breakfast, and met again at a late dinner. I had no idea how he employed his time in the interval, beyond a general knowledge that he was very popular in the place, and had twenty means of actively diverting himself where another man might not have found one.\n\nFor my own part, my occupation in my solitary pilgrimages was to recall every yard of the old road as I went along it, and to haunt the old spots, of which I never tired. I haunted them, as my memory had often done, and lingered among them as my younger thoughts had lingered when I was far away. The grave beneath the tree, where both my parents lay--on which I had looked out, when it was my father’s only, with such curious feelings of compassion, and by which I had stood, so desolate, when it was opened to receive my pretty mother and her baby--the grave which Peggotty’s own faithful care had ever since kept neat, and made a garden of, I walked near, by the hour. It lay a little off the churchyard path, in a quiet corner, not so far removed but I could read the names upon the stone as I walked to and fro, startled by the sound of the church-bell when it struck the hour, for it was like a departed voice to me. My reflections at these times were always associated with the figure I was to make in life, and the distinguished things I was to do. My echoing footsteps went to no other tune, but were as constant to that as if I had come home to build my castles in the air at a living mother’s side.\n\nThere were great changes in my old home. The ragged nests, so long deserted by the rooks, were gone; and the trees were lopped and topped out of their remembered shapes. The garden had run wild, and half the windows of the house were shut up. It was occupied, but only by a poor lunatic gentleman, and the people who took care of him. He was always sitting at my little window, looking out into the churchyard; and I wondered whether his rambling thoughts ever went upon any of the fancies that used to occupy mine, on the rosy mornings when I peeped out of that same little window in my night-clothes, and saw the sheep quietly feeding in the light of the rising sun.\n\nOur old neighbours, Mr. and Mrs. Grayper, were gone to South America, and the rain had made its way through the roof of their empty house, and stained the outer walls. Mr. Chillip was married again to a tall, raw-boned, high-nosed wife; and they had a weazen little baby, with a heavy head that it couldn’t hold up, and two weak staring eyes, with which it seemed to be always wondering why it had ever been born.\n\nIt was with a singular jumble of sadness and pleasure that I used to linger about my native place, until the reddening winter sun admonished me that it was time to start on my returning walk. But, when the place was left behind, and especially when Steerforth and I were happily seated over our dinner by a blazing fire, it was delicious to think of having been there. So it was, though in a softened degree, when I went to my neat room at night; and, turning over the leaves of the crocodile-book (which was always there, upon a little table), remembered with a grateful heart how blest I was in having such a friend as Steerforth, such a friend as Peggotty, and such a substitute for what I had lost as my excellent and generous aunt.\n\nMy nearest way to Yarmouth, in coming back from these long walks, was by a ferry. It landed me on the flat between the town and the sea, which I could make straight across, and so save myself a considerable circuit by the high road. Mr. Peggotty’s house being on that waste-place, and not a hundred yards out of my track, I always looked in as I went by. Steerforth was pretty sure to be there expecting me, and we went on together through the frosty air and gathering fog towards the twinkling lights of the town.\n\nOne dark evening, when I was later than usual--for I had, that day, been making my parting visit to Blunderstone, as we were now about to return home--I found him alone in Mr. Peggotty’s house, sitting thoughtfully before the fire. He was so intent upon his own reflections that he was quite unconscious of my approach. This, indeed, he might easily have been if he had been less absorbed, for footsteps fell noiselessly on the sandy ground outside; but even my entrance failed to rouse him. I was standing close to him, looking at him; and still, with a heavy brow, he was lost in his meditations.\n\nHe gave such a start when I put my hand upon his shoulder, that he made me start too.\n\n‘You come upon me,’ he said, almost angrily, ‘like a reproachful ghost!’\n\n‘I was obliged to announce myself, somehow,’ I replied. ‘Have I called you down from the stars?’\n\n‘No,’ he answered. ‘No.’\n\n‘Up from anywhere, then?’ said I, taking my seat near him.\n\n‘I was looking at the pictures in the fire,’ he returned.\n\n‘But you are spoiling them for me,’ said I, as he stirred it quickly with a piece of burning wood, striking out of it a train of red-hot sparks that went careering up the little chimney, and roaring out into the air.\n\n‘You would not have seen them,’ he returned. ‘I detest this mongrel time, neither day nor night. How late you are! Where have you been?’\n\n‘I have been taking leave of my usual walk,’ said I.\n\n‘And I have been sitting here,’ said Steerforth, glancing round the room, ‘thinking that all the people we found so glad on the night of our coming down, might--to judge from the present wasted air of the place--be dispersed, or dead, or come to I don’t know what harm. David, I wish to God I had had a judicious father these last twenty years!’\n\n‘My dear Steerforth, what is the matter?’\n\n‘I wish with all my soul I had been better guided!’ he exclaimed. ‘I wish with all my soul I could guide myself better!’\n\nThere was a passionate dejection in his manner that quite amazed me. He was more unlike himself than I could have supposed possible.\n\n‘It would be better to be this poor Peggotty, or his lout of a nephew,’ he said, getting up and leaning moodily against the chimney-piece, with his face towards the fire, ‘than to be myself, twenty times richer and twenty times wiser, and be the torment to myself that I have been, in this Devil’s bark of a boat, within the last half-hour!’\n\nI was so confounded by the alteration in him, that at first I could only observe him in silence, as he stood leaning his head upon his hand, and looking gloomily down at the fire. At length I begged him, with all the earnestness I felt, to tell me what had occurred to cross him so unusually, and to let me sympathize with him, if I could not hope to advise him. Before I had well concluded, he began to laugh--fretfully at first, but soon with returning gaiety.\n\n‘Tut, it’s nothing, Daisy! nothing!’ he replied. ‘I told you at the inn in London, I am heavy company for myself, sometimes. I have been a nightmare to myself, just now--must have had one, I think. At odd dull times, nursery tales come up into the memory, unrecognized for what they are. I believe I have been confounding myself with the bad boy who “didn’t care”, and became food for lions--a grander kind of going to the dogs, I suppose. What old women call the horrors, have been creeping over me from head to foot. I have been afraid of myself.’\n\n‘You are afraid of nothing else, I think,’ said I.\n\n‘Perhaps not, and yet may have enough to be afraid of too,’ he answered. ‘Well! So it goes by! I am not about to be hipped again, David; but I tell you, my good fellow, once more, that it would have been well for me (and for more than me) if I had had a steadfast and judicious father!’\n\nHis face was always full of expression, but I never saw it express such a dark kind of earnestness as when he said these words, with his glance bent on the fire.\n\n‘So much for that!’ he said, making as if he tossed something light into the air, with his hand. “‘Why, being gone, I am a man again,” like Macbeth. And now for dinner! If I have not (Macbeth-like) broken up the feast with most admired disorder, Daisy.’\n\n‘But where are they all, I wonder!’ said I.\n\n‘God knows,’ said Steerforth. ‘After strolling to the ferry looking for you, I strolled in here and found the place deserted. That set me thinking, and you found me thinking.’\n\nThe advent of Mrs. Gummidge with a basket, explained how the house had happened to be empty. She had hurried out to buy something that was needed, against Mr. Peggotty’s return with the tide; and had left the door open in the meanwhile, lest Ham and little Em’ly, with whom it was an early night, should come home while she was gone. Steerforth, after very much improving Mrs. Gummidge’s spirits by a cheerful salutation and a jocose embrace, took my arm, and hurried me away.\n\nHe had improved his own spirits, no less than Mrs. Gummidge’s, for they were again at their usual flow, and he was full of vivacious conversation as we went along.\n\n‘And so,’ he said, gaily, ‘we abandon this buccaneer life tomorrow, do we?’\n\n‘So we agreed,’ I returned. ‘And our places by the coach are taken, you know.’\n\n‘Ay! there’s no help for it, I suppose,’ said Steerforth. ‘I have almost forgotten that there is anything to do in the world but to go out tossing on the sea here. I wish there was not.’\n\n‘As long as the novelty should last,’ said I, laughing.\n\n‘Like enough,’ he returned; ‘though there’s a sarcastic meaning in that observation for an amiable piece of innocence like my young friend. Well! I dare say I am a capricious fellow, David. I know I am; but while the iron is hot, I can strike it vigorously too. I could pass a reasonably good examination already, as a pilot in these waters, I think.’\n\n‘Mr. Peggotty says you are a wonder,’ I returned.\n\n‘A nautical phenomenon, eh?’ laughed Steerforth.\n\n‘Indeed he does, and you know how truly; I know how ardent you are in any pursuit you follow, and how easily you can master it. And that amazes me most in you, Steerforth--that you should be contented with such fitful uses of your powers.’\n\n‘Contented?’ he answered, merrily. ‘I am never contented, except with your freshness, my gentle Daisy. As to fitfulness, I have never learnt the art of binding myself to any of the wheels on which the Ixions of these days are turning round and round. I missed it somehow in a bad apprenticeship, and now don’t care about it.---You know I have bought a boat down here?’\n\n‘What an extraordinary fellow you are, Steerforth!’ I exclaimed, stopping--for this was the first I had heard of it. ‘When you may never care to come near the place again!’\n\n‘I don’t know that,’ he returned. ‘I have taken a fancy to the place. At all events,’ walking me briskly on, ‘I have bought a boat that was for sale--a clipper, Mr. Peggotty says; and so she is--and Mr. Peggotty will be master of her in my absence.’\n\n‘Now I understand you, Steerforth!’ said I, exultingly. ‘You pretend to have bought it for yourself, but you have really done so to confer a benefit on him. I might have known as much at first, knowing you. My dear kind Steerforth, how can I tell you what I think of your generosity?’\n\n‘Tush!’ he answered, turning red. ‘The less said, the better.’\n\n‘Didn’t I know?’ cried I, ‘didn’t I say that there was not a joy, or sorrow, or any emotion of such honest hearts that was indifferent to you?’\n\n‘Aye, aye,’ he answered, ‘you told me all that. There let it rest. We have said enough!’\n\nAfraid of offending him by pursuing the subject when he made so light of it, I only pursued it in my thoughts as we went on at even a quicker pace than before.\n\n‘She must be newly rigged,’ said Steerforth, ‘and I shall leave Littimer behind to see it done, that I may know she is quite complete. Did I tell you Littimer had come down?’\n\n‘No.’\n\n‘Oh yes! came down this morning, with a letter from my mother.’\n\nAs our looks met, I observed that he was pale even to his lips, though he looked very steadily at me. I feared that some difference between him and his mother might have led to his being in the frame of mind in which I had found him at the solitary fireside. I hinted so.\n\n‘Oh no!’ he said, shaking his head, and giving a slight laugh. ‘Nothing of the sort! Yes. He is come down, that man of mine.’\n\n‘The same as ever?’ said I.\n\n‘The same as ever,’ said Steerforth. ‘Distant and quiet as the North Pole. He shall see to the boat being fresh named. She’s the “Stormy Petrel” now. What does Mr. Peggotty care for Stormy Petrels! I’ll have her christened again.’\n\n‘By what name?’ I asked.\n\n‘The “Little Em’ly”.’\n\nAs he had continued to look steadily at me, I took it as a reminder that he objected to being extolled for his consideration. I could not help showing in my face how much it pleased me, but I said little, and he resumed his usual smile, and seemed relieved.\n\n‘But see here,’ he said, looking before us, ‘where the original little Em’ly comes! And that fellow with her, eh? Upon my soul, he’s a true knight. He never leaves her!’\n\nHam was a boat-builder in these days, having improved a natural ingenuity in that handicraft, until he had become a skilled workman. He was in his working-dress, and looked rugged enough, but manly withal, and a very fit protector for the blooming little creature at his side. Indeed, there was a frankness in his face, an honesty, and an undisguised show of his pride in her, and his love for her, which were, to me, the best of good looks. I thought, as they came towards us, that they were well matched even in that particular.\n\nShe withdrew her hand timidly from his arm as we stopped to speak to them, and blushed as she gave it to Steerforth and to me. When they passed on, after we had exchanged a few words, she did not like to replace that hand, but, still appearing timid and constrained, walked by herself. I thought all this very pretty and engaging, and Steerforth seemed to think so too, as we looked after them fading away in the light of a young moon.\n\nSuddenly there passed us--evidently following them--a young woman whose approach we had not observed, but whose face I saw as she went by, and thought I had a faint remembrance of. She was lightly dressed; looked bold, and haggard, and flaunting, and poor; but seemed, for the time, to have given all that to the wind which was blowing, and to have nothing in her mind but going after them. As the dark distant level, absorbing their figures into itself, left but itself visible between us and the sea and clouds, her figure disappeared in like manner, still no nearer to them than before.\n\n‘That is a black shadow to be following the girl,’ said Steerforth, standing still; ‘what does it mean?’\n\nHe spoke in a low voice that sounded almost strange to Me.\n\n‘She must have it in her mind to beg of them, I think,’ said I.\n\n‘A beggar would be no novelty,’ said Steerforth; ‘but it is a strange thing that the beggar should take that shape tonight.’\n\n‘Why?’ I asked.\n\n‘For no better reason, truly, than because I was thinking,’ he said, after a pause, ‘of something like it, when it came by. Where the Devil did it come from, I wonder!’\n\n‘From the shadow of this wall, I think,’ said I, as we emerged upon a road on which a wall abutted.\n\n‘It’s gone!’ he returned, looking over his shoulder. ‘And all ill go with it. Now for our dinner!’\n\nBut he looked again over his shoulder towards the sea-line glimmering afar off, and yet again. And he wondered about it, in some broken expressions, several times, in the short remainder of our walk; and only seemed to forget it when the light of fire and candle shone upon us, seated warm and merry, at table.\n\nLittimer was there, and had his usual effect upon me. When I said to him that I hoped Mrs. Steerforth and Miss Dartle were well, he answered respectfully (and of course respectably), that they were tolerably well, he thanked me, and had sent their compliments. This was all, and yet he seemed to me to say as plainly as a man could say: ‘You are very young, sir; you are exceedingly young.’\n\nWe had almost finished dinner, when taking a step or two towards the table, from the corner where he kept watch upon us, or rather upon me, as I felt, he said to his master:\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir. Miss Mowcher is down here.’\n\n‘Who?’ cried Steerforth, much astonished.\n\n‘Miss Mowcher, sir.’\n\n‘Why, what on earth does she do here?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘It appears to be her native part of the country, sir. She informs me that she makes one of her professional visits here, every year, sir. I met her in the street this afternoon, and she wished to know if she might have the honour of waiting on you after dinner, sir.’\n\n‘Do you know the Giantess in question, Daisy?’ inquired Steerforth.\n\nI was obliged to confess--I felt ashamed, even of being at this disadvantage before Littimer--that Miss Mowcher and I were wholly unacquainted.\n\n‘Then you shall know her,’ said Steerforth, ‘for she is one of the seven wonders of the world. When Miss Mowcher comes, show her in.’\n\nI felt some curiosity and excitement about this lady, especially as Steerforth burst into a fit of laughing when I referred to her, and positively refused to answer any question of which I made her the subject. I remained, therefore, in a state of considerable expectation until the cloth had been removed some half an hour, and we were sitting over our decanter of wine before the fire, when the door opened, and Littimer, with his habitual serenity quite undisturbed, announced:\n\n‘Miss Mowcher!’\n\nI looked at the doorway and saw nothing. I was still looking at the doorway, thinking that Miss Mowcher was a long while making her appearance, when, to my infinite astonishment, there came waddling round a sofa which stood between me and it, a pursy dwarf, of about forty or forty-five, with a very large head and face, a pair of roguish grey eyes, and such extremely little arms, that, to enable herself to lay a finger archly against her snub nose, as she ogled Steerforth, she was obliged to meet the finger half-way, and lay her nose against it. Her chin, which was what is called a double chin, was so fat that it entirely swallowed up the strings of her bonnet, bow and all. Throat she had none; waist she had none; legs she had none, worth mentioning; for though she was more than full-sized down to where her waist would have been, if she had had any, and though she terminated, as human beings generally do, in a pair of feet, she was so short that she stood at a common-sized chair as at a table, resting a bag she carried on the seat. This lady--dressed in an off-hand, easy style; bringing her nose and her forefinger together, with the difficulty I have described; standing with her head necessarily on one side, and, with one of her sharp eyes shut up, making an uncommonly knowing face--after ogling Steerforth for a few moments, broke into a torrent of words.\n\n‘What! My flower!’ she pleasantly began, shaking her large head at him. ‘You’re there, are you! Oh, you naughty boy, fie for shame, what do you do so far away from home? Up to mischief, I’ll be bound. Oh, you’re a downy fellow, Steerforth, so you are, and I’m another, ain’t I? Ha, ha, ha! You’d have betted a hundred pound to five, now, that you wouldn’t have seen me here, wouldn’t you? Bless you, man alive, I’m everywhere. I’m here and there, and where not, like the conjurer’s half-crown in the lady’s handkercher. Talking of handkerchers--and talking of ladies--what a comfort you are to your blessed mother, ain’t you, my dear boy, over one of my shoulders, and I don’t say which!’\n\nMiss Mowcher untied her bonnet, at this passage of her discourse, threw back the strings, and sat down, panting, on a footstool in front of the fire--making a kind of arbour of the dining table, which spread its mahogany shelter above her head.\n\n‘Oh my stars and what’s-their-names!’ she went on, clapping a hand on each of her little knees, and glancing shrewdly at me, ‘I’m of too full a habit, that’s the fact, Steerforth. After a flight of stairs, it gives me as much trouble to draw every breath I want, as if it was a bucket of water. If you saw me looking out of an upper window, you’d think I was a fine woman, wouldn’t you?’\n\n‘I should think that, wherever I saw you,’ replied Steerforth.\n\n‘Go along, you dog, do!’ cried the little creature, making a whisk at him with the handkerchief with which she was wiping her face, ‘and don’t be impudent! But I give you my word and honour I was at Lady Mithers’s last week--THERE’S a woman! How SHE wears!--and Mithers himself came into the room where I was waiting for her--THERE’S a man! How HE wears! and his wig too, for he’s had it these ten years--and he went on at that rate in the complimentary line, that I began to think I should be obliged to ring the bell. Ha! ha! ha! He’s a pleasant wretch, but he wants principle.’\n\n‘What were you doing for Lady Mithers?’ asked Steerforth.\n\n‘That’s tellings, my blessed infant,’ she retorted, tapping her nose again, screwing up her face, and twinkling her eyes like an imp of supernatural intelligence. ‘Never YOU mind! You’d like to know whether I stop her hair from falling off, or dye it, or touch up her complexion, or improve her eyebrows, wouldn’t you? And so you shall, my darling--when I tell you! Do you know what my great grandfather’s name was?’\n\n‘No,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘It was Walker, my sweet pet,’ replied Miss Mowcher, ‘and he came of a long line of Walkers, that I inherit all the Hookey estates from.’\n\nI never beheld anything approaching to Miss Mowcher’s wink except Miss Mowcher’s self-possession. She had a wonderful way too, when listening to what was said to her, or when waiting for an answer to what she had said herself, of pausing with her head cunningly on one side, and one eye turned up like a magpie’s. Altogether I was lost in amazement, and sat staring at her, quite oblivious, I am afraid, of the laws of politeness.\n\nShe had by this time drawn the chair to her side, and was busily engaged in producing from the bag (plunging in her short arm to the shoulder, at every dive) a number of small bottles, sponges, combs, brushes, bits of flannel, little pairs of curling-irons, and other instruments, which she tumbled in a heap upon the chair. From this employment she suddenly desisted, and said to Steerforth, much to my confusion:\n\n‘Who’s your friend?’\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ said Steerforth; ‘he wants to know you.’\n\n‘Well, then, he shall! I thought he looked as if he did!’ returned Miss Mowcher, waddling up to me, bag in hand, and laughing on me as she came. ‘Face like a peach!’ standing on tiptoe to pinch my cheek as I sat. ‘Quite tempting! I’m very fond of peaches. Happy to make your acquaintance, Mr. Copperfield, I’m sure.’\n\nI said that I congratulated myself on having the honour to make hers, and that the happiness was mutual.\n\n‘Oh, my goodness, how polite we are!’ exclaimed Miss Mowcher, making a preposterous attempt to cover her large face with her morsel of a hand. ‘What a world of gammon and spinnage it is, though, ain’t it!’\n\nThis was addressed confidentially to both of us, as the morsel of a hand came away from the face, and buried itself, arm and all, in the bag again.\n\n‘What do you mean, Miss Mowcher?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Ha! ha! ha! What a refreshing set of humbugs we are, to be sure, ain’t we, my sweet child?’ replied that morsel of a woman, feeling in the bag with her head on one side and her eye in the air. ‘Look here!’ taking something out. ‘Scraps of the Russian Prince’s nails. Prince Alphabet turned topsy-turvy, I call him, for his name’s got all the letters in it, higgledy-piggledy.’\n\n‘The Russian Prince is a client of yours, is he?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘I believe you, my pet,’ replied Miss Mowcher. ‘I keep his nails in order for him. Twice a week! Fingers and toes.’\n\n‘He pays well, I hope?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Pays, as he speaks, my dear child--through the nose,’ replied Miss Mowcher. ‘None of your close shavers the Prince ain’t. You’d say so, if you saw his moustachios. Red by nature, black by art.’\n\n‘By your art, of course,’ said Steerforth.\n\nMiss Mowcher winked assent. ‘Forced to send for me. Couldn’t help it. The climate affected his dye; it did very well in Russia, but it was no go here. You never saw such a rusty Prince in all your born days as he was. Like old iron!’ ‘Is that why you called him a humbug, just now?’ inquired Steerforth.\n\n‘Oh, you’re a broth of a boy, ain’t you?’ returned Miss Mowcher, shaking her head violently. ‘I said, what a set of humbugs we were in general, and I showed you the scraps of the Prince’s nails to prove it. The Prince’s nails do more for me in private families of the genteel sort, than all my talents put together. I always carry ‘em about. They’re the best introduction. If Miss Mowcher cuts the Prince’s nails, she must be all right. I give ‘em away to the young ladies. They put ‘em in albums, I believe. Ha! ha! ha! Upon my life, “the whole social system” (as the men call it when they make speeches in Parliament) is a system of Prince’s nails!’ said this least of women, trying to fold her short arms, and nodding her large head.\n\nSteerforth laughed heartily, and I laughed too. Miss Mowcher continuing all the time to shake her head (which was very much on one side), and to look into the air with one eye, and to wink with the other.\n\n‘Well, well!’ she said, smiting her small knees, and rising, ‘this is not business. Come, Steerforth, let’s explore the polar regions, and have it over.’\n\nShe then selected two or three of the little instruments, and a little bottle, and asked (to my surprise) if the table would bear. On Steerforth’s replying in the affirmative, she pushed a chair against it, and begging the assistance of my hand, mounted up, pretty nimbly, to the top, as if it were a stage.\n\n‘If either of you saw my ankles,’ she said, when she was safely elevated, ‘say so, and I’ll go home and destroy myself!’\n\n‘I did not,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘I did not,’ said I.\n\n‘Well then,’ cried Miss Mowcher, ‘I’ll consent to live. Now, ducky, ducky, ducky, come to Mrs. Bond and be killed.’\n\nThis was an invitation to Steerforth to place himself under her hands; who, accordingly, sat himself down, with his back to the table, and his laughing face towards me, and submitted his head to her inspection, evidently for no other purpose than our entertainment. To see Miss Mowcher standing over him, looking at his rich profusion of brown hair through a large round magnifying glass, which she took out of her pocket, was a most amazing spectacle.\n\n‘You’re a pretty fellow!’ said Miss Mowcher, after a brief inspection. ‘You’d be as bald as a friar on the top of your head in twelve months, but for me. Just half a minute, my young friend, and we’ll give you a polishing that shall keep your curls on for the next ten years!’\n\nWith this, she tilted some of the contents of the little bottle on to one of the little bits of flannel, and, again imparting some of the virtues of that preparation to one of the little brushes, began rubbing and scraping away with both on the crown of Steerforth’s head in the busiest manner I ever witnessed, talking all the time.\n\n‘There’s Charley Pyegrave, the duke’s son,’ she said. ‘You know Charley?’ peeping round into his face.\n\n‘A little,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘What a man HE is! THERE’S a whisker! As to Charley’s legs, if they were only a pair (which they ain’t), they’d defy competition. Would you believe he tried to do without me--in the Life-Guards, too?’\n\n‘Mad!’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘It looks like it. However, mad or sane, he tried,’ returned Miss Mowcher. ‘What does he do, but, lo and behold you, he goes into a perfumer’s shop, and wants to buy a bottle of the Madagascar Liquid.’\n\n‘Charley does?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Charley does. But they haven’t got any of the Madagascar Liquid.’\n\n‘What is it? Something to drink?’ asked Steerforth.\n\n‘To drink?’ returned Miss Mowcher, stopping to slap his cheek. ‘To doctor his own moustachios with, you know. There was a woman in the shop--elderly female--quite a Griffin--who had never even heard of it by name. “Begging pardon, sir,” said the Griffin to Charley, “it’s not--not--not ROUGE, is it?” “Rouge,” said Charley to the Griffin. “What the unmentionable to ears polite, do you think I want with rouge?” “No offence, sir,” said the Griffin; “we have it asked for by so many names, I thought it might be.” Now that, my child,’ continued Miss Mowcher, rubbing all the time as busily as ever, ‘is another instance of the refreshing humbug I was speaking of. I do something in that way myself--perhaps a good deal--perhaps a little--sharp’s the word, my dear boy--never mind!’\n\n‘In what way do you mean? In the rouge way?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Put this and that together, my tender pupil,’ returned the wary Mowcher, touching her nose, ‘work it by the rule of Secrets in all trades, and the product will give you the desired result. I say I do a little in that way myself. One Dowager, SHE calls it lip-salve. Another, SHE calls it gloves. Another, SHE calls it tucker-edging. Another, SHE calls it a fan. I call it whatever THEY call it. I supply it for ‘em, but we keep up the trick so, to one another, and make believe with such a face, that they’d as soon think of laying it on, before a whole drawing-room, as before me. And when I wait upon ‘em, they’ll say to me sometimes--WITH IT ON--thick, and no mistake--“How am I looking, Mowcher? Am I pale?” Ha! ha! ha! ha! Isn’t THAT refreshing, my young friend!’\n\nI never did in my days behold anything like Mowcher as she stood upon the dining table, intensely enjoying this refreshment, rubbing busily at Steerforth’s head, and winking at me over it.\n\n‘Ah!’ she said. ‘Such things are not much in demand hereabouts. That sets me off again! I haven’t seen a pretty woman since I’ve been here, jemmy.’\n\n‘No?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Not the ghost of one,’ replied Miss Mowcher.\n\n‘We could show her the substance of one, I think?’ said Steerforth, addressing his eyes to mine. ‘Eh, Daisy?’\n\n‘Yes, indeed,’ said I.\n\n‘Aha?’ cried the little creature, glancing sharply at my face, and then peeping round at Steerforth’s. ‘Umph?’\n\nThe first exclamation sounded like a question put to both of us, and the second like a question put to Steerforth only. She seemed to have found no answer to either, but continued to rub, with her head on one side and her eye turned up, as if she were looking for an answer in the air and were confident of its appearing presently.\n\n‘A sister of yours, Mr. Copperfield?’ she cried, after a pause, and still keeping the same look-out. ‘Aye, aye?’\n\n‘No,’ said Steerforth, before I could reply. ‘Nothing of the sort. On the contrary, Mr. Copperfield used--or I am much mistaken--to have a great admiration for her.’\n\n‘Why, hasn’t he now?’ returned Miss Mowcher. ‘Is he fickle? Oh, for shame! Did he sip every flower, and change every hour, until Polly his passion requited?--Is her name Polly?’\n\nThe Elfin suddenness with which she pounced upon me with this question, and a searching look, quite disconcerted me for a moment.\n\n‘No, Miss Mowcher,’ I replied. ‘Her name is Emily.’\n\n‘Aha?’ she cried exactly as before. ‘Umph? What a rattle I am! Mr. Copperfield, ain’t I volatile?’\n\nHer tone and look implied something that was not agreeable to me in connexion with the subject. So I said, in a graver manner than any of us had yet assumed: ‘She is as virtuous as she is pretty. She is engaged to be married to a most worthy and deserving man in her own station of life. I esteem her for her good sense, as much as I admire her for her good looks.’\n\n‘Well said!’ cried Steerforth. ‘Hear, hear, hear! Now I’ll quench the curiosity of this little Fatima, my dear Daisy, by leaving her nothing to guess at. She is at present apprenticed, Miss Mowcher, or articled, or whatever it may be, to Omer and Joram, Haberdashers, Milliners, and so forth, in this town. Do you observe? Omer and Joram. The promise of which my friend has spoken, is made and entered into with her cousin; Christian name, Ham; surname, Peggotty; occupation, boat-builder; also of this town. She lives with a relative; Christian name, unknown; surname, Peggotty; occupation, seafaring; also of this town. She is the prettiest and most engaging little fairy in the world. I admire her--as my friend does--exceedingly. If it were not that I might appear to disparage her Intended, which I know my friend would not like, I would add, that to me she seems to be throwing herself away; that I am sure she might do better; and that I swear she was born to be a lady.’\n\nMiss Mowcher listened to these words, which were very slowly and distinctly spoken, with her head on one side, and her eye in the air as if she were still looking for that answer. When he ceased she became brisk again in an instant, and rattled away with surprising volubility.\n\n‘Oh! And that’s all about it, is it?’ she exclaimed, trimming his whiskers with a little restless pair of scissors, that went glancing round his head in all directions. ‘Very well: very well! Quite a long story. Ought to end “and they lived happy ever afterwards”; oughtn’t it? Ah! What’s that game at forfeits? I love my love with an E, because she’s enticing; I hate her with an E, because she’s engaged. I took her to the sign of the exquisite, and treated her with an elopement, her name’s Emily, and she lives in the east? Ha! ha! ha! Mr. Copperfield, ain’t I volatile?’\n\nMerely looking at me with extravagant slyness, and not waiting for any reply, she continued, without drawing breath:\n\n‘There! If ever any scapegrace was trimmed and touched up to perfection, you are, Steerforth. If I understand any noddle in the world, I understand yours. Do you hear me when I tell you that, my darling? I understand yours,’ peeping down into his face. ‘Now you may mizzle, jemmy (as we say at Court), and if Mr. Copperfield will take the chair I’ll operate on him.’\n\n‘What do you say, Daisy?’ inquired Steerforth, laughing, and resigning his seat. ‘Will you be improved?’\n\n‘Thank you, Miss Mowcher, not this evening.’\n\n‘Don’t say no,’ returned the little woman, looking at me with the aspect of a connoisseur; ‘a little bit more eyebrow?’\n\n‘Thank you,’ I returned, ‘some other time.’\n\n‘Have it carried half a quarter of an inch towards the temple,’ said Miss Mowcher. ‘We can do it in a fortnight.’\n\n‘No, I thank you. Not at present.’\n\n‘Go in for a tip,’ she urged. ‘No? Let’s get the scaffolding up, then, for a pair of whiskers. Come!’\n\nI could not help blushing as I declined, for I felt we were on my weak point, now. But Miss Mowcher, finding that I was not at present disposed for any decoration within the range of her art, and that I was, for the time being, proof against the blandishments of the small bottle which she held up before one eye to enforce her persuasions, said we would make a beginning on an early day, and requested the aid of my hand to descend from her elevated station. Thus assisted, she skipped down with much agility, and began to tie her double chin into her bonnet.\n\n‘The fee,’ said Steerforth, ‘is--’\n\n‘Five bob,’ replied Miss Mowcher, ‘and dirt cheap, my chicken. Ain’t I volatile, Mr. Copperfield?’\n\nI replied politely: ‘Not at all.’ But I thought she was rather so, when she tossed up his two half-crowns like a goblin pieman, caught them, dropped them in her pocket, and gave it a loud slap.\n\n‘That’s the Till!’ observed Miss Mowcher, standing at the chair again, and replacing in the bag a miscellaneous collection of little objects she had emptied out of it. ‘Have I got all my traps? It seems so. It won’t do to be like long Ned Beadwood, when they took him to church “to marry him to somebody”, as he says, and left the bride behind. Ha! ha! ha! A wicked rascal, Ned, but droll! Now, I know I’m going to break your hearts, but I am forced to leave you. You must call up all your fortitude, and try to bear it. Good-bye, Mr. Copperfield! Take care of yourself, jockey of Norfolk! How I have been rattling on! It’s all the fault of you two wretches. I forgive you! “Bob swore!”--as the Englishman said for “Good night”, when he first learnt French, and thought it so like English. “Bob swore,” my ducks!’\n\nWith the bag slung over her arm, and rattling as she waddled away, she waddled to the door, where she stopped to inquire if she should leave us a lock of her hair. ‘Ain’t I volatile?’ she added, as a commentary on this offer, and, with her finger on her nose, departed.\n\nSteerforth laughed to that degree, that it was impossible for me to help laughing too; though I am not sure I should have done so, but for this inducement. When we had had our laugh quite out, which was after some time, he told me that Miss Mowcher had quite an extensive connexion, and made herself useful to a variety of people in a variety of ways. Some people trifled with her as a mere oddity, he said; but she was as shrewdly and sharply observant as anyone he knew, and as long-headed as she was short-armed. He told me that what she had said of being here, and there, and everywhere, was true enough; for she made little darts into the provinces, and seemed to pick up customers everywhere, and to know everybody. I asked him what her disposition was: whether it was at all mischievous, and if her sympathies were generally on the right side of things: but, not succeeding in attracting his attention to these questions after two or three attempts, I forbore or forgot to repeat them. He told me instead, with much rapidity, a good deal about her skill, and her profits; and about her being a scientific cupper, if I should ever have occasion for her service in that capacity.\n\nShe was the principal theme of our conversation during the evening: and when we parted for the night Steerforth called after me over the banisters, ‘Bob swore!’ as I went downstairs.\n\nI was surprised, when I came to Mr. Barkis’s house, to find Ham walking up and down in front of it, and still more surprised to learn from him that little Em’ly was inside. I naturally inquired why he was not there too, instead of pacing the streets by himself?\n\n‘Why, you see, Mas’r Davy,’ he rejoined, in a hesitating manner, ‘Em’ly, she’s talking to some ‘un in here.’\n\n‘I should have thought,’ said I, smiling, ‘that that was a reason for your being in here too, Ham.’\n\n‘Well, Mas’r Davy, in a general way, so ‘t would be,’ he returned; ‘but look’ee here, Mas’r Davy,’ lowering his voice, and speaking very gravely. ‘It’s a young woman, sir--a young woman, that Em’ly knowed once, and doen’t ought to know no more.’\n\nWhen I heard these words, a light began to fall upon the figure I had seen following them, some hours ago.\n\n‘It’s a poor wurem, Mas’r Davy,’ said Ham, ‘as is trod under foot by all the town. Up street and down street. The mowld o’ the churchyard don’t hold any that the folk shrink away from, more.’\n\n‘Did I see her tonight, Ham, on the sand, after we met you?’\n\n‘Keeping us in sight?’ said Ham. ‘It’s like you did, Mas’r Davy. Not that I know’d then, she was theer, sir, but along of her creeping soon arterwards under Em’ly’s little winder, when she see the light come, and whispering “Em’ly, Em’ly, for Christ’s sake, have a woman’s heart towards me. I was once like you!” Those was solemn words, Mas’r Davy, fur to hear!’\n\n‘They were indeed, Ham. What did Em’ly do?’ ‘Says Em’ly, “Martha, is it you? Oh, Martha, can it be you?”--for they had sat at work together, many a day, at Mr. Omer’s.’\n\n‘I recollect her now!’ cried I, recalling one of the two girls I had seen when I first went there. ‘I recollect her quite well!’\n\n‘Martha Endell,’ said Ham. ‘Two or three year older than Em’ly, but was at the school with her.’\n\n‘I never heard her name,’ said I. ‘I didn’t mean to interrupt you.’\n\n‘For the matter o’ that, Mas’r Davy,’ replied Ham, ‘all’s told a’most in them words, “Em’ly, Em’ly, for Christ’s sake, have a woman’s heart towards me. I was once like you!” She wanted to speak to Em’ly. Em’ly couldn’t speak to her theer, for her loving uncle was come home, and he wouldn’t--no, Mas’r Davy,’ said Ham, with great earnestness, ‘he couldn’t, kind-natur’d, tender-hearted as he is, see them two together, side by side, for all the treasures that’s wrecked in the sea.’\n\nI felt how true this was. I knew it, on the instant, quite as well as Ham.\n\n‘So Em’ly writes in pencil on a bit of paper,’ he pursued, ‘and gives it to her out o’ winder to bring here. “Show that,” she says, “to my aunt, Mrs. Barkis, and she’ll set you down by her fire, for the love of me, till uncle is gone out, and I can come.” By and by she tells me what I tell you, Mas’r Davy, and asks me to bring her. What can I do? She doen’t ought to know any such, but I can’t deny her, when the tears is on her face.’\n\nHe put his hand into the breast of his shaggy jacket, and took out with great care a pretty little purse.\n\n‘And if I could deny her when the tears was on her face, Mas’r Davy,’ said Ham, tenderly adjusting it on the rough palm of his hand, ‘how could I deny her when she give me this to carry for her--knowing what she brought it for? Such a toy as it is!’ said Ham, thoughtfully looking on it. ‘With such a little money in it, Em’ly my dear.’\n\nI shook him warmly by the hand when he had put it away again--for that was more satisfactory to me than saying anything--and we walked up and down, for a minute or two, in silence. The door opened then, and Peggotty appeared, beckoning to Ham to come in. I would have kept away, but she came after me, entreating me to come in too. Even then, I would have avoided the room where they all were, but for its being the neat-tiled kitchen I have mentioned more than once. The door opening immediately into it, I found myself among them before I considered whither I was going.\n\nThe girl--the same I had seen upon the sands--was near the fire. She was sitting on the ground, with her head and one arm lying on a chair. I fancied, from the disposition of her figure, that Em’ly had but newly risen from the chair, and that the forlorn head might perhaps have been lying on her lap. I saw but little of the girl’s face, over which her hair fell loose and scattered, as if she had been disordering it with her own hands; but I saw that she was young, and of a fair complexion. Peggotty had been crying. So had little Em’ly. Not a word was spoken when we first went in; and the Dutch clock by the dresser seemed, in the silence, to tick twice as loud as usual. Em’ly spoke first.\n\n‘Martha wants,’ she said to Ham, ‘to go to London.’\n\n‘Why to London?’ returned Ham.\n\nHe stood between them, looking on the prostrate girl with a mixture of compassion for her, and of jealousy of her holding any companionship with her whom he loved so well, which I have always remembered distinctly. They both spoke as if she were ill; in a soft, suppressed tone that was plainly heard, although it hardly rose above a whisper.\n\n‘Better there than here,’ said a third voice aloud--Martha’s, though she did not move. ‘No one knows me there. Everybody knows me here.’\n\n‘What will she do there?’ inquired Ham.\n\nShe lifted up her head, and looked darkly round at him for a moment; then laid it down again, and curved her right arm about her neck, as a woman in a fever, or in an agony of pain from a shot, might twist herself.\n\n‘She will try to do well,’ said little Em’ly. ‘You don’t know what she has said to us. Does he--do they--aunt?’\n\nPeggotty shook her head compassionately.\n\n‘I’ll try,’ said Martha, ‘if you’ll help me away. I never can do worse than I have done here. I may do better. Oh!’ with a dreadful shiver, ‘take me out of these streets, where the whole town knows me from a child!’\n\nAs Em’ly held out her hand to Ham, I saw him put in it a little canvas bag. She took it, as if she thought it were her purse, and made a step or two forward; but finding her mistake, came back to where he had retired near me, and showed it to him.\n\n‘It’s all yourn, Em’ly,’ I could hear him say. ‘I haven’t nowt in all the wureld that ain’t yourn, my dear. It ain’t of no delight to me, except for you!’\n\nThe tears rose freshly in her eyes, but she turned away and went to Martha. What she gave her, I don’t know. I saw her stooping over her, and putting money in her bosom. She whispered something, as she asked was that enough? ‘More than enough,’ the other said, and took her hand and kissed it.\n\nThen Martha arose, and gathering her shawl about her, covering her face with it, and weeping aloud, went slowly to the door. She stopped a moment before going out, as if she would have uttered something or turned back; but no word passed her lips. Making the same low, dreary, wretched moaning in her shawl, she went away.\n\nAs the door closed, little Em’ly looked at us three in a hurried manner and then hid her face in her hands, and fell to sobbing.\n\n‘Doen’t, Em’ly!’ said Ham, tapping her gently on the shoulder. ‘Doen’t, my dear! You doen’t ought to cry so, pretty!’\n\n‘Oh, Ham!’ she exclaimed, still weeping pitifully, ‘I am not so good a girl as I ought to be! I know I have not the thankful heart, sometimes, I ought to have!’\n\n‘Yes, yes, you have, I’m sure,’ said Ham.\n\n‘No! no! no!’ cried little Em’ly, sobbing, and shaking her head. ‘I am not as good a girl as I ought to be. Not near! not near!’ And still she cried, as if her heart would break.\n\n‘I try your love too much. I know I do!’ she sobbed. ‘I’m often cross to you, and changeable with you, when I ought to be far different. You are never so to me. Why am I ever so to you, when I should think of nothing but how to be grateful, and to make you happy!’\n\n‘You always make me so,’ said Ham, ‘my dear! I am happy in the sight of you. I am happy, all day long, in the thoughts of you.’\n\n‘Ah! that’s not enough!’ she cried. ‘That is because you are good; not because I am! Oh, my dear, it might have been a better fortune for you, if you had been fond of someone else--of someone steadier and much worthier than me, who was all bound up in you, and never vain and changeable like me!’\n\n‘Poor little tender-heart,’ said Ham, in a low voice. ‘Martha has overset her, altogether.’\n\n‘Please, aunt,’ sobbed Em’ly, ‘come here, and let me lay my head upon you. Oh, I am very miserable tonight, aunt! Oh, I am not as good a girl as I ought to be. I am not, I know!’\n\nPeggotty had hastened to the chair before the fire. Em’ly, with her arms around her neck, kneeled by her, looking up most earnestly into her face.\n\n‘Oh, pray, aunt, try to help me! Ham, dear, try to help me! Mr. David, for the sake of old times, do, please, try to help me! I want to be a better girl than I am. I want to feel a hundred times more thankful than I do. I want to feel more, what a blessed thing it is to be the wife of a good man, and to lead a peaceful life. Oh me, oh me! Oh my heart, my heart!’\n\nShe dropped her face on my old nurse’s breast, and, ceasing this supplication, which in its agony and grief was half a woman’s, half a child’s, as all her manner was (being, in that, more natural, and better suited to her beauty, as I thought, than any other manner could have been), wept silently, while my old nurse hushed her like an infant.\n\nShe got calmer by degrees, and then we soothed her; now talking encouragingly, and now jesting a little with her, until she began to raise her head and speak to us. So we got on, until she was able to smile, and then to laugh, and then to sit up, half ashamed; while Peggotty recalled her stray ringlets, dried her eyes, and made her neat again, lest her uncle should wonder, when she got home, why his darling had been crying.\n\nI saw her do, that night, what I had never seen her do before. I saw her innocently kiss her chosen husband on the cheek, and creep close to his bluff form as if it were her best support. When they went away together, in the waning moonlight, and I looked after them, comparing their departure in my mind with Martha’s, I saw that she held his arm with both her hands, and still kept close to him.\n\n# CHAPTER 23. I CORROBORATE Mr. DICK, AND CHOOSE A PROFESSION\n\nWhen I awoke in the morning I thought very much of little Em’ly, and her emotion last night, after Martha had left. I felt as if I had come into the knowledge of those domestic weaknesses and tendernesses in a sacred confidence, and that to disclose them, even to Steerforth, would be wrong. I had no gentler feeling towards anyone than towards the pretty creature who had been my playmate, and whom I have always been persuaded, and shall always be persuaded, to my dying day, I then devotedly loved. The repetition to any ears--even to Steerforth’s--of what she had been unable to repress when her heart lay open to me by an accident, I felt would be a rough deed, unworthy of myself, unworthy of the light of our pure childhood, which I always saw encircling her head. I made a resolution, therefore, to keep it in my own breast; and there it gave her image a new grace.\n\nWhile we were at breakfast, a letter was delivered to me from my aunt. As it contained matter on which I thought Steerforth could advise me as well as anyone, and on which I knew I should be delighted to consult him, I resolved to make it a subject of discussion on our journey home. For the present we had enough to do, in taking leave of all our friends. Mr. Barkis was far from being the last among them, in his regret at our departure; and I believe would even have opened the box again, and sacrificed another guinea, if it would have kept us eight-and-forty hours in Yarmouth. Peggotty and all her family were full of grief at our going. The whole house of Omer and Joram turned out to bid us good-bye; and there were so many seafaring volunteers in attendance on Steerforth, when our portmanteaux went to the coach, that if we had had the baggage of a regiment with us, we should hardly have wanted porters to carry it. In a word, we departed to the regret and admiration of all concerned, and left a great many people very sorry behind US.\n\n‘Do you stay long here, Littimer?’ said I, as he stood waiting to see the coach start.\n\n‘No, sir,’ he replied; ‘probably not very long, sir.’\n\n‘He can hardly say, just now,’ observed Steerforth, carelessly. ‘He knows what he has to do, and he’ll do it.’\n\n‘That I am sure he will,’ said I.\n\nLittimer touched his hat in acknowledgement of my good opinion, and I felt about eight years old. He touched it once more, wishing us a good journey; and we left him standing on the pavement, as respectable a mystery as any pyramid in Egypt.\n\nFor some little time we held no conversation, Steerforth being unusually silent, and I being sufficiently engaged in wondering, within myself, when I should see the old places again, and what new changes might happen to me or them in the meanwhile. At length Steerforth, becoming gay and talkative in a moment, as he could become anything he liked at any moment, pulled me by the arm:\n\n‘Find a voice, David. What about that letter you were speaking of at breakfast?’\n\n‘Oh!’ said I, taking it out of my pocket. ‘It’s from my aunt.’\n\n‘And what does she say, requiring consideration?’\n\n‘Why, she reminds me, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘that I came out on this expedition to look about me, and to think a little.’\n\n‘Which, of course, you have done?’\n\n‘Indeed I can’t say I have, particularly. To tell you the truth, I am afraid I have forgotten it.’\n\n‘Well! look about you now, and make up for your negligence,’ said Steerforth. ‘Look to the right, and you’ll see a flat country, with a good deal of marsh in it; look to the left, and you’ll see the same. Look to the front, and you’ll find no difference; look to the rear, and there it is still.’ I laughed, and replied that I saw no suitable profession in the whole prospect; which was perhaps to be attributed to its flatness.\n\n‘What says our aunt on the subject?’ inquired Steerforth, glancing at the letter in my hand. ‘Does she suggest anything?’\n\n‘Why, yes,’ said I. ‘She asks me, here, if I think I should like to be a proctor? What do you think of it?’\n\n‘Well, I don’t know,’ replied Steerforth, coolly. ‘You may as well do that as anything else, I suppose?’\n\nI could not help laughing again, at his balancing all callings and professions so equally; and I told him so.\n\n‘What is a proctor, Steerforth?’ said I.\n\n‘Why, he is a sort of monkish attorney,’ replied Steerforth. ‘He is, to some faded courts held in Doctors’ Commons,--a lazy old nook near St. Paul’s Churchyard--what solicitors are to the courts of law and equity. He is a functionary whose existence, in the natural course of things, would have terminated about two hundred years ago. I can tell you best what he is, by telling you what Doctors’ Commons is. It’s a little out-of-the-way place, where they administer what is called ecclesiastical law, and play all kinds of tricks with obsolete old monsters of acts of Parliament, which three-fourths of the world know nothing about, and the other fourth supposes to have been dug up, in a fossil state, in the days of the Edwards. It’s a place that has an ancient monopoly in suits about people’s wills and people’s marriages, and disputes among ships and boats.’\n\n‘Nonsense, Steerforth!’ I exclaimed. ‘You don’t mean to say that there is any affinity between nautical matters and ecclesiastical matters?’\n\n‘I don’t, indeed, my dear boy,’ he returned; ‘but I mean to say that they are managed and decided by the same set of people, down in that same Doctors’ Commons. You shall go there one day, and find them blundering through half the nautical terms in Young’s Dictionary, apropos of the “Nancy” having run down the “Sarah Jane”, or Mr. Peggotty and the Yarmouth boatmen having put off in a gale of wind with an anchor and cable to the “Nelson” Indiaman in distress; and you shall go there another day, and find them deep in the evidence, pro and con, respecting a clergyman who has misbehaved himself; and you shall find the judge in the nautical case, the advocate in the clergyman’s case, or contrariwise. They are like actors: now a man’s a judge, and now he is not a judge; now he’s one thing, now he’s another; now he’s something else, change and change about; but it’s always a very pleasant, profitable little affair of private theatricals, presented to an uncommonly select audience.’\n\n‘But advocates and proctors are not one and the same?’ said I, a little puzzled. ‘Are they?’\n\n‘No,’ returned Steerforth, ‘the advocates are civilians--men who have taken a doctor’s degree at college--which is the first reason of my knowing anything about it. The proctors employ the advocates. Both get very comfortable fees, and altogether they make a mighty snug little party. On the whole, I would recommend you to take to Doctors’ Commons kindly, David. They plume themselves on their gentility there, I can tell you, if that’s any satisfaction.’\n\nI made allowance for Steerforth’s light way of treating the subject, and, considering it with reference to the staid air of gravity and antiquity which I associated with that ‘lazy old nook near St. Paul’s Churchyard’, did not feel indisposed towards my aunt’s suggestion; which she left to my free decision, making no scruple of telling me that it had occurred to her, on her lately visiting her own proctor in Doctors’ Commons for the purpose of settling her will in my favour.\n\n‘That’s a laudable proceeding on the part of our aunt, at all events,’ said Steerforth, when I mentioned it; ‘and one deserving of all encouragement. Daisy, my advice is that you take kindly to Doctors’ Commons.’\n\nI quite made up my mind to do so. I then told Steerforth that my aunt was in town awaiting me (as I found from her letter), and that she had taken lodgings for a week at a kind of private hotel at Lincoln’s Inn Fields, where there was a stone staircase, and a convenient door in the roof; my aunt being firmly persuaded that every house in London was going to be burnt down every night.\n\nWe achieved the rest of our journey pleasantly, sometimes recurring to Doctors’ Commons, and anticipating the distant days when I should be a proctor there, which Steerforth pictured in a variety of humorous and whimsical lights, that made us both merry. When we came to our journey’s end, he went home, engaging to call upon me next day but one; and I drove to Lincoln’s Inn Fields, where I found my aunt up, and waiting supper.\n\nIf I had been round the world since we parted, we could hardly have been better pleased to meet again. My aunt cried outright as she embraced me; and said, pretending to laugh, that if my poor mother had been alive, that silly little creature would have shed tears, she had no doubt.\n\n‘So you have left Mr. Dick behind, aunt?’ said I. ‘I am sorry for that. Ah, Janet, how do you do?’\n\nAs Janet curtsied, hoping I was well, I observed my aunt’s visage lengthen very much.\n\n‘I am sorry for it, too,’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose. ‘I have had no peace of mind, Trot, since I have been here.’ Before I could ask why, she told me.\n\n‘I am convinced,’ said my aunt, laying her hand with melancholy firmness on the table, ‘that Dick’s character is not a character to keep the donkeys off. I am confident he wants strength of purpose. I ought to have left Janet at home, instead, and then my mind might perhaps have been at ease. If ever there was a donkey trespassing on my green,’ said my aunt, with emphasis, ‘there was one this afternoon at four o’clock. A cold feeling came over me from head to foot, and I know it was a donkey!’\n\nI tried to comfort her on this point, but she rejected consolation.\n\n‘It was a donkey,’ said my aunt; ‘and it was the one with the stumpy tail which that Murdering sister of a woman rode, when she came to my house.’ This had been, ever since, the only name my aunt knew for Miss Murdstone. ‘If there is any Donkey in Dover, whose audacity it is harder to me to bear than another’s, that,’ said my aunt, striking the table, ‘is the animal!’\n\nJanet ventured to suggest that my aunt might be disturbing herself unnecessarily, and that she believed the donkey in question was then engaged in the sand-and-gravel line of business, and was not available for purposes of trespass. But my aunt wouldn’t hear of it.\n\nSupper was comfortably served and hot, though my aunt’s rooms were very high up--whether that she might have more stone stairs for her money, or might be nearer to the door in the roof, I don’t know--and consisted of a roast fowl, a steak, and some vegetables, to all of which I did ample justice, and which were all excellent. But my aunt had her own ideas concerning London provision, and ate but little.\n\n‘I suppose this unfortunate fowl was born and brought up in a cellar,’ said my aunt, ‘and never took the air except on a hackney coach-stand. I hope the steak may be beef, but I don’t believe it. Nothing’s genuine in the place, in my opinion, but the dirt.’\n\n‘Don’t you think the fowl may have come out of the country, aunt?’ I hinted.\n\n‘Certainly not,’ returned my aunt. ‘It would be no pleasure to a London tradesman to sell anything which was what he pretended it was.’\n\nI did not venture to controvert this opinion, but I made a good supper, which it greatly satisfied her to see me do. When the table was cleared, Janet assisted her to arrange her hair, to put on her nightcap, which was of a smarter construction than usual (‘in case of fire’, my aunt said), and to fold her gown back over her knees, these being her usual preparations for warming herself before going to bed. I then made her, according to certain established regulations from which no deviation, however slight, could ever be permitted, a glass of hot wine and water, and a slice of toast cut into long thin strips. With these accompaniments we were left alone to finish the evening, my aunt sitting opposite to me drinking her wine and water; soaking her strips of toast in it, one by one, before eating them; and looking benignantly on me, from among the borders of her nightcap.\n\n‘Well, Trot,’ she began, ‘what do you think of the proctor plan? Or have you not begun to think about it yet?’\n\n‘I have thought a good deal about it, my dear aunt, and I have talked a good deal about it with Steerforth. I like it very much indeed. I like it exceedingly.’\n\n‘Come!’ said my aunt. ‘That’s cheering!’\n\n‘I have only one difficulty, aunt.’\n\n‘Say what it is, Trot,’ she returned.\n\n‘Why, I want to ask, aunt, as this seems, from what I understand, to be a limited profession, whether my entrance into it would not be very expensive?’\n\n‘It will cost,’ returned my aunt, ‘to article you, just a thousand pounds.’\n\n‘Now, my dear aunt,’ said I, drawing my chair nearer, ‘I am uneasy in my mind about that. It’s a large sum of money. You have expended a great deal on my education, and have always been as liberal to me in all things as it was possible to be. You have been the soul of generosity. Surely there are some ways in which I might begin life with hardly any outlay, and yet begin with a good hope of getting on by resolution and exertion. Are you sure that it would not be better to try that course? Are you certain that you can afford to part with so much money, and that it is right that it should be so expended? I only ask you, my second mother, to consider. Are you certain?’\n\nMy aunt finished eating the piece of toast on which she was then engaged, looking me full in the face all the while; and then setting her glass on the chimney-piece, and folding her hands upon her folded skirts, replied as follows:\n\n‘Trot, my child, if I have any object in life, it is to provide for your being a good, a sensible, and a happy man. I am bent upon it--so is Dick. I should like some people that I know to hear Dick’s conversation on the subject. Its sagacity is wonderful. But no one knows the resources of that man’s intellect, except myself!’\n\nShe stopped for a moment to take my hand between hers, and went on:\n\n‘It’s in vain, Trot, to recall the past, unless it works some influence upon the present. Perhaps I might have been better friends with your poor father. Perhaps I might have been better friends with that poor child your mother, even after your sister Betsey Trotwood disappointed me. When you came to me, a little runaway boy, all dusty and way-worn, perhaps I thought so. From that time until now, Trot, you have ever been a credit to me and a pride and a pleasure. I have no other claim upon my means; at least’--here to my surprise she hesitated, and was confused--‘no, I have no other claim upon my means--and you are my adopted child. Only be a loving child to me in my age, and bear with my whims and fancies; and you will do more for an old woman whose prime of life was not so happy or conciliating as it might have been, than ever that old woman did for you.’\n\nIt was the first time I had heard my aunt refer to her past history. There was a magnanimity in her quiet way of doing so, and of dismissing it, which would have exalted her in my respect and affection, if anything could.\n\n‘All is agreed and understood between us, now, Trot,’ said my aunt, ‘and we need talk of this no more. Give me a kiss, and we’ll go to the Commons after breakfast tomorrow.’\n\nWe had a long chat by the fire before we went to bed. I slept in a room on the same floor with my aunt’s, and was a little disturbed in the course of the night by her knocking at my door as often as she was agitated by a distant sound of hackney-coaches or market-carts, and inquiring, ‘if I heard the engines?’ But towards morning she slept better, and suffered me to do so too.\n\nAt about mid-day, we set out for the office of Messrs Spenlow and Jorkins, in Doctors’ Commons. My aunt, who had this other general opinion in reference to London, that every man she saw was a pickpocket, gave me her purse to carry for her, which had ten guineas in it and some silver.\n\nWe made a pause at the toy shop in Fleet Street, to see the giants of Saint Dunstan’s strike upon the bells--we had timed our going, so as to catch them at it, at twelve o’clock--and then went on towards Ludgate Hill, and St. Paul’s Churchyard. We were crossing to the former place, when I found that my aunt greatly accelerated her speed, and looked frightened. I observed, at the same time, that a lowering ill-dressed man who had stopped and stared at us in passing, a little before, was coming so close after us as to brush against her.\n\n‘Trot! My dear Trot!’ cried my aunt, in a terrified whisper, and pressing my arm. ‘I don’t know what I am to do.’\n\n‘Don’t be alarmed,’ said I. ‘There’s nothing to be afraid of. Step into a shop, and I’ll soon get rid of this fellow.’\n\n‘No, no, child!’ she returned. ‘Don’t speak to him for the world. I entreat, I order you!’\n\n‘Good Heaven, aunt!’ said I. ‘He is nothing but a sturdy beggar.’\n\n‘You don’t know what he is!’ replied my aunt. ‘You don’t know who he is! You don’t know what you say!’\n\nWe had stopped in an empty door-way, while this was passing, and he had stopped too.\n\n‘Don’t look at him!’ said my aunt, as I turned my head indignantly, ‘but get me a coach, my dear, and wait for me in St. Paul’s Churchyard.’\n\n‘Wait for you?’ I replied.\n\n‘Yes,’ rejoined my aunt. ‘I must go alone. I must go with him.’\n\n‘With him, aunt? This man?’\n\n‘I am in my senses,’ she replied, ‘and I tell you I must. Get me a coach!’\n\nHowever much astonished I might be, I was sensible that I had no right to refuse compliance with such a peremptory command. I hurried away a few paces, and called a hackney-chariot which was passing empty. Almost before I could let down the steps, my aunt sprang in, I don’t know how, and the man followed. She waved her hand to me to go away, so earnestly, that, all confounded as I was, I turned from them at once. In doing so, I heard her say to the coachman, ‘Drive anywhere! Drive straight on!’ and presently the chariot passed me, going up the hill.\n\nWhat Mr. Dick had told me, and what I had supposed to be a delusion of his, now came into my mind. I could not doubt that this person was the person of whom he had made such mysterious mention, though what the nature of his hold upon my aunt could possibly be, I was quite unable to imagine. After half an hour’s cooling in the churchyard, I saw the chariot coming back. The driver stopped beside me, and my aunt was sitting in it alone.\n\nShe had not yet sufficiently recovered from her agitation to be quite prepared for the visit we had to make. She desired me to get into the chariot, and to tell the coachman to drive slowly up and down a little while. She said no more, except, ‘My dear child, never ask me what it was, and don’t refer to it,’ until she had perfectly regained her composure, when she told me she was quite herself now, and we might get out. On her giving me her purse to pay the driver, I found that all the guineas were gone, and only the loose silver remained.\n\nDoctors’ Commons was approached by a little low archway. Before we had taken many paces down the street beyond it, the noise of the city seemed to melt, as if by magic, into a softened distance. A few dull courts and narrow ways brought us to the sky-lighted offices of Spenlow and Jorkins; in the vestibule of which temple, accessible to pilgrims without the ceremony of knocking, three or four clerks were at work as copyists. One of these, a little dry man, sitting by himself, who wore a stiff brown wig that looked as if it were made of gingerbread, rose to receive my aunt, and show us into Mr. Spenlow’s room.\n\n‘Mr. Spenlow’s in Court, ma’am,’ said the dry man; ‘it’s an Arches day; but it’s close by, and I’ll send for him directly.’\n\nAs we were left to look about us while Mr. Spenlow was fetched, I availed myself of the opportunity. The furniture of the room was old-fashioned and dusty; and the green baize on the top of the writing-table had lost all its colour, and was as withered and pale as an old pauper. There were a great many bundles of papers on it, some endorsed as Allegations, and some (to my surprise) as Libels, and some as being in the Consistory Court, and some in the Arches Court, and some in the Prerogative Court, and some in the Admiralty Court, and some in the Delegates’ Court; giving me occasion to wonder much, how many Courts there might be in the gross, and how long it would take to understand them all. Besides these, there were sundry immense manuscript Books of Evidence taken on affidavit, strongly bound, and tied together in massive sets, a set to each cause, as if every cause were a history in ten or twenty volumes. All this looked tolerably expensive, I thought, and gave me an agreeable notion of a proctor’s business. I was casting my eyes with increasing complacency over these and many similar objects, when hasty footsteps were heard in the room outside, and Mr. Spenlow, in a black gown trimmed with white fur, came hurrying in, taking off his hat as he came.\n\nHe was a little light-haired gentleman, with undeniable boots, and the stiffest of white cravats and shirt-collars. He was buttoned up, mighty trim and tight, and must have taken a great deal of pains with his whiskers, which were accurately curled. His gold watch-chain was so massive, that a fancy came across me, that he ought to have a sinewy golden arm, to draw it out with, like those which are put up over the goldbeaters’ shops. He was got up with such care, and was so stiff, that he could hardly bend himself; being obliged, when he glanced at some papers on his desk, after sitting down in his chair, to move his whole body, from the bottom of his spine, like Punch.\n\nI had previously been presented by my aunt, and had been courteously received. He now said:\n\n‘And so, Mr. Copperfield, you think of entering into our profession? I casually mentioned to Miss Trotwood, when I had the pleasure of an interview with her the other day,’--with another inclination of his body--Punch again--‘that there was a vacancy here. Miss Trotwood was good enough to mention that she had a nephew who was her peculiar care, and for whom she was seeking to provide genteelly in life. That nephew, I believe, I have now the pleasure of’--Punch again. I bowed my acknowledgements, and said, my aunt had mentioned to me that there was that opening, and that I believed I should like it very much. That I was strongly inclined to like it, and had taken immediately to the proposal. That I could not absolutely pledge myself to like it, until I knew something more about it. That although it was little else than a matter of form, I presumed I should have an opportunity of trying how I liked it, before I bound myself to it irrevocably.\n\n‘Oh surely! surely!’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘We always, in this house, propose a month--an initiatory month. I should be happy, myself, to propose two months--three--an indefinite period, in fact--but I have a partner. Mr. Jorkins.’\n\n‘And the premium, sir,’ I returned, ‘is a thousand pounds?’\n\n‘And the premium, Stamp included, is a thousand pounds,’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘As I have mentioned to Miss Trotwood, I am actuated by no mercenary considerations; few men are less so, I believe; but Mr. Jorkins has his opinions on these subjects, and I am bound to respect Mr. Jorkins’s opinions. Mr. Jorkins thinks a thousand pounds too little, in short.’\n\n‘I suppose, sir,’ said I, still desiring to spare my aunt, ‘that it is not the custom here, if an articled clerk were particularly useful, and made himself a perfect master of his profession’--I could not help blushing, this looked so like praising myself--‘I suppose it is not the custom, in the later years of his time, to allow him any--’\n\nMr. Spenlow, by a great effort, just lifted his head far enough out of his cravat to shake it, and answered, anticipating the word ‘salary’:\n\n‘No. I will not say what consideration I might give to that point myself, Mr. Copperfield, if I were unfettered. Mr. Jorkins is immovable.’\n\nI was quite dismayed by the idea of this terrible Jorkins. But I found out afterwards that he was a mild man of a heavy temperament, whose place in the business was to keep himself in the background, and be constantly exhibited by name as the most obdurate and ruthless of men. If a clerk wanted his salary raised, Mr. Jorkins wouldn’t listen to such a proposition. If a client were slow to settle his bill of costs, Mr. Jorkins was resolved to have it paid; and however painful these things might be (and always were) to the feelings of Mr. Spenlow, Mr. Jorkins would have his bond. The heart and hand of the good angel Spenlow would have been always open, but for the restraining demon Jorkins. As I have grown older, I think I have had experience of some other houses doing business on the principle of Spenlow and Jorkins!\n\nIt was settled that I should begin my month’s probation as soon as I pleased, and that my aunt need neither remain in town nor return at its expiration, as the articles of agreement, of which I was to be the subject, could easily be sent to her at home for her signature. When we had got so far, Mr. Spenlow offered to take me into Court then and there, and show me what sort of place it was. As I was willing enough to know, we went out with this object, leaving my aunt behind; who would trust herself, she said, in no such place, and who, I think, regarded all Courts of Law as a sort of powder-mills that might blow up at any time.\n\nMr. Spenlow conducted me through a paved courtyard formed of grave brick houses, which I inferred, from the Doctors’ names upon the doors, to be the official abiding-places of the learned advocates of whom Steerforth had told me; and into a large dull room, not unlike a chapel to my thinking, on the left hand. The upper part of this room was fenced off from the rest; and there, on the two sides of a raised platform of the horse-shoe form, sitting on easy old-fashioned dining-room chairs, were sundry gentlemen in red gowns and grey wigs, whom I found to be the Doctors aforesaid. Blinking over a little desk like a pulpit-desk, in the curve of the horse-shoe, was an old gentleman, whom, if I had seen him in an aviary, I should certainly have taken for an owl, but who, I learned, was the presiding judge. In the space within the horse-shoe, lower than these, that is to say, on about the level of the floor, were sundry other gentlemen, of Mr. Spenlow’s rank, and dressed like him in black gowns with white fur upon them, sitting at a long green table. Their cravats were in general stiff, I thought, and their looks haughty; but in this last respect I presently conceived I had done them an injustice, for when two or three of them had to rise and answer a question of the presiding dignitary, I never saw anything more sheepish. The public, represented by a boy with a comforter, and a shabby-genteel man secretly eating crumbs out of his coat pockets, was warming itself at a stove in the centre of the Court. The languid stillness of the place was only broken by the chirping of this fire and by the voice of one of the Doctors, who was wandering slowly through a perfect library of evidence, and stopping to put up, from time to time, at little roadside inns of argument on the journey. Altogether, I have never, on any occasion, made one at such a cosey, dosey, old-fashioned, time-forgotten, sleepy-headed little family-party in all my life; and I felt it would be quite a soothing opiate to belong to it in any character--except perhaps as a suitor.\n\nVery well satisfied with the dreamy nature of this retreat, I informed Mr. Spenlow that I had seen enough for that time, and we rejoined my aunt; in company with whom I presently departed from the Commons, feeling very young when I went out of Spenlow and Jorkins’s, on account of the clerks poking one another with their pens to point me out.\n\nWe arrived at Lincoln’s Inn Fields without any new adventures, except encountering an unlucky donkey in a costermonger’s cart, who suggested painful associations to my aunt. We had another long talk about my plans, when we were safely housed; and as I knew she was anxious to get home, and, between fire, food, and pickpockets, could never be considered at her ease for half-an-hour in London, I urged her not to be uncomfortable on my account, but to leave me to take care of myself.\n\n‘I have not been here a week tomorrow, without considering that too, my dear,’ she returned. ‘There is a furnished little set of chambers to be let in the Adelphi, Trot, which ought to suit you to a marvel.’\n\nWith this brief introduction, she produced from her pocket an advertisement, carefully cut out of a newspaper, setting forth that in Buckingham Street in the Adelphi there was to be let furnished, with a view of the river, a singularly desirable, and compact set of chambers, forming a genteel residence for a young gentleman, a member of one of the Inns of Court, or otherwise, with immediate possession. Terms moderate, and could be taken for a month only, if required.\n\n‘Why, this is the very thing, aunt!’ said I, flushed with the possible dignity of living in chambers.\n\n‘Then come,’ replied my aunt, immediately resuming the bonnet she had a minute before laid aside. ‘We’ll go and look at ‘em.’\n\nAway we went. The advertisement directed us to apply to Mrs. Crupp on the premises, and we rung the area bell, which we supposed to communicate with Mrs. Crupp. It was not until we had rung three or four times that we could prevail on Mrs. Crupp to communicate with us, but at last she appeared, being a stout lady with a flounce of flannel petticoat below a nankeen gown.\n\n‘Let us see these chambers of yours, if you please, ma’am,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘For this gentleman?’ said Mrs. Crupp, feeling in her pocket for her keys.\n\n‘Yes, for my nephew,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘And a sweet set they is for sich!’ said Mrs. Crupp.\n\nSo we went upstairs.\n\nThey were on the top of the house--a great point with my aunt, being near the fire-escape--and consisted of a little half-blind entry where you could see hardly anything, a little stone-blind pantry where you could see nothing at all, a sitting-room, and a bedroom. The furniture was rather faded, but quite good enough for me; and, sure enough, the river was outside the windows.\n\nAs I was delighted with the place, my aunt and Mrs. Crupp withdrew into the pantry to discuss the terms, while I remained on the sitting-room sofa, hardly daring to think it possible that I could be destined to live in such a noble residence. After a single combat of some duration they returned, and I saw, to my joy, both in Mrs. Crupp’s countenance and in my aunt’s, that the deed was done.\n\n‘Is it the last occupant’s furniture?’ inquired my aunt.\n\n‘Yes, it is, ma’am,’ said Mrs. Crupp.\n\n‘What’s become of him?’ asked my aunt.\n\nMrs. Crupp was taken with a troublesome cough, in the midst of which she articulated with much difficulty. ‘He was took ill here, ma’am, and--ugh! ugh! ugh! dear me!--and he died!’\n\n‘Hey! What did he die of?’ asked my aunt.\n\n‘Well, ma’am, he died of drink,’ said Mrs. Crupp, in confidence. ‘And smoke.’\n\n‘Smoke? You don’t mean chimneys?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘No, ma’am,’ returned Mrs. Crupp. ‘Cigars and pipes.’\n\n‘That’s not catching, Trot, at any rate,’ remarked my aunt, turning to me.\n\n‘No, indeed,’ said I.\n\nIn short, my aunt, seeing how enraptured I was with the premises, took them for a month, with leave to remain for twelve months when that time was out. Mrs. Crupp was to find linen, and to cook; every other necessary was already provided; and Mrs. Crupp expressly intimated that she should always yearn towards me as a son. I was to take possession the day after tomorrow, and Mrs. Crupp said, thank Heaven she had now found summun she could care for!\n\nOn our way back, my aunt informed me how she confidently trusted that the life I was now to lead would make me firm and self-reliant, which was all I wanted. She repeated this several times next day, in the intervals of our arranging for the transmission of my clothes and books from Mr. Wickfield’s; relative to which, and to all my late holiday, I wrote a long letter to Agnes, of which my aunt took charge, as she was to leave on the succeeding day. Not to lengthen these particulars, I need only add, that she made a handsome provision for all my possible wants during my month of trial; that Steerforth, to my great disappointment and hers too, did not make his appearance before she went away; that I saw her safely seated in the Dover coach, exulting in the coming discomfiture of the vagrant donkeys, with Janet at her side; and that when the coach was gone, I turned my face to the Adelphi, pondering on the old days when I used to roam about its subterranean arches, and on the happy changes which had brought me to the surface.\n\n# CHAPTER 24. MY FIRST DISSIPATION\n\nIt was a wonderfully fine thing to have that lofty castle to myself, and to feel, when I shut my outer door, like Robinson Crusoe, when he had got into his fortification, and pulled his ladder up after him. It was a wonderfully fine thing to walk about town with the key of my house in my pocket, and to know that I could ask any fellow to come home, and make quite sure of its being inconvenient to nobody, if it were not so to me. It was a wonderfully fine thing to let myself in and out, and to come and go without a word to anyone, and to ring Mrs. Crupp up, gasping, from the depths of the earth, when I wanted her--and when she was disposed to come. All this, I say, was wonderfully fine; but I must say, too, that there were times when it was very dreary.\n\nIt was fine in the morning, particularly in the fine mornings. It looked a very fresh, free life, by daylight: still fresher, and more free, by sunlight. But as the day declined, the life seemed to go down too. I don’t know how it was; it seldom looked well by candle-light. I wanted somebody to talk to, then. I missed Agnes. I found a tremendous blank, in the place of that smiling repository of my confidence. Mrs. Crupp appeared to be a long way off. I thought about my predecessor, who had died of drink and smoke; and I could have wished he had been so good as to live, and not bother me with his decease.\n\nAfter two days and nights, I felt as if I had lived there for a year, and yet I was not an hour older, but was quite as much tormented by my own youthfulness as ever.\n\nSteerforth not yet appearing, which induced me to apprehend that he must be ill, I left the Commons early on the third day, and walked out to Highgate. Mrs. Steerforth was very glad to see me, and said that he had gone away with one of his Oxford friends to see another who lived near St. Albans, but that she expected him to return tomorrow. I was so fond of him, that I felt quite jealous of his Oxford friends.\n\nAs she pressed me to stay to dinner, I remained, and I believe we talked about nothing but him all day. I told her how much the people liked him at Yarmouth, and what a delightful companion he had been. Miss Dartle was full of hints and mysterious questions, but took a great interest in all our proceedings there, and said, ‘Was it really though?’ and so forth, so often, that she got everything out of me she wanted to know. Her appearance was exactly what I have described it, when I first saw her; but the society of the two ladies was so agreeable, and came so natural to me, that I felt myself falling a little in love with her. I could not help thinking, several times in the course of the evening, and particularly when I walked home at night, what delightful company she would be in Buckingham Street.\n\nI was taking my coffee and roll in the morning, before going to the Commons--and I may observe in this place that it is surprising how much coffee Mrs. Crupp used, and how weak it was, considering--when Steerforth himself walked in, to my unbounded joy.\n\n‘My dear Steerforth,’ cried I, ‘I began to think I should never see you again!’\n\n‘I was carried off, by force of arms,’ said Steerforth, ‘the very next morning after I got home. Why, Daisy, what a rare old bachelor you are here!’\n\nI showed him over the establishment, not omitting the pantry, with no little pride, and he commended it highly. ‘I tell you what, old boy,’ he added, ‘I shall make quite a town-house of this place, unless you give me notice to quit.’\n\nThis was a delightful hearing. I told him if he waited for that, he would have to wait till doomsday.\n\n‘But you shall have some breakfast!’ said I, with my hand on the bell-rope, ‘and Mrs. Crupp shall make you some fresh coffee, and I’ll toast you some bacon in a bachelor’s Dutch-oven, that I have got here.’\n\n‘No, no!’ said Steerforth. ‘Don’t ring! I can’t! I am going to breakfast with one of these fellows who is at the Piazza Hotel, in Covent Garden.’\n\n‘But you’ll come back to dinner?’ said I.\n\n‘I can’t, upon my life. There’s nothing I should like better, but I must remain with these two fellows. We are all three off together tomorrow morning.’\n\n‘Then bring them here to dinner,’ I returned. ‘Do you think they would come?’\n\n‘Oh! they would come fast enough,’ said Steerforth; ‘but we should inconvenience you. You had better come and dine with us somewhere.’\n\nI would not by any means consent to this, for it occurred to me that I really ought to have a little house-warming, and that there never could be a better opportunity. I had a new pride in my rooms after his approval of them, and burned with a desire to develop their utmost resources. I therefore made him promise positively in the names of his two friends, and we appointed six o’clock as the dinner-hour.\n\nWhen he was gone, I rang for Mrs. Crupp, and acquainted her with my desperate design. Mrs. Crupp said, in the first place, of course it was well known she couldn’t be expected to wait, but she knew a handy young man, who she thought could be prevailed upon to do it, and whose terms would be five shillings, and what I pleased. I said, certainly we would have him. Next Mrs. Crupp said it was clear she couldn’t be in two places at once (which I felt to be reasonable), and that ‘a young gal’ stationed in the pantry with a bedroom candle, there never to desist from washing plates, would be indispensable. I said, what would be the expense of this young female? and Mrs. Crupp said she supposed eighteenpence would neither make me nor break me. I said I supposed not; and THAT was settled. Then Mrs. Crupp said, Now about the dinner.\n\nIt was a remarkable instance of want of forethought on the part of the ironmonger who had made Mrs. Crupp’s kitchen fireplace, that it was capable of cooking nothing but chops and mashed potatoes. As to a fish-kittle, Mrs. Crupp said, well! would I only come and look at the range? She couldn’t say fairer than that. Would I come and look at it? As I should not have been much the wiser if I HAD looked at it, I declined, and said, ‘Never mind fish.’ But Mrs. Crupp said, Don’t say that; oysters was in, why not them? So THAT was settled. Mrs. Crupp then said what she would recommend would be this. A pair of hot roast fowls--from the pastry-cook’s; a dish of stewed beef, with vegetables--from the pastry-cook’s; two little corner things, as a raised pie and a dish of kidneys--from the pastrycook’s; a tart, and (if I liked) a shape of jelly--from the pastrycook’s. This, Mrs. Crupp said, would leave her at full liberty to concentrate her mind on the potatoes, and to serve up the cheese and celery as she could wish to see it done.\n\nI acted on Mrs. Crupp’s opinion, and gave the order at the pastry-cook’s myself. Walking along the Strand, afterwards, and observing a hard mottled substance in the window of a ham and beef shop, which resembled marble, but was labelled ‘Mock Turtle’, I went in and bought a slab of it, which I have since seen reason to believe would have sufficed for fifteen people. This preparation, Mrs. Crupp, after some difficulty, consented to warm up; and it shrunk so much in a liquid state, that we found it what Steerforth called ‘rather a tight fit’ for four.\n\nThese preparations happily completed, I bought a little dessert in Covent Garden Market, and gave a rather extensive order at a retail wine-merchant’s in that vicinity. When I came home in the afternoon, and saw the bottles drawn up in a square on the pantry floor, they looked so numerous (though there were two missing, which made Mrs. Crupp very uncomfortable), that I was absolutely frightened at them.\n\nOne of Steerforth’s friends was named Grainger, and the other Markham. They were both very gay and lively fellows; Grainger, something older than Steerforth; Markham, youthful-looking, and I should say not more than twenty. I observed that the latter always spoke of himself indefinitely, as ‘a man’, and seldom or never in the first person singular.\n\n‘A man might get on very well here, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Markham--meaning himself.\n\n‘It’s not a bad situation,’ said I, ‘and the rooms are really commodious.’\n\n‘I hope you have both brought appetites with you?’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Upon my honour,’ returned Markham, ‘town seems to sharpen a man’s appetite. A man is hungry all day long. A man is perpetually eating.’\n\nBeing a little embarrassed at first, and feeling much too young to preside, I made Steerforth take the head of the table when dinner was announced, and seated myself opposite to him. Everything was very good; we did not spare the wine; and he exerted himself so brilliantly to make the thing pass off well, that there was no pause in our festivity. I was not quite such good company during dinner as I could have wished to be, for my chair was opposite the door, and my attention was distracted by observing that the handy young man went out of the room very often, and that his shadow always presented itself, immediately afterwards, on the wall of the entry, with a bottle at its mouth. The ‘young gal’ likewise occasioned me some uneasiness: not so much by neglecting to wash the plates, as by breaking them. For being of an inquisitive disposition, and unable to confine herself (as her positive instructions were) to the pantry, she was constantly peering in at us, and constantly imagining herself detected; in which belief, she several times retired upon the plates (with which she had carefully paved the floor), and did a great deal of destruction.\n\nThese, however, were small drawbacks, and easily forgotten when the cloth was cleared, and the dessert put on the table; at which period of the entertainment the handy young man was discovered to be speechless. Giving him private directions to seek the society of Mrs. Crupp, and to remove the ‘young gal’ to the basement also, I abandoned myself to enjoyment.\n\nI began, by being singularly cheerful and light-hearted; all sorts of half-forgotten things to talk about, came rushing into my mind, and made me hold forth in a most unwonted manner. I laughed heartily at my own jokes, and everybody else’s; called Steerforth to order for not passing the wine; made several engagements to go to Oxford; announced that I meant to have a dinner-party exactly like that, once a week, until further notice; and madly took so much snuff out of Grainger’s box, that I was obliged to go into the pantry, and have a private fit of sneezing ten minutes long.\n\nI went on, by passing the wine faster and faster yet, and continually starting up with a corkscrew to open more wine, long before any was needed. I proposed Steerforth’s health. I said he was my dearest friend, the protector of my boyhood, and the companion of my prime. I said I was delighted to propose his health. I said I owed him more obligations than I could ever repay, and held him in a higher admiration than I could ever express. I finished by saying, ‘I’ll give you Steerforth! God bless him! Hurrah!’ We gave him three times three, and another, and a good one to finish with. I broke my glass in going round the table to shake hands with him, and I said (in two words)\n\n‘Steerforth--you’retheguidingstarofmyexistence.’\n\nI went on, by finding suddenly that somebody was in the middle of a song. Markham was the singer, and he sang ‘When the heart of a man is depressed with care’. He said, when he had sung it, he would give us ‘Woman!’ I took objection to that, and I couldn’t allow it. I said it was not a respectful way of proposing the toast, and I would never permit that toast to be drunk in my house otherwise than as ‘The Ladies!’ I was very high with him, mainly I think because I saw Steerforth and Grainger laughing at me--or at him--or at both of us. He said a man was not to be dictated to. I said a man was. He said a man was not to be insulted, then. I said he was right there--never under my roof, where the Lares were sacred, and the laws of hospitality paramount. He said it was no derogation from a man’s dignity to confess that I was a devilish good fellow. I instantly proposed his health.\n\nSomebody was smoking. We were all smoking. I was smoking, and trying to suppress a rising tendency to shudder. Steerforth had made a speech about me, in the course of which I had been affected almost to tears. I returned thanks, and hoped the present company would dine with me tomorrow, and the day after--each day at five o’clock, that we might enjoy the pleasures of conversation and society through a long evening. I felt called upon to propose an individual. I would give them my aunt. Miss Betsey Trotwood, the best of her sex!\n\nSomebody was leaning out of my bedroom window, refreshing his forehead against the cool stone of the parapet, and feeling the air upon his face. It was myself. I was addressing myself as ‘Copperfield’, and saying, ‘Why did you try to smoke? You might have known you couldn’t do it.’ Now, somebody was unsteadily contemplating his features in the looking-glass. That was I too. I was very pale in the looking-glass; my eyes had a vacant appearance; and my hair--only my hair, nothing else--looked drunk.\n\nSomebody said to me, ‘Let us go to the theatre, Copperfield!’ There was no bedroom before me, but again the jingling table covered with glasses; the lamp; Grainger on my right hand, Markham on my left, and Steerforth opposite--all sitting in a mist, and a long way off. The theatre? To be sure. The very thing. Come along! But they must excuse me if I saw everybody out first, and turned the lamp off--in case of fire.\n\nOwing to some confusion in the dark, the door was gone. I was feeling for it in the window-curtains, when Steerforth, laughing, took me by the arm and led me out. We went downstairs, one behind another. Near the bottom, somebody fell, and rolled down. Somebody else said it was Copperfield. I was angry at that false report, until, finding myself on my back in the passage, I began to think there might be some foundation for it.\n\nA very foggy night, with great rings round the lamps in the streets! There was an indistinct talk of its being wet. I considered it frosty. Steerforth dusted me under a lamp-post, and put my hat into shape, which somebody produced from somewhere in a most extraordinary manner, for I hadn’t had it on before. Steerforth then said, ‘You are all right, Copperfield, are you not?’ and I told him, ‘Neverberrer.’\n\nA man, sitting in a pigeon-hole-place, looked out of the fog, and took money from somebody, inquiring if I was one of the gentlemen paid for, and appearing rather doubtful (as I remember in the glimpse I had of him) whether to take the money for me or not. Shortly afterwards, we were very high up in a very hot theatre, looking down into a large pit, that seemed to me to smoke; the people with whom it was crammed were so indistinct. There was a great stage, too, looking very clean and smooth after the streets; and there were people upon it, talking about something or other, but not at all intelligibly. There was an abundance of bright lights, and there was music, and there were ladies down in the boxes, and I don’t know what more. The whole building looked to me as if it were learning to swim; it conducted itself in such an unaccountable manner, when I tried to steady it.\n\nOn somebody’s motion, we resolved to go downstairs to the dress-boxes, where the ladies were. A gentleman lounging, full dressed, on a sofa, with an opera-glass in his hand, passed before my view, and also my own figure at full length in a glass. Then I was being ushered into one of these boxes, and found myself saying something as I sat down, and people about me crying ‘Silence!’ to somebody, and ladies casting indignant glances at me, and--what! yes!--Agnes, sitting on the seat before me, in the same box, with a lady and gentleman beside her, whom I didn’t know. I see her face now, better than I did then, I dare say, with its indelible look of regret and wonder turned upon me.\n\n‘Agnes!’ I said, thickly, ‘Lorblessmer! Agnes!’\n\n‘Hush! Pray!’ she answered, I could not conceive why. ‘You disturb the company. Look at the stage!’\n\nI tried, on her injunction, to fix it, and to hear something of what was going on there, but quite in vain. I looked at her again by and by, and saw her shrink into her corner, and put her gloved hand to her forehead.\n\n‘Agnes!’ I said. ‘I’mafraidyou’renorwell.’\n\n‘Yes, yes. Do not mind me, Trotwood,’ she returned. ‘Listen! Are you going away soon?’\n\n‘Amigoarawaysoo?’ I repeated.\n\n‘Yes.’\n\nI had a stupid intention of replying that I was going to wait, to hand her downstairs. I suppose I expressed it, somehow; for after she had looked at me attentively for a little while, she appeared to understand, and replied in a low tone:\n\n‘I know you will do as I ask you, if I tell you I am very earnest in it. Go away now, Trotwood, for my sake, and ask your friends to take you home.’\n\nShe had so far improved me, for the time, that though I was angry with her, I felt ashamed, and with a short ‘Goori!’ (which I intended for ‘Good night!’) got up and went away. They followed, and I stepped at once out of the box-door into my bedroom, where only Steerforth was with me, helping me to undress, and where I was by turns telling him that Agnes was my sister, and adjuring him to bring the corkscrew, that I might open another bottle of wine.\n\nHow somebody, lying in my bed, lay saying and doing all this over again, at cross purposes, in a feverish dream all night--the bed a rocking sea that was never still! How, as that somebody slowly settled down into myself, did I begin to parch, and feel as if my outer covering of skin were a hard board; my tongue the bottom of an empty kettle, furred with long service, and burning up over a slow fire; the palms of my hands, hot plates of metal which no ice could cool!\n\nBut the agony of mind, the remorse, and shame I felt when I became conscious next day! My horror of having committed a thousand offences I had forgotten, and which nothing could ever expiate--my recollection of that indelible look which Agnes had given me--the torturing impossibility of communicating with her, not knowing, Beast that I was, how she came to be in London, or where she stayed--my disgust of the very sight of the room where the revel had been held--my racking head--the smell of smoke, the sight of glasses, the impossibility of going out, or even getting up! Oh, what a day it was!\n\nOh, what an evening, when I sat down by my fire to a basin of mutton broth, dimpled all over with fat, and thought I was going the way of my predecessor, and should succeed to his dismal story as well as to his chambers, and had half a mind to rush express to Dover and reveal all! What an evening, when Mrs. Crupp, coming in to take away the broth-basin, produced one kidney on a cheese-plate as the entire remains of yesterday’s feast, and I was really inclined to fall upon her nankeen breast and say, in heartfelt penitence, ‘Oh, Mrs. Crupp, Mrs. Crupp, never mind the broken meats! I am very miserable!’--only that I doubted, even at that pass, if Mrs. Crupp were quite the sort of woman to confide in!\n\n# CHAPTER 25. GOOD AND BAD ANGELS\n\nI was going out at my door on the morning after that deplorable day of headache, sickness, and repentance, with an odd confusion in my mind relative to the date of my dinner-party, as if a body of Titans had taken an enormous lever and pushed the day before yesterday some months back, when I saw a ticket-porter coming upstairs, with a letter in his hand. He was taking his time about his errand, then; but when he saw me on the top of the staircase, looking at him over the banisters, he swung into a trot, and came up panting as if he had run himself into a state of exhaustion.\n\n‘T. Copperfield, Esquire,’ said the ticket-porter, touching his hat with his little cane.\n\nI could scarcely lay claim to the name: I was so disturbed by the conviction that the letter came from Agnes. However, I told him I was T. Copperfield, Esquire, and he believed it, and gave me the letter, which he said required an answer. I shut him out on the landing to wait for the answer, and went into my chambers again, in such a nervous state that I was fain to lay the letter down on my breakfast table, and familiarize myself with the outside of it a little, before I could resolve to break the seal.\n\nI found, when I did open it, that it was a very kind note, containing no reference to my condition at the theatre. All it said was, ‘My dear Trotwood. I am staying at the house of papa’s agent, Mr. Waterbrook, in Ely Place, Holborn. Will you come and see me today, at any time you like to appoint? Ever yours affectionately, AGNES.’\n\nIt took me such a long time to write an answer at all to my satisfaction, that I don’t know what the ticket-porter can have thought, unless he thought I was learning to write. I must have written half-a-dozen answers at least. I began one, ‘How can I ever hope, my dear Agnes, to efface from your remembrance the disgusting impression’--there I didn’t like it, and then I tore it up. I began another, ‘Shakespeare has observed, my dear Agnes, how strange it is that a man should put an enemy into his mouth’--that reminded me of Markham, and it got no farther. I even tried poetry. I began one note, in a six-syllable line, ‘Oh, do not remember’--but that associated itself with the fifth of November, and became an absurdity. After many attempts, I wrote, ‘My dear Agnes. Your letter is like you, and what could I say of it that would be higher praise than that? I will come at four o’clock. Affectionately and sorrowfully, T.C.’ With this missive (which I was in twenty minds at once about recalling, as soon as it was out of my hands), the ticket-porter at last departed.\n\nIf the day were half as tremendous to any other professional gentleman in Doctors’ Commons as it was to me, I sincerely believe he made some expiation for his share in that rotten old ecclesiastical cheese. Although I left the office at half past three, and was prowling about the place of appointment within a few minutes afterwards, the appointed time was exceeded by a full quarter of an hour, according to the clock of St. Andrew’s, Holborn, before I could muster up sufficient desperation to pull the private bell-handle let into the left-hand door-post of Mr. Waterbrook’s house.\n\nThe professional business of Mr. Waterbrook’s establishment was done on the ground-floor, and the genteel business (of which there was a good deal) in the upper part of the building. I was shown into a pretty but rather close drawing-room, and there sat Agnes, netting a purse.\n\nShe looked so quiet and good, and reminded me so strongly of my airy fresh school days at Canterbury, and the sodden, smoky, stupid wretch I had been the other night, that, nobody being by, I yielded to my self-reproach and shame, and--in short, made a fool of myself. I cannot deny that I shed tears. To this hour I am undecided whether it was upon the whole the wisest thing I could have done, or the most ridiculous.\n\n‘If it had been anyone but you, Agnes,’ said I, turning away my head, ‘I should not have minded it half so much. But that it should have been you who saw me! I almost wish I had been dead, first.’\n\nShe put her hand--its touch was like no other hand--upon my arm for a moment; and I felt so befriended and comforted, that I could not help moving it to my lips, and gratefully kissing it.\n\n‘Sit down,’ said Agnes, cheerfully. ‘Don’t be unhappy, Trotwood. If you cannot confidently trust me, whom will you trust?’\n\n‘Ah, Agnes!’ I returned. ‘You are my good Angel!’\n\nShe smiled rather sadly, I thought, and shook her head.\n\n‘Yes, Agnes, my good Angel! Always my good Angel!’\n\n‘If I were, indeed, Trotwood,’ she returned, ‘there is one thing that I should set my heart on very much.’\n\nI looked at her inquiringly; but already with a foreknowledge of her meaning.\n\n‘On warning you,’ said Agnes, with a steady glance, ‘against your bad Angel.’\n\n‘My dear Agnes,’ I began, ‘if you mean Steerforth--’\n\n‘I do, Trotwood,’ she returned. ‘Then, Agnes, you wrong him very much. He my bad Angel, or anyone’s! He, anything but a guide, a support, and a friend to me! My dear Agnes! Now, is it not unjust, and unlike you, to judge him from what you saw of me the other night?’\n\n‘I do not judge him from what I saw of you the other night,’ she quietly replied.\n\n‘From what, then?’\n\n‘From many things--trifles in themselves, but they do not seem to me to be so, when they are put together. I judge him, partly from your account of him, Trotwood, and your character, and the influence he has over you.’\n\nThere was always something in her modest voice that seemed to touch a chord within me, answering to that sound alone. It was always earnest; but when it was very earnest, as it was now, there was a thrill in it that quite subdued me. I sat looking at her as she cast her eyes down on her work; I sat seeming still to listen to her; and Steerforth, in spite of all my attachment to him, darkened in that tone.\n\n‘It is very bold in me,’ said Agnes, looking up again, ‘who have lived in such seclusion, and can know so little of the world, to give you my advice so confidently, or even to have this strong opinion. But I know in what it is engendered, Trotwood,--in how true a remembrance of our having grown up together, and in how true an interest in all relating to you. It is that which makes me bold. I am certain that what I say is right. I am quite sure it is. I feel as if it were someone else speaking to you, and not I, when I caution you that you have made a dangerous friend.’\n\nAgain I looked at her, again I listened to her after she was silent, and again his image, though it was still fixed in my heart, darkened.\n\n‘I am not so unreasonable as to expect,’ said Agnes, resuming her usual tone, after a little while, ‘that you will, or that you can, at once, change any sentiment that has become a conviction to you; least of all a sentiment that is rooted in your trusting disposition. You ought not hastily to do that. I only ask you, Trotwood, if you ever think of me--I mean,’ with a quiet smile, for I was going to interrupt her, and she knew why, ‘as often as you think of me--to think of what I have said. Do you forgive me for all this?’\n\n‘I will forgive you, Agnes,’ I replied, ‘when you come to do Steerforth justice, and to like him as well as I do.’\n\n‘Not until then?’ said Agnes.\n\nI saw a passing shadow on her face when I made this mention of him, but she returned my smile, and we were again as unreserved in our mutual confidence as of old.\n\n‘And when, Agnes,’ said I, ‘will you forgive me the other night?’\n\n‘When I recall it,’ said Agnes.\n\nShe would have dismissed the subject so, but I was too full of it to allow that, and insisted on telling her how it happened that I had disgraced myself, and what chain of accidental circumstances had had the theatre for its final link. It was a great relief to me to do this, and to enlarge on the obligation that I owed to Steerforth for his care of me when I was unable to take care of myself.\n\n‘You must not forget,’ said Agnes, calmly changing the conversation as soon as I had concluded, ‘that you are always to tell me, not only when you fall into trouble, but when you fall in love. Who has succeeded to Miss Larkins, Trotwood?’\n\n‘No one, Agnes.’\n\n‘Someone, Trotwood,’ said Agnes, laughing, and holding up her finger.\n\n‘No, Agnes, upon my word! There is a lady, certainly, at Mrs. Steerforth’s house, who is very clever, and whom I like to talk to--Miss Dartle--but I don’t adore her.’\n\nAgnes laughed again at her own penetration, and told me that if I were faithful to her in my confidence she thought she should keep a little register of my violent attachments, with the date, duration, and termination of each, like the table of the reigns of the kings and queens, in the History of England. Then she asked me if I had seen Uriah.\n\n‘Uriah Heep?’ said I. ‘No. Is he in London?’\n\n‘He comes to the office downstairs, every day,’ returned Agnes. ‘He was in London a week before me. I am afraid on disagreeable business, Trotwood.’\n\n‘On some business that makes you uneasy, Agnes, I see,’ said I. ‘What can that be?’\n\nAgnes laid aside her work, and replied, folding her hands upon one another, and looking pensively at me out of those beautiful soft eyes of hers:\n\n‘I believe he is going to enter into partnership with papa.’\n\n‘What? Uriah? That mean, fawning fellow, worm himself into such promotion!’ I cried, indignantly. ‘Have you made no remonstrance about it, Agnes? Consider what a connexion it is likely to be. You must speak out. You must not allow your father to take such a mad step. You must prevent it, Agnes, while there’s time.’\n\nStill looking at me, Agnes shook her head while I was speaking, with a faint smile at my warmth: and then replied:\n\n‘You remember our last conversation about papa? It was not long after that--not more than two or three days--when he gave me the first intimation of what I tell you. It was sad to see him struggling between his desire to represent it to me as a matter of choice on his part, and his inability to conceal that it was forced upon him. I felt very sorry.’\n\n‘Forced upon him, Agnes! Who forces it upon him?’\n\n‘Uriah,’ she replied, after a moment’s hesitation, ‘has made himself indispensable to papa. He is subtle and watchful. He has mastered papa’s weaknesses, fostered them, and taken advantage of them, until--to say all that I mean in a word, Trotwood,--until papa is afraid of him.’\n\nThere was more that she might have said; more that she knew, or that she suspected; I clearly saw. I could not give her pain by asking what it was, for I knew that she withheld it from me, to spare her father. It had long been going on to this, I was sensible: yes, I could not but feel, on the least reflection, that it had been going on to this for a long time. I remained silent.\n\n‘His ascendancy over papa,’ said Agnes, ‘is very great. He professes humility and gratitude--with truth, perhaps: I hope so--but his position is really one of power, and I fear he makes a hard use of his power.’\n\nI said he was a hound, which, at the moment, was a great satisfaction to me.\n\n‘At the time I speak of, as the time when papa spoke to me,’ pursued Agnes, ‘he had told papa that he was going away; that he was very sorry, and unwilling to leave, but that he had better prospects. Papa was very much depressed then, and more bowed down by care than ever you or I have seen him; but he seemed relieved by this expedient of the partnership, though at the same time he seemed hurt by it and ashamed of it.’\n\n‘And how did you receive it, Agnes?’\n\n‘I did, Trotwood,’ she replied, ‘what I hope was right. Feeling sure that it was necessary for papa’s peace that the sacrifice should be made, I entreated him to make it. I said it would lighten the load of his life--I hope it will!--and that it would give me increased opportunities of being his companion. Oh, Trotwood!’ cried Agnes, putting her hands before her face, as her tears started on it, ‘I almost feel as if I had been papa’s enemy, instead of his loving child. For I know how he has altered, in his devotion to me. I know how he has narrowed the circle of his sympathies and duties, in the concentration of his whole mind upon me. I know what a multitude of things he has shut out for my sake, and how his anxious thoughts of me have shadowed his life, and weakened his strength and energy, by turning them always upon one idea. If I could ever set this right! If I could ever work out his restoration, as I have so innocently been the cause of his decline!’\n\nI had never before seen Agnes cry. I had seen tears in her eyes when I had brought new honours home from school, and I had seen them there when we last spoke about her father, and I had seen her turn her gentle head aside when we took leave of one another; but I had never seen her grieve like this. It made me so sorry that I could only say, in a foolish, helpless manner, ‘Pray, Agnes, don’t! Don’t, my dear sister!’\n\nBut Agnes was too superior to me in character and purpose, as I know well now, whatever I might know or not know then, to be long in need of my entreaties. The beautiful, calm manner, which makes her so different in my remembrance from everybody else, came back again, as if a cloud had passed from a serene sky.\n\n‘We are not likely to remain alone much longer,’ said Agnes, ‘and while I have an opportunity, let me earnestly entreat you, Trotwood, to be friendly to Uriah. Don’t repel him. Don’t resent (as I think you have a general disposition to do) what may be uncongenial to you in him. He may not deserve it, for we know no certain ill of him. In any case, think first of papa and me!’\n\nAgnes had no time to say more, for the room door opened, and Mrs. Waterbrook, who was a large lady--or who wore a large dress: I don’t exactly know which, for I don’t know which was dress and which was lady--came sailing in. I had a dim recollection of having seen her at the theatre, as if I had seen her in a pale magic lantern; but she appeared to remember me perfectly, and still to suspect me of being in a state of intoxication.\n\nFinding by degrees, however, that I was sober, and (I hope) that I was a modest young gentleman, Mrs. Waterbrook softened towards me considerably, and inquired, firstly, if I went much into the parks, and secondly, if I went much into society. On my replying to both these questions in the negative, it occurred to me that I fell again in her good opinion; but she concealed the fact gracefully, and invited me to dinner next day. I accepted the invitation, and took my leave, making a call on Uriah in the office as I went out, and leaving a card for him in his absence.\n\nWhen I went to dinner next day, and on the street door being opened, plunged into a vapour-bath of haunch of mutton, I divined that I was not the only guest, for I immediately identified the ticket-porter in disguise, assisting the family servant, and waiting at the foot of the stairs to carry up my name. He looked, to the best of his ability, when he asked me for it confidentially, as if he had never seen me before; but well did I know him, and well did he know me. Conscience made cowards of us both.\n\nI found Mr. Waterbrook to be a middle-aged gentleman, with a short throat, and a good deal of shirt-collar, who only wanted a black nose to be the portrait of a pug-dog. He told me he was happy to have the honour of making my acquaintance; and when I had paid my homage to Mrs. Waterbrook, presented me, with much ceremony, to a very awful lady in a black velvet dress, and a great black velvet hat, whom I remember as looking like a near relation of Hamlet’s--say his aunt.\n\nMrs. Henry Spiker was this lady’s name; and her husband was there too: so cold a man, that his head, instead of being grey, seemed to be sprinkled with hoar-frost. Immense deference was shown to the Henry Spikers, male and female; which Agnes told me was on account of Mr. Henry Spiker being solicitor to something or to somebody, I forget what or which, remotely connected with the Treasury.\n\nI found Uriah Heep among the company, in a suit of black, and in deep humility. He told me, when I shook hands with him, that he was proud to be noticed by me, and that he really felt obliged to me for my condescension. I could have wished he had been less obliged to me, for he hovered about me in his gratitude all the rest of the evening; and whenever I said a word to Agnes, was sure, with his shadowless eyes and cadaverous face, to be looking gauntly down upon us from behind.\n\nThere were other guests--all iced for the occasion, as it struck me, like the wine. But there was one who attracted my attention before he came in, on account of my hearing him announced as Mr. Traddles! My mind flew back to Salem House; and could it be Tommy, I thought, who used to draw the skeletons!\n\nI looked for Mr. Traddles with unusual interest. He was a sober, steady-looking young man of retiring manners, with a comic head of hair, and eyes that were rather wide open; and he got into an obscure corner so soon, that I had some difficulty in making him out. At length I had a good view of him, and either my vision deceived me, or it was the old unfortunate Tommy.\n\nI made my way to Mr. Waterbrook, and said, that I believed I had the pleasure of seeing an old schoolfellow there.\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Mr. Waterbrook, surprised. ‘You are too young to have been at school with Mr. Henry Spiker?’\n\n‘Oh, I don’t mean him!’ I returned. ‘I mean the gentleman named Traddles.’\n\n‘Oh! Aye, aye! Indeed!’ said my host, with much diminished interest. ‘Possibly.’\n\n‘If it’s really the same person,’ said I, glancing towards him, ‘it was at a place called Salem House where we were together, and he was an excellent fellow.’\n\n‘Oh yes. Traddles is a good fellow,’ returned my host nodding his head with an air of toleration. ‘Traddles is quite a good fellow.’\n\n‘It’s a curious coincidence,’ said I.\n\n‘It is really,’ returned my host, ‘quite a coincidence, that Traddles should be here at all: as Traddles was only invited this morning, when the place at table, intended to be occupied by Mrs. Henry Spiker’s brother, became vacant, in consequence of his indisposition. A very gentlemanly man, Mrs. Henry Spiker’s brother, Mr. Copperfield.’\n\nI murmured an assent, which was full of feeling, considering that I knew nothing at all about him; and I inquired what Mr. Traddles was by profession.\n\n‘Traddles,’ returned Mr. Waterbrook, ‘is a young man reading for the bar. Yes. He is quite a good fellow--nobody’s enemy but his own.’\n\n‘Is he his own enemy?’ said I, sorry to hear this.\n\n‘Well,’ returned Mr. Waterbrook, pursing up his mouth, and playing with his watch-chain, in a comfortable, prosperous sort of way. ‘I should say he was one of those men who stand in their own light. Yes, I should say he would never, for example, be worth five hundred pound. Traddles was recommended to me by a professional friend. Oh yes. Yes. He has a kind of talent for drawing briefs, and stating a case in writing, plainly. I am able to throw something in Traddles’s way, in the course of the year; something--for him--considerable. Oh yes. Yes.’\n\nI was much impressed by the extremely comfortable and satisfied manner in which Mr. Waterbrook delivered himself of this little word ‘Yes’, every now and then. There was wonderful expression in it. It completely conveyed the idea of a man who had been born, not to say with a silver spoon, but with a scaling-ladder, and had gone on mounting all the heights of life one after another, until now he looked, from the top of the fortifications, with the eye of a philosopher and a patron, on the people down in the trenches.\n\nMy reflections on this theme were still in progress when dinner was announced. Mr. Waterbrook went down with Hamlet’s aunt. Mr. Henry Spiker took Mrs. Waterbrook. Agnes, whom I should have liked to take myself, was given to a simpering fellow with weak legs. Uriah, Traddles, and I, as the junior part of the company, went down last, how we could. I was not so vexed at losing Agnes as I might have been, since it gave me an opportunity of making myself known to Traddles on the stairs, who greeted me with great fervour; while Uriah writhed with such obtrusive satisfaction and self-abasement, that I could gladly have pitched him over the banisters. Traddles and I were separated at table, being billeted in two remote corners: he in the glare of a red velvet lady; I, in the gloom of Hamlet’s aunt. The dinner was very long, and the conversation was about the Aristocracy--and Blood. Mrs. Waterbrook repeatedly told us, that if she had a weakness, it was Blood.\n\nIt occurred to me several times that we should have got on better, if we had not been quite so genteel. We were so exceedingly genteel, that our scope was very limited. A Mr. and Mrs. Gulpidge were of the party, who had something to do at second-hand (at least, Mr. Gulpidge had) with the law business of the Bank; and what with the Bank, and what with the Treasury, we were as exclusive as the Court Circular. To mend the matter, Hamlet’s aunt had the family failing of indulging in soliloquy, and held forth in a desultory manner, by herself, on every topic that was introduced. These were few enough, to be sure; but as we always fell back upon Blood, she had as wide a field for abstract speculation as her nephew himself.\n\nWe might have been a party of Ogres, the conversation assumed such a sanguine complexion.\n\n‘I confess I am of Mrs. Waterbrook’s opinion,’ said Mr. Waterbrook, with his wine-glass at his eye. ‘Other things are all very well in their way, but give me Blood!’\n\n‘Oh! There is nothing,’ observed Hamlet’s aunt, ‘so satisfactory to one! There is nothing that is so much one’s beau-ideal of--of all that sort of thing, speaking generally. There are some low minds (not many, I am happy to believe, but there are some) that would prefer to do what I should call bow down before idols. Positively Idols! Before service, intellect, and so on. But these are intangible points. Blood is not so. We see Blood in a nose, and we know it. We meet with it in a chin, and we say, “There it is! That’s Blood!” It is an actual matter of fact. We point it out. It admits of no doubt.’\n\nThe simpering fellow with the weak legs, who had taken Agnes down, stated the question more decisively yet, I thought.\n\n‘Oh, you know, deuce take it,’ said this gentleman, looking round the board with an imbecile smile, ‘we can’t forego Blood, you know. We must have Blood, you know. Some young fellows, you know, may be a little behind their station, perhaps, in point of education and behaviour, and may go a little wrong, you know, and get themselves and other people into a variety of fixes--and all that--but deuce take it, it’s delightful to reflect that they’ve got Blood in ‘em! Myself, I’d rather at any time be knocked down by a man who had got Blood in him, than I’d be picked up by a man who hadn’t!’\n\nThis sentiment, as compressing the general question into a nutshell, gave the utmost satisfaction, and brought the gentleman into great notice until the ladies retired. After that, I observed that Mr. Gulpidge and Mr. Henry Spiker, who had hitherto been very distant, entered into a defensive alliance against us, the common enemy, and exchanged a mysterious dialogue across the table for our defeat and overthrow.\n\n‘That affair of the first bond for four thousand five hundred pounds has not taken the course that was expected, Spiker,’ said Mr. Gulpidge.\n\n‘Do you mean the D. of A.’s?’ said Mr. Spiker.\n\n‘The C. of B.’s!’ said Mr. Gulpidge.\n\nMr. Spiker raised his eyebrows, and looked much concerned.\n\n‘When the question was referred to Lord--I needn’t name him,’ said Mr. Gulpidge, checking himself--\n\n‘I understand,’ said Mr. Spiker, ‘N.’\n\nMr. Gulpidge darkly nodded--‘was referred to him, his answer was, “Money, or no release.”’\n\n‘Lord bless my soul!’ cried Mr. Spiker.\n\n“‘Money, or no release,”’ repeated Mr. Gulpidge, firmly. ‘The next in reversion--you understand me?’\n\n‘K.,’ said Mr. Spiker, with an ominous look.\n\n‘--K. then positively refused to sign. He was attended at Newmarket for that purpose, and he point-blank refused to do it.’\n\nMr. Spiker was so interested, that he became quite stony.\n\n‘So the matter rests at this hour,’ said Mr. Gulpidge, throwing himself back in his chair. ‘Our friend Waterbrook will excuse me if I forbear to explain myself generally, on account of the magnitude of the interests involved.’\n\nMr. Waterbrook was only too happy, as it appeared to me, to have such interests, and such names, even hinted at, across his table. He assumed an expression of gloomy intelligence (though I am persuaded he knew no more about the discussion than I did), and highly approved of the discretion that had been observed. Mr. Spiker, after the receipt of such a confidence, naturally desired to favour his friend with a confidence of his own; therefore the foregoing dialogue was succeeded by another, in which it was Mr. Gulpidge’s turn to be surprised, and that by another in which the surprise came round to Mr. Spiker’s turn again, and so on, turn and turn about. All this time we, the outsiders, remained oppressed by the tremendous interests involved in the conversation; and our host regarded us with pride, as the victims of a salutary awe and astonishment. I was very glad indeed to get upstairs to Agnes, and to talk with her in a corner, and to introduce Traddles to her, who was shy, but agreeable, and the same good-natured creature still. As he was obliged to leave early, on account of going away next morning for a month, I had not nearly so much conversation with him as I could have wished; but we exchanged addresses, and promised ourselves the pleasure of another meeting when he should come back to town. He was greatly interested to hear that I knew Steerforth, and spoke of him with such warmth that I made him tell Agnes what he thought of him. But Agnes only looked at me the while, and very slightly shook her head when only I observed her.\n\nAs she was not among people with whom I believed she could be very much at home, I was almost glad to hear that she was going away within a few days, though I was sorry at the prospect of parting from her again so soon. This caused me to remain until all the company were gone. Conversing with her, and hearing her sing, was such a delightful reminder to me of my happy life in the grave old house she had made so beautiful, that I could have remained there half the night; but, having no excuse for staying any longer, when the lights of Mr. Waterbrook’s society were all snuffed out, I took my leave very much against my inclination. I felt then, more than ever, that she was my better Angel; and if I thought of her sweet face and placid smile, as though they had shone on me from some removed being, like an Angel, I hope I thought no harm.\n\nI have said that the company were all gone; but I ought to have excepted Uriah, whom I don’t include in that denomination, and who had never ceased to hover near us. He was close behind me when I went downstairs. He was close beside me, when I walked away from the house, slowly fitting his long skeleton fingers into the still longer fingers of a great Guy Fawkes pair of gloves.\n\nIt was in no disposition for Uriah’s company, but in remembrance of the entreaty Agnes had made to me, that I asked him if he would come home to my rooms, and have some coffee.\n\n‘Oh, really, Master Copperfield,’ he rejoined--‘I beg your pardon, Mister Copperfield, but the other comes so natural, I don’t like that you should put a constraint upon yourself to ask a numble person like me to your ouse.’\n\n‘There is no constraint in the case,’ said I. ‘Will you come?’\n\n‘I should like to, very much,’ replied Uriah, with a writhe.\n\n‘Well, then, come along!’ said I.\n\nI could not help being rather short with him, but he appeared not to mind it. We went the nearest way, without conversing much upon the road; and he was so humble in respect of those scarecrow gloves, that he was still putting them on, and seemed to have made no advance in that labour, when we got to my place.\n\nI led him up the dark stairs, to prevent his knocking his head against anything, and really his damp cold hand felt so like a frog in mine, that I was tempted to drop it and run away. Agnes and hospitality prevailed, however, and I conducted him to my fireside. When I lighted my candles, he fell into meek transports with the room that was revealed to him; and when I heated the coffee in an unassuming block-tin vessel in which Mrs. Crupp delighted to prepare it (chiefly, I believe, because it was not intended for the purpose, being a shaving-pot, and because there was a patent invention of great price mouldering away in the pantry), he professed so much emotion, that I could joyfully have scalded him.\n\n‘Oh, really, Master Copperfield,--I mean Mister Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘to see you waiting upon me is what I never could have expected! But, one way and another, so many things happen to me which I never could have expected, I am sure, in my umble station, that it seems to rain blessings on my ed. You have heard something, I des-say, of a change in my expectations, Master Copperfield,--I should say, Mister Copperfield?’\n\nAs he sat on my sofa, with his long knees drawn up under his coffee-cup, his hat and gloves upon the ground close to him, his spoon going softly round and round, his shadowless red eyes, which looked as if they had scorched their lashes off, turned towards me without looking at me, the disagreeable dints I have formerly described in his nostrils coming and going with his breath, and a snaky undulation pervading his frame from his chin to his boots, I decided in my own mind that I disliked him intensely. It made me very uncomfortable to have him for a guest, for I was young then, and unused to disguise what I so strongly felt.\n\n‘You have heard something, I des-say, of a change in my expectations, Master Copperfield,--I should say, Mister Copperfield?’ observed Uriah.\n\n‘Yes,’ said I, ‘something.’\n\n‘Ah! I thought Miss Agnes would know of it!’ he quietly returned. ‘I’m glad to find Miss Agnes knows of it. Oh, thank you, Master--Mister Copperfield!’\n\nI could have thrown my bootjack at him (it lay ready on the rug), for having entrapped me into the disclosure of anything concerning Agnes, however immaterial. But I only drank my coffee.\n\n‘What a prophet you have shown yourself, Mister Copperfield!’ pursued Uriah. ‘Dear me, what a prophet you have proved yourself to be! Don’t you remember saying to me once, that perhaps I should be a partner in Mr. Wickfield’s business, and perhaps it might be Wickfield and Heep? You may not recollect it; but when a person is umble, Master Copperfield, a person treasures such things up!’\n\n‘I recollect talking about it,’ said I, ‘though I certainly did not think it very likely then.’ ‘Oh! who would have thought it likely, Mister Copperfield!’ returned Uriah, enthusiastically. ‘I am sure I didn’t myself. I recollect saying with my own lips that I was much too umble. So I considered myself really and truly.’\n\nHe sat, with that carved grin on his face, looking at the fire, as I looked at him.\n\n‘But the umblest persons, Master Copperfield,’ he presently resumed, ‘may be the instruments of good. I am glad to think I have been the instrument of good to Mr. Wickfield, and that I may be more so. Oh what a worthy man he is, Mister Copperfield, but how imprudent he has been!’\n\n‘I am sorry to hear it,’ said I. I could not help adding, rather pointedly, ‘on all accounts.’\n\n‘Decidedly so, Mister Copperfield,’ replied Uriah. ‘On all accounts. Miss Agnes’s above all! You don’t remember your own eloquent expressions, Master Copperfield; but I remember how you said one day that everybody must admire her, and how I thanked you for it! You have forgot that, I have no doubt, Master Copperfield?’\n\n‘No,’ said I, drily.\n\n‘Oh how glad I am you have not!’ exclaimed Uriah. ‘To think that you should be the first to kindle the sparks of ambition in my umble breast, and that you’ve not forgot it! Oh!--Would you excuse me asking for a cup more coffee?’\n\nSomething in the emphasis he laid upon the kindling of those sparks, and something in the glance he directed at me as he said it, had made me start as if I had seen him illuminated by a blaze of light. Recalled by his request, preferred in quite another tone of voice, I did the honours of the shaving-pot; but I did them with an unsteadiness of hand, a sudden sense of being no match for him, and a perplexed suspicious anxiety as to what he might be going to say next, which I felt could not escape his observation.\n\nHe said nothing at all. He stirred his coffee round and round, he sipped it, he felt his chin softly with his grisly hand, he looked at the fire, he looked about the room, he gasped rather than smiled at me, he writhed and undulated about, in his deferential servility, he stirred and sipped again, but he left the renewal of the conversation to me.\n\n‘So, Mr. Wickfield,’ said I, at last, ‘who is worth five hundred of you--or me’; for my life, I think, I could not have helped dividing that part of the sentence with an awkward jerk; ‘has been imprudent, has he, Mr. Heep?’\n\n‘Oh, very imprudent indeed, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, sighing modestly. ‘Oh, very much so! But I wish you’d call me Uriah, if you please. It’s like old times.’\n\n‘Well! Uriah,’ said I, bolting it out with some difficulty.\n\n‘Thank you,’ he returned, with fervour. ‘Thank you, Master Copperfield! It’s like the blowing of old breezes or the ringing of old bellses to hear YOU say Uriah. I beg your pardon. Was I making any observation?’\n\n‘About Mr. Wickfield,’ I suggested.\n\n‘Oh! Yes, truly,’ said Uriah. ‘Ah! Great imprudence, Master Copperfield. It’s a topic that I wouldn’t touch upon, to any soul but you. Even to you I can only touch upon it, and no more. If anyone else had been in my place during the last few years, by this time he would have had Mr. Wickfield (oh, what a worthy man he is, Master Copperfield, too!) under his thumb. Un--der--his thumb,’ said Uriah, very slowly, as he stretched out his cruel-looking hand above my table, and pressed his own thumb upon it, until it shook, and shook the room.\n\nIf I had been obliged to look at him with him splay foot on Mr. Wickfield’s head, I think I could scarcely have hated him more.\n\n‘Oh, dear, yes, Master Copperfield,’ he proceeded, in a soft voice, most remarkably contrasting with the action of his thumb, which did not diminish its hard pressure in the least degree, ‘there’s no doubt of it. There would have been loss, disgrace, I don’t know what at all. Mr. Wickfield knows it. I am the umble instrument of umbly serving him, and he puts me on an eminence I hardly could have hoped to reach. How thankful should I be!’ With his face turned towards me, as he finished, but without looking at me, he took his crooked thumb off the spot where he had planted it, and slowly and thoughtfully scraped his lank jaw with it, as if he were shaving himself.\n\nI recollect well how indignantly my heart beat, as I saw his crafty face, with the appropriately red light of the fire upon it, preparing for something else.\n\n‘Master Copperfield,’ he began--‘but am I keeping you up?’\n\n‘You are not keeping me up. I generally go to bed late.’\n\n‘Thank you, Master Copperfield! I have risen from my umble station since first you used to address me, it is true; but I am umble still. I hope I never shall be otherwise than umble. You will not think the worse of my umbleness, if I make a little confidence to you, Master Copperfield? Will you?’\n\n‘Oh no,’ said I, with an effort.\n\n‘Thank you!’ He took out his pocket-handkerchief, and began wiping the palms of his hands. ‘Miss Agnes, Master Copperfield--’ ‘Well, Uriah?’\n\n‘Oh, how pleasant to be called Uriah, spontaneously!’ he cried; and gave himself a jerk, like a convulsive fish. ‘You thought her looking very beautiful tonight, Master Copperfield?’\n\n‘I thought her looking as she always does: superior, in all respects, to everyone around her,’ I returned.\n\n‘Oh, thank you! It’s so true!’ he cried. ‘Oh, thank you very much for that!’\n\n‘Not at all,’ I said, loftily. ‘There is no reason why you should thank me.’\n\n‘Why that, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘is, in fact, the confidence that I am going to take the liberty of reposing. Umble as I am,’ he wiped his hands harder, and looked at them and at the fire by turns, ‘umble as my mother is, and lowly as our poor but honest roof has ever been, the image of Miss Agnes (I don’t mind trusting you with my secret, Master Copperfield, for I have always overflowed towards you since the first moment I had the pleasure of beholding you in a pony-shay) has been in my breast for years. Oh, Master Copperfield, with what a pure affection do I love the ground my Agnes walks on!’\n\nI believe I had a delirious idea of seizing the red-hot poker out of the fire, and running him through with it. It went from me with a shock, like a ball fired from a rifle: but the image of Agnes, outraged by so much as a thought of this red-headed animal’s, remained in my mind when I looked at him, sitting all awry as if his mean soul griped his body, and made me giddy. He seemed to swell and grow before my eyes; the room seemed full of the echoes of his voice; and the strange feeling (to which, perhaps, no one is quite a stranger) that all this had occurred before, at some indefinite time, and that I knew what he was going to say next, took possession of me.\n\nA timely observation of the sense of power that there was in his face, did more to bring back to my remembrance the entreaty of Agnes, in its full force, than any effort I could have made. I asked him, with a better appearance of composure than I could have thought possible a minute before, whether he had made his feelings known to Agnes.\n\n‘Oh no, Master Copperfield!’ he returned; ‘oh dear, no! Not to anyone but you. You see I am only just emerging from my lowly station. I rest a good deal of hope on her observing how useful I am to her father (for I trust to be very useful to him indeed, Master Copperfield), and how I smooth the way for him, and keep him straight. She’s so much attached to her father, Master Copperfield (oh, what a lovely thing it is in a daughter!), that I think she may come, on his account, to be kind to me.’\n\nI fathomed the depth of the rascal’s whole scheme, and understood why he laid it bare.\n\n‘If you’ll have the goodness to keep my secret, Master Copperfield,’ he pursued, ‘and not, in general, to go against me, I shall take it as a particular favour. You wouldn’t wish to make unpleasantness. I know what a friendly heart you’ve got; but having only known me on my umble footing (on my umblest I should say, for I am very umble still), you might, unbeknown, go against me rather, with my Agnes. I call her mine, you see, Master Copperfield. There’s a song that says, “I’d crowns resign, to call her mine!” I hope to do it, one of these days.’\n\nDear Agnes! So much too loving and too good for anyone that I could think of, was it possible that she was reserved to be the wife of such a wretch as this!\n\n‘There’s no hurry at present, you know, Master Copperfield,’ Uriah proceeded, in his slimy way, as I sat gazing at him, with this thought in my mind. ‘My Agnes is very young still; and mother and me will have to work our way upwards, and make a good many new arrangements, before it would be quite convenient. So I shall have time gradually to make her familiar with my hopes, as opportunities offer. Oh, I’m so much obliged to you for this confidence! Oh, it’s such a relief, you can’t think, to know that you understand our situation, and are certain (as you wouldn’t wish to make unpleasantness in the family) not to go against me!’\n\nHe took the hand which I dared not withhold, and having given it a damp squeeze, referred to his pale-faced watch.\n\n‘Dear me!’ he said, ‘it’s past one. The moments slip away so, in the confidence of old times, Master Copperfield, that it’s almost half past one!’\n\nI answered that I had thought it was later. Not that I had really thought so, but because my conversational powers were effectually scattered.\n\n‘Dear me!’ he said, considering. ‘The ouse that I am stopping at--a sort of a private hotel and boarding ouse, Master Copperfield, near the New River ed--will have gone to bed these two hours.’\n\n‘I am sorry,’ I returned, ‘that there is only one bed here, and that I--’\n\n‘Oh, don’t think of mentioning beds, Master Copperfield!’ he rejoined ecstatically, drawing up one leg. ‘But would you have any objections to my laying down before the fire?’\n\n‘If it comes to that,’ I said, ‘pray take my bed, and I’ll lie down before the fire.’\n\nHis repudiation of this offer was almost shrill enough, in the excess of its surprise and humility, to have penetrated to the ears of Mrs. Crupp, then sleeping, I suppose, in a distant chamber, situated at about the level of low-water mark, soothed in her slumbers by the ticking of an incorrigible clock, to which she always referred me when we had any little difference on the score of punctuality, and which was never less than three-quarters of an hour too slow, and had always been put right in the morning by the best authorities. As no arguments I could urge, in my bewildered condition, had the least effect upon his modesty in inducing him to accept my bedroom, I was obliged to make the best arrangements I could, for his repose before the fire. The mattress of the sofa (which was a great deal too short for his lank figure), the sofa pillows, a blanket, the table-cover, a clean breakfast-cloth, and a great-coat, made him a bed and covering, for which he was more than thankful. Having lent him a night-cap, which he put on at once, and in which he made such an awful figure, that I have never worn one since, I left him to his rest.\n\nI never shall forget that night. I never shall forget how I turned and tumbled; how I wearied myself with thinking about Agnes and this creature; how I considered what could I do, and what ought I to do; how I could come to no other conclusion than that the best course for her peace was to do nothing, and to keep to myself what I had heard. If I went to sleep for a few moments, the image of Agnes with her tender eyes, and of her father looking fondly on her, as I had so often seen him look, arose before me with appealing faces, and filled me with vague terrors. When I awoke, the recollection that Uriah was lying in the next room, sat heavy on me like a waking nightmare; and oppressed me with a leaden dread, as if I had had some meaner quality of devil for a lodger.\n\nThe poker got into my dozing thoughts besides, and wouldn’t come out. I thought, between sleeping and waking, that it was still red hot, and I had snatched it out of the fire, and run him through the body. I was so haunted at last by the idea, though I knew there was nothing in it, that I stole into the next room to look at him. There I saw him, lying on his back, with his legs extending to I don’t know where, gurglings taking place in his throat, stoppages in his nose, and his mouth open like a post-office. He was so much worse in reality than in my distempered fancy, that afterwards I was attracted to him in very repulsion, and could not help wandering in and out every half-hour or so, and taking another look at him. Still, the long, long night seemed heavy and hopeless as ever, and no promise of day was in the murky sky.\n\nWhen I saw him going downstairs early in the morning (for, thank Heaven! he would not stay to breakfast), it appeared to me as if the night was going away in his person. When I went out to the Commons, I charged Mrs. Crupp with particular directions to leave the windows open, that my sitting-room might be aired, and purged of his presence.\n\n# CHAPTER 26. I FALL INTO CAPTIVITY\n\nI saw no more of Uriah Heep, until the day when Agnes left town. I was at the coach office to take leave of her and see her go; and there was he, returning to Canterbury by the same conveyance. It was some small satisfaction to me to observe his spare, short-waisted, high-shouldered, mulberry-coloured great-coat perched up, in company with an umbrella like a small tent, on the edge of the back seat on the roof, while Agnes was, of course, inside; but what I underwent in my efforts to be friendly with him, while Agnes looked on, perhaps deserved that little recompense. At the coach window, as at the dinner-party, he hovered about us without a moment’s intermission, like a great vulture: gorging himself on every syllable that I said to Agnes, or Agnes said to me.\n\nIn the state of trouble into which his disclosure by my fire had thrown me, I had thought very much of the words Agnes had used in reference to the partnership. ‘I did what I hope was right. Feeling sure that it was necessary for papa’s peace that the sacrifice should be made, I entreated him to make it.’ A miserable foreboding that she would yield to, and sustain herself by, the same feeling in reference to any sacrifice for his sake, had oppressed me ever since. I knew how she loved him. I knew what the devotion of her nature was. I knew from her own lips that she regarded herself as the innocent cause of his errors, and as owing him a great debt she ardently desired to pay. I had no consolation in seeing how different she was from this detestable Rufus with the mulberry-coloured great-coat, for I felt that in the very difference between them, in the self-denial of her pure soul and the sordid baseness of his, the greatest danger lay. All this, doubtless, he knew thoroughly, and had, in his cunning, considered well.\n\nYet I was so certain that the prospect of such a sacrifice afar off, must destroy the happiness of Agnes; and I was so sure, from her manner, of its being unseen by her then, and having cast no shadow on her yet; that I could as soon have injured her, as given her any warning of what impended. Thus it was that we parted without explanation: she waving her hand and smiling farewell from the coach window; her evil genius writhing on the roof, as if he had her in his clutches and triumphed.\n\nI could not get over this farewell glimpse of them for a long time. When Agnes wrote to tell me of her safe arrival, I was as miserable as when I saw her going away. Whenever I fell into a thoughtful state, this subject was sure to present itself, and all my uneasiness was sure to be redoubled. Hardly a night passed without my dreaming of it. It became a part of my life, and as inseparable from my life as my own head.\n\nI had ample leisure to refine upon my uneasiness: for Steerforth was at Oxford, as he wrote to me, and when I was not at the Commons, I was very much alone. I believe I had at this time some lurking distrust of Steerforth. I wrote to him most affectionately in reply to his, but I think I was glad, upon the whole, that he could not come to London just then. I suspect the truth to be, that the influence of Agnes was upon me, undisturbed by the sight of him; and that it was the more powerful with me, because she had so large a share in my thoughts and interest.\n\nIn the meantime, days and weeks slipped away. I was articled to Spenlow and Jorkins. I had ninety pounds a year (exclusive of my house-rent and sundry collateral matters) from my aunt. My rooms were engaged for twelve months certain: and though I still found them dreary of an evening, and the evenings long, I could settle down into a state of equable low spirits, and resign myself to coffee; which I seem, on looking back, to have taken by the gallon at about this period of my existence. At about this time, too, I made three discoveries: first, that Mrs. Crupp was a martyr to a curious disorder called ‘the spazzums’, which was generally accompanied with inflammation of the nose, and required to be constantly treated with peppermint; secondly, that something peculiar in the temperature of my pantry, made the brandy-bottles burst; thirdly, that I was alone in the world, and much given to record that circumstance in fragments of English versification.\n\nOn the day when I was articled, no festivity took place, beyond my having sandwiches and sherry into the office for the clerks, and going alone to the theatre at night. I went to see The Stranger, as a Doctors’ Commons sort of play, and was so dreadfully cut up, that I hardly knew myself in my own glass when I got home. Mr. Spenlow remarked, on this occasion, when we concluded our business, that he should have been happy to have seen me at his house at Norwood to celebrate our becoming connected, but for his domestic arrangements being in some disorder, on account of the expected return of his daughter from finishing her education at Paris. But, he intimated that when she came home he should hope to have the pleasure of entertaining me. I knew that he was a widower with one daughter, and expressed my acknowledgements.\n\nMr. Spenlow was as good as his word. In a week or two, he referred to this engagement, and said, that if I would do him the favour to come down next Saturday, and stay till Monday, he would be extremely happy. Of course I said I would do him the favour; and he was to drive me down in his phaeton, and to bring me back.\n\nWhen the day arrived, my very carpet-bag was an object of veneration to the stipendiary clerks, to whom the house at Norwood was a sacred mystery. One of them informed me that he had heard that Mr. Spenlow ate entirely off plate and china; and another hinted at champagne being constantly on draught, after the usual custom of table-beer. The old clerk with the wig, whose name was Mr. Tiffey, had been down on business several times in the course of his career, and had on each occasion penetrated to the breakfast-parlour. He described it as an apartment of the most sumptuous nature, and said that he had drunk brown East India sherry there, of a quality so precious as to make a man wink. We had an adjourned cause in the Consistory that day--about excommunicating a baker who had been objecting in a vestry to a paving-rate--and as the evidence was just twice the length of Robinson Crusoe, according to a calculation I made, it was rather late in the day before we finished. However, we got him excommunicated for six weeks, and sentenced in no end of costs; and then the baker’s proctor, and the judge, and the advocates on both sides (who were all nearly related), went out of town together, and Mr. Spenlow and I drove away in the phaeton.\n\nThe phaeton was a very handsome affair; the horses arched their necks and lifted up their legs as if they knew they belonged to Doctors’ Commons. There was a good deal of competition in the Commons on all points of display, and it turned out some very choice equipages then; though I always have considered, and always shall consider, that in my time the great article of competition there was starch: which I think was worn among the proctors to as great an extent as it is in the nature of man to bear.\n\nWe were very pleasant, going down, and Mr. Spenlow gave me some hints in reference to my profession. He said it was the genteelest profession in the world, and must on no account be confounded with the profession of a solicitor: being quite another sort of thing, infinitely more exclusive, less mechanical, and more profitable. We took things much more easily in the Commons than they could be taken anywhere else, he observed, and that set us, as a privileged class, apart. He said it was impossible to conceal the disagreeable fact, that we were chiefly employed by solicitors; but he gave me to understand that they were an inferior race of men, universally looked down upon by all proctors of any pretensions.\n\nI asked Mr. Spenlow what he considered the best sort of professional business? He replied, that a good case of a disputed will, where there was a neat little estate of thirty or forty thousand pounds, was, perhaps, the best of all. In such a case, he said, not only were there very pretty pickings, in the way of arguments at every stage of the proceedings, and mountains upon mountains of evidence on interrogatory and counter-interrogatory (to say nothing of an appeal lying, first to the Delegates, and then to the Lords), but, the costs being pretty sure to come out of the estate at last, both sides went at it in a lively and spirited manner, and expense was no consideration. Then, he launched into a general eulogium on the Commons. What was to be particularly admired (he said) in the Commons, was its compactness. It was the most conveniently organized place in the world. It was the complete idea of snugness. It lay in a nutshell. For example: You brought a divorce case, or a restitution case, into the Consistory. Very good. You tried it in the Consistory. You made a quiet little round game of it, among a family group, and you played it out at leisure. Suppose you were not satisfied with the Consistory, what did you do then? Why, you went into the Arches. What was the Arches? The same court, in the same room, with the same bar, and the same practitioners, but another judge, for there the Consistory judge could plead any court-day as an advocate. Well, you played your round game out again. Still you were not satisfied. Very good. What did you do then? Why, you went to the Delegates. Who were the Delegates? Why, the Ecclesiastical Delegates were the advocates without any business, who had looked on at the round game when it was playing in both courts, and had seen the cards shuffled, and cut, and played, and had talked to all the players about it, and now came fresh, as judges, to settle the matter to the satisfaction of everybody! Discontented people might talk of corruption in the Commons, closeness in the Commons, and the necessity of reforming the Commons, said Mr. Spenlow solemnly, in conclusion; but when the price of wheat per bushel had been highest, the Commons had been busiest; and a man might lay his hand upon his heart, and say this to the whole world,--‘Touch the Commons, and down comes the country!’\n\nI listened to all this with attention; and though, I must say, I had my doubts whether the country was quite as much obliged to the Commons as Mr. Spenlow made out, I respectfully deferred to his opinion. That about the price of wheat per bushel, I modestly felt was too much for my strength, and quite settled the question. I have never, to this hour, got the better of that bushel of wheat. It has reappeared to annihilate me, all through my life, in connexion with all kinds of subjects. I don’t know now, exactly, what it has to do with me, or what right it has to crush me, on an infinite variety of occasions; but whenever I see my old friend the bushel brought in by the head and shoulders (as he always is, I observe), I give up a subject for lost.\n\nThis is a digression. I was not the man to touch the Commons, and bring down the country. I submissively expressed, by my silence, my acquiescence in all I had heard from my superior in years and knowledge; and we talked about The Stranger and the Drama, and the pairs of horses, until we came to Mr. Spenlow’s gate.\n\nThere was a lovely garden to Mr. Spenlow’s house; and though that was not the best time of the year for seeing a garden, it was so beautifully kept, that I was quite enchanted. There was a charming lawn, there were clusters of trees, and there were perspective walks that I could just distinguish in the dark, arched over with trellis-work, on which shrubs and flowers grew in the growing season. ‘Here Miss Spenlow walks by herself,’ I thought. ‘Dear me!’\n\nWe went into the house, which was cheerfully lighted up, and into a hall where there were all sorts of hats, caps, great-coats, plaids, gloves, whips, and walking-sticks. ‘Where is Miss Dora?’ said Mr. Spenlow to the servant. ‘Dora!’ I thought. ‘What a beautiful name!’\n\nWe turned into a room near at hand (I think it was the identical breakfast-room, made memorable by the brown East Indian sherry), and I heard a voice say, ‘Mr. Copperfield, my daughter Dora, and my daughter Dora’s confidential friend!’ It was, no doubt, Mr. Spenlow’s voice, but I didn’t know it, and I didn’t care whose it was. All was over in a moment. I had fulfilled my destiny. I was a captive and a slave. I loved Dora Spenlow to distraction!\n\nShe was more than human to me. She was a Fairy, a Sylph, I don’t know what she was--anything that no one ever saw, and everything that everybody ever wanted. I was swallowed up in an abyss of love in an instant. There was no pausing on the brink; no looking down, or looking back; I was gone, headlong, before I had sense to say a word to her.\n\n‘I,’ observed a well-remembered voice, when I had bowed and murmured something, ‘have seen Mr. Copperfield before.’\n\nThe speaker was not Dora. No; the confidential friend, Miss Murdstone!\n\nI don’t think I was much astonished. To the best of my judgement, no capacity of astonishment was left in me. There was nothing worth mentioning in the material world, but Dora Spenlow, to be astonished about. I said, ‘How do you do, Miss Murdstone? I hope you are well.’ She answered, ‘Very well.’ I said, ‘How is Mr. Murdstone?’ She replied, ‘My brother is robust, I am obliged to you.’\n\nMr. Spenlow, who, I suppose, had been surprised to see us recognize each other, then put in his word.\n\n‘I am glad to find,’ he said, ‘Copperfield, that you and Miss Murdstone are already acquainted.’\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield and myself,’ said Miss Murdstone, with severe composure, ‘are connexions. We were once slightly acquainted. It was in his childish days. Circumstances have separated us since. I should not have known him.’\n\nI replied that I should have known her, anywhere. Which was true enough.\n\n‘Miss Murdstone has had the goodness,’ said Mr. Spenlow to me, ‘to accept the office--if I may so describe it--of my daughter Dora’s confidential friend. My daughter Dora having, unhappily, no mother, Miss Murdstone is obliging enough to become her companion and protector.’\n\nA passing thought occurred to me that Miss Murdstone, like the pocket instrument called a life-preserver, was not so much designed for purposes of protection as of assault. But as I had none but passing thoughts for any subject save Dora, I glanced at her, directly afterwards, and was thinking that I saw, in her prettily pettish manner, that she was not very much inclined to be particularly confidential to her companion and protector, when a bell rang, which Mr. Spenlow said was the first dinner-bell, and so carried me off to dress.\n\nThe idea of dressing one’s self, or doing anything in the way of action, in that state of love, was a little too ridiculous. I could only sit down before my fire, biting the key of my carpet-bag, and think of the captivating, girlish, bright-eyed lovely Dora. What a form she had, what a face she had, what a graceful, variable, enchanting manner!\n\nThe bell rang again so soon that I made a mere scramble of my dressing, instead of the careful operation I could have wished under the circumstances, and went downstairs. There was some company. Dora was talking to an old gentleman with a grey head. Grey as he was--and a great-grandfather into the bargain, for he said so--I was madly jealous of him.\n\nWhat a state of mind I was in! I was jealous of everybody. I couldn’t bear the idea of anybody knowing Mr. Spenlow better than I did. It was torturing to me to hear them talk of occurrences in which I had had no share. When a most amiable person, with a highly polished bald head, asked me across the dinner table, if that were the first occasion of my seeing the grounds, I could have done anything to him that was savage and revengeful.\n\nI don’t remember who was there, except Dora. I have not the least idea what we had for dinner, besides Dora. My impression is, that I dined off Dora, entirely, and sent away half-a-dozen plates untouched. I sat next to her. I talked to her. She had the most delightful little voice, the gayest little laugh, the pleasantest and most fascinating little ways, that ever led a lost youth into hopeless slavery. She was rather diminutive altogether. So much the more precious, I thought.\n\nWhen she went out of the room with Miss Murdstone (no other ladies were of the party), I fell into a reverie, only disturbed by the cruel apprehension that Miss Murdstone would disparage me to her. The amiable creature with the polished head told me a long story, which I think was about gardening. I think I heard him say, ‘my gardener’, several times. I seemed to pay the deepest attention to him, but I was wandering in a garden of Eden all the while, with Dora.\n\nMy apprehensions of being disparaged to the object of my engrossing affection were revived when we went into the drawing-room, by the grim and distant aspect of Miss Murdstone. But I was relieved of them in an unexpected manner.\n\n‘David Copperfield,’ said Miss Murdstone, beckoning me aside into a window. ‘A word.’\n\nI confronted Miss Murdstone alone.\n\n‘David Copperfield,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘I need not enlarge upon family circumstances. They are not a tempting subject.’ ‘Far from it, ma’am,’ I returned.\n\n‘Far from it,’ assented Miss Murdstone. ‘I do not wish to revive the memory of past differences, or of past outrages. I have received outrages from a person--a female I am sorry to say, for the credit of my sex--who is not to be mentioned without scorn and disgust; and therefore I would rather not mention her.’\n\nI felt very fiery on my aunt’s account; but I said it would certainly be better, if Miss Murdstone pleased, not to mention her. I could not hear her disrespectfully mentioned, I added, without expressing my opinion in a decided tone.\n\nMiss Murdstone shut her eyes, and disdainfully inclined her head; then, slowly opening her eyes, resumed:\n\n‘David Copperfield, I shall not attempt to disguise the fact, that I formed an unfavourable opinion of you in your childhood. It may have been a mistaken one, or you may have ceased to justify it. That is not in question between us now. I belong to a family remarkable, I believe, for some firmness; and I am not the creature of circumstance or change. I may have my opinion of you. You may have your opinion of me.’\n\nI inclined my head, in my turn.\n\n‘But it is not necessary,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘that these opinions should come into collision here. Under existing circumstances, it is as well on all accounts that they should not. As the chances of life have brought us together again, and may bring us together on other occasions, I would say, let us meet here as distant acquaintances. Family circumstances are a sufficient reason for our only meeting on that footing, and it is quite unnecessary that either of us should make the other the subject of remark. Do you approve of this?’\n\n‘Miss Murdstone,’ I returned, ‘I think you and Mr. Murdstone used me very cruelly, and treated my mother with great unkindness. I shall always think so, as long as I live. But I quite agree in what you propose.’\n\nMiss Murdstone shut her eyes again, and bent her head. Then, just touching the back of my hand with the tips of her cold, stiff fingers, she walked away, arranging the little fetters on her wrists and round her neck; which seemed to be the same set, in exactly the same state, as when I had seen her last. These reminded me, in reference to Miss Murdstone’s nature, of the fetters over a jail door; suggesting on the outside, to all beholders, what was to be expected within.\n\nAll I know of the rest of the evening is, that I heard the empress of my heart sing enchanted ballads in the French language, generally to the effect that, whatever was the matter, we ought always to dance, Ta ra la, Ta ra la! accompanying herself on a glorified instrument, resembling a guitar. That I was lost in blissful delirium. That I refused refreshment. That my soul recoiled from punch particularly. That when Miss Murdstone took her into custody and led her away, she smiled and gave me her delicious hand. That I caught a view of myself in a mirror, looking perfectly imbecile and idiotic. That I retired to bed in a most maudlin state of mind, and got up in a crisis of feeble infatuation.\n\nIt was a fine morning, and early, and I thought I would go and take a stroll down one of those wire-arched walks, and indulge my passion by dwelling on her image. On my way through the hall, I encountered her little dog, who was called Jip--short for Gipsy. I approached him tenderly, for I loved even him; but he showed his whole set of teeth, got under a chair expressly to snarl, and wouldn’t hear of the least familiarity.\n\nThe garden was cool and solitary. I walked about, wondering what my feelings of happiness would be, if I could ever become engaged to this dear wonder. As to marriage, and fortune, and all that, I believe I was almost as innocently undesigning then, as when I loved little Em’ly. To be allowed to call her ‘Dora’, to write to her, to dote upon and worship her, to have reason to think that when she was with other people she was yet mindful of me, seemed to me the summit of human ambition--I am sure it was the summit of mine. There is no doubt whatever that I was a lackadaisical young spooney; but there was a purity of heart in all this, that prevents my having quite a contemptuous recollection of it, let me laugh as I may.\n\nI had not been walking long, when I turned a corner, and met her. I tingle again from head to foot as my recollection turns that corner, and my pen shakes in my hand.\n\n‘You--are--out early, Miss Spenlow,’ said I.\n\n‘It’s so stupid at home,’ she replied, ‘and Miss Murdstone is so absurd! She talks such nonsense about its being necessary for the day to be aired, before I come out. Aired!’ (She laughed, here, in the most melodious manner.) ‘On a Sunday morning, when I don’t practise, I must do something. So I told papa last night I must come out. Besides, it’s the brightest time of the whole day. Don’t you think so?’\n\nI hazarded a bold flight, and said (not without stammering) that it was very bright to me then, though it had been very dark to me a minute before.\n\n‘Do you mean a compliment?’ said Dora, ‘or that the weather has really changed?’\n\nI stammered worse than before, in replying that I meant no compliment, but the plain truth; though I was not aware of any change having taken place in the weather. It was in the state of my own feelings, I added bashfully: to clench the explanation.\n\nI never saw such curls--how could I, for there never were such curls!--as those she shook out to hide her blushes. As to the straw hat and blue ribbons which was on the top of the curls, if I could only have hung it up in my room in Buckingham Street, what a priceless possession it would have been!\n\n‘You have just come home from Paris,’ said I.\n\n‘Yes,’ said she. ‘Have you ever been there?’\n‘No.’\n\n‘Oh! I hope you’ll go soon! You would like it so much!’\n\nTraces of deep-seated anguish appeared in my countenance. That she should hope I would go, that she should think it possible I could go, was insupportable. I depreciated Paris; I depreciated France. I said I wouldn’t leave England, under existing circumstances, for any earthly consideration. Nothing should induce me. In short, she was shaking the curls again, when the little dog came running along the walk to our relief.\n\nHe was mortally jealous of me, and persisted in barking at me. She took him up in her arms--oh my goodness!--and caressed him, but he persisted upon barking still. He wouldn’t let me touch him, when I tried; and then she beat him. It increased my sufferings greatly to see the pats she gave him for punishment on the bridge of his blunt nose, while he winked his eyes, and licked her hand, and still growled within himself like a little double-bass. At length he was quiet--well he might be with her dimpled chin upon his head!--and we walked away to look at a greenhouse.\n\n‘You are not very intimate with Miss Murdstone, are you?’ said Dora. --‘My pet.’\n\n(The two last words were to the dog. Oh, if they had only been to me!)\n\n‘No,’ I replied. ‘Not at all so.’\n\n‘She is a tiresome creature,’ said Dora, pouting. ‘I can’t think what papa can have been about, when he chose such a vexatious thing to be my companion. Who wants a protector? I am sure I don’t want a protector. Jip can protect me a great deal better than Miss Murdstone,--can’t you, Jip, dear?’\n\nHe only winked lazily, when she kissed his ball of a head.\n\n‘Papa calls her my confidential friend, but I am sure she is no such thing--is she, Jip? We are not going to confide in any such cross people, Jip and I. We mean to bestow our confidence where we like, and to find out our own friends, instead of having them found out for us--don’t we, Jip?’\n\nJip made a comfortable noise, in answer, a little like a tea-kettle when it sings. As for me, every word was a new heap of fetters, riveted above the last.\n\n‘It is very hard, because we have not a kind Mama, that we are to have, instead, a sulky, gloomy old thing like Miss Murdstone, always following us about--isn’t it, Jip? Never mind, Jip. We won’t be confidential, and we’ll make ourselves as happy as we can in spite of her, and we’ll tease her, and not please her--won’t we, Jip?’\n\nIf it had lasted any longer, I think I must have gone down on my knees on the gravel, with the probability before me of grazing them, and of being presently ejected from the premises besides. But, by good fortune the greenhouse was not far off, and these words brought us to it.\n\nIt contained quite a show of beautiful geraniums. We loitered along in front of them, and Dora often stopped to admire this one or that one, and I stopped to admire the same one, and Dora, laughing, held the dog up childishly, to smell the flowers; and if we were not all three in Fairyland, certainly I was. The scent of a geranium leaf, at this day, strikes me with a half comical half serious wonder as to what change has come over me in a moment; and then I see a straw hat and blue ribbons, and a quantity of curls, and a little black dog being held up, in two slender arms, against a bank of blossoms and bright leaves.\n\nMiss Murdstone had been looking for us. She found us here; and presented her uncongenial cheek, the little wrinkles in it filled with hair powder, to Dora to be kissed. Then she took Dora’s arm in hers, and marched us into breakfast as if it were a soldier’s funeral.\n\nHow many cups of tea I drank, because Dora made it, I don’t know. But, I perfectly remember that I sat swilling tea until my whole nervous system, if I had had any in those days, must have gone by the board. By and by we went to church. Miss Murdstone was between Dora and me in the pew; but I heard her sing, and the congregation vanished. A sermon was delivered--about Dora, of course--and I am afraid that is all I know of the service.\n\nWe had a quiet day. No company, a walk, a family dinner of four, and an evening of looking over books and pictures; Miss Murdstone with a homily before her, and her eye upon us, keeping guard vigilantly. Ah! little did Mr. Spenlow imagine, when he sat opposite to me after dinner that day, with his pocket-handkerchief over his head, how fervently I was embracing him, in my fancy, as his son-in-law! Little did he think, when I took leave of him at night, that he had just given his full consent to my being engaged to Dora, and that I was invoking blessings on his head!\n\nWe departed early in the morning, for we had a Salvage case coming on in the Admiralty Court, requiring a rather accurate knowledge of the whole science of navigation, in which (as we couldn’t be expected to know much about those matters in the Commons) the judge had entreated two old Trinity Masters, for charity’s sake, to come and help him out. Dora was at the breakfast-table to make the tea again, however; and I had the melancholy pleasure of taking off my hat to her in the phaeton, as she stood on the door-step with Jip in her arms.\n\nWhat the Admiralty was to me that day; what nonsense I made of our case in my mind, as I listened to it; how I saw ‘DORA’ engraved upon the blade of the silver oar which they lay upon the table, as the emblem of that high jurisdiction; and how I felt when Mr. Spenlow went home without me (I had had an insane hope that he might take me back again), as if I were a mariner myself, and the ship to which I belonged had sailed away and left me on a desert island; I shall make no fruitless effort to describe. If that sleepy old court could rouse itself, and present in any visible form the daydreams I have had in it about Dora, it would reveal my truth.\n\nI don’t mean the dreams that I dreamed on that day alone, but day after day, from week to week, and term to term. I went there, not to attend to what was going on, but to think about Dora. If ever I bestowed a thought upon the cases, as they dragged their slow length before me, it was only to wonder, in the matrimonial cases (remembering Dora), how it was that married people could ever be otherwise than happy; and, in the Prerogative cases, to consider, if the money in question had been left to me, what were the foremost steps I should immediately have taken in regard to Dora. Within the first week of my passion, I bought four sumptuous waistcoats--not for myself; I had no pride in them; for Dora--and took to wearing straw-coloured kid gloves in the streets, and laid the foundations of all the corns I have ever had. If the boots I wore at that period could only be produced and compared with the natural size of my feet, they would show what the state of my heart was, in a most affecting manner.\n\nAnd yet, wretched cripple as I made myself by this act of homage to Dora, I walked miles upon miles daily in the hope of seeing her. Not only was I soon as well known on the Norwood Road as the postmen on that beat, but I pervaded London likewise. I walked about the streets where the best shops for ladies were, I haunted the Bazaar like an unquiet spirit, I fagged through the Park again and again, long after I was quite knocked up. Sometimes, at long intervals and on rare occasions, I saw her. Perhaps I saw her glove waved in a carriage window; perhaps I met her, walked with her and Miss Murdstone a little way, and spoke to her. In the latter case I was always very miserable afterwards, to think that I had said nothing to the purpose; or that she had no idea of the extent of my devotion, or that she cared nothing about me. I was always looking out, as may be supposed, for another invitation to Mr. Spenlow’s house. I was always being disappointed, for I got none.\n\nMrs. Crupp must have been a woman of penetration; for when this attachment was but a few weeks old, and I had not had the courage to write more explicitly even to Agnes, than that I had been to Mr. Spenlow’s house, ‘whose family,’ I added, ‘consists of one daughter’;--I say Mrs. Crupp must have been a woman of penetration, for, even in that early stage, she found it out. She came up to me one evening, when I was very low, to ask (she being then afflicted with the disorder I have mentioned) if I could oblige her with a little tincture of cardamums mixed with rhubarb, and flavoured with seven drops of the essence of cloves, which was the best remedy for her complaint;--or, if I had not such a thing by me, with a little brandy, which was the next best. It was not, she remarked, so palatable to her, but it was the next best. As I had never even heard of the first remedy, and always had the second in the closet, I gave Mrs. Crupp a glass of the second, which (that I might have no suspicion of its being devoted to any improper use) she began to take in my presence.\n\n‘Cheer up, sir,’ said Mrs. Crupp. ‘I can’t abear to see you so, sir: I’m a mother myself.’\n\nI did not quite perceive the application of this fact to myself, but I smiled on Mrs. Crupp, as benignly as was in my power.\n\n‘Come, sir,’ said Mrs. Crupp. ‘Excuse me. I know what it is, sir. There’s a lady in the case.’\n\n‘Mrs. Crupp?’ I returned, reddening.\n\n‘Oh, bless you! Keep a good heart, sir!’ said Mrs. Crupp, nodding encouragement. ‘Never say die, sir! If She don’t smile upon you, there’s a many as will. You are a young gentleman to be smiled on, Mr. Copperfull, and you must learn your walue, sir.’\n\nMrs. Crupp always called me Mr. Copperfull: firstly, no doubt, because it was not my name; and secondly, I am inclined to think, in some indistinct association with a washing-day.\n\n‘What makes you suppose there is any young lady in the case, Mrs. Crupp?’ said I.\n\n‘Mr. Copperfull,’ said Mrs. Crupp, with a great deal of feeling, ‘I’m a mother myself.’\n\nFor some time Mrs. Crupp could only lay her hand upon her nankeen bosom, and fortify herself against returning pain with sips of her medicine. At length she spoke again.\n\n‘When the present set were took for you by your dear aunt, Mr. Copperfull,’ said Mrs. Crupp, ‘my remark were, I had now found summun I could care for. “Thank Ev’in!” were the expression, “I have now found summun I can care for!”--You don’t eat enough, sir, nor yet drink.’\n\n‘Is that what you found your supposition on, Mrs. Crupp?’ said I.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mrs. Crupp, in a tone approaching to severity, ‘I’ve laundressed other young gentlemen besides yourself. A young gentleman may be over-careful of himself, or he may be under-careful of himself. He may brush his hair too regular, or too un-regular. He may wear his boots much too large for him, or much too small. That is according as the young gentleman has his original character formed. But let him go to which extreme he may, sir, there’s a young lady in both of ‘em.’\n\nMrs. Crupp shook her head in such a determined manner, that I had not an inch of vantage-ground left.\n\n‘It was but the gentleman which died here before yourself,’ said Mrs. Crupp, ‘that fell in love--with a barmaid--and had his waistcoats took in directly, though much swelled by drinking.’\n\n‘Mrs. Crupp,’ said I, ‘I must beg you not to connect the young lady in my case with a barmaid, or anything of that sort, if you please.’\n\n‘Mr. Copperfull,’ returned Mrs. Crupp, ‘I’m a mother myself, and not likely. I ask your pardon, sir, if I intrude. I should never wish to intrude where I were not welcome. But you are a young gentleman, Mr. Copperfull, and my adwice to you is, to cheer up, sir, to keep a good heart, and to know your own walue. If you was to take to something, sir,’ said Mrs. Crupp, ‘if you was to take to skittles, now, which is healthy, you might find it divert your mind, and do you good.’\n\nWith these words, Mrs. Crupp, affecting to be very careful of the brandy--which was all gone--thanked me with a majestic curtsey, and retired. As her figure disappeared into the gloom of the entry, this counsel certainly presented itself to my mind in the light of a slight liberty on Mrs. Crupp’s part; but, at the same time, I was content to receive it, in another point of view, as a word to the wise, and a warning in future to keep my secret better.\n\n# CHAPTER 27. TOMMY TRADDLES\n\nIt may have been in consequence of Mrs. Crupp’s advice, and, perhaps, for no better reason than because there was a certain similarity in the sound of the word skittles and Traddles, that it came into my head, next day, to go and look after Traddles. The time he had mentioned was more than out, and he lived in a little street near the Veterinary College at Camden Town, which was principally tenanted, as one of our clerks who lived in that direction informed me, by gentlemen students, who bought live donkeys, and made experiments on those quadrupeds in their private apartments. Having obtained from this clerk a direction to the academic grove in question, I set out, the same afternoon, to visit my old schoolfellow.\n\nI found that the street was not as desirable a one as I could have wished it to be, for the sake of Traddles. The inhabitants appeared to have a propensity to throw any little trifles they were not in want of, into the road: which not only made it rank and sloppy, but untidy too, on account of the cabbage-leaves. The refuse was not wholly vegetable either, for I myself saw a shoe, a doubled-up saucepan, a black bonnet, and an umbrella, in various stages of decomposition, as I was looking out for the number I wanted.\n\nThe general air of the place reminded me forcibly of the days when I lived with Mr. and Mrs. Micawber. An indescribable character of faded gentility that attached to the house I sought, and made it unlike all the other houses in the street--though they were all built on one monotonous pattern, and looked like the early copies of a blundering boy who was learning to make houses, and had not yet got out of his cramped brick-and-mortar pothooks--reminded me still more of Mr. and Mrs. Micawber. Happening to arrive at the door as it was opened to the afternoon milkman, I was reminded of Mr. and Mrs. Micawber more forcibly yet.\n\n‘Now,’ said the milkman to a very youthful servant girl. ‘Has that there little bill of mine been heerd on?’\n\n‘Oh, master says he’ll attend to it immediate,’ was the reply.\n\n‘Because,’ said the milkman, going on as if he had received no answer, and speaking, as I judged from his tone, rather for the edification of somebody within the house, than of the youthful servant--an impression which was strengthened by his manner of glaring down the passage--‘because that there little bill has been running so long, that I begin to believe it’s run away altogether, and never won’t be heerd of. Now, I’m not a going to stand it, you know!’ said the milkman, still throwing his voice into the house, and glaring down the passage.\n\nAs to his dealing in the mild article of milk, by the by, there never was a greater anomaly. His deportment would have been fierce in a butcher or a brandy-merchant.\n\nThe voice of the youthful servant became faint, but she seemed to me, from the action of her lips, again to murmur that it would be attended to immediate.\n\n‘I tell you what,’ said the milkman, looking hard at her for the first time, and taking her by the chin, ‘are you fond of milk?’\n\n‘Yes, I likes it,’ she replied. ‘Good,’ said the milkman. ‘Then you won’t have none tomorrow. D’ye hear? Not a fragment of milk you won’t have tomorrow.’\n\nI thought she seemed, upon the whole, relieved by the prospect of having any today. The milkman, after shaking his head at her darkly, released her chin, and with anything rather than good-will opened his can, and deposited the usual quantity in the family jug. This done, he went away, muttering, and uttered the cry of his trade next door, in a vindictive shriek.\n\n‘Does Mr. Traddles live here?’ I then inquired.\n\nA mysterious voice from the end of the passage replied ‘Yes.’ Upon which the youthful servant replied ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Is he at home?’ said I.\n\nAgain the mysterious voice replied in the affirmative, and again the servant echoed it. Upon this, I walked in, and in pursuance of the servant’s directions walked upstairs; conscious, as I passed the back parlour-door, that I was surveyed by a mysterious eye, probably belonging to the mysterious voice.\n\nWhen I got to the top of the stairs--the house was only a story high above the ground floor--Traddles was on the landing to meet me. He was delighted to see me, and gave me welcome, with great heartiness, to his little room. It was in the front of the house, and extremely neat, though sparely furnished. It was his only room, I saw; for there was a sofa-bedstead in it, and his blacking-brushes and blacking were among his books--on the top shelf, behind a dictionary. His table was covered with papers, and he was hard at work in an old coat. I looked at nothing, that I know of, but I saw everything, even to the prospect of a church upon his china inkstand, as I sat down--and this, too, was a faculty confirmed in me in the old Micawber times. Various ingenious arrangements he had made, for the disguise of his chest of drawers, and the accommodation of his boots, his shaving-glass, and so forth, particularly impressed themselves upon me, as evidences of the same Traddles who used to make models of elephants’ dens in writing-paper to put flies in; and to comfort himself under ill usage, with the memorable works of art I have so often mentioned.\n\nIn a corner of the room was something neatly covered up with a large white cloth. I could not make out what that was.\n\n‘Traddles,’ said I, shaking hands with him again, after I had sat down, ‘I am delighted to see you.’\n\n‘I am delighted to see YOU, Copperfield,’ he returned. ‘I am very glad indeed to see you. It was because I was thoroughly glad to see you when we met in Ely Place, and was sure you were thoroughly glad to see me, that I gave you this address instead of my address at chambers.’ ‘Oh! You have chambers?’ said I.\n\n‘Why, I have the fourth of a room and a passage, and the fourth of a clerk,’ returned Traddles. ‘Three others and myself unite to have a set of chambers--to look business-like--and we quarter the clerk too. Half-a-crown a week he costs me.’\n\nHis old simple character and good temper, and something of his old unlucky fortune also, I thought, smiled at me in the smile with which he made this explanation.\n\n‘It’s not because I have the least pride, Copperfield, you understand,’ said Traddles, ‘that I don’t usually give my address here. It’s only on account of those who come to me, who might not like to come here. For myself, I am fighting my way on in the world against difficulties, and it would be ridiculous if I made a pretence of doing anything else.’\n\n‘You are reading for the bar, Mr. Waterbrook informed me?’ said I.\n\n‘Why, yes,’ said Traddles, rubbing his hands slowly over one another. ‘I am reading for the bar. The fact is, I have just begun to keep my terms, after rather a long delay. It’s some time since I was articled, but the payment of that hundred pounds was a great pull. A great pull!’ said Traddles, with a wince, as if he had had a tooth out.\n\n‘Do you know what I can’t help thinking of, Traddles, as I sit here looking at you?’ I asked him.\n\n‘No,’ said he.\n\n‘That sky-blue suit you used to wear.’\n\n‘Lord, to be sure!’ cried Traddles, laughing. ‘Tight in the arms and legs, you know? Dear me! Well! Those were happy times, weren’t they?’\n\n‘I think our schoolmaster might have made them happier, without doing any harm to any of us, I acknowledge,’ I returned.\n\n‘Perhaps he might,’ said Traddles. ‘But dear me, there was a good deal of fun going on. Do you remember the nights in the bedroom? When we used to have the suppers? And when you used to tell the stories? Ha, ha, ha! And do you remember when I got caned for crying about Mr. Mell? Old Creakle! I should like to see him again, too!’\n\n‘He was a brute to you, Traddles,’ said I, indignantly; for his good humour made me feel as if I had seen him beaten but yesterday.\n\n‘Do you think so?’ returned Traddles. ‘Really? Perhaps he was rather. But it’s all over, a long while. Old Creakle!’\n\n‘You were brought up by an uncle, then?’ said I.\n\n‘Of course I was!’ said Traddles. ‘The one I was always going to write to. And always didn’t, eh! Ha, ha, ha! Yes, I had an uncle then. He died soon after I left school.’\n\n‘Indeed!’\n\n‘Yes. He was a retired--what do you call it!--draper--cloth-merchant--and had made me his heir. But he didn’t like me when I grew up.’\n\n‘Do you really mean that?’ said I. He was so composed, that I fancied he must have some other meaning.\n\n‘Oh dear, yes, Copperfield! I mean it,’ replied Traddles. ‘It was an unfortunate thing, but he didn’t like me at all. He said I wasn’t at all what he expected, and so he married his housekeeper.’\n\n‘And what did you do?’ I asked.\n\n‘I didn’t do anything in particular,’ said Traddles. ‘I lived with them, waiting to be put out in the world, until his gout unfortunately flew to his stomach--and so he died, and so she married a young man, and so I wasn’t provided for.’\n\n‘Did you get nothing, Traddles, after all?’\n\n‘Oh dear, yes!’ said Traddles. ‘I got fifty pounds. I had never been brought up to any profession, and at first I was at a loss what to do for myself. However, I began, with the assistance of the son of a professional man, who had been to Salem House--Yawler, with his nose on one side. Do you recollect him?’\n\nNo. He had not been there with me; all the noses were straight in my day.\n\n‘It don’t matter,’ said Traddles. ‘I began, by means of his assistance, to copy law writings. That didn’t answer very well; and then I began to state cases for them, and make abstracts, and that sort of work. For I am a plodding kind of fellow, Copperfield, and had learnt the way of doing such things pithily. Well! That put it in my head to enter myself as a law student; and that ran away with all that was left of the fifty pounds. Yawler recommended me to one or two other offices, however--Mr. Waterbrook’s for one--and I got a good many jobs. I was fortunate enough, too, to become acquainted with a person in the publishing way, who was getting up an Encyclopaedia, and he set me to work; and, indeed’ (glancing at his table), ‘I am at work for him at this minute. I am not a bad compiler, Copperfield,’ said Traddles, preserving the same air of cheerful confidence in all he said, ‘but I have no invention at all; not a particle. I suppose there never was a young man with less originality than I have.’\n\nAs Traddles seemed to expect that I should assent to this as a matter of course, I nodded; and he went on, with the same sprightly patience--I can find no better expression--as before.\n\n‘So, by little and little, and not living high, I managed to scrape up the hundred pounds at last,’ said Traddles; ‘and thank Heaven that’s paid--though it was--though it certainly was,’ said Traddles, wincing again as if he had had another tooth out, ‘a pull. I am living by the sort of work I have mentioned, still, and I hope, one of these days, to get connected with some newspaper: which would almost be the making of my fortune. Now, Copperfield, you are so exactly what you used to be, with that agreeable face, and it’s so pleasant to see you, that I sha’n’t conceal anything. Therefore you must know that I am engaged.’\n\nEngaged! Oh, Dora!\n\n‘She is a curate’s daughter,’ said Traddles; ‘one of ten, down in Devonshire. Yes!’ For he saw me glance, involuntarily, at the prospect on the inkstand. ‘That’s the church! You come round here to the left, out of this gate,’ tracing his finger along the inkstand, ‘and exactly where I hold this pen, there stands the house--facing, you understand, towards the church.’\n\nThe delight with which he entered into these particulars, did not fully present itself to me until afterwards; for my selfish thoughts were making a ground-plan of Mr. Spenlow’s house and garden at the same moment.\n\n‘She is such a dear girl!’ said Traddles; ‘a little older than me, but the dearest girl! I told you I was going out of town? I have been down there. I walked there, and I walked back, and I had the most delightful time! I dare say ours is likely to be a rather long engagement, but our motto is “Wait and hope!” We always say that. “Wait and hope,” we always say. And she would wait, Copperfield, till she was sixty--any age you can mention--for me!’\n\nTraddles rose from his chair, and, with a triumphant smile, put his hand upon the white cloth I had observed.\n\n‘However,’ he said, ‘it’s not that we haven’t made a beginning towards housekeeping. No, no; we have begun. We must get on by degrees, but we have begun. Here,’ drawing the cloth off with great pride and care, ‘are two pieces of furniture to commence with. This flower-pot and stand, she bought herself. You put that in a parlour window,’ said Traddles, falling a little back from it to survey it with the greater admiration, ‘with a plant in it, and--and there you are! This little round table with the marble top (it’s two feet ten in circumference), I bought. You want to lay a book down, you know, or somebody comes to see you or your wife, and wants a place to stand a cup of tea upon, and--and there you are again!’ said Traddles. ‘It’s an admirable piece of workmanship--firm as a rock!’ I praised them both, highly, and Traddles replaced the covering as carefully as he had removed it.\n\n‘It’s not a great deal towards the furnishing,’ said Traddles, ‘but it’s something. The table-cloths, and pillow-cases, and articles of that kind, are what discourage me most, Copperfield. So does the ironmongery--candle-boxes, and gridirons, and that sort of necessaries--because those things tell, and mount up. However, “wait and hope!” And I assure you she’s the dearest girl!’\n\n‘I am quite certain of it,’ said I.\n\n‘In the meantime,’ said Traddles, coming back to his chair; ‘and this is the end of my prosing about myself, I get on as well as I can. I don’t make much, but I don’t spend much. In general, I board with the people downstairs, who are very agreeable people indeed. Both Mr. and Mrs. Micawber have seen a good deal of life, and are excellent company.’\n\n‘My dear Traddles!’ I quickly exclaimed. ‘What are you talking about?’\n\nTraddles looked at me, as if he wondered what I was talking about.\n\n‘Mr. and Mrs. Micawber!’ I repeated. ‘Why, I am intimately acquainted with them!’\n\nAn opportune double knock at the door, which I knew well from old experience in Windsor Terrace, and which nobody but Mr. Micawber could ever have knocked at that door, resolved any doubt in my mind as to their being my old friends. I begged Traddles to ask his landlord to walk up. Traddles accordingly did so, over the banister; and Mr. Micawber, not a bit changed--his tights, his stick, his shirt-collar, and his eye-glass, all the same as ever--came into the room with a genteel and youthful air.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Mr. Traddles,’ said Mr. Micawber, with the old roll in his voice, as he checked himself in humming a soft tune. ‘I was not aware that there was any individual, alien to this tenement, in your sanctum.’\n\nMr. Micawber slightly bowed to me, and pulled up his shirt-collar.\n\n‘How do you do, Mr. Micawber?’ said I.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘you are exceedingly obliging. I am in statu quo.’\n\n‘And Mrs. Micawber?’ I pursued.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘she is also, thank God, in statu quo.’\n\n‘And the children, Mr. Micawber?’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘I rejoice to reply that they are, likewise, in the enjoyment of salubrity.’\n\nAll this time, Mr. Micawber had not known me in the least, though he had stood face to face with me. But now, seeing me smile, he examined my features with more attention, fell back, cried, ‘Is it possible! Have I the pleasure of again beholding Copperfield!’ and shook me by both hands with the utmost fervour.\n\n‘Good Heaven, Mr. Traddles!’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘to think that I should find you acquainted with the friend of my youth, the companion of earlier days! My dear!’ calling over the banisters to Mrs. Micawber, while Traddles looked (with reason) not a little amazed at this description of me. ‘Here is a gentleman in Mr. Traddles’s apartment, whom he wishes to have the pleasure of presenting to you, my love!’\n\nMr. Micawber immediately reappeared, and shook hands with me again.\n\n‘And how is our good friend the Doctor, Copperfield?’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘and all the circle at Canterbury?’\n\n‘I have none but good accounts of them,’ said I.\n\n‘I am most delighted to hear it,’ said Mr. Micawber. ‘It was at Canterbury where we last met. Within the shadow, I may figuratively say, of that religious edifice immortalized by Chaucer, which was anciently the resort of Pilgrims from the remotest corners of--in short,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘in the immediate neighbourhood of the Cathedral.’\n\nI replied that it was. Mr. Micawber continued talking as volubly as he could; but not, I thought, without showing, by some marks of concern in his countenance, that he was sensible of sounds in the next room, as of Mrs. Micawber washing her hands, and hurriedly opening and shutting drawers that were uneasy in their action.\n\n‘You find us, Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, with one eye on Traddles, ‘at present established, on what may be designated as a small and unassuming scale; but, you are aware that I have, in the course of my career, surmounted difficulties, and conquered obstacles. You are no stranger to the fact, that there have been periods of my life, when it has been requisite that I should pause, until certain expected events should turn up; when it has been necessary that I should fall back, before making what I trust I shall not be accused of presumption in terming--a spring. The present is one of those momentous stages in the life of man. You find me, fallen back, FOR a spring; and I have every reason to believe that a vigorous leap will shortly be the result.’\n\nI was expressing my satisfaction, when Mrs. Micawber came in; a little more slatternly than she used to be, or so she seemed now, to my unaccustomed eyes, but still with some preparation of herself for company, and with a pair of brown gloves on.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Micawber, leading her towards me, ‘here is a gentleman of the name of Copperfield, who wishes to renew his acquaintance with you.’\n\nIt would have been better, as it turned out, to have led gently up to this announcement, for Mrs. Micawber, being in a delicate state of health, was overcome by it, and was taken so unwell, that Mr. Micawber was obliged, in great trepidation, to run down to the water-butt in the backyard, and draw a basinful to lave her brow with. She presently revived, however, and was really pleased to see me. We had half-an-hour’s talk, all together; and I asked her about the twins, who, she said, were ‘grown great creatures’; and after Master and Miss Micawber, whom she described as ‘absolute giants’, but they were not produced on that occasion.\n\nMr. Micawber was very anxious that I should stay to dinner. I should not have been averse to do so, but that I imagined I detected trouble, and calculation relative to the extent of the cold meat, in Mrs. Micawber’s eye. I therefore pleaded another engagement; and observing that Mrs. Micawber’s spirits were immediately lightened, I resisted all persuasion to forego it.\n\nBut I told Traddles, and Mr. and Mrs. Micawber, that before I could think of leaving, they must appoint a day when they would come and dine with me. The occupations to which Traddles stood pledged, rendered it necessary to fix a somewhat distant one; but an appointment was made for the purpose, that suited us all, and then I took my leave.\n\nMr. Micawber, under pretence of showing me a nearer way than that by which I had come, accompanied me to the corner of the street; being anxious (he explained to me) to say a few words to an old friend, in confidence.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘I need hardly tell you that to have beneath our roof, under existing circumstances, a mind like that which gleams--if I may be allowed the expression--which gleams--in your friend Traddles, is an unspeakable comfort. With a washerwoman, who exposes hard-bake for sale in her parlour-window, dwelling next door, and a Bow-street officer residing over the way, you may imagine that his society is a source of consolation to myself and to Mrs. Micawber. I am at present, my dear Copperfield, engaged in the sale of corn upon commission. It is not an avocation of a remunerative description--in other words, it does not pay--and some temporary embarrassments of a pecuniary nature have been the consequence. I am, however, delighted to add that I have now an immediate prospect of something turning up (I am not at liberty to say in what direction), which I trust will enable me to provide, permanently, both for myself and for your friend Traddles, in whom I have an unaffected interest. You may, perhaps, be prepared to hear that Mrs. Micawber is in a state of health which renders it not wholly improbable that an addition may be ultimately made to those pledges of affection which--in short, to the infantine group. Mrs. Micawber’s family have been so good as to express their dissatisfaction at this state of things. I have merely to observe, that I am not aware that it is any business of theirs, and that I repel that exhibition of feeling with scorn, and with defiance!’\n\nMr. Micawber then shook hands with me again, and left me.\n\n# CHAPTER 28. Mr. MICAWBER’S GAUNTLET\n\nUntil the day arrived on which I was to entertain my newly-found old friends, I lived principally on Dora and coffee. In my love-lorn condition, my appetite languished; and I was glad of it, for I felt as though it would have been an act of perfidy towards Dora to have a natural relish for my dinner. The quantity of walking exercise I took, was not in this respect attended with its usual consequence, as the disappointment counteracted the fresh air. I have my doubts, too, founded on the acute experience acquired at this period of my life, whether a sound enjoyment of animal food can develop itself freely in any human subject who is always in torment from tight boots. I think the extremities require to be at peace before the stomach will conduct itself with vigour.\n\nOn the occasion of this domestic little party, I did not repeat my former extensive preparations. I merely provided a pair of soles, a small leg of mutton, and a pigeon-pie. Mrs. Crupp broke out into rebellion on my first bashful hint in reference to the cooking of the fish and joint, and said, with a dignified sense of injury, ‘No! No, sir! You will not ask me sich a thing, for you are better acquainted with me than to suppose me capable of doing what I cannot do with ampial satisfaction to my own feelings!’ But, in the end, a compromise was effected; and Mrs. Crupp consented to achieve this feat, on condition that I dined from home for a fortnight afterwards.\n\nAnd here I may remark, that what I underwent from Mrs. Crupp, in consequence of the tyranny she established over me, was dreadful. I never was so much afraid of anyone. We made a compromise of everything. If I hesitated, she was taken with that wonderful disorder which was always lying in ambush in her system, ready, at the shortest notice, to prey upon her vitals. If I rang the bell impatiently, after half-a-dozen unavailing modest pulls, and she appeared at last--which was not by any means to be relied upon--she would appear with a reproachful aspect, sink breathless on a chair near the door, lay her hand upon her nankeen bosom, and become so ill, that I was glad, at any sacrifice of brandy or anything else, to get rid of her. If I objected to having my bed made at five o’clock in the afternoon--which I do still think an uncomfortable arrangement--one motion of her hand towards the same nankeen region of wounded sensibility was enough to make me falter an apology. In short, I would have done anything in an honourable way rather than give Mrs. Crupp offence; and she was the terror of my life.\n\nI bought a second-hand dumb-waiter for this dinner-party, in preference to re-engaging the handy young man; against whom I had conceived a prejudice, in consequence of meeting him in the Strand, one Sunday morning, in a waistcoat remarkably like one of mine, which had been missing since the former occasion. The ‘young gal’ was re-engaged; but on the stipulation that she should only bring in the dishes, and then withdraw to the landing-place, beyond the outer door; where a habit of sniffing she had contracted would be lost upon the guests, and where her retiring on the plates would be a physical impossibility.\n\nHaving laid in the materials for a bowl of punch, to be compounded by Mr. Micawber; having provided a bottle of lavender-water, two wax-candles, a paper of mixed pins, and a pincushion, to assist Mrs. Micawber in her toilette at my dressing-table; having also caused the fire in my bedroom to be lighted for Mrs. Micawber’s convenience; and having laid the cloth with my own hands, I awaited the result with composure.\n\nAt the appointed time, my three visitors arrived together. Mr. Micawber with more shirt-collar than usual, and a new ribbon to his eye-glass; Mrs. Micawber with her cap in a whitey-brown paper parcel; Traddles carrying the parcel, and supporting Mrs. Micawber on his arm. They were all delighted with my residence. When I conducted Mrs. Micawber to my dressing-table, and she saw the scale on which it was prepared for her, she was in such raptures, that she called Mr. Micawber to come in and look.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘this is luxurious. This is a way of life which reminds me of the period when I was myself in a state of celibacy, and Mrs. Micawber had not yet been solicited to plight her faith at the Hymeneal altar.’\n\n‘He means, solicited by him, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, archly. ‘He cannot answer for others.’\n\n‘My dear,’ returned Mr. Micawber with sudden seriousness, ‘I have no desire to answer for others. I am too well aware that when, in the inscrutable decrees of Fate, you were reserved for me, it is possible you may have been reserved for one, destined, after a protracted struggle, at length to fall a victim to pecuniary involvements of a complicated nature. I understand your allusion, my love. I regret it, but I can bear it.’\n\n‘Micawber!’ exclaimed Mrs. Micawber, in tears. ‘Have I deserved this! I, who never have deserted you; who never WILL desert you, Micawber!’ ‘My love,’ said Mr. Micawber, much affected, ‘you will forgive, and our old and tried friend Copperfield will, I am sure, forgive, the momentary laceration of a wounded spirit, made sensitive by a recent collision with the Minion of Power--in other words, with a ribald Turncock attached to the water-works--and will pity, not condemn, its excesses.’\n\nMr. Micawber then embraced Mrs. Micawber, and pressed my hand; leaving me to infer from this broken allusion that his domestic supply of water had been cut off that afternoon, in consequence of default in the payment of the company’s rates.\n\nTo divert his thoughts from this melancholy subject, I informed Mr. Micawber that I relied upon him for a bowl of punch, and led him to the lemons. His recent despondency, not to say despair, was gone in a moment. I never saw a man so thoroughly enjoy himself amid the fragrance of lemon-peel and sugar, the odour of burning rum, and the steam of boiling water, as Mr. Micawber did that afternoon. It was wonderful to see his face shining at us out of a thin cloud of these delicate fumes, as he stirred, and mixed, and tasted, and looked as if he were making, instead of punch, a fortune for his family down to the latest posterity. As to Mrs. Micawber, I don’t know whether it was the effect of the cap, or the lavender-water, or the pins, or the fire, or the wax-candles, but she came out of my room, comparatively speaking, lovely. And the lark was never gayer than that excellent woman.\n\nI suppose--I never ventured to inquire, but I suppose--that Mrs. Crupp, after frying the soles, was taken ill. Because we broke down at that point. The leg of mutton came up very red within, and very pale without: besides having a foreign substance of a gritty nature sprinkled over it, as if if had had a fall into the ashes of that remarkable kitchen fireplace. But we were not in condition to judge of this fact from the appearance of the gravy, forasmuch as the ‘young gal’ had dropped it all upon the stairs--where it remained, by the by, in a long train, until it was worn out. The pigeon-pie was not bad, but it was a delusive pie: the crust being like a disappointing head, phrenologically speaking: full of lumps and bumps, with nothing particular underneath. In short, the banquet was such a failure that I should have been quite unhappy--about the failure, I mean, for I was always unhappy about Dora--if I had not been relieved by the great good humour of my company, and by a bright suggestion from Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘My dear friend Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘accidents will occur in the best-regulated families; and in families not regulated by that pervading influence which sanctifies while it enhances the--a--I would say, in short, by the influence of Woman, in the lofty character of Wife, they may be expected with confidence, and must be borne with philosophy. If you will allow me to take the liberty of remarking that there are few comestibles better, in their way, than a Devil, and that I believe, with a little division of labour, we could accomplish a good one if the young person in attendance could produce a gridiron, I would put it to you, that this little misfortune may be easily repaired.’\n\nThere was a gridiron in the pantry, on which my morning rasher of bacon was cooked. We had it in, in a twinkling, and immediately applied ourselves to carrying Mr. Micawber’s idea into effect. The division of labour to which he had referred was this:--Traddles cut the mutton into slices; Mr. Micawber (who could do anything of this sort to perfection) covered them with pepper, mustard, salt, and cayenne; I put them on the gridiron, turned them with a fork, and took them off, under Mr. Micawber’s direction; and Mrs. Micawber heated, and continually stirred, some mushroom ketchup in a little saucepan. When we had slices enough done to begin upon, we fell-to, with our sleeves still tucked up at the wrist, more slices sputtering and blazing on the fire, and our attention divided between the mutton on our plates, and the mutton then preparing.\n\nWhat with the novelty of this cookery, the excellence of it, the bustle of it, the frequent starting up to look after it, the frequent sitting down to dispose of it as the crisp slices came off the gridiron hot and hot, the being so busy, so flushed with the fire, so amused, and in the midst of such a tempting noise and savour, we reduced the leg of mutton to the bone. My own appetite came back miraculously. I am ashamed to record it, but I really believe I forgot Dora for a little while. I am satisfied that Mr. and Mrs. Micawber could not have enjoyed the feast more, if they had sold a bed to provide it. Traddles laughed as heartily, almost the whole time, as he ate and worked. Indeed we all did, all at once; and I dare say there was never a greater success.\n\nWe were at the height of our enjoyment, and were all busily engaged, in our several departments, endeavouring to bring the last batch of slices to a state of perfection that should crown the feast, when I was aware of a strange presence in the room, and my eyes encountered those of the staid Littimer, standing hat in hand before me.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ I involuntarily asked.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir, I was directed to come in. Is my master not here, sir?’\n‘No.’\n\n‘Have you not seen him, sir?’\n\n‘No; don’t you come from him?’\n\n‘Not immediately so, sir.’\n\n‘Did he tell you you would find him here?’\n\n‘Not exactly so, sir. But I should think he might be here tomorrow, as he has not been here today.’\n\n‘Is he coming up from Oxford?’\n\n‘I beg, sir,’ he returned respectfully, ‘that you will be seated, and allow me to do this.’ With which he took the fork from my unresisting hand, and bent over the gridiron, as if his whole attention were concentrated on it.\n\nWe should not have been much discomposed, I dare say, by the appearance of Steerforth himself, but we became in a moment the meekest of the meek before his respectable serving-man. Mr. Micawber, humming a tune, to show that he was quite at ease, subsided into his chair, with the handle of a hastily concealed fork sticking out of the bosom of his coat, as if he had stabbed himself. Mrs. Micawber put on her brown gloves, and assumed a genteel languor. Traddles ran his greasy hands through his hair, and stood it bolt upright, and stared in confusion on the table-cloth. As for me, I was a mere infant at the head of my own table; and hardly ventured to glance at the respectable phenomenon, who had come from Heaven knows where, to put my establishment to rights.\n\nMeanwhile he took the mutton off the gridiron, and gravely handed it round. We all took some, but our appreciation of it was gone, and we merely made a show of eating it. As we severally pushed away our plates, he noiselessly removed them, and set on the cheese. He took that off, too, when it was done with; cleared the table; piled everything on the dumb-waiter; gave us our wine-glasses; and, of his own accord, wheeled the dumb-waiter into the pantry. All this was done in a perfect manner, and he never raised his eyes from what he was about. Yet his very elbows, when he had his back towards me, seemed to teem with the expression of his fixed opinion that I was extremely young.\n\n‘Can I do anything more, sir?’\n\nI thanked him and said, No; but would he take no dinner himself?\n\n‘None, I am obliged to you, sir.’\n\n‘Is Mr. Steerforth coming from Oxford?’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir?’\n\n‘Is Mr. Steerforth coming from Oxford?’\n\n‘I should imagine that he might be here tomorrow, sir. I rather thought he might have been here today, sir. The mistake is mine, no doubt, sir.’\n\n‘If you should see him first--’ said I.\n\n‘If you’ll excuse me, sir, I don’t think I shall see him first.’\n\n‘In case you do,’ said I, ‘pray say that I am sorry he was not here today, as an old schoolfellow of his was here.’\n\n‘Indeed, sir!’ and he divided a bow between me and Traddles, with a glance at the latter.\n\nHe was moving softly to the door, when, in a forlorn hope of saying something naturally--which I never could, to this man--I said:\n\n‘Oh! Littimer!’\n‘Sir!’\n\n‘Did you remain long at Yarmouth, that time?’\n\n‘Not particularly so, sir.’\n\n‘You saw the boat completed?’\n\n‘Yes, sir. I remained behind on purpose to see the boat completed.’\n\n‘I know!’ He raised his eyes to mine respectfully.\n\n‘Mr. Steerforth has not seen it yet, I suppose?’\n\n‘I really can’t say, sir. I think--but I really can’t say, sir. I wish you good night, sir.’\n\nHe comprehended everybody present, in the respectful bow with which he followed these words, and disappeared. My visitors seemed to breathe more freely when he was gone; but my own relief was very great, for besides the constraint, arising from that extraordinary sense of being at a disadvantage which I always had in this man’s presence, my conscience had embarrassed me with whispers that I had mistrusted his master, and I could not repress a vague uneasy dread that he might find it out. How was it, having so little in reality to conceal, that I always DID feel as if this man were finding me out?\n\nMr. Micawber roused me from this reflection, which was blended with a certain remorseful apprehension of seeing Steerforth himself, by bestowing many encomiums on the absent Littimer as a most respectable fellow, and a thoroughly admirable servant. Mr. Micawber, I may remark, had taken his full share of the general bow, and had received it with infinite condescension.\n\n‘But punch, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, tasting it, ‘like time and tide, waits for no man. Ah! it is at the present moment in high flavour. My love, will you give me your opinion?’\n\nMrs. Micawber pronounced it excellent.\n\n‘Then I will drink,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘if my friend Copperfield will permit me to take that social liberty, to the days when my friend Copperfield and myself were younger, and fought our way in the world side by side. I may say, of myself and Copperfield, in words we have sung together before now, that\n\nWe twa hae run about the braes And pu’d the gowans’ fine\n\n--in a figurative point of view--on several occasions. I am not exactly aware,’ said Mr. Micawber, with the old roll in his voice, and the old indescribable air of saying something genteel, ‘what gowans may be, but I have no doubt that Copperfield and myself would frequently have taken a pull at them, if it had been feasible.’\n\nMr. Micawber, at the then present moment, took a pull at his punch. So we all did: Traddles evidently lost in wondering at what distant time Mr. Micawber and I could have been comrades in the battle of the world.\n\n‘Ahem!’ said Mr. Micawber, clearing his throat, and warming with the punch and with the fire. ‘My dear, another glass?’\n\nMrs. Micawber said it must be very little; but we couldn’t allow that, so it was a glassful.\n\n‘As we are quite confidential here, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, sipping her punch, ‘Mr. Traddles being a part of our domesticity, I should much like to have your opinion on Mr. Micawber’s prospects. For corn,’ said Mrs. Micawber argumentatively, ‘as I have repeatedly said to Mr. Micawber, may be gentlemanly, but it is not remunerative. Commission to the extent of two and ninepence in a fortnight cannot, however limited our ideas, be considered remunerative.’\n\nWe were all agreed upon that.\n\n‘Then,’ said Mrs. Micawber, who prided herself on taking a clear view of things, and keeping Mr. Micawber straight by her woman’s wisdom, when he might otherwise go a little crooked, ‘then I ask myself this question. If corn is not to be relied upon, what is? Are coals to be relied upon? Not at all. We have turned our attention to that experiment, on the suggestion of my family, and we find it fallacious.’\n\nMr. Micawber, leaning back in his chair with his hands in his pockets, eyed us aside, and nodded his head, as much as to say that the case was very clearly put.\n\n‘The articles of corn and coals,’ said Mrs. Micawber, still more argumentatively, ‘being equally out of the question, Mr. Copperfield, I naturally look round the world, and say, “What is there in which a person of Mr. Micawber’s talent is likely to succeed?” And I exclude the doing anything on commission, because commission is not a certainty. What is best suited to a person of Mr. Micawber’s peculiar temperament is, I am convinced, a certainty.’\n\nTraddles and I both expressed, by a feeling murmur, that this great discovery was no doubt true of Mr. Micawber, and that it did him much credit.\n\n‘I will not conceal from you, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘that I have long felt the Brewing business to be particularly adapted to Mr. Micawber. Look at Barclay and Perkins! Look at Truman, Hanbury, and Buxton! It is on that extensive footing that Mr. Micawber, I know from my own knowledge of him, is calculated to shine; and the profits, I am told, are e-NOR-MOUS! But if Mr. Micawber cannot get into those firms--which decline to answer his letters, when he offers his services even in an inferior capacity--what is the use of dwelling upon that idea? None. I may have a conviction that Mr. Micawber’s manners--’\n\n‘Hem! Really, my dear,’ interposed Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘My love, be silent,’ said Mrs. Micawber, laying her brown glove on his hand. ‘I may have a conviction, Mr. Copperfield, that Mr. Micawber’s manners peculiarly qualify him for the Banking business. I may argue within myself, that if I had a deposit at a banking-house, the manners of Mr. Micawber, as representing that banking-house, would inspire confidence, and must extend the connexion. But if the various banking-houses refuse to avail themselves of Mr. Micawber’s abilities, or receive the offer of them with contumely, what is the use of dwelling upon THAT idea? None. As to originating a banking-business, I may know that there are members of my family who, if they chose to place their money in Mr. Micawber’s hands, might found an establishment of that description. But if they do NOT choose to place their money in Mr. Micawber’s hands--which they don’t--what is the use of that? Again I contend that we are no farther advanced than we were before.’\n\nI shook my head, and said, ‘Not a bit.’ Traddles also shook his head, and said, ‘Not a bit.’\n\n‘What do I deduce from this?’ Mrs. Micawber went on to say, still with the same air of putting a case lucidly. ‘What is the conclusion, my dear Mr. Copperfield, to which I am irresistibly brought? Am I wrong in saying, it is clear that we must live?’\n\nI answered ‘Not at all!’ and Traddles answered ‘Not at all!’ and I found myself afterwards sagely adding, alone, that a person must either live or die.\n\n‘Just so,’ returned Mrs. Micawber, ‘It is precisely that. And the fact is, my dear Mr. Copperfield, that we can not live without something widely different from existing circumstances shortly turning up. Now I am convinced, myself, and this I have pointed out to Mr. Micawber several times of late, that things cannot be expected to turn up of themselves. We must, in a measure, assist to turn them up. I may be wrong, but I have formed that opinion.’\n\nBoth Traddles and I applauded it highly.\n\n‘Very well,’ said Mrs. Micawber. ‘Then what do I recommend? Here is Mr. Micawber with a variety of qualifications--with great talent--’\n\n‘Really, my love,’ said Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘Pray, my dear, allow me to conclude. Here is Mr. Micawber, with a variety of qualifications, with great talent--I should say, with genius, but that may be the partiality of a wife--’\n\nTraddles and I both murmured ‘No.’\n\n‘And here is Mr. Micawber without any suitable position or employment. Where does that responsibility rest? Clearly on society. Then I would make a fact so disgraceful known, and boldly challenge society to set it right. It appears to me, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, forcibly, ‘that what Mr. Micawber has to do, is to throw down the gauntlet to society, and say, in effect, “Show me who will take that up. Let the party immediately step forward.”’\n\nI ventured to ask Mrs. Micawber how this was to be done.\n\n‘By advertising,’ said Mrs. Micawber--‘in all the papers. It appears to me, that what Mr. Micawber has to do, in justice to himself, in justice to his family, and I will even go so far as to say in justice to society, by which he has been hitherto overlooked, is to advertise in all the papers; to describe himself plainly as so-and-so, with such and such qualifications and to put it thus: “Now employ me, on remunerative terms, and address, post-paid, to W. M., Post Office, Camden Town.”’\n\n‘This idea of Mrs. Micawber’s, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, making his shirt-collar meet in front of his chin, and glancing at me sideways, ‘is, in fact, the Leap to which I alluded, when I last had the pleasure of seeing you.’\n\n‘Advertising is rather expensive,’ I remarked, dubiously.\n\n‘Exactly so!’ said Mrs. Micawber, preserving the same logical air. ‘Quite true, my dear Mr. Copperfield! I have made the identical observation to Mr. Micawber. It is for that reason especially, that I think Mr. Micawber ought (as I have already said, in justice to himself, in justice to his family, and in justice to society) to raise a certain sum of money--on a bill.’\n\nMr. Micawber, leaning back in his chair, trifled with his eye-glass and cast his eyes up at the ceiling; but I thought him observant of Traddles, too, who was looking at the fire.\n\n‘If no member of my family,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘is possessed of sufficient natural feeling to negotiate that bill--I believe there is a better business-term to express what I mean--’\n\nMr. Micawber, with his eyes still cast up at the ceiling, suggested ‘Discount.’\n\n‘To discount that bill,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘then my opinion is, that Mr. Micawber should go into the City, should take that bill into the Money Market, and should dispose of it for what he can get. If the individuals in the Money Market oblige Mr. Micawber to sustain a great sacrifice, that is between themselves and their consciences. I view it, steadily, as an investment. I recommend Mr. Micawber, my dear Mr. Copperfield, to do the same; to regard it as an investment which is sure of return, and to make up his mind to any sacrifice.’\n\nI felt, but I am sure I don’t know why, that this was self-denying and devoted in Mrs. Micawber, and I uttered a murmur to that effect. Traddles, who took his tone from me, did likewise, still looking at the fire.\n\n‘I will not,’ said Mrs. Micawber, finishing her punch, and gathering her scarf about her shoulders, preparatory to her withdrawal to my bedroom: ‘I will not protract these remarks on the subject of Mr. Micawber’s pecuniary affairs. At your fireside, my dear Mr. Copperfield, and in the presence of Mr. Traddles, who, though not so old a friend, is quite one of ourselves, I could not refrain from making you acquainted with the course I advise Mr. Micawber to take. I feel that the time is arrived when Mr. Micawber should exert himself and--I will add--assert himself, and it appears to me that these are the means. I am aware that I am merely a female, and that a masculine judgement is usually considered more competent to the discussion of such questions; still I must not forget that, when I lived at home with my papa and mama, my papa was in the habit of saying, “Emma’s form is fragile, but her grasp of a subject is inferior to none.” That my papa was too partial, I well know; but that he was an observer of character in some degree, my duty and my reason equally forbid me to doubt.’\n\nWith these words, and resisting our entreaties that she would grace the remaining circulation of the punch with her presence, Mrs. Micawber retired to my bedroom. And really I felt that she was a noble woman--the sort of woman who might have been a Roman matron, and done all manner of heroic things, in times of public trouble.\n\nIn the fervour of this impression, I congratulated Mr. Micawber on the treasure he possessed. So did Traddles. Mr. Micawber extended his hand to each of us in succession, and then covered his face with his pocket-handkerchief, which I think had more snuff upon it than he was aware of. He then returned to the punch, in the highest state of exhilaration.\n\nHe was full of eloquence. He gave us to understand that in our children we lived again, and that, under the pressure of pecuniary difficulties, any accession to their number was doubly welcome. He said that Mrs. Micawber had latterly had her doubts on this point, but that he had dispelled them, and reassured her. As to her family, they were totally unworthy of her, and their sentiments were utterly indifferent to him, and they might--I quote his own expression--go to the Devil.\n\nMr. Micawber then delivered a warm eulogy on Traddles. He said Traddles’s was a character, to the steady virtues of which he (Mr. Micawber) could lay no claim, but which, he thanked Heaven, he could admire. He feelingly alluded to the young lady, unknown, whom Traddles had honoured with his affection, and who had reciprocated that affection by honouring and blessing Traddles with her affection. Mr. Micawber pledged her. So did I. Traddles thanked us both, by saying, with a simplicity and honesty I had sense enough to be quite charmed with, ‘I am very much obliged to you indeed. And I do assure you, she’s the dearest girl!--’\n\nMr. Micawber took an early opportunity, after that, of hinting, with the utmost delicacy and ceremony, at the state of my affections. Nothing but the serious assurance of his friend Copperfield to the contrary, he observed, could deprive him of the impression that his friend Copperfield loved and was beloved. After feeling very hot and uncomfortable for some time, and after a good deal of blushing, stammering, and denying, I said, having my glass in my hand, ‘Well! I would give them D.!’ which so excited and gratified Mr. Micawber, that he ran with a glass of punch into my bedroom, in order that Mrs. Micawber might drink D., who drank it with enthusiasm, crying from within, in a shrill voice, ‘Hear, hear! My dear Mr. Copperfield, I am delighted. Hear!’ and tapping at the wall, by way of applause.\n\nOur conversation, afterwards, took a more worldly turn; Mr. Micawber telling us that he found Camden Town inconvenient, and that the first thing he contemplated doing, when the advertisement should have been the cause of something satisfactory turning up, was to move. He mentioned a terrace at the western end of Oxford Street, fronting Hyde Park, on which he had always had his eye, but which he did not expect to attain immediately, as it would require a large establishment. There would probably be an interval, he explained, in which he should content himself with the upper part of a house, over some respectable place of business--say in Piccadilly,--which would be a cheerful situation for Mrs. Micawber; and where, by throwing out a bow-window, or carrying up the roof another story, or making some little alteration of that sort, they might live, comfortably and reputably, for a few years. Whatever was reserved for him, he expressly said, or wherever his abode might be, we might rely on this--there would always be a room for Traddles, and a knife and fork for me. We acknowledged his kindness; and he begged us to forgive his having launched into these practical and business-like details, and to excuse it as natural in one who was making entirely new arrangements in life.\n\nMrs. Micawber, tapping at the wall again to know if tea were ready, broke up this particular phase of our friendly conversation. She made tea for us in a most agreeable manner; and, whenever I went near her, in handing about the tea-cups and bread-and-butter, asked me, in a whisper, whether D. was fair, or dark, or whether she was short, or tall: or something of that kind; which I think I liked. After tea, we discussed a variety of topics before the fire; and Mrs. Micawber was good enough to sing us (in a small, thin, flat voice, which I remembered to have considered, when I first knew her, the very table-beer of acoustics) the favourite ballads of ‘The Dashing White Sergeant’, and ‘Little Tafflin’. For both of these songs Mrs. Micawber had been famous when she lived at home with her papa and mama. Mr. Micawber told us, that when he heard her sing the first one, on the first occasion of his seeing her beneath the parental roof, she had attracted his attention in an extraordinary degree; but that when it came to Little Tafflin, he had resolved to win that woman or perish in the attempt.\n\nIt was between ten and eleven o’clock when Mrs. Micawber rose to replace her cap in the whitey-brown paper parcel, and to put on her bonnet. Mr. Micawber took the opportunity of Traddles putting on his great-coat, to slip a letter into my hand, with a whispered request that I would read it at my leisure. I also took the opportunity of my holding a candle over the banisters to light them down, when Mr. Micawber was going first, leading Mrs. Micawber, and Traddles was following with the cap, to detain Traddles for a moment on the top of the stairs.\n\n‘Traddles,’ said I, ‘Mr. Micawber don’t mean any harm, poor fellow: but, if I were you, I wouldn’t lend him anything.’\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ returned Traddles, smiling, ‘I haven’t got anything to lend.’\n\n‘You have got a name, you know,’ said I.\n\n‘Oh! You call THAT something to lend?’ returned Traddles, with a thoughtful look.\n\n‘Certainly.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Traddles. ‘Yes, to be sure! I am very much obliged to you, Copperfield; but--I am afraid I have lent him that already.’\n\n‘For the bill that is to be a certain investment?’ I inquired.\n\n‘No,’ said Traddles. ‘Not for that one. This is the first I have heard of that one. I have been thinking that he will most likely propose that one, on the way home. Mine’s another.’\n\n‘I hope there will be nothing wrong about it,’ said I. ‘I hope not,’ said Traddles. ‘I should think not, though, because he told me, only the other day, that it was provided for. That was Mr. Micawber’s expression, “Provided for.”’\n\nMr. Micawber looking up at this juncture to where we were standing, I had only time to repeat my caution. Traddles thanked me, and descended. But I was much afraid, when I observed the good-natured manner in which he went down with the cap in his hand, and gave Mrs. Micawber his arm, that he would be carried into the Money Market neck and heels.\n\nI returned to my fireside, and was musing, half gravely and half laughing, on the character of Mr. Micawber and the old relations between us, when I heard a quick step ascending the stairs. At first, I thought it was Traddles coming back for something Mrs. Micawber had left behind; but as the step approached, I knew it, and felt my heart beat high, and the blood rush to my face, for it was Steerforth’s.\n\nI was never unmindful of Agnes, and she never left that sanctuary in my thoughts--if I may call it so--where I had placed her from the first. But when he entered, and stood before me with his hand out, the darkness that had fallen on him changed to light, and I felt confounded and ashamed of having doubted one I loved so heartily. I loved her none the less; I thought of her as the same benignant, gentle angel in my life; I reproached myself, not her, with having done him an injury; and I would have made him any atonement if I had known what to make, and how to make it.\n\n‘Why, Daisy, old boy, dumb-foundered!’ laughed Steerforth, shaking my hand heartily, and throwing it gaily away. ‘Have I detected you in another feast, you Sybarite! These Doctors’ Commons fellows are the gayest men in town, I believe, and beat us sober Oxford people all to nothing!’ His bright glance went merrily round the room, as he took the seat on the sofa opposite to me, which Mrs. Micawber had recently vacated, and stirred the fire into a blaze.\n\n‘I was so surprised at first,’ said I, giving him welcome with all the cordiality I felt, ‘that I had hardly breath to greet you with, Steerforth.’\n\n‘Well, the sight of me is good for sore eyes, as the Scotch say,’ replied Steerforth, ‘and so is the sight of you, Daisy, in full bloom. How are you, my Bacchanal?’\n\n‘I am very well,’ said I; ‘and not at all Bacchanalian tonight, though I confess to another party of three.’\n\n‘All of whom I met in the street, talking loud in your praise,’ returned Steerforth. ‘Who’s our friend in the tights?’\n\nI gave him the best idea I could, in a few words, of Mr. Micawber. He laughed heartily at my feeble portrait of that gentleman, and said he was a man to know, and he must know him. ‘But who do you suppose our other friend is?’ said I, in my turn.\n\n‘Heaven knows,’ said Steerforth. ‘Not a bore, I hope? I thought he looked a little like one.’\n\n‘Traddles!’ I replied, triumphantly.\n\n‘Who’s he?’ asked Steerforth, in his careless way.\n\n‘Don’t you remember Traddles? Traddles in our room at Salem House?’\n\n‘Oh! That fellow!’ said Steerforth, beating a lump of coal on the top of the fire, with the poker. ‘Is he as soft as ever? And where the deuce did you pick him up?’\n\nI extolled Traddles in reply, as highly as I could; for I felt that Steerforth rather slighted him. Steerforth, dismissing the subject with a light nod, and a smile, and the remark that he would be glad to see the old fellow too, for he had always been an odd fish, inquired if I could give him anything to eat? During most of this short dialogue, when he had not been speaking in a wild vivacious manner, he had sat idly beating on the lump of coal with the poker. I observed that he did the same thing while I was getting out the remains of the pigeon-pie, and so forth.\n\n‘Why, Daisy, here’s a supper for a king!’ he exclaimed, starting out of his silence with a burst, and taking his seat at the table. ‘I shall do it justice, for I have come from Yarmouth.’\n\n‘I thought you came from Oxford?’ I returned.\n\n‘Not I,’ said Steerforth. ‘I have been seafaring--better employed.’\n\n‘Littimer was here today, to inquire for you,’ I remarked, ‘and I understood him that you were at Oxford; though, now I think of it, he certainly did not say so.’\n\n‘Littimer is a greater fool than I thought him, to have been inquiring for me at all,’ said Steerforth, jovially pouring out a glass of wine, and drinking to me. ‘As to understanding him, you are a cleverer fellow than most of us, Daisy, if you can do that.’\n\n‘That’s true, indeed,’ said I, moving my chair to the table. ‘So you have been at Yarmouth, Steerforth!’ interested to know all about it. ‘Have you been there long?’\n\n‘No,’ he returned. ‘An escapade of a week or so.’\n\n‘And how are they all? Of course, little Emily is not married yet?’\n\n‘Not yet. Going to be, I believe--in so many weeks, or months, or something or other. I have not seen much of ‘em. By the by’; he laid down his knife and fork, which he had been using with great diligence, and began feeling in his pockets; ‘I have a letter for you.’\n\n‘From whom?’\n\n‘Why, from your old nurse,’ he returned, taking some papers out of his breast pocket. “‘J. Steerforth, Esquire, debtor, to The Willing Mind”; that’s not it. Patience, and we’ll find it presently. Old what’s-his-name’s in a bad way, and it’s about that, I believe.’\n\n‘Barkis, do you mean?’\n\n‘Yes!’ still feeling in his pockets, and looking over their contents: ‘it’s all over with poor Barkis, I am afraid. I saw a little apothecary there--surgeon, or whatever he is--who brought your worship into the world. He was mighty learned about the case, to me; but the upshot of his opinion was, that the carrier was making his last journey rather fast.---Put your hand into the breast pocket of my great-coat on the chair yonder, and I think you’ll find the letter. Is it there?’\n\n‘Here it is!’ said I.\n\n‘That’s right!’\n\nIt was from Peggotty; something less legible than usual, and brief. It informed me of her husband’s hopeless state, and hinted at his being ‘a little nearer’ than heretofore, and consequently more difficult to manage for his own comfort. It said nothing of her weariness and watching, and praised him highly. It was written with a plain, unaffected, homely piety that I knew to be genuine, and ended with ‘my duty to my ever darling’--meaning myself.\n\nWhile I deciphered it, Steerforth continued to eat and drink.\n\n‘It’s a bad job,’ he said, when I had done; ‘but the sun sets every day, and people die every minute, and we mustn’t be scared by the common lot. If we failed to hold our own, because that equal foot at all men’s doors was heard knocking somewhere, every object in this world would slip from us. No! Ride on! Rough-shod if need be, smooth-shod if that will do, but ride on! Ride on over all obstacles, and win the race!’\n\n‘And win what race?’ said I.\n\n‘The race that one has started in,’ said he. ‘Ride on!’\n\nI noticed, I remember, as he paused, looking at me with his handsome head a little thrown back, and his glass raised in his hand, that, though the freshness of the sea-wind was on his face, and it was ruddy, there were traces in it, made since I last saw it, as if he had applied himself to some habitual strain of the fervent energy which, when roused, was so passionately roused within him. I had it in my thoughts to remonstrate with him upon his desperate way of pursuing any fancy that he took--such as this buffeting of rough seas, and braving of hard weather, for example--when my mind glanced off to the immediate subject of our conversation again, and pursued that instead.\n\n‘I tell you what, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘if your high spirits will listen to me--’\n\n‘They are potent spirits, and will do whatever you like,’ he answered, moving from the table to the fireside again.\n\n‘Then I tell you what, Steerforth. I think I will go down and see my old nurse. It is not that I can do her any good, or render her any real service; but she is so attached to me that my visit will have as much effect on her, as if I could do both. She will take it so kindly that it will be a comfort and support to her. It is no great effort to make, I am sure, for such a friend as she has been to me. Wouldn’t you go a day’s journey, if you were in my place?’\n\nHis face was thoughtful, and he sat considering a little before he answered, in a low voice, ‘Well! Go. You can do no harm.’\n\n‘You have just come back,’ said I, ‘and it would be in vain to ask you to go with me?’\n\n‘Quite,’ he returned. ‘I am for Highgate tonight. I have not seen my mother this long time, and it lies upon my conscience, for it’s something to be loved as she loves her prodigal son.---Bah! Nonsense!--You mean to go tomorrow, I suppose?’ he said, holding me out at arm’s length, with a hand on each of my shoulders.\n\n‘Yes, I think so.’\n\n‘Well, then, don’t go till next day. I wanted you to come and stay a few days with us. Here I am, on purpose to bid you, and you fly off to Yarmouth!’\n\n‘You are a nice fellow to talk of flying off, Steerforth, who are always running wild on some unknown expedition or other!’\n\nHe looked at me for a moment without speaking, and then rejoined, still holding me as before, and giving me a shake:\n\n‘Come! Say the next day, and pass as much of tomorrow as you can with us! Who knows when we may meet again, else? Come! Say the next day! I want you to stand between Rosa Dartle and me, and keep us asunder.’\n\n‘Would you love each other too much, without me?’\n\n‘Yes; or hate,’ laughed Steerforth; ‘no matter which. Come! Say the next day!’\n\nI said the next day; and he put on his great-coat and lighted his cigar, and set off to walk home. Finding him in this intention, I put on my own great-coat (but did not light my own cigar, having had enough of that for one while) and walked with him as far as the open road: a dull road, then, at night. He was in great spirits all the way; and when we parted, and I looked after him going so gallantly and airily homeward, I thought of his saying, ‘Ride on over all obstacles, and win the race!’ and wished, for the first time, that he had some worthy race to run.\n\nI was undressing in my own room, when Mr. Micawber’s letter tumbled on the floor. Thus reminded of it, I broke the seal and read as follows. It was dated an hour and a half before dinner. I am not sure whether I have mentioned that, when Mr. Micawber was at any particularly desperate crisis, he used a sort of legal phraseology, which he seemed to think equivalent to winding up his affairs.\n\n‘It is expedient that I should inform you that the undersigned is Crushed. Some flickering efforts to spare you the premature knowledge of his calamitous position, you may observe in him this day; but hope has sunk beneath the horizon, and the undersigned is Crushed.\n\n‘The present communication is penned within the personal range (I cannot call it the society) of an individual, in a state closely bordering on intoxication, employed by a broker. That individual is in legal possession of the premises, under a distress for rent. His inventory includes, not only the chattels and effects of every description belonging to the undersigned, as yearly tenant of this habitation, but also those appertaining to Mr. Thomas Traddles, lodger, a member of the Honourable Society of the Inner Temple.\n\n‘If any drop of gloom were wanting in the overflowing cup, which is now “commended” (in the language of an immortal Writer) to the lips of the undersigned, it would be found in the fact, that a friendly acceptance granted to the undersigned, by the before-mentioned Mr. Thomas Traddles, for the sum Of 23l 4s 9 1/2d is over due, and is NOT provided for. Also, in the fact that the living responsibilities clinging to the undersigned will, in the course of nature, be increased by the sum of one more helpless victim; whose miserable appearance may be looked for--in round numbers--at the expiration of a period not exceeding six lunar months from the present date.\n\n‘After premising thus much, it would be a work of supererogation to add, that dust and ashes are for ever scattered\n\nPoor Traddles! I knew enough of Mr. Micawber by this time, to foresee that he might be expected to recover the blow; but my night’s rest was sorely distressed by thoughts of Traddles, and of the curate’s daughter, who was one of ten, down in Devonshire, and who was such a dear girl, and who would wait for Traddles (ominous praise!) until she was sixty, or any age that could be mentioned.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. I VISIT STEERFORTH AT HIS HOME, AGAIN\n\nI mentioned to Mr. Spenlow in the morning, that I wanted leave of absence for a short time; and as I was not in the receipt of any salary, and consequently was not obnoxious to the implacable Jorkins, there was no difficulty about it. I took that opportunity, with my voice sticking in my throat, and my sight failing as I uttered the words, to express my hope that Miss Spenlow was quite well; to which Mr. Spenlow replied, with no more emotion than if he had been speaking of an ordinary human being, that he was much obliged to me, and she was very well.\n\nWe articled clerks, as germs of the patrician order of proctors, were treated with so much consideration, that I was almost my own master at all times. As I did not care, however, to get to Highgate before one or two o’clock in the day, and as we had another little excommunication case in court that morning, which was called The office of the judge promoted by Tipkins against Bullock for his soul’s correction, I passed an hour or two in attendance on it with Mr. Spenlow very agreeably. It arose out of a scuffle between two churchwardens, one of whom was alleged to have pushed the other against a pump; the handle of which pump projecting into a school-house, which school-house was under a gable of the church-roof, made the push an ecclesiastical offence. It was an amusing case; and sent me up to Highgate, on the box of the stage-coach, thinking about the Commons, and what Mr. Spenlow had said about touching the Commons and bringing down the country.\n\nMrs. Steerforth was pleased to see me, and so was Rosa Dartle. I was agreeably surprised to find that Littimer was not there, and that we were attended by a modest little parlour-maid, with blue ribbons in her cap, whose eye it was much more pleasant, and much less disconcerting, to catch by accident, than the eye of that respectable man. But what I particularly observed, before I had been half-an-hour in the house, was the close and attentive watch Miss Dartle kept upon me; and the lurking manner in which she seemed to compare my face with Steerforth’s, and Steerforth’s with mine, and to lie in wait for something to come out between the two. So surely as I looked towards her, did I see that eager visage, with its gaunt black eyes and searching brow, intent on mine; or passing suddenly from mine to Steerforth’s; or comprehending both of us at once. In this lynx-like scrutiny she was so far from faltering when she saw I observed it, that at such a time she only fixed her piercing look upon me with a more intent expression still. Blameless as I was, and knew that I was, in reference to any wrong she could possibly suspect me of, I shrunk before her strange eyes, quite unable to endure their hungry lustre.\n\nAll day, she seemed to pervade the whole house. If I talked to Steerforth in his room, I heard her dress rustle in the little gallery outside. When he and I engaged in some of our old exercises on the lawn behind the house, I saw her face pass from window to window, like a wandering light, until it fixed itself in one, and watched us. When we all four went out walking in the afternoon, she closed her thin hand on my arm like a spring, to keep me back, while Steerforth and his mother went on out of hearing: and then spoke to me.\n\n‘You have been a long time,’ she said, ‘without coming here. Is your profession really so engaging and interesting as to absorb your whole attention? I ask because I always want to be informed, when I am ignorant. Is it really, though?’\n\nI replied that I liked it well enough, but that I certainly could not claim so much for it.\n\n‘Oh! I am glad to know that, because I always like to be put right when I am wrong,’ said Rosa Dartle. ‘You mean it is a little dry, perhaps?’\n\n‘Well,’ I replied; ‘perhaps it was a little dry.’\n\n‘Oh! and that’s a reason why you want relief and change--excitement and all that?’ said she. ‘Ah! very true! But isn’t it a little--Eh?--for him; I don’t mean you?’\n\nA quick glance of her eye towards the spot where Steerforth was walking, with his mother leaning on his arm, showed me whom she meant; but beyond that, I was quite lost. And I looked so, I have no doubt.\n\n‘Don’t it--I don’t say that it does, mind I want to know--don’t it rather engross him? Don’t it make him, perhaps, a little more remiss than usual in his visits to his blindly-doting--eh?’ With another quick glance at them, and such a glance at me as seemed to look into my innermost thoughts.\n\n‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘pray do not think--’\n\n‘I don’t!’ she said. ‘Oh dear me, don’t suppose that I think anything! I am not suspicious. I only ask a question. I don’t state any opinion. I want to found an opinion on what you tell me. Then, it’s not so? Well! I am very glad to know it.’\n\n‘It certainly is not the fact,’ said I, perplexed, ‘that I am accountable for Steerforth’s having been away from home longer than usual--if he has been: which I really don’t know at this moment, unless I understand it from you. I have not seen him this long while, until last night.’\n\n‘No?’\n‘Indeed, Miss Dartle, no!’\n\nAs she looked full at me, I saw her face grow sharper and paler, and the marks of the old wound lengthen out until it cut through the disfigured lip, and deep into the nether lip, and slanted down the face. There was something positively awful to me in this, and in the brightness of her eyes, as she said, looking fixedly at me:\n\n‘What is he doing?’\n\nI repeated the words, more to myself than her, being so amazed.\n\n‘What is he doing?’ she said, with an eagerness that seemed enough to consume her like a fire. ‘In what is that man assisting him, who never looks at me without an inscrutable falsehood in his eyes? If you are honourable and faithful, I don’t ask you to betray your friend. I ask you only to tell me, is it anger, is it hatred, is it pride, is it restlessness, is it some wild fancy, is it love, what is it, that is leading him?’\n\n‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘how shall I tell you, so that you will believe me, that I know of nothing in Steerforth different from what there was when I first came here? I can think of nothing. I firmly believe there is nothing. I hardly understand even what you mean.’\n\nAs she still stood looking fixedly at me, a twitching or throbbing, from which I could not dissociate the idea of pain, came into that cruel mark; and lifted up the corner of her lip as if with scorn, or with a pity that despised its object. She put her hand upon it hurriedly--a hand so thin and delicate, that when I had seen her hold it up before the fire to shade her face, I had compared it in my thoughts to fine porcelain--and saying, in a quick, fierce, passionate way, ‘I swear you to secrecy about this!’ said not a word more.\n\nMrs. Steerforth was particularly happy in her son’s society, and Steerforth was, on this occasion, particularly attentive and respectful to her. It was very interesting to me to see them together, not only on account of their mutual affection, but because of the strong personal resemblance between them, and the manner in which what was haughty or impetuous in him was softened by age and sex, in her, to a gracious dignity. I thought, more than once, that it was well no serious cause of division had ever come between them; or two such natures--I ought rather to express it, two such shades of the same nature--might have been harder to reconcile than the two extremest opposites in creation. The idea did not originate in my own discernment, I am bound to confess, but in a speech of Rosa Dartle’s.\nShe said at dinner:\n\n‘Oh, but do tell me, though, somebody, because I have been thinking about it all day, and I want to know.’\n\n‘You want to know what, Rosa?’ returned Mrs. Steerforth. ‘Pray, pray, Rosa, do not be mysterious.’\n\n‘Mysterious!’ she cried. ‘Oh! really? Do you consider me so?’\n\n‘Do I constantly entreat you,’ said Mrs. Steerforth, ‘to speak plainly, in your own natural manner?’\n\n‘Oh! then this is not my natural manner?’ she rejoined. ‘Now you must really bear with me, because I ask for information. We never know ourselves.’\n\n‘It has become a second nature,’ said Mrs. Steerforth, without any displeasure; ‘but I remember,--and so must you, I think,--when your manner was different, Rosa; when it was not so guarded, and was more trustful.’\n\n‘I am sure you are right,’ she returned; ‘and so it is that bad habits grow upon one! Really? Less guarded and more trustful? How can I, imperceptibly, have changed, I wonder! Well, that’s very odd! I must study to regain my former self.’\n\n‘I wish you would,’ said Mrs. Steerforth, with a smile.\n\n‘Oh! I really will, you know!’ she answered. ‘I will learn frankness from--let me see--from James.’\n\n‘You cannot learn frankness, Rosa,’ said Mrs. Steerforth quickly--for there was always some effect of sarcasm in what Rosa Dartle said, though it was said, as this was, in the most unconscious manner in the world--‘in a better school.’\n\n‘That I am sure of,’ she answered, with uncommon fervour. ‘If I am sure of anything, of course, you know, I am sure of that.’\n\nMrs. Steerforth appeared to me to regret having been a little nettled; for she presently said, in a kind tone:\n\n‘Well, my dear Rosa, we have not heard what it is that you want to be satisfied about?’\n\n‘That I want to be satisfied about?’ she replied, with provoking coldness. ‘Oh! It was only whether people, who are like each other in their moral constitution--is that the phrase?’\n\n‘It’s as good a phrase as another,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Thank you:--whether people, who are like each other in their moral constitution, are in greater danger than people not so circumstanced, supposing any serious cause of variance to arise between them, of being divided angrily and deeply?’\n\n‘I should say yes,’ said Steerforth.\n\n‘Should you?’ she retorted. ‘Dear me! Supposing then, for instance--any unlikely thing will do for a supposition--that you and your mother were to have a serious quarrel.’\n\n‘My dear Rosa,’ interposed Mrs. Steerforth, laughing good-naturedly, ‘suggest some other supposition! James and I know our duty to each other better, I pray Heaven!’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Miss Dartle, nodding her head thoughtfully. ‘To be sure. That would prevent it? Why, of course it would. Exactly. Now, I am glad I have been so foolish as to put the case, for it is so very good to know that your duty to each other would prevent it! Thank you very much.’\n\nOne other little circumstance connected with Miss Dartle I must not omit; for I had reason to remember it thereafter, when all the irremediable past was rendered plain. During the whole of this day, but especially from this period of it, Steerforth exerted himself with his utmost skill, and that was with his utmost ease, to charm this singular creature into a pleasant and pleased companion. That he should succeed, was no matter of surprise to me. That she should struggle against the fascinating influence of his delightful art--delightful nature I thought it then--did not surprise me either; for I knew that she was sometimes jaundiced and perverse. I saw her features and her manner slowly change; I saw her look at him with growing admiration; I saw her try, more and more faintly, but always angrily, as if she condemned a weakness in herself, to resist the captivating power that he possessed; and finally, I saw her sharp glance soften, and her smile become quite gentle, and I ceased to be afraid of her as I had really been all day, and we all sat about the fire, talking and laughing together, with as little reserve as if we had been children.\n\nWhether it was because we had sat there so long, or because Steerforth was resolved not to lose the advantage he had gained, I do not know; but we did not remain in the dining-room more than five minutes after her departure. ‘She is playing her harp,’ said Steerforth, softly, at the drawing-room door, ‘and nobody but my mother has heard her do that, I believe, these three years.’ He said it with a curious smile, which was gone directly; and we went into the room and found her alone.\n\n‘Don’t get up,’ said Steerforth (which she had already done)’ my dear Rosa, don’t! Be kind for once, and sing us an Irish song.’\n\n‘What do you care for an Irish song?’ she returned.\n\n‘Much!’ said Steerforth. ‘Much more than for any other. Here is Daisy, too, loves music from his soul. Sing us an Irish song, Rosa! and let me sit and listen as I used to do.’\n\nHe did not touch her, or the chair from which she had risen, but sat himself near the harp. She stood beside it for some little while, in a curious way, going through the motion of playing it with her right hand, but not sounding it. At length she sat down, and drew it to her with one sudden action, and played and sang.\n\nI don’t know what it was, in her touch or voice, that made that song the most unearthly I have ever heard in my life, or can imagine. There was something fearful in the reality of it. It was as if it had never been written, or set to music, but sprung out of passion within her; which found imperfect utterance in the low sounds of her voice, and crouched again when all was still. I was dumb when she leaned beside the harp again, playing it, but not sounding it, with her right hand.\n\nA minute more, and this had roused me from my trance:--Steerforth had left his seat, and gone to her, and had put his arm laughingly about her, and had said, ‘Come, Rosa, for the future we will love each other very much!’ And she had struck him, and had thrown him off with the fury of a wild cat, and had burst out of the room.\n\n‘What is the matter with Rosa?’ said Mrs. Steerforth, coming in.\n\n‘She has been an angel, mother,’ returned Steerforth, ‘for a little while; and has run into the opposite extreme, since, by way of compensation.’\n\n‘You should be careful not to irritate her, James. Her temper has been soured, remember, and ought not to be tried.’\n\nRosa did not come back; and no other mention was made of her, until I went with Steerforth into his room to say Good night. Then he laughed about her, and asked me if I had ever seen such a fierce little piece of incomprehensibility.\n\nI expressed as much of my astonishment as was then capable of expression, and asked if he could guess what it was that she had taken so much amiss, so suddenly.\n\n‘Oh, Heaven knows,’ said Steerforth. ‘Anything you like--or nothing! I told you she took everything, herself included, to a grindstone, and sharpened it. She is an edge-tool, and requires great care in dealing with. She is always dangerous. Good night!’\n\n‘Good night!’ said I, ‘my dear Steerforth! I shall be gone before you wake in the morning. Good night!’\n\nHe was unwilling to let me go; and stood, holding me out, with a hand on each of my shoulders, as he had done in my own room.\n\n‘Daisy,’ he said, with a smile--‘for though that’s not the name your godfathers and godmothers gave you, it’s the name I like best to call you by--and I wish, I wish, I wish, you could give it to me!’\n\n‘Why so I can, if I choose,’ said I.\n\n‘Daisy, if anything should ever separate us, you must think of me at my best, old boy. Come! Let us make that bargain. Think of me at my best, if circumstances should ever part us!’\n\n‘You have no best to me, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘and no worst. You are always equally loved, and cherished in my heart.’\n\nSo much compunction for having ever wronged him, even by a shapeless thought, did I feel within me, that the confession of having done so was rising to my lips. But for the reluctance I had to betray the confidence of Agnes, but for my uncertainty how to approach the subject with no risk of doing so, it would have reached them before he said, ‘God bless you, Daisy, and good night!’ In my doubt, it did NOT reach them; and we shook hands, and we parted.\n\nI was up with the dull dawn, and, having dressed as quietly as I could, looked into his room. He was fast asleep; lying, easily, with his head upon his arm, as I had often seen him lie at school.\n\nThe time came in its season, and that was very soon, when I almost wondered that nothing troubled his repose, as I looked at him. But he slept--let me think of him so again--as I had often seen him sleep at school; and thus, in this silent hour, I left him. --Never more, oh God forgive you, Steerforth! to touch that passive hand in love and friendship. Never, never more!\n\n# CHAPTER 30. A LOSS\n\nI got down to Yarmouth in the evening, and went to the inn. I knew that Peggotty’s spare room--my room--was likely to have occupation enough in a little while, if that great Visitor, before whose presence all the living must give place, were not already in the house; so I betook myself to the inn, and dined there, and engaged my bed.\n\nIt was ten o’clock when I went out. Many of the shops were shut, and the town was dull. When I came to Omer and Joram’s, I found the shutters up, but the shop door standing open. As I could obtain a perspective view of Mr. Omer inside, smoking his pipe by the parlour door, I entered, and asked him how he was.\n\n‘Why, bless my life and soul!’ said Mr. Omer, ‘how do you find yourself? Take a seat.---Smoke not disagreeable, I hope?’\n\n‘By no means,’ said I. ‘I like it--in somebody else’s pipe.’\n\n‘What, not in your own, eh?’ Mr. Omer returned, laughing. ‘All the better, sir. Bad habit for a young man. Take a seat. I smoke, myself, for the asthma.’\n\nMr. Omer had made room for me, and placed a chair. He now sat down again very much out of breath, gasping at his pipe as if it contained a supply of that necessary, without which he must perish.\n\n‘I am sorry to have heard bad news of Mr. Barkis,’ said I.\n\nMr. Omer looked at me, with a steady countenance, and shook his head.\n\n‘Do you know how he is tonight?’ I asked.\n\n‘The very question I should have put to you, sir,’ returned Mr. Omer, ‘but on account of delicacy. It’s one of the drawbacks of our line of business. When a party’s ill, we can’t ask how the party is.’\n\nThe difficulty had not occurred to me; though I had had my apprehensions too, when I went in, of hearing the old tune. On its being mentioned, I recognized it, however, and said as much.\n\n‘Yes, yes, you understand,’ said Mr. Omer, nodding his head. ‘We dursn’t do it. Bless you, it would be a shock that the generality of parties mightn’t recover, to say “Omer and Joram’s compliments, and how do you find yourself this morning?”--or this afternoon--as it may be.’\n\nMr. Omer and I nodded at each other, and Mr. Omer recruited his wind by the aid of his pipe.\n\n‘It’s one of the things that cut the trade off from attentions they could often wish to show,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Take myself. If I have known Barkis a year, to move to as he went by, I have known him forty years. But I can’t go and say, “how is he?”’\n\nI felt it was rather hard on Mr. Omer, and I told him so.\n\n‘I’m not more self-interested, I hope, than another man,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Look at me! My wind may fail me at any moment, and it ain’t likely that, to my own knowledge, I’d be self-interested under such circumstances. I say it ain’t likely, in a man who knows his wind will go, when it DOES go, as if a pair of bellows was cut open; and that man a grandfather,’ said Mr. Omer.\n\nI said, ‘Not at all.’\n\n‘It ain’t that I complain of my line of business,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘It ain’t that. Some good and some bad goes, no doubt, to all callings. What I wish is, that parties was brought up stronger-minded.’\n\nMr. Omer, with a very complacent and amiable face, took several puffs in silence; and then said, resuming his first point:\n\n‘Accordingly we’re obleeged, in ascertaining how Barkis goes on, to limit ourselves to Em’ly. She knows what our real objects are, and she don’t have any more alarms or suspicions about us, than if we was so many lambs. Minnie and Joram have just stepped down to the house, in fact (she’s there, after hours, helping her aunt a bit), to ask her how he is tonight; and if you was to please to wait till they come back, they’d give you full partic’lers. Will you take something? A glass of srub and water, now? I smoke on srub and water, myself,’ said Mr. Omer, taking up his glass, ‘because it’s considered softening to the passages, by which this troublesome breath of mine gets into action. But, Lord bless you,’ said Mr. Omer, huskily, ‘it ain’t the passages that’s out of order! “Give me breath enough,” said I to my daughter Minnie, “and I’ll find passages, my dear.”’\n\nHe really had no breath to spare, and it was very alarming to see him laugh. When he was again in a condition to be talked to, I thanked him for the proffered refreshment, which I declined, as I had just had dinner; and, observing that I would wait, since he was so good as to invite me, until his daughter and his son-in-law came back, I inquired how little Emily was?\n\n‘Well, sir,’ said Mr. Omer, removing his pipe, that he might rub his chin: ‘I tell you truly, I shall be glad when her marriage has taken place.’\n\n‘Why so?’ I inquired.\n\n‘Well, she’s unsettled at present,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘It ain’t that she’s not as pretty as ever, for she’s prettier--I do assure you, she is prettier. It ain’t that she don’t work as well as ever, for she does. She WAS worth any six, and she IS worth any six. But somehow she wants heart. If you understand,’ said Mr. Omer, after rubbing his chin again, and smoking a little, ‘what I mean in a general way by the expression, “A long pull, and a strong pull, and a pull altogether, my hearties, hurrah!” I should say to you, that that was--in a general way--what I miss in Em’ly.’\n\nMr. Omer’s face and manner went for so much, that I could conscientiously nod my head, as divining his meaning. My quickness of apprehension seemed to please him, and he went on: ‘Now I consider this is principally on account of her being in an unsettled state, you see. We have talked it over a good deal, her uncle and myself, and her sweetheart and myself, after business; and I consider it is principally on account of her being unsettled. You must always recollect of Em’ly,’ said Mr. Omer, shaking his head gently, ‘that she’s a most extraordinary affectionate little thing. The proverb says, “You can’t make a silk purse out of a sow’s ear.” Well, I don’t know about that. I rather think you may, if you begin early in life. She has made a home out of that old boat, sir, that stone and marble couldn’t beat.’\n\n‘I am sure she has!’ said I.\n\n‘To see the clinging of that pretty little thing to her uncle,’ said Mr. Omer; ‘to see the way she holds on to him, tighter and tighter, and closer and closer, every day, is to see a sight. Now, you know, there’s a struggle going on when that’s the case. Why should it be made a longer one than is needful?’\n\nI listened attentively to the good old fellow, and acquiesced, with all my heart, in what he said.\n\n‘Therefore, I mentioned to them,’ said Mr. Omer, in a comfortable, easy-going tone, ‘this. I said, “Now, don’t consider Em’ly nailed down in point of time, at all. Make it your own time. Her services have been more valuable than was supposed; her learning has been quicker than was supposed; Omer and Joram can run their pen through what remains; and she’s free when you wish. If she likes to make any little arrangement, afterwards, in the way of doing any little thing for us at home, very well. If she don’t, very well still. We’re no losers, anyhow.” For--don’t you see,’ said Mr. Omer, touching me with his pipe, ‘it ain’t likely that a man so short of breath as myself, and a grandfather too, would go and strain points with a little bit of a blue-eyed blossom, like her?’\n\n‘Not at all, I am certain,’ said I.\n\n‘Not at all! You’re right!’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Well, sir, her cousin--you know it’s a cousin she’s going to be married to?’\n\n‘Oh yes,’ I replied. ‘I know him well.’\n\n‘Of course you do,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Well, sir! Her cousin being, as it appears, in good work, and well to do, thanked me in a very manly sort of manner for this (conducting himself altogether, I must say, in a way that gives me a high opinion of him), and went and took as comfortable a little house as you or I could wish to clap eyes on. That little house is now furnished right through, as neat and complete as a doll’s parlour; and but for Barkis’s illness having taken this bad turn, poor fellow, they would have been man and wife--I dare say, by this time. As it is, there’s a postponement.’\n\n‘And Emily, Mr. Omer?’ I inquired. ‘Has she become more settled?’\n\n‘Why that, you know,’ he returned, rubbing his double chin again, ‘can’t naturally be expected. The prospect of the change and separation, and all that, is, as one may say, close to her and far away from her, both at once. Barkis’s death needn’t put it off much, but his lingering might. Anyway, it’s an uncertain state of matters, you see.’\n\n‘I see,’ said I.\n\n‘Consequently,’ pursued Mr. Omer, ‘Em’ly’s still a little down, and a little fluttered; perhaps, upon the whole, she’s more so than she was. Every day she seems to get fonder and fonder of her uncle, and more loth to part from all of us. A kind word from me brings the tears into her eyes; and if you was to see her with my daughter Minnie’s little girl, you’d never forget it. Bless my heart alive!’ said Mr. Omer, pondering, ‘how she loves that child!’\n\nHaving so favourable an opportunity, it occurred to me to ask Mr. Omer, before our conversation should be interrupted by the return of his daughter and her husband, whether he knew anything of Martha.\n\n‘Ah!’ he rejoined, shaking his head, and looking very much dejected. ‘No good. A sad story, sir, however you come to know it. I never thought there was harm in the girl. I wouldn’t wish to mention it before my daughter Minnie--for she’d take me up directly--but I never did. None of us ever did.’\n\nMr. Omer, hearing his daughter’s footstep before I heard it, touched me with his pipe, and shut up one eye, as a caution. She and her husband came in immediately afterwards.\n\nTheir report was, that Mr. Barkis was ‘as bad as bad could be’; that he was quite unconscious; and that Mr. Chillip had mournfully said in the kitchen, on going away just now, that the College of Physicians, the College of Surgeons, and Apothecaries’ Hall, if they were all called in together, couldn’t help him. He was past both Colleges, Mr. Chillip said, and the Hall could only poison him.\n\nHearing this, and learning that Mr. Peggotty was there, I determined to go to the house at once. I bade good night to Mr. Omer, and to Mr. and Mrs. Joram; and directed my steps thither, with a solemn feeling, which made Mr. Barkis quite a new and different creature.\n\nMy low tap at the door was answered by Mr. Peggotty. He was not so much surprised to see me as I had expected. I remarked this in Peggotty, too, when she came down; and I have seen it since; and I think, in the expectation of that dread surprise, all other changes and surprises dwindle into nothing.\n\nI shook hands with Mr. Peggotty, and passed into the kitchen, while he softly closed the door. Little Emily was sitting by the fire, with her hands before her face. Ham was standing near her.\n\nWe spoke in whispers; listening, between whiles, for any sound in the room above. I had not thought of it on the occasion of my last visit, but how strange it was to me, now, to miss Mr. Barkis out of the kitchen!\n\n‘This is very kind of you, Mas’r Davy,’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘It’s oncommon kind,’ said Ham.\n\n‘Em’ly, my dear,’ cried Mr. Peggotty. ‘See here! Here’s Mas’r Davy come! What, cheer up, pretty! Not a wured to Mas’r Davy?’\n\nThere was a trembling upon her, that I can see now. The coldness of her hand when I touched it, I can feel yet. Its only sign of animation was to shrink from mine; and then she glided from the chair, and creeping to the other side of her uncle, bowed herself, silently and trembling still, upon his breast.\n\n‘It’s such a loving art,’ said Mr. Peggotty, smoothing her rich hair with his great hard hand, ‘that it can’t abear the sorrer of this. It’s nat’ral in young folk, Mas’r Davy, when they’re new to these here trials, and timid, like my little bird,--it’s nat’ral.’\n\nShe clung the closer to him, but neither lifted up her face, nor spoke a word.\n\n‘It’s getting late, my dear,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘and here’s Ham come fur to take you home. Theer! Go along with t’other loving art! What’ Em’ly? Eh, my pretty?’\n\nThe sound of her voice had not reached me, but he bent his head as if he listened to her, and then said:\n\n‘Let you stay with your uncle? Why, you doen’t mean to ask me that! Stay with your uncle, Moppet? When your husband that’ll be so soon, is here fur to take you home? Now a person wouldn’t think it, fur to see this little thing alongside a rough-weather chap like me,’ said Mr. Peggotty, looking round at both of us, with infinite pride; ‘but the sea ain’t more salt in it than she has fondness in her for her uncle--a foolish little Em’ly!’\n\n‘Em’ly’s in the right in that, Mas’r Davy!’ said Ham. ‘Lookee here! As Em’ly wishes of it, and as she’s hurried and frightened, like, besides, I’ll leave her till morning. Let me stay too!’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘You doen’t ought--a married man like you--or what’s as good--to take and hull away a day’s work. And you doen’t ought to watch and work both. That won’t do. You go home and turn in. You ain’t afeerd of Em’ly not being took good care on, I know.’\n\nHam yielded to this persuasion, and took his hat to go. Even when he kissed her--and I never saw him approach her, but I felt that nature had given him the soul of a gentleman--she seemed to cling closer to her uncle, even to the avoidance of her chosen husband. I shut the door after him, that it might cause no disturbance of the quiet that prevailed; and when I turned back, I found Mr. Peggotty still talking to her.\n\n‘Now, I’m a going upstairs to tell your aunt as Mas’r Davy’s here, and that’ll cheer her up a bit,’ he said. ‘Sit ye down by the fire, the while, my dear, and warm those mortal cold hands. You doen’t need to be so fearsome, and take on so much. What? You’ll go along with me?--Well! come along with me--come! If her uncle was turned out of house and home, and forced to lay down in a dyke, Mas’r Davy,’ said Mr. Peggotty, with no less pride than before, ‘it’s my belief she’d go along with him, now! But there’ll be someone else, soon,--someone else, soon, Em’ly!’\n\nAfterwards, when I went upstairs, as I passed the door of my little chamber, which was dark, I had an indistinct impression of her being within it, cast down upon the floor. But, whether it was really she, or whether it was a confusion of the shadows in the room, I don’t know now.\n\nI had leisure to think, before the kitchen fire, of pretty little Emily’s dread of death--which, added to what Mr. Omer had told me, I took to be the cause of her being so unlike herself--and I had leisure, before Peggotty came down, even to think more leniently of the weakness of it: as I sat counting the ticking of the clock, and deepening my sense of the solemn hush around me. Peggotty took me in her arms, and blessed and thanked me over and over again for being such a comfort to her (that was what she said) in her distress. She then entreated me to come upstairs, sobbing that Mr. Barkis had always liked me and admired me; that he had often talked of me, before he fell into a stupor; and that she believed, in case of his coming to himself again, he would brighten up at sight of me, if he could brighten up at any earthly thing.\n\nThe probability of his ever doing so, appeared to me, when I saw him, to be very small. He was lying with his head and shoulders out of bed, in an uncomfortable attitude, half resting on the box which had cost him so much pain and trouble. I learned, that, when he was past creeping out of bed to open it, and past assuring himself of its safety by means of the divining rod I had seen him use, he had required to have it placed on the chair at the bed-side, where he had ever since embraced it, night and day. His arm lay on it now. Time and the world were slipping from beneath him, but the box was there; and the last words he had uttered were (in an explanatory tone) ‘Old clothes!’\n\n‘Barkis, my dear!’ said Peggotty, almost cheerfully: bending over him, while her brother and I stood at the bed’s foot. ‘Here’s my dear boy--my dear boy, Master Davy, who brought us together, Barkis! That you sent messages by, you know! Won’t you speak to Master Davy?’\n\nHe was as mute and senseless as the box, from which his form derived the only expression it had.\n\n‘He’s a going out with the tide,’ said Mr. Peggotty to me, behind his hand.\n\nMy eyes were dim and so were Mr. Peggotty’s; but I repeated in a whisper, ‘With the tide?’\n\n‘People can’t die, along the coast,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘except when the tide’s pretty nigh out. They can’t be born, unless it’s pretty nigh in--not properly born, till flood. He’s a going out with the tide. It’s ebb at half-arter three, slack water half an hour. If he lives till it turns, he’ll hold his own till past the flood, and go out with the next tide.’\n\nWe remained there, watching him, a long time--hours. What mysterious influence my presence had upon him in that state of his senses, I shall not pretend to say; but when he at last began to wander feebly, it is certain he was muttering about driving me to school.\n\n‘He’s coming to himself,’ said Peggotty.\n\nMr. Peggotty touched me, and whispered with much awe and reverence. ‘They are both a-going out fast.’\n\n‘Barkis, my dear!’ said Peggotty.\n\n‘C. P. Barkis,’ he cried faintly. ‘No better woman anywhere!’\n\n‘Look! Here’s Master Davy!’ said Peggotty. For he now opened his eyes.\n\nI was on the point of asking him if he knew me, when he tried to stretch out his arm, and said to me, distinctly, with a pleasant smile:\n\n‘Barkis is willin’!’\n\nAnd, it being low water, he went out with the tide.\n\n# CHAPTER 31. A GREATER LOSS\n\nIt was not difficult for me, on Peggotty’s solicitation, to resolve to stay where I was, until after the remains of the poor carrier should have made their last journey to Blunderstone. She had long ago bought, out of her own savings, a little piece of ground in our old churchyard near the grave of ‘her sweet girl’, as she always called my mother; and there they were to rest.\n\nIn keeping Peggotty company, and doing all I could for her (little enough at the utmost), I was as grateful, I rejoice to think, as even now I could wish myself to have been. But I am afraid I had a supreme satisfaction, of a personal and professional nature, in taking charge of Mr. Barkis’s will, and expounding its contents.\n\nI may claim the merit of having originated the suggestion that the will should be looked for in the box. After some search, it was found in the box, at the bottom of a horse’s nose-bag; wherein (besides hay) there was discovered an old gold watch, with chain and seals, which Mr. Barkis had worn on his wedding-day, and which had never been seen before or since; a silver tobacco-stopper, in the form of a leg; an imitation lemon, full of minute cups and saucers, which I have some idea Mr. Barkis must have purchased to present to me when I was a child, and afterwards found himself unable to part with; eighty-seven guineas and a half, in guineas and half-guineas; two hundred and ten pounds, in perfectly clean Bank notes; certain receipts for Bank of England stock; an old horseshoe, a bad shilling, a piece of camphor, and an oyster-shell. From the circumstance of the latter article having been much polished, and displaying prismatic colours on the inside, I conclude that Mr. Barkis had some general ideas about pearls, which never resolved themselves into anything definite.\n\nFor years and years, Mr. Barkis had carried this box, on all his journeys, every day. That it might the better escape notice, he had invented a fiction that it belonged to ‘Mr. Blackboy’, and was ‘to be left with Barkis till called for’; a fable he had elaborately written on the lid, in characters now scarcely legible.\n\nHe had hoarded, all these years, I found, to good purpose. His property in money amounted to nearly three thousand pounds. Of this he bequeathed the interest of one thousand to Mr. Peggotty for his life; on his decease, the principal to be equally divided between Peggotty, little Emily, and me, or the survivor or survivors of us, share and share alike. All the rest he died possessed of, he bequeathed to Peggotty; whom he left residuary legatee, and sole executrix of that his last will and testament.\n\nI felt myself quite a proctor when I read this document aloud with all possible ceremony, and set forth its provisions, any number of times, to those whom they concerned. I began to think there was more in the Commons than I had supposed. I examined the will with the deepest attention, pronounced it perfectly formal in all respects, made a pencil-mark or so in the margin, and thought it rather extraordinary that I knew so much.\n\nIn this abstruse pursuit; in making an account for Peggotty, of all the property into which she had come; in arranging all the affairs in an orderly manner; and in being her referee and adviser on every point, to our joint delight; I passed the week before the funeral. I did not see little Emily in that interval, but they told me she was to be quietly married in a fortnight.\n\nI did not attend the funeral in character, if I may venture to say so. I mean I was not dressed up in a black coat and a streamer, to frighten the birds; but I walked over to Blunderstone early in the morning, and was in the churchyard when it came, attended only by Peggotty and her brother. The mad gentleman looked on, out of my little window; Mr. Chillip’s baby wagged its heavy head, and rolled its goggle eyes, at the clergyman, over its nurse’s shoulder; Mr. Omer breathed short in the background; no one else was there; and it was very quiet. We walked about the churchyard for an hour, after all was over; and pulled some young leaves from the tree above my mother’s grave.\n\nA dread falls on me here. A cloud is lowering on the distant town, towards which I retraced my solitary steps. I fear to approach it. I cannot bear to think of what did come, upon that memorable night; of what must come again, if I go on.\n\nIt is no worse, because I write of it. It would be no better, if I stopped my most unwilling hand. It is done. Nothing can undo it; nothing can make it otherwise than as it was.\n\nMy old nurse was to go to London with me next day, on the business of the will. Little Emily was passing that day at Mr. Omer’s. We were all to meet in the old boathouse that night. Ham would bring Emily at the usual hour. I would walk back at my leisure. The brother and sister would return as they had come, and be expecting us, when the day closed in, at the fireside.\n\nI parted from them at the wicket-gate, where visionary Strap had rested with Roderick Random’s knapsack in the days of yore; and, instead of going straight back, walked a little distance on the road to Lowestoft. Then I turned, and walked back towards Yarmouth. I stayed to dine at a decent alehouse, some mile or two from the Ferry I have mentioned before; and thus the day wore away, and it was evening when I reached it. Rain was falling heavily by that time, and it was a wild night; but there was a moon behind the clouds, and it was not dark.\n\nI was soon within sight of Mr. Peggotty’s house, and of the light within it shining through the window. A little floundering across the sand, which was heavy, brought me to the door, and I went in.\n\nIt looked very comfortable indeed. Mr. Peggotty had smoked his evening pipe and there were preparations for some supper by and by. The fire was bright, the ashes were thrown up, the locker was ready for little Emily in her old place. In her own old place sat Peggotty, once more, looking (but for her dress) as if she had never left it. She had fallen back, already, on the society of the work-box with St. Paul’s upon the lid, the yard-measure in the cottage, and the bit of wax-candle; and there they all were, just as if they had never been disturbed. Mrs. Gummidge appeared to be fretting a little, in her old corner; and consequently looked quite natural, too.\n\n‘You’re first of the lot, Mas’r Davy!’ said Mr. Peggotty with a happy face. ‘Doen’t keep in that coat, sir, if it’s wet.’\n\n‘Thank you, Mr. Peggotty,’ said I, giving him my outer coat to hang up. ‘It’s quite dry.’\n\n‘So ‘tis!’ said Mr. Peggotty, feeling my shoulders. ‘As a chip! Sit ye down, sir. It ain’t o’ no use saying welcome to you, but you’re welcome, kind and hearty.’\n\n‘Thank you, Mr. Peggotty, I am sure of that. Well, Peggotty!’ said I, giving her a kiss. ‘And how are you, old woman?’\n\n‘Ha, ha!’ laughed Mr. Peggotty, sitting down beside us, and rubbing his hands in his sense of relief from recent trouble, and in the genuine heartiness of his nature; ‘there’s not a woman in the wureld, sir--as I tell her--that need to feel more easy in her mind than her! She done her dooty by the departed, and the departed know’d it; and the departed done what was right by her, as she done what was right by the departed;--and--and--and it’s all right!’\n\nMrs. Gummidge groaned.\n\n‘Cheer up, my pritty mawther!’ said Mr. Peggotty. (But he shook his head aside at us, evidently sensible of the tendency of the late occurrences to recall the memory of the old one.) ‘Doen’t be down! Cheer up, for your own self, on’y a little bit, and see if a good deal more doen’t come nat’ral!’\n\n‘Not to me, Dan’l,’ returned Mrs. Gummidge. ‘Nothink’s nat’ral to me but to be lone and lorn.’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Peggotty, soothing her sorrows.\n\n‘Yes, yes, Dan’l!’ said Mrs. Gummidge. ‘I ain’t a person to live with them as has had money left. Things go too contrary with me. I had better be a riddance.’\n\n‘Why, how should I ever spend it without you?’ said Mr. Peggotty, with an air of serious remonstrance. ‘What are you a talking on? Doen’t I want you more now, than ever I did?’\n\n‘I know’d I was never wanted before!’ cried Mrs. Gummidge, with a pitiable whimper, ‘and now I’m told so! How could I expect to be wanted, being so lone and lorn, and so contrary!’\n\nMr. Peggotty seemed very much shocked at himself for having made a speech capable of this unfeeling construction, but was prevented from replying, by Peggotty’s pulling his sleeve, and shaking her head. After looking at Mrs. Gummidge for some moments, in sore distress of mind, he glanced at the Dutch clock, rose, snuffed the candle, and put it in the window.\n\n‘Theer!’ said Mr. Peggotty, cheerily. ‘Theer we are, Missis Gummidge!’ Mrs. Gummidge slightly groaned. ‘Lighted up, accordin’ to custom! You’re a wonderin’ what that’s fur, sir! Well, it’s fur our little Em’ly. You see, the path ain’t over light or cheerful arter dark; and when I’m here at the hour as she’s a comin’ home, I puts the light in the winder. That, you see,’ said Mr. Peggotty, bending over me with great glee, ‘meets two objects. She says, says Em’ly, “Theer’s home!” she says. And likewise, says Em’ly, “My uncle’s theer!” Fur if I ain’t theer, I never have no light showed.’\n\n‘You’re a baby!’ said Peggotty; very fond of him for it, if she thought so.\n\n‘Well,’ returned Mr. Peggotty, standing with his legs pretty wide apart, and rubbing his hands up and down them in his comfortable satisfaction, as he looked alternately at us and at the fire. ‘I doen’t know but I am. Not, you see, to look at.’\n\n‘Not azackly,’ observed Peggotty.\n\n‘No,’ laughed Mr. Peggotty, ‘not to look at, but to--to consider on, you know. I doen’t care, bless you! Now I tell you. When I go a looking and looking about that theer pritty house of our Em’ly’s, I’m--I’m Gormed,’ said Mr. Peggotty, with sudden emphasis--‘theer! I can’t say more--if I doen’t feel as if the littlest things was her, a’most. I takes ‘em up and I put ‘em down, and I touches of ‘em as delicate as if they was our Em’ly. So ‘tis with her little bonnets and that. I couldn’t see one on ‘em rough used a purpose--not fur the whole wureld. There’s a babby fur you, in the form of a great Sea Porkypine!’ said Mr. Peggotty, relieving his earnestness with a roar of laughter.\n\nPeggotty and I both laughed, but not so loud.\n\n‘It’s my opinion, you see,’ said Mr. Peggotty, with a delighted face, after some further rubbing of his legs, ‘as this is along of my havin’ played with her so much, and made believe as we was Turks, and French, and sharks, and every wariety of forinners--bless you, yes; and lions and whales, and I doen’t know what all!--when she warn’t no higher than my knee. I’ve got into the way on it, you know. Why, this here candle, now!’ said Mr. Peggotty, gleefully holding out his hand towards it, ‘I know wery well that arter she’s married and gone, I shall put that candle theer, just the same as now. I know wery well that when I’m here o’ nights (and where else should I live, bless your arts, whatever fortun’ I come into!) and she ain’t here or I ain’t theer, I shall put the candle in the winder, and sit afore the fire, pretending I’m expecting of her, like I’m a doing now. THERE’S a babby for you,’ said Mr. Peggotty, with another roar, ‘in the form of a Sea Porkypine! Why, at the present minute, when I see the candle sparkle up, I says to myself, “She’s a looking at it! Em’ly’s a coming!” THERE’S a babby for you, in the form of a Sea Porkypine! Right for all that,’ said Mr. Peggotty, stopping in his roar, and smiting his hands together; ‘fur here she is!’\n\nIt was only Ham. The night should have turned more wet since I came in, for he had a large sou’wester hat on, slouched over his face.\n\n‘Wheer’s Em’ly?’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\nHam made a motion with his head, as if she were outside. Mr. Peggotty took the light from the window, trimmed it, put it on the table, and was busily stirring the fire, when Ham, who had not moved, said:\n\n‘Mas’r Davy, will you come out a minute, and see what Em’ly and me has got to show you?’\n\nWe went out. As I passed him at the door, I saw, to my astonishment and fright, that he was deadly pale. He pushed me hastily into the open air, and closed the door upon us. Only upon us two.\n\n‘Ham! what’s the matter?’\n\n‘Mas’r Davy!--’ Oh, for his broken heart, how dreadfully he wept!\n\nI was paralysed by the sight of such grief. I don’t know what I thought, or what I dreaded. I could only look at him.\n\n‘Ham! Poor good fellow! For Heaven’s sake, tell me what’s the matter!’\n\n‘My love, Mas’r Davy--the pride and hope of my art--her that I’d have died for, and would die for now--she’s gone!’\n\n‘Gone!’\n\n‘Em’ly’s run away! Oh, Mas’r Davy, think HOW she’s run away, when I pray my good and gracious God to kill her (her that is so dear above all things) sooner than let her come to ruin and disgrace!’\n\nThe face he turned up to the troubled sky, the quivering of his clasped hands, the agony of his figure, remain associated with the lonely waste, in my remembrance, to this hour. It is always night there, and he is the only object in the scene.\n\n‘You’re a scholar,’ he said, hurriedly, ‘and know what’s right and best. What am I to say, indoors? How am I ever to break it to him, Mas’r Davy?’\n\nI saw the door move, and instinctively tried to hold the latch on the outside, to gain a moment’s time. It was too late. Mr. Peggotty thrust forth his face; and never could I forget the change that came upon it when he saw us, if I were to live five hundred years.\n\nI remember a great wail and cry, and the women hanging about him, and we all standing in the room; I with a paper in my hand, which Ham had given me; Mr. Peggotty, with his vest torn open, his hair wild, his face and lips quite white, and blood trickling down his bosom (it had sprung from his mouth, I think), looking fixedly at me.\n\n‘Read it, sir,’ he said, in a low shivering voice. ‘Slow, please. I doen’t know as I can understand.’\n\nIn the midst of the silence of death, I read thus, from a blotted letter:\n\n‘“When you, who love me so much better than I ever have deserved, even when my mind was innocent, see this, I shall be far away.”’\n\n‘I shall be fur away,’ he repeated slowly. ‘Stop! Em’ly fur away. Well!’\n\n‘“When I leave my dear home--my dear home--oh, my dear home!--in the morning,”’\n\nthe letter bore date on the previous night:\n\n‘“--it will be never to come back, unless he brings me back a lady. This will be found at night, many hours after, instead of me. Oh, if you knew how my heart is torn. If even you, that I have wronged so much, that never can forgive me, could only know what I suffer! I am too wicked to write about myself! Oh, take comfort in thinking that I am so bad. Oh, for mercy’s sake, tell uncle that I never loved him half so dear as now. Oh, don’t remember how affectionate and kind you have all been to me--don’t remember we were ever to be married--but try to think as if I died when I was little, and was buried somewhere. Pray Heaven that I am going away from, have compassion on my uncle! Tell him that I never loved him half so dear. Be his comfort. Love some good girl that will be what I was once to uncle, and be true to you, and worthy of you, and know no shame but me. God bless all! I’ll pray for all, often, on my knees. If he don’t bring me back a lady, and I don’t pray for my own self, I’ll pray for all. My parting love to uncle. My last tears, and my last thanks, for uncle!”’\n\nThat was all.\n\nHe stood, long after I had ceased to read, still looking at me. At length I ventured to take his hand, and to entreat him, as well as I could, to endeavour to get some command of himself. He replied, ‘I thankee, sir, I thankee!’ without moving.\n\nHam spoke to him. Mr. Peggotty was so far sensible of HIS affliction, that he wrung his hand; but, otherwise, he remained in the same state, and no one dared to disturb him.\n\nSlowly, at last, he moved his eyes from my face, as if he were waking from a vision, and cast them round the room. Then he said, in a low voice:\n\n‘Who’s the man? I want to know his name.’\n\nHam glanced at me, and suddenly I felt a shock that struck me back.\n\n‘There’s a man suspected,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘Who is it?’\n\n‘Mas’r Davy!’ implored Ham. ‘Go out a bit, and let me tell him what I must. You doen’t ought to hear it, sir.’\n\nI felt the shock again. I sank down in a chair, and tried to utter some reply; but my tongue was fettered, and my sight was weak.\n\n‘I want to know his name!’ I heard said once more.\n\n‘For some time past,’ Ham faltered, ‘there’s been a servant about here, at odd times. There’s been a gen’lm’n too. Both of ‘em belonged to one another.’\n\nMr. Peggotty stood fixed as before, but now looking at him.\n\n‘The servant,’ pursued Ham, ‘was seen along with--our poor girl--last night. He’s been in hiding about here, this week or over. He was thought to have gone, but he was hiding. Doen’t stay, Mas’r Davy, doen’t!’\n\nI felt Peggotty’s arm round my neck, but I could not have moved if the house had been about to fall upon me.\n\n‘A strange chay and hosses was outside town, this morning, on the Norwich road, a’most afore the day broke,’ Ham went on. ‘The servant went to it, and come from it, and went to it again. When he went to it again, Em’ly was nigh him. The t’other was inside. He’s the man.’\n\n‘For the Lord’s love,’ said Mr. Peggotty, falling back, and putting out his hand, as if to keep off what he dreaded. ‘Doen’t tell me his name’s Steerforth!’\n\n‘Mas’r Davy,’ exclaimed Ham, in a broken voice, ‘it ain’t no fault of yourn--and I am far from laying of it to you--but his name is Steerforth, and he’s a damned villain!’\n\nMr. Peggotty uttered no cry, and shed no tear, and moved no more, until he seemed to wake again, all at once, and pulled down his rough coat from its peg in a corner.\n\n‘Bear a hand with this! I’m struck of a heap, and can’t do it,’ he said, impatiently. ‘Bear a hand and help me. Well!’ when somebody had done so. ‘Now give me that theer hat!’\n\nHam asked him whither he was going.\n\n‘I’m a going to seek my niece. I’m a going to seek my Em’ly. I’m a going, first, to stave in that theer boat, and sink it where I would have drownded him, as I’m a living soul, if I had had one thought of what was in him! As he sat afore me,’ he said, wildly, holding out his clenched right hand, ‘as he sat afore me, face to face, strike me down dead, but I’d have drownded him, and thought it right!--I’m a going to seek my niece.’\n\n‘Where?’ cried Ham, interposing himself before the door.\n\n‘Anywhere! I’m a going to seek my niece through the wureld. I’m a going to find my poor niece in her shame, and bring her back. No one stop me! I tell you I’m a going to seek my niece!’\n\n‘No, no!’ cried Mrs. Gummidge, coming between them, in a fit of crying. ‘No, no, Dan’l, not as you are now. Seek her in a little while, my lone lorn Dan’l, and that’ll be but right! but not as you are now. Sit ye down, and give me your forgiveness for having ever been a worrit to you, Dan’l--what have my contraries ever been to this!--and let us speak a word about them times when she was first an orphan, and when Ham was too, and when I was a poor widder woman, and you took me in. It’ll soften your poor heart, Dan’l,’ laying her head upon his shoulder, ‘and you’ll bear your sorrow better; for you know the promise, Dan’l, “As you have done it unto one of the least of these, you have done it unto me”,--and that can never fail under this roof, that’s been our shelter for so many, many year!’\n\nHe was quite passive now; and when I heard him crying, the impulse that had been upon me to go down upon my knees, and ask their pardon for the desolation I had caused, and curse Steerforth, yielded to a better feeling. My overcharged heart found the same relief, and I cried too.\n\n# CHAPTER 32. THE BEGINNING OF A LONG JOURNEY\n\nWhat is natural in me, is natural in many other men, I infer, and so I am not afraid to write that I never had loved Steerforth better than when the ties that bound me to him were broken. In the keen distress of the discovery of his unworthiness, I thought more of all that was brilliant in him, I softened more towards all that was good in him, I did more justice to the qualities that might have made him a man of a noble nature and a great name, than ever I had done in the height of my devotion to him. Deeply as I felt my own unconscious part in his pollution of an honest home, I believed that if I had been brought face to face with him, I could not have uttered one reproach. I should have loved him so well still--though he fascinated me no longer--I should have held in so much tenderness the memory of my affection for him, that I think I should have been as weak as a spirit-wounded child, in all but the entertainment of a thought that we could ever be re-united. That thought I never had. I felt, as he had felt, that all was at an end between us. What his remembrances of me were, I have never known--they were light enough, perhaps, and easily dismissed--but mine of him were as the remembrances of a cherished friend, who was dead.\n\nYes, Steerforth, long removed from the scenes of this poor history! My sorrow may bear involuntary witness against you at the judgement Throne; but my angry thoughts or my reproaches never will, I know!\n\nThe news of what had happened soon spread through the town; insomuch that as I passed along the streets next morning, I overheard the people speaking of it at their doors. Many were hard upon her, some few were hard upon him, but towards her second father and her lover there was but one sentiment. Among all kinds of people a respect for them in their distress prevailed, which was full of gentleness and delicacy. The seafaring men kept apart, when those two were seen early, walking with slow steps on the beach; and stood in knots, talking compassionately among themselves.\n\nIt was on the beach, close down by the sea, that I found them. It would have been easy to perceive that they had not slept all last night, even if Peggotty had failed to tell me of their still sitting just as I left them, when it was broad day. They looked worn; and I thought Mr. Peggotty’s head was bowed in one night more than in all the years I had known him. But they were both as grave and steady as the sea itself, then lying beneath a dark sky, waveless--yet with a heavy roll upon it, as if it breathed in its rest--and touched, on the horizon, with a strip of silvery light from the unseen sun.\n\n‘We have had a mort of talk, sir,’ said Mr. Peggotty to me, when we had all three walked a little while in silence, ‘of what we ought and doen’t ought to do. But we see our course now.’\n\nI happened to glance at Ham, then looking out to sea upon the distant light, and a frightful thought came into my mind--not that his face was angry, for it was not; I recall nothing but an expression of stern determination in it--that if ever he encountered Steerforth, he would kill him.\n\n‘My dooty here, sir,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘is done. I’m a going to seek my--’ he stopped, and went on in a firmer voice: ‘I’m a going to seek her. That’s my dooty evermore.’\n\nHe shook his head when I asked him where he would seek her, and inquired if I were going to London tomorrow? I told him I had not gone today, fearing to lose the chance of being of any service to him; but that I was ready to go when he would.\n\n‘I’ll go along with you, sir,’ he rejoined, ‘if you’re agreeable, tomorrow.’\n\nWe walked again, for a while, in silence.\n\n‘Ham,’ he presently resumed, ‘he’ll hold to his present work, and go and live along with my sister. The old boat yonder--’\n\n‘Will you desert the old boat, Mr. Peggotty?’ I gently interposed.\n\n‘My station, Mas’r Davy,’ he returned, ‘ain’t there no longer; and if ever a boat foundered, since there was darkness on the face of the deep, that one’s gone down. But no, sir, no; I doen’t mean as it should be deserted. Fur from that.’\n\nWe walked again for a while, as before, until he explained:\n\n‘My wishes is, sir, as it shall look, day and night, winter and summer, as it has always looked, since she fust know’d it. If ever she should come a wandering back, I wouldn’t have the old place seem to cast her off, you understand, but seem to tempt her to draw nigher to ‘t, and to peep in, maybe, like a ghost, out of the wind and rain, through the old winder, at the old seat by the fire. Then, maybe, Mas’r Davy, seein’ none but Missis Gummidge there, she might take heart to creep in, trembling; and might come to be laid down in her old bed, and rest her weary head where it was once so gay.’\n\nI could not speak to him in reply, though I tried.\n\n‘Every night,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘as reg’lar as the night comes, the candle must be stood in its old pane of glass, that if ever she should see it, it may seem to say “Come back, my child, come back!” If ever there’s a knock, Ham (partic’ler a soft knock), arter dark, at your aunt’s door, doen’t you go nigh it. Let it be her--not you--that sees my fallen child!’\n\nHe walked a little in front of us, and kept before us for some minutes. During this interval, I glanced at Ham again, and observing the same expression on his face, and his eyes still directed to the distant light, I touched his arm.\n\nTwice I called him by his name, in the tone in which I might have tried to rouse a sleeper, before he heeded me. When I at last inquired on what his thoughts were so bent, he replied:\n\n‘On what’s afore me, Mas’r Davy; and over yon.’ ‘On the life before you, do you mean?’ He had pointed confusedly out to sea.\n\n‘Ay, Mas’r Davy. I doen’t rightly know how ‘tis, but from over yon there seemed to me to come--the end of it like,’ looking at me as if he were waking, but with the same determined face.\n\n‘What end?’ I asked, possessed by my former fear.\n\n‘I doen’t know,’ he said, thoughtfully; ‘I was calling to mind that the beginning of it all did take place here--and then the end come. But it’s gone! Mas’r Davy,’ he added; answering, as I think, my look; ‘you han’t no call to be afeerd of me: but I’m kiender muddled; I don’t fare to feel no matters,’--which was as much as to say that he was not himself, and quite confounded.\n\nMr. Peggotty stopping for us to join him: we did so, and said no more. The remembrance of this, in connexion with my former thought, however, haunted me at intervals, even until the inexorable end came at its appointed time.\n\nWe insensibly approached the old boat, and entered. Mrs. Gummidge, no longer moping in her especial corner, was busy preparing breakfast. She took Mr. Peggotty’s hat, and placed his seat for him, and spoke so comfortably and softly, that I hardly knew her.\n\n‘Dan’l, my good man,’ said she, ‘you must eat and drink, and keep up your strength, for without it you’ll do nowt. Try, that’s a dear soul! An if I disturb you with my clicketten,’ she meant her chattering, ‘tell me so, Dan’l, and I won’t.’\n\nWhen she had served us all, she withdrew to the window, where she sedulously employed herself in repairing some shirts and other clothes belonging to Mr. Peggotty, and neatly folding and packing them in an old oilskin bag, such as sailors carry. Meanwhile, she continued talking, in the same quiet manner:\n\n‘All times and seasons, you know, Dan’l,’ said Mrs. Gummidge, ‘I shall be allus here, and everythink will look accordin’ to your wishes. I’m a poor scholar, but I shall write to you, odd times, when you’re away, and send my letters to Mas’r Davy. Maybe you’ll write to me too, Dan’l, odd times, and tell me how you fare to feel upon your lone lorn journies.’\n\n‘You’ll be a solitary woman heer, I’m afeerd!’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘No, no, Dan’l,’ she returned, ‘I shan’t be that. Doen’t you mind me. I shall have enough to do to keep a Beein for you’ (Mrs. Gummidge meant a home), ‘again you come back--to keep a Beein here for any that may hap to come back, Dan’l. In the fine time, I shall set outside the door as I used to do. If any should come nigh, they shall see the old widder woman true to ‘em, a long way off.’\n\nWhat a change in Mrs. Gummidge in a little time! She was another woman. She was so devoted, she had such a quick perception of what it would be well to say, and what it would be well to leave unsaid; she was so forgetful of herself, and so regardful of the sorrow about her, that I held her in a sort of veneration. The work she did that day! There were many things to be brought up from the beach and stored in the outhouse--as oars, nets, sails, cordage, spars, lobster-pots, bags of ballast, and the like; and though there was abundance of assistance rendered, there being not a pair of working hands on all that shore but would have laboured hard for Mr. Peggotty, and been well paid in being asked to do it, yet she persisted, all day long, in toiling under weights that she was quite unequal to, and fagging to and fro on all sorts of unnecessary errands. As to deploring her misfortunes, she appeared to have entirely lost the recollection of ever having had any. She preserved an equable cheerfulness in the midst of her sympathy, which was not the least astonishing part of the change that had come over her. Querulousness was out of the question. I did not even observe her voice to falter, or a tear to escape from her eyes, the whole day through, until twilight; when she and I and Mr. Peggotty being alone together, and he having fallen asleep in perfect exhaustion, she broke into a half-suppressed fit of sobbing and crying, and taking me to the door, said, ‘Ever bless you, Mas’r Davy, be a friend to him, poor dear!’ Then, she immediately ran out of the house to wash her face, in order that she might sit quietly beside him, and be found at work there, when he should awake. In short I left her, when I went away at night, the prop and staff of Mr. Peggotty’s affliction; and I could not meditate enough upon the lesson that I read in Mrs. Gummidge, and the new experience she unfolded to me.\n\nIt was between nine and ten o’clock when, strolling in a melancholy manner through the town, I stopped at Mr. Omer’s door. Mr. Omer had taken it so much to heart, his daughter told me, that he had been very low and poorly all day, and had gone to bed without his pipe.\n\n‘A deceitful, bad-hearted girl,’ said Mrs. Joram. ‘There was no good in her, ever!’\n\n‘Don’t say so,’ I returned. ‘You don’t think so.’\n\n‘Yes, I do!’ cried Mrs. Joram, angrily.\n‘No, no,’ said I.\n\nMrs. Joram tossed her head, endeavouring to be very stern and cross; but she could not command her softer self, and began to cry. I was young, to be sure; but I thought much the better of her for this sympathy, and fancied it became her, as a virtuous wife and mother, very well indeed.\n\n‘What will she ever do!’ sobbed Minnie. ‘Where will she go! What will become of her! Oh, how could she be so cruel, to herself and him!’\n\nI remembered the time when Minnie was a young and pretty girl; and I was glad she remembered it too, so feelingly.\n\n‘My little Minnie,’ said Mrs. Joram, ‘has only just now been got to sleep. Even in her sleep she is sobbing for Em’ly. All day long, little Minnie has cried for her, and asked me, over and over again, whether Em’ly was wicked? What can I say to her, when Em’ly tied a ribbon off her own neck round little Minnie’s the last night she was here, and laid her head down on the pillow beside her till she was fast asleep! The ribbon’s round my little Minnie’s neck now. It ought not to be, perhaps, but what can I do? Em’ly is very bad, but they were fond of one another. And the child knows nothing!’\n\nMrs. Joram was so unhappy that her husband came out to take care of her. Leaving them together, I went home to Peggotty’s; more melancholy myself, if possible, than I had been yet.\n\nThat good creature--I mean Peggotty--all untired by her late anxieties and sleepless nights, was at her brother’s, where she meant to stay till morning. An old woman, who had been employed about the house for some weeks past, while Peggotty had been unable to attend to it, was the house’s only other occupant besides myself. As I had no occasion for her services, I sent her to bed, by no means against her will, and sat down before the kitchen fire a little while, to think about all this.\n\nI was blending it with the deathbed of the late Mr. Barkis, and was driving out with the tide towards the distance at which Ham had looked so singularly in the morning, when I was recalled from my wanderings by a knock at the door. There was a knocker upon the door, but it was not that which made the sound. The tap was from a hand, and low down upon the door, as if it were given by a child.\n\nIt made me start as much as if it had been the knock of a footman to a person of distinction. I opened the door; and at first looked down, to my amazement, on nothing but a great umbrella that appeared to be walking about of itself. But presently I discovered underneath it, Miss Mowcher.\n\nI might not have been prepared to give the little creature a very kind reception, if, on her removing the umbrella, which her utmost efforts were unable to shut up, she had shown me the ‘volatile’ expression of face which had made so great an impression on me at our first and last meeting. But her face, as she turned it up to mine, was so earnest; and when I relieved her of the umbrella (which would have been an inconvenient one for the Irish Giant), she wrung her little hands in such an afflicted manner; that I rather inclined towards her.\n\n‘Miss Mowcher!’ said I, after glancing up and down the empty street, without distinctly knowing what I expected to see besides; ‘how do you come here? What is the matter?’ She motioned to me with her short right arm, to shut the umbrella for her; and passing me hurriedly, went into the kitchen. When I had closed the door, and followed, with the umbrella in my hand, I found her sitting on the corner of the fender--it was a low iron one, with two flat bars at top to stand plates upon--in the shadow of the boiler, swaying herself backwards and forwards, and chafing her hands upon her knees like a person in pain.\n\nQuite alarmed at being the only recipient of this untimely visit, and the only spectator of this portentous behaviour, I exclaimed again, ‘Pray tell me, Miss Mowcher, what is the matter! are you ill?’\n\n‘My dear young soul,’ returned Miss Mowcher, squeezing her hands upon her heart one over the other. ‘I am ill here, I am very ill. To think that it should come to this, when I might have known it and perhaps prevented it, if I hadn’t been a thoughtless fool!’\n\nAgain her large bonnet (very disproportionate to the figure) went backwards and forwards, in her swaying of her little body to and fro; while a most gigantic bonnet rocked, in unison with it, upon the wall.\n\n‘I am surprised,’ I began, ‘to see you so distressed and serious’--when she interrupted me.\n\n‘Yes, it’s always so!’ she said. ‘They are all surprised, these inconsiderate young people, fairly and full grown, to see any natural feeling in a little thing like me! They make a plaything of me, use me for their amusement, throw me away when they are tired, and wonder that I feel more than a toy horse or a wooden soldier! Yes, yes, that’s the way. The old way!’\n\n‘It may be, with others,’ I returned, ‘but I do assure you it is not with me. Perhaps I ought not to be at all surprised to see you as you are now: I know so little of you. I said, without consideration, what I thought.’\n\n‘What can I do?’ returned the little woman, standing up, and holding out her arms to show herself. ‘See! What I am, my father was; and my sister is; and my brother is. I have worked for sister and brother these many years--hard, Mr. Copperfield--all day. I must live. I do no harm. If there are people so unreflecting or so cruel, as to make a jest of me, what is left for me to do but to make a jest of myself, them, and everything? If I do so, for the time, whose fault is that? Mine?’\n\nNo. Not Miss Mowcher’s, I perceived.\n\n‘If I had shown myself a sensitive dwarf to your false friend,’ pursued the little woman, shaking her head at me, with reproachful earnestness, ‘how much of his help or good will do you think I should ever have had? If little Mowcher (who had no hand, young gentleman, in the making of herself) addressed herself to him, or the like of him, because of her misfortunes, when do you suppose her small voice would have been heard? Little Mowcher would have as much need to live, if she was the bitterest and dullest of pigmies; but she couldn’t do it. No. She might whistle for her bread and butter till she died of Air.’\n\nMiss Mowcher sat down on the fender again, and took out her handkerchief, and wiped her eyes.\n\n‘Be thankful for me, if you have a kind heart, as I think you have,’ she said, ‘that while I know well what I am, I can be cheerful and endure it all. I am thankful for myself, at any rate, that I can find my tiny way through the world, without being beholden to anyone; and that in return for all that is thrown at me, in folly or vanity, as I go along, I can throw bubbles back. If I don’t brood over all I want, it is the better for me, and not the worse for anyone. If I am a plaything for you giants, be gentle with me.’\n\nMiss Mowcher replaced her handkerchief in her pocket, looking at me with very intent expression all the while, and pursued:\n\n‘I saw you in the street just now. You may suppose I am not able to walk as fast as you, with my short legs and short breath, and I couldn’t overtake you; but I guessed where you came, and came after you. I have been here before, today, but the good woman wasn’t at home.’\n\n‘Do you know her?’ I demanded.\n\n‘I know of her, and about her,’ she replied, ‘from Omer and Joram. I was there at seven o’clock this morning. Do you remember what Steerforth said to me about this unfortunate girl, that time when I saw you both at the inn?’\n\nThe great bonnet on Miss Mowcher’s head, and the greater bonnet on the wall, began to go backwards and forwards again when she asked this question.\n\nI remembered very well what she referred to, having had it in my thoughts many times that day. I told her so.\n\n‘May the Father of all Evil confound him,’ said the little woman, holding up her forefinger between me and her sparkling eyes, ‘and ten times more confound that wicked servant; but I believed it was YOU who had a boyish passion for her!’\n\n‘I?’ I repeated.\n\n‘Child, child! In the name of blind ill-fortune,’ cried Miss Mowcher, wringing her hands impatiently, as she went to and fro again upon the fender, ‘why did you praise her so, and blush, and look disturbed?’\n\nI could not conceal from myself that I had done this, though for a reason very different from her supposition.\n\n‘What did I know?’ said Miss Mowcher, taking out her handkerchief again, and giving one little stamp on the ground whenever, at short intervals, she applied it to her eyes with both hands at once. ‘He was crossing you and wheedling you, I saw; and you were soft wax in his hands, I saw. Had I left the room a minute, when his man told me that “Young Innocence” (so he called you, and you may call him “Old Guilt” all the days of your life) had set his heart upon her, and she was giddy and liked him, but his master was resolved that no harm should come of it--more for your sake than for hers--and that that was their business here? How could I BUT believe him? I saw Steerforth soothe and please you by his praise of her! You were the first to mention her name. You owned to an old admiration of her. You were hot and cold, and red and white, all at once when I spoke to you of her. What could I think--what DID I think--but that you were a young libertine in everything but experience, and had fallen into hands that had experience enough, and could manage you (having the fancy) for your own good? Oh! oh! oh! They were afraid of my finding out the truth,’ exclaimed Miss Mowcher, getting off the fender, and trotting up and down the kitchen with her two short arms distressfully lifted up, ‘because I am a sharp little thing--I need be, to get through the world at all!--and they deceived me altogether, and I gave the poor unfortunate girl a letter, which I fully believe was the beginning of her ever speaking to Littimer, who was left behind on purpose!’\n\nI stood amazed at the revelation of all this perfidy, looking at Miss Mowcher as she walked up and down the kitchen until she was out of breath: when she sat upon the fender again, and, drying her face with her handkerchief, shook her head for a long time, without otherwise moving, and without breaking silence.\n\n‘My country rounds,’ she added at length, ‘brought me to Norwich, Mr. Copperfield, the night before last. What I happened to find there, about their secret way of coming and going, without you--which was strange--led to my suspecting something wrong. I got into the coach from London last night, as it came through Norwich, and was here this morning. Oh, oh, oh! too late!’\n\nPoor little Mowcher turned so chilly after all her crying and fretting, that she turned round on the fender, putting her poor little wet feet in among the ashes to warm them, and sat looking at the fire, like a large doll. I sat in a chair on the other side of the hearth, lost in unhappy reflections, and looking at the fire too, and sometimes at her.\n\n‘I must go,’ she said at last, rising as she spoke. ‘It’s late. You don’t mistrust me?’\n\nMeeting her sharp glance, which was as sharp as ever when she asked me, I could not on that short challenge answer no, quite frankly.\n\n‘Come!’ said she, accepting the offer of my hand to help her over the fender, and looking wistfully up into my face, ‘you know you wouldn’t mistrust me, if I was a full-sized woman!’\n\nI felt that there was much truth in this; and I felt rather ashamed of myself.\n\n‘You are a young man,’ she said, nodding. ‘Take a word of advice, even from three foot nothing. Try not to associate bodily defects with mental, my good friend, except for a solid reason.’\n\nShe had got over the fender now, and I had got over my suspicion. I told her that I believed she had given me a faithful account of herself, and that we had both been hapless instruments in designing hands. She thanked me, and said I was a good fellow.\n\n‘Now, mind!’ she exclaimed, turning back on her way to the door, and looking shrewdly at me, with her forefinger up again.--‘I have some reason to suspect, from what I have heard--my ears are always open; I can’t afford to spare what powers I have--that they are gone abroad. But if ever they return, if ever any one of them returns, while I am alive, I am more likely than another, going about as I do, to find it out soon. Whatever I know, you shall know. If ever I can do anything to serve the poor betrayed girl, I will do it faithfully, please Heaven! And Littimer had better have a bloodhound at his back, than little Mowcher!’\n\nI placed implicit faith in this last statement, when I marked the look with which it was accompanied.\n\n‘Trust me no more, but trust me no less, than you would trust a full-sized woman,’ said the little creature, touching me appealingly on the wrist. ‘If ever you see me again, unlike what I am now, and like what I was when you first saw me, observe what company I am in. Call to mind that I am a very helpless and defenceless little thing. Think of me at home with my brother like myself and sister like myself, when my day’s work is done. Perhaps you won’t, then, be very hard upon me, or surprised if I can be distressed and serious. Good night!’\n\nI gave Miss Mowcher my hand, with a very different opinion of her from that which I had hitherto entertained, and opened the door to let her out. It was not a trifling business to get the great umbrella up, and properly balanced in her grasp; but at last I successfully accomplished this, and saw it go bobbing down the street through the rain, without the least appearance of having anybody underneath it, except when a heavier fall than usual from some over-charged water-spout sent it toppling over, on one side, and discovered Miss Mowcher struggling violently to get it right. After making one or two sallies to her relief, which were rendered futile by the umbrella’s hopping on again, like an immense bird, before I could reach it, I came in, went to bed, and slept till morning.\n\nIn the morning I was joined by Mr. Peggotty and by my old nurse, and we went at an early hour to the coach office, where Mrs. Gummidge and Ham were waiting to take leave of us.\n\n‘Mas’r Davy,’ Ham whispered, drawing me aside, while Mr. Peggotty was stowing his bag among the luggage, ‘his life is quite broke up. He doen’t know wheer he’s going; he doen’t know--what’s afore him; he’s bound upon a voyage that’ll last, on and off, all the rest of his days, take my wured for ‘t, unless he finds what he’s a seeking of. I am sure you’ll be a friend to him, Mas’r Davy?’\n\n‘Trust me, I will indeed,’ said I, shaking hands with Ham earnestly.\n\n‘Thankee. Thankee, very kind, sir. One thing furder. I’m in good employ, you know, Mas’r Davy, and I han’t no way now of spending what I gets. Money’s of no use to me no more, except to live. If you can lay it out for him, I shall do my work with a better art. Though as to that, sir,’ and he spoke very steadily and mildly, ‘you’re not to think but I shall work at all times, like a man, and act the best that lays in my power!’\n\nI told him I was well convinced of it; and I hinted that I hoped the time might even come, when he would cease to lead the lonely life he naturally contemplated now.\n\n‘No, sir,’ he said, shaking his head, ‘all that’s past and over with me, sir. No one can never fill the place that’s empty. But you’ll bear in mind about the money, as theer’s at all times some laying by for him?’\n\nReminding him of the fact, that Mr. Peggotty derived a steady, though certainly a very moderate income from the bequest of his late brother-in-law, I promised to do so. We then took leave of each other. I cannot leave him even now, without remembering with a pang, at once his modest fortitude and his great sorrow.\n\nAs to Mrs. Gummidge, if I were to endeavour to describe how she ran down the street by the side of the coach, seeing nothing but Mr. Peggotty on the roof, through the tears she tried to repress, and dashing herself against the people who were coming in the opposite direction, I should enter on a task of some difficulty. Therefore I had better leave her sitting on a baker’s door-step, out of breath, with no shape at all remaining in her bonnet, and one of her shoes off, lying on the pavement at a considerable distance.\n\nWhen we got to our journey’s end, our first pursuit was to look about for a little lodging for Peggotty, where her brother could have a bed. We were so fortunate as to find one, of a very clean and cheap description, over a chandler’s shop, only two streets removed from me. When we had engaged this domicile, I bought some cold meat at an eating-house, and took my fellow-travellers home to tea; a proceeding, I regret to state, which did not meet with Mrs. Crupp’s approval, but quite the contrary. I ought to observe, however, in explanation of that lady’s state of mind, that she was much offended by Peggotty’s tucking up her widow’s gown before she had been ten minutes in the place, and setting to work to dust my bedroom. This Mrs. Crupp regarded in the light of a liberty, and a liberty, she said, was a thing she never allowed.\n\nMr. Peggotty had made a communication to me on the way to London for which I was not unprepared. It was, that he purposed first seeing Mrs. Steerforth. As I felt bound to assist him in this, and also to mediate between them; with the view of sparing the mother’s feelings as much as possible, I wrote to her that night. I told her as mildly as I could what his wrong was, and what my own share in his injury. I said he was a man in very common life, but of a most gentle and upright character; and that I ventured to express a hope that she would not refuse to see him in his heavy trouble. I mentioned two o’clock in the afternoon as the hour of our coming, and I sent the letter myself by the first coach in the morning.\n\nAt the appointed time, we stood at the door--the door of that house where I had been, a few days since, so happy: where my youthful confidence and warmth of heart had been yielded up so freely: which was closed against me henceforth: which was now a waste, a ruin.\n\nNo Littimer appeared. The pleasanter face which had replaced his, on the occasion of my last visit, answered to our summons, and went before us to the drawing-room. Mrs. Steerforth was sitting there. Rosa Dartle glided, as we went in, from another part of the room and stood behind her chair.\n\nI saw, directly, in his mother’s face, that she knew from himself what he had done. It was very pale; and bore the traces of deeper emotion than my letter alone, weakened by the doubts her fondness would have raised upon it, would have been likely to create. I thought her more like him than ever I had thought her; and I felt, rather than saw, that the resemblance was not lost on my companion.\n\nShe sat upright in her arm-chair, with a stately, immovable, passionless air, that it seemed as if nothing could disturb. She looked very steadfastly at Mr. Peggotty when he stood before her; and he looked quite as steadfastly at her. Rosa Dartle’s keen glance comprehended all of us. For some moments not a word was spoken.\n\nShe motioned to Mr. Peggotty to be seated. He said, in a low voice, ‘I shouldn’t feel it nat’ral, ma’am, to sit down in this house. I’d sooner stand.’ And this was succeeded by another silence, which she broke thus:\n\n‘I know, with deep regret, what has brought you here. What do you want of me? What do you ask me to do?’\n\nHe put his hat under his arm, and feeling in his breast for Emily’s letter, took it out, unfolded it, and gave it to her. ‘Please to read that, ma’am. That’s my niece’s hand!’\n\nShe read it, in the same stately and impassive way,--untouched by its contents, as far as I could see,--and returned it to him.\n\n‘“Unless he brings me back a lady,”’ said Mr. Peggotty, tracing out that part with his finger. ‘I come to know, ma’am, whether he will keep his wured?’\n\n‘No,’ she returned.\n\n‘Why not?’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘It is impossible. He would disgrace himself. You cannot fail to know that she is far below him.’\n\n‘Raise her up!’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘She is uneducated and ignorant.’\n\n‘Maybe she’s not; maybe she is,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘I think not, ma’am; but I’m no judge of them things. Teach her better!’\n\n‘Since you oblige me to speak more plainly, which I am very unwilling to do, her humble connexions would render such a thing impossible, if nothing else did.’\n\n‘Hark to this, ma’am,’ he returned, slowly and quietly. ‘You know what it is to love your child. So do I. If she was a hundred times my child, I couldn’t love her more. You doen’t know what it is to lose your child. I do. All the heaps of riches in the wureld would be nowt to me (if they was mine) to buy her back! But, save her from this disgrace, and she shall never be disgraced by us. Not one of us that she’s growed up among, not one of us that’s lived along with her and had her for their all in all, these many year, will ever look upon her pritty face again. We’ll be content to let her be; we’ll be content to think of her, far off, as if she was underneath another sun and sky; we’ll be content to trust her to her husband,--to her little children, p’raps,--and bide the time when all of us shall be alike in quality afore our God!’\n\nThe rugged eloquence with which he spoke, was not devoid of all effect. She still preserved her proud manner, but there was a touch of softness in her voice, as she answered:\n\n‘I justify nothing. I make no counter-accusations. But I am sorry to repeat, it is impossible. Such a marriage would irretrievably blight my son’s career, and ruin his prospects. Nothing is more certain than that it never can take place, and never will. If there is any other compensation--’\n\n‘I am looking at the likeness of the face,’ interrupted Mr. Peggotty, with a steady but a kindling eye, ‘that has looked at me, in my home, at my fireside, in my boat--wheer not?---smiling and friendly, when it was so treacherous, that I go half wild when I think of it. If the likeness of that face don’t turn to burning fire, at the thought of offering money to me for my child’s blight and ruin, it’s as bad. I doen’t know, being a lady’s, but what it’s worse.’\n\nShe changed now, in a moment. An angry flush overspread her features; and she said, in an intolerant manner, grasping the arm-chair tightly with her hands:\n\n‘What compensation can you make to ME for opening such a pit between me and my son? What is your love to mine? What is your separation to ours?’\n\nMiss Dartle softly touched her, and bent down her head to whisper, but she would not hear a word.\n\n‘No, Rosa, not a word! Let the man listen to what I say! My son, who has been the object of my life, to whom its every thought has been devoted, whom I have gratified from a child in every wish, from whom I have had no separate existence since his birth,--to take up in a moment with a miserable girl, and avoid me! To repay my confidence with systematic deception, for her sake, and quit me for her! To set this wretched fancy, against his mother’s claims upon his duty, love, respect, gratitude--claims that every day and hour of his life should have strengthened into ties that nothing could be proof against! Is this no injury?’\n\nAgain Rosa Dartle tried to soothe her; again ineffectually.\n\n‘I say, Rosa, not a word! If he can stake his all upon the lightest object, I can stake my all upon a greater purpose. Let him go where he will, with the means that my love has secured to him! Does he think to reduce me by long absence? He knows his mother very little if he does. Let him put away his whim now, and he is welcome back. Let him not put her away now, and he never shall come near me, living or dying, while I can raise my hand to make a sign against it, unless, being rid of her for ever, he comes humbly to me and begs for my forgiveness. This is my right. This is the acknowledgement I WILL HAVE. This is the separation that there is between us! And is this,’ she added, looking at her visitor with the proud intolerant air with which she had begun, ‘no injury?’\n\nWhile I heard and saw the mother as she said these words, I seemed to hear and see the son, defying them. All that I had ever seen in him of an unyielding, wilful spirit, I saw in her. All the understanding that I had now of his misdirected energy, became an understanding of her character too, and a perception that it was, in its strongest springs, the same.\n\nShe now observed to me, aloud, resuming her former restraint, that it was useless to hear more, or to say more, and that she begged to put an end to the interview. She rose with an air of dignity to leave the room, when Mr. Peggotty signified that it was needless.\n\n‘Doen’t fear me being any hindrance to you, I have no more to say, ma’am,’ he remarked, as he moved towards the door. ‘I come heer with no hope, and I take away no hope. I have done what I thowt should be done, but I never looked fur any good to come of my stan’ning where I do. This has been too evil a house fur me and mine, fur me to be in my right senses and expect it.’\n\nWith this, we departed; leaving her standing by her elbow-chair, a picture of a noble presence and a handsome face.\n\nWe had, on our way out, to cross a paved hall, with glass sides and roof, over which a vine was trained. Its leaves and shoots were green then, and the day being sunny, a pair of glass doors leading to the garden were thrown open. Rosa Dartle, entering this way with a noiseless step, when we were close to them, addressed herself to me:\n\n‘You do well,’ she said, ‘indeed, to bring this fellow here!’\n\nSuch a concentration of rage and scorn as darkened her face, and flashed in her jet-black eyes, I could not have thought compressible even into that face. The scar made by the hammer was, as usual in this excited state of her features, strongly marked. When the throbbing I had seen before, came into it as I looked at her, she absolutely lifted up her hand, and struck it.\n\n‘This is a fellow,’ she said, ‘to champion and bring here, is he not? You are a true man!’\n\n‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘you are surely not so unjust as to condemn ME!’\n\n‘Why do you bring division between these two mad creatures?’ she returned. ‘Don’t you know that they are both mad with their own self-will and pride?’\n\n‘Is it my doing?’ I returned.\n\n‘Is it your doing!’ she retorted. ‘Why do you bring this man here?’\n\n‘He is a deeply-injured man, Miss Dartle,’ I replied. ‘You may not know it.’\n\n‘I know that James Steerforth,’ she said, with her hand on her bosom, as if to prevent the storm that was raging there, from being loud, ‘has a false, corrupt heart, and is a traitor. But what need I know or care about this fellow, and his common niece?’\n\n‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘you deepen the injury. It is sufficient already. I will only say, at parting, that you do him a great wrong.’\n\n‘I do him no wrong,’ she returned. ‘They are a depraved, worthless set. I would have her whipped!’\n\nMr. Peggotty passed on, without a word, and went out at the door.\n\n‘Oh, shame, Miss Dartle! shame!’ I said indignantly. ‘How can you bear to trample on his undeserved affliction!’\n\n‘I would trample on them all,’ she answered. ‘I would have his house pulled down. I would have her branded on the face, dressed in rags, and cast out in the streets to starve. If I had the power to sit in judgement on her, I would see it done. See it done? I would do it! I detest her. If I ever could reproach her with her infamous condition, I would go anywhere to do so. If I could hunt her to her grave, I would. If there was any word of comfort that would be a solace to her in her dying hour, and only I possessed it, I wouldn’t part with it for Life itself.’\n\nThe mere vehemence of her words can convey, I am sensible, but a weak impression of the passion by which she was possessed, and which made itself articulate in her whole figure, though her voice, instead of being raised, was lower than usual. No description I could give of her would do justice to my recollection of her, or to her entire deliverance of herself to her anger. I have seen passion in many forms, but I have never seen it in such a form as that.\n\nWhen I joined Mr. Peggotty, he was walking slowly and thoughtfully down the hill. He told me, as soon as I came up with him, that having now discharged his mind of what he had purposed doing in London, he meant ‘to set out on his travels’, that night. I asked him where he meant to go? He only answered, ‘I’m a going, sir, to seek my niece.’\n\nWe went back to the little lodging over the chandler’s shop, and there I found an opportunity of repeating to Peggotty what he had said to me. She informed me, in return, that he had said the same to her that morning. She knew no more than I did, where he was going, but she thought he had some project shaped out in his mind.\n\nI did not like to leave him, under such circumstances, and we all three dined together off a beefsteak pie--which was one of the many good things for which Peggotty was famous--and which was curiously flavoured on this occasion, I recollect well, by a miscellaneous taste of tea, coffee, butter, bacon, cheese, new loaves, firewood, candles, and walnut ketchup, continually ascending from the shop. After dinner we sat for an hour or so near the window, without talking much; and then Mr. Peggotty got up, and brought his oilskin bag and his stout stick, and laid them on the table.\n\nHe accepted, from his sister’s stock of ready money, a small sum on account of his legacy; barely enough, I should have thought, to keep him for a month. He promised to communicate with me, when anything befell him; and he slung his bag about him, took his hat and stick, and bade us both ‘Good-bye!’\n\n‘All good attend you, dear old woman,’ he said, embracing Peggotty, ‘and you too, Mas’r Davy!’ shaking hands with me. ‘I’m a-going to seek her, fur and wide. If she should come home while I’m away--but ah, that ain’t like to be!--or if I should bring her back, my meaning is, that she and me shall live and die where no one can’t reproach her. If any hurt should come to me, remember that the last words I left for her was, “My unchanged love is with my darling child, and I forgive her!”’\n\nHe said this solemnly, bare-headed; then, putting on his hat, he went down the stairs, and away. We followed to the door. It was a warm, dusty evening, just the time when, in the great main thoroughfare out of which that by-way turned, there was a temporary lull in the eternal tread of feet upon the pavement, and a strong red sunshine. He turned, alone, at the corner of our shady street, into a glow of light, in which we lost him.\n\nRarely did that hour of the evening come, rarely did I wake at night, rarely did I look up at the moon, or stars, or watch the falling rain, or hear the wind, but I thought of his solitary figure toiling on, poor pilgrim, and recalled the words:\n\n‘I’m a going to seek her, fur and wide. If any hurt should come to me, remember that the last words I left for her was, “My unchanged love is with my darling child, and I forgive her!”’\n\n# CHAPTER 33. BLISSFUL\n\nAll this time, I had gone on loving Dora, harder than ever. Her idea was my refuge in disappointment and distress, and made some amends to me, even for the loss of my friend. The more I pitied myself, or pitied others, the more I sought for consolation in the image of Dora. The greater the accumulation of deceit and trouble in the world, the brighter and the purer shone the star of Dora high above the world. I don’t think I had any definite idea where Dora came from, or in what degree she was related to a higher order of beings; but I am quite sure I should have scouted the notion of her being simply human, like any other young lady, with indignation and contempt.\n\nIf I may so express it, I was steeped in Dora. I was not merely over head and ears in love with her, but I was saturated through and through. Enough love might have been wrung out of me, metaphorically speaking, to drown anybody in; and yet there would have remained enough within me, and all over me, to pervade my entire existence.\n\nThe first thing I did, on my own account, when I came back, was to take a night-walk to Norwood, and, like the subject of a venerable riddle of my childhood, to go ‘round and round the house, without ever touching the house’, thinking about Dora. I believe the theme of this incomprehensible conundrum was the moon. No matter what it was, I, the moon-struck slave of Dora, perambulated round and round the house and garden for two hours, looking through crevices in the palings, getting my chin by dint of violent exertion above the rusty nails on the top, blowing kisses at the lights in the windows, and romantically calling on the night, at intervals, to shield my Dora--I don’t exactly know what from, I suppose from fire. Perhaps from mice, to which she had a great objection.\n\nMy love was so much in my mind and it was so natural to me to confide in Peggotty, when I found her again by my side of an evening with the old set of industrial implements, busily making the tour of my wardrobe, that I imparted to her, in a sufficiently roundabout way, my great secret. Peggotty was strongly interested, but I could not get her into my view of the case at all. She was audaciously prejudiced in my favour, and quite unable to understand why I should have any misgivings, or be low-spirited about it. ‘The young lady might think herself well off,’ she observed, ‘to have such a beau. And as to her Pa,’ she said, ‘what did the gentleman expect, for gracious sake!’\n\nI observed, however, that Mr. Spenlow’s proctorial gown and stiff cravat took Peggotty down a little, and inspired her with a greater reverence for the man who was gradually becoming more and more etherealized in my eyes every day, and about whom a reflected radiance seemed to me to beam when he sat erect in Court among his papers, like a little lighthouse in a sea of stationery. And by the by, it used to be uncommonly strange to me to consider, I remember, as I sat in Court too, how those dim old judges and doctors wouldn’t have cared for Dora, if they had known her; how they wouldn’t have gone out of their senses with rapture, if marriage with Dora had been proposed to them; how Dora might have sung, and played upon that glorified guitar, until she led me to the verge of madness, yet not have tempted one of those slow-goers an inch out of his road!\n\nI despised them, to a man. Frozen-out old gardeners in the flower-beds of the heart, I took a personal offence against them all. The Bench was nothing to me but an insensible blunderer. The Bar had no more tenderness or poetry in it, than the bar of a public-house.\n\nTaking the management of Peggotty’s affairs into my own hands, with no little pride, I proved the will, and came to a settlement with the Legacy Duty-office, and took her to the Bank, and soon got everything into an orderly train. We varied the legal character of these proceedings by going to see some perspiring Wax-work, in Fleet Street (melted, I should hope, these twenty years); and by visiting Miss Linwood’s Exhibition, which I remember as a Mausoleum of needlework, favourable to self-examination and repentance; and by inspecting the Tower of London; and going to the top of St. Paul’s. All these wonders afforded Peggotty as much pleasure as she was able to enjoy, under existing circumstances: except, I think, St. Paul’s, which, from her long attachment to her work-box, became a rival of the picture on the lid, and was, in some particulars, vanquished, she considered, by that work of art.\n\nPeggotty’s business, which was what we used to call ‘common-form business’ in the Commons (and very light and lucrative the common-form business was), being settled, I took her down to the office one morning to pay her bill. Mr. Spenlow had stepped out, old Tiffey said, to get a gentleman sworn for a marriage licence; but as I knew he would be back directly, our place lying close to the Surrogate’s, and to the Vicar-General’s office too, I told Peggotty to wait.\n\nWe were a little like undertakers, in the Commons, as regarded Probate transactions; generally making it a rule to look more or less cut up, when we had to deal with clients in mourning. In a similar feeling of delicacy, we were always blithe and light-hearted with the licence clients. Therefore I hinted to Peggotty that she would find Mr. Spenlow much recovered from the shock of Mr. Barkis’s decease; and indeed he came in like a bridegroom.\n\nBut neither Peggotty nor I had eyes for him, when we saw, in company with him, Mr. Murdstone. He was very little changed. His hair looked as thick, and was certainly as black, as ever; and his glance was as little to be trusted as of old.\n\n‘Ah, Copperfield?’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘You know this gentleman, I believe?’\n\nI made my gentleman a distant bow, and Peggotty barely recognized him. He was, at first, somewhat disconcerted to meet us two together; but quickly decided what to do, and came up to me.\n\n‘I hope,’ he said, ‘that you are doing well?’\n\n‘It can hardly be interesting to you,’ said I. ‘Yes, if you wish to know.’\n\nWe looked at each other, and he addressed himself to Peggotty.\n\n‘And you,’ said he. ‘I am sorry to observe that you have lost your husband.’\n\n‘It’s not the first loss I have had in my life, Mr. Murdstone,’ replied Peggotty, trembling from head to foot. ‘I am glad to hope that there is nobody to blame for this one,--nobody to answer for it.’\n\n‘Ha!’ said he; ‘that’s a comfortable reflection. You have done your duty?’\n\n‘I have not worn anybody’s life away,’ said Peggotty, ‘I am thankful to think! No, Mr. Murdstone, I have not worrited and frightened any sweet creetur to an early grave!’\n\nHe eyed her gloomily--remorsefully I thought--for an instant; and said, turning his head towards me, but looking at my feet instead of my face:\n\n‘We are not likely to encounter soon again;--a source of satisfaction to us both, no doubt, for such meetings as this can never be agreeable. I do not expect that you, who always rebelled against my just authority, exerted for your benefit and reformation, should owe me any good-will now. There is an antipathy between us--’\n\n‘An old one, I believe?’ said I, interrupting him.\n\nHe smiled, and shot as evil a glance at me as could come from his dark eyes.\n\n‘It rankled in your baby breast,’ he said. ‘It embittered the life of your poor mother. You are right. I hope you may do better, yet; I hope you may correct yourself.’\n\nHere he ended the dialogue, which had been carried on in a low voice, in a corner of the outer office, by passing into Mr. Spenlow’s room, and saying aloud, in his smoothest manner:\n\n‘Gentlemen of Mr. Spenlow’s profession are accustomed to family differences, and know how complicated and difficult they always are!’ With that, he paid the money for his licence; and, receiving it neatly folded from Mr. Spenlow, together with a shake of the hand, and a polite wish for his happiness and the lady’s, went out of the office.\n\nI might have had more difficulty in constraining myself to be silent under his words, if I had had less difficulty in impressing upon Peggotty (who was only angry on my account, good creature!) that we were not in a place for recrimination, and that I besought her to hold her peace. She was so unusually roused, that I was glad to compound for an affectionate hug, elicited by this revival in her mind of our old injuries, and to make the best I could of it, before Mr. Spenlow and the clerks.\n\nMr. Spenlow did not appear to know what the connexion between Mr. Murdstone and myself was; which I was glad of, for I could not bear to acknowledge him, even in my own breast, remembering what I did of the history of my poor mother. Mr. Spenlow seemed to think, if he thought anything about the matter, that my aunt was the leader of the state party in our family, and that there was a rebel party commanded by somebody else--so I gathered at least from what he said, while we were waiting for Mr. Tiffey to make out Peggotty’s bill of costs.\n\n‘Miss Trotwood,’ he remarked, ‘is very firm, no doubt, and not likely to give way to opposition. I have an admiration for her character, and I may congratulate you, Copperfield, on being on the right side. Differences between relations are much to be deplored--but they are extremely general--and the great thing is, to be on the right side’: meaning, I take it, on the side of the moneyed interest.\n\n‘Rather a good marriage this, I believe?’ said Mr. Spenlow.\n\nI explained that I knew nothing about it.\n\n‘Indeed!’ he said. ‘Speaking from the few words Mr. Murdstone dropped--as a man frequently does on these occasions--and from what Miss Murdstone let fall, I should say it was rather a good marriage.’\n\n‘Do you mean that there is money, sir?’ I asked.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mr. Spenlow, ‘I understand there’s money. Beauty too, I am told.’\n\n‘Indeed! Is his new wife young?’\n\n‘Just of age,’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘So lately, that I should think they had been waiting for that.’\n\n‘Lord deliver her!’ said Peggotty. So very emphatically and unexpectedly, that we were all three discomposed; until Tiffey came in with the bill.\n\nOld Tiffey soon appeared, however, and handed it to Mr. Spenlow, to look over. Mr. Spenlow, settling his chin in his cravat and rubbing it softly, went over the items with a deprecatory air--as if it were all Jorkins’s doing--and handed it back to Tiffey with a bland sigh.\n\n‘Yes,’ he said. ‘That’s right. Quite right. I should have been extremely happy, Copperfield, to have limited these charges to the actual expenditure out of pocket, but it is an irksome incident in my professional life, that I am not at liberty to consult my own wishes. I have a partner--Mr. Jorkins.’\n\nAs he said this with a gentle melancholy, which was the next thing to making no charge at all, I expressed my acknowledgements on Peggotty’s behalf, and paid Tiffey in banknotes. Peggotty then retired to her lodging, and Mr. Spenlow and I went into Court, where we had a divorce-suit coming on, under an ingenious little statute (repealed now, I believe, but in virtue of which I have seen several marriages annulled), of which the merits were these. The husband, whose name was Thomas Benjamin, had taken out his marriage licence as Thomas only; suppressing the Benjamin, in case he should not find himself as comfortable as he expected. NOT finding himself as comfortable as he expected, or being a little fatigued with his wife, poor fellow, he now came forward, by a friend, after being married a year or two, and declared that his name was Thomas Benjamin, and therefore he was not married at all. Which the Court confirmed, to his great satisfaction.\n\nI must say that I had my doubts about the strict justice of this, and was not even frightened out of them by the bushel of wheat which reconciles all anomalies. But Mr. Spenlow argued the matter with me. He said, Look at the world, there was good and evil in that; look at the ecclesiastical law, there was good and evil in THAT. It was all part of a system. Very good. There you were!\n\nI had not the hardihood to suggest to Dora’s father that possibly we might even improve the world a little, if we got up early in the morning, and took off our coats to the work; but I confessed that I thought we might improve the Commons. Mr. Spenlow replied that he would particularly advise me to dismiss that idea from my mind, as not being worthy of my gentlemanly character; but that he would be glad to hear from me of what improvement I thought the Commons susceptible?\n\nTaking that part of the Commons which happened to be nearest to us--for our man was unmarried by this time, and we were out of Court, and strolling past the Prerogative Office--I submitted that I thought the Prerogative Office rather a queerly managed institution. Mr. Spenlow inquired in what respect? I replied, with all due deference to his experience (but with more deference, I am afraid, to his being Dora’s father), that perhaps it was a little nonsensical that the Registry of that Court, containing the original wills of all persons leaving effects within the immense province of Canterbury, for three whole centuries, should be an accidental building, never designed for the purpose, leased by the registrars for their Own private emolument, unsafe, not even ascertained to be fire-proof, choked with the important documents it held, and positively, from the roof to the basement, a mercenary speculation of the registrars, who took great fees from the public, and crammed the public’s wills away anyhow and anywhere, having no other object than to get rid of them cheaply. That, perhaps, it was a little unreasonable that these registrars in the receipt of profits amounting to eight or nine thousand pounds a year (to say nothing of the profits of the deputy registrars, and clerks of seats), should not be obliged to spend a little of that money, in finding a reasonably safe place for the important documents which all classes of people were compelled to hand over to them, whether they would or no. That, perhaps, it was a little unjust, that all the great offices in this great office should be magnificent sinecures, while the unfortunate working-clerks in the cold dark room upstairs were the worst rewarded, and the least considered men, doing important services, in London. That perhaps it was a little indecent that the principal registrar of all, whose duty it was to find the public, constantly resorting to this place, all needful accommodation, should be an enormous sinecurist in virtue of that post (and might be, besides, a clergyman, a pluralist, the holder of a staff in a cathedral, and what not),--while the public was put to the inconvenience of which we had a specimen every afternoon when the office was busy, and which we knew to be quite monstrous. That, perhaps, in short, this Prerogative Office of the diocese of Canterbury was altogether such a pestilent job, and such a pernicious absurdity, that but for its being squeezed away in a corner of St. Paul’s Churchyard, which few people knew, it must have been turned completely inside out, and upside down, long ago.\n\nMr. Spenlow smiled as I became modestly warm on the subject, and then argued this question with me as he had argued the other. He said, what was it after all? It was a question of feeling. If the public felt that their wills were in safe keeping, and took it for granted that the office was not to be made better, who was the worse for it? Nobody. Who was the better for it? All the Sinecurists. Very well. Then the good predominated. It might not be a perfect system; nothing was perfect; but what he objected to, was, the insertion of the wedge. Under the Prerogative Office, the country had been glorious. Insert the wedge into the Prerogative Office, and the country would cease to be glorious. He considered it the principle of a gentleman to take things as he found them; and he had no doubt the Prerogative Office would last our time. I deferred to his opinion, though I had great doubts of it myself. I find he was right, however; for it has not only lasted to the present moment, but has done so in the teeth of a great parliamentary report made (not too willingly) eighteen years ago, when all these objections of mine were set forth in detail, and when the existing stowage for wills was described as equal to the accumulation of only two years and a half more. What they have done with them since; whether they have lost many, or whether they sell any, now and then, to the butter shops; I don’t know. I am glad mine is not there, and I hope it may not go there, yet awhile.\n\nI have set all this down, in my present blissful chapter, because here it comes into its natural place. Mr. Spenlow and I falling into this conversation, prolonged it and our saunter to and fro, until we diverged into general topics. And so it came about, in the end, that Mr. Spenlow told me this day week was Dora’s birthday, and he would be glad if I would come down and join a little picnic on the occasion. I went out of my senses immediately; became a mere driveller next day, on receipt of a little lace-edged sheet of note-paper, ‘Favoured by papa. To remind’; and passed the intervening period in a state of dotage.\n\nI think I committed every possible absurdity in the way of preparation for this blessed event. I turn hot when I remember the cravat I bought. My boots might be placed in any collection of instruments of torture. I provided, and sent down by the Norwood coach the night before, a delicate little hamper, amounting in itself, I thought, almost to a declaration. There were crackers in it with the tenderest mottoes that could be got for money. At six in the morning, I was in Covent Garden Market, buying a bouquet for Dora. At ten I was on horseback (I hired a gallant grey, for the occasion), with the bouquet in my hat, to keep it fresh, trotting down to Norwood.\n\nI suppose that when I saw Dora in the garden and pretended not to see her, and rode past the house pretending to be anxiously looking for it, I committed two small fooleries which other young gentlemen in my circumstances might have committed--because they came so very natural to me. But oh! when I DID find the house, and DID dismount at the garden-gate, and drag those stony-hearted boots across the lawn to Dora sitting on a garden-seat under a lilac tree, what a spectacle she was, upon that beautiful morning, among the butterflies, in a white chip bonnet and a dress of celestial blue! There was a young lady with her--comparatively stricken in years--almost twenty, I should say. Her name was Miss Mills. And Dora called her Julia. She was the bosom friend of Dora. Happy Miss Mills!\n\nJip was there, and Jip WOULD bark at me again. When I presented my bouquet, he gnashed his teeth with jealousy. Well he might. If he had the least idea how I adored his mistress, well he might!\n\n‘Oh, thank you, Mr. Copperfield! What dear flowers!’ said Dora.\n\nI had had an intention of saying (and had been studying the best form of words for three miles) that I thought them beautiful before I saw them so near HER. But I couldn’t manage it. She was too bewildering. To see her lay the flowers against her little dimpled chin, was to lose all presence of mind and power of language in a feeble ecstasy. I wonder I didn’t say, ‘Kill me, if you have a heart, Miss Mills. Let me die here!’\n\nThen Dora held my flowers to Jip to smell. Then Jip growled, and wouldn’t smell them. Then Dora laughed, and held them a little closer to Jip, to make him. Then Jip laid hold of a bit of geranium with his teeth, and worried imaginary cats in it. Then Dora beat him, and pouted, and said, ‘My poor beautiful flowers!’ as compassionately, I thought, as if Jip had laid hold of me. I wished he had!\n\n‘You’ll be so glad to hear, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Dora, ‘that that cross Miss Murdstone is not here. She has gone to her brother’s marriage, and will be away at least three weeks. Isn’t that delightful?’\n\nI said I was sure it must be delightful to her, and all that was delightful to her was delightful to me. Miss Mills, with an air of superior wisdom and benevolence, smiled upon us.\n\n‘She is the most disagreeable thing I ever saw,’ said Dora. ‘You can’t believe how ill-tempered and shocking she is, Julia.’\n\n‘Yes, I can, my dear!’ said Julia.\n\n‘YOU can, perhaps, love,’ returned Dora, with her hand on Julia’s. ‘Forgive my not excepting you, my dear, at first.’\n\nI learnt, from this, that Miss Mills had had her trials in the course of a chequered existence; and that to these, perhaps, I might refer that wise benignity of manner which I had already noticed. I found, in the course of the day, that this was the case: Miss Mills having been unhappy in a misplaced affection, and being understood to have retired from the world on her awful stock of experience, but still to take a calm interest in the unblighted hopes and loves of youth.\n\nBut now Mr. Spenlow came out of the house, and Dora went to him, saying, ‘Look, papa, what beautiful flowers!’ And Miss Mills smiled thoughtfully, as who should say, ‘Ye Mayflies, enjoy your brief existence in the bright morning of life!’ And we all walked from the lawn towards the carriage, which was getting ready.\n\nI shall never have such a ride again. I have never had such another. There were only those three, their hamper, my hamper, and the guitar-case, in the phaeton; and, of course, the phaeton was open; and I rode behind it, and Dora sat with her back to the horses, looking towards me. She kept the bouquet close to her on the cushion, and wouldn’t allow Jip to sit on that side of her at all, for fear he should crush it. She often carried it in her hand, often refreshed herself with its fragrance. Our eyes at those times often met; and my great astonishment is that I didn’t go over the head of my gallant grey into the carriage.\n\nThere was dust, I believe. There was a good deal of dust, I believe. I have a faint impression that Mr. Spenlow remonstrated with me for riding in it; but I knew of none. I was sensible of a mist of love and beauty about Dora, but of nothing else. He stood up sometimes, and asked me what I thought of the prospect. I said it was delightful, and I dare say it was; but it was all Dora to me. The sun shone Dora, and the birds sang Dora. The south wind blew Dora, and the wild flowers in the hedges were all Doras, to a bud. My comfort is, Miss Mills understood me. Miss Mills alone could enter into my feelings thoroughly.\n\nI don’t know how long we were going, and to this hour I know as little where we went. Perhaps it was near Guildford. Perhaps some Arabian-night magician, opened up the place for the day, and shut it up for ever when we came away. It was a green spot, on a hill, carpeted with soft turf. There were shady trees, and heather, and, as far as the eye could see, a rich landscape.\n\nIt was a trying thing to find people here, waiting for us; and my jealousy, even of the ladies, knew no bounds. But all of my own sex--especially one impostor, three or four years my elder, with a red whisker, on which he established an amount of presumption not to be endured--were my mortal foes.\n\nWe all unpacked our baskets, and employed ourselves in getting dinner ready. Red Whisker pretended he could make a salad (which I don’t believe), and obtruded himself on public notice. Some of the young ladies washed the lettuces for him, and sliced them under his directions. Dora was among these. I felt that fate had pitted me against this man, and one of us must fall.\n\nRed Whisker made his salad (I wondered how they could eat it. Nothing should have induced ME to touch it!) and voted himself into the charge of the wine-cellar, which he constructed, being an ingenious beast, in the hollow trunk of a tree. By and by, I saw him, with the majority of a lobster on his plate, eating his dinner at the feet of Dora!\n\nI have but an indistinct idea of what happened for some time after this baleful object presented itself to my view. I was very merry, I know; but it was hollow merriment. I attached myself to a young creature in pink, with little eyes, and flirted with her desperately. She received my attentions with favour; but whether on my account solely, or because she had any designs on Red Whisker, I can’t say. Dora’s health was drunk. When I drank it, I affected to interrupt my conversation for that purpose, and to resume it immediately afterwards. I caught Dora’s eye as I bowed to her, and I thought it looked appealing. But it looked at me over the head of Red Whisker, and I was adamant.\n\nThe young creature in pink had a mother in green; and I rather think the latter separated us from motives of policy. Howbeit, there was a general breaking up of the party, while the remnants of the dinner were being put away; and I strolled off by myself among the trees, in a raging and remorseful state. I was debating whether I should pretend that I was not well, and fly--I don’t know where--upon my gallant grey, when Dora and Miss Mills met me.\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ said Miss Mills, ‘you are dull.’\n\nI begged her pardon. Not at all.\n\n‘And Dora,’ said Miss Mills, ‘YOU are dull.’\n\nOh dear no! Not in the least.\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield and Dora,’ said Miss Mills, with an almost venerable air. ‘Enough of this. Do not allow a trivial misunderstanding to wither the blossoms of spring, which, once put forth and blighted, cannot be renewed. I speak,’ said Miss Mills, ‘from experience of the past--the remote, irrevocable past. The gushing fountains which sparkle in the sun, must not be stopped in mere caprice; the oasis in the desert of Sahara must not be plucked up idly.’\n\nI hardly knew what I did, I was burning all over to that extraordinary extent; but I took Dora’s little hand and kissed it--and she let me! I kissed Miss Mills’s hand; and we all seemed, to my thinking, to go straight up to the seventh heaven. We did not come down again. We stayed up there all the evening. At first we strayed to and fro among the trees: I with Dora’s shy arm drawn through mine: and Heaven knows, folly as it all was, it would have been a happy fate to have been struck immortal with those foolish feelings, and have stayed among the trees for ever!\n\nBut, much too soon, we heard the others laughing and talking, and calling ‘where’s Dora?’ So we went back, and they wanted Dora to sing. Red Whisker would have got the guitar-case out of the carriage, but Dora told him nobody knew where it was, but I. So Red Whisker was done for in a moment; and I got it, and I unlocked it, and I took the guitar out, and I sat by her, and I held her handkerchief and gloves, and I drank in every note of her dear voice, and she sang to ME who loved her, and all the others might applaud as much as they liked, but they had nothing to do with it!\n\nI was intoxicated with joy. I was afraid it was too happy to be real, and that I should wake in Buckingham Street presently, and hear Mrs. Crupp clinking the teacups in getting breakfast ready. But Dora sang, and others sang, and Miss Mills sang--about the slumbering echoes in the caverns of Memory; as if she were a hundred years old--and the evening came on; and we had tea, with the kettle boiling gipsy-fashion; and I was still as happy as ever.\n\nI was happier than ever when the party broke up, and the other people, defeated Red Whisker and all, went their several ways, and we went ours through the still evening and the dying light, with sweet scents rising up around us. Mr. Spenlow being a little drowsy after the champagne--honour to the soil that grew the grape, to the grape that made the wine, to the sun that ripened it, and to the merchant who adulterated it!--and being fast asleep in a corner of the carriage, I rode by the side and talked to Dora. She admired my horse and patted him--oh, what a dear little hand it looked upon a horse!--and her shawl would not keep right, and now and then I drew it round her with my arm; and I even fancied that Jip began to see how it was, and to understand that he must make up his mind to be friends with me.\n\nThat sagacious Miss Mills, too; that amiable, though quite used up, recluse; that little patriarch of something less than twenty, who had done with the world, and mustn’t on any account have the slumbering echoes in the caverns of Memory awakened; what a kind thing she did!\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ said Miss Mills, ‘come to this side of the carriage a moment--if you can spare a moment. I want to speak to you.’\n\nBehold me, on my gallant grey, bending at the side of Miss Mills, with my hand upon the carriage door!\n\n‘Dora is coming to stay with me. She is coming home with me the day after tomorrow. If you would like to call, I am sure papa would be happy to see you.’ What could I do but invoke a silent blessing on Miss Mills’s head, and store Miss Mills’s address in the securest corner of my memory! What could I do but tell Miss Mills, with grateful looks and fervent words, how much I appreciated her good offices, and what an inestimable value I set upon her friendship!\n\nThen Miss Mills benignantly dismissed me, saying, ‘Go back to Dora!’ and I went; and Dora leaned out of the carriage to talk to me, and we talked all the rest of the way; and I rode my gallant grey so close to the wheel that I grazed his near fore leg against it, and ‘took the bark off’, as his owner told me, ‘to the tune of three pun’ sivin’--which I paid, and thought extremely cheap for so much joy. What time Miss Mills sat looking at the moon, murmuring verses--and recalling, I suppose, the ancient days when she and earth had anything in common.\n\nNorwood was many miles too near, and we reached it many hours too soon; but Mr. Spenlow came to himself a little short of it, and said, ‘You must come in, Copperfield, and rest!’ and I consenting, we had sandwiches and wine-and-water. In the light room, Dora blushing looked so lovely, that I could not tear myself away, but sat there staring, in a dream, until the snoring of Mr. Spenlow inspired me with sufficient consciousness to take my leave. So we parted; I riding all the way to London with the farewell touch of Dora’s hand still light on mine, recalling every incident and word ten thousand times; lying down in my own bed at last, as enraptured a young noodle as ever was carried out of his five wits by love.\n\nWhen I awoke next morning, I was resolute to declare my passion to Dora, and know my fate. Happiness or misery was now the question. There was no other question that I knew of in the world, and only Dora could give the answer to it. I passed three days in a luxury of wretchedness, torturing myself by putting every conceivable variety of discouraging construction on all that ever had taken place between Dora and me. At last, arrayed for the purpose at a vast expense, I went to Miss Mills’s, fraught with a declaration.\n\nHow many times I went up and down the street, and round the square--painfully aware of being a much better answer to the old riddle than the original one--before I could persuade myself to go up the steps and knock, is no matter now. Even when, at last, I had knocked, and was waiting at the door, I had some flurried thought of asking if that were Mr. Blackboy’s (in imitation of poor Barkis), begging pardon, and retreating. But I kept my ground.\n\nMr. Mills was not at home. I did not expect he would be. Nobody wanted HIM. Miss Mills was at home. Miss Mills would do.\n\nI was shown into a room upstairs, where Miss Mills and Dora were. Jip was there. Miss Mills was copying music (I recollect, it was a new song, called ‘Affection’s Dirge’), and Dora was painting flowers. What were my feelings, when I recognized my own flowers; the identical Covent Garden Market purchase! I cannot say that they were very like, or that they particularly resembled any flowers that have ever come under my observation; but I knew from the paper round them which was accurately copied, what the composition was.\n\nMiss Mills was very glad to see me, and very sorry her papa was not at home: though I thought we all bore that with fortitude. Miss Mills was conversational for a few minutes, and then, laying down her pen upon ‘Affection’s Dirge’, got up, and left the room.\n\nI began to think I would put it off till tomorrow.\n\n‘I hope your poor horse was not tired, when he got home at night,’ said Dora, lifting up her beautiful eyes. ‘It was a long way for him.’\n\nI began to think I would do it today.\n\n‘It was a long way for him,’ said I, ‘for he had nothing to uphold him on the journey.’\n\n‘Wasn’t he fed, poor thing?’ asked Dora.\n\nI began to think I would put it off till tomorrow.\n\n‘Ye-yes,’ I said, ‘he was well taken care of. I mean he had not the unutterable happiness that I had in being so near you.’\n\nDora bent her head over her drawing and said, after a little while--I had sat, in the interval, in a burning fever, and with my legs in a very rigid state--\n\n‘You didn’t seem to be sensible of that happiness yourself, at one time of the day.’\n\nI saw now that I was in for it, and it must be done on the spot.\n\n‘You didn’t care for that happiness in the least,’ said Dora, slightly raising her eyebrows, and shaking her head, ‘when you were sitting by Miss Kitt.’\n\nKitt, I should observe, was the name of the creature in pink, with the little eyes.\n\n‘Though certainly I don’t know why you should,’ said Dora, ‘or why you should call it a happiness at all. But of course you don’t mean what you say. And I am sure no one doubts your being at liberty to do whatever you like. Jip, you naughty boy, come here!’\n\nI don’t know how I did it. I did it in a moment. I intercepted Jip. I had Dora in my arms. I was full of eloquence. I never stopped for a word. I told her how I loved her. I told her I should die without her. I told her that I idolized and worshipped her. Jip barked madly all the time.\n\nWhen Dora hung her head and cried, and trembled, my eloquence increased so much the more. If she would like me to die for her, she had but to say the word, and I was ready. Life without Dora’s love was not a thing to have on any terms. I couldn’t bear it, and I wouldn’t. I had loved her every minute, day and night, since I first saw her. I loved her at that minute to distraction. I should always love her, every minute, to distraction. Lovers had loved before, and lovers would love again; but no lover had loved, might, could, would, or should ever love, as I loved Dora. The more I raved, the more Jip barked. Each of us, in his own way, got more mad every moment.\n\nWell, well! Dora and I were sitting on the sofa by and by, quiet enough, and Jip was lying in her lap, winking peacefully at me. It was off my mind. I was in a state of perfect rapture. Dora and I were engaged.\n\nI suppose we had some notion that this was to end in marriage. We must have had some, because Dora stipulated that we were never to be married without her papa’s consent. But, in our youthful ecstasy, I don’t think that we really looked before us or behind us; or had any aspiration beyond the ignorant present. We were to keep our secret from Mr. Spenlow; but I am sure the idea never entered my head, then, that there was anything dishonourable in that.\n\nMiss Mills was more than usually pensive when Dora, going to find her, brought her back;--I apprehend, because there was a tendency in what had passed to awaken the slumbering echoes in the caverns of Memory. But she gave us her blessing, and the assurance of her lasting friendship, and spoke to us, generally, as became a Voice from the Cloister.\n\nWhat an idle time it was! What an insubstantial, happy, foolish time it was!\n\nWhen I measured Dora’s finger for a ring that was to be made of Forget-me-nots, and when the jeweller, to whom I took the measure, found me out, and laughed over his order-book, and charged me anything he liked for the pretty little toy, with its blue stones--so associated in my remembrance with Dora’s hand, that yesterday, when I saw such another, by chance, on the finger of my own daughter, there was a momentary stirring in my heart, like pain!\n\nWhen I walked about, exalted with my secret, and full of my own interest, and felt the dignity of loving Dora, and of being beloved, so much, that if I had walked the air, I could not have been more above the people not so situated, who were creeping on the earth!\n\nWhen we had those meetings in the garden of the square, and sat within the dingy summer-house, so happy, that I love the London sparrows to this hour, for nothing else, and see the plumage of the tropics in their smoky feathers! When we had our first great quarrel (within a week of our betrothal), and when Dora sent me back the ring, enclosed in a despairing cocked-hat note, wherein she used the terrible expression that ‘our love had begun in folly, and ended in madness!’ which dreadful words occasioned me to tear my hair, and cry that all was over!\n\nWhen, under cover of the night, I flew to Miss Mills, whom I saw by stealth in a back kitchen where there was a mangle, and implored Miss Mills to interpose between us and avert insanity. When Miss Mills undertook the office and returned with Dora, exhorting us, from the pulpit of her own bitter youth, to mutual concession, and the avoidance of the Desert of Sahara!\n\nWhen we cried, and made it up, and were so blest again, that the back kitchen, mangle and all, changed to Love’s own temple, where we arranged a plan of correspondence through Miss Mills, always to comprehend at least one letter on each side every day!\n\nWhat an idle time! What an insubstantial, happy, foolish time! Of all the times of mine that Time has in his grip, there is none that in one retrospect I can smile at half so much, and think of half so tenderly.\n\n# CHAPTER 34. MY AUNT ASTONISHES ME\n\nI wrote to Agnes as soon as Dora and I were engaged. I wrote her a long letter, in which I tried to make her comprehend how blest I was, and what a darling Dora was. I entreated Agnes not to regard this as a thoughtless passion which could ever yield to any other, or had the least resemblance to the boyish fancies that we used to joke about. I assured her that its profundity was quite unfathomable, and expressed my belief that nothing like it had ever been known.\n\nSomehow, as I wrote to Agnes on a fine evening by my open window, and the remembrance of her clear calm eyes and gentle face came stealing over me, it shed such a peaceful influence upon the hurry and agitation in which I had been living lately, and of which my very happiness partook in some degree, that it soothed me into tears. I remember that I sat resting my head upon my hand, when the letter was half done, cherishing a general fancy as if Agnes were one of the elements of my natural home. As if, in the retirement of the house made almost sacred to me by her presence, Dora and I must be happier than anywhere. As if, in love, joy, sorrow, hope, or disappointment; in all emotions; my heart turned naturally there, and found its refuge and best friend.\n\nOf Steerforth I said nothing. I only told her there had been sad grief at Yarmouth, on account of Emily’s flight; and that on me it made a double wound, by reason of the circumstances attending it. I knew how quick she always was to divine the truth, and that she would never be the first to breathe his name.\n\nTo this letter, I received an answer by return of post. As I read it, I seemed to hear Agnes speaking to me. It was like her cordial voice in my ears. What can I say more!\n\nWhile I had been away from home lately, Traddles had called twice or thrice. Finding Peggotty within, and being informed by Peggotty (who always volunteered that information to whomsoever would receive it), that she was my old nurse, he had established a good-humoured acquaintance with her, and had stayed to have a little chat with her about me. So Peggotty said; but I am afraid the chat was all on her own side, and of immoderate length, as she was very difficult indeed to stop, God bless her! when she had me for her theme.\n\nThis reminds me, not only that I expected Traddles on a certain afternoon of his own appointing, which was now come, but that Mrs. Crupp had resigned everything appertaining to her office (the salary excepted) until Peggotty should cease to present herself. Mrs. Crupp, after holding divers conversations respecting Peggotty, in a very high-pitched voice, on the staircase--with some invisible Familiar it would appear, for corporeally speaking she was quite alone at those times--addressed a letter to me, developing her views. Beginning it with that statement of universal application, which fitted every occurrence of her life, namely, that she was a mother herself, she went on to inform me that she had once seen very different days, but that at all periods of her existence she had had a constitutional objection to spies, intruders, and informers. She named no names, she said; let them the cap fitted, wear it; but spies, intruders, and informers, especially in widders’ weeds (this clause was underlined), she had ever accustomed herself to look down upon. If a gentleman was the victim of spies, intruders, and informers (but still naming no names), that was his own pleasure. He had a right to please himself; so let him do. All that she, Mrs. Crupp, stipulated for, was, that she should not be ‘brought in contract’ with such persons. Therefore she begged to be excused from any further attendance on the top set, until things were as they formerly was, and as they could be wished to be; and further mentioned that her little book would be found upon the breakfast-table every Saturday morning, when she requested an immediate settlement of the same, with the benevolent view of saving trouble ‘and an ill-conwenience’ to all parties.\n\nAfter this, Mrs. Crupp confined herself to making pitfalls on the stairs, principally with pitchers, and endeavouring to delude Peggotty into breaking her legs. I found it rather harassing to live in this state of siege, but was too much afraid of Mrs. Crupp to see any way out of it.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ cried Traddles, punctually appearing at my door, in spite of all these obstacles, ‘how do you do?’\n\n‘My dear Traddles,’ said I, ‘I am delighted to see you at last, and very sorry I have not been at home before. But I have been so much engaged--’\n\n‘Yes, yes, I know,’ said Traddles, ‘of course. Yours lives in London, I think.’\n\n‘What did you say?’\n\n‘She--excuse me--Miss D., you know,’ said Traddles, colouring in his great delicacy, ‘lives in London, I believe?’\n\n‘Oh yes. Near London.’\n\n‘Mine, perhaps you recollect,’ said Traddles, with a serious look, ‘lives down in Devonshire--one of ten. Consequently, I am not so much engaged as you--in that sense.’\n\n‘I wonder you can bear,’ I returned, ‘to see her so seldom.’\n\n‘Hah!’ said Traddles, thoughtfully. ‘It does seem a wonder. I suppose it is, Copperfield, because there is no help for it?’\n\n‘I suppose so,’ I replied with a smile, and not without a blush. ‘And because you have so much constancy and patience, Traddles.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said Traddles, considering about it, ‘do I strike you in that way, Copperfield? Really I didn’t know that I had. But she is such an extraordinarily dear girl herself, that it’s possible she may have imparted something of those virtues to me. Now you mention it, Copperfield, I shouldn’t wonder at all. I assure you she is always forgetting herself, and taking care of the other nine.’\n\n‘Is she the eldest?’ I inquired.\n\n‘Oh dear, no,’ said Traddles. ‘The eldest is a Beauty.’\n\nHe saw, I suppose, that I could not help smiling at the simplicity of this reply; and added, with a smile upon his own ingenuous face:\n\n‘Not, of course, but that my Sophy--pretty name, Copperfield, I always think?’\n\n‘Very pretty!’ said I.\n\n‘Not, of course, but that Sophy is beautiful too in my eyes, and would be one of the dearest girls that ever was, in anybody’s eyes (I should think). But when I say the eldest is a Beauty, I mean she really is a--’ he seemed to be describing clouds about himself, with both hands: ‘Splendid, you know,’ said Traddles, energetically. ‘Indeed!’ said I.\n\n‘Oh, I assure you,’ said Traddles, ‘something very uncommon, indeed! Then, you know, being formed for society and admiration, and not being able to enjoy much of it in consequence of their limited means, she naturally gets a little irritable and exacting, sometimes. Sophy puts her in good humour!’\n\n‘Is Sophy the youngest?’ I hazarded.\n\n‘Oh dear, no!’ said Traddles, stroking his chin. ‘The two youngest are only nine and ten. Sophy educates ‘em.’\n\n‘The second daughter, perhaps?’ I hazarded.\n\n‘No,’ said Traddles. ‘Sarah’s the second. Sarah has something the matter with her spine, poor girl. The malady will wear out by and by, the doctors say, but in the meantime she has to lie down for a twelvemonth. Sophy nurses her. Sophy’s the fourth.’\n\n‘Is the mother living?’ I inquired.\n\n‘Oh yes,’ said Traddles, ‘she is alive. She is a very superior woman indeed, but the damp country is not adapted to her constitution, and--in fact, she has lost the use of her limbs.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said I.\n\n‘Very sad, is it not?’ returned Traddles. ‘But in a merely domestic view it is not so bad as it might be, because Sophy takes her place. She is quite as much a mother to her mother, as she is to the other nine.’\n\nI felt the greatest admiration for the virtues of this young lady; and, honestly with the view of doing my best to prevent the good-nature of Traddles from being imposed upon, to the detriment of their joint prospects in life, inquired how Mr. Micawber was?\n\n‘He is quite well, Copperfield, thank you,’ said Traddles. ‘I am not living with him at present.’\n‘No?’\n\n‘No. You see the truth is,’ said Traddles, in a whisper, ‘he had changed his name to Mortimer, in consequence of his temporary embarrassments; and he don’t come out till after dark--and then in spectacles. There was an execution put into our house, for rent. Mrs. Micawber was in such a dreadful state that I really couldn’t resist giving my name to that second bill we spoke of here. You may imagine how delightful it was to my feelings, Copperfield, to see the matter settled with it, and Mrs. Micawber recover her spirits.’\n\n‘Hum!’ said I. ‘Not that her happiness was of long duration,’ pursued Traddles, ‘for, unfortunately, within a week another execution came in. It broke up the establishment. I have been living in a furnished apartment since then, and the Mortimers have been very private indeed. I hope you won’t think it selfish, Copperfield, if I mention that the broker carried off my little round table with the marble top, and Sophy’s flower-pot and stand?’\n\n‘What a hard thing!’ I exclaimed indignantly.\n\n‘It was a--it was a pull,’ said Traddles, with his usual wince at that expression. ‘I don’t mention it reproachfully, however, but with a motive. The fact is, Copperfield, I was unable to repurchase them at the time of their seizure; in the first place, because the broker, having an idea that I wanted them, ran the price up to an extravagant extent; and, in the second place, because I--hadn’t any money. Now, I have kept my eye since, upon the broker’s shop,’ said Traddles, with a great enjoyment of his mystery, ‘which is up at the top of Tottenham Court Road, and, at last, today I find them put out for sale. I have only noticed them from over the way, because if the broker saw me, bless you, he’d ask any price for them! What has occurred to me, having now the money, is, that perhaps you wouldn’t object to ask that good nurse of yours to come with me to the shop--I can show it her from round the corner of the next street--and make the best bargain for them, as if they were for herself, that she can!’\n\nThe delight with which Traddles propounded this plan to me, and the sense he had of its uncommon artfulness, are among the freshest things in my remembrance.\n\nI told him that my old nurse would be delighted to assist him, and that we would all three take the field together, but on one condition. That condition was, that he should make a solemn resolution to grant no more loans of his name, or anything else, to Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, ‘I have already done so, because I begin to feel that I have not only been inconsiderate, but that I have been positively unjust to Sophy. My word being passed to myself, there is no longer any apprehension; but I pledge it to you, too, with the greatest readiness. That first unlucky obligation, I have paid. I have no doubt Mr. Micawber would have paid it if he could, but he could not. One thing I ought to mention, which I like very much in Mr. Micawber, Copperfield. It refers to the second obligation, which is not yet due. He don’t tell me that it is provided for, but he says it WILL BE. Now, I think there is something very fair and honest about that!’\n\nI was unwilling to damp my good friend’s confidence, and therefore assented. After a little further conversation, we went round to the chandler’s shop, to enlist Peggotty; Traddles declining to pass the evening with me, both because he endured the liveliest apprehensions that his property would be bought by somebody else before he could re-purchase it, and because it was the evening he always devoted to writing to the dearest girl in the world.\n\nI never shall forget him peeping round the corner of the street in Tottenham Court Road, while Peggotty was bargaining for the precious articles; or his agitation when she came slowly towards us after vainly offering a price, and was hailed by the relenting broker, and went back again. The end of the negotiation was, that she bought the property on tolerably easy terms, and Traddles was transported with pleasure.\n\n‘I am very much obliged to you, indeed,’ said Traddles, on hearing it was to be sent to where he lived, that night. ‘If I might ask one other favour, I hope you would not think it absurd, Copperfield?’\n\nI said beforehand, certainly not.\n\n‘Then if you WOULD be good enough,’ said Traddles to Peggotty, ‘to get the flower-pot now, I think I should like (it being Sophy’s, Copperfield) to carry it home myself!’\n\nPeggotty was glad to get it for him, and he overwhelmed her with thanks, and went his way up Tottenham Court Road, carrying the flower-pot affectionately in his arms, with one of the most delighted expressions of countenance I ever saw.\n\nWe then turned back towards my chambers. As the shops had charms for Peggotty which I never knew them possess in the same degree for anybody else, I sauntered easily along, amused by her staring in at the windows, and waiting for her as often as she chose. We were thus a good while in getting to the Adelphi.\n\nOn our way upstairs, I called her attention to the sudden disappearance of Mrs. Crupp’s pitfalls, and also to the prints of recent footsteps. We were both very much surprised, coming higher up, to find my outer door standing open (which I had shut) and to hear voices inside.\n\nWe looked at one another, without knowing what to make of this, and went into the sitting-room. What was my amazement to find, of all people upon earth, my aunt there, and Mr. Dick! My aunt sitting on a quantity of luggage, with her two birds before her, and her cat on her knee, like a female Robinson Crusoe, drinking tea. Mr. Dick leaning thoughtfully on a great kite, such as we had often been out together to fly, with more luggage piled about him!\n\n‘My dear aunt!’ cried I. ‘Why, what an unexpected pleasure!’\n\nWe cordially embraced; and Mr. Dick and I cordially shook hands; and Mrs. Crupp, who was busy making tea, and could not be too attentive, cordially said she had knowed well as Mr. Copperfull would have his heart in his mouth, when he see his dear relations.\n\n‘Holloa!’ said my aunt to Peggotty, who quailed before her awful presence. ‘How are YOU?’\n\n‘You remember my aunt, Peggotty?’ said I.\n\n‘For the love of goodness, child,’ exclaimed my aunt, ‘don’t call the woman by that South Sea Island name! If she married and got rid of it, which was the best thing she could do, why don’t you give her the benefit of the change? What’s your name now,--P?’ said my aunt, as a compromise for the obnoxious appellation.\n\n‘Barkis, ma’am,’ said Peggotty, with a curtsey.\n\n‘Well! That’s human,’ said my aunt. ‘It sounds less as if you wanted a missionary. How d’ye do, Barkis? I hope you’re well?’\n\nEncouraged by these gracious words, and by my aunt’s extending her hand, Barkis came forward, and took the hand, and curtseyed her acknowledgements.\n\n‘We are older than we were, I see,’ said my aunt. ‘We have only met each other once before, you know. A nice business we made of it then! Trot, my dear, another cup.’\n\nI handed it dutifully to my aunt, who was in her usual inflexible state of figure; and ventured a remonstrance with her on the subject of her sitting on a box.\n\n‘Let me draw the sofa here, or the easy-chair, aunt,’ said I. ‘Why should you be so uncomfortable?’\n\n‘Thank you, Trot,’ replied my aunt, ‘I prefer to sit upon my property.’ Here my aunt looked hard at Mrs. Crupp, and observed, ‘We needn’t trouble you to wait, ma’am.’\n\n‘Shall I put a little more tea in the pot afore I go, ma’am?’ said Mrs. Crupp.\n\n‘No, I thank you, ma’am,’ replied my aunt.\n\n‘Would you let me fetch another pat of butter, ma’am?’ said Mrs. Crupp. ‘Or would you be persuaded to try a new-laid hegg? or should I brile a rasher? Ain’t there nothing I could do for your dear aunt, Mr. Copperfull?’\n\n‘Nothing, ma’am,’ returned my aunt. ‘I shall do very well, I thank you.’\n\nMrs. Crupp, who had been incessantly smiling to express sweet temper, and incessantly holding her head on one side, to express a general feebleness of constitution, and incessantly rubbing her hands, to express a desire to be of service to all deserving objects, gradually smiled herself, one-sided herself, and rubbed herself, out of the room. ‘Dick!’ said my aunt. ‘You know what I told you about time-servers and wealth-worshippers?’\n\nMr. Dick--with rather a scared look, as if he had forgotten it--returned a hasty answer in the affirmative.\n\n‘Mrs. Crupp is one of them,’ said my aunt. ‘Barkis, I’ll trouble you to look after the tea, and let me have another cup, for I don’t fancy that woman’s pouring-out!’\n\nI knew my aunt sufficiently well to know that she had something of importance on her mind, and that there was far more matter in this arrival than a stranger might have supposed. I noticed how her eye lighted on me, when she thought my attention otherwise occupied; and what a curious process of hesitation appeared to be going on within her, while she preserved her outward stiffness and composure. I began to reflect whether I had done anything to offend her; and my conscience whispered me that I had not yet told her about Dora. Could it by any means be that, I wondered!\n\nAs I knew she would only speak in her own good time, I sat down near her, and spoke to the birds, and played with the cat, and was as easy as I could be. But I was very far from being really easy; and I should still have been so, even if Mr. Dick, leaning over the great kite behind my aunt, had not taken every secret opportunity of shaking his head darkly at me, and pointing at her.\n\n‘Trot,’ said my aunt at last, when she had finished her tea, and carefully smoothed down her dress, and wiped her lips--‘you needn’t go, Barkis!--Trot, have you got to be firm and self-reliant?’\n\n‘I hope so, aunt.’\n\n‘What do you think?’ inquired Miss Betsey.\n\n‘I think so, aunt.’\n\n‘Then why, my love,’ said my aunt, looking earnestly at me, ‘why do you think I prefer to sit upon this property of mine tonight?’\n\nI shook my head, unable to guess.\n\n‘Because,’ said my aunt, ‘it’s all I have. Because I’m ruined, my dear!’\n\nIf the house, and every one of us, had tumbled out into the river together, I could hardly have received a greater shock.\n\n‘Dick knows it,’ said my aunt, laying her hand calmly on my shoulder. ‘I am ruined, my dear Trot! All I have in the world is in this room, except the cottage; and that I have left Janet to let. Barkis, I want to get a bed for this gentleman tonight. To save expense, perhaps you can make up something here for myself. Anything will do. It’s only for tonight. We’ll talk about this, more, tomorrow.’\n\nI was roused from my amazement, and concern for her--I am sure, for her--by her falling on my neck, for a moment, and crying that she only grieved for me. In another moment she suppressed this emotion; and said with an aspect more triumphant than dejected:\n\n‘We must meet reverses boldly, and not suffer them to frighten us, my dear. We must learn to act the play out. We must live misfortune down, Trot!’\n\n# CHAPTER 35. DEPRESSION\n\nAs soon as I could recover my presence of mind, which quite deserted me in the first overpowering shock of my aunt’s intelligence, I proposed to Mr. Dick to come round to the chandler’s shop, and take possession of the bed which Mr. Peggotty had lately vacated. The chandler’s shop being in Hungerford Market, and Hungerford Market being a very different place in those days, there was a low wooden colonnade before the door (not very unlike that before the house where the little man and woman used to live, in the old weather-glass), which pleased Mr. Dick mightily. The glory of lodging over this structure would have compensated him, I dare say, for many inconveniences; but, as there were really few to bear, beyond the compound of flavours I have already mentioned, and perhaps the want of a little more elbow-room, he was perfectly charmed with his accommodation. Mrs. Crupp had indignantly assured him that there wasn’t room to swing a cat there; but, as Mr. Dick justly observed to me, sitting down on the foot of the bed, nursing his leg, ‘You know, Trotwood, I don’t want to swing a cat. I never do swing a cat. Therefore, what does that signify to ME!’\n\nI tried to ascertain whether Mr. Dick had any understanding of the causes of this sudden and great change in my aunt’s affairs. As I might have expected, he had none at all. The only account he could give of it was, that my aunt had said to him, the day before yesterday, ‘Now, Dick, are you really and truly the philosopher I take you for?’ That then he had said, Yes, he hoped so. That then my aunt had said, ‘Dick, I am ruined.’ That then he had said, ‘Oh, indeed!’ That then my aunt had praised him highly, which he was glad of. And that then they had come to me, and had had bottled porter and sandwiches on the road.\n\nMr. Dick was so very complacent, sitting on the foot of the bed, nursing his leg, and telling me this, with his eyes wide open and a surprised smile, that I am sorry to say I was provoked into explaining to him that ruin meant distress, want, and starvation; but I was soon bitterly reproved for this harshness, by seeing his face turn pale, and tears course down his lengthened cheeks, while he fixed upon me a look of such unutterable woe, that it might have softened a far harder heart than mine. I took infinitely greater pains to cheer him up again than I had taken to depress him; and I soon understood (as I ought to have known at first) that he had been so confident, merely because of his faith in the wisest and most wonderful of women, and his unbounded reliance on my intellectual resources. The latter, I believe, he considered a match for any kind of disaster not absolutely mortal.\n\n‘What can we do, Trotwood?’ said Mr. Dick. ‘There’s the Memorial-’\n\n‘To be sure there is,’ said I. ‘But all we can do just now, Mr. Dick, is to keep a cheerful countenance, and not let my aunt see that we are thinking about it.’\n\nHe assented to this in the most earnest manner; and implored me, if I should see him wandering an inch out of the right course, to recall him by some of those superior methods which were always at my command. But I regret to state that the fright I had given him proved too much for his best attempts at concealment. All the evening his eyes wandered to my aunt’s face, with an expression of the most dismal apprehension, as if he saw her growing thin on the spot. He was conscious of this, and put a constraint upon his head; but his keeping that immovable, and sitting rolling his eyes like a piece of machinery, did not mend the matter at all. I saw him look at the loaf at supper (which happened to be a small one), as if nothing else stood between us and famine; and when my aunt insisted on his making his customary repast, I detected him in the act of pocketing fragments of his bread and cheese; I have no doubt for the purpose of reviving us with those savings, when we should have reached an advanced stage of attenuation.\n\nMy aunt, on the other hand, was in a composed frame of mind, which was a lesson to all of us--to me, I am sure. She was extremely gracious to Peggotty, except when I inadvertently called her by that name; and, strange as I knew she felt in London, appeared quite at home. She was to have my bed, and I was to lie in the sitting-room, to keep guard over her. She made a great point of being so near the river, in case of a conflagration; and I suppose really did find some satisfaction in that circumstance.\n\n‘Trot, my dear,’ said my aunt, when she saw me making preparations for compounding her usual night-draught, ‘No!’\n\n‘Nothing, aunt?’\n‘Not wine, my dear. Ale.’\n\n‘But there is wine here, aunt. And you always have it made of wine.’\n\n‘Keep that, in case of sickness,’ said my aunt. ‘We mustn’t use it carelessly, Trot. Ale for me. Half a pint.’\n\nI thought Mr. Dick would have fallen, insensible. My aunt being resolute, I went out and got the ale myself. As it was growing late, Peggotty and Mr. Dick took that opportunity of repairing to the chandler’s shop together. I parted from him, poor fellow, at the corner of the street, with his great kite at his back, a very monument of human misery.\n\nMy aunt was walking up and down the room when I returned, crimping the borders of her nightcap with her fingers. I warmed the ale and made the toast on the usual infallible principles. When it was ready for her, she was ready for it, with her nightcap on, and the skirt of her gown turned back on her knees.\n\n‘My dear,’ said my aunt, after taking a spoonful of it; ‘it’s a great deal better than wine. Not half so bilious.’\n\nI suppose I looked doubtful, for she added:\n\n‘Tut, tut, child. If nothing worse than Ale happens to us, we are well off.’\n\n‘I should think so myself, aunt, I am sure,’ said I.\n\n‘Well, then, why DON’T you think so?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Because you and I are very different people,’ I returned.\n\n‘Stuff and nonsense, Trot!’ replied my aunt.\n\nMy aunt went on with a quiet enjoyment, in which there was very little affectation, if any; drinking the warm ale with a tea-spoon, and soaking her strips of toast in it.\n\n‘Trot,’ said she, ‘I don’t care for strange faces in general, but I rather like that Barkis of yours, do you know!’\n\n‘It’s better than a hundred pounds to hear you say so!’ said I.\n\n‘It’s a most extraordinary world,’ observed my aunt, rubbing her nose; ‘how that woman ever got into it with that name, is unaccountable to me. It would be much more easy to be born a Jackson, or something of that sort, one would think.’\n\n‘Perhaps she thinks so, too; it’s not her fault,’ said I.\n\n‘I suppose not,’ returned my aunt, rather grudging the admission; ‘but it’s very aggravating. However, she’s Barkis now. That’s some comfort. Barkis is uncommonly fond of you, Trot.’\n\n‘There is nothing she would leave undone to prove it,’ said I.\n\n‘Nothing, I believe,’ returned my aunt. ‘Here, the poor fool has been begging and praying about handing over some of her money--because she has got too much of it. A simpleton!’\n\nMy aunt’s tears of pleasure were positively trickling down into the warm ale.\n\n‘She’s the most ridiculous creature that ever was born,’ said my aunt. ‘I knew, from the first moment when I saw her with that poor dear blessed baby of a mother of yours, that she was the most ridiculous of mortals. But there are good points in Barkis!’\n\nAffecting to laugh, she got an opportunity of putting her hand to her eyes. Having availed herself of it, she resumed her toast and her discourse together.\n\n‘Ah! Mercy upon us!’ sighed my aunt. ‘I know all about it, Trot! Barkis and myself had quite a gossip while you were out with Dick. I know all about it. I don’t know where these wretched girls expect to go to, for my part. I wonder they don’t knock out their brains against--against mantelpieces,’ said my aunt; an idea which was probably suggested to her by her contemplation of mine.\n\n‘Poor Emily!’ said I.\n\n‘Oh, don’t talk to me about poor,’ returned my aunt. ‘She should have thought of that, before she caused so much misery! Give me a kiss, Trot. I am sorry for your early experience.’\n\nAs I bent forward, she put her tumbler on my knee to detain me, and said:\n\n‘Oh, Trot, Trot! And so you fancy yourself in love! Do you?’\n\n‘Fancy, aunt!’ I exclaimed, as red as I could be. ‘I adore her with my whole soul!’\n\n‘Dora, indeed!’ returned my aunt. ‘And you mean to say the little thing is very fascinating, I suppose?’\n\n‘My dear aunt,’ I replied, ‘no one can form the least idea what she is!’\n\n‘Ah! And not silly?’ said my aunt.\n‘Silly, aunt!’\n\nI seriously believe it had never once entered my head for a single moment, to consider whether she was or not. I resented the idea, of course; but I was in a manner struck by it, as a new one altogether.\n\n‘Not light-headed?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Light-headed, aunt!’ I could only repeat this daring speculation with the same kind of feeling with which I had repeated the preceding question.\n\n‘Well, well!’ said my aunt. ‘I only ask. I don’t depreciate her. Poor little couple! And so you think you were formed for one another, and are to go through a party-supper-table kind of life, like two pretty pieces of confectionery, do you, Trot?’\n\nShe asked me this so kindly, and with such a gentle air, half playful and half sorrowful, that I was quite touched.\n\n‘We are young and inexperienced, aunt, I know,’ I replied; ‘and I dare say we say and think a good deal that is rather foolish. But we love one another truly, I am sure. If I thought Dora could ever love anybody else, or cease to love me; or that I could ever love anybody else, or cease to love her; I don’t know what I should do--go out of my mind, I think!’\n\n‘Ah, Trot!’ said my aunt, shaking her head, and smiling gravely; ‘blind, blind, blind!’\n\n‘Someone that I know, Trot,’ my aunt pursued, after a pause, ‘though of a very pliant disposition, has an earnestness of affection in him that reminds me of poor Baby. Earnestness is what that Somebody must look for, to sustain him and improve him, Trot. Deep, downright, faithful earnestness.’\n\n‘If you only knew the earnestness of Dora, aunt!’ I cried.\n\n‘Oh, Trot!’ she said again; ‘blind, blind!’ and without knowing why, I felt a vague unhappy loss or want of something overshadow me like a cloud.\n\n‘However,’ said my aunt, ‘I don’t want to put two young creatures out of conceit with themselves, or to make them unhappy; so, though it is a girl and boy attachment, and girl and boy attachments very often--mind! I don’t say always!--come to nothing, still we’ll be serious about it, and hope for a prosperous issue one of these days. There’s time enough for it to come to anything!’\n\nThis was not upon the whole very comforting to a rapturous lover; but I was glad to have my aunt in my confidence, and I was mindful of her being fatigued. So I thanked her ardently for this mark of her affection, and for all her other kindnesses towards me; and after a tender good night, she took her nightcap into my bedroom.\n\nHow miserable I was, when I lay down! How I thought and thought about my being poor, in Mr. Spenlow’s eyes; about my not being what I thought I was, when I proposed to Dora; about the chivalrous necessity of telling Dora what my worldly condition was, and releasing her from her engagement if she thought fit; about how I should contrive to live, during the long term of my articles, when I was earning nothing; about doing something to assist my aunt, and seeing no way of doing anything; about coming down to have no money in my pocket, and to wear a shabby coat, and to be able to carry Dora no little presents, and to ride no gallant greys, and to show myself in no agreeable light! Sordid and selfish as I knew it was, and as I tortured myself by knowing that it was, to let my mind run on my own distress so much, I was so devoted to Dora that I could not help it. I knew that it was base in me not to think more of my aunt, and less of myself; but, so far, selfishness was inseparable from Dora, and I could not put Dora on one side for any mortal creature. How exceedingly miserable I was, that night!\n\nAs to sleep, I had dreams of poverty in all sorts of shapes, but I seemed to dream without the previous ceremony of going to sleep. Now I was ragged, wanting to sell Dora matches, six bundles for a halfpenny; now I was at the office in a nightgown and boots, remonstrated with by Mr. Spenlow on appearing before the clients in that airy attire; now I was hungrily picking up the crumbs that fell from old Tiffey’s daily biscuit, regularly eaten when St. Paul’s struck one; now I was hopelessly endeavouring to get a licence to marry Dora, having nothing but one of Uriah Heep’s gloves to offer in exchange, which the whole Commons rejected; and still, more or less conscious of my own room, I was always tossing about like a distressed ship in a sea of bed-clothes.\n\nMy aunt was restless, too, for I frequently heard her walking to and fro. Two or three times in the course of the night, attired in a long flannel wrapper in which she looked seven feet high, she appeared, like a disturbed ghost, in my room, and came to the side of the sofa on which I lay. On the first occasion I started up in alarm, to learn that she inferred from a particular light in the sky, that Westminster Abbey was on fire; and to be consulted in reference to the probability of its igniting Buckingham Street, in case the wind changed. Lying still, after that, I found that she sat down near me, whispering to herself ‘Poor boy!’ And then it made me twenty times more wretched, to know how unselfishly mindful she was of me, and how selfishly mindful I was of myself.\n\nIt was difficult to believe that a night so long to me, could be short to anybody else. This consideration set me thinking and thinking of an imaginary party where people were dancing the hours away, until that became a dream too, and I heard the music incessantly playing one tune, and saw Dora incessantly dancing one dance, without taking the least notice of me. The man who had been playing the harp all night, was trying in vain to cover it with an ordinary-sized nightcap, when I awoke; or I should rather say, when I left off trying to go to sleep, and saw the sun shining in through the window at last.\n\nThere was an old Roman bath in those days at the bottom of one of the streets out of the Strand--it may be there still--in which I have had many a cold plunge. Dressing myself as quietly as I could, and leaving Peggotty to look after my aunt, I tumbled head foremost into it, and then went for a walk to Hampstead. I had a hope that this brisk treatment might freshen my wits a little; and I think it did them good, for I soon came to the conclusion that the first step I ought to take was, to try if my articles could be cancelled and the premium recovered. I got some breakfast on the Heath, and walked back to Doctors’ Commons, along the watered roads and through a pleasant smell of summer flowers, growing in gardens and carried into town on hucksters’ heads, intent on this first effort to meet our altered circumstances.\n\nI arrived at the office so soon, after all, that I had half an hour’s loitering about the Commons, before old Tiffey, who was always first, appeared with his key. Then I sat down in my shady corner, looking up at the sunlight on the opposite chimney-pots, and thinking about Dora; until Mr. Spenlow came in, crisp and curly.\n\n‘How are you, Copperfield?’ said he. ‘Fine morning!’\n\n‘Beautiful morning, sir,’ said I. ‘Could I say a word to you before you go into Court?’\n\n‘By all means,’ said he. ‘Come into my room.’\n\nI followed him into his room, and he began putting on his gown, and touching himself up before a little glass he had, hanging inside a closet door.\n\n‘I am sorry to say,’ said I, ‘that I have some rather disheartening intelligence from my aunt.’\n\n‘No!’ said he. ‘Dear me! Not paralysis, I hope?’\n\n‘It has no reference to her health, sir,’ I replied. ‘She has met with some large losses. In fact, she has very little left, indeed.’\n\n‘You as-tound me, Copperfield!’ cried Mr. Spenlow.\n\nI shook my head. ‘Indeed, sir,’ said I, ‘her affairs are so changed, that I wished to ask you whether it would be possible--at a sacrifice on our part of some portion of the premium, of course,’ I put in this, on the spur of the moment, warned by the blank expression of his face--‘to cancel my articles?’\n\nWhat it cost me to make this proposal, nobody knows. It was like asking, as a favour, to be sentenced to transportation from Dora.\n\n‘To cancel your articles, Copperfield? Cancel?’\n\nI explained with tolerable firmness, that I really did not know where my means of subsistence were to come from, unless I could earn them for myself. I had no fear for the future, I said--and I laid great emphasis on that, as if to imply that I should still be decidedly eligible for a son-in-law one of these days--but, for the present, I was thrown upon my own resources. ‘I am extremely sorry to hear this, Copperfield,’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘Extremely sorry. It is not usual to cancel articles for any such reason. It is not a professional course of proceeding. It is not a convenient precedent at all. Far from it. At the same time--’\n\n‘You are very good, sir,’ I murmured, anticipating a concession.\n\n‘Not at all. Don’t mention it,’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘At the same time, I was going to say, if it had been my lot to have my hands unfettered--if I had not a partner--Mr. Jorkins--’\n\nMy hopes were dashed in a moment, but I made another effort.\n\n‘Do you think, sir,’ said I, ‘if I were to mention it to Mr. Jorkins--’\n\nMr. Spenlow shook his head discouragingly. ‘Heaven forbid, Copperfield,’ he replied, ‘that I should do any man an injustice: still less, Mr. Jorkins. But I know my partner, Copperfield. Mr. Jorkins is not a man to respond to a proposition of this peculiar nature. Mr. Jorkins is very difficult to move from the beaten track. You know what he is!’\n\nI am sure I knew nothing about him, except that he had originally been alone in the business, and now lived by himself in a house near Montagu Square, which was fearfully in want of painting; that he came very late of a day, and went away very early; that he never appeared to be consulted about anything; and that he had a dingy little black-hole of his own upstairs, where no business was ever done, and where there was a yellow old cartridge-paper pad upon his desk, unsoiled by ink, and reported to be twenty years of age.\n\n‘Would you object to my mentioning it to him, sir?’ I asked.\n\n‘By no means,’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘But I have some experience of Mr. Jorkins, Copperfield. I wish it were otherwise, for I should be happy to meet your views in any respect. I cannot have the objection to your mentioning it to Mr. Jorkins, Copperfield, if you think it worth while.’\n\nAvailing myself of this permission, which was given with a warm shake of the hand, I sat thinking about Dora, and looking at the sunlight stealing from the chimney-pots down the wall of the opposite house, until Mr. Jorkins came. I then went up to Mr. Jorkins’s room, and evidently astonished Mr. Jorkins very much by making my appearance there.\n\n‘Come in, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr. Jorkins. ‘Come in!’\n\nI went in, and sat down; and stated my case to Mr. Jorkins pretty much as I had stated it to Mr. Spenlow. Mr. Jorkins was not by any means the awful creature one might have expected, but a large, mild, smooth-faced man of sixty, who took so much snuff that there was a tradition in the Commons that he lived principally on that stimulant, having little room in his system for any other article of diet.\n\n‘You have mentioned this to Mr. Spenlow, I suppose?’ said Mr. Jorkins; when he had heard me, very restlessly, to an end.\n\nI answered Yes, and told him that Mr. Spenlow had introduced his name.\n\n‘He said I should object?’ asked Mr. Jorkins.\n\nI was obliged to admit that Mr. Spenlow had considered it probable.\n\n‘I am sorry to say, Mr. Copperfield, I can’t advance your object,’ said Mr. Jorkins, nervously. ‘The fact is--but I have an appointment at the Bank, if you’ll have the goodness to excuse me.’\n\nWith that he rose in a great hurry, and was going out of the room, when I made bold to say that I feared, then, there was no way of arranging the matter?\n\n‘No!’ said Mr. Jorkins, stopping at the door to shake his head. ‘Oh, no! I object, you know,’ which he said very rapidly, and went out. ‘You must be aware, Mr. Copperfield,’ he added, looking restlessly in at the door again, ‘if Mr. Spenlow objects--’\n\n‘Personally, he does not object, sir,’ said I.\n\n‘Oh! Personally!’ repeated Mr. Jorkins, in an impatient manner. ‘I assure you there’s an objection, Mr. Copperfield. Hopeless! What you wish to be done, can’t be done. I--I really have got an appointment at the Bank.’ With that he fairly ran away; and to the best of my knowledge, it was three days before he showed himself in the Commons again.\n\nBeing very anxious to leave no stone unturned, I waited until Mr. Spenlow came in, and then described what had passed; giving him to understand that I was not hopeless of his being able to soften the adamantine Jorkins, if he would undertake the task.\n\n‘Copperfield,’ returned Mr. Spenlow, with a gracious smile, ‘you have not known my partner, Mr. Jorkins, as long as I have. Nothing is farther from my thoughts than to attribute any degree of artifice to Mr. Jorkins. But Mr. Jorkins has a way of stating his objections which often deceives people. No, Copperfield!’ shaking his head. ‘Mr. Jorkins is not to be moved, believe me!’\n\nI was completely bewildered between Mr. Spenlow and Mr. Jorkins, as to which of them really was the objecting partner; but I saw with sufficient clearness that there was obduracy somewhere in the firm, and that the recovery of my aunt’s thousand pounds was out of the question. In a state of despondency, which I remember with anything but satisfaction, for I know it still had too much reference to myself (though always in connexion with Dora), I left the office, and went homeward.\n\nI was trying to familiarize my mind with the worst, and to present to myself the arrangements we should have to make for the future in their sternest aspect, when a hackney-chariot coming after me, and stopping at my very feet, occasioned me to look up. A fair hand was stretched forth to me from the window; and the face I had never seen without a feeling of serenity and happiness, from the moment when it first turned back on the old oak staircase with the great broad balustrade, and when I associated its softened beauty with the stained-glass window in the church, was smiling on me.\n\n‘Agnes!’ I joyfully exclaimed. ‘Oh, my dear Agnes, of all people in the world, what a pleasure to see you!’\n\n‘Is it, indeed?’ she said, in her cordial voice.\n\n‘I want to talk to you so much!’ said I. ‘It’s such a lightening of my heart, only to look at you! If I had had a conjuror’s cap, there is no one I should have wished for but you!’\n\n‘What?’ returned Agnes.\n\n‘Well! perhaps Dora first,’ I admitted, with a blush.\n\n‘Certainly, Dora first, I hope,’ said Agnes, laughing.\n\n‘But you next!’ said I. ‘Where are you going?’\n\nShe was going to my rooms to see my aunt. The day being very fine, she was glad to come out of the chariot, which smelt (I had my head in it all this time) like a stable put under a cucumber-frame. I dismissed the coachman, and she took my arm, and we walked on together. She was like Hope embodied, to me. How different I felt in one short minute, having Agnes at my side!\n\nMy aunt had written her one of the odd, abrupt notes--very little longer than a Bank note--to which her epistolary efforts were usually limited. She had stated therein that she had fallen into adversity, and was leaving Dover for good, but had quite made up her mind to it, and was so well that nobody need be uncomfortable about her. Agnes had come to London to see my aunt, between whom and herself there had been a mutual liking these many years: indeed, it dated from the time of my taking up my residence in Mr. Wickfield’s house. She was not alone, she said. Her papa was with her--and Uriah Heep.\n\n‘And now they are partners,’ said I. ‘Confound him!’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Agnes. ‘They have some business here; and I took advantage of their coming, to come too. You must not think my visit all friendly and disinterested, Trotwood, for--I am afraid I may be cruelly prejudiced--I do not like to let papa go away alone, with him.’ ‘Does he exercise the same influence over Mr. Wickfield still, Agnes?’\n\nAgnes shook her head. ‘There is such a change at home,’ said she, ‘that you would scarcely know the dear old house. They live with us now.’\n\n‘They?’ said I.\n\n‘Mr. Heep and his mother. He sleeps in your old room,’ said Agnes, looking up into my face.\n\n‘I wish I had the ordering of his dreams,’ said I. ‘He wouldn’t sleep there long.’\n\n‘I keep my own little room,’ said Agnes, ‘where I used to learn my lessons. How the time goes! You remember? The little panelled room that opens from the drawing-room?’\n\n‘Remember, Agnes? When I saw you, for the first time, coming out at the door, with your quaint little basket of keys hanging at your side?’\n\n‘It is just the same,’ said Agnes, smiling. ‘I am glad you think of it so pleasantly. We were very happy.’\n\n‘We were, indeed,’ said I.\n\n‘I keep that room to myself still; but I cannot always desert Mrs. Heep, you know. And so,’ said Agnes, quietly, ‘I feel obliged to bear her company, when I might prefer to be alone. But I have no other reason to complain of her. If she tires me, sometimes, by her praises of her son, it is only natural in a mother. He is a very good son to her.’\n\nI looked at Agnes when she said these words, without detecting in her any consciousness of Uriah’s design. Her mild but earnest eyes met mine with their own beautiful frankness, and there was no change in her gentle face.\n\n‘The chief evil of their presence in the house,’ said Agnes, ‘is that I cannot be as near papa as I could wish--Uriah Heep being so much between us--and cannot watch over him, if that is not too bold a thing to say, as closely as I would. But if any fraud or treachery is practising against him, I hope that simple love and truth will be strong in the end. I hope that real love and truth are stronger in the end than any evil or misfortune in the world.’\n\nA certain bright smile, which I never saw on any other face, died away, even while I thought how good it was, and how familiar it had once been to me; and she asked me, with a quick change of expression (we were drawing very near my street), if I knew how the reverse in my aunt’s circumstances had been brought about. On my replying no, she had not told me yet, Agnes became thoughtful, and I fancied I felt her arm tremble in mine.\n\nWe found my aunt alone, in a state of some excitement. A difference of opinion had arisen between herself and Mrs. Crupp, on an abstract question (the propriety of chambers being inhabited by the gentler sex); and my aunt, utterly indifferent to spasms on the part of Mrs. Crupp, had cut the dispute short, by informing that lady that she smelt of my brandy, and that she would trouble her to walk out. Both of these expressions Mrs. Crupp considered actionable, and had expressed her intention of bringing before a ‘British Judy’--meaning, it was supposed, the bulwark of our national liberties.\n\nMy aunt, however, having had time to cool, while Peggotty was out showing Mr. Dick the soldiers at the Horse Guards--and being, besides, greatly pleased to see Agnes--rather plumed herself on the affair than otherwise, and received us with unimpaired good humour. When Agnes laid her bonnet on the table, and sat down beside her, I could not but think, looking on her mild eyes and her radiant forehead, how natural it seemed to have her there; how trustfully, although she was so young and inexperienced, my aunt confided in her; how strong she was, indeed, in simple love and truth.\n\nWe began to talk about my aunt’s losses, and I told them what I had tried to do that morning.\n\n‘Which was injudicious, Trot,’ said my aunt, ‘but well meant. You are a generous boy--I suppose I must say, young man, now--and I am proud of you, my dear. So far, so good. Now, Trot and Agnes, let us look the case of Betsey Trotwood in the face, and see how it stands.’\n\nI observed Agnes turn pale, as she looked very attentively at my aunt. My aunt, patting her cat, looked very attentively at Agnes.\n\n‘Betsey Trotwood,’ said my aunt, who had always kept her money matters to herself. ‘--I don’t mean your sister, Trot, my dear, but myself--had a certain property. It don’t matter how much; enough to live on. More; for she had saved a little, and added to it. Betsey funded her property for some time, and then, by the advice of her man of business, laid it out on landed security. That did very well, and returned very good interest, till Betsey was paid off. I am talking of Betsey as if she was a man-of-war. Well! Then, Betsey had to look about her, for a new investment. She thought she was wiser, now, than her man of business, who was not such a good man of business by this time, as he used to be--I am alluding to your father, Agnes--and she took it into her head to lay it out for herself. So she took her pigs,’ said my aunt, ‘to a foreign market; and a very bad market it turned out to be. First, she lost in the mining way, and then she lost in the diving way--fishing up treasure, or some such Tom Tiddler nonsense,’ explained my aunt, rubbing her nose; ‘and then she lost in the mining way again, and, last of all, to set the thing entirely to rights, she lost in the banking way. I don’t know what the Bank shares were worth for a little while,’ said my aunt; ‘cent per cent was the lowest of it, I believe; but the Bank was at the other end of the world, and tumbled into space, for what I know; anyhow, it fell to pieces, and never will and never can pay sixpence; and Betsey’s sixpences were all there, and there’s an end of them. Least said, soonest mended!’\n\nMy aunt concluded this philosophical summary, by fixing her eyes with a kind of triumph on Agnes, whose colour was gradually returning.\n\n‘Dear Miss Trotwood, is that all the history?’ said Agnes.\n\n‘I hope it’s enough, child,’ said my aunt. ‘If there had been more money to lose, it wouldn’t have been all, I dare say. Betsey would have contrived to throw that after the rest, and make another chapter, I have little doubt. But there was no more money, and there’s no more story.’\n\nAgnes had listened at first with suspended breath. Her colour still came and went, but she breathed more freely. I thought I knew why. I thought she had had some fear that her unhappy father might be in some way to blame for what had happened. My aunt took her hand in hers, and laughed.\n\n‘Is that all?’ repeated my aunt. ‘Why, yes, that’s all, except, “And she lived happy ever afterwards.” Perhaps I may add that of Betsey yet, one of these days. Now, Agnes, you have a wise head. So have you, Trot, in some things, though I can’t compliment you always’; and here my aunt shook her own at me, with an energy peculiar to herself. ‘What’s to be done? Here’s the cottage, taking one time with another, will produce say seventy pounds a year. I think we may safely put it down at that. Well!--That’s all we’ve got,’ said my aunt; with whom it was an idiosyncrasy, as it is with some horses, to stop very short when she appeared to be in a fair way of going on for a long while.\n\n‘Then,’ said my aunt, after a rest, ‘there’s Dick. He’s good for a hundred a-year, but of course that must be expended on himself. I would sooner send him away, though I know I am the only person who appreciates him, than have him, and not spend his money on himself. How can Trot and I do best, upon our means? What do you say, Agnes?’\n\n‘I say, aunt,’ I interposed, ‘that I must do something!’\n\n‘Go for a soldier, do you mean?’ returned my aunt, alarmed; ‘or go to sea? I won’t hear of it. You are to be a proctor. We’re not going to have any knockings on the head in THIS family, if you please, sir.’\n\nI was about to explain that I was not desirous of introducing that mode of provision into the family, when Agnes inquired if my rooms were held for any long term?\n\n‘You come to the point, my dear,’ said my aunt. ‘They are not to be got rid of, for six months at least, unless they could be underlet, and that I don’t believe. The last man died here. Five people out of six would die--of course--of that woman in nankeen with the flannel petticoat. I have a little ready money; and I agree with you, the best thing we can do, is, to live the term out here, and get a bedroom hard by.’\n\nI thought it my duty to hint at the discomfort my aunt would sustain, from living in a continual state of guerilla warfare with Mrs. Crupp; but she disposed of that objection summarily by declaring that, on the first demonstration of hostilities, she was prepared to astonish Mrs. Crupp for the whole remainder of her natural life.\n\n‘I have been thinking, Trotwood,’ said Agnes, diffidently, ‘that if you had time--’\n\n‘I have a good deal of time, Agnes. I am always disengaged after four or five o’clock, and I have time early in the morning. In one way and another,’ said I, conscious of reddening a little as I thought of the hours and hours I had devoted to fagging about town, and to and fro upon the Norwood Road, ‘I have abundance of time.’\n\n‘I know you would not mind,’ said Agnes, coming to me, and speaking in a low voice, so full of sweet and hopeful consideration that I hear it now, ‘the duties of a secretary.’\n\n‘Mind, my dear Agnes?’\n\n‘Because,’ continued Agnes, ‘Doctor Strong has acted on his intention of retiring, and has come to live in London; and he asked papa, I know, if he could recommend him one. Don’t you think he would rather have his favourite old pupil near him, than anybody else?’\n\n‘Dear Agnes!’ said I. ‘What should I do without you! You are always my good angel. I told you so. I never think of you in any other light.’\n\nAgnes answered with her pleasant laugh, that one good Angel (meaning Dora) was enough; and went on to remind me that the Doctor had been used to occupy himself in his study, early in the morning, and in the evening--and that probably my leisure would suit his requirements very well. I was scarcely more delighted with the prospect of earning my own bread, than with the hope of earning it under my old master; in short, acting on the advice of Agnes, I sat down and wrote a letter to the Doctor, stating my object, and appointing to call on him next day at ten in the forenoon. This I addressed to Highgate--for in that place, so memorable to me, he lived--and went and posted, myself, without losing a minute.\n\nWherever Agnes was, some agreeable token of her noiseless presence seemed inseparable from the place. When I came back, I found my aunt’s birds hanging, just as they had hung so long in the parlour window of the cottage; and my easy-chair imitating my aunt’s much easier chair in its position at the open window; and even the round green fan, which my aunt had brought away with her, screwed on to the window-sill. I knew who had done all this, by its seeming to have quietly done itself; and I should have known in a moment who had arranged my neglected books in the old order of my school days, even if I had supposed Agnes to be miles away, instead of seeing her busy with them, and smiling at the disorder into which they had fallen.\n\nMy aunt was quite gracious on the subject of the Thames (it really did look very well with the sun upon it, though not like the sea before the cottage), but she could not relent towards the London smoke, which, she said, ‘peppered everything’. A complete revolution, in which Peggotty bore a prominent part, was being effected in every corner of my rooms, in regard of this pepper; and I was looking on, thinking how little even Peggotty seemed to do with a good deal of bustle, and how much Agnes did without any bustle at all, when a knock came at the door.\n\n‘I think,’ said Agnes, turning pale, ‘it’s papa. He promised me that he would come.’\n\nI opened the door, and admitted, not only Mr. Wickfield, but Uriah Heep. I had not seen Mr. Wickfield for some time. I was prepared for a great change in him, after what I had heard from Agnes, but his appearance shocked me.\n\nIt was not that he looked many years older, though still dressed with the old scrupulous cleanliness; or that there was an unwholesome ruddiness upon his face; or that his eyes were full and bloodshot; or that there was a nervous trembling in his hand, the cause of which I knew, and had for some years seen at work. It was not that he had lost his good looks, or his old bearing of a gentleman--for that he had not--but the thing that struck me most, was, that with the evidences of his native superiority still upon him, he should submit himself to that crawling impersonation of meanness, Uriah Heep. The reversal of the two natures, in their relative positions, Uriah’s of power and Mr. Wickfield’s of dependence, was a sight more painful to me than I can express. If I had seen an Ape taking command of a Man, I should hardly have thought it a more degrading spectacle.\n\nHe appeared to be only too conscious of it himself. When he came in, he stood still; and with his head bowed, as if he felt it. This was only for a moment; for Agnes softly said to him, ‘Papa! Here is Miss Trotwood--and Trotwood, whom you have not seen for a long while!’ and then he approached, and constrainedly gave my aunt his hand, and shook hands more cordially with me. In the moment’s pause I speak of, I saw Uriah’s countenance form itself into a most ill-favoured smile. Agnes saw it too, I think, for she shrank from him.\n\nWhat my aunt saw, or did not see, I defy the science of physiognomy to have made out, without her own consent. I believe there never was anybody with such an imperturbable countenance when she chose. Her face might have been a dead-wall on the occasion in question, for any light it threw upon her thoughts; until she broke silence with her usual abruptness.\n\n‘Well, Wickfield!’ said my aunt; and he looked up at her for the first time. ‘I have been telling your daughter how well I have been disposing of my money for myself, because I couldn’t trust it to you, as you were growing rusty in business matters. We have been taking counsel together, and getting on very well, all things considered. Agnes is worth the whole firm, in my opinion.’\n\n‘If I may umbly make the remark,’ said Uriah Heep, with a writhe, ‘I fully agree with Miss Betsey Trotwood, and should be only too appy if Miss Agnes was a partner.’\n\n‘You’re a partner yourself, you know,’ returned my aunt, ‘and that’s about enough for you, I expect. How do you find yourself, sir?’\n\nIn acknowledgement of this question, addressed to him with extraordinary curtness, Mr. Heep, uncomfortably clutching the blue bag he carried, replied that he was pretty well, he thanked my aunt, and hoped she was the same.\n\n‘And you, Master--I should say, Mister Copperfield,’ pursued Uriah. ‘I hope I see you well! I am rejoiced to see you, Mister Copperfield, even under present circumstances.’ I believed that; for he seemed to relish them very much. ‘Present circumstances is not what your friends would wish for you, Mister Copperfield, but it isn’t money makes the man: it’s--I am really unequal with my umble powers to express what it is,’ said Uriah, with a fawning jerk, ‘but it isn’t money!’\n\nHere he shook hands with me: not in the common way, but standing at a good distance from me, and lifting my hand up and down like a pump handle, that he was a little afraid of.\n\n‘And how do you think we are looking, Master Copperfield,--I should say, Mister?’ fawned Uriah. ‘Don’t you find Mr. Wickfield blooming, sir? Years don’t tell much in our firm, Master Copperfield, except in raising up the umble, namely, mother and self--and in developing,’ he added, as an afterthought, ‘the beautiful, namely, Miss Agnes.’\n\nHe jerked himself about, after this compliment, in such an intolerable manner, that my aunt, who had sat looking straight at him, lost all patience.\n\n‘Deuce take the man!’ said my aunt, sternly, ‘what’s he about? Don’t be galvanic, sir!’\n\n‘I ask your pardon, Miss Trotwood,’ returned Uriah; ‘I’m aware you’re nervous.’\n\n‘Go along with you, sir!’ said my aunt, anything but appeased. ‘Don’t presume to say so! I am nothing of the sort. If you’re an eel, sir, conduct yourself like one. If you’re a man, control your limbs, sir! Good God!’ said my aunt, with great indignation, ‘I am not going to be serpentined and corkscrewed out of my senses!’\n\nMr. Heep was rather abashed, as most people might have been, by this explosion; which derived great additional force from the indignant manner in which my aunt afterwards moved in her chair, and shook her head as if she were making snaps or bounces at him. But he said to me aside in a meek voice:\n\n‘I am well aware, Master Copperfield, that Miss Trotwood, though an excellent lady, has a quick temper (indeed I think I had the pleasure of knowing her, when I was a numble clerk, before you did, Master Copperfield), and it’s only natural, I am sure, that it should be made quicker by present circumstances. The wonder is, that it isn’t much worse! I only called to say that if there was anything we could do, in present circumstances, mother or self, or Wickfield and Heep,--we should be really glad. I may go so far?’ said Uriah, with a sickly smile at his partner.\n\n‘Uriah Heep,’ said Mr. Wickfield, in a monotonous forced way, ‘is active in the business, Trotwood. What he says, I quite concur in. You know I had an old interest in you. Apart from that, what Uriah says I quite concur in!’\n\n‘Oh, what a reward it is,’ said Uriah, drawing up one leg, at the risk of bringing down upon himself another visitation from my aunt, ‘to be so trusted in! But I hope I am able to do something to relieve him from the fatigues of business, Master Copperfield!’\n\n‘Uriah Heep is a great relief to me,’ said Mr. Wickfield, in the same dull voice. ‘It’s a load off my mind, Trotwood, to have such a partner.’\n\nThe red fox made him say all this, I knew, to exhibit him to me in the light he had indicated on the night when he poisoned my rest. I saw the same ill-favoured smile upon his face again, and saw how he watched me.\n\n‘You are not going, papa?’ said Agnes, anxiously. ‘Will you not walk back with Trotwood and me?’\n\nHe would have looked to Uriah, I believe, before replying, if that worthy had not anticipated him.\n\n‘I am bespoke myself,’ said Uriah, ‘on business; otherwise I should have been appy to have kept with my friends. But I leave my partner to represent the firm. Miss Agnes, ever yours! I wish you good-day, Master Copperfield, and leave my umble respects for Miss Betsey Trotwood.’\n\nWith those words, he retired, kissing his great hand, and leering at us like a mask.\n\nWe sat there, talking about our pleasant old Canterbury days, an hour or two. Mr. Wickfield, left to Agnes, soon became more like his former self; though there was a settled depression upon him, which he never shook off. For all that, he brightened; and had an evident pleasure in hearing us recall the little incidents of our old life, many of which he remembered very well. He said it was like those times, to be alone with Agnes and me again; and he wished to Heaven they had never changed. I am sure there was an influence in the placid face of Agnes, and in the very touch of her hand upon his arm, that did wonders for him.\n\nMy aunt (who was busy nearly all this while with Peggotty, in the inner room) would not accompany us to the place where they were staying, but insisted on my going; and I went. We dined together. After dinner, Agnes sat beside him, as of old, and poured out his wine. He took what she gave him, and no more--like a child--and we all three sat together at a window as the evening gathered in. When it was almost dark, he lay down on a sofa, Agnes pillowing his head and bending over him a little while; and when she came back to the window, it was not so dark but I could see tears glittering in her eyes.\n\nI pray Heaven that I never may forget the dear girl in her love and truth, at that time of my life; for if I should, I must be drawing near the end, and then I would desire to remember her best! She filled my heart with such good resolutions, strengthened my weakness so, by her example, so directed--I know not how, she was too modest and gentle to advise me in many words--the wandering ardour and unsettled purpose within me, that all the little good I have done, and all the harm I have forborne, I solemnly believe I may refer to her.\n\nAnd how she spoke to me of Dora, sitting at the window in the dark; listened to my praises of her; praised again; and round the little fairy-figure shed some glimpses of her own pure light, that made it yet more precious and more innocent to me! Oh, Agnes, sister of my boyhood, if I had known then, what I knew long afterwards--!\n\nThere was a beggar in the street, when I went down; and as I turned my head towards the window, thinking of her calm seraphic eyes, he made me start by muttering, as if he were an echo of the morning: ‘Blind! Blind! Blind!’\n\n# CHAPTER 36. ENTHUSIASM\n\nI began the next day with another dive into the Roman bath, and then started for Highgate. I was not dispirited now. I was not afraid of the shabby coat, and had no yearnings after gallant greys. My whole manner of thinking of our late misfortune was changed. What I had to do, was, to show my aunt that her past goodness to me had not been thrown away on an insensible, ungrateful object. What I had to do, was, to turn the painful discipline of my younger days to account, by going to work with a resolute and steady heart. What I had to do, was, to take my woodman’s axe in my hand, and clear my own way through the forest of difficulty, by cutting down the trees until I came to Dora. And I went on at a mighty rate, as if it could be done by walking.\n\nWhen I found myself on the familiar Highgate road, pursuing such a different errand from that old one of pleasure, with which it was associated, it seemed as if a complete change had come on my whole life. But that did not discourage me. With the new life, came new purpose, new intention. Great was the labour; priceless the reward. Dora was the reward, and Dora must be won.\n\nI got into such a transport, that I felt quite sorry my coat was not a little shabby already. I wanted to be cutting at those trees in the forest of difficulty, under circumstances that should prove my strength. I had a good mind to ask an old man, in wire spectacles, who was breaking stones upon the road, to lend me his hammer for a little while, and let me begin to beat a path to Dora out of granite. I stimulated myself into such a heat, and got so out of breath, that I felt as if I had been earning I don’t know how much.\n\nIn this state, I went into a cottage that I saw was to let, and examined it narrowly,--for I felt it necessary to be practical. It would do for me and Dora admirably: with a little front garden for Jip to run about in, and bark at the tradespeople through the railings, and a capital room upstairs for my aunt. I came out again, hotter and faster than ever, and dashed up to Highgate, at such a rate that I was there an hour too early; and, though I had not been, should have been obliged to stroll about to cool myself, before I was at all presentable.\n\nMy first care, after putting myself under this necessary course of preparation, was to find the Doctor’s house. It was not in that part of Highgate where Mrs. Steerforth lived, but quite on the opposite side of the little town. When I had made this discovery, I went back, in an attraction I could not resist, to a lane by Mrs. Steerforth’s, and looked over the corner of the garden wall. His room was shut up close. The conservatory doors were standing open, and Rosa Dartle was walking, bareheaded, with a quick, impetuous step, up and down a gravel walk on one side of the lawn. She gave me the idea of some fierce thing, that was dragging the length of its chain to and fro upon a beaten track, and wearing its heart out.\n\nI came softly away from my place of observation, and avoiding that part of the neighbourhood, and wishing I had not gone near it, strolled about until it was ten o’clock. The church with the slender spire, that stands on the top of the hill now, was not there then to tell me the time. An old red-brick mansion, used as a school, was in its place; and a fine old house it must have been to go to school at, as I recollect it.\n\nWhen I approached the Doctor’s cottage--a pretty old place, on which he seemed to have expended some money, if I might judge from the embellishments and repairs that had the look of being just completed--I saw him walking in the garden at the side, gaiters and all, as if he had never left off walking since the days of my pupilage. He had his old companions about him, too; for there were plenty of high trees in the neighbourhood, and two or three rooks were on the grass, looking after him, as if they had been written to about him by the Canterbury rooks, and were observing him closely in consequence.\n\nKnowing the utter hopelessness of attracting his attention from that distance, I made bold to open the gate, and walk after him, so as to meet him when he should turn round. When he did, and came towards me, he looked at me thoughtfully for a few moments, evidently without thinking about me at all; and then his benevolent face expressed extraordinary pleasure, and he took me by both hands.\n\n‘Why, my dear Copperfield,’ said the Doctor, ‘you are a man! How do you do? I am delighted to see you. My dear Copperfield, how very much you have improved! You are quite--yes--dear me!’\n\nI hoped he was well, and Mrs. Strong too.\n\n‘Oh dear, yes!’ said the Doctor; ‘Annie’s quite well, and she’ll be delighted to see you. You were always her favourite. She said so, last night, when I showed her your letter. And--yes, to be sure--you recollect Mr. Jack Maldon, Copperfield?’\n\n‘Perfectly, sir.’\n\n‘Of course,’ said the Doctor. ‘To be sure. He’s pretty well, too.’\n\n‘Has he come home, sir?’ I inquired.\n\n‘From India?’ said the Doctor. ‘Yes. Mr. Jack Maldon couldn’t bear the climate, my dear. Mrs. Markleham--you have not forgotten Mrs. Markleham?’\n\nForgotten the Old Soldier! And in that short time!\n\n‘Mrs. Markleham,’ said the Doctor, ‘was quite vexed about him, poor thing; so we have got him at home again; and we have bought him a little Patent place, which agrees with him much better.’ I knew enough of Mr. Jack Maldon to suspect from this account that it was a place where there was not much to do, and which was pretty well paid. The Doctor, walking up and down with his hand on my shoulder, and his kind face turned encouragingly to mine, went on:\n\n‘Now, my dear Copperfield, in reference to this proposal of yours. It’s very gratifying and agreeable to me, I am sure; but don’t you think you could do better? You achieved distinction, you know, when you were with us. You are qualified for many good things. You have laid a foundation that any edifice may be raised upon; and is it not a pity that you should devote the spring-time of your life to such a poor pursuit as I can offer?’\n\nI became very glowing again, and, expressing myself in a rhapsodical style, I am afraid, urged my request strongly; reminding the Doctor that I had already a profession.\n\n‘Well, well,’ said the Doctor, ‘that’s true. Certainly, your having a profession, and being actually engaged in studying it, makes a difference. But, my good young friend, what’s seventy pounds a year?’\n\n‘It doubles our income, Doctor Strong,’ said I.\n\n‘Dear me!’ replied the Doctor. ‘To think of that! Not that I mean to say it’s rigidly limited to seventy pounds a-year, because I have always contemplated making any young friend I might thus employ, a present too. Undoubtedly,’ said the Doctor, still walking me up and down with his hand on my shoulder. ‘I have always taken an annual present into account.’\n\n‘My dear tutor,’ said I (now, really, without any nonsense), ‘to whom I owe more obligations already than I ever can acknowledge--’\n\n‘No, no,’ interposed the Doctor. ‘Pardon me!’\n\n‘If you will take such time as I have, and that is my mornings and evenings, and can think it worth seventy pounds a year, you will do me such a service as I cannot express.’\n\n‘Dear me!’ said the Doctor, innocently. ‘To think that so little should go for so much! Dear, dear! And when you can do better, you will? On your word, now?’ said the Doctor,--which he had always made a very grave appeal to the honour of us boys.\n\n‘On my word, sir!’ I returned, answering in our old school manner.\n\n‘Then be it so,’ said the Doctor, clapping me on the shoulder, and still keeping his hand there, as we still walked up and down.\n\n‘And I shall be twenty times happier, sir,’ said I, with a little--I hope innocent--flattery, ‘if my employment is to be on the Dictionary.’\n\nThe Doctor stopped, smilingly clapped me on the shoulder again, and exclaimed, with a triumph most delightful to behold, as if I had penetrated to the profoundest depths of mortal sagacity, ‘My dear young friend, you have hit it. It IS the Dictionary!’\n\nHow could it be anything else! His pockets were as full of it as his head. It was sticking out of him in all directions. He told me that since his retirement from scholastic life, he had been advancing with it wonderfully; and that nothing could suit him better than the proposed arrangements for morning and evening work, as it was his custom to walk about in the daytime with his considering cap on. His papers were in a little confusion, in consequence of Mr. Jack Maldon having lately proffered his occasional services as an amanuensis, and not being accustomed to that occupation; but we should soon put right what was amiss, and go on swimmingly. Afterwards, when we were fairly at our work, I found Mr. Jack Maldon’s efforts more troublesome to me than I had expected, as he had not confined himself to making numerous mistakes, but had sketched so many soldiers, and ladies’ heads, over the Doctor’s manuscript, that I often became involved in labyrinths of obscurity.\n\nThe Doctor was quite happy in the prospect of our going to work together on that wonderful performance, and we settled to begin next morning at seven o’clock. We were to work two hours every morning, and two or three hours every night, except on Saturdays, when I was to rest. On Sundays, of course, I was to rest also, and I considered these very easy terms.\n\nOur plans being thus arranged to our mutual satisfaction, the Doctor took me into the house to present me to Mrs. Strong, whom we found in the Doctor’s new study, dusting his books,--a freedom which he never permitted anybody else to take with those sacred favourites.\n\nThey had postponed their breakfast on my account, and we sat down to table together. We had not been seated long, when I saw an approaching arrival in Mrs. Strong’s face, before I heard any sound of it. A gentleman on horseback came to the gate, and leading his horse into the little court, with the bridle over his arm, as if he were quite at home, tied him to a ring in the empty coach-house wall, and came into the breakfast parlour, whip in hand. It was Mr. Jack Maldon; and Mr. Jack Maldon was not at all improved by India, I thought. I was in a state of ferocious virtue, however, as to young men who were not cutting down trees in the forest of difficulty; and my impression must be received with due allowance.\n\n‘Mr. Jack!’ said the Doctor. ‘Copperfield!’\n\nMr. Jack Maldon shook hands with me; but not very warmly, I believed; and with an air of languid patronage, at which I secretly took great umbrage. But his languor altogether was quite a wonderful sight; except when he addressed himself to his cousin Annie. ‘Have you breakfasted this morning, Mr. Jack?’ said the Doctor.\n\n‘I hardly ever take breakfast, sir,’ he replied, with his head thrown back in an easy-chair. ‘I find it bores me.’\n\n‘Is there any news today?’ inquired the Doctor.\n\n‘Nothing at all, sir,’ replied Mr. Maldon. ‘There’s an account about the people being hungry and discontented down in the North, but they are always being hungry and discontented somewhere.’\n\nThe Doctor looked grave, and said, as though he wished to change the subject, ‘Then there’s no news at all; and no news, they say, is good news.’\n\n‘There’s a long statement in the papers, sir, about a murder,’ observed Mr. Maldon. ‘But somebody is always being murdered, and I didn’t read it.’\n\nA display of indifference to all the actions and passions of mankind was not supposed to be such a distinguished quality at that time, I think, as I have observed it to be considered since. I have known it very fashionable indeed. I have seen it displayed with such success, that I have encountered some fine ladies and gentlemen who might as well have been born caterpillars. Perhaps it impressed me the more then, because it was new to me, but it certainly did not tend to exalt my opinion of, or to strengthen my confidence in, Mr. Jack Maldon.\n\n‘I came out to inquire whether Annie would like to go to the opera tonight,’ said Mr. Maldon, turning to her. ‘It’s the last good night there will be, this season; and there’s a singer there, whom she really ought to hear. She is perfectly exquisite. Besides which, she is so charmingly ugly,’ relapsing into languor.\n\nThe Doctor, ever pleased with what was likely to please his young wife, turned to her and said:\n\n‘You must go, Annie. You must go.’\n\n‘I would rather not,’ she said to the Doctor. ‘I prefer to remain at home. I would much rather remain at home.’\n\nWithout looking at her cousin, she then addressed me, and asked me about Agnes, and whether she should see her, and whether she was not likely to come that day; and was so much disturbed, that I wondered how even the Doctor, buttering his toast, could be blind to what was so obvious.\n\nBut he saw nothing. He told her, good-naturedly, that she was young and ought to be amused and entertained, and must not allow herself to be made dull by a dull old fellow. Moreover, he said, he wanted to hear her sing all the new singer’s songs to him; and how could she do that well, unless she went? So the Doctor persisted in making the engagement for her, and Mr. Jack Maldon was to come back to dinner. This concluded, he went to his Patent place, I suppose; but at all events went away on his horse, looking very idle.\n\nI was curious to find out next morning, whether she had been. She had not, but had sent into London to put her cousin off; and had gone out in the afternoon to see Agnes, and had prevailed upon the Doctor to go with her; and they had walked home by the fields, the Doctor told me, the evening being delightful. I wondered then, whether she would have gone if Agnes had not been in town, and whether Agnes had some good influence over her too!\n\nShe did not look very happy, I thought; but it was a good face, or a very false one. I often glanced at it, for she sat in the window all the time we were at work; and made our breakfast, which we took by snatches as we were employed. When I left, at nine o’clock, she was kneeling on the ground at the Doctor’s feet, putting on his shoes and gaiters for him. There was a softened shade upon her face, thrown from some green leaves overhanging the open window of the low room; and I thought all the way to Doctors’ Commons, of the night when I had seen it looking at him as he read.\n\nI was pretty busy now; up at five in the morning, and home at nine or ten at night. But I had infinite satisfaction in being so closely engaged, and never walked slowly on any account, and felt enthusiastically that the more I tired myself, the more I was doing to deserve Dora. I had not revealed myself in my altered character to Dora yet, because she was coming to see Miss Mills in a few days, and I deferred all I had to tell her until then; merely informing her in my letters (all our communications were secretly forwarded through Miss Mills), that I had much to tell her. In the meantime, I put myself on a short allowance of bear’s grease, wholly abandoned scented soap and lavender water, and sold off three waistcoats at a prodigious sacrifice, as being too luxurious for my stern career.\n\nNot satisfied with all these proceedings, but burning with impatience to do something more, I went to see Traddles, now lodging up behind the parapet of a house in Castle Street, Holborn. Mr. Dick, who had been with me to Highgate twice already, and had resumed his companionship with the Doctor, I took with me.\n\nI took Mr. Dick with me, because, acutely sensitive to my aunt’s reverses, and sincerely believing that no galley-slave or convict worked as I did, he had begun to fret and worry himself out of spirits and appetite, as having nothing useful to do. In this condition, he felt more incapable of finishing the Memorial than ever; and the harder he worked at it, the oftener that unlucky head of King Charles the First got into it. Seriously apprehending that his malady would increase, unless we put some innocent deception upon him and caused him to believe that he was useful, or unless we could put him in the way of being really useful (which would be better), I made up my mind to try if Traddles could help us. Before we went, I wrote Traddles a full statement of all that had happened, and Traddles wrote me back a capital answer, expressive of his sympathy and friendship.\n\nWe found him hard at work with his inkstand and papers, refreshed by the sight of the flower-pot stand and the little round table in a corner of the small apartment. He received us cordially, and made friends with Mr. Dick in a moment. Mr. Dick professed an absolute certainty of having seen him before, and we both said, ‘Very likely.’\n\nThe first subject on which I had to consult Traddles was this,--I had heard that many men distinguished in various pursuits had begun life by reporting the debates in Parliament. Traddles having mentioned newspapers to me, as one of his hopes, I had put the two things together, and told Traddles in my letter that I wished to know how I could qualify myself for this pursuit. Traddles now informed me, as the result of his inquiries, that the mere mechanical acquisition necessary, except in rare cases, for thorough excellence in it, that is to say, a perfect and entire command of the mystery of short-hand writing and reading, was about equal in difficulty to the mastery of six languages; and that it might perhaps be attained, by dint of perseverance, in the course of a few years. Traddles reasonably supposed that this would settle the business; but I, only feeling that here indeed were a few tall trees to be hewn down, immediately resolved to work my way on to Dora through this thicket, axe in hand.\n\n‘I am very much obliged to you, my dear Traddles!’ said I. ‘I’ll begin tomorrow.’\n\nTraddles looked astonished, as he well might; but he had no notion as yet of my rapturous condition.\n\n‘I’ll buy a book,’ said I, ‘with a good scheme of this art in it; I’ll work at it at the Commons, where I haven’t half enough to do; I’ll take down the speeches in our court for practice--Traddles, my dear fellow, I’ll master it!’\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Traddles, opening his eyes, ‘I had no idea you were such a determined character, Copperfield!’\n\nI don’t know how he should have had, for it was new enough to me. I passed that off, and brought Mr. Dick on the carpet.\n\n‘You see,’ said Mr. Dick, wistfully, ‘if I could exert myself, Mr. Traddles--if I could beat a drum--or blow anything!’\n\nPoor fellow! I have little doubt he would have preferred such an employment in his heart to all others. Traddles, who would not have smiled for the world, replied composedly:\n\n‘But you are a very good penman, sir. You told me so, Copperfield?’ ‘Excellent!’ said I. And indeed he was. He wrote with extraordinary neatness.\n\n‘Don’t you think,’ said Traddles, ‘you could copy writings, sir, if I got them for you?’\n\nMr. Dick looked doubtfully at me. ‘Eh, Trotwood?’\n\nI shook my head. Mr. Dick shook his, and sighed. ‘Tell him about the Memorial,’ said Mr. Dick.\n\nI explained to Traddles that there was a difficulty in keeping King Charles the First out of Mr. Dick’s manuscripts; Mr. Dick in the meanwhile looking very deferentially and seriously at Traddles, and sucking his thumb.\n\n‘But these writings, you know, that I speak of, are already drawn up and finished,’ said Traddles after a little consideration. ‘Mr. Dick has nothing to do with them. Wouldn’t that make a difference, Copperfield? At all events, wouldn’t it be well to try?’\n\nThis gave us new hope. Traddles and I laying our heads together apart, while Mr. Dick anxiously watched us from his chair, we concocted a scheme in virtue of which we got him to work next day, with triumphant success.\n\nOn a table by the window in Buckingham Street, we set out the work Traddles procured for him--which was to make, I forget how many copies of a legal document about some right of way--and on another table we spread the last unfinished original of the great Memorial. Our instructions to Mr. Dick were that he should copy exactly what he had before him, without the least departure from the original; and that when he felt it necessary to make the slightest allusion to King Charles the First, he should fly to the Memorial. We exhorted him to be resolute in this, and left my aunt to observe him. My aunt reported to us, afterwards, that, at first, he was like a man playing the kettle-drums, and constantly divided his attentions between the two; but that, finding this confuse and fatigue him, and having his copy there, plainly before his eyes, he soon sat at it in an orderly business-like manner, and postponed the Memorial to a more convenient time. In a word, although we took great care that he should have no more to do than was good for him, and although he did not begin with the beginning of a week, he earned by the following Saturday night ten shillings and nine-pence; and never, while I live, shall I forget his going about to all the shops in the neighbourhood to change this treasure into sixpences, or his bringing them to my aunt arranged in the form of a heart upon a waiter, with tears of joy and pride in his eyes. He was like one under the propitious influence of a charm, from the moment of his being usefully employed; and if there were a happy man in the world, that Saturday night, it was the grateful creature who thought my aunt the most wonderful woman in existence, and me the most wonderful young man.\n\n‘No starving now, Trotwood,’ said Mr. Dick, shaking hands with me in a corner. ‘I’ll provide for her, Sir!’ and he flourished his ten fingers in the air, as if they were ten banks.\n\nI hardly know which was the better pleased, Traddles or I. ‘It really,’ said Traddles, suddenly, taking a letter out of his pocket, and giving it to me, ‘put Mr. Micawber quite out of my head!’\n\nThe letter (Mr. Micawber never missed any possible opportunity of writing a letter) was addressed to me, ‘By the kindness of T. Traddles, Esquire, of the Inner Temple.’ It ran thus:--\n\n‘You may possibly not be unprepared to receive the intimation that something has turned up. I may have mentioned to you on a former occasion that I was in expectation of such an event.\n\n‘I am about to establish myself in one of the provincial towns of our favoured island (where the society may be described as a happy admixture of the agricultural and the clerical), in immediate connexion with one of the learned professions. Mrs. Micawber and our offspring will accompany me. Our ashes, at a future period, will probably be found commingled in the cemetery attached to a venerable pile, for which the spot to which I refer has acquired a reputation, shall I say from China to Peru?\n\n‘In bidding adieu to the modern Babylon, where we have undergone many vicissitudes, I trust not ignobly, Mrs. Micawber and myself cannot disguise from our minds that we part, it may be for years and it may be for ever, with an individual linked by strong associations to the altar of our domestic life. If, on the eve of such a departure, you will accompany our mutual friend, Mr. Thomas Traddles, to our present abode, and there reciprocate the wishes natural to the occasion, you will confer a Boon\n\n‘On ‘One ‘Who ‘Is ‘Ever yours, ‘WILKINS MICAWBER.’\n\nI was glad to find that Mr. Micawber had got rid of his dust and ashes, and that something really had turned up at last. Learning from Traddles that the invitation referred to the evening then wearing away, I expressed my readiness to do honour to it; and we went off together to the lodging which Mr. Micawber occupied as Mr. Mortimer, and which was situated near the top of the Gray’s Inn Road.\n\nThe resources of this lodging were so limited, that we found the twins, now some eight or nine years old, reposing in a turn-up bedstead in the family sitting-room, where Mr. Micawber had prepared, in a wash-hand-stand jug, what he called ‘a Brew’ of the agreeable beverage for which he was famous. I had the pleasure, on this occasion, of renewing the acquaintance of Master Micawber, whom I found a promising boy of about twelve or thirteen, very subject to that restlessness of limb which is not an unfrequent phenomenon in youths of his age. I also became once more known to his sister, Miss Micawber, in whom, as Mr. Micawber told us, ‘her mother renewed her youth, like the Phoenix’.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘yourself and Mr. Traddles find us on the brink of migration, and will excuse any little discomforts incidental to that position.’\n\nGlancing round as I made a suitable reply, I observed that the family effects were already packed, and that the amount of luggage was by no means overwhelming. I congratulated Mrs. Micawber on the approaching change.\n\n‘My dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘of your friendly interest in all our affairs, I am well assured. My family may consider it banishment, if they please; but I am a wife and mother, and I never will desert Mr. Micawber.’\n\nTraddles, appealed to by Mrs. Micawber’s eye, feelingly acquiesced.\n\n‘That,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘that, at least, is my view, my dear Mr. Copperfield and Mr. Traddles, of the obligation which I took upon myself when I repeated the irrevocable words, “I, Emma, take thee, Wilkins.” I read the service over with a flat-candle on the previous night, and the conclusion I derived from it was, that I never could desert Mr. Micawber. And,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘though it is possible I may be mistaken in my view of the ceremony, I never will!’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Micawber, a little impatiently, ‘I am not conscious that you are expected to do anything of the sort.’\n\n‘I am aware, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ pursued Mrs. Micawber, ‘that I am now about to cast my lot among strangers; and I am also aware that the various members of my family, to whom Mr. Micawber has written in the most gentlemanly terms, announcing that fact, have not taken the least notice of Mr. Micawber’s communication. Indeed I may be superstitious,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘but it appears to me that Mr. Micawber is destined never to receive any answers whatever to the great majority of the communications he writes. I may augur, from the silence of my family, that they object to the resolution I have taken; but I should not allow myself to be swerved from the path of duty, Mr. Copperfield, even by my papa and mama, were they still living.’\n\nI expressed my opinion that this was going in the right direction. ‘It may be a sacrifice,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘to immure one’s-self in a Cathedral town; but surely, Mr. Copperfield, if it is a sacrifice in me, it is much more a sacrifice in a man of Mr. Micawber’s abilities.’\n\n‘Oh! You are going to a Cathedral town?’ said I.\n\nMr. Micawber, who had been helping us all, out of the wash-hand-stand jug, replied:\n\n‘To Canterbury. In fact, my dear Copperfield, I have entered into arrangements, by virtue of which I stand pledged and contracted to our friend Heep, to assist and serve him in the capacity of--and to be--his confidential clerk.’\n\nI stared at Mr. Micawber, who greatly enjoyed my surprise.\n\n‘I am bound to state to you,’ he said, with an official air, ‘that the business habits, and the prudent suggestions, of Mrs. Micawber, have in a great measure conduced to this result. The gauntlet, to which Mrs. Micawber referred upon a former occasion, being thrown down in the form of an advertisement, was taken up by my friend Heep, and led to a mutual recognition. Of my friend Heep,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘who is a man of remarkable shrewdness, I desire to speak with all possible respect. My friend Heep has not fixed the positive remuneration at too high a figure, but he has made a great deal, in the way of extrication from the pressure of pecuniary difficulties, contingent on the value of my services; and on the value of those services I pin my faith. Such address and intelligence as I chance to possess,’ said Mr. Micawber, boastfully disparaging himself, with the old genteel air, ‘will be devoted to my friend Heep’s service. I have already some acquaintance with the law--as a defendant on civil process--and I shall immediately apply myself to the Commentaries of one of the most eminent and remarkable of our English jurists. I believe it is unnecessary to add that I allude to Mr. justice Blackstone.’\n\nThese observations, and indeed the greater part of the observations made that evening, were interrupted by Mrs. Micawber’s discovering that Master Micawber was sitting on his boots, or holding his head on with both arms as if he felt it loose, or accidentally kicking Traddles under the table, or shuffling his feet over one another, or producing them at distances from himself apparently outrageous to nature, or lying sideways with his hair among the wine-glasses, or developing his restlessness of limb in some other form incompatible with the general interests of society; and by Master Micawber’s receiving those discoveries in a resentful spirit. I sat all the while, amazed by Mr. Micawber’s disclosure, and wondering what it meant; until Mrs. Micawber resumed the thread of the discourse, and claimed my attention.\n\n‘What I particularly request Mr. Micawber to be careful of, is,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘that he does not, my dear Mr. Copperfield, in applying himself to this subordinate branch of the law, place it out of his power to rise, ultimately, to the top of the tree. I am convinced that Mr. Micawber, giving his mind to a profession so adapted to his fertile resources, and his flow of language, must distinguish himself. Now, for example, Mr. Traddles,’ said Mrs. Micawber, assuming a profound air, ‘a judge, or even say a Chancellor. Does an individual place himself beyond the pale of those preferments by entering on such an office as Mr. Micawber has accepted?’\n\n‘My dear,’ observed Mr. Micawber--but glancing inquisitively at Traddles, too; ‘we have time enough before us, for the consideration of those questions.’\n\n‘Micawber,’ she returned, ‘no! Your mistake in life is, that you do not look forward far enough. You are bound, in justice to your family, if not to yourself, to take in at a comprehensive glance the extremest point in the horizon to which your abilities may lead you.’\n\nMr. Micawber coughed, and drank his punch with an air of exceeding satisfaction--still glancing at Traddles, as if he desired to have his opinion.\n\n‘Why, the plain state of the case, Mrs. Micawber,’ said Traddles, mildly breaking the truth to her. ‘I mean the real prosaic fact, you know--’\n\n‘Just so,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘my dear Mr. Traddles, I wish to be as prosaic and literal as possible on a subject of so much importance.’\n\n‘--Is,’ said Traddles, ‘that this branch of the law, even if Mr. Micawber were a regular solicitor--’\n\n‘Exactly so,’ returned Mrs. Micawber. (‘Wilkins, you are squinting, and will not be able to get your eyes back.’)\n\n‘--Has nothing,’ pursued Traddles, ‘to do with that. Only a barrister is eligible for such preferments; and Mr. Micawber could not be a barrister, without being entered at an inn of court as a student, for five years.’\n\n‘Do I follow you?’ said Mrs. Micawber, with her most affable air of business. ‘Do I understand, my dear Mr. Traddles, that, at the expiration of that period, Mr. Micawber would be eligible as a Judge or Chancellor?’\n\n‘He would be ELIGIBLE,’ returned Traddles, with a strong emphasis on that word.\n\n‘Thank you,’ said Mrs. Micawber. ‘That is quite sufficient. If such is the case, and Mr. Micawber forfeits no privilege by entering on these duties, my anxiety is set at rest. I speak,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘as a female, necessarily; but I have always been of opinion that Mr. Micawber possesses what I have heard my papa call, when I lived at home, the judicial mind; and I hope Mr. Micawber is now entering on a field where that mind will develop itself, and take a commanding station.’\n\nI quite believe that Mr. Micawber saw himself, in his judicial mind’s eye, on the woolsack. He passed his hand complacently over his bald head, and said with ostentatious resignation:\n\n‘My dear, we will not anticipate the decrees of fortune. If I am reserved to wear a wig, I am at least prepared, externally,’ in allusion to his baldness, ‘for that distinction. I do not,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘regret my hair, and I may have been deprived of it for a specific purpose. I cannot say. It is my intention, my dear Copperfield, to educate my son for the Church; I will not deny that I should be happy, on his account, to attain to eminence.’\n\n‘For the Church?’ said I, still pondering, between whiles, on Uriah Heep.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mr. Micawber. ‘He has a remarkable head-voice, and will commence as a chorister. Our residence at Canterbury, and our local connexion, will, no doubt, enable him to take advantage of any vacancy that may arise in the Cathedral corps.’\n\nOn looking at Master Micawber again, I saw that he had a certain expression of face, as if his voice were behind his eyebrows; where it presently appeared to be, on his singing us (as an alternative between that and bed) ‘The Wood-Pecker tapping’. After many compliments on this performance, we fell into some general conversation; and as I was too full of my desperate intentions to keep my altered circumstances to myself, I made them known to Mr. and Mrs. Micawber. I cannot express how extremely delighted they both were, by the idea of my aunt’s being in difficulties; and how comfortable and friendly it made them.\n\nWhen we were nearly come to the last round of the punch, I addressed myself to Traddles, and reminded him that we must not separate, without wishing our friends health, happiness, and success in their new career. I begged Mr. Micawber to fill us bumpers, and proposed the toast in due form: shaking hands with him across the table, and kissing Mrs. Micawber, to commemorate that eventful occasion. Traddles imitated me in the first particular, but did not consider himself a sufficiently old friend to venture on the second.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, rising with one of his thumbs in each of his waistcoat pockets, ‘the companion of my youth: if I may be allowed the expression--and my esteemed friend Traddles: if I may be permitted to call him so--will allow me, on the part of Mrs. Micawber, myself, and our offspring, to thank them in the warmest and most uncompromising terms for their good wishes. It may be expected that on the eve of a migration which will consign us to a perfectly new existence,’ Mr. Micawber spoke as if they were going five hundred thousand miles, ‘I should offer a few valedictory remarks to two such friends as I see before me. But all that I have to say in this way, I have said. Whatever station in society I may attain, through the medium of the learned profession of which I am about to become an unworthy member, I shall endeavour not to disgrace, and Mrs. Micawber will be safe to adorn. Under the temporary pressure of pecuniary liabilities, contracted with a view to their immediate liquidation, but remaining unliquidated through a combination of circumstances, I have been under the necessity of assuming a garb from which my natural instincts recoil--I allude to spectacles--and possessing myself of a cognomen, to which I can establish no legitimate pretensions. All I have to say on that score is, that the cloud has passed from the dreary scene, and the God of Day is once more high upon the mountain tops. On Monday next, on the arrival of the four o’clock afternoon coach at Canterbury, my foot will be on my native heath--my name, Micawber!’\n\nMr. Micawber resumed his seat on the close of these remarks, and drank two glasses of punch in grave succession. He then said with much solemnity:\n\n‘One thing more I have to do, before this separation is complete, and that is to perform an act of justice. My friend Mr. Thomas Traddles has, on two several occasions, “put his name”, if I may use a common expression, to bills of exchange for my accommodation. On the first occasion Mr. Thomas Traddles was left--let me say, in short, in the lurch. The fulfilment of the second has not yet arrived. The amount of the first obligation,’ here Mr. Micawber carefully referred to papers, ‘was, I believe, twenty-three, four, nine and a half, of the second, according to my entry of that transaction, eighteen, six, two. These sums, united, make a total, if my calculation is correct, amounting to forty-one, ten, eleven and a half. My friend Copperfield will perhaps do me the favour to check that total?’\n\nI did so and found it correct.\n\n‘To leave this metropolis,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘and my friend Mr. Thomas Traddles, without acquitting myself of the pecuniary part of this obligation, would weigh upon my mind to an insupportable extent. I have, therefore, prepared for my friend Mr. Thomas Traddles, and I now hold in my hand, a document, which accomplishes the desired object. I beg to hand to my friend Mr. Thomas Traddles my I.O.U. for forty-one, ten, eleven and a half, and I am happy to recover my moral dignity, and to know that I can once more walk erect before my fellow man!’\n\nWith this introduction (which greatly affected him), Mr. Micawber placed his I.O.U. in the hands of Traddles, and said he wished him well in every relation of life. I am persuaded, not only that this was quite the same to Mr. Micawber as paying the money, but that Traddles himself hardly knew the difference until he had had time to think about it. Mr. Micawber walked so erect before his fellow man, on the strength of this virtuous action, that his chest looked half as broad again when he lighted us downstairs. We parted with great heartiness on both sides; and when I had seen Traddles to his own door, and was going home alone, I thought, among the other odd and contradictory things I mused upon, that, slippery as Mr. Micawber was, I was probably indebted to some compassionate recollection he retained of me as his boy-lodger, for never having been asked by him for money. I certainly should not have had the moral courage to refuse it; and I have no doubt he knew that (to his credit be it written), quite as well as I did.\n\n# CHAPTER 37. A LITTLE COLD WATER\n\nMy new life had lasted for more than a week, and I was stronger than ever in those tremendous practical resolutions that I felt the crisis required. I continued to walk extremely fast, and to have a general idea that I was getting on. I made it a rule to take as much out of myself as I possibly could, in my way of doing everything to which I applied my energies. I made a perfect victim of myself. I even entertained some idea of putting myself on a vegetable diet, vaguely conceiving that, in becoming a graminivorous animal, I should sacrifice to Dora.\n\nAs yet, little Dora was quite unconscious of my desperate firmness, otherwise than as my letters darkly shadowed it forth. But another Saturday came, and on that Saturday evening she was to be at Miss Mills’s; and when Mr. Mills had gone to his whist-club (telegraphed to me in the street, by a bird-cage in the drawing-room middle window), I was to go there to tea.\n\nBy this time, we were quite settled down in Buckingham Street, where Mr. Dick continued his copying in a state of absolute felicity. My aunt had obtained a signal victory over Mrs. Crupp, by paying her off, throwing the first pitcher she planted on the stairs out of window, and protecting in person, up and down the staircase, a supernumerary whom she engaged from the outer world. These vigorous measures struck such terror to the breast of Mrs. Crupp, that she subsided into her own kitchen, under the impression that my aunt was mad. My aunt being supremely indifferent to Mrs. Crupp’s opinion and everybody else’s, and rather favouring than discouraging the idea, Mrs. Crupp, of late the bold, became within a few days so faint-hearted, that rather than encounter my aunt upon the staircase, she would endeavour to hide her portly form behind doors--leaving visible, however, a wide margin of flannel petticoat--or would shrink into dark corners. This gave my aunt such unspeakable satisfaction, that I believe she took a delight in prowling up and down, with her bonnet insanely perched on the top of her head, at times when Mrs. Crupp was likely to be in the way.\n\nMy aunt, being uncommonly neat and ingenious, made so many little improvements in our domestic arrangements, that I seemed to be richer instead of poorer. Among the rest, she converted the pantry into a dressing-room for me; and purchased and embellished a bedstead for my occupation, which looked as like a bookcase in the daytime as a bedstead could. I was the object of her constant solicitude; and my poor mother herself could not have loved me better, or studied more how to make me happy.\n\nPeggotty had considered herself highly privileged in being allowed to participate in these labours; and, although she still retained something of her old sentiment of awe in reference to my aunt, had received so many marks of encouragement and confidence, that they were the best friends possible. But the time had now come (I am speaking of the Saturday when I was to take tea at Miss Mills’s) when it was necessary for her to return home, and enter on the discharge of the duties she had undertaken in behalf of Ham. ‘So good-bye, Barkis,’ said my aunt, ‘and take care of yourself! I am sure I never thought I could be sorry to lose you!’\n\nI took Peggotty to the coach office and saw her off. She cried at parting, and confided her brother to my friendship as Ham had done. We had heard nothing of him since he went away, that sunny afternoon.\n\n‘And now, my own dear Davy,’ said Peggotty, ‘if, while you’re a prentice, you should want any money to spend; or if, when you’re out of your time, my dear, you should want any to set you up (and you must do one or other, or both, my darling); who has such a good right to ask leave to lend it you, as my sweet girl’s own old stupid me!’\n\nI was not so savagely independent as to say anything in reply, but that if ever I borrowed money of anyone, I would borrow it of her. Next to accepting a large sum on the spot, I believe this gave Peggotty more comfort than anything I could have done.\n\n‘And, my dear!’ whispered Peggotty, ‘tell the pretty little angel that I should so have liked to see her, only for a minute! And tell her that before she marries my boy, I’ll come and make your house so beautiful for you, if you’ll let me!’\n\nI declared that nobody else should touch it; and this gave Peggotty such delight that she went away in good spirits.\n\nI fatigued myself as much as I possibly could in the Commons all day, by a variety of devices, and at the appointed time in the evening repaired to Mr. Mills’s street. Mr. Mills, who was a terrible fellow to fall asleep after dinner, had not yet gone out, and there was no bird-cage in the middle window.\n\nHe kept me waiting so long, that I fervently hoped the Club would fine him for being late. At last he came out; and then I saw my own Dora hang up the bird-cage, and peep into the balcony to look for me, and run in again when she saw I was there, while Jip remained behind, to bark injuriously at an immense butcher’s dog in the street, who could have taken him like a pill.\n\nDora came to the drawing-room door to meet me; and Jip came scrambling out, tumbling over his own growls, under the impression that I was a Bandit; and we all three went in, as happy and loving as could be. I soon carried desolation into the bosom of our joys--not that I meant to do it, but that I was so full of the subject--by asking Dora, without the smallest preparation, if she could love a beggar?\n\nMy pretty, little, startled Dora! Her only association with the word was a yellow face and a nightcap, or a pair of crutches, or a wooden leg, or a dog with a decanter-stand in his mouth, or something of that kind; and she stared at me with the most delightful wonder.\n\n‘How can you ask me anything so foolish?’ pouted Dora. ‘Love a beggar!’\n\n‘Dora, my own dearest!’ said I. ‘I am a beggar!’\n\n‘How can you be such a silly thing,’ replied Dora, slapping my hand, ‘as to sit there, telling such stories? I’ll make Jip bite you!’\n\nHer childish way was the most delicious way in the world to me, but it was necessary to be explicit, and I solemnly repeated:\n\n‘Dora, my own life, I am your ruined David!’\n\n‘I declare I’ll make Jip bite you!’ said Dora, shaking her curls, ‘if you are so ridiculous.’\n\nBut I looked so serious, that Dora left off shaking her curls, and laid her trembling little hand upon my shoulder, and first looked scared and anxious, then began to cry. That was dreadful. I fell upon my knees before the sofa, caressing her, and imploring her not to rend my heart; but, for some time, poor little Dora did nothing but exclaim Oh dear! Oh dear! And oh, she was so frightened! And where was Julia Mills! And oh, take her to Julia Mills, and go away, please! until I was almost beside myself.\n\nAt last, after an agony of supplication and protestation, I got Dora to look at me, with a horrified expression of face, which I gradually soothed until it was only loving, and her soft, pretty cheek was lying against mine. Then I told her, with my arms clasped round her, how I loved her, so dearly, and so dearly; how I felt it right to offer to release her from her engagement, because now I was poor; how I never could bear it, or recover it, if I lost her; how I had no fears of poverty, if she had none, my arm being nerved and my heart inspired by her; how I was already working with a courage such as none but lovers knew; how I had begun to be practical, and look into the future; how a crust well earned was sweeter far than a feast inherited; and much more to the same purpose, which I delivered in a burst of passionate eloquence quite surprising to myself, though I had been thinking about it, day and night, ever since my aunt had astonished me.\n\n‘Is your heart mine still, dear Dora?’ said I, rapturously, for I knew by her clinging to me that it was.\n\n‘Oh, yes!’ cried Dora. ‘Oh, yes, it’s all yours. Oh, don’t be dreadful!’\n\nI dreadful! To Dora!\n\n‘Don’t talk about being poor, and working hard!’ said Dora, nestling closer to me. ‘Oh, don’t, don’t!’\n\n‘My dearest love,’ said I, ‘the crust well-earned--’\n\n‘Oh, yes; but I don’t want to hear any more about crusts!’ said Dora. ‘And Jip must have a mutton-chop every day at twelve, or he’ll die.’\n\nI was charmed with her childish, winning way. I fondly explained to Dora that Jip should have his mutton-chop with his accustomed regularity. I drew a picture of our frugal home, made independent by my labour--sketching in the little house I had seen at Highgate, and my aunt in her room upstairs.\n\n‘I am not dreadful now, Dora?’ said I, tenderly.\n\n‘Oh, no, no!’ cried Dora. ‘But I hope your aunt will keep in her own room a good deal. And I hope she’s not a scolding old thing!’\n\nIf it were possible for me to love Dora more than ever, I am sure I did. But I felt she was a little impracticable. It damped my new-born ardour, to find that ardour so difficult of communication to her. I made another trial. When she was quite herself again, and was curling Jip’s ears, as he lay upon her lap, I became grave, and said:\n\n‘My own! May I mention something?’\n\n‘Oh, please don’t be practical!’ said Dora, coaxingly. ‘Because it frightens me so!’\n\n‘Sweetheart!’ I returned; ‘there is nothing to alarm you in all this. I want you to think of it quite differently. I want to make it nerve you, and inspire you, Dora!’\n\n‘Oh, but that’s so shocking!’ cried Dora.\n\n‘My love, no. Perseverance and strength of character will enable us to bear much worse things.’ ‘But I haven’t got any strength at all,’ said Dora, shaking her curls. ‘Have I, Jip? Oh, do kiss Jip, and be agreeable!’\n\nIt was impossible to resist kissing Jip, when she held him up to me for that purpose, putting her own bright, rosy little mouth into kissing form, as she directed the operation, which she insisted should be performed symmetrically, on the centre of his nose. I did as she bade me--rewarding myself afterwards for my obedience--and she charmed me out of my graver character for I don’t know how long.\n\n‘But, Dora, my beloved!’ said I, at last resuming it; ‘I was going to mention something.’\n\nThe judge of the Prerogative Court might have fallen in love with her, to see her fold her little hands and hold them up, begging and praying me not to be dreadful any more.\n\n‘Indeed I am not going to be, my darling!’ I assured her. ‘But, Dora, my love, if you will sometimes think,--not despondingly, you know; far from that!--but if you will sometimes think--just to encourage yourself--that you are engaged to a poor man--’\n\n‘Don’t, don’t! Pray don’t!’ cried Dora. ‘It’s so very dreadful!’\n\n‘My soul, not at all!’ said I, cheerfully. ‘If you will sometimes think of that, and look about now and then at your papa’s housekeeping, and endeavour to acquire a little habit--of accounts, for instance--’\n\nPoor little Dora received this suggestion with something that was half a sob and half a scream.\n\n‘--It would be so useful to us afterwards,’ I went on. ‘And if you would promise me to read a little--a little Cookery Book that I would send you, it would be so excellent for both of us. For our path in life, my Dora,’ said I, warming with the subject, ‘is stony and rugged now, and it rests with us to smooth it. We must fight our way onward. We must be brave. There are obstacles to be met, and we must meet, and crush them!’\n\nI was going on at a great rate, with a clenched hand, and a most enthusiastic countenance; but it was quite unnecessary to proceed. I had said enough. I had done it again. Oh, she was so frightened! Oh, where was Julia Mills! Oh, take her to Julia Mills, and go away, please! So that, in short, I was quite distracted, and raved about the drawing-room.\n\nI thought I had killed her, this time. I sprinkled water on her face. I went down on my knees. I plucked at my hair. I denounced myself as a remorseless brute and a ruthless beast. I implored her forgiveness. I besought her to look up. I ravaged Miss Mills’s work-box for a smelling-bottle, and in my agony of mind applied an ivory needle-case instead, and dropped all the needles over Dora. I shook my fists at Jip, who was as frantic as myself. I did every wild extravagance that could be done, and was a long way beyond the end of my wits when Miss Mills came into the room.\n\n‘Who has done this?’ exclaimed Miss Mills, succouring her friend.\n\nI replied, ‘I, Miss Mills! I have done it! Behold the destroyer!’--or words to that effect--and hid my face from the light, in the sofa cushion.\n\nAt first Miss Mills thought it was a quarrel, and that we were verging on the Desert of Sahara; but she soon found out how matters stood, for my dear affectionate little Dora, embracing her, began exclaiming that I was ‘a poor labourer’; and then cried for me, and embraced me, and asked me would I let her give me all her money to keep, and then fell on Miss Mills’s neck, sobbing as if her tender heart were broken.\n\nMiss Mills must have been born to be a blessing to us. She ascertained from me in a few words what it was all about, comforted Dora, and gradually convinced her that I was not a labourer--from my manner of stating the case I believe Dora concluded that I was a navigator, and went balancing myself up and down a plank all day with a wheelbarrow--and so brought us together in peace. When we were quite composed, and Dora had gone up-stairs to put some rose-water to her eyes, Miss Mills rang for tea. In the ensuing interval, I told Miss Mills that she was evermore my friend, and that my heart must cease to vibrate ere I could forget her sympathy.\n\nI then expounded to Miss Mills what I had endeavoured, so very unsuccessfully, to expound to Dora. Miss Mills replied, on general principles, that the Cottage of content was better than the Palace of cold splendour, and that where love was, all was.\n\nI said to Miss Mills that this was very true, and who should know it better than I, who loved Dora with a love that never mortal had experienced yet? But on Miss Mills observing, with despondency, that it were well indeed for some hearts if this were so, I explained that I begged leave to restrict the observation to mortals of the masculine gender.\n\nI then put it to Miss Mills, to say whether she considered that there was or was not any practical merit in the suggestion I had been anxious to make, concerning the accounts, the housekeeping, and the Cookery\n\nMiss Mills, after some consideration, thus replied:\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield, I will be plain with you. Mental suffering and trial supply, in some natures, the place of years, and I will be as plain with you as if I were a Lady Abbess. No. The suggestion is not appropriate to our Dora. Our dearest Dora is a favourite child of nature. She is a thing of light, and airiness, and joy. I am free to confess that if it could be done, it might be well, but--’ And Miss Mills shook her head.\n\nI was encouraged by this closing admission on the part of Miss Mills to ask her, whether, for Dora’s sake, if she had any opportunity of luring her attention to such preparations for an earnest life, she would avail herself of it? Miss Mills replied in the affirmative so readily, that I further asked her if she would take charge of the Cookery Book; and, if she ever could insinuate it upon Dora’s acceptance, without frightening her, undertake to do me that crowning service. Miss Mills accepted this trust, too; but was not sanguine.\n\nAnd Dora returned, looking such a lovely little creature, that I really doubted whether she ought to be troubled with anything so ordinary. And she loved me so much, and was so captivating (particularly when she made Jip stand on his hind legs for toast, and when she pretended to hold that nose of his against the hot teapot for punishment because he wouldn’t), that I felt like a sort of Monster who had got into a Fairy’s bower, when I thought of having frightened her, and made her cry.\n\nAfter tea we had the guitar; and Dora sang those same dear old French songs about the impossibility of ever on any account leaving off dancing, La ra la, La ra la, until I felt a much greater Monster than before.\n\nWe had only one check to our pleasure, and that happened a little while before I took my leave, when, Miss Mills chancing to make some allusion to tomorrow morning, I unluckily let out that, being obliged to exert myself now, I got up at five o’clock. Whether Dora had any idea that I was a Private Watchman, I am unable to say; but it made a great impression on her, and she neither played nor sang any more.\n\nIt was still on her mind when I bade her adieu; and she said to me, in her pretty coaxing way--as if I were a doll, I used to think:\n\n‘Now don’t get up at five o’clock, you naughty boy. It’s so nonsensical!’\n\n‘My love,’ said I, ‘I have work to do.’\n\n‘But don’t do it!’ returned Dora. ‘Why should you?’\n\nIt was impossible to say to that sweet little surprised face, otherwise than lightly and playfully, that we must work to live.\n\n‘Oh! How ridiculous!’ cried Dora.\n\n‘How shall we live without, Dora?’ said I.\n\n‘How? Any how!’ said Dora.\n\nShe seemed to think she had quite settled the question, and gave me such a triumphant little kiss, direct from her innocent heart, that I would hardly have put her out of conceit with her answer, for a fortune.\n\nWell! I loved her, and I went on loving her, most absorbingly, entirely, and completely. But going on, too, working pretty hard, and busily keeping red-hot all the irons I now had in the fire, I would sit sometimes of a night, opposite my aunt, thinking how I had frightened Dora that time, and how I could best make my way with a guitar-case through the forest of difficulty, until I used to fancy that my head was turning quite grey.\n\n# CHAPTER 38. A DISSOLUTION OF PARTNERSHIP\n\nI did not allow my resolution, with respect to the Parliamentary Debates, to cool. It was one of the irons I began to heat immediately, and one of the irons I kept hot, and hammered at, with a perseverance I may honestly admire. I bought an approved scheme of the noble art and mystery of stenography (which cost me ten and sixpence); and plunged into a sea of perplexity that brought me, in a few weeks, to the confines of distraction. The changes that were rung upon dots, which in such a position meant such a thing, and in such another position something else, entirely different; the wonderful vagaries that were played by circles; the unaccountable consequences that resulted from marks like flies’ legs; the tremendous effects of a curve in a wrong place; not only troubled my waking hours, but reappeared before me in my sleep. When I had groped my way, blindly, through these difficulties, and had mastered the alphabet, which was an Egyptian Temple in itself, there then appeared a procession of new horrors, called arbitrary characters; the most despotic characters I have ever known; who insisted, for instance, that a thing like the beginning of a cobweb, meant expectation, and that a pen-and-ink sky-rocket, stood for disadvantageous. When I had fixed these wretches in my mind, I found that they had driven everything else out of it; then, beginning again, I forgot them; while I was picking them up, I dropped the other fragments of the system; in short, it was almost heart-breaking.\n\nIt might have been quite heart-breaking, but for Dora, who was the stay and anchor of my tempest-driven bark. Every scratch in the scheme was a gnarled oak in the forest of difficulty, and I went on cutting them down, one after another, with such vigour, that in three or four months I was in a condition to make an experiment on one of our crack speakers in the Commons. Shall I ever forget how the crack speaker walked off from me before I began, and left my imbecile pencil staggering about the paper as if it were in a fit!\n\nThis would not do, it was quite clear. I was flying too high, and should never get on, so. I resorted to Traddles for advice; who suggested that he should dictate speeches to me, at a pace, and with occasional stoppages, adapted to my weakness. Very grateful for this friendly aid, I accepted the proposal; and night after night, almost every night, for a long time, we had a sort of Private Parliament in Buckingham Street, after I came home from the Doctor’s.\n\nI should like to see such a Parliament anywhere else! My aunt and Mr. Dick represented the Government or the Opposition (as the case might be), and Traddles, with the assistance of Enfield’s Speakers, or a volume of parliamentary orations, thundered astonishing invectives against them. Standing by the table, with his finger in the page to keep the place, and his right arm flourishing above his head, Traddles, as Mr. Pitt, Mr. Fox, Mr. Sheridan, Mr. Burke, Lord Castlereagh, Viscount Sidmouth, or Mr. Canning, would work himself into the most violent heats, and deliver the most withering denunciations of the profligacy and corruption of my aunt and Mr. Dick; while I used to sit, at a little distance, with my notebook on my knee, fagging after him with all my might and main. The inconsistency and recklessness of Traddles were not to be exceeded by any real politician. He was for any description of policy, in the compass of a week; and nailed all sorts of colours to every denomination of mast. My aunt, looking very like an immovable Chancellor of the Exchequer, would occasionally throw in an interruption or two, as ‘Hear!’ or ‘No!’ or ‘Oh!’ when the text seemed to require it: which was always a signal to Mr. Dick (a perfect country gentleman) to follow lustily with the same cry. But Mr. Dick got taxed with such things in the course of his Parliamentary career, and was made responsible for such awful consequences, that he became uncomfortable in his mind sometimes. I believe he actually began to be afraid he really had been doing something, tending to the annihilation of the British constitution, and the ruin of the country.\n\nOften and often we pursued these debates until the clock pointed to midnight, and the candles were burning down. The result of so much good practice was, that by and by I began to keep pace with Traddles pretty well, and should have been quite triumphant if I had had the least idea what my notes were about. But, as to reading them after I had got them, I might as well have copied the Chinese inscriptions of an immense collection of tea-chests, or the golden characters on all the great red and green bottles in the chemists’ shops!\n\nThere was nothing for it, but to turn back and begin all over again. It was very hard, but I turned back, though with a heavy heart, and began laboriously and methodically to plod over the same tedious ground at a snail’s pace; stopping to examine minutely every speck in the way, on all sides, and making the most desperate efforts to know these elusive characters by sight wherever I met them. I was always punctual at the office; at the Doctor’s too: and I really did work, as the common expression is, like a cart-horse. One day, when I went to the Commons as usual, I found Mr. Spenlow in the doorway looking extremely grave, and talking to himself. As he was in the habit of complaining of pains in his head--he had naturally a short throat, and I do seriously believe he over-starched himself--I was at first alarmed by the idea that he was not quite right in that direction; but he soon relieved my uneasiness.\n\nInstead of returning my ‘Good morning’ with his usual affability, he looked at me in a distant, ceremonious manner, and coldly requested me to accompany him to a certain coffee-house, which, in those days, had a door opening into the Commons, just within the little archway in St. Paul’s Churchyard. I complied, in a very uncomfortable state, and with a warm shooting all over me, as if my apprehensions were breaking out into buds. When I allowed him to go on a little before, on account of the narrowness of the way, I observed that he carried his head with a lofty air that was particularly unpromising; and my mind misgave me that he had found out about my darling Dora.\n\nIf I had not guessed this, on the way to the coffee-house, I could hardly have failed to know what was the matter when I followed him into an upstairs room, and found Miss Murdstone there, supported by a background of sideboard, on which were several inverted tumblers sustaining lemons, and two of those extraordinary boxes, all corners and flutings, for sticking knives and forks in, which, happily for mankind, are now obsolete.\n\nMiss Murdstone gave me her chilly finger-nails, and sat severely rigid. Mr. Spenlow shut the door, motioned me to a chair, and stood on the hearth-rug in front of the fireplace.\n\n‘Have the goodness to show Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr. Spenlow, what you have in your reticule, Miss Murdstone.’\n\nI believe it was the old identical steel-clasped reticule of my childhood, that shut up like a bite. Compressing her lips, in sympathy with the snap, Miss Murdstone opened it--opening her mouth a little at the same time--and produced my last letter to Dora, teeming with expressions of devoted affection.\n\n‘I believe that is your writing, Mr. Copperfield?’ said Mr. Spenlow.\n\nI was very hot, and the voice I heard was very unlike mine, when I said, ‘It is, sir!’\n\n‘If I am not mistaken,’ said Mr. Spenlow, as Miss Murdstone brought a parcel of letters out of her reticule, tied round with the dearest bit of blue ribbon, ‘those are also from your pen, Mr. Copperfield?’\n\nI took them from her with a most desolate sensation; and, glancing at such phrases at the top, as ‘My ever dearest and own Dora,’ ‘My best beloved angel,’ ‘My blessed one for ever,’ and the like, blushed deeply, and inclined my head.\n\n‘No, thank you!’ said Mr. Spenlow, coldly, as I mechanically offered them back to him. ‘I will not deprive you of them. Miss Murdstone, be so good as to proceed!’\n\nThat gentle creature, after a moment’s thoughtful survey of the carpet, delivered herself with much dry unction as follows.\n\n‘I must confess to having entertained my suspicions of Miss Spenlow, in reference to David Copperfield, for some time. I observed Miss Spenlow and David Copperfield, when they first met; and the impression made upon me then was not agreeable. The depravity of the human heart is such--’\n\n‘You will oblige me, ma’am,’ interrupted Mr. Spenlow, ‘by confining yourself to facts.’\n\nMiss Murdstone cast down her eyes, shook her head as if protesting against this unseemly interruption, and with frowning dignity resumed:\n\n‘Since I am to confine myself to facts, I will state them as dryly as I can. Perhaps that will be considered an acceptable course of proceeding. I have already said, sir, that I have had my suspicions of Miss Spenlow, in reference to David Copperfield, for some time. I have frequently endeavoured to find decisive corroboration of those suspicions, but without effect. I have therefore forborne to mention them to Miss Spenlow’s father’; looking severely at him--‘knowing how little disposition there usually is in such cases, to acknowledge the conscientious discharge of duty.’\n\nMr. Spenlow seemed quite cowed by the gentlemanly sternness of Miss Murdstone’s manner, and deprecated her severity with a conciliatory little wave of his hand.\n\n‘On my return to Norwood, after the period of absence occasioned by my brother’s marriage,’ pursued Miss Murdstone in a disdainful voice, ‘and on the return of Miss Spenlow from her visit to her friend Miss Mills, I imagined that the manner of Miss Spenlow gave me greater occasion for suspicion than before. Therefore I watched Miss Spenlow closely.’\n\nDear, tender little Dora, so unconscious of this Dragon’s eye!\n\n‘Still,’ resumed Miss Murdstone, ‘I found no proof until last night. It appeared to me that Miss Spenlow received too many letters from her friend Miss Mills; but Miss Mills being her friend with her father’s full concurrence,’ another telling blow at Mr. Spenlow, ‘it was not for me to interfere. If I may not be permitted to allude to the natural depravity of the human heart, at least I may--I must--be permitted, so far to refer to misplaced confidence.’\n\nMr. Spenlow apologetically murmured his assent.\n\n‘Last evening after tea,’ pursued Miss Murdstone, ‘I observed the little dog starting, rolling, and growling about the drawing-room, worrying something. I said to Miss Spenlow, “Dora, what is that the dog has in his mouth? It’s paper.” Miss Spenlow immediately put her hand to her frock, gave a sudden cry, and ran to the dog. I interposed, and said, “Dora, my love, you must permit me.”’\n\nOh Jip, miserable Spaniel, this wretchedness, then, was your work!\n\n‘Miss Spenlow endeavoured,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘to bribe me with kisses, work-boxes, and small articles of jewellery--that, of course, I pass over. The little dog retreated under the sofa on my approaching him, and was with great difficulty dislodged by the fire-irons. Even when dislodged, he still kept the letter in his mouth; and on my endeavouring to take it from him, at the imminent risk of being bitten, he kept it between his teeth so pertinaciously as to suffer himself to be held suspended in the air by means of the document. At length I obtained possession of it. After perusing it, I taxed Miss Spenlow with having many such letters in her possession; and ultimately obtained from her the packet which is now in David Copperfield’s hand.’\n\nHere she ceased; and snapping her reticule again, and shutting her mouth, looked as if she might be broken, but could never be bent.\n\n‘You have heard Miss Murdstone,’ said Mr. Spenlow, turning to me. ‘I beg to ask, Mr. Copperfield, if you have anything to say in reply?’\n\nThe picture I had before me, of the beautiful little treasure of my heart, sobbing and crying all night--of her being alone, frightened, and wretched, then--of her having so piteously begged and prayed that stony-hearted woman to forgive her--of her having vainly offered her those kisses, work-boxes, and trinkets--of her being in such grievous distress, and all for me--very much impaired the little dignity I had been able to muster. I am afraid I was in a tremulous state for a minute or so, though I did my best to disguise it.\n\n‘There is nothing I can say, sir,’ I returned, ‘except that all the blame is mine. Dora--’\n\n‘Miss Spenlow, if you please,’ said her father, majestically.\n\n‘--was induced and persuaded by me,’ I went on, swallowing that colder designation, ‘to consent to this concealment, and I bitterly regret it.’\n\n‘You are very much to blame, sir,’ said Mr. Spenlow, walking to and fro upon the hearth-rug, and emphasizing what he said with his whole body instead of his head, on account of the stiffness of his cravat and spine. ‘You have done a stealthy and unbecoming action, Mr. Copperfield. When I take a gentleman to my house, no matter whether he is nineteen, twenty-nine, or ninety, I take him there in a spirit of confidence. If he abuses my confidence, he commits a dishonourable action, Mr. Copperfield.’\n\n‘I feel it, sir, I assure you,’ I returned. ‘But I never thought so, before. Sincerely, honestly, indeed, Mr. Spenlow, I never thought so, before. I love Miss Spenlow to that extent--’\n\n‘Pooh! nonsense!’ said Mr. Spenlow, reddening. ‘Pray don’t tell me to my face that you love my daughter, Mr. Copperfield!’\n\n‘Could I defend my conduct if I did not, sir?’ I returned, with all humility.\n\n‘Can you defend your conduct if you do, sir?’ said Mr. Spenlow, stopping short upon the hearth-rug. ‘Have you considered your years, and my daughter’s years, Mr. Copperfield? Have you considered what it is to undermine the confidence that should subsist between my daughter and myself? Have you considered my daughter’s station in life, the projects I may contemplate for her advancement, the testamentary intentions I may have with reference to her? Have you considered anything, Mr. Copperfield?’\n\n‘Very little, sir, I am afraid;’ I answered, speaking to him as respectfully and sorrowfully as I felt; ‘but pray believe me, I have considered my own worldly position. When I explained it to you, we were already engaged--’\n\n‘I BEG,’ said Mr. Spenlow, more like Punch than I had ever seen him, as he energetically struck one hand upon the other--I could not help noticing that even in my despair; ‘that YOU Will NOT talk to me of engagements, Mr. Copperfield!’\n\nThe otherwise immovable Miss Murdstone laughed contemptuously in one short syllable.\n\n‘When I explained my altered position to you, sir,’ I began again, substituting a new form of expression for what was so unpalatable to him, ‘this concealment, into which I am so unhappy as to have led Miss Spenlow, had begun. Since I have been in that altered position, I have strained every nerve, I have exerted every energy, to improve it. I am sure I shall improve it in time. Will you grant me time--any length of time? We are both so young, sir,--’\n\n‘You are right,’ interrupted Mr. Spenlow, nodding his head a great many times, and frowning very much, ‘you are both very young. It’s all nonsense. Let there be an end of the nonsense. Take away those letters, and throw them in the fire. Give me Miss Spenlow’s letters to throw in the fire; and although our future intercourse must, you are aware, be restricted to the Commons here, we will agree to make no further mention of the past. Come, Mr. Copperfield, you don’t want sense; and this is the sensible course.’\n\nNo. I couldn’t think of agreeing to it. I was very sorry, but there was a higher consideration than sense. Love was above all earthly considerations, and I loved Dora to idolatry, and Dora loved me. I didn’t exactly say so; I softened it down as much as I could; but I implied it, and I was resolute upon it. I don’t think I made myself very ridiculous, but I know I was resolute.\n\n‘Very well, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr. Spenlow, ‘I must try my influence with my daughter.’\n\nMiss Murdstone, by an expressive sound, a long drawn respiration, which was neither a sigh nor a moan, but was like both, gave it as her opinion that he should have done this at first.\n\n‘I must try,’ said Mr. Spenlow, confirmed by this support, ‘my influence with my daughter. Do you decline to take those letters, Mr. Copperfield?’ For I had laid them on the table.\n\nYes. I told him I hoped he would not think it wrong, but I couldn’t possibly take them from Miss Murdstone.\n\n‘Nor from me?’ said Mr. Spenlow.\n\nNo, I replied with the profoundest respect; nor from him.\n\n‘Very well!’ said Mr. Spenlow.\n\nA silence succeeding, I was undecided whether to go or stay. At length I was moving quietly towards the door, with the intention of saying that perhaps I should consult his feelings best by withdrawing: when he said, with his hands in his coat pockets, into which it was as much as he could do to get them; and with what I should call, upon the whole, a decidedly pious air:\n\n‘You are probably aware, Mr. Copperfield, that I am not altogether destitute of worldly possessions, and that my daughter is my nearest and dearest relative?’\n\nI hurriedly made him a reply to the effect, that I hoped the error into which I had been betrayed by the desperate nature of my love, did not induce him to think me mercenary too?\n\n‘I don’t allude to the matter in that light,’ said Mr. Spenlow. ‘It would be better for yourself, and all of us, if you WERE mercenary, Mr. Copperfield--I mean, if you were more discreet and less influenced by all this youthful nonsense. No. I merely say, with quite another view, you are probably aware I have some property to bequeath to my child?’\n\nI certainly supposed so.\n\n‘And you can hardly think,’ said Mr. Spenlow, ‘having experience of what we see, in the Commons here, every day, of the various unaccountable and negligent proceedings of men, in respect of their testamentary arrangements--of all subjects, the one on which perhaps the strangest revelations of human inconsistency are to be met with--but that mine are made?’\n\nI inclined my head in acquiescence.\n\n‘I should not allow,’ said Mr. Spenlow, with an evident increase of pious sentiment, and slowly shaking his head as he poised himself upon his toes and heels alternately, ‘my suitable provision for my child to be influenced by a piece of youthful folly like the present. It is mere folly. Mere nonsense. In a little while, it will weigh lighter than any feather. But I might--I might--if this silly business were not completely relinquished altogether, be induced in some anxious moment to guard her from, and surround her with protections against, the consequences of any foolish step in the way of marriage. Now, Mr. Copperfield, I hope that you will not render it necessary for me to open, even for a quarter of an hour, that closed page in the book of life, and unsettle, even for a quarter of an hour, grave affairs long since composed.’\n\nThere was a serenity, a tranquillity, a calm sunset air about him, which quite affected me. He was so peaceful and resigned--clearly had his affairs in such perfect train, and so systematically wound up--that he was a man to feel touched in the contemplation of. I really think I saw tears rise to his eyes, from the depth of his own feeling of all this.\n\nBut what could I do? I could not deny Dora and my own heart. When he told me I had better take a week to consider of what he had said, how could I say I wouldn’t take a week, yet how could I fail to know that no amount of weeks could influence such love as mine?\n\n‘In the meantime, confer with Miss Trotwood, or with any person with any knowledge of life,’ said Mr. Spenlow, adjusting his cravat with both hands. ‘Take a week, Mr. Copperfield.’\n\nI submitted; and, with a countenance as expressive as I was able to make it of dejected and despairing constancy, came out of the room. Miss Murdstone’s heavy eyebrows followed me to the door--I say her eyebrows rather than her eyes, because they were much more important in her face--and she looked so exactly as she used to look, at about that hour of the morning, in our parlour at Blunderstone, that I could have fancied I had been breaking down in my lessons again, and that the dead weight on my mind was that horrible old spelling-book, with oval woodcuts, shaped, to my youthful fancy, like the glasses out of spectacles.\n\nWhen I got to the office, and, shutting out old Tiffey and the rest of them with my hands, sat at my desk, in my own particular nook, thinking of this earthquake that had taken place so unexpectedly, and in the bitterness of my spirit cursing Jip, I fell into such a state of torment about Dora, that I wonder I did not take up my hat and rush insanely to Norwood. The idea of their frightening her, and making her cry, and of my not being there to comfort her, was so excruciating, that it impelled me to write a wild letter to Mr. Spenlow, beseeching him not to visit upon her the consequences of my awful destiny. I implored him to spare her gentle nature--not to crush a fragile flower--and addressed him generally, to the best of my remembrance, as if, instead of being her father, he had been an Ogre, or the Dragon of Wantley. This letter I sealed and laid upon his desk before he returned; and when he came in, I saw him, through the half-opened door of his room, take it up and read it.\n\nHe said nothing about it all the morning; but before he went away in the afternoon he called me in, and told me that I need not make myself at all uneasy about his daughter’s happiness. He had assured her, he said, that it was all nonsense; and he had nothing more to say to her. He believed he was an indulgent father (as indeed he was), and I might spare myself any solicitude on her account.\n\n‘You may make it necessary, if you are foolish or obstinate, Mr. Copperfield,’ he observed, ‘for me to send my daughter abroad again, for a term; but I have a better opinion of you. I hope you will be wiser than that, in a few days. As to Miss Murdstone,’ for I had alluded to her in the letter, ‘I respect that lady’s vigilance, and feel obliged to her; but she has strict charge to avoid the subject. All I desire, Mr. Copperfield, is, that it should be forgotten. All you have got to do, Mr. Copperfield, is to forget it.’\n\nAll! In the note I wrote to Miss Mills, I bitterly quoted this sentiment. All I had to do, I said, with gloomy sarcasm, was to forget Dora. That was all, and what was that! I entreated Miss Mills to see me, that evening. If it could not be done with Mr. Mills’s sanction and concurrence, I besought a clandestine interview in the back kitchen where the Mangle was. I informed her that my reason was tottering on its throne, and only she, Miss Mills, could prevent its being deposed. I signed myself, hers distractedly; and I couldn’t help feeling, while I read this composition over, before sending it by a porter, that it was something in the style of Mr. Micawber.\n\nHowever, I sent it. At night I repaired to Miss Mills’s street, and walked up and down, until I was stealthily fetched in by Miss Mills’s maid, and taken the area way to the back kitchen. I have since seen reason to believe that there was nothing on earth to prevent my going in at the front door, and being shown up into the drawing-room, except Miss Mills’s love of the romantic and mysterious.\n\nIn the back kitchen, I raved as became me. I went there, I suppose, to make a fool of myself, and I am quite sure I did it. Miss Mills had received a hasty note from Dora, telling her that all was discovered, and saying. ‘Oh pray come to me, Julia, do, do!’ But Miss Mills, mistrusting the acceptability of her presence to the higher powers, had not yet gone; and we were all benighted in the Desert of Sahara.\n\nMiss Mills had a wonderful flow of words, and liked to pour them out. I could not help feeling, though she mingled her tears with mine, that she had a dreadful luxury in our afflictions. She petted them, as I may say, and made the most of them. A deep gulf, she observed, had opened between Dora and me, and Love could only span it with its rainbow. Love must suffer in this stern world; it ever had been so, it ever would be so. No matter, Miss Mills remarked. Hearts confined by cobwebs would burst at last, and then Love was avenged.\n\nThis was small consolation, but Miss Mills wouldn’t encourage fallacious hopes. She made me much more wretched than I was before, and I felt (and told her with the deepest gratitude) that she was indeed a friend. We resolved that she should go to Dora the first thing in the morning, and find some means of assuring her, either by looks or words, of my devotion and misery. We parted, overwhelmed with grief; and I think Miss Mills enjoyed herself completely.\n\nI confided all to my aunt when I got home; and in spite of all she could say to me, went to bed despairing. I got up despairing, and went out despairing. It was Saturday morning, and I went straight to the Commons.\n\nI was surprised, when I came within sight of our office-door, to see the ticket-porters standing outside talking together, and some half-dozen stragglers gazing at the windows which were shut up. I quickened my pace, and, passing among them, wondering at their looks, went hurriedly in.\n\nThe clerks were there, but nobody was doing anything. Old Tiffey, for the first time in his life I should think, was sitting on somebody else’s stool, and had not hung up his hat.\n\n‘This is a dreadful calamity, Mr. Copperfield,’ said he, as I entered.\n\n‘What is?’ I exclaimed. ‘What’s the matter?’\n\n‘Don’t you know?’ cried Tiffey, and all the rest of them, coming round me.\n\n‘No!’ said I, looking from face to face.\n\n‘Mr. Spenlow,’ said Tiffey.\n\n‘What about him!’\n\n‘Dead!’ I thought it was the office reeling, and not I, as one of the clerks caught hold of me. They sat me down in a chair, untied my neck-cloth, and brought me some water. I have no idea whether this took any time.\n‘Dead?’ said I.\n\n‘He dined in town yesterday, and drove down in the phaeton by himself,’ said Tiffey, ‘having sent his own groom home by the coach, as he sometimes did, you know--’\n\n‘Well?’\n\n‘The phaeton went home without him. The horses stopped at the stable-gate. The man went out with a lantern. Nobody in the carriage.’\n\n‘Had they run away?’\n\n‘They were not hot,’ said Tiffey, putting on his glasses; ‘no hotter, I understand, than they would have been, going down at the usual pace. The reins were broken, but they had been dragging on the ground. The house was roused up directly, and three of them went out along the road. They found him a mile off.’\n\n‘More than a mile off, Mr. Tiffey,’ interposed a junior.\n\n‘Was it? I believe you are right,’ said Tiffey,--‘more than a mile off--not far from the church--lying partly on the roadside, and partly on the path, upon his face. Whether he fell out in a fit, or got out, feeling ill before the fit came on--or even whether he was quite dead then, though there is no doubt he was quite insensible--no one appears to know. If he breathed, certainly he never spoke. Medical assistance was got as soon as possible, but it was quite useless.’\n\nI cannot describe the state of mind into which I was thrown by this intelligence. The shock of such an event happening so suddenly, and happening to one with whom I had been in any respect at variance--the appalling vacancy in the room he had occupied so lately, where his chair and table seemed to wait for him, and his handwriting of yesterday was like a ghost--the indefinable impossibility of separating him from the place, and feeling, when the door opened, as if he might come in--the lazy hush and rest there was in the office, and the insatiable relish with which our people talked about it, and other people came in and out all day, and gorged themselves with the subject--this is easily intelligible to anyone. What I cannot describe is, how, in the innermost recesses of my own heart, I had a lurking jealousy even of Death. How I felt as if its might would push me from my ground in Dora’s thoughts. How I was, in a grudging way I have no words for, envious of her grief. How it made me restless to think of her weeping to others, or being consoled by others. How I had a grasping, avaricious wish to shut out everybody from her but myself, and to be all in all to her, at that unseasonable time of all times.\n\nIn the trouble of this state of mind--not exclusively my own, I hope, but known to others--I went down to Norwood that night; and finding from one of the servants, when I made my inquiries at the door, that Miss Mills was there, got my aunt to direct a letter to her, which I wrote. I deplored the untimely death of Mr. Spenlow, most sincerely, and shed tears in doing so. I entreated her to tell Dora, if Dora were in a state to hear it, that he had spoken to me with the utmost kindness and consideration; and had coupled nothing but tenderness, not a single or reproachful word, with her name. I know I did this selfishly, to have my name brought before her; but I tried to believe it was an act of justice to his memory. Perhaps I did believe it.\n\nMy aunt received a few lines next day in reply; addressed, outside, to her; within, to me. Dora was overcome by grief; and when her friend had asked her should she send her love to me, had only cried, as she was always crying, ‘Oh, dear papa! oh, poor papa!’ But she had not said No, and that I made the most of.\n\nMr. Jorkins, who had been at Norwood since the occurrence, came to the office a few days afterwards. He and Tiffey were closeted together for some few moments, and then Tiffey looked out at the door and beckoned me in.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Mr. Jorkins. ‘Mr. Tiffey and myself, Mr. Copperfield, are about to examine the desks, the drawers, and other such repositories of the deceased, with the view of sealing up his private papers, and searching for a Will. There is no trace of any, elsewhere. It may be as well for you to assist us, if you please.’\n\nI had been in agony to obtain some knowledge of the circumstances in which my Dora would be placed--as, in whose guardianship, and so forth--and this was something towards it. We began the search at once; Mr. Jorkins unlocking the drawers and desks, and we all taking out the papers. The office-papers we placed on one side, and the private papers (which were not numerous) on the other. We were very grave; and when we came to a stray seal, or pencil-case, or ring, or any little article of that kind which we associated personally with him, we spoke very low.\n\nWe had sealed up several packets; and were still going on dustily and quietly, when Mr. Jorkins said to us, applying exactly the same words to his late partner as his late partner had applied to him:\n\n‘Mr. Spenlow was very difficult to move from the beaten track. You know what he was! I am disposed to think he had made no will.’\n\n‘Oh, I know he had!’ said I.\n\nThey both stopped and looked at me. ‘On the very day when I last saw him,’ said I, ‘he told me that he had, and that his affairs were long since settled.’\n\nMr. Jorkins and old Tiffey shook their heads with one accord.\n\n‘That looks unpromising,’ said Tiffey.\n\n‘Very unpromising,’ said Mr. Jorkins.\n\n‘Surely you don’t doubt--’ I began.\n\n‘My good Mr. Copperfield!’ said Tiffey, laying his hand upon my arm, and shutting up both his eyes as he shook his head: ‘if you had been in the Commons as long as I have, you would know that there is no subject on which men are so inconsistent, and so little to be trusted.’\n\n‘Why, bless my soul, he made that very remark!’ I replied persistently.\n\n‘I should call that almost final,’ observed Tiffey. ‘My opinion is--no will.’\n\nIt appeared a wonderful thing to me, but it turned out that there was no will. He had never so much as thought of making one, so far as his papers afforded any evidence; for there was no kind of hint, sketch, or memorandum, of any testamentary intention whatever. What was scarcely less astonishing to me, was, that his affairs were in a most disordered state. It was extremely difficult, I heard, to make out what he owed, or what he had paid, or of what he died possessed. It was considered likely that for years he could have had no clear opinion on these subjects himself. By little and little it came out, that, in the competition on all points of appearance and gentility then running high in the Commons, he had spent more than his professional income, which was not a very large one, and had reduced his private means, if they ever had been great (which was exceedingly doubtful), to a very low ebb indeed. There was a sale of the furniture and lease, at Norwood; and Tiffey told me, little thinking how interested I was in the story, that, paying all the just debts of the deceased, and deducting his share of outstanding bad and doubtful debts due to the firm, he wouldn’t give a thousand pounds for all the assets remaining.\n\nThis was at the expiration of about six weeks. I had suffered tortures all the time; and thought I really must have laid violent hands upon myself, when Miss Mills still reported to me, that my broken-hearted little Dora would say nothing, when I was mentioned, but ‘Oh, poor papa! Oh, dear papa!’ Also, that she had no other relations than two aunts, maiden sisters of Mr. Spenlow, who lived at Putney, and who had not held any other than chance communication with their brother for many years. Not that they had ever quarrelled (Miss Mills informed me); but that having been, on the occasion of Dora’s christening, invited to tea, when they considered themselves privileged to be invited to dinner, they had expressed their opinion in writing, that it was ‘better for the happiness of all parties’ that they should stay away. Since which they had gone their road, and their brother had gone his.\n\nThese two ladies now emerged from their retirement, and proposed to take Dora to live at Putney. Dora, clinging to them both, and weeping, exclaimed, ‘O yes, aunts! Please take Julia Mills and me and Jip to Putney!’ So they went, very soon after the funeral.\n\nHow I found time to haunt Putney, I am sure I don’t know; but I contrived, by some means or other, to prowl about the neighbourhood pretty often. Miss Mills, for the more exact discharge of the duties of friendship, kept a journal; and she used to meet me sometimes, on the Common, and read it, or (if she had not time to do that) lend it to me. How I treasured up the entries, of which I subjoin a sample--!\n\n‘Monday. My sweet D. still much depressed. Headache. Called attention to J. as being beautifully sleek. D. fondled J. Associations thus awakened, opened floodgates of sorrow. Rush of grief admitted. (Are tears the dewdrops of the heart? J. M.)\n\n‘Tuesday. D. weak and nervous. Beautiful in pallor. (Do we not remark this in moon likewise? J. M.) D., J. M. and J. took airing in carriage. J. looking out of window, and barking violently at dustman, occasioned smile to overspread features of D. (Of such slight links is chain of life composed! J. M.)\n\n‘Wednesday. D. comparatively cheerful. Sang to her, as congenial melody, “Evening Bells”. Effect not soothing, but reverse. D. inexpressibly affected. Found sobbing afterwards, in own room. Quoted verses respecting self and young Gazelle. Ineffectually. Also referred to Patience on Monument. (Qy. Why on monument? J. M.)\n\n‘Thursday. D. certainly improved. Better night. Slight tinge of damask revisiting cheek. Resolved to mention name of D. C. Introduced same, cautiously, in course of airing. D. immediately overcome. “Oh, dear, dear Julia! Oh, I have been a naughty and undutiful child!” Soothed and caressed. Drew ideal picture of D. C. on verge of tomb. D. again overcome. “Oh, what shall I do, what shall I do? Oh, take me somewhere!” Much alarmed. Fainting of D. and glass of water from public-house. (Poetical affinity. Chequered sign on door-post; chequered human life. Alas! J. M.)\n\n‘Friday. Day of incident. Man appears in kitchen, with blue bag, “for lady’s boots left out to heel”. Cook replies, “No such orders.” Man argues point. Cook withdraws to inquire, leaving man alone with J. On Cook’s return, man still argues point, but ultimately goes. J. missing. D. distracted. Information sent to police. Man to be identified by broad nose, and legs like balustrades of bridge. Search made in every direction. No J. D. weeping bitterly, and inconsolable. Renewed reference to young Gazelle. Appropriate, but unavailing. Towards evening, strange boy calls. Brought into parlour. Broad nose, but no balustrades. Says he wants a pound, and knows a dog. Declines to explain further, though much pressed. Pound being produced by D. takes Cook to little house, where J. alone tied up to leg of table. Joy of D. who dances round J. while he eats his supper. Emboldened by this happy change, mention D. C. upstairs. D. weeps afresh, cries piteously, “Oh, don’t, don’t, don’t! It is so wicked to think of anything but poor papa!”--embraces J. and sobs herself to sleep. (Must not D. C. confine himself to the broad pinions of Time? J. M.)’\n\nMiss Mills and her journal were my sole consolation at this period. To see her, who had seen Dora but a little while before--to trace the initial letter of Dora’s name through her sympathetic pages--to be made more and more miserable by her--were my only comforts. I felt as if I had been living in a palace of cards, which had tumbled down, leaving only Miss Mills and me among the ruins; I felt as if some grim enchanter had drawn a magic circle round the innocent goddess of my heart, which nothing indeed but those same strong pinions, capable of carrying so many people over so much, would enable me to enter!\n\n# CHAPTER 39. WICKFIELD AND HEEP\n\nMy aunt, beginning, I imagine, to be made seriously uncomfortable by my prolonged dejection, made a pretence of being anxious that I should go to Dover, to see that all was working well at the cottage, which was let; and to conclude an agreement, with the same tenant, for a longer term of occupation. Janet was drafted into the service of Mrs. Strong, where I saw her every day. She had been undecided, on leaving Dover, whether or no to give the finishing touch to that renunciation of mankind in which she had been educated, by marrying a pilot; but she decided against that venture. Not so much for the sake of principle, I believe, as because she happened not to like him.\n\nAlthough it required an effort to leave Miss Mills, I fell rather willingly into my aunt’s pretence, as a means of enabling me to pass a few tranquil hours with Agnes. I consulted the good Doctor relative to an absence of three days; and the Doctor wishing me to take that relaxation,--he wished me to take more; but my energy could not bear that,--I made up my mind to go.\n\nAs to the Commons, I had no great occasion to be particular about my duties in that quarter. To say the truth, we were getting in no very good odour among the tip-top proctors, and were rapidly sliding down to but a doubtful position. The business had been indifferent under Mr. Jorkins, before Mr. Spenlow’s time; and although it had been quickened by the infusion of new blood, and by the display which Mr. Spenlow made, still it was not established on a sufficiently strong basis to bear, without being shaken, such a blow as the sudden loss of its active manager. It fell off very much. Mr. Jorkins, notwithstanding his reputation in the firm, was an easy-going, incapable sort of man, whose reputation out of doors was not calculated to back it up. I was turned over to him now, and when I saw him take his snuff and let the business go, I regretted my aunt’s thousand pounds more than ever.\n\nBut this was not the worst of it. There were a number of hangers-on and outsiders about the Commons, who, without being proctors themselves, dabbled in common-form business, and got it done by real proctors, who lent their names in consideration of a share in the spoil;--and there were a good many of these too. As our house now wanted business on any terms, we joined this noble band; and threw out lures to the hangers-on and outsiders, to bring their business to us. Marriage licences and small probates were what we all looked for, and what paid us best; and the competition for these ran very high indeed. Kidnappers and inveiglers were planted in all the avenues of entrance to the Commons, with instructions to do their utmost to cut off all persons in mourning, and all gentlemen with anything bashful in their appearance, and entice them to the offices in which their respective employers were interested; which instructions were so well observed, that I myself, before I was known by sight, was twice hustled into the premises of our principal opponent. The conflicting interests of these touting gentlemen being of a nature to irritate their feelings, personal collisions took place; and the Commons was even scandalized by our principal inveigler (who had formerly been in the wine trade, and afterwards in the sworn brokery line) walking about for some days with a black eye. Any one of these scouts used to think nothing of politely assisting an old lady in black out of a vehicle, killing any proctor whom she inquired for, representing his employer as the lawful successor and representative of that proctor, and bearing the old lady off (sometimes greatly affected) to his employer’s office. Many captives were brought to me in this way. As to marriage licences, the competition rose to such a pitch, that a shy gentleman in want of one, had nothing to do but submit himself to the first inveigler, or be fought for, and become the prey of the strongest. One of our clerks, who was an outsider, used, in the height of this contest, to sit with his hat on, that he might be ready to rush out and swear before a surrogate any victim who was brought in. The system of inveigling continues, I believe, to this day. The last time I was in the Commons, a civil able-bodied person in a white apron pounced out upon me from a doorway, and whispering the word ‘Marriage-licence’ in my ear, was with great difficulty prevented from taking me up in his arms and lifting me into a proctor’s. From this digression, let me proceed to Dover.\n\nI found everything in a satisfactory state at the cottage; and was enabled to gratify my aunt exceedingly by reporting that the tenant inherited her feud, and waged incessant war against donkeys. Having settled the little business I had to transact there, and slept there one night, I walked on to Canterbury early in the morning. It was now winter again; and the fresh, cold windy day, and the sweeping downland, brightened up my hopes a little.\n\nComing into Canterbury, I loitered through the old streets with a sober pleasure that calmed my spirits, and eased my heart. There were the old signs, the old names over the shops, the old people serving in them. It appeared so long, since I had been a schoolboy there, that I wondered the place was so little changed, until I reflected how little I was changed myself. Strange to say, that quiet influence which was inseparable in my mind from Agnes, seemed to pervade even the city where she dwelt. The venerable cathedral towers, and the old jackdaws and rooks whose airy voices made them more retired than perfect silence would have done; the battered gateways, one stuck full with statues, long thrown down, and crumbled away, like the reverential pilgrims who had gazed upon them; the still nooks, where the ivied growth of centuries crept over gabled ends and ruined walls; the ancient houses, the pastoral landscape of field, orchard, and garden; everywhere--on everything--I felt the same serener air, the same calm, thoughtful, softening spirit.\n\nArrived at Mr. Wickfield’s house, I found, in the little lower room on the ground floor, where Uriah Heep had been of old accustomed to sit, Mr. Micawber plying his pen with great assiduity. He was dressed in a legal-looking suit of black, and loomed, burly and large, in that small office.\n\nMr. Micawber was extremely glad to see me, but a little confused too. He would have conducted me immediately into the presence of Uriah, but I declined.\n\n‘I know the house of old, you recollect,’ said I, ‘and will find my way upstairs. How do you like the law, Mr. Micawber?’\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ he replied. ‘To a man possessed of the higher imaginative powers, the objection to legal studies is the amount of detail which they involve. Even in our professional correspondence,’ said Mr. Micawber, glancing at some letters he was writing, ‘the mind is not at liberty to soar to any exalted form of expression. Still, it is a great pursuit. A great pursuit!’\n\nHe then told me that he had become the tenant of Uriah Heep’s old house; and that Mrs. Micawber would be delighted to receive me, once more, under her own roof.\n\n‘It is humble,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘--to quote a favourite expression of my friend Heep; but it may prove the stepping-stone to more ambitious domiciliary accommodation.’\n\nI asked him whether he had reason, so far, to be satisfied with his friend Heep’s treatment of him? He got up to ascertain if the door were close shut, before he replied, in a lower voice:\n\n‘My dear Copperfield, a man who labours under the pressure of pecuniary embarrassments, is, with the generality of people, at a disadvantage. That disadvantage is not diminished, when that pressure necessitates the drawing of stipendiary emoluments, before those emoluments are strictly due and payable. All I can say is, that my friend Heep has responded to appeals to which I need not more particularly refer, in a manner calculated to redound equally to the honour of his head, and of his heart.’\n\n‘I should not have supposed him to be very free with his money either,’ I observed.\n\n‘Pardon me!’ said Mr. Micawber, with an air of constraint, ‘I speak of my friend Heep as I have experience.’\n\n‘I am glad your experience is so favourable,’ I returned.\n\n‘You are very obliging, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber; and hummed a tune.\n\n‘Do you see much of Mr. Wickfield?’ I asked, to change the subject.\n\n‘Not much,’ said Mr. Micawber, slightingly. ‘Mr. Wickfield is, I dare say, a man of very excellent intentions; but he is--in short, he is obsolete.’\n\n‘I am afraid his partner seeks to make him so,’ said I.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield!’ returned Mr. Micawber, after some uneasy evolutions on his stool, ‘allow me to offer a remark! I am here, in a capacity of confidence. I am here, in a position of trust. The discussion of some topics, even with Mrs. Micawber herself (so long the partner of my various vicissitudes, and a woman of a remarkable lucidity of intellect), is, I am led to consider, incompatible with the functions now devolving on me. I would therefore take the liberty of suggesting that in our friendly intercourse--which I trust will never be disturbed!--we draw a line. On one side of this line,’ said Mr. Micawber, representing it on the desk with the office ruler, ‘is the whole range of the human intellect, with a trifling exception; on the other, IS that exception; that is to say, the affairs of Messrs Wickfield and Heep, with all belonging and appertaining thereunto. I trust I give no offence to the companion of my youth, in submitting this proposition to his cooler judgement?’\n\nThough I saw an uneasy change in Mr. Micawber, which sat tightly on him, as if his new duties were a misfit, I felt I had no right to be offended. My telling him so, appeared to relieve him; and he shook hands with me.\n\n‘I am charmed, Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘let me assure you, with Miss Wickfield. She is a very superior young lady, of very remarkable attractions, graces, and virtues. Upon my honour,’ said Mr. Micawber, indefinitely kissing his hand and bowing with his genteelest air, ‘I do Homage to Miss Wickfield! Hem!’ ‘I am glad of that, at least,’ said I.\n\n‘If you had not assured us, my dear Copperfield, on the occasion of that agreeable afternoon we had the happiness of passing with you, that D. was your favourite letter,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘I should unquestionably have supposed that A. had been so.’\n\nWe have all some experience of a feeling, that comes over us occasionally, of what we are saying and doing having been said and done before, in a remote time--of our having been surrounded, dim ages ago, by the same faces, objects, and circumstances--of our knowing perfectly what will be said next, as if we suddenly remembered it! I never had this mysterious impression more strongly in my life, than before he uttered those words.\n\nI took my leave of Mr. Micawber, for the time, charging him with my best remembrances to all at home. As I left him, resuming his stool and his pen, and rolling his head in his stock, to get it into easier writing order, I clearly perceived that there was something interposed between him and me, since he had come into his new functions, which prevented our getting at each other as we used to do, and quite altered the character of our intercourse.\n\nThere was no one in the quaint old drawing-room, though it presented tokens of Mrs. Heep’s whereabouts. I looked into the room still belonging to Agnes, and saw her sitting by the fire, at a pretty old-fashioned desk she had, writing.\n\nMy darkening the light made her look up. What a pleasure to be the cause of that bright change in her attentive face, and the object of that sweet regard and welcome!\n\n‘Ah, Agnes!’ said I, when we were sitting together, side by side; ‘I have missed you so much, lately!’\n\n‘Indeed?’ she replied. ‘Again! And so soon?’\n\nI shook my head.\n\n‘I don’t know how it is, Agnes; I seem to want some faculty of mind that I ought to have. You were so much in the habit of thinking for me, in the happy old days here, and I came so naturally to you for counsel and support, that I really think I have missed acquiring it.’\n\n‘And what is it?’ said Agnes, cheerfully.\n\n‘I don’t know what to call it,’ I replied. ‘I think I am earnest and persevering?’\n\n‘I am sure of it,’ said Agnes.\n\n‘And patient, Agnes?’ I inquired, with a little hesitation.\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Agnes, laughing. ‘Pretty well.’\n\n‘And yet,’ said I, ‘I get so miserable and worried, and am so unsteady and irresolute in my power of assuring myself, that I know I must want--shall I call it--reliance, of some kind?’\n\n‘Call it so, if you will,’ said Agnes.\n\n‘Well!’ I returned. ‘See here! You come to London, I rely on you, and I have an object and a course at once. I am driven out of it, I come here, and in a moment I feel an altered person. The circumstances that distressed me are not changed, since I came into this room; but an influence comes over me in that short interval that alters me, oh, how much for the better! What is it? What is your secret, Agnes?’\n\nHer head was bent down, looking at the fire.\n\n‘It’s the old story,’ said I. ‘Don’t laugh, when I say it was always the same in little things as it is in greater ones. My old troubles were nonsense, and now they are serious; but whenever I have gone away from my adopted sister--’\n\nAgnes looked up--with such a Heavenly face!--and gave me her hand, which I kissed.\n\n‘Whenever I have not had you, Agnes, to advise and approve in the beginning, I have seemed to go wild, and to get into all sorts of difficulty. When I have come to you, at last (as I have always done), I have come to peace and happiness. I come home, now, like a tired traveller, and find such a blessed sense of rest!’\n\nI felt so deeply what I said, it affected me so sincerely, that my voice failed, and I covered my face with my hand, and broke into tears. I write the truth. Whatever contradictions and inconsistencies there were within me, as there are within so many of us; whatever might have been so different, and so much better; whatever I had done, in which I had perversely wandered away from the voice of my own heart; I knew nothing of. I only knew that I was fervently in earnest, when I felt the rest and peace of having Agnes near me.\n\nIn her placid sisterly manner; with her beaming eyes; with her tender voice; and with that sweet composure, which had long ago made the house that held her quite a sacred place to me; she soon won me from this weakness, and led me on to tell all that had happened since our last meeting.\n\n‘And there is not another word to tell, Agnes,’ said I, when I had made an end of my confidence. ‘Now, my reliance is on you.’\n\n‘But it must not be on me, Trotwood,’ returned Agnes, with a pleasant smile. ‘It must be on someone else.’\n\n‘On Dora?’ said I.\n\n‘Assuredly.’\n\n‘Why, I have not mentioned, Agnes,’ said I, a little embarrassed, ‘that Dora is rather difficult to--I would not, for the world, say, to rely upon, because she is the soul of purity and truth--but rather difficult to--I hardly know how to express it, really, Agnes. She is a timid little thing, and easily disturbed and frightened. Some time ago, before her father’s death, when I thought it right to mention to her--but I’ll tell you, if you will bear with me, how it was.’\n\nAccordingly, I told Agnes about my declaration of poverty, about the cookery-book, the housekeeping accounts, and all the rest of it.\n\n‘Oh, Trotwood!’ she remonstrated, with a smile. ‘Just your old headlong way! You might have been in earnest in striving to get on in the world, without being so very sudden with a timid, loving, inexperienced girl. Poor Dora!’\n\nI never heard such sweet forbearing kindness expressed in a voice, as she expressed in making this reply. It was as if I had seen her admiringly and tenderly embracing Dora, and tacitly reproving me, by her considerate protection, for my hot haste in fluttering that little heart. It was as if I had seen Dora, in all her fascinating artlessness, caressing Agnes, and thanking her, and coaxingly appealing against me, and loving me with all her childish innocence.\n\nI felt so grateful to Agnes, and admired her so! I saw those two together, in a bright perspective, such well-associated friends, each adorning the other so much!\n\n‘What ought I to do then, Agnes?’ I inquired, after looking at the fire a little while. ‘What would it be right to do?’\n\n‘I think,’ said Agnes, ‘that the honourable course to take, would be to write to those two ladies. Don’t you think that any secret course is an unworthy one?’\n\n‘Yes. If YOU think so,’ said I.\n\n‘I am poorly qualified to judge of such matters,’ replied Agnes, with a modest hesitation, ‘but I certainly feel--in short, I feel that your being secret and clandestine, is not being like yourself.’\n\n‘Like myself, in the too high opinion you have of me, Agnes, I am afraid,’ said I.\n\n‘Like yourself, in the candour of your nature,’ she returned; ‘and therefore I would write to those two ladies. I would relate, as plainly and as openly as possible, all that has taken place; and I would ask their permission to visit sometimes, at their house. Considering that you are young, and striving for a place in life, I think it would be well to say that you would readily abide by any conditions they might impose upon you. I would entreat them not to dismiss your request, without a reference to Dora; and to discuss it with her when they should think the time suitable. I would not be too vehement,’ said Agnes, gently, ‘or propose too much. I would trust to my fidelity and perseverance--and to Dora.’\n\n‘But if they were to frighten Dora again, Agnes, by speaking to her,’ said I. ‘And if Dora were to cry, and say nothing about me!’\n\n‘Is that likely?’ inquired Agnes, with the same sweet consideration in her face.\n\n‘God bless her, she is as easily scared as a bird,’ said I. ‘It might be! Or if the two Miss Spenlows (elderly ladies of that sort are odd characters sometimes) should not be likely persons to address in that way!’\n\n‘I don’t think, Trotwood,’ returned Agnes, raising her soft eyes to mine, ‘I would consider that. Perhaps it would be better only to consider whether it is right to do this; and, if it is, to do it.’\n\nI had no longer any doubt on the subject. With a lightened heart, though with a profound sense of the weighty importance of my task, I devoted the whole afternoon to the composition of the draft of this letter; for which great purpose, Agnes relinquished her desk to me. But first I went downstairs to see Mr. Wickfield and Uriah Heep.\n\nI found Uriah in possession of a new, plaster-smelling office, built out in the garden; looking extraordinarily mean, in the midst of a quantity of books and papers. He received me in his usual fawning way, and pretended not to have heard of my arrival from Mr. Micawber; a pretence I took the liberty of disbelieving. He accompanied me into Mr. Wickfield’s room, which was the shadow of its former self--having been divested of a variety of conveniences, for the accommodation of the new partner--and stood before the fire, warming his back, and shaving his chin with his bony hand, while Mr. Wickfield and I exchanged greetings.\n\n‘You stay with us, Trotwood, while you remain in Canterbury?’ said Mr. Wickfield, not without a glance at Uriah for his approval.\n\n‘Is there room for me?’ said I.\n\n‘I am sure, Master Copperfield--I should say Mister, but the other comes so natural,’ said Uriah,--‘I would turn out of your old room with pleasure, if it would be agreeable.’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘Why should you be inconvenienced? There’s another room. There’s another room.’ ‘Oh, but you know,’ returned Uriah, with a grin, ‘I should really be delighted!’\n\nTo cut the matter short, I said I would have the other room or none at all; so it was settled that I should have the other room; and, taking my leave of the firm until dinner, I went upstairs again.\n\nI had hoped to have no other companion than Agnes. But Mrs. Heep had asked permission to bring herself and her knitting near the fire, in that room; on pretence of its having an aspect more favourable for her rheumatics, as the wind then was, than the drawing-room or dining-parlour. Though I could almost have consigned her to the mercies of the wind on the topmost pinnacle of the Cathedral, without remorse, I made a virtue of necessity, and gave her a friendly salutation.\n\n‘I’m umbly thankful to you, sir,’ said Mrs. Heep, in acknowledgement of my inquiries concerning her health, ‘but I’m only pretty well. I haven’t much to boast of. If I could see my Uriah well settled in life, I couldn’t expect much more I think. How do you think my Ury looking, sir?’\n\nI thought him looking as villainous as ever, and I replied that I saw no change in him.\n\n‘Oh, don’t you think he’s changed?’ said Mrs. Heep. ‘There I must umbly beg leave to differ from you. Don’t you see a thinness in him?’\n\n‘Not more than usual,’ I replied.\n\n‘Don’t you though!’ said Mrs. Heep. ‘But you don’t take notice of him with a mother’s eye!’\n\nHis mother’s eye was an evil eye to the rest of the world, I thought as it met mine, howsoever affectionate to him; and I believe she and her son were devoted to one another. It passed me, and went on to Agnes.\n\n‘Don’t YOU see a wasting and a wearing in him, Miss Wickfield?’ inquired Mrs. Heep.\n\n‘No,’ said Agnes, quietly pursuing the work on which she was engaged. ‘You are too solicitous about him. He is very well.’\n\nMrs. Heep, with a prodigious sniff, resumed her knitting.\n\nShe never left off, or left us for a moment. I had arrived early in the day, and we had still three or four hours before dinner; but she sat there, plying her knitting-needles as monotonously as an hour-glass might have poured out its sands. She sat on one side of the fire; I sat at the desk in front of it; a little beyond me, on the other side, sat Agnes. Whensoever, slowly pondering over my letter, I lifted up my eyes, and meeting the thoughtful face of Agnes, saw it clear, and beam encouragement upon me, with its own angelic expression, I was conscious presently of the evil eye passing me, and going on to her, and coming back to me again, and dropping furtively upon the knitting. What the knitting was, I don’t know, not being learned in that art; but it looked like a net; and as she worked away with those Chinese chopsticks of knitting-needles, she showed in the firelight like an ill-looking enchantress, baulked as yet by the radiant goodness opposite, but getting ready for a cast of her net by and by.\n\nAt dinner she maintained her watch, with the same unwinking eyes. After dinner, her son took his turn; and when Mr. Wickfield, himself, and I were left alone together, leered at me, and writhed until I could hardly bear it. In the drawing-room, there was the mother knitting and watching again. All the time that Agnes sang and played, the mother sat at the piano. Once she asked for a particular ballad, which she said her Ury (who was yawning in a great chair) doted on; and at intervals she looked round at him, and reported to Agnes that he was in raptures with the music. But she hardly ever spoke--I question if she ever did--without making some mention of him. It was evident to me that this was the duty assigned to her.\n\nThis lasted until bedtime. To have seen the mother and son, like two great bats hanging over the whole house, and darkening it with their ugly forms, made me so uncomfortable, that I would rather have remained downstairs, knitting and all, than gone to bed. I hardly got any sleep. Next day the knitting and watching began again, and lasted all day.\n\nI had not an opportunity of speaking to Agnes, for ten minutes. I could barely show her my letter. I proposed to her to walk out with me; but Mrs. Heep repeatedly complaining that she was worse, Agnes charitably remained within, to bear her company. Towards the twilight I went out by myself, musing on what I ought to do, and whether I was justified in withholding from Agnes, any longer, what Uriah Heep had told me in London; for that began to trouble me again, very much.\n\nI had not walked out far enough to be quite clear of the town, upon the Ramsgate road, where there was a good path, when I was hailed, through the dust, by somebody behind me. The shambling figure, and the scanty great-coat, were not to be mistaken. I stopped, and Uriah Heep came up.\n\n‘Well?’ said I.\n‘How fast you walk!’ said he. ‘My legs are pretty long, but you’ve given ‘em quite a job.’\n\n‘Where are you going?’ said I.\n\n‘I am going with you, Master Copperfield, if you’ll allow me the pleasure of a walk with an old acquaintance.’ Saying this, with a jerk of his body, which might have been either propitiatory or derisive, he fell into step beside me.\n\n‘Uriah!’ said I, as civilly as I could, after a silence.\n\n‘Master Copperfield!’ said Uriah.\n\n‘To tell you the truth (at which you will not be offended), I came Out to walk alone, because I have had so much company.’\n\nHe looked at me sideways, and said with his hardest grin, ‘You mean mother.’\n\n‘Why yes, I do,’ said I.\n\n‘Ah! But you know we’re so very umble,’ he returned. ‘And having such a knowledge of our own umbleness, we must really take care that we’re not pushed to the wall by them as isn’t umble. All stratagems are fair in love, sir.’\n\nRaising his great hands until they touched his chin, he rubbed them softly, and softly chuckled; looking as like a malevolent baboon, I thought, as anything human could look.\n\n‘You see,’ he said, still hugging himself in that unpleasant way, and shaking his head at me, ‘you’re quite a dangerous rival, Master Copperfield. You always was, you know.’\n\n‘Do you set a watch upon Miss Wickfield, and make her home no home, because of me?’ said I.\n\n‘Oh! Master Copperfield! Those are very arsh words,’ he replied.\n\n‘Put my meaning into any words you like,’ said I. ‘You know what it is, Uriah, as well as I do.’\n\n‘Oh no! You must put it into words,’ he said. ‘Oh, really! I couldn’t myself.’\n\n‘Do you suppose,’ said I, constraining myself to be very temperate and quiet with him, on account of Agnes, ‘that I regard Miss Wickfield otherwise than as a very dear sister?’\n\n‘Well, Master Copperfield,’ he replied, ‘you perceive I am not bound to answer that question. You may not, you know. But then, you see, you may!’\n\nAnything to equal the low cunning of his visage, and of his shadowless eyes without the ghost of an eyelash, I never saw.\n\n‘Come then!’ said I. ‘For the sake of Miss Wickfield--’\n\n‘My Agnes!’ he exclaimed, with a sickly, angular contortion of himself. ‘Would you be so good as call her Agnes, Master Copperfield!’\n\n‘For the sake of Agnes Wickfield--Heaven bless her!’\n\n‘Thank you for that blessing, Master Copperfield!’ he interposed.\n\n‘I will tell you what I should, under any other circumstances, as soon have thought of telling to--Jack Ketch.’\n\n‘To who, sir?’ said Uriah, stretching out his neck, and shading his ear with his hand.\n\n‘To the hangman,’ I returned. ‘The most unlikely person I could think of,’--though his own face had suggested the allusion quite as a natural sequence. ‘I am engaged to another young lady. I hope that contents you.’\n\n‘Upon your soul?’ said Uriah.\n\nI was about indignantly to give my assertion the confirmation he required, when he caught hold of my hand, and gave it a squeeze.\n\n‘Oh, Master Copperfield!’ he said. ‘If you had only had the condescension to return my confidence when I poured out the fulness of my art, the night I put you so much out of the way by sleeping before your sitting-room fire, I never should have doubted you. As it is, I’m sure I’ll take off mother directly, and only too appy. I know you’ll excuse the precautions of affection, won’t you? What a pity, Master Copperfield, that you didn’t condescend to return my confidence! I’m sure I gave you every opportunity. But you never have condescended to me, as much as I could have wished. I know you have never liked me, as I have liked you!’\n\nAll this time he was squeezing my hand with his damp fishy fingers, while I made every effort I decently could to get it away. But I was quite unsuccessful. He drew it under the sleeve of his mulberry-coloured great-coat, and I walked on, almost upon compulsion, arm-in-arm with him.\n\n‘Shall we turn?’ said Uriah, by and by wheeling me face about towards the town, on which the early moon was now shining, silvering the distant windows.\n\n‘Before we leave the subject, you ought to understand,’ said I, breaking a pretty long silence, ‘that I believe Agnes Wickfield to be as far above you, and as far removed from all your aspirations, as that moon herself!’\n\n‘Peaceful! Ain’t she!’ said Uriah. ‘Very! Now confess, Master Copperfield, that you haven’t liked me quite as I have liked you. All along you’ve thought me too umble now, I shouldn’t wonder?’\n\n‘I am not fond of professions of humility,’ I returned, ‘or professions of anything else.’ ‘There now!’ said Uriah, looking flabby and lead-coloured in the moonlight. ‘Didn’t I know it! But how little you think of the rightful umbleness of a person in my station, Master Copperfield! Father and me was both brought up at a foundation school for boys; and mother, she was likewise brought up at a public, sort of charitable, establishment. They taught us all a deal of umbleness--not much else that I know of, from morning to night. We was to be umble to this person, and umble to that; and to pull off our caps here, and to make bows there; and always to know our place, and abase ourselves before our betters. And we had such a lot of betters! Father got the monitor-medal by being umble. So did I. Father got made a sexton by being umble. He had the character, among the gentlefolks, of being such a well-behaved man, that they were determined to bring him in. “Be umble, Uriah,” says father to me, “and you’ll get on. It was what was always being dinned into you and me at school; it’s what goes down best. Be umble,” says father, “and you’ll do!” And really it ain’t done bad!’\n\nIt was the first time it had ever occurred to me, that this detestable cant of false humility might have originated out of the Heep family. I had seen the harvest, but had never thought of the seed.\n\n‘When I was quite a young boy,’ said Uriah, ‘I got to know what umbleness did, and I took to it. I ate umble pie with an appetite. I stopped at the umble point of my learning, and says I, “Hold hard!” When you offered to teach me Latin, I knew better. “People like to be above you,” says father, “keep yourself down.” I am very umble to the present moment, Master Copperfield, but I’ve got a little power!’\n\nAnd he said all this--I knew, as I saw his face in the moonlight--that I might understand he was resolved to recompense himself by using his power. I had never doubted his meanness, his craft and malice; but I fully comprehended now, for the first time, what a base, unrelenting, and revengeful spirit, must have been engendered by this early, and this long, suppression.\n\nHis account of himself was so far attended with an agreeable result, that it led to his withdrawing his hand in order that he might have another hug of himself under the chin. Once apart from him, I was determined to keep apart; and we walked back, side by side, saying very little more by the way. Whether his spirits were elevated by the communication I had made to him, or by his having indulged in this retrospect, I don’t know; but they were raised by some influence. He talked more at dinner than was usual with him; asked his mother (off duty, from the moment of our re-entering the house) whether he was not growing too old for a bachelor; and once looked at Agnes so, that I would have given all I had, for leave to knock him down.\n\nWhen we three males were left alone after dinner, he got into a more adventurous state. He had taken little or no wine; and I presume it was the mere insolence of triumph that was upon him, flushed perhaps by the temptation my presence furnished to its exhibition.\n\nI had observed yesterday, that he tried to entice Mr. Wickfield to drink; and, interpreting the look which Agnes had given me as she went out, had limited myself to one glass, and then proposed that we should follow her. I would have done so again today; but Uriah was too quick for me.\n\n‘We seldom see our present visitor, sir,’ he said, addressing Mr. Wickfield, sitting, such a contrast to him, at the end of the table, ‘and I should propose to give him welcome in another glass or two of wine, if you have no objections. Mr. Copperfield, your elth and appiness!’\n\nI was obliged to make a show of taking the hand he stretched across to me; and then, with very different emotions, I took the hand of the broken gentleman, his partner.\n\n‘Come, fellow-partner,’ said Uriah, ‘if I may take the liberty,--now, suppose you give us something or another appropriate to Copperfield!’\n\nI pass over Mr. Wickfield’s proposing my aunt, his proposing Mr. Dick, his proposing Doctors’ Commons, his proposing Uriah, his drinking everything twice; his consciousness of his own weakness, the ineffectual effort that he made against it; the struggle between his shame in Uriah’s deportment, and his desire to conciliate him; the manifest exultation with which Uriah twisted and turned, and held him up before me. It made me sick at heart to see, and my hand recoils from writing it.\n\n‘Come, fellow-partner!’ said Uriah, at last, ‘I’ll give you another one, and I umbly ask for bumpers, seeing I intend to make it the divinest of her sex.’\n\nHer father had his empty glass in his hand. I saw him set it down, look at the picture she was so like, put his hand to his forehead, and shrink back in his elbow-chair.\n\n‘I’m an umble individual to give you her elth,’ proceeded Uriah, ‘but I admire--adore her.’\n\nNo physical pain that her father’s grey head could have borne, I think, could have been more terrible to me, than the mental endurance I saw compressed now within both his hands.\n\n‘Agnes,’ said Uriah, either not regarding him, or not knowing what the nature of his action was, ‘Agnes Wickfield is, I am safe to say, the divinest of her sex. May I speak out, among friends? To be her father is a proud distinction, but to be her usband--’\n\nSpare me from ever again hearing such a cry, as that with which her father rose up from the table! ‘What’s the matter?’ said Uriah, turning of a deadly colour. ‘You are not gone mad, after all, Mr. Wickfield, I hope? If I say I’ve an ambition to make your Agnes my Agnes, I have as good a right to it as another man. I have a better right to it than any other man!’\n\nI had my arms round Mr. Wickfield, imploring him by everything that I could think of, oftenest of all by his love for Agnes, to calm himself a little. He was mad for the moment; tearing out his hair, beating his head, trying to force me from him, and to force himself from me, not answering a word, not looking at or seeing anyone; blindly striving for he knew not what, his face all staring and distorted--a frightful spectacle.\n\nI conjured him, incoherently, but in the most impassioned manner, not to abandon himself to this wildness, but to hear me. I besought him to think of Agnes, to connect me with Agnes, to recollect how Agnes and I had grown up together, how I honoured her and loved her, how she was his pride and joy. I tried to bring her idea before him in any form; I even reproached him with not having firmness to spare her the knowledge of such a scene as this. I may have effected something, or his wildness may have spent itself; but by degrees he struggled less, and began to look at me--strangely at first, then with recognition in his eyes. At length he said, ‘I know, Trotwood! My darling child and you--I know! But look at him!’\n\nHe pointed to Uriah, pale and glowering in a corner, evidently very much out in his calculations, and taken by surprise.\n\n‘Look at my torturer,’ he replied. ‘Before him I have step by step abandoned name and reputation, peace and quiet, house and home.’\n\n‘I have kept your name and reputation for you, and your peace and quiet, and your house and home too,’ said Uriah, with a sulky, hurried, defeated air of compromise. ‘Don’t be foolish, Mr. Wickfield. If I have gone a little beyond what you were prepared for, I can go back, I suppose? There’s no harm done.’\n\n‘I looked for single motives in everyone,’ said Mr. Wickfield, ‘and I was satisfied I had bound him to me by motives of interest. But see what he is--oh, see what he is!’\n\n‘You had better stop him, Copperfield, if you can,’ cried Uriah, with his long forefinger pointing towards me. ‘He’ll say something presently--mind you!--he’ll be sorry to have said afterwards, and you’ll be sorry to have heard!’\n\n‘I’ll say anything!’ cried Mr. Wickfield, with a desperate air. ‘Why should I not be in all the world’s power if I am in yours?’\n\n‘Mind! I tell you!’ said Uriah, continuing to warn me. ‘If you don’t stop his mouth, you’re not his friend! Why shouldn’t you be in all the world’s power, Mr. Wickfield? Because you have got a daughter. You and me know what we know, don’t we? Let sleeping dogs lie--who wants to rouse ‘em? I don’t. Can’t you see I am as umble as I can be? I tell you, if I’ve gone too far, I’m sorry. What would you have, sir?’\n\n‘Oh, Trotwood, Trotwood!’ exclaimed Mr. Wickfield, wringing his hands. ‘What I have come down to be, since I first saw you in this house! I was on my downward way then, but the dreary, dreary road I have traversed since! Weak indulgence has ruined me. Indulgence in remembrance, and indulgence in forgetfulness. My natural grief for my child’s mother turned to disease; my natural love for my child turned to disease. I have infected everything I touched. I have brought misery on what I dearly love, I know--you know! I thought it possible that I could truly love one creature in the world, and not love the rest; I thought it possible that I could truly mourn for one creature gone out of the world, and not have some part in the grief of all who mourned. Thus the lessons of my life have been perverted! I have preyed on my own morbid coward heart, and it has preyed on me. Sordid in my grief, sordid in my love, sordid in my miserable escape from the darker side of both, oh see the ruin I am, and hate me, shun me!’\n\nHe dropped into a chair, and weakly sobbed. The excitement into which he had been roused was leaving him. Uriah came out of his corner.\n\n‘I don’t know all I have done, in my fatuity,’ said Mr. Wickfield, putting out his hands, as if to deprecate my condemnation. ‘He knows best,’ meaning Uriah Heep, ‘for he has always been at my elbow, whispering me. You see the millstone that he is about my neck. You find him in my house, you find him in my business. You heard him, but a little time ago. What need have I to say more!’\n\n‘You haven’t need to say so much, nor half so much, nor anything at all,’ observed Uriah, half defiant, and half fawning. ‘You wouldn’t have took it up so, if it hadn’t been for the wine. You’ll think better of it tomorrow, sir. If I have said too much, or more than I meant, what of it? I haven’t stood by it!’\n\nThe door opened, and Agnes, gliding in, without a vestige of colour in her face, put her arm round his neck, and steadily said, ‘Papa, you are not well. Come with me!’\n\nHe laid his head upon her shoulder, as if he were oppressed with heavy shame, and went out with her. Her eyes met mine for but an instant, yet I saw how much she knew of what had passed.\n\n‘I didn’t expect he’d cut up so rough, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah. ‘But it’s nothing. I’ll be friends with him tomorrow. It’s for his good. I’m umbly anxious for his good.’\n\nI gave him no answer, and went upstairs into the quiet room where Agnes had so often sat beside me at my books. Nobody came near me until late at night. I took up a book, and tried to read. I heard the clocks strike twelve, and was still reading, without knowing what I read, when Agnes touched me.\n\n‘You will be going early in the morning, Trotwood! Let us say good-bye, now!’\n\nShe had been weeping, but her face then was so calm and beautiful!\n\n‘Heaven bless you!’ she said, giving me her hand.\n\n‘Dearest Agnes!’ I returned, ‘I see you ask me not to speak of tonight--but is there nothing to be done?’\n\n‘There is God to trust in!’ she replied.\n\n‘Can I do nothing--I, who come to you with my poor sorrows?’\n\n‘And make mine so much lighter,’ she replied. ‘Dear Trotwood, no!’\n\n‘Dear Agnes,’ I said, ‘it is presumptuous for me, who am so poor in all in which you are so rich--goodness, resolution, all noble qualities--to doubt or direct you; but you know how much I love you, and how much I owe you. You will never sacrifice yourself to a mistaken sense of duty, Agnes?’\n\nMore agitated for a moment than I had ever seen her, she took her hands from me, and moved a step back.\n\n‘Say you have no such thought, dear Agnes! Much more than sister! Think of the priceless gift of such a heart as yours, of such a love as yours!’\n\nOh! long, long afterwards, I saw that face rise up before me, with its momentary look, not wondering, not accusing, not regretting. Oh, long, long afterwards, I saw that look subside, as it did now, into the lovely smile, with which she told me she had no fear for herself--I need have none for her--and parted from me by the name of Brother, and was gone!\n\nIt was dark in the morning, when I got upon the coach at the inn door. The day was just breaking when we were about to start, and then, as I sat thinking of her, came struggling up the coach side, through the mingled day and night, Uriah’s head.\n\n‘Copperfield!’ said he, in a croaking whisper, as he hung by the iron on the roof, ‘I thought you’d be glad to hear before you went off, that there are no squares broke between us. I’ve been into his room already, and we’ve made it all smooth. Why, though I’m umble, I’m useful to him, you know; and he understands his interest when he isn’t in liquor! What an agreeable man he is, after all, Master Copperfield!’\n\nI obliged myself to say that I was glad he had made his apology.\n\n‘Oh, to be sure!’ said Uriah. ‘When a person’s umble, you know, what’s an apology? So easy! I say! I suppose,’ with a jerk, ‘you have sometimes plucked a pear before it was ripe, Master Copperfield?’\n\n‘I suppose I have,’ I replied.\n\n‘I did that last night,’ said Uriah; ‘but it’ll ripen yet! It only wants attending to. I can wait!’\n\nProfuse in his farewells, he got down again as the coachman got up. For anything I know, he was eating something to keep the raw morning air out; but he made motions with his mouth as if the pear were ripe already, and he were smacking his lips over it.\n\n# CHAPTER 40. THE WANDERER\n\nWe had a very serious conversation in Buckingham Street that night, about the domestic occurrences I have detailed in the last chapter. My aunt was deeply interested in them, and walked up and down the room with her arms folded, for more than two hours afterwards. Whenever she was particularly discomposed, she always performed one of these pedestrian feats; and the amount of her discomposure might always be estimated by the duration of her walk. On this occasion she was so much disturbed in mind as to find it necessary to open the bedroom door, and make a course for herself, comprising the full extent of the bedrooms from wall to wall; and while Mr. Dick and I sat quietly by the fire, she kept passing in and out, along this measured track, at an unchanging pace, with the regularity of a clock-pendulum.\n\nWhen my aunt and I were left to ourselves by Mr. Dick’s going out to bed, I sat down to write my letter to the two old ladies. By that time she was tired of walking, and sat by the fire with her dress tucked up as usual. But instead of sitting in her usual manner, holding her glass upon her knee, she suffered it to stand neglected on the chimney-piece; and, resting her left elbow on her right arm, and her chin on her left hand, looked thoughtfully at me. As often as I raised my eyes from what I was about, I met hers. ‘I am in the lovingest of tempers, my dear,’ she would assure me with a nod, ‘but I am fidgeted and sorry!’\n\nI had been too busy to observe, until after she was gone to bed, that she had left her night-mixture, as she always called it, untasted on the chimney-piece. She came to her door, with even more than her usual affection of manner, when I knocked to acquaint her with this discovery; but only said, ‘I have not the heart to take it, Trot, tonight,’ and shook her head, and went in again.\n\nShe read my letter to the two old ladies, in the morning, and approved of it. I posted it, and had nothing to do then, but wait, as patiently as I could, for the reply. I was still in this state of expectation, and had been, for nearly a week; when I left the Doctor’s one snowy night, to walk home.\n\nIt had been a bitter day, and a cutting north-east wind had blown for some time. The wind had gone down with the light, and so the snow had come on. It was a heavy, settled fall, I recollect, in great flakes; and it lay thick. The noise of wheels and tread of people were as hushed, as if the streets had been strewn that depth with feathers.\n\nMy shortest way home,--and I naturally took the shortest way on such a night--was through St. Martin’s Lane. Now, the church which gives its name to the lane, stood in a less free situation at that time; there being no open space before it, and the lane winding down to the Strand. As I passed the steps of the portico, I encountered, at the corner, a woman’s face. It looked in mine, passed across the narrow lane, and disappeared. I knew it. I had seen it somewhere. But I could not remember where. I had some association with it, that struck upon my heart directly; but I was thinking of anything else when it came upon me, and was confused.\n\nOn the steps of the church, there was the stooping figure of a man, who had put down some burden on the smooth snow, to adjust it; my seeing the face, and my seeing him, were simultaneous. I don’t think I had stopped in my surprise; but, in any case, as I went on, he rose, turned, and came down towards me. I stood face to face with Mr. Peggotty!\n\nThen I remembered the woman. It was Martha, to whom Emily had given the money that night in the kitchen. Martha Endell--side by side with whom, he would not have seen his dear niece, Ham had told me, for all the treasures wrecked in the sea.\n\nWe shook hands heartily. At first, neither of us could speak a word.\n\n‘Mas’r Davy!’ he said, gripping me tight, ‘it do my art good to see you, sir. Well met, well met!’\n\n‘Well met, my dear old friend!’ said I.\n\n‘I had my thowts o’ coming to make inquiration for you, sir, tonight,’ he said, ‘but knowing as your aunt was living along wi’ you--fur I’ve been down yonder--Yarmouth way--I was afeerd it was too late. I should have come early in the morning, sir, afore going away.’\n\n‘Again?’ said I.\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ he replied, patiently shaking his head, ‘I’m away tomorrow.’\n\n‘Where were you going now?’ I asked.\n\n‘Well!’ he replied, shaking the snow out of his long hair, ‘I was a-going to turn in somewheers.’\n\nIn those days there was a side-entrance to the stable-yard of the Golden Cross, the inn so memorable to me in connexion with his misfortune, nearly opposite to where we stood. I pointed out the gateway, put my arm through his, and we went across. Two or three public-rooms opened out of the stable-yard; and looking into one of them, and finding it empty, and a good fire burning, I took him in there.\n\nWhen I saw him in the light, I observed, not only that his hair was long and ragged, but that his face was burnt dark by the sun. He was greyer, the lines in his face and forehead were deeper, and he had every appearance of having toiled and wandered through all varieties of weather; but he looked very strong, and like a man upheld by steadfastness of purpose, whom nothing could tire out. He shook the snow from his hat and clothes, and brushed it away from his face, while I was inwardly making these remarks. As he sat down opposite to me at a table, with his back to the door by which we had entered, he put out his rough hand again, and grasped mine warmly.\n\n‘I’ll tell you, Mas’r Davy,’ he said,--‘wheer all I’ve been, and what-all we’ve heerd. I’ve been fur, and we’ve heerd little; but I’ll tell you!’\n\nI rang the bell for something hot to drink. He would have nothing stronger than ale; and while it was being brought, and being warmed at the fire, he sat thinking. There was a fine, massive gravity in his face, I did not venture to disturb.\n\n‘When she was a child,’ he said, lifting up his head soon after we were left alone, ‘she used to talk to me a deal about the sea, and about them coasts where the sea got to be dark blue, and to lay a-shining and a-shining in the sun. I thowt, odd times, as her father being drownded made her think on it so much. I doen’t know, you see, but maybe she believed--or hoped--he had drifted out to them parts, where the flowers is always a-blowing, and the country bright.’\n\n‘It is likely to have been a childish fancy,’ I replied.\n\n‘When she was--lost,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘I know’d in my mind, as he would take her to them countries. I know’d in my mind, as he’d have told her wonders of ‘em, and how she was to be a lady theer, and how he got her to listen to him fust, along o’ sech like. When we see his mother, I know’d quite well as I was right. I went across-channel to France, and landed theer, as if I’d fell down from the sky.’\n\nI saw the door move, and the snow drift in. I saw it move a little more, and a hand softly interpose to keep it open.\n\n‘I found out an English gen’leman as was in authority,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘and told him I was a-going to seek my niece. He got me them papers as I wanted fur to carry me through--I doen’t rightly know how they’re called--and he would have give me money, but that I was thankful to have no need on. I thank him kind, for all he done, I’m sure! “I’ve wrote afore you,” he says to me, “and I shall speak to many as will come that way, and many will know you, fur distant from here, when you’re a-travelling alone.” I told him, best as I was able, what my gratitoode was, and went away through France.’\n\n‘Alone, and on foot?’ said I.\n\n‘Mostly a-foot,’ he rejoined; ‘sometimes in carts along with people going to market; sometimes in empty coaches. Many mile a day a-foot, and often with some poor soldier or another, travelling to see his friends. I couldn’t talk to him,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘nor he to me; but we was company for one another, too, along the dusty roads.’\n\nI should have known that by his friendly tone.\n\n‘When I come to any town,’ he pursued, ‘I found the inn, and waited about the yard till someone turned up (someone mostly did) as know’d English. Then I told how that I was on my way to seek my niece, and they told me what manner of gentlefolks was in the house, and I waited to see any as seemed like her, going in or out. When it warn’t Em’ly, I went on agen. By little and little, when I come to a new village or that, among the poor people, I found they know’d about me. They would set me down at their cottage doors, and give me what-not fur to eat and drink, and show me where to sleep; and many a woman, Mas’r Davy, as has had a daughter of about Em’ly’s age, I’ve found a-waiting fur me, at Our Saviour’s Cross outside the village, fur to do me sim’lar kindnesses. Some has had daughters as was dead. And God only knows how good them mothers was to me!’\n\nIt was Martha at the door. I saw her haggard, listening face distinctly. My dread was lest he should turn his head, and see her too.\n\n‘They would often put their children--particular their little girls,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘upon my knee; and many a time you might have seen me sitting at their doors, when night was coming in, a’most as if they’d been my Darling’s children. Oh, my Darling!’\n\nOverpowered by sudden grief, he sobbed aloud. I laid my trembling hand upon the hand he put before his face. ‘Thankee, sir,’ he said, ‘doen’t take no notice.’\n\nIn a very little while he took his hand away and put it on his breast, and went on with his story. ‘They often walked with me,’ he said, ‘in the morning, maybe a mile or two upon my road; and when we parted, and I said, “I’m very thankful to you! God bless you!” they always seemed to understand, and answered pleasant. At last I come to the sea. It warn’t hard, you may suppose, for a seafaring man like me to work his way over to Italy. When I got theer, I wandered on as I had done afore. The people was just as good to me, and I should have gone from town to town, maybe the country through, but that I got news of her being seen among them Swiss mountains yonder. One as know’d his servant see ‘em there, all three, and told me how they travelled, and where they was. I made fur them mountains, Mas’r Davy, day and night. Ever so fur as I went, ever so fur the mountains seemed to shift away from me. But I come up with ‘em, and I crossed ‘em. When I got nigh the place as I had been told of, I began to think within my own self, “What shall I do when I see her?”’\n\nThe listening face, insensible to the inclement night, still drooped at the door, and the hands begged me--prayed me--not to cast it forth.\n\n‘I never doubted her,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘No! Not a bit! On’y let her see my face--on’y let her heer my voice--on’y let my stanning still afore her bring to her thoughts the home she had fled away from, and the child she had been--and if she had growed to be a royal lady, she’d have fell down at my feet! I know’d it well! Many a time in my sleep had I heerd her cry out, “Uncle!” and seen her fall like death afore me. Many a time in my sleep had I raised her up, and whispered to her, “Em’ly, my dear, I am come fur to bring forgiveness, and to take you home!”’\n\nHe stopped and shook his head, and went on with a sigh.\n\n‘He was nowt to me now. Em’ly was all. I bought a country dress to put upon her; and I know’d that, once found, she would walk beside me over them stony roads, go where I would, and never, never, leave me more. To put that dress upon her, and to cast off what she wore--to take her on my arm again, and wander towards home--to stop sometimes upon the road, and heal her bruised feet and her worse-bruised heart--was all that I thowt of now. I doen’t believe I should have done so much as look at him. But, Mas’r Davy, it warn’t to be--not yet! I was too late, and they was gone. Wheer, I couldn’t learn. Some said heer, some said theer. I travelled heer, and I travelled theer, but I found no Em’ly, and I travelled home.’\n\n‘How long ago?’ I asked.\n\n‘A matter o’ fower days,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘I sighted the old boat arter dark, and the light a-shining in the winder. When I come nigh and looked in through the glass, I see the faithful creetur Missis Gummidge sittin’ by the fire, as we had fixed upon, alone. I called out, “Doen’t be afeerd! It’s Dan’l!” and I went in. I never could have thowt the old boat would have been so strange!’ From some pocket in his breast, he took out, with a very careful hand a small paper bundle containing two or three letters or little packets, which he laid upon the table.\n\n‘This fust one come,’ he said, selecting it from the rest, ‘afore I had been gone a week. A fifty pound Bank note, in a sheet of paper, directed to me, and put underneath the door in the night. She tried to hide her writing, but she couldn’t hide it from Me!’\n\nHe folded up the note again, with great patience and care, in exactly the same form, and laid it on one side.\n\n‘This come to Missis Gummidge,’ he said, opening another, ‘two or three months ago.’ After looking at it for some moments, he gave it to me, and added in a low voice, ‘Be so good as read it, sir.’\n\nI read as follows:\n\n‘Oh what will you feel when you see this writing, and know it comes from my wicked hand! But try, try--not for my sake, but for uncle’s goodness, try to let your heart soften to me, only for a little little time! Try, pray do, to relent towards a miserable girl, and write down on a bit of paper whether he is well, and what he said about me before you left off ever naming me among yourselves--and whether, of a night, when it is my old time of coming home, you ever see him look as if he thought of one he used to love so dear. Oh, my heart is breaking when I think about it! I am kneeling down to you, begging and praying you not to be as hard with me as I deserve--as I well, well, know I deserve--but to be so gentle and so good, as to write down something of him, and to send it to me. You need not call me Little, you need not call me by the name I have disgraced; but oh, listen to my agony, and have mercy on me so far as to write me some word of uncle, never, never to be seen in this world by my eyes again!\n\n‘Dear, if your heart is hard towards me--justly hard, I know--but, listen, if it is hard, dear, ask him I have wronged the most--him whose wife I was to have been--before you quite decide against my poor poor prayer! If he should be so compassionate as to say that you might write something for me to read--I think he would, oh, I think he would, if you would only ask him, for he always was so brave and so forgiving--tell him then (but not else), that when I hear the wind blowing at night, I feel as if it was passing angrily from seeing him and uncle, and was going up to God against me. Tell him that if I was to die tomorrow (and oh, if I was fit, I would be so glad to die!) I would bless him and uncle with my last words, and pray for his happy home with my last breath!’\n\nSome money was enclosed in this letter also. Five pounds. It was untouched like the previous sum, and he refolded it in the same way. Detailed instructions were added relative to the address of a reply, which, although they betrayed the intervention of several hands, and made it difficult to arrive at any very probable conclusion in reference to her place of concealment, made it at least not unlikely that she had written from that spot where she was stated to have been seen.\n\n‘What answer was sent?’ I inquired of Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘Missis Gummidge,’ he returned, ‘not being a good scholar, sir, Ham kindly drawed it out, and she made a copy on it. They told her I was gone to seek her, and what my parting words was.’\n\n‘Is that another letter in your hand?’ said I.\n\n‘It’s money, sir,’ said Mr. Peggotty, unfolding it a little way. ‘Ten pound, you see. And wrote inside, “From a true friend,” like the fust. But the fust was put underneath the door, and this come by the post, day afore yesterday. I’m a-going to seek her at the post-mark.’\n\nHe showed it to me. It was a town on the Upper Rhine. He had found out, at Yarmouth, some foreign dealers who knew that country, and they had drawn him a rude map on paper, which he could very well understand. He laid it between us on the table; and, with his chin resting on one hand, tracked his course upon it with the other.\n\nI asked him how Ham was? He shook his head.\n\n‘He works,’ he said, ‘as bold as a man can. His name’s as good, in all that part, as any man’s is, anywheres in the wureld. Anyone’s hand is ready to help him, you understand, and his is ready to help them. He’s never been heerd fur to complain. But my sister’s belief is (‘twixt ourselves) as it has cut him deep.’\n\n‘Poor fellow, I can believe it!’\n\n‘He ain’t no care, Mas’r Davy,’ said Mr. Peggotty in a solemn whisper--‘kinder no care no-how for his life. When a man’s wanted for rough sarvice in rough weather, he’s theer. When there’s hard duty to be done with danger in it, he steps for’ard afore all his mates. And yet he’s as gentle as any child. There ain’t a child in Yarmouth that doen’t know him.’\n\nHe gathered up the letters thoughtfully, smoothing them with his hand; put them into their little bundle; and placed it tenderly in his breast again. The face was gone from the door. I still saw the snow drifting in; but nothing else was there.\n\n‘Well!’ he said, looking to his bag, ‘having seen you tonight, Mas’r Davy (and that doos me good!), I shall away betimes tomorrow morning. You have seen what I’ve got heer’; putting his hand on where the little packet lay; ‘all that troubles me is, to think that any harm might come to me, afore that money was give back. If I was to die, and it was lost, or stole, or elseways made away with, and it was never know’d by him but what I’d took it, I believe the t’other wureld wouldn’t hold me! I believe I must come back!’\n\nHe rose, and I rose too; we grasped each other by the hand again, before going out.\n\n‘I’d go ten thousand mile,’ he said, ‘I’d go till I dropped dead, to lay that money down afore him. If I do that, and find my Em’ly, I’m content. If I doen’t find her, maybe she’ll come to hear, sometime, as her loving uncle only ended his search for her when he ended his life; and if I know her, even that will turn her home at last!’\n\nAs he went out into the rigorous night, I saw the lonely figure flit away before us. I turned him hastily on some pretence, and held him in conversation until it was gone.\n\nHe spoke of a traveller’s house on the Dover Road, where he knew he could find a clean, plain lodging for the night. I went with him over Westminster Bridge, and parted from him on the Surrey shore. Everything seemed, to my imagination, to be hushed in reverence for him, as he resumed his solitary journey through the snow.\n\nI returned to the inn yard, and, impressed by my remembrance of the face, looked awfully around for it. It was not there. The snow had covered our late footprints; my new track was the only one to be seen; and even that began to die away (it snowed so fast) as I looked back over my shoulder.\n\n# CHAPTER 41. DORA’S AUNTS\n\nAt last, an answer came from the two old ladies. They presented their compliments to Mr. Copperfield, and informed him that they had given his letter their best consideration, ‘with a view to the happiness of both parties’--which I thought rather an alarming expression, not only because of the use they had made of it in relation to the family difference before-mentioned, but because I had (and have all my life) observed that conventional phrases are a sort of fireworks, easily let off, and liable to take a great variety of shapes and colours not at all suggested by their original form. The Misses Spenlow added that they begged to forbear expressing, ‘through the medium of correspondence’, an opinion on the subject of Mr. Copperfield’s communication; but that if Mr. Copperfield would do them the favour to call, upon a certain day (accompanied, if he thought proper, by a confidential friend), they would be happy to hold some conversation on the subject.\n\nTo this favour, Mr. Copperfield immediately replied, with his respectful compliments, that he would have the honour of waiting on the Misses Spenlow, at the time appointed; accompanied, in accordance with their kind permission, by his friend Mr. Thomas Traddles of the Inner Temple. Having dispatched which missive, Mr. Copperfield fell into a condition of strong nervous agitation; and so remained until the day arrived.\n\nIt was a great augmentation of my uneasiness to be bereaved, at this eventful crisis, of the inestimable services of Miss Mills. But Mr. Mills, who was always doing something or other to annoy me--or I felt as if he were, which was the same thing--had brought his conduct to a climax, by taking it into his head that he would go to India. Why should he go to India, except to harass me? To be sure he had nothing to do with any other part of the world, and had a good deal to do with that part; being entirely in the India trade, whatever that was (I had floating dreams myself concerning golden shawls and elephants’ teeth); having been at Calcutta in his youth; and designing now to go out there again, in the capacity of resident partner. But this was nothing to me. However, it was so much to him that for India he was bound, and Julia with him; and Julia went into the country to take leave of her relations; and the house was put into a perfect suit of bills, announcing that it was to be let or sold, and that the furniture (Mangle and all) was to be taken at a valuation. So, here was another earthquake of which I became the sport, before I had recovered from the shock of its predecessor!\n\nI was in several minds how to dress myself on the important day; being divided between my desire to appear to advantage, and my apprehensions of putting on anything that might impair my severely practical character in the eyes of the Misses Spenlow. I endeavoured to hit a happy medium between these two extremes; my aunt approved the result; and Mr. Dick threw one of his shoes after Traddles and me, for luck, as we went downstairs.\n\nExcellent fellow as I knew Traddles to be, and warmly attached to him as I was, I could not help wishing, on that delicate occasion, that he had never contracted the habit of brushing his hair so very upright. It gave him a surprised look--not to say a hearth-broomy kind of expression--which, my apprehensions whispered, might be fatal to us.\n\nI took the liberty of mentioning it to Traddles, as we were walking to Putney; and saying that if he WOULD smooth it down a little--\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, lifting off his hat, and rubbing his hair all kinds of ways, ‘nothing would give me greater pleasure. But it won’t.’\n\n‘Won’t be smoothed down?’ said I.\n\n‘No,’ said Traddles. ‘Nothing will induce it. If I was to carry a half-hundred-weight upon it, all the way to Putney, it would be up again the moment the weight was taken off. You have no idea what obstinate hair mine is, Copperfield. I am quite a fretful porcupine.’\n\nI was a little disappointed, I must confess, but thoroughly charmed by his good-nature too. I told him how I esteemed his good-nature; and said that his hair must have taken all the obstinacy out of his character, for he had none.\n\n‘Oh!’ returned Traddles, laughing. ‘I assure you, it’s quite an old story, my unfortunate hair. My uncle’s wife couldn’t bear it. She said it exasperated her. It stood very much in my way, too, when I first fell in love with Sophy. Very much!’\n\n‘Did she object to it?’\n\n‘SHE didn’t,’ rejoined Traddles; ‘but her eldest sister--the one that’s the Beauty--quite made game of it, I understand. In fact, all the sisters laugh at it.’\n\n‘Agreeable!’ said I.\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Traddles with perfect innocence, ‘it’s a joke for us. They pretend that Sophy has a lock of it in her desk, and is obliged to shut it in a clasped book, to keep it down. We laugh about it.’\n\n‘By the by, my dear Traddles,’ said I, ‘your experience may suggest something to me. When you became engaged to the young lady whom you have just mentioned, did you make a regular proposal to her family? Was there anything like--what we are going through today, for instance?’ I added, nervously.\n\n‘Why,’ replied Traddles, on whose attentive face a thoughtful shade had stolen, ‘it was rather a painful transaction, Copperfield, in my case. You see, Sophy being of so much use in the family, none of them could endure the thought of her ever being married. Indeed, they had quite settled among themselves that she never was to be married, and they called her the old maid. Accordingly, when I mentioned it, with the greatest precaution, to Mrs. Crewler--’\n\n‘The mama?’ said I.\n\n‘The mama,’ said Traddles--‘Reverend Horace Crewler--when I mentioned it with every possible precaution to Mrs. Crewler, the effect upon her was such that she gave a scream and became insensible. I couldn’t approach the subject again, for months.’\n\n‘You did at last?’ said I.\n\n‘Well, the Reverend Horace did,’ said Traddles. ‘He is an excellent man, most exemplary in every way; and he pointed out to her that she ought, as a Christian, to reconcile herself to the sacrifice (especially as it was so uncertain), and to bear no uncharitable feeling towards me. As to myself, Copperfield, I give you my word, I felt a perfect bird of prey towards the family.’\n\n‘The sisters took your part, I hope, Traddles?’\n\n‘Why, I can’t say they did,’ he returned. ‘When we had comparatively reconciled Mrs. Crewler to it, we had to break it to Sarah. You recollect my mentioning Sarah, as the one that has something the matter with her spine?’\n\n‘Perfectly!’\n\n‘She clenched both her hands,’ said Traddles, looking at me in dismay; ‘shut her eyes; turned lead-colour; became perfectly stiff; and took nothing for two days but toast-and-water, administered with a tea-spoon.’\n\n‘What a very unpleasant girl, Traddles!’ I remarked.\n\n‘Oh, I beg your pardon, Copperfield!’ said Traddles. ‘She is a very charming girl, but she has a great deal of feeling. In fact, they all have. Sophy told me afterwards, that the self-reproach she underwent while she was in attendance upon Sarah, no words could describe. I know it must have been severe, by my own feelings, Copperfield; which were like a criminal’s. After Sarah was restored, we still had to break it to the other eight; and it produced various effects upon them of a most pathetic nature. The two little ones, whom Sophy educates, have only just left off de-testing me.’\n\n‘At any rate, they are all reconciled to it now, I hope?’ said I.\n\n‘Ye-yes, I should say they were, on the whole, resigned to it,’ said Traddles, doubtfully. ‘The fact is, we avoid mentioning the subject; and my unsettled prospects and indifferent circumstances are a great consolation to them. There will be a deplorable scene, whenever we are married. It will be much more like a funeral, than a wedding. And they’ll all hate me for taking her away!’\n\nHis honest face, as he looked at me with a serio-comic shake of his head, impresses me more in the remembrance than it did in the reality, for I was by this time in a state of such excessive trepidation and wandering of mind, as to be quite unable to fix my attention on anything. On our approaching the house where the Misses Spenlow lived, I was at such a discount in respect of my personal looks and presence of mind, that Traddles proposed a gentle stimulant in the form of a glass of ale. This having been administered at a neighbouring public-house, he conducted me, with tottering steps, to the Misses Spenlow’s door.\n\nI had a vague sensation of being, as it were, on view, when the maid opened it; and of wavering, somehow, across a hall with a weather-glass in it, into a quiet little drawing-room on the ground-floor, commanding a neat garden. Also of sitting down here, on a sofa, and seeing Traddles’s hair start up, now his hat was removed, like one of those obtrusive little figures made of springs, that fly out of fictitious snuff-boxes when the lid is taken off. Also of hearing an old-fashioned clock ticking away on the chimney-piece, and trying to make it keep time to the jerking of my heart,--which it wouldn’t. Also of looking round the room for any sign of Dora, and seeing none. Also of thinking that Jip once barked in the distance, and was instantly choked by somebody. Ultimately I found myself backing Traddles into the fireplace, and bowing in great confusion to two dry little elderly ladies, dressed in black, and each looking wonderfully like a preparation in chip or tan of the late Mr. Spenlow.\n\n‘Pray,’ said one of the two little ladies, ‘be seated.’\n\nWhen I had done tumbling over Traddles, and had sat upon something which was not a cat--my first seat was--I so far recovered my sight, as to perceive that Mr. Spenlow had evidently been the youngest of the family; that there was a disparity of six or eight years between the two sisters; and that the younger appeared to be the manager of the conference, inasmuch as she had my letter in her hand--so familiar as it looked to me, and yet so odd!--and was referring to it through an eye-glass. They were dressed alike, but this sister wore her dress with a more youthful air than the other; and perhaps had a trifle more frill, or tucker, or brooch, or bracelet, or some little thing of that kind, which made her look more lively. They were both upright in their carriage, formal, precise, composed, and quiet. The sister who had not my letter, had her arms crossed on her breast, and resting on each other, like an Idol.\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield, I believe,’ said the sister who had got my letter, addressing herself to Traddles.\n\nThis was a frightful beginning. Traddles had to indicate that I was Mr. Copperfield, and I had to lay claim to myself, and they had to divest themselves of a preconceived opinion that Traddles was Mr. Copperfield, and altogether we were in a nice condition. To improve it, we all distinctly heard Jip give two short barks, and receive another choke.\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield!’ said the sister with the letter.\n\nI did something--bowed, I suppose--and was all attention, when the other sister struck in.\n\n‘My sister Lavinia,’ said she ‘being conversant with matters of this nature, will state what we consider most calculated to promote the happiness of both parties.’\n\nI discovered afterwards that Miss Lavinia was an authority in affairs of the heart, by reason of there having anciently existed a certain Mr. Pidger, who played short whist, and was supposed to have been enamoured of her. My private opinion is, that this was entirely a gratuitous assumption, and that Pidger was altogether innocent of any such sentiments--to which he had never given any sort of expression that I could ever hear of. Both Miss Lavinia and Miss Clarissa had a superstition, however, that he would have declared his passion, if he had not been cut short in his youth (at about sixty) by over-drinking his constitution, and over-doing an attempt to set it right again by swilling Bath water. They had a lurking suspicion even, that he died of secret love; though I must say there was a picture of him in the house with a damask nose, which concealment did not appear to have ever preyed upon.\n\n‘We will not,’ said Miss Lavinia, ‘enter on the past history of this matter. Our poor brother Francis’s death has cancelled that.’\n\n‘We had not,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘been in the habit of frequent association with our brother Francis; but there was no decided division or disunion between us. Francis took his road; we took ours. We considered it conducive to the happiness of all parties that it should be so. And it was so.’\n\nEach of the sisters leaned a little forward to speak, shook her head after speaking, and became upright again when silent. Miss Clarissa never moved her arms. She sometimes played tunes upon them with her fingers--minuets and marches I should think--but never moved them.\n\n‘Our niece’s position, or supposed position, is much changed by our brother Francis’s death,’ said Miss Lavinia; ‘and therefore we consider our brother’s opinions as regarded her position as being changed too. We have no reason to doubt, Mr. Copperfield, that you are a young gentleman possessed of good qualities and honourable character; or that you have an affection--or are fully persuaded that you have an affection--for our niece.’\n\nI replied, as I usually did whenever I had a chance, that nobody had ever loved anybody else as I loved Dora. Traddles came to my assistance with a confirmatory murmur.\n\nMiss Lavinia was going on to make some rejoinder, when Miss Clarissa, who appeared to be incessantly beset by a desire to refer to her brother Francis, struck in again:\n\n‘If Dora’s mama,’ she said, ‘when she married our brother Francis, had at once said that there was not room for the family at the dinner-table, it would have been better for the happiness of all parties.’\n\n‘Sister Clarissa,’ said Miss Lavinia. ‘Perhaps we needn’t mind that now.’\n\n‘Sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘it belongs to the subject. With your branch of the subject, on which alone you are competent to speak, I should not think of interfering. On this branch of the subject I have a voice and an opinion. It would have been better for the happiness of all parties, if Dora’s mama, when she married our brother Francis, had mentioned plainly what her intentions were. We should then have known what we had to expect. We should have said “Pray do not invite us, at any time”; and all possibility of misunderstanding would have been avoided.’\n\nWhen Miss Clarissa had shaken her head, Miss Lavinia resumed: again referring to my letter through her eye-glass. They both had little bright round twinkling eyes, by the way, which were like birds’ eyes. They were not unlike birds, altogether; having a sharp, brisk, sudden manner, and a little short, spruce way of adjusting themselves, like canaries.\n\nMiss Lavinia, as I have said, resumed:\n\n‘You ask permission of my sister Clarissa and myself, Mr. Copperfield, to visit here, as the accepted suitor of our niece.’\n\n‘If our brother Francis,’ said Miss Clarissa, breaking out again, if I may call anything so calm a breaking out, ‘wished to surround himself with an atmosphere of Doctors’ Commons, and of Doctors’ Commons only, what right or desire had we to object? None, I am sure. We have ever been far from wishing to obtrude ourselves on anyone. But why not say so? Let our brother Francis and his wife have their society. Let my sister Lavinia and myself have our society. We can find it for ourselves, I hope.’\n\nAs this appeared to be addressed to Traddles and me, both Traddles and I made some sort of reply. Traddles was inaudible. I think I observed, myself, that it was highly creditable to all concerned. I don’t in the least know what I meant.\n\n‘Sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa, having now relieved her mind, ‘you can go on, my dear.’\n\nMiss Lavinia proceeded:\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield, my sister Clarissa and I have been very careful indeed in considering this letter; and we have not considered it without finally showing it to our niece, and discussing it with our niece. We have no doubt that you think you like her very much.’\n\n‘Think, ma’am,’ I rapturously began, ‘oh!--’\n\nBut Miss Clarissa giving me a look (just like a sharp canary), as requesting that I would not interrupt the oracle, I begged pardon.\n\n‘Affection,’ said Miss Lavinia, glancing at her sister for corroboration, which she gave in the form of a little nod to every clause, ‘mature affection, homage, devotion, does not easily express itself. Its voice is low. It is modest and retiring, it lies in ambush, waits and waits. Such is the mature fruit. Sometimes a life glides away, and finds it still ripening in the shade.’\n\nOf course I did not understand then that this was an allusion to her supposed experience of the stricken Pidger; but I saw, from the gravity with which Miss Clarissa nodded her head, that great weight was attached to these words.\n\n‘The light--for I call them, in comparison with such sentiments, the light--inclinations of very young people,’ pursued Miss Lavinia, ‘are dust, compared to rocks. It is owing to the difficulty of knowing whether they are likely to endure or have any real foundation, that my sister Clarissa and myself have been very undecided how to act, Mr. Copperfield, and Mr.--’\n\n‘Traddles,’ said my friend, finding himself looked at.\n\n‘I beg pardon. Of the Inner Temple, I believe?’ said Miss Clarissa, again glancing at my letter.\n\nTraddles said ‘Exactly so,’ and became pretty red in the face.\n\nNow, although I had not received any express encouragement as yet, I fancied that I saw in the two little sisters, and particularly in Miss Lavinia, an intensified enjoyment of this new and fruitful subject of domestic interest, a settling down to make the most of it, a disposition to pet it, in which there was a good bright ray of hope. I thought I perceived that Miss Lavinia would have uncommon satisfaction in superintending two young lovers, like Dora and me; and that Miss Clarissa would have hardly less satisfaction in seeing her superintend us, and in chiming in with her own particular department of the subject whenever that impulse was strong upon her. This gave me courage to protest most vehemently that I loved Dora better than I could tell, or anyone believe; that all my friends knew how I loved her; that my aunt, Agnes, Traddles, everyone who knew me, knew how I loved her, and how earnest my love had made me. For the truth of this, I appealed to Traddles. And Traddles, firing up as if he were plunging into a Parliamentary Debate, really did come out nobly: confirming me in good round terms, and in a plain sensible practical manner, that evidently made a favourable impression.\n\n‘I speak, if I may presume to say so, as one who has some little experience of such things,’ said Traddles, ‘being myself engaged to a young lady--one of ten, down in Devonshire--and seeing no probability, at present, of our engagement coming to a termination.’\n\n‘You may be able to confirm what I have said, Mr. Traddles,’ observed Miss Lavinia, evidently taking a new interest in him, ‘of the affection that is modest and retiring; that waits and waits?’\n\n‘Entirely, ma’am,’ said Traddles.\n\nMiss Clarissa looked at Miss Lavinia, and shook her head gravely. Miss Lavinia looked consciously at Miss Clarissa, and heaved a little sigh. ‘Sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘take my smelling-bottle.’\n\nMiss Lavinia revived herself with a few whiffs of aromatic vinegar--Traddles and I looking on with great solicitude the while; and then went on to say, rather faintly:\n\n‘My sister and myself have been in great doubt, Mr. Traddles, what course we ought to take in reference to the likings, or imaginary likings, of such very young people as your friend Mr. Copperfield and our niece.’\n\n‘Our brother Francis’s child,’ remarked Miss Clarissa. ‘If our brother Francis’s wife had found it convenient in her lifetime (though she had an unquestionable right to act as she thought best) to invite the family to her dinner-table, we might have known our brother Francis’s child better at the present moment. Sister Lavinia, proceed.’\n\nMiss Lavinia turned my letter, so as to bring the superscription towards herself, and referred through her eye-glass to some orderly-looking notes she had made on that part of it.\n\n‘It seems to us,’ said she, ‘prudent, Mr. Traddles, to bring these feelings to the test of our own observation. At present we know nothing of them, and are not in a situation to judge how much reality there may be in them. Therefore we are inclined so far to accede to Mr. Copperfield’s proposal, as to admit his visits here.’\n\n‘I shall never, dear ladies,’ I exclaimed, relieved of an immense load of apprehension, ‘forget your kindness!’\n\n‘But,’ pursued Miss Lavinia,--‘but, we would prefer to regard those visits, Mr. Traddles, as made, at present, to us. We must guard ourselves from recognizing any positive engagement between Mr. Copperfield and our niece, until we have had an opportunity--’\n\n‘Until YOU have had an opportunity, sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa.\n\n‘Be it so,’ assented Miss Lavinia, with a sigh--‘until I have had an opportunity of observing them.’\n\n‘Copperfield,’ said Traddles, turning to me, ‘you feel, I am sure, that nothing could be more reasonable or considerate.’\n\n‘Nothing!’ cried I. ‘I am deeply sensible of it.’\n\n‘In this position of affairs,’ said Miss Lavinia, again referring to her notes, ‘and admitting his visits on this understanding only, we must require from Mr. Copperfield a distinct assurance, on his word of honour, that no communication of any kind shall take place between him and our niece without our knowledge. That no project whatever shall be entertained with regard to our niece, without being first submitted to us--’ ‘To you, sister Lavinia,’ Miss Clarissa interposed.\n\n‘Be it so, Clarissa!’ assented Miss Lavinia resignedly--‘to me--and receiving our concurrence. We must make this a most express and serious stipulation, not to be broken on any account. We wished Mr. Copperfield to be accompanied by some confidential friend today,’ with an inclination of her head towards Traddles, who bowed, ‘in order that there might be no doubt or misconception on this subject. If Mr. Copperfield, or if you, Mr. Traddles, feel the least scruple, in giving this promise, I beg you to take time to consider it.’\n\nI exclaimed, in a state of high ecstatic fervour, that not a moment’s consideration could be necessary. I bound myself by the required promise, in a most impassioned manner; called upon Traddles to witness it; and denounced myself as the most atrocious of characters if I ever swerved from it in the least degree.\n\n‘Stay!’ said Miss Lavinia, holding up her hand; ‘we resolved, before we had the pleasure of receiving you two gentlemen, to leave you alone for a quarter of an hour, to consider this point. You will allow us to retire.’\n\nIt was in vain for me to say that no consideration was necessary. They persisted in withdrawing for the specified time. Accordingly, these little birds hopped out with great dignity; leaving me to receive the congratulations of Traddles, and to feel as if I were translated to regions of exquisite happiness. Exactly at the expiration of the quarter of an hour, they reappeared with no less dignity than they had disappeared. They had gone rustling away as if their little dresses were made of autumn-leaves: and they came rustling back, in like manner.\n\nI then bound myself once more to the prescribed conditions.\n\n‘Sister Clarissa,’ said Miss Lavinia, ‘the rest is with you.’\n\nMiss Clarissa, unfolding her arms for the first time, took the notes and glanced at them.\n\n‘We shall be happy,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘to see Mr. Copperfield to dinner, every Sunday, if it should suit his convenience. Our hour is three.’\nI bowed.\n\n‘In the course of the week,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘we shall be happy to see Mr. Copperfield to tea. Our hour is half-past six.’\n\nI bowed again.\n\n‘Twice in the week,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘but, as a rule, not oftener.’\n\nI bowed again.\n\n‘Miss Trotwood,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘mentioned in Mr. Copperfield’s letter, will perhaps call upon us. When visiting is better for the happiness of all parties, we are glad to receive visits, and return them. When it is better for the happiness of all parties that no visiting should take place, (as in the case of our brother Francis, and his establishment) that is quite different.’\n\nI intimated that my aunt would be proud and delighted to make their acquaintance; though I must say I was not quite sure of their getting on very satisfactorily together. The conditions being now closed, I expressed my acknowledgements in the warmest manner; and, taking the hand, first of Miss Clarissa, and then of Miss Lavinia, pressed it, in each case, to my lips.\n\nMiss Lavinia then arose, and begging Mr. Traddles to excuse us for a minute, requested me to follow her. I obeyed, all in a tremble, and was conducted into another room. There I found my blessed darling stopping her ears behind the door, with her dear little face against the wall; and Jip in the plate-warmer with his head tied up in a towel.\n\nOh! How beautiful she was in her black frock, and how she sobbed and cried at first, and wouldn’t come out from behind the door! How fond we were of one another, when she did come out at last; and what a state of bliss I was in, when we took Jip out of the plate-warmer, and restored him to the light, sneezing very much, and were all three reunited!\n\n‘My dearest Dora! Now, indeed, my own for ever!’\n\n‘Oh, DON’T!’ pleaded Dora. ‘Please!’\n\n‘Are you not my own for ever, Dora?’\n\n‘Oh yes, of course I am!’ cried Dora, ‘but I am so frightened!’\n\n‘Frightened, my own?’\n\n‘Oh yes! I don’t like him,’ said Dora. ‘Why don’t he go?’\n\n‘Who, my life?’\n\n‘Your friend,’ said Dora. ‘It isn’t any business of his. What a stupid he must be!’\n\n‘My love!’ (There never was anything so coaxing as her childish ways.) ‘He is the best creature!’\n\n‘Oh, but we don’t want any best creatures!’ pouted Dora.\n\n‘My dear,’ I argued, ‘you will soon know him well, and like him of all things. And here is my aunt coming soon; and you’ll like her of all things too, when you know her.’\n\n‘No, please don’t bring her!’ said Dora, giving me a horrified little kiss, and folding her hands. ‘Don’t. I know she’s a naughty, mischief-making old thing! Don’t let her come here, Doady!’ which was a corruption of David.\n\nRemonstrance was of no use, then; so I laughed, and admired, and was very much in love and very happy; and she showed me Jip’s new trick of standing on his hind legs in a corner--which he did for about the space of a flash of lightning, and then fell down--and I don’t know how long I should have stayed there, oblivious of Traddles, if Miss Lavinia had not come in to take me away. Miss Lavinia was very fond of Dora (she told me Dora was exactly like what she had been herself at her age--she must have altered a good deal), and she treated Dora just as if she had been a toy. I wanted to persuade Dora to come and see Traddles, but on my proposing it she ran off to her own room and locked herself in; so I went to Traddles without her, and walked away with him on air.\n\n‘Nothing could be more satisfactory,’ said Traddles; ‘and they are very agreeable old ladies, I am sure. I shouldn’t be at all surprised if you were to be married years before me, Copperfield.’\n\n‘Does your Sophy play on any instrument, Traddles?’ I inquired, in the pride of my heart.\n\n‘She knows enough of the piano to teach it to her little sisters,’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Does she sing at all?’ I asked.\n\n‘Why, she sings ballads, sometimes, to freshen up the others a little when they’re out of spirits,’ said Traddles. ‘Nothing scientific.’\n\n‘She doesn’t sing to the guitar?’ said I.\n\n‘Oh dear no!’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Paint at all?’\n\n‘Not at all,’ said Traddles.\n\nI promised Traddles that he should hear Dora sing, and see some of her flower-painting. He said he should like it very much, and we went home arm in arm in great good humour and delight. I encouraged him to talk about Sophy, on the way; which he did with a loving reliance on her that I very much admired. I compared her in my mind with Dora, with considerable inward satisfaction; but I candidly admitted to myself that she seemed to be an excellent kind of girl for Traddles, too.\n\nOf course my aunt was immediately made acquainted with the successful issue of the conference, and with all that had been said and done in the course of it. She was happy to see me so happy, and promised to call on Dora’s aunts without loss of time. But she took such a long walk up and down our rooms that night, while I was writing to Agnes, that I began to think she meant to walk till morning.\n\nMy letter to Agnes was a fervent and grateful one, narrating all the good effects that had resulted from my following her advice. She wrote, by return of post, to me. Her letter was hopeful, earnest, and cheerful. She was always cheerful from that time.\n\nI had my hands more full than ever, now. My daily journeys to Highgate considered, Putney was a long way off; and I naturally wanted to go there as often as I could. The proposed tea-drinkings being quite impracticable, I compounded with Miss Lavinia for permission to visit every Saturday afternoon, without detriment to my privileged Sundays. So, the close of every week was a delicious time for me; and I got through the rest of the week by looking forward to it.\n\nI was wonderfully relieved to find that my aunt and Dora’s aunts rubbed on, all things considered, much more smoothly than I could have expected. My aunt made her promised visit within a few days of the conference; and within a few more days, Dora’s aunts called upon her, in due state and form. Similar but more friendly exchanges took place afterwards, usually at intervals of three or four weeks. I know that my aunt distressed Dora’s aunts very much, by utterly setting at naught the dignity of fly-conveyance, and walking out to Putney at extraordinary times, as shortly after breakfast or just before tea; likewise by wearing her bonnet in any manner that happened to be comfortable to her head, without at all deferring to the prejudices of civilization on that subject. But Dora’s aunts soon agreed to regard my aunt as an eccentric and somewhat masculine lady, with a strong understanding; and although my aunt occasionally ruffled the feathers of Dora’s aunts, by expressing heretical opinions on various points of ceremony, she loved me too well not to sacrifice some of her little peculiarities to the general harmony.\n\nThe only member of our small society who positively refused to adapt himself to circumstances, was Jip. He never saw my aunt without immediately displaying every tooth in his head, retiring under a chair, and growling incessantly: with now and then a doleful howl, as if she really were too much for his feelings. All kinds of treatment were tried with him, coaxing, scolding, slapping, bringing him to Buckingham Street (where he instantly dashed at the two cats, to the terror of all beholders); but he never could prevail upon himself to bear my aunt’s society. He would sometimes think he had got the better of his objection, and be amiable for a few minutes; and then would put up his snub nose, and howl to that extent, that there was nothing for it but to blind him and put him in the plate-warmer. At length, Dora regularly muffled him in a towel and shut him up there, whenever my aunt was reported at the door.\n\nOne thing troubled me much, after we had fallen into this quiet train. It was, that Dora seemed by one consent to be regarded like a pretty toy or plaything. My aunt, with whom she gradually became familiar, always called her Little Blossom; and the pleasure of Miss Lavinia’s life was to wait upon her, curl her hair, make ornaments for her, and treat her like a pet child. What Miss Lavinia did, her sister did as a matter of course. It was very odd to me; but they all seemed to treat Dora, in her degree, much as Dora treated Jip in his.\n\nI made up my mind to speak to Dora about this; and one day when we were out walking (for we were licensed by Miss Lavinia, after a while, to go out walking by ourselves), I said to her that I wished she could get them to behave towards her differently.\n\n‘Because you know, my darling,’ I remonstrated, ‘you are not a child.’\n\n‘There!’ said Dora. ‘Now you’re going to be cross!’\n\n‘Cross, my love?’\n\n‘I am sure they’re very kind to me,’ said Dora, ‘and I am very happy--’\n\n‘Well! But my dearest life!’ said I, ‘you might be very happy, and yet be treated rationally.’\n\nDora gave me a reproachful look--the prettiest look!--and then began to sob, saying, if I didn’t like her, why had I ever wanted so much to be engaged to her? And why didn’t I go away, now, if I couldn’t bear her?\n\nWhat could I do, but kiss away her tears, and tell her how I doted on her, after that!\n\n‘I am sure I am very affectionate,’ said Dora; ‘you oughtn’t to be cruel to me, Doady!’\n\n‘Cruel, my precious love! As if I would--or could--be cruel to you, for the world!’\n\n‘Then don’t find fault with me,’ said Dora, making a rosebud of her mouth; ‘and I’ll be good.’\n\nI was charmed by her presently asking me, of her own accord, to give her that cookery-book I had once spoken of, and to show her how to keep accounts as I had once promised I would. I brought the volume with me on my next visit (I got it prettily bound, first, to make it look less dry and more inviting); and as we strolled about the Common, I showed her an old housekeeping-book of my aunt’s, and gave her a set of tablets, and a pretty little pencil-case and box of leads, to practise housekeeping with.\n\nBut the cookery-book made Dora’s head ache, and the figures made her cry. They wouldn’t add up, she said. So she rubbed them out, and drew little nosegays and likenesses of me and Jip, all over the tablets.\n\nThen I playfully tried verbal instruction in domestic matters, as we walked about on a Saturday afternoon. Sometimes, for example, when we passed a butcher’s shop, I would say:\n\n‘Now suppose, my pet, that we were married, and you were going to buy a shoulder of mutton for dinner, would you know how to buy it?’\n\nMy pretty little Dora’s face would fall, and she would make her mouth into a bud again, as if she would very much prefer to shut mine with a kiss.\n\n‘Would you know how to buy it, my darling?’ I would repeat, perhaps, if I were very inflexible.\n\nDora would think a little, and then reply, perhaps, with great triumph:\n\n‘Why, the butcher would know how to sell it, and what need I know? Oh, you silly boy!’\n\nSo, when I once asked Dora, with an eye to the cookery-book, what she would do, if we were married, and I were to say I should like a nice Irish stew, she replied that she would tell the servant to make it; and then clapped her little hands together across my arm, and laughed in such a charming manner that she was more delightful than ever.\n\nConsequently, the principal use to which the cookery-book was devoted, was being put down in the corner for Jip to stand upon. But Dora was so pleased, when she had trained him to stand upon it without offering to come off, and at the same time to hold the pencil-case in his mouth, that I was very glad I had bought it.\n\nAnd we fell back on the guitar-case, and the flower-painting, and the songs about never leaving off dancing, Ta ra la! and were as happy as the week was long. I occasionally wished I could venture to hint to Miss Lavinia, that she treated the darling of my heart a little too much like a plaything; and I sometimes awoke, as it were, wondering to find that I had fallen into the general fault, and treated her like a plaything too--but not often.\n\n# CHAPTER 42. MISCHIEF\n\nI feel as if it were not for me to record, even though this manuscript is intended for no eyes but mine, how hard I worked at that tremendous short-hand, and all improvement appertaining to it, in my sense of responsibility to Dora and her aunts. I will only add, to what I have already written of my perseverance at this time of my life, and of a patient and continuous energy which then began to be matured within me, and which I know to be the strong part of my character, if it have any strength at all, that there, on looking back, I find the source of my success. I have been very fortunate in worldly matters; many men have worked much harder, and not succeeded half so well; but I never could have done what I have done, without the habits of punctuality, order, and diligence, without the determination to concentrate myself on one object at a time, no matter how quickly its successor should come upon its heels, which I then formed. Heaven knows I write this, in no spirit of self-laudation. The man who reviews his own life, as I do mine, in going on here, from page to page, had need to have been a good man indeed, if he would be spared the sharp consciousness of many talents neglected, many opportunities wasted, many erratic and perverted feelings constantly at war within his breast, and defeating him. I do not hold one natural gift, I dare say, that I have not abused. My meaning simply is, that whatever I have tried to do in life, I have tried with all my heart to do well; that whatever I have devoted myself to, I have devoted myself to completely; that in great aims and in small, I have always been thoroughly in earnest. I have never believed it possible that any natural or improved ability can claim immunity from the companionship of the steady, plain, hard-working qualities, and hope to gain its end. There is no such thing as such fulfilment on this earth. Some happy talent, and some fortunate opportunity, may form the two sides of the ladder on which some men mount, but the rounds of that ladder must be made of stuff to stand wear and tear; and there is no substitute for thorough-going, ardent, and sincere earnestness. Never to put one hand to anything, on which I could throw my whole self; and never to affect depreciation of my work, whatever it was; I find, now, to have been my golden rules.\n\nHow much of the practice I have just reduced to precept, I owe to Agnes, I will not repeat here. My narrative proceeds to Agnes, with a thankful love.\n\nShe came on a visit of a fortnight to the Doctor’s. Mr. Wickfield was the Doctor’s old friend, and the Doctor wished to talk with him, and do him good. It had been matter of conversation with Agnes when she was last in town, and this visit was the result. She and her father came together. I was not much surprised to hear from her that she had engaged to find a lodging in the neighbourhood for Mrs. Heep, whose rheumatic complaint required change of air, and who would be charmed to have it in such company. Neither was I surprised when, on the very next day, Uriah, like a dutiful son, brought his worthy mother to take possession.\n\n‘You see, Master Copperfield,’ said he, as he forced himself upon my company for a turn in the Doctor’s garden, ‘where a person loves, a person is a little jealous--leastways, anxious to keep an eye on the beloved one.’\n\n‘Of whom are you jealous, now?’ said I.\n\n‘Thanks to you, Master Copperfield,’ he returned, ‘of no one in particular just at present--no male person, at least.’\n\n‘Do you mean that you are jealous of a female person?’\n\nHe gave me a sidelong glance out of his sinister red eyes, and laughed.\n‘Really, Master Copperfield,’ he said, ‘--I should say Mister, but I know you’ll excuse the abit I’ve got into--you’re so insinuating, that you draw me like a corkscrew! Well, I don’t mind telling you,’ putting his fish-like hand on mine, ‘I’m not a lady’s man in general, sir, and I never was, with Mrs. Strong.’\n\nHis eyes looked green now, as they watched mine with a rascally cunning.\n\n‘What do you mean?’ said I.\n\n‘Why, though I am a lawyer, Master Copperfield,’ he replied, with a dry grin, ‘I mean, just at present, what I say.’\n\n‘And what do you mean by your look?’ I retorted, quietly.\n\n‘By my look? Dear me, Copperfield, that’s sharp practice! What do I mean by my look?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said I. ‘By your look.’\n\nHe seemed very much amused, and laughed as heartily as it was in his nature to laugh. After some scraping of his chin with his hand, he went on to say, with his eyes cast downward--still scraping, very slowly:\n\n‘When I was but an umble clerk, she always looked down upon me. She was for ever having my Agnes backwards and forwards at her ouse, and she was for ever being a friend to you, Master Copperfield; but I was too far beneath her, myself, to be noticed.’\n\n‘Well?’ said I; ‘suppose you were!’\n\n‘--And beneath him too,’ pursued Uriah, very distinctly, and in a meditative tone of voice, as he continued to scrape his chin.\n\n‘Don’t you know the Doctor better,’ said I, ‘than to suppose him conscious of your existence, when you were not before him?’\n\nHe directed his eyes at me in that sidelong glance again, and he made his face very lantern-jawed, for the greater convenience of scraping, as he answered:\n\n‘Oh dear, I am not referring to the Doctor! Oh no, poor man! I mean Mr. Maldon!’\n\nMy heart quite died within me. All my old doubts and apprehensions on that subject, all the Doctor’s happiness and peace, all the mingled possibilities of innocence and compromise, that I could not unravel, I saw, in a moment, at the mercy of this fellow’s twisting.\n\n‘He never could come into the office, without ordering and shoving me about,’ said Uriah. ‘One of your fine gentlemen he was! I was very meek and umble--and I am. But I didn’t like that sort of thing--and I don’t!’\n\nHe left off scraping his chin, and sucked in his cheeks until they seemed to meet inside; keeping his sidelong glance upon me all the while.\n\n‘She is one of your lovely women, she is,’ he pursued, when he had slowly restored his face to its natural form; ‘and ready to be no friend to such as me, I know. She’s just the person as would put my Agnes up to higher sort of game. Now, I ain’t one of your lady’s men, Master Copperfield; but I’ve had eyes in my ed, a pretty long time back. We umble ones have got eyes, mostly speaking--and we look out of ‘em.’\n\nI endeavoured to appear unconscious and not disquieted, but, I saw in his face, with poor success.\n\n‘Now, I’m not a-going to let myself be run down, Copperfield,’ he continued, raising that part of his countenance, where his red eyebrows would have been if he had had any, with malignant triumph, ‘and I shall do what I can to put a stop to this friendship. I don’t approve of it. I don’t mind acknowledging to you that I’ve got rather a grudging disposition, and want to keep off all intruders. I ain’t a-going, if I know it, to run the risk of being plotted against.’\n\n‘You are always plotting, and delude yourself into the belief that everybody else is doing the like, I think,’ said I.\n\n‘Perhaps so, Master Copperfield,’ he replied. ‘But I’ve got a motive, as my fellow-partner used to say; and I go at it tooth and nail. I mustn’t be put upon, as a numble person, too much. I can’t allow people in my way. Really they must come out of the cart, Master Copperfield!’\n\n‘I don’t understand you,’ said I.\n\n‘Don’t you, though?’ he returned, with one of his jerks. ‘I’m astonished at that, Master Copperfield, you being usually so quick! I’ll try to be plainer, another time.---Is that Mr. Maldon a-norseback, ringing at the gate, sir?’\n\n‘It looks like him,’ I replied, as carelessly as I could.\n\nUriah stopped short, put his hands between his great knobs of knees, and doubled himself up with laughter. With perfectly silent laughter. Not a sound escaped from him. I was so repelled by his odious behaviour, particularly by this concluding instance, that I turned away without any ceremony; and left him doubled up in the middle of the garden, like a scarecrow in want of support.\n\nIt was not on that evening; but, as I well remember, on the next evening but one, which was a Sunday; that I took Agnes to see Dora. I had arranged the visit, beforehand, with Miss Lavinia; and Agnes was expected to tea.\n\nI was in a flutter of pride and anxiety; pride in my dear little betrothed, and anxiety that Agnes should like her. All the way to Putney, Agnes being inside the stage-coach, and I outside, I pictured Dora to myself in every one of the pretty looks I knew so well; now making up my mind that I should like her to look exactly as she looked at such a time, and then doubting whether I should not prefer her looking as she looked at such another time; and almost worrying myself into a fever about it.\n\nI was troubled by no doubt of her being very pretty, in any case; but it fell out that I had never seen her look so well. She was not in the drawing-room when I presented Agnes to her little aunts, but was shyly keeping out of the way. I knew where to look for her, now; and sure enough I found her stopping her ears again, behind the same dull old door.\n\nAt first she wouldn’t come at all; and then she pleaded for five minutes by my watch. When at length she put her arm through mine, to be taken to the drawing-room, her charming little face was flushed, and had never been so pretty. But, when we went into the room, and it turned pale, she was ten thousand times prettier yet.\n\nDora was afraid of Agnes. She had told me that she knew Agnes was ‘too clever’. But when she saw her looking at once so cheerful and so earnest, and so thoughtful, and so good, she gave a faint little cry of pleased surprise, and just put her affectionate arms round Agnes’s neck, and laid her innocent cheek against her face.\n\nI never was so happy. I never was so pleased as when I saw those two sit down together, side by side. As when I saw my little darling looking up so naturally to those cordial eyes. As when I saw the tender, beautiful regard which Agnes cast upon her.\n\nMiss Lavinia and Miss Clarissa partook, in their way, of my joy. It was the pleasantest tea-table in the world. Miss Clarissa presided. I cut and handed the sweet seed-cake--the little sisters had a bird-like fondness for picking up seeds and pecking at sugar; Miss Lavinia looked on with benignant patronage, as if our happy love were all her work; and we were perfectly contented with ourselves and one another.\n\nThe gentle cheerfulness of Agnes went to all their hearts. Her quiet interest in everything that interested Dora; her manner of making acquaintance with Jip (who responded instantly); her pleasant way, when Dora was ashamed to come over to her usual seat by me; her modest grace and ease, eliciting a crowd of blushing little marks of confidence from Dora; seemed to make our circle quite complete.\n\n‘I am so glad,’ said Dora, after tea, ‘that you like me. I didn’t think you would; and I want, more than ever, to be liked, now Julia Mills is gone.’\n\nI have omitted to mention it, by the by. Miss Mills had sailed, and Dora and I had gone aboard a great East Indiaman at Gravesend to see her; and we had had preserved ginger, and guava, and other delicacies of that sort for lunch; and we had left Miss Mills weeping on a camp-stool on the quarter-deck, with a large new diary under her arm, in which the original reflections awakened by the contemplation of Ocean were to be recorded under lock and key.\n\nAgnes said she was afraid I must have given her an unpromising character; but Dora corrected that directly.\n\n‘Oh no!’ she said, shaking her curls at me; ‘it was all praise. He thinks so much of your opinion, that I was quite afraid of it.’\n\n‘My good opinion cannot strengthen his attachment to some people whom he knows,’ said Agnes, with a smile; ‘it is not worth their having.’\n\n‘But please let me have it,’ said Dora, in her coaxing way, ‘if you can!’\n\nWe made merry about Dora’s wanting to be liked, and Dora said I was a goose, and she didn’t like me at any rate, and the short evening flew away on gossamer-wings. The time was at hand when the coach was to call for us. I was standing alone before the fire, when Dora came stealing softly in, to give me that usual precious little kiss before I went.\n\n‘Don’t you think, if I had had her for a friend a long time ago, Doady,’ said Dora, her bright eyes shining very brightly, and her little right hand idly busying itself with one of the buttons of my coat, ‘I might have been more clever perhaps?’\n\n‘My love!’ said I, ‘what nonsense!’\n\n‘Do you think it is nonsense?’ returned Dora, without looking at me. ‘Are you sure it is?’\n\n‘Of course I am!’ ‘I have forgotten,’ said Dora, still turning the button round and round, ‘what relation Agnes is to you, you dear bad boy.’\n\n‘No blood-relation,’ I replied; ‘but we were brought up together, like brother and sister.’\n\n‘I wonder why you ever fell in love with me?’ said Dora, beginning on another button of my coat.\n\n‘Perhaps because I couldn’t see you, and not love you, Dora!’\n\n‘Suppose you had never seen me at all,’ said Dora, going to another button.\n\n‘Suppose we had never been born!’ said I, gaily.\n\nI wondered what she was thinking about, as I glanced in admiring silence at the little soft hand travelling up the row of buttons on my coat, and at the clustering hair that lay against my breast, and at the lashes of her downcast eyes, slightly rising as they followed her idle fingers. At length her eyes were lifted up to mine, and she stood on tiptoe to give me, more thoughtfully than usual, that precious little kiss--once, twice, three times--and went out of the room.\n\nThey all came back together within five minutes afterwards, and Dora’s unusual thoughtfulness was quite gone then. She was laughingly resolved to put Jip through the whole of his performances, before the coach came. They took some time (not so much on account of their variety, as Jip’s reluctance), and were still unfinished when it was heard at the door. There was a hurried but affectionate parting between Agnes and herself; and Dora was to write to Agnes (who was not to mind her letters being foolish, she said), and Agnes was to write to Dora; and they had a second parting at the coach door, and a third when Dora, in spite of the remonstrances of Miss Lavinia, would come running out once more to remind Agnes at the coach window about writing, and to shake her curls at me on the box.\n\nThe stage-coach was to put us down near Covent Garden, where we were to take another stage-coach for Highgate. I was impatient for the short walk in the interval, that Agnes might praise Dora to me. Ah! what praise it was! How lovingly and fervently did it commend the pretty creature I had won, with all her artless graces best displayed, to my most gentle care! How thoughtfully remind me, yet with no pretence of doing so, of the trust in which I held the orphan child!\n\nNever, never, had I loved Dora so deeply and truly, as I loved her that night. When we had again alighted, and were walking in the starlight along the quiet road that led to the Doctor’s house, I told Agnes it was her doing.\n\n‘When you were sitting by her,’ said I, ‘you seemed to be no less her guardian angel than mine; and you seem so now, Agnes.’\n\n‘A poor angel,’ she returned, ‘but faithful.’\n\nThe clear tone of her voice, going straight to my heart, made it natural to me to say:\n\n‘The cheerfulness that belongs to you, Agnes (and to no one else that ever I have seen), is so restored, I have observed today, that I have begun to hope you are happier at home?’\n\n‘I am happier in myself,’ she said; ‘I am quite cheerful and light-hearted.’\n\nI glanced at the serene face looking upward, and thought it was the stars that made it seem so noble.\n\n‘There has been no change at home,’ said Agnes, after a few moments.\n\n‘No fresh reference,’ said I, ‘to--I wouldn’t distress you, Agnes, but I cannot help asking--to what we spoke of, when we parted last?’\n\n‘No, none,’ she answered.\n\n‘I have thought so much about it.’\n\n‘You must think less about it. Remember that I confide in simple love and truth at last. Have no apprehensions for me, Trotwood,’ she added, after a moment; ‘the step you dread my taking, I shall never take.’\n\nAlthough I think I had never really feared it, in any season of cool reflection, it was an unspeakable relief to me to have this assurance from her own truthful lips. I told her so, earnestly.\n\n‘And when this visit is over,’ said I,--‘for we may not be alone another time,--how long is it likely to be, my dear Agnes, before you come to London again?’\n\n‘Probably a long time,’ she replied; ‘I think it will be best--for papa’s sake--to remain at home. We are not likely to meet often, for some time to come; but I shall be a good correspondent of Dora’s, and we shall frequently hear of one another that way.’\n\nWe were now within the little courtyard of the Doctor’s cottage. It was growing late. There was a light in the window of Mrs. Strong’s chamber, and Agnes, pointing to it, bade me good night.\n\n‘Do not be troubled,’ she said, giving me her hand, ‘by our misfortunes and anxieties. I can be happier in nothing than in your happiness. If you can ever give me help, rely upon it I will ask you for it. God bless you always!’ In her beaming smile, and in these last tones of her cheerful voice, I seemed again to see and hear my little Dora in her company. I stood awhile, looking through the porch at the stars, with a heart full of love and gratitude, and then walked slowly forth. I had engaged a bed at a decent alehouse close by, and was going out at the gate, when, happening to turn my head, I saw a light in the Doctor’s study. A half-reproachful fancy came into my mind, that he had been working at the Dictionary without my help. With the view of seeing if this were so, and, in any case, of bidding him good night, if he were yet sitting among his books, I turned back, and going softly across the hall, and gently opening the door, looked in.\n\nThe first person whom I saw, to my surprise, by the sober light of the shaded lamp, was Uriah. He was standing close beside it, with one of his skeleton hands over his mouth, and the other resting on the Doctor’s table. The Doctor sat in his study chair, covering his face with his hands. Mr. Wickfield, sorely troubled and distressed, was leaning forward, irresolutely touching the Doctor’s arm.\n\nFor an instant, I supposed that the Doctor was ill. I hastily advanced a step under that impression, when I met Uriah’s eye, and saw what was the matter. I would have withdrawn, but the Doctor made a gesture to detain me, and I remained.\n\n‘At any rate,’ observed Uriah, with a writhe of his ungainly person, ‘we may keep the door shut. We needn’t make it known to ALL the town.’\n\nSaying which, he went on his toes to the door, which I had left open, and carefully closed it. He then came back, and took up his former position. There was an obtrusive show of compassionate zeal in his voice and manner, more intolerable--at least to me--than any demeanour he could have assumed.\n\n‘I have felt it incumbent upon me, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘to point out to Doctor Strong what you and me have already talked about. You didn’t exactly understand me, though?’\n\nI gave him a look, but no other answer; and, going to my good old master, said a few words that I meant to be words of comfort and encouragement. He put his hand upon my shoulder, as it had been his custom to do when I was quite a little fellow, but did not lift his grey head.\n\n‘As you didn’t understand me, Master Copperfield,’ resumed Uriah in the same officious manner, ‘I may take the liberty of umbly mentioning, being among friends, that I have called Doctor Strong’s attention to the goings-on of Mrs. Strong. It’s much against the grain with me, I assure you, Copperfield, to be concerned in anything so unpleasant; but really, as it is, we’re all mixing ourselves up with what oughtn’t to be. That was what my meaning was, sir, when you didn’t understand me.’ I wonder now, when I recall his leer, that I did not collar him, and try to shake the breath out of his body.\n\n‘I dare say I didn’t make myself very clear,’ he went on, ‘nor you neither. Naturally, we was both of us inclined to give such a subject a wide berth. Hows’ever, at last I have made up my mind to speak plain; and I have mentioned to Doctor Strong that--did you speak, sir?’\n\nThis was to the Doctor, who had moaned. The sound might have touched any heart, I thought, but it had no effect upon Uriah’s.\n\n‘--mentioned to Doctor Strong,’ he proceeded, ‘that anyone may see that Mr. Maldon, and the lovely and agreeable lady as is Doctor Strong’s wife, are too sweet on one another. Really the time is come (we being at present all mixing ourselves up with what oughtn’t to be), when Doctor Strong must be told that this was full as plain to everybody as the sun, before Mr. Maldon went to India; that Mr. Maldon made excuses to come back, for nothing else; and that he’s always here, for nothing else. When you come in, sir, I was just putting it to my fellow-partner,’ towards whom he turned, ‘to say to Doctor Strong upon his word and honour, whether he’d ever been of this opinion long ago, or not. Come, Mr. Wickfield, sir! Would you be so good as tell us? Yes or no, sir? Come, partner!’\n\n‘For God’s sake, my dear Doctor,’ said Mr. Wickfield again laying his irresolute hand upon the Doctor’s arm, ‘don’t attach too much weight to any suspicions I may have entertained.’\n\n‘There!’ cried Uriah, shaking his head. ‘What a melancholy confirmation: ain’t it? Him! Such an old friend! Bless your soul, when I was nothing but a clerk in his office, Copperfield, I’ve seen him twenty times, if I’ve seen him once, quite in a taking about it--quite put out, you know (and very proper in him as a father; I’m sure I can’t blame him), to think that Miss Agnes was mixing herself up with what oughtn’t to be.’\n\n‘My dear Strong,’ said Mr. Wickfield in a tremulous voice, ‘my good friend, I needn’t tell you that it has been my vice to look for some one master motive in everybody, and to try all actions by one narrow test. I may have fallen into such doubts as I have had, through this mistake.’\n\n‘You have had doubts, Wickfield,’ said the Doctor, without lifting up his head. ‘You have had doubts.’\n\n‘Speak up, fellow-partner,’ urged Uriah.\n\n‘I had, at one time, certainly,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘I--God forgive me--I thought YOU had.’\n\n‘No, no, no!’ returned the Doctor, in a tone of most pathetic grief. ‘I thought, at one time,’ said Mr. Wickfield, ‘that you wished to send Maldon abroad to effect a desirable separation.’\n\n‘No, no, no!’ returned the Doctor. ‘To give Annie pleasure, by making some provision for the companion of her childhood. Nothing else.’\n\n‘So I found,’ said Mr. Wickfield. ‘I couldn’t doubt it, when you told me so. But I thought--I implore you to remember the narrow construction which has been my besetting sin--that, in a case where there was so much disparity in point of years--’\n\n‘That’s the way to put it, you see, Master Copperfield!’ observed Uriah, with fawning and offensive pity.\n\n‘--a lady of such youth, and such attractions, however real her respect for you, might have been influenced in marrying, by worldly considerations only. I make no allowance for innumerable feelings and circumstances that may have all tended to good. For Heaven’s sake remember that!’\n\n‘How kind he puts it!’ said Uriah, shaking his head.\n\n‘Always observing her from one point of view,’ said Mr. Wickfield; ‘but by all that is dear to you, my old friend, I entreat you to consider what it was; I am forced to confess now, having no escape-’\n\n‘No! There’s no way out of it, Mr. Wickfield, sir,’ observed Uriah, ‘when it’s got to this.’\n\n‘--that I did,’ said Mr. Wickfield, glancing helplessly and distractedly at his partner, ‘that I did doubt her, and think her wanting in her duty to you; and that I did sometimes, if I must say all, feel averse to Agnes being in such a familiar relation towards her, as to see what I saw, or in my diseased theory fancied that I saw. I never mentioned this to anyone. I never meant it to be known to anyone. And though it is terrible to you to hear,’ said Mr. Wickfield, quite subdued, ‘if you knew how terrible it is for me to tell, you would feel compassion for me!’\n\nThe Doctor, in the perfect goodness of his nature, put out his hand. Mr. Wickfield held it for a little while in his, with his head bowed down.\n\n‘I am sure,’ said Uriah, writhing himself into the silence like a Conger-eel, ‘that this is a subject full of unpleasantness to everybody. But since we have got so far, I ought to take the liberty of mentioning that Copperfield has noticed it too.’\n\nI turned upon him, and asked him how he dared refer to me!\n\n‘Oh! it’s very kind of you, Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, undulating all over, ‘and we all know what an amiable character yours is; but you know that the moment I spoke to you the other night, you knew what I meant. You know you knew what I meant, Copperfield. Don’t deny it! You deny it with the best intentions; but don’t do it, Copperfield.’\n\nI saw the mild eye of the good old Doctor turned upon me for a moment, and I felt that the confession of my old misgivings and remembrances was too plainly written in my face to be overlooked. It was of no use raging. I could not undo that. Say what I would, I could not unsay it.\n\nWe were silent again, and remained so, until the Doctor rose and walked twice or thrice across the room. Presently he returned to where his chair stood; and, leaning on the back of it, and occasionally putting his handkerchief to his eyes, with a simple honesty that did him more honour, to my thinking, than any disguise he could have effected, said:\n\n‘I have been much to blame. I believe I have been very much to blame. I have exposed one whom I hold in my heart, to trials and aspersions--I call them aspersions, even to have been conceived in anybody’s inmost mind--of which she never, but for me, could have been the object.’\n\nUriah Heep gave a kind of snivel. I think to express sympathy.\n\n‘Of which my Annie,’ said the Doctor, ‘never, but for me, could have been the object. Gentlemen, I am old now, as you know; I do not feel, tonight, that I have much to live for. But my life--my Life--upon the truth and honour of the dear lady who has been the subject of this conversation!’\n\nI do not think that the best embodiment of chivalry, the realization of the handsomest and most romantic figure ever imagined by painter, could have said this, with a more impressive and affecting dignity than the plain old Doctor did.\n\n‘But I am not prepared,’ he went on, ‘to deny--perhaps I may have been, without knowing it, in some degree prepared to admit--that I may have unwittingly ensnared that lady into an unhappy marriage. I am a man quite unaccustomed to observe; and I cannot but believe that the observation of several people, of different ages and positions, all too plainly tending in one direction (and that so natural), is better than mine.’\n\nI had often admired, as I have elsewhere described, his benignant manner towards his youthful wife; but the respectful tenderness he manifested in every reference to her on this occasion, and the almost reverential manner in which he put away from him the lightest doubt of her integrity, exalted him, in my eyes, beyond description.\n\n‘I married that lady,’ said the Doctor, ‘when she was extremely young. I took her to myself when her character was scarcely formed. So far as it was developed, it had been my happiness to form it. I knew her father well. I knew her well. I had taught her what I could, for the love of all her beautiful and virtuous qualities. If I did her wrong; as I fear I did, in taking advantage (but I never meant it) of her gratitude and her affection; I ask pardon of that lady, in my heart!’\n\nHe walked across the room, and came back to the same place; holding the chair with a grasp that trembled, like his subdued voice, in its earnestness.\n\n‘I regarded myself as a refuge, for her, from the dangers and vicissitudes of life. I persuaded myself that, unequal though we were in years, she would live tranquilly and contentedly with me. I did not shut out of my consideration the time when I should leave her free, and still young and still beautiful, but with her judgement more matured--no, gentlemen--upon my truth!’\n\nHis homely figure seemed to be lightened up by his fidelity and generosity. Every word he uttered had a force that no other grace could have imparted to it.\n\n‘My life with this lady has been very happy. Until tonight, I have had uninterrupted occasion to bless the day on which I did her great injustice.’\n\nHis voice, more and more faltering in the utterance of these words, stopped for a few moments; then he went on:\n\n‘Once awakened from my dream--I have been a poor dreamer, in one way or other, all my life--I see how natural it is that she should have some regretful feeling towards her old companion and her equal. That she does regard him with some innocent regret, with some blameless thoughts of what might have been, but for me, is, I fear, too true. Much that I have seen, but not noted, has come back upon me with new meaning, during this last trying hour. But, beyond this, gentlemen, the dear lady’s name never must be coupled with a word, a breath, of doubt.’\n\nFor a little while, his eye kindled and his voice was firm; for a little while he was again silent. Presently, he proceeded as before:\n\n‘It only remains for me, to bear the knowledge of the unhappiness I have occasioned, as submissively as I can. It is she who should reproach; not I. To save her from misconstruction, cruel misconstruction, that even my friends have not been able to avoid, becomes my duty. The more retired we live, the better I shall discharge it. And when the time comes--may it come soon, if it be His merciful pleasure!--when my death shall release her from constraint, I shall close my eyes upon her honoured face, with unbounded confidence and love; and leave her, with no sorrow then, to happier and brighter days.’\n\nI could not see him for the tears which his earnestness and goodness, so adorned by, and so adorning, the perfect simplicity of his manner, brought into my eyes. He had moved to the door, when he added:\n\n‘Gentlemen, I have shown you my heart. I am sure you will respect it. What we have said tonight is never to be said more. Wickfield, give me an old friend’s arm upstairs!’\n\nMr. Wickfield hastened to him. Without interchanging a word they went slowly out of the room together, Uriah looking after them.\n\n‘Well, Master Copperfield!’ said Uriah, meekly turning to me. ‘The thing hasn’t took quite the turn that might have been expected, for the old Scholar--what an excellent man!--is as blind as a brickbat; but this family’s out of the cart, I think!’\n\nI needed but the sound of his voice to be so madly enraged as I never was before, and never have been since.\n\n‘You villain,’ said I, ‘what do you mean by entrapping me into your schemes? How dare you appeal to me just now, you false rascal, as if we had been in discussion together?’\n\nAs we stood, front to front, I saw so plainly, in the stealthy exultation of his face, what I already so plainly knew; I mean that he forced his confidence upon me, expressly to make me miserable, and had set a deliberate trap for me in this very matter; that I couldn’t bear it. The whole of his lank cheek was invitingly before me, and I struck it with my open hand with that force that my fingers tingled as if I had burnt them.\n\nHe caught the hand in his, and we stood in that connexion, looking at each other. We stood so, a long time; long enough for me to see the white marks of my fingers die out of the deep red of his cheek, and leave it a deeper red.\n\n‘Copperfield,’ he said at length, in a breathless voice, ‘have you taken leave of your senses?’\n\n‘I have taken leave of you,’ said I, wresting my hand away. ‘You dog, I’ll know no more of you.’\n\n‘Won’t you?’ said he, constrained by the pain of his cheek to put his hand there. ‘Perhaps you won’t be able to help it. Isn’t this ungrateful of you, now?’\n\n‘I have shown you often enough,’ said I, ‘that I despise you. I have shown you now, more plainly, that I do. Why should I dread your doing your worst to all about you? What else do you ever do?’\n\nHe perfectly understood this allusion to the considerations that had hitherto restrained me in my communications with him. I rather think that neither the blow, nor the allusion, would have escaped me, but for the assurance I had had from Agnes that night. It is no matter.\n\nThere was another long pause. His eyes, as he looked at me, seemed to take every shade of colour that could make eyes ugly.\n\n‘Copperfield,’ he said, removing his hand from his cheek, ‘you have always gone against me. I know you always used to be against me at Mr. Wickfield’s.’\n\n‘You may think what you like,’ said I, still in a towering rage. ‘If it is not true, so much the worthier you.’\n\n‘And yet I always liked you, Copperfield!’ he rejoined.\n\nI deigned to make him no reply; and, taking up my hat, was going out to bed, when he came between me and the door.\n\n‘Copperfield,’ he said, ‘there must be two parties to a quarrel. I won’t be one.’\n\n‘You may go to the devil!’ said I.\n\n‘Don’t say that!’ he replied. ‘I know you’ll be sorry afterwards. How can you make yourself so inferior to me, as to show such a bad spirit? But I forgive you.’\n\n‘You forgive me!’ I repeated disdainfully.\n\n‘I do, and you can’t help yourself,’ replied Uriah. ‘To think of your going and attacking me, that have always been a friend to you! But there can’t be a quarrel without two parties, and I won’t be one. I will be a friend to you, in spite of you. So now you know what you’ve got to expect.’\n\nThe necessity of carrying on this dialogue (his part in which was very slow; mine very quick) in a low tone, that the house might not be disturbed at an unseasonable hour, did not improve my temper; though my passion was cooling down. Merely telling him that I should expect from him what I always had expected, and had never yet been disappointed in, I opened the door upon him, as if he had been a great walnut put there to be cracked, and went out of the house. But he slept out of the house too, at his mother’s lodging; and before I had gone many hundred yards, came up with me.\n\n‘You know, Copperfield,’ he said, in my ear (I did not turn my head), ‘you’re in quite a wrong position’; which I felt to be true, and that made me chafe the more; ‘you can’t make this a brave thing, and you can’t help being forgiven. I don’t intend to mention it to mother, nor to any living soul. I’m determined to forgive you. But I do wonder that you should lift your hand against a person that you knew to be so umble!’\n\nI felt only less mean than he. He knew me better than I knew myself. If he had retorted or openly exasperated me, it would have been a relief and a justification; but he had put me on a slow fire, on which I lay tormented half the night.\n\nIn the morning, when I came out, the early church-bell was ringing, and he was walking up and down with his mother. He addressed me as if nothing had happened, and I could do no less than reply. I had struck him hard enough to give him the toothache, I suppose. At all events his face was tied up in a black silk handkerchief, which, with his hat perched on the top of it, was far from improving his appearance. I heard that he went to a dentist’s in London on the Monday morning, and had a tooth out. I hope it was a double one.\n\nThe Doctor gave out that he was not quite well; and remained alone, for a considerable part of every day, during the remainder of the visit. Agnes and her father had been gone a week, before we resumed our usual work. On the day preceding its resumption, the Doctor gave me with his own hands a folded note not sealed. It was addressed to myself; and laid an injunction on me, in a few affectionate words, never to refer to the subject of that evening. I had confided it to my aunt, but to no one else. It was not a subject I could discuss with Agnes, and Agnes certainly had not the least suspicion of what had passed.\n\nNeither, I felt convinced, had Mrs. Strong then. Several weeks elapsed before I saw the least change in her. It came on slowly, like a cloud when there is no wind. At first, she seemed to wonder at the gentle compassion with which the Doctor spoke to her, and at his wish that she should have her mother with her, to relieve the dull monotony of her life. Often, when we were at work, and she was sitting by, I would see her pausing and looking at him with that memorable face. Afterwards, I sometimes observed her rise, with her eyes full of tears, and go out of the room. Gradually, an unhappy shadow fell upon her beauty, and deepened every day. Mrs. Markleham was a regular inmate of the cottage then; but she talked and talked, and saw nothing.\n\nAs this change stole on Annie, once like sunshine in the Doctor’s house, the Doctor became older in appearance, and more grave; but the sweetness of his temper, the placid kindness of his manner, and his benevolent solicitude for her, if they were capable of any increase, were increased. I saw him once, early on the morning of her birthday, when she came to sit in the window while we were at work (which she had always done, but now began to do with a timid and uncertain air that I thought very touching), take her forehead between his hands, kiss it, and go hurriedly away, too much moved to remain. I saw her stand where he had left her, like a statue; and then bend down her head, and clasp her hands, and weep, I cannot say how sorrowfully.\n\nSometimes, after that, I fancied that she tried to speak even to me, in intervals when we were left alone. But she never uttered a word. The Doctor always had some new project for her participating in amusements away from home, with her mother; and Mrs. Markleham, who was very fond of amusements, and very easily dissatisfied with anything else, entered into them with great good-will, and was loud in her commendations. But Annie, in a spiritless unhappy way, only went whither she was led, and seemed to have no care for anything.\n\nI did not know what to think. Neither did my aunt; who must have walked, at various times, a hundred miles in her uncertainty. What was strangest of all was, that the only real relief which seemed to make its way into the secret region of this domestic unhappiness, made its way there in the person of Mr. Dick.\n\nWhat his thoughts were on the subject, or what his observation was, I am as unable to explain, as I dare say he would have been to assist me in the task. But, as I have recorded in the narrative of my school days, his veneration for the Doctor was unbounded; and there is a subtlety of perception in real attachment, even when it is borne towards man by one of the lower animals, which leaves the highest intellect behind. To this mind of the heart, if I may call it so, in Mr. Dick, some bright ray of the truth shot straight.\n\nHe had proudly resumed his privilege, in many of his spare hours, of walking up and down the garden with the Doctor; as he had been accustomed to pace up and down The Doctor’s Walk at Canterbury. But matters were no sooner in this state, than he devoted all his spare time (and got up earlier to make it more) to these perambulations. If he had never been so happy as when the Doctor read that marvellous performance, the Dictionary, to him; he was now quite miserable unless the Doctor pulled it out of his pocket, and began. When the Doctor and I were engaged, he now fell into the custom of walking up and down with Mrs. Strong, and helping her to trim her favourite flowers, or weed the beds. I dare say he rarely spoke a dozen words in an hour: but his quiet interest, and his wistful face, found immediate response in both their breasts; each knew that the other liked him, and that he loved both; and he became what no one else could be--a link between them.\n\nWhen I think of him, with his impenetrably wise face, walking up and down with the Doctor, delighted to be battered by the hard words in the Dictionary; when I think of him carrying huge watering-pots after Annie; kneeling down, in very paws of gloves, at patient microscopic work among the little leaves; expressing as no philosopher could have expressed, in everything he did, a delicate desire to be her friend; showering sympathy, trustfulness, and affection, out of every hole in the watering-pot; when I think of him never wandering in that better mind of his to which unhappiness addressed itself, never bringing the unfortunate King Charles into the garden, never wavering in his grateful service, never diverted from his knowledge that there was something wrong, or from his wish to set it right--I really feel almost ashamed of having known that he was not quite in his wits, taking account of the utmost I have done with mine.\n\n‘Nobody but myself, Trot, knows what that man is!’ my aunt would proudly remark, when we conversed about it. ‘Dick will distinguish himself yet!’\n\nI must refer to one other topic before I close this chapter. While the visit at the Doctor’s was still in progress, I observed that the postman brought two or three letters every morning for Uriah Heep, who remained at Highgate until the rest went back, it being a leisure time; and that these were always directed in a business-like manner by Mr. Micawber, who now assumed a round legal hand. I was glad to infer, from these slight premises, that Mr. Micawber was doing well; and consequently was much surprised to receive, about this time, the following letter from his amiable wife.\n\n‘CANTERBURY, Monday Evening.\n\n‘You will doubtless be surprised, my dear Mr. Copperfield, to receive this communication. Still more so, by its contents. Still more so, by the stipulation of implicit confidence which I beg to impose. But my feelings as a wife and mother require relief; and as I do not wish to consult my family (already obnoxious to the feelings of Mr. Micawber), I know no one of whom I can better ask advice than my friend and former lodger.\n\n‘You may be aware, my dear Mr. Copperfield, that between myself and Mr. Micawber (whom I will never desert), there has always been preserved a spirit of mutual confidence. Mr. Micawber may have occasionally given a bill without consulting me, or he may have misled me as to the period when that obligation would become due. This has actually happened. But, in general, Mr. Micawber has had no secrets from the bosom of affection--I allude to his wife--and has invariably, on our retirement to rest, recalled the events of the day.\n\n‘You will picture to yourself, my dear Mr. Copperfield, what the poignancy of my feelings must be, when I inform you that Mr. Micawber is entirely changed. He is reserved. He is secret. His life is a mystery to the partner of his joys and sorrows--I again allude to his wife--and if I should assure you that beyond knowing that it is passed from morning to night at the office, I now know less of it than I do of the man in the south, connected with whose mouth the thoughtless children repeat an idle tale respecting cold plum porridge, I should adopt a popular fallacy to express an actual fact.\n\n‘But this is not all. Mr. Micawber is morose. He is severe. He is estranged from our eldest son and daughter, he has no pride in his twins, he looks with an eye of coldness even on the unoffending stranger who last became a member of our circle. The pecuniary means of meeting our expenses, kept down to the utmost farthing, are obtained from him with great difficulty, and even under fearful threats that he will Settle himself (the exact expression); and he inexorably refuses to give any explanation whatever of this distracting policy.\n\n‘This is hard to bear. This is heart-breaking. If you will advise me, knowing my feeble powers such as they are, how you think it will be best to exert them in a dilemma so unwonted, you will add another friendly obligation to the many you have already rendered me. With loves from the children, and a smile from the happily-unconscious stranger, I remain, dear Mr. Copperfield,\n\n‘Your afflicted, ‘EMMA MICAWBER.’\n\nI did not feel justified in giving a wife of Mrs. Micawber’s experience any other recommendation, than that she should try to reclaim Mr. Micawber by patience and kindness (as I knew she would in any case); but the letter set me thinking about him very much.\n\n# CHAPTER 43. ANOTHER RETROSPECT\n\nOnce again, let me pause upon a memorable period of my life. Let me stand aside, to see the phantoms of those days go by me, accompanying the shadow of myself, in dim procession.\n\nWeeks, months, seasons, pass along. They seem little more than a summer day and a winter evening. Now, the Common where I walk with Dora is all in bloom, a field of bright gold; and now the unseen heather lies in mounds and bunches underneath a covering of snow. In a breath, the river that flows through our Sunday walks is sparkling in the summer sun, is ruffled by the winter wind, or thickened with drifting heaps of ice. Faster than ever river ran towards the sea, it flashes, darkens, and rolls away.\n\nNot a thread changes, in the house of the two little bird-like ladies. The clock ticks over the fireplace, the weather-glass hangs in the hall. Neither clock nor weather-glass is ever right; but we believe in both, devoutly.\n\nI have come legally to man’s estate. I have attained the dignity of twenty-one. But this is a sort of dignity that may be thrust upon one. Let me think what I have achieved.\n\nI have tamed that savage stenographic mystery. I make a respectable income by it. I am in high repute for my accomplishment in all pertaining to the art, and am joined with eleven others in reporting the debates in Parliament for a Morning Newspaper. Night after night, I record predictions that never come to pass, professions that are never fulfilled, explanations that are only meant to mystify. I wallow in words. Britannia, that unfortunate female, is always before me, like a trussed fowl: skewered through and through with office-pens, and bound hand and foot with red tape. I am sufficiently behind the scenes to know the worth of political life. I am quite an Infidel about it, and shall never be converted.\n\nMy dear old Traddles has tried his hand at the same pursuit, but it is not in Traddles’s way. He is perfectly good-humoured respecting his failure, and reminds me that he always did consider himself slow. He has occasional employment on the same newspaper, in getting up the facts of dry subjects, to be written about and embellished by more fertile minds. He is called to the bar; and with admirable industry and self-denial has scraped another hundred pounds together, to fee a Conveyancer whose chambers he attends. A great deal of very hot port wine was consumed at his call; and, considering the figure, I should think the Inner Temple must have made a profit by it.\n\nI have come out in another way. I have taken with fear and trembling to authorship. I wrote a little something, in secret, and sent it to a magazine, and it was published in the magazine. Since then, I have taken heart to write a good many trifling pieces. Now, I am regularly paid for them. Altogether, I am well off, when I tell my income on the fingers of my left hand, I pass the third finger and take in the fourth to the middle joint.\n\nWe have removed, from Buckingham Street, to a pleasant little cottage very near the one I looked at, when my enthusiasm first came on. My aunt, however (who has sold the house at Dover, to good advantage), is not going to remain here, but intends removing herself to a still more tiny cottage close at hand. What does this portend? My marriage? Yes!\n\nYes! I am going to be married to Dora! Miss Lavinia and Miss Clarissa have given their consent; and if ever canary birds were in a flutter, they are. Miss Lavinia, self-charged with the superintendence of my darling’s wardrobe, is constantly cutting out brown-paper cuirasses, and differing in opinion from a highly respectable young man, with a long bundle, and a yard measure under his arm. A dressmaker, always stabbed in the breast with a needle and thread, boards and lodges in the house; and seems to me, eating, drinking, or sleeping, never to take her thimble off. They make a lay-figure of my dear. They are always sending for her to come and try something on. We can’t be happy together for five minutes in the evening, but some intrusive female knocks at the door, and says, ‘Oh, if you please, Miss Dora, would you step upstairs!’\n\nMiss Clarissa and my aunt roam all over London, to find out articles of furniture for Dora and me to look at. It would be better for them to buy the goods at once, without this ceremony of inspection; for, when we go to see a kitchen fender and meat-screen, Dora sees a Chinese house for Jip, with little bells on the top, and prefers that. And it takes a long time to accustom Jip to his new residence, after we have bought it; whenever he goes in or out, he makes all the little bells ring, and is horribly frightened.\n\nPeggotty comes up to make herself useful, and falls to work immediately. Her department appears to be, to clean everything over and over again. She rubs everything that can be rubbed, until it shines, like her own honest forehead, with perpetual friction. And now it is, that I begin to see her solitary brother passing through the dark streets at night, and looking, as he goes, among the wandering faces. I never speak to him at such an hour. I know too well, as his grave figure passes onward, what he seeks, and what he dreads.\n\nWhy does Traddles look so important when he calls upon me this afternoon in the Commons--where I still occasionally attend, for form’s sake, when I have time? The realization of my boyish day-dreams is at hand. I am going to take out the licence.\n\nIt is a little document to do so much; and Traddles contemplates it, as it lies upon my desk, half in admiration, half in awe. There are the names, in the sweet old visionary connexion, David Copperfield and Dora Spenlow; and there, in the corner, is that Parental Institution, the Stamp Office, which is so benignantly interested in the various transactions of human life, looking down upon our Union; and there is the Archbishop of Canterbury invoking a blessing on us in print, and doing it as cheap as could possibly be expected.\n\nNevertheless, I am in a dream, a flustered, happy, hurried dream. I can’t believe that it is going to be; and yet I can’t believe but that everyone I pass in the street, must have some kind of perception, that I am to be married the day after tomorrow. The Surrogate knows me, when I go down to be sworn; and disposes of me easily, as if there were a Masonic understanding between us. Traddles is not at all wanted, but is in attendance as my general backer.\n\n‘I hope the next time you come here, my dear fellow,’ I say to Traddles, ‘it will be on the same errand for yourself. And I hope it will be soon.’\n\n‘Thank you for your good wishes, my dear Copperfield,’ he replies. ‘I hope so too. It’s a satisfaction to know that she’ll wait for me any length of time, and that she really is the dearest girl--’\n\n‘When are you to meet her at the coach?’ I ask.\n\n‘At seven,’ says Traddles, looking at his plain old silver watch--the very watch he once took a wheel out of, at school, to make a water-mill. ‘That is about Miss Wickfield’s time, is it not?’\n\n‘A little earlier. Her time is half past eight.’ ‘I assure you, my dear boy,’ says Traddles, ‘I am almost as pleased as if I were going to be married myself, to think that this event is coming to such a happy termination. And really the great friendship and consideration of personally associating Sophy with the joyful occasion, and inviting her to be a bridesmaid in conjunction with Miss Wickfield, demands my warmest thanks. I am extremely sensible of it.’\n\nI hear him, and shake hands with him; and we talk, and walk, and dine, and so on; but I don’t believe it. Nothing is real.\n\nSophy arrives at the house of Dora’s aunts, in due course. She has the most agreeable of faces,--not absolutely beautiful, but extraordinarily pleasant,--and is one of the most genial, unaffected, frank, engaging creatures I have ever seen. Traddles presents her to us with great pride; and rubs his hands for ten minutes by the clock, with every individual hair upon his head standing on tiptoe, when I congratulate him in a corner on his choice.\n\nI have brought Agnes from the Canterbury coach, and her cheerful and beautiful face is among us for the second time. Agnes has a great liking for Traddles, and it is capital to see them meet, and to observe the glory of Traddles as he commends the dearest girl in the world to her acquaintance.\n\nStill I don’t believe it. We have a delightful evening, and are supremely happy; but I don’t believe it yet. I can’t collect myself. I can’t check off my happiness as it takes place. I feel in a misty and unsettled kind of state; as if I had got up very early in the morning a week or two ago, and had never been to bed since. I can’t make out when yesterday was. I seem to have been carrying the licence about, in my pocket, many months.\n\nNext day, too, when we all go in a flock to see the house--our house--Dora’s and mine--I am quite unable to regard myself as its master. I seem to be there, by permission of somebody else. I half expect the real master to come home presently, and say he is glad to see me. Such a beautiful little house as it is, with everything so bright and new; with the flowers on the carpets looking as if freshly gathered, and the green leaves on the paper as if they had just come out; with the spotless muslin curtains, and the blushing rose-coloured furniture, and Dora’s garden hat with the blue ribbon--do I remember, now, how I loved her in such another hat when I first knew her!--already hanging on its little peg; the guitar-case quite at home on its heels in a corner; and everybody tumbling over Jip’s pagoda, which is much too big for the establishment. Another happy evening, quite as unreal as all the rest of it, and I steal into the usual room before going away. Dora is not there. I suppose they have not done trying on yet. Miss Lavinia peeps in, and tells me mysteriously that she will not be long. She is rather long, notwithstanding; but by and by I hear a rustling at the door, and someone taps.\n\nI say, ‘Come in!’ but someone taps again.\n\nI go to the door, wondering who it is; there, I meet a pair of bright eyes, and a blushing face; they are Dora’s eyes and face, and Miss Lavinia has dressed her in tomorrow’s dress, bonnet and all, for me to see. I take my little wife to my heart; and Miss Lavinia gives a little scream because I tumble the bonnet, and Dora laughs and cries at once, because I am so pleased; and I believe it less than ever.\n\n‘Do you think it pretty, Doady?’ says Dora.\n\nPretty! I should rather think I did.\n\n‘And are you sure you like me very much?’ says Dora.\n\nThe topic is fraught with such danger to the bonnet, that Miss Lavinia gives another little scream, and begs me to understand that Dora is only to be looked at, and on no account to be touched. So Dora stands in a delightful state of confusion for a minute or two, to be admired; and then takes off her bonnet--looking so natural without it!--and runs away with it in her hand; and comes dancing down again in her own familiar dress, and asks Jip if I have got a beautiful little wife, and whether he’ll forgive her for being married, and kneels down to make him stand upon the cookery-book, for the last time in her single life.\n\nI go home, more incredulous than ever, to a lodging that I have hard by; and get up very early in the morning, to ride to the Highgate road and fetch my aunt.\n\nI have never seen my aunt in such state. She is dressed in lavender-coloured silk, and has a white bonnet on, and is amazing. Janet has dressed her, and is there to look at me. Peggotty is ready to go to church, intending to behold the ceremony from the gallery. Mr. Dick, who is to give my darling to me at the altar, has had his hair curled. Traddles, whom I have taken up by appointment at the turnpike, presents a dazzling combination of cream colour and light blue; and both he and Mr. Dick have a general effect about them of being all gloves.\n\nNo doubt I see this, because I know it is so; but I am astray, and seem to see nothing. Nor do I believe anything whatever. Still, as we drive along in an open carriage, this fairy marriage is real enough to fill me with a sort of wondering pity for the unfortunate people who have no part in it, but are sweeping out the shops, and going to their daily occupations.\n\nMy aunt sits with my hand in hers all the way. When we stop a little way short of the church, to put down Peggotty, whom we have brought on the box, she gives it a squeeze, and me a kiss.\n\n‘God bless you, Trot! My own boy never could be dearer. I think of poor dear Baby this morning.’ ‘So do I. And of all I owe to you, dear aunt.’\n\n‘Tut, child!’ says my aunt; and gives her hand in overflowing cordiality to Traddles, who then gives his to Mr. Dick, who then gives his to me, who then gives mine to Traddles, and then we come to the church door.\n\nThe church is calm enough, I am sure; but it might be a steam-power loom in full action, for any sedative effect it has on me. I am too far gone for that.\n\nThe rest is all a more or less incoherent dream.\n\nA dream of their coming in with Dora; of the pew-opener arranging us, like a drill-sergeant, before the altar rails; of my wondering, even then, why pew-openers must always be the most disagreeable females procurable, and whether there is any religious dread of a disastrous infection of good-humour which renders it indispensable to set those vessels of vinegar upon the road to Heaven.\n\nOf the clergyman and clerk appearing; of a few boatmen and some other people strolling in; of an ancient mariner behind me, strongly flavouring the church with rum; of the service beginning in a deep voice, and our all being very attentive.\n\nOf Miss Lavinia, who acts as a semi-auxiliary bridesmaid, being the first to cry, and of her doing homage (as I take it) to the memory of Pidger, in sobs; of Miss Clarissa applying a smelling-bottle; of Agnes taking care of Dora; of my aunt endeavouring to represent herself as a model of sternness, with tears rolling down her face; of little Dora trembling very much, and making her responses in faint whispers.\n\nOf our kneeling down together, side by side; of Dora’s trembling less and less, but always clasping Agnes by the hand; of the service being got through, quietly and gravely; of our all looking at each other in an April state of smiles and tears, when it is over; of my young wife being hysterical in the vestry, and crying for her poor papa, her dear papa.\n\nOf her soon cheering up again, and our signing the register all round. Of my going into the gallery for Peggotty to bring her to sign it; of Peggotty’s hugging me in a corner, and telling me she saw my own dear mother married; of its being over, and our going away.\n\nOf my walking so proudly and lovingly down the aisle with my sweet wife upon my arm, through a mist of half-seen people, pulpits, monuments, pews, fonts, organs, and church windows, in which there flutter faint airs of association with my childish church at home, so long ago.\n\nOf their whispering, as we pass, what a youthful couple we are, and what a pretty little wife she is. Of our all being so merry and talkative in the carriage going back. Of Sophy telling us that when she saw Traddles (whom I had entrusted with the licence) asked for it, she almost fainted, having been convinced that he would contrive to lose it, or to have his pocket picked. Of Agnes laughing gaily; and of Dora being so fond of Agnes that she will not be separated from her, but still keeps her hand.\n\nOf there being a breakfast, with abundance of things, pretty and substantial, to eat and drink, whereof I partake, as I should do in any other dream, without the least perception of their flavour; eating and drinking, as I may say, nothing but love and marriage, and no more believing in the viands than in anything else.\n\nOf my making a speech in the same dreamy fashion, without having an idea of what I want to say, beyond such as may be comprehended in the full conviction that I haven’t said it. Of our being very sociably and simply happy (always in a dream though); and of Jip’s having wedding cake, and its not agreeing with him afterwards.\n\nOf the pair of hired post-horses being ready, and of Dora’s going away to change her dress. Of my aunt and Miss Clarissa remaining with us; and our walking in the garden; and my aunt, who has made quite a speech at breakfast touching Dora’s aunts, being mightily amused with herself, but a little proud of it too.\n\nOf Dora’s being ready, and of Miss Lavinia’s hovering about her, loth to lose the pretty toy that has given her so much pleasant occupation. Of Dora’s making a long series of surprised discoveries that she has forgotten all sorts of little things; and of everybody’s running everywhere to fetch them.\n\nOf their all closing about Dora, when at last she begins to say good-bye, looking, with their bright colours and ribbons, like a bed of flowers. Of my darling being almost smothered among the flowers, and coming out, laughing and crying both together, to my jealous arms.\n\nOf my wanting to carry Jip (who is to go along with us), and Dora’s saying no, that she must carry him, or else he’ll think she don’t like him any more, now she is married, and will break his heart. Of our going, arm in arm, and Dora stopping and looking back, and saying, ‘If I have ever been cross or ungrateful to anybody, don’t remember it!’ and bursting into tears.\n\nOf her waving her little hand, and our going away once more. Of her once more stopping, and looking back, and hurrying to Agnes, and giving Agnes, above all the others, her last kisses and farewells.\n\nWe drive away together, and I awake from the dream. I believe it at last. It is my dear, dear, little wife beside me, whom I love so well!\n\n‘Are you happy now, you foolish boy?’ says Dora, ‘and sure you don’t repent?’\n\nI have stood aside to see the phantoms of those days go by me. They are gone, and I resume the journey of my story.\n\n# CHAPTER 44. OUR HOUSEKEEPING\n\nIt was a strange condition of things, the honeymoon being over, and the bridesmaids gone home, when I found myself sitting down in my own small house with Dora; quite thrown out of employment, as I may say, in respect of the delicious old occupation of making love.\n\nIt seemed such an extraordinary thing to have Dora always there. It was so unaccountable not to be obliged to go out to see her, not to have any occasion to be tormenting myself about her, not to have to write to her, not to be scheming and devising opportunities of being alone with her. Sometimes of an evening, when I looked up from my writing, and saw her seated opposite, I would lean back in my chair, and think how queer it was that there we were, alone together as a matter of course--nobody’s business any more--all the romance of our engagement put away upon a shelf, to rust--no one to please but one another--one another to please, for life.\n\nWhen there was a debate, and I was kept out very late, it seemed so strange to me, as I was walking home, to think that Dora was at home! It was such a wonderful thing, at first, to have her coming softly down to talk to me as I ate my supper. It was such a stupendous thing to know for certain that she put her hair in papers. It was altogether such an astonishing event to see her do it!\n\nI doubt whether two young birds could have known less about keeping house, than I and my pretty Dora did. We had a servant, of course. She kept house for us. I have still a latent belief that she must have been Mrs. Crupp’s daughter in disguise, we had such an awful time of it with Mary Anne.\n\nHer name was Paragon. Her nature was represented to us, when we engaged her, as being feebly expressed in her name. She had a written character, as large as a proclamation; and, according to this document, could do everything of a domestic nature that ever I heard of, and a great many things that I never did hear of. She was a woman in the prime of life; of a severe countenance; and subject (particularly in the arms) to a sort of perpetual measles or fiery rash. She had a cousin in the Life-Guards, with such long legs that he looked like the afternoon shadow of somebody else. His shell-jacket was as much too little for him as he was too big for the premises. He made the cottage smaller than it need have been, by being so very much out of proportion to it. Besides which, the walls were not thick, and, whenever he passed the evening at our house, we always knew of it by hearing one continual growl in the kitchen.\n\nOur treasure was warranted sober and honest. I am therefore willing to believe that she was in a fit when we found her under the boiler; and that the deficient tea-spoons were attributable to the dustman.\n\nBut she preyed upon our minds dreadfully. We felt our inexperience, and were unable to help ourselves. We should have been at her mercy, if she had had any; but she was a remorseless woman, and had none. She was the cause of our first little quarrel.\n\n‘My dearest life,’ I said one day to Dora, ‘do you think Mary Anne has any idea of time?’\n\n‘Why, Doady?’ inquired Dora, looking up, innocently, from her drawing.\n\n‘My love, because it’s five, and we were to have dined at four.’\n\nDora glanced wistfully at the clock, and hinted that she thought it was too fast.\n\n‘On the contrary, my love,’ said I, referring to my watch, ‘it’s a few minutes too slow.’\n\nMy little wife came and sat upon my knee, to coax me to be quiet, and drew a line with her pencil down the middle of my nose; but I couldn’t dine off that, though it was very agreeable.\n\n‘Don’t you think, my dear,’ said I, ‘it would be better for you to remonstrate with Mary Anne?’\n\n‘Oh no, please! I couldn’t, Doady!’ said Dora.\n\n‘Why not, my love?’ I gently asked.\n\n‘Oh, because I am such a little goose,’ said Dora, ‘and she knows I am!’\n\nI thought this sentiment so incompatible with the establishment of any system of check on Mary Anne, that I frowned a little.\n\n‘Oh, what ugly wrinkles in my bad boy’s forehead!’ said Dora, and still being on my knee, she traced them with her pencil; putting it to her rosy lips to make it mark blacker, and working at my forehead with a quaint little mockery of being industrious, that quite delighted me in spite of myself.\n\n‘There’s a good child,’ said Dora, ‘it makes its face so much prettier to laugh.’ ‘But, my love,’ said I.\n\n‘No, no! please!’ cried Dora, with a kiss, ‘don’t be a naughty Blue Beard! Don’t be serious!’\n\n‘My precious wife,’ said I, ‘we must be serious sometimes. Come! Sit down on this chair, close beside me! Give me the pencil! There! Now let us talk sensibly. You know, dear’; what a little hand it was to hold, and what a tiny wedding-ring it was to see! ‘You know, my love, it is not exactly comfortable to have to go out without one’s dinner. Now, is it?’\n\n‘N-n-no!’ replied Dora, faintly.\n\n‘My love, how you tremble!’\n\n‘Because I KNOW you’re going to scold me,’ exclaimed Dora, in a piteous voice.\n\n‘My sweet, I am only going to reason.’\n\n‘Oh, but reasoning is worse than scolding!’ exclaimed Dora, in despair. ‘I didn’t marry to be reasoned with. If you meant to reason with such a poor little thing as I am, you ought to have told me so, you cruel boy!’\n\nI tried to pacify Dora, but she turned away her face, and shook her curls from side to side, and said, ‘You cruel, cruel boy!’ so many times, that I really did not exactly know what to do: so I took a few turns up and down the room in my uncertainty, and came back again.\n\n‘Dora, my darling!’\n\n‘No, I am not your darling. Because you must be sorry that you married me, or else you wouldn’t reason with me!’ returned Dora.\n\nI felt so injured by the inconsequential nature of this charge, that it gave me courage to be grave.\n\n‘Now, my own Dora,’ said I, ‘you are very childish, and are talking nonsense. You must remember, I am sure, that I was obliged to go out yesterday when dinner was half over; and that, the day before, I was made quite unwell by being obliged to eat underdone veal in a hurry; today, I don’t dine at all--and I am afraid to say how long we waited for breakfast--and then the water didn’t boil. I don’t mean to reproach you, my dear, but this is not comfortable.’\n\n‘Oh, you cruel, cruel boy, to say I am a disagreeable wife!’ cried Dora.\n\n‘Now, my dear Dora, you must know that I never said that!’\n\n‘You said, I wasn’t comfortable!’ cried Dora. ‘I said the housekeeping was not comfortable!’\n\n‘It’s exactly the same thing!’ cried Dora. And she evidently thought so, for she wept most grievously.\n\nI took another turn across the room, full of love for my pretty wife, and distracted by self-accusatory inclinations to knock my head against the door. I sat down again, and said:\n\n‘I am not blaming you, Dora. We have both a great deal to learn. I am only trying to show you, my dear, that you must--you really must’ (I was resolved not to give this up)--‘accustom yourself to look after Mary Anne. Likewise to act a little for yourself, and me.’\n\n‘I wonder, I do, at your making such ungrateful speeches,’ sobbed Dora. ‘When you know that the other day, when you said you would like a little bit of fish, I went out myself, miles and miles, and ordered it, to surprise you.’\n\n‘And it was very kind of you, my own darling,’ said I. ‘I felt it so much that I wouldn’t on any account have even mentioned that you bought a Salmon--which was too much for two. Or that it cost one pound six--which was more than we can afford.’\n\n‘You enjoyed it very much,’ sobbed Dora. ‘And you said I was a Mouse.’\n\n‘And I’ll say so again, my love,’ I returned, ‘a thousand times!’\n\nBut I had wounded Dora’s soft little heart, and she was not to be comforted. She was so pathetic in her sobbing and bewailing, that I felt as if I had said I don’t know what to hurt her. I was obliged to hurry away; I was kept out late; and I felt all night such pangs of remorse as made me miserable. I had the conscience of an assassin, and was haunted by a vague sense of enormous wickedness.\n\nIt was two or three hours past midnight when I got home. I found my aunt, in our house, sitting up for me.\n\n‘Is anything the matter, aunt?’ said I, alarmed.\n\n‘Nothing, Trot,’ she replied. ‘Sit down, sit down. Little Blossom has been rather out of spirits, and I have been keeping her company. That’s all.’\n\nI leaned my head upon my hand; and felt more sorry and downcast, as I sat looking at the fire, than I could have supposed possible so soon after the fulfilment of my brightest hopes. As I sat thinking, I happened to meet my aunt’s eyes, which were resting on my face. There was an anxious expression in them, but it cleared directly.\n\n‘I assure you, aunt,’ said I, ‘I have been quite unhappy myself all night, to think of Dora’s being so. But I had no other intention than to speak to her tenderly and lovingly about our home-affairs.’\n\nMy aunt nodded encouragement.\n\n‘You must have patience, Trot,’ said she.\n\n‘Of course. Heaven knows I don’t mean to be unreasonable, aunt!’\n\n‘No, no,’ said my aunt. ‘But Little Blossom is a very tender little blossom, and the wind must be gentle with her.’\n\nI thanked my good aunt, in my heart, for her tenderness towards my wife; and I was sure that she knew I did.\n\n‘Don’t you think, aunt,’ said I, after some further contemplation of the fire, ‘that you could advise and counsel Dora a little, for our mutual advantage, now and then?’\n\n‘Trot,’ returned my aunt, with some emotion, ‘no! Don’t ask me such a thing.’\n\nHer tone was so very earnest that I raised my eyes in surprise.\n\n‘I look back on my life, child,’ said my aunt, ‘and I think of some who are in their graves, with whom I might have been on kinder terms. If I judged harshly of other people’s mistakes in marriage, it may have been because I had bitter reason to judge harshly of my own. Let that pass. I have been a grumpy, frumpy, wayward sort of a woman, a good many years. I am still, and I always shall be. But you and I have done one another some good, Trot,--at all events, you have done me good, my dear; and division must not come between us, at this time of day.’\n\n‘Division between us!’ cried I.\n\n‘Child, child!’ said my aunt, smoothing her dress, ‘how soon it might come between us, or how unhappy I might make our Little Blossom, if I meddled in anything, a prophet couldn’t say. I want our pet to like me, and be as gay as a butterfly. Remember your own home, in that second marriage; and never do both me and her the injury you have hinted at!’\n\nI comprehended, at once, that my aunt was right; and I comprehended the full extent of her generous feeling towards my dear wife.\n\n‘These are early days, Trot,’ she pursued, ‘and Rome was not built in a day, nor in a year. You have chosen freely for yourself’; a cloud passed over her face for a moment, I thought; ‘and you have chosen a very pretty and a very affectionate creature. It will be your duty, and it will be your pleasure too--of course I know that; I am not delivering a lecture--to estimate her (as you chose her) by the qualities she has, and not by the qualities she may not have. The latter you must develop in her, if you can. And if you cannot, child,’ here my aunt rubbed her nose, ‘you must just accustom yourself to do without ‘em. But remember, my dear, your future is between you two. No one can assist you; you are to work it out for yourselves. This is marriage, Trot; and Heaven bless you both, in it, for a pair of babes in the wood as you are!’\n\nMy aunt said this in a sprightly way, and gave me a kiss to ratify the blessing.\n\n‘Now,’ said she, ‘light my little lantern, and see me into my bandbox by the garden path’; for there was a communication between our cottages in that direction. ‘Give Betsey Trotwood’s love to Blossom, when you come back; and whatever you do, Trot, never dream of setting Betsey up as a scarecrow, for if I ever saw her in the glass, she’s quite grim enough and gaunt enough in her private capacity!’\n\nWith this my aunt tied her head up in a handkerchief, with which she was accustomed to make a bundle of it on such occasions; and I escorted her home. As she stood in her garden, holding up her little lantern to light me back, I thought her observation of me had an anxious air again; but I was too much occupied in pondering on what she had said, and too much impressed--for the first time, in reality--by the conviction that Dora and I had indeed to work out our future for ourselves, and that no one could assist us, to take much notice of it.\n\nDora came stealing down in her little slippers, to meet me, now that I was alone; and cried upon my shoulder, and said I had been hard-hearted and she had been naughty; and I said much the same thing in effect, I believe; and we made it up, and agreed that our first little difference was to be our last, and that we were never to have another if we lived a hundred years.\n\nThe next domestic trial we went through, was the Ordeal of Servants. Mary Anne’s cousin deserted into our coal-hole, and was brought out, to our great amazement, by a piquet of his companions in arms, who took him away handcuffed in a procession that covered our front-garden with ignominy. This nerved me to get rid of Mary Anne, who went so mildly, on receipt of wages, that I was surprised, until I found out about the tea-spoons, and also about the little sums she had borrowed in my name of the tradespeople without authority. After an interval of Mrs. Kidgerbury--the oldest inhabitant of Kentish Town, I believe, who went out charing, but was too feeble to execute her conceptions of that art--we found another treasure, who was one of the most amiable of women, but who generally made a point of falling either up or down the kitchen stairs with the tray, and almost plunged into the parlour, as into a bath, with the tea-things. The ravages committed by this unfortunate, rendering her dismissal necessary, she was succeeded (with intervals of Mrs. Kidgerbury) by a long line of Incapables; terminating in a young person of genteel appearance, who went to Greenwich Fair in Dora’s bonnet. After whom I remember nothing but an average equality of failure.\n\nEverybody we had anything to do with seemed to cheat us. Our appearance in a shop was a signal for the damaged goods to be brought out immediately. If we bought a lobster, it was full of water. All our meat turned out to be tough, and there was hardly any crust to our loaves. In search of the principle on which joints ought to be roasted, to be roasted enough, and not too much, I myself referred to the Cookery Book, and found it there established as the allowance of a quarter of an hour to every pound, and say a quarter over. But the principle always failed us by some curious fatality, and we never could hit any medium between redness and cinders.\n\nI had reason to believe that in accomplishing these failures we incurred a far greater expense than if we had achieved a series of triumphs. It appeared to me, on looking over the tradesmen’s books, as if we might have kept the basement storey paved with butter, such was the extensive scale of our consumption of that article. I don’t know whether the Excise returns of the period may have exhibited any increase in the demand for pepper; but if our performances did not affect the market, I should say several families must have left off using it. And the most wonderful fact of all was, that we never had anything in the house.\n\nAs to the washerwoman pawning the clothes, and coming in a state of penitent intoxication to apologize, I suppose that might have happened several times to anybody. Also the chimney on fire, the parish engine, and perjury on the part of the Beadle. But I apprehend that we were personally fortunate in engaging a servant with a taste for cordials, who swelled our running account for porter at the public-house by such inexplicable items as ‘quartern rum shrub (Mrs. C.)’; ‘Half-quartern gin and cloves (Mrs. C.)’; ‘Glass rum and peppermint (Mrs. C.)’--the parentheses always referring to Dora, who was supposed, it appeared on explanation, to have imbibed the whole of these refreshments.\n\nOne of our first feats in the housekeeping way was a little dinner to Traddles. I met him in town, and asked him to walk out with me that afternoon. He readily consenting, I wrote to Dora, saying I would bring him home. It was pleasant weather, and on the road we made my domestic happiness the theme of conversation. Traddles was very full of it; and said, that, picturing himself with such a home, and Sophy waiting and preparing for him, he could think of nothing wanting to complete his bliss.\n\nI could not have wished for a prettier little wife at the opposite end of the table, but I certainly could have wished, when we sat down, for a little more room. I did not know how it was, but though there were only two of us, we were at once always cramped for room, and yet had always room enough to lose everything in. I suspect it may have been because nothing had a place of its own, except Jip’s pagoda, which invariably blocked up the main thoroughfare. On the present occasion, Traddles was so hemmed in by the pagoda and the guitar-case, and Dora’s flower-painting, and my writing-table, that I had serious doubts of the possibility of his using his knife and fork; but he protested, with his own good-humour, ‘Oceans of room, Copperfield! I assure you, Oceans!’\n\nThere was another thing I could have wished, namely, that Jip had never been encouraged to walk about the tablecloth during dinner. I began to think there was something disorderly in his being there at all, even if he had not been in the habit of putting his foot in the salt or the melted butter. On this occasion he seemed to think he was introduced expressly to keep Traddles at bay; and he barked at my old friend, and made short runs at his plate, with such undaunted pertinacity, that he may be said to have engrossed the conversation.\n\nHowever, as I knew how tender-hearted my dear Dora was, and how sensitive she would be to any slight upon her favourite, I hinted no objection. For similar reasons I made no allusion to the skirmishing plates upon the floor; or to the disreputable appearance of the castors, which were all at sixes and sevens, and looked drunk; or to the further blockade of Traddles by wandering vegetable dishes and jugs. I could not help wondering in my own mind, as I contemplated the boiled leg of mutton before me, previous to carving it, how it came to pass that our joints of meat were of such extraordinary shapes--and whether our butcher contracted for all the deformed sheep that came into the world; but I kept my reflections to myself.\n\n‘My love,’ said I to Dora, ‘what have you got in that dish?’\n\nI could not imagine why Dora had been making tempting little faces at me, as if she wanted to kiss me.\n\n‘Oysters, dear,’ said Dora, timidly.\n\n‘Was that YOUR thought?’ said I, delighted.\n\n‘Ye-yes, Doady,’ said Dora.\n\n‘There never was a happier one!’ I exclaimed, laying down the carving-knife and fork. ‘There is nothing Traddles likes so much!’\n\n‘Ye-yes, Doady,’ said Dora, ‘and so I bought a beautiful little barrel of them, and the man said they were very good. But I--I am afraid there’s something the matter with them. They don’t seem right.’ Here Dora shook her head, and diamonds twinkled in her eyes.\n\n‘They are only opened in both shells,’ said I. ‘Take the top one off, my love.’\n\n‘But it won’t come off!’ said Dora, trying very hard, and looking very much distressed.\n\n‘Do you know, Copperfield,’ said Traddles, cheerfully examining the dish, ‘I think it is in consequence--they are capital oysters, but I think it is in consequence--of their never having been opened.’\n\nThey never had been opened; and we had no oyster-knives--and couldn’t have used them if we had; so we looked at the oysters and ate the mutton. At least we ate as much of it as was done, and made up with capers. If I had permitted him, I am satisfied that Traddles would have made a perfect savage of himself, and eaten a plateful of raw meat, to express enjoyment of the repast; but I would hear of no such immolation on the altar of friendship, and we had a course of bacon instead; there happening, by good fortune, to be cold bacon in the larder.\n\nMy poor little wife was in such affliction when she thought I should be annoyed, and in such a state of joy when she found I was not, that the discomfiture I had subdued, very soon vanished, and we passed a happy evening; Dora sitting with her arm on my chair while Traddles and I discussed a glass of wine, and taking every opportunity of whispering in my ear that it was so good of me not to be a cruel, cross old boy. By and by she made tea for us; which it was so pretty to see her do, as if she was busying herself with a set of doll’s tea-things, that I was not particular about the quality of the beverage. Then Traddles and I played a game or two at cribbage; and Dora singing to the guitar the while, it seemed to me as if our courtship and marriage were a tender dream of mine, and the night when I first listened to her voice were not yet over.\n\nWhen Traddles went away, and I came back into the parlour from seeing him out, my wife planted her chair close to mine, and sat down by my side. ‘I am very sorry,’ she said. ‘Will you try to teach me, Doady?’\n\n‘I must teach myself first, Dora,’ said I. ‘I am as bad as you, love.’\n\n‘Ah! But you can learn,’ she returned; ‘and you are a clever, clever man!’\n\n‘Nonsense, mouse!’ said I.\n\n‘I wish,’ resumed my wife, after a long silence, ‘that I could have gone down into the country for a whole year, and lived with Agnes!’\n\nHer hands were clasped upon my shoulder, and her chin rested on them, and her blue eyes looked quietly into mine.\n\n‘Why so?’ I asked.\n\n‘I think she might have improved me, and I think I might have learned from her,’ said Dora.\n\n‘All in good time, my love. Agnes has had her father to take care of for these many years, you should remember. Even when she was quite a child, she was the Agnes whom we know,’ said I.\n\n‘Will you call me a name I want you to call me?’ inquired Dora, without moving.\n\n‘What is it?’ I asked with a smile.\n\n‘It’s a stupid name,’ she said, shaking her curls for a moment. ‘Child-wife.’\n\nI laughingly asked my child-wife what her fancy was in desiring to be so called. She answered without moving, otherwise than as the arm I twined about her may have brought her blue eyes nearer to me:\n\n‘I don’t mean, you silly fellow, that you should use the name instead of Dora. I only mean that you should think of me that way. When you are going to be angry with me, say to yourself, “it’s only my child-wife!” When I am very disappointing, say, “I knew, a long time ago, that she would make but a child-wife!” When you miss what I should like to be, and I think can never be, say, “still my foolish child-wife loves me!” For indeed I do.’\n\nI had not been serious with her; having no idea until now, that she was serious herself. But her affectionate nature was so happy in what I now said to her with my whole heart, that her face became a laughing one before her glittering eyes were dry. She was soon my child-wife indeed; sitting down on the floor outside the Chinese House, ringing all the little bells one after another, to punish Jip for his recent bad behaviour; while Jip lay blinking in the doorway with his head out, even too lazy to be teased.\n\nThis appeal of Dora’s made a strong impression on me. I look back on the time I write of; I invoke the innocent figure that I dearly loved, to come out from the mists and shadows of the past, and turn its gentle head towards me once again; and I can still declare that this one little speech was constantly in my memory. I may not have used it to the best account; I was young and inexperienced; but I never turned a deaf ear to its artless pleading.\n\nDora told me, shortly afterwards, that she was going to be a wonderful housekeeper. Accordingly, she polished the tablets, pointed the pencil, bought an immense account-book, carefully stitched up with a needle and thread all the leaves of the Cookery Book which Jip had torn, and made quite a desperate little attempt ‘to be good’, as she called it. But the figures had the old obstinate propensity--they WOULD NOT add up. When she had entered two or three laborious items in the account-book, Jip would walk over the page, wagging his tail, and smear them all out. Her own little right-hand middle finger got steeped to the very bone in ink; and I think that was the only decided result obtained.\n\nSometimes, of an evening, when I was at home and at work--for I wrote a good deal now, and was beginning in a small way to be known as a writer--I would lay down my pen, and watch my child-wife trying to be good. First of all, she would bring out the immense account-book, and lay it down upon the table, with a deep sigh. Then she would open it at the place where Jip had made it illegible last night, and call Jip up, to look at his misdeeds. This would occasion a diversion in Jip’s favour, and some inking of his nose, perhaps, as a penalty. Then she would tell Jip to lie down on the table instantly, ‘like a lion’--which was one of his tricks, though I cannot say the likeness was striking--and, if he were in an obedient humour, he would obey. Then she would take up a pen, and begin to write, and find a hair in it. Then she would take up another pen, and begin to write, and find that it spluttered. Then she would take up another pen, and begin to write, and say in a low voice, ‘Oh, it’s a talking pen, and will disturb Doady!’ And then she would give it up as a bad job, and put the account-book away, after pretending to crush the lion with it.\n\nOr, if she were in a very sedate and serious state of mind, she would sit down with the tablets, and a little basket of bills and other documents, which looked more like curl-papers than anything else, and endeavour to get some result out of them. After severely comparing one with another, and making entries on the tablets, and blotting them out, and counting all the fingers of her left hand over and over again, backwards and forwards, she would be so vexed and discouraged, and would look so unhappy, that it gave me pain to see her bright face clouded--and for me!--and I would go softly to her, and say:\n\n‘What’s the matter, Dora?’\n\nDora would look up hopelessly, and reply, ‘They won’t come right. They make my head ache so. And they won’t do anything I want!’\n\nThen I would say, ‘Now let us try together. Let me show you, Dora.’\n\nThen I would commence a practical demonstration, to which Dora would pay profound attention, perhaps for five minutes; when she would begin to be dreadfully tired, and would lighten the subject by curling my hair, or trying the effect of my face with my shirt-collar turned down. If I tacitly checked this playfulness, and persisted, she would look so scared and disconsolate, as she became more and more bewildered, that the remembrance of her natural gaiety when I first strayed into her path, and of her being my child-wife, would come reproachfully upon me; and I would lay the pencil down, and call for the guitar.\n\nI had a great deal of work to do, and had many anxieties, but the same considerations made me keep them to myself. I am far from sure, now, that it was right to do this, but I did it for my child-wife’s sake. I search my breast, and I commit its secrets, if I know them, without any reservation to this paper. The old unhappy loss or want of something had, I am conscious, some place in my heart; but not to the embitterment of my life. When I walked alone in the fine weather, and thought of the summer days when all the air had been filled with my boyish enchantment, I did miss something of the realization of my dreams; but I thought it was a softened glory of the Past, which nothing could have thrown upon the present time. I did feel, sometimes, for a little while, that I could have wished my wife had been my counsellor; had had more character and purpose, to sustain me and improve me by; had been endowed with power to fill up the void which somewhere seemed to be about me; but I felt as if this were an unearthly consummation of my happiness, that never had been meant to be, and never could have been.\n\nI was a boyish husband as to years. I had known the softening influence of no other sorrows or experiences than those recorded in these leaves. If I did any wrong, as I may have done much, I did it in mistaken love, and in my want of wisdom. I write the exact truth. It would avail me nothing to extenuate it now.\n\nThus it was that I took upon myself the toils and cares of our life, and had no partner in them. We lived much as before, in reference to our scrambling household arrangements; but I had got used to those, and Dora I was pleased to see was seldom vexed now. She was bright and cheerful in the old childish way, loved me dearly, and was happy with her old trifles.\n\nWhen the debates were heavy--I mean as to length, not quality, for in the last respect they were not often otherwise--and I went home late, Dora would never rest when she heard my footsteps, but would always come downstairs to meet me. When my evenings were unoccupied by the pursuit for which I had qualified myself with so much pains, and I was engaged in writing at home, she would sit quietly near me, however late the hour, and be so mute, that I would often think she had dropped asleep. But generally, when I raised my head, I saw her blue eyes looking at me with the quiet attention of which I have already spoken.\n\n‘Oh, what a weary boy!’ said Dora one night, when I met her eyes as I was shutting up my desk.\n\n‘What a weary girl!’ said I. ‘That’s more to the purpose. You must go to bed another time, my love. It’s far too late for you.’\n\n‘No, don’t send me to bed!’ pleaded Dora, coming to my side. ‘Pray, don’t do that!’\n\n‘Dora!’ To my amazement she was sobbing on my neck. ‘Not well, my dear! not happy!’\n\n‘Yes! quite well, and very happy!’ said Dora. ‘But say you’ll let me stop, and see you write.’\n\n‘Why, what a sight for such bright eyes at midnight!’ I replied.\n\n‘Are they bright, though?’ returned Dora, laughing. ‘I’m so glad they’re bright.’ ‘Little Vanity!’ said I.\n\nBut it was not vanity; it was only harmless delight in my admiration. I knew that very well, before she told me so.\n\n‘If you think them pretty, say I may always stop, and see you write!’ said Dora. ‘Do you think them pretty?’\n\n‘Very pretty.’\n\n‘Then let me always stop and see you write.’\n\n‘I am afraid that won’t improve their brightness, Dora.’\n\n‘Yes, it will! Because, you clever boy, you’ll not forget me then, while you are full of silent fancies. Will you mind it, if I say something very, very silly?---more than usual?’ inquired Dora, peeping over my shoulder into my face.\n\n‘What wonderful thing is that?’ said I.\n\n‘Please let me hold the pens,’ said Dora. ‘I want to have something to do with all those many hours when you are so industrious. May I hold the pens?’\n\nThe remembrance of her pretty joy when I said yes, brings tears into my eyes. The next time I sat down to write, and regularly afterwards, she sat in her old place, with a spare bundle of pens at her side. Her triumph in this connexion with my work, and her delight when I wanted a new pen--which I very often feigned to do--suggested to me a new way of pleasing my child-wife. I occasionally made a pretence of wanting a page or two of manuscript copied. Then Dora was in her glory. The preparations she made for this great work, the aprons she put on, the bibs she borrowed from the kitchen to keep off the ink, the time she took, the innumerable stoppages she made to have a laugh with Jip as if he understood it all, her conviction that her work was incomplete unless she signed her name at the end, and the way in which she would bring it to me, like a school-copy, and then, when I praised it, clasp me round the neck, are touching recollections to me, simple as they might appear to other men.\n\nShe took possession of the keys soon after this, and went jingling about the house with the whole bunch in a little basket, tied to her slender waist. I seldom found that the places to which they belonged were locked, or that they were of any use except as a plaything for Jip--but Dora was pleased, and that pleased me. She was quite satisfied that a good deal was effected by this make-belief of housekeeping; and was as merry as if we had been keeping a baby-house, for a joke.\n\nSo we went on. Dora was hardly less affectionate to my aunt than to me, and often told her of the time when she was afraid she was ‘a cross old thing’. I never saw my aunt unbend more systematically to anyone. She courted Jip, though Jip never responded; listened, day after day, to the guitar, though I am afraid she had no taste for music; never attacked the Incapables, though the temptation must have been severe; went wonderful distances on foot to purchase, as surprises, any trifles that she found out Dora wanted; and never came in by the garden, and missed her from the room, but she would call out, at the foot of the stairs, in a voice that sounded cheerfully all over the house:\n\n‘Where’s Little Blossom?’\n\n# CHAPTER 45. MR. DICK FULFILS MY AUNT’S PREDICTIONS\n\nIt was some time now, since I had left the Doctor. Living in his neighbourhood, I saw him frequently; and we all went to his house on two or three occasions to dinner or tea. The Old Soldier was in permanent quarters under the Doctor’s roof. She was exactly the same as ever, and the same immortal butterflies hovered over her cap.\n\nLike some other mothers, whom I have known in the course of my life, Mrs. Markleham was far more fond of pleasure than her daughter was. She required a great deal of amusement, and, like a deep old soldier, pretended, in consulting her own inclinations, to be devoting herself to her child. The Doctor’s desire that Annie should be entertained, was therefore particularly acceptable to this excellent parent; who expressed unqualified approval of his discretion.\n\nI have no doubt, indeed, that she probed the Doctor’s wound without knowing it. Meaning nothing but a certain matured frivolity and selfishness, not always inseparable from full-blown years, I think she confirmed him in his fear that he was a constraint upon his young wife, and that there was no congeniality of feeling between them, by so strongly commending his design of lightening the load of her life.\n\n‘My dear soul,’ she said to him one day when I was present, ‘you know there is no doubt it would be a little pokey for Annie to be always shut up here.’\n\nThe Doctor nodded his benevolent head. ‘When she comes to her mother’s age,’ said Mrs. Markleham, with a flourish of her fan, ‘then it’ll be another thing. You might put ME into a Jail, with genteel society and a rubber, and I should never care to come out. But I am not Annie, you know; and Annie is not her mother.’\n\n‘Surely, surely,’ said the Doctor.\n\n‘You are the best of creatures--no, I beg your pardon!’ for the Doctor made a gesture of deprecation, ‘I must say before your face, as I always say behind your back, you are the best of creatures; but of course you don’t--now do you?---enter into the same pursuits and fancies as Annie?’\n\n‘No,’ said the Doctor, in a sorrowful tone.\n\n‘No, of course not,’ retorted the Old Soldier. ‘Take your Dictionary, for example. What a useful work a Dictionary is! What a necessary work! The meanings of words! Without Doctor Johnson, or somebody of that sort, we might have been at this present moment calling an Italian-iron, a bedstead. But we can’t expect a Dictionary--especially when it’s making--to interest Annie, can we?’\n\nThe Doctor shook his head.\n\n‘And that’s why I so much approve,’ said Mrs. Markleham, tapping him on the shoulder with her shut-up fan, ‘of your thoughtfulness. It shows that you don’t expect, as many elderly people do expect, old heads on young shoulders. You have studied Annie’s character, and you understand it. That’s what I find so charming!’\n\nEven the calm and patient face of Doctor Strong expressed some little sense of pain, I thought, under the infliction of these compliments.\n\n‘Therefore, my dear Doctor,’ said the Old Soldier, giving him several affectionate taps, ‘you may command me, at all times and seasons. Now, do understand that I am entirely at your service. I am ready to go with Annie to operas, concerts, exhibitions, all kinds of places; and you shall never find that I am tired. Duty, my dear Doctor, before every consideration in the universe!’\n\nShe was as good as her word. She was one of those people who can bear a great deal of pleasure, and she never flinched in her perseverance in the cause. She seldom got hold of the newspaper (which she settled herself down in the softest chair in the house to read through an eye-glass, every day, for two hours), but she found out something that she was certain Annie would like to see. It was in vain for Annie to protest that she was weary of such things. Her mother’s remonstrance always was, ‘Now, my dear Annie, I am sure you know better; and I must tell you, my love, that you are not making a proper return for the kindness of Doctor Strong.’\n\nThis was usually said in the Doctor’s presence, and appeared to me to constitute Annie’s principal inducement for withdrawing her objections when she made any. But in general she resigned herself to her mother, and went where the Old Soldier would.\n\nIt rarely happened now that Mr. Maldon accompanied them. Sometimes my aunt and Dora were invited to do so, and accepted the invitation. Sometimes Dora only was asked. The time had been, when I should have been uneasy in her going; but reflection on what had passed that former night in the Doctor’s study, had made a change in my mistrust. I believed that the Doctor was right, and I had no worse suspicions.\n\nMy aunt rubbed her nose sometimes when she happened to be alone with me, and said she couldn’t make it out; she wished they were happier; she didn’t think our military friend (so she always called the Old Soldier) mended the matter at all. My aunt further expressed her opinion, ‘that if our military friend would cut off those butterflies, and give ‘em to the chimney-sweepers for May-day, it would look like the beginning of something sensible on her part.’\n\nBut her abiding reliance was on Mr. Dick. That man had evidently an idea in his head, she said; and if he could only once pen it up into a corner, which was his great difficulty, he would distinguish himself in some extraordinary manner.\n\nUnconscious of this prediction, Mr. Dick continued to occupy precisely the same ground in reference to the Doctor and to Mrs. Strong. He seemed neither to advance nor to recede. He appeared to have settled into his original foundation, like a building; and I must confess that my faith in his ever Moving, was not much greater than if he had been a building.\n\nBut one night, when I had been married some months, Mr. Dick put his head into the parlour, where I was writing alone (Dora having gone out with my aunt to take tea with the two little birds), and said, with a significant cough:\n\n‘You couldn’t speak to me without inconveniencing yourself, Trotwood, I am afraid?’\n\n‘Certainly, Mr. Dick,’ said I; ‘come in!’\n\n‘Trotwood,’ said Mr. Dick, laying his finger on the side of his nose, after he had shaken hands with me. ‘Before I sit down, I wish to make an observation. You know your aunt?’\n\n‘A little,’ I replied.\n\n‘She is the most wonderful woman in the world, sir!’\n\nAfter the delivery of this communication, which he shot out of himself as if he were loaded with it, Mr. Dick sat down with greater gravity than usual, and looked at me.\n\n‘Now, boy,’ said Mr. Dick, ‘I am going to put a question to you.’\n\n‘As many as you please,’ said I.\n\n‘What do you consider me, sir?’ asked Mr. Dick, folding his arms.\n\n‘A dear old friend,’ said I. ‘Thank you, Trotwood,’ returned Mr. Dick, laughing, and reaching across in high glee to shake hands with me. ‘But I mean, boy,’ resuming his gravity, ‘what do you consider me in this respect?’ touching his forehead.\n\nI was puzzled how to answer, but he helped me with a word.\n\n‘Weak?’ said Mr. Dick.\n\n‘Well,’ I replied, dubiously. ‘Rather so.’\n\n‘Exactly!’ cried Mr. Dick, who seemed quite enchanted by my reply. ‘That is, Trotwood, when they took some of the trouble out of you-know-who’s head, and put it you know where, there was a--’ Mr. Dick made his two hands revolve very fast about each other a great number of times, and then brought them into collision, and rolled them over and over one another, to express confusion. ‘There was that sort of thing done to me somehow. Eh?’\n\nI nodded at him, and he nodded back again.\n\n‘In short, boy,’ said Mr. Dick, dropping his voice to a whisper, ‘I am simple.’\n\nI would have qualified that conclusion, but he stopped me.\n\n‘Yes, I am! She pretends I am not. She won’t hear of it; but I am. I know I am. If she hadn’t stood my friend, sir, I should have been shut up, to lead a dismal life these many years. But I’ll provide for her! I never spend the copying money. I put it in a box. I have made a will. I’ll leave it all to her. She shall be rich--noble!’\n\nMr. Dick took out his pocket-handkerchief, and wiped his eyes. He then folded it up with great care, pressed it smooth between his two hands, put it in his pocket, and seemed to put my aunt away with it.\n\n‘Now you are a scholar, Trotwood,’ said Mr. Dick. ‘You are a fine scholar. You know what a learned man, what a great man, the Doctor is. You know what honour he has always done me. Not proud in his wisdom. Humble, humble--condescending even to poor Dick, who is simple and knows nothing. I have sent his name up, on a scrap of paper, to the kite, along the string, when it has been in the sky, among the larks. The kite has been glad to receive it, sir, and the sky has been brighter with it.’\n\nI delighted him by saying, most heartily, that the Doctor was deserving of our best respect and highest esteem.\n\n‘And his beautiful wife is a star,’ said Mr. Dick. ‘A shining star. I have seen her shine, sir. But,’ bringing his chair nearer, and laying one hand upon my knee--‘clouds, sir--clouds.’\n\nI answered the solicitude which his face expressed, by conveying the same expression into my own, and shaking my head.\n\n‘What clouds?’ said Mr. Dick.\n\nHe looked so wistfully into my face, and was so anxious to understand, that I took great pains to answer him slowly and distinctly, as I might have entered on an explanation to a child.\n\n‘There is some unfortunate division between them,’ I replied. ‘Some unhappy cause of separation. A secret. It may be inseparable from the discrepancy in their years. It may have grown up out of almost nothing.’\n\nMr. Dick, who had told off every sentence with a thoughtful nod, paused when I had done, and sat considering, with his eyes upon my face, and his hand upon my knee.\n\n‘Doctor not angry with her, Trotwood?’ he said, after some time.\n\n‘No. Devoted to her.’\n\n‘Then, I have got it, boy!’ said Mr. Dick.\n\nThe sudden exultation with which he slapped me on the knee, and leaned back in his chair, with his eyebrows lifted up as high as he could possibly lift them, made me think him farther out of his wits than ever. He became as suddenly grave again, and leaning forward as before, said--first respectfully taking out his pocket-handkerchief, as if it really did represent my aunt:\n\n‘Most wonderful woman in the world, Trotwood. Why has she done nothing to set things right?’\n\n‘Too delicate and difficult a subject for such interference,’ I replied.\n\n‘Fine scholar,’ said Mr. Dick, touching me with his finger. ‘Why has HE done nothing?’\n\n‘For the same reason,’ I returned.\n\n‘Then, I have got it, boy!’ said Mr. Dick. And he stood up before me, more exultingly than before, nodding his head, and striking himself repeatedly upon the breast, until one might have supposed that he had nearly nodded and struck all the breath out of his body.\n\n‘A poor fellow with a craze, sir,’ said Mr. Dick, ‘a simpleton, a weak-minded person--present company, you know!’ striking himself again, ‘may do what wonderful people may not do. I’ll bring them together, boy. I’ll try. They’ll not blame me. They’ll not object to me. They’ll not mind what I do, if it’s wrong. I’m only Mr. Dick. And who minds Dick? Dick’s nobody! Whoo!’ He blew a slight, contemptuous breath, as if he blew himself away.\n\nIt was fortunate he had proceeded so far with his mystery, for we heard the coach stop at the little garden gate, which brought my aunt and Dora home.\n\n‘Not a word, boy!’ he pursued in a whisper; ‘leave all the blame with Dick--simple Dick--mad Dick. I have been thinking, sir, for some time, that I was getting it, and now I have got it. After what you have said to me, I am sure I have got it. All right!’ Not another word did Mr. Dick utter on the subject; but he made a very telegraph of himself for the next half-hour (to the great disturbance of my aunt’s mind), to enjoin inviolable secrecy on me.\n\nTo my surprise, I heard no more about it for some two or three weeks, though I was sufficiently interested in the result of his endeavours; descrying a strange gleam of good sense--I say nothing of good feeling, for that he always exhibited--in the conclusion to which he had come. At last I began to believe, that, in the flighty and unsettled state of his mind, he had either forgotten his intention or abandoned it.\n\nOne fair evening, when Dora was not inclined to go out, my aunt and I strolled up to the Doctor’s cottage. It was autumn, when there were no debates to vex the evening air; and I remember how the leaves smelt like our garden at Blunderstone as we trod them under foot, and how the old, unhappy feeling, seemed to go by, on the sighing wind.\n\nIt was twilight when we reached the cottage. Mrs. Strong was just coming out of the garden, where Mr. Dick yet lingered, busy with his knife, helping the gardener to point some stakes. The Doctor was engaged with someone in his study; but the visitor would be gone directly, Mrs. Strong said, and begged us to remain and see him. We went into the drawing-room with her, and sat down by the darkening window. There was never any ceremony about the visits of such old friends and neighbours as we were.\n\nWe had not sat here many minutes, when Mrs. Markleham, who usually contrived to be in a fuss about something, came bustling in, with her newspaper in her hand, and said, out of breath, ‘My goodness gracious, Annie, why didn’t you tell me there was someone in the Study!’\n\n‘My dear mama,’ she quietly returned, ‘how could I know that you desired the information?’\n\n‘Desired the information!’ said Mrs. Markleham, sinking on the sofa. ‘I never had such a turn in all my life!’\n\n‘Have you been to the Study, then, mama?’ asked Annie.\n\n‘BEEN to the Study, my dear!’ she returned emphatically. ‘Indeed I have! I came upon the amiable creature--if you’ll imagine my feelings, Miss Trotwood and David--in the act of making his will.’\n\nHer daughter looked round from the window quickly.\n\n‘In the act, my dear Annie,’ repeated Mrs. Markleham, spreading the newspaper on her lap like a table-cloth, and patting her hands upon it, ‘of making his last Will and Testament. The foresight and affection of the dear! I must tell you how it was. I really must, in justice to the darling--for he is nothing less!--tell you how it was. Perhaps you know, Miss Trotwood, that there is never a candle lighted in this house, until one’s eyes are literally falling out of one’s head with being stretched to read the paper. And that there is not a chair in this house, in which a paper can be what I call, read, except one in the Study. This took me to the Study, where I saw a light. I opened the door. In company with the dear Doctor were two professional people, evidently connected with the law, and they were all three standing at the table: the darling Doctor pen in hand. “This simply expresses then,” said the Doctor--Annie, my love, attend to the very words--“this simply expresses then, gentlemen, the confidence I have in Mrs. Strong, and gives her all unconditionally?” One of the professional people replied, “And gives her all unconditionally.” Upon that, with the natural feelings of a mother, I said, “Good God, I beg your pardon!” fell over the door-step, and came away through the little back passage where the pantry is.’\n\nMrs. Strong opened the window, and went out into the verandah, where she stood leaning against a pillar.\n\n‘But now isn’t it, Miss Trotwood, isn’t it, David, invigorating,’ said Mrs. Markleham, mechanically following her with her eyes, ‘to find a man at Doctor Strong’s time of life, with the strength of mind to do this kind of thing? It only shows how right I was. I said to Annie, when Doctor Strong paid a very flattering visit to myself, and made her the subject of a declaration and an offer, I said, “My dear, there is no doubt whatever, in my opinion, with reference to a suitable provision for you, that Doctor Strong will do more than he binds himself to do.”’\n\nHere the bell rang, and we heard the sound of the visitors’ feet as they went out.\n\n‘It’s all over, no doubt,’ said the Old Soldier, after listening; ‘the dear creature has signed, sealed, and delivered, and his mind’s at rest. Well it may be! What a mind! Annie, my love, I am going to the Study with my paper, for I am a poor creature without news. Miss Trotwood, David, pray come and see the Doctor.’\n\nI was conscious of Mr. Dick’s standing in the shadow of the room, shutting up his knife, when we accompanied her to the Study; and of my aunt’s rubbing her nose violently, by the way, as a mild vent for her intolerance of our military friend; but who got first into the Study, or how Mrs. Markleham settled herself in a moment in her easy-chair, or how my aunt and I came to be left together near the door (unless her eyes were quicker than mine, and she held me back), I have forgotten, if I ever knew. But this I know,--that we saw the Doctor before he saw us, sitting at his table, among the folio volumes in which he delighted, resting his head calmly on his hand. That, in the same moment, we saw Mrs. Strong glide in, pale and trembling. That Mr. Dick supported her on his arm. That he laid his other hand upon the Doctor’s arm, causing him to look up with an abstracted air. That, as the Doctor moved his head, his wife dropped down on one knee at his feet, and, with her hands imploringly lifted, fixed upon his face the memorable look I had never forgotten. That at this sight Mrs. Markleham dropped the newspaper, and stared more like a figure-head intended for a ship to be called The Astonishment, than anything else I can think of.\n\nThe gentleness of the Doctor’s manner and surprise, the dignity that mingled with the supplicating attitude of his wife, the amiable concern of Mr. Dick, and the earnestness with which my aunt said to herself, ‘That man mad!’ (triumphantly expressive of the misery from which she had saved him)--I see and hear, rather than remember, as I write about it.\n\n‘Doctor!’ said Mr. Dick. ‘What is it that’s amiss? Look here!’\n\n‘Annie!’ cried the Doctor. ‘Not at my feet, my dear!’\n\n‘Yes!’ she said. ‘I beg and pray that no one will leave the room! Oh, my husband and father, break this long silence. Let us both know what it is that has come between us!’\n\nMrs. Markleham, by this time recovering the power of speech, and seeming to swell with family pride and motherly indignation, here exclaimed, ‘Annie, get up immediately, and don’t disgrace everybody belonging to you by humbling yourself like that, unless you wish to see me go out of my mind on the spot!’\n\n‘Mama!’ returned Annie. ‘Waste no words on me, for my appeal is to my husband, and even you are nothing here.’\n\n‘Nothing!’ exclaimed Mrs. Markleham. ‘Me, nothing! The child has taken leave of her senses. Please to get me a glass of water!’\n\nI was too attentive to the Doctor and his wife, to give any heed to this request; and it made no impression on anybody else; so Mrs. Markleham panted, stared, and fanned herself.\n\n‘Annie!’ said the Doctor, tenderly taking her in his hands. ‘My dear! If any unavoidable change has come, in the sequence of time, upon our married life, you are not to blame. The fault is mine, and only mine. There is no change in my affection, admiration, and respect. I wish to make you happy. I truly love and honour you. Rise, Annie, pray!’\n\nBut she did not rise. After looking at him for a little while, she sank down closer to him, laid her arm across his knee, and dropping her head upon it, said:\n\n‘If I have any friend here, who can speak one word for me, or for my husband in this matter; if I have any friend here, who can give a voice to any suspicion that my heart has sometimes whispered to me; if I have any friend here, who honours my husband, or has ever cared for me, and has anything within his knowledge, no matter what it is, that may help to mediate between us, I implore that friend to speak!’\n\nThere was a profound silence. After a few moments of painful hesitation, I broke the silence.\n\n‘Mrs. Strong,’ I said, ‘there is something within my knowledge, which I have been earnestly entreated by Doctor Strong to conceal, and have concealed until tonight. But, I believe the time has come when it would be mistaken faith and delicacy to conceal it any longer, and when your appeal absolves me from his injunction.’\n\nShe turned her face towards me for a moment, and I knew that I was right. I could not have resisted its entreaty, if the assurance that it gave me had been less convincing.\n\n‘Our future peace,’ she said, ‘may be in your hands. I trust it confidently to your not suppressing anything. I know beforehand that nothing you, or anyone, can tell me, will show my husband’s noble heart in any other light than one. Howsoever it may seem to you to touch me, disregard that. I will speak for myself, before him, and before God afterwards.’\n\nThus earnestly besought, I made no reference to the Doctor for his permission, but, without any other compromise of the truth than a little softening of the coarseness of Uriah Heep, related plainly what had passed in that same room that night. The staring of Mrs. Markleham during the whole narration, and the shrill, sharp interjections with which she occasionally interrupted it, defy description.\n\nWhen I had finished, Annie remained, for some few moments, silent, with her head bent down, as I have described. Then, she took the Doctor’s hand (he was sitting in the same attitude as when we had entered the room), and pressed it to her breast, and kissed it. Mr. Dick softly raised her; and she stood, when she began to speak, leaning on him, and looking down upon her husband--from whom she never turned her eyes.\n\n‘All that has ever been in my mind, since I was married,’ she said in a low, submissive, tender voice, ‘I will lay bare before you. I could not live and have one reservation, knowing what I know now.’\n\n‘Nay, Annie,’ said the Doctor, mildly, ‘I have never doubted you, my child. There is no need; indeed there is no need, my dear.’\n\n‘There is great need,’ she answered, in the same way, ‘that I should open my whole heart before the soul of generosity and truth, whom, year by year, and day by day, I have loved and venerated more and more, as Heaven knows!’\n\n‘Really,’ interrupted Mrs. Markleham, ‘if I have any discretion at all--’\n\n(‘Which you haven’t, you Marplot,’ observed my aunt, in an indignant whisper.) --‘I must be permitted to observe that it cannot be requisite to enter into these details.’\n\n‘No one but my husband can judge of that, mama,’ said Annie without removing her eyes from his face, ‘and he will hear me. If I say anything to give you pain, mama, forgive me. I have borne pain first, often and long, myself.’\n\n‘Upon my word!’ gasped Mrs. Markleham.\n\n‘When I was very young,’ said Annie, ‘quite a little child, my first associations with knowledge of any kind were inseparable from a patient friend and teacher--the friend of my dead father--who was always dear to me. I can remember nothing that I know, without remembering him. He stored my mind with its first treasures, and stamped his character upon them all. They never could have been, I think, as good as they have been to me, if I had taken them from any other hands.’\n\n‘Makes her mother nothing!’ exclaimed Mrs. Markleham.\n\n‘Not so mama,’ said Annie; ‘but I make him what he was. I must do that. As I grew up, he occupied the same place still. I was proud of his interest: deeply, fondly, gratefully attached to him. I looked up to him, I can hardly describe how--as a father, as a guide, as one whose praise was different from all other praise, as one in whom I could have trusted and confided, if I had doubted all the world. You know, mama, how young and inexperienced I was, when you presented him before me, of a sudden, as a lover.’\n\n‘I have mentioned the fact, fifty times at least, to everybody here!’ said Mrs. Markleham.\n\n(‘Then hold your tongue, for the Lord’s sake, and don’t mention it any more!’ muttered my aunt.)\n\n‘It was so great a change: so great a loss, I felt it, at first,’ said Annie, still preserving the same look and tone, ‘that I was agitated and distressed. I was but a girl; and when so great a change came in the character in which I had so long looked up to him, I think I was sorry. But nothing could have made him what he used to be again; and I was proud that he should think me so worthy, and we were married.’ ‘--At Saint Alphage, Canterbury,’ observed Mrs. Markleham.\n\n(‘Confound the woman!’ said my aunt, ‘she WON’T be quiet!’)\n\n‘I never thought,’ proceeded Annie, with a heightened colour, ‘of any worldly gain that my husband would bring to me. My young heart had no room in its homage for any such poor reference. Mama, forgive me when I say that it was you who first presented to my mind the thought that anyone could wrong me, and wrong him, by such a cruel suspicion.’\n\n‘Me!’ cried Mrs. Markleham.\n\n(‘Ah! You, to be sure!’ observed my aunt, ‘and you can’t fan it away, my military friend!’)\n\n‘It was the first unhappiness of my new life,’ said Annie. ‘It was the first occasion of every unhappy moment I have known. These moments have been more, of late, than I can count; but not--my generous husband!--not for the reason you suppose; for in my heart there is not a thought, a recollection, or a hope, that any power could separate from you!’\n\nShe raised her eyes, and clasped her hands, and looked as beautiful and true, I thought, as any Spirit. The Doctor looked on her, henceforth, as steadfastly as she on him.\n\n‘Mama is blameless,’ she went on, ‘of having ever urged you for herself, and she is blameless in intention every way, I am sure,--but when I saw how many importunate claims were pressed upon you in my name; how you were traded on in my name; how generous you were, and how Mr. Wickfield, who had your welfare very much at heart, resented it; the first sense of my exposure to the mean suspicion that my tenderness was bought--and sold to you, of all men on earth--fell upon me like unmerited disgrace, in which I forced you to participate. I cannot tell you what it was--mama cannot imagine what it was--to have this dread and trouble always on my mind, yet know in my own soul that on my marriage-day I crowned the love and honour of my life!’\n\n‘A specimen of the thanks one gets,’ cried Mrs. Markleham, in tears, ‘for taking care of one’s family! I wish I was a Turk!’\n\n(‘I wish you were, with all my heart--and in your native country!’ said my aunt.)\n\n‘It was at that time that mama was most solicitous about my Cousin Maldon. I had liked him’: she spoke softly, but without any hesitation: ‘very much. We had been little lovers once. If circumstances had not happened otherwise, I might have come to persuade myself that I really loved him, and might have married him, and been most wretched. There can be no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’\n\nI pondered on those words, even while I was studiously attending to what followed, as if they had some particular interest, or some strange application that I could not divine. ‘There can be no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose’--‘no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’\n\n‘There is nothing,’ said Annie, ‘that we have in common. I have long found that there is nothing. If I were thankful to my husband for no more, instead of for so much, I should be thankful to him for having saved me from the first mistaken impulse of my undisciplined heart.’\n\nShe stood quite still, before the Doctor, and spoke with an earnestness that thrilled me. Yet her voice was just as quiet as before.\n\n‘When he was waiting to be the object of your munificence, so freely bestowed for my sake, and when I was unhappy in the mercenary shape I was made to wear, I thought it would have become him better to have worked his own way on. I thought that if I had been he, I would have tried to do it, at the cost of almost any hardship. But I thought no worse of him, until the night of his departure for India. That night I knew he had a false and thankless heart. I saw a double meaning, then, in Mr. Wickfield’s scrutiny of me. I perceived, for the first time, the dark suspicion that shadowed my life.’\n\n‘Suspicion, Annie!’ said the Doctor. ‘No, no, no!’\n\n‘In your mind there was none, I know, my husband!’ she returned. ‘And when I came to you, that night, to lay down all my load of shame and grief, and knew that I had to tell that, underneath your roof, one of my own kindred, to whom you had been a benefactor, for the love of me, had spoken to me words that should have found no utterance, even if I had been the weak and mercenary wretch he thought me--my mind revolted from the taint the very tale conveyed. It died upon my lips, and from that hour till now has never passed them.’\n\nMrs. Markleham, with a short groan, leaned back in her easy-chair; and retired behind her fan, as if she were never coming out any more.\n\n‘I have never, but in your presence, interchanged a word with him from that time; then, only when it has been necessary for the avoidance of this explanation. Years have passed since he knew, from me, what his situation here was. The kindnesses you have secretly done for his advancement, and then disclosed to me, for my surprise and pleasure, have been, you will believe, but aggravations of the unhappiness and burden of my secret.’\n\nShe sunk down gently at the Doctor’s feet, though he did his utmost to prevent her; and said, looking up, tearfully, into his face:\n\n‘Do not speak to me yet! Let me say a little more! Right or wrong, if this were to be done again, I think I should do just the same. You never can know what it was to be devoted to you, with those old associations; to find that anyone could be so hard as to suppose that the truth of my heart was bartered away, and to be surrounded by appearances confirming that belief. I was very young, and had no adviser. Between mama and me, in all relating to you, there was a wide division. If I shrunk into myself, hiding the disrespect I had undergone, it was because I honoured you so much, and so much wished that you should honour me!’\n\n‘Annie, my pure heart!’ said the Doctor, ‘my dear girl!’\n\n‘A little more! a very few words more! I used to think there were so many whom you might have married, who would not have brought such charge and trouble on you, and who would have made your home a worthier home. I used to be afraid that I had better have remained your pupil, and almost your child. I used to fear that I was so unsuited to your learning and wisdom. If all this made me shrink within myself (as indeed it did), when I had that to tell, it was still because I honoured you so much, and hoped that you might one day honour me.’\n\n‘That day has shone this long time, Annie,’ said the Doctor, ‘and can have but one long night, my dear.’\n\n‘Another word! I afterwards meant--steadfastly meant, and purposed to myself--to bear the whole weight of knowing the unworthiness of one to whom you had been so good. And now a last word, dearest and best of friends! The cause of the late change in you, which I have seen with so much pain and sorrow, and have sometimes referred to my old apprehension--at other times to lingering suppositions nearer to the truth--has been made clear tonight; and by an accident I have also come to know, tonight, the full measure of your noble trust in me, even under that mistake. I do not hope that any love and duty I may render in return, will ever make me worthy of your priceless confidence; but with all this knowledge fresh upon me, I can lift my eyes to this dear face, revered as a father’s, loved as a husband’s, sacred to me in my childhood as a friend’s, and solemnly declare that in my lightest thought I have never wronged you; never wavered in the love and the fidelity I owe you!’\n\nShe had her arms around the Doctor’s neck, and he leant his head down over her, mingling his grey hair with her dark brown tresses.\n\n‘Oh, hold me to your heart, my husband! Never cast me out! Do not think or speak of disparity between us, for there is none, except in all my many imperfections. Every succeeding year I have known this better, as I have esteemed you more and more. Oh, take me to your heart, my husband, for my love was founded on a rock, and it endures!’\n\nIn the silence that ensued, my aunt walked gravely up to Mr. Dick, without at all hurrying herself, and gave him a hug and a sounding kiss. And it was very fortunate, with a view to his credit, that she did so; for I am confident that I detected him at that moment in the act of making preparations to stand on one leg, as an appropriate expression of delight.\n\n‘You are a very remarkable man, Dick!’ said my aunt, with an air of unqualified approbation; ‘and never pretend to be anything else, for I know better!’\n\nWith that, my aunt pulled him by the sleeve, and nodded to me; and we three stole quietly out of the room, and came away.\n\n‘That’s a settler for our military friend, at any rate,’ said my aunt, on the way home. ‘I should sleep the better for that, if there was nothing else to be glad of!’\n\n‘She was quite overcome, I am afraid,’ said Mr. Dick, with great commiseration.\n\n‘What! Did you ever see a crocodile overcome?’ inquired my aunt.\n\n‘I don’t think I ever saw a crocodile,’ returned Mr. Dick, mildly.\n\n‘There never would have been anything the matter, if it hadn’t been for that old Animal,’ said my aunt, with strong emphasis. ‘It’s very much to be wished that some mothers would leave their daughters alone after marriage, and not be so violently affectionate. They seem to think the only return that can be made them for bringing an unfortunate young woman into the world--God bless my soul, as if she asked to be brought, or wanted to come!--is full liberty to worry her out of it again. What are you thinking of, Trot?’\n\nI was thinking of all that had been said. My mind was still running on some of the expressions used. ‘There can be no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’ ‘The first mistaken impulse of an undisciplined heart.’ ‘My love was founded on a rock.’ But we were at home; and the trodden leaves were lying under-foot, and the autumn wind was blowing.\n\n# CHAPTER 46. INTELLIGENCE\n\nI must have been married, if I may trust to my imperfect memory for dates, about a year or so, when one evening, as I was returning from a solitary walk, thinking of the book I was then writing--for my success had steadily increased with my steady application, and I was engaged at that time upon my first work of fiction--I came past Mrs. Steerforth’s house. I had often passed it before, during my residence in that neighbourhood, though never when I could choose another road. Howbeit, it did sometimes happen that it was not easy to find another, without making a long circuit; and so I had passed that way, upon the whole, pretty often.\n\nI had never done more than glance at the house, as I went by with a quickened step. It had been uniformly gloomy and dull. None of the best rooms abutted on the road; and the narrow, heavily-framed old-fashioned windows, never cheerful under any circumstances, looked very dismal, close shut, and with their blinds always drawn down. There was a covered way across a little paved court, to an entrance that was never used; and there was one round staircase window, at odds with all the rest, and the only one unshaded by a blind, which had the same unoccupied blank look. I do not remember that I ever saw a light in all the house. If I had been a casual passer-by, I should have probably supposed that some childless person lay dead in it. If I had happily possessed no knowledge of the place, and had seen it often in that changeless state, I should have pleased my fancy with many ingenious speculations, I dare say.\n\nAs it was, I thought as little of it as I might. But my mind could not go by it and leave it, as my body did; and it usually awakened a long train of meditations. Coming before me, on this particular evening that I mention, mingled with the childish recollections and later fancies, the ghosts of half-formed hopes, the broken shadows of disappointments dimly seen and understood, the blending of experience and imagination, incidental to the occupation with which my thoughts had been busy, it was more than commonly suggestive. I fell into a brown study as I walked on, and a voice at my side made me start.\n\nIt was a woman’s voice, too. I was not long in recollecting Mrs. Steerforth’s little parlour-maid, who had formerly worn blue ribbons in her cap. She had taken them out now, to adapt herself, I suppose, to the altered character of the house; and wore but one or two disconsolate bows of sober brown.\n\n‘If you please, sir, would you have the goodness to walk in, and speak to Miss Dartle?’\n\n‘Has Miss Dartle sent you for me?’ I inquired.\n\n‘Not tonight, sir, but it’s just the same. Miss Dartle saw you pass a night or two ago; and I was to sit at work on the staircase, and when I saw you pass again, to ask you to step in and speak to her.’\n\nI turned back, and inquired of my conductor, as we went along, how Mrs. Steerforth was. She said her lady was but poorly, and kept her own room a good deal.\n\nWhen we arrived at the house, I was directed to Miss Dartle in the garden, and left to make my presence known to her myself. She was sitting on a seat at one end of a kind of terrace, overlooking the great city. It was a sombre evening, with a lurid light in the sky; and as I saw the prospect scowling in the distance, with here and there some larger object starting up into the sullen glare, I fancied it was no inapt companion to the memory of this fierce woman.\n\nShe saw me as I advanced, and rose for a moment to receive me. I thought her, then, still more colourless and thin than when I had seen her last; the flashing eyes still brighter, and the scar still plainer.\n\nOur meeting was not cordial. We had parted angrily on the last occasion; and there was an air of disdain about her, which she took no pains to conceal.\n\n‘I am told you wish to speak to me, Miss Dartle,’ said I, standing near her, with my hand upon the back of the seat, and declining her gesture of invitation to sit down.\n\n‘If you please,’ said she. ‘Pray has this girl been found?’\n\n‘No.’\n‘And yet she has run away!’\n\nI saw her thin lips working while she looked at me, as if they were eager to load her with reproaches.\n\n‘Run away?’ I repeated.\n\n‘Yes! From him,’ she said, with a laugh. ‘If she is not found, perhaps she never will be found. She may be dead!’\n\nThe vaunting cruelty with which she met my glance, I never saw expressed in any other face that ever I have seen.\n\n‘To wish her dead,’ said I, ‘may be the kindest wish that one of her own sex could bestow upon her. I am glad that time has softened you so much, Miss Dartle.’\n\nShe condescended to make no reply, but, turning on me with another scornful laugh, said:\n\n‘The friends of this excellent and much-injured young lady are friends of yours. You are their champion, and assert their rights. Do you wish to know what is known of her?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said I.\nShe rose with an ill-favoured smile, and taking a few steps towards a wall of holly that was near at hand, dividing the lawn from a kitchen-garden, said, in a louder voice, ‘Come here!’--as if she were calling to some unclean beast.\n\n‘You will restrain any demonstrative championship or vengeance in this place, of course, Mr. Copperfield?’ said she, looking over her shoulder at me with the same expression.\n\nI inclined my head, without knowing what she meant; and she said, ‘Come here!’ again; and returned, followed by the respectable Mr. Littimer, who, with undiminished respectability, made me a bow, and took up his position behind her. The air of wicked grace: of triumph, in which, strange to say, there was yet something feminine and alluring: with which she reclined upon the seat between us, and looked at me, was worthy of a cruel Princess in a Legend.\n\n‘Now,’ said she, imperiously, without glancing at him, and touching the old wound as it throbbed: perhaps, in this instance, with pleasure rather than pain. ‘Tell Mr. Copperfield about the flight.’\n\n‘Mr. James and myself, ma’am--’\n\n‘Don’t address yourself to me!’ she interrupted with a frown.\n\n‘Mr. James and myself, sir--’\n\n‘Nor to me, if you please,’ said I.\n\nMr. Littimer, without being at all discomposed, signified by a slight obeisance, that anything that was most agreeable to us was most agreeable to him; and began again.\n\n‘Mr. James and myself have been abroad with the young woman, ever since she left Yarmouth under Mr. James’s protection. We have been in a variety of places, and seen a deal of foreign country. We have been in France, Switzerland, Italy, in fact, almost all parts.’\n\nHe looked at the back of the seat, as if he were addressing himself to that; and softly played upon it with his hands, as if he were striking chords upon a dumb piano.\n\n‘Mr. James took quite uncommonly to the young woman; and was more settled, for a length of time, than I have known him to be since I have been in his service. The young woman was very improvable, and spoke the languages; and wouldn’t have been known for the same country-person. I noticed that she was much admired wherever we went.’\n\nMiss Dartle put her hand upon her side. I saw him steal a glance at her, and slightly smile to himself.\n\n‘Very much admired, indeed, the young woman was. What with her dress; what with the air and sun; what with being made so much of; what with this, that, and the other; her merits really attracted general notice.’\n\nHe made a short pause. Her eyes wandered restlessly over the distant prospect, and she bit her nether lip to stop that busy mouth.\n\nTaking his hands from the seat, and placing one of them within the other, as he settled himself on one leg, Mr. Littimer proceeded, with his eyes cast down, and his respectable head a little advanced, and a little on one side:\n\n‘The young woman went on in this manner for some time, being occasionally low in her spirits, until I think she began to weary Mr. James by giving way to her low spirits and tempers of that kind; and things were not so comfortable. Mr. James he began to be restless again. The more restless he got, the worse she got; and I must say, for myself, that I had a very difficult time of it indeed between the two. Still matters were patched up here, and made good there, over and over again; and altogether lasted, I am sure, for a longer time than anybody could have expected.’\n\nRecalling her eyes from the distance, she looked at me again now, with her former air. Mr. Littimer, clearing his throat behind his hand with a respectable short cough, changed legs, and went on:\n\n‘At last, when there had been, upon the whole, a good many words and reproaches, Mr. James he set off one morning, from the neighbourhood of Naples, where we had a villa (the young woman being very partial to the sea), and, under pretence of coming back in a day or so, left it in charge with me to break it out, that, for the general happiness of all concerned, he was’--here an interruption of the short cough--‘gone. But Mr. James, I must say, certainly did behave extremely honourable; for he proposed that the young woman should marry a very respectable person, who was fully prepared to overlook the past, and who was, at least, as good as anybody the young woman could have aspired to in a regular way: her connexions being very common.’\n\nHe changed legs again, and wetted his lips. I was convinced that the scoundrel spoke of himself, and I saw my conviction reflected in Miss Dartle’s face.\n\n‘This I also had it in charge to communicate. I was willing to do anything to relieve Mr. James from his difficulty, and to restore harmony between himself and an affectionate parent, who has undergone so much on his account. Therefore I undertook the commission. The young woman’s violence when she came to, after I broke the fact of his departure, was beyond all expectations. She was quite mad, and had to be held by force; or, if she couldn’t have got to a knife, or got to the sea, she’d have beaten her head against the marble floor.’\n\nMiss Dartle, leaning back upon the seat, with a light of exultation in her face, seemed almost to caress the sounds this fellow had uttered.\n\n‘But when I came to the second part of what had been entrusted to me,’ said Mr. Littimer, rubbing his hands uneasily, ‘which anybody might have supposed would have been, at all events, appreciated as a kind intention, then the young woman came out in her true colours. A more outrageous person I never did see. Her conduct was surprisingly bad. She had no more gratitude, no more feeling, no more patience, no more reason in her, than a stock or a stone. If I hadn’t been upon my guard, I am convinced she would have had my blood.’\n\n‘I think the better of her for it,’ said I, indignantly.\n\nMr. Littimer bent his head, as much as to say, ‘Indeed, sir? But you’re young!’ and resumed his narrative.\n\n‘It was necessary, in short, for a time, to take away everything nigh her, that she could do herself, or anybody else, an injury with, and to shut her up close. Notwithstanding which, she got out in the night; forced the lattice of a window, that I had nailed up myself; dropped on a vine that was trailed below; and never has been seen or heard of, to my knowledge, since.’\n\n‘She is dead, perhaps,’ said Miss Dartle, with a smile, as if she could have spurned the body of the ruined girl.\n\n‘She may have drowned herself, miss,’ returned Mr. Littimer, catching at an excuse for addressing himself to somebody. ‘It’s very possible. Or, she may have had assistance from the boatmen, and the boatmen’s wives and children. Being given to low company, she was very much in the habit of talking to them on the beach, Miss Dartle, and sitting by their boats. I have known her do it, when Mr. James has been away, whole days. Mr. James was far from pleased to find out, once, that she had told the children she was a boatman’s daughter, and that in her own country, long ago, she had roamed about the beach, like them.’\n\nOh, Emily! Unhappy beauty! What a picture rose before me of her sitting on the far-off shore, among the children like herself when she was innocent, listening to little voices such as might have called her Mother had she been a poor man’s wife; and to the great voice of the sea, with its eternal ‘Never more!’\n\n‘When it was clear that nothing could be done, Miss Dartle--’\n\n‘Did I tell you not to speak to me?’ she said, with stern contempt.\n\n‘You spoke to me, miss,’ he replied. ‘I beg your pardon. But it is my service to obey.’\n\n‘Do your service,’ she returned. ‘Finish your story, and go!’\n\n‘When it was clear,’ he said, with infinite respectability and an obedient bow, ‘that she was not to be found, I went to Mr. James, at the place where it had been agreed that I should write to him, and informed him of what had occurred. Words passed between us in consequence, and I felt it due to my character to leave him. I could bear, and I have borne, a great deal from Mr. James; but he insulted me too far. He hurt me. Knowing the unfortunate difference between himself and his mother, and what her anxiety of mind was likely to be, I took the liberty of coming home to England, and relating--’\n\n‘For money which I paid him,’ said Miss Dartle to me.\n\n‘Just so, ma’am--and relating what I knew. I am not aware,’ said Mr. Littimer, after a moment’s reflection, ‘that there is anything else. I am at present out of employment, and should be happy to meet with a respectable situation.’\n\nMiss Dartle glanced at me, as though she would inquire if there were anything that I desired to ask. As there was something which had occurred to my mind, I said in reply:\n\n‘I could wish to know from this--creature,’ I could not bring myself to utter any more conciliatory word, ‘whether they intercepted a letter that was written to her from home, or whether he supposes that she received it.’\n\nHe remained calm and silent, with his eyes fixed on the ground, and the tip of every finger of his right hand delicately poised against the tip of every finger of his left.\n\nMiss Dartle turned her head disdainfully towards him.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, miss,’ he said, awakening from his abstraction, ‘but, however submissive to you, I have my position, though a servant. Mr. Copperfield and you, miss, are different people. If Mr. Copperfield wishes to know anything from me, I take the liberty of reminding Mr. Copperfield that he can put a question to me. I have a character to maintain.’\n\nAfter a momentary struggle with myself, I turned my eyes upon him, and said, ‘You have heard my question. Consider it addressed to yourself, if you choose. What answer do you make?’\n\n‘Sir,’ he rejoined, with an occasional separation and reunion of those delicate tips, ‘my answer must be qualified; because, to betray Mr. James’s confidence to his mother, and to betray it to you, are two different actions. It is not probable, I consider, that Mr. James would encourage the receipt of letters likely to increase low spirits and unpleasantness; but further than that, sir, I should wish to avoid going.’\n\n‘Is that all?’ inquired Miss Dartle of me.\n\nI indicated that I had nothing more to say. ‘Except,’ I added, as I saw him moving off, ‘that I understand this fellow’s part in the wicked story, and that, as I shall make it known to the honest man who has been her father from her childhood, I would recommend him to avoid going too much into public.’\n\nHe had stopped the moment I began, and had listened with his usual repose of manner.\n\n‘Thank you, sir. But you’ll excuse me if I say, sir, that there are neither slaves nor slave-drivers in this country, and that people are not allowed to take the law into their own hands. If they do, it is more to their own peril, I believe, than to other people’s. Consequently speaking, I am not at all afraid of going wherever I may wish, sir.’\n\nWith that, he made a polite bow; and, with another to Miss Dartle, went away through the arch in the wall of holly by which he had come. Miss Dartle and I regarded each other for a little while in silence; her manner being exactly what it was, when she had produced the man.\n\n‘He says besides,’ she observed, with a slow curling of her lip, ‘that his master, as he hears, is coasting Spain; and this done, is away to gratify his seafaring tastes till he is weary. But this is of no interest to you. Between these two proud persons, mother and son, there is a wider breach than before, and little hope of its healing, for they are one at heart, and time makes each more obstinate and imperious. Neither is this of any interest to you; but it introduces what I wish to say. This devil whom you make an angel of. I mean this low girl whom he picked out of the tide-mud,’ with her black eyes full upon me, and her passionate finger up, ‘may be alive,--for I believe some common things are hard to die. If she is, you will desire to have a pearl of such price found and taken care of. We desire that, too; that he may not by any chance be made her prey again. So far, we are united in one interest; and that is why I, who would do her any mischief that so coarse a wretch is capable of feeling, have sent for you to hear what you have heard.’\n\nI saw, by the change in her face, that someone was advancing behind me. It was Mrs. Steerforth, who gave me her hand more coldly than of yore, and with an augmentation of her former stateliness of manner, but still, I perceived--and I was touched by it--with an ineffaceable remembrance of my old love for her son. She was greatly altered. Her fine figure was far less upright, her handsome face was deeply marked, and her hair was almost white. But when she sat down on the seat, she was a handsome lady still; and well I knew the bright eye with its lofty look, that had been a light in my very dreams at school.\n\n‘Is Mr. Copperfield informed of everything, Rosa?’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘And has he heard Littimer himself?’\n\n‘Yes; I have told him why you wished it.’ ‘You are a good girl. I have had some slight correspondence with your former friend, sir,’ addressing me, ‘but it has not restored his sense of duty or natural obligation. Therefore I have no other object in this, than what Rosa has mentioned. If, by the course which may relieve the mind of the decent man you brought here (for whom I am sorry--I can say no more), my son may be saved from again falling into the snares of a designing enemy, well!’\n\nShe drew herself up, and sat looking straight before her, far away.\n\n‘Madam,’ I said respectfully, ‘I understand. I assure you I am in no danger of putting any strained construction on your motives. But I must say, even to you, having known this injured family from childhood, that if you suppose the girl, so deeply wronged, has not been cruelly deluded, and would not rather die a hundred deaths than take a cup of water from your son’s hand now, you cherish a terrible mistake.’\n\n‘Well, Rosa, well!’ said Mrs. Steerforth, as the other was about to interpose, ‘it is no matter. Let it be. You are married, sir, I am told?’\n\nI answered that I had been some time married.\n\n‘And are doing well? I hear little in the quiet life I lead, but I understand you are beginning to be famous.’\n\n‘I have been very fortunate,’ I said, ‘and find my name connected with some praise.’\n\n‘You have no mother?’--in a softened voice.\n‘No.’\n\n‘It is a pity,’ she returned. ‘She would have been proud of you. Good night!’\n\nI took the hand she held out with a dignified, unbending air, and it was as calm in mine as if her breast had been at peace. Her pride could still its very pulses, it appeared, and draw the placid veil before her face, through which she sat looking straight before her on the far distance.\n\nAs I moved away from them along the terrace, I could not help observing how steadily they both sat gazing on the prospect, and how it thickened and closed around them. Here and there, some early lamps were seen to twinkle in the distant city; and in the eastern quarter of the sky the lurid light still hovered. But, from the greater part of the broad valley interposed, a mist was rising like a sea, which, mingling with the darkness, made it seem as if the gathering waters would encompass them. I have reason to remember this, and think of it with awe; for before I looked upon those two again, a stormy sea had risen to their feet.\n\nReflecting on what had been thus told me, I felt it right that it should be communicated to Mr. Peggotty. On the following evening I went into London in quest of him. He was always wandering about from place to place, with his one object of recovering his niece before him; but was more in London than elsewhere. Often and often, now, had I seen him in the dead of night passing along the streets, searching, among the few who loitered out of doors at those untimely hours, for what he dreaded to find.\n\nHe kept a lodging over the little chandler’s shop in Hungerford Market, which I have had occasion to mention more than once, and from which he first went forth upon his errand of mercy. Hither I directed my walk. On making inquiry for him, I learned from the people of the house that he had not gone out yet, and I should find him in his room upstairs.\n\nHe was sitting reading by a window in which he kept a few plants. The room was very neat and orderly. I saw in a moment that it was always kept prepared for her reception, and that he never went out but he thought it possible he might bring her home. He had not heard my tap at the door, and only raised his eyes when I laid my hand upon his shoulder.\n\n‘Mas’r Davy! Thankee, sir! thankee hearty, for this visit! Sit ye down. You’re kindly welcome, sir!’\n\n‘Mr. Peggotty,’ said I, taking the chair he handed me, ‘don’t expect much! I have heard some news.’\n‘Of Em’ly!’\n\nHe put his hand, in a nervous manner, on his mouth, and turned pale, as he fixed his eyes on mine.\n\n‘It gives no clue to where she is; but she is not with him.’\n\nHe sat down, looking intently at me, and listened in profound silence to all I had to tell. I well remember the sense of dignity, beauty even, with which the patient gravity of his face impressed me, when, having gradually removed his eyes from mine, he sat looking downward, leaning his forehead on his hand. He offered no interruption, but remained throughout perfectly still. He seemed to pursue her figure through the narrative, and to let every other shape go by him, as if it were nothing.\n\nWhen I had done, he shaded his face, and continued silent. I looked out of the window for a little while, and occupied myself with the plants.\n\n‘How do you fare to feel about it, Mas’r Davy?’ he inquired at length.\n\n‘I think that she is living,’ I replied.\n\n‘I doen’t know. Maybe the first shock was too rough, and in the wildness of her art--! That there blue water as she used to speak on. Could she have thowt o’ that so many year, because it was to be her grave!’\n\nHe said this, musing, in a low, frightened voice; and walked across the little room.\n\n‘And yet,’ he added, ‘Mas’r Davy, I have felt so sure as she was living--I have know’d, awake and sleeping, as it was so trew that I should find her--I have been so led on by it, and held up by it--that I doen’t believe I can have been deceived. No! Em’ly’s alive!’\n\nHe put his hand down firmly on the table, and set his sunburnt face into a resolute expression.\n\n‘My niece, Em’ly, is alive, sir!’ he said, steadfastly. ‘I doen’t know wheer it comes from, or how ‘tis, but I am told as she’s alive!’\n\nHe looked almost like a man inspired, as he said it. I waited for a few moments, until he could give me his undivided attention; and then proceeded to explain the precaution, that, it had occurred to me last night, it would be wise to take.\n\n‘Now, my dear friend--‘I began.\n\n‘Thankee, thankee, kind sir,’ he said, grasping my hand in both of his.\n\n‘If she should make her way to London, which is likely--for where could she lose herself so readily as in this vast city; and what would she wish to do, but lose and hide herself, if she does not go home?--’\n\n‘And she won’t go home,’ he interposed, shaking his head mournfully. ‘If she had left of her own accord, she might; not as It was, sir.’\n\n‘If she should come here,’ said I, ‘I believe there is one person, here, more likely to discover her than any other in the world. Do you remember--hear what I say, with fortitude--think of your great object!--do you remember Martha?’\n\n‘Of our town?’\n\nI needed no other answer than his face.\n\n‘Do you know that she is in London?’\n\n‘I have seen her in the streets,’ he answered, with a shiver.\n\n‘But you don’t know,’ said I, ‘that Emily was charitable to her, with Ham’s help, long before she fled from home. Nor, that, when we met one night, and spoke together in the room yonder, over the way, she listened at the door.’\n\n‘Mas’r Davy!’ he replied in astonishment. ‘That night when it snew so hard?’\n\n‘That night. I have never seen her since. I went back, after parting from you, to speak to her, but she was gone. I was unwilling to mention her to you then, and I am now; but she is the person of whom I speak, and with whom I think we should communicate. Do you understand?’\n\n‘Too well, sir,’ he replied. We had sunk our voices, almost to a whisper, and continued to speak in that tone.\n\n‘You say you have seen her. Do you think that you could find her? I could only hope to do so by chance.’\n\n‘I think, Mas’r Davy, I know wheer to look.’\n\n‘It is dark. Being together, shall we go out now, and try to find her tonight?’\n\nHe assented, and prepared to accompany me. Without appearing to observe what he was doing, I saw how carefully he adjusted the little room, put a candle ready and the means of lighting it, arranged the bed, and finally took out of a drawer one of her dresses (I remember to have seen her wear it), neatly folded with some other garments, and a bonnet, which he placed upon a chair. He made no allusion to these clothes, neither did I. There they had been waiting for her, many and many a night, no doubt.\n\n‘The time was, Mas’r Davy,’ he said, as we came downstairs, ‘when I thowt this girl, Martha, a’most like the dirt underneath my Em’ly’s feet. God forgive me, theer’s a difference now!’\n\nAs we went along, partly to hold him in conversation, and partly to satisfy myself, I asked him about Ham. He said, almost in the same words as formerly, that Ham was just the same, ‘wearing away his life with kiender no care nohow for ‘t; but never murmuring, and liked by all’.\n\nI asked him what he thought Ham’s state of mind was, in reference to the cause of their misfortunes? Whether he believed it was dangerous? What he supposed, for example, Ham would do, if he and Steerforth ever should encounter?\n\n‘I doen’t know, sir,’ he replied. ‘I have thowt of it oftentimes, but I can’t awize myself of it, no matters.’\n\nI recalled to his remembrance the morning after her departure, when we were all three on the beach. ‘Do you recollect,’ said I, ‘a certain wild way in which he looked out to sea, and spoke about “the end of it”?’\n\n‘Sure I do!’ said he.\n\n‘What do you suppose he meant?’\n\n‘Mas’r Davy,’ he replied, ‘I’ve put the question to myself a mort o’ times, and never found no answer. And theer’s one curious thing--that, though he is so pleasant, I wouldn’t fare to feel comfortable to try and get his mind upon ‘t. He never said a wured to me as warn’t as dootiful as dootiful could be, and it ain’t likely as he’d begin to speak any other ways now; but it’s fur from being fleet water in his mind, where them thowts lays. It’s deep, sir, and I can’t see down.’\n\n‘You are right,’ said I, ‘and that has sometimes made me anxious.’\n\n‘And me too, Mas’r Davy,’ he rejoined. ‘Even more so, I do assure you, than his ventersome ways, though both belongs to the alteration in him. I doen’t know as he’d do violence under any circumstances, but I hope as them two may be kep asunders.’\n\nWe had come, through Temple Bar, into the city. Conversing no more now, and walking at my side, he yielded himself up to the one aim of his devoted life, and went on, with that hushed concentration of his faculties which would have made his figure solitary in a multitude. We were not far from Blackfriars Bridge, when he turned his head and pointed to a solitary female figure flitting along the opposite side of the street. I knew it, readily, to be the figure that we sought.\n\nWe crossed the road, and were pressing on towards her, when it occurred to me that she might be more disposed to feel a woman’s interest in the lost girl, if we spoke to her in a quieter place, aloof from the crowd, and where we should be less observed. I advised my companion, therefore, that we should not address her yet, but follow her; consulting in this, likewise, an indistinct desire I had, to know where she went.\n\nHe acquiescing, we followed at a distance: never losing sight of her, but never caring to come very near, as she frequently looked about. Once, she stopped to listen to a band of music; and then we stopped too.\n\nShe went on a long way. Still we went on. It was evident, from the manner in which she held her course, that she was going to some fixed destination; and this, and her keeping in the busy streets, and I suppose the strange fascination in the secrecy and mystery of so following anyone, made me adhere to my first purpose. At length she turned into a dull, dark street, where the noise and crowd were lost; and I said, ‘We may speak to her now’; and, mending our pace, we went after her.\n\n# CHAPTER 47. MARTHA\n\nWe were now down in Westminster. We had turned back to follow her, having encountered her coming towards us; and Westminster Abbey was the point at which she passed from the lights and noise of the leading streets. She proceeded so quickly, when she got free of the two currents of passengers setting towards and from the bridge, that, between this and the advance she had of us when she struck off, we were in the narrow water-side street by Millbank before we came up with her. At that moment she crossed the road, as if to avoid the footsteps that she heard so close behind; and, without looking back, passed on even more rapidly.\n\nA glimpse of the river through a dull gateway, where some waggons were housed for the night, seemed to arrest my feet. I touched my companion without speaking, and we both forbore to cross after her, and both followed on that opposite side of the way; keeping as quietly as we could in the shadow of the houses, but keeping very near her.\n\nThere was, and is when I write, at the end of that low-lying street, a dilapidated little wooden building, probably an obsolete old ferry-house. Its position is just at that point where the street ceases, and the road begins to lie between a row of houses and the river. As soon as she came here, and saw the water, she stopped as if she had come to her destination; and presently went slowly along by the brink of the river, looking intently at it.\n\nAll the way here, I had supposed that she was going to some house; indeed, I had vaguely entertained the hope that the house might be in some way associated with the lost girl. But that one dark glimpse of the river, through the gateway, had instinctively prepared me for her going no farther.\n\nThe neighbourhood was a dreary one at that time; as oppressive, sad, and solitary by night, as any about London. There were neither wharves nor houses on the melancholy waste of road near the great blank Prison. A sluggish ditch deposited its mud at the prison walls. Coarse grass and rank weeds straggled over all the marshy land in the vicinity. In one part, carcases of houses, inauspiciously begun and never finished, rotted away. In another, the ground was cumbered with rusty iron monsters of steam-boilers, wheels, cranks, pipes, furnaces, paddles, anchors, diving-bells, windmill-sails, and I know not what strange objects, accumulated by some speculator, and grovelling in the dust, underneath which--having sunk into the soil of their own weight in wet weather--they had the appearance of vainly trying to hide themselves. The clash and glare of sundry fiery Works upon the river-side, arose by night to disturb everything except the heavy and unbroken smoke that poured out of their chimneys. Slimy gaps and causeways, winding among old wooden piles, with a sickly substance clinging to the latter, like green hair, and the rags of last year’s handbills offering rewards for drowned men fluttering above high-water mark, led down through the ooze and slush to the ebb-tide. There was a story that one of the pits dug for the dead in the time of the Great Plague was hereabout; and a blighting influence seemed to have proceeded from it over the whole place. Or else it looked as if it had gradually decomposed into that nightmare condition, out of the overflowings of the polluted stream.\n\nAs if she were a part of the refuse it had cast out, and left to corruption and decay, the girl we had followed strayed down to the river’s brink, and stood in the midst of this night-picture, lonely and still, looking at the water.\n\nThere were some boats and barges astrand in the mud, and these enabled us to come within a few yards of her without being seen. I then signed to Mr. Peggotty to remain where he was, and emerged from their shade to speak to her. I did not approach her solitary figure without trembling; for this gloomy end to her determined walk, and the way in which she stood, almost within the cavernous shadow of the iron bridge, looking at the lights crookedly reflected in the strong tide, inspired a dread within me.\n\nI think she was talking to herself. I am sure, although absorbed in gazing at the water, that her shawl was off her shoulders, and that she was muffling her hands in it, in an unsettled and bewildered way, more like the action of a sleep-walker than a waking person. I know, and never can forget, that there was that in her wild manner which gave me no assurance but that she would sink before my eyes, until I had her arm within my grasp.\n\nAt the same moment I said ‘Martha!’\n\nShe uttered a terrified scream, and struggled with me with such strength that I doubt if I could have held her alone. But a stronger hand than mine was laid upon her; and when she raised her frightened eyes and saw whose it was, she made but one more effort and dropped down between us. We carried her away from the water to where there were some dry stones, and there laid her down, crying and moaning. In a little while she sat among the stones, holding her wretched head with both her hands.\n\n‘Oh, the river!’ she cried passionately. ‘Oh, the river!’\n\n‘Hush, hush!’ said I. ‘Calm yourself.’\n\nBut she still repeated the same words, continually exclaiming, ‘Oh, the river!’ over and over again.\n\n‘I know it’s like me!’ she exclaimed. ‘I know that I belong to it. I know that it’s the natural company of such as I am! It comes from country places, where there was once no harm in it--and it creeps through the dismal streets, defiled and miserable--and it goes away, like my life, to a great sea, that is always troubled--and I feel that I must go with it!’ I have never known what despair was, except in the tone of those words.\n\n‘I can’t keep away from it. I can’t forget it. It haunts me day and night. It’s the only thing in all the world that I am fit for, or that’s fit for me. Oh, the dreadful river!’\n\nThe thought passed through my mind that in the face of my companion, as he looked upon her without speech or motion, I might have read his niece’s history, if I had known nothing of it. I never saw, in any painting or reality, horror and compassion so impressively blended. He shook as if he would have fallen; and his hand--I touched it with my own, for his appearance alarmed me--was deadly cold.\n\n‘She is in a state of frenzy,’ I whispered to him. ‘She will speak differently in a little time.’\n\nI don’t know what he would have said in answer. He made some motion with his mouth, and seemed to think he had spoken; but he had only pointed to her with his outstretched hand.\n\nA new burst of crying came upon her now, in which she once more hid her face among the stones, and lay before us, a prostrate image of humiliation and ruin. Knowing that this state must pass, before we could speak to her with any hope, I ventured to restrain him when he would have raised her, and we stood by in silence until she became more tranquil.\n\n‘Martha,’ said I then, leaning down, and helping her to rise--she seemed to want to rise as if with the intention of going away, but she was weak, and leaned against a boat. ‘Do you know who this is, who is with me?’\n\nShe said faintly, ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Do you know that we have followed you a long way tonight?’\n\nShe shook her head. She looked neither at him nor at me, but stood in a humble attitude, holding her bonnet and shawl in one hand, without appearing conscious of them, and pressing the other, clenched, against her forehead.\n\n‘Are you composed enough,’ said I, ‘to speak on the subject which so interested you--I hope Heaven may remember it!--that snowy night?’\n\nHer sobs broke out afresh, and she murmured some inarticulate thanks to me for not having driven her away from the door.\n\n‘I want to say nothing for myself,’ she said, after a few moments. ‘I am bad, I am lost. I have no hope at all. But tell him, sir,’ she had shrunk away from him, ‘if you don’t feel too hard to me to do it, that I never was in any way the cause of his misfortune.’ ‘It has never been attributed to you,’ I returned, earnestly responding to her earnestness.\n\n‘It was you, if I don’t deceive myself,’ she said, in a broken voice, ‘that came into the kitchen, the night she took such pity on me; was so gentle to me; didn’t shrink away from me like all the rest, and gave me such kind help! Was it you, sir?’\n\n‘It was,’ said I.\n\n‘I should have been in the river long ago,’ she said, glancing at it with a terrible expression, ‘if any wrong to her had been upon my mind. I never could have kept out of it a single winter’s night, if I had not been free of any share in that!’\n\n‘The cause of her flight is too well understood,’ I said. ‘You are innocent of any part in it, we thoroughly believe,--we know.’\n\n‘Oh, I might have been much the better for her, if I had had a better heart!’ exclaimed the girl, with most forlorn regret; ‘for she was always good to me! She never spoke a word to me but what was pleasant and right. Is it likely I would try to make her what I am myself, knowing what I am myself, so well? When I lost everything that makes life dear, the worst of all my thoughts was that I was parted for ever from her!’\n\nMr. Peggotty, standing with one hand on the gunwale of the boat, and his eyes cast down, put his disengaged hand before his face.\n\n‘And when I heard what had happened before that snowy night, from some belonging to our town,’ cried Martha, ‘the bitterest thought in all my mind was, that the people would remember she once kept company with me, and would say I had corrupted her! When, Heaven knows, I would have died to have brought back her good name!’\n\nLong unused to any self-control, the piercing agony of her remorse and grief was terrible.\n\n‘To have died, would not have been much--what can I say?---I would have lived!’ she cried. ‘I would have lived to be old, in the wretched streets--and to wander about, avoided, in the dark--and to see the day break on the ghastly line of houses, and remember how the same sun used to shine into my room, and wake me once--I would have done even that, to save her!’\n\nSinking on the stones, she took some in each hand, and clenched them up, as if she would have ground them. She writhed into some new posture constantly: stiffening her arms, twisting them before her face, as though to shut out from her eyes the little light there was, and drooping her head, as if it were heavy with insupportable recollections.\n\n‘What shall I ever do!’ she said, fighting thus with her despair. ‘How can I go on as I am, a solitary curse to myself, a living disgrace to everyone I come near!’ Suddenly she turned to my companion. ‘Stamp upon me, kill me! When she was your pride, you would have thought I had done her harm if I had brushed against her in the street. You can’t believe--why should you?---a syllable that comes out of my lips. It would be a burning shame upon you, even now, if she and I exchanged a word. I don’t complain. I don’t say she and I are alike--I know there is a long, long way between us. I only say, with all my guilt and wretchedness upon my head, that I am grateful to her from my soul, and love her. Oh, don’t think that all the power I had of loving anything is quite worn out! Throw me away, as all the world does. Kill me for being what I am, and having ever known her; but don’t think that of me!’\n\nHe looked upon her, while she made this supplication, in a wild distracted manner; and, when she was silent, gently raised her.\n\n‘Martha,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘God forbid as I should judge you. Forbid as I, of all men, should do that, my girl! You doen’t know half the change that’s come, in course of time, upon me, when you think it likely. Well!’ he paused a moment, then went on. ‘You doen’t understand how ‘tis that this here gentleman and me has wished to speak to you. You doen’t understand what ‘tis we has afore us. Listen now!’\n\nHis influence upon her was complete. She stood, shrinkingly, before him, as if she were afraid to meet his eyes; but her passionate sorrow was quite hushed and mute.\n\n‘If you heerd,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘owt of what passed between Mas’r Davy and me, th’ night when it snew so hard, you know as I have been--wheer not--fur to seek my dear niece. My dear niece,’ he repeated steadily. ‘Fur she’s more dear to me now, Martha, than she was dear afore.’\n\nShe put her hands before her face; but otherwise remained quiet.\n\n‘I have heerd her tell,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘as you was early left fatherless and motherless, with no friend fur to take, in a rough seafaring-way, their place. Maybe you can guess that if you’d had such a friend, you’d have got into a way of being fond of him in course of time, and that my niece was kiender daughter-like to me.’\n\nAs she was silently trembling, he put her shawl carefully about her, taking it up from the ground for that purpose.\n\n‘Whereby,’ said he, ‘I know, both as she would go to the wureld’s furdest end with me, if she could once see me again; and that she would fly to the wureld’s furdest end to keep off seeing me. For though she ain’t no call to doubt my love, and doen’t--and doen’t,’ he repeated, with a quiet assurance of the truth of what he said, ‘there’s shame steps in, and keeps betwixt us.’\n\nI read, in every word of his plain impressive way of delivering himself, new evidence of his having thought of this one topic, in every feature it presented.\n\n‘According to our reckoning,’ he proceeded, ‘Mas’r Davy’s here, and mine, she is like, one day, to make her own poor solitary course to London. We believe--Mas’r Davy, me, and all of us--that you are as innocent of everything that has befell her, as the unborn child. You’ve spoke of her being pleasant, kind, and gentle to you. Bless her, I knew she was! I knew she always was, to all. You’re thankful to her, and you love her. Help us all you can to find her, and may Heaven reward you!’\n\nShe looked at him hastily, and for the first time, as if she were doubtful of what he had said.\n\n‘Will you trust me?’ she asked, in a low voice of astonishment.\n\n‘Full and free!’ said Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘To speak to her, if I should ever find her; shelter her, if I have any shelter to divide with her; and then, without her knowledge, come to you, and bring you to her?’ she asked hurriedly.\n\nWe both replied together, ‘Yes!’\n\nShe lifted up her eyes, and solemnly declared that she would devote herself to this task, fervently and faithfully. That she would never waver in it, never be diverted from it, never relinquish it, while there was any chance of hope. If she were not true to it, might the object she now had in life, which bound her to something devoid of evil, in its passing away from her, leave her more forlorn and more despairing, if that were possible, than she had been upon the river’s brink that night; and then might all help, human and Divine, renounce her evermore!\n\nShe did not raise her voice above her breath, or address us, but said this to the night sky; then stood profoundly quiet, looking at the gloomy water.\n\nWe judged it expedient, now, to tell her all we knew; which I recounted at length. She listened with great attention, and with a face that often changed, but had the same purpose in all its varying expressions. Her eyes occasionally filled with tears, but those she repressed. It seemed as if her spirit were quite altered, and she could not be too quiet.\n\nShe asked, when all was told, where we were to be communicated with, if occasion should arise. Under a dull lamp in the road, I wrote our two addresses on a leaf of my pocket-book, which I tore out and gave to her, and which she put in her poor bosom. I asked her where she lived herself. She said, after a pause, in no place long. It were better not to know.\n\nMr. Peggotty suggesting to me, in a whisper, what had already occurred to myself, I took out my purse; but I could not prevail upon her to accept any money, nor could I exact any promise from her that she would do so at another time. I represented to her that Mr. Peggotty could not be called, for one in his condition, poor; and that the idea of her engaging in this search, while depending on her own resources, shocked us both. She continued steadfast. In this particular, his influence upon her was equally powerless with mine. She gratefully thanked him but remained inexorable.\n\n‘There may be work to be got,’ she said. ‘I’ll try.’\n\n‘At least take some assistance,’ I returned, ‘until you have tried.’\n\n‘I could not do what I have promised, for money,’ she replied. ‘I could not take it, if I was starving. To give me money would be to take away your trust, to take away the object that you have given me, to take away the only certain thing that saves me from the river.’\n\n‘In the name of the great judge,’ said I, ‘before whom you and all of us must stand at His dread time, dismiss that terrible idea! We can all do some good, if we will.’\n\nShe trembled, and her lip shook, and her face was paler, as she answered:\n\n‘It has been put into your hearts, perhaps, to save a wretched creature for repentance. I am afraid to think so; it seems too bold. If any good should come of me, I might begin to hope; for nothing but harm has ever come of my deeds yet. I am to be trusted, for the first time in a long while, with my miserable life, on account of what you have given me to try for. I know no more, and I can say no more.’\n\nAgain she repressed the tears that had begun to flow; and, putting out her trembling hand, and touching Mr. Peggotty, as if there was some healing virtue in him, went away along the desolate road. She had been ill, probably for a long time. I observed, upon that closer opportunity of observation, that she was worn and haggard, and that her sunken eyes expressed privation and endurance.\n\nWe followed her at a short distance, our way lying in the same direction, until we came back into the lighted and populous streets. I had such implicit confidence in her declaration, that I then put it to Mr. Peggotty, whether it would not seem, in the onset, like distrusting her, to follow her any farther. He being of the same mind, and equally reliant on her, we suffered her to take her own road, and took ours, which was towards Highgate. He accompanied me a good part of the way; and when we parted, with a prayer for the success of this fresh effort, there was a new and thoughtful compassion in him that I was at no loss to interpret.\n\nIt was midnight when I arrived at home. I had reached my own gate, and was standing listening for the deep bell of St. Paul’s, the sound of which I thought had been borne towards me among the multitude of striking clocks, when I was rather surprised to see that the door of my aunt’s cottage was open, and that a faint light in the entry was shining out across the road.\n\nThinking that my aunt might have relapsed into one of her old alarms, and might be watching the progress of some imaginary conflagration in the distance, I went to speak to her. It was with very great surprise that I saw a man standing in her little garden.\n\nHe had a glass and bottle in his hand, and was in the act of drinking. I stopped short, among the thick foliage outside, for the moon was up now, though obscured; and I recognized the man whom I had once supposed to be a delusion of Mr. Dick’s, and had once encountered with my aunt in the streets of the city.\n\nHe was eating as well as drinking, and seemed to eat with a hungry appetite. He seemed curious regarding the cottage, too, as if it were the first time he had seen it. After stooping to put the bottle on the ground, he looked up at the windows, and looked about; though with a covert and impatient air, as if he was anxious to be gone.\n\nThe light in the passage was obscured for a moment, and my aunt came out. She was agitated, and told some money into his hand. I heard it chink.\n\n‘What’s the use of this?’ he demanded.\n\n‘I can spare no more,’ returned my aunt.\n\n‘Then I can’t go,’ said he. ‘Here! You may take it back!’\n\n‘You bad man,’ returned my aunt, with great emotion; ‘how can you use me so? But why do I ask? It is because you know how weak I am! What have I to do, to free myself for ever of your visits, but to abandon you to your deserts?’\n\n‘And why don’t you abandon me to my deserts?’ said he.\n\n‘You ask me why!’ returned my aunt. ‘What a heart you must have!’\n\nHe stood moodily rattling the money, and shaking his head, until at length he said:\n\n‘Is this all you mean to give me, then?’\n\n‘It is all I CAN give you,’ said my aunt. ‘You know I have had losses, and am poorer than I used to be. I have told you so. Having got it, why do you give me the pain of looking at you for another moment, and seeing what you have become?’\n\n‘I have become shabby enough, if you mean that,’ he said. ‘I lead the life of an owl.’\n\n‘You stripped me of the greater part of all I ever had,’ said my aunt. ‘You closed my heart against the whole world, years and years. You treated me falsely, ungratefully, and cruelly. Go, and repent of it. Don’t add new injuries to the long, long list of injuries you have done me!’\n\n‘Aye!’ he returned. ‘It’s all very fine--Well! I must do the best I can, for the present, I suppose.’\n\nIn spite of himself, he appeared abashed by my aunt’s indignant tears, and came slouching out of the garden. Taking two or three quick steps, as if I had just come up, I met him at the gate, and went in as he came out. We eyed one another narrowly in passing, and with no favour.\n\n‘Aunt,’ said I, hurriedly. ‘This man alarming you again! Let me speak to him. Who is he?’\n\n‘Child,’ returned my aunt, taking my arm, ‘come in, and don’t speak to me for ten minutes.’\n\nWe sat down in her little parlour. My aunt retired behind the round green fan of former days, which was screwed on the back of a chair, and occasionally wiped her eyes, for about a quarter of an hour. Then she came out, and took a seat beside me.\n\n‘Trot,’ said my aunt, calmly, ‘it’s my husband.’\n\n‘Your husband, aunt? I thought he had been dead!’\n\n‘Dead to me,’ returned my aunt, ‘but living.’\n\nI sat in silent amazement.\n\n‘Betsey Trotwood don’t look a likely subject for the tender passion,’ said my aunt, composedly, ‘but the time was, Trot, when she believed in that man most entirely. When she loved him, Trot, right well. When there was no proof of attachment and affection that she would not have given him. He repaid her by breaking her fortune, and nearly breaking her heart. So she put all that sort of sentiment, once and for ever, in a grave, and filled it up, and flattened it down.’\n\n‘My dear, good aunt!’\n\n‘I left him,’ my aunt proceeded, laying her hand as usual on the back of mine, ‘generously. I may say at this distance of time, Trot, that I left him generously. He had been so cruel to me, that I might have effected a separation on easy terms for myself; but I did not. He soon made ducks and drakes of what I gave him, sank lower and lower, married another woman, I believe, became an adventurer, a gambler, and a cheat. What he is now, you see. But he was a fine-looking man when I married him,’ said my aunt, with an echo of her old pride and admiration in her tone; ‘and I believed him--I was a fool!--to be the soul of honour!’\n\nShe gave my hand a squeeze, and shook her head.\n\n‘He is nothing to me now, Trot--less than nothing. But, sooner than have him punished for his offences (as he would be if he prowled about in this country), I give him more money than I can afford, at intervals when he reappears, to go away. I was a fool when I married him; and I am so far an incurable fool on that subject, that, for the sake of what I once believed him to be, I wouldn’t have even this shadow of my idle fancy hardly dealt with. For I was in earnest, Trot, if ever a woman was.’\n\nMy aunt dismissed the matter with a heavy sigh, and smoothed her dress.\n\n‘There, my dear!’ she said. ‘Now you know the beginning, middle, and end, and all about it. We won’t mention the subject to one another any more; neither, of course, will you mention it to anybody else. This is my grumpy, frumpy story, and we’ll keep it to ourselves, Trot!’\n\n# CHAPTER 48. DOMESTIC\n\nI laboured hard at my book, without allowing it to interfere with the punctual discharge of my newspaper duties; and it came out and was very successful. I was not stunned by the praise which sounded in my ears, notwithstanding that I was keenly alive to it, and thought better of my own performance, I have little doubt, than anybody else did. It has always been in my observation of human nature, that a man who has any good reason to believe in himself never flourishes himself before the faces of other people in order that they may believe in him. For this reason, I retained my modesty in very self-respect; and the more praise I got, the more I tried to deserve.\n\nIt is not my purpose, in this record, though in all other essentials it is my written memory, to pursue the history of my own fictions. They express themselves, and I leave them to themselves. When I refer to them, incidentally, it is only as a part of my progress.\n\nHaving some foundation for believing, by this time, that nature and accident had made me an author, I pursued my vocation with confidence. Without such assurance I should certainly have left it alone, and bestowed my energy on some other endeavour. I should have tried to find out what nature and accident really had made me, and to be that, and nothing else. I had been writing, in the newspaper and elsewhere, so prosperously, that when my new success was achieved, I considered myself reasonably entitled to escape from the dreary debates. One joyful night, therefore, I noted down the music of the parliamentary bagpipes for the last time, and I have never heard it since; though I still recognize the old drone in the newspapers, without any substantial variation (except, perhaps, that there is more of it), all the livelong session.\n\nI now write of the time when I had been married, I suppose, about a year and a half. After several varieties of experiment, we had given up the housekeeping as a bad job. The house kept itself, and we kept a page. The principal function of this retainer was to quarrel with the cook; in which respect he was a perfect Whittington, without his cat, or the remotest chance of being made Lord Mayor.\n\nHe appears to me to have lived in a hail of saucepan-lids. His whole existence was a scuffle. He would shriek for help on the most improper occasions,--as when we had a little dinner-party, or a few friends in the evening,--and would come tumbling out of the kitchen, with iron missiles flying after him. We wanted to get rid of him, but he was very much attached to us, and wouldn’t go. He was a tearful boy, and broke into such deplorable lamentations, when a cessation of our connexion was hinted at, that we were obliged to keep him. He had no mother--no anything in the way of a relative, that I could discover, except a sister, who fled to America the moment we had taken him off her hands; and he became quartered on us like a horrible young changeling. He had a lively perception of his own unfortunate state, and was always rubbing his eyes with the sleeve of his jacket, or stooping to blow his nose on the extreme corner of a little pocket-handkerchief, which he never would take completely out of his pocket, but always economized and secreted.\n\nThis unlucky page, engaged in an evil hour at six pounds ten per annum, was a source of continual trouble to me. I watched him as he grew--and he grew like scarlet beans--with painful apprehensions of the time when he would begin to shave; even of the days when he would be bald or grey. I saw no prospect of ever getting rid of him; and, projecting myself into the future, used to think what an inconvenience he would be when he was an old man.\n\nI never expected anything less, than this unfortunate’s manner of getting me out of my difficulty. He stole Dora’s watch, which, like everything else belonging to us, had no particular place of its own; and, converting it into money, spent the produce (he was always a weak-minded boy) in incessantly riding up and down between London and Uxbridge outside the coach. He was taken to Bow Street, as well as I remember, on the completion of his fifteenth journey; when four-and-sixpence, and a second-hand fife which he couldn’t play, were found upon his person.\n\nThe surprise and its consequences would have been much less disagreeable to me if he had not been penitent. But he was very penitent indeed, and in a peculiar way--not in the lump, but by instalments. For example: the day after that on which I was obliged to appear against him, he made certain revelations touching a hamper in the cellar, which we believed to be full of wine, but which had nothing in it except bottles and corks. We supposed he had now eased his mind, and told the worst he knew of the cook; but, a day or two afterwards, his conscience sustained a new twinge, and he disclosed how she had a little girl, who, early every morning, took away our bread; and also how he himself had been suborned to maintain the milkman in coals. In two or three days more, I was informed by the authorities of his having led to the discovery of sirloins of beef among the kitchen-stuff, and sheets in the rag-bag. A little while afterwards, he broke out in an entirely new direction, and confessed to a knowledge of burglarious intentions as to our premises, on the part of the pot-boy, who was immediately taken up. I got to be so ashamed of being such a victim, that I would have given him any money to hold his tongue, or would have offered a round bribe for his being permitted to run away. It was an aggravating circumstance in the case that he had no idea of this, but conceived that he was making me amends in every new discovery: not to say, heaping obligations on my head.\n\nAt last I ran away myself, whenever I saw an emissary of the police approaching with some new intelligence; and lived a stealthy life until he was tried and ordered to be transported. Even then he couldn’t be quiet, but was always writing us letters; and wanted so much to see Dora before he went away, that Dora went to visit him, and fainted when she found herself inside the iron bars. In short, I had no peace of my life until he was expatriated, and made (as I afterwards heard) a shepherd of, ‘up the country’ somewhere; I have no geographical idea where.\n\nAll this led me into some serious reflections, and presented our mistakes in a new aspect; as I could not help communicating to Dora one evening, in spite of my tenderness for her.\n\n‘My love,’ said I, ‘it is very painful to me to think that our want of system and management, involves not only ourselves (which we have got used to), but other people.’\n\n‘You have been silent for a long time, and now you are going to be cross!’ said Dora.\n\n‘No, my dear, indeed! Let me explain to you what I mean.’\n\n‘I think I don’t want to know,’ said Dora.\n\n‘But I want you to know, my love. Put Jip down.’\n\nDora put his nose to mine, and said ‘Boh!’ to drive my seriousness away; but, not succeeding, ordered him into his Pagoda, and sat looking at me, with her hands folded, and a most resigned little expression of countenance.\n\n‘The fact is, my dear,’ I began, ‘there is contagion in us. We infect everyone about us.’\n\nI might have gone on in this figurative manner, if Dora’s face had not admonished me that she was wondering with all her might whether I was going to propose any new kind of vaccination, or other medical remedy, for this unwholesome state of ours. Therefore I checked myself, and made my meaning plainer.\n\n‘It is not merely, my pet,’ said I, ‘that we lose money and comfort, and even temper sometimes, by not learning to be more careful; but that we incur the serious responsibility of spoiling everyone who comes into our service, or has any dealings with us. I begin to be afraid that the fault is not entirely on one side, but that these people all turn out ill because we don’t turn out very well ourselves.’\n\n‘Oh, what an accusation,’ exclaimed Dora, opening her eyes wide; ‘to say that you ever saw me take gold watches! Oh!’\n\n‘My dearest,’ I remonstrated, ‘don’t talk preposterous nonsense! Who has made the least allusion to gold watches?’\n\n‘You did,’ returned Dora. ‘You know you did. You said I hadn’t turned out well, and compared me to him.’\n\n‘To whom?’ I asked.\n\n‘To the page,’ sobbed Dora. ‘Oh, you cruel fellow, to compare your affectionate wife to a transported page! Why didn’t you tell me your opinion of me before we were married? Why didn’t you say, you hard-hearted thing, that you were convinced I was worse than a transported page? Oh, what a dreadful opinion to have of me! Oh, my goodness!’\n\n‘Now, Dora, my love,’ I returned, gently trying to remove the handkerchief she pressed to her eyes, ‘this is not only very ridiculous of you, but very wrong. In the first place, it’s not true.’\n\n‘You always said he was a story-teller,’ sobbed Dora. ‘And now you say the same of me! Oh, what shall I do! What shall I do!’\n\n‘My darling girl,’ I retorted, ‘I really must entreat you to be reasonable, and listen to what I did say, and do say. My dear Dora, unless we learn to do our duty to those whom we employ, they will never learn to do their duty to us. I am afraid we present opportunities to people to do wrong, that never ought to be presented. Even if we were as lax as we are, in all our arrangements, by choice--which we are not--even if we liked it, and found it agreeable to be so--which we don’t--I am persuaded we should have no right to go on in this way. We are positively corrupting people. We are bound to think of that. I can’t help thinking of it, Dora. It is a reflection I am unable to dismiss, and it sometimes makes me very uneasy. There, dear, that’s all. Come now. Don’t be foolish!’\n\nDora would not allow me, for a long time, to remove the handkerchief. She sat sobbing and murmuring behind it, that, if I was uneasy, why had I ever been married? Why hadn’t I said, even the day before we went to church, that I knew I should be uneasy, and I would rather not? If I couldn’t bear her, why didn’t I send her away to her aunts at Putney, or to Julia Mills in India? Julia would be glad to see her, and would not call her a transported page; Julia never had called her anything of the sort. In short, Dora was so afflicted, and so afflicted me by being in that condition, that I felt it was of no use repeating this kind of effort, though never so mildly, and I must take some other course.\n\nWhat other course was left to take? To ‘form her mind’? This was a common phrase of words which had a fair and promising sound, and I resolved to form Dora’s mind.\n\nI began immediately. When Dora was very childish, and I would have infinitely preferred to humour her, I tried to be grave--and disconcerted her, and myself too. I talked to her on the subjects which occupied my thoughts; and I read Shakespeare to her--and fatigued her to the last degree. I accustomed myself to giving her, as it were quite casually, little scraps of useful information, or sound opinion--and she started from them when I let them off, as if they had been crackers. No matter how incidentally or naturally I endeavoured to form my little wife’s mind, I could not help seeing that she always had an instinctive perception of what I was about, and became a prey to the keenest apprehensions. In particular, it was clear to me, that she thought Shakespeare a terrible fellow. The formation went on very slowly.\n\nI pressed Traddles into the service without his knowledge; and whenever he came to see us, exploded my mines upon him for the edification of Dora at second hand. The amount of practical wisdom I bestowed upon Traddles in this manner was immense, and of the best quality; but it had no other effect upon Dora than to depress her spirits, and make her always nervous with the dread that it would be her turn next. I found myself in the condition of a schoolmaster, a trap, a pitfall; of always playing spider to Dora’s fly, and always pouncing out of my hole to her infinite disturbance.\n\nStill, looking forward through this intermediate stage, to the time when there should be a perfect sympathy between Dora and me, and when I should have ‘formed her mind’ to my entire satisfaction, I persevered, even for months. Finding at last, however, that, although I had been all this time a very porcupine or hedgehog, bristling all over with determination, I had effected nothing, it began to occur to me that perhaps Dora’s mind was already formed.\n\nOn further consideration this appeared so likely, that I abandoned my scheme, which had had a more promising appearance in words than in action; resolving henceforth to be satisfied with my child-wife, and to try to change her into nothing else by any process. I was heartily tired of being sagacious and prudent by myself, and of seeing my darling under restraint; so I bought a pretty pair of ear-rings for her, and a collar for Jip, and went home one day to make myself agreeable.\n\nDora was delighted with the little presents, and kissed me joyfully; but there was a shadow between us, however slight, and I had made up my mind that it should not be there. If there must be such a shadow anywhere, I would keep it for the future in my own breast.\n\nI sat down by my wife on the sofa, and put the ear-rings in her ears; and then I told her that I feared we had not been quite as good company lately, as we used to be, and that the fault was mine. Which I sincerely felt, and which indeed it was.\n\n‘The truth is, Dora, my life,’ I said; ‘I have been trying to be wise.’\n\n‘And to make me wise too,’ said Dora, timidly. ‘Haven’t you, Doady?’\n\nI nodded assent to the pretty inquiry of the raised eyebrows, and kissed the parted lips.\n\n‘It’s of not a bit of use,’ said Dora, shaking her head, until the ear-rings rang again. ‘You know what a little thing I am, and what I wanted you to call me from the first. If you can’t do so, I am afraid you’ll never like me. Are you sure you don’t think, sometimes, it would have been better to have--’\n\n‘Done what, my dear?’ For she made no effort to proceed.\n\n‘Nothing!’ said Dora.\n‘Nothing?’ I repeated.\n\nShe put her arms round my neck, and laughed, and called herself by her favourite name of a goose, and hid her face on my shoulder in such a profusion of curls that it was quite a task to clear them away and see it.\n\n‘Don’t I think it would have been better to have done nothing, than to have tried to form my little wife’s mind?’ said I, laughing at myself. ‘Is that the question? Yes, indeed, I do.’\n\n‘Is that what you have been trying?’ cried Dora. ‘Oh what a shocking boy!’\n\n‘But I shall never try any more,’ said I. ‘For I love her dearly as she is.’\n\n‘Without a story--really?’ inquired Dora, creeping closer to me.\n\n‘Why should I seek to change,’ said I, ‘what has been so precious to me for so long! You never can show better than as your own natural self, my sweet Dora; and we’ll try no conceited experiments, but go back to our old way, and be happy.’\n\n‘And be happy!’ returned Dora. ‘Yes! All day! And you won’t mind things going a tiny morsel wrong, sometimes?’\n\n‘No, no,’ said I. ‘We must do the best we can.’\n\n‘And you won’t tell me, any more, that we make other people bad,’ coaxed Dora; ‘will you? Because you know it’s so dreadfully cross!’\n\n‘No, no,’ said I.\n\n‘It’s better for me to be stupid than uncomfortable, isn’t it?’ said Dora.\n\n‘Better to be naturally Dora than anything else in the world.’\n\n‘In the world! Ah, Doady, it’s a large place!’\n\nShe shook her head, turned her delighted bright eyes up to mine, kissed me, broke into a merry laugh, and sprang away to put on Jip’s new collar.\n\nSo ended my last attempt to make any change in Dora. I had been unhappy in trying it; I could not endure my own solitary wisdom; I could not reconcile it with her former appeal to me as my child-wife. I resolved to do what I could, in a quiet way, to improve our proceedings myself, but I foresaw that my utmost would be very little, or I must degenerate into the spider again, and be for ever lying in wait.\n\nAnd the shadow I have mentioned, that was not to be between us any more, but was to rest wholly on my own heart? How did that fall?\n\nThe old unhappy feeling pervaded my life. It was deepened, if it were changed at all; but it was as undefined as ever, and addressed me like a strain of sorrowful music faintly heard in the night. I loved my wife dearly, and I was happy; but the happiness I had vaguely anticipated, once, was not the happiness I enjoyed, and there was always something wanting.\n\nIn fulfilment of the compact I have made with myself, to reflect my mind on this paper, I again examine it, closely, and bring its secrets to the light. What I missed, I still regarded--I always regarded--as something that had been a dream of my youthful fancy; that was incapable of realization; that I was now discovering to be so, with some natural pain, as all men did. But that it would have been better for me if my wife could have helped me more, and shared the many thoughts in which I had no partner; and that this might have been; I knew.\n\nBetween these two irreconcilable conclusions: the one, that what I felt was general and unavoidable; the other, that it was particular to me, and might have been different: I balanced curiously, with no distinct sense of their opposition to each other. When I thought of the airy dreams of youth that are incapable of realization, I thought of the better state preceding manhood that I had outgrown; and then the contented days with Agnes, in the dear old house, arose before me, like spectres of the dead, that might have some renewal in another world, but never more could be reanimated here.\n\nSometimes, the speculation came into my thoughts, What might have happened, or what would have happened, if Dora and I had never known each other? But she was so incorporated with my existence, that it was the idlest of all fancies, and would soon rise out of my reach and sight, like gossamer floating in the air.\n\nI always loved her. What I am describing, slumbered, and half awoke, and slept again, in the innermost recesses of my mind. There was no evidence of it in me; I know of no influence it had in anything I said or did. I bore the weight of all our little cares, and all my projects; Dora held the pens; and we both felt that our shares were adjusted as the case required. She was truly fond of me, and proud of me; and when Agnes wrote a few earnest words in her letters to Dora, of the pride and interest with which my old friends heard of my growing reputation, and read my book as if they heard me speaking its contents, Dora read them out to me with tears of joy in her bright eyes, and said I was a dear old clever, famous boy.\n\n‘The first mistaken impulse of an undisciplined heart.’ Those words of Mrs. Strong’s were constantly recurring to me, at this time; were almost always present to my mind. I awoke with them, often, in the night; I remember to have even read them, in dreams, inscribed upon the walls of houses. For I knew, now, that my own heart was undisciplined when it first loved Dora; and that if it had been disciplined, it never could have felt, when we were married, what it had felt in its secret experience.\n\n‘There can be no disparity in marriage, like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’ Those words I remembered too. I had endeavoured to adapt Dora to myself, and found it impracticable. It remained for me to adapt myself to Dora; to share with her what I could, and be happy; to bear on my own shoulders what I must, and be happy still. This was the discipline to which I tried to bring my heart, when I began to think. It made my second year much happier than my first; and, what was better still, made Dora’s life all sunshine.\n\nBut, as that year wore on, Dora was not strong. I had hoped that lighter hands than mine would help to mould her character, and that a baby-smile upon her breast might change my child-wife to a woman. It was not to be. The spirit fluttered for a moment on the threshold of its little prison, and, unconscious of captivity, took wing.\n\n‘When I can run about again, as I used to do, aunt,’ said Dora, ‘I shall make Jip race. He is getting quite slow and lazy.’\n\n‘I suspect, my dear,’ said my aunt quietly working by her side, ‘he has a worse disorder than that. Age, Dora.’\n\n‘Do you think he is old?’ said Dora, astonished. ‘Oh, how strange it seems that Jip should be old!’\n\n‘It’s a complaint we are all liable to, Little One, as we get on in life,’ said my aunt, cheerfully; ‘I don’t feel more free from it than I used to be, I assure you.’\n\n‘But Jip,’ said Dora, looking at him with compassion, ‘even little Jip! Oh, poor fellow!’\n\n‘I dare say he’ll last a long time yet, Blossom,’ said my aunt, patting Dora on the cheek, as she leaned out of her couch to look at Jip, who responded by standing on his hind legs, and baulking himself in various asthmatic attempts to scramble up by the head and shoulders. ‘He must have a piece of flannel in his house this winter, and I shouldn’t wonder if he came out quite fresh again, with the flowers in the spring. Bless the little dog!’ exclaimed my aunt, ‘if he had as many lives as a cat, and was on the point of losing ‘em all, he’d bark at me with his last breath, I believe!’\n\nDora had helped him up on the sofa; where he really was defying my aunt to such a furious extent, that he couldn’t keep straight, but barked himself sideways. The more my aunt looked at him, the more he reproached her; for she had lately taken to spectacles, and for some inscrutable reason he considered the glasses personal.\n\nDora made him lie down by her, with a good deal of persuasion; and when he was quiet, drew one of his long ears through and through her hand, repeating thoughtfully, ‘Even little Jip! Oh, poor fellow!’\n\n‘His lungs are good enough,’ said my aunt, gaily, ‘and his dislikes are not at all feeble. He has a good many years before him, no doubt. But if you want a dog to race with, Little Blossom, he has lived too well for that, and I’ll give you one.’\n\n‘Thank you, aunt,’ said Dora, faintly. ‘But don’t, please!’\n\n‘No?’ said my aunt, taking off her spectacles.\n\n‘I couldn’t have any other dog but Jip,’ said Dora. ‘It would be so unkind to Jip! Besides, I couldn’t be such friends with any other dog but Jip; because he wouldn’t have known me before I was married, and wouldn’t have barked at Doady when he first came to our house. I couldn’t care for any other dog but Jip, I am afraid, aunt.’\n\n‘To be sure!’ said my aunt, patting her cheek again. ‘You are right.’\n\n‘You are not offended,’ said Dora. ‘Are you?’\n\n‘Why, what a sensitive pet it is!’ cried my aunt, bending over her affectionately. ‘To think that I could be offended!’\n\n‘No, no, I didn’t really think so,’ returned Dora; ‘but I am a little tired, and it made me silly for a moment--I am always a silly little thing, you know, but it made me more silly--to talk about Jip. He has known me in all that has happened to me, haven’t you, Jip? And I couldn’t bear to slight him, because he was a little altered--could I, Jip?’\n\nJip nestled closer to his mistress, and lazily licked her hand.\n\n‘You are not so old, Jip, are you, that you’ll leave your mistress yet?’ said Dora. ‘We may keep one another company a little longer!’\n\nMy pretty Dora! When she came down to dinner on the ensuing Sunday, and was so glad to see old Traddles (who always dined with us on Sunday), we thought she would be ‘running about as she used to do’, in a few days. But they said, wait a few days more; and then, wait a few days more; and still she neither ran nor walked. She looked very pretty, and was very merry; but the little feet that used to be so nimble when they danced round Jip, were dull and motionless.\n\nI began to carry her downstairs every morning, and upstairs every night. She would clasp me round the neck and laugh, the while, as if I did it for a wager. Jip would bark and caper round us, and go on before, and look back on the landing, breathing short, to see that we were coming. My aunt, the best and most cheerful of nurses, would trudge after us, a moving mass of shawls and pillows. Mr. Dick would not have relinquished his post of candle-bearer to anyone alive. Traddles would be often at the bottom of the staircase, looking on, and taking charge of sportive messages from Dora to the dearest girl in the world. We made quite a gay procession of it, and my child-wife was the gayest there.\n\nBut, sometimes, when I took her up, and felt that she was lighter in my arms, a dead blank feeling came upon me, as if I were approaching to some frozen region yet unseen, that numbed my life. I avoided the recognition of this feeling by any name, or by any communing with myself; until one night, when it was very strong upon me, and my aunt had left her with a parting cry of ‘Good night, Little Blossom,’ I sat down at my desk alone, and tried to think, Oh what a fatal name it was, and how the blossom withered in its bloom upon the tree!\n\n# CHAPTER 49. I AM INVOLVED IN MYSTERY\n\nI received one morning by the post, the following letter, dated Canterbury, and addressed to me at Doctor’s Commons; which I read with some surprise:\n\n‘Circumstances beyond my individual control have, for a considerable lapse of time, effected a severance of that intimacy which, in the limited opportunities conceded to me in the midst of my professional duties, of contemplating the scenes and events of the past, tinged by the prismatic hues of memory, has ever afforded me, as it ever must continue to afford, gratifying emotions of no common description. This fact, my dear sir, combined with the distinguished elevation to which your talents have raised you, deters me from presuming to aspire to the liberty of addressing the companion of my youth, by the familiar appellation of Copperfield! It is sufficient to know that the name to which I do myself the honour to refer, will ever be treasured among the muniments of our house (I allude to the archives connected with our former lodgers, preserved by Mrs. Micawber), with sentiments of personal esteem amounting to affection.\n\n‘It is not for one, situated, through his original errors and a fortuitous combination of unpropitious events, as is the foundered Bark (if he may be allowed to assume so maritime a denomination), who now takes up the pen to address you--it is not, I repeat, for one so circumstanced, to adopt the language of compliment, or of congratulation. That he leaves to abler and to purer hands.\n\n‘If your more important avocations should admit of your ever tracing these imperfect characters thus far--which may be, or may not be, as circumstances arise--you will naturally inquire by what object am I influenced, then, in inditing the present missive? Allow me to say that I fully defer to the reasonable character of that inquiry, and proceed to develop it; premising that it is not an object of a pecuniary nature.\n\n‘Without more directly referring to any latent ability that may possibly exist on my part, of wielding the thunderbolt, or directing the devouring and avenging flame in any quarter, I may be permitted to observe, in passing, that my brightest visions are for ever dispelled--that my peace is shattered and my power of enjoyment destroyed--that my heart is no longer in the right place--and that I no more walk erect before my fellow man. The canker is in the flower. The cup is bitter to the brim. The worm is at his work, and will soon dispose of his victim. The sooner the better. But I will not digress. ‘Placed in a mental position of peculiar painfulness, beyond the assuaging reach even of Mrs. Micawber’s influence, though exercised in the tripartite character of woman, wife, and mother, it is my intention to fly from myself for a short period, and devote a respite of eight-and-forty hours to revisiting some metropolitan scenes of past enjoyment. Among other havens of domestic tranquillity and peace of mind, my feet will naturally tend towards the King’s Bench Prison. In stating that I shall be (D. V.) on the outside of the south wall of that place of incarceration on civil process, the day after tomorrow, at seven in the evening, precisely, my object in this epistolary communication is accomplished.\n\n‘I do not feel warranted in soliciting my former friend Mr. Copperfield, or my former friend Mr. Thomas Traddles of the Inner Temple, if that gentleman is still existent and forthcoming, to condescend to meet me, and renew (so far as may be) our past relations of the olden time. I confine myself to throwing out the observation, that, at the hour and place I have indicated, may be found such ruined vestiges as yet\n\n‘Remain, ‘Of ‘A ‘Fallen Tower, ‘WILKINS MICAWBER.\n\n‘P.S. It may be advisable to superadd to the above, the statement that Mrs. Micawber is not in confidential possession of my intentions.’\n\nI read the letter over several times. Making due allowance for Mr. Micawber’s lofty style of composition, and for the extraordinary relish with which he sat down and wrote long letters on all possible and impossible occasions, I still believed that something important lay hidden at the bottom of this roundabout communication. I put it down, to think about it; and took it up again, to read it once more; and was still pursuing it, when Traddles found me in the height of my perplexity.\n\n‘My dear fellow,’ said I, ‘I never was better pleased to see you. You come to give me the benefit of your sober judgement at a most opportune time. I have received a very singular letter, Traddles, from Mr. Micawber.’\n\n‘No?’ cried Traddles. ‘You don’t say so? And I have received one from Mrs. Micawber!’\n\nWith that, Traddles, who was flushed with walking, and whose hair, under the combined effects of exercise and excitement, stood on end as if he saw a cheerful ghost, produced his letter and made an exchange with me. I watched him into the heart of Mr. Micawber’s letter, and returned the elevation of eyebrows with which he said “‘Wielding the thunderbolt, or directing the devouring and avenging flame!” Bless me, Copperfield!’--and then entered on the perusal of Mrs. Micawber’s epistle.\n\nIt ran thus:\n\n‘My best regards to Mr. Thomas Traddles, and if he should still remember one who formerly had the happiness of being well acquainted with him, may I beg a few moments of his leisure time? I assure Mr. T. T. that I would not intrude upon his kindness, were I in any other position than on the confines of distraction.\n\n‘Though harrowing to myself to mention, the alienation of Mr. Micawber (formerly so domesticated) from his wife and family, is the cause of my addressing my unhappy appeal to Mr. Traddles, and soliciting his best indulgence. Mr. T. can form no adequate idea of the change in Mr. Micawber’s conduct, of his wildness, of his violence. It has gradually augmented, until it assumes the appearance of aberration of intellect. Scarcely a day passes, I assure Mr. Traddles, on which some paroxysm does not take place. Mr. T. will not require me to depict my feelings, when I inform him that I have become accustomed to hear Mr. Micawber assert that he has sold himself to the D. Mystery and secrecy have long been his principal characteristic, have long replaced unlimited confidence. The slightest provocation, even being asked if there is anything he would prefer for dinner, causes him to express a wish for a separation. Last night, on being childishly solicited for twopence, to buy ‘lemon-stunners’--a local sweetmeat--he presented an oyster-knife at the twins!\n\n‘I entreat Mr. Traddles to bear with me in entering into these details. Without them, Mr. T. would indeed find it difficult to form the faintest conception of my heart-rending situation.\n\n‘May I now venture to confide to Mr. T. the purport of my letter? Will he now allow me to throw myself on his friendly consideration? Oh yes, for I know his heart!\n\n‘The quick eye of affection is not easily blinded, when of the female sex. Mr. Micawber is going to London. Though he studiously concealed his hand, this morning before breakfast, in writing the direction-card which he attached to the little brown valise of happier days, the eagle-glance of matrimonial anxiety detected, d, o, n, distinctly traced. The West-End destination of the coach, is the Golden Cross. Dare I fervently implore Mr. T. to see my misguided husband, and to reason with him? Dare I ask Mr. T. to endeavour to step in between Mr. Micawber and his agonized family? Oh no, for that would be too much!\n\n‘If Mr. Copperfield should yet remember one unknown to fame, will Mr. T. take charge of my unalterable regards and similar entreaties? In any case, he will have the benevolence to consider this communication strictly private, and on no account whatever to be alluded to, however distantly, in the presence of Mr. Micawber. If Mr. T. should ever reply to it (which I cannot but feel to be most improbable), a letter addressed to M. E., Post Office, Canterbury, will be fraught with less painful consequences than any addressed immediately to one, who subscribes herself, in extreme distress,\n\n‘Mr. Thomas Traddles’s respectful friend and suppliant,\n\n‘What do you think of that letter?’ said Traddles, casting his eyes upon me, when I had read it twice.\n\n‘What do you think of the other?’ said I. For he was still reading it with knitted brows.\n\n‘I think that the two together, Copperfield,’ replied Traddles, ‘mean more than Mr. and Mrs. Micawber usually mean in their correspondence--but I don’t know what. They are both written in good faith, I have no doubt, and without any collusion. Poor thing!’ he was now alluding to Mrs. Micawber’s letter, and we were standing side by side comparing the two; ‘it will be a charity to write to her, at all events, and tell her that we will not fail to see Mr. Micawber.’\n\nI acceded to this the more readily, because I now reproached myself with having treated her former letter rather lightly. It had set me thinking a good deal at the time, as I have mentioned in its place; but my absorption in my own affairs, my experience of the family, and my hearing nothing more, had gradually ended in my dismissing the subject. I had often thought of the Micawbers, but chiefly to wonder what ‘pecuniary liabilities’ they were establishing in Canterbury, and to recall how shy Mr. Micawber was of me when he became clerk to Uriah Heep.\n\nHowever, I now wrote a comforting letter to Mrs. Micawber, in our joint names, and we both signed it. As we walked into town to post it, Traddles and I held a long conference, and launched into a number of speculations, which I need not repeat. We took my aunt into our counsels in the afternoon; but our only decided conclusion was, that we would be very punctual in keeping Mr. Micawber’s appointment.\n\nAlthough we appeared at the stipulated place a quarter of an hour before the time, we found Mr. Micawber already there. He was standing with his arms folded, over against the wall, looking at the spikes on the top, with a sentimental expression, as if they were the interlacing boughs of trees that had shaded him in his youth.\n\nWhen we accosted him, his manner was something more confused, and something less genteel, than of yore. He had relinquished his legal suit of black for the purposes of this excursion, and wore the old surtout and tights, but not quite with the old air. He gradually picked up more and more of it as we conversed with him; but, his very eye-glass seemed to hang less easily, and his shirt-collar, though still of the old formidable dimensions, rather drooped.\n\n‘Gentlemen!’ said Mr. Micawber, after the first salutations, ‘you are friends in need, and friends indeed. Allow me to offer my inquiries with reference to the physical welfare of Mrs. Copperfield in esse, and Mrs. Traddles in posse,--presuming, that is to say, that my friend Mr. Traddles is not yet united to the object of his affections, for weal and for woe.’\n\nWe acknowledged his politeness, and made suitable replies. He then directed our attention to the wall, and was beginning, ‘I assure you, gentlemen,’ when I ventured to object to that ceremonious form of address, and to beg that he would speak to us in the old way.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ he returned, pressing my hand, ‘your cordiality overpowers me. This reception of a shattered fragment of the Temple once called Man--if I may be permitted so to express myself--bespeaks a heart that is an honour to our common nature. I was about to observe that I again behold the serene spot where some of the happiest hours of my existence fleeted by.’\n\n‘Made so, I am sure, by Mrs. Micawber,’ said I. ‘I hope she is well?’\n\n‘Thank you,’ returned Mr. Micawber, whose face clouded at this reference, ‘she is but so-so. And this,’ said Mr. Micawber, nodding his head sorrowfully, ‘is the Bench! Where, for the first time in many revolving years, the overwhelming pressure of pecuniary liabilities was not proclaimed, from day to day, by importune voices declining to vacate the passage; where there was no knocker on the door for any creditor to appeal to; where personal service of process was not required, and detainees were merely lodged at the gate! Gentlemen,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘when the shadow of that iron-work on the summit of the brick structure has been reflected on the gravel of the Parade, I have seen my children thread the mazes of the intricate pattern, avoiding the dark marks. I have been familiar with every stone in the place. If I betray weakness, you will know how to excuse me.’\n\n‘We have all got on in life since then, Mr. Micawber,’ said I.\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ returned Mr. Micawber, bitterly, ‘when I was an inmate of that retreat I could look my fellow-man in the face, and punch his head if he offended me. My fellow-man and myself are no longer on those glorious terms!’\n\nTurning from the building in a downcast manner, Mr. Micawber accepted my proffered arm on one side, and the proffered arm of Traddles on the other, and walked away between us.\n\n‘There are some landmarks,’ observed Mr. Micawber, looking fondly back over his shoulder, ‘on the road to the tomb, which, but for the impiety of the aspiration, a man would wish never to have passed. Such is the Bench in my chequered career.’\n\n‘Oh, you are in low spirits, Mr. Micawber,’ said Traddles.\n\n‘I am, sir,’ interposed Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘I hope,’ said Traddles, ‘it is not because you have conceived a dislike to the law--for I am a lawyer myself, you know.’\n\nMr. Micawber answered not a word.\n\n‘How is our friend Heep, Mr. Micawber?’ said I, after a silence.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ returned Mr. Micawber, bursting into a state of much excitement, and turning pale, ‘if you ask after my employer as YOUR friend, I am sorry for it; if you ask after him as MY friend, I sardonically smile at it. In whatever capacity you ask after my employer, I beg, without offence to you, to limit my reply to this--that whatever his state of health may be, his appearance is foxy: not to say diabolical. You will allow me, as a private individual, to decline pursuing a subject which has lashed me to the utmost verge of desperation in my professional capacity.’\n\nI expressed my regret for having innocently touched upon a theme that roused him so much. ‘May I ask,’ said I, ‘without any hazard of repeating the mistake, how my old friends Mr. and Miss Wickfield are?’\n\n‘Miss Wickfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, now turning red, ‘is, as she always is, a pattern, and a bright example. My dear Copperfield, she is the only starry spot in a miserable existence. My respect for that young lady, my admiration of her character, my devotion to her for her love and truth, and goodness!--Take me,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘down a turning, for, upon my soul, in my present state of mind I am not equal to this!’\n\nWe wheeled him off into a narrow street, where he took out his pocket-handkerchief, and stood with his back to a wall. If I looked as gravely at him as Traddles did, he must have found our company by no means inspiriting.\n\n‘It is my fate,’ said Mr. Micawber, unfeignedly sobbing, but doing even that, with a shadow of the old expression of doing something genteel; ‘it is my fate, gentlemen, that the finer feelings of our nature have become reproaches to me. My homage to Miss Wickfield, is a flight of arrows in my bosom. You had better leave me, if you please, to walk the earth as a vagabond. The worm will settle my business in double-quick time.’\n\nWithout attending to this invocation, we stood by, until he put up his pocket-handkerchief, pulled up his shirt-collar, and, to delude any person in the neighbourhood who might have been observing him, hummed a tune with his hat very much on one side. I then mentioned--not knowing what might be lost if we lost sight of him yet--that it would give me great pleasure to introduce him to my aunt, if he would ride out to Highgate, where a bed was at his service.\n\n‘You shall make us a glass of your own punch, Mr. Micawber,’ said I, ‘and forget whatever you have on your mind, in pleasanter reminiscences.’\n\n‘Or, if confiding anything to friends will be more likely to relieve you, you shall impart it to us, Mr. Micawber,’ said Traddles, prudently.\n\n‘Gentlemen,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘do with me as you will! I am a straw upon the surface of the deep, and am tossed in all directions by the elephants--I beg your pardon; I should have said the elements.’\n\nWe walked on, arm-in-arm, again; found the coach in the act of starting; and arrived at Highgate without encountering any difficulties by the way. I was very uneasy and very uncertain in my mind what to say or do for the best--so was Traddles, evidently. Mr. Micawber was for the most part plunged into deep gloom. He occasionally made an attempt to smarten himself, and hum the fag-end of a tune; but his relapses into profound melancholy were only made the more impressive by the mockery of a hat exceedingly on one side, and a shirt-collar pulled up to his eyes.\n\nWe went to my aunt’s house rather than to mine, because of Dora’s not being well. My aunt presented herself on being sent for, and welcomed Mr. Micawber with gracious cordiality. Mr. Micawber kissed her hand, retired to the window, and pulling out his pocket-handkerchief, had a mental wrestle with himself.\n\nMr. Dick was at home. He was by nature so exceedingly compassionate of anyone who seemed to be ill at ease, and was so quick to find any such person out, that he shook hands with Mr. Micawber, at least half-a-dozen times in five minutes. To Mr. Micawber, in his trouble, this warmth, on the part of a stranger, was so extremely touching, that he could only say, on the occasion of each successive shake, ‘My dear sir, you overpower me!’ Which gratified Mr. Dick so much, that he went at it again with greater vigour than before.\n\n‘The friendliness of this gentleman,’ said Mr. Micawber to my aunt, ‘if you will allow me, ma’am, to cull a figure of speech from the vocabulary of our coarser national sports--floors me. To a man who is struggling with a complicated burden of perplexity and disquiet, such a reception is trying, I assure you.’\n\n‘My friend Mr. Dick,’ replied my aunt proudly, ‘is not a common man.’\n\n‘That I am convinced of,’ said Mr. Micawber. ‘My dear sir!’ for Mr. Dick was shaking hands with him again; ‘I am deeply sensible of your cordiality!’\n\n‘How do you find yourself?’ said Mr. Dick, with an anxious look.\n\n‘Indifferent, my dear sir,’ returned Mr. Micawber, sighing.\n\n‘You must keep up your spirits,’ said Mr. Dick, ‘and make yourself as comfortable as possible.’\n\nMr. Micawber was quite overcome by these friendly words, and by finding Mr. Dick’s hand again within his own. ‘It has been my lot,’ he observed, ‘to meet, in the diversified panorama of human existence, with an occasional oasis, but never with one so green, so gushing, as the present!’\n\nAt another time I should have been amused by this; but I felt that we were all constrained and uneasy, and I watched Mr. Micawber so anxiously, in his vacillations between an evident disposition to reveal something, and a counter-disposition to reveal nothing, that I was in a perfect fever. Traddles, sitting on the edge of his chair, with his eyes wide open, and his hair more emphatically erect than ever, stared by turns at the ground and at Mr. Micawber, without so much as attempting to put in a word. My aunt, though I saw that her shrewdest observation was concentrated on her new guest, had more useful possession of her wits than either of us; for she held him in conversation, and made it necessary for him to talk, whether he liked it or not.\n\n‘You are a very old friend of my nephew’s, Mr. Micawber,’ said my aunt. ‘I wish I had had the pleasure of seeing you before.’\n\n‘Madam,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘I wish I had had the honour of knowing you at an earlier period. I was not always the wreck you at present behold.’\n\n‘I hope Mrs. Micawber and your family are well, sir,’ said my aunt.\n\nMr. Micawber inclined his head. ‘They are as well, ma’am,’ he desperately observed after a pause, ‘as Aliens and Outcasts can ever hope to be.’\n\n‘Lord bless you, sir!’ exclaimed my aunt, in her abrupt way. ‘What are you talking about?’\n\n‘The subsistence of my family, ma’am,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘trembles in the balance. My employer--’\n\nHere Mr. Micawber provokingly left off; and began to peel the lemons that had been under my directions set before him, together with all the other appliances he used in making punch.\n\n‘Your employer, you know,’ said Mr. Dick, jogging his arm as a gentle reminder.\n\n‘My good sir,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘you recall me, I am obliged to you.’ They shook hands again. ‘My employer, ma’am--Mr. Heep--once did me the favour to observe to me, that if I were not in the receipt of the stipendiary emoluments appertaining to my engagement with him, I should probably be a mountebank about the country, swallowing a sword-blade, and eating the devouring element. For anything that I can perceive to the contrary, it is still probable that my children may be reduced to seek a livelihood by personal contortion, while Mrs. Micawber abets their unnatural feats by playing the barrel-organ.’\n\nMr. Micawber, with a random but expressive flourish of his knife, signified that these performances might be expected to take place after he was no more; then resumed his peeling with a desperate air.\n\nMy aunt leaned her elbow on the little round table that she usually kept beside her, and eyed him attentively. Notwithstanding the aversion with which I regarded the idea of entrapping him into any disclosure he was not prepared to make voluntarily, I should have taken him up at this point, but for the strange proceedings in which I saw him engaged; whereof his putting the lemon-peel into the kettle, the sugar into the snuffer-tray, the spirit into the empty jug, and confidently attempting to pour boiling water out of a candlestick, were among the most remarkable. I saw that a crisis was at hand, and it came. He clattered all his means and implements together, rose from his chair, pulled out his pocket-handkerchief, and burst into tears.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, behind his handkerchief, ‘this is an occupation, of all others, requiring an untroubled mind, and self-respect. I cannot perform it. It is out of the question.’\n\n‘Mr. Micawber,’ said I, ‘what is the matter? Pray speak out. You are among friends.’\n\n‘Among friends, sir!’ repeated Mr. Micawber; and all he had reserved came breaking out of him. ‘Good heavens, it is principally because I AM among friends that my state of mind is what it is. What is the matter, gentlemen? What is NOT the matter? Villainy is the matter; baseness is the matter; deception, fraud, conspiracy, are the matter; and the name of the whole atrocious mass is--HEEP!’\n\nMy aunt clapped her hands, and we all started up as if we were possessed.\n\n‘The struggle is over!’ said Mr. Micawber violently gesticulating with his pocket-handkerchief, and fairly striking out from time to time with both arms, as if he were swimming under superhuman difficulties. ‘I will lead this life no longer. I am a wretched being, cut off from everything that makes life tolerable. I have been under a Taboo in that infernal scoundrel’s service. Give me back my wife, give me back my family, substitute Micawber for the petty wretch who walks about in the boots at present on my feet, and call upon me to swallow a sword tomorrow, and I’ll do it. With an appetite!’\n\nI never saw a man so hot in my life. I tried to calm him, that we might come to something rational; but he got hotter and hotter, and wouldn’t hear a word.\n\n‘I’ll put my hand in no man’s hand,’ said Mr. Micawber, gasping, puffing, and sobbing, to that degree that he was like a man fighting with cold water, ‘until I have--blown to fragments--the--a--detestable--serpent--HEEP! I’ll partake of no one’s hospitality, until I have--a--moved Mount Vesuvius--to eruption--on--a--the abandoned rascal--HEEP! Refreshment--a--underneath this roof--particularly punch--would--a--choke me--unless--I had--previously--choked the eyes--out of the head--a--of--interminable cheat, and liar--HEEP! I--a--I’ll know nobody--and--a--say nothing--and--a--live nowhere--until I have crushed--to--a--undiscoverable atoms--the--transcendent and immortal hypocrite and perjurer--HEEP!’\n\nI really had some fear of Mr. Micawber’s dying on the spot. The manner in which he struggled through these inarticulate sentences, and, whenever he found himself getting near the name of Heep, fought his way on to it, dashed at it in a fainting state, and brought it out with a vehemence little less than marvellous, was frightful; but now, when he sank into a chair, steaming, and looked at us, with every possible colour in his face that had no business there, and an endless procession of lumps following one another in hot haste up his throat, whence they seemed to shoot into his forehead, he had the appearance of being in the last extremity. I would have gone to his assistance, but he waved me off, and wouldn’t hear a word.\n\n‘No, Copperfield!--No communication--a--until--Miss Wickfield--a--redress from wrongs inflicted by consummate scoundrel--HEEP!’ (I am quite convinced he could not have uttered three words, but for the amazing energy with which this word inspired him when he felt it coming.) ‘Inviolable secret--a--from the whole world--a--no exceptions--this day week--a--at breakfast-time--a--everybody present--including aunt--a--and extremely friendly gentleman--to be at the hotel at Canterbury--a--where--Mrs. Micawber and myself--Auld Lang Syne in chorus--and--a--will expose intolerable ruffian--HEEP! No more to say--a--or listen to persuasion--go immediately--not capable--a--bear society--upon the track of devoted and doomed traitor--HEEP!’\n\nWith this last repetition of the magic word that had kept him going at all, and in which he surpassed all his previous efforts, Mr. Micawber rushed out of the house; leaving us in a state of excitement, hope, and wonder, that reduced us to a condition little better than his own. But even then his passion for writing letters was too strong to be resisted; for while we were yet in the height of our excitement, hope, and wonder, the following pastoral note was brought to me from a neighbouring tavern, at which he had called to write it:--\n\n‘Most secret and confidential. ‘MY DEAR SIR,\n\n‘I beg to be allowed to convey, through you, my apologies to your excellent aunt for my late excitement. An explosion of a smouldering volcano long suppressed, was the result of an internal contest more easily conceived than described.\n\n‘I trust I rendered tolerably intelligible my appointment for the morning of this day week, at the house of public entertainment at Canterbury, where Mrs. Micawber and myself had once the honour of uniting our voices to yours, in the well-known strain of the Immortal exciseman nurtured beyond the Tweed.\n\n‘The duty done, and act of reparation performed, which can alone enable me to contemplate my fellow mortal, I shall be known no more. I shall simply require to be deposited in that place of universal resort, where\n\nEach in his narrow cell for ever laid, The rude forefathers of the hamlet sleep,\n\n‘--With the plain Inscription,\n\n# CHAPTER 50. Mr. PEGGOTTY’S DREAM COMES TRUE\n\nBy this time, some months had passed since our interview on the bank of the river with Martha. I had never seen her since, but she had communicated with Mr. Peggotty on several occasions. Nothing had come of her zealous intervention; nor could I infer, from what he told me, that any clue had been obtained, for a moment, to Emily’s fate. I confess that I began to despair of her recovery, and gradually to sink deeper and deeper into the belief that she was dead.\n\nHis conviction remained unchanged. So far as I know--and I believe his honest heart was transparent to me--he never wavered again, in his solemn certainty of finding her. His patience never tired. And, although I trembled for the agony it might one day be to him to have his strong assurance shivered at a blow, there was something so religious in it, so affectingly expressive of its anchor being in the purest depths of his fine nature, that the respect and honour in which I held him were exalted every day.\n\nHis was not a lazy trustfulness that hoped, and did no more. He had been a man of sturdy action all his life, and he knew that in all things wherein he wanted help he must do his own part faithfully, and help himself. I have known him set out in the night, on a misgiving that the light might not be, by some accident, in the window of the old boat, and walk to Yarmouth. I have known him, on reading something in the newspaper that might apply to her, take up his stick, and go forth on a journey of three--or four-score miles. He made his way by sea to Naples, and back, after hearing the narrative to which Miss Dartle had assisted me. All his journeys were ruggedly performed; for he was always steadfast in a purpose of saving money for Emily’s sake, when she should be found. In all this long pursuit, I never heard him repine; I never heard him say he was fatigued, or out of heart.\n\nDora had often seen him since our marriage, and was quite fond of him. I fancy his figure before me now, standing near her sofa, with his rough cap in his hand, and the blue eyes of my child-wife raised, with a timid wonder, to his face. Sometimes of an evening, about twilight, when he came to talk with me, I would induce him to smoke his pipe in the garden, as we slowly paced to and fro together; and then, the picture of his deserted home, and the comfortable air it used to have in my childish eyes of an evening when the fire was burning, and the wind moaning round it, came most vividly into my mind.\n\nOne evening, at this hour, he told me that he had found Martha waiting near his lodging on the preceding night when he came out, and that she had asked him not to leave London on any account, until he should have seen her again.\n\n‘Did she tell you why?’ I inquired.\n\n‘I asked her, Mas’r Davy,’ he replied, ‘but it is but few words as she ever says, and she on’y got my promise and so went away.’\n\n‘Did she say when you might expect to see her again?’ I demanded.\n\n‘No, Mas’r Davy,’ he returned, drawing his hand thoughtfully down his face. ‘I asked that too; but it was more (she said) than she could tell.’\n\nAs I had long forborne to encourage him with hopes that hung on threads, I made no other comment on this information than that I supposed he would see her soon. Such speculations as it engendered within me I kept to myself, and those were faint enough.\n\nI was walking alone in the garden, one evening, about a fortnight afterwards. I remember that evening well. It was the second in Mr. Micawber’s week of suspense. There had been rain all day, and there was a damp feeling in the air. The leaves were thick upon the trees, and heavy with wet; but the rain had ceased, though the sky was still dark; and the hopeful birds were singing cheerfully. As I walked to and fro in the garden, and the twilight began to close around me, their little voices were hushed; and that peculiar silence which belongs to such an evening in the country when the lightest trees are quite still, save for the occasional droppings from their boughs, prevailed.\n\nThere was a little green perspective of trellis-work and ivy at the side of our cottage, through which I could see, from the garden where I was walking, into the road before the house. I happened to turn my eyes towards this place, as I was thinking of many things; and I saw a figure beyond, dressed in a plain cloak. It was bending eagerly towards me, and beckoning.\n\n‘Martha!’ said I, going to it.\n\n‘Can you come with me?’ she inquired, in an agitated whisper. ‘I have been to him, and he is not at home. I wrote down where he was to come, and left it on his table with my own hand. They said he would not be out long. I have tidings for him. Can you come directly?’\n\nMy answer was, to pass out at the gate immediately. She made a hasty gesture with her hand, as if to entreat my patience and my silence, and turned towards London, whence, as her dress betokened, she had come expeditiously on foot.\n\nI asked her if that were not our destination? On her motioning Yes, with the same hasty gesture as before, I stopped an empty coach that was coming by, and we got into it. When I asked her where the coachman was to drive, she answered, ‘Anywhere near Golden Square! And quick!’--then shrunk into a corner, with one trembling hand before her face, and the other making the former gesture, as if she could not bear a voice.\n\nNow much disturbed, and dazzled with conflicting gleams of hope and dread, I looked at her for some explanation. But seeing how strongly she desired to remain quiet, and feeling that it was my own natural inclination too, at such a time, I did not attempt to break the silence. We proceeded without a word being spoken. Sometimes she glanced out of the window, as though she thought we were going slowly, though indeed we were going fast; but otherwise remained exactly as at first.\n\nWe alighted at one of the entrances to the Square she had mentioned, where I directed the coach to wait, not knowing but that we might have some occasion for it. She laid her hand on my arm, and hurried me on to one of the sombre streets, of which there are several in that part, where the houses were once fair dwellings in the occupation of single families, but have, and had, long degenerated into poor lodgings let off in rooms. Entering at the open door of one of these, and releasing my arm, she beckoned me to follow her up the common staircase, which was like a tributary channel to the street.\n\nThe house swarmed with inmates. As we went up, doors of rooms were opened and people’s heads put out; and we passed other people on the stairs, who were coming down. In glancing up from the outside, before we entered, I had seen women and children lolling at the windows over flower-pots; and we seemed to have attracted their curiosity, for these were principally the observers who looked out of their doors. It was a broad panelled staircase, with massive balustrades of some dark wood; cornices above the doors, ornamented with carved fruit and flowers; and broad seats in the windows. But all these tokens of past grandeur were miserably decayed and dirty; rot, damp, and age, had weakened the flooring, which in many places was unsound and even unsafe. Some attempts had been made, I noticed, to infuse new blood into this dwindling frame, by repairing the costly old wood-work here and there with common deal; but it was like the marriage of a reduced old noble to a plebeian pauper, and each party to the ill-assorted union shrunk away from the other. Several of the back windows on the staircase had been darkened or wholly blocked up. In those that remained, there was scarcely any glass; and, through the crumbling frames by which the bad air seemed always to come in, and never to go out, I saw, through other glassless windows, into other houses in a similar condition, and looked giddily down into a wretched yard, which was the common dust-heap of the mansion.\n\nWe proceeded to the top-storey of the house. Two or three times, by the way, I thought I observed in the indistinct light the skirts of a female figure going up before us. As we turned to ascend the last flight of stairs between us and the roof, we caught a full view of this figure pausing for a moment, at a door. Then it turned the handle, and went in.\n\n‘What’s this!’ said Martha, in a whisper. ‘She has gone into my room. I don’t know her!’\n\nI knew her. I had recognized her with amazement, for Miss Dartle.\n\nI said something to the effect that it was a lady whom I had seen before, in a few words, to my conductress; and had scarcely done so, when we heard her voice in the room, though not, from where we stood, what she was saying. Martha, with an astonished look, repeated her former action, and softly led me up the stairs; and then, by a little back-door which seemed to have no lock, and which she pushed open with a touch, into a small empty garret with a low sloping roof, little better than a cupboard. Between this, and the room she had called hers, there was a small door of communication, standing partly open. Here we stopped, breathless with our ascent, and she placed her hand lightly on my lips. I could only see, of the room beyond, that it was pretty large; that there was a bed in it; and that there were some common pictures of ships upon the walls. I could not see Miss Dartle, or the person whom we had heard her address. Certainly, my companion could not, for my position was the best. A dead silence prevailed for some moments. Martha kept one hand on my lips, and raised the other in a listening attitude.\n\n‘It matters little to me her not being at home,’ said Rosa Dartle haughtily, ‘I know nothing of her. It is you I come to see.’\n\n‘Me?’ replied a soft voice.\n\nAt the sound of it, a thrill went through my frame. For it was Emily’s!\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Miss Dartle, ‘I have come to look at you. What? You are not ashamed of the face that has done so much?’\n\nThe resolute and unrelenting hatred of her tone, its cold stern sharpness, and its mastered rage, presented her before me, as if I had seen her standing in the light. I saw the flashing black eyes, and the passion-wasted figure; and I saw the scar, with its white track cutting through her lips, quivering and throbbing as she spoke.\n\n‘I have come to see,’ she said, ‘James Steerforth’s fancy; the girl who ran away with him, and is the town-talk of the commonest people of her native place; the bold, flaunting, practised companion of persons like James Steerforth. I want to know what such a thing is like.’\n\nThere was a rustle, as if the unhappy girl, on whom she heaped these taunts, ran towards the door, and the speaker swiftly interposed herself before it. It was succeeded by a moment’s pause.\n\nWhen Miss Dartle spoke again, it was through her set teeth, and with a stamp upon the ground.\n\n‘Stay there!’ she said, ‘or I’ll proclaim you to the house, and the whole street! If you try to evade me, I’ll stop you, if it’s by the hair, and raise the very stones against you!’\n\nA frightened murmur was the only reply that reached my ears. A silence succeeded. I did not know what to do. Much as I desired to put an end to the interview, I felt that I had no right to present myself; that it was for Mr. Peggotty alone to see her and recover her. Would he never come? I thought impatiently.\n\n‘So!’ said Rosa Dartle, with a contemptuous laugh, ‘I see her at last! Why, he was a poor creature to be taken by that delicate mock-modesty, and that hanging head!’\n\n‘Oh, for Heaven’s sake, spare me!’ exclaimed Emily. ‘Whoever you are, you know my pitiable story, and for Heaven’s sake spare me, if you would be spared yourself!’\n\n‘If I would be spared!’ returned the other fiercely; ‘what is there in common between US, do you think!’\n\n‘Nothing but our sex,’ said Emily, with a burst of tears.\n\n‘And that,’ said Rosa Dartle, ‘is so strong a claim, preferred by one so infamous, that if I had any feeling in my breast but scorn and abhorrence of you, it would freeze it up. Our sex! You are an honour to our sex!’\n\n‘I have deserved this,’ said Emily, ‘but it’s dreadful! Dear, dear lady, think what I have suffered, and how I am fallen! Oh, Martha, come back! Oh, home, home!’\n\nMiss Dartle placed herself in a chair, within view of the door, and looked downward, as if Emily were crouching on the floor before her. Being now between me and the light, I could see her curled lip, and her cruel eyes intently fixed on one place, with a greedy triumph.\n\n‘Listen to what I say!’ she said; ‘and reserve your false arts for your dupes. Do you hope to move me by your tears? No more than you could charm me by your smiles, you purchased slave.’\n\n‘Oh, have some mercy on me!’ cried Emily. ‘Show me some compassion, or I shall die mad!’\n\n‘It would be no great penance,’ said Rosa Dartle, ‘for your crimes. Do you know what you have done? Do you ever think of the home you have laid waste?’\n\n‘Oh, is there ever night or day, when I don’t think of it!’ cried Emily; and now I could just see her, on her knees, with her head thrown back, her pale face looking upward, her hands wildly clasped and held out, and her hair streaming about her. ‘Has there ever been a single minute, waking or sleeping, when it hasn’t been before me, just as it used to be in the lost days when I turned my back upon it for ever and for ever! Oh, home, home! Oh dear, dear uncle, if you ever could have known the agony your love would cause me when I fell away from good, you never would have shown it to me so constant, much as you felt it; but would have been angry to me, at least once in my life, that I might have had some comfort! I have none, none, no comfort upon earth, for all of them were always fond of me!’ She dropped on her face, before the imperious figure in the chair, with an imploring effort to clasp the skirt of her dress.\n\nRosa Dartle sat looking down upon her, as inflexible as a figure of brass. Her lips were tightly compressed, as if she knew that she must keep a strong constraint upon herself--I write what I sincerely believe--or she would be tempted to strike the beautiful form with her foot. I saw her, distinctly, and the whole power of her face and character seemed forced into that expression.---Would he never come?\n\n‘The miserable vanity of these earth-worms!’ she said, when she had so far controlled the angry heavings of her breast, that she could trust herself to speak. ‘YOUR home! Do you imagine that I bestow a thought on it, or suppose you could do any harm to that low place, which money would not pay for, and handsomely? YOUR home! You were a part of the trade of your home, and were bought and sold like any other vendible thing your people dealt in.’\n\n‘Oh, not that!’ cried Emily. ‘Say anything of me; but don’t visit my disgrace and shame, more than I have done, on folks who are as honourable as you! Have some respect for them, as you are a lady, if you have no mercy for me.’\n\n‘I speak,’ she said, not deigning to take any heed of this appeal, and drawing away her dress from the contamination of Emily’s touch, ‘I speak of HIS home--where I live. Here,’ she said, stretching out her hand with her contemptuous laugh, and looking down upon the prostrate girl, ‘is a worthy cause of division between lady-mother and gentleman-son; of grief in a house where she wouldn’t have been admitted as a kitchen-girl; of anger, and repining, and reproach. This piece of pollution, picked up from the water-side, to be made much of for an hour, and then tossed back to her original place!’\n\n‘No! no!’ cried Emily, clasping her hands together. ‘When he first came into my way--that the day had never dawned upon me, and he had met me being carried to my grave!--I had been brought up as virtuous as you or any lady, and was going to be the wife of as good a man as you or any lady in the world can ever marry. If you live in his home and know him, you know, perhaps, what his power with a weak, vain girl might be. I don’t defend myself, but I know well, and he knows well, or he will know when he comes to die, and his mind is troubled with it, that he used all his power to deceive me, and that I believed him, trusted him, and loved him!’\n\nRosa Dartle sprang up from her seat; recoiled; and in recoiling struck at her, with a face of such malignity, so darkened and disfigured by passion, that I had almost thrown myself between them. The blow, which had no aim, fell upon the air. As she now stood panting, looking at her with the utmost detestation that she was capable of expressing, and trembling from head to foot with rage and scorn, I thought I had never seen such a sight, and never could see such another.\n\n‘YOU love him? You?’ she cried, with her clenched hand, quivering as if it only wanted a weapon to stab the object of her wrath.\n\nEmily had shrunk out of my view. There was no reply.\n\n‘And tell that to ME,’ she added, ‘with your shameful lips? Why don’t they whip these creatures? If I could order it to be done, I would have this girl whipped to death.’\n\nAnd so she would, I have no doubt. I would not have trusted her with the rack itself, while that furious look lasted. She slowly, very slowly, broke into a laugh, and pointed at Emily with her hand, as if she were a sight of shame for gods and men.\n\n‘SHE love!’ she said. ‘THAT carrion! And he ever cared for her, she’d tell me. Ha, ha! The liars that these traders are!’\n\nHer mockery was worse than her undisguised rage. Of the two, I would have much preferred to be the object of the latter. But, when she suffered it to break loose, it was only for a moment. She had chained it up again, and however it might tear her within, she subdued it to herself.\n\n‘I came here, you pure fountain of love,’ she said, ‘to see--as I began by telling you--what such a thing as you was like. I was curious. I am satisfied. Also to tell you, that you had best seek that home of yours, with all speed, and hide your head among those excellent people who are expecting you, and whom your money will console. When it’s all gone, you can believe, and trust, and love again, you know! I thought you a broken toy that had lasted its time; a worthless spangle that was tarnished, and thrown away. But, finding you true gold, a very lady, and an ill-used innocent, with a fresh heart full of love and trustfulness--which you look like, and is quite consistent with your story!--I have something more to say. Attend to it; for what I say I’ll do. Do you hear me, you fairy spirit? What I say, I mean to do!’\n\nHer rage got the better of her again, for a moment; but it passed over her face like a spasm, and left her smiling.\n\n‘Hide yourself,’ she pursued, ‘if not at home, somewhere. Let it be somewhere beyond reach; in some obscure life--or, better still, in some obscure death. I wonder, if your loving heart will not break, you have found no way of helping it to be still! I have heard of such means sometimes. I believe they may be easily found.’\n\nA low crying, on the part of Emily, interrupted her here. She stopped, and listened to it as if it were music.\n\n‘I am of a strange nature, perhaps,’ Rosa Dartle went on; ‘but I can’t breathe freely in the air you breathe. I find it sickly. Therefore, I will have it cleared; I will have it purified of you. If you live here tomorrow, I’ll have your story and your character proclaimed on the common stair. There are decent women in the house, I am told; and it is a pity such a light as you should be among them, and concealed. If, leaving here, you seek any refuge in this town in any character but your true one (which you are welcome to bear, without molestation from me), the same service shall be done you, if I hear of your retreat. Being assisted by a gentleman who not long ago aspired to the favour of your hand, I am sanguine as to that.’\n\nWould he never, never come? How long was I to bear this? How long could I bear it? ‘Oh me, oh me!’ exclaimed the wretched Emily, in a tone that might have touched the hardest heart, I should have thought; but there was no relenting in Rosa Dartle’s smile. ‘What, what, shall I do!’\n\n‘Do?’ returned the other. ‘Live happy in your own reflections! Consecrate your existence to the recollection of James Steerforth’s tenderness--he would have made you his serving-man’s wife, would he not?---or to feeling grateful to the upright and deserving creature who would have taken you as his gift. Or, if those proud remembrances, and the consciousness of your own virtues, and the honourable position to which they have raised you in the eyes of everything that wears the human shape, will not sustain you, marry that good man, and be happy in his condescension. If this will not do either, die! There are doorways and dust-heaps for such deaths, and such despair--find one, and take your flight to Heaven!’\n\nI heard a distant foot upon the stairs. I knew it, I was certain. It was his, thank God!\n\nShe moved slowly from before the door when she said this, and passed out of my sight.\n\n‘But mark!’ she added, slowly and sternly, opening the other door to go away, ‘I am resolved, for reasons that I have and hatreds that I entertain, to cast you out, unless you withdraw from my reach altogether, or drop your pretty mask. This is what I had to say; and what I say, I mean to do!’\n\nThe foot upon the stairs came nearer--nearer--passed her as she went down--rushed into the room!\n\n‘Uncle!’\n\nA fearful cry followed the word. I paused a moment, and looking in, saw him supporting her insensible figure in his arms. He gazed for a few seconds in the face; then stooped to kiss it--oh, how tenderly!--and drew a handkerchief before it.\n\n‘Mas’r Davy,’ he said, in a low tremulous voice, when it was covered, ‘I thank my Heav’nly Father as my dream’s come true! I thank Him hearty for having guided of me, in His own ways, to my darling!’\n\nWith those words he took her up in his arms; and, with the veiled face lying on his bosom, and addressed towards his own, carried her, motionless and unconscious, down the stairs.\n\n# CHAPTER 51. THE BEGINNING OF A LONGER JOURNEY\n\nIt was yet early in the morning of the following day, when, as I was walking in my garden with my aunt (who took little other exercise now, being so much in attendance on my dear Dora), I was told that Mr. Peggotty desired to speak with me. He came into the garden to meet me half-way, on my going towards the gate; and bared his head, as it was always his custom to do when he saw my aunt, for whom he had a high respect. I had been telling her all that had happened overnight. Without saying a word, she walked up with a cordial face, shook hands with him, and patted him on the arm. It was so expressively done, that she had no need to say a word. Mr. Peggotty understood her quite as well as if she had said a thousand.\n\n‘I’ll go in now, Trot,’ said my aunt, ‘and look after Little Blossom, who will be getting up presently.’\n\n‘Not along of my being heer, ma’am, I hope?’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘Unless my wits is gone a bahd’s neezing’--by which Mr. Peggotty meant to say, bird’s-nesting--‘this morning, ‘tis along of me as you’re a-going to quit us?’\n\n‘You have something to say, my good friend,’ returned my aunt, ‘and will do better without me.’\n\n‘By your leave, ma’am,’ returned Mr. Peggotty, ‘I should take it kind, pervising you doen’t mind my clicketten, if you’d bide heer.’\n\n‘Would you?’ said my aunt, with short good-nature. ‘Then I am sure I will!’\n\nSo, she drew her arm through Mr. Peggotty’s, and walked with him to a leafy little summer-house there was at the bottom of the garden, where she sat down on a bench, and I beside her. There was a seat for Mr. Peggotty too, but he preferred to stand, leaning his hand on the small rustic table. As he stood, looking at his cap for a little while before beginning to speak, I could not help observing what power and force of character his sinewy hand expressed, and what a good and trusty companion it was to his honest brow and iron-grey hair.\n\n‘I took my dear child away last night,’ Mr. Peggotty began, as he raised his eyes to ours, ‘to my lodging, wheer I have a long time been expecting of her and preparing fur her. It was hours afore she knowed me right; and when she did, she kneeled down at my feet, and kiender said to me, as if it was her prayers, how it all come to be. You may believe me, when I heerd her voice, as I had heerd at home so playful--and see her humbled, as it might be in the dust our Saviour wrote in with his blessed hand--I felt a wownd go to my ‘art, in the midst of all its thankfulness.’\n\nHe drew his sleeve across his face, without any pretence of concealing why; and then cleared his voice.\n\n‘It warn’t for long as I felt that; for she was found. I had on’y to think as she was found, and it was gone. I doen’t know why I do so much as mention of it now, I’m sure. I didn’t have it in my mind a minute ago, to say a word about myself; but it come up so nat’ral, that I yielded to it afore I was aweer.’\n\n‘You are a self-denying soul,’ said my aunt, ‘and will have your reward.’\n\nMr. Peggotty, with the shadows of the leaves playing athwart his face, made a surprised inclination of the head towards my aunt, as an acknowledgement of her good opinion; then took up the thread he had relinquished.\n\n‘When my Em’ly took flight,’ he said, in stern wrath for the moment, ‘from the house wheer she was made a prisoner by that theer spotted snake as Mas’r Davy see,--and his story’s trew, and may GOD confound him!--she took flight in the night. It was a dark night, with a many stars a-shining. She was wild. She ran along the sea beach, believing the old boat was theer; and calling out to us to turn away our faces, for she was a-coming by. She heerd herself a-crying out, like as if it was another person; and cut herself on them sharp-pinted stones and rocks, and felt it no more than if she had been rock herself. Ever so fur she run, and there was fire afore her eyes, and roarings in her ears. Of a sudden--or so she thowt, you unnerstand--the day broke, wet and windy, and she was lying b’low a heap of stone upon the shore, and a woman was a-speaking to her, saying, in the language of that country, what was it as had gone so much amiss?’\n\nHe saw everything he related. It passed before him, as he spoke, so vividly, that, in the intensity of his earnestness, he presented what he described to me, with greater distinctness than I can express. I can hardly believe, writing now long afterwards, but that I was actually present in these scenes; they are impressed upon me with such an astonishing air of fidelity.\n\n‘As Em’ly’s eyes--which was heavy--see this woman better,’ Mr. Peggotty went on, ‘she know’d as she was one of them as she had often talked to on the beach. Fur, though she had run (as I have said) ever so fur in the night, she had oftentimes wandered long ways, partly afoot, partly in boats and carriages, and know’d all that country, ‘long the coast, miles and miles. She hadn’t no children of her own, this woman, being a young wife; but she was a-looking to have one afore long. And may my prayers go up to Heaven that ‘twill be a happiness to her, and a comfort, and a honour, all her life! May it love her and be dootiful to her, in her old age; helpful of her at the last; a Angel to her heer, and heerafter!’\n\n‘Amen!’ said my aunt.\n\n‘She had been summat timorous and down,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘and had sat, at first, a little way off, at her spinning, or such work as it was, when Em’ly talked to the children. But Em’ly had took notice of her, and had gone and spoke to her; and as the young woman was partial to the children herself, they had soon made friends. Sermuchser, that when Em’ly went that way, she always giv Em’ly flowers. This was her as now asked what it was that had gone so much amiss. Em’ly told her, and she--took her home. She did indeed. She took her home,’ said Mr. Peggotty, covering his face.\n\nHe was more affected by this act of kindness, than I had ever seen him affected by anything since the night she went away. My aunt and I did not attempt to disturb him.\n\n‘It was a little cottage, you may suppose,’ he said, presently, ‘but she found space for Em’ly in it,--her husband was away at sea,--and she kep it secret, and prevailed upon such neighbours as she had (they was not many near) to keep it secret too. Em’ly was took bad with fever, and, what is very strange to me is,--maybe ‘tis not so strange to scholars,--the language of that country went out of her head, and she could only speak her own, that no one unnerstood. She recollects, as if she had dreamed it, that she lay there always a-talking her own tongue, always believing as the old boat was round the next pint in the bay, and begging and imploring of ‘em to send theer and tell how she was dying, and bring back a message of forgiveness, if it was on’y a wured. A’most the whole time, she thowt,--now, that him as I made mention on just now was lurking for her unnerneath the winder; now that him as had brought her to this was in the room,--and cried to the good young woman not to give her up, and know’d, at the same time, that she couldn’t unnerstand, and dreaded that she must be took away. Likewise the fire was afore her eyes, and the roarings in her ears; and theer was no today, nor yesterday, nor yet tomorrow; but everything in her life as ever had been, or as ever could be, and everything as never had been, and as never could be, was a crowding on her all at once, and nothing clear nor welcome, and yet she sang and laughed about it! How long this lasted, I doen’t know; but then theer come a sleep; and in that sleep, from being a many times stronger than her own self, she fell into the weakness of the littlest child.’\n\nHere he stopped, as if for relief from the terrors of his own description. After being silent for a few moments, he pursued his story.\n\n‘It was a pleasant arternoon when she awoke; and so quiet, that there warn’t a sound but the rippling of that blue sea without a tide, upon the shore. It was her belief, at first, that she was at home upon a Sunday morning; but the vine leaves as she see at the winder, and the hills beyond, warn’t home, and contradicted of her. Then, come in her friend to watch alongside of her bed; and then she know’d as the old boat warn’t round that next pint in the bay no more, but was fur off; and know’d where she was, and why; and broke out a-crying on that good young woman’s bosom, wheer I hope her baby is a-lying now, a-cheering of her with its pretty eyes!’\n\nHe could not speak of this good friend of Emily’s without a flow of tears. It was in vain to try. He broke down again, endeavouring to bless her!\n\n‘That done my Em’ly good,’ he resumed, after such emotion as I could not behold without sharing in; and as to my aunt, she wept with all her heart; ‘that done Em’ly good, and she begun to mend. But, the language of that country was quite gone from her, and she was forced to make signs. So she went on, getting better from day to day, slow, but sure, and trying to learn the names of common things--names as she seemed never to have heerd in all her life--till one evening come, when she was a-setting at her window, looking at a little girl at play upon the beach. And of a sudden this child held out her hand, and said, what would be in English, “Fisherman’s daughter, here’s a shell!”--for you are to unnerstand that they used at first to call her “Pretty lady”, as the general way in that country is, and that she had taught ‘em to call her “Fisherman’s daughter” instead. The child says of a sudden, “Fisherman’s daughter, here’s a shell!” Then Em’ly unnerstands her; and she answers, bursting out a-crying; and it all comes back!\n\n‘When Em’ly got strong again,’ said Mr. Peggotty, after another short interval of silence, ‘she cast about to leave that good young creetur, and get to her own country. The husband was come home, then; and the two together put her aboard a small trader bound to Leghorn, and from that to France. She had a little money, but it was less than little as they would take for all they done. I’m a’most glad on it, though they was so poor! What they done, is laid up wheer neither moth or rust doth corrupt, and wheer thieves do not break through nor steal. Mas’r Davy, it’ll outlast all the treasure in the wureld.\n\n‘Em’ly got to France, and took service to wait on travelling ladies at a inn in the port. Theer, theer come, one day, that snake. --Let him never come nigh me. I doen’t know what hurt I might do him!--Soon as she see him, without him seeing her, all her fear and wildness returned upon her, and she fled afore the very breath he draw’d. She come to England, and was set ashore at Dover.\n\n‘I doen’t know,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘for sure, when her ‘art begun to fail her; but all the way to England she had thowt to come to her dear home. Soon as she got to England she turned her face tow’rds it. But, fear of not being forgiv, fear of being pinted at, fear of some of us being dead along of her, fear of many things, turned her from it, kiender by force, upon the road: “Uncle, uncle,” she says to me, “the fear of not being worthy to do what my torn and bleeding breast so longed to do, was the most fright’ning fear of all! I turned back, when my ‘art was full of prayers that I might crawl to the old door-step, in the night, kiss it, lay my wicked face upon it, and theer be found dead in the morning.”\n\n‘She come,’ said Mr. Peggotty, dropping his voice to an awe-stricken whisper, ‘to London. She--as had never seen it in her life--alone--without a penny--young--so pretty--come to London. A’most the moment as she lighted heer, all so desolate, she found (as she believed) a friend; a decent woman as spoke to her about the needle-work as she had been brought up to do, about finding plenty of it fur her, about a lodging fur the night, and making secret inquiration concerning of me and all at home, tomorrow. When my child,’ he said aloud, and with an energy of gratitude that shook him from head to foot, ‘stood upon the brink of more than I can say or think on--Martha, trew to her promise, saved her.’\n\nI could not repress a cry of joy.\n\n‘Mas’r Davy!’ said he, gripping my hand in that strong hand of his, ‘it was you as first made mention of her to me. I thankee, sir! She was arnest. She had know’d of her bitter knowledge wheer to watch and what to do. She had done it. And the Lord was above all! She come, white and hurried, upon Em’ly in her sleep. She says to her, “Rise up from worse than death, and come with me!” Them belonging to the house would have stopped her, but they might as soon have stopped the sea. “Stand away from me,” she says, “I am a ghost that calls her from beside her open grave!” She told Em’ly she had seen me, and know’d I loved her, and forgive her. She wrapped her, hasty, in her clothes. She took her, faint and trembling, on her arm. She heeded no more what they said, than if she had had no ears. She walked among ‘em with my child, minding only her; and brought her safe out, in the dead of the night, from that black pit of ruin!\n\n‘She attended on Em’ly,’ said Mr. Peggotty, who had released my hand, and put his own hand on his heaving chest; ‘she attended to my Em’ly, lying wearied out, and wandering betwixt whiles, till late next day. Then she went in search of me; then in search of you, Mas’r Davy. She didn’t tell Em’ly what she come out fur, lest her ‘art should fail, and she should think of hiding of herself. How the cruel lady know’d of her being theer, I can’t say. Whether him as I have spoke so much of, chanced to see ‘em going theer, or whether (which is most like, to my thinking) he had heerd it from the woman, I doen’t greatly ask myself. My niece is found.\n\n‘All night long,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘we have been together, Em’ly and me. ‘Tis little (considering the time) as she has said, in wureds, through them broken-hearted tears; ‘tis less as I have seen of her dear face, as grow’d into a woman’s at my hearth. But, all night long, her arms has been about my neck; and her head has laid heer; and we knows full well, as we can put our trust in one another, ever more.’\n\nHe ceased to speak, and his hand upon the table rested there in perfect repose, with a resolution in it that might have conquered lions.\n\n‘It was a gleam of light upon me, Trot,’ said my aunt, drying her eyes, ‘when I formed the resolution of being godmother to your sister Betsey Trotwood, who disappointed me; but, next to that, hardly anything would have given me greater pleasure, than to be godmother to that good young creature’s baby!’\n\nMr. Peggotty nodded his understanding of my aunt’s feelings, but could not trust himself with any verbal reference to the subject of her commendation. We all remained silent, and occupied with our own reflections (my aunt drying her eyes, and now sobbing convulsively, and now laughing and calling herself a fool); until I spoke.\n\n‘You have quite made up your mind,’ said I to Mr. Peggotty, ‘as to the future, good friend? I need scarcely ask you.’\n\n‘Quite, Mas’r Davy,’ he returned; ‘and told Em’ly. Theer’s mighty countries, fur from heer. Our future life lays over the sea.’\n\n‘They will emigrate together, aunt,’ said I.\n\n‘Yes!’ said Mr. Peggotty, with a hopeful smile. ‘No one can’t reproach my darling in Australia. We will begin a new life over theer!’\n\nI asked him if he yet proposed to himself any time for going away.\n\n‘I was down at the Docks early this morning, sir,’ he returned, ‘to get information concerning of them ships. In about six weeks or two months from now, there’ll be one sailing--I see her this morning--went aboard--and we shall take our passage in her.’\n\n‘Quite alone?’ I asked.\n\n‘Aye, Mas’r Davy!’ he returned. ‘My sister, you see, she’s that fond of you and yourn, and that accustomed to think on’y of her own country, that it wouldn’t be hardly fair to let her go. Besides which, theer’s one she has in charge, Mas’r Davy, as doen’t ought to be forgot.’\n\n‘Poor Ham!’ said I.\n\n‘My good sister takes care of his house, you see, ma’am, and he takes kindly to her,’ Mr. Peggotty explained for my aunt’s better information. ‘He’ll set and talk to her, with a calm spirit, wen it’s like he couldn’t bring himself to open his lips to another. Poor fellow!’ said Mr. Peggotty, shaking his head, ‘theer’s not so much left him, that he could spare the little as he has!’\n\n‘And Mrs. Gummidge?’ said I.\n\n‘Well, I’ve had a mort of consideration, I do tell you,’ returned Mr. Peggotty, with a perplexed look which gradually cleared as he went on, ‘concerning of Missis Gummidge. You see, wen Missis Gummidge falls a-thinking of the old ‘un, she an’t what you may call good company. Betwixt you and me, Mas’r Davy--and you, ma’am--wen Mrs. Gummidge takes to wimicking,’--our old country word for crying,--‘she’s liable to be considered to be, by them as didn’t know the old ‘un, peevish-like. Now I DID know the old ‘un,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘and I know’d his merits, so I unnerstan’ her; but ‘tan’t entirely so, you see, with others--nat’rally can’t be!’\n\nMy aunt and I both acquiesced.\n\n‘Wheerby,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘my sister might--I doen’t say she would, but might--find Missis Gummidge give her a leetle trouble now-and-again. Theerfur ‘tan’t my intentions to moor Missis Gummidge ‘long with them, but to find a Beein’ fur her wheer she can fisherate for herself.’ (A Beein’ signifies, in that dialect, a home, and to fisherate is to provide.) ‘Fur which purpose,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘I means to make her a ‘lowance afore I go, as’ll leave her pretty comfort’ble. She’s the faithfullest of creeturs. ‘Tan’t to be expected, of course, at her time of life, and being lone and lorn, as the good old Mawther is to be knocked about aboardship, and in the woods and wilds of a new and fur-away country. So that’s what I’m a-going to do with her.’\n\nHe forgot nobody. He thought of everybody’s claims and strivings, but his own.\n\n‘Em’ly,’ he continued, ‘will keep along with me--poor child, she’s sore in need of peace and rest!--until such time as we goes upon our voyage. She’ll work at them clothes, as must be made; and I hope her troubles will begin to seem longer ago than they was, wen she finds herself once more by her rough but loving uncle.’\n\nMy aunt nodded confirmation of this hope, and imparted great satisfaction to Mr. Peggotty.\n\n‘Theer’s one thing furder, Mas’r Davy,’ said he, putting his hand in his breast-pocket, and gravely taking out the little paper bundle I had seen before, which he unrolled on the table. ‘Theer’s these here banknotes--fifty pound, and ten. To them I wish to add the money as she come away with. I’ve asked her about that (but not saying why), and have added of it up. I an’t a scholar. Would you be so kind as see how ‘tis?’\n\nHe handed me, apologetically for his scholarship, a piece of paper, and observed me while I looked it over. It was quite right.\n\n‘Thankee, sir,’ he said, taking it back. ‘This money, if you doen’t see objections, Mas’r Davy, I shall put up jest afore I go, in a cover directed to him; and put that up in another, directed to his mother. I shall tell her, in no more wureds than I speak to you, what it’s the price on; and that I’m gone, and past receiving of it back.’\n\nI told him that I thought it would be right to do so--that I was thoroughly convinced it would be, since he felt it to be right.\n\n‘I said that theer was on’y one thing furder,’ he proceeded with a grave smile, when he had made up his little bundle again, and put it in his pocket; ‘but theer was two. I warn’t sure in my mind, wen I come out this morning, as I could go and break to Ham, of my own self, what had so thankfully happened. So I writ a letter while I was out, and put it in the post-office, telling of ‘em how all was as ‘tis; and that I should come down tomorrow to unload my mind of what little needs a-doing of down theer, and, most-like, take my farewell leave of Yarmouth.’\n\n‘And do you wish me to go with you?’ said I, seeing that he left something unsaid.\n\n‘If you could do me that kind favour, Mas’r Davy,’ he replied. ‘I know the sight on you would cheer ‘em up a bit.’\n\nMy little Dora being in good spirits, and very desirous that I should go--as I found on talking it over with her--I readily pledged myself to accompany him in accordance with his wish. Next morning, consequently, we were on the Yarmouth coach, and again travelling over the old ground.\n\nAs we passed along the familiar street at night--Mr. Peggotty, in despite of all my remonstrances, carrying my bag--I glanced into Omer and Joram’s shop, and saw my old friend Mr. Omer there, smoking his pipe. I felt reluctant to be present, when Mr. Peggotty first met his sister and Ham; and made Mr. Omer my excuse for lingering behind.\n\n‘How is Mr. Omer, after this long time?’ said I, going in.\n\nHe fanned away the smoke of his pipe, that he might get a better view of me, and soon recognized me with great delight.\n\n‘I should get up, sir, to acknowledge such an honour as this visit,’ said he, ‘only my limbs are rather out of sorts, and I am wheeled about. With the exception of my limbs and my breath, howsoever, I am as hearty as a man can be, I’m thankful to say.’\n\nI congratulated him on his contented looks and his good spirits, and saw, now, that his easy-chair went on wheels.\n\n‘It’s an ingenious thing, ain’t it?’ he inquired, following the direction of my glance, and polishing the elbow with his arm. ‘It runs as light as a feather, and tracks as true as a mail-coach. Bless you, my little Minnie--my grand-daughter you know, Minnie’s child--puts her little strength against the back, gives it a shove, and away we go, as clever and merry as ever you see anything! And I tell you what--it’s a most uncommon chair to smoke a pipe in.’\n\nI never saw such a good old fellow to make the best of a thing, and find out the enjoyment of it, as Mr. Omer. He was as radiant, as if his chair, his asthma, and the failure of his limbs, were the various branches of a great invention for enhancing the luxury of a pipe.\n\n‘I see more of the world, I can assure you,’ said Mr. Omer, ‘in this chair, than ever I see out of it. You’d be surprised at the number of people that looks in of a day to have a chat. You really would! There’s twice as much in the newspaper, since I’ve taken to this chair, as there used to be. As to general reading, dear me, what a lot of it I do get through! That’s what I feel so strong, you know! If it had been my eyes, what should I have done? If it had been my ears, what should I have done? Being my limbs, what does it signify? Why, my limbs only made my breath shorter when I used ‘em. And now, if I want to go out into the street or down to the sands, I’ve only got to call Dick, Joram’s youngest ‘prentice, and away I go in my own carriage, like the Lord Mayor of London.’\n\nHe half suffocated himself with laughing here.\n\n‘Lord bless you!’ said Mr. Omer, resuming his pipe, ‘a man must take the fat with the lean; that’s what he must make up his mind to, in this life. Joram does a fine business. Ex-cellent business!’\n\n‘I am very glad to hear it,’ said I.\n\n‘I knew you would be,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘And Joram and Minnie are like Valentines. What more can a man expect? What’s his limbs to that!’\n\nHis supreme contempt for his own limbs, as he sat smoking, was one of the pleasantest oddities I have ever encountered.\n\n‘And since I’ve took to general reading, you’ve took to general writing, eh, sir?’ said Mr. Omer, surveying me admiringly. ‘What a lovely work that was of yours! What expressions in it! I read it every word--every word. And as to feeling sleepy! Not at all!’\n\nI laughingly expressed my satisfaction, but I must confess that I thought this association of ideas significant.\n\n‘I give you my word and honour, sir,’ said Mr. Omer, ‘that when I lay that book upon the table, and look at it outside; compact in three separate and indiwidual wollumes--one, two, three; I am as proud as Punch to think that I once had the honour of being connected with your family. And dear me, it’s a long time ago, now, ain’t it? Over at Blunderstone. With a pretty little party laid along with the other party. And you quite a small party then, yourself. Dear, dear!’\n\nI changed the subject by referring to Emily. After assuring him that I did not forget how interested he had always been in her, and how kindly he had always treated her, I gave him a general account of her restoration to her uncle by the aid of Martha; which I knew would please the old man. He listened with the utmost attention, and said, feelingly, when I had done:\n\n‘I am rejoiced at it, sir! It’s the best news I have heard for many a day. Dear, dear, dear! And what’s going to be undertook for that unfortunate young woman, Martha, now?’\n\n‘You touch a point that my thoughts have been dwelling on since yesterday,’ said I, ‘but on which I can give you no information yet, Mr. Omer. Mr. Peggotty has not alluded to it, and I have a delicacy in doing so. I am sure he has not forgotten it. He forgets nothing that is disinterested and good.’\n\n‘Because you know,’ said Mr. Omer, taking himself up, where he had left off, ‘whatever is done, I should wish to be a member of. Put me down for anything you may consider right, and let me know. I never could think the girl all bad, and I am glad to find she’s not. So will my daughter Minnie be. Young women are contradictory creatures in some things--her mother was just the same as her--but their hearts are soft and kind. It’s all show with Minnie, about Martha. Why she should consider it necessary to make any show, I don’t undertake to tell you. But it’s all show, bless you. She’d do her any kindness in private. So, put me down for whatever you may consider right, will you be so good? and drop me a line where to forward it. Dear me!’ said Mr. Omer, ‘when a man is drawing on to a time of life, where the two ends of life meet; when he finds himself, however hearty he is, being wheeled about for the second time, in a speeches of go-cart; he should be over-rejoiced to do a kindness if he can. He wants plenty. And I don’t speak of myself, particular,’ said Mr. Omer, ‘because, sir, the way I look at it is, that we are all drawing on to the bottom of the hill, whatever age we are, on account of time never standing still for a single moment. So let us always do a kindness, and be over-rejoiced. To be sure!’\n\nHe knocked the ashes out of his pipe, and put it on a ledge in the back of his chair, expressly made for its reception.\n\n‘There’s Em’ly’s cousin, him that she was to have been married to,’ said Mr. Omer, rubbing his hands feebly, ‘as fine a fellow as there is in Yarmouth! He’ll come and talk or read to me, in the evening, for an hour together sometimes. That’s a kindness, I should call it! All his life’s a kindness.’\n\n‘I am going to see him now,’ said I.\n\n‘Are you?’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Tell him I was hearty, and sent my respects. Minnie and Joram’s at a ball. They would be as proud to see you as I am, if they was at home. Minnie won’t hardly go out at all, you see, “on account of father”, as she says. So I swore tonight, that if she didn’t go, I’d go to bed at six. In consequence of which,’ Mr. Omer shook himself and his chair with laughter at the success of his device, ‘she and Joram’s at a ball.’\n\nI shook hands with him, and wished him good night.\n\n‘Half a minute, sir,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘If you was to go without seeing my little elephant, you’d lose the best of sights. You never see such a sight! Minnie!’ A musical little voice answered, from somewhere upstairs, ‘I am coming, grandfather!’ and a pretty little girl with long, flaxen, curling hair, soon came running into the shop.\n\n‘This is my little elephant, sir,’ said Mr. Omer, fondling the child. ‘Siamese breed, sir. Now, little elephant!’\n\nThe little elephant set the door of the parlour open, enabling me to see that, in these latter days, it was converted into a bedroom for Mr. Omer who could not be easily conveyed upstairs; and then hid her pretty forehead, and tumbled her long hair, against the back of Mr. Omer’s chair.\n\n‘The elephant butts, you know, sir,’ said Mr. Omer, winking, ‘when he goes at a object. Once, elephant. Twice. Three times!’\n\nAt this signal, the little elephant, with a dexterity that was next to marvellous in so small an animal, whisked the chair round with Mr. Omer in it, and rattled it off, pell-mell, into the parlour, without touching the door-post: Mr. Omer indescribably enjoying the performance, and looking back at me on the road as if it were the triumphant issue of his life’s exertions.\n\nAfter a stroll about the town I went to Ham’s house. Peggotty had now removed here for good; and had let her own house to the successor of Mr. Barkis in the carrying business, who had paid her very well for the good-will, cart, and horse. I believe the very same slow horse that Mr. Barkis drove was still at work.\n\nI found them in the neat kitchen, accompanied by Mrs. Gummidge, who had been fetched from the old boat by Mr. Peggotty himself. I doubt if she could have been induced to desert her post, by anyone else. He had evidently told them all. Both Peggotty and Mrs. Gummidge had their aprons to their eyes, and Ham had just stepped out ‘to take a turn on the beach’. He presently came home, very glad to see me; and I hope they were all the better for my being there. We spoke, with some approach to cheerfulness, of Mr. Peggotty’s growing rich in a new country, and of the wonders he would describe in his letters. We said nothing of Emily by name, but distantly referred to her more than once. Ham was the serenest of the party.\n\nBut, Peggotty told me, when she lighted me to a little chamber where the Crocodile book was lying ready for me on the table, that he always was the same. She believed (she told me, crying) that he was broken-hearted; though he was as full of courage as of sweetness, and worked harder and better than any boat-builder in any yard in all that part. There were times, she said, of an evening, when he talked of their old life in the boat-house; and then he mentioned Emily as a child. But, he never mentioned her as a woman.\n\nI thought I had read in his face that he would like to speak to me alone. I therefore resolved to put myself in his way next evening, as he came home from his work. Having settled this with myself, I fell asleep. That night, for the first time in all those many nights, the candle was taken out of the window, Mr. Peggotty swung in his old hammock in the old boat, and the wind murmured with the old sound round his head.\n\nAll next day, he was occupied in disposing of his fishing-boat and tackle; in packing up, and sending to London by waggon, such of his little domestic possessions as he thought would be useful to him; and in parting with the rest, or bestowing them on Mrs. Gummidge. She was with him all day. As I had a sorrowful wish to see the old place once more, before it was locked up, I engaged to meet them there in the evening. But I so arranged it, as that I should meet Ham first.\n\nIt was easy to come in his way, as I knew where he worked. I met him at a retired part of the sands, which I knew he would cross, and turned back with him, that he might have leisure to speak to me if he really wished. I had not mistaken the expression of his face. We had walked but a little way together, when he said, without looking at me:\n\n‘Mas’r Davy, have you seen her?’\n\n‘Only for a moment, when she was in a swoon,’ I softly answered.\n\nWe walked a little farther, and he said:\n\n‘Mas’r Davy, shall you see her, d’ye think?’\n\n‘It would be too painful to her, perhaps,’ said I.\n\n‘I have thowt of that,’ he replied. ‘So ‘twould, sir, so ‘twould.’\n\n‘But, Ham,’ said I, gently, ‘if there is anything that I could write to her, for you, in case I could not tell it; if there is anything you would wish to make known to her through me; I should consider it a sacred trust.’\n\n‘I am sure on’t. I thankee, sir, most kind! I think theer is something I could wish said or wrote.’\n\n‘What is it?’\n\nWe walked a little farther in silence, and then he spoke.\n\n‘’Tan’t that I forgive her. ‘Tan’t that so much. ‘Tis more as I beg of her to forgive me, for having pressed my affections upon her. Odd times, I think that if I hadn’t had her promise fur to marry me, sir, she was that trustful of me, in a friendly way, that she’d have told me what was struggling in her mind, and would have counselled with me, and I might have saved her.’\n\nI pressed his hand. ‘Is that all?’ ‘Theer’s yet a something else,’ he returned, ‘if I can say it, Mas’r Davy.’\n\nWe walked on, farther than we had walked yet, before he spoke again. He was not crying when he made the pauses I shall express by lines. He was merely collecting himself to speak very plainly.\n\n‘I loved her--and I love the mem’ry of her--too deep--to be able to lead her to believe of my own self as I’m a happy man. I could only be happy--by forgetting of her--and I’m afeerd I couldn’t hardly bear as she should be told I done that. But if you, being so full of learning, Mas’r Davy, could think of anything to say as might bring her to believe I wasn’t greatly hurt: still loving of her, and mourning for her: anything as might bring her to believe as I was not tired of my life, and yet was hoping fur to see her without blame, wheer the wicked cease from troubling and the weary are at rest--anything as would ease her sorrowful mind, and yet not make her think as I could ever marry, or as ‘twas possible that anyone could ever be to me what she was--I should ask of you to say that--with my prayers for her--that was so dear.’\n\nI pressed his manly hand again, and told him I would charge myself to do this as well as I could.\n\n‘I thankee, sir,’ he answered. ‘’Twas kind of you to meet me. ‘Twas kind of you to bear him company down. Mas’r Davy, I unnerstan’ very well, though my aunt will come to Lon’on afore they sail, and they’ll unite once more, that I am not like to see him agen. I fare to feel sure on’t. We doen’t say so, but so ‘twill be, and better so. The last you see on him--the very last--will you give him the lovingest duty and thanks of the orphan, as he was ever more than a father to?’\n\nThis I also promised, faithfully.\n\n‘I thankee agen, sir,’ he said, heartily shaking hands. ‘I know wheer you’re a-going. Good-bye!’\n\nWith a slight wave of his hand, as though to explain to me that he could not enter the old place, he turned away. As I looked after his figure, crossing the waste in the moonlight, I saw him turn his face towards a strip of silvery light upon the sea, and pass on, looking at it, until he was a shadow in the distance.\n\nThe door of the boat-house stood open when I approached; and, on entering, I found it emptied of all its furniture, saving one of the old lockers, on which Mrs. Gummidge, with a basket on her knee, was seated, looking at Mr. Peggotty. He leaned his elbow on the rough chimney-piece, and gazed upon a few expiring embers in the grate; but he raised his head, hopefully, on my coming in, and spoke in a cheery manner.\n\n‘Come, according to promise, to bid farewell to ‘t, eh, Mas’r Davy?’ he said, taking up the candle. ‘Bare enough, now, an’t it?’ ‘Indeed you have made good use of the time,’ said I.\n\n‘Why, we have not been idle, sir. Missis Gummidge has worked like a--I doen’t know what Missis Gummidge an’t worked like,’ said Mr. Peggotty, looking at her, at a loss for a sufficiently approving simile.\n\nMrs. Gummidge, leaning on her basket, made no observation.\n\n‘Theer’s the very locker that you used to sit on, ‘long with Em’ly!’ said Mr. Peggotty, in a whisper. ‘I’m a-going to carry it away with me, last of all. And heer’s your old little bedroom, see, Mas’r Davy! A’most as bleak tonight, as ‘art could wish!’\n\nIn truth, the wind, though it was low, had a solemn sound, and crept around the deserted house with a whispered wailing that was very mournful. Everything was gone, down to the little mirror with the oyster-shell frame. I thought of myself, lying here, when that first great change was being wrought at home. I thought of the blue-eyed child who had enchanted me. I thought of Steerforth: and a foolish, fearful fancy came upon me of his being near at hand, and liable to be met at any turn.\n\n‘’Tis like to be long,’ said Mr. Peggotty, in a low voice, ‘afore the boat finds new tenants. They look upon ‘t, down heer, as being unfortunate now!’\n\n‘Does it belong to anybody in the neighbourhood?’ I asked.\n\n‘To a mast-maker up town,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘I’m a-going to give the key to him tonight.’\n\nWe looked into the other little room, and came back to Mrs. Gummidge, sitting on the locker, whom Mr. Peggotty, putting the light on the chimney-piece, requested to rise, that he might carry it outside the door before extinguishing the candle.\n\n‘Dan’l,’ said Mrs. Gummidge, suddenly deserting her basket, and clinging to his arm ‘my dear Dan’l, the parting words I speak in this house is, I mustn’t be left behind. Doen’t ye think of leaving me behind, Dan’l! Oh, doen’t ye ever do it!’\n\nMr. Peggotty, taken aback, looked from Mrs. Gummidge to me, and from me to Mrs. Gummidge, as if he had been awakened from a sleep.\n\n‘Doen’t ye, dearest Dan’l, doen’t ye!’ cried Mrs. Gummidge, fervently. ‘Take me ‘long with you, Dan’l, take me ‘long with you and Em’ly! I’ll be your servant, constant and trew. If there’s slaves in them parts where you’re a-going, I’ll be bound to you for one, and happy, but doen’t ye leave me behind, Dan’l, that’s a deary dear!’\n\n‘My good soul,’ said Mr. Peggotty, shaking his head, ‘you doen’t know what a long voyage, and what a hard life ‘tis!’ ‘Yes, I do, Dan’l! I can guess!’ cried Mrs. Gummidge. ‘But my parting words under this roof is, I shall go into the house and die, if I am not took. I can dig, Dan’l. I can work. I can live hard. I can be loving and patient now--more than you think, Dan’l, if you’ll on’y try me. I wouldn’t touch the ‘lowance, not if I was dying of want, Dan’l Peggotty; but I’ll go with you and Em’ly, if you’ll on’y let me, to the world’s end! I know how ‘tis; I know you think that I am lone and lorn; but, deary love, ‘tan’t so no more! I ain’t sat here, so long, a-watching, and a-thinking of your trials, without some good being done me. Mas’r Davy, speak to him for me! I knows his ways, and Em’ly’s, and I knows their sorrows, and can be a comfort to ‘em, some odd times, and labour for ‘em allus! Dan’l, deary Dan’l, let me go ‘long with you!’\n\nAnd Mrs. Gummidge took his hand, and kissed it with a homely pathos and affection, in a homely rapture of devotion and gratitude, that he well deserved.\n\nWe brought the locker out, extinguished the candle, fastened the door on the outside, and left the old boat close shut up, a dark speck in the cloudy night. Next day, when we were returning to London outside the coach, Mrs. Gummidge and her basket were on the seat behind, and Mrs. Gummidge was happy.\n\n# CHAPTER 52. I ASSIST AT AN EXPLOSION\n\nWhen the time Mr. Micawber had appointed so mysteriously, was within four-and-twenty hours of being come, my aunt and I consulted how we should proceed; for my aunt was very unwilling to leave Dora. Ah! how easily I carried Dora up and down stairs, now!\n\nWe were disposed, notwithstanding Mr. Micawber’s stipulation for my aunt’s attendance, to arrange that she should stay at home, and be represented by Mr. Dick and me. In short, we had resolved to take this course, when Dora again unsettled us by declaring that she never would forgive herself, and never would forgive her bad boy, if my aunt remained behind, on any pretence.\n\n‘I won’t speak to you,’ said Dora, shaking her curls at my aunt. ‘I’ll be disagreeable! I’ll make Jip bark at you all day. I shall be sure that you really are a cross old thing, if you don’t go!’\n\n‘Tut, Blossom!’ laughed my aunt. ‘You know you can’t do without me!’\n\n‘Yes, I can,’ said Dora. ‘You are no use to me at all. You never run up and down stairs for me, all day long. You never sit and tell me stories about Doady, when his shoes were worn out, and he was covered with dust--oh, what a poor little mite of a fellow! You never do anything at all to please me, do you, dear?’ Dora made haste to kiss my aunt, and say, ‘Yes, you do! I’m only joking!’-lest my aunt should think she really meant it.\n\n‘But, aunt,’ said Dora, coaxingly, ‘now listen. You must go. I shall tease you, ‘till you let me have my own way about it. I shall lead my naughty boy such a life, if he don’t make you go. I shall make myself so disagreeable--and so will Jip! You’ll wish you had gone, like a good thing, for ever and ever so long, if you don’t go. Besides,’ said Dora, putting back her hair, and looking wonderingly at my aunt and me, ‘why shouldn’t you both go? I am not very ill indeed. Am I?’\n\n‘Why, what a question!’ cried my aunt.\n\n‘What a fancy!’ said I.\n\n‘Yes! I know I am a silly little thing!’ said Dora, slowly looking from one of us to the other, and then putting up her pretty lips to kiss us as she lay upon her couch. ‘Well, then, you must both go, or I shall not believe you; and then I shall cry!’\n\nI saw, in my aunt’s face, that she began to give way now, and Dora brightened again, as she saw it too.\n\n‘You’ll come back with so much to tell me, that it’ll take at least a week to make me understand!’ said Dora. ‘Because I know I shan’t understand, for a length of time, if there’s any business in it. And there’s sure to be some business in it! If there’s anything to add up, besides, I don’t know when I shall make it out; and my bad boy will look so miserable all the time. There! Now you’ll go, won’t you? You’ll only be gone one night, and Jip will take care of me while you are gone. Doady will carry me upstairs before you go, and I won’t come down again till you come back; and you shall take Agnes a dreadfully scolding letter from me, because she has never been to see us!’\n\nWe agreed, without any more consultation, that we would both go, and that Dora was a little Impostor, who feigned to be rather unwell, because she liked to be petted. She was greatly pleased, and very merry; and we four, that is to say, my aunt, Mr. Dick, Traddles, and I, went down to Canterbury by the Dover mail that night.\n\nAt the hotel where Mr. Micawber had requested us to await him, which we got into, with some trouble, in the middle of the night, I found a letter, importing that he would appear in the morning punctually at half past nine. After which, we went shivering, at that uncomfortable hour, to our respective beds, through various close passages; which smelt as if they had been steeped, for ages, in a solution of soup and stables.\n\nEarly in the morning, I sauntered through the dear old tranquil streets, and again mingled with the shadows of the venerable gateways and churches. The rooks were sailing about the cathedral towers; and the towers themselves, overlooking many a long unaltered mile of the rich country and its pleasant streams, were cutting the bright morning air, as if there were no such thing as change on earth. Yet the bells, when they sounded, told me sorrowfully of change in everything; told me of their own age, and my pretty Dora’s youth; and of the many, never old, who had lived and loved and died, while the reverberations of the bells had hummed through the rusty armour of the Black Prince hanging up within, and, motes upon the deep of Time, had lost themselves in air, as circles do in water.\n\nI looked at the old house from the corner of the street, but did not go nearer to it, lest, being observed, I might unwittingly do any harm to the design I had come to aid. The early sun was striking edgewise on its gables and lattice-windows, touching them with gold; and some beams of its old peace seemed to touch my heart.\n\nI strolled into the country for an hour or so, and then returned by the main street, which in the interval had shaken off its last night’s sleep. Among those who were stirring in the shops, I saw my ancient enemy the butcher, now advanced to top-boots and a baby, and in business for himself. He was nursing the baby, and appeared to be a benignant member of society.\n\nWe all became very anxious and impatient, when we sat down to breakfast. As it approached nearer and nearer to half past nine o’clock, our restless expectation of Mr. Micawber increased. At last we made no more pretence of attending to the meal, which, except with Mr. Dick, had been a mere form from the first; but my aunt walked up and down the room. Traddles sat upon the sofa affecting to read the paper with his eyes on the ceiling; and I looked out of the window to give early notice of Mr. Micawber’s coming. Nor had I long to watch, for, at the first chime of the half hour, he appeared in the street.\n\n‘Here he is,’ said I, ‘and not in his legal attire!’\n\nMy aunt tied the strings of her bonnet (she had come down to breakfast in it), and put on her shawl, as if she were ready for anything that was resolute and uncompromising. Traddles buttoned his coat with a determined air. Mr. Dick, disturbed by these formidable appearances, but feeling it necessary to imitate them, pulled his hat, with both hands, as firmly over his ears as he possibly could; and instantly took it off again, to welcome Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘Gentlemen, and madam,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘good morning! My dear sir,’ to Mr. Dick, who shook hands with him violently, ‘you are extremely good.’\n\n‘Have you breakfasted?’ said Mr. Dick. ‘Have a chop!’\n\n‘Not for the world, my good sir!’ cried Mr. Micawber, stopping him on his way to the bell; ‘appetite and myself, Mr. Dixon, have long been strangers.’\n\nMr. Dixon was so well pleased with his new name, and appeared to think it so obliging in Mr. Micawber to confer it upon him, that he shook hands with him again, and laughed rather childishly.\n\n‘Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘attention!’\n\nMr. Dick recovered himself, with a blush.\n\n‘Now, sir,’ said my aunt to Mr. Micawber, as she put on her gloves, ‘we are ready for Mount Vesuvius, or anything else, as soon as YOU please.’\n\n‘Madam,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘I trust you will shortly witness an eruption. Mr. Traddles, I have your permission, I believe, to mention here that we have been in communication together?’\n\n‘It is undoubtedly the fact, Copperfield,’ said Traddles, to whom I looked in surprise. ‘Mr. Micawber has consulted me in reference to what he has in contemplation; and I have advised him to the best of my judgement.’\n\n‘Unless I deceive myself, Mr. Traddles,’ pursued Mr. Micawber, ‘what I contemplate is a disclosure of an important nature.’\n\n‘Highly so,’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Perhaps, under such circumstances, madam and gentlemen,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘you will do me the favour to submit yourselves, for the moment, to the direction of one who, however unworthy to be regarded in any other light but as a Waif and Stray upon the shore of human nature, is still your fellow-man, though crushed out of his original form by individual errors, and the accumulative force of a combination of circumstances?’\n\n‘We have perfect confidence in you, Mr. Micawber,’ said I, ‘and will do what you please.’\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘your confidence is not, at the existing juncture, ill-bestowed. I would beg to be allowed a start of five minutes by the clock; and then to receive the present company, inquiring for Miss Wickfield, at the office of Wickfield and Heep, whose Stipendiary I am.’\n\nMy aunt and I looked at Traddles, who nodded his approval.\n\n‘I have no more,’ observed Mr. Micawber, ‘to say at present.’\n\nWith which, to my infinite surprise, he included us all in a comprehensive bow, and disappeared; his manner being extremely distant, and his face extremely pale.\n\nTraddles only smiled, and shook his head (with his hair standing upright on the top of it), when I looked to him for an explanation; so I took out my watch, and, as a last resource, counted off the five minutes. My aunt, with her own watch in her hand, did the like. When the time was expired, Traddles gave her his arm; and we all went out together to the old house, without saying one word on the way.\n\nWe found Mr. Micawber at his desk, in the turret office on the ground floor, either writing, or pretending to write, hard. The large office-ruler was stuck into his waistcoat, and was not so well concealed but that a foot or more of that instrument protruded from his bosom, like a new kind of shirt-frill.\n\nAs it appeared to me that I was expected to speak, I said aloud:\n\n‘How do you do, Mr. Micawber?’\n\n‘Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber, gravely, ‘I hope I see you well?’\n\n‘Is Miss Wickfield at home?’ said I.\n\n‘Mr. Wickfield is unwell in bed, sir, of a rheumatic fever,’ he returned; ‘but Miss Wickfield, I have no doubt, will be happy to see old friends. Will you walk in, sir?’\n\nHe preceded us to the dining-room--the first room I had entered in that house--and flinging open the door of Mr. Wickfield’s former office, said, in a sonorous voice:\n\n‘Miss Trotwood, Mr. David Copperfield, Mr. Thomas Traddles, and Mr. Dixon!’\n\nI had not seen Uriah Heep since the time of the blow. Our visit astonished him, evidently; not the less, I dare say, because it astonished ourselves. He did not gather his eyebrows together, for he had none worth mentioning; but he frowned to that degree that he almost closed his small eyes, while the hurried raising of his grisly hand to his chin betrayed some trepidation or surprise. This was only when we were in the act of entering his room, and when I caught a glance at him over my aunt’s shoulder. A moment afterwards, he was as fawning and as humble as ever.\n\n‘Well, I am sure,’ he said. ‘This is indeed an unexpected pleasure! To have, as I may say, all friends round St. Paul’s at once, is a treat unlooked for! Mr. Copperfield, I hope I see you well, and--if I may umbly express myself so--friendly towards them as is ever your friends, whether or not. Mrs. Copperfield, sir, I hope she’s getting on. We have been made quite uneasy by the poor accounts we have had of her state, lately, I do assure you.’\n\nI felt ashamed to let him take my hand, but I did not know yet what else to do.\n\n‘Things are changed in this office, Miss Trotwood, since I was an umble clerk, and held your pony; ain’t they?’ said Uriah, with his sickliest smile. ‘But I am not changed, Miss Trotwood.’\n\n‘Well, sir,’ returned my aunt, ‘to tell you the truth, I think you are pretty constant to the promise of your youth; if that’s any satisfaction to you.’\n\n‘Thank you, Miss Trotwood,’ said Uriah, writhing in his ungainly manner, ‘for your good opinion! Micawber, tell ‘em to let Miss Agnes know--and mother. Mother will be quite in a state, when she sees the present company!’ said Uriah, setting chairs.\n\n‘You are not busy, Mr. Heep?’ said Traddles, whose eye the cunning red eye accidentally caught, as it at once scrutinized and evaded us.\n\n‘No, Mr. Traddles,’ replied Uriah, resuming his official seat, and squeezing his bony hands, laid palm to palm between his bony knees. ‘Not so much so as I could wish. But lawyers, sharks, and leeches, are not easily satisfied, you know! Not but what myself and Micawber have our hands pretty full, in general, on account of Mr. Wickfield’s being hardly fit for any occupation, sir. But it’s a pleasure as well as a duty, I am sure, to work for him. You’ve not been intimate with Mr. Wickfield, I think, Mr. Traddles? I believe I’ve only had the honour of seeing you once myself?’\n\n‘No, I have not been intimate with Mr. Wickfield,’ returned Traddles; ‘or I might perhaps have waited on you long ago, Mr. Heep.’\n\nThere was something in the tone of this reply, which made Uriah look at the speaker again, with a very sinister and suspicious expression. But, seeing only Traddles, with his good-natured face, simple manner, and hair on end, he dismissed it as he replied, with a jerk of his whole body, but especially his throat:\n\n‘I am sorry for that, Mr. Traddles. You would have admired him as much as we all do. His little failings would only have endeared him to you the more. But if you would like to hear my fellow-partner eloquently spoken of, I should refer you to Copperfield. The family is a subject he’s very strong upon, if you never heard him.’\n\nI was prevented from disclaiming the compliment (if I should have done so, in any case), by the entrance of Agnes, now ushered in by Mr. Micawber. She was not quite so self-possessed as usual, I thought; and had evidently undergone anxiety and fatigue. But her earnest cordiality, and her quiet beauty, shone with the gentler lustre for it.\n\nI saw Uriah watch her while she greeted us; and he reminded me of an ugly and rebellious genie watching a good spirit. In the meanwhile, some slight sign passed between Mr. Micawber and Traddles; and Traddles, unobserved except by me, went out.\n\n‘Don’t wait, Micawber,’ said Uriah.\n\nMr. Micawber, with his hand upon the ruler in his breast, stood erect before the door, most unmistakably contemplating one of his fellow-men, and that man his employer.\n\n‘What are you waiting for?’ said Uriah. ‘Micawber! did you hear me tell you not to wait?’\n\n‘Yes!’ replied the immovable Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘Then why DO you wait?’ said Uriah.\n\n‘Because I--in short, choose,’ replied Mr. Micawber, with a burst.\n\nUriah’s cheeks lost colour, and an unwholesome paleness, still faintly tinged by his pervading red, overspread them. He looked at Mr. Micawber attentively, with his whole face breathing short and quick in every feature.\n\n‘You are a dissipated fellow, as all the world knows,’ he said, with an effort at a smile, ‘and I am afraid you’ll oblige me to get rid of you. Go along! I’ll talk to you presently.’\n\n‘If there is a scoundrel on this earth,’ said Mr. Micawber, suddenly breaking out again with the utmost vehemence, ‘with whom I have already talked too much, that scoundrel’s name is--HEEP!’\n\nUriah fell back, as if he had been struck or stung. Looking slowly round upon us with the darkest and wickedest expression that his face could wear, he said, in a lower voice:\n\n‘Oho! This is a conspiracy! You have met here by appointment! You are playing Booty with my clerk, are you, Copperfield? Now, take care. You’ll make nothing of this. We understand each other, you and me. There’s no love between us. You were always a puppy with a proud stomach, from your first coming here; and you envy me my rise, do you? None of your plots against me; I’ll counterplot you! Micawber, you be off. I’ll talk to you presently.’\n\n‘Mr. Micawber,’ said I, ‘there is a sudden change in this fellow, in more respects than the extraordinary one of his speaking the truth in one particular, which assures me that he is brought to bay. Deal with him as he deserves!’\n\n‘You are a precious set of people, ain’t you?’ said Uriah, in the same low voice, and breaking out into a clammy heat, which he wiped from his forehead, with his long lean hand, ‘to buy over my clerk, who is the very scum of society,--as you yourself were, Copperfield, you know it, before anyone had charity on you,--to defame me with his lies? Miss Trotwood, you had better stop this; or I’ll stop your husband shorter than will be pleasant to you. I won’t know your story professionally, for nothing, old lady! Miss Wickfield, if you have any love for your father, you had better not join that gang. I’ll ruin him, if you do. Now, come! I have got some of you under the harrow. Think twice, before it goes over you. Think twice, you, Micawber, if you don’t want to be crushed. I recommend you to take yourself off, and be talked to presently, you fool! while there’s time to retreat. Where’s mother?’ he said, suddenly appearing to notice, with alarm, the absence of Traddles, and pulling down the bell-rope. ‘Fine doings in a person’s own house!’\n\n‘Mrs. Heep is here, sir,’ said Traddles, returning with that worthy mother of a worthy son. ‘I have taken the liberty of making myself known to her.’\n\n‘Who are you to make yourself known?’ retorted Uriah. ‘And what do you want here?’\n\n‘I am the agent and friend of Mr. Wickfield, sir,’ said Traddles, in a composed and business-like way. ‘And I have a power of attorney from him in my pocket, to act for him in all matters.’\n\n‘The old ass has drunk himself into a state of dotage,’ said Uriah, turning uglier than before, ‘and it has been got from him by fraud!’\n\n‘Something has been got from him by fraud, I know,’ returned Traddles quietly; ‘and so do you, Mr. Heep. We will refer that question, if you please, to Mr. Micawber.’\n\n‘Ury--!’ Mrs. Heep began, with an anxious gesture.\n\n‘YOU hold your tongue, mother,’ he returned; ‘least said, soonest mended.’\n\n‘But, my Ury--’\n\n‘Will you hold your tongue, mother, and leave it to me?’\n\nThough I had long known that his servility was false, and all his pretences knavish and hollow, I had had no adequate conception of the extent of his hypocrisy, until I now saw him with his mask off. The suddenness with which he dropped it, when he perceived that it was useless to him; the malice, insolence, and hatred, he revealed; the leer with which he exulted, even at this moment, in the evil he had done--all this time being desperate too, and at his wits’ end for the means of getting the better of us--though perfectly consistent with the experience I had of him, at first took even me by surprise, who had known him so long, and disliked him so heartily.\n\nI say nothing of the look he conferred on me, as he stood eyeing us, one after another; for I had always understood that he hated me, and I remembered the marks of my hand upon his cheek. But when his eyes passed on to Agnes, and I saw the rage with which he felt his power over her slipping away, and the exhibition, in their disappointment, of the odious passions that had led him to aspire to one whose virtues he could never appreciate or care for, I was shocked by the mere thought of her having lived, an hour, within sight of such a man.\n\nAfter some rubbing of the lower part of his face, and some looking at us with those bad eyes, over his grisly fingers, he made one more address to me, half whining, and half abusive.\n\n‘You think it justifiable, do you, Copperfield, you who pride yourself so much on your honour and all the rest of it, to sneak about my place, eaves-dropping with my clerk? If it had been ME, I shouldn’t have wondered; for I don’t make myself out a gentleman (though I never was in the streets either, as you were, according to Micawber), but being you!--And you’re not afraid of doing this, either? You don’t think at all of what I shall do, in return; or of getting yourself into trouble for conspiracy and so forth? Very well. We shall see! Mr. What’s-your-name, you were going to refer some question to Micawber. There’s your referee. Why don’t you make him speak? He has learnt his lesson, I see.’\n\nSeeing that what he said had no effect on me or any of us, he sat on the edge of his table with his hands in his pockets, and one of his splay feet twisted round the other leg, waiting doggedly for what might follow.\n\nMr. Micawber, whose impetuosity I had restrained thus far with the greatest difficulty, and who had repeatedly interposed with the first syllable of SCOUN-drel! without getting to the second, now burst forward, drew the ruler from his breast (apparently as a defensive weapon), and produced from his pocket a foolscap document, folded in the form of a large letter. Opening this packet, with his old flourish, and glancing at the contents, as if he cherished an artistic admiration of their style of composition, he began to read as follows:\n\n‘“Dear Miss Trotwood and gentlemen--“’\n\n‘Bless and save the man!’ exclaimed my aunt in a low voice. ‘He’d write letters by the ream, if it was a capital offence!’\n\nMr. Micawber, without hearing her, went on.\n\n‘“In appearing before you to denounce probably the most consummate Villain that has ever existed,”’ Mr. Micawber, without looking off the letter, pointed the ruler, like a ghostly truncheon, at Uriah Heep, ‘“I ask no consideration for myself. The victim, from my cradle, of pecuniary liabilities to which I have been unable to respond, I have ever been the sport and toy of debasing circumstances. Ignominy, Want, Despair, and Madness, have, collectively or separately, been the attendants of my career.”’\n\nThe relish with which Mr. Micawber described himself as a prey to these dismal calamities, was only to be equalled by the emphasis with which he read his letter; and the kind of homage he rendered to it with a roll of his head, when he thought he had hit a sentence very hard indeed.\n\n‘“In an accumulation of Ignominy, Want, Despair, and Madness, I entered the office--or, as our lively neighbour the Gaul would term it, the Bureau--of the Firm, nominally conducted under the appellation of Wickfield and--HEEP, but in reality, wielded by--HEEP alone. HEEP, and only HEEP, is the mainspring of that machine. HEEP, and only HEEP, is the Forger and the Cheat.”’\n\nUriah, more blue than white at these words, made a dart at the letter, as if to tear it in pieces. Mr. Micawber, with a perfect miracle of dexterity or luck, caught his advancing knuckles with the ruler, and disabled his right hand. It dropped at the wrist, as if it were broken. The blow sounded as if it had fallen on wood.\n\n‘The Devil take you!’ said Uriah, writhing in a new way with pain. ‘I’ll be even with you.’\n\n‘Approach me again, you--you--you HEEP of infamy,’ gasped Mr. Micawber, ‘and if your head is human, I’ll break it. Come on, come on!’\n\nI think I never saw anything more ridiculous--I was sensible of it, even at the time--than Mr. Micawber making broad-sword guards with the ruler, and crying, ‘Come on!’ while Traddles and I pushed him back into a corner, from which, as often as we got him into it, he persisted in emerging again.\n\nHis enemy, muttering to himself, after wringing his wounded hand for sometime, slowly drew off his neck-kerchief and bound it up; then held it in his other hand, and sat upon his table with his sullen face looking down.\n\nMr. Micawber, when he was sufficiently cool, proceeded with his letter.\n\n‘“The stipendiary emoluments in consideration of which I entered into the service of--HEEP,”’ always pausing before that word and uttering it with astonishing vigour, ‘“were not defined, beyond the pittance of twenty-two shillings and six per week. The rest was left contingent on the value of my professional exertions; in other and more expressive words, on the baseness of my nature, the cupidity of my motives, the poverty of my family, the general moral (or rather immoral) resemblance between myself and--HEEP. Need I say, that it soon became necessary for me to solicit from--HEEP--pecuniary advances towards the support of Mrs. Micawber, and our blighted but rising family? Need I say that this necessity had been foreseen by--HEEP? That those advances were secured by I.O.U.’s and other similar acknowledgements, known to the legal institutions of this country? And that I thus became immeshed in the web he had spun for my reception?”’\n\nMr. Micawber’s enjoyment of his epistolary powers, in describing this unfortunate state of things, really seemed to outweigh any pain or anxiety that the reality could have caused him. He read on:\n\n‘“Then it was that--HEEP--began to favour me with just so much of his confidence, as was necessary to the discharge of his infernal business. Then it was that I began, if I may so Shakespearianly express myself, to dwindle, peak, and pine. I found that my services were constantly called into requisition for the falsification of business, and the mystification of an individual whom I will designate as Mr. W. That Mr. W. was imposed upon, kept in ignorance, and deluded, in every possible way; yet, that all this while, the ruffian--HEEP--was professing unbounded gratitude to, and unbounded friendship for, that much-abused gentleman. This was bad enough; but, as the philosophic Dane observes, with that universal applicability which distinguishes the illustrious ornament of the Elizabethan Era, worse remains behind!”’\n\nMr. Micawber was so very much struck by this happy rounding off with a quotation, that he indulged himself, and us, with a second reading of the sentence, under pretence of having lost his place.\n\n‘“It is not my intention,”’ he continued reading on, ‘“to enter on a detailed list, within the compass of the present epistle (though it is ready elsewhere), of the various malpractices of a minor nature, affecting the individual whom I have denominated Mr. W., to which I have been a tacitly consenting party. My object, when the contest within myself between stipend and no stipend, baker and no baker, existence and non-existence, ceased, was to take advantage of my opportunities to discover and expose the major malpractices committed, to that gentleman’s grievous wrong and injury, by--HEEP. Stimulated by the silent monitor within, and by a no less touching and appealing monitor without--to whom I will briefly refer as Miss W.--I entered on a not unlaborious task of clandestine investigation, protracted--now, to the best of my knowledge, information, and belief, over a period exceeding twelve calendar months.”’\n\nHe read this passage as if it were from an Act of Parliament; and appeared majestically refreshed by the sound of the words.\n\n‘“My charges against--HEEP,”’ he read on, glancing at him, and drawing the ruler into a convenient position under his left arm, in case of need, ‘“are as follows.”’\n\nWe all held our breath, I think. I am sure Uriah held his.\n\n‘“First,”’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘“When Mr. W.’s faculties and memory for business became, through causes into which it is not necessary or expedient for me to enter, weakened and confused,--HEEP--designedly perplexed and complicated the whole of the official transactions. When Mr. W. was least fit to enter on business,--HEEP was always at hand to force him to enter on it. He obtained Mr. W.’s signature under such circumstances to documents of importance, representing them to be other documents of no importance. He induced Mr. W. to empower him to draw out, thus, one particular sum of trust-money, amounting to twelve six fourteen, two and nine, and employed it to meet pretended business charges and deficiencies which were either already provided for, or had never really existed. He gave this proceeding, throughout, the appearance of having originated in Mr. W.’s own dishonest intention, and of having been accomplished by Mr. W.’s own dishonest act; and has used it, ever since, to torture and constrain him.”’\n\n‘You shall prove this, you Copperfield!’ said Uriah, with a threatening shake of the head. ‘All in good time!’\n\n‘Ask--HEEP--Mr. Traddles, who lived in his house after him,’ said Mr. Micawber, breaking off from the letter; ‘will you?’\n\n‘The fool himself--and lives there now,’ said Uriah, disdainfully.\n\n‘Ask--HEEP--if he ever kept a pocket-book in that house,’ said Mr. Micawber; ‘will you?’\n\nI saw Uriah’s lank hand stop, involuntarily, in the scraping of his chin.\n\n‘Or ask him,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘if he ever burnt one there. If he says yes, and asks you where the ashes are, refer him to Wilkins Micawber, and he will hear of something not at all to his advantage!’\n\nThe triumphant flourish with which Mr. Micawber delivered himself of these words, had a powerful effect in alarming the mother; who cried out, in much agitation:\n\n‘Ury, Ury! Be umble, and make terms, my dear!’\n\n‘Mother!’ he retorted, ‘will you keep quiet? You’re in a fright, and don’t know what you say or mean. Umble!’ he repeated, looking at me, with a snarl; ‘I’ve umbled some of ‘em for a pretty long time back, umble as I was!’\n\nMr. Micawber, genteelly adjusting his chin in his cravat, presently proceeded with his composition.\n\n‘“Second. HEEP has, on several occasions, to the best of my knowledge, information, and belief--“’\n\n‘But that won’t do,’ muttered Uriah, relieved. ‘Mother, you keep quiet.’\n\n‘We will endeavour to provide something that WILL do, and do for you finally, sir, very shortly,’ replied Mr. Micawber.\n\n‘“Second. HEEP has, on several occasions, to the best of my knowledge, information, and belief, systematically forged, to various entries, books, and documents, the signature of Mr. W.; and has distinctly done so in one instance, capable of proof by me. To wit, in manner following, that is to say:”’\n\nAgain, Mr. Micawber had a relish in this formal piling up of words, which, however ludicrously displayed in his case, was, I must say, not at all peculiar to him. I have observed it, in the course of my life, in numbers of men. It seems to me to be a general rule. In the taking of legal oaths, for instance, deponents seem to enjoy themselves mightily when they come to several good words in succession, for the expression of one idea; as, that they utterly detest, abominate, and abjure, or so forth; and the old anathemas were made relishing on the same principle. We talk about the tyranny of words, but we like to tyrannize over them too; we are fond of having a large superfluous establishment of words to wait upon us on great occasions; we think it looks important, and sounds well. As we are not particular about the meaning of our liveries on state occasions, if they be but fine and numerous enough, so, the meaning or necessity of our words is a secondary consideration, if there be but a great parade of them. And as individuals get into trouble by making too great a show of liveries, or as slaves when they are too numerous rise against their masters, so I think I could mention a nation that has got into many great difficulties, and will get into many greater, from maintaining too large a retinue of words.\n\nMr. Micawber read on, almost smacking his lips:\n\n‘“To wit, in manner following, that is to say. Mr. W. being infirm, and it being within the bounds of probability that his decease might lead to some discoveries, and to the downfall of--HEEP’S--power over the W. family,--as I, Wilkins Micawber, the undersigned, assume--unless the filial affection of his daughter could be secretly influenced from allowing any investigation of the partnership affairs to be ever made, the said--HEEP--deemed it expedient to have a bond ready by him, as from Mr. W., for the before-mentioned sum of twelve six fourteen, two and nine, with interest, stated therein to have been advanced by--HEEP--to Mr. W. to save Mr. W. from dishonour; though really the sum was never advanced by him, and has long been replaced. The signatures to this instrument purporting to be executed by Mr. W. and attested by Wilkins Micawber, are forgeries by--HEEP. I have, in my possession, in his hand and pocket-book, several similar imitations of Mr. W.’s signature, here and there defaced by fire, but legible to anyone. I never attested any such document. And I have the document itself, in my possession.”’ Uriah Heep, with a start, took out of his pocket a bunch of keys, and opened a certain drawer; then, suddenly bethought himself of what he was about, and turned again towards us, without looking in it.\n\n‘“And I have the document,”’ Mr. Micawber read again, looking about as if it were the text of a sermon, ‘“in my possession,--that is to say, I had, early this morning, when this was written, but have since relinquished it to Mr. Traddles.”’\n\n‘It is quite true,’ assented Traddles.\n\n‘Ury, Ury!’ cried the mother, ‘be umble and make terms. I know my son will be umble, gentlemen, if you’ll give him time to think. Mr. Copperfield, I’m sure you know that he was always very umble, sir!’\n\nIt was singular to see how the mother still held to the old trick, when the son had abandoned it as useless.\n\n‘Mother,’ he said, with an impatient bite at the handkerchief in which his hand was wrapped, ‘you had better take and fire a loaded gun at me.’\n\n‘But I love you, Ury,’ cried Mrs. Heep. And I have no doubt she did; or that he loved her, however strange it may appear; though, to be sure, they were a congenial couple. ‘And I can’t bear to hear you provoking the gentlemen, and endangering of yourself more. I told the gentleman at first, when he told me upstairs it was come to light, that I would answer for your being umble, and making amends. Oh, see how umble I am, gentlemen, and don’t mind him!’\n\n‘Why, there’s Copperfield, mother,’ he angrily retorted, pointing his lean finger at me, against whom all his animosity was levelled, as the prime mover in the discovery; and I did not undeceive him; ‘there’s Copperfield, would have given you a hundred pound to say less than you’ve blurted out!’\n\n‘I can’t help it, Ury,’ cried his mother. ‘I can’t see you running into danger, through carrying your head so high. Better be umble, as you always was.’\n\nHe remained for a little, biting the handkerchief, and then said to me with a scowl:\n\n‘What more have you got to bring forward? If anything, go on with it. What do you look at me for?’\n\nMr. Micawber promptly resumed his letter, glad to revert to a performance with which he was so highly satisfied.\n\n‘“Third. And last. I am now in a condition to show, by--HEEP’S--false books, and--HEEP’S--real memoranda, beginning with the partially destroyed pocket-book (which I was unable to comprehend, at the time of its accidental discovery by Mrs. Micawber, on our taking possession of our present abode, in the locker or bin devoted to the reception of the ashes calcined on our domestic hearth), that the weaknesses, the faults, the very virtues, the parental affections, and the sense of honour, of the unhappy Mr. W. have been for years acted on by, and warped to the base purposes of--HEEP. That Mr. W. has been for years deluded and plundered, in every conceivable manner, to the pecuniary aggrandisement of the avaricious, false, and grasping--HEEP. That the engrossing object of--HEEP--was, next to gain, to subdue Mr. and Miss W. (of his ulterior views in reference to the latter I say nothing) entirely to himself. That his last act, completed but a few months since, was to induce Mr. W. to execute a relinquishment of his share in the partnership, and even a bill of sale on the very furniture of his house, in consideration of a certain annuity, to be well and truly paid by--HEEP--on the four common quarter-days in each and every year. That these meshes; beginning with alarming and falsified accounts of the estate of which Mr. W. is the receiver, at a period when Mr. W. had launched into imprudent and ill-judged speculations, and may not have had the money, for which he was morally and legally responsible, in hand; going on with pretended borrowings of money at enormous interest, really coming from--HEEP--and by--HEEP--fraudulently obtained or withheld from Mr. W. himself, on pretence of such speculations or otherwise; perpetuated by a miscellaneous catalogue of unscrupulous chicaneries--gradually thickened, until the unhappy Mr. W. could see no world beyond. Bankrupt, as he believed, alike in circumstances, in all other hope, and in honour, his sole reliance was upon the monster in the garb of man,”’--Mr. Micawber made a good deal of this, as a new turn of expression,--‘“who, by making himself necessary to him, had achieved his destruction. All this I undertake to show. Probably much more!”’\n\nI whispered a few words to Agnes, who was weeping, half joyfully, half sorrowfully, at my side; and there was a movement among us, as if Mr. Micawber had finished. He said, with exceeding gravity, ‘Pardon me,’ and proceeded, with a mixture of the lowest spirits and the most intense enjoyment, to the peroration of his letter.\n\n‘“I have now concluded. It merely remains for me to substantiate these accusations; and then, with my ill-starred family, to disappear from the landscape on which we appear to be an encumbrance. That is soon done. It may be reasonably inferred that our baby will first expire of inanition, as being the frailest member of our circle; and that our twins will follow next in order. So be it! For myself, my Canterbury Pilgrimage has done much; imprisonment on civil process, and want, will soon do more. I trust that the labour and hazard of an investigation--of which the smallest results have been slowly pieced together, in the pressure of arduous avocations, under grinding penurious apprehensions, at rise of morn, at dewy eve, in the shadows of night, under the watchful eye of one whom it were superfluous to call Demon--combined with the struggle of parental Poverty to turn it, when completed, to the right account, may be as the sprinkling of a few drops of sweet water on my funeral pyre. I ask no more. Let it be, in justice, merely said of me, as of a gallant and eminent naval Hero, with whom I have no pretensions to cope, that what I have done, I did, in despite of mercenary and selfish objects,\n\nFor England, home, and Beauty.\n\n‘“Remaining always, &c. &c., WILKINS MICAWBER.”’\n\nMuch affected, but still intensely enjoying himself, Mr. Micawber folded up his letter, and handed it with a bow to my aunt, as something she might like to keep.\n\nThere was, as I had noticed on my first visit long ago, an iron safe in the room. The key was in it. A hasty suspicion seemed to strike Uriah; and, with a glance at Mr. Micawber, he went to it, and threw the doors clanking open. It was empty.\n\n‘Where are the books?’ he cried, with a frightful face. ‘Some thief has stolen the books!’\n\nMr. Micawber tapped himself with the ruler. ‘I did, when I got the key from you as usual--but a little earlier--and opened it this morning.’\n\n‘Don’t be uneasy,’ said Traddles. ‘They have come into my possession. I will take care of them, under the authority I mentioned.’\n\n‘You receive stolen goods, do you?’ cried Uriah.\n\n‘Under such circumstances,’ answered Traddles, ‘yes.’\n\nWhat was my astonishment when I beheld my aunt, who had been profoundly quiet and attentive, make a dart at Uriah Heep, and seize him by the collar with both hands!\n\n‘You know what I want?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘A strait-waistcoat,’ said he.\n\n‘No. My property!’ returned my aunt. ‘Agnes, my dear, as long as I believed it had been really made away with by your father, I wouldn’t--and, my dear, I didn’t, even to Trot, as he knows--breathe a syllable of its having been placed here for investment. But, now I know this fellow’s answerable for it, and I’ll have it! Trot, come and take it away from him!’\n\nWhether my aunt supposed, for the moment, that he kept her property in his neck-kerchief, I am sure I don’t know; but she certainly pulled at it as if she thought so. I hastened to put myself between them, and to assure her that we would all take care that he should make the utmost restitution of everything he had wrongly got. This, and a few moments’ reflection, pacified her; but she was not at all disconcerted by what she had done (though I cannot say as much for her bonnet) and resumed her seat composedly.\n\nDuring the last few minutes, Mrs. Heep had been clamouring to her son to be ‘umble’; and had been going down on her knees to all of us in succession, and making the wildest promises. Her son sat her down in his chair; and, standing sulkily by her, holding her arm with his hand, but not rudely, said to me, with a ferocious look:\n\n‘What do you want done?’\n\n‘I will tell you what must be done,’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Has that Copperfield no tongue?’ muttered Uriah, ‘I would do a good deal for you if you could tell me, without lying, that somebody had cut it out.’\n\n‘My Uriah means to be umble!’ cried his mother. ‘Don’t mind what he says, good gentlemen!’\n\n‘What must be done,’ said Traddles, ‘is this. First, the deed of relinquishment, that we have heard of, must be given over to me now--here.’\n\n‘Suppose I haven’t got it,’ he interrupted.\n\n‘But you have,’ said Traddles; ‘therefore, you know, we won’t suppose so.’ And I cannot help avowing that this was the first occasion on which I really did justice to the clear head, and the plain, patient, practical good sense, of my old schoolfellow. ‘Then,’ said Traddles, ‘you must prepare to disgorge all that your rapacity has become possessed of, and to make restoration to the last farthing. All the partnership books and papers must remain in our possession; all your books and papers; all money accounts and securities, of both kinds. In short, everything here.’\n\n‘Must it? I don’t know that,’ said Uriah. ‘I must have time to think about that.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ replied Traddles; ‘but, in the meanwhile, and until everything is done to our satisfaction, we shall maintain possession of these things; and beg you--in short, compel you--to keep to your own room, and hold no communication with anyone.’\n\n‘I won’t do it!’ said Uriah, with an oath.\n\n‘Maidstone jail is a safer place of detention,’ observed Traddles; ‘and though the law may be longer in righting us, and may not be able to right us so completely as you can, there is no doubt of its punishing YOU. Dear me, you know that quite as well as I! Copperfield, will you go round to the Guildhall, and bring a couple of officers?’\n\nHere, Mrs. Heep broke out again, crying on her knees to Agnes to interfere in their behalf, exclaiming that he was very humble, and it was all true, and if he didn’t do what we wanted, she would, and much more to the same purpose; being half frantic with fears for her darling. To inquire what he might have done, if he had had any boldness, would be like inquiring what a mongrel cur might do, if it had the spirit of a tiger. He was a coward, from head to foot; and showed his dastardly nature through his sullenness and mortification, as much as at any time of his mean life.\n\n‘Stop!’ he growled to me; and wiped his hot face with his hand. ‘Mother, hold your noise. Well! Let ‘em have that deed. Go and fetch it!’\n\n‘Do you help her, Mr. Dick,’ said Traddles, ‘if you please.’\n\nProud of his commission, and understanding it, Mr. Dick accompanied her as a shepherd’s dog might accompany a sheep. But, Mrs. Heep gave him little trouble; for she not only returned with the deed, but with the box in which it was, where we found a banker’s book and some other papers that were afterwards serviceable.\n\n‘Good!’ said Traddles, when this was brought. ‘Now, Mr. Heep, you can retire to think: particularly observing, if you please, that I declare to you, on the part of all present, that there is only one thing to be done; that it is what I have explained; and that it must be done without delay.’\n\nUriah, without lifting his eyes from the ground, shuffled across the room with his hand to his chin, and pausing at the door, said:\n\n‘Copperfield, I have always hated you. You’ve always been an upstart, and you’ve always been against me.’\n\n‘As I think I told you once before,’ said I, ‘it is you who have been, in your greed and cunning, against all the world. It may be profitable to you to reflect, in future, that there never were greed and cunning in the world yet, that did not do too much, and overreach themselves. It is as certain as death.’\n\n‘Or as certain as they used to teach at school (the same school where I picked up so much umbleness), from nine o’clock to eleven, that labour was a curse; and from eleven o’clock to one, that it was a blessing and a cheerfulness, and a dignity, and I don’t know what all, eh?’ said he with a sneer. ‘You preach, about as consistent as they did. Won’t umbleness go down? I shouldn’t have got round my gentleman fellow-partner without it, I think. --Micawber, you old bully, I’ll pay YOU!’\n\nMr. Micawber, supremely defiant of him and his extended finger, and making a great deal of his chest until he had slunk out at the door, then addressed himself to me, and proffered me the satisfaction of ‘witnessing the re-establishment of mutual confidence between himself and Mrs. Micawber’. After which, he invited the company generally to the contemplation of that affecting spectacle.\n\n‘The veil that has long been interposed between Mrs. Micawber and myself, is now withdrawn,’ said Mr. Micawber; ‘and my children and the Author of their Being can once more come in contact on equal terms.’\n\nAs we were all very grateful to him, and all desirous to show that we were, as well as the hurry and disorder of our spirits would permit, I dare say we should all have gone, but that it was necessary for Agnes to return to her father, as yet unable to bear more than the dawn of hope; and for someone else to hold Uriah in safe keeping. So, Traddles remained for the latter purpose, to be presently relieved by Mr. Dick; and Mr. Dick, my aunt, and I, went home with Mr. Micawber. As I parted hurriedly from the dear girl to whom I owed so much, and thought from what she had been saved, perhaps, that morning--her better resolution notwithstanding--I felt devoutly thankful for the miseries of my younger days which had brought me to the knowledge of Mr. Micawber.\n\nHis house was not far off; and as the street door opened into the sitting-room, and he bolted in with a precipitation quite his own, we found ourselves at once in the bosom of the family. Mr. Micawber exclaiming, ‘Emma! my life!’ rushed into Mrs. Micawber’s arms. Mrs. Micawber shrieked, and folded Mr. Micawber in her embrace. Miss Micawber, nursing the unconscious stranger of Mrs. Micawber’s last letter to me, was sensibly affected. The stranger leaped. The twins testified their joy by several inconvenient but innocent demonstrations. Master Micawber, whose disposition appeared to have been soured by early disappointment, and whose aspect had become morose, yielded to his better feelings, and blubbered.\n\n‘Emma!’ said Mr. Micawber. ‘The cloud is past from my mind. Mutual confidence, so long preserved between us once, is restored, to know no further interruption. Now, welcome poverty!’ cried Mr. Micawber, shedding tears. ‘Welcome misery, welcome houselessness, welcome hunger, rags, tempest, and beggary! Mutual confidence will sustain us to the end!’\n\nWith these expressions, Mr. Micawber placed Mrs. Micawber in a chair, and embraced the family all round; welcoming a variety of bleak prospects, which appeared, to the best of my judgement, to be anything but welcome to them; and calling upon them to come out into Canterbury and sing a chorus, as nothing else was left for their support.\n\nBut Mrs. Micawber having, in the strength of her emotions, fainted away, the first thing to be done, even before the chorus could be considered complete, was to recover her. This my aunt and Mr. Micawber did; and then my aunt was introduced, and Mrs. Micawber recognized me.\n\n‘Excuse me, dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said the poor lady, giving me her hand, ‘but I am not strong; and the removal of the late misunderstanding between Mr. Micawber and myself was at first too much for me.’\n\n‘Is this all your family, ma’am?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘There are no more at present,’ returned Mrs. Micawber.\n\n‘Good gracious, I didn’t mean that, ma’am,’ said my aunt. ‘I mean, are all these yours?’\n\n‘Madam,’ replied Mr. Micawber, ‘it is a true bill.’\n\n‘And that eldest young gentleman, now,’ said my aunt, musing, ‘what has he been brought up to?’\n\n‘It was my hope when I came here,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘to have got Wilkins into the Church: or perhaps I shall express my meaning more strictly, if I say the Choir. But there was no vacancy for a tenor in the venerable Pile for which this city is so justly eminent; and he has--in short, he has contracted a habit of singing in public-houses, rather than in sacred edifices.’\n\n‘But he means well,’ said Mrs. Micawber, tenderly.\n\n‘I dare say, my love,’ rejoined Mr. Micawber, ‘that he means particularly well; but I have not yet found that he carries out his meaning, in any given direction whatsoever.’\n\nMaster Micawber’s moroseness of aspect returned upon him again, and he demanded, with some temper, what he was to do? Whether he had been born a carpenter, or a coach-painter, any more than he had been born a bird? Whether he could go into the next street, and open a chemist’s shop? Whether he could rush to the next assizes, and proclaim himself a lawyer? Whether he could come out by force at the opera, and succeed by violence? Whether he could do anything, without being brought up to something?\n\nMy aunt mused a little while, and then said:\n\n‘Mr. Micawber, I wonder you have never turned your thoughts to emigration.’\n\n‘Madam,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘it was the dream of my youth, and the fallacious aspiration of my riper years.’ I am thoroughly persuaded, by the by, that he had never thought of it in his life.\n\n‘Aye?’ said my aunt, with a glance at me. ‘Why, what a thing it would be for yourselves and your family, Mr. and Mrs. Micawber, if you were to emigrate now.’\n\n‘Capital, madam, capital,’ urged Mr. Micawber, gloomily.\n\n‘That is the principal, I may say the only difficulty, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ assented his wife.\n\n‘Capital?’ cried my aunt. ‘But you are doing us a great service--have done us a great service, I may say, for surely much will come out of the fire--and what could we do for you, that would be half so good as to find the capital?’\n\n‘I could not receive it as a gift,’ said Mr. Micawber, full of fire and animation, ‘but if a sufficient sum could be advanced, say at five per cent interest, per annum, upon my personal liability--say my notes of hand, at twelve, eighteen, and twenty-four months, respectively, to allow time for something to turn up--’\n\n‘Could be? Can be and shall be, on your own terms,’ returned my aunt, ‘if you say the word. Think of this now, both of you. Here are some people David knows, going out to Australia shortly. If you decide to go, why shouldn’t you go in the same ship? You may help each other. Think of this now, Mr. and Mrs. Micawber. Take your time, and weigh it well.’\n\n‘There is but one question, my dear ma’am, I could wish to ask,’ said Mrs. Micawber. ‘The climate, I believe, is healthy?’\n\n‘Finest in the world!’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Just so,’ returned Mrs. Micawber. ‘Then my question arises. Now, are the circumstances of the country such, that a man of Mr. Micawber’s abilities would have a fair chance of rising in the social scale? I will not say, at present, might he aspire to be Governor, or anything of that sort; but would there be a reasonable opening for his talents to develop themselves--that would be amply sufficient--and find their own expansion?’\n\n‘No better opening anywhere,’ said my aunt, ‘for a man who conducts himself well, and is industrious.’\n\n‘For a man who conducts himself well,’ repeated Mrs. Micawber, with her clearest business manner, ‘and is industrious. Precisely. It is evident to me that Australia is the legitimate sphere of action for Mr. Micawber!’\n\n‘I entertain the conviction, my dear madam,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘that it is, under existing circumstances, the land, the only land, for myself and family; and that something of an extraordinary nature will turn up on that shore. It is no distance--comparatively speaking; and though consideration is due to the kindness of your proposal, I assure you that is a mere matter of form.’\n\nShall I ever forget how, in a moment, he was the most sanguine of men, looking on to fortune; or how Mrs. Micawber presently discoursed about the habits of the kangaroo! Shall I ever recall that street of Canterbury on a market-day, without recalling him, as he walked back with us; expressing, in the hardy roving manner he assumed, the unsettled habits of a temporary sojourner in the land; and looking at the bullocks, as they came by, with the eye of an Australian farmer!\n\n# CHAPTER 53. ANOTHER RETROSPECT\n\nI must pause yet once again. O, my child-wife, there is a figure in the moving crowd before my memory, quiet and still, saying in its innocent love and childish beauty, Stop to think of me--turn to look upon the Little Blossom, as it flutters to the ground!\n\nI do. All else grows dim, and fades away. I am again with Dora, in our cottage. I do not know how long she has been ill. I am so used to it in feeling, that I cannot count the time. It is not really long, in weeks or months; but, in my usage and experience, it is a weary, weary while.\n\nThey have left off telling me to ‘wait a few days more’. I have begun to fear, remotely, that the day may never shine, when I shall see my child-wife running in the sunlight with her old friend Jip.\n\nHe is, as it were suddenly, grown very old. It may be that he misses in his mistress, something that enlivened him and made him younger; but he mopes, and his sight is weak, and his limbs are feeble, and my aunt is sorry that he objects to her no more, but creeps near her as he lies on Dora’s bed--she sitting at the bedside--and mildly licks her hand.\n\nDora lies smiling on us, and is beautiful, and utters no hasty or complaining word. She says that we are very good to her; that her dear old careful boy is tiring himself out, she knows; that my aunt has no sleep, yet is always wakeful, active, and kind. Sometimes, the little bird-like ladies come to see her; and then we talk about our wedding-day, and all that happy time.\n\nWhat a strange rest and pause in my life there seems to be--and in all life, within doors and without--when I sit in the quiet, shaded, orderly room, with the blue eyes of my child-wife turned towards me, and her little fingers twining round my hand! Many and many an hour I sit thus; but, of all those times, three times come the freshest on my mind.\n\nIt is morning; and Dora, made so trim by my aunt’s hands, shows me how her pretty hair will curl upon the pillow yet, an how long and bright it is, and how she likes to have it loosely gathered in that net she wears.\n\n‘Not that I am vain of it, now, you mocking boy,’ she says, when I smile; ‘but because you used to say you thought it so beautiful; and because, when I first began to think about you, I used to peep in the glass, and wonder whether you would like very much to have a lock of it. Oh what a foolish fellow you were, Doady, when I gave you one!’\n\n‘That was on the day when you were painting the flowers I had given you, Dora, and when I told you how much in love I was.’\n\n‘Ah! but I didn’t like to tell you,’ says Dora, ‘then, how I had cried over them, because I believed you really liked me! When I can run about again as I used to do, Doady, let us go and see those places where we were such a silly couple, shall we? And take some of the old walks? And not forget poor papa?’\n\n‘Yes, we will, and have some happy days. So you must make haste to get well, my dear.’\n\n‘Oh, I shall soon do that! I am so much better, you don’t know!’\n\nIt is evening; and I sit in the same chair, by the same bed, with the same face turned towards me. We have been silent, and there is a smile upon her face. I have ceased to carry my light burden up and down stairs now. She lies here all the day.\n\n‘Doady!’\n\n‘My dear Dora!’\n\n‘You won’t think what I am going to say, unreasonable, after what you told me, such a little while ago, of Mr. Wickfield’s not being well? I want to see Agnes. Very much I want to see her.’\n\n‘I will write to her, my dear.’\n\n‘Will you?’\n\n‘Directly.’\n\n‘What a good, kind boy! Doady, take me on your arm. Indeed, my dear, it’s not a whim. It’s not a foolish fancy. I want, very much indeed, to see her!’\n\n‘I am certain of it. I have only to tell her so, and she is sure to come.’\n\n‘You are very lonely when you go downstairs, now?’ Dora whispers, with her arm about my neck.\n\n‘How can I be otherwise, my own love, when I see your empty chair?’\n\n‘My empty chair!’ She clings to me for a little while, in silence. ‘And you really miss me, Doady?’ looking up, and brightly smiling. ‘Even poor, giddy, stupid me?’\n\n‘My heart, who is there upon earth that I could miss so much?’\n\n‘Oh, husband! I am so glad, yet so sorry!’ creeping closer to me, and folding me in both her arms. She laughs and sobs, and then is quiet, and quite happy.\n\n‘Quite!’ she says. ‘Only give Agnes my dear love, and tell her that I want very, very, much to see her; and I have nothing left to wish for.’\n\n‘Except to get well again, Dora.’\n\n‘Ah, Doady! Sometimes I think--you know I always was a silly little thing!--that that will never be!’\n\n‘Don’t say so, Dora! Dearest love, don’t think so!’\n\n‘I won’t, if I can help it, Doady. But I am very happy; though my dear boy is so lonely by himself, before his child-wife’s empty chair!’\n\nIt is night; and I am with her still. Agnes has arrived; has been among us for a whole day and an evening. She, my aunt, and I, have sat with Dora since the morning, all together. We have not talked much, but Dora has been perfectly contented and cheerful. We are now alone.\n\nDo I know, now, that my child-wife will soon leave me? They have told me so; they have told me nothing new to my thoughts--but I am far from sure that I have taken that truth to heart. I cannot master it. I have withdrawn by myself, many times today, to weep. I have remembered Who wept for a parting between the living and the dead. I have bethought me of all that gracious and compassionate history. I have tried to resign myself, and to console myself; and that, I hope, I may have done imperfectly; but what I cannot firmly settle in my mind is, that the end will absolutely come. I hold her hand in mine, I hold her heart in mine, I see her love for me, alive in all its strength. I cannot shut out a pale lingering shadow of belief that she will be spared.\n\n‘I am going to speak to you, Doady. I am going to say something I have often thought of saying, lately. You won’t mind?’ with a gentle look.\n\n‘Mind, my darling?’\n\n‘Because I don’t know what you will think, or what you may have thought sometimes. Perhaps you have often thought the same. Doady, dear, I am afraid I was too young.’\n\nI lay my face upon the pillow by her, and she looks into my eyes, and speaks very softly. Gradually, as she goes on, I feel, with a stricken heart, that she is speaking of herself as past.\n\n‘I am afraid, dear, I was too young. I don’t mean in years only, but in experience, and thoughts, and everything. I was such a silly little creature! I am afraid it would have been better, if we had only loved each other as a boy and girl, and forgotten it. I have begun to think I was not fit to be a wife.’\n\nI try to stay my tears, and to reply, ‘Oh, Dora, love, as fit as I to be a husband!’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ with the old shake of her curls. ‘Perhaps! But if I had been more fit to be married I might have made you more so, too. Besides, you are very clever, and I never was.’\n\n‘We have been very happy, my sweet Dora.’\n\n‘I was very happy, very. But, as years went on, my dear boy would have wearied of his child-wife. She would have been less and less a companion for him. He would have been more and more sensible of what was wanting in his home. She wouldn’t have improved. It is better as it is.’\n\n‘Oh, Dora, dearest, dearest, do not speak to me so. Every word seems a reproach!’\n\n‘No, not a syllable!’ she answers, kissing me. ‘Oh, my dear, you never deserved it, and I loved you far too well to say a reproachful word to you, in earnest--it was all the merit I had, except being pretty--or you thought me so. Is it lonely, down-stairs, Doady?’\n\n‘Very! Very!’\n\n‘Don’t cry! Is my chair there?’\n\n‘In its old place.’\n\n‘Oh, how my poor boy cries! Hush, hush! Now, make me one promise. I want to speak to Agnes. When you go downstairs, tell Agnes so, and send her up to me; and while I speak to her, let no one come--not even aunt. I want to speak to Agnes by herself. I want to speak to Agnes, quite alone.’\n\nI promise that she shall, immediately; but I cannot leave her, for my grief.\n\n‘I said that it was better as it is!’ she whispers, as she holds me in her arms. ‘Oh, Doady, after more years, you never could have loved your child-wife better than you do; and, after more years, she would so have tried and disappointed you, that you might not have been able to love her half so well! I know I was too young and foolish. It is much better as it is!’\n\nAgnes is downstairs, when I go into the parlour; and I give her the message. She disappears, leaving me alone with Jip.\n\nHis Chinese house is by the fire; and he lies within it, on his bed of flannel, querulously trying to sleep. The bright moon is high and clear. As I look out on the night, my tears fall fast, and my undisciplined heart is chastened heavily--heavily.\n\nI sit down by the fire, thinking with a blind remorse of all those secret feelings I have nourished since my marriage. I think of every little trifle between me and Dora, and feel the truth, that trifles make the sum of life. Ever rising from the sea of my remembrance, is the image of the dear child as I knew her first, graced by my young love, and by her own, with every fascination wherein such love is rich. Would it, indeed, have been better if we had loved each other as a boy and a girl, and forgotten it? Undisciplined heart, reply!\n\nHow the time wears, I know not; until I am recalled by my child-wife’s old companion. More restless than he was, he crawls out of his house, and looks at me, and wanders to the door, and whines to go upstairs.\n\n‘Not tonight, Jip! Not tonight!’\n\nHe comes very slowly back to me, licks my hand, and lifts his dim eyes to my face.\n\n‘Oh, Jip! It may be, never again!’\n\nHe lies down at my feet, stretches himself out as if to sleep, and with a plaintive cry, is dead.\n\n‘Oh, Agnes! Look, look, here!’ --That face, so full of pity, and of grief, that rain of tears, that awful mute appeal to me, that solemn hand upraised towards Heaven!\n\n‘Agnes?’\n\nIt is over. Darkness comes before my eyes; and, for a time, all things are blotted out of my remembrance.\n\n# CHAPTER 54. Mr. MICAWBER’S TRANSACTIONS\n\nThis is not the time at which I am to enter on the state of my mind beneath its load of sorrow. I came to think that the Future was walled up before me, that the energy and action of my life were at an end, that I never could find any refuge but in the grave. I came to think so, I say, but not in the first shock of my grief. It slowly grew to that. If the events I go on to relate, had not thickened around me, in the beginning to confuse, and in the end to augment, my affliction, it is possible (though I think not probable), that I might have fallen at once into this condition. As it was, an interval occurred before I fully knew my own distress; an interval, in which I even supposed that its sharpest pangs were past; and when my mind could soothe itself by resting on all that was most innocent and beautiful, in the tender story that was closed for ever.\n\nWhen it was first proposed that I should go abroad, or how it came to be agreed among us that I was to seek the restoration of my peace in change and travel, I do not, even now, distinctly know. The spirit of Agnes so pervaded all we thought, and said, and did, in that time of sorrow, that I assume I may refer the project to her influence. But her influence was so quiet that I know no more.\n\nAnd now, indeed, I began to think that in my old association of her with the stained-glass window in the church, a prophetic foreshadowing of what she would be to me, in the calamity that was to happen in the fullness of time, had found a way into my mind. In all that sorrow, from the moment, never to be forgotten, when she stood before me with her upraised hand, she was like a sacred presence in my lonely house. When the Angel of Death alighted there, my child-wife fell asleep--they told me so when I could bear to hear it--on her bosom, with a smile. From my swoon, I first awoke to a consciousness of her compassionate tears, her words of hope and peace, her gentle face bending down as from a purer region nearer Heaven, over my undisciplined heart, and softening its pain.\n\nLet me go on.\n\nI was to go abroad. That seemed to have been determined among us from the first. The ground now covering all that could perish of my departed wife, I waited only for what Mr. Micawber called the ‘final pulverization of Heep’; and for the departure of the emigrants.\n\nAt the request of Traddles, most affectionate and devoted of friends in my trouble, we returned to Canterbury: I mean my aunt, Agnes, and I. We proceeded by appointment straight to Mr. Micawber’s house; where, and at Mr. Wickfield’s, my friend had been labouring ever since our explosive meeting. When poor Mrs. Micawber saw me come in, in my black clothes, she was sensibly affected. There was a great deal of good in Mrs. Micawber’s heart, which had not been dunned out of it in all those many years.\n\n‘Well, Mr. and Mrs. Micawber,’ was my aunt’s first salutation after we were seated. ‘Pray, have you thought about that emigration proposal of mine?’\n\n‘My dear madam,’ returned Mr. Micawber, ‘perhaps I cannot better express the conclusion at which Mrs. Micawber, your humble servant, and I may add our children, have jointly and severally arrived, than by borrowing the language of an illustrious poet, to reply that our Boat is on the shore, and our Bark is on the sea.’\n\n‘That’s right,’ said my aunt. ‘I augur all sort of good from your sensible decision.’\n\n‘Madam, you do us a great deal of honour,’ he rejoined. He then referred to a memorandum. ‘With respect to the pecuniary assistance enabling us to launch our frail canoe on the ocean of enterprise, I have reconsidered that important business-point; and would beg to propose my notes of hand--drawn, it is needless to stipulate, on stamps of the amounts respectively required by the various Acts of Parliament applying to such securities--at eighteen, twenty-four, and thirty months. The proposition I originally submitted, was twelve, eighteen, and twenty-four; but I am apprehensive that such an arrangement might not allow sufficient time for the requisite amount of--Something--to turn up. We might not,’ said Mr. Micawber, looking round the room as if it represented several hundred acres of highly cultivated land, ‘on the first responsibility becoming due, have been successful in our harvest, or we might not have got our harvest in. Labour, I believe, is sometimes difficult to obtain in that portion of our colonial possessions where it will be our lot to combat with the teeming soil.’\n\n‘Arrange it in any way you please, sir,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Madam,’ he replied, ‘Mrs. Micawber and myself are deeply sensible of the very considerate kindness of our friends and patrons. What I wish is, to be perfectly business-like, and perfectly punctual. Turning over, as we are about to turn over, an entirely new leaf; and falling back, as we are now in the act of falling back, for a Spring of no common magnitude; it is important to my sense of self-respect, besides being an example to my son, that these arrangements should be concluded as between man and man.’\n\nI don’t know that Mr. Micawber attached any meaning to this last phrase; I don’t know that anybody ever does, or did; but he appeared to relish it uncommonly, and repeated, with an impressive cough, ‘as between man and man’.\n\n‘I propose,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘Bills--a convenience to the mercantile world, for which, I believe, we are originally indebted to the Jews, who appear to me to have had a devilish deal too much to do with them ever since--because they are negotiable. But if a Bond, or any other description of security, would be preferred, I should be happy to execute any such instrument. As between man and man.’\n\nMy aunt observed, that in a case where both parties were willing to agree to anything, she took it for granted there would be no difficulty in settling this point. Mr. Micawber was of her opinion.\n\n‘In reference to our domestic preparations, madam,’ said Mr. Micawber, with some pride, ‘for meeting the destiny to which we are now understood to be self-devoted, I beg to report them. My eldest daughter attends at five every morning in a neighbouring establishment, to acquire the process--if process it may be called--of milking cows. My younger children are instructed to observe, as closely as circumstances will permit, the habits of the pigs and poultry maintained in the poorer parts of this city: a pursuit from which they have, on two occasions, been brought home, within an inch of being run over. I have myself directed some attention, during the past week, to the art of baking; and my son Wilkins has issued forth with a walking-stick and driven cattle, when permitted, by the rugged hirelings who had them in charge, to render any voluntary service in that direction--which I regret to say, for the credit of our nature, was not often; he being generally warned, with imprecations, to desist.’\n\n‘All very right indeed,’ said my aunt, encouragingly. ‘Mrs. Micawber has been busy, too, I have no doubt.’\n\n‘My dear madam,’ returned Mrs. Micawber, with her business-like air. ‘I am free to confess that I have not been actively engaged in pursuits immediately connected with cultivation or with stock, though well aware that both will claim my attention on a foreign shore. Such opportunities as I have been enabled to alienate from my domestic duties, I have devoted to corresponding at some length with my family. For I own it seems to me, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, who always fell back on me, I suppose from old habit, to whomsoever else she might address her discourse at starting, ‘that the time is come when the past should be buried in oblivion; when my family should take Mr. Micawber by the hand, and Mr. Micawber should take my family by the hand; when the lion should lie down with the lamb, and my family be on terms with Mr. Micawber.’\n\nI said I thought so too.\n\n‘This, at least, is the light, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ pursued Mrs. Micawber, ‘in which I view the subject. When I lived at home with my papa and mama, my papa was accustomed to ask, when any point was under discussion in our limited circle, “In what light does my Emma view the subject?” That my papa was too partial, I know; still, on such a point as the frigid coldness which has ever subsisted between Mr. Micawber and my family, I necessarily have formed an opinion, delusive though it may be.’\n\n‘No doubt. Of course you have, ma’am,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Precisely so,’ assented Mrs. Micawber. ‘Now, I may be wrong in my conclusions; it is very likely that I am, but my individual impression is, that the gulf between my family and Mr. Micawber may be traced to an apprehension, on the part of my family, that Mr. Micawber would require pecuniary accommodation. I cannot help thinking,’ said Mrs. Micawber, with an air of deep sagacity, ‘that there are members of my family who have been apprehensive that Mr. Micawber would solicit them for their names.---I do not mean to be conferred in Baptism upon our children, but to be inscribed on Bills of Exchange, and negotiated in the Money Market.’\n\nThe look of penetration with which Mrs. Micawber announced this discovery, as if no one had ever thought of it before, seemed rather to astonish my aunt; who abruptly replied, ‘Well, ma’am, upon the whole, I shouldn’t wonder if you were right!’\n\n‘Mr. Micawber being now on the eve of casting off the pecuniary shackles that have so long enthralled him,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘and of commencing a new career in a country where there is sufficient range for his abilities,--which, in my opinion, is exceedingly important; Mr. Micawber’s abilities peculiarly requiring space,--it seems to me that my family should signalize the occasion by coming forward. What I could wish to see, would be a meeting between Mr. Micawber and my family at a festive entertainment, to be given at my family’s expense; where Mr. Micawber’s health and prosperity being proposed, by some leading member of my family, Mr. Micawber might have an opportunity of developing his views.’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Micawber, with some heat, ‘it may be better for me to state distinctly, at once, that if I were to develop my views to that assembled group, they would possibly be found of an offensive nature: my impression being that your family are, in the aggregate, impertinent Snobs; and, in detail, unmitigated Ruffians.’\n\n‘Micawber,’ said Mrs. Micawber, shaking her head, ‘no! You have never understood them, and they have never understood you.’\n\nMr. Micawber coughed.\n\n‘They have never understood you, Micawber,’ said his wife. ‘They may be incapable of it. If so, that is their misfortune. I can pity their misfortune.’\n\n‘I am extremely sorry, my dear Emma,’ said Mr. Micawber, relenting, ‘to have been betrayed into any expressions that might, even remotely, have the appearance of being strong expressions. All I would say is, that I can go abroad without your family coming forward to favour me,--in short, with a parting Shove of their cold shoulders; and that, upon the whole, I would rather leave England with such impetus as I possess, than derive any acceleration of it from that quarter. At the same time, my dear, if they should condescend to reply to your communications--which our joint experience renders most improbable--far be it from me to be a barrier to your wishes.’\n\nThe matter being thus amicably settled, Mr. Micawber gave Mrs. Micawber his arm, and glancing at the heap of books and papers lying before Traddles on the table, said they would leave us to ourselves; which they ceremoniously did.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, leaning back in his chair when they were gone, and looking at me with an affection that made his eyes red, and his hair all kinds of shapes, ‘I don’t make any excuse for troubling you with business, because I know you are deeply interested in it, and it may divert your thoughts. My dear boy, I hope you are not worn out?’\n\n‘I am quite myself,’ said I, after a pause. ‘We have more cause to think of my aunt than of anyone. You know how much she has done.’\n\n‘Surely, surely,’ answered Traddles. ‘Who can forget it!’\n\n‘But even that is not all,’ said I. ‘During the last fortnight, some new trouble has vexed her; and she has been in and out of London every day. Several times she has gone out early, and been absent until evening. Last night, Traddles, with this journey before her, it was almost midnight before she came home. You know what her consideration for others is. She will not tell me what has happened to distress her.’\n\nMy aunt, very pale, and with deep lines in her face, sat immovable until I had finished; when some stray tears found their way to her cheeks, and she put her hand on mine.\n\n‘It’s nothing, Trot; it’s nothing. There will be no more of it. You shall know by and by. Now Agnes, my dear, let us attend to these affairs.’\n\n‘I must do Mr. Micawber the justice to say,’ Traddles began, ‘that although he would appear not to have worked to any good account for himself, he is a most untiring man when he works for other people. I never saw such a fellow. If he always goes on in the same way, he must be, virtually, about two hundred years old, at present. The heat into which he has been continually putting himself; and the distracted and impetuous manner in which he has been diving, day and night, among papers and books; to say nothing of the immense number of letters he has written me between this house and Mr. Wickfield’s, and often across the table when he has been sitting opposite, and might much more easily have spoken; is quite extraordinary.’\n\n‘Letters!’ cried my aunt. ‘I believe he dreams in letters!’\n\n‘There’s Mr. Dick, too,’ said Traddles, ‘has been doing wonders! As soon as he was released from overlooking Uriah Heep, whom he kept in such charge as I never saw exceeded, he began to devote himself to Mr. Wickfield. And really his anxiety to be of use in the investigations we have been making, and his real usefulness in extracting, and copying, and fetching, and carrying, have been quite stimulating to us.’\n\n‘Dick is a very remarkable man,’ exclaimed my aunt; ‘and I always said he was. Trot, you know it.’\n\n‘I am happy to say, Miss Wickfield,’ pursued Traddles, at once with great delicacy and with great earnestness, ‘that in your absence Mr. Wickfield has considerably improved. Relieved of the incubus that had fastened upon him for so long a time, and of the dreadful apprehensions under which he had lived, he is hardly the same person. At times, even his impaired power of concentrating his memory and attention on particular points of business, has recovered itself very much; and he has been able to assist us in making some things clear, that we should have found very difficult indeed, if not hopeless, without him. But what I have to do is to come to results; which are short enough; not to gossip on all the hopeful circumstances I have observed, or I shall never have done.’ His natural manner and agreeable simplicity made it transparent that he said this to put us in good heart, and to enable Agnes to hear her father mentioned with greater confidence; but it was not the less pleasant for that.\n\n‘Now, let me see,’ said Traddles, looking among the papers on the table. ‘Having counted our funds, and reduced to order a great mass of unintentional confusion in the first place, and of wilful confusion and falsification in the second, we take it to be clear that Mr. Wickfield might now wind up his business, and his agency-trust, and exhibit no deficiency or defalcation whatever.’\n\n‘Oh, thank Heaven!’ cried Agnes, fervently.\n\n‘But,’ said Traddles, ‘the surplus that would be left as his means of support--and I suppose the house to be sold, even in saying this--would be so small, not exceeding in all probability some hundreds of pounds, that perhaps, Miss Wickfield, it would be best to consider whether he might not retain his agency of the estate to which he has so long been receiver. His friends might advise him, you know; now he is free. You yourself, Miss Wickfield--Copperfield--I--’\n\n‘I have considered it, Trotwood,’ said Agnes, looking to me, ‘and I feel that it ought not to be, and must not be; even on the recommendation of a friend to whom I am so grateful, and owe so much.’\n\n‘I will not say that I recommend it,’ observed Traddles. ‘I think it right to suggest it. No more.’\n\n‘I am happy to hear you say so,’ answered Agnes, steadily, ‘for it gives me hope, almost assurance, that we think alike. Dear Mr. Traddles and dear Trotwood, papa once free with honour, what could I wish for! I have always aspired, if I could have released him from the toils in which he was held, to render back some little portion of the love and care I owe him, and to devote my life to him. It has been, for years, the utmost height of my hopes. To take our future on myself, will be the next great happiness--the next to his release from all trust and responsibility--that I can know.’\n\n‘Have you thought how, Agnes?’\n\n‘Often! I am not afraid, dear Trotwood. I am certain of success. So many people know me here, and think kindly of me, that I am certain. Don’t mistrust me. Our wants are not many. If I rent the dear old house, and keep a school, I shall be useful and happy.’\n\nThe calm fervour of her cheerful voice brought back so vividly, first the dear old house itself, and then my solitary home, that my heart was too full for speech. Traddles pretended for a little while to be busily looking among the papers.\n\n‘Next, Miss Trotwood,’ said Traddles, ‘that property of yours.’\n\n‘Well, sir,’ sighed my aunt. ‘All I have got to say about it is, that if it’s gone, I can bear it; and if it’s not gone, I shall be glad to get it back.’\n\n‘It was originally, I think, eight thousand pounds, Consols?’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Right!’ replied my aunt.\n\n‘I can’t account for more than five,’ said Traddles, with an air of perplexity.\n\n‘--thousand, do you mean?’ inquired my aunt, with uncommon composure, ‘or pounds?’\n\n‘Five thousand pounds,’ said Traddles.\n\n‘It was all there was,’ returned my aunt. ‘I sold three, myself. One, I paid for your articles, Trot, my dear; and the other two I have by me. When I lost the rest, I thought it wise to say nothing about that sum, but to keep it secretly for a rainy day. I wanted to see how you would come out of the trial, Trot; and you came out nobly--persevering, self-reliant, self-denying! So did Dick. Don’t speak to me, for I find my nerves a little shaken!’\n\nNobody would have thought so, to see her sitting upright, with her arms folded; but she had wonderful self-command.\n\n‘Then I am delighted to say,’ cried Traddles, beaming with joy, ‘that we have recovered the whole money!’\n\n‘Don’t congratulate me, anybody!’ exclaimed my aunt. ‘How so, sir?’\n\n‘You believed it had been misappropriated by Mr. Wickfield?’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Of course I did,’ said my aunt, ‘and was therefore easily silenced. Agnes, not a word!’\n\n‘And indeed,’ said Traddles, ‘it was sold, by virtue of the power of management he held from you; but I needn’t say by whom sold, or on whose actual signature. It was afterwards pretended to Mr. Wickfield, by that rascal,--and proved, too, by figures,--that he had possessed himself of the money (on general instructions, he said) to keep other deficiencies and difficulties from the light. Mr. Wickfield, being so weak and helpless in his hands as to pay you, afterwards, several sums of interest on a pretended principal which he knew did not exist, made himself, unhappily, a party to the fraud.’\n\n‘And at last took the blame upon himself,’ added my aunt; ‘and wrote me a mad letter, charging himself with robbery, and wrong unheard of. Upon which I paid him a visit early one morning, called for a candle, burnt the letter, and told him if he ever could right me and himself, to do it; and if he couldn’t, to keep his own counsel for his daughter’s sake.---If anybody speaks to me, I’ll leave the house!’\n\nWe all remained quiet; Agnes covering her face.\n\n‘Well, my dear friend,’ said my aunt, after a pause, ‘and you have really extorted the money back from him?’\n\n‘Why, the fact is,’ returned Traddles, ‘Mr. Micawber had so completely hemmed him in, and was always ready with so many new points if an old one failed, that he could not escape from us. A most remarkable circumstance is, that I really don’t think he grasped this sum even so much for the gratification of his avarice, which was inordinate, as in the hatred he felt for Copperfield. He said so to me, plainly. He said he would even have spent as much, to baulk or injure Copperfield.’\n\n‘Ha!’ said my aunt, knitting her brows thoughtfully, and glancing at Agnes. ‘And what’s become of him?’\n\n‘I don’t know. He left here,’ said Traddles, ‘with his mother, who had been clamouring, and beseeching, and disclosing, the whole time. They went away by one of the London night coaches, and I know no more about him; except that his malevolence to me at parting was audacious. He seemed to consider himself hardly less indebted to me, than to Mr. Micawber; which I consider (as I told him) quite a compliment.’\n\n‘Do you suppose he has any money, Traddles?’ I asked.\n\n‘Oh dear, yes, I should think so,’ he replied, shaking his head, seriously. ‘I should say he must have pocketed a good deal, in one way or other. But, I think you would find, Copperfield, if you had an opportunity of observing his course, that money would never keep that man out of mischief. He is such an incarnate hypocrite, that whatever object he pursues, he must pursue crookedly. It’s his only compensation for the outward restraints he puts upon himself. Always creeping along the ground to some small end or other, he will always magnify every object in the way; and consequently will hate and suspect everybody that comes, in the most innocent manner, between him and it. So the crooked courses will become crookeder, at any moment, for the least reason, or for none. It’s only necessary to consider his history here,’ said Traddles, ‘to know that.’\n\n‘He’s a monster of meanness!’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Really I don’t know about that,’ observed Traddles thoughtfully. ‘Many people can be very mean, when they give their minds to it.’\n\n‘And now, touching Mr. Micawber,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Well, really,’ said Traddles, cheerfully, ‘I must, once more, give Mr. Micawber high praise. But for his having been so patient and persevering for so long a time, we never could have hoped to do anything worth speaking of. And I think we ought to consider that Mr. Micawber did right, for right’s sake, when we reflect what terms he might have made with Uriah Heep himself, for his silence.’\n\n‘I think so too,’ said I.\n\n‘Now, what would you give him?’ inquired my aunt.\n\n‘Oh! Before you come to that,’ said Traddles, a little disconcerted, ‘I am afraid I thought it discreet to omit (not being able to carry everything before me) two points, in making this lawless adjustment--for it’s perfectly lawless from beginning to end--of a difficult affair. Those I.O.U.’s, and so forth, which Mr. Micawber gave him for the advances he had--’\n\n‘Well! They must be paid,’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Yes, but I don’t know when they may be proceeded on, or where they are,’ rejoined Traddles, opening his eyes; ‘and I anticipate, that, between this time and his departure, Mr. Micawber will be constantly arrested, or taken in execution.’\n\n‘Then he must be constantly set free again, and taken out of execution,’ said my aunt. ‘What’s the amount altogether?’\n\n‘Why, Mr. Micawber has entered the transactions--he calls them transactions--with great form, in a book,’ rejoined Traddles, smiling; ‘and he makes the amount a hundred and three pounds, five.’\n\n‘Now, what shall we give him, that sum included?’ said my aunt. ‘Agnes, my dear, you and I can talk about division of it afterwards. What should it be? Five hundred pounds?’\n\nUpon this, Traddles and I both struck in at once. We both recommended a small sum in money, and the payment, without stipulation to Mr. Micawber, of the Uriah claims as they came in. We proposed that the family should have their passage and their outfit, and a hundred pounds; and that Mr. Micawber’s arrangement for the repayment of the advances should be gravely entered into, as it might be wholesome for him to suppose himself under that responsibility. To this, I added the suggestion, that I should give some explanation of his character and history to Mr. Peggotty, who I knew could be relied on; and that to Mr. Peggotty should be quietly entrusted the discretion of advancing another hundred. I further proposed to interest Mr. Micawber in Mr. Peggotty, by confiding so much of Mr. Peggotty’s story to him as I might feel justified in relating, or might think expedient; and to endeavour to bring each of them to bear upon the other, for the common advantage. We all entered warmly into these views; and I may mention at once, that the principals themselves did so, shortly afterwards, with perfect good will and harmony.\n\nSeeing that Traddles now glanced anxiously at my aunt again, I reminded him of the second and last point to which he had adverted.\n\n‘You and your aunt will excuse me, Copperfield, if I touch upon a painful theme, as I greatly fear I shall,’ said Traddles, hesitating; ‘but I think it necessary to bring it to your recollection. On the day of Mr. Micawber’s memorable denunciation a threatening allusion was made by Uriah Heep to your aunt’s--husband.’\n\nMy aunt, retaining her stiff position, and apparent composure, assented with a nod.\n\n‘Perhaps,’ observed Traddles, ‘it was mere purposeless impertinence?’\n\n‘No,’ returned my aunt.\n\n‘There was--pardon me--really such a person, and at all in his power?’ hinted Traddles.\n\n‘Yes, my good friend,’ said my aunt.\n\nTraddles, with a perceptible lengthening of his face, explained that he had not been able to approach this subject; that it had shared the fate of Mr. Micawber’s liabilities, in not being comprehended in the terms he had made; that we were no longer of any authority with Uriah Heep; and that if he could do us, or any of us, any injury or annoyance, no doubt he would.\n\nMy aunt remained quiet; until again some stray tears found their way to her cheeks. ‘You are quite right,’ she said. ‘It was very thoughtful to mention it.’\n\n‘Can I--or Copperfield--do anything?’ asked Traddles, gently.\n\n‘Nothing,’ said my aunt. ‘I thank you many times. Trot, my dear, a vain threat! Let us have Mr. and Mrs. Micawber back. And don’t any of you speak to me!’ With that she smoothed her dress, and sat, with her upright carriage, looking at the door.\n\n‘Well, Mr. and Mrs. Micawber!’ said my aunt, when they entered. ‘We have been discussing your emigration, with many apologies to you for keeping you out of the room so long; and I’ll tell you what arrangements we propose.’\n\nThese she explained to the unbounded satisfaction of the family,--children and all being then present,--and so much to the awakening of Mr. Micawber’s punctual habits in the opening stage of all bill transactions, that he could not be dissuaded from immediately rushing out, in the highest spirits, to buy the stamps for his notes of hand. But, his joy received a sudden check; for within five minutes, he returned in the custody of a sheriff’s officer, informing us, in a flood of tears, that all was lost. We, being quite prepared for this event, which was of course a proceeding of Uriah Heep’s, soon paid the money; and in five minutes more Mr. Micawber was seated at the table, filling up the stamps with an expression of perfect joy, which only that congenial employment, or the making of punch, could impart in full completeness to his shining face. To see him at work on the stamps, with the relish of an artist, touching them like pictures, looking at them sideways, taking weighty notes of dates and amounts in his pocket-book, and contemplating them when finished, with a high sense of their precious value, was a sight indeed.\n\n‘Now, the best thing you can do, sir, if you’ll allow me to advise you,’ said my aunt, after silently observing him, ‘is to abjure that occupation for evermore.’\n\n‘Madam,’ replied Mr. Micawber, ‘it is my intention to register such a vow on the virgin page of the future. Mrs. Micawber will attest it. I trust,’ said Mr. Micawber, solemnly, ‘that my son Wilkins will ever bear in mind, that he had infinitely better put his fist in the fire, than use it to handle the serpents that have poisoned the life-blood of his unhappy parent!’ Deeply affected, and changed in a moment to the image of despair, Mr. Micawber regarded the serpents with a look of gloomy abhorrence (in which his late admiration of them was not quite subdued), folded them up and put them in his pocket.\n\nThis closed the proceedings of the evening. We were weary with sorrow and fatigue, and my aunt and I were to return to London on the morrow. It was arranged that the Micawbers should follow us, after effecting a sale of their goods to a broker; that Mr. Wickfield’s affairs should be brought to a settlement, with all convenient speed, under the direction of Traddles; and that Agnes should also come to London, pending those arrangements. We passed the night at the old house, which, freed from the presence of the Heeps, seemed purged of a disease; and I lay in my old room, like a shipwrecked wanderer come home.\n\nWe went back next day to my aunt’s house--not to mine--and when she and I sat alone, as of old, before going to bed, she said:\n\n‘Trot, do you really wish to know what I have had upon my mind lately?’\n\n‘Indeed I do, aunt. If there ever was a time when I felt unwilling that you should have a sorrow or anxiety which I could not share, it is now.’\n\n‘You have had sorrow enough, child,’ said my aunt, affectionately, ‘without the addition of my little miseries. I could have no other motive, Trot, in keeping anything from you.’\n\n‘I know that well,’ said I. ‘But tell me now.’\n\n‘Would you ride with me a little way tomorrow morning?’ asked my aunt.\n\n‘Of course.’\n\n‘At nine,’ said she. ‘I’ll tell you then, my dear.’\n\nAt nine, accordingly, we went out in a little chariot, and drove to London. We drove a long way through the streets, until we came to one of the large hospitals. Standing hard by the building was a plain hearse. The driver recognized my aunt, and, in obedience to a motion of her hand at the window, drove slowly off; we following.\n\n‘You understand it now, Trot,’ said my aunt. ‘He is gone!’\n\n‘Did he die in the hospital?’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\nShe sat immovable beside me; but, again I saw the stray tears on her face.\n\n‘He was there once before,’ said my aunt presently. ‘He was ailing a long time--a shattered, broken man, these many years. When he knew his state in this last illness, he asked them to send for me. He was sorry then. Very sorry.’\n\n‘You went, I know, aunt.’\n\n‘I went. I was with him a good deal afterwards.’\n\n‘He died the night before we went to Canterbury?’ said I. My aunt nodded. ‘No one can harm him now,’ she said. ‘It was a vain threat.’\n\nWe drove away, out of town, to the churchyard at Hornsey. ‘Better here than in the streets,’ said my aunt. ‘He was born here.’\n\nWe alighted; and followed the plain coffin to a corner I remember well, where the service was read consigning it to the dust.\n\n‘Six-and-thirty years ago, this day, my dear,’ said my aunt, as we walked back to the chariot, ‘I was married. God forgive us all!’ We took our seats in silence; and so she sat beside me for a long time, holding my hand. At length she suddenly burst into tears, and said:\n\n‘He was a fine-looking man when I married him, Trot--and he was sadly changed!’\n\nIt did not last long. After the relief of tears, she soon became composed, and even cheerful. Her nerves were a little shaken, she said, or she would not have given way to it. God forgive us all!\n\nSo we rode back to her little cottage at Highgate, where we found the following short note, which had arrived by that morning’s post from Mr. Micawber:\n\n‘Canterbury,\n\n‘Friday.\n\n‘My dear Madam, and Copperfield,\n\n‘The fair land of promise lately looming on the horizon is again enveloped in impenetrable mists, and for ever withdrawn from the eyes of a drifting wretch whose Doom is sealed!\n\n‘Another writ has been issued (in His Majesty’s High Court of King’s Bench at Westminster), in another cause of HEEP V. MICAWBER, and the defendant in that cause is the prey of the sheriff having legal jurisdiction in this bailiwick.\n\n‘Now’s the day, and now’s the hour, See the front of battle lower, See approach proud EDWARD’S power-- Chains and slavery!\n\n‘Consigned to which, and to a speedy end (for mental torture is not supportable beyond a certain point, and that point I feel I have attained), my course is run. Bless you, bless you! Some future traveller, visiting, from motives of curiosity, not unmingled, let us hope, with sympathy, the place of confinement allotted to debtors in this city, may, and I trust will, Ponder, as he traces on its wall, inscribed with a rusty nail,\n\n‘The obscure initials,\n\n‘P.S. I re-open this to say that our common friend, Mr. Thomas Traddles (who has not yet left us, and is looking extremely well), has paid the debt and costs, in the noble name of Miss Trotwood; and that myself and family are at the height of earthly bliss.’\n\n# CHAPTER 55. TEMPEST\n\nI now approach an event in my life, so indelible, so awful, so bound by an infinite variety of ties to all that has preceded it, in these pages, that, from the beginning of my narrative, I have seen it growing larger and larger as I advanced, like a great tower in a plain, and throwing its fore-cast shadow even on the incidents of my childish days.\n\nFor years after it occurred, I dreamed of it often. I have started up so vividly impressed by it, that its fury has yet seemed raging in my quiet room, in the still night. I dream of it sometimes, though at lengthened and uncertain intervals, to this hour. I have an association between it and a stormy wind, or the lightest mention of a sea-shore, as strong as any of which my mind is conscious. As plainly as I behold what happened, I will try to write it down. I do not recall it, but see it done; for it happens again before me.\n\nThe time drawing on rapidly for the sailing of the emigrant-ship, my good old nurse (almost broken-hearted for me, when we first met) came up to London. I was constantly with her, and her brother, and the Micawbers (they being very much together); but Emily I never saw.\n\nOne evening when the time was close at hand, I was alone with Peggotty and her brother. Our conversation turned on Ham. She described to us how tenderly he had taken leave of her, and how manfully and quietly he had borne himself. Most of all, of late, when she believed he was most tried. It was a subject of which the affectionate creature never tired; and our interest in hearing the many examples which she, who was so much with him, had to relate, was equal to hers in relating them.\n\nMy aunt and I were at that time vacating the two cottages at Highgate; I intending to go abroad, and she to return to her house at Dover. We had a temporary lodging in Covent Garden. As I walked home to it, after this evening’s conversation, reflecting on what had passed between Ham and myself when I was last at Yarmouth, I wavered in the original purpose I had formed, of leaving a letter for Emily when I should take leave of her uncle on board the ship, and thought it would be better to write to her now. She might desire, I thought, after receiving my communication, to send some parting word by me to her unhappy lover. I ought to give her the opportunity.\n\nI therefore sat down in my room, before going to bed, and wrote to her. I told her that I had seen him, and that he had requested me to tell her what I have already written in its place in these sheets. I faithfully repeated it. I had no need to enlarge upon it, if I had had the right. Its deep fidelity and goodness were not to be adorned by me or any man. I left it out, to be sent round in the morning; with a line to Mr. Peggotty, requesting him to give it to her; and went to bed at daybreak.\n\nI was weaker than I knew then; and, not falling asleep until the sun was up, lay late, and unrefreshed, next day. I was roused by the silent presence of my aunt at my bedside. I felt it in my sleep, as I suppose we all do feel such things.\n\n‘Trot, my dear,’ she said, when I opened my eyes, ‘I couldn’t make up my mind to disturb you. Mr. Peggotty is here; shall he come up?’\n\nI replied yes, and he soon appeared.\n\n‘Mas’r Davy,’ he said, when we had shaken hands, ‘I giv Em’ly your letter, sir, and she writ this heer; and begged of me fur to ask you to read it, and if you see no hurt in’t, to be so kind as take charge on’t.’\n\n‘Have you read it?’ said I.\n\nHe nodded sorrowfully. I opened it, and read as follows:\n\n‘I have got your message. Oh, what can I write, to thank you for your good and blessed kindness to me!\n\n‘I have put the words close to my heart. I shall keep them till I die. They are sharp thorns, but they are such comfort. I have prayed over them, oh, I have prayed so much. When I find what you are, and what uncle is, I think what God must be, and can cry to him.\n\n‘Good-bye for ever. Now, my dear, my friend, good-bye for ever in this world. In another world, if I am forgiven, I may wake a child and come to you. All thanks and blessings. Farewell, evermore.’\n\nThis, blotted with tears, was the letter.\n\n‘May I tell her as you doen’t see no hurt in’t, and as you’ll be so kind as take charge on’t, Mas’r Davy?’ said Mr. Peggotty, when I had read it. ‘Unquestionably,’ said I--‘but I am thinking--’\n\n‘Yes, Mas’r Davy?’\n\n‘I am thinking,’ said I, ‘that I’ll go down again to Yarmouth. There’s time, and to spare, for me to go and come back before the ship sails. My mind is constantly running on him, in his solitude; to put this letter of her writing in his hand at this time, and to enable you to tell her, in the moment of parting, that he has got it, will be a kindness to both of them. I solemnly accepted his commission, dear good fellow, and cannot discharge it too completely. The journey is nothing to me. I am restless, and shall be better in motion. I’ll go down tonight.’\n\nThough he anxiously endeavoured to dissuade me, I saw that he was of my mind; and this, if I had required to be confirmed in my intention, would have had the effect. He went round to the coach office, at my request, and took the box-seat for me on the mail. In the evening I started, by that conveyance, down the road I had traversed under so many vicissitudes.\n\n‘Don’t you think that,’ I asked the coachman, in the first stage out of London, ‘a very remarkable sky? I don’t remember to have seen one like it.’\n\n‘Nor I--not equal to it,’ he replied. ‘That’s wind, sir. There’ll be mischief done at sea, I expect, before long.’\n\nIt was a murky confusion--here and there blotted with a colour like the colour of the smoke from damp fuel--of flying clouds, tossed up into most remarkable heaps, suggesting greater heights in the clouds than there were depths below them to the bottom of the deepest hollows in the earth, through which the wild moon seemed to plunge headlong, as if, in a dread disturbance of the laws of nature, she had lost her way and were frightened. There had been a wind all day; and it was rising then, with an extraordinary great sound. In another hour it had much increased, and the sky was more overcast, and blew hard.\n\nBut, as the night advanced, the clouds closing in and densely over-spreading the whole sky, then very dark, it came on to blow, harder and harder. It still increased, until our horses could scarcely face the wind. Many times, in the dark part of the night (it was then late in September, when the nights were not short), the leaders turned about, or came to a dead stop; and we were often in serious apprehension that the coach would be blown over. Sweeping gusts of rain came up before this storm, like showers of steel; and, at those times, when there was any shelter of trees or lee walls to be got, we were fain to stop, in a sheer impossibility of continuing the struggle.\n\nWhen the day broke, it blew harder and harder. I had been in Yarmouth when the seamen said it blew great guns, but I had never known the like of this, or anything approaching to it. We came to Ipswich--very late, having had to fight every inch of ground since we were ten miles out of London; and found a cluster of people in the market-place, who had risen from their beds in the night, fearful of falling chimneys. Some of these, congregating about the inn-yard while we changed horses, told us of great sheets of lead having been ripped off a high church-tower, and flung into a by-street, which they then blocked up. Others had to tell of country people, coming in from neighbouring villages, who had seen great trees lying torn out of the earth, and whole ricks scattered about the roads and fields. Still, there was no abatement in the storm, but it blew harder.\n\nAs we struggled on, nearer and nearer to the sea, from which this mighty wind was blowing dead on shore, its force became more and more terrific. Long before we saw the sea, its spray was on our lips, and showered salt rain upon us. The water was out, over miles and miles of the flat country adjacent to Yarmouth; and every sheet and puddle lashed its banks, and had its stress of little breakers setting heavily towards us. When we came within sight of the sea, the waves on the horizon, caught at intervals above the rolling abyss, were like glimpses of another shore with towers and buildings. When at last we got into the town, the people came out to their doors, all aslant, and with streaming hair, making a wonder of the mail that had come through such a night.\n\nI put up at the old inn, and went down to look at the sea; staggering along the street, which was strewn with sand and seaweed, and with flying blotches of sea-foam; afraid of falling slates and tiles; and holding by people I met, at angry corners. Coming near the beach, I saw, not only the boatmen, but half the people of the town, lurking behind buildings; some, now and then braving the fury of the storm to look away to sea, and blown sheer out of their course in trying to get zigzag back.\n\nJoining these groups, I found bewailing women whose husbands were away in herring or oyster boats, which there was too much reason to think might have foundered before they could run in anywhere for safety. Grizzled old sailors were among the people, shaking their heads, as they looked from water to sky, and muttering to one another; ship-owners, excited and uneasy; children, huddling together, and peering into older faces; even stout mariners, disturbed and anxious, levelling their glasses at the sea from behind places of shelter, as if they were surveying an enemy.\n\nThe tremendous sea itself, when I could find sufficient pause to look at it, in the agitation of the blinding wind, the flying stones and sand, and the awful noise, confounded me. As the high watery walls came rolling in, and, at their highest, tumbled into surf, they looked as if the least would engulf the town. As the receding wave swept back with a hoarse roar, it seemed to scoop out deep caves in the beach, as if its purpose were to undermine the earth. When some white-headed billows thundered on, and dashed themselves to pieces before they reached the land, every fragment of the late whole seemed possessed by the full might of its wrath, rushing to be gathered to the composition of another monster. Undulating hills were changed to valleys, undulating valleys (with a solitary storm-bird sometimes skimming through them) were lifted up to hills; masses of water shivered and shook the beach with a booming sound; every shape tumultuously rolled on, as soon as made, to change its shape and place, and beat another shape and place away; the ideal shore on the horizon, with its towers and buildings, rose and fell; the clouds fell fast and thick; I seemed to see a rending and upheaving of all nature.\n\nNot finding Ham among the people whom this memorable wind--for it is still remembered down there, as the greatest ever known to blow upon that coast--had brought together, I made my way to his house. It was shut; and as no one answered to my knocking, I went, by back ways and by-lanes, to the yard where he worked. I learned, there, that he had gone to Lowestoft, to meet some sudden exigency of ship-repairing in which his skill was required; but that he would be back tomorrow morning, in good time.\n\nI went back to the inn; and when I had washed and dressed, and tried to sleep, but in vain, it was five o’clock in the afternoon. I had not sat five minutes by the coffee-room fire, when the waiter, coming to stir it, as an excuse for talking, told me that two colliers had gone down, with all hands, a few miles away; and that some other ships had been seen labouring hard in the Roads, and trying, in great distress, to keep off shore. Mercy on them, and on all poor sailors, said he, if we had another night like the last!\n\nI was very much depressed in spirits; very solitary; and felt an uneasiness in Ham’s not being there, disproportionate to the occasion. I was seriously affected, without knowing how much, by late events; and my long exposure to the fierce wind had confused me. There was that jumble in my thoughts and recollections, that I had lost the clear arrangement of time and distance. Thus, if I had gone out into the town, I should not have been surprised, I think, to encounter someone who I knew must be then in London. So to speak, there was in these respects a curious inattention in my mind. Yet it was busy, too, with all the remembrances the place naturally awakened; and they were particularly distinct and vivid.\n\nIn this state, the waiter’s dismal intelligence about the ships immediately connected itself, without any effort of my volition, with my uneasiness about Ham. I was persuaded that I had an apprehension of his returning from Lowestoft by sea, and being lost. This grew so strong with me, that I resolved to go back to the yard before I took my dinner, and ask the boat-builder if he thought his attempting to return by sea at all likely? If he gave me the least reason to think so, I would go over to Lowestoft and prevent it by bringing him with me.\n\nI hastily ordered my dinner, and went back to the yard. I was none too soon; for the boat-builder, with a lantern in his hand, was locking the yard-gate. He quite laughed when I asked him the question, and said there was no fear; no man in his senses, or out of them, would put off in such a gale of wind, least of all Ham Peggotty, who had been born to seafaring.\n\nSo sensible of this, beforehand, that I had really felt ashamed of doing what I was nevertheless impelled to do, I went back to the inn. If such a wind could rise, I think it was rising. The howl and roar, the rattling of the doors and windows, the rumbling in the chimneys, the apparent rocking of the very house that sheltered me, and the prodigious tumult of the sea, were more fearful than in the morning. But there was now a great darkness besides; and that invested the storm with new terrors, real and fanciful.\n\nI could not eat, I could not sit still, I could not continue steadfast to anything. Something within me, faintly answering to the storm without, tossed up the depths of my memory and made a tumult in them. Yet, in all the hurry of my thoughts, wild running with the thundering sea,--the storm, and my uneasiness regarding Ham were always in the fore-ground.\n\nMy dinner went away almost untasted, and I tried to refresh myself with a glass or two of wine. In vain. I fell into a dull slumber before the fire, without losing my consciousness, either of the uproar out of doors, or of the place in which I was. Both became overshadowed by a new and indefinable horror; and when I awoke--or rather when I shook off the lethargy that bound me in my chair--my whole frame thrilled with objectless and unintelligible fear.\n\nI walked to and fro, tried to read an old gazetteer, listened to the awful noises: looked at faces, scenes, and figures in the fire. At length, the steady ticking of the undisturbed clock on the wall tormented me to that degree that I resolved to go to bed.\n\nIt was reassuring, on such a night, to be told that some of the inn-servants had agreed together to sit up until morning. I went to bed, exceedingly weary and heavy; but, on my lying down, all such sensations vanished, as if by magic, and I was broad awake, with every sense refined.\n\nFor hours I lay there, listening to the wind and water; imagining, now, that I heard shrieks out at sea; now, that I distinctly heard the firing of signal guns; and now, the fall of houses in the town. I got up, several times, and looked out; but could see nothing, except the reflection in the window-panes of the faint candle I had left burning, and of my own haggard face looking in at me from the black void.\n\nAt length, my restlessness attained to such a pitch, that I hurried on my clothes, and went downstairs. In the large kitchen, where I dimly saw bacon and ropes of onions hanging from the beams, the watchers were clustered together, in various attitudes, about a table, purposely moved away from the great chimney, and brought near the door. A pretty girl, who had her ears stopped with her apron, and her eyes upon the door, screamed when I appeared, supposing me to be a spirit; but the others had more presence of mind, and were glad of an addition to their company. One man, referring to the topic they had been discussing, asked me whether I thought the souls of the collier-crews who had gone down, were out in the storm?\n\nI remained there, I dare say, two hours. Once, I opened the yard-gate, and looked into the empty street. The sand, the sea-weed, and the flakes of foam, were driving by; and I was obliged to call for assistance before I could shut the gate again, and make it fast against the wind.\n\nThere was a dark gloom in my solitary chamber, when I at length returned to it; but I was tired now, and, getting into bed again, fell--off a tower and down a precipice--into the depths of sleep. I have an impression that for a long time, though I dreamed of being elsewhere and in a variety of scenes, it was always blowing in my dream. At length, I lost that feeble hold upon reality, and was engaged with two dear friends, but who they were I don’t know, at the siege of some town in a roar of cannonading.\n\nThe thunder of the cannon was so loud and incessant, that I could not hear something I much desired to hear, until I made a great exertion and awoke. It was broad day--eight or nine o’clock; the storm raging, in lieu of the batteries; and someone knocking and calling at my door.\n\n‘What is the matter?’ I cried.\n\n‘A wreck! Close by!’\n\nI sprung out of bed, and asked, what wreck?\n\n‘A schooner, from Spain or Portugal, laden with fruit and wine. Make haste, sir, if you want to see her! It’s thought, down on the beach, she’ll go to pieces every moment.’\n\nThe excited voice went clamouring along the staircase; and I wrapped myself in my clothes as quickly as I could, and ran into the street.\n\nNumbers of people were there before me, all running in one direction, to the beach. I ran the same way, outstripping a good many, and soon came facing the wild sea.\n\nThe wind might by this time have lulled a little, though not more sensibly than if the cannonading I had dreamed of, had been diminished by the silencing of half-a-dozen guns out of hundreds. But the sea, having upon it the additional agitation of the whole night, was infinitely more terrific than when I had seen it last. Every appearance it had then presented, bore the expression of being swelled; and the height to which the breakers rose, and, looking over one another, bore one another down, and rolled in, in interminable hosts, was most appalling. In the difficulty of hearing anything but wind and waves, and in the crowd, and the unspeakable confusion, and my first breathless efforts to stand against the weather, I was so confused that I looked out to sea for the wreck, and saw nothing but the foaming heads of the great waves. A half-dressed boatman, standing next me, pointed with his bare arm (a tattoo’d arrow on it, pointing in the same direction) to the left. Then, O great Heaven, I saw it, close in upon us!\n\nOne mast was broken short off, six or eight feet from the deck, and lay over the side, entangled in a maze of sail and rigging; and all that ruin, as the ship rolled and beat--which she did without a moment’s pause, and with a violence quite inconceivable--beat the side as if it would stave it in. Some efforts were even then being made, to cut this portion of the wreck away; for, as the ship, which was broadside on, turned towards us in her rolling, I plainly descried her people at work with axes, especially one active figure with long curling hair, conspicuous among the rest. But a great cry, which was audible even above the wind and water, rose from the shore at this moment; the sea, sweeping over the rolling wreck, made a clean breach, and carried men, spars, casks, planks, bulwarks, heaps of such toys, into the boiling surge.\n\nThe second mast was yet standing, with the rags of a rent sail, and a wild confusion of broken cordage flapping to and fro. The ship had struck once, the same boatman hoarsely said in my ear, and then lifted in and struck again. I understood him to add that she was parting amidships, and I could readily suppose so, for the rolling and beating were too tremendous for any human work to suffer long. As he spoke, there was another great cry of pity from the beach; four men arose with the wreck out of the deep, clinging to the rigging of the remaining mast; uppermost, the active figure with the curling hair.\n\nThere was a bell on board; and as the ship rolled and dashed, like a desperate creature driven mad, now showing us the whole sweep of her deck, as she turned on her beam-ends towards the shore, now nothing but her keel, as she sprung wildly over and turned towards the sea, the bell rang; and its sound, the knell of those unhappy men, was borne towards us on the wind. Again we lost her, and again she rose. Two men were gone. The agony on the shore increased. Men groaned, and clasped their hands; women shrieked, and turned away their faces. Some ran wildly up and down along the beach, crying for help where no help could be. I found myself one of these, frantically imploring a knot of sailors whom I knew, not to let those two lost creatures perish before our eyes.\n\nThey were making out to me, in an agitated way--I don’t know how, for the little I could hear I was scarcely composed enough to understand--that the lifeboat had been bravely manned an hour ago, and could do nothing; and that as no man would be so desperate as to attempt to wade off with a rope, and establish a communication with the shore, there was nothing left to try; when I noticed that some new sensation moved the people on the beach, and saw them part, and Ham come breaking through them to the front.\n\nI ran to him--as well as I know, to repeat my appeal for help. But, distracted though I was, by a sight so new to me and terrible, the determination in his face, and his look out to sea--exactly the same look as I remembered in connexion with the morning after Emily’s flight--awoke me to a knowledge of his danger. I held him back with both arms; and implored the men with whom I had been speaking, not to listen to him, not to do murder, not to let him stir from off that sand!\n\nAnother cry arose on shore; and looking to the wreck, we saw the cruel sail, with blow on blow, beat off the lower of the two men, and fly up in triumph round the active figure left alone upon the mast.\n\nAgainst such a sight, and against such determination as that of the calmly desperate man who was already accustomed to lead half the people present, I might as hopefully have entreated the wind. ‘Mas’r Davy,’ he said, cheerily grasping me by both hands, ‘if my time is come, ‘tis come. If ‘tan’t, I’ll bide it. Lord above bless you, and bless all! Mates, make me ready! I’m a-going off!’\n\nI was swept away, but not unkindly, to some distance, where the people around me made me stay; urging, as I confusedly perceived, that he was bent on going, with help or without, and that I should endanger the precautions for his safety by troubling those with whom they rested. I don’t know what I answered, or what they rejoined; but I saw hurry on the beach, and men running with ropes from a capstan that was there, and penetrating into a circle of figures that hid him from me. Then, I saw him standing alone, in a seaman’s frock and trousers: a rope in his hand, or slung to his wrist: another round his body: and several of the best men holding, at a little distance, to the latter, which he laid out himself, slack upon the shore, at his feet.\n\nThe wreck, even to my unpractised eye, was breaking up. I saw that she was parting in the middle, and that the life of the solitary man upon the mast hung by a thread. Still, he clung to it. He had a singular red cap on,--not like a sailor’s cap, but of a finer colour; and as the few yielding planks between him and destruction rolled and bulged, and his anticipative death-knell rung, he was seen by all of us to wave it. I saw him do it now, and thought I was going distracted, when his action brought an old remembrance to my mind of a once dear friend.\n\nHam watched the sea, standing alone, with the silence of suspended breath behind him, and the storm before, until there was a great retiring wave, when, with a backward glance at those who held the rope which was made fast round his body, he dashed in after it, and in a moment was buffeting with the water; rising with the hills, falling with the valleys, lost beneath the foam; then drawn again to land. They hauled in hastily.\n\nHe was hurt. I saw blood on his face, from where I stood; but he took no thought of that. He seemed hurriedly to give them some directions for leaving him more free--or so I judged from the motion of his arm--and was gone as before.\n\nAnd now he made for the wreck, rising with the hills, falling with the valleys, lost beneath the rugged foam, borne in towards the shore, borne on towards the ship, striving hard and valiantly. The distance was nothing, but the power of the sea and wind made the strife deadly. At length he neared the wreck. He was so near, that with one more of his vigorous strokes he would be clinging to it,--when a high, green, vast hill-side of water, moving on shoreward, from beyond the ship, he seemed to leap up into it with a mighty bound, and the ship was gone!\n\nSome eddying fragments I saw in the sea, as if a mere cask had been broken, in running to the spot where they were hauling in. Consternation was in every face. They drew him to my very feet--insensible--dead. He was carried to the nearest house; and, no one preventing me now, I remained near him, busy, while every means of restoration were tried; but he had been beaten to death by the great wave, and his generous heart was stilled for ever.\n\nAs I sat beside the bed, when hope was abandoned and all was done, a fisherman, who had known me when Emily and I were children, and ever since, whispered my name at the door.\n\n‘Sir,’ said he, with tears starting to his weather-beaten face, which, with his trembling lips, was ashy pale, ‘will you come over yonder?’\n\nThe old remembrance that had been recalled to me, was in his look. I asked him, terror-stricken, leaning on the arm he held out to support me:\n\n‘Has a body come ashore?’\n\nHe said, ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Do I know it?’ I asked then.\n\nHe answered nothing.\n\nBut he led me to the shore. And on that part of it where she and I had looked for shells, two children--on that part of it where some lighter fragments of the old boat, blown down last night, had been scattered by the wind--among the ruins of the home he had wronged--I saw him lying with his head upon his arm, as I had often seen him lie at school.\n\n# CHAPTER 56. THE NEW WOUND, AND THE OLD\n\nNo need, O Steerforth, to have said, when we last spoke together, in that hour which I so little deemed to be our parting-hour--no need to have said, ‘Think of me at my best!’ I had done that ever; and could I change now, looking on this sight!\n\nThey brought a hand-bier, and laid him on it, and covered him with a flag, and took him up and bore him on towards the houses. All the men who carried him had known him, and gone sailing with him, and seen him merry and bold. They carried him through the wild roar, a hush in the midst of all the tumult; and took him to the cottage where Death was already.\n\nBut when they set the bier down on the threshold, they looked at one another, and at me, and whispered. I knew why. They felt as if it were not right to lay him down in the same quiet room.\n\nWe went into the town, and took our burden to the inn. So soon as I could at all collect my thoughts, I sent for Joram, and begged him to provide me a conveyance in which it could be got to London in the night. I knew that the care of it, and the hard duty of preparing his mother to receive it, could only rest with me; and I was anxious to discharge that duty as faithfully as I could.\n\nI chose the night for the journey, that there might be less curiosity when I left the town. But, although it was nearly midnight when I came out of the yard in a chaise, followed by what I had in charge, there were many people waiting. At intervals, along the town, and even a little way out upon the road, I saw more: but at length only the bleak night and the open country were around me, and the ashes of my youthful friendship.\n\nUpon a mellow autumn day, about noon, when the ground was perfumed by fallen leaves, and many more, in beautiful tints of yellow, red, and brown, yet hung upon the trees, through which the sun was shining, I arrived at Highgate. I walked the last mile, thinking as I went along of what I had to do; and left the carriage that had followed me all through the night, awaiting orders to advance.\n\nThe house, when I came up to it, looked just the same. Not a blind was raised; no sign of life was in the dull paved court, with its covered way leading to the disused door. The wind had quite gone down, and nothing moved.\n\nI had not, at first, the courage to ring at the gate; and when I did ring, my errand seemed to me to be expressed in the very sound of the bell. The little parlour-maid came out, with the key in her hand; and looking earnestly at me as she unlocked the gate, said:\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir. Are you ill?’\n\n‘I have been much agitated, and am fatigued.’\n\n‘Is anything the matter, sir?---Mr. James?--’ ‘Hush!’ said I. ‘Yes, something has happened, that I have to break to Mrs. Steerforth. She is at home?’\n\nThe girl anxiously replied that her mistress was very seldom out now, even in a carriage; that she kept her room; that she saw no company, but would see me. Her mistress was up, she said, and Miss Dartle was with her. What message should she take upstairs?\n\nGiving her a strict charge to be careful of her manner, and only to carry in my card and say I waited, I sat down in the drawing-room (which we had now reached) until she should come back. Its former pleasant air of occupation was gone, and the shutters were half closed. The harp had not been used for many and many a day. His picture, as a boy, was there. The cabinet in which his mother had kept his letters was there. I wondered if she ever read them now; if she would ever read them more!\n\nThe house was so still that I heard the girl’s light step upstairs. On her return, she brought a message, to the effect that Mrs. Steerforth was an invalid and could not come down; but that if I would excuse her being in her chamber, she would be glad to see me. In a few moments I stood before her.\n\nShe was in his room; not in her own. I felt, of course, that she had taken to occupy it, in remembrance of him; and that the many tokens of his old sports and accomplishments, by which she was surrounded, remained there, just as he had left them, for the same reason. She murmured, however, even in her reception of me, that she was out of her own chamber because its aspect was unsuited to her infirmity; and with her stately look repelled the least suspicion of the truth.\n\nAt her chair, as usual, was Rosa Dartle. From the first moment of her dark eyes resting on me, I saw she knew I was the bearer of evil tidings. The scar sprung into view that instant. She withdrew herself a step behind the chair, to keep her own face out of Mrs. Steerforth’s observation; and scrutinized me with a piercing gaze that never faltered, never shrunk.\n\n‘I am sorry to observe you are in mourning, sir,’ said Mrs. Steerforth.\n\n‘I am unhappily a widower,’ said I.\n\n‘You are very young to know so great a loss,’ she returned. ‘I am grieved to hear it. I am grieved to hear it. I hope Time will be good to you.’\n\n‘I hope Time,’ said I, looking at her, ‘will be good to all of us. Dear Mrs. Steerforth, we must all trust to that, in our heaviest misfortunes.’\n\nThe earnestness of my manner, and the tears in my eyes, alarmed her. The whole course of her thoughts appeared to stop, and change.\n\nI tried to command my voice in gently saying his name, but it trembled. She repeated it to herself, two or three times, in a low tone. Then, addressing me, she said, with enforced calmness:\n\n‘My son is ill.’\n\n‘You have seen him?’\n\n‘I have.’\n\n‘Are you reconciled?’\n\nI could not say Yes, I could not say No. She slightly turned her head towards the spot where Rosa Dartle had been standing at her elbow, and in that moment I said, by the motion of my lips, to Rosa, ‘Dead!’\n\nThat Mrs. Steerforth might not be induced to look behind her, and read, plainly written, what she was not yet prepared to know, I met her look quickly; but I had seen Rosa Dartle throw her hands up in the air with vehemence of despair and horror, and then clasp them on her face.\n\nThe handsome lady--so like, oh so like!--regarded me with a fixed look, and put her hand to her forehead. I besought her to be calm, and prepare herself to bear what I had to tell; but I should rather have entreated her to weep, for she sat like a stone figure.\n\n‘When I was last here,’ I faltered, ‘Miss Dartle told me he was sailing here and there. The night before last was a dreadful one at sea. If he were at sea that night, and near a dangerous coast, as it is said he was; and if the vessel that was seen should really be the ship which--’\n\n‘Rosa!’ said Mrs. Steerforth, ‘come to me!’\n\nShe came, but with no sympathy or gentleness. Her eyes gleamed like fire as she confronted his mother, and broke into a frightful laugh.\n\n‘Now,’ she said, ‘is your pride appeased, you madwoman? Now has he made atonement to you--with his life! Do you hear?---His life!’\n\nMrs. Steerforth, fallen back stiffly in her chair, and making no sound but a moan, cast her eyes upon her with a wide stare.\n\n‘Aye!’ cried Rosa, smiting herself passionately on the breast, ‘look at me! Moan, and groan, and look at me! Look here!’ striking the scar, ‘at your dead child’s handiwork!’\n\nThe moan the mother uttered, from time to time, went to My heart. Always the same. Always inarticulate and stifled. Always accompanied with an incapable motion of the head, but with no change of face. Always proceeding from a rigid mouth and closed teeth, as if the jaw were locked and the face frozen up in pain.\n\n‘Do you remember when he did this?’ she proceeded. ‘Do you remember when, in his inheritance of your nature, and in your pampering of his pride and passion, he did this, and disfigured me for life? Look at me, marked until I die with his high displeasure; and moan and groan for what you made him!’\n\n‘Miss Dartle,’ I entreated her. ‘For Heaven’s sake--’\n\n‘I WILL speak!’ she said, turning on me with her lightning eyes. ‘Be silent, you! Look at me, I say, proud mother of a proud, false son! Moan for your nurture of him, moan for your corruption of him, moan for your loss of him, moan for mine!’\n\nShe clenched her hand, and trembled through her spare, worn figure, as if her passion were killing her by inches.\n\n‘You, resent his self-will!’ she exclaimed. ‘You, injured by his haughty temper! You, who opposed to both, when your hair was grey, the qualities which made both when you gave him birth! YOU, who from his cradle reared him to be what he was, and stunted what he should have been! Are you rewarded, now, for your years of trouble?’\n\n‘Oh, Miss Dartle, shame! Oh cruel!’\n\n‘I tell you,’ she returned, ‘I WILL speak to her. No power on earth should stop me, while I was standing here! Have I been silent all these years, and shall I not speak now? I loved him better than you ever loved him!’ turning on her fiercely. ‘I could have loved him, and asked no return. If I had been his wife, I could have been the slave of his caprices for a word of love a year. I should have been. Who knows it better than I? You were exacting, proud, punctilious, selfish. My love would have been devoted--would have trod your paltry whimpering under foot!’\n\nWith flashing eyes, she stamped upon the ground as if she actually did it.\n\n‘Look here!’ she said, striking the scar again, with a relentless hand. ‘When he grew into the better understanding of what he had done, he saw it, and repented of it! I could sing to him, and talk to him, and show the ardour that I felt in all he did, and attain with labour to such knowledge as most interested him; and I attracted him. When he was freshest and truest, he loved me. Yes, he did! Many a time, when you were put off with a slight word, he has taken Me to his heart!’\n\nShe said it with a taunting pride in the midst of her frenzy--for it was little less--yet with an eager remembrance of it, in which the smouldering embers of a gentler feeling kindled for the moment.\n\n‘I descended--as I might have known I should, but that he fascinated me with his boyish courtship--into a doll, a trifle for the occupation of an idle hour, to be dropped, and taken up, and trifled with, as the inconstant humour took him. When he grew weary, I grew weary. As his fancy died out, I would no more have tried to strengthen any power I had, than I would have married him on his being forced to take me for his wife. We fell away from one another without a word. Perhaps you saw it, and were not sorry. Since then, I have been a mere disfigured piece of furniture between you both; having no eyes, no ears, no feelings, no remembrances. Moan? Moan for what you made him; not for your love. I tell you that the time was, when I loved him better than you ever did!’\n\nShe stood with her bright angry eyes confronting the wide stare, and the set face; and softened no more, when the moaning was repeated, than if the face had been a picture.\n\n‘Miss Dartle,’ said I, ‘if you can be so obdurate as not to feel for this afflicted mother--’\n\n‘Who feels for me?’ she sharply retorted. ‘She has sown this. Let her moan for the harvest that she reaps today!’\n\n‘And if his faults--’ I began.\n\n‘Faults!’ she cried, bursting into passionate tears. ‘Who dares malign him? He had a soul worth millions of the friends to whom he stooped!’\n\n‘No one can have loved him better, no one can hold him in dearer remembrance than I,’ I replied. ‘I meant to say, if you have no compassion for his mother; or if his faults--you have been bitter on them--’\n\n‘It’s false,’ she cried, tearing her black hair; ‘I loved him!’\n\n‘--if his faults cannot,’ I went on, ‘be banished from your remembrance, in such an hour; look at that figure, even as one you have never seen before, and render it some help!’\n\nAll this time, the figure was unchanged, and looked unchangeable. Motionless, rigid, staring; moaning in the same dumb way from time to time, with the same helpless motion of the head; but giving no other sign of life. Miss Dartle suddenly kneeled down before it, and began to loosen the dress.\n\n‘A curse upon you!’ she said, looking round at me, with a mingled expression of rage and grief. ‘It was in an evil hour that you ever came here! A curse upon you! Go!’\n\nAfter passing out of the room, I hurried back to ring the bell, the sooner to alarm the servants. She had then taken the impassive figure in her arms, and, still upon her knees, was weeping over it, kissing it, calling to it, rocking it to and fro upon her bosom like a child, and trying every tender means to rouse the dormant senses. No longer afraid of leaving her, I noiselessly turned back again; and alarmed the house as I went out.\n\nLater in the day, I returned, and we laid him in his mother’s room. She was just the same, they told me; Miss Dartle never left her; doctors were in attendance, many things had been tried; but she lay like a statue, except for the low sound now and then.\n\nI went through the dreary house, and darkened the windows. The windows of the chamber where he lay, I darkened last. I lifted up the leaden hand, and held it to my heart; and all the world seemed death and silence, broken only by his mother’s moaning.\n\n# CHAPTER 57. THE EMIGRANTS\n\nOne thing more, I had to do, before yielding myself to the shock of these emotions. It was, to conceal what had occurred, from those who were going away; and to dismiss them on their voyage in happy ignorance. In this, no time was to be lost.\n\nI took Mr. Micawber aside that same night, and confided to him the task of standing between Mr. Peggotty and intelligence of the late catastrophe. He zealously undertook to do so, and to intercept any newspaper through which it might, without such precautions, reach him.\n\n‘If it penetrates to him, sir,’ said Mr. Micawber, striking himself on the breast, ‘it shall first pass through this body!’\n\nMr. Micawber, I must observe, in his adaptation of himself to a new state of society, had acquired a bold buccaneering air, not absolutely lawless, but defensive and prompt. One might have supposed him a child of the wilderness, long accustomed to live out of the confines of civilization, and about to return to his native wilds.\n\nHe had provided himself, among other things, with a complete suit of oilskin, and a straw hat with a very low crown, pitched or caulked on the outside. In this rough clothing, with a common mariner’s telescope under his arm, and a shrewd trick of casting up his eye at the sky as looking out for dirty weather, he was far more nautical, after his manner, than Mr. Peggotty. His whole family, if I may so express it, were cleared for action. I found Mrs. Micawber in the closest and most uncompromising of bonnets, made fast under the chin; and in a shawl which tied her up (as I had been tied up, when my aunt first received me) like a bundle, and was secured behind at the waist, in a strong knot. Miss Micawber I found made snug for stormy weather, in the same manner; with nothing superfluous about her. Master Micawber was hardly visible in a Guernsey shirt, and the shaggiest suit of slops I ever saw; and the children were done up, like preserved meats, in impervious cases. Both Mr. Micawber and his eldest son wore their sleeves loosely turned back at the wrists, as being ready to lend a hand in any direction, and to ‘tumble up’, or sing out, ‘Yeo--Heave--Yeo!’ on the shortest notice.\n\nThus Traddles and I found them at nightfall, assembled on the wooden steps, at that time known as Hungerford Stairs, watching the departure of a boat with some of their property on board. I had told Traddles of the terrible event, and it had greatly shocked him; but there could be no doubt of the kindness of keeping it a secret, and he had come to help me in this last service. It was here that I took Mr. Micawber aside, and received his promise.\n\nThe Micawber family were lodged in a little, dirty, tumble-down public-house, which in those days was close to the stairs, and whose protruding wooden rooms overhung the river. The family, as emigrants, being objects of some interest in and about Hungerford, attracted so many beholders, that we were glad to take refuge in their room. It was one of the wooden chambers upstairs, with the tide flowing underneath. My aunt and Agnes were there, busily making some little extra comforts, in the way of dress, for the children. Peggotty was quietly assisting, with the old insensible work-box, yard-measure, and bit of wax-candle before her, that had now outlived so much.\n\nIt was not easy to answer her inquiries; still less to whisper Mr. Peggotty, when Mr. Micawber brought him in, that I had given the letter, and all was well. But I did both, and made them happy. If I showed any trace of what I felt, my own sorrows were sufficient to account for it.\n\n‘And when does the ship sail, Mr. Micawber?’ asked my aunt.\n\nMr. Micawber considered it necessary to prepare either my aunt or his wife, by degrees, and said, sooner than he had expected yesterday.\n\n‘The boat brought you word, I suppose?’ said my aunt.\n\n‘It did, ma’am,’ he returned.\n\n‘Well?’ said my aunt. ‘And she sails--’\n\n‘Madam,’ he replied, ‘I am informed that we must positively be on board before seven tomorrow morning.’\n\n‘Heyday!’ said my aunt, ‘that’s soon. Is it a sea-going fact, Mr. Peggotty?’ ‘’Tis so, ma’am. She’ll drop down the river with that theer tide. If Mas’r Davy and my sister comes aboard at Gravesen’, arternoon o’ next day, they’ll see the last on us.’\n\n‘And that we shall do,’ said I, ‘be sure!’\n\n‘Until then, and until we are at sea,’ observed Mr. Micawber, with a glance of intelligence at me, ‘Mr. Peggotty and myself will constantly keep a double look-out together, on our goods and chattels. Emma, my love,’ said Mr. Micawber, clearing his throat in his magnificent way, ‘my friend Mr. Thomas Traddles is so obliging as to solicit, in my ear, that he should have the privilege of ordering the ingredients necessary to the composition of a moderate portion of that Beverage which is peculiarly associated, in our minds, with the Roast Beef of Old England. I allude to--in short, Punch. Under ordinary circumstances, I should scruple to entreat the indulgence of Miss Trotwood and Miss Wickfield, but-’\n\n‘I can only say for myself,’ said my aunt, ‘that I will drink all happiness and success to you, Mr. Micawber, with the utmost pleasure.’\n\n‘And I too!’ said Agnes, with a smile.\n\nMr. Micawber immediately descended to the bar, where he appeared to be quite at home; and in due time returned with a steaming jug. I could not but observe that he had been peeling the lemons with his own clasp-knife, which, as became the knife of a practical settler, was about a foot long; and which he wiped, not wholly without ostentation, on the sleeve of his coat. Mrs. Micawber and the two elder members of the family I now found to be provided with similar formidable instruments, while every child had its own wooden spoon attached to its body by a strong line. In a similar anticipation of life afloat, and in the Bush, Mr. Micawber, instead of helping Mrs. Micawber and his eldest son and daughter to punch, in wine-glasses, which he might easily have done, for there was a shelf-full in the room, served it out to them in a series of villainous little tin pots; and I never saw him enjoy anything so much as drinking out of his own particular pint pot, and putting it in his pocket at the close of the evening.\n\n‘The luxuries of the old country,’ said Mr. Micawber, with an intense satisfaction in their renouncement, ‘we abandon. The denizens of the forest cannot, of course, expect to participate in the refinements of the land of the Free.’\n\nHere, a boy came in to say that Mr. Micawber was wanted downstairs.\n\n‘I have a presentiment,’ said Mrs. Micawber, setting down her tin pot, ‘that it is a member of my family!’\n\n‘If so, my dear,’ observed Mr. Micawber, with his usual suddenness of warmth on that subject, ‘as the member of your family--whoever he, she, or it, may be--has kept us waiting for a considerable period, perhaps the Member may now wait MY convenience.’\n\n‘Micawber,’ said his wife, in a low tone, ‘at such a time as this--’\n\n‘“It is not meet,”’ said Mr. Micawber, rising, ‘“that every nice offence should bear its comment!” Emma, I stand reproved.’\n\n‘The loss, Micawber,’ observed his wife, ‘has been my family’s, not yours. If my family are at length sensible of the deprivation to which their own conduct has, in the past, exposed them, and now desire to extend the hand of fellowship, let it not be repulsed.’\n\n‘My dear,’ he returned, ‘so be it!’\n\n‘If not for their sakes; for mine, Micawber,’ said his wife.\n\n‘Emma,’ he returned, ‘that view of the question is, at such a moment, irresistible. I cannot, even now, distinctly pledge myself to fall upon your family’s neck; but the member of your family, who is now in attendance, shall have no genial warmth frozen by me.’\n\nMr. Micawber withdrew, and was absent some little time; in the course of which Mrs. Micawber was not wholly free from an apprehension that words might have arisen between him and the Member. At length the same boy reappeared, and presented me with a note written in pencil, and headed, in a legal manner, ‘Heep v. Micawber’. From this document, I learned that Mr. Micawber being again arrested, ‘Was in a final paroxysm of despair; and that he begged me to send him his knife and pint pot, by bearer, as they might prove serviceable during the brief remainder of his existence, in jail. He also requested, as a last act of friendship, that I would see his family to the Parish Workhouse, and forget that such a Being ever lived.\n\nOf course I answered this note by going down with the boy to pay the money, where I found Mr. Micawber sitting in a corner, looking darkly at the Sheriff ‘s Officer who had effected the capture. On his release, he embraced me with the utmost fervour; and made an entry of the transaction in his pocket-book--being very particular, I recollect, about a halfpenny I inadvertently omitted from my statement of the total.\n\nThis momentous pocket-book was a timely reminder to him of another transaction. On our return to the room upstairs (where he accounted for his absence by saying that it had been occasioned by circumstances over which he had no control), he took out of it a large sheet of paper, folded small, and quite covered with long sums, carefully worked. From the glimpse I had of them, I should say that I never saw such sums out of a school ciphering-book. These, it seemed, were calculations of compound interest on what he called ‘the principal amount of forty-one, ten, eleven and a half’, for various periods. After a careful consideration of these, and an elaborate estimate of his resources, he had come to the conclusion to select that sum which represented the amount with compound interest to two years, fifteen calendar months, and fourteen days, from that date. For this he had drawn a note-of-hand with great neatness, which he handed over to Traddles on the spot, a discharge of his debt in full (as between man and man), with many acknowledgements.\n\n‘I have still a presentiment,’ said Mrs. Micawber, pensively shaking her head, ‘that my family will appear on board, before we finally depart.’\n\nMr. Micawber evidently had his presentiment on the subject too, but he put it in his tin pot and swallowed it.\n\n‘If you have any opportunity of sending letters home, on your passage, Mrs. Micawber,’ said my aunt, ‘you must let us hear from you, you know.’\n\n‘My dear Miss Trotwood,’ she replied, ‘I shall only be too happy to think that anyone expects to hear from us. I shall not fail to correspond. Mr. Copperfield, I trust, as an old and familiar friend, will not object to receive occasional intelligence, himself, from one who knew him when the twins were yet unconscious?’\n\nI said that I should hope to hear, whenever she had an opportunity of writing.\n\n‘Please Heaven, there will be many such opportunities,’ said Mr. Micawber. ‘The ocean, in these times, is a perfect fleet of ships; and we can hardly fail to encounter many, in running over. It is merely crossing,’ said Mr. Micawber, trifling with his eye-glass, ‘merely crossing. The distance is quite imaginary.’\n\nI think, now, how odd it was, but how wonderfully like Mr. Micawber, that, when he went from London to Canterbury, he should have talked as if he were going to the farthest limits of the earth; and, when he went from England to Australia, as if he were going for a little trip across the channel.\n\n‘On the voyage, I shall endeavour,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘occasionally to spin them a yarn; and the melody of my son Wilkins will, I trust, be acceptable at the galley-fire. When Mrs. Micawber has her sea-legs on--an expression in which I hope there is no conventional impropriety--she will give them, I dare say, “Little Tafflin”. Porpoises and dolphins, I believe, will be frequently observed athwart our Bows; and, either on the starboard or the larboard quarter, objects of interest will be continually descried. In short,’ said Mr. Micawber, with the old genteel air, ‘the probability is, all will be found so exciting, alow and aloft, that when the lookout, stationed in the main-top, cries Land-oh! we shall be very considerably astonished!’\n\nWith that he flourished off the contents of his little tin pot, as if he had made the voyage, and had passed a first-class examination before the highest naval authorities.\n\n‘What I chiefly hope, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘is, that in some branches of our family we may live again in the old country. Do not frown, Micawber! I do not now refer to my own family, but to our children’s children. However vigorous the sapling,’ said Mrs. Micawber, shaking her head, ‘I cannot forget the parent-tree; and when our race attains to eminence and fortune, I own I should wish that fortune to flow into the coffers of Britannia.’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘Britannia must take her chance. I am bound to say that she has never done much for me, and that I have no particular wish upon the subject.’\n\n‘Micawber,’ returned Mrs. Micawber, ‘there, you are wrong. You are going out, Micawber, to this distant clime, to strengthen, not to weaken, the connexion between yourself and Albion.’\n\n‘The connexion in question, my love,’ rejoined Mr. Micawber, ‘has not laid me, I repeat, under that load of personal obligation, that I am at all sensitive as to the formation of another connexion.’\n\n‘Micawber,’ returned Mrs. Micawber. ‘There, I again say, you are wrong. You do not know your power, Micawber. It is that which will strengthen, even in this step you are about to take, the connexion between yourself and Albion.’\n\nMr. Micawber sat in his elbow-chair, with his eyebrows raised; half receiving and half repudiating Mrs. Micawber’s views as they were stated, but very sensible of their foresight.\n\n‘My dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘I wish Mr. Micawber to feel his position. It appears to me highly important that Mr. Micawber should, from the hour of his embarkation, feel his position. Your old knowledge of me, my dear Mr. Copperfield, will have told you that I have not the sanguine disposition of Mr. Micawber. My disposition is, if I may say so, eminently practical. I know that this is a long voyage. I know that it will involve many privations and inconveniences. I cannot shut my eyes to those facts. But I also know what Mr. Micawber is. I know the latent power of Mr. Micawber. And therefore I consider it vitally important that Mr. Micawber should feel his position.’\n\n‘My love,’ he observed, ‘perhaps you will allow me to remark that it is barely possible that I DO feel my position at the present moment.’\n\n‘I think not, Micawber,’ she rejoined. ‘Not fully. My dear Mr. Copperfield, Mr. Micawber’s is not a common case. Mr. Micawber is going to a distant country expressly in order that he may be fully understood and appreciated for the first time. I wish Mr. Micawber to take his stand upon that vessel’s prow, and firmly say, “This country I am come to conquer! Have you honours? Have you riches? Have you posts of profitable pecuniary emolument? Let them be brought forward. They are mine!”’\n\nMr. Micawber, glancing at us all, seemed to think there was a good deal in this idea.\n\n‘I wish Mr. Micawber, if I make myself understood,’ said Mrs. Micawber, in her argumentative tone, ‘to be the Caesar of his own fortunes. That, my dear Mr. Copperfield, appears to me to be his true position. From the first moment of this voyage, I wish Mr. Micawber to stand upon that vessel’s prow and say, “Enough of delay: enough of disappointment: enough of limited means. That was in the old country. This is the new. Produce your reparation. Bring it forward!”’\n\nMr. Micawber folded his arms in a resolute manner, as if he were then stationed on the figure-head.\n\n‘And doing that,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘--feeling his position--am I not right in saying that Mr. Micawber will strengthen, and not weaken, his connexion with Britain? An important public character arising in that hemisphere, shall I be told that its influence will not be felt at home? Can I be so weak as to imagine that Mr. Micawber, wielding the rod of talent and of power in Australia, will be nothing in England? I am but a woman; but I should be unworthy of myself and of my papa, if I were guilty of such absurd weakness.’\n\nMrs. Micawber’s conviction that her arguments were unanswerable, gave a moral elevation to her tone which I think I had never heard in it before.\n\n‘And therefore it is,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘that I the more wish, that, at a future period, we may live again on the parent soil. Mr. Micawber may be--I cannot disguise from myself that the probability is, Mr. Micawber will be--a page of History; and he ought then to be represented in the country which gave him birth, and did NOT give him employment!’\n\n‘My love,’ observed Mr. Micawber, ‘it is impossible for me not to be touched by your affection. I am always willing to defer to your good sense. What will be--will be. Heaven forbid that I should grudge my native country any portion of the wealth that may be accumulated by our descendants!’\n\n‘That’s well,’ said my aunt, nodding towards Mr. Peggotty, ‘and I drink my love to you all, and every blessing and success attend you!’\n\nMr. Peggotty put down the two children he had been nursing, one on each knee, to join Mr. and Mrs. Micawber in drinking to all of us in return; and when he and the Micawbers cordially shook hands as comrades, and his brown face brightened with a smile, I felt that he would make his way, establish a good name, and be beloved, go where he would.\n\nEven the children were instructed, each to dip a wooden spoon into Mr. Micawber’s pot, and pledge us in its contents. When this was done, my aunt and Agnes rose, and parted from the emigrants. It was a sorrowful farewell. They were all crying; the children hung about Agnes to the last; and we left poor Mrs. Micawber in a very distressed condition, sobbing and weeping by a dim candle, that must have made the room look, from the river, like a miserable light-house.\n\nI went down again next morning to see that they were away. They had departed, in a boat, as early as five o’clock. It was a wonderful instance to me of the gap such partings make, that although my association of them with the tumble-down public-house and the wooden stairs dated only from last night, both seemed dreary and deserted, now that they were gone.\n\nIn the afternoon of the next day, my old nurse and I went down to Gravesend. We found the ship in the river, surrounded by a crowd of boats; a favourable wind blowing; the signal for sailing at her mast-head. I hired a boat directly, and we put off to her; and getting through the little vortex of confusion of which she was the centre, went on board.\n\nMr. Peggotty was waiting for us on deck. He told me that Mr. Micawber had just now been arrested again (and for the last time) at the suit of Heep, and that, in compliance with a request I had made to him, he had paid the money, which I repaid him. He then took us down between decks; and there, any lingering fears I had of his having heard any rumours of what had happened, were dispelled by Mr. Micawber’s coming out of the gloom, taking his arm with an air of friendship and protection, and telling me that they had scarcely been asunder for a moment, since the night before last.\n\nIt was such a strange scene to me, and so confined and dark, that, at first, I could make out hardly anything; but, by degrees, it cleared, as my eyes became more accustomed to the gloom, and I seemed to stand in a picture by OSTADE. Among the great beams, bulks, and ringbolts of the ship, and the emigrant-berths, and chests, and bundles, and barrels, and heaps of miscellaneous baggage--‘lighted up, here and there, by dangling lanterns; and elsewhere by the yellow daylight straying down a windsail or a hatchway--were crowded groups of people, making new friendships, taking leave of one another, talking, laughing, crying, eating and drinking; some, already settled down into the possession of their few feet of space, with their little households arranged, and tiny children established on stools, or in dwarf elbow-chairs; others, despairing of a resting-place, and wandering disconsolately. From babies who had but a week or two of life behind them, to crooked old men and women who seemed to have but a week or two of life before them; and from ploughmen bodily carrying out soil of England on their boots, to smiths taking away samples of its soot and smoke upon their skins; every age and occupation appeared to be crammed into the narrow compass of the ‘tween decks.\n\nAs my eye glanced round this place, I thought I saw sitting, by an open port, with one of the Micawber children near her, a figure like Emily’s; it first attracted my attention, by another figure parting from it with a kiss; and as it glided calmly away through the disorder, reminding me of--Agnes! But in the rapid motion and confusion, and in the unsettlement of my own thoughts, I lost it again; and only knew that the time was come when all visitors were being warned to leave the ship; that my nurse was crying on a chest beside me; and that Mrs. Gummidge, assisted by some younger stooping woman in black, was busily arranging Mr. Peggotty’s goods.\n\n‘Is there any last wured, Mas’r Davy?’ said he. ‘Is there any one forgotten thing afore we parts?’\n\n‘One thing!’ said I. ‘Martha!’\n\nHe touched the younger woman I have mentioned on the shoulder, and Martha stood before me.\n\n‘Heaven bless you, you good man!’ cried I. ‘You take her with you!’\n\nShe answered for him, with a burst of tears. I could speak no more at that time, but I wrung his hand; and if ever I have loved and honoured any man, I loved and honoured that man in my soul.\n\nThe ship was clearing fast of strangers. The greatest trial that I had, remained. I told him what the noble spirit that was gone, had given me in charge to say at parting. It moved him deeply. But when he charged me, in return, with many messages of affection and regret for those deaf ears, he moved me more.\n\nThe time was come. I embraced him, took my weeping nurse upon my arm, and hurried away. On deck, I took leave of poor Mrs. Micawber. She was looking distractedly about for her family, even then; and her last words to me were, that she never would desert Mr. Micawber.\n\nWe went over the side into our boat, and lay at a little distance, to see the ship wafted on her course. It was then calm, radiant sunset. She lay between us, and the red light; and every taper line and spar was visible against the glow. A sight at once so beautiful, so mournful, and so hopeful, as the glorious ship, lying, still, on the flushed water, with all the life on board her crowded at the bulwarks, and there clustering, for a moment, bare-headed and silent, I never saw.\n\nSilent, only for a moment. As the sails rose to the wind, and the ship began to move, there broke from all the boats three resounding cheers, which those on board took up, and echoed back, and which were echoed and re-echoed. My heart burst out when I heard the sound, and beheld the waving of the hats and handkerchiefs--and then I saw her!\n\nThen I saw her, at her uncle’s side, and trembling on his shoulder. He pointed to us with an eager hand; and she saw us, and waved her last good-bye to me. Aye, Emily, beautiful and drooping, cling to him with the utmost trust of thy bruised heart; for he has clung to thee, with all the might of his great love!\n\nSurrounded by the rosy light, and standing high upon the deck, apart together, she clinging to him, and he holding her, they solemnly passed away. The night had fallen on the Kentish hills when we were rowed ashore--and fallen darkly upon me.\n\n# CHAPTER 58. ABSENCE\n\nIt was a long and gloomy night that gathered on me, haunted by the ghosts of many hopes, of many dear remembrances, many errors, many unavailing sorrows and regrets.\n\nI went away from England; not knowing, even then, how great the shock was, that I had to bear. I left all who were dear to me, and went away; and believed that I had borne it, and it was past. As a man upon a field of battle will receive a mortal hurt, and scarcely know that he is struck, so I, when I was left alone with my undisciplined heart, had no conception of the wound with which it had to strive.\n\nThe knowledge came upon me, not quickly, but little by little, and grain by grain. The desolate feeling with which I went abroad, deepened and widened hourly. At first it was a heavy sense of loss and sorrow, wherein I could distinguish little else. By imperceptible degrees, it became a hopeless consciousness of all that I had lost--love, friendship, interest; of all that had been shattered--my first trust, my first affection, the whole airy castle of my life; of all that remained--a ruined blank and waste, lying wide around me, unbroken, to the dark horizon.\n\nIf my grief were selfish, I did not know it to be so. I mourned for my child-wife, taken from her blooming world, so young. I mourned for him who might have won the love and admiration of thousands, as he had won mine long ago. I mourned for the broken heart that had found rest in the stormy sea; and for the wandering remnants of the simple home, where I had heard the night-wind blowing, when I was a child.\n\nFrom the accumulated sadness into which I fell, I had at length no hope of ever issuing again. I roamed from place to place, carrying my burden with me everywhere. I felt its whole weight now; and I drooped beneath it, and I said in my heart that it could never be lightened.\n\nWhen this despondency was at its worst, I believed that I should die. Sometimes, I thought that I would like to die at home; and actually turned back on my road, that I might get there soon. At other times, I passed on farther away,--from city to city, seeking I know not what, and trying to leave I know not what behind.\n\nIt is not in my power to retrace, one by one, all the weary phases of distress of mind through which I passed. There are some dreams that can only be imperfectly and vaguely described; and when I oblige myself to look back on this time of my life, I seem to be recalling such a dream. I see myself passing on among the novelties of foreign towns, palaces, cathedrals, temples, pictures, castles, tombs, fantastic streets--the old abiding places of History and Fancy--as a dreamer might; bearing my painful load through all, and hardly conscious of the objects as they fade before me. Listlessness to everything, but brooding sorrow, was the night that fell on my undisciplined heart. Let me look up from it--as at last I did, thank Heaven!--and from its long, sad, wretched dream, to dawn.\n\nFor many months I travelled with this ever-darkening cloud upon my mind. Some blind reasons that I had for not returning home--reasons then struggling within me, vainly, for more distinct expression--kept me on my pilgrimage. Sometimes, I had proceeded restlessly from place to place, stopping nowhere; sometimes, I had lingered long in one spot. I had had no purpose, no sustaining soul within me, anywhere.\n\nI was in Switzerland. I had come out of Italy, over one of the great passes of the Alps, and had since wandered with a guide among the by-ways of the mountains. If those awful solitudes had spoken to my heart, I did not know it. I had found sublimity and wonder in the dread heights and precipices, in the roaring torrents, and the wastes of ice and snow; but as yet, they had taught me nothing else.\n\nI came, one evening before sunset, down into a valley, where I was to rest. In the course of my descent to it, by the winding track along the mountain-side, from which I saw it shining far below, I think some long-unwonted sense of beauty and tranquillity, some softening influence awakened by its peace, moved faintly in my breast. I remember pausing once, with a kind of sorrow that was not all oppressive, not quite despairing. I remember almost hoping that some better change was possible within me.\n\nI came into the valley, as the evening sun was shining on the remote heights of snow, that closed it in, like eternal clouds. The bases of the mountains forming the gorge in which the little village lay, were richly green; and high above this gentler vegetation, grew forests of dark fir, cleaving the wintry snow-drift, wedge-like, and stemming the avalanche. Above these, were range upon range of craggy steeps, grey rock, bright ice, and smooth verdure-specks of pasture, all gradually blending with the crowning snow. Dotted here and there on the mountain’s-side, each tiny dot a home, were lonely wooden cottages, so dwarfed by the towering heights that they appeared too small for toys. So did even the clustered village in the valley, with its wooden bridge across the stream, where the stream tumbled over broken rocks, and roared away among the trees. In the quiet air, there was a sound of distant singing--shepherd voices; but, as one bright evening cloud floated midway along the mountain’s-side, I could almost have believed it came from there, and was not earthly music. All at once, in this serenity, great Nature spoke to me; and soothed me to lay down my weary head upon the grass, and weep as I had not wept yet, since Dora died!\n\nI had found a packet of letters awaiting me but a few minutes before, and had strolled out of the village to read them while my supper was making ready. Other packets had missed me, and I had received none for a long time. Beyond a line or two, to say that I was well, and had arrived at such a place, I had not had fortitude or constancy to write a letter since I left home.\n\nThe packet was in my hand. I opened it, and read the writing of Agnes.\n\nShe was happy and useful, was prospering as she had hoped. That was all she told me of herself. The rest referred to me.\n\nShe gave me no advice; she urged no duty on me; she only told me, in her own fervent manner, what her trust in me was. She knew (she said) how such a nature as mine would turn affliction to good. She knew how trial and emotion would exalt and strengthen it. She was sure that in my every purpose I should gain a firmer and a higher tendency, through the grief I had undergone. She, who so gloried in my fame, and so looked forward to its augmentation, well knew that I would labour on. She knew that in me, sorrow could not be weakness, but must be strength. As the endurance of my childish days had done its part to make me what I was, so greater calamities would nerve me on, to be yet better than I was; and so, as they had taught me, would I teach others. She commended me to God, who had taken my innocent darling to His rest; and in her sisterly affection cherished me always, and was always at my side go where I would; proud of what I had done, but infinitely prouder yet of what I was reserved to do.\n\nI put the letter in my breast, and thought what had I been an hour ago! When I heard the voices die away, and saw the quiet evening cloud grow dim, and all the colours in the valley fade, and the golden snow upon the mountain-tops become a remote part of the pale night sky, yet felt that the night was passing from my mind, and all its shadows clearing, there was no name for the love I bore her, dearer to me, henceforward, than ever until then.\n\nI read her letter many times. I wrote to her before I slept. I told her that I had been in sore need of her help; that without her I was not, and I never had been, what she thought me; but that she inspired me to be that, and I would try.\n\nI did try. In three months more, a year would have passed since the beginning of my sorrow. I determined to make no resolutions until the expiration of those three months, but to try. I lived in that valley, and its neighbourhood, all the time.\n\nThe three months gone, I resolved to remain away from home for some time longer; to settle myself for the present in Switzerland, which was growing dear to me in the remembrance of that evening; to resume my pen; to work.\n\nI resorted humbly whither Agnes had commended me; I sought out Nature, never sought in vain; and I admitted to my breast the human interest I had lately shrunk from. It was not long, before I had almost as many friends in the valley as in Yarmouth: and when I left it, before the winter set in, for Geneva, and came back in the spring, their cordial greetings had a homely sound to me, although they were not conveyed in English words.\n\nI worked early and late, patiently and hard. I wrote a Story, with a purpose growing, not remotely, out of my experience, and sent it to Traddles, and he arranged for its publication very advantageously for me; and the tidings of my growing reputation began to reach me from travellers whom I encountered by chance. After some rest and change, I fell to work, in my old ardent way, on a new fancy, which took strong possession of me. As I advanced in the execution of this task, I felt it more and more, and roused my utmost energies to do it well. This was my third work of fiction. It was not half written, when, in an interval of rest, I thought of returning home.\n\nFor a long time, though studying and working patiently, I had accustomed myself to robust exercise. My health, severely impaired when I left England, was quite restored. I had seen much. I had been in many countries, and I hope I had improved my store of knowledge.\n\nI have now recalled all that I think it needful to recall here, of this term of absence--with one reservation. I have made it, thus far, with no purpose of suppressing any of my thoughts; for, as I have elsewhere said, this narrative is my written memory. I have desired to keep the most secret current of my mind apart, and to the last. I enter on it now. I cannot so completely penetrate the mystery of my own heart, as to know when I began to think that I might have set its earliest and brightest hopes on Agnes. I cannot say at what stage of my grief it first became associated with the reflection, that, in my wayward boyhood, I had thrown away the treasure of her love. I believe I may have heard some whisper of that distant thought, in the old unhappy loss or want of something never to be realized, of which I had been sensible. But the thought came into my mind as a new reproach and new regret, when I was left so sad and lonely in the world.\n\nIf, at that time, I had been much with her, I should, in the weakness of my desolation, have betrayed this. It was what I remotely dreaded when I was first impelled to stay away from England. I could not have borne to lose the smallest portion of her sisterly affection; yet, in that betrayal, I should have set a constraint between us hitherto unknown.\n\nI could not forget that the feeling with which she now regarded me had grown up in my own free choice and course. That if she had ever loved me with another love--and I sometimes thought the time was when she might have done so--I had cast it away. It was nothing, now, that I had accustomed myself to think of her, when we were both mere children, as one who was far removed from my wild fancies. I had bestowed my passionate tenderness upon another object; and what I might have done, I had not done; and what Agnes was to me, I and her own noble heart had made her.\n\nIn the beginning of the change that gradually worked in me, when I tried to get a better understanding of myself and be a better man, I did glance, through some indefinite probation, to a period when I might possibly hope to cancel the mistaken past, and to be so blessed as to marry her. But, as time wore on, this shadowy prospect faded, and departed from me. If she had ever loved me, then, I should hold her the more sacred; remembering the confidences I had reposed in her, her knowledge of my errant heart, the sacrifice she must have made to be my friend and sister, and the victory she had won. If she had never loved me, could I believe that she would love me now?\n\nI had always felt my weakness, in comparison with her constancy and fortitude; and now I felt it more and more. Whatever I might have been to her, or she to me, if I had been more worthy of her long ago, I was not now, and she was not. The time was past. I had let it go by, and had deservedly lost her.\n\nThat I suffered much in these contentions, that they filled me with unhappiness and remorse, and yet that I had a sustaining sense that it was required of me, in right and honour, to keep away from myself, with shame, the thought of turning to the dear girl in the withering of my hopes, from whom I had frivolously turned when they were bright and fresh--which consideration was at the root of every thought I had concerning her--is all equally true. I made no effort to conceal from myself, now, that I loved her, that I was devoted to her; but I brought the assurance home to myself, that it was now too late, and that our long-subsisting relation must be undisturbed.\n\nI had thought, much and often, of my Dora’s shadowing out to me what might have happened, in those years that were destined not to try us; I had considered how the things that never happen, are often as much realities to us, in their effects, as those that are accomplished. The very years she spoke of, were realities now, for my correction; and would have been, one day, a little later perhaps, though we had parted in our earliest folly. I endeavoured to convert what might have been between myself and Agnes, into a means of making me more self-denying, more resolved, more conscious of myself, and my defects and errors. Thus, through the reflection that it might have been, I arrived at the conviction that it could never be.\n\nThese, with their perplexities and inconsistencies, were the shifting quicksands of my mind, from the time of my departure to the time of my return home, three years afterwards. Three years had elapsed since the sailing of the emigrant ship; when, at that same hour of sunset, and in the same place, I stood on the deck of the packet vessel that brought me home, looking on the rosy water where I had seen the image of that ship reflected.\n\nThree years. Long in the aggregate, though short as they went by. And home was very dear to me, and Agnes too--but she was not mine--she was never to be mine. She might have been, but that was past!\n\n# CHAPTER 59. RETURN\n\nI landed in London on a wintry autumn evening. It was dark and raining, and I saw more fog and mud in a minute than I had seen in a year. I walked from the Custom House to the Monument before I found a coach; and although the very house-fronts, looking on the swollen gutters, were like old friends to me, I could not but admit that they were very dingy friends.\n\nI have often remarked--I suppose everybody has--that one’s going away from a familiar place, would seem to be the signal for change in it. As I looked out of the coach window, and observed that an old house on Fish-street Hill, which had stood untouched by painter, carpenter, or bricklayer, for a century, had been pulled down in my absence; and that a neighbouring street, of time-honoured insalubrity and inconvenience, was being drained and widened; I half expected to find St. Paul’s Cathedral looking older.\n\nFor some changes in the fortunes of my friends, I was prepared. My aunt had long been re-established at Dover, and Traddles had begun to get into some little practice at the Bar, in the very first term after my departure. He had chambers in Gray’s Inn, now; and had told me, in his last letters, that he was not without hopes of being soon united to the dearest girl in the world.\n\nThey expected me home before Christmas; but had no idea of my returning so soon. I had purposely misled them, that I might have the pleasure of taking them by surprise. And yet, I was perverse enough to feel a chill and disappointment in receiving no welcome, and rattling, alone and silent, through the misty streets.\n\nThe well-known shops, however, with their cheerful lights, did something for me; and when I alighted at the door of the Gray’s Inn Coffee-house, I had recovered my spirits. It recalled, at first, that so-different time when I had put up at the Golden Cross, and reminded me of the changes that had come to pass since then; but that was natural.\n\n‘Do you know where Mr. Traddles lives in the Inn?’ I asked the waiter, as I warmed myself by the coffee-room fire.\n\n‘Holborn Court, sir. Number two.’\n\n‘Mr. Traddles has a rising reputation among the lawyers, I believe?’ said I.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ returned the waiter, ‘probably he has, sir; but I am not aware of it myself.’\n\nThis waiter, who was middle-aged and spare, looked for help to a waiter of more authority--a stout, potential old man, with a double chin, in black breeches and stockings, who came out of a place like a churchwarden’s pew, at the end of the coffee-room, where he kept company with a cash-box, a Directory, a Law-list, and other books and papers.\n\n‘Mr. Traddles,’ said the spare waiter. ‘Number two in the Court.’\n\nThe potential waiter waved him away, and turned, gravely, to me.\n\n‘I was inquiring,’ said I, ‘whether Mr. Traddles, at number two in the Court, has not a rising reputation among the lawyers?’\n\n‘Never heard his name,’ said the waiter, in a rich husky voice.\n\nI felt quite apologetic for Traddles.\n\n‘He’s a young man, sure?’ said the portentous waiter, fixing his eyes severely on me. ‘How long has he been in the Inn?’\n\n‘Not above three years,’ said I.\n\nThe waiter, who I supposed had lived in his churchwarden’s pew for forty years, could not pursue such an insignificant subject. He asked me what I would have for dinner?\n\nI felt I was in England again, and really was quite cast down on Traddles’s account. There seemed to be no hope for him. I meekly ordered a bit of fish and a steak, and stood before the fire musing on his obscurity.\n\nAs I followed the chief waiter with my eyes, I could not help thinking that the garden in which he had gradually blown to be the flower he was, was an arduous place to rise in. It had such a prescriptive, stiff-necked, long-established, solemn, elderly air. I glanced about the room, which had had its sanded floor sanded, no doubt, in exactly the same manner when the chief waiter was a boy--if he ever was a boy, which appeared improbable; and at the shining tables, where I saw myself reflected, in unruffled depths of old mahogany; and at the lamps, without a flaw in their trimming or cleaning; and at the comfortable green curtains, with their pure brass rods, snugly enclosing the boxes; and at the two large coal fires, brightly burning; and at the rows of decanters, burly as if with the consciousness of pipes of expensive old port wine below; and both England, and the law, appeared to me to be very difficult indeed to be taken by storm. I went up to my bedroom to change my wet clothes; and the vast extent of that old wainscoted apartment (which was over the archway leading to the Inn, I remember), and the sedate immensity of the four-post bedstead, and the indomitable gravity of the chests of drawers, all seemed to unite in sternly frowning on the fortunes of Traddles, or on any such daring youth. I came down again to my dinner; and even the slow comfort of the meal, and the orderly silence of the place--which was bare of guests, the Long Vacation not yet being over--were eloquent on the audacity of Traddles, and his small hopes of a livelihood for twenty years to come.\n\nI had seen nothing like this since I went away, and it quite dashed my hopes for my friend. The chief waiter had had enough of me. He came near me no more; but devoted himself to an old gentleman in long gaiters, to meet whom a pint of special port seemed to come out of the cellar of its own accord, for he gave no order. The second waiter informed me, in a whisper, that this old gentleman was a retired conveyancer living in the Square, and worth a mint of money, which it was expected he would leave to his laundress’s daughter; likewise that it was rumoured that he had a service of plate in a bureau, all tarnished with lying by, though more than one spoon and a fork had never yet been beheld in his chambers by mortal vision. By this time, I quite gave Traddles up for lost; and settled in my own mind that there was no hope for him.\n\nBeing very anxious to see the dear old fellow, nevertheless, I dispatched my dinner, in a manner not at all calculated to raise me in the opinion of the chief waiter, and hurried out by the back way. Number two in the Court was soon reached; and an inscription on the door-post informing me that Mr. Traddles occupied a set of chambers on the top storey, I ascended the staircase. A crazy old staircase I found it to be, feebly lighted on each landing by a club-headed little oil wick, dying away in a little dungeon of dirty glass.\n\nIn the course of my stumbling upstairs, I fancied I heard a pleasant sound of laughter; and not the laughter of an attorney or barrister, or attorney’s clerk or barrister’s clerk, but of two or three merry girls. Happening, however, as I stopped to listen, to put my foot in a hole where the Honourable Society of Gray’s Inn had left a plank deficient, I fell down with some noise, and when I recovered my footing all was silent.\n\nGroping my way more carefully, for the rest of the journey, my heart beat high when I found the outer door, which had Mr. TRADDLES painted on it, open. I knocked. A considerable scuffling within ensued, but nothing else. I therefore knocked again.\n\nA small sharp-looking lad, half-footboy and half-clerk, who was very much out of breath, but who looked at me as if he defied me to prove it legally, presented himself.\n\n‘Is Mr. Traddles within?’ I said.\n\n‘Yes, sir, but he’s engaged.’\n\n‘I want to see him.’\n\nAfter a moment’s survey of me, the sharp-looking lad decided to let me in; and opening the door wider for that purpose, admitted me, first, into a little closet of a hall, and next into a little sitting-room; where I came into the presence of my old friend (also out of breath), seated at a table, and bending over papers.\n\n‘Good God!’ cried Traddles, looking up. ‘It’s Copperfield!’ and rushed into my arms, where I held him tight.\n\n‘All well, my dear Traddles?’\n\n‘All well, my dear, dear Copperfield, and nothing but good news!’\n\nWe cried with pleasure, both of us.\n\n‘My dear fellow,’ said Traddles, rumpling his hair in his excitement, which was a most unnecessary operation, ‘my dearest Copperfield, my long-lost and most welcome friend, how glad I am to see you! How brown you are! How glad I am! Upon my life and honour, I never was so rejoiced, my beloved Copperfield, never!’\n\nI was equally at a loss to express my emotions. I was quite unable to speak, at first.\n\n‘My dear fellow!’ said Traddles. ‘And grown so famous! My glorious Copperfield! Good gracious me, WHEN did you come, WHERE have you come from, WHAT have you been doing?’\n\nNever pausing for an answer to anything he said, Traddles, who had clapped me into an easy-chair by the fire, all this time impetuously stirred the fire with one hand, and pulled at my neck-kerchief with the other, under some wild delusion that it was a great-coat. Without putting down the poker, he now hugged me again; and I hugged him; and, both laughing, and both wiping our eyes, we both sat down, and shook hands across the hearth.\n\n‘To think,’ said Traddles, ‘that you should have been so nearly coming home as you must have been, my dear old boy, and not at the ceremony!’\n\n‘What ceremony, my dear Traddles?’\n\n‘Good gracious me!’ cried Traddles, opening his eyes in his old way. ‘Didn’t you get my last letter?’\n\n‘Certainly not, if it referred to any ceremony.’\n\n‘Why, my dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, sticking his hair upright with both hands, and then putting his hands on my knees, ‘I am married!’\n\n‘Married!’ I cried joyfully.\n\n‘Lord bless me, yes!’ said Traddles--‘by the Reverend Horace--to Sophy--down in Devonshire. Why, my dear boy, she’s behind the window curtain! Look here!’\n\nTo my amazement, the dearest girl in the world came at that same instant, laughing and blushing, from her place of concealment. And a more cheerful, amiable, honest, happy, bright-looking bride, I believe (as I could not help saying on the spot) the world never saw. I kissed her as an old acquaintance should, and wished them joy with all my might of heart.\n\n‘Dear me,’ said Traddles, ‘what a delightful re-union this is! You are so extremely brown, my dear Copperfield! God bless my soul, how happy I am!’\n\n‘And so am I,’ said I.\n\n‘And I am sure I am!’ said the blushing and laughing Sophy.\n\n‘We are all as happy as possible!’ said Traddles. ‘Even the girls are happy. Dear me, I declare I forgot them!’\n\n‘Forgot?’ said I.\n\n‘The girls,’ said Traddles. ‘Sophy’s sisters. They are staying with us. They have come to have a peep at London. The fact is, when--was it you that tumbled upstairs, Copperfield?’\n\n‘It was,’ said I, laughing.\n\n‘Well then, when you tumbled upstairs,’ said Traddles, ‘I was romping with the girls. In point of fact, we were playing at Puss in the Corner. But as that wouldn’t do in Westminster Hall, and as it wouldn’t look quite professional if they were seen by a client, they decamped. And they are now--listening, I have no doubt,’ said Traddles, glancing at the door of another room.\n\n‘I am sorry,’ said I, laughing afresh, ‘to have occasioned such a dispersion.’\n\n‘Upon my word,’ rejoined Traddles, greatly delighted, ‘if you had seen them running away, and running back again, after you had knocked, to pick up the combs they had dropped out of their hair, and going on in the maddest manner, you wouldn’t have said so. My love, will you fetch the girls?’\n\nSophy tripped away, and we heard her received in the adjoining room with a peal of laughter.\n\n‘Really musical, isn’t it, my dear Copperfield?’ said Traddles. ‘It’s very agreeable to hear. It quite lights up these old rooms. To an unfortunate bachelor of a fellow who has lived alone all his life, you know, it’s positively delicious. It’s charming. Poor things, they have had a great loss in Sophy--who, I do assure you, Copperfield is, and ever was, the dearest girl!--and it gratifies me beyond expression to find them in such good spirits. The society of girls is a very delightful thing, Copperfield. It’s not professional, but it’s very delightful.’\n\nObserving that he slightly faltered, and comprehending that in the goodness of his heart he was fearful of giving me some pain by what he had said, I expressed my concurrence with a heartiness that evidently relieved and pleased him greatly.\n\n‘But then,’ said Traddles, ‘our domestic arrangements are, to say the truth, quite unprofessional altogether, my dear Copperfield. Even Sophy’s being here, is unprofessional. And we have no other place of abode. We have put to sea in a cockboat, but we are quite prepared to rough it. And Sophy’s an extraordinary manager! You’ll be surprised how those girls are stowed away. I am sure I hardly know how it’s done!’\n\n‘Are many of the young ladies with you?’ I inquired.\n\n‘The eldest, the Beauty is here,’ said Traddles, in a low confidential voice, ‘Caroline. And Sarah’s here--the one I mentioned to you as having something the matter with her spine, you know. Immensely better! And the two youngest that Sophy educated are with us. And Louisa’s here.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ cried I.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Traddles. ‘Now the whole set--I mean the chambers--is only three rooms; but Sophy arranges for the girls in the most wonderful way, and they sleep as comfortably as possible. Three in that room,’ said Traddles, pointing. ‘Two in that.’\n\nI could not help glancing round, in search of the accommodation remaining for Mr. and Mrs. Traddles. Traddles understood me.\n\n‘Well!’ said Traddles, ‘we are prepared to rough it, as I said just now, and we did improvise a bed last week, upon the floor here. But there’s a little room in the roof--a very nice room, when you’re up there--which Sophy papered herself, to surprise me; and that’s our room at present. It’s a capital little gipsy sort of place. There’s quite a view from it.’\n\n‘And you are happily married at last, my dear Traddles!’ said I. ‘How rejoiced I am!’\n\n‘Thank you, my dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, as we shook hands once more. ‘Yes, I am as happy as it’s possible to be. There’s your old friend, you see,’ said Traddles, nodding triumphantly at the flower-pot and stand; ‘and there’s the table with the marble top! All the other furniture is plain and serviceable, you perceive. And as to plate, Lord bless you, we haven’t so much as a tea-spoon.’\n\n‘All to be earned?’ said I, cheerfully.\n\n‘Exactly so,’ replied Traddles, ‘all to be earned. Of course we have something in the shape of tea-spoons, because we stir our tea. But they’re Britannia metal.’\n\n‘The silver will be the brighter when it comes,’ said I.\n\n‘The very thing we say!’ cried Traddles. ‘You see, my dear Copperfield,’ falling again into the low confidential tone, ‘after I had delivered my argument in DOE dem. JIPES versus WIGZIELL, which did me great service with the profession, I went down into Devonshire, and had some serious conversation in private with the Reverend Horace. I dwelt upon the fact that Sophy--who I do assure you, Copperfield, is the dearest girl!--’\n\n‘I am certain she is!’ said I.\n\n‘She is, indeed!’ rejoined Traddles. ‘But I am afraid I am wandering from the subject. Did I mention the Reverend Horace?’\n\n‘You said that you dwelt upon the fact--’\n\n‘True! Upon the fact that Sophy and I had been engaged for a long period, and that Sophy, with the permission of her parents, was more than content to take me--in short,’ said Traddles, with his old frank smile, ‘on our present Britannia-metal footing. Very well. I then proposed to the Reverend Horace--who is a most excellent clergyman, Copperfield, and ought to be a Bishop; or at least ought to have enough to live upon, without pinching himself--that if I could turn the corner, say of two hundred and fifty pounds, in one year; and could see my way pretty clearly to that, or something better, next year; and could plainly furnish a little place like this, besides; then, and in that case, Sophy and I should be united. I took the liberty of representing that we had been patient for a good many years; and that the circumstance of Sophy’s being extraordinarily useful at home, ought not to operate with her affectionate parents, against her establishment in life--don’t you see?’\n\n‘Certainly it ought not,’ said I.\n\n‘I am glad you think so, Copperfield,’ rejoined Traddles, ‘because, without any imputation on the Reverend Horace, I do think parents, and brothers, and so forth, are sometimes rather selfish in such cases. Well! I also pointed out, that my most earnest desire was, to be useful to the family; and that if I got on in the world, and anything should happen to him--I refer to the Reverend Horace--’\n\n‘I understand,’ said I.\n\n‘--Or to Mrs. Crewler--it would be the utmost gratification of my wishes, to be a parent to the girls. He replied in a most admirable manner, exceedingly flattering to my feelings, and undertook to obtain the consent of Mrs. Crewler to this arrangement. They had a dreadful time of it with her. It mounted from her legs into her chest, and then into her head--’\n‘What mounted?’ I asked.\n\n‘Her grief,’ replied Traddles, with a serious look. ‘Her feelings generally. As I mentioned on a former occasion, she is a very superior woman, but has lost the use of her limbs. Whatever occurs to harass her, usually settles in her legs; but on this occasion it mounted to the chest, and then to the head, and, in short, pervaded the whole system in a most alarming manner. However, they brought her through it by unremitting and affectionate attention; and we were married yesterday six weeks. You have no idea what a Monster I felt, Copperfield, when I saw the whole family crying and fainting away in every direction! Mrs. Crewler couldn’t see me before we left--couldn’t forgive me, then, for depriving her of her child--but she is a good creature, and has done so since. I had a delightful letter from her, only this morning.’\n\n‘And in short, my dear friend,’ said I, ‘you feel as blest as you deserve to feel!’\n\n‘Oh! That’s your partiality!’ laughed Traddles. ‘But, indeed, I am in a most enviable state. I work hard, and read Law insatiably. I get up at five every morning, and don’t mind it at all. I hide the girls in the daytime, and make merry with them in the evening. And I assure you I am quite sorry that they are going home on Tuesday, which is the day before the first day of Michaelmas Term. But here,’ said Traddles, breaking off in his confidence, and speaking aloud, ‘ARE the girls! Mr. Copperfield, Miss Crewler--Miss Sarah--Miss Louisa--Margaret and Lucy!’\n\nThey were a perfect nest of roses; they looked so wholesome and fresh. They were all pretty, and Miss Caroline was very handsome; but there was a loving, cheerful, fireside quality in Sophy’s bright looks, which was better than that, and which assured me that my friend had chosen well. We all sat round the fire; while the sharp boy, who I now divined had lost his breath in putting the papers out, cleared them away again, and produced the tea-things. After that, he retired for the night, shutting the outer door upon us with a bang. Mrs. Traddles, with perfect pleasure and composure beaming from her household eyes, having made the tea, then quietly made the toast as she sat in a corner by the fire.\n\nShe had seen Agnes, she told me while she was toasting. ‘Tom’ had taken her down into Kent for a wedding trip, and there she had seen my aunt, too; and both my aunt and Agnes were well, and they had all talked of nothing but me. ‘Tom’ had never had me out of his thoughts, she really believed, all the time I had been away. ‘Tom’ was the authority for everything. ‘Tom’ was evidently the idol of her life; never to be shaken on his pedestal by any commotion; always to be believed in, and done homage to with the whole faith of her heart, come what might.\n\nThe deference which both she and Traddles showed towards the Beauty, pleased me very much. I don’t know that I thought it very reasonable; but I thought it very delightful, and essentially a part of their character. If Traddles ever for an instant missed the tea-spoons that were still to be won, I have no doubt it was when he handed the Beauty her tea. If his sweet-tempered wife could have got up any self-assertion against anyone, I am satisfied it could only have been because she was the Beauty’s sister. A few slight indications of a rather petted and capricious manner, which I observed in the Beauty, were manifestly considered, by Traddles and his wife, as her birthright and natural endowment. If she had been born a Queen Bee, and they labouring Bees, they could not have been more satisfied of that.\n\nBut their self-forgetfulness charmed me. Their pride in these girls, and their submission of themselves to all their whims, was the pleasantest little testimony to their own worth I could have desired to see. If Traddles were addressed as ‘a darling’, once in the course of that evening; and besought to bring something here, or carry something there, or take something up, or put something down, or find something, or fetch something, he was so addressed, by one or other of his sisters-in-law, at least twelve times in an hour. Neither could they do anything without Sophy. Somebody’s hair fell down, and nobody but Sophy could put it up. Somebody forgot how a particular tune went, and nobody but Sophy could hum that tune right. Somebody wanted to recall the name of a place in Devonshire, and only Sophy knew it. Something was wanted to be written home, and Sophy alone could be trusted to write before breakfast in the morning. Somebody broke down in a piece of knitting, and no one but Sophy was able to put the defaulter in the right direction. They were entire mistresses of the place, and Sophy and Traddles waited on them. How many children Sophy could have taken care of in her time, I can’t imagine; but she seemed to be famous for knowing every sort of song that ever was addressed to a child in the English tongue; and she sang dozens to order with the clearest little voice in the world, one after another (every sister issuing directions for a different tune, and the Beauty generally striking in last), so that I was quite fascinated. The best of all was, that, in the midst of their exactions, all the sisters had a great tenderness and respect both for Sophy and Traddles. I am sure, when I took my leave, and Traddles was coming out to walk with me to the coffee-house, I thought I had never seen an obstinate head of hair, or any other head of hair, rolling about in such a shower of kisses.\n\nAltogether, it was a scene I could not help dwelling on with pleasure, for a long time after I got back and had wished Traddles good night. If I had beheld a thousand roses blowing in a top set of chambers, in that withered Gray’s Inn, they could not have brightened it half so much. The idea of those Devonshire girls, among the dry law-stationers and the attorneys’ offices; and of the tea and toast, and children’s songs, in that grim atmosphere of pounce and parchment, red-tape, dusty wafers, ink-jars, brief and draft paper, law reports, writs, declarations, and bills of costs; seemed almost as pleasantly fanciful as if I had dreamed that the Sultan’s famous family had been admitted on the roll of attorneys, and had brought the talking bird, the singing tree, and the golden water into Gray’s Inn Hall. Somehow, I found that I had taken leave of Traddles for the night, and come back to the coffee-house, with a great change in my despondency about him. I began to think he would get on, in spite of all the many orders of chief waiters in England.\n\nDrawing a chair before one of the coffee-room fires to think about him at my leisure, I gradually fell from the consideration of his happiness to tracing prospects in the live-coals, and to thinking, as they broke and changed, of the principal vicissitudes and separations that had marked my life. I had not seen a coal fire, since I had left England three years ago: though many a wood fire had I watched, as it crumbled into hoary ashes, and mingled with the feathery heap upon the hearth, which not inaptly figured to me, in my despondency, my own dead hopes.\n\nI could think of the past now, gravely, but not bitterly; and could contemplate the future in a brave spirit. Home, in its best sense, was for me no more. She in whom I might have inspired a dearer love, I had taught to be my sister. She would marry, and would have new claimants on her tenderness; and in doing it, would never know the love for her that had grown up in my heart. It was right that I should pay the forfeit of my headlong passion. What I reaped, I had sown.\n\nI was thinking. And had I truly disciplined my heart to this, and could I resolutely bear it, and calmly hold the place in her home which she had calmly held in mine,--when I found my eyes resting on a countenance that might have arisen out of the fire, in its association with my early remembrances.\n\nLittle Mr. Chillip the Doctor, to whose good offices I was indebted in the very first chapter of this history, sat reading a newspaper in the shadow of an opposite corner. He was tolerably stricken in years by this time; but, being a mild, meek, calm little man, had worn so easily, that I thought he looked at that moment just as he might have looked when he sat in our parlour, waiting for me to be born.\n\nMr. Chillip had left Blunderstone six or seven years ago, and I had never seen him since. He sat placidly perusing the newspaper, with his little head on one side, and a glass of warm sherry negus at his elbow. He was so extremely conciliatory in his manner that he seemed to apologize to the very newspaper for taking the liberty of reading it.\n\nI walked up to where he was sitting, and said, ‘How do you do, Mr. Chillip?’\n\nHe was greatly fluttered by this unexpected address from a stranger, and replied, in his slow way, ‘I thank you, sir, you are very good. Thank you, sir. I hope YOU are well.’\n\n‘You don’t remember me?’ said I.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ returned Mr. Chillip, smiling very meekly, and shaking his head as he surveyed me, ‘I have a kind of an impression that something in your countenance is familiar to me, sir; but I couldn’t lay my hand upon your name, really.’\n\n‘And yet you knew it, long before I knew it myself,’ I returned.\n\n‘Did I indeed, sir?’ said Mr. Chillip. ‘Is it possible that I had the honour, sir, of officiating when--?’\n\n‘Yes,’ said I.\n\n‘Dear me!’ cried Mr. Chillip. ‘But no doubt you are a good deal changed since then, sir?’\n\n‘Probably,’ said I.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ observed Mr. Chillip, ‘I hope you’ll excuse me, if I am compelled to ask the favour of your name?’\n\nOn my telling him my name, he was really moved. He quite shook hands with me--which was a violent proceeding for him, his usual course being to slide a tepid little fish-slice, an inch or two in advance of his hip, and evince the greatest discomposure when anybody grappled with it. Even now, he put his hand in his coat-pocket as soon as he could disengage it, and seemed relieved when he had got it safe back.\n\n‘Dear me, sir!’ said Mr. Chillip, surveying me with his head on one side. ‘And it’s Mr. Copperfield, is it? Well, sir, I think I should have known you, if I had taken the liberty of looking more closely at you. There’s a strong resemblance between you and your poor father, sir.’\n\n‘I never had the happiness of seeing my father,’ I observed.\n\n‘Very true, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip, in a soothing tone. ‘And very much to be deplored it was, on all accounts! We are not ignorant, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip, slowly shaking his little head again, ‘down in our part of the country, of your fame. There must be great excitement here, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip, tapping himself on the forehead with his forefinger. ‘You must find it a trying occupation, sir!’\n\n‘What is your part of the country now?’ I asked, seating myself near him.\n\n‘I am established within a few miles of Bury St. Edmund’s, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip. ‘Mrs. Chillip, coming into a little property in that neighbourhood, under her father’s will, I bought a practice down there, in which you will be glad to hear I am doing well. My daughter is growing quite a tall lass now, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip, giving his little head another little shake. ‘Her mother let down two tucks in her frocks only last week. Such is time, you see, sir!’\n\nAs the little man put his now empty glass to his lips, when he made this reflection, I proposed to him to have it refilled, and I would keep him company with another. ‘Well, sir,’ he returned, in his slow way, ‘it’s more than I am accustomed to; but I can’t deny myself the pleasure of your conversation. It seems but yesterday that I had the honour of attending you in the measles. You came through them charmingly, sir!’\n\nI acknowledged this compliment, and ordered the negus, which was soon produced. ‘Quite an uncommon dissipation!’ said Mr. Chillip, stirring it, ‘but I can’t resist so extraordinary an occasion. You have no family, sir?’\n\nI shook my head.\n\n‘I was aware that you sustained a bereavement, sir, some time ago,’ said Mr. Chillip. ‘I heard it from your father-in-law’s sister. Very decided character there, sir?’\n\n‘Why, yes,’ said I, ‘decided enough. Where did you see her, Mr. Chillip?’\n\n‘Are you not aware, sir,’ returned Mr. Chillip, with his placidest smile, ‘that your father-in-law is again a neighbour of mine?’\n\n‘No,’ said I.\n‘He is indeed, sir!’ said Mr. Chillip. ‘Married a young lady of that part, with a very good little property, poor thing.---And this action of the brain now, sir? Don’t you find it fatigue you?’ said Mr. Chillip, looking at me like an admiring Robin.\n\nI waived that question, and returned to the Murdstones. ‘I was aware of his being married again. Do you attend the family?’ I asked.\n\n‘Not regularly. I have been called in,’ he replied. ‘Strong phrenological developments of the organ of firmness, in Mr. Murdstone and his sister, sir.’\n\nI replied with such an expressive look, that Mr. Chillip was emboldened by that, and the negus together, to give his head several short shakes, and thoughtfully exclaim, ‘Ah, dear me! We remember old times, Mr. Copperfield!’\n\n‘And the brother and sister are pursuing their old course, are they?’ said I.\n\n‘Well, sir,’ replied Mr. Chillip, ‘a medical man, being so much in families, ought to have neither eyes nor ears for anything but his profession. Still, I must say, they are very severe, sir: both as to this life and the next.’\n\n‘The next will be regulated without much reference to them, I dare say,’ I returned: ‘what are they doing as to this?’\n\nMr. Chillip shook his head, stirred his negus, and sipped it.\n\n‘She was a charming woman, sir!’ he observed in a plaintive manner.\n\n‘The present Mrs. Murdstone?’\n\n‘A charming woman indeed, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip; ‘as amiable, I am sure, as it was possible to be! Mrs. Chillip’s opinion is, that her spirit has been entirely broken since her marriage, and that she is all but melancholy mad. And the ladies,’ observed Mr. Chillip, timorously, ‘are great observers, sir.’\n\n‘I suppose she was to be subdued and broken to their detestable mould, Heaven help her!’ said I. ‘And she has been.’\n\n‘Well, sir, there were violent quarrels at first, I assure you,’ said Mr. Chillip; ‘but she is quite a shadow now. Would it be considered forward if I was to say to you, sir, in confidence, that since the sister came to help, the brother and sister between them have nearly reduced her to a state of imbecility?’\n\nI told him I could easily believe it.\n\n‘I have no hesitation in saying,’ said Mr. Chillip, fortifying himself with another sip of negus, ‘between you and me, sir, that her mother died of it--or that tyranny, gloom, and worry have made Mrs. Murdstone nearly imbecile. She was a lively young woman, sir, before marriage, and their gloom and austerity destroyed her. They go about with her, now, more like her keepers than her husband and sister-in-law. That was Mrs. Chillip’s remark to me, only last week. And I assure you, sir, the ladies are great observers. Mrs. Chillip herself is a great observer!’\n\n‘Does he gloomily profess to be (I am ashamed to use the word in such association) religious still?’ I inquired.\n\n‘You anticipate, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip, his eyelids getting quite red with the unwonted stimulus in which he was indulging. ‘One of Mrs. Chillip’s most impressive remarks. Mrs. Chillip,’ he proceeded, in the calmest and slowest manner, ‘quite electrified me, by pointing out that Mr. Murdstone sets up an image of himself, and calls it the Divine Nature. You might have knocked me down on the flat of my back, sir, with the feather of a pen, I assure you, when Mrs. Chillip said so. The ladies are great observers, sir?’\n\n‘Intuitively,’ said I, to his extreme delight.\n\n‘I am very happy to receive such support in my opinion, sir,’ he rejoined. ‘It is not often that I venture to give a non-medical opinion, I assure you. Mr. Murdstone delivers public addresses sometimes, and it is said,--in short, sir, it is said by Mrs. Chillip,--that the darker tyrant he has lately been, the more ferocious is his doctrine.’\n\n‘I believe Mrs. Chillip to be perfectly right,’ said I.\n\n‘Mrs. Chillip does go so far as to say,’ pursued the meekest of little men, much encouraged, ‘that what such people miscall their religion, is a vent for their bad humours and arrogance. And do you know I must say, sir,’ he continued, mildly laying his head on one side, ‘that I DON’T find authority for Mr. and Miss Murdstone in the New Testament?’\n\n‘I never found it either!’ said I.\n\n‘In the meantime, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip, ‘they are much disliked; and as they are very free in consigning everybody who dislikes them to perdition, we really have a good deal of perdition going on in our neighbourhood! However, as Mrs. Chillip says, sir, they undergo a continual punishment; for they are turned inward, to feed upon their own hearts, and their own hearts are very bad feeding. Now, sir, about that brain of yours, if you’ll excuse my returning to it. Don’t you expose it to a good deal of excitement, sir?’\n\nI found it not difficult, in the excitement of Mr. Chillip’s own brain, under his potations of negus, to divert his attention from this topic to his own affairs, on which, for the next half-hour, he was quite loquacious; giving me to understand, among other pieces of information, that he was then at the Gray’s Inn Coffee-house to lay his professional evidence before a Commission of Lunacy, touching the state of mind of a patient who had become deranged from excessive drinking. ‘And I assure you, sir,’ he said, ‘I am extremely nervous on such occasions. I could not support being what is called Bullied, sir. It would quite unman me. Do you know it was some time before I recovered the conduct of that alarming lady, on the night of your birth, Mr. Copperfield?’\n\nI told him that I was going down to my aunt, the Dragon of that night, early in the morning; and that she was one of the most tender-hearted and excellent of women, as he would know full well if he knew her better. The mere notion of the possibility of his ever seeing her again, appeared to terrify him. He replied with a small pale smile, ‘Is she so, indeed, sir? Really?’ and almost immediately called for a candle, and went to bed, as if he were not quite safe anywhere else. He did not actually stagger under the negus; but I should think his placid little pulse must have made two or three more beats in a minute, than it had done since the great night of my aunt’s disappointment, when she struck at him with her bonnet.\n\nThoroughly tired, I went to bed too, at midnight; passed the next day on the Dover coach; burst safe and sound into my aunt’s old parlour while she was at tea (she wore spectacles now); and was received by her, and Mr. Dick, and dear old Peggotty, who acted as housekeeper, with open arms and tears of joy. My aunt was mightily amused, when we began to talk composedly, by my account of my meeting with Mr. Chillip, and of his holding her in such dread remembrance; and both she and Peggotty had a great deal to say about my poor mother’s second husband, and ‘that murdering woman of a sister’,--on whom I think no pain or penalty would have induced my aunt to bestow any Christian or Proper Name, or any other designation.\n\n# CHAPTER 60. AGNES\n\nMy aunt and I, when we were left alone, talked far into the night. How the emigrants never wrote home, otherwise than cheerfully and hopefully; how Mr. Micawber had actually remitted divers small sums of money, on account of those ‘pecuniary liabilities’, in reference to which he had been so business-like as between man and man; how Janet, returning into my aunt’s service when she came back to Dover, had finally carried out her renunciation of mankind by entering into wedlock with a thriving tavern-keeper; and how my aunt had finally set her seal on the same great principle, by aiding and abetting the bride, and crowning the marriage-ceremony with her presence; were among our topics--already more or less familiar to me through the letters I had had. Mr. Dick, as usual, was not forgotten. My aunt informed me how he incessantly occupied himself in copying everything he could lay his hands on, and kept King Charles the First at a respectful distance by that semblance of employment; how it was one of the main joys and rewards of her life that he was free and happy, instead of pining in monotonous restraint; and how (as a novel general conclusion) nobody but she could ever fully know what he was.\n\n‘And when, Trot,’ said my aunt, patting the back of my hand, as we sat in our old way before the fire, ‘when are you going over to Canterbury?’\n\n‘I shall get a horse, and ride over tomorrow morning, aunt, unless you will go with me?’\n\n‘No!’ said my aunt, in her short abrupt way. ‘I mean to stay where I am.’\n\nThen, I should ride, I said. I could not have come through Canterbury today without stopping, if I had been coming to anyone but her.\n\nShe was pleased, but answered, ‘Tut, Trot; MY old bones would have kept till tomorrow!’ and softly patted my hand again, as I sat looking thoughtfully at the fire.\n\nThoughtfully, for I could not be here once more, and so near Agnes, without the revival of those regrets with which I had so long been occupied. Softened regrets they might be, teaching me what I had failed to learn when my younger life was all before me, but not the less regrets. ‘Oh, Trot,’ I seemed to hear my aunt say once more; and I understood her better now--‘Blind, blind, blind!’\n\nWe both kept silence for some minutes. When I raised my eyes, I found that she was steadily observant of me. Perhaps she had followed the current of my mind; for it seemed to me an easy one to track now, wilful as it had been once.\n\n‘You will find her father a white-haired old man,’ said my aunt, ‘though a better man in all other respects--a reclaimed man. Neither will you find him measuring all human interests, and joys, and sorrows, with his one poor little inch-rule now. Trust me, child, such things must shrink very much, before they can be measured off in that way.’\n\n‘Indeed they must,’ said I.\n\n‘You will find her,’ pursued my aunt, ‘as good, as beautiful, as earnest, as disinterested, as she has always been. If I knew higher praise, Trot, I would bestow it on her.’\n\nThere was no higher praise for her; no higher reproach for me. Oh, how had I strayed so far away!\n\n‘If she trains the young girls whom she has about her, to be like herself,’ said my aunt, earnest even to the filling of her eyes with tears, ‘Heaven knows, her life will be well employed! Useful and happy, as she said that day! How could she be otherwise than useful and happy!’\n\n‘Has Agnes any--’ I was thinking aloud, rather than speaking.\n\n‘Well? Hey? Any what?’ said my aunt, sharply.\n\n‘Any lover,’ said I.\n\n‘A score,’ cried my aunt, with a kind of indignant pride. ‘She might have married twenty times, my dear, since you have been gone!’\n\n‘No doubt,’ said I. ‘No doubt. But has she any lover who is worthy of her? Agnes could care for no other.’\n\nMy aunt sat musing for a little while, with her chin upon her hand. Slowly raising her eyes to mine, she said:\n\n‘I suspect she has an attachment, Trot.’\n\n‘A prosperous one?’ said I.\n\n‘Trot,’ returned my aunt gravely, ‘I can’t say. I have no right to tell you even so much. She has never confided it to me, but I suspect it.’\n\nShe looked so attentively and anxiously at me (I even saw her tremble), that I felt now, more than ever, that she had followed my late thoughts. I summoned all the resolutions I had made, in all those many days and nights, and all those many conflicts of my heart.\n\n‘If it should be so,’ I began, ‘and I hope it is-’\n\n‘I don’t know that it is,’ said my aunt curtly. ‘You must not be ruled by my suspicions. You must keep them secret. They are very slight, perhaps. I have no right to speak.’\n\n‘If it should be so,’ I repeated, ‘Agnes will tell me at her own good time. A sister to whom I have confided so much, aunt, will not be reluctant to confide in me.’\n\nMy aunt withdrew her eyes from mine, as slowly as she had turned them upon me; and covered them thoughtfully with her hand. By and by she put her other hand on my shoulder; and so we both sat, looking into the past, without saying another word, until we parted for the night.\n\nI rode away, early in the morning, for the scene of my old school-days. I cannot say that I was yet quite happy, in the hope that I was gaining a victory over myself; even in the prospect of so soon looking on her face again.\n\nThe well-remembered ground was soon traversed, and I came into the quiet streets, where every stone was a boy’s book to me. I went on foot to the old house, and went away with a heart too full to enter. I returned; and looking, as I passed, through the low window of the turret-room where first Uriah Heep, and afterwards Mr. Micawber, had been wont to sit, saw that it was a little parlour now, and that there was no office. Otherwise the staid old house was, as to its cleanliness and order, still just as it had been when I first saw it. I requested the new maid who admitted me, to tell Miss Wickfield that a gentleman who waited on her from a friend abroad, was there; and I was shown up the grave old staircase (cautioned of the steps I knew so well), into the unchanged drawing-room. The books that Agnes and I had read together, were on their shelves; and the desk where I had laboured at my lessons, many a night, stood yet at the same old corner of the table. All the little changes that had crept in when the Heeps were there, were changed again. Everything was as it used to be, in the happy time.\n\nI stood in a window, and looked across the ancient street at the opposite houses, recalling how I had watched them on wet afternoons, when I first came there; and how I had used to speculate about the people who appeared at any of the windows, and had followed them with my eyes up and down stairs, while women went clicking along the pavement in pattens, and the dull rain fell in slanting lines, and poured out of the water-spout yonder, and flowed into the road. The feeling with which I used to watch the tramps, as they came into the town on those wet evenings, at dusk, and limped past, with their bundles drooping over their shoulders at the ends of sticks, came freshly back to me; fraught, as then, with the smell of damp earth, and wet leaves and briar, and the sensation of the very airs that blew upon me in my own toilsome journey.\n\nThe opening of the little door in the panelled wall made me start and turn. Her beautiful serene eyes met mine as she came towards me. She stopped and laid her hand upon her bosom, and I caught her in my arms.\n\n‘Agnes! my dear girl! I have come too suddenly upon you.’\n\n‘No, no! I am so rejoiced to see you, Trotwood!’\n\n‘Dear Agnes, the happiness it is to me, to see you once again!’\n\nI folded her to my heart, and, for a little while, we were both silent. Presently we sat down, side by side; and her angel-face was turned upon me with the welcome I had dreamed of, waking and sleeping, for whole years.\n\nShe was so true, she was so beautiful, she was so good,--I owed her so much gratitude, she was so dear to me, that I could find no utterance for what I felt. I tried to bless her, tried to thank her, tried to tell her (as I had often done in letters) what an influence she had upon me; but all my efforts were in vain. My love and joy were dumb.\n\nWith her own sweet tranquillity, she calmed my agitation; led me back to the time of our parting; spoke to me of Emily, whom she had visited, in secret, many times; spoke to me tenderly of Dora’s grave. With the unerring instinct of her noble heart, she touched the chords of my memory so softly and harmoniously, that not one jarred within me; I could listen to the sorrowful, distant music, and desire to shrink from nothing it awoke. How could I, when, blended with it all, was her dear self, the better angel of my life?\n\n‘And you, Agnes,’ I said, by and by. ‘Tell me of yourself. You have hardly ever told me of your own life, in all this lapse of time!’\n\n‘What should I tell?’ she answered, with her radiant smile. ‘Papa is well. You see us here, quiet in our own home; our anxieties set at rest, our home restored to us; and knowing that, dear Trotwood, you know all.’\n\n‘All, Agnes?’ said I.\n\nShe looked at me, with some fluttering wonder in her face.\n\n‘Is there nothing else, Sister?’ I said.\n\nHer colour, which had just now faded, returned, and faded again. She smiled; with a quiet sadness, I thought; and shook her head.\n\nI had sought to lead her to what my aunt had hinted at; for, sharply painful to me as it must be to receive that confidence, I was to discipline my heart, and do my duty to her. I saw, however, that she was uneasy, and I let it pass.\n\n‘You have much to do, dear Agnes?’\n\n‘With my school?’ said she, looking up again, in all her bright composure.\n\n‘Yes. It is laborious, is it not?’\n\n‘The labour is so pleasant,’ she returned, ‘that it is scarcely grateful in me to call it by that name.’\n\n‘Nothing good is difficult to you,’ said I.\n\nHer colour came and went once more; and once more, as she bent her head, I saw the same sad smile.\n\n‘You will wait and see papa,’ said Agnes, cheerfully, ‘and pass the day with us? Perhaps you will sleep in your own room? We always call it yours.’\n\nI could not do that, having promised to ride back to my aunt’s at night; but I would pass the day there, joyfully.\n\n‘I must be a prisoner for a little while,’ said Agnes, ‘but here are the old books, Trotwood, and the old music.’\n\n‘Even the old flowers are here,’ said I, looking round; ‘or the old kinds.’\n\n‘I have found a pleasure,’ returned Agnes, smiling, ‘while you have been absent, in keeping everything as it used to be when we were children. For we were very happy then, I think.’\n\n‘Heaven knows we were!’ said I.\n\n‘And every little thing that has reminded me of my brother,’ said Agnes, with her cordial eyes turned cheerfully upon me, ‘has been a welcome companion. Even this,’ showing me the basket-trifle, full of keys, still hanging at her side, ‘seems to jingle a kind of old tune!’\n\nShe smiled again, and went out at the door by which she had come.\n\nIt was for me to guard this sisterly affection with religious care. It was all that I had left myself, and it was a treasure. If I once shook the foundations of the sacred confidence and usage, in virtue of which it was given to me, it was lost, and could never be recovered. I set this steadily before myself. The better I loved her, the more it behoved me never to forget it.\n\nI walked through the streets; and, once more seeing my old adversary the butcher--now a constable, with his staff hanging up in the shop--went down to look at the place where I had fought him; and there meditated on Miss Shepherd and the eldest Miss Larkins, and all the idle loves and likings, and dislikings, of that time. Nothing seemed to have survived that time but Agnes; and she, ever a star above me, was brighter and higher.\n\nWhen I returned, Mr. Wickfield had come home, from a garden he had, a couple of miles or so out of town, where he now employed himself almost every day. I found him as my aunt had described him. We sat down to dinner, with some half-dozen little girls; and he seemed but the shadow of his handsome picture on the wall.\n\nThe tranquillity and peace belonging, of old, to that quiet ground in my memory, pervaded it again. When dinner was done, Mr. Wickfield taking no wine, and I desiring none, we went up-stairs; where Agnes and her little charges sang and played, and worked. After tea the children left us; and we three sat together, talking of the bygone days.\n\n‘My part in them,’ said Mr. Wickfield, shaking his white head, ‘has much matter for regret--for deep regret, and deep contrition, Trotwood, you well know. But I would not cancel it, if it were in my power.’\n\nI could readily believe that, looking at the face beside him.\n\n‘I should cancel with it,’ he pursued, ‘such patience and devotion, such fidelity, such a child’s love, as I must not forget, no! even to forget myself.’\n\n‘I understand you, sir,’ I softly said. ‘I hold it--I have always held it--in veneration.’\n\n‘But no one knows, not even you,’ he returned, ‘how much she has done, how much she has undergone, how hard she has striven. Dear Agnes!’\n\nShe had put her hand entreatingly on his arm, to stop him; and was very, very pale.\n\n‘Well, well!’ he said with a sigh, dismissing, as I then saw, some trial she had borne, or was yet to bear, in connexion with what my aunt had told me. ‘Well! I have never told you, Trotwood, of her mother. Has anyone?’\n\n‘Never, sir.’\n\n‘It’s not much--though it was much to suffer. She married me in opposition to her father’s wish, and he renounced her. She prayed him to forgive her, before my Agnes came into this world. He was a very hard man, and her mother had long been dead. He repulsed her. He broke her heart.’\n\nAgnes leaned upon his shoulder, and stole her arm about his neck.\n\n‘She had an affectionate and gentle heart,’ he said; ‘and it was broken. I knew its tender nature very well. No one could, if I did not. She loved me dearly, but was never happy. She was always labouring, in secret, under this distress; and being delicate and downcast at the time of his last repulse--for it was not the first, by many--pined away and died. She left me Agnes, two weeks old; and the grey hair that you recollect me with, when you first came.’ He kissed Agnes on her cheek.\n\n‘My love for my dear child was a diseased love, but my mind was all unhealthy then. I say no more of that. I am not speaking of myself, Trotwood, but of her mother, and of her. If I give you any clue to what I am, or to what I have been, you will unravel it, I know. What Agnes is, I need not say. I have always read something of her poor mother’s story, in her character; and so I tell it you tonight, when we three are again together, after such great changes. I have told it all.’\n\nHis bowed head, and her angel-face and filial duty, derived a more pathetic meaning from it than they had had before. If I had wanted anything by which to mark this night of our re-union, I should have found it in this.\n\nAgnes rose up from her father’s side, before long; and going softly to her piano, played some of the old airs to which we had often listened in that place.\n\n‘Have you any intention of going away again?’ Agnes asked me, as I was standing by.\n\n‘What does my sister say to that?’\n\n‘I hope not.’\n\n‘Then I have no such intention, Agnes.’\n\n‘I think you ought not, Trotwood, since you ask me,’ she said, mildly. ‘Your growing reputation and success enlarge your power of doing good; and if I could spare my brother,’ with her eyes upon me, ‘perhaps the time could not.’\n\n‘What I am, you have made me, Agnes. You should know best.’\n\n‘I made you, Trotwood?’\n\n‘Yes! Agnes, my dear girl!’ I said, bending over her. ‘I tried to tell you, when we met today, something that has been in my thoughts since Dora died. You remember, when you came down to me in our little room--pointing upward, Agnes?’\n\n‘Oh, Trotwood!’ she returned, her eyes filled with tears. ‘So loving, so confiding, and so young! Can I ever forget?’\n\n‘As you were then, my sister, I have often thought since, you have ever been to me. Ever pointing upward, Agnes; ever leading me to something better; ever directing me to higher things!’\n\nShe only shook her head; through her tears I saw the same sad quiet smile.\n\n‘And I am so grateful to you for it, Agnes, so bound to you, that there is no name for the affection of my heart. I want you to know, yet don’t know how to tell you, that all my life long I shall look up to you, and be guided by you, as I have been through the darkness that is past. Whatever betides, whatever new ties you may form, whatever changes may come between us, I shall always look to you, and love you, as I do now, and have always done. You will always be my solace and resource, as you have always been. Until I die, my dearest sister, I shall see you always before me, pointing upward!’\n\nShe put her hand in mine, and told me she was proud of me, and of what I said; although I praised her very far beyond her worth. Then she went on softly playing, but without removing her eyes from me. ‘Do you know, what I have heard tonight, Agnes,’ said I, ‘strangely seems to be a part of the feeling with which I regarded you when I saw you first--with which I sat beside you in my rough school-days?’\n\n‘You knew I had no mother,’ she replied with a smile, ‘and felt kindly towards me.’\n\n‘More than that, Agnes, I knew, almost as if I had known this story, that there was something inexplicably gentle and softened, surrounding you; something that might have been sorrowful in someone else (as I can now understand it was), but was not so in you.’\n\nShe softly played on, looking at me still.\n\n‘Will you laugh at my cherishing such fancies, Agnes?’\n\n‘No!’\n\n‘Or at my saying that I really believe I felt, even then, that you could be faithfully affectionate against all discouragement, and never cease to be so, until you ceased to live?---Will you laugh at such a dream?’\n\n‘Oh, no! Oh, no!’\n\nFor an instant, a distressful shadow crossed her face; but, even in the start it gave me, it was gone; and she was playing on, and looking at me with her own calm smile.\n\nAs I rode back in the lonely night, the wind going by me like a restless memory, I thought of this, and feared she was not happy. I was not happy; but, thus far, I had faithfully set the seal upon the Past, and, thinking of her, pointing upward, thought of her as pointing to that sky above me, where, in the mystery to come, I might yet love her with a love unknown on earth, and tell her what the strife had been within me when I loved her here.\n\n# CHAPTER 61. I AM SHOWN TWO INTERESTING PENITENTS\n\nFor a time--at all events until my book should be completed, which would be the work of several months--I took up my abode in my aunt’s house at Dover; and there, sitting in the window from which I had looked out at the moon upon the sea, when that roof first gave me shelter, I quietly pursued my task.\n\nIn pursuance of my intention of referring to my own fictions only when their course should incidentally connect itself with the progress of my story, I do not enter on the aspirations, the delights, anxieties, and triumphs of my art. That I truly devoted myself to it with my strongest earnestness, and bestowed upon it every energy of my soul, I have already said. If the books I have written be of any worth, they will supply the rest. I shall otherwise have written to poor purpose, and the rest will be of interest to no one.\n\nOccasionally, I went to London; to lose myself in the swarm of life there, or to consult with Traddles on some business point. He had managed for me, in my absence, with the soundest judgement; and my worldly affairs were prospering. As my notoriety began to bring upon me an enormous quantity of letters from people of whom I had no knowledge--chiefly about nothing, and extremely difficult to answer--I agreed with Traddles to have my name painted up on his door. There, the devoted postman on that beat delivered bushels of letters for me; and there, at intervals, I laboured through them, like a Home Secretary of State without the salary.\n\nAmong this correspondence, there dropped in, every now and then, an obliging proposal from one of the numerous outsiders always lurking about the Commons, to practise under cover of my name (if I would take the necessary steps remaining to make a proctor of myself), and pay me a percentage on the profits. But I declined these offers; being already aware that there were plenty of such covert practitioners in existence, and considering the Commons quite bad enough, without my doing anything to make it worse.\n\nThe girls had gone home, when my name burst into bloom on Traddles’s door; and the sharp boy looked, all day, as if he had never heard of Sophy, shut up in a back room, glancing down from her work into a sooty little strip of garden with a pump in it. But there I always found her, the same bright housewife; often humming her Devonshire ballads when no strange foot was coming up the stairs, and blunting the sharp boy in his official closet with melody.\n\nI wondered, at first, why I so often found Sophy writing in a copy-book; and why she always shut it up when I appeared, and hurried it into the table-drawer. But the secret soon came out. One day, Traddles (who had just come home through the drizzling sleet from Court) took a paper out of his desk, and asked me what I thought of that handwriting?\n\n‘Oh, DON’T, Tom!’ cried Sophy, who was warming his slippers before the fire.\n\n‘My dear,’ returned Tom, in a delighted state, ‘why not? What do you say to that writing, Copperfield?’\n\n‘It’s extraordinarily legal and formal,’ said I. ‘I don’t think I ever saw such a stiff hand.’\n\n‘Not like a lady’s hand, is it?’ said Traddles.\n\n‘A lady’s!’ I repeated. ‘Bricks and mortar are more like a lady’s hand!’\n\nTraddles broke into a rapturous laugh, and informed me that it was Sophy’s writing; that Sophy had vowed and declared he would need a copying-clerk soon, and she would be that clerk; that she had acquired this hand from a pattern; and that she could throw off--I forget how many folios an hour. Sophy was very much confused by my being told all this, and said that when ‘Tom’ was made a judge he wouldn’t be so ready to proclaim it. Which ‘Tom’ denied; averring that he should always be equally proud of it, under all circumstances.\n\n‘What a thoroughly good and charming wife she is, my dear Traddles!’ said I, when she had gone away, laughing.\n\n‘My dear Copperfield,’ returned Traddles, ‘she is, without any exception, the dearest girl! The way she manages this place; her punctuality, domestic knowledge, economy, and order; her cheerfulness, Copperfield!’\n\n‘Indeed, you have reason to commend her!’ I returned. ‘You are a happy fellow. I believe you make yourselves, and each other, two of the happiest people in the world.’\n\n‘I am sure we ARE two of the happiest people,’ returned Traddles. ‘I admit that, at all events. Bless my soul, when I see her getting up by candle-light on these dark mornings, busying herself in the day’s arrangements, going out to market before the clerks come into the Inn, caring for no weather, devising the most capital little dinners out of the plainest materials, making puddings and pies, keeping everything in its right place, always so neat and ornamental herself, sitting up at night with me if it’s ever so late, sweet-tempered and encouraging always, and all for me, I positively sometimes can’t believe it, Copperfield!’\n\nHe was tender of the very slippers she had been warming, as he put them on, and stretched his feet enjoyingly upon the fender.\n\n‘I positively sometimes can’t believe it,’ said Traddles. ‘Then our pleasures! Dear me, they are inexpensive, but they are quite wonderful! When we are at home here, of an evening, and shut the outer door, and draw those curtains--which she made--where could we be more snug? When it’s fine, and we go out for a walk in the evening, the streets abound in enjoyment for us. We look into the glittering windows of the jewellers’ shops; and I show Sophy which of the diamond-eyed serpents, coiled up on white satin rising grounds, I would give her if I could afford it; and Sophy shows me which of the gold watches that are capped and jewelled and engine-turned, and possessed of the horizontal lever-escape-movement, and all sorts of things, she would buy for me if she could afford it; and we pick out the spoons and forks, fish-slices, butter-knives, and sugar-tongs, we should both prefer if we could both afford it; and really we go away as if we had got them! Then, when we stroll into the squares, and great streets, and see a house to let, sometimes we look up at it, and say, how would THAT do, if I was made a judge? And we parcel it out--such a room for us, such rooms for the girls, and so forth; until we settle to our satisfaction that it would do, or it wouldn’t do, as the case may be. Sometimes, we go at half-price to the pit of the theatre--the very smell of which is cheap, in my opinion, at the money--and there we thoroughly enjoy the play: which Sophy believes every word of, and so do I. In walking home, perhaps we buy a little bit of something at a cook’s-shop, or a little lobster at the fishmongers, and bring it here, and make a splendid supper, chatting about what we have seen. Now, you know, Copperfield, if I was Lord Chancellor, we couldn’t do this!’\n\n‘You would do something, whatever you were, my dear Traddles,’ thought I, ‘that would be pleasant and amiable. And by the way,’ I said aloud, ‘I suppose you never draw any skeletons now?’\n\n‘Really,’ replied Traddles, laughing, and reddening, ‘I can’t wholly deny that I do, my dear Copperfield. For being in one of the back rows of the King’s Bench the other day, with a pen in my hand, the fancy came into my head to try how I had preserved that accomplishment. And I am afraid there’s a skeleton--in a wig--on the ledge of the desk.’\n\nAfter we had both laughed heartily, Traddles wound up by looking with a smile at the fire, and saying, in his forgiving way, ‘Old Creakle!’\n\n‘I have a letter from that old--Rascal here,’ said I. For I never was less disposed to forgive him the way he used to batter Traddles, than when I saw Traddles so ready to forgive him himself.\n\n‘From Creakle the schoolmaster?’ exclaimed Traddles. ‘No!’\n\n‘Among the persons who are attracted to me in my rising fame and fortune,’ said I, looking over my letters, ‘and who discover that they were always much attached to me, is the self-same Creakle. He is not a schoolmaster now, Traddles. He is retired. He is a Middlesex Magistrate.’\n\nI thought Traddles might be surprised to hear it, but he was not so at all.\n\n‘How do you suppose he comes to be a Middlesex Magistrate?’ said I.\n\n‘Oh dear me!’ replied Traddles, ‘it would be very difficult to answer that question. Perhaps he voted for somebody, or lent money to somebody, or bought something of somebody, or otherwise obliged somebody, or jobbed for somebody, who knew somebody who got the lieutenant of the county to nominate him for the commission.’\n\n‘On the commission he is, at any rate,’ said I. ‘And he writes to me here, that he will be glad to show me, in operation, the only true system of prison discipline; the only unchallengeable way of making sincere and lasting converts and penitents--which, you know, is by solitary confinement. What do you say?’\n\n‘To the system?’ inquired Traddles, looking grave.\n\n‘No. To my accepting the offer, and your going with me?’\n\n‘I don’t object,’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Then I’ll write to say so. You remember (to say nothing of our treatment) this same Creakle turning his son out of doors, I suppose, and the life he used to lead his wife and daughter?’\n\n‘Perfectly,’ said Traddles.\n\n‘Yet, if you’ll read his letter, you’ll find he is the tenderest of men to prisoners convicted of the whole calendar of felonies,’ said I; ‘though I can’t find that his tenderness extends to any other class of created beings.’\n\nTraddles shrugged his shoulders, and was not at all surprised. I had not expected him to be, and was not surprised myself; or my observation of similar practical satires would have been but scanty. We arranged the time of our visit, and I wrote accordingly to Mr. Creakle that evening.\n\nOn the appointed day--I think it was the next day, but no matter--Traddles and I repaired to the prison where Mr. Creakle was powerful. It was an immense and solid building, erected at a vast expense. I could not help thinking, as we approached the gate, what an uproar would have been made in the country, if any deluded man had proposed to spend one half the money it had cost, on the erection of an industrial school for the young, or a house of refuge for the deserving old.\n\nIn an office that might have been on the ground-floor of the Tower of Babel, it was so massively constructed, we were presented to our old schoolmaster; who was one of a group, composed of two or three of the busier sort of magistrates, and some visitors they had brought. He received me, like a man who had formed my mind in bygone years, and had always loved me tenderly. On my introducing Traddles, Mr. Creakle expressed, in like manner, but in an inferior degree, that he had always been Traddles’s guide, philosopher, and friend. Our venerable instructor was a great deal older, and not improved in appearance. His face was as fiery as ever; his eyes were as small, and rather deeper set. The scanty, wet-looking grey hair, by which I remembered him, was almost gone; and the thick veins in his bald head were none the more agreeable to look at.\n\nAfter some conversation among these gentlemen, from which I might have supposed that there was nothing in the world to be legitimately taken into account but the supreme comfort of prisoners, at any expense, and nothing on the wide earth to be done outside prison-doors, we began our inspection. It being then just dinner-time, we went, first into the great kitchen, where every prisoner’s dinner was in course of being set out separately (to be handed to him in his cell), with the regularity and precision of clock-work. I said aside, to Traddles, that I wondered whether it occurred to anybody, that there was a striking contrast between these plentiful repasts of choice quality, and the dinners, not to say of paupers, but of soldiers, sailors, labourers, the great bulk of the honest, working community; of whom not one man in five hundred ever dined half so well. But I learned that the ‘system’ required high living; and, in short, to dispose of the system, once for all, I found that on that head and on all others, ‘the system’ put an end to all doubts, and disposed of all anomalies. Nobody appeared to have the least idea that there was any other system, but THE system, to be considered.\n\nAs we were going through some of the magnificent passages, I inquired of Mr. Creakle and his friends what were supposed to be the main advantages of this all-governing and universally over-riding system? I found them to be the perfect isolation of prisoners--so that no one man in confinement there, knew anything about another; and the reduction of prisoners to a wholesome state of mind, leading to sincere contrition and repentance.\n\nNow, it struck me, when we began to visit individuals in their cells, and to traverse the passages in which those cells were, and to have the manner of the going to chapel and so forth, explained to us, that there was a strong probability of the prisoners knowing a good deal about each other, and of their carrying on a pretty complete system of intercourse. This, at the time I write, has been proved, I believe, to be the case; but, as it would have been flat blasphemy against the system to have hinted such a doubt then, I looked out for the penitence as diligently as I could.\n\nAnd here again, I had great misgivings. I found as prevalent a fashion in the form of the penitence, as I had left outside in the forms of the coats and waistcoats in the windows of the tailors’ shops. I found a vast amount of profession, varying very little in character: varying very little (which I thought exceedingly suspicious), even in words. I found a great many foxes, disparaging whole vineyards of inaccessible grapes; but I found very few foxes whom I would have trusted within reach of a bunch. Above all, I found that the most professing men were the greatest objects of interest; and that their conceit, their vanity, their want of excitement, and their love of deception (which many of them possessed to an almost incredible extent, as their histories showed), all prompted to these professions, and were all gratified by them.\n\nHowever, I heard so repeatedly, in the course of our goings to and fro, of a certain Number Twenty Seven, who was the Favourite, and who really appeared to be a Model Prisoner, that I resolved to suspend my judgement until I should see Twenty Seven. Twenty Eight, I understood, was also a bright particular star; but it was his misfortune to have his glory a little dimmed by the extraordinary lustre of Twenty Seven. I heard so much of Twenty Seven, of his pious admonitions to everybody around him, and of the beautiful letters he constantly wrote to his mother (whom he seemed to consider in a very bad way), that I became quite impatient to see him.\n\nI had to restrain my impatience for some time, on account of Twenty Seven being reserved for a concluding effect. But, at last, we came to the door of his cell; and Mr. Creakle, looking through a little hole in it, reported to us, in a state of the greatest admiration, that he was reading a Hymn Book.\n\nThere was such a rush of heads immediately, to see Number Twenty Seven reading his Hymn Book, that the little hole was blocked up, six or seven heads deep. To remedy this inconvenience, and give us an opportunity of conversing with Twenty Seven in all his purity, Mr. Creakle directed the door of the cell to be unlocked, and Twenty Seven to be invited out into the passage. This was done; and whom should Traddles and I then behold, to our amazement, in this converted Number Twenty Seven, but Uriah Heep!\n\nHe knew us directly; and said, as he came out--with the old writhe,--\n\n‘How do you do, Mr. Copperfield? How do you do, Mr. Traddles?’\n\nThis recognition caused a general admiration in the party. I rather thought that everyone was struck by his not being proud, and taking notice of us.\n\n‘Well, Twenty Seven,’ said Mr. Creakle, mournfully admiring him. ‘How do you find yourself today?’\n\n‘I am very umble, sir!’ replied Uriah Heep.\n\n‘You are always so, Twenty Seven,’ said Mr. Creakle.\n\nHere, another gentleman asked, with extreme anxiety: ‘Are you quite comfortable?’\n\n‘Yes, I thank you, sir!’ said Uriah Heep, looking in that direction. ‘Far more comfortable here, than ever I was outside. I see my follies, now, sir. That’s what makes me comfortable.’\n\nSeveral gentlemen were much affected; and a third questioner, forcing himself to the front, inquired with extreme feeling: ‘How do you find the beef?’\n\n‘Thank you, sir,’ replied Uriah, glancing in the new direction of this voice, ‘it was tougher yesterday than I could wish; but it’s my duty to bear. I have committed follies, gentlemen,’ said Uriah, looking round with a meek smile, ‘and I ought to bear the consequences without repining.’ A murmur, partly of gratification at Twenty Seven’s celestial state of mind, and partly of indignation against the Contractor who had given him any cause of complaint (a note of which was immediately made by Mr. Creakle), having subsided, Twenty Seven stood in the midst of us, as if he felt himself the principal object of merit in a highly meritorious museum. That we, the neophytes, might have an excess of light shining upon us all at once, orders were given to let out Twenty Eight.\n\nI had been so much astonished already, that I only felt a kind of resigned wonder when Mr. Littimer walked forth, reading a good book!\n\n‘Twenty Eight,’ said a gentleman in spectacles, who had not yet spoken, ‘you complained last week, my good fellow, of the cocoa. How has it been since?’\n\n‘I thank you, sir,’ said Mr. Littimer, ‘it has been better made. If I might take the liberty of saying so, sir, I don’t think the milk which is boiled with it is quite genuine; but I am aware, sir, that there is a great adulteration of milk, in London, and that the article in a pure state is difficult to be obtained.’\n\nIt appeared to me that the gentleman in spectacles backed his Twenty Eight against Mr. Creakle’s Twenty Seven, for each of them took his own man in hand.\n\n‘What is your state of mind, Twenty Eight?’ said the questioner in spectacles.\n\n‘I thank you, sir,’ returned Mr. Littimer; ‘I see my follies now, sir. I am a good deal troubled when I think of the sins of my former companions, sir; but I trust they may find forgiveness.’\n\n‘You are quite happy yourself?’ said the questioner, nodding encouragement.\n\n‘I am much obliged to you, sir,’ returned Mr. Littimer. ‘Perfectly so.’\n\n‘Is there anything at all on your mind now?’ said the questioner. ‘If so, mention it, Twenty Eight.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr. Littimer, without looking up, ‘if my eyes have not deceived me, there is a gentleman present who was acquainted with me in my former life. It may be profitable to that gentleman to know, sir, that I attribute my past follies, entirely to having lived a thoughtless life in the service of young men; and to having allowed myself to be led by them into weaknesses, which I had not the strength to resist. I hope that gentleman will take warning, sir, and will not be offended at my freedom. It is for his good. I am conscious of my own past follies. I hope he may repent of all the wickedness and sin to which he has been a party.’\n\nI observed that several gentlemen were shading their eyes, each with one hand, as if they had just come into church.\n\n‘This does you credit, Twenty Eight,’ returned the questioner. ‘I should have expected it of you. Is there anything else?’\n\n‘Sir,’ returned Mr. Littimer, slightly lifting up his eyebrows, but not his eyes, ‘there was a young woman who fell into dissolute courses, that I endeavoured to save, sir, but could not rescue. I beg that gentleman, if he has it in his power, to inform that young woman from me that I forgive her her bad conduct towards myself, and that I call her to repentance--if he will be so good.’\n\n‘I have no doubt, Twenty Eight,’ returned the questioner, ‘that the gentleman you refer to feels very strongly--as we all must--what you have so properly said. We will not detain you.’\n\n‘I thank you, sir,’ said Mr. Littimer. ‘Gentlemen, I wish you a good day, and hoping you and your families will also see your wickedness, and amend!’\n\nWith this, Number Twenty Eight retired, after a glance between him and Uriah; as if they were not altogether unknown to each other, through some medium of communication; and a murmur went round the group, as his door shut upon him, that he was a most respectable man, and a beautiful case.\n\n‘Now, Twenty Seven,’ said Mr. Creakle, entering on a clear stage with his man, ‘is there anything that anyone can do for you? If so, mention it.’\n\n‘I would umbly ask, sir,’ returned Uriah, with a jerk of his malevolent head, ‘for leave to write again to mother.’\n\n‘It shall certainly be granted,’ said Mr. Creakle.\n\n‘Thank you, sir! I am anxious about mother. I am afraid she ain’t safe.’\n\nSomebody incautiously asked, what from? But there was a scandalized whisper of ‘Hush!’\n\n‘Immortally safe, sir,’ returned Uriah, writhing in the direction of the voice. ‘I should wish mother to be got into my state. I never should have been got into my present state if I hadn’t come here. I wish mother had come here. It would be better for everybody, if they got took up, and was brought here.’\n\nThis sentiment gave unbounded satisfaction--greater satisfaction, I think, than anything that had passed yet.\n\n‘Before I come here,’ said Uriah, stealing a look at us, as if he would have blighted the outer world to which we belonged, if he could, ‘I was given to follies; but now I am sensible of my follies. There’s a deal of sin outside. There’s a deal of sin in mother. There’s nothing but sin everywhere--except here.’\n\n‘You are quite changed?’ said Mr. Creakle.\n\n‘Oh dear, yes, sir!’ cried this hopeful penitent.\n\n‘You wouldn’t relapse, if you were going out?’ asked somebody else.\n\n‘Oh de-ar no, sir!’\n\n‘Well!’ said Mr. Creakle, ‘this is very gratifying. You have addressed Mr. Copperfield, Twenty Seven. Do you wish to say anything further to him?’\n\n‘You knew me, a long time before I came here and was changed, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Uriah, looking at me; and a more villainous look I never saw, even on his visage. ‘You knew me when, in spite of my follies, I was umble among them that was proud, and meek among them that was violent--you was violent to me yourself, Mr. Copperfield. Once, you struck me a blow in the face, you know.’\n\nGeneral commiseration. Several indignant glances directed at me.\n\n‘But I forgive you, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Uriah, making his forgiving nature the subject of a most impious and awful parallel, which I shall not record. ‘I forgive everybody. It would ill become me to bear malice. I freely forgive you, and I hope you’ll curb your passions in future. I hope Mr. W. will repent, and Miss W., and all of that sinful lot. You’ve been visited with affliction, and I hope it may do you good; but you’d better have come here. Mr. W. had better have come here, and Miss W. too. The best wish I could give you, Mr. Copperfield, and give all of you gentlemen, is, that you could be took up and brought here. When I think of my past follies, and my present state, I am sure it would be best for you. I pity all who ain’t brought here!’\n\nHe sneaked back into his cell, amidst a little chorus of approbation; and both Traddles and I experienced a great relief when he was locked in.\n\nIt was a characteristic feature in this repentance, that I was fain to ask what these two men had done, to be there at all. That appeared to be the last thing about which they had anything to say. I addressed myself to one of the two warders, who, I suspected from certain latent indications in their faces, knew pretty well what all this stir was worth.\n\n‘Do you know,’ said I, as we walked along the passage, ‘what felony was Number Twenty Seven’s last “folly”?’\n\nThe answer was that it was a Bank case.\n\n‘A fraud on the Bank of England?’ I asked. ‘Yes, sir. Fraud, forgery, and conspiracy. He and some others. He set the others on. It was a deep plot for a large sum. Sentence, transportation for life. Twenty Seven was the knowingest bird of the lot, and had very nearly kept himself safe; but not quite. The Bank was just able to put salt upon his tail--and only just.’\n\n‘Do you know Twenty Eight’s offence?’\n\n‘Twenty Eight,’ returned my informant, speaking throughout in a low tone, and looking over his shoulder as we walked along the passage, to guard himself from being overheard, in such an unlawful reference to these Immaculates, by Creakle and the rest; ‘Twenty Eight (also transportation) got a place, and robbed a young master of a matter of two hundred and fifty pounds in money and valuables, the night before they were going abroad. I particularly recollect his case, from his being took by a dwarf.’\n\n‘A what?’\n\n‘A little woman. I have forgot her name?’\n\n‘Not Mowcher?’\n\n‘That’s it! He had eluded pursuit, and was going to America in a flaxen wig, and whiskers, and such a complete disguise as never you see in all your born days; when the little woman, being in Southampton, met him walking along the street--picked him out with her sharp eye in a moment--ran betwixt his legs to upset him--and held on to him like grim Death.’\n\n‘Excellent Miss Mowcher!’ cried I.\n\n‘You’d have said so, if you had seen her, standing on a chair in the witness-box at the trial, as I did,’ said my friend. ‘He cut her face right open, and pounded her in the most brutal manner, when she took him; but she never loosed her hold till he was locked up. She held so tight to him, in fact, that the officers were obliged to take ‘em both together. She gave her evidence in the gamest way, and was highly complimented by the Bench, and cheered right home to her lodgings. She said in Court that she’d have took him single-handed (on account of what she knew concerning him), if he had been Samson. And it’s my belief she would!’\n\nIt was mine too, and I highly respected Miss Mowcher for it.\n\nWe had now seen all there was to see. It would have been in vain to represent to such a man as the Worshipful Mr. Creakle, that Twenty Seven and Twenty Eight were perfectly consistent and unchanged; that exactly what they were then, they had always been; that the hypocritical knaves were just the subjects to make that sort of profession in such a place; that they knew its market-value at least as well as we did, in the immediate service it would do them when they were expatriated; in a word, that it was a rotten, hollow, painfully suggestive piece of business altogether. We left them to their system and themselves, and went home wondering.\n\n‘Perhaps it’s a good thing, Traddles,’ said I, ‘to have an unsound Hobby ridden hard; for it’s the sooner ridden to death.’\n\n‘I hope so,’ replied Traddles.\n\n# CHAPTER 62. A LIGHT SHINES ON MY WAY\n\nThe year came round to Christmas-time, and I had been at home above two months. I had seen Agnes frequently. However loud the general voice might be in giving me encouragement, and however fervent the emotions and endeavours to which it roused me, I heard her lightest word of praise as I heard nothing else.\n\nAt least once a week, and sometimes oftener, I rode over there, and passed the evening. I usually rode back at night; for the old unhappy sense was always hovering about me now--most sorrowfully when I left her--and I was glad to be up and out, rather than wandering over the past in weary wakefulness or miserable dreams. I wore away the longest part of many wild sad nights, in those rides; reviving, as I went, the thoughts that had occupied me in my long absence.\n\nOr, if I were to say rather that I listened to the echoes of those thoughts, I should better express the truth. They spoke to me from afar off. I had put them at a distance, and accepted my inevitable place. When I read to Agnes what I wrote; when I saw her listening face; moved her to smiles or tears; and heard her cordial voice so earnest on the shadowy events of that imaginative world in which I lived; I thought what a fate mine might have been--but only thought so, as I had thought after I was married to Dora, what I could have wished my wife to be.\n\nMy duty to Agnes, who loved me with a love, which, if I disquieted, I wronged most selfishly and poorly, and could never restore; my matured assurance that I, who had worked out my own destiny, and won what I had impetuously set my heart on, had no right to murmur, and must bear; comprised what I felt and what I had learned. But I loved her: and now it even became some consolation to me, vaguely to conceive a distant day when I might blamelessly avow it; when all this should be over; when I could say ‘Agnes, so it was when I came home; and now I am old, and I never have loved since!’\n\nShe did not once show me any change in herself. What she always had been to me, she still was; wholly unaltered.\n\nBetween my aunt and me there had been something, in this connexion, since the night of my return, which I cannot call a restraint, or an avoidance of the subject, so much as an implied understanding that we thought of it together, but did not shape our thoughts into words. When, according to our old custom, we sat before the fire at night, we often fell into this train; as naturally, and as consciously to each other, as if we had unreservedly said so. But we preserved an unbroken silence. I believed that she had read, or partly read, my thoughts that night; and that she fully comprehended why I gave mine no more distinct expression.\n\nThis Christmas-time being come, and Agnes having reposed no new confidence in me, a doubt that had several times arisen in my mind--whether she could have that perception of the true state of my breast, which restrained her with the apprehension of giving me pain--began to oppress me heavily. If that were so, my sacrifice was nothing; my plainest obligation to her unfulfilled; and every poor action I had shrunk from, I was hourly doing. I resolved to set this right beyond all doubt;--if such a barrier were between us, to break it down at once with a determined hand.\n\nIt was--what lasting reason have I to remember it!--a cold, harsh, winter day. There had been snow, some hours before; and it lay, not deep, but hard-frozen on the ground. Out at sea, beyond my window, the wind blew ruggedly from the north. I had been thinking of it, sweeping over those mountain wastes of snow in Switzerland, then inaccessible to any human foot; and had been speculating which was the lonelier, those solitary regions, or a deserted ocean.\n\n‘Riding today, Trot?’ said my aunt, putting her head in at the door.\n\n‘Yes,’ said I, ‘I am going over to Canterbury. It’s a good day for a ride.’\n\n‘I hope your horse may think so too,’ said my aunt; ‘but at present he is holding down his head and his ears, standing before the door there, as if he thought his stable preferable.’\n\nMy aunt, I may observe, allowed my horse on the forbidden ground, but had not at all relented towards the donkeys.\n\n‘He will be fresh enough, presently!’ said I.\n\n‘The ride will do his master good, at all events,’ observed my aunt, glancing at the papers on my table. ‘Ah, child, you pass a good many hours here! I never thought, when I used to read books, what work it was to write them.’\n\n‘It’s work enough to read them, sometimes,’ I returned. ‘As to the writing, it has its own charms, aunt.’\n\n‘Ah! I see!’ said my aunt. ‘Ambition, love of approbation, sympathy, and much more, I suppose? Well: go along with you!’\n\n‘Do you know anything more,’ said I, standing composedly before her--she had patted me on the shoulder, and sat down in my chair--‘of that attachment of Agnes?’\n\nShe looked up in my face a little while, before replying:\n\n‘I think I do, Trot.’\n\n‘Are you confirmed in your impression?’ I inquired.\n\n‘I think I am, Trot.’\n\nShe looked so steadfastly at me: with a kind of doubt, or pity, or suspense in her affection: that I summoned the stronger determination to show her a perfectly cheerful face.\n\n‘And what is more, Trot--’ said my aunt.\n\n‘Yes!’\n\n‘I think Agnes is going to be married.’\n\n‘God bless her!’ said I, cheerfully.\n\n‘God bless her!’ said my aunt, ‘and her husband too!’\n\nI echoed it, parted from my aunt, and went lightly downstairs, mounted, and rode away. There was greater reason than before to do what I had resolved to do.\n\nHow well I recollect the wintry ride! The frozen particles of ice, brushed from the blades of grass by the wind, and borne across my face; the hard clatter of the horse’s hoofs, beating a tune upon the ground; the stiff-tilled soil; the snowdrift, lightly eddying in the chalk-pit as the breeze ruffled it; the smoking team with the waggon of old hay, stopping to breathe on the hill-top, and shaking their bells musically; the whitened slopes and sweeps of Down-land lying against the dark sky, as if they were drawn on a huge slate!\n\nI found Agnes alone. The little girls had gone to their own homes now, and she was alone by the fire, reading. She put down her book on seeing me come in; and having welcomed me as usual, took her work-basket and sat in one of the old-fashioned windows.\n\nI sat beside her on the window-seat, and we talked of what I was doing, and when it would be done, and of the progress I had made since my last visit. Agnes was very cheerful; and laughingly predicted that I should soon become too famous to be talked to, on such subjects.\n\n‘So I make the most of the present time, you see,’ said Agnes, ‘and talk to you while I may.’\n\nAs I looked at her beautiful face, observant of her work, she raised her mild clear eyes, and saw that I was looking at her.\n\n‘You are thoughtful today, Trotwood!’\n\n‘Agnes, shall I tell you what about? I came to tell you.’\n\nShe put aside her work, as she was used to do when we were seriously discussing anything; and gave me her whole attention.\n\n‘My dear Agnes, do you doubt my being true to you?’\n\n‘No!’ she answered, with a look of astonishment.\n\n‘Do you doubt my being what I always have been to you?’\n\n‘No!’ she answered, as before.\n\n‘Do you remember that I tried to tell you, when I came home, what a debt of gratitude I owed you, dearest Agnes, and how fervently I felt towards you?’\n\n‘I remember it,’ she said, gently, ‘very well.’\n\n‘You have a secret,’ said I. ‘Let me share it, Agnes.’\n\nShe cast down her eyes, and trembled.\n\n‘I could hardly fail to know, even if I had not heard--but from other lips than yours, Agnes, which seems strange--that there is someone upon whom you have bestowed the treasure of your love. Do not shut me out of what concerns your happiness so nearly! If you can trust me, as you say you can, and as I know you may, let me be your friend, your brother, in this matter, of all others!’\n\nWith an appealing, almost a reproachful, glance, she rose from the window; and hurrying across the room as if without knowing where, put her hands before her face, and burst into such tears as smote me to the heart.\n\nAnd yet they awakened something in me, bringing promise to my heart. Without my knowing why, these tears allied themselves with the quietly sad smile which was so fixed in my remembrance, and shook me more with hope than fear or sorrow.\n\n‘Agnes! Sister! Dearest! What have I done?’\n\n‘Let me go away, Trotwood. I am not well. I am not myself. I will speak to you by and by--another time. I will write to you. Don’t speak to me now. Don’t! don’t!’\n\nI sought to recollect what she had said, when I had spoken to her on that former night, of her affection needing no return. It seemed a very world that I must search through in a moment. ‘Agnes, I cannot bear to see you so, and think that I have been the cause. My dearest girl, dearer to me than anything in life, if you are unhappy, let me share your unhappiness. If you are in need of help or counsel, let me try to give it to you. If you have indeed a burden on your heart, let me try to lighten it. For whom do I live now, Agnes, if it is not for you!’\n\n‘Oh, spare me! I am not myself! Another time!’ was all I could distinguish.\n\nWas it a selfish error that was leading me away? Or, having once a clue to hope, was there something opening to me that I had not dared to think of?\n\n‘I must say more. I cannot let you leave me so! For Heaven’s sake, Agnes, let us not mistake each other after all these years, and all that has come and gone with them! I must speak plainly. If you have any lingering thought that I could envy the happiness you will confer; that I could not resign you to a dearer protector, of your own choosing; that I could not, from my removed place, be a contented witness of your joy; dismiss it, for I don’t deserve it! I have not suffered quite in vain. You have not taught me quite in vain. There is no alloy of self in what I feel for you.’\n\nShe was quiet now. In a little time, she turned her pale face towards me, and said in a low voice, broken here and there, but very clear:\n\n‘I owe it to your pure friendship for me, Trotwood--which, indeed, I do not doubt--to tell you, you are mistaken. I can do no more. If I have sometimes, in the course of years, wanted help and counsel, they have come to me. If I have sometimes been unhappy, the feeling has passed away. If I have ever had a burden on my heart, it has been lightened for me. If I have any secret, it is--no new one; and is--not what you suppose. I cannot reveal it, or divide it. It has long been mine, and must remain mine.’\n\n‘Agnes! Stay! A moment!’\n\nShe was going away, but I detained her. I clasped my arm about her waist. ‘In the course of years!’ ‘It is not a new one!’ New thoughts and hopes were whirling through my mind, and all the colours of my life were changing.\n\n‘Dearest Agnes! Whom I so respect and honour--whom I so devotedly love! When I came here today, I thought that nothing could have wrested this confession from me. I thought I could have kept it in my bosom all our lives, till we were old. But, Agnes, if I have indeed any new-born hope that I may ever call you something more than Sister, widely different from Sister!--’\n\nHer tears fell fast; but they were not like those she had lately shed, and I saw my hope brighten in them.\n\n‘Agnes! Ever my guide, and best support! If you had been more mindful of yourself, and less of me, when we grew up here together, I think my heedless fancy never would have wandered from you. But you were so much better than I, so necessary to me in every boyish hope and disappointment, that to have you to confide in, and rely upon in everything, became a second nature, supplanting for the time the first and greater one of loving you as I do!’\n\nStill weeping, but not sadly--joyfully! And clasped in my arms as she had never been, as I had thought she never was to be!\n\n‘When I loved Dora--fondly, Agnes, as you know--’\n\n‘Yes!’ she cried, earnestly. ‘I am glad to know it!’\n\n‘When I loved her--even then, my love would have been incomplete, without your sympathy. I had it, and it was perfected. And when I lost her, Agnes, what should I have been without you, still!’\n\nCloser in my arms, nearer to my heart, her trembling hand upon my shoulder, her sweet eyes shining through her tears, on mine!\n\n‘I went away, dear Agnes, loving you. I stayed away, loving you. I returned home, loving you!’\n\nAnd now, I tried to tell her of the struggle I had had, and the conclusion I had come to. I tried to lay my mind before her, truly, and entirely. I tried to show her how I had hoped I had come into the better knowledge of myself and of her; how I had resigned myself to what that better knowledge brought; and how I had come there, even that day, in my fidelity to this. If she did so love me (I said) that she could take me for her husband, she could do so, on no deserving of mine, except upon the truth of my love for her, and the trouble in which it had ripened to be what it was; and hence it was that I revealed it. And O, Agnes, even out of thy true eyes, in that same time, the spirit of my child-wife looked upon me, saying it was well; and winning me, through thee, to tenderest recollections of the Blossom that had withered in its bloom!\n\n‘I am so blest, Trotwood--my heart is so overcharged--but there is one thing I must say.’\n‘Dearest, what?’\n\nShe laid her gentle hands upon my shoulders, and looked calmly in my face.\n\n‘Do you know, yet, what it is?’\n\n‘I am afraid to speculate on what it is. Tell me, my dear.’\n\n‘I have loved you all my life!’\n\nO, we were happy, we were happy! Our tears were not for the trials (hers so much the greater) through which we had come to be thus, but for the rapture of being thus, never to be divided more!\n\nWe walked, that winter evening, in the fields together; and the blessed calm within us seemed to be partaken by the frosty air. The early stars began to shine while we were lingering on, and looking up to them, we thanked our GOD for having guided us to this tranquillity.\n\nWe stood together in the same old-fashioned window at night, when the moon was shining; Agnes with her quiet eyes raised up to it; I following her glance. Long miles of road then opened out before my mind; and, toiling on, I saw a ragged way-worn boy, forsaken and neglected, who should come to call even the heart now beating against mine, his own.\n\nIt was nearly dinner-time next day when we appeared before my aunt. She was up in my study, Peggotty said: which it was her pride to keep in readiness and order for me. We found her, in her spectacles, sitting by the fire.\n\n‘Goodness me!’ said my aunt, peering through the dusk, ‘who’s this you’re bringing home?’\n\n‘Agnes,’ said I.\n\nAs we had arranged to say nothing at first, my aunt was not a little discomfited. She darted a hopeful glance at me, when I said ‘Agnes’; but seeing that I looked as usual, she took off her spectacles in despair, and rubbed her nose with them.\n\nShe greeted Agnes heartily, nevertheless; and we were soon in the lighted parlour downstairs, at dinner. My aunt put on her spectacles twice or thrice, to take another look at me, but as often took them off again, disappointed, and rubbed her nose with them. Much to the discomfiture of Mr. Dick, who knew this to be a bad symptom.\n\n‘By the by, aunt,’ said I, after dinner; ‘I have been speaking to Agnes about what you told me.’\n\n‘Then, Trot,’ said my aunt, turning scarlet, ‘you did wrong, and broke your promise.’\n\n‘You are not angry, aunt, I trust? I am sure you won’t be, when you learn that Agnes is not unhappy in any attachment.’\n\n‘Stuff and nonsense!’ said my aunt.\n\nAs my aunt appeared to be annoyed, I thought the best way was to cut her annoyance short. I took Agnes in my arm to the back of her chair, and we both leaned over her. My aunt, with one clap of her hands, and one look through her spectacles, immediately went into hysterics, for the first and only time in all my knowledge of her.\n\nThe hysterics called up Peggotty. The moment my aunt was restored, she flew at Peggotty, and calling her a silly old creature, hugged her with all her might. After that, she hugged Mr. Dick (who was highly honoured, but a good deal surprised); and after that, told them why. Then, we were all happy together.\n\nI could not discover whether my aunt, in her last short conversation with me, had fallen on a pious fraud, or had really mistaken the state of my mind. It was quite enough, she said, that she had told me Agnes was going to be married; and that I now knew better than anyone how true it was.\n\nWe were married within a fortnight. Traddles and Sophy, and Doctor and Mrs. Strong, were the only guests at our quiet wedding. We left them full of joy; and drove away together. Clasped in my embrace, I held the source of every worthy aspiration I had ever had; the centre of myself, the circle of my life, my own, my wife; my love of whom was founded on a rock!\n\n‘Dearest husband!’ said Agnes. ‘Now that I may call you by that name, I have one thing more to tell you.’\n\n‘Let me hear it, love.’\n\n‘It grows out of the night when Dora died. She sent you for me.’\n\n‘She did.’\n\n‘She told me that she left me something. Can you think what it was?’\n\nI believed I could. I drew the wife who had so long loved me, closer to my side.\n\n‘She told me that she made a last request to me, and left me a last charge.’\n\n‘And it was--’\n\n‘That only I would occupy this vacant place.’\n\nAnd Agnes laid her head upon my breast, and wept; and I wept with her, though we were so happy.\n\n# CHAPTER 63. A VISITOR\n\nWhat I have purposed to record is nearly finished; but there is yet an incident conspicuous in my memory, on which it often rests with delight, and without which one thread in the web I have spun would have a ravelled end.\n\nI had advanced in fame and fortune, my domestic joy was perfect, I had been married ten happy years. Agnes and I were sitting by the fire, in our house in London, one night in spring, and three of our children were playing in the room, when I was told that a stranger wished to see me.\n\nHe had been asked if he came on business, and had answered No; he had come for the pleasure of seeing me, and had come a long way. He was an old man, my servant said, and looked like a farmer.\n\nAs this sounded mysterious to the children, and moreover was like the beginning of a favourite story Agnes used to tell them, introductory to the arrival of a wicked old Fairy in a cloak who hated everybody, it produced some commotion. One of our boys laid his head in his mother’s lap to be out of harm’s way, and little Agnes (our eldest child) left her doll in a chair to represent her, and thrust out her little heap of golden curls from between the window-curtains, to see what happened next.\n\n‘Let him come in here!’ said I.\n\nThere soon appeared, pausing in the dark doorway as he entered, a hale, grey-haired old man. Little Agnes, attracted by his looks, had run to bring him in, and I had not yet clearly seen his face, when my wife, starting up, cried out to me, in a pleased and agitated voice, that it was Mr. Peggotty!\n\nIt WAS Mr. Peggotty. An old man now, but in a ruddy, hearty, strong old age. When our first emotion was over, and he sat before the fire with the children on his knees, and the blaze shining on his face, he looked, to me, as vigorous and robust, withal as handsome, an old man, as ever I had seen.\n\n‘Mas’r Davy,’ said he. And the old name in the old tone fell so naturally on my ear! ‘Mas’r Davy, ‘tis a joyful hour as I see you, once more, ‘long with your own trew wife!’\n\n‘A joyful hour indeed, old friend!’ cried I.\n\n‘And these heer pretty ones,’ said Mr. Peggotty. ‘To look at these heer flowers! Why, Mas’r Davy, you was but the heighth of the littlest of these, when I first see you! When Em’ly warn’t no bigger, and our poor lad were BUT a lad!’\n\n‘Time has changed me more than it has changed you since then,’ said I. ‘But let these dear rogues go to bed; and as no house in England but this must hold you, tell me where to send for your luggage (is the old black bag among it, that went so far, I wonder!), and then, over a glass of Yarmouth grog, we will have the tidings of ten years!’\n\n‘Are you alone?’ asked Agnes.\n\n‘Yes, ma’am,’ he said, kissing her hand, ‘quite alone.’\n\nWe sat him between us, not knowing how to give him welcome enough; and as I began to listen to his old familiar voice, I could have fancied he was still pursuing his long journey in search of his darling niece.\n\n‘It’s a mort of water,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘fur to come across, and on’y stay a matter of fower weeks. But water (‘specially when ‘tis salt) comes nat’ral to me; and friends is dear, and I am heer. --Which is verse,’ said Mr. Peggotty, surprised to find it out, ‘though I hadn’t such intentions.’\n\n‘Are you going back those many thousand miles, so soon?’ asked Agnes.\n\n‘Yes, ma’am,’ he returned. ‘I giv the promise to Em’ly, afore I come away. You see, I doen’t grow younger as the years comes round, and if I hadn’t sailed as ‘twas, most like I shouldn’t never have done ‘t. And it’s allus been on my mind, as I must come and see Mas’r Davy and your own sweet blooming self, in your wedded happiness, afore I got to be too old.’\n\nHe looked at us, as if he could never feast his eyes on us sufficiently. Agnes laughingly put back some scattered locks of his grey hair, that he might see us better.\n\n‘And now tell us,’ said I, ‘everything relating to your fortunes.’\n\n‘Our fortuns, Mas’r Davy,’ he rejoined, ‘is soon told. We haven’t fared nohows, but fared to thrive. We’ve allus thrived. We’ve worked as we ought to ‘t, and maybe we lived a leetle hard at first or so, but we have allus thrived. What with sheep-farming, and what with stock-farming, and what with one thing and what with t’other, we are as well to do, as well could be. Theer’s been kiender a blessing fell upon us,’ said Mr. Peggotty, reverentially inclining his head, ‘and we’ve done nowt but prosper. That is, in the long run. If not yesterday, why then today. If not today, why then tomorrow.’\n\n‘And Emily?’ said Agnes and I, both together.\n\n‘Em’ly,’ said he, ‘arter you left her, ma’am--and I never heerd her saying of her prayers at night, t’other side the canvas screen, when we was settled in the Bush, but what I heerd your name--and arter she and me lost sight of Mas’r Davy, that theer shining sundown--was that low, at first, that, if she had know’d then what Mas’r Davy kep from us so kind and thowtful, ‘tis my opinion she’d have drooped away. But theer was some poor folks aboard as had illness among ‘em, and she took care of them; and theer was the children in our company, and she took care of them; and so she got to be busy, and to be doing good, and that helped her.’\n\n‘When did she first hear of it?’ I asked.\n\n‘I kep it from her arter I heerd on ‘t,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘going on nigh a year. We was living then in a solitary place, but among the beautifullest trees, and with the roses a-covering our Beein to the roof. Theer come along one day, when I was out a-working on the land, a traveller from our own Norfolk or Suffolk in England (I doen’t rightly mind which), and of course we took him in, and giv him to eat and drink, and made him welcome. We all do that, all the colony over. He’d got an old newspaper with him, and some other account in print of the storm. That’s how she know’d it. When I came home at night, I found she know’d it.’\n\nHe dropped his voice as he said these words, and the gravity I so well remembered overspread his face.\n\n‘Did it change her much?’ we asked.\n\n‘Aye, for a good long time,’ he said, shaking his head; ‘if not to this present hour. But I think the solitoode done her good. And she had a deal to mind in the way of poultry and the like, and minded of it, and come through. I wonder,’ he said thoughtfully, ‘if you could see my Em’ly now, Mas’r Davy, whether you’d know her!’\n\n‘Is she so altered?’ I inquired.\n\n‘I doen’t know. I see her ev’ry day, and doen’t know; But, odd-times, I have thowt so. A slight figure,’ said Mr. Peggotty, looking at the fire, ‘kiender worn; soft, sorrowful, blue eyes; a delicate face; a pritty head, leaning a little down; a quiet voice and way--timid a’most. That’s Em’ly!’\n\nWe silently observed him as he sat, still looking at the fire.\n\n‘Some thinks,’ he said, ‘as her affection was ill-bestowed; some, as her marriage was broken off by death. No one knows how ‘tis. She might have married well, a mort of times, “but, uncle,” she says to me, “that’s gone for ever.” Cheerful along with me; retired when others is by; fond of going any distance fur to teach a child, or fur to tend a sick person, or fur to do some kindness tow’rds a young girl’s wedding (and she’s done a many, but has never seen one); fondly loving of her uncle; patient; liked by young and old; sowt out by all that has any trouble. That’s Em’ly!’\n\nHe drew his hand across his face, and with a half-suppressed sigh looked up from the fire.\n\n‘Is Martha with you yet?’ I asked.\n\n‘Martha,’ he replied, ‘got married, Mas’r Davy, in the second year. A young man, a farm-labourer, as come by us on his way to market with his mas’r’s drays--a journey of over five hundred mile, theer and back--made offers fur to take her fur his wife (wives is very scarce theer), and then to set up fur their two selves in the Bush. She spoke to me fur to tell him her trew story. I did. They was married, and they live fower hundred mile away from any voices but their own and the singing birds.’\n\n‘Mrs. Gummidge?’ I suggested.\n\nIt was a pleasant key to touch, for Mr. Peggotty suddenly burst into a roar of laughter, and rubbed his hands up and down his legs, as he had been accustomed to do when he enjoyed himself in the long-shipwrecked boat.\n\n‘Would you believe it!’ he said. ‘Why, someun even made offer fur to marry her! If a ship’s cook that was turning settler, Mas’r Davy, didn’t make offers fur to marry Missis Gummidge, I’m Gormed--and I can’t say no fairer than that!’\n\nI never saw Agnes laugh so. This sudden ecstasy on the part of Mr. Peggotty was so delightful to her, that she could not leave off laughing; and the more she laughed the more she made me laugh, and the greater Mr. Peggotty’s ecstasy became, and the more he rubbed his legs.\n\n‘And what did Mrs. Gummidge say?’ I asked, when I was grave enough.\n\n‘If you’ll believe me,’ returned Mr. Peggotty, ‘Missis Gummidge, ‘stead of saying “thank you, I’m much obleeged to you, I ain’t a-going fur to change my condition at my time of life,” up’d with a bucket as was standing by, and laid it over that theer ship’s cook’s head ‘till he sung out fur help, and I went in and reskied of him.’\n\nMr. Peggotty burst into a great roar of laughter, and Agnes and I both kept him company.\n\n‘But I must say this, for the good creetur,’ he resumed, wiping his face, when we were quite exhausted; ‘she has been all she said she’d be to us, and more. She’s the willingest, the trewest, the honestest-helping woman, Mas’r Davy, as ever draw’d the breath of life. I have never know’d her to be lone and lorn, for a single minute, not even when the colony was all afore us, and we was new to it. And thinking of the old ‘un is a thing she never done, I do assure you, since she left England!’\n\n‘Now, last, not least, Mr. Micawber,’ said I. ‘He has paid off every obligation he incurred here--even to Traddles’s bill, you remember my dear Agnes--and therefore we may take it for granted that he is doing well. But what is the latest news of him?’\n\nMr. Peggotty, with a smile, put his hand in his breast-pocket, and produced a flat-folded, paper parcel, from which he took out, with much care, a little odd-looking newspaper.\n\n‘You are to understan’, Mas’r Davy,’ said he, ‘as we have left the Bush now, being so well to do; and have gone right away round to Port Middlebay Harbour, wheer theer’s what we call a town.’\n\n‘Mr. Micawber was in the Bush near you?’ said I.\n\n‘Bless you, yes,’ said Mr. Peggotty, ‘and turned to with a will. I never wish to meet a better gen’l’man for turning to with a will. I’ve seen that theer bald head of his a perspiring in the sun, Mas’r Davy, till I a’most thowt it would have melted away. And now he’s a Magistrate.’\n\n‘A Magistrate, eh?’ said I.\n\nMr. Peggotty pointed to a certain paragraph in the newspaper, where I read aloud as follows, from the Port Middlebay Times:\n\n‘The public dinner to our distinguished fellow-colonist and townsman, WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, Port Middlebay District Magistrate, came off yesterday in the large room of the Hotel, which was crowded to suffocation. It is estimated that not fewer than forty-seven persons must have been accommodated with dinner at one time, exclusive of the company in the passage and on the stairs. The beauty, fashion, and exclusiveness of Port Middlebay, flocked to do honour to one so deservedly esteemed, so highly talented, and so widely popular. Doctor Mell (of Colonial Salem-House Grammar School, Port Middlebay) presided, and on his right sat the distinguished guest. After the removal of the cloth, and the singing of Non Nobis (beautifully executed, and in which we were at no loss to distinguish the bell-like notes of that gifted amateur, WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, JUNIOR), the usual loyal and patriotic toasts were severally given and rapturously received. Doctor Mell, in a speech replete with feeling, then proposed “Our distinguished Guest, the ornament of our town. May he never leave us but to better himself, and may his success among us be such as to render his bettering himself impossible!” The cheering with which the toast was received defies description. Again and again it rose and fell, like the waves of ocean. At length all was hushed, and WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, presented himself to return thanks. Far be it from us, in the present comparatively imperfect state of the resources of our establishment, to endeavour to follow our distinguished townsman through the smoothly-flowing periods of his polished and highly-ornate address! Suffice it to observe, that it was a masterpiece of eloquence; and that those passages in which he more particularly traced his own successful career to its source, and warned the younger portion of his auditory from the shoals of ever incurring pecuniary liabilities which they were unable to liquidate, brought a tear into the manliest eye present. The remaining toasts were DOCTOR MELL; Mrs. MICAWBER (who gracefully bowed her acknowledgements from the side-door, where a galaxy of beauty was elevated on chairs, at once to witness and adorn the gratifying scene), Mrs. RIDGER BEGS (late Miss Micawber); Mrs. MELL; WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, JUNIOR (who convulsed the assembly by humorously remarking that he found himself unable to return thanks in a speech, but would do so, with their permission, in a song); Mrs. MICAWBER’S FAMILY (well known, it is needless to remark, in the mother-country), &c. &c. &c. At the conclusion of the proceedings the tables were cleared as if by art-magic for dancing. Among the votaries of TERPSICHORE, who disported themselves until Sol gave warning for departure, Wilkins Micawber, Esquire, Junior, and the lovely and accomplished Miss Helena, fourth daughter of Doctor Mell, were particularly remarkable.’\n\nI was looking back to the name of Doctor Mell, pleased to have discovered, in these happier circumstances, Mr. Mell, formerly poor pinched usher to my Middlesex magistrate, when Mr. Peggotty pointing to another part of the paper, my eyes rested on my own name, and I read thus:\n\n‘My Dear Sir,\n\n‘Years have elapsed, since I had an opportunity of ocularly perusing the lineaments, now familiar to the imaginations of a considerable portion of the civilized world.\n\n‘But, my dear Sir, though estranged (by the force of circumstances over which I have had no control) from the personal society of the friend and companion of my youth, I have not been unmindful of his soaring flight. Nor have I been debarred,\n\nThough seas between us braid ha’ roared,\n\n(BURNS) from participating in the intellectual feasts he has spread before us.\n\n‘I cannot, therefore, allow of the departure from this place of an individual whom we mutually respect and esteem, without, my dear Sir, taking this public opportunity of thanking you, on my own behalf, and, I may undertake to add, on that of the whole of the Inhabitants of Port Middlebay, for the gratification of which you are the ministering agent.\n\n‘Go on, my dear Sir! You are not unknown here, you are not unappreciated. Though “remote”, we are neither “unfriended”, “melancholy”, nor (I may add) “slow”. Go on, my dear Sir, in your Eagle course! The inhabitants of Port Middlebay may at least aspire to watch it, with delight, with entertainment, with instruction!\n\n‘Among the eyes elevated towards you from this portion of the globe, will ever be found, while it has light and life,\n\n‘The ‘Eye ‘Appertaining to\n\n‘WILKINS MICAWBER, ‘Magistrate.’\n\nI found, on glancing at the remaining contents of the newspaper, that Mr. Micawber was a diligent and esteemed correspondent of that journal. There was another letter from him in the same paper, touching a bridge; there was an advertisement of a collection of similar letters by him, to be shortly republished, in a neat volume, ‘with considerable additions’; and, unless I am very much mistaken, the Leading Article was his also.\n\nWe talked much of Mr. Micawber, on many other evenings while Mr. Peggotty remained with us. He lived with us during the whole term of his stay,--which, I think, was something less than a month,--and his sister and my aunt came to London to see him. Agnes and I parted from him aboard-ship, when he sailed; and we shall never part from him more, on earth.\n\nBut before he left, he went with me to Yarmouth, to see a little tablet I had put up in the churchyard to the memory of Ham. While I was copying the plain inscription for him at his request, I saw him stoop, and gather a tuft of grass from the grave and a little earth.\n\n‘For Em’ly,’ he said, as he put it in his breast. ‘I promised, Mas’r Davy.’\n\n# CHAPTER 64. A LAST RETROSPECT\n\nAnd now my written story ends. I look back, once more--for the last time--before I close these leaves.\n\nI see myself, with Agnes at my side, journeying along the road of life. I see our children and our friends around us; and I hear the roar of many voices, not indifferent to me as I travel on.\n\nWhat faces are the most distinct to me in the fleeting crowd? Lo, these; all turning to me as I ask my thoughts the question!\n\nHere is my aunt, in stronger spectacles, an old woman of four-score years and more, but upright yet, and a steady walker of six miles at a stretch in winter weather.\n\nAlways with her, here comes Peggotty, my good old nurse, likewise in spectacles, accustomed to do needle-work at night very close to the lamp, but never sitting down to it without a bit of wax candle, a yard-measure in a little house, and a work-box with a picture of St. Paul’s upon the lid.\n\nThe cheeks and arms of Peggotty, so hard and red in my childish days, when I wondered why the birds didn’t peck her in preference to apples, are shrivelled now; and her eyes, that used to darken their whole neighbourhood in her face, are fainter (though they glitter still); but her rough forefinger, which I once associated with a pocket nutmeg-grater, is just the same, and when I see my least child catching at it as it totters from my aunt to her, I think of our little parlour at home, when I could scarcely walk. My aunt’s old disappointment is set right, now. She is godmother to a real living Betsey Trotwood; and Dora (the next in order) says she spoils her.\n\nThere is something bulky in Peggotty’s pocket. It is nothing smaller than the Crocodile Book, which is in rather a dilapidated condition by this time, with divers of the leaves torn and stitched across, but which Peggotty exhibits to the children as a precious relic. I find it very curious to see my own infant face, looking up at me from the Crocodile stories; and to be reminded by it of my old acquaintance Brooks of Sheffield.\n\nAmong my boys, this summer holiday time, I see an old man making giant kites, and gazing at them in the air, with a delight for which there are no words. He greets me rapturously, and whispers, with many nods and winks, ‘Trotwood, you will be glad to hear that I shall finish the Memorial when I have nothing else to do, and that your aunt’s the most extraordinary woman in the world, sir!’\n\nWho is this bent lady, supporting herself by a stick, and showing me a countenance in which there are some traces of old pride and beauty, feebly contending with a querulous, imbecile, fretful wandering of the mind? She is in a garden; and near her stands a sharp, dark, withered woman, with a white scar on her lip. Let me hear what they say.\n\n‘Rosa, I have forgotten this gentleman’s name.’\n\nRosa bends over her, and calls to her, ‘Mr. Copperfield.’\n\n‘I am glad to see you, sir. I am sorry to observe you are in mourning. I hope Time will be good to you.’\n\nHer impatient attendant scolds her, tells her I am not in mourning, bids her look again, tries to rouse her.\n\n‘You have seen my son, sir,’ says the elder lady. ‘Are you reconciled?’\n\nLooking fixedly at me, she puts her hand to her forehead, and moans. Suddenly, she cries, in a terrible voice, ‘Rosa, come to me. He is dead!’ Rosa kneeling at her feet, by turns caresses her, and quarrels with her; now fiercely telling her, ‘I loved him better than you ever did!’--now soothing her to sleep on her breast, like a sick child. Thus I leave them; thus I always find them; thus they wear their time away, from year to year.\n\nWhat ship comes sailing home from India, and what English lady is this, married to a growling old Scotch Croesus with great flaps of ears? Can this be Julia Mills?\n\nIndeed it is Julia Mills, peevish and fine, with a black man to carry cards and letters to her on a golden salver, and a copper-coloured woman in linen, with a bright handkerchief round her head, to serve her Tiffin in her dressing-room. But Julia keeps no diary in these days; never sings Affection’s Dirge; eternally quarrels with the old Scotch Croesus, who is a sort of yellow bear with a tanned hide. Julia is steeped in money to the throat, and talks and thinks of nothing else. I liked her better in the Desert of Sahara.\n\nOr perhaps this IS the Desert of Sahara! For, though Julia has a stately house, and mighty company, and sumptuous dinners every day, I see no green growth near her; nothing that can ever come to fruit or flower. What Julia calls ‘society’, I see; among it Mr. Jack Maldon, from his Patent Place, sneering at the hand that gave it him, and speaking to me of the Doctor as ‘so charmingly antique’. But when society is the name for such hollow gentlemen and ladies, Julia, and when its breeding is professed indifference to everything that can advance or can retard mankind, I think we must have lost ourselves in that same Desert of Sahara, and had better find the way out.\n\nAnd lo, the Doctor, always our good friend, labouring at his Dictionary (somewhere about the letter D), and happy in his home and wife. Also the Old Soldier, on a considerably reduced footing, and by no means so influential as in days of yore!\n\nWorking at his chambers in the Temple, with a busy aspect, and his hair (where he is not bald) made more rebellious than ever by the constant friction of his lawyer’s-wig, I come, in a later time, upon my dear old Traddles. His table is covered with thick piles of papers; and I say, as I look around me:\n\n‘If Sophy were your clerk, now, Traddles, she would have enough to do!’\n\n‘You may say that, my dear Copperfield! But those were capital days, too, in Holborn Court! Were they not?’\n\n‘When she told you you would be a judge? But it was not the town talk then!’\n\n‘At all events,’ says Traddles, ‘if I ever am one--’ ‘Why, you know you will be.’\n\n‘Well, my dear Copperfield, WHEN I am one, I shall tell the story, as I said I would.’\n\nWe walk away, arm in arm. I am going to have a family dinner with Traddles. It is Sophy’s birthday; and, on our road, Traddles discourses to me of the good fortune he has enjoyed.\n\n‘I really have been able, my dear Copperfield, to do all that I had most at heart. There’s the Reverend Horace promoted to that living at four hundred and fifty pounds a year; there are our two boys receiving the very best education, and distinguishing themselves as steady scholars and good fellows; there are three of the girls married very comfortably; there are three more living with us; there are three more keeping house for the Reverend Horace since Mrs. Crewler’s decease; and all of them happy.’\n\n‘Except--’ I suggest.\n\n‘Except the Beauty,’ says Traddles. ‘Yes. It was very unfortunate that she should marry such a vagabond. But there was a certain dash and glare about him that caught her. However, now we have got her safe at our house, and got rid of him, we must cheer her up again.’\n\nTraddles’s house is one of the very houses--or it easily may have been--which he and Sophy used to parcel out, in their evening walks. It is a large house; but Traddles keeps his papers in his dressing-room and his boots with his papers; and he and Sophy squeeze themselves into upper rooms, reserving the best bedrooms for the Beauty and the girls. There is no room to spare in the house; for more of ‘the girls’ are here, and always are here, by some accident or other, than I know how to count. Here, when we go in, is a crowd of them, running down to the door, and handing Traddles about to be kissed, until he is out of breath. Here, established in perpetuity, is the poor Beauty, a widow with a little girl; here, at dinner on Sophy’s birthday, are the three married girls with their three husbands, and one of the husband’s brothers, and another husband’s cousin, and another husband’s sister, who appears to me to be engaged to the cousin. Traddles, exactly the same simple, unaffected fellow as he ever was, sits at the foot of the large table like a Patriarch; and Sophy beams upon him, from the head, across a cheerful space that is certainly not glittering with Britannia metal.\n\nAnd now, as I close my task, subduing my desire to linger yet, these faces fade away. But one face, shining on me like a Heavenly light by which I see all other objects, is above them and beyond them all. And that remains.\n\nI turn my head, and see it, in its beautiful serenity, beside me.\n\nMy lamp burns low, and I have written far into the night; but the dear presence, without which I were nothing, bears me company.\n\nO Agnes, O my soul, so may thy face be by me when I close my life indeed; so may I, when realities are melting from me, like the shadows which I now dismiss, still find thee near me, pointing upward!";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-766"]="내가 내 인생의 주인공이 될지, 아니면 그 자리를 다른 누군가가 차지할지는 이 책이 보여 줄 것이다. 내 인생을 처음부터 이야기하자면, 나는 (전해 들어서 그렇게 믿고 있는데) 어느 금요일 밤 열두 시에 태어났다. 시계가 종을 치기 시작하는 것과 내가 울음을 터뜨린 것이 동시였다는 말이 있었다.\n\n내가 태어난 날짜와 시각을 두고, 산파와 동네의 지혜로운 아낙 몇몇이 — 우리가 서로 얼굴을 익힐 가능성이 생기기도 한참 전부터 나에게 깊은 관심을 보였던 분들이다 — 이렇게 단언했다. 첫째, 나는 평생 운이 없을 팔자라는 것, 둘째, 나에게는 유령과 혼령을 볼 특권이 주어졌다는 것이다. 이 두 가지 재능은, 그들의 믿음에 따르면, 금요일 밤 자정 무렵에 태어난 불운한 아기라면 남자든 여자든 반드시 따라붙는 것이라고 했다.\n\n첫째 항목에 대해서는 여기서 굳이 할 말이 없다. 그 예언이 결과로써 들어맞았는지 빗나갔는지는 내 인생 이야기보다 더 잘 보여 줄 것이 없을 테니까. 둘째 항목에 대해서는 이것만 말해 두겠다. 내가 아기였을 적에 그 유산을 이미 다 써 버린 게 아니라면, 나는 아직 그것을 물려받지 못했다. 그렇다고 이 재산을 받지 못한 것을 조금도 불평하지는 않는다. 혹시 다른 누군가가 지금 그것을 누리고 있다면, 진심으로 마음껏 가지라고 권하고 싶다.\n\n나는 양막을 쓰고 태어났는데, 이것이 신문에 헐값인 십오 기니에 매물로 광고로 나왔다. 그 무렵 뱃사람들에게 돈이 없었던 건지, 아니면 믿음이 모자라 코르크 구명조끼를 더 좋아했던 건지 나는 모른다. 내가 아는 거라곤 단 한 건의 입찰밖에 없었다는 것뿐인데, 그것도 어음 중개업과 연줄이 있는 변호사 하나가 현금 이 파운드에 나머지는 셰리주로 치르겠다고 제안하면서, 그보다 높은 값으로는 물에 빠져 죽지 않을 보증을 받지 않겠다고 거절한 것이었다. 그래서 광고는 완전한 손해를 보고 내려졌다 — 셰리주로 말하자면, 그 무렵 내 가엾은 어머니의 셰리주가 시장에 나와 있던 참이었으니까 — 그리고 십 년이 지난 뒤, 그 양막은 우리 고장에서 한 사람당 반 크라운씩 받고 오십 명을 모아 추첨에 부쳐졌고, 당첨된 사람은 오 실링을 더 내기로 되어 있었다. 나도 그 자리에 있었는데, 내 몸의 일부가 그런 식으로 처분되는 것이 영 거북하고 어리둥절하게 느껴졌던 기억이 난다. 양막은, 내 기억으로는, 손바구니를 든 어느 노부인이 차지했는데, 그 노부인은 몹시 마지못해 바구니에서 정해진 오 실링을 꺼냈으되, 그것도 죄다 반 페니짜리 동전으로, 그나마 이 펜스 반이 모자랐다 — 그 사실을 노부인에게 입증하려고 무진 시간과 산수를 허비했지만 아무 소용이 없었다. 그 노부인이 끝내 물에 빠져 죽지 않고, 아흔두 살에 침대에서 의기양양하게 숨을 거두었다는 것은 그 고장에서 오래도록 기억될 놀라운 사실이다. 들리는 말에 따르면, 노부인은 죽는 날까지 자기 평생 다리 위 말고는 단 한 번도 물 위에 오른 적이 없다는 것을 가장 자랑스러운 자랑거리로 삼았다고 한다. 그리고 차(그녀가 무척이나 좋아했던 음료다)를 마시면서, 죽는 날까지, 감히 세상을 '싸돌아다니는' 뱃사람이며 다른 사람들의 불경함에 분노를 토했다고 한다. 그 못마땅한 행위 덕분에 차를 비롯한 몇몇 편의를 누리게 된 것이라고 아무리 설명해도 헛수고였다. 노부인은 늘, 더 힘주어, 그리고 자기 반대가 얼마나 굳건한지를 본능으로 알고 있는 듯이 이렇게 받아쳤다. \"싸돌아다니는 짓 따위는 하지 맙시다.\"\n\n나 자신도 지금 싸돌아다니지 않으려면, 다시 내가 태어난 이야기로 돌아가야겠다.\n\n나는 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서, 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나 '그 언저리'에서 태어났다. 나는 유복자였다. 아버지의 두 눈이 이 세상의 빛을 향해 감긴 지 여섯 달 만에 내 두 눈이 그 빛을 향해 열렸다. 아버지가 나를 한 번도 보지 못했다는 사실을 떠올리면 지금도 어딘가 묘한 기분이 든다. 그리고 더 묘한 것은, 교회 묘지에 선 아버지의 하얀 묘비를 두고 어린 시절 처음 품었던 어렴풋한 기억이다. 우리 집 작은 거실은 난롯불과 촛불로 따뜻하고 환한데, 그 묘비만 캄캄한 밤 홀로 바깥에 누워 있다는 생각에 나는 형언할 수 없는 연민을 느끼곤 했다. 게다가 우리 집 문은 — 때로는 거의 매정하다 싶을 만큼 — 그 묘비를 막아 빗장을 지르고 자물쇠로 잠겨 있었다.\n* 유복자: 아버지가 죽은 뒤에 태어난 자식. * 기니: 옛 영국 화폐 단위로 일 파운드 일 실링에 해당함.\n\n아버지의 고모, 따라서 나에게는 대고모뻘 되는 분이, 우리 집안의 가장 큰 어른이었다. 이분에 대해서는 차차 더 이야기할 일이 많을 것이다. 트롯우드 양, 또는 내 가엾은 어머니가 늘 부르던 대로 베시 양은, 이 만만찮은 인물을 입에 올릴 정도로 두려움을 가까스로 이겨 냈을 때 (그런 일은 드물었다) 그렇게 불렸는데, 자기보다 나이 어린 남편과 결혼했었다. 그 남편은 아주 잘생겼으되, '잘난 사람은 잘난 일을 한다'는 흔한 속담의 뜻으로 따지면 결코 잘난 사람이 아니었다 — 그가 베시 양을 때렸다는 의심이 강하게 돌았고, 심지어 한번은 생활비 문제로 다투다가 그녀를 이 층 창밖으로 내던지려고 성급하지만 단호한 채비를 한 적이 있다는 소문까지 있었기 때문이다. 이렇게 성미가 맞지 않는다는 증거들이 쌓이자 베시 양은 그에게 돈을 쥐여 주고 합의하에 갈라서기로 했다. 남편은 자기 몫의 돈을 챙겨 인도로 떠났고, 우리 집안에 떠도는 황당한 전설에 따르면, 그곳에서 한번은 비비를 거느리고 코끼리를 타고 다니는 모습이 목격되었다고 한다. 하지만 내 생각에 그건 비비가 아니라 인도 신사였거나 — 아니면 인도 귀부인이었지 싶다. 어쨌거나 그가 죽었다는 소식이 인도에서 본국으로 전해진 것은 십 년이 채 안 되어서였다. 그 소식이 고모님에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 갈라서자마자 고모님은 처녀 적 성을 다시 쓰기 시작했고, 멀리 떨어진 바닷가 외딴 마을에 작은 오두막을 사서, 그곳에 하녀 하나만 두고 독신녀로 자리를 잡았으며, 그 후로는 줄곧 굽힐 줄 모르는 은둔 속에 외따로 산다고 전해졌다.\n\n아버지는 한때 고모님의 귀여움을 받았던 모양이다. 그러나 고모님은 아버지의 결혼에 죽도록 모욕을 느꼈는데, 내 어머니가 '밀랍 인형'이라는 이유에서였다. 고모님은 어머니를 한 번도 본 적이 없었으나, 어머니가 아직 스무 살도 안 됐다는 것은 알고 있었다. 아버지와 베시 양은 두 번 다시 만나지 않았다. 아버지는 결혼할 때 어머니보다 나이가 곱절이었고, 몸도 그리 튼튼하지 못했다. 그리고 일 년 뒤에 세상을 떠났는데, 앞서 말했듯이 내가 세상에 나오기 여섯 달 전이었다.\n\n그 운명적이고 중요한 금요일이라고 불러도 봐 줄 만한 날의 오후, 사정은 이러했다. 그러니 나는 그때 사정이 어떠했는지를 그 자리에서 알고 있었다고 주장할 수 없고, 이제부터 이야기할 일들을 내 감각의 증거에 기대어 기억한다고 내세울 수도 없다.\n\n어머니는 난롯가에 앉아 있었는데, 몸은 약했고 마음은 한없이 가라앉아, 눈물 너머로 불을 바라보며 자기 자신과 아비 없는 어린 손님 — 위층 서랍 속 점지의 핀 몇 그로스가 벌써 환영하고 있던 그 손님 — 을 생각하며 무겁게 절망하고 있었다. 그 손님이 도착하는 일에 세상은 조금도 들떠 있지 않았다. 다시 말하건대 어머니는, 바람 부는 화창한 삼월 오후에, 난롯가에 앉아, 몹시 겁먹고 슬픈 채로, 앞에 놓인 시련에서 과연 살아 나올 수 있을지 깊이 의심하고 있었다. 그러다 눈물을 닦으며 맞은편 창문으로 눈을 들었을 때, 낯선 부인이 정원을 가로질러 다가오는 것을 보았다.\n\n어머니는 두 번째로 흘끗 본 순간, 그분이 베시 양이라는 확실한 예감이 들었다. 정원 울타리 너머로 지는 햇살이 그 낯선 부인을 붉게 비추고 있었고, 그녀는 다른 누구의 것일 수도 없는 무시무시할 만큼 꼿꼿한 자태와 침착한 낯빛으로 문을 향해 걸어 올라왔다.\n\n집에 다다랐을 때, 부인은 자기가 누구인지를 또 한 번 증명해 보였다. 아버지는 그분이 좀처럼 여느 신실한 사람처럼 처신하지 않는다고 종종 넌지시 말하곤 했는데, 과연 그녀는 초인종을 울리는 대신, 바로 그 창문 앞으로 와서 안을 들여다보았다. 어찌나 코끝을 유리에 바짝 눌렀던지, 내 가엾은 어머니는 그 코가 순식간에 납작하고 하얗게 변했다고 늘 말하곤 했다.\n\n부인이 어머니를 어찌나 놀라게 했던지, 나는 내가 금요일에 태어난 것이 베시 양 덕분이라고 늘 굳게 믿어 왔다.\n\n어머니는 너무 놀란 나머지 의자에서 일어나 그 뒤편 구석으로 가 버렸다. 베시 양은 천천히 캐묻듯이 방 안을 둘러보았는데, 반대편에서 시작해, 네덜란드제 시계의 사라센 머리 인형처럼 눈동자를 빙 돌려 가다가, 마침내 어머니에게 이르렀다. 그러고는 명령에 복종하는 데 익숙한 사람답게, 와서 문을 열라는 듯 어머니에게 눈살을 찌푸리며 손짓했다. 어머니는 갔다.\n\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드 부인이시겠지?\" 베시 양이 말했다. 그 말투에 실린 강조는 아마도 어머니의 상복과 그 처지를 가리키는 것이었으리라.\n\n\"네.\" 어머니가 가냘프게 대답했다.\n\n\"트롯우드 양이오.\" 손님이 말했다. \"내 얘기는 들어 봤겠지?\"\n\n어머니는 그런 영광을 누렸노라고 대답했다. 그러면서, 그 영광이 그리 압도적인 것이었다는 뜻으로 들리지 않는 것 같아 마음이 영 불편했다.\n\n\"이제 직접 보는구먼.\" 베시 양이 말했다. 어머니는 고개를 숙이고 안으로 드시라고 청했다.\n\n두 사람은 어머니가 나왔던 거실로 들어갔다. 복도 건너편 제일 좋은 방의 난로에는 불이 지펴져 있지 않았으니 — 사실 아버지의 장례식 이후로 한 번도 불을 지핀 적이 없었다 — 그래서였다. 둘이 다 자리에 앉았는데 베시 양이 아무 말도 하지 않자, 어머니는 애써 참아 보았지만 끝내 울음을 터뜨렸다. \"어허, 쯧, 쯧, 쯧!\" 베시 양이 황급히 말했다. \"그러지 마! 자, 자!\"\n\n그래도 어머니는 어쩔 수가 없어서, 실컷 울고 나서야 그쳤다.\n\n\"모자를 벗어 봐, 얘야.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"얼굴 좀 보게.\"\n\n어머니는 설령 거절하고 싶은 마음이 있었더라도 그분이 너무 무서워서, 이 별난 부탁을 거역하지 못했다. 그래서 시키는 대로 했는데, 손이 어찌나 떨렸던지 머리카락이 (숱이 많고 아름다웠다) 온 얼굴로 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"어머나, 세상에!\" 베시 양이 소리쳤다. \"넌 완전 아기로구나!\"\n\n어머니는 분명 제 나이치고도 유난히 앳돼 보였다. 어머니는 그것이 제 잘못이라도 되는 양 고개를 떨구더니, 흐느끼며 말하기를, 정말이지 자기는 어린애 같은 과부일 뿐이고, 살아남는다 해도 어린애 같은 어미밖에 못 될까 봐 두렵다고 했다. 잠깐 침묵이 흐르는 사이, 어머니는 베시 양이 자기 머리카락을 만지는 듯한, 그것도 결코 무뚝뚝하지 않은 손길로 만지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그러나 겁먹은 채로 한 가닥 희망을 품고 그분을 올려다보았을 때, 그 부인은 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고 두 손을 한쪽 무릎 위에 포갠 채, 두 발을 난로 가리개에 올려놓고 불을 향해 눈살을 찌푸리고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 도대체,\" 베시 양이 불쑥 말했다. \"왜 까마귀 둥지냐?\"\n\n\"집 말씀이세요, 부인?\" 어머니가 물었다.\n\n\"왜 까마귀 둥지냐고.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"너희 둘 중 누구라도 살림에 대한 실속 있는 생각이 조금이라도 있었다면, 차라리 부엌살림이 더 어울렸을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그 이름은 코퍼필드 씨가 고른 거예요.\" 어머니가 대답했다. \"그이가 이 집을 살 때, 둘레에 까마귀가 산다고 생각하는 게 좋다고 했어요.\"\n\n마침 그때 저녁 바람이 정원 끝에 선 키 큰 늙은 느릅나무들 사이를 어찌나 휘저어 놓던지, 어머니도 베시 양도 그쪽을 흘끗거리지 않을 수 없었다. 느릅나무들은 비밀을 속삭이는 거인들처럼 서로에게 몸을 기울이다가, 그렇게 몇 초쯤 잠잠하더니, 갑자기 격렬하게 술렁이며 사나운 팔을 마구 휘둘러 댔다. 마치 방금 주고받은 속내가 마음의 평화를 깨뜨릴 만큼 너무도 사악하기라도 한 듯이. 그 높은 가지에 매달려 무게를 더하던 비바람에 닳고 해진 낡은 까마귀 둥지 몇 개가, 폭풍우 치는 바다 위 난파선처럼 흔들렸다.\n\n\"새들은 어디 있나?\" 베시 양이 물었다.\n\n\"네에—?\" 어머니는 다른 생각에 잠겨 있던 참이었다.\n\n\"까마귀들 말이야 — 다들 어디로 갔느냔 말이야.\" 베시 양이 물었다.\n\n\"우리가 여기 산 뒤로는 한 마리도 없었어요.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"우린 생각했어요 — 코퍼필드 씨는 생각했지요 — 꽤 큰 까마귀 둥지라고요. 하지만 둥지가 아주 오래된 거라, 새들은 진작에 그걸 버리고 떠난 거였어요.\"\n\n\"영락없이 데이비드 코퍼필드로구나!\" 베시 양이 소리쳤다. \"머리끝부터 발끝까지 데이비드 코퍼필드야! 까마귀 한 마리 얼씬 안 하는데 집을 까마귀 둥지라 부르고, 둥지가 보인다고 새들이 거기 있겠거니 곧이곧대로 믿어 버리다니!\"\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨는,\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"이미 세상을 떠난 사람이에요. 그러니 그이를 두고 제 앞에서 함부로 험담하시려거든—\"\n\n내 가엾은 어머니는, 짐작건대, 한순간 고모님에게 폭력이라도 휘두를 작정이었던 듯하다. 그러나 고모님은 한 손만으로도 어머니를 쉽게 제압했을 테고, 설령 그날 저녁 어머니가 그런 맞대결에 훨씬 잘 단련된 상태였다 해도 마찬가지였을 것이다. 하지만 그 기세는 의자에서 몸을 일으키는 동작과 함께 스러졌고, 어머니는 다시 아주 얌전히 주저앉더니, 그대로 정신을 잃었다.\n\n어머니가 스스로 정신을 차렸는지, 아니면 베시 양이 깨운 것인지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 어머니가 깨어났을 때 그분은 창가에 서 있었다. 그 무렵 황혼은 이미 어둠으로 짙어 가고 있었다. 두 사람이 서로를 어렴풋이나마 알아볼 수 있었던 것도 난롯불의 도움이 없었다면 불가능했을 것이다.\n\n\"자?\" 그저 무심코 바깥 경치나 둘러본 것처럼 베시 양이 의자로 돌아오며 말했다. \"그래, 언제쯤 나올—\"\n\n\"온몸이 다 떨려요.\" 어머니가 더듬거렸다. \"무슨 일인지 모르겠어요. 저는 분명 죽을 거예요, 틀림없어요!\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"차나 좀 마셔.\"\n\n\"아이고 저런, 저런, 그게 제게 조금이라도 도움이 될까요?\" 어머니가 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다.\n\n\"당연히 되지.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"다 마음 탓이야. 너희 집 계집애 이름이 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"아직 그게 여자애일지 어떨지 모르겠는데요, 부인.\" 어머니가 천진하게 말했다.\n\n\"아이고 이 아기야!\" 베시 양이 외쳤는데, 자기도 모르게 위층 서랍 속 바늘겨레의 두 번째 글귀를 인용한 셈이었으나, 나 대신 어머니에게 갖다 붙인 것이었다. \"그 얘기가 아니라. 너희 집 하녀 말이야.\"\n\n\"패거티예요.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"패거티!\" 베시 양이 다소 분개하며 되받았다. \"얘야, 너 지금, 멀쩡한 사람이 신실한 교회에 들어가서 패거티라는 이름을 받았다고 말하는 게냐?\" \"그건 그 애 성이에요.\" 어머니가 가냘프게 말했다. \"그 애 세례명이 제 이름과 같아서, 코퍼필드 씨가 성으로 불렀어요.\"\n\n\"이봐! 패거티!\" 베시 양이 거실 문을 열며 소리쳤다. \"차. 네 주인마님이 몸이 좀 안 좋으시다. 꾸물대지 마.\"\n\n집이 집으로 세워진 이래 줄곧 그 집의 공인된 권위자였기라도 한 듯한 위세로 이 명령을 내리고는, 낯선 목소리에 촛불을 들고 복도를 따라 어리둥절하게 다가오는 패거티와 맞닥뜨리려고 밖을 한번 내다본 다음, 베시 양은 다시 문을 닫고 아까처럼 앉았다. 두 발은 난로 가리개에 올리고, 치맛자락은 걷어 올리고, 두 손은 한쪽 무릎 위에 포갠 채로.\n\n\"여자애일 거라는 얘기를 하던 참이지.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"난 그게 여자애일 거라고 조금도 의심치 않아. 여자애일 거라는 예감이 들거든. 자 얘야, 이 여자애가 태어나는 그 순간부터—\"\n\n\"어쩌면 사내애일지도요.\" 어머니가 외람되이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내가 여자애일 거라는 예감이 든다고 했잖니.\" 베시 양이 되받았다. \"토 달지 마. 얘야, 이 여자애가 태어나는 그 순간부터, 나는 그 애의 벗이 되어 줄 작정이다. 그 애의 대모가 되어 줄 작정이야. 그러니 그 애를 베시 트롯우드 코퍼필드라고 불러 주렴. 이 베시 트롯우드만큼은 인생에서 실수가 있어선 안 돼. 가엾은 것, 그 애의 마음을 가지고 함부로 장난쳐서도 안 되고. 잘 길러야 하고, 받을 자격도 없는 데다가 어리석은 정을 함부로 쏟지 않도록 잘 지켜 줘야 해. 그건 내가 맡아야 할 일이야.\"\n\n이런 말을 한마디씩 내뱉을 때마다 베시 양은 머리를 한 번씩 씰룩거렸는데, 마치 자기가 옛날에 당했던 억울한 일들이 속에서 다시 들끓는데, 그것을 노골적으로 입에 올리지 않으려 안간힘으로 억누르는 듯했다. 적어도 어머니는 그렇게 짐작했다. 어머니는 낮게 깜박이는 난롯불 빛에 기대어 그분을 살폈는데, 베시 양이 너무 무섭고, 제 처지가 너무 불안하고, 또 온통 주눅 들고 어리둥절해 있던 탓에, 무엇 하나 또렷이 살피지도, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지도 못했다.\n\n\"그래, 데이비드가 너한테 잘해 줬니, 얘야?\" 잠시 침묵하다가, 머리를 씰룩이던 동작이 차츰 잦아들었을 때 베시 양이 물었다. \"둘이 같이 편안하게 지냈어?\"\n\n\"우린 무척 행복했어요.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"코퍼필드 씨는 저한테 너무도 잘해 줬어요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 그러니까 널 응석받이로 만들어 놨다는 거지?\" 베시 양이 되받았다.\n\n\"이 거친 세상에 다시 홀로 남아 제 한 몸을 제가 건사해야 하는 처지로 보자면, 네, 정말로 그랬던 게 아닌가 싶어요.\" 어머니가 흐느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"자! 울지 마!\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"너희는 짝이 잘 맞지 않았어, 얘야 — 세상에 짝이 잘 맞는 두 사람이라는 게 있기나 하다면 말이지만 — 그래서 물어본 거야. 너 고아였지?\" \"네.\"\n\n\"가정교사였고?\"\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨가 드나들던 집에서 보모 겸 가정교사로 있었어요. 코퍼필드 씨가 저한테 무척 다정했고, 저를 많이 눈여겨봐 주고, 마음도 많이 써 주다가, 마침내 청혼을 했어요. 그래서 받아들였고요. 그렇게 우린 결혼했어요.\" 어머니가 담담하게 말했다.\n\n\"하! 가엾은 아기 같으니!\" 베시 양은 여전히 불을 향해 눈살을 찌푸린 채 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"넌 뭐 아는 게 있긴 하니?\"\n\n\"네? 무슨 말씀이신지요, 부인.\" 어머니가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"이를테면 살림하는 거 말이야.\" 베시 양이 말했다.\n\n\"많이는 못해요, 그게.\" 어머니가 대답했다. \"제가 바라는 만큼은요. 하지만 코퍼필드 씨가 가르쳐 주고 있었—\"\n\n(\"자기는 그런 걸 뭐 그리 많이 알았다고!\") 베시 양이 곁다리로 한마디 했다. \"—저도 무척 배우고 싶었고 그이도 무척 참을성 있게 가르쳐 줬으니, 그이가 죽는 그 큰 불행만 없었다면 분명 나아졌을 거예요.\" 여기서 어머니는 또 무너져 내려, 더는 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"—가계부도 꼬박꼬박 적었고, 매일 밤 코퍼필드 씨하고 같이 셈을 맞춰 봤어요.\" 어머니가 또 한바탕 슬픔을 터뜨리며 외치다가 또 무너졌다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"더는 울지 마.\" \"—그리고 그 일로 우린 정말이지 단 한마디도 다툰 적이 없어요. 그저 코퍼필드 씨가 제 삼 자랑 오 자가 너무 비슷하다고 한다거나, 칠 자랑 구 자에 꼬리를 배배 꼬아 붙인다고 한 적 말고는요.\" 어머니가 또 한바탕 터뜨리며 말하다가 또 무너졌다.\n\n\"너 그러다 병나겠다.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"그게 너한테도, 내 대녀한테도 좋을 게 없다는 걸 너도 알잖니. 자! 그러면 안 돼!\"\n\n이 말도 어머니를 가라앉히는 데 얼마쯤 몫을 했지만, 점점 심해지는 진통이 훨씬 큰 몫을 했다. 잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 베시 양이 두 발을 난로 가리개에 올린 채 이따금 \"하!\" 하고 내뱉는 소리만이 그 정적을 깰 뿐이었다.\n\n\"데이비드가 제 돈으로 연금을 들어 뒀다는 건 나도 알아.\" 이윽고 그녀가 말했다. \"널 위해선 뭘 해 뒀니?\"\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨는,\" 어머니가 힘겹게 대답했다. \"참 사려 깊고 마음씨 고운 사람이라, 그 연금의 일부를 제게 물려주도록 해 뒀어요.\"\n\n\"얼마나?\" 베시 양이 물었다.\n\n\"한 해에 백오 파운드요.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"그만하면 더 못한 짓도 할 수 있었을 텐데.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n* 연금: 일정 금액을 미리 내고 살아 있는 동안 또는 정해진 기간에 정기적으로 일정액을 받는 제도. * 위자료·유산 따위로 받는 정기 수입.\n그 말은 그 순간에 꼭 들어맞았다. 어머니가 너무도 위중해진 탓에, 차 쟁반과 촛불을 들고 들어온 패거티가, 한눈에 마님이 얼마나 위독한지를 알아채고는 — 빛만 충분했더라면 베시 양도 진작 알아챘을 것이다 — 부랴부랴 어머니를 위층 방으로 옮겼다. 그러고는 며칠 전부터 어머니 몰래 집 안에 숨겨 두었던 — 만일의 비상사태에 대비한 특별 심부름꾼으로 둔 — 조카 햄 패거티를 곧장 보내, 산파와 의사를 불러오게 했다.\n\n이 두 협력자는 몇 분 간격으로 도착해서는, 난로 앞에 보닛을 왼팔에 걸쳐 묶은 채로 앉아, 보석상에서 쓰는 솜으로 두 귀를 틀어막고 있는, 심상찮은 행색의 낯선 부인을 발견하고는 적잖이 놀랐다. 패거티는 그 부인에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 어머니도 그녀에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 그녀는 거실 안에서 완전한 수수께끼였다. 게다가 주머니에 보석상 솜을 잔뜩 쟁여 두고는 그것을 그런 식으로 귀에 쑤셔 박고 있다는 사실도, 그녀가 풍기는 엄숙한 분위기를 조금도 덜어 주지 못했다.\n\n의사는 위층에 올라갔다 다시 내려와서는, 짐작건대 이 낯선 부인과 자기가 몇 시간이고 마주 앉아 있어야 할 가능성이 크다는 것을 알아채고는, 정중하고 붙임성 있게 굴기로 작정한 모양이었다. 그는 같은 남자 중에서도 가장 온순한 사람이었고, 자그마한 남자들 가운데서도 가장 부드러운 사람이었다. 그는 자리를 덜 차지하려고 몸을 모로 비틀며 방을 드나들었다. 그는 햄릿에 나오는 유령처럼 사뿐사뿐, 그보다 더 느릿느릿 걸었다. 그는 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울이고 다녔는데, 한편으로는 겸손하게 자기를 낮추느라, 또 한편으로는 겸손하게 남들의 비위를 맞추느라 그랬다. 그가 개한테조차 던질 말 한마디가 없었다고 하면 약과다. 그는 미친개한테도 말 한마디 던지지 못했을 사람이다. 어쩌면 한마디쯤 부드럽게 건넸거나, 반 마디나 한마디 조각쯤 건넸을지는 몰라도 — 그는 걸음만큼이나 말도 느릿느릿했으니까 — 무슨 일이 있어도 미친개한테조차 무례하게 굴지 못했을 것이고, 어떤 세상의 사정을 들이대도 그를 다그칠 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n칠립 씨는 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 고모님을 부드럽게 바라보며 가볍게 고개를 숙여 인사하더니, 자기 왼쪽 귀를 살며시 만지며 그 보석상 솜을 빗대어 말했다.\n\n\"어디 좀 거북하신 데라도 있으신가요, 부인?\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 고모님이 한쪽 귀에서 솜을 코르크 마개 뽑듯 쑥 빼내며 대꾸했다.\n\n칠립 씨는 그 퉁명스러움에 어찌나 놀랐던지 — 나중에 어머니에게 그렇게 말했다 — 침착함을 잃지 않은 게 천만다행이라고 했다. 그래도 그는 상냥하게 다시 물었다.\n\n\"어디 좀 거북하신 데라도 있으신가요, 부인?\"\n\n\"쓸데없는 소리!\" 고모님이 대꾸하고는, 단번에 다시 귀를 틀어막았다.\n\n이렇게 되니 칠립 씨로서는 별수 없이, 그저 앉아서 그녀가 불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 동안 그녀를 맥없이 바라보고 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그러다 다시 위층에서 부름을 받았다. 십오 분쯤 자리를 비웠다가 그는 돌아왔다.\n\n\"어떻소?\" 고모님이 그에게 가까운 쪽 귀에서 솜을 빼며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 부인,\" 칠립 씨가 대답했다. \"저희가 — 저희가 더디게, 더디게 진척되고 있습니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"체에—에—엣!\" 고모님이, 그 경멸 어린 감탄사를 떨림까지 완벽하게 실어 내뱉었다. 그러고는 또 아까처럼 귀를 틀어막았다.\n\n칠립 씨가 어머니에게 말하기를, 정말이지 — 정말이지 — 그는 거의 충격을 받았다고 했다. 순전히 직업적인 견지에서만 보아도, 그는 거의 충격을 받았다는 것이었다. 그래도 그는 그녀가 불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 동안 거의 두 시간을 앉아서 그녀를 바라보고 있다가, 다시 불려 나갔다. 또 한참 자리를 비웠다가 그는 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n\"어떻소?\" 고모님이 그쪽 귀에서 다시 솜을 빼며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 부인,\" 칠립 씨가 대답했다. \"저희가 — 저희가 더디게, 더디게 진척되고 있습니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"이야—아—앗!\" 고모님이 어찌나 으르렁대며 쏘아붙였던지, 칠립 씨는 정말이지 도저히 견딜 수가 없었다. 그건 정말 사람 기를 꺾어 놓으려고 작정한 거나 다름없었다고, 그는 나중에 말했다. 그래서 그는 차라리 계단에 나가 앉아, 어둠 속에서 거센 외풍을 맞으며, 다시 부름을 받을 때까지 기다리는 편을 택했다.\n\n햄 패거티는 국민학교에 다녔고 교리문답이라면 둘째가라면 서러울 정도였으니, 그런 만큼 믿을 만한 증인으로 봐도 좋을 것이다. 그가 이튿날 전한 바에 따르면, 이 일이 있고 한 시간쯤 뒤 마침 거실 문 안을 빼꼼 들여다보았다가, 그때 안절부절못하며 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하던 베시 양에게 곧장 들켜, 미처 달아나기도 전에 덥석 붙잡히고 말았다고 한다. 그리고 그때쯤 위층에서 이따금 발소리며 사람 목소리가 들려왔는데, 그 소리가 가장 클 때마다 부인이 넘쳐흐르는 동요를 쏟아 낼 제물로 삼는 양 자기를 꽉 움켜쥐는 걸로 미루어, 솜으로도 그 소리까지 막지는 못한 모양이라고 했다. 또 부인은 (그가 마치 아편을 과하게 먹기라도 한 듯이) 멱살을 잡고 줄곧 그를 위아래로 끌고 다니면서, 그럴 때마다 그를 흔들어 대고, 머리카락을 헝클어뜨리고, 셔츠를 함부로 다루고, 마치 자기 귀와 헷갈리기라도 한 듯 그의 귀를 틀어막고, 그 밖에도 이리저리 쥐어뜯고 들들 볶았다고 한다. 이 일은 그의 이모가 어느 정도 사실임을 확인해 주었는데, 그가 풀려난 직후인 열두 시 반에 그를 본 이모는, 그때 그의 얼굴이 나만큼이나 새빨갰다고 단언했다.\n\n온순한 칠립 씨는, 설령 다른 때라면 모를까, 이런 때에 도무지 앙심을 품을 수 없는 사람이었다. 그는 풀려나자마자 몸을 모로 비틀며 거실로 들어와, 더없이 온순한 태도로 고모님에게 말했다.\n\n\"네, 부인, 기쁜 마음으로 축하드립니다.\"\n\n\"뭘 축하한다는 거요?\" 고모님이 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n칠립 씨는 고모님의 지극히 매서운 태도에 또다시 안절부절못하더니, 그녀를 누그러뜨리려고 가볍게 고개를 숙이고 살짝 미소를 지어 보였다.\n\n\"맙소사, 이 양반이 대체 뭘 하는 거야!\" 고모님이 조바심을 내며 외쳤다. \"말도 못해?\"\n\n\"진정하세요, 부인.\" 칠립 씨가 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"이제 더는 마음 졸이실 일이 없습니다, 부인. 진정하세요.\"\n\n훗날 사람들은 이때 고모님이 칠립 씨를 붙잡고 흔들어, 그가 할 말까지 흔들어 빼내지 않은 것이 거의 기적이라고 여겼다. 고모님은 그저 그를 향해 자기 머리를 한 번 흔들었을 뿐인데, 그 모양새가 그를 움찔하게 만들었다.\n\n\"네, 부인,\" 칠립 씨가 용기를 내자마자 말을 이었다. \"기쁜 마음으로 축하드립니다. 이제 다 끝났습니다, 부인. 무사히 끝났어요.\"\n\n칠립 씨가 이 일장 연설을 늘어놓는 데 바친 오 분쯤 되는 시간 동안, 고모님은 그를 빤히 뜯어보았다.\n\n\"그 앤 어떻소?\" 보닛을 여전히 한쪽 팔에 걸친 채 두 팔을 포개며 고모님이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 부인, 곧 아주 편안해지실 겁니다, 그러길 바랍니다.\" 칠립 씨가 대답했다. \"이런 침울한 집안 사정에서 젊은 어머니가 누릴 수 있을 만큼은 편안해지실 거예요. 이제 곧 만나 보셔도 아무 문제 없습니다, 부인. 만나 보시면 그분에게 도움이 될지도 모르고요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 앤. 그 앤 어떻소?\" 고모님이 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n칠립 씨는 고개를 좀 더 한쪽으로 기울이며, 다정한 새처럼 고모님을 바라보았다.\n\n\"아기 말이야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 앤 어떻소?\"\n\n\"부인,\" 칠립 씨가 대답했다. \"이미 아신 줄 알았습니다. 사내아이입니다.\"\n\n고모님은 한마디도 하지 않고, 보닛 끈을 마치 투석기 끈처럼 거머쥐더니, 그것으로 칠립 씨 머리를 향해 한 방 후려갈기고는, 찌그러진 채로 머리에 눌러쓰고, 밖으로 걸어 나가, 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않았다. 그녀는 심술 난 요정처럼, 아니면 세상 사람들이 으레 내가 볼 자격이 있다고 여겼던 그 초자연적인 존재들 가운데 하나처럼 홀연히 사라졌고, 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않았다.\n\n그렇다. 나는 바구니에 누워 있었고, 어머니는 침대에 누워 있었다. 그러나 베시 트롯우드 코퍼필드는 영원히 꿈과 그림자의 나라, 내가 그토록 얼마 전에 떠나온 그 어마어마한 영역에 머무르게 되었다. 그리고 우리 방 창문에 비친 불빛은, 그 모든 나그네가 이르는 이승의 종착지를 비추었고, 한때 그였던 — 그가 없었더라면 나 또한 결코 없었을 — 재와 흙 위에 솟은 무덤을 비추고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 2. I OBSERVE\n\n저 멀리, 내 갓난 시절의 텅 빈 자리를 돌아볼 때, 가장 먼저 또렷한 모습으로 떠오르는 것은 예쁜 머리카락과 앳된 몸매를 가진 어머니, 그리고 몸매라곤 도무지 찾아볼 수 없는 패거티다. 패거티의 눈은 어찌나 검던지 얼굴에서 그 둘레까지 죄다 어둡게 물들이는 것 같았고, 두 뺨과 두 팔은 어찌나 단단하고 붉던지 새들이 사과 대신 그녀를 쪼아 먹지 않는 게 신기할 정도였다.\n\n내 생각에 나는 그 두 사람이 조금 떨어져 서 있던 모습을 기억할 수 있다. 둘 다 몸을 숙이거나 마룻바닥에 무릎을 꿇어 내 눈높이에 맞추느라 작아 보였고, 나는 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 비틀비틀 걸어가곤 했다. 패거티가 나에게 내밀던 집게손가락의 감촉이, 마치 호주머니 속 육두구 강판처럼 바느질로 거칠어져 있던 그 느낌이 내 마음에 또렷이 남아 있는데, 나는 그것을 실제 기억과 구별해 낼 수가 없다.\n\n이게 그저 상상일지도 모르겠다. 그러나 나는 우리 대부분의 기억이 흔히들 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 먼 옛날까지 거슬러 올라갈 수 있다고 믿는다. 마찬가지로 나는 아주 어린 아이들 상당수의 관찰력이 그 세밀함과 정확함에서 자못 놀랍다고 믿는다. 정말이지 내 생각에, 이 점에서 두드러진 어른들 대부분은 그 능력을 얻었다기보다 잃지 않은 것이라고 말하는 편이 더 맞을 듯하다. 더구나 내가 보기에 그런 사람들은 어떤 싱그러움과 부드러움, 그리고 무언가에 기뻐할 줄 아는 능력을 간직하고 있는데, 그것 또한 어린 시절에서 고스란히 지켜 온 유산이기 때문이다.\n\n이런 말을 하려고 멈춰 서는 것이 '싸돌아다니는' 짓은 아닐까 싶어 마음이 켕기지만, 그래도 이 말 덕분에 나는 이런 결론들을 일부나마 나 자신에 대한 경험에 기대어 세운다는 점을 짚어 둘 수 있다. 그리고 이 이야기에서 내가 적어 내려갈 무언가를 통해, 내가 세밀한 관찰력을 지닌 아이였다거나, 어른이 되어서도 어린 시절을 또렷이 기억한다는 사실이 드러난다면, 나는 그 두 가지 특성 모두를 분명히 내 것이라 주장하겠다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 내 갓난 시절의 텅 빈 자리를 돌아볼 때, 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉킨 것들 사이에서 저 홀로 또렷이 떠오르는 첫 번째 모습은 어머니와 패거티다. 또 무엇이 기억날까? 어디 보자.\n\n안개 속에서 우리 집이 떠오른다. 가장 이른 기억 속에서도 그 집은 낯설지 않고 아주 친숙하다. 일 층에는 뒤뜰로 통하는 패거티의 부엌이 있다. 뜰 한가운데에는 장대 위에 비둘기 집이 얹혀 있는데, 비둘기는 한 마리도 없다. 한쪽 구석에는 커다란 개집이 있는데, 개는 한 마리도 없다. 그리고 닭들이 잔뜩 있는데, 내 눈에는 그것들이 무시무시하게 커 보였고, 위협하듯 사납게 돌아다녔다. 수탉 한 마리가 말뚝에 올라가 울었는데, 내가 부엌 창문으로 그놈을 바라보면 마치 나를 콕 집어 노려보는 듯해서, 그 사나운 기세에 나는 부르르 몸을 떨었다. 옆문 밖의 거위들은 내가 그쪽으로 가면 긴 목을 쭉 빼고 뒤뚱뒤뚱 쫓아왔는데, 나는 밤마다 그놈들 꿈을 꾸었다. 들짐승에 둘러싸인 사람이 사자 꿈을 꾸듯이.\n\n여기 긴 복도가 있다. 내가 얼마나 어마어마하게 까마득한 거리로 그것을 그려 내는지! 그 복도는 패거티의 부엌에서 현관문까지 이어진다. 그 복도에서 어두운 저장실 하나가 열리는데, 밤이면 그 앞을 후다닥 뛰어 지나가야 하는 곳이다. 안에 사람이 없고 희미한 불빛 하나도 없을 때면, 그 통이며 단지며 낡은 차 상자 사이에 무엇이 도사리고 있을지 알 수 없기 때문이다. 문틈으로는 곰팡내가 새어 나오는데, 그 안에는 비누, 절임, 후추, 양초, 커피 냄새가 단숨에 한꺼번에 풍긴다. 그다음에는 거실이 둘 있다. 하나는 저녁이면 우리가 함께 앉아 있는 거실로, 어머니와 나와 패거티가 거기 있다. 일이 끝나고 우리끼리만 남으면 패거티는 어엿한 우리 동무가 되기 때문이다. 다른 하나는 일요일에 앉는 제일 좋은 거실인데, 그럴듯하지만 그리 편하지는 않다. 그 방은 나에게 어쩐지 음울한 느낌이 도는데, 패거티가 — 언제였는지는 모르지만 아무래도 까마득한 옛날인 듯하다 — 아버지의 장례식 이야기며, 손님들이 검은 외투를 두르고 있었다는 이야기를 해 주었기 때문이다. 어느 일요일 밤, 어머니가 그 방에서 패거티와 나에게 나사로가 죽음에서 다시 살아난 이야기를 읽어 주었다. 나는 어찌나 무서웠던지, 결국 두 사람이 나를 침대에서 끌어내, 침실 창문으로 고요한 교회 묘지를 보여 주어야 했다. 엄숙한 달빛 아래, 죽은 이들이 모두 제 무덤 속에 편히 누워 있는 그 묘지를.\n\n내가 아는 그 어디에도 그 교회 묘지의 풀만큼 푸른 것은 없고, 그곳 나무들만큼 그늘진 것도 없으며, 그곳 묘비들만큼 고요한 것도 없다. 이른 아침, 어머니 방 안의 골방에 놓인 내 작은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 무릎을 꿇고 그곳을 내다보면, 양 떼가 풀을 뜯고 있다. 그리고 해시계 위로 붉은 햇살이 비치는 것을 보며 나는 속으로 생각한다. '해시계는 다시 시간을 알려 줄 수 있어서 기쁠까?'\n\n여기 교회에 우리 자리가 있다. 등받이가 어찌나 높은 자리인지! 그 곁에는 창문이 있어 우리 집이 내다보이는데, 패거티는 아침 예배 내내 몇 번이고 그 창으로 우리 집을 내다본다. 집에 도둑이 들지는 않았는지, 불이 나지는 않았는지 가능한 한 확실히 해 두고 싶어서다. 그런데 패거티는 제 눈은 여기저기 돌리면서도, 내 눈이 그러면 몹시 못마땅해하며, 자리에 올라선 나에게 목사님을 보라고 눈살을 찌푸린다. 하지만 나는 줄곧 목사님만 볼 수는 없다. 그 하얀 옷을 걸치지 않은 모습으로도 나는 그분을 알아보는데, 내가 왜 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보는지 그분이 의아해하다가, 어쩌면 예배를 멈추고 물어볼까 봐 겁이 난다. 그러니 나는 어쩌면 좋단 말인가? 입을 헤벌리고 있는 건 끔찍한 짓이지만, 무엇이든 하긴 해야 한다. 어머니를 본다. 그런데 어머니는 나를 못 본 척한다. 통로에 있는 사내애를 본다. 그런데 그 애는 나에게 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지어 보인다. 현관을 지나 열린 문으로 들어오는 햇살을 본다. 그러자 거기서 길 잃은 양 한 마리가 — 죄인을 말하는 게 아니라 진짜 양고기 말이다 — 교회 안으로 들어올까 말까 망설이는 모습이 보인다. 내가 그놈을 더 오래 쳐다봤다간 큰 소리로 무슨 말을 내뱉고 싶어질 것만 같다. 그러면 나는 또 어떻게 되겠는가! 벽에 걸린 기념 명판을 올려다보며, 이 교구에 살다 가신 보저스 씨를 떠올려 보려 애쓰고, 보저스 씨가 오랜 세월 모진 병고를 견디고 의사들이 다 헛수고였을 때, 보저스 부인의 심정이 어떠했을지 헤아려 본다. 그분들이 칠립 의사 선생님을 불렀는데도 그분이 헛수고였던 건 아닐까, 그리고 만약 그랬다면, 일주일에 한 번씩 그 일을 떠올려야 하는 게 그분에게 어떤 기분일까 궁금해한다. 일요일용 목도리를 두른 칠립 의사 선생님에게서 설교단으로 눈을 옮기며, 거기가 놀기엔 얼마나 좋은 곳일까, 또 다른 사내애가 계단을 올라와 공격해 오면 그 위에서 술 달린 벨벳 방석을 그 애 머리에 떨어뜨리는, 얼마나 멋진 성채가 될까 하고 생각한다. 이윽고 내 두 눈은 차츰차츰 감기고, 목사님이 그 열기 속에서 졸음 겨운 노래를 부르는 것처럼 들리는가 싶더니, 마침내 아무 소리도 들리지 않다가, 쿵 하고 자리에서 굴러떨어진 뒤에야, 산 것도 죽은 것도 아닌 꼴로 패거티에게 들려 밖으로 나간다.\n\n이제 우리 집 바깥이 보인다. 격자무늬 침실 창문들은 향긋한 바람을 들이려 활짝 열려 있고, 앞뜰 끝의 느릅나무들에는 너덜너덜한 낡은 까마귀 둥지들이 여전히 매달려 있다. 이제 나는 빈 비둘기 집과 개집이 있는 뜰 너머, 뒤뜰 정원에 있다. 내 기억으로 그곳은 나비들의 보금자리나 다름없었는데, 높은 울타리에 문이 달리고 자물쇠가 채워져 있었다. 나무마다 열매가 송이송이 매달려 있었는데, 그 뒤로 다른 어느 정원에서 본 어떤 열매보다도 더 무르익고 탐스러웠다. 어머니가 그 열매 몇 개를 바구니에 따 담는 동안, 나는 그 곁에 서서, 까치밥나무 열매를 몰래 입에 털어 넣으며, 시치미를 뚝 떼려고 애를 쓴다. 큰바람이 일더니, 여름은 순식간에 가 버린다. 우리는 겨울 황혼 속에서 거실을 빙빙 돌며 춤추듯 놀고 있다. 어머니가 숨이 차서 안락의자에 기대 쉴 때면, 나는 어머니가 반짝이는 곱슬머리를 손가락에 돌돌 감고 허리를 곧게 펴는 모습을 지켜본다. 어머니가 그렇게 예쁘게 보이는 걸 좋아하고, 그토록 어여쁜 것을 자랑스러워한다는 사실은, 세상에 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 없다.\n\n그것이 내 가장 이른 기억 가운데 하나다. 그것, 그리고 우리 둘 다 패거티를 조금은 무서워하면서 거의 모든 일에서 그녀의 지시에 따랐다는 느낌, 그것이 내가 본 것에서 끌어낸 — 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 — 최초의 견해 가운데 하나였다.\n\n어느 날 밤, 패거티와 나는 단둘이 거실 난롯가에 앉아 있었다. 나는 패거티에게 악어 이야기를 읽어 주던 참이었다. 내가 무척 또렷하게 읽었거나, 아니면 그 가엾은 사람이 깊이 빠져들었던 게 분명한데, 다 읽고 났을 때 패거티가 악어를 무슨 채소 같은 것으로 어렴풋이 알아들었다는 게 기억나기 때문이다. 나는 읽기에 지쳐 죽도록 졸렸다. 그래도 특별한 호사로, 어머니가 이웃집에서 저녁을 보내고 돌아올 때까지 자지 않고 앉아 있어도 좋다는 허락을 받았으니, 나는 (당연히) 침대로 가느니 차라리 내 자리에서 죽기를 택했을 것이다. 졸음이 한껏 차오른 그 무렵, 패거티는 부풀어 올라 어마어마하게 커지는 것 같았다. 나는 두 집게손가락으로 눈꺼풀을 떠받쳐 억지로 뜨고, 일하느라 앉아 있는 그녀를 끈질기게 바라보았다. 그녀가 실에 쓰려고 챙겨 둔 작은 밀랍 토막을 — 사방으로 어찌나 쪼글쪼글한지 어찌나 낡아 보이던지! — 그리고 줄자가 사는 초가지붕 얹은 작은 집을, 뚜껑에 (분홍빛 둥근 지붕을 얹은) 세인트폴 대성당이 그려진 채 미닫이뚜껑이 달린 반짇고리를, 손가락에 낀 놋쇠 골무를, 그리고 내가 사랑스럽다 여기던 그녀 자신을 바라보았다. 어찌나 졸렸던지, 잠깐이라도 무언가에서 눈을 떼면 나는 끝장이라는 걸 알았다.\n\n\"패거티.\" 나는 불쑥 말했다. \"결혼해 본 적 있어?\"\n\n\"어머나, 데이비 도련님.\" 패거티가 대답했다. \"결혼은 또 무슨 바람이 불어서 머릿속에 들어온 거예요?\"\n\n어찌나 화들짝 놀라며 대답하던지, 나는 그만 잠이 확 달아났다. 그러고는 일손을 멈추고, 바늘을 실 길이만큼 쭉 빼낸 채로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"근데 진짜 결혼해 본 적 있어, 패거티?\" 내가 말했다. \"패거티는 아주 예쁜 여자잖아, 안 그래?\"\n\n물론 나는 그녀가 어머니와는 다른 결의 미인이라고 여겼다. 하지만 또 다른 부류의 아름다움으로 치면, 나는 그녀를 더없이 완벽한 본보기로 생각했다. 제일 좋은 거실에는 붉은 벨벳 발받침이 하나 있었는데, 그 위에 어머니가 꽃다발을 그려 놓았다. 그 발받침의 바탕천과 패거티의 살빛은, 내 눈에 똑같은 것으로 보였다. 발받침은 매끈하고 패거티는 거칠었지만, 그런 건 아무 상관도 없었다.\n\n\"내가 예쁘다고, 데이비?\" 패거티가 말했다. \"어머나, 아니에요, 도련님! 그런데 결혼은 또 무슨 바람이 불어서 머릿속에 들어온 거예요?\"\n\n\"몰라! 근데 한 번에 두 사람하고 결혼하면 안 되는 거지, 안 그래, 패거티?\"\n\n\"당연히 안 되지요.\" 패거티가 한 치의 망설임도 없이 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"근데 어떤 사람하고 결혼했는데 그 사람이 죽으면, 그럼 다른 사람하고 또 결혼해도 되는 거지, 안 그래, 패거티?\"\n\n\"그래도 돼요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"도련님 마음만 정해지면요. 그건 사람마다 생각하기 나름이에요.\"\n\n\"근데 패거티 생각은 어때, 패거티?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n내가 그렇게 물으며 그녀를 궁금하다는 듯 바라본 것은, 그녀가 나를 어찌나 궁금하다는 듯 바라보던지 그래서였다.\n\n\"내 생각은요,\" 패거티가 잠깐 머뭇거리다가 나에게서 눈을 떼고 다시 일을 이어 가며 말했다. \"나는 한 번도 결혼한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 할 일이 없을 거라는 거예요, 데이비 도련님. 이 일에 대해 내가 아는 건 그게 다예요.\"\n\n\"패거티, 화난 건 아니지, 응?\" 잠시 가만히 앉아 있다가 내가 말했다.\n\n나는 정말 그녀가 화난 줄 알았다. 나에게 어찌나 퉁명스럽게 굴던지. 그러나 그건 완전히 내 착각이었다. 패거티가 일감을 (자기 양말이었다) 내려놓고, 두 팔을 활짝 벌려 내 곱슬머리를 그 안에 안고는, 꽉 끌어안아 주었기 때문이다. 그게 꽉 끌어안은 거였다는 건 내가 안다. 그녀는 워낙 통통해서, 옷을 다 입은 뒤에 조금이라도 힘을 쓰면, 가운 등판의 단추 몇 개가 튕겨 나가곤 했기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 나를 껴안는 동안 단추 두 개가 거실 맞은편으로 튀어 날아간 걸 기억한다.\n\n\"자, 그 악악어 얘기 좀 더 들려줘 봐요.\" 아직 이름을 제대로 못 외운 패거티가 말했다. \"반도 못 들은 것 같으니까.\"\n\n나는 패거티가 왜 그렇게 묘한 표정을 짓는지, 왜 그렇게 선뜻 악어 이야기로 돌아가려 하는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 그래도 우리는 그 괴물들에게로 돌아갔고, 나는 정신이 새로 말짱해진 채로 그놈들의 알을 햇볕에 부화하라고 모래밭에 남겨 두었다. 그리고 우리는 그놈들에게서 달아나, 끊임없이 방향을 휙휙 틀어 그놈들을 따돌렸는데, 그놈들은 몸뚱이가 둔해서 빨리 돌지 못했다. 또 우리는 원주민이 되어 그놈들을 따라 물속으로 들어가, 그놈들 목구멍에 날카로운 나무토막을 쑤셔 넣었다. 한마디로 우리는 악어와 관련된 온갖 모험을 다 헤쳐 나갔다. 적어도 나는 그랬다. 그러나 패거티는 그러는 내내 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 제 얼굴과 팔 여기저기에 바늘을 콕콕 찌르고 있었으니, 나는 그녀까지 그랬는지는 의심스럽다.\n\n악어를 다 끝내고 악어붙이로 막 넘어가려는데, 정원의 종이 울렸다. 우리는 문으로 나갔다. 거기 어머니가 있었는데, 내 눈엔 유난히 예뻐 보였다. 그리고 어머니 곁에는 아름다운 검은 머리와 구레나룻을 기른 신사가 있었는데, 지난 일요일에 교회에서 우리와 함께 집까지 걸어온 사람이었다.\n\n어머니가 문지방에서 몸을 숙여 나를 두 팔로 안고 입을 맞추자, 그 신사가 나더러 임금님보다도 더 큰 복을 받은 꼬마라고 — 뭐 그 비슷한 말을 했다. 여기서는 훗날 자라서 깨달은 것이 내 기억을 거든다는 걸 나도 안다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\" 나는 어머니 어깨 너머로 그에게 물었다.\n\n그가 내 머리를 토닥였다. 그런데 어쩐지 나는 그도, 그 굵은 목소리도 마음에 들지 않았고, 나를 만지면서 그의 손이 어머니의 손에 닿는 것이 — 실제로 닿았다 — 질투가 났다. 나는 할 수 있는 한 그 손을 떼어 냈다.\n\n\"어머나, 데이비!\" 어머니가 나무랐다.\n\n\"착하기도 하지!\" 신사가 말했다. \"이렇게 엄마를 끔찍이 위하는 게 어찌 놀랍지 않겠습니까!\"\n\n나는 어머니 얼굴에 그렇게 고운 빛이 도는 걸 그전엔 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 어머니는 내가 버릇없게 군다고 부드럽게 나무라고는, 나를 제 숄에 바싹 끌어안은 채, 집까지 데려다주는 수고를 해 준 신사에게 고맙다는 인사를 하려고 돌아섰다. 그렇게 말하며 어머니가 그에게 손을 내밀자, 그가 자기 손으로 그 손을 맞잡았는데, 그 순간 어머니가 나를 흘끗 보았던 것 같다.\n\n\"그럼 '잘 자거라' 인사하자꾸나, 꼬마 신사.\" 신사가 — 나는 보았다! — 어머니의 작은 장갑 위로 고개를 숙이고 나서 말했다.\n\n\"안녕히 가세요!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자! 우리 세상에서 둘도 없는 친구가 되자꾸나!\" 신사가 웃으며 말했다. \"악수하자!\"\n\n내 오른손은 어머니의 왼손에 쥐여 있어서, 나는 그에게 다른 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"이런, 그건 틀린 손이잖니, 데이비!\" 신사가 웃었다.\n\n어머니가 내 오른손을 앞으로 끌어 주었지만, 나는 앞서 말한 그 까닭으로 그 손을 주지 않기로 마음먹은 터라, 끝내 주지 않았다. 나는 그에게 다른 손을 내밀었고, 그는 그 손을 힘껏 흔들며 나더러 용감한 녀석이라고 하고는 가 버렸다.\n\n지금도 나는 그가 문이 닫히기 전에 정원에서 몸을 돌려, 그 불길한 검은 눈으로 우리를 마지막으로 한 번 바라보던 모습이 보인다.\n\n한마디도 하지 않고 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않던 패거티는 곧장 빗장을 단단히 채웠고, 우리는 모두 거실로 들어갔다. 어머니는 여느 때 버릇과 달리 난롯가 안락의자로 오지 않고, 방 저편 끝에 머물러 혼자 노래를 흥얼거리며 앉아 있었다. \"마님, 즐거운 저녁 보내셨길 바라요.\" 손에 촛대를 든 채 방 한가운데에 술통처럼 빳빳이 선 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 고마워, 패거티.\" 어머니가 명랑한 목소리로 받았다. \"아주 즐거운 저녁이었어.\"\n\n\"낯선 손님이라도 좀 오면 기분 전환이 되긴 하지요.\" 패거티가 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"정말 기분 좋은 전환이었지.\" 어머니가 받았다.\n\n패거티가 계속 방 한가운데 꼼짝 않고 서 있고 어머니가 다시 노래를 흥얼거리는 사이, 나는 잠이 들었다. 그래도 아주 깊이 잠든 건 아니어서, 무슨 말인지는 알아듣지 못해도 목소리는 들을 수 있었다. 이 불편한 선잠에서 반쯤 깨어났을 때, 나는 패거티와 어머니 둘 다 눈물을 흘리며 이야기하고 있는 걸 보았다.\n\n\"이런 사람은, 코퍼필드 나리께서 결코 마음에 들어 하지 않으셨을 거예요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그건 제가 단언하고, 맹세까지 할 수 있어요!\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"너 때문에 미치겠어! 세상에 나만큼 하인한테 못된 꼴 당하는 가엾은 계집애가 또 있을까! 아니, 나 자신을 계집애라 부르다니, 내가 나한테 무슨 부당한 짓을 하는 거람? 나 결혼한 적 없니, 패거티?\"\n\n\"하느님은 아시지요, 마님은 결혼하셨어요.\" 패거티가 받았다. \"그럼 어떻게 네가 감히,\" 어머니가 말했다. \"ㅡ감히라고 한 게 무슨 뜻인지 너도 알지, 패거티. 내 말은, 어떻게 네가 그런 모진 말로 날 이렇게 불편하게 만들 마음이 드느냔 말이야. 이 동네 말고는 기댈 친구 하나 없다는 걸 빤히 알면서!\"\n\n\"바로 그러니까,\" 패거티가 받았다. \"그건 안 될 일이라고 말씀드리는 거예요. 안 돼요! 그건 절대 안 돼요. 안 됩니다! 그 어떤 값을 쳐도 안 될 일이에요. 안 돼요!\" 패거티가 어찌나 촛대를 들고 힘주어 말하던지, 나는 그녀가 그 촛대를 내던질 줄 알았다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 사람 속을 긁어 놓니,\" 어머니가 아까보다 더 많은 눈물을 쏟으며 말했다. \"그렇게 부당하게 말을 하다니! 어떻게 모든 게 다 정해지고 짜여진 일인 양 그러는 거야, 패거티. 이 모진 것아, 내가 몇 번이고 말하잖니, 그저 흔한 예의범절 말고는 아무 일도 없었다고! 흠모니 뭐니 하는데, 그럼 난 어쩌란 말이야? 사람들이 어리석게 그런 마음을 품는다면, 그게 내 잘못이니? 내가 어쩌면 좋겠어, 응? 내가 머리를 박박 밀고 얼굴을 시커멓게 칠하고, 아니면 데이거나 끓는 물에 데어 흉측하게라도 망가뜨려야 속이 시원하겠니? 그래, 너는 그러길 바라겠지, 패거티. 너는 그러면 아주 신날 거야.\"\n\n내가 보기에 패거티는 이 비난을 몹시 마음에 담아 두는 듯했다.\n\n\"그리고 우리 귀한 아들아,\" 어머니가 내가 앉아 있던 안락의자로 다가와 나를 어루만지며 외쳤다. \"내 사랑하는 어린 데이비! 내가 우리 귀한 보물, 세상에 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 우리 꼬마한테 정이 모자란다고, 그런 소리가 내 귀에 들려야 한단 말이니!\"\n\n\"누가 언제 그런 말 한 적도 없는데요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 했잖아, 패거티!\" 어머니가 받았다. \"네가 한 거 너도 알잖니. 이 매정한 것아, 네가 한 말에서 달리 뭘 더 미루어 짐작하란 말이니? 너도 나만큼 잘 알면서. 이 애 때문에 지난 분기에는 새 양산도 안 샀잖아. 그 낡은 초록색 양산은 위쪽까지 죄다 올이 풀리고 술 장식도 다 빠져 너덜너덜한데도 말이야. 너도 알잖아, 패거티. 부인 못 하지.\" 그러더니 다정하게 나에게로 돌아서서, 제 뺨을 내 뺨에 대고 말했다. \"내가 너한테 못된 엄마니, 데이비? 내가 고약하고, 모질고, 제멋대로에, 나쁜 엄마야? 그렇다고 말해 봐, 아가. '응'이라고 말해, 우리 아들. 그러면 패거티가 널 사랑해 줄 거야. 패거티의 사랑이 내 사랑보다 훨씬 나으니까, 데이비. 난 널 조금도 사랑하지 않지, 그렇지?\"\n\n이 말에 우리는 다 같이 울음을 터뜨렸다. 우리 중에서 내가 가장 큰 소리로 울었던 것 같은데, 우리 모두 진심으로 운 것만은 분명하다. 나 자신도 가슴이 찢어질 듯했는데, 상처받은 애틋함이 처음 북받쳐 오른 통에 패거티를 '짐승'이라 불렀던 게 아닌가 싶다. 그 정직한 사람은, 내 기억으로, 깊은 슬픔에 빠져 있었고, 그 바람에 단추가 죄다 떨어져 나갔을 게 분명하다. 어머니와 화해한 뒤 안락의자 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 나와도 화해할 때, 그 작은 폭탄들이 한바탕 터져 나갔으니까.\n\n우리는 몹시 풀이 죽은 채로 잠자리에 들었다. 내 흐느낌은 한참 동안 나를 거듭 깨웠다. 그러다 유난히 큰 흐느낌 하나가 나를 침대에서 벌떡 일으켜 세웠을 때, 나는 어머니가 이불 위에 앉아 나에게 몸을 숙이고 있는 걸 보았다. 그러고 나서 나는 어머니 품에 안겨 잠이 들었고, 곤히 잤다.\n\n그 신사를 다시 본 것이 그다음 일요일이었는지, 아니면 그가 다시 나타나기까지 더 긴 시간이 흘렀는지, 나는 떠올리지 못한다. 날짜를 또렷이 안다고 우길 생각은 없다. 어쨌든 그는 교회에 있었고, 예배가 끝난 뒤 우리와 함께 집까지 걸어왔다. 그는 거실 창가에 둔 우리 집의 멋진 제라늄을 보겠다며 안으로 들어오기까지 했다. 내 보기엔 그가 그 꽃에 별 관심을 두는 것 같지 않았는데, 가기 전에 어머니에게 그 꽃송이를 한 조각 달라고 했다. 어머니가 직접 골라 보라고 권했지만, 그는 한사코 마다했다. 나는 왜 그러는지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그래서 어머니가 그에게 꺾어서 손에 쥐여 주었다. 그는 이 꽃을 절대로, 절대로 손에서 놓지 않겠다고 했는데, 나는 그게 하루 이틀이면 바스러져 버린다는 걸 모르다니 어지간한 바보로구나 싶었다.\n\n패거티는 저녁이면 늘 그래 왔던 것보다 우리와 함께 있는 시간이 점점 줄어들었다. 어머니는 패거티를 무척이나 — 내가 보기엔 평소보다 더 — 깍듯이 대했고, 우리 셋은 더없이 좋은 친구였다. 그래도 우리는 예전 같지 않았고, 우리끼리 있어도 그리 편치가 않았다. 나는 이따금 패거티가 어머니가 서랍 속 예쁜 옷들을 죄다 꺼내 입는 것이나, 그 이웃집에 자주 마실 가는 것을 못마땅해하는 게 아닐까 짐작했지만, 그게 어찌 된 영문인지 속 시원히 알아낼 수는 없었다.\n\n차츰 나는 그 검은 구레나룻 신사를 보는 데 익숙해졌다. 그래도 처음보다 그가 더 좋아지지는 않았고, 여전히 그를 향한 그 불편한 질투를 느꼈다. 하지만 어린아이의 본능적인 반감과, 패거티와 나 둘이면 누구의 도움 없이도 어머니를 떠받들 수 있다는 막연한 생각 말고 거기에 다른 까닭이 있었다 해도, 그건 결코 내가 좀 더 컸다면 찾아냈을 법한 그 까닭은 아니었다. 그런 생각은 내 마음에 떠오른 적도, 근처에 얼씬한 적도 없었다. 나는 말하자면 자잘한 조각들을 하나하나 관찰할 수는 있었다. 그러나 그 여러 조각들로 그물을 엮어 누군가를 거기 옭아넣는 일은, 아직 나에겐 능력 밖이었다.\n\n어느 가을 아침, 나는 앞뜰에서 어머니와 함께 있었는데, 머드스톤 씨가 — 이제 나는 그를 그 이름으로 알았다 — 말을 타고 지나갔다. 그는 말고삐를 당겨 어머니에게 인사하고는, 요트를 타고 거기 와 있는 친구들을 만나러 로스토프트에 가는 길이라고 하더니, 나더러 말 타는 게 좋으면 자기 앞쪽 안장에 태워 주겠다고 명랑하게 제안했다.\n\n공기가 어찌나 맑고 상쾌하던지, 그리고 말도 정원 문 앞에서 콧김을 내뿜고 발을 구르며 그 나들이를 무척 반기는 것 같아서, 나는 가고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다. 그래서 나는 패거티에게 올라가 말끔하게 단장을 했고, 그동안 머드스톤 씨는 말에서 내려, 고삐를 팔에 걸친 채 들장미 울타리 바깥쪽을 천천히 오르락내리락 거닐었고, 어머니는 안쪽에서 그와 짝을 맞춰 천천히 오르락내리락 거닐었다. 나는 패거티와 내가 내 작은 창문으로 두 사람을 엿보던 게 기억난다. 두 사람이 거니는 동안 사이에 놓인 들장미를 어찌나 골똘히 살피는 것 같던지, 그리고 더없이 천사 같던 패거티가 순식간에 뾰로통해져서, 내 머리를 거꾸로, 그것도 사납도록 세게 빗질하던 게 기억난다.\n\n머드스톤 씨와 나는 곧 길을 나서, 길가 푸른 잔디 위를 달가닥달가닥 달렸다. 그는 한쪽 팔만으로도 나를 거뜬히 붙들었는데, 나는 평소엔 가만히 못 있는 아이가 아니었던 것 같은데도, 그의 앞에 앉아 이따금 고개를 돌려 그의 얼굴을 올려다보지 않고는 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다. 그는 그런 부류의 얕은 검은 눈을 가지고 있었는데 — 들여다볼 깊이가 없는 눈을 가리키기엔 더 나은 말이 있으면 좋겠다 — 그 눈은 멍하니 딴생각에 잠기면, 빛의 어떤 묘한 조화 탓인지, 한순간씩 사팔뜨기처럼 일그러져 보였다. 그를 흘끗거릴 때마다 나는 그 모습을 일종의 두려움을 품고 바라보며, 그가 무슨 생각을 그토록 골똘히 하는지 궁금해했다. 이렇게 가까이서 보니 그의 머리와 구레나룻은 내가 여태 짐작했던 것보다도 더 검고 더 짙었다. 얼굴 아래쪽의 각진 윤곽과, 날마다 바짝 깎아 내는 짙고 억센 수염이 점점이 비치는 자국은, 한 반년쯤 전에 우리 동네로 흘러들었던 밀랍 인형을 떠올리게 했다. 이것, 그리고 그의 반듯한 눈썹과, 희고 검고 갈색이 어우러진 짙은 살빛은 — 그 살빛도, 그 기억도 다 망할 것! — 나로 하여금, 그 켕기는 마음에도 불구하고, 그를 아주 잘생긴 남자라고 여기게 했다. 내 가엾고 사랑하는 어머니도 그를 그렇게 여겼으리라는 데에는 나는 한 점 의심이 없다.\n\n우리는 바닷가의 어느 호텔로 갔는데, 거기서 신사 둘이 한 방에 따로 들어앉아 시가를 피우고 있었다. 둘 다 의자를 적어도 네 개씩 깔고 드러누워, 크고 거친 재킷을 걸치고 있었다. 한쪽 구석에는 외투며 뱃사람 망토며 깃발이 한데 뒤엉켜 수북이 쌓여 있었다.\n\n우리가 들어가자 둘 다 부스스 몸을 일으키더니 말했다. \"어이, 머드스톤! 우린 자네가 죽은 줄 알았네!\"\n\n\"아직은 아니라네.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"근데 이 꼬마는 누군가?\" 신사 하나가 나를 붙잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"데이비라네.\" 머드스톤 씨가 받았다.\n\n\"데이비 누구?\" 신사가 말했다. \"존스인가?\"\n\n\"코퍼필드라네.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 그 매혹적인 코퍼필드 부인의 짐짝 말인가?\" 신사가 외쳤다. \"그 어여쁜 젊은 과부 말일세?\"\n\n\"퀴니언,\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"제발 말조심하게. 영리한 녀석이 있으니.\"\n\n\"누가?\" 신사가 웃으며 물었다. 나는 궁금해서 얼른 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"셰필드의 브룩스 말일세.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다.\n\n나는 그게 그저 셰필드의 브룩스라는 걸 알고 무척 마음을 놓았다. 처음엔 그게 정말 나인 줄 알았으니까.\n\n셰필드의 브룩스 씨라는 평판에는 무언가 몹시 우스운 구석이 있는 모양이었다. 그 이름이 나오자 두 신사가 다 같이 껄껄 웃었고, 머드스톤 씨도 자못 재미있어했으니까. 한바탕 웃고 나서, 그가 퀴니언이라고 부른 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그 계획 중인 일에 대해서 셰필드의 브룩스 씨 의견은 어떻다나?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 브룩스가 지금은 그 일을 잘 이해하는 것 같진 않네만,\" 머드스톤 씨가 받았다. \"내 보기에 대체로 탐탁잖아하는 눈치라네.\"\n\n이 말에 또 웃음이 터졌고, 퀴니언 씨는 브룩스를 위해 건배할 셰리주를 가져오라고 종을 울리겠다고 했다. 그가 그렇게 했고, 술이 나오자 그는 나에게도 비스킷과 함께 조금 마시게 하고는, 마시기 전에 일어서서 \"셰필드의 브룩스에게 파멸을!\" 하고 외치게 했다. 이 건배는 우레와 같은 박수와, 어찌나 호탕한 웃음으로 맞아졌던지 나까지 웃음이 나왔고, 그러자 그들은 더 크게 웃었다. 한마디로 우리는 아주 즐거운 시간을 보냈다.\n\n그 뒤에 우리는 절벽 위를 거닐다가, 풀밭에 앉아, 망원경으로 이것저것 구경했다. 망원경을 내 눈에 대어 주었을 때 나는 아무것도 알아볼 수 없었지만, 알아보는 척했다. 그러고 나서 우리는 호텔로 돌아와 이른 저녁을 먹었다. 우리가 밖에 나가 있는 내내 두 신사는 쉴 새 없이 담배를 피워 댔는데, 그 거친 재킷 냄새로 미루어 짐작하자면, 그 재킷이 처음 양복점에서 나온 그날부터 줄곧 그래 온 게 분명했다. 우리가 요트에 올라탔던 일도 빠뜨려선 안 되겠다. 거기서 셋은 모두 선실로 내려가 무슨 서류를 들고 분주했다. 열린 천창으로 내려다보니 그들이 한창 열심히 일하는 게 보였다. 그들은 그동안 나를 아주 친절한 사람에게 맡겨 두었는데, 그 사람은 머리가 아주 크고 붉은 머리카락이 수북했으며, 그 위에 아주 작고 반들거리는 모자를 얹고 있었다. 그는 격자무늬 셔츠인지 조끼인지를 입었는데, 가슴팍에 대문자로 '스카이라크'라고 적혀 있었다. 나는 그게 그의 이름인 줄 알았다. 그가 배 위에서 사니까 제 이름을 내걸 길거리 대문이 없어서, 대신 거기 적어 둔 거려니 했다. 그런데 내가 그를 스카이라크 씨라고 부르자, 그는 그게 배 이름이라고 했다.\n\n나는 하루 종일 머드스톤 씨가 두 신사보다 더 무겁고 진중하다는 걸 눈여겨보았다. 그 둘은 아주 들떠 있고 무심했다. 그들은 서로 거리낌 없이 농을 주고받았지만, 그에게는 좀처럼 그러지 않았다. 내 보기에 그는 그들보다 더 영리하고 차가웠고, 그들도 나와 비슷한 감정으로 그를 대하는 것 같았다. 나는 한두 번, 퀴니언 씨가 이야기하다가, 그가 언짢아하지는 않는지 확인하려는 듯 머드스톤 씨를 곁눈질하는 걸 보았다. 또 한 번은 패스니지 씨(다른 신사다)가 한껏 들떠 있을 때, 퀴니언 씨가 그의 발을 밟고는, 엄숙히 입을 다물고 앉아 있는 머드스톤 씨를 잘 살피라고 눈짓으로 슬며시 주의를 주는 것도 보았다. 그리고 그날 머드스톤 씨가 웃은 적이 있었던가 싶은데, 셰필드 농담 때 빼고는 도무지 기억나지 않는다. 그나저나 그 농담은 본디 그가 만든 것이었다.\n\n우리는 저녁 일찍 집으로 돌아왔다. 아주 좋은 저녁이었고, 어머니와 그는 들장미 곁을 또 한 번 거닐었으며, 그동안 나는 차를 마시러 안으로 들여보내졌다. 그가 떠난 뒤, 어머니는 그날 하루를 어떻게 보냈는지, 그들이 무슨 말을 하고 무얼 했는지 나에게 죄다 캐물었다. 나는 그들이 어머니에 대해 한 말을 들려주었고, 어머니는 웃으며 그들이 허튼소리나 늘어놓는 뻔뻔한 자들이라고 했다. 하지만 나는 그게 어머니를 기쁘게 했다는 걸 알았다. 지금 아는 것만큼이나 똑똑히 알았다. 나는 이참에 어머니가 셰필드의 브룩스 씨를 조금이라도 아는지 물어보았는데, 어머니는 아니라며, 다만 그가 나이프와 포크 만드는 일을 하는 제조업자이려니 짐작할 뿐이라고 대답했다.\n\n내가 어머니의 얼굴을 두고 — 내가 떠올릴 수밖에 없는 그 변해 버린 얼굴, 이제는 스러져 버린 줄 아는 그 얼굴을 두고 — 그것이 사라졌다고 말할 수 있을까? 지금 이 순간, 붐비는 거리에서 내가 골라 바라볼 어떤 얼굴 못지않게 또렷이 내 앞에 떠오르는데? 내가 어머니의 그 순결하고 소녀 같은 아름다움을 두고, 그것이 시들어 더는 없다고 말할 수 있을까? 그날 밤 그랬듯이 지금도 그 숨결이 내 뺨에 와 닿는데? 내가 어머니가 변했다고 말할 수 있을까? 내 기억이 오직 이렇게 어머니를 되살려 내는데. 그리고 내가 그래 왔던 것보다, 또 누구라도 그러는 것보다 그 사랑스러운 젊음에 더 진실하게, 그 시절 소중히 품었던 것을 여태 굳게 붙들고 있는데.\n\n나는 어머니를, 이 이야기를 나눈 뒤 내가 잠자리에 들고 어머니가 잘 자라는 인사를 하러 왔을 때 그 모습 그대로 적는다. 어머니는 장난스럽게 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 두 손에 턱을 괴고 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람들이 뭐라고 했다고, 데이비? 다시 말해 봐. 도무지 못 믿겠으니까.\"\n\n\"'매혹적인—'\" 내가 입을 떼었다.\n\n어머니가 두 손을 내 입술에 대어 막았다.\n\n\"매혹적이라니, 그럴 리가.\" 어머니가 웃으며 말했다. \"그게 매혹적이었을 리 없어, 데이비. 자, 이제 알겠다, 그런 게 아니었어!\"\n\n\"맞아, 그랬어. '매혹적인 코퍼필드 부인.'\" 나는 꿋꿋이 되풀이했다. \"그리고 '어여쁘다'고도.\"\n\n\"아니야, 아니야, 어여쁘다니 그럴 리 없어. 어여쁘긴.\" 어머니가 다시 내 입술에 손가락을 대며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"맞아, 그랬어. '어여쁜 젊은 과부.'\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 어리석고 뻔뻔한 사람들이람!\" 어머니가 웃으며 얼굴을 가리고 외쳤다. \"어쩜 그런 우스꽝스러운 사람들이! 안 그러니? 데이비야—\"\n\n\"네, 엄마.\"\n\n\"패거티한테는 말하지 마. 그 애가 그 사람들한테 화낼지도 모르니까. 나도 그 사람들한테 끔찍이 화가 나지만, 그래도 패거티는 모르는 게 낫겠어.\"\n\n나는 물론 그러겠다고 약속했고, 우리는 몇 번이고 거듭 입을 맞추었으며, 나는 이내 곤히 잠들었다.\n\n이렇게 오랜 세월이 지난 지금 돌이켜 보면, 패거티가 이제 이야기할 그 놀랍고도 모험 가득한 제안을 꺼낸 것이 바로 그다음 날 같지만, 아마 두어 달쯤 뒤였을 것이다.\n\n어느 날 저녁, 우리는 (어머니가 또 외출한 사이) 여느 때처럼 양말과 줄자와 밀랍 토막과, 뚜껑에 세인트폴 대성당이 그려진 반짇고리와, 악어 책을 곁에 두고 앉아 있었다. 그때 패거티가 나를 몇 번이나 바라보고, 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 입을 벌렸다가는 끝내 다물기를 거듭하더니 — 나는 그게 그냥 하품인 줄 알았으니 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 적잖이 놀랐을 것이다 — 살살 구슬리듯 말했다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님, 저랑 같이 야머스에 있는 우리 오라버니 댁에 가서 두 주쯤 지내다 오면 어떨까요? 그거 참 신나지 않겠어요?\"\n\n\"패거티네 오라버니는 좋은 사람이야, 패거티?\" 나는 일단 따져 물었다.\n\n\"어머나, 얼마나 좋은 사람인데요!\" 패거티가 두 손을 번쩍 들며 외쳤다. \"게다가 바다도 있고요. 보트랑 배도 있고, 어부들도 있고, 모래밭도 있고, 그리고 함하고 같이 놀 수도—\"\n\n패거티가 말한 건 첫 장에서 이야기한 조카 함이었다. 그런데 그 이름을 어찌나 짧게 끊어 말하던지 꼭 영문법 토막 같았다.\n\n나는 그녀가 줄줄이 늘어놓은 즐거움에 얼굴이 화끈 달아올라, 정말 신날 것 같다고 대답하면서도, 어머니가 뭐라고 할까 싶다고 했다.\n\n\"그럼 내 일 기니를 걸어도 좋아요,\" 패거티가 내 얼굴을 골똘히 들여다보며 말했다. \"마님이 우릴 보내 주실 거예요. 도련님만 좋다면, 마님이 돌아오시는 대로 바로 여쭤볼게요. 자, 어때요!\"\n\n\"근데 우리가 가 있는 동안 엄만 어떡해?\" 나는 그 점을 따져 보려고 작은 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴며 말했다. \"엄만 혼자 못 사는데.\"\n\n만약 패거티가 그 순간 양말 뒤꿈치에 난 구멍을 찾고 있던 거라면, 그건 기워 봐야 별 소용도 없을 만큼 아주 작은 구멍이었을 게 분명하다.\n\n\"있잖아! 패거티! 엄만 혼자 못 산다니까.\"\n\n\"어머나, 세상에!\" 마침내 패거티가 다시 나를 보며 말했다. \"도련님은 모르세요? 마님은 그레이퍼 부인 댁에서 두 주 동안 지내실 거예요. 그레이퍼 부인 댁에 손님이 많이 온대요.\"\n\n아! 그게 그렇다면야, 나는 갈 채비가 다 된 셈이었다. 나는 어머니가 그레이퍼 부인 댁에서 (바로 그 이웃이었다) 돌아올 때까지, 이 위대한 계획을 실행에 옮길 허락을 받을 수 있을지 알아보려고 애가 타게 기다렸다. 어머니는 내가 예상했던 만큼 그리 놀라지도 않고 선뜻 찬성했고, 모든 것이 그날 밤 매듭지어졌으며, 머무는 동안의 내 숙식비도 치르기로 했다.\n\n떠날 날이 곧 다가왔다. 그토록 가까운 날이라, 기대로 들떠 안달이 나서, 지진이나 불타는 산, 아니면 무슨 어마어마한 천재지변이라도 끼어들어 이 원정을 가로막지나 않을까 반쯤 겁먹고 있던 나에게도 그날은 금세 왔다. 우리는 짐마차를 타고 가기로 했는데, 아침 식사 뒤에 출발하는 마차였다. 나는 전날 밤부터 옷을 둘둘 차려입고, 모자와 장화까지 신은 채로 자도 좋다는 허락만 받을 수 있다면, 얼마든지 돈을 냈을 것이다.\n\n지금 이 일을 가볍게 이야기하고 있지만, 내가 그 행복한 보금자리를 떠나려고 얼마나 안달했는지를, 내가 영영 두고 떠나는 것이 무엇인지를 얼마나 까맣게 몰랐는지를 떠올리면, 지금도 가슴이 저민다.\n\n짐마차가 문 앞에 와 있고, 어머니가 거기 서서 나에게 입을 맞출 때, 어머니에 대한, 그리고 한 번도 등진 적 없던 그 정든 집에 대한 고마운 애틋함이 북받쳐 내가 울었던 것을 떠올리면 마음이 놓인다. 어머니도 울었다는 것, 그리고 어머니의 심장이 내 심장에 맞대어 뛰는 걸 내가 느꼈다는 것을 떠올리면 마음이 놓인다.\n\n짐마차가 막 움직이기 시작했을 때, 어머니가 문밖으로 뛰어나와 마차를 세우라고 외쳤던 것을 떠올리면 마음이 놓인다. 나에게 한 번 더 입 맞추려고. 어머니가 얼마나 간절하고 애틋하게 제 얼굴을 내 얼굴로 들어 올려, 그렇게 입을 맞추었는지를 곱씹으면 마음이 놓인다.\n\n우리가 어머니를 길에 남겨 두고 떠날 때, 머드스톤 씨가 어머니가 선 자리로 다가오더니, 그렇게 마음을 쏟는다고 어머니를 나무라는 듯했다. 나는 마차 차양 너머로 뒤를 돌아보며, 그게 무슨 자기 일이라고 저러나 싶었다. 반대편에서 함께 뒤를 돌아보던 패거티도, 마차로 다시 돌려놓은 그 얼굴로 미루어 보아, 영 못마땅한 기색이었다.\n\n나는 한참 동안 패거티를 바라보며, 이런 가정에 잠겨 공상에 빠졌다. 만약 동화 속 사내애처럼 그녀가 나를 내다 버리는 일을 맡는다면, 그녀가 흘리고 다닐 단추를 따라 내가 다시 집으로 길을 더듬어 찾아올 수 있을까 하는 공상에.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. I HAVE A CHANGE\n\n짐꾼의 말은 내가 보기에 세상에서 가장 게으른 말이었지 싶다. 그 말은 머리를 푹 숙인 채 발을 질질 끌며 걸었는데, 마치 짐을 받을 사람들을 일부러 기다리게 하는 걸 즐기기라도 하는 듯했다. 사실 나는 그 말이 이따금 그런 생각에 들떠 소리 내어 킥킥거리는 것 같기도 했지만, 짐꾼은 그저 기침을 좀 앓을 뿐이라고 했다. 짐꾼도 제 말처럼 머리를 푹 숙이는 버릇이 있었고, 마차를 몰 때면 두 팔을 양쪽 무릎에 하나씩 얹은 채 졸린 듯 앞으로 축 늘어졌다. '몰았다'고는 했지만, 내가 보기에 그 사람이 없어도 마차는 똑같이 야머스까지 잘 갔을 것이다. 그 일은 죄다 말이 했으니까. 그리고 대화로 말하자면, 짐꾼은 휘파람 부는 것 말고는 그런 걸 할 줄도 몰랐다.\n\n패거티는 먹을거리가 담긴 바구니를 무릎에 올려놓고 있었는데, 우리가 이 마차로 런던까지 갔더라도 넉넉히 버틸 만한 양이었다. 우리는 꽤 많이 먹었고, 꽤 많이 잤다. 패거티는 늘 바구니 손잡이에 턱을 괸 채 잠들었는데, 그 손잡이를 쥔 손은 한 번도 풀어진 적이 없었다. 그리고 직접 듣지 않았더라면, 무방비 상태의 여인 하나가 그렇게 요란하게 코를 골 수 있다는 걸 결코 믿지 못했을 것이다.\n\n우리는 골목을 따라 위로 아래로 어찌나 많이 돌아갔던지, 게다가 어느 선술집에 침대틀 하나를 내려놓는 데 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 또 다른 곳들에도 들렀던 탓에, 나는 완전히 지쳐 버렸고, 야머스가 눈에 들어왔을 때 무척 기뻤다. 강 건너편에 펼쳐진 그 크고 황량한 벌판으로 눈길을 던지며, 나는 그곳이 어쩐지 물기 머금어 질척하고 흐물흐물해 보인다고 생각했다. 그리고 세상이 정말로 내 지리책 말처럼 둥글다면, 어떻게 그 일부분이 이토록 평평할 수 있는지 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다. 하지만 곰곰이 생각해 보니 야머스는 극지방 어느 한 곳에 자리한 것인지도 몰랐고, 그렇다면 설명이 되긴 했다.\n\n조금 더 가까이 다가가, 둘레의 온 풍경이 하늘 아래 곧고 나지막한 선을 그리며 누워 있는 것을 보자, 나는 패거티에게 둔덕이라도 몇 개 있었으면 좀 나아 보였을 거라고 슬쩍 말했다. 그리고 땅과 바다가 조금만 더 떨어져 있고, 마을과 밀물이 토스트와 물처럼 그렇게까지 뒤섞여 있지 않았더라면 더 보기 좋았을 거라고도 했다. 그러나 패거티는 평소보다 더 힘주어, 세상일은 있는 그대로 받아들여야 하는 법이며, 자기로 말하자면 스스로를 야머스 청어라 부르는 것이 자랑스럽다고 했다.\n\n우리가 (나에게는 무척 낯설었던) 거리로 들어서서 생선과 송진과 뱃밥과 타르 냄새를 맡고, 뱃사람들이 이리저리 걸어 다니는 것과 짐수레들이 자갈길 위로 덜그럭거리며 오르내리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 이렇게 부산한 곳을 내가 너무 박하게 봤다는 느낌이 들었다. 그래서 패거티에게 그렇게 말했더니, 패거티는 내 감탄을 무척 흐뭇하게 들으며, 야머스가 전체적으로 보아 온 우주에서 가장 멋진 곳이라는 건 (청어로 태어나는 행운을 누린 사람들 사이에서는) 익히 알려진 사실이라고 했다.\n\n\"우리 햄이 저기 있네!\" 패거티가 비명을 질렀다. \"몰라보게 컸어!\"\n\n햄은 정말로 선술집에서 우리를 기다리고 있었는데, 오랜 친구라도 되는 양 내게 그동안 어떻게 지냈느냐고 물었다. 나는 처음엔 그가 나를 아는 만큼 내가 그를 잘 안다는 느낌이 들지 않았다. 그는 내가 태어난 날 밤 이후로 우리 집에 한 번도 온 적이 없었으니, 당연히 그가 나보다 유리한 처지였던 것이다. 하지만 그가 나를 등에 업어 집까지 데려가면서 우리의 친밀함은 부쩍 깊어졌다. 그는 이제 키가 육 척에 그에 걸맞게 떡 벌어진, 어깨가 둥글게 굽은 거대하고 힘센 사내였다. 그러나 실실 웃는 듯한 소년 같은 얼굴에 곱슬곱슬한 연한 머리카락이 그를 영락없이 어수룩해 보이게 했다. 그는 캔버스 천 윗옷에, 어찌나 빳빳한지 다리를 넣지 않아도 혼자서 그대로 서 있을 법한 바지를 입고 있었다. 그리고 그가 모자를 썼다기보다는, 낡은 건물처럼 머리 꼭대기가 뭔가 송진 같은 것으로 덮여 있었다고 하는 편이 더 맞을 것이다.\n\n햄이 나를 등에 업고 우리 작은 상자 하나를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 패거티가 또 다른 작은 상자를 들고서, 우리는 나무 부스러기와 자그마한 모래 둔덕이 흩뿌려진 골목길로 접어들었다. 그러고는 가스 공장과 밧줄 공장, 보트 만드는 작업장, 배 짓는 작업장, 배 부수는 작업장, 뱃밥 메우는 작업장, 삭구 다루는 다락, 대장간, 그리고 그 비슷한 곳들이 잔뜩 어질러진 사이를 지나, 마침내 내가 멀리서 이미 보았던 그 황량한 벌판으로 나왔다. 그러자 햄이 말했다.\n\n\"저게 우리 집이에요, 데이비 도련님!\"\n\n나는 그 벌판 위로 눈에 닿는 데까지 사방을 둘러보고, 멀리 바다 쪽도 보고, 멀리 강 쪽도 보았지만, 집이라고는 도무지 찾아낼 수가 없었다. 멀지 않은 곳에 검은 거룻배, 아니 무슨 한물간 배 같은 것이 땅 위에 높직이 마른 채로 놓여 있었고, 거기서 굴뚝 삼은 쇠 연통이 삐죽 솟아 무척 아늑하게 연기를 피워 올리고 있었다. 그러나 사람이 사는 곳이라고 할 만한 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 내 눈에 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"저건 아니지?\" 내가 말했다. \"저 배처럼 생긴 거 말이야?\"\n\n\"저게 맞아요, 데이비 도련님.\" 햄이 대답했다.\n\n설령 그것이 알라딘의 궁전이고, 거기 로크 새의 알까지 다 있었다 한들, 나는 그 안에 산다는 낭만적인 생각에 그보다 더 홀딱 반할 수는 없었지 싶다. 옆구리에 잘라 낸 멋진 문이 하나 있었고, 지붕이 덮여 있었으며, 자그마한 창문들도 달려 있었다. 그러나 그 놀라운 매력은, 그것이 분명 수백 번은 물 위에 떠 있었을 진짜 배이며, 마른 땅 위에서 사람이 살라고 만든 물건이 결코 아니라는 데 있었다. 바로 그것이 나를 사로잡았다. 만약 그것이 애초에 사람 살라고 지은 것이었다면, 나는 비좁다거나 불편하다거나 외롭다거나 했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그런 쓰임새로 만든 것이 전혀 아니었기에, 그것은 더없이 완벽한 보금자리가 되었다.\n\n안쪽은 아름답도록 깨끗했고, 더할 나위 없이 정갈했다. 탁자가 하나, 네덜란드제 시계가 하나, 서랍장이 하나 있었고, 서랍장 위에는 차 쟁반이 하나 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에는 양산을 든 부인이 굴렁쇠를 굴리는 군인처럼 차려입은 아이와 함께 산책하는 그림이 그려져 있었다. 그 쟁반이 굴러떨어지지 않도록 받쳐 주는 것은 성경책 한 권이었다. 만약 쟁반이 굴러떨어졌더라면, 그 책 둘레에 모여 있던 찻잔과 받침 접시 한 무더기와 찻주전자가 죄다 박살이 났을 것이다. 벽에는 성경 이야기를 그린, 액자에 끼우고 유리를 댄 흔한 색채화 몇 점이 걸려 있었다. 그 뒤로 행상들 손에 든 그런 그림을 볼 때마다, 나는 어김없이 패거티 오라버니네 집 안 전체를 한눈에 다시 떠올리게 되곤 했다. 그중에서도 빨간 옷을 입고 파란 옷을 입은 이삭을 제물로 바치러 가는 아브라함, 그리고 노란 옷을 입고 초록빛 사자 굴에 던져진 다니엘이 가장 눈에 띄었다. 자그마한 벽난로 선반 위에는 선덜랜드에서 만든 '세라 제인'호 외돛배 그림이 걸려 있었는데, 거기에는 진짜 나무로 만든 작은 고물이 떡하니 붙어 있었다. 그것은 그림과 목공을 한데 어우른 예술품으로, 나는 세상이 베풀 수 있는 가장 부러운 소유물 가운데 하나라고 여겼다. 천장 들보에는 갈고리 몇 개가 박혀 있었는데, 그때는 그 쓰임새를 짐작도 못 했다. 그리고 의자 노릇을 하며 모자라는 의자를 메워 주는 사물함이며 상자며 그런 따위의 편의용 물건들도 몇 개 있었다.\n\n문턱을 넘자마자 첫눈에 이 모든 것을 다 보았는데 — 내 지론대로 아이답게 말이다 — 그러고 나서 패거티가 작은 문 하나를 열고 내 침실을 보여 주었다. 그것은 여태 본 가운데 가장 완벽하고 더없이 탐나는 침실로, 배의 고물 쪽에 있었다. 예전에 방향타가 지나던 자리에는 자그마한 창문이 하나 있었고, 딱 내 키에 맞는 작은 거울이 굴 껍데기로 테를 둘러 벽에 못 박혀 있었으며, 겨우 들어가 누울 만한 작은 침대가 하나, 그리고 탁자 위 파란 머그잔에는 해초를 꽂은 꽃다발이 놓여 있었다. 벽은 우유처럼 새하얗게 회칠이 되어 있었고, 조각보를 이은 침대보는 어찌나 화사한지 눈이 다 아릴 지경이었다. 이 즐거운 집에서 내가 특히 눈여겨본 것 한 가지는 생선 냄새였는데, 어찌나 속속들이 배어들었던지, 코를 닦으려고 주머니 손수건을 꺼냈을 때 영락없이 바닷가재라도 싸 두었던 것처럼 그 냄새가 났다. 이 발견을 패거티에게 가만히 털어놓았더니, 패거티는 제 오라버니가 바닷가재며 게며 가재 따위를 다룬다고 알려 주었다. 그리고 나는 나중에야, 그 짐승들이 한 무더기로 서로 놀랍도록 뒤엉킨 채, 무엇이든 붙잡으면 쉴 새 없이 집게로 집어 대면서, 냄비며 솥을 보관하는 작은 나무 헛간 안에 으레 모여 있다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n우리는 하얀 앞치마를 두른 무척 예의 바른 아낙의 환대를 받았는데, 내가 햄의 등에 업혀 사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 있을 때 문간에서 무릎을 굽혀 인사하던 그 여인이었다. 또 무척 어여쁜 (적어도 내 눈에는 그렇게 보인) 어린 여자아이도 우리를 맞아 주었는데, 파란 구슬 목걸이를 하고 있었고, 내가 입맞춤을 하려 했더니 마다하며 달아나 숨어 버렸다. 이윽고 우리가 삶은 가자미와 녹인 버터, 감자로, 게다가 나에게는 따로 고기 토막까지 곁들여 호화롭게 식사를 마쳤을 무렵, 무척 사람 좋아 보이는 얼굴에 털북숭이인 남자가 집으로 돌아왔다. 그가 패거티를 '아가씨'라 부르며 뺨에 정겹게 쪽 입을 맞추는 데다, 패거티가 줄곧 점잖게 처신하는 것으로 미루어, 나는 그가 패거티의 오라버니임을 의심치 않았다. 과연 그러했으니, 이내 그는 이 집의 주인 패거티 씨라고 내게 소개되었다.\n\n\"만나서 반가워요, 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"여긴 거칠다고 느낄 거예요, 도련님. 하지만 채비만큼은 야무지다고 느낄 거예요.\"\n\n나는 고맙다고 인사하고는, 이렇게 즐거운 곳에서라면 틀림없이 행복할 것 같다고 대답했다.\n\n\"어머님은 어떠세요, 도련님?\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"꽤 명랑하게 잘 계시던가요?\"\n\n나는 어머니가 더 바랄 나위 없이 명랑하시며, 안부를 전하더라고 패거티 씨에게 일러 주었다 — 사실은 내 쪽에서 점잖게 지어낸 거짓말이었다.\n\n\"정말 고마우신 말씀이네요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그럼, 도련님, 여기서 보름쯤, 우리 누이랑,\" 누이동생을 향해 고갯짓을 하며, \"그리고 햄이랑 어린 에밀리랑 같이 잘 지내 주신다면, 우린 도련님과 함께한 걸 자랑스레 여길 거예요.\"\n\n이렇게 손님 대접하는 인사치레를 후하게 마치고 나서, 패거티 씨는 솥 가득한 뜨거운 물로 몸을 씻으러 밖으로 나가며, \"찬물로는 이 때가 절대 안 벗겨진다\"고 한마디 했다. 그는 곧 몰라보게 말끔해진 모습으로 돌아왔는데, 어찌나 벌건지, 나는 그의 얼굴이 바닷가재며 게며 가재와 한 가지 닮은 점이 있다는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었다 — 새카만 채로 뜨거운 물에 들어갔다가 시뻘게져 나온다는 점 말이다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤, 문이 닫히고 모든 것이 아늑해지자 (이제 밤은 춥고 안개가 끼었다), 그곳은 인간의 상상이 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 황홀한 은신처처럼 느껴졌다. 바다 저편에서 바람이 일어나는 소리를 듣고, 바깥 황량한 벌판 위로 안개가 슬금슬금 기어드는 것을 알면서, 난롯불을 바라보며, 이 집 말고는 근처에 집 한 채 없고, 게다가 이 집이 배라는 걸 떠올리는 것은 마치 마법 같았다. 어린 에밀리는 수줍음을 떨쳐 내고, 가장 낮고 가장 작은 사물함 위에 내 곁에 앉아 있었는데, 그것은 딱 우리 둘이 앉을 만한 크기였고 벽난로 모퉁이에 꼭 맞게 들어앉아 있었다. 하얀 앞치마를 두른 거미지 부인은 난로 맞은편에서 뜨개질을 하고 있었다. 패거티는 바느질거리를 앞에 두고서, 성 바울 성당이 그려진 반짇고리와 밀랍 양초 토막을 마치 다른 지붕 아래선 한 번도 본 적 없다는 듯이 정겹게 다루고 있었다. 햄은 내게 카드놀이 첫 수업을 막 가르쳐 주던 참인데, 이번엔 더러운 카드로 점치는 법을 떠올려 보려 애쓰면서, 뒤집는 카드마다 생선 냄새 밴 엄지 자국을 꾹꾹 찍어 대고 있었다. 패거티 씨는 파이프 담배를 피우고 있었다. 나는 이제 이야기를 나누며 흉금을 털어놓을 때라고 느꼈다.\n\"패거티 아저씨!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 도련님.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아들 이름을 햄이라고 지은 건, 일종의 방주 같은 데 살아서 그런 거예요?\"\n\n패거티 씨는 그것이 꽤 심오한 생각이라고 여기는 듯했으나, 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 도련님. 그 애한테 이름은 내가 안 지었어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 누가 그 이름을 지었어요?\" 나는 패거티 씨에게 문답 둘째 항목을 던지며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그게요, 도련님, 그 애 아버지가 지었어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아저씨가 그 애 아버지인 줄 알았는데요!\"\n\n\"내 동생 조가 그 애 아버지였지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"돌아가셨어요, 패거티 아저씨?\" 나는 정중하게 한 박자 쉬고 나서 슬쩍 물었다.\n\n\"물에 빠져 돌아갔어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n나는 패거티 씨가 햄의 아버지가 아니라는 데 무척 놀랐고, 혹시 거기 있는 다른 사람들과의 관계도 내가 잘못 알고 있는 게 아닐까 의아해지기 시작했다. 너무도 알고 싶었던 나머지, 나는 패거티 씨에게 끝까지 캐물어 보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"어린 에밀리는,\" 내가 에밀리를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"아저씨 딸이죠, 그렇죠, 패거티 아저씨?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 도련님. 내 처남 톰이 그 애 아버지였어요.\"\n\n나는 어쩔 수가 없었다. \"돌아가셨어요, 패거티 아저씨?\" 나는 또 한 번 정중하게 침묵을 둔 뒤에 슬쩍 물었다.\n\n\"물에 빠져 돌아갔어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n나는 그 화제를 다시 꺼내기가 거북하다고 느꼈으나, 아직 바닥까지 파헤치지 못했으니, 어떻게든 끝장을 봐야 했다. 그래서 나는 말했다.\n\n\"아저씨는 자식이 하나도 없어요, 패거티 아저씨?\"\n\n\"없어요, 도련님.\" 그가 짤막하게 웃으며 대답했다. \"난 총각이에요.\"\n\n\"총각이라뇨!\" 나는 깜짝 놀라 말했다. \"그럼 저분은 누구예요, 패거티 아저씨?\" 앞치마를 두른 채 뜨개질하는 사람을 가리키며.\n\n\"저 사람은 거미지 부인이에요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"거미지요, 패거티 아저씨?\"\n\n그러나 이 대목에서 패거티가 — 그러니까 내 특별한 패거티 말이다 — 더는 아무것도 묻지 말라고 어찌나 단단히 손짓을 해 보였던지, 나는 그저 앉아서 그 말없는 일행을 다 둘러보다가 잠자리에 들 시간을 맞을 수밖에 없었다. 그러고 나서 내 작은 선실에서 단둘이 있을 때, 패거티는 햄과 에밀리가 고아가 된 조카들로, 둘이 어릴 적 빈털터리로 남겨졌을 때 우리 집주인이 이런저런 때에 거두어 들였다고 알려 주었다. 그리고 거미지 부인은 그의 배 동업자였던 사람의 미망인인데, 그 사람이 무척 가난하게 죽었다고 했다. 패거티 말로는, 그 자신도 가난한 사람일 뿐이지만, 황금처럼 착하고 강철처럼 진실하다고 했다 — 그것이 패거티가 든 비유였다. 그가 사납게 성을 내거나 욕설을 내뱉는 유일한 화제는 바로 이 베푸는 마음씨라고 했다. 만약 누구라도 그 일을 입에 올리면, 그는 오른손으로 탁자를 쾅 내리치고는 (한번은 그러다 탁자를 쪼개기도 했다), 그 얘기가 또다시 나오면 영영 뛰쳐나가 버릴 거라고, 안 그러면 자기가 '고름드'가 되겠다며 무시무시한 맹세를 했다. 내가 캐물어 보니, 이 무서운 수동태 동사 '고름드가 되다'의 어원을 아는 사람은 아무도 없는 듯했으나, 다들 그것을 더없이 엄숙한 저주의 말로 여기고 있었다.\n\n나는 나를 맞아 준 주인의 착한 마음씨를 깊이 느끼면서, 여자들이 배 반대쪽 끝에 있는, 내 것과 같은 작은 침대로 자러 가는 소리며, 패거티 씨와 햄이 내가 눈여겨봤던 천장 갈고리에 제 해먹 두 개를 매다는 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 무척 호사스러운 기분이었고, 졸음이 밀려와 그 기분은 한층 더 짙어졌다. 잠이 차츰 나를 덮쳐 올 때, 나는 바다 저편에서 바람이 울부짖으며 벌판을 가로질러 어찌나 사납게 몰려오는지 듣고서, 밤사이 저 깊은 바다가 솟구쳐 오르지나 않을까 하는 막연한 두려움이 들었다. 그러나 어쨌든 나는 배 안에 있고, 만에 하나 무슨 일이 벌어지더라도 패거티 씨 같은 사람이 함께 타고 있다면 결코 나쁘지 않다고 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n그러나 아침 말고는 더 나쁜 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 굴 껍데기 테를 두른 내 거울 위로 아침 햇살이 비치자마자, 나는 침대에서 빠져나와 어린 에밀리와 함께 밖으로 나가, 바닷가에서 돌멩이를 주웠다.\n\n\"넌 영락없는 뱃사람이겠지?\" 내가 에밀리에게 말했다. 딱히 그런 생각이 들었던 건 아니지만, 무언가 한마디 건네는 것이 신사다운 행동이라고 느꼈다. 게다가 바로 그 순간, 가까이서 반짝이는 돛 하나가 에밀리의 빛나는 눈동자 속에 자그마하고 예쁜 제 모습을 비추는 바람에, 이런 말이 문득 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"아니.\" 에밀리가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"난 바다가 무서워.\"\n\n\"무섭다고!\" 나는 제법 대담한 척하며, 저 드넓은 대양을 향해 한껏 거드름을 피우는 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"난 안 그런데!\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 바다는 잔인해.\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"난 바다가 우리 마을 사람들 몇몇한테 얼마나 잔인했는지 봤어. 우리 집만 한 배를 산산조각 내는 것도 봤는걸.\"\n\n\"그게 설마 그 배는—\"\n\n\"우리 아버지가 빠져 죽은 배냐고?\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"아니. 그 배는 아니야. 난 그 배는 한 번도 못 봤어.\"\n\n\"아버지도?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n어린 에밀리는 고개를 저었다. \"기억에 남을 만큼은 못 봤어!\"\n\n이런 우연이 다 있나! 나는 곧장 설명을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 나도 내 아버지를 한 번도 본 적이 없다는 것, 어머니와 나는 늘 단둘이서 더없이 행복하게 살아왔고 지금도 그렇게 살며 앞으로도 죽 그렇게 살 작정이라는 것, 그리고 내 아버지 무덤은 우리 집 가까운 교회 묘지에 있는데, 나무 그늘에 가려져 있고, 나는 즐거운 아침마다 그 가지 밑을 거닐며 새소리를 들었다는 것을 말이다. 그러나 에밀리의 고아 신세와 내 처지 사이에는 몇 가지 다른 점이 있는 듯했다. 에밀리는 아버지보다 어머니를 먼저 잃었고, 아버지 무덤이 어디 있는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 다만 그 깊은 바닷속 어딘가라는 것만 알 뿐이었다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 에밀리가 조개껍데기와 조약돌을 찾아 두리번거리며 말했다. \"네 아버지는 신사였고 네 어머니는 귀부인이잖아. 우리 아버지는 어부였고 우리 어머니는 어부의 딸이었고, 우리 댄 아저씨도 어부야.\"\n\n\"댄이 패거티 아저씨지?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"댄 아저씨 — 저기 계신.\" 에밀리가 그 배 모양 집을 향해 고갯짓하며 대답했다.\n\n\"응. 그분 말이야. 무척 좋은 분 같은데, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"좋다고?\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"내가 만약 귀부인이 된다면, 아저씨한테 다이아몬드 단추가 달린 하늘색 외투랑, 무명 바지랑, 빨간 벨벳 조끼랑, 삼각모자랑, 커다란 금시계랑, 은 파이프랑, 돈 상자를 드릴 거야.\"\n\n나는 패거티 씨가 그런 보물들을 받을 자격이 충분하다는 데 의심의 여지가 없다고 말했다. 솔직히 고백하건대, 고마워하는 어린 조카가 골라 준 그 차림새를 걸친 그가 마음 편히 있는 모습은 도무지 그려지지 않았고, 특히 삼각모자가 과연 현명한 선택일지 영 미심쩍었지만, 이런 생각은 혼자 속으로만 간직했다.\n\n어린 에밀리는 이 물건들을 하나하나 늘어놓다 말고 걸음을 멈추고는, 그것들이 무슨 찬란한 환영이라도 되는 양 하늘을 올려다보았다. 우리는 다시 걸음을 옮기며 조개껍데기와 조약돌을 주웠다.\n\n\"넌 귀부인이 되고 싶구나?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n에밀리는 나를 보고 웃으며 \"응\" 하고 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"난 정말 그렇게 되고 싶어. 그러면 우리 다 같이 양반이 되는 거잖아. 나랑, 아저씨랑, 햄이랑, 거미지 부인이랑. 그럼 사나운 날씨가 와도 무섭지 않을 거야. 우리 자신을 위해서라는 게 아니라 말이야. 물론 가엾은 어부들을 위해서 무섭긴 하겠지. 그래서 그 사람들이 무슨 일이라도 당하면 우리가 돈으로 도와줄 거야.\" 이것은 나에게 무척 만족스러운, 따라서 전혀 있을 법하지 않은 그림은 아니라고 여겨졌다. 나는 그 그림을 그려 보는 것이 즐겁다고 말했고, 그러자 어린 에밀리는 용기를 내어 수줍게 말했다.\n\n\"넌 이제 바다가 무섭지 않은 것 같아?\"\n\n바다는 충분히 잔잔해서 나를 안심시킬 만했지만, 만약 제법 큰 파도가 굽이치며 밀려오는 것을 봤더라면, 에밀리의 물에 빠져 죽은 친척들을 무섭게 떠올리며 줄행랑을 쳤을 게 분명하다. 그래도 나는 \"안 무서워\" 하고 말했고, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"넌 무섭다면서, 정작 무서워하는 것 같지가 않은걸.\" 에밀리가 우리가 거닐던 낡은 부두인지 나무 둑길인지 모를 그 가장자리에 너무 바짝 붙어 걷고 있어서, 나는 그녀가 떨어질까 봐 겁이 났던 것이다.\n\n\"난 그런 식으로는 안 무서워.\" 어린 에밀리가 말했다. \"하지만 바람이 불면 잠이 깨고, 댄 아저씨랑 햄이 생각나서 벌벌 떨려. 두 사람이 살려 달라고 울부짖는 소리가 들리는 것 같거든. 그래서 귀부인이 그렇게나 되고 싶은 거야. 하지만 그런 식으로는 안 무서워. 조금도. 자, 이거 봐!\"\n\n에밀리는 내 곁에서 훌쩍 뛰쳐나가, 우리가 서 있던 곳에서 삐죽 튀어나온 들쭉날쭉한 통나무를 따라 달려갔는데, 그 통나무는 깊은 물 위로 제법 높이 매달려 있는 데다 아무런 난간 하나 없었다. 그 광경이 내 기억에 어찌나 깊이 새겨졌던지, 내가 화가였다면 지금 여기에 그날 그대로의 모습을 정확하게 그려 낼 수 있을 것이다. 어린 에밀리가 (내 눈에는 그렇게 보였는데) 제 파멸을 향해 앞으로 훌쩍 뛰어들며, 저 멀리 바다 쪽을 향한, 내가 결코 잊지 못할 표정을 짓고 있는 모습을 말이다.\n\n그 가뿐하고 대담하고 팔랑거리는 작은 모습은 돌아서서 무사히 내게 되돌아왔고, 나는 이내 내 두려움을, 그리고 내가 질렀던 비명을 비웃었다. 어차피 헛된 비명이었으니, 근처에는 아무도 없었던 것이다. 그러나 그 뒤로, 어른이 된 다음, 나는 여러 번, 정말 여러 번 이런 생각을 해 보았다. 숨겨진 일들의 온갖 가능성 가운데, 그 아이의 느닷없는 무모함과 저 멀리 던진 그 거친 눈빛 속에, 죽은 아버지가 딸을 위험 쪽으로 자비롭게 끌어당기는 어떤 힘이, 그 애를 자기 곁으로 유혹하도록 허락된 어떤 손길이 있어서, 그 애의 목숨이 그날로 끝날 기회를 얻은 것은 아니었을까 하고. 그 뒤로 나는 또 이런 의문을 품은 적도 있다. 만약 그 애 앞에 놓인 삶이 한눈에 내게 드러났더라면, 그것도 어린아이가 온전히 이해할 만큼 드러났더라면, 그리고 그 애의 목숨이 내 손짓 하나에 달려 있었더라면, 나는 과연 그 애를 살리려고 손을 들었어야 했을까 하고. 그 뒤로 나는 또 이런 자문을 한 적도 있다 — 오래 가지는 않았다고 해 두자, 하지만 그런 적이 있었다 — 그날 아침 내가 보는 앞에서 그 물이 어린 에밀리의 머리 위로 덮쳤더라면 그 애한테 차라리 나았을까, 하고. 그리고 나는 그렇다고, 차라리 나았을 거라고 답한 적도 있다.\n\n이건 너무 이른 이야기인지도 모른다. 어쩌면 너무 일찍 적어 버렸는지도 모른다. 하지만 그대로 두기로 한다.\n\n우리는 멀리까지 거닐며, 신기하다 싶은 것들을 잔뜩 짊어졌고, 물가에 떠밀려 온 불가사리 몇 마리를 조심스레 물속에 도로 넣어 주었다 — 지금도 나는 그 종족에 대해 잘 알지 못해, 우리가 그렇게 해 준 것을 그것들이 고맙게 여길 까닭이 있었는지 아니면 그 반대였는지 도무지 확실히 알 수 없다 — 그러고 나서 패거티 씨네 집으로 발길을 돌렸다. 우리는 바닷가재 헛간의 바람 그늘 아래 멈춰 서서 순수한 입맞춤을 한 번 나누고는, 건강과 즐거움으로 발그레 달아오른 채 아침을 먹으러 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"꼭 어린 개똥지빠귀 두 마리 같구먼.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. 나는 이 말이 우리 고장 사투리로 어린 티티새 두 마리 같다는 뜻임을 알았기에, 그것을 칭찬으로 받아들였다.\n\n물론 나는 어린 에밀리에게 사랑에 빠져 있었다. 나는 그 어린것을, 훗날의 그 어떤 더없이 고귀하고 숭고한 사랑에도 깃들 수 없을 만큼, 참되게, 다정하게, 더 순수하고 더 사심 없이 사랑했다고 확신한다. 나는 내 환상이 그 파란 눈동자의 자그마한 아이 둘레에 무언가를 피워 올려, 그 애를 영묘하게 만들고 완연한 천사로 빚어냈다고 확신한다. 햇살 좋은 어느 오전에, 그 애가 작은 날개 한 쌍을 펼쳐 내 눈앞에서 훌쩍 날아가 버렸더라도, 나는 그것을 능히 예상했던 일 이상으로 여기지는 않았을 것 같다.\n\n우리는 야머스의 그 어슴푸레하고 오래된 벌판을 정답게 돌아다니곤 했는데, 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 그랬다. 나날은 우리 곁을 깡충거리며 지나갔는데, 마치 '시간'이라는 녀석마저 아직 다 자라지 않은 채, 자기도 하나의 아이가 되어 늘 노는 데만 정신이 팔려 있는 듯했다. 나는 에밀리에게 너를 흠모한다고, 네가 나를 흠모한다고 고백하지 않으면 칼로 자결할 수밖에 없는 처지에 내몰릴 거라고 말했다. 에밀리는 흠모한다고 했고, 나는 그 애가 정말 그랬다고 믿어 의심치 않는다.\n\n처지가 서로 맞지 않는다거나, 너무 어리다거나, 그 밖에 우리 앞을 가로막을 무슨 어려움이 있다거나 하는 생각으로 말하자면, 어린 에밀리와 나에게는 그런 고민이 전혀 없었다. 우리에게는 미래라는 것이 없었으니까. 우리는 나이를 더 먹을 준비 따위, 더 어려질 준비를 하지 않는 것만큼이나 하지 않았다. 우리는 거미지 부인과 패거티의 감탄거리였는데, 두 사람은 우리가 저녁이면 작은 사물함 위에 나란히 정답게 앉아 있을 때면 이렇게 속삭이곤 했다. \"어머나! 어쩜 저렇게 예쁠까!\" 패거티 씨는 파이프 너머로 우리를 보며 빙그레 웃었고, 햄은 저녁 내내 싱글거리기만 할 뿐 다른 일은 아무것도 하지 않았다. 그 사람들은 우리에게서, 예쁜 장난감이나 콜로세움 손바닥 모형에서 느낄 법한 그런 즐거움을 얻었던 것 같다.\n\n나는 거미지 부인이, 패거티 씨와 함께 사는 그 처지로 미루어 으레 기대됨 직한 만큼 늘 상냥하게 구는 것은 아님을 곧 알아차렸다. 거미지 부인은 다소 짜증을 잘 내는 성미여서, 이렇게 자그마한 살림에서 다른 식구들이 편치 않을 만큼 이따금 훌쩍이곤 했다. 나는 그 부인이 무척 안쓰러웠다. 그러나 거미지 부인에게 물러가 쉴 만한 제 방이 따로 있어서, 기분이 풀릴 때까지 거기 들어가 있어 준다면 한결 좋겠다 싶은 순간들도 있었다.\n\n패거티 씨는 이따금 '윌링 마인드'라는 선술집에 갔다. 나는 우리가 머문 둘째인가 셋째 날 저녁에 그가 밖에 나가 있고, 거미지 부인이 여덟 시에서 아홉 시 사이에 네덜란드제 시계를 올려다보며 그가 거기 가 있다고, 게다가 아침부터 그가 거기 갈 줄 알고 있었다고 말하는 것을 보고서 이 사실을 알아챘다.\n\n거미지 부인은 그날 하루 종일 기분이 가라앉아 있었는데, 오전에는 난로에서 연기가 나자 와락 눈물을 터뜨렸다. \"난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세야.\" 그 언짢은 일이 벌어졌을 때 거미지 부인이 한 말이다. \"뭐든 죄다 내 뜻과는 어긋나게만 돌아간다니까.\"\n\n\"아유, 곧 그칠 거예요.\" 패거티가 말했다 — 다시 말하건대 우리 패거티 말이다 — \"게다가, 아시잖아요, 그게 우리보다 부인한테 더 언짢은 일도 아니잖아요.\"\n\n\"난 더 사무치게 느껴.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다.\n\n무척 추운 날이었고, 살을 에는 바람이 몰아쳤다. 거미지 부인이 차지한 난롯가 그 특별한 모퉁이는 내가 보기에 그 집에서 가장 따뜻하고 아늑한 자리였고, 그 의자도 분명 가장 편한 의자였지만, 그날따라 그녀에게는 도무지 맞지 않았다. 그녀는 끊임없이 춥다고, 그 추위 때문에 등줄기에 '소름'이라고 부르는 게 끼친다고 투덜댔다. 마침내 그녀는 그 일을 두고 눈물을 흘리며, 자기는 '외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세'고 뭐든 죄다 자기 뜻과 어긋나게만 돌아간다고 또 말했다.\n\n\"확실히 무척 춥긴 하네요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"다들 그렇게 느낄 거예요.\"\n\n\"난 다른 사람들보다 더 사무치게 느껴.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다.\n\n저녁 식사 때도 그러했다. 거미지 부인은 늘 나 다음으로 곧장 음식을 받았는데, 나에게는 귀한 손님이라고 우선권을 주었던 것이다. 생선은 작고 가시가 많았고, 감자는 좀 탔다. 우리 모두 이것이 다소 실망스럽다고 인정했다. 그러나 거미지 부인은 자기는 우리보다 더 사무치게 느낀다고 하면서, 또다시 눈물을 흘리고는 아까 그 말을 몹시 쓰라리게 되뇌었다.\n\n그리하여 패거티 씨가 아홉 시쯤 집에 돌아왔을 때, 이 가엾은 거미지 부인은 제 모퉁이에서 무척 비참하고 처량한 꼴로 뜨개질을 하고 있었다. 패거티는 명랑하게 일하고 있었다. 햄은 커다란 방수 장화 한 켤레를 깁고 있었고, 나는 어린 에밀리를 곁에 두고 그들에게 책을 읽어 주고 있었다. 거미지 부인은 처량한 한숨 말고는 그 어떤 말도 하지 않았고, 차를 마신 뒤로는 한 번도 눈을 들지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 동무들,\" 패거티 씨가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"다들 어떻게 지냈나?\"\n\n우리는 저마다 무어라 한마디씩 하거나 무어라 눈짓을 보내며 그를 맞았지만, 거미지 부인만은 뜨개질감 위로 그저 고개만 가로저을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"왜 그래?\" 패거티 씨가 손뼉을 짝 치며 말했다. \"기운 내요, 할멈!\" (패거티 씨는 '할망구'라는 뜻으로 한 말이었다.)\n\n거미지 부인은 도무지 기운을 낼 수 없는 모양이었다. 그녀는 낡은 검은 비단 손수건을 꺼내 눈을 닦았다. 그러나 그것을 주머니에 도로 넣는 대신 손에 쥐고 있다가 다시 눈을 닦았고, 또 쓰려는 듯 그대로 손에 들고 있었다.\n\n\"왜 그래, 부인?\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 거미지 부인이 대답했다. \"윌링 마인드에서 오시는 길이죠, 댄 양반?\"\n\n\"아 그래요, 오늘 밤 윌링 마인드에서 잠깐 한잔하고 왔지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 거기로 내모는 것 같아 미안하네요.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"내몰긴! 누가 날 내몰 것도 없어요.\" 패거티 씨가 솔직하게 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"내가 너무 기꺼이 갈 뿐이지.\"\n\n\"무척 기꺼이도 가시지요.\" 거미지 부인이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 눈을 닦았다. \"그래요, 그래요, 무척 기꺼이. 당신이 그렇게 기꺼이 가는 게 나 때문인 것 같아 미안해요.\"\n\n\"당신 때문이라니! 당신 때문이 아니에요!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그런 말은 한 톨도 믿지 말아요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요, 맞아요.\" 거미지 부인이 외쳤다. \"내가 어떤 사람인지는 내가 알아요. 난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세고, 뭐든 죄다 내 뜻과 어긋날 뿐만 아니라, 나 자신이 모두와 어긋나게 군다는 것도 알아요. 그래요, 그래요. 난 다른 사람들보다 더 사무치게 느끼고, 그걸 더 드러내요. 그게 내 불행이지요.\"\n\n이 모든 이야기를 앉아서 듣고 있자니, 그 불행이 거미지 부인 말고도 그 집안 다른 식구들에게까지 미친다는 생각이 정말이지 들지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 패거티 씨는 그런 핀잔은 한마디도 하지 않고, 그저 거미지 부인에게 기운을 내라고 다시 한번 간청할 따름이었다.\n\n\"난 내가 바라는 그런 사람이 못 돼요.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다. \"그러기엔 한참 멀었지요. 내가 어떤 사람인지는 내가 알아요. 내 고생이 날 이렇게 비뚤어지게 만든 거예요. 난 내 고생을 사무치게 느끼고, 그게 날 비뚤어지게 해요. 안 느꼈으면 좋으련만, 난 느껴요. 거기에 무뎌질 수 있으면 좋으련만, 난 못 그래요. 내가 이 집을 불편하게 만들어요. 그래도 난 놀랍지가 않아요. 오늘 하루 종일 당신 누이를 그렇게 만들었고, 데이비 도련님도요.\"\n\n이 대목에서 나는 갑자기 마음이 녹아내려, 몹시 괴로운 심정으로 버럭 소리쳤다. \"아니에요, 그러지 않으셨어요, 거미지 부인.\"\n\n\"내가 이러는 건 도리에 한참 어긋나는 일이에요.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다. \"이건 마땅한 보답이 못 되지요. 차라리 구빈원에 들어가 죽는 게 낫겠어요. 난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세니, 여기서 비뚤어지게 굴지 않는 게 훨씬 낫지요. 일이 죄다 내 뜻과 어긋나야 하고, 나 자신도 비뚤어지게 굴어야 한다면, 차라리 내 고향 교구에서 비뚤어지게 굴게 해 줘요. 댄 양반, 차라리 구빈원에 들어가 죽어서 짐이나 덜어 주는 게 낫겠어요!\"\n\n거미지 부인은 이 말을 남기고 물러가 잠자리에 들었다. 그녀가 가고 나자, 더없이 깊은 연민 말고는 어떤 감정도 내비치지 않던 패거티 씨가 우리를 둘러보더니, 그 연민이 여전히 얼굴에 생생히 어린 채로 고개를 끄덕이며 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"옛사람 생각을 하고 있었던 게야!\"\n\n거미지 부인이 도대체 어떤 옛사람에게 마음을 두고 있었다는 건지 나는 잘 이해하지 못했는데, 패거티가 나를 재워 주며, 그것이 작고한 거미지 씨를 가리키는 말이고, 자기 오라버니는 그럴 때면 늘 그것을 당연한 사실로 받아들이며, 그것이 늘 오라버니의 마음을 짠하게 한다고 설명해 주었다. 그날 밤 그가 해먹에 누운 뒤 얼마쯤 지나, 나는 그가 햄에게 \"가엾은 사람! 옛사람 생각을 하고 있었던 거야!\" 하고 되뇌는 소리를 직접 들었다. 그리고 우리가 머무는 나머지 동안 거미지 부인이 비슷하게 북받칠 때마다 (그런 일이 몇 번 있었다), 그는 늘 그 상황을 두둔하며 똑같은 말을 했고, 늘 더없이 다정한 연민을 담아 그렇게 했다.\n\n이렇게 보름은 흘러갔는데, 밀물 썰물의 변화 말고는 달라지는 것이 아무것도 없었다. 그 조수가 패거티 씨가 나가고 들어오는 시간을 바꾸었고, 햄의 일거리도 바꾸어 놓았다. 햄은 일이 없을 때면 이따금 우리와 함께 거닐며 보트며 배를 구경시켜 주었고, 한두 번은 우리를 노 젓는 배에 태워 주기도 했다. 어째서 어느 한 가지 자그마한 인상의 묶음이 다른 것보다 유독 어떤 장소와 더 끈끈하게 얽히는지 나는 모르겠으나, 특히 어린 시절의 기억과 관련해서는 대다수 사람들이 다 그러리라 믿는다. 나는 야머스라는 이름을 듣거나 읽을 때마다, 어김없이 어느 일요일 아침의 바닷가가 떠오른다. 교회를 알리는 종이 울리고, 어린 에밀리는 내 어깨에 기대어 있고, 햄은 나른하게 물속으로 돌멩이를 떨어뜨리고, 저 멀리 바다에서는 해가 막 짙은 안개를 뚫고 나와, 마치 제 그림자 같은 배들을 우리에게 보여 주던 그 광경 말이다.\n\n마침내 집으로 돌아갈 날이 왔다. 나는 패거티 씨와 거미지 부인과 헤어지는 것은 그럭저럭 견뎌 냈지만, 어린 에밀리를 두고 떠나는 마음의 괴로움은 가슴을 후벼 팠다. 우리는 짐꾼이 묵는 선술집까지 팔짱을 끼고 걸었고, 나는 가는 길에 에밀리에게 편지를 쓰겠다고 약속했다. (나는 나중에 그 약속을 지켰는데, 흔히 셋방 내놓는다고 손으로 써 붙이는 글씨보다 더 큼지막한 글자로 써 보냈다.) 우리는 헤어지면서 무척 북받쳐 올랐다. 그리고 내 평생 가슴에 구멍이 뻥 뚫린 적이 있었다면, 바로 그날 하나 뚫린 것이었다.\n\n그런데 가만 보니, 내가 이 집을 떠나 와 있는 내내, 나는 다시 내 집에 무심했고, 그곳을 거의, 아니 전혀 떠올리지 않았다. 그러나 발길을 집 쪽으로 돌리기가 무섭게, 나를 책망하는 어린 양심이 냉큼 손가락을 들어 그쪽을 가리키는 듯했다. 그리고 기운이 가라앉을수록 더더욱, 나는 그곳이 내 둥지이며, 어머니가 나를 달래 주는 사람이자 벗이라는 것을 사무치게 느꼈다.\n\n이런 마음은 우리가 길을 갈수록 점점 더 커졌다. 그래서 가까이 다가갈수록, 지나치는 사물들이 더 낯익어질수록, 나는 어서 그곳에 닿아 어머니 품으로 달려들고 싶어 더욱 들떴다. 그러나 패거티는 그런 들뜬 마음을 함께 나누기는커녕 (무척 다정하게나마) 그것을 누그러뜨리려 했고, 어딘가 혼란스럽고 영 마음이 불편해 보였다.\n\n그래도 블런더스톤 까마귀 둥지는, 짐꾼의 말이 내켜만 한다면 패거티가 어떻게 하든 다가오게 마련이었고 — 과연 다가왔다. 음울한 하늘이 비를 머금어 잔뜩 위협하던 어느 춥고 잿빛인 오후의 그 광경을, 나는 어찌나 또렷이 기억하는지!\n\n문이 열렸고, 나는 즐거운 설렘에 반쯤 웃고 반쯤 울며 어머니를 찾아 둘러보았다. 그러나 어머니가 아니라 낯선 하녀였다.\n\n\"어머, 패거티!\" 나는 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"어머니가 집에 안 돌아오셨어?\"\n\n\"아녜요, 아녜요, 데이비 도련님.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"마님은 돌아오셨어요. 잠깐만요, 데이비 도련님, 제가 — 제가 도련님한테 할 말이 있어요.\"\n\n흥분한 데다 본디 마차에서 내리는 것이 영 굼떴던 탓에, 패거티는 제 몸을 어찌나 우스꽝스럽게 비비 꼬았는지 마치 화환이라도 만드는 것 같았다. 그러나 나는 너무 멍하고 이상한 기분이라 그 말을 해 줄 수가 없었다. 패거티는 내려서더니 내 손을 잡고, 어리둥절해하는 나를 부엌으로 이끈 다음, 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"패거티!\" 나는 잔뜩 겁에 질려 말했다. \"무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"아무 일도 없어요, 우리 귀한 데이비 도련님, 안심하세요!\" 패거티가 짐짓 쾌활한 척하며 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있는 게 분명해. 어머니는 어디 계셔?\"\n\n\"마님이 어디 계시냐고요, 데이비 도련님?\" 패거티가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"응. 왜 대문까지 안 나오셨고, 우린 왜 여기 들어온 거야? 아, 패거티!\" 내 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했고, 금방이라도 폭삭 주저앉을 것만 같았다.\n\n\"아이고 우리 가여운 도련님!\" 패거티가 나를 와락 붙들며 외쳤다. \"왜 그래요? 말해 봐요, 우리 귀염둥이!\"\n\n\"어머니도 돌아가신 거 아니지! 아, 어머니가 돌아가신 건 아니지, 패거티?\"\n\n패거티는 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 큰 목소리로 아니라고 외쳤다. 그러고는 주저앉아 헐떡거리기 시작하더니, 내가 자기를 깜짝 놀라게 했다고 했다.\n\n나는 그 놀란 가슴을 가라앉혀 주려고, 아니면 옳은 방향으로 다시 한번 놀라게 해 주려고 패거티를 꼭 끌어안았고, 그러고 나서 그녀 앞에 서서 애타게 묻는 눈빛으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 도련님, 진작에 말씀드렸어야 했는데,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"기회가 없었어요. 어쩌면 일부러라도 기회를 만들었어야 했는데, 도무지 정짝히는\"— 이것은 패거티의 어수룩한 어휘 부대에서 늘 '정확히'를 대신하던 말이었다 —\"마음을 못 다잡겠더라고요.\"\n\n\"어서 말해, 패거티.\" 나는 아까보다 더 겁에 질려 말했다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님,\" 패거티가 떨리는 손으로 모자를 풀며, 숨이 가쁜 듯한 말투로 말했다. \"어떻게 된 것 같아요? 도련님한테 아버지가 생겼어요!\"\n\n나는 부르르 떨며 새파랗게 질렸다. 무언가가 — 무엇인지, 어떻게 그런 건지는 모르겠으나 — 교회 묘지의 그 무덤과, 죽은 자가 되살아나는 일과 얽혀, 마치 불길한 바람처럼 나를 후려치는 것 같았다.\n\n\"새 아버지요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"새 아버지?\" 내가 되뇌었다.\n\n패거티는 무척 딱딱한 무언가를 삼키기라도 하는 듯 숨을 헉 들이켜더니, 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"가서 만나 봐요.\"\n\n\"만나기 싫어.\"\n\n\"그리고 어머님도요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n나는 더는 뒷걸음치지 않았고, 우리는 곧장 제일 좋은 거실로 갔는데, 패거티는 거기서 나를 두고 가 버렸다. 난로 한쪽에는 어머니가 앉아 있었고, 다른 한쪽에는 머드스톤 씨가 앉아 있었다. 어머니는 하던 일감을 떨어뜨리고 황급히 일어났지만, 내가 보기엔 어딘가 주뼛거리는 듯했다.\n\n\"자, 클라라, 여보.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"잊지 말아요! 자제해요, 늘 자제하란 말이오! 데이비, 잘 있었니?\"\n\n나는 그에게 손을 내밀었다. 잠시 머뭇거린 끝에, 나는 다가가 어머니에게 입을 맞췄다. 어머니도 나에게 입을 맞추고는, 내 어깨를 가볍게 토닥인 다음, 다시 앉아 일감을 들었다. 나는 어머니를 차마 볼 수 없었고, 그를 차마 볼 수 없었다. 나는 그가 우리 둘을 빤히 바라보고 있다는 것을 너무도 잘 알았다. 그래서 나는 창가로 돌아서서, 추위에 고개를 떨군 관목 몇 그루를 내다보았다.\n\n나는 슬그머니 빠져나올 수 있게 되자마자, 살금살금 위층으로 올라갔다. 정답던 내 옛 침실은 달라져 있었고, 나는 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 자야 했다. 모든 것이 어찌나 달라져 보이던지, 나는 예전 그대로인 것이 무엇이라도 없을까 하고 아래층을 헤매다가, 마당으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나갔다. 그러나 나는 이내 거기서 화들짝 물러서고 말았으니, 텅 비어 있던 개집이 커다란 개 한 마리로 채워져 있었기 때문이다 — 그놈은 입이 떡 벌어지고 털이 새카만 것이 꼭 그를 닮았는데 — 그놈은 나를 보자 몹시 성을 내며, 나를 물려고 펄쩍 뛰어나왔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 4. I FALL INTO DISGRACE\n\n내 침대가 옮겨진 그 방이, 만일 증언할 수 있는 감각을 지닌 존재였다면, 나는 오늘 이 순간에도 그 방에 호소했을 것이다 — 지금은 그곳에서 누가 자고 있을까, 문득 궁금하다! — 내가 얼마나 무거운 마음을 안고 그리로 올라갔는지 증언해 달라고 말이다. 나는 계단을 오르는 내내 마당의 개가 등 뒤에서 짖어 대는 소리를 들으며 그 방으로 올라갔다. 그러고는 방이 나를 멍하니 낯설게 바라보는 만큼이나 나도 그 방을 멍하니 낯설게 바라보며, 작은 두 손을 포갠 채 주저앉아 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n나는 더없이 엉뚱한 것들을 떠올렸다. 방의 모양, 천장에 난 금, 벽에 발린 종이, 창유리의 흠집이 바깥 풍경에 잔물결과 오목한 자국을 만들어 내는 모양, 세면대가 세 다리로 비틀비틀 서 있는 꼴, 그리고 거기에 깃든 어딘가 불만스러운 기운 — 그것이 '옛것'에 사로잡힌 거미지 부인을 떠올리게 했다. 나는 내내 울고 있었지만, 춥고 풀이 죽었다는 것만 느꼈을 뿐, 왜 우는지는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었던 게 분명하다. 마침내 그 쓸쓸함 속에서 나는, 내가 어린 에밀리를 끔찍이도 사랑하고 있는데, 그 애만큼 나를 원하거나 아껴 주는 이가 아무도 없어 보이는 이곳으로 오느라 그 애에게서 떨어져 나온 거라고 곱씹기 시작했다. 그렇게 생각하니 이 모든 일이 어찌나 비참하게 여겨졌던지, 나는 침대보 한 귀퉁이에 몸을 둘둘 말고 울다가 잠이 들었다.\n\n\"여기 있어요!\" 하는 누군가의 목소리에, 그리고 내 화끈거리는 머리를 덮은 이불이 걷히는 바람에 나는 잠에서 깼다. 어머니와 패거티가 나를 찾으러 온 것이었고, 이불을 걷은 사람은 둘 중 하나였다.\n\n\"데이비.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"무슨 일이니?\"\n\n나는 어머니가 그걸 묻는다는 게 무척 이상하게 느껴져서 \"아무것도 아니에요\" 하고 대답했다. 떨리는 입술을 감추려고 얼굴을 돌려 엎드렸던 게 기억난다. 그 입술이야말로 어머니에게 더 진실한 대답을 하고 있었으니까. \"데이비.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"데이비, 내 아가!\"\n\n그때 어머니가 했을 어떤 말도, 나를 '내 아가'라고 불러 준 그 말만큼 나를 크게 흔들어 놓지는 못했을 것이다. 나는 이불 속에 눈물을 감추었고, 어머니가 나를 일으키려 하자 손으로 그녀를 밀어냈다.\n\n\"이게 다 네 짓이야, 패거티, 이 모진 것아!\" 어머니가 말했다. \"조금도 의심하지 않아. 도대체 어떻게 네 양심이 그걸 견디는지 모르겠구나. 내 아들을 내게서, 아니 내가 아끼는 누구에게서든 등 돌리게 만들다니. 무슨 속셈이야, 패거티?\"\n\n가엾은 패거티는 두 손과 두 눈을 치켜들고는, 내가 저녁 식사 뒤에 늘 따라 외우던 감사 기도를 일종의 풀어 쓴 말투로 이렇게만 대답했다. \"주님께서 부인을 용서하시길요, 코퍼필드 부인. 그리고 방금 하신 그 말씀을, 부디 진심으로 후회하실 일이 결코 없기를요!\"\n\n\"정말 미쳐 버리겠네.\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"그것도 신혼 중에 말이야. 아무리 지독한 원수라도 마음을 누그러뜨릴 만한 때인데, 내가 조금이라도 마음 편하고 행복한 걸 시샘하지 않을 법한 때인데. 데이비, 이 못된 녀석! 패거티, 이 매정한 인간아! 아이고, 세상에!\" 어머니는 토라진 제멋대로의 태도로 우리 둘을 번갈아 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"세상이 가장 즐겁기를 바랄 자격이 있을 때, 어쩌면 이렇게 골치 아픈 세상인지!\"\n\n나는 어머니의 손도 패거티의 손도 아니라는 걸 단번에 알 수 있는 어떤 손길을 느꼈고, 침대 옆으로 미끄러져 내려와 두 발로 섰다. 그것은 머드스톤 씨의 손이었는데, 그는 그 손을 내 팔에 얹은 채 말했다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 짓이오? 클라라, 여보, 잊었소? — 단호함이오, 여보!\"\n\n\"정말 미안해요, 에드워드.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"정말 잘하려고 했는데, 마음이 너무 불편해서요.\"\n\n\"그래요!\" 그가 대답했다. \"이렇게 빨리 그런 소릴 듣다니, 영 좋지 않은 징조로구려, 클라라.\"\n\n\"지금 이런 사람이 돼야 한다는 게 너무 가혹하다는 거예요.\" 어머니가 입술을 삐죽이며 받아쳤다. \"그래요 — 너무 가혹하잖아요 — 안 그래요?\"\n\n그는 어머니를 끌어당겨 귀에 대고 속삭이고는 입을 맞추었다. 어머니의 머리가 그의 어깨로 기울고, 그녀의 팔이 그의 목에 닿는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그가 어머니의 무른 천성을 자기 마음대로 빚어낼 수 있다는 걸 똑똑히 알았다 — 지금 그가 실제로 그렇게 했다는 걸 아는 것만큼이나 똑똑히.\n\n\"당신은 아래로 내려가요, 여보.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"데이비드하고 나는 같이 내려가리다. 이봐.\" 어머니가 나가는 것을 지켜보고 고갯짓과 미소로 그녀를 내려보낸 뒤, 그는 어두워지는 얼굴을 패거티에게 돌리며 말했다. \"당신 주인마님의 이름을 아나?\"\n\n\"오래도록 제 주인마님이셨으니까요, 나리.\" 패거티가 대답했다. \"당연히 알지요.\" \"맞아.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그런데 내가 계단을 올라오면서 들으니, 자네가 마님을 마님 것이 아닌 이름으로 부르는 것 같던데. 마님은 내 이름을 쓰게 됐다는 걸 자네도 알 텐데. 그걸 기억해 두겠나?\"\n\n패거티는 나를 불안하게 흘끔거리면서, 아무 대답도 없이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 방을 나갔다. 짐작건대 자기가 나가 주기를 바라는 눈치인데 남아 있을 핑계가 없다는 걸 알아챈 것이리라. 우리 둘만 남게 되자 그는 문을 닫더니, 의자에 앉아 나를 자기 앞에 세워 둔 채로 내 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다. 나는 내 눈도 그에 못지않게 똑바로 그의 눈에 이끌리는 것을 느꼈다. 우리가 그렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 마주 섰던 일을 떠올리면, 지금도 내 심장이 빠르고 거세게 뛰는 소리가 다시 들리는 듯하다.\n\n\"데이비드.\" 그는 입술을 꾹 눌러 얇게 만들며 말했다. \"내가 고집 센 말이나 개를 다뤄야 할 때, 내가 어떻게 한다고 생각하느냐?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"때린다.\"\n\n나는 숨이 막힌 듯한 속삭임으로 대답했지만, 침묵 속에서 이제 내 숨이 더 가빠졌다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"움찔하고 따끔하게 만들지. 나는 속으로 이렇게 말한다. '저놈을 굴복시키고 말겠다.' 그러려면 그놈의 피를 죄다 쏟게 하는 한이 있어도 나는 해낸다. 네 얼굴에 그게 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"먼지예요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그는 나만큼이나 그것이 눈물 자국이라는 걸 잘 알고 있었다. 하지만 그가 그 질문을 스무 번 던지면서 매번 스무 대씩 때렸다 해도, 나는 그렇다고 말하느니 차라리 어린 가슴이 터져 버렸을 거라고 믿는다.\n\n\"꼬마치고는 머리가 제법 도는구나.\" 그는 자기 특유의 근엄한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"그리고 내 말을 아주 잘 알아들은 모양이로구나. 얼굴 씻어라, 이놈아. 그리고 나하고 같이 내려가자.\"\n\n그는 내가 거미지 부인 같다고 여겼던 그 세면대를 가리키며, 당장 시키는 대로 하라고 고갯짓으로 명령했다. 만일 내가 머뭇거렸다면 그는 눈곱만큼의 거리낌도 없이 나를 때려눕혔으리라는 걸, 그때도 거의 의심하지 않았고 지금은 더더욱 의심하지 않는다.\n\n\"클라라, 여보.\" 내가 그가 시킨 대로 하자, 그는 여전히 내 팔에 손을 얹은 채 나를 데리고 거실로 들어가며 말했다. \"이제 더는 마음 불편할 일이 없기를 바라오. 우리는 머지않아 이 어린것의 성미를 고쳐 놓을 테니까.\"\n\n하느님 맙소사, 그 시절에 다정한 말 한마디만 있었더라면 나는 평생 더 나은 사람이 되었을지도, 어쩌면 평생 다른 사람이 되었을지도 모른다. 격려와 설명의 말 한마디, 내 어린아이다운 무지를 가엾이 여기는 말 한마디, 집에 온 걸 반기는 말 한마디, 여기가 집이라고 안심시켜 주는 말 한마디만 있었더라면, 나는 이후로 겉으로만 위선적으로 따르는 대신 마음으로 그에게 순종했을지도 모르고, 그를 미워하는 대신 존경했을지도 모른다. 어머니는 내가 그렇게 겁먹고 낯설어하며 방에 서 있는 것을 보고 안타까워하는 듯했고, 이윽고 내가 슬그머니 의자로 가자 더욱 슬픈 눈으로 나를 좇았는데 — 어쩌면 내 어린아이다운 발걸음에서 사라진 어떤 자유로움을 아쉬워한 것이리라 — 그러나 그 말은 끝내 나오지 않았고, 그럴 때는 지나가 버렸다.\n\n우리는 셋이서만 식사를 했다. 그는 어머니를 무척 사랑하는 듯 보였고 — 그래서 내가 그를 조금이라도 더 좋아하게 됐다고는 도무지 말 못 하겠다 — 어머니도 그를 무척 사랑했다. 둘이 나누는 말에서, 그의 손위 누이가 함께 지내러 올 거라는 것, 그리고 그날 저녁 도착할 예정이라는 걸 알게 됐다. 그가 어떤 사업에 직접 손을 대지는 않았으되, 그의 집안이 증조할아버지 대부터 인연을 맺어 온 런던의 어느 포도주 상회에서 이익의 일부를 받거나 해마다 일정한 몫을 챙기고 있었고, 그의 누이도 거기에 비슷한 지분을 갖고 있었다는 것은 — 내가 그때 알았는지 나중에 알았는지는 확실치 않지만 — 어쨌든 여기서 짚어 두어도 무방하겠다.\n\n식사가 끝나고 우리가 난롯가에 앉아 있을 때, 나는 집주인의 심기를 거스를까 봐 슬쩍 빠져나갈 배짱은 없으면서도 패거티에게로 달아날 궁리를 하고 있었는데, 그때 마차 한 대가 정원 문 앞에 와 멎었고 그는 손님을 맞으러 나갔다. 어머니가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 나도 머뭇머뭇 어머니를 따라가는데, 어스름 속에서 어머니가 거실 문께에서 돌아서더니, 예전에 늘 그러던 것처럼 나를 품에 안고서, 새 아버지를 사랑하고 그의 말을 잘 들으라고 속삭였다. 어머니는 그것이 잘못된 일이기라도 한 듯 황급히, 몰래, 그러나 다정하게 그렇게 했다. 그러고는 한 손을 등 뒤로 내밀어 내 손을 잡았는데, 정원에 그가 서 있는 곳 가까이 다다를 때까지 그렇게 쥐고 있다가, 거기서 내 손을 놓고 자기 손을 그의 팔에 끼웠다.\n\n도착한 사람은 머드스톤 양이었는데, 음울한 인상의 여자였다. 자기 오빠처럼 가무잡잡한 것이, 얼굴이며 목소리며 무척 닮았다. 게다가 큼지막한 코 위에서 거의 맞붙을 만큼 짙은 눈썹을 가졌는데, 마치 여자라는 약점 탓에 구레나룻을 기르지 못하니 그 몫까지 눈썹으로 채워 넣은 듯했다. 그녀는 단단한 놋쇠 못으로 뚜껑에 자기 머리글자를 박은, 타협이라곤 모를 단단하고 시커먼 상자 두 개를 가져왔다. 마부에게 삯을 치를 때는 단단한 강철 지갑에서 돈을 꺼냈고, 그 지갑은 영락없이 감옥 같은 가방 속에 넣어 두었는데, 그 가방은 묵직한 사슬로 그녀의 팔에 매달려 있었고 짐승이 콱 무는 것처럼 딱 닫혔다. 그 당시 나는 머드스톤 양만큼 통째로 쇠붙이 같은 부인은 본 적이 없었다.\n\n그녀는 온갖 환영의 인사를 받으며 거실로 안내됐고, 거기서 정식으로 내 어머니를 새롭고 가까운 친척으로 인정했다. 그러더니 나를 보고 말했다.\n\n\"쟤가 올케 아들이오?\"\n\n어머니는 그렇다고 나를 인정했다.\n\n\"대체로 말해서,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"나는 사내애들을 안 좋아해. 잘 있었니, 얘?\"\n\n이런 격려가 넘치는 분위기 속에서, 나는 잘 지냈고 그쪽도 그러시길 바란다고 대답했는데, 어찌나 무덤덤하고 시큰둥했던지 머드스톤 양은 두 마디로 나를 처분해 버렸다.\n\n\"버르장머리가 없군!\"\n\n그 말을 또박또박 내뱉고 나서, 그녀는 자기 방으로 안내해 주기를 청했다. 그 방은 그때부터 내게 두려움과 공포의 장소가 되었는데, 그 시커먼 상자 두 개는 한 번도 열린 채로 보인 적이 없었고 자물쇠가 풀린 채 놓여 있던 적도 없었으며, (그녀가 나간 사이 한두 번 들여다본 적이 있는데) 거기에는 머드스톤 양이 차려입을 때 몸을 치장하는 자그마한 강철 족쇄와 대갈못 따위가 거울 위에 으리으리하게 줄줄이 걸려 있었다.\n\n내가 알아챈 바로는, 그녀는 아예 눌러앉으러 온 것이었고 다시 떠날 생각은 조금도 없었다. 그녀는 이튿날 아침부터 어머니를 '거들기' 시작했고, 하루 종일 식료품 창고를 들락거리며 물건들을 제자리에 놓는답시고 예전의 정리 방식을 죄다 뒤엎어 놓았다. 머드스톤 양에게서 처음으로 알아챈 눈에 띄는 점은, 하인들이 집 안 어딘가에 사내 하나를 숨겨 놓았다는 의심에 끊임없이 사로잡혀 있다는 것이었다. 이 망상에 사로잡혀 그녀는 더없이 엉뚱한 시각에 석탄 창고로 뛰어들었고, 어두컴컴한 벽장 문을 열 때마다 거의 매번, 드디어 그놈을 잡았다는 믿음으로 문을 도로 쾅 닫곤 했다.\n\n머드스톤 양에게 가뿐한 구석이라곤 전혀 없었지만, 일찍 일어나는 데서만큼은 영락없는 종달새였다. 그녀는 집 안 누구도 꼼지락거리기 전에 (그리고 지금까지도 나는 그녀가 그 사내를 찾고 있었다고 믿는다) 일어나 있었다. 패거티는 그녀가 한쪽 눈을 뜨고 잔다는 게 자기 생각이라고 말했지만, 나는 그 말에 동의할 수 없었다. 그 얘기를 듣고 나도 직접 해 봤는데, 도저히 안 되는 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n도착한 바로 다음 날 아침, 그녀는 닭이 울 무렵 벌써 일어나 종을 울리고 있었다. 어머니가 아침을 먹으러 내려와 차를 끓이려 하자, 머드스톤 양은 입맞춤에 가장 가까운 행동이랍시고 어머니 뺨을 콕 쪼는 시늉을 하고는 말했다.\n\n\"자, 클라라, 새아씨, 알다시피 나는 새아씨 수고를 덜어 줄 수 있는 데까진 다 덜어 주려고 여기 온 거예요. 새아씨는 너무 곱고 생각이 없어서\" — 어머니는 얼굴을 붉히면서도 웃었고, 그런 평가를 싫어하지 않는 듯했다 — \"내가 맡을 수 있는 집안일을 떠맡길 수가 없어요. 미안하지만 새아씨, 열쇠를 내게 주면, 앞으로 이런 일은 다 내가 챙길게요.\"\n\n그때부터 머드스톤 양은 낮에는 그 작은 감옥 같은 가방에, 밤에는 자기 베개 밑에 열쇠를 간직했고, 어머니는 나만큼이나 그 열쇠와 아무런 상관이 없게 되었다.\n\n어머니가 자기 권한을 손에서 놓으면서 일말의 항의 그림자조차 없었던 건 아니다. 어느 날 밤 머드스톤 양이 자기 오빠에게 어떤 살림 계획들을 늘어놓고 그가 그것에 찬성의 뜻을 보이자, 어머니가 별안간 울음을 터뜨리며 자기와 의논했어도 됐을 거라고 말했다.\n\n\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 씨가 엄하게 말했다. \"클라라! 당신이 놀랍구려.\"\n\n\"오, 놀랍다는 말이야 하기 좋죠, 에드워드!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"단호함이 어쩌고 떠드는 것도 당신한테야 좋겠죠. 하지만 당신이라면 그걸 좋아하지 않을걸요.\"\n\n여기서 짚어 두자면, 단호함이야말로 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양 둘 다 떠받드는 으뜸가는 미덕이었다. 그때 누가 물었다면 내가 그것을 어떻게 풀어 말했을지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 나는 내 나름으로 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 그것은 폭정의 다른 이름이며, 그 둘 모두에게 깃든 음울하고 오만한, 악마 같은 심술의 다른 이름이라는 것을. 그 신조를 지금 내가 정리하자면 이렇다. 머드스톤 씨는 단호하다. 그의 세상에서 머드스톤 씨만큼 단호한 사람은 없어야 한다. 그의 세상에서 다른 누구도 결코 단호해서는 안 된다. 모든 이가 그의 단호함 앞에 굽혀야 하니까. 머드스톤 양은 예외였다. 그녀는 단호할 수 있었으나, 오직 혈연의 자격으로, 그것도 낮고 종속된 정도로만 그러했다. 어머니는 또 다른 예외였다. 어머니도 단호할 수 있었고 또 단호해야 했지만, 오직 그들의 단호함을 견뎌 내는 일에서만, 그리고 이 세상에 다른 단호함이란 없다고 굳게 믿는 일에서만 그러했다.\n\n\"너무하잖아요.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"내 집에서—\"\n\n\"내 집이라고?\" 머드스톤 씨가 되받았다. \"클라라!\"\n\n\"우리 집 말이에요.\" 어머니가 또렷이 겁에 질려 더듬거렸다. \"내가 무슨 말 하는지 알 거예요, 에드워드 — 당신 집에서 내가 집안일에 대해 한마디도 못 한다는 게 너무하다는 거죠. 결혼하기 전엔 내가 얼마나 잘 꾸려 나갔는데요. 증거도 있어요.\" 어머니가 흐느끼며 말했다. \"패거티한테 물어봐요, 아무도 참견하지 않을 때 내가 얼마나 잘했는지!\"\n\n\"에드워드.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"이쯤에서 끝냅시다. 나는 내일 떠나겠어요.\"\n\n\"제인 머드스톤.\" 그녀의 오빠가 말했다. \"잠자코 있어! 네 말투가 풍기는 것처럼 네가 내 성격을 그것밖에 모른다고 넌지시 흘리다니, 어디 감히!\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 가엾은 어머니가 한심한 처지로, 눈물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 말을 이었다. \"나는 누구도 떠나길 바라지 않아요. 누구라도 떠난다면 난 정말 비참하고 불행할 거예요. 많은 걸 바라는 게 아니에요. 무리한 사람이 아니라고요. 그저 가끔 의논 좀 했으면 하는 것뿐이에요. 날 도와주는 사람한테는 정말 고마워하고 있어요. 그저 가끔, 형식으로라도 의논 좀 했으면 하는 거예요. 에드워드, 한때 당신은 내가 좀 미숙하고 계집애 같은 걸 좋아한다고 했잖아요 — 분명 그렇게 말했잖아요 — 그런데 이제는 그걸로 날 미워하는 것 같아요, 당신이 어찌나 매정한지.\"\n\n\"에드워드.\" 머드스톤 양이 다시 말했다. \"이쯤에서 끝냅시다. 나는 내일 떠나겠어요.\"\n\n\"제인 머드스톤.\" 머드스톤 씨가 천둥 치듯 호통쳤다. \"잠자코 못 있겠냐? 어디 감히?\"\n\n머드스톤 양은 자기 손수건을 감옥에서 풀어 주듯 꺼내 두 눈 앞에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"클라라,\" 그가 어머니를 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"당신은 나를 놀라게 하는구려! 아연실색하게 만들어! 그래, 나는 미숙하고 꾸밈없는 사람과 결혼해서, 그 성품을 빚어내고, 거기에 필요한 단호함과 결단력을 얼마간 불어넣겠다는 생각에 흐뭇해했소. 그런데 제인 머드스톤이 친절하게도 이 일을 거들어 주러 와서, 나를 위해 가정부나 다름없는 처지를 떠맡아 줬는데, 그 보답으로 이런 비열한 대접을 받다니—\"\n\n\"오, 제발, 제발요, 에드워드.\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"내가 은혜를 모른다고 몰아붙이지 말아요. 정말이지 난 은혜를 모르는 사람이 아니에요. 전엔 누구도 나더러 그렇다고 한 적 없어요. 내게 흠이 많지만, 그건 아니에요. 오, 제발 그러지 말아요, 여보!\"\n\n\"내 말은, 제인 머드스톤이,\" 그는 어머니가 잠잠해질 때까지 기다렸다가 말을 이었다. \"이런 비열한 대접을 받으니, 내 그 마음이 싸늘히 식어 변해 버린다는 거요.\"\n\n\"제발, 여보, 그런 말 말아요!\" 어머니가 가엾기 그지없이 애원했다. \"오, 그러지 말아요, 에드워드! 그 말은 차마 못 듣겠어요. 내가 어떤 사람이든, 나는 정이 깊은 사람이에요. 내가 정이 깊다는 거 나도 알아요. 확신이 없다면 이런 말 안 했을 거예요. 패거티한테 물어봐요. 분명 내가 정이 깊다고 말해 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"한낱 나약함이란 건, 클라라,\" 머드스톤 씨가 대꾸했다. \"아무리 끝없이 늘어놓아도 내겐 눈곱만큼의 무게도 없소. 숨만 낭비할 뿐이지.\"\n\n\"제발 우리 좋게 지내요.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"냉랭함이나 매정함 속에선 도저히 못 살겠어요. 정말 미안해요. 내게 흠이 아주 많다는 거 나도 알아요. 그리고 에드워드, 당신은 그 굳센 마음으로 날 위해 그걸 고쳐 주려고 애쓰니 참 고마워요. 제인, 나는 아무것도 반대하지 않아요. 당신이 떠날 생각을 한다면 난 가슴이 찢어질 거예요—\" 어머니는 너무 북받쳐 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n\"제인 머드스톤.\" 머드스톤 씨가 누이에게 말했다. \"우리 사이에 모진 말이 오가는 건, 바라건대, 드문 일이지. 오늘 밤 이렇게 별난 일이 벌어진 건 내 잘못이 아니야. 다른 사람 때문에 거기 휘말린 거지. 그리고 네 잘못도 아니야. 너도 다른 사람 때문에 거기 휘말린 거니까. 우리 둘 다 그 일을 잊도록 애써 보자. 그리고 이건,\" 그는 이 너그러운 말을 마친 뒤 덧붙였다. \"애한테 보일 만한 장면이 아니지 — 데이비드, 가서 자거라!\"\n\n두 눈에 그렁그렁 고인 눈물 너머로 문을 거의 찾을 수가 없었다. 어머니가 그토록 괴로워하는 것이 너무도 가여웠다. 하지만 나는 더듬더듬 길을 찾아 나가, 어둠 속에서 더듬더듬 내 방까지 올라갔다. 패거티에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넬 마음도, 그녀에게서 촛불 하나 얻어 올 마음도 나지 않았다. 한 시간쯤 지나 그녀가 나를 찾으러 올라온 바람에 잠에서 깼을 때, 그녀는 어머니가 몸이 좋지 않아 잠자리에 들었고, 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양만 단둘이 앉아 있다고 말했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 여느 때보다 좀 일찍 내려가던 나는 어머니의 목소리가 들려서 거실 문밖에 멈춰 섰다. 어머니는 무척 간절하고 비굴하게 머드스톤 양에게 용서를 빌고 있었고, 그 부인은 그것을 받아들였으며, 그렇게 완벽한 화해가 이루어졌다. 그 후로 어머니가 무슨 일에든 의견을 내는 걸 나는 한 번도 보지 못했다. 먼저 머드스톤 양에게 여쭙거나, 아니면 어떤 확실한 수단으로 머드스톤 양의 의견이 무엇인지 미리 알아내지 않고서는. 또 머드스톤 양이 심기가 틀어졌을 때 (그녀는 그런 쪽으로 병약했다) 열쇠를 꺼내 어머니에게 도로 내놓으려는 듯 가방으로 손을 뻗는 걸 볼 때마다, 나는 어김없이 어머니가 무섭게 겁먹는 모습을 보았다.\n\n머드스톤 집안의 피에 흐르는 음울한 기운은 머드스톤 집안의 종교를 어둡게 물들였고, 그 종교는 가혹하고 노여움에 차 있었다. 그것이 그런 성격을 띠게 된 것은 머드스톤 씨의 단호함에서 비롯된 필연적 결과였다는 생각이 그 후로 들었다. 그 단호함은, 핑계를 댈 수 있는 한 가장 혹독한 벌의 가장 무거운 무게를 그 누구에게서도 면제해 주는 법이 없었으니까. 어찌 됐든, 우리가 교회에 갈 때 짓던 그 무시무시한 얼굴들과, 그곳의 달라진 분위기가 똑똑히 기억난다. 또다시, 두려운 일요일이 돌아온다. 나는 마치 사형 집행 예배에 끌려온 감시받는 포로처럼 맨 먼저 낡은 신도석으로 줄지어 들어간다. 또다시, 상여 덮개로 지은 듯한 검은 우단 가운 차림의 머드스톤 양이 내 바로 뒤를 바짝 따른다. 그다음이 어머니, 그다음이 그녀의 남편이다. 옛날과 달리 이제 패거티는 없다. 또다시, 나는 머드스톤 양이 응답 기도를 우물거리며 무시무시한 단어마다 잔인한 쾌감으로 힘주어 발음하는 소리를 듣는다. 또다시, 나는 그녀가 '비참한 죄인들' 하고 말할 때 마치 온 신도를 싸잡아 욕하기라도 하듯 그 검은 눈을 교회 안에 휘휘 굴리는 것을 본다. 또다시, 나는 두 사람 사이에서 어머니가 머뭇머뭇 입술을 달싹이는 모습을 가끔 흘끗 잡아내는데, 그 두 사람은 양쪽 귀에 대고 낮은 천둥처럼 중얼대고 있다. 또다시, 나는 우리의 인자한 노목사님이 틀리고 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 옳을 수가, 그리고 천국의 모든 천사가 멸하는 천사일 수가 과연 있을까, 하는 갑작스러운 두려움에 휩싸인다. 또다시, 내가 손가락 하나라도 까딱하거나 얼굴 근육 하나라도 풀면, 머드스톤 양이 기도서로 나를 콕 찔러 옆구리를 아프게 한다.\n\n그래, 또다시, 집으로 걸어가는 길에 나는 이웃 몇몇이 어머니와 나를 바라보며 수군대는 것을 알아챈다. 또다시, 세 사람이 팔짱을 끼고 앞서 가고 나 혼자 뒤에 처져 따라갈 때, 나는 그 시선 몇몇을 좇으며, 어머니의 발걸음이 정말 내가 보아 온 만큼 가볍지 않은 건지, 그 아름다움의 발랄함이 정말 거의 시들어 버린 건지 궁금해한다. 또다시, 나는 그 이웃 누군가도 나처럼, 예전에 어머니와 내가 함께 집으로 걸어가던 모습을 떠올릴까 궁금해한다. 그리고 그 음울하고 쓸쓸한 하루 내내, 나는 그것을 멍청하게 궁금해한다.\n\n이따금 나를 기숙학교에 보내자는 이야기가 오갔다. 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 그걸 꺼냈고, 물론 어머니도 그들에게 동의했다. 하지만 아직 그 문제로 결론이 난 건 없었다. 그동안 나는 집에서 공부했다. 그 공부 시간을 내가 어찌 잊을 수 있을까! 명목상으로는 어머니가 주관했지만 실제로는 머드스톤 씨와 그의 누이가 주관했는데, 두 사람은 늘 자리를 지키면서 그 시간을, 우리 둘의 인생을 망쳐 놓은 그 잘못 이름 붙은 단호함을 어머니에게 가르치는 좋은 기회로 삼았다. 나는 바로 그 목적 때문에 집에 붙들려 있었던 거라고 믿는다. 어머니와 나 단둘이 살 때만 해도 나는 배움에 제법 재주가 있었고 의욕도 넘쳤다. 어머니 무릎에 앉아 알파벳을 배우던 게 어렴풋이 떠오른다. 지금도 입문서의 통통하고 시커먼 글자들을 들여다보면, 그 모양의 알쏭달쏭한 새로움과, O며 Q며 S의 너그럽고 인심 좋은 생김새가, 예전 그대로 다시 눈앞에 떠오르는 듯하다. 그러나 그 글자들은 역겨움이나 거부감 같은 감정은 조금도 불러일으키지 않는다. 오히려 나는 악어 나오는 책에 이르기까지 꽃길을 걸어왔던 것 같고, 그 길 내내 어머니의 다정한 목소리와 태도에 힘을 얻었던 것 같다. 하지만 그 뒤를 이은 이 엄숙한 공부 시간은, 내 평화의 숨통을 끊어 놓은 일격으로, 그리고 매일같이 이어지는 괴롭고 고된 비참함으로 기억된다. 그것은 무척 길고, 무척 많고, 무척 어려웠으며 — 그중 몇몇은 내겐 도무지 알아먹을 수 없는 것이었다 — 나는 대개 그것 때문에 어쩔 줄 몰라 했는데, 가엾은 어머니 본인도 그랬으리라 믿는다.\n\n그때가 어떠했는지 떠올려 보고, 어느 아침 하나를 다시 불러와 보겠다.\n\n나는 아침을 먹고 나서 책 몇 권과 연습장 하나, 석판 하나를 들고 둘째로 좋은 거실로 들어선다. 어머니는 글 쓰는 책상 앞에서 나를 맞을 채비를 하고 있지만, 창가 안락의자에 앉은 머드스톤 씨(책을 읽는 척하고는 있다)나, 어머니 곁에 앉아 강철 구슬을 꿰고 있는 머드스톤 양만큼 단단히 채비하고 있지는 않다. 이 두 사람을 보기만 해도 어찌나 영향을 받는지, 나는 무진 애를 써서 머릿속에 집어넣어 둔 단어들이 죄다 미끄러져 어디론가 사라져 버리는 걸 느끼기 시작한다. 그나저나 그것들은 대체 어디로 가는 걸까?\n\n나는 첫 번째 책을 어머니에게 건넨다. 어쩌면 문법책일 수도, 어쩌면 역사책이나 지리책일 수도 있다. 책을 어머니 손에 넘기면서 나는 물에 빠지는 사람처럼 그 쪽을 마지막으로 한 번 더 훑고는, 머릿속에 생생할 때 냅다 소리 내어 내달리기 시작한다. 한 단어에서 발이 걸린다. 머드스톤 씨가 눈을 든다. 또 한 단어에서 발이 걸린다. 머드스톤 양이 눈을 든다. 나는 얼굴이 빨개진 채로 대여섯 단어를 곤두박질치다가 멈춰 버린다. 어머니는 용기만 있다면 내게 책을 보여 주고 싶어 하겠지만, 그럴 용기가 없어서, 다만 나직이 말한다.\n\n\"오, 데이비, 데이비!\"\n\n\"자, 클라라,\" 머드스톤 씨가 말한다. \"그 애한테 단호하게 대해요. '오, 데이비, 데이비!' 같은 소리 하지 말고. 그건 어린애 같은 짓이오. 그 애는 외웠거나, 못 외웠거나 둘 중 하나니까.\"\n\n\"못 외웠어요.\" 머드스톤 양이 으스스하게 끼어든다.\n\n\"정말이지 못 외운 게 아닐까 걱정이에요.\" 어머니가 말한다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 봐요, 클라라,\" 머드스톤 양이 받는다. \"그냥 책을 도로 돌려주고 외우게 만들면 되잖아요.\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요.\" 어머니가 말한다. \"그렇게 할 작정이에요, 제인. 자, 데이비, 한 번 더 해 봐. 멍청하게 굴지 말고.\"\n\n나는 한 번 더 해 보는 것으로 그 명령의 첫째 항목엔 따르지만, 둘째 항목엔 그리 성공하지 못한다. 나는 정말 멍청하니까. 아까는 멀쩡히 넘어갔던 대목에서, 옛 자리에 이르기도 전에 곤두박질치고는 생각하려고 멈춰 선다. 그러나 나는 그 공부에 대해 도무지 생각할 수가 없다. 머드스톤 양의 모자에 달린 그물이 몇 야드나 될까, 머드스톤 씨의 실내복 값이 얼마일까, 따위, 나와는 아무 상관도 없고 조금도 엮이고 싶지 않은 그런 터무니없는 문제만 생각한다. 머드스톤 씨가 내가 오래전부터 예상해 온 그 짜증 섞인 몸짓을 한다. 머드스톤 양도 똑같이 한다. 어머니는 순순히 그들을 흘끗 보고는 책을 덮어, 다른 과제가 끝난 뒤에 해치워야 할 밀린 빚으로 한쪽에 치워 둔다.\n\n이런 밀린 빚이 금세 한 무더기가 되어, 굴러가는 눈덩이처럼 불어난다. 그게 커질수록 나는 더 멍청해진다. 사정이 어찌나 절망적인지, 나는 이런 헛소리의 수렁에서 허우적대고 있다는 느낌에, 빠져나갈 생각일랑 죄다 포기하고 내 운명에 나를 맡겨 버린다. 내가 비척대며 더듬어 나가는 동안 어머니와 내가 서로를 바라보는 그 절망스러운 모습은 정말이지 처량하기 그지없다. 하지만 이 비참한 공부 시간에 가장 큰 파장을 일으키는 건, 어머니가 (아무도 자기를 안 본다고 여기고) 입술의 움직임으로 내게 실마리를 주려고 할 때다. 바로 그 순간, 줄곧 다른 건 아랑곳없이 오로지 그것만 노리고 도사리고 있던 머드스톤 양이, 묵직한 경고의 목소리로 말한다.\n\n\"클라라!\"\n\n어머니는 흠칫하며 얼굴을 붉히고 희미하게 미소 짓는다. 머드스톤 씨가 의자에서 일어나 책을 집어 들고는, 나한테 던지거나 그걸로 내 귀싸대기를 후려치고, 두 어깨를 잡아 나를 방 밖으로 내쫓는다.\n\n공부가 끝난 뒤에도, 가장 끔찍한 것은 아직 닥치지 않았으니, 그건 바로 소름 끼치는 산수 문제의 모습으로 온다. 이건 나를 위해 지어내져, 머드스톤 씨가 입으로 불러 주는데, 이렇게 시작된다. \"내가 치즈 가게에 들어가서 더블 글로스터 치즈 오천 개를 한 개에 사 펜스 반 페니씩 주고 현금으로 산다면—\" 이 대목에서 나는 머드스톤 양이 몰래 좋아 어쩔 줄 모르는 걸 본다. 나는 이 치즈들을 두고 아무런 성과도 깨달음도 없이 저녁 먹을 때까지 끙끙대는데, 그쯤 되면 석판의 때를 살갗 땀구멍에 묻혀 물라토 꼴이 되어 있고, 그 치즈를 헤쳐 나가는 데 보탬이 되라고 빵 한 조각을 받으며, 그날 밤 내내 망신스러운 신세로 여겨진다.\n\n이렇게 세월이 흐른 지금 돌이켜 보면, 내 불운한 공부는 대체로 이런 식으로 흘러간 것 같다. 머드스톤 남매만 없었다면 나는 아주 잘했을 텐데, 그들이 내게 미친 영향은 가엾은 어린 새 한 마리를 사로잡는 두 마리 뱀의 흘림 같은 것이었다. 어쩌다 오전을 그럭저럭 봐줄 만하게 넘긴다 해도 얻는 거라곤 저녁밥뿐이었다. 머드스톤 양은 내가 일을 안 하고 있는 꼴을 도무지 못 봐줬으니까. 내가 경솔하게 한가한 티라도 낼라치면, 그녀는 \"클라라, 새아씨, 일만 한 게 없어요 — 애한테 연습 문제 좀 줘요\" 하고 말하며 오빠의 시선을 내게 끌어왔고, 그러면 나는 그 자리에서 곧장 새로운 노동에 처박혔다. 내 또래 다른 아이들과 노는 일로 말하자면, 나는 그런 걸 거의 누리지 못했다. 머드스톤 남매의 음울한 신학은 모든 아이를 작은 독사 떼로 몰아붙였고 (예전에 한 아이가 제자들 한가운데에 세워진 적도 있건만), 아이들끼리는 서로를 더럽힌다고 여겼으니까.\n\n이런 대접이 짐작건대 여섯 달 남짓 이어진 자연스러운 결과로, 나는 시무룩하고 굼뜨고 고집스러운 아이가 되었다. 게다가 날이 갈수록 점점 더 어머니에게서 떼어지고 멀어진다는 느낌이 나를 더더욱 그렇게 만들었다. 한 가지 사정만 아니었다면 나는 거의 얼이 빠져 버렸을 거라고 믿는다.\n\n그건 이런 것이었다. 아버지는 위층 작은 방에 자그마한 책 모음을 남겨 두었는데, 그 방은 (내 방과 붙어 있어서) 내가 드나들 수 있었고 우리 집의 다른 누구도 거기엔 신경 쓰지 않았다. 그 축복받은 작은 방에서, 로더릭 랜덤, 페러그린 피클, 험프리 클링커, 톰 존스, 웨이크필드의 목사, 돈키호테, 질 블라스, 로빈슨 크루소가 영광스러운 무리를 이루어 나와서 내 벗이 되어 주었다. 그 책들은, 그리고 아라비안나이트와 정령 이야기는, 내 공상과, 그곳과 그때 너머의 무언가에 대한 희망을 살아 있게 해 주었고, 내게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았다. 그중 몇몇에 해로운 게 들어 있었다 한들, 그건 내게는 거기 있는 게 아니었으니까. 나는 그런 건 전혀 몰랐다. 더 무거운 주제들을 끙끙대며 더듬는 와중에 어떻게 그 책들을 그렇게 읽어 낼 시간을 냈는지, 지금 생각하면 놀랍기만 하다. 내 자그마한 괴로움들 (내게는 큰 괴로움이었다) 속에서, 그 책들 속 좋아하는 인물이 되어 — 실제로 나는 그렇게 했다 — 또 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양을 온갖 악당 역에 집어넣어 — 이것도 실제로 했다 — 어떻게 스스로를 위로할 수 있었는지, 지금 생각하면 신기하다. 나는 일주일 내내 톰 존스 (어린아이판 톰 존스, 해 끼칠 줄 모르는 녀석이었다)가 되어 보기도 했다. 한 달을 내리 로더릭 랜덤이라는 내 나름의 상을 지켜 나간 적도 있다고, 정말이지 나는 믿는다. 그 선반에 있던 항해기며 여행기 몇 권 — 지금은 무엇이었는지 잊었다 — 을 나는 게걸스레 탐했다. 그리고 날이면 날마다 우리 집 내 구역을 헌 구두골 한 벌에서 빼낸 가운데 토막으로 무장하고 돌아다니던 게 기억나는데 — 영국 왕립 해군 소속 아무개 함장이, 야만인들에게 둘러싸일 위기에 처해 제 목숨을 비싼 값에 팔아넘기기로 결심한 모습을 완벽하게 구현한 것이었다. 그 함장은 라틴어 문법책으로 귀싸대기를 얻어맞고도 결코 위엄을 잃지 않았다. 나는 잃었다. 하지만 함장은, 세상의 살아 있든 죽었든 그 어떤 언어의 그 어떤 문법책에도 아랑곳없이, 함장이자 영웅이었다.\n\n이것이 나의 유일하고도 한결같은 위안이었다. 이걸 떠올리면 늘 어떤 그림 하나가 머릿속에 떠오른다. 어느 여름 저녁, 아이들은 교회 묘지에서 놀고, 나는 침대에 앉아 목숨이라도 걸린 듯 책을 읽는 그림이다. 이웃의 헛간 하나하나, 교회의 돌 하나하나, 교회 묘지의 한 뼘 한 뼘이, 내 마음속에서는 이 책들과 얽힌 저마다의 사연을 지니고 있었고, 그 책들 속에서 이름을 떨친 어느 고장을 대신하고 있었다. 나는 톰 파이프스가 교회 첨탑을 기어오르는 걸 보았고, 스트랩이 등에 배낭을 진 채 쪽문에 멈춰 서서 한숨 돌리는 걸 지켜보았으며, 트러니언 함장이 우리 작은 마을 선술집 거실에서 피클 씨와 그 곤봉을 두고 한판 벌인 것을 안다.\n\n이제 독자도 나만큼이나 잘 알 것이다. 내가 다시 이르고 있는 내 어린 시절 이야기의 그 대목에 다다랐을 때 내가 어떤 아이였는지를.\n\n어느 날 아침 책을 들고 거실로 들어가니, 어머니는 불안한 얼굴이고, 머드스톤 양은 단호한 얼굴이며, 머드스톤 씨는 회초리 끝에 무언가를 칭칭 감고 있었다 — 낭창낭창 잘 휘는 회초리였는데, 그는 내가 들어오자 감던 손을 멈추고 그것을 허공에 가늠하며 휙휙 휘둘렀다.\n\n\"내 말하건대, 클라라,\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"나도 어릴 때 매를 자주 맞았소.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 당연하죠.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다.\n\n\"물론이죠, 제인.\" 어머니가 고분고분 더듬거렸다. \"하지만 — 하지만 그게 에드워드한테 도움이 됐다고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"그게 에드워드한테 해가 됐다고 생각하오, 클라라?\" 머드스톤 씨가 근엄하게 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그게 요점이에요.\" 그의 누이가 말했다.\n\n이에 어머니는 \"물론이죠, 제인\" 하고 답하고는 더는 말이 없었다.\n\n나는 이 대화에 내가 직접 걸려 있는 게 아닐까 불안한 마음이 들어, 내게 와 닿는 머드스톤 씨의 눈을 찾았다.\n\n\"자, 데이비드,\" 그가 말했다 — 그가 그 말을 하면서 또 그 눈초리를 던지는 걸 나는 보았다 — \"오늘은 평소보다 훨씬 더 조심해야 할 거다.\" 그는 회초리를 다시 한 번 가늠하고, 다시 한 번 휙 휘둘렀다. 그렇게 손질을 마치고는, 의미심장한 표정으로 그것을 자기 옆에 내려놓고 책을 집어 들었다.\n\n이것은 그 시작으로, 내 정신을 번쩍 들게 하기에 딱 좋았다. 나는 공부한 단어들이 하나씩, 또는 한 줄씩이 아니라 통째로 한 쪽씩 미끄러져 달아나는 걸 느꼈다. 붙잡으려 애썼지만, 그것들은, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 스케이트를 신은 듯 거침없이 매끄럽게 내게서 스르르 미끄러져 나가, 도무지 멈출 길이 없었다.\n\n우리는 출발이 나빴고, 갈수록 더 나빠졌다. 나는 채비를 아주 잘해 왔다고 여겨, 오히려 좀 두각을 나타내 보겠다는 생각으로 들어왔지만, 그건 완전한 착각으로 드러났다. 머드스톤 양이 줄곧 우리 모두를 단호히 감시하는 가운데, 책이 한 권 또 한 권 실패의 더미에 보태졌다. 그리고 마침내 그 오천 개의 치즈에 (그날은 회초리로 바꿔 냈던 게 기억난다) 이르렀을 때, 어머니가 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 경고하는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"나 몸이 좀 안 좋은 것 같아요, 제인.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n그가 일어서서 회초리를 집어 들며 누이에게 근엄하게 눈을 찡긋하는 걸 나는 보았다.\n\n\"이런, 제인, 클라라가 데이비드 때문에 오늘 겪은 그 골치와 시달림을, 완벽한 단호함으로 견디기를 바라긴 어렵잖아. 그건 금욕주의자나 할 일이지. 클라라가 무척 굳세지고 나아지긴 했지만, 그렇게까지 바랄 순 없어. 데이비드, 너와 나는 위층으로 가자, 이놈아.\"\n\n그가 나를 문밖으로 데리고 나가자 어머니가 우리 쪽으로 달려왔다. 머드스톤 양이 \"클라라! 당신 완전 바보예요?\" 하고 말하며 가로막았다. 그때 어머니가 두 귀를 틀어막는 걸 나는 보았고, 어머니가 우는 소리를 들었다.\n\n그는 나를 천천히, 근엄하게 내 방으로 데려갔는데 — 나는 그가 그렇게 격식을 갖춰 정의를 집행하는 행렬을 즐겼다고 확신한다 — 거기 다다르자 별안간 내 머리를 자기 겨드랑이 밑으로 비틀어 처박았다.\n\n\"머드스톤 씨! 나리!\" 나는 그에게 외쳤다. \"그러지 마세요! 제발 때리지 마세요! 저 배우려고 애썼어요, 나리. 하지만 나리와 머드스톤 양이 곁에 있으면 배울 수가 없어요. 정말 못 배우겠어요!\"\n\n\"정말 못 배우겠다고, 데이비드?\" 그가 말했다. \"어디 한번 보자.\"\n\n그는 내 머리를 죔쇠처럼 꽉 쥐고 있었지만, 나는 어떻게든 그에게 몸을 휘감아 잠시 그를 멈춰 세우고는, 때리지 말아 달라고 애원했다. 그를 멈춰 세운 건 단 한순간뿐이었으니, 바로 다음 순간 그가 나를 호되게 내리쳤고, 그와 동시에 나는 그가 나를 붙들고 있던 그 손을 내 입속에 넣고 이로 콱 물어 뚫어 버렸다. 그걸 생각하면 지금도 이가 다 시리다.\n\n그러자 그는 나를 때려죽이기라도 할 듯이 때렸다. 우리가 내는 그 온갖 소란 너머로, 사람들이 계단을 뛰어 올라오며 소리치는 게 들렸다 — 어머니가 소리치는 게 들렸고 — 패거티도. 그러더니 그는 가 버렸고, 문은 밖에서 잠겼고, 나는 열에 들떠 화끈거리고, 찢기고, 쓰라리고, 내 보잘것없는 방식으로 분노에 차서 바닥에 널브러져 있었다.\n\n내가 잠잠해졌을 때, 온 집 안에 어찌나 부자연스러운 고요가 감돌던지, 지금도 똑똑히 기억난다! 쓰라림과 격정이 가라앉기 시작하면서, 내가 얼마나 못된 짓을 했다는 기분이 들기 시작했는지, 지금도 똑똑히 기억난다!\n\n나는 오랫동안 앉아서 귀를 기울였지만, 아무 소리도 나지 않았다. 나는 바닥에서 기어 올라와 거울에 비친 내 얼굴을 보았는데, 어찌나 퉁퉁 붓고 벌겋고 흉측한지 나 자신조차 거의 겁이 날 지경이었다. 매 맞은 자국들이 쓰라리고 뻣뻣해서, 몸을 움직일 때마다 새삼 울음이 났다. 하지만 그것도 내가 느끼는 죄책감에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그 죄책감은, 감히 말하건대, 내가 세상에서 가장 극악무도한 죄인이었다 한들 그럴까 싶을 만큼 내 가슴을 무겁게 짓눌렀다.\n\n날이 어둑해지기 시작해서 나는 창문을 닫았는데 (나는 거의 내내 창턱에 머리를 얹고 누워, 번갈아 가며 울다가 졸다가 멍하니 바깥을 내다보고 있었다), 그때 열쇠가 돌아가더니 머드스톤 양이 빵과 고기 약간, 그리고 우유를 들고 들어왔다. 그녀는 모범적인 단호함으로 나를 노려보며 그것들을 말없이 탁자 위에 내려놓고는, 도로 나가서 문을 잠갔다.\n\n날이 어두워진 뒤로도 한참을, 나는 거기 앉아 또 누가 오지 않을까 하고 생각했다. 그날 밤은 그럴 가망이 없어 보이자, 나는 옷을 벗고 잠자리에 들었다. 그러고는 거기서, 내게 무슨 일이 닥칠까 두려워하며 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다. 내가 저지른 게 범죄 행위였을까? 나는 체포되어 감옥에 보내질까? 나는 교수형에 처해질 위험이라도 있는 걸까?\n\n이튿날 아침 잠에서 깨던 일을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 처음 한순간은 명랑하고 상쾌하다가, 이내 케케묵고 음울한 기억의 짓누름에 짓눌리던 그 느낌을. 머드스톤 양은 내가 침대에서 나오기도 전에 다시 나타나, 똑똑히 말하기를, 정원을 반 시간 동안 거닐어도 좋지만 그 이상은 안 된다고 했다. 그러고는 내가 그 허락을 누릴 수 있도록 문을 열어 둔 채로 나갔다.\n\n나는 그렇게 했고, 닷새간 이어진 내 유폐 생활 동안 아침마다 그렇게 했다. 어머니를 혼자 만날 수만 있었다면, 나는 어머니 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 용서를 빌었을 것이다. 하지만 그 내내 머드스톤 양 말고는 아무도 보지 못했다 — 거실의 저녁 기도 시간만 빼고는. 그 시간엔 다른 사람들이 다 자리를 잡은 뒤에 머드스톤 양이 나를 거기까지 데려다 놓았는데, 나는 어린 무법자가 되어, 문 가까이에 나 혼자만 외따로 세워졌다. 그리고 누구도 경건한 자세에서 일어나기 전에, 내 간수의 손에 이끌려 엄숙하게 끌려 나왔다. 다만 내가 알아챈 건, 어머니가 나에게서 되도록 멀리 떨어져 있었고, 얼굴을 딴 데로 돌려 내가 끝내 그 얼굴을 보지 못했다는 것, 그리고 머드스톤 씨의 손이 커다란 아마포 붕대로 동여매여 있었다는 것뿐이었다.\n\n그 닷새가 얼마나 길었는지, 나는 누구에게도 도무지 짐작시킬 수가 없다. 그것은 내 기억 속에서 여러 해의 자리를 차지하고 있다. 내 귀에 들려오는 집 안의 온갖 일에 내가 귀 기울이던 모습 — 종소리, 문이 열리고 닫히는 소리, 도란거리는 목소리, 계단의 발소리. 바깥에서 들려오는 웃음소리나 휘파람 소리, 노랫소리는, 외롭고 망신스러운 내게 그 무엇보다 음울하게 느껴졌다. 시간이 흐르는 그 종잡을 수 없는 속도 — 특히 밤이면, 아침인 줄 알고 깼다가 식구들이 아직 잠자리에도 들지 않았고 그 긴 밤이 통째로 아직 남았다는 걸 알게 되곤 했다. 내가 꾼 가라앉은 꿈과 악몽들. 낮이, 정오가, 오후가, 저녁이 돌아오던 일, 아이들이 교회 묘지에서 놀 때면 나는 내가 갇혔다는 걸 그 애들이 알까 봐 창가에 모습을 드러내기가 부끄러워서 방 안에서 멀찍이 그 애들을 지켜보았다. 내 목소리를 한 번도 들을 일 없다는 그 묘한 느낌. 먹고 마실 때 함께 찾아왔다가 그것과 함께 떠나 버리던, 명랑함 비슷한 것의 덧없는 순간들. 어느 날 저녁 비가 내리기 시작하던 일, 상큼한 냄새와 함께 나와 교회 사이로 점점 더 세차게 쏟아져 내리더니, 마침내 그 비와 짙어 가는 밤이 나를 음울함과 두려움과 회한 속에 잠겨 꺼뜨려 버리는 듯하던 일 — 이 모든 것이 며칠이 아니라 여러 해 동안 빙글빙글 돌아간 것처럼 여겨지니, 그만큼 내 기억에 또렷하고 강렬하게 새겨져 있다. 갇혀 지내던 마지막 밤, 나는 누군가 내 이름을 속삭이는 소리에 잠에서 깼다. 나는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 어둠 속으로 두 팔을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"너야, 패거티?\"\n\n곧바로 대답은 없었지만, 이내 또 한 번 내 이름이 들렸는데, 어찌나 으스스하고 무시무시한 어조였던지, 그게 분명 열쇠 구멍을 통해 들려온 거라는 생각이 떠오르지 않았다면 나는 발작이라도 일으켰을 것이다.\n\n나는 더듬더듬 문께로 가서, 내 입술을 열쇠 구멍에 대고 속삭였다. \"너야, 패거티?\"\n\n\"그래, 내 소중한 데이비.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"쥐새끼처럼 살살 해, 안 그러면 고양이가 우릴 들을 거야.\"\n\n나는 이것이 머드스톤 양을 가리키는 말임을 알아들었고, 사정이 다급하다는 걸 절감했다. 그녀의 방이 바로 옆이었으니까.\n\n\"엄마는 어때, 패거티? 나한테 많이 화났어?\"\n\n내가 내 쪽 열쇠 구멍에서 그랬듯, 패거티가 자기 쪽 열쇠 구멍에서 가만가만 우는 소리가 들리더니, 이윽고 그녀가 대답했다. \"아니. 많이는 아냐.\"\n\n\"나 어떻게 되는 거야, 패거티? 너 알아?\"\n\n\"학교. 런던 근처.\" 패거티의 대답이었다. 나는 그녀에게 다시 말해 달라고 해야 했는데, 처음엔 그녀가 그걸 곧장 내 목구멍 속으로 말해 버렸기 때문이다. 내가 입을 떼고 귀를 갖다 대는 걸 깜빡한 탓이었다. 그래서 그 말이 무척 간지러웠을망정, 나는 알아듣지 못했다.\n\n\"언제, 패거티?\"\n\n\"내일.\"\n\n\"그래서 머드스톤 양이 내 서랍에서 옷을 죄다 꺼낸 거야?\" 그녀가 그랬는데, 내가 미처 말하는 걸 잊었다.\n\n\"응.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"상자에.\"\n\n\"엄마는 못 봐?\"\n\n\"봐.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"아침에.\"\n\n그러고는 패거티가 열쇠 구멍에 입을 바짝 갖다 대고, 이 말들을 그 구멍을 통해 내뱉었는데, 일찍이 어떤 열쇠 구멍이 무언가를 전하는 매개가 되어 본 적이 없으리만치 그렇게 절절하고 진심 어린 마음을 담아서였다고, 나는 감히 단언한다. 끊어진 짤막한 문장 하나하나를 저마다 발작하듯 짧게 터뜨려 가며.\n\n\"데이비, 얘야. 내가 너랑. 요즘. 예전만큼. 가깝게 못 지냈다면. 그건 내가 널 사랑하지 않아서가 아니야. 예전만큼, 아니 그보다 더, 내 어여쁜 강아지야. 그건 그게 너한테 더 낫다고 생각해서야. 그리고 또 다른 누구한테도. 데이비, 내 사랑, 듣고 있니? 들려?\"\n\n\"으-으-응, 패거티!\" 나는 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"내 새끼!\" 패거티가 한없는 연민을 담아 말했다. \"내가 하고 싶은 말은. 넌 날 절대 잊으면 안 된다는 거야. 나도 절대 널 안 잊을 테니까. 그리고 데이비, 내가 네 엄마를 정성껏 보살필게. 너를 보살핀 만큼이나. 절대 곁을 안 떠날 거야. 언젠가 네 엄마가 그 가엾은 머리를. 다시 멍청하고 까탈스러운 늙은 패거티의 팔에 기대고 싶어 할 날이 올지도 몰라. 그리고 네게 편지할게, 얘야. 내가 배운 건 없지만. 그리고 난, 난—\" 패거티는 나에게 입맞출 수 없으니 열쇠 구멍에 입을 맞추기 시작했다.\n\n\"고마워, 패거티!\" 내가 말했다. \"오, 고마워! 고마워! 한 가지만 약속해 줄래, 패거티? 패거티 아저씨랑 어린 에밀리한테, 그리고 거미지 부인이랑 햄한테 편지해서, 내가 그분들이 생각하실 만큼 그렇게 나쁜 애가 아니라고, 모두에게 내 사랑을 전한다고 — 특히 어린 에밀리한테 — 그렇게 써 줄래? 그래 줄래, 패거티, 부탁이야?\"\n\n마음씨 고운 그 사람은 약속해 주었고, 우리 둘은 더없이 다정하게 열쇠 구멍에 입을 맞추었다 — 나는 그것이 그녀의 정직한 얼굴이기라도 한 듯 손으로 토닥였던 기억이 난다 — 그리고 헤어졌다. 그날 밤부터 내 가슴속에는 패거티를 향한, 무어라 잘 정의할 수 없는 감정이 자라났다. 그녀가 어머니를 대신한 건 아니었다. 누구도 그럴 순 없었다. 하지만 그녀는 내 마음속 어떤 빈자리로 들어와 그 자리를 채우며 닫혔고, 나는 그녀에게 다른 어떤 인간에게도 느껴 본 적 없는 무언가를 느꼈다. 그것은 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 정이기도 했다. 그러면서도 만약 그녀가 죽었다면, 나는 내가 어떻게 했을지, 그것이 내게 되었을 그 비극을 어떻게 견뎌 냈을지 도무지 모르겠다.\n\n아침에 머드스톤 양이 여느 때처럼 나타나, 내가 학교에 가게 됐다고 말했는데, 그녀가 짐작한 것만큼 내게 전혀 새로운 소식은 아니었다. 그녀는 또, 옷을 다 입거든 아래층 거실로 내려와 아침을 먹으라고 일러 주었다. 거기서 나는 몹시 창백하고 눈이 빨개진 어머니를 보았다. 나는 그 품으로 달려들어, 괴로워하는 내 영혼을 다해 용서를 빌었다.\n\n\"오, 데이비!\" 어머니가 말했다. \"어떻게 내가 사랑하는 사람을 다치게 할 수가 있니! 더 착해지려고 애써 다오, 더 착해지게 해 달라고 기도해 다오! 너를 용서하마. 하지만, 데이비, 네 마음속에 그런 못된 격정이 있다는 게 나는 너무도 슬프구나.\"\n\n그들은 어머니에게 내가 못된 녀석이라고 믿게 만들어 놓았고, 어머니는 내가 떠나는 것보다 그 일을 더 슬퍼했다. 나는 그게 사무치게 아팠다. 작별의 아침밥을 먹어 보려 했지만, 눈물이 버터 바른 빵 위로 뚝뚝 떨어지고 찻잔 속으로 주르르 흘러내렸다. 어머니가 가끔 나를 바라보다가, 감시하는 머드스톤 양을 흘끔 보고는, 고개를 떨구거나 눈길을 딴 데로 돌리는 걸 나는 보았다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 도련님 상자 저기 있어요!\" 대문께에서 바퀴 소리가 들리자 머드스톤 양이 말했다.\n\n나는 패거티를 찾았지만, 그녀가 아니었다. 그녀도 머드스톤 씨도 나타나지 않았다. 내 옛 지인인 짐마차꾼이 문 앞에 와 있었다. 상자가 그의 수레로 옮겨져 실렸다.\n\n\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 경고하는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"준비됐어요, 제인.\" 어머니가 대답했다. \"잘 가렴, 데이비. 네가 잘되라고 가는 거야. 잘 가, 내 아가. 방학 때 집에 와서, 더 착한 아이가 되어 다오.\"\n\n\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 제인.\" 나를 안고 있던 어머니가 대답했다. \"널 용서한다, 내 사랑하는 아들아. 하느님이 너를 지켜 주시길!\"\n\n\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 되풀이했다.\n\n머드스톤 양은 친절하게도 나를 수레까지 데려다주었고, 가는 길에, 내가 비참한 끝장을 보기 전에 부디 뉘우치길 바란다고 말했다. 그러고 나서 나는 수레에 올랐고, 게으른 말이 그걸 끌고 터덜터덜 떠났다.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. I AM SENT AWAY FROM HOME\n\n우리가 한 반 마일쯤 갔을 무렵, 내 손수건은 흠뻑 젖어 있었는데, 그때 마부가 갑자기 멈춰 섰다. 무슨 일인가 싶어 밖을 내다보니, 놀랍게도 패거티가 산울타리 사이에서 불쑥 튀어나와 마차로 기어 올라왔다. 패거티는 두 팔로 나를 끌어안고 자기 코르셋에 어찌나 꽉 눌러 댔는지 코가 몹시 아팠는데, 정작 그것을 알아챈 건 나중에 코끝이 아릿한 걸 느꼈을 때였다. 패거티는 단 한마디도 하지 않았다. 한쪽 팔을 풀더니 팔꿈치까지 주머니에 깊숙이 찔러 넣어 종이봉지에 든 과자 몇 개를 꺼내 내 주머니에 가득 쑤셔 넣고, 지갑 하나를 내 손에 쥐여 주었지만, 그러는 내내 한마디도 없었다. 두 팔로 마지막으로 한 번 더 꽉 안아 주고는 마차에서 내려 달아났는데, 내 믿음으로는, 그리고 줄곧 그렇게 믿어 온 바로는, 그때 패거티 옷에는 단추가 단 한 개도 남아 있지 않았다. 여기저기 굴러다니던 여러 단추 가운데 하나를 주웠는데, 나는 그것을 오래도록 기념물로 소중히 간직했다.\n\n마부는 패거티가 다시 올 건지 묻기라도 하듯 나를 바라보았다. 나는 고개를 저으며 안 올 것 같다고 말했다. \"그럼 가자.\" 마부가 게으른 말에게 말했다. 그러자 말은 그 말대로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n이쯤 되니 울 수 있는 만큼 다 울고 나서, 더 울어 봐야 아무 소용없다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 더구나 로더릭 랜덤도, 영국 왕립 해군의 그 함장도, 내 기억에 따르면 어려운 처지에 놓여서 운 적이 한 번도 없었으니 말이다. 마부는 내가 이렇게 마음을 다잡은 것을 보고는, 손수건을 말리게 말 등에 펴 놓자고 했다. 나는 고맙다고 하고 그러자고 했다. 그렇게 펼쳐 놓고 보니 손수건은 유난히 작아 보였다.\n\n이제 지갑을 살펴볼 여유가 생겼다. 그것은 똑딱단추가 달린 빳빳한 가죽 지갑이었는데, 안에는 반짝이는 일 실링짜리 동전 세 개가 들어 있었다. 패거티가 나를 더 기쁘게 해 주려고 백묵 가루로 닦아 윤을 낸 게 분명했다. 그러나 그 안에서 가장 값진 것은 종잇조각에 함께 접혀 있던 반 크라운 동전 두 개였는데, 그 종이에는 어머니의 글씨로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"데이비에게. 사랑을 담아.\" 나는 이것에 너무 북받쳐서, 마부에게 미안하지만 손수건을 다시 건네 달라고 부탁했다. 그러나 마부는 그냥 손수건 없이 견디는 게 낫겠다고 했고, 나도 정말 그게 낫겠다 싶어서, 소매로 눈을 닦고 울음을 그쳤다.\n\n그것도 아주 그쳤다. 다만 앞서 북받친 감정 탓에, 이따금 폭풍 같은 흐느낌이 불쑥 솟구치곤 했다. 한동안 덜컹덜컹 달리고 나서, 나는 마부에게 끝까지 가느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"끝까지 어디?\" 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"거기요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"거기가 어딘데?\" 마부가 물었다.\n\n\"런던 근처요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 이 말로는,\" 마부가 말을 가리키려고 고삐를 홱 당기며 말했다. \"절반도 못 가서 돼지고기보다 더 죽어 버릴걸.\"\n\n\"그럼 야머스까지만 가시는 거예요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그쯤이지.\" 마부가 말했다. \"거기 가서 너를 역마차에 태워 줄 거고, 그 역마차가 너를 데려다줄 거다 — 거기가 어디든 말이야.\"\n\n이건 마부치고는 (그의 이름은 바키스 씨였다) 엄청나게 많은 말이었는데 — 앞 장에서 말했듯이 그는 무던하고 굼뜬 성미라 도무지 말수가 없는 사람이었으니 — 나는 정성의 표시로 과자 하나를 건넸고, 그는 그것을 영락없이 코끼리처럼 한입에 꿀꺽 삼켰는데, 그 큰 얼굴에 남긴 인상은 코끼리 얼굴에 남겼을 정도밖에 안 되었다.\n\n\"이거, 그 여자가 만든 거냐?\" 바키스 씨가 늘 그러듯 구부정한 자세로 마차 발판 위로 몸을 앞으로 기울이며, 두 무릎에 한쪽씩 팔을 얹고 물었다.\n\n\"패거티 말씀이세요, 아저씨?\"\n\n\"그래!\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. \"그 여자.\"\n\n\"네. 우리 집 빵이며 과자는 죄다 그 사람이 만들고, 요리도 다 해요.\"\n\n\"그래, 정말?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. 그는 휘파람이라도 불 것처럼 입을 모았지만, 휘파람을 불지는 않았다. 그러고는 말의 두 귀에서 무슨 새로운 거라도 보이는 듯이 그것을 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 한참을 그렇게 앉아 있다가, 이윽고 그가 말했다.\n\n\"애인은 없겠지, 아마?\"\n\n\"단것 말인가요, 바키스 아저씨?\" 나는 그가 뭔가 더 먹을 것을 원하면서, 그 종류의 군것질거리를 콕 집어 넌지시 말하는 거라고 생각했다.\n* 영어로 'sweethearts(애인)'와 'sweetmeats(단것)'의 발음이 비슷해서 데이비드가 잘못 알아들은 것이다.\n\n\"애인 말이야.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. \"애인 말이다. 그 여자랑 같이 다니는 사람이 없느냔 말이야!\"\n\n\"패거티하고요?\"\n\n\"그래!\" 그가 말했다. \"그 여자.\"\n\n\"아, 없어요. 그 사람은 애인을 가져 본 적이 한 번도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 없어!\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 다시 휘파람을 불려고 입을 모았지만, 또 휘파람을 불지 않고, 말의 두 귀만 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"그래서 그 여자가,\" 바키스 씨가 한참 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"사과 파이를 다 만들고, 요리도 다 한단 말이지, 그렇지?\"\n\n나는 정말 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"흠. 그럼 내 말 들어 봐라.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. \"혹시 너 그 여자한테 편지를 쓸지도 모르겠구나?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 꼭 쓸 거예요.\" 내가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그래!\" 그가 천천히 나를 향해 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"그럼! 혹시 그 여자한테 편지를 쓰거든, 바키스가 마음 있다고 잊지 말고 전해 줄 수 있겠냐?\"\n\n\"바키스가 마음 있다고요.\" 내가 천진하게 따라 말했다. \"그게 전할 말 전부예요?\"\n\n\"그래—.\" 그가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"그래—. 바키스가 마음 있다고.\"\n\n\"하지만 아저씨, 내일이면 다시 블런더스톤에 가시잖아요.\" 그때쯤이면 나는 거기서 멀리 떨어져 있을 거라는 생각에 목소리가 조금 떨렸지만 나는 말했다. \"그럼 직접 전하시는 게 훨씬 더 나을 텐데요.\"\n\n그러나 그가 고개를 홱 저어 이 제안을 물리치고, 더없이 진지한 얼굴로 \"바키스가 마음 있다고. 그게 전할 말이야\"라고 말하며 앞서 한 부탁을 다시 한번 못 박자, 나는 기꺼이 전해 주겠노라 떠맡았다. 바로 그날 오후 야머스의 여관에서 역마차를 기다리는 동안, 나는 종이 한 장과 잉크병을 구해서 패거티에게 짤막한 편지를 썼는데, 내용은 이러했다. \"사랑하는 패거티에게. 나는 여기 무사히 도착했어. 바키스가 마음 있대. 엄마한테 사랑한다고 전해 줘. 사랑을 담아. 추신. 바키스 아저씨가 특히 이걸 꼭 알려 달래 — 바키스가 마음 있대.\"\n\n이 심부름을 앞으로 떠맡기로 하고 나자, 바키스 씨는 완전한 침묵으로 다시 빠져들었고, 나는 요즘 일어난 온갖 일로 완전히 녹초가 되어, 마차 안 자루 위에 드러누워 잠들어 버렸다. 야머스에 다다를 때까지 나는 푹 잤다. 마차가 들어선 여관 마당은 나에게 어찌나 온통 낯설고 새로웠던지, 거기서 혹시 패거티 씨네 식구 누군가를, 어쩌면 어린 에밀리까지 만날지도 모른다고 남몰래 품었던 희망을 나는 단번에 접어 버렸다.\n\n역마차는 마당에 있었는데, 온통 번쩍번쩍 빛났으나 아직 말이 매여 있지 않았다. 그 꼴을 보니 이 마차가 런던으로 간다는 게 도무지 있을 법하지 않은 일처럼 보였다. 내가 이런 생각에 잠겨, 바키스 씨가 (마차를 돌리려고 마당 안으로 몰고 들어온 터라) 마당 포석 위 끌채 옆에 내려놓은 내 상자가 결국 어찌 될까, 또 나는 결국 어찌 될까 궁금해하고 있을 때, 닭이며 고깃덩이가 걸려 있는 활처럼 굽은 창문에서 한 부인이 내다보며 말했다.\n\n\"블런더스톤에서 온 꼬마 신사가 너냐?\"\n\n\"네, 부인.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이름이 뭐냐?\" 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"코퍼필드예요, 부인.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 안 되는데.\" 부인이 대꾸했다. \"그런 이름으로는 여기 점심값을 치른 사람이 없어.\"\n\n\"머드스톤 아닐까요, 부인?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 머드스톤 도련님이라면,\" 부인이 말했다. \"왜 처음에 딴 이름을 대고 그러느냐?\"\n\n나는 어찌 된 일인지 부인에게 설명했고, 부인은 종을 울리며 외쳤다. \"윌리엄! 커피실로 안내해!\" 그러자 마당 맞은편 부엌에서 웨이터 한 명이 안내하러 뛰어나왔는데, 안내할 상대가 고작 나뿐이라는 것을 알고는 적잖이 놀란 눈치였다.\n\n그곳은 커다란 지도 몇 장이 걸린, 길고 널찍한 방이었다. 만약 그 지도들이 진짜 외국 땅이고 내가 그 한복판에 내던져진 거라 해도, 그보다 더 낯설지는 않았을 성싶다. 모자를 손에 든 채, 문에서 가장 가까운 의자 귀퉁이에 앉는 것조차 내겐 외람된 짓 같았다. 그래서 웨이터가 일부러 나를 위해 식탁보를 깔고 그 위에 양념병 한 벌을 놓았을 때, 나는 부끄러움에 온몸이 새빨개졌을 것이다.\n\n웨이터가 양고기 토막과 채소를 가져와서 어찌나 거칠게 덮개를 홱 벗기던지, 나는 내가 그의 비위를 거스른 건 아닌가 겁이 났다. 하지만 그가 식탁에 내 의자를 놓아 주며 아주 싹싹하게 \"자, 키다리 양반! 어서!\" 하고 말하자, 내 마음은 한결 놓였다.\n\n* 키다리 양반(six-foot): 키가 6피트나 되는 어른을 부르듯 농담조로 어린 데이비드를 부른 것이다.\n나는 고맙다고 하고 식탁에 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 그가 맞은편에 서서 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지, 그리고 그와 눈이 마주칠 때마다 끔찍하리만치 얼굴이 빨개지던지, 나이프와 포크를 솜씨 있게 다루기도, 옷에 고기 국물을 튀기지 않기도 몹시 힘들었다. 그는 내가 두 번째 양고기 토막을 먹기 시작할 때까지 지켜보다가 말했다.\n\n\"맥주 반 파인트 시켜 둔 게 있어요. 지금 드실래요?\"\n\n나는 고맙다고 하고 \"네\"라고 말했다. 그러자 그는 주전자에서 큼직한 잔에 맥주를 따라, 빛에 비춰 들어 보이며 그것을 근사하게 보이도록 만들었다.\n\n\"이야!\" 그가 말했다. \"꽤 많아 보이죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"정말 많아 보이네요.\" 나는 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그가 이렇게 싹싹하게 구는 게 내겐 무척 기뻤다. 그는 눈을 반짝거리고 얼굴에 여드름이 난 사내였는데, 머리카락이 온통 곤두서 있었다. 한 손은 옆구리에 척 얹고 다른 손으로 잔을 빛에 비춰 든 채 서 있는 그 모습은 자못 다정해 보였다.\n\n\"어제 여기 한 신사가 왔었거든요.\" 그가 말했다. \"톱소여라는 이름의 뚱뚱한 신사인데 — 혹시 아세요?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 내가 말했다. \"잘 모르겠는—\"\n\n\"반바지에 각반을 차고, 챙 넓은 모자에, 잿빛 외투, 얼룩무늬 목도리를 했어요.\" 웨이터가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 내가 수줍게 말했다. \"그런 분은 영광스럽게도 알지 못—\"\n\n\"그분이 여기 들어와서,\" 웨이터가 잔 너머로 빛을 바라보며 말했다. \"이 맥주를 한 잔 시켰어요 — 기어이 시키더라고요 — 제가 그러지 말라고 했는데도 — 마셨고, 그대로 푹 쓰러져 죽었지 뭐예요. 그분한테는 너무 독한 거였어요. 따라서는 안 되는 거였죠. 사실이 그래요.\"\n\n나는 이 서글픈 사고 이야기에 몹시 충격을 받아, 그냥 물이나 좀 마시는 게 낫겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"그게 말이죠,\" 웨이터가 한쪽 눈을 찡그린 채 여전히 잔 너머로 빛을 바라보며 말했다. \"여기 사람들은 시켜 놓고 안 마시는 걸 안 좋아하거든요. 기분 나빠해요. 하지만 원하신다면 제가 마실게요. 저는 익숙하니까요, 익숙한 게 전부죠. 머리를 뒤로 젖히고 단숨에 들이켜면 저한테는 별 탈 없을 거예요. 그럴까요?\"\n\n나는 안전하게 마실 수 있다고 생각한다면 그렇게 해 주면 정말 고맙겠지만, 그렇지 않다면 절대 안 된다고 대답했다. 그가 정말로 머리를 뒤로 젖히고 단숨에 들이켤 때, 솔직히 고백하건대, 나는 그가 애통한 톱소여 씨와 똑같은 운명을 맞아 양탄자 위에 푹 고꾸라져 죽지나 않을까 끔찍하게 겁이 났다. 하지만 그에게는 아무 탈이 없었다. 오히려 그는 그걸 마시고 한결 생기가 도는 것 같았다.\n\n\"여기 이건 뭐죠?\" 그가 내 접시에 포크를 찔러 넣으며 말했다. \"양고기 토막 아니에요?\"\n\n\"양고기 토막이에요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\"아이고, 맙소사!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"양고기 토막인 줄 몰랐네요. 아니, 양고기 토막이야말로 그 맥주의 나쁜 기운을 가시게 해 주는 바로 그거잖아요! 운이 좋지 않아요?\"\n\n그러더니 그는 한 손에 뼈를 잡아 양고기 토막 하나를 들고, 다른 손에 감자 하나를 들고, 아주 맛있게 먹어 치웠는데, 그게 나는 더없이 흐뭇했다. 그는 뒤이어 또 양고기 토막 하나에 감자 하나를 먹고, 그러고 나서 또 양고기 토막 하나에 감자 하나를 먹었다. 다 먹고 나자 그는 나에게 푸딩을 가져왔는데, 그것을 내 앞에 놓고는 무슨 생각에 잠긴 듯, 잠시 멍하니 넋이 나간 것 같았다.\n\n\"파이 맛이 어때요?\" 그가 정신을 차리며 말했다.\n\n\"푸딩인데요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"푸딩!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"아니, 세상에, 정말 그러네! 이런!\" 그가 더 가까이 들여다보았다. \"이거 설마 반죽 푸딩이라는 건 아니겠죠!\"\n\n\"네, 정말 그래요.\"\n\n\"아니, 반죽 푸딩은,\" 그가 큰 숟가락을 집어 들며 말했다. \"제가 제일 좋아하는 푸딩이라고요! 운이 좋지 않아요? 자, 꼬마 친구, 누가 더 많이 먹나 한번 봅시다.\"\n\n웨이터가 더 많이 먹은 건 두말할 나위가 없었다. 그는 한 번 이상 나더러 어서 덤벼서 이겨 보라고 부추겼지만, 그의 큰 숟가락에 내 찻숟가락, 그의 잽싼 손놀림에 내 손놀림, 그의 식욕에 내 식욕으로는, 첫 한입에서 이미 한참 뒤처져 그를 당해 낼 재간이 없었다. 푸딩을 그렇게 맛있게 먹는 사람은 그때껏 본 적이 없는 것 같다. 그리고 푸딩이 다 없어졌을 때, 그는 마치 그 즐거움이 아직도 이어지는 듯이 웃었다.\n\n그가 이렇게 무척 다정하고 붙임성 있는 것을 보고, 그제야 나는 패거티에게 편지를 쓰려고 펜과 잉크와 종이를 달라고 했다. 그는 그것을 당장 가져다주었을 뿐 아니라, 친절하게도 내가 편지를 쓰는 동안 곁에서 들여다봐 주기까지 했다. 내가 편지를 다 쓰자, 그는 내가 어느 학교로 가느냐고 물었다.\n\n나는 \"런던 근처요\"라고 했는데, 그게 내가 아는 전부였다.\n\n\"아이고! 저런!\" 그가 몹시 풀 죽은 얼굴로 말했다. \"그거 안됐네요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 내가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"아이고, 맙소사!\" 그가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다. \"거기가 바로 애 갈비뼈를 부러뜨린 그 학교잖아요 — 갈비뼈 두 대를 — 조그만 사내애였는데. 그 애가 — 가만, 그러니까 — 너는 몇 살쯤 되니, 대략?\"\n\n나는 여덟 살에서 아홉 살 사이라고 말해 주었다.\n\n\"꼭 그 애 나이로구나.\" 그가 말했다. \"걔는 여덟 살하고 여섯 달일 때 첫 번째 갈비뼈가 부러졌고, 여덟 살하고 여덟 달일 때 두 번째 갈비뼈가 부러져서 끝장이 났지.\"\n\n이게 영 꺼림칙한 우연의 일치라는 사실을, 나는 나 자신에게도 웨이터에게도 숨길 수가 없어서, 어쩌다 그렇게 됐느냐고 물었다. 그의 대답은 내 마음을 도무지 달래 주지 못했으니, 그것은 음울한 한마디 — \"두들겨 패서요\"였다.\n\n마당에서 마차 나팔 소리가 울린 것은 때마침 알맞은 기분 전환거리였는데, 그 덕에 나는 자리에서 일어나, 지갑을 (주머니에서 꺼내며) 가졌다는 자부심과 머뭇거림이 뒤섞인 채로, 치를 게 있느냐고 우물쭈물 물었다.\n\n\"편지지 한 장이 있죠.\" 그가 대답했다. \"편지지 한 장 사 보신 적 있어요?\"\n\n나는 사 본 적이 있는지 도무지 기억나지 않았다.\n\n\"비싸요.\" 그가 말했다. \"세금 때문에요. 삼 펜스예요. 이 나라에서는 이런 식으로 세금을 매기거든요. 그것 말고는 없어요, 웨이터 빼고는요. 잉크는 신경 쓰지 마세요. 그건 제가 손해 보는 거니까요.\"\n\n\"제가 — 그러니까 제가 — 웨이터한테 얼마나 — 얼마를 드리는 게 맞을까요, 괜찮으시다면?\" 나는 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"저한테 식구가 없고, 그 식구가 마마에 안 걸렸다면,\" 웨이터가 말했다. \"육 펜스도 안 받을 거예요. 제가 늙은 부모님하고 사랑스러운 누이를 부양하지 않는다면,\" — 여기서 웨이터는 몹시 격해졌다 — \"한 푼도 안 받을 거예요. 제게 좋은 일자리가 있고 여기서 대접을 잘 받는다면, 받기는커녕 한 푼이라도 받아 달라고 빌어야 마땅하죠. 하지만 저는 먹다 남은 음식으로 연명하고 — 석탄 위에서 잔답니다\" — 여기서 웨이터는 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n나는 그의 불행이 몹시 안쓰러워서, 구 펜스에 못 미치는 어떤 사례도 그저 잔인하고 모진 짓일 뿐이라는 생각이 들었다. 그래서 나는 반짝이는 일 실링짜리 동전 세 개 중 하나를 그에게 주었고, 그는 그것을 몹시 황송하고 공손한 태도로 받더니, 곧바로 엄지손가락으로 휙 튕겨 진짜인지 확인했다.\n\n마차 뒤에 올라타도록 부축을 받을 때, 그 점심을 누구의 도움도 없이 나 혼자 다 먹어 치운 걸로 여겨지고 있다는 걸 알고 나는 좀 당황스러웠다. 활처럼 굽은 창문의 부인이 차장에게 \"조지, 저 애 잘 지켜봐, 안 그러면 배가 터질 거야!\" 하고 말하는 것을 엿듣고, 또 그곳에 있던 하녀들이 나를 어린 진귀한 구경거리라도 되는 양 보고 키득거리려고 나오는 것을 보고 나서야 알아챈 것이다. 한결 기운을 차린 나의 불운한 친구 웨이터는 이 일에 조금도 동요하는 기색 없이, 전혀 멋쩍어하지 않고 다 함께 감탄하는 데 끼어들었다. 만약 그에게 의심이 들었다면, 아마 이 일이 그 의심을 어렴풋이 일깨웠을 테지만, 내 생각엔, 아이 특유의 순진한 믿음과 손윗사람에 대한 아이 특유의 자연스러운 의지로 (아이들이 이런 자질을 너무 일찍 세상 물정에 밝은 약삭빠름과 맞바꾸는 건 정말 안타까운 일이다), 그때조차도 나는 그를 대체로 크게 미심쩍어하지는 않았던 것 같다.\n\n솔직히 고백하건대, 받아도 싼 짓을 한 것도 아닌데, 내가 거기 앉아 있는 탓에 마차 뒤쪽이 무거워졌다느니, 차라리 짐마차로 가는 게 더 나을 거라느니 하는 마부와 차장 사이의 농담거리가 된 것은 좀 견디기 힘들었다. 내가 먹어 치웠다는 그 식욕 이야기가 바깥쪽 손님들 사이에 퍼지자, 그들도 그걸로 흥겨워하며, 학교에서 내 식비를 형제 두 사람 몫으로 내느냐 세 사람 몫으로 내느냐, 정찰제로 계약했느냐 보통 요금으로 다니느냐 따위 유쾌한 질문을 던져 댔다. 하지만 그중에서도 최악은, 기회가 와도 부끄러워서 아무것도 못 먹을 게 뻔하다는 것, 그래서 점심을 다소 가볍게 먹은 탓에 밤새 굶주린 채로 있게 되리라는 것이었다 — 서두르는 바람에 과자를 여관에 두고 와 버렸으니까. 내 걱정은 그대로 들어맞았다. 저녁을 먹으려고 멈춰 섰을 때, 나는 무척 먹고 싶었으면서도 도무지 무엇이든 먹을 용기를 내지 못해, 불가에 앉아 아무것도 안 먹겠다고만 했다. 그렇다고 더한 농담을 면한 것도 아니었으니, 거의 내내 샌드위치 통에서 뭔가를 꺼내 먹다가 이따금 병에서 술을 들이켜던, 거친 얼굴에 목소리가 쉰 한 신사가, 나더러 한 끼에 오래 버틸 만큼 잔뜩 먹어 두는 보아 구렁이 같다고 했다. 그러고는 정작 자기가 삶은 쇠고기를 먹고 두드러기가 돋았다.\n\n우리는 오후 세 시에 야머스를 출발했고, 이튿날 아침 여덟 시쯤 런던에 닿을 예정이었다. 한여름 날씨라 저녁은 무척 상쾌했다. 마을을 지날 때면, 나는 집 안이 어떤 모습일지, 사람들은 무엇을 하고 있을지 마음속에 그려 보았다. 그리고 사내애들이 우리를 뒤쫓아 달려와 마차 뒤에 올라타 잠깐씩 매달려 갈 때면, 그 애들의 아버지는 살아 있을까, 그 애들은 집에서 행복할까 궁금해했다. 그러니 내가 가고 있는 곳이 어떤 데일까 끊임없이 마음을 졸이는 것 말고도 — 그건 무시무시한 상상이었다 — 생각할 거리가 많았다. 가끔, 기억하건대, 나는 집과 패거티 생각에 몸을 맡기기도 했다. 그리고 어수선하고 막막한 심정으로, 내가 머드스톤 씨를 물기 전에 어떤 기분이었는지, 어떤 아이였는지 떠올려 보려 애쓰기도 했다. 하지만 그건 도무지 만족스럽게 떠오르지 않았으니, 그를 문 것이 까마득히 먼 옛날 일처럼 느껴졌기 때문이다.\n\n밤은 저녁만큼 상쾌하지 않았다. 쌀쌀해졌기 때문이다. 게다가 마차에서 굴러떨어지지 않게 두 신사 (거친 얼굴의 그 신사와 또 다른 신사) 사이에 끼인 탓에, 그들이 잠들어 나를 완전히 틀어막는 바람에 나는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 그들은 가끔 나를 어찌나 세게 짓누르던지, 나는 \"아! 제발요!\" 하고 외치지 않을 수가 없었는데 — 그러면 그들은 잠이 깨는 통에 그걸 영 싫어했다. 내 맞은편에는 커다란 모피 외투를 두른 나이 든 부인이 있었는데, 어찌나 칭칭 싸맸던지 어둠 속에서는 부인이라기보다 건초 더미처럼 보였다. 이 부인은 바구니 하나를 가지고 있었는데, 오랫동안 그걸 어찌해야 할지 몰라 하다가, 내 다리가 짧으니 그 바구니를 내 밑에 넣을 수 있다는 걸 알아냈다. 그게 나를 어찌나 옥죄고 아프게 하던지 나는 완전히 비참한 꼴이 됐다. 그런데 내가 조금이라도 움직여서 바구니 안의 유리잔이 다른 무언가에 부딪쳐 달그락 소리를 내면 (틀림없이 그런 소리가 났다), 부인은 발로 나를 더없이 모질게 쿡 찌르며 말했다. \"이봐, 가만 좀 있어. 네 뼈는 분명 아직 젊잖니!\"\n\n마침내 해가 떴고, 그제야 동행들은 좀 편히 자는 듯했다. 그들이 밤새 시달리며 더없이 끔찍한 헐떡임과 콧방귀로 토해 낸 그 고생이란 이루 말할 수 없을 지경이었다. 해가 높이 솟을수록 그들의 잠은 얕아졌고, 그렇게 하나씩 차례로 잠에서 깨어났다. 그때 다들 잠 따위는 한숨도 안 잔 척 시치미를 뚝 떼는 것에, 그리고 그런 혐의를 받으면 저마다 유난스레 발끈 화를 내는 것에 내가 무척 놀랐던 기억이 난다. 나는 오늘날까지도 똑같은 종류의 놀라움에 빠져 있으니, 인간의 온갖 약점 가운데 우리 인지상정이 가장 인정하기 싫어하는 것이 (왜 그런지는 도무지 모르겠지만) 바로 마차 안에서 잠들었다는 약점임을 한결같이 보아 왔기 때문이다.\n\n멀리서 런던을 바라보았을 때 그곳이 나에게 얼마나 놀라운 곳이었는지, 내가 가장 좋아하는 영웅들의 온갖 모험이 죄다 그곳에서 끊임없이 펼쳐지고 또 펼쳐지고 있으리라고 얼마나 굳게 믿었는지, 그곳이 이 세상 어떤 도시보다도 경이와 사악함으로 가득 찬 곳이라고 내 마음속에서 얼마나 어렴풋이 그려 냈는지는 여기서 굳이 멈춰 이야기할 필요가 없겠다. 우리는 조금씩 런던에 다가가, 때맞춰 목적지인 화이트채플 구역의 여관에 다다랐다. 그게 블루 불이었는지 블루 보어였는지는 잊어버렸지만, '블루 무엇'이었다는 것, 그리고 그 그림이 마차 뒤에 그려져 있었다는 것만은 기억난다.\n* 블루 불, 블루 보어: 각각 '푸른 황소', '푸른 멧돼지'라는 뜻의 여관 이름이다.\n\n차장이 마차에서 내리다가 나에게 눈길이 닿자, 그는 매표소 문 앞에서 말했다.\n\n\"여기, 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 머드스톤이란 이름으로 예약하고, 데리러 올 때까지 맡겨 두라고 한 꼬맹이를 찾으러 온 사람 있소?\"\n\n아무도 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"코퍼필드로 한번 찾아 주세요, 아저씨.\" 내가 막막하게 고개를 숙인 채 말했다.\n\n\"여기, 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 머드스톤이란 이름으로 예약했는데, 코퍼필드란 이름이라고 우기는 꼬맹이를, 데리러 올 때까지 맡겨 두라고 한 거 찾으러 온 사람 있소?\" 차장이 말했다. \"이봐요! 누구 없소?\"\n\n없었다. 아무도 없었다. 나는 불안하게 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그 물음은 구경꾼 누구에게도 아무 인상을 남기지 못했으니, 외눈에 각반을 찬 사내 하나만 빼고는 그랬다. 그 사내는 차라리 내 목에 놋쇠 목줄을 채워서 마구간에 묶어 두는 게 낫겠다고 한마디 했다.\n\n사다리가 와서, 나는 건초 더미 같던 그 부인 다음에 내렸는데, 부인의 바구니가 치워질 때까지 감히 꼼짝도 못 했다. 그 무렵 마차에서 승객은 모두 빠져나갔고, 짐도 곧 다 치워졌으며, 말은 짐보다 먼저 풀려 나갔고, 이제 마차 자체도 마부들이 굴려서 뒤로 빼내 한쪽으로 치워 버렸다. 그래도 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 온 먼지투성이 꼬맹이를 찾으러 나타나는 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n자기가 외톨이라는 걸 보아 줄 사람조차 없었던 로빈슨 크루소보다도 더 외로운 신세로, 나는 매표소 안으로 들어갔고, 당번 직원이 권하기에 계산대 뒤로 가서 짐 무게를 다는 저울 위에 앉았다. 거기 앉아 소포며 꾸러미며 장부 들을 바라보고, 마구간 냄새를 들이마시며 (그 냄새는 그 후로 줄곧 그날 아침과 한데 얽혀 떠오른다), 더없이 무시무시한 온갖 상념이 내 마음속을 줄지어 행진하기 시작했다. 만약 아무도 영영 나를 데리러 오지 않으면, 저 사람들은 나를 얼마나 오래 여기 두는 데 동의해 줄까? 칠 실링을 다 쓸 만큼 오래 두어 줄까? 밤에는 다른 짐들과 함께 저 나무통 같은 칸 가운데 하나에서 자고, 아침에는 마당 펌프에서 몸을 씻어야 하나? 아니면 매일 밤 쫓겨났다가, 이튿날 매표소 문이 열리면 다시 와서 데리러 올 때까지 맡겨져 있어야 하나? 만약 이게 착오 같은 게 아니라, 머드스톤 씨가 나를 떼어 버리려고 이 계획을 꾸민 거라면, 나는 어찌해야 하나? 칠 실링을 다 쓸 때까지 여기 머무르게 해 준다 해도, 막상 굶주리기 시작하면 여기 더 있기를 바랄 수는 없을 것이다. 그건 손님들에게 분명 불편하고 거북한 일일 테고, 게다가 그 블루 무엇이라는 여관에 장례 비용까지 떠안길 위험이 있을 테니까. 만약 당장 떠나서 걸어서 집으로 돌아가려 한다면, 어떻게 길을 찾으며, 그 먼 길을 걸어갈 엄두를 어찌 내며, 설령 돌아간들 패거티 말고 누구를 믿을 수 있단 말인가? 만약 가장 가까운 관청을 찾아내 군인이나 뱃사람으로 가겠다고 나선다 해도, 나는 워낙 조그만 녀석이라 십중팔구 받아 주지 않을 것이다. 이런 생각, 그리고 이와 비슷한 수백 가지 생각에 나는 온몸이 후끈 달아오르고, 두려움과 절망으로 머리가 어질어질했다. 한창 이런 열병에 빠져 있을 때, 한 사내가 들어와 직원에게 뭐라고 속삭였고, 직원은 곧 나를 저울에서 미끄러뜨려 그 사내 쪽으로 떠밀었는데, 마치 내가 무게를 달아 팔리고, 넘겨지고, 값이 치러진 물건이라도 되는 것 같았다.\n\n이 새로 알게 된 사람과 손을 맞잡고 매표소를 나서면서, 나는 그를 몰래 흘끔 살폈다. 그는 핼쑥하고 누르께한 얼굴빛의 젊은이로, 두 볼이 움푹 꺼졌고, 턱은 머드스톤 씨만큼이나 거뭇했다. 그러나 닮은 점은 거기까지였으니, 그는 구레나룻을 밀어 버렸고, 머리카락도 윤기가 흐르기는커녕 푸석푸석하고 메말라 있었다. 그는 검은 양복 한 벌을 입고 있었는데, 그것 역시 꽤 낡고 메마른 데다, 소매와 바짓단이 좀 짧았다. 그리고 그리 깨끗하달 수 없는 흰 목도리를 두르고 있었다. 나는 이 목도리가 그가 걸친 속옷의 전부라고 그때도 생각지 않았고 지금도 생각지 않지만, 어쨌든 그가 내보이거나 그런 낌새라도 풍긴 것은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n\"네가 새로 온 애냐?\" 그가 말했다. \"네, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n나는 그런가 보다 했다. 나도 잘 몰랐다.\n\n\"나는 세일럼 하우스의 선생들 중 하나다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 그에게 고개 숙여 인사하면서 몹시 주눅이 들었다. 세일럼 하우스의 학자이자 선생님에게 내 상자 따위 같은 시시한 것을 입에 올리기가 어찌나 부끄럽던지, 마당에서 좀 떨어진 데까지 가고 나서야 가까스로 용기를 내어 그 얘기를 꺼냈다. 그것이 나중에 내게 쓸모가 있을지도 모른다고 겸손하게 슬쩍 비추자, 우리는 발길을 되돌렸다. 그는 직원에게, 마부가 정오에 그것을 가지러 오라는 지시를 받았다고 일러 두었다.\n\n\"저, 선생님,\" 우리가 아까와 비슷한 거리를 걸어왔을 때 내가 말했다. \"거기까지 먼가요?\"\n\n\"블랙히스 쪽에 있단다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 먼가요, 선생님?\" 내가 조심스레 물었다.\n\n\"제법 걸어야 하지.\" 그가 말했다. \"역마차로 갈 거다. 한 육 마일쯤 돼.\"\n\n나는 어찌나 기진맥진하고 지쳤던지, 육 마일을 더 버틴다는 생각만으로도 견디기 힘들었다. 나는 용기를 내어 그에게, 밤새 아무것도 먹지 못했다고, 그러니 뭔가 먹을 것을 사게 허락해 주신다면 정말 고맙겠다고 말했다. 그는 이 말에 놀란 듯했다 — 지금도 그가 걸음을 멈추고 나를 바라보던 모습이 눈에 선하다 — 그러고는 잠시 생각하더니, 멀지 않은 곳에 사는 어떤 노인을 찾아갈 참이라며, 가장 좋은 방법은 내가 빵이든, 몸에 해롭지 않으면서 제일 먹고 싶은 것이든 사 가지고, 그 노인 집에서 아침을 먹는 거라고, 거기서 우유도 좀 얻을 수 있다고 했다.\n\n그래서 우리는 빵집 진열창을 들여다보았는데, 내가 가게 안의 느끼한 것들을 죄다 사겠다고 줄줄이 제안하고, 그가 그것을 하나씩 하나씩 물리친 끝에, 우리는 삼 펜스짜리 갈색 빵 한 덩이를 사기로 했다. 그러고 나서 식료품점에서 달걀 하나와 비계가 섞인 베이컨 한 조각을 샀는데, 그러고도 반짝이는 일 실링짜리 동전 두 번째 것에서 내 생각에 꽤 많은 거스름돈이 남아서, 나는 런던이 무척 값싼 곳이라고 여기게 되었다. 이 먹을거리를 챙겨 넣고, 우리는 내 지친 머리를 이루 말할 수 없이 어지럽히는 엄청난 소음과 소란을 뚫고 나아가, 분명 런던 다리였을 다리를 (실은 그가 그렇게 말해 준 것 같은데, 나는 반쯤 졸고 있었다) 건너서, 마침내 그 가난한 노인의 집에 다다랐다. 그곳은 한눈에 봐도, 그리고 대문 위 돌에 새겨진, 가난한 여인 스물다섯 명을 위해 세워졌다는 글귀로 보아도, 자선 양로원의 한 채였다.\n\n세일럼 하우스의 선생님은 죄다 똑같이 생긴 자그마한 검은 문 여러 개 가운데 하나의 걸쇠를 들어 올렸는데, 그 문들은 저마다 한쪽에 마름모꼴 격자창이 하나, 그 위에 또 마름모꼴 격자창이 하나 달려 있었다. 우리는 그 가난한 노파들 중 한 사람의 작은 집으로 들어갔는데, 노파는 작은 냄비를 끓이려고 불을 후후 불고 있었다. 선생님이 들어오는 것을 보자 노파는 풀무를 무릎에 얹은 채로 멈추고, 내 귀에는 \"우리 찰리!\"처럼 들린 무슨 말을 했다. 그러나 나까지 들어오는 것을 보고는 일어나서, 두 손을 비비며 어정쩡하게 어설픈 무릎인사를 했다.\n\n\"괜찮으시면 이 어린 신사분 아침을 좀 차려 주시겠어요?\" 세일럼 하우스의 선생님이 말했다.\n\n\"되고말고!\" 노파가 말했다. \"암, 되고말고요!\"\n\n\"오늘 피비슨 부인은 좀 어떠세요?\" 선생님이, 불가의 커다란 의자에 앉은 또 다른 노파를 바라보며 말했다. 그 노파는 옷가지를 한 덩어리로 둘둘 뭉쳐 놓은 꼴이라, 잘못해서 그 위에 깔고 앉지 않은 게 지금 이 순간까지도 고마울 지경이었다.\n\n\"아휴, 그이는 시름시름해요.\" 첫 번째 노파가 말했다. \"오늘은 안 좋은 날 중 하루라서요. 어쩌다 불이라도 꺼지면, 정말이지 그이도 같이 꺼져서 다시는 살아나지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n그들이 그 노파를 바라보자, 나도 그 노파를 바라보았다. 따뜻한 날이었는데도, 그 노파는 오로지 불 생각밖에 없는 듯했다. 나는 그 노파가 불에 올려놓은 냄비마저도 시샘하는 게 아닌가 싶었다. 그리고 내 달걀을 삶고 베이컨을 굽는 일에 그 냄비가 징발된 것을 노파가 못마땅해했다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 있으니, 내 두 눈으로, 그 부엌일이 한창이고 아무도 보지 않을 때 노파가 나를 향해 한 번 주먹을 흔들어 대는 것을 똑똑히 보았기 때문이다. 햇살이 작은 창으로 쏟아져 들어왔지만, 노파는 자기 등과 커다란 의자 등받이를 그쪽으로 돌린 채 앉아, 마치 불이 자기를 따뜻하게 해 주는 게 아니라 자기가 부지런히 불을 따뜻하게 지켜 주기라도 하는 양 불을 가리고서, 더없이 미심쩍은 눈초리로 불을 지켜보고 있었다. 내 아침 채비가 마무리되어 불이 풀려나자, 노파는 어찌나 기뻤던지 소리 내어 웃었는데 — 그 웃음소리가 도무지 곱지 않았다고 말해 두어야겠다.\n\n나는 갈색 빵 덩이와 달걀, 베이컨 한 조각, 거기에 우유 한 사발까지 차려진 앞에 앉아, 더없이 맛있는 식사를 했다. 내가 아직 한창 그것을 즐기고 있을 때, 그 집 노파가 선생님에게 말했다.\n\n\"플루트 가져왔수?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"한 곡 불어 봐요.\" 노파가 어르듯이 말했다. \"어서!\"\n\n이 말에 선생님은 외투 자락 밑으로 손을 넣어 세 토막으로 된 플루트를 꺼내, 그것들을 돌려 맞추더니, 곧장 불기 시작했다. 여러 해 곰곰이 생각해 본 끝에 내가 받은 인상은, 이 세상에 그보다 더 못 부는 사람은 결코 있을 수 없으리라는 것이다. 그는 자연적이든 인위적이든 어떤 수단으로 낸 것 중에서도 내가 들어 본 가장 음울한 소리를 냈다. 그 곡조가 무엇이었는지 나는 모른다 — 애당초 그 연주에 곡조라는 게 있었기나 했는지도 의심스럽지만 — 어쨌든 그 가락이 내게 미친 영향은, 처음에는 내 온갖 슬픔을 떠올리게 해서 눈물을 참기 힘들 지경으로 만들었고, 다음에는 식욕을 가시게 했으며, 마지막에는 어찌나 졸음이 쏟아지던지 눈을 뜨고 있을 수가 없게 만들었다. 그 기억이 새삼 생생히 떠오르는 지금도, 다시 눈이 감기고 고개가 끄덕끄덕 졸린다. 또다시 그 작은 방이 — 모서리에 열린 찬장이며, 등받이가 네모난 의자들이며, 위층 방으로 이어지는 모난 작은 계단이며, 벽난로 선반 위에 펼쳐 놓은 공작 깃털 세 개가 (처음 들어갔을 때, 만약 그 공작이 제 화려한 깃털이 이런 신세로 전락할 줄 알았더라면 무슨 생각을 했을까 궁금해했던 기억이 난다) 있던 그 방이 — 내 눈앞에서 흐려지고, 나는 고개를 끄덕이며 잠든다. 플루트 소리는 들리지 않게 되고, 그 대신 마차 바퀴 소리가 들리며, 나는 여행길에 올라 있다. 마차가 덜컹하고, 나는 화들짝 깨어나고, 플루트 소리는 다시 돌아와 있으며, 세일럼 하우스의 선생님은 다리를 꼰 채 앉아 구슬프게 그것을 불고, 그 집 노파는 황홀해하며 바라보고 있다. 이번에는 노파가 흐려지고, 그가 흐려지고, 모든 것이 흐려지더니, 플루트도 없고, 선생님도 없고, 세일럼 하우스도 없고, 데이비드 코퍼필드도 없고, 깊은 잠 말고는 아무것도 없게 된다.\n\n나는 이런 꿈을 꾼 것 같았다. 그가 이 음울한 플루트를 불고 있던 어느 순간, 황홀한 감탄에 젖어 그에게 점점 더 가까이 다가가던 그 집 노파가 의자 등받이 너머로 몸을 기울여 그의 목을 다정하게 꼭 끌어안았고, 그 바람에 그의 연주가 잠깐 멎었다는 꿈을. 나는 그때, 혹은 바로 그 직후에, 잠과 깸 사이의 어중간한 상태에 있었다. 왜냐하면 그가 다시 연주를 시작했을 때 — 그가 연주를 멈췄던 것은 실제 있었던 일이다 — 나는 그 똑같은 노파가 피비슨 부인에게 (플루트를 가리키며) 황홀하지 않으냐고 묻는 것을 보고 들었고, 그러자 피비슨 부인이 \"아무렴, 아무렴! 그렇고말고!\" 하고 대답하며 불을 향해 고개를 끄덕이는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 나는 그 노파가 이 모든 연주의 공을 불에 돌렸다고 확신한다.\n\n내가 오랫동안 꾸벅꾸벅 존 것 같았을 즈음, 세일럼 하우스의 선생님은 플루트를 세 토막으로 돌려 풀어 아까처럼 챙겨 넣고, 나를 데리고 떠났다. 우리는 아주 가까운 데서 마차를 찾아 지붕 위에 올라탔다. 그러나 나는 죽은 듯이 졸려서, 도중에 다른 사람을 태우려고 멈춰 섰을 때, 사람들이 손님이 아무도 없는 안쪽에 나를 들여놓았고, 나는 그 안에서 깊이 잠들었다가, 마차가 푸른 잎새들 사이로 가파른 언덕을 걸음 속도로 오르고 있을 때에야 정신을 차렸다. 이윽고 마차가 멈췄고, 목적지에 다다른 것이었다.\n\n조금 걷자 우리는 — 그러니까 선생님과 나는 — 세일럼 하우스에 이르렀는데, 그곳은 높다란 벽돌 담으로 둘러싸여 있어 무척 음침해 보였다. 이 담의 문 위에는 '세일럼 하우스'라고 쓰인 간판이 걸려 있었고, 우리가 종을 울리자 이 문의 쇠창살 너머로 어떤 무뚝뚝한 얼굴이 우리를 살펴보았는데, 문이 열리고 보니 그 얼굴은 황소 같은 목에, 나무 의족을 하고, 관자놀이가 툭 튀어나오고, 머리는 빙 둘러 짧게 깎은 건장한 사내의 것이었다.\n\n\"새로 온 애요.\" 선생님이 말했다.\n\n나무 의족을 한 사내는 나를 위아래로 훑어보았는데 — 나라는 게 별로 볼 것도 없어서 오래 걸리지는 않았다 — 우리 뒤로 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 빼냈다. 우리가 어둡고 묵직한 나무들 사이로 집을 향해 올라가고 있을 때, 그가 내 인솔자를 뒤에서 불렀다. \"이봐요!\"\n\n우리가 뒤돌아보니, 그는 자기가 사는 작은 수위실 문 앞에 부츠 한 켤레를 손에 들고 서 있었다.\n\n\"여기! 구두장이가 다녀갔소.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신 나가 있는 동안에요, 멜 선생. 이젠 더 못 고치겠다는데. 본래 부츠는 한 조각도 안 남았다고, 당신이 그걸 바라는 게 신기하다더군.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 부츠를 멜 선생 쪽으로 휙 던졌고, 멜 선생은 몇 걸음 되돌아가 그것을 주워, 우리가 함께 걸어가는 동안 (몹시 풀이 죽은 채로, 내겐 그렇게 보였다) 그것을 바라보았다. 그제야 나는 처음으로, 그가 신고 있는 부츠가 꽤 닳아 빠졌다는 것, 그리고 그의 양말이 한 군데에서 마치 꽃봉오리처럼 막 터져 나오고 있다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n세일럼 하우스는 양 날개가 달린 네모난 벽돌 건물이었는데, 휑하고 휘휑한 모습이었다. 그 둘레가 어찌나 고요하던지, 나는 멜 선생에게 아이들이 다 나가 있나 보다고 말했다. 그러나 그는 지금이 방학 때라는 걸 내가 모른다는 사실에 놀란 듯했다. 아이들이 모두 저마다 집에 가 있다는 것, 학교 주인인 크리클 선생은 크리클 부인과 크리클 양과 함께 바닷가에 가 있다는 것, 그리고 내가 잘못을 저지른 벌로 방학 때 보내졌다는 것을, 그는 우리가 걸어가는 동안 죄다 설명해 주었다.\n\n그가 나를 데리고 들어간 교실을, 나는 그때껏 본 가운데 가장 쓸쓸하고 황량한 곳이라며 바라보았다. 지금도 눈에 선하다. 길쭉한 책상이 세 줄, 긴 의자가 여섯 줄 놓인 기다란 방인데, 사방으로 모자와 석판을 거는 못이 빙 둘러 삐죽삐죽 돋아 있었다. 너덜너덜한 옛 습자 공책과 연습장 조각들이 지저분한 바닥에 어지러이 널려 있었다. 같은 재료로 만든 누에 집 몇 개가 책상 위에 흩어져 있었다. 주인이 두고 간 가엾은 흰 생쥐 두 마리가, 판지와 철사로 만든 곰팡내 나는 성채 안을 위아래로 오르내리며, 빨간 눈으로 먹을 게 없나 구석구석 살피고 있었다. 새장 하나는 그 안의 새보다 별로 크지도 않았는데, 그 새는 이따금 두 인치 높이의 횃대에 폴짝 뛰어오르거나 거기서 뛰어내리면서 구슬픈 소리를 달그락 냈지만, 노래하지도 지저귀지도 않았다. 그 방에는 곰팡이 핀 코듀로이며, 바람을 못 쐰 단 사과며, 썩은 책 냄새 같은, 이상하고 퀴퀴한 냄새가 배어 있었다. 그곳에 잉크가 그보다 더 튀어 있을 수는 없었으니, 마치 처음 지어질 때부터 지붕이 없어, 일 년 사철 내내 하늘에서 잉크가 비처럼, 눈처럼, 우박처럼 쏟아지고 잉크 바람이 분 것만 같았다.\n\n멜 선생이 고칠 수 없게 된 부츠를 들고 위층으로 올라가며 나를 두고 간 사이, 나는 방의 위쪽 끝으로 살금살금 걸어가, 기어가듯 다가가면서 이 모든 것을 살펴보았다. 그러다가 문득, 책상 위에 놓여 있던 한 판지 푯말과 마주쳤는데, 거기에는 아름다운 글씨로 이런 말이 적혀 있었다. \"이 아이를 조심할 것. 무는 버릇이 있음.\"\n\n나는 적어도 책상 밑에 커다란 개가 있겠거니 겁이 나서, 곧장 책상 위로 올라갔다. 그러나 불안한 눈으로 사방을 둘러봐도, 개라고는 도무지 보이지 않았다. 내가 아직도 두리번거리고 있을 때, 멜 선생이 돌아와서 거기서 뭘 하느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"죄송해요, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"저, 개를 찾고 있는데요.\"\n\n\"개?\" 그가 말했다. \"무슨 개?\"\n\n\"개 아닌가요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"뭐가 개라는 거냐?\"\n\n\"조심하라는 거요, 선생님. 문다는 거요.\"\n\n\"아니다, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 진지하게 말했다. \"그건 개가 아니야. 그건 아이다. 코퍼필드, 내가 받은 지시는 이 푯말을 네 등에 붙이라는 거다. 너와 이런 식으로 시작하게 되어 미안하지만, 나는 그렇게 해야만 한다.\" 그러면서 그는 나를 책상에서 내려놓고, 그 목적에 맞게 깔끔하게 만들어진 그 푯말을, 배낭처럼 내 어깨에 묶었다. 그리고 그 후로 어디를 가든, 나는 그것을 짊어지고 다니는 위안을 누리게 되었다.\n\n그 푯말 때문에 내가 어떤 고통을 겪었는지는 아무도 상상하지 못할 것이다. 사람들이 나를 볼 수 있든 없든, 나는 늘 누군가 그걸 읽고 있다는 상상에 시달렸다. 뒤를 돌아봐 아무도 없다는 걸 알아도 마음이 놓이지 않았으니, 내 등이 가는 곳마다 거기에는 늘 누군가 있으리라고 상상했기 때문이다. 그 나무 의족을 한 잔인한 사내는 내 고통을 더 키웠다. 그는 권한을 쥐고 있었는데, 내가 나무나 담벼락이나 건물에 기대 있는 걸 볼 때면 언제든 자기 수위실 문에서 어마어마한 목소리로 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"이봐, 거기 너! 너 코퍼필드! 그 표를 잘 보이게 내놓지 않으면 일러바칠 테다!\" 운동장은 휑한 자갈 마당으로, 집 뒤편과 부속 건물들이 죄다 내다보였다. 그래서 나는 하인들도 그걸 읽고, 푸주한도 그걸 읽고, 빵장수도 그걸 읽는다는 것을, 한마디로 내가 거기서 걸으라는 명을 받은 아침에 그 집을 드나드는 사람이라면 누구나, 내가 무니까 조심해야 한다고 적힌 그것을 읽는다는 것을 알았다. 기억하건대 나는, 정말로 무는 버릇이 있는 일종의 야생 소년이라며, 나 자신을 두려워하기 시작했다.\n\n이 운동장에는 낡은 문이 하나 있었는데, 아이들은 거기에 자기 이름을 새기는 풍습이 있었다. 그 문은 그런 새김 글씨로 빈틈없이 뒤덮여 있었다. 방학이 끝나고 아이들이 돌아올 일이 두려운 나머지, 나는 한 아이의 이름을 읽으면서, 그 애가 어떤 말투로, 어떤 강세를 실어 \"이 아이를 조심할 것. 무는 버릇이 있음\"을 읽을까 떠올려 보지 않고는 견딜 수 없었다. 한 아이는 — J. 스티어포스라는 아이는 — 자기 이름을 아주 깊게, 아주 자주 새겨 놓았는데, 나는 그 애가 그것을 다소 우렁찬 목소리로 읽고 나서 내 머리카락을 잡아당기리라고 생각했다. 또 다른 아이로 토미 트래들스라는 애가 있었는데, 나는 그 애가 그것을 가지고 장난치며 나를 끔찍이 무서워하는 척하지나 않을까 겁이 났다. 세 번째로 조지 뎀플이라는 애가 있었는데, 나는 그 애가 그것을 노래로 부를 것 같았다. 나는 잔뜩 움츠러든 작은 존재가 되어 그 문을 바라보았는데, 마침내 그 모든 이름의 주인들이 — 멜 선생 말로는 그때 학교에 마흔다섯 명이 있었다 — 다 함께 환호하며 나를 따돌리고, 저마다 제 방식대로 \"이 아이를 조심할 것. 무는 버릇이 있음!\" 하고 외쳐 대는 것만 같았다.\n\n책상과 의자의 자리들도 마찬가지였다. 내 침대로 가는 길에, 그리고 내 침대에 누워서 슬쩍 들여다본, 텅 빈 침대들이 늘어선 그 숲도 마찬가지였다. 기억하건대 나는 밤이면 밤마다 꿈을 꾸었다. 예전 모습 그대로인 어머니와 함께 있는 꿈, 패거티 씨 집 잔치에 가는 꿈, 역마차 바깥쪽에 타고 가는 꿈, 나의 불운한 친구 웨이터와 다시 식사하는 꿈을. 그런데 이 모든 상황에서, 나는 내가 조그만 잠옷 하나에 그 푯말 말고는 아무것도 걸치지 않았다는 불행한 사실을 들통내는 바람에, 사람들이 비명을 지르며 빤히 쳐다보게 만들었다.\n\n단조로운 나의 일상 속에서, 그리고 학교가 다시 열린다는 끊임없는 두려움 속에서, 그것은 정말이지 견딜 수 없는 괴로움이었다! 나는 날마다 멜 선생과 함께 긴 과제를 해야 했다. 그러나 여기에는 머드스톤 씨도 머드스톤 양도 없었기에, 나는 그 과제를 해냈고, 망신당하는 일 없이 마쳤다. 과제 전후로 나는 이리저리 거닐었는데 — 앞서 말했듯 나무 의족을 한 사내의 감시를 받으며 그랬다. 집 둘레의 축축함, 안마당의 금이 가고 푸르스름한 포석들, 물이 새는 낡은 빗물통, 그리고 음산한 나무들 중 몇 그루의 색이 바랜 줄기 — 다른 나무들보다 비를 더 많이 맞아 물이 뚝뚝 떨어지고, 햇볕은 덜 쬔 듯한 그 줄기들이 얼마나 생생하게 떠오르는지! 한 시가 되면 멜 선생과 나는 식사를 했는데, 전나무 식탁이 가득 들어차고 비계 냄새가 풍기는 길쭉하고 휑한 식당의 위쪽 끝에서였다. 그러고 나서 차 마실 시간까지 또 과제를 했는데, 차는 멜 선생은 파란 찻잔으로, 나는 양철 주전자로 마셨다. 온종일, 그리고 저녁 일고여덟 시까지, 멜 선생은 교실 안 자기만의 외딴 책상에서, 펜과 잉크와 자와 책과 종이를 가지고 부지런히 일했는데, (내가 알아낸 바로는) 지난 학기 청구서를 작성하는 것이었다. 밤에 물건들을 챙겨 넣고 나면 그는 플루트를 꺼내 불었는데, 어찌나 부는지 나는 거의, 그가 차츰차츰 자기 온 존재를 그 꼭대기 큰 구멍 속으로 불어 넣어, 음 구멍들로 스르르 새어 나가 버리는 게 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들었다.\n\n희미한 불빛 아래 방에서, 한 손에 머리를 괴고 앉아, 멜 선생의 구슬픈 연주에 귀 기울이며, 내일 수업을 외우고 있는 어린 내 모습이 그려진다. 책을 덮어 놓고도, 여전히 멜 선생의 구슬픈 연주에 귀 기울이며, 그 소리 너머로 예전 집에 있던 것들에, 야머스 갯벌에 불던 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이면서, 몹시 슬프고 외로워하던 내 모습이 그려진다. 아무도 쓰지 않는 방들 사이로 잠자리에 올라가, 침대 곁에 걸터앉아 패거티의 다정한 말 한마디가 그리워 울던 내 모습이 그려진다. 아침이면 아래층으로 내려와, 길쭉하고 으스스하게 째진 틈 같은 계단 창문 너머로, 풍향계가 얹힌 헛간 꼭대기에 매달린 학교 종을 바라보던 내 모습이 그려진다. 그러면서 그 종이 J. 스티어포스와 나머지 아이들을 공부하라고 울려 댈 때를 두려워하던 내 모습이. 그 두려움은 나의 불길한 걱정 속에서, 나무 의족을 한 사내가 녹슨 문을 열어 무시무시한 크리클 선생을 들여보낼 때를 두려워하는 것에 버금가는 것이었다. 이 어느 면으로 보나 내가 그리 위험한 인물이었다고는 생각할 수 없지만, 그 모든 면에서 나는 같은 경고문을 등에 짊어지고 다녔다.\n\n멜 선생은 나에게 말을 많이 거는 법이 없었지만, 결코 모질게 대하지도 않았다. 우리는 말없이도 서로에게 동무가 되어 주었던 셈이다. 깜빡 말하지 못했는데, 그는 가끔 혼잣말을 하며 씩 웃고, 주먹을 불끈 쥐고, 이를 갈고, 영문 모를 모습으로 머리카락을 쥐어뜯곤 했다. 하지만 그에게는 이런 별난 버릇들이 있었고, 처음에는 그것들이 나를 겁먹게 했으나, 나는 이내 거기에 익숙해졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. I ENLARGE MY CIRCLE OF ACQUAINTANCE\n\n이런 생활을 한 달쯤 했을 무렵, 나무다리를 단 사내가 대걸레와 물통을 들고 쿵쿵 돌아다니기 시작했고, 나는 거기서 크리클 선생님과 학생들을 맞이할 채비가 시작되었구나 하고 짐작했다. 내 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 머잖아 대걸레가 교실로 들이닥쳐 멜 선생님과 나를 쫓아냈고, 우리는 며칠 동안 머물 수 있는 곳에 머물고 지낼 수 있는 대로 지냈는데, 그동안 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않던 젊은 여자 두세 명이 늘 우리 앞을 가로막고 있었고, 그들은 어찌나 줄곧 먼지 한가운데 있던지 나는 세일럼 하우스가 무슨 커다란 코담뱃갑이라도 되는 양 줄기차게 재채기를 해 댔다.\n\n어느 날 멜 선생님에게서 크리클 선생님이 그날 저녁에 돌아오신다는 말을 들었다. 저녁에 차를 마시고 난 뒤, 나는 그분이 도착했다는 소식을 들었다. 잠자리에 들기 전에, 나는 나무다리를 단 사내에게 이끌려 그분 앞에 불려 갔다.\n\n크리클 선생님이 쓰는 집 쪽은 우리 쪽보다 훨씬 안락했고, 그분에게는 아담하고 아늑한 정원이 딸려 있었는데, 그것은 먼지투성이 운동장 다음이라 더욱 보기 좋았다. 그 운동장은 어찌나 작은 사막 같던지, 나는 낙타나 단봉낙타가 아니고서는 그 안에서 편안할 사람이 없겠다고 생각했다. 떨면서 크리클 선생님이 계신 곳으로 가는 길에, 복도가 안락해 보인다는 것을 알아챈 것조차 내게는 어쩐지 대담한 짓처럼 느껴졌다. 그래서 안으로 안내받아 들어갔을 때 나는 어찌나 주눅이 들었던지, (둘 다 거실에 있던) 크리클 부인이나 크리클 양은 거의 보이지도 않았고, 시곗줄과 인장이 한 다발 달린, 안락의자에 앉아 곁에 텀블러와 술병을 둔 뚱뚱한 신사 크리클 선생님 말고는 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다.\n\n\"그래!\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"이놈이 그 이빨을 줄로 갈아 줘야 한다는 어린 신사로구먼! 돌려 봐.\"\n\n나무다리를 단 사내가 나를 빙 돌려 등에 붙은 팻말을 내보였고, 그것을 찬찬히 살펴볼 시간을 충분히 준 다음, 다시 나를 돌려 크리클 선생님 쪽으로 얼굴을 향하게 하고는, 자기는 크리클 선생님 곁에 가서 섰다. 크리클 선생님의 얼굴은 불그죽죽했고, 두 눈은 작고 움푹 들어가 있었다. 이마에는 굵은 핏줄이 서 있고, 코는 자그마하고, 턱은 큼지막했다. 정수리는 벗어졌고, 막 희끗희끗해지기 시작한 가늘고 축축해 보이는 머리카락 몇 가닥을 양쪽 관자놀이 너머로 빗어 넘겨, 양쪽이 이마에서 서로 엇갈려 있었다. 그러나 그분에게서 나에게 가장 강한 인상을 남긴 점은, 목소리가 없어서 속삭이듯 말한다는 것이었다. 그렇게 말하느라 들이는 안간힘 때문인지, 아니면 그토록 힘없는 식으로 말한다는 의식 때문인지, 그분이 입을 열 때면 성난 얼굴은 한층 더 성나 보였고 굵은 핏줄은 한층 더 굵어졌으니, 지금 돌이켜 봐도 이 별난 점이 그분의 가장 큰 특징으로 내게 박힌 것이 놀랍지 않다. \"자,\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"이놈에 대한 보고는 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"아직은 이놈에게 불리한 게 없습니다.\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 대답했다. \"그럴 기회가 없었으니까요.\"\n\n내 생각에 크리클 선생님은 실망한 것 같았다. 그리고 (그제야 처음으로 흘끗 본, 둘 다 마르고 조용한) 크리클 부인과 크리클 양은 실망하지 않은 것 같았다.\n\n\"이리 오너라, 이놈아!\" 크리클 선생님이 나에게 손짓하며 말했다.\n\n\"이리 와!\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 그 손짓을 따라 하며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 다행히 네 양아버지를 잘 안다.\" 크리클 선생님이 내 귀를 잡으며 속삭였다. \"참 훌륭한 분이고, 강단 있는 분이지. 그분은 나를 알고, 나는 그분을 알아. 너는 나를 아느냐? 응?\" 크리클 선생님이 사납게 장난치듯 내 귀를 비틀며 말했다.\n\n\"아직 모릅니다, 선생님.\" 나는 아파서 움찔하며 말했다.\n\n\"아직 몰라? 응?\" 크리클 선생님이 되받았다. \"하지만 곧 알게 될 거다. 응?\"\n\n\"곧 알게 될 거다. 응?\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 되풀이했다. 나중에 알게 된 일이지만, 그는 보통 자기의 우렁찬 목소리로 학생들에게 크리클 선생님의 말을 통역하는 역할을 했다.\n\n나는 몹시 겁이 나서, 부디 그러길 바란다고 말했다. 그러는 내내 귀가 활활 타오르는 것만 같았다. 그분이 어찌나 세게 꼬집었던지.\n\n\"내가 어떤 사람인지 일러 주마.\" 크리클 선생님이 마침내 귀를 놓아주며 속삭였는데, 헤어지면서 한 번 비트는 통에 내 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌았다. \"나는 타타르 사람이야.\"\n\n\"타타르 사람이지.\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 어떤 일을 하겠다고 하면, 나는 그걸 한다.\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"그리고 어떤 일을 시키겠다고 하면, 나는 기어이 그걸 시키지.\"\n\n\"—어떤 일을 시키겠다고 하면, 기어이 그걸 시키지.\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"나는 마음먹은 대로 하는 사람이야.\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"그게 나라는 사람이다. 나는 내 할 일을 한다. 그게 내가 하는 일이야. 내 살붙이라도\"—이 말을 하면서 그분은 크리클 부인을 바라보았다—\"나에게 거역하면, 그건 내 살붙이가 아니야. 나는 내쳐 버려. 그 녀석이\"—나무다리를 단 사내에게—\"또 여기 왔던 거냐?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아니라고.\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"그놈은 분수를 알지. 나를 잘 알아. 얼씬도 못 하게 둬. 얼씬도 못 하게 두라고 했다.\" 크리클 선생님이 손으로 탁자를 내리치며, 크리클 부인을 바라보면서 말했다. \"그놈은 나를 잘 아니까. 자, 너도 이제 나를 좀 알기 시작했겠다, 어린 친구야, 그만 가도 좋아. 데리고 나가.\"\n\n나는 가도 좋다는 명령이 무척 반가웠다. 크리클 부인과 크리클 양이 둘 다 눈물을 훔치고 있어서, 나는 나 자신만큼이나 그분들이 안쓰러워 마음이 불편했기 때문이다. 하지만 나에게는 나와 너무도 깊이 얽힌 청이 하나 마음에 걸려 있어서, 내 스스로도 그 용기에 놀라면서도, 끝내 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"저, 선생님—\"\n\n크리클 선생님이 속삭였다. \"허! 이게 뭐냐?\" 그러고는 두 눈으로 나를 태워 버리기라도 할 듯 노려보았다.\n\n\"저, 선생님,\" 나는 더듬거렸다. \"혹시 허락해 주신다면 (제가 한 짓에 대해 정말로 죄송합니다, 선생님), 다른 애들이 돌아오기 전에 이 글씨를 떼 버릴 수 있게—\"\n\n크리클 선생님이 진심이었는지, 아니면 그저 나를 겁주려고 그런 것인지는 모르겠으나, 그분은 의자에서 벌떡 몸을 일으켰고, 나는 그 앞에서 나무다리를 단 사내의 호위를 기다릴 새도 없이 부랴부랴 도망쳐, 내 침실에 다다를 때까지 단 한 번도 멈추지 않았다. 그곳에서 뒤따라오는 사람이 없는 것을 확인하고는, 마침 잠잘 시각이라 잠자리에 들어, 두어 시간을 벌벌 떨며 누워 있었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 샤프 선생님이 돌아왔다. 샤프 선생님은 수석 선생님으로, 멜 선생님보다 윗자리였다. 멜 선생님은 학생들과 함께 끼니를 먹었지만, 샤프 선생님은 크리클 선생님의 식탁에서 점심과 저녁을 들었다. 내가 보기에 그분은 흐느적거리고 가냘파 보이는 신사로, 코가 꽤 컸고, 머리가 자기에게 좀 무겁기라도 한 양 한쪽으로 기울이고 다니는 버릇이 있었다. 머리카락은 아주 매끈하고 곱슬곱슬했지만, 맨 먼저 돌아온 한 학생에게서 그게 가발이라는 (게다가 그 애 말로는 헌 가발이라는) 것과, 샤프 선생님이 토요일 오후마다 그것을 말러 나간다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n이 소식을 내게 일러 준 사람은 다름 아닌 토미 트래들스였다. 그가 맨 먼저 돌아온 학생이었다. 그는 자기 이름을 정문 오른쪽 모퉁이, 맨 위 빗장 위에서 찾을 수 있을 거라고 일러 주는 것으로 자기소개를 대신했다. 그래서 내가 \"트래들스?\" 하고 묻자, 그는 \"바로 그거야\" 하고 대답하더니, 이내 나와 우리 집안에 대해 죄다 이야기해 보라고 했다.\n\n트래들스가 맨 먼저 돌아온 것은 나에게 다행스러운 일이었다. 그는 내 팻말을 어찌나 재미있어했던지, 돌아오는 학생마다, 크든 작든, 도착하자마자 곧장 나를 이런 식으로 소개함으로써, 나는 사실을 밝혀야 할지 숨겨야 할지 하는 곤혹스러움에서 벗어날 수 있었다. \"이거 봐! 여기 재미난 게 있어!\" 게다가 다행스럽게도 학생들 대부분은 풀이 죽은 채 돌아왔고, 내 예상만큼 나를 가지고 떠들썩하게 굴지는 않았다. 분명 몇몇은 야만 인디언처럼 내 주위에서 춤을 추었고, 대부분은 내가 개라도 되는 척하며 내가 물까 봐 토닥토닥 달래는 시늉을 하고는 \"엎드려!\" 하면서 나를 타우저라고 부르는 유혹을 떨치지 못했다. 그 많은 낯선 사람들 틈에서 이것은 당연히 어리둥절한 노릇이었고 눈물도 좀 흘리게 했지만, 그래도 전체적으로는 내가 각오했던 것보다 훨씬 나았다.\n\n* 타우저: 영국에서 개에게 흔히 붙이던 이름.\n그렇지만 J. 스티어포스가 도착하기 전까지는, 나는 이 학교에 정식으로 받아들여졌다고 여겨지지 않았다. 대단한 학자로 소문난 데다 아주 잘생기고 나보다 적어도 예닐곱 살은 위인 이 학생 앞으로, 나는 마치 치안판사 앞에 끌려가듯 인도되었다. 그는 운동장의 헛간 아래에서 내가 받은 벌의 자초지종을 캐물었고, 그것이 \"참 고약한 짓\"이라는 의견을 기꺼이 밝혀 주었다. 그 일로 나는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그에게 마음으로 매이게 되었다.\n\n\"너 돈 얼마나 있어, 코퍼필드?\" 이런 식으로 내 일을 마무리 짓고 나서, 그가 나를 한쪽으로 데려가 걸으며 말했다. 나는 칠 실링이라고 말했다.\n\n\"나한테 맡겨서 간수하게 하는 게 좋을 거야.\" 그가 말했다. \"뭐, 네가 원하면 말이지. 싫으면 안 해도 되고.\"\n\n나는 부랴부랴 그 다정한 제안을 따랐고, 패거티의 지갑을 열어 그의 손바닥에 거꾸로 탈탈 쏟았다.\n\n\"지금 뭐 사고 싶은 거 있어?\" 그가 나에게 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 괜찮아.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"원하면 사도 돼, 알지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"말만 해.\"\n\n\"아니, 괜찮아.\" 나는 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그럼 이따가 침실에 올라가서 까치밥나무주 한 병에다 한 두어 실링쯤 쓰는 건 어때?\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"보니까 너 우리 침실 식구더라.\"\n\n전에는 미처 생각해 본 적도 없었지만, 나는 그래, 그러고 싶다고 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"아몬드 과자에도 한 실링쯤 더 쓰면 좋겠지, 안 그래?\"\n\n나는 그래, 그것도 좋겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"비스킷에 또 한 실링쯤, 과일에 또 한 실링쯤, 어때?\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"야, 어린 코퍼필드, 너 제대로 한턱내는구나!\"\n\n그가 웃기에 나도 웃었지만, 마음 한구석은 조금 켕겼다.\n\n\"뭐!\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"할 수 있는 데까지 늘려 쓰면 되지, 그뿐이야. 내가 너를 위해 힘닿는 데까지 해 볼게. 나는 내키면 언제든 밖에 나갈 수 있으니까, 먹을 걸 몰래 들여올게.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 돈을 자기 주머니에 넣고는, 마음 졸이지 말라고, 다 잘되게 자기가 알아서 챙기겠다고 다정하게 일러 주었다. 그는 정말로 약속을 지켰다. 사실 나는 그게 대부분 잘못된 일이 아닐까 남몰래 께름칙해하면서도—어머니의 반 크라운짜리 두 닢을 헛되이 쓰는 게 아닐까 걱정스러웠으니까—그래도 그게 잘된 일이라고 친다면 말이다. 그래도 나는 그 돈을 쌌던 종잇조각만큼은 고이 간직해 두었으니, 그건 값진 절약이었다. 잠자리에 들려고 이층으로 올라갔을 때, 그는 칠 실링어치를 죄다 꺼내 달빛 아래 내 침대 위에 펼쳐 놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 어린 코퍼필드, 임금님 잔칫상이 다 차려졌다.\"\n\n그가 곁에 있는데, 내 나이로는 도저히 잔치의 주인 노릇을 할 엄두가 나지 않았다. 그 생각만 해도 손이 다 떨렸다. 나는 부디 좌장 노릇을 맡아 달라고 그에게 청했고, 그 방에 있던 다른 학생들이 내 청을 거들자, 그는 그것을 받아들여 내 베개 위에 앉아 음식을 나누어 주었다—참으로 공평하게, 이건 꼭 말해 두어야겠다—까치밥나무주는 자기 것인 굽 없는 작은 잔에 따라 돌렸다. 나로 말하자면 그의 왼편에 앉았고, 나머지 애들은 가장 가까운 침대와 바닥에 무리 지어 우리 둘레에 둘러앉았다.\n\n우리가 거기 앉아 소곤소곤 이야기를 나누던 그 정경이, 아니 그들이 이야기하고 내가 공손히 귀 기울이던 그 정경이 — 이렇게 말하는 편이 옳겠다 — 지금도 얼마나 또렷이 떠오르는지! 달빛이 창문을 통해 방 안으로 조금 흘러들어 바닥에 희끄무레한 창문 하나를 그려 놓았고, 우리는 대부분 어둠 속에 잠겨 있었다. 다만 스티어포스가 상 위에서 무언가를 찾으려고 성냥을 인광 통에 담갔다 그을 때면, 우리 위로 푸르스름한 섬광이 번쩍였다가 이내 사라지곤 했다. 어둠과, 그 잔치의 은밀함과, 모든 말을 속삭이듯 주고받은 데서 비롯된 어떤 야릇한 기분이 다시금 내게 스며들어, 나는 어렴풋한 엄숙함과 외경심에 잠긴 채 그들이 들려주는 이야기에 모두 귀를 기울인다. 그 기분은 그들이 다들 이토록 가까이 있는 것을 반갑게 하면서도, 트래들스가 구석에 유령이 보이는 척할 때면 (나는 웃는 척하지만) 나를 겁먹게 만든다.\n\n나는 학교와 거기 딸린 온갖 것에 관해 별의별 이야기를 다 들었다. 크리클 선생님이 자기를 타타르 사람이라고 내세운 데에 까닭이 없는 게 아니라는 것, 그분이 선생들 가운데 가장 모질고 가장 매서운 사람이라는 것, 살아 있는 날마다 좌우로 후려치며 기병처럼 학생들 한복판으로 돌격해 인정사정없이 매질을 해 댄다는 것을 들었다. 그분 자신은 매질하는 재주 말고는 아무것도 모르고, (J. 스티어포스의 말로는) 학교에서 제일 못한 학생보다도 더 무식하다는 것, 여러 해 전에는 버러에서 홉을 파는 자그마한 장수였는데, 홉 장사로 파산하고 크리클 부인의 돈을 다 들어먹은 뒤 학교 사업에 손을 댔다는 것도 들었다. 그밖에도 그런 이야기가 한참 더 나왔는데, 그들이 그걸 어떻게 알았을까 신기했다.\n* 버러: 런던 템스강 남쪽의 한 지구.\n\n나무다리를 단 사내는 이름이 텅게이인데, 고집불통의 야만인으로, 전에는 홉 장사를 거들었으나 크리클 선생님을 따라 학교 일에 들어왔다는 이야기를 들었다. 학생들 사이의 짐작으로는, 그가 크리클 선생님 밑에서 일하다 다리가 부러진 데다, 그분을 위해 떳떳지 못한 일을 숱하게 해 주었고 그분의 비밀을 쥐고 있기 때문이라고 했다. 텅게이는 오직 크리클 선생님 한 사람만 빼고는, 선생이든 학생이든 학교 전체를 자기의 타고난 원수로 여겼으며, 그의 인생에서 유일한 낙은 심술궂고 못되게 구는 것이라는 이야기도 들었다. 크리클 선생님에게 아들이 하나 있는데, 텅게이와는 사이가 좋지 않았고, 학교 일을 거들다가 한번은 학교의 체벌이 너무도 잔인하게 행해진 일을 두고 아버지에게 항의한 적이 있으며, 게다가 아버지가 어머니를 대하는 방식에 대해서도 들고일어났다고 짐작된다는 이야기도 들었다. 그 일로 크리클 선생님이 아들을 집 밖으로 내쫓았고, 그 뒤로 크리클 부인과 크리클 양이 줄곧 가엾은 처지에 놓여 있다는 이야기도 들었다.\n\n그러나 크리클 선생님에 관해 들은 것 가운데 가장 놀라운 일은, 학교에 그분이 감히 손찌검할 엄두를 내지 못하는 학생이 하나 있다는 것, 그리고 그 학생이 바로 J. 스티어포스라는 것이었다. 그 말이 나왔을 때 스티어포스 자신이 그것을 인정하면서, 어디 한번 그가 손대는 꼴을 보고 싶다고 했다. 한 온순한 학생이 (내가 아니다) 만약 정말로 그분이 손대는 꼴을 보게 되면 어떻게 할 거냐고 묻자, 그는 일부러 성냥을 인광 통에 담갔다 그어 자기 대답 위로 섬광을 비추면서, 늘 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있는 칠 실링 육 펜스짜리 잉크병으로 그분의 이마를 한 방 갈겨 쓰러뜨리는 것으로 시작하겠다고 말했다. 우리는 한동안 숨을 죽이고 어둠 속에 앉아 있었다.\n\n샤프 선생님과 멜 선생님은 둘 다 형편없는 박봉에 시달리는 것으로 여겨진다는 이야기를 들었다. 크리클 선생님의 식탁에 더운 고기와 식은 고기가 함께 나오면, 샤프 선생님은 늘 식은 고기가 더 좋다고 말하기를 기대받는다는 것이었는데, 이것 또한 유일한 거실 기숙생인 J. 스티어포스가 사실이라고 확인해 주었다. 샤프 선생님의 가발이 머리에 맞지 않는다는 것, 그리고 그것을 두고 그렇게 \"거들먹거릴\" 것은—누군가는 \"잘난 척할\" 것은이라고 했다—없다는 것도 들었다. 뒤로는 그분의 빨간 머리가 빤히 다 보였으니까.\n\n한 학생은 석탄 상인의 아들인데, 석탄 외상값을 갚는 셈 치고 들어왔다 하여, 그 까닭으로 \"교환 또는 물물거래\"라고 불린다는 이야기를 들었다—이 거래를 나타낸다 하여 산수 책에서 골라낸 별명이었다. 식탁에 나오는 맥주는 부모들에게서 돈을 뜯어내는 강도질이고, 푸딩은 속임수라는 것도 들었다. 크리클 양은 학교 전체에서 스티어포스에게 반한 것으로 여겨진다는 이야기도 들었는데, 어둠 속에 앉아 그의 고운 목소리며 멋진 얼굴이며 느긋한 몸가짐이며 곱슬곱슬한 머리카락을 떠올려 보니, 나도 정말 그럴 법하다는 생각이 들었다. 멜 선생님은 나쁜 사람은 아니지만 제 몸뚱이 하나 건사할 육 펜스도 없으며, 그분의 어머니인 늙은 멜 부인도 욥처럼 가난한 것이 틀림없다는 이야기도 들었다. 그때 나는 그 아침 식사와, \"내 찰리!\"처럼 들렸던 그 말을 떠올렸지만, 다행히 기억하건대, 나는 그것에 대해 쥐 죽은 듯 입을 다물고 있었다.\n* 욥처럼 가난한: 성경 「욥기」의 주인공 욥이 모든 재산을 잃은 데서 나온, 몹시 가난하다는 뜻의 영어 관용 표현.\n\n이 모든 이야기와, 그밖에도 한참 더 많은 이야기를 듣느라, 잔치가 끝난 뒤로도 한동안 시간이 흘렀다. 손님들 대부분은 먹고 마시는 일이 끝나자마자 잠자리에 들었고, 옷을 반쯤 벗은 채 속닥이며 귀 기울이던 우리도 마침내 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n\"잘 자, 어린 코퍼필드.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"내가 너 챙겨 줄게.\" \"정말 친절하구나.\" 나는 고마워하며 대꾸했다. \"참 고마워.\"\n\n\"너 누이는 없지, 그렇지?\" 스티어포스가 하품하며 말했다.\n\n\"응, 없어.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"그거 안됐네.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"만약 누이가 있었다면, 분명 예쁘고 수줍음 많고 자그마하고 눈이 초롱초롱한 여자애였을 것 같은데. 알고 지내면 좋았을걸. 잘 자, 어린 코퍼필드.\"\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n나는 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 그를 자꾸 떠올렸고, 기억하건대 몸을 일으켜, 달빛 속에 누워 잘생긴 얼굴을 위로 향한 채 팔을 베고 편안히 머리를 기댄 그를 바라보았다. 그는 내 눈에 굉장한 힘을 지닌 사람이었다. 물론 그래서 내 마음이 줄곧 그에게로 향한 것이었다. 달빛 속에서 그에게 어슴푸레 비치는 베일에 싸인 미래 따위는 없었다. 내가 밤새 거닐던 꿈속의 그 정원에, 그의 발자국이 남긴 어두운 그림자 같은 것도 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 7. MY ‘FIRST HALF’ AT SALEM HOUSE\n\n이튿날부터 학교 수업이 본격적으로 시작되었다. 그날 아침을 먹고 나서 크리클 선생님이 들어와 문간에 서서, 이야기책 속 거인이 제 포로들을 둘러보듯 우리를 죽 훑어볼 때, 교실을 가득 메우던 떠들썩한 목소리가 순식간에 죽음처럼 잦아들던 그 광경이 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼던 것이 지금도 기억난다.\n\n텅게이는 크리클 선생님 팔꿈치 옆에 버티고 섰다. 굳이 그렇게 사납게 \"조용히!\" 하고 외칠 까닭도 없어 보였다. 아이들은 죄다 말문이 막히고 몸이 굳어 버렸으니까.\n\n크리클 선생님이 입을 움직이는 게 보였고, 텅게이의 목소리가 이렇게 들려왔다.\n\n\"자, 너희들, 이번엔 새 학기다. 이 새 학기 동안 처신 똑똑히 해. 수업에 산뜻하게 임하라고 충고해 둔다, 나는 매질에 산뜻하게 임할 테니까. 나는 절대 봐주지 않아. 비벼 봐야 소용없다. 내가 남길 자국은 아무리 비벼도 안 지워질 테니까. 자, 일들 시작해, 한 놈도 빠짐없이!\"\n\n이 무시무시한 첫머리 연설이 끝나고 텅게이가 다시 쿵쿵거리며 나가 버리자, 크리클 선생님은 내가 앉은 자리로 다가와, 내가 무는 것으로 유명하다면 자기도 무는 것으로 유명하다고 했다. 그러고는 회초리를 내보이며, 이것을 이빨로 치면 어떻겠느냐고 물었다. 날카로운 이빨이지 않으냐, 응? 어금니지 않으냐, 응? 뿌리가 깊지 않으냐, 응? 잘 물지 않으냐, 응? 물지? 그러면서 한 마디 물을 때마다 나에게 살을 파고드는 매질을 한 대씩 안겨, 나는 몸부림쳤다. 그렇게 해서 나는 (스티어포스 말마따나) 곧장 세일럼 하우스의 정식 일원이 되었고, 또한 곧장 눈물범벅이 되었다.\n\n이것이 나만 받은 특별한 표창 같은 것이었다는 말은 결코 아니다. 오히려 크리클 선생님이 교실을 한 바퀴 도는 동안, 아이들 대다수가 (특히 어린 축들이) 그와 비슷한 관심의 사례를 받았다. 그날 일과가 시작되기도 전에 학교의 절반이 몸부림치며 울고 있었고, 일과가 끝날 무렵에는 그 가운데 얼마나 많은 아이가 몸부림치며 울었는지는, 행여 과장하는 것처럼 보일까 봐 떠올리기조차 솔직히 두렵다.\n\n크리클 선생님만큼 제 직업을 즐긴 사람은 세상에 다시없으리라 싶다. 그는 아이들을 후려치는 데서, 갈망하던 식욕을 채울 때 같은 만족을 느꼈다. 나는 그가 특히 통통한 아이를 보면 도저히 참지 못했다고 확신한다. 그런 아이에게는 묘한 끌림이 있어서, 그날 그 아이를 후려쳐 자국을 남겨 놓기 전까지는 마음이 영 안절부절못했던 것이다. 나 자신이 통통한 편이었으니, 잘 안다. 지금 그 작자를 떠올리면, 그의 손아귀에 한 번도 든 적 없이 그에 관한 모든 것을 알게 되었더라도 느꼈을 법한 사심 없는 분노로 피가 끓어오른다. 하지만 그 피가 뜨겁게 끓는 까닭은, 그가 무능한 짐승이었음을 내가 알기 때문이다. 그는 자기가 거머쥔 그 막중한 직무를 맡을 자격이 해군 대제독이나 총사령관 자리를 맡을 자격만큼도 없는 사람이었으니 — 차라리 그 두 자리 중 하나라도 맡았더라면 끼친 해악이 훨씬 적었을 것이다.\n\n가차 없는 우상 앞에 빌붙던 가엾고 자그마한 아첨꾼들, 우리는 그자에게 얼마나 비굴했던가! 이제 돌이켜 보면, 그만한 재주와 허세를 지닌 인물에게 그토록 비열하고 굽실거리며 굴었다는 것이 인생의 출발로서 참 한심하기 짝이 없다는 생각이 든다.\n\n여기 나는 다시 책상 앞에 앉아 그의 눈치를 살핀다. 굽실거리며 그의 눈치를 살핀다. 그는 또 다른 희생양을 위해 산수 공책에 선을 긋고 있는데, 그 아이는 방금 바로 그 자로 손바닥을 납작하게 얻어맞고는 손수건으로 얼얼한 통증을 닦아 내려 애쓰는 중이다. 나는 할 일이 잔뜩 있다. 한가해서 그의 눈을 살피는 게 아니라, 다음엔 그가 무슨 짓을 할지, 당할 차례가 나인지 다른 누구인지 알고 싶은 두려운 욕망에 병적으로 끌려서다. 내 너머로 죽 늘어선 어린 아이들도 그의 눈에 같은 관심을 품고 함께 살핀다. 그도 그걸 알고 있는 것 같다. 모르는 척하지만 말이다. 그는 산수 공책에 줄을 그으면서 무시무시하게 입을 씰룩거린다. 그러다 이제 곁눈질로 우리 줄을 죽 훑어 내려가면, 우리는 모두 책 위로 고개를 떨군 채 부들부들 떤다. 잠시 뒤 우리는 다시 그를 곁눈질하고 있다. 연습 문제를 제대로 못 풀었다고 유죄 판결을 받은 불행한 죄인 하나가 그의 명령에 다가온다. 죄인은 더듬더듬 변명을 늘어놓고, 내일은 더 잘하겠노라 다짐한다. 크리클 선생님은 때리기 전에 농담을 한마디 던지고, 우리는 그 농담에 웃는다. 가엾고 자그마한 강아지들인 우리는, 잿빛으로 핏기 가신 얼굴에, 심장은 장화 속으로 가라앉은 채로, 그 농담에 웃는다.\n\n여기 나는 다시 책상 앞에 앉아 있다. 나른한 여름 오후다. 마치 아이들이 떼 지은 쉬파리라도 되는 양, 윙윙거리고 웅웅거리는 소리가 내 둘레로 피어오른다. (한두 시간 전에 점심을 먹었으니) 미적지근한 고기 기름이 명치께를 짓누르는 느낌이고, 머리는 납덩이처럼 무겁다. 잠들 수만 있다면 온 세상이라도 내주겠다. 나는 크리클 선생님에게 눈을 박은 채 새끼 올빼미처럼 눈을 끔벅인다. 잠이 일 분쯤 나를 짓눌러도, 그는 여전히 내 선잠 속에 어른거리며 그 산수 공책에 줄을 긋다가, 마침내 슬그머니 내 뒤로 다가와, 등줄기에 벌건 골을 하나 그어 놓으며 나를 깨워 그를 더 또렷이 알아보게 한다.\n\n여기 나는 운동장에 있다. 그가 보이지는 않지만 내 눈은 여전히 그에게 홀려 있다. 조금 떨어진 곳에 그가 식사를 하고 있는 창문이 있고, 그 창문이 그를 대신하므로, 나는 그 대신 그 창문에 눈을 둔다. 그가 그 근처에 얼굴을 내밀면, 내 얼굴은 애원하며 복종하는 표정을 짓는다. 그가 유리 너머로 내다보면, 가장 대담한 아이도 (스티어포스만 빼고) 소리를 지르거나 고함치다 말고 뚝 멈춰 깊은 사색에 잠긴다. 어느 날, 트래들스가 (세상에서 가장 운 없는 아이다) 어쩌다 공으로 그 창문을 깨뜨렸다. 나는 그 일이 벌어지는 것을 보았던, 그리고 그 공이 크리클 선생님의 신성한 머리통 위로 튀어 올랐겠구나 느꼈던 그 어마어마한 충격에, 지금 이 순간에도 몸서리가 쳐진다.\n\n가엾은 트래들스! 꽉 끼는 하늘색 양복을 입어 팔다리가 마치 독일 소시지나 둥글둥글한 푸딩처럼 보이던 그 아이는, 모든 아이 가운데 가장 명랑하면서도 가장 비참했다. 그는 늘 회초리를 맞았다. 그 학기 동안 하루도 빠짐없이 매를 맞았던 것 같은데, 딱 한 번 어느 공휴일이던 월요일에만 양손에 자로 맞고 넘어갔지 싶다. 그리고 그는 늘 그 일을 삼촌에게 편지로 일러바치겠다고 했지만, 한 번도 쓰지 않았다. 잠시 책상에 머리를 처박고 있다가도, 그는 어떻게든 기운을 차려 다시 웃기 시작했고, 눈물이 채 마르기도 전에 석판 가득 해골을 그려 댔다. 나는 처음엔 트래들스가 해골을 그리는 데서 무슨 위안을 얻는지 궁금했다. 한동안은 그를 일종의 은둔자로 여겼다. 그 죽음의 상징들로 매질이란 영원히 가지 않으리란 걸 스스로 일깨우는 것이려니 했던 것이다. 하지만 나는 그가 그저 해골이 그리기 쉽고, 이목구비가 필요 없어서 그렸을 뿐이라고 믿는다.\n\n트래들스는 무척 의리 있는 아이였고, 아이들끼리 서로 감싸 주는 것을 엄숙한 의무로 여겼다. 그는 이 때문에 여러 번 고초를 겪었다. 특히 한번은 스티어포스가 교회에서 웃었는데, 교회지기가 트래들스가 웃은 줄로 알고 그를 끌어냈다. 신도들의 멸시를 받으며 붙들려 나가던 그의 모습이 지금도 눈에 선하다. 그는 이튿날 그 일로 따끔하게 매를 맞고, 라틴어 사전 위에 온 교회 묘지를 가득 채울 만큼 해골이 우글거릴 정도로 오랜 시간 갇혀 있다 나왔으면서도, 끝내 진짜 범인이 누구인지 발설하지 않았다. 하지만 그는 보답을 받았다. 스티어포스가 트래들스에게는 고자질쟁이 기질이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없다고 했고, 우리는 모두 그것이 더없이 높은 칭찬이라 여겼다. 나로 말하자면, 그런 보답을 얻을 수만 있었다면 (트래들스보다 훨씬 덜 용감하고 나이도 한참 어렸지만) 어지간한 고생쯤은 기꺼이 견뎌 냈을 것이다.\n\n스티어포스가 우리보다 앞장서서 크리클 양과 팔짱을 끼고 교회로 걸어가는 모습은 내 인생의 큰 구경거리 가운데 하나였다. 나는 크리클 양이 아름다움으로 치면 꼬마 에밀리에 미치지 못한다고 생각했고, 그녀를 사랑하지도 않았다 (감히 그러지도 못했다). 하지만 그녀가 비범한 매력을 지닌 아가씨이고, 기품으로 말하자면 따를 자가 없다고 여겼다. 흰 바지를 입은 스티어포스가 그녀의 양산을 들어 줄 때면, 나는 그를 안다는 것이 자랑스러웠고, 그녀가 온 마음을 다해 그를 흠모하지 않을 수 없으리라 믿었다. 샤프 선생님과 멜 선생님도 내 눈에는 둘 다 대단한 인물이었지만, 스티어포스는 그 둘에게 마치 두 별 곁의 해 같은 존재였다.\n\n스티어포스는 계속해서 나를 보호해 주었고, 아주 쓸모 있는 친구임을 입증했다. 그가 얼굴을 알아 주며 아껴 주는 아이를 감히 괴롭히려는 자가 아무도 없었기 때문이다. 그는 나에게 몹시 가혹했던 크리클 선생님으로부터는 나를 지켜 주지 못했다 — 아니, 적어도 지켜 주지 않았다. 하지만 내가 여느 때보다 심하게 당하고 나면, 그는 늘 나에게 자기 배짱을 조금 본받을 필요가 있다고, 자기 같으면 그런 꼴을 가만두지 않았으리라 말했다. 나는 그것이 나를 격려하려는 뜻임을 느꼈고, 무척 다정한 말이라 여겼다. 크리클 선생님의 가혹함에는 내가 아는 한 단 하나의 이점이 있었다. 그가 내가 앉은 의자 뒤로 오르락내리락하다가 지나가는 길에 나를 한 대 후려치려 할 때면 내 등에 붙은 푯말이 거치적거렸던 것이다. 그래서 그 푯말은 곧 떼어졌고, 나는 두 번 다시 그것을 보지 못했다.\n\n우연한 사건 하나가 스티어포스와 나 사이의 친분을 단단히 굳혀 주었는데, 이는 나에게 큰 자부심과 만족을 안겨 주었으나, 때로는 곤란을 불러오기도 했다. 그 일은, 어느 날 그가 운동장에서 나에게 말을 걸어 주는 영광을 베풀던 참에 일어났다. 나는 무엇 혹은 누군가가 — 지금은 무엇이었는지 잊었다 — 『페레그린 피클』에 나오는 무엇 혹은 누군가와 비슷하다는 소견을 슬쩍 내놓았다. 그는 그때는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 밤에 내가 잠자리에 들려 할 때 그 책을 가지고 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n나는 아니라고 대답하고, 어떻게 해서 그 책을, 그리고 앞서 말한 다른 책들을 다 읽게 되었는지 설명했다.\n\n\"그래서 그걸 다 기억하고 있어?\" 스티어포스가 물었다.\n\n\"응, 그럼.\" 나는 대답했다. 나는 기억력이 좋았고, 그 책들을 아주 잘 기억하고 있다고 믿었다.\n\n\"그럼 이렇게 하자, 꼬마 코퍼필드.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"네가 나한테 그걸 얘기해 주는 거야. 나는 밤에 일찍 잠들지를 못하고, 아침엔 대개 좀 일찍 깨거든. 한 편씩 차례로 짚어 나가자. 우리만의 그럴듯한 『천일야화』를 만드는 거야.\"\n\n나는 이 약속에 무척 우쭐해졌고, 바로 그날 저녁부터 실행에 옮기기 시작했다. 내가 그 책들을 풀어내는 과정에서 내가 좋아하는 작가들에게 얼마나 큰 만행을 저질렀는지는 말할 처지가 못 되고, 알고 싶지도 않다. 하지만 나는 그 책들을 깊이 믿었고, 적어도 내 생각에는, 내가 들려주는 이야기를 소박하고 진지한 태도로 풀어 갔다. 그리고 이 자질들이 큰 몫을 했다.\n\n골칫거리는, 내가 밤에 자주 졸리거나, 기운이 없어 이야기를 다시 이어 갈 마음이 나지 않는다는 것이었다. 그럴 때면 꽤나 고역이었지만 그래도 해내야만 했다. 스티어포스를 실망시키거나 언짢게 하는 일은 당연히 있을 수 없었으니까. 아침에도, 몸이 노곤해서 한 시간쯤 더 자면 더없이 좋았을 때, 술탄의 왕비 셰에라자드처럼 깨워져 기상종이 울리기도 전에 긴 이야기를 억지로 늘어놓아야 하는 것은 성가신 노릇이었다. 그러나 스티어포스는 단호했고, 그 대가로 나에게 산수며 연습 문제며 내가 풀기 너무 어려운 과제 따위를 설명해 주었으므로, 이 거래에서 나는 손해 본 것이 없었다. 하지만 나에게도 공정을 기해야겠다. 나는 사사로운 이해나 이기심에 끌려 움직인 것이 아니었고, 그가 두려워서 움직인 것도 아니었다. 나는 그를 흠모하고 사랑했으며, 그의 인정만으로도 충분한 보답이 되었다. 그것이 어찌나 소중했던지, 이제 이 사소한 일들을 돌이켜 보면 가슴이 아려 온다.\n\n스티어포스는 또한 자상한 데가 있었고, 한 가지 일에서는 그 자상함을 굽힘 없이 드러냈는데, 짐작건대 가엾은 트래들스를 비롯한 나머지 아이들에게는 좀 약 오르는 노릇이었으리라. 패거티가 보내 주마 약속했던 편지가 — 어찌나 마음 푸근한 편지였던지! — 학기가 시작된 지 몇 주 되지 않아 도착했다. 그 편지와 함께, 오렌지로 완벽하게 둥지를 틀어 놓은 한가운데 케이크 하나와 앵초주 두 병이 들어 있었다. 나는 도리에 따라 이 보물을 스티어포스의 발치에 바치고, 나누어 주십사 부탁했다.\n\n\"자, 이렇게 하자, 꼬마 코퍼필드.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 술은 네가 이야기를 들려줄 때 목을 축이라고 남겨 두는 거야.\"\n\n나는 그 말에 얼굴이 붉어졌고, 겸손하게 그러지 말라고 부탁했다. 그러나 그는 내 목소리가 이따금 쉰다는 걸 — '약간 컬컬하다'는 게 그의 정확한 표현이었다 — 눈여겨봤다며, 한 방울도 빠짐없이 그 용도에 바치겠다고 했다. 그리하여 술은 그의 상자에 잠겨 보관되었고, 그가 직접 작은 약병에 따라 두었다가, 내가 기력을 보충해야 할 성싶을 때면 코르크 마개에 꽂은 깃대 한 토막을 통해 나에게 먹여 주었다. 때로는 더 만병통치약처럼 만들겠다고 친절하게도 거기에 오렌지즙을 짜 넣거나, 생강을 휘저어 넣거나, 박하 사탕을 하나 녹여 넣기도 했다. 이런 실험으로 맛이 나아졌다거나, 밤에 자기 직전과 아침에 일어나 맨 먼저 마실 위장약으로 딱히 골랐을 법한 조합이었다고 장담할 수는 없지만, 나는 고맙게 그것을 마셨고 그의 배려를 절절히 느꼈다.\n\n내 느낌으로는 『페레그린』을 끝내는 데만도 몇 달이 걸렸고, 다른 이야기들에는 또 몇 달이 더 걸린 것 같다. 우리 모임이 이야깃거리가 모자라 시들해지는 일은 결코 없었다고 확신한다. 술도 이야깃거리에 거의 맞먹게 오래갔다. 가엾은 트래들스는 — 그 아이를 떠올릴 때면 나는 늘 웃고 싶은 묘한 충동이 일면서도 눈에는 눈물이 고인다 — 대개 일종의 합창단 노릇을 했다. 익살스러운 대목에서는 웃겨 죽겠다는 듯 몸을 비틀어 댔고, 이야기에 무시무시한 대목이 나오면 겁에 질린 시늉을 했다. 이게 나를 자주 당황하게 했다. 기억나기로, 『질 블라스』의 모험과 관련해 포졸이 등장할 때마다 그는 이가 딱딱 부딪치는 걸 못 참겠다는 시늉을 즐겨 했다. 그리고 질 블라스가 마드리드에서 도적 두목을 만나는 대목에서, 이 운 없는 익살꾼이 어찌나 학질에 걸린 듯 부들부들 떠는 시늉을 했던지, 복도를 어슬렁거리던 크리클 선생님 귀에 들리고 말았고, 침실에서 소란을 피웠다는 죄목으로 멋들어지게 매를 맞았다. 내 안에 있던 낭만적이고 몽상적인 기질이 어떤 것이었든, 어둠 속에서 그토록 많은 이야기를 들려주는 동안 더욱 부추겨졌다. 그런 점에서 이 일은 나에게 그리 이로운 것이 아니었을지도 모른다. 하지만 내 방에서 일종의 노리갯감처럼 귀여움을 받고, 이 재주가 아이들 사이에 소문이 나서, 비록 내가 거기서 가장 어렸는데도 적잖은 주목을 받고 있다는 의식이 나를 분발하게 만들었다. 순전히 잔혹함으로만 굴러가는 학교에서는, 우두머리가 멍청이든 아니든, 배우는 것이 많기 어렵다. 우리 아이들은 대체로 세상의 어느 학생들 못지않게 무지한 무리였다고 나는 믿는다. 그들은 너무 시달리고 두들겨 맞아서 배울 수가 없었다. 끊임없는 불행과 고통과 근심으로 점철된 삶 속에서 무슨 일을 제대로 해낼 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 그들도 공부를 제대로 해낼 수 없었던 것이다. 하지만 나의 자그마한 허영심과 스티어포스의 도움이 어떻게든 나를 떠밀었고, 벌받는 일에서 나를 별로, 아니 거의 구해 주지는 못했어도, 적어도 내가 거기 머무는 동안만큼은 나를 전체 무리 가운데 예외로 만들어 주어서, 나는 꾸준히 지식의 부스러기를 조금씩 주워 모을 수 있었다.\n\n이 일에서 나는 멜 선생님의 도움을 많이 받았는데, 그분은 나를 아껴 주었고 나는 그것을 고맙게 기억한다. 스티어포스가 그분을 두고 늘 깔보듯 대하고, 그분의 마음에 상처를 주거나 남들로 하여금 그러게 하는 기회를 좀처럼 놓치지 않는 것을 볼 때면 나는 늘 마음이 아팠다. 나는 케이크나 다른 어떤 손에 잡히는 물건도 숨기지 못하는 것처럼 그런 비밀도 스티어포스에게는 숨길 수 없어서, 멜 선생님이 나를 데려가 보여 준 두 노부인 이야기를 진작 그에게 털어놓았던 터라, 이 일이 오랫동안 더더욱 나를 괴롭혔다. 나는 스티어포스가 그 일을 발설해서 그분을 놀려 댈까 봐 늘 두려웠다.\n\n그 첫날 아침 내가 아침을 먹고, 피리 소리에 맞춰 공작 깃털 그늘 아래서 잠이 들 때만 해도, 우리 가운데 그 누구도 짐작하지 못했으리라. 보잘것없는 내가 그 양로원에 발을 들인 일이 어떤 결과를 불러올지 말이다. 하지만 그 방문에는 예기치 못한 결과가 따랐고, 그것도 나름대로 자못 심각한 종류의 것이었다.\n\n어느 날 크리클 선생님이 몸이 안 좋아 집에 누워 있었는데, 이는 당연하게도 학교 전체에 생기 넘치는 기쁨을 퍼뜨렸고, 그날 오전 일과 내내 소란이 자못 컸다. 아이들은 크나큰 해방감과 만족감에 들떠서 다루기가 힘들었다. 무서운 텅게이가 나무다리를 끌고 두세 번 들어와 주모자 아이들의 이름을 적어 갔지만 별 효과가 없었다. 어차피 무슨 짓을 하든 내일이면 십중팔구 곤란에 처할 게 뻔했으니, 오늘은 실컷 즐기는 게 현명하다고 다들 여겼던 게 분명하다.\n\n그날은 토요일이라 원래는 반공일이었다. 하지만 운동장에서 떠드는 소리가 크리클 선생님을 방해할 테고, 날씨도 산책 나가기에 좋지 않아서, 우리는 오후에 교실로 들어가라는 명령을 받았고, 이날을 위해 마련된, 평소보다 가벼운 과제를 받았다. 그날은 샤프 선생님이 가발을 곱슬곱슬 말러 나가는 요일이었다. 그래서 무슨 궂은일이든 늘 도맡던 멜 선생님이 혼자 교실을 맡았다. 멜 선생님처럼 온순한 사람에게 황소나 곰의 형상을 갖다 붙일 수 있다면, 소란이 절정에 달했던 그날 오후의 그분을, 천 마리 개에게 물어뜯기는 그런 짐승 가운데 하나로 떠올렸을 것이다. 나는 그분이 욱신거리는 머리를 앙상한 손으로 받친 채 책상 위 책 쪽으로 숙이고, 하원 의장조차 어지럼증을 느꼈을 법한 소란 속에서, 그 지긋지긋한 일을 비참하게 이어 가려 애쓰던 모습이 떠오른다. 아이들은 제자리에서 들락날락하며 다른 아이들과 술래잡기를 했다. 웃는 아이, 노래하는 아이, 떠드는 아이, 춤추는 아이, 울부짖는 아이들이 있었다. 아이들은 발을 질질 끌었고, 그분 주위를 빙빙 돌며 히죽거리고, 얼굴을 찡그리고, 등 뒤에서 또 면전에서 그분을 흉내 냈다. 그분의 가난을, 그분의 장화를, 그분의 외투를, 그분의 어머니를, 그분에게 딸린 것이라면 마땅히 헤아려 줬어야 할 모든 것을 흉내 냈다.\n\n\"조용히!\" 멜 선생님이 별안간 벌떡 일어서며 책으로 책상을 내리쳐 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 짓이냐! 도저히 못 참겠다. 미칠 노릇이구나. 너희가 어떻게 나한테 이럴 수 있느냐?\"\n\n그분이 책상을 내리친 것은 내 책이었다. 그분 곁에 서서, 방 안을 휙 둘러보는 그분의 눈길을 따라가던 나는, 아이들이 모두 멈추는 것을 보았다. 더러는 갑작스레 놀라고, 더러는 반쯤 겁먹고, 더러는 어쩌면 미안해하는 듯했다.\n\n스티어포스의 자리는 교실 맨 끝, 그 긴 방의 반대쪽 끝이었다. 그는 벽에 등을 기대고 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 빈둥거리고 있었는데, 멜 선생님이 그를 쳐다보았을 때, 마치 휘파람이라도 부는 양 입을 꾹 다물고 멜 선생님을 마주 보았다.\n\n\"조용히 해, 스티어포스 군!\" 멜 선생님이 말했다.\n\n\"선생님이나 조용히 하시죠.\" 스티어포스가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"누구한테 하는 말입니까?\"\n\n\"앉아.\" 멜 선생님이 말했다.\n\n\"선생님이나 앉으시죠.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그리고 선생님 일이나 신경 쓰세요.\"\n\n낄낄거리는 소리와 약간의 박수가 일었다. 하지만 멜 선생님의 얼굴이 어찌나 하얗게 질렸던지 곧장 정적이 뒤따랐고, 그분 등 뒤로 잽싸게 튀어나가 그분 어머니를 또 흉내 내려던 한 아이는 마음을 바꿔 펜을 고쳐 달라는 척했다.\n\n\"스티어포스 군,\" 멜 선생님이 말했다. \"자네가 여기 누구의 마음이든 휘어잡을 수 있는 그 힘을 내가 모른다고 생각한다면\" — 그분은 (내 짐작에는) 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채 한 손을 내 머리에 얹었다 — \"또는 자네가 불과 몇 분 사이에 후배들을 부추겨 온갖 방식으로 나에게 행패를 부리게 하는 것을 내가 보지 못했다고 생각한다면, 그건 큰 착각이야.\"\n\n\"저는 선생님에 대해 생각하는 수고 따위는 아예 하지 않습니다.\" 스티어포스가 차갑게 말했다. \"그러니 마침 저는 착각한 게 없네요.\"\n\n\"그리고 자네가 여기서 총애받는 처지를 이용해서, 한 신사를 모욕하기에 이른다면—\" 멜 선생님이 입술을 몹시 떨며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"신사라니요? 어디 있는데요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n이때 누군가가 외쳤다. \"부끄러운 줄 알아, 제이 스티어포스! 너무하잖아!\" 트래들스였다. 멜 선생님은 곧장 그에게 입 다물라고 일러 그의 기를 꺾어 놓았다. \"인생에 운이 따라 주지 않고, 자네에게 한 번도 털끝만 한 해를 끼친 적이 없으며, 모욕해선 안 될 까닭이 숱하게 있는 사람을 — 그 까닭을 자네는 이해할 만큼 나이도 충분하고 지각도 충분하건만 — 모욕한다면,\" 멜 선생님이 입술을 점점 더 심하게 떨며 말했다. \"자네는 비열하고 천박한 짓을 저지르는 거야. 앉든 서든 자네 마음대로 하게, 군. 코퍼필드, 계속해라.\"\n\n\"꼬마 코퍼필드,\" 스티어포스가 교실 위쪽으로 걸어 나오며 말했다. \"잠깐 멈춰라. 멜 선생님, 단번에 분명히 말해 두죠. 선생님이 멋대로 나를 비열하다느니 천박하다느니, 그런 따위로 부른다면, 선생님은 뻔뻔스러운 거지예요. 선생님은 언제나 거지이긴 하죠. 하지만 그런 식으로 나오면, 선생님은 뻔뻔스러운 거지인 겁니다.\"\n\n그가 멜 선생님을 치려던 것인지, 멜 선생님이 그를 치려던 것인지, 아니면 어느 쪽에도 그런 의도가 있긴 했던 것인지 나는 잘 모르겠다. 나는 학교 전체가 마치 돌로 변한 듯 뻣뻣하게 굳는 것을 보았고, 어느새 우리 한가운데 크리클 선생님이 텅게이를 옆에 거느리고 서 있는 것을, 그리고 크리클 부인과 크리클 양이 겁에 질린 듯 문간에서 들여다보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 멜 선생님은 두 팔꿈치를 책상에 짚고 얼굴을 두 손에 묻은 채, 한동안 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"멜 선생,\" 크리클 선생님이 그분 팔을 잡고 흔들며 말했다. 그의 속삭임은 이제 또렷이 들려서, 텅게이도 굳이 그 말을 되풀이할 필요를 느끼지 못했다. \"제정신을 잃은 건 아니길 바라오?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님, 아닙니다.\" 멜 선생님이 얼굴을 들고, 고개를 흔들며, 몹시 동요한 채 두 손을 비비며 대답했다. \"아닙니다, 선생님. 아니에요. 저는 제정신을 차렸습니다, 저는 — 아니요, 크리클 선생님, 저는 제정신을 잃지 않았습니다, 저는 — 저는 제정신을 차렸어요, 선생님. 저는 — 저는 — 선생님이 저를 조금만 더 일찍 떠올려 주셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 크리클 선생님. 그게 — 그게 — 더 친절한 처사였을 겁니다, 선생님, 더 공정한 처사였을 거고요, 선생님. 그랬으면 제가 무언가는 면했을 텐데요, 선생님.\"\n\n크리클 선생님은 멜 선생님을 매섭게 노려보며 텅게이의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 두 발을 바로 옆 의자에 올린 채 책상 위에 걸터앉았다. 그 옥좌에서 여전히 멜 선생님을 매섭게 노려보며, 멜 선생님이 고개를 흔들고 두 손을 비비며 여전히 똑같이 동요한 상태로 있는 동안, 크리클 선생님은 스티어포스를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 군, 저 사람이 황송하게도 나한테 말해 주려 들지 않으니, 이게 다 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n스티어포스는 잠시 그 질문을 피했다. 멸시와 분노에 찬 눈으로 자기 상대를 바라보며 입을 다물었다. 나는 그 짧은 순간에도, 그가 겉모습으로 얼마나 고귀한 친구로 보이는지, 그에 맞선 멜 선생님이 얼마나 초라하고 볼품없어 보이는지 생각하지 않을 수 없었던 것이 기억난다.\n\n\"그럼 저 사람이 총애 어쩌고 한 건 무슨 뜻이었답니까?\" 마침내 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"총애?\" 크리클 선생님이 이마의 핏줄을 순식간에 부풀리며 되받았다. \"누가 총애 얘기를 했단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"저 사람이요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"한데, 도대체, 자네 그건 무슨 뜻으로 한 말이오, 선생?\" 크리클 선생님이 자기 조교를 향해 성을 내며 따져 물었다.\n\n\"제 말은요, 크리클 선생님,\" 그분이 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. \"방금 말한 그대로입니다. 어떤 학생도 자기가 총애받는 처지를 이용해 저를 깎아내릴 권리는 없다는 거지요.\"\n\n\"자네를 깎아내려?\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"이런 별일을 다 보겠나! 한데 한 가지 물어봅시다, 그 이름이 뭐라더라 선생.\" 여기서 크리클 선생님은 회초리까지 끼고 두 팔을 가슴에 척 포개더니, 눈썹을 어찌나 잔뜩 찡그렸던지 그 아래로 작은 두 눈이 거의 보이지 않을 지경이었다. \"자네가 총애 어쩌고 떠들 때, 나한테는 마땅한 존경을 보였소? 나한테 말이오, 선생,\" 크리클 선생님이 별안간 머리를 그분 쪽으로 쑥 내밀었다가 다시 거두며 말했다. \"이 학교의 교장이자 자네 고용주인 나한테.\"\n\n\"분별 있는 처신은 아니었습니다, 선생님, 인정합니다.\" 멜 선생님이 말했다. \"제가 냉정했더라면 그러지 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n이때 스티어포스가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그러더니 저 사람이 나더러 비열하다고 했고, 또 천박하다고 했고, 그래서 내가 저 사람을 거지라고 불렀습니다. 내가 냉정했더라면 어쩌면 거지라고 부르지 않았을지도 모르죠. 하지만 나는 그렇게 불렀고, 그 대가는 기꺼이 치를 각오가 되어 있습니다.\"\n\n치러야 할 대가라는 게 있기나 한지는 미처 생각해 보지도 않은 채, 나는 이 늠름한 일갈에 온몸이 후끈 달아올랐다. 그것은 아이들에게도 인상을 남겼다. 아무도 한마디 입을 떼지는 않았지만, 그들 사이에 낮은 술렁임이 일었으니까.\n\n\"놀랍구나, 스티어포스 — 네 솔직함은 명예로운 것이지만,\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"분명, 명예로운 것이지만 — 놀랍다, 스티어포스, 정말이지, 세일럼 하우스에 고용되어 봉급을 받는 사람에게 네가 그런 호칭을 갖다 붙이다니 말이다, 군.\"\n\n스티어포스는 짧게 웃었다.\n\n\"그건 답이 아니다, 군,\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"내 말에 대한 답이 아니야. 나는 너한테 그 이상을 기대한다, 스티어포스.\"\n\n내 눈에 멜 선생님이 그 잘생긴 아이 앞에서 초라해 보였다면, 크리클 선생님은 얼마나 초라해 보였는지 도저히 말로 다 할 수 없다. \"저 사람더러 부인해 보라고 하시죠.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"저 사람이 거지라는 걸 부인하라고, 스티어포스?\" 크리클 선생님이 외쳤다. \"아니, 저 사람이 어디 가서 동냥을 한단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"저 사람 본인이 거지가 아니라면, 가까운 친척이 거지예요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그게 그거죠.\"\n\n그가 나를 흘끗 쳐다보았고, 멜 선생님의 손이 내 어깨를 가만히 토닥였다. 나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르고 가슴엔 가책이 차오른 채 고개를 들었는데, 멜 선생님의 눈은 스티어포스에게 고정되어 있었다. 그분은 내 어깨를 다정하게 계속 토닥였지만, 시선은 스티어포스를 향했다.\n\n\"크리클 선생님이 제가 스스로를 변호하길 바라시고,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"제 말뜻을 밝히길 바라시니 — 제가 할 말은, 저 사람 어머니가 양로원에서 자선에 기대어 산다는 겁니다.\"\n\n멜 선생님은 여전히 그를 바라보며, 여전히 내 어깨를 다정하게 토닥이면서, 내가 제대로 들었다면, 속삭이듯 혼잣말을 했다. \"그래, 그럴 줄 알았어.\"\n\n크리클 선생님이 매서운 눈살과 억지스러운 정중함으로 자기 조교를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"자, 이 도련님이 하는 말을 들었을 거요, 멜 선생. 부디 모쪼록, 모인 학교 학생들 앞에서 이 도련님 말을 바로잡아 주시오.\"\n\n\"바로잡을 것도 없이 도련님 말이 맞습니다, 선생님.\" 멜 선생님이 쥐 죽은 듯한 정적 속에서 대답했다. \"저 학생이 한 말은 사실입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 부디, 공개적으로 밝혀 주겠소,\" 크리클 선생님이 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 두 눈을 굴려 학교를 휘둘러보며 말했다. \"이 일이 이 순간까지 단 한 번이라도 내 귀에 들어온 적이 있었는지를 말이오?\"\n\n\"직접적으로는 없었지 싶습니다.\" 그분이 대답했다.\n\n\"이거 봐, 모를 리가 없잖소,\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"안 그렇소, 선생?\"\n\n\"선생님은 제 형편이 그리 넉넉하다고는 한 번도 여기지 않으셨으리라 봅니다.\" 조교가 대답했다. \"여기서 제 처지가 어떤지, 또 줄곧 어떠했는지 잘 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그 얘기를 꺼내자면 말이오,\" 크리클 선생님이 핏줄을 그 어느 때보다 더 굵게 부풀리며 말했다. \"내가 보기에 자네는 애초부터 처지를 잘못 잡은 거고, 여길 자선 학교로 착각한 거요. 멜 선생, 그럼 갈라섭시다, 괜찮다면. 빠를수록 좋소.\"\n\n\"지금만 한 때가,\" 멜 선생님이 자리에서 일어서며 대답했다. \"어디 또 있겠습니까.\"\n\n\"잘 가시오, 선생!\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다.\n\n\"작별을 고합니다, 크리클 선생님, 그리고 여러분 모두에게도.\" 멜 선생님이 방 안을 둘러보고는, 다시 한번 내 어깨를 가만히 토닥이며 말했다. \"제임스 스티어포스, 내가 자네에게 남길 수 있는 가장 좋은 바람은, 자네가 오늘 저지른 일을 언젠가 부끄러워하게 되는 것이네. 지금으로선, 나에게든, 내가 마음 쓰는 그 누구에게든, 자네가 친구가 되는 것 말고 다른 무엇이라도 되는 편이 차라리 낫겠네.\"\n\n그분은 다시 한번 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 그러고는 책상에서 피리와 책 몇 권을 챙기고, 후임자를 위해 책상에 열쇠를 꽂아 둔 채, 제 짐을 옆구리에 끼고 교실을 나갔다. 그러자 크리클 선생님은 텅게이를 통해 한바탕 연설을 했는데, 세일럼 하우스의 독립성과 명예를 (어쩌면 너무 열을 올리긴 했어도) 지켜 세운 스티어포스에게 고맙다고 했고, 스티어포스와 악수를 나누며 연설을 끝맺었다. 그동안 우리는 만세 삼창을 했다. 나는 무엇을 위한 만세인지 잘 몰랐지만, 스티어포스를 위한 것이려니 짐작하고 비참한 심정이면서도 열렬히 함께 외쳤다. 그러고 나서 크리클 선생님은 멜 선생님이 떠난 일을 두고 만세 대신 눈물을 흘리다 들킨 토미 트래들스를 회초리로 때린 뒤, 자기 소파로든 침대로든, 그가 나온 그 어디로든 돌아갔다.\n\n이제 우리끼리만 남게 되자, 우리는 멍한 얼굴로 서로를 바라보았던 것이 기억난다. 나로 말하자면, 그날 벌어진 일에서 내가 한 몫에 대해 자책과 뉘우침이 어찌나 컸던지, 스티어포스가 — 내가 보기에 그는 나를 자주 쳐다보았다 — 내가 나를 짓누르던 그 감정을 드러내면 그것을 우정에 어긋난다고, 아니, 우리의 나이 차이와 내가 그를 우러르던 마음을 헤아리자면 도리에 어긋난다고 여길까 봐 두려운 것 말고는, 그 무엇도 내 눈물을 막아 줄 수 없었다. 그는 트래들스에게 몹시 화가 나서, 그가 매를 맞아 고소하다고 했다.\n\n가엾은 트래들스는, 책상에 머리를 처박는 단계는 이미 넘어서서 여느 때처럼 해골을 한바탕 그려 대며 마음을 달래는 중이었는데, 자기는 상관없다고 했다. 멜 선생님이 부당한 대접을 받았다고 했다.\n\n\"누가 그분을 부당하게 대했다는 거야, 이 계집애 같은 녀석아?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 네가 그랬잖아.\" 트래들스가 되받았다.\n\n\"내가 뭘 어쨌는데?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 뭘 어쨌냐고?\" 트래들스가 쏘아붙였다. \"그분 마음에 상처를 주고, 그분 일자리까지 잃게 했잖아.\"\n\n\"그분 마음?\" 스티어포스가 경멸하듯 되뇌었다. \"그분 마음쯤이야 곧 추슬러질 게 뻔하다, 장담하지. 그분 마음은 너 같은 거랑은 다르거든, 트래들스 아가씨. 그분 일자리로 말하자면 — 그게 뭐 대단한 자리이기라도 했냐? — 내가 집에 편지를 써서 그분이 돈푼이라도 받게끔 손쓰지 않을 것 같아? 폴리 양?\"\n\n우리는 스티어포스의 이 마음 씀씀이를 무척 고귀하게 여겼다. 그의 어머니는 과부에 부자였고, 그가 부탁하는 거라면 거의 무엇이든 들어준다는 말이 있었으니까. 우리는 트래들스가 그렇게 코가 납작해진 것이 너무도 통쾌했고, 스티어포스를 하늘 높이 떠받들었다. 특히 그가 황송하게도 우리에게, 자기가 한 일은 다 우리를 위해, 우리의 대의를 위해 한 것이며, 사심 없이 그 일을 함으로써 우리에게 큰 은혜를 베푼 것이라고 말해 주자 더욱 그랬다. 하지만 솔직히 말하지 않을 수 없다. 그날 밤 어둠 속에서 이야기를 이어 가던 중에 멜 선생님의 그 낡은 피리가 내 귓가에 한두 번 구슬프게 울리는 것 같았고, 마침내 스티어포스가 지쳐서 내가 잠자리에 누웠을 때, 어디선가 그 피리가 어찌나 서글프게 연주되는 것만 같던지, 나는 도저히 비참한 심정을 가눌 수 없었다.\n\n나는 곧 스티어포스를 바라보며 그를 잊어버렸다. 새 선생님을 구할 때까지, 스티어포스는 책도 한 권 없이 (나에게는 그가 모든 걸 외우고 있는 것처럼 보였다) 느긋한 아마추어 솜씨로 몇몇 반을 맡아 가르쳤다. 새 선생님은 어느 문법 학교에서 왔는데, 부임하기 전에 스티어포스에게 소개되려고 어느 날 응접실에서 식사를 했다. 스티어포스는 그를 무척 마음에 들어 했고, 우리에게 그가 '벽돌' 같은 사람이라고 했다. 이게 무슨 학식 높은 구별을 뜻하는지 정확히는 몰랐지만, 나는 그 말 때문에 그를 크게 존경했고, 그가 더 뛰어난 지식을 갖췄음을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 비록 그는 멜 선생님이 나에게 기울였던 만큼의 수고를 — 내가 뭐 대단한 사람이라서가 아니지만 — 나에게 한 번도 기울이지 않았지만 말이다.\n\n이 학기 동안, 날마다 되풀이되던 학교생활 말고, 지금까지도 가시지 않은 인상을 나에게 남긴 사건이 딱 하나 더 있었다. 그것이 가시지 않는 데에는 여러 까닭이 있다.\n\n어느 날 오후, 우리가 모두 시달릴 대로 시달려 끔찍한 혼란에 빠져 있고, 크리클 선생님이 무시무시하게 사방을 후려쳐 대고 있을 때, 텅게이가 들어와 여느 때처럼 우렁찬 소리로 외쳤다. \"코퍼필드한테 손님이오!\"\n\n손님이 누구이며 어느 방으로 안내해야 할지를 두고 그와 크리클 선생님 사이에 몇 마디가 오갔다. 그러고 나서, 관례대로 호명이 떨어지자 벌떡 일어서서 놀라움에 정신이 아득해져 있던 나는, 식당으로 가기 전에 뒤쪽 계단으로 가서 깨끗한 옷깃을 달고 오라는 말을 들었다. 나는 그 명령에 따랐는데, 어린 마음이 그토록 떨리고 다급하게 두근거린 적은 그전에 없었다. 그리고 응접실 문에 다다라, 어쩌면 어머니일지도 모른다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐을 때 — 그때까지 나는 머드스톤 씨나 머드스톤 양만 떠올리고 있었던 것이다 — 나는 문고리에서 손을 거두고 잠시 멈춰 서서 한 번 흐느낀 뒤에야 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n처음엔 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그런데 문에 무언가 눌리는 느낌이 들어 문 뒤를 돌아보니, 놀랍게도 거기에 패거티 씨와 햄이 모자를 들어 나에게 꾸벅거리며, 서로를 벽에 밀어붙이고 있었다. 나는 웃지 않을 수 없었다. 하지만 그건 그들의 꼬락서니가 우스워서라기보다는 그들을 보는 기쁨이 훨씬 컸기 때문이었다. 우리는 무척 정겹게 악수를 나누었고, 나는 웃고 또 웃다가 결국 손수건을 꺼내 눈을 닦았다.\n\n패거티 씨는 (그 방문 내내 입을 한 번도 다물지 않았던 게 기억난다) 내가 그러는 것을 보고 몹시 걱정스러워하며, 햄을 슬쩍 찔러 뭐라도 말하라고 했다.\n\n\"기운 내요, 데이비 도련님!\" 햄이 헤벌쭉 웃으며 말했다. \"아니, 어쩜 이렇게 컸대요!\"\n\n\"내가 컸어?\" 나는 눈을 닦으며 말했다. 딱히 무슨 일로 운 것도 아니었는데, 어쩐지 옛 친구들을 보니 눈물이 났다.\n\n\"컸지요, 데이비 도련님? 안 컸나, 이거!\" 햄이 말했다.\n\n\"안 컸나, 이거!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n그들이 서로를 보고 웃어 대는 통에 나는 다시 웃음이 터졌고, 그러다 우리 셋은 내가 또 울 뻔할 만큼 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"엄마는 잘 계세요, 패거티 씨?\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 우리 정답고 정다운, 그리운 패거티는요?\"\n\n\"기차게 잘 있지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 꼬마 에밀리랑 거미지 부인은요?\"\n\n\"기—차게 잘 있어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 패거티 씨는 그 침묵을 메우려고 주머니에서 어마어마하게 큰 바닷가재 두 마리와 엄청난 게 한 마리, 그리고 새우가 가득 든 커다란 천 자루를 꺼내, 햄의 두 팔에 차곡차곡 쌓아 올렸다.\n\n\"실은 말이죠,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"도련님이 우리랑 같이 있을 적에 끼니에 곁들이는 군것질을 좋아했던 게 생각나서, 외람되지만 좀 가져왔지요. 그 늙은 아낙이 삶았어요, 그랬다고요. 거미지 부인이 삶았지요. 그렇소,\" 패거티 씨가 천천히 말했는데, 내 생각에 그는 달리 꺼낼 화제가 마땅찮아서 이 화제에 매달리는 듯했다. \"거미지 부인이, 내 장담하는데, 그분이 삶았다고요.\"\n\n내가 고맙다는 인사를 하자, 패거티 씨는 조개붙이를 안고 멋쩍게 웃으며 서 있는, 그러나 그를 거들 생각은 도통 안 하는 햄을 한번 쳐다보더니 말했다.\n\n\"보다시피, 바람도 물때도 우리한테 맞춰 줘서, 우리 야머스 거룻배 한 척을 타고 그레이브젠드까지 왔답니다. 누이가 나한테 이 동네 이름을 편지에 적어 보냈는데, 혹시라도 내가 그레이브젠드에 들를 일이 생기거든 건너가서 데이비 도련님을 찾아 안부를 전하고, 부디 잘 지내시길 바라며 식구들도 죄다 기차게 잘 있다고 알려 드리라 했지요. 꼬마 에밀리는 말이죠, 내가 돌아가면 누이한테 편지를 쓸 거예요. 내가 도련님을 만났는데 도련님도 마찬가지로 기차게 잘 있더라고요. 그래서 우린 이걸 아주 빙글빙글 도는 회전목마처럼 만드는 셈이지요.\"\n\n나는 소식이 한 바퀴 빙 도는 것을 나타낸 패거티 씨의 그 표현을 알아듣기까지 잠시 생각을 좀 해야 했다. 그러고 나서 나는 진심으로 고맙다고 했고, 얼굴이 붉어지는 걸 의식하면서, 우리가 해변에서 조가비와 조약돌을 줍던 그 시절과는 꼬마 에밀리도 달라졌겠지요, 하고 말했다.\n\n\"걘 여자가 다 되어 가고 있지요, 그게 걔가 되어 가는 거예요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"쟤한테 물어봐요.\" 그가 가리킨 건 햄이었는데, 햄은 새우 자루 위로 기쁨과 동의에 겨워 함박웃음을 지었다.\n\n\"그 예쁜 얼굴이라니!\" 패거티 씨가 제 얼굴을 등불처럼 환히 빛내며 말했다.\n\n\"걔 공부 솜씨라니!\" 햄이 말했다.\n\n\"걔 글씨라니!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"아, 그게 흑옥처럼 새까맣다니까요! 게다가 얼마나 큼지막한지, 어디서든 다 보일 정도라오.\"\n\n패거티 씨가 그 어린 귀염둥이를 떠올리며 어찌나 신명이 솟던지, 보고 있자니 더없이 흐뭇했다. 그가 지금도 내 앞에 서 있는 듯하다. 텁수룩하니 거친 얼굴이 기쁜 사랑과 자랑으로 환히 빛나는데, 나는 그것을 뭐라 형용할 길이 없다. 그의 정직한 두 눈이 마치 깊은 속에서 무언가 환한 것이 일렁이기라도 하듯 불꽃처럼 타오르며 반짝인다. 그의 떡 벌어진 가슴이 기쁨으로 들썩인다. 그의 억세고 헐거운 두 손이 진심에 겨워 불끈 쥐어진다. 그리고 그는 자기가 하는 말을, 내 난쟁이 같은 눈에는 큰 쇠망치처럼 보이는 오른팔을 휘저으며 힘주어 강조한다.\n\n햄도 그 못지않게 진지했다. 짐작건대 그들은 에밀리에 관해 훨씬 더 많은 이야기를 했을 텐데, 스티어포스가 뜻밖에 들어오는 바람에 머쓱해지고 말았다. 스티어포스는 내가 한구석에서 낯선 두 사람과 이야기하는 것을 보고는, 부르던 노래를 뚝 그치고 말했다. \"여기 있는 줄은 몰랐네, 꼬마 코퍼필드!\" (여기는 여느 때 손님을 맞는 방이 아니었으니까) 그러고는 나가는 길에 우리 곁을 가로질러 갔다.\n\n스티어포스 같은 친구가 있다는 자부심에서였는지, 아니면 어쩌다 패거티 씨 같은 친구를 두게 되었는지를 그에게 설명하고 싶은 마음에서였는지는 잘 모르겠다. 어쨌든 나는 그가 떠나려는 참에 그를 불러 세웠다. 그리고 나는 겸손하게 말했다 — 아아, 이 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에 그 모든 것이 어찌 이리 생생히 되살아나는지! —\n\n\"가지 마, 스티어포스, 괜찮다면 말이야. 이분들은 야머스에서 온 뱃사람 두 분인데 — 무척 친절하고 좋은 분들이야 — 우리 유모의 친척이고, 나를 보려고 그레이브젠드에서 오셨어.\"\n\n\"아, 그래?\" 스티어포스가 되돌아오며 말했다. \"두 분을 뵙게 되어 반갑습니다. 두 분 다 안녕하세요?\"\n\n그의 태도에는 어떤 편안함이 있었다 — 명랑하고 가뿐한 태도였지만 거들먹거리는 데가 전혀 없었다 — 나는 그것이 일종의 마법을 품고 있었다고 지금도 믿는다. 나는 지금도 믿는다. 이 몸가짐과 넘치는 생기, 듣기 좋은 목소리, 잘생긴 얼굴과 체격, 그리고 내가 알지 못하는 어떤 타고난 매력의 힘까지 (몇몇 사람은 그런 것을 지녔다고 나는 생각한다) 더해져, 그가 어떤 주문을 지니고 다녔으며, 거기에 굴복하는 것은 자연스러운 약점이었고 좀처럼 거역할 수 있는 사람이 많지 않았으리라고 말이다. 나는 그들이 그를 얼마나 마음에 들어 하는지, 또 어떻게 한순간에 그에게 마음을 활짝 여는지를 보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"부디 그 편지를 보내실 때, 패거티 씨,\" 내가 말했다. \"집에 알려 주세요. 스티어포스 씨가 나한테 무척 잘해 준다고, 그가 없으면 여기서 어떻게 지냈을지 모르겠다고요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리.\" 스티어포스가 웃으며 말했다. \"그런 건 절대 알리면 안 돼.\"\n\n\"그리고 스티어포스 씨가 혹시라도 노퍽이나 서퍽에 오게 되면, 패거티 씨,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 거기 있을 때라면, 장담하는데, 본인만 허락한다면 야머스로 데려가서 댁을 보여 드릴게요. 너 이런 멋진 집은 본 적 없을걸, 스티어포스. 배로 만든 집이거든!\"\n\n\"배로 만들었다고?\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"뼛속까지 뱃사람인 분에게 딱 어울리는 집이로군.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 도련님, 그렇고말고요, 도련님.\" 햄이 싱글거리며 말했다. \"맞아요, 젊은 양반! 데이비 도련님, 이 양반 말이 옳아요. 뼛속까지 뱃사람이라니! 허, 허! 바로 그게 이 양반이지요, 정말!\"\n\n패거티 씨도 조카 못지않게 흐뭇해했지만, 겸손한 성미라 그렇게 떠들썩하게 자기 칭찬을 받아들이지는 못했다.\n\n\"음, 그게, 도련님,\" 그가 꾸벅꾸벅 절을 하고 껄껄 웃으며, 목도리 끝자락을 가슴께로 여며 넣으며 말했다. \"고맙소이다, 도련님, 고맙소! 나야 내 분야에서 그저 애쓸 따름이지요, 도련님.\"\n\n\"세상 가장 훌륭한 사람도 그 이상은 못 하는 법이죠, 패거티 씨.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. 그는 벌써 그의 이름을 알고 있었다.\n\n\"내 장담컨대, 그건 도련님 본인이 하는 일이지요, 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 고개를 흔들며 말했다. \"게다가 아주 잘 해내고요 — 아주 잘! 고맙소이다, 도련님. 이렇게 나를 반갑게 맞아 줘서 고마워요, 도련님. 나야 거칠지만, 그래도 준비된 사람이오 — 적어도, 준비된 사람이길 바라지요, 무슨 말인지 알겠죠. 우리 집이야 뭐 볼 게 별로 없소이다만, 도련님이 행여 데이비 도련님이랑 같이 보러 오게 되거든 진심으로 맞이하리다. 나야 천상 굼벵이라서,\" 패거티 씨가 말했는데, 이건 달팽이를 뜻하는 거였고, 그가 갈 듯 갈 듯 떠나지 못하는 걸 빗댄 말이었다. 실은 그는 말끝마다 가려고 했다가 어찌어찌 도로 돌아오곤 했으니까. \"어쨌거나 두 분 다 잘 지내시길, 행복하시길 바라오!\"\n\n햄도 이 말에 맞장구를 쳤고, 우리는 더없이 정겹게 그들과 작별했다. 그날 저녁 나는 예쁜 꼬마 에밀리 이야기를 스티어포스에게 하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았지만, 그녀의 이름을 입에 올리기가 너무 수줍었고, 그가 나를 놀려 댈까 봐 너무 겁이 났다. 패거티 씨가 그녀가 여자가 다 되어 간다고 한 말이, 어쩐지 마음 한구석을 영 불편하게 휘저어 놓아 나는 한참을 곱씹었던 것이 기억난다. 하지만 나는 그게 다 부질없는 소리라고 결론지었다.\n\n우리는 조개붙이를, 아니 패거티 씨가 겸손하게 일컬은 대로 그 '곁들이 거리'를 아무도 모르게 우리 방으로 날라다, 그날 저녁 거나하게 잔치를 벌였다. 하지만 트래들스는 그 일에서 무사히 빠져나오질 못했다. 그는 남들처럼 평범하게 잔치 한 끼를 치르고 넘어갈 정도의 운조차 없었다. 그는 한밤중에 게 때문에 탈이 나서 — 아주 녹초가 되도록 앓았다 — 그리고 (아버지가 의사인) 뎀플이 말로는 말 한 마리의 기력도 거덜낼 만한 양이라던 검은 물약과 파란 알약을 잔뜩 처방받아 먹은 뒤에도, 자백하기를 거부했다는 이유로 회초리찜질과 그리스어 신약 여섯 장을 받았다.\n\n학기의 나머지는 내 기억 속에 뒤죽박죽 엉켜 있다. 날마다 이어지던 우리 삶의 다툼과 몸부림이며, 기울어 가던 여름과 바뀌어 가던 계절이며, 종소리에 침대에서 끌려 나오던 서리 내린 아침들과, 종소리에 다시 침대로 떠밀려 들어가던 캄캄한 밤의 그 차디차고 차디찬 냄새며, 침침하게 불 밝히고 미적지근하게 데워지던 저녁 교실과, 그저 거대한 오들오들 떨림 기계나 다름없던 아침 교실이며, 삶은 소고기와 구운 소고기, 삶은 양고기와 구운 양고기가 번갈아 나오던 것이며, 버터 발린 빵 덩이며, 귀퉁이가 개 귀처럼 접힌 교과서며, 금 간 석판이며, 눈물 자국 얼룩진 습자 공책이며, 회초리질이며, 자질이며, 머리 깎이던 일이며, 비 오던 일요일들이며, 쇠기름 푸딩이며, 그리고 이 모든 것을 휘감던 잉크 냄새 자욱한 지저분한 공기가 한데 엉켜 있다.\n\n그래도 또렷이 기억난다. 멀찍이 있던 방학이라는 관념이, 어마어마하게 오랫동안 꼼짝 않는 점 하나로만 보이다가, 마침내 우리 쪽으로 다가오기 시작해, 점점 더 커지고 또 커지던 것을. 어떻게 우리가 달을 세다가 주를 세고, 다시 날을 세게 되었는지를. 그러다 어떻게 나는 행여 데리러 오는 사람이 없을까 봐 겁이 나기 시작했고, 스티어포스에게서 나를 데리러 사람이 왔으며 분명히 집에 가게 된다는 말을 들었을 때는, 그 전에 다리라도 부러지면 어쩌나 하는 어슴푸레한 불길한 예감에 사로잡혔는지를. 어떻게 종업식 날이 마침내 빠르게 자리를 옮겨, 다다음 주에서 다음 주로, 이번 주로, 모레로, 내일로, 오늘로, 오늘 밤으로 바짝 다가왔는지를 — 그리하여 나는 야머스행 우편 마차 안에 올라, 집으로 가고 있었다.\n\n나는 야머스행 우편 마차 안에서 잠을 여러 번 설쳤고, 이 모든 일에 관한 두서없는 꿈을 숱하게 꾸었다. 하지만 이따금 깨어 보면, 창밖의 땅은 세일럼 하우스의 운동장이 아니었고, 귓가에 울리는 소리도 크리클 선생님이 트래들스를 후려치는 소리가 아니라, 마부가 말들을 채찍질해 다그치는 소리였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. MY HOLIDAYS. ESPECIALLY ONE HAPPY AFTERNOON\n\n우편 마차가 멈추는 여관에 동이 트기 전에 도착했는데, 그 여관은 내 친구인 종업원이 일하던 곳은 아니었다. 나는 문에 '돌고래'라고 칠해진 아담하고 좋은 침실로 안내받았다. 아래층에서 큰 난롯불 앞에 앉아 뜨거운 차를 마셨는데도 몸이 어찌나 차가웠던지 지금도 기억이 난다. 그래서 나는 돌고래의 침대로 기어들어 돌고래의 담요를 머리까지 끌어당기고 잠드는 게 더없이 반가웠다.\n\n짐마차꾼 바키스 씨가 아침 아홉 시에 나를 데리러 오기로 되어 있었다. 나는 여덟 시에 일어났는데, 밤잠이 짧았던 탓에 머리가 좀 어질어질했고, 약속 시간이 되기도 전에 채비를 마쳤다. 바키스 씨는 우리가 마지막으로 함께한 뒤로 오 분도 지나지 않은 듯이, 그러니까 내가 그저 육 펜스짜리를 잔돈으로 바꾸려고 여관에 잠깐 들렀다 나온 것쯤으로 여기는 듯이 나를 맞았다.\n\n나와 내 상자가 마차에 실리고 짐마차꾼도 자리에 앉자마자, 게으른 말은 늘 그렇듯이 익숙한 걸음으로 우리 모두를 끌고 천천히 걸어 나갔다.\n\n\"안색이 아주 좋아 보이세요, 바키스 씨.\" 나는 그가 그 말을 알면 좋아하겠거니 싶어 말을 건넸다.\n\n바키스 씨는 소맷부리로 뺨을 문지르고는, 거기에 혈색이 좀 묻어났기를 바라기라도 한 것처럼 소맷부리를 들여다보았다. 그러나 그 칭찬에 대해 그 밖에 다른 반응은 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"바키스 씨 말씀 전했어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"패거티에게 편지를 썼거든요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n바키스 씨는 무뚝뚝해 보였고, 대답도 메마르게 했다.\n\n\"제대로 안 된 거였나요, 바키스 씨?\" 나는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 물었다.\n\n\"뭐, 그게 그렇다네.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"전갈이 잘못됐어요?\"\n\n\"전갈은 그런대로 괜찮았을 거야.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 거기서 그냥 끝나 버렸지 뭐냐.\"\n\n나는 그 말뜻을 알아듣지 못해서, 궁금한 마음에 되물었다. \"끝나 버렸다고요, 바키스 씨?\"\n\n\"아무 일도 안 일어났단 말이야.\" 그가 나를 곁눈질하며 설명했다. \"답이 없었어.\"\n\n\"답을 기다리셨던 거예요, 바키스 씨?\" 내가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 말했다. 이건 나에게 새로운 사실이었으니까.\n\n\"사내가 그럴 마음이 있다고 말할 때는,\" 바키스 씨가 다시 천천히 나에게 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"그건 그 사내가 답을 기다린다는 뜻이나 다름없는 거야.\"\n\n\"그래서요, 바키스 씨?\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 바키스 씨가 눈길을 다시 말의 두 귀로 돌리며 말했다. \"그 사내는 그때부터 줄곧 답을 기다리고 있었던 거지.\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 그렇게 말해 주셨어요, 바키스 씨?\"\n\n\"아니 — 아니야.\" 바키스 씨가 그 일을 곰곰이 되짚으며 으르렁거리듯 말했다. \"내가 가서 그렇게 말할 까닭이 없잖아. 내가 그 여자한테 직접 여섯 마디도 안 해 봤는데, 그렇게 말할 수야 없지.\"\n\n\"제가 대신 해 드릴까요, 바키스 씨?\" 나는 미덥잖다는 듯이 말했다. \"네가 해 줄 수 있다면,\" 바키스 씨가 다시 한번 나를 천천히 바라보며 말했다. \"바키스가 답을 기다린다고 말해 주렴. 너 — 그 여자 이름이 뭐랬지?\"\n\n\"그 사람 이름요?\"\n\n\"아!\" 바키스 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"패거티예요.\"\n\n\"세례명이냐? 아니면 그냥 부르는 이름이냐?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그건 세례명이 아니에요. 세례명은 클라라예요.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 이 사실에서 무궁무진한 생각거리를 발견한 듯, 한동안 가만히 골똘히 앉아 속으로 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 마침내 그가 다시 입을 열었다. \"이렇게 말하는 거야. '패거티! 바키스가 답을 기다려요.' 그럼 그 여자가, 아마, '무슨 답요?' 하고 묻겠지. 그럼 네가, '내가 전한 그 말에 대한 답요.' 하는 거야. '그게 뭔데요?' 하고 그 여자가 묻거든. '바키스는 그럴 마음이 있어요.' 하고 네가 말하는 거지.\"\n\n이 지극히 교묘한 제안을 하면서 바키스 씨는 팔꿈치로 나를 쿡 찔렀는데, 어찌나 세게 찔렀던지 옆구리가 다 결릴 지경이었다. 그러고는 평소처럼 말 위로 구부정하게 몸을 숙였다. 그러고는 그 일에 대해 더는 말을 꺼내지 않다가, 삼십 분쯤 지난 뒤에 주머니에서 분필 한 조각을 꺼내, 마차 포장 안쪽에다 '클라라 패거티'라고 적어 넣었다 — 아무래도 자기만의 비망록인 듯했다.\n\n아아, 집이 아닌 곳으로 집에 가는 기분이라니, 그 얼마나 묘한 느낌이었던가. 게다가 눈에 들어오는 것마다 행복했던 옛집을 떠올리게 하는데, 그 옛집은 두 번 다시 꿀 수 없는 꿈만 같았다! 어머니와 나와 패거티가 서로에게 전부였고, 우리 사이에 끼어들 사람이 아무도 없던 그 시절이 길 위에서 어찌나 서글프게 떠오르던지, 나는 거기 가는 게 정말 기뻤는지조차 확신할 수 없었다 — 차라리 멀리 떨어진 채로 스티어포스와 어울리며 그 일을 잊고 지내는 편이 낫지 않았을까 싶기도 했다. 하지만 나는 거기 있었다. 그리고 곧 우리 집에 다다랐는데, 헐벗은 늙은 느릅나무들이 황량한 겨울 공기 속에서 수많은 손을 비틀어 짜고 있었고, 낡은 까마귀 둥지의 부스러기들이 바람에 흩날려 떠가고 있었다.\n\n짐마차꾼은 정원 문 앞에 내 상자를 내려놓고 떠났다. 나는 창문들을 흘끔거리며 집을 향해 난 길을 걸었는데, 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 머드스톤 씨나 머드스톤 양이 그 창문 어딘가에서 잔뜩 인상을 쓰고 내다보지나 않을까 겁이 났다. 그러나 아무 얼굴도 나타나지 않았다. 집에 이르렀고, 날이 어두워지기 전이라 노크하지 않고도 문 여는 법을 알았기에, 나는 조용하고 머뭇거리는 걸음으로 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n복도에 발을 들여놓았을 때, 옛 거실에서 들려온 어머니의 목소리가 내 안에서 일깨운 그 기억이 얼마나 어린 시절의 것이었는지는 하느님만 아실 것이다. 어머니는 나직한 목소리로 노래를 부르고 있었다. 내가 갓난아기였을 적에 분명 어머니 품에 안겨 그렇게 나에게 불러 주는 노래를 들었던 게 틀림없다. 그 가락은 나에게 낯설었으나, 또 한편으로는 너무도 오래된 것이어서 내 가슴을 가득 채워 넘치게 했다. 마치 오랜만에 돌아온 벗처럼.\n\n어머니가 외롭고 생각에 잠긴 듯한 투로 흥얼거리는 것으로 보아, 나는 어머니가 혼자라고 믿었다. 그래서 나는 살그머니 방으로 들어갔다. 어머니는 난롯가에 앉아 갓난아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었는데, 그 작은 손을 자기 목에 갖다 대고 있었다. 어머니의 눈은 아기의 얼굴을 내려다보고 있었고, 어머니는 그 아이에게 노래를 불러 주고 있었다. 어머니에게 다른 동무가 없다는 점에서, 내 짐작은 들어맞은 셈이었다.\n\n내가 말을 건네자 어머니는 깜짝 놀라 외마디 소리를 질렀다. 그러나 나를 보고는, 사랑스러운 데이비, 내 아들이라고 부르며! 나를 맞으러 방을 가로질러 절반쯤 다가오더니, 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 나에게 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 거기 파고들어 있던 작은 생명체 가까이 내 머리를 자기 가슴에 기대게 하고, 그 아기의 손을 내 입술에 갖다 댔다.\n\n차라리 그때 죽었더라면 좋았을 것을. 그 느낌을 가슴에 품은 채 그때 죽었더라면! 그랬다면 나는 그 뒤로 살아온 어느 때보다도 천국에 더 어울리는 사람이었을 텐데.\n\n\"이 애가 네 동생이란다.\" 어머니가 나를 어루만지며 말했다. \"데이비, 내 예쁜 아들! 가엾은 내 아가!\" 그러고는 자꾸자꾸 입을 맞추며 내 목을 끌어안았다. 어머니가 그러고 있을 때 패거티가 달려 들어와, 우리 곁 바닥에 털썩 주저앉더니, 십오 분 동안이나 우리 둘을 두고 정신없이 좋아했다.\n\n내가 이렇게 일찍 올 줄은 아무도 몰랐던 모양인데, 짐마차꾼이 평소보다 훨씬 일찍 도착한 탓이었다. 또한 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양은 이웃에 다니러 나가서 밤이 되기 전에는 돌아오지 않을 거라고 했다. 나는 이런 행운은 꿈에도 바라지 않았다. 우리 셋이 다시 한번 아무 방해 없이 함께 있을 수 있으리라고는 생각조차 못 했었다. 그래서 나는 그 순간만큼은 옛 시절이 되돌아온 것만 같았다.\n\n우리는 난롯가에서 함께 저녁을 먹었다. 패거티가 우리 시중을 들려고 곁에 섰지만, 어머니는 그렇게 하도록 두지 않고 함께 먹게 했다. 내 앞에는 내 옛 접시가 놓였는데, 돛을 활짝 편 군함이 갈색으로 그려진 그 접시는, 내가 떠나 있던 내내 패거티가 어딘가에 고이 간직해 둔 것이었다. 백 파운드를 준대도 그걸 깨뜨리지는 않았을 거라고 패거티는 말했다. 내 앞에는 데이비드라고 적힌 내 옛 머그잔과, 잘 잘리지도 않는 내 옛 작은 나이프와 포크가 놓여 있었다.\n\n식탁에 앉아 있는 동안, 나는 패거티에게 바키스 씨 이야기를 들려주기 딱 좋은 때라고 생각했는데, 내 이야기가 채 끝나기도 전에 패거티는 웃음을 터뜨리며 앞치마를 끌어다 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"패거티.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"무슨 일이야?\"\n\n패거티는 더욱 웃기만 할 뿐이었고, 어머니가 앞치마를 잡아당기려 하자 더욱 꼭 끌어다 얼굴을 가린 채, 머리에 자루라도 뒤집어쓴 사람처럼 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 짓이야, 이 어리석은 사람아?\" 어머니가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"아유, 그놈의 남자!\" 패거티가 소리쳤다. \"저더러 결혼하자잖아요.\"\n\n\"너한테는 아주 좋은 짝이지 않니?\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"아이고! 모르겠어요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"묻지 마세요. 그 사람이 금으로 만들어졌대도 저는 싫어요. 아니, 누구라도 싫다고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 그렇게 말해 주지 않는 게냐, 이 우스운 사람아?\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 말해 주라고요.\" 패거티가 앞치마 밖으로 내다보며 받아쳤다. \"그 사람은 저한테 그런 얘기를 한마디도 한 적이 없어요. 그럴 줄 모르는 사람도 아니고요. 만약 뻔뻔스럽게 저한테 한마디라도 했다간, 제가 따귀를 올려붙였을 거예요.\"\n\n그러는 패거티의 얼굴은 내가 본 어떤 얼굴보다도, 아니 그 누구의 얼굴보다도 시뻘게져 있었다. 하지만 패거티는 격렬한 웃음 발작이 도질 때마다 잠깐씩만 다시 얼굴을 가렸고, 그런 발작을 두세 번 더 겪고 나서는 다시 저녁을 먹었다.\n\n나는 어머니가 패거티를 볼 때면 미소를 짓다가도, 점점 더 진지하고 생각에 잠긴 표정이 되는 걸 알아챘다. 어머니가 변했다는 건 처음부터 느꼈었다. 어머니의 얼굴은 여전히 무척 예뻤지만 근심에 찌든 듯 보였고, 너무 가냘파 보였다. 어머니의 손은 어찌나 가늘고 하얀지, 나에게는 거의 투명해 보일 지경이었다. 그러나 지금 내가 말하는 변화는 이런 것 위에 더해진 것이었다. 그것은 어머니의 태도에 있었는데, 불안하고 안절부절못하게 변해 있었다. 마침내 어머니는 손을 뻗어, 오랜 하녀의 손 위에 다정하게 자기 손을 포개며 말했다.\n\n\"패거티, 얘야, 너 설마 결혼하려는 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"제가요, 마님?\" 패거티가 빤히 쳐다보며 되물었다. \"에구머니나, 아니에요!\"\n\n\"아직은 아닌 거지?\" 어머니가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"절대로요!\" 패거티가 외쳤다.\n\n어머니는 패거티의 손을 잡고 말했다.\n\n\"날 떠나지 마, 패거티. 나하고 함께 있어 줘. 아마 그리 오래는 아닐 거야. 너 없이 내가 어떻게 살겠니!\"\n\n\"제가 마님을 떠나다니요, 소중한 마님!\" 패거티가 외쳤다. \"이 세상을 다 준대도, 그 마누라까지 다 준대도 그럴 일 없어요. 아니, 그런 어리석은 생각이 그 작은 머릿속에 어쩌다 들어왔대요?\" — 패거티는 예전부터 가끔 어머니를 어린애 대하듯 말하곤 했으니까.\n\n그러나 어머니는 고맙다는 말 외에는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 패거티는 제 식대로 말을 줄줄 이어 갔다.\n\n\"제가 마님을 떠난다고요? 어디 그런 꼴 좀 봐야겠네요. 패거티가 마님 곁을 떠난다고요? 그런 짓 하는 꼴 한번 잡아 보고 싶네요! 아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.\" 패거티가 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"그런 일은 없어요, 마님. 그렇게 되면 좋아라 할 고양이 같은 것들이 없는 건 아니지만, 그것들 좋은 일은 안 시켜 줄 거예요. 외려 약이 바짝 오르게 해 줄 거예요. 저는 심술궂고 까탈스러운 늙은이가 될 때까지 마님 곁에 있을 거예요. 그러다 너무 귀가 멀고, 너무 다리를 절고, 너무 눈이 안 보이고, 이가 다 빠져 너무 우물거려서 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없어지면, 그러니까 잔소리 들을 거리조차 못 되면, 그땐 우리 데이비한테 가서 좀 거둬 달라고 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 패거티.\" 내가 말했다. \"그땐 내가 정말 반갑게 맞을게. 여왕님처럼 떠받들어 줄게.\"\n\n\"고마운 우리 도련님!\" 패거티가 외쳤다. \"그러실 줄 알았어요!\" 그러고는 내 환대에 고마운 마음을 표하느라 미리 나에게 입을 맞추었다. 그런 다음 다시 앞치마로 머리를 가리고 바키스 씨 이야기에 또 한바탕 웃었다. 그런 다음 작은 요람에서 아기를 꺼내 어르며 돌보았다. 그런 다음 저녁 식탁을 치웠고, 그런 다음 다른 모자를 쓰고, 반짇고리와 자, 밀랍 양초 토막을 들고 들어왔다 — 죄다 예전과 조금도 다름이 없었다.\n\n우리는 난롯가에 둘러앉아 즐겁게 이야기를 나누었다. 나는 크리클 선생이 얼마나 모진 선생인지를 들려주었고, 둘은 나를 몹시 가엾게 여겼다. 나는 스티어포스가 얼마나 훌륭한 친구이며 나를 얼마나 잘 봐주는지를 들려주었고, 패거티는 그를 한번 보려면 이십 마일이라도 걷겠다고 했다. 나는 아기가 깨어 있을 때 두 팔로 안아 다정하게 어르고 돌봤다. 아기가 다시 잠들자, 나는 오랫동안 끊겼던 옛 버릇대로 어머니 곁에 바짝 다가가, 두 팔로 어머니 허리를 감싸고, 작은 붉은 뺨을 어머니 어깨에 댄 채 앉았다. 그러자 또 한 번 어머니의 아름다운 머리카락이 내 위로 드리워지는 게 느껴졌다 — 예전에 내가 늘 천사의 날개 같다고 생각했던 그 머리카락이, 지금도 그렇게 기억난다 — 나는 정말이지 더없이 행복했다.\n\n그렇게 앉아 난롯불을 바라보며 새빨갛게 단 숯덩이 속에서 그림들을 보고 있노라니, 나는 내가 한 번도 떠난 적이 없었던 것만 같았다. 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양도 그저 그런 그림에 지나지 않아서 불이 사그라지면 사라져 버릴 것 같았고, 내가 기억하는 그 모든 것 가운데 어머니와 패거티와 나 말고는 진짜인 게 하나도 없는 것만 같았다.\n\n패거티는 눈이 보이는 한 줄곧 양말을 깁다가, 더는 보이지 않게 되자 그것을 장갑처럼 왼손에 끼우고, 오른손에는 바늘을 쥔 채, 불꽃이 일렁일 때마다 한 땀씩 더 뜨려고 채비하고 앉아 있었다. 패거티가 늘 깁고 있던 그 양말이 도대체 누구의 것이었는지, 또 그렇게 깁기를 기다리는 양말이 어디서 그토록 끊이지 않고 공급되었는지 나는 도무지 알 수가 없다. 내가 갓난아기였을 적부터 패거티는 늘 그런 종류의 바느질에만 매달려 있었고, 어쩌다가라도 다른 바느질을 하는 법이 없었던 것 같다.\n\n\"궁금하네.\" 패거티가 말했는데, 그녀는 가끔 가장 뜻밖의 화제를 두고 궁금증 발작에 사로잡히곤 했다. \"데이비 도련님의 대고모님은 어떻게 되셨을까?\" \"원, 패거티!\" 어머니가 깊은 생각에서 깨어나며 말했다. \"무슨 그런 쓸데없는 소릴 하니!\"\n\n\"하지만 마님, 정말 궁금한걸요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌다 그런 사람이 네 머릿속에 떠올랐대?\" 어머니가 물었다. \"이 세상에 거기 떠올릴 사람이 그렇게 없니?\"\n\n\"어떻게 된 노릇인지 저도 모르겠어요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"제가 멍청해서 그런가, 제 머리는 사람을 골라 떠올릴 줄을 몰라요. 멋대로 왔다가 가고, 멋대로 안 왔다가 안 가고 그러는걸요. 그분은 어떻게 되셨을까요?\"\n\n\"넌 어쩜 그렇게 엉뚱하니, 패거티!\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"꼭 그분이 한 번 더 찾아오기라도 바라는 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"아이고, 그런 일은 없어야지요!\" 패거티가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 그런 거북한 얘긴 하지 마, 착한 사람아.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"베시 양은 분명 바닷가 오두막에 틀어박혀 계실 테고, 거기 그대로 계실 거야. 어쨌든 다시는 우리를 성가시게 할 일은 없을 거다.\"\n\n\"그렇겠죠!\" 패거티가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"그래요, 그럴 리야 없겠죠. — 그런데 만약 그분이 돌아가시면, 데이비 도련님한테 뭐라도 남겨 주실까요?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 패거티.\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"넌 어쩜 그렇게 말도 안 되는 소릴 하니! 그분이 이 가엾은 아이가 세상에 태어났다는 사실 자체에 노여워하셨다는 걸 뻔히 알면서.\"\n\n\"이제는 도련님을 용서하실 마음이 없으시겠죠.\" 패거티가 넌지시 비쳤다.\n\n\"이제 와서 왜 이 애를 용서하실 마음이 생기겠니?\" 어머니가 다소 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 동생이 생겼으니 하는 말이에요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n어머니는 곧장 울음을 터뜨리더니, 패거티가 어떻게 감히 그런 말을 할 수 있느냐고 따졌다.\n\n\"요람 속의 이 가엾고 죄 없는 것이 너한테든 누구한테든 무슨 해라도 끼쳤다는 거니, 이 샘 많은 사람아!\" 어머니가 말했다. \"차라리 짐마차꾼 바키스 씨한테 시집이나 가는 게 훨씬 낫겠다. 왜 안 가는 거야?\"\n\n\"제가 그러면 머드스톤 양이 좋아하겠죠.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 어쩜 그렇게 심보가 고약하니, 패거티!\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"넌 머드스톤 양을 우스운 사람이 시샘할 수 있는 한 최대로 시샘하고 있어. 네가 직접 열쇠를 쥐고, 모든 걸 네가 내주고 싶은 거지? 안 그래? 정말 그래도 난 놀라지 않을 거야. 그분이 다 친절한 마음에서, 더없이 좋은 뜻으로 그러시는 걸 뻔히 알면서! 너도 알잖아, 패거티 — 잘 알면서.\"\n\n패거티는 \"좋은 뜻은 무슨 놈의 좋은 뜻!\" 비슷한 말을 중얼거렸고, 그 좋은 뜻이라는 게 좀 지나치게 넘쳐 난다는 식의 말도 덧붙였다.\n\n\"네 속을 다 알아, 이 심술궂은 사람아.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"네 마음을 환히 꿰뚫고 있다고, 패거티. 내가 그런다는 걸 너도 알 테고, 어째서 네가 불처럼 얼굴이 달아오르지 않는지 이상할 지경이다. 하지만 한 번에 한 가지씩만 하자. 지금은 머드스톤 양 얘기야, 패거티. 넌 거기서 빠져나갈 생각 마라. 그분이 입버릇처럼 하시는 말씀 못 들었니? 내가 너무 경솔하고 너무 — 어 — 어 —\"\n\n\"예쁘다고요.\" 패거티가 거들었다.\n\n\"뭐,\" 어머니가 반쯤 웃으며 받았다. \"그분이 그렇게 어리석게 그런 말씀을 하신다 한들, 그게 내 탓이겠니?\"\n\n\"아무도 마님 탓이라고는 안 해요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 정말 그래야지!\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"그분이 그래서 나를 위해 온갖 수고를 덜어 주고 싶어 하신다고 입버릇처럼 말씀하시는 거 못 들었니? 내가 그런 일에 어울리지 않는다고 여기시고, 나도 정말이지 내가 그런 일에 어울리는지 어떤지 모르겠는데 말이야. 그분은 아침저녁으로 부지런히 움직이고, 쉴 새 없이 이리저리 오가시지 않니 — 온갖 일을 다 하시고, 석탄 광이며 식료품 저장실이며 어딘지도 모를 온갖 곳을 다 뒤지고 다니시잖아. 결코 유쾌할 리 없는 일들을 말이야 — 그런데 거기에 일종의 헌신이 없다고 넌 넌지시 비꼬려는 거니?\"\n\n\"전 비꼬는 거 하나도 없어요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 비꼬는 거야, 패거티.\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"넌 네 일 말고는 늘 그것밖에 안 하잖아. 늘 비꼰다고. 거기에 신이 나서. 그리고 머드스톤 씨의 좋은 뜻을 두고 네가 말할 때면 —\"\n\n\"전 그분 얘긴 한마디도 안 했어요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 패거티.\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"하지만 넌 넌지시 비꼬았어. 방금 내가 그렇게 말했잖니. 그게 너의 가장 못된 버릇이야. 넌 기어이 비꼰다니까. 내가 아까도 네 속을 다 안다고 했고, 봐라, 정말 알았잖아. 머드스톤 씨의 좋은 뜻을 두고 네가 말하면서 그걸 깔보는 척할 때 (네 마음속으로는 정말로 그러는 건 아니라고 믿지만, 패거티), 너도 나만큼이나 그 뜻이 얼마나 좋은지, 그 뜻이 그분을 매사에 어떻게 움직이는지 잘 알고 있을 거야. 그분이 어떤 사람에게 다소 엄하게 구신 것처럼 보였다면, 패거티 — 너도 알 테고, 데이비도 분명 알 텐데, 내가 지금 여기 있는 누구를 두고 하는 말이 아니라는 걸 — 그건 오로지 그게 어떤 사람을 위하는 길이라고 확신하시기 때문이야. 그분은 나 때문에 자연히 어떤 사람을 사랑하시고, 오로지 어떤 사람을 위해서만 행동하시는 거야. 그 일을 판단하는 데는 나보다 그분이 훨씬 나아. 나야 약하고 가볍고 계집애 같은 사람이고, 그분은 굳건하고 진중하고 진지한 분이라는 걸 잘 아니까. 그리고 그분은,\" 어머니가, 그 다정한 천성에서 우러난 눈물을 얼굴에 살그머니 흘리며 말했다. \"나를 위해 무던히 애를 써 주셔. 그러니 나는 그분께 무척 감사해야 하고, 마음속으로까지도 그분께 순종해야 해. 그런데 그러지 못할 때면, 패거티, 나는 마음을 졸이며 나 자신을 탓하고, 내 마음을 의심하면서, 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어.\"\n\n패거티는 양말 발끝에 턱을 괸 채, 말없이 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"자, 패거티.\" 어머니가 어조를 바꾸어 말했다. \"우리 서로 다투지는 말자. 난 그걸 견딜 수가 없어. 이 세상에 나한테 진정한 벗이 하나라도 있다면, 그게 너라는 걸 안다. 내가 너를 우스운 사람이라느니, 골치 아픈 사람이라느니, 그런 비슷한 말로 부를 때, 패거티, 그건 다만 네가 내 진정한 벗이라는 뜻일 뿐이야. 코퍼필드 씨가 나를 처음 이 집으로 데려오던 날 밤, 네가 문간으로 마중 나왔던 그때부터 줄곧 그래 왔다는 뜻이야.\"\n\n패거티는 지체 없이 응답하여, 나를 한 번 힘껏 끌어안는 것으로 우정의 조약을 비준했다. 그때도 나는 이 대화의 진짜 속내를 얼마간 어렴풋이 알아챘던 것 같다. 하지만 이제는 분명히 안다. 그 착한 사람이 이 대화를 먼저 꺼내고 거기서 제 몫을 맡았던 것은, 오로지 어머니가 스스로 즐겨 늘어놓은 그 조그맣고 앞뒤 안 맞는 결론으로 마음의 위안을 얻게 하려는 것이었음을. 그 뜻은 효험이 있었다. 그날 저녁 내내 어머니가 한결 마음 편해 보였고, 패거티도 어머니를 덜 살피는 듯했던 게 기억나니까.\n\n차를 다 마시고, 재를 긁어 올리고, 촛불 심지를 다듬고 나서, 나는 옛 시절을 추억하며 패거티에게 악어 책에서 한 장을 읽어 주었다 — 패거티가 주머니에서 그 책을 꺼냈는데, 그동안 줄곧 거기 넣어 두었던 건지는 모르겠다 — 그러고 나서 우리는 세일럼 하우스 이야기를 했고, 그러다 보니 다시 스티어포스 이야기로 돌아오게 되었는데, 그는 내 단골 화제였다. 우리는 무척 행복했다. 그리고 그날 저녁은, 그런 저녁의 마지막이자 내 인생의 그 권(卷)을 영영 닫게끔 정해진 저녁으로서, 내 기억에서 결코 사라지지 않을 것이다.\n\n마차 바퀴 소리가 들린 것은 거의 열 시가 다 되어서였다. 그러자 우리는 다들 일어섰다. 어머니는 시간이 너무 늦었고, 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 젊은 사람은 일찍 자는 게 좋다고 여기시니, 내가 가서 자는 편이 낫겠다고 황급히 말했다. 나는 어머니에게 입을 맞추고는, 그들이 들어오기 전에 곧장 촛불을 들고 이층으로 올라갔다. 예전에 갇혀 있던 그 침실로 올라가던 중에, 어린 마음에 이런 느낌이 들었다. 그들이 집 안으로 차가운 바람 한 줄기를 몰고 들어와, 그 옛날의 정겨운 느낌을 깃털처럼 날려 버리는 것 같다고.\n\n아침에 내려가 아침을 먹는 게 영 마음이 편치 않았는데, 내가 그 잊지 못할 잘못을 저지른 날 이후로 머드스톤 씨를 한 번도 마주친 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그래도 안 할 수는 없는 노릇이라, 나는 계단 중간까지 갔다가 두세 번 멈칫 되돌아오고, 발끝으로 또 그만큼 내 방으로 도로 뛰어가기를 거듭한 끝에, 거실에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n그는 난로를 등진 채 그 앞에 서 있었고, 머드스톤 양은 차를 끓이고 있었다. 내가 들어서자 그는 나를 빤히 쳐다보았으나, 아는 척하는 기색은 조금도 없었다. 나는 잠깐 어쩔 줄 몰라 하다가 그에게 다가가 말했다. \"죄송합니다, 아저씨. 제가 한 일을 무척 뉘우치고 있어요. 부디 용서해 주세요.\"\n\n\"네가 뉘우친다니 다행이구나, 데이비드.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n그가 나에게 내민 손은 내가 물어뜯었던 그 손이었다. 나는 거기 난 붉은 자국에 한순간 눈길이 머무는 걸 어쩌지 못했다. 하지만 그 얼굴에 떠오른 음험한 표정과 마주쳐 내 얼굴이 시뻘게졌을 때, 그 손의 자국은 그만큼 붉지는 않았다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 아주머니?\" 나는 머드스톤 양에게 말했다.\n\n\"아유, 저런!\" 머드스톤 양이 한숨을 쉬며, 손가락 대신 차통 숟가락을 나에게 내밀었다. \"방학이 며칠이라더냐?\"\n\n\"한 달이에요, 아주머니.\"\n\n\"언제부터 치는데?\"\n\n\"오늘부터요, 아주머니.\"\n\n\"오!\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"그럼 하루가 지나간 거구나.\"\n\n그녀는 이런 식으로 방학 달력을 매기며, 매일 아침 똑같은 방식으로 하루씩 지워 나갔다. 열흘째가 될 때까지는 우울하게 지워 나갔지만, 두 자릿수로 들어서면 한결 기대에 부풀었고, 날이 갈수록 심지어 농담까지 던졌다.\n\n평소에는 그런 약한 모습을 보이는 사람이 아니었건만, 바로 이 첫날 나는 불행히도 그녀를 격렬한 경악 상태에 빠뜨리고 말았다. 나는 그녀와 어머니가 앉아 있던 방으로 들어갔는데, 아기(태어난 지 겨우 몇 주밖에 안 된)가 어머니 무릎에 있어서, 나는 그 아기를 아주 조심스럽게 두 팔로 안았다. 그 순간 머드스톤 양이 어찌나 비명을 질러 댔던지, 나는 하마터면 아기를 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다.\n\n\"제인, 얘!\" 어머니가 외쳤다.\n\n\"맙소사, 클라라, 저게 보여?\" 머드스톤 양이 부르짖었다.\n\n\"뭐가 보인다는 거야, 제인?\" 어머니가 말했다. \"어디?\"\n\n\"저 애가 안았어!\" 머드스톤 양이 외쳤다. \"저 애가 아기를 안았다고!\"\n\n그녀는 공포에 질려 맥이 풀렸지만, 몸을 다잡고 나에게 달려들어 내 팔에서 아기를 빼앗아 갔다. 그러고는 정신이 아득해지더니, 어찌나 심하게 아파했던지 사람들은 그녀에게 체리 브랜디를 먹여야 했다. 그녀는 정신을 차리고 나서, 어떤 핑계로든 내 동생을 두 번 다시 만지지 말라고 나에게 엄숙히 명했다. 그리고 내 가엾은 어머니는, 내가 보기엔 그러지 않기를 바랐으면서도, \"네 말이 맞아, 제인.\" 하고 말하며 그 금령을 순순히 못 박았다.\n\n또 한 번은 우리 셋이 함께 있을 때, 바로 이 사랑스러운 아기가 — 어머니 때문에라도 나에게는 정말로 사랑스러웠던 — 머드스톤 양이 발끈하는 죄 없는 빌미가 되었다. 어머니는 아기를 무릎에 뉘어 놓고 그 눈을 들여다보다가 말했다.\n\n\"데이비! 이리 와 보렴!\" 그러고는 내 눈을 들여다보았다.\n\n나는 머드스톤 양이 묵주를 내려놓는 걸 보았다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 어머니가 부드럽게 말했다. \"둘이 똑 닮았어. 내 눈을 닮은 것 같아. 색깔이 내 눈빛 같아. 그런데 정말 놀랍도록 닮았네.\"\n\n\"무슨 소릴 하는 거야, 클라라?\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다.\n\n\"제인,\" 어머니가 이 매서운 물음투에 다소 주눅이 들어 더듬거렸다. \"아기 눈하고 데이비 눈이 똑 닮은 걸 알았다는 거야.\"\n\n\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 성을 내며 일어섰다. \"넌 가끔 영락없는 바보로구나.\"\n\n\"제인, 얘.\" 어머니가 항변했다.\n\n\"영락없는 바보야.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"그 누가 우리 오라버니 아기를 네 아들 따위와 견준단 말이냐? 둘은 조금도 안 닮았어. 정반대로 다르지. 모든 면에서 완전히 딴판이야. 앞으로도 영영 그러기를 바란다. 난 여기 앉아서 그런 비교나 듣고 있진 않겠어.\" 그 말과 함께 그녀는 성큼성큼 걸어 나가, 문을 쾅 닫아 버렸다.\n\n요컨대, 나는 머드스톤 양의 귀염을 받는 아이가 아니었다. 요컨대, 나는 거기서 그 누구의 귀염도, 심지어 나 자신의 귀염조차 받지 못했다. 나를 좋아하는 사람들은 그걸 드러낼 수 없었고, 나를 좋아하지 않는 사람들은 어찌나 노골적으로 드러내던지, 나는 늘 내가 거북하고 촌스럽고 굼떠 보이는 것 같다는 걸 예민하게 의식하고 있었으니까.\n\n나는 내가 그들을 거북하게 하는 만큼 그들도 나를 거북하게 한다고 느꼈다. 내가 그들이 함께 이야기를 나누고 있는 방에 들어서면, 어머니가 즐거워 보이다가도 내가 들어서는 순간부터 그 얼굴에 근심의 그늘이 슬며시 드리워졌다. 머드스톤 씨가 기분이 더없이 좋을 때면 나는 그 기분을 꺾어 놓았다. 머드스톤 양이 기분이 더없이 나쁠 때면 나는 그걸 더 부추겼다. 나는 어머니가 늘 희생양이라는 걸 알아챌 만큼은 눈치가 있었다. 어머니는 나에게 말을 걸거나 다정하게 대했다가, 그 태도가 그들에게 무슨 거슬림을 주어 나중에 잔소리를 들을까 봐 그러기를 두려워했다. 어머니는 끊임없이 자기가 잘못할까 봐 두려워했을 뿐 아니라 내가 잘못할까 봐도 두려워했고, 내가 조금만 움직여도 불안하게 그들의 눈치를 살폈다. 그래서 나는 되도록 그들의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 마음먹었고, 음울한 내 침실에 앉아 작은 외투를 두른 채 책에 코를 박고 있노라면, 교회 종이 울리는 소리를 겨울날 몇 시간이고 듣곤 했다.\n\n저녁이면 나는 가끔 부엌에 가서 패거티와 함께 앉아 있었다. 거기서는 마음이 편했고, 나 자신으로 있는 게 두렵지 않았다. 그러나 이 두 가지 도피처는 거실에서 허락되지 않았다. 거기를 지배하는 그 괴롭히기 좋아하는 심술이 둘 다 가로막았다. 나는 여전히 내 가엾은 어머니의 훈육에 꼭 필요한 존재로, 또 어머니의 시련 가운데 하나로 여겨졌으니, 자리를 비우는 게 용납되지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"데이비드.\" 어느 날 저녁을 먹고 나서 내가 늘 그렇듯 방을 나가려 하자 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"네가 시무룩한 성미를 지녔다는 걸 알게 되어 유감이구나.\"\n\n\"곰처럼 뚱한 게야!\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다.\n\n나는 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"자, 데이비드.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"시무룩하고 고집스러운 성미야말로 모든 기질 가운데 가장 못된 것이다.\"\n\n\"게다가 이 애 성미는, 내가 본 그런 모든 성미 가운데,\" 그의 누이가 거들었다. \"가장 뿌리 깊고 고집불통이지. 클라라, 너조차도 이쯤은 알아챌 거다, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 제인.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"정말 확실해 — 분명 날 이해해 줄 거야, 제인 — 네가 데이비를 제대로 알고 있다고 말이야?\"\n\n\"내가 이 애나 다른 어떤 애를 이해 못 한다면, 클라라, 나 자신이 좀 부끄러울 거다.\" 머드스톤 양이 받아쳤다. \"내가 무슨 깊은 사람인 척하는 건 아니지만, 상식만큼은 갖췄다고 자부한다.\"\n\n\"물론이지, 제인.\" 어머니가 받았다. \"네 이해력이야 무척 빈틈없지 —\"\n\n\"아이고, 천만에! 제발 그런 소린 마, 클라라.\" 머드스톤 양이 성을 내며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"하지만 정말 그런걸.\" 어머니가 다시 말했다. \"다들 그런 줄 알아. 나야말로 여러모로 거기서 큰 덕을 보고 있고 — 적어도 봐야 마땅하지 — 그러니 나만큼 그걸 절실히 아는 사람도 없을 거야. 그래서 정말이지 무척 조심스럽게 말하는 거야, 제인.\"\n\n\"내가 이 애를 이해 못 한다고 치자, 클라라.\" 머드스톤 양이 손목에 찬 작은 족쇄 같은 장신구를 매만지며 받아쳤다. \"네가 원한다면, 내가 이 애를 도무지 이해 못 한다고 해 두자. 이 애는 나한테 너무 속이 깊으니까. 하지만 어쩌면 우리 오라버니의 통찰력이라면 이 애의 성격을 얼마간 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있을지도 모르지. 그리고 우리가 — 그리 점잖지 못하게 — 끼어들었을 때, 마침 오라버니가 그 얘길 하고 있었던 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 클라라.\" 머드스톤 씨가 낮고 진중한 목소리로 말했다. \"그런 문제를 두고는 너보다 더 낫고 더 냉정한 판단자가 있을 듯하구나.\"\n\n\"에드워드.\" 어머니가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"당신은 모든 문제에서 제가 감히 견줄 수도 없을 만큼 훨씬 나은 판단자예요. 당신도 제인도 다 그래요. 저는 그저 —\"\n\n\"당신은 그저 어리석고 경솔한 말을 했을 뿐이야.\" 그가 대답했다. \"다시는 그러지 않도록 애쓰고, 자신을 잘 단속해, 클라라.\"\n\n어머니의 입술이, '네, 에드워드.' 하고 대답하듯 달싹였지만, 입 밖으로는 아무 말도 내지 않았다.\n\n\"내가 유감이라고 했지, 데이비드.\" 머드스톤 씨가 고개와 두 눈을 뻣뻣하게 나에게로 돌리며 말했다. \"네가 시무룩한 성미를 지녔다는 걸 알게 되어 말이다. 이건 내 눈앞에서 고쳐 보려는 노력도 없이 멋대로 자라도록 두고 볼 수 있는 성격이 아니다. 너는 그걸 바꾸도록 애써야 한다. 우리도 너를 위해 그걸 바꾸도록 애써야 하고.\"\n\n\"죄송해요, 아저씨.\" 나는 더듬거렸다. \"돌아온 뒤로 시무룩하게 굴 생각은 한 번도 없었어요.\"\n\n\"거짓말 뒤에 숨지 마라!\" 그가 어찌나 사납게 받아치던지, 어머니가 우리 사이에 끼어들기라도 하려는 듯 떨리는 손을 저도 모르게 내미는 게 보였다. \"너는 시무룩한 심사로 네 방에 틀어박혔어. 여기 있어야 마땅할 때 네 방에만 처박혀 있었지. 이제 단단히 알아 둬라. 나는 네가 거기가 아니라 여기 있기를 바란다. 게다가 나는 네가 여기에 순종을 들고 오기를 바라. 너는 나를 알지, 데이비드. 나는 기어이 이 일을 시킬 거야.\"\n\n머드스톤 양이 쉰 목소리로 낄낄거렸다.\n\n\"나는 나 자신에 대해, 그리고 제인 머드스톤에 대해, 그리고 네 어머니에 대해, 공손하고 즉각적이며 선뜻한 태도를 보길 바란다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"나는 이 방을 무슨 병이라도 옮은 곳인 양 어린애 멋대로 피해 다니는 꼴을 두고 보지 않겠다. 앉아라.\"\n\n그는 개를 부리듯 나에게 명령했고, 나는 개처럼 따랐다.\n\n\"한 가지 더.\" 그가 말했다. \"보아하니 너는 천하고 상스러운 무리를 좋아하는 모양이더구나. 너는 하인들과 어울려선 안 된다. 부엌은, 네가 고쳐야 할 그 숱한 면에서 너를 조금도 나아지게 하지 못해. 너를 부추기는 그 여자에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠다 — 클라라, 너는,\" 그가 어머니를 향해 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"오랜 인연과 뿌리 깊은 정 때문에 그 여자를 두고 아직 다 떨쳐 내지 못한 약한 마음이 있으니까.\"\n\n\"참으로 알다가도 모를 망상이지요!\" 머드스톤 양이 외쳤다.\n\n\"나는 다만 이 말만 하겠다.\" 그가 나를 향해 다시 말했다. \"나는 네가 패거티 부인 같은 무리를 좋아하는 걸 못마땅하게 여기며, 그건 그만두어야 한다. 자, 데이비드, 너는 내 말을 알아들었고, 내 말을 한 자도 어김없이 따르지 않으면 어떤 결과가 따를지도 안다.\"\n\n나는 잘 알았다 — 내 가엾은 어머니에 관한 한, 어쩌면 그가 생각하는 것보다 더 잘 알았다 — 그래서 나는 그의 말을 한 자도 어김없이 따랐다. 나는 두 번 다시 내 방으로 물러가지 않았고, 두 번 다시 패거티에게로 피신하지 않았다. 그저 날마다 지치도록 거실에 앉아, 밤이 오기를, 잠자리에 들 시간이 오기를 손꼽아 기다렸다.\n\n내가 견뎌야 했던 그 진절머리 나는 속박이라니. 몇 시간이고 똑같은 자세로 앉아, 팔 하나 다리 하나 움직였다간 머드스톤 양이 (그녀는 아주 사소한 핑곗거리에도 그랬다) 내가 안절부절못한다고 트집 잡을까 봐 두렵고, 눈 한 번 굴렸다간 내 눈에서 새로운 트집거리를 찾아낼 만한 싫증이나 의심의 빛이 그녀 눈에 떠오를까 봐 두려웠으니! 시계가 째깍거리는 소리에 귀 기울이고, 머드스톤 양이 자그마한 반짝이는 강철 구슬을 꿰는 걸 지켜보며, 그녀가 과연 시집을 가긴 갈지, 간다면 어떤 불행한 사내한테 갈지 궁금해하고, 벽난로 선반 쇠시리의 칸들을 헤아리고, 벽지에 그려진 곱슬곱슬하고 나선으로 꼬인 무늬들 사이로 천장까지 눈길을 떠돌리며 앉아 있는 그 견딜 수 없는 따분함이라니!\n\n궂은 겨울 날씨에 진흙투성이 골목길을 따라 나는 또 얼마나 많이 홀로 걸어 다녔던가. 그 거실을, 그리고 그 안의 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양을 어디로든 짊어지고서. 그것은 내가 짊어질 수밖에 없는 끔찍한 짐이었고, 도무지 깨고 나올 길이 없는 한낮의 악몽이었으며, 내 정신 위에 내려앉아 그것을 무디게 만드는 무게였다!\n\n내가 먹은 그 식사들은 또 얼마나 침묵과 거북함 속이었던가. 나이프와 포크가 하나 너무 많은데 그게 바로 내 것이고, 식욕이 하나 너무 많은데 그게 바로 내 것이며, 접시와 의자가 하나 너무 많은데 그게 바로 내 것이고, 누군가 하나가 너무 많은데 그게 바로 나라는 느낌을 늘 받으면서!\n\n촛불이 들어오고 나도 무언가 일거리를 찾아야 할 그 저녁들은 또 어땠던가. 재미난 책은 감히 읽지도 못하고, 머리가 딱딱하고 마음은 더 딱딱한 산수 책 따위를 들여다보고 있노라면, 도량형 표가 저절로 '룰 브리타니아'며 '우울아 물러가라' 같은 곡조에 맞춰 노래를 불러 댔다. 그것들은 외워지려고 가만히 있어 주기는커녕, 우리 할머니의 바늘귀에 실을 꿰듯 내 가엾은 머릿속을 한쪽 귀로 들어왔다 다른 쪽 귀로 빠져나가며 줄줄이 꿰뚫고 지나가는 것이었다! 그토록 조심을 했건만 나는 또 얼마나 자주 하품을 하고 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았던가. 몰래 빠져든 잠에서 또 얼마나 화들짝 깨어났던가. 내가 어쩌다 한마디씩 던지는 사소한 말에 돌아오는 대답은 또 얼마나 없었던가. 나는 모두가 못 본 척하면서도 모두에게 거치적거리는, 텅 빈 공백 같은 존재였다. 밤 아홉 시 첫 종소리를 머드스톤 양이 반기며 나에게 잠자리에 들라고 명할 때, 그게 또 얼마나 묵직한 안도였던가!\n\n이렇게 방학은 더디 흘러갔고, 마침내 머드스톤 양이 \"오늘이 마지막 하루구나!\" 하고 말하며 방학의 마지막 찻잔을 나에게 건네는 아침이 찾아왔다.\n\n나는 떠나는 게 서운하지 않았다. 나는 멍청한 상태로 가라앉아 있었지만, 조금씩 기운을 차리며 스티어포스를 만날 일을 기대하고 있었다. 비록 그 뒤로 크리클 선생이 어른거리기는 했지만. 다시 바키스 씨가 문 앞에 나타났고, 어머니가 작별 인사를 하려고 내 위로 몸을 굽혔을 때, 다시 머드스톤 양이 경고하는 목소리로 \"클라라!\" 하고 말했다.\n\n나는 어머니와 갓난 동생에게 입을 맞추었고, 그제야 무척 서글펐다. 하지만 떠나는 게 서운하지는 않았다. 우리 사이에는 날마다 그 깊은 골이 가로놓여 있었고, 날마다 그 이별이 있었으니까. 그리고 내 마음속에 살아 있는 것은 어머니가 나를 안아 준 그 포옹이라기보다 — 그것은 더없이 뜨거웠지만 — 그 포옹 뒤에 이어진 광경이다.\n\n내가 짐마차에 올라타 있을 때, 어머니가 나를 부르는 소리가 들렸다. 내다보니, 어머니가 정원 문간에 홀로 서서, 나에게 보여 주려고 아기를 두 팔로 높이 안아 들고 있었다. 춥고 고요한 날씨였다. 어머니가 아기를 안아 든 채 나를 뚫어지게 바라볼 때, 그 머리카락 한 올도, 옷자락 한 주름도 까딱하지 않았다.\n\n그렇게 나는 어머니를 잃었다. 그 뒤로 나는 학교에서 잠들 때면 그렇게 어머니를 보았다 — 내 침대 곁의 말 없는 존재로 — 똑같이 뚫어지게 바라보는 그 얼굴로 — 두 팔에 아기를 높이 안아 든 채로.\n\n# CHAPTER 9. I HAVE A MEMORABLE BIRTHDAY\n\n학교에서 일어난 일은 삼월에 내 생일 기념일이 다시 돌아올 때까지 죄다 건너뛰겠다. 스티어포스가 그 어느 때보다 더 우러러볼 만한 사람이었다는 것 말고는 아무것도 기억나지 않는다. 그는 학기 말, 어쩌면 그보다 더 일찍 떠날 예정이었고, 내 눈에는 전보다 더 기개 넘치고 자립심 강한 사람으로 비쳤으며, 그래서 전보다 더 매력적이었다. 하지만 그것 말고는 아무것도 떠오르지 않는다. 그 무렵을 내 마음에 새겨 놓은 그 거대한 기억이, 자잘한 기억들을 모조리 삼켜 버리고 홀로 남아 버린 듯하다.\n\n세일럼 하우스로 돌아온 때와 그 생일이 찾아온 때 사이에 꼬박 두 달이라는 틈이 있었다는 것조차 나로서는 믿기 어렵다. 그것이 사실임을 받아들일 수 있는 유일한 까닭은, 틀림없이 그러했음을 내가 알기 때문이다. 그렇지 않았다면, 그 사이에 아무런 간격도 없었으며, 한 사건이 다른 사건의 뒤꿈치를 곧장 밟고 따라온 것이라고 확신했으리라.\n\n그날이 어떤 날이었는지 나는 어찌나 또렷이 기억하는지! 나는 그곳에 자욱이 깔린 안개 냄새를 맡는다. 그 안개 사이로 유령처럼 어른거리는 흰 서리를 본다. 서리 맞은 내 머리카락이 축축하게 뺨에 늘어지는 것을 느낀다. 흐릿하게 길게 뻗은 교실 풍경을 따라 눈길을 던지면, 안개 낀 아침을 밝히려고 여기저기 촛불이 지지직거리고, 사내아이들이 손가락에 입김을 호호 불며 바닥을 발로 톡톡 구를 때 그 입김이 살을 에는 추위 속에 김처럼 서려 피어오른다. 아침 식사가 끝나고, 운동장에서 호출되어 들어왔을 때였다. 샤프 선생님이 들어와 말했다.\n\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드는 응접실로 들어가도록.\"\n\n나는 패거티가 보낸 꾸러미를 기대하며, 그 명령에 얼굴이 환해졌다. 내가 잽싸게 자리에서 일어나자, 곁에 있던 몇몇 아이들이 그 좋은 것들을 나눌 때 자기들도 빼놓지 말라고 미리 한마디씩 청을 넣었다.\n\n\"서두르지 마라, 데이비드.\" 샤프 선생님이 말했다. \"시간은 넉넉하니까, 얘야, 서두르지 마라.\"\n\n내가 만약 그 말투에 담긴 안쓰러운 어조를 잠깐이라도 곱씹어 보았더라면 어쩌면 놀랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 나는 나중에야 그것을 헤아렸다. 나는 응접실로 부랴부랴 달려갔다. 거기서 크리클 교장 선생님이 회초리와 신문을 앞에 놓고 아침을 들고 있었고, 크리클 부인은 뜯어 본 편지 한 통을 손에 쥐고 있었다. 하지만 꾸러미는 없었다.\n\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드.\" 크리클 부인이 나를 소파로 이끌어 옆에 앉히며 말했다. \"너에게 아주 긴히 할 말이 있단다. 너에게 들려줄 게 있어, 얘야.\"\n\n내가 당연히 눈길을 던졌던 크리클 교장 선생님은, 나를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 고개를 가로젓고는, 버터 바른 토스트를 큼지막하게 한 입 베어 물어 한숨을 틀어막았다.\n\n\"넌 세상이 날마다 어떻게 변하는지, 그 안의 사람들이 어떻게 세상을 떠나가는지 알기엔 너무 어리지.\" 크리클 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 우리는 모두 그것을 배워야 한단다, 데이비드. 누구는 젊을 때, 누구는 늙어서, 또 누구는 평생 어느 때고 말이야.\"\n\n나는 그분을 골똘히 바라보았다.\n\n\"방학이 끝나고 집에서 떠나올 때,\" 잠시 뜸을 들이고 나서 크리클 부인이 말했다. \"다들 잘 지냈니?\" 다시 한 번 뜸을 들이고는, \"네 엄마는 건강하셨고?\"\n\n나는 까닭도 분명히 모른 채 몸을 떨었고, 여전히 그분을 골똘히 바라보기만 할 뿐 대답하려는 시도조차 하지 못했다.\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 그분이 말했다. \"이런 말을 전하게 되어 가슴이 아프지만, 오늘 아침에 네 엄마가 몹시 편찮으시다는 소식을 들었단다.\"\n\n크리클 부인과 나 사이로 안개가 피어올랐고, 그 속에서 그분의 모습이 한순간 일렁이는 것 같았다. 그러다 뜨거운 눈물이 내 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 느꼈고, 그러자 다시 모든 것이 또렷해졌다.\n\n\"위중하실 정도로 편찮으시단다.\" 그분이 덧붙였다.\n\n이제 나는 다 알았다.\n\n\"돌아가셨어.\"\n\n굳이 그렇게 말해 줄 필요도 없었다. 나는 이미 처량한 울음을 터뜨렸고, 이 넓은 세상에 홀로 남은 고아가 된 기분이었다.\n\n그분은 나에게 무척 다정했다. 그분은 나를 하루 종일 거기 머물게 하면서, 때때로 혼자 있게 내버려 두었다. 나는 울다가 지쳐 잠들었고, 깨어나서는 또 울었다. 더 이상 울 수 없게 되자 나는 생각에 잠기기 시작했다. 그러자 가슴을 짓누르는 무게가 가장 무거워졌고, 내 슬픔은 달랠 길 없는 둔한 통증이 되었다.\n\n그런데도 내 생각은 부질없이 떠돌기만 했다. 가슴을 짓누르는 재앙에 골똘히 매달리지 못하고, 그 언저리를 하릴없이 어슬렁거렸다. 나는 문이 닫히고 적막에 잠긴 우리 집을 떠올렸다. 크리클 부인이 한동안 시름시름 앓아 왔다던, 그래서 사람들이 그 아이마저 죽을 거라 여긴다던 그 어린 아기를 떠올렸다. 우리 집 곁 교회 묘지에 있는 아버지의 무덤을, 그리고 내가 그토록 잘 아는 그 나무 아래 누워 계실 어머니를 떠올렸다. 혼자 남겨졌을 때 나는 의자 위에 올라가 거울을 들여다보며 내 눈이 얼마나 빨갛게 부었는지, 내 얼굴이 얼마나 슬퍼 보이는지를 살폈다. 몇 시간이 흐른 뒤, 나는 곰곰이 생각해 보았다. 지금 보이는 것처럼 정말로 눈물이 잘 나오지 않는다면, 집에 가까이 다가갈 때 내가 잃은 것과 관련해 무엇을 떠올리는 것이 나를 가장 마음 아프게 할까 하고 말이다 — 나는 장례식 때문에 집으로 가는 길이었으니까. 나는 다른 아이들 사이에서 나에게 어떤 위엄이 깃든 듯한 느낌을, 그리고 내가 그 슬픔 덕분에 중요한 사람이 된 듯한 느낌을 받았던 것을 분명히 기억한다.\n\n세상에 진실로 슬픔에 짓눌린 아이가 있다면 그게 바로 나였다. 하지만 그날 오후 다른 아이들이 수업을 받는 동안 내가 운동장을 거닐 때, 그 중요한 사람이 된 느낌이 일종의 만족감으로 다가왔던 것을 나는 기억한다. 아이들이 교실로 올라가며 창문 너머로 나를 흘끔거리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 내가 남다른 사람이 된 듯한 기분이 들어 더욱 우울한 표정을 짓고 더 느릿느릿 걸었다. 수업이 끝나 아이들이 나와 나에게 말을 걸었을 때, 나는 누구에게도 으스대지 않고 예전과 똑같이 모두를 한결같이 대하는 내 모습이 제법 갸륵하게 여겨졌다.\n\n나는 이튿날 밤에 집으로 갈 예정이었다. 우편 마차가 아니라, '농부'라고 불리는 묵직한 야간 합승 마차였는데, 주로 길에서 가까운 중간 거리를 오가는 시골 사람들이 이용하는 마차였다. 그날 저녁에는 이야기 들려주기도 없었고, 트래들스는 자기 베개를 나에게 빌려주겠다고 우겼다. 나도 내 베개가 있었으니 그게 무슨 도움이 될 거라 여겼는지 모르겠지만, 가엾은 녀석에게는 그것밖에 빌려줄 게 없었다 — 해골이 잔뜩 그려진 편지지 한 장 말고는. 그것마저 그는 헤어질 때 나에게 건네주었는데, 내 슬픔을 달래 주고 내 마음에 평안을 보태 주려는 뜻에서였다.\n\n나는 이튿날 오후 세일럼 하우스를 떠났다. 그때 나는 그곳을 영영 다시 돌아오지 못할 채로 떠나는 줄은 까맣게 몰랐다. 우리는 밤새 아주 느릿느릿 달렸고, 야머스에 들어선 것은 아침 아홉 시인가 열 시가 되어서였다. 나는 바키스 씨를 찾아 두리번거렸지만 그는 없었다. 그 대신 까만 옷을 입은, 뚱뚱하고 숨이 가쁘며 쾌활해 보이는 자그마한 노인이 — 반바지 무릎께에 녹슨 듯한 리본 다발을 자그맣게 달고, 검은 양말에 챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고 — 헐떡거리며 마차 창문께로 다가와 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 도련님?\"\n\n\"네, 그런데요.\"\n\n\"괜찮으시다면 저와 함께 가시지요, 도련님.\" 그가 문을 열며 말했다. \"제가 도련님을 집까지 모셔다드리는 기쁨을 누리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n나는 이 사람이 누구인가 궁금해하며 그의 손에 내 손을 맡겼고, 우리는 좁은 거리에 있는 어느 가게로 걸어갔다. 가게에는 '오머. 포목상, 양복점, 잡화상, 장의용품 일체 등'이라고 쓰여 있었다. 답답하고 숨 막히는 자그마한 가게였는데, 다 지은 것과 짓다 만 것을 가리지 않고 온갖 옷가지가 가득했고, 진열창 하나는 비버 가죽 모자와 보닛으로 빼곡했다. 우리는 가게 뒤편의 작은 거실로 들어갔다. 거기서 젊은 여자 셋이 검은 천 무더기를 가지고 일하고 있었는데, 천은 탁자 위에 산더미처럼 쌓여 있었고, 자투리와 잘린 조각들이 온 바닥에 어지러이 널려 있었다. 방에는 불이 활활 타오르고 있었고, 따뜻한 검은 크레이프 천에서 풍기는 숨 막히는 냄새가 가득했다 — 그때는 그게 무슨 냄새인지 몰랐지만 지금은 안다.\n\n매우 부지런하고 편안해 보이는 그 젊은 여자 셋은 고개를 들어 나를 한번 보고는, 다시 하던 일을 이어 갔다. 바느질, 바느질, 바느질. 그러는 동안 창밖 작은 마당 건너편 작업장에서, 가락을 맞추는 듯한 규칙적인 망치질 소리가 들려왔다. 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 한 치의 변화도 없이.\n\n\"그래.\" 나를 데려온 사람이 젊은 여자 셋 가운데 하나에게 말했다. \"어떻게 돼 가니, 미니?\"\n\n\"가봉할 시간까지는 다 될 거예요.\" 그 여자가 고개도 들지 않고 명랑하게 대답했다. \"걱정 마세요, 아버지.\"\n\n오머 씨는 챙 넓은 모자를 벗고 자리에 앉아 헐떡였다. 어찌나 뚱뚱한지, 한참을 헐떡인 뒤에야 비로소 말을 할 수 있었다.\n\n\"그래야지.\"\n\n\"아버지!\" 미니가 장난스럽게 말했다. \"어쩜 그렇게 돌고래처럼 점점 불어나세요!\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 어쩌다 이렇게 됐는지 모르겠구나, 얘야.\" 그가 곰곰 따져 보며 대답했다. \"내가 영 이렇구나.\"\n\n\"아버진 워낙 마음이 느긋하시잖아요.\" 미니가 말했다. \"뭐든 그렇게 편하게 받아들이시니까요.\"\n\n\"달리 받아들여 봐야 소용없잖니, 얘야.\" 오머 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 정말 그래요.\" 딸이 받아쳤다. \"여긴 다들 꽤나 명랑하답니다, 하느님 감사합니다! 안 그래요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"그러길 바란다, 얘야.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"이제 숨도 좀 돌렸으니, 이 어린 학생 치수를 재 볼까 싶구나. 가게로 들어와 주시겠어요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\"\n\n나는 그분의 청에 따라 오머 씨 앞서서 들어갔다. 그분은 나에게 천 한 두루마리를 보여 주며 이건 특상품인데, 부모상이 아니고서는 쓰기 아까울 만큼 좋은 상복감이라고 했다. 그러고는 내 몸의 이런저런 치수를 재어 장부에 적었다. 치수를 적어 가면서 그분은 자기 가게 물건들에 내 눈길을 끌어, '막 유행하기 시작한' 어떤 방식과 '막 유행이 지나간' 또 다른 어떤 방식들을 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"그런 것 때문에 우리는 종종 적잖은 돈을 손해 보지요.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 유행이란 사람과 같답니다. 언제, 왜, 어떻게 들어오는지 아무도 모르고, 또 언제, 왜, 어떻게 사라지는지도 아무도 모르지요. 그런 관점에서 보면, 제 생각엔 세상 모든 게 다 삶과 같답니다.\"\n\n나는 그 문제를 두고 따져 볼 만큼 마음에 여유가 없었고, 어차피 어떤 처지에서든 나로서는 헤아리기 벅찬 이야기였을 것이다. 오머 씨는 나를 다시 거실로 데려갔는데, 가는 길에 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬었다.\n\n그러더니 그분은 문 뒤편의 가파른 계단 아래로 대고 소리쳤다. \"차하고 버터 바른 빵 좀 올려 보내!\" 한참이 지나는 동안 나는 앉아서 둘레를 둘러보고 이런저런 생각에 잠긴 채, 방 안의 바느질 소리와 마당 건너편에서 망치로 두드리는 가락 소리에 귀를 기울였는데, 이윽고 그것이 쟁반에 담겨 나타났고, 알고 보니 나를 위한 것이었다.\n\n\"내가 자네를 알고 지낸 지가 말이지,\" 오머 씨가 몇 분간 나를 지켜본 뒤에 말했다. 그동안 나는 아침을 거의 입에 대지 못했는데, 검은 천 때문에 입맛이 싹 가셨기 때문이다. \"내가 자네를 알고 지낸 지가 아주 오래됐다네, 젊은 친구.\"\n\n\"그러셨어요?\"\n\n\"자네가 태어난 뒤로 줄곧이지.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"태어나기 전부터라고도 할 수 있겠군. 난 자네보다 먼저 자네 아버지를 알았으니까. 그분은 키가 다섯 자 아홉 치 반이셨는데, 지금은 스—물다섯 자 땅속에 누워 계시지.\"\n\n\"탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁.\" 마당 건너편에서.\n\n\"한 치도 어김없이 스—물다섯 자 땅속에 누워 계신다네.\" 오머 씨가 정답게 말했다. \"그분이 부탁하신 건지, 아니면 부인이 지시하신 건지, 어느 쪽인지는 잊어버렸군.\"\n\n\"제 어린 동생은 어떤지 아세요, 아저씨?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n오머 씨는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁.\"\n\n\"그 아이는 제 어머니 품에 안겨 있다네.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 가엾은 것! 죽은 거예요?\"\n\n\"되도록 너무 마음 쓰지 말게.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그래. 아기는 죽었어.\"\n\n이 소식에 내 상처가 다시 터져 벌어졌다. 나는 거의 입도 대지 못한 아침을 그대로 두고, 작은 방 한쪽 구석의 다른 탁자로 가서 그 위에 머리를 기댔다. 미니는 거기 놓인 상복에 내 눈물이 얼룩질까 봐 황급히 치워 주었다. 그 여자는 예쁘고 마음씨 고운 처녀여서, 부드럽고 다정한 손길로 내 눈을 가린 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겨 주었다. 하지만 일을 거의 끝내고 제때에 맞추게 되었다며 무척 들떠 있어서, 나와는 너무도 딴판이었다!\n\n이윽고 가락 소리가 멎더니, 잘생긴 젊은 사내가 마당을 가로질러 방으로 들어왔다. 손에는 망치를 들었고, 입안 가득 작은 못을 물고 있어서 말을 하려면 먼저 그것들을 빼내야 했다.\n\n\"그래, 조람!\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"어떻게 돼 가나?\"\n\n\"다 됐습니다.\" 조람이 말했다. \"끝냈어요, 사장님.\"\n\n미니가 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔고, 다른 두 처녀가 서로를 보며 빙긋 웃었다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 그럼 내가 클럽에 가 있던 어젯밤에 자네 촛불 켜 놓고 그걸 했단 말인가? 그랬어?\" 오머 씨가 한쪽 눈을 찡긋 감으며 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 조람이 말했다. \"일이 끝나면 우리끼리 짧은 나들이라도 해서, 다 같이 다녀와도 좋다고 하셨잖아요. 미니랑 저랑 — 그리고 사장님까지요.\"\n\n\"오! 난 자네가 날 아예 빼놓을 줄 알았지.\" 오머 씨가 기침이 날 만큼 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"— 사장님이 그렇게 좋은 말씀을 해 주셨으니,\" 젊은 사내가 말을 이었다. \"그래서 제가 신이 나서 달려든 거지요, 아시겠어요? 한번 보시고 어떤지 말씀해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"그러지.\" 오머 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"얘야.\" 그러다 그분이 말을 멈추고 나를 돌아보았다. \"혹시 네 — 보고 싶니—\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 아버지.\" 미니가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"마음이 놓일까 싶어서 그랬다, 얘야.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 네 말이 맞을지도 모르겠구나.\"\n\n그들이 보러 간 것이 내 사랑하는, 그리운 어머니의 관이라는 걸 내가 어떻게 알았는지는 말로 다 못 하겠다. 나는 관 만드는 소리를 들어 본 적도 없었고, 내가 아는 한 관을 본 적도 없었다. 하지만 그 소리가 나는 동안, 그게 무슨 소리인지 문득 마음에 떠올랐다. 그리고 그 젊은 사내가 들어왔을 때, 나는 그가 무슨 일을 하고 있었는지 분명히 알아챘다.\n\n이제 일이 끝나자, 이름은 듣지 못한 두 처녀가 옷에 묻은 천 부스러기와 실밥을 털어 내고, 가게로 나가 그쪽을 정돈하고 손님을 기다렸다. 미니는 뒤에 남아 그들이 지은 것을 개켜서 바구니 두 개에 챙겨 넣었다. 무릎을 꿇고 그 일을 하면서, 흥겨운 짧은 가락을 흥얼거렸다. 분명 그녀의 연인일 조람이 들어와, 그녀가 바삐 일하는 틈에 살짝 입을 맞추고는 (나는 전혀 안중에도 없는 듯했다), 아버지가 마차를 가지러 가셨으니 어서 채비를 해야겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 다시 나갔다. 그러자 미니는 골무와 가위를 주머니에 넣고, 검은 실을 꿴 바늘을 옷 가슴팍에 가지런히 꽂은 다음, 문 뒤의 작은 거울 앞에서 잽싸게 겉옷을 걸쳤는데, 그 거울 속에 비친 그녀의 흡족한 얼굴이 나에게도 보였다.\n\n이 모든 것을 나는 구석 탁자에 앉아 손에 머리를 괴고 지켜보았으며, 내 생각은 전혀 다른 데로 치닫고 있었다. 곧 마차가 가게 앞으로 돌아왔고, 바구니들이 먼저 실리고, 그다음에 내가 태워졌으며, 그 세 사람이 뒤따라 올랐다. 내 기억으로 그것은 반쯤은 짐마차, 반쯤은 피아노 운반차 같은 것으로, 침침한 빛깔로 칠해져 있었고, 꼬리가 긴 검은 말이 끌었다. 우리 모두가 타고도 자리가 넉넉했다.\n\n내 평생 그토록 기묘한 느낌을 겪어 본 적이 있나 싶다 (이제는 어쩌면 좀 더 철이 들었지만). 그들이 무슨 일을 했었는지를 떠올리면서, 그들과 함께 있으며 그들이 마차 나들이를 즐기는 모습을 바라보는 느낌 말이다. 나는 그들에게 화가 나지는 않았다. 오히려 그들이 무서웠는데, 마치 나와는 본성이 전혀 다른 생명체들 가운데 내던져진 것만 같았다. 그들은 무척 명랑했다. 노인은 앞자리에 앉아 마차를 몰았고, 두 젊은이는 그 뒤에 앉아, 그가 말을 걸 때마다 통통한 그의 얼굴 양쪽으로 하나는 이쪽에서, 하나는 저쪽에서 몸을 기울이며 그를 한껏 떠받들었다. 그들은 나에게도 말을 걸려 했지만, 나는 뒤로 물러나 구석에서 시무룩이 웅크렸다. 그들의 사랑놀음과 들뜬 웃음이 — 결코 요란스럽지는 않았으나 — 나를 겁먹게 했고, 그토록 모진 마음에도 아무런 천벌이 내리지 않는 것이 거의 의아하게 느껴졌다.\n\n그래서 그들이 말에게 먹이를 주려고 멈춰 서서 먹고 마시며 흥겹게 즐길 때에도, 나는 그들이 손댄 것에는 아무것도 손대지 못하고 단식을 깨뜨리지 않았다. 그래서 집에 다다랐을 때에도, 나는 최대한 잽싸게 마차 뒤로 내려서, 한때 빛나던 두 눈이 감긴 것처럼 멀거니 나를 내려다보는 그 엄숙한 창문들 앞에서 그들과 함께 있지 않으려 했다. 아, 그리고 돌아왔을 때 무엇이 나를 울리게 할까 따위를 굳이 생각할 필요가 어디 있었던가 — 어머니 방의 창문을, 그리고 그 옆에, 좋았던 시절 내 방이었던 창문을 보게 되었으니!\n\n나는 문에 채 닿기도 전에 패거티의 품에 안겼고, 패거티는 나를 집 안으로 데려갔다. 그녀는 나를 처음 보았을 때 슬픔이 북받쳐 터져 나왔지만, 곧 그것을 억누르고 속삭이듯 말하며, 마치 죽은 이를 깨우기라도 할까 봐 살금살금 걸었다. 알고 보니 그녀는 오랫동안 잠자리에 들지 못한 채였다. 밤이면 여전히 자지 않고 앉아 지켰다. 가엾고 사랑스러운 자기 아씨가 땅 위에 머무는 한, 결코 곁을 떠나지 않겠노라고 그녀는 말했다.\n\n머드스톤 씨는 내가 그가 있는 거실로 들어갔을 때 나에게 눈길 한 번 주지 않고, 난롯가에 앉아 소리 없이 눈물을 흘리며 안락의자에 잠겨 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 편지와 서류로 뒤덮인 책상에서 부지런히 일하고 있던 머드스톤 양은 차가운 손톱을 나에게 내밀더니, 쇳소리 같은 속삭임으로 상복 치수를 다 쟀느냐고 물었다.\n\n나는 대답했다. \"네.\"\n\n\"셔츠는,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"집으로 가져왔니?\"\n\n\"네, 아주머니. 제 옷은 전부 다 가져왔어요.\"\n\n이것이 그녀의 그 야무진 태도가 나에게 베푼 위로의 전부였다. 그녀가 이런 상황에서 자신이 자제력이라 부르는 것, 그리고 야무짐과 굳은 의지, 분별력, 또 그 밖에 정나미 떨어지는 온갖 악마 같은 자질을 늘어놓아 보이는 데서 각별한 즐거움을 느꼈으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다. 그녀는 특히 자기가 일을 야무지게 처리하는 솜씨를 자랑스러워했고, 지금도 모든 것을 펜과 잉크로 처리하며 무슨 일에도 흔들리지 않는 모습으로 그것을 뽐냈다. 그날 내내, 그리고 그 후로도 아침부터 밤까지, 그녀는 그 책상에 앉아 딱딱한 펜으로 차분하게 사각사각 써 댔고, 누구에게나 한결같이 흔들림 없는 속삭임으로 말했으며, 얼굴 근육 하나 풀어지지 않고, 목소리 한 가락 누그러지지 않으며, 옷매무새 한 올 흐트러진 채로 나타나는 법이 없었다.\n\n그녀의 오빠는 가끔 책을 집어 들었지만, 내가 본 한 그것을 읽은 적은 없었다. 책을 펴서 마치 읽는 것처럼 들여다보면서도, 한 시간 내내 책장 한 장 넘기지 않은 채 있다가, 이내 책을 내려놓고 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었다. 나는 두 손을 모은 채 앉아, 몇 시간이고 그를 지켜보며 그의 발걸음을 헤아리곤 했다. 그는 그녀에게 좀처럼 말을 걸지 않았고, 나에게는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그는 이 미동조차 없는 집 안에서, 시계들 말고는 유일하게 안절부절못하는 존재인 듯했다.\n\n장례식 전의 그 며칠 동안, 나는 패거티를 거의 보지 못했다. 다만 계단을 오르내릴 때면 언제나 어머니와 아기가 누워 있는 방 가까이에 그녀가 있는 것을 보았고, 또 밤마다 그녀가 내게 와서 내가 잠들 때까지 침대 머리맡에 앉아 있었던 것은 예외였다. 매장하기 하루나 이틀 전에 — 하루나 이틀 전이었던 것 같은데, 그 무거운 시간에 대해서는 나아간 자취를 짚을 만한 표시가 아무것도 없어서 내 기억이 뒤죽박죽이라는 걸 안다 — 그녀가 나를 그 방으로 데려갔다. 내가 기억하는 것이라곤, 침대 위 어떤 흰 덮개 아래, 둘레가 온통 아름답게 정갈하고 산뜻한 가운데, 이 집을 감돌던 그 엄숙한 정적이 형체를 입고 누워 있는 듯했다는 것뿐이다. 그리고 그녀가 그 덮개를 살며시 젖히려 했을 때, 나는 \"아, 안 돼요! 아, 안 돼요!\" 하고 외치며 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n장례식이 어제 일이었다 해도 이보다 더 또렷이 기억할 수는 없을 것이다. 제일 좋은 거실의 그 공기, 문으로 들어설 때의 그 환한 난롯불, 디캔터 속 포도주의 반짝임, 잔과 접시의 무늬, 어렴풋이 달콤한 케이크 냄새, 머드스톤 양의 옷에서 풍기던 냄새, 그리고 우리의 검은 옷. 칠립 의사 선생님이 방 안에 있다가 나에게 말을 걸러 온다.\n\n\"데이비드 도련님은 좀 어떤가요?\" 그가 다정하게 묻는다.\n\n나는 그에게 뭐라 제대로 대답하지 못한다. 나는 그에게 손을 내밀고, 그는 그 손을 제 손에 쥐어 준다.\n\n\"이런, 저런!\" 칠립 의사 선생님이 눈에 무언가 반짝이는 것을 띤 채 온화하게 미소 지으며 말한다. \"우리 어린 친구들이 우리 곁에서 무럭무럭 자라나는군요. 어느새 우리가 못 알아볼 만큼 커 버리지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 이건 머드스톤 양에게 한 말인데, 그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않는다.\n\n\"이 댁 도련님이 부쩍 자랐지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 칠립 의사 선생님이 말한다.\n\n머드스톤 양은 그저 눈살을 찌푸리고 형식적으로 고개를 까딱하는 것으로 답할 뿐이다. 무안해진 칠립 의사 선생님은 나를 데리고 한쪽 구석으로 가더니, 더는 입을 열지 않는다.\n\n내가 이런 것을 일일이 짚어 두는 까닭은, 내가 일어나는 모든 일을 낱낱이 짚기 때문이지, 나 자신을 챙겨서가 아니다. 집에 돌아온 뒤로 나는 나 자신 따위를 챙긴 적이 없었다. 이제 종소리가 울리기 시작하고, 오머 씨와 또 한 사람이 우리에게 채비를 갖춰 주러 온다. 오래전 패거티가 곧잘 들려주었듯이, 아버지를 같은 무덤으로 따라간 사람들도 이 같은 방에서 채비를 갖췄었다.\n\n머드스톤 씨와 우리 이웃 그레이퍼 씨, 칠립 의사 선생님, 그리고 내가 있다. 우리가 문을 나서자, 운구하는 사람들과 그들이 짊어진 짐이 정원에 있다. 그들은 우리 앞서서 오솔길을 따라, 느릅나무들을 지나, 문을 통과해, 교회 묘지로 들어선다. 여름 아침이면 새들이 노래하는 소리를 내가 그토록 자주 듣곤 했던 곳이다.\n\n우리는 무덤 둘레에 둘러선다. 그날은 다른 모든 날과 달라 보이고, 햇빛도 여느 때와 같은 빛깔이 아니다 — 더 슬픈 빛깔이다. 이제 엄숙한 정적이 감도는데, 흙 속에 잠든 것과 함께 우리가 집에서 가져온 정적이다. 우리가 맨머리로 서 있는 동안, 나는 탁 트인 바깥에서 아득히 들려오면서도 또렷하고 분명한 목사님의 목소리를 듣는다. \"나는 부활이요 생명이니, 주께서 이르시되!\" 이윽고 흐느낌이 들리고, 지켜보는 사람들 가운데 외따로 떨어져 서서, 나는 이 땅 위의 누구보다도 내가 가장 사랑하며, 언젠가 주께서 \"착하고 충성된 종아, 잘하였도다\" 하고 말씀해 주시리라고 내 어린 마음이 확신하는, 그 착하고 충실한 종을 본다.\n\n자그마한 무리 속에는 내가 아는 얼굴들이 많다. 내가 늘 두리번거리던 시절 교회에서 보았던 얼굴들, 어머니가 젊고 꽃다운 시절 이 마을로 왔을 때 어머니를 처음 본 얼굴들. 나는 그들에게 마음 쓰지 않는다 — 내 슬픔 말고는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않는다 — 그런데도 나는 그들 모두를 보고 알아본다. 멀리 뒤편에서, 미니가 지켜보고 있는 것마저, 그리고 내 곁에 있는 자기 연인에게 흘긋 던지는 그녀의 눈길마저 본다.\n\n다 끝나고, 흙이 메워지고, 우리는 돌아서서 떠나려 한다. 우리 앞에는 우리 집이 서 있다. 그토록 예쁘고 변함없으며, 이제는 사라진 그 어린 시절의 생각과 내 마음속에 너무도 단단히 얽혀 있어서, 지금껏 내가 겪은 슬픔이 그것이 불러일으키는 슬픔에 비하면 아무것도 아닌 것만 같다. 하지만 사람들이 나를 데려가고, 칠립 의사 선생님이 나에게 말을 건다. 집에 다다르자 그는 내 입술에 물을 적셔 주고, 내가 내 방으로 올라가게 해 달라고 청하자, 여인 같은 다정함으로 나를 보내 준다.\n\n다시 말하건대, 이 모든 것이 어제의 일이다. 그 뒤에 일어난 일들은 잊힌 모든 것이 다시 떠오르는 그 기슭으로 나에게서 떠내려가 버렸지만, 이 일만은 바다 한가운데 우뚝 솟은 높은 바위처럼 서 있다.\n\n나는 패거티가 내 방으로 올 것임을 알고 있었다. 그 무렵의 안식일 같은 정적이 (그날은 너무도 일요일 같았다! 그걸 깜빡했구나) 우리 둘에게 꼭 어울렸다. 그녀는 내 작은 침대로 와서 내 곁에 앉았다. 그러고는 내 손을 잡고, 때로는 그 손을 입술에 갖다 대고, 때로는 자기 손으로 쓸어 주면서, 마치 내 어린 동생을 달래기라도 하듯이, 일어난 일에 관해 들려줄 것을 모두 자기 나름의 방식으로 나에게 이야기해 주었다.\n\n\"아씨는 오랫동안,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"내내 편찮으셨단다. 마음이 갈피를 못 잡고, 행복하지가 않으셨어. 아기가 태어났을 때 난 처음엔 좀 나아지시려나 했는데, 더 여위어 가시면서 날마다 조금씩 가라앉으셨지. 아기가 태어나기 전엔 혼자 앉아 계시기를 좋아하셨는데, 그럴 때면 우셨단다. 하지만 그 뒤엔 아기에게 노래를 불러 주셨지 — 어찌나 부드럽던지, 한번은 그 소리를 들으면서, 마치 저 하늘 위로 솟아오르며 멀어져 가는 목소리 같다고 생각했어.\n\n\"요즘 들어선 더 겁이 많아지고, 더 무서움을 타시는 것 같았어. 모진 말 한마디가 매를 맞는 것이나 다름없으셨지. 하지만 나한텐 늘 한결같으셨단다. 내 사랑스러운 아씨는, 어리석은 자기 패거티한테는 결코 변함이 없으셨어.\"\n\n여기서 패거티는 말을 멈추고, 잠시 내 손을 가만가만 토닥였다.\n\n\"아씨가 예전의 그 모습 그대로인 걸 마지막으로 본 건, 얘야, 네가 집에 돌아온 날 밤이었어. 네가 떠나던 날, 아씨가 나한테 그러셨지. '나는 내 어여쁜 보물을 다시는 못 볼 거야. 무언가가 나한테 그렇게 일러 줘. 그게 진실을 말하고 있다는 걸 난 알아.'\n\n\"그 뒤로 아씨는 버텨 보려 애쓰셨어. 사람들이 아씨더러 철없고 속없이 들떠 있다고 할 때마다, 여러 번 그런 척 꾸며 보이기도 하셨지. 하지만 그건 이미 다 지난 일이었어. 아씨는 나한테 말한 걸 남편한텐 끝내 털어놓지 않으셨어 — 다른 누구한테도 그 말을 하기가 두려우셨던 거야 — 그러다 일이 벌어지기 한 주하고도 며칠 전 어느 날 밤, 마침내 그이한테 이러셨대. '여보, 나 죽어 가는 것 같아요.'\n\n\"'이제 마음에서 내려놓았어, 패거티.' 그날 밤 내가 아씨를 침대에 뉘어 드릴 때 아씨가 나한테 그러셨어. '저이는, 가엾은 사람, 앞으로 며칠 동안 날마다 그 말을 점점 더 믿게 될 거야. 그러고 나면 다 지나가겠지. 난 무척 지쳤어. 이게 잠이라면, 내가 자는 동안 내 곁에 앉아 있어 줘. 날 떠나지 마. 하느님, 제 두 아이를 축복하소서! 하느님, 아비 없는 제 아들을 지키고 보살펴 주소서!'\n\n\"그 뒤로 난 결코 아씨 곁을 떠나지 않았단다.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"아씨는 아래층 그 두 사람한테 종종 말을 거셨어 — 그들을 사랑하셨으니까. 곁에 있는 누구든 사랑하지 않고는 못 견디는 분이셨거든 — 하지만 그들이 침대 머리맡에서 물러가면, 아씨는 늘 나에게로 돌아서셨어. 마치 패거티가 있는 곳에 안식이 있다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 다른 어떤 식으로도 잠들지 않으셨지.\n\n\"마지막 날 밤, 저녁이 되자 아씨는 나에게 입을 맞추며 그러셨어. '내 아기마저 죽거든, 패거티, 부디 그 애를 내 품에 안겨서 우리를 함께 묻어 줘.' (그대로 되었단다. 가엾은 어린양은 아씨보다 단 하루를 더 살았을 뿐이거든.) '내 사랑하는 아들도 우리가 쉴 곳까지 함께 가게 해 줘.' 아씨가 그러셨어. '그리고 그 애한테 전해 줘. 제 어미가 여기 누워서 그 애를 축복했다고. 한 번이 아니라 천 번을.'\"\n\n이 말 뒤에 또 한 차례 침묵이 흘렀고, 내 손을 가만가만 토닥이는 손길이 또 한 번 이어졌다.\n\n\"밤이 꽤 깊었을 때,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"아씨가 나한테 마실 것을 좀 달라고 하셨어. 그러고는 그걸 받아 드시고 나서, 그 다정한 분이, 그렇게도 참을성 있는 미소를 나한테 지어 보이셨단다 — 어찌나 아름답던지!\n\n\"동이 트고 해가 떠오를 무렵, 아씨가 나한테 그러셨어. 코퍼필드 어른이 늘 얼마나 다정하고 자상하셨는지, 그이가 아씨를 얼마나 너그러이 받아 주셨는지, 그리고 아씨가 자신을 못 미더워할 때면 그이가 이르셨다는구나. 사랑하는 마음이 지혜보다 더 낫고 더 강한 거라고, 그리고 자기는 아씨의 그 마음 덕분에 행복한 사람이라고. '패거티, 내 사랑하는 사람,' 그때 아씨가 그러셨어. '날 너한테 더 가까이 붙여 줘.' 아씨가 무척 쇠약하셨거든. '네 그 좋은 팔을 내 목 밑에 받쳐 주고,' 아씨가 그러셨어. '날 너 쪽으로 돌려 줘. 네 얼굴이 점점 멀어져 가는데, 난 그게 가까이 있었으면 좋겠어.' 난 아씨가 청하신 대로 해 드렸지. 아, 데이비, 그러자 내가 너랑 처음 헤어지면서 했던 그 말이 참말이 되는 순간이 온 거야 — 아씨가 그 가엾은 머리를, 어리석고 심술궂은 늙은 패거티의 팔에 기꺼이 기대시던 그 순간이 말이야 — 그러고는 아씨는 잠든 어린아이처럼 숨을 거두셨단다!\"\n\n이렇게 패거티의 이야기가 끝났다. 어머니가 돌아가셨다는 것을 안 그 순간부터, 요즘의 어머니 모습은 내게서 사라져 버렸다. 그 순간부터 나는 오직 내 가장 어린 시절의 기억 속 젊은 어머니로만 그분을 떠올렸다. 빛나는 곱슬머리를 손가락에 돌돌 감곤 하고, 황혼 녘이면 거실에서 나와 함께 춤을 추던 그 어머니로. 패거티가 방금 나에게 들려준 이야기는, 나를 그 뒷날의 시절로 되돌려 놓기는커녕, 오히려 그 더 이른 시절의 모습을 내 마음에 더욱 깊이 뿌리내리게 했다. 묘하게 들릴지 모르지만 사실이다. 어머니는 죽음 속에서 평온하고 근심 없던 젊은 날로 날개를 펴 되돌아갔고, 그 밖의 모든 것을 지워 버렸다.\n\n무덤 속에 누운 어머니는 내 갓난 시절의 어머니였다. 그 품에 안긴 자그마한 것은, 한때의 나, 그분의 가슴에 안겨 영원히 잠든 나 자신이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. I BECOME NEGLECTED, AND AM PROVIDED FOR\n\n머드스톤 양은 장례라는 엄숙한 의식의 날이 끝나고 집 안에 빛이 거리낌 없이 들어오게 되자, 맨 먼저 한 일이 패거티에게 한 달 뒤에 나가라고 통보한 것이었다. 패거티가 그런 일자리를 아무리 싫어했더라도, 나를 위해서라면 세상에서 가장 좋은 자리보다 그 자리를 지켰을 거라고 나는 믿는다. 패거티는 우리가 헤어져야 한다고, 그 까닭이 무엇인지 나에게 말해 주었다. 그리고 우리는 진심을 다해 서로를 위로했다.\n\n나에 대해서나 내 앞날에 대해서는 단 한마디 말도, 단 한 걸음의 조치도 없었다. 감히 말하건대, 나까지 한 달 통보로 내보낼 수 있었다면 그들은 더없이 기뻐했을 것이다. 나는 한 번 용기를 내어 머드스톤 양에게 학교로 언제 돌아가느냐고 물어보았다. 그러자 그녀는 무뚝뚝하게, 내가 아예 돌아가지 않을 것 같다고 대답했다. 그 이상은 아무것도 듣지 못했다. 나는 내가 어떻게 될 것인지 무척 알고 싶었고, 패거티도 마찬가지였지만, 그녀도 나도 그 일에 대해서는 어떤 정보도 주워 모을 수가 없었다.\n\n내 처지에 한 가지 변화가 있었는데, 그것은 당장의 거북함을 크게 덜어 주기는 했으나, 내가 그것을 곰곰이 따져 볼 줄 아는 아이였다면 오히려 앞날에 대해 더 마음을 졸이게 만들었을지도 모른다. 그것은 이러했다. 나에게 씌워졌던 속박이 깡그리 사라진 것이다. 거실에서 따분한 자리를 지키도록 요구받기는커녕, 여러 번은 내가 거기 앉으려 하면 머드스톤 양이 눈살을 찌푸리며 나가라고 손짓했다. 패거티와 어울리지 말라는 경고를 받기는커녕, 머드스톤 씨 곁에만 있지 않으면 나를 찾거나 내 안부를 묻는 사람도 없었다. 처음에는 그가 다시 내 교육을 손에 쥐거나, 머드스톤 양이 거기에 매달릴까 봐 날마다 겁이 났다. 그러나 곧 그런 두려움은 근거가 없으며, 내가 예상해야 할 것은 오로지 방치뿐이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n이런 사실을 깨달은 것이 그때 나에게 큰 고통을 주었다고는 생각하지 않는다. 나는 아직 어머니의 죽음이라는 충격에 어지러웠고, 거기에 딸린 모든 일에 대해 일종의 멍한 상태였다. 사실, 가끔 한가할 때면 이런 가능성들을 이리저리 그려 보던 기억이 난다. 내가 더는 무엇도 배우지 못하고, 누구의 보살핌도 받지 못한 채, 후줄근하고 우울한 사내로 자라나, 마을 언저리에서 빈둥빈둥 게으른 삶을 흘려보내는 모습. 그런가 하면, 이야기 속 주인공처럼 어딘가로 떠나 내 운을 찾아 나섬으로써 이 그림을 떨쳐 버릴 수 있을지 가늠해 보기도 했다. 그러나 그것들은 덧없는 환영이었고, 마치 내 방 벽에 희미하게 그려지거나 적혀 있기라도 한 듯 내가 가끔 앉아서 바라보던 백일몽이었으며, 그것들이 녹아 사라지면 벽은 다시 텅 비곤 했다.\n\n\"패거티.\" 어느 저녁, 부엌 난롯불에 손을 쬐다가 나는 생각에 잠긴 채 속삭였다. \"머드스톤 씨는 예전보다 날 덜 좋아해. 원래 날 별로 안 좋아했지만, 패거티, 지금은 할 수만 있으면 날 보기조차 싫어해.\"\n\n\"슬픔 때문인지도 모르지.\" 패거티가 내 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 패거티, 나도 슬퍼. 그게 그분의 슬픔 때문이라고 믿는다면, 난 그런 건 아예 신경도 안 쓸 거야. 하지만 그게 아니야. 아, 아니야, 그건 아니야.\"\n\n\"그게 아닌 걸 어떻게 알아?\" 잠시 가만히 있다가 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그분의 슬픔은 또 다른, 아주 별개의 거야. 그분은 지금 이 순간 머드스톤 양이랑 난롯가에 앉아서 슬퍼하고 있겠지. 하지만 내가 들어가면, 패거티, 그분은 슬픔 말고 또 다른 게 될걸.\"\n\n\"뭐가 되는데?\" 패거티가 물었다.\n\n\"화가 나는 거지.\" 나는 나도 모르게 그의 어두운 찌푸림을 흉내 내며 대답했다. \"그분이 그저 슬프기만 하다면, 날 저런 눈으로 보진 않을 거야. 난 그냥 슬프기만 한데, 그래서 마음이 더 다정해지거든.\"\n\n패거티는 한동안 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 나는 그녀처럼 말없이 손을 쬐었다.\n\n\"데이비.\" 마침내 그녀가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"응, 패거티?\" \"얘야, 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 방법으로 애써 봤단다. 다시 말해서 있는 방법이란 방법, 없는 방법까지 죄다 동원해서, 여기 블런더스톤에서 마땅한 일자리를 구해 보려 했어. 하지만 그런 건 없구나, 우리 아가.\"\n\n\"그럼 어쩔 셈이야, 패거티.\" 나는 애틋하게 물었다. \"운을 찾아 떠날 작정이야?\"\n\n\"아무래도 야머스로 가야 할 것 같다.\" 패거티가 대답했다. \"거기서 사는 거지.\"\n\n\"더 멀리 갈 수도 있었으니까.\" 나는 조금 환해지며 말했다. \"그랬으면 영영 잃어버린 거나 마찬가지였을 거야. 거기선 가끔 널 볼 수 있겠네, 정겨운 우리 패거티. 세상 저쪽 끝까지 가는 건 아니지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"천만에, 하느님 덕분이지!\" 패거티가 잔뜩 신이 나서 외쳤다. \"네가 여기 있는 한, 우리 아가, 난 평생 매주 한 번씩 널 보러 건너올 거야. 살아 있는 동안 일주일에 하루씩, 꼭!\"\n\n이 약속에 나는 마음에서 큰 짐 하나가 내려가는 걸 느꼈다. 하지만 그게 다가 아니었으니, 패거티가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"데이비, 있잖니, 난 우선 우리 오라버니한테로 가서 또 두 주쯤 머물 거야. 둘레를 좀 살펴보고, 다시 예전의 나로 돌아갈 짬을 가질 때까지만. 그런데 내가 생각해 봤는데 말이야, 지금 여기서 널 필요로 하지 않으니, 어쩌면 너도 나랑 같이 가도록 해 줄지 몰라.\"\n\n패거티만 빼고 내 둘레의 모든 사람과 전혀 다른 관계에 놓이는 일을 제외하고, 그 무렵 나에게 기쁨을 줄 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 다른 무엇보다도 바로 이 계획이었을 것이다. 그 정직한 얼굴들에 다시 둘러싸여 환한 환영을 받는 일, 종이 울리고 돌멩이가 물에 떨어지고 어슴푸레한 배들이 안개를 뚫고 나오던 그 달콤한 일요일 아침의 평온을 되살리는 일, 어린 에밀리와 위아래로 거닐며 내 시름을 털어놓고 바닷가의 조개와 조약돌에서 그 시름을 막아 줄 부적을 찾는 일을 떠올리니, 마음에 고요가 깃들었다. 물론 다음 순간 머드스톤 양이 허락해 줄까 하는 의심에 그 고요가 흔들리기는 했다. 하지만 그것조차 곧 가라앉았으니, 우리가 아직 이야기를 나누고 있을 때 그녀가 저녁 무렵 저장실을 더듬어 보려고 나왔고, 패거티가 나를 놀라게 할 만큼 대담하게 그 자리에서 이 화제를 꺼냈기 때문이다.\n\n\"그 애는 거기 가면 빈둥거릴 거야.\" 머드스톤 양이 피클 단지 속을 들여다보며 말했다. \"그리고 게으름은 모든 악의 뿌리지. 하지만 분명, 그 애는 여기서도 빈둥거릴 테니까. 내가 보기엔 어디서든 마찬가지야.\"\n\n패거티가 발끈한 대답을 준비하고 있는 게 보였다. 그러나 그녀는 나를 위해 그것을 꿀꺽 삼키고 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"흥!\" 머드스톤 양이 여전히 피클에 눈을 둔 채 말했다. \"무엇보다 중요한 건, 그 무엇보다도 으뜸으로 중요한 건, 내 오라버니가 방해받거나 불편해지지 않는 거야. 그래을 거라고 해 두는 게 낫겠지.\"\n\n나는 기쁜 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않고 그녀에게 고맙다고 했다. 자칫 그것이 그녀로 하여금 승낙을 거두게 만들까 봐서였다. 그게 신중한 처사였다고 생각하지 않을 수가 없었으니, 그녀가 피클 단지 속에서 나를 쳐다보는데, 마치 그 검은 두 눈이 단지 속 내용물을 죄다 빨아들이기라도 한 듯 한층 시큼한 낯빛이었기 때문이다. 어쨌든 허락은 떨어졌고, 결코 번복되지 않았다. 한 달이 지났을 때, 패거티와 나는 떠날 채비를 마쳤다.\n\n바키스 씨가 패거티의 짐 상자를 가지러 집으로 들어왔다. 그가 정원 문을 넘어 들어오는 건 그때까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었는데, 이번에는 집 안까지 들어온 것이다. 그리고 가장 큰 상자를 어깨에 짊어지고 나가면서 나에게 어떤 눈길을 던졌는데, 만일 의미라는 것이 바키스 씨의 얼굴에 길을 찾아들 수 있다고 칠 수 있다면, 거기엔 뭔가 의미가 담겨 있다고 나는 생각했다.\n\n패거티는 그토록 오랜 세월 자기 집이었던 곳, 그리고 평생의 두 가지 깊은 정 — 어머니와 나에 대한 정 — 이 맺어진 그곳을 떠나면서 당연히 마음이 가라앉아 있었다. 그녀는 아주 이른 아침에 교회 묘지를 거닐기도 했다. 그러고는 마차에 올라 손수건을 눈에 댄 채 앉아 있었다.\n\n그녀가 그런 상태로 있는 동안, 바키스 씨는 살아 있다는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않았다. 그는 여느 때의 자리에 여느 때의 자세로, 커다란 박제 인형처럼 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 둘레를 살피며 나에게 말을 걸기 시작하자, 그는 고개를 끄덕이며 몇 번이나 히죽거렸다. 누구를 향한 것인지, 무슨 뜻인지는 도무지 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"날씨 참 좋네요, 바키스 씨!\" 나는 예의상 말했다.\n\n\"나쁘진 않지.\" 바키스 씨가 말했는데, 그는 으레 말끝을 흐리며 좀처럼 단정을 짓지 않았다.\n\n\"패거티는 이제 꽤 편안한 것 같아요, 바키스 씨.\" 나는 그를 흐뭇하게 해 주려고 한마디 했다.\n\n\"정말 그런가?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n바키스 씨는 그 말을 곰곰이 따져 보는 듯 지혜로운 표정을 짓더니, 그녀를 흘끗 보며 말했다.\n\n\"댁은 꽤 편안한가?\"\n\n패거티가 웃으면서 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 정말로, 진짜로 말이야. 그런가?\" 바키스 씨가 으르렁대듯 말하며 좌석에서 그녀 쪽으로 미끄러져 다가와 팔꿈치로 쿡 찔렀다. \"그래? 정말로 진짜로 꽤 편안한가? 그래? 응?\"\n\n이렇게 물을 때마다 바키스 씨는 그녀 쪽으로 슬금슬금 더 다가와 또 한 번씩 쿡 찔렀다. 그래서 마침내 우리는 모두 마차 왼쪽 구석으로 한데 몰리고 말았고, 나는 어찌나 짓눌렸던지 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다.\n\n패거티가 내 고통에 그의 주의를 돌리자, 바키스 씨는 곧 나에게 조금 더 자리를 내주고 차츰차츰 물러났다. 하지만 그가 대화를 새로 짜내는 수고 없이도, 말끔하고 흐뭇하고 콕 집어 자기 마음을 표현할 기막힌 방편을 찾아냈다고 여기는 듯한 것을 나는 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 한동안 그것을 떠올리며 쿡쿡 웃는 게 분명했다. 이윽고 그는 다시 패거티 쪽으로 돌아서서 \"그래도 꽤 편안한가?\" 하고 되풀이하며, 아까처럼 우리를 짓눌러 와서, 내 몸에서 숨이 거의 빠져나갈 지경이었다. 이윽고 그는 똑같은 질문과 똑같은 결과로 또 한 번 우리를 덮쳤다. 마침내 나는 그가 다가오는 걸 볼 때마다 일어나서 발판에 올라서서 바깥 경치를 보는 척했다. 그 뒤로는 아주 잘 지냈다.\n\n그는 친절하게도 오로지 우리를 위해 선술집에 들러, 구운 양고기와 맥주를 대접했다. 패거티가 한창 마시고 있을 때조차 그는 그 발작 같은 접근 중 하나를 일으켜 그녀를 거의 사레들게 했다. 그러나 우리가 여정의 끝에 가까워질수록 그는 할 일이 많아지고 사근사근할 짬이 줄어들었다. 야머스의 포장도로에 들어섰을 때, 우리는 모두 너무 흔들리고 덜컹거려서, 다른 무엇에 마음 쓸 여유가 없었던 것 같다.\n\n패거티 씨와 햄이 늘 만나던 그 자리에서 우리를 기다리고 있었다. 그들은 나와 패거티를 다정하게 맞이하고, 바키스 씨와 악수를 했는데, 바키스 씨는 모자를 뒤통수에 한껏 걸친 채, 멋쩍은 곁눈질이 얼굴에, 아니 다리까지 온통 퍼진 모습으로, 내가 보기엔 그저 멍한 행색이었다. 두 사람이 패거티의 짐 가방을 하나씩 들었고, 우리가 막 떠나려는데, 바키스 씨가 엄숙하게 집게손가락으로 나에게 신호를 보내 어느 아치 길 아래로 오라고 했다.\n\n\"있잖아.\" 바키스 씨가 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"다 잘됐어.\"\n\n나는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 무척 심오해 보이려고 애쓰며 대답했다. \"아!\"\n\n\"거기서 끝난 게 아니야.\" 바키스 씨가 은밀하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"다 잘됐다고.\"\n\n나는 또 대답했다. \"아!\"\n\n\"누가 마음 있었는지 너도 알지.\" 내 친구가 말했다. \"바키스였어, 바키스뿐이었지.\"\n\n나는 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다.\n\"다 잘됐어.\" 바키스 씨가 악수하며 말했다. \"난 네 편이야. 네가 먼저 다 잘되게 해 줬잖아. 다 잘됐어.\"\n\n유독 명료하게 말하려는 노력 속에서, 바키스 씨는 어찌나 지독히 알쏭달쏭했던지, 내가 한 시간을 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 서 있었더라도, 멈춰 버린 시계의 얼굴에서 얻는 만큼의 정보밖에 얻지 못했을 게 틀림없었으나, 패거티가 나를 불러서 데려갔다. 우리가 걸어가는데 그녀가 그가 무슨 말을 했느냐고 물었다. 나는 다 잘됐다고 했다고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"뻔뻔하기는.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그래도 난 그게 싫지 않아! 데이비, 얘야, 내가 결혼할 생각을 한다면 넌 어떨 것 같으냐?\"\n\n\"음, 그럼 패거티, 넌 그때도 지금만큼 날 좋아해 줄 거지?\" 나는 잠깐 생각한 뒤에 되물었다.\n\n거리의 행인들도, 앞서 걸어가던 그녀의 친척들도 몹시 놀랐는데, 이 착한 사람이 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 나를 끌어안고는, 변치 않는 사랑을 거듭거듭 다짐할 수밖에 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"넌 뭐라고 할지 말해 봐, 우리 아가.\" 그 일이 끝나고 우리가 다시 걸어가는데 그녀가 또 물었다.\n\n\"네가 결혼할 생각을 한다면 — 바키스 씨하고, 패거티?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"난 그게 아주 좋은 일이라고 생각할 거야. 그러면 있잖아, 패거티, 넌 늘 말과 마차가 있어서 날 보러 건너올 수 있고, 공짜로 올 수 있고, 틀림없이 올 수 있을 테니까.\"\n\n\"아이고 우리 똑똑한 것!\" 패거티가 외쳤다. \"내가 지난 한 달 내내 생각하던 게 바로 그거란다! 그래, 우리 보배. 그리고 내 생각엔 나도 통틀어 더 떳떳해질 것 같아, 알겠니. 남의집살이를 하는 것보다 내 집에서 더 즐거운 마음으로 일할 수 있는 건 말할 것도 없고. 지금 와서 내가 낯선 사람 밑에서 하녀 노릇이나 제대로 할 수 있을지도 모르겠고. 게다가 늘 우리 예쁜이가 쉬는 곳 가까이 있을 테고.\" 패거티가 골똘히 잠겨 말했다. \"보고 싶을 때 볼 수도 있고. 그리고 나도 쉬러 누울 때면, 우리 귀여운 아씨에게서 멀지 않은 데 묻힐 수 있겠지!\"\n\n우리는 둘 다 한동안 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"하지만 우리 데이비가 조금이라도 반대한다면 난 두 번 다시 생각도 안 할 거야.\" 패거티가 명랑하게 말했다. \"교회에서 서른 번씩 세 번을 결혼 예고를 했고, 주머니 속 반지가 닳도록 끼고 있다 해도 말이지.\"\n\n\"날 봐, 패거티.\" 나는 대답했다. \"내가 정말로 기뻐하는지, 진심으로 그걸 바라는지 봐!\" 정말이지 나는 온 마음을 다해 그랬다.\n\n\"그래, 내 목숨 같은 것아.\" 패거티가 나를 꼭 껴안으며 말했다. \"난 밤낮으로, 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 방법으로 생각해 봤고, 부디 옳은 방법이길 바라. 하지만 다시 한번 생각해 보고, 오라버니한테도 얘기할 거야. 그동안은 우리끼리만 알자꾸나, 데이비, 너랑 나랑. 바키스는 착하고 순박한 사람이야.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그리고 내가 그 사람한테 도리를 다하려고 애쓴다면, 내가 — 내가 꽤 편안하지 않다면, 그건 내 잘못일 거야.\" 패거티가 신나게 웃으며 말했다. 바키스 씨의 이 말을 따 온 게 어찌나 안성맞춤이었던지, 우리는 둘 다 어찌나 우스웠던지, 거듭거듭 웃었고, 패거티 씨의 오두막이 시야에 들어올 무렵에는 아주 유쾌한 기분이 되어 있었다.\n\n그곳은 예전과 똑같아 보였는데, 다만 어쩌면 내 눈에 조금 작아진 것 같았다. 그리고 거미지 부인은 문간에서 마치 그때부터 줄곧 거기 서 있었던 것처럼 기다리고 있었다. 안은 죄다 그대로였으니, 내 침실의 파란 머그잔에 든 해초까지도 그러했다. 나는 둘레를 살펴보러 헛간으로 들어갔다. 그러자 세상을 통째로 집게로 집어 보고 싶어 하는 그 똑같은 욕망에 사로잡힌 바로 그 가재며 게며 새우들이, 그 옛 구석에서 그대로 한데 엉겨 있는 모습이었다.\n\n그러나 어린 에밀리는 보이지 않았다. 그래서 나는 패거티 씨에게 그 애가 어디 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"학교에 갔어요, 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 패거티의 짐 상자를 나른 끝에 이마에 솟은 열기를 닦으며 말했다. \"집에 올 거예요,\" 그는 네덜란드제 시계를 보며 말했다. \"이제 한 이십 분에서 반 시간쯤 있으면요. 우리 다 그 애가 없어서 허전하답니다, 거참!\"\n\n거미지 부인이 끙 신음했다.\n\n\"기운 내요, 마더!\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"난 누구보다도 더 허전해요.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다. \"난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세인데, 그 애는 내 뜻을 거스르지 않던 거의 유일한 존재였거든요.\"\n\n거미지 부인은 훌쩍거리며 고개를 절레절레 흔들더니, 불 피우는 일에 매달렸다. 패거티 씨는 그녀가 그렇게 바삐 움직이는 동안 우리를 둘러보며, 손으로 입가를 가리고 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"저 노인네!\" 이로써 나는 지난번 방문 이후로 거미지 부인의 심기 상태에 아무 진전이 없었음을 옳게 짐작했다.\n\n그런데 그곳은 죄다, 적어도 응당, 예나 다름없이 즐거운 곳이었을 텐데도, 나에게는 예전 같은 인상을 주지 못했다. 나는 오히려 좀 실망스러웠다. 어쩌면 어린 에밀리가 집에 없어서였는지도 모른다. 나는 그 애가 올 길을 알고 있었고, 곧 그 애를 마중하러 오솔길을 따라 거니는 나 자신을 발견했다.\n\n이윽고 저 멀리 한 모습이 나타났고, 나는 곧 그것이 에밀리임을 알아보았는데, 그 애는 자라긴 했어도 여전히 키가 자그마한 아이였다. 그러나 그 애가 더 가까이 다가와, 그 파란 두 눈이 더 파랗게 보이고, 보조개 진 얼굴이 더 환해 보이고, 온 모습이 더 예쁘고 명랑해 보이자, 묘한 기분이 나를 사로잡아서, 나는 그 애를 못 본 척하며 멀리 무언가를 바라보는 양 지나쳐 갔다. 그런 짓을 나는 훗날 인생에서도 한 적이 있다. 내 착각이 아니라면.\n\n어린 에밀리는 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그 애는 나를 똑똑히 보았으면서도, 돌아서서 나를 부르기는커녕 웃으며 달아났다. 그 바람에 나는 그 애를 쫓아가야 했는데, 그 애가 어찌나 빨리 달리던지 내가 그 애를 붙잡았을 때는 오두막에 거의 다 와 있었다.\n\n\"어머, 너였구나, 그렇지?\" 어린 에밀리가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 누군지 너도 알았잖아, 에밀리.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 넌 누군지 몰랐어?\" 에밀리가 말했다. 나는 그 애에게 입을 맞추려 했지만, 그 애는 두 손으로 앵두 같은 입술을 가리고는, 자기는 이제 아기가 아니라고 말하더니, 어느 때보다 더 웃으면서 집으로 달아났다.\n\n그 애는 나를 놀리는 게 즐거운 듯했는데, 그건 내가 무척 의아하게 여긴 변화였다. 차탁이 차려졌고, 우리의 작은 사물함이 그 옛 자리에 놓였지만, 그 애는 내 곁에 와 앉기는커녕, 저 투덜대는 거미지 부인에게 가서 함께 있어 주었다. 그리고 패거티 씨가 왜 그러느냐고 묻자, 머리카락을 온통 얼굴에 흐트러뜨려 그것을 감추며, 그저 웃기만 할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"요 깜찍한 것!\" 패거티 씨가 큼직한 손으로 그 애를 토닥이며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요! 그럼요!\" 햄이 외쳤다. \"데이비 도련님, 그럼요!\" 그러고는 앉아서 한동안 그 애를 보며 쿡쿡 웃었는데, 감탄과 기쁨이 뒤섞인 상태라 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아올랐다.\n\n사실 어린 에밀리는 그들 모두에게 응석받이로 길러졌고, 그 누구보다도 패거티 씨가 그러했는데, 그 애는 그저 가서 그의 까칠한 구레나룻에 뺨을 갖다 대기만 하면 무슨 일이든 그를 구슬릴 수 있었다. 적어도 그 애가 그러는 걸 보았을 때 내 생각은 그러했다. 그리고 나는 패거티 씨가 전적으로 옳다고 여겼다. 하지만 그 애는 어찌나 다정하고 마음씨가 곱던지, 또 능청맞으면서도 수줍어하는 그 사랑스러운 태도를 한꺼번에 지녔던지, 나를 어느 때보다 더 사로잡았다.\n\n그 애는 마음씨도 여렸다. 차를 마신 뒤 우리가 난롯가에 둘러앉았을 때, 패거티 씨가 파이프를 물고 내가 겪은 상실을 넌지시 언급하자, 그 애의 두 눈에 눈물이 고였고, 식탁 너머로 나를 어찌나 다정하게 바라보던지, 나는 그 애에게 무척 고마운 마음이 들었다.\n\n\"아!\" 패거티 씨가 그 애의 곱슬머리를 집어 들어 물처럼 손 위로 주르륵 흘려보내며 말했다. \"여기 또 한 명의 고아가 있지요, 도련님. 그리고 여기,\" 패거티 씨가 햄의 가슴팍을 손등으로 툭 치며 말했다. \"여기 또 한 명이 있는데, 그렇게는 안 보이지만 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 패거티 씨를 후견인으로 두었다면,\" 나는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그렇게 느껴지지는 않을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"옳지, 데이비 도련님!\" 햄이 황홀해하며 외쳤다. \"만세! 옳은 말씀이오! 정말 그렇게 안 느껴질 거요! 하! 하!\" 여기서 그는 패거티 씨의 손등 펀치를 되갚았고, 어린 에밀리는 일어나서 패거티 씨에게 입을 맞췄다. \"그래, 도련님 친구는 잘 있나요?\" 패거티 씨가 나에게 물었다.\n\n\"스티어포스요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 이름이오!\" 패거티 씨가 햄을 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"우리 식하고 비슷한 거더라니까.\"\n\n\"러더포드라고 하셨잖아요.\" 햄이 웃으며 일러 주었다.\n\n\"흥!\" 패거티 씨가 받아쳤다. \"그래도 키는 러더, 키로 배를 모는 거 아니냐? 그리 멀지 않아. 그래, 그 친구는 잘 있어요, 도련님?\"\n\n\"제가 떠나올 때는 정말 잘 있었어요, 패거티 씨.\"\n\n\"그게 친구지!\" 패거티 씨가 파이프를 쭉 내밀며 말했다. \"친구 얘기라면 그게 진짜 친구지! 아이고 맙소사, 그 친구를 보는 것만으로도 눈호강이 따로 없으니!\"\n\n\"그 애는 아주 잘생겼어요, 그렇죠?\" 내가 이 칭찬에 가슴이 따뜻해지며 말했다.\n\n\"잘생겼고말고!\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다. \"그 친구는 도련님 앞에 떡 버티고 서면 — 마치 — 아니, 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠네. 어쨌든 떡 버티고 선단 말이오. 어찌나 당찬지!\"\n\n\"네! 그게 바로 그 애 성격이에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그 앤 사자처럼 용감하고, 또 얼마나 솔직한지 패거티 씨는 짐작도 못 하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래, 내가 짐작하기로,\" 패거티 씨가 파이프 연기 너머로 나를 보며 말했다. \"책 공부로 치면 그 친구는 거의 뭐든 다 앞지를 거 같은데.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 내가 기뻐하며 말했다. \"그 앤 뭐든 다 알아요. 놀랄 만큼 똑똑하죠.\"\n\n\"그게 친구지!\" 패거티 씨가 진지하게 고개를 한 번 끄덕이며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그 앤 무슨 일이든 힘들이지 않는 것 같아요.\" 내가 말했다. \"과제도 한 번 훑어보기만 하면 알아요. 그 앤 여태껏 본 적 없는 최고의 크리켓 선수예요. 체커 놀이도 원하는 만큼 말을 양보해 주고도 거뜬히 이긴다고요.\"\n\n패거티 씨가 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕였는데, \"당연히 그렇겠지\" 하는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"그 앤 말솜씨가 어찌나 좋은지,\" 내가 이어 갔다. \"누구든 다 자기편으로 끌어들일 수 있어요. 그리고 그 애가 노래하는 걸 들으시면 뭐라고 하실지 모르겠네요, 패거티 씨.\"\n\n패거티 씨가 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕였는데, \"그야 의심할 여지가 없지\" 하는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"게다가 그 앤 어찌나 너그럽고 훌륭하고 고결한 친구인지,\" 내가 가장 좋아하는 주제에 푹 빠져 말했다. \"받을 만한 칭찬을 다 해 주기조차 어려워요. 나보다 훨씬 어리고 학교에서도 한참 아래인 나를 그 애가 너그럽게 지켜 줬으니, 아무리 고마워해도 모자랄 거예요.\"\n\n내가 정말이지 신나게 떠들어 대다가, 문득 어린 에밀리의 얼굴에 눈길이 멎었다. 그 애는 식탁 위로 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채, 숨을 죽이고, 파란 두 눈을 보석처럼 반짝이고, 두 뺨에 발그레한 빛을 띠고서, 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 듣고 있었다. 그 애가 어찌나 유난히 진지하고 예뻐 보였던지, 나는 일종의 놀라움에 말을 뚝 멈췄다. 그러자 모두가 동시에 그 애를 바라보았으니, 내가 멈추자 그들이 웃으며 그 애를 쳐다보았던 것이다.\n\n\"에밀리는 나랑 똑같아서,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그 친구를 보고 싶을 거야.\"\n\n에밀리는 우리가 모두 자기를 바라보자 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 고개를 떨구었고, 얼굴은 발갛게 물들었다. 이윽고 흘러내린 곱슬머리 사이로 흘끗 올려다보고는, 우리가 여전히 자기를 보고 있는 걸 알아채자(맹세컨대, 적어도 나는 몇 시간이라도 그 애를 바라볼 수 있었을 것이다), 그 애는 달아나서 잠자리에 들 때가 거의 다 되도록 모습을 보이지 않았다.\n\n나는 배의 고물 쪽에 있는 그 옛 작은 침대에 누웠고, 바람이 예전처럼 평지를 가로질러 끙끙대며 불어왔다. 그러나 이제 나는 그 바람이 떠나간 사람들을 두고 끙끙대는 거라는 상상을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그리고 밤사이 바닷물이 차올라 배를 둥둥 떠내려가게 할지도 모른다고 생각하는 대신, 내가 지난번에 그 소리를 들은 이후로 차올라서 내 행복한 보금자리를 삼켜 버린 그 바다를 생각했다. 바람과 물소리가 내 귀에 차츰 희미해질 무렵, 내가 자라서 어린 에밀리와 결혼하게 해 달라고 비는 짧은 구절을 기도에 끼워 넣고는, 사랑스럽게 잠에 빠져들었던 기억이 난다.\n\n날들은 예전과 거의 다름없이 흘러갔다. 다만 — 이건 큰 예외였는데 — 어린 에밀리와 내가 이제는 좀처럼 바닷가를 거닐지 않는다는 점이 달랐다. 그 애는 배워야 할 과제와 바느질거리가 있어서, 매일 하루의 상당 부분을 자리를 비웠다. 하지만 사정이 그렇지 않았더라도 우리가 그 옛 거닒을 다시 누리지는 못했으리라는 걸 나는 느꼈다. 에밀리는 자유분방하고 아이다운 변덕이 가득했어도, 내가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 작은 어른이었다. 그 애는 일 년 남짓 만에 나로부터 멀찍이 떨어져 버린 듯했다. 그 애는 나를 좋아했지만, 나를 비웃고 나를 괴롭혔다. 내가 그 애를 마중하러 가면 다른 길로 몰래 집에 와 버렸고, 내가 실망해서 돌아오면 문간에서 웃고 있었다. 가장 좋은 때는 그 애가 문간에서 조용히 일감을 들고 앉아 있고, 내가 그 발치 나무 계단에 앉아 그 애에게 책을 읽어 줄 때였다. 지금 이 순간 내게는, 그 화창한 사월 오후만큼 눈부신 햇살을 본 적이 없는 것만 같고, 그 옛 배의 문간에 앉아 있던 그 햇살 같은 자그마한 모습만큼 햇살 어린 형상을 본 적이 없는 것만 같고, 그토록 아름다운 하늘과 물과, 황금빛 대기 속으로 미끄러져 떠나가던 그 영광스러운 배들을 본 적이 없는 것만 같다.\n\n우리가 도착한 바로 그 첫날 저녁, 바키스 씨가 지극히 멍하고 어색한 모습으로, 손수건에 싸맨 오렌지 꾸러미를 들고 나타났다. 그가 이 물건에 대해 어떤 언급도 하지 않았으므로, 떠날 때 깜빡 잊고 두고 간 거라고 다들 여겼다. 그런데 햄이 그것을 돌려주려고 그를 뒤쫓아 갔다가, 그게 패거티에게 주려던 거라는 소식을 가지고 돌아왔다. 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 매일 저녁 어김없이 똑같은 시각에, 늘 작은 꾸러미를 들고 나타났는데, 그것에 대해 결코 언급하지 않고, 으레 문 뒤에 놓아두고 가곤 했다. 이 애정의 선물들은 더없이 다채롭고 별난 종류였다. 그중에서 내가 기억하는 것으로는 돼지 족발 두 벌, 커다란 바늘꽂이, 사과 반 부셀쯤, 흑옥 귀고리 한 쌍, 스페인 양파 몇 개, 도미노 한 통, 새장에 든 카나리아 한 마리, 그리고 절인 돼지 다리 하나가 있었다.\n\n내가 기억하기로, 바키스 씨의 구애는 도무지 별스러운 종류였다. 그는 좀처럼 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그저 자기 마차에 앉아 있을 때와 거의 똑같은 자세로 난롯가에 앉아, 맞은편에 있는 패거티를 묵직하게 바라보곤 했다. 어느 밤, 짐작건대 사랑에 사로잡혔는지, 그는 그녀가 실에 쓰려고 두었던 밀랍 양초 토막에 달려들어, 그것을 조끼 주머니에 넣고 가져가 버렸다. 그 뒤로 그의 큰 즐거움은, 양초가 필요할 때면 주머니 안감에 들러붙어 반쯤 녹은 그것을 꺼내 보였다가, 다 쓰고 나면 다시 주머니에 넣는 것이었다. 그는 무척 즐거워하는 듯했고, 말을 해야 한다는 의무감은 전혀 느끼지 않는 듯했다. 패거티를 데리고 평지로 산책을 나갈 때조차, 내 생각에 그는 그 점에 대해 아무 불안이 없었다. 이따금 그녀에게 꽤 편안하냐고 물어보는 것으로 만족했으니까. 그리고 그가 가고 나면 패거티가 앞치마를 얼굴에 뒤집어쓰고 반 시간이나 웃곤 했던 기억이 난다. 정말이지 우리는 모두 어느 정도 재미있어했는데, 다만 저 가엾은 거미지 부인만은 예외였으니, 그녀의 구애도 꼭 이와 똑같은 식이었던 모양이라, 이런 일들을 볼 때마다 끊임없이 옛 구애가 떠올랐기 때문이다.\n\n마침내 내 방문 기간이 거의 끝나갈 무렵, 패거티와 바키스 씨가 함께 하루 휴가를 보내기로 했고, 어린 에밀리와 나도 그들과 동행하기로 했다는 발표가 나왔다. 나는 에밀리와 온종일을 보낼 기쁨을 미리 그리느라 그 전날 밤 잠을 설쳤다. 우리는 모두 아침 일찍 부산을 떨었다. 그리고 우리가 아직 아침을 먹고 있는데, 바키스 씨가 저 멀리서 사랑하는 사람을 향해 이륜마차를 몰고 나타났다.\n\n패거티는 늘 그렇듯 단정하고 차분한 상복 차림이었다. 그러나 바키스 씨는 새 파란색 외투를 입고 활짝 피어 있었는데, 재단사가 어찌나 넉넉하게 치수를 잡았던지, 그 소맷부리는 아무리 추운 날씨에도 장갑이 필요 없을 정도였고, 옷깃은 어찌나 높던지 머리카락을 머리 꼭대기로 곧추세워 밀어 올릴 지경이었다. 게다가 그의 반짝이는 단추들도 가장 큰 치수였다. 거기에 황갈색 바지와 담황색 조끼로 완성된 차림을 보니, 나는 바키스 씨가 점잖음의 화신 같다고 생각했다.\n\n우리가 모두 문밖에서 부산을 떨고 있을 때, 나는 패거티 씨가 낡은 신발 한 짝을 준비해 둔 것을 알게 되었는데, 행운을 빌며 우리 뒤로 던질 신발이었고, 그가 거미지 부인에게 그 일을 맡아 달라고 내밀었다.\n\n\"싫어요. 그건 다른 사람이 하는 게 낫겠어요, 댄얼.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다. \"난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세인데, 외롭지도 쓸쓸하지도 않은 사람들을 떠올리게 하는 건 죄다 내 뜻을 거스른답니다.\"\n\n\"이봐요, 할멈!\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다. \"어서 집어 들고 던져요.\"\n\n\"싫어요, 댄얼.\" 거미지 부인이 훌쩍거리며 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 대답했다. \"내가 덜 느꼈으면 더 할 수 있을 텐데. 당신은 나처럼 느끼지 않잖아요, 댄얼. 당신한테는 일이 거스르지도 않고, 당신도 일을 거스르지 않으니까. 당신이 직접 하는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n그런데 이때 패거티가, 그동안 부산하게 이 사람 저 사람을 오가며 모두에게 입을 맞추다가, 이제 우리가 모두 올라타 있던 마차에서(에밀리와 나는 작은 의자 두 개에 나란히 앉아 있었다) 거미지 부인이 꼭 그 일을 해야 한다고 소리쳤다. 그래서 거미지 부인이 그 일을 했다. 그런데 안타깝게도 그녀는 곧장 울음을 터뜨리고는, 자기는 짐덩어리인 줄 알고 있으니 당장 구빈원으로 데려가는 게 낫겠다고 선언하며 햄의 품에 풀썩 무너져 안기는 바람에, 우리 출발의 잔치 같은 분위기에 찬물을 끼얹고 말았다. 그건 정말이지 그럴듯한 생각이라, 햄이 실행에 옮겨도 좋았겠다고 나는 진심으로 여겼다.\n* 구빈원: 가난한 사람을 수용해 돌보던 옛 영국의 시설.\n\n그래도 우리는 휴가 나들이를 떠났다. 우리가 맨 처음 한 일은 어느 교회 앞에 멈춰 선 것이었는데, 거기서 바키스 씨는 말을 난간에 매어 두고 패거티와 함께 안으로 들어가, 어린 에밀리와 나만 마차에 남겨 두었다. 나는 그 틈을 타서 에밀리의 허리에 팔을 두르고, 이제 곧 내가 떠날 테니, 우리 둘이 하루 종일 서로 무척 다정하고 무척 행복하게 지내기로 다짐하자고 제안했다. 어린 에밀리가 동의하며 나에게 입맞춤을 허락하자, 나는 무모해져서, 결코 다른 누구도 사랑할 수 없으며, 누구든 그 애의 사랑을 넘본다면 그자의 피를 흘리게 할 각오가 되어 있다고 일러 준 기억이 난다.\n\n어린 에밀리는 그 일을 두고 얼마나 즐거워했던지! 나보다 한없이 나이 많고 슬기로운 척 새침하게 굴면서, 그 요정 같은 작은 여인은 내가 \"바보 같은 사내애\"라고 말했다. 그러고는 어찌나 사랑스럽게 웃던지, 나는 그 깎아내리는 이름으로 불린 아픔을, 그 애를 바라보는 기쁨 속에 까맣게 잊고 말았다.\n\n바키스 씨와 패거티는 교회 안에 꽤 오래 있다가 마침내 나왔고, 그러고 나서 우리는 시골 쪽으로 마차를 몰았다. 가는 길에 바키스 씨가 나를 돌아보며 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. 그런데 말이지, 그가 윙크를 할 수 있는 사람일 거라곤 그전에는 도무지 생각지 못했다.\n\n\"내가 마차에 써 붙였던 이름이 뭐였더라?\"\n\n\"클라라 패거티요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"여기에 차양이 있다면, 지금 써 붙일 이름은 뭐가 되겠어?\"\n\n\"또 클라라 패거티요?\" 내가 넘겨짚었다.\n\n\"클라라 패거티 바키스지!\" 그가 받아치더니, 마차가 다 흔들리도록 와하하 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n한마디로, 두 사람은 결혼했고, 다른 목적 없이 오직 그 일을 위해 교회에 들어갔던 것이다. 패거티는 그 일을 조용히 치르기로 마음먹었던 터라, 교회 서기가 그녀를 신랑에게 인도했고, 그 예식에는 증인도 없었다. 바키스 씨가 두 사람의 결합을 이렇게 불쑥 발표하자 그녀는 잠시 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며, 변함없는 애정의 표시로 나를 아무리 끌어안아도 모자라 했다. 하지만 그녀는 곧 본래의 모습으로 돌아와, 일이 끝나서 무척 기쁘다고 말했다.\n\n우리는 샛길에 있는 작은 여관으로 마차를 몰았는데, 우리를 기다리고 있던 그곳에서 아주 푸짐한 점심을 먹고, 더없이 흡족하게 하루를 보냈다. 패거티가 지난 십 년 동안 날마다 결혼을 했다 한들, 이보다 더 결혼을 대수롭지 않게 여길 수는 없었을 것이다. 그녀에게는 아무런 변화도 없었으니, 그녀는 여전히 예전 그대로였고, 차를 마시기 전에 어린 에밀리와 나를 데리고 산책을 나갔으며, 그동안 바키스 씨는 철학자처럼 파이프를 피우며, 짐작건대 자기 행복을 음미하며 즐거워했다. 만일 그랬다면 그것이 그의 식욕을 돋우었던 모양이다. 분명히 기억하는데, 그는 점심때 돼지고기와 채소를 잔뜩 먹고 닭 한두 마리로 마무리까지 했는데도, 차를 마실 때 또 차가운 삶은 베이컨을 먹어야 했고, 아무런 감흥 없이 어마어마한 양을 해치웠으니 말이다.\n\n그 뒤로 나는 그게 얼마나 별나고 순박하고 색다른 결혼식이었을까 하고 자주 생각하곤 했다! 우리는 어두워진 직후 다시 마차에 올라, 별들을 올려다보며 별 이야기를 나누면서 아늑하게 돌아왔다. 나는 별에 관한 으뜸가는 해설자였고, 바키스 씨의 마음을 놀라울 정도로 활짝 열어 주었다. 나는 내가 아는 걸 죄다 일러 주었는데, 내가 어쩌다 마음먹고 전해 주는 거라면 그는 무엇이든 곧이곧대로 믿었을 것이다. 그는 내 재능을 깊이 떠받들고 있어서, 바로 그 자리에서 내가 듣는 데서 자기 아내에게 내가 \"어린 로셔스\"라고 알려 주었다. 그가 한 말은 신동이라는 뜻이었지 싶다.\n* 로셔스: 고대 로마의 이름난 희극 배우. 뛰어난 재능을 가진 사람을 빗대는 말로 쓰였다.\n\n별 이야기가 다 동나자, 아니 정확히 말하면 내가 바키스 씨의 지력을 다 동내고 나자, 어린 에밀리와 나는 낡은 덮개를 망토 삼아, 남은 여정 동안 그 밑에 들어가 앉았다. 아, 나는 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했던가! 우리가 결혼해서 어딘가로 떠나 나무들 사이에서, 들판에서 살며, 결코 나이도 들지 않고, 결코 영악해지지도 않고, 영원히 아이인 채로, 손에 손을 잡고 햇살 속을, 꽃 핀 풀밭 사이를 거닐고, 밤이면 이끼에 머리를 누이고 순수하고 평화로운 단잠에 들고, 우리가 죽으면 새들이 우리를 묻어 준다면 (나는 그렇게 생각했다) 얼마나 행복할까! 현실 세계라곤 없고, 우리 순결의 빛으로 환하고, 저 멀리 별처럼 아련한 그런 어떤 그림이 가는 내내 내 마음속에 있었다. 패거티의 결혼식에 어린 에밀리와 나의 것 같은 그렇게 순진무구한 두 마음이 있었다는 걸 생각하면 나는 기쁘다. 그 소박한 행렬 속에서 사랑과 우아의 정령들이 그토록 가뿐한 모습을 띠었다는 걸 생각하면 나는 기쁘다.\n\n자, 우리는 밤이 늦지 않게 그 옛 배에 다시 다다랐다. 그리고 거기서 바키스 부부는 우리에게 작별을 고하고, 아늑하게 자기네 집으로 마차를 몰고 갔다. 그제야 나는 처음으로, 내가 패거티를 잃었다는 걸 느꼈다. 어린 에밀리의 머리를 지붕 아래 품어 주는 그 집이 아닌 다른 어떤 지붕 아래였다면, 나는 정말이지 쓰라린 마음으로 잠자리에 들었을 것이다.\n\n패거티 씨와 햄은 내 마음속에 무엇이 들었는지 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었고, 그것을 쫓아 줄 야식과 살가운 얼굴을 준비해 두고 있었다. 어린 에밀리가 와서, 그 방문 내내 단 한 번, 사물함 위 내 곁에 앉았다. 그것은 더없이 멋진 하루를 마무리하는 더없이 멋진 끝맺음이었다.\n\n만조의 밤이었다. 그래서 우리가 잠자리에 든 직후, 패거티 씨와 햄이 고기잡이를 하러 나갔다. 나는 그 외딴집에 혼자 남아 에밀리와 거미지 부인의 보호자가 된 것에 무척 용감해진 기분이었고, 그저 사자나 뱀이나 무슨 못된 괴물이라도 우리를 공격해 와서, 내가 그것을 무찌르고 영광에 휩싸였으면 하고 바랄 뿐이었다. 하지만 그날 밤 야머스 평지를 그런 게 어슬렁대고 다닐 리가 없었으므로, 나는 그 대신 할 수 있는 최선의 것을 마련했으니, 아침이 올 때까지 용들 꿈을 꾼 것이다.\n\n아침과 함께 패거티가 왔다. 그녀는 여느 때처럼 내 창문 아래에서 나를 불렀는데, 마치 마부 바키스 씨가 처음부터 끝까지 한낱 꿈이기라도 했던 듯했다. 아침을 먹고 나서 그녀는 나를 자기 집으로 데려갔는데, 그건 참으로 아름다운 작은 보금자리였다. 그 안의 온갖 세간 중에서, 거실(타일을 깐 부엌이 거실 노릇을 했다)에 있던 짙은 색 나무로 만든 어떤 낡은 책상에 나는 깊은 인상을 받았던 게 틀림없다. 그 책상은 윗부분이 뒤로 물러나며 열려서 아래로 내려뜨리면 쓰는 책상이 되었는데, 그 안에는 폭스의 『순교자 열전』 사절판 큰 책이 들어 있었다. 한마디도 기억나지 않는 이 귀한 책을 나는 곧장 발견하고 곧장 거기에 빠져들었다. 그 뒤로 그 집을 찾을 때마다 나는 어김없이 의자에 무릎을 꿇고, 이 보석이 모셔진 함을 열고, 책상 위에 두 팔을 펼치고는, 그 책을 다시 게걸스레 파먹기 시작했다. 안타깝게도 내가 주로 감화받은 건 그림이었는데, 그 그림들은 수도 많았고 온갖 음울한 공포를 그리고 있었다. 하지만 그 순교자들과 패거티의 집은 그 이후로 내 마음속에서 떼려야 뗄 수 없게 되었고, 지금도 그러하다.\n\n나는 그날 패거티 씨와 햄과 거미지 부인과 어린 에밀리에게 작별을 고했다. 그리고 패거티네 집, 지붕 아래 작은 방(머리맡 선반에는 그 악어 책이 놓여 있었다)에서 밤을 보냈는데, 그 방은 패거티 말로는 늘 내 것이 될 것이고, 늘 꼭 같은 상태로 나를 위해 간직해 두겠노라 했다.\n\n\"젊든 늙든, 데이비, 내가 살아 있고 이 집이 내 머리 위에 있는 한,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"넌 마치 내가 당장 이 순간 널 기다리고 있기라도 한 것처럼 이 방을 보게 될 거야. 내가 네 옛 작은 방을 간직하던 것처럼, 우리 아가, 날마다 이 방을 간직할 거란다. 그러니 네가 중국에 간다 해도, 떠나 있는 내내 이 방이 꼭 같이 간직되고 있다고 생각하면 돼.\"\n\n나는 사랑하는 우리 옛 유모의 진심과 한결같음을 온 마음으로 느꼈고, 할 수 있는 한 잘 고마움을 전했다. 그게 그리 잘되지는 않았는데, 그녀가 아침에 두 팔로 내 목을 감싼 채 그렇게 말했고, 나도 아침에 집으로 가는 길이었고, 결국 나는 아침에 그녀와 바키스 씨와 함께 마차를 타고 집으로 갔기 때문이다. 그들은 문 앞에서 나를 내려놓았는데, 쉽사리도 가벼이도 그러지 못했다. 그리고 마차가 패거티를 싣고 떠나가며, 나를 그 옛 느릅나무들 아래 남겨 두고, 이제 더는 사랑이나 호의로 내 얼굴을 마주 봐 줄 얼굴 하나 없는 그 집을 바라보게 하는 광경은, 나에게 참 묘한 풍경이었다.\n\n이제 나는 방치된 상태에 빠져들었는데, 그것은 지금 돌이켜 보면 연민 없이는 떠올릴 수 없는 일이다. 나는 단숨에 외톨이 처지에 빠지고 말았다. 다정한 관심에서도 동떨어지고, 또래 사내아이들 모두의 무리에서도 동떨어지고, 내 기죽은 생각 말고는 어떤 벗에서도 동떨어진 그 처지는, 내가 이 글을 쓰는 지금에도 이 종이 위에 그 어둠을 드리우는 것만 같다.\n\n여태껏 세워진 그 어떤 가장 혹독한 학교에라도 보내졌더라면, 나는 무엇이든 내주었을 것이다! 어떻게든, 어디서든, 무엇이라도 배웠더라면! 그런 희망은 나에게 동터 오지 않았다. 그들은 나를 싫어했다. 그리고 시무룩하게, 매정하게, 한결같이 나를 못 본 체했다. 내 생각에 머드스톤 씨의 형편은 그 무렵 쪼들리고 있었던 듯하다. 하지만 그건 별로 상관없는 일이다. 그는 나를 견딜 수 없어 했고, 나를 곁에서 떼어 놓음으로써, 내가 그에게 무슨 권리라도 주장할 거라는 생각을 떨쳐 버리려 했던 거라고 나는 믿는다. 그리고 그는 성공했다.\n\n나는 적극적으로 학대당하지는 않았다. 매를 맞거나 굶주리지도 않았다. 하지만 나에게 가해진 부당함에는 누그러지는 틈이라곤 없었고, 그것은 조직적이고 무덤덤한 방식으로 행해졌다. 날이면 날마다, 주가 가고 또 주가 가고, 달이 가고 또 달이 가도록, 나는 차갑게 방치되었다. 그것을 생각할 때면 나는 가끔 궁금해진다. 내가 병에라도 걸렸다면 그들은 어찌했을까. 내가 그 외로운 방에 드러누워 여느 때의 그 외톨이 신세로 병을 앓아 넘겼을지, 아니면 누구라도 나를 헤어나게 도와주었을지.\n\n머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 집에 있을 때면 나는 그들과 함께 식사를 했고, 그들이 없을 때면 혼자서 먹고 마셨다. 어느 때든 나는 집과 동네를 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채 어슬렁댔는데, 다만 그들은 내가 친구를 사귀는 것만은 시샘했다. 어쩌면 내가 친구를 사귀면 누군가에게 하소연할까 봐 그랬는지도 모른다. 그런 까닭에, 칠립 씨가 자주 나에게 놀러 오라고 청했는데도(그는 홀아비였는데, 몇 해 전에 머리 빛깔이 옅은 자그마하고 가냘픈 아내를 잃었고, 나는 그녀를 떠올릴 때면 어렴풋이 창백한 거북등무늬 고양이와 연관 짓곤 했다), 나는 그의 골방 같은 진료실에서 오후 한나절을 보내는 행복을 좀처럼 누리지 못했다. 그 진료실에서는 약전에 실린 온갖 약 냄새가 코를 찌르는 가운데 나에게 새로운 책을 읽거나, 그의 부드러운 지시에 따라 절구에 무언가를 빻곤 했던 것이다.\n* 약전: 약품의 종류·품질·제법 따위를 규정한 책.\n\n같은 까닭에, 게다가 분명 그녀가 예전부터 나를 싫어한 탓도 보태어져, 나는 패거티를 만나러 가는 것이 좀처럼 허락되지 않았다. 패거티는 약속에 충실하여, 일주일에 한 번씩 나를 보러 오거나 우리 집 근처 어딘가에서 나를 만났고, 결코 빈손으로 온 적이 없었다. 하지만 그녀의 집으로 찾아가는 게 허락되지 않아 겪은 실망은 많고도 쓰라렸다. 그래도 아주 가끔, 오랜 간격을 두고 거기 가도 좋다는 허락이 떨어지기도 했다. 그러면 나는 바키스 씨가 다소 구두쇠라는 걸, 아니 패거티가 곱게 둘러 말한 대로 \"좀 인색하다는\" 걸, 그리고 침대 밑 상자에 돈을 한 무더기 쟁여 두고는 외투랑 바지만 가득하다고 시치미를 뗀다는 걸 알게 되었다. 그의 재물은 이 궤짝 속에서 어찌나 끈질기게 점잔 빼며 숨어 있던지, 아주 적은 액수조차 꾀를 부려야만 꾀어낼 수 있었다. 그래서 패거티는 토요일마다 쓸 돈을 마련하느라 길고 정교한 계략, 그야말로 화약 음모 같은 것을 짜야 했다.\n* 화약 음모: 1605년 영국에서 가톨릭 세력이 국회의사당을 폭파하려다 발각된 사건. 치밀한 모의를 빗대는 말로 쓰인다.\n\n이 모든 시간 동안 나는 내가 받은 어떤 약속도 헛되이 흘러가고 있다는 것, 그리고 내가 철저히 방치되고 있다는 것을 너무도 절절히 느끼고 있어서, 그 옛 책들이 없었더라면 의심할 여지 없이 완전히 비참했을 것이다. 그 책들은 내 유일한 위안이었다. 나는 책들이 나에게 그러했듯 책들에게 충실했고, 몇 번이나 더인지 셀 수도 없을 만큼 그것들을 읽고 또 읽었다.\n\n이제 나는 내 인생에서, 무엇이든 기억하는 한 결코 그 기억을 잃을 수 없는 한 시기에 다가선다. 그 기억은 내가 부르지 않아도 종종 유령처럼 내 앞에 나타나, 더 행복한 시절을 따라다니며 괴롭혔다.\n\n어느 날 나는 밖에 나가서, 내 생활방식이 빚어낸 그 멍하고 골똘한 태도로 어딘가를 어슬렁거리고 있었는데, 우리 집 근처 골목 모퉁이를 돌다가 어떤 신사와 함께 걷고 있는 머드스톤 씨와 맞닥뜨렸다. 나는 당황해서 그들을 지나치려 했는데, 그 신사가 소리쳤다.\n\"이런! 브룩스 아니냐!\"\n\n\"아닌데요, 선생님. 데이비드 코퍼필드인데요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 소리 말아라. 너는 브룩스야.\" 신사가 말했다. \"너는 셰필드의 브룩스다. 그게 네 이름이지.\"\n\n이 말에 나는 그 신사를 더 유심히 살펴보았다. 그의 웃음소리도 떠올라서, 나는 그가 퀴니언 씨임을 알아보았다. 전에 — 언제인지는 상관없다, 굳이 떠올릴 필요는 없다 — 내가 머드스톤 씨와 함께 만나러 로스토프트까지 갔던 그 사람이었다.\n\n\"그래, 잘 지내냐, 어디서 교육받고 있느냐, 브룩스?\" 퀴니언 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 나를 돌려세워, 함께 걷게 했다. 나는 무어라 대답해야 할지 몰라서, 머드스톤 씨를 미심쩍게 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"이 애는 지금 집에 있소.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"어디서도 교육받고 있지 않소. 어찌해야 할지 모르겠소. 다루기 까다로운 녀석이라.\"\n\n그 옛, 그 두 겹의 눈길이 한순간 내게 머물렀다. 그러더니 그 눈길은 못마땅한 듯 다른 데로 돌려지면서, 두 눈이 찌푸림과 함께 어두워졌다.\n\n\"흠!\" 퀴니언 씨가 내 생각엔 우리 둘을 번갈아 보며 말했다. \"날씨 좋군!\"\n\n침묵이 이어졌고, 나는 어떻게 하면 그의 손에서 어깨를 빼내어 가 버릴 수 있을까 궁리하고 있었는데, 그때 그가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 아직 꽤 영리한 녀석이렷다? 응, 브룩스?\"\n\n\"그래요! 영리하기는 충분히 영리하지요.\" 머드스톤 씨가 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"그만 보내 주는 게 낫겠소. 이 녀석은 귀찮게 군다고 당신한테 고마워할 녀석이 아니오.\"\n\n이 눈치에 퀴니언 씨가 나를 놓아주었고, 나는 부리나케 집으로 향했다. 앞뜰로 접어들며 뒤를 돌아보니, 머드스톤 씨가 교회 묘지 쪽문에 기대어 있고 퀴니언 씨가 그에게 이야기를 하고 있었다. 두 사람 다 내 뒷모습을 지켜보고 있었고, 나는 그들이 내 이야기를 하고 있다는 걸 느꼈다.\n\n퀴니언 씨는 그날 밤 우리 집에서 묵었다. 이튿날 아침 식사를 마치고 내가 의자를 치우고 방을 나가려는데, 머드스톤 씨가 나를 다시 불렀다. 그러더니 그는 엄숙하게 다른 탁자로 가는데, 거기에는 그의 누이가 자기 책상 앞에 자리 잡고 앉아 있었다. 퀴니언 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 창밖을 내다보며 서 있었고, 나는 서서 그들 모두를 바라보았다.\n\n\"데이비드,\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"젊은이에게 이 세상은 행동하는 무대이지, 의기소침해서 빈둥대며 우물쭈물할 곳이 아니다.\" \"네가 하는 것처럼 말이지.\" 그의 누이가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제인 머드스톤, 부디 나한테 맡겨 주시오. 데이비드, 내 말은, 젊은이에게 이 세상은 행동하는 무대이지, 의기소침해서 빈둥대며 우물쭈물할 곳이 아니라는 거다. 너처럼 단단히 고쳐야 할 기질을 가진 어린 녀석한테는 특히 그렇지. 그런 기질에 해 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 일은, 그것을 일하는 세상의 방식에 따르도록 억지로 밀어붙이고, 휘어 꺾어 놓는 것이다.\"\n\n\"고집은 여기서 안 통하니까.\" 그의 누이가 말했다. \"필요한 건 그걸 짓뭉개는 거야. 짓뭉개야 해. 그리고 짓뭉개고말고!\"\n\n그는 나무람 반, 칭찬 반의 눈길을 그녀에게 던지고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"데이비드, 너도 알겠지만 나는 부자가 아니다. 어쨌든 이제는 알겠지. 너는 이미 상당한 교육을 받았다. 교육에는 돈이 든다. 설령 돈이 들지 않고 내가 감당할 수 있다 해도, 너를 계속 학교에 두는 게 너에게 조금도 이로울 게 없다는 게 내 의견이다. 네 앞에 놓인 것은 세상과의 한판 싸움이고, 그걸 일찍 시작할수록 좋다.\"\n\n내 생각엔 나도 이미 내 딴에는 그 싸움을 시작했다는 게 그때 머릿속을 스쳤던 것 같다. 하지만 어쨌거나 그건 지금 떠오르는 생각이다.\n\n\"너도 '경리부'라는 말을 가끔 들어 봤겠지.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"경리부요, 선생님?\" 내가 되물었다. \"포도주 거래를 하는 머드스톤 앤드 그린비 상회 말이다.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n내가 어리둥절해 보였던 모양인지, 그가 서둘러 말을 이었다.\n\n\"'경리부'라는 말이나, 그 사업이나, 창고나, 부두나, 뭐 그런 걸 들어 본 적 있겠지.\"\n\n\"그 사업 얘기는 들어 본 것 같아요, 선생님.\" 나는 그와 그의 누이의 재산에 대해 어렴풋이 알던 바를 떠올리며 말했다. \"하지만 언제인지는 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"언제든 상관없다.\" 그가 받았다. \"그 사업은 퀴니언 씨가 운영한다.\"\n\n나는 창밖을 내다보며 서 있는 그를 공손히 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"퀴니언 씨가 제안하기를, 그 사업이 다른 사내아이 몇몇에게 일자리를 주고 있는데, 같은 조건으로 너에게도 일자리를 주지 못할 까닭이 없다는 거다.\"\n\n\"이 애한테,\" 퀴니언 씨가 반쯤 몸을 돌리며 낮은 목소리로 한마디 했다. \"달리 앞길이 없으니 말이오, 머드스톤.\"\n\n머드스톤 씨는 짜증 섞인, 아니 화난 듯한 몸짓으로, 그가 한 말은 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그 조건이란, 네가 먹고 마실 것과 용돈을 스스로 충당할 만큼 벌어야 한다는 거다. 네 숙소는(내가 마련해 두었다) 내가 대 주마. 빨래 삯도 그렇고—\"\n\n\"—그건 내가 정한 액수 안에서 맞출 거고요.\" 그의 누이가 말했다.\n\n\"네 옷가지도 너 대신 챙겨 줄 거다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"당분간은 네가 스스로 옷을 마련할 수 없을 테니까. 그러니 데이비드, 너는 이제 퀴니언 씨와 함께 런던으로 가서, 네 힘으로 세상살이를 시작하는 거다.\"\n\n\"한마디로, 네 살길이 마련된 거다.\" 그의 누이가 한마디 했다. \"그러니 부디 네 본분을 다하도록 해라.\"\n\n이 발표의 목적이 나를 떼어 내려는 것임은 똑똑히 알았으나, 그것이 나를 기쁘게 했는지 두렵게 했는지는 또렷한 기억이 없다. 내 짐작에 나는 그 일을 두고 어리둥절한 상태였고, 두 마음 사이를 오락가락하느라 어느 쪽에도 가닿지 못했던 것 같다. 게다가 퀴니언 씨가 이튿날 떠날 참이었으므로, 생각을 정리할 시간도 그리 많지 않았다.\n\n자, 보라, 이튿날 나는 닳고 닳은 작은 흰 모자에 어머니를 기리는 검은 상장을 두르고, 검은 윗옷에, 빳빳하고 뻣뻣한 코르덴 바지 한 벌을 입고 있는데 — 머드스톤 양은 이제 벌어질 그 세상과의 한판 싸움에서 다리를 지킬 최고의 갑옷이 그거라고 여겼다. 보라, 그렇게 차려입고, 내 보잘것없는 전 재산을 작은 짐 가방에 담아 앞에 둔 채, (거미지 부인이라면 말했을 법한) 외롭고 쓸쓸한 아이가 되어, 퀴니언 씨를 야머스의 런던행 역마차까지 태워다 주는 우편 마차에 앉아 있는 나를! 보라, 우리 집과 교회가 멀어지며 작아지는 걸. 나무 아래 그 무덤이 가로막는 것들에 가려 지워지는 걸. 첨탑이 내 옛 놀이터에서 더는 하늘을 가리키지 않고, 하늘이 텅 비어 가는 걸!\n\n# CHAPTER 11. I BEGIN LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT, AND DON’T LIKE IT\n\n이제 나는 세상을 충분히 겪어, 무슨 일에든 크게 놀라는 능력을 거의 잃어버렸다. 그렇지만 지금도 어딘가 놀라운 것은, 내가 그토록 어린 나이에 그렇게 쉽게 내팽개쳐질 수 있었다는 사실이다. 능력이 뛰어나고 관찰력이 예리하며, 영민하고 열심이며, 섬세해서 몸이든 마음이든 쉬이 상처받는 아이였는데, 나를 위해 누구 하나 나서서 손짓 한 번 해 주지 않았다는 것이 나로서는 도무지 믿기지 않는다. 그러나 아무도 그러지 않았고, 나는 열 살 나이에 머드스톤과 그린비 상회에서 종처럼 일하는 어린 일꾼이 되었다.\n\n머드스톤과 그린비의 창고는 강가에 있었다. 블랙프라이어스 아래쪽이었다. 요즘 들어 손을 본 통에 그 자리는 달라졌지만, 그곳은 강을 향해 비탈을 따라 굽이쳐 내려가는 좁은 거리의 맨 끝 집이었고, 거리 끝에는 사람들이 배를 타는 계단이 몇 개 있었다. 그것은 제 부두를 거느린 낡고 다 쓰러져 가는 집이었는데, 물이 들어오면 부두는 물에 맞닿고, 물이 빠지면 진흙에 맞닿았으며, 말 그대로 쥐가 들끓었다. 백 년 묵은 때와 연기에 거뭇해진 판자벽 방들, 썩어 가는 마룻바닥과 계단, 지하실 아래에서 늙은 잿빛 쥐들이 찍찍대며 부스럭거리는 소리, 그곳의 더러움과 썩은 내음 — 이런 것들은 내 마음속에서 여러 해 전의 일이 아니라 바로 지금 이 순간의 일이다. 그것들이 모두 내 눈앞에 있다. 퀴니언 씨 손에 떨리는 내 손을 맡긴 채 처음으로 그들 사이에 들어섰던 그 불행한 시각에 그러했던 그대로.\n\n머드스톤과 그린비의 거래처는 온갖 부류의 사람들이었으나, 그 중 중요한 한 갈래는 어떤 정기 우편선들에 포도주와 독한 술을 대는 일이었다. 지금은 그 배들이 주로 어디로 갔는지 잊어버렸지만, 그중 몇 척은 동인도와 서인도를 오가며 항해했던 것 같다. 이 거래가 빚어내는 결과 가운데 하나가 어마어마하게 많은 빈 병이었다는 것, 그리고 어떤 어른들과 소년들이 그 병을 빛에 비추어 살피고, 흠 있는 것을 골라내고, 헹구고 씻는 일에 부려졌다는 것은 내가 안다. 빈 병이 부족할 때면 가득 찬 병에 상표를 붙이거나, 코르크 마개를 끼우거나, 마개에 봉인을 찍거나, 완성된 병을 통에 담는 일을 했다. 이 모든 일이 내 일이었고, 그 일에 부려진 소년들 가운데 나도 하나였다.\n\n나까지 셈해서 서넛이 있었다. 내 작업 자리는 창고 한 귀퉁이에 마련되었는데, 퀴니언 씨가 회계실에서 자기 걸상의 맨 아래 가로대에 올라서서, 책상 위쪽 창문 너머로 나를 들여다보고 싶을 때면 볼 수 있는 자리였다. 그토록 상서롭게 제 힘으로 인생을 시작한 첫날 아침, 정식으로 일하는 소년들 중 가장 나이 많은 아이가 이리로 불려 와 내게 일을 가르쳐 주게 되었다. 그 아이 이름은 믹 워커였고, 누덕누덕한 앞치마를 두르고 종이 모자를 쓰고 있었다. 그 아이는 제 아버지가 거룻배 사공이며, 검은 벨벳 머리 장식을 쓰고 시장 행렬에 걸어 나간다고 일러 주었다. 또 우리의 주된 동료가 될 또 한 아이를 소개해 주었는데, 그 아이를 — 내게는 — 기묘하기 짝이 없는 이름인 '밀리 포테이토'라고 불렀다. 하지만 나는 곧, 그 소년이 그런 이름으로 세례를 받은 것이 아니라, 창백하다 못해 흙빛인 그 낯빛 때문에 창고에서 그 이름을 얻은 것임을 알게 되었다. 밀리의 아버지는 나룻배 사공이었는데, 거기에 더해 소방수라는 명예까지 누리고 있어서, 어느 큰 극장에 그런 일로 고용되어 있었다. 그곳에서는 밀리의 어린 친척 하나가 — 내 생각에 그 애의 여동생인 듯한데 — 무언극에서 꼬마 도깨비 역을 맡았다.\n\n이런 무리 속으로 가라앉으며, 이제부터 날마다 함께할 이 동무들을 더 행복했던 어린 시절의 벗들과 — 스티어포스나 트래들스, 그 밖의 다른 소년들은 말할 것도 없고 — 견주어 보던 그때, 학식 있고 명망 높은 어른으로 자라리라는 내 희망이 가슴속에서 짓이겨지는 것을 느끼던 그 순간, 내 영혼의 은밀한 고통은 어떤 말로도 다 담을 수 없다. 이제 나에게는 아무 희망도 남지 않았다는 그 느낌, 내 처지가 부끄럽다는 그 느낌, 그리고 내가 배우고 생각하고 즐거워하며 그것으로 내 상상과 분발심을 끌어올렸던 모든 것이 날이 갈수록 조금씩 내게서 떠나가, 두 번 다시 되돌아오지 않으리라 믿는 것이 어린 가슴에 얼마나 비참한 일이었던지 — 그 깊은 기억은 글로 옮길 수가 없다. 그날 오전 내내 믹 워커가 자리를 비울 때마다, 나는 병을 씻던 물에 눈물을 섞었고, 마치 내 가슴속에 흠이 하나 나 있어 금방이라도 터져 버릴 것처럼 흐느꼈다.\n\n회계실 시계가 열두 시 반을 가리키고, 다들 점심을 먹으러 갈 채비를 하던 참에, 퀴니언 씨가 회계실 창문을 톡톡 두드리며 내게 들어오라고 손짓했다. 내가 들어가 보니, 갈색 프록코트에 검은 타이츠와 신발을 신은, 다부진 중년 사내가 있었다. 머리(크고 무척이나 번들거렸다)에는 달걀에 난 털만큼도 머리카락이 없었고, 무척이나 넓은 얼굴을 나를 향해 정면으로 돌렸다. 옷차림은 추레했으나, 위풍당당한 셔츠 깃을 달고 있었다. 그는 큼직하게 녹슨 술 한 쌍이 달린, 멋을 부린 듯한 지팡이를 들고 있었고, 외알 안경을 코트 바깥에 늘어뜨리고 있었다 — 나중에 알게 되었지만 그건 장식용이었다. 그가 그 안경을 들여다보는 일은 거의 없었고, 들여다본들 아무것도 보지 못했으니까.\n\n\"이 아이입니다.\" 퀴니언 씨가 나를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"이분이,\" 그 낯선 사내가, 어딘가 거들먹거리는 듯한 가락을 목소리에 실으며, 또 형언하기 어려운 점잖은 일을 해내는 듯한 태도를 풍기며 — 그 태도에 나는 무척 감명을 받았다 — 말했다. \"코퍼필드 도련님이로군요. 건강하시지요, 도련님?\"\n\n나는 아주 잘 있다고, 그리고 그분도 그러시기를 바란다고 대답했다. 하느님은 아시겠지만, 나는 마음이 영 편치 못했다. 하지만 그 시절 내 본성은 그리 자주 푸념을 늘어놓는 것이 아니었으므로, 나는 아주 잘 있다고, 그리고 그분도 그러시기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n\"나는,\" 그 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"하느님 덕분에, 아주 잘 있소이다. 머드스톤 씨에게서 편지를 한 통 받았는데, 거기서 말하기를, 지금 비어 있는 우리 집 뒤편의 한 방을 — 요컨대 세를 놓으려는 — 요컨대,\" 그 낯선 사내가 미소를 지으며, 갑자기 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다. \"침실로 말이오 — 그 방에, 지금 내가 이렇게 알게 되어 기쁜 이 어린 신참을 받아 주었으면 하더군요.\" 그러더니 낯선 사내는 한 손을 휘저으며 턱을 셔츠 깃에 파묻었다.\n\n\"이분이 미코버 씨란다.\" 퀴니언 씨가 내게 말했다.\n\n\"에헴!\" 그 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"그게 내 이름이오.\"\n\n\"미코버 씨는,\" 퀴니언 씨가 말했다. \"머드스톤 씨와 아는 사이지. 우리 회사 주문을 수수료를 받고 따 오시는데, 따 올 수 있을 때에 한해서다. 머드스톤 씨가 네 하숙 문제로 편지를 보내셨고, 미코버 씨가 너를 하숙생으로 받아 주실 거야.\"\n\n\"내 주소는,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"시티 로드의 윈저 테라스요. 나는 — 요컨대,\" 미코버 씨가 아까와 똑같이 점잖은 태도로, 또 한 번 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다. \"내가 거기 산다오.\"\n\n나는 그에게 고개 숙여 인사했다.\n\n\"이 큰 도시에서 도련님의 발걸음이 아직 그리 멀리까지 미치지 못했으리라는 짐작에서,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"또 시티 로드 방향으로 이 현대판 바빌론의 깊은 속내를 헤쳐 나가는 데 다소 어려움을 겪을지도 모른다는 짐작에서 — 요컨대,\" 미코버 씨가 또 한 번 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다. \"도련님이 길을 잃을지도 모른다는 짐작에서, 오늘 저녁에 들러서 가장 가까운 길을 일러 드릴 수 있다면 기쁘겠소이다.\"\n\n나는 진심으로 고맙다고 인사했다. 그렇게 수고를 마다 않고 나서 주겠다니, 참으로 다정한 일이었으니까.\n\n\"그럼 몇 시쯤에,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내가 —\"\n\n\"여덟 시쯤이요.\" 퀴니언 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여덟 시쯤이라.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그럼 안녕히 계시오, 퀴니언 씨. 더는 폐를 끼치지 않겠소이다.\"\n\n그러고는 모자를 쓰고 지팡이를 옆구리에 낀 채 밖으로 나갔다. 몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세우고, 회계실에서 멀어지자 콧노래를 흥얼거리면서.\n\n그러고 나서 퀴니언 씨는 머드스톤과 그린비 창고에서 나더러 할 수 있는 한 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 달라고 정식으로 일을 맡겼는데, 보수는 주급 육 실링이었던 것 같다. 육 실링이었는지 칠 실링이었는지 분명치 않다. 이 대목이 헷갈리는 걸 보면, 처음에는 육 실링이었다가 나중에 칠 실링이 된 게 아닐까 싶다. 그는 일주일 치를 미리 내주었는데(내 짐작에 자기 주머니에서였다), 나는 거기서 육 펜스를 밀리에게 주어 그날 밤 내 트렁크를 윈저 테라스까지 날라다 달라고 했다. 트렁크가 작긴 했어도 내 힘으로 들기에는 너무 무거웠으니까. 나는 점심값으로 육 펜스를 더 썼는데, 점심은 고기 파이 한 조각에다가 근처 펌프 물 한 모금이었다. 그리고 그 끼니에 허락된 한 시간을 거리를 이리저리 거닐며 보냈다.\n\n저녁 약속 시각에 미코버 씨가 다시 나타났다. 나는 그의 점잖음에 좀 더 예를 갖추려고 손과 얼굴을 씻었고, 우리는 함께 우리 집을 — 이제는 그렇게 불러야 할 것 같다 — 향해 걸었다. 미코버 씨는 가는 길에 거리 이름과 모퉁이 집들의 생김새를 내게 새겨 주었는데, 아침에 내가 쉽게 길을 되짚어 갈 수 있도록 하기 위해서였다.\n\n윈저 테라스의 이 집에 다다랐을 때(나는 그 집이 그 사람처럼 추레하면서도, 그 사람처럼 할 수 있는 한 멋을 한껏 부려 놓았다는 것을 알아챘다), 그는 나를 미코버 부인에게 소개했다. 부인은 야위고 빛바랜, 조금도 젊지 않은 여인이었는데, 응접실에 앉아(이 층은 아예 가구 하나 없었고, 이웃들 눈을 속이려고 차양을 내려 두었다) 아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었다. 이 아기는 쌍둥이 중 하나였다. 그리고 여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 그 집을 드나든 내내, 쌍둥이 둘 다 미코버 부인에게서 떨어져 있는 모습은 거의 본 적이 없다. 둘 중 하나는 늘 무언가를 빨고 있었다.\n\n다른 아이도 둘 있었다. 네 살쯤 된 미코버 도련님과 세 살쯤 된 미코버 양이었다. 이 아이들과, 콧방귀 뀌는 버릇이 있는 가무잡잡한 젊은 여자 — 그 집 하녀였는데, 채 삼십 분도 지나기 전에 자기가 '고아'이며 근처 세인트 루크 구빈원에서 왔노라고 내게 알려 주었다 — 이렇게 해서 그 집 식구가 다 모였다. 내 방은 집 꼭대기, 뒤쪽에 있었다. 갑갑한 방이었고, 온 벽에 어떤 무늬가 본을 떠 찍혀 있었는데, 내 어린 상상에는 그것이 파란 머핀처럼 보였다. 그리고 가구는 무척 빈약했다.\n\n\"나는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없어요.\" 미코버 부인이 쌍둥이까지 안고 올라와 그 방을 보여 주고는, 숨을 돌리려고 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"결혼하기 전, 아빠 엄마와 함께 살 적에는, 하숙을 칠 필요가 생기리라고는 말이에요. 하지만 미코버 씨가 곤경에 처했으니, 사사로운 감정 따위는 다 접어 둘 수밖에요.\"\n\n나는 말했다. \"네, 부인.\"\n\n\"미코버 씨의 곤경은 지금 거의 감당할 수 없는 지경이에요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그이를 거기서 빠져나오게 할 수 있을지 어떨지, 나는 모르겠어요. 아빠 엄마와 함께 집에 살 적에는, 지금 내가 쓰는 뜻으로 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 사실 거의 알지도 못했을 거예요. 하지만 경험이 그렇게 만들지요 — 아빠가 늘 하시던 말씀처럼요.\"\n\n부인이 미코버 씨가 해병대 장교였다고 내게 말한 것인지, 아니면 내가 지어낸 상상인지는 나도 영 알 수가 없다. 다만 나는 지금 이 순간까지도, 까닭은 모르겠지만, 그가 한때 해병대에 있었다고 믿고 있다는 것만은 안다. 그는 지금 이런저런 잡다한 가게들을 도는 일종의 외판원이었으나, 그 일로는 아무것도 못 벌거나 거의 못 벌었던 듯하다.\n\n\"미코버 씨의 채권자들이 그이에게 시간을 주지 않겠다면,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그 결과는 그 사람들이 감당해야지요. 게다가 빨리 매듭지을수록 좋고요. 돌에서 피를 짜낼 수 없듯이, 지금 미코버 씨에게서는 외상값이라곤 한 푼도(소송 비용은 말할 것도 없고) 받아 낼 수가 없으니까요.\"\n\n내가 또래답지 않게 일찍 제 앞가림을 하는 것이 내 나이를 두고 미코버 부인을 헷갈리게 한 것인지, 아니면 부인이 그 일에 너무 골몰한 나머지 말 상대가 달리 아무도 없었다면 쌍둥이한테라도 그 얘기를 늘어놓았을 것인지, 나는 도무지 알 수가 없다. 어쨌든 부인은 이런 가락으로 이야기를 시작했고, 내가 부인을 알고 지낸 내내 그런 식으로 이어 갔다.\n\n가엾은 미코버 부인! 부인은 자기도 애를 써 보았노라고 말했고, 나도 부인이 정말 애를 썼으리라 믿어 의심치 않는다. 현관문 한복판에는 큼직한 놋쇠 명패가 떡하니 붙어 있었는데, 거기에는 '미코버 부인 영양 기숙 학원'이라고 새겨져 있었다. 그러나 나는 그 학원에 다닌 영양이 단 한 명이라도 있었다는 것을, 또는 영양이 한 명이라도 왔거나 오겠다고 했다는 것을, 또는 영양을 맞을 채비를 손톱만큼이라도 갖췄다는 것을 끝내 본 적이 없다. 내가 보았거나 들은 방문객이라곤 채권자들뿐이었다. 그자들은 아무 때나 찾아왔고, 그중 몇몇은 아주 사나웠다. 더러운 얼굴의 한 사내는, 내 생각에 구두장이였던 듯한데, 아침 일곱 시도 안 되어 복도에 비집고 들어와서는 계단 위쪽 미코버 씨를 향해 소리치곤 했다 — \"이봐요! 아직 안 나왔구먼, 알지요. 돈을 갚으시오, 응? 숨지 마요, 알겠소. 그건 비겁한 짓이오. 내가 당신이라면 비겁하게 굴진 않겠소. 돈을 갚으시오, 응? 그냥 갚으면 돼요, 알겠소? 이봐요!\" 이런 비아냥에 아무 대답이 없으면, 그자는 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 '사기꾼'이니 '도둑놈'이니 하는 말까지 끌어 올렸고, 그래도 통하지 않으면 때로는 길 건너편까지 가서, 미코버 씨가 있는 줄 빤히 아는 이 층 창문을 향해 고래고래 소리를 질러 댔다. 그럴 때면 미코버 씨는 슬픔과 굴욕에 사로잡혀, (한번은 부인의 비명 소리로 알게 되었듯이) 면도칼을 자기 목에 갖다 대는 시늉을 할 지경에까지 이르렀다. 그러나 그러고 나서 반 시간도 채 안 되어, 그는 유난히 공들여 구두를 닦고는, 그 어느 때보다도 점잖은 태도로 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 밖으로 나가곤 했다. 미코버 부인도 꼭 그만큼이나 회복이 빨랐다. 나는 부인이 세 시에 국세 때문에 까무러쳐 쓰러졌다가, 네 시에는 빵가루를 입힌 양고기 갈비를 먹고 따뜻한 에일을 마시는(전당포에 잡힌 찻숟가락 두 개로 마련한 돈이었다) 것을 보기도 했다. 한번은 압류 집행이 막 들어왔을 때, 우연히 여섯 시라는 이른 시각에 집에 돌아왔더니, 부인이 머리카락을 죄다 얼굴로 흐트러뜨린 채(물론 쌍둥이 하나를 안고) 난로 아래에서 까무러쳐 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 하지만 바로 그날 밤, 부엌 난롯불 앞에서 송아지 고기 커틀릿을 앞에 놓고, 자기 아빠 엄마와 그분들이 어울리던 사람들 이야기를 내게 들려주던 부인보다 더 명랑한 부인을 나는 본 적이 없다.\n\n이 집에서, 이 식구와 함께, 나는 일 없는 시간을 보냈다. 오로지 나만의 아침거리인 일 페니짜리 빵 한 덩이와 일 페니어치 우유는 내가 직접 마련했다. 밤에 돌아와 저녁으로 먹을 또 다른 작은 빵 한 덩이와 치즈 한 조각은, 어느 찬장의 어느 선반에 따로 두었다. 이렇게 하니 육 실링인지 칠 실링인지 하는 돈에 구멍이 났다, 잘 알고 있다. 게다가 나는 온종일 창고에 나가 있었고, 그 돈으로 한 주를 내내 먹고살아야 했다. 월요일 아침부터 토요일 밤까지, 나는 그 누구에게서도 어떤 종류의 충고도, 조언도, 격려도, 위로도, 도움도, 지지도 — 내가 기억하는 한 — 받지 못했다, 천국에 가기를 바라는 마음을 걸고 맹세하건대!\n\n나는 너무도 어리고 철없었으며, 내 존재 전부를 떠맡기에는 — 어찌 그러지 않을 수 있었으랴 — 너무도 자격이 모자랐던 터라, 아침에 머드스톤과 그린비로 가는 길에, 빵집 문 앞에 반값으로 내놓은 묵은 과자를 차마 그냥 지나치지 못하고, 점심값으로 아껴 둬야 할 돈을 거기다 써 버리기 일쑤였다. 그러면 점심을 거르거나, 둥근 빵 하나나 푸딩 한 조각을 샀다. 내 형편에 따라 마음이 갈리던 푸딩 가게 둘이 기억난다. 하나는 세인트 마틴 교회 가까운 골목 안에 — 교회 뒤편에 — 있었는데, 지금은 아예 사라지고 없다. 그 가게 푸딩은 건포도로 만든 것으로, 꽤 특별한 푸딩이었지만 값이 비싸서, 이 페니어치가 보통 푸딩 일 페니어치만 한 크기밖에 안 됐다. 보통 푸딩을 사기에 좋은 가게는 스트랜드에 — 그 뒤로 새로 지어 올린 어딘가에 — 있었다. 그건 묵직하고 흐물흐물한, 두툼하고 허연 푸딩으로, 큼직하고 납작한 건포도가 통째로 멀찍멀찍 박혀 있었다. 그것은 날마다 거의 내가 가는 시각쯤에 따끈하게 나왔고, 나는 그것으로 점심을 때운 날이 숱하게 많았다. 제대로, 풍족하게 점심을 먹을 때면, 나는 소시지 하나에 일 페니짜리 빵을 곁들이거나, 음식점에서 사 페니짜리 소고기 한 접시를 먹었다. 또는 우리 일터 맞은편에 있는, '라이언', 아니면 '라이언'에다가 내가 잊어버린 무슨 이름이 붙은, 초라하고 낡은 술집에서 빵과 치즈 한 접시에 맥주 한 잔을 먹기도 했다. 한번은, 아침에 집에서 가져온 빵을 책처럼 종이에 싸서 옆구리에 끼고, 드루리 레인 근처의 이름난 알라모드 소고기 집에 가서, 그 빵과 함께 먹으려고 그 별미를 '작은 접시'로 하나 주문했던 기억이 난다. 그렇게 혼자 들어온 이 기묘한 꼬마 도깨비를 종업원이 어떻게 생각했는지는 모르겠다. 하지만 내가 점심을 먹는 동안 그가 나를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 다른 종업원까지 데려와 구경시키던 모습이 지금도 눈에 선하다. 나는 그에게 팁으로 반 페니를 주었는데, 그가 그걸 받지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을.\n\n차를 마시는 시간은, 내 생각에 반 시간쯤이었다. 돈이 넉넉할 때면 나는 미리 타 놓은 커피 반 파인트에다 버터 바른 빵 한 조각을 사 먹곤 했다. 돈이 없을 때면 플리트 거리의 사슴고기 가게를 들여다보거나, 그런 시각에 코번트 가든 시장까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가 파인애플을 멀거니 바라보곤 했다. 나는 어둑한 아치들이 있어 신비로운 곳이었던 애덜파이 일대를 쏘다니기를 좋아했다. 어느 저녁, 이 아치들 가운데 하나에서 빠져나와, 강 가까이에 있는 작은 술집 앞에 다다른 내 모습이 보인다. 술집 앞에는 넓은 공터가 있었고, 거기서 석탄 짐꾼 몇이 춤을 추고 있었다. 나는 그것을 구경하려고 긴 의자에 앉았다. 그자들이 나를 어떻게 생각했을지 궁금하다!\n\n나는 어찌나 어린애였고 어찌나 작았던지, 점심으로 먹은 것을 적시려고 처음 가 보는 술집 바에 들어가 에일이나 포터 한 잔을 청하면, 그 사람들이 겁이 나서 내게 술을 안 내주는 일이 잦았다. 어느 무더운 저녁, 내가 어느 술집 바에 들어가 주인에게 이렇게 말했던 기억이 난다. \"여기서 가장 좋은 — 정말 가장 좋은 — 에일이 한 잔에 얼마요?\" 특별한 날이었으니까. 무슨 날이었는지는 모르겠다. 어쩌면 내 생일이었을지도 모른다.\n\n\"이 펜스 반이,\" 주인이 말한다. \"정품 끝내주는 에일 값이지.\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 내가 돈을 꺼내며 말한다. \"그 정품 끝내주는 걸로 한 잔 따라 주시오, 거품을 듬뿍 얹어서요.\"\n\n주인은 그 답으로, 바 너머로 나를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며, 얼굴에 야릇한 미소를 띠었다. 그러고는 맥주를 따르는 대신, 칸막이 뒤를 돌아보며 제 아내에게 뭐라고 말했다. 아내는 일감을 손에 든 채 칸막이 뒤에서 나와, 그와 함께 나를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 셋이 다 지금 내 눈앞에 서 있다. 셔츠 바람으로 바의 창틀에 기댄 주인, 작은 반쪽짜리 문 너머로 넘겨다보는 그 아내, 그리고 다소 어리둥절한 채로 칸막이 바깥에서 그들을 올려다보는 나. 그들은 내게 이런저런 질문을 많이 했다. 이름은 무엇인지, 나이는 몇인지, 어디 사는지, 무슨 일을 하는지, 어쩌다 여기 오게 됐는지. 나는 누구에게도 해가 가지 않도록, 미안하지만, 그 모든 물음에 그럴듯한 대답을 지어냈다. 그들은 내게 에일을 내주었는데, 그게 정품 끝내주는 에일은 아니었지 싶다. 그리고 주인의 아내는 바의 작은 반쪽 문을 열고 몸을 굽혀, 내 돈을 돌려주고, 입을 맞춰 주었다. 반은 감탄이 담기고 반은 가엾어하는, 그러나 모두가 여인다운, 선한 입맞춤이었다, 분명히.\n\n나는 내 형편의 궁색함이나 내 삶의 고달픔을 나도 모르게, 본의 아니게 과장하고 있지는 않다고 안다. 퀴니언 씨가 어쩌다 내게 일 실링을 주면, 나는 그것을 점심이나 차를 마시는 데 써 버렸다는 것을 안다. 추레한 아이로, 아침부터 밤까지 천한 어른들과 소년들 틈에서 일했다는 것을 안다. 변변히, 흡족히 먹지도 못한 채 거리를 어슬렁거렸다는 것을 안다. 그리고 하느님의 자비가 아니었다면, 나를 돌보는 손길이라곤 없었으니, 나는 자칫 어린 도둑이나 어린 부랑아가 되기 십상이었으리라는 것을 안다.\n\n그래도 나는 머드스톤과 그린비에서 나름의 자리를 지켰다. 퀴니언 씨가 그토록 바쁘고 무심한 사람으로서, 게다가 그토록 비정상적인 일을 다루면서도, 나를 나머지 아이들과는 다른 처지에 놓인 사람으로 대해 주려고 할 수 있는 만큼은 해 준 것도 있었거니와, 나는 그 누구에게도, 어른에게든 소년에게든, 내가 어쩌다 거기 오게 됐는지를 말한 적이 없었고, 거기 있는 것을 서글퍼하는 기색을 손톱만큼도 내비치지 않았다. 내가 남몰래 괴로워했다는 것, 그것도 사무치게 괴로워했다는 것은 나 말고는 아무도 몰랐다. 내가 얼마나 괴로웠는지는, 앞서 이미 말했듯이, 도무지 내 힘으로 다 말할 수가 없다. 그러나 나는 속내를 혼자 간직했고, 내 일을 했다. 처음부터 나는, 내가 다른 누구만큼 일을 잘 해내지 못하면, 멸시와 업신여김을 면할 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었다. 나는 곧 적어도 다른 소년들 못지않게 날래고 능숙해졌다. 그들과 아주 격의 없이 지냈으면서도, 내 행동거지와 태도는 그들의 것과는 사뭇 달라서 우리 사이에 간격이 생겼다. 그들과 어른들은 대개 나를 '꼬마 신사'나 '서퍽 도련님'이라고 불렀다. 짐 싸는 일의 십장인 그레고리라는 사내와, 짐마차꾼으로 붉은 윗옷을 입고 다니던 팁이라는 사내는 가끔 나를 '데이비드'라고 부르곤 했다. 하지만 그건 대개 우리가 아주 허물없이 굴 때, 그리고 내가 일하는 틈틈이 옛날에 읽은 책 이야기 — 내 기억에서 빠르게 사라져 가고 있던 것들 — 로 그들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 애쓴 끝에 그러했던 것 같다. 한번은 밀리 포테이토가 들고일어나 내가 그렇게 따로 대접받는 것에 반기를 들었으나, 믹 워커가 그를 단숨에 잠재워 버렸다.\n\n나는 이런 삶에서 벗어날 길은 영 가망이 없다고 여겼고, 가망 없는 것으로 아예 단념해 버렸다. 단 한 시간도 이 삶에 마음을 붙인 적이 없었고, 비참하게 불행한 것 외에 다른 적이 없었음을 나는 엄숙히 확신한다. 그러나 나는 그것을 견뎠다. 그리고 패거티에게조차, 한편으로는 그녀를 사랑하는 마음에서, 한편으로는 부끄러움에서, 그 어떤 편지에서도(우리 사이에 편지가 숱하게 오갔지만) 진실을 결코 털어놓지 않았다.\n\n미코버 씨의 곤경은 내 시름겨운 마음에 또 하나의 짐을 보탰다. 외롭고 처량한 처지였던 나는 그 식구와 무척 정이 들어, 미코버 부인의 살림 셈에 골몰하고, 미코버 씨의 빚 무게에 짓눌린 채 이리저리 거닐곤 했다. 토요일 밤은 나에게 더없는 즐거움이었는데 — 한편으로는 주머니에 육 실링인지 칠 실링인지를 넣고 집까지 걸어가며, 가게들을 들여다보고 그만한 돈이면 무엇을 살 수 있을까 헤아려 보는 것이 대단한 일이었고, 한편으로는 일찍 집에 들어갔기 때문이었다 — 그런 토요일 밤이면 미코버 부인은 가슴이 미어지는 속내를 내게 털어놓곤 했다. 일요일 아침에도 그랬는데, 그때면 나는 전날 밤 사 둔 차나 커피를 작은 면도용 단지에 타서, 느지막이 앉아 아침을 먹었다. 미코버 씨가 이런 토요일 밤 이야기를 시작할 무렵에는 격하게 흐느끼다가, 끝날 무렵에는 '잭의 기쁨은 그의 사랑스러운 낸'이라는 노래를 부르는 것은 조금도 별난 일이 아니었다. 나는 그가 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 저녁을 먹으러 돌아와서는 이제 남은 건 감옥뿐이라고 단언하더니, 잠자리에 들 무렵에는 '혹시 무슨 수가 생길 경우에' 대비해 집에 내닫이창을 다는 비용을 셈하는 — '혹시 무슨 수가 생기면'은 그가 즐겨 쓰던 말이었다 — 것을 본 적도 있다. 그리고 미코버 부인도 똑같았다.\n\n우리 사이의 나이 차가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 컸음에도, 나와 이 사람들 사이에는 묘하게 대등한 우정이 싹텄는데, 짐작건대 서로의 처지에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그러나 나는 그들이 자기네 양식에서 덜어 함께 먹고 마시자고 권할 때마다, 한 번도 거기 넘어가지 않았다(푸줏간이며 빵집과 사이가 좋지 않아 자기네 먹을 것도 넉넉지 못한 적이 많다는 것을 알았으니까). 미코버 부인이 내게 속내를 죄다 털어놓기 전까지는 그랬다. 부인은 어느 저녁 이렇게 털어놓았다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 도련님,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"나는 도련님을 남으로 여기지 않으니, 미코버 씨의 곤경이 고비에 이르렀다는 말을 망설임 없이 하겠어요.\"\n\n그 말을 들으니 무척이나 비참한 심정이 되어, 나는 더없는 연민을 담아 미코버 부인의 빨개진 눈을 바라보았다.\n\n\"네덜란드 치즈 끄트머리 한 조각만 빼면 — 그것도 어린 식구들 입에는 안 맞는 것이지만요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"정말이지 식료품 저장실에 먹을 것이라곤 부스러기 하나 없어요. 아빠 엄마와 함께 살 적에는 식료품 저장실이라는 말을 입에 달고 살았던지라, 거의 무심코 그 말을 쓰게 되네요. 내가 하려는 말은, 집에 먹을 게 아무것도 없다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"아이고 저런!\" 나는 몹시 걱정스러워 말했다.\n\n내 주머니에는 그 주 봉급에서 남은 이삼 실링이 있었는데 — 이걸 보면 우리가 이 이야기를 나눈 건 수요일 밤이었던 모양이다 — 나는 얼른 그것을 꺼내, 진심에서 우러나는 마음으로 미코버 부인에게 빌려 드릴 테니 받아 달라고 간청했다. 그러나 부인은 내게 입을 맞추고는 그것을 도로 주머니에 넣게 하면서, 그건 생각조차 할 수 없는 일이라고 대답했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 부인이 말했다. \"내 머릿속엔 그런 생각이 전혀 없어요! 하지만 도련님은 나이답지 않게 분별이 있으니, 마음만 있다면 내게 다른 종류의 도움을 줄 수 있어요. 그런 도움이라면 나도 고맙게 받겠고요.\"\n\n나는 미코버 부인에게 그게 무엇인지 말해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"식기는 내가 직접 처분했어요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"찻숟가락 여섯, 소금 숟가락 둘, 설탕 숟가락 한 쌍을, 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 남몰래, 내 손으로 직접 돈을 빌려 잡혔지요. 하지만 쌍둥이가 발목을 단단히 잡으니, 게다가 아빠 엄마에 대한 추억이 있는 나로서는, 이런 거래가 무척이나 가슴 아파요. 그래도 우리가 처분할 만한 자질구레한 물건이 아직 몇 가지 남아 있어요. 미코버 씨의 마음으로는 그걸 내다 팔 엄두가 도무지 안 날 테고, 클리킷은 — 이게 구빈원에서 온 그 여자아이였다 — 마음 씀씀이가 천박해서, 그렇게 큰일을 맡겼다가는 주제넘게 굴어 속을 썩일 거예요. 코퍼필드 도련님, 부탁드려도 될까요 —\"\n\n나는 이제 미코버 부인의 뜻을 알아채고, 얼마든지 나를 부려 달라고 청했다. 나는 바로 그날 저녁부터 들고 다니기 좋은 물건들을 처분하기 시작했고, 거의 매일 아침, 머드스톤과 그린비로 가기 전에 비슷한 심부름을 하러 나갔다.\n\n미코버 씨에게는 작은 장식장 위에 책이 몇 권 있었는데, 그는 그것을 서재라고 불렀다. 그것들이 먼저 나갔다. 나는 그 책들을 한 권 한 권, 시티 로드에 있는 어느 헌책 노점으로 날라다가 — 우리 집 가까운 그 일대는 그때 거의 다 헌책 노점과 새 가게뿐이었다 — 값나가는 대로 팔았다. 이 노점 주인은 그 뒤편 작은 집에 살았는데, 밤마다 곤드레만드레 취했고 아침마다 제 아내에게 사납게 닦달을 당했다. 내가 일찍 그곳에 갔을 때, 접이식 침대에 누운 그를 만나는 일이 한두 번이 아니었다. 이마에 베인 상처가 있거나 눈가가 시퍼렇게 멍든 채로, 전날 밤의 과음을 증언하고 있었고(술이 들어가면 시비를 거는 버릇이 있었던 듯하다), 그는 떨리는 손으로 마룻바닥에 널브러진 옷가지의 이 주머니 저 주머니를 뒤져 필요한 실링을 찾으려 애쓰는 동안, 아기를 품에 안고 신발 뒤축이 닳아빠진 그의 아내는 끊임없이 그를 들들 볶아 댔다. 어떤 때는 그가 돈을 잃어버려서, 다시 들르라고 내게 부탁하기도 했다. 하지만 그의 아내는 늘 얼마간 돈을 갖고 있었고 — 분명히 그가 취한 사이에 그의 돈을 챙겨 둔 것이리라 — 우리가 함께 계단을 내려갈 때 몰래 그 계단에서 거래를 마무리 지었다. 전당포에서도 나는 꽤 낯이 익은 손님이 되기 시작했다. 계산대 뒤를 맡은 주된 신사는 나를 무척 눈여겨보았고, 내 거래를 처리하는 동안 종종 나더러 라틴어 명사나 형용사를 변화시켜 보라거나 라틴어 동사를 활용시켜 제 귀에 들려 달라고 시키곤 했던 기억이 난다. 이 모든 일을 치르고 나면 미코버 부인은 작은 잔치를 벌였는데, 대개는 저녁상이었고, 이 끼니에는 내가 지금도 또렷이 기억하는 별스러운 맛이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n마침내 미코버 씨의 곤경이 고비에 이르렀고, 그는 어느 날 아침 일찍 붙잡혀 자치구의 킹스 벤치 감옥으로 끌려갔다. 그는 집을 나서면서 내게, 이제 자기에게서 한낮의 해가 졌노라고 말했다 — 그리고 나는 정말로 그의 가슴이 무너졌고, 내 가슴도 무너졌다고 생각했다. 그러나 나중에 들으니, 그는 한낮이 되기도 전에 신나게 구주희 놀이를 하더라는 것이었다.\n\n그가 거기 끌려간 뒤 처음 맞는 일요일, 나는 그를 보러 가서 함께 점심을 먹기로 했다. 어디어디로 가는 길을 묻고, 그 자리에 거의 다다르면 또 다른 어디어디가 보일 것이며, 그 자리에 거의 다다르면 마당이 하나 보일 텐데, 그 마당을 가로질러 곧장 가다 보면 간수가 보일 거라고 했다. 나는 이 모든 것을 그대로 따랐다. 그리고 마침내 간수가 정말로 눈에 들어왔을 때(가엾고 작은 나였다!), 로더릭 랜덤이 채무자 감옥에 있을 적에 거기 낡은 깔개 한 장만 걸친 사내가 있었다는 대목을 떠올리자, 흐려진 내 두 눈과 두근거리는 가슴 앞에서 간수의 모습이 일렁거렸다.\n\n미코버 씨가 정문 안쪽에서 나를 기다리고 있었고, 우리는 그의 방으로(꼭대기 바로 아래층이었다) 올라가 한참을 펑펑 울었다. 그가 엄숙하게 나더러 자기의 운명을 경계 삼으라고, 그리고 한 사람의 한 해 수입이 이십 파운드인데 십구 파운드 십구 실링 육 펜스를 쓰면 행복하겠지만, 이십 파운드 일 실링을 쓰면 비참해진다는 것을 명심하라고 당부하던 기억이 난다. 그러고 나서 그는 포터 값으로 내게 일 실링을 빌려, 그 액수만큼 미코버 부인에게 지급하라는 증서를 써 주더니, 손수건을 집어넣고 기운을 차렸다.\n\n우리는 녹슨 화격자에 벽돌 두 장을 양쪽으로 하나씩 끼워 둔 — 석탄이 너무 많이 타들어 가는 것을 막으려는 것이었다 — 작은 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있었다. 그러다 미코버 씨와 방을 함께 쓰는 또 다른 채무자가 빵 굽는 데서 양고기 허릿살을 가지고 들어왔는데, 그것이 우리의 공동 식사였다. 그러고 나서 나는 미코버 씨의 안부 인사를 전하고, 내가 그의 어린 친구인데, 홉킨스 대위께서 나이프와 포크를 빌려 주실 수 있겠느냐고 여쭈러, 바로 위층 방에 있는 '홉킨스 대위'에게 보내졌다.\n\n홉킨스 대위는 미코버 씨에게 안부를 전하며 나이프와 포크를 빌려 주었다. 그의 작은 방에는 무척 지저분한 부인과, 텁수룩한 머리를 한 그의 두 딸인 핼쑥한 소녀 둘이 있었다. 나는 홉킨스 대위의 빗을 빌리기보다는 홉킨스 대위의 나이프와 포크를 빌리는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. 대위 자신은 추레하기가 극에 달한 차림이었는데, 커다란 구레나룻에다, 그 밑에 다른 코트라곤 없이 아주 오래되고 낡은 갈색 외투를 걸치고 있었다. 한구석에 둘둘 말아 놓은 그의 침대가 보였고, 선반에는 그가 가진 접시며 그릇이며 항아리가 놓여 있었다. 그리고 나는 (어떻게 그랬는지는 하느님만 아시리라) 텁수룩한 머리의 두 소녀는 홉킨스 대위의 딸이지만, 그 지저분한 부인은 홉킨스 대위와 결혼한 사이가 아니라는 것을 알아챘다. 그의 문지방에 겁먹은 채 서 있던 시간은 길어야 이삼 분에 지나지 않았으나, 나는 손에 나이프와 포크가 들려 있는 것만큼이나 확실하게, 이 모든 것을 알아챈 채로 다시 내려왔다.\n\n그래도 그 점심에는 어딘가 집시 같고 흐뭇한 데가 있었다. 나는 오후 일찍 홉킨스 대위의 나이프와 포크를 돌려주고, 내 방문 이야기를 들려주어 미코버 부인을 위로하려고 집으로 돌아왔다. 부인은 내가 돌아오는 것을 보자 까무러쳤고, 그러고 나서는 우리가 그 일을 두고 이야기하는 동안 마음을 달래려고 달걀을 푼 따끈한 술 한 단지를 만들었다.\n\n집안 살림살이가 어쩌다가 가족을 위해 팔리게 됐는지, 또 누가 그것을 팔았는지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그걸 판 사람이 나는 아니다. 그러나 살림은 팔렸고, 침대와 의자 몇 개, 부엌 탁자만 빼고는 짐마차에 실려 나갔다. 우리는 이 세간을 가지고, 말하자면 텅 빈 윈저 테라스 집의 응접실 두 칸에 진을 쳤다. 미코버 부인과 아이들, 그 고아 소녀, 그리고 나, 이렇게 그 방에서 밤낮없이 지냈다. 그게 얼마 동안이었는지는 모르겠지만, 내게는 꽤 오랜 시간으로 느껴진다. 마침내 미코버 부인은 감옥으로 옮겨 가기로 마음먹었다. 미코버 씨가 이제 거기서 자기 혼자 쓸 방을 하나 마련해 두었던 것이다. 그래서 나는 집 열쇠를 집주인에게 갖다주었고, 집주인은 그것을 돌려받게 되어 무척 기뻐했다. 침대들은 내 것만 빼고 모두 킹스 벤치로 보내졌는데, 내 침대를 위해서는 그 시설 근처 담장 바깥에 작은 방 하나를 빌렸다. 미코버 식구와 나는 그 고생을 함께 겪으며 서로 너무 익숙해진 터라 떨어지기가 싫었으니, 이는 내게 무척 흡족한 일이었다. 그 고아 소녀도 마찬가지로 같은 동네에 값싼 하숙을 마련했다. 내 방은 비스듬한 지붕이 덮인 조용한 뒤채 다락방이었는데, 목재 하치장이 시원하게 내다보였다. 그 방을 차지하면서, 미코버 씨의 시련이 드디어 고비를 넘겼다는 생각을 곱씹으니, 나는 그곳이 아주 천국 같다고 여겼다.\n\n이 내내 나는 머드스톤과 그린비에서 처음과 똑같이 천한 일을, 똑같이 천한 동무들과, 그리고 까닭 없이 추락했다는 똑같은 느낌을 안고 일하고 있었다. 그러나 나에게는 다행스럽게도, 나는 단 한 명의 친구도 사귀지 않았고, 창고로 오가는 길에서나 끼니때 거리를 어슬렁거릴 때 날마다 마주치던 그 숱한 소년들 가운데 그 누구와도 말을 섞지 않았다. 나는 여전히 남몰래 불행한 삶을 살았다. 그러나 여전히 외롭게, 제 힘에만 기대어 그렇게 살았다. 내가 의식하는 변화라곤 단 두 가지뿐이다. 첫째, 내 차림이 더 추레해졌다는 것, 둘째, 이제 미코버 부부의 시름이라는 무거운 짐을 상당히 덜게 됐다는 것이다. 어떤 친척인지 친구인지가 지금의 이 고비를 넘기도록 그들을 돕기로 했고, 그들은 오랫동안 감옥 밖에서 살던 것보다 감옥 안에서 더 편히 지냈으니까. 나는 이제 어떤 약조 덕분에 그들과 함께 아침을 먹곤 했는데, 그 약조의 자세한 내용은 잊어버렸다. 아침에 몇 시에 문이 열려 내가 들어갈 수 있었는지도 잊어버렸다. 다만 나는 자주 여섯 시에 일어났다는 것, 그리고 그 짬에 내가 가장 즐겨 어슬렁거리던 곳이 낡은 런던 다리였다는 것은 안다. 거기서 나는 돌로 움푹 파인 자리 가운데 한 곳에 앉아 오가는 사람들을 바라보거나, 난간 너머로 물에 비쳐 반짝이는 햇살이 모뉴먼트 꼭대기의 황금빛 불길을 밝히는 것을 내려다보곤 했다. 그 고아 소녀가 가끔 여기서 나를 만나, 부두와 런던탑에 얽힌 놀라운 꾸민 이야기들을 들려달라 하곤 했는데, 그에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 나 자신도 그 이야기들을 믿었기를 바란다는 것뿐이다. 저녁이면 나는 감옥으로 돌아가, 미코버 씨와 함께 산책로를 오르락내리락 거닐거나, 미코버 부인과 카지노 놀이를 하며 그녀의 아빠 엄마에 대한 추억담을 듣곤 했다. 머드스톤 씨가 내가 어디 있는지 알았는지는 나도 말할 수 없다. 나는 머드스톤과 그린비에서는 그 일을 절대로 말하지 않았다.\n\n미코버 씨의 일은, 고비는 넘겼다 해도, 어떤 '증서' 때문에 무척이나 뒤얽혀 있었다. 나는 그 얘기를 숱하게 들었는데, 지금 짐작건대 그것은 예전에 채권자들과 맺은 어떤 화해 약정이었던 듯하다. 하지만 그때는 그게 무언지 도무지 알 수가 없어서, 옛날에 독일에 한때 널리 퍼졌다는 그 악마와 맺은 양피지 계약서와 그것을 헷갈렸던 기억이 난다. 마침내 이 서류가 어떻게든 처리된 모양이었고, 어쨌든 그것이 앞을 가로막던 암초이기를 그쳤다. 그리고 미코버 부인이 내게 알려 주기를, '자기 집안'에서 미코버 씨가 파산 채무자법에 따라 석방 신청을 하기로 결정했다는 것이었는데, 그러면 그가 한 육 주쯤 뒤에는 풀려나리라고 부인은 기대했다.\n\n\"그러면 그때는,\" 그 자리에 있던 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"틀림없이, 하느님이 도우시면, 세상에 한발 앞서기 시작해서, 아주 새로운 방식으로 살아갈 수 있을 게요. 만약 — 요컨대, 무슨 수가 생기기만 한다면 말이오.\"\n\n무슨 수든 생길 만한 것에 기대를 거는 셈으로, 미코버 씨가 이 무렵 채무자 투옥에 관한 법을 고쳐 달라고 간청하는 청원서를 하원 앞으로 지었던 기억이 난다. 내가 이 기억을 여기 적어 두는 것은, 이것이 내가 옛날에 읽은 책들을 달라진 내 삶에 어떻게 끼워 맞췄는지, 거리와 남녀들에게서 어떻게 나 자신을 위한 이야기를 지어냈는지, 그리고 내가 내 인생을 쓰면서 나도 모르게 펼쳐 보일 성격의 어떤 중요한 대목들이 이 내내 어떻게 조금씩 빚어지고 있었는지를 나 스스로에게 보여 주는 한 본보기이기 때문이다.\n\n감옥 안에는 클럽이 하나 있었는데, 신사인 미코버 씨는 거기서 대단한 권위자였다. 미코버 씨가 클럽에 이 청원서에 대한 자기 생각을 밝히자, 클럽은 그것을 강력히 찬성했다. 그리하여 미코버 씨는(천성이 더없이 선량한 사람이었고, 자기 일만 빼면 무슨 일에든 세상에 둘도 없이 부지런한 위인이었으며, 자기에게 아무 득도 될 수 없는 무언가에 바삐 매달릴 때보다 더 행복한 적이 없는 사람이었다) 청원서 작업에 착수하여, 그것을 지어내고, 어마어마하게 큰 종이에 정성껏 옮겨 적은 다음, 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓고, 클럽 사람 전부, 그리고 원하는 사람이라면 담장 안 누구라도 자기 방으로 올라와 거기 서명하도록 시각을 정해 두었다.\n\n이 다가오는 의식 이야기를 들었을 때, 나는 그들이 한 사람씩 차례로 들어오는 모습을 어찌나 보고 싶었던지 — 비록 그들 대부분을 이미 알고 있었고 그들도 나를 알고 있었지만 — 그 일을 위해 머드스톤과 그린비에서 한 시간 외출 허가를 받아, 한 귀퉁이에 자리를 잡았다. 그 작은 방을 꽉 채우지 않으면서 들어올 수 있는 만큼의 클럽 주요 회원들이 청원서 앞에 미코버 씨를 둘러싸고 받쳐 섰고, 내 옛 친구 홉킨스 대위는(이토록 엄숙한 자리를 빛내려고 몸을 씻은 채로) 그 곁에 바짝 자리를 잡고 서서, 내용을 모르는 모든 이에게 그것을 읽어 주었다. 그러고 나서 문이 활짝 열리고, 일반 재소자들이 긴 줄을 지어 들어오기 시작했다. 한 사람이 들어와 서명하고 나가는 동안 여럿이 바깥에서 기다렸다. 차례차례 들어오는 모든 사람에게 홉킨스 대위는 말했다. \"읽어 보셨소?\" — \"아니요.\" — \"읽어 드릴까요?\" 그자가 조금이라도 들어 볼 뜻을 살짝 비치는 어수룩함을 보이기만 하면, 홉킨스 대위는 우렁차고 낭랑한 목소리로 그것을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 읽어 주었다. 만 명이 한 사람씩 차례로 들어 주기만 한다면, 대위는 그것을 이만 번이라도 읽었을 것이다. '의회에 모인 국민의 대표 여러분', '하여 청원인들은 삼가 귀 명예로운 의회에 다가서나이다', '인자하신 폐하의 불운한 신민들' 같은 구절들을 그가 어찌나 감칠맛 나게 굴려 댔던지, 마치 그 말들이 그의 입속에서 무슨 실체라도 되고 음미할수록 맛깔스럽기라도 한 듯하던 기억이 난다. 그러는 동안 미코버 씨는 작가다운 허영심을 조금 풍기며 귀를 기울였고, 맞은편 벽의 (그리 모질지 않은 눈길로) 쇠창살 끝을 물끄러미 바라보고 있었다.\n\n내가 날마다 사우스워크와 블랙프라이어스 사이를 오가며, 끼니때면 어두컴컴한 거리들을 — 내가 알기로 그 자리의 돌은 어쩌면 지금 이 순간에도 그 시절 내 어린 발길에 닳고 있을지 모른다 — 어슬렁거리던 그때, 홉킨스 대위의 목소리가 메아리치는 가운데 내 앞에 다시 줄지어 행진하듯 지나가던 그 사람들 중에 몇이나 그 무리에 끼어 있지 않았을까 싶다! 이제 내 생각이 내 젊은 날의 그 더디고 고통스러운 시절로 돌아갈 때면, 그런 사람들을 두고 내가 지어낸 이야기들이 또렷이 기억나는 사실들 위에 얼마나 짙은 환상의 안개처럼 드리워져 있는지 새삼 놀랍다! 그 옛 자리를 다시 밟으면, 그토록 기묘한 경험들과 추레한 일들로 제 상상의 세계를 빚어 가며 내 앞에서 걸어가는 천진하고 몽상에 빠진 한 소년이 보이고, 또 그 아이가 가엾게 여겨지는 것도 조금도 이상하지 않다!\n\n# CHAPTER 12. LIKING LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT NO BETTER, I FORM A GREAT RESOLUTION\n\n때가 무르익어, 미코버 씨의 청원이 심리를 받을 만하게 되었고, 그리하여 그분은 채무자 구제법에 따라 풀려나라는 명을 받았으니, 나는 더없이 기뻤다. 채권자들도 매정하지는 않았다. 미코버 부인이 알려 준 바로는, 그토록 앙심을 품었던 구둣방 주인조차 공개된 법정에서, 자기는 그분에게 아무런 악의도 없으며 다만 돈을 받을 게 있으면 받고 싶을 뿐이라고 단언했다는 것이었다. 그 사람은 그게 인지상정이라고 생각한다고 했다.\n\n미코버 씨는 자기 사건이 끝나자 다시 킹스 벤치로 돌아갔는데, 실제로 풀려나기 전에 몇 가지 수수료를 정산하고 몇 가지 절차를 밟아야 했기 때문이다. 동아리는 그분을 열렬히 맞아들였고, 그날 저녁 그분을 기리는 합창 모임을 열었다. 그동안 미코버 부인과 나는, 잠든 식구들에 둘러싸인 채, 단둘이서 양 내장 볶음을 먹었다.\n\n\"이런 날에는 말이에요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"플립을 조금만 더 곁들여서요.\" 우리는 이미 얼마쯤 마시고 있던 참이었다. \"제 아빠와 엄마를 추모하며 한잔 올리겠어요.\"\n\n\"그분들은 돌아가셨나요, 부인?\" 나는 와인 잔으로 그 건배를 들이켠 뒤에 여쭈었다.\n\n\"제 엄마는 이 세상을 떠나셨어요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"미코버 씨의 곤경이 시작되기 전에, 아니 적어도 그게 다급해지기 전에요. 제 아빠는 미코버 씨의 보석금을 여러 번 대 주실 만큼 오래 사셨다가, 그러고는 숨을 거두셨는데, 수많은 분이 애석해했지요.\"\n\n미코버 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 마침 품에 안고 있던 쌍둥이 위로 경건한 눈물 한 방울을 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n나로서는 나에게 절실한 관심사를 여쭤보기에 이보다 더 좋은 기회를 좀처럼 바랄 수 없었으므로, 미코버 부인에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"여쭤봐도 될까요, 부인. 이제 미코버 씨가 곤경에서 벗어나 자유로워지셨으니, 부인과 미코버 씨는 앞으로 어쩌실 작정이세요? 벌써 정하셨나요?\"\n\n\"우리 친정 식구들은,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. 부인은 늘 그 '친정 식구들'이라는 말을 거드름을 잔뜩 실어서 했는데, 나는 도대체 누가 그 이름에 들어가는지 끝내 알아내지 못했다. \"우리 친정 식구들은 미코버 씨가 런던을 떠나 시골에서 그 재능을 발휘해야 한다는 의견이에요. 미코버 씨는 큰 재능을 지닌 분이거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\"\n\n나는 그 점은 정말 그렇다고 말했다.\n\n\"대단한 재능이지요.\" 미코버 부인이 거듭 말했다. \"우리 친정 식구들은, 연줄을 조금만 쓰면 그분만큼 능력 있는 사람을 위해 세관에서 무슨 자리든 마련할 수 있으리라는 의견이에요. 우리 친정 식구들의 영향력이 그 지방에 있어서, 미코버 씨가 플리머스로 내려가기를 바라고 있어요. 그분이 반드시 현장에 있어야 한다고들 생각하거든요.\"\n\n\"준비를 갖추고 계실 수 있게요?\" 내가 한마디 거들었다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요.\" 미코버 부인이 받았다. \"준비를 갖추고 계실 수 있게요 — 무슨 수가 생길 경우를 대비해서요.\"\n\n\"그러면 부인도 함께 가시나요?\"\n\n그날 있었던 일들이, 플립까지는 몰라도 쌍둥이와 어우러져 미코버 부인을 격해지게 만든 모양인지, 부인은 눈물을 흘리며 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 거예요. 미코버 씨가 처음에는 곤경을 저한테 숨겼을지도 몰라요. 하지만 그분의 낙천적인 성품 탓에, 그걸 능히 헤쳐 나가리라 기대하셨던 거겠지요. 엄마한테 물려받은 진주 목걸이와 팔찌는 그 값의 절반도 못 받고 처분해 버렸어요. 그리고 아빠가 결혼 선물로 주신 산호 세트는, 실은 한 푼도 못 받고 거저 내던지듯 팔아 버렸고요. 그래도 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 거예요. 절대로요!\" 미코버 부인은 아까보다 더 격해져서 외쳤다. \"저는 절대로 그러지 않을 거예요! 저한테 물어봐야 소용없어요!\"\n\n나는 무척이나 거북했다 — 마치 미코버 부인이, 내가 그런 짓을 하라고 자기한테 요구하기라도 한 줄로 여기는 것 같아서 말이다! — 그래서 깜짝 놀라 부인을 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"미코버 씨한테도 허물은 있어요. 그분이 앞날을 헤아릴 줄 모른다는 건 부인하지 않겠어요. 그분이 자기 형편과 빚을 둘 다 저한테 깜깜하게 숨겨 왔다는 것도 부인하지 않아요.\" 부인은 벽을 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"하지만 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n미코버 부인이 이제 목소리를 완전히 비명에 가깝게 높이는 바람에, 나는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 동아리 방으로 뛰어갔다. 그리고 긴 식탁에서 좌장 노릇을 하며 합창을 이끌고 있던 미코버 씨를 방해하고 말았다.\n\n이랴, 도빈, 워, 도빈, 이랴, 도빈, 이랴, 그리고 워어어!\n\n— 그러니까 미코버 부인의 상태가 위태롭다는 소식을 전한 것이었고, 그 말에 그분은 곧장 울음을 터뜨리더니, 먹다 만 새우의 머리와 꼬리로 조끼를 잔뜩 채운 채 나를 따라 나왔다.\n\n\"엠마, 내 천사!\" 미코버 씨가 방으로 뛰어들며 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"저는 결코 당신을 버리지 않을 거예요, 미코버!\" 부인이 부르짖었다.\n\n\"내 생명!\" 미코버 씨가 부인을 품에 안으며 말했다. \"나도 잘 알고 있소.\"\n\n\"이이는 내 아이들의 부모예요! 내 쌍둥이의 아빠라고요! 내가 사랑하는 남편이에요.\" 미코버 부인이 발버둥 치며 외쳤다. \"그러니 저는 결—코—미코버 씨를—버리지—않을 거예요!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 부인이 보여 준 이 헌신의 증거에 어찌나 깊이 감동했던지 (나로 말하자면 눈물에 흠뻑 녹아 버렸다), 격정에 사로잡혀 부인 위로 몸을 굽힌 채, 고개를 들어 마음을 가라앉히라고 애원했다. 그러나 미코버 씨가 고개를 들라고 청할수록 부인은 더더욱 허공에다 눈길을 박았고, 마음을 가다듬으라고 청할수록 더더욱 가다듬으려 들지 않았다. 그 결과 미코버 씨는 이내 너무도 북받쳐서, 자기 눈물을 부인과 나의 눈물에 한데 섞어 버렸다. 그러다 끝내, 자기가 부인을 침대에 눕히는 동안 계단에 의자를 놓고 좀 앉아 있어 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 나에게 부탁했다. 나는 그만 밤 인사를 하고 떠나려 했으나, 그분은 면회객을 알리는 종이 울리기 전에는 내가 떠나는 것을 한사코 들으려 하지 않았다. 그래서 나는 계단 창가에 앉아 있었는데, 마침내 그분이 의자를 하나 더 들고 나와 내 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"미코버 부인은 이제 좀 어떠세요, 아저씨?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"몹시 가라앉아 있어.\" 미코버 씨가 고개를 흔들며 말했다. \"반동이지. 아, 오늘은 끔찍한 하루였어! 우린 이제 외톨이야 — 모든 게 우리한테서 사라져 버렸어!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는 신음하더니, 이윽고 눈물을 흘렸다. 나는 크게 마음이 움직였고, 한편으로는 실망도 했다. 이렇게 기쁘고 오래도록 기다려 온 날이니만큼 우리가 무척 흥겨우리라 기대했기 때문이다. 그러나 미코버 부부는, 내 생각에, 묵은 곤경에 어찌나 익숙해져 있었던지, 막상 그 곤경에서 풀려났다는 것을 곱씹어 보니 도리어 완전히 난파당한 기분이 들었던 모양이다. 두 사람의 그 탄력은 죄다 사라져 버렸고, 나는 이날 밤만큼 그들이 비참해하는 모습을 그 절반이라도 본 적이 없었다. 그래서 종이 울리고 미코버 씨가 나와 함께 정문까지 걸어 나와 거기서 축복을 건네며 작별할 때, 나는 그분이 너무도 깊이 처량해 보여, 그분을 홀로 두고 떠나기가 차마 두려웠다.\n\n그러나 나로서는 너무도 뜻밖에 휩쓸려 든 그 모든 혼란과 가라앉은 마음을 헤치고서도, 나는 미코버 부부와 그 식구들이 런던을 떠나려 한다는 것과, 우리 사이의 이별이 코앞에 닥쳤다는 것을 또렷이 알아차렸다. 그날 밤 집으로 걸어 돌아오는 길에, 그리고 그 뒤 침대에 누워 잠 못 이루던 시각에, 나에게 처음으로 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐는데 — 그게 어떻게 머릿속에 들어왔는지는 모르겠으나 — 나중에 그 생각은 굳은 결심으로 모양을 갖추었다.\n\n나는 미코버 부부에게 너무도 익숙해진 데다, 그들의 곤경 속에서 무척이나 가깝게 지냈고, 그들이 없으면 완전히 의지가지없는 처지였기에, 새로 거처를 마련하느라 또 한 번 궁리를 짜내고 다시금 낯선 사람들 틈으로 들어가야 한다는 전망은, 마치 그 순간 다시 지금의 이 삶 속으로 정처 없이 내던져지는 것만 같았다 — 그것도 경험이 안겨 준, 이미 알 만큼 다 아는 그 삶에 대한 지식을 고스란히 챙겨 든 채로 말이다. 그 삶이 그토록 모질게 할퀴어 놓은 온갖 예민한 감정, 그 삶이 내 가슴속에 살려 둔 온갖 수치와 비참함은, 이 생각을 곱씹을수록 더 사무치게 다가왔다. 그리하여 나는 이런 삶은 도저히 견딜 수 없다고 단단히 마음먹었다.\n\n이 삶에서 벗어날 길은, 그 탈출이 내 손으로 직접 이루어지지 않는 한 아무런 희망이 없다는 것을, 나는 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 머드스톤 양에게서는 좀처럼 소식이 오지 않았고, 머드스톤 씨한테서는 단 한 번도 오지 않았다. 그러나 새로 짓거나 손본 옷가지 꾸러미가 두세 차례 키니언 씨 앞으로 부쳐져 나에게 왔는데, 그 꾸러미마다 쪽지가 한 장씩 들어 있었으니, 그 내용인즉슨 J. M.은 D. C.가 일에 매진하고 본분에 전적으로 헌신하기를 바란다는 것이었다 — 내가 빠르게 굳어져 가는 그 흔해 빠진 막일꾼 말고 무언가 다른 사람이 될 수 있으리라는 기미는 손톱만큼도 없었다.\n\n바로 그 이튿날, 내 마음이 막 품은 생각으로 처음 들끓고 있던 참에, 미코버 부인이 떠난다고 한 말이 빈말이 아니었음이 드러났다. 그들은 내가 사는 집에 일주일짜리 셋방을 얻었고, 그 기한이 다하면 플리머스로 출발할 예정이었다. 미코버 씨는 그날 오후 직접 회계사무소로 내려와, 자기가 떠나는 날 나를 놓아주어야 한다고 키니언 씨에게 일렀고, 나에 대해 후한 평을 해 주었는데, 나는 분명 그럴 자격이 있다고 믿는다. 그러자 키니언 씨는, 결혼한 몸으로 세놓을 방이 있던 마부 팁을 불러들여, 나를 앞으로 그 사람한테 묵게끔 정해 두었다 — 그로서는 우리 양쪽이 합의한 것으로 여길 만한 온갖 이유가 있었으니까. 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 내 결심은 이미 굳어진 뒤였다.\n\n한 지붕 아래 머무는 남은 기간 동안, 나는 미코버 부부와 더불어 저녁 시간을 보냈고, 시간이 흐를수록 우리는 서로를 더욱 아끼게 된 것 같다. 마지막 일요일, 두 분은 나를 저녁 식사에 초대했고, 우리는 돼지 등심에 사과 소스를 곁들이고 푸딩까지 먹었다. 나는 전날 밤 어린 윌킨스 미코버 — 그게 사내아이였다 — 에게 줄 작별 선물로 얼룩무늬 나무 말을 사 두었고, 어린 엠마에게 줄 인형도 사 두었다. 그리고 곧 해고될 처지였던 그 고아 하녀에게도 일 실링을 쥐여 주었다.\n\n우리는 다가오는 헤어짐 때문에 다들 마음이 애틋한 가운데에서도, 무척 즐거운 하루를 보냈다.\n\n\"저는 결코, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"미코버 씨가 곤경에 처했던 그 시절을 떠올릴 때마다, 도련님 생각을 안 할 수가 없을 거예요. 도련님의 처신은 늘 더없이 섬세하고 자상했어요. 도련님은 한 번도 그저 세 든 사람이었던 적이 없어요. 도련님은 우리 친구였어요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"코퍼필드는,\" 요즘 들어 그분은 나를 그렇게 부르는 데 익숙해져 있었다. \"동료 인간들이 먹구름 뒤에 가려 곤경에 빠졌을 때 그 처지를 함께 아파할 줄 아는 마음을 지녔고, 궁리할 줄 아는 머리를 지녔고, 또 — 한마디로, 처분할 수 있는 재산이라면 무엇이든 잘 처리할 줄 아는 두루 갖춘 능력을 지녔지.\"\n\n나는 이 칭찬에 대한 고마움을 표하며, 우리가 서로 헤어지게 되어 무척 안타깝다고 말했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 젊은 친구,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"나는 자네보다 나이가 위고, 세상살이에 어느 정도 경험이 있는 사람이고, 또 — 또 한마디로, 곤경에 관해서라면 대체로 보아 어느 정도 경험이 있는 사람이지. 지금으로선, 무슨 수가 생길 때까지는 (솔직히 나는 시시각각 그걸 고대하고 있다네) 자네한테 베풀 거라곤 충고밖에 없네. 그래도 내 충고는 새겨들을 만한 값어치가 있는데, 그건 — 한마디로, 내가 정작 그걸 한 번도 스스로 따른 적이 없어서, 그래서 이렇게 — \" 여기서 미코버 씨는, 그 순간까지 머리며 얼굴 전체가 환히 빛나며 미소 짓고 있다가, 문득 멈칫하더니 인상을 찌푸렸다. \"— 자네가 지금 보고 있는 이 비참한 가련뱅이가 된 거라네.\"\n\n\"여보, 미코버!\" 부인이 다그쳤다.\n\n\"하긴,\" 미코버 씨가 그새 또 자기를 잊고 다시 미소 지으며 받았다. \"자네가 지금 보고 있는 비참한 가련뱅이지. 내 충고는 이거야. 오늘 할 수 있는 일을 결코 내일로 미루지 말게. 미루는 버릇은 시간을 훔쳐 가는 도둑이야. 그놈의 멱살을 잡게!\"\n\n\"우리 가엾은 아빠의 좌우명이지요.\" 미코버 부인이 한마디 덧붙였다.\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"당신 아버님은 그 나름으로 참 훌륭한 분이셨고, 내가 그분을 깎아내린다는 건 천부당만부당한 일이오. 모든 점을 다 따져 보아도, 우리는 두 번 다시 — 한마디로, 그분 연세에 그만한 다리로 각반을 차고, 안경도 없이 그만한 활자를 읽어 낼 만한 사람을 아마 두 번 다시 만나지 못할 거요. 하지만 그분은 그 좌우명을 우리 결혼에 적용하셨고, 그 바람에 우리 결혼이 너무 일찍 치러진 셈이라, 그 비용을 나는 끝내 만회하지 못했지.\" 미코버 씨는 곁눈으로 미코버 부인을 흘끗 보고는 덧붙였다. \"그렇다고 그걸 후회한다는 건 아니오. 오히려 정반대요, 내 사랑.\" 그러고는 일 분쯤 자못 엄숙해졌다.\n\n\"내가 자네한테 줄 또 한 가지 충고는, 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"자네도 알고 있는 거지. 연 수입 이십 파운드에 연 지출 십구 파운드 십구 실링 육 펜스면, 결과는 행복이야. 연 수입 이십 파운드에 연 지출 이십 파운드와 육 펜스면, 결과는 비참함이지. 꽃봉오리는 시들고, 잎은 마르고, 한낮의 신은 그 황량한 풍경 위로 저물어 가고, 또 — 한마디로 자네는 영영 나가떨어지는 거야. 지금 나처럼 말일세!\"\n\n자기 본보기를 한층 더 절실하게 새겨 주려고, 미코버 씨는 더없이 흡족하고 만족스러운 표정으로 펀치 한 잔을 들이켜고는, 칼리지 혼파이프 곡을 휘파람으로 불었다.\n\n나는 이 가르침을 마음속에 잘 간직하겠노라고 그분에게 빠짐없이 다짐했는데, 실은 그럴 필요도 없었던 것이, 그때 그 말은 눈에 띌 만큼 나를 뭉클하게 했기 때문이다. 이튿날 아침 나는 역마차 사무소에서 온 식구를 만나, 가슴이 휑한 채로, 그들이 마차 바깥 뒷자리에 오르는 모습을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 도련님,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"하느님의 가호가 있기를! 저는 결코 그 모든 일을 잊을 수 없을 거예요, 알지요. 잊을 수 있다 해도 결코 잊지 않을 거고요.\"\n\n\"코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"잘 가게! 온갖 행복과 번영이 함께하기를! 만일 세월이 굽이굽이 흐르는 가운데, 망가져 버린 내 운명이 자네한테 하나의 경고가 되었노라고 스스로 믿을 수 있게 된다면, 나는 이 세상에서 남의 자리를 차지하고 산 것이 아주 헛되지만은 않았다고 느끼게 될 걸세. 무슨 수가 생기는 경우에는 (나는 꽤 그리되리라 믿네만), 내 힘으로 자네의 앞날을 밝혀 줄 수 있다면 더없이 기쁠 거야.\"\n\n미코버 부인이 아이들과 함께 마차 뒷자리에 앉아 있고, 내가 길에 서서 그들을 애틋하게 바라보고 있는 동안, 부인의 눈에서 안개 같은 것이 걷히면서, 부인은 내가 정말로 얼마나 어린것인지를 깨달았던 것 같다. 내가 그렇게 생각하는 까닭은, 부인이 더없이 새롭고 어머니다운 표정을 얼굴에 띤 채 나에게 올라오라고 손짓하더니, 내 목을 한 팔로 감싸 안고, 마치 제 친아들에게나 해 줄 법한 입맞춤을 나에게 해 주었기 때문이다. 마차가 출발하기 전에 나는 가까스로 다시 내려올 틈밖에 없었고, 그들이 흔들어 대는 손수건 때문에 그 식구들이 거의 보이지도 않았다. 마차는 순식간에 사라졌다. 그 고아 하녀와 나는 길 한복판에 서서 멍하니 서로를 바라보다가, 이윽고 악수를 하고 작별 인사를 나누었다. 그 애는, 내 짐작에, 세인트 루크 구빈원으로 돌아갔고, 나는 머드스톤과 그린비 상회에서 또 하루의 고단한 일과를 시작하러 갔다.\n\n그러나 거기서 고단한 날을 더 많이 보낼 생각은 조금도 없었다. 천만에. 나는 달아나기로 결심한 터였다. — 어떻게 해서든 시골로 내려가, 이 세상에 단 하나뿐인 친척인 고모, 베시 양을 찾아가, 내 사연을 털어놓기로 말이다. 이 절박한 생각이 어떻게 내 머릿속에 들어왔는지 모르겠다는 말은 앞서도 했다. 그러나 일단 거기 자리 잡고 나니, 그것은 그대로 머물러 있었고, 내 평생 단 한 번도 품어 본 적 없을 만큼 굳센 결심으로 단단히 굳어졌다. 그 안에 무슨 희망이 있다고 믿었던 것 같지는 도무지 않으나, 어쨌든 그것은 반드시 실행에 옮겨야 한다고 내 마음은 완전히 정해져 있었다.\n\n그 생각이 처음 떠올라 내 잠을 쫓아내 버린 그날 밤 이후로, 나는 거듭, 거듭, 그리고 백 번도 더, 내 출생에 얽힌 가엾은 어머니의 그 옛날이야기를 되짚어 보았다. 그 이야기는 옛 시절 어머니가 들려주는 것을 듣는 것이 내 큰 즐거움 가운데 하나였고, 나는 그것을 줄줄 외고 있었다. 고모는 그 이야기 속으로 걸어 들어왔다가 다시 걸어 나갔는데, 무섭고도 위압적인 인물이었다. 그러나 고모의 처신 가운데 내가 곱씹기 좋아하는, 그래서 나에게 어렴풋한 격려의 그림자를 안겨 주는 자그마한 면모가 하나 있었다. 어머니가, 고모가 자기의 고운 머리카락을 결코 무뚝뚝하지 않은 손길로 만지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다고 했던 것을 나는 잊을 수 없었다. 그것이 온전히 어머니의 착각이었을 수도 있고, 사실에 아무런 근거도 없는 것이었을 수도 있다. 그래도 나는 그것을 바탕으로, 내가 그토록 또렷이 기억하고 그토록 사랑했던 그 앳된 아름다움을 향해 마음이 누그러지는 무서운 고모의 모습을 자그맣게 하나 그려 보았는데, 그 그림이 그 이야기 전체를 부드럽게 만들어 주었다. 그것이 오랫동안 내 마음속에 자리하여, 차츰차츰 내 결심을 키워 냈으리라는 것도 충분히 있을 법한 일이다.\n\n나는 베시 양이 어디에 사는지조차 몰랐으므로, 패거티에게 긴 편지를 써서, 지나가는 말인 양 그 주소를 기억하느냐고 물었다. 어느 부인이 — 그러면서 내가 아무렇게나 지어낸 어떤 곳을 댔다 — 거기 산다는 얘기를 들었는데, 그게 같은 분인지 궁금해서 그런다는 시늉을 한 것이다. 그 편지에서 나는 패거티에게, 반 기니가 꼭 필요한 특별한 사정이 생겼다고, 갚을 수 있을 때까지 그 돈을 빌려준다면 무척 고맙겠으며, 그게 무엇에 쓸 돈인지는 나중에 알려 주겠노라고 했다.\n\n패거티의 답장은 곧 도착했고, 여느 때처럼 애정 어린 정성으로 가득했다. 그녀는 반 기니를 동봉했고 (바키스 씨의 돈궤에서 그걸 꺼내느라 그녀가 얼마나 애를 먹었을지 나는 마음이 쓰였다), 베시 양이 도버 근처에 산다고, 다만 도버 시내인지, 하이드인지, 샌드게이트인지, 폴크스턴인지는 알 수 없다고 알려 주었다. 그런데 우리 일꾼 하나가, 내가 그 지명들을 물어보자 거기가 죄다 서로 가까이 붙어 있다고 일러 주었으므로, 나는 내 목적에는 이만하면 충분하다 여기고, 그 주가 끝날 무렵에 떠나기로 마음먹었다.\n\n나는 무척 정직한 어린것인 데다, 머드스톤과 그린비 상회에 남기고 떠날 내 평판을 더럽히고 싶지 않았으므로, 토요일 밤까지는 남아 있는 것이 도리라고 여겼다. 그리고 처음 그곳에 왔을 때 일주일 치 품삯을 미리 받았던 터라, 평소 시각에 회계사무소에 들어가 봉급을 받지는 않기로 했다. 바로 그 까닭에 나는 반 기니를 빌렸던 것이니, 여비로 쓸 밑천이 한 푼도 없는 신세가 되지 않으려는 것이었다. 그리하여 토요일 밤이 와서, 우리 모두 창고에서 품삯을 받으려고 기다리고 있을 때, 늘 맨 앞 차례인 마부 팁이 먼저 들어가 자기 돈을 받자, 나는 믹 워커와 악수를 나누었다. 그리고 그의 차례가 되어 돈을 받게 되거든, 내가 짐 상자를 팁의 집으로 옮기러 갔다고 키니언 씨에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 그러고는 가루 감자에게 마지막 밤 인사를 건네고, 달아났다.\n\n내 짐 상자는 강 건너 옛 하숙집에 있었는데, 나는 우리가 술통에 못으로 박아 두던 주소 카드 한 장의 뒷면에다 그 짐의 행선지를 적어 두었다. \"데이비드 도련님, 찾으러 올 때까지 보관 요망, 도버 역마차 사무소.\" 나는 짐을 집에서 꺼낸 뒤에 거기다 붙이려고 그것을 호주머니에 챙겨 두었고, 하숙집을 향해 가면서, 그것을 운임 접수소까지 날라 줄 누군가를 두리번거리며 찾았다.\n\n블랙프라이어스 로드의 오벨리스크 근처에, 텅 빈 자그마한 당나귀 수레를 세워 둔 다리 긴 젊은이가 하나 있었는데, 내가 지나가는 길에 그와 눈이 마주쳤다. 그는 나를 두고 \"엿 같은 반 페니짜리 육 펜스어치\"라고 부르더니, \"내 얼굴을 잘 봐 두었다가 나중에 증언이라도 할 셈이냐\"고 빈정거렸다 — 틀림없이 내가 자기를 빤히 쳐다본 것을 두고 한 말이었다. 나는 걸음을 멈추고, 무례하게 굴려던 게 아니라고 그에게 분명히 말해 두었는데, 다만 그가 일감을 좋아할지 어떨지 몰라서였다.\n\n\"무슨 일감?\" 다리 긴 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n\"짐 상자 하나 옮기는 일이요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 상자?\" 다리 긴 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n나는 그게 내 거라고, 저쪽 저 거리에 있는데, 그걸 도버 역마차 사무소까지 육 펜스에 옮겨 주었으면 한다고 말했다.\n\n\"육 펜스에 합시다!\" 다리 긴 젊은이가 말하더니, 곧장 자기 수레에 올라탔는데, 그건 바퀴 달린 커다란 나무 쟁반에 지나지 않았다. 그러고는 어찌나 빠르게 덜컹덜컹 달려 나가던지, 나는 당나귀를 따라잡느라 할 수 있는 데까지 안간힘을 다 써야 했다.\n\n이 젊은이에게는 어딘가 시비조의 태도가 있었고, 특히 나에게 말을 하면서 짚을 질겅질겅 씹는 그 꼴이 나는 그리 마음에 들지 않았다. 그래도 흥정은 이미 끝났으므로, 나는 그를 내가 떠나려는 그 방까지 데리고 올라가, 둘이서 짐 상자를 들고 내려와 그의 수레에 실었다. 그런데 나는 행선지 카드를 거기다 붙이기가 꺼려졌으니, 하숙집 주인 식구 누군가가 내가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알아채고 나를 붙잡을까 봐서였다. 그래서 나는 그 젊은이에게, 킹스 벤치 감옥의 막다른 담장에 이르거든 잠깐만 멈춰 주면 고맙겠다고 말했다. 그런데 그 말이 입에서 떨어지기가 무섭게, 그는 마치 자기도, 내 짐도, 수레도, 당나귀도 죄다 똑같이 미쳐 버리기라도 한 듯이 덜컹덜컹 내달았다. 그래서 나는 약속한 그 자리에서 그를 따라잡았을 무렵에는, 뒤를 쫓으며 불러 대느라 완전히 숨이 턱에 차 있었다.\n\n너무나 얼굴이 달아오르고 흥분한 나머지, 나는 카드를 꺼내려다 호주머니에서 반 기니를 굴러떨어뜨리고 말았다. 나는 안전하게 두려고 그것을 입에 물었는데, 손이 자못 떨리기는 했어도 카드를 아주 흡족하게 막 동여맨 참에, 별안간 그 다리 긴 젊은이에게 턱밑을 사납게 후려쳐 올림을 당했고, 내 반 기니가 입에서 튕겨 나가 그의 손바닥으로 날아드는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"뭐야!\" 젊은이가 소름 끼치는 히죽한 웃음을 띠며 내 윗도리 깃을 움켜쥐고 말했다. \"이거 순경한테 끌고 갈 일이로구먼, 응? 너 지금 튀려는 거지, 그렇지? 순경한테 가자, 이 어린 도둑놈아, 순경한테 가!\"\n\n\"제발 제 돈이나 돌려주세요.\" 내가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 말했다. \"그리고 저 좀 내버려 두세요.\"\n\n\"순경한테 가!\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"그게 네 거라는 걸 순경한테 증명해 봐.\"\n\n\"제 상자랑 돈을 돌려주세요, 네?\" 나는 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다.\n\n젊은이는 여전히 \"순경한테 가!\"라고만 받았고, 마치 그 짐승과 치안판사 사이에 무슨 연이라도 있다는 듯이 나를 당나귀 쪽으로 사납게 끌고 갔다. 그러더니 마음을 바꿔 수레에 훌쩍 올라타, 내 짐 상자 위에 걸터앉고는, 곧장 순경한테 몰고 가겠다고 외치며 어느 때보다도 더 거세게 덜컹덜컹 달아났다.\n\n나는 있는 힘껏 그를 뒤쫓았으나, 소리쳐 부를 숨조차 없었고, 설령 숨이 있었더라도 이제는 감히 소리쳐 부르지도 못했을 것이다. 반 마일을 가는 동안 적어도 스무 번은 수레에 깔릴 뻔한 것을 아슬아슬하게 모면했다. 그를 놓쳤다가, 그가 보였다가, 다시 놓쳤다가, 채찍에 후려쳐지다가, 고함을 들었다가, 진창에 처박혔다가, 다시 일어섰다가, 누군가의 품으로 뛰어들었다가, 기둥을 향해 곤두박질치듯 달려들었다가 했다. 마침내 두려움과 열기로 정신이 아뜩해지고, 어쩌면 이쯤 되면 런던의 절반이 나를 붙잡으러 몰려나온 게 아닐까 의심스러워진 나는, 그 젊은이가 내 짐과 돈을 가지고 어디로든 가 버리도록 내버려 두었다. 그리고 헐떡이며 울면서도 결코 멈추지 않은 채, 도버 가도에 있다고 들은 그리니치를 향해 방향을 틀었다. 고모인 베시 양의 은신처를 향해 떠나는 마당에, 내가 세상에서 챙겨 가는 것이라곤, 내가 도착하던 그날 밤 고모를 그토록 언짢게 만들며 이 세상에 가지고 들어왔던 것보다 조금도 더 많지 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. THE SEQUEL OF MY RESOLUTION\n\n당나귀 수레를 끄는 그 젊은이를 뒤쫓는 것을 포기하고 그리니치를 향해 걸음을 옮길 무렵, 어쩌면 나는 도버까지 내내 달려갈 작정이라는 엉뚱한 생각을 품고 있었는지도 모른다. 설령 그런 마음이 있었다 해도, 그 점에 대한 흐트러진 정신은 곧 제자리를 찾았다. 켄트 로드의 어느 테라스 앞에서 내가 걸음을 멈췄기 때문이다. 그 앞에는 물웅덩이가 있었고, 한가운데에는 마른 소라 껍데기를 부는, 크고 멍청해 보이는 조각상 하나가 서 있었다. 나는 거기 문간 계단에 주저앉았다. 이미 쏟아부은 안간힘에 완전히 지치고 기진맥진해서, 내 상자와 반 기니를 잃어버린 것을 두고 울 숨조차 제대로 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n이 무렵엔 이미 날이 어두웠다. 앉아서 쉬는 동안 시계가 열 시를 치는 소리를 들었다. 그래도 다행히 여름밤이었고 날씨도 좋았다. 숨을 좀 돌리고 목에 걸린 듯 답답한 느낌을 떨쳐 내고 나서, 나는 몸을 일으켜 다시 걸음을 옮겼다. 그렇게 비참한 처지에 놓였어도 돌아갈 생각은 조금도 없었다. 설령 켄트 로드에 스위스의 눈보라가 몰아쳤다 해도 돌아갈 마음은 들지 않았을 것이다.\n\n하지만 이 세상에서 내가 가진 것이라곤 고작 일 페니 반밖에 없다는 사실은(토요일 밤에 그것이 어떻게 내 주머니에 남아 있었는지는 지금도 정말 신기하다!) 걸음을 옮기는 와중에도 어김없이 나를 괴롭혔다. 나는 하루나 이틀 뒤에 어느 울타리 밑에서 죽은 채 발견되는 내 모습을, 신문 한 귀퉁이에 실린 짤막한 소식처럼 머릿속에 그리기 시작했다. 그리고 비참한 심정으로, 그래도 할 수 있는 한 빨리 터덜터덜 걷다가, 우연히 작은 가게 하나를 지나치게 되었다. 그곳에는 신사 숙녀의 헌옷을 사들이며, 넝마와 뼈와 부엌 부산물에 가장 좋은 값을 쳐 준다고 적혀 있었다. 이 가게 주인은 셔츠 차림으로 문간에 앉아 담배를 피우고 있었다. 낮은 천장에는 외투며 바지가 잔뜩 매달려 있었고, 그것들이 무엇인지 비춰 줄 거라곤 안에서 가물거리는 촛불 두 개뿐이었으므로, 나는 그가 마치 원수들을 모조리 목매달아 놓고 흐뭇해하는, 복수심 가득한 사내처럼 보인다고 생각했다.\n\n미코버 씨 내외와 얼마 전 함께 지내며 겪은 일이, 여기서라면 잠시나마 굶주림이라는 늑대를 물리칠 수단이 생길지도 모른다는 생각을 떠올리게 했다. 나는 다음 골목길로 들어가 조끼를 벗어 겨드랑이에 단정히 말아 끼고는 다시 가게 문 앞으로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 아저씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"이걸 적당한 값에 팔려고요.\"\n\n돌로비 씨는 — 적어도 가게 문 위에 적힌 이름은 돌로비였다 — 조끼를 받아 들고, 파이프를 문기둥에 거꾸로 세워 놓더니, 가게 안으로 들어갔다. 나도 뒤를 따라 들어갔다. 그는 손가락으로 촛불 두 개의 심지를 다듬은 다음, 조끼를 계산대 위에 펼쳐 놓고 거기서 들여다보고, 다시 불빛에 비춰 들여다보더니, 마침내 입을 열었다.\n\n\"자, 이 자그마한 조끼에 얼마쯤 부르겠냐?\"\n\n\"아이고! 아저씨가 더 잘 아시잖아요.\" 나는 겸손하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"내가 사는 사람이면서 파는 사람까지 될 순 없잖냐.\" 돌로비 씨가 말했다. \"이 자그마한 조끼에 값을 매겨 봐.\"\n\n\"십팔 펜스쯤이면—?\" 나는 한참 망설인 끝에 슬며시 운을 띄웠다.\n\n돌로비 씨는 조끼를 다시 둘둘 말아 내게 돌려주었다. \"이걸 구 펜스에 사겠다고 하면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 내 식구 등쳐 먹는 짓이지.\"\n\n이렇게 흥정을 거는 방식은 영 못마땅했다. 생판 모르는 남인 나에게, 나 때문에 돌로비 씨더러 식구를 등쳐 먹어 달라고 부탁해야 하는 거북한 처지를 떠안기는 셈이었으니까. 그러나 워낙 사정이 급박했던 터라, 나는 정 그러시다면 구 펜스에 넘기겠다고 말했다. 돌로비 씨는 투덜거리지 않은 것은 아니었으나 구 펜스를 내주었다. 나는 안녕히 계시라고 인사하고는, 그 액수만큼 부자가 되고 조끼 하나만큼 가난해진 채 가게를 나섰다. 그래도 윗도리 단추를 채우고 보니 그리 큰 차이는 아니었다. 사실 나는 다음 차례로 윗도리마저 떠날 것이고, 결국 셔츠와 바지 차림으로 어떻게든 도버까지 가야 하리란 것을, 그런 꼴로라도 그곳에 닿는다면 그나마 운이 좋은 거라고 여겨야 하리란 것을 꽤 또렷이 내다보고 있었다. 하지만 내 마음이 거기에만 그리 골몰해 있던 건 아니었다. 앞에 놓인 길이 멀다는 막연한 느낌과, 당나귀 수레를 끄는 그 젊은이가 나를 모질게 대했다는 생각 말고는, 구 펜스를 주머니에 넣고 다시 길을 나설 무렵 내 어려움이 그리 절박하게 느껴지지는 않았던 것 같다.\n\n밤을 보낼 한 가지 방도가 떠올랐고, 나는 그것을 실행에 옮길 참이었다. 그건 옛 학교 뒤편 담장 너머, 예전에 건초더미가 있던 한쪽 구석에 누워 자는 것이었다. 아이들과, 내가 옛날에 이야기를 들려주던 침실이 이렇게 가까이 있다는 것이 일종의 벗이 되어 주리라는 생각이 들었다. 비록 아이들은 내가 거기 있는 줄 전혀 모를 테고, 그 침실이 나에게 잠자리를 내어 줄 리도 없었지만.\n\n하루 종일 힘겹게 걸은 터라, 마침내 기어오르듯 블랙히스의 평지에 올라섰을 무렵엔 몸이 거의 녹초가 되어 있었다. 세일럼 하우스를 찾는 데 적잖이 애를 먹었다. 하지만 끝내 찾아냈고, 구석에 있는 건초더미도 찾아냈으며, 그 곁에 몸을 뉘었다. 먼저 담장을 한 바퀴 돌며 창문들을 올려다보고, 안이 온통 캄캄하고 고요한 것을 확인한 뒤였다. 머리 위에 지붕 하나 없이 처음 자리에 누웠을 때의 그 외로운 느낌을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다!\n\n그날 밤, 집집마다 문이 잠기고 집집의 개가 짖어 대며 쫓아낸 수많은 떠돌이들에게 그러했듯, 나에게도 잠이 찾아왔다. 그리고 나는 옛 학교 침대에 누워 방 안 아이들과 이야기하는 꿈을 꾸었다. 그러다 정신을 차려 보니 나는 몸을 똑바로 일으켜 앉아, 입술에 스티어포스의 이름을 올린 채, 머리 위에서 반짝이며 가물거리는 별들을 넋 나간 듯 올려다보고 있었다. 이 한밤중에 내가 어디에 있는지를 떠올리자, 무언가 알 수 없는 두려움에 자리에서 일어나 서성거리게 만드는 어떤 기분이 슬며시 나를 사로잡았다. 하지만 점점 희미해지는 별빛과, 동이 트는 쪽 하늘의 창백한 빛이 나를 다시 안심시켜 주었다. 두 눈이 무척이나 무거웠던 터라, 나는 다시 자리에 누워 잠들었다. 잠결에도 춥다는 것은 알고 있었지만, 따뜻한 햇살과 세일럼 하우스의 기상 종소리가 나를 깨울 때까지 잤다. 만약 스티어포스가 거기 있으리라 기대할 수 있었다면, 나는 그가 혼자 나올 때까지 근처에 숨어 있었을 것이다. 하지만 그가 진작에 떠났으리란 것을 나는 알고 있었다. 트래들스는 어쩌면 아직 남아 있을지도 몰랐으나, 그것조차 무척 미심쩍었다. 게다가 그의 착한 마음씨만큼은 굳게 믿었지만, 내 처지를 털어놓을 만큼 그의 입단속이나 운수를 믿을 자신은 없었다. 그래서 크리클 씨의 학생들이 일어날 무렵, 나는 담장에서 슬그머니 빠져나와, 내가 그 학생들 중 하나였을 적에 도버 로드라고 처음 알게 된 그 길고 먼지투성이 길로 접어들었다. 그때만 해도 누군가의 눈이 지금처럼 이 길 위를 떠도는 나그네가 된 나를 보게 되리라곤 거의 상상도 못 했던 그 길로.\n\n야머스에서 맞던 옛 일요일 아침과는 얼마나 다른 일요일 아침이던지! 터덜터덜 걷는 동안 때맞춰 교회 종소리가 들려왔다. 교회로 가는 사람들과 마주쳤고, 신도들이 안에 들어차 있는 교회를 한두 곳 지나치기도 했는데, 햇살 속으로 찬송 소리가 흘러나오는 동안 교회지기는 현관 그늘에 앉아 더위를 식히거나, 주목 아래 서서 이마에 손을 얹고 지나가는 나를 노려보았다. 하지만 옛 일요일 아침의 그 평화와 안식은, 나를 빼고는 만물에 깃들어 있었다. 그게 바로 차이였다. 흙먼지를 뒤집어쓴 데다 머리카락은 엉겨 붙은 내 꼴이, 나 자신이 영락없는 죄인처럼 느껴졌다. 젊고 아름다운 시절의 어머니가 난롯가에서 울고, 고모님이 그런 어머니에게 마음을 누그러뜨리는, 내가 마음속에 그려 낸 그 고요한 그림이 아니었다면, 다음 날까지 걸어 나아갈 용기를 도무지 내지 못했을 것 같다. 하지만 그 그림은 늘 내 앞에서 길잡이가 되어 주었고, 나는 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n그 일요일에 나는 곧게 뻗은 길로 이십삼 마일을 걸었다. 그리 수월하지는 않았는데, 그런 고생에는 영 서툴렀기 때문이다. 저녁이 깃들 무렵, 발은 부르트고 몸은 지친 채 로체스터의 다리를 건너오며, 저녁거리로 사 둔 빵을 먹는 내 모습이 지금도 눈에 선하다. '여행자 숙소'라는 안내문을 내건 자그마한 집 한두 채가 나를 솔깃하게 했지만, 가진 몇 푼을 쓰기가 두려웠고, 길에서 마주치거나 따라잡은 떠돌이들의 험상궂은 낯빛은 그보다 더 무서웠다. 그래서 나는 하늘 말고는 어떤 지붕도 구하지 않았다. 그리고 채텀으로 힘겹게 걸어 들어갔다 — 그날 밤의 모습으로 보자면, 채텀은 그저 백악과 도개교와, 진흙탕 강물 위에 노아의 방주처럼 지붕을 인 돛대 없는 배들이 어우러진 한바탕 꿈만 같았다 — 그러다 마침내 어느 골목길 위로 비스듬히 솟은, 풀이 무성한 포대 같은 곳으로 기어 올라갔는데, 거기서는 보초 하나가 이리저리 오가고 있었다. 나는 거기 대포 하나 곁에 몸을 뉘었다. 세일럼 하우스의 아이들이 담장 옆에 누운 나를 몰랐듯, 보초도 자기 위쪽에 내가 있는 줄 전혀 몰랐지만, 그 보초의 발소리를 벗 삼으니 마음이 흐뭇해져서, 아침까지 곤히 잤다.\n\n아침에 일어나니 몸은 뻣뻣하고 발은 욱신거렸다. 좁고 긴 거리 쪽으로 내려갈 때 사방에서 나를 에워싸는 듯한 북소리와 군대의 행진에 정신마저 멍해졌다. 여정의 끝까지 갈 기력을 조금이라도 남겨 두려면 그날은 아주 조금밖에 걷지 못하겠다는 생각이 들어, 나는 윗도리를 파는 일을 그날의 주된 과업으로 삼기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 윗도리 없이 지내는 데 익숙해지려고 그것을 벗어 겨드랑이에 끼고는, 여러 헌옷 가게를 둘러보러 나섰다.\n\n윗도리를 팔기에는 그럴듯한 곳이었다. 헌옷 장수가 수두룩했고, 대개들 가게 문 앞에서 손님을 노리고 있었기 때문이다. 하지만 그들 대부분이 사들인 물건들 틈에 견장까지 달린 장교 외투를 한두 벌씩 매달아 놓은 터라, 나는 그들 거래의 값비싼 성격에 주눅이 들어, 한참 동안 누구에게도 내 물건을 내밀지 못한 채 서성였다.\n\n이런 소심함 때문에 나는 정식 옷 장수보다는 선박용 잡화점이나 돌로비 씨네 같은 가게로 눈길을 돌리게 되었다. 마침내 그럴듯해 보이는 곳을 한 군데 찾았는데, 더러운 골목 모퉁이에 있었다. 골목 끝은 쐐기풀이 가득한 공터로 막혀 있었고, 그 울타리에는 가게에서 흘러넘친 듯한 헌 뱃사람 옷가지가 간이침대며 녹슨 총이며 방수모 따위와 함께 나부끼고 있었다. 또 어떤 쟁반에는 어찌나 크기가 제각각인 낡고 녹슨 열쇠가 잔뜩 담겨 있던지, 세상 모든 문을 다 열 수 있을 만큼 종류가 다양해 보였다.\n\n이 가게는 낮고 작았으며, 옷가지가 잔뜩 드리워진 작은 창문 하나가 빛을 들이기는커녕 오히려 가게를 어둡게 만들었고, 안으로 들어가려면 계단을 몇 개 내려가야 했다. 나는 두근거리는 가슴을 안고 그 안으로 들어갔는데, 뒤편의 더러운 굴 같은 방에서 추레한 잿빛 수염으로 얼굴 아랫부분을 온통 뒤덮은 흉측한 노인이 뛰쳐나와 내 머리카락을 움켜쥐었을 때 그 두근거림은 조금도 가라앉지 않았다. 보기만 해도 끔찍한 노인이었다. 더러운 플란넬 조끼를 입고, 럼주 냄새를 지독하게 풍겼다. 그가 나온 굴 같은 방 안에는, 헝클어지고 너덜너덜한 누비이불을 덮은 침대가 있었고, 거기 또 다른 작은 창문으로는 쐐기풀이 더 많이 우거진 풍경과 절름발이 당나귀 한 마리가 내다보였다.\n\n\"오, 뭘 원하는 게냐?\" 이 노인이 사납고 단조로운 콧소리로 이를 드러내며 으르렁댔다. \"오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야, 뭘 원하는 게냐? 오, 내 허파와 간이야, 뭘 원하는 게냐? 오, 고루, 고루!\"\n\n이 말에, 특히 마지막의 알 수 없는 그 한마디 — 목구멍에서 그르렁대는 소리 같은 것 — 를 거듭 내뱉는 통에 어찌나 질겁했던지, 나는 아무 대답도 할 수 없었다. 그러자 노인은 여전히 내 머리카락을 움켜쥔 채 되풀이했다.\n\n\"오, 뭘 원하는 게냐? 오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야, 뭘 원하는 게냐? 오, 내 허파와 간이야, 뭘 원하는 게냐? 오, 고루!\" 그는 두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 만큼 안간힘을 쥐어짜며 그 말을 비틀어 냈다.\n\n\"알고 싶은 게 있어서요.\" 나는 떨면서 말했다. \"윗도리를 사 주실까 해서요.\"\n\n\"오, 어디 윗도리 좀 보자!\" 노인이 외쳤다. \"오, 내 가슴팍에 불이 붙는구나, 어서 그 윗도리를 보여 다오! 오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야, 그 윗도리를 꺼내 보거라!\"\n\n그러면서 그는 커다란 새의 발톱 같은, 부들부들 떨리는 두 손을 내 머리카락에서 떼더니, 충혈된 두 눈에 전혀 어울리지 않는 안경 하나를 걸쳤다.\n\n\"오, 윗도리에 얼마를 쳐 줄까?\" 노인이 그것을 살펴본 뒤 외쳤다. \"오 — 고루! — 윗도리에 얼마면 되겠냐?\"\n\n\"반 크라운이요.\" 나는 정신을 추스르며 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 내 허파와 간이야,\" 노인이 외쳤다. \"안 돼! 오, 내 눈깔아, 안 돼! 오, 내 팔다리야, 안 돼! 십팔 펜스다. 고루!\"\n\n이 외마디를 내뱉을 때마다 그의 두 눈은 금방이라도 튀어나올 듯했다. 그리고 그가 하는 말은 한마디 한마디가 일종의 가락에 실려 나왔는데, 늘 토씨 하나 틀림없이 똑같았고, 내가 달리 견줄 만한 게 없으니 굳이 비유하자면 낮게 시작했다가 높이 솟구치고 다시 잦아드는 한 줄기 바람결 같았다.\n\n\"좋아요.\" 나는 흥정을 마무리 짓게 되어 반가운 마음에 말했다. \"십팔 펜스에 할게요.\"\n\n\"오, 내 간이야!\" 노인이 윗도리를 선반에 내던지며 외쳤다. \"가게에서 나가! 오, 내 허파야, 가게에서 나가! 오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야 — 고루! — 돈은 달라고 하지 마라, 물물교환으로 하자.\" 내 평생 그때만큼, 그 전에도 그 후에도 그렇게 겁먹은 적은 없었다. 하지만 나는 공손히, 돈이 필요하며 다른 것은 나에게 아무 쓸모가 없다고, 하지만 바깥에서 그가 원하는 대로 기다리겠으며 그를 재촉할 마음은 없다고 말했다. 그래서 나는 밖으로 나가 한쪽 구석 그늘에 앉았다. 그리고 어찌나 오래 거기 앉아 있었던지, 그늘이 볕으로 변하고 그 볕이 다시 그늘로 변하도록, 나는 그저 돈을 기다리며 그 자리에 앉아 있었다.\n\n그 장사판에 그런 술 취한 미치광이가 또 있으리라고는, 부디 없기를 바란다. 그가 동네에서 유명짜한 인물이고, 악마에게 제 몸을 팔아넘겼다는 평판을 누리고 있다는 것을, 나는 곧 아이들의 출몰로 알아챘다. 아이들이 끊임없이 가게 주위로 몰려와 싸움을 걸 듯 그 전설을 외쳐 대며, 어서 금을 꺼내 보이라고 노인에게 소리쳤기 때문이다. \"찰리, 네가 꾸미는 것처럼 그렇게 가난한 게 아니잖아. 금 좀 꺼내 봐. 악마한테 몸 팔고 받은 금 좀 내놔 보라고. 어서! 그거 매트리스 안감 속에 있잖아, 찰리. 죽 찢어서 좀 보여 줘!\" 이런 말과 함께, 그러라고 칼을 빌려주겠다는 제안이 수두룩하게 쏟아지자, 노인은 어찌나 약이 올랐던지 하루 종일 그가 달려들면 아이들이 달아나는 일이 끝없이 되풀이되었다. 더러는 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 나를 그 아이들 중 하나로 착각하고, 마치 나를 갈가리 찢어 놓을 듯 입을 우물거리며 덤벼들기도 했다. 그러다 아슬아슬하게 나를 알아보고는 가게 안으로 뛰어들어가, 목소리로 미루어 짐작하건대 침대에 드러누운 채, 제 특유의 그 바람 같은 가락에 실어 미친 듯이 「넬슨의 죽음」을 악써 부르곤 했다. 매 행 앞에 \"오!\"를 붙이고, 그 사이사이에 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 \"고루\"를 끼워 가면서. 이것만으로도 나에게는 충분히 고약한 일이었거늘, 아이들은 내가 옷도 변변히 못 걸친 채 바깥에 끈질기게 앉아 버티는 꼴을 보고 나를 그 가게와 한패로 엮어서는, 하루 종일 나에게 돌팔매질을 하며 몹시 못살게 굴었다.\n\n노인은 물물교환에 응하도록 나를 꾀려고 갖은 시도를 했다. 한 번은 낚싯대를 들고 나오고, 또 한 번은 바이올린을, 또 한 번은 삼각모를, 또 한 번은 플루트를 들고 나왔다. 하지만 나는 그 모든 제안을 뿌리치고 절망 속에 그 자리에 앉아, 그때마다 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈으로 내 돈이 아니면 윗도리를 돌려 달라고 졸랐다. 마침내 그는 반 페니씩 끊어서 돈을 치르기 시작했는데, 야금야금 단계를 밟아 한 실링을 채우는 데 꼬박 두 시간이 걸렸다.\n\n\"오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야!\" 한참 뜸을 들인 뒤, 노인이 가게에서 흉측하게 내다보며 외쳤다. \"이 펜스만 깎고 갈 텐가?\"\n\n\"못 깎아요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그럼 굶어 죽어요.\"\n\n\"오, 내 허파와 간이야, 삼 펜스 깎고 갈 텐가?\"\n\n\"그럴 수만 있다면 한 푼도 안 받고 가겠지만,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 돈이 정말 절실해요.\"\n\n\"오, 고-루!\" (문기둥 너머로 교활한 늙은 머리통만 빼꼼 내밀고 나를 엿보며, 이 외마디를 제 몸에서 어떻게 비틀어 냈는지는 정말이지 글로 옮길 길이 없다.) \"사 펜스 깎고 갈 텐가?\"\n\n나는 너무도 기진하고 지친 터라 이 제안을 받아들였다. 그러고는 떨지 않을 수 없는 손길로 그의 발톱에서 돈을 받아 들고, 해가 지기 조금 전, 그 어느 때보다도 굶주리고 목마른 채로 자리를 떴다. 하지만 삼 펜스를 들여 나는 곧 기운을 말끔히 되찾았다. 그렇게 한결 좋아진 기분으로, 다리를 절뚝이며 칠 마일을 더 걸었다.\n\n그날 밤 내 잠자리는 또 다른 건초더미 아래였다. 시냇물에 물집 잡힌 발을 씻고, 시원한 잎사귀 몇 장으로 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 싸맨 뒤, 나는 그곳에서 편안히 쉬었다. 이튿날 아침 다시 길을 나섰을 때, 그 길이 홉밭과 과수원을 줄줄이 지나간다는 것을 알았다. 과수원이 잘 익은 사과로 발갛게 물들 만큼 한 해가 깊어 있었고, 몇몇 곳에서는 홉을 따는 사람들이 벌써 일을 하고 있었다. 나는 그 모든 풍경이 더없이 아름답다고 생각했고, 그날 밤은 홉밭에서 자기로 마음먹었다. 길게 늘어선 장대들과 그것을 우아하게 휘감은 잎사귀들 사이에서 어떤 정겨운 벗을 그려 보면서.\n\n그날은 떠돌이들이 어느 때보다 더 험악해서, 지금도 생생하게 떠오르는 공포를 내게 심어 주었다. 그중 몇몇은 가장 흉포해 보이는 불한당이었는데, 내가 지나가면 나를 노려보다가, 어쩌면 멈춰 서서 돌아와 말 좀 하자고 불러 댔고, 내가 줄행랑을 치면 돌을 던졌다. 어떤 젊은 사내가 기억난다 — 봇짐과 화로로 미루어 땜장이였지 싶다 — 여자 하나를 데리고 다니던 그 사내는 홱 돌아서서 그렇게 나를 노려보더니, 어찌나 무시무시한 목소리로 돌아오라고 호통을 쳤던지, 나는 걸음을 멈추고 돌아보고 말았다.\n\n\"부르면 이리 와.\" 땜장이가 말했다. \"안 그러면 네 어린 배때기를 갈라 버릴 테다.\"\n\n돌아가는 게 상책이라고 나는 생각했다. 그들에게 가까이 다가가며, 표정으로나마 땜장이의 비위를 맞추려 애쓰다가, 나는 여자의 한쪽 눈이 멍들어 있는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"어디 가는 길이냐?\" 땜장이가 시커먼 손으로 내 셔츠 앞섶을 움켜쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"도버에 가요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"어디서 오는 길이냐?\" 땜장이가 나를 더 단단히 붙잡으려고 셔츠를 한 번 더 비틀어 쥐며 물었다.\n\n\"런던에서 와요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 수작을 부리는 거냐?\" 땜장이가 물었다. \"도둑놈이냐?\"\n\n\"아—아니에요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아니라고, 빌어먹을? 내 앞에서 정직한 척 으스대다간,\" 땜장이가 말했다. \"네 골통을 박살 내 버릴 테다.\"\n\n그는 비어 있는 손으로 나를 때리는 시늉을 하더니, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 나를 훑어보았다.\n\n\"맥주 한 잔 살 돈은 몸에 지녔겠지?\" 땜장이가 말했다. \"있으면 빼앗기기 전에 어서 내놔!\"\n\n나는 틀림없이 돈을 꺼냈을 것이다. 그런데 그 여자의 눈길과 마주쳤고, 그녀가 아주 살짝 고개를 가로젓더니 입술로 \"안 돼!\" 하는 모양을 짓는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"전 아주 가난해서,\" 나는 애써 웃어 보이며 말했다. \"돈이 한 푼도 없어요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 땜장이가 어찌나 매섭게 나를 노려보던지, 나는 그가 내 주머니 속 돈을 꿰뚫어 본 게 아닐까 겁이 날 지경이었다.\n\n\"아저씨!\" 내가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻이냐,\" 땜장이가 말했다. \"우리 형 비단 손수건을 두르고 다니다니! 이리 내놔!\" 그러고는 순식간에 내 목에서 손수건을 잡아채 여자에게 던졌다.\n\n여자는 이게 무슨 농담이라도 되는 양 까르르 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 그것을 도로 나에게 던지고는, 아까처럼 살짝 한 번 고개를 끄덕이고 입술로 \"가!\" 하는 모양을 지었다. 하지만 내가 그 말을 따르기도 전에, 땜장이는 어찌나 거칠게 내 손에서 손수건을 낚아챘던지 나를 깃털처럼 내동댕이쳤고, 그것을 제 목에 헐겁게 두르더니 욕설을 내뱉으며 여자에게 돌아서서 그녀를 후려쳐 쓰러뜨렸다. 그녀가 딱딱한 길바닥에 발랑 나자빠져, 보닛이 벗겨진 채 머리카락이 온통 먼지에 허옇게 뒤덮인 채로 누워 있던 모습을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 또 멀리서 돌아보았을 때, 길가 둑이던 그 보도에 그녀가 주저앉아 숄 귀퉁이로 얼굴의 피를 닦고 있는데, 그 사내는 앞장서서 가 버리던 그 모습도.\n\n이 일에 어찌나 겁을 먹었던지, 그 뒤로 나는 그런 사람들이 다가오는 게 보이면, 숨을 곳을 찾을 때까지 되돌아가서 그들이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 거기 머물렀다. 그런 일이 하도 잦아서 길이 몹시 더뎌졌다. 하지만 이 어려움 속에서도, 여정의 다른 온갖 어려움 속에서와 마찬가지로, 내가 이 세상에 나오기 전, 젊은 시절 어머니의 모습을 그린 그 마음속 그림이 나를 떠받치고 이끌어 주는 것 같았다. 그 그림은 늘 나와 함께였다. 잠을 청하려 누웠을 때 홉밭 사이에도 그것이 있었고, 아침에 깨어날 때도 나와 함께였으며, 하루 종일 내 앞에서 길잡이가 되어 주었다. 그 뒤로 나는 그 그림을, 무더운 햇살 속에서 마치 졸고 있는 듯한 캔터베리의 양지바른 거리와, 그곳의 오래된 집들과 성문들, 그리고 탑 둘레를 떼까마귀가 맴도는 위풍당당한 잿빛 대성당의 광경과 함께 떠올리게 되었다. 마침내 도버 근처의 휑하니 너른 구릉지에 이르렀을 때, 그 그림은 그 적막한 풍경에 희망을 보태 주었다. 그리고 내 여정의 그 첫 번째 큰 목표지에 닿아, 도주 엿새째에 마침내 그 마을에 발을 들여놓고 나서야 비로소 그 그림은 나를 떠났다. 하지만 그때, 참 이상하게도, 너덜너덜한 신발과 먼지투성이에 햇볕에 그을린, 옷도 변변히 못 걸친 몰골로, 그토록 오래 바라던 그곳에 막상 서고 보니, 그 그림은 꿈처럼 스러져 버리고, 나를 어쩔 줄 모르고 풀 죽은 채로 내버려 두는 것만 같았다.\n\n나는 먼저 뱃사람들에게 고모님에 대해 물어보았는데, 갖가지 대답이 돌아왔다. 어떤 이는 고모님이 사우스 포어랜드 등대에 살면서 그 바람에 구레나룻을 그슬렸다고 했고, 다른 이는 항구 바깥 큰 부표에 매여 있어서 썰물 때나 찾아갈 수 있다고 했으며, 또 다른 이는 아이를 훔친 죄로 메이드스톤 감옥에 갇혀 있다고 했고, 네 번째 사람은 지난번 거센 바람 때 빗자루에 올라타 곧장 칼레로 날아가는 걸 봤다고 했다. 다음으로 물어본 합승마차 마부들도 하나같이 익살맞고 하나같이 무례했다. 가게 주인들은 내 행색이 마음에 들지 않았던지, 대개는 내 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않고 너한테 줄 건 아무것도 없다고 대꾸했다. 나는 도망쳐 나온 이래 어느 때보다도 비참하고 막막한 기분이 들었다. 돈은 다 떨어졌고, 처분할 것이라곤 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다. 배고프고, 목마르고, 기진맥진했다. 그리고 마치 런던에 그대로 머물러 있었던 것처럼, 목적지가 아득히 멀게만 느껴졌다.\n\n이렇게 묻고 다니는 사이 아침나절이 다 가 버렸고, 나는 장터 근처 길모퉁이의 빈 가게 계단에 앉아, 사람들이 말한 그 다른 곳들로 발길을 옮겨 볼까 궁리하고 있었다. 그때 합승마차 한 대가 지나가다가 마부가 말 덮개를 떨어뜨렸다. 내가 그것을 집어 건네자, 그 사내의 얼굴에 어린 어딘가 선량한 기색에 용기를 얻어, 나는 혹시 트롯우드 양이 어디 사는지 아느냐고 물어보았다. 워낙 여러 번 던진 질문이라, 그 말이 입술에서 거의 사그라들 지경이었지만.\n\n\"트롯우드라.\" 그가 말했다. \"어디 보자. 그 이름은 나도 알지. 나이 든 부인인가?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"꽤 그러세요.\"\n\n\"등이 꽤 꼿꼿하고?\" 그가 몸을 똑바로 세워 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"아마 그러실 거예요.\"\n\n\"가방을 들고 다니지?\" 그가 말했다. \"속이 넉넉한 가방 말이야 — 좀 무뚝뚝하고, 사람을 매섭게 몰아붙이고?\"\n\n이 묘사가 의심할 여지 없이 정확하다는 것을 인정하면서, 내 가슴은 철렁 내려앉았다.\n\n\"그렇다면 말이지, 내 일러 주마.\" 그가 말했다. \"저 위로 올라가서,\" 채찍으로 언덕배기를 가리키며 그가 말했다. \"바다를 마주 보고 선 집들이 나올 때까지 곧장 가면, 거기서 그분 소식을 들을 수 있을 게다. 내 생각에 그분은 아무것도 그냥 봐주는 법이 없을 테니, 옜다, 너 일 페니 가져라.\"\n\n나는 고맙게 그 선물을 받아 그것으로 빵 한 덩이를 샀다. 이 요깃거리를 가는 길에 해치우며, 나는 그 사람이 일러 준 방향으로 한참을 걸었지만 그가 말한 집들은 좀처럼 나오지 않았다. 마침내 저 앞에 집 몇 채가 보였고, 가까이 다가가 작은 가게로 들어갔다(우리 고향에서 흔히 만물상이라고 부르던 곳이었다). 그러고는 혹시 트롯우드 양이 어디 사는지 일러 주실 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 나는 계산대 뒤에 있는 남자에게 말을 건넸는데, 그는 한 젊은 여자에게 줄 쌀을 달고 있었다. 그런데 그 여자가 이 질문을 자기에게 한 것으로 여기고는 홱 돌아섰다.\n\n\"우리 마님 말이니?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그분한테 무슨 볼일이 있어, 얘?\"\n\n\"저는,\" 내가 대답했다. \"괜찮으시다면, 그분께 말씀을 좀 드리고 싶어서요.\"\n\n\"구걸하려는 거겠지.\" 그 처녀가 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"정말이에요.\" 하지만 사실 내가 다른 목적으로 온 게 아니라는 것이 문득 떠올라, 나는 당황한 채 입을 다물었고, 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n말투로 미루어 고모님네 하녀이지 싶은 그 여자는, 쌀을 작은 바구니에 담더니 가게 밖으로 걸어 나가며, 트롯우드 양이 어디 사는지 알고 싶으면 자기를 따라오라고 했다. 두 번 권할 것도 없었다. 그 무렵 나는 어찌나 놀라고 떨렸던지 다리가 후들거릴 지경이었지만. 나는 그 젊은 여자를 따라갔고, 우리는 곧 산뜻한 활꼴 창이 달린 아주 아담한 작은 오두막에 이르렀다. 그 앞에는 자그마한 네모난 자갈 마당, 아니 정원이 있었는데, 정성껏 가꾼 꽃이 가득해서 향기가 그윽했다.\n\n\"여기가 트롯우드 양 댁이야.\" 그 젊은 여자가 말했다. \"자, 이제 알았지. 내가 할 말은 이게 다야.\" 그러고는 마치 내가 나타난 책임을 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 서둘러 집 안으로 들어가 버렸고, 나는 정원 문 앞에 서서, 그 너머로 거실 창문 쪽을 시무룩하게 바라보았다. 가운데가 반쯤 걷힌 모슬린 커튼과, 창턱에 고정된 크고 둥근 초록색 가리개, 작은 탁자, 커다란 의자가, 어쩌면 지금 이 순간 고모님이 위풍당당하게 거기 앉아 계실지도 모른다는 생각을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n이 무렵 내 신발은 처참한 꼴이었다. 밑창은 야금야금 떨어져 나갔고, 가죽 윗부분은 터지고 갈라져 신발의 형체조차 사라지고 없었다. 모자는(밤에는 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 노릇도 했다) 어찌나 찌그러지고 뒤틀렸던지, 거름더미 위에 나뒹구는 손잡이 떨어진 낡은 냄비라도 그것과 견주는 걸 부끄러워할 필요는 없을 정도였다. 더위와 이슬과 풀물, 그리고 내가 자고 일어난 켄트의 흙으로 얼룩진 데다 군데군데 찢어지기까지 한 내 셔츠와 바지는, 내가 정원 문 앞에 선 채로 고모님의 정원에서 새들을 쫓아 버렸을지도 모를 지경이었다. 머리카락은 런던을 떠난 뒤로 빗이나 솔이라곤 구경도 못 했다. 얼굴과 목과 손은, 익숙지 않게 바깥 공기와 햇볕에 노출된 탓에 산딸기처럼 가무잡잡하게 타 있었다. 머리끝부터 발끝까지 백악과 먼지를 어찌나 뒤집어썼던지 거의 허옇게 분칠을 한 듯, 마치 석회 가마에서 막 기어 나온 꼴이었다. 이런 몰골로, 그 몰골을 뼈저리게 의식하면서, 나는 나의 만만찮은 고모님께 나를 소개하고 첫인상을 남길 순간을 기다렸다.\n\n거실 창문이 줄곧 미동조차 없는 것으로 미루어 한참 만에 고모님이 거기 안 계시는구나 짐작하고, 나는 그 위층 창문으로 눈을 들어 올렸다. 거기 혈색 좋고 인상이 서글서글한, 머리가 희끗한 신사가 보였는데, 그는 괴상한 모양새로 한쪽 눈을 찡긋 감더니, 나를 향해 몇 번이나 고개를 끄덕이고, 또 그만큼이나 고개를 가로젓고는, 웃음을 터뜨리더니 사라져 버렸다.\n\n그렇잖아도 잔뜩 마음이 흐트러져 있던 참인데, 이 뜻밖의 행동에 마음이 한층 더 어수선해져서, 나는 어떻게 하는 게 상책일지 곰곰이 따져 보려고 슬그머니 자리를 뜰까 하던 차였다. 그때 집에서 한 부인이 나왔다. 모자 위에 손수건을 동여매고, 두 손에는 정원용 장갑을 끼고, 통행료 받는 사람의 앞치마 같은 정원용 주머니를 두르고, 커다란 칼 하나를 든 채였다. 나는 그분이 베시 양이라는 것을 단박에 알아보았다. 가엾은 어머니가 그토록 자주 들려주던, 블런더스톤 루커리의 우리 집 정원을 성큼성큼 걸어 올라오던 바로 그 모습 그대로, 그녀는 집에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나왔기 때문이다.\n\n\"저리 가!\" 베시 양이 고개를 가로저으며, 칼로 허공을 향해 멀찍이 한 번 내리치는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"가 버려! 여기 사내애는 안 받아!\"\n\n나는 가슴이 입까지 차오른 듯한 심정으로, 그녀가 정원 한쪽 구석으로 성큼성큼 가더니 거기 있는 작은 뿌리를 캐려고 몸을 숙이는 것을 지켜보았다. 그러고는 용기라곤 한 톨도 없이, 그러나 절박함만은 한가득 안고, 나는 살그머니 안으로 들어가 그녀 곁에 서서 손가락으로 그녀를 슬쩍 건드렸다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 부인.\" 내가 입을 떼었다.\n\n그녀가 흠칫하며 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 고모님.\"\n\n\"어엉?\" 베시 양이 외쳤다. 내가 평생 그에 견줄 만한 것조차 들어 본 적 없는 경악에 찬 어조였다.\n\n\"실례지만, 고모님, 저는 고모님의 조카예요.\"\n\n\"아이고, 맙소사!\" 고모님이 말했다. 그러고는 정원 오솔길에 털썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"저는 데이비드 코퍼필드예요. 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 왔어요 — 제가 태어나던 날 밤에 고모님이 오셔서, 제 사랑하는 엄마를 만나셨던 그곳이요. 엄마가 돌아가신 뒤로 저는 무척 불행했어요. 천대받고, 아무것도 못 배우고, 혼자 내팽개쳐지고, 저한테 맞지도 않는 일에 내몰렸어요. 그래서 고모님께 도망쳐 온 거예요. 길을 처음 나설 때 가진 걸 다 털리고, 여기까지 줄곧 걸어왔어요. 여정을 시작한 뒤로 침대에서 자 본 적이 한 번도 없어요.\" 여기서 나를 지탱하던 힘이 한꺼번에 무너져 내렸다. 나는 내 너덜너덜한 몰골을 그녀에게 보여 주려는, 그리고 내가 무언가 고생을 했음을 그것으로 증언하려는 듯 두 손을 한 번 휘젓고는, 한바탕 격렬한 울음을 터뜨렸다. 일주일 내내 가슴속에 꾹꾹 눌러 담아 두었던 울음이지 싶었다.\n\n고모님은 놀라움을 빼고는 온갖 표정이 다 사라진 얼굴로, 자갈밭에 주저앉아 나를 빤히 바라보다가, 내가 울음을 터뜨리자 황급히 일어나 내 멱살을 잡고 거실로 데리고 들어갔다. 거기서 고모님이 맨 처음 한 일은 키 큰 찬장을 열어 병 몇 개를 꺼내, 그 안의 내용물을 조금씩 내 입에 부어 넣는 것이었다. 짐작건대 그것들은 손에 잡히는 대로 꺼낸 게 틀림없는데, 내가 아니스 향수와 멸치 소스와 샐러드 드레싱 맛을 본 게 분명하기 때문이다. 이런 회복제들을 먹이고 나서도 내가 여전히 흐느낌을 가누지 못하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하자, 고모님은 나를 소파에 눕히고 머리 밑에는 숄을 받쳐 주었으며, 내가 덮개를 더럽힐까 봐 발밑에는 자기 머리에 둘렀던 손수건을 깔아 주었다. 그러고는 앞서 말한 그 초록색 부채, 아니 가리개 뒤에 자기 몸을 앉혀 내가 자기 얼굴을 볼 수 없게 하더니, 이따금씩 \"어이구, 맙소사!\" 하고 외마디를 터뜨렸는데, 그 소리가 마치 일 분마다 한 발씩 쏘는 예포 같았다.\n\n얼마 뒤 고모님이 종을 울렸다. \"재닛.\" 하녀가 들어오자 고모님이 말했다. \"위층에 올라가서 딕 씨께 내 안부를 전하고, 내가 얘기 좀 하고 싶다고 말씀드려라.\"\n\n재닛은 내가 소파에 뻣뻣하게 누워 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀란 눈치였지만(나는 행여 고모님 심기를 거스를까 봐 움직이기가 두려웠다), 시킨 심부름을 하러 갔다. 고모님은 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌린 채 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었고, 그러다 위층 창문에서 나에게 곁눈질하던 그 신사가 웃으며 들어왔다.\n\n\"딕 씨.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"바보처럼 굴지 마세요. 마음만 먹으면 댁만큼 분별 있는 사람도 없잖아요. 그건 우리 모두 알아요. 그러니 당신이 어떤 사람이든 간에, 바보처럼 굴진 마세요.\"\n\n신사는 곧바로 진지해지더니, 내가 보기엔 마치 그 창문 일은 아무 말 말아 달라고 나에게 애원하는 듯한 표정으로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"딕 씨.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 데이비드 코퍼필드 얘기 하는 거 들으셨죠? 자, 기억 안 나는 척하지 마세요. 당신이나 나나 다 아는 사실이니까.\"\n\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드요?\" 딕 씨가 말했는데, 내가 보기엔 그것에 대해 별로 기억하는 것 같지 않았다. \"데이비드 코퍼필드요? 아, 그럼요, 물론이죠. 데이비드, 당연하죠.\"\n\n\"그런데,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 애가 그 사람 아들이에요 — 그 사람 자식이요. 어쩌면 이렇게 제 어미를 빼닮지만 않았어도, 영락없이 제 아비랑 똑같았을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그 사람 아들이라고요?\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"데이비드의 아들이요? 저런!\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"게다가 이 애가 일을 아주 멋들어지게 저질렀지 뭐예요. 글쎄 도망을 쳤다잖아요. 아! 그 애 누이, 베시 트롯우드라면 절대 도망치지 않았을 텐데.\" 고모님은 단호하게 고개를 가로저었다. 끝내 태어나지 않은 그 여자아이의 됨됨이와 행실을 굳게 확신하면서.\n\n\"오! 그 애는 도망치지 않았을 거라고 생각하세요?\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"하느님 맙소사, 이 양반 좀 봐.\" 고모님이 매섭게 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 소리람! 그 애가 도망 안 쳤을 거란 걸 내가 모를까 봐요? 그 애는 자기 대모랑 같이 살았을 거고, 우리 둘은 서로에게 끔찍이도 정을 쏟았을 거예요. 도대체, 세상에, 그 애 누이 베시 트롯우드가 어디서, 어디로 도망을 친단 말이에요?\"\n\n\"아무 데도요.\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그렇지.\" 고모님은 그 대답에 마음이 누그러져서 말했다. \"그런데 딕, 당신은 외과의의 침처럼 예리한 사람이면서, 어쩜 그렇게 정신 나간 척을 한답니까? 자, 여기 어린 데이비드 코퍼필드가 있어요. 내가 당신에게 묻고 싶은 건, 내가 이 애를 어떻게 하면 좋겠느냐는 거예요.\"\n\n\"이 애를 어떻게 하느냐고요?\" 딕 씨가 머리를 긁적이며 맥없이 말했다. \"오! 이 애를 어떻게 하느냐고요?\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 고모님이 자못 진지한 표정으로, 집게손가락을 치켜들고 말했다. \"어서요! 정말로 쓸 만한 조언이 필요해요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 내가 댁이라면,\" 딕 씨가 골똘히 생각하며, 멍하니 나를 바라보면서 말했다. \"나라면—\" 나를 가만히 들여다보던 것이 그에게 문득 어떤 생각을 불어넣은 듯, 그는 활기차게 덧붙였다. \"나라면 이 애를 씻기겠어요!\"\n\n\"재닛.\" 고모님이, 그때는 내가 미처 이해하지 못한 어떤 조용한 의기양양함을 띠고 돌아서며 말했다. \"딕 씨가 우리 모두를 바로잡아 주는구나. 목욕물을 데워라!\"\n\n이 대화에 깊이 빠져 있긴 했지만, 나는 그 와중에도 고모님과 딕 씨와 재닛을 유심히 살피지 않을 수 없었고, 또 진작부터 하고 있던 방 안 둘러보기도 마저 끝냈다.\n\n고모님은 키가 크고 이목구비가 단단한 부인이었으나, 결코 못생긴 것은 아니었다. 그 얼굴에도, 목소리에도, 걸음걸이와 몸가짐에도 굽힐 줄 모르는 강단이 서려 있어서, 우리 어머니처럼 여린 사람에게 그분이 끼친 영향력을 넉넉히 설명하고도 남았다. 하지만 그 이목구비는, 비록 빳빳하고 근엄하기는 해도, 못났다기보다 오히려 잘난 편이었다. 나는 특히 그분의 눈이 무척 빠르고 초롱초롱하다는 것을 알아챘다. 잿빛 머리카락은 두 갈래로 단정하게 가른 채, 내 생각엔 머리쓰개라고 부를 만한 것 아래로 정리되어 있었다. 그러니까 지금보다 그 시절에 훨씬 흔하던, 양옆 자락을 턱밑에서 여미는 모자 말이다. 옷은 연보라색에 더없이 단정했으나, 되도록 거추장스러운 게 없기를 바라기라도 한 듯 천을 아껴 지은 것이었다. 그 모양새가 무엇보다 군더더기 치맛자락을 잘라 낸 승마복에 가깝다고 생각했던 기억이 난다. 옆구리에는 신사용 금시계를 차고 있었는데, 크기와 만듦새로 미루어 보건대 그러했고, 거기 어울리는 줄과 인장도 달려 있었다. 목에는 셔츠 깃과 다르지 않은 아마포를 둘렀고, 손목에는 작은 셔츠 소맷부리 같은 것을 차고 있었다.\n\n딕 씨는, 앞서 말했듯이 머리가 희끗하고 혈색이 좋았다. 그것만 말하면 그에 대해 할 말을 다 한 셈이었을 텐데, 그의 머리가 묘하게 수그러져 있지만 않았더라도 그랬을 것이다 — 나이 탓은 아니었다. 그 모습은 매를 맞은 뒤의 크리클 씨네 학생들 머리통을 떠올리게 했다 — 게다가 그의 잿빛 눈은 툭 불거지고 큼직했으며, 그 안에는 묘하게 촉촉한 광채가 어려 있었다. 그 광채가 그의 멍한 태도, 고모님에게 고분고분 따르는 모습, 고모님이 칭찬해 줄 때의 어린애 같은 기쁨과 어우러져, 나로 하여금 그가 좀 정신이 이상한 게 아닌가 의심하게 만들었다. 하지만 그가 정말 미친 사람이라면 어떻게 여기 와 있게 된 건지가 나를 몹시 어리둥절하게 했다. 그는 여느 평범한 신사와 다름없이, 헐렁한 잿빛 실내 외투에 조끼, 흰 바지를 차려입었고, 시계는 호주머니에, 돈은 주머니에 넣어 두었는데, 그 돈을 마치 무척이나 자랑스러워하듯 짤랑짤랑 흔들어 댔다.\n\n재닛은 열아홉이나 스무 살쯤 된 예쁘장하고 발랄한 처녀로, 단정함의 본보기였다. 그 순간엔 그녀를 더 눈여겨보지 않았지만, 여기서 나중에야 알게 된 사실 하나를 말해 두어야겠다. 그러니까 그녀는, 고모님이 일부러 인류를 멀리하는 법을 가르치려고 거두어들인 일련의 보호자녀 중 하나였는데, 이들은 대개 빵집 주인과 결혼함으로써 그 결심을 매듭짓곤 했다.\n\n방은 재닛이나 고모님만큼이나 단정했다. 조금 전, 그 방을 떠올리려고 펜을 내려놓는데, 또다시 바다에서 불어오는 바람이 꽃향기를 실어 안으로 밀려들었다. 나는 환하게 닦고 윤을 낸 고풍스러운 가구며, 활꼴 창가의 둥근 초록 부채 곁에 놓인, 누구도 범할 수 없는 고모님의 의자와 탁자며, 거친 모직물을 깐 카펫이며, 고양이며, 주전자 받침이며, 카나리아 두 마리며, 오래된 도자기며, 말린 장미 잎이 가득한 펀치 그릇이며, 온갖 병과 단지를 지키고 선 키 큰 찬장이며, 그리고 나머지 풍경과는 놀랍도록 어울리지 않게, 소파 위에 앉아 모든 것을 눈여겨보던 먼지투성이의 나 자신을 보았다.\n\n재닛이 목욕물을 준비하러 나간 사이, 무척이나 놀랍게도, 고모님은 한순간에 분노로 뻣뻣하게 굳더니, 가까스로 목소리를 짜내어 외쳤다. \"재닛! 당나귀들이야!\"\n\n그러자 재닛이 집에 불이라도 난 듯 계단을 뛰어 올라와, 앞쪽의 작은 풀밭으로 쏜살같이 달려 나가, 감히 거기에 발굽을 들이민, 부인들이 올라탄 안장 얹은 당나귀 두 마리를 쫓아 버렸다. 그사이 고모님은 집에서 뛰쳐나가, 어린아이를 등에 태운 세 번째 짐승의 고삐를 움켜쥐고는, 녀석을 돌려세워 그 신성한 구역에서 끌어낸 다음, 그 거룩한 땅을 더럽힌 시중꾼 불량배 녀석의 따귀를 갈겼다.\n\n지금 이 순간까지도 나는 고모님이 그 풀밭에 대해 어떤 합법적인 통행권을 가졌는지 어쨌는지 알지 못한다. 하지만 고모님은 자기 마음속으로 그렇다고 못 박아 두었고, 그녀에게는 그게 다 매한가지였다. 평생 끊임없이 복수해야 마땅한, 그녀 인생의 하나뿐인 크나큰 분노거리는, 그 티 없이 깨끗한 자리를 당나귀가 지나가는 일이었다. 무슨 일에 몰두해 있든, 자기가 한몫 끼어든 대화가 아무리 흥미롭든, 당나귀 한 마리면 순식간에 그녀의 생각의 물줄기를 돌려놓았고, 그녀는 곧장 녀석에게 달려들었다. 물 주전자와 물뿌리개가 비밀스러운 곳곳에 숨겨져 있다가 못된 짓을 한 아이들에게 쏟아부어졌고, 막대기들이 문 뒤에 매복해 있었으며, 시도 때도 없이 기습이 감행되어, 끊임없는 전쟁이 벌어졌다. 어쩌면 이것이 당나귀 모는 아이들에게는 즐거운 자극거리였는지도 모른다. 아니면 사정을 좀 더 영리하게 알아챈 당나귀들이, 타고난 고집으로 일부러 그 길로 오는 것을 즐겼는지도 모를 일이다. 내가 아는 것이라곤, 목욕물이 준비되기까지 세 번이나 비상사태가 벌어졌다는 것, 그리고 그 마지막이자 가장 격렬했던 비상사태 때, 고모님이 모래빛 머리의 열다섯 살짜리 녀석을 혼자서 상대해, 그 녀석이 무슨 영문인지 미처 깨닫기도 전에 모래빛 머리통을 자기 집 대문에 쾅 박는 것을 내가 보았다는 것뿐이다. 이런 방해들이 나에게는 더더욱 우스꽝스러웠다. 그때 고모님이 나에게 큰 숟가락으로 수프를 떠먹이던 참이었기 때문이다(내가 정말로 굶주리고 있어서 처음에는 아주 조금씩만 영양분을 줘야 한다고 그녀는 굳게 믿어 버린 터였다). 그러다 내 입이 아직 숟가락을 받으려고 벌어져 있는데도, 고모님은 그 숟가락을 도로 그릇에 놓고는 \"재닛! 당나귀들이야!\" 하고 외치며 공격에 나서곤 했다.\n\n목욕은 큰 위안이 되었다. 들판에서 노숙한 탓에 팔다리가 욱신욱신 쑤셔 오기 시작했고, 게다가 이제는 너무 지치고 기운이 빠져서 오 분도 채 깨어 있기가 힘들 지경이었으니까. 목욕을 마치자 그들은(고모님과 재닛 말이다) 딕 씨의 셔츠와 바지를 나에게 입히고, 큼지막한 숄로 두세 겹 칭칭 동여맸다. 내가 무슨 보따리 꼴로 보였는지는 모르겠지만, 어쨌든 무척 더운 보따리였던 것만은 분명하다. 게다가 몹시 어질어질하고 졸려서, 나는 곧 다시 소파에 누워 잠이 들고 말았다.\n\n그건 어쩌면, 그토록 오래 내 마음을 사로잡고 있던 그 상상에서 비롯된 꿈이었는지도 모른다. 하지만 나는 고모님이 다가와 내 위로 몸을 굽히고, 내 얼굴에서 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겨 주고, 내 머리를 더 편안하게 받쳐 준 다음, 가만히 서서 나를 바라보았다는 인상을 받으며 잠에서 깼다. \"예쁜 녀석\" 또는 \"가엾은 녀석\"이라는 말도 내 귀에 맴도는 것 같았다. 하지만 깨어났을 때, 그 말을 고모님이 했다고 믿게 할 만한 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 없었다. 고모님은 활꼴 창가에 앉아, 일종의 회전축에 달려 어느 쪽으로든 돌아가는 그 초록 부채 뒤에서 바다를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n내가 깨어난 직후 우리는 구운 닭고기와 푸딩으로 저녁을 먹었다. 나는 식탁에 앉았는데, 묶어 놓은 새 한 마리와 다를 바 없는 꼴로, 팔을 움직이기조차 여간 힘들지 않았다. 하지만 고모님이 나를 칭칭 싸매 놓은 터라, 나는 불편하다는 불평 한마디 하지 않았다. 그러는 내내 나는 고모님이 나를 어떻게 할 작정인지 무척이나 알고 싶었다. 하지만 고모님은 깊은 침묵 속에 식사를 했고, 다만 이따금씩 맞은편에 앉은 나에게 눈길을 고정하고는 \"어이구, 맙소사!\" 하고 내뱉을 뿐이었는데, 그것은 내 불안을 결코 조금도 덜어 주지 못했다.\n\n식탁보가 걷히고 셰리주가 식탁에 올려진 뒤(나도 한 잔 마셨다), 고모님은 다시 딕 씨를 불러오라고 사람을 올려보냈다. 딕 씨가 합류하자, 고모님은 그에게 내 이야기에 귀 기울여 달라고 청했고, 잇따른 질문을 통해 그 이야기를 나에게서 차근차근 끌어냈는데, 그러는 동안 딕 씨는 할 수 있는 한 현명해 보이는 표정을 지었다. 내가 이야기하는 동안 고모님은 줄곧 딕 씨에게 눈길을 두었는데, 그게 아니었다면 딕 씨는 잠들어 버렸지 싶었다. 게다가 그는 어쩌다 슬며시 미소를 지을라치면 어김없이 고모님의 찌푸린 눈살에 제지당했다.\n\n\"그 가엾고 불행한 아기가, 도대체 무슨 마가 끼었기에 가서 또 결혼을 했단 말인지,\" 내 이야기가 끝나자 고모님이 말했다. \"나는 도무지 알 수가 없네.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 두 번째 남편을 사랑하게 됐던 거겠죠.\" 딕 씨가 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"사랑에 빠져!\" 고모님이 되받았다. \"그게 무슨 소리예요? 그 애가 무슨 권리로 그런 짓을 해요?\"\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 딕 씨가 잠시 생각하더니 헤벌쭉 웃으며 말했다. \"재미 삼아 그랬는지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"재미라니, 참도!\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"그 가엾은 아기가 이런저런 식으로 자기를 못살게 굴 게 뻔한, 개 같은 작자 따위에게 그 순진한 믿음을 갖다 바친 게 무슨 대단한 재밋거리란 말이에요. 도대체 그 애가 자기 딴엔 뭘 어쩌자는 속셈이었는지, 정말 알고 싶네요! 그 애는 이미 남편이 하나 있었어요. 요람에 누웠을 적부터 늘 밀랍 인형이나 졸졸 쫓아다니던 데이비드 코퍼필드를 저세상으로 떠나보냈고요. 게다가 아기도 하나 있었어요 — 아니, 그 금요일 밤에 여기 앉은 이 애를 낳았을 때, 거기엔 아기가 한 쌍이나 있었지! — 그런데 도대체 뭘 더 바란 거예요?\"\n\n딕 씨가 나를 향해 슬그머니 고개를 가로저었다. 마치 이건 도무지 어쩔 도리가 없는 일이라고 여기는 듯이.\n\n\"그 애는 남들처럼 평범하게 아기 하나 낳는 것조차 못 했다니까요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 애의 누이 베시 트롯우드는 어디 있느냐고요? 코빼기도 안 비쳤어요. 더는 말도 마세요!\"\n\n딕 씨는 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯했다.\n\n\"머리를 한쪽으로 갸우뚱 기울인 그 자그마한 의사 양반,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"젤립스인가 뭔가 하는 이름이었는데, 그 작자는 도대체 뭘 하고 있었답니까? 그자가 한 거라곤 고작, 꼭 울새처럼 — 실제로 그렇게 생겼지만 — 나한테 이렇게 말한 게 다예요. '사내아이로군요.' 사내아이라니! 흥, 그 작자들이란 죄다 어쩜 그리 멍청한지!\"\n\n이 외마디에 실린 열기에 딕 씨는 화들짝 놀랐고, 솔직히 말하면 나도 그랬다.\n\n\"게다가 그것도 모자라, 이 애의 누이 베시 트롯우드의 앞길을 가로막은 것으로도 충분치 않았던지,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 애는 두 번째로 결혼을 해 버렸어요 — 가서 살인자랑, 아니면 그 비슷한 이름을 가진 작자랑 결혼해서는, 이번엔 이 애의 앞길까지 가로막았지 뭐예요! 그래서 당연한 결과로, 아기가 아닌 다음에야 누구라도 내다봤을 일이지만, 이 애가 이렇게 떠돌아다니며 헤매게 된 거예요. 다 자라기 전의 카인이랑 어쩜 이리 똑같은지 모르겠네.\"\n\n딕 씨는 그 인물이 정말 나인지 확인이라도 하려는 듯 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"게다가 그 이교도 이름을 가진 여자 말이에요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 패거티라는 여자도 가서 다음 차례로 결혼을 해 버렸어요. 그런 일에 따르는 화근을 충분히 보지 못해서인지, 글쎄 이 애 말로는 다음으로 가서 결혼을 했다잖아요. 나는 그저 바랄 뿐이에요.\" 고모님이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그 여자 남편이라는 작자가, 신문에 차고 넘치는 부지깽이 남편들 중 하나라서, 부지깽이로 그 여자를 흠씬 두들겨 패 주기를.\"\n\n내 옛 유모가 그렇게 헐뜯기고, 그런 악담의 대상이 되는 것을 차마 들을 수가 없었다. 나는 고모님께, 정말이지 그건 오해라고 말했다. 패거티는 세상에서 가장 착하고, 가장 진실되고, 가장 충직하고, 가장 헌신적이며, 가장 자기를 희생할 줄 아는 벗이자 하녀로, 늘 나를 끔찍이 아껴 주었고, 늘 우리 어머니를 끔찍이 아껴 주었으며, 어머니가 숨을 거둘 때 그 머리를 자기 팔로 받쳐 주었고, 그 얼굴에 어머니가 마지막 감사의 입맞춤을 남긴 사람이라고. 그러다 그 두 사람에 대한 기억이 북받쳐 목이 메었고, 패거티의 집이 곧 내 집이며, 그녀가 가진 모든 것이 곧 내 것이고, 그녀의 보잘것없는 처지가, 행여 그녀에게 누를 끼칠까 두렵게 만들지만 않았어도 나는 그녀에게 몸을 의탁하러 갔을 거라고 말하려는 찰나 — 그 말을 하려던 찰나, 나는 그만 무너져 내렸고, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 식탁에 엎드렸다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"자기 편이 되어 준 사람들 편을 드는 건 옳은 일이지 — 재닛! 당나귀들이야!\"\n\n그 불운한 당나귀들만 아니었다면, 우리는 틀림없이 마음이 잘 통하게 되었으리라고 나는 굳게 믿는다. 고모님이 내 어깨에 손을 얹었고, 나는 그렇게 용기를 얻어, 그녀를 와락 끌어안고 보살펴 달라고 애원하고 싶은 충동이 일었기 때문이다. 하지만 그 방해와, 바깥의 옥신각신으로 그녀가 빠져든 혼란이 당분간 모든 부드러운 생각에 종지부를 찍었고, 고모님은 차 마실 시간이 될 때까지 딕 씨에게 분개하며 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다. 나라의 법에 호소해 배상을 받아 내고, 도버의 당나귀 주인이란 주인은 죄다 무단 침입으로 고소하겠노라는 자신의 결심에 대해서.\n\n차를 마신 뒤, 우리는 창가에 앉아 있었다 — 고모님의 매서운 표정으로 미루어 짐작하건대, 또 다른 침입자들을 망보면서 — 그렇게 땅거미가 질 때까지. 그 무렵 재닛이 식탁에 촛불과 주사위놀이판을 올려놓고 블라인드를 내렸다.\n\n\"자, 딕 씨.\" 고모님이 자못 진지한 표정으로, 아까처럼 집게손가락을 치켜들고 말했다. \"내가 또 한 가지 물어볼 게 있어요. 이 애를 봐요.\"\n\n\"데이비드의 아들이요?\" 딕 씨가 주의 깊으면서도 어리둥절한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그래요.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"이 애를 이제 어떻게 하면 좋겠어요?\"\n\n\"데이비드의 아들을 어떻게 하느냐고요?\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"데이비드의 아들 말이에요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지. 어떻게 하느냐면 — 나라면 이 애를 재우겠어요.\"\n\n\"재닛!\" 고모님이, 앞서 내가 눈여겨봤던 것과 똑같은 흐뭇한 의기양양함을 띠고 외쳤다. \"딕 씨가 우리 모두를 바로잡아 주는구나. 잠자리가 준비됐거든, 이 애를 데리고 올라가자.\"\n\n재닛이 잠자리가 다 준비됐다고 알리자, 나는 위층으로 데려가졌다. 다정하게, 하지만 어딘가 죄수와 같은 모양새로. 고모님이 앞장서고 재닛이 뒤를 따랐다. 나에게 무언가 새로운 희망을 안겨 준 단 하나의 정황은, 고모님이 계단에서 걸음을 멈추고 거기 자욱하게 퍼진 무언가 타는 냄새에 대해 물어본 일이었다. 재닛은 부엌에서 내 헌 셔츠로 부싯깃을 만들던 참이라고 대답했다. 하지만 내 방에는 내가 걸친 그 잡동사니 더미 말고는 다른 옷가지가 없었다. 그리고 정확히 오 분만 타들어 갈 거라고 고모님이 미리 일러 준 자그마한 양초 하나와 함께 그 방에 홀로 남겨졌을 때, 나는 그들이 바깥에서 내 방문을 잠그는 소리를 들었다. 이런저런 일들을 마음속으로 곱씹어 보다가, 나는 나에 대해 아무것도 알 리 없는 고모님이, 어쩌면 내게 도망치는 버릇이 있지 않을까 의심하고, 그래서 나를 안전하게 단속해 두려고 그런 조치를 취한 게 아닐까 싶었다.\n\n그 방은 집의 맨 위층에 자리한, 기분 좋은 방이었다. 바다가 내려다보였고, 그 위로 달이 휘영청 빛나고 있었다. 기도를 마치고 촛불이 다 타 버린 뒤에도, 나는 마치 밝은 책을 읽듯 그 물 위에서 내 운명을 읽어 낼 수 있기라도 한 양, 아니면 어머니가 아기를 안고 천국에서 그 반짝이는 길을 따라 내려와, 내가 마지막으로 보았던 그 사랑스러운 얼굴 그대로 나를 굽어봐 주기라도 할 양, 물 위의 달빛을 하염없이 바라보며 앉아 있던 기억이 난다. 마침내 그 광경에서 눈을 돌렸을 때 나를 사로잡았던 그 엄숙한 기분이, 흰 커튼이 드리워진 침대를 바라보며 — 아니, 그보다 훨씬 더, 그 위에 살그머니 몸을 누이고 눈처럼 새하얀 시트 속에 폭 파묻혔을 때 — 차오른 그 고마움과 안식의 감각에 자리를 내어 주던 것도 기억난다. 밤하늘 아래, 내가 잠들었던 그 모든 외로운 곳들을 떠올리며, 다시는 집 없는 신세가 되지 않기를, 그리고 집 없는 이들을 결코 잊지 않기를 기도하던 것도 기억난다. 그러고는 그때 내가, 바다 위 그 길의 구슬프도록 찬란한 빛을 따라 둥실 떠내려가, 꿈의 세계 속으로 아스라이 흘러가는 것만 같던 그 느낌도 기억난다.\n\n# CHAPTER 14. MY AUNT MAKES UP HER MIND ABOUT ME\n\n아침에 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 고모님이 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 쟁반 위로 어찌나 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있던지, 차 끓이는 통의 물이 찻주전자를 넘쳐흘러 식탁보를 온통 물바다로 만들어 놓은 참이었는데, 내가 들어서자 그제야 고모님의 사색이 흩어졌다. 나는 내가 고모님의 생각거리였다고 확신했고, 나를 어떻게 할 작정인지 그 어느 때보다 간절히 알고 싶었다. 그렇지만 행여 고모님의 비위를 거스를까 봐 그 조바심을 입 밖에 낼 엄두는 나지 않았다.\n\n하지만 내 두 눈은 혀만큼 단속이 되지 않아서, 아침을 먹는 내내 자꾸만 고모님 쪽으로 끌려갔다. 잠깐이라도 고모님을 바라볼라치면 어김없이 고모님도 나를 바라보고 있는 것이었다 — 그 작고 둥근 식탁 맞은편이 아니라 까마득히 먼 곳에라도 있는 사람을 보듯, 묘하게 생각에 잠긴 눈빛으로. 아침을 다 드시고 나자, 고모님은 아주 천천히 의자에 등을 기대고, 눈살을 찌푸리고, 팔짱을 끼고, 어찌나 한곳에 못 박힌 듯 골똘한 눈길로 느긋하게 나를 뜯어보던지, 나는 거북함에 그만 짓눌릴 지경이었다. 나는 아직 내 아침을 다 먹지 못한 터라, 식사를 계속하는 척하며 당황한 기색을 감춰 보려 했다. 그러나 내 나이프가 포크 위로 엎어지고, 포크가 나이프에 걸려 넘어지고, 먹으려고 자르기는커녕 베이컨 조각을 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 높이 공중으로 튕겨 올리고, 차마저 제대로 넘어가지 않고 자꾸만 엉뚱한 데로 넘어가 사레가 들리는 통에, 나는 결국 모든 걸 포기하고, 고모님의 빈틈없는 눈초리 아래 얼굴만 붉힌 채 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 한참 만에 고모님이 말했다.\n\n나는 고개를 들어, 날카롭게 빛나는 그 눈길을 공손히 마주했다.\n\n\"그 사람한테 편지를 보냈다.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"누구한테—요?\"\n\"네 의붓아버지한테 말이다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 단단히 신경 써서 답하라고 편지를 보냈으니, 안 그러면 그자랑 나랑 한판 붙는 수밖에 없다고, 그렇게 일러 두었지!\"\n\n\"제가 어디 있는지 그 사람이 알아요, 고모님?\" 나는 겁이 나서 물었다.\n\n\"내가 알려 줬다.\" 고모님이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"저를—그 사람한테—넘겨주실 건가요?\" 나는 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"모르겠다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"두고 봐야지.\"\n\n\"아! 머드스톤 씨한테 다시 돌아가야 한다면, 저는 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠어요.\" 나는 외쳤다.\n\n\"나도 그건 아무것도 모른다.\" 고모님이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"정말이지 뭐라 말을 못 하겠구나. 두고 봐야지.\"\n\n이 말에 내 마음은 가라앉았고, 나는 몹시 풀이 죽어 가슴이 무거워졌다. 고모님은 나에게 별로 신경 쓰는 기색도 없이, 찬장에서 꺼낸 가슴받이가 달린 거친 앞치마를 두르고, 손수 찻잔을 씻었다. 그리고 모든 것을 다 씻어 쟁반에 도로 정돈하고, 식탁보를 개어 그 위에 얹은 다음, 종을 울려 재닛을 불러 치우게 했다. 그다음에는 (먼저 장갑부터 한 켤레 끼고) 작은 빗자루로 부스러기를 쓸어, 양탄자 위에 티끌 하나 남지 않은 듯 보일 때까지 쓸었다. 그러고는 이미 머리카락 한 올만큼의 빈틈도 없이 먼지를 털고 정돈해 둔 방을, 다시 먼지를 털고 정돈했다. 이 모든 일을 흡족할 만큼 마치고 나자, 고모님은 장갑과 앞치마를 벗어 개키고, 그것들을 꺼냈던 찬장의 그 특정한 구석에 도로 넣은 뒤, 활짝 열린 창가 자기 탁자로 반짇고리를 가져와 자리에 앉았다. 빛과 자기 사이에 초록색 부채를 놓고, 바느질을 시작했다.\n\n\"네가 위층에 좀 올라가 줬으면 한다.\" 고모님이 바늘에 실을 꿰며 말했다. \"그래서 딕 씨에게 내 안부를 전하고, 진정서 작업이 어떻게 되어 가는지 좀 알아봐 다오.\"\n\n나는 이 심부름을 해내려고 냉큼 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"내 짐작에는,\" 바늘에 실을 꿸 때 그 바늘을 살피던 만큼이나 빈틈없는 눈으로 나를 살피며 고모님이 말했다. \"네가 딕 씨를 짧은 이름이라고 여기는 모양인데, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"어제 좀 짧은 이름이라고 생각하긴 했어요.\" 나는 털어놓았다.\n\n\"그 사람이 더 긴 이름이 없어서 그러는 줄 알면 안 된다, 본인이 쓰고 싶기만 하면 얼마든지 쓸 수 있는 이름이 있으니까.\" 고모님이 한결 거만한 태도로 말했다. \"배블리—리처드 배블리 씨—그게 그분의 진짜 이름이야.\"\n\n나는 내 어린 나이와 이미 저지른 무람없음을 겸허히 의식하며, 그분께 그 이름을 온전히 다 불러 드리는 게 낫겠다고 슬며시 말해 볼 참이었는데, 고모님이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"하지만 무슨 일이 있어도 그 이름으로는 부르지 마라. 그 사람은 자기 이름을 견디질 못해. 그게 그 사람의 별난 점이지. 하긴 그게 그리 별난 점이랄 것도 없는 게, 그 이름을 가진 몇몇 사람한테 어찌나 모질게 당했던지, 그 이름을 죽도록 싫어할 만도 하다는 걸 하늘은 아실 게다. 여기서나 다른 어디서나, 이제 그 사람 이름은 딕 씨야—하긴 다른 데를 갈 일이라곤 없는 사람이지만. 그러니 얘야, 조심해서 그 사람을 오직 딕 씨라고만 부르거라.\"\n\n나는 그러겠노라 약속하고, 그 전갈을 가지고 위층으로 올라갔다. 올라가면서 생각하기를, 아까 내가 내려올 때 열린 문틈으로 보았던 그 속도로 딕 씨가 오랫동안 진정서에 매달려 왔다면, 작업이 어지간히 잘 진척되고 있을 게 틀림없다 싶었다. 가 보니 딕 씨는 여전히 긴 펜으로 진정서를 몰아붙이고 있었는데, 머리가 거의 종이에 닿을 지경이었다. 어찌나 골똘했던지, 그가 내가 와 있는 걸 알아채기 전까지, 나는 한쪽 구석의 커다란 종이 연이며, 어수선하게 쌓인 원고 뭉치며, 그 많은 펜이며, 무엇보다 그 엄청난 잉크 양(반 갤런들이 단지로 열 몇 개씩 들여놓은 듯했다)을 느긋이 살펴볼 짬이 충분했다.\n\n\"하! 포이보스로구나!\" 딕 씨가 펜을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"세상은 어떻게 돌아가나? 내가 한마디 해 주마.\" 그가 목소리를 낮춰 덧붙였다. \"남한테 떠벌리고 싶진 않다만, 이건 말이야—\" 여기서 그는 나에게 손짓하더니 입술을 내 귀에 바짝 갖다 댔다. \"미친 세상이야. 정신병원처럼 미쳤다고, 얘야!\" 딕 씨는 탁자 위 둥근 통에서 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 그렇게 말하고는 호탕하게 웃었다.\n\n이 문제에 대해 감히 내 의견을 내놓을 처지는 못 되었으므로, 나는 전갈만 전했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 딕 씨가 대답으로 말했다. \"고모님께 내 안부를 전해 다오, 그리고 나는—나는 이제 첫발을 뗀 것 같다. 첫발을 뗀 것 같아.\" 딕 씨는 희끗한 머리칼 사이로 손을 쓸어 넣으며, 자기 원고를 향해 도무지 자신 없는 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"너 학교에 다녀 봤지?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"잠깐 다녔어요.\"\n\n\"너 그 날짜를 기억하느냐,\" 딕 씨가 나를 진지하게 바라보더니, 받아 적으려고 펜을 집어 들며 말했다. \"찰스 일 세 임금이 목이 잘린 날 말이다.\" 나는 그게 천육백사십구 년에 일어난 일로 알고 있다고 말했다.\n\n\"흠.\" 딕 씨가 펜으로 귀를 긁으며, 미심쩍은 눈으로 나를 보며 대꾸했다. \"책에는 그렇게 나와 있지. 하지만 나는 어떻게 그럴 수 있는지 모르겠어. 만약 그게 그렇게 오래전 일이라면, 그 사람 머리가 잘린 다음에, 그 머릿속에 있던 골칫거리 얼마를 그 곁에 있던 사람들이 어떻게 내 머릿속으로 옮겨 넣는 실수를 저지를 수 있었겠느냐?\"\n\n나는 그 물음에 무척 놀랐으나, 이 점에 대해서는 아무런 정보도 줄 수가 없었다.\n\n\"참 이상한 일이야.\" 딕 씨가 자기 서류를 풀 죽은 눈으로 내려다보며, 다시 머리칼 사이로 손을 넣고 말했다. \"나는 도무지 그걸 제대로 맞춰 볼 수가 없어. 그걸 완벽하게 깔끔히 풀어내질 못하겠단 말이야. 하지만 괜찮아, 괜찮아!\" 그는 기운을 차려 명랑하게 말했다. \"시간은 넉넉하니까! 트롯우드 양께 안부 전해 다오, 일은 아주 잘되어 가고 있다고.\"\n\n내가 막 나가려는데, 그가 연 쪽으로 내 눈길을 끌었다.\n\n\"저 연 어떠냐?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 멋진 연이라고 대답했다. 내 짐작에 그 높이가 칠 피트는 족히 되었을 것 같았다.\n\n\"내가 만들었지. 우리 함께 가서 날려 보자, 너랑 나랑.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"이게 보이느냐?\"\n\n그는 연이 원고로 뒤덮여 있는 걸 나에게 보여 주었는데, 아주 빽빽하고 공들여 쓴 글씨였으나, 어찌나 또렷한지 줄을 따라 읽어 가다 보니, 한두 군데에서 또 찰스 일 세 임금의 머리를 넌지시 가리키는 대목이 보이는 듯싶었다.\n\n\"실이 넉넉히 있어.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"그래서 연이 높이 날면, 사실들을 멀리까지 실어 나르지. 그게 내가 사실들을 퍼뜨리는 방식이란다. 어디로 떨어질지는 나도 몰라. 형편이며, 바람이며, 그런 것들에 달렸으니까. 하지만 나는 거기다 운을 걸어 보는 거지.\"\n\n그의 얼굴이 어찌나 온순하고 흐뭇하던지, 그리고 정정하고 활기차면서도 어딘가 우러러볼 만한 데가 있던지, 나는 그가 나를 데리고 짓궂지 않게 농담을 주고받는 건 아닌가 싶기도 했다. 그래서 내가 웃자 그도 웃었고, 우리는 더없이 좋은 친구가 되어 헤어졌다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야.\" 내가 아래층으로 내려가자 고모님이 말했다. \"오늘 아침 딕 씨는 어떻든?\"\n\n나는 그분이 안부를 전했고, 일이 아주 잘되어 가고 있다고 알려 드렸다.\n\n\"그 사람을 어떻게 생각하느냐?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n나는 아주 좋은 분이라고 생각한다고 대답하여 그 물음을 슬쩍 피해 볼까 하는 어렴풋한 궁리를 했으나, 고모님은 그렇게 넘어갈 분이 아니어서, 바느질감을 무릎에 내려놓고 그 위에 두 손을 포개며 말했다.\n\n\"자! 네 누이 베시 트롯우드라면 누구든 어떻게 생각하는지 당장 나한테 말했을 거다. 되도록 네 누이처럼, 솔직히 말해 보거라!\"\n\n\"그분은—딕 씨는—제가 몰라서 여쭙는 건데요, 고모님—그럼 그분이 좀 정신이 온전치 못하신 건가요?\" 나는 더듬거렸다. 내가 위태로운 땅을 밟고 있다는 걸 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n\"털끝만큼도 아니다.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇군요!\" 나는 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"이 세상에서 딕 씨가 아닌 게 한 가지 있다면,\" 고모님이 아주 단호하고 힘찬 태도로 말했다. \"그게 바로 그거다.\"\n\n나는 또 한 번 머뭇머뭇 \"아, 그렇군요!\" 하는 말밖에 더 나은 대꾸를 내놓지 못했다.\n\n\"그 사람이 미쳤다는 소리를 듣긴 했지.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 그 사람이 미쳤다는 소리를 들었다고 말하는 데에는 이기적인 즐거움이 있단다. 안 그랬으면 지난 십 년 넘게 그 사람과 함께 지내며 그 충고를 누리는 복을 못 누렸을 테니까—사실, 네 누이 베시 트롯우드가 나를 실망시킨 그때 이후로 줄곧 말이다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 오래됐어요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람을 미쳤다고 떠벌릴 만큼 뻔뻔했던 작자들이, 또 그렇게 점잖은 사람들이었지.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"딕 씨는 나하고 먼 친척뻘 되는 사람인데—어떻게 되는지는 상관없으니, 그것까진 들어갈 필요 없고. 만약 내가 아니었으면, 친형이라는 작자가 그 사람을 평생 가둬 놓았을 게다. 그게 다야.\"\n\n내가 위선을 떤 건지도 모르겠지만, 고모님이 이 일에 강한 감정을 품고 있는 걸 보고, 나도 강한 감정을 느끼는 척 애써 보였다.\n\n\"오만한 멍청이 같으니!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"자기 동생이 좀 별나다고—세상의 수많은 사람들 절반만큼도 안 별난데도—집 안에 그 사람이 어른거리는 걸 싫어해서, 어디 사설 요양원 같은 데로 내쫓아 버렸어. 돌아가신 자기네 아버지가, 그 애가 거의 천치나 다름없다고 여겨, 특별히 잘 보살피라고 그 형한테 맡겨 두었는데도 말이다. 그렇게 생각했다니, 그 아버지도 어지간히 현명한 양반이었나 보지! 본인이야말로 미친 게 분명해.\"\n\n또다시, 고모님이 아주 확신에 차 보였으므로, 나도 아주 확신하는 양 애써 보였다.\n\n\"그래서 내가 끼어들었지.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그래서 그 사람한테 제안을 했어. 내가 그랬다. '당신 동생은 멀쩡해—당신보다, 또 당신이 앞으로 될 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 멀쩡해, 그러길 바라야겠지만. 그 사람 몫의 자그마한 수입은 그대로 두고, 나한테 와서 살게 해 줘요. 나는 그 사람이 무섭지 않고, 잘난 척할 것도 없고, 그 사람을 보살필 준비가 됐고, 몇몇 사람들(요양원 사람들 말고도)이 그랬던 것처럼 그 사람을 함부로 대하지도 않을 테니.' 한참을 옥신각신한 끝에,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 그 사람을 데려왔지. 그 뒤로 줄곧 여기 있는 거야. 그 사람은 세상에 둘도 없이 다정하고 고분고분한 사람이란다. 충고로 말할 것 같으면!—하지만 그 사람 머릿속이 어떤지는 나 말고는 아무도 몰라.\"\n\n고모님은 옷자락을 매만지고 고개를 한 번 흔들었는데, 마치 옷자락에서는 온 세상에 맞서는 도전을 매만져 펴 버리고, 머리에서는 그걸 떨쳐 버리기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그 사람한테는 아끼는 누이가 하나 있었어.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"착한 사람이었고, 그 사람한테 무척 잘해 줬지. 그런데 그 여자도 다들 하는 짓을 했어—남편을 얻은 거야. 그리고 그 남편이라는 작자도 다들 하는 짓을 했지—그 여자를 비참하게 만든 거야. 그게 딕 씨의 마음에 어찌나 영향을 끼쳤던지(이건 미친 게 아니길 바란다!), 그 일이 형에 대한 두려움과 형이 모질게 굴었다는 생각과 겹치면서, 그 사람을 열병으로 몰아넣었어. 그게 나한테 오기 전 일이지만, 그 기억은 지금도 그 사람을 짓누르고 있어. 얘야, 그 사람이 너한테 찰스 일 세 임금에 대해 무슨 말을 하더냐?\"\n\n\"네, 고모님.\"\n\n\"아!\" 고모님이 약간 짜증이 난 듯 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"그게 그 사람이 그 일을 빗대어 표현하는 방식이야. 그 사람은 자기 병을 어떤 큰 소동이며 동요와 연결 짓는데, 그건 당연한 일이고, 그래서 그 비유든, 그 직유든, 뭐라고 부르든 간에, 그걸 골라 쓰는 거란다. 본인이 그게 옳다고 여긴다면, 그러지 못할 게 뭐가 있겠니!\"\n\n나는 말했다. \"그럼요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"그게 사무적인 말투는 아니지.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"세속적인 말투도 아니고. 나도 그건 안다. 그래서 내가 우기는 거야, 그 사람 진정서에는 그 얘기가 한마디도 들어가선 안 된다고 말이다.\"\n\n\"그분이 쓰고 계신 게 자기 신상에 관한 진정서예요, 고모님?\"\n\n\"그렇단다, 얘야.\" 고모님이 다시 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"그 사람은 대법관님인지, 무슨 무슨 경인지—하여간 진정을 받는 대가로 봉급을 받는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나한테, 자기 사정을 진정하고 있는 거야. 머잖아 그게 들어가긴 하겠지. 아직은 그런 식의 표현을 끼워 넣지 않고서는 작성을 못 하고 있다만, 그건 상관없어. 그 일이 그 사람을 바쁘게 해 주니까.\"\n\n실은, 나중에 알게 된 것인데, 딕 씨는 십 년 넘게 찰스 일 세 임금을 진정서에서 빼내려고 애써 왔으나, 그 임금은 줄곧 슬그머니 끼어들었고, 지금도 그 안에 들어 있었다.\n\n\"내가 다시 말하지만,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 사람 머릿속이 어떤지는 나 말고는 아무도 몰라. 그리고 그 사람은 세상에 둘도 없이 고분고분하고 다정한 사람이야. 가끔 연을 날리고 싶어 한들, 그게 뭐 어떻단 말이냐! 프랭클린도 연을 날리곤 했어. 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그 사람은 퀘이커교도였든지 그 비슷한 거였지. 그리고 퀘이커교도가 연을 날리는 게, 다른 누가 그러는 것보다 훨씬 더 우스꽝스러운 광경이고 말이다.\"\n\n만약 고모님이 이런 시시콜콜한 사정을 오로지 나를 위해, 또 나를 믿고 털어놓는 것이라고 생각할 수 있었다면, 나는 무척 영광스러웠을 테고, 그렇게 나를 좋게 보아 주는 표시에서 길조를 점쳤을 것이다. 그러나 고모님이 이 이야기에 빠져든 것은, 비록 곁에 다른 사람이 없어서 나를 상대로 말을 건넨 것이긴 해도, 주로 그 물음이 고모님 자신의 마음속에서 떠올랐기 때문이지 나하고는 별 상관이 없다는 것을, 나는 알아채지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그러는 한편, 한마디 해 두자면, 가엾고 해롭잖은 딕 씨를 너그러이 두둔하는 고모님의 그 마음씨는, 내 어린 가슴에 나 자신을 위한 어떤 이기적인 희망을 불어넣었을 뿐 아니라, 고모님을 향해서도 사심 없는 따스함으로 데워 주었다. 나는 비로소, 고모님의 그 숱한 별스러움과 괴팍한 변덕에도 불구하고, 고모님에게는 존경하고 믿을 만한 무언가가 있다는 걸 알기 시작한 것 같았다. 그날도 고모님은 어제만큼이나 매서웠고, 당나귀 때문에 들락날락하기를 똑같이 잦게 했고, 어떤 젊은 사내가 지나가다 창가의 재닛에게 추파를 던지자(이는 고모님의 위엄에 대한 가장 중대한 위반 행위 가운데 하나였다) 무시무시한 분노에 휩싸였지만, 그래도 나에게는, 두려움이 줄어든 건 아닐지언정, 더 큰 존경을 자아내는 분으로 보였다.\n\n머드스톤 씨에게 보낸 편지의 답장이 도착하기까지 자연히 흘러야 했던 그 사이에, 내가 겪은 불안은 이루 말할 수 없었다. 그러나 나는 그것을 억누르려 애썼고, 조용한 방식으로나마 고모님과 딕 씨 두 분 모두에게 되도록 싹싹하게 굴려고 했다. 딕 씨와 나는 그 커다란 연을 날리러 밖에 나가고 싶었지만, 나에게는 첫날 입혀진 그 도무지 볼품없는 옷가지밖에 다른 옷이 없어, 잠자리에 들기 전 고모님이 내 건강을 위해 한 시간씩 바깥 절벽 위로 나를 데리고 거니는 어둑한 때를 빼고는, 줄곧 집 안에 갇혀 있었다. 마침내 머드스톤 씨의 답장이 왔고, 고모님은 나에게, 이루 말할 수 없는 두려움을 안기며, 그가 이튿날 직접 자기를 만나러 온다고 알려 주었다. 이튿날, 여전히 그 괴상한 옷가지에 둘둘 싸인 채, 나는 가라앉는 희망과 솟아오르는 두려움이 안에서 다투는 통에 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채로, 시간을 헤아리며 앉아 있었다. 그리고 그 음울한 얼굴이 나타나면 깜짝 놀랄 줄 알면서 기다렸는데, 그자가 나타나지 않는 그 한순간 한순간이 도리어 나를 놀라게 했다.\n\n고모님은 평소보다 조금 더 위압적이고 근엄했으나, 내가 그토록 두려워하는 손님을 맞을 채비를 한다는 다른 기색은 보이지 않았다. 고모님은 창가에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있었고, 나는 그 곁에 앉아, 머드스톤 씨의 방문이 가져올 가능한 결과며 불가능한 결과며 온갖 것에 마음이 갈팡질팡한 채로, 오후도 꽤 늦은 시각까지 그러고 있었다. 저녁 식사는 기약 없이 미뤄진 참이었으나, 시간이 어찌나 늦어졌던지 고모님이 식사를 준비하라고 일러 두었을 무렵, 갑자기 당나귀가 나타났다고 경보를 울렸다. 그러고는, 나의 경악과 놀라움 속에, 나는 머드스톤 양이 여자용 안장에 올라타고 그 신성한 풀밭을 태연히 가로질러 와서는, 집 앞에 멈춰 서서 둘레를 둘러보는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"썩 꺼지지 못해!\" 고모님이 창문을 향해 고개를 흔들고 주먹을 휘두르며 소리쳤다. \"거기 들어올 일 없어. 어디 함부로 침범이야? 썩 가! 아! 이 뻔뻔한 것이!\"\n\n머드스톤 양이 둘레를 둘러보는 그 태연함에 고모님은 어찌나 분통이 터졌던지, 정말이지 그 순간만큼은 평소처럼 뛰쳐나가지 못하고 그대로 굳어 버린 게 아닌가 싶었다. 나는 그 틈을 타, 저 사람이 누구인지, 그리고 지금 그 침입자에게 다가오는 신사(올라오는 길이 몹시 가팔라서 그가 뒤처져 있었다)가 바로 머드스톤 씨라고 고모님께 알려 드렸다.\n\n\"그게 누구든 난 상관없어!\" 고모님이 여전히 고개를 흔들며, 활 모양 퇴창에서 도무지 환영이라고는 할 수 없는 손짓을 해 대며 소리쳤다. \"침범당하지 않을 테야. 절대로 안 돼. 썩 꺼져! 재닛, 저놈을 돌려세워. 끌고 가 버려!\" 그러자 고모님 등 뒤에서, 나는 일종의 급박한 전투 장면을 보았다. 당나귀는 네 다리를 제각각 다른 방향으로 떡 버틴 채 모두에게 버티고 섰고, 재닛은 고삐를 잡아 돌려세우려 했고, 머드스톤 씨는 끌고 나아가려 했고, 머드스톤 양은 양산으로 재닛을 후려쳤고, 이 한판 싸움을 구경하러 몰려든 사내아이 몇몇은 기운차게 소리를 질러 댔다. 그러나 고모님은, 그 무리 가운데 갑자기 그 당나귀의 보호자인 어린 악당을 알아보았는데, 그놈은 아직 열 살 남짓밖에 안 됐는데도 고모님에게 가장 뿌리 깊은 위반자 가운데 하나였던지라, 싸움터로 쏜살같이 달려 나가 그놈을 덮쳤다. 그러고는 그놈을 붙잡아, 윗도리를 머리 위로 뒤집어씌운 채, 발꿈치가 땅에 질질 끌리도록 정원으로 끌고 들어와, 재닛더러 순경과 치안판사를 데려와 그놈을 잡아다 그 자리에서 재판해 처형할 수 있게 하라고 외치며, 거기서 그놈을 옴짝달싹 못 하게 붙들었다. 그러나 이 대목은 오래가지 않았으니, 그 어린 악동은 고모님이 짐작도 못 하는 갖가지 속임수와 잔꾀에 능했던지라, 이내 휘파람을 불며 달아나면서, 화단에 못 박힌 장화 자국을 몇 군데 깊이 남기고, 자기 당나귀를 의기양양하게 끌고 가 버렸다.\n\n머드스톤 양은 싸움이 뒷부분에 이를 무렵 말에서 내려, 이제 고모님이 자기들을 맞이할 여유가 생길 때까지 오라비와 함께 층계 아래에서 기다리고 있었다. 고모님은 싸움으로 조금 헝클어진 채로, 대단한 위엄을 차리고 그들 곁을 지나 집 안으로 행진해 들어가서는, 재닛이 그들의 방문을 알릴 때까지 그들이 있는 줄도 모르는 체했다.\n\n\"제가 나가 있을까요, 고모님?\" 나는 떨면서 물었다.\n\n\"아니다, 얘야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"절대 안 돼!\" 그러면서 고모님은 나를 자기 곁 한 구석으로 밀어 넣고, 마치 그곳이 감옥이나 피고석이라도 되는 양 의자로 나를 둘러막았다. 나는 면담이 진행되는 내내 이 자리를 지켰고, 이 자리에서 나는 이제 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 방으로 들어서는 걸 보았다.\n\n\"아!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 처음에는 누구한테 항의하는 기쁨을 누리는 건지 미처 몰랐군요. 하지만 나는 누구든 저 풀밭 위로 말을 타고 지나가는 걸 그냥 두지 않아요. 예외는 없어요. 누구한테도 그러게 두지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"댁의 규칙이 낯선 사람한테는 꽤 거북하군요.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다.\n\n\"그래요!\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n머드스톤 씨는 적대 행위가 다시 불붙을까 두려운 듯, 끼어들어 입을 열었다.\n\n\"트롯우드 양!\"\n\n\"실례합니다만,\" 고모님이 날카로운 눈빛으로 말했다. \"당신이, 우리 죽은 조카 데이비드 코퍼필드, 그러니까 블런더스톤 까마귀 둥지의 그 사람의 미망인과 결혼한 그 머드스톤 씨로군요!—그런데 왜 까마귀 둥지인지는 나도 모르겠지만!\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이런 말씀 드려 미안합니다만, 선생.\" 고모님이 받아쳤다. \"당신이 그 가엾은 아이를 그냥 내버려 뒀더라면 훨씬 더 좋고 행복한 일이 됐을 거라고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"트롯우드 양께서 말씀하신 바에 그만큼은 저도 동의합니다.\" 머드스톤 양이 거만하게 고개를 곧추세우며 말했다. \"저는 우리의 애통한 클라라가, 모든 본질적인 면에서, 한낱 어린애였다고 보거든요.\"\n\n\"그건 우리 같은 사람들한테는 위안이 되겠군요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나이가 들어 가는 데다, 제 미모로 누굴 불행하게 만들 일도 없을 우리한테는, 우리를 두고는 아무도 그런 소리를 할 수 없을 테니 말이에요.\"\n\n\"물론이지요!\" 머드스톤 양이 대꾸했으나, 내 생각엔 그리 선뜻 우러나오거나 우아한 동의는 아니었다. \"그리고 정말이지, 말씀대로, 제 오라비가 그런 결혼에 발을 들이지 않았더라면 더 좋고 행복한 일이 됐을 겁니다. 저는 늘 그렇게 생각해 왔어요.\"\n\n\"당연히 그러셨겠지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"재닛,\" 종을 울리며, \"딕 씨께 내 안부를 전하고, 좀 내려와 달라고 청해 다오.\"\n\n그가 내려올 때까지, 고모님은 더없이 꼿꼿하고 뻣뻣하게 앉아, 벽을 노려보고 있었다. 그가 오자, 고모님은 소개 절차를 치렀다.\n\n\"딕 씨예요. 오래된 절친한 벗이지요. 그분의 판단을,\" 고모님이, 검지를 깨물며 다소 멍청한 표정을 짓고 있던 딕 씨에게 일러 두려는 듯, 힘주어 말했다. \"나는 믿습니다.\"\n\n이 귀띔에 딕 씨는 입에서 손가락을 빼고, 진지하고 주의 깊은 표정을 지으며 무리 가운데 섰다.\n\n고모님이 머드스톤 씨를 향해 고개를 까딱하자, 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"트롯우드 양. 당신의 편지를 받고, 저는 그것이 저 자신에 대한 더 큰 정의이며, 어쩌면 당신에 대한 더 큰 예의라고 여겨—\"\n\n\"고맙군요.\" 고모님이 여전히 날카롭게 그를 노려보며 말했다. \"나는 신경 쓸 것 없어요.\"\n\n\"여정이 아무리 불편할지언정, 편지로가 아니라 직접 만나 답하는 게 옳다고 여겼습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말을 이었다. \"제 벗들과 제 일자리에서 달아난 이 가엾은 아이는—\"\n\n\"그리고 이 아이의 꼴이,\" 그의 누이가 형언할 수 없는 옷차림의 나를 가리키며 끼어들었다. \"참으로 망측하고 창피스럽기 짝이 없지요.\"\n\n\"제인 머드스톤,\" 그의 오라비가 말했다. \"내 말을 끊지 말아 주면 고맙겠다. 이 가엾은 아이는, 트롯우드 양, 적잖은 집안의 말썽과 골칫거리의 원인이 돼 왔습니다. 내 사랑하는 죽은 아내가 살아 있는 동안에도, 그 뒤로도요. 이 아이는 음침하고 반항적인 기질에, 사나운 성미에, 비뚤어지고 다루기 힘든 성품을 지녔습니다. 제 누이와 저는 이 아이의 악덕을 바로잡으려 애썼으나, 헛수고였지요. 그리고 저는 느꼈습니다—우리 둘 다 느꼈다고 해도 좋겠군요, 제 누이는 제 속을 온전히 알고 있으니—이 진중하고 냉정한 단언을 우리 입으로 당신께 들려 드리는 게 옳다고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제 오라비가 말한 것을 제가 굳이 확인해 드릴 필요는 없겠지요.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"하지만 한마디 짚어 두자면, 세상의 모든 사내아이 가운데, 저는 이 아이가 가장 못된 아이라고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"심한 말이군!\" 고모님이 짧게 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 사실에 비하면 조금도 심한 말이 아니지요.\" 머드스톤 양이 받아쳤다.\n\n\"하!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그래서요, 선생?\"\n\n\"저에게는 이 아이를 기르는 최선의 방법에 관한 제 나름의 견해가 있습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 다시 말을 이었는데, 그와 고모님이 서로를 빤히 뜯어볼수록 그의 얼굴은 점점 더 어두워졌다. \"그 견해는 일부는 이 아이에 대한 제 앎에, 일부는 제 형편과 재산에 대한 제 앎에 바탕을 둔 것입니다. 저는 그 견해에 대해 제 자신에게 책임이 있고, 그에 따라 행동하며, 그에 대해 더는 말하지 않겠습니다. 제가 이 아이를 점잖은 사업에 종사하는 제 벗의 눈 아래 맡겨 두었고, 이 아이는 그게 마음에 들지 않아, 그곳에서 달아나, 시골을 떠돌며 한낱 부랑아가 되고, 누더기를 걸치고 여기까지 와서 당신께 호소하고 있다, 그것으로 충분합니다. 저는 당신이 이 호소를 부추긴 데서 비롯될 정확한 결과를—제가 아는 한도 안에서—당신께 떳떳이 말씀드리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 점잖은 사업부터 짚어 봅시다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 아이가 당신의 친자식이었더라도, 마찬가지로 그 일을 시켰을 거라고 보는데요, 아닌가요?\"\n\n\"이 아이가 제 오라비의 친자식이었다면,\" 머드스톤 양이 끼어들며 말했다. \"그 성품이 아주 달랐을 거라고 저는 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"아니면 이 가엾은 아이의 어머니가 아직 살아 있었더라도, 이 아이는 그 점잖은 사업에 들어갔을 텐가요, 그래요?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각에는,\" 머드스톤 씨가 고개를 까딱이며 말했다. \"저와 제 누이 제인 머드스톤이 가장 좋다고 합의한 것이라면, 클라라는 무엇 하나 따지지 않았을 겁니다.\"\n\n머드스톤 양이 들릴 만한 중얼거림으로 이 말을 확인해 주었다.\n\n\"흥!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"불쌍한 아기!\"\n\n이 내내 자기 돈을 짤랑거리던 딕 씨가, 이제 어찌나 요란하게 짤랑거리던지, 고모님은 다음 말을 꺼내기 전에 눈짓으로 그를 말려야겠다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"그 가엾은 아이의 연금은 그 애가 죽으면서 함께 끝났겠지요?\"\n\n\"그 사람과 함께 끝났습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 그 자그마한 재산—집과 정원—까마귀 한 마리 없는데 까마귀 둥지라나 뭐라나 하는 그것을, 그 애의 아들 몫으로 남기는 합의 같은 건 없었고요?\"\n\n\"그것은 그 사람의 첫 남편이 아무 조건 없이 그 사람에게 남긴 것이었습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 입을 열자, 고모님이 더없이 발끈하며 못 참겠다는 듯 말허리를 잘랐다.\n\n\"맙소사, 이 양반아, 그런 말은 할 것도 없어요. 아무 조건 없이 남기다니! 데이비드 코퍼필드가 무슨 조건이든, 어떤 종류의 조건이든 미리 내다봤을 것 같소, 그게 눈앞에 빤히 들이닥쳤더라도 말이오! 당연히 아무 조건 없이 남겼겠지. 하지만 그 애가 재혼했을 때—요컨대, 당신과 결혼하는 그 더없이 끔찍한 걸음을 내디뎠을 때,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"솔직히 말해서—그때 그 애 아들을 위해 한마디 거들어 준 사람은 아무도 없었소?\"\n\n\"제 죽은 아내는 두 번째 남편을 사랑했고, 부인,\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"그를 온전히 믿었습니다.\"\n\n\"당신의 죽은 아내는, 선생, 더없이 세상 물정 모르고, 더없이 불행하고, 더없이 불운한 아기였소.\" 고모님이 그를 향해 고개를 흔들며 받아쳤다. \"그게 그 애였소. 자, 그래서 그다음엔 또 무슨 할 말이 있소?\"\n\n\"그저 이겁니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"저는 데이비드를 도로 데려가려고 여기 왔습니다—아무 조건 없이 데려가서, 제가 옳다고 여기는 대로 처분하고, 제가 옳다고 여기는 대로 다루려고 말입니다. 저는 누구에게도 무슨 약속을 하거나 무슨 다짐을 하려고 온 게 아닙니다. 트롯우드 양, 당신은 어쩌면 이 아이가 달아난 것이며 당신께 호소한 것을 부추길 생각을 품고 있을지도 모르겠습니다. 당신의 태도가, 솔직히 말해 화해를 꾀하는 것으로는 도무지 보이지 않으니, 그럴 법도 하다는 생각이 듭니다. 자, 미리 경고해 두는데, 당신이 이 아이를 한번 부추기면, 영영 부추기는 셈입니다. 지금 당신이 이 아이와 저 사이에 끼어든다면, 트롯우드 양, 당신은 영원히 끼어들어야 합니다. 저는 장난칠 줄도 모르고, 저를 가지고 장난치게 둘 사람도 아닙니다. 저는 처음이자 마지막으로 이 아이를 데려가려고 여기 왔습니다. 이 아이는 갈 준비가 됐습니까? 만약 그렇지 않다면—당신이 그렇지 않다고 한다면, 무슨 핑계로든, 무슨 핑계인지는 제게 상관없습니다만—제 문은 이제부터 이 아이에게 닫혀 있을 테고, 당신 문은, 당연히, 이 아이에게 열려 있겠지요.\"\n\n이 말에, 고모님은 더없이 꼿꼿이 앉아, 두 손을 한쪽 무릎에 포갠 채, 말하는 이를 험상궂게 노려보며 빈틈없이 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 그가 말을 마치자, 고모님은 자세는 전혀 흩뜨리지 않고 눈만 돌려 머드스톤 양을 똑바로 겨누며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 부인, 당신은 뭐 할 말이 있소?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 트롯우드 양.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"제가 할 수 있는 말은 제 오라비가 죄다 훌륭하게 해 주었고, 제가 사실로 아는 바도 다 그가 똑똑히 밝혔으니, 저는 당신의 정중함에 감사드린다는 말 외에 덧붙일 게 없군요. 정말이지 그 대단한 정중함에 말이에요.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했는데, 그 빈정거림은, 내가 채텀에서 그 곁에 잠들었던 대포를 흔들지 못했던 만큼이나 고모님을 흔들지 못했다.\n\n\"그래, 이 아이는 뭐라고 하느냐?\" 고모님이 말했다. \"갈 준비가 됐느냐, 데이비드?\"\n\n나는 싫다고 대답하며, 제발 저를 보내지 말아 달라고 고모님께 간청했다. 나는 머드스톤 씨도 머드스톤 양도 나를 좋아한 적이 없고, 나에게 다정했던 적도 없다고 말했다. 늘 나를 끔찍이 사랑했던 우리 엄마를 그들이 나 때문에 불행하게 만들었고, 나는 그걸 잘 알고 있으며, 패거티도 알고 있다고 말했다. 나는 내가 얼마나 어린지만 아는 사람이라면 누구도 믿지 못할 만큼 비참했다고 말했다. 그리고 나는 고모님께, 우리 아버지를 봐서라도 나를 벗 삼아 지켜 달라고 빌고 또 빌었다—지금은 그게 무슨 말이었는지 잊었지만, 그때는 그 말들이 나 자신을 몹시 북받치게 했던 게 기억난다.\n\n\"딕 씨,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 아이를 어쩌면 좋겠어요?\"\n\n딕 씨는 곰곰 생각하고, 머뭇거리고, 얼굴이 환해지더니, 대꾸했다. \"당장 옷 한 벌 맞추게 치수를 재게 하시죠.\"\n\n\"딕 씨,\" 고모님이 의기양양하게 말했다. \"손 좀 줘 봐요, 당신의 그 상식은 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼 귀하니까.\" 고모님은 더없이 다정하게 그 손을 흔들고 나서, 나를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 머드스톤 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"가고 싶을 때 가시오. 나는 이 아이에게 운을 걸어 보겠소. 이 아이가 당신 말 그대로라면, 그때는 적어도 당신이 한 만큼은 나도 이 아이를 위해 해 줄 수 있을 테니. 하지만 나는 그 말을 한마디도 안 믿어요.\"\n\n\"트롯우드 양,\" 머드스톤 씨가 일어서며 어깨를 으쓱하고 대꾸했다. \"당신이 신사였더라면—\"\n\n\"흥! 헛소리에 어림없는 소리!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나한테 말 걸지 말아요!\"\n\n\"어쩌면 이리도 절묘하게 공손하실까!\" 머드스톤 양이 일어서며 외쳤다. \"정말이지, 압도적이네요!\"\n\n\"내가 모를 줄 아시오,\" 고모님이 그 누이는 들은 척도 않고, 계속 그 오라비를 향해 말하며, 한없는 표정을 담아 그를 향해 고개를 흔들며 말했다. \"당신이 그 가엾고 불행하고 그릇 이끌린 아기를 어떤 삶으로 몰아넣었을지를? 그 여리고 자그마한 사람이 당신과 처음 마주친 게 얼마나 비통한 날이었을지를 내가 모를 줄 아시오?—거위한테 '워이!' 소리 한 번 못 칠 사람인 양, 능글맞게 웃으며 그 애한테 눈웃음을 쳐 댔겠지, 내 장담하건대!\"\n\n\"이렇게 우아한 말은 난생처음 듣네!\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 직접 본 것처럼 훤히 꿰뚫고 있다는 걸 모를 줄 아시오,\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"이제 정말로 당신을 보고 듣고 있는 마당에—솔직히 말하건대, 그건 나한테 도무지 기쁜 일이 아니지만 말이오? 아, 그렇고말고, 맙소사! 처음엔 머드스톤 씨만큼 매끄럽고 비단결 같은 사람이 어디 있었겠소! 그 가엾고 어리석은 순진한 사람은 그런 남자를 본 적이 없었지. 당신은 달콤함 그 자체였어. 당신은 그 애를 떠받들었지. 그 애의 아들을 끔찍이 아꼈고—한없이 다정하게 아꼈고말고! 당신은 그 애한테 또 하나의 아버지가 되어 줄 거였고, 다들 장미 정원에서 오순도순 함께 살 거였지, 안 그랬소? 으윽! 썩 꺼지시오, 어서!\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"내 평생 이런 사람은 처음 봐!\" 머드스톤 양이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 그 가엾은 어린 바보를 손에 넣었다 확신하고 나자,\" 고모님이 말했다—\"그 애를 그렇게 부르다니 하느님 용서하소서, 게다가 그 애는 당신이 서둘러 갈 일 없는 그곳으로 가 버렸는데—당신은 그 애와 그 애의 식구한테 아직 충분히 못된 짓을 못 했다 싶어, 그 애를 길들이기 시작해야만 했지, 안 그렇소? 가엾은 새장 속 새처럼 그 애를 꺾어 버리고, 당신의 가락을 노래하라고 가르치며 그 속아 넘어간 목숨을 시들어 가게 해야만 했지?\"\n\n\"이건 미친 거 아니면 취한 거야.\" 머드스톤 양이 고모님의 말줄기를 자기 쪽으로 돌리지 못해 완전히 안달이 나서 말했다. \"내 짐작엔 취한 게 분명해.\"\n\n베시 아주머니는 이 끼어듦에는 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳없이, 그런 일이라곤 없었던 양 계속 머드스톤 씨만 상대로 말했다.\n\n\"머드스톤 씨,\" 고모님이 그를 향해 손가락질하며 말했다. \"당신은 그 순진한 아기한테 폭군이었고, 그 애의 가슴을 찢어 놓았소. 그 애는 사랑이 넘치는 아기였소—나는 그걸 알아요. 당신이 그 애를 보기 몇 해 전부터 알고 있었지—그리고 당신은 그 애의 가장 여린 약점을 파고들어, 그 애가 그 때문에 죽은 그 상처를 입혔소. 당신 좋을 대로 받아들이든 말든, 위안 삼으라고 일러 주는 진실이오. 당신과 당신의 도구들이나 그걸 실컷 우려먹든지.\"\n\n\"한 가지 여쭙겠는데요, 트롯우드 양.\" 머드스톤 양이 끼어들었다. \"제가 익숙지 않은 그 골라 쓰신 말솜씨로, 누구를 제 오라비의 도구라고 부르시는 건지요?\"\n\n\"내가 말했듯이, 당신이 그 애를 보기 몇 해 전부터 빤한 일이었소—그리고 섭리의 그 알 수 없는 작정 속에서 당신이 대체 어쩌다 그 애를 보게 됐는지는, 사람의 머리로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 일이지만—그 가엾고 여린 자그마한 사람이 언젠가는 누군가와 결혼하리라는 건 빤한 일이었소. 하지만 나는 일이 이렇게까지 고약하게 풀리지는 않기를 바랐단 말이오. 그때였소, 머드스톤 씨, 그 애가 여기서 이 아이를 낳은 게,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"당신이 나중에 그 애를 괴롭힐 때 그 빌미로 삼았던 이 가엾은 아이를 말이오. 그건 떠올리기조차 거북한 기억이라, 이제 이 아이를 보는 것마저 역겹게 만드는구려. 그래, 그래! 움찔할 것 없소!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그러지 않아도 그게 사실이라는 건 나도 아니까.\"\n\n그는 이 내내 문가에 서서, 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 고모님을 지켜보고 있었으나, 그 검은 눈썹은 무겁게 찌푸려져 있었다. 나는 이제, 미소는 여전히 그 얼굴에 떠 있었지만, 그의 핏기가 한순간에 가셨고, 마치 달음박질이라도 친 사람처럼 숨을 몰아쉬는 듯한 걸 알아챘다.\n\n\"안녕히 가시오, 선생.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그리고 잘 가시오! 당신도 안녕히 가시고, 부인.\" 고모님이 갑자기 그 누이에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"다시 한번 내 풀밭 위로 당나귀를 타고 지나가기만 해 봐, 당신 어깨 위에 머리가 붙어 있는 게 확실한 만큼이나 확실하게, 내 당신 모자를 후려쳐 떨어뜨리고 그걸 짓밟아 줄 테니!\"\n\n이 너무도 뜻밖의 선언을 내뱉을 때의 고모님 얼굴과, 그것을 들을 때의 머드스톤 양 얼굴을 그려 내려면, 화가가, 그것도 여간한 화가가 아니라 대단한 화가가 필요할 것이다. 그러나 그 말씨는 그 내용 못지않게 어찌나 불같았던지, 머드스톤 양은 대꾸 한마디 없이, 슬며시 오라비의 팔짱을 끼고 거만하게 오두막을 나섰다. 고모님은 창가에 남아 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보았는데, 당나귀가 다시 나타날 경우에 대비해, 그 위협을 즉시 실행에 옮길 채비를 하고 있었으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다.\n\n그러나 아무런 도전의 기미도 보이지 않자, 고모님의 얼굴은 차츰 풀어져 어찌나 흐뭇해졌던지, 나는 용기를 내어 고모님께 입을 맞추고 감사를 드렸다. 나는 두 팔을 고모님 목에 둘러 감고, 더없이 진심으로 그렇게 했다. 그러고 나서 나는 딕 씨와 악수를 나눴는데, 딕 씨는 나와 몇 번이고 악수하며, 이 즐거운 결말을 거듭거듭 웃음을 터뜨리며 반겨 주었다.\n\n\"딕 씨, 당신이 나와 함께 이 아이의 공동 후견인이 되어 주는 걸로 알겠어요.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"저야 더없이 기쁘지요.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"데이비드 아들의 후견인이 된다니요.\"\n\n\"좋아요.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"그럼 그걸로 정해졌고. 내가 생각해 봤는데 말이에요, 딕 씨, 이 아이를 트롯우드라고 부르면 어떨까 해요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 그럼요. 트롯우드라고 부르세요, 그럼요.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"데이비드 아들은 트롯우드.\"\n\n\"트롯우드 코퍼필드라는 뜻이에요.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"네, 그렇고말고요. 네. 트롯우드 코퍼필드.\" 딕 씨가 조금 머쓱해하며 말했다.\n\n고모님은 이 생각이 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 그날 오후에 나를 위해 사 온 기성복 몇 벌에, 내가 그것을 입기도 전에, 고모님이 손수, 지워지지 않는 표시용 잉크로, '트롯우드 코퍼필드'라고 표시를 해 두었다. 그리고 나를 위해 새로 짓기로 주문한 다른 옷가지들(그날 오후에 한 벌 빠짐없이 갖춰진 차림새를 통째로 맞췄다)도 죄다 같은 식으로 표시하기로 정해졌다.\n\n이리하여 나는 새 이름으로, 온통 새로운 것들에 둘러싸여 새 삶을 시작했다. 이제 막막함의 시간이 끝나고 나니, 나는 여러 날 동안 꿈을 꾸는 사람 같았다. 나는 고모님과 딕 씨라는, 한 쌍의 진기한 후견인을 두었다는 생각 따위는 한 번도 하지 않았다. 나는 나 자신에 관해서도 무엇 하나 또렷이 생각하지 않았다. 내 마음속에 가장 또렷한 두 가지는, 옛 블런더스톤 시절이 아득히 멀어져—헤아릴 수 없는 거리의 안개 속에 누워 있는 듯했다는 것, 그리고 머드스톤 앤드 그린비에서의 내 삶 위로 영영 막이 내려졌다는 것이었다. 그 후로 아무도 그 막을 걷어 올린 적이 없다. 나는 이 이야기 속에서조차, 마지못한 손길로 잠깐 그것을 들어 올렸다가, 기꺼이 도로 내려놓았다. 그 삶의 기억은 나에게 너무도 많은 고통과, 너무도 많은 마음의 괴로움과 절망으로 가득 차 있어서, 나는 내가 얼마나 오래 그 삶을 살도록 운명 지어졌었는지 헤아려 볼 엄두조차 한 번도 내지 못했다. 그것이 일 년 동안 이어졌는지, 그보다 길었는지, 짧았는지, 나는 모른다. 다만 그것이 있었고, 그쳤다는 것, 그리고 내가 그것을 적었으니, 이제 거기 그대로 두려 한다는 것만 알 뿐이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 15. I MAKE ANOTHER BEGINNING\n\n딕 씨와 나는 곧 둘도 없는 벗이 되었고, 그가 하루 일과를 마치고 나면 우리는 함께 밖으로 나가 그 커다란 연을 날리곤 했다. 그는 날마다 진정서 앞에 오래도록 앉아 있었지만, 아무리 애를 써도 그 일은 조금도 진척이 없었다. 찰스 일세 임금님이 늦든 빠르든 늘 그 안으로 슬그머니 끼어들었고, 그러면 그 종이는 한쪽으로 내던져지고 새로운 종이가 시작되었기 때문이다. 끊임없이 되풀이되는 이 좌절을 견디는 그의 인내와 희망, 찰스 일세 임금님에게 뭔가 잘못된 구석이 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 느끼는 그 헤아림, 임금님을 들어오지 못하게 막으려는 그 가냘픈 안간힘, 그러고도 임금님이 어김없이 들어와 진정서를 온통 엉망으로 헝클어 놓는 그 확실함은 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 만일 진정서가 완성된다면 거기서 무엇이 나올 것이라고 딕 씨가 여겼는지, 그것이 어디로 가야 한다고 생각했는지, 또 그것이 무슨 일을 해야 한다고 여겼는지는, 내 생각에 그 자신도 다른 누구 못지않게 전혀 몰랐다. 그렇다고 그가 그런 물음으로 골머리를 앓을 필요도 전혀 없었으니, 이 하늘 아래 무엇 하나가 확실하다면, 그 진정서는 결코 완성되지 못하리라는 것이야말로 확실했기 때문이다. 연이 공중 까마득히 높이 떠올랐을 때 그것을 붙들고 선 그의 모습은, 내 생각에 정말이지 가슴 뭉클한 광경이었다. 연에 붙여 놓은 글귀들 — 그것은 미완으로 끝난 진정서의 낡은 종이쪽지에 지나지 않았다 — 이 그렇게 세상에 널리 퍼진다고 믿는다며 자기 방에서 나에게 들려준 이야기는, 어쩌면 평소엔 그저 그의 공상이었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그가 밖에 나와 하늘의 연을 올려다보며, 연이 그의 손을 잡아당기고 끌어채는 것을 느낄 때만큼은 그렇지 않았다. 그는 그때만큼 마음이 평온해 보인 적이 없었다. 저녁이면 푸른 비탈에 그의 곁에 앉아, 고요한 하늘 높이 뜬 연을 바라보는 그를 지켜보면서, 나는 그 연이 그의 어지러운 마음을 들어 올려 (이것이 어린 내가 품은 생각이었다) 하늘로 데려가는 거라고 곧잘 상상하곤 했다. 그가 연줄을 감아들이면 연은 그 아름다운 빛 속에서 점점 아래로 아래로 내려와, 마침내 땅으로 펄럭이며 떨어져 죽은 것처럼 누워 버렸는데, 그러면 그는 차츰 꿈에서 깨어나는 듯 보였다. 그리고 내가 본 기억으로는, 그는 연을 집어 들고 마치 둘이 함께 떨어지기라도 한 양 넋 나간 얼굴로 둘레를 두리번거리곤 했으니, 나는 진심으로 그가 가엾었다.\n\n딕 씨와의 우정과 친밀함이 깊어지는 동안, 나는 그의 든든한 벗인 고모님의 사랑에서도 뒤처지지 않았다. 고모님은 어찌나 나를 살갑게 여겼던지, 몇 주가 지나는 사이에 트롯우드라는 내 양자 이름을 트롯으로 줄여 불렀고, 심지어 내가 시작한 대로 계속해 나간다면 죽은 베시 트롯우드 누이와 똑같은 자리를 자기 마음속에서 차지할 수 있을 거라는 희망까지 품게 해 주었다.\n\n\"트롯,\" 어느 날 저녁, 여느 때처럼 고모님과 딕 씨를 위해 주사위 놀이판이 차려졌을 때 고모님이 말했다. \"네 교육을 잊으면 안 되지.\"\n\n이것은 내가 유일하게 마음 졸이던 일이었던지라, 고모님이 그 이야기를 꺼내자 나는 무척 기뻤다.\n\n\"캔터베리에 있는 학교에 다니고 싶니?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n나는 그 학교가 고모님과 아주 가까우니 정말 다니고 싶다고 대답했다.\n\n\"좋아,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내일 가고 싶으냐?\"\n\n이미 고모님의 일 처리가 대체로 얼마나 잽싼지 익히 알던 터라, 나는 그 느닷없는 제안에도 놀라지 않고 \"네.\"라고 말했다.\n\n\"좋아,\" 고모님이 다시 말했다. \"재닛, 내일 아침 열 시에 회색 조랑말과 마차를 빌리고, 트롯우드 도련님 옷가지를 오늘 밤에 챙겨 두어라.\"\n\n이 명령에 나는 무척 들떴다. 그러나 그 명령이 딕 씨에게 미친 영향을 보자 내 이기심에 가슴이 찔렸다. 그는 우리가 헤어진다는 생각에 어찌나 풀이 죽었던지 놀이도 형편없이 해 버렸고, 그러자 고모님은 주사위 통으로 그의 손가락 마디를 몇 번 톡톡 나무라듯 친 뒤, 판을 접고 더는 그와 놀이하기를 마다했다. 그러나 내가 가끔 토요일에 들르고, 그도 가끔 수요일에 나를 보러 올 수 있다는 말을 고모님에게 듣자, 그는 기운을 되찾았다. 그러면서 그런 날을 위해 지금 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 새 연을 만들겠노라 다짐했다. 아침이 되자 그는 다시 시무룩해졌고, 자기가 가진 돈을 죄다 — 금화고 은화고 할 것 없이 — 나에게 줌으로써 마음을 다잡으려 했으나, 고모님이 끼어들어 그 선물을 오 실링으로 제한했다. 그러나 그 돈은 그가 간곡히 청한 끝에 나중에 십 실링으로 늘어났다. 우리는 정원 문에서 더없이 다정하게 작별했고, 딕 씨는 고모님이 마차를 몰아 내가 집에서 보이지 않게 될 때까지 안으로 들어가지 않았다.\n\n세상의 평판 따위는 도통 개의치 않는 고모님은 회색 조랑말을 능숙하게 몰아 도버를 지나갔다. 마치 궁정 마부처럼 높직하고 꼿꼿하게 앉아, 조랑말이 어디로 가든 한결같이 매서운 눈으로 지켜보았고, 어떤 점에서도 제멋대로 굴게 두지 않으려 단단히 마음먹은 채였다. 그래도 시골길로 접어들자 고모님은 조랑말이 조금쯤 느슨해지도록 허락했고, 곁에 방석 골짜기에 파묻혀 앉은 나를 내려다보며 행복하냐고 물었다.\n\n\"네, 정말 행복해요, 고모님, 고맙습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n고모님은 무척 흐뭇해했다. 그러나 두 손이 다 막혀 있던 터라, 채찍으로 내 머리를 토닥였다.\n\n\"큰 학교인가요, 고모님?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 나도 잘 모르겠다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"우선 위크필드 씨한테 들를 거야.\"\n\n\"그분이 학교를 운영하나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아니다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"사무소를 운영하지.\"\n\n고모님이 더는 알려 주지 않기에 나도 위크필드 씨에 대해 더 묻지 않았고, 우리는 캔터베리에 닿을 때까지 다른 이야기를 나누었다. 마침 장날이라 고모님은 짐수레며 바구니며 채소며 행상꾼의 물건 사이로 회색 조랑말을 비집어 넣을 절호의 기회를 잡았다. 우리가 머리카락 한 올 너비로 아슬아슬하게 틀고 꺾는 통에, 둘레에 서 있던 사람들에게서 갖가지 한마디씩이 쏟아졌는데, 늘 칭찬은 아니었다. 그러나 고모님은 더없이 태연하게 마차를 몰았고, 감히 말하건대 적의 나라를 가로지른다 해도 그만큼 침착하게 제 길을 갔을 것이다.\n\n마침내 우리는 길 위로 불룩 튀어나온 아주 오래된 집 앞에 멈췄다. 길고 낮은 격자창이 한층 더 불룩 튀어나오고, 끝에 머리 모양이 새겨진 들보들도 불룩 튀어나온 집이라, 나는 집 전체가 앞으로 몸을 기울여 저 아래 좁은 길을 지나가는 이가 누구인지 들여다보려 애쓰는 것만 같았다. 집은 흠 하나 없이 깨끗했다. 낮은 아치형 문에 달린 구식 놋쇠 문고리는 과일과 꽃 모양 화환이 새겨진 채 별처럼 반짝였고, 문으로 내려가는 두 단의 돌계단은 마치 고운 아마포를 덮어 놓은 듯 새하얬다. 그리고 모든 모서리와 귀퉁이, 조각과 장식 쇠시리, 예스러운 작은 유리 조각들과 한층 더 예스러운 작은 창문들은, 산만큼이나 오래되었으되 그 산에 내린 어떤 눈보다도 깨끗했다.\n\n조랑말 마차가 문 앞에 멈추고 내 눈이 그 집에 빠져 있을 때, 일 층의 작은 창문에서 (집 한쪽을 이루는 작고 둥근 탑에 난 창이었다) 송장 같은 얼굴 하나가 나타났다가 잽싸게 사라졌다. 이윽고 낮은 아치형 문이 열리더니 그 얼굴이 밖으로 나왔다. 그 얼굴은 창문에서 보였던 것과 똑같이 송장 같았는데, 다만 그 살결에는 머리카락이 붉은 사람의 피부에서 이따금 볼 수 있는 그 불그스름한 기운이 어려 있었다. 그것은 붉은 머리의 사람 — 지금 헤아려 보면 열다섯 살쯤 된 소년이었으나 훨씬 나이 들어 보였다 — 의 얼굴이었다. 그의 머리카락은 가장 짧게 벤 그루터기만큼 바짝 깎여 있었고, 눈썹은 거의 없다시피 했으며, 속눈썹은 아예 없고, 적갈색 두 눈은 어찌나 가려 주는 것도 그늘져 주는 것도 없던지, 나는 저 사람이 도대체 어떻게 잠드는지 의아했던 기억이 난다. 그는 어깨가 추켜올라가고 뼈만 앙상했으며, 점잖은 검정 옷을 입고 흰 헝겊 조각 같은 목수건을 목까지 단추로 채우고 있었다. 그리고 길고 비쩍 마른 해골 같은 손을 지녔는데, 조랑말 머리맡에 서서 그 손으로 턱을 문지르며 마차에 탄 우리를 올려다보던 그 모습이 유난히 내 눈길을 끌었다.\n\n\"위크필드 씨 계신가, 유라이아 힙?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"위크필드 씨는 댁에 계십니다, 부인.\" 유라이아 힙이 그 길쭉한 손으로 자기가 가리키는 방을 짚으며 말했다. \"괜찮으시면 저쪽으로 드시지요.\"\n\n우리는 마차에서 내렸고, 그에게 조랑말을 맡긴 채 거리 쪽으로 난 길고 낮은 응접실로 들어갔다. 들어가면서 나는 그 방 창문 너머로, 유라이아 힙이 조랑말의 콧구멍에 숨을 불어넣고는 마치 무슨 주문이라도 거는 양 곧바로 손으로 콧구멍을 덮는 모습을 흘끗 보았다. 키 큰 낡은 벽난로 선반 맞은편에는 초상화 두 점이 걸려 있었다. 하나는 회색 머리(그렇다고 결코 노인은 아니었다)에 검은 눈썹을 한 신사로, 붉은 끈으로 묶은 서류 뭉치를 들여다보고 있었다. 다른 하나는 더없이 차분하고 다정한 표정의 부인으로, 나를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n유라이아의 초상화를 찾으려고 둘레를 두리번거리던 참이었던 것 같은데, 그때 방 저쪽 끝의 문이 열리며 한 신사가 들어왔다. 그를 보자 나는 앞서 말한 그 초상화를 다시 돌아보며, 그림이 액자에서 빠져나온 게 아닌지 확인하려 했다. 그러나 그림은 그대로 걸려 있었고, 신사가 빛 속으로 들어서자 나는 그가 초상화를 그렸을 때보다 몇 살 더 늙었다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"베시 트롯우드 양,\" 신사가 말했다. \"어서 드시지요. 잠깐 손이 묶여 있었으니, 바쁜 것을 부디 양해해 주십시오. 제 까닭은 아실 겁니다. 저에게는 평생 단 하나뿐인 까닭이지요.\"\n\n베시 양은 그에게 고맙다고 인사했고, 우리는 그의 방으로 들어갔다. 그 방은 책과 서류, 양철 상자 따위로 사무실처럼 꾸며져 있었다. 정원이 내다보였고, 벽에 무쇠 금고가 박혀 있었는데, 벽난로 선반 바로 위에 박혀 있어서 나는 자리에 앉으면서 굴뚝 청소부가 굴뚝을 쓸 때 저걸 어떻게 빙 돌아가는지 의아했다.\n\n\"자, 트롯우드 양,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. 그가 위크필드 씨이며, 변호사이고, 이 고장 어느 부유한 신사의 영지 관리인이라는 것을 나는 곧 알게 되었다. \"무슨 바람이 불어 여기까지 오셨습니까? 나쁜 바람은 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"아닐세,\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"법률 문제로 온 게 아니야.\"\n\n\"잘하셨습니다, 부인.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"다른 무슨 일로든 오시는 편이 낫지요.\" 그의 머리카락은 이제 완전히 하얗게 셌지만 눈썹은 여전히 검었다. 그는 무척 호감 가는 얼굴이었고, 내 생각에는 잘생긴 편이었다. 그의 혈색에는 어떤 불그레한 윤기가 돌았는데, 나는 패거티의 가르침 덕에 그것을 포트와인과 결부 짓는 데 오래도록 익숙해져 있었다. 그리고 그의 목소리에도 그런 기운이 어려 있다는 느낌이 들어, 점점 불어 가는 그의 살집까지 같은 까닭으로 돌렸다. 그는 파란 코트에 줄무늬 조끼, 무명 바지 차림으로 아주 깔끔하게 차려입었고, 고운 주름 장식 셔츠와 삼베 목수건은 유난히 부드럽고 하얘서, 떠도는 내 공상에 (지금 떠오르는데) 백조의 가슴 깃털을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n\"이 아이가 내 조카일세.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"조카가 있으신 줄은 몰랐습니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 내 종손이지.\" 고모님이 일렀다.\n\n\"종손이 있으신 줄도 정말이지 몰랐습니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 양자로 들였네.\" 고모님이 손을 한번 내저으며 말했는데, 그가 알든 모르든 자기에게는 매한가지라는 뜻이었다. \"그래서 이 아이를 데려왔어. 제대로 잘 가르치고 잘 대접해 줄 학교에 넣으려고 말이야. 자, 그런 학교가 어디 있는지, 어떤 곳인지, 죄다 일러 주게나.\"\n\n\"제대로 조언을 드리기 전에,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"그 해묵은 물음이 있지요. 이번 일에 부인의 까닭은 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"이 사람이 정말!\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"까닭이 빤히 드러나 있는데도 늘 까닭을 캐려 드는군! 아니, 이 아이를 행복하고 쓸모 있게 만들려는 거지.\"\n\n\"제 생각에는 까닭이 뒤섞여 있는 듯한데요.\" 위크필드 씨가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 못 미덥다는 듯이 웃었다.\n\n\"뒤섞이긴 무슨 얼어 죽을.\" 고모님이 받아쳤다. \"자네는 자기가 하는 일마다 단 하나의 빤한 까닭이 있다고 주장하지. 설마 세상에서 자네만이 속이 빤히 들여다보이는 사람이라고 여기는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"아 그렇지만, 저는 평생 단 하나의 까닭뿐입니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 그가 웃으며 응수했다. \"다른 사람들은 까닭이 열도 되고 스물도 되고 백도 되지요. 저는 단 하나뿐입니다. 거기에 차이가 있지요. 어쨌거나, 그건 논점에서 벗어난 얘기군요. 가장 좋은 학교 말입니까? 까닭이야 어떻든, 가장 좋은 곳을 원하시는 거지요?\"\n\n고모님이 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다.\n\n\"우리 고장에서 가장 좋은 학교라 해도,\" 위크필드 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"조카분이 당장은 거기서 기숙할 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 다른 어딘가에서는 기숙할 수 있겠지?\" 고모님이 넌지시 물었다.\n\n위크필드 씨는 내가 그럴 수 있으리라 보았다. 잠깐 의논한 끝에, 그는 고모님을 학교로 데려가 직접 보고 판단하게 하고, 또 같은 목적으로 내가 기숙할 만하다고 여겨지는 두세 집으로 안내하겠다고 제안했다. 고모님이 그 제안을 받아들여, 우리 셋이 함께 나서려는데, 그가 걸음을 멈추고 말했다.\n\n\"여기 우리 어린 친구가 그 결정에 반대할 만한 까닭이 좀 있을지도 모르겠군요. 이 아이는 두고 가는 편이 낫지 않을까요?\"\n\n고모님은 그 점을 두고 따져 볼 마음인 듯했지만, 나는 일을 매끄럽게 하려고, 괜찮으시다면 기꺼이 남아 있겠다고 말한 뒤, 위크필드 씨의 사무실로 되돌아갔다. 그리고 처음 앉았던 그 의자에 다시 앉아 두 사람이 돌아오기를 기다렸다.\n\n마침 이 의자는 좁은 통로와 마주하고 있었는데, 그 통로 끝에는 유라이아 힙의 창백한 얼굴이 창밖을 내다보던 작은 원형 방이 있었다. 유라이아는 조랑말을 이웃 마구간에 맡기고 와서, 그 방의 책상에 앉아 일하고 있었다. 책상 위쪽에는 종이를 걸어 두는 놋쇠 틀이 있었고, 거기에 그가 베껴 쓰고 있는 글이 걸려 있었다. 그의 얼굴이 나를 향해 있었지만, 한동안 나는 우리 사이에 그 글이 걸려 있으니 그가 나를 보지 못하리라 여겼다. 그러나 그쪽을 더 유심히 들여다보다가, 그의 잠들지 않는 두 눈이 이따금 그 글 아래로 두 개의 붉은 태양처럼 내려와서, 감히 말하건대 한 번에 일 분 내내 나를 슬그머니 빤히 노려보곤 한다는 것을 알아채고는 마음이 영 불편해졌다. 그러는 동안에도 그의 펜은 여느 때처럼 능란하게 움직였다 — 아니, 움직이는 척했다. 나는 그 눈길을 피하려고 여러 번 애를 썼다 — 의자에 올라서서 방 반대편의 지도를 들여다본다든지, 켄트 지방 신문의 기사 단을 골똘히 읽는다든지 하면서 말이다 — 그러나 그 눈은 늘 나를 도로 끌어당겼고, 내가 그 두 개의 붉은 태양 쪽으로 눈길을 줄 때마다 어김없이 그것은 막 떠오르거나 막 지는 참이었다.\n\n마침내, 더없이 마음이 놓이게도, 꽤 오래 자리를 비웠던 고모님과 위크필드 씨가 돌아왔다. 그러나 그들은 내가 바라던 만큼 성과를 거두지는 못했다. 학교의 이점은 부인할 수 없었으나, 고모님이 나를 위해 제안된 기숙집 가운데 어느 한 곳도 마음에 들어 하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n\"이거 참 안됐구나.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"어찌해야 할지 모르겠다, 트롯.\"\n\n\"공교롭게 일이 그리되었군요.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 어찌하면 좋을지 일러 드리지요, 트롯우드 양.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n\"조카분을 당분간 여기 두십시오. 조용한 아이입니다. 저를 조금도 방해하지 않을 거예요. 공부하기에는 더없이 좋은 집이지요. 수도원만큼 조용하고, 거의 그만큼 넓습니다. 여기 두십시오.\"\n\n고모님은 그 제안이 마음에 드는 게 분명했지만, 선뜻 받아들이기를 조심스러워했다. 나도 마찬가지였다. \"자요, 트롯우드 양,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"이게 난처함을 푸는 방법입니다. 어디까지나 임시방편이니까요. 잘 풀리지 않거나 서로의 형편에 딱 맞지 않으면, 아이는 손쉽게 방향을 틀면 됩니다. 그동안 더 나은 곳을 찾을 시간도 있을 거고요. 당분간은 여기 두기로 마음을 정하시는 편이 낫습니다!\"\n\n\"정말 고맙네.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"보아하니 이 아이도 그렇고. 하지만—\"\n\n\"자요! 무슨 말씀이신지 알겠습니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 외쳤다. \"신세를 진다는 부담에 짓눌리실 필요 없습니다, 트롯우드 양. 원하시면 아이 몫의 비용을 치르셔도 됩니다. 조건을 두고 야박하게 굴진 않겠지만, 정 원하신다면 치르십시오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 한다면,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"비록 실제로 진 신세가 가벼워지진 않겠지만, 기꺼이 이 아이를 맡기겠네.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리 집 어린 살림꾼을 보러 가시지요.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다.\n\n그래서 우리는 놀라운 옛 계단을 올라갔다. 난간이 어찌나 널찍했던지 그 위로도 거의 그만큼 쉽게 올라갈 수 있을 듯했다. 그러고는 그늘진 옛 응접실로 들어갔는데, 내가 거리에서 올려다보았던 그 예스러운 창문 서너 개로 빛이 들어왔다. 그 창에는 오래된 떡갈나무 의자가 붙어 있었는데, 반들거리는 떡갈나무 마룻바닥이며 천장의 굵직한 들보와 같은 나무에서 나온 것만 같았다. 피아노 한 대와 빨강과 초록의 화사한 가구 몇 점, 그리고 꽃 몇 송이가 놓인, 곱게 꾸민 방이었다. 온통 오래된 후미진 구석과 모퉁이로 이루어진 듯했고, 어느 후미진 구석이며 모퉁이마다 별난 작은 탁자며 찬장이며 책장이며 의자며 그 비슷한 무엇인가가 있어서, 나는 이 방에 이만한 좋은 구석은 다시 없겠다고 여기곤 했다. 그러다 다음 구석을 보면, 그것이 앞의 것과 맞먹거나, 어쩌면 더 낫다는 것을 알게 되었다. 모든 것에는 이 집 바깥을 물들이던 것과 똑같은, 한적함과 깨끗함의 기운이 어려 있었다.\n\n위크필드 씨가 판자벽 한쪽 구석의 문을 톡톡 두드리자, 나와 또래쯤 되는 소녀가 잽싸게 나와 그에게 입을 맞추었다. 나는 그 얼굴에서, 아래층에서 나를 바라보던 그 부인 초상화의 차분하고 다정한 표정을 단번에 알아보았다. 마치 그 초상화는 여인으로 자라났는데 그 실물은 아직 아이로 남아 있는 것만 같았다. 그녀의 얼굴은 더없이 밝고 행복했지만, 그 얼굴에는, 그리고 그녀 전체에는 어떤 평온함이 — 조용하고 선량하며 잔잔한 마음씨가 — 깃들어 있었으니, 나는 그것을 결코 잊은 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 위크필드 씨는 이 아이가 자기 집의 어린 살림꾼, 자기 딸 애그니스라고 말했다. 그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣고, 그가 딸의 손을 잡는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 그의 평생 단 하나뿐인 까닭이 무엇인지 알아챘다.\n\n그녀는 옆구리에 열쇠가 든 작은 바구니 같은 것을 매달고 있었는데, 이 오래된 집이 둘 만한 살림꾼 가운데 더없이 차분하고 사려 깊어 보였다. 그녀는 아버지가 나에 대해 이야기하는 것을 환한 얼굴로 들었고, 아버지의 말이 끝나자 고모님에게 위층으로 올라가 내 방을 보자고 청했다. 우리는 그녀를 앞세우고 모두 함께 올라갔다. 떡갈나무 들보가 더 많고 마름모꼴 유리창이 달린, 으리으리한 옛 방이었다. 그 넓은 난간이 그 방까지 죽 이어져 있었다.\n\n어린 시절 어느 교회에서 색유리 창을 보았는지, 그게 어디였고 언제였는지는 도무지 떠오르지 않는다. 그 그림이 무엇이었는지도 기억나지 않는다. 그러나 그녀가 그 오래된 계단의 엄숙한 빛 속에서 몸을 돌려, 위쪽에서 우리를 기다리는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 그 창을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그 후로 줄곧 나는 그 창의 고요한 환함 같은 무엇인가를 애그니스 위크필드와 결부시켰다.\n\n나를 위해 마련된 이 일에 고모님도 나만큼 행복해했고, 우리는 흐뭇하고 만족스러운 마음으로 다시 응접실로 내려왔다. 고모님은 어떻게든 날이 어두워지기 전에 회색 조랑말을 몰아 집에 닿지 못할까 봐, 저녁 식사를 하고 가라는 권유는 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 그리고 내 짐작에 위크필드 씨도 고모님을 너무 잘 알아 어떤 점에서도 굳이 다투려 하지 않았으므로, 그 자리에서 고모님을 위해 가벼운 점심이 차려졌고, 애그니스는 가정교사에게 돌아갔으며 위크필드 씨는 자기 사무실로 돌아갔다. 그리하여 우리는 아무런 거리낌 없이 서로 작별 인사를 나눌 수 있게 되었다.\n\n고모님은 모든 일이 위크필드 씨를 통해 마련될 것이고, 내가 무엇 하나 아쉬운 게 없을 거라고 말하며, 더없이 다정한 말과 더없이 좋은 충고를 건넸다.\n\n\"트롯,\" 고모님이 마지막으로 말했다. \"너 자신과 나와 딕 씨에게 떳떳한 사람이 되어라. 하늘이 너와 함께하기를!\"\n\n나는 가슴이 북받쳐, 그저 거듭거듭 고모님에게 고맙다는 말밖에 못 했고, 딕 씨에게도 안부를 전해 달라고 했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이든,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"절대 비열하게 굴지 마라. 절대 거짓되지 마라. 절대 모질게 굴지 마라. 그 세 가지 악덕을 피한다면, 트롯, 나는 너에게 늘 희망을 걸 수 있을 게다.\"\n\n나는 할 수 있는 한, 고모님의 친절을 저버리지도 그 가르침을 잊지도 않겠노라 약속했다.\n\n\"조랑말이 문 앞에 와 있구나.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나는 간다! 너는 여기 있어라.\" 이 말과 함께 고모님은 황급히 나를 끌어안고는 방을 나가, 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 처음에는 그토록 갑작스러운 떠남에 깜짝 놀라, 행여 내가 고모님을 언짢게 한 게 아닌가 두려운 마음마저 들었다. 그러나 거리를 내다보니, 고모님이 풀 죽은 모습으로 마차에 올라, 한 번도 고개를 들지 않은 채 떠나가는 것을 보고는, 고모님을 더 잘 헤아리게 되어 그런 부당한 오해를 거두었다.\n\n위크필드 씨의 저녁 식사 시각인 다섯 시가 되자, 나는 다시 기운을 차려 칼과 포크를 들 채비가 되었다. 식탁보는 우리 둘 몫만 깔려 있었지만, 애그니스는 식사 전에 응접실에서 기다리고 있다가 아버지와 함께 내려와 식탁에서 아버지와 마주 보고 앉았다. 나는 그가 과연 딸 없이 식사를 할 수 있을지 의심스러웠다.\n\n우리는 식사 뒤에 그곳에 머물지 않고 다시 위층 응접실로 올라갔다. 그 방의 아늑한 한쪽 구석에 애그니스는 아버지를 위해 잔과 포트와인 한 병을 차려 놓았다. 나는 만일 다른 누군가의 손으로 그것이 놓였더라면 그가 늘 즐기던 그 맛을 느끼지 못했으리라 생각했다.\n\n그는 거기 앉아 두 시간 동안 술을 마셨는데, 꽤 많이 마셨다. 그동안 애그니스는 피아노를 치고, 바느질을 하고, 아버지와 나에게 말을 건넸다. 그는 대체로 우리와 흥겹고 명랑하게 지냈다. 그러나 이따금 그의 눈길이 딸에게 머물면, 그는 깊은 시름에 잠겨 말이 없어지곤 했다. 내 생각에 그녀는 늘 그것을 재빨리 알아채고, 늘 물음이나 다정한 몸짓으로 그를 일깨웠다. 그러면 그는 깊은 생각에서 빠져나와 술을 더 마셨다.\n\n애그니스가 차를 우려 자리를 보살폈고, 그 뒤로는 저녁 식사 뒤와 마찬가지로 그녀가 잠자리에 들 때까지 시간이 흘러갔다. 그녀가 잠자리에 들 때, 아버지는 딸을 품에 안고 입을 맞추었으며, 딸이 가고 나자 자기 사무실에 촛불을 켜라고 일렀다. 그러고 나서 나도 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n그러나 저녁나절 어느 무렵, 나는 슬슬 문간까지 내려갔다가 거리를 따라 조금 더 걸어 나갔다. 그 오래된 집들과 회색 대성당을 다시 한번 들여다보고, 또 길을 떠나 그 옛 도시를 지나오던 일을, 그리고 내가 지금 살게 된 바로 그 집을 그런 줄도 모르고 스쳐 지나갔던 일을 떠올려 보고 싶어서였다. 돌아오는 길에 나는 유라이아 힙이 사무실 문을 닫는 것을 보았는데, 누구에게나 살가운 마음이 들던 터라 안으로 들어가 그에게 말을 건넸고, 헤어질 때 손을 내밀어 악수를 청했다. 그런데 아아, 그의 손이 어찌나 축축하던지! 보기에 으스스한 만큼이나 만져 보아도 으스스했다! 나는 그 뒤로 내 손을 따뜻하게 데우려고, 그리고 그의 손길을 문질러 없애려고 한참을 비벼 댔다.\n\n어찌나 거북한 손이었던지, 방으로 돌아온 뒤에도 그것은 내 기억 속에 여전히 차갑고 축축하게 남아 있었다. 창밖으로 몸을 내밀다가, 들보 끝에 새겨진 머리 하나가 나를 곁눈질로 바라보는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그것이 어떻게든 거기 올라가 있는 유라이아 힙이라는 망상에 사로잡혀 황급히 창을 닫아 그를 가두어 버렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER 16. I AM A NEW BOY IN MORE SENSES THAN ONE\n\n이튿날 아침, 식사를 마치고 나는 다시 학교생활에 발을 들였다. 위크필드 씨와 함께, 앞으로 내가 공부할 곳으로 갔다. 안마당에 들어선 묵직한 건물이었는데, 학식 있는 분위기가 풍겨 나서, 대성당 탑에서 내려와 잔디밭 위를 점잖은 서기처럼 거니는 떠돌이 까마귀와 갈까마귀에게 썩 잘 어울려 보였다. 그곳에서 나는 새 선생님인 스트롱 박사님을 소개받았다.\n\n스트롱 박사님은 내 눈에, 집 바깥에 늘어선 키 큰 쇠 난간이며 대문만큼이나 녹슬어 보였다. 또한 그 양옆에 선 커다란 돌 항아리들만큼이나 뻣뻣하고 묵직해 보였는데, 그 항아리들은 붉은 벽돌담 위에, 안마당을 빙 둘러 일정한 간격으로 세워져 있어서, 마치 세월이 가지고 놀라고 갖다 놓은 거룩한 볼링핀 같았다. 박사님은 서재에 (스트롱 박사님 말이다) 계셨는데, 옷은 그다지 잘 솔질되어 있지 않았고, 머리도 그다지 잘 빗겨 있지 않았다. 무릎 아래 바지의 끈은 풀려 있었고, 긴 검정 각반은 단추가 채워지지 않았으며, 두 신발은 난로 깔개 위에서 두 개의 동굴처럼 하품하듯 벌어져 있었다. 박사님은 나를 향해, 윤기 없는 눈길을 돌렸는데, 그 눈은 옛날 블런더스톤 교회 묘지에서 풀을 뜯고 무덤들 위로 비틀거리며 넘어지곤 하던, 오래도록 잊고 있던 눈먼 늙은 말을 떠올리게 했다. 박사님은 나를 만나서 기쁘다고 말했다. 그러고는 나에게 손을 내밀었는데, 나는 그 손을 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 손이 스스로 아무것도 하지 않았으니까.\n\n그런데 스트롱 박사님에게서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에, 무언가 일을 하며 앉아 있는 아주 어여쁜 젊은 부인이 있었다. 박사님은 그녀를 애니라고 불렀는데, 나는 그분이 박사님의 딸이려니 짐작했다. 그분이 무릎을 꿇고 스트롱 박사님의 신발을 신겨 주고 각반의 단추를 채워 주면서 — 무척 명랑하고 잽싼 손길로 그렇게 하면서 — 나를 그 곤란에서 구해 주었다. 그분이 일을 마치고, 우리가 교실로 나가려는데, 위크필드 씨가 그분에게 안녕히 계시라고 인사하면서 '스트롱 부인'이라고 부르는 것을 듣고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다. 그분이 스트롱 박사님 아들의 아내인가, 아니면 스트롱 박사 부인인가 하고 어리둥절해하던 차에, 스트롱 박사님이 직접 무심코 그 의문을 풀어 주었다.\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 위크필드,\" 박사님이 복도에서 걸음을 멈추고 내 어깨에 손을 얹은 채 말했다. \"내 아내의 사촌 자리는 아직 적당한 데를 못 찾았나?\"\n\n\"못 찾았습니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"예. 아직요.\"\n\n\"되도록 빨리 해결되면 좋겠는데, 위크필드,\" 스트롱 박사님이 말했다. \"잭 맬던이 가진 것은 없는데다 빈둥거리니 말이야. 그리고 그 두 가지 나쁜 것에서 더 나쁜 것들이 가끔 생겨나거든. 와츠 박사가 뭐라고 했더라,\" 박사님은 나를 보며, 인용에 맞춰 고개를 까딱이며 덧붙였다. \"'사탄은 노는 손에 시킬 못된 짓을 늘 찾아낸다.'\"\n\n\"맙소사, 박사님,\" 위크필드 씨가 받아쳤다. \"와츠 박사가 사람을 좀 알았더라면, 똑같이 맞는 말로 이렇게 썼을 겁니다. '사탄은 바쁜 손에 시킬 못된 짓을 늘 찾아낸다.' 바쁜 사람들이야말로 이 세상에서 못된 짓의 제 몫을 톡톡히 해낸다니까요, 그건 믿어도 좋습니다. 지난 한두 세기 동안, 돈을 긁어모으고 권력을 쥐는 데 누구보다 바빴던 사람들이 도대체 무슨 짓을 해 왔습니까? 못된 짓 하나 안 했나요?\"\n\n\"잭 맬던은 그 둘 중 어느 것을 쥐는 데도 그다지 바쁠 일은 없을 것 같네만.\" 스트롱 박사님이 생각에 잠겨 턱을 문지르며 말했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 옆길로 샌 걸 사과드리면서, 다시 본론으로 돌아오게 해 주시는군요. 예, 아직 잭 맬던 씨의 자리를 마련하지 못했습니다. 제 생각에는,\" 그는 다소 머뭇거리며 이렇게 말했다. \"박사님의 속뜻을 제가 짐작하고 있는데, 그것 때문에 일이 더 어려워집니다.\"\n\n\"내 속뜻은,\" 스트롱 박사님이 받았다. \"애니의 사촌이자 옛 소꿉동무에게 적당한 자리를 마련해 주려는 것뿐일세.\"\n\n\"예, 압니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"국내에서든 외국에서든 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇지!\" 박사님이, 위크필드 씨가 어째서 그 말에 그토록 힘을 주는지 의아해하는 듯이 대답했다. \"국내에서든 외국에서든.\"\n\n\"박사님이 직접 하신 표현이잖습니까.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"아니면 외국에서든, 하고요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고.\" 박사님이 대답했다. \"그렇고말고. 둘 중 하나지.\"\n\n\"둘 중 하나라고요? 마음에 둔 쪽이 없으십니까?\" 위크필드 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"없네.\" 박사님이 대답했다.\n\n\"없다고요?\" 그는 놀라서 되물었다.\n\n\"조금도 없어.\"\n\n\"외국이지 국내가 아니라고 두실 이유가,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"전혀 없으시단 말씀이지요?\"\n\n\"없네.\" 박사님이 대답했다.\n\n\"믿을 수밖에 없고, 물론 믿습니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"이걸 진작 알았더라면 제 일이 한결 단순해졌을 텐데요. 하지만 솔직히 말씀드려, 저는 다른 인상을 품고 있었습니다.\"\n\n스트롱 박사님은 어리둥절하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 그를 바라보았는데, 그 눈길은 거의 곧바로 미소로 가라앉았다. 그 미소에 나는 크게 용기를 얻었다. 그것은 다정함과 부드러움으로 가득했고, 거기에는 — 그리고 사실 박사님의 태도 전체에는 — 학구적이고 생각에 잠긴 서릿발 같은 기운을 뚫고 나오면, 나 같은 어린 학생에게는 무척 매력적이고 희망을 주는 소박함이 있었다. \"없다\", \"조금도 없어\", 그리고 같은 뜻의 짤막한 다짐들을 거듭하면서, 스트롱 박사님은 묘하게 들쭉날쭉한 걸음으로 우리 앞을 종종걸음 쳐 나아갔고, 우리는 그 뒤를 따랐다. 위크필드 씨는, 내가 보기에, 자기가 보이는 줄도 모른 채, 심각한 얼굴로 혼자 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 있었다.\n\n교실은 꽤 큰 강당이었는데, 집에서 가장 조용한 쪽에 있었고, 커다란 항아리 대여섯 개가 위엄 있게 노려보는 정면을 마주하고 있었으며, 박사님 소유의 한적한 옛 정원이 얼핏 들여다보였다. 그 정원에서는 햇살이 드는 남쪽 담벼락에서 복숭아가 익어 가고 있었다. 창문 바깥 잔디밭에는 통에 심긴 커다란 알로에 두 그루가 있었는데, 그 식물의 넓고 단단한 잎사귀는 (마치 색칠한 양철로 만든 것처럼 보였다) 그 뒤로 줄곧, 연상 작용으로 인해, 나에게는 침묵과 은둔의 상징이 되었다. 우리가 들어갔을 때 스물다섯 명쯤 되는 아이들이 책에 열중하고 있었는데, 박사님께 아침 인사를 하려고 일어섰고, 위크필드 씨와 나를 보고는 그대로 서 있었다.\n\n\"새로 온 학생이다, 신사 여러분.\" 박사님이 말했다. \"트롯우드 코퍼필드다.\"\n\n그러자 우두머리 학생인 애덤스라는 아이가 제자리에서 나와 나를 맞아 주었다. 하얀 넥타이를 매고 있어 젊은 목사님처럼 보였지만, 무척 상냥하고 쾌활했다. 그는 내 자리를 알려 주고, 선생님들께 나를 소개해 주었는데, 그 신사다운 태도는, 무엇이 됐든 나를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 분명히 나를 편안하게 해 주었을 것이다.\n\n하지만 나에게는, 이런 아이들 사이에, 혹은 믹 워커와 멀리 포테이토를 빼고는 내 또래 동무들 사이에 있어 본 것이 어찌나 까마득하게 느껴지던지, 내 평생 그 어느 때 못지않게 어색했다. 나는 이 아이들로서는 알 길이 없는 일들을 겪어 왔고, 그들 가운데 한 명으로서의 내 나이와 외모와 처지에 어울리지 않는 경험을 쌓아 왔다는 것을 너무도 또렷이 의식한 나머지, 평범한 꼬마 학생으로 여기 온 것이 사기가 아닌가 하고 반쯤 믿을 지경이었다. 머드스톤앤그린비 시절을 거치면서, 그것이 길었든 짧았든, 나는 아이들의 놀이와 게임에 어찌나 서툴러졌던지, 그들에게 속한 가장 흔한 것들에서조차 내가 어설프고 경험이 없다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 무엇을 배웠든 간에, 아침부터 밤까지 누추한 걱정거리에 시달리며 사는 동안 죄다 빠져나가 버려서, 이제 내가 아는 것을 시험받았을 때 나는 아무것도 몰랐고, 학교에서 가장 낮은 반에 들어갔다. 그러나 아이다운 재주가 없고 책 공부도 부족해 마음이 괴로웠지만, 그보다 훨씬 더 나를 불편하게 한 것은, 내가 아는 것에서 오히려 모르는 것에서보다 동무들과 한참 더 동떨어져 있다는 생각이었다. 내 머릿속은 이런 생각으로 가득 찼다. 내가 왕립 재판소 감옥과 그렇게 가깝게 지냈다는 것을 이 아이들이 안다면 어떻게 생각할까? 나도 모르게, 미코버 일가와 얽혀 벌인 일들 — 그 모든 전당포 출입이며, 물건을 팔아 치운 일이며, 야식들 — 을 드러낼 무언가가 내게 묻어 있지 않을까? 행여 어떤 아이가 내가 길에 지치고 누더기를 걸친 채 캔터베리를 지나오는 것을 보았고, 그 사실을 알아채면 어쩌나? 돈을 그토록 우습게 여기는 이 아이들이, 내가 날마다 먹을 소시지며 맥주며, 혹은 푸딩 몇 조각을 사려고 어떻게 반 페니씩 긁어모았는지 알게 된다면 뭐라고 할까? 런던 생활과 런던 거리에 대해 그토록 순진한 이 아이들이, 그 두 가지의 가장 천한 모습 몇 가지에 내가 얼마나 훤한지를 (그리고 그게 부끄러웠는데) 알게 된다면 어떤 마음이 들까? 스트롱 박사님 학교에서 보낸 그 첫날, 이 모든 것이 어찌나 머릿속을 맴돌던지, 나는 내 사소한 눈빛 하나 몸짓 하나도 믿지 못했다. 새 동무 가운데 누군가가 다가올 때마다 나는 움츠러들었고, 다정한 인사나 다가옴에 대답하다가 내 정체를 드러낼까 두려워, 수업이 끝나기가 무섭게 서둘러 빠져나왔다.\n\n하지만 위크필드 씨의 오래된 집에는 어떤 힘이 깃들어 있어서, 새 교과서를 옆구리에 끼고 그 집 문을 두드릴 때면, 나는 마음의 불편함이 누그러지는 것을 느끼기 시작했다. 바람 잘 통하는 내 오래된 방으로 올라갈 때면, 계단의 묵직한 그림자가 내 의심과 두려움 위로 드리워, 지난날을 더 어렴풋하게 만들어 주는 것 같았다. 나는 거기 앉아 저녁 식사 시간까지 (우리는 세 시면 완전히 학교에서 풀려났다) 꿋꿋이 책을 외웠고, 그래도 그럭저럭 봐 줄 만한 아이가 될 수 있으리라는 희망을 안고 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n애그니스는 응접실에서 아버지를 기다리고 있었는데, 사무실에서 누군가에게 붙잡혀 있었던 것이다. 애그니스는 상냥한 미소로 나를 맞으며, 학교가 어떠냐고 물었다. 나는 무척 마음에 들 것 같다고, 그러기를 바란다고 대답했다. 하지만 처음엔 조금 낯설다고 했다.\n\n\"너는 학교에 가 본 적이 없지,\" 내가 말했다. \"그렇지?\" \"아니, 가 봤어! 날마다 가는걸.\"\n\n\"아, 하지만 그건 여기, 너희 집에서 말이지?\"\n\n\"아빠가 나를 다른 데 보낼 수 없으셨거든.\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 고개를 가로저었다. \"아빠 집에는 살림 맡을 사람이 있어야 하잖아, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"아버지가 너를 무척 아끼시는구나, 분명히.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n애그니스는 \"응\" 하고 고개를 끄덕이더니, 아버지가 올라오는 소리를 들으려고 문가로 가서 계단에서 마주치려 했다. 하지만 아버지가 와 있지 않자, 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n\"엄마는 내가 태어나면서부터 돌아가셨어.\" 애그니스가 그 차분한 투로 말했다. \"나는 아래층에 있는 그림으로만 엄마를 알아. 어제 네가 그걸 보고 있는 걸 봤어. 누구 그림인지 생각해 봤니?\"\n\n나는 그렇다고, 너랑 너무 닮았기 때문이라고 대답했다.\n\n\"아빠도 그렇게 말씀하셔.\" 애그니스가 기뻐하며 말했다. \"쉿! 저게 지금 아빠야!\"\n\n애그니스가 아버지를 맞으러 가서 손을 잡고 함께 들어올 때, 그 환하고 차분한 얼굴이 기쁨으로 밝아졌다. 아버지는 나를 다정하게 맞아 주며, 스트롱 박사님 밑에서 분명히 행복할 거라고, 그분은 더없이 온화한 사람 중 하나라고 말해 주었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 더러는 — 그런 사람이 있는지는 모르겠지만 — 그분의 친절을 악용하는 자도 있을 거다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"트롯우드, 무슨 일에서든 그런 자가 되지 말거라. 그분은 사람을 가장 의심할 줄 모르는 분이야. 그게 미덕인지 흠인지는 모르겠다만, 그분과의 거래에서는, 크든 작든, 늘 그 점을 헤아려야 한다.\"\n\n내가 보기에 그는 지쳤거나, 무언가에 만족하지 못하는 사람처럼 말했다. 하지만 나는 마음속으로 그 의문을 더 파고들지 않았다. 바로 그때 저녁 식사가 알려졌고, 우리는 아래층으로 내려가 전과 같은 자리에 앉았기 때문이다.\n\n우리가 막 자리에 앉기가 무섭게, 유라이어 힙이 그 붉은 머리와 비쩍 마른 손을 문틈으로 들이밀고 말했다.\n\n\"맬던 씨가 한 말씀 청하십니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"맬던 씨와는 방금 막 헤어졌네만.\" 그의 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"예, 어르신.\" 유라이어가 대답했다. \"하지만 맬던 씨가 다시 오셔서, 한 말씀 청하십니다.\"\n\n문을 손으로 잡아 열어 둔 채로, 유라이어는 나를 보고, 애그니스를 보고, 음식 접시들을 보고, 그릇들을 보고, 방 안의 모든 것을 다 보는 것 같았다. 그런데도 아무것도 보지 않는 것 같았다. 그러면서도 줄곧, 그 붉은 눈을 충실하게 주인에게 두고 있는 듯한 모양새를 하고 있었다. \"죄송합니다. 다만 다시 생각해 보고 드리는 말씀은,\" 유라이어 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 들리더니, 유라이어의 머리가 밀려나고 그 자리에 말하는 사람의 머리가 들어섰다. \"이렇게 불쑥 들어온 것을 부디 용서하십시오만 — 보아하니 이 일에 제게 선택의 여지가 없는 것 같으니, 빨리 외국에 나갈수록 좋겠다는 겁니다. 제 사촌 애니가, 이 이야기를 할 때, 친구들을 멀리 내쫓느니 가까이 두는 편이 좋다고 했고, 그 늙은 박사님도—\"\n\n\"스트롱 박사님 말씀이오?\" 위크필드 씨가 심각하게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"물론 스트롱 박사님이지요.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"제가 그분을 늙은 박사님이라고 부르는 겁니다. 다 같은 말 아닙니까, 아시다시피.\"\n\n\"나는 모르겠소.\" 위크필드 씨가 받았다.\n\n\"그럼, 스트롱 박사님 말입니다.\" 상대가 말했다. \"스트롱 박사님도 같은 생각이신 줄 알았지요. 한데 당신이 저를 대하는 태도로 보아하니 박사님이 마음을 바꾸신 모양이니, 더 할 말이 없습니다. 빨리 떠날수록 좋다는 것 말고는요. 그래서 다시 돌아와서, 빨리 떠날수록 좋겠다고 말씀드리려고 한 겁니다. 물에 풍덩 뛰어들어야 한다면, 강둑에서 꾸물거려 봐야 소용없으니까요.\"\n\n\"맬던 씨, 당신 경우에는 꾸물거릴 일이 되도록 없을 거요, 그건 믿어도 좋소.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 상대가 말했다. \"정말 감사합니다. 받은 말의 입속까지 들여다볼 생각은 없습니다, 그건 점잖지 못한 짓이니까요. 안 그랬으면, 감히 말하건대, 제 사촌 애니가 자기 식대로 쉽게 처리해 줄 수도 있었을 텐데요. 제 생각에 애니가 그 늙은 박사님께 그저 한마디만 하면—\"\n\n\"스트롱 부인이 그저 자기 남편에게 한마디만 하면 된다는 뜻이오 — 내가 제대로 알아들은 거요?\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇지요.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"— 이러이러한 일이 이렇게 됐으면 좋겠다고 한마디만 하면, 당연히 그렇게 될 거라는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 어째서 당연하다는 거요, 맬던 씨?\" 위크필드 씨가 차분히 식사를 하며 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 애니는 매력적인 젊은 아가씨이고, 그 늙은 박사님은 — 스트롱 박사님 말입니다 — 그다지 매력적인 젊은 청년이 아니니까요.\" 잭 맬던 씨가 웃으며 말했다. \"누구를 흉보려는 건 아닙니다, 위크필드 씨. 그저 그런 종류의 결혼에서는 어느 정도의 보상이 공정하고 합당하지 않겠나 하는 거지요.\"\n\n\"부인에 대한 보상 말이오, 선생?\" 위크필드 씨가 심각하게 물었다.\n\n\"부인에 대한 보상이지요, 선생.\" 잭 맬던 씨가 웃으며 대답했다. 하지만 위크필드 씨가 여전히 차분하고 흔들림 없는 태도로 식사를 계속하며, 얼굴 근육 하나 풀 기색이 없다는 것을 알아챈 듯, 그가 덧붙였다. \"어쨌든, 하려고 온 말은 다 했으니, 이렇게 불쑥 들어온 것을 다시 한번 사과드리고, 이만 물러가겠습니다. 물론 이 일은 오로지 당신과 저 사이에서만 처리할 문제로 여기고, 박사님 댁에서는 입에 올리지 말라는 당신 지시를 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"식사는 하셨소?\" 위크필드 씨가 식탁 쪽으로 손짓하며 물었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다. 저는 식사를 하려고 합니다.\" 맬던 씨가 말했다. \"제 사촌 애니랑요. 안녕히 계십시오!\"\n\n위크필드 씨는 일어나지 않은 채, 그가 나가는 것을 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다. 내가 보기에 그는 다소 얄팍한 부류의 젊은 신사로, 잘생긴 얼굴에, 말이 빨랐고, 자신만만하고 대담한 태도를 지니고 있었다. 이것이 내가 잭 맬던 씨를 처음 본 순간이었다. 그날 아침 박사님이 그를 입에 올렸을 때만 해도, 이렇게 빨리 보게 될 줄은 몰랐다.\n\n식사를 마치고 우리는 다시 위층으로 올라갔는데, 모든 일이 전날과 똑같이 흘러갔다. 애그니스는 술잔과 유리병을 같은 구석에 놓았고, 위크필드 씨는 앉아서 술을 마셨고, 꽤 많이 마셨다. 애그니스는 아버지에게 피아노를 쳐 드리고, 곁에 앉아 일을 하고 이야기를 나누었으며, 나와 도미노 놀이를 몇 판 했다. 때가 되자 애그니스는 차를 끓였고, 그 뒤 내가 책을 들고 내려오자, 들여다보더니, 자기가 그것에 대해 아는 것을 (스스로는 별것 아니라고 했지만, 결코 사소한 게 아니었다) 보여 주었고, 그것을 배우고 이해하는 가장 좋은 방법을 알려 주었다. 이 글을 쓰는 지금도 나는, 그 겸손하고 단정하고 차분한 태도의 애그니스를 보고, 그 아름답고 잔잔한 목소리를 듣는다. 훗날 애그니스가 나에게 미치게 된 그 온갖 선한 영향이, 벌써 내 가슴 위로 내려앉기 시작한다. 나는 어린 에밀리를 사랑하고, 애그니스는 사랑하지 않는다 — 아니, 결코 그런 식으로는 아니다 — 그러나 애그니스가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 선함과 평화와 진실이 있다는 것을 나는 느낀다. 그리고 오래전에 보았던, 교회의 색유리창에서 비쳐 들던 그 부드러운 빛이, 늘 애그니스에게, 그리고 내가 애그니스 곁에 있을 때면 나에게도, 또 그 둘레의 모든 것에 내려앉는다는 것을.\n\n애그니스가 밤이 깊어 물러갈 시간이 되어 자리를 뜨자, 나도 그만 가려고 위크필드 씨에게 손을 내밀었다. 하지만 그가 나를 붙들고 말했다. \"트롯우드, 우리와 함께 지내는 게 좋으냐, 아니면 다른 데로 가는 게 좋으냐?\"\n\n\"함께 지내겠습니다.\" 나는 얼른 대답했다.\n\n\"정말이냐?\"\n\n\"괜찮으시다면요. 그래도 된다면요!\"\n\n\"그런데 말이다, 여기서 우리가 사는 게 좀 따분한 생활이라, 그게 걱정이구나, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"애그니스보다 더 따분하진 않을 거예요, 어르신. 조금도 따분하지 않아요!\"\n\n\"애그니스보다라.\" 그가 천천히 커다란 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 걸어가 거기 기대며 되뇌었다. \"애그니스보다!\"\n\n그날 저녁 그는 술을 마신 터라 (아니면 내 착각이었는지) 눈이 충혈되어 있었다. 지금은 눈을 내리깔고 손으로 가리고 있어 보이지 않았지만, 조금 전에 나는 그 눈을 보았던 것이다.\n\n\"이제 와 궁금하구나,\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"내 애그니스가 나에게 싫증이 났는지. 내가 그 애한테 싫증 날 일이 어디 있겠느냐만! 하지만 그건 다르지, 그건 아주 다른 얘기야.\"\n\n그는 혼자 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 것이지, 나에게 말하는 게 아니었다. 그래서 나는 가만히 있었다.\n\n\"따분하고 낡은 집,\" 그가 말했다. \"단조로운 생활. 하지만 나는 그 애를 곁에 두어야 해. 그 애를 곁에 두어야 한다고. 내가 죽어 내 사랑하는 딸을 두고 떠날지도 모른다는, 또는 내 사랑하는 딸이 죽어 나를 두고 떠날지도 모른다는 생각이, 유령처럼 찾아와, 내 가장 행복한 시간을 괴롭히고, 오직 술에 빠져서만 가라앉힐 수—\"\n\n그는 그 단어를 채우지 않았다. 그러나 천천히 자기가 앉았던 자리로 걸어가, 빈 유리병에서 술을 따르는 동작을 기계적으로 흉내 내더니, 병을 내려놓고 다시 걸어 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그 애가 여기 있는데도 견디기 비참하다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애가 떠나고 나면 어떻겠는가? 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 그건 시도조차 할 수 없어.\"\n\n그는 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채 어찌나 오래 골똘히 잠겨 있던지, 나는 가서 그를 방해하는 위험을 무릅쓸지, 아니면 그가 몽상에서 깨어날 때까지 가만히 제자리에 있을지 결정할 수가 없었다. 마침내 그가 정신을 차리고 방 안을 둘러보다가, 그 눈길이 내 눈과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"우리와 함께 지내자고, 트롯우드, 응?\" 그가 평소의 투로, 마치 내가 방금 한 말에 대답하기라도 하듯 말했다. \"그거 반갑구나. 너는 우리 둘에게 좋은 벗이다. 네가 여기 있는 건 이로운 일이야. 나에게도 이롭고, 애그니스에게도 이롭고, 어쩌면 우리 모두에게 이롭지.\"\n\n\"저에게는 분명히 그래요, 어르신.\" 내가 말했다. \"여기 있게 돼서 정말 기뻐요.\"\n\n\"그것참 기특한 녀석이구나!\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"네가 여기 있어서 기쁜 한, 너는 여기 머물러도 좋다.\" 그는 그 다짐의 표시로 나와 악수를 하고, 내 등을 토닥였다. 그러고는, 애그니스가 자리를 뜬 뒤 밤에 할 일이 있거나, 내 즐거움을 위해 책을 읽고 싶을 때면, 그가 자기 방에 있고 또 내가 벗 삼아 함께 있고 싶다면, 언제든 내려와 그와 함께 앉아 있어도 좋다고 했다. 나는 그 배려에 고마워했다. 그리고 그가 곧이어 아래층으로 내려갔고 나도 피곤하지 않았기에, 책을 한 권 손에 들고 나도 내려가, 삼십 분쯤 그의 허락을 누려 볼 참이었다.\n\n그런데 작고 둥근 사무실에 불이 켜져 있는 것을 보고, 또 곧바로 유라이어 힙에게 끌리는 것을 느껴 — 그에게는 나를 묘하게 사로잡는 데가 있었다 — 그 대신 그 안으로 들어갔다. 유라이어는 두툼하고 커다란 책을 읽고 있었는데, 어찌나 티 나게 열중해 있던지, 비쩍 마른 집게손가락이 읽는 줄마다 따라가며, 종이 위에 달팽이처럼 끈적끈적한 자국을 남기고 있었다 (적어도 나는 그렇게 굳게 믿었다).\n\n\"오늘 밤 늦게까지 일하는구나, 유라이어.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"예, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다.\n\n그에게 더 편히 이야기를 건네려고 맞은편 걸상에 올라앉다가, 나는 그에게 미소라는 것이 없다는 것을 알아챘다. 그가 할 수 있는 거라곤 입을 옆으로 벌리고 양쪽 뺨에 딱딱한 주름을 하나씩 두 줄 잡아, 그것으로 미소를 대신하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"사무 일을 하는 건 아닙니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 무슨 일인데?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"제 법률 지식을 갈고닦는 중이지요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"티드의 『법률 실무』를 훑어보고 있습니다. 아, 티드 씨는 정말 대단한 저술가예요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n내 걸상은 어찌나 좋은 관찰 망루였던지, 그가 이 황홀한 감탄을 토한 뒤 다시 책을 읽으며 집게손가락으로 줄을 따라가는 것을 지켜보다가, 나는 그의 콧구멍이 — 얇고 뾰족했으며, 날카로운 홈이 패어 있었다 — 묘하고도 몹시 불쾌하게 벌어졌다 오므라졌다 하는 버릇이 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 좀처럼 반짝이는 법이 없는 그의 눈을 대신해, 콧구멍이 반짝이는 것 같았다.\n\n\"넌 꽤 대단한 변호사인 모양이구나?\" 한참 그를 바라보다가 내가 말했다.\n\n\"제가요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"아이고, 천만에요! 저는 아주 미천한 사람인걸요.\"\n\n그의 손에 대한 내 생각이 결코 헛것이 아니었음을, 나는 알아챘다. 그는 자주 손바닥을 맞비벼 짜내듯 말리고 데우려는 듯했고, 게다가 슬그머니 손수건에 손을 닦아 대곤 했다.\n\n\"제가 둘러봐도 가장 미천한 사람이라는 걸 잘 알지요.\" 유라이어 힙이 겸손하게 말했다. \"다른 누가 어디에 있든 간에요. 제 어머니 역시 아주 미천한 사람입니다. 저희는 보잘것없는 거처에서 삽니다, 코퍼필드 도련님. 하지만 감사할 일이 많지요. 제 아버지가 예전에 하셨던 일도 미천했습니다. 교회 묘지기였거든요.\"\n\n\"지금은 뭘 하시는데?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"지금은 영광에 동참하고 계십니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어 힙이 말했다. \"하지만 저희는 감사할 일이 많아요. 위크필드 씨와 함께 살게 된 것만 해도 제가 얼마나 감사할 일인지요!\"\n\n나는 유라이어에게 위크필드 씨와 함께한 지 오래되었느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"함께한 지 사 년째로 접어듭니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가, 읽다 만 곳을 조심스레 표시한 다음 책을 덮으며 말했다. \"제 아버지가 돌아가시고 일 년 뒤부터지요. 그것만 해도 제가 얼마나 감사할 일인지요! 위크필드 씨가 친절하게도 저에게 견습 계약서를 마련해 주시려는 그 뜻만 해도 제가 얼마나 감사할 일인지요. 안 그랬으면 어머니와 저의 미천한 형편으로는 어림도 없었을 텐데요!\"\n\n\"그럼, 견습 기간이 끝나면 너는 정식 변호사가 되겠구나?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"하느님의 은총이 있다면요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 대답했다.\n\n\"언젠가는 네가 위크필드 씨의 사업에 동업자가 될지도 모르겠네.\" 나는 그를 기쁘게 해 주려고 말했다. \"그래서 '위크필드앤힙'이나 '힙, 전 위크필드'가 되는 거지.\"\n\n\"아이고, 천만에요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다. \"저는 그러기엔 너무나 미천한걸요!\"\n\n그가 그렇게, 그 미천함 속에서, 입을 옆으로 벌리고 뺨에 주름을 잡은 채 나를 곁눈질하며 앉아 있는 모습은, 정말이지 내 방 창문 밖 들보에 새겨진 얼굴과 유난히 닮아 보였다.\n\n\"위크필드 씨는 더없이 훌륭한 분입니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"도련님이 그분을 오래 아셨다면, 제가 알려 드릴 것도 없이, 분명 저보다 훨씬 잘 아시겠지요.\"\n\n나는 그분이 훌륭한 분인 게 분명하다고 대답했다. 하지만 나 자신은 그분이 우리 고모님의 친구이긴 해도, 오래 안 사이는 아니라고 했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇군요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"도련님의 고모님은 참 좋은 분이시지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n그는 열의를 나타내고 싶을 때면 몸을 비비 꼬는 버릇이 있었는데, 그게 무척 보기 흉했다. 그 탓에 나는, 그가 우리 친척에게 한 칭찬에서 그 뱀 같은 목과 몸뚱이의 뒤틀림으로 주의가 옮겨 가 버렸다.\n\n\"참 좋은 분이세요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어 힙이 말했다. \"고모님이 애그니스 아가씨를 무척 흠모하신다지요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제가 알기로는?\"\n\n나는 \"응\" 하고 당돌하게 대답했다. 그것에 대해 아는 게 전혀 없으면서 말이다, 하느님, 용서하소서!\n\n\"도련님도 그러시기를 바랍니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"하지만 분명히 그러시겠지요.\"\n\n\"누구든 그럴 수밖에 없지.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 유라이어 힙이 말했다. \"그렇게 말씀해 주시니! 정말 맞는 말씀이에요! 미천한 저지만, 저도 그게 맞는 말이라는 걸 알지요! 아, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그는 감정이 북받쳐 걸상에서 몸을 배배 꼬며 내려오더니, 일단 내려오자, 집에 갈 채비를 하기 시작했다.\n\n\"어머니가 저를 기다리실 거예요.\" 그가 주머니에서 핼쑥하고 표정 없는 얼굴을 한 회중시계를 꺼내 보며 말했다. \"그리고 마음을 졸이시겠지요. 저희가 아주 미천하긴 해도, 코퍼필드 도련님, 서로 무척 정이 깊거든요. 언제 오후에라도 저희를 찾아오셔서, 저희 보잘것없는 거처에서 차라도 한 잔 드신다면, 제가 그렇듯 어머니도 도련님이 와 주신 걸 자랑스러워하실 거예요.\"\n\n나는 기꺼이 가겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 책을 선반에 도로 꽂으며 대답했다. \"도련님은 한동안 여기 머무르시는 거지요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\"\n\n나는 학교에 다니는 동안에는 줄곧 거기서 자라게 될 것 같다고 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇군요!\" 유라이어가 소리쳤다. \"제 생각엔 도련님이 결국 이 사업에 들어오시게 될 것 같은데요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n나는 그런 생각은 전혀 없으며, 누구도 나를 두고 그런 계획을 품고 있지 않다고 항변했다. 그러나 유라이어는 내 모든 다짐에 대해 능청스럽게, \"아, 그럼요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제 생각엔 정말 그러실 것 같아요!\", \"아, 그렇고말고요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제 생각엔 분명히 그러실 거예요!\" 하고 자꾸만 되풀이해 대꾸했다. 마침내 밤이 깊어 사무실을 떠날 채비를 마치자, 그는 불을 꺼도 괜찮으시겠냐고 나에게 물었고, 내가 \"그래\" 하고 대답하자 즉시 불을 껐다. 나와 악수를 한 다음 — 어둠 속에서 그의 손은 마치 물고기 같은 느낌이었다 — 그는 거리로 난 문을 아주 조금 열고, 살금살금 빠져나가, 문을 닫았다. 나만 남겨 두고서, 더듬더듬 집 안으로 돌아가게 한 것인데, 그게 꽤나 애를 먹였고, 그의 걸상에 걸려 넘어지기까지 했다. 짐작건대 이것이 그를 꿈에서 보게 된 직접적인 원인이었을 텐데, 나에게는 밤의 절반쯤은 되어 보이는 동안 내내 꿈을 꾸었다. 무엇보다, 꿈에서 그가 패거티 씨의 집을 띄워 해적 원정에 나서게 했는데, 돛대 꼭대기에는 '티드의 법률 실무'라고 새긴 검은 깃발이 걸려 있었고, 그 악마 같은 깃발 아래서 그가 나와 어린 에밀리를 익사시키려고 스페인령 해안으로 실어 가는 꿈이었다.\n\n이튿날 학교에 갔을 때 내 불안은 조금 가셨고, 그다음 날에는 훨씬 더 가셨다. 그렇게 차차 그것을 떨쳐 내, 채 보름도 지나지 않아 나는 새 동무들 사이에서 아주 편안하고 행복해졌다. 그들의 놀이에는 충분히 어설펐고, 공부에서도 충분히 뒤처졌다. 하지만 첫째 점에서는 습관이 나를 나아지게 해 주리라 기대했고, 둘째 점에서는 노력이 그래 주리라 기대했다. 그리하여 나는 놀이에서든 진지한 공부에서든 무척 열심히 매달려, 큰 칭찬을 받았다. 그리고 아주 짧은 사이에, 머드스톤앤그린비 시절은 어찌나 낯설어졌던지 그것이 있었다는 게 좀처럼 믿기지 않을 지경이었고, 반면에 지금의 생활은 어찌나 익숙해졌던지 오래도록 그 생활을 해 온 것만 같았다.\n\n스트롱 박사님의 학교는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 학교였다. 선과 악만큼이나 크리클 씨의 학교와는 딴판이었다. 무척 진중하고 예의 바르게 운영되었고, 건전한 체계 위에 서 있었으며, 매사에 학생들의 명예와 신의에 호소했고, 학생들이 스스로 그럴 자격이 없음을 입증하지 않는 한 그들이 그런 자질을 지녔음을 믿겠다는 뜻을 떳떳이 밝혔는데, 그것이 놀라운 효과를 냈다. 우리 모두는 이 학교를 꾸려 가고, 그 명예와 품위를 지키는 데 우리 몫이 있다고 느꼈다. 그래서 우리는 곧 그곳에 따뜻하게 정이 들었다 — 적어도 나만큼은 분명히 그랬고, 내가 다니는 동안 다르게 구는 아이는 한 명도 본 적이 없다 — 그리고 학교의 명예를 드높이고자 기꺼운 마음으로 배웠다. 수업이 없는 시간에는 멋진 놀이를 했고, 자유도 넘쳤다. 하지만 그때조차도, 내 기억으로는, 우리는 마을에서 좋은 평판을 들었고, 우리의 행색이나 태도로 스트롱 박사님과 스트롱 박사님 학생들의 명성에 먹칠을 하는 일은 거의 없었다.\n\n상급생 가운데 몇몇은 박사님 댁에서 하숙을 했는데, 그들을 통해 나는 박사님 내력의 몇 가지 자세한 사정을 간접적으로 알게 되었다. 이를테면, 박사님이 서재에서 본 그 아름다운 젊은 부인과 결혼한 지 아직 일 년도 안 되었고, 사랑해서 결혼한 것이라는 것 — 부인에게는 단돈 육 펜스도 없었고, 가난한 친척이 세상에 가득해서 (우리 아이들 말로는) 박사님을 집 밖으로 떼 지어 몰아낼 판이었으니까. 또한 박사님이 늘 생각에 잠긴 듯한 태도를 보이는 것은, 노상 그리스어 어근을 찾는 일에 매달려 있기 때문이라는 것. 나는 순진하고 무지한 탓에, 그게 박사님의 식물학적 열병인 줄로만 알았다. 특히 박사님이 거닐 때면 늘 땅바닥을 내려다보았기 때문이다. 그것이 박사님이 구상 중인 새 사전을 위한, 낱말의 어근이라는 것을 알게 되기 전까지는 말이다. 수학에 소질이 있던 우리 우두머리 학생 애덤스가, 들리는 말로는, 박사님 계획대로, 또 박사님의 진행 속도로, 이 사전을 완성하는 데 걸릴 시간을 계산해 보았다고 했다. 그의 계산으로는, 박사님의 지난 예순두 번째 생일부터 세어, 천육백사십구 년이 걸릴 거라고 했다.\n\n하지만 박사님 자신은 학교 전체의 우상이었다. 그가 그렇지 않았다면 형편없이 짜인 학교였을 게 틀림없는데, 그는 더없이 친절한 사람이었고, 그 안에는, 담장 위 항아리들의 돌처럼 굳은 마음마저 움직일 만한 순진한 믿음이 깃들어 있었으니까. 그가 집 옆쪽 안마당을 거닐 때면, 떠돌이 까마귀와 갈까마귀들이 고개를 음흉하게 갸웃하며 그를 좇아보았는데, 마치 세상일에 관해서라면 자기들이 그보다 얼마나 더 약은지를 안다는 듯했다. 그럴 때, 어떤 부랑자든 그의 삐걱대는 신발 가까이 다가가, 가련한 사연 한 토막에 그의 관심을 끌기만 하면, 그 부랑자는 이틀은 팔자가 폈다. 이는 그 집에서 어찌나 유명했던지, 선생들과 우두머리 학생들은 이 침입자들을 모서리에서 가로채고, 창문으로 나가, 박사님이 그 존재를 알아채기 전에 안마당에서 내쫓느라 애를 썼다. 박사님이 종종걸음으로 오락가락하는 동안, 더러는 그가 까맣게 모른 채로, 그의 코앞 몇 발짝 안에서 일이 다행히도 처리되곤 했다. 자기 영역 바깥에서, 보호받지 못할 때면, 그는 털 깎으러 온 사람 앞의 영락없는 양이었다. 그는 자기 다리에서 각반을 벗어서까지 내줄 사람이었다. 사실, 우리 사이에는 이런 이야기가 떠돌았다 (무슨 근거였는지는 도통 모르고, 알았던 적도 없지만, 하도 오랜 세월 믿어 와서 이젠 사실이라고 꽤 확신하게 되었다). 어느 겨울, 서리 내린 날, 박사님이 정말로 자기 각반을 어떤 거지 여인에게 내주었다는 것이다. 그 여인은 잘생긴 갓난아기를 그 각반으로 싸서 이 집 저 집 보여 주며 다녀, 동네에 적잖은 소동을 일으켰다는데, 그 각반은 누구나 다 알아보았다. 대성당만큼이나 동네에서 익히 알려진 물건이었으니까. 전설은 이렇게 덧붙였다. 그 각반을 알아보지 못한 단 한 사람이 바로 박사님 자신이었는데, 얼마 뒤 그 각반이, 이런 물건을 진을 받고 바꿔 주는 평판이 그리 좋지 않은 어느 작은 중고품 가게 문 앞에 내걸렸을 때, 박사님이 그것을 흐뭇하게 만지작거리는 모습이 여러 번 목격되었다는 것이다. 마치 무늬의 진귀한 새로움에 감탄하며, 자기 것보다 더 나은 물건이라 여기는 듯이.\n\n박사님이 그 어여쁜 젊은 아내와 함께 있는 모습은 보기에 무척 흐뭇했다. 그는 아내에 대한 애정을 드러낼 때 아버지처럼 자애로운 태도를 보였는데, 그것만으로도 그가 선한 사람임을 말해 주는 듯했다. 나는 복숭아가 있는 정원에서 그들이 거니는 것을 자주 보았고, 서재나 응접실에서 더 가까이서 그들을 살펴볼 때도 더러 있었다. 부인은 내 눈에 박사님을 무척 살뜰히 보살피고, 그를 무척 좋아하는 듯이 보였다. 다만 사전에 절실히 관심이 있다고는 결코 생각하지 않았다. 박사님은 그 사전의 거추장스러운 토막들을 늘 호주머니며 모자 안감 속에 넣고 다녔고, 거닐면서 으레 부인에게 그것을 풀어 설명하는 것 같았다.\n\n나는 스트롱 부인을 자주 보았는데, 부인이 내가 박사님께 소개받던 그날 아침에 나를 마음에 들어 했고, 그 후로 늘 나에게 친절하며 관심을 가져 주었기 때문이기도 했고, 또 부인이 애그니스를 무척 좋아해서 우리 집에 자주 드나들었기 때문이기도 했다. 내가 보기에 부인과 위크필드 씨 사이에는 묘한 어색함이 있었는데 (부인은 그를 무서워하는 듯했다), 그것이 좀처럼 가시지 않았다. 부인이 저녁에 우리 집에 왔을 때면, 늘 그가 집까지 바래다주겠다는 것을 마다하고, 대신 나와 함께 달아나곤 했다. 그리고 때때로, 우리가 아무도 마주치지 않으리라 여기며 대성당 안마당을 함께 명랑하게 달려갈 때면, 잭 맬던 씨와 마주치곤 했는데, 그는 늘 우리를 보고 놀라워했다.\n\n스트롱 부인의 어머니는 내가 무척 좋아한 부인이었다. 이름은 마클럼 부인이었는데, 우리 아이들은 그분을 '노병'이라고 부르곤 했다. 통솔력이 대단하고, 박사님에 맞서 친척의 대군을 거느리는 솜씨가 능란했기 때문이다. 작은 키에 눈매가 날카로운 부인으로, 차려입을 때면 한 가지 변함없는 모자를 썼는데, 거기엔 조화 몇 송이가 장식되어 있고, 그 꽃 위에 떠 있는 듯한 조화 나비 두 마리가 달려 있었다. 우리 사이에는 이 모자가 프랑스에서 온 것이고, 그 솜씨 좋은 나라 사람들의 손끝에서나 나올 법한 것이라는 미신이 떠돌았다. 하지만 내가 그것에 대해 확실히 아는 거라곤, 마클럼 부인이 어디에 나타나든 그 모자도 으레 저녁이면 나타났다는 것, 그것이 힌두식 바구니에 담겨 친목 모임에 들려 다녔다는 것, 그 나비들에게는 끊임없이 떨리는 재주가 있었다는 것, 그리고 그것들이 부지런한 벌처럼 빛나는 시간을 알차게 보냈다는 것 — 스트롱 박사님의 돈으로 — 뿐이다.\n\n나는 이 노병을 — 무례하게 그렇게 부르려는 건 아니다 — 어느 날 밤 꽤 잘 살펴보게 되었는데, 그날 밤은 내가 따로 이야기할 어떤 일 때문에 나에게 잊지 못할 밤이 되었다. 잭 맬던 씨가 인도로 떠나는 것을 기념해 박사님 댁에서 열린 작은 모임의 밤이었다. 그는 사관후보생인지 뭔지로 인도에 가는 길이었는데, 위크필드 씨가 마침내 그 일을 마무리 지어 주었던 것이다. 마침 그날은 박사님의 생일이기도 했다. 우리는 휴일을 보냈고, 아침에 박사님께 선물을 드렸고, 우두머리 학생을 통해 박사님께 축사를 했으며, 목이 쉬고 박사님이 눈물을 흘릴 때까지 만세를 외쳤다. 그리고 이제, 저녁에 위크필드 씨와 애그니스와 나는 사사로이 박사님과 함께 차를 마시러 갔다.\n\n잭 맬던 씨는 우리보다 먼저 와 있었다. 스트롱 부인은 흰옷에 앵두빛 리본을 두르고서 우리가 들어갔을 때 피아노를 치고 있었고, 그는 부인 위로 몸을 기울여 악보를 넘겨 주고 있었다. 부인이 고개를 돌렸을 때, 그 또렷한 붉고 흰 낯빛이 여느 때처럼 화사하게 꽃 같지는 않다고 나는 생각했다. 하지만 부인은 무척 어여뻤다. 놀랍도록 어여뻤다.\n\n\"제가 깜빡했네요, 박사님.\" 우리가 자리에 앉자 스트롱 부인의 어머니가 말했다. \"생신 인사를 드리는 걸요 — 물론 짐작하시겠지만, 제 경우엔 그게 그저 입에 발린 인사와는 한참 거리가 멀지요. 앞으로 행복한 생신을 많이 맞으시기를 빌게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 부인.\" 박사님이 대답했다.\n\n\"많이, 많이, 많이 맞으세요, 행복한 생신을.\" 노병이 말했다. \"박사님 자신을 위해서만이 아니라, 애니를 위해서도, 존 맬던을 위해서도, 다른 많은 사람을 위해서도요. 나에겐 엊그제 일만 같구나, 존, 네가 자그마한 꼬마였을 때, 코퍼필드 도련님보다 머리 하나는 작았을 때, 뒤뜰 구스베리 덤불 뒤에서 애니에게 어린애 같은 사랑을 속삭이던 게 말이다.\"\n\n\"엄마, 어머나.\" 스트롱 부인이 말했다. \"이제 와서 그 얘긴 그만하세요.\"\n\n\"애니, 우습게 굴지 마라.\" 어머니가 받았다. \"이제 늙은 유부녀가 다 되어서 그런 얘기를 듣고 얼굴을 붉힐 거라면, 대체 언제는 얼굴 안 붉히고 듣겠다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"늙은?\" 잭 맬던 씨가 외쳤다. \"애니가요? 어이쿠!\"\n\n\"그래, 존.\" 노병이 받았다. \"사실상 늙은 유부녀지. 나이로야 늙지 않았지 — 내가 언제 스무 살 처녀를 두고 나이로 늙었다고 말한 적이 있더냐, 그런 말을 들은 사람이 누가 있어! — 하지만 네 사촌은 박사님의 아내이고, 그런 만큼, 내가 말한 그대로다. 너에겐 다행한 일이지, 존, 네 사촌이 박사님의 아내라는 게. 너는 그분에게서 영향력 있고 친절한 벗을 얻은 거고, 네가 그럴 자격이 있다면 더욱 친절해지시리라고, 내 감히 점쳐 본다. 나는 헛된 자존심 따위 없어. 우리 집안 식구 중에 벗이 필요한 이들이 더러 있다는 걸 솔직히 인정하는 데 한 번도 망설인 적이 없지. 너도 한때 그중 하나였잖느냐, 네 사촌의 영향력이 너에게 벗을 마련해 주기 전까지는.\"\n\n박사님은, 그 너그러운 마음으로, 별일 아니라는 듯, 또 잭 맬던 씨에게 더는 그 일을 들먹이지 않도록 손을 내저었다. 하지만 마클럼 부인은 자리를 박사님 옆자리로 옮기더니, 부채를 그의 윗도리 소매에 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 정말로, 박사님, 제가 이 얘기를 좀 자꾸 곱씹는 것처럼 보여도 양해해 주세요, 워낙 절절히 느껴서 그러니까요. 이건 정말 제 광적인 집착이라고 할까요, 제가 그렇게 좋아하는 주제거든요. 박사님은 저희에게 축복이에요. 정말이지 박사님은 하늘이 주신 선물이세요, 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"별말씀을, 별말씀을.\" 박사님이 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아니, 죄송하지만요.\" 노병이 받았다. \"여기 우리 소중하고 막역한 벗인 위크필드 씨밖에 없는 자리에서, 제가 입을 다물 수는 없지요. 박사님이 계속 그러시면, 저는 장모의 권리를 내세우기 시작할 거예요, 그리고 박사님을 야단칠 거고요. 저는 더없이 정직하고 거침이 없는 사람이에요. 지금 제가 하는 말은, 박사님이 애니에게 청혼해서 저를 놀라움으로 압도하셨을 때 — 제가 얼마나 놀랐는지 기억하시죠? — 그때 제가 했던 말 그대로예요. 청혼했다는 그 사실 자체에 그렇게 엄청나게 뜻밖인 게 있었다는 건 아니에요 — 그렇게 말하면 우습지요! — 다만 박사님이 그 애의 가엾은 아버지를 아셨고, 그 애가 생후 여섯 달 갓난아기 때부터 알아 오셨으니, 박사님을 그런 눈으로는 전혀 생각해 본 적이 없었던 거예요, 아니 결혼할 사람이라고도 도통 생각 못 했지요 — 그저 그뿐이에요, 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 박사님이 사람 좋게 받았다. \"괘념치 마세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 괘념해요.\" 노병이 부채를 그의 입술에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"아주 많이 괘념하지요. 제가 틀렸다면 반박당하라고, 이런 일들을 끄집어내는 거예요. 자! 그래서 제가 애니에게 말을 꺼냈고, 무슨 일이 있었는지 일러 줬어요. '얘야, 글쎄 스트롱 박사님이 분명히 너를 두고 점잖게 마음을 밝히시고 청혼을 하셨단다.' 제가 조금이라도 그걸 밀어붙였나요? 아니에요. 저는 말했지요. '자 애니, 지금 당장 솔직히 말해 보렴. 네 마음에 둔 사람이 없니?' '엄마,' 그 애가 울면서 말하더군요, '저는 너무 어려요' — 그건 더없이 맞는 말이었지요 — '그리고 제게 마음이라는 게 있기는 한지도 잘 모르겠어요.' '그럼, 얘야,' 제가 말했지요, '믿어도 좋다, 네 마음은 자유로운 거란다. 어쨌거나, 우리 사랑하는 딸아,' 제가 말했지요, '스트롱 박사님이 마음이 어지러운 상태시고, 대답을 들으셔야 해. 지금 같은 애타는 상태로 두실 수는 없어.' '엄마,' 애니가 여전히 울면서 말하더군요, '제가 없으면 그분이 불행하실까요? 그러시다면, 저는 그분을 무척 우러르고 존경하니까, 그분과 결혼해야겠어요.' 그렇게 정해진 거예요. 그러고 나서야, 그제야 비로소, 제가 애니에게 말했지요. '애니, 스트롱 박사님은 네 남편이 되어 주실 뿐 아니라, 네 돌아가신 아버지를 대신하실 거다. 우리 집안의 가장을 대신하실 거고, 우리 집안의 지혜와 지위를, 그리고 말하자면, 재력을 대신하실 거야. 한마디로, 우리 집안에 하늘이 주신 선물이 되실 게다.' 저는 그때 그 말을 썼고, 오늘 다시 그 말을 썼지요. 저에게 무슨 미덕이 있다면, 그건 한결같음이에요.\"\n\n이 연설이 이어지는 내내 딸은 눈을 땅바닥에 박은 채 아무 말 없이 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그 사촌은 곁에 서서 역시 땅바닥을 바라보고 있었다. 이제 부인이 떨리는 목소리로 아주 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"엄마, 다 끝나신 거죠?\" \"아니, 우리 애니,\" 노병이 받았다. \"아직 다 못 끝냈단다. 네가 물어보니, 얘야, 다 못 끝냈다고 대답하마. 나는 네가 정말이지 네 친정 식구들한테 좀 매정하다고 불평하는 거란다. 그리고 너한테 불평해 봐야 소용없으니, 네 남편한테 불평할 작정이다. 자, 박사님, 저 어리석은 부인 좀 보세요.\"\n\n박사님이 그 친절한 얼굴을, 순진하고 온화한 미소를 띤 채, 부인 쪽으로 돌리자, 부인은 고개를 더 떨구었다. 나는 위크필드 씨가 부인을 가만히 응시하는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"내가 며칠 전에 저 못된 것한테 우연히, 박사님께 말씀드릴 만한 — 정말이지, 마땅히 말씀드려야 한다고 생각하는 — 집안일이 하나 있다고 했더니,\" 어머니가 부인을 향해 장난스레 고개와 부채를 흔들며 이어 갔다. \"글쎄 저 애가, 그걸 말씀드리는 건 부탁드리는 거나 마찬가지고, 박사님이 워낙 너그러우셔서 저 애가 부탁하면 늘 들어주시니, 그래서 말씀 안 드리겠다지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"애니, 우리 딸.\" 박사님이 말했다. \"그건 잘못이오. 그건 나에게서 기쁨을 빼앗는 일이야.\"\n\n\"제가 저 애한테 한 말이랑 거의 토씨 하나 안 틀려요!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"자 정말로, 다음번에, 저 애가 이런 이유로 박사님께 말씀 안 드리려는 게 뭔지 제가 알게 되면, 박사님, 제가 직접 말씀드릴 마음이 굴뚝같네요.\"\n\n\"그래 주시면 기쁘겠습니다.\" 박사님이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럴까요?\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그렇게 하지요!\" 노병이 말했다. \"약속한 거예요.\" 그러고는, 짐작건대 자기 뜻을 관철한 셈이어서, 박사님의 손을 부채로 여러 번 가볍게 두드린 다음 (먼저 부채에 입을 맞추고서), 의기양양하게 원래 자리로 돌아갔다.\n\n손님 몇이 더 들어왔는데, 그중에는 선생 둘과 애덤스도 있었다. 이야기는 자연히 잭 맬던 씨와 그의 항해, 그가 갈 나라, 그리고 그의 갖가지 계획과 전망 쪽으로 흘러갔다. 그는 그날 밤 저녁을 먹은 뒤, 역마차를 타고 그레이브젠드로 떠날 예정이었다. 그가 항해할 배가 거기 정박해 있었던 것이다. 그리고 — 휴가로 본국에 돌아오거나 건강 때문에 오는 게 아니라면 — 몇 년이 될지 모를 만큼 가 있을 작정이었다. 내 기억으로는, 인도라는 나라가 꽤나 오해받는 나라이고, 호랑이 한두 마리와 한낮 더운 때의 약간의 더위 말고는 거슬릴 만한 게 없다는 데에 모두가 입을 모았다. 나로 말하면, 잭 맬던 씨를 현대판 신드바드로 여기고, 그가 동방의 온갖 왕족과 막역한 벗이 되어, 천막 아래 앉아, 똘똘 말린 황금 담뱃대를 — 곧게 펴면 일 마일은 됨 직한 — 빠는 모습을 그려 보았다.\n\n스트롱 부인은 노래를 무척 잘했다. 부인이 혼자 노래하는 것을 자주 들었던 나는 잘 알았다. 하지만 사람들 앞에서 노래하기가 두려웠던 건지, 아니면 그날 저녁 목 상태가 좋지 않았던 건지, 어쨌든 부인은 영 노래를 부르지 못했다. 한번은 사촌 맬던과 이중창을 해 보려 했지만, 시작조차 하지 못했다. 그러고는, 혼자 불러 보려 했을 때도, 곱게 시작하다가, 갑자기 목소리가 잦아들어, 건반 위로 고개를 떨군 채 몹시 괴로워했다. 마음씨 좋은 박사님은 부인이 긴장한 거라며, 부인을 달래 주려고 카드 돌림 놀이를 하자고 했다. 박사님은 트롬본 연주술만큼이나 카드에 대해 잘 알았지만 말이다. 그런데 나는 노병이 곧장 그를 자기 짝으로 잡아채는 것을 보았다. 그리고 입문의 첫 절차랍시고, 박사님에게 호주머니에 있는 은화를 죄다 자기한테 내놓으라고 가르쳤다.\n\n우리는 즐겁게 놀았는데, 박사님의 실수 덕에 놀이가 한결 더 즐거워졌다. 나비들의 감시에도 아랑곳없이, 또 그 나비들을 크게 약 올리며, 박사님은 셀 수 없이 많은 실수를 저질렀다. 스트롱 부인은 몸이 영 좋지 않다는 이유로 놀이를 마다했고, 그 사촌 맬던도 짐을 좀 싸야 한다며 빠졌다. 하지만 짐을 다 싸자, 그는 돌아와서 부인과 함께 소파에 앉아 이야기를 나누었다. 이따금 부인은 와서 박사님의 패를 들여다보고, 무엇을 낼지 일러 주었다. 박사님 위로 몸을 숙일 때 부인은 무척 핼쑥했고, 카드를 가리키는 손가락이 떨리는 것 같았다. 하지만 박사님은 부인의 보살핌에 그저 흐뭇해할 뿐, 설령 그랬다 하더라도 그것을 알아채지 못했다.\n\n저녁 식사 때 우리는 그다지 흥겹지 못했다. 그런 종류의 작별이 거북한 일이고, 그것이 가까워질수록 더 거북해진다는 것을, 다들 느끼는 듯했다. 잭 맬던 씨는 무척 말이 많은 척했지만, 편치 못한 기색이었고, 일을 더 그르쳤다. 게다가 내가 보기에, 노병 탓에 분위기가 나아지지 않았다. 그녀가 자꾸만 잭 맬던 씨의 어린 시절 일들을 들추어냈기 때문이다.\n\n하지만 박사님은, 분명히 자기가 모두를 즐겁게 해 주고 있다고 느꼈는지, 무척 흡족해하며, 우리 모두가 더없이 즐거움의 절정에 있다고 믿어 의심치 않았다.\n\n\"애니, 우리 딸.\" 박사님이 회중시계를 보고 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"네 사촌 잭이 떠날 시간이 지났구나. 그를 붙잡아 두어선 안 되지, 세월과 조수는 — 이 경우엔 둘 다 관련이 있는데 — 사람을 기다려 주지 않으니까. 잭 맬던 씨, 자네 앞에는 긴 항해와 낯선 나라가 놓여 있네. 하지만 많은 이들이 그 둘을 겪었고, 세상 끝나는 날까지 많은 이들이 또 그 둘을 겪을 걸세. 자네가 도전하려는 그 바람은 수천수만 명을 부귀로 실어 갔고, 또 수천수만 명을 무사히 데려왔다네.\"\n\n\"가슴 뭉클한 일이에요.\" 마클럼 부인이 말했다. \"어떻게 봐도, 가슴 뭉클하지요, 갓난아기 때부터 알아 온 훌륭한 청년이, 세상 저쪽 끝으로 떠나가고, 아는 것 다 뒤에 두고, 앞일은 모르는 채로 가는 걸 보는 게 말이에요. 그런 희생을 치르는 청년이라면,\" 박사님을 바라보며, \"정말이지 변함없는 지원과 후원을 받을 자격이 있지요.\"\n\n\"자네에게도 세월은 빨리 흐를 걸세, 잭 맬던 씨.\" 박사님이 이어 갔다. \"우리 모두에게도 빨리 흐르고. 어쩌면 우리 중 누군가는, 자연의 순리대로라면, 자네가 돌아올 때 맞아 줄 수 있으리라고 기대하기 어려울지도 모르지. 그다음으로 좋은 것은 그러기를 바라는 것이고, 내가 바로 그렇다네. 좋은 충고로 자네를 지치게 하진 않겠네. 자네에겐 오랫동안 좋은 본보기가 곁에 있었으니, 자네 사촌 애니 말일세. 그 사람의 미덕을 되도록 가까이 본받게나.\"\n\n마클럼 부인은 부채질을 하며 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"잘 가게, 잭.\" 박사님이 일어서며 말했고, 그러자 우리도 모두 일어섰다. \"나가는 길의 순조로운 항해, 외국에서의 번창하는 앞길, 그리고 행복한 귀향을 비네!\"\n\n우리는 모두 그 축배를 들고, 모두 잭 맬던 씨와 악수를 했다. 그런 다음 그는 거기 있던 부인들에게 황급히 작별을 고하고, 서둘러 문으로 나갔는데, 마차에 오르는 그를, 그러려고 잔디밭에 모여 있던 우리 아이들이 우레 같은 만세 일제사격으로 맞아 주었다. 나도 그 무리에 끼어 대열을 불리려고 달려간 터라, 마차가 굴러갈 때 마차 가까이에 있었다. 그 소란과 먼지 속에서, 나는 잭 맬던 씨가 어지러운 얼굴로, 손에 앵두빛 무언가를 쥐고 덜컹덜컹 지나가는 것을 본 듯한 생생한 인상을 받았다.\n\n박사님을 위한 만세 일제사격이 한 번 더, 그리고 박사님 부인을 위한 만세가 또 한 번 터진 뒤, 아이들은 흩어졌고, 나는 집 안으로 돌아갔다. 들어가 보니 손님들이 모두 박사님 둘레에 무리 지어 서서, 잭 맬던 씨가 어떻게 떠났는지, 그가 그것을 어떻게 견뎠는지, 어떻게 느꼈는지, 그 밖의 이런저런 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 이런 이야기가 한창일 때, 마클럼 부인이 외쳤다. \"애니는 어디 있지?\"\n\n애니는 거기 없었다. 그리고 사람들이 부인을 불러도, 애니는 대답이 없었다. 하지만 무슨 일인가 싶어 다들 무리 지어 방에서 우르르 몰려나가 보니, 부인이 현관 마룻바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견했다. 처음엔 다들 크게 놀랐지만, 부인이 까무러친 것이고, 그 까무러침이 흔히 쓰는 방법으로 곧 풀리고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그러자 박사님이 부인의 머리를 자기 무릎 위로 받쳐 올리고, 손으로 부인의 곱슬머리를 쓸어 넘기며, 둘레를 둘러보고 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 애니! 워낙 정이 깊고 마음이 여리니! 옛 소꿉동무이자 벗이었던 — 가장 아끼던 사촌과 — 헤어진 게 이렇게 만든 거야. 아! 안됐구나! 내가 다 가슴이 아파!\"\n\n부인이 눈을 떠서, 자기가 어디 있는지, 또 우리가 모두 둘레에 서 있다는 것을 알아챘을 때, 부축을 받아 일어났다. 일어나면서, 부인은 고개를 돌려 박사님의 어깨에 기댔다 — 아니면 얼굴을 묻으려는 것이었는지, 어느 쪽인지는 모르겠다. 우리는 부인을 박사님과 그 어머니에게 맡겨 두려고 응접실로 들어갔다. 그런데 부인은, 들리는 말로는, 아침 이래로 어느 때보다 몸이 나아졌으니, 차라리 우리 사이에 끼고 싶다고 했단다. 그래서 다들 부인을 데리고 들어왔는데, 내가 보기에 무척 핼쑥하고 기운 없는 모습이었고, 부인을 소파에 앉혔다.\n\n\"애니, 우리 딸.\" 어머니가 부인의 옷매무새를 손보며 말했다. \"이것 보렴! 너 리본 하나를 잃어버렸구나. 누가 좀 찾아 주시겠어요, 리본을, 앵두빛 리본을요?\"\n\n그것은 부인이 가슴께에 달고 있던 것이었다. 우리는 모두 그것을 찾았다. 나 자신도 분명히 사방을 다 뒤졌다 — 하지만 아무도 찾지 못했다.\n\n\"애니, 마지막으로 그걸 어디서 봤는지 기억나니?\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n부인이, 조금 전까지만 해도 분명히 잘 달고 있었던 것 같은데, 굳이 찾을 것까지는 없다고 대답했을 때, 나는 내가 어떻게 부인이 핼쑥하다고, 아니 활활 타듯 붉은 것 말고 다른 무엇이라고 생각할 수 있었던 건지 의아했다.\n\n그런데도 그것을 다시 찾았지만, 여전히 못 찾았다. 부인은 제발 더는 찾지 말아 달라고 간청했다. 하지만 부인이 완전히 회복되고 손님들이 떠날 때까지, 그것은 띄엄띄엄 계속 찾아졌다.\n\n우리는 무척 천천히 집으로 걸어갔다, 위크필드 씨와 애그니스와 나는. 애그니스와 나는 달빛을 감탄하며 보았고, 위크필드 씨는 땅바닥에서 좀처럼 눈을 떼지 않았다. 마침내 우리 집 문 앞에 다다랐을 때, 애그니스는 자기가 작은 손가방을 두고 왔다는 것을 알아챘다. 애그니스에게 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있어 기뻤던 나는, 그것을 가지러 달려 돌아갔다.\n\n나는 그것을 두고 온 저녁 식사 방으로 들어갔는데, 사람 하나 없이 캄캄했다. 하지만 그 방과 불이 켜진 박사님 서재 사이의 연결문이 열려 있어서, 나는 내가 온 까닭을 말하고 촛불을 얻으려고 그쪽으로 갔다.\n\n박사님은 난롯가의 안락의자에 앉아 있었고, 그 젊은 아내는 박사님 발치의 걸상에 앉아 있었다. 박사님은 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며, 그 끝도 없는 사전에서 어떤 이론에 대한 필사된 설명인지 진술인지를 소리 내어 읽고 있었고, 부인은 박사님을 올려다보고 있었다. 하지만 내가 일찍이 본 적 없는 그런 얼굴로. 그 얼굴은 생김새가 너무도 아름다웠고, 잿빛으로 너무도 핼쑥했고, 너무도 넋이 빠진 채 굳어 있었고, 무언지 모를 것에 대한, 사납고, 몽유병자 같고, 꿈결 같은 공포로 너무도 가득했다. 두 눈은 활짝 열려 있었고, 갈색 머리카락은 잃어버린 리본이 없어 흐트러진 채로, 두 갈래 풍성한 다발이 되어 어깨와 흰 드레스 위로 흘러내려 있었다. 그 표정을 또렷이 기억하면서도, 나는 그것이 무엇을 나타냈는지 말할 수 없다. 이제 와, 더 나이 든 내 판단 앞에 다시 떠오르는 그것이 나에게 무엇을 나타내는지조차 말할 수 없다. 뉘우침, 굴욕, 수치, 자존심, 사랑, 그리고 믿음 — 나는 그것을 다 본다. 그리고 그 모든 것 속에서, 무언지 모를 것에 대한 그 공포를 본다.\n\n내가 들어가서 내가 온 까닭을 말하자, 부인이 정신을 차렸다. 그것은 박사님도 놀라게 했는데, 내가 식탁에서 가져갔던 촛불을 도로 갖다 놓으려고 돌아갔을 때, 박사님은 그 아버지 같은 태도로 부인의 머리를 쓰다듬으며, 자기가 인정머리 없는 수벌이라 부인이 자기를 꾀어 계속 읽게 만들도록 내버려 두었다고, 이제 부인을 자러 보내야겠다고 말하고 있었다.\n\n하지만 부인은 빠르고 다급한 투로, 자기를 머무르게 해 달라고 — 그날 밤 자기가 박사님의 믿음을 받고 있다는 것을 확신할 수 있게 해 달라고 (부인이 이런 뜻으로 띄엄띄엄 중얼거리는 것을 나는 들었다) 박사님에게 청했다. 그리고, 내가 방을 나가 문밖으로 나설 때 나를 흘끗 본 다음, 부인이 다시 박사님 쪽으로 돌아설 때, 나는 부인이 두 손을 박사님 무릎 위에 포개고, 좀 가라앉기는 했으나 아까와 똑같은 그 얼굴로, 박사님이 다시 읽기를 이어 갈 때 그를 올려다보는 것을 보았다.\n\n그것은 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼고, 나는 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤에도 그것을 기억했다. 때가 되면 이야기할 기회가 있을 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. SOMEBODY TURNS UP\n\n내가 도망친 뒤로 패거티 이야기를 꺼낼 겨를이 없었다. 하지만 물론, 도버에 자리를 잡자마자 나는 그녀에게 편지를 한 통 썼고, 고모님이 나를 정식으로 보호하에 두었을 때 또 한 통, 모든 자초지종을 빠짐없이 적은 더 긴 편지를 보냈다. 스트롱 박사 학교에 자리를 잡고서는 다시 편지를 써서 내 행복한 처지와 앞날을 상세히 알렸다. 딕 씨가 준 돈을 쓰는 데서는, 이 마지막 편지에 동봉해 그녀에게 빌렸던 돈을 갚으려고 우편으로 반 기니짜리 금화를 부치면서 느낀 기쁨에 견줄 만한 즐거움을 결코 끌어낼 수 없었으리라. 바로 그 편지에서, 그 전이 아니라, 나는 당나귀 수레를 끌던 젊은이 이야기를 적었다.\n\n이런 소식에 패거티는, 간결하지는 않았으되, 상인의 점원만큼이나 즉각 답장을 보냈다. 내 여정에 대해 자기가 느낀 바를 적어 보려는 시도에 그녀의 온갖 표현력이 (잉크로 적는 솜씨가 대단치 않았던 것은 분명하지만) 죄다 소진되었다. 끝도 없이 — 잉크 얼룩 말고는 — 두서없고 감탄사뿐인 문장들의 첫머리가 종이 넉 면을 가득 채웠으나, 그녀에게 아무런 위안이 되어 주지 못했다. 그러나 그 얼룩들이 나에게는 어떤 명문보다도 더 많은 것을 말해 주었다. 패거티가 종이가 다 젖도록 펑펑 울었다는 것을 보여 주었으니, 그보다 더 무엇을 바랐겠는가?\n\n나는 그녀가 아직 고모님을 썩 살갑게 받아들이지 못한다는 것을 별 어려움 없이 알아챘다. 그토록 오랫동안 정반대로 마음을 굳혀 온 터라, 새 사정을 받아들이기에는 통보가 너무 갑작스러웠던 것이다. 그녀는 사람 속은 도무지 알 수 없다고 썼다. 베시 양이라는 분이 사람들이 여태 알고 있던 모습과 그토록 달라 보일 수 있다니, 그건 정말 교훈이라고! — 그게 그녀가 쓴 말이었다. 그녀는 분명 아직도 베시 양을 두려워하고 있었으니, 베시 양께 감사 인사를 전하면서도 잔뜩 움츠러든 채였다. 그리고 나 또한 두려워하는 게 분명했으니, 내가 머지않아 또 도망칠 가능성을 마음에 품고 있었던 것이다. 야머스까지 가는 마차 삯은 언제든 말만 하면 그녀에게서 받을 수 있다고 거듭거듭 흘린 암시로 미루어 보면 말이다.\n\n그녀는 나를 몹시 마음 아프게 한 소식을 하나 전해 주었다. 다름 아니라, 우리 옛집의 가구가 경매로 팔렸고, 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양은 떠나 버렸으며, 집은 세를 놓거나 팔려고 문을 닫아걸었다는 것이었다. 하느님은 아실 테지만, 그들이 거기 살던 동안에는 나에게 그 집에 대한 아무런 몫도 없었다. 하지만 그 정든 옛집이 그렇게 완전히 버려졌다고 생각하니 마음이 아팠다. 정원에는 잡초가 키 높이 자라고, 오솔길에는 떨어진 잎들이 젖은 채 두껍게 쌓여 있을 것을 생각하니. 나는 상상해 보았다. 겨울바람이 그 집을 휘돌며 울부짖고, 차가운 빗줄기가 창유리를 두드리고, 달빛이 텅 빈 방들의 벽에 유령을 그리며 밤새도록 그 적막을 지켜보는 모습을. 나는 교회 묘지의, 나무 밑에 있는 그 무덤을 새삼 떠올렸다. 그러자 이제는 그 집마저 죽어 버린 것 같았고, 아버지와 어머니에 얽힌 모든 것이 다 빛바래 사라져 버린 듯했다.\n\n패거티의 편지에 다른 소식은 없었다. 바키스 씨는 더없이 훌륭한 남편이라고 그녀는 적었다. 비록 아직도 조금 인색하긴 하지만 말이다. 하지만 누구에게나 결점은 있는 법이고, 그녀에게도 결점이 많다고 했다(도대체 그게 뭔지 나는 도무지 모르겠지만). 그리고 그가 안부를 전한다고 했고, 내 작은 침실은 언제나 나를 위해 마련되어 있다고 했다. 패거티 씨는 잘 지내고, 햄도 잘 지내고, 거미지 부인은 그저 그렇고, 어린 에밀리는 사랑을 전하지 않겠다지만 패거티가 원한다면 대신 전해도 좋다고 했단다.\n\n이 모든 소식을 나는 고모님께 빠짐없이 전해 드렸다. 다만 어린 에밀리 이야기만은 나 혼자 간직했으니, 본능적으로 고모님이 그 아이에게 그리 다정하게 마음을 기울이지 않으리라는 느낌이 들었던 것이다. 내가 아직 스트롱 박사 학교에 갓 들어갔을 무렵, 고모님은 나를 보러 캔터베리까지 몇 차례 행차하셨는데, 늘 엉뚱한 시간에 나타나셨다. 짐작건대 나를 불시에 덮칠 셈이었던 것이다. 그러나 내가 공부에 충실하고 평판도 좋으며, 사방에서 학교에서 빠르게 두각을 나타낸다는 소리를 듣고서, 고모님은 곧 이 방문을 그만두셨다. 나는 서너 주에 한 번씩 토요일이면 도버로 건너가 대접을 받으며 고모님을 뵈었고, 격주 수요일마다 딕 씨를 뵈었는데, 그는 정오에 역마차로 도착해 이튿날 아침까지 머물렀다.\n\n이런 때면 딕 씨는 가죽 필기 책상을 빼놓고는 결코 다니지 않았는데, 그 안에는 문구류와 진정서가 한가득 들어 있었다. 그 문서에 관해서는, 이제는 시간이 촉박해지기 시작했으니 정말이지 서둘러 마무리 지어야 한다는 생각을 품고 있었다.\n\n딕 씨는 생강빵을 무척 좋아했다. 그의 방문을 한결 더 즐겁게 해 주려고, 고모님은 나에게 어느 과자 가게에 그가 외상으로 살 수 있도록 미리 일러 두라고 시키셨는데, 다만 하루에 일 실링어치를 넘게는 사 주지 못한다는 단서가 붙어 있었다. 이 일과 함께, 그가 묵는 마을 여관의 자잘한 청구서를 죄다 갚기 전에 고모님께 확인받게 한 것을 보고, 나는 그가 그저 돈을 짤랑거리며 가지고 다니도록만 허락받았을 뿐 쓰지는 못하는 게 아닐까 하고 의심하게 되었다. 더 알아보니 과연 그러했다. 아니, 적어도 그와 고모님 사이에는 그가 쓴 모든 비용을 고모님께 정산해 보고하기로 한 합의가 있었다. 그는 고모님을 속일 마음이 조금도 없었고 늘 고모님을 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶어 했으니, 그렇게 해서 함부로 돈을 쓰지 않도록 조심하게 된 것이다. 이 점에 관해서도, 또 생각할 수 있는 다른 모든 점에 관해서도, 딕 씨는 고모님이야말로 세상에서 가장 지혜롭고 가장 놀라운 여인이라고 확신하고 있었다. 그가 나에게 끝없이 은밀하게, 그것도 늘 속삭이는 목소리로 거듭 일러 주었듯이 말이다.\n\n\"트롯우드,\" 어느 수요일, 이 비밀을 나에게 털어놓고 나서 딕 씨가 수수께끼 같은 표정으로 말했다. \"우리 집 근처에 숨어서 고모님을 겁주는 그 남자는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"고모님을 겁준다고요, 선생님?\"\n\n딕 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다. \"난 그 무엇도 고모님을 겁줄 순 없을 거라고 생각했단다.\" 그가 말했다. \"왜냐면 고모님은 — \" 여기서 그는 나직이 속삭였다. \"이건 말하지 마라 — 세상에서 가장 지혜롭고 가장 놀라운 여인이니까.\" 이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 이 묘사가 나에게 어떤 효과를 일으키는지 보려고 몸을 뒤로 물렸다.\n\n\"그자가 처음 온 게,\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"그게 — 가만있자 — 천육백사십구 년이 찰스 왕이 처형당한 해였지. 네가 천육백사십구 년이라고 했던 것 같은데?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그럴 수 있는지 모르겠구나.\" 딕 씨가 몹시 어리둥절해하며 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다. \"내가 그렇게까지 나이를 먹은 것 같진 않은데.\"\n\n\"그 남자가 나타난 게 그해였나요, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 정말이지,\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"그게 그해였을 리가 없을 것 같구나, 트롯우드. 너 그 날짜를 역사책에서 알아냈니?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"역사책은 결코 거짓말을 하지 않겠지, 그렇지?\" 딕 씨가 한 줄기 희망을 반짝이며 말했다.\n\n\"아이고, 그럼요, 선생님!\" 나는 더없이 단호하게 대답했다. 나는 순진하고 어렸으니, 정말 그렇게 믿었다.\n\n\"도무지 모르겠구나.\" 딕 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"어딘가 뭔가가 잘못됐어. 어쨌든, 찰스 왕 머릿속에 있던 골칫거리를 얼마쯤 내 머릿속에 옮겨 넣는 실수가 저질러지고 나서 얼마 지나지 않아 그 남자가 처음 왔단다. 차를 마시고 나서 막 어두워질 무렵에 트롯우드 양과 함께 산책을 나갔는데, 거기 그자가 있었어, 우리 집 바로 옆에.\"\n\n\"서성거리고 있던가요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"서성거리고 있었느냐고?\" 딕 씨가 되뇌었다. \"가만있자, 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠구나. 아 — 아니, 아니야. 서성거리고 있던 건 아니었어.\"\n\n나는 그 답에 이르는 가장 빠른 길로, 그렇다면 그자가 무엇을 하고 있었느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"음, 그자는 거기 전혀 없었어.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"고모님 뒤로 다가와 속삭이기 전까지는 말이다. 그러자 고모님이 돌아서더니 정신을 잃었고, 나는 가만히 서서 그자를 바라보았는데, 그자는 걸어가 버렸어. 그런데 그 뒤로 줄곧 (땅속이든 어디든) 숨어 있었다는 게 가장 기이한 일이야!\"\n\n\"그 뒤로 줄곧 숨어 있었다고요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그럼, 당연하지.\" 딕 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 고개를 끄덕이며 받았다. \"어젯밤까지 한 번도 안 나타났어! 어젯밤에 우리가 산책을 하고 있는데, 그자가 또 고모님 뒤로 다가왔고, 나는 그자를 또 알아봤단다.\"\n\n\"그래서 고모님을 또 겁주던가요?\"\n\n\"온통 부들부들 떠셨지.\" 딕 씨가 그 모습을 흉내 내며 이를 딱딱 부딪쳐 보이며 말했다. \"울타리를 붙들고. 우셨어. 그런데, 트롯우드, 이리 와 보렴.\" 그가 아주 나직이 속삭일 수 있도록 나를 바짝 끌어당겼다. \"왜 고모님이 그자에게 돈을 줬을까, 얘야, 그것도 달빛 아래서?\"\n\n\"거지였나 보지요, 아마.\"\n\n딕 씨는 그 짐작을 완전히 일축하듯 고개를 저었다. 그러고는 아주 자신만만하게 여러 번 \"거지 아니야, 거지 아니야, 거지 아니야, 선생!\" 하고 대답하고 나서, 그 뒤에 밤늦게 자기 방 창문으로, 고모님이 달빛 아래 정원 울타리 밖에서 그자에게 돈을 주는 것을 보았다고 말을 이었다. 그자는 그러고 나서 슬그머니 사라져 — 그가 보기엔 아마도 또 땅속으로 — 두 번 다시 보이지 않았고, 한편 고모님은 황급히, 몰래 집으로 돌아왔으며, 바로 그날 아침까지도 평소의 모습과는 사뭇 달랐다는 것이었다. 그게 딕 씨의 마음을 짓누르고 있었다.\n\n이 이야기를 처음 들었을 때 나는 그 정체 모를 자가 딕 씨의 망상에 지나지 않으며, 그에게 그토록 많은 골칫거리를 안겨 준 그 불운한 왕의 일족 가운데 하나일 뿐이라고 조금도 의심치 않았다. 그러나 좀 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 가엾은 딕 씨 본인을 고모님의 보호 아래서 떼어 내려는 시도, 혹은 시도하겠다는 위협이 두 번쯤 있었던 것은 아닐까, 그리고 그를 향한 다정한 정이 얼마나 깊은지 고모님 본인에게서 직접 들어 알고 있던 터라, 고모님이 그의 평온과 안녕을 위해 값을 치르도록 떠밀린 것은 아닐까 하는 의문을 품기 시작했다. 나는 이미 딕 씨에게 무척 정이 들었고 그의 안녕을 몹시 염려하던 터라, 내 두려움이 이 짐작에 힘을 실어 주었다. 그래서 오랫동안 수요일이 돌아올 때마다 그가 여느 때처럼 마차 마부석에 앉아 있지 않으면 어쩌나 하는 불안을 거의 매번 품지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그는 언제나 거기 나타났으니, 희끗희끗한 머리에 웃음을 띠고 행복한 얼굴로 말이다. 그리고 고모님을 겁줄 수 있는 그 남자에 대해서는 더 이상 들려줄 이야기가 없었다.\n\n이 수요일들은 딕 씨의 인생에서 가장 행복한 날들이었다. 나에게도 결코 가장 덜 행복한 날들이 아니었다. 그는 곧 학교의 모든 아이에게 알려졌다. 그리고 비록 연날리기 말고는 어떤 놀이에도 직접 끼어들지 않았으나, 우리 가운데 누구 못지않게 우리 놀이 전부에 깊이 빠져들었다. 구슬치기나 팽이 시합에 정신을 쏟은 채, 형언할 수 없는 흥미가 가득한 얼굴로 지켜보며, 결정적인 순간이면 숨조차 제대로 못 쉬던 그의 모습을 나는 얼마나 자주 보았던가! 토끼와 사냥개 놀이를 할 때면 작은 둔덕 위에 올라서서 운동장 전체를 향해 어서 뛰라고 응원하며, 찰스 순교왕의 머리며 그에 얽힌 모든 것을 까맣게 잊은 채, 희끗희끗한 머리 위로 모자를 휘젓던 그의 모습을 또 얼마나 자주 보았던가! 크리켓 경기장에서 보낸 여름날의 숱한 시간이 그에게는 그저 더없이 행복한 몇 분에 지나지 않았음을 나는 알고 있다! 눈과 동풍 속에 코끝이 파래진 채 서서, 긴 빙판 미끄럼을 타고 내려가는 아이들을 바라보며, 털장갑 낀 두 손을 황홀하게 마주치던 그의 모습을 나는 또 얼마나 많은 겨울날에 보았던가!\n\n그는 모두의 사랑을 한 몸에 받았고, 자잘한 일에서 그의 손재주는 따를 자가 없었다. 그는 우리로서는 짐작도 못 할 갖가지 모양으로 오렌지를 깎아 낼 줄 알았다. 꼬치 하나에서부터 시작해 무엇으로든 배를 만들 줄 알았다. 동물 발목뼈를 체스 말로 바꾸고, 낡은 카드짝으로 로마식 전차를 빚어내고, 실패로 바큇살 달린 바퀴를 만들고, 낡은 철사로 새장을 만들 줄 알았다. 하지만 아마도 그가 가장 뛰어난 분야는 노끈과 지푸라기였으니, 손으로 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 그가 그것으로 해낼 수 있다고 우리 모두 믿어 의심치 않았다.\n\n딕 씨의 명성은 오래지 않아 우리에게만 머무르지 않았다. 몇 번의 수요일이 지난 뒤, 스트롱 박사가 직접 나에게 그에 관해 이것저것 물어왔다. 나는 고모님이 들려준 이야기를 전부 전해 드렸고, 박사는 그 이야기에 어찌나 흥미를 느꼈던지 다음번 방문 때 그를 소개해 달라고 청했다. 나는 이 의식을 치러 주었다. 그러자 박사는 딕 씨에게, 마차 사무소에서 나를 만나지 못하거든 언제든 그리로 와서 우리 오전 수업이 끝날 때까지 쉬고 있으라고 권했고, 그리하여 딕 씨가 으레 그리로 오는 것이 곧 관습이 되었다. 그리고 수요일마다 흔히 그랬듯이 우리가 조금 늦으면, 그는 안뜰을 거닐며 나를 기다렸다. 여기서 그는 박사의 아름답고 젊은 아내와 알게 되었는데(이 무렵 그녀는 예전보다 더 창백했고, 내 보기에 나든 누구든 그녀를 보는 일이 더 드물어졌으며, 예전만큼 명랑하지도 않았으나 그렇다고 덜 아름다운 것도 아니었다), 그렇게 차츰차츰 더 가까워지더니, 마침내 학교 안까지 들어와 기다리게 되었다. 그는 늘 어느 특정한 구석의, 어느 특정한 걸상에 앉았는데, 그 걸상은 그의 이름을 따 '딕'이라고 불렸다. 여기 앉아 그는 희끗희끗한 머리를 앞으로 숙인 채, 무슨 일이 벌어지든 귀 기울여 들으며, 끝내 익히지 못한 학문에 깊은 경외를 품곤 했다.\n\n이 경외를 딕 씨는 박사에게까지 넓혔으니, 그는 박사를 어느 시대를 통틀어 가장 명민하고 가장 뛰어난 철학자라고 여겼다. 한참 동안 딕 씨는 모자를 벗지 않고서는 결코 박사에게 말을 건네지 못했다. 심지어 그와 박사가 제법 친한 사이가 되어, 우리 사이에서 '박사의 산책로'라고 불리던 안뜰 한쪽을 몇 시간이고 함께 거닐 때조차, 딕 씨는 지혜와 학식에 대한 존경을 드러내려고 이따금 모자를 벗어 들곤 했다. 이 산책 중에 박사가 그 유명한 사전의 토막들을 소리 내어 읽기 시작한 것이 어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 나는 끝내 알지 못했다. 어쩌면 박사는 처음엔 그것이 혼자 읽는 것과 다를 바 없다고 느꼈는지도 모른다. 어쨌거나 그것 역시 관습이 되었다. 그리고 딕 씨는 자랑스러움과 즐거움으로 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 귀 기울여 들으며, 마음속 깊은 곳에서 그 사전이야말로 세상에서 가장 즐거운 책이라고 믿었다.\n\n그 두 사람이 교실 창문 앞을 오르락내리락하는 모습을 떠올리면 — 박사는 흡족한 미소를 띤 채 이따금 원고를 한 번 휘저어 보이거나 자못 진중하게 고개를 끄덕이며 읽고, 딕 씨는 흥미에 사로잡혀 귀 기울이는데, 그 가엾은 정신은 어려운 낱말들의 날개를 타고 하느님만 아실 어딘가로 평온히 떠돌고 있으니 — 나는 그것이, 잔잔한 방식으로, 내가 여태껏 본 가장 기분 좋은 광경 가운데 하나라고 여긴다. 두 사람이 영원토록 그렇게 오락가락 거닐어도 좋을 것 같고, 그러면 어쩐지 세상이 조금은 더 나아질 것 같은 느낌이 든다 — 세상이 떠들썩하게 떠받드는 천 가지 일들이, 세상에든 나에게든 그 절반만큼도 좋지 않은 것 같은 느낌이.\n\n아그네스도 머지않아 딕 씨의 벗 가운데 하나가 되었다. 그리고 그는 그 집에 자주 드나들면서 유라이어와도 알게 되었다. 그와 나 사이의 우정은 끊임없이 깊어졌는데, 그 우정은 이런 묘한 토대 위에 유지되었다. 즉, 딕 씨는 내 보호자로서 나를 보살피겠다고 표방하며 찾아왔으면서도, 무슨 자잘한 의심거리가 생기면 늘 나에게 의논했고, 한결같이 내 조언을 길잡이로 삼았던 것이다. 내 타고난 슬기를 높이 산 데다, 내가 고모님에게서 상당히 많은 것을 물려받았다고 여긴 까닭이었다.\n\n어느 목요일 아침, 학교로 돌아가기 전에 딕 씨와 함께 호텔에서 마차 사무소까지 걸어가려던 참이었는데(아침을 먹기 전에 한 시간 수업이 있었다), 거리에서 유라이어를 만났다. 그는 내가 자기와 자기 어머니와 함께 차를 마시러 오겠다고 한 약속을 일깨우면서, 몸을 배배 꼬며 덧붙였다. \"하지만 코퍼필드 도련님이 그 약속을 지키실 거라곤 기대 안 했어요. 저희가 워낙 비천한 사람들이니까요.\"\n\n나는 사실 아직도 내가 유라이어를 좋아하는지 싫어하는지 마음을 정하지 못한 상태였다. 그래서 거리에서 그의 얼굴을 마주 보고 선 채로도 여전히 몹시 망설여졌다. 하지만 내가 거만한 사람으로 여겨지는 것이 무척 모욕스럽게 느껴졌고, 그래서 그저 청해 주기만을 바랐을 뿐이라고 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그게 다라면, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"그리고 정말로 저희의 비천함 때문에 못 오시는 게 아니라면, 오늘 저녁에 와 주시겠어요? 하지만 만약 저희의 비천함 때문이라면, 그걸 솔직히 인정해 주셔도 괜찮습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님. 저희는 저희 처지를 잘 알고 있으니까요.\"\n\n나는 위크필드 씨에게 말해 보겠다고, 그리고 그분이 허락하신다면 — 틀림없이 허락하시겠지만 — 기꺼이 가겠다고 했다. 그래서 그날 저녁 여섯 시, 사무소가 일찍 문 닫는 날 가운데 하루였는데, 나는 유라이어에게 준비가 됐다고 알렸다.\n\n\"어머니가 정말 자랑스러워하실 겁니다.\" 우리가 함께 걸어가는데 그가 말했다. \"아니, 자랑이 죄가 아니라면 자랑스러워하셨을 거예요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\"\n\n\"그런데 오늘 아침엔 나를 거만하다고 짐작하면서도 아무렇지 않았잖아.\" 내가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"아이고, 천만에요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어가 받았다. \"오, 믿어 주세요, 절대 아닙니다! 그런 생각은 제 머릿속에 떠오른 적도 없어요! 도련님이 저희를 너무 비천해서 상대 못 할 사람들이라고 여기셨더라도, 저는 그걸 조금도 거만하다고 생각하지 않았을 거예요. 저희가 워낙 비천한 사람들이니까요.\"\n\n\"요즘 법 공부 많이 했어?\" 화제를 돌리려고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 자신을 한껏 낮추는 투로 말했다. \"제 독서는 공부라고 부를 만한 것도 못 됩니다. 저녁에 가끔 티드 선생 책으로 한두 시간 보낼 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"꽤 어렵겠지, 아마?\" 내가 말했다. \"저한테는 가끔 어렵습니다.\" 유라이어가 받았다. \"하지만 재능 있는 분에게는 그게 어떨지 저야 알 수 없지요.\"\n\n걸어가면서 해골 같은 오른손의 두 집게손가락으로 턱에 가락을 잠깐 두드리더니, 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"보시다시피, 티드 선생 책에는 — 라틴어 낱말이며 용어들이 — 저처럼 비천한 학식을 가진 독자에게는 버거운 표현들이 있거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\"\n\n\"라틴어를 배우고 싶어?\" 내가 활기차게 말했다. \"내가 배우는 대로 기꺼이 가르쳐 줄게.\"\n\n\"오, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그렇게 제안해 주시다니 정말 친절하신 건 분명하지만, 저는 너무 비천해서 받아들일 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말도 안 되는 소리야, 유라이어!\"\n\n\"오, 정말이지 양해해 주셔야 합니다, 코퍼필드 도련님! 정말 감사하고, 무엇보다도 그러고 싶지만, 정말입니다, 저는 너무나 비천하거든요. 제 미천한 처지에 저를 짓밟을 사람들이 이미 차고 넘치는데, 제가 학식까지 갖춰서 그들의 심기를 거스를 것까지야 없지요. 학식은 제 몫이 아닙니다. 저 같은 사람은 분수에 넘치는 걸 바라지 않는 게 낫습니다. 세상에서 출세를 하려거든, 비천하게 출세해야지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n이런 속내를 토해 낼 때만큼 그의 입이 그토록 크게 벌어지고 두 뺨의 주름이 그토록 깊게 패는 것을 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 내내 고개를 절레절레 저으며, 겸손하게 몸을 배배 꼬면서 말이다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 네가 틀린 것 같아, 유라이어.\" 내가 말했다. \"분명 내가 너에게 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 게 여러 가지 있을 텐데, 네가 배우고 싶다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 의심치 않습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"조금도 의심치 않아요. 하지만 도련님은 비천하지 않으시니, 비천한 사람들 처지를 잘은 헤아리지 못하실지도 모르지요. 저는 학식으로 윗분들의 심기를 거스르지 않겠습니다, 고맙지만요. 저는 너무나 비천하거든요. 여기가 제 비천한 거처입니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n우리는 거리에서 곧장 들어가게 되어 있는, 천장 낮은 구식 방으로 들어섰고, 거기서 힙 부인을 만났는데, 그녀는 유라이어를 빼다 박은 모습이었으나 다만 키가 작았다. 그녀는 더없이 비천한 태도로 나를 맞이하고, 아들에게 입을 맞춘 것을 나에게 사과하면서, 자기들이 아무리 미천해도 타고난 정은 있는 법이니 그것이 부디 누구에게도 거슬리지 않기를 바란다고 말했다. 거기는 더없이 단정한 방으로, 절반은 응접실이고 절반은 부엌이었으나, 결코 아늑한 방은 아니었다. 차 도구가 식탁 위에 차려져 있고, 주전자는 화덕 받침 위에서 끓고 있었다. 유라이어가 저녁에 읽거나 쓸 수 있도록 윗면이 책상으로 된 서랍장이 있었고, 유라이어의 파란 가방이 종이를 토해 내듯 입을 벌린 채 널브러져 있었으며, 티드 선생이 지휘하는 유라이어의 책 한 무리가 있었고, 구석에는 찬장이, 그리고 여느 가구들이 있었다. 어느 한 물건이 따로 헐벗고 옹색하고 빈약해 보였던 기억은 없으나, 그 방 전체가 그러했던 것만은 똑똑히 기억난다.\n\n힙 부인이 아직도 상복을 입고 있던 것도 어쩌면 그녀의 비천함의 한 부분이었으리라. 힙 씨가 세상을 떠난 지 적잖은 세월이 흘렀는데도, 그녀는 여전히 상복을 입고 있었다. 모자에는 얼마쯤 타협이 있었던 듯하나, 그 밖에는 상을 막 당했던 초기 시절과 다름없이 온통 상복 차림이었다.\n\n\"오늘은 두고두고 기억할 날이에요, 우리 유라이어, 정말이지.\" 차를 우리며 힙 부인이 말했다. \"코퍼필드 도련님이 우리를 찾아 주시다니 말이야.\"\n\n\"제가 그렇게 생각하실 거라고 했잖아요, 어머니.\" 유라이어가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 이유로든 아버지가 우리 곁에 남아 계시기를 바랄 수 있었다면,\" 힙 부인이 말했다. \"그건 오늘 오후의 이 귀한 손님을 그이가 알아뵐 수 있게 하기 위해서였을 거예요.\"\n\n이런 찬사에 나는 거북함을 느꼈으나, 그러면서도 귀한 손님으로 대접받고 있다는 것이 느껴졌고, 힙 부인이 상냥한 여인이라고 생각했다.\n\n\"우리 유라이어는,\" 힙 부인이 말했다. \"이날을 오래도록 손꼽아 기다렸답니다, 도련님. 저희의 비천함이 걸림돌이 될까 봐 그 애도 걱정했고, 저도 그 걱정에 함께했지요. 저희는 비천하고, 비천해 왔으며, 앞으로도 영영 비천할 거예요.\" 힙 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"굳이 그러실 필요는 없다고 생각합니다, 부인.\" 내가 말했다. \"원하지 않으신다면요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 힙 부인이 받았다. \"저희는 저희 분수를 알고, 그 안에서 감사하며 지냅니다.\"\n\n문득 보니 힙 부인은 차츰차츰 나에게 가까이 다가오고, 유라이어는 차츰차츰 내 맞은편으로 자리를 옮겼으며, 두 사람은 공손하게 식탁 위의 가장 좋은 먹을거리를 나에게 자꾸 권했다. 사실 거기 특별히 좋은 거라곤 없었지만, 나는 그 마음만으로도 고맙게 받아들였고, 두 사람이 무척 정성스럽다고 느꼈다. 이윽고 그들은 고모 이야기를 꺼냈고, 그러자 나는 우리 고모님 이야기를 했다. 또 아버지와 어머니 이야기가 나오자, 나는 우리 부모님 이야기를 했다. 그러자 힙 부인이 의붓아버지 이야기를 꺼냈고, 나는 우리 의붓아버지 이야기를 시작했으나 — 멈추었다. 고모님이 그 일에 대해서는 입을 다물라고 충고하셨기 때문이다. 그러나 연하고 어린 코르크 마개가 한 쌍의 코르크 마개뽑이를 당해 낼 재간이 없고, 연하고 어린 이가 한 쌍의 치과 의사를 당해 낼 재간이 없으며, 작은 깃털공이 두 개의 라켓을 당해 낼 재간이 없듯이, 나도 유라이어와 힙 부인을 당해 낼 재간이 없었다. 그들은 나를 제멋대로 주물렀고, 내가 털어놓을 마음이 조금도 없던 것들을, 떠올리면 얼굴이 붉어질 만큼 능숙하게 캐냈다. 더구나 어린아이다운 솔직함에 빠진 나는, 이렇게 속을 다 터놓는 것을 스스로 제법 대견하게 여기며, 나야말로 이 공손한 두 접대꾼의 후원자라도 된 듯이 느꼈으니, 더더욱 그러했다.\n\n그들은 서로를 무척 아꼈으니, 그것만은 분명했다. 짐작건대 그것이 인지상정의 정취로서 나에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤을 것이다. 하지만 한쪽이 무슨 말을 하면 다른 쪽이 그것을 받아 이어 가는 그 솜씨는, 내가 한층 더 당해 낼 수 없는 기교의 정취였다. 나 자신에 관해 더 캐낼 것이 없어지자(머드스톤 앤드 그린비 시절과 내 여정에 대해서는 나는 입을 꾹 다물었으니), 그들은 위크필드 씨와 아그네스 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 유라이어가 힙 부인에게 공을 던지면, 힙 부인이 그것을 받아 유라이어에게 되던지고, 유라이어가 잠시 받아쳤다가 다시 힙 부인에게 보내고, 그렇게 두 사람이 공을 주거니 받거니 하는 통에, 나는 이제 누가 그 공을 쥐고 있는지조차 알 수 없어 완전히 어리둥절해졌다. 공 자체도 줄곧 바뀌었다. 어느 땐 위크필드 씨였다가, 어느 땐 아그네스였다가, 어느 땐 위크필드 씨의 훌륭함이었다가, 어느 땐 아그네스를 향한 내 흠모였다가, 어느 땐 위크필드 씨의 사업과 재산의 규모였다가, 어느 땐 저녁 식사 뒤 우리 집안의 가정생활이었다가, 어느 땐 위크필드 씨가 마시는 술, 그가 그것을 마시는 이유, 그가 그토록 많이 마시는 것이 안타깝다는 이야기였다가, 어느 땐 이것, 어느 땐 저것, 그러다 한꺼번에 모든 것이 되었다. 그리고 그 내내, 자주 말하는 것처럼도, 또 그들이 자신들의 비천함과 내가 함께해 주는 영광에 짓눌릴까 봐 이따금 조금 격려해 주는 것 말고는 무언가를 하는 것처럼도 보이지 않으면서, 나는 끊임없이 무언가, 발설해선 안 될 무언가를 흘리고 있었고, 그 효과가 유라이어의 우묵 팬 콧구멍이 씰룩이는 데서 드러나는 것을 지켜보았다.\n\n이쯤 되자 나는 조금 거북해지기 시작했고, 이 방문에서 어서 벗어나고 싶어지던 참이었는데, 거리를 내려오던 한 사람이 문 앞을 지나갔다 — 문은 방을 환기하려고 열려 있었으니, 그해의 그 시기치고는 날이 후텁지근해 방 안이 따뜻했던 것이다 — 그러더니 그 사람이 되돌아와 안을 들여다보고는, 안으로 걸어 들어오며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"코퍼필드! 이게 웬일인가?\"\n\n미코버 씨였다! 미코버 씨가, 외알 안경에, 지팡이에, 셔츠 깃에, 그 점잖은 풍채에, 거들먹거리듯 굴리는 목소리까지, 죄다 갖추고 나타난 것이다!\n\n\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"이거야말로 인간 만사의 덧없음과 불확실함을 마음에 새겨 주기에 꼭 알맞은 만남일세 — 한마디로, 더없이 기이한 만남이야. 거리를 걸으며 뭔가 풀릴 일이 생기지 않을까(이 점에 대해서는 지금 나는 제법 낙관하고 있다네) 곰곰 생각하던 차에, 젊되 귀한 벗이 떡하니 나타나는데, 내 인생에서 가장 파란만장했던 시절과 — 아니, 내 존재의 전환점과 인연이 닿은 친구가 말이야. 코퍼필드, 이 친구야, 그래 잘 지냈는가?\"\n\n미코버 씨를 거기서 보게 되어 기뻤다고는, 정말이지 말할 수 없다. 그래도 그를 본 것이 반갑기는 했으니, 나는 그와 진심으로 악수를 나누고 미코버 부인은 안녕하시냐고 물었다.\n\n\"고맙네.\" 미코버 씨가 예전처럼 손을 한 번 휘젓고 셔츠 깃에 턱을 묻으며 말했다. \"그럭저럭 회복되어 가는 중이지. 쌍둥이는 이제 자연의 샘에서 양식을 길어 먹지 않게 되었어 — 한마디로,\" 미코버 씨가 특유의 속내를 한바탕 털어놓는 투로 말했다. \"젖을 뗐다네 — 그래서 미코버 부인이 지금은 내 여행의 길벗이지. 부인도 무척 기뻐할 걸세, 코퍼필드, 모든 면에서 우정이라는 신성한 제단의 합당한 사제임을 스스로 증명해 보인 이와 다시 정을 나누게 되었으니 말이야.\"\n\n나는 부인을 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁘겠다고 했다.\n\n\"참으로 고마운 사람일세.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다.\n\n그러더니 미코버 씨는 빙긋 웃고, 다시 턱을 깃에 묻은 다음, 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"내가 내 벗 코퍼필드를 발견했는데,\" 미코버 씨가 점잖게, 누구를 콕 집어 말하는 것도 아니게 말했다. \"혼자가 아니라, 한 과부댁과, 그리고 보아하니 그 자제인 듯한 이와 더불어 정겨운 식사를 함께하고 있더란 말이지 — 한마디로,\" 미코버 씨가 또 한 번 속내를 한바탕 털어놓는 투로 말했다. \"그 아드님과 함께. 소개를 받게 된다면 영광으로 알겠네.\"\n\n이런 상황에서 나로서는 미코버 씨를 유라이어 힙과 그의 어머니에게 소개해 주는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었고, 그래서 그렇게 했다. 그들이 그의 앞에서 한껏 몸을 낮추는 동안, 미코버 씨는 자리에 앉아 더없이 정중한 태도로 손을 한 번 휘저었다.\n\n\"내 벗 코퍼필드의 벗이라면,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"누구든 나 자신에게도 친구일 권리가 있는 셈이지요.\"\n\n\"저희는 코퍼필드 도련님의 벗이 되기에는 너무나 비천한 사람들입니다, 선생님.\" 힙 부인이 말했다. \"제 아들과 저는요. 도련님이 저희와 함께 차를 들어 주시는 친절을 베푸셨으니, 함께해 주신 데 대해서도, 또 선생님이 이렇게 알은체해 주신 데 대해서도 감사할 따름이지요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 미코버 씨가 허리를 굽히며 받았다. \"참으로 친절하십니다. 그래, 자네는 요즘 뭘 하고 지내나, 코퍼필드? 아직도 포도주 장사인가?\"\n\n나는 미코버 씨를 어서 거기서 떼어 내고 싶어 안달이 나서, 모자를 손에 든 채, 분명 얼굴이 새빨개진 채로, 나는 스트롱 박사 학교의 학생이라고 대답했다.\n\n\"학생이라고?\" 미코버 씨가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. \"그것참 듣던 중 반가운 소릴세. 비록 내 벗 코퍼필드 같은 정신은\" — 유라이어와 힙 부인을 향해 — \"사람과 세상에 대한 그의 식견이 없었더라면 필요했을 그런 도야까지야 필요 없겠지만, 그래도 그것은 잠재된 초목으로 가득한 비옥한 토양이니 — 한마디로,\" 미코버 씨가 빙긋 웃으며 또 한 번 속내를 한바탕 털어놓는 투로 말했다. \"고전을 얼마든지 깊이 익힐 수 있는 지성이란 말이지.\"\n\n유라이어는 그 긴 두 손을 천천히 서로 비비 꼬며, 나에 대한 이 평가에 동의한다는 뜻으로 허리 위쪽을 섬뜩하게 배배 꼬았다.\n\n\"미코버 부인을 뵈러 가실까요, 선생님?\" 미코버 씨를 떼어 내려고 내가 말했다.\n\n\"부인에게 그런 호의를 베풀어 주겠다면야, 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 일어서며 받았다. \"여기 우리 벗들 앞에서 거리낌 없이 말하건대, 나는 여러 해 동안 금전적 곤경의 압박과 맞서 싸워 온 사람일세.\" 그가 이런 류의 말을 꼭 하리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었다. 그는 늘 자기 곤경을 두고 그렇게 으스대곤 했으니까. \"어떤 때는 내가 곤경을 딛고 우뚝 일어섰지. 또 어떤 때는 곤경이 — 한마디로, 나를 때려눕혔어. 내가 곤경에 연거푸 정통으로 한 방씩 먹인 때도 있었고, 곤경이 나에게 너무 벅차서 두 손 들고 미코버 부인에게 카토의 말을 빌려 이렇게 말한 때도 있었지. '플라톤이여, 그대의 추론은 옳도다. 이제 다 끝났구나. 더는 싸울 수 없도다.' 하지만 내 평생 어느 때에도,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 슬픔을(주로 위임장이며 두 달짜리, 넉 달짜리 약속어음에서 비롯된 곤경을 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 말일세) 내 벗 코퍼필드의 가슴에 쏟아붓던 그때만큼 더 큰 만족을 누린 적이 없었네.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 이 멋들어진 헌사를 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"힙 군! 잘 있게나. 힙 부인! 안녕히 계십시오.\" 그러고는 더없이 멋들어진 태도로 나와 함께 걸어 나가, 구두로 보도를 요란하게 울리며 콧노래까지 흥얼거렸다.\n\n미코버 씨가 묵는 곳은 자그마한 여관이었고, 그는 그 안의 작은 방을 차지하고 있었는데, 영업실에서 칸막이로 막아 놓은 그 방은 담배 연기 냄새가 짙게 배어 있었다. 바닥 틈새로 따뜻하고 기름진 냄새가 올라오는 듯한 것을 보면 부엌 위에 있는 방이지 싶었고, 벽에는 끈적한 땀이 배어나 있었다. 술 냄새와 유리잔 짤랑거리는 소리로 미루어 술집 바로 옆이라는 것도 알 수 있었다. 여기, 경주마 그림 아래 작은 소파에 비스듬히 기대어, 머리는 난로에 바짝 붙이고, 두 발로는 방 저쪽 끝의 식기 운반대에서 겨자를 밀어내고 있는 이가, 바로 미코버 부인이었다. 미코버 씨는 먼저 들어가 부인에게 말했다. \"여보, 스트롱 박사 학교의 학생을 소개하리다.\"\n\n말이 나온 김에 짚어 두자면, 미코버 씨는 내 나이와 처지에 대해 여전히 예전과 다름없이 헷갈려 했으나, 내가 스트롱 박사 학교의 학생이라는 것만은 점잖은 일이라며 늘 잊지 않고 기억했다.\n\n미코버 부인은 깜짝 놀라면서도 나를 보고 무척 반가워했다. 나도 부인을 보고 무척 반가웠고, 양쪽이 다정하게 인사를 나눈 뒤, 나는 부인 곁의 작은 소파에 앉았다.\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"우리의 지금 처지가 어떤지 코퍼필드에게 좀 들려주구려. 이 친구도 분명 알고 싶어 할 테니. 나는 그동안 신문이나 좀 들여다보면서 광고들 가운데 뭔가 풀릴 일이 있나 살펴보겠소.\"\n\n\"전 두 분이 플리머스에 계신 줄 알았어요, 부인.\" 그가 나가자 내가 미코버 부인에게 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 도련님,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"우리는 플리머스에 갔었지요.\"\n\n\"현장에 자리 잡으시려고요.\" 내가 넌지시 비쳤다.\n\n\"바로 그래요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"현장에 자리를 잡으려고요. 하지만 사실은, 세관에서는 재능을 원하지 않더군요. 미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 사람에게 그 부서에서 일자리 하나 얻어 주는 데 우리 집안의 지역 영향력이라곤 도무지 쓸모가 없었어요. 그들은 차라리 미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 사람을 두고 싶어 하지 않아요. 그이는 다른 사람들의 모자람만 드러내 보일 테니까요. 그뿐 아니라,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"도련님께는 숨기지 않겠어요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 플리머스에 자리 잡은 우리 집안의 그 갈래가, 미코버 씨가 저와 어린 윌킨스와 그 누이, 그리고 쌍둥이를 데리고 왔다는 것을 알고는, 갓 구금에서 풀려난 그이가 기대했음 직한 그런 열의로 그이를 맞아 주지 않더란 말이에요. 사실은,\" 미코버 부인이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"이건 우리끼리 얘기지만 — 우리가 받은 대접은 냉랭했어요.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"인간을 그런 면에서 바라보자니 참으로 마음 아픈 일이지요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 하지만 우리가 받은 대접은 확실히 냉랭했어요. 그건 의심할 여지가 없지요. 사실, 플리머스에 자리 잡은 우리 집안의 그 갈래는, 우리가 거기 간 지 일주일도 채 안 되어 미코버 씨에게 아주 노골적으로 인신공격을 하기 시작했어요.\"\n\n나는 그들이 부끄러운 줄 알아야 한다고 말했고, 또 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"어쨌든 그렇게 됐지요.\" 미코버 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그런 상황에서 미코버 씨 같은 기개를 지닌 사람이 무얼 할 수 있었겠어요? 한 가지 빤한 길밖에 없었지요. 우리 집안의 그 갈래에게서 런던으로 돌아갈 돈을 빌려, 어떤 희생을 치르고서라도 돌아오는 것 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그래서 다들 다시 돌아오셨군요, 부인?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 모두 다시 돌아왔지요.\" 미코버 부인이 대답했다. \"그 뒤로 나는 미코버 씨가 취하기에 가장 마땅한 길에 대해 우리 집안의 다른 갈래들에게 의논했어요 — 나는 그이가 어떤 길이든 택해야 한다고 주장하니까요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 미코버 부인이 따지듯이 말했다. \"하인을 빼고도 여섯이나 되는 식구가 허공만 먹고 살 수는 없는 노릇이 분명하잖아요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요, 부인.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 집안의 그 다른 갈래들의 의견은,\" 미코버 부인이 말을 이었다. \"미코버 씨가 당장 석탄에 관심을 돌려야 한다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"무엇에요, 부인?\"\n\n\"석탄이요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"석탄 장사요. 미코버 씨는 알아본 끝에, 메드웨이 석탄 사업에 그이의 재능을 지닌 사람에게 길이 열려 있을지도 모른다는 생각을 품게 됐지요. 그래서, 미코버 씨가 아주 지당하게 말했듯이, 가장 먼저 밟아야 할 단계는 분명 가서 메드웨이를 직접 보는 것이었어요. 그래서 우리는 가서 봤지요. 내가 '우리'라고 하는 건요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 나는 결코,\" 미코버 부인이 북받쳐 말했다. \"결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 것이기 때문이에요.\"\n\n나는 감탄과 찬동을 나직이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"우리는 가서,\" 미코버 부인이 되풀이했다. \"메드웨이를 봤어요. 그 강의 석탄 사업에 대한 내 의견은, 그게 재능을 필요로 할지는 몰라도 자본은 분명히 필요로 한다는 거예요. 재능이라면 미코버 씨가 가졌지요. 자본은, 미코버 씨에게 없고요. 우리는 메드웨이의 대부분을 본 것 같아요. 그게 내 개인적인 결론이지요. 여기서 이렇게 가까우니, 미코버 씨는 여기까지 와서 대성당을 보지 않는 건 경솔한 일일 거라는 의견이었어요. 첫째로는, 그게 그토록 볼 만한 가치가 있는데 우리가 한 번도 본 적이 없었기 때문이고, 둘째로는, 대성당이 있는 도시에서는 뭔가 풀릴 일이 생길 가능성이 크기 때문이지요. 우리가 여기 온 지,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"사흘이 됐어요. 아직까지 풀린 일은 아무것도 없고요. 그리고, 코퍼필드 도련님, 우리가 지금 이 호텔에서 진 빚을 갚으려고 런던에서 올 송금을 기다리고 있다는 것은, 낯선 사람만큼 도련님을 그리 놀라게 하지는 않을 거예요. 그 송금이 도착하기 전까지는,\" 미코버 부인이 자못 감정에 북받쳐 말했다. \"나는 내 집(펜턴빌의 하숙을 두고 하는 말이에요)과, 내 아들딸과, 내 쌍둥이로부터 떨어져 있는 셈이지요.\"\n\n이 애타는 궁지에 빠진 미코버 부부에게 나는 더없는 동정을 느꼈고, 마침 돌아온 미코버 씨에게 그 마음을 그대로 전하며, 그저 내게 돈이 넉넉히 있어서 두 분에게 필요한 액수를 빌려드릴 수 있다면 좋으련만 하고 덧붙였다. 미코버 씨의 대답은 그의 어지러운 심정을 드러내는 것이었다. 그는 나와 악수하며 말했다. \"코퍼필드, 자네는 진정한 친구일세. 하지만 최악의 사태가 닥쳐도, 면도 도구를 가진 사람에게는 친구가 없는 법이 없지.\" 이 끔찍한 암시에 미코버 부인은 두 팔로 미코버 씨의 목을 끌어안고 부디 진정하라고 애원했다. 그는 눈물을 흘렸으나, 거의 곧바로 정신을 추슬러, 종을 울려 종업원을 부르고는 이튿날 아침 식사로 뜨거운 콩팥 푸딩 하나와 새우 한 접시를 주문했다.\n\n내가 작별을 고하자, 두 사람은 떠나기 전에 꼭 와서 함께 식사하자고 하도 졸라 대는 통에, 나는 거절할 수가 없었다. 하지만 이튿날은 저녁에 준비할 일이 많아 갈 수 없을 것을 알았으므로, 미코버 씨는 오전 중에 스트롱 박사 학교로 나를 찾아와(그 우편으로 송금이 도착하리라는 예감이 든다면서), 내게 형편이 더 나으면 모레로 정하자고 제안하기로 했다. 그리하여 이튿날 오전 나는 수업 중에 불려 나갔고, 응접실에서 미코버 씨를 만났는데, 그는 식사를 제안한 대로 그날 하기로 하자고 말하러 온 것이었다. 송금이 왔느냐고 물으니, 그는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는 떠나갔다.\n\n바로 그날 저녁, 창밖을 내다보고 있던 나는, 미코버 씨와 유라이어 힙이 팔짱을 끼고 지나가는 것을 보고 깜짝 놀랐으며 적잖이 마음이 불편해졌다. 유라이어는 자기에게 베풀어진 영광을 비천하게 의식하고 있었고, 미코버 씨는 유라이어에게 후원의 손길을 뻗는 데 흐뭇한 기쁨을 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 이튿날 약속한 식사 시간인 네 시에 그 작은 호텔로 갔을 때, 미코버 씨의 말로 미루어 그가 유라이어와 함께 그 집으로 가서 힙 부인 댁에서 브랜디에 물을 타 마셨다는 것을 알고, 나는 더더욱 놀랐다.\n\n\"그래서 말인데 말이야, 이보게 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"자네 벗 힙은 장차 검찰총장이 될 만한 젊은이일세. 내가 내 곤경이 막바지에 이르렀던 그 시절에 저 젊은이를 알았더라면, 한마디로 말해, 내 채권자들이 실제보다 훨씬 더 잘 다뤄졌으리라고 나는 믿네.\"\n\n미코버 씨가 사실상 채권자들에게 한 푼도 갚지 않은 마당에, 어떻게 그럴 수 있었다는 건지 나는 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 하지만 굳이 묻고 싶지는 않았다. 또한 그가 유라이어에게 너무 속을 다 털어놓지 않았기를 바란다고 말하고 싶지도, 그들이 내 이야기를 많이 했는지 물어보고 싶지도 않았다. 미코버 씨의 마음을, 아니 적어도 미코버 부인의 마음을 상하게 할까 봐 두려웠으니, 부인이 무척 예민한 사람이었기 때문이다. 하지만 그 일이 마음에 걸렸고, 그 뒤로도 자주 그 생각이 났다.\n\n우리는 멋진 자그마한 만찬을 들었다. 우아한 생선 요리 한 접시에, 송아지 허릿살의 콩팥 쪽을 구운 것, 소시지 고기 튀김, 자고새 한 마리, 그리고 푸딩이 있었다. 포도주도 있고 독한 에일도 있었으며, 식사 뒤에는 미코버 부인이 손수 뜨거운 펀치를 한 사발 만들어 주었다.\n\n미코버 씨는 유난히 흥겨웠다. 그토록 좋은 말벗인 그를 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 그는 펀치로 얼굴을 번들번들 빛냈는데, 마치 온통 니스 칠이라도 한 것처럼 보였다. 그는 이 도시를 두고 즐겁게 감상에 젖어 도시의 번영을 위해 건배를 제안하면서, 미코버 부인과 자기가 여기서 더없이 아늑하고 편안하게 지냈으며 캔터베리에서 보낸 즐거운 시간을 결코 잊지 못할 거라고 했다. 이어서 그는 나를 위해 건배했고, 그와 미코버 부인과 나는 지난 우리 인연을 돌이켜 보았는데, 그러는 가운데 우리는 그 가재도구들을 또 한 번 죄다 팔아 치웠다. 그다음에는 내가 미코버 부인을 위해 건배를 제안했다. 아니, 적어도 나는 점잖게 이렇게 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면, 미코버 부인, 이제 부인의 건강을 위해 잔을 들 영광을 누리고 싶습니다.\" 그러자 미코버 씨가 미코버 부인의 인품에 대한 찬사를 늘어놓으며, 부인이야말로 한결같이 자기의 길잡이요 철학자요 벗이었다고 했고, 내가 장가들 나이가 되거든 이런 또 한 사람의 여인을, 그런 여인을 찾을 수만 있다면, 꼭 아내로 맞으라고 권한다고 했다.\n\n펀치가 줄어들수록 미코버 씨는 한층 더 다정하고 흥겨워졌다. 미코버 부인의 기분도 한껏 들떠, 우리는 '올드 랭 사인'을 불렀다. '여기 내 손이 있네, 나의 미더운 벗이여'라는 대목에 이르렀을 때 우리는 모두 식탁을 빙 둘러 손을 맞잡았고, '우리 한 잔 흠뻑 들이켜세'라고 외칠 때는, 그게 무슨 뜻인지는 도무지 알지 못하면서도, 정말로 가슴이 뭉클해졌다.\n\n한마디로, 그날 저녁의 마지막 순간, 내가 그와 그의 사랑스러운 아내에게 진심 어린 작별을 고할 때까지, 미코버 씨만큼 더없이 유쾌한 사람을 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 따라서 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 다음과 같은 편지를 받게 되리라고는 미처 마음의 준비를 하지 못했으니, 그 편지에 적힌 날짜는 전날 저녁 아홉 시 반, 그러니까 내가 그와 헤어진 지 십오 분 뒤였다.\n\"나의 친애하는 젊은 벗,\n\n주사위는 던져졌네 — 모든 게 끝났어. 병색 도는 명랑함의 가면으로 근심의 상흔을 감춘 채, 나는 오늘 저녁 자네에게 송금이 가망 없다는 사실을 알리지 못했네! 이런 상황에서 — 견디기에도 굴욕스럽고, 헤아리기에도 굴욕스럽고, 입에 담기에도 한결같이 굴욕스러운 — 나는 이 업소에서 진 금전적 빚을, 발행일로부터 십사 일 뒤에 내 거처인 런던 펜턴빌에서 지급하기로 한 약속어음 한 장을 써 주어 청산했네. 그 어음이 만기가 되어도, 결제되지 못할 걸세. 그 결과는 파멸이지. 벼락은 머리 위에 드리워져 있고, 나무는 쓰러질 수밖에 없네.\n\n지금 자네에게 이 글을 쓰는 이 가련한 사내가, 부디 자네에게 평생의 등불이 되기를 바라네, 친애하는 코퍼필드. 그런 뜻으로, 그런 희망으로 이 글을 쓰는 걸세. 만약 그가 자신을 그토록 쓸모 있는 존재로 여길 수 있다면, 어쩌면 그의 남은 삶의 음울한 토굴 속으로 한 줄기 햇살이라도 스며들지 모르지 — 하기야 그의 수명이라는 것도 지금으로선(아무리 좋게 말해도) 극히 불확실하다네만.\n\n이것이 자네가 받게 될 마지막 편지일세, 친애하는 코퍼필드,\n거지가 되어 버림받은 자,\n\n윌킨스 미코버\n\n로부터.\"\n\n이 가슴 찢어지는 편지의 내용에 어찌나 충격을 받았던지, 나는 스트롱 박사 학교로 가는 길에 그 작은 호텔에 들러 위로의 말 한마디로 미코버 씨를 달래 보려고 곧장 그리로 달려갔다. 그러나 절반쯤 갔을 때 런던행 마차와 마주쳤는데, 그 뒷자리에 미코버 부부가 타고 있었다. 미코버 씨는 그야말로 평온한 만족의 화신처럼, 미코버 부인의 이야기에 미소 지으며, 종이봉투에서 호두를 꺼내 먹고 있었고, 가슴 주머니에서는 술병 하나가 삐죽 튀어나와 있었다. 그들이 나를 보지 못했으므로, 나는 이모저모 따져 보아 그들을 못 본 척하는 게 상책이라고 생각했다. 그래서, 마음의 큰 짐을 던 채, 나는 학교로 가는 가장 가까운 길인 샛길로 접어들었고, 대체로 그들이 떠나 버려 홀가분하다고 느꼈다. 그러면서도 여전히 그들이 무척 좋았지만 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. A RETROSPECT\n\n내 학창 시절! 어린 시절부터 청년기에 이르기까지, 소리 없이 미끄러져 흘러간 내 존재 — 보이지도 않고 느껴지지도 않게 나아간 내 삶이여! 이제는 메말라 잎으로 뒤덮인 물길이 되어 버린 그 흐르던 강물을 되돌아보며, 그 흐름이 어떠했는지를 기억해 낼 만한 표지가 그 물줄기를 따라 어딘가 남아 있는지 한번 헤아려 보자.\n\n한순간, 나는 성당 안 내 자리에 앉아 있다. 우리는 일요일 아침마다 다 같이 그곳에 갔는데, 그러려고 먼저 학교에 모이곤 했다. 흙냄새, 햇빛 한 줄기 들지 않는 공기, 세상이 바깥에 차단되어 있다는 느낌, 검고 흰 아치형 회랑과 통로를 따라 울려 퍼지는 오르간 소리 — 이것들은 나를 그 시절로 데려가는 날개가 되어, 반쯤 잠들고 반쯤 깨어 있는 꿈속에서 그 나날 위를 맴돌게 붙잡아 둔다.\n\n나는 학교에서 꼴찌가 아니다. 몇 달 사이에 여러 아이를 제치고 위로 올라섰다. 그러나 일등 하는 아이는 내게 까마득히 먼 곳에 사는 막강한 존재로 보였고, 그 아찔한 높이는 도저히 닿을 수 없는 것 같았다. 애그니스는 \"아니야\"라고 했지만 나는 \"맞아\"라고 우기며, 그 놀라운 존재가 얼마나 많은 지식을 통달했는지 너는 짐작도 못 한다고 말했다. 그런데도 애그니스는 나, 그래, 이 보잘것없는 지망생인 나조차도 언젠가는 그 자리에 이를 수 있으리라 여겼다. 그는 스티어포스가 그랬던 것처럼 나만의 친구이자 공공연한 후원자는 아니었으나, 나는 그를 경외에 가까운 마음으로 우러러보았다. 무엇보다 나는 그가 스트롱 박사 학교를 떠나면 어떤 사람이 될지, 그리고 그에게 맞서 어떤 자리를 지키려면 인류가 무슨 수를 써야 할지가 궁금했다.\n\n그런데 이렇게 불쑥 내 마음에 끼어드는 이 사람은 누구인가? 바로 셰퍼드 양인데, 나는 그녀를 사랑한다.\n\n셰퍼드 양은 네팅걸 자매가 운영하는 기숙학교의 기숙생이다. 나는 셰퍼드 양을 흠모한다. 그녀는 짧은 웃옷을 걸친 자그마한 소녀로, 둥근 얼굴에 곱슬곱슬한 아마빛 머리카락을 지녔다. 네팅걸 자매네 아가씨들도 성당에 온다. 나는 책에 눈을 둘 수가 없으니, 셰퍼드 양만 바라봐야 하기 때문이다. 성가대가 성가를 부를 때면 나는 셰퍼드 양의 목소리만 듣는다. 예배 중에 나는 마음속으로 셰퍼드 양의 이름을 끼워 넣어 — 그녀를 왕족들 틈에 넣어 둔다. 집에서, 내 방에 혼자 있을 때면, 사랑에 겨운 황홀경에 빠져 이따금 \"아, 셰퍼드 양!\" 하고 소리치고 싶어진다.\n\n한동안 나는 셰퍼드 양의 마음을 확신하지 못하다가, 마침내 운명이 우리 편이 되어 무용 학원에서 만난다. 셰퍼드 양이 내 짝이 된다. 셰퍼드 양의 장갑에 손이 닿는 순간, 짜릿한 떨림이 내 윗옷 오른팔을 타고 올라와 머리카락 끝으로 빠져나가는 게 느껴진다. 나는 셰퍼드 양에게 아무 말도 하지 않지만, 우리는 서로를 알아챈다. 셰퍼드 양과 나는 오직 맺어지기 위해서만 산다.\n\n나는 왜 셰퍼드 양에게 브라질너트 열두 알을 몰래 선물로 주는 걸까, 정말 모르겠다. 그것은 애정을 드러내는 물건도 아니고, 반듯한 모양으로 꾸러미에 싸기도 어렵고, 방문틈에 끼워서도 깨기 힘들며, 깨고 나면 기름이 잔뜩 묻어난다. 그런데도 나는 그것이 셰퍼드 양에게 딱 어울린다고 느낀다. 부드럽고 씨가 박힌 비스킷도 셰퍼드 양에게 갖다 바치고, 오렌지는 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 안긴다. 한번은 외투 보관실에서 셰퍼드 양에게 입을 맞춘다. 황홀하다! 그런데 이튿날, 셰퍼드 양이 안짱걸음을 걷는다고 네팅걸 자매가 그녀를 발에 차꼬를 채워 세워 두었다는 소문이 퍼져 나가니, 그날 내가 느낀 고통과 분노는 또 어떠한가!\n\n셰퍼드 양은 내 삶을 온통 휘감은 단 하나의 주제이자 환영인데, 도대체 어쩌다 나는 그녀와 갈라서게 되는 걸까? 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없다. 그런데도 셰퍼드 양과 나 사이에는 점점 냉랭한 기운이 자라난다. 셰퍼드 양이 내가 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보지 않았으면 좋겠다고 말했다는 둥, 존스 군을 더 좋아한다고 털어놓았다는 둥 하는 소문이 내 귀에 들려온다 — 그것도 존스를! 아무런 장점도 없는 녀석을! 나와 셰퍼드 양 사이의 골은 점점 깊어진다. 마침내 어느 날, 나는 산책을 나온 네팅걸 자매네 학생들과 마주친다. 셰퍼드 양은 지나가면서 얼굴을 찡그리더니 동무에게 까르르 웃는다. 모든 것이 끝났다. 한평생의 헌신이 — 한평생처럼 느껴지니 어차피 마찬가지다 — 막을 내렸다. 셰퍼드 양은 아침 예배에서 걸어 나오고, 왕족들은 이제 그녀를 알지 못한다.\n\n나는 학교에서 더 윗자리로 올라가고, 아무도 내 평온을 깨뜨리지 못한다. 이제 나는 네팅걸 자매네 아가씨들에게 조금도 예의를 차리지 않으며, 그들이 지금의 곱절로 많고 스무 배로 아름답다 한들 누구 하나에게도 빠지지 않을 것이다. 나는 무용 학원이 따분한 일이라 여기고, 여자애들이 자기들끼리 춤추고 우리를 가만히 내버려 두면 안 되나 싶어 의아해한다. 나는 라틴어 운문에 점점 두각을 나타내고, 부츠 끈 묶는 일 따위는 등한시한다. 스트롱 박사는 여러 사람 앞에서 나를 두고 장래가 촉망되는 젊은 학자라고 일컫는다. 딕 씨는 기뻐서 어쩔 줄 모르고, 고모님은 바로 다음 우편으로 나에게 일 기니를 부쳐 준다.\n\n한 젊은 푸주한의 그림자가, 「맥베스」에 나오는 투구 쓴 머리의 환영처럼 떠오른다. 이 젊은 푸주한은 누구인가? 그는 캔터베리 청소년들의 공포다. 그가 머리에 바르는 쇠기름이 그에게 초자연적인 힘을 주어 어른과도 맞먹는다는 막연한 믿음이 널리 퍼져 있다. 그는 얼굴이 넓적하고 황소처럼 목이 굵은 젊은 푸주한으로, 두 뺨은 거칠게 붉고, 심보는 고약하며, 혀는 사람을 해코지한다. 이 혀의 주된 쓰임새는 스트롱 박사의 젊은 신사들을 깎아내리는 것이다. 그는 공공연히 떠벌리기를, 그자들이 뭔가 원한다면 자기가 톡톡히 안겨 주겠다고 한다. 그러면서 그자들 가운데 몇몇(나도 포함해서)을 콕 집어, 한 손은 등 뒤로 묶은 채 다른 한 손만으로도 처리해 버릴 수 있다고 떠든다. 그는 어린 학생들을 길목에서 기다렸다가 무방비한 머리통을 쥐어박고, 훤한 거리에서 내 뒤꽁무니에 대고 한판 붙자고 소리친다. 이런 충분한 이유들 때문에 나는 그 푸주한과 한판 붙기로 결심한다.\n\n어느 여름 저녁, 담장 모퉁이의 풀 우거진 움푹한 곳에서다. 나는 약속대로 그 푸주한을 만난다. 내 곁에는 우리 학교에서 가려 뽑은 아이들 한 무리가 따라붙고, 푸주한 곁에는 다른 푸주한 둘, 젊은 술집 주인 하나, 그리고 굴뚝 청소부 하나가 따른다. 준비가 갖추어지고, 푸주한과 나는 얼굴을 맞대고 선다. 한순간에 푸주한이 내 왼쪽 눈썹에서 촛불 만 개를 피워 낸다. 또 한순간, 나는 담장이 어디 있는지, 내가 어디 있는지, 누가 어디 있는지조차 알 수 없다. 우리는 짓밟힌 풀밭 위를 뒹굴며 줄곧 한데 뒤엉켜 맞붙어 싸우는 통에, 나는 어느 쪽이 나고 어느 쪽이 푸주한인지도 거의 분간하지 못한다. 어떤 때는 피투성이지만 의기양양한 푸주한이 보이고, 어떤 때는 아무것도 보이지 않아 내 후원자의 무릎에 앉아 숨을 헐떡이며, 어떤 때는 미친 듯이 푸주한에게 달려들었다가 그의 얼굴에 주먹 마디가 까져 터지지만 그는 눈 하나 까딱하지 않는 듯하다. 마침내 나는, 아찔한 잠에서 깨어나듯 머리가 몹시 멍한 채로 정신을 차리고, 푸주한이 다른 두 푸주한과 굴뚝 청소부, 술집 주인의 축하를 받으며 가는 길에 외투를 걸치고 걸어가 버리는 모습을 본다. 그 모습에서 나는, 옳게도, 승리는 그의 것이라고 헤아린다.\n\n나는 처참한 몰골로 집에 실려 가, 두 눈에 비프스테이크를 갖다 대고, 식초와 브랜디로 문질러진 채, 윗입술에 크게 부풀어 오른 곳이 터져 나와 보기 흉할 만큼 부어오른 것을 발견한다. 사나흘 동안 나는 눈에 초록 가리개를 쓰고 몹시 흉한 꼴로 집에 머무른다. 무척이나 따분했을 테지만, 애그니스가 나에게 누이처럼 굴어 위로해 주고, 책을 읽어 주며, 시간을 가볍고 즐겁게 만들어 주니 그렇지 않다. 애그니스는 늘, 언제나, 내 속내를 온전히 받아 주는 사람이다. 나는 그녀에게 푸주한 이야기며 그가 나에게 안긴 온갖 부당한 일을 죄다 털어놓는다. 그녀는 내가 그 푸주한과 싸우는 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었으리라 여기면서도, 내가 그와 싸웠다는 사실에 몸을 움츠리며 떤다.\n\n세월은 어느새 슬그머니 흘러갔다. 이제 와서는 애덤스가 더는 우두머리 학생이 아니고, 그런 지도 벌써 오래되었으니 말이다. 애덤스가 학교를 떠난 지 너무 오래라, 그가 스트롱 박사를 찾아 학교에 들렀을 때 나 말고는 그를 알아보는 이가 거의 없다. 애덤스는 머지않아 변호사 자격을 얻어 법정 변호사가 되고 가발을 쓰게 될 참이다. 나는 그가 내 짐작보다 더 온순한 사람이고 풍채도 덜 위풍당당해진 것에 놀란다. 게다가 그는 아직 세상을 뒤흔들지도 못했다. 세상은 (내가 헤아릴 수 있는 한) 그가 거기에 끼어든 적이 없는 것이나 거의 다름없이 굴러가고 있으니까.\n\n시와 역사 속의 용사들이 끝이 없을 듯한 위풍당당한 대열을 이루어 행군하는 텅 빈 공백 — 그리고 다음에 무엇이 오는가! 이제 나는 우두머리 학생이다! 나는 내 아래로 늘어선 아이들의 줄을 내려다보면서, 그 가운데 내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 적의 나 자신을 떠올리게 하는 아이들에게는 너그러이 관심을 보낸다. 그 자그마한 녀석은 이제 나의 한 부분 같지가 않다. 나는 그를 인생의 길 위에 남겨 두고 온 무언가로 — 실제로 내가 그였다기보다는 내가 지나쳐 온 무언가로 기억하며, 거의 다른 누군가를 떠올리듯 그를 떠올린다.\n\n그리고 위크필드 씨 댁에서 첫날 보았던 그 자그마한 소녀는 어디로 갔을까? 그 아이도 사라졌다. 그 자리에는 그림과 똑 닮은, 더는 아이의 모습이 아닌 모습이 집 안을 거닐고 있다. 그리고 애그니스는 — 마음속으로 내가 사랑스러운 누이라 부르는, 나의 조언자이자 벗이며, 차분하고 선하며 자기를 잊는 그 영향력 안에 들어오는 모든 이의 삶에 더 나은 천사가 되어 주는 그녀는 — 어엿한 여인이 되었다.\n\n내 키와 외모가 자라고, 그동안 내가 쌓아 온 지식이 늘어난 것 말고, 또 어떤 변화가 나에게 찾아왔을까? 나는 금시계와 시곗줄을 차고, 새끼손가락에 반지를 끼며, 긴 자락의 외투를 입는다. 그리고 곰 기름을 잔뜩 바르는데 — 이건 반지와 함께 놓고 보면 영 볼썽사납다. 내가 또 사랑에 빠진 건가? 그렇다. 나는 라킨스 맏딸을 떠받든다.\n\n라킨스 맏딸은 자그마한 소녀가 아니다. 키가 크고 가무잡잡하며 검은 눈을 지닌, 훤칠한 몸매의 여인이다. 라킨스 맏딸은 새파란 풋내기가 아니다. 라킨스 막내딸도 풋내기가 아닌데, 맏딸은 그보다 서너 살은 위가 분명하니까. 어쩌면 라킨스 맏딸은 서른 살쯤 됐을지도 모른다. 그녀를 향한 내 열정은 그 어떤 경계도 넘어선다.\n\n라킨스 맏딸은 장교들과 알고 지낸다. 견디기 끔찍한 일이다. 나는 그들이 거리에서 그녀에게 말을 거는 모습을 본다. 그녀의 보닛이 (그녀는 보닛 고르는 안목이 산뜻하다) 언니의 보닛을 데리고 인도를 따라 내려오는 게 보이면, 그들이 그녀를 맞으려고 길을 건너오는 모습을 본다. 그녀는 웃고 떠들며, 그것을 즐기는 듯하다. 나는 내 한가한 시간을 적잖이 그녀를 만나려고 이리저리 걸어 다니는 데 쓴다. 하루에 한 번이라도 그녀에게 인사를 건넬 수 있으면 (나는 라킨스 씨와 아는 사이라, 그녀와도 인사를 나눌 정도의 사이다) 나는 더 행복하다. 나는 가끔은 인사를 받을 자격이 있다. 라킨스 맏딸이 군인들과 춤출 게 뻔한 경마 무도회가 열리는 그 밤에 내가 겪는 미칠 듯한 고통은, 세상에 공평한 정의라는 게 있다면 마땅히 무언가로 보상받아야 한다.\n\n내 열정은 식욕을 앗아 가고, 가장 새것인 비단 목도리를 줄곧 두르게 만든다. 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고 부츠를 닦고 또 닦는 것 말고는 위안이 없다. 그러면 나는 라킨스 맏딸에게 좀 더 어울리는 사람이 된 듯하다. 그녀에게 속한 것, 그녀와 이어진 것이라면 무엇이든 나에게는 귀하다. 라킨스 씨는 (이중턱에 한쪽 눈은 머릿속에 박힌 듯 꼼짝하지 않는, 무뚝뚝한 노신사다) 나에게 흥미로운 사람이다. 그의 딸을 만날 수 없을 때면, 나는 그를 만날 만한 곳으로 간다. \"안녕하십니까, 라킨스 씨? 아가씨들과 온 가족이 다들 평안하신지요?\"라고 말하는 게 어찌나 속이 빤히 들여다보이는지, 나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른다.\n\n나는 끊임없이 내 나이를 곱씹는다. 내가 열일곱 살이라 치고, 열일곱이 라킨스 맏딸에게 어리다 친들, 그게 뭐 어떻단 말인가? 게다가 나는 곧 스물한 살이 될 텐데. 나는 저녁마다 으레 라킨스 씨네 집 밖을 거니는데, 장교들이 안으로 들어가는 걸 보거나, 라킨스 맏딸이 하프를 켜는 응접실에서 그들의 소리가 들려오면 가슴이 미어진다. 심지어 나는 두세 번쯤은, 식구들이 다 잠자리에 든 뒤에 병이라도 든 듯 얼빠진 꼴로 집 둘레를 빙빙 돌면서, 어느 방이 라킨스 맏딸의 침실일까 헤아려 본다(지금 와 생각하면 아마 라킨스 씨의 방을 골랐을 게 뻔하지만). 그러면서 불이 확 났으면, 모여든 사람들이 겁에 질려 우두커니 섰으면, 그 틈으로 내가 사다리를 들고 뛰어들어 그녀의 창문에 그것을 걸치고, 내 품에 그녀를 안아 구해 내고, 그녀가 두고 온 무언가를 가지러 다시 들어갔다가 불길 속에 스러질 수 있으면 하고 빈다. 나는 사랑에 있어 대체로 사심이 없는 사람이라, 라킨스 양 앞에서 멋진 모습 한번 보이고 죽을 수 있다면 그것으로 흡족하리라 여기기 때문이다.\n\n대체로 그렇지만, 늘 그런 건 아니다. 가끔은 더 환한 환상이 내 앞에 떠오른다. 라킨스 씨네서 열리는 성대한 무도회를 위해 (삼 주 동안 고대해 온 일이다) 옷을 차려입을 때면 (두 시간이나 걸리는 일이다), 나는 흐뭇한 그림들에 상상을 마음껏 풀어 놓는다. 나는 용기를 내어 라킨스 양에게 사랑을 고백하는 내 모습을 그려 본다. 라킨스 양이 내 어깨에 고개를 묻으며 \"어머, 코퍼필드 씨, 제 귀를 믿어도 되나요!\"라고 말하는 모습을 그려 본다. 라킨스 씨가 이튿날 아침 나를 찾아와 \"코퍼필드 군, 딸아이가 다 이야기했네. 나이는 문제 될 게 없어. 여기 이만 파운드가 있네. 부디 행복하게!\"라고 말하는 모습을 그려 본다. 고모님이 마음을 누그러뜨리고 우리를 축복해 주는 모습, 그리고 딕 씨와 스트롱 박사가 결혼식에 와 주는 모습도 그려 본다. 나는 분별 있는 녀석이라고 믿는다 — 돌이켜 보건대 그랬다고 믿는다는 뜻이다 — 게다가 겸손하기도 한 게 분명하다. 그런데도 이런 일은 어김없이 펼쳐진다. 나는 그 마법에 걸린 집으로 가는데, 그곳에는 불빛과 재잘거림, 음악, 꽃, 장교들 (보기 딱하게도), 그리고 눈부신 아름다움의 화신인 라킨스 맏딸이 있다. 그녀는 파란 옷을 입고 머리에는 파란 꽃을 — 물망초를 — 꽂았는데, 마치 그녀가 물망초를 꽂아야 할 까닭이라도 있는 것처럼. 이번이 내가 초대받은 첫 어른들의 진짜 파티라, 나는 조금 거북하다. 누구에게도 속하지 않은 듯하고, 라킨스 씨 말고는 아무도 나에게 할 말이 없는 듯하니 말이다. 그런데 라킨스 씨는 내 학교 친구들이 어떻게 지내느냐고 묻는데, 나는 모욕당하러 거기 온 게 아니니 그런 건 묻지 않아도 될 일이다.\n\n그러나 한동안 문간에 서서 내 마음의 여신을 실컷 눈에 담고 있노라니, 그녀가 — 그녀가, 라킨스 맏딸이! — 나에게 다가와, 춤을 추느냐고 상냥하게 묻는다.\n\n나는 고개를 숙이며 더듬더듬 말한다. \"당신과라면요, 라킨스 양.\"\n\n\"다른 사람과는 안 추고요?\" 라킨스 양이 묻는다.\n\n\"다른 누구와 춤춰도 즐겁지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n라킨스 양은 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고 (아니, 붉힌 것 같다고 생각하고), 이렇게 말한다. \"다다음 곡에 추면 정말 좋겠네요.\"\n\n그 차례가 온다. \"이건 왈츠인 것 같은데요.\" 내가 다가서자 라킨스 양이 미심쩍은 듯 말한다. \"왈츠 추실 줄 알아요? 못 추시면 베일리 대위가—\"\n\n그러나 나는 왈츠를 출 줄 안다 (게다가 마침 꽤 잘 추기까지 한다). 그래서 나는 라킨스 양을 데리고 나간다. 나는 그녀를 베일리 대위 곁에서 단호하게 데려온다. 그는 비참할 게 틀림없다. 하지만 그가 나와 무슨 상관이랴. 나도 비참했었으니까. 내가 라킨스 맏딸과 왈츠를 추다니! 나는 어디서, 누구들 틈에서, 얼마나 오래 췄는지도 모르겠다. 그저 내가 파란 천사와 함께 더없이 행복한 황홀경에 빠져 허공을 떠다니다가, 어느 작은 방에서 그녀와 단둘이 소파에 기대어 쉬고 있는 나를 발견했다는 것만 안다. 그녀가 내 단춧구멍에 꽂힌 꽃(분홍 동백, 값은 반 크라운)을 보고 감탄한다. 나는 그것을 그녀에게 건네며 말한다.\n\n\"그 값으로 헤아릴 수 없는 것을 청합니다, 라킨스 양.\"\n\n\"어머나! 그게 뭔데요?\" 라킨스 양이 되묻는다.\n\n\"당신의 꽃 한 송이입니다. 구두쇠가 황금을 끌어안듯 그것을 소중히 간직하게요.\"\n\n\"대담한 소년이군요.\" 라킨스 양이 말한다. \"자, 여기요.\"\n\n그녀는 싫지 않은 기색으로 나에게 꽃을 건넨다. 나는 그것을 입술에 갖다 댄 다음 가슴팍에 품는다. 라킨스 양은 웃으며 내 팔에 자기 손을 끼우고는 말한다. \"이제 베일리 대위에게 데려다주세요.\"\n\n이 달콤한 만남과 그 왈츠를 떠올리느라 정신을 놓고 있는데, 그녀가 밤새 휘스트만 친 수수한 노신사 하나를 팔짱 끼고 나에게 다시 다가와 말한다.\n\n\"어머! 여기 제 대담한 친구가 있네요! 체슬 씨가 당신과 알고 지내고 싶어 하세요, 코퍼필드 씨.\"\n\n나는 그가 이 집안의 친구라는 것을 단박에 알아채고, 무척 흐뭇해한다.\n\n\"자네 안목에 감탄했네, 젊은이.\" 체슬 씨가 말한다. \"보는 눈이 있어. 자네야 홉 따위엔 별로 관심이 없겠지만, 나는 제법 크게 홉을 재배하는 사람이라네. 혹시 우리 동네 — 애시퍼드 근방 — 에 한번 들러 우리 농장을 둘러보고 싶거든 언제든 오게. 머물고 싶은 만큼 머물러도 우린 반가울 테니.\"\n\n나는 체슬 씨에게 진심으로 고맙다고 인사하며 악수를 나눈다. 나는 행복한 꿈속에 있는 것만 같다. 나는 라킨스 맏딸과 또 한 번 왈츠를 춘다. 그녀는 내가 춤을 어찌나 잘 추는지 모르겠다고 말한다! 나는 이루 말할 수 없는 더없는 행복에 젖어 집으로 가고, 밤새도록 상상 속에서 내 사랑스러운 여신의 파란 허리에 팔을 두르고 왈츠를 춘다. 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 나는 황홀한 회상에 빠져 지낸다. 그러나 거리에서도 그녀를 보지 못하고, 찾아가도 그녀를 만나지 못한다. 이 실망에 대한 위안은 그 신성한 정표, 시들어 버린 꽃뿐이라 어딘가 미진하다.\n\n\"트롯우드.\" 어느 날 저녁 식사를 마친 뒤 애그니스가 말한다. \"내일 누가 결혼하는지 알아? 네가 좋아하는 사람이야.\"\n\n\"설마 너는 아니겠지, 애그니스?\"\n\n\"내가 아니지!\" 그녀가 베껴 적던 악보에서 명랑한 얼굴을 든다. \"아버지, 이 사람 좀 보세요. — 라킨스 맏딸이야.\"\n\n\"베, 베일리 대위한테?\" 나는 가까스로 그만큼 물을 힘만 낸다.\n\n\"아니, 무슨 대위가 아니야. 홉 재배하는 체슬 씨한테.\"\n\n나는 한 일이 주쯤 끔찍하게 풀이 죽어 지낸다. 나는 반지를 빼고, 가장 허름한 옷을 입고, 곰 기름도 바르지 않고, 고인이 된 라킨스 양의 시든 꽃을 두고 자주 한탄한다. 그러다 그 무렵에는 이런 식의 삶에 다소 진력이 나고, 게다가 그 푸주한에게서 새로운 도발까지 받았던 터라, 나는 꽃을 내던지고 푸주한과 한판 붙으러 나가 그를 보란 듯이 때려눕힌다.\n\n이것, 그리고 반지를 다시 끼고 곰 기름도 적당히 다시 바르기 시작한 것이, 이제 와 내가 열일곱 살로 나아가던 길에서 분간해 낼 수 있는 마지막 표지들이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 19. I LOOK ABOUT ME, AND MAKE A DISCOVERY\n\n학교생활이 막바지에 다다라 스트롱 박사님 곁을 떠날 때가 되었을 때, 마음 깊은 곳에서 기뻤는지 슬펐는지 나조차 잘 모르겠다. 나는 그곳에서 무척 행복했고, 박사님을 깊이 따랐으며, 그 작은 세계에서 손꼽히는 두드러진 학생이었다. 이런 이유로 떠나는 것이 서운했다. 하지만 다른 이유로는, 그리 알맹이 없는 이유들이었으되, 기쁘기도 했다. 내 마음대로 할 수 있는 어엿한 청년이 된다는 것, 제 앞가림을 하는 청년에게 따라붙는 위엄, 그 당당한 존재가 보고 또 해낼 놀라운 일들, 그리고 그가 세상에 틀림없이 일으킬 놀라운 파장 — 이런 어렴풋한 상상들이 나를 꾀어냈다. 이 헛된 공상이 내 어린 마음을 어찌나 강하게 사로잡았던지, 지금 돌이켜 생각해 보면 나는 학교를 떠나면서도 마땅히 느껴야 할 아쉬움을 거의 느끼지 못했던 것 같다. 이 이별은 다른 이별들이 내게 남긴 것 같은 자국을 남기지 않았다. 그때 내가 어떤 기분이었는지, 그 사정이 어떠했는지 떠올려 보려 애써도 헛수고였다. 그 일은 내 기억 속에서 그리 대단치 않았던 것이다. 짐작건대 눈앞에 펼쳐지는 전망이 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 모양이다. 그때 내 어린 시절의 경험은 거의 아무런 무게도 지니지 못했다는 것, 그리고 그 무렵 인생이란 무엇보다도, 막 첫 장을 넘기려는 한 편의 거대한 동화 같았다는 것만은 분명히 안다.\n\n고모님과 나는 내가 무슨 일에 몸담아야 할지를 두고 진지한 의논을 여러 차례 나누었다. 일 년 남짓, 나는 고모님이 거듭 던지는 \"넌 뭐가 되고 싶으냐?\"라는 물음에 흡족한 대답을 찾으려 애썼다. 하지만 아무리 살펴봐도 나에게는 딱히 마음 끌리는 것이 없었다. 만일 항해술이라는 학문을 깨치도록 영감을 받아, 날쌘 탐험선의 지휘를 맡고, 의기양양한 발견의 항해로 세계를 한 바퀴 돌 수 있었다면, 나는 내가 완벽히 어울리는 자리를 찾았다고 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 그런 기적 같은 길이 마련되어 있지 않은 마당에, 내 바람이라곤 고모님의 지갑에 너무 무거운 짐을 지우지 않을 만한 일에 매달려, 그것이 무엇이든 제 몫을 다하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n딕 씨도 사색에 잠긴 듯 점잖은 태도로 우리 회의에 꼬박꼬박 함께했다. 그는 딱 한 번 말고는 의견을 낸 적이 없었다. 그 한 번 (무엇이 그의 머릿속에 그런 생각을 집어넣었는지는 모르겠다), 그는 느닷없이 내가 '놋쇠장이'가 되어야 한다고 제안했다. 고모님이 이 제안을 어찌나 쌀쌀맞게 받아넘겼던지, 그는 두 번 다시 입을 떼지 못했고, 그 후로는 줄곧 고모님의 의견을 기다리며 그녀를 조심스레 바라보다가, 주머니 속 동전을 짤랑거리는 일에만 매달렸다.\n\n\"트롯, 내 얘기 좀 들어 봐라, 얘야.\" 내가 학교를 떠나던 크리스마스 무렵의 어느 아침, 고모님이 말했다. \"이 까다로운 문제가 아직도 풀리지 않았고, 또 우리가 결정에 실수를 하지 않으려면, 잠시 숨 좀 돌리는 게 낫겠다 싶구나. 그동안 너는 이 일을 새로운 눈으로, 학생이 아닌 사람의 눈으로 바라보도록 애써 보거라.\"\n\n\"그럴게요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"문득 이런 생각이 들더구나.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"기분 전환을 좀 하고, 바깥세상을 잠깐 엿보는 게 네 마음을 알아차리고 더 차분한 판단을 내리는 데 도움이 될 거라고 말이야. 이를테면 네가 다시 그 옛 고장으로 내려가서, 그 — 그 야만스럽기 짝이 없는 이름을 가진 외딴 데 사는 여자나 한번 보고 오면 어떻겠니.\" 고모님은 이렇게 말하며 코를 문질렀다. 패거티가 그런 이름으로 불린다는 걸 끝내 온전히 용서하지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n\"세상 무엇보다도, 고모님, 저는 그게 제일 좋아요!\"\n\n\"그래.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"다행이구나, 나도 그게 좋으니까. 하지만 네가 그걸 좋아하는 건 자연스럽고 이치에 맞는 일이지. 그리고 나는 네가 무슨 일을 하든, 트롯, 늘 자연스럽고 이치에 맞게 하리라고 굳게 믿는다.\"\n\n\"그러길 바라요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"네 누이동생, 베시 트롯우드는 말이다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 세상에 숨 쉬어 본 그 어떤 여자애 못지않게 자연스럽고 이치에 맞는 아이였을 게다. 너는 그 애에게 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 될 거지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"저는 고모님께 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 되길 바라요. 그것만으로도 저에겐 충분해요.\"\n\n\"네 그 가엾고 어린애 같던 엄마가 오래 살지 못한 건 차라리 다행이었어.\" 고모님이 흐뭇하게 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"안 그랬으면 지금쯤 제 아들이 자랑스러워 어쩔 줄 모르다가, 그 여리고 작은 머리가 — 돌아갈 머리가 조금이라도 남아 있었다면 — 완전히 돌아 버렸을 테니까.\" (고모님은 늘 자신의 약한 구석을 이렇게 내 가엾은 어머니에게 떠넘기는 식으로 나를 위해 변명하곤 했다.) \"어머나 세상에, 트롯우드, 넌 어쩜 그렇게 그 애를 닮았니!\"\n\n\"기분 좋게 닮았길 바라요, 고모님?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이 애가 그 애를 얼마나 닮았는지, 딕.\" 고모님이 힘주어 말했다. \"이 애가 그 애를 닮은 게, 꼭 그 애가 속을 끓이기 시작하기 전 그날 오후 같다니까 — 어머나 세상에, 이 애가 그 두 눈으로 나를 바라보는 게 그 애를 쏙 빼닮았어!\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니까?\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 데이비드도 닮았어.\" 고모님이 단정 짓듯 말했다.\n\n\"데이비드를 정말 많이 닮았네요!\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 네게 바라는 건 말이다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 다시 입을 열었다. \"몸을 두고 하는 말이 아니라 마음을 두고 하는 말이야. 몸이야 아주 멀쩡하니까. 내가 바라는 건, 네가 단단한 사내가 되는 거야. 제 뜻을 지닌 멋지고 단단한 사내 말이다. 결단력을 갖춘,\" 고모님은 나를 향해 모자를 흔들고 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 말했다. \"각오를 갖춘. 줏대를 갖춘 사람, 트롯 — 그 누구에게도, 그 무엇에도, 정당한 이유가 아니고서는 흔들리지 않는 굳센 줏대를 갖춘 사람이 되는 거야. 그게 내가 네게 바라는 거다. 그게 네 아버지도 네 어머니도 둘 다 될 수 있었던 모습이고, 하늘은 아시지, 그랬더라면 둘 다 훨씬 나았을 게다.\"\n\n나는 고모님이 그려 보인 그런 사람이 되길 바란다고 넌지시 일렀다.\n\n\"네가 작은 일에서부터 스스로를 믿고, 스스로 행동하기 시작하도록,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나는 너를 이번 여행에 혼자 보낼 작정이다. 한때는 딕 씨를 너와 함께 보낼까도 생각했지만, 다시 생각해 보니 그분은 나를 돌보도록 곁에 두어야겠구나.\"\n\n딕 씨는 한순간 조금 실망한 듯 보였다. 그러나 세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인을 돌봐야 한다는 명예와 위엄이 그의 얼굴에 다시 햇살을 되돌려 놓았다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 진정서도 있고—\"\n\n\"오, 물론이지요.\" 딕 씨가 황급히 말했다. \"트롯우드, 나는 그걸 당장 끝낼 작정이야 — 정말 당장 끝내야 하거든! 그러고 나면 그게 들어갈 거고, 알다시피 — 그러고 나면—\" 딕 씨는 말을 멈추고 한참을 뜸 들이더니 이렇게 말했다. \"한바탕 난리가 나겠지!\"\n\n고모님의 다정한 계획에 따라, 나는 머지않아 두둑한 돈주머니와 여행 가방을 갖추고서, 다정한 배웅을 받으며 길을 떠났다. 헤어질 때 고모님은 나에게 좋은 충고 몇 마디와 입맞춤을 잔뜩 해 주었다. 그러면서 자신의 목적은 내가 주위를 둘러보고 조금이나마 생각해 보는 데 있으니, 마음 내키면 서퍽으로 내려가는 길에든 돌아오는 길에든 런던에서 며칠 묵어도 좋다고 일러 주었다. 한마디로, 나는 세 주에서 한 달가량 내가 하고 싶은 대로 할 자유를 얻은 셈이었다. 그 자유에 붙은 조건이라곤 앞서 말한 대로 생각하고 주위를 둘러보는 것, 그리고 일주일에 세 번씩 편지를 써서 내 소식을 빠짐없이 전하겠다는 약속뿐이었다.\n\n나는 먼저 캔터베리로 갔다. 애그니스와 위크필드 씨 (그 집에 있던 내 옛 방을 나는 아직 내놓지 않은 채였다)에게, 그리고 마음씨 고운 박사님에게도 작별을 고하기 위해서였다. 애그니스는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐하면서, 내가 떠난 뒤로 그 집이 예전 같지 않았다고 말했다.\n\n\"멀리 떨어져 있으면 저도 제가 아닌 것만 같아요.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 곁에 없으면 오른손을 잃은 것 같은 기분이에요. 하긴 그건 별 대단한 말도 아니죠. 제 오른손에는 머리도 없고 마음도 없으니까요. 당신을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 당신에게 의논하고, 당신을 길잡이로 삼잖아요, 애그니스.\"\n\n\"저를 아는 사람들은 다들 저를 응석받이로 만드는 것 같은데요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"아니에요. 그건 당신이 다른 누구와도 같지 않기 때문이에요. 당신은 그렇게 착하고, 마음씨가 곱잖아요. 그렇게 부드러운 천성을 지녔고, 늘 옳고요.\"\n\n\"당신 말투를 들으니,\" 애그니스가 일감을 손에 쥔 채 즐겁게 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"꼭 제가 옛날의 라킨스 양이라도 된 것 같네요.\"\n\n\"이런! 제 속을 털어놓은 걸 그렇게 놀려먹는 건 너무하잖아요.\" 나는 그 파란 옷을 입은 나의 정복자를 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 대답했다. \"그래도 저는 여전히 당신에게 속을 털어놓을 거예요, 애그니스. 저는 그 버릇에서 절대 벗어나지 못할 거예요. 곤경에 빠질 때나 사랑에 빠질 때나, 당신이 허락만 해 준다면 저는 늘 당신에게 말할 거예요 — 언젠가 정말 진지하게 사랑에 빠지게 되더라도요.\"\n\n\"어머, 당신은 늘 진지했잖아요!\" 애그니스가 다시 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"오! 그건 어린애였을 때, 아니면 학생이었을 때 얘기죠.\" 나도 따라 웃으며, 조금 멋쩍은 기색을 감추지 못한 채 말했다. \"이제 시절이 달라지고 있으니, 언젠가는 저도 무섭도록 진지한 상태에 빠지겠죠. 정작 제가 궁금한 건, 애그니스, 어째서 당신은 이때껏 진지해지지 않았느냐는 거예요.\"\n\n애그니스는 또 한 번 웃으며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"오, 저는 당신이 그렇지 않다는 걸 알아요!\" 내가 말했다. \"왜냐하면 만일 그랬다면 당신은 저에게 말했을 테니까요. 아니면 적어도,\" — 그녀의 얼굴에 어렴풋이 홍조가 스치는 것을 보았기에 — \"제가 스스로 알아차리게 내버려 두었을 테니까요. 하지만 제가 아는 한, 당신의 사랑을 받을 자격이 있는 사람은 아무도 없어요, 애그니스. 제가 여기서 본 그 누구보다도 더 고귀한 인품을 지니고, 모든 면에서 더 자격 있는 누군가가 나타나야만 저도 허락을 할 거예요. 앞으로 저는 당신을 흠모하는 자들을 모두 빈틈없이 지켜볼 거고, 그 행운의 주인공에게는 아주 많은 걸 요구할 거예요, 정말이에요.\"\n\n여기까지 우리는, 그저 어린애로 시작해 오랫동안 자연스레 자라난 정다운 사이에서 우러나온, 속내를 터놓는 농담과 진심이 뒤섞인 말을 주고받았다. 그런데 애그니스가 갑자기 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보더니, 사뭇 다른 투로 말했다.\n\n\"트롯우드, 당신에게 묻고 싶은 게 하나 있는데, 어쩌면 앞으로 오랫동안 다시 물을 기회가 없을지도 몰라요 — 제 생각엔 다른 누구에게도 묻지 못할 그런 거예요. 아버지에게서 조금씩 일어나는 어떤 변화를 눈치챈 적 있나요?\"\n\n나는 그걸 눈치채고 있었고, 그녀도 그런지 종종 궁금했던 터였다. 지금 내 얼굴에 그 마음이 그대로 드러났던 게 분명하다. 그녀의 눈이 이내 아래로 떨궈졌고, 나는 그 눈에 고인 눈물을 보았다.\n\n\"그게 무엇인지 말해 줘요.\" 그녀가 나직한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 — 솔직히 다 털어놔도 될까요, 애그니스, 그분을 이토록 좋아하는 제가?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔, 그분이 제가 여기 처음 온 뒤로 점점 심해진 그 버릇 때문에 스스로를 해치고 계신 것 같아요. 그분은 자주 몹시 신경이 곤두서 있어요 — 아니면 제 착각일까요.\"\n\n\"착각이 아니에요.\" 애그니스가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"그분은 손을 떨고, 말이 또렷하지 않고, 눈빛이 사나워져요. 제가 눈여겨봤는데, 바로 그런 때, 그러니까 그분이 가장 평소답지 않을 때, 어김없이 무슨 일 때문에 그분을 찾는 사람이 있더군요.\"\n\n\"유라이아가요.\" 애그니스가 말했다.\n\n\"네. 그래서 그 일을 감당할 수 없다는 느낌, 혹은 그 일을 제대로 이해하지 못했다는 느낌, 혹은 자기도 모르게 자기 상태를 드러내고 말았다는 느낌이 그분을 어찌나 불안하게 만드는지, 이튿날이면 더 나빠지고, 그다음 날이면 또 더 나빠져서, 결국 그분은 기진맥진하고 초췌해지는 거예요. 제 말에 놀라지 말아요, 애그니스, 하지만 바로 며칠 전 저녁에도 그런 상태에 빠진 그분이 책상에 머리를 묻고 어린애처럼 눈물을 흘리는 걸 봤어요.\"\n\n내가 아직 말을 다 끝내기도 전에 그녀의 손이 부드럽게 내 입술 앞을 스쳐 지나갔고, 다음 순간 그녀는 방문께에서 아버지를 맞이하며 그의 어깨에 매달려 있었다. 두 사람이 함께 나를 바라볼 때 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을, 나는 무척 가슴 뭉클하게 느꼈다. 그 아름다운 눈빛에는 아버지를 향한 그토록 깊은 사랑과, 그분의 온갖 애정과 보살핌에 대한 고마움이 담겨 있었다. 또한 거기에는, 내 마음속 가장 깊은 생각에서까지 부디 아버지를 다정하게 대해 주고, 그분에게 어떤 모진 해석도 들이대지 말아 달라는 간절한 호소가 담겨 있었다. 그녀는 그 순간 아버지를 그토록 자랑스러워하면서도 헌신적이었고, 그러면서도 그토록 안쓰러워하고 애달파했으며, 또 나 역시 그러하리라 그토록 믿고 의지하고 있었다. 그러니 그녀가 어떤 말을 했더라도 그 눈빛보다 더 많은 것을 나에게 전하거나, 나를 더 깊이 움직이지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n우리는 박사님 댁에서 차를 마시기로 되어 있었다. 우리는 여느 때처럼 그 시각에 그리로 갔고, 서재 난롯가에 둘러앉은 박사님과 젊은 부인, 그리고 그녀의 어머니를 만났다. 박사님은 내가 마치 중국에라도 가는 양 내 떠남을 크게 떠들썩하게 여기며, 나를 귀한 손님으로 맞이했다. 그러고는 옛 제자의 얼굴이 불길에 발갛게 물드는 것을 보고 싶다며, 난로에 장작 한 토막을 더 넣으라고 일렀다.\n\n\"트롯우드를 대신할 새 얼굴을 이제 많이는 못 볼 걸세, 위크필드.\" 박사님이 두 손을 녹이며 말했다. \"나도 점점 게을러지고, 편한 게 좋아지는구먼. 앞으로 여섯 달만 지나면 내 젊은 제자들을 다 내려놓고 더 조용한 삶을 살 작정이야.\"\n\n\"십 년째 그 말씀만 하고 계십니다, 박사님.\" 위크필드 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 이번엔 정말 그럴 작정이야.\" 박사님이 되받았다. \"내 수석 교사가 내 자리를 물려받을 걸세 — 드디어 진심이라네 — 그러니 자네도 곧 우리 계약을 마련해서, 우리 두 사람을 한 쌍의 악당처럼 단단히 묶어 두어야 할 거야.\"\n\n\"그리고 잘 살피셔야지요.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"박사님이 손해 보지 않도록 말입니다, 안 그렇습니까? 박사님 혼자 계약을 맺으셨다간 틀림없이 손해를 보실 테니까요. 좋습니다! 저는 준비됐습니다. 제 직업에서 그보다 고약한 일도 많으니까요.\"\n\n\"그러면 나는 그때 가서 생각할 거라곤 아무것도 없겠군.\" 박사님이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내 사전, 그리고 이 또 다른 계약 거래 — 애니뿐일 테니.\"\n\n위크필드 씨가 애그니스 곁에서 찻상에 앉아 있는 애니 쪽으로 눈길을 던지자, 그녀는 평소답지 않은 머뭇거림과 수줍음으로 그의 시선을 피하는 듯했다. 그러자 그의 관심이 그녀에게 못 박히듯 쏠렸는데, 마치 무언가가 그의 생각을 건드린 것만 같았다.\n\n\"인도에서 우편물이 한 통 온 모양이더군요.\" 그가 잠시 침묵하다가 말했다.\n\n\"참, 그렇지! 잭 맬든 씨에게서 온 편지들이라네!\" 박사님이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니까!\" \"가엾은 우리 잭!\" 마클럼 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그 견디기 힘든 기후라니! — 듣자 하니, 활활 타는 볼록 렌즈 밑에서 모래더미 위에 사는 거나 매한가지라더군요! 그 애가 튼튼해 보여도 실은 그렇지 못했어요. 사랑하는 박사님, 그 애가 그토록 대담하게 나선 건 그 애의 정신이지 체질이 아니었어요. 애니야, 사랑하는 내 딸아, 너도 네 사촌이 한 번도 튼튼한 적이 없었다는 걸 — 흔히 말하는 그 강건한 축에는 끼지 못했다는 걸 똑똑히 기억할 거야.\" 마클럼 부인은 우리 모두를 둘러보며 힘주어 말했다. \"내 딸과 그 애가 어린 시절을 함께 보내며 온종일 팔짱을 끼고 쏘다니던 그때부터 말이지.\"\n\n이렇게 지목당한 애니는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"부인 말씀을 들으니, 맬든 씨가 편찮으신 모양이군요?\" 위크필드 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"편찮다고요!\" 노병이 대답했다. \"이보세요, 선생, 그 애는 별의별 병을 다 앓고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"건강한 것만 빼고요?\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지, 건강한 것만 빼고요!\" 노병이 말했다. \"틀림없이 무시무시한 일사병을 여러 번 앓았을 테고, 정글 열병에 학질에, 그 밖에 입에 올릴 수 있는 온갖 병을 다 앓았을 거예요. 간으로 말하자면,\" 노병이 체념한 듯 말했다. \"그야 물론, 그곳에 처음 발을 디뎠을 때 이미 완전히 손을 놓아 버렸지요!\"\n\n\"그가 이 모든 걸 직접 그렇게 말하던가요?\" 위크필드 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"말하느냐고요? 이보세요, 선생.\" 마클럼 부인이 고개와 부채를 함께 내저으며 되받았다. \"그런 질문을 하시는 걸 보니 우리 가엾은 잭 맬든을 도통 모르시는군요. 말하느냐고요? 천만에요. 야생마 네 마리 발꿈치에 매달아 끌고 다녀도 그 애 입에서는 그런 말 안 나올 거예요.\"\n\n\"엄마!\" 스트롱 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"애니야, 사랑하는 딸아.\" 그녀의 어머니가 되받았다. \"내 진심으로 부탁하는데, 이번 한 번만큼은 내가 하는 말을 거들 게 아니라면 제발 끼어들지 말아 다오. 너도 나만큼이나 잘 알지 않니, 네 사촌 맬든이라면 야생마가 몇 마리든 그 발꿈치에 매달려 끌려다닐 사람이라는 걸 — 그러니 내가 왜 굳이 네 마리로만 한정하겠니! 나는 네 마리로 한정 안 할 거야 — 여덟 마리든, 열여섯 마리든, 서른두 마리든, 박사님의 계획을 뒤엎을 만한 말은 한마디도 하느니 차라리 그게 낫지.\"\n\n\"위크필드의 계획이지요.\" 박사님이 얼굴을 쓸어내리며, 자신의 조언자를 미안한 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"그러니까, 그 애를 위한 우리 둘의 공동 계획 말입니다. 나야 본디 외국이든 본국이든 상관없다고 했으니까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 저는,\" 위크필드 씨가 무겁게 덧붙였다. \"외국이라고 했지요. 그를 외국으로 보낸 건 저였습니다. 그건 제 책임입니다.\"\n\n\"오! 책임이라니요!\" 노병이 말했다. \"모든 게 다 잘되라고 한 일이었어요, 사랑하는 위크필드 씨. 모든 게 더없이 친절하고 좋은 뜻에서 한 일이었다는 걸 우리 다 알잖아요. 하지만 그 가여운 애가 거기서 못 산다면, 못 사는 거예요. 그리고 거기서 못 산다면, 박사님의 계획을 뒤엎느니 차라리 거기서 죽고 말 거예요. 나는 그 애를 잘 알아요.\" 노병은 일종의 차분한 예언자적 비탄에 잠겨 부채질을 하며 말했다. \"그래서 알아요, 그 애는 박사님의 계획을 뒤엎느니 차라리 거기서 죽을 거예요.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 부인.\" 박사님이 쾌활하게 말했다. \"나는 내 계획에 그리 집착하지 않으니, 내 손으로 얼마든지 뒤엎을 수 있어요. 다른 계획으로 바꾸면 그만이지요. 만일 잭 맬든 씨가 건강 때문에 본국으로 돌아온다면, 다시 돌려보내선 안 되고, 우리는 이 나라에서 그에게 더 알맞고 복된 자리를 마련해 주도록 애써야 할 겁니다.\"\n\n마클럼 부인은 이 너그러운 말에 — 두말할 것 없이, 그녀가 전혀 기대하지도 유도하지도 않은 말이었기에 — 어찌나 감격했던지, 그저 박사님답다고 말할 수밖에 없었다. 그러고는 부챗살에 입을 맞추었다가 그것으로 그의 손을 톡톡 두드리는 동작을 여러 차례 되풀이했다. 그런 다음 자기 딸 애니를 가만히 나무랐는데, 자기를 봐서 옛 소꿉친구에게 이런 친절이 쏟아지는데도 더 살갑게 굴지 않는다는 이유에서였다. 그러더니 자기 집안에서 마땅히 도움을 받아 제 발로 일어서게 해 줄 만한 다른 갸륵한 식구들에 관한 이런저런 이야기로 우리를 즐겁게 해 주었다.\n\n이러는 내내, 그녀의 딸 애니는 단 한 번도 입을 떼거나 눈을 들지 않았다. 이러는 내내, 위크필드 씨는 제 딸 곁에 앉은 그녀에게서 눈길을 떼지 않았다. 내가 보기에 그는 누군가 자기를 지켜본다는 생각은 조금도 하지 못한 채, 그녀에게, 그리고 그녀와 얽힌 자신의 생각에 어찌나 골몰했던지 완전히 빠져든 듯했다. 이제 그는 잭 맬든 씨가 자기 자신에 관해 실제로 무어라 썼는지, 또 누구에게 썼는지 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 여기 있잖아요.\" 마클럼 부인이 박사님 머리 위 벽난로 선반에서 편지 한 통을 집어 들며 말했다. \"그 가여운 애가 박사님께 직접 이렇게 썼어요 — 어디 보자? 오! '제 건강이 심하게 나빠지고 있음을 알려 드리게 되어 송구합니다. 회복할 유일한 희망으로, 한동안 본국으로 돌아가야 할 처지에 몰리지 않을까 두렵습니다.' 가여운 것, 이건 꽤 분명하죠! 회복할 유일한 희망이라니! 그런데 애니에게 보낸 편지는 더더욱 분명해요. 애니야, 그 편지를 다시 보여 다오.\"\n\n\"지금은 안 돼요, 엄마.\" 그녀가 나직한 목소리로 사정했다.\n\n\"얘야, 너는 정말이지 어떤 일에 있어선 세상에서 가장 우스꽝스러운 사람 중 하나야.\" 그녀의 어머니가 되받았다. \"그리고 어쩌면 제 집안의 도리에 가장 매정한 사람일지도 모르고. 내가 직접 달라고 하지 않았더라면, 그 편지가 있다는 것조차 우리는 끝내 듣지도 못했을 게다. 얘야, 그걸 스트롱 박사님께 대한 신뢰라고 부를 수 있겠니? 어이가 없구나. 너는 더 잘 알아야 할 텐데.\"\n\n편지는 마지못해 꺼내졌다. 그리고 내가 그것을 노부인에게 건넬 때, 내가 받아 든 그 내키지 않는 손이 어떻게 떨리는지를 나는 보았다.\n\n\"자 어디 보자.\" 마클럼 부인이 안경을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"그 대목이 어디 있더라. '지난날의 추억이, 더없이 사랑하는 애니'— 어쩌고저쩌고 — 거긴 아니고. '인자하신 늙은 대리인'— 이 사람이 누구지? 어머나, 애니야, 네 사촌 맬든은 어쩜 글씨를 이렇게 알아보기 힘들게 쓰니, 그리고 나는 또 왜 이리 멍청하니! 물론 '박사님'이지. 아! 정말이지 인자하시고말고!\" 여기서 그녀는 말을 멈추고 다시 부채에 입을 맞춘 다음, 잔잔한 만족감에 잠겨 우리를 바라보고 있던 박사님을 향해 부채를 흔들었다. \"자, 이제 찾았다. '애니, 자네는 이 소식을 듣고도 놀라지 않을지 모르겠네'— 그래, 그렇고말고, 그 애가 정말 튼튼한 적이 없었다는 걸 알았으니까. 내가 방금 뭐라고 했더라? — '내가 이 머나먼 곳에서 어찌나 많은 걸 견뎌 왔는지, 이제 무슨 수를 써서라도 이곳을 떠나기로 마음먹었다네. 가능하면 병가로, 그게 안 된다면 아예 사직으로라도. 내가 이곳에서 견뎌 왔고 또 견디고 있는 것은 도저히 참을 수 없는 것이라네.' 그리고 그 더없이 훌륭한 분의 발 빠른 처사가 아니었다면,\" 마클럼 부인이 아까처럼 박사님에게 신호를 보내며 편지를 다시 접고 말했다. \"생각만 해도 저로선 도저히 참을 수 없는 일이었을 거예요.\"\n\n위크필드 씨는, 비록 노부인이 이 소식에 대한 그의 논평이라도 기다리는 양 그를 바라보았지만, 단 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그저 눈을 땅에 박은 채 굳은 듯 침묵하고 앉아 있었다. 그 화제가 끝나고 다른 이야깃거리로 자리가 채워진 한참 뒤까지도 그는 그대로였다. 이따금 생각에 잠긴 채 눈살을 찌푸리며, 박사님이나 그의 부인, 혹은 두 사람 모두에게 잠깐 눈길을 머무는 것 말고는, 좀처럼 눈을 들지 않았다.\n\n박사님은 음악을 무척 좋아했다. 애그니스는 더없이 곱고 풍부한 감정을 담아 노래했고, 스트롱 부인도 그러했다. 두 사람은 함께 노래하고 함께 이중주를 연주했으며, 우리는 자그마한 음악회를 연 셈이었다. 그러나 나는 두 가지를 눈여겨봤다. 첫째, 애니가 이내 평정을 되찾아 완전히 제 모습으로 돌아왔는데도, 그녀와 위크필드 씨 사이에는 둘을 완전히 갈라놓는 어떤 빈틈이 가로놓여 있다는 것이었다. 둘째, 위크필드 씨가 그녀와 애그니스 사이의 친밀함을 못마땅해하면서, 불안한 눈으로 그것을 지켜보는 듯하다는 것이었다. 그리고 이제, 고백하건대, 맬든 씨가 떠나던 그날 밤 내가 본 광경에 대한 기억이, 그때껏 한 번도 지니지 못했던 의미를 품은 채 처음으로 내게 되살아나 나를 괴롭히기 시작했다. 그녀 얼굴의 순결한 아름다움은 예전만큼 순결하게 다가오지 않았다. 나는 그녀의 자연스러운 우아함과 매력을 의심했고, 그녀 곁에 앉은 애그니스를 바라보며 애그니스가 얼마나 착하고 참된지를 생각하노라니, 이것이 어울리지 않는 우정이 아닐까 하는 의심이 마음속에 솟아올랐다.\n\n그러나 정작 그녀 자신은 그 우정 속에서 그토록 행복했고, 다른 한 사람도 그토록 행복했기에, 두 사람은 그날 저녁을 마치 한 시간처럼 훌쩍 흘려보냈다. 그날 저녁은 내가 또렷이 기억하는 한 가지 사건으로 막을 내렸다. 두 사람이 서로 작별을 고하고, 애그니스가 그녀를 끌어안고 입을 맞추려는 참이었는데, 위크필드 씨가 마치 우연인 듯 둘 사이로 끼어들더니 애그니스를 재빨리 끌어당겼다. 그때 나는, 마치 그사이의 모든 시간이 지워지고 내가 아직도 그 떠나던 밤의 문간에 서 있기라도 한 듯이, 그 밤에 보았던 그 표정이 스트롱 부인의 얼굴에, 그의 얼굴을 마주한 그 얼굴에 떠오르는 것을 보았다.\n\n이것이 내게 얼마나 깊은 인상을 남겼는지, 또 나중에 그녀를 떠올릴 때 그녀를 이 표정에서 떼어 내어 그 순결하고 사랑스러운 얼굴로 다시 기억하기가 얼마나 불가능했는지, 나는 말로 다 할 수가 없다. 집에 돌아온 뒤에도 그 표정이 나를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다. 나는 어두운 먹구름이 낮게 드리운 박사님 댁 지붕을 뒤로하고 떠나온 것만 같았다. 그분의 희끗한 머리에 대해 내가 품었던 경외심에는, 자신을 배신하는 자들을 믿는 그 모습에 대한 가여움과, 그분을 해치는 자들에 대한 분노가 뒤섞여 있었다. 닥쳐오는 크나큰 고통의 그림자, 그리고 아직 뚜렷한 형체조차 갖추지 못한 크나큰 치욕의 그림자가, 내가 소년 시절에 일하고 뛰놀던 그 고요한 곳에 얼룩처럼 내려앉아 그곳에 모진 상처를 입혔다. 백 년을 줄곧 제 안에 틀어박혀 있던 그 근엄하고 잎 넓은 늙은 알로에 나무들도, 단정하게 손질된 매끈한 잔디밭도, 돌 항아리들도, 박사님의 산책로도, 그 모든 것 위를 떠도는 대성당 종소리의 정겨운 울림도, 더는 떠올려도 즐겁지 않았다. 그것은 마치 내 소년 시절의 고요한 성소가 내 눈앞에서 약탈당하고, 그 평화와 명예가 바람결에 흩어져 버린 것만 같았다.\n\n그러나 아침이 밝자 애그니스의 손길이 구석구석 배어 있는 그 오래된 집과 작별할 일이 다가왔고, 그것만으로도 내 마음은 충분히 채워졌다. 머지않아 다시 그곳에 올 것이 분명했고, 어쩌면 내 옛 방에서 다시, 어쩌면 자주 잘 수 있을 터였다. 하지만 그곳에 살던 날들은 지나갔고, 그 옛 시절은 끝나 버린 것이었다. 도버로 부치려고 그곳에 아직 남아 있던 책과 옷가지를 챙길 때, 내 마음은 유라이아 힙에게 차마 드러내고 싶지 않을 만큼 무거웠다. 그는 나를 어찌나 부산스레 도왔던지, 나는 박정하게도 그가 내 떠남을 엄청나게 반기는 게 아닐까 하고 생각했다.\n\n나는 어찌어찌, 자못 사내다운 척 무심한 태도를 꾸며 가며 애그니스와 그 아버지에게서 빠져나와, 런던행 마차의 마부석에 올라앉았다. 시내를 지나는 동안 내 마음이 어찌나 누그러지고 너그러워졌던지, 옛 원수인 푸줏간 주인에게 고개라도 까딱하며 술이나 한잔하라고 오 실링을 던져 줄까 하는 생각이 반쯤 들 정도였다. 하지만 가게에서 커다란 도마를 박박 긁어 대며 서 있는 그의 모습이 어찌나 지독하게 고집스러운 푸줏간 주인 같았던지, 게다가 내가 부러뜨려 놓은 앞니 하나가 빠진 탓에 그 몰골이 조금도 나아지지 않았던지라, 나는 아예 다가서지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 마음먹었다.\n\n마차가 길에 제대로 접어들었을 때, 내 마음을 사로잡은 가장 큰 목적은, 기억하건대, 마부에게 되도록 나이 들어 보이는 것과 몹시 무뚝뚝하게 말하는 것이었다. 뒤엣것은 적잖이 몸이 거북했지만 그래도 끝까지 밀고 나갔으니, 그게 어른스러운 짓이라고 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n\"끝까지 가십니까, 선생님?\" 마부가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 윌리엄.\" 나는 짐짓 거들먹거리며 말했다 (나는 그를 알고 있었다). \"런던까지 가네. 그다음엔 서퍽으로 내려갈 거야.\"\n\n\"사냥하시려고요, 선생님?\" 마부가 말했다.\n\n이맘때라면 내가 거기 고래잡이를 하러 내려가는 거나 매한가지로 그럴듯하지 않다는 걸 그도 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었다. 그래도 나는 우쭐한 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 나는 아직 정하지 못한 척하며 말했다. \"한 방 쏠지 말지는 모르겠군.\" \"요즘 새들이 어지간히 겁이 많아졌다더군요.\" 윌리엄이 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇게 들었네.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"서퍽이 선생님 고향이십니까?\" 윌리엄이 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 나는 제법 무게를 잡으며 말했다. \"서퍽이 내 고향이지.\"\n\n\"거기 경단이 유난히 맛있다던데요.\" 윌리엄이 말했다.\n\n나 자신은 그런 줄도 몰랐으나, 내 고향의 명물을 떠받들고 거기에 익숙한 척해야 할 것 같아서, 나는 \"그렇고말고!\" 하는 양 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그리고 그 펀치 말입니다.\" 윌리엄이 말했다. \"그게 바로 명마지요! 서퍽 펀치는 잘난 놈일 때는 제 몸무게만큼의 금값을 한답니다. 선생님은 서퍽 펀치를 직접 길러 보신 적 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"아, 아니.\" 내가 말했다. \"딱히 그렇진 않네.\"\n\n\"제 뒤에 앉으신 신사분 말입니다, 제가 장담하는데,\" 윌리엄이 말했다. \"그놈들을 도매로 길러 내신 분이지요.\"\n\n말한 그 신사는 영 미덥지 못하게 사팔뜨기 눈에 턱이 툭 튀어나온 사람으로, 챙이 좁고 납작한 키 큰 흰 모자를 쓰고 있었으며, 몸에 딱 붙는 누런 바지는 장화에서부터 엉덩이까지 다리 바깥쪽을 따라 온통 단추로 채워진 듯했다. 그의 턱이 마부의 어깨 너머로 쑥 내밀어져 내게 어찌나 가까웠던지, 그 숨결이 내 뒤통수를 간질일 정도였다. 그리고 내가 그를 바라보자, 그는 사팔뜨기가 아닌 쪽 눈으로 매우 능청스럽게 앞에 선 말들을 곁눈질했다.\n\n\"안 그렇습니까?\" 윌리엄이 물었다.\n\n\"안 그렇다니, 뭐가?\" 뒤에 앉은 신사가 말했다.\n\n\"그 서퍽 펀치들을 도매로 길러 내지 않으셨냐고요?\"\n\n\"그런 셈이지.\" 신사가 말했다. \"내가 안 길러 본 말이라곤 없고, 안 길러 본 개라곤 없으니까. 말과 개는 어떤 사람들에겐 그저 취미지. 나한테는 그게 먹을 거고 마실 거야 — 잠자리고, 마누라고, 자식이고 — 읽기고, 쓰기고, 셈이고 — 코담배고, 담배고, 잠이라네.\"\n\n\"마차 뒷자리에 앉아 있을 분 같지는 않죠, 안 그렇습니까?\" 윌리엄이 고삐를 다루며 내 귀에 대고 말했다.\n\n나는 이 말을 그가 내 자리를 차지하고 싶어 하는 뜻으로 받아들여, 얼굴을 붉히며 자리를 내드리겠다고 했다.\n\n\"뭐, 선생님만 괜찮으시다면요.\" 윌리엄이 말했다. \"그게 더 격에 맞을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n나는 늘 이 일을 내 인생에서 겪은 첫 번째 추락이라 여겨 왔다. 마차 사무소에서 자리를 예약할 때 나는 장부의 그 항목에 '마부석'이라고 적게 하고 사무원에게 반 크라운을 쥐여 줬었다. 그 빛나는 높은 자리에 어울리는 영예를 갖추려고 특별히 좋은 외투에 목도리까지 두르고 한껏 차려입었었고, 그 위에 올라앉아 잔뜩 우쭐댔으며, 내가 이 마차의 자랑거리라고 느꼈었다. 그런데 여기 첫 번째 정거장에서, 마구간 냄새를 풍기고 말들이 구보로 달리는 와중에도 사람이라기보다 파리처럼 내 몸 위로 척척 건너다닐 줄 아는 것 말고는 아무 잘난 점도 없는, 사팔뜨기에 추레한 사내에게 자리를 빼앗긴 것이다!\n\n작은 일에서, 차라리 없는 편이 나았을 때조차 내 인생 내내 곧잘 나를 따라다니던 그 자기 불신이, 이 캔터베리 마차 위에서 벌어진 이 사소한 사건으로 자라기를 멈추기는커녕 도리어 부추겨진 것만은 분명했다. 무뚝뚝한 말투로 도망쳐 봐야 헛일이었다. 나는 남은 여정 내내 배 속 깊은 데서 끌어올린 목소리로 말했지만, 완전히 꺼져 버린 기분이었고, 끔찍하게 어리게만 느껴졌다.\n\n그래도 네 마리 말 뒤에 그렇게 올라앉아 있는 것은 묘하고도 흥미진진했다. 잘 배우고, 잘 차려입고, 주머니에는 돈이 두둑한 채로 말이다. 그리고 그 고단한 여정에서 내가 잠을 청했던 곳들을 찾아보는 것도 그러했다. 길가의 눈에 띄는 표지마다 내 생각을 골똘히 붙들어 둘 거리가 넘쳐났다. 우리가 지나치는 떠돌이들을 내려다보다가 그 낯익은 얼굴 생김새가 위로 쳐들리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 마치 그 땜장이의 시커먼 손이 다시 내 셔츠 가슴팍에 들어오기라도 한 듯한 기분이 들었다. 채텀의 좁은 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나면서, 내 윗도리를 사 갔던 그 늙은 괴물이 살던 골목을 스쳐 가며 흘끗 보았을 때, 나는 햇볕 아래서, 또 그늘 속에서 내 돈을 기다리며 앉아 있던 그 자리를 찾으려고 간절히 목을 길게 뺐다. 마침내 런던까지 한 정거장을 남겨 두고, 크리클 선생이 그 무거운 손으로 닥치는 대로 매질을 해 대던 바로 그 세일럼 학원을 지나칠 때, 나는 정당하게 내려가서 그를 흠씬 두들겨 패고, 새장에 갇힌 참새 떼처럼 모든 아이들을 풀어 줄 허락만 받을 수 있다면 내가 가진 전부라도 내놓았을 것이다.\n\n우리는 채링 크로스의 골든 크로스로 갔는데, 그 무렵 그곳은 비좁은 동네에 자리 잡은 곰팡내 나는 여관이었다. 종업원이 나를 휴게실로 안내했고, 객실 하녀가 나를 내 작은 침실로 들여보냈는데, 그 방은 삯마차 같은 냄새가 났고 가족 납골당처럼 꼭꼭 닫혀 있었다. 나는 여전히 내 어린 나이가 뼈저리게 의식되었으니, 누구 하나 나를 조금도 어려워하지 않았기 때문이다. 객실 하녀는 어떤 문제에 대한 내 의견에도 통 관심이 없었고, 종업원은 나를 허물없이 대하며 풋내기인 나에게 충고를 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 종업원이 속을 터놓는 투로 말했다. \"저녁은 무얼 드시겠습니까? 젊은 신사분들은 대개 새고기를 좋아하시던데요. 닭 한 마리 드시지요!\"\n\n나는 되도록 위엄을 갖춰, 닭고기는 당기지 않는다고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"안 당기십니까?\" 종업원이 말했다. \"젊은 신사분들은 대개 쇠고기랑 양고기엔 물려 하시던데요. 송아지 커틀릿 하나 드시지요!\"\n\n나는 달리 무어라 떠올릴 수가 없어서 이 제안에 응했다.\n\n\"감자는 좋아하십니까?\" 종업원이 알랑거리는 미소를 지으며 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 말했다. \"젊은 신사분들은 대개 감자를 너무 많이 드셔서 질리시던데요.\"\n\n나는 더없이 굵은 목소리로 그에게 송아지 커틀릿과 감자, 그리고 거기에 어울리는 것을 모두 주문하라고, 그리고 카운터에 가서 트롯우드 코퍼필드 님 앞으로 온 편지가 있는지 물어보라고 명령했다 — 그런 편지가 없다는 것도, 있을 수가 없다는 것도 알았지만, 그걸 기다리는 척하는 게 사내다워 보일 것 같았다.\n\n그는 곧 돌아와서 편지는 한 통도 없다고 말했고 (나는 무척 놀란 척했다), 난롯가 칸막이 자리에 내 저녁상을 차리기 시작했다. 그러면서 그는 곁들여 무얼 들겠느냐고 물었는데, 내가 \"셰리주 반 파인트\"라고 대답하자, 미안한 말이지만 그는 이때를 틈타 자그마한 술병 여러 개 바닥에 남은 김빠진 찌꺼기를 그만큼 모아 따라낸 듯하다. 내가 이렇게 짐작하는 까닭은, 신문을 읽다가 그가 자기만의 공간인 낮은 나무 칸막이 뒤에서, 마치 약제사가 처방을 조제하듯 그 여러 병에서 한 병으로 분주히 따라 붓는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 게다가 술이 나왔을 때 나는 그 술이 김빠졌다고 느꼈다. 그리고 그 술에는, 그런대로 순수한 상태의 외국산 포도주에서 기대할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 많은 영국 빵 부스러기가 들어 있었던 게 분명했다. 하지만 나는 너무 수줍은 나머지 군말 없이 그것을 마셔 버렸다.\n\n그러고서 기분 좋은 상태에 빠진 채 (이로 미루어, 독을 먹는 과정도 어떤 단계에서는 늘 불쾌하기만 한 것은 아닌 모양이다), 나는 연극을 보러 가기로 마음먹었다. 내가 고른 것은 코번트 가든 극장이었고, 거기 가운데 박스석 뒤편에서 나는 「율리우스 카이사르」와 새로 나온 무언극을 보았다. 그 고귀한 로마인들이 모두 내 눈앞에 살아 움직이며, 학교에서처럼 엄한 감독관이 아니라 나를 즐겁게 해 주려고 들락날락하는 모습은 더없이 새롭고 기쁜 광경이었다. 그러나 그 모든 공연에 뒤섞인 현실감과 신비, 시와 조명과 음악과 사람들이 나에게 미친 영향, 그리고 반짝이고 눈부신 무대 배경이 매끄럽게 바뀌는 어마어마한 광경이 어찌나 황홀했던지, 또 그것이 끝없는 환희의 세계를 어찌나 활짝 열어젖혔던지, 밤 열두 시에 비 내리는 거리로 나섰을 때 나는 마치 오랜 세월 낭만적인 삶을 살아온 구름 속에서, 고함치고, 물 튀기고, 가로등 불빛이 어른거리고, 우산끼리 부딪치고, 삯마차가 비집고 다니고, 나막신이 딸각거리는, 진창에 빠진 비참한 세상으로 떨어져 내린 것만 같았다.\n\n나는 다른 문으로 빠져나와, 정말로 이 땅 위의 이방인이라도 된 양 잠시 거리에 서 있었다. 하지만 나를 향한 거침없는 떠밀림과 밀침에 곧 정신을 차려, 여관으로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 그 길 내내 그 찬란한 환상을 되새기며 여관으로 갔고, 거기서 흑맥주와 굴을 좀 먹은 뒤에도, 새벽 한 시가 넘도록 휴게실 난롯불에 눈을 둔 채 여전히 그 환상을 되새기며 앉아 있었다.\n\n나는 그 연극으로, 또 지난날로 어찌나 가슴이 가득 찼던지 — 그것은 어떤 면에서, 그것을 통해 내 지난 삶이 흘러가는 것이 비쳐 보이는 빛나는 투명막 같았기에 — 잘생기고 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 멋스럽고 편안한 무심함으로 차려입은 한 젊은이의 모습이 — 그 차림새를 나는 아주 잘 기억할 만한 까닭이 있다 — 언제부터 내게 실제 모습으로 다가왔는지 알 수가 없다. 다만 나는 그가 들어오는 것을 알아채지도 못한 채 그가 곁에 있음을 느끼고 있었다는 것, 그리고 내가 여전히 휴게실 난롯불 앞에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다는 것만은 기억난다.\n\n마침내 나는 자리에서 일어나 잠자리에 들려고 했는데, 졸음에 겨운 종업원에게는 여간 다행이 아니었다. 그는 다리가 근질근질해서, 그 좁은 식기실에서 다리를 비틀고, 두드리고, 온갖 모양으로 배배 꼬아 대던 참이었다. 문 쪽으로 가다가 나는 아까 들어온 그 사람을 스쳐 지나며 그를 또렷이 보았다. 나는 곧장 돌아서서, 다시 다가가 한 번 더 그를 보았다. 그는 나를 알아보지 못했지만, 나는 단번에 그를 알아보았다.\n\n다른 때였다면 나는 그에게 말을 걸 자신감이나 결단력이 모자라, 그것을 이튿날로 미루다가 그를 놓쳐 버렸을지도 모른다. 그러나 연극의 흥분이 아직도 한껏 끓어오르던 그때 내 마음 상태에서는, 지난날 그가 나를 보살펴 준 일이 어찌나 내 고마움을 받아 마땅하게 여겨졌던지, 또 그를 향한 나의 옛 사랑이 어찌나 싱그럽고 절로 내 가슴에 차올랐던지, 나는 콩닥거리는 심장을 안고 그에게 곧장 다가가 말했다.\n\n\"스티어포스! 나한테 말 좀 안 걸어 줄래?\"\n\n그는 나를 바라보았다 — 가끔 나를 바라보곤 하던 바로 그 눈빛으로 — 하지만 그의 얼굴에서 나를 알아본 기색은 찾을 수 없었다.\n\n\"날 기억 못 하는 모양이구나.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 그가 별안간 소리쳤다. \"꼬마 코퍼필드잖아!\"\n\n나는 두 손으로 그의 손을 움켜쥐고는 도저히 놓을 수가 없었다. 부끄러움과, 혹시 그가 싫어할지 모른다는 두려움만 아니었다면, 나는 그의 목을 끌어안고 울었을 것이다.\n\n\"이렇게, 이렇게, 이렇게 기뻤던 적이 없어! 사랑하는 스티어포스, 널 만나서 정말 너무 기뻐!\"\n\n\"나도 너를 만나서 기뻐!\" 그가 내 손을 다정하게 흔들며 말했다. \"이런, 코퍼필드, 이 친구야, 너무 그러지 마!\" 그러면서도 그는, 그를 만난 내 기쁨이 나를 이토록 사로잡은 모습을 보고 자기도 기뻐하는 것 같았다.\n\n나는 아무리 안간힘을 써도 끝내 참지 못하고 흘러나온 눈물을 닦아 내고는, 어설피 웃어넘겼다. 그러고서 우리는 나란히 함께 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"그런데, 어쩌다 여기 있게 된 거야?\" 스티어포스가 내 어깨를 툭 치며 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 캔터베리 마차로 여기 왔어. 그 고장에 사는 고모님이 나를 거두셨거든, 그래서 거기서 막 학업을 마쳤어. 너는 어쩌다 여기 있게 된 거야, 스티어포스?\"\n\n\"음, 나는 사람들이 말하는 옥스퍼드 사람이지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그러니까, 나는 주기적으로 거기서 죽을 만큼 따분해진단 말이야 — 그래서 지금 어머니 댁으로 가는 길이야. 너 참 지독히도 상냥해 보이는 친구로구나, 코퍼필드. 지금 다시 보니, 딱 옛날 그대로야! 조금도 안 변했어!\"\n\n\"나는 너를 단번에 알아봤어.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 너야 더 쉽게 기억할 수 있지.\"\n\n그는 곱슬곱슬 뭉친 머리칼을 손으로 쓸어 넘기며 웃더니, 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 의무를 다하러 가는 원정 중이야. 어머니가 시내에서 좀 떨어진 곳에 사시는데, 길이 지독한 데다 우리 집이 어지간히 따분해서, 오늘 밤은 곧장 가지 않고 여기 묵기로 했어. 시내에 들어온 지 여섯 시간도 채 안 됐는데, 그것도 연극을 보면서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 투덜대며 다 흘려보냈지.\"\n\n\"나도 연극을 보고 왔어.\" 내가 말했다. \"코번트 가든에서. 정말 즐겁고 으리으리한 구경거리였어, 스티어포스!\"\n\n스티어포스가 호탕하게 웃었다.\n\n\"내 사랑스러운 어린 데이비.\" 그가 다시 내 어깨를 툭 치며 말했다. \"너는 영락없는 데이지로구나. 해 뜰 녘 들판의 데이지도 너보다 싱그럽진 않을 거야. 나도 코번트 가든에 갔었는데, 그렇게 형편없는 공연은 또 없었어. 어이, 자네!\"\n\n이것은 우리가 서로를 알아보는 광경을 멀찍이서 무척 눈여겨보다가, 이제 공손하게 앞으로 다가온 종업원에게 한 말이었다.\n\n\"내 친구 코퍼필드 씨를 어디에 모셨나?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 무슨 말씀이신지요, 손님?\"\n\n\"그가 어디서 자느냐고. 방 번호가 몇 번이야? 내 말 무슨 뜻인지 알잖아.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 네, 손님.\" 종업원이 미안한 기색으로 말했다. \"코퍼필드 씨는 지금 사십사 호에 계십니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"그래서 대체 무슨 생각으로,\" 스티어포스가 쏘아붙였다. \"코퍼필드 씨를 마구간 위 자그마한 다락방에 처박은 거지?\"\n\n\"그게, 저희가 미처 몰랐지 뭡니까, 손님.\" 종업원이 여전히 미안해하며 대답했다. \"코퍼필드 씨가 그런 걸 까다롭게 여기시는 분인 줄을요. 원하신다면 코퍼필드 씨께 칠십이 호를 드릴 수 있습니다, 손님. 손님 바로 옆방으로요.\"\n\n\"당연히 그게 낫지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그것도 당장.\" 종업원은 곧바로 물러가 방을 바꾸었다. 스티어포스는 내가 사십사 호에 처박혔던 일이 무척이나 재미있다는 듯 또 한 번 웃고는, 또 한 번 내 어깨를 툭 치더니, 이튿날 아침 열 시에 자기와 함께 아침을 먹자고 청했다 — 나로서는 더없이 자랑스럽고 기쁜 마음으로 받아들인 청이었다. 시각이 이제 꽤 늦은지라, 우리는 저마다 촛불을 들고 위층으로 올라가, 그의 방문 앞에서 다정하고 살가운 인사를 나누며 헤어졌다. 새 방은 옛 방보다 훨씬 나았으니, 곰팡내라곤 전혀 없었고, 그 안에는 작은 영지나 다름없는 어마어마한 사주식 침대가 놓여 있었다. 여섯 사람이 베고도 남을 베개들 틈에서 나는 이내 더없이 행복한 기분으로 잠이 들어, 옛 로마와 스티어포스와 우정을 꿈꾸었다. 그러다 새벽 마차들이 바로 아래 아치문을 빠져나가며 우르릉거리는 소리에, 나는 천둥과 신들을 꿈꾸기 시작했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 20. STEERFORTH’S HOME\n\n여덟 시에 객실 하녀가 내 방문을 똑똑 두드리며 면도용 물을 문밖에 놓아 두었다고 알려 주었을 때, 나는 그 물이 쓸데없다는 사실이 뼈저리게 느껴져 침대 속에서 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다. 그 말을 하면서 하녀도 웃은 것 같다는 의심이, 옷을 입는 내내 마음을 갉아먹었다. 아침을 먹으러 내려가다가 계단에서 그녀를 지나칠 때, 나는 내가 봐도 슬그머니 죄지은 듯한 기색을 띠고 있었다. 사실 나는 바라는 것보다 어리다는 사실을 어찌나 예민하게 의식했던지, 이런 한심한 처지에서는 한동안 그녀를 지나칠 엄두조차 내지 못했다. 그러다 그녀가 거기서 빗자루를 들고 있는 소리를 듣고는, 창밖으로 말을 탄 찰스 왕을 내다보며 서 있었는데, 그 동상은 삯마차들이 빚어내는 미로 같은 행렬에 둘러싸여, 부슬부슬 내리는 비와 검누런 안개 속에서 도무지 위엄이라곤 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그러다 마침내 종업원이 그 신사분이 나를 기다리고 계신다고 일러 주었다.\n\n스티어포스가 나를 기다리던 곳은 커피룸이 아니라, 붉은 휘장을 드리우고 터키산 양탄자를 깐 아늑한 독실이었는데, 그곳은 난롯불이 환히 타오르고, 깨끗한 식탁보를 씌운 탁자 위에 김이 모락모락 나는 훌륭한 아침상이 차려져 있었다. 그리고 그 방과 난롯불과 아침상과 스티어포스와 그 모든 것이, 찬장 위에 걸린 작고 둥근 거울 속에 정겨운 축소판으로 빛나고 있었다. 처음에는 좀 수줍었는데, 스티어포스가 어찌나 침착하고 우아하며 모든 면에서 (나이까지 포함해) 나보다 윗길이었던지 그랬다. 하지만 그의 거리낌 없는 보살핌이 이내 그런 마음을 바로잡아 주어, 나는 완전히 마음을 놓았다. 그가 골든 크로스 여관에 일으킨 변화에 나는 아무리 감탄해도 모자랐고, 어제 내가 겪었던 따분하고 쓸쓸한 처지를 오늘 아침의 안락함과 즐거움에 견주어 보아도 그랬다. 종업원의 그 허물없던 태도로 말하자면, 마치 처음부터 없었던 양 싹 사라져 버렸다. 그는, 말하자면, 베옷을 걸치고 재를 뒤집어쓴 죄인처럼 우리 시중을 들었다.\n\n\"자, 코퍼필드.\" 둘만 남게 되자 스티어포스가 말했다. \"네가 뭘 하고 있는지, 어디로 가는지, 너에 관한 모든 걸 듣고 싶구나. 꼭 네가 내 소유물 같은 기분이 들거든.\" 그가 여전히 나에게 이런 관심을 품고 있다는 사실에 나는 기쁨으로 달아올라, 고모님이 내 앞에 놓인 이 작은 여행을 제안하셨다는 것과 그 여행이 어디로 향하는지를 들려주었다.\n\n\"그럼, 급할 게 없다면,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"나랑 같이 하이게이트 우리 집으로 가서 하루 이틀 묵고 가. 우리 어머니가 마음에 들 거야 — 어머니는 내 얘기라면 좀 우쭐대고 장황하시지만, 그쯤은 너도 봐 줄 수 있을 테고 — 어머니도 너를 마음에 들어 하실 거야.\"\n\n\"네가 친절하게 확신하는 만큼 나도 확신할 수 있으면 좋겠어.\" 나는 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"오!\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"나를 좋아하는 사람이라면 누구든 어머니에게 인정받을 자격이 있고, 그건 어김없이 인정받게 돼 있어.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 귀여움을 받겠는걸.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"좋았어!\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"와서 증명해 봐. 한두 시간쯤 명소들을 구경하자고 — 너처럼 신선한 친구한테 그것들을 보여 주는 것도 제법 괜찮은 일이야, 코퍼필드 — 그러고 나서 역마차를 타고 하이게이트로 가는 거야.\"\n\n이게 꿈이 아닌가 싶었다. 곧 깨어나 보면 다시 사십사 호실에, 커피룸의 외딴 칸막이 자리에, 그 허물없던 종업원이 있을 것만 같았다. 고모님께 편지를 써서, 흠모하던 옛 학교 친구를 운 좋게 만난 일과 그의 초대를 받아들였다는 소식을 전한 뒤, 우리는 삯마차를 타고 나가 파노라마며 다른 구경거리를 보았고, 박물관을 거닐었는데, 거기서 나는 스티어포스가 온갖 분야에 대해 얼마나 많이 알고 있는지, 그러면서도 자기 지식을 얼마나 대수롭지 않게 여기는지 새삼 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"넌 대학에서 높은 학위를 받을 거야, 스티어포스.\" 내가 말했다. \"아직 안 받았다면 말이지. 학교에서도 너를 자랑스러워할 충분한 이유가 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"내가 학위를 받는다고!\" 스티어포스가 소리쳤다. \"천만에! 사랑하는 데이지 — 너를 데이지라고 불러도 괜찮겠지?\"\n\n\"전혀 상관없어!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"역시 좋은 녀석이야! 사랑하는 데이지.\" 스티어포스가 웃으며 말했다. \"나는 그런 식으로 두각을 나타내고 싶은 마음도, 그럴 뜻도 조금도 없어. 내 목적에 필요한 만큼은 이미 다 했어. 나는 이대로도 나 자신에게 충분히 버거운 길동무거든.\"\n\n\"하지만 명성이—\" 내가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"이 낭만적인 데이지 녀석아!\" 스티어포스가 한결 더 신나게 웃으며 말했다. \"왜 내가 머리 둔한 녀석들 한 무더기가 입을 헤벌리고 두 손을 치켜들게 하려고 애를 써야 하지? 그런 건 다른 사람한테나 가서 해 보라지. 명성이라면 그 사람 몫이고, 얼마든지 가지라고 해.\"\n\n이렇게 큰 실수를 저질렀다는 게 부끄러워서, 나는 얼른 화제를 바꾸게 되어 다행이었다. 다행히 그건 어렵지 않은 일이었는데, 스티어포스는 늘 그 사람 특유의 무심함과 가벼움으로 이 화제에서 저 화제로 옮겨 다닐 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n구경을 마친 뒤 점심을 먹었고, 짧은 겨울 하루가 어찌나 빨리 저물던지, 역마차가 우리를 태운 채 언덕 꼭대기 하이게이트의 오래된 벽돌집 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때는 이미 땅거미가 지고 있었다. 나이가 그리 많지는 않으나 지긋한 한 부인이, 자긍심 어린 자태와 아름다운 얼굴로 우리가 내릴 때 문간에 서 있었다. 그녀는 스티어포스를 \"내 사랑하는 제임스\"라고 부르며 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 스티어포스는 그 부인에게 나를 어머니라고 소개했고, 그녀는 나에게 위엄 있는 환영의 인사를 건넸다.\n\n품격 있고 고풍스러운 집으로, 아주 조용하고 정돈되어 있었다. 내 방 창문에서는 런던 전체가 멀리 거대한 수증기처럼 누워 있고, 그 사이로 군데군데 불빛이 깜박이는 게 보였다. 옷을 갈아입으면서, 막 지핀 난롯불이 탁탁 튀며 타오르는 동안, 묵직한 가구들과 액자에 든 수예품들 (짐작건대 스티어포스의 어머니가 소녀 시절에 만든 것이리라), 그리고 머리에 분을 바르고 코르셋을 한 부인들을 크레용으로 그린 그림 몇 점이 벽 위에서 어른어른 떠올랐다 사라지는 것을 흘끗 볼 짬밖에 없었는데, 그때 저녁 식사하러 오라는 부름을 받았다.\n\n식당에는 또 다른 부인이 있었는데, 호리호리하고 작은 몸집에, 가무잡잡하고 보기에 그리 곱지는 않으나 어딘가 미인다운 구석도 있어서 내 주의를 끌었다. 어쩌면 그녀를 보리라고 예상하지 못했기 때문일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 내가 그녀의 맞은편에 앉게 되었기 때문일 수도 있으며, 어쩌면 그녀에게 정말로 눈에 띄는 무언가가 있었기 때문일 수도 있다. 그녀는 검은 머리에 형형한 검은 눈을 지녔고, 야위었으며, 입술에 흉터가 하나 있었다. 오래된 흉터였다 — 차라리 흉이라고 부르는 편이 맞겠는데, 변색되지도 않았고 오래전에 아문 자국이었기 때문이다 — 한때 그녀의 입을 가르며 아래로 턱을 향해 내려간 상처였으나, 이제는 식탁 너머로는 거의 보이지 않았다. 다만 윗입술 위쪽으로, 그 입술 모양을 바꿔 놓은 부분만은 예외였다. 나는 속으로 그녀가 서른 살쯤 되었으며 결혼하고 싶어 하리라고 결론지었다. 그녀는 너무 오래 세놓인 집처럼 — 어딘가 조금 낡고 허름했으나 — 그래도 앞서 말했듯 미인다운 데가 있었다. 그 야윈 모습은 그녀 안에서 무언가를 태우는 불길이 빚어낸 결과 같았는데, 그 불길은 그녀의 퀭한 두 눈에서 출구를 찾고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 다틀 양으로 소개되었고, 스티어포스도 그의 어머니도 그녀를 로자라고 불렀다. 알고 보니 그녀는 이 집에 살았으며, 오래도록 스티어포스 부인의 말벗 노릇을 해 왔다. 내가 보기에 그녀는 하고 싶은 말을 결코 대놓고 하는 법이 없었다. 대신 슬쩍 암시를 던졌고, 그런 방식으로 그 말을 훨씬 더 크게 부풀렸다. 이를테면 스티어포스 부인이 진담 반 농담 반으로, 아들이 대학에서 방탕하게 지내지나 않을까 걱정이라고 말했을 때, 다틀 양은 이렇게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"어머, 정말요? 제가 얼마나 무지한지 아시잖아요, 그저 알고 싶어서 여쭤보는 거예요, 하지만 늘 그렇지 않나요? 그런 생활은 누구나 다 그렇게 이해하는 줄 알았는데 — 아닌가요?\" \"그게 아주 진지한 직업을 위한 수업이라는 뜻이라면 맞아, 로자.\" 스티어포스 부인이 다소 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다.\n\n\"오! 네! 정말 맞는 말씀이에요.\" 다틀 양이 받았다. \"하지만 그래도 그렇지 않나요? — 제가 틀렸다면 바로잡아 주세요 — 정말 그렇지 않나요?\"\n\n\"정말 뭐가 그렇다는 게냐?\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"오! 그렇지 않다는 말씀이군요!\" 다틀 양이 받았다. \"어머, 그 말씀을 들으니 정말 기뻐요! 이제 어떻게 해야 할지 알겠어요! 이게 바로 여쭤보는 것의 이점이지요. 앞으로는 그런 생활과 관련해서 낭비니 방탕이니 하는 말을 제 앞에서 하도록 절대 두지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"그래야 옳지.\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다. \"내 아들의 가정교사는 양심적인 신사야. 내가 아들을 전적으로 믿지 못한다 하더라도, 그분만큼은 믿을 거다.\"\n\n\"그러세요?\" 다틀 양이 말했다. \"어머나! 양심적이라고요? 정말로 양심적인가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 확신한다.\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"어쩜 그리 훌륭할까요!\" 다틀 양이 외쳤다. \"얼마나 마음 놓이는지! 정말로 양심적이라고요? 그렇다면 그분은 — 하지만 정말로 양심적이라면 당연히 그럴 리 없겠지요. 그럼 이제부터 저는 그분에 대해 아주 흐뭇한 생각을 품게 되겠네요. 그분이 정말로 양심적이라는 걸 확실히 아니까 제 마음속에서 얼마나 그분이 높아지는지 모르실 거예요!\"\n\n다틀 양은 온갖 문제에 대한 자기 견해며, 자기가 반대하는 모든 말에 대한 자기 나름의 정정을, 이런 식으로 슬며시 흘렸다. 때로는, 나도 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었는데, 상대가 스티어포스라 해도 그에 맞서면서까지 대단한 위력을 발휘했다. 그런 일이 저녁 식사가 끝나기 전에 한 차례 벌어졌다. 스티어포스 부인이 서퍽으로 내려가려는 내 계획에 대해 말을 꺼내자, 나는 스티어포스가 나와 함께 그곳에 가 주기만 한다면 얼마나 기쁠지 모르겠다고 무심코 말했고, 내가 옛 유모와 패거티 씨네 식구를 보러 가는 길이라고 설명하면서, 학창 시절에 그가 본 적 있는 그 뱃사람을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n\"오! 그 우락부락한 친구!\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"아들을 하나 데리고 있지 않았나?\"\n\n\"아니. 그건 조카였어.\" 내가 대답했다. \"그래도 아들처럼 거두었지. 아주 예쁘장한 어린 조카딸도 하나 있는데, 딸처럼 거두었어. 한마디로 그 사람 집은 — 아니, 차라리 배라고 해야겠지, 마른 땅 위에 놓인 배에서 사니까 — 그 사람의 너그러움과 친절을 입은 사람들로 가득해. 그 집안을 보면 너도 흐뭇할 거야.\"\n\n\"그럴까?\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"음, 그럴 것 같긴 해. 어떻게 할 수 있을지 알아봐야겠어. 그런 종류의 사람들이 한데 모여 있는 걸 보고, 그 틈에 끼어 보는 건 (너와 함께하는 여행의 즐거움은 말할 것도 없고, 데이지) 한번 가 볼 만한 일이겠어.\"\n\n새로운 기쁨의 희망에 내 가슴이 뛰었다. 그러나 그가 \"그런 종류의 사람들\"이라고 말한 그 어투를 두고, 줄곧 반짝이는 눈으로 우리를 지켜보던 다틀 양이 또다시 끼어들었다.\n\n\"어머, 하지만 정말요? 좀 말씀해 주세요. 그 사람들이 정말 그런가요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람들이 뭐가 그렇다는 거죠? 누가 뭐가 그렇다는 거예요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 종류의 사람들 말이에요. — 그들이 정말로 짐승이나 흙덩이 같은, 다른 부류의 존재들인가요? 너무너무 알고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"음, 그들과 우리 사이에는 꽤 넓은 간극이 있지.\" 스티어포스가 무심히 말했다. \"그들이 우리만큼 예민하기를 바랄 수는 없어. 그들의 섬세함은 쉬이 충격받거나 상처 입을 만한 게 아니거든. 아마 놀랄 만큼 덕성스럽긴 할 거야 — 적어도 어떤 사람들은 그렇게 우기지. 나도 그걸 반박할 생각은 전혀 없고 — 하지만 그들은 그리 고운 천성을 지니지 못했고, 거칠고 투박한 살갗처럼 쉬이 다치지 않는다는 걸 오히려 고맙게 여겨야 할 거야.\"\n\n\"정말요!\" 다틀 양이 말했다. \"어머, 글쎄요, 그 말을 들은 것만큼 기뻤던 적이 또 있었나 모르겠어요. 어쩜 그리 위안이 되는지! 그들이 고통받을 때 아무것도 못 느낀다는 걸 알게 되니 얼마나 기쁜지! 가끔 저는 그런 종류의 사람들 때문에 꽤 마음이 편치 않았는데, 이제 그런 생각은 깨끗이 떨쳐 버려야겠어요. 살다 보면 배우는군요. 솔직히 저도 의심이 들긴 했는데, 이제 다 풀렸어요. 몰랐던 걸 이제 알게 됐으니, 이것만 봐도 여쭤보는 게 이롭다는 걸 알 수 있죠 — 안 그래요?\"\n\n나는 스티어포스가 그런 말을 농담으로, 또는 다틀 양의 속내를 끌어내려고 했으리라 믿었다. 그래서 그녀가 가고 우리 둘만 난롯가에 앉게 되면 그가 그렇게 말하리라 기대했다. 그러나 그는 그저 그녀를 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"무척 영리한 사람이지, 안 그래?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"영리하다고! 그 여자는 뭐든 숫돌에 갖다 대고,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"갈아 버려. 지난 몇 년간 자기 얼굴과 몸매를 갈아 댄 것처럼 말이야. 끊임없이 갈아 대느라 자기 자신을 닳아 없애 버린 거지. 온통 날만 서 있어.\"\n\n\"입술에 난 저 흉터, 참 눈에 띄던데!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n스티어포스의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 그는 잠시 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"음, 사실은,\" 그가 받았다. \"내가 저렇게 만든 거야.\"\n\n\"불행한 사고로!\"\n\n\"아니야. 나는 어린 소년이었고, 저 여자가 나를 약 올렸지. 그래서 망치를 던졌어. 어지간히 될성부른 어린 천사였던 셈이지!\" 나는 이런 가슴 아픈 화제를 건드린 게 몹시 미안했으나, 이제는 소용없는 일이었다.\n\n\"보다시피 저 여자는 그 후로 줄곧 저 자국을 지니고 있어.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그리고 무덤에 들어가서도 지니고 있을 거야, 언젠가 무덤에서 쉴 수나 있다면 말이지 — 하긴 저 여자가 어디서든 쉴 수 있으리라곤 도무지 믿기지 않지만. 저 여자는 우리 아버지의 사촌뻘 되는 어떤 분의, 어미 없는 자식이었어. 그분이 어느 날 돌아가셨지. 그때 과부였던 우리 어머니가 말벗 삼으려고 저 여자를 이리로 데려왔어. 저 여자한테는 제 몫으로 이천 파운드쯤이 있는데, 해마다 그 이자를 아껴 모아서 원금에 보태지. 자, 이게 로자 다틀 양의 내력이야.\"\n\n\"그래도 저분은 분명 너를 오라비처럼 사랑할 거야?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"흥!\" 스티어포스가 난롯불을 바라보며 받아쳤다. \"어떤 오라비들은 그리 많이 사랑받지 못하고, 또 어떤 사랑은 — 자, 마음껏 들어, 코퍼필드! 들판의 데이지꽃을 위해 마시자, 너를 기리며. 그리고 수고도 길쌈도 하지 않는 골짜기의 백합을 위해서도 마시자, 나를 기리며 — 그건 나로서는 더 부끄러운 일이지만!\" 그의 이목구비에 번졌던 침울한 미소가 이 말을 명랑하게 내뱉는 사이 걷혔고, 그는 다시 그 솔직하고 사람을 사로잡는 본래의 모습으로 돌아왔다.\n\n차를 마시러 들어갔을 때, 나는 가슴 아픈 관심으로 그 흉터를 흘끔거리지 않을 수 없었다. 이내 나는 그것이 그녀 얼굴에서 가장 예민한 부분이며, 그녀의 얼굴이 창백해지면 그 자국이 가장 먼저 변해, 마치 불에 쬐어 드러난 투명 잉크 자국처럼, 칙칙한 납빛 줄로 길게 본래 길이만큼 늘어진다는 것을 알아챘다. 백개먼 주사위 던지기를 두고 그녀와 스티어포스 사이에 작은 말다툼이 일었는데 — 한순간 그녀가 분노의 폭풍에 휩싸였다고 느껴졌다. 그러자 그 자국이 마치 옛날 벽에 떠올랐던 글씨처럼 불쑥 솟아나는 것을 나는 보았다.\n\n스티어포스 부인이 아들에게 푹 빠져 있다는 걸 알게 된 건 나에게 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그녀는 아들 말고는 아무것도 말하거나 생각할 수 없는 사람 같았다. 그녀는 나에게 아들이 갓난아기였을 적의 그림을, 그 안에 아기 적 머리카락 몇 올을 넣은 로켓에 담아 보여 주었고, 내가 처음 알았을 무렵의 그림도 보여 주었으며, 지금 모습을 담은 그림은 가슴에 달고 있었다. 아들이 그동안 그녀에게 쓴 편지란 편지는 죄다 난롯가 자기 의자 옆 작은 장 안에 간직해 두었는데, 만일 그가 끼어들어 어머니를 어르고 달래 그 마음을 돌려놓지 않았더라면, 그녀는 그중 몇 통을 나에게 읽어 주었을 것이고, 나도 그 편지들을 무척 기쁘게 들었을 것이다.\n\n\"내 아들 말로는, 두 사람이 처음 알게 된 게 크리클 씨 학교에서였다더구나.\" 스티어포스 부인이, 저쪽 탁자에서 두 사람이 백개먼을 두는 동안 이쪽 탁자에서 나와 이야기를 나누며 말했다. \"그러고 보니 그 무렵 아들이, 그곳에서 마음에 든 자기보다 어린 학생 하나에 대해 말하던 게 기억나는구나. 하지만 짐작하겠지만, 네 이름까지는 내 기억에 남아 있지 않았단다.\"\n\n\"그 시절 그 친구는 저에게 참으로 너그럽고 고매했답니다, 부인.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 저에게는 그런 친구가 절실했지요. 그 친구가 없었다면 저는 완전히 짓눌려 버렸을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 애는 언제나 너그럽고 고매하지.\" 스티어포스 부인이 자랑스럽게 말했다.\n\n나는 진심으로 그 말에 동의했고, 그건 하느님도 아신다. 그녀도 내가 그런다는 것을 알았다. 그녀의 위엄 어린 태도가 나를 대할 때는 이미 누그러져 있었기 때문이다. 다만 그녀가 아들을 칭찬할 때만은 예외였는데, 그럴 때면 그 기색이 늘 도도해졌다.\n\n\"그 학교가 대체로 내 아들에게 어울리는 학교는 아니었지.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어울리기는커녕. 하지만 그 무렵에는 학교 선택보다도 더 중요하게 고려해야 할 특별한 사정들이 있었단다. 내 아들의 드높은 기상 탓에, 그 우월함을 알아보고 그 앞에 기꺼이 머리 숙일 만한 사람 밑에 두는 것이 바람직했는데, 우리는 그런 사람을 그곳에서 찾아냈던 거야.\"\n\n나는 그자를 아니까, 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았다. 그래도 그 때문에 그자를 더 경멸하지는 않았고, 오히려 스티어포스처럼 거역할 수 없는 사람에게 맞서지 않은 점에 조금이나마 면죄부를 줄 수 있다면, 그것은 그자의 한 가지 미덕이라고 여겼다.\n\n\"내 아들의 큰 재능은, 그곳에서 자발적인 경쟁심과 스스로에 대한 긍지로 한껏 북돋워졌단다.\" 그 다정한 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그 애는 어떤 속박에도 들고일어났을 테지. 그러나 그곳에서 자기가 그 세계의 군주임을 깨닫고는, 자기 지위에 걸맞은 사람이 되기로 도도하게 마음먹은 거야. 그 애다운 일이지.\"\n\n나는 온 마음과 영혼을 다해, 정말 그 친구다운 일이라고 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"그렇게 내 아들은, 제 뜻으로, 누가 시켜서가 아니라, 마음만 먹으면 언제든 어떤 경쟁자도 앞지를 수 있는 그 길로 나아간 거야.\" 그녀가 계속했다. \"내 아들이 그러는데, 코퍼필드 군, 자네가 그 애를 무척 따랐고, 어제 만났을 때 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 그 애에게 자네를 알렸다더구나. 내 아들이 그런 감정을 불러일으킨 것을 두고 놀란 척이라도 한다면 나는 가식적인 여자가 되겠지. 하지만 그 애의 됨됨이를 그토록 알아주는 사람이라면 누구든 나는 무심할 수가 없단다. 그러니 자네가 이곳에 와 주어 무척 기쁘고, 단언컨대 그 애가 자네에게 남다른 우정을 느끼고 있으니, 자네는 그 애의 보살핌에 기댈 수 있을 거야.\"\n\n다틀 양은 무슨 일이든 그랬듯 백개먼도 열심히 두었다. 만일 내가 그녀를 게임판 앞에서 처음 보았더라면, 세상 다른 무엇도 아닌 오로지 그 놀이에 빠져 몸이 야위고 눈이 커졌으리라 짐작했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 이 대화 가운데 단 한마디라도 놓쳤다거나, 내가 더없는 기쁨으로 그 말을 받아들이며 스티어포스 부인의 신뢰에 우쭐해져 캔터베리를 떠난 이래 가장 어른이 된 듯한 기분을 느낄 때 내 표정 하나라도 빠뜨렸다면, 그건 내가 크게 잘못 본 것이리라.\n\n저녁이 꽤 깊어, 유리잔과 술병을 얹은 쟁반이 들어왔을 때, 스티어포스는 난롯가에서 나와 함께 시골에 내려가는 일을 진지하게 생각해 보겠노라 약속했다. 서두를 것 없다고 그가 말했다. 일주일쯤 뒤면 된다고. 그의 어머니도 인심 좋게 같은 말을 했다. 이야기를 나누는 동안 그는 한 번 이상 나를 데이지라고 불렀는데, 그 바람에 다틀 양이 또다시 끼어들었다.\n\n\"하지만 정말요, 코퍼필드 군.\" 그녀가 물었다. \"그게 별명인가요? 그리고 왜 그 애가 자네에게 그런 별명을 붙였죠? 그게 — 그러니까 — 자네가 어리고 순진하다고 여겨서인가요? 저는 이런 일에 어찌나 둔한지.\"\n\n나는 얼굴을 붉히며, 아마 그래서일 거라고 대답했다.\n\n\"오!\" 다틀 양이 말했다. \"이제 그걸 알게 되어 기뻐요! 알고 싶어서 여쭤보는 건데, 알게 되니 기쁘네요. 그 애는 자네를 어리고 순진하다 여기고, 그래서 자네가 그 애의 벗인 거군요. 어머, 참 흐뭇한 일이에요!\"\n\n이 일이 있고 얼마 안 되어 그녀는 잠자리에 들었고, 스티어포스 부인도 물러갔다. 스티어포스와 나는 난롯가에서 반 시간쯤 머뭇거리며 옛 세일럼 하우스의 트래들스며 다른 친구들 모두에 대해 이야기하다가, 함께 이 층으로 올라갔다. 스티어포스의 방은 내 방 옆이었는데, 나는 한번 들여다보러 들어갔다. 그곳은 안락함 그 자체를 그린 그림 같았다. 어머니의 손으로 수놓은 안락의자며 방석이며 발받침이 가득했고, 그 방을 완벽하게 만드는 데 보탬이 될 만한 것이라면 무엇 하나 빠진 게 없었다. 그리고 마지막으로, 벽에 걸린 초상화 속 그녀의 아름다운 이목구비가 사랑하는 아들을 내려다보고 있었는데, 마치 제 그림이나마 아들이 잠든 동안 지켜보는 것이 그녀에게 무언가 의미라도 되는 듯했다.\n\n그 무렵 내 방의 난롯불은 제법 환히 타오르고 있었고, 창마다 그리고 침대 둘레마다 휘장이 드리워져 방이 무척 아늑해 보였다. 나는 난로 앞 큰 의자에 앉아 내 행복을 곱씹었고, 한동안 그 흐뭇한 사색에 잠겨 있었는데, 그러다 문득 다틀 양의 초상이 벽난로 위에서 나를 형형하게 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했다.\n\n깜짝 놀랄 만큼 닮은 초상이었고, 따라서 어쩔 수 없이 깜짝 놀랄 만한 표정을 짓고 있었다. 화가가 그 흉터를 그려 넣은 것은 아니었으나, 내가 그것을 그려 넣었다. 그래서 그 자국이 거기에 있어, 떠올랐다 사라지기를 거듭했다. 어떤 때는 저녁 식사 자리에서 보았던 대로 윗입술에만 머물러 있다가, 어떤 때는 그녀가 격앙되었을 때 보았던 대로 망치가 낸 상처의 온 길이를 그대로 드러냈다.\n\n나는 짜증스럽게도, 왜 하필 그녀를 다른 데 두지 않고 내 방에 떠넘겼을까 의아했다. 그녀에게서 벗어나려고 나는 얼른 옷을 벗고, 불을 끄고, 잠자리에 들었다. 그러나 잠이 드는 순간에도, 그녀가 여전히 거기서 \"그런데 정말 그런가요? 알고 싶어요\" 하고 바라보고 있다는 것을 잊을 수가 없었다. 그리고 밤중에 깨어났을 때, 나는 꿈속에서 온갖 사람들에게 그게 정말 그런지 아닌지를 불안하게 묻고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다 — 무슨 뜻으로 그러는지도 모른 채.\n\n# CHAPTER 21. LITTLE EM’LY\n\n그 집에는 하인이 하나 있었는데, 내가 알기로 평소 스티어포스를 따라다니는 사내로, 대학 시절부터 그의 시중을 들기 시작했으며, 겉모습이 점잖음의 본보기 같았다. 그 신분에서 그보다 더 점잖아 보이는 사람은 일찍이 없었으리라 싶다. 그는 말수가 적고, 발소리가 부드러웠으며, 몸가짐이 무척 조용하고, 깍듯하고, 빈틈없이 살피며, 필요할 때면 언제나 가까이 있고, 필요 없을 때는 결코 곁에 얼씬하지 않았다. 그러나 그가 사람들의 존중을 받는 가장 큰 까닭은 바로 그 점잖음이었다. 그는 잘 휘는 얼굴이 아니라 오히려 뻣뻣한 목을 가졌고, 다소 빳빳하고 매끈한 머리에는 짧은 머리카락이 양옆으로 달라붙어 있었으며, 말투가 부드러운 데다가, S 음을 어찌나 또렷하게 속삭이는 묘한 버릇이 있던지 다른 어떤 사람보다도 그 글자를 더 자주 쓰는 것처럼 들렸다. 하지만 그는 자기가 지닌 온갖 특이한 점을 죄다 점잖아 보이게 만들었다. 코가 거꾸로 붙어 있었다 해도 그는 그것마저 점잖아 보이게 만들었을 것이다. 그는 점잖음의 분위기로 자기 둘레를 감싸고, 그 안에서 든든하게 걸어 다녔다. 그가 무언가 잘못을 저질렀으리라고 의심하기란 거의 불가능에 가까웠다. 그만큼 그는 철저하게 점잖았다. 그에게 하인 제복을 입힐 생각을 할 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그만큼 그는 더없이 점잖았다. 그에게 천한 일을 떠맡긴다는 것은, 더없이 점잖은 사람의 감정에 까닭 없는 모욕을 안기는 짓이나 마찬가지였을 것이다. 그래서 나는 알아차렸는데 — 이 집 하녀들은 그 사실을 어찌나 본능으로 꿰뚫고 있던지, 그런 일은 늘 자기들이 직접 했고, 대개는 그가 식료품실 난롯가에서 신문을 읽는 동안 해치웠다.\n\n그토록 자기 안에 갇혀 사는 사람을 나는 본 적이 없다. 그러나 그 자질도, 그가 지닌 다른 온갖 자질과 마찬가지로, 그를 더욱 점잖아 보이게만 할 뿐이었다. 그의 세례명을 아무도 모른다는 사실조차도 그의 점잖음의 한 부분을 이루는 듯했다. 그가 세상에 알려진 성, 리티머에 대해서는 아무도 트집을 잡을 수 없었다. 피터라면 교수형을 당했을 수도 있고, 톰이라면 유배를 갔을 수도 있겠지만, 리티머는 완벽하게 점잖았다.\n\n추상으로 따진 점잖음이라는 것의 거룩한 성질 탓이었지 싶은데, 나는 이 사내 앞에 있으면 유난히 어리게 느껴졌다. 그 자신이 몇 살인지는 짐작조차 할 수 없었는데 — 그 점 역시 같은 이유로 그의 신용을 높여 주었다. 점잖음의 차분함 속에서라면 그는 쉰 살이라 해도, 서른 살이라 해도 어울렸을 테니까.\n\n아침이면 리티머는 내가 일어나기도 전에 내 방에 들어와, 그 책망하는 듯한 면도용 물을 가져다 놓고 옷을 챙겨 두었다. 내가 침대에서 커튼을 걷고 밖을 내다보면, 그는 일월의 동풍에도 아랑곳없이 점잖음의 한결같은 온도 속에서, 입김 한 번 서리지 않고 서서, 내 장화를 발레의 첫 동작처럼 좌우로 가지런히 세워 놓고, 내 외투를 갓난아기 뉘듯 눕히며 거기에 앉은 먼지 티끌을 후후 불어 내고 있었다.\n\n내가 좋은 아침이라 인사하며 몇 시냐고 물으면, 그는 주머니에서 내가 본 것 가운데 가장 점잖은 회중시계를 꺼내, 용수철이 너무 활짝 열리지 않도록 엄지로 누른 채, 마치 신탁을 내리는 굴이라도 들여다보듯 시계판을 살피더니, 다시 탁 닫고는, 괜찮으시다면 여덟 시 반이라고 말했다.\n\n\"스티어포스 도련님께서 도련님이 잘 주무셨는지 들으시면 기뻐하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"고맙네.\" 내가 말했다. \"아주 잘 잤다네. 스티어포스 군은 잘 있나?\"\n\n\"감사합니다, 도련님. 스티어포스 도련님은 그런대로 잘 계십니다.\" 이것도 그의 특징 가운데 하나였다 — 최상급은 절대 쓰지 않는다는 것. 언제나 서늘하고 차분한 중간이었다.\n\n\"제가 더 해 드릴 영광스러운 일이 있겠습니까, 도련님? 예고 종은 아홉 시에 울립니다. 식구분들은 아홉 시 반에 아침을 드십니다.\"\n\n\"없네, 고맙네.\"\n\n\"제가 도리어 감사드립니다, 도련님, 괜찮으시다면요.\" 그러고는 침대 곁을 지나며 나를 바로잡은 것에 대한 사과인 양 고개를 살짝 숙이고는, 마치 내가 목숨이 달린 달콤한 잠에 막 빠져들기라도 한 듯 더없이 조심스럽게 문을 닫으며 나갔다.\n\n매일 아침 우리는 한 치도 더하지 않고 한 치도 덜하지 않은, 꼭 이만큼의 대화를 나누었다. 그런데도, 전날 밤 스티어포스와 어울리며, 혹은 스티어포스 부인의 신임을 받으며, 혹은 다틀 양과 이야기를 나누며 내 자신을 한껏 벗어나 더 어른스러운 나이로 나아갔다 한들, 이 더없이 점잖은 사내 앞에 서기만 하면, 우리네 군소 시인들이 노래하듯 나는 어김없이 '다시 소년'이 되고 말았다.\n\n그는 우리를 위해 말을 구해 왔고, 모르는 게 없는 스티어포스는 나에게 승마를 가르쳐 주었다. 그는 우리에게 펜싱용 검을 마련해 주었고, 스티어포스는 펜싱을 가르쳐 주었다 — 권투 장갑도 마련해 주어, 나는 같은 스승에게서 권투 솜씨를 늘리기 시작했다. 이런 기술에서 스티어포스가 나를 풋내기로 여기는 것은 조금도 거리낄 게 없었으나, 점잖은 리티머 앞에서 내 서투른 솜씨를 드러내는 것만은 도무지 견딜 수 없었다. 리티머 자신이 그런 기술을 안다고 믿을 만한 근거는 없었다. 그는 자기의 점잖은 속눈썹 하나 떨어 보이는 일 없이, 그런 짐작을 하게 만든 적이 없었다. 그런데도 우리가 연습할 때 그가 곁에 있기만 하면, 나는 내가 세상에서 가장 풋내기에다 가장 미숙한 인간처럼 느껴졌다.\n\n내가 이 사내에 대해 이렇게 시시콜콜 이야기하는 것은, 그가 그 무렵 나에게 유난히 강한 인상을 남겼기 때문이고, 또 그 뒤에 벌어진 일 때문이기도 하다.\n\n그 한 주는 더없이 즐겁게 흘러갔다. 짐작하다시피, 나처럼 넋이 나간 사람에게 시간은 쏜살같이 지나갔다. 그런데도 그 한 주는 스티어포스를 더 깊이 알고 천 가지 면에서 그를 더 우러르게 할 기회를 어찌나 많이 안겨 주었던지, 한 주가 끝날 무렵에는 훨씬 오래 그와 함께 지낸 것만 같았다. 그가 나를 장난감처럼 다루는 그 호기로운 방식은, 그가 취할 수 있었던 다른 어떤 태도보다도 나에게 흐뭇했다. 그것은 우리의 오랜 사이를 떠올리게 했고, 그 사이의 자연스러운 연장처럼 느껴졌으며, 그가 조금도 변하지 않았음을 보여 주었고, 내 장점을 그의 장점과 견주거나 우리 우정에 대한 내 몫을 무슨 대등한 잣대로 재면서 품었을 법한 어떤 거북함도 덜어 주었다. 무엇보다도 그것은 그가 다른 누구에게도 보이지 않는, 친근하고 거리낌 없고 다정한 태도였다. 학교에서 그가 나를 다른 누구와도 다르게 대했듯이, 나는 그가 인생에서도 다른 어떤 친구와도 다르게 나를 대한다고 기쁘게 믿었다. 나는 내가 다른 어떤 친구보다도 그의 마음에 더 가깝다고 믿었고, 내 마음은 그에 대한 애착으로 따뜻하게 데워졌다. 그는 나와 함께 시골로 가기로 마음을 굳혔고, 마침내 우리가 떠날 날이 왔다. 처음에 그는 리티머를 데려갈지 말지 망설였으나, 집에 두고 가기로 정했다. 제 처지가 어떻든 만족하는 그 점잖은 사내는, 우리를 런던까지 데려다줄 작은 마차에 우리 여행 가방을 마치 백 년 세월의 충격에도 끄떡없도록 만들기라도 하듯 차곡차곡 실었고, 내가 조심스레 건넨 사례를 더없이 평온하게 받았다.\n\n우리는 스티어포스 부인과 다틀 양에게 작별을 고했는데, 내 쪽에서는 거듭 고마움을 전했고, 그 헌신적인 어머니 쪽에서는 갖은 친절을 베풀었다. 마지막으로 내 눈에 들어온 것은 흐트러짐 없는 리티머의 눈이었다. 내가 보기에 그 눈에는, 너는 정말이지 아주 어리다는 말없는 확신이 가득 차 있었다.\n\n그토록 상서롭게 옛 정든 곳으로 돌아가면서 내가 무엇을 느꼈는지는 굳이 적어 보려 하지 않겠다. 우리는 우편 마차를 타고 내려갔다. 기억하건대 나는 야머스의 체면을 두고도 어찌나 마음을 쓰던지, 마차를 타고 어두운 거리를 지나 여관으로 가는 길에 스티어포스가 자기가 보기엔 여긴 괜찮은, 별나고, 외진 구석 같은 곳이라고 말하자 몹시 기뻤다. 도착하자마자 우리는 잠자리에 들었고 (그 문을 지나칠 때 내 옛 친구 돌핀 여관과 관련된 더러운 신발 한 켤레와 각반이 눈에 띄었다), 아침에는 늦게 식사를 했다. 기분이 한껏 들떠 있던 스티어포스는 내가 일어나기도 전에 바닷가를 거닐었고, 이곳 뱃사람의 절반과 안면을 텄다고 했다. 게다가 멀리서 굴뚝에서 연기가 피어오르는, 틀림없이 페거티 씨의 바로 그 집이 분명한 곳을 보았다면서, 안으로 쑥 들어가 자기가 몰라보게 자란 나라고 우겨 볼까 하는 생각이 굴뚝같았노라고 말했다.\n\n\"언제 거기로 나를 데려갈 생각인가, 데이지?\" 그가 말했다. \"나야 자네 뜻대로 따르겠네. 자네가 알아서 정하게.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 오늘 저녁이 좋을 것 같아, 스티어포스. 다들 난롯가에 둘러앉아 있을 때 말이야. 아늑할 때 자네가 봤으면 좋겠어. 참 묘한 곳이거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 하지!\" 스티어포스가 받았다. \"오늘 저녁.\"\n\n\"우리가 왔다는 걸 미리 알리지는 않을 거야, 알겠나.\" 내가 신이 나서 말했다. \"깜짝 놀라게 해 줘야지.\"\n\n\"오, 물론이지! 깜짝 놀라게 하지 않으면,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"무슨 재미가 있겠나. 원주민들이 천연 그대로 있는 꼴을 한번 보자고.\"\n\n\"비록 그 사람들이 자네가 말했던 그런 부류이긴 하지만 말이야.\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"아하! 뭐라고! 자네 내가 로자랑 옥신각신한 걸 기억하는군, 그렇지?\" 그가 휙 눈길을 던지며 외쳤다. \"그 계집애, 빌어먹을, 나는 그 애가 반쯤 무섭다네. 나한테는 도깨비 같단 말이야. 하지만 그 애 얘긴 됐고. 자, 자네 이제 뭘 할 텐가? 유모를 만나러 가겠지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"응, 그래.\" 내가 말했다. \"무엇보다 먼저 페거티를 만나야지.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 스티어포스가 시계를 보며 대답했다. \"내가 자네를 두어 시간쯤 실컷 눈물바다에 내맡겨 줄까. 그 정도면 넉넉한가?\"\n\n나는 웃으며, 그 시간이면 그 일을 다 치를 수 있을 것 같다고, 하지만 자네도 와야 한다고 대답했다. 가 보면 자네의 명성이 이미 자네보다 먼저 당도해 있을 테고, 자네도 나만큼이나 대단한 인물이라는 걸 알게 될 테니까.\n\n\"자네가 가라는 데면 어디든 가겠네.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"자네가 시키는 거면 뭐든 하겠어. 어디로 가면 되는지 일러 주게. 두 시간 뒤에 자네가 원하는 모습으로, 감상적이든 익살맞든, 나타나겠네.\"\n\n나는 그에게 블런더스톤을 비롯한 여러 곳을 다니는 짐마차꾼 바키스 씨의 거처를 찾아오는 길을 시시콜콜 일러 준 다음, 이렇게 약속하고 혼자 나섰다. 살갗을 에는 듯 상쾌한 바람이 불었고, 땅은 메말랐으며, 바다는 또렷하고 맑았다. 햇살은 온기는 그리 많지 않아도 빛만은 넉넉히 뿌리고 있었고, 모든 것이 싱그럽고 생기가 넘쳤다. 나 자신도 그곳에 있다는 기쁨에 어찌나 싱그럽고 생기가 넘치던지, 거리에서 사람들을 붙잡고 악수라도 청하고 싶을 지경이었다.\n\n물론 거리는 작아 보였다. 어릴 때만 보았던 거리란 다시 가 보면 으레 그런 법이지 싶다. 그러나 나는 그 거리에서 무엇 하나 잊지 않았고, 무엇 하나 바뀐 것을 찾지 못했다 — 오머 씨의 가게에 이르기 전까지는. 예전에 그냥 '오머'라고만 적혀 있던 자리에 이제는 '오머와 조램'이라 적혀 있었으나, '포목상, 양복점, 잡화상, 장의용품 일체' 운운하는 문구는 옛날 그대로였다.\n\n길 건너편에서 이 글귀를 읽고 나자, 내 발걸음은 어찌나 자연스럽게 가게 문 쪽으로 향하던지, 나는 길을 건너 안을 들여다보았다. 가게 안쪽에 어여쁜 여인이 어린 아기를 품에 안고 어르고 있었고, 또 다른 어린 녀석 하나가 그녀의 앞치마에 매달려 있었다. 미니인지, 미니의 아이들인지 알아보기는 조금도 어렵지 않았다. 거실로 통하는 유리문은 열려 있지 않았으나, 마당 건너편 작업장에서는 마치 한 번도 멈춘 적 없다는 듯 그 옛 가락이 희미하게 들려왔다.\n\n\"오머 씨 댁에 계신가요?\" 내가 들어서며 말했다. \"계시다면 잠깐 뵙고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 네, 손님, 안에 계세요.\" 미니가 말했다. \"이 날씨가 아버지 천식에 안 좋아서 바깥에는 못 나가시거든요. 조, 가서 할아버지 불러 오렴!\"\n\n그녀의 앞치마를 붙잡고 있던 어린 녀석이 어찌나 우렁차게 소리를 지르던지, 제 소리에 제가 부끄러워져서는 어머니 치맛자락에 얼굴을 파묻었고, 어머니는 그 모습을 흐뭇하게 바라보았다. 묵직하게 헐떡이며 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬는 소리가 우리 쪽으로 다가오더니, 이내 오머 씨가 내 앞에 섰다. 예전보다 숨은 더 가빠졌으되 얼굴은 그리 늙어 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"어서 오십시오, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"무엇을 도와드릴까요, 손님?\" \"괜찮으시다면 저와 악수나 한번 해 주십시오, 오머 씨.\" 내가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"예전에 저에게 무척 잘해 주셨는데, 그때 제가 고마운 줄도 모르는 것처럼 굴었던 것 같아서요.\"\n\n\"내가 그랬던가요?\" 노인이 받았다. \"그렇다니 반갑소만, 언제였는지는 기억이 안 나는구려. 정말 나였소?\"\n\"틀림없습니다.\"\n\n\"내 기억력도 이젠 내 숨처럼 짧아진 모양이오.\" 오머 씨가 나를 바라보며 고개를 가로저었다. \"도무지 손님이 기억나질 않으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"저를 마중하러 역마차까지 나와 주셨던 거, 제가 여기서 아침을 먹었던 거, 그리고 우리가 다 같이 블런더스톤까지 마차를 타고 갔던 거 기억 안 나세요? 어르신하고, 저하고, 조램 부인하고, 조램 씨도요 — 그때는 부인의 남편이 아니었지만요.\"\n\n\"아니, 이런, 세상에!\" 오머 씨가 깜짝 놀라 기침을 한바탕 쏟아낸 끝에 외쳤다. \"설마! 미니야, 얘야, 기억나니? 저런, 그래, 그 일행에 부인이 한 분 계셨던 것 같은데, 아닌가?\"\n\n\"제 어머니셨지요.\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"옳—거니,\" 오머 씨가 집게손가락으로 내 조끼를 톡 건드리며 말했다. \"그리고 어린 아기도 하나 있었지! 일행이 둘이었어. 그 어린 일행은 그 다른 일행과 함께 누였지. 물론 저기 블런더스톤에서 말이오. 저런, 저런! 그래, 그동안 잘 지냈소?\"\n\n아주 잘 지냈다고, 어르신도 그러셨기를 바란다고 고마움을 전했다.\n\n\"오! 뭐 투덜댈 거야 없지요.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"숨이 짧아지긴 하는데, 사람이 늙는다고 숨이 길어지는 법은 좀처럼 없으니까. 오는 대로 받아들이고, 그저 최대한 누리는 거지. 그게 제일 좋은 방법 아니겠소?\"\n\n오머 씨는 웃다가 또 기침을 했고, 이제는 우리 곁에 바짝 다가서서 막내를 계산대 위에 올려 어르고 있던 딸이 그가 기침 발작에서 벗어나도록 거들었다.\n\n\"저런!\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그래, 옳거니. 일행이 둘이었지! 그게 말이오, 글쎄 그 마차 길에서, 믿거나 말거나, 우리 미니가 조램과 혼인할 날을 잡았다오. '날을 정하시지요, 어르신.' 조램이 그러기에. '네, 그래요, 아버지.' 미니가 그러더군. 그래서 이제 그 사람도 이 일을 함께 하게 됐지. 그리고 자, 보시오! 막내요!\"\n\n미니가 웃으며 관자놀이께로 곱게 빗어 넘긴 머리를 쓰다듬는 사이, 아버지는 계산대 위에서 어르고 있던 아이의 손에 자기 통통한 손가락 하나를 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"물론, 일행이 둘이었지!\" 오머 씨가 옛일을 돌이키며 고개를 끄덕였다. \"바—로 그랬다오! 그리고 조램이 지금 이 순간에도, 은못을 박은 회색 관을 짜고 있는데, 그게 이만한 치수는 아니고\" — 계산대 위에서 어르던 아이의 치수를 가리키며 — \"넉넉히 두 치는 더 크지. 뭐라도 좀 드시겠소?\"\n\n고맙지만 사양했다.\n\n\"가만 보자.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"바키스, 그 짐마차꾼의 아내 말이오 — 페거티, 그 뱃사람의 누이 — 손님네 집안과 무슨 인연이 있었지? 거기서 일했던 게 분명한데?\"\n\n내가 그렇다고 대답하자 그는 무척 흡족해했다.\n\n\"이러다 내 숨도 다시 길어지지 싶소. 기억력이 이렇게 좋아지는 걸 보니.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 말이오, 손님, 그 사람의 어린 친척 하나가 도제로 우리 가게에 와 있는데, 옷 짓는 솜씨의 안목이 어찌나 곱던지 — 정말이지 영국 안에 그 애를 따를 공작 부인이 있을까 싶소.\"\n\n\"꼬마 에밀리 말씀인가요?\" 나도 모르게 입에서 나왔다.\n\n\"에밀리가 그 애 이름이오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 꼬마는 꼬마지. 한데 믿거나 말거나, 그 애 얼굴이 어찌나 제 빛깔이 곱던지 이 동네 여자들 절반이 그 애한테 이를 갈고 있다오.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 아버지!\" 미니가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 오머 씨가 나에게 눈짓을 하며 말했다. \"너를 두고 하는 말은 아니다만, 야머스 여자들 절반이 — 아니! 오 마일 둘레의 여자들까지 — 그 애한테 이를 갈고 있다는 말이다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 애가 제 분수에 맞게 처신했어야지요, 아버지.\" 미니가 말했다. \"수군댈 빌미를 주지 말았어야지요. 그랬으면 사람들이 그러지도 못했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그러지도 못했을 거라고, 얘야!\" 오머 씨가 받아쳤다. \"그러지도 못했을 거라니! 그게 네가 아는 세상사냐? 여자가 못 할 일이 어디 있느냐, 안 해야 마땅한 일이라도 — 더구나 다른 여자의 미모를 두고서야?\"\n\n이 험담 섞인 농담을 내뱉고 난 뒤, 나는 오머 씨가 정말 숨이 넘어가는 줄 알았다. 어찌나 기침을 해 대던지, 그리고 숨이 어찌나 끈질기게 그의 온갖 노력을 비껴가며 돌아오질 않던지, 나는 그의 머리가 계산대 뒤로 폭 꺼지고, 무릎께에 녹슨 듯한 리본 매듭을 단 그 작고 검은 반바지가 마지막 헛된 발버둥을 치며 바르르 떨려 올라오는 광경을 영락없이 보게 되리라 여겼다. 하지만 결국 그는 좀 나아졌다. 여전히 거칠게 헐떡이긴 했고 어찌나 기진했던지 가게 책상 걸상에 주저앉을 수밖에 없었지만.\n\n\"그러니까 말이오.\" 그가 머리를 훔치며 힘겹게 숨을 몰아쉬었다. \"그 애가 여기서 또래들과 그리 어울리질 않았소. 딱히 친한 동무나 벗을 두지도 않았고, 정인은 더 말할 것도 없지. 그러다 보니 에밀리가 귀부인 행세를 하고 싶어 한다는 심술궂은 소문이 돌았다오. 한데 내 생각엔, 그 애가 학교에서 이따금 한 말 탓에 그 소문이 주로 퍼진 듯하오 — 자기가 귀부인이라면 외삼촌을 위해 이러저러한 일을 해 주고 싶다 — 무슨 말인지 아시겠소? — 이러저러한 좋은 물건을 사 드리고 싶다, 뭐 그런 말 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말이지요, 오머 씨, 그 애가 저한테도 그런 말을 했답니다.\" 내가 진지하게 받았다. \"둘 다 어린아이였을 때 말이에요.\"\n\n오머 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 턱을 문질렀다. \"바로 그거요. 게다가 그 애는 아주 적은 것으로도, 보시오, 남들이 많이 들여서 차려입는 것보다 더 잘 차려입을 줄 알았으니, 그게 또 일을 거북하게 만들었지. 더구나 그 애는 좀 변덕스러운 데가 있었는데 — 내가 변덕스럽다고까지 말하겠소.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"제 마음을 제가 잘 모른달까 — 좀 응석받이였달까 — 처음엔 도무지 제 몸을 다잡질 못했지. 그 애를 두고 그 이상 험한 말이 나온 적은 없었지, 미니?\"\n\n\"네, 아버지.\" 조램 부인이 말했다. \"그게 제일 심한 거예요, 아마.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 애가 일자리를 얻었는데,\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"성마른 노부인의 말동무를 하는 자리였소. 한데 둘이 영 맞질 않아서, 그 애가 오래 못 붙어 있었지. 마침내 여기로 와서 삼 년 도제로 들어왔다오. 그게 거의 이 년이 지났는데, 그동안 둘도 없이 착한 애였소. 여섯 사람 몫은 되지! 미니, 그 애가 지금 여섯 사람 몫은 되지 않니?\"\n\n\"네, 아버지.\" 미니가 대답했다. \"제가 그 애를 깎아내렸다는 말은 절대 마세요!\"\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그래야지. 그러니 젊은 양반,\" 그가 잠시 턱을 더 문지른 뒤 덧붙였다. \"내가 숨이 짧은 데다 말까지 길다 여기지 않도록, 그 애 얘긴 이쯤이면 다 한 것 같소.\"\n\n에밀리 이야기를 할 때 두 사람이 낮은 목소리로 말하기에, 나는 그 애가 가까이 있으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 그래서 이제 그렇지 않으냐고 묻자, 오머 씨는 그렇다고 끄덕이며 거실 문 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다. 안을 살짝 들여다봐도 되겠냐고 다급하게 묻자 선뜻 허락이 떨어졌다. 유리문 너머로 보니, 그 애가 앉아서 일을 하고 있었다. 보니, 더없이 어여쁜 작은 아가씨가, 일찍이 내 어린 마음속을 들여다보았던 그 구름 한 점 없는 푸른 눈을, 곁에서 놀던 미니의 또 다른 아이에게 웃으며 돌리고 있었다. 그 환한 얼굴에는 내가 들은 말을 수긍할 만큼의 고집스러움이 어려 있었고, 옛날의 그 변덕스러운 수줍음도 적잖이 깃들어 있었다. 그러나 그 어여쁜 얼굴에는, 분명히 말하건대, 선함과 행복을 위한 것, 그리고 선하고 행복한 길 위에 있는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다.\n\n마당 건너에서 한 번도 멈춘 적 없는 듯 들려오던 그 가락은 — 아아! 그것은 결코 멈추지 않는 가락이었다 — 내내 나직이 박자를 두드리고 있었다.\n\n\"한번 들어가서,\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그 애한테 말이라도 건네 보지 않겠소? 들어가서 말을 거시오, 손님! 편하게 하시구려!\"\n\n그때 나는 너무 부끄러워서 그러지 못했다 — 그 애를 당황하게 할까 봐 두려웠고, 나 자신을 당황하게 할까 봐 그에 못지않게 두려웠다 — 그래서 그저 그 애가 저녁에 몇 시에 일을 마치는지를 알아 두어, 우리 방문 시각을 거기에 맞추기로 했다. 그러고는 오머 씨와 그의 어여쁜 딸과 그 어린아이들에게 작별을 고하고, 정든 페거티에게로 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n페거티는 거기, 타일 깔린 부엌에서, 저녁을 짓고 있었다! 내가 문을 두드리자마자 그녀는 문을 열더니, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 나는 미소를 띠고 그녀를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 답례로 미소를 보내지 않았다. 나는 그녀에게 편지 쓰는 일을 한 번도 그친 적이 없었으나, 우리가 직접 만나지 못한 지는 칠 년쯤 되었을 것이다.\n\n\"바키스 씨 댁에 계신가요, 아주머니?\" 내가 일부러 퉁명스레 말하는 척하며 물었다.\n\n\"댁에 계세요, 손님.\" 페거티가 받았다. \"한데 류머티즘으로 자리보전을 하고 계셔서요.\"\n\n\"요즘은 블런더스톤에 안 건너가시나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"몸이 성하면 가시죠.\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"아주머니는 거기 가 보신 적 있나요, 바키스 부인?\"\n\n그녀가 나를 더 유심히 바라보았고, 나는 그녀의 두 손이 잽싸게 서로를 향해 모아지는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"실은 거기 있는 어떤 집에 대해 여쭤볼 게 있어서요. 그 집을 뭐라고 부르더라 — 까마귀 둥지라고 했던가.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 한 걸음 뒤로 물러서더니, 마치 나를 막으려는 듯 머뭇머뭇 겁먹은 손길로 두 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"페거티!\" 내가 그녀에게 외쳤다.\n\n그녀가 \"내 귀여운 도련님!\" 하고 부르짖었고, 우리 둘은 그대로 울음을 터뜨리며 서로의 품에 꼭 끌어안겼다.\n\n그녀가 얼마나 호들갑을 떨었는지, 나를 두고 얼마나 웃고 또 울었는지, 얼마나 자랑스러워했는지, 얼마나 기뻐하고, 또 자기가 자랑이요 기쁨으로 삼았을 법한 그 아이를 한 번도 품에 다정히 안아 주지 못했다는 사실에 얼마나 슬퍼했는지는, 차마 다 적을 마음이 나지 않는다. 그녀의 감정에 화답하는 것이 어린애 같은 짓이라는 꺼림칙함은 조금도 들지 않았다. 단언컨대 나는 평생, 그날 아침만큼 마음껏 웃고 운 적이 없었다 — 그녀에게조차도.\n\n\"바키스가 무척 기뻐할 거예요.\" 페거티가 앞치마로 눈물을 훔치며 말했다. \"도련님 오신 게 연고를 몇 파인트씩 바르는 것보다 더 그이한테 약이 될 거예요. 가서 도련님 오셨다고 알려도 될까요? 올라가서 그이를 좀 봐 주시겠어요, 도련님?\"\n\n물론 그러겠노라 했다. 그러나 페거티는 마음먹은 만큼 쉬이 방을 나서지 못했는데, 문께에 이르러 나를 돌아볼 때마다 다시 돌아와 내 어깨에 기대 또 한 번 웃고 또 한 번 울었기 때문이다. 결국 일을 수월하게 하려고 나는 그녀와 함께 위층으로 올라갔고, 그녀가 바키스 씨에게 마음의 채비를 시키려고 몇 마디 하는 동안 잠깐 밖에서 기다린 뒤, 그 병자 앞에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n그는 그야말로 열렬하게 나를 맞았다. 류머티즘이 심해 악수는 할 수 없었으나, 그는 자기 나이트캡 꼭대기에 달린 술을 흔들어 달라고 청했고, 나는 더없이 다정하게 그것을 흔들어 주었다. 침대 옆에 앉자 그는, 다시 나를 태우고 블런더스톤 길을 모는 것 같은 기분이 들어 더없이 좋다고 했다. 그는 침대에 얼굴을 위로 향하고 누워 있었는데, 그 얼굴만 빼고는 어찌나 꽁꽁 덮여 있던지 마치 얼굴 하나만 달랑 있는 것처럼 보여서 — 흔히 그리는 천사 그림 같았다 — 내가 본 것 가운데 가장 우스꽝스러운 물건처럼 보였다.\n\n\"제가 짐마차에 써 붙였던 이름이 뭐였더라, 도련님?\" 바키스 씨가 류머티즘으로 굳은 얼굴에 느릿한 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"아! 바키스 씨, 우리 그 일을 두고 자못 진지한 이야기를 나눴었지요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"제가 오랫동안 마음이 있었지요, 도련님?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오랫동안 그러셨죠.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 후회 안 합니다.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. \"전에 도련님이 나한테 했던 말 기억하시오? 그 사람이 사과 파이를 다 굽고 요리도 도맡아 한다고 했던 거 말이오.\"\n\n\"네, 아주 잘 기억하지요.\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"그게 다 사실이었소.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. \"순무가 진짜인 것만큼이나 사실이었지. 그게 다 사실이었소.\" 바키스 씨가 자기로서는 유일한 강조 수단인 나이트캡을 끄덕이며 말했다. \"세금이 진짜인 것만큼이나 사실이었지. 그리고 그것들만큼 확실한 건 없잖소.\"\n\n바키스 씨는, 침대에 누워 곱씹은 이 결론에 내 동의를 구하기라도 하듯 나에게 눈길을 돌렸고, 나는 동의해 주었다.\n\n\"그것들만큼 확실한 건 없지.\" 바키스 씨가 되뇌었다. \"나처럼 가난한 사람은, 자리보전을 하고 누워 있으면 마음속으로 그걸 깨닫는 법이오. 나는 아주 가난한 사람이라오, 도련님!\"\n\n\"그렇다니 안타깝습니다, 바키스 씨.\"\n\n\"참으로 가난한 사람이지요, 정말이오.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n이때 그의 오른손이 이불 밑에서 느릿하고 힘없이 빠져나오더니, 정처 없고 미덥지 못한 손길로 침대 옆에 헐겁게 매여 있던 막대기 하나를 거머쥐었다. 이 도구로 한참을 이리저리 쑤셔 대는 사이 그의 얼굴에는 갖가지 산만한 표정이 떠올랐는데, 마침내 바키스 씨는 그 막대기로, 내내 한쪽 끝이 내 눈에 보였던 상자 하나를 쿡 찔렀다. 그러자 그의 얼굴이 차분해졌다.\n\n\"헌 옷가지요.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"돈이면 좋으련만, 도련님.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그러면 좋겠네요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"한데 그게 아니란 말이오.\" 바키스 씨가 두 눈을 있는 힘껏 크게 뜨며 말했다.\n\n나는 그 말이 정말 확실하다고 했고, 바키스 씨는 한결 부드러운 눈길을 아내에게 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람은 세상에서 가장 쓸모 있고 가장 착한 여자요, C. P. 바키스 말이오. 누가 C. P. 바키스한테 어떤 칭찬을 해도, 이 사람은 그 칭찬을 받아 마땅하고, 그 이상도 받을 만하지! 여보, 오늘은 손님이 오셨으니 저녁을 좀 잘 차려 주구려. 뭐 맛난 거 먹고 마실 거 말이오, 응?\"\n\n나는 나를 위한 이 불필요한 야단법석에 한사코 사양하려 했으나, 침대 맞은편에 선 페거티가 내가 그러지 않기를 몹시 바라는 눈치라 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"내가 어딘가에 돈을 좀 갖고 있긴 한데, 여보.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다. \"내가 좀 피곤해서. 당신하고 데이비드 도련님이 잠깐 한숨 자게 나가 주면, 깨서 한번 찾아보리다.\"\n\n우리는 이 청에 따라 방을 나섰다. 문밖에 나오자 페거티가 일러 주기를, 바키스 씨가 이제는 예전보다 '좀 더 인색해져서' 자기 돈주머니에서 동전 한 닢을 꺼낼 때마다 늘 똑같은 이 수법을 쓴다고 했다. 그리고 혼자 침대에서 기어 나와 그 망할 상자에서 돈을 꺼낼 때면 이루 말할 수 없는 고통을 견딘다고 했다. 과연 우리는 이내, 까치 같은 그 짓이 그의 마디마디를 쥐어짜는 통에 그가 더없이 처량하게 신음을 억누르는 소리를 들었다. 그러나 페거티의 눈에는 그를 향한 연민이 가득했어도, 그녀는 그의 너그러운 충동이 그에게 약이 될 테니 막지 않는 편이 낫다고 했다. 그래서 그는 다시 침대에 기어들 때까지 줄곧 신음을 토했다 — 의심할 바 없이 순교자의 고난을 겪으며. 그러고는 우리를 불러들여, 마치 개운한 잠에서 막 깬 척하면서 베개 밑에서 일 기니를 꺼내 보였다. 우리를 이렇게 감쪽같이 속여 넘긴 것과, 상자의 뚫을 수 없는 비밀을 지켜 낸 것에 대한 그의 흡족함은, 그가 겪은 온갖 고통에 대한 넉넉한 보상이 되는 듯했다.\n\n나는 스티어포스가 오리라고 페거티에게 미리 일러 두었고, 머지않아 그가 왔다. 나는 확신하건대, 페거티는 그가 자기에게 직접 은혜를 베푼 사람인지, 아니면 그저 나에게 좋은 친구인지를 구분하지 못했고, 어느 쪽이었더라도 더없는 고마움과 정성으로 그를 맞이했을 것이다. 그러나 그의 거리낌 없고 활기찬 쾌활함, 다정한 몸가짐, 잘생긴 외모, 누구에게든 제 마음대로 자신을 맞추는 타고난 재주, 그리고 마음만 먹으면 누구의 가슴속에서든 곧장 가장 관심 가는 대목으로 파고드는 솜씨가, 단 오 분 만에 그녀를 그에게 온통 사로잡았다. 나를 대하는 그의 태도만으로도 그녀의 마음을 얻기에 충분했을 것이다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 한데 어우러진 덕분에, 그가 그날 밤 그 집을 나서기도 전에 그녀가 그에게 일종의 흠모를 품게 되었다고 나는 진심으로 믿는다.\n\n그는 거기서 나와 함께 저녁을 먹으려고 머물렀다 — 기꺼이 그랬다고 말한다면, 그가 얼마나 선뜻, 얼마나 흥겹게 그랬는지를 절반도 못 담아내는 셈이다. 그는 빛과 공기처럼 바키스 씨의 방으로 들어가, 마치 자신이 화창한 날씨라도 되는 양 그 방을 환하게 밝히고 산뜻하게 했다. 그가 하는 일에는 소란도, 애씀도, 의식적인 꾸밈도 없었다. 다만 모든 것에 형언할 수 없는 가벼움이, 달리는 도무지 할 수 없을 것 같은, 더 잘할 수도 없을 것 같은 그 무엇이 깃들어 있었는데, 그것이 어찌나 우아하고 자연스럽고 흐뭇하던지, 지금 돌이켜 보아도 나는 그 기억에 압도되고 만다.\n\n우리는 그 작은 거실에서 즐겁게 놀았다. 내가 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 손때가 묻지 않은 듯한 『순교자 열전』이 옛날처럼 책상 위에 놓여 있었고, 나는 이제 그 끔찍한 삽화들을 한 장 한 장 넘기며 옛날 그것들이 불러일으켰던 그 감정들을 떠올렸으나, 정작 그 감정이 일지는 않았다. 페거티가 자기 말로 내 방이라 부르는 방 이야기를 꺼내며, 밤에 그 방이 채비되어 있을 거라고, 내가 거기서 묵어 주기를 바란다고 했을 때, 내가 미처 머뭇거리는 눈길로 스티어포스를 쳐다볼 새도 없이 그가 사정을 죄다 알아챘다.\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 머무는 동안 자네는 여기서 자게. 나는 여관에서 잘 테니.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네를 이렇게 멀리까지 데려와 놓고,\" 내가 받았다. \"따로 떨어져 자는 건 우정에 어긋나는 일 같아, 스티어포스.\"\n\n\"아니, 도대체, 자네가 본디 있어야 할 곳이 어디겠나?\" 그가 말했다. \"그것에 비하면 '같아'가 다 뭔가?\" 그렇게 단번에 정해졌다.\n\n그는 여덟 시에 우리가 페거티 씨의 배를 향해 길을 나설 때까지 그 사랑스러운 자질들을 끝끝내 잃지 않았다. 아니, 시간이 흐를수록 그것들은 더욱더 환하게 드러났다. 그때도 나는 생각했고 지금도 의심치 않거니와, 남을 즐겁게 하겠다는 결심이 성공을 거두고 있다는 자각이 그에게 새로운 섬세한 감각을 불어넣어, 본디도 은근했던 그 솜씨를 한결 수월하게 만들어 주었기 때문이다. 만약 그때 누군가 나에게, 이 모든 것이 한순간의 흥을 위해, 넘치는 혈기를 쏟을 데를 찾으려고, 우월감에 젖은 생각 없는 애착으로, 그에게는 하찮은 것을 헛되이 함부로 얻어 내고는 다음 순간 내팽개치는 그저 그런 짓에 불과한, 눈부신 노름이라고 말했다면 — 그러니까, 그날 밤 누군가 나에게 그런 거짓말을 했다면, 내 분노가 어떤 식으로 터져 나왔을지 궁금하다! 아마도, 그게 가능했다면, 어두운 겨울 모래밭을 건너 그 낡은 배를 향해 그와 나란히 걸으며 내 마음속에 차오르던 충직함과 우정의 그 낭만적인 감정이 더욱 짙어지는 것으로밖에는 터지지 않았으리라. 내가 처음 페거티 씨의 문간을 어둑하게 가렸던 그날 밤보다도 바람은 우리 둘레에서 한층 더 구슬프게 흐느끼고 있었다.\n\n\"여긴 좀 거친 데지, 스티어포스, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"어둠 속에서는 꽤나 음산하군.\" 그가 말했다. \"게다가 바다는 우리를 집어삼키려고 굶주리기라도 한 듯 으르렁대고 말이야. 저기 불빛이 보이는 데가 그 배인가?\" \"저게 그 배야.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 오늘 아침에 본 바로 그거로군.\" 그가 받았다. \"내가 곧장 그리로 갔던 건, 본능이었던 모양이야.\"\n\n불빛에 다가가는 동안 우리는 더는 말을 나누지 않고, 살그머니 문께로 향했다. 나는 빗장에 손을 얹었고, 스티어포스에게 나에게 바짝 붙으라고 속삭이고는 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n바깥에서도 두런두런 말소리가 들려왔는데, 우리가 들어서는 바로 그 순간 손뼉 치는 소리가 났다. 그런데 놀랍게도 그 손뼉 소리는 늘 풀이 죽어 있던 거미지 부인에게서 나는 것이었다. 그러나 거기서 유난히 들떠 있던 사람은 거미지 부인만이 아니었다. 페거티 씨는 보기 드문 흡족함으로 얼굴이 환하게 빛난 채 힘껏 웃으며, 꼬마 에밀리가 뛰어들기라도 하라는 듯 거친 두 팔을 활짝 벌리고 있었다. 햄은 흠모와 환희와, 그에게 무척 잘 어울리는 어딘가 굼뜬 듯한 수줍음이 뒤섞인 표정으로, 마치 에밀리를 페거티 씨에게 내보이기라도 하듯 꼬마 에밀리의 손을 잡고 있었다. 꼬마 에밀리는 얼굴을 붉히며 수줍어하면서도, 그 기쁨에 찬 눈이 말해 주듯 페거티 씨가 기뻐하는 것이 기뻐서, 햄에게서 폴짝 뛰어 페거티 씨의 품에 안기려던 바로 그 순간 우리가 들어오는 바람에 (그녀가 우리를 제일 먼저 보았다) 멈칫했다. 어둡고 차가운 밤에서 따뜻하고 환한 방으로 막 들어서던 그 찰나, 우리가 그들 모두를 처음 흘끗 본 광경 속에서 다들 이렇게 한창이었다. 거미지 부인은 뒤편에서 미친 여자처럼 손뼉을 쳐 대고 있었다.\n\n그 작은 그림은 우리가 들어서자마자 어찌나 순식간에 흩어지던지, 그런 것이 정말 있기나 했나 싶을 정도였다. 어느새 나는 깜짝 놀란 식구들 한가운데서 페거티 씨와 얼굴을 마주한 채 그에게 손을 내밀고 있었고, 그때 햄이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님! 데이비 도련님이여!\"\n\n순식간에 우리는 모두 서로 악수를 나누고, 어떻게 지냈느냐고 서로 묻고, 만나서 얼마나 반가운지 서로 떠들어 대며, 다들 한꺼번에 떠들고 있었다. 페거티 씨는 우리를 보고 어찌나 우쭐하고 기쁘던지 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 몰라, 나와 악수를 했다가, 다음엔 스티어포스와, 또 다음엔 나와, 거듭거듭 악수를 하고는, 그러다 텁수룩한 머리를 머리통 위로 마구 헝클어뜨리며, 어찌나 흐뭇하고 의기양양하게 웃어 대던지 보는 것만으로도 흐뭇했다.\n\n\"아니, 두 분 신사 양반이 — 어엿한 신사로 자라셔서 — 하필 내 평생 다른 어느 밤도 아닌 오늘 밤에 이 지붕 밑에 찾아오시다니,\" 페거티 씨가 말했다. \"이런 일은 일찍이 없었지요, 내 똑똑히 믿거니와! 에밀리, 내 귀염둥아, 이리 온! 이리 오너라, 우리 꼬마 마녀야! 데이비 도련님의 친구분이시다, 얘야! 네가 얘기로 들었던 그 신사 양반이시다, 에밀리. 데이비 도련님하고 같이 너를 보러 오셨단다, 네 외삼촌 평생에 이제껏 없었고 앞으로도 없을 가장 환한 밤에 말이다. 저기 또 한 분 잘 보아라, 만세로다!\"\n\n이 말을 단숨에, 더없는 신명과 기쁨으로 쏟아 낸 뒤, 페거티 씨는 큼지막한 두 손을 황홀하게 조카딸의 두 뺨에 갖다 대고, 그 얼굴에 열두 번이나 입을 맞추더니, 점잖은 자랑과 사랑을 담아 자기 떡 벌어진 가슴에 그 얼굴을 갖다 대고는, 마치 그 손이 귀부인의 손이라도 되는 양 토닥였다. 그러고는 그 애를 놓아주었다. 그 애가 내가 자던 그 작은 방으로 달려가자, 그는 보기 드문 흡족함에 후끈 달아올라 숨을 헐떡이며 우리를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"두 분 신사 양반이 — 이제는 어엿하게 자란 신사 양반들, 그것도 이런 신사 양반들이 —\" 페거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요!\" 햄이 외쳤다. \"옳거니! 그렇고말고요. 데이비 도련님 말이여 — 어엿하게 자라신 신사 양반 — 그렇고말고요!\"\n\n\"두 분 신사 양반, 어엿하게 자란 신사 양반들이,\" 페거티 씨가 말했다. \"사정을 아시고 나서, 내가 이렇게 들떠 있는 걸 너그러이 봐주지 않으신다면, 용서를 빌어야겠지요. 에밀리, 얘야! — 저 애는 내가 이야기하려는 걸 알고,\" 여기서 그의 기쁨이 다시 터져 나왔다. \"달아나 버렸구먼. 잠깐 저 애 좀 봐 주시겠소, 아주머니?\"\n\n거미지 부인이 고개를 끄덕이고는 사라졌다.\n\n\"만약 이게,\" 페거티 씨가 난롯가에 우리 틈에 끼어 앉으며 말했다. \"내 평생 가장 환한 밤이 아니라면, 나는 조개요 — 그것도 삶은 조개 — 그 이상은 더 할 말이 없소. 이 꼬마 에밀리가 말이오, 손님,\" 스티어포스에게 나직이 말했다. \"아까 여기서 얼굴 붉히는 걸 보신 그 애가 —\"\n\n스티어포스는 그저 고개만 끄덕였으나, 어찌나 흐뭇하게 관심을 보이는 표정으로, 페거티 씨의 감정에 함께 어우러지는 낯빛으로 끄덕이던지, 페거티 씨는 그가 말이라도 한 양 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 페거티 씨가 말했다. \"그게 그 애요, 바로 그렇답니다. 고맙습니다, 손님.\"\n\n햄도 나에게 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕여 보였는데, 마치 자기도 그렇게 말하고 싶다는 듯했다.\n\n\"우리 이 꼬마 에밀리는 말이오,\" 페거티 씨가 말했다. \"우리 집에서, 내 짐작에 (나는 무지렁이지만, 그래도 내 믿음으론) 눈망울 초롱초롱한 어린 것 말고는 누구도 한 집안에서 될 수 없는 그런 존재였소. 그 애는 내 자식이 아니오. 나는 자식을 둔 적이 없으니까. 하지만 그 애를 이보다 더 사랑할 수는 없을 게요. 아시겠소! 더는 못 한단 말이오!\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"손님이 아시는 줄 압니다, 손님.\" 페거티 씨가 받았다. \"다시 한번 고맙소. 데이비 도련님은 저 애가 어땠는지 기억하시고, 손님은 저 애가 지금 어떤지를 손수 가늠하실 수 있겠지요. 하지만 두 분 다, 저 애가 내 사랑하는 가슴에 어떤 존재였고, 지금 어떤 존재이며, 또 앞으로 어떤 존재일지는 온전히 알 수 없으실 게요. 나는 거칠지요, 손님.\" 페거티 씨가 말했다. \"바다 고슴도치만큼이나 거친 사람이지만, 우리 꼬마 에밀리가 나한테 어떤 존재인지는, 어쩌면 여자라면 모를까, 아무도 알 수 없을 거라 생각하오. 그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만,\" 목소리를 한층 더 낮추며, \"그 여자란 거미지 부인은 아니라오. 그 사람도 장점이야 수두룩하지만 말이오.\" 페거티 씨는 자기가 하려는 말에 대한 채비라도 더 하려는 듯 두 손으로 다시 머리를 헝클어뜨리고는, 두 무릎에 한 손씩 얹고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"우리 에밀리가 제 아비를 물에 잃었을 때부터 그 애를 알아 온 사람이 하나 있소. 줄곧 그 애를 봐 온 사람이지. 갓난아기 때도, 어린 계집애 때도, 다 큰 처녀가 됐을 때도. 보기에 대단한 사람은 아니었소.\" 페거티 씨가 말했다. \"나하고 비슷한 몸피에 — 거칠고 — 남서풍 비바람에 절은 데가 많고 — 짭짤하게 짠내 나는 — 하지만 통틀어 보면 정직한 녀석이라, 마음 둘 데에 마음을 둔 사람이었소.\"\n\n내 생각에 나는 햄이 지금 우리를 향해 앉아 씩 웃는 그 정도로 입이 찢어지게 웃는 모습을 일찍이 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"이 복받은 방수포 같은 녀석이 가서 뭘 했느냐 하면 말이오,\" 페거티 씨가 얼굴 가득 한낮 같은 환한 즐거움을 띠며 말했다. \"그 마음을 우리 꼬마 에밀리한테 빼앗겨 버렸지 뭐요. 그 애를 졸졸 따라다니고, 그 애한테 종이라도 된 양 굴고, 밥맛까지 크게 잃어버리더니, 마침내 나한테 뭐가 잘못됐는지를 분명히 일러 주더란 말이오. 자, 나로서야, 보다시피, 우리 꼬마 에밀리가 어엿하게 시집갈 길에 들어섰으면 하는 바람이지요. 어찌 됐든 그 애를 지켜 줄 권리가 있는 정직한 사내한테 그 애가 매인 모습을 보고 싶었소. 내가 얼마나 더 살지, 언제 죽을지 나야 모르오. 하지만 만약 내가 어느 밤, 여기 야머스 앞바다에서 거센 바람에 배가 뒤집혀, 도무지 거슬러 나아갈 수 없는 그 물결 너머로 마지막으로 마을의 불빛이 반짝이는 걸 본다 해도, '저기 뭍에 우리 꼬마 에밀리한테 쇠처럼 진실한 사내가 하나 있다, 하느님께서 그 애를 보살피시기를, 그 사내가 살아 있는 한 우리 에밀리한테는 아무 해도 닿지 못하리라' 이렇게 생각하며 한결 마음 편히 가라앉을 수 있다는 건 알지요.\"\n\n페거티 씨는 꾸밈없는 진심으로 오른팔을 휘저었는데, 마치 마을의 불빛을 향해 마지막으로 손을 흔드는 것 같았다. 그러고는 눈이 마주친 햄과 고갯짓을 주고받은 뒤, 아까처럼 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자! 나는 그 녀석한테 에밀리한테 말을 해 보라고 일렀소. 덩치는 멀쩡한데, 어린것보다도 더 부끄럼을 타서, 도무지 내켜 하질 않더란 말이오. 그래서 내가 말을 했지. '뭐라고요! 저이를요!' 에밀리가 그러더군. '여러 해 그렇게 살갑게 알고 지내고, 그렇게 좋아하던 저이를요. 아, 외삼촌! 나는 절대 저이랑은 안 돼요. 저이는 그렇게 좋은 사람인걸요!' 나는 그 애한테 입을 맞추고, 그저 이렇게만 말했소. '얘야, 솔직하게 말하길 잘했다, 네 일은 네가 고르는 거다, 너는 작은 새처럼 자유로운 몸이란다.' 그러고는 그 녀석한테 가서 말했지. '그렇게 됐으면 했다만, 그게 안 되겠다. 그래도 너희 둘은 예전 그대로 지낼 수 있어, 내가 너한테 하는 말은, 사내답게 그 애하고 예전처럼 지내라는 거다.' 그러자 그 녀석이 내 손을 흔들며 말하더군. '그러겠어요!' 그렇게요. 그리고 그 녀석은 그랬소 — 떳떳하고 사내답게 — 꼬박 이 년 동안이나. 그래서 우리는 여기 집에서 예전과 똑같이 지냈지요.\"\n\n페거티 씨의 얼굴은 이야기가 굽이굽이 흐르는 동안 표정이 갖가지로 바뀌었는데, 이제 내 무릎에 한 손을, 스티어포스의 무릎에 한 손을 얹으며 (행동에 한층 힘을 싣느라 두 손에 먼저 침까지 발랐다) 다시 아까처럼 의기양양한 기쁨을 온통 되찾고는, 다음 말을 우리 둘에게 나누어 건넸다.\n\n\"한데 난데없이, 어느 날 저녁 — 바로 오늘 밤 같은 날이었지요 — 꼬마 에밀리가 일터에서 돌아오는데, 그 녀석이 같이 오는 거요! 그게 뭐 대수냐 하시겠지. 그렇소, 그 녀석은 날이 저문 뒤면, 아니 저물기 전에도, 언제든 오라비처럼 그 애를 챙기니까. 한데 이 방수포 같은 녀석이 그 애 손을 덥석 잡고는, 기뻐서 나한테 소리치는 거요. '여기 좀 보세요! 이이가 제 작은 아내가 될 사람이에요!' 그러자 그 애가, 반쯤은 대담하고 반쯤은 수줍게, 반쯤은 웃고 반쯤은 울면서 말하더군. '네, 외삼촌! 괜찮으시다면요.' — 괜찮으냐고!\" 페거티 씨가 그 말을 생각하며 황홀해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 외쳤다. \"원, 내가 뭐 다른 걸 할 사람이라고! — '괜찮으시다면요, 저도 이젠 마음이 더 다잡혔고, 곰곰 다시 생각해 봤어요. 저이한테 될 수 있는 한 좋은 작은 아내가 될게요. 저이는 다정하고 착한 사람이니까요!' 그러니까 거미지 아주머니가 연극에서처럼 손뼉을 짝짝 치는데, 마침 두 분이 들어오신 거요. 자! 이제 다 털어놓았소!\" 페거티 씨가 말했다. \"두 분이 들어오셨지! 바로 이 순간에 그 일이 벌어진 거요. 그리고 여기 이 사내가, 그 애가 도제 기한을 마치는 그날로 그 애와 혼인할 사람이오.\"\n\n햄은, 그럴 만도 했지만, 페거티 씨가 끝없는 기쁨에 겨워 믿음과 우정의 표시로 후려친 그 한 방에 휘청거렸다. 그러나 우리에게 무언가 말을 해야겠다는 생각이 든 듯, 그는 몹시 더듬거리며 무던히 애를 써서 말했다.\n\n\"그 앤 도련님만큼도 안 됐었지요, 데이비 도련님 — 도련님이 처음 오셨을 때 말이여 — 그때 나는 그 애가 자라서 어찌 될까 생각했더랬소. 그 애가 자라는 걸 봤지요 — 신사 양반들 — 꽃처럼요. 나는 그 애를 위해서라면 목숨도 내놓을 수 있소 — 데이비 도련님 — 아! 더없이 흐뭇하고 기쁘게요! 그 앤 나한테 — 신사 양반들 — 그 무엇보다도 — 그 애는 나한테 내가 바랄 수 있는 전부고, 그 이상이라, 내가 — 내가 도무지 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼이오. 나는 — 나는 그 애를 진심으로 사랑하오. 이 온 땅에 — 아니, 온 바다 위를 떠도는 누구를 데려와도 — 나만큼 제 색시를 사랑하는 신사 양반은 없을 거요. 비록 흔한 뱃사람들 가운데 — 제 속마음을 — 더 잘 말할 사람이야 많겠지만 말이오.\"\n\n햄처럼 다부진 사내가, 제 마음을 사로잡은 그 어여쁜 작은 아가씨를 향한 감정의 힘에 못 이겨 떨고 있는 모습을 보는 것은 가슴 뭉클한 일이었다. 페거티 씨와 햄이 우리에게 보여 준 그 꾸밈없는 신뢰 자체가 가슴 뭉클했다. 나는 그 이야기 전체에 마음이 뭉클해졌다. 내 감정이 어릴 적 추억에 얼마나 휘둘렸는지는 모르겠다. 내가 여전히 꼬마 에밀리를 사랑해야 한다는 어떤 미련 같은 마음을 품고 그곳에 갔던 것인지도 모르겠다. 다만 이 모든 일로 내 가슴이 기쁨으로 가득 찼다는 것은 안다. 그러나 처음에는, 형언할 수 없이 예민한 기쁨이라, 아주 사소한 일에도 금세 아픔으로 변해 버릴 그런 기쁨이었다.\n\n그러니, 그들 사이에 가득한 그 정조를 어떤 솜씨로 건드리는 일이 나에게 달려 있었다면, 나는 일을 서투르게 망쳤을 것이다. 그러나 그것은 스티어포스에게 달려 있었고, 그는 어찌나 능란하게 해내던지, 몇 분 만에 우리는 더없이 편안하고 더없이 행복한 사이가 되었다.\n\n\"페거티 씨,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 더없이 좋은 분이시고, 오늘 밤 누리시는 만큼 행복할 자격이 있으십니다. 자, 제 손을 잡으시지요! 햄, 자네에게 기쁨을 비네, 이 친구야. 여기에도 제 손을 잡게! 데이지, 불을 좀 쑤셔서 활활 타오르게 해 주게! 그리고 페거티 씨, 당신의 얌전한 조카따님을 다시 데려오도록 구슬려 주시지 않는다면 (저는 이 구석 자리를 그분께 비워 드리겠습니다) 저는 그만 가 보겠습니다. 이런 밤에 당신네 난롯가에 빈자리가 생기는 건 — 더구나 이런 빈자리는 더더욱 — 인도의 온갖 재물을 준다 해도 저는 만들지 않겠습니다!\"\n\n그래서 페거티 씨가 꼬마 에밀리를 데려오려고 내 옛 방으로 들어갔다. 처음에 꼬마 에밀리는 나오기 싫어했고, 그러자 이번엔 햄이 갔다. 이윽고 두 사람이 그 애를 난롯가로 데려왔는데, 몹시 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 무척 수줍어했다. 그러나 스티어포스가 자기에게 얼마나 다정하고 깍듯하게 말을 건네는지, 자기를 난처하게 할 만한 것은 얼마나 솜씨 좋게 피해 가는지, 페거티 씨에게 배며 선박이며 조수며 물고기 이야기를 어떻게 풀어 가는지, 그가 세일럼 하우스에서 페거티 씨를 만났던 그때를 두고 나에게 어떻게 말을 거는지, 그가 그 배와 그에 딸린 모든 것을 얼마나 좋아하는지, 그가 얼마나 가뿐하고 수월하게 이야기를 이어 가다가, 차츰차츰 우리를 홀린 듯한 한 무리로 만들어, 마침내 우리 모두가 아무 거리낌 없이 이야기꽃을 피우게 되는지를 알게 되자, 그 애도 이내 한결 마음을 놓았다.\n\n사실 에밀리는 그날 저녁 내내 말은 거의 없었다. 그러나 바라보고, 귀 기울이는 사이 그 얼굴에 생기가 돌았고, 그 애는 사랑스러웠다. 스티어포스는 (페거티 씨와 나눈 이야기에서 나온) 음산한 난파 이야기를 마치 눈앞에 그 광경이 다 펼쳐지기라도 한 듯 들려주었는데 — 꼬마 에밀리의 눈은 마치 자기도 그 광경을 보고 있는 듯 내내 그에게 박혀 있었다. 그는 그 분위기를 누그러뜨리려고 자기가 겪은 유쾌한 모험담을, 그 이야기가 우리에게처럼 자기에게도 갓 겪은 일인 양 흥겹게 들려주었고 — 꼬마 에밀리는 배 안이 그 가락 같은 웃음소리로 울리도록 깔깔 웃었으며, 우리도 모두 (스티어포스까지도) 그 즐겁고 가뿐한 분위기에 끌려 어쩔 수 없이 함께 웃었다. 그는 페거티 씨에게 '거센 바람이 불 때, 불 때, 불 때'라는 노래를 부르게, 아니 외치게 했고, 자기도 뱃사람의 노래 한 가락을 어찌나 애절하고 아름답게 부르던지, 나는 슬프게 집 둘레를 기어 다니며 우리의 끊김 없는 침묵 사이로 나직이 흐느끼는 그 진짜 바람마저도, 그 노래를 들으러 거기 와 있는 것이 아닐까 하는 생각이 들 지경이었다.\n\n거미지 부인으로 말하자면, 그는 (페거티 씨가 일러 준 바로는) 옛 영감이 세상을 뜬 뒤로 누구도 거두지 못한 성공을 거두며 그 낙담의 화신을 흥겹게 만들어 놓았다. 그가 그녀에게 비참할 겨를을 어찌나 안 주던지, 그녀는 이튿날 자기가 무슨 흘림에라도 걸렸던 게 틀림없다고 말했다.\n\n그러나 그는 모두의 관심이나 대화를 독차지하지는 않았다. 꼬마 에밀리가 점점 용기를 내어, 난로 너머로 (그래도 여전히 수줍게) 나에게, 조개껍데기며 자갈을 주우러 바닷가를 거닐던 우리 옛 시절 이야기를 건넸을 때, 그리고 내가 그 애에게 내가 그 애한테 얼마나 푹 빠져 있었는지 기억하느냐고 물었을 때, 그리고 우리 둘이 함께 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고, 지금 보면 그토록 비현실적인 그 정겨운 옛 시절을 돌아보던 그때, 그는 잠자코 귀 기울이며 우리를 곰곰이 지켜보았다. 그 애는 이때도, 그리고 저녁 내내, 난롯가의 작고 익숙한 제 구석에 놓인 그 낡은 사물함 위에 앉아 있었다 — 햄은 그 곁, 예전에 내가 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었다. 그 애가 자기 특유의 그 짓궂은 버릇으로 그러는 것인지, 아니면 우리 앞이라 처녀다운 수줍음으로 그러는 것인지 나는 도무지 가늠할 수 없었으나, 그 애는 벽에 바짝 붙어 햄에게서 떨어져 있었고, 나는 그 애가 저녁 내내 그러는 것을 눈여겨보았다.\n\n내 기억으로는, 우리가 작별할 무렵에는 거의 자정이었다. 우리는 저녁으로 비스킷과 말린 생선을 좀 먹었고, 스티어포스가 주머니에서 네덜란드 진이 가득 든 술병을 꺼내, 우리 사내들이 (이제는 얼굴 붉히지 않고 우리 사내들이라 말해도 되겠다) 그것을 비웠다. 우리는 흥겹게 헤어졌다. 그들 모두가 우리가 길을 가는 데 될 수 있는 한 멀리까지 비추어 주려고 문께에 빙 둘러 모여 섰을 때, 나는 꼬마 에밀리의 그 어여쁜 푸른 눈이 햄의 등 뒤에서 우리를 살며시 좇는 것을 보았고, 조심해서 가라고 우리에게 외치는 그 애의 부드러운 목소리를 들었다.\n\n\"여간 사랑스러운 작은 미인이 아니로군!\" 스티어포스가 내 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"그래! 묘한 곳에, 묘한 사람들이야. 저런 사람들과 어울리는 건 꽤 새로운 기분이군.\"\n\n\"우리가 운이 참 좋기도 하지.\" 내가 받았다. \"이렇게 와서, 그 약속된 혼인으로 행복해하는 모습을 다 보게 됐으니 말이야! 그렇게 행복한 사람들은 처음 봤어. 그걸 보고, 또 우리가 그들의 꾸밈없는 기쁨을 함께 나누게 되다니, 얼마나 흐뭇한 일인지!\"\n\n\"그 처녀한테는 좀 멍청한 친구지, 안 그런가?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n그는 그에게도, 또 그들 모두에게도 그토록 다정했던 터라, 나는 이 뜻밖의 싸늘한 대답에 충격을 받았다. 그러나 휙 그를 돌아보고 그의 눈에 어린 웃음을 보고는, 한결 마음이 놓여 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 스티어포스! 자네야 가난한 사람들을 두고 농담할 처지가 되니 좋겠네! 다틀 양과 옥신각신하든, 농담으로 나한테 자네 속정을 감추려 들든, 나는 다 알아. 자네가 저 사람들을 얼마나 완벽하게 이해하는지, 저 소박한 어부의 행복 같은 데에 얼마나 절묘하게 녹아드는지, 우리 옛 유모의 사랑 같은 걸 얼마나 잘 어루만지는지를 보면, 저런 사람들의 기쁨이든 슬픔이든, 어떤 감정 하나도 자네한테 아무래도 좋은 것은 없다는 걸 알 수 있거든. 그래서 나는 그 점 때문에 자네를 스무 배는 더 우러르고 사랑하는 거야, 스티어포스!\"\n\n그가 걸음을 멈추고 내 얼굴을 들여다보더니 말했다. \"데이지, 자네 진심인 것 같군, 자네는 참 착해. 우리 모두가 다 그랬으면!\" 다음 순간 그는 흥겹게 페거티 씨의 노래를 부르고 있었고, 우리는 빠른 걸음으로 야머스를 향해 되돌아갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. SOME OLD SCENES, AND SOME NEW PEOPLE\n\n스티어포스와 나는 그 고장에서 이 주가 넘도록 머물렀다. 우리가 거의 늘 붙어 다녔다는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지만, 가끔은 몇 시간씩 따로 떨어져 지내기도 했다. 그는 솜씨 좋은 뱃사람이었고 나는 그저 그런 풋내기였다. 그래서 그가 패거티 아저씨와 함께 배를 타러 나갈 때면 — 그건 그가 즐겨 하던 놀이였다 — 나는 대개 뭍에 남았다. 나는 패거티의 손님방을 쓰고 있던 터라 거기에 매여 있었지만, 그는 그런 데서 자유로웠다. 패거티가 온종일 바키스 씨를 얼마나 정성껏 돌보는지 잘 알았기에, 나는 밤늦게까지 밖에 있고 싶지 않았다. 반면에 여관에 묵던 스티어포스는 제 기분 말고는 살필 것이 없었다. 그렇게 해서, 내가 잠자리에 든 뒤에 그가 패거티 아저씨네 단골 술집 '윌링 마인드'에서 어부들에게 자그마한 술자리를 베풀었다는 이야기며, 어부 옷을 걸치고 달빛이 환한 밤을 통째로 바다 위에서 보내다가 아침 만조 때에야 돌아왔다는 이야기를 듣게 되었다. 하지만 그 무렵 나는, 그의 가만있지 못하는 천성과 대담한 기질이 다른 새로운 자극거리에서 그러듯 거친 노동과 사나운 날씨 속에서 분출구를 찾기를 즐긴다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러니 그의 어떤 행동도 나를 놀라게 하지 않았다.\n\n우리가 가끔 떨어져 지낸 또 다른 까닭은, 나로서는 당연히 블런더스톤으로 건너가 어린 시절의 정든 옛 풍경을 다시 찾아보고 싶은 마음이 있었던 반면, 스티어포스는 거기에 한 번 가 본 뒤로는 두 번 다시 갈 흥미가 별로 없었다는 데 있었다. 그래서 지금 당장 떠올릴 수 있는 사나흘 동안은, 우리는 이른 아침을 먹고 나서 제각기 갈 길로 흩어졌다가 늦은 저녁 식사 자리에서 다시 만났다. 그가 그 사이의 시간을 어떻게 보내는지 나는 알 수 없었고, 다만 그가 그 고장에서 무척 인기가 있어서, 다른 사람이라면 한 가지도 못 찾았을 자리에서 그는 스무 가지 방법으로 활기차게 즐긴다는 것만 어렴풋이 알 뿐이었다.\n\n나로 말하자면, 나 홀로 떠나는 순례에서 내가 하던 일은, 옛길을 따라 걸으며 한 자 한 자 그 길을 되새기고, 아무리 보아도 싫증 나지 않던 옛 자리들을 두루 찾아 헤매는 것이었다. 나는 그곳들을, 기억이 종종 그러했듯이 찾아 헤맸고, 멀리 떨어져 지내던 시절 어린 마음이 머물렀던 것처럼 그곳들 사이에 오래 머물렀다. 나무 아래, 부모님이 함께 누운 무덤 — 아버지만 누워 있을 적에는 묘한 연민의 마음으로 바라보았고, 예쁜 어머니와 그 아기를 받아들이려 파헤쳐졌을 때에는 너무도 쓸쓸히 그 곁에 서 있던 무덤, 그 뒤로 줄곧 패거티의 한결같은 손길이 단정하게 가꾸어 작은 정원처럼 만들어 놓은 무덤 곁을, 나는 몇 시간이고 거닐었다. 그 무덤은 교회 묘지의 오솔길에서 살짝 비켜난 조용한 구석에 있었으되, 그렇게까지 멀지는 않아서 나는 오가며 비석에 새겨진 이름들을 읽을 수 있었다. 교회 종이 시각을 알리며 울릴 때면 나는 흠칫 놀라곤 했는데, 그 소리가 내게는 마치 떠나간 이의 목소리처럼 들렸기 때문이다. 그런 때 내 머릿속을 채우던 생각은 늘, 내가 인생에서 이루어 낼 모습과 내가 해낼 빛나는 일들에 관한 것이었다. 내 발소리는 오로지 그 한 가락에 맞춰 울려 퍼졌으니, 마치 살아 계신 어머니 곁으로 집에 돌아와 허공에 성을 쌓기라도 하듯 한결같았다.\n\n내 옛집은 크게 달라져 있었다. 까마귀들이 오랫동안 버려 둔 너덜너덜한 둥지들은 사라졌고, 나무들은 가지가 잘리고 우듬지가 베여 내가 기억하던 모습을 잃어버렸다. 정원은 제멋대로 황폐해졌고, 집의 창문은 절반쯤 닫혀 있었다. 집에는 사람이 살았지만, 가엾은 미친 신사 하나와 그를 돌보는 사람들이 전부였다. 그 신사는 늘 내 작은 창가에 앉아 교회 묘지를 내다보았다. 나는 그의 두서없는 생각이, 옛날 내가 잠옷 차림으로 바로 그 작은 창문에서 밖을 내다보며 떠오르는 햇살 속에 양들이 조용히 풀을 뜯는 모습을 보던 장밋빛 아침마다 내 머릿속을 채우던 그런 공상 가운데 어느 하나라도 떠돈 적이 있을까 궁금했다.\n\n우리 옛 이웃이던 그레이퍼 부부는 남아메리카로 떠나 버렸고, 빗물이 빈집의 지붕을 뚫고 새어 들어 바깥벽을 얼룩지게 해 놓았다. 칠립 의사 선생님은 키가 크고 뼈마디가 굵으며 코가 높은 부인과 재혼했다. 부부에게는 빼빼 마른 어린 아기가 하나 있었는데, 무거운 머리를 가누지 못하고 흐릿한 두 눈을 멀거니 뜬 채, 마치 자기가 도대체 왜 태어났는지 늘 궁금해하는 것만 같았다.\n\n붉어지는 겨울 해가 이제 돌아갈 채비를 하라고 일러 줄 때까지, 나는 묘하게 뒤섞인 슬픔과 즐거움을 안고 내 고향 땅을 서성이곤 했다. 그러나 그곳을 등 뒤로 남겨 두고 나서, 특히 스티어포스와 내가 활활 타오르는 난롯불 곁에 앉아 즐겁게 저녁을 먹을 때면, 거기에 다녀왔다는 생각이 더없이 달콤했다. 비록 누그러진 정도이긴 했으나, 밤에 내 단정한 방으로 돌아가, 늘 작은 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 악어 그림책을 뒤적이며, 스티어포스 같은 벗과 패거티 같은 벗, 그리고 내가 잃은 것을 메워 주는 훌륭하고 너그러운 고모님이 있으니 내가 얼마나 복받은 사람인가를 고마운 마음으로 떠올릴 때도 그러했다.\n\n이 긴 산책에서 돌아올 때 야머스로 가는 가장 가까운 길은 나룻배를 타는 것이었다. 그것은 나를 마을과 바다 사이 평평한 벌판에 내려놓았는데, 거기를 곧장 가로지르면 큰길을 따라 빙 도는 상당한 거리를 줄일 수 있었다. 패거티 아저씨네 집이 바로 그 황량한 벌판에 있었고, 내가 가는 길에서 백 야드도 떨어져 있지 않았기에, 나는 지나갈 때마다 늘 안을 들여다보았다. 스티어포스는 거기서 나를 기다리고 있을 게 거의 틀림없었고, 그러면 우리는 서리 내린 공기와 자욱이 끼어 오는 안개를 헤치고 마을의 반짝이는 불빛을 향해 함께 걸어갔다.\n\n어느 어두운 저녁, 평소보다 늦은 때였다 — 그날 나는 이제 곧 집으로 돌아갈 참이라 블런더스톤에 작별 인사를 다녀온 터였다 — 패거티 아저씨네 집에 들어가 보니 그가 혼자 난롯불 앞에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그는 자기 생각에 어찌나 깊이 빠져 있었던지 내가 다가가는 것을 전혀 알아채지 못했다. 사실 바깥의 모래땅 위에서는 발소리가 소리 없이 떨어졌으니, 그가 덜 몰두해 있었더라도 알아채지 못하기 쉬웠을 것이다. 하지만 내가 안으로 들어섰는데도 그는 깨어나지 못했다. 나는 그의 바로 곁에 서서 그를 바라보았다. 그래도 그는 미간을 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 깊은 생각 속에 빠져 있었다.\n\n내가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹자 그가 어찌나 화들짝 놀랐던지, 나까지 덩달아 놀라고 말았다.\n\n\"자네는 마치,\" 그가 거의 성난 듯이 말했다. \"사람을 나무라는 유령처럼 불쑥 나타나는군!\"\n\n\"어떻게든 내가 왔다는 걸 알려야 했으니까.\" 내가 대답했다. \"내가 자네를 별나라에서 불러 내리기라도 했나?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 그가 대답했다. \"아니야.\"\n\n\"그럼 어딘가에서 끌어 올린 건가?\" 내가 그의 곁에 자리를 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"난 불 속의 그림들을 보고 있었네.\" 그가 받았다.\n\n\"그런데 자네가 그걸 내게서 망쳐 버리고 있군.\" 그가 타고 있던 장작개비로 불을 빠르게 쑤셔, 시뻘건 불똥 한 줄기를 튀겨 내고, 그것들이 작은 굴뚝을 타고 솟구쳐 올라 허공으로 으르렁대며 빠져나가는 것을 보며 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 그걸 못 봤을 거야.\" 그가 받았다. \"난 이 어중간한 시간이 질색이야. 낮도 밤도 아닌 이 시간이. 자넨 어찌 이리 늦었나! 어디 갔다 왔어?\"\n\n\"늘 다니던 산책길에 작별을 고하고 왔지.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 여기 앉아서,\" 스티어포스가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다. \"우리가 처음 내려오던 날 밤에 그토록 반가워하던 사람들이 모두 — 지금 이 집의 휑한 분위기로 미루어 보자면 — 뿔뿔이 흩어졌거나, 죽었거나, 아니면 무슨 화를 당했는지도 모르겠다는 생각을 하던 참이었네. 데이비드, 지난 이십 년 동안 내게 사리 분별 밝은 아버지가 있었더라면 얼마나 좋았을까!\"\n\n\"이보게 스티어포스, 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"내가 더 잘 인도받았더라면 얼마나 좋았을까, 온 마음으로 그렇게 바라네!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내가 나 자신을 더 잘 이끌 수 있었더라면, 온 마음으로 그렇게 바라!\"\n\n그의 태도에는 나를 어리둥절하게 할 만큼 격정에 찬 의기소침함이 어려 있었다. 그는 내가 가능하리라 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 더 그답지 않았다.\n\n\"차라리 이 가엾은 패거티가 되거나, 그 미련한 조카가 되는 편이 나았을 거야.\" 그가 일어나서 음울하게 난로 선반에 몸을 기대고 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 향한 채 말했다. \"나 자신이 되는 것보다는 말이야. 스무 배는 더 부유하고 스무 배는 더 영리한 이 나 자신, 지난 반 시간 동안 이 빌어먹을 배의 껍데기 속에서 스스로를 괴롭혀 온 이 나보다는!\"\n\n나는 그의 그 변화에 어찌나 당황했던지, 처음에는 그저 말없이 그를 바라보기만 했다. 그는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 서서 음울하게 불을 내려다보고 있었다. 마침내 나는 내가 느끼는 온 진심을 담아, 무엇이 그를 그토록 유난히 어지럽혔는지 말해 달라고, 충고는 못 해 줄지언정 함께 아파할 수는 있게 해 달라고 간청했다. 내가 말을 채 맺기도 전에 그는 웃기 시작했는데, 처음에는 신경질적으로 웃다가, 이내 본래의 명랑함을 되찾으며 웃었다.\n\n\"쳇, 아무것도 아니야, 데이지! 아무것도 아니라고!\" 그가 대답했다. \"런던 여관에서도 말했잖아, 가끔 나는 나 자신에게 버거운 동무라고. 방금 나 자신에게 악몽이 되어 있었어 — 내 생각엔 악몽을 꾼 게 틀림없어. 가끔 따분하고 음침한 때에는, 옛날 옛적 동화들이 무엇인지도 모를 모습으로 기억 속에 떠오르거든. 아마 나는 '나는 상관없어' 하다가 사자 밥이 되어 버린 못된 아이와 나 자신을 헷갈렸던 모양이야 — 짐작건대 좀 더 거창하게 신세를 망치는 셈이지. 노파들이 '오싹증'이라 부르는 게 머리끝부터 발끝까지 슬금슬금 기어 다녔어. 나는 나 자신이 무서웠던 거야.\"\n\n\"자넨 그것 말고는 무서운 게 없는 사람 같은데.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지, 하지만 무서워해야 할 것도 어쩌면 넉넉히 있을 거야.\" 그가 대답했다. \"뭐! 그렇게 지나가는 거지! 데이비드, 다시 우울해질 생각은 없네. 하지만, 이 친구야, 다시 한번 말해 두건대, 내게 한결같고 사리 분별 밝은 아버지가 있었더라면 나에게는 (그리고 나뿐 아니라 더 많은 이에게도) 좋았을 거야!\"\n\n그의 얼굴은 늘 표정이 풍부했지만, 시선을 불에 박은 채 그 말을 할 때처럼 그토록 어두운 진지함을 드러내는 것을 나는 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"그 얘기는 그만하지!\" 그가 손으로 무언가 가벼운 것을 허공에 던져 버리는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"'아, 그것이 사라지니 나는 다시 사람이 되었도다.' 맥베스처럼 말이야. 자, 이제 저녁을 먹자고! 내가 (맥베스처럼) 더없이 놀랄 만한 소란으로 잔치판을 깨 버린 게 아니라면 말이지, 데이지.\"\n\n\"한데 다들 어디 갔을까 궁금하군!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"누가 알겠나.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"자네를 찾으러 나루터까지 어슬렁어슬렁 갔다가, 여기로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어와 보니 집이 텅 비어 있더군. 그래서 생각에 잠겼고, 그렇게 생각하고 있는 걸 자네가 찾아낸 거지.\"\n\n거미지 부인이 바구니를 들고 나타나면서 어찌하여 집이 비게 되었는지가 밝혀졌다. 부인은 조수와 함께 돌아올 패거티 아저씨를 위해 필요한 것을 사러 부랴부랴 나갔던 것이고, 그 사이에 이른 밤이면 으레 일찍 잠자리에 드는 햄과 어린 에밀리가 자기 없는 틈에 집에 돌아올까 봐 문을 열어 둔 채로 갔던 것이다. 스티어포스는 명랑한 인사와 익살스러운 포옹으로 거미지 부인의 기분을 한껏 북돋아 준 다음, 내 팔을 끼고 서둘러 나를 데리고 나왔다.\n\n그는 거미지 부인 못지않게 제 기분도 북돋아 놓은 터라, 다시 평소의 흥이 흘러넘쳤고, 길을 걷는 내내 활기찬 이야기로 넘쳤다.\n\n\"그래서 말이지,\" 그가 흥겹게 말했다. \"우린 내일 이 해적 생활을 접는 거지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"그렇게 정했잖아.\" 내가 받았다. \"그리고 역마차 자리도 잡아 뒀고, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"그래! 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"이 세상에 여기서 바다 위에 흔들리며 나가는 것 말고도 할 일이 있다는 걸 까맣게 잊어버릴 뻔했어. 차라리 그런 게 없었으면 좋겠군.\"\n\n\"새로움이 가시지 않는 동안이나 그렇겠지.\" 내가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 법도 하지.\" 그가 받았다. \"하긴 그 말 속엔 내 어린 친구처럼 상냥하고 순진한 사람에게는 비꼬는 뜻이 담겨 있긴 하지만 말이야. 뭐! 내가 변덕쟁이라는 건 나도 인정하네, 데이비드. 그건 나도 알아. 하지만 쇠가 달아올라 있는 동안에는, 나도 그걸 힘차게 두들길 수 있다고. 지금이라도 이 일대의 물길을 안내하는 도선사로 꽤 괜찮은 시험을 치를 수 있을 것 같아.\"\n\n\"패거티 아저씨는 자네가 놀라운 사람이라고 하던데.\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"바다의 경이로운 존재란 말이지, 응?\" 스티어포스가 웃었다.\n\n\"정말 그렇게 말하던걸, 게다가 그게 얼마나 맞는 말인지 자네도 알잖아. 자네가 어떤 일을 좇든 얼마나 열성적인지, 또 얼마나 손쉽게 통달해 버리는지 나는 잘 알지. 그래서 자네에게서 가장 놀라운 게 바로 그거야, 스티어포스 — 자네가 그 재능을 그렇게 변덕스럽게 띄엄띄엄 쓰면서도 만족한다는 게 말이야.\"\n\n\"만족한다고?\" 그가 즐겁게 대답했다. \"나는 결코 만족하는 법이 없어, 내 상냥한 데이지, 자네의 그 싱그러움만 빼고는 말이지. 변덕스럽다는 것으로 말하자면, 나는 요즘의 익시온들이 빙글빙글 매여 도는 그 어떤 수레바퀴에도 나 자신을 동여매는 재주를 끝내 배우지 못했네. 어쩌다 잘못된 견습 시절에 그걸 놓쳐 버렸고, 이제는 그런 데 관심도 없어. — 내가 여기서 배 한 척 산 거 알아?\"\n\n\"자넨 참 별난 친구야, 스티어포스!\" 내가 걸음을 멈추며 외쳤다 — 그건 처음 듣는 이야기였으니까. \"두 번 다시 이 근방에 오고 싶지 않을지도 모르면서!\"\n\n\"그건 또 모르는 일이지.\" 그가 받았다. \"이곳이 마음에 들었거든. 아무튼,\" 그가 나를 발랄하게 걷게 하며 말했다. \"매물로 나와 있던 배를 한 척 샀어 — 패거티 아저씨 말로는 쾌속선이라더군, 정말 그렇기도 하고 — 그래서 내가 없는 동안엔 패거티 아저씨가 그 배의 선장이 되는 거야.\"\n\n\"이제 알겠어, 스티어포스!\" 내가 의기양양하게 말했다. \"자넨 자네 쓰려고 산 척하지만, 사실은 아저씨에게 은혜를 베풀려고 그런 거지. 자넬 잘 아니까 처음부터 그쯤은 알아챘어야 했는데. 내 친절한 스티어포스, 자네의 너그러움을 내가 어떻게 다 말로 표현하겠나?\"\n\n\"쳇!\" 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. \"말은 적을수록 좋아.\"\n\n\"내가 모를 줄 알아?\" 내가 외쳤다. \"그렇게 정직한 마음들의 기쁨이나 슬픔이나 그 어떤 감정도 자네에게는 남의 일이 아니라고, 내가 그러지 않았던가?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그가 대답했다. \"자네 다 말했어. 그 얘긴 그쯤 해 두자고. 충분히 했어!\"\n\n그가 그 얘기를 그토록 대수롭지 않게 여기는 마당에 더 파고들어 그의 기분을 상하게 할까 봐, 나는 그저 마음속으로만 그 생각을 이어 갔고, 우리는 아까보다 한층 더 빠른 걸음으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"배에 삭구를 새로 갖춰야 해.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"리티머를 남겨 두고 그 일을 맡길 참이야, 배가 완전히 다 갖춰졌는지 확인하려고. 리티머가 내려왔다는 얘기 했던가?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"아, 그래! 오늘 아침에 내려왔어, 어머니 편지를 가지고.\"\n\n우리 눈길이 마주쳤을 때, 나는 그가 나를 아주 차분하게 바라보면서도 입술까지 창백해져 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 나는 그와 그의 어머니 사이에 무슨 다툼이 있어서, 그가 그 외로운 난롯가에서 내가 본 그런 심정에 빠졌던 게 아닐까 걱정스러웠다. 그래서 그렇게 넌지시 말해 보았다.\n\n\"아, 천만에!\" 그가 고개를 저으며 가볍게 웃었다. \"그런 건 전혀 아니야! 응. 그자가 내려왔어, 내 그 하인 말이야.\"\n\n\"여전한가?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"여전하지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"북극처럼 데면데면하고 조용하지. 그자가 배 이름을 새로 다는 걸 챙길 거야. 지금은 '폭풍의 바다제비'라는 이름이거든. 패거티 아저씨가 폭풍의 바다제비 따위를 알 게 뭐야! 다시 세례를 받게 해 줘야지.\"\n\n\"무슨 이름으로?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"'어린 에밀리.'\"\n\n그가 줄곧 나를 차분하게 바라보고 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 그가 자기 배려심을 추켜세우는 것에 거북해한다는 신호로 받아들였다. 나는 그게 얼마나 기쁜지 얼굴에 드러내지 않을 수 없었지만, 말은 거의 하지 않았다. 그러자 그는 다시 평소의 미소를 되찾았고, 마음이 놓인 듯했다.\n\n\"한데 저기 좀 봐.\" 그가 우리 앞을 바라보며 말했다. \"진짜 어린 에밀리가 오는군! 그리고 함께 오는 저 친구도 있고, 응? 정말이지, 저 친구는 진짜배기 기사야. 절대로 에밀리 곁을 떠나는 법이 없으니!\"\n\n햄은 요즘 들어 배 목수가 되어 있었는데, 그 일에 타고난 손재주를 갈고닦은 끝에 솜씨 좋은 일꾼이 되어 있었다. 그는 작업복 차림이라 꽤나 거칠어 보였지만, 그래도 사내다웠고, 곁에 선 꽃처럼 피어나는 작은 아가씨를 지켜 주기에 더없이 어울리는 사람이었다. 사실 그의 얼굴에는 진솔함과 정직함, 그리고 그녀에 대한 자랑스러움과 사랑을 감추지 않고 드러내는 표정이 있었는데, 내게는 그것이 가장 잘난 외모였다. 그들이 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보면서, 나는 그 점에서도 둘이 참 잘 어울린다고 생각했다.\n\n우리가 그들과 이야기를 나누려고 걸음을 멈추자, 그녀는 수줍게 그의 팔에서 손을 거두었고, 스티어포스와 내게 손을 내밀며 얼굴을 붉혔다. 몇 마디 주고받은 뒤 그들이 지나쳐 갈 때, 그녀는 그 손을 다시 그의 팔에 얹고 싶어 하지 않으면서, 여전히 수줍고 어색한 기색으로 혼자 떨어져 걸었다. 나는 이 모든 것이 무척 어여쁘고 사랑스럽다고 생각했고, 어린 달빛 속으로 그들이 멀어져 가는 모습을 바라보는 스티어포스도 그렇게 여기는 듯했다.\n\n그때 갑자기 우리 곁을 — 분명 그들을 뒤따르며 — 한 젊은 여자가 지나갔다. 우리는 그녀가 다가오는 것을 미처 보지 못했으나, 지나칠 때 나는 그 얼굴을 보았고, 어딘가 어렴풋이 본 적이 있는 듯하다고 생각했다. 그녀는 차림새가 가벼웠고, 대담하면서도 초췌하고, 야하면서도 가난해 보였다. 하지만 그 순간만큼은 그 모든 것을 불어오는 바람에 다 내맡긴 듯했고, 마음속엔 오직 그들을 뒤쫓는 생각밖에 없는 것 같았다. 멀리 어두운 평지가 그들의 모습을 제 속으로 빨아들여, 우리와 바다와 구름 사이에 그저 어둠만 남겨 놓았을 때, 그녀의 모습도 마찬가지로 사라져 버렸으되, 여전히 그들에게 조금도 더 가까워지지는 않은 채였다.\n\n\"저 여자가 시커먼 그림자처럼 그 아가씨를 따라가는군.\" 스티어포스가 걸음을 멈추고 서서 말했다. \"무슨 뜻일까?\"\n\n그가 어찌나 낮은 목소리로 말했던지, 내게는 거의 낯설게 들렸다.\n\n\"저 여자도 그들에게 동냥할 마음을 품고 있나 보지.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"거지라면 별일도 아니지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그런데 하필 오늘 밤 그 거지가 저런 모습을 하고 있다는 게 이상하단 말이야.\"\n\n\"왜?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"솔직히 별다른 까닭은 없어, 그저 그게 지나갈 때,\" 그가 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다. \"내가 그것과 비슷한 무언가를 생각하고 있었기 때문이지. 대체 저게 어디서 나타난 걸까, 참!\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 이 담벼락 그늘에서 나온 것 같아.\" 우리가 담벼락이 맞닿은 길로 나서며 내가 말했다.\n\n\"사라졌군!\" 그가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 받았다. \"그 여자랑 같이 온갖 흉한 것도 다 사라져 버려라. 자, 이제 저녁 먹으러 가자고!\"\n\n하지만 그는 다시 어깨 너머로, 저 멀리 어슴푸레 빛나는 수평선 쪽을 돌아보았고, 또 한 번 돌아보았다. 그리고 우리가 걸어가는 짧은 남은 길 동안에도, 그는 몇 번이나 끊어진 말투로 그 일을 두고 의아해했다. 그리고 따뜻하고 흥겹게 식탁에 둘러앉아 난롯불과 촛불이 우리를 비출 때에야 비로소 그 일을 잊은 듯했다.\n\n리티머가 거기 있었고, 그는 늘 그랬듯 나를 주눅 들게 했다. 내가 그에게 스티어포스 부인과 다틀 양은 잘 계시느냐고 물었더니, 그는 정중하게 (물론 격식 있게) 두 분 다 그럭저럭 잘 지내신다고, 고맙다고, 그리고 안부를 전하셨다고 대답했다. 그게 다였지만, 그래도 그는 사람이 할 수 있는 한 분명하게 이렇게 말하는 것만 같았다. \"도련님은 아주 어리십니다, 손님. 지나치게 어리시지요.\"\n\n저녁을 거의 다 먹어 갈 무렵, 우리를 — 아니, 내가 느끼기로는 차라리 나를 — 지켜보던 구석에서 그가 식탁 쪽으로 한두 걸음 다가오더니 제 주인에게 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 도련님. 마우처 양이 여기 내려와 있습니다.\"\n\n\"누구?\" 스티어포스가 몹시 놀라 외쳤다.\n\n\"마우처 양입니다, 도련님.\"\n\n\"아니, 도대체 그 여자가 여긴 무슨 일로?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"이 일대가 그 여자의 고향인 듯합니다, 도련님. 본인 말로는 해마다 여기 한 번씩 일하러 내려온다고 합니다, 도련님. 오늘 오후에 길에서 마주쳤는데, 저녁 식사 후에 도련님을 뵙는 영광을 누릴 수 있겠느냐고 묻더군요, 도련님.\"\n\n\"자네, 지금 말하는 그 여자 거인을 아나, 데이지?\" 스티어포스가 물었다.\n\n나는 — 리티머 앞에서 이런 처지에 놓이는 것조차 부끄러웠지만 — 마우처 양과 나는 전혀 모르는 사이라고 털어놓을 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"그럼 알게 될 걸세.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그 여자는 세상의 일곱 가지 불가사의 중 하나거든. 마우처 양이 오거든 들여보내게.\"\n\n나는 이 부인에 대해 적잖은 호기심과 흥분을 느꼈는데, 특히 내가 그녀 이야기를 꺼낼 때마다 스티어포스가 웃음보를 터뜨리며 그녀를 화제로 삼은 질문에는 단 하나도 대답하기를 한사코 거부했기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 상당한 기대 속에 잠겨 있었다. 식탁보가 치워지고 반 시간쯤 지나, 우리가 난롯불 앞에서 포도주 병을 앞에 두고 앉아 있을 때 문이 열렸고, 리티머가 평소의 평온함을 조금도 흩뜨리지 않은 채 알렸다.\n\n\"마우처 양이십니다!\"\n\n나는 문간을 바라보았으나 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 마우처 양이 모습을 드러내는 데 어지간히 오래 걸린다고 생각하며 여전히 문간을 보고 있는데, 놀랍게도 나와 문 사이에 놓인 소파 모퉁이를 돌아 뒤뚱뒤뚱 걸어 나오는 것이 있었다. 마흔이나 마흔다섯쯤 된 통통한 난쟁이였는데, 머리와 얼굴은 아주 컸고, 한 쌍의 장난기 어린 잿빛 눈을 하고 있었으며, 팔은 어찌나 짧던지, 스티어포스에게 추파를 던지며 들창코에 손가락을 짐짓 갖다 대려면 손가락을 절반쯤 마중 보내고 거기에 코를 들이밀어야 할 정도였다. 흔히 이중 턱이라 부르는 그녀의 턱은 어찌나 살집이 두툼했던지 보닛 끈을 매듭이며 뭐며 통째로 삼켜 버렸다. 목이라 할 것도 없었고, 허리라 할 것도 없었으며, 다리라 내세울 만한 것도 없었다. 허리가 있었다면 허리쯤 될 자리까지는 풍만하다 못해 넘쳤고, 또 사람들이 대개 그렇듯 두 발로 끝맺기는 했지만, 키가 어찌나 작던지 보통 크기의 의자 앞에 서 있는 모습이 마치 식탁 앞에 선 것 같았고, 들고 온 가방을 그 의자 좌석 위에 올려놓았다. 이 부인은 — 무심한 듯 편안한 차림새로, 앞서 말한 그 어려움을 무릅쓰고 코와 집게손가락을 맞붙인 채, 머리를 어쩔 수 없이 한쪽으로 기울이고, 날카로운 눈 하나는 감아서, 유난히 알 듯 말 듯한 표정을 지으며 — 잠시 스티어포스에게 추파를 던지더니, 봇물 터지듯 말을 쏟아 내기 시작했다.\n\n\"어머! 내 꽃송이!\" 그녀가 그에게 큰 머리를 흔들어 보이며 기분 좋게 시작했다. \"거기 있었구나, 응! 아이고 이 못된 녀석, 부끄러운 줄 알아야지, 집에서 이리 멀리 떨어진 데서 뭐 하는 거니? 틀림없이 무슨 못된 짓 꾸미는 게지. 아이고, 넌 참 약삭빠른 녀석이야, 스티어포스, 정말이지. 나도 한 약삭빠름 하지, 안 그래? 하하하! 자, 백 파운드 대 오 파운드로 내기를 걸었더라도 여기서 날 만나리라곤 생각도 못 했겠지, 그렇지? 어이구 이 사람아, 난 어디에나 있다고. 여기도 있고 저기도 있고, 안 가는 데가 없어, 마치 마술사의 반 크라운 동전이 부인의 손수건 속에 들어가듯이 말이야. 손수건 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데 — 그리고 부인들 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데 — 너야말로 네 그 복받은 어머니에게 얼마나 큰 위안이겠니, 안 그래, 내 귀여운 녀석, 내 한쪽 어깨 너머로 보자면 말이야, 어느 쪽인지는 말 안 하련다!\"\n\n마우처 양은 이야기를 이 대목까지 풀어 놓더니, 보닛을 끄르고 끈을 뒤로 젖힌 뒤, 헐떡거리며 난롯불 앞 발받침 위에 주저앉았다 — 머리 위로 마호가니 그늘을 드리운 식탁이 일종의 정자 노릇을 해 주는 모양새였다.\n\n\"아이고 맙소사, 별이며 뭐며 다!\" 그녀가 작은 무릎을 한쪽씩 손으로 탁탁 치며, 나를 빈틈없이 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다. \"내가 너무 살이 쪘다는 게 사실이야, 스티어포스. 계단 한 번 오르고 나면, 숨 한 번 들이쉬는 게 마치 물 한 동이를 길어 올리는 것만큼이나 힘들다니까. 만약 내가 위층 창문에서 내다보는 걸 본다면, 날 멋진 미인이라고 여길걸, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"어디서 뵙든 그렇게 여길 텐데요.\" 스티어포스가 받았다.\n\n\"저리 가, 이 능청맞은 녀석, 정말!\" 그 작은 사람이, 제 얼굴을 닦던 손수건을 그에게 휙 휘두르며 외쳤다. \"그리고 까불지 좀 마! 하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 지난주에 미더스 부인 댁에 갔었거든 — 그 부인이야말로 대단한 여자지! 어찌나 잘 가꾸는지! — 그런데 미더스 씨가 본인이, 내가 부인을 기다리던 방으로 들어오지 뭐야 — 그 양반이야말로 대단한 남자지! 어찌나 잘 가꾸는지! 가발까지도, 그게 십 년째 쓰고 있는 건데 말이야 — 그러더니 어찌나 칭찬을 늘어놓는지, 나는 종이라도 울려서 사람을 불러야 하나 싶을 지경이었다니까. 하하하! 유쾌한 망나니지만, 줏대가 없어.\"\n\n\"미더스 부인 댁에서는 무슨 일을 한 겁니까?\" 스티어포스가 물었다.\n\n\"그건 비밀이지, 내 복덩이 아기야.\" 그녀가 다시 코를 톡톡 두드리고, 얼굴을 잔뜩 찌푸리고, 초자연적인 지혜를 지닌 꼬마 도깨비처럼 두 눈을 반짝이며 되받았다. \"넌 신경 끄셔! 내가 그 부인 머리 빠지는 걸 막아 주는지, 염색을 해 주는지, 안색을 손봐 주는지, 눈썹을 다듬어 주는지 알고 싶은 게지, 안 그래? 그럼 알게 해 주마, 내 귀여운 것 — 내가 말해 줄 때 말이야! 내 증조부 이름이 뭐였는지 아니?\"\n\n\"모릅니다.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"워커였단다, 내 사랑스러운 녀석.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다. \"워커 집안의 유서 깊은 혈통을 이으신 분이지, 그 덕에 내가 후키 집안의 영지를 죄다 물려받은 거고.\"\n\n마우처 양의 눈짓에 견줄 만한 것은 마우처 양의 그 침착함 말고는 본 적이 없다. 그녀에게는 또 한 가지 놀라운 버릇이 있었는데, 누가 하는 말을 들을 때나 자기가 한 말에 대한 대답을 기다릴 때면, 까치처럼 한쪽 눈을 위로 치뜨고 머리를 교묘하게 한쪽으로 기울인 채 멈추는 것이었다. 아무튼 나는 어찌나 넋이 빠졌던지, 예의범절도 까맣게 잊은 채 그녀를 멀거니 바라보며 앉아 있었던 것 같다.\n\n그러는 사이 그녀는 의자를 자기 곁으로 끌어다 놓고, 가방에서 (한 번 손을 넣을 때마다 짧은 팔을 어깨까지 푹 찔러 넣으며) 작은 병들, 해면, 빗, 솔, 플란넬 조각, 자그마한 고데기 따위 온갖 도구를 꺼내 의자 위에 수북이 쌓느라 분주했다. 그러다 갑자기 그 일을 멈추더니, 나를 몹시 당황스럽게 하는 말을 스티어포스에게 던졌다.\n\n\"네 친구는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨예요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"당신과 알고 지내고 싶어 하지요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그래야지! 내 보기에도 그런 것 같더라!\" 마우처 양이 가방을 든 채 뒤뚱뒤뚱 내게로 다가오며, 다가오는 내내 나를 향해 웃으며 받았다. \"복숭아 같은 얼굴이네!\" 그녀가 발끝으로 서서, 앉아 있는 내 뺨을 꼬집으며 말했다. \"정말 군침 도는걸! 난 복숭아를 아주 좋아하거든. 만나서 정말 반가워요, 코퍼필드 씨.\"\n\n나는 그녀와 알게 되는 영광을 누리게 되어 스스로 자랑스럽고, 그 기쁨은 서로의 것이라고 말했다.\n\n\"어머나 세상에, 우리 어쩜 이리 정중할까!\" 마우처 양이 그 손바닥만 한 손으로 자기 커다란 얼굴을 가리려고 터무니없는 시도를 하며 외쳤다. \"그래도 세상이란 게 죄다 허울 좋은 헛소리투성이 아니겠어, 안 그래!\"\n\n이 말은 우리 둘 모두에게 은밀히 건네는 것이었는데, 그 손바닥만 한 손이 얼굴에서 떨어지더니 팔째로 다시 가방 속에 파묻혔다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 마우처 양?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"하하하! 우린 정말 산뜻하게 한심한 사기꾼들 아니겠니, 안 그래, 내 귀여운 녀석?\" 그 손바닥만 한 여인이 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이고 눈을 허공에 둔 채 가방 속을 더듬으며 대답했다. \"이것 좀 봐!\" 그녀가 무언가를 꺼내며 말했다. \"러시아 공작의 손톱 부스러기야. 나는 그를 거꾸로 뒤집힌 알파벳 공작이라고 부르지, 이름 속에 글자가 죄다 뒤죽박죽 들어 있거든.\"\n\n\"러시아 공작이 당신 손님이라고요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"정말이라니까, 내 귀염둥이.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다. \"그 양반 손톱을 내가 단정하게 다듬어 주거든. 일주일에 두 번! 손가락이며 발가락이며.\"\n\n\"돈은 후하게 주겠지요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"말하는 거나, 돈 내는 거나, 내 귀여운 아기야 — 둘 다 콧소리로 한다니까.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다. \"그 공작은 결코 짜게 구는 사람이 아니야. 그 콧수염을 보면 너도 그렇게 말할걸. 본디 빨갛던 걸 기술로 까맣게 만든 거지.\"\n\n\"물론 당신 기술로요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n마우처 양은 그렇다고 눈을 찡긋했다. \"날 부를 수밖에 없었지. 어쩔 도리가 없었으니까. 기후 탓에 염색이 영 안 받더라고. 러시아에선 멀쩡했는데, 여기선 통 안 먹히는 거야. 그렇게 녹슨 공작은 평생 처음 봤다니까. 꼭 낡은 쇳덩이 같았어!\" \"그래서 방금 그를 사기꾼이라고 부른 건가요?\" 스티어포스가 물었다.\n\n\"아이고, 넌 참 똑똑한 녀석이구나, 안 그래?\" 마우처 양이 머리를 사납게 흔들며 받았다. \"난 우리가 대체로 다 사기꾼 무리라고 말했고, 그걸 증명하려고 공작의 손톱 부스러기를 보여 준 거야. 그 공작의 손톱이 점잖은 양반네 안방에서 내 온갖 재주를 다 합친 것보다 더 큰 노릇을 한다니까. 난 늘 그걸 가지고 다녀. 그게 제일 좋은 소개장이거든. 마우처 양이 공작의 손톱을 다듬는다면, 틀림없이 믿을 만한 사람이렷다, 이거지. 아가씨들한테 그걸 나눠 주거든. 그러면 앨범에 끼워 둔다나 봐. 하하하! 맹세코, '온 사회 제도'(의원 나리들이 의회에서 연설할 때 쓰는 그 말이지)란 게 죄다 공작의 손톱으로 굴러가는 제도라니까!\" 이 세상에서 가장 작은 여인이 짤막한 두 팔을 끼려고 애쓰며 큰 머리를 끄덕여 보이며 말했다.\n\n스티어포스가 진심으로 웃었고, 나도 따라 웃었다. 마우처 양은 그러는 내내 (몹시 한쪽으로 기울어진) 머리를 계속 흔들면서, 한쪽 눈으로는 허공을 바라보고 다른 한쪽 눈으로는 찡긋거렸다.\n\n\"자, 자!\" 그녀가 작은 무릎을 탁 치고 일어서며 말했다. \"이러고 있을 게 아니지. 자 스티어포스, 극지방 탐험에 나서서 끝장을 보자고.\"\n\n그러더니 그녀는 작은 도구 두세 개와 작은 병 하나를 골라 들고는, (놀랍게도) 식탁이 견딜 만하겠느냐고 물었다. 스티어포스가 그렇다고 대답하자, 그녀는 식탁에 의자 하나를 밀어 붙이고 내 손을 빌려 달라고 청하더니, 꽤나 잽싸게 식탁 꼭대기로 기어 올라갔는데, 마치 무대에라도 오르는 듯했다.\n\n\"둘 중 누구라도 내 발목이 보이거든,\" 그녀가 무사히 높이 올라서고 나서 말했다. \"그렇다고 말해, 그럼 집에 가서 자결해 버릴 테니!\"\n\n\"안 보였습니다.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"안 보였습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 좋아.\" 마우처 양이 외쳤다. \"계속 살기로 하지. 자, 아가야, 아가야, 아가야, 본드 부인께 와서 죽임을 당하시지.\"\n\n이것은 스티어포스에게 자기 손에 몸을 맡기라는 권유였다. 그러자 그는 식탁에 등을 돌리고 웃는 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 향한 채 자리에 앉아, 그녀의 검사에 머리를 내맡겼는데, 누가 봐도 그건 그저 우리를 즐겁게 해 주려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없는 짓이었다. 마우처 양이 그의 위에 올라서서, 주머니에서 꺼낸 커다랗고 둥근 돋보기로 그의 풍성한 갈색 머리카락을 들여다보는 광경은 더없이 놀라운 구경거리였다.\n\n\"넌 참 잘생긴 녀석이야!\" 마우처 양이 잠깐 살펴보고 나서 말했다. \"내가 없었으면 일 년 안에 정수리가 수도사처럼 훌렁 벗어졌을걸. 반 분만 가만있어, 내 어린 친구, 그럼 앞으로 십 년은 그 곱슬머리가 붙어 있게 윤기를 좀 내 주마!\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 그녀는 그 작은 병 속 내용물을 작은 플란넬 조각 하나에 약간 따라 붓고, 또 그 약물의 효험을 작은 솔 하나에 좀 묻혀서, 내가 본 중에 가장 분주한 손놀림으로 두 가지를 다 써 가며 스티어포스의 정수리를 문지르고 긁어 대기 시작했는데, 그러는 내내 쉴 새 없이 떠들었다.\n\n\"공작 아드님인 찰리 파이그레이브란 사람이 있거든.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"찰리 알지?\" 그러며 그의 얼굴을 빼꼼히 들여다보았다.\n\n\"조금요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람이야말로 대단한 남자지! 그 구레나룻이라니! 찰리 다리로 말하자면, 그게 짝이 맞기만 하다면 (안 맞지만 말이야) 따라올 자가 없을 거야. 그 사람이 나 없이 해 보려고 했다면 믿겠니 — 그것도 근위 기병대에 있으면서?\"\n\n\"미쳤군요!\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"그래 보이지. 하지만 미쳤든 멀쩡하든, 해 보긴 했어.\" 마우처 양이 받았다. \"그 사람이 어쨌느냐면, 글쎄, 향수 가게에 들어가서, 마다가스카르 물약을 한 병 사겠다는 거야.\"\n\n\"찰리가요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"찰리가. 그런데 마다가스카르 물약 같은 건 없거든.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐죠? 마시는 거예요?\" 스티어포스가 물었다.\n\n\"마시는 거?\" 마우처 양이 손을 멈추고 그의 뺨을 찰싹 때리며 받았다. \"제 콧수염을 손질하는 거 말이야, 알겠니. 가게에 여자가 하나 있었는데 — 나이 지긋한 여편네였지 — 영락없는 그리핀이었어 — 그게 무슨 이름인지조차 들어 본 적이 없는 거야. '죄송합니다만, 손님,' 하고 그 그리핀이 찰리한테 그러더래, '그게 — 그게 — 그게 연지는 아니지요?' '연지라니,' 찰리가 그 그리핀한테 그랬다지, '점잖은 귀에 입에 담기도 뭣한, 내가 연지를 어디다 쓴다는 거요?' '기분 상하셨다면 죄송합니다, 손님,' 하고 그리핀이 그랬대, '하도 여러 이름으로 찾는 분들이 많아서, 그런가 했지요.' 자, 이게 말이지, 내 아가,\" 마우처 양이 여전히 부지런히 문질러 대며 말을 이었다. \"내가 아까 말한 그 산뜻한 사기질의 또 다른 본보기란다. 나도 그쪽으로 좀 하는 게 있거든 — 어쩌면 꽤 많이 — 어쩌면 조금 — 입조심이 상책이야, 내 귀여운 녀석 — 신경 쓰지 마!\"\n\n\"무슨 식으로 말입니까? 연지 식으로요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"이거하고 저거를 한데 맞춰 봐, 내 살가운 제자야.\" 신중한 마우처 양이 자기 코를 만지며 받았다. \"어떤 장사에나 비밀이 있는 법, 그 법칙대로 풀어 보면, 그 답이 네가 바라던 결과를 알려 줄 거야. 나도 그쪽으로 좀 한다 그랬지. 어떤 노부인은 그걸 입술 연고라고 부르고. 또 어떤 부인은 장갑이라 부르고. 또 어떤 부인은 깃 장식이라 부르고. 또 어떤 부인은 부채라 부르고. 나도 그 부인들이 부르는 대로 따라 부르지. 그걸 갖다 드리는 건 난데, 우린 서로에게 그렇게 시치미를 떼고, 어찌나 능청스러운 얼굴로 모르는 척하는지, 그 부인들은 객실 사람들 앞에서 그걸 바르느니 차라리 내 앞에서 바르는 게 낫겠다 싶을 정도라니까. 그러고는 내가 시중을 들 때면, 가끔 나한테 그러는 거야 — 그걸 떡칠해 놓고도, 떡칠해 놓은 게 빤한데 — '내가 어때 보여, 마우처? 창백해 보이나?' 하하하하! 그거야말로 산뜻하지 않니, 내 어린 친구!\"\n\n식탁 위에 올라서서, 이 산뜻한 즐거움을 한껏 만끽하며, 스티어포스의 머리를 부지런히 문질러 대고, 그 너머로 내게 눈을 찡긋거리던 마우처 양 같은 사람은 내 평생 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"아!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이런 건 이 근방에선 별로 수요가 없어. 그러니 또 김이 새지! 여기 온 뒤로 예쁜 여자라곤 하나도 못 봤다니까, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"그림자조차 못 봤어.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다.\n\n\"우리가 예쁜 여자의 실물을 하나 보여 드릴 수 있을 것 같은데요?\" 스티어포스가 내게 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"그렇지, 데이지?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 정말이지.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아하?\" 그 작은 사람이 내 얼굴을 날카롭게 흘끔 보고는 스티어포스의 얼굴을 빼꼼히 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"음?\"\n\n첫 번째 감탄사는 우리 둘 모두에게 던진 물음 같았고, 두 번째 것은 스티어포스에게만 던진 물음 같았다. 그녀는 어느 쪽에도 답을 못 찾은 듯했으나, 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이고 눈을 위로 치뜬 채, 마치 허공에서 답을 찾기라도 하듯, 그리고 그 답이 곧 나타나리라 자신하기라도 하듯 계속 문질러 댔다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨 누이동생인가요?\" 그녀가 잠시 뒤에, 여전히 똑같은 자세로 살피며 외쳤다. \"응, 응?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 내가 미처 대답하기도 전에 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그런 건 전혀 아닙니다. 오히려, 코퍼필드 씨가 한때는 — 내가 한참 잘못 안 게 아니라면 — 그 아가씨를 무척이나 흠모했지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 지금은 안 그래요?\" 마우처 양이 받았다. \"변덕쟁이인가요? 어머, 부끄러운 줄 알아야지! 꽃마다 꿀을 빨고 때마다 마음을 바꾸다가, 폴리가 마침내 그 마음을 받아 준 건가? — 그 아가씨 이름이 폴리예요?\"\n\n요정 같은 갑작스러움으로 이 질문과 함께 캐묻는 눈길을 던지며 내게 덤벼드는 통에, 나는 한순간 적잖이 당황했다.\n\n\"아니요, 마우처 양.\" 내가 대답했다. \"이름은 에밀리입니다.\"\n\n\"아하?\" 그녀가 아까와 똑같이 외쳤다. \"음? 내가 어찌 이리 수다스러울까! 코퍼필드 씨, 나 참 들떠 있죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n그녀의 말투와 눈빛에는 이 화제와 관련해 내게 그리 달갑지 않은 무언가가 담겨 있었다. 그래서 나는, 우리 중 누구도 아직 취한 적 없는 진지한 태도로 말했다. \"그 아가씨는 예쁜 만큼 정숙합니다. 자기 신분에 어울리는, 더없이 훌륭하고 떳떳한 사람과 약혼한 사이고요. 나는 그 아가씨의 외모를 흠모하는 만큼이나 그 분별 있음을 존경합니다.\"\n\n\"맞는 말이야!\" 스티어포스가 외쳤다. \"옳소, 옳소, 옳소! 자, 이 작은 파티마의 호기심을 잠재워 주겠어, 내 사랑하는 데이지, 더는 짐작할 거리를 남기지 않게 말이야. 마우처 양, 그 아가씨는 지금 이 마을의 잡화상이자 모자 가게인 오머와 조럼 상회에 견습생으로, 아니 계약 직공으로, 아무튼 뭐라 부르든 그렇게 매여 있습니다. 알겠습니까? 오머와 조럼. 내 친구가 말한 그 약혼은, 그 아가씨의 사촌과 맺어진 사이입니다. 세례명은 햄, 성은 패거티, 직업은 배 목수, 역시 이 마을 사람이지요. 그 아가씨는 한 친척과 함께 사는데, 세례명은 모르고, 성은 패거티, 직업은 뱃사람, 역시 이 마을 사람입니다. 세상에서 가장 예쁘고 가장 사랑스러운 작은 요정이지요. 나는 그 아가씨를 — 내 친구가 그러하듯 — 더없이 흠모합니다. 그 약혼자를 깎아내리는 것처럼 비칠까 봐 — 내 친구가 그걸 싫어할 걸 아니까 — 안 그러는 거지, 그게 아니라면 이렇게 덧붙이고 싶군요. 내 보기엔 그 아가씨가 제 인생을 너무 헐값에 던져 버리는 것 같다고, 분명 더 나은 사람을 만날 수 있을 거라고, 그리고 맹세코 그 아가씨는 귀부인이 되려고 태어난 사람이라고요.\"\n\n마우처 양은 아주 느리고 또렷하게 이어진 이 말을, 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이고 눈을 허공에 둔 채, 여전히 그 답을 찾고 있기라도 하듯 귀 기울여 들었다. 그가 말을 멈추자 그녀는 순식간에 다시 활기를 되찾아, 놀라운 입심으로 떠들어 댔다.\n\n\"오! 그래서, 그게 다인가요, 응?\" 작고 쉴 새 없는 가위 한 자루를 그의 머리 둘레로 사방팔방 번뜩이며 구레나룻을 다듬으면서 그녀가 외쳤다. \"아주 좋아요, 아주 좋아! 정말 긴 이야기네. '그리하여 그들은 오래오래 행복하게 살았답니다'로 끝나야 마땅한데, 안 그래요? 아! 벌금놀이 그게 뭐더라? 나는 내 사랑을 ㅇ으로 사랑해, 사랑스러우니까. 나는 그녀를 ㅇ으로 미워해, 약혼했으니까. 나는 그녀를 '예외'의 간판으로 데려가, '야반도주'로 대접했네, 그녀의 이름은 에밀리, 그녀는 동쪽에 산다네? 하하하! 코퍼필드 씨, 나 참 들떠 있죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n그녀는 그저 짓궂기 그지없는 표정으로 나를 바라볼 뿐, 어떤 대답도 기다리지 않고, 숨도 안 돌리고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자! 이 세상 어떤 망나니가 흠잡을 데 없이 다듬어지고 손질됐다면, 그게 바로 너야, 스티어포스. 내가 이 세상 어떤 골통이든 알아본다면, 네 골통은 훤히 안다고. 내가 그렇게 말할 때 듣고 있니, 내 귀염둥이? 네 골통은 내가 다 안다니까.\" 그러며 그의 얼굴을 빼꼼히 내려다보았다. \"자 이제 넌 슬슬 꺼져도 좋아, 얘야 (궁정에서 쓰는 말로 하자면 말이야). 그리고 코퍼필드 씨가 이 의자에 앉으면 내가 손봐 드리지.\"\n\n\"어쩌겠나, 데이지?\" 스티어포스가 웃으며 제 자리를 내주며 물었다. \"좀 더 나아져 보겠나?\"\n\n\"고맙지만, 마우처 양, 오늘 저녁은 됐습니다.\"\n\n\"싫다고 하지 말아요.\" 그 작은 여인이 감정사 같은 눈초리로 나를 보며 받았다. \"눈썹만 조금 더 손볼까?\"\n\n\"고맙지만,\" 내가 받았다. \"다음에 하지요.\"\n\n\"관자놀이 쪽으로 한 사 분의 일 인치만 끌어 줄게요.\" 마우처 양이 말했다. \"이 주면 돼요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮습니다. 지금은 안 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"끄트머리만이라도 한번 해 봐요.\" 그녀가 졸랐다. \"안 돼요? 그럼 구레나룻 한 쌍을 위해 발판부터 세워 볼까요. 자!\"\n\n거절하면서 나는 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었는데, 우리가 이제 내 약점을 건드리고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 하지만 마우처 양은, 내가 지금으로서는 자기 기술이 미치는 범위 안의 어떤 치장도 받을 마음이 없으며, 또 당장은 그녀가 설득에 힘을 싣느라 한쪽 눈앞에 치켜든 그 작은 병의 꾐에도 끄떡없다는 것을 알아채고는, 가까운 날에 한번 시작해 보자고 말하고는, 그 높은 자리에서 내려오게 손을 좀 빌려 달라고 청했다. 그렇게 도움을 받아 그녀는 무척 날렵하게 뛰어내리더니, 이중 턱을 보닛 속에 끈으로 동여매기 시작했다.\n\n\"보수는,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그러니까—\"\n\n\"오 실링.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다. \"그야말로 거저나 다름없지, 내 병아리야. 나 참 들떠 있죠, 코퍼필드 씨?\"\n\n나는 공손히 대답했다. \"전혀요.\" 하지만 그녀가 두 닢의 반 크라운 동전을 도깨비 파이 장수처럼 휙 던져 올렸다가 받아서 주머니에 떨어뜨리고 그 주머니를 탁 소리 나게 두드리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그녀가 꽤나 들떠 있구나 싶었다.\n\n\"이게 금고지!\" 마우처 양이 다시 의자 곁에 서서, 가방에서 쏟아 냈던 자질구레한 잡동사니를 도로 챙겨 넣으며 말했다. \"내 짐 다 챙겼나? 그런 것 같네. '누군가와 짝지어 주려고' 멀대 같은 네드 비드우드를 교회로 데려갔다가, 본인 말마따나 신부는 두고 와 버렸다던, 그 짝이 나면 안 되니까. 하하하! 못된 망나니지, 네드는, 그래도 우습다니까! 자, 이제 너희 가슴을 찢어 놓을 줄 알지만, 어쩔 수 없이 떠나야겠다. 너희도 있는 용기를 다 끌어모아서 견뎌 보렴. 잘 있어요, 코퍼필드 씨! 몸조심해요, 노퍽의 멋쟁이 기수님! 내가 어찌 이리 떠들어 댔을까! 다 너희 두 망나니 탓이야. 용서해 주마! '밥 스워!' — 어떤 영국 사람이 프랑스어를 처음 배우고는 그게 영어랑 너무 비슷하다고 '굿 나이트' 대신 그렇게 말했다지. '밥 스워,' 내 귀염둥이들!\"\n\n가방을 팔에 둘러메고, 뒤뚱뒤뚱 걸어가는 동안 가방을 달그락거리며, 그녀는 문가로 뒤뚱뒤뚱 걸어가더니, 거기서 멈춰 우리에게 자기 머리카락 한 타래를 남기고 갈까 물었다. \"나 참 들떠 있죠?\" 그녀가 이 제안에 대한 해설이라도 되는 양 덧붙이고는, 코에 손가락을 댄 채 떠나갔다.\n\n스티어포스가 어찌나 웃어 대던지 나도 따라 웃지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 그렇게 부추기지 않았더라면 내가 웃었을지는 잘 모르겠지만 말이다. 우리가 한참 만에 실컷 웃고 나자, 그는 마우처 양이 발이 꽤나 넓어서 갖가지 사람들에게 갖가지 방식으로 쓸모를 보인다고 말해 주었다. 어떤 사람들은 그녀를 그저 별난 구경거리로 여기며 가지고 논다고 했지만, 그녀는 그가 아는 누구 못지않게 빈틈없고 날카롭게 관찰하며, 팔이 짧은 만큼이나 머리가 길다고 했다. 그녀가 여기에도 있고 저기에도 있고 안 가는 데가 없다고 한 말은 충분히 사실이라고도 했다. 그녀는 지방으로 잽싸게 드나들면서, 어디서나 손님을 끌어모으고 누구든 다 아는 듯하다는 것이었다. 나는 그녀의 됨됨이가 어떤지 물었다. 조금이라도 심술궂은 데가 있는지, 그리고 그 동정심이 대체로 옳은 쪽을 향하는지 말이다. 하지만 두세 번 시도해 보아도 그 질문으로 그의 주의를 끄는 데 성공하지 못하자, 나는 다시 묻기를 그만두었거나 잊어버렸다. 그는 그 대신, 무척 빠른 말투로, 그녀의 솜씨와 벌이에 관한 이야기를, 그리고 혹시라도 내가 부항을 떠야 할 일이 생기면 그녀가 솜씨 좋은 부항사라는 이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았다.\n\n그날 저녁 우리 대화의 주된 화제는 그녀였다. 그리고 밤이 되어 헤어질 때, 내가 아래층으로 내려가는데 스티어포스가 난간 너머로 나를 향해 외쳤다. \"밥 스워!\"\n\n바키스 씨 집에 이르렀을 때, 나는 햄이 집 앞을 서성이고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐고, 어린 에밀리가 안에 있다는 말을 그에게서 듣고는 더욱 놀랐다. 나는 당연히, 왜 그도 안에 있지 않고 혼자 길거리를 서성이고 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그게 말이죠, 데이비 도련님,\" 그가 머뭇거리며 받았다. \"에밀리가, 안에서 누구하고 이야기를 나누고 있어서요.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이야,\" 내가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그거야말로 자네도 안에 들어가 있을 까닭이 될 텐데, 햄.\"\n\n\"뭐, 데이비 도련님, 보통이라면야 그렇겠지요.\" 그가 받았다. \"그런데 이것 좀 보세요, 데이비 도련님,\" 그가 목소리를 낮추고 아주 무겁게 말했다. \"젊은 여자거든요, 도련님 — 한때 에밀리가 알던 젊은 여자인데, 이젠 더 알고 지내선 안 될 사람이에요.\"\n\n그 말을 들었을 때, 몇 시간 전에 그들을 뒤따라가던 그 모습 위로 한 줄기 빛이 비추기 시작했다.\n\n\"가엾은 여자예요, 데이비 도련님.\" 햄이 말했다. \"온 마을 사람들한테 짓밟히고 사는 여자죠. 윗길이든 아랫길이든 어디서나요. 교회 묘지의 흙도 그 여자만큼 사람들이 피하는 이는 품지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 내가 그 여자를 봤나, 햄, 우리가 자넬 만난 뒤에 모래밭에서?\"\n\n\"우릴 계속 지켜보던 여자요?\" 햄이 말했다. \"아마 보셨을 거예요, 데이비 도련님. 그땐 저도 그 여자가 거기 있는 줄 몰랐지만요, 도련님. 그런데 곧 뒤이어 그 여자가 에밀리네 작은 창문 아래로 살금살금 기어 와서, 불빛이 비치는 걸 보고는 속삭이더라고요. '에밀리, 에밀리, 제발 부탁이야, 같은 여자로서 내게 마음을 좀 가져 줘. 나도 한때는 너 같았어!' 듣기에 참 가슴 저민 말이었어요, 데이비 도련님!\"\n\n\"정말 그랬겠군, 햄. 그래서 에밀리는 어쨌나?\" \"에밀리가 그러더라고요. '마사, 너니? 아, 마사, 정말 너야?' — 둘이 오머 씨 가게에서 여러 날 함께 일했던 사이거든요.\"\n\n\"이제 그 여자가 기억나!\" 내가 외쳤다. 처음 그곳에 갔을 때 보았던 두 처녀 가운데 하나를 떠올리면서. \"아주 또렷이 기억나는걸!\"\n\n\"마사 엔델이에요.\" 햄이 말했다. \"에밀리보다 두세 살 위지만, 같은 학교에 다녔지요.\"\n\n\"그 여자 이름은 못 들었었네.\" 내가 말했다. \"말을 끊을 생각은 아니었어.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고요, 데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 받았다. \"그 말 속에 거의 다 들어 있어요. '에밀리, 에밀리, 제발 부탁이야, 같은 여자로서 내게 마음을 좀 가져 줘. 나도 한때는 너 같았어!' 그 여자가 에밀리하고 이야기를 하고 싶어 했어요. 에밀리는 거기서 이야기를 나눌 수가 없었지요. 그 다정한 외삼촌이 마침 집에 와 있었으니까요. 외삼촌은 그 두 사람을 — 아니, 데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 무척 진지하게 말했다. \"그분이 워낙 마음씨 곱고 다정한 분이라, 그 두 사람이 나란히 함께 있는 걸, 바다에 가라앉은 온갖 보물을 다 준다 해도, 차마 못 보실 거예요.\"\n\n나는 그것이 얼마나 사실인지 느꼈다. 나는 그 순간, 햄만큼이나 그것을 잘 알았다.\n\n\"그래서 에밀리가 종잇조각에 연필로 글을 적어서,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"창밖으로 그 여자한테 건네주며 여기로 가져가라고 했어요. '이걸 보여 드려,' 하고요, '우리 외숙모 바키스 부인한테. 그러면 나를 봐서, 외삼촌이 나가시고 내가 갈 수 있을 때까지, 부인이 너를 난롯가에 앉혀 주실 거야.' 이윽고 그 여자가 제게 도련님께 말씀드린 그 이야기를 하고는, 자기를 여기로 데려다 달라고 부탁하더군요. 제가 어쩌겠어요? 그런 사람을 알아선 안 되지만, 얼굴에 눈물이 흐르는데 어떻게 거절을 하겠어요.\"\n\n그는 텁수룩한 윗도리 가슴팍에 손을 넣더니, 무척 조심스럽게 예쁘고 작은 지갑 하나를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"얼굴에 눈물이 흐르는데도 제가 거절할 수 있었다 쳐도요, 데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 거친 손바닥 위에 그것을 정성스레 올려놓으며 말했다. \"이걸 자기 대신 맡아 달라고 줬는데 — 무엇 때문에 가져왔는지 빤히 알면서 — 어떻게 거절을 하겠어요? 이렇게 앙증맞은 물건을!\" 햄이 그것을 골똘히 들여다보며 말했다. \"이 안에 그렇게 쥐꼬리만 한 돈을 넣어서 말이에요, 내 사랑 에밀리.\"\n\n그가 그것을 도로 집어넣었을 때 나는 그의 손을 따뜻하게 잡고 흔들었다 — 그게 무슨 말을 하는 것보다 내게는 더 흡족했으니까 — 그리고 우리는 잠시 말없이 한두 차례 오르락내리락 거닐었다. 그때 문이 열리고 패거티가 나타나, 햄에게 들어오라고 손짓했다. 나는 비켜서 있으려 했지만, 그녀가 내 뒤를 따라와 나도 함께 들어오라고 간곡히 청했다. 그래도 나는 그들이 다 모여 있는 그 방을 피하고 싶었으나, 그곳이 내가 한두 번 말한 적 있는 그 깔끔한 타일 깔린 부엌이었기에 어쩔 수 없었다. 문이 바로 그 부엌으로 통해 있었던지라, 나는 어디로 가고 있는지 미처 헤아릴 새도 없이 그들 한가운데 들어서 있었다.\n\n그 처녀는 — 내가 모래밭에서 보았던 바로 그 여자는 — 난롯가에 있었다. 그녀는 바닥에 앉아, 머리와 한쪽 팔을 의자에 기대고 있었다. 그 몸짓으로 보아, 에밀리가 방금 그 의자에서 일어선 참이고, 그 가엾은 머리가 어쩌면 에밀리의 무릎에 얹혀 있었던 게 아닐까 싶었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 거의 보이지 않았는데, 마치 제 손으로 헝클어 놓기라도 한 듯 머리카락이 풀어 헤쳐져 그 위로 흘러내려 있었기 때문이다. 하지만 그녀가 젊고, 살결이 흰 사람이라는 것은 알 수 있었다. 패거티는 울고 있었다. 어린 에밀리도 울고 있었다. 우리가 처음 들어섰을 때는 아무 말도 오가지 않았고, 그 정적 속에서 찬장 옆 네덜란드제 시계가 평소보다 곱절은 크게 똑딱이는 듯했다. 에밀리가 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n\"마사가,\" 그녀가 햄에게 말했다. \"런던으로 가고 싶대요.\"\n\n\"왜 런던으로?\" 햄이 받았다.\n\n그는 두 사람 사이에 서서, 엎드려 있는 그 처녀를 향한 연민과, 자기가 그토록 사랑하는 사람과 그녀가 어울린다는 것에 대한 시샘이 뒤섞인 눈으로 내려다보고 있었는데, 나는 그 모습을 늘 또렷이 기억해 왔다. 두 사람은 마치 그녀가 병자라도 되는 양, 부드럽고 억눌린 어조로 말했는데, 그 소리는 속삭임을 겨우 넘는 정도였으나 또렷이 들렸다.\n\n\"여기보다는 거기가 나아요.\" 세 번째 목소리가 또렷이 말했다 — 마사였다, 비록 몸은 꿈쩍하지 않았지만. \"거기선 아무도 나를 몰라요. 여기선 누구나 나를 알고요.\"\n\n\"거기 가서 뭘 하려고?\" 햄이 물었다.\n\n그녀는 고개를 들어 잠시 그를 어둡게 둘러보더니, 다시 고개를 떨구고는, 열병에 걸린 여자나 총에 맞아 괴로움에 몸부림치는 사람처럼 오른팔을 제 목에 휘감았다.\n\n\"제대로 살아 보려는 거예요.\" 어린 에밀리가 말했다. \"이 사람이 우리한테 뭐라고 했는지 오빠는 몰라요. 외삼촌은 — 다들 — 외숙모는 알아요?\"\n\n패거티가 가엾다는 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"해 볼 거예요.\" 마사가 말했다. \"여기서 떠나게만 도와준다면요. 여기서 살아온 것보다 더 나쁘게 살 수는 없을 거예요. 어쩌면 더 나아질지도 몰라요. 아!\" 그녀가 끔찍하게 몸서리치며 말했다. \"이 거리에서 날 좀 빼내 줘, 어릴 적부터 온 마을이 날 다 아는 이 거리에서!\"\n\n에밀리가 햄에게 손을 내밀자, 나는 그가 그 손에 작은 천 주머니 하나를 쥐여 주는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 그것을 제 지갑인 줄 알았는지 받아 들고 한두 걸음 앞으로 나아갔으나, 잘못 안 것을 깨닫고는, 그가 물러나 내 곁에 서 있던 자리로 돌아와 그것을 그에게 보여 주었다.\n\n\"전부 네 거야, 에밀리.\" 나는 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들을 수 있었다. \"이 세상에 네 것이 아닌 건 내게 아무것도 없어, 내 사랑. 그 어떤 것도 나한텐 기쁨이 안 돼, 너를 위한 것만 빼고는!\"\n\n그녀의 눈에 다시 눈물이 차올랐지만, 그녀는 돌아서서 마사에게로 갔다. 그녀가 마사에게 무엇을 주었는지는 모르겠다. 나는 그녀가 마사 위로 몸을 숙여 그 품속에 돈을 넣어 주는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 무언가 속삭이며, 이만하면 되겠느냐고 물었다. \"넘치고도 남아.\" 상대가 말하며, 그녀의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었다.\n\n그러고는 마사가 일어나, 숄을 끌어당겨 몸을 감싸고, 그것으로 얼굴을 가린 채 소리 내어 울며 천천히 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그녀는 나가기 직전에 잠시 멈춰 서서, 마치 무언가 말하려 하거나 되돌아오려는 듯했으나, 입술 사이로는 한마디도 새어 나오지 않았다. 숄 속에서 그 낮고 음울하고 처량한 흐느낌을 똑같이 흘리며, 그녀는 떠나갔다.\n\n문이 닫히자, 어린 에밀리는 황급히 우리 셋을 둘러보더니, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느껴 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"그러지 마, 에밀리!\" 햄이 그녀의 어깨를 가만히 토닥이며 말했다. \"그러지 마, 내 사랑! 그렇게 울면 안 돼, 어여쁜 사람!\"\n\n\"아, 햄!\" 그녀가 여전히 가엾게 울며 외쳤다. \"난 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 착한 아이가 못 돼! 가끔은 마땅히 가져야 할 만큼 고마운 마음을 못 가질 때가 있다는 걸 나도 알아!\"\n\n\"아냐, 아냐, 넌 그래, 틀림없이.\" 햄이 말했다.\n\n\"아냐! 아냐! 아냐!\" 어린 에밀리가 흐느끼며 고개를 저으며 외쳤다. \"난 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 착한 아이가 못 돼. 어림도 없어! 어림도 없어!\" 그러고도 그녀는 마치 가슴이 미어지기라도 할 듯 계속 울었다.\n\n\"난 오빠 사랑을 너무 시험에 들게 해. 나도 알아!\" 그녀가 흐느꼈다. \"난 오빠한테 자주 짜증을 내고 변덕을 부려, 한참 다르게 굴어야 마땅한데. 오빠는 나한테 절대 그러지 않잖아. 난 어쩌자고 오빠한테 그러는 걸까, 오로지 어떻게 고마워하고 어떻게 오빠를 행복하게 해 줄지만 생각해야 마땅한데!\"\n\n\"넌 늘 날 행복하게 해 줘.\" 햄이 말했다. \"내 사랑! 난 널 보는 것만으로도 행복해. 난 하루 종일 네 생각만 해도 행복하다고.\"\n\n\"아! 그걸론 부족해!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"그건 오빠가 착해서지, 내가 착해서가 아냐! 아, 내 사랑, 오빠가 다른 누군가를 — 나보다 더 한결같고 훨씬 떳떳한 사람, 오빠한테 온통 마음을 쏟고, 나처럼 허영스럽거나 변덕스럽지 않은 사람을 좋아했더라면, 오빠한테 더 나은 복이 됐을 텐데!\"\n\n\"가엾은 여린 마음.\" 햄이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"마사가 이 아이를 완전히 뒤흔들어 놓았어요.\"\n\n\"제발요, 외숙모.\" 에밀리가 흐느꼈다. \"이리 오셔서 제가 머리를 기대게 해 주세요. 아, 오늘 밤은 너무 비참해요, 외숙모! 아, 난 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 착한 아이가 못 돼요. 그래요, 나도 알아요!\"\n\n패거티는 어느새 난롯불 앞 의자로 서둘러 가 있었다. 에밀리는 두 팔로 그녀의 목을 끌어안고 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇은 채, 더없이 간절하게 그 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"아, 제발요, 외숙모, 저를 좀 도와주세요! 햄, 오빠, 저를 좀 도와줘요! 데이비드 씨, 옛정을 봐서라도, 제발, 저를 좀 도와주세요! 저는 지금보다 더 나은 아이가 되고 싶어요. 지금보다 백 배는 더 고마운 마음을 느끼고 싶어요. 착한 사람의 아내가 되어 평온하게 사는 게 얼마나 복된 일인지 더 절실히 느끼고 싶어요. 아 어쩌나, 아 어쩌나! 아 내 마음, 내 마음이!\"\n\n그녀는 내 옛 유모의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고, 그 괴로움과 슬픔이 절반은 여인의 것이요 절반은 아이의 것이던 이 간청을 멈추고는 — 그녀의 모든 태도가 그러했듯이, 그 점에서 (내 생각에는) 다른 어떤 태도보다 더 자연스럽고 그 아름다움에 더 어울렸다 — 소리 없이 울었고, 내 옛 유모는 갓난아기를 달래듯 그녀를 어르고 달랬다.\n\n그녀는 차츰 진정되었고, 그러자 우리는 그녀를 다독였다. 때로는 기운을 북돋는 말을 하고, 때로는 가벼운 농담을 건네다 보니, 마침내 그녀가 고개를 들어 우리에게 말을 하기 시작했다. 그렇게 우리는 점점 나아갔고, 마침내 그녀는 미소 짓다가, 이내 웃다가, 좀 부끄러운 듯 일어나 앉을 수 있게 되었다. 그러는 동안 패거티는 그녀의 흐트러진 곱슬머리를 매만지고, 눈물을 닦아 주고, 다시 단정하게 다듬어 주었다. 집에 돌아갔을 때, 외삼촌이 어쩌자고 제 귀염둥이가 울었을까 의아해하지 않도록 말이다.\n\n그날 밤 나는 그녀가 전에는 한 번도 보지 못한 행동을 하는 것을 보았다. 나는 그녀가 천진하게 제가 택한 남편의 뺨에 입 맞추고, 그 우람한 몸에 마치 그것이 자기에게 가장 든든한 버팀목이라도 되는 양 바싹 다가서는 것을 보았다. 두 사람이 이지러져 가는 달빛 속을 함께 떠나갈 때, 나는 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보며 마음속으로 그 떠나가는 모습을 마사의 모습과 견주어 보았는데, 그녀가 두 손으로 그의 팔을 붙들고, 여전히 그에게 바싹 붙어 가는 것이 보였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 23. I CORROBORATE Mr. DICK, AND CHOOSE A PROFESSION\n\n아침에 잠에서 깨어났을 때 나는 어린 에밀리와, 어젯밤 마사가 떠난 뒤에 그 애가 보였던 격한 마음을 한참 동안 생각했다. 마치 내가 어떤 거룩한 믿음 속에서 그 집안의 약함과 다정함을 알게 된 것 같았고, 그것을 누군가에게 — 스티어포스에게라도 — 발설하는 건 잘못된 일이라는 느낌이 들었다. 내 어린 시절의 놀이 동무였던 그 어여쁜 아이만큼 부드러운 마음을 품게 하는 사람은 세상에 없었으며, 그때 내가 그 애를 진심으로 사랑했다고 나는 지금도 믿고 있고, 죽는 날까지 늘 그렇게 믿을 것이다. 어떤 우연 덕분에 그 애의 마음이 내 앞에 활짝 열렸을 때 그 애가 차마 억누르지 못하고 쏟아 낸 것을, 누군가의 귀에 — 스티어포스의 귀에라도 — 다시 옮긴다는 건, 나 자신에게도 어울리지 않고 그 애의 머리 둘레를 늘 감싸고 있다고 내가 느끼던 우리 순수한 어린 시절의 빛에도 어울리지 않는, 거친 짓이라는 생각이 들었다. 그래서 나는 그것을 내 가슴속에만 간직하기로 마음먹었다. 그러자 그 비밀은 내 가슴속에서 그 애의 모습에 새로운 우아함을 더해 주었다.\n\n아침을 먹는 동안 고모님이 보낸 편지 한 통이 나에게 전해졌다. 그 안에는 누구 못지않게 스티어포스가 조언해 줄 만한 일이 담겨 있었고, 그와 의논하면 분명 기쁠 거라는 것도 알았기에, 나는 집으로 돌아가는 길에 그것을 이야깃거리로 삼기로 했다. 당장은 모든 벗들과 작별하느라 할 일이 많았다. 바키스 씨는 우리가 떠나는 것을 아쉬워하는 사람들 가운데서도 결코 뒤지지 않았다. 내 생각에 그는 만약 그렇게 해서 우리를 야머스에 마흔여덟 시간만 더 붙잡아 둘 수 있었다면, 그 상자를 다시 열어 또 한 기니를 기꺼이 바쳤을 것이다. 패거티와 그 가족은 모두 우리가 떠나는 것에 슬픔으로 가득했다. 오머와 조램의 집안 전체가 우리를 배웅하러 나왔고, 우리 여행 가방을 마차로 옮길 때 스티어포스를 거들겠다고 나선 뱃사람 자원자가 어찌나 많던지, 우리가 연대 하나의 짐을 다 짊어지고 있었다 해도 그것을 나를 짐꾼이 모자라지 않았을 것이다. 한마디로, 우리는 관련된 모든 이들의 아쉬움과 흠모를 받으며 떠났고, 우리 뒤에는 무척이나 서운해하는 사람들을 잔뜩 남겨 두었다.\n\n\"리티머, 자네는 여기 오래 머무나?\" 마차가 출발하는 것을 지켜보려고 서서 기다리는 그에게 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"아마 그리 오래는 아닐 겁니다, 도련님.\"\n\n\"지금 당장은 그도 잘 모를 거야.\" 스티어포스가 무심하게 말했다. \"자기가 해야 할 일을 알고 있고, 그걸 해낼 테니까.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그러겠지.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n리티머는 나를 좋게 봐 준 데에 답하듯 모자에 손을 댔는데, 나는 내가 여덟 살쯤 된 어린애가 된 기분이었다. 그는 우리에게 즐거운 여행을 빌며 다시 한 번 모자에 손을 댔고, 우리는 그를 인도에 세워 둔 채 떠났으니, 그는 이집트의 어느 피라미드 못지않게 점잖은 수수께끼였다.\n\n한동안 우리는 아무 대화도 나누지 않았다. 스티어포스는 유난히 말이 없었고, 나는 나대로 그 옛 장소들을 언제 다시 보게 될지, 그동안 나에게나 그곳에 어떤 새로운 변화가 닥칠지 속으로 골똘히 생각하느라 정신이 팔려 있었다. 마침내 스티어포스가, 언제든 마음만 먹으면 무엇이든 될 수 있는 사람답게 순식간에 명랑하고 수다스러워지더니, 내 팔을 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"입 좀 떼 봐, 데이비드. 아침에 말하던 그 편지는 무슨 얘기야?\"\n\n\"아!\" 내가 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내며 말했다. \"고모님이 보내신 거야.\"\n\n\"그래, 고려해 볼 만한 게 뭐라고 쓰셨는데?\"\n\n\"그게, 스티어포스, 고모님이 나한테 일깨워 주시는 거야.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 이번 여행을 떠난 건 주위를 둘러보고 좀 생각해 보라는 뜻이었다고 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래서 당연히 둘러보고 생각도 했겠지?\"\n\n\"솔직히 딱히 그랬다고는 못 하겠어. 사실대로 말하자면, 그걸 까맣게 잊고 있었던 것 같아.\"\n\n\"그럼! 지금이라도 주위를 둘러보고 게으름을 만회하라고.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"오른쪽을 봐, 늪이 잔뜩 깔린 평평한 들판이 보일 거야. 왼쪽을 봐, 똑같은 게 보이지. 앞을 봐, 다를 게 없어. 뒤를 봐, 거기도 마찬가지고.\" 나는 웃으면서, 이 풍경을 통틀어 봐도 나한테 어울릴 만한 직업이 하나도 안 보인다고, 아마 이 평평함 탓인 것 같다고 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그 문제에 대해선 우리 고모님이 뭐라셔?\" 스티어포스가 내 손에 든 편지를 흘끗 보며 물었다. \"뭔가 제안하시는 게 있어?\"\n\n\"응, 있어.\" 내가 말했다. \"여기서, 내가 송무대리인이 되면 좋겠다고 생각하는지 물어보셔. 넌 어떻게 생각해?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 잘 모르겠는데.\" 스티어포스가 무덤덤하게 대답했다. \"다른 거나 마찬가지로 그것도 해 볼 만하지 않을까 싶다만?\"\n\n그가 모든 직업과 천직을 그렇게 똑같이 저울질하는 통에 나는 또 웃지 않을 수 없었고, 그 말을 그에게 그대로 해 주었다.\n\n\"송무대리인이 뭐야, 스티어포스?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그게 말이지, 수도승 같은 변호사라고나 할까.\" 스티어포스가 대답했다. \"변호사가 보통법 법정이나 형평법 법정에서 하는 일을, 그 사람은 박사회관에서 열리는 한물간 몇몇 법정에서 하는 거야 — 박사회관이라는 데가 세인트폴 묘지 근처에 있는 게으른 옛 구석탱이지. 자연스러운 이치대로라면 그런 직업은 한 이백 년 전쯤에 사라졌어야 할 거야. 그가 뭐 하는 사람인지는, 박사회관이 뭔지를 말해 주면 가장 잘 알 수 있을 거야. 외진 자그마한 곳인데, 거기서 이른바 교회법이라는 걸 다루면서, 세상 사람들 사분의 삼은 들어 본 적도 없고 나머지 사분의 일은 에드워드 왕들 시절에 화석 상태로 발굴된 거라고 여기는, 케케묵은 의회 제정법이라는 낡은 괴물들을 가지고 온갖 재주를 부려. 사람들의 유언이며 혼인에 관한 소송, 그리고 배와 보트끼리의 분쟁을 두고 예부터 독점권을 쥐고 있는 데지.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼, 스티어포스!\" 내가 외쳤다. \"설마 뱃일하고 교회 일 사이에 무슨 연관이 있다는 얘기는 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"아무렴, 그건 아니지, 이 친구야.\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"내 말은, 바로 그 박사회관에서 같은 사람들이 그 두 가지를 다 맡아 처리하고 판결한다는 거야. 어느 날 자네가 거기 가 보면, '낸시'호가 '세라 제인'호를 들이받았다든가, 아니면 패거티 씨와 야머스 뱃사람들이 폭풍을 뚫고 닻과 밧줄을 들고 조난당한 인도 무역선 '넬슨'호로 출동했다든가 하는 사건을 두고, 그들이 영 사전에 실린 항해 용어의 절반을 더듬더듬 헤매는 꼴을 보게 될 거야. 또 어느 날 가 보면, 행실이 나빴던 어느 성직자를 둘러싸고 찬반 양쪽 증언에 푹 빠져 있는 모습을 보게 될 텐데, 항해 사건의 판사가 성직자 사건의 변호인이거나, 아니면 그 반대거나 하단 말이지. 그들은 배우들 같아. 어떤 때는 판사였다가 어떤 때는 판사가 아니고, 어떤 때는 이것이었다가 어떤 때는 저것이고, 또 어떤 때는 전혀 다른 무언가가 돼서 서로 돌아가며 바뀐단 말이야. 하지만 그건 언제나 아주 즐겁고 짭짤한, 유난히 선택받은 소수의 관객 앞에서 펼쳐지는 자그마한 아마추어 연극이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 변호인하고 송무대리인이 같은 사람은 아니지?\" 내가 조금 어리둥절해서 물었다. \"그렇지?\"\n\n\"아니야.\" 스티어포스가 대답했다. \"변호인은 민법학자들이야 — 대학에서 박사 학위를 딴 사람들이지 — 내가 이런 걸 조금이나마 아는 것도 바로 그 때문이고. 송무대리인이 변호인을 고용하는 거야. 양쪽 다 아주 두둑한 수임료를 챙기니까, 다 합쳐서 보면 굉장히 아늑하고 자그마한 패거리인 셈이지. 전체적으로 보면, 데이비드, 나는 자네가 박사회관에 마음 붙이고 들어가는 걸 권하겠어. 위안이 될지 모르겠다만, 거기 사람들은 자기네 점잖음을 무척이나 자랑스러워한다고.\"\n\n나는 스티어포스가 이 문제를 가볍게 다루는 투를 감안했고, 또 '세인트폴 묘지 근처의 그 게으른 옛 구석탱이'에서 내가 연상하던 차분하고 진중하며 고풍스러운 분위기와 견주어 보니, 고모님의 제안이 영 싫지만은 않았다. 고모님은 그 결정을 온전히 내 자유에 맡기면서, 얼마 전에 내 앞으로 유언을 정리하려고 박사회관에 있는 당신의 송무대리인을 찾아갔다가 그 생각이 떠올랐노라고 거리낌 없이 말해 주었다.\n\n\"어쨌거나 그건 우리 고모님으로서는 칭찬받을 만한 처사로구먼.\" 내가 그 이야기를 꺼내자 스티어포스가 말했다. \"한껏 북돋아 줄 만한 일이고. 데이지, 내 충고는 자네가 박사회관에 기꺼이 마음을 붙이라는 거야.\"\n\n나는 그렇게 하기로 단단히 마음을 정했다. 그러고 나서 나는 스티어포스에게, (편지를 보고 알았는데) 고모님이 나를 기다리느라 시내에 와 계시며, 링컨스 인 광장에 있는 어느 사설 호텔 같은 곳에 일주일 묵을 방을 잡으셨는데, 거기에는 돌계단과 지붕으로 통하는 편리한 문이 있다고 말해 주었다. 고모님은 런던의 집이란 집은 죄다 밤마다 불에 타 버릴 거라고 굳게 믿는 분이었으니까.\n\n우리는 이따금 박사회관 이야기로 돌아가, 내가 거기서 송무대리인이 될 먼 훗날을 그려 보며 즐겁게 남은 여정을 마쳤다. 스티어포스가 그 모습을 우스꽝스럽고 별난 갖가지 빛깔로 그려 내는 통에 우리는 둘 다 유쾌해졌다. 여정의 끝에 다다르자 그는 모레 나를 찾아오기로 약속하고 집으로 갔고, 나는 링컨스 인 광장으로 마차를 몰았다. 거기서 나는 아직 잠자리에 들지 않고 저녁상을 차려 놓고 기다리는 고모님을 만났다.\n\n설령 헤어진 뒤로 내가 세계를 한 바퀴 돌고 왔다 해도 우리가 다시 만나 이보다 더 기뻐할 수는 없었을 것이다. 고모님은 나를 끌어안으며 대놓고 울었다. 그러고는 웃는 척하면서, 만약 내 가엾은 어머니가 살아 있었다면 그 어리석은 작은 것이 분명 눈물을 흘렸을 거라고 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 딕 씨는 두고 오신 거예요, 고모님?\" 내가 말했다. \"그거 안됐네요. 아, 재닛, 잘 지냈어?\"\n\n재닛이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 내 안부를 묻는 사이, 나는 고모님의 얼굴이 몹시 길어지는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"나도 그게 안됐다.\" 고모님이 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"여기 온 뒤로 마음이 도무지 편치를 않구나, 트롯.\" 내가 왜냐고 물어보기도 전에, 고모님은 이유를 털어놓았다.\n\n\"내가 확신하건대.\" 고모님이 우울한 단호함으로 한 손을 식탁 위에 올려놓으며 말했다. \"딕은 당나귀를 쫓아낼 만한 성격이 못 돼. 의지가 모자라는 게 분명해. 차라리 재닛을 집에 두고 올걸 그랬어, 그랬으면 내 마음이 좀 편했을지도 모르는데. 만약 내 잔디밭에 함부로 들어온 당나귀가 한 마리라도 있었다면,\" 고모님이 힘주어 말했다. \"바로 오늘 오후 네 시에 한 마리가 있었던 거야. 머리끝부터 발끝까지 서늘한 기운이 끼쳐 왔으니, 그게 당나귀라는 걸 난 알아!\"\n\n나는 이 문제로 고모님을 달래 보려 했지만, 고모님은 위로를 받아들이지 않았다.\n\n\"그건 당나귀였어.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"게다가 그 살인자 같은 여편네의 누이가 우리 집에 왔을 때 타고 온, 꼬리가 뭉툭한 바로 그 당나귀였다고.\" 그 후로 줄곧 고모님이 머드스톤 양을 부르는 이름은 이것뿐이었다. \"도버에 있는 당나귀 가운데 그 어떤 놈의 뻔뻔함보다도 내가 더 견디기 힘든 놈이 있다면, 바로,\" 고모님이 식탁을 내리치며 말했다. \"그 짐승이야!\"\n\n재닛이 조심스레, 마님께서 괜한 일로 속을 끓이시는 것 같다고, 그 당나귀로 말하자면 지금 모래와 자갈을 나르는 일에 매여 있어서 남의 땅을 함부로 침범할 처지가 못 될 거라고 넌지시 말했다. 하지만 고모님은 들으려고도 하지 않았다.\n\n저녁상은 고모님 방이 무척 높은 데 있는데도 — 같은 돈으로 돌계단을 더 많이 누리려는 건지, 아니면 지붕의 문에 더 가까이 있으려는 건지는 모르겠지만 — 따뜻하고 편안하게 차려졌고, 닭구이와 스테이크, 그리고 채소 몇 가지가 있었는데, 나는 그 모든 것을 양껏 먹어 치웠고 죄다 훌륭했다. 그러나 고모님은 런던 음식에 대해 나름의 견해가 있어서 거의 들지 않았다.\n\n\"이 가엾은 닭은 십중팔구 지하실에서 나고 자랐을 거다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그리고 삯마차 정거장 말고는 한 번도 바깥바람을 쐰 적이 없었겠지. 이 스테이크가 정말 소고기이기를 바라지만, 난 못 믿겠어. 내 생각엔 이곳에서 진짜인 건 더러움뿐이야.\"\n\n\"고모님, 이 닭이 시골에서 왔을 수도 있지 않을까요?\" 내가 슬쩍 운을 띄웠다.\n\n\"천만에.\" 고모님이 받아쳤다. \"런던 장사꾼한테는, 자기가 그렇다고 내세운 그대로의 물건을 파는 일만큼 재미없는 게 없거든.\"\n\n나는 감히 이 견해에 반박하지 못했지만, 저녁만큼은 푸짐하게 먹었고, 내가 그러는 모습을 보고 고모님은 무척이나 흡족해했다. 상이 치워지자 재닛이 고모님을 거들어 머리를 매만지고, 평소보다 더 멋진 모양새의 잠자리 모자를 (고모님 말로는 '불이 날 때를 대비해서'라고 했다) 씌워 드리고, 무릎 위로 치맛자락을 접어 올려 드렸는데, 이것이 잠자리에 들기 전에 몸을 데우려는 고모님의 평소 채비였다. 그러고 나서 나는, 아무리 사소한 일탈도 결코 용납되지 않는 어떤 정해진 규칙에 따라, 고모님께 따뜻한 포도주 물 한 잔과 길고 가늘게 썬 토스트 한 조각을 만들어 드렸다. 이런 것들을 곁들이고 우리 둘만 남아 저녁 시간을 마무리했는데, 고모님은 내 맞은편에 앉아 포도주 물을 마시면서, 토스트 조각을 하나씩 거기에 적셔 먹고는, 잠자리 모자의 테두리 사이로 나를 인자하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 트롯.\" 고모님이 입을 열었다. \"그 송무대리인 계획을 어떻게 생각하니? 아니면 아직 생각해 보지도 않았니?\"\n\n\"아주 많이 생각해 봤어요, 고모님. 그리고 스티어포스하고도 그 얘기를 많이 나눴고요. 정말로 마음에 들어요. 더할 나위 없이 마음에 들어요.\"\n\n\"그래!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그거 반가운 소리구나!\"\n\n\"다만 한 가지 걸리는 게 있어요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔지 말해 보렴, 트롯.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그게요, 고모님, 제가 듣기로 이게 한정된 직업인 것 같아서 여쭤보고 싶은데요, 거기 들어가는 데에 돈이 아주 많이 들지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"네 견습 계약을 맺는 데에,\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"딱 천 파운드가 들 거다.\"\n\n\"이거 보세요, 고모님.\" 내가 의자를 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다. \"그 점이 제 마음을 영 불편하게 해요. 큰돈이잖아요. 고모님은 제 교육에 이미 큰돈을 쓰셨고, 모든 일에서 늘 더없이 너그럽게 대해 주셨어요. 그야말로 관대함의 화신이셨죠. 분명 거의 돈을 들이지 않고도 인생을 시작할 수 있는 길, 그러면서도 결심과 노력으로 잘 풀려 나갈 좋은 희망을 품고 출발할 길이 있을 거예요. 그쪽을 한번 시도해 보는 게 더 낫지 않을까요? 그렇게 큰돈을 내놓으셔도 정말 괜찮으신 건지, 그렇게 쓰는 게 옳은 일인지 확신하세요? 그저 부탁드리는 거예요, 제 두 번째 어머니, 한번 생각해 봐 주세요. 정말 확신하세요?\"\n\n고모님은 그때 먹고 있던 토스트 조각을 마저 다 먹으면서, 그러는 내내 내 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다. 그러고는 잔을 벽난로 선반에 내려놓고, 접은 치마 위에 두 손을 포갠 채, 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"트롯, 얘야, 만약 내 인생에 무슨 목적이 있다면, 그건 네가 착하고, 분별 있고, 행복한 사람이 되도록 뒷바라지하는 거야. 난 거기에 마음을 다 쏟고 있어 — 딕도 마찬가지고. 내가 아는 몇몇 사람들이 이 문제에 관한 딕의 이야기를 들어 봤으면 좋겠구나. 그 명민함이 놀랍거든. 하지만 그 사람 머릿속에 든 자산을 아는 사람은 나 말고는 아무도 없지!\"\n\n고모님은 내 손을 두 손 사이에 잡으려고 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이어 갔다.\n\n\"트롯, 지난날을 떠올리는 건, 그것이 지금에 어떤 영향을 끼치지 못한다면 다 헛된 일이야. 어쩌면 난 네 가엾은 아버지하고 더 좋은 사이로 지낼 수 있었을지도 몰라. 어쩌면 네 누이 베시 트롯우드가 나를 실망시킨 뒤에라도, 네 어머니라는 그 가엾은 아이하고 더 좋은 사이로 지낼 수 있었을지도 모르고. 네가 먼지투성이에 길에 지친 채로 달아나 온 어린 사내애가 되어 나를 찾아왔을 때, 어쩌면 난 그런 생각을 했던 것 같다. 그때부터 지금까지, 트롯, 너는 늘 나의 자랑이자 긍지이자 기쁨이었어. 나에겐 내 재산에 대한 다른 권리 주장자가 없단다. 적어도\" — 여기서 놀랍게도 고모님은 머뭇거리며 당황했다 — \"아니, 내 재산에 대한 다른 권리 주장자는 없어 — 그리고 너는 내 양자야. 그저 늘그막의 나에게 다정한 자식이 되어 주고, 내 변덕과 별난 생각들을 참아 주기만 하렴. 그러면 한창때가 마땅히 그랬어야 할 만큼 행복하지도 다정하지도 못했던 한 늙은 여자에게, 그 늙은 여자가 너에게 해 준 것보다 더 많은 걸 해 주는 셈이 될 거야.\"\n\n고모님이 자신의 지난 내력을 언급하는 것을 들은 건 그때가 처음이었다. 그것을 조용히 꺼냈다가 이내 떨쳐 버리는 그 태도에는 어떤 너그러운 기품이 있어서, 무엇으로든 더 높일 수 있었다면, 그것은 내 마음속에서 고모님에 대한 존경과 애정을 한층 드높였을 것이다.\n\n\"이제 우리 사이에 모든 게 합의되고 이해됐으니, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 얘긴 더 할 필요가 없겠다. 자, 입 한번 맞춰 주렴. 내일 아침 먹고 나서 같이 박사회관에 가 보자꾸나.\"\n\n우리는 잠자리에 들기 전에 난롯가에서 오래도록 이야기를 나누었다. 나는 고모님 방과 같은 층에 있는 방에서 잤는데, 밤중에 멀리서 삯마차나 시장 수레 소리가 들려와 마음이 들썩일 때마다 고모님이 내 방문을 두드리며 \"불자동차 소리 들리지 않니?\" 하고 물어 대는 통에 잠을 좀 설쳤다. 그래도 새벽녘이 되자 고모님은 한결 푹 주무셨고, 나도 그러게 내버려 두었다.\n\n정오쯤 우리는 박사회관에 있는 스펜로 앤 조킨스 사무소를 향해 길을 나섰다. 고모님은 런던에 관해 또 하나의 일반적인 견해를 품고 있었는데, 눈에 띄는 사람마다 죄다 소매치기라는 것이었다. 그래서 고모님은 십 기니와 은화 몇 닢이 든 지갑을 나더러 대신 들고 다니라고 건네주었다.\n\n우리는 세인트 던스턴 성당의 거인들이 종을 치는 모습을 보려고 플리트 가의 장난감 가게 앞에 잠시 걸음을 멈추었다 — 그것을 마침 보려고 시간을 맞춰 열두 시에 갔던 것이다 — 그러고는 러드게이트 언덕과 세인트폴 묘지 쪽으로 계속 나아갔다. 우리가 러드게이트 언덕 쪽으로 길을 건너고 있을 때, 나는 고모님이 갑자기 걸음을 부쩍 빨리하면서 겁먹은 표정을 짓는 것을 알아챘다. 동시에, 조금 전 지나가다 멈춰 서서 우리를 빤히 쳐다보던 음침하고 허름한 차림의 사내가 우리 바로 뒤까지 바짝 따라붙어 고모님 몸에 스칠 듯 다가오는 것이 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n\"트롯! 얘, 트롯!\" 고모님이 겁에 질린 목소리로 속삭이며 내 팔을 꽉 붙들고 외쳤다. \"어쩌면 좋니, 어쩌면 좋아.\"\n\n\"놀라지 마세요.\" 내가 말했다. \"겁낼 거 하나도 없어요. 가게 안으로 들어가세요. 제가 금방 이 작자를 떼어 놓을게요.\"\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼, 얘야!\" 고모님이 받아쳤다. \"절대로 저 사람한테 말 걸지 마라. 부탁이다, 명령이야!\"\n\n\"맙소사, 고모님!\" 내가 말했다. \"그냥 억센 거지일 뿐이잖아요.\"\n\n\"네가 저 사람이 뭐 하는 자인지 몰라서 그래!\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"저 사람이 누군지 모르잖니! 네가 무슨 소리를 하는지도 모르고!\"\n\n이렇게 말이 오가는 동안 우리는 텅 빈 어느 문간에 멈춰 서 있었는데, 그 사내도 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"저 사람 쳐다보지 마라!\" 내가 분이 나서 고개를 돌리자 고모님이 말했다. \"그러지 말고, 얘야, 마차나 한 대 잡아 주고, 세인트폴 묘지에서 나를 기다려 다오.\"\n\n\"고모님을 기다리라고요?\" 내가 되물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 고모님이 받았다. \"나 혼자 가야 해. 저 사람하고 같이 가야 한다고.\"\n\n\"저 사람하고요, 고모님? 저 남자하고요?\"\n\n\"나 정신 멀쩡하다.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"그러니 가야 한다고 말하는 거야. 마차나 잡아 다오!\"\n\n내가 아무리 놀랐다 한들, 그토록 단호한 명령을 거역할 권리가 나에게 없다는 것쯤은 알았다. 나는 몇 걸음 부리나케 달려가, 빈 채로 지나가던 삯마차 한 대를 불러 세웠다. 내가 미처 발판을 내리기도 전에, 고모님은 어떻게 했는지 모르게 훌쩍 마차에 올랐고, 그 사내가 뒤따라 올랐다. 고모님이 어찌나 간절하게 손짓으로 나더러 가라고 하던지, 나는 어리벙벙한 와중에도 곧장 그들에게서 돌아섰다. 그러다가, 고모님이 마부에게 \"아무 데로나 갑시다! 곧장 달려요!\" 하고 말하는 소리를 들었고, 이내 그 마차가 언덕을 올라가며 내 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n딕 씨가 나에게 했던 이야기, 그리고 내가 그의 망상이려니 했던 그 이야기가 이제 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 이 사람이 바로 그가 그토록 알쏭달쏭하게 입에 올렸던 그 사람이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 다만 그가 고모님을 옭아쥐고 있는 그 힘의 정체가 도대체 무엇인지는 도무지 짐작조차 할 수 없었다. 묘지에서 삼십 분쯤 마음을 가라앉히며 기다리고 있으려니, 그 마차가 돌아오는 것이 보였다. 마부가 내 곁에 멈춰 섰는데, 고모님은 그 안에 홀로 앉아 있었다.\n\n고모님은 아직 흔들린 마음을 충분히 추스르지 못해, 우리가 가야 할 방문을 치를 채비가 되어 있지 않았다. 고모님은 나더러 마차에 타라고 하고는, 마부에게 잠시 천천히 오르내려 달라고 이르게 했다. 고모님은 \"얘야, 그게 무슨 일이었는지 절대 묻지 말고, 다시는 입에 올리지도 말아라\" 하는 말 외에는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 마음이 완전히 평정을 되찾고 나서야, 이제 다 괜찮아졌으니 내려도 되겠다고 말했다. 마부에게 삯을 치르라고 고모님이 지갑을 건넸을 때, 나는 기니가 죄다 사라지고 잔돈 은화만 남아 있는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n박사회관으로 들어가는 길에는 야트막한 작은 아치문 하나가 있었다. 그 너머의 거리로 몇 걸음 채 들어서기도 전에, 도시의 소음은 마법에라도 걸린 듯 부드럽게 멀어지며 녹아 사라지는 것 같았다. 흐릿한 안마당 몇 개와 좁은 길들을 지나니 천장으로 빛이 드는 스펜로 앤 조킨스 사무소가 나왔는데, 노크라는 격식 없이도 순례자들이 드나들 수 있는 이 신전의 현관에서는 서기 서너 명이 필사 일을 하고 있었다. 그중 한 사람, 진저브레드로 만든 것처럼 보이는 빳빳한 갈색 가발을 쓰고 혼자 떨어져 앉아 있던 자그마하고 메마른 남자가 일어나 고모님을 맞이하고는, 우리를 스펜로 씨의 방으로 안내했다.\n\n\"스펜로 씨는 지금 법정에 계십니다, 부인.\" 그 메마른 남자가 말했다. \"오늘은 아치스 법정이 열리는 날이거든요. 하지만 바로 코앞이라, 곧장 사람을 보내 모셔 오겠습니다.\"\n\n스펜로 씨를 불러오는 동안 우리는 알아서 주위를 둘러보도록 남겨졌고, 나는 그 기회를 한껏 활용했다. 방의 가구는 구식에다 먼지투성이였고, 필기용 책상 위에 깔린 초록색 모직 천은 빛깔이 다 바래, 늙은 빈민처럼 시들고 핏기 없어 보였다. 그 위에는 서류 뭉치가 잔뜩 쌓여 있었는데, 더러는 '주장서'라 적혀 있고, 더러는 (놀랍게도) '명예훼손소장'이라 적혀 있었으며, 더러는 종교법원, 더러는 아치스 법원, 더러는 유언검인법원, 더러는 해사법원, 또 더러는 위임법원에 속하는 것이라 적혀 있어서, 나는 도대체 법정이 통틀어 몇 개나 되며 그것을 죄다 이해하는 데 얼마나 걸릴까 하고 무척이나 궁금해했다. 이것들 말고도, 선서 진술로 받은 어마어마한 양의 증거 필사본 여러 권이 단단히 제본된 채, 사건마다 한 묶음씩 묵직하게 끈으로 묶여 있었는데, 마치 사건 하나하나가 열 권 스무 권에 걸친 한 편의 역사이기라도 한 것 같았다. 이 모든 게 꽤 돈이 들어 보인다는 생각이 들었고, 송무대리인의 일에 대해 흐뭇한 인상을 안겨 주었다. 이것들을 비롯한 여러 비슷한 물건들 위로 점점 흡족한 마음을 담아 눈길을 옮기고 있는데, 바깥방에서 다급한 발소리가 들리더니, 스펜로 씨가 흰 모피로 가장자리를 두른 검은 법복 차림으로 들어오면서 모자를 벗으며 황급히 들어왔다.\n\n그는 머리가 옅고 자그마한 신사였는데, 나무랄 데 없는 장화에, 더없이 빳빳한 흰 넥타이와 셔츠 깃을 두르고 있었다. 그는 위까지 단추를 야무지고 꼭 끼게 채우고 있었고, 정확하게 곱슬곱슬 말려 있는 구레나룻에 어지간히 공을 들였음이 틀림없었다. 금시계 줄이 어찌나 묵직하던지, 금박장이 가게 위에 걸려 있는 것 같은 우람한 황금 팔이라도 있어야 그것을 끌어낼 수 있겠다는 엉뚱한 생각이 들 정도였다. 그는 어찌나 정성껏 차려입고 또 어찌나 뻣뻣하던지 몸을 거의 굽히지 못해서, 의자에 앉은 뒤 책상 위 서류를 흘끗 보려 할 때면 펀치 인형처럼 척추 밑동부터 몸 전체를 통째로 움직여야만 했다.\n\n나는 앞서 고모님의 소개를 받았고, 정중한 대접을 받은 터였다. 그가 이제 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그래서, 코퍼필드 군, 자네는 우리 직업에 들어올 생각이로군? 며칠 전에 트롯우드 양과 면담하는 기쁨을 누렸을 때, 마침 이곳에 빈자리가 하나 있다고 지나가는 말로 언급했더랬지.\" — 다시 한 번 몸을 굽히면서 — 또 펀치 인형이었다 — \"그랬더니 트롯우드 양께서 친절하게도, 당신이 각별히 돌보는 조카가 하나 있는데, 그 아이가 인생을 점잖게 꾸려 나가도록 마련해 주려 한다고 말씀하셨네. 그 조카를, 내 생각에, 지금 이렇게 만나는 기쁨을 누리는 것 같은데\" — 또 펀치 인형이었다. 나는 고개를 숙여 감사를 표하며, 고모님께 그런 자리가 났다는 이야기를 들었고, 나 역시 그 일이 무척 마음에 들 것 같다고 말했다. 거기에 강하게 끌리는 마음이 들어 그 제안을 곧바로 받아들였노라고. 다만 그 일에 대해 좀 더 알기 전에는 좋아할 거라고 딱 잘라 장담할 수는 없다고. 비록 형식적인 절차에 지나지 않을지라도, 돌이킬 수 없게 거기에 매이기 전에 그 일이 나에게 맞는지 한번 가늠해 볼 기회가 있으리라 생각한다고 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그럼요! 그럼요!\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 이곳에서 늘 한 달을 — 시범 삼아 한 달을 두자고 제안한다네. 나로서야 두 달을 — 세 달을 — 사실 무기한이라도 기꺼이 제안하고 싶네만, 나에겐 동업자가 있어서 말이야. 조킨스 씨라고.\"\n\n\"그리고 사례금은요,\" 내가 받았다. \"천 파운드인가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 사례금은 인지대를 포함해서 천 파운드일세.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"내가 트롯우드 양께도 말씀드렸다시피, 나는 돈을 밝히는 마음에서 움직이는 사람이 아니야. 나만큼 그런 사람도 드물 거라고 믿네만, 조킨스 씨는 이런 문제에 자기 나름의 견해가 있고, 나로서는 조킨스 씨의 견해를 존중하지 않을 수가 없거든. 한마디로, 조킨스 씨는 천 파운드도 너무 적다고 생각하지.\"\n\n\"제 생각에는요,\" 내가 여전히 고모님의 부담을 덜어 드리고 싶은 마음에 말했다. \"만약 견습 서기가 유난히 쓸모가 있어서 자기 일을 완벽하게 통달하게 된다면 — \" 마치 나 자신을 칭찬하는 꼴 같아 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다 — \"견습 기간의 후반부에 가서는 그에게 무언가 — 그런 관례는 여기에 없겠지요?\"\n\n스펜로 씨는 안간힘을 써서, 넥타이 밖으로 고개를 겨우 가로저을 수 있을 만큼만 빼 들고는, '봉급'이라는 말을 미리 앞질러 대답했다.\n\n\"없네. 만약 내가 매인 데가 없다면 그 점에 대해 내가 어떤 배려를 베풀지는 말하지 않겠네, 코퍼필드 군. 조킨스 씨는 꿈쩍도 안 하니까.\"\n\n나는 이 무시무시한 조킨스라는 사람이라는 생각에 적잖이 기가 죽었다. 그러나 나중에 알고 보니, 그는 굼뜬 기질의 온순한 사람으로, 이 사업에서 그가 맡은 역할이란 늘 뒤로 물러나 있으면서 가장 고집 세고 무자비한 사람으로 그 이름만 끊임없이 내세워지는 것이었다. 어느 서기가 봉급을 올려 달라고 하면, 조킨스 씨는 그런 제안 따위는 들으려고도 하지 않는다. 어느 의뢰인이 소송 비용 계산서를 늑장 부리며 치르지 않으면, 조킨스 씨가 기어이 받아 내고야 만다. 그리고 이런 일들이 스펜로 씨의 마음에 아무리 괴로웠다 한들 (실제로 늘 괴로웠다), 조킨스 씨는 자기 몫의 빚을 끝내 챙긴다. 착한 천사 스펜로의 마음과 손은 늘 활짝 열려 있었을 테지만, 그것을 가로막는 악마 조킨스가 있었던 것이다. 나이가 들면서 보니, 스펜로 앤 조킨스의 방식대로 일을 꾸려 가는 다른 사업체들도 여럿 겪어 본 것 같다!\n\n내가 원하는 대로 곧 한 달간의 시험 기간을 시작하기로 정해졌고, 내가 당사자가 될 계약서는 고모님 댁으로 손쉽게 부쳐 서명을 받을 수 있으니, 고모님은 시내에 더 머물 필요도, 시험 기간이 끝날 때 다시 올 필요도 없게 되었다. 이야기가 여기까지 이르자, 스펜로 씨는 지금 당장 나를 법정으로 데려가 그곳이 어떤 곳인지 보여 주겠다고 했다. 나도 알고 싶은 마음이 충분했기에, 우리는 그러려고 밖으로 나섰고, 고모님은 뒤에 남겨 두었다. 고모님은 그런 곳에는 도무지 발을 들여놓을 마음이 없다고 했는데, 내 생각에 고모님은 모든 법정이라는 것을 언제든 터져 버릴 수 있는 일종의 화약 공장쯤으로 여기는 것 같았다.\n\n스펜로 씨는 근엄한 벽돌집들로 둘러싸인 포장된 안마당을 지나 나를 안내했는데, 나는 그 문마다 적힌 박사들의 이름으로 미루어, 거기가 스티어포스가 말해 주었던 그 박식한 변호인들의 공식 거처라고 짐작했다. 그러고는 왼편에 있는, 내가 보기에 예배당과 어딘가 비슷한 크고 음침한 방으로 들어갔다. 이 방의 윗부분은 나머지 공간과 칸막이로 나뉘어 있었는데, 거기 말굽 모양으로 한 단 높인 단상의 양옆으로, 구식의 안락한 식당 의자에 빨간 법복과 회색 가발 차림의 신사 여럿이 앉아 있었으니, 알고 보니 그들이 바로 앞서 말한 박사들이었다. 말굽의 곡선 부분에서, 설교단 책상 같은 작은 책상 너머로 눈을 끔벅거리고 있던 한 노신사는, 내가 만약 새장 안에서 보았더라면 영락없이 올빼미로 여겼을 테지만, 알고 보니 그가 재판을 주재하는 판사였다. 말굽 안쪽의, 이들보다 낮은 공간, 그러니까 거의 바닥 높이쯤 되는 자리에는, 스펜로 씨와 같은 신분에다 그처럼 흰 모피를 두른 검은 법복 차림의 다른 신사 여럿이 길쭉한 초록색 탁자에 앉아 있었다. 내 생각에 그들의 넥타이는 대체로 빳빳했고 표정은 거만해 보였다. 그러나 이 마지막 점에 대해서는 곧 내가 그들을 잘못 본 것이라는 생각이 들었으니, 그들 가운데 두세 명이 일어나 재판장의 질문에 답해야 했을 때, 나는 그보다 더 쭈뼛거리는 모습을 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 목도리를 두른 소년 하나와, 슬그머니 외투 주머니에서 빵 부스러기를 꺼내 먹고 있는 점잖은 척하는 초라한 사내 하나로 대표되는 방청객들은, 법정 한가운데 놓인 난로에 몸을 녹이고 있었다. 그곳의 나른한 고요는 오직 이 난롯불이 타닥타닥 지저귀는 소리와, 증거의 완벽한 도서관을 천천히 헤매고 다니다가 이따금 길가의 자그마한 논거 여인숙에 들러 쉬어 가곤 하던 어느 박사의 목소리에만 깨질 뿐이었다. 다 통틀어, 나는 평생 그 어느 자리에서도 그토록 아늑하고, 졸리고, 구식이고, 세월에 잊히고, 꾸벅꾸벅 졸음에 겨운 자그마한 한 가족 모임의 일원이 되어 본 적이 없었다. 그래서 나는 — 어쩌면 소송 당사자로서만 빼고 — 어떤 자격으로든 거기에 끼어든다면 그것이야말로 마음을 달래 주는 아편 같은 일이 되리라는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n이 은신처의 꿈결 같은 분위기에 무척 흡족해진 나는, 오늘은 이만하면 충분히 봤다고 스펜로 씨에게 알리고는, 고모님과 다시 합류했다. 그러고는 이내 고모님과 함께 박사회관을 떠났는데, 스펜로 앤 조킨스 사무소를 나설 때 서기들이 펜으로 서로를 콕콕 찌르며 나를 가리키는 통에 나는 무척 어린애가 된 기분이었다.\n\n우리는 손수레에 매인 운 나쁜 당나귀 한 마리와 마주친 것 말고는 별다른 새 사건 없이 링컨스 인 광장에 다다랐는데, 그 당나귀는 고모님에게 괴로운 연상을 불러일으켰다. 무사히 숙소에 들고 나서 우리는 내 계획에 대해 다시 한참 이야기를 나누었다. 나는 고모님이 어서 집에 돌아가고 싶어 한다는 것을, 그리고 불과 음식과 소매치기 사이에서 런던에서는 단 반 시간도 마음 편할 새가 없다는 것을 알았기에, 나 때문에 불편해하지 마시고 나를 알아서 잘 살도록 내버려 두시라고 권했다.\n\n\"여기 온 지 내일이면 일주일인데, 나도 그동안 그 점을 생각 안 해 본 게 아니란다, 얘야.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"아델파이에 가구 딸린 자그마한 셋방이 하나 나와 있는데, 트롯, 너한테 기막히게 딱 맞을 거야.\"\n\n이렇게 짤막한 운을 떼고는, 고모님은 신문에서 정성껏 오려 낸 광고 한 장을 주머니에서 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 아델파이의 버킹엄 가에, 강이 내려다보이는, 유난히 탐나고 아담한 셋방이 가구 딸린 채로 나왔으며, 법학원의 회원이거나 그 밖의 젊은 신사에게 점잖은 거처가 될 만하고, 즉시 입주가 가능하다고 적혀 있었다. 조건은 적당하며, 필요하다면 한 달만 빌릴 수도 있다고 했다.\n\n\"아니, 이거야말로 딱이네요, 고모님!\" 셋방에 산다는 데서 오는 그럴듯한 위엄에 들떠 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 가 보자꾸나.\" 고모님이 조금 전 벗어 두었던 보닛을 곧장 다시 쓰며 대답했다. \"가서 한번 보자고.\"\n\n우리는 곧장 나섰다. 광고에는 건물 안에 있는 크럽 부인에게 문의하라고 적혀 있었기에, 우리는 크럽 부인과 통할 것이라 짐작되는 지하층 초인종을 눌렀다. 서너 번이나 누르고 나서야 겨우 크럽 부인을 우리에게 응해 나오게 할 수 있었는데, 마침내 그녀가 나타났으니, 무명천 가운 아래로 플란넬 페티코트 주름 장식을 드러낸 뚱뚱한 부인이었다.\n\n\"괜찮으시면 그 셋방을 좀 보여 주세요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"이 신사분이 쓰실 건가요?\" 크럽 부인이 주머니를 더듬어 열쇠를 찾으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 내 조카가 쓸 거예요.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"그런 분께야 더없이 안성맞춤인 방들이죠!\" 크럽 부인이 말했다.\n\n그래서 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n방들은 집 꼭대기에 있었는데 — 화재 비상구가 가깝다는 점에서 이것은 고모님에게 큰 미덕이었다 — 거의 아무것도 보이지 않는 반쯤 캄캄한 작은 입구와, 아예 아무것도 안 보이는 깜깜한 작은 곳간, 그리고 거실 하나와 침실 하나로 이루어져 있었다. 가구는 좀 빛이 바랬지만 나에게는 더없이 좋았고, 과연 창밖에는 강이 흐르고 있었다.\n\n내가 그곳이 무척 마음에 들어 하자, 고모님과 크럽 부인은 조건을 흥정하러 곳간으로 물러갔고, 나는 거실 소파에 남아, 내가 이런 으리으리한 거처에 살 운명이 될 수도 있다는 걸 도무지 감히 믿지 못하고 있었다. 한참에 걸친 일대일 격전 끝에 두 사람이 돌아왔는데, 나는 기쁘게도 크럽 부인의 얼굴에서도 고모님의 얼굴에서도, 거래가 성사되었다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"이게 전에 살던 사람의 가구인가요?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그렇답니다, 부인.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람은 어떻게 됐어요?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n크럽 부인은 성가신 기침에 사로잡혔는데, 그 와중에 무척이나 힘겹게 또박또박 말했다. \"그분은 여기서 병이 나셨어요, 부인. 그러고는 — 어흠! 어흠! 어흠! 아이고 저런! — 돌아가셨답니다!\"\n\n\"이런! 무엇 때문에 죽었어요?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n\"그게요, 부인, 술 때문에 돌아가셨어요.\" 크럽 부인이 은밀하게 말했다. \"그리고 담배 때문에요.\"\n\n\"담배라고요? 굴뚝을 말하는 건 아니겠지요?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 부인.\" 크럽 부인이 대꾸했다. \"여송연하고 파이프 담배요.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 그건 옮는 건 아니로구나, 트롯.\" 고모님이 나를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그렇고말고요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n요컨대 고모님은 내가 그 집에 얼마나 푹 빠져 있는지를 보고는, 한 달간 빌리되 그 기간이 끝나면 열두 달 더 머물러도 좋다는 조건으로 그 방을 잡았다. 크럽 부인이 침구를 마련하고 요리를 해 주기로 했고, 그 밖의 필요한 것들은 이미 다 갖춰져 있었다. 그리고 크럽 부인은 자기가 늘 나를 친아들처럼 여기겠다고 분명히 내비쳤다. 나는 모레 입주하기로 했고, 크럽 부인은 이제야 마음 쓸 누군가를 찾았으니 하늘에 감사한다고 말했다!\n\n돌아오는 길에 고모님은, 이제부터 내가 살아갈 삶이 나를 굳건하고 자립적인 사람으로 만들어 주리라 굳게 믿는다고, 그것이야말로 내게 필요한 전부라고 일러 주었다. 이튿날 우리가 위크필드 씨 댁에서 내 옷가지와 책들을 부쳐 받을 채비를 하는 틈틈이, 고모님은 이 말을 여러 번 되풀이했다. 그 일과 지난 휴가에 관한 모든 것을 적어, 나는 아그네스에게 긴 편지를 한 통 썼고, 고모님이 이튿날 떠날 예정이었기에 그 편지는 고모님이 맡아 가기로 했다. 이런 자질구레한 이야기를 더 늘어놓지 않으려면, 이것만 덧붙이면 되겠다. 고모님은 내 한 달 시험 기간 동안 있을 수 있는 모든 필요에 대비해 넉넉히 돈을 마련해 주었고, 스티어포스는 나에게도 고모님에게도 무척 실망스럽게도 고모님이 떠나기 전에 끝내 나타나지 않았다. 나는 재닛을 곁에 거느리고, 떠돌이 당나귀들을 곧 혼쭐낼 일에 신이 난 고모님이 도버행 마차에 무사히 자리 잡는 것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 마차가 떠나자, 나는 아델파이 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고서, 한때 그곳 지하 아치 사이를 어슬렁거리며 쏘다니던 옛 시절과, 나를 지상으로 끌어올려 준 그 행복한 변화들을 곰곰이 떠올렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER 24. MY FIRST DISSIPATION\n\n그 높다란 성채를 나 혼자 차지하고서, 바깥문을 닫을 때면 마치 로빈슨 크루소가 자기 요새 안으로 들어가 사다리를 끌어 올린 듯한 기분을 느끼는 것은 더할 나위 없이 멋진 일이었다. 내 집 열쇠를 주머니에 넣고 시내를 거닐며, 누구에게든 우리 집에 오라고 청할 수 있고, 나만 괜찮다면 그 누구에게도 불편을 끼칠 일이 없으리라는 것을 확신하는 것도 더할 나위 없이 멋진 일이었다. 누구에게도 한마디 없이 마음대로 드나들고 오가며, 크럽 부인이 필요할 때 땅속 저 깊은 곳에서 헐떡이며 올라오도록 — 부인이 올 마음이 내켰을 때 — 종을 울려 부르는 것도 더할 나위 없이 멋진 일이었다. 이 모든 것이, 다시 말하건대, 더할 나위 없이 멋졌다. 그러나 솔직히 말하건대, 때로는 몹시 적막한 순간도 있었다.\n\n아침이면 멋졌고, 특히 화창한 아침이면 더욱 멋졌다. 햇빛 속에서 보면 이 생활은 무척 신선하고 자유로워 보였다. 햇살이 비치면 더 신선하고 더 자유로워 보였다. 그러나 하루가 저물어 가면 그 생활도 함께 가라앉는 듯했다. 어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠으나, 촛불 아래에서는 좀처럼 좋아 보이지 않았다. 그때면 함께 이야기 나눌 누군가가 그리웠다. 애그니스가 그리웠다. 내 속내를 털어놓던 그 미소 짓는 보금자리가 있던 자리에 어마어마한 빈터가 생긴 것을 느꼈다. 크럽 부인은 까마득히 멀게만 느껴졌다. 술과 담배로 죽었다는 내 전임자를 떠올리며, 그 사람이 좀 살아 있어서 죽음 따위로 나를 성가시게 하지나 말았으면 좋았을 텐데 하고 바라기도 했다.\n\n이틀 밤낮이 지나자 나는 거기서 일 년은 산 것 같은 기분이 들었으나, 정작 한 시간도 더 나이를 먹지 못한 채, 여전히 내 풋내 나는 젊음에 예전 못지않게 시달리고 있었다.\n\n스티어포스가 여태 나타나지 않자 나는 그가 아픈 게 아닐까 걱정스러워, 사흘째 되는 날 일찌감치 민법박사회관을 나와 하이게이트까지 걸어갔다. 스티어포스 부인은 나를 보고 무척 반가워하며, 아들이 옥스퍼드 친구 하나와 함께 세인트올번스 근처에 사는 다른 친구를 보러 떠났는데 내일쯤 돌아올 것 같다고 했다. 나는 그를 어찌나 좋아했던지, 그의 옥스퍼드 친구들이 다 질투가 날 지경이었다.\n\n부인이 저녁까지 들고 가라고 권하기에 나는 머물렀고, 우리는 하루 종일 그의 이야기 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았던 것 같다. 야머스 사람들이 그를 얼마나 좋아했는지, 그가 얼마나 유쾌한 길동무였는지 나는 부인에게 들려주었다. 다틀 양은 넌지시 던지는 말과 알쏭달쏭한 질문으로 가득했지만, 우리가 거기서 한 일에 큰 관심을 보이며 \"그게 정말 그랬어요?\" 따위의 말을 어찌나 자주 되풀이하던지, 자기가 알고 싶은 것을 죄다 내게서 캐냈다. 그녀의 겉모습은 내가 처음 보았을 때 묘사한 그대로였다. 하지만 두 부인과 함께 어울리는 것이 어찌나 즐겁고 자연스럽던지, 나는 그녀에게 조금씩 마음이 끌리는 것을 느꼈다. 그날 저녁 내내, 특히 밤에 집으로 걸어 돌아오면서, 그녀가 버킹엄 거리에 있다면 얼마나 즐거운 벗이 되어 줄까 하는 생각을 몇 번이고 떨칠 수가 없었다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 민법박사회관으로 가기 전에 커피와 빵을 들고 있었는데 — 그런데 여기서 한마디 짚고 넘어가자면, 크럽 부인이 그토록 많은 커피를 쓰면서 정작 그 커피가 얼마나 묽었는지는 놀라울 따름이었다 — 바로 그때 스티어포스가 직접 걸어 들어와, 나는 한없이 기뻤다.\n\n\"이보게 스티어포스,\" 내가 외쳤다. \"다시는 자네를 못 보는 줄 알았다네!\"\n\n\"집에 도착한 바로 다음 날 아침, 완력으로 끌려갔지 뭔가.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"이런, 데이지, 여기서 아주 보기 드문 노총각 생활을 하고 있구먼!\"\n\n나는 식료품 저장실까지 빼놓지 않고 적잖은 자랑을 담아 집 안을 두루 보여 주었고, 그는 크게 칭찬했다. \"그래, 친구,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"자네가 나가라고 하지 않는 한, 나는 여기를 아주 내 시내 별장으로 삼을 작정일세.\"\n\n이건 듣던 중 반가운 소리였다. 나는 그가 그런 통고를 기다린다면 세상이 끝나는 날까지 기다려야 할 거라고 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 아침 좀 들게!\" 종 끈에 손을 얹으며 내가 말했다. \"크럽 부인더러 신선한 커피를 새로 끓여 달라고 하고, 여기 마련해 둔 노총각용 더치오븐에 베이컨을 구워 주겠네.\"\n\n\"아니, 아냐!\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"종은 울리지 말게! 그럴 수 없어! 코번트가든 피아자 호텔에 묵고 있는 친구 하나하고 아침을 먹기로 했거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 저녁은 돌아와서 먹겠지?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그럴 수가 없네. 무엇보다 그러고 싶지만, 그 두 친구하고 같이 있어야 해. 우리 셋은 내일 아침에 함께 떠나거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 친구들을 여기로 데려와서 저녁을 먹게.\" 내가 받았다. \"그들이 와 줄 것 같은가?\"\n\n\"오! 오라면 냉큼 오겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"하지만 자네한테 폐가 될 거야. 자네가 우리한테 와서 어디 적당한 데서 같이 먹는 게 낫겠네.\"\n\n나는 결코 그 말에 응할 수 없었다. 마침 집들이를 조촐하게나마 해야겠다는 생각이 떠올랐고, 이보다 더 좋은 기회는 없을 듯했기 때문이다. 그가 내 방을 칭찬해 준 뒤로 나는 내 방에 새삼 자부심이 생겼고, 그 방의 역량을 끝까지 펼쳐 보이고 싶은 마음에 활활 타올랐다. 그래서 나는 그에게 두 친구의 이름까지 걸고 꼭 오겠다는 약속을 받아 냈고, 저녁 식사 시각은 여섯 시로 정했다.\n\n그가 떠난 뒤, 나는 크럽 부인을 종을 울려 불러서 나의 무모한 계획을 알렸다. 크럽 부인은 먼저, 자기가 시중을 들리라고 기대해선 안 된다는 것은 다들 익히 아는 바라고 운을 떼더니, 그래도 손재주 있는 젊은이 하나를 알고 있는데 잘만 구슬리면 그 일을 맡길 수 있을 것 같고, 그 사람 삯은 오 실링에 내가 기꺼이 얹어 주는 만큼이면 된다고 했다. 나는 당연히 그를 부르자고 했다. 다음으로 크럽 부인은, 자기가 한꺼번에 두 군데에 있을 수 없는 것은 뻔한 노릇이라며(나도 그게 이치에 맞다고 느꼈다), 침실용 촛불을 든 '젊은 계집애' 하나가 식료품 저장실에 붙박여 끊임없이 접시를 닦아 줘야 꼭 필요할 거라고 했다. 나는 그 젊은 여자애 비용은 얼마나 드느냐고 물었고, 크럽 부인은 일 실링 육 펜스쯤이면 내 살림을 일으키지도 거덜 내지도 않을 거라고 했다. 나는 그렇겠다고 했고, 그 일은 그렇게 정해졌다. 그러자 크럽 부인이 말했다. 자, 이제 저녁 음식 이야기를 하지요.\n\n크럽 부인의 부엌 화덕을 만든 철물장이가 얼마나 앞일을 못 내다봤는지를 보여 주는 두드러진 예가 있었으니, 그 화덕으로는 양고기 토막과 으깬 감자 말고는 아무것도 요리할 수 없었다는 것이다. 생선 냄비로 말하자면, 크럽 부인은, 글쎄요! 그냥 와서 화덕을 한번 보시지 그래요, 라고 했다. 그보다 더 정직하게는 말 못 한다고 했다. 와서 보시겠어요? 내가 화덕을 본들 별로 더 똑똑해질 것도 없으니, 나는 사양하며 \"생선은 신경 쓰지 마세요.\"라고 했다. 그러나 크럽 부인은, 그런 말씀 마세요, 굴이 제철인데 그건 어떠세요, 라고 했다. 그래서 그건 그렇게 정해졌다. 이어서 크럽 부인은 자기가 권하고 싶은 건 이런 거라고 했다. 따뜻하게 구운 닭 한 쌍 — 빵집에서. 채소를 곁들인 쇠고기 스튜 한 접시 — 빵집에서. 자그마한 곁들이 두 가지로 두툼한 파이 하나와 콩팥 요리 한 접시 — 빵집에서. 타르트 하나, 그리고 (내가 원한다면) 틀에 굳힌 젤리 하나 — 빵집에서. 이렇게 하면, 크럽 부인 말로는, 자기가 마음 놓고 감자에만 정신을 쏟을 수 있고, 치즈와 셀러리도 바라는 대로 보기 좋게 차려 낼 수 있다는 것이었다.\n\n나는 크럽 부인의 의견대로 움직여, 빵집에 직접 주문을 넣었다. 그러고서 스트랜드 거리를 걷다가, 햄·쇠고기 가게 진열창에서 대리석처럼 보이는 딱딱하고 얼룩덜룩한 덩어리를 발견했는데 '모조 거북 수프'라는 딱지가 붙어 있어서, 나는 안으로 들어가 그 한 덩이를 샀다. 나중에 가서야 그것이 열다섯 명은 너끈히 먹였을 양이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 크럽 부인은 다소 옥신각신한 끝에 이 음식을 데워 주기로 했는데, 끓는 동안 어찌나 줄어들던지, 스티어포스가 표현한 대로 네 사람이 먹기에는 '좀 빠듯한' 정도가 되고 말았다.\n\n이 채비를 다행히 마치고, 나는 코번트가든 시장에서 후식거리를 조금 사고, 그 근처 술 소매상에 제법 푸짐한 주문을 넣었다. 오후에 집에 돌아와, 식료품 저장실 바닥에 네모반듯하게 줄지어 늘어선 술병들을 보았을 때, 그것들이 어찌나 많아 보이던지(두 병이 모자라서 크럽 부인이 무척 거북해했지만) 나는 그 앞에서 그야말로 겁이 났다.\n\n스티어포스의 친구 하나는 이름이 그레인저였고, 다른 하나는 마크햄이었다. 둘 다 무척 명랑하고 활기찬 친구들이었다. 그레인저는 스티어포스보다 좀 나이가 위였고, 마크햄은 앳돼 보였는데 스무 살이 넘지 않았지 싶었다. 마크햄은 늘 자기 자신을 '한 사내'라고 두루뭉술하게 일컬으며, 일인칭 단수로는 좀처럼, 아니 거의 말하지 않는다는 것을 나는 눈여겨보았다.\n\n\"이런 데서는 한 사내가 아주 잘 지낼 수 있겠는걸요, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 마크햄이 말했다 — 자기를 두고 하는 말이었다.\n\n\"그리 나쁜 자리는 아니죠.\" 내가 말했다. \"방도 정말 널찍하고요.\"\n\n\"두 분 다 입맛을 단단히 챙겨 오셨길 바라네.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"맹세코,\" 마크햄이 받았다. \"도시에 오면 한 사내의 입맛이 날카로워지는 모양입디다. 한 사내가 하루 종일 배가 고프지요. 한 사내가 쉴 새 없이 먹어 댄다니까요.\"\n\n처음에는 조금 쑥스러웠고, 또 좌장 노릇을 하기에는 내가 너무 어리게 느껴져서, 저녁이 차려졌다는 소리가 들리자 나는 스티어포스를 상석에 앉히고 그 맞은편에 자리를 잡았다. 모든 것이 아주 훌륭했다. 우리는 술을 아끼지 않았고, 그는 자리를 흥겹게 이끌려고 어찌나 눈부시게 애를 쓰던지 우리의 잔치에는 끊김이라곤 없었다. 정작 나는 저녁 내내 내가 바라던 만큼 좋은 길동무가 되지 못했는데, 내 의자가 문 맞은편이었던 탓에, 손재주 있는 젊은이가 방을 자주 들락거리는 것과, 그러고 나면 그 그림자가 어김없이 현관 벽에 술병을 입에 댄 모습으로 나타나는 것을 지켜보느라 정신이 자꾸 흩어졌기 때문이다. '젊은 계집애' 역시 내게 적잖은 불안을 안겼다. 접시 닦는 일을 게을리해서라기보다, 접시를 깨뜨려서였다. 그 애는 호기심 많은 성미라, (분명하게 지시받은 대로) 식료품 저장실에만 들어앉아 있지를 못하고, 끊임없이 우리 쪽을 기웃거리다가, 끊임없이 자기가 들킨 줄로만 여겼다. 그렇게 여길 때마다 그 애는 몇 번이고 접시들 위로 (제 손으로 바닥에 고이 깔아 둔 그 접시들 위로) 물러나는 바람에, 어지간히도 많이 깨먹었다.\n\n하지만 이런 것들은 사소한 흠이었고, 식탁보가 치워지고 후식이 식탁에 오르자 쉽사리 잊혔다. 그 잔치가 그 대목에 이르렀을 무렵, 손재주 있는 젊은이는 말을 잃은 상태였음이 드러났다. 나는 그에게 크럽 부인과 어울리고, '젊은 계집애'도 함께 지하실로 데려가라고 넌지시 일러둔 뒤, 흥겨움에 나 자신을 내맡겼다.\n\n나는 유난히 쾌활하고 들뜬 기분으로 시작했다. 반쯤 잊고 있던 온갖 이야깃거리가 머릿속으로 밀려들어, 나는 평소답지 않게 떠벌리게 되었다. 내 농담에도, 남의 농담에도 호탕하게 웃었다. 스티어포스가 술을 안 돌린다고 나무랐고, 옥스퍼드에 가겠다는 약속을 몇 번이나 했으며, 추후 통고가 있을 때까지 일주일에 한 번씩 꼭 이런 저녁 모임을 갖겠노라 선언했다. 그러고는 그레인저의 코담배갑에서 코담배를 어찌나 미친 듯이 집어 들이켰던지, 식료품 저장실로 들어가 십 분 동안 혼자 재채기를 쏟아 내야 했다.\n\n나는 계속해서, 점점 더 빨리 술을 돌렸고, 아직 술이 필요해지기도 한참 전부터 코르크 마개뽑이를 들고 연신 벌떡 일어나 새 병을 땄다. 나는 스티어포스의 건강을 위해 건배를 제안했다. 그가 내 가장 소중한 벗이요, 내 소년 시절의 보호자요, 내 한창때의 길동무라고 했다. 그의 건강을 빌게 되어 기쁘다고 했다. 그에게 도저히 갚을 수 없을 만큼 큰 신세를 졌으며, 결코 다 표현할 수 없을 만큼 그를 우러러본다고 했다. 끝으로 나는 이렇게 외쳤다. \"스티어포스를 위하여! 신의 가호가 있기를! 만세!\" 우리는 그를 위해 세 번 곱하기 세 번, 거기에 한 번 더, 그리고 마무리로 멋들어진 한 번을 외쳤다. 나는 그와 악수하려고 식탁을 빙 돌다가 잔을 깨뜨리고는 (단숨에 붙여서) 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"스티어포스—자넨내인생의길잡이별일세.\"\n\n나는 계속해서, 어느새 누군가가 한창 노래를 부르고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 노래하는 사람은 마크햄이었고, 그는 \"한 사내의 마음이 근심에 짓눌릴 때\"를 불렀다. 다 부르고 나서 그는 이어서 \"여자!\"를 위해 건배하겠다고 했다. 나는 거기에 이의를 달았고, 도저히 허락할 수 없었다. 그건 건배를 청하는 점잖은 방식이 아니며, 내 집에서는 그 건배를 결코 그런 식으로 하게 둘 수 없고 오직 \"숙녀분들을 위하여!\"라고만 해야 한다고 했다. 나는 그에게 자못 거세게 굴었는데, 아무래도 스티어포스와 그레인저가 나를 보고 — 아니면 그를 보고 — 아니면 우리 둘 다를 보고 웃는 것이 눈에 들어왔기 때문이지 싶다. 그는 한 사내한테 이래라저래라 명령해선 안 된다고 했다. 나는 한 사내한테 명령해도 된다고 했다. 그러자 그는 한 사내를 모욕해선 안 된다고 했다. 나는 그건 자네 말이 옳다고 했다 — 내 지붕 아래에서는 결코 안 되지, 여기는 집안 수호신이 신성하고 손님 대접의 법도가 무엇보다 으뜸인 곳이니까. 그는 내가 지독하게 좋은 친구라고 인정하는 것이 한 사내의 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일은 아니라고 했다. 나는 그 자리에서 곧장 그의 건강을 위해 건배를 청했다.\n\n누군가가 담배를 피우고 있었다. 우리는 모두 담배를 피우고 있었다. 나도 담배를 피우면서, 자꾸만 치미는 진저리를 억누르려 애쓰고 있었다. 스티어포스가 나에 대해 연설을 했는데, 그 와중에 나는 거의 눈물이 날 만큼 감동했다. 나는 감사 인사를 전하며, 부디 이 자리에 모인 분들이 내일도, 모레도 — 매일 다섯 시에 — 나와 저녁을 함께하여, 긴긴 밤 내내 대화와 교제의 즐거움을 누릴 수 있기를 바란다고 했다. 나는 누군가 한 사람을 위해 건배를 청해야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 우리 고모님을 위해 건배하겠다고 했다. 베시 트롯우드 양, 그 성별 중에서도 으뜸가는 분을 위하여!\n\n누군가가 내 침실 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고, 난간의 차가운 돌에 이마를 식히며, 얼굴에 닿는 바람을 느끼고 있었다. 그건 나였다. 나는 나 자신을 '코퍼필드'라 부르며 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"왜 담배를 피우려 들었어? 못 할 줄 알았으면서.\" 이번에는 누군가가 거울 속 제 얼굴을 비틀거리며 들여다보고 있었다. 그것도 나였다. 거울 속의 나는 몹시 창백했고, 두 눈은 멍한 빛을 띠었으며, 머리카락은 — 오직 머리카락만, 다른 건 아무것도 아니고 — 술 취한 것처럼 보였다.\n\n누군가가 내게 말했다. \"극장에 가세, 코퍼필드!\" 내 앞에는 침실이 아니라, 다시 술잔들이 잔뜩 놓여 짤랑거리는 식탁이 있었다. 램프, 내 오른쪽의 그레인저, 왼쪽의 마크햄, 그리고 맞은편의 스티어포스 — 다들 안개 속에 까마득히 멀리 앉아 있었다. 극장? 그래, 좋고말고. 그게 딱이지. 가세! 하지만 다들 먼저 내보내고, 램프를 끄고 나서 — 불이 날까 봐 — 가는 건 양해해 주게.\n\n어둠 속에서 무언가 헷갈리는 바람에 문이 사라져 버렸다. 창문 커튼 속을 더듬어 문을 찾고 있는데, 스티어포스가 웃으며 내 팔을 잡고 밖으로 이끌어 주었다. 우리는 한 사람씩 차례로 계단을 내려갔다. 거의 바닥에 다다랐을 때, 누군가가 넘어져 데굴데굴 굴러 떨어졌다. 또 다른 누군가가 그건 코퍼필드라고 했다. 나는 그 헛소문에 화가 났는데, 정작 내가 복도에서 벌렁 나자빠진 꼴을 깨닫고 나서야, 거기에 일말의 근거가 있을지도 모르겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n지독하게 안개 낀 밤, 거리의 가로등마다 커다란 무리가 둘러져 있었다! 비가 온다는 둥 하는 어렴풋한 말소리가 들렸다. 나는 서리가 내린 거라고 여겼다. 스티어포스가 가로등 기둥 아래에서 내 옷을 털어 주고, 어디선가 누군가가 참으로 기묘하게 꺼내 온 내 모자를 매만져 모양을 잡아 주었다 — 나는 아까 모자를 쓰고 있지도 않았으니 말이다. 그러고서 스티어포스가 말했다. \"괜찮은 거지, 코퍼필드, 그렇지?\" 나는 그에게 \"이보다조올수없네.\"라고 했다.\n\n비둘기집 같은 칸막이 안에 앉은 한 사내가 안개 너머로 내다보며 누군가에게서 돈을 받더니, 내가 입장료를 대신 내 준 신사들 중 하나냐고 물었고, (얼핏 본 기억으로는) 내 몫의 돈을 받아야 할지 말지 자못 미심쩍어하는 눈치였다. 곧이어 우리는 어느 몹시 더운 극장의 아주 높다란 곳에 자리해, 내 눈에는 연기가 피어오르는 듯한 커다란 일층 평석을 내려다보고 있었다. 그 자리를 빼곡히 메운 사람들이 어찌나 흐릿하던지. 커다란 무대도 있었는데, 거리에 비하면 무척 깨끗하고 매끈해 보였다. 그 위에는 사람들이 있어서 무언가에 대해 떠들고 있었으나 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다. 환한 불빛이 넘쳐 났고, 음악이 흘렀고, 아래쪽 특별석에는 숙녀들이 있었으며, 그 밖에 무엇이 더 있었는지는 모르겠다. 건물 전체가 마치 헤엄을 배우고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 내가 그걸 바로잡아 보려 할 때마다 도무지 설명할 수 없는 방식으로 출렁거렸기 때문이다.\n\n누군가의 제안에 따라, 우리는 아래층 특별석으로 내려가기로 했다. 거기엔 숙녀들이 있었다. 정장을 차려입은 한 신사가 손에 오페라글라스를 들고 소파에 비스듬히 기대 있는 모습이 내 시야를 스쳐 지나갔고, 거울에 비친 내 모습도 온몸이 통째로 지나갔다. 그러고 나서 나는 그 특별석 중 하나로 안내되어 들어갔는데, 자리에 앉으며 무슨 말인가를 하고 있는 나 자신을 깨달았고, 주위 사람들이 누군가를 향해 \"조용히!\"라고 외쳤으며, 숙녀들이 나를 향해 분개한 눈길을 던졌고, 그리고 — 아니! 정말로! — 애그니스가 내 앞자리에, 같은 특별석에, 내가 모르는 숙녀와 신사를 양옆에 두고 앉아 있었다. 그 얼굴을 나는 지금 그때보다 더 또렷이 본다, 감히 말하건대, 후회와 놀라움이 지워지지 않을 듯이 새겨진 채 나를 향한 그 얼굴을.\n\n\"애그니스!\" 내가 혀 꼬부라진 소리로 말했다. \"하느님맙소사! 애그니스!\"\n\n\"쉿! 제발!\" 그녀가 대답했는데, 나는 왜 그러는지 도무지 알 수 없었다. \"사람들한테 방해가 돼요. 무대를 봐요!\"\n\n그녀의 분부에 따라 나는 무대에 시선을 고정하고 거기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 좀 들어 보려 했으나, 전혀 헛수고였다. 이윽고 나는 다시 그녀를 보았고, 그녀가 제 자리 구석으로 움츠러들며 장갑 낀 손을 이마에 가져다 대는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"애그니스!\" 내가 말했다. \"어디아픈거아니에요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 괜찮아요. 제 걱정은 말아요, 트롯우드.\" 그녀가 받았다. \"들어 봐요! 곧 가실 거죠?\"\n\n\"내가곧가냐고?\" 내가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n나는 그녀를 아래층까지 바래다주려고 기다리겠다고 대답하려는 어리석은 마음을 품었다. 어떻게든 그 뜻을 내비쳤던 모양이다. 그녀가 한동안 나를 찬찬히 들여다보더니, 알아들은 듯 낮은 목소리로 대답했기 때문이다.\n\n\"내가 정말 진심으로 부탁한다고 말하면, 당신이 내 말대로 해 주리란 걸 알아요. 지금 가요, 트롯우드, 날 봐서라도, 그리고 친구들한테 집까지 데려다 달라고 해요.\"\n\n그녀는 그 순간만큼은 나를 어지간히 사람답게 돌려놓아서, 나는 그녀에게 화가 났으면서도 부끄러움을 느꼈고, 짤막하게 \"나잘가요!\"(나는 \"안녕히 주무세요!\"라고 할 작정이었다) 하고는 일어나 자리를 떴다. 친구들이 뒤따라왔고, 나는 단숨에 특별석 문을 나서서 내 침실로 들어섰는데, 거기엔 스티어포스만이 나와 함께 있으면서 내가 옷 벗는 것을 거들고 있었고, 나는 그에게 애그니스가 내 누이라고 번갈아 일러 가며, 또 한 병 술을 따게 코르크 마개뽑이를 가져오라고 다그치고 있었다.\n\n내 침대에 누운 누군가가, 밤새도록 열에 들뜬 꿈속에서, 앞뒤도 안 맞게, 이 모든 것을 어떻게 거듭 말하고 또 거듭 행하던지 — 침대는 잠시도 잠잠하지 않고 출렁이는 바다였다! 그 누군가가 어떻게 천천히 나 자신으로 가라앉던지, 나는 목이 바싹바싹 타들어 가며, 살갗의 겉껍질은 딱딱한 널빤지가 되고, 혀는 오랜 세월 부려 먹혀 더께가 잔뜩 앉은 빈 주전자 밑바닥이 되어 약한 불 위에서 타들어 가는 듯했고, 두 손바닥은 그 어떤 얼음으로도 식힐 수 없는 뜨겁게 달군 쇳덩이 같았다!\n\n그러나 이튿날 정신이 들었을 때 느낀 그 마음의 고통, 그 회한, 그 부끄러움이라니! 까맣게 잊어버린 데다 무엇으로도 결코 씻어 낼 수 없을 천 가지 잘못을 저질렀다는 끔찍함 — 애그니스가 내게 던졌던 그 지워지지 않을 눈길의 기억 — 짐승만도 못한 내가, 그녀가 어쩌다 런던에 왔는지, 어디에 묵는지조차 알지 못한 채, 그녀와 연락할 길이 도무지 없다는 괴로움 — 그 흥청거림이 벌어졌던 방의 꼴을 보는 것만으로도 치미는 역겨움 — 깨질 듯한 머리 — 담배 냄새, 술잔들의 광경, 밖에 나가기는커녕 일어날 수조차 없는 막막함! 오, 그 얼마나 끔찍한 하루였던가!\n\n오, 그 얼마나 끔찍한 저녁이었던가. 난롯가에 앉아, 기름이 둥둥 떠 자글거리는 양고기 국물 한 그릇을 앞에 놓고, 내가 전임자의 전철을 밟아 가고 있구나, 그의 방뿐 아니라 그 음울한 신세까지 그대로 물려받겠구나 생각하며, 당장 도버로 달려가 죄다 털어놓고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 들던 그 저녁이! 그 얼마나 끔찍한 저녁이었던가. 크럽 부인이 국그릇을 치우러 들어와, 어제 잔치의 잔해라고는 통틀어 치즈 접시에 콩팥 하나만을 달랑 내놓았을 때, 나는 정말이지 그녀의 무명 앞가슴에 매달려, 진심 어린 뉘우침으로 이렇게 말하고 싶을 지경이었다. \"오, 크럽 부인, 크럽 부인, 깨진 고기 따위는 신경 쓰지 마세요! 저는 너무나 비참합니다!\" — 다만 그 지경에 이르러서도, 크럽 부인이 과연 속내를 털어놓을 만한 그런 부류의 여자일지는 미덥지가 않았을 뿐이다!\n\n# CHAPTER 25. GOOD AND BAD ANGELS\n\n두통과 구역질과 후회로 점철된 그 한심한 날 이튿날 아침, 나는 내가 연 저녁 만찬의 날짜를 두고 머릿속이 묘하게 뒤엉킨 채로 문을 나서던 참이었다. 마치 어떤 거인 무리가 거대한 지렛대를 들고 그저께를 몇 달이나 뒤로 밀쳐 버리기라도 한 것 같은 느낌이었다. 그때 짐꾼 한 사람이 손에 편지를 들고 계단을 올라오는 것이 보였다. 그는 그때까지 심부름을 느긋하게 처리하던 중이었다. 그러나 내가 계단 맨 위에서 난간 너머로 자기를 내려다보는 것을 보자, 그는 종종걸음으로 후다닥 올라오더니, 마치 스스로 녹초가 될 만큼 내달리기라도 한 듯 헐떡이며 다가왔다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 귀하.\" 짐꾼이 작은 지팡이로 모자를 톡 건드리며 말했다.\n\n나는 그 이름의 주인이라고 선뜻 나서기 어려웠다. 그 편지가 아그네스에게서 온 것이라는 확신에 몹시 마음이 흔들렸기 때문이다. 그래도 나는 내가 코퍼필드 귀하가 맞다고 말했고, 그는 그 말을 믿고 편지를 건네면서 답장이 필요하다고 했다. 나는 그를 층계참에 내보내 답장을 기다리게 한 다음 다시 방으로 들어왔는데, 어찌나 신경이 곤두섰던지 봉인을 뜯기로 마음먹기 전에 우선 편지를 아침 식탁 위에 내려놓고 겉봉부터 한참 들여다보며 익숙해질 필요가 있었다.\n\n마침내 편지를 펼쳐 보니, 그것은 극장에서의 내 꼴에 대해서는 한마디도 적혀 있지 않은 무척 다정한 쪽지였다. 거기에는 이렇게만 적혀 있었다. \"사랑하는 트롯우드에게. 저는 지금 홀번 일라이 플레이스에 있는 아버지의 대리인 워터브룩 씨 댁에 머물고 있어요. 오늘 편할 때 아무 때나 시간을 정해서 저를 보러 와 주실래요? 언제나 다정한 마음으로, 아그네스.\"\n\n답장을 내 마음에 들게 쓰는 데 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 그 짐꾼이 무슨 생각을 했을지 모르겠다. 내가 글씨 쓰기를 배우는 중이라고 여겼다면 또 모를까. 적어도 답장을 대여섯 통은 썼을 것이다. 한 통은 이렇게 시작했다. \"사랑하는 아그네스, 내가 어찌 그 역겨운 인상을 당신 기억에서 지우기를 바랄 수—\" 거기서 마음에 들지 않아 찢어 버렸다. 또 한 통은 이렇게 시작했다. \"사랑하는 아그네스, 셰익스피어가 말하기를, 사람이 제 입에 적을 집어넣는다는 것이 얼마나 이상한 일이냐고—\" 그건 마컴이 떠올라서 더 나아가지 못했다. 심지어 시까지 시도해 보았다. 여섯 음절짜리 시행으로 한 통을 시작했다. \"오, 부디 기억하지 마오—\" 그러나 그것이 십일월 오일과 엮이는 바람에 우스꽝스러워지고 말았다. 여러 번 애쓴 끝에 나는 이렇게 썼다. \"사랑하는 아그네스. 당신의 편지는 당신을 꼭 닮았으니, 그보다 더 큰 칭찬이 어디 있어 그것에 대해 무슨 말을 더하겠소? 네 시에 가겠소. 다정하고도 슬픈 마음으로, 트롯우드 코퍼필드.\" 이 편지를 들고 (손에서 떠나기가 무섭게 도로 거두어들이고 싶은 마음이 한꺼번에 스무 갈래나 들었다) 짐꾼은 마침내 떠났다.\n\n만일 그날이 박사회관의 다른 어느 전문직 신사에게도 나에게 그랬던 것의 절반만큼이라도 끔찍한 하루였다면, 나는 그 사람이 그 케케묵은 교회법 치즈 조각에서 자기가 맡은 몫에 대해 어느 정도 속죄를 했으리라고 진심으로 믿는다. 나는 세 시 반에 사무실을 나와 몇 분 만에 약속 장소 근처를 어슬렁거렸으나, 홀번 세인트 앤드루 교회 시계로 따져 약속 시각이 꼬박 십오 분이나 지난 뒤에야 비로소, 워터브룩 씨 댁 왼쪽 문기둥에 박힌 사삿집 초인종 손잡이를 잡아당길 만큼 충분한 결심을 끌어모을 수 있었다.\n\n워터브룩 씨 집안의 업무 가운데 사무적인 일은 일 층에서, 점잖은 사교 행사는 (이것도 꽤 많았다) 건물 위층에서 이루어졌다. 나는 예쁘지만 다소 갑갑한 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그곳에 아그네스가 앉아 지갑을 뜨고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 어찌나 차분하고 선해 보이던지, 캔터베리에서의 산뜻하고 가벼웠던 학창 시절과, 며칠 전 밤의 후줄근하고 술 냄새에 절어 멍청하던 가련한 내 꼴을 어찌나 강하게 떠올리게 하던지, 곁에 아무도 없는 터라 나는 자책과 수치심에 그만 무너지고 말았다. 한마디로 바보짓을 한 셈이다. 눈물을 흘렸다는 것을 부정할 수 없다. 지금 이 순간까지도 나는 그것이 통틀어 내가 할 수 있었던 가장 현명한 일이었는지, 아니면 가장 우스꽝스러운 짓이었는지 결정하지 못했다.\n\n\"당신 말고 다른 누가 봤더라면, 아그네스,\" 나는 고개를 돌리며 말했다. \"이렇게까지 마음 쓰이지는 않았을 거예요. 그런데 하필 그걸 본 사람이 당신이라니! 차라리 그 전에 죽어 버렸으면 싶을 지경이에요.\"\n\n그녀는 한순간 손을 내 팔에 얹었는데 — 그 손길은 어떤 다른 손과도 같지 않았다 — 나는 어찌나 위로받고 의지가 되었던지, 그 손을 입술로 가져가 고맙게 입 맞추지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"앉으세요.\" 아그네스가 밝게 말했다. \"괴로워하지 마세요, 트롯우드. 저를 마음 놓고 믿지 못하면, 대체 누구를 믿겠어요?\"\n\n\"아, 아그네스!\" 나는 받아쳤다. \"당신은 나의 선한 천사예요!\"\n\n그녀는 내가 보기에 다소 슬프게 미소 지으며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그래요, 아그네스, 나의 선한 천사! 언제나 나의 선한 천사예요!\"\n\n\"제가 정말 그렇다면, 트롯우드,\" 그녀가 받아 말했다. \"제가 꼭 마음에 두고 싶은 일이 하나 있어요.\"\n\n나는 묻듯이 그녀를 바라보았다. 하지만 이미 그녀의 뜻을 미리 짐작하고 있었다.\n\n\"당신의 악한 천사를 조심하라고,\" 아그네스가 흔들림 없는 눈길로 말했다. \"당신에게 일러 주는 일이에요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 아그네스,\" 나는 입을 열었다. \"스티어포스를 두고 하는 말이라면—\"\n\n\"맞아요, 트롯우드.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"그렇다면, 아그네스, 당신은 그를 크게 오해하고 있어요. 그가 내 악한 천사라니, 아니 그 누구의 악한 천사라니요! 그는 나에게 길잡이요 버팀목이요 벗일 뿐, 결코 그런 사람이 아니에요! 사랑하는 아그네스! 자, 며칠 전 밤 당신이 본 내 꼴만으로 그를 판단하는 건 부당하고, 당신답지도 않은 일 아닌가요?\"\n\n\"며칠 전 밤 제가 본 당신 꼴만으로 그를 판단하는 게 아니에요.\" 그녀가 조용히 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 뭘로요?\"\n\n\"여러 가지로요 — 그 하나하나는 사소한 것들이지만, 한데 모아 놓으면 저에게는 그렇게 사소해 보이지 않아요. 저는 그를, 한편으로는 당신이 그에 대해 들려준 이야기로, 트롯우드, 그리고 당신의 성품과, 그가 당신에게 미치는 영향으로 판단하는 거예요.\"\n\n그녀의 조심스러운 목소리에는 언제나 내 안의 어떤 현을, 오직 그 소리에만 응답하는 현을 건드리는 무언가가 있었다. 그 목소리는 늘 진지했는데, 지금처럼 몹시 진지할 때면 그 안에 나를 완전히 굴복시키는 떨림이 있었다. 나는 그녀가 뜨개질에 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 있는 것을 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 나는 여전히 그녀의 말에 귀 기울이는 듯한 모습으로 앉아 있었다. 그리고 스티어포스는, 그에 대한 내 온 애정에도 불구하고, 그 어조 속에서 빛을 잃어 갔다.\n\n\"이렇게 외따로 살아온 데다 세상을 너무도 모르는 제가, 당신에게 이토록 자신 있게 충고를 한다든지, 심지어 이렇게 강한 의견을 갖는다든지 하는 것은 무척 당돌한 짓이지요.\" 아그네스가 다시 눈을 들며 말했다. \"하지만 저는 그것이 어디서 비롯되었는지 알아요, 트롯우드 — 우리가 함께 자라 왔다는 그토록 참된 기억에서, 그리고 당신에 관한 모든 일에 대한 그토록 참된 관심에서 비롯된 거예요. 그것이 저를 당돌하게 만들어요. 저는 제 말이 옳다고 확신해요. 정말이지 확신해요. 당신이 위험한 친구를 사귀었다고 일러 줄 때면, 마치 제가 아니라 다른 누군가가 당신에게 말하고 있는 듯한 느낌마저 들어요.\"\n\n나는 다시 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀가 입을 다문 뒤에도 다시 그녀의 말에 귀 기울였다. 그리고 그의 모습은, 아직 내 가슴에 단단히 박혀 있었으되, 다시 빛을 잃어 갔다.\n\n\"당신이 이미 굳은 신념이 되어 버린 어떤 감정을, 그것도 당장에 바꾸리라거나 바꿀 수 있으리라고 기대할 만큼 제가 분별없지는 않아요.\" 잠시 뒤 아그네스가 평소 어조로 돌아오며 말했다. \"더구나 당신의 그 사람을 잘 믿는 성품에 뿌리내린 감정이라면 더더욱요. 그걸 성급하게 바꿔서도 안 되고요. 저는 그저 부탁하는 거예요, 트롯우드, 혹시라도 저를 생각하거든 — 그러니까,\" 그녀가 조용히 미소 지으며 말했다. 내가 끼어들려 하는 것을, 그리고 그 까닭을 그녀가 알았기 때문이다. \"저를 생각할 때마다, 제가 한 말을 떠올려 달라고요. 이 모든 것을 용서해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"용서할게요, 아그네스.\" 나는 대답했다. \"당신이 스티어포스를 공정하게 대하고, 나만큼 그를 좋아하게 되면요.\"\n\n\"그때까지는 안 되나요?\" 아그네스가 말했다.\n\n내가 그를 입에 올렸을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 스쳐 가는 그림자가 보였다. 그러나 그녀는 내 미소에 미소로 답했고, 우리는 다시 옛날처럼 서로에게 거리낌 없이 속내를 터놓는 사이가 되었다.\n\n\"그러면 아그네스,\" 내가 말했다. \"며칠 전 밤 일은 언제 용서해 주려고요?\"\n\n\"그 일을 다시 떠올리게 될 때요.\" 아그네스가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 그렇게 화제를 접으려 했으나, 나는 그 일이 너무도 마음에 가득 차 그냥 넘길 수 없었다. 그래서 어쩌다 내가 그렇게 망신스러운 꼴이 되었는지, 어떤 우연한 사정들이 사슬처럼 이어져 그 마지막 고리가 극장이 되었는지를 굳이 그녀에게 털어놓았다. 이렇게 이야기하는 것이, 그리고 내가 스스로를 돌보지 못할 때 나를 보살펴 준 스티어포스에게 진 신세를 길게 늘어놓는 것이 나에게는 크나큰 위안이 되었다.\n\n\"잊으면 안 돼요.\" 내 이야기가 끝나자마자 아그네스가 차분하게 화제를 바꾸며 말했다. \"곤경에 빠질 때뿐 아니라 사랑에 빠질 때도 저에게 꼭 말해 줘야 한다는 걸요. 라킨스 양 다음 자리는 누가 차지했나요, 트롯우드?\"\n\n\"아무도 없어요, 아그네스.\"\n\n\"누군가 있어요, 트롯우드.\" 아그네스가 웃으며 손가락을 들어 보였다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아그네스, 정말로요! 스티어포스 부인 댁에 무척 똑똑한 부인이 한 분 있긴 해요. 이야기 나누기를 좋아하는 분이지요 — 다틀 양이라고요 — 하지만 그녀를 흠모하는 건 아니에요.\"\n\n아그네스는 자기 통찰력에 다시 한번 웃으며, 만일 내가 속내를 털어놓는 데 변함이 없다면 내 격렬한 애정 사건들을 하나하나 기록한 작은 명부를 만들어 두어야겠다고, 영국사에 나오는 왕과 여왕의 재위표처럼 각 사건의 날짜와 지속 기간과 종결을 적어 두겠노라고 말했다. 그러더니 우라이어를 보았느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"우라이어 힙이요?\" 내가 말했다. \"아뇨. 그가 런던에 있어요?\"\n\n\"매일 아래층 사무실로 와요.\" 아그네스가 대답했다. \"저보다 일주일 먼저 런던에 와 있었어요. 안타깝게도 달갑잖은 일 때문인 듯해요, 트롯우드.\"\n\n\"당신을 불안하게 하는 어떤 일 때문이군요, 아그네스. 알겠어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"대체 무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n아그네스는 뜨개질감을 옆으로 치우더니, 두 손을 포개고 그 아름답고 부드러운 눈으로 나를 수심에 잠긴 듯 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"그가 아버지와 동업자가 되려는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 우라이어가요? 그 비굴하게 아첨이나 떠는 자가, 그런 자리에까지 슬금슬금 기어 올라온다고요!\" 나는 분개하여 외쳤다. \"그 일에 아무런 항의도 하지 않았어요, 아그네스? 그게 어떤 관계가 될지 생각해 봐요. 분명하게 말해야 해요. 아버님이 그런 미친 짓을 하시도록 내버려 두면 안 돼요. 막아야 해요, 아그네스, 아직 늦지 않았을 때.\"\n\n내가 말하는 동안에도 아그네스는 여전히 나를 바라보며, 내 격앙된 모습에 옅은 미소를 띤 채 고개를 저었다. 그러고는 대답했다.\n\n\"아버지에 관한 우리의 지난번 대화를 기억하세요? 그로부터 오래지 않아 — 이삼일도 채 안 되어 — 아버지가 지금 제가 당신에게 말하는 일을 처음으로 넌지시 비치셨어요. 그것을 마치 당신 스스로 택한 일인 양 저에게 보이고 싶어 하는 마음과, 그것이 떠밀려서 하는 일임을 숨기지 못하는 처지 사이에서 괴로워하시는 모습을 보는 것은 슬펐어요. 저는 무척 마음이 아팠어요.\"\n\n\"떠밀려서라니, 아그네스! 누가 아버님을 떠미는 거예요?\"\n\n\"우라이어가,\" 그녀가 잠시 머뭇거리다 대답했다. \"아버지에게 없어서는 안 될 존재가 되어 버렸어요. 그는 교묘하고 빈틈이 없어요. 아버지의 약점을 속속들이 파악하고, 그것을 키우고, 그것을 이용해서, 마침내 — 제가 하고 싶은 말을 한마디로 다 하자면, 트롯우드 — 마침내 아버지가 그를 두려워하게 만들었어요.\"\n\n그녀가 더 할 수 있었을 말이, 그녀가 알거나 의심하고 있는 일이 더 있다는 것을 나는 똑똑히 알 수 있었다. 그것이 무엇이냐고 물어 그녀를 아프게 할 수는 없었다. 그녀가 아버지를 감싸려고 그것을 나에게 숨기고 있다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 일이 오래도록 이 지경으로 흘러왔다는 것을 나는 어렴풋이 깨달았다. 그렇다, 조금만 곱씹어 봐도, 일이 오래전부터 이 지경으로 흘러왔다는 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 나는 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"그가 아버지에게 휘두르는 위세가 대단해요.\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"그는 겸손과 감사를 입에 달고 살아요 — 어쩌면 진심으로요. 그러기를 바라요 — 하지만 그의 처지는 사실상 권력을 쥔 자리이고, 저는 그가 그 권력을 모질게 휘두를까 두려워요.\"\n\n나는 그가 개 같은 놈이라고 말했는데, 그 순간만큼은 그 말이 나에게 크나큰 만족감을 주었다.\n\n\"제가 말하는 그때, 그러니까 아버지가 저에게 그 얘기를 꺼낸 그때,\" 아그네스가 말을 이었다. \"그는 아버지에게 자기가 떠나겠다고 말했대요. 무척 미안하고 떠나기 싫지만, 더 나은 자리가 생겼다고요. 그때 아버지는 몹시 풀이 죽으셨고, 당신이나 제가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 근심에 짓눌려 계셨어요. 그런데 이 동업이라는 방편으로 한시름 놓으신 것 같았어요. 동시에 그것 때문에 상처받고 부끄러워하시는 것도 같았지만요.\"\n\n\"그래서 당신은 그걸 어떻게 받아들였어요, 아그네스?\"\n\n\"저는, 트롯우드,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"옳은 일이기를 바라며 그렇게 했어요. 아버지의 마음의 평화를 위해 그 희생이 꼭 필요하다고 확신했기에, 저는 아버지께 그렇게 하시라고 간청했어요. 그게 아버지 인생의 짐을 덜어 줄 거라고요 — 부디 그러기를 바라요! — 그리고 그러면 제가 아버지의 동무가 되어 드릴 기회도 더 많아질 거라고요. 오, 트롯우드!\" 아그네스가 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 외쳤다. 눈물이 솟구쳐 그 위로 흘렀다. \"저는 아버지의 사랑하는 딸이기는커녕 아버지의 원수였던 게 아닌가 싶기까지 해요. 저를 향한 헌신 속에서 아버지가 어떻게 변해 가셨는지 저는 아니까요. 아버지가 온 마음을 저에게만 쏟느라 당신의 동정심과 의무의 테두리를 얼마나 좁혀 버리셨는지 저는 아니까요. 저 때문에 아버지가 얼마나 많은 것들을 밀쳐 내 버리셨는지, 그리고 저를 향한 그 노심초사가 어떻게 아버지의 삶에 그늘을 드리우고, 모든 기력을 늘 한 가지 생각에만 돌리게 하여 그 힘과 기운을 어떻게 약하게 만들었는지 저는 아니까요. 제가 이걸 바로잡을 수만 있다면! 제가 그토록 철없이 아버지의 쇠락의 원인이 되었듯이, 제가 아버지의 회복을 이뤄 낼 수만 있다면!\"\n\n나는 아그네스가 우는 것을 그전까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 학교에서 새로운 영예를 안고 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그녀의 눈에 어린 눈물을 본 적은 있었고, 우리가 마지막으로 그녀의 아버지에 관해 이야기했을 때도 그 눈에 눈물이 어린 것을 본 적이 있었으며, 우리가 작별할 때 그녀가 그 다소곳한 고개를 옆으로 돌리는 것을 본 적은 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 이렇게 슬퍼하는 것은 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 그 모습에 어찌나 마음이 아프던지, 나는 그저 어리석고 속수무책인 투로 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없었다. \"제발, 아그네스, 그러지 마요! 그러지 마요, 사랑하는 누이!\"\n\n그러나 아그네스는 — 그때 내가 무엇을 알았든 몰랐든, 지금은 잘 아는 바이지만 — 성품과 마음가짐에서 나보다 너무도 뛰어난 사람이라, 내 간청을 오래 필요로 하지 않았다. 내 기억 속에서 그녀를 다른 누구와도 그토록 다르게 만드는 그 아름답고 차분한 태도가, 마치 맑은 하늘에서 구름 한 점이 지나가듯, 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n\"우리가 더 오래 단둘이 있을 것 같지는 않으니,\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"기회가 있는 동안에 간곡히 부탁할게요, 트롯우드. 우라이어에게 친절하게 대해 주세요. 그를 매몰차게 밀어내지 마세요. 그에게 당신과 맞지 않는 점이 있더라도 (당신이 대체로 그러는 성향이 있다고 저는 생각하는데) 그걸 못마땅해하지 마세요. 그가 그런 대접을 받아 마땅하지 않을 수도 있어요. 우리는 그에 대해 확실히 나쁜 점을 아는 게 없으니까요. 어느 경우든, 무엇보다 먼저 아버지와 저를 생각해 주세요!\"\n\n아그네스는 더 말할 겨를이 없었다. 방문이 열리며, 덩치가 큰 부인이 — 아니, 큰 드레스를 입은 부인이라고 해야 할까. 어느 쪽이 드레스고 어느 쪽이 부인인지 분간이 안 되니 정확히는 모르겠다 — 미끄러지듯 들어왔기 때문이다. 나는 그녀를 극장에서 본 듯한 어렴풋한 기억이 있었는데, 마치 흐릿한 환등기 속에서 본 것 같은 느낌이었다. 그러나 그녀는 나를 완벽하게 기억하는 듯했고, 여전히 내가 취해 있는 상태라고 의심하는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 차츰 내가 멀쩡하다는 것을, 그리고 (그러기를 바라거니와) 내가 점잖은 청년이라는 것을 알게 되자, 워터브룩 부인은 나를 향해 한결 누그러졌다. 그녀는 우선 내가 공원에 자주 나가느냐고, 다음으로 사교계에 자주 드나드느냐고 물었다. 이 두 질문에 다 아니라고 대답하자, 나는 그녀의 호감을 다시 잃은 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그러나 그녀는 그 사실을 우아하게 감추고는, 이튿날 만찬에 나를 초대했다. 나는 초대를 받아들이고 작별을 고했는데, 나가는 길에 사무실로 우라이어를 찾아갔다가 그가 없어서 명함을 한 장 남겨 두었다.\n\n이튿날 만찬에 갔다가 현관문이 열리는 순간 양 다리 고기에서 피어오르는 김의 한증탕 속으로 곤두박질치면서, 나는 내가 유일한 손님이 아니라는 것을 알아챘다. 변장한 그 짐꾼이 집안 하인을 거들며 계단 아래에서 내 이름을 받아 위층에 알리려고 기다리고 있는 것을 단번에 알아보았기 때문이다. 그는 은밀히 내 이름을 물으며, 마치 나를 한 번도 본 적이 없다는 듯 제 딴에는 능청을 떨었다. 그러나 나는 그를 똑똑히 알아보았고, 그도 나를 똑똑히 알아보았다. 양심이 우리 둘 다를 겁쟁이로 만든 셈이었다.\n\n워터브룩 씨는 목이 짧고 셔츠 깃이 유난히 큰 중년 신사였는데, 코끝만 까맣다면 영락없이 퍼그 개의 초상화였다. 그는 나와 알게 된 영광을 누리게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다. 그리고 내가 워터브룩 부인에게 인사를 올리고 나자, 그는 자못 격식을 차려 나를 검은 벨벳 드레스에 큼직한 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 무척 위압적인 부인에게 소개했는데, 내 기억에 그녀는 햄릿의 가까운 친척, 이를테면 고모처럼 보였다.\n\n이 부인의 이름은 헨리 스파이커 부인이었고, 그녀의 남편도 그 자리에 있었다. 어찌나 차가운 사람인지, 그의 머리가 희끗한 게 아니라 마치 서리가 뿌려진 것처럼 보일 지경이었다. 헨리 스파이커 내외에게는 남자에게나 여자에게나 엄청난 경의가 표해졌다. 아그네스가 알려 준 바에 따르면, 그것은 헨리 스파이커 씨가 무언가의, 아니 누군가의 변호사라서인데, 그게 무엇인지 누구인지는 잊었지만 어쨌든 재무성과 멀찍이 연줄이 닿아 있기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n손님들 가운데 우라이어 힙도 끼어 있었는데, 검은 양복 차림에 깊은 겸손에 잠겨 있었다. 내가 그와 악수할 때 그는, 나에게 눈여겨봐 주심을 받아 영광이며, 내가 베풀어 준 황송한 친절에 정말로 고맙다고 말했다. 차라리 그가 나에게 덜 고마워했더라면 좋았을 것을, 그날 저녁 내내 그는 감사에 겨워 내 주위를 맴돌았다. 그리고 내가 아그네스에게 한마디라도 할라치면, 영락없이 그 그림자 없는 두 눈과 송장 같은 얼굴로 등 뒤에서 우리를 음산하게 내려다보고 있었다.\n\n다른 손님들도 있었는데 — 내가 보기엔 죄다 이 자리를 위해 포도주처럼 차갑게 얼려 놓은 것 같았다. 그러나 그중 한 사람은 방에 들어오기도 전부터 내 주의를 끌었으니, 트래들스 씨라고 호명되는 소리를 들었기 때문이다! 내 머릿속은 단숨에 세일럼 하우스로 날아갔다. 혹시 그 해골 그림을 그리던 토미가 아닐까, 하고 나는 생각했다!\n\n나는 평소와 다른 관심을 품고 트래들스 씨를 찾았다. 그는 점잖고 차분해 보이는, 수줍은 태도의 청년이었는데, 머리카락이 우스꽝스럽게 뻗쳐 있고 두 눈은 다소 휘둥그렇게 뜨여 있었다. 그가 어찌나 잽싸게 후미진 구석으로 들어가 버렸던지, 나는 그를 알아보기가 조금 어려웠다. 마침내 그를 제대로 볼 수 있었는데, 내 눈이 나를 속인 게 아니라면 그는 영락없이 그 가엾던 옛날의 토미였다.\n\n나는 워터브룩 씨에게 다가가, 옛 학교 친구를 만나는 기쁨을 누리게 된 것 같다고 말했다.\n\n\"그래요!\" 워터브룩 씨가 놀라며 말했다. \"헨리 스파이커 씨와 학교를 같이 다녔다기에는 너무 젊으신데요?\"\n\n\"오, 그분 말이 아니에요!\" 나는 받아쳤다. \"트래들스라는 분 말이에요.\"\n\n\"오! 아, 아! 그렇군요!\" 주인장은 흥미가 한결 시들해진 투로 말했다. \"그럴 수도 있겠네요.\"\n\n\"정말 같은 사람이라면,\" 나는 그를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"우리는 세일럼 하우스라는 곳에서 같이 지냈는데, 그는 정말 좋은 친구였어요.\"\n\n\"오 그래요. 트래들스는 좋은 친구지요.\" 주인장은 너그러이 봐준다는 투로 고개를 끄덕이며 받아쳤다. \"트래들스는 꽤 괜찮은 친구예요.\"\n\n\"참 묘한 우연이네요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 주인장이 받아쳤다. \"트래들스가 여기 와 있다는 것부터가 꽤나 우연이지요. 트래들스는 오늘 아침에야 초대받았거든요. 헨리 스파이커 부인의 남동생이 앉기로 되어 있던 자리가 그분이 몸이 편찮은 바람에 비게 되어서요. 무척 신사다운 분이지요, 헨리 스파이커 부인의 남동생 말입니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\"\n\n나는 그에 대해 아무것도 모르면서도 자못 정감 어린 동의를 우물거렸다. 그러고는 트래들스 씨가 직업이 무엇이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"트래들스는,\" 워터브룩 씨가 받아쳤다. \"법정 변호사가 되려고 공부하는 청년이에요. 네. 꽤 괜찮은 친구지요 — 남에게는 적이 없고 제 자신한테나 적인.\"\n\n\"그가 제 자신의 적이라고요?\" 나는 그 말이 안타까워 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 워터브룩 씨가 입을 오므리고는 편안하고 풍족한 태도로 회중시곗줄을 만지작거리며 받아쳤다. \"내가 보기엔 제 앞길을 제가 막는 부류의 사람이에요. 네, 가령 그는 결코 오백 파운드의 값어치는 못 될 사람이라고 봅니다. 트래들스는 어느 동업자 친구의 소개로 알게 됐어요. 오 그래요. 네. 소장을 작성하고, 사건을 글로 또박또박 정리하는 데에는 일종의 재주가 있지요. 일 년에 걸쳐 트래들스에게 일거리를 더러 던져 줄 수 있는데, 그에게는 — 꽤 짭짤한 거지요. 오 그래요. 네.\"\n\n워터브룩 씨가 이따금 이 짤막한 '네'라는 한마디를 내뱉을 때의 그 더없이 편안하고 흡족한 태도에 나는 깊은 인상을 받았다. 그 안에는 놀라운 표현력이 담겨 있었다. 그것은, 은수저까지는 아니더라도 공성 사다리를 입에 물고 태어나, 인생의 온갖 높은 곳을 하나씩 차례로 기어 올라, 이제는 요새 꼭대기에서 철학자이자 후원자의 눈으로 저 아래 참호 속의 사람들을 굽어보는 사람의 모습을 고스란히 전달했다.\n\n이 주제에 대한 내 사색이 아직 진행 중일 때 만찬이 알려졌다. 워터브룩 씨는 햄릿의 고모를 모시고 내려갔다. 헨리 스파이커 씨는 워터브룩 부인을 데려갔다. 내가 직접 데려가고 싶었던 아그네스는, 다리가 부실하고 실실 웃기나 하는 사내에게 맡겨졌다. 우라이어와 트래들스와 나는, 일행 가운데 가장 어린 축이라, 알아서 마지막에 내려갔다. 아그네스를 빼앗긴 것이 그리 분하지만은 않았는데, 덕분에 계단에서 트래들스에게 내가 누구인지 알릴 기회를 얻었기 때문이다. 그는 무척 뜨겁게 나를 맞아 주었다. 한편 우라이어는 어찌나 거슬릴 만큼 흡족해하면서 또 비굴하게 몸을 뒤틀던지, 나는 그를 난간 너머로 기꺼이 내던져 버릴 수 있을 것 같았다. 트래들스와 나는 식탁에서 동떨어진 두 구석에 배치되어 떨어져 앉았다. 그는 붉은 벨벳 차림 부인의 눈부신 광채 속에, 나는 햄릿의 고모가 드리운 음울함 속에 자리 잡았다. 만찬은 무척 길었고, 대화는 줄곧 귀족 — 그리고 핏줄에 관한 것이었다. 워터브룩 부인은 자기에게 약점이 하나 있다면 그것은 핏줄이라고 거듭 말했다.\n\n우리가 이토록 점잔을 빼지만 않았더라면 자리가 더 잘 풀렸을 텐데, 하는 생각이 여러 번 들었다. 우리는 어찌나 지독하게 점잔을 빼던지, 이야기의 폭이 몹시 좁았다. 일행 중에는 걸피지 씨 내외도 있었는데, 그들은 (적어도 걸피지 씨는) 간접적으로 은행의 법률 업무와 무슨 관련이 있었다. 그래서 은행이며 재무성이며 하는 통에, 우리는 궁정 소식란만큼이나 폐쇄적이 되었다. 설상가상으로 햄릿의 고모는 혼잣말을 즐기는 집안 내력이 있어서, 무슨 화제가 나오든 혼자 두서없이 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다. 화제야 정말이지 몇 가지뿐이었지만, 우리가 늘 핏줄로 되돌아왔기에, 그녀는 자기 조카 못지않게 추상적인 사색을 펼칠 너른 마당을 누렸다.\n\n대화가 어찌나 핏빛으로 물들었던지, 우리는 마치 식인귀 무리라도 된 것 같았다.\n\n\"솔직히 저는 워터브룩 부인과 같은 생각입니다.\" 워터브룩 씨가 포도주잔을 눈높이에 들고 말했다. \"다른 것들도 다 나름대로 좋지만, 뭐니 뭐니 해도 핏줄이지요!\"\n\n\"오! 그만큼 만족스러운 것이 어디 있겠어요!\" 햄릿의 고모가 한마디 했다. \"대체로 말하자면, 그 모든 것들 가운데 그토록 이상적인 것이 어디 있겠어요. 더러 천한 마음을 가진 사람들이 (다행히 많지는 않지만, 더러는 있지요) 제가 우상 숭배라 부르는 짓을 더 좋아하더군요. 정말이지 우상이라니까요! 공로니 지성이니 하는 것들 앞에 머리를 조아리다니요. 하지만 그런 것들은 손에 잡히지 않는 거예요. 핏줄은 그렇지 않아요. 우리는 코에서 핏줄을 보고, 그것을 알아봅니다. 턱에서 그것과 마주치면 이렇게 말하지요. '저기 있구나! 저게 핏줄이야!' 그건 엄연한 사실이에요. 우리는 그것을 콕 집어낼 수 있어요. 거기엔 한 점의 의심도 없지요.\"\n\n아그네스를 데려갔던 다리가 부실하고 실실 웃는 사내는, 내가 보기에 그 문제를 한층 더 단호하게 못 박았다.\n\n\"오, 그러니까 말이죠, 제기랄,\" 이 신사가 멍청한 미소를 띠며 식탁을 빙 둘러보고 말했다. \"핏줄을 포기할 순 없는 거예요, 안 그래요. 핏줄은 있어야 한다고요, 안 그래요. 어떤 젊은 친구들은, 그러니까, 교육이나 처신 면에서 제 신분에 좀 못 미칠 수도 있고, 좀 삐딱하게 나갈 수도 있고, 제 자신이며 남이며 온갖 곤경에 빠뜨릴 수도 있지요 — 뭐 그런 거 말이에요 — 하지만 제기랄, 그래도 그들 몸속에 핏줄이 흐른다고 생각하면 흐뭇하다니까요! 저로 말하면, 핏줄 없는 자한테 일으켜 세워지느니, 차라리 언제든 핏줄 있는 자한테 한 대 얻어맞고 쓰러지는 편이 낫겠어요!\"\n\n이 견해는 그 일반론을 한 알의 호두 껍데기 속에 압축한 것이라, 더없는 만족을 자아냈고, 부인들이 자리를 뜰 때까지 그 신사를 크게 돋보이게 했다. 그 뒤로, 그때까지 무척 서먹하게 굴던 걸피지 씨와 헨리 스파이커 씨가 공동의 적인 우리에 맞서 방어 동맹을 맺고는, 우리를 무찌르고 깔아뭉개려는 듯 식탁 너머로 알쏭달쏭한 대화를 주고받는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"사천오백 파운드짜리 첫 번째 채권 건은 예상했던 대로 흘러가지 않았어요, 스파이커.\" 걸피지 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"에이 공작 건 말이오?\" 스파이커 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"비 백작 건이오!\" 걸피지 씨가 말했다.\n\n스파이커 씨는 눈썹을 치켜올리며 자못 걱정스러운 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"그 문제가 어느 경에게 — 이름은 댈 것 없지만 — 회부되었을 때,\" 걸피지 씨가 말을 끊으며 말했다 —\n\n\"알겠소,\" 스파이커 씨가 말했다. \"엔 말이군요.\"\n\n걸피지 씨가 음울하게 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그분께 회부되었더니, 그분 대답이 '돈을 내든지, 아니면 면제는 없다'였다오.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 세상에!\" 스파이커 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"'돈을 내든지, 아니면 면제는 없다.'\" 걸피지 씨가 단호하게 되풀이했다. \"다음 상속 순위자가 — 무슨 말인지 아시겠소?\"\n\n\"케이 말이군요.\" 스파이커 씨가 불길한 눈빛으로 말했다.\n\n\"— 그래요, 케이가 서명을 딱 잘라 거절했소. 그 일로 그를 뉴마켓까지 찾아갔는데, 그가 단칼에 거절해 버렸지요.\"\n\n스파이커 씨는 어찌나 흥미진진해하던지, 아예 돌처럼 굳어 버렸다.\n\n\"그래서 지금 이 시각까지 그 문제는 그대로 묶여 있소.\" 걸피지 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"얽힌 이해관계가 워낙 막중한지라 일반적인 사정을 자세히 밝히기를 삼가는 것을, 우리 친구 워터브룩이 너그러이 이해해 주리라 믿소.\"\n\n내가 보기에 워터브룩 씨는 자기 식탁에서 그런 이해관계와 그런 이름들이, 비록 넌지시 비치는 정도일망정 오가는 것이 그저 더없이 기뻐 보였다. 그는 (이 논의에 대해 나보다 더 아는 게 없으리라고 나는 확신하지만) 음울하게 사정을 다 안다는 표정을 지으며, 그토록 신중함이 지켜진 것을 크게 칭찬했다. 스파이커 씨는 이런 속내를 전해 받았으니, 자연히 제 친구에게 자기 나름의 속내로 보답하고 싶어 했다. 그리하여 앞의 대화에 뒤이어 또 다른 대화가 이어졌는데, 이번에는 걸피지 씨가 놀랄 차례였고, 다시 그에 뒤이은 대화에서는 놀랄 차례가 다시 스파이커 씨에게 돌아갔으며, 그런 식으로 번갈아 가며 주거니 받거니 했다. 그러는 내내 우리 바깥사람들은 그 대화에 얽힌 어마어마한 이해관계에 짓눌려 있었다. 그리고 우리 주인장은 우리를 유익한 경외와 경탄의 제물이라도 되는 양 자랑스레 바라보았다. 나는 위층의 아그네스에게로 올라가, 한쪽 구석에서 그녀와 이야기를 나누고, 트래들스를 그녀에게 소개하게 되어 정말로 무척 기뻤다. 트래들스는 수줍어했으나 상냥했고, 여전히 그 사람 좋은 친구 그대로였다. 그는 이튿날 아침 한 달간 떠나야 하는 탓에 일찍 자리를 떠야 했으므로, 나는 바라던 만큼 그와 이야기를 많이 나누지는 못했다. 그래도 우리는 주소를 주고받았고, 그가 시내로 돌아오면 다시 만나는 즐거움을 누리자고 다짐했다. 그는 내가 스티어포스를 안다는 것을 듣고 무척 흥미로워하면서 어찌나 따뜻하게 그에 대해 말하던지, 나는 그더러 아그네스에게도 그를 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 보라고 했다. 그러나 아그네스는 그동안 나만 바라보다가, 나만 보고 있을 때 아주 살짝 고개를 저었다.\n\n그녀가 그리 마음 편히 어울릴 만한 사람들 속에 있는 것 같지 않았기에, 나는 그녀가 며칠 안에 떠난다는 말을 듣고 거의 다행스럽기까지 했다. 그토록 빨리 다시 그녀와 헤어질 생각에 서운하기는 했지만 말이다. 이 때문에 나는 손님들이 다 갈 때까지 남아 있었다. 그녀와 이야기를 나누고 그녀의 노래를 듣는 것은, 그녀가 그토록 아름답게 가꿔 놓은 그 고풍스러운 옛집에서 보낸 행복한 시절을 어찌나 즐겁게 떠올려 주던지, 나는 밤이 절반은 새도록 그곳에 머물 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 더 머무를 핑계가 없었기에, 워터브룩 씨 사교 모임의 불빛이 모두 꺼지자, 나는 영 내키지 않는 마음으로 작별을 고했다. 그때 나는 그 어느 때보다도, 그녀가 나의 더 선한 천사라는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 마치 천사처럼 어딘가 동떨어진 존재로부터 그녀의 다정한 얼굴과 잔잔한 미소가 나를 비춰 주기라도 한 듯 그것들을 떠올렸다 해도, 나쁜 마음은 아니었으리라 믿는다.\n\n손님들이 다 갔다고 말했지만, 우라이어만은 빼야 했다. 나는 그를 그 부류에 끼워 주지 않는데, 그는 우리 곁을 맴도는 것을 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 내가 계단을 내려갈 때 그는 바로 내 등 뒤에 붙어 있었다. 내가 집을 나서서 걸어갈 때 그는 바로 내 옆에 붙어, 거대한 가이 포크스 장갑의 더 긴 손가락 부분에 자기 해골 같은 긴 손가락을 천천히 끼워 넣고 있었다.\n\n우라이어와 함께 있고 싶은 마음이 들어서가 아니라, 아그네스가 나에게 한 간청을 떠올리고서, 나는 그에게 우리 방에 가서 커피라도 들겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"오, 정말이지, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 받아 말했다. \"아, 죄송합니다, 코퍼필드 씨. 그런데 그쪽이 너무 자연스럽게 나와서요. 저같이 미천한 사람을 댁에 초대하려고 도련님이 굳이 억지로 애쓰시는 건 제가 바라지 않아서요.\"\n\n\"억지로 그러는 게 아니에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"오겠어요?\"\n\n\"정말 가고 싶습니다.\" 우라이어가 몸을 배배 꼬며 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼, 갑시다!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n나는 그에게 다소 퉁명스럽게 굴지 않을 수 없었으나, 그는 개의치 않는 듯했다. 우리는 길에서 별 대화 없이 가장 가까운 길로 갔다. 그는 그 허수아비 같은 장갑을 끼느라 어찌나 굼뜨던지, 우리가 내 처소에 다다랐을 때까지도 여전히 그것을 끼는 중이었고, 그 일에 진척이라곤 전혀 없어 보였다.\n\n나는 그가 어디에 머리를 부딪치지 않도록 어두운 계단을 앞장서 안내했는데, 정말이지 그의 축축하고 차가운 손이 내 손안에서 어찌나 개구리처럼 느껴지던지, 그것을 떨어뜨려 버리고 달아나고 싶을 지경이었다. 그러나 아그네스와 손님 대접의 도리가 이겨, 나는 그를 내 난롯가로 데려갔다. 내가 촛불을 켜자, 그는 눈앞에 드러난 방에 온순한 황홀경에 빠졌다. 그리고 내가 크럽 부인이 즐겨 커피를 끓이던 수수한 양철 통에 (그녀가 그것을 좋아한 건, 내 생각에 주로 그것이 본래 면도용 그릇이라 커피 끓이는 용도가 아니었던 데다, 식료품 저장실에는 비싼 값을 주고 산 특허 발명품이 곰팡이를 피우며 썩어 가고 있었기 때문이다) 커피를 데우자, 그는 어찌나 감격을 늘어놓던지, 나는 그에게 그 끓는 물을 끼얹어 버리고 싶을 만큼 즐거웠을 것이다.\n\n\"오, 정말이지, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 아니, 코퍼필드 씨,\" 우라이어가 말했다. \"도련님이 저를 시중드시다니, 이건 제가 도저히 기대도 못 한 일입니다요! 하지만 이러저러하게, 제 미천한 처지에 도저히 기대도 못 한 일들이 어찌나 많이 생기는지, 정말이지 제 머리 위로 축복이 비처럼 쏟아지는 것만 같습니다요. 제 앞날에 변화가 생겼다는 얘기를, 아마 좀 들으셨겠지요, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 아니, 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야겠네요?\"\n\n그가 내 소파에 앉아, 긴 무릎을 커피잔 밑으로 끌어 올리고, 모자와 장갑을 발치 가까운 바닥에 내려놓고, 숟가락을 슬슬 휘휘 돌리며, 속눈썹마저 다 태워 버린 듯한 그 그림자 없는 붉은 눈을, 나를 보지도 않으면서 내 쪽으로 돌리고, 앞서 묘사한 바 있는 그 콧구멍의 그 거슬리는 우묵한 자국이 숨결에 따라 폈다 오므라들었다 하고, 턱에서 구두에 이르기까지 뱀처럼 굽이치는 물결이 온몸에 흐르는 모습을 보면서, 나는 마음속으로 그가 지독하게 싫다고 결론지었다. 그를 손님으로 두고 있자니 무척 불편했는데, 그때 나는 어렸고, 그토록 강하게 느끼는 감정을 감추는 데 서툴렀기 때문이다.\n\n\"제 앞날에 변화가 생겼다는 얘기를, 아마 좀 들으셨겠지요, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 아니, 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야겠네요?\" 우라이어가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"네,\" 내가 말했다. \"좀 들었어요.\"\n\n\"아! 아그네스 아씨가 알고 계실 줄 알았습니다요!\" 그가 조용히 받아쳤다. \"아그네스 아씨가 아신다니 기쁘네요. 오, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련 — 코퍼필드 씨!\"\n\n나는 그에게 구두 벗기개를 집어 던질 수도 있었을 것이다 (그것이 마침 깔개 위에 놓여 있었다). 아무리 사소한 것일망정 아그네스에 관한 무언가를 발설하도록 나를 옭아 넣었으니 말이다. 그러나 나는 그저 커피를 마셨다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 알고 보니 정말 대단한 예언자셨어요!\" 우라이어가 말을 이었다. \"이런, 정말이지 대단한 예언자임이 입증되셨다니까요! 언젠가 저더러, 어쩌면 제가 위크필드 씨 사업의 동업자가 될지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그게 위크필드 앤드 힙이 될지도 모른다고 하셨던 것, 기억 안 나세요? 도련님은 기억 못 하실지 몰라도, 미천한 사람은 말입니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 그런 말을 가슴에 고이 간직하는 법이지요!\"\n\n\"그런 얘기를 했던 건 기억나요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그때는 그게 그리 그럴듯하다고 생각하진 않았지만요.\"\n\"오! 누가 그게 그럴듯하다고 생각이나 했겠습니까요, 코퍼필드 씨!\" 우라이어가 신이 나서 받아쳤다. \"정말이지 저 자신도 그러지 못했는걸요. 제 입으로 직접, 저는 너무도 미천하다고 말했던 게 기억납니다요. 그래서 정말이지 진심으로 제 자신을 그렇게 여겼지요.\"\n\n그는 그 새겨 놓은 듯한 히죽거림을 얼굴에 띤 채 앉아 불을 바라보았고, 나는 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"하지만 가장 미천한 사람도, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 이윽고 말을 이었다. \"선한 일의 도구가 될 수 있는 거지요. 저는 제가 위크필드 씨에게 선한 일의 도구가 되어 왔다고, 그리고 앞으로 더 그럴 수 있으리라고 생각하니 기쁩니다요. 오, 그분은 얼마나 훌륭한 분인지요, 코퍼필드 씨. 하지만 얼마나 경솔하셨던지요!\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 안타깝네요.\" 내가 말했다. 나는 다소 콕 집어 이렇게 덧붙이지 않을 수 없었다. \"어느 모로 보나요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다요, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 우라이어가 대답했다. \"어느 모로 보나요. 무엇보다 아그네스 아씨를 봐서요! 도련님은 도련님 자신이 했던 그 멋진 말씀을 기억 못 하시겠지만, 저는 도련님이 언젠가 누구라도 그분을 흠모하지 않을 수 없다고 하셨던 것과, 제가 그 말씀에 얼마나 감사드렸는지를 기억합니다요! 그건 잊으셨겠지요, 틀림없이, 코퍼필드 도련님?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 내가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 잊지 않으셨다니 얼마나 기쁜지요!\" 우라이어가 외쳤다. \"제 미천한 가슴에 야망의 불씨를 처음 지펴 주신 분이 도련님이고, 게다가 도련님이 그걸 잊지 않으셨다니요! 오! — 커피 한 잔만 더 청해도 될까요?\"\n\n그가 그 불씨를 지폈다는 말에 실은 어떤 강조와, 그 말을 하며 나에게 던진 어떤 눈길에, 나는 마치 그가 한 줄기 섬광에 환히 비치는 것을 본 듯 흠칫 놀랐다. 전혀 다른 어조로 건넨 그의 청에 정신이 돌아온 나는, 면도용 그릇으로 손님 대접의 도리를 다했다. 그러나 그렇게 하면서도, 손이 떨렸고, 내가 그의 적수가 못 된다는 느낌이 불현듯 들었으며, 그가 다음에 무슨 말을 하려는 것일까 하는 어리둥절하고 미심쩍은 불안에 사로잡혔는데, 이것이 그의 눈을 피할 수 없으리라는 것을 나는 느꼈다.\n\n그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 커피를 휘휘 돌리고, 한 모금씩 홀짝이고, 그 으스스한 손으로 턱을 가만히 쓰다듬고, 불을 바라보고, 방 안을 두루 둘러보고, 나를 향해 미소라기보다는 헐떡임에 가까운 표정을 짓고, 그 굽실대는 비굴함 속에서 몸을 배배 꼬며 굽이쳤고, 다시 휘휘 젓고 홀짝였으나, 대화를 다시 시작하는 일은 나에게 떠넘겼다.\n\n\"그래서, 위크필드 씨가,\" 마침내 내가 말했다. \"당신 몫의 — 아니 내 몫의 오백 배는 되는 분이,\" (내가 살아 있는 한, 정말이지 그 문장의 그 부분을 어색하게 뚝 끊어 둘로 가르지 않을 수 없었을 것이다) \"경솔하셨다, 그런 거예요, 힙 씨?\"\n\n\"오, 정말이지 무척 경솔하셨지요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 우라이어가 겸손하게 한숨을 쉬며 받아쳤다. \"오, 무척이나요! 그런데 괜찮으시면 저를 그냥 우라이어라고 불러 주셨으면 합니다요. 그게 옛날 같고 좋아서요.\"\n\n\"좋아요! 우라이어.\" 나는 다소 힘겹게 그 말을 불쑥 내뱉으며 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 그가 뜨겁게 받아쳤다. \"고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님! 도련님 입에서 우라이어 소리를 들으니, 옛 산들바람이 불어오는 듯, 옛 종소리가 울려 퍼지는 듯합니다요. 죄송합니다. 제가 무슨 얘기를 하던 중이었지요?\"\n\n\"위크필드 씨 얘기요.\" 내가 일러 주었다.\n\n\"오! 그래요, 정말이지.\" 우라이어가 말했다. \"아! 크나큰 경솔이지요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 도련님 말고는 어느 누구에게도 입에 올리지 않을 화제입니다요. 도련님께도 그저 살짝 건드리고 말 뿐, 그 이상은 안 됩니다요. 지난 몇 년간 제 자리에 다른 누가 있었더라면, 지금쯤 그자는 위크필드 씨를 (오, 그분도 얼마나 훌륭한 분인지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!) 제 엄지손가락 밑에 깔아 놓았을 겁니다요. 엄 — 지손 — 가락 밑에요.\" 우라이어가 그 잔인해 보이는 손을 내 탁자 위로 쭉 뻗더니, 그 위에 자기 엄지를 꾹 눌러, 그것이 떨리고 또 방이 떨릴 때까지 누르며, 아주 천천히 말했다.\n\n만일 그가 위크필드 씨의 머리 위에 그 넓적한 발을 올려놓고 있는 것을 내가 봐야만 했더라도, 나는 그를 이보다 더 미워할 수는 없었으리라 싶다.\n\n\"오, 이런, 그렇습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 부드러운 목소리로 말을 이었는데, 그 엄지의 동작과는 너무도 대조적이었다. 그 엄지는 그 모진 압력을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않았다. \"그건 의심할 여지가 없지요. 손실이, 망신이, 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 모릅니다요. 위크필드 씨도 그걸 아세요. 저는 미천하게 그분을 모시는 미천한 도구일 뿐인데, 그분이 저를 제가 감히 다다르리라고는 바라지도 못했을 높은 자리에 올려 주십니다요. 제가 얼마나 감사해야 마땅한지요!\" 말을 마칠 무렵 그는 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 돌렸으되 나를 보지는 않은 채, 자기가 박아 두었던 그 자리에서 굽은 엄지를 떼더니, 마치 면도라도 하듯 그 깡마른 턱을 천천히 생각에 잠겨 긁어 댔다.\n\n그 교활한 얼굴이, 그 위에 알맞게 비친 붉은 불빛을 받으며, 무언가 다른 것을 준비하고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 내 심장이 얼마나 분개하여 두근거렸는지 나는 똑똑히 기억한다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"그런데 제가 도련님을 못 주무시게 붙잡고 있는 건 아닌지요?\"\n\n\"붙잡고 있는 거 아니에요. 나는 대체로 늦게 자니까요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님! 도련님이 처음 저를 부르시던 때 이후로 제가 미천한 처지에서 좀 올라선 건 사실입니다요. 하지만 저는 여전히 미천합니다요. 부디 제가 미천하지 않게 되는 일은 결코 없기를 바랍니다요. 제가 도련님께 자그마한 속내를 털어놓는다 해도, 제 미천함을 더 나쁘게 여기지는 않으시겠지요, 코퍼필드 도련님? 그러시겠어요?\"\n\n\"오 그럼요.\" 나는 애써 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다!\" 그는 손수건을 꺼내 손바닥을 닦기 시작했다. \"아그네스 아씨가요, 코퍼필드 도련님 —\"\n\"그래서요, 우라이어?\"\n\n\"오, 누가 시키지도 않았는데 우라이어라 불러 주시니 어찌나 기쁜지요!\" 그가 외치며, 경련하는 물고기처럼 몸을 한 번 움찔했다. \"오늘 밤 아씨가 무척 아름다워 보인다고 생각하셨지요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\"\n\n\"늘 그렇듯 보인다고 생각했어요. 모든 면에서, 주위의 누구보다도 뛰어나다고요.\" 내가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"오, 고맙습니다! 정말 그렇다니까요!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"오, 그 말씀, 정말 고맙습니다요!\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 나는 거만하게 말했다. \"당신이 나에게 고마워할 까닭이 없어요.\"\n\n\"왜냐면 그게, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 우라이어가 말했다. \"실은, 제가 외람되게 털어놓으려는 그 속내거든요. 제가 미천하지만,\" 그는 손을 더 세게 닦으며 손과 불을 번갈아 바라보았다. \"제 어머니가 미천하지만, 그리고 우리의 가난하나 정직한 지붕이 늘 누추했지만, 아그네스 아씨의 모습이 (제 비밀을 도련님께 맡기는 건 꺼리지 않습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 처음 조랑말 마차에서 도련님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 그 순간부터 저는 늘 도련님께 마음이 넘쳐흘렀으니까요) 여러 해 동안 제 가슴속에 자리해 왔습니다요. 오, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제 아그네스가 밟고 다니는 그 땅을 저는 얼마나 순수한 사랑으로 사랑하는지요!\"\n\n내 생각에 나는 불에서 시뻘겋게 달군 부지깽이를 낚아채 그를 꿰뚫어 버리고 싶은 미친 충동에 사로잡혔던 것 같다. 그것은 마치 소총에서 발사된 총알처럼 충격과 함께 내게서 떠나갔다. 그러나 이 빨강 머리 짐승의 생각 하나만으로도 능욕당한 아그네스의 모습이, 그 비열한 영혼이 그 몸뚱이를 옥죄기라도 한 듯 온통 비뚜름하게 앉아 있는 그를 바라보는 동안 내 마음속에 남아, 나를 어지럽게 했다. 그는 내 눈앞에서 부풀어 올라 커지는 듯했고, 방은 그의 목소리의 메아리로 가득 찬 듯했다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 언젠가 정해지지 않은 어느 때에 전에도 벌어진 적이 있으며, 그가 다음에 무슨 말을 할지 내가 안다는 그 기묘한 느낌이 (어쩌면 누구도 전혀 모르지는 않을 그 느낌이) 나를 사로잡았다.\n\n그의 얼굴에 어린 권력의 기색을 때맞춰 알아챈 것이, 내가 할 수 있었던 그 어떤 노력보다도, 아그네스의 간청을 그 온전한 힘으로 내 기억에 되살려 주었다. 나는 일 분 전이라면 도저히 불가능하리라 여겼을 만큼 침착한 모양새로, 그에게 자기 감정을 아그네스에게 알린 적이 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아닙니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"오, 이런, 아니지요! 도련님 말고는 어느 누구에게도요. 보시다시피 저는 이제 막 그 낮은 처지에서 벗어나는 참인걸요. 저는 아씨가 제가 자기 아버지에게 얼마나 쓸모 있는지 (정말이지 그분께 무척 쓸모 있는 사람이 되기를 바라거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님), 그리고 제가 어떻게 그분의 앞길을 평탄하게 닦고 그분을 바로 세우는지를 눈여겨봐 주시리라는 데 적잖은 희망을 걸고 있습니다요. 아씨는 아버지를 무척 따르시니까요, 코퍼필드 도련님 (오, 딸로서 얼마나 사랑스러운 일인지요!), 저는 아씨가 아버지를 봐서 저에게 다정하게 대해 주시게 될지도 모른다고 생각합니다요.\"\n\n나는 그 악당의 음모 전체의 깊이를 가늠해 보았고, 그가 그것을 까발리는 까닭을 알아챘다.\n\n\"제 비밀을 지켜 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시고, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"대체로 저를 거스르지 말아 주신다면, 저는 그것을 각별한 은혜로 알겠습니다요. 도련님은 불쾌한 일을 만들고 싶지 않으시겠지요. 도련님이 얼마나 다정한 마음을 가지셨는지 저는 압니다요. 하지만 도련님은 저를 그저 미천한 처지에서만 (가장 미천한 처지에서라고 해야겠네요, 저는 여전히 무척 미천하니까요) 알고 지내셨으니, 어쩌면 저도 모르는 새에 제 아그네스 일로 저를 다소 거스르실지도 모르잖습니까요. 아씨를 두고 제 아그네스라 부르는 거, 보시다시피 그렇습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님. '왕관이라도 내놓으리, 그녀를 내 것이라 부를 수만 있다면!'이라는 노래가 있지요. 저도 언젠가 그러기를 바랍니다요.\"\n\n사랑하는 아그네스! 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 누구에게도 너무나 다정하고 너무나 선한 그녀가, 이런 가련한 놈의 아내가 될 운명으로 점지되었다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일인가!\n\n\"지금 당장 서두를 건 없습니다요, 아시다시피, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 내가 이런 생각을 품고 그를 빤히 바라보며 앉아 있는 동안, 우라이어가 그 끈적끈적한 투로 말을 이었다. \"제 아그네스는 아직 무척 어리니까요. 그리고 어머니와 저는 위로 올라가려고 애써야 하고, 일이 완전히 무르익으려면 새로 짜야 할 것들이 한둘이 아니거든요. 그러니 저는 기회 닿는 대로 차츰차츰 아씨가 제 희망에 익숙해지도록 할 시간이 있을 겁니다요. 오, 이렇게 속내를 들어 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다요! 오, 도련님이 우리 처지를 이해하시고, 게다가 (집안에 불쾌한 일을 만들고 싶지 않으실 테니) 틀림없이 저를 거스르지 않으시리라는 걸 아니, 얼마나 마음이 놓이는지 도련님은 모르실 겁니다요!\"\n\n그는 내가 감히 거두지 못한 손을 잡아, 축축하게 한 번 꽉 쥐고는, 자기의 핼쑥한 시계 문자판을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"이런!\" 그가 말했다. \"한 시가 넘었네요. 옛정을 나누다 보면 시간이 어찌나 잘도 흘러가는지요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 벌써 거의 한 시 반입니다요!\"\n\n나는 더 늦은 줄 알았다고 대답했다. 정말로 그렇게 생각해서가 아니라, 내 대화 능력이 완전히 흩어져 버렸기 때문이었다.\n\n\"이런!\" 그가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"제가 묵고 있는 집은 — 일종의 사삿집 호텔 겸 하숙집인데요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 뉴 리버 수원지 근처라 — 두 시간 전에 다 잠자리에 들었을 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"안타깝네요,\" 내가 받아쳤다. \"여기 침대가 하나뿐이라, 내가—\"\n\n\"오, 침대 얘기는 꺼내지도 마십시오, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 한쪽 다리를 끌어 올리며 황홀하게 받아쳤다. \"그런데 제가 난로 앞에 누워도 괜찮으실까요?\"\n\n\"기왕 그렇다면,\" 내가 말했다. \"부디 내 침대를 쓰세요. 나는 난로 앞에 눕겠어요.\"\n\n이 제안에 대한 그의 거절은, 놀라움과 겸손이 넘쳐흘러, 그때 아마 멀리 떨어진 방에서 잠들어 있었을 크럽 부인의 귀에까지 닿을 만큼 거의 날카로웠다. 그 방은 대략 간조선 높이쯤에 자리 잡고 있었는데, 부인은 늘 그 고질병 같은 시계의 째깍거림에 잠을 달랬다. 시간 약속 문제로 우리 사이에 자그마한 다툼이 있을 때면 그녀는 늘 그 시계를 들먹였는데, 그것은 언제나 사십오 분 넘게 느렸고, 늘 아침이면 가장 권위 있는 분들이 맞춰 놓는 것이었다. 어리둥절한 처지에서 내가 아무리 갖은 논리를 들이대도, 내 침실을 받아들이도록 그의 겸손을 움직이는 데 조금도 효과가 없었기에, 나는 그가 난로 앞에서 쉴 수 있도록 할 수 있는 한 최선의 잠자리를 마련해 줄 수밖에 없었다. 소파의 매트리스(그의 깡마른 몸뚱이에는 턱없이 짧았다)와 소파 쿠션들, 담요 한 장, 식탁보, 깨끗한 아침 식탁용 천, 그리고 외투 한 벌로 그에게 잠자리와 덮을 것을 만들어 주었더니, 그는 그것에 더없이 고마워했다. 그에게 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 하나 빌려주었더니 그는 그것을 곧장 썼는데, 그 꼴이 어찌나 끔찍하던지, 나는 그 후로 두 번 다시 그런 모자를 써 본 적이 없다. 나는 그를 두고 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n그날 밤을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 내가 어떻게 뒤척이고 또 뒤척였는지, 아그네스와 이 작자를 생각하느라 어떻게 스스로를 녹초로 만들었는지, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있을까 또 무엇을 해야 할까를 어떻게 곱씹었는지, 그녀의 마음의 평화를 위한 최선의 길은 아무것도 하지 않고 내가 들은 것을 가슴속에만 묻어 두는 것이라는 결론 말고는 어떤 다른 결론에도 이르지 못했는지를, 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 잠깐이라도 잠이 들면, 그 다정한 눈을 한 아그네스의 모습과, 내가 그토록 자주 보아 온 대로 그녀를 애틋하게 바라보는 그녀 아버지의 모습이, 애원하는 얼굴로 내 앞에 떠올라 막연한 공포로 나를 가득 채웠다. 잠에서 깨면, 우라이어가 옆방에 누워 있다는 생각이, 마치 깨어 있는 채로 꾸는 악몽처럼 내 위에 무겁게 내려앉았다. 그리고 마치 좀 더 비루한 종류의 악마를 하숙인으로 들이기라도 한 듯, 납덩이 같은 두려움으로 나를 짓눌렀다.\n\n게다가 부지깽이까지 내 어렴풋한 꿈속으로 끼어들어 도무지 나가질 않았다. 자는 둥 마는 둥 하는 사이에, 나는 그것이 아직도 시뻘겋게 달아 있어서 내가 그것을 불에서 낚아채 그의 몸뚱이를 꿰뚫어 버렸다고 생각했다. 마침내 그 생각에 어찌나 시달렸던지, 비록 거기엔 아무것도 없다는 것을 알면서도, 나는 그를 보려고 옆방으로 살금살금 들어갔다. 거기서 나는 그가 등을 대고 누워, 두 다리는 어디까지 뻗었는지도 모르게 늘어뜨리고, 목구멍에서 그르렁대는 소리를 내고, 코는 막혔다 뚫렸다 하며, 입은 우체통처럼 떡 벌린 채로 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 내 어지러운 상상 속에서보다 실제로 훨씬 더 끔찍했기에, 그 후로 나는 바로 그 혐오감에 이끌려, 삼십 분 남짓마다 들락날락하며 그를 한 번 더 들여다보지 않을 수 없었다. 그래도 길고 긴 밤은 여전히 무겁고 가망 없게 느껴졌고, 침침한 하늘에는 날이 밝아 올 기미조차 없었다.\n\n이른 아침 그가 계단을 내려가는 것을 보았을 때 (천만다행으로 그는 아침을 먹으려고 머물지 않았다), 나에게는 마치 밤이 그의 몸을 빌려 떠나가는 것처럼 보였다. 박사회관으로 나가면서, 나는 크럽 부인에게 창문을 활짝 열어 두라고, 그래서 내 거실의 공기가 잘 통하고 그의 존재가 말끔히 씻겨 나가게 하라고 각별히 일러두었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 26. I FALL INTO CAPTIVITY\n\n아그네스가 런던을 떠나는 날까지 나는 유라이어 힙을 다시 보지 못했다. 나는 역마차 사무소에 나가 그녀와 작별하고 떠나는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그런데 거기 그가 있었다. 같은 마차를 타고 캔터베리로 돌아가는 길이었다. 그가 입은, 몸에 비해 빈약하고 허리는 짧고 어깨는 솟은 오디색 외투가, 작은 천막 같은 우산과 나란히, 지붕 위 뒷자리 가장자리에 위태롭게 얹혀 있는 것을 보는 것이 그나마 작은 위안이었다. 물론 아그네스는 마차 안에 타고 있었다. 그러나 아그네스가 지켜보는 가운데 그와 다정하게 지내 보려고 애쓰면서 내가 겪은 고통은, 어쩌면 그 정도의 작은 보상을 받을 만했는지도 모른다. 만찬 자리에서 그랬던 것처럼, 그는 마차 창가에서도 단 한순간도 쉬지 않고 우리 주위를 맴돌았다. 마치 거대한 독수리처럼, 내가 아그네스에게 한 말, 아그네스가 나에게 한 말의 한 음절 한 음절을 게걸스럽게 집어삼키면서.\n\n난롯가에서 그가 털어놓은 이야기 때문에 빠져든 그 괴로운 마음 상태에서, 나는 아그네스가 동업을 두고 했던 말을 곱씹고 또 곱씹었다. \"나는 옳다고 믿는 일을 했어요. 아버지의 마음이 편안하시려면 그 희생이 꼭 필요하다는 확신이 들어서, 내가 아버지께 그렇게 해 달라고 간청했어요.\" 그녀가 그를 위한 어떤 희생이든 똑같은 마음으로 받아들이고, 또 그 마음으로 자신을 지탱하리라는 비참한 예감이, 그때부터 줄곧 나를 짓눌렀다. 나는 그녀가 그를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알았다. 그녀의 천성에 깃든 헌신이 어떤 것인지 알았다. 그녀가 자신을 아버지가 저지른 잘못의 죄 없는 원인이라 여기고, 자신이 아버지에게 갚아야 할 크나큰 빚을 졌으며 그 빚을 간절히 갚고 싶어 한다는 것을, 나는 그녀 자신의 입으로 들어 알고 있었다. 그녀가 오디색 외투를 걸친 이 가증스러운 루퍼스와 얼마나 다른지 보면서도 나는 조금도 위안을 얻지 못했는데, 바로 그 둘 사이의 차이, 곧 그녀의 순결한 영혼이 보이는 자기희생과 그의 추잡한 비열함 사이에 가장 큰 위험이 도사리고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 이 모든 것을, 그는 틀림없이 속속들이 알고 있었을 테고, 그 교활한 머리로 곰곰이 따져 두었을 것이다.\n\n그렇지만 나는 그런 희생의 가능성이 아무리 멀리 있더라도 결국 아그네스의 행복을 무너뜨리고 말리라고 확신했다. 그리고 그녀의 태도로 보아, 그녀가 그때까지는 그것을 보지 못했고, 그것이 아직 그녀에게 아무런 그림자도 드리우지 않았다고 굳게 믿었다. 그러니 닥쳐오는 일을 그녀에게 경고하느니 차라리 그녀를 다치게 하는 편이 나았을 것이다. 그렇게 우리는 아무 설명도 없이 헤어졌다. 그녀는 마차 창문으로 손을 흔들며 미소로 작별을 고했고, 그녀의 악령은 지붕 위에서 몸을 비틀어 댔다. 마치 그녀를 손아귀에 거머쥐고 의기양양해하기라도 하듯이.\n\n나는 두 사람의 그 작별 장면을 한참 동안 떨쳐 내지 못했다. 아그네스가 무사히 도착했다는 편지를 보내왔을 때도, 나는 그녀가 떠나는 모습을 보았을 때만큼이나 비참했다. 생각에 잠길 때마다 이 문제가 어김없이 떠올랐고, 내 모든 불안이 어김없이 곱절로 불어났다. 그 꿈을 꾸지 않고 지나가는 밤이 거의 없었다. 그것은 내 삶의 일부가 되었고, 내 머리가 내 몸에서 떼어 낼 수 없듯이 내 삶에서 떼어 낼 수 없게 되었다.\n\n나는 내 불안을 더욱 곱씹을 시간이 차고도 넘쳤다. 스티어포스는 편지로 알려 온 대로 옥스퍼드에 가 있었고, 내가 민법박사회관에 나가 있지 않을 때면 나는 거의 혼자였기 때문이다. 이 무렵 나는 스티어포스에게 어딘가 숨은 불신을 품고 있었던 것 같다. 그의 편지에 답하며 나는 더없이 다정하게 썼지만, 솔직히 그가 바로 그때 런던에 올 수 없다는 것이 전체적으로는 다행스러웠던 듯하다. 짐작건대 진실은 이러했다. 아그네스의 영향이, 그를 보는 일에 흐트러지지 않은 채로 나를 사로잡고 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그 영향이 나에게 더욱 강하게 작용했던 것은, 그녀가 내 생각과 관심에서 그토록 큰 자리를 차지하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그러는 사이 나날과 몇 주가 미끄러지듯 흘러갔다. 나는 스펜로 앤드 조킨스 사무소의 견습 서기로 들어갔다. 고모님에게서 일 년에 구십 파운드(집세와 여러 부수적인 일들은 제외하고)를 받았다. 내 방은 적어도 열두 달 치를 계약해 두었다. 비록 여전히 저녁이면 방이 음산했고 저녁 시간이 길게만 느껴졌지만, 나는 어느 정도 한결같은 침울한 상태에 안착할 수 있었고, 커피에 몸을 맡겼다. 돌이켜 보면 내 생애 이 무렵에 나는 커피를 갤런 단위로 마셔 댄 것 같다. 또 이 무렵에 나는 세 가지를 발견했다. 첫째, 크럽 부인이 '발작'이라는 묘한 병에 시달리는 순교자라는 것인데, 그 병은 으레 코의 염증을 동반했고 끊임없이 박하로 다스려야 했다. 둘째, 내 식료품 저장실의 온도에 무언가 별난 데가 있어서 브랜디 병들이 터진다는 것이었다. 셋째, 내가 이 세상에 홀로라는 것, 그리고 그 사정을 영시의 토막들에 적어 넣기를 무척이나 즐긴다는 것이었다.\n\n내가 견습 서기로 들어간 날, 사무소로 서기들에게 줄 샌드위치와 셰리주를 들여보내고, 밤에 혼자 극장에 간 것 말고는 아무런 잔치도 벌어지지 않았다. 나는 「이방인」을 보러 갔는데, 민법박사회관 풍의 연극이라 그랬는지 어찌나 끔찍하게 마음이 무너졌던지, 집에 돌아와 거울 속의 나조차 알아보기 힘들 지경이었다. 스펜로 씨는 그날 우리 볼일을 마무리 지으면서, 우리가 인연을 맺게 된 것을 축하하는 뜻으로 노우드에 있는 자기 집에 나를 초대해 보고 싶었으나, 딸이 파리에서 학업을 마치고 돌아올 예정이라 집안일이 다소 어수선한 형편이라고 말했다. 그러나 딸이 집에 오면 나를 대접하는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바란다고 넌지시 비쳤다. 나는 그가 딸 하나를 둔 홀아비라는 것을 알고 있었기에, 감사의 뜻을 표했다.\n\n스펜로 씨는 약속을 어기지 않았다. 한두 주 뒤에 그는 이 약속을 다시 꺼내며, 다음 토요일에 내려와 월요일까지 묵어 주는 호의를 베풀어 준다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고 말했다. 물론 나는 기꺼이 그 호의를 베풀겠다고 했다. 그가 자기 사두마차에 나를 태워 내려갔다가 다시 데려오기로 했다.\n\n그날이 되자, 내 여행 가방마저 봉급 받는 서기들에게는 경배의 대상이 되었다. 그들에게 노우드의 집은 신성한 수수께끼였다. 그중 하나는, 스펜로 씨가 오로지 은식기와 도자기로만 식사를 한다는 말을 들었다고 일러 주었다. 또 하나는, 보통 사람들이 식탁용 맥주를 마시듯 그 집에서는 샴페인이 늘 통째로 따라진다고 슬쩍 흘렸다. 가발을 쓴 늙은 서기, 이름이 티피 씨인 사람은, 살아오는 동안 볼일로 그 집에 여러 차례 내려갔었고 그때마다 아침 식사용 응접실까지 들어가 본 적이 있다고 했다. 그는 그곳을 더없이 호화로운 방이라 묘사하면서, 거기서 갈색 동인도 셰리주를 마셨는데 그 품질이 어찌나 귀한지 절로 눈이 찡긋해질 정도였다고 했다. 그날 우리는 종교법원에서 연기되었던 사건 하나를 다루었다. 어느 빵집 주인이 교구 회의에서 도로포장세에 이의를 제기한 일로 그를 파문하느냐 마느냐 하는 문제였다. 내가 계산해 보니 그 증거가 「로빈슨 크루소」의 딱 두 배 길이라, 일을 끝마쳤을 때는 이미 날이 꽤 저문 뒤였다. 어쨌거나 우리는 그를 여섯 주 동안 파문에 처하고 한도 끝도 없는 소송비용을 물도록 선고했다. 그러고 나서 빵집 주인 쪽 소송 대리인과 판사, 그리고 양쪽 변호사들(다들 거의 친척 사이였다)이 함께 시내를 빠져나갔고, 스펜로 씨와 나는 사두마차를 타고 떠났다.\n\n그 사두마차는 무척이나 멋진 물건이었다. 말들은 마치 자기들이 민법박사회관 소속임을 안다는 듯 목을 둥글게 세우고 다리를 높이 쳐들었다. 박사회관에서는 모든 겉치레를 두고 경쟁이 상당했고, 그 덕에 더러는 아주 빼어난 마차들도 나타났다. 다만 나는 늘 생각해 왔고 앞으로도 늘 생각하겠지만, 내가 있던 시절 그곳에서 가장 큰 경쟁 품목은 풀 먹인 빳빳함이었다. 내가 보기에 소송 대리인들 사이에서 그것은 인간이 견딜 수 있는 한계까지 입혀지고 있었다.\n\n내려가는 길 우리는 무척 유쾌했고, 스펜로 씨는 내 직업에 관해 몇 가지 귀띔을 해 주었다. 그는 그것이 세상에서 가장 점잖은 직업이며, 결코 사무변호사라는 직업과 혼동해서는 안 된다고 했다. 그것과는 전혀 다른 부류로, 비할 데 없이 더 배타적이고, 덜 기계적이며, 더 돈벌이가 된다는 것이었다. 그는 박사회관에서는 다른 어디서보다 훨씬 느긋하게 일을 처리하며, 그것이 우리를 특권 계층으로서 따로 구별 짓는다고 했다. 그는 우리가 주로 사무변호사들에게 일을 받는다는 거북한 사실은 감출 수 없다고 했지만, 그들은 열등한 족속이며 어지간한 자부심을 가진 소송 대리인이라면 누구나 깔보는 자들이라는 점을 내게 일러 주었다.\n\n나는 스펜로 씨에게 어떤 종류의 일이 가장 좋은 직업적 사건이라고 보느냐고 물었다. 그는, 삼사만 파운드쯤 되는 아담한 재산이 걸린 유언 분쟁 사건이 아마 그 무엇보다 가장 좋을 것이라고 답했다. 그런 사건에서는, 그가 말하기를, 소송의 단계마다 변론에서 아주 짭짤한 부스러기가 떨어질 뿐 아니라, 신문과 반대신문에서 산더미 위에 또 산더미처럼 증거가 쌓이고(처음에는 대표위원회로, 그다음에는 상원으로 가는 항소는 말할 것도 없고), 게다가 비용은 결국 그 재산에서 나오게 마련이라 양쪽 다 활기차고 신나게 달려들어, 경비 따위는 안중에도 없게 된다는 것이었다. 그러고는 박사회관에 대한 전반적인 찬사로 흘러들었다. 박사회관에서 특히 감탄할 만한 점은(그가 말하기를) 그 아담함이라고 했다. 세상에서 가장 편리하게 짜인 곳이라는 것이었다. 그것은 아늑함의 완벽한 화신이었다. 그것은 호두 껍데기 안에 들어 있었다. 예를 들면 이렇다. 당신이 종교법원에 이혼 사건이나 부부 동거 회복 사건을 가져온다. 아주 좋다. 당신은 그것을 종교법원에서 심리한다. 한 가족끼리 둘러앉아 조용한 카드놀이 한판을 벌이듯, 느긋하게 끝까지 즐긴다. 종교법원이 만족스럽지 않다고 치자, 그러면 당신은 어떻게 하는가? 그야, 아치스 법원으로 간다. 아치스 법원이 무엇인가? 같은 법정, 같은 방, 같은 법조계, 같은 종사자들인데 판사만 다르다. 거기서는 종교법원 판사도 여느 개정일이면 변호사로 나서서 변론할 수 있기 때문이다. 자, 당신은 그 카드놀이 한판을 또 끝까지 즐긴다. 그래도 여전히 만족스럽지 않다. 아주 좋다. 그러면 당신은 어떻게 하는가? 그야, 대표위원회로 간다. 대표위원들이 누구인가? 그야, 교회 대표위원들이란 일감이 하나도 없는 변호사들로, 두 법정에서 그 카드놀이가 벌어질 때 곁에서 구경하며 패가 섞이고 떼이고 놓이는 것을 죄다 지켜보고, 놀이꾼 모두와 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나눈 자들인데, 이제 판사랍시고 새 얼굴로 나서서 모두가 만족하도록 그 문제를 매듭짓는 것이다! 불만 많은 자들은 박사회관의 부패니, 박사회관의 폐쇄성이니, 박사회관을 개혁해야 할 필요니 떠들어 댈지도 모른다고, 스펜로 씨는 엄숙하게 결론을 내리며 말했다. 그러나 부셸당 밀값이 가장 높았을 때 박사회관도 가장 분주했으니, 사람이라면 누구나 가슴에 손을 얹고 온 세상을 향해 이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것이었다. \"박사회관을 건드려 봐라, 그러면 나라가 무너진다!\"\n\n나는 이 모든 말을 주의 깊게 들었다. 그리고 솔직히, 나라가 과연 스펜로 씨가 말하는 만큼이나 박사회관에 신세를 지고 있는지 의심스럽기는 했지만, 그래도 정중하게 그의 의견을 따랐다. 부셸당 밀값에 관한 그 대목은, 내 처지로는 너무 벅차서 그 문제를 완전히 결판내 버렸다고 겸손히 느꼈다. 나는 지금 이 순간까지도 그 밀 한 부셸을 끝내 이겨 내지 못했다. 그것은 평생토록 온갖 종류의 주제와 엮여 나를 짓뭉개려고 거듭 나타났다. 지금도 나는 그것이 정확히 나와 무슨 상관인지, 무슨 권리로 끝없이 다양한 자리에서 나를 짓이기는지 모르겠다. 그러나 내 옛 친구인 그 부셸이 머리채를 잡힌 듯 억지로 끌려 나오는 것을(내가 보기에 늘 그런 식으로 등장한다) 볼 때마다, 나는 그 주제를 가망 없는 것으로 단념해 버린다.\n\n이건 곁다리 이야기다. 나는 박사회관을 건드려 나라를 무너뜨릴 위인이 못 되었다. 나는 나보다 나이도 지식도 위인 분에게서 들은 모든 말에 순순히 동의한다는 뜻을 침묵으로 표했다. 그리고 우리는 「이방인」과 연극, 그리고 두 필씩 짝지은 말들에 관해 이야기를 나누다가, 스펜로 씨네 대문 앞에 다다랐다.\n\n스펜로 씨네 집에는 사랑스러운 정원이 딸려 있었다. 정원을 구경하기에 가장 좋은 철은 아니었지만, 어찌나 아름답게 가꿔져 있던지 나는 그만 넋을 잃었다. 매혹적인 잔디밭이 있었고, 나무들이 무리 지어 있었으며, 어둠 속에서 겨우 분간할 수 있는 멀어지는 산책로들이 있었는데, 그 위로는 격자 시렁이 아치를 이루고 있어서 자라는 철이면 거기에 관목과 꽃들이 피어날 터였다. '여기를 스펜로 양이 홀로 거니는구나.' 나는 생각했다. '아, 이런!'\n\n우리는 환하게 불을 밝힌 집으로 들어가, 온갖 모자며 두건이며 외투며 격자무늬 숄이며 장갑이며 채찍이며 지팡이가 놓인 현관으로 들어섰다. \"도라 아가씨는 어디 있느냐?\" 스펜로 씨가 하인에게 물었다. '도라!' 나는 생각했다. '얼마나 아름다운 이름인가!'\n\n우리는 가까운 방으로 들어섰는데(내 생각에 그 갈색 동인도 셰리주로 기억할 만한 바로 그 아침 식사용 방이었다), 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다. \"코퍼필드 군, 이쪽은 내 딸 도라, 그리고 내 딸 도라의 가장 가까운 벗일세!\" 그것은 분명 스펜로 씨의 목소리였으나, 나는 알아듣지 못했고, 누구 목소리인지 신경도 쓰지 않았다. 모든 것이 한순간에 끝나 버렸다. 나는 내 운명을 완수했다. 나는 사로잡힌 자요, 노예였다. 나는 도라 스펜로를 미칠 듯이 사랑하게 되었다!\n\n그녀는 나에게 인간 이상의 존재였다. 그녀는 요정이었고, 공기의 정령이었고, 무엇인지 나도 모르겠다. 그 누구도 한 번도 본 적 없는 모든 것이자, 누구나 한 번쯤 바라던 모든 것이었다. 나는 순식간에 사랑의 심연 속으로 삼켜져 버렸다. 벼랑 끝에서 멈칫거릴 틈도 없었고, 아래를 내려다보거나 뒤돌아볼 겨를도 없었다. 그녀에게 한마디 건넬 정신을 차리기도 전에, 나는 곤두박질치며 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"저는,\" 잘 기억나는 한 목소리가, 내가 고개 숙여 인사하며 무언가를 중얼거리고 났을 때 말했다. \"코퍼필드 군을 전에 뵌 적이 있답니다.\"\n\n말한 사람은 도라가 아니었다. 아니었다. 그 가장 가까운 벗이라는 사람, 머드스톤 양이었다!\n\n나는 그다지 놀라지 않았던 것 같다. 내 짐작으로는, 내 안에 놀랄 능력이라곤 한 톨도 남아 있지 않았다. 이 물질세계에 도라 스펜로 말고는 놀랄 만한 것이 단 하나도 없었던 것이다. 나는 말했다. \"안녕하세요, 머드스톤 양? 건강하시길 바랍니다.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"아주 건강해요.\" 나는 말했다. \"머드스톤 씨는 안녕하시고요?\" 그녀가 응했다. \"오라버니는 정정하답니다, 고마워요.\"\n\n스펜로 씨는, 짐작건대 우리가 서로를 알아보는 것에 놀란 듯, 이때 한마디 거들었다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 군, 자네와 머드스톤 양이 이미 아는 사이라니 반갑군.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 군과 저는,\" 머드스톤 양이 매서운 침착함으로 말했다. \"친척 간이랍니다. 한때 가볍게 알고 지냈지요. 그가 어렸던 시절이었어요. 그 뒤로 사정이 있어 서로 갈라졌고요. 저라면 그를 못 알아봤을 거예요.\"\n\n나는 어디서든 그녀만큼은 알아봤을 거라고 답했다. 그건 충분히 사실이었다.\n\n\"머드스톤 양께서 고맙게도,\" 스펜로 씨가 나에게 말했다. \"내 딸 도라의 가장 가까운 벗이라는 — 그렇게 불러도 된다면 — 그 소임을 맡아 주셨다네. 내 딸 도라에게 불행히도 어머니가 없는 터라, 머드스톤 양이 친절하게도 그 애의 동무이자 보호자가 되어 주신 거지.\"\n\n문득 이런 생각이 스쳤다. 머드스톤 양은, 호신용 곤봉이라 불리는 호주머니 도구처럼, 보호가 아니라 공격이 목적으로 만들어진 존재가 아닐까. 그러나 도라 말고는 어떤 주제든 스쳐 가는 생각밖에 들지 않았으므로, 나는 곧장 그녀를 흘끗 바라보았고, 그녀의 그 어여쁘게 토라진 태도에서, 자기 동무이자 보호자에게 그리 속내를 터놓고 싶어 하지는 않는다는 것을 본 듯하다고 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 종이 울렸다. 스펜로 씨가 그것이 첫 번째 만찬 종이라고 하더니, 나를 옷 갈아입으라며 데려갔다.\n\n그런 사랑의 상태에서 옷을 갈아입는다거나 무언가 행동이랍시고 한다는 발상 자체가 좀 너무 우스꽝스러웠다. 나는 그저 난로 앞에 앉아 여행 가방 열쇠를 깨물며, 그 매혹적이고 소녀답고 눈이 반짝이는 사랑스러운 도라를 생각하는 수밖에 없었다. 어쩌면 그런 자태, 어쩌면 그런 얼굴, 어쩌면 그토록 우아하고 변화무쌍하며 사람을 홀리는 태도란 말인가!\n\n종이 어찌나 빨리 다시 울렸던지, 나는 그런 상황에서 마땅히 했어야 할 정성스러운 단장 대신 그저 허둥지둥 옷을 걸치고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 손님이 몇 있었다. 도라는 백발의 노신사와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그토록 머리가 셌는데도 — 게다가 증조부라기까지 했다, 본인이 그렇게 말했으니까 — 나는 그가 미칠 듯이 질투가 났다.\n\n나는 어떤 심정이었던가! 나는 모든 사람이 질투가 났다. 누군가가 나보다 스펜로 씨를 더 잘 안다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. 내가 끼지 못했던 일들을 그들이 이야기하는 것을 듣자니 고문이 따로 없었다. 더없이 상냥한 한 사람이, 번들번들 윤이 나는 대머리를 한 채, 식탁 너머로 나에게 이 정원을 본 것이 이번이 처음이냐고 물었을 때, 나는 그에게 무엇이든 야만스럽고 앙심에 찬 짓을 저지를 수도 있었다.\n\n거기 누가 있었는지는 도라 말고는 기억나지 않는다. 만찬에 도라 말고 무엇이 나왔는지는 전혀 모르겠다. 내 인상으로는, 나는 오로지 도라만 먹었고, 여섯 접시쯤은 손도 대지 않은 채 물려 보냈다. 나는 그녀 옆자리에 앉았다. 나는 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 그녀는 더없이 사랑스러운 작은 목소리에, 더없이 명랑한 작은 웃음에, 길 잃은 젊은이를 가망 없는 노예 신세로 이끈 그 어떤 것보다 더 유쾌하고 매혹적인 작은 몸짓을 지니고 있었다. 그녀는 통틀어 꽤 자그마했다. 그래서 더더욱 귀하게 느껴졌다, 나는 생각했다.\n\n그녀가 머드스톤 양과 함께 방을 나가자(다른 부인은 일행에 없었다), 나는 공상에 잠겼는데, 머드스톤 양이 그녀에게 나를 헐뜯을지도 모른다는 잔인한 불안만이 그 공상을 깨뜨렸다. 번들거리는 머리를 한 그 상냥한 인물이 나에게 긴 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 아마 정원 가꾸기에 관한 이야기였던 것 같다. 그가 \"우리 집 정원사\"라고 몇 번이나 말하는 것을 들은 듯하다. 나는 그에게 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울이는 척했지만, 그동안 내내 도라와 함께 에덴동산을 거닐고 있었다.\n\n내가 흠뻑 빠진 그 사랑의 대상에게 헐뜯기지나 않을까 하는 불안은, 우리가 응접실로 들어섰을 때 머드스톤 양의 음산하고 쌀쌀맞은 기색을 보고 되살아났다. 그러나 나는 뜻밖의 방식으로 그 불안에서 벗어났다.\n\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드.\" 머드스톤 양이 나를 창가로 따로 불러내며 말했다. \"할 말이 있어요.\"\n\n나는 머드스톤 양과 단둘이 마주 섰다.\n\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"집안 사정을 구구절절 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠지요. 입에 올려 즐거운 화제는 아니니까요.\" \"전혀 그렇지 않습니다, 부인.\" 내가 응했다.\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아요.\" 머드스톤 양이 맞장구쳤다. \"나는 지난날의 불화나 지난날의 모욕을 다시 떠올리고 싶지 않아요. 나는 어떤 사람에게서 — 같은 여자로서 부끄럽지만 여자한테서 — 모욕을 당했는데, 그 사람은 경멸과 혐오 없이는 입에 올릴 수도 없는 자라, 차라리 언급하지 않는 편이 낫겠어요.\"\n\n나는 고모님을 생각하니 속에서 불같이 끓어올랐다. 그러나 나는, 머드스톤 양이 괜찮다면 그녀를 언급하지 않는 편이 분명 더 낫겠다고 말했다. 그녀가 함부로 무례하게 언급되는 것을 듣고서는 내 의견을 단호한 어조로 밝히지 않고는 못 배긴다고 덧붙였다.\n\n머드스톤 양은 눈을 감고 경멸하듯 고개를 숙였다. 그러더니 천천히 눈을 뜨고 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드, 사실을 숨길 생각은 없는데, 나는 당신의 어린 시절에 당신에 대해 좋지 않은 견해를 품었어요. 그게 잘못된 견해였을 수도 있고, 당신이 더는 그런 평가를 받을 만하지 않게 되었을 수도 있겠지요. 그건 지금 우리 사이에서 따질 문제가 아니에요. 나는 어느 정도 굳건함으로 이름난, 내 믿기로는 그런 집안의 사람이고, 형편이나 변덕에 휘둘리는 사람이 아니에요. 나는 당신에 대해 내 견해를 품을 수 있고, 당신은 나에 대해 당신 견해를 품을 수 있어요.\"\n\n이번에는 내가 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"이런 견해들이 여기서 충돌할 필요는 없어요. 지금 처지로는, 여러모로 그러지 않는 편이 나아요. 인생의 우연이 우리를 다시 한자리에 모이게 했고, 또 다른 자리에서 모이게 할 수도 있으니, 여기서는 서로 데면데면한 지인으로 만나자고 하고 싶어요. 우리가 그 정도 관계로만 만나기에는 집안 사정이라는 이유 하나로 충분하고, 둘 중 누구도 상대를 입방아의 거리로 삼을 필요는 전혀 없어요. 이 제안에 찬성해요?\"\n\n\"머드스톤 양.\" 내가 응했다. \"당신과 머드스톤 씨는 저를 아주 잔인하게 대했고, 제 어머니에게도 몹시 매정하게 굴었다고 생각합니다. 살아 있는 한 늘 그렇게 생각할 거예요. 하지만 제안하신 것에는 전적으로 동의합니다.\"\n\n머드스톤 양은 다시 눈을 감고 고개를 숙였다. 그러고는 차갑고 뻣뻣한 손가락 끝으로 내 손등을 살짝 건드리더니, 손목과 목에 두른 작은 족쇄들을 매만지며 걸어가 버렸다. 그 족쇄들은 내가 마지막으로 보았을 때와 똑같은 한 벌이, 똑같은 상태 그대로인 것 같았다. 그것들은 머드스톤 양의 천성을 떠올리게 하면서, 마치 감옥 문 위에 걸린 족쇄처럼, 바깥의 모든 구경꾼에게 그 안에서 무엇을 기대해야 할지를 일러 주는 듯했다.\n\n그날 저녁 나머지에 대해 내가 아는 것이라곤 이것뿐이다. 내 마음의 여제가 프랑스어로 황홀한 발라드를 부르는 것을 들었는데, 대개 무슨 일이 있더라도 우리는 늘 춤을 추어야 한다는 내용으로, 타 라 라, 타 라 라! 하며, 기타를 닮은 화려한 악기로 직접 반주를 했다는 것. 내가 더없이 행복한 도취에 빠져 넋을 잃었다는 것. 내가 다과를 거절했다는 것. 특히 펀치술 앞에서는 내 영혼이 움츠러들었다는 것. 머드스톤 양이 그녀를 거두어 데려갈 때, 그녀가 미소 지으며 나에게 그 감미로운 손을 내밀었다는 것. 내가 거울에 비친 내 모습을 흘끗 보았는데, 영락없이 얼간이에 멍청이처럼 보였다는 것. 내가 더없이 청승맞은 심정으로 잠자리에 들었다가, 가냘픈 도취의 고비 속에서 일어났다는 것.\n\n날씨 좋은 이른 아침이었다. 나는 철 시렁이 아치를 이룬 그 산책로 가운데 하나로 내려가 거닐며, 그녀의 모습을 곱씹는 것으로 내 열정을 마음껏 누려야겠다고 생각했다. 현관을 지나던 길에 나는 그녀의 작은 개와 마주쳤는데, 이름이 집이라고, 집시를 줄인 것이었다. 나는 그 녀석마저 사랑했기에 다정하게 다가갔다. 그러나 녀석은 이빨을 죄다 드러내더니, 일부러 으르렁대려고 의자 밑으로 기어들어 가서는, 조금의 친한 척도 들으려 하지 않았다.\n\n정원은 서늘하고 호젓했다. 나는 이리저리 거닐며, 만약 이 사랑스러운 경이로움과 약혼이라도 하게 된다면 내 행복의 감정이 어떨까 상상해 보았다. 결혼이며 재산이며 그 따위 모든 것에 대해서는, 그때 나는 어린 에밀리를 사랑하던 시절만큼이나 거의 천진하게 아무런 속셈이 없었던 것 같다. 그녀를 '도라'라고 부를 수 있고, 그녀에게 편지를 쓰고, 그녀에게 푹 빠져 그녀를 숭배하고, 그녀가 다른 사람들과 함께 있을 때조차 나를 마음에 두고 있다고 믿을 만한 근거를 갖는 것, 그것이 나에게는 인간이 품을 수 있는 야망의 정점으로 보였다. 적어도 내 야망의 정점이었던 것은 분명하다. 내가 맥없이 흐느적대는 풋사랑꾼이었다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그러나 이 모든 것에는 마음의 순수함이 깃들어 있어서, 내가 아무리 그것을 비웃는다 해도, 그 시절을 아주 경멸스럽게 회상하지는 못하게 한다.\n\n오래 걷지 않았는데, 모퉁이를 돌다가 그녀와 마주쳤다. 기억이 그 모퉁이를 돌아설 때면 나는 지금도 머리끝부터 발끝까지 짜릿하고, 펜을 쥔 내 손이 떨린다.\n\n\"이렇게 — 일찍 — 나오셨군요, 스펜로 양.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"집안이 어찌나 따분한지요.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"게다가 머드스톤 양은 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지! 제가 나오기 전에 하루의 공기가 충분히 데워져야 한다나, 그런 말도 안 되는 소리를 하는 거예요. 데워지다니요!\"(그녀는 여기서 더없이 곱고 가락진 웃음을 터뜨렸다.) \"연습을 안 하는 일요일 아침이면 저는 무언가는 해야 하잖아요. 그래서 어젯밤에 아빠한테 꼭 나가야겠다고 말했어요. 게다가 지금이 하루 중 가장 환한 때이고요. 그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\"\n\n나는 대담하게 날아올라 보기로 하고, 말했다(더듬거리지 않은 것은 아니다). 일 분 전만 해도 나에게는 무척 어두웠는데 지금은 무척 환하다고.\n\n\"칭찬을 하시는 거예요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"아니면 정말로 날씨가 바뀌었다는 거예요?\"\n\n나는 아까보다 더 심하게 더듬으며 답하기를, 칭찬이 아니라 있는 그대로의 진실을 말한 것이라고, 다만 날씨에 어떤 변화가 일어났는지는 모르겠다고 했다. 그건 내 마음의 상태에 일어난 일이라고, 나는 수줍게 덧붙였다. 설명에 쐐기를 박으려고.\n\n나는 그런 곱슬머리를 본 적이 없다 — 어찌 보았겠는가, 그런 곱슬머리는 세상에 없었으니! — 그녀가 발그레해진 뺨을 가리려고 흔들어 흩뜨리던 그 곱슬머리 같은 것을. 그 곱슬머리 위에 얹힌 밀짚모자와 파란 리본으로 말하자면, 그것을 버킹엄가의 내 방에 걸어 둘 수만 있었다면, 그것은 얼마나 값을 매길 수 없는 보물이었을까!\n\n\"파리에서 막 돌아오셨군요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"거기 가 보신 적 있어요?\"\n\n\"없어요.\"\n\"어머! 곧 꼭 가 보세요! 정말 좋아하실 거예요!\"\n\n깊디깊은 고뇌의 흔적이 내 얼굴에 떠올랐다. 그녀가 내가 가기를 바란다는 것, 내가 갈 수도 있다고 그녀가 생각한다는 것은 견딜 수 없는 일이었다. 나는 파리를 깎아내렸다. 나는 프랑스를 깎아내렸다. 나는 지금 같은 처지에서는 이 세상 그 어떤 것을 준다 해도 잉글랜드를 떠나지 않겠다고 말했다. 그 무엇으로도 나를 꾈 수 없을 거라고. 요컨대, 그녀가 다시 곱슬머리를 흔들고 있을 때, 그 작은 개가 산책로를 따라 달려와 우리를 구해 주었다.\n\n녀석은 나를 죽도록 질투해서, 한사코 나를 향해 짖어 댔다. 그녀가 녀석을 품에 안아 들고는 — 오, 맙소사! — 어루만졌으나, 녀석은 그래도 한사코 짖어 댔다. 내가 만져 보려 하자 녀석은 손도 못 대게 했다. 그러자 그녀가 녀석을 때렸다. 벌을 준답시고 녀석의 뭉툭한 콧등을 톡톡 두드리는 것을 보자니 내 괴로움은 한층 커졌는데, 녀석은 그러는 동안 눈을 끔벅이고 그녀의 손을 핥으면서도, 여전히 속으로 작은 콘트라베이스처럼 으르렁거렸다. 마침내 녀석은 잠잠해졌다 — 그럴 만도 했지, 보조개 팬 그녀의 턱이 녀석의 머리 위에 얹혀 있었으니! — 그리고 우리는 온실을 구경하러 걸어갔다.\n\n\"머드스톤 양과는 그리 가깝지 않으시죠, 그렇죠?\" 도라가 말했다. \"— 우리 아가.\"\n\n(뒤의 두 마디는 개에게 한 말이었다. 오, 그것이 나에게 한 말이었더라면!)\n\n\"아니요.\" 내가 답했다. \"전혀 그렇지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그분은 정말 지긋지긋한 사람이에요.\" 도라가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다. \"아빠가 무슨 생각으로 그런 짜증 나는 사람을 골라 제 동무로 삼았는지 모르겠어요. 보호자가 누가 필요하대요? 저는 정말이지 보호자 따위 필요 없어요. 머드스톤 양보다 집이 저를 훨씬 더 잘 지켜 줄 수 있어요 — 그렇지 않니, 집, 우리 아가?\"\n\n녀석은 그녀가 공처럼 동그란 머리에 입을 맞추자, 그저 나른하게 눈을 끔벅일 뿐이었다.\n\n\"아빠는 그분을 제 가장 가까운 벗이라고 부르지만, 분명 그런 사람 아니에요 — 그렇지, 집? 우리는 그런 심술궂은 사람들한테는 속내를 털어놓지 않을 거야, 집이랑 나는. 우리는 우리가 좋아하는 데에 속내를 터놓을 거고, 누가 골라 주는 게 아니라 우리 손으로 친구를 찾아낼 거야 — 그렇지, 집?\"\n\n집은 대답으로 편안한 소리를, 노래하는 주전자가 끓을 때와 좀 비슷한 소리를 냈다. 나로 말하자면, 한마디 한마디가 앞의 것 위에 다시 박히는 새로운 족쇄 무더기였다.\n\n\"우리한테 다정한 엄마가 없다는 것만으로도 참 가혹한데, 그 대신 머드스톤 양 같은 시무룩하고 음울한 늙은이가 늘 우리를 졸졸 따라다녀야 하다니 — 그렇지 않니, 집? 괜찮아, 집. 우리는 속내를 안 털어놓을 거고, 그래도 그 사람 때문에 굴하지 않고 우리끼리 최대한 즐겁게 지낼 거고, 그 사람을 약 올리되 비위는 맞춰 주지 않을 거야 — 그렇지, 집?\"\n\n이 말이 조금만 더 이어졌더라면, 나는 자갈밭에 무릎을 꿇고 말았을 것 같다. 무릎이 까질 게 뻔하고, 게다가 곧장 이 집에서 쫓겨날 게 뻔한데도 말이다. 그러나 다행히도 온실이 멀지 않았고, 이 말이 끝날 즈음 우리는 그 앞에 다다랐다.\n\n온실 안에는 아름다운 제라늄이 한 무더기 흐드러져 있었다. 우리는 그 앞을 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐었고, 도라는 이것저것 감탄하느라 자주 멈춰 섰고, 나도 똑같은 것을 감탄하느라 멈춰 섰고, 도라는 웃으며 개를 어린아이처럼 안아 들어 꽃향기를 맡게 했다. 그리고 우리 셋이 다 요정 나라에 있었던 것은 아닐지라도, 적어도 나는 분명 거기 있었다. 오늘날에도 제라늄 잎의 향기를 맡으면, 한순간에 나에게 무슨 변화가 일어났던 것인지 반쯤은 우스꽝스럽고 반쯤은 진지한 경이로움이 나를 덮친다. 그러면 밀짚모자와 파란 리본, 그리고 한 무더기의 곱슬머리가 보이고, 흐드러진 꽃과 환한 잎사귀를 배경으로, 가느다란 두 팔에 안겨 들어 올려진 작고 까만 개가 보인다.\n\n머드스톤 양이 우리를 찾고 있었다. 그녀는 여기서 우리를 찾아냈다. 그러고는 잔주름마다 머릿가루가 들어찬 그 무뚝뚝한 뺨을, 도라에게 입맞춤하라고 내밀었다. 그런 다음 도라의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼더니, 마치 군인의 장례 행렬이라도 되는 듯 우리를 이끌고 아침 식탁으로 행군해 갔다.\n\n도라가 차를 따라 준다는 이유로 내가 차를 몇 잔이나 마셨는지는 모르겠다. 그러나 분명히 기억하는 것은, 내가 차를 들이켜고 또 들이켜며 앉아 있었다는 것인데, 그 시절 나에게 신경계라는 게 있었다면 그 전부가 결딴나 버렸을 정도였다. 이윽고 우리는 교회에 갔다. 신도석에서 머드스톤 양이 도라와 나 사이에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 나는 그녀의 노랫소리를 들었고, 그러자 신도들이 죄다 사라져 버렸다. 설교가 전해졌는데 — 물론 도라에 관한 것이었다 — 그 예배에 대해 내가 아는 것이라곤 그게 전부인 듯하다.\n\n우리는 조용한 하루를 보냈다. 손님도 없었고, 산책 한 번, 넷이 둘러앉은 가족 만찬, 그리고 책과 그림을 들여다보는 저녁이 있었다. 머드스톤 양은 앞에 설교집을 펼쳐 놓고, 우리에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 빈틈없이 망을 보았다. 아! 그날 만찬을 마친 뒤 손수건을 머리에 덮어쓴 채 내 맞은편에 앉아 있던 스펜로 씨는, 내가 공상 속에서 사위로서 그를 얼마나 뜨겁게 끌어안고 있었는지 꿈에도 몰랐으리라! 밤에 그에게 작별을 고할 때, 그는 자신이 방금 내가 도라와 약혼하는 것에 완전한 동의를 해 준 셈이라는 것도, 내가 그의 머리 위로 온갖 축복을 빌고 있다는 것도 까맣게 몰랐으리라!\n\n우리는 이른 아침에 떠났다. 해사법원에서 해난 구조 사건 하나가 열릴 예정이었는데, 항해라는 학문 전반에 관한 꽤 정확한 지식을 요하는 사건이라(박사회관에서야 우리가 그런 문제에 대해 잘 알 리가 없으니), 판사가 자비를 베푸는 셈 치고 와서 자기를 좀 도와 달라고 늙은 트리니티 선장 두 명에게 간청해 둔 터였다. 그래도 도라는 다시 차를 따라 주려고 아침 식탁에 나와 있었다. 그래서 나는 그녀가 집을 품에 안고 문간 계단에 서 있을 때, 사두마차에서 그녀에게 모자를 벗어 인사하는 우울한 기쁨을 누렸다.\n\n그날 해사법원이 나에게 어떤 곳이었는지, 사건 이야기를 들으면서 내가 그것을 마음속에서 얼마나 엉터리로 만들어 버렸는지, 그 높은 재판권의 상징으로 그들이 탁자 위에 올려놓은 은제 노의 날에 '도라'라는 글자가 새겨진 것을 어떻게 보았는지, 그리고 스펜로 씨가 나를 빼놓고 혼자 집으로 돌아갔을 때(나는 그가 나를 다시 데려가 줄지도 모른다는 미친 희망을 품고 있었다) 내가 어떤 심정이었는지 — 마치 내가 한낱 뱃사람이고, 내가 속한 배는 떠나 버려 나를 무인도에 남겨 둔 것만 같았다 — 이 모든 것을 나는 굳이 헛되이 묘사하려 들지 않겠다. 만약 그 졸음에 겨운 늙은 법정이 제 몸을 추슬러 일으켜, 내가 그 안에서 도라에 대해 품었던 백일몽을 무슨 눈에 보이는 형태로든 보여 줄 수 있다면, 그것은 내 진심을 드러내 보일 것이다.\n\n내가 말하는 건 그날 하루에 꾼 꿈만이 아니라, 날이면 날마다, 주에서 주로, 개정기에서 개정기로 이어진 꿈이다. 나는 거기 가되, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 살피려는 게 아니라 도라를 생각하려고 갔다. 사건들이 내 앞에서 더디고 길게 끌려갈 때 행여 거기에 한 가닥 생각이라도 쏟았다면, 그건 다만 혼인 사건들에서는(도라를 떠올리며) 결혼한 사람들이 어떻게 행복하지 않을 수가 있는지 의아해하느라, 그리고 유언 검인 사건들에서는, 만약 그 문제의 돈이 나에게 남겨졌다면 내가 도라를 위해 곧장 밟았을 첫 단계가 무엇이었을지 따져 보느라 그랬을 뿐이다. 사랑에 빠진 첫 주 안에 나는 화려한 조끼 네 벌을 샀는데 — 나를 위해서가 아니었다, 나는 그것들이 조금도 자랑스럽지 않았으니까. 도라를 위해서였다 — 그리고 거리에서 밀짚색 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 다니기 시작했으며, 평생 나를 괴롭힐 모든 티눈의 주춧돌을 놓았다. 그 시절 내가 신던 구두를 지금 꺼내어 내 발의 실제 크기와 견주어 볼 수만 있다면, 그것은 내 마음이 어떤 상태였는지를 더없이 가슴 저리게 보여 줄 것이다.\n\n그런데도 도라에게 바치는 이 경배의 행위로 내 몸을 가련한 절름발이로 만들어 가면서도, 나는 그녀를 한 번이라도 볼 수 있을까 하는 희망에 날마다 몇 마일이고 몇 마일이고 걸었다. 나는 머지않아 노우드 가도에서 그 구역을 도는 우편배달부만큼이나 잘 알려진 얼굴이 되었을 뿐 아니라, 런던 곳곳에도 두루 출몰했다. 나는 부인용 좋은 가게들이 늘어선 거리를 거닐었고, 안식을 못 찾는 혼령처럼 잡화시장을 떠돌았으며, 완전히 녹초가 되고도 한참 뒤까지 공원을 거듭거듭 헤집고 다녔다. 더러, 긴 사이를 두고 드물게나마, 나는 그녀를 보았다. 어쩌면 마차 창문으로 흔드는 그녀의 장갑을 보았을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그녀를 만나 그녀와 머드스톤 양과 함께 잠깐 걸으며 그녀에게 말을 건넸을 수도 있다. 뒤의 경우, 나는 나중에 늘 몹시 비참해졌는데, 내가 핵심이 되는 말은 아무것도 못 했다는 생각에, 혹은 내 헌신이 얼마나 깊은지 그녀가 짐작도 못 한다는 생각에, 혹은 그녀가 나에 대해 아무 관심도 없다는 생각에 그랬다. 짐작할 수 있듯이, 나는 늘 스펜로 씨 댁으로 또 한 번 초대받기를 눈 빠지게 기다렸다. 그리고 늘 실망했다, 한 번도 초대를 받지 못했으니까.\n\n크럽 부인은 통찰력 있는 여인이었음이 틀림없다. 이 사랑이 생겨난 지 고작 몇 주밖에 안 되었고, 아그네스에게조차 스펜로 씨 댁에 다녀왔다는 것 이상으로는 솔직히 쓸 용기가 나지 않아 \"그 집안 식구는,\" 하고 내가 덧붙이기를 \"딸 하나로 이루어져 있다\"라고만 적었을 무렵인데도 — 다시 말하지만 크럽 부인은 통찰력 있는 여인이었음이 틀림없다, 그 이른 단계에서조차 그 사실을 알아챘으니 말이다. 어느 날 저녁, 내가 몹시 풀이 죽어 있을 때 그녀가 올라와서(그때 그녀는 앞서 말한 그 병에 시달리고 있었다), 카더먼 팅크처를 대황과 섞고 정향 정유 일곱 방울로 향을 낸 것을 조금 나누어 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었는데, 그것이 자기 병에 가장 좋은 약이라는 것이었다. 혹시 그런 게 없거든, 그다음으로 좋은 브랜디라도 조금 달라고 했다. 그것이 자기 입맛에 그리 맞지는 않지만 그다음으로 좋은 약이라고 했다. 나는 첫 번째 약은 들어 본 적조차 없었고 두 번째 것은 늘 벽장에 두고 있었으므로, 크럽 부인에게 두 번째 것을 한 잔 주었다. 그러자 그녀는(그것이 어떤 부적절한 용도로 쓰이는 게 아닌가 하는 의심을 내가 품지 않도록) 내가 보는 앞에서 그것을 마시기 시작했다.\n\n\"기운 내세요, 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다. \"이렇게 풀 죽은 모습을 보고는 못 견디겠어요, 나리. 저도 한 아이의 어미랍니다.\"\n\n나는 그 사실이 나에게 어떻게 적용되는지 잘 알 수 없었으나, 그래도 최대한 너그럽게 크럽 부인을 향해 미소 지었다.\n\n\"자, 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다. \"외람되지만, 저는 그게 뭔지 알아요, 나리. 여기에는 한 아가씨가 얽혀 있는 거지요.\"\n\n\"크럽 부인?\" 내가 얼굴을 붉히며 응했다.\n\n\"오, 어쩜 그러세요! 마음 굳게 잡수세요, 나리!\" 크럽 부인이 격려하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"절대 포기하지 마세요, 나리! 그 아가씨가 나리한테 미소 짓지 않더라도, 미소 지어 줄 여자야 수두룩하답니다. 나리는 미소를 받을 만한 젊은 신사분이세요, 코퍼풀 나리, 그러니 나리는 자신의 값어치를 깨달으셔야 해요, 나리.\"\n\n크럽 부인은 늘 나를 코퍼풀 나리라고 불렀다. 첫째로는 분명 그게 내 이름이 아니어서였고, 둘째로는 내 짐작에, 어렴풋이나마 빨래하는 날과 무언가 연결 지어 그랬던 것 같다.\n\n\"무얼 보고 여기에 어떤 아가씨가 얽혀 있다고 짐작하시는 거예요, 크럽 부인?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼풀 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 자못 절절하게 말했다. \"저도 한 아이의 어미랍니다.\"\n\n한동안 크럽 부인은 그저 한 손을 자기 무명천 가슴에 얹은 채, 약을 홀짝홀짝 마시며 되돌아오는 통증에 대비해 몸을 다잡을 뿐이었다. 마침내 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"나리의 자상하신 고모님께서 나리를 위해 지금 이 방을 잡으셨을 때, 코퍼풀 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다. \"제가 한 말이 있어요, 이제야 제가 마음 쓸 만한 분을 찾았다고요. '하느님 감사합니다!' 이게 그때 제가 한 말이었어요, '이제야 제가 마음 쓸 만한 분을 찾았네!' — 나리는 잘 잡숫지도 않으시고, 잘 드시지도 않아요.\"\n\n\"바로 그걸 보고 그렇게 짐작하시는 거예요, 크럽 부인?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"나리.\" 크럽 부인이 자못 매서운 어조로 말했다. \"저는 나리 말고도 여러 젊은 신사분들 빨래를 해 드렸답니다. 젊은 신사분은 자기 몸을 너무 챙기시기도 하고, 너무 안 챙기시기도 해요. 머리를 너무 규칙적으로 빗기도 하고, 너무 불규칙하게 빗기도 하고요. 구두를 자기 발에 너무 큰 걸 신기도 하고, 너무 작은 걸 신기도 하지요. 그건 그 젊은 신사분이 타고난 성품이 어떻게 잡혀 있느냐에 따라 다른 거예요. 하지만 어느 쪽 끝으로 가시든, 나리, 그 두 가지 다에 한 아가씨가 얽혀 있는 거랍니다.\"\n\n크럽 부인이 어찌나 단호하게 고개를 가로젓던지, 나에게는 발 디딜 한 치의 유리한 자리도 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n\"바로 나리보다 먼저 이 집에서 돌아가신 그 신사분도요.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다. \"사랑에 빠지셨거든요 — 술집 아가씨한테요 — 그러고는 술을 많이 드셔서 한껏 부풀어 올랐는데도 곧장 조끼를 줄이게 하셨답니다.\"\n\n\"크럽 부인.\" 내가 말했다. \"부탁이니, 제 경우의 그 아가씨를 술집 아가씨나 그 비슷한 것과 엮지 말아 주세요, 제발.\"\n\n\"코퍼풀 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 응했다. \"저도 한 아이의 어미인데, 그럴 리가요. 외람되었다면 용서하세요, 나리. 저는 결코 환영받지 못할 데에 끼어들고 싶지는 않답니다. 하지만 나리는 젊은 신사분이세요, 코퍼풀 나리, 그러니 제가 드리는 충고는, 기운을 내시고, 나리, 마음을 굳게 잡수시고, 자신의 값어치를 아시라는 거예요. 만약 나리가 무언가에 빠져 보신다면, 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다. \"이를테면 구주희에라도 빠져 보신다면, 그건 몸에도 좋으니, 마음이 풀리고 나리한테도 도움이 될 거예요.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 크럽 부인은 브랜디를 — 다 비워 버렸으면서 — 무척 아끼는 척하며, 위풍당당한 절로 나에게 고마움을 표하고 물러갔다. 그녀의 모습이 어둑한 입구 속으로 사라질 때, 이 충고는 분명 크럽 부인 쪽에서 약간 주제넘게 군 처사로 내 마음에 비쳤다. 그러나 동시에 나는 그것을, 다른 관점에서 보아, 지혜로운 자에게 건네는 한마디이자 앞으로 내 비밀을 좀 더 잘 간수하라는 경고로 기꺼이 받아들였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 27. TOMMY TRADDLES\n\n크럽 부인의 충고 때문이었는지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그저 스키틀즈라는 낱말과 트래들스라는 이름의 소리가 어딘가 닮았다는 것보다 더 그럴듯한 이유가 없었는지도 모르지만, 어쨌든 이튿날 나는 트래들스를 찾아가 봐야겠다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐다. 그가 말했던 기한은 이미 한참 지나 있었고, 그는 캠던타운의 수의학 대학 근처 작은 거리에 살고 있었다. 그쪽 방면에 사는 우리 사무소 직원 하나가 일러 주기로, 그 거리에는 주로 신사 학생들이 세 들어 사는데, 그들이 살아 있는 당나귀를 사다가 자기 방에서 그 네발짐승을 가지고 실험을 한다고 했다. 그 직원에게서 문제의 학구적인 동네로 가는 길을 알아낸 뒤, 나는 바로 그날 오후에 옛 학교 친구를 찾아 나섰다.\n\n그 거리는, 트래들스를 생각하면, 내가 바랐던 만큼 마음에 드는 곳이 아니었다. 그곳 주민들은 자기에게 필요 없는 자질구레한 것들을 죄다 길바닥에 내던지는 버릇이 있는 듯했는데, 그래서 거리는 고약한 냄새가 나고 질척거릴 뿐 아니라, 양배추 잎사귀 때문에 지저분하기까지 했다. 게다가 쓰레기가 죄다 채소만은 아니었으니, 내가 찾는 번지를 두리번거리며 살피는 동안 신발 한 짝, 구겨진 냄비 하나, 검은 보닛 하나, 그리고 우산 하나가 저마다 다른 정도로 썩어 가는 꼴을 내 눈으로 똑똑히 보았다.\n\n그곳의 전반적인 분위기는 내가 미코버 씨 내외와 함께 살던 시절을 강렬하게 떠올리게 했다. 내가 찾는 그 집에는 뭐라 형용할 수 없는, 빛바랜 점잖음의 기색이 배어 있어서, 그 거리의 다른 집들과는 사뭇 달라 보였다. 그 거리의 집들은 죄다 단조롭기 짝이 없는 한 가지 본을 따라 지어져, 마치 집 그리기를 배우는 서툰 사내아이가 벽돌과 회반죽으로 어설픈 갈고리 모양을 끄적이던 버릇에서 아직 벗어나지 못한 채 그려 낸 초기 습작들처럼 보였는데 말이다. 그 빛바랜 분위기가 나에게 미코버 씨 내외를 더더욱 또렷이 떠올리게 했다. 마침 오후의 우유 배달부에게 문이 열리는 순간 그 집 앞에 다다른 나는 미코버 씨 내외가 한층 더 강렬하게 떠올랐다.\n\n\"이봐,\" 우유 배달부가 아주 어린 하녀에게 말했다. \"내 그 외상 장부 말이야, 무슨 소식이라도 들었나?\"\n\n\"아, 주인님이 당장 처리하시겠다고 하셨어요.\" 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그게 말이지,\" 우유 배달부는 아무 대답도 못 들은 양 말을 이어 갔는데, 그 말투로 미루어 보아 어린 하녀보다는 집 안에 있는 누군가에게 일러 주려고 하는 말 같았다 — 통로 안쪽을 쏘아보는 그 태도가 그런 인상을 더욱 굳혔다 — \"그게 말이지, 그 외상 장부가 어찌나 오래 굴러가는지, 이제 아주 영영 도망쳐서 다시는 소식도 못 듣게 생긴 것 같단 말씀이야. 자, 나는 그런 꼴 못 참아, 알겠어!\" 우유 배달부는 여전히 목소리를 집 안으로 던지며, 통로 안쪽을 노려보면서 말했다.\n\n말이 난 김에 덧붙이자면, 우유처럼 순한 물건을 파는 사람치고 이보다 더 어울리지 않는 자도 없었다. 그 사나운 거동은 푸줏간 주인이나 브랜디 장수에게나 어울렸을 법했다.\n\n어린 하녀의 목소리는 가물가물해졌지만, 그 입술이 움직이는 모양으로 보아 또다시 당장 처리될 거라고 중얼거리는 듯했다.\n\n\"내 한마디 하지,\" 우유 배달부가 그제야 처음으로 그 애를 빤히 쏘아보며, 턱을 잡고 말했다. \"너 우유 좋아하냐?\"\n\n\"네, 좋아해요.\" 그 애가 대답했다. \"좋아.\" 우유 배달부가 말했다. \"그럼 내일은 한 방울도 못 먹을 줄 알아. 알아들었어? 내일은 우유 부스러기 하나도 못 받을 거야.\"\n\n내 보기에 그 애는 대체로, 오늘만이라도 우유를 받을 수 있다는 사실에 오히려 안도하는 눈치였다. 우유 배달부는 음침하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 나서 턱을 놓아 주더니, 호의라고는 도무지 찾아볼 수 없는 태도로 통을 열어 그 집 항아리에 늘 받던 만큼을 부어 넣었다. 그러고는 투덜거리며 떠나갔고, 옆집 앞에서 앙심에 찬 비명 같은 소리로 자기 장사 외치는 소리를 질러 댔다.\n\n\"트래들스 씨가 여기 사나요?\" 내가 그제야 물었다.\n\n통로 끝에서 정체 모를 목소리가 \"네\" 하고 대답했다. 그러자 어린 하녀가 \"네\" 하고 따라 했다.\n\n\"집에 계시나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n다시 그 정체 모를 목소리가 그렇다고 대답했고, 다시 하녀가 그 말을 메아리처럼 따라 했다. 그래서 나는 안으로 들어가, 하녀가 일러 주는 대로 위층으로 올라갔다. 뒤편 거실 문을 지날 때, 아마도 그 정체 모를 목소리의 임자일 정체 모를 눈 하나가 나를 살피고 있다는 것을 의식하면서.\n\n계단 꼭대기에 다다랐을 때 — 그 집은 일 층 위로 딱 한 층밖에 없었다 — 트래들스가 층계참에서 나를 맞으러 나와 있었다. 그는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐하며, 진심 어린 환대로 자기 작은 방으로 맞아들였다. 그 방은 집 앞쪽에 있었고, 가구는 단출했지만 무척이나 깔끔했다. 보아하니 그가 가진 방은 그것 하나뿐이었으니, 방 안에 소파 겸 침대가 놓여 있었고, 그의 구두약 솔과 구두약은 책들 틈에 — 맨 위 선반 사전 뒤에 — 끼어 있었다. 그의 탁자는 서류로 뒤덮여 있었고, 그는 낡은 외투를 걸친 채 한창 일에 매달려 있었다. 내가 무엇을 유심히 본 것은 아니었지만, 자리에 앉으면서 나는 모든 것을, 그의 도자기 잉크스탠드에 그려진 교회 풍경까지도 다 보았다 — 이 또한 옛 미코버 시절에 내 안에 굳어진 능력이었다. 그가 서랍장을 가리고, 장화와 면도 거울 따위를 갈무리하려고 이리저리 궁리해서 마련한 여러 가지 솜씨가 유독 내 마음에 와닿았으니, 이는 옛날에 편지지로 파리를 넣어 둘 코끼리 굴 모형을 만들고, 모진 대접을 받을 때면 내가 앞서 그토록 자주 이야기한 그 잊지 못할 예술품을 만들며 스스로를 달래던 바로 그 트래들스가 여전하다는 증거였다.\n\n방 한구석에는 무언가가 커다란 흰 천으로 말끔히 덮여 있었다. 나는 그게 무엇인지 도무지 알아볼 수가 없었다.\n\n\"트래들스,\" 자리에 앉은 뒤 다시 그와 악수하며 내가 말했다. \"너를 보니 정말 반갑다.\"\n\n\"나야말로 너를 보니 반갑다, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답했다. \"정말이지 너를 보니 기뻐. 일리 플레이스에서 우리가 마주쳤을 때, 내가 너를 보고 진심으로 기뻤고, 너도 나를 보고 진심으로 기뻐했다고 확신했기에, 사무실 주소 대신 이 주소를 알려 준 거야.\" \"아! 너 사무실도 있어?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 방 하나에 통로 하나를 사 분의 일씩 나눠 쓰고, 사무원도 사 분의 일만 쓰는 셈이지.\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"나 말고 셋이 더 합쳐서 사무실 한 칸을 같이 쓰거든 — 그래야 일하는 티가 나니까 — 사무원도 넷이 나눠 쓴단 말이야. 나한테는 일주일에 반 크라운씩 드는 셈이지.\"\n\n그가 이 설명을 하며 짓는 그 미소 속에서, 나는 그의 옛날 그대로의 순박한 성품과 너그러운 성미가, 그리고 또 그의 옛날 그대로의 박복한 운수마저 나를 향해 빙긋 웃고 있는 것 같았다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 자존심 같은 게 있어서, 코퍼필드, 그래서 여기 주소를 잘 안 알려 주는 게 아니란 건 너도 알 거야.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그저 나를 찾아오는 사람들 때문이지. 그 사람들이 여기 오는 걸 별로 안 좋아할지도 모르니까. 나 자신으로 말하자면, 나는 온갖 어려움과 맞서 가며 세상을 헤쳐 나가고 있고, 그렇지 않은 척 시늉을 한다면 그거야말로 우스운 일이지.\"\n\n\"법정 변호사가 되려고 공부하고 있다고, 워터브룩 씨가 알려 주던데?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 그래.\" 트래들스가 두 손을 천천히 비비며 말했다. \"법정 변호사가 되려고 공부 중이야. 사실은, 꽤 오래 미뤄 두었다가 이제 막 학기 출석을 시작했어. 견습 계약을 맺은 지는 한참 됐는데, 그 백 파운드 치르는 게 어찌나 힘들던지. 정말 죽을 맛이었어!\" 트래들스가 마치 이를 뽑기라도 한 듯 움찔하며 말했다.\n\n\"트래들스, 여기 앉아서 너를 보고 있으니 자꾸 떠오르는 게 뭔지 알아?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아니.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 입던 그 하늘색 양복.\"\n\n\"이런, 그래 맞아!\" 트래들스가 웃으며 외쳤다. \"팔이랑 다리가 꽉 끼던 거 말이지? 이런! 참! 그때가 행복한 시절이었어, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"우리 교장 선생님이 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않고도 우리를 좀 더 행복하게 해 줄 수 있었을 거라고 나는 생각하지만 말이야.\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"그럴 수도 있었겠지.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"하지만 이런, 그래도 재미난 일이 꽤 많았잖아. 침실에서 보내던 밤들 기억나? 우리가 야식 먹던 거 말이야. 네가 이야기 들려주던 거? 하하하! 그리고 내가 멜 선생님 일로 울다가 매 맞은 거 기억나? 늙은 크리클! 그 양반도 다시 한번 보고 싶다니까!\"\n\n\"그 사람은 너한테 짐승 같은 짓을 했어, 트래들스.\" 내가 분개해서 말했다. 그가 워낙 좋은 기분으로 말하니, 마치 어제 그가 매 맞는 꼴을 본 것처럼 느껴졌던 것이다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각해?\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"정말? 하긴 좀 그랬을지도 모르지. 하지만 다 끝난 일이야, 한참 전에. 늙은 크리클!\"\n\n\"그럼 너는 큰아버지 손에서 자란 거지?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 그렇고말고!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"내가 늘 편지 쓰겠다고 하던 그 양반. 그러고는 늘 안 썼던 그분 말이야, 응! 하하하! 그래, 그때는 큰아버지가 계셨지. 내가 학교를 떠나고 얼마 안 돼서 돌아가셨어.\"\n\n\"저런!\"\n\n\"응. 은퇴한 — 그걸 뭐라고 부르더라! — 포목상 — 옷감 장수였는데 — 나를 상속자로 삼으셨지. 그런데 내가 다 크고 나니까 나를 안 좋아하시더라고.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇다는 거야?\" 내가 말했다. 그가 어찌나 태연하던지, 무슨 다른 뜻이 있는 게 아닌가 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"아이고, 그렇다니까, 코퍼필드! 정말이야.\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"안된 일이었지만, 그분이 나를 도무지 좋아하지 않으셨어. 내가 당신 기대와는 전혀 딴판이라고 하시더니, 그래서 가정부랑 결혼해 버리셨지.\"\n\n\"그래서 너는 어떻게 했는데?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"딱히 뭘 한 건 없어.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"세상에 내보내질 때를 기다리면서 그분들이랑 같이 살았지. 그러다 안타깝게도 그분 통풍이 위장으로 번지는 바람에 — 그래서 돌아가셨고, 그래서 그 부인은 젊은 남자랑 결혼했고, 그래서 나는 아무것도 못 받게 됐어.\"\n\n\"결국 아무것도 못 받았단 말이야, 트래들스?\"\n\n\"아이고, 받긴 받았지!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"오십 파운드 받았어. 나는 어떤 직업으로도 길러진 적이 없어서, 처음엔 무얼 해야 할지 막막했지. 그래도 살렘 하우스를 다녔던 어느 전문직 양반의 아들 — 야울러라고, 코가 한쪽으로 비뚤어진 녀석 도움으로 시작했어. 그 녀석 기억나?\"\n\n아니. 그는 나와 같은 시절에 있지 않았다. 내가 다니던 시절에는 코들이 다 곧았다.\n\n\"뭐 상관없어.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그 녀석 도움으로, 나는 법률 서류를 베껴 쓰는 일부터 시작했어. 그게 그리 신통치 않아서, 그 다음엔 사건 개요를 정리해 주고, 발췌문을 만들고, 그런 일을 하기 시작했지. 나는 우직하게 매달리는 부류라서 말이야, 코퍼필드, 그런 일을 요점만 추려서 하는 법을 익혀 뒀거든. 자! 그러다 보니 나도 법학생으로 등록해 볼까 하는 생각이 들었고, 그게 오십 파운드 남은 걸 죄다 잡아먹어 버렸어. 그래도 야울러가 사무소 한두 군데를 더 소개해 줬는데 — 워터브룩 씨 사무소도 그중 하나야 — 그래서 일거리를 꽤 많이 얻었지. 게다가 운 좋게도 출판 쪽에 종사하는 사람 하나와 알게 됐는데, 백과사전을 만들던 참이라, 나한테 일을 맡기더라고. 그리고 사실,\" (탁자를 흘끗 보며) \"바로 지금도 그 사람 일을 하고 있는 중이야. 나는 편찬자로서는 그리 나쁘지 않아, 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 무슨 말을 하든 한결같이 쾌활하고 자신 있는 태도를 잃지 않으며 말했다. \"그런데 창의력이라곤 도통 없어. 한 톨도 없지. 나만큼 독창성 없는 젊은이는 세상에 또 없을걸.\"\n\n트래들스가 이 말에 내가 당연히 맞장구쳐 주기를 바라는 눈치였으므로, 나는 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러자 그는 아까처럼 한결같이 쾌활하면서도 참을성 있는 태도로 — 이보다 더 나은 표현을 못 찾겠다 — 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그래서, 조금씩 조금씩, 호화롭게 살지 않으면서, 결국 마침내 백 파운드를 긁어모았어.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그리고 다행히 그건 다 갚았지 — 비록 그게 — 정말이지 그게,\" 트래들스가 또 이를 뽑은 사람처럼 움찔하며 말했다. \"죽을 맛이었지만 말이야. 나는 지금도 아까 말한 그런 일로 먹고살고 있고, 언젠가는 어느 신문사하고 연줄이 닿았으면 해. 그렇게 되면 거의 출셋길이 트이는 셈이거든. 자, 코퍼필드, 너는 그 사람 좋은 얼굴 그대로, 옛날하고 어찌나 똑같은지, 너를 보고 있는 게 너무 즐거워서 나는 아무것도 숨기지 않을 거야. 그러니까 너도 알아 둬야 해, 나 약혼했다는 걸.\"\n\n약혼! 아, 도라!\n\n\"그 사람은 부목사의 딸이야.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"데번셔 시골에 사는, 열 남매 중 하나지. 그래!\" 그가 내 눈이 저도 모르게 잉크스탠드의 그 풍경으로 흘끗 향하는 것을 보았던 것이다. \"그게 바로 그 교회야! 이 문에서 나와서 이쪽 왼쪽으로 돌아오면,\" 손가락으로 잉크스탠드를 짚어 가며, \"내가 지금 이 펜을 쥐고 있는 바로 이 자리에, 그 집이 서 있어 — 그러니까, 교회 쪽을 마주 보고 말이지.\"\n\n그가 이런 세세한 이야기에 푹 빠져 드는 그 기쁨이 나에게 온전히 와닿은 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다. 바로 그 순간 내 이기적인 생각은 스펜로 씨의 집과 정원의 평면도를 그리고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"그 사람은 정말 사랑스러운 아가씨야!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"나보다 나이가 조금 많지만, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라니까! 내가 시외로 나간다고 했었지? 거기 다녀온 거야. 걸어서 갔다가 걸어서 돌아왔는데, 더없이 즐거운 시간을 보냈어! 우리 약혼은 아마 꽤 길어질 것 같지만, 우리 좌우명이 '기다리며 바라자!'거든. 우린 늘 그렇게 말해. '기다리며 바라자', 늘 그렇게 말하지. 그리고 그 사람은 나를 위해서라면, 코퍼필드, 예순 살이 될 때까지라도 — 네가 댈 수 있는 어떤 나이까지라도 기다려 줄 거야!\"\n\n트래들스는 의자에서 일어나, 의기양양한 미소를 띠고, 아까 내가 눈여겨보았던 그 흰 천 위에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"하지만 말이야,\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 살림 채비를 아예 시작도 안 한 건 아니야. 아니, 아니지. 우린 시작했어. 차차 늘려 가야겠지만, 어쨌든 시작은 한 거야. 자,\" 무척이나 자랑스럽고 조심스럽게 천을 걷어 내며, \"이게 살림 밑천으로 마련한 가구 두 점이야. 이 화분이랑 받침대는 그 사람이 직접 산 거고. 이걸 거실 창가에 놓고,\" 트래들스가 그것을 더 흐뭇하게 바라보려고 조금 물러서며 말했다. \"거기다 화초를 심으면 — 그럼 끝나는 거야! 이 대리석 상판을 얹은 자그마한 둥근 탁자는 (둘레가 두 자 열 치야) 내가 샀지. 책을 한 권 내려놓고 싶다든가, 아니면 누가 너나 네 아내를 보러 와서 찻잔 놓을 자리가 필요하다든가 — 그럼 또 그걸로 끝나는 거지!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"정말 솜씨가 기막힌 물건이야 — 바위처럼 든든하다니까!\" 나는 그 둘 다를 한껏 칭찬했고, 트래들스는 걷어 냈던 것만큼이나 조심스럽게 천을 다시 덮어 두었다.\n\n\"살림 채비로 치자면 대단한 건 아니지만,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그래도 어디야. 식탁보랑 베갯잇이랑, 그런 종류 물건들이 제일 기를 죽인단 말이야, 코퍼필드. 철물도 그렇지 — 양초 상자랑 석쇠랑, 그런 살림 필수품들 말이야 — 그런 게 돈이 들어서 쌓이고 쌓이거든. 그래도, '기다리며 바라자!'야. 그리고 정말이지 그 사람은 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라니까!\"\n\n\"나도 그건 아주 확신해.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그러는 동안에도,\" 트래들스가 의자로 돌아오며 말했다. \"이게 내 신세 한탄 이야기의 끝이야 — 나는 할 수 있는 한 잘 버텨 나가고 있어. 벌이는 많지 않아도, 쓰는 것도 많지 않으니까. 보통은 아래층 사람들한테서 하숙을 하는데, 정말이지 더없이 유쾌한 사람들이야. 미코버 씨 내외 두 분 다 세상 풍파를 꽤 겪은 분들이라, 함께 있으면 정말 좋아.\"\n\n\"트래들스, 이 친구야!\" 내가 얼른 외쳤다. \"지금 무슨 소리를 하는 거야?\"\n\n트래들스는 내가 무슨 소리를 하는 거냐는 듯 나를 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"미코버 씨 내외라니!\" 내가 되풀이했다. \"아니, 나 그 두 분하고 아주 가깝게 아는 사이야!\"\n\n마침 절묘하게 현관문을 두 번 두드리는 소리가 났는데, 윈저 테라스 시절의 옛 경험으로 익히 아는 그 소리, 미코버 씨 말고는 그 문을 그렇게 두드릴 사람이 결코 없는 그 소리가, 그분들이 바로 내 옛 친구들이라는 것에 대한 내 마음속 일말의 의심마저 깨끗이 풀어 주었다. 나는 트래들스에게 집주인더러 올라오시라고 청해 달라고 부탁했다. 트래들스는 그 말대로 난간 너머로 그렇게 청했고, 미코버 씨가 — 조금도 변하지 않은 채로, 그 꼭 끼는 바지며 지팡이며 셔츠 깃이며 외알 안경까지 죄다 옛날 그대로인 채로 — 점잖으면서도 젊은이다운 기색으로 방 안으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"실례했습니다, 트래들스 씨.\" 미코버 씨가 부드러운 곡조를 흥얼거리다 멈추며, 옛날 그대로 굴리는 말투로 말했다. \"이 거처와 무관한 다른 분이 선생의 보금자리에 와 계신 줄은 미처 몰랐습니다.\"\n\n미코버 씨가 나에게 가볍게 고개를 숙이고 셔츠 깃을 추켜올렸다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 미코버 씨?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"정말 황송한 인사로군요. 저는 현상 그대로올시다.\"\n\n\"미코버 부인께서는요?\" 내가 이어서 물었다.\n\n\"선생,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그 사람도, 고맙게도, 현상 그대로올시다.\"\n\n\"아이들은요, 미코버 씨?\"\n\n\"선생,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"기쁘게 답하건대, 그 아이들도 마찬가지로 무병의 즐거움을 누리고 있소이다.\"\n\n이러는 내내, 미코버 씨는 나와 얼굴을 마주 대고 서 있으면서도 나를 도무지 알아보지 못했다. 그러나 이제 내가 빙긋 웃는 것을 보더니, 그는 내 이목구비를 더 유심히 뜯어보고, 뒤로 한 발 물러서며 외쳤다. \"이럴 수가! 코퍼필드를 다시 뵙는 기쁨을 누리다니!\" 그러고는 더없이 격렬하게 두 손을 잡고 흔들었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 트래들스 씨!\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"선생이 내 젊은 날의 벗이요, 지난날의 동무를 알고 지내는 사이라는 걸 알게 되다니! 여보!\" 그가 난간 너머로 미코버 부인을 부르는 동안, 트래들스는 나를 두고 하는 이런 묘사에 (그럴 만도 하게) 적잖이 놀란 표정을 지었다. \"트래들스 씨 방에 한 신사분이 와 계신데, 당신에게 소개해 드리는 기쁨을 누리고 싶다는구려, 여보!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 곧바로 다시 나타나, 나와 또 한 번 악수를 했다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 좋은 벗 박사님은 어떻게 지내시나, 코퍼필드?\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 캔터베리의 그 일가분들 모두는?\"\n\n\"다들 좋은 소식뿐입니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그것 참 더없이 기쁜 소식이로군.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"우리가 마지막으로 만난 게 캔터베리였지. 초서가 불멸로 남긴 그 종교적 건축물의 그늘 아래에서 — 비유적으로 말하자면 말일세 — 그곳은 예부터 가장 머나먼 구석구석에서 온 순례자들의 발길이 모이던 곳이었지 — 요컨대,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그 대성당 바로 코앞에서 말이오.\"\n\n나는 그렇다고 대답했다. 미코버 씨는 가능한 한 청산유수로 이야기를 이어 갔지만, 내가 보기에는 그 얼굴에 어린 어떤 근심의 기색으로 미루어, 옆방에서 나는 소리에 — 미코버 부인이 손을 씻는 소리며, 뻑뻑하게 잘 안 열리는 서랍들을 황급히 여닫는 소리에 — 신경을 쓰고 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"보다시피 우리는, 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 트래들스를 한쪽 눈으로 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"지금은, 소박하고 보잘것없다 할 만한 규모로 자리를 잡고 있다네. 하지만 자네도 알다시피, 나는 내 살아온 여정에서 온갖 어려움을 헤쳐 나오고, 숱한 장애물을 정복해 왔지. 또한 자네도 익히 아는 사실이지만, 내 인생에는, 어떤 기대하던 일이 떠오를 때까지 잠시 멈춰 서 있어야 할 때가 있었고, 무언가 — 외람되게도 그렇게 부른다고 비난받지 않기를 바라네만 — 도약을 하기에 앞서 한 걸음 물러나야 할 때가 있었네. 지금이 바로 인간의 삶에서 그런 중대한 고비 가운데 하나일세. 자네는 지금, 도약을 위해 한 걸음 물러나 있는 나를 보고 있는 것이고, 머잖아 힘찬 도약이 그 결실로 나타나리라고 나는 굳게 믿을 만한 온갖 근거를 가지고 있네.\"\n\n내가 만족스럽다는 뜻을 표하고 있는데, 미코버 부인이 들어왔다. 예전보다 옷차림이 조금 더 후줄근해진 것 같았다 — 아니면 그저 익숙지 않은 내 눈에 그렇게 보인 것인지도 모르지만 — 그래도 손님 맞을 채비는 어느 정도 해서, 갈색 장갑을 한 켤레 끼고 있었다.\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 그녀를 나에게로 이끌며 말했다. \"여기 코퍼필드라는 이름의 신사분이, 당신과 다시 알고 지내기를 바라고 계신다오.\"\n\n결과적으로 보면, 이 소식은 부드럽게 차근차근 꺼내는 편이 나았을 것이다. 미코버 부인이 몸이 약한 상태였던 터라 그 말에 그만 정신을 못 차리고 어찌나 몸이 안 좋아졌던지, 미코버 씨가 몹시 허둥대며 뒤뜰 빗물통으로 달려가 대야 가득 물을 떠다가 그녀의 이마를 적셔 주어야 했다. 그래도 그녀는 곧 정신을 차렸고, 정말로 나를 보고 기뻐했다. 우리는 다 같이 반 시간쯤 이야기를 나누었다. 나는 쌍둥이들 안부를 물었는데, 부인 말로는 그 아이들이 '엄청 큰 녀석들로 자랐다'고 했고, 미코버 도련님과 미코버 아가씨의 안부도 물었더니, 그 둘은 '완전 거인이 됐다'고 했지만, 그날은 정작 모습을 보이지 않았다.\n\n미코버 씨는 내가 저녁 식사까지 하고 가기를 무척이나 바랐다. 나도 그러는 게 싫지는 않았겠지만, 미코버 부인의 눈빛에서 식은 고기가 얼마나 되나 가늠하며 곤혹스러워하는 기색을 읽은 것 같았다. 그래서 나는 다른 약속이 있다는 핑계를 댔고, 그러자 미코버 부인의 안색이 대번에 환해지는 것을 보고서, 그 약속을 무르라는 온갖 권유에 끝내 넘어가지 않았다.\n\n하지만 나는 트래들스와 미코버 씨 내외에게, 떠나기 전에 두 분이 우리 집에 와서 함께 저녁을 들 날을 정해야 한다고 말했다. 트래들스가 약속해 둔 일거리가 있어서 날짜를 좀 멀찍이 잡을 수밖에 없었지만, 어쨌든 우리 모두에게 맞는 날로 약속이 정해졌고, 그러고 나서 나는 작별을 고했다.\n\n미코버 씨는 내가 왔던 길보다 가까운 길을 알려 주겠다는 핑계로 거리 모퉁이까지 나를 바래다주었는데, 옛 친구에게 은밀히 몇 마디 하고 싶어서였다고 (그가 나에게 설명하기를) 했다.\n\n\"이보게, 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"지금 같은 처지에, 우리 지붕 아래에 — 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 — 자네 친구 트래들스에게서 반짝이는 그런 정신을, 그런 반짝임을 두고 산다는 게 말로 다 할 수 없는 위안이라는 걸 굳이 자네한테 일러 줄 필요도 없겠지. 거실 창에 단단한 사탕과자를 늘어놓고 파는 세탁부 아낙이 바로 옆집에 살고, 보 가의 경찰관이 길 건너에 사는 처지이니, 그 친구와 어울리는 것이 나와 미코버 부인에게 얼마나 큰 위로가 되는지 자네도 짐작할 수 있을 걸세. 나는 지금, 코퍼필드, 수수료를 받고 곡물을 파는 일에 종사하고 있네. 그게 그리 돈벌이가 되는 직업은 아니야 — 다시 말해, 벌이가 시원찮다는 게지 — 그래서 일시적으로 금전적인 곤경을 좀 겪고 있는 게 그 결과일세. 하지만 기쁘게 덧붙이건대, 이제 머잖아 무언가 떠오를 전망이 (어느 방면인지는 말할 자유가 없네만) 눈앞에 있는데, 나는 그것이 나 자신과, 또 내가 진심으로 마음을 쓰고 있는 자네 친구 트래들스를 모두 영구히 건사할 수 있게 해 주리라고 믿네. 어쩌면 자네는, 미코버 부인이 머잖아 그 애정의 결실들에 또 하나가 보태질 가능성이 — 요컨대 그 어린것들 무리에 하나가 더해질 가능성이 — 영 없지만은 않은 몸 상태라는 소식을 들을 마음의 준비가 되어 있을지도 모르겠군. 미코버 부인의 친정 식구들은 친절하게도 이런 형편에 대해 못마땅함을 표해 왔네. 나는 그저, 그게 그들이 참견할 일인지 도무지 모르겠다는 것과, 그런 감정의 발로를 나는 경멸과 항거로 물리친다는 것을 한마디 해 둘 뿐일세!\"\n\n그러고 나서 미코버 씨는 나와 또 한 번 악수를 하고, 떠나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 28. Mr. MICAWBER’S GAUNTLET\n\n내가 새로 다시 찾은 옛 친구들을 대접하기로 한 날이 다가오기 전까지, 나는 주로 도라와 커피로 살았다. 사랑에 빠져 애태우는 처지였던지라 입맛도 시들었는데, 나는 그게 오히려 반가웠다. 저녁밥을 자연스레 맛있게 먹는다는 것이 어쩐지 도라를 향한 배신 행위처럼 느껴졌기 때문이다. 그렇게 많이 걸어 다녀도 평소처럼 입맛이 돋는 효과는 나타나지 않았는데, 상심이 신선한 바깥 공기를 상쇄해 버린 탓이었다. 게다가 나는 내 인생의 이 시기에 얻은 뼈저린 경험에 비추어, 꽉 끼는 구두 때문에 늘 고통에 시달리는 사람이라면 누구든 고기 음식을 마음껏 즐기는 즐거움을 자유로이 키울 수 있을지 의심스럽다. 위장이 힘차게 제 일을 하기 전에는, 먼저 발끝이 평화로워야 하는 법인 것 같다.\n\n이 자그마한 집들이 모임을 위해, 나는 예전처럼 거창하게 준비하는 짓을 되풀이하지 않았다. 그저 가자미 두 마리, 양고기 다리 살 한 덩이, 그리고 비둘기 파이 하나를 마련했을 뿐이다. 생선과 고기를 요리하는 일을 두고 내가 처음 머뭇머뭇 운을 떼자, 크루프 부인은 반란을 일으키더니 모욕이라도 당한 양 위엄을 차리며 이렇게 말했다. \"안 돼요! 안 돼요, 나리! 그런 부탁을 나리가 저한테 하실 리가 없지요. 나리는 절 잘 아시니까, 제 마음이 흡—족하게 만족할 수 없는 일은 제가 못 한다는 걸 아실 텐데요!\" 하지만 결국에는 타협이 이루어졌다. 크루프 부인은 그 뒤로 두 주일 동안 내가 바깥에서 저녁을 먹는다는 조건으로 이 위업을 해내기로 승낙했다.\n\n여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 크루프 부인이 나를 상대로 폭정을 세운 탓에 내가 겪은 고초는 끔찍했다. 나는 그토록 누군가를 무서워한 적이 없었다. 우리는 무슨 일이든 타협으로 결판을 냈다. 내가 조금이라도 머뭇거리면, 그녀는 늘 자기 몸속 어딘가에 매복해 있다가 아주 짧은 통보만으로도 당장 그녀의 급소를 덮칠 채비를 하고 있는 그 놀라운 병에 사로잡혔다. 점잖게 여섯 번이나 당겨도 소용이 없어 내가 조바심에 못 이겨 초인종 줄을 잡아당기면, 마침내 그녀가 나타나는데 — 그나마도 결코 믿을 수 없는 일이었다 — 그녀는 나무라는 낯빛으로 나타나, 숨을 헐떡이며 문 가까운 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 무명천을 댄 가슴팍에 손을 얹고는, 어찌나 몸져눕는지 나는 브랜디든 뭐든 무엇을 내주는 희생을 치르고서라도 그녀를 떨쳐 버리게 되어 차라리 다행이라 여길 지경이었다. 내가 오후 다섯 시에 잠자리를 펴는 것에 반대하기라도 하면 — 지금도 나는 그게 불편한 처사라고 생각한다 — 그 상처받은 감수성이 깃든 무명천 가슴팍 쪽으로 손짓 한 번 까딱하는 것만으로도 나는 더듬더듬 사과를 늘어놓기에 충분했다. 한마디로, 나는 크루프 부인의 비위를 거스르느니 떳떳한 방법이라면 무슨 짓이든 했을 것이다. 그녀는 내 인생의 공포였다.\n\n나는 이 만찬을 위해 손재주 좋은 그 젊은이를 다시 부르는 대신, 중고 식기 운반대를 하나 샀다. 그 젊은이에게 편견을 품게 된 것은, 어느 일요일 아침 스트랜드 거리에서 그와 마주쳤는데, 그가 내 조끼와 놀랍도록 닮은 조끼를 입고 있었던 탓이다. 그 조끼는 지난번 그 모임 이후로 사라지고 없던 것이었다. '젊은 계집애'는 다시 불렀다. 다만 음식 접시를 들여오기만 하고, 그런 다음에는 바깥문 너머 층계참으로 물러나 있어야 한다는 조건을 달았다. 거기라면 그녀가 버릇처럼 들이는 코를 훌쩍거리는 소리도 손님들에게는 들리지 않을 테고, 접시 위로 코를 빠뜨리는 일도 물리적으로 불가능할 터였다.\n\n미코버 씨가 제조할 펀치 한 사발의 재료를 갖춰 두고, 미코버 부인이 내 화장대에서 몸단장을 하는 데 보탬이 되도록 라벤더 화장수 한 병, 밀랍 양초 두 자루, 갖가지 핀을 꽂은 종이 한 장, 그리고 바늘겨레 하나를 마련해 두고, 또 미코버 부인이 편하도록 내 침실 난로에 불도 지피게 하고, 식탁보까지 내 손으로 직접 깔아 둔 다음, 나는 차분한 마음으로 결과를 기다렸다.\n\n약속한 시간에, 손님 셋이 함께 도착했다. 미코버 씨는 평소보다 셔츠 깃을 더 세우고, 외알 안경에는 새 리본을 달고 있었다. 미코버 부인은 모자를 누르스름한 갈색 종이 꾸러미에 싸서 들고 있었다. 트래들스가 그 꾸러미를 들고, 한쪽 팔로는 미코버 부인을 부축하고 있었다. 세 사람 모두 내 거처를 보고 무척 기뻐했다. 내가 미코버 부인을 화장대로 안내하자, 그녀는 자기를 위해 마련된 그 규모를 보고는 어찌나 황홀해하던지, 미코버 씨를 불러 들어와서 보라고 했다.\n\n\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"이거 정말 호사로구먼. 이런 생활 방식을 보니, 내가 아직 독신이고 미코버 부인이 혼인의 제단 앞에서 신의를 맹세해 달라는 청을 받기도 전이던 시절이 떠오르네그려.\"\n\n\"이이 말은, 자기가 청혼하기 전이라는 뜻이에요, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 미코버 부인이 짐짓 장난스럽게 말했다. \"다른 사람들 일까지는 장담 못 하니까요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 갑자기 진지해지며 받았다. \"나는 다른 사람들 일을 장담할 마음이 조금도 없소. 헤아릴 길 없는 운명의 섭리 속에서 당신이 나를 위해 점지되었을 때, 어쩌면 당신은, 오래도록 발버둥 치다가 끝내 복잡하기 짝이 없는 금전 문제의 희생양으로 쓰러질 운명을 타고난 어떤 사내를 위해 점지되었을지도 모른다는 걸, 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있으니 말이오. 당신이 무얼 빗대어 말하는지 알겠소, 여보. 가슴 아프지만, 견딜 수 있소.\"\n\n\"미코버!\" 미코버 부인이 눈물을 글썽이며 외쳤다. \"내가 이런 소릴 들어야 마땅한가요! 단 한 번도 당신을 저버린 적 없고, 앞으로도 결코 당신을 저버리지 않을 이 내가요, 미코버!\" \"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 몹시 감동하여 말했다. \"당신도 용서해 주고, 우리의 오래되고 믿음직한 벗 코퍼필드도 분명 용서해 줄 거요. 권력의 앞잡이와 — 다시 말해, 수도국에 딸린 상스러운 수도꼭지 담당자와 — 방금 부딪히는 바람에 예민해진, 상처 입은 영혼이 한순간 찢어져 터진 것을 말이오. 그리고 그 지나침을 나무라지 않고 가엾이 여겨 줄 거요.\"\n\n그러더니 미코버 씨는 미코버 부인을 끌어안고 내 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 나는 이 두서없는 암시로 미루어, 그날 오후 그 집의 수돗물 공급이 끊겼다는 것을, 그것도 수도 회사 요금을 내지 않은 탓에 그리되었다는 것을 짐작할 수 있었다.\n\n이 우울한 화제에서 그의 생각을 돌리려고, 나는 미코버 씨에게 펀치 한 사발은 당신만 믿겠노라고 일러 주고는 그를 레몬이 있는 데로 데려갔다. 방금까지의 의기소침함은, 절망이라 해도 좋을 그 기색은, 순식간에 사라졌다. 나는 그날 오후의 미코버 씨만큼, 레몬 껍질과 설탕의 향기, 럼주가 끓는 냄새, 펄펄 끓는 물의 김 한가운데서 그토록 흠뻑 즐기는 사람을 본 적이 없다. 그가 휘젓고, 섞고, 맛보면서, 이 섬세한 김의 엷은 구름 사이로 우리를 향해 얼굴을 환히 빛내는 모습은 참으로 볼 만했다. 마치 펀치가 아니라, 먼 후손에 이르기까지 제 식구가 누릴 한밑천을 장만하고 있는 사람 같았다. 미코버 부인으로 말하자면, 모자 덕분인지, 라벤더 화장수 덕분인지, 핀 덕분인지, 난로 덕분인지, 밀랍 양초 덕분인지는 모르겠으나, 비교적 따져 말하건대 사랑스러운 모습으로 내 방에서 나왔다. 그리고 그 훌륭한 부인은 종달새 못지않게 명랑했다.\n\n짐작건대 — 감히 물어본 적은 없으나, 짐작건대 — 크루프 부인은 가자미를 튀긴 뒤에 몸져누운 모양이었다. 거기서부터 일이 어그러져 버렸으니까. 양고기 다리 살은 속은 시뻘겋고 겉은 새하얀 채로 올라왔다. 게다가 자글자글한 이물질이 그 위에 뿌려져 있었는데, 마치 그 별난 부엌 난로의 잿더미 속에 한번 굴러떨어지기라도 한 듯했다. 하지만 우리는 그 사실을 고기 국물의 모양새로 판단할 처지가 못 되었다. '젊은 계집애'가 국물을 죄다 계단에 쏟아 버린 탓이었는데 — 그건 그렇고, 그 국물은 그 뒤로도 발에 다 닳아 없어질 때까지 길게 줄을 이루며 거기 그대로 남아 있었다. 비둘기 파이는 나쁘지 않았지만, 사람을 속이는 파이였다. 파이 껍질이 골상학으로 따지자면 실망스러운 머리통 같았으니, 울퉁불퉁 혹투성이인데 그 아래에는 이렇다 할 게 아무것도 없었다. 한마디로 이 잔치는 어찌나 실패작이었던지, 손님들의 더없이 좋은 흥과 미코버 씨의 기발한 제안으로 마음을 놓지 못했더라면 나는 무척 불행했을 것이다 — 잔치가 실패한 것 때문에 말이다. 도라 때문에야 나는 늘 불행했으니까.\n\n\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"사고란 아무리 잘 다스려지는 집안에서도 일어나는 법이지. 그리고 그 두루 미치는 영향력으로 다스려지지 않는 집안에서는 — 그러니까 격조 높은 아내라는 자리에 선 여성의 영향력으로 다스려지지 않는, 그 영향력은 격을 높이는 동시에 신성하게도 하는 것인데 — 그런 집안에서는 사고가 일어나리라고 자신 있게 내다보아야 하고, 또 철학으로써 견뎌야 하는 법이라네. 외람되이 한마디 하자면, 데블 구이만큼 그 나름으로 훌륭한 음식도 드물다네. 그리고 일을 조금씩 나눠 맡으면, 시중드는 저 젊은 사람이 석쇠 하나만 가져와 준다면 우리가 썩 괜찮은 걸 하나 만들어 낼 수 있으리라 믿네. 내 생각엔, 이 작은 불운쯤은 쉽게 만회할 수 있을 듯하이.\"\n\n식료품 저장실에 석쇠가 하나 있었는데, 내 아침 베이컨 한 조각을 굽던 것이었다. 우리는 눈 깜짝할 새에 그걸 들여와, 곧장 미코버 씨의 착상을 실행에 옮기는 데 매달렸다. 그가 말한 분업이란 이러했다. 트래들스가 양고기를 저며 썰면, 미코버 씨가 (이런 종류의 일이라면 무엇이든 완벽하게 해내는 사람이었다) 거기에 후추와 겨자와 소금과 카옌 고춧가루를 뿌렸다. 나는 그것을 석쇠에 올리고, 미코버 씨의 지휘에 따라 포크로 뒤집었다가 다시 내렸다. 그리고 미코버 부인은 작은 냄비에다 버섯 케첩을 데우며 쉴 새 없이 휘저었다. 우선 먹기 시작할 만큼 고기가 충분히 구워지자, 우리는 손목까지 소매를 걷어붙인 채로 달려들었는데, 그러는 동안에도 또 다른 고기 조각들이 불 위에서 지글지글 타오르고 있었고, 우리의 관심은 접시 위의 양고기와 그때 구워지고 있는 양고기 사이로 갈렸다.\n\n이 요리의 신기함, 그 맛깔스러움, 그 부산함, 자꾸만 벌떡 일어나 고기를 살피던 일, 바삭한 고기 조각이 석쇠에서 뜨끈뜨끈 막 구워져 나올 때마다 자꾸만 도로 앉아 그걸 해치우던 일, 그렇게 바쁘고, 불기운에 그렇게 얼굴이 달아오르고, 그렇게 흥겹고, 그토록 군침 도는 소리와 냄새 한가운데 있던 덕분에, 우리는 양고기 다리 살을 뼈만 남기고 다 먹어 치웠다. 내 입맛도 기적처럼 되돌아왔다. 이런 걸 적어 두려니 부끄럽지만, 사실 나는 잠시나마 도라를 잊었던 것 같다. 미코버 부부가 이 잔치를 차리려고 침대 하나를 팔았다 한들, 그보다 더 즐기지는 못했으리라 나는 확신한다. 트래들스는 먹고 일하는 거의 내내 진심으로 호탕하게 웃어 댔다. 사실 우리 모두가, 한꺼번에 그랬다. 그러니 이보다 더 큰 성공은 다시없었으리라 감히 말하겠다.\n\n우리가 즐거움의 절정에 올라, 저마다 맡은 부서에서 잔치를 멋지게 마무리할 마지막 고기 한 무더기를 완벽한 상태로 구워 내려고 바쁘게 매달려 있을 때, 나는 방 안에 낯선 기척이 있음을 깨달았다. 그리고 내 눈은 점잖은 리티머의 눈과 마주쳤는데, 그는 모자를 손에 들고 내 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 나도 모르게 물었다.\n\n\"죄송합니다, 나리. 들어오라는 분부를 받았습니다. 제 주인님이 여기 안 계신지요, 나리?\"\n\n\"안 계시네.\"\n\"못 보셨습니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"못 봤네. 주인이 보내서 온 게 아닌가?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지는 않습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"주인이 자네더러 여기서 자기를 찾을 거라고 하던가?\"\n\n\"꼭 그렇지는 않습니다, 나리. 다만 오늘은 여기 안 오셨으니, 내일쯤 여기 오시지 않을까 합니다.\"\n\n\"옥스퍼드에서 올라오는 길인가?\"\n\n\"나리,\" 그가 공손히 대답했다. \"부디 앉으시고, 이건 제가 하도록 해 주십시오.\" 그러면서 그는 내가 순순히 내미는 포크를 손에서 받아 들더니, 마치 온 정신을 거기에 쏟아붓는 사람처럼 석쇠 위로 몸을 숙였다.\n\n감히 말하건대, 스티어포스 본인이 나타났다 한들 우리는 그리 크게 흐트러지지 않았을 것이다. 그런데 우리는 그의 점잖은 하인 앞에서 순식간에 더없이 온순한 양 떼가 되고 말았다. 미코버 씨는 자기가 아주 편안하다는 걸 보이려고 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 의자에 도로 주저앉았는데, 황급히 감춘 포크 자루가 외투 가슴팍에서 삐죽 튀어나와, 마치 제 몸을 찌르기라도 한 것 같았다. 미코버 부인은 갈색 장갑을 끼더니 우아하게 나른한 자태를 지어 보였다. 트래들스는 기름 묻은 손으로 머리카락을 쓸어 올려 빳빳이 곤두세우고는, 어쩔 줄 몰라 식탁보만 멍하니 바라보았다. 나로 말하자면, 내 식탁의 윗자리에 앉은 한낱 갓난애나 다름없었다. 그래서 어디서 왔는지 하느님이나 아실, 내 살림을 바로잡으러 온 그 점잖은 진풍경을 감히 흘끗 쳐다보지도 못했다.\n\n그러는 동안 그는 석쇠에서 고기를 내려 점잖게 돌렸다. 우리는 다들 조금씩 받았지만, 고기를 음미하는 마음은 이미 사라져 버려서, 그저 먹는 시늉만 했다. 우리가 저마다 접시를 밀어내자, 그는 소리 없이 접시를 치우고 치즈를 내놓았다. 치즈도 다 먹고 나자 그것 역시 치웠다. 식탁을 정리하고, 모든 걸 식기 운반대에 차곡차곡 쌓고, 우리에게 포도주 잔을 건네고, 그러고는 제 스스로 식기 운반대를 식료품 저장실로 밀어 넣었다. 이 모든 일이 완벽하게 이루어졌고, 그는 자기가 하는 일에서 단 한 번도 눈을 들지 않았다. 그런데도 그가 내게 등을 돌리고 있을 때면, 그 팔꿈치마저도 내가 지독히도 어리다는 자신의 확고한 견해를 잔뜩 내뿜는 것만 같았다.\n\n\"더 해 드릴 일이 있는지요, 나리?\"\n\n나는 고맙다며 없다고 했다. 그런데 자네는 저녁을 좀 들지 않겠나?\n\n\"안 들겠습니다. 감사합니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"스티어포스 씨가 옥스퍼드에서 오는 길인가?\"\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨는지요, 나리?\"\n\n\"스티어포스 씨가 옥스퍼드에서 오는 길이냐고.\"\n\n\"내일쯤 여기 오시지 않을까 짐작합니다, 나리. 오늘 오시려나 했는데요, 나리. 제가 잘못 안 게 틀림없습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"혹시 자네가 먼저 그분을 보거든—\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"실례지만, 나리, 제가 그분을 먼저 뵐 것 같지는 않습니다.\"\n\n\"혹여 보거든,\" 내가 말했다. \"그분의 옛 학교 친구가 여기 왔었는데, 오늘 그분이 안 계셔서 섭섭했다고 좀 전해 주게.\"\n\n\"그러십니까, 나리!\" 그러더니 그는 나와 트래들스에게 절을 반씩 나누어 하면서, 트래들스 쪽을 한 번 흘끗 보았다.\n\n그가 사뿐히 문 쪽으로 걸어가는데, 나는 이 사내에게는 도무지 그래 본 적이 없는 일이지만, 무어라도 자연스럽게 한마디 해 보겠다는 가망 없는 바람으로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"아 참! 리티머!\"\n\n\"네, 나리!\"\n\"그때, 야머스에 오래 머물렀나?\"\n\n\"그리 오래는 아니었습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"배가 완성된 걸 봤겠지?\"\n\n\"네, 나리. 배가 완성되는 걸 보려고 일부러 남아 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"알지!\" 그가 공손히 내 눈으로 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"스티어포스 씨는 아직 그 배를 못 봤겠지?\"\n\n\"정말 모르겠습니다, 나리. 제 생각엔 — 하지만 정말 모르겠습니다, 나리. 안녕히 주무십시오, 나리.\"\n\n그는 이 말끝에 이어진 공손한 절 속에 그 자리의 모든 사람을 다 아우르고는, 사라졌다. 그가 떠나자 손님들은 숨통이 좀 트인 듯했다. 하지만 내가 느낀 안도감은 무척 컸다. 이 사내 앞에만 서면 늘 따라붙던, 내가 어쩐지 불리한 처지에 놓여 있다는 그 묘한 느낌에서 비롯된 거북함도 거북함이려니와, 내가 그의 주인을 의심했다는 사실을 두고 양심이 속닥속닥 나를 곤혹스럽게 했고, 그가 그걸 알아챌지도 모른다는 막연하고 께름칙한 두려움을 도무지 억누를 수가 없었기 때문이다. 실은 숨길 것이라곤 거의 없는데도, 어째서 나는 늘 이 사내가 내 속을 꿰뚫어 보는 것만 같았을까?\n\n미코버 씨가 이 상념에서 나를 일깨워 주었는데, 그 상념에는 스티어포스 본인을 만나게 되리라는, 어딘가 뉘우치는 듯한 불안이 뒤섞여 있던 터였다. 그는 자리에 없는 리티머를 두고 더없이 점잖은 친구이자 진정 감탄스러운 하인이라며 칭찬을 잔뜩 늘어놓았다. 한마디 덧붙이자면, 미코버 씨도 그 두루 나눈 절을 제 몫만큼 충분히 받았고, 그것도 한없이 너그러운 태도로 받았다.\n\n\"하지만 펀치 말일세, 이보게 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 펀치를 맛보며 말했다. \"이건 세월이며 조수와 같아서, 사람을 기다려 주지 않지. 아! 지금 이 순간이 한창 맛이 무르익었네. 여보, 당신 의견을 좀 말해 주겠소?\"\n\n미코버 부인은 일품이라고 단언했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 한잔 마셔야겠네.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 벗 코퍼필드가 이런 격의 없는 호의를 허락해 준다면, 내 벗 코퍼필드와 내가 더 젊었던 시절, 어깨를 나란히 하고 세상과 싸워 헤쳐 나가던 그 나날을 위해 마시겠네. 나와 코퍼필드를 두고, 우리가 전에 함께 부르던 노랫말로 말하자면 이렇다네.\n\n우리 둘이 언덕을 누비며 뛰놀았네 그리고 고운 데이지꽃을 꺾었네\n\n— 비유적인 관점에서 — 여러 번 그랬지. 데이지꽃이라는 게 정확히 무엇인지,\" 미코버 씨가 예의 그 굴리는 목소리로, 무언가 격조 있는 말을 한다는 예의 그 형언할 수 없는 분위기로 말했다. \"잘은 모르겠네만, 그럴 형편만 됐다면 코퍼필드와 내가 그 꽃을 곧잘 꺾어 당겼으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없네그려.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 마침 그 순간에 펀치를 한 모금 쭉 들이켰다. 그래서 우리 모두 그렇게 했다. 트래들스는 도대체 얼마나 먼 옛날에 미코버 씨와 내가 세상이라는 싸움터에서 전우가 될 수 있었던 것인지 궁금해하느라 넋이 나간 게 분명했다.\n\n\"에헴!\" 미코버 씨가 목청을 가다듬으며 말했는데, 펀치와 난롯불로 몸이 후끈 달아오른 참이었다. \"여보, 한 잔 더 하겠소?\"\n\n미코버 부인은 아주 조금만이라고 했지만, 우리는 그걸 허락할 수 없었으니, 결국 한 잔 가득이 되었다.\n\n\"우리끼리 아주 허물없는 자리니까요, 코퍼필드 씨,\" 미코버 부인이 펀치를 홀짝이며 말했다. \"트래들스 씨도 우리 식구나 다름없으니, 미코버 씨의 앞날에 대해 당신 의견을 꼭 좀 듣고 싶어요. 곡물 장사로 말하자면,\" 미코버 부인이 따지듯이 말했다. \"제가 미코버 씨한테 거듭 말씀드렸다시피, 신사답기는 할지언정 벌이가 되질 않아요. 두 주일에 고작 이 실링 구 펜스짜리 수수료라니, 우리 살림 규모가 아무리 소박하다 해도, 그걸 벌이라고 할 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\"\n\n우리는 모두 그 말에 동의했다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 미코버 부인이 말을 이었는데, 그녀는 사물을 명료하게 보는 것을, 그리고 미코버 씨가 자칫 좀 비뚤어질 만하면 여자다운 지혜로 바로잡아 주는 것을 자랑으로 여기는 사람이었다. \"그래서 저는 제 자신에게 이렇게 물어봐요. 곡물을 믿을 수 없다면, 무얼 믿을 수 있나? 석탄은 믿을 만한가? 천만에요. 우리 친정 식구들 권유로 그 시도에 관심을 돌려 봤지만, 헛것임을 알았답니다.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 의자에 등을 기대고 앉아, 우리를 곁눈질하며 고개를 끄덕였는데, 마치 사정이 아주 명료하게 정리되었다는 듯한 시늉이었다.\n\n\"곡물과 석탄이라는 품목이,\" 미코버 부인이 한층 더 따지듯이 말했다. \"똑같이 논외라고 한다면요, 코퍼필드 씨, 저는 자연스레 온 세상을 둘러보며 이렇게 말하지요. '미코버 씨 같은 재능을 지닌 사람이 성공할 만한 일이 대체 무엇일까?' 그리고 저는 수수료를 받고 하는 일은 다 제외해요. 수수료라는 건 확실한 게 아니니까요. 미코버 씨의 그 독특한 기질을 지닌 사람에게 가장 잘 맞는 것은, 저는 확신하건대, 확실한 일이에요.\"\n\n트래들스와 나는 둘 다 절절한 웅얼거림으로, 이 위대한 발견이 미코버 씨에게는 의심할 바 없이 들어맞으며 그것이 그분의 자랑거리라는 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"숨기지 않고 말씀드리는데요, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"저는 양조업이야말로 미코버 씨에게 특히 잘 맞는다고 오래전부터 느껴 왔답니다. 바클레이 앤드 퍼킨스를 보세요! 트루먼, 핸버리, 앤드 버크스턴을 보세요! 미코버 씨가 빛을 발하도록 점지된 곳은, 제가 그이를 잘 아는 바로 미루어, 바로 그렇게 큰 규모랍니다. 그리고 듣자 하니 그 수익이 어—마—어마하다더군요! 하지만 미코버 씨가 그런 회사에 들어가지 못한다면 — 그이가 한참 낮은 직책으로라도 일하겠다고 제안해도 회사들은 편지에 답조차 안 해요 — 그런 생각에 매달려 봐야 무슨 소용이겠어요? 아무 소용 없지요. 저는 미코버 씨의 몸가짐이—\"\n\n\"흠! 정말이지, 여보,\" 미코버 씨가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"여보, 가만히 좀 계세요.\" 미코버 부인이 갈색 장갑을 낀 손을 그의 손 위에 얹으며 말했다. \"코퍼필드 씨, 저는 미코버 씨의 몸가짐이 은행업에 유달리 잘 맞는다고 확신할 수 있어요. 저는 속으로 이렇게 따져 봐요. 만약 내가 어느 은행에 예금을 맡겨 두었다면, 그 은행을 대표하는 미코버 씨의 몸가짐이 신뢰를 자아낼 테고, 틀림없이 거래를 넓혀 줄 거라고요. 하지만 여러 은행이 미코버 씨의 능력을 쓰려 하지 않거나, 그 제안을 모욕으로 받아넘긴다면, 그런 생각에 매달려 봐야 무슨 소용이겠어요? 아무 소용 없지요. 은행업을 새로 차리는 일로 말하자면, 우리 친정 식구들 중에는 마음만 먹으면 자기 돈을 미코버 씨 손에 맡겨서 그런 사업체를 세워 줄 만한 사람들이 있다는 것도 저는 알아요. 하지만 그들이 자기 돈을 미코버 씨 손에 맡길 마음이 없다면 — 실제로 그들은 그럴 마음이 없는데요 — 그게 다 무슨 소용이겠어요? 저는 다시 한번 주장하건대, 우리는 전혀 한 발짝도 나아가지 못한 거예요.\"\n\n나는 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"조금도요.\" 트래들스도 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"조금도요.\"\n\n\"제가 이로부터 무얼 추론하느냐고요?\" 미코버 부인이, 여전히 사정을 명료하게 펼쳐 보이는 그 태도로 말을 이었다. \"이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 제가 거역할 수 없이 다다르는 결론이 무엇이겠어요? 우리가 살아가야 한다는 것이 명백하다고 말한다면, 제가 틀린 건가요?\"\n\n나는 \"전혀요!\" 하고 대답했고, 트래들스도 \"전혀요!\" 하고 대답했다. 그러고 나서 나는 혼자, 사람이란 살든지 죽든지 둘 중 하나일 수밖에 없노라고 제법 지혜로운 척 덧붙이고 있었다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요.\" 미코버 부인이 받았다. \"정확히 그거랍니다. 그런데 사실인즉슨, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 지금 형편과는 크게 다른 무언가가 머지않아 생겨나지 않고서는 우리가 살아갈 수가 없어요. 그런데 저는요, 저 자신은 확신하는데, 그리고 이건 제가 요즘 미코버 씨한테 몇 번이나 짚어 드린 건데요, 일이란 게 저절로 생겨나기를 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이에요. 우리가 어느 정도는 그 일이 생겨나도록 거들어야 해요. 제가 틀렸을지도 모르지만, 저는 그런 의견을 갖게 되었답니다.\"\n\n트래들스도 나도 그 말에 열렬히 박수를 보냈다.\n\n\"좋아요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그럼 제가 무얼 권하느냐고요? 여기 미코버 씨가 있어요. 갖가지 자질을 갖추고 — 대단한 재능을 지니고—\"\n\n\"정말이지, 여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여보, 제발, 끝까지 말하게 해 주세요. 여기 미코버 씨가 있어요. 갖가지 자질을 갖추고, 대단한 재능을 지니고 — 천재라고 말하고 싶지만, 그건 아내의 편애일지도 모르니—\"\n\n트래들스도 나도 둘 다 \"아니요.\" 하고 웅얼거렸다.\n\n\"그런데 여기 미코버 씨가 마땅한 자리도, 일자리도 없이 있어요. 그 책임이 어디에 있나요? 명백히 사회에 있지요. 그러니 저라면 그렇게 수치스러운 사실을 널리 알리고, 사회를 향해 그걸 바로잡아 보라고 당당히 도전장을 내밀겠어요. 제가 보기엔요, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨,\" 미코버 부인이 힘주어 말했다. \"미코버 씨가 해야 할 일은, 사회를 향해 결투의 장갑을 내던지고는 사실상 이렇게 말하는 거예요. '누가 이걸 집어 드는지 어디 보자. 그럴 사람은 당장 앞으로 나서라.'\"\n\n나는 미코버 부인에게 그걸 어떻게 하면 되느냐고 조심스레 물어보았다.\n\n\"광고를 내는 거지요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"모든 신문에다요. 제가 보기엔요, 미코버 씨가 자기 자신에 대한 도리로, 또 자기 식구에 대한 도리로, 심지어 지금껏 그이를 외면해 온 사회에 대한 도리로 말하더라도, 마땅히 해야 할 일은, 모든 신문에 광고를 내는 거예요. 자기가 어떤 사람이고 이러이러한 자질을 갖췄다고 분명히 밝히고는, 이렇게 적는 거지요. '지금 당장 저를 합당한 보수로 고용하시고, 우편 요금 선납으로 캠던 타운 우체국 더블유 엠 앞으로 연락 주시오.'\"\n\n\"미코버 부인의 이 착상은 말일세, 이보게 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 셔츠 깃을 턱 밑에서 맞물리게 세우고 나를 곁눈으로 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"실은, 내가 지난번에 자네를 만나는 기쁨을 누렸을 때 넌지시 말한 그 도약이라네.\"\n\n\"광고는 비용이 꽤 들 텐데요.\" 나는 미심쩍어하며 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요!\" 미코버 부인이 여전히 그 논리정연한 태도를 지키며 말했다. \"지당하신 말씀이에요, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨! 제가 미코버 씨한테 똑같은 지적을 했답니다. 특히 그 때문에라도, 저는 미코버 씨가 마땅히 (이미 말씀드렸듯이, 자기 자신에 대한 도리로, 자기 식구에 대한 도리로, 또 사회에 대한 도리로) 어음을 잡혀 일정한 돈을 마련해야 한다고 생각해요.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 의자에 등을 기댄 채 외알 안경을 만지작거리며 천장으로 눈길을 올려보냈다. 하지만 내 보기에 그는 난롯불을 바라보고 있던 트래들스도 살피고 있는 것 같았다.\n\n\"우리 친정 식구들 가운데,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그 어음을 처리해 줄 만한 인지상정을 지닌 사람이 하나도 없다면 — 제가 말하려는 걸 표현하는 더 나은 사업 용어가 있을 텐데요—\"\n\n미코버 씨가 여전히 천장으로 눈길을 올려둔 채로 \"할인\"이라고 거들었다.\n\n\"그 어음을 할인해 줄 사람이 하나도 없다면요,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 제 의견은 이래요. 미코버 씨가 시티로 가서, 그 어음을 들고 금융 시장에 나가, 받을 수 있는 만큼 받고 처분해야 한다는 거예요. 금융 시장의 사람들이 미코버 씨로 하여금 큰 희생을 치르게 한다면, 그건 그들과 그들 양심 사이의 문제지요. 저는 그걸 한결같이 투자로 봐요. 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 저는 미코버 씨에게도 똑같이 하라고 권하고 있어요. 그걸 틀림없이 돌아올 투자로 여기고, 어떤 희생이든 감수할 각오를 하라고요.\"\n\n나는, 왜 그런지는 정말 모르겠으나, 이것이 미코버 부인다운 헌신이자 희생이라고 느꼈고, 그런 뜻으로 한마디 웅얼거렸다. 나를 따라 장단을 맞추던 트래들스도 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며 똑같이 했다.\n\n\"저는 더 이상,\" 미코버 부인이 펀치를 다 비우고는, 내 침실로 물러갈 채비로 어깨에 두른 숄을 여미며 말했다. \"미코버 씨의 금전 문제에 관한 이 이야기를 길게 늘이지 않겠어요. 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 당신의 난롯가에서, 그리고 그리 오랜 벗은 아니어도 우리 식구나 다름없는 트래들스 씨가 계신 자리에서, 저는 미코버 씨에게 권하는 방도를 당신께 알려 드리지 않을 수가 없었어요. 이제 미코버 씨가 분발하고 — 한마디 덧붙이자면 — 자기 자신을 내세워야 할 때가 왔다고 저는 느끼고, 또 이것이 그 방도라고 봐요. 제가 한낱 아녀자에 지나지 않는다는 것도, 또 이런 문제를 따지는 데에는 으레 남자의 판단이 더 미덥다고 여겨진다는 것도 잘 알아요. 그래도 저는 잊지 말아야겠지요. 제가 친정 아빠 엄마와 함께 살던 시절, 우리 아빠는 곧잘 이렇게 말씀하셨어요. '에마는 몸은 약하지만, 사안을 파악하는 능력만큼은 누구한테도 뒤지지 않는다.' 우리 아빠가 너무 편애하셨다는 건 저도 잘 알아요. 하지만 아빠가 사람 됨됨이를 어느 정도는 볼 줄 아는 분이셨다는 것은, 제 도리로 보나 제 이성으로 보나 똑같이 의심할 수 없는 일이랍니다.\"\n\n이런 말을 남기고는, 펀치가 남아 도는 동안만이라도 자리를 빛내 달라는 우리의 간청을 뿌리치며, 미코버 부인은 내 침실로 물러갔다. 그리고 나는 정말로 그녀가 고결한 여성이라는 — 나라가 환난을 겪는 때라면 로마의 귀부인이라도 되어 온갖 영웅적인 일을 해냈을 법한 그런 여성이라는 — 느낌이 들었다.\n\n이 감회에 사무친 나머지, 나는 미코버 씨에게 그가 지닌 보배를 두고 축하의 말을 건넸다. 트래들스도 그랬다. 미코버 씨는 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 차례로 손을 내밀더니, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸는데, 내 생각에 그 손수건에는 그가 미처 모르는 코담배 가루가 잔뜩 묻어 있었다. 그러고는 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 다시 펀치 쪽으로 돌아왔다.\n\n그는 청산유수였다. 그는 우리에게, 우리는 우리 자식들 속에서 다시 살아가는 것이며, 금전 곤란의 압박을 받는 처지라 해도 식구 수가 하나 느는 것은 갑절로 반가운 일이라는 뜻을 일러 주었다. 그는 미코버 부인이 근래에 이 점을 두고 의구심을 품었으나, 자기가 그것을 떨쳐 주고 다시 안심시켰노라고 했다. 처가 식구들로 말하자면, 그들은 부인에게는 도무지 어울리지 않는 사람들이고, 그들의 심정 따위는 자기에게 조금도 대수롭지 않으니, 그들은 — 그의 표현을 그대로 옮기자면 — 악마한테나 가 버려도 좋다고 했다.\n\n이어서 미코버 씨는 트래들스에 대한 따뜻한 찬사를 늘어놓았다. 그는 트래들스의 인품으로 말하자면, 그 한결같은 미덕은 자기(미코버 씨)로서는 도저히 가졌다고 내세울 수 없는 것이지만, 하늘에 감사하게도 자기가 우러러볼 줄은 안다고 했다. 그는 트래들스가 애정을 바치는 영광을 안긴, 그리고 트래들스에게 애정을 돌려주어 그를 빛내고 축복해 준, 그 알지 못하는 젊은 아가씨를 정겹게 입에 올렸다. 미코버 씨가 그녀를 위해 잔을 들었다. 나도 그랬다. 트래들스는, 내가 듣고 흠뻑 반할 만큼 소박하고 진솔하게, 이렇게 말하며 우리 둘에게 고마워했다. \"정말이지 두 분께 무척 감사합니다. 그리고 장담하건대, 그녀는 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨예요!—\"\n\n그 뒤에 미코버 씨는 일찌감치 기회를 잡아, 더없이 섬세하고 격식 차린 태도로 내 연정의 상태를 넌지시 건드렸다. 그는, 자기 벗 코퍼필드가 사랑을 하고 또 사랑을 받고 있다는 인상을, 정작 벗 코퍼필드 본인이 그렇지 않다고 진지하게 단언하지 않고서는 결코 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없다고 말했다. 한동안 몸이 무척 후끈거리고 거북한 데다, 한참을 얼굴 붉히고 더듬거리며 잡아떼고 난 끝에, 나는 잔을 손에 든 채 말했다. \"좋습니다! 그녀의 머리글자 디를 위해 마시겠어요!\" 이 말에 미코버 씨가 어찌나 흥이 나고 흐뭇해하던지, 미코버 부인도 디를 위해 마실 수 있도록 펀치 한 잔을 들고 내 침실로 달려갔다. 부인은 그것을 열렬히 들이켜고는, 안에서 새된 목소리로 외쳤다. \"옳소, 옳소! 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 정말 기뻐요. 옳소!\" 그러면서 박수 대신 벽을 톡톡 두드렸다.\n\n그 뒤로 우리의 대화는 한결 세속적인 쪽으로 흘렀다. 미코버 씨는 우리에게, 캠던 타운이 불편하더라며, 광고 덕분에 흡족한 무언가가 생겨나거든 가장 먼저 할 작정인 일은 이사라고 했다. 그는 옥스퍼드 거리 서쪽 끝, 하이드 파크를 마주 본 어느 연립주택을 입에 올렸는데, 늘 거기에 눈독을 들이고 있었으나 당장 손에 넣기를 바라지는 않는다고 했다. 큰 살림이 필요할 테니 말이다. 그는 설명하기를, 아마 그 중간에 한동안은 어느 번듯한 상점 위층 — 이를테면 피커딜리쯤의 — 윗부분으로 만족하며 지낼 텐데, 거기라면 미코버 부인에게 쾌적한 자리일 테고, 내닫이창을 하나 내거나, 지붕을 한 층 더 올리거나, 그런 식으로 자그마한 손질을 좀 하면, 몇 해쯤은 안락하고 떳떳하게 살 수 있으리라고 했다. 자기에게 무엇이 점지되어 있든, 또 자기 거처가 어디가 되든, 이것만은 우리가 믿어도 좋다고 그는 분명히 못 박았다 — 거기에는 언제나 트래들스를 위한 방 하나와 나를 위한 나이프와 포크 한 벌이 마련되어 있으리라고. 우리는 그의 친절에 고마움을 표했다. 그러자 그는, 이렇게 실속 있고 사무적인 세부 사항을 늘어놓은 것을 용서해 달라며, 인생의 전혀 새로운 채비를 꾸리고 있는 사람으로서는 자연스러운 일이려니 너그러이 봐 달라고 청했다.\n\n미코버 부인이 차가 준비됐는지 알아보려고 다시 벽을 톡톡 두드리는 바람에, 우리의 정겨운 대화는 이 대목에서 끊겼다. 부인은 더없이 상냥한 솜씨로 우리에게 차를 끓여 주었다. 그리고 내가 찻잔과 버터 바른 빵을 돌리느라 그녀 곁에 갈 때마다, 그녀는 속삭임으로 내게 묻기를, 디는 살결이 흰지 검은지, 키가 작은지 큰지, 뭐 그런 것들을 물었는데, 내 생각에 나는 그게 싫지 않았다. 차를 마신 뒤, 우리는 난롯가에서 갖가지 화제를 놓고 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 미코버 부인은 고맙게도 우리에게 '늠름한 백의의 하사관'과 '꼬마 태플린'이라는 즐겨 부르는 발라드 두 곡을 (가늘고 얇고 밋밋한 목소리로 불러 주었는데, 나는 그녀를 처음 알았을 적에 그 목소리를 음향계의 맹물 맥주쯤으로 여겼던 기억이 난다) 불러 주었다. 이 두 노래로 미코버 부인은 친정 아빠 엄마와 함께 살던 시절에 이름을 날린 바 있었다. 미코버 씨가 우리에게 들려주기를, 친정집 지붕 아래서 그녀를 처음 보았을 때 첫 곡을 부르는 걸 들었는데, 그녀가 자기 마음을 어찌나 유난히도 사로잡던지, 그러다 '꼬마 태플린'에 이르렀을 때는, 저 여인을 손에 넣지 못하면 그 시도 끝에 죽어 버리리라 결심했다고 했다.\n\n미코버 부인이 일어나 모자를 다시 누르스름한 갈색 종이 꾸러미에 넣고 보닛을 쓴 것은 열 시에서 열한 시 사이였다. 미코버 씨는 트래들스가 외투를 걸치는 틈을 타, 내 손에 편지 한 통을 슬쩍 쥐여 주며, 한가할 때 읽어 달라고 속삭여 부탁했다. 나도 기회를 잡았는데, 미코버 씨가 먼저 미코버 부인을 이끌고 앞장서고 트래들스가 모자를 들고 뒤따르며 내려갈 때, 내가 난간 너머로 촛불을 비춰 길을 밝혀 주면서, 잠시 트래들스를 계단 꼭대기에 붙들어 둔 것이다.\n\n\"트래들스,\" 내가 말했다. \"미코버 씨는 나쁜 뜻이야 없는 분이지, 가엾은 양반. 하지만 내가 자네라면, 그분한테 아무것도 빌려주지 않겠네.\"\n\n\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 웃으며 받았다. \"빌려줄 게 있어야 말이지.\"\n\n\"이름은 있잖나.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 그걸 빌려줄 것이라고 하는 건가?\" 트래들스가 골똘한 낯빛으로 받았다.\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"오!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그래, 그렇고말고! 정말 고맙네, 코퍼필드. 하지만 — 그건 이미 빌려준 것 같아서 걱정일세.\"\n\n\"틀림없는 투자가 된다는 그 어음 때문에?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아니,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그 어음 때문은 아니야. 그 어음 얘기는 나도 지금 처음 듣는걸. 아마 집에 가는 길에 그 어음 얘기를 꺼내실 것 같다는 생각은 들지만. 내 건 다른 거야.\"\n\n\"아무 탈 없으면 좋으련만.\" 내가 말했다. \"탈이 없어야 할 텐데.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그래도 탈은 없을 것 같아. 바로 며칠 전에 그분이 나한테 그건 대비가 다 돼 있다고 했거든. 그게 미코버 씨의 표현이었지. '대비가 돼 있다.'\"\n\n마침 그때 미코버 씨가 우리가 서 있는 쪽으로 고개를 들어 올려다보는 바람에, 나는 내 당부를 한 번 더 되뇔 겨를밖에 없었다. 트래들스는 내게 고마워하고는 내려갔다. 하지만 그가 손에 모자를 든 채 그 사람 좋은 태도로 내려가, 미코버 부인에게 팔을 내미는 모습을 지켜보고 있자니, 나는 그가 멱살을 잡힌 채 금융 시장으로 통째로 끌려가는 게 아닐까 무척 두려웠다.\n\n나는 다시 난롯가로 돌아와, 미코버 씨의 사람됨과 우리 사이의 오랜 인연을 두고 반은 진지하게 반은 우습게 곰곰 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그러다 계단을 빠르게 올라오는 발소리를 들었다. 처음에는 미코버 부인이 두고 간 무언가를 가지러 트래들스가 되돌아오는 줄 알았다. 하지만 그 발소리가 가까워지자, 나는 그것을 알아채고는 가슴이 쿵쾅쿵쾅 뛰고 얼굴로 피가 확 쏠리는 것을 느꼈으니, 그건 스티어포스의 발소리였던 것이다.\n\n나는 한시도 애그니스를 마음에서 놓은 적이 없었고, 그녀는 내가 처음부터 그녀를 모셔 둔, 내 마음속 그 성소라고 불러도 좋을 자리를 결코 떠난 적이 없었다. 그러나 그가 들어와 손을 내밀며 내 앞에 서자, 그를 덮고 있던 어둠은 빛으로 바뀌었고, 나는 그토록 진심으로 아끼는 사람을 의심했던 것이 부끄럽고 면구스러웠다. 그렇다고 그녀를 덜 사랑하게 된 것은 아니었다. 나는 여전히 그녀를 내 인생의 한결같이 자애롭고 다정한 천사로 여겼다. 나는 그녀가 아니라 나 자신을, 그에게 못할 짓을 했다고 나무랐다. 그리고 어떻게 보상해야 할지, 또 어떻게 해야 할지를 알았더라면 어떤 보상이든 그에게 해 주었을 것이다.\n\n\"이런, 데이지, 이 친구야, 넋이 나갔구먼!\" 스티어포스가 내 손을 진심으로 꼭 쥐었다가 흥겹게 휙 놓으며 웃었다. \"또 잔치를 벌이는 현장을 내가 딱 붙잡았군그래, 이 사치꾼아! 이 민법박사회관 친구들이 런던에서 제일가는 한량들이라더니, 우리 술도 안 마시는 옥스퍼드 사람들쯤은 완전히 코를 납작하게 만들어 버리는걸!\" 미코버 부인이 방금 비우고 간, 나와 마주 보는 소파 자리에 앉으며, 그의 환한 눈길이 즐겁게 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌더니, 난롯불을 들쑤셔 활활 타오르게 했다.\n\n\"처음엔 어찌나 놀랐던지,\" 나는 진심에서 우러난 온 정성으로 그를 맞으며 말했다. \"자넬 반길 숨조차 거의 안 나오더군, 스티어포스.\"\n\n\"뭐, 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나, 내 얼굴을 보면 아픈 눈도 낫는 법이지.\" 스티어포스가 대꾸했다. \"활짝 핀 자네 얼굴을 보는 것도 그렇고 말이야, 데이지. 잘 지냈나, 나의 술꾼 양반?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지냈네.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 오늘 밤은 도무지 술꾼이 아닐세. 셋이서 또 한바탕 모이긴 했네만.\"\n\n\"내가 길에서 그 셋을 다 만났는데, 자네 칭찬을 큰 소리로 늘어놓더군.\" 스티어포스가 받았다. \"그 쫄바지 입은 친구는 누군가?\"\n\n나는 미코버 씨에 대해 몇 마디로 최대한 잘 알려 주었다. 그는 그 신사에 대한 내 어설픈 묘사에 진심으로 호탕하게 웃고는, 알아 둘 만한 사람이니 자기가 꼭 알아야겠다고 했다. \"그런데 다른 친구는 누군지 짐작이 가나?\" 이번에는 내가 물었다.\n\n\"낸들 알겠나.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"따분한 친구는 아니겠지? 내 보기엔 좀 그래 보이던데.\"\n\n\"트래들스야!\" 내가 의기양양하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그게 누군데?\" 스티어포스가 예의 그 무심한 투로 물었다.\n\n\"트래들스 기억 안 나? 세일럼 하우스에서 우리랑 한 방 쓰던 트래들스 말이야.\"\n\n\"오! 그 친구!\" 스티어포스가 부지깽이로 난롯불 위의 석탄 덩어리를 툭툭 치며 말했다. \"여전히 그렇게 물러 터졌나? 그런데 도대체 어디서 그 친구를 주워 왔어?\"\n\n나는 대답으로 트래들스를 할 수 있는 한 한껏 추켜세웠다. 스티어포스가 그를 좀 깔보는 듯한 느낌이 들었기 때문이다. 스티어포스는 가벼운 끄덕임과 미소로, 그리고 그 친구도 늘 별난 놈이긴 했으니 자기도 만나면 반갑겠다는 말로 그 화제를 털어 버리고는, 내게 뭐 먹을 것 좀 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 이 짤막한 대화 내내, 그는 거칠게 활기찬 투로 말을 하지 않을 때면 부지깽이로 석탄 덩어리를 멍하니 툭툭 치며 앉아 있었다. 내가 비둘기 파이 남은 것이며 이것저것을 꺼내는 동안에도 그가 똑같이 그러고 있는 것을 나는 알아챘다.\n\n\"이야, 데이지, 이건 임금님이 먹을 만한 저녁상인걸!\" 그가 침묵에서 불쑥 튀어나오듯 외치며 식탁 자리에 앉았다. \"내 이걸 제대로 대접해 주지. 야머스에서 오는 길이라 말이야.\"\n\n\"옥스퍼드에서 오는 줄 알았는데?\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"천만에.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"나는 뱃놀이를 하고 있었네 — 그게 더 나은 소일거리지.\"\n\n\"리티머가 오늘 자넬 찾으러 여기 왔었네.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 그 사람 말을 자네가 옥스퍼드에 있다는 뜻으로 알아들었는데, 지금 가만 생각해 보니 분명 그렇게 말하지는 않았어.\"\n\n\"리티머가 도대체 나를 찾으러 다녔다니, 내가 생각했던 것보다 더한 멍청이로군.\" 스티어포스가 유쾌하게 포도주를 한 잔 따라 내게 권하며 말했다. \"그 친구 말을 알아듣는 것으로 말하자면, 데이지, 자네가 그걸 해낼 수 있다면 우리 중 누구보다도 영리한 사람일세.\"\n\n\"그건 정말 그래.\" 내가 의자를 식탁 쪽으로 당기며 말했다. \"그래, 야머스에 다녀왔다니, 스티어포스!\" 나는 그 일에 대해 죄다 알고 싶어서 물었다. \"거기 오래 있었나?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 그가 받았다. \"한 일주일쯤 훌쩍 다녀온 거야.\"\n\n\"다들 어떻게 지내나? 물론 꼬마 에밀리는 아직 결혼 안 했겠지?\"\n\n\"아직. 곧 할 모양이야 — 몇 주 뒤든, 몇 달 뒤든, 뭐 그쯤에. 그 사람들이랑 그렇게 많이 어울리진 않았어. 참 그렇지.\" 그는 그토록 부지런히 놀리던 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고는, 주머니를 뒤지기 시작했다. \"자네한테 줄 편지가 있어.\"\n\n\"누구한테서 온 건데?\"\n\n\"아, 자네의 옛 유모한테서지.\" 그가 가슴 주머니에서 종이 몇 장을 꺼내며 받았다. \"'제이 스티어포스 귀하, 더 윌링 마인드에 외상값 빚짐.' 이건 아니고. 좀 참아 봐, 곧 찾을 테니. 그 뭐냐 하는 노인네가 형편이 안 좋다는데, 그 일에 관한 것 같아.\"\n\n\"바키스 말인가?\"\n\n\"맞아!\" 그가 여전히 주머니를 뒤지며 그 안의 것들을 훑어보았다. \"가엾은 바키스도 이젠 다 끝난 것 같아 걱정일세. 거기서 자그마한 약제사를 — 외과의든 뭐든 — 하나 만났는데, 그 양반이 자네 나리를 이 세상에 받아 낸 사람이더군. 나한테 그 병에 대해 어찌나 유식하게 떠들던지. 하지만 그 말의 요지인즉슨, 그 짐마차꾼이 마지막 길을 제법 빠르게 가고 있다는 거였어. — 저기 의자에 걸린 내 외투 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣어 보게. 거기 편지가 있을 거야. 있나?\"\n\n\"여기 있네!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"됐어!\"\n\n페거티에게서 온 것이었다. 평소보다 다소 알아보기 어렵고 짤막했다. 편지는 남편의 가망 없는 상태를 알리면서, 그가 전보다 '조금 가까워졌고', 그래서 그를 편안하게 보살피기가 더 어려워졌다고 넌지시 비쳤다. 자기가 얼마나 지치고 밤을 새우는지에 대해서는 한마디도 없이, 그를 한껏 칭찬했다. 그것은 내가 진실하다고 알고 있는, 꾸밈없고 수수하고 정겨운 신앙심으로 쓰여 있었고, '나의 영원한 사랑둥이에게 정성을 다하여'라는 말로 끝맺었다 — 나를 두고 하는 말이었다.\n\n내가 그 편지를 더듬더듬 읽어 가는 동안, 스티어포스는 줄곧 먹고 마셨다.\n\n\"안된 일이군.\" 내가 다 읽고 나자 그가 말했다. \"하지만 해는 날마다 지고, 사람은 시시각각 죽는 법이니, 우리가 누구에게나 닥치는 그 흔한 운명에 겁먹어서는 안 되지. 만인의 문간을 똑같이 두드리는 그 발소리가 어디선가 들려온다고 해서 우리가 제 자리를 지키지 못한다면, 이 세상 모든 것이 우리 손아귀에서 빠져나가고 말 거야. 안 돼! 계속 달려! 필요하면 험하게 박차를 가하든, 그걸로 되면 부드럽게 가든, 어쨌든 계속 달려! 모든 걸림돌을 넘어 계속 달려서, 경주에서 이겨 내!\"\n\n\"무슨 경주에서 이긴단 말인가?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자기가 뛰어든 그 경주에서.\" 그가 말했다. \"계속 달리는 거지!\"\n\n기억하건대, 그가 잘생긴 머리를 약간 뒤로 젖히고 손에 잔을 든 채 말을 멈추고 나를 바라보았을 때, 나는 알아챘다. 그의 얼굴에는 바닷바람의 싱그러움이 어려 발그레했지만, 내가 지난번에 보았을 때 이후로 새로 생긴 흔적이 있었다는 것을. 마치 한번 불붙으면 그토록 격정적으로 타오르는 그 뜨거운 열정을, 무슨 버릇처럼 끊임없이 쥐어짜며 매달려 온 사람 같았다. 나는 그가 무슨 변덕이든 한번 꽂히면 그것을 그토록 무모하게 좇는 방식을 — 이를테면 이번처럼 거친 바다에 부대끼고 험한 날씨에 맞서는 짓을 — 두고 한마디 타이를 마음을 먹고 있었다. 그런데 그때 내 마음이 다시 우리 대화의 당장의 화제로 빗겨 가, 그것을 대신 좇아갔다.\n\n\"내 말 좀 들어 보게, 스티어포스.\" 내가 말했다. \"자네의 그 드높은 기운이 내 말에 좀 귀를 기울여 준다면—\"\n\n\"강력한 기운이지. 자네가 원하는 건 뭐든 들어줄 걸세.\" 그가 식탁에서 다시 난롯가로 옮겨 앉으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 내 말 좀 들어 보게, 스티어포스. 나는 내려가서 내 옛 유모를 만나 볼까 하네. 내가 그녀에게 무슨 도움을 줄 수 있다거나, 무슨 실질적인 보탬이 된다는 건 아니야. 다만 그녀가 나를 어찌나 따르는지, 내가 방문하는 것만으로도 내가 그 둘 다를 해 줄 수 있는 것만큼이나 그녀에게 큰 위로가 될 거란 말일세. 그녀는 그걸 무척 고맙게 여길 테고, 그게 그녀에게 위안이 되고 버팀목이 될 거야. 그녀가 내게 그토록 좋은 벗이 되어 준 걸 생각하면, 분명 이건 그리 대단한 수고도 아니지. 자네가 내 처지라면 하루 길쯤은 가지 않겠나?\"\n\n그의 얼굴은 생각에 잠겼다. 그는 잠시 골똘히 헤아리다가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. \"그래! 가게. 해 될 거야 없으니.\"\n\n\"자네는 막 돌아온 참이니,\" 내가 말했다. \"함께 가자고 청해 봐야 헛일이겠지?\"\n\n\"전혀 안 되지.\" 그가 받았다. \"나는 오늘 밤 하이게이트로 갈 참이야. 어머니를 못 뵌 지 한참 됐고, 그게 양심에 걸리거든. 어머니가 그 탕아 같은 아들을 사랑하시는 만큼 사랑받는다는 건, 그래도 대단한 일이니까. — 에이! 다 부질없는 소리지! — 자네는 내일 갈 작정인 거지?\" 그가 두 손을 내 양어깨에 하나씩 얹고 나를 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"응, 그럴 생각이야.\"\n\n\"그럼, 모레 가게. 자네가 와서 우리랑 며칠 묵었으면 했단 말이야. 일부러 자넬 부르러 여기 왔는데, 자네는 야머스로 훌쩍 날아가 버리겠다니!\"\n\n\"훌쩍 날아간다는 말을 할 처지가 못 되는걸, 스티어포스. 자네야말로 늘 무슨 알 수 없는 원정인지 뭔지를 찾아 거칠게 싸돌아다니지 않나!\"\n\n그는 아무 말 없이 잠시 나를 바라보다가, 아까처럼 나를 붙든 채 한 번 흔들며 다시 받았다.\n\n\"자! 모레라고 하게. 그리고 내일도 되도록 많은 시간을 우리랑 보내! 안 그러면 우리가 언제 또 만날지 누가 알겠나? 자! 모레라고 하게! 나는 자네가 로자 다틀과 나 사이에 끼어 우리 둘을 떼어 놔 주면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"내가 없으면 자네 둘이 서로를 너무 사랑하기라도 할까 봐?\"\n\n\"그래, 아니면 너무 미워하든가.\" 스티어포스가 웃었다. \"어느 쪽이든 상관없어. 자! 모레라고 하게!\"\n\n나는 모레라고 했다. 그러자 그는 외투를 걸치고 여송연에 불을 붙이고는 집을 향해 걸어가려고 나섰다. 그가 이럴 작정인 것을 알고, 나도 내 외투를 걸치고 (다만 내 여송연에는 불을 붙이지 않았는데, 당분간은 그것으로 족할 만큼 피웠던 터였다) 그와 함께 큰길까지 걸었다. 그 무렵 밤에는 음산한 길이었다. 그는 가는 내내 기운이 넘쳤다. 그리고 우리가 헤어진 뒤, 그가 그토록 늠름하고 경쾌하게 집을 향해 가는 뒷모습을 바라보고 있자니, '모든 걸림돌을 넘어 계속 달려서, 경주에서 이겨 내!'라던 그의 말이 떠올랐다. 그리고 나는, 그가 달려 볼 만한 값진 경주가 있었으면 하고, 난생처음 바랐다.\n내 방에서 옷을 벗고 있는데, 미코버 씨의 편지가 바닥으로 툭 떨어졌다. 그래서 그 편지가 생각나, 나는 봉인을 뜯고 다음과 같이 읽었다. 그것은 저녁 식사 한 시간 반 전에 쓰인 것이었다. 내가 앞서 말했는지 모르겠으나, 미코버 씨는 무슨 유달리 절망적인 위기에 처할 때면 일종의 법률 투의 말투를 쓰곤 했는데, 그게 자기 신변을 정리하는 일에 맞먹는다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n'선생 — 감히 친애하는 코퍼필드라고는 부르지 못하겠기에,\n'본인은 짓이겨졌음을 귀하께 알려 드리는 것이 마땅하겠습니다. 자신의 비참한 처지를 귀하께서 너무 일찍 알게 되지 않도록 막아 보려는 가물거리는 노력의 흔적을, 귀하께서는 오늘 본인에게서 보셨을 것입니다. 그러나 희망은 지평선 아래로 가라앉았고, 본인은 짓이겨졌습니다.\n\n'이 서신은 어느 한 사람의 — 그 인품 곁이라고는 차마 말 못 하겠고 — 그 신변 범위 안에서 쓰이고 있습니다. 그자는 만취에 가까운 상태로, 어느 차압업자에게 고용된 사람입니다. 그자는 집세 미납에 따른 압류 명목으로, 이 거처를 법적으로 점유하고 있습니다. 그자의 재산 목록에는, 이 집의 연차 임차인인 본인에게 속한 온갖 종류의 동산과 세간뿐 아니라, 이너 템플 명예 협회 회원인 하숙인 토머스 트래들스 씨에게 속한 물건들까지 들어 있습니다.\n'지금 (불멸의 어느 작가의 표현을 빌리자면) 본인의 입술로 \"넘겨진\" 그 넘쳐흐르는 잔에, 만에 하나 한 방울의 암울함이라도 모자랐다면, 그것은 다음과 같은 사실에서 채워질 것입니다. 즉, 앞서 말한 토머스 트래들스 씨가 본인에게 호의로 인수해 준 이십삼 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스 반의 어음이 기한을 넘겼는데도, 대비가 돼 있지 않다는 사실에서 말입니다. 또한, 본인에게 매달린 산 입들의 책임이, 자연의 순리에 따라, 가엾은 희생자 하나가 더 늘어남으로써 무거워지리라는 사실에서 말입니다. 그 비참한 출현은 — 어림잡아 — 지금으로부터 여섯 달을 넘기지 않는 기간이 다하는 무렵에 예견될 수 있습니다.\n\n'이만큼 미리 말씀드렸으니, 티끌과 잿더미가 영원토록 흩뿌려졌다는 말을 덧붙이는 것은 사족이 될 것입니다.\n'그\n\n'머\n'리\n\n'위\n'에\n\n'윌킨스 미코버.'\n가엾은 트래들스! 이 무렵 나는 미코버 씨를 충분히 알았던 터라, 그가 이 타격에서 회복하리라고 능히 내다볼 수 있었다. 하지만 그날 밤 내 잠자리는 트래들스 생각으로, 그리고 데번셔 시골에 사는 그 부목사의 딸 생각으로 몹시 어지러웠다. 열 남매 중 하나인 그 아가씨는 그토록 사랑스러운 처녀였고, 예순 살이 되도록, 아니 입에 올릴 수 있는 그 어떤 나이가 되도록 트래들스를 기다려 줄 (불길한 칭찬이로다!) 사람이었으니.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. I VISIT STEERFORTH AT HIS HOME, AGAIN\n\n아침에 나는 스펜로 씨에게 잠깐 휴가를 얻고 싶다고 말했다. 나는 봉급을 받는 처지가 아니었고, 따라서 그 무자비한 조킨스의 미움을 살 까닭도 없었으므로, 휴가를 얻는 데는 아무런 어려움이 없었다. 나는 그 기회를 빌려, 목소리가 목구멍에 걸리고 말을 내뱉는 동안 눈앞이 아득해지는 채로, 스펜로 양이 잘 지내시기를 바란다는 뜻을 전했다. 그러자 스펜로 씨는 마치 흔해 빠진 보통 사람을 두고 말하기라도 하는 양 아무런 감정도 없이, 고맙다고, 딸은 아주 잘 지낸다고 대답했다.\n\n우리 견습 서기들은 귀족적인 대리인 계급의 씨앗으로서 어찌나 융숭한 대접을 받았던지, 나는 거의 언제나 내 시간의 주인이나 다름없었다. 그렇지만 나는 한낮 한두 시 전에는 하이게이트에 도착하고 싶지 않았고, 마침 그날 아침 법정에는 또 하나의 자그마한 파문 사건이 걸려 있었는데, 사건명은 '팁킨스가 영혼의 교정을 위해 불록을 상대로 제기하여 판사가 직권으로 다루는 사건'이었다. 그래서 나는 스펜로 씨를 따라 한두 시간을 자못 즐겁게 그 사건에 들이부었다. 사건은 두 교회 관리인 사이의 몸싸움에서 비롯된 것이었는데, 그중 한 사람이 다른 한 사람을 펌프 쪽으로 밀쳤다는 혐의였다. 그 펌프 손잡이가 학교 건물 안으로 비죽 튀어나와 있었고, 그 학교 건물은 교회 지붕의 박공 아래에 있었던 까닭에, 그 밀치는 행위가 교회법상의 위반이 된 것이었다. 재미있는 사건이었다. 그 사건 덕분에 나는 역마차 마부석에 올라앉아 하이게이트로 가는 내내, 박사회와, 박사회를 건드리면 나라가 무너진다던 스펜로 씨의 말을 곱씹었다.\n\n스티어포스 부인은 나를 보고 반가워했고, 로자 다틀도 그랬다. 리티머가 그 자리에 없다는 것을 알게 되어 나는 기분 좋게 놀랐는데, 우리 시중을 든 것은 모자에 파란 리본을 단 얌전하고 자그마한 응접실 하녀였다. 그 점잖은 사내의 눈을 우연히 마주치는 것보다, 그 하녀의 눈을 마주치는 편이 훨씬 더 기분 좋고 훨씬 덜 거북했다. 그런데 그 집에 들어선 지 반 시간도 채 되기 전에 내가 유독 눈여겨본 것은, 다틀 양이 나를 향해 바짝, 잔뜩 신경을 곤두세우고 지켜본다는 사실이었다. 그녀는 내 얼굴을 스티어포스의 얼굴과, 또 스티어포스의 얼굴을 내 얼굴과 슬그머니 견주어 보면서, 둘 사이에서 무언가 튀어나오기를 도사려 기다리는 듯한 태도를 보였다. 내가 그녀 쪽으로 눈길을 돌릴 때마다, 그 간절한 얼굴이, 움푹 꺼진 검은 눈과 캐묻는 듯한 이마를 하고서, 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 노리거나, 내 얼굴에서 갑자기 스티어포스의 얼굴로 옮겨 가거나, 우리 둘을 한꺼번에 거머쥐고 있는 것이 어김없이 보였다. 이 스라소니 같은 감시에서, 그녀는 내가 그것을 알아챈 것을 보고서도 결코 움츠러들기는커녕, 오히려 그럴 때면 더욱더 골똘한 표정으로 그 꿰뚫는 시선을 나에게 못 박았다. 그녀가 나를 두고 의심할 만한 어떤 잘못과 관련해서도 나는 떳떳했고, 또 떳떳하다는 것을 스스로 알고 있었음에도, 나는 그 굶주린 광채를 도무지 견디지 못하고 그 기이한 눈앞에서 움츠러들었다.\n\n온종일 그녀는 온 집안을 떠도는 것만 같았다. 내가 스티어포스의 방에서 그와 이야기를 나누면, 바깥 작은 복도에서 그녀의 옷자락 스치는 소리가 들려왔다. 그와 내가 집 뒤 잔디밭에서 옛날에 하던 운동을 좀 해 보려고 하면, 그녀의 얼굴이 떠도는 불빛처럼 이 창에서 저 창으로 옮겨 가다가, 마침내 어느 한 창에 멈춰 서서 우리를 지켜보는 것이 보였다. 오후에 우리 넷이서 산책을 나갔을 때는, 스티어포스와 그의 어머니가 듣지 못할 만큼 앞서 걸어 나가자, 그녀가 그 가느다란 손을 용수철처럼 내 팔에 척 감아 나를 붙들어 세우더니, 이렇게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"오랫동안,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여기 발걸음을 안 했더군요. 당신 일이 정말로 그렇게나 마음을 사로잡고 흥미진진해서, 온통 정신을 다 빼앗아 가나요? 내가 모르는 게 있으면 늘 알고 싶어서 묻는 거예요. 정말 그래요?\"\n\n나는 그 일이 제법 마음에 들기는 하지만, 그 정도로까지 대단하다고는 도저히 내세울 수 없다고 대답했다.\n\n\"어머! 그걸 알게 되어 다행이네요. 나는 내가 틀렸을 때 바로잡아 주는 걸 늘 좋아하거든요.\" 로자 다틀이 말했다. \"그러니까 좀 무미건조하다는 말씀인가요, 아마?\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 나는 대답했다. \"어쩌면 좀 무미건조하기는 하지요.\"\n\n\"어머! 그래서 당신은 기분 전환과 변화가, 자극이며 그런 것들이 필요한 거로군요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아아! 정말 그렇겠네요! 그런데 그게 그분한테는 좀, 에—? 당신 말고 말이에요.\"\n\n스티어포스가 어머니를 자기 팔에 기대게 한 채 거닐고 있는 쪽으로 그녀의 눈이 잽싸게 한 번 흘끗 가는 것을 보고, 나는 그녀가 누구를 가리키는지는 알아챘다. 그러나 그 이상은 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수 없었다. 내 표정이 그래 보였으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다.\n\n\"그게, 꼭 그렇다는 건 아니에요, 잘 들어요, 난 알고 싶은 것뿐이니까, 그게 그분을 좀 너무 사로잡지는 않나요? 그것 때문에 그분이, 어쩌면, 자기를 눈멀도록 끔찍이 아끼는 분을 찾아오는 일에 평소보다 좀 더 소홀해지지는 않나요, 에—?\" 그러면서 또 한 번 그들 쪽을 잽싸게 흘끗 보고는, 내 가장 깊은 속마음까지 들여다보려는 듯한 눈길을 나에게 던졌다.\n\n\"다틀 양,\" 나는 대답했다. \"제발 그런 생각은 마—\"\n\n\"안 해요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어머나 세상에, 내가 무슨 생각을 한다고 여기지 말아요! 난 의심하는 게 아니에요. 그저 물어볼 뿐이지요. 무슨 의견을 내세우는 게 아니라고요. 당신이 일러 주는 말을 바탕으로 의견을 세워 보려는 거예요. 그래서, 그렇지 않다는 거지요? 좋아요! 그걸 알게 되어 정말 기쁘네요.\"\n\n\"분명히 사실이 아닙니다.\" 나는 어리둥절해하며 말했다. \"스티어포스가 평소보다 더 오래 집을 비웠다고 해서 그게 제 탓이라는 건요. 정말 그가 그랬다 해도 말이지요. 사실 저는 지금 이 순간까지도 그걸 몰랐어요, 당신한테 듣고서야 알았을 뿐이지요. 어젯밤에 보기 전까지는 한참 동안 그를 본 적이 없거든요.\"\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"정말입니다, 다틀 양, 없어요!\"\n\n그녀가 나를 똑바로 쳐다보았을 때, 나는 그녀의 얼굴이 더 날카로워지고 더 창백해지는 것을, 그리고 그 오래된 상처 자국이 길게 늘어나면서 일그러진 윗입술을 가르고 아랫입술 깊숙이 파고들어, 얼굴을 비스듬히 내리긋는 것을 보았다. 거기에는, 그리고 그녀가 나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 이렇게 말할 때 그 두 눈에 서린 광채에는, 나로서는 정말이지 끔찍하다 싶은 무언가가 있었다.\n\n\"그가 뭘 하고 있죠?\"\n\n나는 너무 어안이 벙벙한 나머지, 그녀에게라기보다 나 자신에게 하듯 그 말을 따라 되뇌었다.\n\n\"그가 뭘 하고 있느냐고요?\" 그녀가, 불길처럼 제 몸을 태워 버릴 듯한 간절함으로 말했다. \"나를 볼 때마다 그 눈에 알 수 없는 거짓을 담고 있는 그 사내가, 그를 거들어 도대체 무슨 짓을 꾸미고 있는 거죠? 당신이 명예롭고 신의 있는 사람이라면, 당신더러 친구를 배신하라고는 하지 않아요. 그저 이것만 말해 줘요. 그를 이끌고 있는 게 노여움인가요, 미움인가요, 자존심인가요, 들뜬 마음인가요, 어떤 거친 망상인가요, 사랑인가요, 도대체 그게 뭐죠?\"\n\n\"다틀 양,\" 나는 대답했다. \"스티어포스에게서 제가 여기 처음 왔을 때와 달라진 점이라곤 아무것도 모른다는 걸, 어떻게 말씀드려야 당신이 믿어 주시겠습니까? 짚이는 게 아무것도 없어요. 정말이지 아무것도 없다고 굳게 믿습니다. 무슨 말씀을 하시는지조차 거의 알아듣지 못하겠어요.\"\n\n그녀가 여전히 나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 서 있는 사이, 그 잔인한 흉터에, 내가 고통이라는 생각과 도저히 떼어 놓을 수 없는 어떤 씰룩거림 혹은 욱신거림이 떠오르더니, 입술 한쪽 끝을 들어 올렸다. 마치 비웃듯이, 아니면 그 대상을 경멸하는 듯한 연민으로. 그녀는 황급히 거기에 손을 갖다 댔다. 어찌나 가늘고 여린 손이었던지, 그녀가 그 손을 들어 올려 난롯불 앞에서 얼굴을 가리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 속으로 그 손을 고운 자기에 견주었었다. 그러고는 빠르고 사납고 격정에 찬 목소리로 \"이 일에 대해선 비밀로 하겠다고 맹세해요!\" 하고 말하고는, 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n스티어포스 부인은 아들과 함께 있는 것을 유달리 행복해했고, 스티어포스도 이번에는 어머니에게 유달리 다정하고 정중했다. 두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 나에게 무척 흥미로웠는데, 서로를 향한 애정 때문만이 아니라, 둘 사이의 강한 외모의 닮음과, 그에게서 도도하거나 격하게 드러나는 것이 그녀에게서는 나이와 성별 덕분에 부드러워져 기품 있는 위엄으로 누그러진다는 점 때문이었다. 나는 한 번 이상 이런 생각을 했다. 두 사람 사이에 갈라설 만한 심각한 까닭이 한 번도 끼어들지 않은 것은 다행한 일이라고. 그렇지 않았다면 그토록 닮은 두 천성은, 아니 차라리 같은 천성의 두 빛깔이라고 해야겠는데, 세상에서 가장 극단으로 맞선 두 정반대보다도 화해시키기가 더 어려웠을지도 모른다고. 솔직히 고백하건대, 그 생각은 내 분별에서 비롯된 것이 아니라 로자 다틀의 한마디에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 저녁 식사 자리에서 이렇게 말했다.\n\"아, 그런데 누구든 좀 말해 줘요. 온종일 그것만 생각하고 있었거든요. 정말 알고 싶어서요.\"\n\n\"무엇을 알고 싶다는 거니, 로자?\" 스티어포스 부인이 받아 말했다. \"제발, 제발, 로자, 알쏭달쏭하게 굴지 좀 마라.\"\n\n\"알쏭달쏭하다고요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"어머! 정말요? 저를 그렇게 보세요?\"\n\n\"내가 늘 너한테 부탁하지 않니,\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다. \"있는 그대로, 네 본래의 모습 그대로 분명하게 말하라고?\"\n\n\"어머! 그러면 이게 제 본래 모습이 아니라는 건가요?\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"이제 정말 저를 좀 참아 주셔야겠어요. 알고 싶어서 여쭙는 거니까요. 우리는 우리 자신을 결코 모르잖아요.\"\n\n\"그게 어느새 제이의 천성이 되어 버렸지.\" 스티어포스 부인이 조금도 언짢은 기색 없이 말했다. \"하지만 나는 기억한단다. 너도 기억할 거야, 아마. 네 태도가 지금과 달랐던 때를 말이다, 로자. 그렇게 경계심에 차 있지 않고, 더 마음을 열고 있던 때를.\"\n\n\"정말 맞는 말씀이세요.\" 그녀가 받아 말했다. \"그렇게 나쁜 버릇이 사람한테 차츰 배는 거지요! 정말요? 덜 경계하고 더 마음을 열고 있었다고요? 어떻게 나도 모르는 새에 그렇게 변할 수가 있었을까요! 어머, 참 묘하네요! 옛날의 저를 되찾도록 애써 봐야겠어요.\"\n\n\"그래 주면 좋겠구나.\" 스티어포스 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"어머! 정말로 그럴게요, 아시겠죠!\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"솔직함을 배워야겠어요, 누구한테냐면, 가만있자, 제임스한테서요.\"\n\n\"솔직함이라면, 로자,\" 스티어포스 부인이 얼른 말했다. 로자 다틀의 말에는, 이번처럼 세상에 둘도 없이 무심한 태도로 내뱉어도, 늘 어딘가 빈정대는 느낌이 묻어났기 때문이다. \"그보다 더 좋은 스승은 없겠지.\"\n\n\"그건 저도 확신해요.\" 그녀가 보기 드문 열의를 담아 대답했다. \"제가 무언가를 확신한다면, 당연히, 아시다시피, 그건 확신하지요.\"\n\n스티어포스 부인은 조금 발끈했던 것을 후회하는 듯 보였다. 이내 다정한 어조로 이렇게 말했으니까.\n\n\"자, 로자야, 네가 무엇을 알고 싶어 하는지 우리는 아직 못 들었구나?\"\n\n\"제가 무엇을 알고 싶어 하느냐고요?\" 그녀가 사람 약 올리는 듯한 냉랭함으로 대답했다. \"어머! 그저, 도덕적 기질이 서로 닮은 사람들이, 그게 맞는 표현인가요?\"\n\n\"다른 어떤 표현 못지않게 좋은 표현이지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"고마워요. 그러니까, 도덕적 기질이 서로 닮은 사람들이, 그렇지 않은 처지의 사람들보다, 둘 사이에 무언가 심각한 다툼의 씨앗이 생겼다고 칠 때, 화를 내며 깊이 갈라설 위험이 더 큰가 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 봐야겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴까요?\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"어머나! 그럼 가령, 예를 들어, 있을 법하지 않은 일이라야 가정이 되니까, 당신과 어머님이 심각하게 다투신다고 쳐 봐요.\"\n\n\"얘, 로자야,\" 스티어포스 부인이 사람 좋게 웃으며 끼어들었다. \"다른 가정을 좀 들려무나! 제임스와 나는 서로에 대한 도리를 그보다는 잘 안단다, 하느님 감사하게도!\"\n\n\"어머!\" 다틀 양이 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그렇겠지요. 그게 다툼을 막아 준다는 거지요? 아니, 물론 그렇겠지요. 바로 그거예요. 자, 제가 이렇게 어리석게 그런 경우를 들어 본 게 다행이네요. 서로에 대한 도리가 그걸 막아 준다는 걸 알게 되어 정말 좋으니까요! 정말 고마워요.\"\n\n다틀 양과 관련하여 또 하나의 자그마한 일을 빠뜨려서는 안 되겠다. 훗날, 돌이킬 수 없는 지난 일이 모두 환히 드러났을 때, 나는 그것을 떠올릴 까닭이 있었으니까. 그날 하루 종일, 그러나 특히 이 무렵부터, 스티어포스는 자신의 온갖 재주를 다해, 그것도 더없이 수월하게 다해, 이 별난 사람을 흐뭇하고 유쾌한 길동무로 홀려 놓으려 애썼다. 그가 성공하리라는 것은 나에게 조금도 놀라운 일이 아니었다. 그녀가 그의 유쾌한 재주, 그때 나로서는 유쾌한 천성이라 여겼던 그 매혹적인 힘에 맞서 버둥대는 것 또한 놀랍지 않았다. 그녀가 이따금 비뚤어지고 심술궂어진다는 것을 나는 알고 있었으니까. 나는 그녀의 얼굴빛과 태도가 천천히 변해 가는 것을 보았다. 나는 그녀가 점점 더 큰 흠모의 눈길로 그를 바라보는 것을 보았다. 나는 그녀가, 마치 제 안의 나약함을 꾸짖기라도 하듯, 점점 더 힘없이, 그러나 늘 화가 난 듯이, 그가 지닌 그 사람 홀리는 힘에 맞서려 애쓰는 것을 보았다. 그리고 마침내 나는 그녀의 날카로운 눈길이 부드러워지고, 그 미소가 자못 온화해지는 것을 보았으며, 온종일 정말로 품고 있던 그녀에 대한 두려움도 어느새 사라져, 우리 넷은 모두 난롯가에 둘러앉아, 마치 어린아이들처럼 거리낌 없이 이야기하고 웃었다.\n\n우리가 그곳에 너무 오래 앉아 있었기 때문인지, 아니면 스티어포스가 애써 얻은 이점을 놓치지 않겠다고 작정한 탓인지는 모르겠으나, 그녀가 자리를 뜬 뒤로 우리가 식당에 남아 있던 시간은 오 분도 되지 않았다. \"그녀가 하프를 켜고 있군.\" 응접실 문 앞에서 스티어포스가 나직이 말했다. \"내가 알기로, 어머니 말고는 지난 삼 년 동안 그녀가 하프 켜는 걸 들은 사람이 아무도 없을 거야.\" 그는 묘한 미소를 띠고 그렇게 말했는데, 그 미소는 이내 사라졌다. 우리가 방으로 들어가 보니 그녀는 혼자 있었다.\n\n\"일어나지 마요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다, 그녀는 이미 일어선 뒤였다. \"로자, 일어나지 말아요! 한 번만 너그럽게, 우리한테 아일랜드 노래 한 곡 불러 줘요.\"\n\n\"당신이 아일랜드 노래 따위에 무슨 관심이 있어요?\" 그녀가 되받았다.\n\n\"많아요!\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"다른 어떤 노래보다 훨씬 더. 여기 데이지도 마음속 깊이 음악을 사랑한답니다. 아일랜드 노래 한 곡 불러 줘요, 로자! 그러면 옛날처럼 내가 앉아서 들을게요.\"\n\n그는 그녀에게도, 그녀가 일어선 의자에도 손대지 않고, 하프 가까이에 자리를 잡고 앉았다. 그녀는 한동안 묘한 모양새로 그 옆에 서서, 오른손으로 하프를 켜는 시늉만 할 뿐 소리는 내지 않았다. 마침내 그녀는 자리에 앉아, 단번에 하프를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기더니, 켜며 노래했다.\n\n그녀의 손길에서였는지 목소리에서였는지, 무엇이 그 노래를, 내 평생 들어 본, 아니 상상할 수 있는 그 어떤 노래보다도 이 세상 것 같지 않게 만들었는지 나는 모른다. 그 생생함에는 어딘가 무서운 데가 있었다. 마치 그 노래가 애초에 쓰인 적도, 곡이 붙은 적도 없이, 그녀 안의 격정에서 솟아난 것만 같았다. 그 격정은 나직한 목소리로 어설프게 터져 나왔다가, 모든 것이 잠잠해지자 다시 웅크렸다. 그녀가 다시 하프 옆에 몸을 기울이고, 오른손으로 켜는 시늉만 할 뿐 소리는 내지 않았을 때, 나는 말을 잃은 채로 있었다.\n\n그러고 다시 한참이 지나서야, 무언가가 나를 그 황홀경에서 깨워 놓았다. 스티어포스가 자리를 떠나 그녀에게로 가더니, 웃으며 그녀의 몸에 팔을 두르고, \"자, 로자, 앞으로 우리 서로 무척 사랑하기로 해요!\" 하고 말한 것이다. 그러자 그녀는 그를 후려치고는, 들고양이 같은 사나움으로 그를 뿌리치고 방을 박차고 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"로자한테 무슨 일이라도 있니?\" 스티어포스 부인이 들어오며 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐 동안,\" 스티어포스가 대답했다. \"천사 노릇을 했어요, 어머니. 그러고는 보상이라도 하려는 듯 정반대 끝으로 내달은 거지요.\"\n\n\"그 애를 화나게 하지 않도록 조심해야 한다, 제임스. 그 애 성미가 비뚤어져 있다는 걸 잊지 말거라. 함부로 건드릴 게 못 돼.\"\n\n로자는 돌아오지 않았다. 내가 잘 자라는 인사를 하러 스티어포스와 함께 그의 방으로 들어갈 때까지, 그녀 이야기는 더 나오지 않았다. 그제야 그는 그녀를 두고 웃으며, 그렇게 사납고 자그마한, 도무지 알 수 없는 존재를 본 적이 있느냐고 나에게 물었다.\n\n나는 그때 표현할 수 있는 만큼 놀라움을 나타내며, 그녀가 그토록 갑자기, 그토록 심하게 마음 상해 한 까닭이 무엇인지 짐작할 수 있겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그건 하느님이나 아시겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"네 마음대로 갖다 붙여, 아니면 아무것도 아니거나! 누이는 자기 자신을 포함해서 모든 걸 숫돌에 갈아 날을 세운다고 내가 말했잖아. 누이는 날붙이라서, 다룰 때 여간 조심하지 않으면 안 돼. 늘 위험한 사람이야. 잘 자!\"\n\n\"잘 자!\" 나는 말했다. \"친애하는 스티어포스! 나는 아침에 네가 깨기 전에 떠날 거야. 잘 자!\"\n\n그는 나를 보내기 싫어하며, 내 방에서 그랬듯이, 내 양어깨에 한 손씩 얹고 나를 멀찍이 밀어내듯 붙든 채로 서 있었다.\n\n\"데이지,\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"네 대부 대모가 지어 준 이름은 아니지만, 너를 부를 때 내가 제일 좋아하는 이름이지. 그리고 나는 바라, 정말 바라, 진정 바라, 네가 그 이름을 나한테 줄 수 있다면 좋겠어!\"\n\n\"왜, 내가 마음만 먹으면 줄 수 있지.\" 나는 말했다.\n\n\"데이지, 혹시라도 무엇이 우리를 갈라놓는 일이 생기거든, 너는 나를 내 가장 좋았던 모습으로 떠올려 줘야 해, 친구. 자! 우리 그렇게 약속하자. 혹시라도 사정이 우리를 갈라놓거든, 나를 내 가장 좋았던 모습으로 생각해 줘!\"\n\n\"너에게는 나에게 가장 좋은 모습도, 가장 나쁜 모습도 따로 없어, 스티어포스.\" 나는 말했다. \"너는 언제나 한결같이 사랑받고, 내 마음속에 소중히 간직되어 있으니까.\"\n\n내가 그를, 비록 형체도 없는 한 가닥 생각으로나마 잘못 의심한 적이 있다는 것에 대한 가책이 어찌나 내 안에 사무쳤던지, 그런 적이 있노라는 고백이 입가에까지 차올랐다. 애그니스의 믿음을 저버리기가 꺼려졌던 것, 그리고 그 믿음을 저버릴 위험 없이 그 이야기를 어떻게 꺼내야 할지 막막했던 것만 아니었다면, 그가 \"신의 가호가 있기를, 데이지, 잘 자!\" 하고 말하기 전에 그 고백이 입 밖으로 나왔으리라. 그러나 그 망설임 속에서, 그것은 끝내 입 밖으로 나오지 못했다. 그리고 우리는 악수를 나누고 헤어졌다.\n\n나는 희뿌연 새벽녘에 일어나, 되도록 소리 나지 않게 옷을 챙겨 입고서, 그의 방을 들여다보았다. 그는 깊이 잠들어 있었다. 학창 시절 누워 있는 모습을 자주 보았던 그대로, 한쪽 팔을 베고 편안하게 누워서.\n\n때가 제철에 이르렀을 때, 그것도 머지않아 이르렀을 때, 나는 그를 바라보며, 어떻게 아무것도 그의 잠을 어지럽히지 않는지 거의 의아할 지경이었다. 하지만 그는 잠들어 있었다. 다시 한번 그렇게 떠올리게 해 다오, 학창 시절 잠든 모습을 자주 보았던 그대로. 그렇게, 이 고요한 시각에, 나는 그를 두고 떠났다. 다시는, 오, 하느님 그대를 용서하소서, 스티어포스! 다시는 그 무력한 손을 사랑과 우정으로 잡지 못하리라. 다시는, 영영 다시는!\n\n# CHAPTER 30. A LOSS\n\n저녁 무렵 야머스에 도착해 여관으로 갔다. 패거티의 빈방, 곧 내 방이 머지않아 사람들로 북적이게 될 것이 뻔했다. 모든 산 자가 그 앞에서 길을 비켜 줘야 하는 그 위대한 손님이 이미 그 집에 들어와 있지 않다면 말이다. 그래서 나는 여관에 들어 저녁을 먹고 잠자리를 잡아 두었다.\n\n밖으로 나섰을 때는 열 시였다. 가게는 대부분 문을 닫았고, 거리는 적막했다. 오머앤조럼 가게에 이르렀을 때 덧문은 닫혀 있었으나 가게 문은 활짝 열려 있었다. 안쪽 거실 문가에서 파이프를 피우고 있는 오머 씨의 모습이 비스듬히 눈에 들어와, 나는 안으로 들어가 안부를 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 이런 세상에, 맙소사!\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"어떻게 지내셨소? 자, 앉으시오. 담배 연기, 거북하지 않으면 좋겠는데?\"\n\n\"전혀요.\" 내가 말했다. \"전 담배가 좋아요. 남이 피우는 파이프라면요.\"\n\n\"뭐라, 본인 것은 아니라는 말이오, 응?\" 오머 씨가 웃으며 받았다. \"그게 더 낫지요, 선생. 젊은이한테는 나쁜 버릇이니까. 자, 앉으시오. 나야 천식 때문에 피우는 거라오.\"\n\n오머 씨는 나에게 자리를 내주고 의자를 놓아 주었다. 그러고는 다시 앉았는데 숨이 무척 차서, 파이프 속에 자기 목숨이 달린 무언가가 들어 있어 그게 없으면 죽기라도 하는 양 거기에 대고 헐떡였다.\n\n\"바키스 씨가 안 좋다는 소식을 들어서 마음이 아픕니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n오머 씨는 흔들림 없는 얼굴로 나를 바라보더니 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 그분 상태가 어떤지 아세요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그건 내가 선생한테 물었어야 할 질문인데,\" 오머 씨가 받았다. \"체면상 못 묻는 거라오. 우리 같은 장사의 곤란한 점이지. 누가 앓고 있으면, 우리는 그 사람이 어떤지 물을 수가 없거든.\"\n\n그런 곤란함은 미처 생각해 보지 못했다. 들어올 때 그 익숙한 곡조를 듣게 될까 봐 나도 걱정이 없진 않았지만 말이다. 그가 입에 올리자 비로소 나도 알아채고, 그렇다고 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 알아들으셨구먼.\" 오머 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"우린 감히 그럴 수가 없소. 글쎄, '오머앤조럼이 안부 여쭙니다, 오늘 아침은 좀 어떠신지요?'—아니면 오후라든가, 형편 따라—이렇게 말했다간, 웬만한 사람은 충격에서 못 헤어날 거요.\"\n\n오머 씨와 나는 서로 고개를 끄덕였고, 오머 씨는 파이프의 힘을 빌려 숨을 추슬렀다.\n\n\"그게 바로 우리 장사가 마음 쓰고 싶어도 못 쓰게 되는 점 중 하나라오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"내 경우만 봐도 그렇지. 바키스를 안 지가, 그가 지나갈 때 알은체할 만큼 사귄 지가 사십 년이오. 그런데도 가서 '좀 어떻소?' 하고 물을 수가 없으니.\"\n\n오머 씨에게는 좀 가혹한 일이다 싶어, 그렇게 말해 주었다.\n\n\"나도 남들보다 더 제 잇속만 챙기는 사람은 아닐 거요.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"날 좀 보시오! 내 숨이 언제 멎을지 모르는데, 내가 알기로, 이런 처지에 제 잇속을 챙길 리가 있겠소. 풀무가 두 쪽으로 갈라지듯 숨이 갈 때 가 버린다는 걸 아는 사람이, 그것도 할애비까지 된 사람이 그럴 리가 없다는 말이오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 우리 장사를 두고 불평하는 건 아니라오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그게 아니야. 어느 직업에나 좋은 면도 있고 나쁜 면도 있는 법이지, 두말할 것 없이. 내가 바라는 건 다만, 사람들이 좀 더 마음 단단하게 자라났으면 하는 거요.\"\n\n오머 씨는 더없이 흡족하고 인자한 얼굴로 말없이 몇 모금 빨아들이더니, 처음 이야기로 돌아와 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 우리는 바키스의 형편이 어떤지 알아보려면 에밀리한테 묻는 수밖에 없소. 그 애는 우리의 진짜 속셈이 뭔지 아니까, 우리가 새끼 양 떼이기라도 한 것처럼 우리를 두고 더는 놀라거나 의심하지 않는다오. 사실 미니랑 조럼이 방금 그 집에 내려갔소(그 애가 일 끝나고 거기 가서 제 고모를 좀 도와주거든). 오늘 밤 그분이 좀 어떤지 물어보러 말이오. 선생이 그 애들 돌아올 때까지 기다려 주시겠다면, 자세한 사정을 다 들려드릴 거요. 뭐 좀 드시겠소? 럼주에 물 탄 거 한 잔, 어떻소? 나야 럼주에 물 타서 마시며 담배를 피운다오.\" 오머 씨가 제 잔을 집어 들며 말했다. \"이 성가신 숨이 드나드는 그 통로를 부드럽게 해 준다고들 해서 말이오. 그렇지만, 원 세상에,\" 오머 씨가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"탈이 난 건 통로가 아니라오! '나한테 숨만 넉넉히 다오, 그럼 통로는 내가 알아서 찾을 테니, 얘야.' 우리 딸 미니한테 그렇게 말한다오.\"\n\n그는 정말로 아낄 숨이 없는 사람이라, 그가 웃는 모습을 보는 것은 여간 조마조마한 일이 아니었다. 다시 말을 받을 수 있는 상태가 되었을 때, 나는 권해 준 음료에 고맙다고 인사한 뒤 방금 저녁을 먹었다며 사양했다. 그러고는 이렇게 친절히 청해 주시니 따님 내외가 돌아올 때까지 기다리겠다고 하면서, 어린 에밀리는 어떻게 지내는지 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 선생,\" 오머 씨가 턱을 문지르려고 파이프를 떼며 말했다. \"솔직히 말하면, 나는 그 애 혼인이 치러지면 마음이 놓일 것 같소.\"\n\n\"왜 그러시죠?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 지금은 그 애가 마음을 못 잡고 있어서 말이오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"예전만 못해서가 아니오, 오히려 더 예뻐졌으니까—정말이오, 더 예뻐졌다오. 일을 예전만큼 못 해서도 아니오, 잘하니까. 예전에도 여섯 사람 몫을 했고, 지금도 여섯 사람 몫을 한다오. 그런데 어쩐지 마음이 빠진 것 같단 말이지. '자, 길게 한 번, 세게 한 번, 다 같이 한 번, 자 동지들, 만세!' 이런 표현으로 내가 대충 무슨 말을 하려는 건지 알아들으신다면,\" 오머 씨가 다시 턱을 문지르고 담배를 좀 빨고 나서 말했다. \"선생한테 이렇게 말하겠소. 그게 바로—대충 말해서—요즘 에밀리한테서 안 보이는 거라고 말이오.\"\n\n오머 씨의 얼굴과 몸짓에 워낙 많은 것이 담겨 있어서, 나는 진심으로 그 뜻을 헤아린 듯 고개를 끄덕일 수 있었다. 내가 빨리 알아듣는 것이 그를 흐뭇하게 한 모양인지, 그는 말을 이어 갔다. \"한데 내가 보기엔, 이게 주로 그 애가 마음을 못 잡은 상태라서 그런 거란 말이오, 아시겠소. 일 끝나고 그 애 외삼촌이랑 나랑, 또 그 애 연인이랑 나랑 이 얘기를 꽤나 했다오. 내가 보기엔 주로 그 애가 마음을 못 잡아서야. 에밀리에 대해서는 늘 잊지 말아야 할 게 있소.\" 오머 씨가 부드럽게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그 애는 더없이 정 많은 어린것이란 말이오. 속담에 '암퇘지 귀로는 비단 지갑을 못 만든다'고 하지요. 글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠소. 내 생각엔, 어려서부터 시작하면 만들 수 있을 것도 같소. 그 애는 저 낡은 배를 보금자리로 만들었다오, 선생. 돌이며 대리석으로도 못 따라갈 보금자리를 말이오.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 했지요!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 예쁜 어린것이 외삼촌한테 매달리는 걸 보면 말이오,\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"날마다 점점 더 꼭, 점점 더 바짝 그이한테 매달리는 걸 보면, 그게 정말 볼만한 광경이오. 한데 그렇게 되면, 아시다시피 마음속에 싸움이 벌어지는 법이지. 그걸 굳이 필요 이상으로 길게 끌 까닭이 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n나는 이 마음씨 좋은 노인의 말에 귀를 기울이며, 그의 말에 진심으로 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그래서 내가 그 애들한테 이렇게 말했소.\" 오머 씨가 느긋하고 편안한 어조로 말했다. \"이렇게 말이오. '자, 에밀리를 시간으로 못 박아 두었다고는 절대 생각지들 마라. 너희들 형편대로 정하렴. 그 애가 해 준 일은 생각보다 훨씬 값졌고, 그 애가 배운 솜씨도 생각보다 빨랐다. 남은 거야 오머앤조럼이 펜으로 쓱 그어 지워 버리면 되고, 너희가 원할 때 그 애는 자유의 몸이다. 그 뒤에 그 애가 집에서 우리한테 자잘한 일을 좀 해 주겠다고 약조라도 하고 싶다면, 그것도 좋고. 안 한다 해도, 그것도 좋고. 어쨌든 우리야 손해 볼 게 없으니.' 왜냐하면—아시겠소,\" 오머 씨가 파이프로 나를 툭 건드리며 말했다. \"나처럼 숨이 모자라는 사람이, 그것도 할애비까지 된 사람이, 그 애처럼 자그마한 파란 눈의 꽃봉오리 같은 애를 두고 야박하게 따질 리가 있겠소?\"\n\n\"전혀 그러실 분이 아니지요, 분명히요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 아니고말고! 옳은 말씀이오!\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그래, 선생, 그 애 사촌이—그 애가 사촌이랑 혼인한다는 건 아시지요?\"\n\n\"아, 네.\" 내가 대답했다. \"그 사람 잘 알아요.\"\n\n\"하긴 잘 아시겠지.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그래, 선생! 그 애 사촌이, 보아하니 일자리도 좋고 살림도 넉넉한데, 이 일을 두고 아주 사내답게 나한테 고마워하더란 말이오(그 사람 행동거지가 전반적으로, 정말이지, 내가 그 사람을 높이 보게 만들었소). 그러더니 가서, 선생이나 나나 한번 보면 탐낼 만한, 아담하고 편안한 집을 한 채 구해 놨소. 그 작은 집이 이제 안팎으로 다 꾸며져서, 인형의 집 거실처럼 말끔하고 빈틈이 없다오. 바키스 병세가 이렇게 나빠지지만 않았어도, 가엾은 양반, 그 애들은 진작에—아마 지금쯤은—부부가 됐을 거요. 사정이 이러니, 미뤄진 거지.\"\n\n\"그럼 에밀리는요, 오머 씨?\" 내가 물었다. \"마음을 좀 더 잡았나요?\"\n\n\"글쎄 그건, 아시다시피,\" 그가 다시 겹턱을 문지르며 받았다. \"당연히 기대할 수 없는 일이지. 곧 닥칠 변화며 이별이며 그런 게, 말하자면 그 애한테 코앞에 다가온 동시에 아득히 멀게도 느껴지는 거라오. 바키스가 죽는다고 혼인이 그리 미뤄질 건 없지만, 이렇게 질질 끄는 건 미뤄질 수 있지. 아무튼 형편이 뒤숭숭한 거요, 아시겠소.\"\n\n\"알겠어요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그러니,\" 오머 씨가 말을 이었다. \"에밀리는 여전히 좀 가라앉아 있고, 좀 안절부절못한다오. 어쩌면 전체로 봐서 예전보다 더하지. 날마다 외삼촌을 점점 더 따르고, 우리 모두랑 헤어지기를 점점 더 싫어하는 것 같소. 내가 다정한 말 한마디만 건네도 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해진다오. 우리 딸 미니의 어린 딸이랑 같이 있는 모습을 보면, 선생도 평생 못 잊을 거요. 아이고 세상에!\" 오머 씨가 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"그 애가 그 아기를 얼마나 사랑하는지!\"\n\n이렇게 좋은 기회가 온 김에, 그의 딸 내외가 돌아와 우리 이야기가 끊기기 전에, 오머 씨에게 마사에 대해 뭔가 아는 게 있는지 물어볼 생각이 들었다.\n\n\"아!\" 그가 고개를 가로저으며, 몹시 풀이 죽은 얼굴로 받았다. \"안된 일이오. 슬픈 사연이지요, 선생이 어떻게 알게 됐든 간에. 난 그 애한테 나쁜 구석이 있다고는 한 번도 생각 안 했소. 우리 딸 미니 앞에서야 이 얘기를 꺼내고 싶지 않지만—꺼냈다간 당장 나한테 따지고 들 테니까—그래도 난 한 번도 안 그랬소. 우리 중 누구도 안 그랬지.\"\n\n오머 씨는 내가 듣기 전에 먼저 딸의 발소리를 듣고는, 파이프로 나를 툭 건드리고 한쪽 눈을 찡긋 감아 조심하라는 신호를 보냈다. 곧이어 딸 내외가 들어왔다.\n\n그들이 전한 소식은, 바키스 씨가 '더할 나위 없이 나쁜' 상태라는 것, 의식이 전혀 없다는 것, 그리고 칠립 의사 선생이 방금 떠나면서 부엌에서 침통하게 하던 말, 곧 내과의사회며 외과의사회며 약제사 회관을 다 한꺼번에 불러 모은들 그분을 못 살린다고 했다는 것이었다. 그분은 두 의사회의 손을 이미 떠났고, 약제사 회관은 그저 그분을 독살하는 일밖에 못 한다고 칠립 의사 선생이 말했다고 했다.\n\n이 말을 듣고, 또 패거티 씨가 거기 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 나는 곧장 그 집으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 오머 씨와 조럼 씨 내외에게 잘 자라고 인사하고, 그 집을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다. 엄숙한 심정이 가슴에 차오르면서, 바키스 씨가 전혀 새롭고 다른 존재로 느껴졌다.\n\n문을 나직이 두드리자 패거티 씨가 나왔다. 그는 내 예상만큼 나를 보고 놀라지 않았다. 잠시 뒤 내려온 패거티에게서도 그 점을 알아챘다. 그 뒤로도 나는 그런 모습을 여러 번 보았는데, 생각건대 그 무시무시한 놀라움을 기다리는 마음 앞에서는 다른 모든 변화와 놀라움이 아무것도 아닌 것으로 쪼그라드는 모양이다.\n\n나는 패거티 씨와 악수하고 부엌으로 들어갔고, 그는 살그머니 문을 닫았다. 어린 에밀리가 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 난롯가에 앉아 있었다. 함이 그 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n우리는 소곤소곤 이야기했다. 이따금 말을 멈추고 위층 방에서 무슨 소리가 나는지 귀를 기울이면서. 지난번 방문 때는 미처 생각지 못했는데, 부엌에서 바키스 씨가 보이지 않는다는 것이 이제 와 어찌나 이상하게 느껴지던지!\n\n\"이렇게 와 주시니 정말 고맙소, 데이비 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"보통 친절이 아니지요.\" 함이 말했다.\n\n\"에밀리, 얘야,\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다. \"이것 봐라! 데이비 도련님이 오셨다! 자, 기운 내거라, 예쁜아! 데이비 도련님께 한마디 안 할 게냐?\"\n\n그 애의 온몸이 떨리고 있었는데, 지금도 그 모습이 눈에 선하다. 내가 손을 잡았을 때 느껴지던 그 손의 차가움은 지금도 손끝에 남아 있다. 그 손이 살아 있다는 유일한 표시는 내 손을 피해 움츠러드는 것뿐이었다. 그러고는 의자에서 스르르 미끄러져 내려와, 외삼촌의 반대편으로 기어가더니, 여전히 말없이 떨면서 그의 가슴에 고개를 묻었다.\n\n\"이 애 마음이 어찌나 정이 깊은지,\" 패거티 씨가 크고 거친 손으로 그 애의 탐스러운 머리카락을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"이런 슬픔은 견디질 못한다오. 젊은것들이야 본디 그런 거지요, 데이비 도련님. 이런 시련을 처음 겪고, 게다가 내 작은 새처럼 겁이 많으면—본디 그런 거라오.\"\n\n그 애는 외삼촌에게 더욱 바짝 매달렸으나, 얼굴을 들지도, 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"밤이 깊었구나, 얘야,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"함이 너를 집에 데려다주려고 왔단다. 자! 이 또 다른 정 깊은 사람이랑 같이 가렴! 뭐라고, 에밀리? 응, 예쁜아?\"\n\n그 애의 목소리는 나한테까지 닿지 않았지만, 그는 그 애 말에 귀 기울이듯 고개를 숙이더니 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"외삼촌이랑 같이 있겠다고? 아니, 너 설마 나한테 그런 걸 물으려는 게냐! 외삼촌이랑 있겠다고, 우리 아가? 곧 네 남편이 될 사람이 너를 집에 데려다주러 여기 와 있는데? 이 자그마한 애가 나처럼 비바람에 거칠어진 사내 옆에 서 있는 걸 보면 아무도 짐작 못 하겠지만,\" 패거티 씨가 한없는 자랑스러움을 담아 우리 둘을 둘러보며 말했다. \"바닷물의 소금기보다도 이 애가 제 외삼촌을 향해 품은 정이 더 짙다오—어리석은 우리 에밀리!\"\n\n\"에밀리 말이 맞아요, 데이비 도련님!\" 함이 말했다. \"여기 봐요! 에밀리가 그러길 원하고, 게다가 마음이 이렇게 뒤숭숭하고 겁에 질려 있으니, 아침까지 그냥 두겠소. 나도 같이 있게 해 주세요!\"\n\n\"아니, 아니다.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"너 같은 가장이—아니, 곧 가장이 될 사람이—하루 일감을 내팽개치면 못쓴다. 게다가 밤새 지켜보고 일까지 둘 다 하면 못써. 그건 안 될 일이지. 너는 집에 가서 잠을 청하거라. 에밀리가 보살핌을 잘 못 받을까 봐 걱정하는 건 아니잖니, 내 다 안다.\"\n\n함은 이 설득에 따라, 가려고 모자를 집어 들었다. 그가 그 애에게 입을 맞출 때조차도—그가 그 애에게 다가가는 모습을 볼 때마다, 나는 하늘이 그에게 신사의 마음을 주었음을 느꼈다—그 애는 외삼촌에게 더욱 바짝 매달리며, 제가 택한 남편마저 피하려는 듯했다. 나는 그가 나간 뒤 문을 닫아, 감도는 고요가 흐트러지지 않게 했다. 그러고 돌아서니, 패거티 씨가 여전히 그 애에게 이야기하고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 나는 위층에 올라가서 네 고모한테 데이비 도련님이 오셨다고 알려야겠다. 그러면 고모도 좀 기운이 날 거야.\" 그가 말했다. \"그동안 너는 난롯가에 앉아 그 얼음장 같은 손을 좀 녹이렴. 그렇게까지 무서워하고 안달할 것 없다. 뭐라고? 나랑 같이 가겠다고?—그래! 같이 가자꾸나—오너라! 만약 외삼촌이 집에서 쫓겨나 도랑에 누워야 하는 신세가 된다 해도, 데이비 도련님,\" 패거티 씨가 아까 못지않은 자랑스러움을 담아 말했다. \"내 생각엔 이 애가 지금 같아선 외삼촌을 따라나설 거요! 그렇지만 머지않아 다른 사람이 생길 게다—머지않아, 다른 사람이, 에밀리야!\"\n\n그 뒤에 위층으로 올라가다가, 캄캄한 내 작은 방 문 앞을 지날 때, 그 애가 그 안에서 바닥에 엎드려 있는 듯한 어렴풋한 느낌이 들었다. 그러나 정말로 그 애였는지, 아니면 방 안 그림자가 어지럽게 뒤엉킨 것이었는지는 지금도 알 수 없다.\n\n나는 부엌 난롯가에서, 예쁜 어린 에밀리가 죽음을 두려워하는 마음을 곰곰이 생각해 볼 여유가 있었다. 오머 씨가 들려준 이야기에 그 마음까지 더해 보니, 그것이 그 애가 그토록 제 본모습 같지 않은 까닭이라 짐작되었다. 그리고 패거티가 내려오기 전까지, 나에게는 그 나약함을 좀 더 너그럽게 헤아릴 여유마저 있었다. 시계 똑딱이는 소리를 세고, 주위를 감싼 그 엄숙한 정적의 무게를 곱씹으며 앉아 있는 동안 말이다. 패거티는 나를 품에 안고, 이 슬픔 속에서 내가 이토록 큰 위로가 되어 준다고(그렇게 말했다) 거듭거듭 축복하며 고마워했다. 그러고는 위층으로 올라오라고 간곡히 청하면서, 바키스 씨가 늘 나를 좋아하고 우러러보았다고, 정신을 잃기 전까지 자주 내 이야기를 했다고 흐느꼈다. 또 만약 그이가 다시 정신을 차린다면, 이 세상 무엇에든 환해질 수 있는 사람이라면, 나를 보고 환해질 거라고 믿는다고 했다.\n\n그러나 그를 보았을 때, 그이가 다시 정신을 차릴 가망은 내가 보기에 아주 희박했다. 그는 머리와 어깨를 침대 밖으로 내민 채, 불편한 자세로 누워, 그토록 숱한 고통과 애를 먹였던 그 궤짝에 반쯤 몸을 기대고 있었다. 알고 보니, 그이가 침대에서 기어 나가 그것을 열어 볼 기력도, 내가 그이가 쓰는 걸 본 적 있는 그 점치는 막대기로 궤짝이 무사한지 확인할 기력도 없어지자, 침대 머리맡 의자에 그것을 올려놓아 달라고 했고, 그 뒤로 밤낮없이 그것을 끌어안고 있었다는 것이다. 지금도 그의 팔은 그 위에 얹혀 있었다. 시간과 세상이 그의 발밑에서 빠져나가고 있었으나, 궤짝만은 그 자리에 있었다. 그리고 그가 마지막으로 내뱉은 말은 (무언가 설명하는 듯한 어조로) \"헌 옷가지야!\"였다.\n\n\"바키스, 여보!\" 패거티가 거의 명랑하다 싶을 만큼 밝게 말하며 그이 위로 몸을 숙였다. 그동안 그녀의 오빠와 나는 침대 발치에 서 있었다. \"여기 우리 귀한 도련님이 오셨어요—우리 귀한 도련님, 데이비 도련님이요, 우리 둘을 맺어 준 그분이잖아요, 바키스! 당신이 도련님 편에 전갈을 보냈잖아요! 데이비 도련님께 말 좀 안 할래요?\"\n\n그는 궤짝처럼 말이 없고 아무것도 느끼지 못했다. 그의 모습이 띤 유일한 표정이라곤 그 궤짝에서 흘러나온 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"썰물 따라 떠나는 거라오.\" 패거티 씨가 손으로 입을 가리고 나에게 말했다.\n\n내 눈도 흐릿했고 패거티 씨의 눈도 그러했다. 그러나 나는 속삭이듯 되물었다. \"썰물 따라요?\"\n\n\"바닷가 사람들은 죽지를 못한다오,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"물이 거의 다 빠질 무렵이 아니면 말이오. 태어나지도 못해요, 물이 거의 다 들어찰 무렵이 아니면—제대로 태어나질 못하지, 밀물이 차야 비로소. 그이는 썰물 따라 떠나는 거라오. 세 시 반에 썰물이 빠지고, 그 뒤 반 시간은 물이 멈춘다오. 물이 돌아설 때까지 버틴다면, 밀물이 지나도록 견디다가, 다음 썰물 따라 떠날 거요.\"\n\n우리는 거기 머물며 그를 오래도록 지켜보았다—몇 시간을. 그런 의식 상태에 빠진 그에게 내 존재가 어떤 알 수 없는 영향을 끼쳤는지는 감히 말하지 못하겠다. 그러나 그가 마침내 가냘프게 헛소리를 시작했을 때, 분명 그는 나를 학교에 데려다준 일을 두고 중얼거리고 있었다.\n\n\"정신이 돌아오나 봐요.\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n패거티 씨가 나를 건드리더니, 깊은 경외와 두려움을 담아 속삭였다. \"둘 다 빠르게 떠나가고 있구려.\"\n\n\"바키스, 여보!\" 패거티가 말했다.\n\n\"클래라 페거티 바키스.\" 그가 희미하게 외쳤다. \"세상에 다시없을 좋은 사람이지!\"\n\n\"봐요! 여기 데이비 도련님이 계세요!\" 패거티가 말했다. 그가 이제 눈을 떴기 때문이다.\n\n내가 막 그에게 나를 알아보겠느냐고 물으려는 찰나, 그가 팔을 뻗으려 애쓰며, 또렷한 목소리로 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"바키스는 좋다 이거야!\"\n\n그리고 물이 가장 낮게 빠진 그때, 그는 썰물 따라 떠나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 31. A GREATER LOSS\n\n패거티가 간곡히 청하니, 그 가엾은 짐마차꾼의 유해가 블런더스톤으로 마지막 여행을 다녀올 때까지 이곳에 머무르기로 마음먹는 일은 나에게 그리 어렵지 않았다. 패거티는 오래전에 자기가 모은 돈을 털어, 우리 옛 교회 묘지에 '자기 귀여운 아가씨'—패거티는 늘 내 어머니를 그렇게 불렀다—의 무덤 가까이 작은 땅 한 뙈기를 사 두었고, 그곳에 두 사람이 함께 잠들게 될 터였다.\n\n패거티 곁에서 함께 지내며 내가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 해 주는 동안(아무리 애써 봐야 보잘것없었지만), 나는 지금 돌이켜 보아도 흐뭇할 만큼, 그때 내가 그래 주었기를 바라는 만큼 진심으로 고마운 마음을 다했다고 자부한다. 그러나 솔직히 고백하건대, 바키스 씨의 유언장을 떠맡아 그 내용을 풀어 설명하는 일에서는, 나 개인의, 그리고 내 직업과 관련된 더할 나위 없는 만족감을 느꼈던 것 같아 부끄럽다.\n\n유언장을 그 상자 안에서 찾아보자고 처음 제안한 공로만큼은 내 것이라 주장해도 좋으리라. 한참을 뒤진 끝에 유언장은 상자 안, 말 먹이주머니 밑바닥에서 나왔다. 그 주머니 안에서는 (건초 말고도) 갖가지 물건이 발견되었다. 바키스 씨가 결혼식 날 차고는 그 전에도 그 후에도 한 번도 본 적 없던 줄과 봉인이 달린 낡은 금시계, 다리 모양으로 된 은 담배 누름쇠, 자그마한 찻잔과 받침이 가득 든 모조 레몬—이건 내 짐작에 바키스 씨가 내가 어릴 적에 선물로 주려고 사 두었다가 나중에 차마 손에서 놓지 못한 물건이 아니었을까 싶다—, 기니 금화와 반 기니 금화로 된 여든일곱 기니 반, 깔끔하기 그지없는 은행권으로 된 이백십 파운드, 잉글랜드 은행 주식의 영수증 몇 장, 낡은 편자 하나, 가짜 일 실링 동전, 장뇌 한 덩이, 그리고 굴 껍데기 하나가 그것이었다. 마지막 굴 껍데기는 잘 닦여 안쪽이 무지갯빛으로 반짝이고 있는 것으로 보아, 나는 바키스 씨가 진주에 관해 어렴풋한 생각을 품고 있었으되 그것이 끝내 무엇으로도 결실을 맺지 못했다고 결론지었다.\n\n여러 해 동안, 바키스 씨는 길을 나설 때면 날마다 이 상자를 지니고 다녔다. 남의 눈에 더 잘 띄지 않도록, 그는 이것이 '블랙보이 씨'의 것이며 '찾으러 올 때까지 바키스에게 맡겨 둔다'는 이야기를 꾸며 냈고, 그 그럴싸한 거짓말을 뚜껑에 정성껏 적어 두었는데, 글자는 이제 거의 알아보기 힘들 정도였다.\n\n알고 보니, 그는 이 여러 해 동안 알뜰한 목적으로 돈을 모아 두었던 것이다. 그의 돈 재산은 거의 삼천 파운드에 이르렀다. 이 중 천 파운드의 이자는 패거티 씨가 살아 있는 동안 받도록 남겼고, 그가 죽으면 원금은 패거티와 어린 에밀리와 나에게, 혹은 그중 살아남은 사람에게 똑같이 나누어 갖게 했다. 그가 남기고 떠난 나머지 전부는 패거티에게 물려주었고, 패거티를 잔여 재산 상속인이자 그 마지막 유언장의 유일한 유언 집행인으로 지정했다.\n\n나는 이 문서를 격식을 한껏 차려 큰 소리로 읽고, 그 조항들을 관계자들에게 몇 번이고 거듭 설명하면서, 내가 어엿한 대리인이라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다. 나는 민사 법원이라는 곳이 내가 생각했던 것보다 더 알찬 데라고 여기기 시작했다. 나는 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 유언장을 살핀 뒤, 모든 면에서 완벽하게 형식을 갖추었다고 단언하고, 여백에 연필로 표시를 몇 군데 해 두고는, 내가 이렇게 많이 알고 있다는 것이 자못 놀랍다고 생각했다.\n\n이 어려운 일에 매달리면서, 패거티에게 그녀가 물려받은 모든 재산을 정리해 명세를 만들어 주면서, 모든 일을 조리 있게 처리하면서, 그리고 모든 사안에서 그녀의 자문이자 조언자 노릇을 하면서—이건 우리 둘 다에게 기쁜 일이었다—나는 장례식 전 그 한 주를 보냈다. 그동안 어린 에밀리는 보지 못했지만, 보름 뒤에 그 애가 조용히 결혼식을 올릴 거라는 이야기를 들었다.\n\n감히 말하자면, 나는 격식을 갖춰 장례식에 참석하지는 않았다. 새들을 놀래킬 만한 검은 외투에 상장 띠를 두르고 차려입지 않았다는 뜻이다. 다만 아침 일찍 블런더스톤까지 걸어가서, 운구 행렬이 도착했을 때 교회 묘지에 있었다. 행렬을 따라온 사람이라고는 패거티와 그 오라비뿐이었다. 그 미친 신사는 내 작은 창문 너머로 내다보고 있었고, 칠립 씨네 아기는 유모의 어깨 너머로 목사를 향해 무거운 머리를 까닥이며 똥그란 눈을 데굴데굴 굴렸으며, 오머 씨는 뒤편에서 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬었다. 그 밖에는 아무도 없었고, 사방이 무척 고요했다. 모든 것이 끝난 뒤, 우리는 한 시간 남짓 교회 묘지를 거닐었고, 내 어머니의 무덤 위에 선 나무에서 어린 잎사귀 몇 장을 따 왔다.\n\n여기서 나는 두려움에 사로잡힌다. 내가 외로운 걸음을 되짚어 향하던 그 먼 마을 위로 먹구름이 무겁게 드리운다. 나는 거기 다가서기가 두렵다. 그 잊지 못할 밤에 닥쳐온 일을, 그리고 내가 계속 이야기를 이어 간다면 다시금 닥쳐올 그 일을 떠올리는 것을 차마 견딜 수가 없다.\n\n내가 그것을 글로 쓴다고 해서 더 나빠지는 것은 아니다. 더없이 내키지 않는 이 손을 멈춘다고 해서 더 나아지는 것도 아니다. 일은 이미 벌어졌다. 그 무엇도 되돌릴 수 없고, 그 무엇도 일을 있는 그대로가 아닌 다른 것으로 만들 수 없다.\n\n나의 옛 유모는 이튿날 유언장 문제로 나와 함께 런던에 가기로 되어 있었다. 어린 에밀리는 그날을 오머 씨네서 보내고 있었다. 우리는 그날 밤 그 낡은 배 집에서 다 함께 모이기로 했다. 햄이 늘 그렇듯 그 시각에 에밀리를 데려올 터였다. 나는 한가로이 걸어 돌아갈 생각이었다. 오라비와 누이는 왔던 길로 돌아가, 날이 저물 무렵 난롯가에서 우리를 기다리기로 했다.\n\n나는 쪽문에서 그들과 헤어졌다. 그 옛날 환영 속의 스트랩이 로데릭 랜덤의 배낭을 메고 쉬어 가던 그 문이었다. 그러고는 곧장 돌아가지 않고, 로스토프트로 가는 길을 따라 조금 걸었다. 그런 다음 발길을 돌려 야머스 쪽으로 되짚어 걸었다. 앞서 말한 나루터에서 일이 마일쯤 떨어진 그럴듯한 선술집에 들러 저녁을 먹었다. 그렇게 하루가 흘러가, 거기 다다랐을 때는 이미 저녁이었다. 그 무렵 비가 거세게 쏟아지고 있었고 사나운 밤이었다. 그래도 구름 뒤에 달이 있어서, 캄캄하지는 않았다.\n\n이내 패거티 씨네 집과 창문 너머로 새어 나오는 그 안의 불빛이 눈에 들어왔다. 발이 푹푹 빠지는 무거운 모래밭을 허우적허우적 가로지르자 문 앞에 이르렀고, 나는 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n집 안은 정말이지 더없이 아늑해 보였다. 패거티 씨는 저녁 담배를 한 대 피운 참이었고, 곧 차릴 저녁 식사 채비도 되어 있었다. 난롯불은 환했고, 재는 위로 그러모아 두었으며, 어린 에밀리의 그 옛 자리에는 사물함이 놓여 있었다. 패거티는 다시금 자기 옛 자리에 앉아, (옷차림만 빼면) 마치 한 번도 그 자리를 떠난 적 없는 사람처럼 보였다. 그녀는 어느새 뚜껑에 세인트 폴 성당이 그려진 반짇고리며, 오두막이 그려진 줄자며, 밀랍 양초 토막의 벗 노릇하던 시절로 되돌아가 있었다. 그리고 그 물건들은 모두, 한 번도 누가 건드린 적 없다는 듯 그대로 거기 있었다. 거미지 부인은 자기 옛 구석 자리에서 조금 끙끙대며 시름에 잠긴 듯했고, 그래서 마찬가지로 아주 자연스러워 보였다.\n\n\"제일 먼저 오셨네요, 데이비 도련님!\" 패거티 씨가 환한 얼굴로 말했다. \"도련님, 젖었으면 그 외투를 계속 입고 계시면 안 됩니다요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 패거티 씨.\" 나는 그에게 겉외투를 벗어 걸어 달라고 건네며 말했다. \"아주 보송보송한걸요.\"\n\n\"그렇네요!\" 내 어깨를 만져 보며 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"바싹 마른 나뭇조각 같습니다요! 앉으시지요, 도련님. 도련님한테 어서 오시라 말하는 건 다 부질없는 일이지만, 그래도 진심으로, 마음 다해 환영합니다요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 패거티 씨, 그건 잘 알아요. 그래, 패거티!\" 나는 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"어떻게 지냈어요, 우리 할멈?\"\n\n\"하, 하!\" 패거티 씨가 웃으며 우리 곁에 앉더니, 근래 시름에서 풀려난 안도감과 천성 그대로의 정 깊은 마음에서 두 손을 비볐다. \"도련님, 이 세상에 저 사람보다 마음 편히 지내도 될 여자는 없습니다요—제가 늘 그렇게 말해요. 저 사람은 떠난 양반한테 도리를 다했고, 떠난 양반도 그걸 알았지요. 그리고 떠난 양반도 저 사람한테 옳게 해 주었고, 저 사람도 떠난 양반한테 옳게 해 주었으니—그러니—그러니—다 잘된 거예요!\"\n\n거미지 부인이 끙 신음했다.\n\n\"기운 내세요, 우리 곱디고운 아주머니!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. (하지만 그는 우리 쪽으로 고개를 돌려 살래살래 저었는데, 근래 일들이 옛일의 기억을 다시 불러일으키리라는 걸 분명히 알아챈 눈치였다.) \"축 처져 있지 마세요! 아주머니 자신을 위해서라도, 딱 조금만 기운을 내 봐요. 그럼 훨씬 더 많은 기운이 절로 따라오는지 안 오는지 두고 보자고요!\"\n\n\"나한텐 안 그래요, 대니얼.\" 거미지 부인이 받아쳤다. \"나한텐 외롭고 쓸쓸한 것 말고는 뭐 하나 절로 되는 게 없어요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 패거티 씨가 그녀의 슬픔을 달래며 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요, 맞다고요, 대니얼!\" 거미지 부인이 말했다. \"난 돈을 물려받은 사람들하고 같이 살 처지가 못 돼요. 만사가 나랑은 너무 어긋나거든. 차라리 내가 없어지는 게 나아요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아주머니 없이 내가 그 돈을 어떻게 쓰겠어요?\" 패거티 씨가 자못 진지하게 나무라는 투로 말했다. \"무슨 그런 말씀을 하세요? 이제는 어느 때보다 아주머니가 더 필요하지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"난 진작부터 누가 날 필요로 한 적이 없는 줄 알고 있었어요!\" 거미지 부인이 가엾게 흐느끼며 외쳤다. \"그런데 이젠 그 소리를 대놓고 듣는구려! 이렇게 외롭고 쓸쓸하고 만사가 어긋나는 내가, 누가 날 필요로 하길 어찌 바라겠어요!\"\n\n패거티 씨는 그토록 매정하게 받아들여질 만한 말을 한 자신에게 몹시 충격을 받은 듯했지만, 패거티가 소맷자락을 잡아당기며 고개를 가로젓는 바람에 대꾸하지 못했다. 그는 깊은 시름에 잠겨 한동안 거미지 부인을 바라보다가, 네덜란드제 시계를 흘끗 보더니, 자리에서 일어나 촛불 심지를 다듬어 창가에 놓았다.\n\n\"자!\" 패거티 씨가 기운차게 말했다. \"자, 됐습니다요, 거미지 아주머니!\" 거미지 부인이 살짝 끙 신음했다. \"늘 하던 대로 불을 밝혔지요! 도련님은 저게 무엇 때문인지 궁금하시겠지요! 글쎄요, 저건 우리 어린 에밀리를 위해서랍니다. 보세요, 어두워지면 그 길이 그리 밝지도 명랑하지도 않거든요. 그래서 그 애가 집에 오는 시각에 제가 여기 있으면, 창가에 불을 놓아둡니다요. 저게 말이지요,\" 패거티 씨가 흐뭇함에 겨워 내 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 말했다. \"두 가지 뜻이 있어요. 에밀리가 그래요, '저기가 우리 집이구나!' 하고요. 또 에밀리가 이렇게도 말하지요. '우리 외삼촌이 저기 계시는구나!' 하고요. 제가 거기 없으면 불을 안 밝히니까요.\"\n\n\"외삼촌은 어린애 같아요!\" 패거티가 말했다. 정말로 그렇게 여겼다 해도, 그래서 더 그가 사랑스럽다는 투였다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 패거티 씨가 다리를 꽤 넓게 벌리고 서서, 흐뭇한 만족감에 두 손으로 다리를 위아래로 문지르며, 우리와 난롯불을 번갈아 바라보며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그럴지도 모르겠네요. 보세요, 겉으로 봐선 그렇지 않지만요.\"\n\n\"딱히 그렇진 않죠.\" 패거티가 한마디 거들었다.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 패거티 씨가 웃으며 말했다. \"겉으로 봐선 아니지만, 그—속을 들여다보면 말이에요. 아이고, 상관없어요! 자, 내 말 좀 들어 보세요. 제가 우리 에밀리의 그 예쁘장한 집을 둘러보고 또 둘러보고 있노라면, 저는—저는, 빌어먹을,\" 패거티 씨가 갑자기 힘주어 말했다. \"자! 더는 못 하겠네—꼭 그 자그마한 물건 하나하나가 그 애 같은 느낌이 든단 말이지요. 그걸 집어 들었다 내려놨다 하고, 우리 에밀리라도 되는 양 조심조심 어루만져요. 그 애 작은 모자며 그런 것들도 마찬가지고요. 누가 일부러 그것들을 함부로 다루는 꼴은 못 봅니다요—온 세상을 다 준대도요. 거대한 바다 호저(豪猪) 꼴을 한 어린애가 여기 있구먼!\" 패거티 씨가 한바탕 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃으며 진지함을 털어 냈다.\n\n패거티와 나도 함께 웃었지만, 그렇게 크게는 아니었다.\n\n\"제 생각은 말이지요,\" 패거티 씨가 다시 한참 다리를 문지른 뒤, 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 말했다. \"이게 다 제가 그 애랑 그렇게 많이 놀아 준 탓인 것 같아요. 우리가 튀르키예 사람이네, 프랑스 사람이네, 상어네 하고, 별별 외국 것들 흉내를 다 냈거든요—아이고, 그럼요. 사자에 고래에, 또 뭐였는지도 모를 온갖 것까지요!—그 애가 내 무릎 높이도 안 됐을 적에 말이에요. 그게 버릇이 돼 버렸나 봅니다요. 자, 이 촛불만 봐도 그래요!\" 패거티 씨가 신이 나서 촛불 쪽으로 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"그 애가 시집가서 떠나고 나면, 그래도 저는 저 촛불을 지금처럼 똑같이 거기 놓아둘 게 뻔하단 말이지요. 제가 밤마다 여기 있을 때면 (어떤 복이 굴러들어 온대도, 아이고 참, 제가 여기 말고 어디서 살겠어요!) 그 애가 여기 없거나 제가 거기 없거나 해도, 저는 창가에 촛불을 놓고 난로 앞에 앉아, 지금 이러는 것처럼 그 애를 기다리는 척하고 있을 거예요. 거대한 바다 호저 꼴을 한 어린애가 여기 있다니까요!\" 패거티 씨가 또 한바탕 껄껄거리며 말했다. \"글쎄, 지금 이 순간에도, 촛불이 환히 타오르는 걸 보면 저는 속으로 '그 애가 저걸 보고 있구나! 에밀리가 오고 있어!' 하고 중얼거린다니까요. 거대한 바다 호저 꼴을 한 어린애가 여기 있다고요! 그래도 다 맞는 말이지요.\" 패거티 씨가 너털웃음을 뚝 그치고 두 손을 탁 마주치며 말했다. \"왜냐면 그 애가 여기 왔거든요!\"\n\n그러나 햄뿐이었다. 내가 들어온 뒤로 밤이 더 사나워진 모양인지, 그는 커다란 방수모를 얼굴 위로 푹 눌러쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"에밀리는 어디 있냐?\" 패거티 씨가 물었다.\n\n햄이 그 애가 밖에 있다는 듯 고갯짓을 했다. 패거티 씨는 창가에서 촛불을 가져다 심지를 다듬어 탁자에 놓고는, 부지런히 난롯불을 휘젓고 있었다. 그때, 꼼짝도 않고 서 있던 햄이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님, 잠깐 밖에 나오셔서, 에밀리하고 제가 도련님께 보여 드릴 게 있는데 한번 봐 주시겠어요?\"\n\n우리는 밖으로 나갔다. 문간에서 그를 지나치는 순간, 나는 그가 새파랗게 질려 있는 것을 보고 소스라치게 놀랐다. 그는 다급히 나를 한데로 떠밀더니, 우리 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 우리 둘만 남았다.\n\n\"햄! 무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"데이비 도련님!—\" 아, 그 찢어진 가슴이여, 그는 어찌나 끔찍하게 흐느끼던지!\n\n나는 그런 비탄의 모습 앞에서 온몸이 굳어 버렸다. 내가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 무엇을 두려워했는지조차 모르겠다. 그저 그를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"햄! 가엾고 착한 친구! 제발, 무슨 일인지 말해 줘!\"\n\n\"제 사랑이, 데이비 도련님—제 가슴의 자랑이자 희망이—제가 그 애를 위해서라면 죽었을 테고 지금도 죽을 그 애가—떠나 버렸어요!\"\n\n\"떠나다니!\"\n\n\"에밀리가 달아나 버렸어요! 아, 데이비 도련님, 그 애가 어떻게 달아났는지 좀 생각해 보세요. 저는 자비롭고 거룩하신 하느님께, 그 애가(세상 그 무엇보다 소중한 그 애가) 파멸과 치욕에 빠지게 하시느니 차라리 그 애를 거두어 가시기를 비는데도요!\"\n\n그가 어지러운 하늘을 향해 쳐든 얼굴, 맞잡은 두 손의 떨림, 그 몸뚱이의 고통은, 내 기억 속에서 지금 이 순간까지도 그 외로운 황무지와 한데 얽혀 떠오른다. 그곳은 늘 밤이고, 그 풍경 속 유일한 존재는 바로 그다.\n\n\"도련님은 배운 분이시니까,\" 그가 다급히 말했다. \"뭐가 옳고 뭐가 가장 좋은지 아실 거예요. 제가 집 안에 들어가서 뭐라고 말해야 하나요? 도련님, 제가 외삼촌한테 이걸 도대체 어떻게 알려야 하지요?\"\n\n문이 움직이는 것이 보였고, 나는 본능적으로 한순간이라도 시간을 벌려고 바깥쪽에서 빗장을 붙잡으려 했다. 너무 늦었다. 패거티 씨가 얼굴을 쑥 내밀었다. 우리를 본 순간 그 얼굴에 떠오른 변화를, 내가 오백 년을 산다 해도 결코 잊지 못하리라.\n\n큰 통곡과 울부짖음이 있었고, 여자들이 그에게 매달렸으며, 우리 모두 방 안에 서 있던 것이 기억난다. 나는 햄이 건네준 종이 한 장을 손에 쥐고 있었고, 패거티 씨는 조끼가 찢겨 풀어헤쳐진 채, 머리는 산발이고, 얼굴과 입술은 새하얗게 질렸으며, 가슴팍으로는 피가 (입에서 솟구친 것이지 싶다) 뚝뚝 흘러내리는 채로, 나를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"읽어 주세요, 도련님.\" 그가 떨리는 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"천천히요, 부디. 제가 알아들을 수 있을지 모르겠네요.\"\n\n죽음 같은 정적 속에서, 나는 얼룩진 편지를 이렇게 읽어 내려갔다.\n\n\"'제가 마음이 순결했던 시절에도 받을 자격이 없던 저를, 이토록 끔찍이 사랑해 주신 외삼촌이 이 글을 보실 때면, 저는 멀리 떠나 있을 거예요.'\"\n\n\"멀리 떠나 있겠다.\" 그가 천천히 따라 읊었다. \"잠깐! 에밀리가 멀리 떠나 있다. 그래!\"\n\n\"'제가 사랑하는 우리 집—사랑하는 우리 집—아, 사랑하는 우리 집을!—아침에 떠날 때면,'\"\n\n편지에는 그 전날 밤 날짜가 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"'—그건 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않으려는 거예요. 그이가 저를 귀부인으로 만들어 데려오지 않는 한은요. 이 편지는 밤에, 여러 시간이 지난 뒤에야, 저 대신 발견되겠지요. 아, 제 가슴이 얼마나 찢어지는지 외삼촌이 아신다면. 제가 그토록 몹쓸 짓을 한 외삼촌이, 결코 저를 용서하실 수 없는 외삼촌이, 제가 얼마나 괴로운지 알기만이라도 하실 수 있다면! 저는 너무 사악해서 제 이야기를 쓸 수도 없어요! 아, 제가 이렇게 못된 애라고 생각하시며 위안 삼으세요. 아, 제발, 외삼촌한테 전해 주세요. 제가 지금만큼 외삼촌을 절반만큼도 사랑한 적이 없다고요. 아, 다들 저한테 얼마나 다정하고 친절하셨는지 떠올리지 마세요—우리가 결혼하기로 했던 일도 떠올리지 마세요—그저 제가 어렸을 때 죽어서 어딘가에 묻힌 셈 치고 생각하려 해 보세요. 제가 떠나가는 곳, 그 하늘에 비나니, 외삼촌을 가엾이 여겨 주시기를! 제가 외삼촌을 절반만큼도 이리 사랑한 적이 없다고 전해 주세요. 외삼촌의 위안이 되어 주세요. 한때 제가 외삼촌께 그러했듯, 그리고 당신께 진실하고, 당신께 어울리며, 저 말고는 부끄러움이라곤 모를, 그런 착한 아가씨를 사랑하세요. 모두에게 하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를! 저는 무릎 꿇고, 모두를 위해 자주 기도할게요. 그이가 저를 귀부인으로 만들어 데려오지 않는다면, 저 자신을 위해서는 기도하지 않고, 모두를 위해 기도할게요. 외삼촌께 이별의 사랑을 보냅니다. 제 마지막 눈물과, 제 마지막 감사를, 외삼촌께!'\"\n\n그게 다였다.\n\n내가 다 읽기를 멈춘 뒤에도 그는 한참을 그대로 서서, 여전히 나를 바라보았다. 마침내 나는 용기를 내어 그의 손을 잡고, 할 수 있는 한 정성껏, 부디 마음을 좀 다잡아 보시라고 간곡히 청했다. 그는 꼼짝도 않은 채 대답했다. \"고맙습니다, 도련님, 고맙습니다요!\"\n\n햄이 그에게 말을 건넸다. 패거티 씨는 햄의 슬픔만큼은 가까스로 헤아릴 수 있을 만큼은 정신이 있어서, 그의 손을 꼭 그러쥐었다. 그러나 그것 말고는 여전히 똑같은 상태였고, 아무도 감히 그를 건드리지 못했다.\n\n마침내 그는 환상에서 깨어나기라도 하듯 천천히 내 얼굴에서 눈길을 떼더니, 방 안을 두루 둘러보았다. 그러고는 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그놈이 누구냐? 난 그놈 이름을 알아야겠다.\"\n\n햄이 나를 흘끗 보았고, 순간 나는 뒤로 나동그라질 만큼 큰 충격에 휩싸였다.\n\n\"의심 가는 놈이 있구나.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그게 누구냐?\"\n\n\"데이비 도련님!\" 햄이 애원했다. \"잠깐 나가 계세요. 제가 외삼촌한테 마땅히 해야 할 말을 하게요. 도련님은 이 얘길 들으시면 안 됩니다요.\"\n\n나는 또다시 충격을 느꼈다. 의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 무어라 대답을 해 보려 했지만, 혀가 묶인 듯했고 눈앞이 흐릿했다.\n\n\"그놈 이름을 알아야겠다니까!\" 또 한 번 그렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"얼마 전부터,\" 햄이 더듬더듬 말했다. \"이 근방에 종놈 하나가 이따금 얼쩡거렸어요. 신사 양반도 한 명 있었고요. 둘이 한패였어요.\"\n\n패거티 씨는 아까처럼 그대로 굳은 채 서 있었으나, 이제는 그를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"그 종놈이,\" 햄이 말을 이었다. \"어젯밤에—우리 가엾은 그 애랑—함께 있는 걸 누가 봤대요. 그놈은 이 일주일 넘게 이 근방에 숨어 있었어요. 다들 그놈이 떠난 줄 알았는데, 숨어 있었던 거지요. 가지 마세요, 데이비 도련님, 가지 말아요!\"\n\n패거티의 팔이 내 목을 감는 것이 느껴졌지만, 설령 집이 무너져 내릴 참이었다 해도 나는 꼼짝할 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"오늘 아침, 날이 채 밝기도 전에, 노리치 가는 길 마을 어귀에 낯선 사륜마차하고 말들이 서 있었대요.\" 햄이 말을 이었다. \"그 종놈이 거기로 갔다가, 다시 돌아왔다가, 또 거기로 갔답니다. 마지막으로 거기 갔을 때, 에밀리가 그놈 곁에 있었고요. 다른 한 놈은 마차 안에 있었어요. 그놈이 바로 그 작자예요.\"\n\n\"하느님 맙소사.\" 패거티 씨가 뒤로 물러서며, 두려워하는 무언가를 막아 내려는 듯 손을 쑥 내밀며 말했다. \"그놈 이름이 스티어포스라고는 말하지 마라!\"\n\n\"데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 갈라진 목소리로 외쳤다. \"이건 도련님 잘못이 아니에요—저도 도련님 탓을 할 마음은 추호도 없고요—하지만 그놈 이름이 스티어포스고, 빌어먹을 악당이에요!\"\n\n패거티 씨는 비명도 지르지 않고, 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않고, 더는 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 그러다 별안간 다시 정신이 든 듯, 구석 못에 걸려 있던 자기 거친 외투를 끌어 내렸다.\n\n\"이것 좀 거들어 다오! 정신이 얼떨떨해서 혼자 못 입겠다.\" 그가 조급하게 말했다. \"손 좀 빌려 다오. 자!\" 누군가 그렇게 해 주자 그가 말했다. \"이제 저기 저 모자를 다오!\"\n\n햄이 그에게 어디로 가시려느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다. 내 에밀리를 찾으러 갈 거야. 먼저, 저기 저 배부터 박살을 내서, 만약 내가 그놈 속을 한 가닥이라도 짐작했더라면—내 산 목숨을 걸고—그놈을 빠뜨려 죽였을 바로 그 자리에 가라앉혀 버릴 거다! 그놈이 내 앞에 앉아 있을 때,\" 그가 꽉 쥔 오른손을 쑥 내밀며 사납게 말했다. \"그놈이 내 앞에 마주 앉아 있을 때, 내가 거짓말이면 그 자리에서 벼락 맞아 죽어도 좋다, 나는 그놈을 빠뜨려 죽이고도 그게 옳다고 여겼을 거다!—내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다.\"\n\n\"어디로요?\" 햄이 문 앞을 가로막아 서며 외쳤다.\n\n\"어디든! 온 세상을 다 뒤져서라도 내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다. 치욕에 빠진 내 가엾은 조카딸을 찾아내서 데려올 거야. 아무도 날 막지 마라! 분명히 말하건대, 나는 내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다!\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼요!\" 거미지 부인이 울음을 터뜨리며 두 사람 사이로 끼어들며 외쳤다. \"안 돼요, 안 돼, 대니얼, 지금 그 꼴로는 안 돼요. 좀 있다가 그 애를 찾으러 가요, 외롭고 쓸쓸한 우리 대니얼, 그래야 옳지요! 하지만 지금 그 꼴로는 안 돼요. 앉아요. 그리고 내가 그동안 당신한테 골칫거리였던 걸 용서해 줘요, 대니얼—내 그 어긋난 성미가 이 일에 비하면 다 뭐예요!—그리고 그 애가 처음 고아가 됐을 때, 햄도 그랬을 때, 그리고 내가 가난한 과부였을 때 당신이 날 거둬 준 그 시절 이야기나 한마디 나눠요. 그러면 당신의 가엾은 마음도 누그러질 거예요, 대니얼.\" 그녀가 그의 어깨에 머리를 기대며 말했다. \"그럼 당신 슬픔도 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 거예요. 당신도 그 약속을 알잖아요, 대니얼. '너희가 여기 지극히 작은 자 하나에게 한 것이 곧 내게 한 것이니라'—그 말씀은 그토록 여러 해 동안 이토록 많은 이들의 보금자리가 되어 준 이 지붕 아래서는 결코 헛되지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n이제 그는 모든 것을 내맡긴 듯 잠잠해졌다. 그가 우는 소리를 들었을 때, 내가 일으킨 이 참담함을 무릎 꿇고 빌고 스티어포스를 저주하고 싶었던 충동은 더 나은 감정에 자리를 내주었다. 미어터지던 내 가슴도 똑같은 위안을 찾아냈고, 나 또한 울었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 32. THE BEGINNING OF A LONG JOURNEY\n\n내 안에 자연스러운 것은 다른 많은 사람들 안에서도 자연스러우리라 짐작하니, 나는 두려움 없이 이렇게 쓰겠다. 나를 스티어포스와 묶어 주던 끈이 끊어진 바로 그때만큼 그를 진하게 사랑한 적이 없었다고. 그가 그럴 만한 사람이 못 된다는 걸 알아챘을 때의 그 사무치는 고통 속에서, 나는 그에게 있던 온갖 빛나는 면을 더 많이 떠올렸고, 그에게 있던 온갖 선한 면에 더 부드러워졌으며, 그를 고귀한 천성과 위대한 이름을 가진 사람으로 만들어 주었을 법한 자질들에 대해 그 어느 때보다도, 그에게 가장 헌신적으로 빠져 있던 절정의 순간보다도 더 공정한 평가를 내렸다. 정직한 한 가정을 그가 더럽히는 데 나도 모르는 사이 한몫 거들었다는 걸 뼈저리게 느꼈으면서도, 만일 그와 얼굴을 맞대는 처지가 되었더라면 나는 단 한마디 질책도 입에 올리지 못했으리라 믿는다. 나는 여전히 그를 그토록 깊이 사랑했을 테니까 — 그가 더는 나를 매혹하지 못했음에도 — 그를 향한 내 애정의 기억을 그토록 애틋하게 품고 있었을 테니까, 우리가 다시 하나가 될 수도 있으리라는 생각만 빼면, 나는 영혼에 상처 입은 어린아이처럼 한없이 나약했으리라 싶다. 그러나 그 생각만은 단 한 번도 품지 않았다. 그가 느꼈던 것처럼, 나도 우리 사이의 모든 것이 끝났음을 느꼈다. 나에 대한 그의 기억이 어떤 것이었는지 나는 끝내 알지 못했다 — 아마 가벼웠을 테고, 쉽게 떨쳐 버렸을 것이다 — 그러나 그에 대한 내 기억은, 세상을 떠난 소중한 벗을 추억하는 그것과 같았다.\n\n그래, 스티어포스, 이 가엾은 이야기의 무대에서 멀리 떠나 버린 자여! 내 슬픔은 심판의 옥좌 앞에서 본의 아니게 너를 증언할지 모르나, 내 노여운 생각이나 질책은 결코 그러지 않으리라는 걸, 나는 안다!\n\n무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 그 소식은 이내 온 동네에 퍼졌다. 어찌나 빠르던지 이튿날 아침 거리를 지나는데 사람들이 제 집 문간에서 그 이야기를 주고받는 것이 귀에 들어올 정도였다. 많은 이들은 그 처녀를 모질게 몰아세웠고, 더러 몇몇은 그 사내를 모질게 나무랐으나, 그 처녀의 두 번째 아버지와 그 처녀의 연인을 향해서는 단 하나의 감정뿐이었다. 온갖 부류의 사람들 사이에 그 두 사람의 비탄을 향한 존중이 가득했는데, 그것은 다정함과 섬세함으로 충만한 것이었다. 그 두 사람이 이른 아침에 바닷가를 느릿느릿 걷는 모습이 보이면 뱃사람들은 멀찍이 비켜섰고, 무리 지어 서서 자기들끼리 측은한 마음으로 두런두런 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n내가 두 사람을 찾아낸 곳은 바닷가, 바다와 바짝 맞닿은 곳이었다. 패거티가 미리 일러 주지 않았더라도 — 날이 훤히 밝았을 때 내가 자리를 떴을 때 그대로 두 사람이 아직 앉아 있더라고 말해 주지 않았더라도 — 그들이 간밤에 한숨도 자지 못했음을 알아채기는 쉬웠을 것이다. 둘은 지쳐 보였다. 게다가 패거티 씨의 머리는, 내가 그를 알고 지낸 그 모든 세월보다 단 하룻밤에 더 깊이 수그러든 것 같았다. 그러나 두 사람은 바다 그 자체만큼이나 진중하고 흔들림이 없었는데, 그 바다는 그때 어두운 하늘 아래 물결 한 점 없이 누워 있었으되 — 그러면서도 쉬는 와중에 숨이라도 쉬는 듯 묵직하게 너울거렸고 — 수평선 위로는 보이지 않는 해가 던진 은빛 한 줄기 빛에 어려 있었다.\n\n\"우리는 한참을 얘기했답니다, 도련님.\" 우리 셋이 한동안 말없이 걷고 난 뒤 패거티 씨가 나에게 말했다. \"뭘 해야 하고 뭘 하면 안 되는지를요. 그치만 이제 우리가 갈 길이 보입니다.\"\n\n나는 마침 햄을 흘끗 보았는데, 그는 그때 저 멀리 빛을 향해 바다를 내다보고 있었다. 그러자 끔찍한 생각이 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다 — 그의 얼굴이 노여워서가 아니라, 노여움은 거기 없었으니까. 나는 그 얼굴에서 단호한 결의의 표정 말고는 아무것도 떠올릴 수 없는데 — 만일 언젠가 그가 스티어포스와 마주친다면, 그를 죽이고 말리라는 생각이었다.\n\n\"여기서 제가 할 도리는요, 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"다 했습니다. 저는 이제 제 — 을 찾으러 갑니다.\" 그는 말을 멈췄다가, 한결 굳은 목소리로 이어 갔다. \"저는 이제 그 애를 찾으러 갑니다. 그게 앞으로 영원토록 제가 할 도리예요.\"\n\n내가 어디로 그 애를 찾으러 갈 거냐고 묻자 그는 고개를 가로저었고, 내가 내일 런던으로 가느냐고 물었다. 나는 오늘은 가지 않았다고, 그를 위해 무어라도 도움이 될 기회를 놓칠까 봐 그랬다고 말했다. 하지만 그가 갈 때면 나도 갈 채비가 되어 있다고 했다.\n\n\"도련님만 좋으시다면 함께 가지요.\" 그가 받아 말했다. \"내일요.\"\n\n우리는 또 한동안 말없이 걸었다.\n\n\"햄은요.\" 그가 이윽고 다시 말을 이었다. \"지금 하는 일을 계속하고, 제 누이랑 같이 가서 살 겁니다. 저기 저 낡은 배는—\"\n\n\"그 낡은 배를 버리실 거예요, 패거티 씨?\" 내가 부드럽게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"제 자리는요, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이제 거기 없습니다. 그리고 깊은 바다 위에 어둠이 깔린 이래 침몰한 배가 하나라도 있다면, 그 배는 가라앉은 거예요. 하지만 아니요, 도련님, 아닙니다. 그 배를 버려야 한다는 뜻은 아니에요. 그런 게 결코 아닙니다.\"\n\n우리는 아까처럼 또 한동안 걸었고, 마침내 그가 설명했다.\n\n\"제 바람은요, 도련님, 그 배가 밤이고 낮이고, 겨울이고 여름이고, 그 애가 처음 그 배를 알았던 그때 모습 그대로 늘 보였으면 하는 겁니다. 혹여라도 그 애가 헤매다 돌아온다면, 그 옛집이 그 애를 내쳤다고 여기게 하고 싶진 않거든요, 아시겠어요. 오히려 그 애가 가까이 다가오고 싶게, 어쩌면 유령처럼 비바람을 피해 낡은 창문 너머로 난롯가 옛 자리를 살짝 들여다보고 싶게 하고 싶어요. 그러면, 어쩌면, 데이비 도련님, 거기 거미지 부인 말고는 아무도 없는 걸 보고서, 그 애가 용기를 내어 떨면서 살그머니 기어들어 올지도 모르지요. 그래서 자기 옛 침대에 누워, 한때 그토록 즐거웠던 그곳에 지친 머리를 뉘고 쉬게 될지도 모르고요.\"\n\n나는 대꾸하려 애썼지만 그에게 한마디도 할 수가 없었다.\n\n\"밤마다요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"밤이 찾아오는 만큼 어김없이, 그 옛 유리창에 촛불을 세워 둬야 합니다. 혹여라도 그 애가 그걸 본다면, 그 불빛이 '돌아오너라, 내 아가, 돌아오너라!' 하고 말하는 것처럼 보이게요. 혹여라도 날이 저문 뒤에 누가 자네 고모 댁 문을 두드리거든, 햄아 (특히 살그머니 두드리거든), 자넨 거기 가까이 가지 마라. 내 가엾이 타락한 그 애를 보는 건 — 자네가 아니라 — 그 사람이라야 한다!\"\n\n그는 우리보다 조금 앞서 걷더니, 몇 분 동안 줄곧 우리 앞에 섰다. 그 사이 나는 다시 햄을 흘끗 보았는데, 그 얼굴에 똑같은 표정이 어려 있고 두 눈은 여전히 저 멀리 빛을 향한 것을 보고서, 그의 팔을 건드렸다.\n\n그가 나를 알아채기까지 나는 두 번이나 그의 이름을 불렀는데, 잠든 사람을 깨우려 할 때 쓸 법한 그런 어조였다. 마침내 무슨 생각에 그토록 마음을 쏟고 있느냐고 묻자, 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"제 앞에 놓인 것에요, 데이비 도련님. 그리고 저 너머에요.\" \"자네 앞에 놓인 인생을 말하는 건가?\" 그는 어수선하게 바다 쪽을 가리켰다.\n\n\"네, 데이비 도련님. 어찌 그런지 저도 잘 모르겠는데, 저 너머에서 무언가 오는 것 같았어요 — 그게 끝 같은 거 말이에요.\" 그가 마치 잠에서 깨어나는 사람처럼, 그러나 여전히 단호한 얼굴로 나를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 끝?\" 내가 물었다, 아까의 그 두려움에 사로잡힌 채로.\n\n\"모르겠어요.\" 그가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"이 모든 게 여기서 시작됐다는 걸 떠올리고 있었어요 — 그리고 그다음에 끝이 오는 거지요. 하지만 가 버렸어요! 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 덧붙였다. 내 생각에는, 내 표정에 답하느라 그런 것 같았다. \"저를 무서워하실 거 없어요. 그냥 제가 좀 얼떨떨해서요. 아무것도 제대로 느껴지질 않네요.\" 이는 곧, 제정신이 아니고 완전히 갈피를 못 잡고 있다는 말이나 다름없었다.\n\n패거티 씨가 합류하라고 우리를 위해 멈춰 섰고, 우리는 그렇게 했으며, 더는 말을 하지 않았다. 그러나 이 일에 대한 기억은, 아까의 그 생각과 한데 얽혀, 정해진 때에 거역할 수 없는 그 끝이 찾아오기까지 줄곧 이따금씩 나를 사로잡았다.\n\n우리는 나도 모르는 사이에 낡은 배에 다가가 안으로 들어갔다. 거미지 부인은 더 이상 늘 앉던 구석에서 풀이 죽어 있지 않고, 부지런히 아침을 차리고 있었다. 그녀는 패거티 씨의 모자를 받아 들고, 그의 자리를 마련해 주고, 어찌나 따뜻하고 부드럽게 말을 거는지, 나는 그녀를 알아보기조차 힘들었다.\n\n\"댄을, 우리 착한 양반.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"먹고 마시고, 기운을 차려야 해요, 안 그러면 아무것도 못 할 테니까. 좀 들어 봐요, 우리 귀한 양반! 그리고 내가 종알종알 떠들어서 거슬리거든,\" 그녀가 말한 것은 자기가 재잘대는 것이었다, \"그렇다고 말해 줘요, 댄을, 그럼 안 그럴게요.\"\n\n우리 모두에게 시중을 다 들고 나서, 그녀는 창가로 물러나, 패거티 씨의 셔츠 몇 벌과 다른 옷가지를 손보는 일에 정성껏 매달려, 그것들을 가지런히 접어 뱃사람들이 들고 다니는 그런 낡은 방수포 가방에 챙겨 넣었다. 그러는 내내, 그녀는 한결같이 차분한 투로 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"어느 때 어느 철이든, 알지요, 댄을.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다. \"나는 언제고 여기 있을 거고, 모든 게 당신 바람대로 돌아가게 할 거예요. 나야 배운 게 변변찮지만, 이따금 당신이 떠나 있을 때 편지를 써서 데이비 도련님 앞으로 부칠게요. 어쩌면 당신도 나한테 써 줄지 모르지요, 댄을, 이따금, 그 외롭고 쓸쓸한 길에서 어떤 심정인지를요.\"\n\n\"당신, 여기서 외톨이 신세가 될까 봐 걱정이오!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니에요, 댄을.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"그렇진 않을 거예요. 내 걱정은 마요. 나는 당신을 위해 보금자리를 지키느라 할 일이 넘칠 테니까\" (거미지 부인이 말한 것은 집이었다) \"당신이 돌아올 때까지요 — 혹여 돌아올지 모를 누구를 위해서라도 여기 보금자리를 지키느라요, 댄을. 날 좋을 때면 나는 예전처럼 문간에 나가 앉을 거예요. 누가 가까이 오거든, 그 늙은 과부가 멀찍이서부터 자기들한테 변함없는 걸 보게 될 거예요.\"\n\n짧은 시간 사이에 거미지 부인은 어찌나 달라졌는지! 그녀는 딴사람이었다. 어찌나 헌신적인지, 무슨 말을 하면 좋고 무슨 말을 안 하는 게 좋을지 어찌나 재바르게 알아채는지, 자기 자신은 어찌나 잊고 주위의 슬픔은 어찌나 살뜰히 챙기는지, 나는 그녀에게 일종의 경외감마저 품게 되었다. 그날 그녀가 해낸 일이라니! 바닷가에서 헛간으로 끌어 올려 챙겨 둬야 할 것이 많았다 — 노며, 그물이며, 돛이며, 밧줄이며, 활대며, 바닷가재 통발이며, 바닥짐 자루며, 그 비슷한 것들이. 그리고 일손이야 넘치도록 거들었으니, 그 바닷가에 패거티 씨를 위해서라면 기꺼이 힘껏 일할 사람, 그 일을 부탁받는 것만으로도 충분히 보람을 느낄 사람이 한 쌍도 빠짐없었음에도, 그녀는 온종일 자기 힘에 도무지 부치는 무거운 짐을 지고 끙끙대고, 온갖 쓸데없는 심부름으로 이리저리 부산을 떨기를 고집했다. 자기 불행을 한탄하는 일로 말하자면, 그녀는 자기에게 그런 게 있었다는 사실 자체를 까맣게 잊어버린 듯했다. 그녀는 동정심 한가운데서도 한결같은 명랑함을 잃지 않았는데, 이것이 그녀에게 일어난 변화 가운데서도 결코 덜 놀라운 대목이 아니었다. 푸념은 입에 올릴 일조차 없었다. 그날 하루 내내 황혼이 질 때까지 나는 그녀의 목소리가 떨리는 것도, 눈에서 눈물 한 방울 흐르는 것도 보지 못했다. 그러다 그녀와 나, 그리고 패거티 씨만 남게 되었을 때, 그가 완전히 기진맥진해 잠들어 버리자, 그녀는 반쯤 억누른 흐느낌과 울음을 터뜨리며, 나를 문간으로 데려가 말했다. \"부디 복 받으세요, 데이비 도련님, 저 가엾은 양반의 벗이 되어 주세요!\" 그러고는 곧장 집 밖으로 달려 나가 얼굴을 씻었는데, 그래야 조용히 그의 곁에 앉아, 그가 깨어났을 때 거기서 일하는 모습으로 발견될 수 있을 터였다. 한마디로, 그날 밤 내가 떠날 때, 나는 그녀를 패거티 씨의 비탄을 떠받치는 버팀목이자 지팡이로 남겨 두고 왔다. 나는 거미지 부인에게서 읽어 낸 그 교훈을, 그녀가 나에게 펼쳐 보여 준 그 새로운 깨달음을 아무리 곱씹어도 모자랐다.\n\n내가 우울한 걸음으로 동네를 거닐다가 오머 씨의 문 앞에 멈춰 선 것은 아홉 시에서 열 시 사이였다. 그의 딸이 일러 주기를, 오머 씨는 이 일을 어찌나 가슴 아파하는지 온종일 몹시 풀이 죽고 기운이 없어, 파이프 담배도 피우지 않은 채 잠자리에 들었다고 했다.\n\n\"속이 시커멓고 마음씨 고약한 계집애예요.\" 조램 부인이 말했다. \"애초에 글러 먹은 애였다고요!\"\n\n\"그런 말 마세요.\" 내가 받아 말했다. \"그렇게 생각하지 않으시면서.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇게 생각해요!\" 조램 부인이 성을 내며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아뇨, 아니에요.\" 내가 말했다.\n조램 부인은 어떻게든 아주 엄하고 쌀쌀맞게 굴어 보려고 고개를 홱 젖혔으나, 자신의 부드러운 본심을 다스리지 못하고 울기 시작했다. 나야 분명 어렸지만, 이 동정심 때문에 그녀를 훨씬 더 좋게 보았고, 정숙한 아내이자 어머니로서 그녀에게 그런 모습이 참으로 잘 어울린다고 생각했다.\n\n\"걔는 이제 어떡한대요!\" 미니가 흐느꼈다. \"어디로 간대요! 걔는 어떻게 되는 거예요! 아아, 어떻게 저 자신한테도 그이한테도 그렇게 모질 수가 있어요!\"\n\n나는 미니가 젊고 예쁜 아가씨였던 시절을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그녀도 그 시절을 그토록 살뜰히 기억하고 있다는 것이 반가웠다.\n\n\"우리 어린 미니는요.\" 조램 부인이 말했다. \"이제 막 겨우 잠이 들었어요. 잠결에도 에밀리를 부르며 흐느낀답니다. 우리 어린 미니가 온종일 그 애를 찾아 울면서, 에밀리가 못된 애냐고 나한테 묻고 또 물었어요. 에밀리가 여기 있던 마지막 날 밤에 제 목에 두른 리본을 풀어 우리 어린 미니 목에 둘러 주고, 아이가 곤히 잠들 때까지 베개 옆에 머리를 뉘어 줬는데, 내가 그 애한테 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요! 그 리본이 지금도 우리 어린 미니 목에 둘려 있어요. 아마 그래선 안 되겠지만, 내가 어쩌겠어요? 에밀리가 아주 못된 짓을 했어도, 둘은 서로 끔찍이 아꼈어요. 그리고 아이는 아무것도 모른답니다!\"\n\n조램 부인이 어찌나 비통해하는지 그녀의 남편이 그녀를 돌보려고 나왔다. 두 사람을 함께 두고, 나는 패거티의 집으로 돌아갔다. 가능하다면, 그때까지보다도 더 우울한 심정으로.\n\n그 착한 사람은 — 패거티를 두고 하는 말이다 — 근래의 근심과 잠 못 든 밤들로도 조금도 지치지 않은 채, 제 오라비의 집에 가 있었는데, 거기서 아침까지 머물 작정이었다. 패거티가 집안일을 돌볼 겨를이 없던 지난 몇 주 동안 일손으로 들였던 어느 노파가, 나 말고는 그 집의 유일한 식구였다. 나는 그녀의 시중이 필요하지 않았으므로, 그녀를 잠자리로 보냈는데, 그녀로서도 결코 마다할 일이 아니었다. 그러고는 한동안 부엌 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 이 모든 일을 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n나는 그것을 고인이 된 바키스 씨의 임종과 한데 뒤섞으며, 아침에 햄이 그토록 야릇하게 바라보던 그 먼 곳을 향해 밀물을 타고 떠밀려 나가고 있었는데, 그때 문 두드리는 소리에 그 헤맴에서 불려 나왔다. 문에는 노커가 달려 있었지만, 그 소리를 낸 것은 그게 아니었다. 그 똑똑 소리는 손으로 낸 것이었고, 문 아래쪽에서 났는데, 마치 어린아이가 두드린 것 같았다.\n\n그 소리는 지체 높은 분을 찾아온 하인의 노크라도 되는 양 나를 흠칫 놀라게 했다. 나는 문을 열었다. 그리고 처음에는 놀랍게도, 저 혼자 돌아다니는 듯한 커다란 우산 하나밖에 보이지 않아 시선을 아래로 떨궜다. 그러나 이내 그 밑에서 모처 양을 발견했다.\n\n만일 그녀가 아무리 안간힘을 써도 접지 못하던 그 우산을 치웠을 때, 우리가 처음이자 마지막으로 만났을 적에 나에게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼던 그 '변덕스러운' 표정을 지어 보였더라면, 나는 그 자그마한 사람을 그리 살갑게 맞이할 마음이 들지 않았을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀가 나를 향해 쳐든 얼굴은 어찌나 간절한지, 게다가 내가 그 우산을 (아일랜드 거인이 들기에도 거추장스러웠을 그것을) 대신 받아 들자, 그녀가 어찌나 비통하게 자그마한 두 손을 비틀어 짜는지, 나는 오히려 그녀 쪽으로 마음이 기울었다.\n\n\"모처 양!\" 텅 빈 거리를 위아래로 한번 흘끗거리며 — 무얼 더 보게 되리라 기대한 건지 나 자신도 분명히 알지 못한 채 — 내가 말했다. \"어떻게 여기까지 오셨어요? 무슨 일이에요?\" 그녀는 짤막한 오른팔로 나에게 우산을 접어 달라는 시늉을 했고, 황급히 나를 지나쳐 부엌으로 들어갔다. 내가 문을 닫고 우산을 손에 든 채 뒤따라가 보니, 그녀는 난로 가리개 모서리에 앉아 있었는데 — 그것은 낮은 무쇠 가리개로, 위에 접시를 올려놓을 수 있게 납작한 살 두 개가 있었다 — 보일러의 그늘 속에서 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며, 아픈 사람처럼 두 손을 무릎에 대고 비벼 대고 있었다.\n\n이 난데없는 방문을 받은 유일한 사람이자, 이 불길한 거동을 지켜보는 유일한 구경꾼이 된 것에 적잖이 놀란 나는, 다시 외쳤다. \"제발 말씀해 주세요, 모처 양, 무슨 일이에요! 어디 편찮으세요?\"\n\n\"내 귀한 젊은이.\" 모처 양이 두 손을 포개어 제 가슴을 꼭 누르며 대꾸했다. \"나는 여기가 아파요, 몹시 아프다고요. 일이 이 지경에 이르다니, 내가 그 생각 없는 바보만 아니었더라면 진작 알아채고 어쩌면 막을 수도 있었을 텐데!\"\n\n다시 그녀의 커다란 보닛이 (몸집에 견주면 영 어울리지 않게 큰 것이었다) 자그마한 몸을 이리저리 흔드는 통에 앞뒤로 까딱거렸고, 벽에는 거대하기 짝이 없는 보닛 그림자가 그것과 박자를 맞추어 흔들거렸다.\n\n\"놀랍네요.\" 내가 입을 뗐다. \"이렇게 비탄에 잠겨 진지해하시는 걸 보니—\" 그때 그녀가 내 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"그래요, 늘 그렇지요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"다들 놀라요, 이 분별없는 젊은이들은, 어엿이 다 자란 어른들이, 나 같은 자그마한 것한테 무슨 자연스러운 감정이 있는 걸 보면! 다들 나를 장난감으로 삼고, 저희 즐거움에 써먹다가, 싫증 나면 내던지고는, 내가 장난감 목마나 나무 병정보다 더 깊이 느낀다고 놀라워하지요! 그래요, 그래, 다 그런 식이에요. 늘 그래 온 그 방식 말이에요!\"\n\n\"다른 사람들은 그럴지 몰라도요.\" 내가 받아 말했다. \"분명히 말씀드리지만 저는 그렇지 않습니다. 어쩌면 지금 모처 양의 이런 모습을 보고 제가 조금도 놀라지 말았어야 했는지도 모르지요. 제가 모처 양을 너무 모르니까요. 저는 깊이 생각하지 않고, 그저 떠오른 대로 말했던 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 어쩌겠어요?\" 그 자그마한 여인이 일어서서 두 팔을 벌려 제 모습을 보이며 대꾸했다. \"보세요! 지금 내 모습, 그게 우리 아버지 모습이었고, 우리 언니 모습이고, 우리 오빠 모습이에요. 나는 이 여러 해 동안 언니와 오빠를 위해 일해 왔어요 — 고되게요, 코퍼필드 씨 — 온종일요. 나는 살아야 하니까요. 나는 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않아요. 만일 나를 두고 농지거리를 할 만큼 생각 없고 모진 사람들이 있다면, 나한테 남은 길이 나 자신을, 그들을, 세상 모든 걸 농담거리로 삼는 것 말고 또 뭐가 있겠어요? 내가 당장이야 그렇게 한다 친들, 그게 누구 잘못이에요? 내 잘못인가요?\"\n\n아니. 모처 양의 잘못이 아니라는 걸, 나는 알아챘다.\n\n\"내가 당신의 그 가짜 친구한테 감수성 예민한 난쟁이 행세를 했더라면요.\" 그 자그마한 여인이 나무라듯 진지하게 나를 향해 고개를 가로저으며 말을 이었다. \"그자한테서 도움이나 호의를 얼마나 받았을 성싶어요? 만일 어린 모처가 (이봐요 젊은이, 자기를 이렇게 만드는 데는 아무 손도 보탠 적 없는 이 모처가) 제 불행을 빌미로 그자나 그 비슷한 자한테 호소했더라면, 그 가냘픈 목소리가 언제 한 번이라도 귀에 들렸겠어요? 어린 모처가 난쟁이 가운데서도 가장 모질고 가장 무딘 난쟁이라 한들 살아야 하는 건 마찬가지지요. 하지만 그런 식으론 살 수가 없어요. 안 되지요. 굶어 죽도록 빵 한 조각 버터 한 조각 달라고 휘파람이나 불어 댈 수밖에요, 바람을 먹다 죽도록 말이에요.\"\n\n모처 양은 다시 난로 가리개에 앉아 손수건을 꺼내 눈을 닦았다.\n\n\"당신이 내 생각대로 다정한 마음을 지녔다면, 나를 위해 고마워해 줘요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 내 처지를 빤히 알면서도, 명랑할 수 있고 이 모든 걸 견딜 수 있다는 걸요. 어쨌든 나 자신을 위해서 나는 고마워해요, 누구한테도 신세 지지 않고 이 세상을 내 자그마한 방식대로 헤쳐 나갈 수 있다는 것이요. 그리고 길을 가는 동안 어리석음이나 허영에서 나한테 던져지는 그 모든 것에 대한 답례로, 나는 거품을 되던질 수 있다는 것이요. 내가 원하는 것들을 죄다 끌어안고 곱씹지 않는다면, 그건 나한테 나은 일이고, 누구한테도 나쁠 게 없어요. 내가 당신네 거인들한테 장난감이라면, 부디 나한테 다정하게 굴어 줘요.\"\n\n모처 양은 손수건을 다시 주머니에 넣고는, 내내 무척 골똘한 표정으로 나를 바라보며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"방금 거리에서 당신을 봤어요. 짐작하겠지만 나는 짧은 다리와 가쁜 숨으로는 당신만큼 빨리 걷지 못하니, 당신을 따라잡을 수가 없었지요. 하지만 당신이 어디로 가는지 짐작하고서 뒤따라왔어요. 오늘 아까도 여기 왔었는데, 그 착한 부인이 집에 없었어요.\"\n\n\"그분을 아세요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그 부인에 대해 알아요, 그 부인 사정도요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"오머랑 조램한테서요. 오늘 아침 일곱 시에 거기 있었어요. 그 여관에서 당신네 두 사람을 봤을 때, 이 불행한 처녀를 두고 스티어포스가 나한테 했던 말 기억해요?\"\n\n모처 양이 이 질문을 던지자, 그녀 머리 위의 그 커다란 보닛과 벽에 비친 더 커다란 보닛이 다시 앞뒤로 까딱거리기 시작했다.\n\n나는 그녀가 가리키는 것을 아주 잘 기억하고 있었으니, 그날 머릿속에서 여러 번 떠올렸던 터였다. 나는 그렇다고 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"온갖 악의 아비가 그자를 멸하기를.\" 그 자그마한 여인이 나와 자신의 반짝이는 두 눈 사이로 집게손가락을 곧추세우며 말했다. \"그리고 그 못된 하인놈은 열 배로 더 멸하기를. 그렇지만 나는 그 애한테 풋사랑을 품은 게 바로 당신인 줄 알았다고요!\"\n\n\"제가요?\" 내가 되물었다.\n\n\"이런, 이런! 눈먼 악운을 두고 맹세하건대.\" 모처 양이 다시 난로 가리개 위를 이리저리 오가며, 안달이 나서 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 외쳤다. \"그럼 왜 그 애를 그렇게 칭찬하고, 얼굴을 붉히고, 안절부절못하는 표정을 지었던 거예요?\"\n\n내가 그렇게 했다는 사실은, 비록 그녀의 짐작과는 전혀 다른 까닭에서였을지라도, 나 자신에게조차 숨길 수 없었다.\n\n\"내가 뭘 알았겠어요?\" 모처 양이 다시 손수건을 꺼내, 짧은 사이를 두고 양손으로 한꺼번에 눈가에 갖다 댈 때마다 바닥을 톡톡 한 번씩 구르며 말했다. \"그자가 당신을 어르고 구슬리는 게 보였어요. 그리고 당신이 그자 손안에서 물렁한 밀랍 같은 것도 보였고요. 그자의 하인이 나한테 '순진한 도련님'이 (그자가 당신을 그렇게 불렀어요. 그러니 당신은 평생토록 그자를 '늙은 죄인'이라 불러도 돼요) 그 애한테 마음을 뺐는데, 그 애가 들떠서 그를 좋아하지만, 자기 주인은 거기서 아무 탈도 안 나게 하기로 단단히 마음먹었다고 — 그 애보다 당신을 위해서요 — 그래서 그게 여기 온 용건이라고 말했을 때, 내가 그 방을 단 일 분이라도 비웠던가요? 그러니 내가 그자를 안 믿고 어쩌겠어요? 스티어포스가 그 애를 칭찬하며 당신을 달래고 흐뭇하게 해 주는 걸 봤다고요! 그 애 이름을 먼저 꺼낸 것도 당신이었어요. 당신은 오래전부터 그 애를 흠모해 왔다고 인정했고요. 내가 그 애 얘기를 꺼냈을 때 당신은 뜨거웠다 차가웠다, 빨개졌다 하얘졌다 했어요. 그러니 내가 뭘 생각할 수 있었겠어요 — 내가 실제로 뭘 생각했겠어요 — 당신이 경험만 없다 뿐이지 모든 면에서 젊은 난봉꾼이고, 경험이 차고 넘쳐 (당신한테 그런 욕망이 있으니) 당신 자신을 위해 당신을 주무를 수 있는 손아귀에 빠진 거라고 여기는 것 말고요? 아아! 아아! 아아! 그자들은 내가 진실을 알아챌까 봐 두려워했던 거예요.\" 모처 양이 난로 가리개에서 내려와, 짤막한 두 팔을 비통하게 쳐든 채 부엌을 종종걸음으로 오르내리며 외쳤다. \"내가 영악한 자그마한 것이니까요 — 이 세상을 조금이라도 헤쳐 나가려면 그래야만 하거든요! — 그래서 그자들이 나를 깡그리 속였고, 나는 그 가엾고 불행한 처녀한테 편지를 건넸어요. 그게, 나는 분명히 믿는데, 일부러 남겨졌던 그 리티머한테 그 애가 말을 트게 된 시초였던 거예요!\"\n\n이 모든 배신의 정체가 드러나자 나는 어안이 벙벙해진 채로, 숨이 찰 때까지 부엌을 오르내리는 모처 양을 바라보고 서 있었다. 이윽고 그녀는 다시 난로 가리개에 앉아, 손수건으로 얼굴을 닦으며, 다른 데는 미동도 없이, 침묵도 깨지 않고, 한참을 고개만 가로저었다.\n\n\"내가 시골을 도는 길에.\" 그녀가 마침내 덧붙였다. \"그저께 밤 노리치에 닿았어요, 코퍼필드 씨. 거기서 우연히 알게 된 것이 — 그자들이 당신도 없이 남몰래 오가는 그 방식에 대해서요, 그게 이상했거든요 — 무언가 잘못됐다는 의심으로 이어졌어요. 어젯밤 런던에서 오는 마차가 노리치를 지날 때 그걸 잡아타고, 오늘 아침 여기 온 거예요. 아아, 아아, 아아! 너무 늦었어!\"\n\n가엾은 어린 모처는 그렇게 실컷 울고 안달한 끝에 몸이 어찌나 차가워졌는지, 난로 가리개 위에서 몸을 돌려, 가엾고 자그마한 젖은 두 발을 잿더미 속에 묻어 녹이며, 커다란 인형처럼 앉아 불을 바라보았다. 나는 난롯가 맞은편 의자에 앉아, 비참한 상념에 잠긴 채, 나 또한 불을 바라보다가 이따금 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"가 봐야겠어요.\" 그녀가 마침내 말하며 일어섰다. \"늦었네요. 나를 못 믿는 건 아니지요?\"\n\n그녀가 나에게 그렇게 물을 때 여전히 날카롭던 그 날카로운 눈빛과 마주치자, 나는 그 짧은 다그침에 정말 솔직하게 아니라고 대답할 수가 없었다.\n\n\"이리 줘요!\" 그녀가 난로 가리개를 넘는 걸 도와주려고 내민 내 손을 잡으며, 그리고 내 얼굴을 애틋하게 올려다보며 말했다. \"당신도 알잖아요, 내가 멀쩡한 키의 여자였다면 나를 못 믿거나 하진 않았을 거라는 걸요!\"\n\n나는 이 말에 많은 진실이 담겨 있음을 느꼈고, 나 자신이 어쩐지 부끄러워졌다.\n\n\"당신은 젊은 사람이에요.\" 그녀가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"삼 척도 안 되는 것한테서라도 충고 한마디 새겨들어요. 몸의 결함을 마음의 결함과 결부 짓지 않도록 애써 봐요, 내 좋은 벗, 확실한 까닭이 있는 게 아니라면요.\"\n\n이제 그녀는 난로 가리개를 넘어선 참이었고, 나는 내 의심을 넘어선 참이었다. 나는 그녀가 자기 자신에 대해 솔직히 다 털어놓았다고 믿는다고, 그리고 우리 둘 다 음흉한 손아귀에 놀아난 불운한 도구였다고 말했다. 그녀는 나에게 고마워하며, 나더러 좋은 사람이라고 했다.\n\n\"자, 명심해요!\" 그녀가 문 쪽으로 가던 길을 되돌아서서, 다시 집게손가락을 쳐든 채 나를 빈틈없이 바라보며 외쳤다. \"내가 들은 바로 미루어 의심할 만한 까닭이 좀 있어요 — 내 귀는 늘 열려 있거든요, 나는 가진 능력을 아껴 둘 처지가 못 되니까요 — 그자들이 외국으로 갔다는 거예요. 하지만 혹여라도 그들이 돌아온다면, 혹여라도 그중 누구 하나라도 내가 살아 있는 동안 돌아온다면, 나처럼 이리저리 돌아다니는 사람이라면 누구보다도 그걸 빨리 알아챌 가능성이 높아요. 내가 무얼 알게 되든, 당신도 알게 될 거예요. 혹여라도 내가 그 가엾고 배신당한 처녀를 위해 무어라도 할 수 있다면, 하늘이 허락하는 한 충실히 하겠어요! 그리고 리티머로 말하자면, 그자는 어린 모처를 뒤에 두느니 차라리 사냥개를 뒤에 두는 편이 나을 거예요!\"\n\n이 마지막 말에 곁들여진 그 눈빛을 보았을 때, 나는 그 말을 한 치의 의심도 없이 믿었다.\n\n\"멀쩡한 키의 여자를 믿을 만큼만 나를 믿어 줘요, 그보다 더도 말고 덜도 말고요.\" 그 자그마한 사람이 호소하듯 내 손목을 건드리며 말했다. \"혹여라도 당신이 나를 다시 보게 되는데, 그게 지금의 내 모습과는 다르고 당신이 나를 처음 봤을 때의 모습 같거든, 내가 어떤 무리에 끼어 있는지 잘 봐요. 내가 아주 무력하고 무방비한 자그마한 것이라는 걸 떠올려 줘요. 하루 일을 마치고 나처럼 자그마한 오빠와 나처럼 자그마한 언니와 함께 집에 있는 나를 생각해 줘요. 그러면 아마 당신도 나를 그리 모질게 대하지 않을 테고, 내가 비탄에 잠겨 진지해할 수 있다는 데 놀라지도 않겠지요. 잘 자요!\"\n\n나는 지금껏 품어 온 것과는 사뭇 다른 마음으로 모처 양에게 손을 내밀어 주고는, 그녀를 내보내려고 문을 열었다. 그 커다란 우산을 펴서 그녀의 손아귀에 제대로 균형을 잡아 들리게 하는 것은 결코 사소한 일이 아니었다. 하지만 마침내 나는 그 일을 성공적으로 해냈고, 그 우산이 빗속에서 거리를 따라 까딱까딱 멀어져 가는 것을 지켜보았는데, 그 밑에 누가 있다는 기색이라곤 조금도 없었다 — 다만 어느 넘쳐 나는 빗물받이에서 평소보다 더 거센 물줄기가 쏟아져 그 우산을 한쪽으로 휘청 넘어뜨려, 그것을 바로 세우려고 사납게 안간힘을 쓰는 모처 양이 드러날 때만 빼고는. 내가 그녀를 거들려고 한두 번 달려 나갔으나, 내가 미처 닿기도 전에 그 우산이 거대한 새처럼 다시 폴짝 뛰어 달아나는 바람에 헛수고가 되었고, 나는 안으로 들어와 잠자리에 들어 아침까지 잤다.\n\n아침에 패거티 씨와 내 옛 유모가 나와 합류했고, 우리는 이른 시각에 마차 사무소로 갔는데, 거기서 거미지 부인과 햄이 우리를 배웅하려고 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 짐 사이에 자기 가방을 챙겨 넣는 사이, 햄이 나를 한쪽으로 끌며 속삭였다. \"삼촌은 인생이 완전히 무너져 버렸어요. 어디로 가는지도 모르고, 앞에 뭐가 있는지도 몰라요. 삼촌은 이제 길을 떠나는데, 찾고 있는 그 애를 찾지 못한다면, 제 말 믿으세요, 그 길은 끊겼다 이어졌다 하면서 남은 평생토록 이어질 거예요. 도련님이 삼촌의 벗이 되어 주실 거지요, 데이비 도련님?\"\n\n\"믿어 주게, 정말 그러겠네.\" 내가 햄과 진심으로 악수하며 말했다.\n\n\"고마워요. 정말 고맙습니다, 인정 많으세요, 도련님. 한 가지만 더요. 도련님도 아시다시피 저는 일자리가 좋고, 이제 버는 걸 쓸 데가 없어요. 돈은 이제 사는 데 말고는 저한테 아무 쓸모가 없거든요. 그걸 삼촌을 위해 좀 써 주실 수 있다면, 저는 더 신명 나게 일할 거예요. 그렇다고 도련님, \" 그가 아주 차분하고 부드럽게 말했다, \"제가 언제든 사내답게 일하지 않거나, 제 힘이 닿는 한 최선을 다하지 않을 거라고는 생각지 마세요!\"\n\n나는 그 점을 충분히 믿는다고 그에게 말했다. 그리고 그가 지금 으레 그러려니 여기는 그 외로운 삶을 그만두게 될 날이 언젠가 오기를 바란다고 넌지시 비쳤다.\n\n\"아뇨, 도련님.\" 그가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그런 건 다 지나갔어요, 끝났습니다, 도련님. 텅 빈 그 자리를 채워 줄 사람은 누구도 없어요. 그치만 돈 얘기는 마음에 새겨 두세요, 늘 삼촌을 위해 따로 떼어 둘 게 좀 있을 테니까요?\"\n\n패거티 씨가 고인이 된 매부의 유산에서 비록 매우 적은 액수일망정 꾸준한 수입을 받고 있다는 사실을 그에게 일깨워 주며, 나는 그러겠노라 약속했다. 그러고서 우리는 서로 작별했다. 나는 지금도 그를, 그 겸손한 꿋꿋함과 그 크나큰 슬픔을 가슴 저미는 마음 없이는 떠올릴 수가 없다.\n\n거미지 부인으로 말하자면, 만일 내가 그녀가 어떻게 마차 옆을 따라 거리를 달려 내려갔는지를 그려 내려 한다면 — 억누르려 애쓰는 눈물 너머로 마차 지붕 위의 패거티 씨밖에는 아무것도 보이지 않은 채, 맞은편에서 오는 사람들에게 자기 몸을 부딪쳐 가며 — 자못 어려운 일에 발을 들이는 셈이 되리라. 그러니 나는 그녀를 어느 빵집 문간 계단에 앉혀 둔 채 떠나는 편이 낫겠다, 숨을 헐떡이며, 보닛은 형체라곤 하나도 남지 않고, 신발 한 짝은 벗겨져 저만치 떨어진 포도(鋪道) 위에 나뒹구는 채로.\n\n여정의 끝에 다다랐을 때, 우리가 맨 먼저 한 일은 패거티가 묵을 작은 셋방을 알아보는 것이었는데, 그녀의 오라비가 잠잘 침대를 둘 만한 곳이라야 했다. 우리는 운 좋게도 그런 곳을 하나 찾아냈으니, 아주 깨끗하고 값싼 곳으로, 잡화점 위층이었고, 내 집에서 겨우 두 골목 떨어진 데였다. 이 거처를 잡고 나서, 나는 음식점에서 차가운 고기를 좀 사다가, 내 길동무들을 집으로 데려와 차를 들었다. 유감스럽게도 이 일은 크럽 부인의 마음에 들지 않았는데, 그러기는커녕 정반대였다. 다만 그 부인의 심사를 설명하자면 이 점은 짚어 두어야겠다. 그녀는 패거티가 여기 온 지 십 분도 채 안 되어 과부의 치맛자락을 걷어붙이고 내 침실을 먼지 터는 일에 나선 것에 몹시 기분이 상했던 것이다. 크럽 부인은 이를 주제넘은 짓으로 여겼고, 주제넘은 짓이란 자기가 결코 용납하지 않는 것이라고 말했다.\n\n패거티 씨는 런던으로 오는 길에 나에게 한 가지를 일러 주었는데, 나로서는 마음의 준비가 안 된 것은 아니었다. 그것은 그가 먼저 스티어포스 부인을 만나 볼 작정이라는 거였다. 나는 이 일에서 그를 거들고, 또 두 사람 사이를 중재할 의무가 있다고 느꼈으므로 — 그 어머니의 심정을 되도록 다치지 않게 하려는 생각에서 — 그날 밤 그녀에게 편지를 썼다. 나는 되도록 부드럽게 그가 입은 부당한 일이 무엇이고, 그가 받은 상처에 내가 무슨 몫을 했는지를 말했다. 나는 그가 아주 평범한 처지의 사람이지만, 더없이 온화하고 강직한 성품을 지녔다고 했다. 그리고 외람되나마, 그가 이 무거운 곤경에 처한 지금, 부인이 그를 만나기를 거절하지 않으시기를 바란다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 우리가 갈 시각으로 오후 두 시를 일러두고, 나는 아침 첫 마차 편으로 그 편지를 직접 부쳤다.\n\n정해진 시각에, 우리는 그 문 앞에 섰다 — 며칠 전에 내가 그토록 행복했던 그 집의 문, 내 젊은 신뢰와 따뜻한 마음을 그토록 거리낌 없이 내어 주었던 곳, 이제부터는 나에게 닫혀 버린 곳, 이제는 황량한 폐허가 된 곳의 문이었다.\n\n리티머는 나타나지 않았다. 지난번 내 방문 때 그를 대신했던 그 한결 상냥한 얼굴이 우리 부름에 응하여, 우리를 앞장서 응접실로 안내했다. 스티어포스 부인이 거기 앉아 있었다. 우리가 들어서자 로자 다틀이 방의 다른 쪽에서 미끄러지듯 다가와 그녀의 의자 뒤에 섰다.\n\n나는 그 어머니의 얼굴에서 곧바로, 그녀가 그가 한 일을 그의 입을 통해 알고 있음을 알아챘다. 그 얼굴은 몹시 창백했고, 내 편지 하나만으로는 — 그것도 그녀의 애정이 거기 일으켰을 의심에 힘이 빠진 그 편지만으로는 — 자아낼 법하지 않은 깊은 동요의 흔적이 어려 있었다. 나는 그녀가 그 어느 때보다도 그를 닮았다고 생각했다. 그리고 그 닮음이 내 길동무에게도 놓치지 않고 보였다는 것을, 나는 보았다기보다 느꼈다.\n\n그녀는 안락의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아, 어떤 것도 흔들 수 없을 듯한, 위엄 있고 미동도 없는 무감한 기색이었다. 패거티 씨가 그녀 앞에 섰을 때 그녀는 그를 아주 또렷이 바라보았고, 그 또한 못지않게 또렷이 그녀를 바라보았다. 로자 다틀의 날카로운 눈길은 우리 모두를 한눈에 담았다. 한동안 단 한마디도 오가지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 패거티 씨에게 앉으라는 손짓을 했다. 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이 집에서 앉는 건 제 마음에 영 자연스럽지가 않습니다, 마님. 차라리 서 있겠어요.\" 그러자 또 한 차례 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그녀가 그것을 이렇게 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"무엇이 당신을 이리로 데려왔는지, 나는 깊은 유감 속에 알고 있어요. 나에게서 무엇을 바라나요? 나더러 무엇을 하라는 거예요?\"\n\n그는 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 가슴팍을 더듬어 에밀리의 편지를 찾아 꺼내, 그것을 펼쳐 그녀에게 건넸다. \"그걸 읽어 보세요, 마님. 그게 제 조카딸의 글씨예요!\"\n\n그녀는 똑같이 위엄 있고 무덤덤한 태도로 그것을 읽었다 — 내가 보기에는, 그 내용에 조금도 마음 흔들리는 기색 없이 — 그러고는 그것을 그에게 돌려주었다.\n\n\"'그이가 저를 숙녀로 만들어 데려오지 않는 한.'\" 패거티 씨가 손가락으로 그 대목을 짚어 가며 말했다. \"저는 그걸 알아보려고 왔습니다, 마님, 그이가 자기 말을 지킬지를요.\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 안 되지요?\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"불가능하니까요. 그 애는 제 체면을 망치게 될 거예요. 그 처녀가 그 애보다 한참 아래라는 걸 모르실 리 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"그 애를 끌어올려 주세요!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그 처녀는 못 배웠고 무지해요.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도, 안 그럴 수도 있지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"저는 안 그렇다고 봅니다, 마님. 하지만 저야 그런 걸 가릴 줄 모르지요. 그 애를 가르쳐 주세요!\"\n\n\"당신이 나로 하여금 더 분명히 말하게 만드는군요, 나로서는 무척 내키지 않는 일인데. 다른 게 아무것도 걸리지 않는다 해도, 그 처녀의 미천한 집안 내력만으로도 그런 일은 불가능해요.\"\n\n\"이 말 좀 들어 보세요, 마님.\" 그가 천천히, 그리고 조용히 받아 말했다. \"자식을 사랑한다는 게 어떤 건지 마님은 아십니다. 저도 그래요. 그 애가 백 배로 제 자식이라 해도, 지금보다 더 사랑할 순 없을 거예요. 마님은 자식을 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지 모르세요. 저는 알아요. 세상의 온갖 재물을 산더미로 쌓아 놓는다 해도 (그게 제 거라 한들) 그 애를 되사 올 수 있다면 저한테는 아무것도 아닐 겁니다! 하지만 그 애를 이 치욕에서 구해만 주신다면, 그 애는 결코 우리 때문에 욕을 보는 일이 없을 거예요. 그 애가 자라난 우리 가운데 단 한 사람도, 그 애와 함께 살며 이 여러 해 그 애를 전부로 여겨 온 우리 가운데 단 한 사람도, 다시는 그 애의 예쁜 얼굴을 보지 않을 거예요. 우리는 그 애를 그냥 내버려 두는 데 만족하겠어요. 그 애를 멀리, 마치 다른 해와 다른 하늘 아래 있는 것처럼 여기는 데 만족하겠어요. 그 애를 그 애 남편한테 — 어쩌면 그 애의 어린 자식들한테 — 맡기고, 우리 모두가 하느님 앞에서 신분이 다 똑같아질 그때를 기다리는 데 만족하겠어요!\"\n\n그가 말한 그 투박한 웅변은 아무런 효력이 없지는 않았다. 그녀는 여전히 그 거만한 태도를 지켰으나, 대답할 때 그 목소리에는 한 가닥 부드러움이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n\"나는 아무것도 옳다 하지 않아요. 맞고소를 하지도 않고요. 하지만 거듭 말하게 되어 유감스럽지만, 그건 불가능해요. 그런 결혼은 내 아들의 앞길을 돌이킬 수 없이 망치고, 그 애의 장래를 무너뜨릴 거예요. 그런 일이 결코 일어날 수 없고, 결코 일어나지 않으리라는 것보다 더 확실한 건 없어요. 만일 다른 어떤 보상이라도—\"\n\n\"저는 그 얼굴을 빼닮은 모습을 보고 있습니다.\" 패거티 씨가 흔들림 없으나 점점 타오르는 눈으로 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"제 집에서, 제 난롯가에서, 제 배에서 — 어딘들 안 그랬겠어요 — 저를 바라보던 그 얼굴 말입니다. 그토록 음흉했으면서도 웃으며 다정하던 그 얼굴, 떠올리기만 해도 제가 반쯤 미쳐 버리는 그 얼굴이요. 그 빼닮은 얼굴이, 제 자식을 망치고 무너뜨린 값으로 저한테 돈을 내미는 그 생각에 불길로 타오르지 않는다면, 그건 그것대로 못된 거예요. 잘은 모르겠습니다만, 귀부인의 얼굴이고 보니, 그게 차라리 더 못된 건지도 모르지요.\"\n\n그녀는 이제 한순간에 달라졌다. 노여운 홍조가 그녀의 이목구비를 뒤덮었다. 그리고 그녀는 두 손으로 안락의자를 꽉 움켜쥔 채, 못 참겠다는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"나와 내 아들 사이에 그런 구렁을 벌려 놓은 일에 대해, 당신이 나한테 무슨 보상을 할 수 있다는 거죠? 당신의 사랑이 내 사랑에 견줄 게 무엇이에요? 당신의 이별이 우리의 이별에 견줄 게 무엇이냐고요?\"\n\n다틀 양이 그녀를 살며시 건드리며 무어라 귀엣말을 하려고 고개를 숙였으나, 그녀는 단 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"안 돼, 로자, 한마디도! 이 사람더러 내가 하는 말을 듣게 해! 내 아들, 내 삶의 목표였던 그 애, 내 모든 생각을 다 바쳐 온 그 애, 어릴 적부터 무슨 소원이든 다 이루어 주었던 그 애, 태어난 이래로 나와 따로 떨어진 존재라곤 없던 그 애가 — 한순간에 어느 비참한 계집애한테 빠져서 나를 피하다니! 그 애를 위해 빈틈없이 속임수를 써서 내 신뢰를 갚고, 그 애 때문에 나를 버리다니! 이 한심한 변덕을, 의무며 사랑이며 존경이며 감사에 대한 어미의 권리에 — 그 애 삶의 하루하루, 한시 한시가 어떤 것도 깨뜨릴 수 없는 끈으로 더 단단히 다져 왔어야 마땅한 그 권리에 맞세우다니! 이게 상처가 아니란 말이에요?\"\n\n다시 로자 다틀이 그녀를 달래려 했으나, 다시 헛일이었다.\n\n\"내 말했지, 로자, 한마디도! 그 애가 더없이 하찮은 것에 제 전부를 걸 수 있다면, 나는 더 큰 목적에 내 전부를 걸 수 있어. 내 사랑이 그 애한테 마련해 준 그 수단을 가지고 어디로든 가라지! 그 애가 오래 떨어져 있으면 나를 꺾을 수 있다고 생각하는 건가? 그렇다면 제 어미를 너무 모르는 거지. 지금 그 변덕을 치워 버린다면, 나는 그 애를 기꺼이 다시 맞을 거야. 지금 그 계집을 치워 버리지 않는다면, 살아서든 죽어서든, 내가 손을 들어 거부의 표시를 할 수 있는 한, 그 애는 결코 내 곁에 올 수 없어 — 그 계집을 영영 떼어 내고 겸손히 내게 와서 용서를 빌지 않는 한은. 이게 내 권리야. 이게 내가 반드시 받아 낼 인정이고. 이게 우리 사이에 있는 이별이야! 그래, 이게,\" 그녀가 처음에 그랬던 그 거만하고 못 참겠다는 기색으로 손님을 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"상처가 아니란 말이에요?\"\n\n그 어머니가 이 말들을 내뱉는 것을 듣고 보는 동안, 나는 그 말들에 맞서던 그 아들을 듣고 보는 것만 같았다. 내가 그에게서 본 적 있는 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 고집스러운 기질을, 나는 죄다 그녀에게서 보았다. 그릇된 데로 쏟아부어진 그의 정력을 이제껏 내가 이해한 그 모든 것이, 그녀의 성격에 대한 이해이기도 하다는 것을, 그리고 그것이 가장 깊은 근원에서는 똑같은 것임을, 나는 깨달았다.\n\n그녀는 이제, 다시 아까의 절제된 태도로 돌아가, 더 듣거나 더 말해 봐야 소용없으니 이 면담을 끝내고 싶다고 나에게 소리 내어 말했다. 그녀가 위엄 있는 태도로 방을 나서려고 몸을 일으키는데, 패거티 씨가 그럴 필요 없다는 뜻을 비쳤다.\n\n\"제가 마님께 무슨 방해가 될까 봐 걱정하실 거 없습니다, 저는 더 할 말이 없으니까요, 마님.\" 그가 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮기며 말했다. \"저는 아무 희망도 없이 여기 왔고, 아무 희망도 없이 갑니다. 저는 마땅히 해야겠다 싶은 걸 했을 뿐, 제가 선 이 자리에서 무슨 좋은 게 나오리라고는 결코 바라지 않았어요. 여긴 저와 제 식구한테 너무도 사악한 집이라서요, 제정신으로 그런 걸 바랄 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 우리는 떠났다. 그녀를, 고귀한 풍채와 아름다운 얼굴을 한 한 폭의 그림처럼 자기 안락의자 곁에 선 채로 남겨 두고서.\n\n나가는 길에 우리는 유리 벽과 유리 지붕으로 된, 포도(鋪道)가 깔린 복도를 가로질러야 했는데, 그 위로는 포도덩굴이 뻗어 있었다. 그 잎과 새순은 그때 푸르렀고, 날이 화창한 까닭에 정원으로 통하는 유리문 한 쌍이 활짝 열려 있었다. 우리가 그 문에 바짝 다가섰을 때, 로자 다틀이 소리 없는 걸음으로 이쪽으로 들어와, 나에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"잘하셨네요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"정말이지, 이런 작자를 여기 데려오다니!\"\n\n분노와 경멸이 어찌나 응축되어 그녀의 얼굴을 어둡게 하고 칠흑 같은 두 눈에서 번득이는지, 나는 그 얼굴에 그토록 많은 것이 압축될 수 있으리라고는 생각조차 못 했을 것이다. 망치에 맞아 생긴 그 흉터는, 그녀의 이목구비가 이렇게 격앙된 상태에서 늘 그렇듯, 도드라지게 드러나 있었다. 내가 그녀를 바라보는데 전에 본 적 있는 그 욱신거림이 거기 떠오르자, 그녀는 영락없이 손을 쳐들어 그것을 후려쳤다.\n\n\"이런 작자를 두둔하고 여기로 데려오다니, 안 그래요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 참 의리 있는 사람이군요!\"\n\n\"다틀 양.\" 내가 받아 말했다. \"설마 저를 탓할 만큼 불공평하시진 않겠지요!\"\n\n\"왜 이 미쳐 날뛰는 두 인간 사이에 분란을 끌어들이는 거죠?\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"저 둘이 다 제 고집과 자존심으로 미쳐 있는 걸 모르세요?\"\n\n\"이게 제가 한 일이라고요?\" 내가 받아 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 한 일이냐고요!\" 그녀가 쏘아붙였다. \"그럼 왜 이 작자를 여기 데려오는 건데요?\"\n\n\"이분은 깊이 상처 입은 사람입니다, 다틀 양.\" 내가 대답했다. \"당신은 모르실지 몰라도요.\"\n\n\"제임스 스티어포스가,\" 그녀가 가슴속에서 휘몰아치는 폭풍이 소리 내지 못하게 막으려는 듯 가슴에 손을 얹고 말했다. \"거짓되고 썩어 빠진 마음을 지닌 배신자라는 건 나도 알아요. 하지만 이 작자랑 이 작자의 천한 조카딸 따위를 내가 알 까닭이, 신경 쓸 까닭이 뭐가 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"다틀 양.\" 내가 받아 말했다. \"당신은 상처를 더 깊게 하고 있어요. 이미 충분합니다. 떠나면서 이 말만은 하겠어요, 당신은 이분에게 큰 잘못을 저지르고 있다고요.\"\n\n\"나는 이 작자한테 아무 잘못도 안 해요.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"저들은 타락하고 쓸모없는 부류예요. 나라면 저 계집을 채찍질했을 거라고요!\"\n\n패거티 씨는 한마디도 없이 지나쳐 문밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"아, 부끄러운 줄 아세요, 다틀 양! 부끄러운 줄 아세요!\" 내가 분개하며 말했다. \"어떻게 까닭 없이 고통받는 이분을 짓밟고도 견딜 수가 있어요!\"\n\n\"나라면 저들을 죄다 짓밟아 줬을 거예요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"나라면 그 집을 헐어 버렸을 거라고요. 나라면 그 계집의 얼굴에 낙인을 찍고, 누더기를 입혀, 거리로 내쫓아 굶겨 죽였을 거예요. 내게 그 계집을 심판할 권한이 있다면, 나는 기어이 그렇게 되는 걸 봤을 거예요. 보기만 하겠어요? 내 손으로 했을 거라고요! 나는 그 계집이 진저리 나게 싫어요. 그 계집의 그 수치스러운 처지를 두고 한번 나무랄 수만 있다면, 나는 그러러 어디든 갈 거예요. 그 계집을 무덤까지 쫓을 수 있다면, 나는 그러겠어요. 죽어 가는 순간에 그 계집에게 위안이 될 말 한마디가 있고 그걸 오직 나만 가졌다면, 나는 목숨을 준다 해도 그걸 내놓지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n그 말의 격렬함만으로는, 나도 잘 알거니와, 그녀를 사로잡은 그 격정을 그저 희미하게나마 전할 수 있을 뿐이다. 비록 그 목소리는 높아지기는커녕 평소보다 낮았으나, 그 격정은 그녀의 온몸을 통해 또렷이 드러나고 있었다. 내가 그녀를 어떻게 묘사한들 그녀에 대한 내 기억에도, 노여움에 자기 자신을 송두리째 내맡긴 그 모습에도 온당히 값하지 못할 것이다. 나는 여러 형태의 격정을 보아 왔으나, 그런 형태의 격정은 단 한 번도 본 적이 없다.\n\n내가 패거티 씨와 다시 합류했을 때, 그는 언덕을 천천히, 생각에 잠겨 내려가고 있었다. 그는 내가 따라붙자마자, 런던에서 하려고 마음먹었던 일을 이제 다 덜어 냈으니, 그날 밤 '길을 떠날' 작정이라고 나에게 말했다. 나는 어디로 갈 생각이냐고 물었다. 그는 그저 이렇게만 대답했다. \"저는요, 도련님, 제 조카딸을 찾으러 갑니다.\"\n\n우리는 잡화점 위층 그 작은 셋방으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 나는 그가 나에게 한 말을 패거티에게 그대로 전할 기회를 얻었다. 그러자 그녀는, 그가 그날 아침 자기에게도 똑같이 말했다고 일러 주었다. 그녀도 그가 어디로 가는지는 나만큼이나 알지 못했으나, 그가 마음속에 무언가 꾸려 놓은 계획이 있는 것 같다고 했다.\n\n나는 이런 처지의 그를 두고 떠나기가 내키지 않았다. 그래서 우리 셋은 비프스테이크 파이를 놓고 함께 저녁을 들었는데 — 그것은 패거티가 솜씨 좋기로 이름난 여러 음식 가운데 하나였다 — 이번만큼은, 내 또렷이 기억하건대, 가게에서 끊임없이 피어오르는 차며 커피며 버터며 베이컨이며 치즈며 갓 구운 빵이며 장작이며 양초며 호두 케첩의 잡다한 냄새가 묘하게 그 맛에 배어들어 있었다. 저녁을 먹고 나서 우리는 한 시간 남짓 창가에 앉아, 별말 없이 있었다. 그러고는 패거티 씨가 일어나, 자기 방수포 가방과 튼튼한 지팡이를 가져와 탁자 위에 놓았다.\n\n그는 누이가 가진 현금에서 제 유산의 일부로 적은 액수를 받았는데, 내 생각으로는 한 달을 버티기에도 빠듯한 정도였다. 그는 무슨 일이 생기면 나에게 연락하겠노라 약속했다. 그러고는 가방을 어깨에 둘러메고, 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들고, 우리 둘에게 '잘 있게나!' 하고 작별을 고했다.\n\n\"온갖 복이 당신을 따르기를, 우리 정다운 누이.\" 그가 패거티를 끌어안으며 말했다. \"도련님도요!\" 나와 악수하며 말했다. \"저는 그 애를 찾으러 갑니다, 멀리멀리요. 제가 떠나 있는 동안 그 애가 집으로 돌아온다면 — 하지만 아아, 그럴 것 같진 않지만! — 아니면 제가 그 애를 데려온다면, 제 뜻은요, 그 애와 제가 아무도 그 애를 나무랄 수 없는 곳에서 살다 죽자는 겁니다. 혹여라도 저한테 무슨 변고가 생기거든, 제가 그 애한테 남긴 마지막 말이 이거였다는 걸 기억해 주세요. '내 변함없는 사랑은 내 사랑하는 아이와 함께 있으며, 나는 그 애를 용서한다!'\"\n\n그는 모자를 벗은 채 이 말을 엄숙하게 했다. 그러고는 모자를 쓰고, 계단을 내려가, 떠나갔다. 우리는 문까지 뒤따라갔다. 따뜻하고 먼지 자욱한 저녁이었는데, 마침 그 샛길이 갈라져 나온 큰길에서 포도(鋪道) 위를 끊임없이 밟아 대던 발소리가 잠시 멎고, 붉은 햇살이 강하게 내리쬐던 그때였다. 그는 홀로, 우리 그늘진 거리 모퉁이에서 돌아, 환한 빛무리 속으로 들어섰고, 우리는 그 안에서 그를 놓쳤다.\n\n그 저녁 시각이 찾아올 때면, 밤에 잠이 깰 때면, 달이나 별을 올려다볼 때면, 흩날리는 빗줄기를 바라보거나 바람 소리를 들을 때면, 나는 어김없이 묵묵히 길을 가는 그 외로운 모습을, 그 가엾은 순례자를 떠올렸고, 그 말들을 되새기곤 했다.\n\n\"저는 그 애를 찾으러 갑니다, 멀리멀리요. 혹여라도 저한테 무슨 변고가 생기거든, 제가 그 애한테 남긴 마지막 말이 이거였다는 걸 기억해 주세요. '내 변함없는 사랑은 내 사랑하는 아이와 함께 있으며, 나는 그 애를 용서한다!'\"\n\n# CHAPTER 33. BLISSFUL\n\n그동안 나는 줄곧 도라를 사랑해 왔는데, 그 사랑은 어느 때보다도 더 격렬했다. 도라를 떠올리는 것이 실망과 괴로움 속에서 나의 피난처였고, 친구를 잃은 슬픔까지도 어느 정도 달래 주었다. 나 자신이 가엾게 느껴질수록, 또는 남들이 가엾게 느껴질수록, 나는 도라의 모습에서 위안을 더욱 구했다. 세상에 거짓과 근심이 쌓일수록, 그 세상 높은 곳에서 도라라는 별은 더 밝고 더 맑게 빛났다. 도라가 어디서 왔는지, 또 더 높은 차원의 존재들과 얼마나 가까운 사이인지 내가 무슨 분명한 생각을 품었던 것 같지는 않다. 하지만 도라가 그저 여느 아가씨와 다를 바 없는 한낱 인간일 뿐이라는 말을 누가 했다면, 나는 분개와 경멸로 그 생각을 단번에 내쳤을 것이 분명하다.\n\n이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 나는 도라에 흠뻑 절어 있었다. 그저 머리끝까지 사랑에 빠진 정도가 아니라, 속속들이 흠뻑 배어들어 있었다. 비유로 말하자면, 나에게서 사랑을 짜내면 누구라도 빠뜨려 죽일 만큼 쏟아져 나왔을 것이고, 그러고도 내 안에는, 그리고 내 온몸에는, 나의 존재 전체를 채우고도 남을 만큼 사랑이 남아 있었을 것이다.\n\n런던에 돌아와서 내 마음대로 한 첫 일은, 밤에 노우드까지 걸어가, 어린 시절 들었던 그 점잖은 수수께끼의 주인공처럼, '집을 한 번도 건드리지 않으면서 집 둘레를 빙빙 도는' 것이었다. 물론 도라를 생각하면서 말이다. 이 알쏭달쏭한 수수께끼의 답은 달이었던 것 같다. 그게 무엇이었든 간에, 도라에게 홀린 노예인 나는, 두 시간 동안 집과 정원 둘레를 빙빙 돌며, 울타리 틈으로 안을 들여다보고, 안간힘을 써 가며 울타리 꼭대기의 녹슨 못 위로 턱을 간신히 올리고, 창문의 불빛에 입맞춤을 날리고, 이따금 낭만에 젖어 밤을 향해 내 도라를 지켜 달라고 외쳤다 — 무엇으로부터 지켜 달라는 건지는 나도 잘 모르겠는데, 아마 불이었지 싶다. 어쩌면 쥐였을지도 모른다. 도라가 쥐라면 끔찍이 싫어했으니까.\n\n내 사랑은 마음속에 가득했고, 어느 날 저녁 패거티가 옛날 그 바느질 도구 한 벌을 들고 다시 내 곁에 앉아 내 옷가지를 바삐 손보고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 패거티에게 속내를 털어놓는 것은 너무도 자연스러운 일이라, 나는 빙빙 둘러대는 식으로 충분히 에둘러서 나의 큰 비밀을 그녀에게 알렸다. 패거티는 무척 관심을 보였으나, 도무지 나처럼 이 일을 바라보게 만들 수가 없었다. 패거티는 뻔뻔스러울 만큼 내 편으로 치우쳐 있어서, 내가 왜 무슨 불안을 느끼거나 이 일로 풀이 죽어 있어야 하는지 도통 이해하지 못했다. \"그 아가씨야말로 운이 좋다 여겨야지요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"이런 멋진 신랑감을 두다니요. 그리고 그 아가씨 아버지로 말하자면,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"아니, 도련님, 그 양반이 대체 뭘 더 바란답니까!\"\n\n그렇지만 나는 알아챘다. 스펜로 씨의 법무사 가운과 빳빳한 크라바트가 패거티의 기를 조금 꺾어 놓았고, 날이 갈수록 내 눈에 점점 더 신성한 존재로 변해 가던 그 사람에 대한 더 큰 경외심을 패거티에게 불어넣었다는 것을. 그 사람이 법정에서 서류 더미 사이에 꼿꼿이 앉아 있을 때면, 문방구의 바다 위에 떠 있는 작은 등대처럼, 반사된 광채가 그를 둘러싸고 환히 빛나는 것만 같았다. 그러고 보니, 나 역시 법정에 앉아 있을 때면, 저 침침하고 늙은 판사들과 박사들이, 도라를 알았더라면 도라 따위에는 눈길조차 주지 않았으리라는 것이 — 도라와 결혼하라는 제안을 받았더라도 황홀해서 넋이 나갈 일은 결코 없었으리라는 것이 — 도라가 노래를 부르고 그 거룩한 기타를 켜며 나를 미칠 지경까지 몰아간다 해도, 저 굼뜬 작자들 중 단 한 명도 제 길에서 한 치도 꾀어내지 못했으리라는 것이, 어찌나 유난히 이상하게 느껴졌던지 기억이 난다.\n\n나는 그들을 한 사람도 빠짐없이 경멸했다. 마음이라는 꽃밭에서 얼어 죽은 늙은 정원사들이라 여기며, 나는 그들 모두에게 사사로운 반감을 품었다. 내게 판사석은 한낱 무감각한 얼간이에 지나지 않았다. 변호사석에는 선술집 카운터보다도 다정함이나 시정(詩情)이 없었다.\n\n나는 적잖은 자부심을 품고 패거티의 일을 내 손으로 도맡아, 유언장을 검인받고, 상속세 사무소와 합의를 보고, 패거티를 은행에 데려가, 머지않아 모든 일을 순조로운 궤도에 올려놓았다. 우리는 이런 법률 일의 딱딱함을, 플리트 거리에서 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리는 듯한 밀랍 인형 전시를 보러 가는 것으로 누그러뜨렸고 (그것들은 이 이십 년 사이에 녹아 없어졌기를 바란다), 린우드 양의 전시회를 찾아가는 것으로도 그러했는데, 나는 그곳을 자기반성과 회개에 알맞은 자수(刺繡)의 영묘로 기억한다. 또 런던탑을 둘러보고, 세인트폴 대성당 꼭대기까지 올라가는 것으로도 그렇게 했다. 이 온갖 진기한 구경거리는 지금 처지에서 패거티가 누릴 수 있는 만큼의 즐거움을 안겨 주었는데, 다만 세인트폴 대성당만은 예외였던 것 같다. 패거티는 제 반짇고리를 오래도록 애지중지해 온 터라, 그 성당이 반짇고리 뚜껑에 그려진 그림과 경쟁 상대가 되고 말았고, 몇 가지 점에서는 그 예술 작품에 패배했다고 패거티는 여겼던 것이다.\n\n우리가 민사법원에서 흔히 '정식 절차 사건'이라 부르던 패거티의 일이 (그 정식 절차 사건이라는 게 무척 수월하고 돈벌이가 짭짤했다) 마무리되자, 어느 날 아침 나는 패거티를 사무소로 데려가 비용을 치르게 했다. 늙은 티피의 말로는, 스펜로 씨는 어떤 신사에게 혼인 허가증 선서를 받게 하려고 잠깐 자리를 비웠다고 했다. 하지만 우리 사무소가 대리 판사 사무실, 그리고 부주교 사무실과도 가까이 붙어 있어서, 그가 곧 돌아오리라는 것을 알았기에, 나는 패거티에게 기다리라고 일렀다.\n\n유언 검인 거래에 관한 한, 우리 민사법원 사람들은 장의사와 좀 비슷한 데가 있었다. 상복을 입은 의뢰인을 상대할 때면 으레 어느 정도 침통한 표정을 짓는 것이 규칙이었다. 그와 비슷한 섬세한 마음씨에서, 허가증 의뢰인을 대할 때는 늘 명랑하고 들떠 있었다. 그래서 나는 패거티에게, 스펜로 씨가 바키스 씨의 별세에서 받은 충격을 한결 회복한 모습일 거라고 넌지시 일러 두었다. 과연 그는 신랑처럼 환하게 들어왔다.\n\n그러나 그와 함께 들어오는 머드스톤 씨를 보았을 때, 패거티도 나도 그에게는 눈길조차 줄 수 없었다. 머드스톤 씨는 거의 변한 데가 없었다. 머리숱은 여전히 빽빽해 보였고, 색깔도 분명 예전처럼 새까맸다. 그리고 그 눈초리는 옛날과 다름없이 도무지 믿을 만한 구석이 없었다.\n\n\"아, 코퍼필드 군?\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"이분과는 아는 사이지 싶은데, 그렇지?\"\n\n나는 그 신사에게 데면데면하게 고개를 까딱했고, 패거티는 그를 알은체하는 둥 마는 둥 했다. 그는 처음에는 우리 둘이 함께 있는 것을 보고 다소 당황했으나, 이내 어떻게 할지 마음을 정하고 나에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"잘 지내기를 바라네.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 관심 가질 만한 일은 아닐 텐데요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그래요, 굳이 알고 싶으시다면, 잘 지냅니다.\"\n\n우리는 서로를 마주 보았고, 그는 패거티에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"그리고 자네는,\" 그가 말했다. \"남편을 잃었다니 안됐군.\"\n\n\"제 평생 처음 겪는 상실은 아니에요, 머드스톤 씨.\" 패거티가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 대꾸했다. \"이번 일에는 탓할 사람이 아무도 없다는 게, 책임질 사람이 아무도 없다는 게 다행이라 여겨 기쁠 따름이지요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 그가 말했다. \"마음 편한 생각이군. 자네는 도리를 다했고?\"\n\n\"저는 그 누구의 목숨도 시들게 한 적이 없어요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그렇게 생각할 수 있으니 고맙지요! 아무렴요, 머드스톤 씨, 저는 그 어떤 사랑스러운 사람도 들들 볶고 겁주어 일찍 무덤으로 보낸 적이 없답니다!\"\n\n그는 한순간 패거티를 음울하게 — 내 생각에는 뉘우치듯이 — 바라보았다. 그러고는 나를 향해 고개를 돌렸으되, 내 얼굴 대신 내 발을 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 가까운 시일에 다시 마주칠 일은 없겠지. 둘 다에게 다행한 일임은 의심할 여지가 없네. 이런 만남이 유쾌할 리 없으니 말이야. 자네의 이익과 교화를 위해 행사한 나의 정당한 권위에 늘 반항하던 자네가, 이제 와서 나에게 무슨 호의를 품으리라고 기대하지는 않네. 우리 사이에는 반감이 있으니까—\"\n\n\"오래된 반감이지요?\" 내가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다.\n\n그가 미소를 지었고, 그 검은 눈에서 나올 수 있는 가장 사악한 눈초리를 나에게 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"그건 자네의 어린 가슴속에서 곪았지.\" 그가 말했다. \"그것이 자네의 가엾은 어머니의 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들었어. 자네 말이 맞네. 그래도 자네가 더 나아지기를 바라네. 자네가 스스로를 바로잡기를 바라.\"\n\n여기서 그는 바깥 사무실 한구석에서 낮은 목소리로 주고받던 이 대화를 끝내고, 스펜로 씨의 방으로 들어가며, 더없이 매끄러운 말투로 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"스펜로 씨 같은 직업을 가진 분들은 집안의 불화에 이골이 나서, 그런 일이 늘 얼마나 복잡하고 까다로운지 잘 아시지요!\" 그렇게 말하고 그는 허가증 값을 치렀다. 그리고 스펜로 씨가 깔끔하게 접어 건네주는 허가증을, 악수와 더불어, 자기 자신과 그 부인의 행복을 비는 정중한 인사와 함께 받아 들고, 사무소를 나갔다.\n\n그의 말을 듣고도 잠자코 있도록 나 자신을 다잡는 데 더 애를 먹을 뻔했으나, 다행히 패거티를 (나를 위해서만 화를 내던 착한 사람!) 달래는 데 그만큼 애를 먹지 않았다. 여기는 서로 비난을 주고받을 자리가 아니라고, 부디 잠자코 있어 달라고 나는 패거티에게 통사정했다. 패거티가 어찌나 유난히 흥분했던지, 나는 우리가 옛날에 당했던 부당한 일들이 그녀의 마음속에 되살아나면서 터져 나온 다정한 포옹으로 무마하고, 스펜로 씨와 사무원들 앞에서 그것을 최대한 잘 넘어가게 한 것만으로도 다행이라 여겼다.\n\n스펜로 씨는 머드스톤 씨와 나 사이가 어떤 관계인지 모르는 듯했다. 나로서는 그게 다행이었는데, 내 가엾은 어머니의 사연을 기억하는 처지에, 그를 친척으로 인정하는 것을 — 나 자신의 가슴속에서조차 — 견딜 수가 없었기 때문이다. 스펜로 씨는, 이 문제에 대해 무언가 생각한 게 있다면, 우리 집안에서 우리 고모님이 정부(政府) 측의 지도자이고, 다른 누군가가 이끄는 반란 측이 있다고 여기는 듯했다. 적어도 티피 씨가 패거티의 비용 청구서를 작성하기를 기다리는 동안 그가 한 말로 미루어 나는 그렇게 짐작했다.\n\n\"트롯우드 양은,\" 그가 말했다. \"의심할 여지 없이 무척 단호하시고, 반대에 굴할 분이 아니지. 나는 그분의 성품을 흠모한다네. 그리고 코퍼필드 군, 자네가 옳은 편에 서 있는 것을 축하해도 되겠군. 친척들 사이의 불화는 참으로 개탄할 일이지만, 그런 일은 지극히 흔하다네. 중요한 건 옳은 편에 서는 것이지.\" 내가 보기에 그 말은 돈줄을 쥔 편에 서라는 뜻이었다.\n\n\"이번 혼인은 꽤 좋은 혼사라지, 아마?\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다.\n\n나는 그 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모른다고 설명했다.\n\n\"그래?\" 그가 말했다. \"머드스톤 씨가 흘린 몇 마디로 미루어 — 사람들이 그런 자리에서 곧잘 그러듯이 말이야 — 그리고 머드스톤 양이 흘린 말로 미루어 보건대, 꽤 좋은 혼사라 하겠더군.\"\n\n\"돈이 있다는 말씀이신가요, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"내가 알기로는 돈이 있다지. 게다가 미모까지 갖췄다더군.\"\n\n\"그래요! 그 새 부인이 젊은가요?\"\n\n\"이제 막 성년이 됐다네.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"어찌나 최근인지, 내 생각엔 그들이 그때까지 기다린 듯싶더군.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 그 사람을 구해 주시길!\" 패거티가 말했다. 어찌나 힘주어, 또 뜻밖에 내뱉었던지, 우리 셋 다 당황하고 말았는데, 티피가 청구서를 들고 들어올 때까지 그러했다.\n\n그러나 늙은 티피는 곧 나타나, 그것을 스펜로 씨에게 건네 살펴보게 했다. 스펜로 씨는 턱을 크라바트에 파묻고 그것을 부드럽게 문지르며, 마치 그게 죄다 조킨스가 한 짓이라는 듯 못마땅한 기색으로 항목들을 훑어 내려가더니, 부드러운 한숨과 함께 티피에게 도로 건넸다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"맞네. 아주 잘됐어. 코퍼필드 군, 나는 이 비용을 실제로 내 주머니에서 나간 지출에만 한정할 수 있었더라면 더없이 기뻤겠네만, 내 직업 생활에서 영 성가신 점이 하나 있으니, 내 뜻대로 할 자유가 없다는 거야. 나에게는 동업자가 있거든. 조킨스 씨라고.\"\n\n그가 이 말을, 아예 한 푼도 청구하지 않는 것이나 다름없는 부드러운 우수에 잠겨 하기에, 나는 패거티를 대신해 고마움을 표하고, 티피에게 지폐로 값을 치렀다. 그러고 나서 패거티는 숙소로 물러갔고, 스펜로 씨와 나는 법정으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서 우리는 어떤 기발한 소법령(지금은 폐지되었지 싶으나, 이 법령에 따라 여러 건의 혼인이 무효가 되는 것을 나는 보았다)에 근거한 이혼 소송 한 건을 맡고 있었고, 사건의 요지는 이러했다. 토머스 벤저민이라는 이름의 남편이, 혹시 자기가 기대만큼 편치 못할 경우에 대비해 벤저민을 빼고 그저 토머스라는 이름으로만 혼인 허가증을 받았던 것이다. 그러다 기대만큼 편하지 않다는 것을 알았는지, 아니면 가엾게도 아내에게 조금 싫증이 났는지, 그는 결혼한 지 일이 년이 지나, 친구를 내세워 나서서, 자기 이름은 토머스 벤저민이므로 애초에 결혼한 적이 없다고 선언했다. 법원은 이를 인정해 주었고, 그는 무척 흡족해했다.\n\n이것이 엄밀한 정의에 들어맞는지 나로서는 미심쩍었다는 것을 밝혀 두어야겠는데, 온갖 변칙을 다 무마해 준다는 밀 한 부셸을 들이대도 그 의심을 떨치지 못했다. 그러나 스펜로 씨는 이 문제를 두고 나와 논쟁을 벌였다. 그가 말하기를, 세상을 보라, 거기에 선과 악이 있다, 교회법을 보라, 거기에도 선과 악이 있다는 것이었다. 그것은 모두 하나의 체계의 일부라고 했다. 아주 훌륭하다. 그러니 어쩌겠는가!\n\n나는 도라의 아버지에게, 우리가 아침 일찍 일어나 웃옷을 벗어부치고 일에 달려든다면 어쩌면 세상을 조금 더 낫게 만들 수도 있지 않겠느냐고 감히 말할 배짱은 없었다. 다만 민사법원만은 우리가 개선할 수 있을 것 같다고 털어놓았다. 스펜로 씨는 그런 생각은 내 신사다운 품성에 어울리지 않으니 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버리라고 각별히 충고하고 싶다고 대꾸했다. 그러면서도 민사법원의 어떤 점을 개선할 수 있다고 보는지 내 이야기를 기꺼이 들어 주겠다고 했다.\n\n마침 우리에게서 제일 가까운 부분의 민사법원을 들어 — 그 무렵엔 그 사내의 혼인이 무효로 처리된 뒤라, 우리는 법정을 나와 유언 검인 사무소를 지나며 거닐고 있었다 — 나는 그 검인 사무소가 운영이 좀 괴상한 기관 같다고 말했다. 스펜로 씨가 어떤 점에서 그러냐고 물었다. 나는 그의 경험에 마땅한 경의를 표하면서 (다만 송구스럽게도, 그가 도라의 아버지라는 점에 더 큰 경의를 표하면서) 대답하기를, 어쩌면 이런 점이 좀 어처구니없는 것 같다고 했다. 그 법원의 등기소는 광대한 캔터베리 관구 안에서 유산을 남긴 모든 사람의 유언장 원본을 무려 삼백 년 동안 보관하고 있는데, 그 건물이라는 게 애초에 그 용도로 설계된 적도 없이 우연히 떠맡은 건물이고, 등기관들이 제 사사로운 잇속을 위해 빌린 것이며, 안전하지도 않고, 화재에 견딘다는 보장조차 없으며, 거기 보관된 중요한 문서들로 발 디딜 틈 없이 빼곡한 데다, 지붕에서 지하실까지 그야말로 등기관들의 돈벌이 투기판이라는 점 말이다. 그들은 대중에게서 막대한 수수료를 챙기면서, 대중의 유언장을 닥치는 대로 아무 데나 쑤셔 넣어, 그저 값싸게 처치해 버리는 것 말고는 다른 목적이 없었다. 또 어쩌면 이런 점도 좀 부당한 것 같다고 했다. 연 수입이 팔구천 파운드에 이르는 (부등기관과 좌석 서기들의 수입은 말할 것도 없고) 이 등기관들이, 모든 계층의 사람들이 좋든 싫든 어쩔 수 없이 넘겨야만 하는 그 중요한 문서들을 위해 그럭저럭 안전한 장소 하나 마련하는 데 그 돈을 조금이라도 쓰도록 의무 지워지지 않는다는 점 말이다. 또 어쩌면 이런 점도 좀 불공평한 것 같다고 했다. 이 거대한 관청 안의 큰 자리들은 죄다 거창한 한직(閑職)인데, 위층의 춥고 어두운 방에서 일하는 가엾은 실무 서기들은 런던에서 가장 푸대접받고 가장 무시당하면서도 중요한 일을 떠맡고 있다는 점 말이다. 또 어쩌면 이런 점도 좀 꼴사나운 것 같다고 했다. 이곳을 끊임없이 드나드는 대중에게 필요한 온갖 편의를 제공하는 것이 본분인 수석 등기관이, 그 직책 덕분에 어마어마한 한직을 차지하고 있고 (게다가 성직자에다, 여러 직책을 겸한 사람이며, 대성당의 한 자리를 꿰찬 자에다, 그 밖에도 온갖 직함을 거머쥐었을 수도 있는데), 그러는 동안 대중은 불편을 겪어야만 하는데, 그 본보기를 우리는 사무소가 분주한 오후마다 보았고, 그것이 그야말로 끔찍하다는 것을 우리는 알고 있었다는 점 말이다. 요컨대 어쩌면 캔터베리 교구의 이 유언 검인 사무소라는 것이, 통째로 그토록 해로운 폐단이요, 그토록 고약한 모순덩어리라, 아는 사람이 거의 없는 세인트폴 교회 묘지 한구석에 처박혀 있지 않았더라면, 진작에 완전히 안팎이 뒤집히고 위아래가 뒤바뀌었어야 마땅하다는 점 말이다.\n\n내가 이 화제에 점잖게 열을 올리자 스펜로 씨는 미소를 짓더니, 앞서 다른 문제를 두고 그랬던 것처럼 이 문제를 놓고도 나와 논쟁을 벌였다. 그가 말하기를, 결국 그게 무슨 문제냐는 것이었다. 그것은 감정의 문제라고 했다. 대중이 제 유언장이 안전하게 보관되고 있다고 느끼고, 그 사무소를 더 낫게 만들 필요가 없다고 당연하게 여긴다면, 그로써 손해 보는 사람이 누가 있느냐고 했다. 아무도 없다. 그로써 덕 보는 사람이 누구냐고 했다. 한직을 차지한 자들 모두다. 아주 좋다. 그러니 좋은 쪽이 우세하다는 것이었다. 그것이 완벽한 체계는 아닐지 모르나, 완벽한 것은 어디에도 없다고 했다. 다만 그가 반대하는 것은 쐐기를 박아 넣는 일이라고 했다. 유언 검인 사무소 아래에서 이 나라는 영광을 누려 왔다는 것이다. 그 사무소에 쐐기를 박아 넣으면, 이 나라는 더 이상 영광스럽지 못하리라고 했다. 그는 매사를 있는 그대로 받아들이는 것이 신사의 원칙이라 여기며, 유언 검인 사무소가 우리 당대까지는 무난히 갈 것을 의심치 않는다고 했다. 나는 그의 의견에 따랐으나, 나 자신은 그것을 크게 의심했다. 하지만 알고 보니 그가 옳았다. 그 사무소는 지금 이 순간까지 버텨 왔을 뿐 아니라, 십팔 년 전에 (그리 내키지 않는 마음으로) 작성된 어느 굵직한 의회 보고서에도 아랑곳없이 그래 왔으니까. 그 보고서에는 내가 말한 이 모든 반대 의견이 자세히 적혀 있었고, 유언장을 쌓아 둘 현재의 공간은 고작 이 년 반 어치를 더 보관할 정도밖에 안 된다고 적혀 있었다. 그 뒤로 그들이 유언장을 어떻게 했는지, 많이 잃어버렸는지, 아니면 이따금 버터 가게에 팔아넘기는지, 나는 모른다. 내 유언장이 거기 있지 않아서 다행이고, 한동안은 그리로 가지 않기를 바란다.\n\n내가 이 모든 것을 지금의 이 더없이 행복한 장에 적어 둔 까닭은, 여기가 그것이 자연스레 들어맞을 자리이기 때문이다. 스펜로 씨와 나는 이런 대화에 빠져들어, 이리저리 거니는 산책과 더불어 그 이야기를 길게 끌다가, 마침내 두루뭉술한 화제로 넘어갔다. 그렇게 해서 결국, 스펜로 씨가 나에게, 오늘로부터 일주일 뒤가 도라의 생일이니, 그날을 맞아 내가 내려와 자그마한 소풍에 함께해 준다면 기쁘겠다고 말하게 된 것이다. 나는 그 자리에서 넋이 나가 버렸다. 이튿날에는 '아빠가 보내 줌. 잊지 말라고'라고 적힌, 레이스로 가장자리를 두른 작은 편지지 한 장을 받고 그만 얼간이가 되어 버렸다. 그리고 그날까지의 시간을 망령 든 사람처럼 흘려보냈다.\n\n이 복된 행사를 준비하느라 나는 가능한 온갖 어리석은 짓을 다 저지른 것 같다. 내가 산 크라바트를 떠올리면 지금도 얼굴이 화끈거린다. 내 부츠는 어떤 고문 도구 진열장에 갖다 놓아도 손색이 없을 정도였다. 나는 전날 밤 노우드행 마차 편으로 자그마하고 고급스러운 바구니 하나를 마련해 내려보냈는데, 내 생각엔 그것만으로도 거의 사랑 고백이나 다름없었다. 그 안에는 돈으로 구할 수 있는 가장 다정한 글귀가 적힌 봉봉 과자가 들어 있었다. 아침 여섯 시에 나는 코번트 가든 시장에 가서 도라에게 줄 꽃다발을 샀다. 열 시에는 (그 일을 위해 늠름한 잿빛 말 한 마리를 빌렸다) 그 꽃다발을 싱싱하게 보존하려고 모자에 꽂은 채 말에 올라타, 노우드를 향해 달려 내려갔다.\n\n정원에 있는 도라를 보고도 못 본 척하면서, 집을 애타게 찾는 척 그 집을 지나쳐 달렸을 때, 나는 내 처지에 놓인 다른 젊은 신사들도 저질렀을 법한 자그마한 어리석음 두 가지를 저질렀던 것 같다. 그것들이 내게는 너무도 자연스럽게 우러나왔으니까. 그러나 아아! 마침내 그 집을 찾아내, 정원 문 앞에서 말에서 내려, 그 돌처럼 딱딱한 부츠를 끌고 잔디밭을 가로질러, 라일락 나무 아래 정원 의자에 앉아 있는 도라에게 다가갔을 때, 그 아름다운 아침에, 나비들 사이에서, 하얀 챙 없는 보닛을 쓰고 천상의 푸른빛 드레스를 입은 도라는 얼마나 황홀한 광경이었던지! 도라 곁에는 한 아가씨가 있었는데 — 비교적 나이가 든 편으로 — 거의 스무 살은 됐지 싶었다. 그녀의 이름은 밀스 양이었다. 그리고 도라는 그녀를 줄리아라고 불렀다. 그녀는 도라의 둘도 없는 벗이었다. 행복한 밀스 양 같으니!\n\n집은 거기 있었고, 집은 또다시 나를 보고 짖어 댔다. 내가 꽃다발을 내밀자, 집은 질투에 이를 갈았다. 그럴 만도 했다. 내가 제 안주인을 얼마나 흠모하는지 조금이라도 눈치챘다면, 그럴 만도 했고말고!\n\n\"어머, 고마워요, 코퍼필드 씨! 정말 예쁜 꽃이네요!\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n나는 (그리고 삼 마일을 오는 내내 가장 멋진 표현을 궁리한 끝에) 도라 곁에서 이토록 가까이 보기 전부터 그 꽃이 아름답다고 여겼노라고 말할 작정이었다. 하지만 도무지 입이 떨어지지 않았다. 도라는 너무도 정신을 아찔하게 만드는 사람이었다. 도라가 그 꽃을 자신의 작고 보조개 진 턱에 갖다 대는 것을 보자, 나는 정신을 차릴 힘도, 말을 할 힘도 죄다 잃고 가냘픈 황홀경에 빠져들었다. \"밀스 양, 자비심이 있다면 저를 죽여 주세요. 여기서 죽게 해 주세요!\"라고 말하지 않은 것이 신기할 따름이다.\n\n이윽고 도라는 내 꽃을 집에게 내밀어 냄새를 맡게 했다. 그러자 집은 으르렁대며 냄새 맡기를 거부했다. 그러자 도라는 웃으면서 꽃을 집에게 조금 더 가까이 들이밀어 억지로 맡게 하려 했다. 그러자 집은 제라늄 한 송이를 이빨로 덥석 물고, 거기에 든 가상의 고양이를 마구 물어뜯어 댔다. 그러자 도라는 집을 때리고는 입을 삐죽이며, \"내 가엾고 예쁜 꽃들!\" 하고 말했는데, 내 생각엔 집이 나를 물어뜯기라도 한 것처럼 안타까워하는 투였다. 차라리 집이 나를 물어뜯었더라면!\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 정말 반가운 소식이 있어요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그 심술궂은 머드스톤 양이 여기 없거든요. 자기 오빠 결혼식에 가서, 적어도 삼 주는 비울 거예요. 정말 신나지 않아요?\"\n\n나는 도라에게 그것이 분명 신나는 일일 것이며, 도라에게 신나는 일은 무엇이든 나에게도 신나는 일이라고 말했다. 밀스 양은 한층 지혜롭고 자애로운 기색으로 우리를 보고 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"내가 본 사람 중에 제일 못마땅한 사람이에요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그 여자가 얼마나 심술궂고 끔찍한지, 줄리아, 너는 못 믿을 거야.\"\n\n\"아냐, 믿을 수 있어, 얘!\" 줄리아가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 어쩌면 믿을 수 있겠지.\" 도라가 줄리아의 손에 제 손을 얹으며 대꾸했다. \"처음에 너를 빼놓고 말한 거 용서해 줘, 얘.\"\n\n이로써 나는, 밀스 양이 파란만장한 인생을 살아오는 동안 갖은 시련을 겪었다는 것과, 어쩌면 내가 이미 눈여겨본 그 지혜로운 자애로움이 그 시련에서 비롯되었으리라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그날 하루를 보내는 동안 나는 그것이 사실임을 알았다. 밀스 양은 잘못 둔 정 때문에 불행을 겪은 적이 있었고, 그 무시무시한 경험을 밑천 삼아 세상을 등진 것으로 통했으나, 그러면서도 시들지 않은 젊음의 희망과 사랑에는 여전히 잔잔한 관심을 두고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n그런데 이때 스펜로 씨가 집에서 나왔고, 도라가 그에게 달려가 말했다. \"보세요, 아빠, 얼마나 예쁜 꽃이에요!\" 그러자 밀스 양은 생각에 잠긴 듯 미소 지었는데, 마치 \"그대 하루살이들이여, 인생의 환한 아침에 주어진 짧은 생을 마음껏 누리려무나!\" 하고 말하는 것 같았다. 그리고 우리는 모두 잔디밭에서 마차 쪽으로 걸어갔는데, 마차는 떠날 채비를 갖추고 있었다.\n\n나는 다시는 그런 마차 길을 누리지 못하리라. 그런 길은 그 후로 단 한 번도 없었다. 사륜마차에는 그 셋과, 그들의 바구니, 내 바구니, 그리고 기타 케이스밖에 없었다. 그리고 물론 그 마차는 덮개 없는 것이었다. 나는 그 뒤를 따라 말을 달렸고, 도라는 말 쪽으로 등을 돌리고 앉아 나를 바라보았다. 도라는 꽃다발을 방석 위 제 곁에 바짝 붙여 놓고, 행여 그것이 짓눌릴까 봐 집을 그쪽에는 아예 앉지 못하게 했다. 도라는 자주 그것을 손에 들었고, 자주 그 향기로 기운을 돋우었다. 그럴 때면 우리 눈은 자주 마주쳤다. 내가 그 늠름한 잿빛 말 머리를 넘어 마차 안으로 거꾸러지지 않은 것이 그저 놀라울 따름이다.\n\n먼지가 일었던 것 같다. 먼지가 꽤 많이 일었던 것 같다. 스펜로 씨가 그 속에서 말을 달린다고 나를 나무란 듯한 어렴풋한 기억이 있다. 하지만 나는 아무것도 의식하지 못했다. 나는 도라를 에워싼 사랑과 아름다움의 안개만 느꼈을 뿐, 다른 것은 아무것도 느끼지 못했다. 그는 이따금 일어서서, 경치를 어떻게 보느냐고 나에게 물었다. 나는 멋지다고 대답했고, 정말 멋졌을 것이다. 하지만 나에게는 모든 것이 도라였다. 해는 도라로 빛났고, 새들은 도라를 노래했다. 남풍은 도라로 불었고, 산울타리의 들꽃들은 봉오리 하나하나까지 죄다 도라였다. 위안이 되는 것은, 밀스 양이 나를 이해해 주었다는 사실이다. 밀스 양만이 내 심정에 온전히 공감해 줄 수 있었다.\n\n우리가 얼마나 오래 갔는지 나는 모르고, 지금 이 순간까지도 우리가 어디로 갔는지 도통 알지 못한다. 어쩌면 길퍼드 근처였는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 어느 아라비안나이트의 마법사가, 그날 하루만 그곳을 열어 놓았다가 우리가 떠나오자 영영 닫아 버린 것인지도 모른다. 그곳은 언덕 위의 푸른 자리였고, 부드러운 잔디가 양탄자처럼 깔려 있었다. 그늘진 나무들과 히스가 있었고, 눈길이 닿는 데까지 풍요로운 풍경이 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n그곳에서 우리를 기다리는 사람들을 발견한 것은 견디기 힘든 일이었고, 여자들에 대해서까지도 내 질투는 끝을 몰랐다. 그러나 내 동성들은 모조리 — 특히 나보다 서너 살 위에, 붉은 구레나룻을 기른 어떤 사기꾼은, 그 구레나룻을 밑천 삼아 도무지 참아 줄 수 없을 만큼 거들먹거렸는데 — 나의 철천지원수였다.\n\n우리는 모두 바구니를 풀어, 저녁 식사 채비를 하느라 분주했다. 붉은 구레나룻은 샐러드를 만들 줄 안다고 떠벌리며 (나는 믿지 않았지만) 뭇사람의 눈에 띄려고 나섰다. 젊은 아가씨 몇몇이 그를 위해 상추를 씻었고, 그의 지시에 따라 그것을 썰었다. 도라도 그들 틈에 끼어 있었다. 운명이 나를 이 사내와 맞붙여 놓았으니, 우리 둘 중 하나는 쓰러질 수밖에 없다는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n붉은 구레나룻이 샐러드를 만들었다 (사람들이 그걸 어떻게 먹는지 신기했다. 나라면 무슨 일이 있어도 손도 대지 않았으리라!). 그러고는 자기가 술 창고를 맡겠다고 자청했는데, 약삭빠른 짐승답게 나무의 텅 빈 둥치 속에다 그 술 창고를 만들었다. 이윽고 나는 그가 접시에 바닷가재의 대부분을 담아 들고, 도라의 발치에서 저녁을 먹는 것을 보았다!\n\n이 끔찍한 광경이 내 눈앞에 펼쳐진 뒤로 한동안 무슨 일이 있었는지는 어렴풋한 기억밖에 없다. 내가 무척 들떠 있었다는 것은 안다. 하지만 그것은 속 빈 들뜸이었다. 나는 눈이 작은 분홍 옷 차림의 젊은 처자에게 들러붙어, 죽기 살기로 수작을 걸었다. 그녀는 내 관심을 호의로 받아들였다. 그러나 그게 오로지 나 때문이었는지, 아니면 그녀가 붉은 구레나룻에게 무슨 속셈이 있어서였는지는 알 수 없다. 도라를 위한 축배가 돌았다. 나는 그 축배를 들 때, 일부러 하던 대화를 끊고 잔을 든 다음, 곧바로 다시 대화를 이어 가는 척했다. 도라에게 고개 숙여 인사하다가 도라의 눈길을 붙잡았는데, 무언가 호소하는 듯한 눈빛으로 보였다. 그러나 그 눈빛은 붉은 구레나룻의 머리 너머로 나를 보고 있었고, 나는 매정하게 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n분홍 옷의 젊은 처자에게는 초록 옷을 입은 어머니가 있었다. 내 생각엔 그 어머니가 무슨 속셈이 있어 우리를 떼어 놓은 것 같다. 어쨌든 저녁 식사의 남은 것을 치우는 사이, 모임이 전체적으로 흩어졌고, 나는 사납고 회한에 찬 심정으로 홀로 나무들 사이를 거닐었다. 내가 몸이 안 좋은 척하고, 늠름한 잿빛 말을 타고 어딘가로 — 어디로인지는 모르겠지만 — 달아나 버릴까 궁리하고 있을 때, 도라와 밀스 양이 나를 마주쳤다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨,\" 밀스 양이 말했다. \"기운이 없으시네요.\"\n\n나는 그녀에게 양해를 구했다. 전혀 그렇지 않다고.\n\n\"그리고 도라,\" 밀스 양이 말했다. \"너도 기운이 없구나.\"\n\n어머, 아니에요! 조금도 그렇지 않아요.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 그리고 도라.\" 밀스 양이 거의 위엄 어린 기색으로 말했다. \"이쯤 하세요. 하찮은 오해 때문에 봄의 꽃송이를 시들게 하지 마세요. 그 꽃송이는 한번 피었다 시들면 다시는 되살릴 수 없으니까요. 저는,\" 밀스 양이 말했다. \"지난날의 경험에서 우러나 말하는 거예요. 저 멀고도 되돌릴 수 없는 지난날 말이에요. 햇빛에 반짝이며 솟구치는 샘물을 한낱 변덕으로 막아서는 안 됩니다. 사하라 사막의 오아시스를 함부로 뽑아 버려서도 안 되고요.\"\n\n나는 내가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 잘 몰랐다. 온몸이 그렇게나 유난히 활활 타오르고 있었으니까. 하지만 나는 도라의 작은 손을 잡고 거기에 입을 맞추었다 — 그리고 도라는 그러도록 내버려 두었다! 나는 밀스 양의 손에도 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 내 생각에, 우리는 모두 곧장 일곱째 하늘로 솟구쳐 오르는 것만 같았다. 우리는 다시 내려오지 않았다. 우리는 저녁 내내 그 위에 머물렀다. 처음에 우리는 나무들 사이를 이리저리 거닐었다. 나는 도라의 수줍은 팔을 내 팔에 끼고서. 그리고 그 모든 것이 어리석은 짓이긴 했어도, 그 어리석은 감정에 사로잡힌 채 불멸의 몸이 되어 영원토록 그 나무들 사이에 머물 수만 있었다면, 정말이지 그것은 행복한 운명이었으리라는 것을, 하느님은 아실 것이다!\n\n그러나 너무도 빨리, 우리는 다른 사람들이 웃고 떠들며 \"도라는 어디 있어?\" 하고 부르는 소리를 들었다. 그래서 우리는 돌아갔고, 사람들은 도라에게 노래를 청했다. 붉은 구레나룻이 마차에서 기타 케이스를 꺼내 오려 했으나, 도라는 그것이 어디 있는지 나 말고는 아무도 모른다고 그에게 일러 주었다. 그리하여 붉은 구레나룻은 단번에 끝장났다. 그리고 내가 그것을 가져와, 내가 그것을 열고, 내가 기타를 꺼내, 내가 도라 곁에 앉아, 내가 그녀의 손수건과 장갑을 들어 주고, 내가 그녀의 사랑스러운 목소리의 한 음 한 음을 들이마셨다. 그리고 그녀는 자기를 사랑하는 나에게 노래해 주었으니, 다른 사람들이야 좋을 대로 갈채를 보내든 말든, 그들은 그 노래와 아무 상관이 없었다!\n\n나는 기쁨에 취했다. 그것이 너무 행복해서 도무지 현실 같지가 않아, 머잖아 버킹엄 거리에서 잠을 깨, 크럽 부인이 아침을 차리느라 찻잔을 달그락거리는 소리나 듣게 되는 것은 아닐까 두려웠다. 그러나 도라가 노래했고, 다른 사람들도 노래했고, 밀스 양도 노래했다. 기억의 동굴 속에 잠든 메아리에 대해 — 마치 자기가 백 살이라도 먹은 양 — 노래했다. 그리고 저녁이 깔려 왔다. 우리는 집시처럼 주전자를 끓여 차를 마셨다. 그리고 나는 여전히 더없이 행복했다.\n\n모임이 파하고, 패배한 붉은 구레나룻을 비롯한 다른 사람들이 저마다 제 갈 길로 가고, 우리는 우리 길로 접어들어, 사방에 달콤한 향기가 피어오르는 고요한 저녁과 스러져 가는 빛 속을 지날 때, 나는 어느 때보다도 더 행복했다. 스펜로 씨는 샴페인을 마신 뒤라 조금 졸려 했는데 — 그 포도를 길러 낸 토양에, 그 포도로 술을 빚어 낸 포도에, 그것을 익혀 준 햇빛에, 그리고 그것에 물을 탄 상인에게 영광 있으라! — 그래서 마차 한구석에서 곤히 잠들어 버렸기에, 나는 마차 옆에서 말을 달리며 도라와 이야기를 나누었다. 도라는 내 말을 칭찬하며 토닥여 주었는데 — 아아, 말 위에 얹힌 그 손은 얼마나 사랑스러운 작은 손으로 보이던지! — 그리고 도라의 숄이 자꾸 흘러내리기에, 나는 이따금 팔로 그것을 그녀에게 둘러 주었다. 심지어 나는 집마저도 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 눈치채기 시작해, 마음을 정하고 나와 친해지는 수밖에 없다는 것을 깨닫는구나 하고 상상했다.\n\n게다가 저 슬기로운 밀스 양, 비록 진이 다 빠지긴 했어도 그 상냥한 은둔자, 세상과는 이미 인연을 끊어 기억의 동굴 속에 잠든 메아리를 무슨 일이 있어도 깨워서는 안 되는, 스무 살이 채 안 된 그 자그마한 가모장(家母長)은, 얼마나 친절한 일을 해 주었던지!\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨,\" 밀스 양이 말했다. \"잠깐 마차 이쪽으로 와 보시겠어요? 잠깐 시간이 되신다면요. 드릴 말씀이 있어서요.\"\n\n자, 보라, 늠름한 잿빛 말에 올라탄 채, 마차 문에 손을 얹고, 밀스 양 곁으로 몸을 숙이고 있는 나를!\n\n\"도라가 저와 함께 지내러 올 거예요. 모레 저와 함께 집으로 갈 거랍니다. 찾아오고 싶으시다면, 분명 아빠도 코퍼필드 씨를 반가워하실 거예요.\" 내가 밀스 양의 머리 위로 말없이 축복을 빌고, 밀스 양의 주소를 내 기억의 가장 안전한 한구석에 새겨 두는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴! 내가 밀스 양에게, 고마운 눈빛과 뜨거운 말로, 그녀의 호의를 얼마나 고맙게 여기는지, 그녀의 우정에 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없는 가치를 두는지 전하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴!\n\n그러고 나서 밀스 양은 자애롭게 나를 물러가게 하며, \"도라에게 돌아가세요!\" 하고 말했다. 그래서 나는 갔다. 도라가 마차 밖으로 몸을 내밀어 나에게 말을 걸었고, 우리는 남은 길 내내 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 나는 늠름한 잿빛 말을 바퀴에 어찌나 바짝 붙여 몰았던지, 그놈의 앞다리를 바퀴에 긁혀, 말 주인의 말마따나 \"삼 파운드 칠 실링어치로 살가죽을 벗겨\" 먹고 말았다 — 나는 그 값을 치렀고, 그만한 기쁨에 비하면 더없이 싸다고 여겼다. 그동안 밀스 양은 달을 바라보며, 시구를 읊조리고 앉아 있었다 — 짐작건대, 자기가 이 세상과 무언가 통하던 것이 있던 그 옛날을 떠올리면서.\n\n노우드는 몇 마일이나 너무 가까웠고, 우리는 몇 시간이나 너무 일찍 그곳에 다다랐다. 하지만 스펜로 씨가 그곳에 채 못 미쳐 정신을 조금 차리고는 말했다. \"코퍼필드 군, 들어와서 좀 쉬게나!\" 그리고 내가 응하자, 우리는 샌드위치와 포도주 탄 물을 먹었다. 불빛 환한 방에서, 발그레해진 도라가 어찌나 사랑스러워 보였던지, 나는 도무지 자리를 뜰 수가 없어, 꿈결에 잠긴 듯 멍하니 도라만 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 그러다 스펜로 씨의 코 고는 소리에 가까스로 정신을 차리고 자리를 떴다. 그렇게 우리는 헤어졌다. 나는 도라의 손이 작별로 닿았던 가벼운 감촉을 여전히 내 손에 느끼며, 그날의 사건 하나하나와 말 한마디 한마디를 만 번이고 곱씹으며, 런던까지 내내 말을 달렸다. 그리고 마침내 내 침대에 누웠으니, 사랑에 정신이 홀딱 나가 버린, 일찍이 없던 황홀한 풋내기 얼간이였다.\n\n이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때, 나는 도라에게 내 사랑을 고백하고 내 운명을 알아내리라 굳게 마음먹었다. 이제는 행복이냐 비참함이냐가 문제였다. 세상에 내가 아는 다른 문제는 없었고, 오직 도라만이 그 답을 줄 수 있었다. 나는 도라와 나 사이에 일찍이 있었던 모든 일에 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 비관적인 해석을 죄다 갖다 붙이며 나 자신을 괴롭히는, 비참함의 사치 속에서 사흘을 보냈다. 마침내 나는 그 목적을 위해 엄청난 돈을 들여 차려입고, 사랑 고백을 잔뜩 품은 채 밀스 양의 집으로 갔다.\n\n내가 그 계단을 올라가 문을 두드릴 결심이 설 때까지, 그 거리를 몇 번이나 오르내리고 광장 둘레를 몇 번이나 돌았는지는 — 그 옛날 수수께끼의 본래 답보다 내가 훨씬 더 그럴듯한 답이라는 것을 뼈저리게 느끼면서 말이다 — 이제 와서는 아무래도 좋다. 마침내 문을 두드리고 문 앞에서 기다리고 있을 때조차, 나는 (가엾은 바키스를 흉내 내어) 여기가 블랙보이 씨 댁이냐고 물은 뒤, 죄송하다며 물러나 버릴까 하는 허둥대는 생각마저 들었다. 하지만 나는 자리를 지켰다.\n\n밀스 씨는 집에 없었다. 나는 그가 있으리라 기대하지도 않았다. 아무도 그를 찾지 않았다. 밀스 양은 집에 있었다. 밀스 양이면 됐다.\n\n나는 위층의 어느 방으로 안내되었는데, 그곳에 밀스 양과 도라가 있었다. 집도 거기 있었다. 밀스 양은 악보를 베껴 쓰고 있었고 (기억하기로, '애정의 만가'라는 새 노래였다), 도라는 꽃을 그리고 있었다. 그 그림이 내 꽃이라는 것을, 바로 그 코번트 가든 시장에서 산 꽃이라는 것을 알아보았을 때, 내 심정이 어떠했겠는가! 그 그림이 실물과 아주 닮았다거나, 내가 여태껏 본 어떤 꽃과 유난히 비슷했다고는 말할 수 없다. 하지만 그 꽃을 두른 종이가 정확히 베껴진 것을 보고, 나는 그 그림이 무엇을 그린 것인지 알아챘다.\n\n밀스 양은 나를 보고 무척 반가워했고, 아빠가 집에 없어 무척 안타까워했다. 하지만 우리 모두 그 사실을 꿋꿋이 잘 견뎌 내고 있다는 생각이 들었다. 밀스 양은 몇 분간 이야기를 나누더니, '애정의 만가' 위에 펜을 내려놓고 일어나, 방을 나갔다.\n\n나는 이 일을 내일로 미룰까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n\"가엾은 말이 밤에 집에 갔을 때 지치지 않았기를 바라요.\" 도라가 그 아름다운 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"그 말한테는 먼 길이었을 텐데요.\"\n\n나는 이 일을 오늘 해야겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n\"그 말한테는 먼 길이었지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그 여정에서 자기를 버티게 해 줄 게 아무것도 없었으니까요.\"\n\n\"불쌍하게도, 먹이는 줬나요?\" 도라가 물었다.\n\n나는 이 일을 내일로 미룰까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n\"네, 네.\" 내가 말했다. \"잘 보살핌을 받았어요. 제 말은, 그 말은 당신 곁에 이토록 가까이 있는, 저 같은 형언할 수 없는 행복은 누리지 못했다는 거예요.\"\n\n도라는 그림 위로 고개를 숙이더니, 잠시 뒤에 말했다 — 그동안 나는 활활 타오르는 열병에 들뜬 채, 두 다리를 몹시 뻣뻣하게 굳히고 앉아 있었다 —\n\n\"오늘 낮의 어느 때엔, 그 행복을 정작 본인은 느끼지 못하는 것 같던데요.\"\n\n이제 나는 일이 벌어졌고, 당장 그 자리에서 해치워야 한다는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n\"당신은 그 행복에 조금도 관심이 없었잖아요.\" 도라가 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리며 고개를 가로젓고 말했다. \"키트 양 곁에 앉아 있을 때 말이에요.\"\n\n밝혀 두자면, 키트는 그 눈 작은 분홍 옷 차림의 처자 이름이었다.\n\n\"하긴 당신이 왜 그래야 하는지, 또 왜 그걸 행복이라고 부르는지 저야 모르지만요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"하지만 물론 당신은 마음에도 없는 말을 하는 거겠죠. 그리고 당신이 무엇이든 하고 싶은 대로 할 자유가 있다는 걸 의심하는 사람은 분명 아무도 없을 거예요. 집, 이 못된 녀석, 이리 와!\"\n\n내가 어떻게 그랬는지 모르겠다. 나는 순식간에 해치웠다. 나는 집을 가로막았다. 나는 도라를 품에 안았다. 나는 청산유수로 말을 쏟아 냈다. 나는 단 한 마디도 막힘이 없었다. 나는 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지 말했다. 나는 그녀 없이는 죽고 말 거라고 말했다. 나는 그녀를 신처럼 떠받들고 숭배한다고 말했다. 그동안 집은 미친 듯이 짖어 댔다.\n\n도라가 고개를 떨구고 울며 떨자, 내 청산유수는 그만큼 더 거세졌다. 그녀가 자기를 위해 내가 죽기를 바란다면, 한마디만 하면 된다고, 나는 죽을 각오가 되어 있다고 했다. 도라의 사랑 없는 삶이란 어떤 조건으로도 가질 만한 것이 못 된다고 했다. 나는 그것을 견딜 수 없고, 견디지도 않겠노라고 했다. 나는 그녀를 처음 본 순간부터, 밤낮으로, 매 순간 그녀를 사랑해 왔다고 했다. 나는 그 순간에도 정신이 나갈 만큼 그녀를 사랑한다고 했다. 나는 언제까지나, 매 순간, 정신이 나갈 만큼 그녀를 사랑하리라고 했다. 옛날에도 연인들은 사랑했고, 앞으로도 연인들은 사랑하리라. 그러나 내가 도라를 사랑하듯 사랑한 연인은 없었고, 사랑할 수도, 사랑할 리도, 사랑할 마음도, 사랑해서도 안 되리라고 했다. 내가 더 미쳐 날뛸수록, 집은 더 짖어 댔다. 우리는 저마다 제 나름의 방식으로, 순간순간 더 미쳐 갔다.\n\n자, 자! 이윽고 도라와 나는 소파에 나란히 앉아, 제법 차분해져 있었고, 집은 도라의 무릎에 누워 나를 향해 평화롭게 눈을 끔뻑이고 있었다. 짐을 덜었다. 나는 더없는 황홀경에 잠겨 있었다. 도라와 나는 약혼했다.\n\n우리는 이것이 결혼으로 이어지리라는 막연한 생각쯤은 했던 것 같다. 분명 했을 것이다. 도라가, 자기 아빠의 동의 없이는 절대 결혼하지 않겠다고 못 박았으니까. 그러나 우리는 젊은 황홀경에 빠져, 정작 앞날을 내다보거나 지난날을 돌아보지는 않았던 것 같다. 철없는 현재 너머의 그 어떤 바람도 품지 않았다. 우리는 이 비밀을 스펜로 씨에게 숨기기로 했다. 하지만 분명 그때 나는, 거기에 무슨 떳떳하지 못한 구석이 있다는 생각은 결코 머릿속에 떠올리지 못했다.\n\n도라가 밀스 양을 찾으러 가서 그녀를 데리고 돌아왔을 때, 밀스 양은 여느 때보다 한층 더 수심에 잠겨 있었다 — 짐작건대, 방금 일어난 일에 기억의 동굴 속에 잠든 메아리를 깨우는 구석이 있었기 때문이리라. 그러나 밀스 양은 우리에게 축복을 내려 주고, 변치 않을 우정을 약속해 주었으며, 대체로 수도원에서 들려오는 목소리답게 우리에게 말해 주었다.\n\n얼마나 한가로운 시절이었던가! 얼마나 덧없고, 행복하고, 어리석은 시절이었던가!\n\n물망초로 만들 반지에 맞추려고 내가 도라의 손가락을 쟀을 때, 그리고 그 치수를 가져간 보석상이 내 속을 꿰뚫어 보고 주문 장부를 들여다보며 깔깔 웃고는, 그 파란 보석이 박힌 예쁜 노리개에 제멋대로 값을 매겼을 때 — 그 반지는 내 기억 속에서 도라의 손과 어찌나 깊이 얽혀 있던지, 어제 우연히 내 딸의 손가락에서 그와 똑같은 반지를 보았을 때, 내 가슴속에 한순간, 아픔과도 같은 떨림이 일었다!\n\n나의 비밀에 들떠, 나 자신에 대한 관심으로 가득 차 거리를 거닐며, 도라를 사랑하는, 또 그토록 사랑받는 그 고귀함을 느꼈을 때 — 어찌나 그러했던지, 설령 내가 허공을 걸었다 한들, 땅바닥을 기어 다니는 그 처지 못한 사람들 위로 그보다 더 높이 솟아오를 수는 없었으리라!\n\n우리가 광장의 정원에서 그 만남을 가지며, 그 칙칙한 정자 안에 앉아 어찌나 행복했던지, 나는 지금 이 순간까지도 다른 까닭은 하나 없이 그저 그 까닭만으로 런던의 참새들을 사랑하고, 그 그을린 깃털 속에서 열대의 깃을 보는 것이다! 우리가 처음으로 크게 다투었을 때 (약혼한 지 일주일도 안 되어서였다), 그리고 도라가 절망에 찬 삼각 모자 모양으로 접은 쪽지에 반지를 넣어 돌려보냈을 때, 그 쪽지에서 도라는 '우리의 사랑은 어리석음에서 시작되어 광기로 끝났다!'는 그 끔찍한 표현을 썼고, 그 무시무시한 말 때문에 나는 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 모든 게 끝났다고 부르짖었으니!\n\n밤을 틈타 내가 밀스 양에게 달려가, 압착기가 놓인 뒤편 부엌에서 남몰래 그녀를 만나, 우리 사이에 끼어들어 광기를 막아 달라고 애원했을 때. 밀스 양이 그 소임을 떠맡아 도라를 데리고 돌아와, 자신의 쓰라린 젊음이라는 설교단에서, 서로 양보하고 사하라 사막을 피하라고 우리를 타일렀을 때!\n\n우리가 울고, 화해하고, 다시 어찌나 큰 축복을 받았던지, 그 뒤편 부엌이, 압착기며 뭐며 죄다, 사랑의 신전으로 바뀌어, 거기서 우리가 밀스 양을 통해 편지를 주고받을 계획을 짰을 때, 날마다 양쪽에서 적어도 한 통씩은 꼭 주고받기로 했을 때!\n\n얼마나 한가로운 시절이었던가! 얼마나 덧없고, 행복하고, 어리석은 시절이었던가! 시간의 손아귀에 쥐어진 내 모든 시절 가운데, 단 한 번 되돌아보아 이만큼 흐뭇하게 미소 지을 수 있고, 이만큼 애틋하게 떠올릴 수 있는 시절은 다시없다.\n\n# CHAPTER 34. MY AUNT ASTONISHES ME\n\n도라와 약혼하자마자 나는 곧바로 아그네스에게 편지를 썼다. 긴 편지였는데, 내가 얼마나 큰 축복을 받았는지, 도라가 얼마나 사랑스러운 사람인지를 그녀가 알아주기를 바라며 애써 풀어냈다. 나는 아그네스에게, 이것을 언젠가 다른 무엇에 자리를 내줄 가벼운 열정쯤으로 여기지 말아 달라고, 우리가 예전에 농담거리로 삼곤 했던 풋내기 시절의 들뜬 공상과는 털끝만큼도 닮지 않았다고 간곡히 호소했다. 나는 그녀에게 그 깊이가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 깊다고 단언했고, 이런 감정은 일찍이 세상에 알려진 적이 없으리라는 내 믿음을 털어놓았다.\n\n어찌 된 일인지, 어느 화창한 저녁 열린 창가에 앉아 아그네스에게 편지를 쓰는 동안, 그 맑고 차분한 눈동자와 부드러운 얼굴에 대한 기억이 슬그머니 내 안으로 스며들면서, 요즘 내가 빠져 살던 그 부산함과 들뜬 마음에 — 내 행복마저도 어느 정도는 거기 물들어 있었는데 — 어찌나 평화로운 기운을 드리워 주던지, 나는 마음이 누그러져 눈물이 났다. 편지를 절반쯤 썼을 때, 나는 한 손에 머리를 괴고 앉아, 아그네스가 마치 내 본디 집을 이루는 한 요소이기라도 한 듯한 어렴풋한 환상을 마음속에 품고 있었던 기억이 난다. 마치 그녀의 존재만으로 거의 신성하게 느껴지는 그 집의 고요함 속에서라면, 도라와 나는 세상 어디에서보다 더 행복할 수밖에 없을 것 같았다. 마치 사랑이든, 기쁨이든, 슬픔이든, 희망이든, 실망이든, 그 어떤 감정 속에서든, 내 마음은 자연스레 그곳으로 향해 거기서 안식처와 가장 좋은 벗을 찾아내는 것만 같았다.\n\n스티어포스에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 다만 에밀리의 도주로 야머스에 깊은 슬픔이 닥쳤다는 것, 그리고 그 일에 얽힌 사정 때문에 나에게는 그것이 이중의 상처가 되었다는 것만 알렸다. 나는 그녀가 늘 진실을 얼마나 빨리 알아채는지 알고 있었고, 그녀가 결코 먼저 그 이름을 입에 올리지 않으리라는 것도 알고 있었다.\n\n이 편지에 대해, 나는 바로 다음 우편으로 답장을 받았다. 그것을 읽노라니, 아그네스가 나에게 말을 거는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 그 다정한 목소리가 귓가에 울리는 것만 같았다. 무슨 말을 더 하겠는가!\n\n요즘 내가 집을 비운 사이, 트래들스가 두세 번 다녀갔다. 그는 안에 패거티가 있는 것을 발견하고, 또 패거티가 (받아 줄 사람만 있으면 누구에게나 자청해서 일러 주던 정보였다) 자기가 내 옛 유모라고 알려 주자, 그녀와 화기애애한 사이가 되어, 나에 대해 잠깐 도란도란 이야기를 나누다 가곤 했다. 패거티 말로는 그랬다. 하지만 내 짐작에 그 도란도란은 죄다 패거티 혼자만의 것이었고, 또 도가 지나치게 길었을 것이다. 나를 화제로 삼았을 때의 그녀는, 하느님 그녀를 축복하시길, 정말이지 멈추게 하기가 여간 어렵지 않았으니 말이다.\n\n이 일로 떠오르는 것은, 트래들스가 직접 정한 어느 날 오후 — 바로 오늘이 그날이었다 — 에 그를 기다리고 있었다는 사실만이 아니라, 크럽 부인이 패거티가 더는 모습을 드러내지 않을 때까지 자기 직무에 딸린 일을 (봉급만 빼고) 죄다 손에서 놓아 버렸다는 사실이었다. 크럽 부인은 계단에서 — 겉으로 보아서는 어떤 보이지 않는 정령을 상대로, 그런 때면 육신으로 따져 그녀는 늘 혼자였으므로 — 아주 새된 목소리로 패거티에 관한 여러 대화를 나눈 끝에, 자기 견해를 펼쳐 보이는 편지 한 통을 나에게 보냈다. 그 편지는 그녀의 인생 모든 일에 두루 들어맞는, 어디에나 통하는 그 진술로 시작했는데, 곧 자기도 한 사람의 어미라는 것이었다. 이어서 그녀는, 자기도 한때는 지금과 사뭇 다른 시절을 누려 본 적이 있으되, 평생 어느 시기에도 첩자며 침입자며 밀고자라면 천성적으로 질색이었다고 알려 주었다. 이름을 대지는 않겠노라고 그녀는 말했다. 모자가 맞는 자가 있거든 알아서 쓰면 그만이라고. 하지만 첩자며 침입자며 밀고자, 특히 과부의 상복을 걸친 자들이라면 (이 구절에는 밑줄이 그어져 있었다) 자기는 늘 멸시하는 데 이골이 나 있었다고 했다. 만약 어떤 신사가 첩자며 침입자며 밀고자의 희생양이 된다면 (그래도 이름은 대지 않았다), 그건 그 신사의 취향이라는 것이었다. 그에게는 제 좋을 대로 할 권리가 있으니, 마음대로 하시라는 거였다. 다만 자기, 크럽 부인이 못 박아 두고 싶은 것은, 자기가 그런 자들과 '결탁'당하는 일은 없어야 한다는 것뿐이라고 했다. 그러므로 그녀는, 모든 일이 예전 같아지고 또 마땅히 바랄 만한 상태로 돌아갈 때까지, 꼭대기 층 살림을 더는 돌보지 못함을 양해해 달라고 청했다. 덧붙여, 토요일 아침마다 자기 작은 외상장부가 식탁 위에 놓여 있을 터이니 즉시 정산해 주기를 바란다고, 모든 당사자의 수고와 '불편함'을 덜어 주려는 갸륵한 뜻에서 그리한다고 적었다.\n\n이런 일이 있은 뒤로, 크럽 부인은 계단에 함정을 파는 일에만, 그것도 주로 물주전자를 동원해 매달리며, 패거티를 꾀어 다리라도 부러뜨려 보려고 갖은 애를 썼다. 나는 이런 포위 상태로 살아가는 것이 꽤나 괴로웠지만, 크럽 부인이 너무도 무서운 탓에 거기서 벗어날 길을 도무지 찾지 못했다.\n\n\"이보게 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 이 온갖 장애물에도 아랑곳없이 정확히 제시간에 내 집 문 앞에 나타난 트래들스가 외쳤다. \"잘 지내나?\"\n\n\"이보게 친애하는 트래들스,\" 내가 말했다. \"드디어 자네를 보게 되어 정말 반갑네. 그리고 진작 집에 있지 못해서 정말 미안하이. 그런데 내가 워낙 정신없이 바빴던 터라—\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 알지.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"물론이고말고. 자네 사람은 런던에 산다지, 아마?\"\n\n\"방금 뭐라고 했나?\"\n\n\"그녀가 — 아 실례하네 — 그러니까 디 양 말일세,\" 트래들스가 그 지극한 조심성으로 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"런던에 산다지, 내가 알기로는?\"\n\n\"아 그래. 런던 근처라네.\"\n\n\"내 사람은, 자네도 기억할는지 모르겠네만,\" 트래들스가 자못 진지한 표정으로 말했다. \"데번셔 시골에 산다네 — 열 남매 중 하나지. 그러니 나는 자네만큼 바쁘질 못해 — 그런 뜻에서 말일세.\"\n\n\"그렇게 어쩌다 한 번씩밖에 못 보면서 어떻게 견디나,\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"하!\" 트래들스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"그게 참 신기하긴 하지. 아마도 그건, 코퍼필드, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없어서일 거야, 그렇지 않겠나?\"\n\n\"그렇겠지.\" 나는 미소 띤 채, 얼굴을 살짝 붉히며 대답했다. \"그리고 자네에게 한결같음과 인내심이 그토록 많아서이기도 하겠고, 트래들스.\"\n\n\"아이고 이런!\" 트래들스가 그 말을 곱씹으며 말했다. \"내가 자네 눈에 그렇게 보이나, 코퍼필드? 정말이지 나한테 그런 게 있는 줄은 몰랐는걸. 하지만 그녀 자신이 워낙 더없이 사랑스러운 처자라서, 어쩌면 그 미덕을 나한테도 조금 옮겨 주었는지 모르지. 자네가 그렇게 말하니, 코퍼필드, 그래도 조금도 이상할 게 없겠어. 정말이지 그녀는 늘 자기 자신은 잊은 채 나머지 아홉을 돌본다네.\"\n\n\"그녀가 맏이인가?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아이고 저런, 아닐세.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"맏이는 미인이지.\"\n\n내 짐작에 그는, 내가 그 천진한 대답에 웃음을 참지 못하는 것을 본 모양이었다. 그래서 자기 순박한 얼굴에도 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"물론, 내 소피가 — 예쁜 이름이지, 코퍼필드, 난 늘 그렇게 생각한다네 —\"\n\n\"아주 예쁜 이름이야!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"물론, 내 눈에는 소피도 아름답고, 또 (내 생각엔) 누구의 눈에 비추어 봐도 일찍이 둘도 없을 만큼 사랑스러운 처자임에는 틀림없지. 하지만 내가 맏이를 미인이라고 할 때는, 정말이지 그녀가 —\" 그는 두 손으로 제 둘레에 구름이라도 그려 보이는 듯한 시늉을 했다. \"눈부시다, 그런 뜻일세.\" 트래들스가 힘주어 말했다. \"허허!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 정말이라니까,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"정말이지 보기 드문 미인이야! 그런데 알다시피, 사교계와 뭇사람의 찬사를 받도록 태어났으면서도, 형편이 넉넉지 못한 탓에 그런 걸 마음껏 누리지 못하니, 자연히 가끔은 좀 짜증을 내고 까다롭게 굴기도 한다네. 그러면 소피가 그녀의 기분을 풀어 주지!\"\n\n\"소피가 막내인가?\" 내가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n\"아이고 저런, 아닐세!\" 트래들스가 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"막내 둘은 겨우 아홉 살, 열 살이라네. 소피가 그 애들을 가르치지.\"\n\n\"그럼 혹시 둘째인가?\" 내가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n\"아니야.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"둘째는 새러라네. 새러는 가엾게도 척추에 무슨 탈이 있어. 그 병은 차차 나아질 거라고 의사들이 그러긴 하는데, 그동안은 일 년쯤 누워 지내야 한다네. 소피가 그 애를 간병하지. 소피는 넷째라네.\"\n\n\"어머니는 살아 계신가?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아 그럼,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"살아 계시지. 정말이지 아주 훌륭한 분이신데, 다만 그 축축한 시골이 체질에 맞질 않아서 — 사실은, 손발을 못 쓰게 되셨다네.\"\n\n\"이런 저런!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"무척 딱한 일이지, 그렇지 않나?\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"하지만 그저 살림이라는 관점에서만 보면 그렇게까지 나쁘진 않다네. 소피가 그 자리를 대신해 주니까. 그녀는 어머니에게도 어머니나 다름없고, 나머지 아홉에게도 어머니나 다름없거든.\"\n\n나는 이 젊은 처자의 미덕에 더없는 감탄을 느꼈다. 그리고, 진심으로, 두 사람이 함께 꾸려 갈 인생의 앞날을 해치면서까지 트래들스의 그 선량함이 이용당하는 것을 막아 보려는 마음에서, 미코버 씨는 어떻게 지내는지 물었다.\n\n\"아주 잘 지내신다네, 코퍼필드, 물어봐 줘서 고맙군.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"지금은 내가 그분과 함께 살지 않아.\"\n\n\"아니라고?\"\n\"그래. 실은 말일세,\" 트래들스가 속삭이듯 말했다. \"그분이 일시적인 곤경 때문에 이름을 모티머로 바꾸셨다네. 그리고 날이 어두워진 뒤에야 밖에 나오시고 — 그것도 안경을 쓰고 말이야. 우리 집에 집세 때문에 압류가 들어왔거든. 미코버 부인이 어찌나 끔찍한 상태였던지, 나는 도무지 거절할 수가 없어서 여기서 우리가 이야기했던 그 두 번째 어음에 내 이름을 빌려 주고 말았지. 코퍼필드, 그 일이 그걸로 해결되고 미코버 부인이 기운을 되찾는 걸 보았을 때 내 마음이 얼마나 흐뭇했는지 자네도 짐작이 갈 걸세.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 내가 말했다. \"그렇다고 그분의 행복이 오래간 건 아니라네,\" 트래들스가 말을 이었다. \"불행히도, 일주일도 안 돼서 또 다른 압류가 들어왔거든. 그 바람에 살림이 통째로 거덜 났지. 그 뒤로 나는 가구 딸린 셋방에서 지내고 있고, 모티머 씨네는 정말이지 아주 은밀하게 숨어 지내고 있다네. 코퍼필드, 내가 이런 말을 한다고 이기적이라 여기진 말아 주게나. 그 거간꾼이 대리석 상판이 달린 내 작은 둥근 탁자며 소피의 화분이랑 받침대까지 죄다 가져가 버렸지 뭔가?\"\n\n\"그것참 너무한 일이로군!\" 나는 분개하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"그건 — 그건 참 곤혹스러운 일이었지.\" 트래들스가 그 표현이 나올 때면 늘 그러듯 움찔하며 말했다. \"그래도 나는 원망하는 마음에서가 아니라, 다 까닭이 있어서 이 얘기를 꺼내는 걸세. 사실은 말이야, 코퍼필드, 그게 압류당했을 때 나는 그걸 되살 형편이 못 됐다네. 첫째로는, 거간꾼이 내가 그걸 원한다는 낌새를 챘는지 값을 터무니없이 올려 버려서였고, 둘째로는, 내게 — 돈이 한 푼도 없어서였지. 그런데 그 뒤로 내가 줄곧 그 거간꾼 가게를 눈여겨봐 왔거든,\" 트래들스가 제 비밀스러운 계획을 무척이나 즐기며 말했다. \"그 가게가 토트넘 코트 로드 꼭대기에 있는데, 마침내 오늘, 그 물건들이 다시 팔려고 내놓인 걸 발견했지 뭔가. 길 건너에서만 슬쩍 봤는데, 거간꾼이 나를 봤다간, 아이고, 부르는 게 값이 될 게 뻔하니까 말일세! 이제 돈이 생겼으니 내가 떠올린 생각은 이거라네. 혹시 자네 그 착한 유모님에게 부탁해서 나랑 같이 그 가게에 가 달라고 해 줄 수 있겠나 — 다음 골목 모퉁이에서 가게를 가리켜 보여 드릴 테니 — 그래서 마치 자기가 쓸 물건인 양, 되도록 값을 잘 깎아서 사 주실 수 있다면 어떨까 싶어서!\"\n\n트래들스가 이 계획을 나에게 털어놓을 때의 그 기쁨이며, 그 계획이 어지간히 영리하다고 여기던 그 흐뭇함은, 내 기억 속에서 가장 생생하게 남아 있는 것들 가운데 하나다.\n\n나는 그에게, 우리 옛 유모가 기꺼이 도와줄 것이고, 우리 셋이서 함께 나서겠노라고 말했다. 다만 한 가지 조건이 있었다. 그 조건이란, 그가 다시는 자기 이름이든 그 무엇이든 미코버 씨에게 빌려 주지 않겠노라고 엄숙히 결심해야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n\"이보게 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"나는 이미 그렇게 결심했다네. 내가 그동안 경솔했을 뿐만 아니라, 소피에게 정말이지 부당하게 굴었구나 하는 생각이 들기 시작했거든. 나 자신에게 약속을 했으니 더는 걱정할 일이 없네만, 자네에게도 더없이 기꺼이 그 약속을 맹세하겠네. 그 첫 번째 운 나쁜 빚은 내가 갚았어. 미코버 씨도 갚을 수 있었다면 분명 갚았을 텐데, 그러질 못한 것뿐이지. 한 가지 꼭 말해 두고 싶은 게 있는데, 미코버 씨한테는 내가 무척 마음에 드는 점이 있다네, 코퍼필드. 그건 아직 만기가 안 된 두 번째 빚에 관한 건데 말이야. 그분은 그게 다 마련돼 있다고는 안 하셔. 다만 마련될 것이라고 하시지. 그런데 내 생각에 거기엔 뭔가 아주 공정하고 정직한 데가 있단 말일세!\"\n\n나는 이 착한 친구의 믿음에 찬물을 끼얹고 싶지 않았으므로, 그저 맞장구를 쳤다. 잠깐 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 우리는 패거티를 끌어들이려고 잡화점으로 향했다. 트래들스는 나와 함께 저녁을 보내기를 사양했는데, 자기가 그 물건들을 되사기도 전에 누가 채 가 버릴까 봐 마음이 잔뜩 조마조마했던 데다가, 그날 저녁은 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 처자에게 편지를 쓰는 날로 늘 정해 두었기 때문이었다.\n\n패거티가 그 소중한 물건들 값을 흥정하는 동안, 토트넘 코트 로드의 길모퉁이를 돌아 빠끔히 내다보던 그의 모습을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 또 그녀가 값을 불렀다가 헛물을 켜고 우리 쪽으로 천천히 걸어오는데, 마음이 누그러진 거간꾼이 그녀를 불러 세워 다시 되돌아가던 그때의 그의 안절부절못하던 모습도. 흥정의 끝은, 그녀가 그런대로 수월한 값에 그 물건들을 사들였다는 것이었고, 트래들스는 기쁨에 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다.\n\n\"정말이지 자네한테 신세를 많이 졌네.\" 그날 밤 그 물건들을 자기가 사는 곳으로 보내 주기로 했다는 말을 듣고 트래들스가 말했다. \"혹시 한 가지 부탁을 더 드려도 될까, 코퍼필드, 터무니없다고 여기진 않았으면 좋겠는데?\"\n\n나는 미리, 천만에, 결코 그렇지 않다고 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 친절을 베풀어,\" 트래들스가 패거티에게 말했다. \"지금 그 화분만 좀 받아 주시겠어요? 제 생각엔 (그게 소피 거라서요, 코퍼필드) 그건 제가 직접 집까지 들고 가고 싶거든요!\"\n\n패거티는 기꺼이 그것을 받아 그에게 건네주었고, 그는 고맙다는 인사를 잔뜩 퍼붓고는, 그 화분을 두 팔에 다정하게 끌어안은 채, 내가 일찍이 본 가운데 가장 기쁜 표정 가운데 하나를 지으며 토트넘 코트 로드를 따라 제 갈 길로 올라갔다.\n\n그러고 나서 우리는 내 거처 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다. 패거티에게는 상점들이 어찌나 매력적으로 보였던지, 나는 다른 누구에게서도 그만큼은 본 적이 없을 정도였는데, 나는 어슬렁어슬렁 느긋이 걸으며, 그녀가 진열창을 들여다보며 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨는 모습을 재미있어하고, 그녀가 원할 때마다 몇 번이고 기다려 주었다. 그렇게 우리는 애덜파이에 이르기까지 꽤 오랜 시간이 걸렸다.\n\n계단을 올라가다가, 나는 그녀에게 크럽 부인의 함정들이 갑자기 사라진 것과, 또 방금 누가 다녀간 듯한 발자국 자국들을 알려 주었다. 우리는 더 위로 올라가다가, 내가 분명히 닫아 두었던 바깥문이 활짝 열려 있고 안에서 사람 목소리가 들리는 것을 발견하고는 둘 다 무척 놀랐다.\n\n우리는 영문을 모른 채 서로를 마주 보고는 거실로 들어갔다. 세상 어느 누구도 아닌, 바로 우리 고모님과 딕 씨가 거기 있는 것을 발견했을 때 내 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가! 고모님은 한 무더기의 짐 위에 앉아, 앞에 새 두 마리를 두고, 무릎에는 고양이를 올려놓은 채, 마치 여자 로빈슨 크루소처럼 차를 마시고 있었다. 딕 씨는, 우리가 함께 곧잘 띄우러 나가곤 했던 그런 커다란 연에 생각에 잠긴 듯 기대선 채, 그 둘레에 더 많은 짐을 잔뜩 쌓아 두고 있었다!\n\n\"고모님!\" 내가 외쳤다. \"아니, 이게 웬 뜻밖의 기쁨이에요!\"\n\n우리는 다정하게 끌어안았다. 그리고 딕 씨와 나도 다정하게 악수를 나누었다. 한창 차를 끓이느라 분주하던 크럽 부인은, 더없이 살뜰하게 굴며, 코퍼풀 씨가 사랑하는 친척을 보면 가슴이 벅차오를 줄 진작 알고 있었노라고 다정스레 말했다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 고모님이, 그 위압적인 존재 앞에서 잔뜩 움츠러든 패거티에게 말했다. \"자넨 어떻게 지내나?\"\n\n\"고모님, 패거티 기억하시죠?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 얘야,\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"그 사람을 그런 남태평양 섬나라 이름으로 부르지 좀 마라! 결혼해서 그 이름을 떼어 냈다면, 그게 제일 잘한 일인데, 왜 그 바뀐 덕을 못 보게 한단 말이냐? 이제 자네 이름이 뭔가 — 피?\" 고모님은 그 거슬리는 호칭을 절충해 보려고 그렇게 물었다.\n\n\"바키스예요, 마님.\" 패거티가 무릎을 살짝 굽히며 말했다.\n\n\"옳지! 그게 사람 이름답구먼,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"선교사라도 보내 달라는 것처럼 들리진 않으니 말이야. 바키스, 어떻게 지내나? 잘 지내고 있겠지?\"\n\n이 너그러운 말에, 또 고모님이 손을 내미는 데 용기를 얻은 바키스는 앞으로 나아가, 그 손을 잡고, 무릎을 굽혀 고마움을 표했다.\n\n\"우리 둘 다 예전보다 나이를 먹었구먼,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"알다시피 우리가 전에 만난 건 딱 한 번뿐이었지. 그때 우리 둘이 참 잘도 일을 그르쳐 놓았더랬지! 트롯, 얘야, 한 잔 더 다오.\"\n\n나는 공손히 고모님께 찻잔을 건넸다. 고모님은 여느 때처럼 꼿꼿하기 그지없는 자세였는데, 나는 큰맘 먹고 상자 위에 앉아 계신 일에 대해 한마디 여쭈었다.\n\n\"제가 소파를 이리로 끌어 올게요, 아니면 안락의자라도요, 고모님,\" 내가 말했다. \"왜 그렇게 불편하게 계세요?\"\n\n\"고맙다, 트롯,\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"난 내 재산 위에 앉아 있는 게 더 좋다.\" 여기서 고모님은 크럽 부인을 매섭게 쏘아보며 한마디 했다. \"더는 수고스럽게 시중들 것 없네, 부인.\"\n\n\"가기 전에 찻주전자에 차를 조금 더 넣어 드릴까요, 마님?\" 크럽 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 됐네, 부인.\" 고모님이 대답했다.\n\n\"버터를 한 덩이 더 가져다 드릴까요, 마님?\" 크럽 부인이 말했다. \"아니면 갓 낳은 달걀이라도 한번 드셔 보시겠어요? 아니면 베이컨이라도 한 점 구워 올릴까요? 코퍼풀 씨, 사랑하는 고모님을 위해 제가 해 드릴 게 정말 아무것도 없을까요?\"\n\n\"아무것도 없네, 부인,\" 고모님이 받았다. \"나는 아주 잘 지낼 테니, 고맙네.\"\n\n크럽 부인은, 상냥한 성품을 드러내려고 쉴 새 없이 미소를 짓고, 타고난 허약한 체질을 드러내려고 쉴 새 없이 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고, 도움받을 자격 있는 모든 이에게 이바지하고 싶은 마음을 드러내려고 쉴 새 없이 두 손을 비비더니, 차츰차츰 미소를 짓고, 고개를 갸웃하고, 손을 비비며 방 밖으로 사라져 갔다. \"딕!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 자네한테 시류에 빌붙는 자들이며 재물을 떠받드는 자들에 대해 한 얘기 기억하지?\"\n\n딕 씨는 — 그걸 깜빡 잊기라도 한 듯 다소 겁먹은 표정으로 — 그렇다는 대답을 황급히 내놓았다.\n\n\"크럽 부인이 바로 그런 자들 중 하나야,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"바키스, 미안하지만 차 좀 맡아 줘. 그리고 한 잔 더 따라 줘. 저 여자가 따라 주는 건 영 내키지 않으니 말이야!\"\n\n나는 고모님을 충분히 잘 알고 있었으므로, 고모님 마음속에 뭔가 중대한 일이 자리 잡고 있다는 것, 그리고 이 갑작스러운 방문에는 모르는 사람이 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 깊은 사연이 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 나는, 내 주의가 딴 데 쏠려 있다고 여길 때면 고모님의 눈길이 어떻게 내게로 와 닿는지를 눈여겨보았다. 또 겉으로는 그 꼿꼿함과 침착함을 지키면서도, 그 속에서는 어떤 묘한 망설임의 과정이 진행되고 있는 듯한 것도 알아챘다. 나는 혹시 내가 고모님의 비위를 거스를 무슨 일이라도 한 건 아닌가 곰곰이 돌이켜 보기 시작했다. 그러자 양심이 나에게, 아직 고모님께 도라 이야기를 하지 않았다고 속삭였다. 혹시 어쩌다 그것 때문일까, 하고 나는 의아해했다!\n\n나는 고모님이 적당한 때가 되어야만 입을 열리라는 것을 알았으므로, 고모님 곁에 앉아, 새들에게 말을 걸고, 고양이를 어르며, 최대한 마음 편한 척했다. 그러나 나는 진짜로 마음이 편한 것과는 거리가 멀었다. 그리고 고모님 뒤편에서 그 커다란 연에 기대선 딕 씨가, 틈만 나면 몰래 나를 향해 음울하게 고개를 가로젓고 고모님을 손가락으로 가리키지 않았더라도, 나는 여전히 마음이 편치 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"트롯,\" 마침내 고모님이 차를 다 마시고, 옷자락을 정성스레 매만지고, 입술을 닦은 뒤 말했다. \"자넨 안 가도 되네, 바키스! 트롯, 너는 이제 꿋꿋하고 자기 힘으로 설 줄 알게 되었느냐?\"\n\n\"그러길 바라요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"네 생각엔 어떠냐?\" 베시 양이 물었다.\n\n\"그런 것 같아요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서, 얘야,\" 고모님이 나를 진지하게 바라보며 말했다. \"어째서 내가 오늘 밤 이 내 재산 위에 앉아 있기를 더 좋아한다고 보느냐?\"\n\n나는 짐작할 수 없어 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이게 내가 가진 전부니까. 왜냐하면 내가 망했으니까, 얘야!\"\n\n집이며 우리 모두가 한꺼번에 강물 속으로 굴러떨어졌다 해도, 그보다 더 큰 충격을 받기는 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n\"딕은 알고 있어,\" 고모님이 내 어깨에 차분히 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"나는 망했단다, 사랑하는 트롯! 그 오두막만 빼면, 내가 이 세상에서 가진 거라곤 죄다 이 방 안에 있어. 그 오두막은 자넷한테 세를 놓으라고 맡기고 왔지. 바키스, 오늘 밤 이 도련님이 잘 침대를 좀 마련해 주면 좋겠네. 비용을 아끼게, 나는 여기 아무거나 좀 깔아서 자도 되니까. 무엇이든 상관없어. 그저 하룻밤뿐인걸. 이 일은 내일 더 이야기하자꾸나.\"\n\n나는 고모님을 향한 놀라움과 걱정에서 — 정말이지, 고모님이 걱정되어서였다 — 정신이 번쩍 들었는데, 고모님이 잠깐 내 목을 끌어안고는, 자기는 오직 나 때문에 슬플 뿐이라고 흐느꼈기 때문이었다. 다음 순간 고모님은 그 감정을 억누르고는, 풀이 죽었다기보다는 오히려 의기양양한 낯빛으로 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 역경을 용감하게 맞서야 한다, 그리고 그것에 겁먹어선 안 돼, 얘야. 우리는 이 연극을 끝까지 해내는 법을 배워야 해. 우리는 불행을 이겨 내고 꿋꿋이 살아가야 한다, 트롯!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 35. DEPRESSION\n\n고모님의 이야기가 안긴 첫 충격에 정신이 그만 송두리째 달아났다가, 겨우 마음을 추스르자마자 나는 딕 씨에게 양초 가게로 가서 패거티 씨가 얼마 전까지 쓰다 비운 침대를 차지하라고 권했다. 양초 가게는 헝거퍼드 시장에 있었는데, 그 무렵 헝거퍼드 시장은 지금과는 사뭇 다른 곳이었다. 가게 문 앞에는 나지막한 나무 회랑이 있었는데(옛날 날씨 알리는 유리 기압계 속에 사는 작은 남자와 여자의 집 앞에 있던 것과 그리 다르지 않았다), 이것이 딕 씨를 무척이나 기쁘게 했다. 이런 구조물 위에서 묵는다는 영광이라면, 감히 말하건대 어지간한 불편쯤은 다 상쇄하고도 남았을 것이다. 그러나 사실 견뎌야 할 불편이라곤 앞서 말한 그 뒤섞인 냄새들과, 어쩌면 팔꿈치 놀릴 자리가 조금 더 있었으면 싶은 것 말고는 별로 없었으니, 딕 씨는 자기 숙소에 완전히 반해 버렸다. 크룹 부인은 거기는 고양이를 휘둘러 돌릴 공간조차 없다고 분개하며 장담했지만, 딕 씨가 침대 발치에 앉아 다리를 감싸 쥐고서 나에게 정당하게 지적했듯이 말이다. \"이봐요, 트롯우드, 난 고양이를 휘두를 생각이 없어요. 난 고양이를 휘둘러 본 적이 한 번도 없다고요. 그러니 그게 나한테 무슨 상관이겠어요!\"\n\n나는 딕 씨가 고모님의 살림에 일어난 이 갑작스럽고 엄청난 변화의 원인을 조금이라도 알고 있는지 알아보려고 했다. 그러나 예상했던 대로, 그는 전혀 알지 못했다. 그가 들려줄 수 있는 설명이라고는, 그저께 고모님이 그에게 \"이봐요 딕, 당신은 정말이지 내가 생각하는 그런 철학자가 맞아요?\"라고 물었다는 것뿐이었다. 그러자 그가 \"네, 그러길 바라지요\"라고 대답했고, 그러자 고모님이 \"딕, 나 망했어요\"라고 했고, 그러자 그가 \"아, 그래요!\"라고 했고, 그러자 고모님이 그를 한껏 칭찬해 주어서 그가 기뻤다는 것이다. 그러고 나서 둘이 나에게로 왔는데, 오는 길에 병맥주와 샌드위치를 먹었다는 것이었다.\n\n딕 씨는 침대 발치에 앉아 다리를 감싸 쥔 채, 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 놀란 미소를 지으며 이 이야기를 나에게 들려주는 모습이 어찌나 흡족해 보였던지, 나는 말하기 부끄럽지만 그만 발끈해서 그에게 망했다는 것은 곤궁과 결핍과 굶주림을 뜻한다고 설명하고 말았다. 그러나 나는 이 모진 말에 곧 쓰라린 책망을 받았다. 그의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리고 길쭉해진 두 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내리는 것을 보았기 때문인데, 그가 나에게 던진 그 형언할 수 없는 비탄의 눈길은 나보다 훨씬 모진 마음마저 누그러뜨릴 만했다. 나는 그를 풀이 죽게 만들 때보다 다시 기운을 북돋아 주는 데 한없이 더 큰 공을 들였다. 그리고 이내 깨달았는데(처음부터 알았어야 마땅했다), 그가 그토록 태평했던 것은 단지 세상에서 가장 현명하고 가장 놀라운 여인에 대한 믿음과, 내 지적 능력에 대한 무한한 의지 때문이었다. 후자에 대해서는, 짐작건대 그는 절대적으로 치명적이지만 않다면 어떤 종류의 재앙이든 능히 상대할 수 있는 것으로 여기는 듯했다.\n\n\"우리가 어떻게 하면 좋겠어요, 트롯우드?\" 딕 씨가 물었다. \"그 청원서가 있잖아요—\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 물론이에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 지금 우리가 할 수 있는 거라곤, 딕 씨, 밝은 낯빛을 지키면서 고모님이 우리가 이 일을 걱정한다는 걸 눈치채지 못하게 하는 거예요.\"\n\n그는 더없이 진지하게 이 말에 동의했고, 혹시라도 내가 보기에 그가 한 치라도 옳은 길에서 벗어나 헤매거든 언제든 내가 부릴 수 있는 그 뛰어난 방법들로 자기를 불러 세워 달라고 간청했다. 그러나 유감스럽게도, 내가 그에게 안긴 두려움은 감추려는 그의 갖은 노력으로도 도무지 감당이 안 되는 것이었다. 저녁 내내 그의 눈은 마치 고모님이 그 자리에서 점점 야위어 가는 것을 보기라도 하는 양, 더없이 음울한 근심을 담고 고모님의 얼굴로 자꾸만 향했다. 그도 이것을 의식하고 머리를 꼼짝 않게 붙들려고 애썼지만, 머리를 그렇게 고정한 채 앉아 기계 장치처럼 눈알만 굴려 대니 사정은 조금도 나아지지 않았다. 나는 그가 저녁 식사 때 빵 한 덩이를 (마침 작은 덩이였다) 바라보는 것을 보았는데, 그것 말고는 우리와 굶주림 사이에 아무것도 가로막은 게 없기라도 한 듯한 눈빛이었다. 그리고 고모님이 그에게 늘 하던 대로 식사를 하라고 고집하자, 나는 그가 빵과 치즈 조각을 주머니에 슬쩍 챙겨 넣는 현장을 잡아냈다. 분명 우리가 야윌 대로 야윈 지경에 이르렀을 때 그렇게 아껴 둔 것으로 우리를 되살리려는 속셈이었으리라.\n\n반면에 고모님은 차분한 마음가짐을 유지하고 있었는데, 이것은 우리 모두에게—적어도 나에게는 분명히—하나의 가르침이었다. 고모님은 패거티에게 더없이 자상했는데, 다만 내가 무심코 그녀를 그 이름으로 부를 때만은 예외였다. 그리고 런던에서 영 낯설어한다는 것을 내가 알고 있었음에도, 고모님은 아주 마음 편안한 듯이 보였다. 고모님은 내 침대를 쓰기로 했고, 나는 거실에 누워 고모님을 지키기로 했다. 고모님은 화재가 났을 경우를 대비해 강가에 이토록 가까이 있다는 것을 대단히 중요하게 여겼고, 짐작건대 정말로 그 점에서 어떤 만족을 느끼는 듯했다.\n\n\"트롯, 얘야.\" 내가 고모님이 늘 마시는 잠자리 음료를 타려고 채비하는 것을 보고는 고모님이 말했다. \"안 돼!\"\n\"아무것도 안 마시게요, 고모님?\"\n\n\"포도주는 안 돼, 얘야. 에일로.\"\n\n\"하지만 여기 포도주가 있는걸요, 고모님. 게다가 늘 포도주로 만들어 드시잖아요.\"\n\n\"그건 아플 때를 위해 남겨 둬.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"함부로 써서는 안 돼, 트롯. 나한텐 에일로 줘. 반 파인트.\"\n\n나는 딕 씨가 정신을 잃고 쓰러지는 줄 알았다. 고모님이 단호하니, 나는 직접 나가서 에일을 사 왔다. 밤이 이슥해지고 있던 터라, 패거티와 딕 씨는 이 기회를 틈타 함께 양초 가게로 물러갔다. 나는 가엾은 그와 길모퉁이에서 헤어졌는데, 그는 커다란 연을 등에 둘러멘 채 마치 인간 비참함의 기념비 같았다.\n\n내가 돌아왔을 때 고모님은 손가락으로 잠자리 모자의 가장자리를 잡아 주름을 잡으며 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 나는 늘 하던 어김없는 방식대로 에일을 데우고 토스트를 만들었다. 그것이 준비되자, 고모님도 잠자리 모자를 쓰고 가운 자락을 무릎 위로 걷어 올린 채 그것을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"얘야.\" 한 숟가락 떠먹고 나서 고모님이 말했다. \"이게 포도주보다 훨씬 낫구나. 속이 절반도 안 거북해.\"\n\n내가 미심쩍은 표정을 지었던 모양인지, 고모님이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"쯧, 쯧, 얘야. 우리한테 에일보다 더 나쁜 일이 안 닥친다면, 우린 형편이 좋은 거야.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇게 생각해요, 고모님, 정말이에요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 그러면, 왜 그렇게 생각을 안 하는데?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"고모님하고 저는 아주 다른 사람이니까요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"쓸데없는 소리, 트롯!\" 고모님이 받아쳤다.\n\n고모님은 가식이라곤 거의, 아니 조금도 없는 조용한 즐거움 속에서 음료를 들었다. 따뜻한 에일을 찻숟가락으로 떠먹으며, 토스트 조각을 그 안에 적셔 가면서.\n\n\"트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"난 대체로 낯선 얼굴을 좋아하지 않는데, 네 그 바키스라는 여자는 어쩐지 마음에 들지 뭐냐, 그거 아니?\"\n\n\"고모님 입에서 그 말을 듣다니 백 파운드보다 더 값지네요!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"참 별난 세상이야.\" 고모님이 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"그 여자가 어쩌다 그런 이름을 달고 세상에 나왔는지 도무지 알 수가 없어. 차라리 잭슨이나 뭐 그런 이름으로 태어나는 게 훨씬 쉬웠을 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그 여자도 그렇게 생각할지 몰라요. 그게 그 여자 잘못은 아니잖아요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지.\" 고모님이 다소 마지못해 인정하며 받았다. \"하지만 정말 속 터지는 일이야. 그래도 이제 바키스가 됐으니. 그나마 다행이지. 바키스가 너를 끔찍이도 아끼더구나, 트롯.\"\n\n\"그걸 증명하기 위해서라면 그 여자가 못 할 일이 없을 거예요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"못 할 일이 없겠지, 정말로.\" 고모님이 받았다. \"여기, 그 가엾은 바보가 자기 돈을 좀 넘겨받아 달라고 빌고 또 빌었지 뭐냐—돈이 너무 많다면서. 어리석은 것!\"\n\n고모님의 기쁨에 겨운 눈물이 정말이지 따뜻한 에일 속으로 뚝뚝 떨어지고 있었다.\n\n\"그 여자는 세상에 태어난 사람 중에 제일 우스꽝스러운 인간이야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"네 그 가엾고 사랑스러운 아기 같은 어미와 함께 있는 모습을 처음 본 순간부터 나는 알았어, 그 여자가 인간 중에 제일 우스꽝스럽다는 걸. 하지만 바키스한테도 좋은 구석이 있어!\"\n\n웃는 척하면서, 고모님은 손을 눈으로 가져갈 기회를 얻었다. 그 기회를 한껏 이용하고 나서, 고모님은 토스트와 이야기를 함께 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n\"아! 우리에게 자비를!\" 고모님이 한숨을 쉬었다. \"난 다 알아, 트롯! 네가 딕하고 나가 있는 동안 바키스랑 나랑 한참 수다를 떨었거든. 난 다 안다고. 그 가엾은 계집애들이 대체 어디로 가게 될 거라고 생각하는지, 나로서는 알 수가 없어. 차라리 머리를 박살 내 버리지 않는 게 신기하다니까—저기 벽난로 선반에다가.\" 고모님이 말했는데, 이 발상은 아마도 자기 집 벽난로 선반을 떠올린 데서 비롯된 듯했다.\n\n\"가엾은 에밀리!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 가엾다는 소리는 내 앞에서 하지 마라.\" 고모님이 받았다. \"그렇게 많은 사람을 불행하게 만들기 전에 진작 그 생각을 했어야지! 입 좀 맞춰 다오, 트롯. 네가 어린 나이에 그런 일을 겪다니 안됐구나.\"\n\n내가 몸을 앞으로 숙이자, 고모님은 나를 붙들어 두려고 술잔을 내 무릎 위에 올려놓고 말했다.\n\n\"오, 트롯, 트롯! 그래서 네가 사랑에 빠졌다고 여기는 거냐? 그래?\"\n\n\"여기다니요, 고모님!\" 나는 더 붉어질 수 없을 만큼 새빨개져서 외쳤다. \"저는 온 영혼을 다해 그녀를 흠모해요!\"\n\n\"도라란 말이지!\" 고모님이 받았다. \"그러니까 그 조그만 것이 아주 매혹적이라고, 그렇게 말하고 싶은 거지?\"\n\n\"고모님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"아무도 그녀가 어떤 사람인지 손톱만큼도 짐작 못 할 거예요!\"\n\n\"아! 그리고 어리석지는 않고?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"어리석다니요, 고모님!\"\n진심으로 말하건대, 그녀가 어리석은지 아닌지 따져 볼 생각은 단 한 순간도 내 머릿속에 떠오른 적이 없었다. 물론 나는 그 발상에 발끈했지만, 어쩐지 그것이 아주 새로운 것으로 다가와 적잖이 충격을 받았다.\n\n\"머리가 가볍지는 않고?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"머리가 가볍다니요, 고모님!\" 나는 앞서 던진 질문에 느꼈던 것과 똑같은 종류의 심정으로 이 당돌한 억측을 그저 되뇌는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그냥 물어본 거야. 그 애를 깎아내리려는 게 아니야. 가엾은 어린 한 쌍! 그래서 너희 둘은 서로를 위해 만들어졌다고, 두 개의 예쁜 과자처럼 잔치 음식상 같은 인생을 살아갈 거라고 생각하는 거지, 트롯?\"\n\n고모님이 어찌나 다정하게, 절반은 장난스럽고 절반은 슬픈 그 부드러운 태도로 이것을 물었던지, 나는 마음이 자못 뭉클했다.\n\n\"우리가 젊고 미숙하다는 건 저도 알아요, 고모님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"그리고 우리가 다소 어리석은 말과 생각을 꽤 많이 한다는 것도 감히 인정해요. 하지만 우리는 진실로 서로를 사랑해요, 분명히요. 만약 도라가 다른 누구를 사랑하게 된다거나 저를 사랑하기를 멈춘다면, 혹은 제가 다른 누구를 사랑하게 된다거나 그녀를 사랑하기를 멈춘다면—저는 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요—제정신이 아니게 될 거예요, 분명히!\"\n\n\"아, 트롯!\" 고모님이 고개를 저으며, 진지하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"눈멀었구나, 눈멀었어, 눈멀었어!\"\n\n\"내가 아는 누군가는 말이다, 트롯.\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"성품은 무척 유순하지만, 그 안에 가엾은 아기를 떠올리게 하는 진실한 애정이 있어. 진실함이야말로 그 누군가가 자기를 지탱하고 더 나은 사람으로 만들기 위해 찾아야 할 거야, 트롯. 깊고, 곧고, 한결같은 진실함 말이다.\"\n\n\"고모님이 도라의 진실함을 아시기만 한다면요!\" 내가 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 트롯!\" 고모님이 다시 말했다. \"눈멀었어, 눈멀었어!\" 그리고 영문도 모른 채, 나는 무언가를 잃었거나 무언가가 모자란다는 어렴풋하고 불행한 느낌이 구름처럼 나를 뒤덮는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"하지만 말이다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나는 두 젊은것이 자기네 마음을 의심하게 만들거나 불행하게 만들고 싶지는 않아. 그러니, 비록 이것이 풋사랑이고, 풋사랑이란 게 흔히—명심해라! 언제나 그렇다는 건 아니야!—아무 결실 없이 끝나기도 하지만, 그래도 우린 이걸 진지하게 받아들이고, 언젠가 좋은 결말이 나기를 바라기로 하자. 무언가로 무르익는 데는 시간이 넉넉하니까!\"\n\n이것은 전체적으로 황홀경에 빠진 연인에게 그리 위안이 되는 말은 아니었지만, 나는 고모님이 내 속내를 알아주는 것이 기뻤고, 또 고모님이 지쳐 있다는 것을 염두에 두었다. 그래서 나는 이런 애정의 표시에 대해, 그리고 나를 향한 다른 모든 친절에 대해 고모님에게 뜨겁게 감사를 드렸다. 그러고는 다정한 작별 인사를 나눈 뒤, 고모님은 잠자리 모자를 들고 내 침실로 들어갔다.\n\n자리에 누웠을 때 나는 얼마나 비참했던가! 나는 얼마나 생각하고 또 생각했던가—내가 스펜로 씨 눈에 가난뱅이라는 것을, 도라에게 청혼했을 때 내가 스스로 생각하던 그런 사람이 아니라는 것을, 내 세속적 처지가 어떠한지를 도라에게 말하고 그녀가 원한다면 약혼에서 풀어 주어야 한다는 그 기사도적인 의무를, 아무것도 벌지 못하는 긴 견습 기간 동안 어떻게 살아갈 방도를 마련할지를, 고모님을 도울 무언가를 해야 하는데 그 무엇도 할 방도가 보이지 않는다는 것을, 주머니에 돈 한 푼 없는 신세로 전락해 초라한 외투를 걸치고, 도라에게 작은 선물 하나 가져다주지 못하고, 늠름한 회색 말을 타지도 못하고, 어디서도 멋진 모습을 보여 주지 못하게 된다는 것을! 내 자신의 곤경에 마음을 그토록 골몰하는 것이 비루하고 이기적이라는 걸 알면서도, 또 그것이 그렇다는 걸 알기에 스스로를 괴롭히면서도, 나는 도라에게 너무나 헌신적이어서 어찌할 수가 없었다. 고모님을 더 생각하고 나 자신을 덜 생각하지 않는 것이 비열한 짓임을 알았지만, 지금까지 이기심은 도라와 떼려야 뗄 수 없었고, 나는 그 어떤 사람을 위해서도 도라를 한쪽으로 밀어 둘 수가 없었다. 그날 밤 나는 얼마나 한없이 비참했던가!\n\n잠으로 말하자면, 나는 온갖 모양의 가난을 꿈꾸었는데, 어쩐지 잠드는 사전 의식도 없이 곧장 꿈을 꾸는 것 같았다. 어느새 나는 누더기를 걸친 채 도라에게 성냥을 팔려 하고 있었는데, 반 페니에 여섯 묶음이었다. 또 어느새 나는 잠옷에 장화 차림으로 사무소에 가 있었는데, 그런 산뜻한 옷차림으로 의뢰인들 앞에 나타났다고 스펜로 씨에게 핀잔을 들었다. 또 어느새 나는 늙은 티피가 세인트폴 성당이 한 시를 칠 때마다 어김없이 먹는 그 일과의 비스킷에서 떨어지는 부스러기를 허기진 채 주워 먹고 있었다. 또 어느새 나는 도라와 결혼할 허가증을 얻으려고 가망 없이 애쓰고 있었는데, 그 대가로 내밀 거라곤 유라이어 힙의 장갑 한 짝뿐이어서 민법 박사회 전체가 거절해 버렸다. 그러면서도 내 방을 어느 정도 의식하고 있는 채로, 나는 이불의 바다에서 조난당한 배처럼 늘 이리저리 뒤척이고 있었다.\n\n고모님도 잠을 이루지 못하고 있었으니, 나는 고모님이 이리저리 거니는 소리를 자주 들었다. 밤사이 두세 번, 키가 칠 척은 되어 보이게 만드는 긴 플란넬 실내복을 걸치고서, 고모님은 잠 못 든 유령처럼 내 방에 나타나, 내가 누운 소파 곁으로 다가왔다. 처음에 나는 놀라서 벌떡 일어났는데, 알고 보니 고모님은 하늘에 어린 어떤 특이한 빛을 보고 웨스트민스터 사원에 불이 났다고 짐작한 것이었고, 만약 바람이 바뀐다면 그 불이 버킹엄 거리로 옮겨붙을 가능성에 대해 내 의견을 듣고자 한 것이었다. 그 뒤로 가만히 누워 있자니, 고모님이 내 곁에 앉아 \"가엾은 것!\" 하고 혼잣말로 속삭이는 것을 알게 되었다. 그리고 그때, 고모님이 얼마나 사심 없이 나를 마음에 두는지, 또 내가 얼마나 이기적으로 나 자신만 마음에 두는지를 알게 되니, 나는 스무 배는 더 비참해졌다.\n\n나에게 그토록 길었던 밤이 다른 누군가에게는 짧을 수 있다는 것은 믿기 어려웠다. 이 생각을 곱씹다 보니 사람들이 밤이 새도록 춤추며 시간을 흘려보내는 어떤 가상의 잔치를 자꾸자꾸 떠올리게 되었고, 마침내 그것마저 꿈이 되어, 나는 음악이 쉬지 않고 한 곡만을 연주하는 소리를 듣고, 도라가 쉬지 않고 한 춤만을 추면서 나에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않는 것을 보았다. 밤새 하프를 켜던 남자가 보통 크기의 잠자리 모자로 그것을 덮어 보려고 헛되이 애쓰고 있을 때 나는 잠에서 깨어났다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하자면, 잠들려는 애를 그만두었을 때, 그리고 마침내 창문으로 햇살이 비쳐 드는 것을 보았을 때라고 해야겠다.\n\n그 무렵 스트랜드에서 갈라져 나온 어느 거리 끝에는 오래된 로마식 목욕탕이 있었는데—아마 지금도 거기 있을 것이다—나는 거기서 찬물에 몸을 던진 적이 여러 번 있었다. 나는 되도록 조용히 옷을 갈아입고, 고모님은 패거티에게 맡겨 둔 채, 그 안으로 머리부터 곤두박질치듯 뛰어든 다음, 햄프스테드로 산책을 나갔다. 나는 이 활기찬 처방이 머리를 조금이나마 맑게 해 주기를 바랐고, 실제로 효과가 있었던 것 같다. 곧 나는 내가 가장 먼저 밟아야 할 단계가, 견습 계약을 해지하고 그 사례금을 돌려받을 수 있는지 알아보는 것이라는 결론에 이르렀기 때문이다. 나는 히스 들판에서 아침을 좀 먹고, 물 뿌려진 길을 따라, 정원에서 자라 행상들의 머리에 이고 시내로 들어오는 여름 꽃들의 향긋한 냄새를 가로질러, 우리의 달라진 형편에 맞서려는 이 첫걸음에 골몰하며 민법 박사회로 걸어 돌아왔다.\n\n결국 너무 일찍 사무소에 도착하는 바람에, 늘 제일 먼저 나오는 늙은 티피가 열쇠를 들고 나타날 때까지 나는 박사회 근처에서 반 시간을 어슬렁거려야 했다. 그러고 나서 나는 내 그늘진 구석에 앉아, 맞은편 굴뚝 갓에 비친 햇살을 올려다보며 도라를 생각했다. 스펜로 씨가 산뜻하게, 머리를 곱슬곱슬 매만진 채로 들어올 때까지.\n\n\"잘 지냈나, 코퍼필드?\" 그가 말했다. \"좋은 아침이군!\"\n\n\"아름다운 아침입니다, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"법정에 들어가시기 전에 잠깐 말씀 좀 드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 방으로 들어오게.\"\n\n나는 그를 따라 방으로 들어갔고, 그는 가운을 걸치고 벽장 문 안쪽에 걸어 둔 작은 거울 앞에서 매무새를 다듬기 시작했다.\n\n\"유감스럽지만,\" 내가 말했다. \"고모님께 다소 낙담스러운 소식을 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 그가 말했다. \"이런! 설마 중풍은 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"건강과는 무관한 일입니다, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"고모님께서 큰 손실을 보셨어요. 사실, 거의 남은 게 없으세요, 정말로요.\"\n\n\"이거 놀라운데, 코퍼필드!\" 스펜로 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n나는 고개를 저었다. \"정말입니다, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"고모님 형편이 너무나 바뀌어서, 여쭤 보고 싶었습니다. 혹시—물론 저희 쪽에서 사례금의 일부를 포기하는 조건으로요.\" 그의 멍한 표정에 경고를 받아, 나는 순간적으로 이 말을 끼워 넣었다. \"—제 견습 계약을 해지하는 것이 가능할지요?\"\n\n이 제안을 꺼내는 데 내가 얼마나 큰 대가를 치렀는지는 아무도 모른다. 그것은 마치 호의를 베풀어 달라며, 도라에게서 추방되는 형을 선고해 달라고 청하는 것과 같았다.\n\n\"자네 견습 계약을 해지한다고, 코퍼필드? 해지?\"\n\n나는 제법 단호하게, 스스로 생계를 벌지 못한다면 도무지 어디서 살림 밑천이 나올지 알 수가 없다고 설명했다. 미래에 대해서는 아무 두려움이 없습니다, 라고 나는 말했고—마치 언젠가는 여전히 사윗감으로 손색없는 사람이 되리라는 뜻을 넌지시 비치려는 듯, 그 점을 한껏 강조했다—하지만 당장은 제 힘만으로 버텨야 하는 처지가 되었다고 했다. \"이런 말을 듣게 되어 대단히 유감일세, 코퍼필드.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"대단히 유감이야. 그런 이유로 견습 계약을 해지하는 건 흔한 일이 아닐세. 직업적으로 온당한 절차도 아니고. 편리한 선례라곤 결코 아니야. 천만의 말씀이지. 그렇지만 동시에—\"\n\n\"정말 자비로우십니다, 선생님.\" 나는 양보를 기대하며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"천만에. 그런 말 말게.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지만 동시에, 내가 말하려던 건, 만약 내 두 손이 자유로운 처지였다면—만약 나에게 동업자가 없었다면—조킨스 씨 말일세—\"\n\n내 희망은 순식간에 산산조각이 났지만, 나는 다시 한번 시도해 보았다.\n\n\"선생님, 혹시,\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 조킨스 씨께 그 이야기를 꺼내 본다면—\"\n\n스펜로 씨가 맥 빠지게 고개를 저었다. \"당치도 않네, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답했다. \"내가 누군가에게 부당한 짓을 하다니, 하물며 조킨스 씨에게라니. 하지만 나는 내 동업자를 알아, 코퍼필드. 조킨스 씨는 이런 특이한 성격의 제안에 응할 사람이 아닐세. 조킨스 씨는 정해진 길에서 한 발짝도 움직이게 하기가 무척 어려운 사람이야. 자네도 그가 어떤 사람인지 알잖나!\"\n\n사실 나는 그에 대해 아는 게 전혀 없었다. 다만 그가 본래 혼자서 이 사업을 했다는 것, 지금은 몬터규 광장 근처의, 페인트칠이 끔찍하게 필요한 집에서 혼자 산다는 것, 날마다 아주 늦게 와서 아주 일찍 간다는 것, 무슨 일이든 그에게 의논하는 법이 없어 보인다는 것, 그리고 위층에 자기만의 침침하고 자그마한 골방을 두고 있는데 거기서는 도무지 아무 일도 처리된 적이 없고, 책상 위에는 잉크 한 방울 묻지 않은, 스무 살은 됐다고 소문난 누런 옛 종이 받침대가 놓여 있다는 것 정도였다.\n\n\"제가 그분께 그 이야기를 꺼내도 괜찮으시겠습니까, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"무방하지.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 나는 조킨스 씨를 좀 겪어 봤네, 코퍼필드. 그렇지 않았으면 좋겠어, 어떤 식으로든 자네 뜻에 부응할 수 있다면 나야 기쁠 테니까. 자네가 굳이 그럴 만하다고 여긴다면, 조킨스 씨에게 그 이야기를 꺼내는 데 반대하진 않겠네, 코퍼필드.\"\n\n이 허락을—따뜻한 악수와 함께 주어진 허락이었다—이용하기로 하고, 나는 앉아서 도라를 생각하며, 맞은편 집 벽을 따라 굴뚝 갓에서 슬며시 내려오는 햇살을 바라보았다. 조킨스 씨가 올 때까지. 그러고 나서 나는 조킨스 씨의 방으로 올라갔는데, 내가 거기 나타난 것이 조킨스 씨를 무척이나 놀라게 한 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"들어오게, 코퍼필드 군.\" 조킨스 씨가 말했다. \"들어와요!\"\n\n나는 안으로 들어가 앉았고, 스펜로 씨에게 했던 것과 거의 똑같이 내 사정을 조킨스 씨에게 이야기했다. 조킨스 씨는 결코 사람들이 예상할 법한 무시무시한 인물이 아니라, 코담배를 어찌나 많이 피우는지 박사회에는 그가 주로 그 자극제로 연명하며 다른 음식이 들어갈 자리가 별로 없다는 전설까지 있는, 덩치 크고 온화하며 얼굴이 매끈한 예순 살 노인이었다.\n\n\"이 이야기를 스펜로 씨에게 했겠지?\" 내 말을 무척 안절부절못하며 끝까지 듣고 나서 조킨스 씨가 말했다.\n\n나는 그렇다고 대답하고, 스펜로 씨가 그분의 이름을 거론했다고 말했다.\n\n\"내가 반대할 거라고 하던가?\" 조킨스 씨가 물었다.\n\n나는 스펜로 씨가 그럴 가능성이 높다고 여겼음을 인정할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"유감스럽지만, 코퍼필드 군, 나는 자네의 목적을 도울 수가 없네.\" 조킨스 씨가 안절부절못하며 말했다. \"사실은—그런데 내가 은행에 약속이 있어서, 양해해 주면 고맙겠네만.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 몹시 서둘러 일어나 방을 나서려 했고, 나는 용기를 내어 그렇다면 이 일을 처리할 방도가 없는 것 같아 두렵다고 말했다.\n\n\"없네!\" 조킨스 씨가 문가에 멈춰 서서 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"아, 없어! 내가 반대하니까 말이야.\" 그는 이 말을 아주 빠르게 내뱉고는 나가 버렸다. \"자네도 알겠지만, 코퍼필드 군.\" 그가 다시 문 안으로 안절부절못하며 들여다보면서 덧붙였다. \"스펜로 씨가 반대한다면—\"\n\n\"개인적으로는 그분이 반대하지 않으십니다, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 개인적으로!\" 조킨스 씨가 조바심 난 투로 되뇌었다. \"분명히 말하건대 반대가 있네, 코퍼필드 군. 가망 없어! 자네가 바라는 일은 될 수가 없어. 나—나는 정말 은행에 약속이 있어서.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 그야말로 줄행랑을 쳤다. 그리고 내가 아는 한, 그가 박사회에 다시 모습을 드러낸 것은 사흘이 지나서였다.\n\n나는 어떤 수단도 빠뜨리지 않으려는 마음이 간절해서, 스펜로 씨가 들어올 때까지 기다렸다가 그간의 경위를 들려주었다. 그리고 그가 마음만 먹는다면 그 쇠처럼 단단한 조킨스를 누그러뜨릴 수 있으리라는 희망을 버리지 않았다는 뜻을 그에게 내비쳤다.\n\n\"코퍼필드,\" 스펜로 씨가 자비로운 미소를 지으며 받았다. \"자네는 내 동업자 조킨스 씨를 나만큼 오래 알지 못했네. 조킨스 씨에게 조금이라도 술수가 있다고 돌리는 것만큼 내 생각과 동떨어진 일은 없어. 하지만 조킨스 씨에게는 자기 반대를 늘어놓는 방식이 있는데, 그게 사람들을 종종 속인단 말이지. 안 돼, 코퍼필드!\" 그가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"조킨스 씨는 움직일 수가 없어, 내 말을 믿게!\"\n\n나는 스펜로 씨와 조킨스 씨 둘 중 누가 정말로 반대하는 동업자인지를 두고 완전히 어리둥절해졌다. 그러나 이 회사 어딘가에 완고함이 도사리고 있다는 것, 그리고 고모님의 천 파운드를 되찾는 일은 가망이 없다는 것만은 충분히 또렷이 알 수 있었다. 지금 떠올려도 도무지 흡족하지 않은 낙담 속에서—그것이 여전히 (언제나 도라와 결부되어 있긴 했으나) 나 자신에 관한 것이 너무 많았음을 알기에 그렇다—나는 사무소를 나와 집으로 향했다.\n\n나는 마음을 최악의 상황에 길들이려고, 그리고 앞으로 우리가 마련해야 할 채비를 가장 가혹한 모습 그대로 그려 보려고 애쓰고 있었다. 그때 삯마차 한 대가 내 뒤를 따라오다가 바로 내 발치에 멈춰 서는 바람에 나는 고개를 들었다. 창문에서 고운 손 하나가 나를 향해 내밀어졌다. 그리고 그 얼굴이—커다랗고 널찍한 난간이 달린 그 오래된 떡갈나무 계단에서 처음으로 나를 돌아보던 순간부터, 그리고 그 부드러운 아름다움을 교회의 스테인드글라스 창문과 연결 짓던 순간부터, 평온함과 행복감 없이는 한 번도 본 적이 없는 그 얼굴이—나를 향해 미소 짓고 있었다.\n\n\"아그네스!\" 나는 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다. \"오, 사랑하는 아그네스, 세상 모든 사람 중에 너를 보다니, 이 얼마나 기쁜 일이야!\"\n\n\"정말 그래?\" 그녀가 그 다정한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"네게 이야기하고 싶은 게 정말 많아!\" 내가 말했다. \"그냥 너를 보는 것만으로도 마음이 이렇게나 가벼워지는걸! 만약 나에게 마법사의 모자가 있었다면, 내가 바랐을 사람은 너 말고는 아무도 없었을 거야!\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 아그네스가 받았다.\n\n\"음! 어쩌면 도라가 먼저겠지.\" 나는 얼굴을 붉히며 인정했다.\n\n\"당연히 도라가 먼저라야지, 그러길 바라.\" 아그네스가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그다음은 너야!\" 내가 말했다. \"어디 가는 길이야?\"\n\n그녀는 내 방으로 고모님을 뵈러 가는 길이었다. 날씨가 무척 좋아서, 그녀는 (오이 모종 틀 아래 마구간을 들여놓은 것 같은 냄새가 나는—그동안 내내 나는 거기에 머리를 들이밀고 있었다) 마차에서 나오게 되어 기뻐했다. 나는 마부를 돌려보냈고, 그녀가 내 팔을 잡았으며, 우리는 함께 걸어갔다. 그녀는 나에게 마치 희망이 사람의 모습을 입은 것 같았다. 곁에 아그네스를 두니, 단 일 분 사이에 나는 얼마나 달라진 기분이었던가!\n\n고모님은 그녀에게 그 별나고 무뚝뚝한 쪽지 가운데 하나를 써 보낸 터였다—고모님의 편지란 으레 은행권 지폐보다 그리 길지 않은 정도가 한계였다. 그 쪽지에서 고모님은 자신이 역경에 빠졌으며 도버를 아주 떠나기로 했지만, 마음은 단단히 먹었고 또 아주 건강하니 아무도 자기 때문에 마음 불편해할 필요 없다고 적었다. 아그네스는 고모님을 뵈러 런던에 온 것이었는데, 두 사람 사이에는 오랜 세월에 걸친 상호 호감이 있었다. 사실 그것은 내가 위크필드 씨 댁에 머물게 된 시절로 거슬러 올라가는 것이었다. 그녀는 혼자 온 게 아니라고 했다. 아버지가 함께 왔다고—그리고 유라이어 힙도.\n\n\"이제 둘이 동업자가 됐지.\" 내가 말했다. \"빌어먹을 자식!\"\n\n\"맞아.\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"여기 볼일이 좀 있어서, 나도 그들이 오는 길에 따라온 거야. 트롯우드, 내 방문이 전적으로 다정하고 사심 없는 거라고만 생각하진 말아 줘. 왜냐하면—내가 너무 모질게 편견을 가진 건지도 모르지만—아버지가 그 사람하고만 단둘이 떠나시게 두는 게 싫거든.\" \"그 자식이 아직도 위크필드 씨에게 똑같이 영향력을 휘두르고 있어, 아그네스?\"\n\n아그네스가 고개를 저었다. \"집안이 너무 많이 바뀌어서,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네가 그 정든 옛집을 알아보기도 어려울 거야. 이제 그들이 우리와 함께 살거든.\"\n\n\"그들이라니?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"힙 씨하고 그 어머니 말이야. 그 사람이 네 옛날 방에서 잔단다.\" 아그네스가 내 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그자의 꿈을 내가 짜 줄 수 있다면 좋으련만.\" 내가 말했다. \"거기서 오래 못 잘 텐데.\"\n\n\"나는 내 작은 방을 그대로 지키고 있어.\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"예전에 공부하던 그 방 말이야. 세월이 어쩜 이리도 흐르는지! 기억나? 응접실에서 이어지는, 판자를 댄 그 작은 방?\"\n\n\"기억나느냐고, 아그네스? 내가 처음으로 너를 봤을 때, 그 정겨운 작은 열쇠 바구니를 허리춤에 늘어뜨린 채 그 문에서 나오던 너를?\"\n\n\"여전히 그대로야.\" 아그네스가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"네가 그걸 그렇게 기분 좋게 떠올려 주니 기뻐. 우린 정말 행복했었지.\"\n\n\"정말 그랬어.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그 방을 아직도 나만의 것으로 지키고 있어. 하지만 힙 부인을 늘 외면할 수만은 없잖아. 그래서,\" 아그네스가 조용히 말했다. \"혼자 있고 싶을 때에도 그 부인 곁을 지켜야 한다는 의무감을 느껴. 하지만 그것 말고는 그 부인을 두고 불평할 거리가 없어. 가끔 아들 자랑으로 나를 지치게 하긴 해도, 그건 어머니로서 지극히 당연한 일이지. 그 사람은 그 부인에게 무척 효성스러운 아들이거든.\"\n\n아그네스가 이 말을 할 때 나는 그녀를 바라보았지만, 그녀에게서 유라이어의 속셈을 알아챈 기색은 전혀 찾을 수 없었다. 부드럽지만 진지한 그녀의 두 눈은 그 특유의 아름다운 솔직함으로 내 눈을 마주했고, 그 온화한 얼굴에는 아무런 변화가 없었다.\n\n\"그들이 그 집에 있어서 생기는 가장 큰 폐단은,\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"내가 바라는 만큼 아버지 가까이에 있을 수 없다는 거야—유라이어 힙이 우리 사이를 워낙 가로막고 있어서—그리고, 이런 말이 너무 당돌하지 않다면, 내가 바라는 만큼 가까이서 아버지를 보살펴 드릴 수 없다는 거지. 하지만 만약 아버지를 상대로 어떤 속임수나 배신이 꾸며지고 있다 해도, 나는 결국 단순한 사랑과 진실이 강하리라고 믿어. 진정한 사랑과 진실이 이 세상 그 어떤 악이나 불행보다도 끝내 더 강하리라고 믿어.\"\n\n다른 어떤 얼굴에서도 본 적 없는 어떤 환한 미소가, 그것이 얼마나 선한지, 그리고 한때 나에게 얼마나 익숙했던 것인지를 떠올리는 사이에도 어느새 스러져 갔다. 그러고는 그녀가 (우리는 우리 거리에 무척 가까워지고 있었다) 표정을 빠르게 바꾸며, 고모님 형편이 어쩌다 그렇게 뒤바뀌게 됐는지 아느냐고 나에게 물었다. 모른다고, 고모님이 아직 나에게 말해 주지 않았다고 내가 대답하자, 아그네스는 생각에 잠겼고, 나는 그녀의 팔이 내 팔에 닿은 채 떨리는 것을 느낀 듯했다.\n\n우리가 가 보니 고모님은 혼자였는데, 다소 흥분한 상태였다. 고모님과 크룹 부인 사이에 어떤 추상적인 문제(점잖은 여성이 셋방에 거주하는 것이 온당한가 하는 문제)를 두고 의견 차이가 생긴 것이었다. 그래서 고모님은 크룹 부인 쪽의 발작 따위에는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고, 그 부인에게서 내 브랜디 냄새가 난다고, 그러니 미안하지만 좀 나가 주셔야겠다고 일러 줌으로써 논쟁을 단번에 잘라 버렸다. 크룹 부인은 이 두 표현을 모두 소송감으로 여겨, '브리티시 주디' 앞에 끌고 가겠다는 뜻을 밝혔는데—짐작건대 우리 국민적 자유의 보루를 두고 하는 말인 듯했다.\n\n그러나 고모님은, 패거티가 딕 씨에게 호스 가즈의 병사들을 구경시켜 주러 나가 있는 사이 마음을 식힐 시간이 있었던 데다가, 아그네스를 보게 되어 무척 기뻤던 터라, 이 일을 도리어 자랑스레 우쭐대며 변함없는 좋은 기분으로 우리를 맞았다. 아그네스가 모자를 탁자에 내려놓고 고모님 곁에 앉았을 때, 나는 그녀의 온화한 눈과 빛나는 이마를 바라보며 이런 생각을 하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 거기 있는 것이 얼마나 자연스러워 보이는지, 그녀가 그토록 젊고 미숙한데도 고모님이 얼마나 미더이 그녀를 믿는지, 그리고 그녀가 단순한 사랑과 진실로 정말 얼마나 강한지를.\n\n우리는 고모님의 손실에 대해 이야기하기 시작했고, 나는 그날 아침 내가 해 보려 했던 일을 그들에게 들려주었다.\n\n\"그건 분별없는 짓이었어, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"하지만 뜻은 좋았지. 너는 너그러운 아이야—이제는 청년이라고 해야 하나 보다—그리고 나는 네가 자랑스럽다, 얘야. 거기까진 좋아. 자, 이제 트롯과 아그네스, 우리 베시 트롯우드의 처지를 정면으로 마주 보고, 어떻게 돌아가는지 따져 보자.\"\n\n나는 아그네스가 고모님을 무척 유심히 바라보면서 얼굴이 새파래지는 것을 보았다. 고모님은 고양이를 쓰다듬으며 아그네스를 무척 유심히 바라보았다.\n\n\"베시 트롯우드는,\" 늘 자기 돈 문제는 혼자만 간직해 온 고모님이 말했다. \"—네 누이를 두고 하는 말이 아니라, 얘야, 나 자신을 두고 하는 말이다—얼마간의 재산이 있었어. 얼마나 되는지는 중요하지 않아. 먹고살 만큼은 됐지. 그 이상이기도 했고. 조금 모아서 보태기도 했으니까. 베시는 한동안 그 재산을 공채에 넣어 뒀다가, 자기 사업 대리인의 조언에 따라 토지 담보에 투자했어. 그건 아주 잘됐고, 이자도 아주 짭짤하게 들어왔지, 베시가 원금을 다 돌려받을 때까지. 내가 지금 베시를 무슨 군함처럼 말하고 있구나. 자! 그러고 나서 베시는 새로운 투자처를 찾아 둘러봐야 했어. 베시는 이제 자기가 사업 대리인보다 더 현명하다고 생각했지. 그 무렵엔 그 사람이 예전만큼 유능한 사업가가 아니었거든—아그네스, 네 아버지 얘기다—그래서 자기 손으로 직접 투자하기로 마음먹은 거야. 그래서 자기 돼지들을 끌고,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"낯선 시장으로 갔는데, 알고 보니 아주 형편없는 시장이었지 뭐냐. 처음엔 광산 쪽에서 잃고, 그다음엔 잠수 쪽에서 잃었어—보물을 건져 올린다나 뭐 그런 톰 티들러 같은 허튼소리였지.\" 고모님이 코를 문지르며 설명했다. \"그러고는 또 광산 쪽에서 잃고, 마지막으로, 일을 아주 완전히 결딴내려는 듯, 은행 쪽에서 잃었어. 한동안 그 은행 주식이 얼마나 값나갔는지는 모르겠다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"백 퍼센트가 그중 제일 낮은 거였던 것 같아. 하지만 그 은행은 세상 저편 끝에 있었고, 내가 아는 한 허공 속으로 곤두박질쳐 버렸어. 어쨌든 산산조각이 나서, 육 펜스도 갚지 못할 거고 갚을 수도 없어. 그리고 베시의 육 펜스들이 죄다 거기 들어 있었으니, 그것들도 그것으로 끝장이야. 말이 적을수록 빨리 아무는 법이지!\"\n\n고모님은 이 철학적인 요약을 마무리하며, 아그네스에게 일종의 승리감 어린 눈길을 박았는데, 아그네스의 얼굴빛은 차츰 되돌아오고 있었다.\n\n\"트롯우드 아주머니, 그게 이야기의 전부인가요?\" 아그네스가 말했다.\n\n\"이만하면 충분하길 바란다, 얘야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"잃을 돈이 더 있었더라면 이게 전부는 아니었을 거야, 분명히. 베시가 그것마저 나머지 뒤로 던져 버려서 또 한 장을 채웠을 게 틀림없지. 하지만 더는 돈이 없었으니, 더는 이야기도 없는 거야.\"\n\n아그네스는 처음에 숨을 죽이고 듣고 있었다. 얼굴빛이 여전히 들락날락했지만, 그녀의 숨결은 한결 편안해졌다. 나는 그 까닭을 안다고 생각했다. 그녀는 불행한 자기 아버지가 일어난 일에 어떤 식으로든 책임이 있을까 봐 두려워했던 것이라고 나는 생각했다. 고모님이 그녀의 손을 자기 손으로 잡으며 웃었다.\n\n\"그게 다냐고?\" 고모님이 되뇌었다. \"그래, 그렇지, 그게 다야. '그리고 그녀는 그 뒤로도 행복하게 살았답니다'를 빼면 말이지. 어쩌면 언젠가 베시에게 그 한마디를 더 붙일 수 있을지도 모르지. 자, 아그네스, 너는 지혜로운 머리를 가졌어. 너도 그래, 트롯, 어떤 일에 있어서는 말이다. 비록 늘 칭찬해 줄 수는 없지만.\" 그러면서 고모님은 자기 특유의 기세로 나를 향해 고개를 흔들어 댔다. \"어떻게 하면 좋을까? 여기 이 오두막이, 이런저런 때를 다 따져 보면, 일 년에 한 칠십 파운드쯤은 나올 거야. 그쯤으로 잡아 두면 안전할 것 같구나. 자!—그게 우리가 가진 전부야.\" 고모님이 말했는데, 어떤 말들이 그렇듯이 고모님도 한참 더 이어 갈 것처럼 보이다가 아주 뚝 멈춰 버리는 별난 버릇이 있었다.\n\n\"그리고,\" 잠시 쉬었다가 고모님이 말했다. \"딕이 있지. 그 사람은 일 년에 백 파운드쯤 되지만, 물론 그건 그 사람 자신을 위해 써야 해. 그 사람을 곁에 두고 그 돈을 그 사람한테 안 쓰느니, 차라리 떠나보내는 편이 낫겠어—비록 그 사람을 알아주는 사람이 나밖에 없다는 걸 알지만 말이야. 우리 형편으로 트롯과 내가 어떻게 하는 게 최선일까? 어떻게 생각하니, 아그네스?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 고모님,\" 내가 끼어들었다. \"제가 무언가를 해야 해요!\"\n\n\"군인이 되겠다는 거냐?\" 고모님이 놀라서 받았다. \"아니면 바다로 나가겠다고? 그런 소리는 듣지 않겠어. 너는 변호사가 될 거야. 이 집안에서는, 미안하지만, 누구도 머리통이 깨지는 일 따위는 없을 거다, 도련님.\"\n\n나는 그런 생계 방편을 우리 집안에 들여올 생각은 없다고 설명하려던 참이었는데, 그때 아그네스가 내 방을 오래 계약했느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그게 핵심이야, 얘야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"적어도 여섯 달 동안은 처분할 수가 없어. 전대를 줄 수만 있다면 모를까, 그건 안 될 것 같고. 여기서 마지막에 살던 사람은 죽어 나갔지. 여섯 중 다섯은 죽을 거야—당연하지—플란넬 속치마에 황색 무명옷 차림인 저 여자 때문에. 나한테 현찰이 조금 있으니, 우리가 할 수 있는 최선은 여기서 계약 기간을 다 채우고 사는 거라는 네 말에 동의한다. 그리고 바로 가까이에 침실을 하나 얻는 거지.\"\n\n나는 크룹 부인과 끊임없는 게릴라전을 벌이며 사는 통에 고모님이 겪을 불편함을 넌지시 일러 드리는 것이 내 도리라고 생각했다. 그러나 고모님은, 적대 행위의 첫 기미가 보이는 순간 크룹 부인을 평생 두고두고 까무러치게 만들 채비가 되어 있다고 선언함으로써 그 반대를 단번에 처리해 버렸다.\n\n\"내가 생각해 봤는데, 트롯우드,\" 아그네스가 조심스레 말했다. \"만약 네가 시간이 있다면—\"\n\n\"나는 시간이 아주 많아, 아그네스. 네다섯 시 이후에는 늘 비어 있고, 이른 아침에도 시간이 있어. 이래저래,\" 시내를 쏘다니고 노우드 길을 오가는 데 들인 그 숱한 시간들을 떠올리며 얼굴이 조금 붉어지는 걸 의식한 채 나는 말했다. \"나는 시간이 넘쳐.\"\n\n\"네가 꺼리지 않을 거라고 생각해,\" 아그네스가 나에게 다가오며, 어찌나 달콤하고 희망찬 배려가 가득 담긴 낮은 목소리로 말했던지 지금도 그 소리가 들리는 듯한데, \"비서 일을 말이야.\"\n\n\"꺼리다니, 아그네스?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 아그네스가 말을 이었다. \"스트롱 박사님이 은퇴하시려던 뜻을 실행에 옮겨서, 런던에 와서 사시거든. 그리고 아버지에게, 내가 알기로, 적당한 사람을 추천해 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물으셨대. 박사님이 다른 누구보다 자기가 아끼던 옛 제자를 곁에 두고 싶어 하지 않으실까?\"\n\n\"사랑하는 아그네스!\" 내가 말했다. \"네가 없으면 나는 어떻게 해야 할까! 너는 언제나 나의 선한 천사야. 전에도 그렇게 말했잖아. 나는 너를 다른 어떤 모습으로도 떠올린 적이 없어.\"\n\n아그네스는 그 환한 웃음으로 대답하기를, 선한 천사 하나면(도라를 두고 하는 말이었다) 충분하다고 했다. 그러고는 박사님이 이른 아침과 저녁에 서재에서 일하시는 습관이 있었으니, 아마 내 한가한 시간이 박사님의 필요와 잘 맞을 거라고 일러 주었다. 나는 내 손으로 빵을 번다는 전망에 기뻤거니와, 그 빵을 옛 스승 밑에서 벌게 되리라는 희망에는 그에 못지않게 기뻤다. 요컨대, 아그네스의 조언에 따라 나는 자리에 앉아 박사님께 편지를 써서 내 목적을 밝히고, 이튿날 오전 열 시에 찾아뵙겠다고 약속을 잡았다. 이 편지를 나는 하이게이트로 부쳤는데—나에게 그토록 잊지 못할 그곳에 박사님이 살고 계셨기 때문이다—그리고 일 분도 지체하지 않고 직접 가서 부쳤다.\n\n아그네스가 있는 곳이라면 어디든, 그녀의 소리 없는 손길이 남긴 어떤 흐뭇한 흔적이 그 자리와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 듯했다. 내가 돌아와 보니, 고모님의 새들이 오두막의 거실 창가에 그토록 오래 걸려 있던 그대로 걸려 있었고, 내 안락의자는 열린 창가에 놓인 고모님의 훨씬 더 편안한 의자의 자리를 흉내 내고 있었으며, 심지어 고모님이 가지고 온 그 둥글고 초록빛 부채까지 창턱에 단단히 나사로 고정되어 있었다. 나는 이 모든 것을 누가 했는지 알아챘는데, 그것이 마치 저절로 조용히 된 것처럼 보였기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 내 학창 시절의 옛 순서대로 정리된, 내가 내팽개쳐 두었던 책들을 보았는데, 설령 아그네스가 거기서 몇 마일은 떨어져 있다고 여겼더라도, 그것들이 빠진 그 어수선함을 보고 미소 짓는 그녀를 직접 보지 않았더라도, 나는 누가 그것들을 정리했는지 한순간에 알아보았을 것이다.\n\n고모님은 템스강에 대해서는 무척 너그러웠지만(햇살이 비치니 정말 아주 보기 좋았다. 오두막 앞 바다 같지는 않았지만), 런던의 매연에 대해서는 도무지 마음을 풀지 못하고, 그것이 '모든 것에 후추를 뿌려 놓는다'고 말했다. 이 후추를 두고 내 방 구석구석에서는 패거티가 두드러진 역할을 맡은 일대 혁명이 일어나고 있었다. 나는 그 광경을 지켜보면서, 패거티는 부산을 떠는 것에 비해 어쩜 이리도 하는 일이 적어 보이고, 아그네스는 도무지 부산이라곤 떨지 않으면서도 어쩜 이리도 많은 일을 해내는지 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 문에서 노크 소리가 났다.\n\n\"아무래도,\" 아그네스가 얼굴이 새파래지며 말했다. \"아버지인 것 같아. 오시겠다고 약속하셨거든.\"\n\n나는 문을 열어, 위크필드 씨뿐만 아니라 유라이어 힙까지 들어오게 했다. 나는 위크필드 씨를 한동안 보지 못한 터였다. 아그네스에게 들은 이야기가 있어 그가 크게 달라졌으리라 마음의 준비는 했지만, 그의 모습은 나에게 충격을 주었다.\n\n그가 여러 해는 더 늙어 보여서가 아니었다—여전히 예전처럼 꼼꼼하고 정갈하게 차려입긴 했지만. 그의 얼굴에 병색이 도는 불그스레함이 어려 있어서도, 그의 두 눈이 충혈되어 부풀어 있어서도, 그의 손이 신경질적으로 떨려서도 아니었다—그 떨림의 원인을 나는 알고 있었고, 몇 해 전부터 그것이 작용하는 것을 지켜봐 온 터였다. 그가 잘생긴 외모를, 혹은 신사다운 옛 풍모를 잃어서도 아니었다—그것은 잃지 않았으니까. 나에게 가장 충격을 준 것은, 그가 타고난 우월함의 증거를 여전히 지닌 채로, 저 비루함이 기어 다니는 화신, 유라이어 힙에게 자신을 굽실거리며 내맡기고 있다는 사실이었다. 두 천성의 상대적 위치가 뒤바뀐 것—유라이어가 권력의 자리에, 위크필드 씨가 의존의 자리에 선 것—은 내가 표현할 수 없을 만큼 고통스러운 광경이었다. 만약 원숭이가 사람을 부리는 것을 보았다 한들, 나는 그보다 더 굴욕적인 구경거리라고 여기기 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n그 자신도 그것을 너무나 잘 의식하는 듯했다. 그는 들어오면서 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 그것을 느끼기라도 하는 양 고개를 숙였다. 이것은 잠깐뿐이었으니, 아그네스가 부드럽게 그에게 \"아버지! 여기 트롯우드 아주머니가 계세요—그리고 오랫동안 못 보신 트롯우드도요!\"라고 말했기 때문이다. 그러자 그가 다가와, 어색하게 고모님에게 손을 내밀었고, 나와는 좀 더 진심으로 악수했다. 내가 말한 그 잠깐의 멈춤 사이에, 나는 유라이어의 얼굴이 더없이 흉측한 미소로 일그러지는 것을 보았다. 아그네스도 그것을 본 것 같았는데, 그녀가 그에게서 움찔 물러섰기 때문이다.\n\n고모님이 무엇을 보았는지, 혹은 무엇을 보지 못했는지는, 고모님 자신의 동의 없이는 관상학이라는 학문도 알아낼 수 없으리라고 나는 장담한다. 고모님이 마음만 먹으면 그토록 흔들림 없는 얼굴을 한 사람은 일찍이 없었으리라고 나는 믿는다. 문제의 그 순간, 고모님의 얼굴은 자기 생각에 대해 어떤 빛도 비추지 않는, 막다른 벽 같았을 것이다. 마침내 고모님이 늘 그렇듯 불쑥 침묵을 깨뜨릴 때까지.\n\n\"그래, 위크필드!\" 고모님이 말하자, 그가 처음으로 고모님을 올려다보았다. \"내가 당신 딸한테 얘기하던 참이었어요. 내가 내 돈을 나 혼자 힘으로 얼마나 잘 굴렸는지 말이에요. 당신이 사업 일에 점점 녹슬어 가니, 당신한테 맡길 수가 없어서요. 우리는 함께 의논을 해 왔고, 이래저래 따져 보면 아주 잘 풀리고 있어요. 내 생각엔 아그네스가 그 회사 전체만큼의 값어치가 있어요.\"\n\n\"외람되이 한 말씀 드리자면,\" 유라이어 힙이 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"저는 베시 트롯우드 양의 말씀에 전적으로 동감입니다요. 아그네스 양이 동업자가 되어 주신다면 그저 더없이 기쁠 따름이겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"당신 자신이 동업자잖아, 안 그래?\" 고모님이 받았다. \"그쯤이면 당신한테는 충분하고도 남을 것 같은데. 당신은 어떻게 지내요, 선생?\"\n\n남달리 무뚝뚝하게 자기에게 던져진 이 질문에 답하느라, 힙 씨는 들고 있던 푸른 가방을 불편하게 움켜쥔 채, 덕분에 그럭저럭 잘 지낸다고, 고모님께 감사하다고, 고모님도 그러하시기를 바란다고 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 너는 말이다, 도련님—아니, 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야겠군요.\" 유라이어가 말을 이었다. \"잘 지내시는 것 같아 보이는데요! 코퍼필드 씨, 당신을 보게 되어 기쁩니다요, 지금 같은 형편에서도 말이지요.\" 나는 그 말을 믿었다. 그는 그 형편을 무척이나 음미하는 듯 보였으니까. \"지금 같은 형편이야 당신 친구들이 당신께 바라던 바는 아니겠지요, 코퍼필드 씨. 하지만 사람을 만드는 건 돈이 아니에요. 그건—저는 정말이지 제 미천한 능력으로는 그게 뭔지 표현할 수가 없습니다요.\" 유라이어가 알랑거리며 몸을 홱 비틀었다. \"하지만 그건 돈이 아니에요!\"\n\n여기서 그는 나와 악수했다. 보통의 방식이 아니라, 나에게서 꽤 멀찍이 떨어져 선 채로, 마치 펌프 손잡이라도 되는 양 내 손을 위아래로 들었다 놓았다 하면서, 그것을 약간 두려워하는 듯이.\n\n\"그래, 우리가 어때 보이세요, 코퍼필드 도련님—아니, 씨라고 해야겠군요?\" 유라이어가 알랑거렸다. \"위크필드 씨가 활짝 피어 보이지 않습니까요, 선생? 우리 회사에서는 세월이 별 표를 안 내요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 다만 미천한 자들을 일으켜 세운 것만은 예외지요. 그러니까 어머니와 저 말입니다요—그리고 발현시킨 것도요.\" 그가 뒤늦게 떠올린 듯 덧붙였다. \"아름다운 분, 그러니까 아그네스 양을요.\"\n\n이 찬사를 늘어놓은 뒤 그는 어찌나 견디기 힘들게 몸을 홱홱 비틀어 댔던지, 줄곧 그를 똑바로 바라보며 앉아 있던 고모님이 그만 인내심을 죄다 잃고 말았다.\n\n\"이 인간을 어쩌면 좋아!\" 고모님이 매섭게 말했다. \"도대체 뭐 하는 거야? 전기에 감전된 사람처럼 굴지 말아요, 선생!\"\n\n\"용서를 빕니다요, 트롯우드 양.\" 유라이어가 받았다. \"당신께서 신경이 예민하시다는 걸 잘 압니다요.\"\n\n\"썩 비켜요, 선생!\" 고모님이 도무지 가라앉지 않은 채 말했다. \"감히 그런 소리 마요! 나는 그런 사람이 전혀 아니에요. 당신이 뱀장어라면, 선생, 뱀장어답게 굴어요. 당신이 사람이라면, 그 사지를 좀 가만히 두라고요, 선생! 맙소사!\" 고모님이 크게 분개하며 말했다. \"나는 뱀처럼 꿈틀대고 코르크 마개뽑이처럼 비틀어 대는 통에 제정신을 빼앗기진 않을 거예요!\"\n\n힙 씨는 이 폭발에 자못 무안해했는데, 누구라도 그랬을 법했다. 그 뒤에 고모님이 분개한 태도로 의자에서 몸을 들썩이며, 마치 그를 향해 콱 물어뜯거나 덮치려는 듯 고개를 흔들어 댄 것이 그 폭발에 큰 힘을 더했다. 그러나 그는 나에게만 들리게 온순한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"저는 잘 압니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 트롯우드 양께서는 훌륭한 분이시지만 성미가 급하시다는 걸요(사실 제가 미천한 사무원이던 시절에, 당신보다 먼저 저분을 알게 되는 기쁨을 누렸던 것 같습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님). 그리고 지금 같은 형편 때문에 그 성미가 더 급해지신 것도 지극히 당연한 일이지요, 분명히요. 오히려 훨씬 더 심해지지 않은 게 놀라울 따름이에요! 저는 그저, 지금 같은 형편에서 저희가 할 수 있는 일이 무엇이든 있다면, 어머니든 저든, 혹은 위크필드 앤드 힙이든—정말로 기쁘게 하겠다는 말씀을 드리러 들렀을 뿐입니다요. 이렇게까지 말씀드려도 될까요?\" 유라이어가 자기 동업자를 향해 병약한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"유라이어 힙은,\" 위크필드 씨가 단조롭고 억지스러운 투로 말했다. \"사업에 적극적이라네, 트롯우드. 그가 하는 말에 나는 전적으로 동의해. 자네도 알다시피 나는 자네에게 오랜 애정을 품어 왔지. 그건 그렇다 치고, 유라이어가 하는 말에 나는 전적으로 동의하네!\"\n\n\"오, 이 얼마나 큰 보답인지요,\" 고모님에게서 또 한 번 호된 일격을 자초할 위험을 무릅쓰고 한쪽 다리를 끌어올리며 유라이어가 말했다. \"이토록 신임을 받다니요! 하지만 저는 그분을 사업의 고단함에서 덜어 드리는 데 무언가 보탬이 될 수 있기를 바랍니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n\"유라이어 힙은 나에게 큰 위안일세.\" 위크필드 씨가 여전히 맥없는 목소리로 말했다. \"그런 동업자를 둔 것이, 트롯우드, 내 마음의 짐을 덜어 준다네.\"\n\n그 붉은 여우가 그로 하여금 이 모든 말을 하게 시킨 것임을, 나는 알았다. 그가 내 안식을 망쳐 놓던 그날 밤 넌지시 비쳤던 그 모습대로 위크필드 씨를 나에게 보여 주려는 것이었다. 나는 그의 얼굴에 다시 그 흉측한 미소가 떠오르는 것을 보았고, 그가 나를 살피는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"가시려는 거 아니죠, 아버지?\" 아그네스가 근심스레 말했다. \"트롯우드와 저와 함께 걸어서 돌아가시지 않을래요?\"\n\n내 생각에 그는 대답하기 전에 유라이어를 쳐다보았을 것이다. 만약 그 잘난 자가 그를 앞지르지 않았더라면.\n\n\"저는 볼일이 있어 미리 약속이 되어 있습니다요.\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"그게 아니라면 친구분들과 함께하게 되어 기뻤을 텐데요. 하지만 회사를 대표하는 일은 제 동업자에게 맡기겠습니다요. 아그네스 양, 언제나 당신의 종입니다요! 안녕히 계십시오, 코퍼필드 도련님. 베시 트롯우드 양께 제 미천한 경의를 남기고 갑니다요.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 자기 커다란 손에 입을 맞추고, 가면처럼 우리를 곁눈질로 흘겨보며 물러갔다.\n\n우리는 거기 앉아, 정겨운 옛 캔터베리 시절을 이야기하며 한두 시간을 보냈다. 아그네스에게 맡겨진 위크필드 씨는 곧 예전의 자기 모습에 한결 가까워졌다. 비록 끝내 떨쳐 내지 못하는 깊이 가라앉은 우울이 그에게 깔려 있긴 했지만. 그래도 그는 환해졌고, 우리가 옛 시절의 자잘한 일들을 떠올리는 것을 듣는 데 분명한 기쁨을 느꼈는데, 그 가운데 많은 것을 그는 아주 또렷이 기억하고 있었다. 그는 아그네스와 나와 다시 단둘이—아니, 셋이서—함께 있으니 마치 그 시절 같다고 했고, 그 시절이 차라리 변하지 않았더라면 하고 하늘에 빌었다. 아그네스의 평온한 얼굴에, 그리고 그의 팔에 닿는 그녀의 손길 그 자체에 어떤 힘이 깃들어 있어, 그것이 그에게 놀라운 일을 해냈음을 나는 분명히 안다.\n\n고모님은(그동안 거의 내내 안쪽 방에서 패거티와 함께 부산했다) 그들이 묵는 곳까지 우리와 함께 가려 하지 않았지만, 나에게는 꼭 가라고 우겼다. 그래서 나는 갔다. 우리는 함께 저녁을 먹었다. 저녁 식사 후, 아그네스는 옛날처럼 그의 곁에 앉아 그의 잔에 포도주를 따라 주었다. 그는 그녀가 주는 만큼만 받았고 그 이상은 받지 않았다—마치 어린아이처럼. 그리고 우리 셋은 저녁이 깃들어 가는 동안 창가에 함께 앉아 있었다. 거의 어두워졌을 때, 그는 소파에 누웠고, 아그네스는 그의 머리를 받쳐 주고 잠시 그 위로 몸을 숙였다. 그녀가 창가로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀의 눈에 어린 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 내가 알아볼 수 있을 만큼은 아직 어둡지 않았다.\n\n내 인생의 그 시절에, 사랑과 진실 속에 있던 그 사랑스러운 소녀를 결코 잊지 않게 해 달라고 나는 하늘에 빈다. 만약 잊는다면, 그것은 내가 종말에 가까워졌다는 뜻일 테고, 그렇다면 나는 그녀를 가장 또렷이 기억하기를 바랄 것이다! 그녀는 그토록 선한 결심들로 내 마음을 채워 주었고, 자신의 본보기로 내 나약함을 그토록 굳세게 해 주었으며, 내 안에서 갈팡질팡하던 열정과 정처 없던 목적을 그토록—어떻게 그랬는지는 모르겠다. 그녀는 너무나 겸손하고 온화해서 여러 말로 충고하는 법이 없었다—이끌어 주었기에, 내가 행한 그 자그마한 선과, 내가 삼간 그 모든 해악을 나는 엄숙히 그녀에게 돌릴 수 있다고 믿는다.\n\n그리고 어두운 가운데 창가에 앉아, 그녀가 도라에 대해 나에게 어떻게 이야기해 주었던가. 도라를 향한 내 찬사에 귀 기울여 주고, 다시 그녀를 칭찬해 주며, 그 작은 요정 같은 모습을 둘러 자기 순결한 빛을 조금씩 비추어 주어, 그것을 나에게 한층 더 소중하고 한층 더 천진한 것으로 만들어 주었던가! 오, 아그네스, 내 소년 시절의 누이여, 만약 내가 그때, 훗날에야 알게 된 것을 알았더라면—!\n\n내가 아래로 내려갈 때 거리에 거지 하나가 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 고요하고 천사 같은 두 눈을 떠올리며 창문 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸을 때, 그가 마치 아침의 메아리이기라도 한 듯 중얼거리는 바람에 나는 흠칫 놀랐다. \"눈멀었어! 눈멀었어! 눈멀었어!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 36. ENTHUSIASM\n\n이튿날 나는 또 한 번 로마식 욕조에 풍덩 뛰어드는 것으로 하루를 시작하고, 하이게이트로 출발했다. 이제는 풀이 죽어 있지 않았다. 허름한 외투도 두렵지 않았고, 늠름한 잿빛 말을 향한 동경 따위도 없었다. 우리가 최근에 겪은 불운을 생각하는 내 마음가짐 전체가 달라져 있었다. 내가 해야 할 일은, 고모님이 지금껏 나에게 베푼 은혜가 무정하고 배은망덕한 자에게 헛되이 쏟아부어진 게 아니라는 사실을 보여 드리는 것이었다. 내가 해야 할 일은, 굳세고 흔들림 없는 마음으로 일에 뛰어들어, 어린 시절의 그 고통스러운 단련을 밑천으로 삼는 것이었다. 내가 해야 할 일은, 나무꾼의 도끼를 손에 쥐고, 도라에게 이를 때까지 나무를 베어 넘기며 곤경의 숲을 헤치고 나만의 길을 트는 것이었다. 그리고 나는 걷는 것만으로도 그 일이 이루어지기라도 할 듯이, 무서운 속도로 내달았다.\n\n낯익은 하이게이트 길에 들어서서, 예전 이 길과 얽혀 있던 그 즐거운 나들이와는 전혀 다른 볼일을 좇아 걸으니, 내 삶 전체에 완전한 변화가 들이닥친 것만 같았다. 그러나 그것이 나를 주눅 들게 하지는 못했다. 새로운 삶과 함께 새로운 목적, 새로운 다짐이 찾아왔다. 수고는 컸고, 보상은 값을 매길 수 없었다. 도라가 그 보상이었고, 도라는 반드시 쟁취해야 했다.\n\n나는 어찌나 황홀경에 빠져들었던지, 내 외투가 아직 조금이라도 더 허름하지 않은 것이 못내 아쉬울 지경이었다. 나는 내 힘을 입증해 줄 그런 상황에서 곤경의 숲에 선 저 나무들을 베어 넘기고 싶었다. 길에서 돌을 깨고 있던, 철테 안경을 쓴 노인에게 잠깐만 그 망치를 빌려 달라고, 화강암을 깨뜨려 도라에게 이르는 길을 닦기 시작하게 해 달라고 부탁하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다. 나는 스스로를 어찌나 후끈 달아오르게 자극했던지, 그리고 숨을 어찌나 헐떡였던지, 마치 얼마인지도 모를 큰돈을 벌어들이기라도 한 것 같은 기분이 들었다.\n\n이런 상태로, 나는 세놓는다는 표시가 붙은 어느 오두막에 들어가, 그곳을 꼼꼼히 살펴보았다 — 실속을 차려야 한다는 생각이 들었기 때문이다. 나와 도라가 살기에 더할 나위 없이 좋을 듯했다. 집 짚이 울타리 사이로 장사꾼들을 향해 짖어 대며 뛰어다닐 작은 앞뜰도 있고, 위층에는 고모님이 쓰실 멋진 방도 있었다. 나는 다시 밖으로 나와, 그 어느 때보다 더 후끈 달아올라 더 빠른 걸음으로, 하이게이트를 향해 어찌나 내달렸던지 약속 시간보다 한 시간이나 일찍 그곳에 닿았다. 설령 그렇게 일찍 닿지 않았더라도, 그나마 봐줄 만한 꼴이 되려면 몸을 식히려고 한동안 어슬렁거려야 했을 것이다.\n\n이렇게 꼭 필요한 준비 절차를 거친 뒤, 내가 제일 먼저 마음 쓴 일은 박사님의 집을 찾는 것이었다. 그 집은 스티어포스 부인이 사는 하이게이트의 그 동네가 아니라, 작은 마을 정반대편에 있었다. 그 사실을 알아내고 나서, 나는 도저히 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 끌림에 이끌려, 스티어포스 부인의 집 옆 골목으로 되돌아가, 정원 담장 모서리 너머를 들여다보았다. 그의 방은 굳게 닫혀 있었다. 온실 문은 활짝 열려 있었고, 로자 다틀이 모자도 쓰지 않은 채, 빠르고 격한 걸음으로 잔디밭 한쪽 자갈길을 오락가락 거닐고 있었다. 그녀는 마치 다져진 길 위에서 제 사슬의 길이만큼 이리저리 끌려다니며 제 가슴을 갉아 닳리는 어떤 사나운 짐승 같다는 느낌을 나에게 주었다.\n\n나는 살그머니 망보던 자리에서 물러나, 그 동네를 피해 가며, 괜히 그 근처에 갔다 싶어 후회하면서, 열 시가 될 때까지 어슬렁거렸다. 지금은 언덕 꼭대기에 서서 시각을 알려 주는 가느다란 첨탑의 교회가, 그때는 거기 없었다. 그 자리에는 학교로 쓰이던 낡은 붉은 벽돌 저택이 있었는데, 내 기억으로는 학교로 다니기에 멋진 옛 집이었음이 틀림없다.\n\n박사님의 오두막에 다가갔을 때 — 막 끝난 듯한 장식과 손질로 미루어 보건대 그가 적잖이 돈을 들인 듯한, 예쁘고 오래된 곳이었다 — 나는 그가 정원 한쪽에서 각반까지 차고 거니는 모습을 보았는데, 마치 내가 학생이던 시절부터 단 한 번도 거니는 일을 멈춘 적이 없는 것만 같았다. 그에게는 옛 동무들도 곁에 있었다. 근방에 키 큰 나무가 잔뜩 있었던 덕에, 까마귀 두세 마리가 잔디밭에서 그를 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 마치 캔터베리의 까마귀들이 그에 대해 편지로 일러바치기라도 한 듯, 그 결과 그를 유심히 살피고 있는 것 같았다.\n\n그 거리에서 그의 주의를 끌기란 도무지 가망 없는 일임을 알기에, 나는 용기를 내어 문을 열고 그의 뒤를 따라 걸어가, 그가 몸을 돌릴 때 마주치도록 했다. 마침내 그가 돌아서서 나에게 다가왔을 때, 그는 몇 순간 나를 골똘히 바라보았으나, 분명 나에 대해서는 아무 생각도 하지 않는 눈치였다. 그러더니 그 인자한 얼굴에 더없는 기쁨이 떠오르며, 그가 두 손으로 내 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"아니, 이런, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\" 박사님이 말했다. \"어엿한 어른이 다 되었구나! 잘 지냈는가? 자네를 보니 정말 기쁘네. 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 어쩌면 이렇게 훤칠해졌나! 자넨 아주 — 그래 — 어이구, 이런!\"\n\n나는 그가 건강하기를, 그리고 스트롱 부인도 건강하기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼, 그럼!\" 박사님이 말했다. \"애니도 아주 잘 지내고, 자네를 보면 무척 기뻐할 걸세. 자넨 늘 그 사람이 제일 아끼던 학생이었지. 어젯밤에 자네 편지를 보여 줬더니 그렇게 말하더군. 그리고 — 그래, 그렇지 — 자네 잭 몰던 군을 기억하나, 코퍼필드?\"\n\n\"완벽하게 기억합니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지,\" 박사님이 말했다. \"암, 그렇고말고. 그 친구도 잘 지내네.\"\n\n\"고국으로 돌아왔나요, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"인도에서 말인가?\" 박사님이 말했다. \"그래. 잭 몰던 군이 그곳 기후를 견디지 못했다네. 마클햄 부인 말이야 — 마클햄 부인을 잊진 않았겠지?\"\n\n그 노병을 잊다니! 그 짧은 사이에!\n\n\"마클햄 부인이,\" 박사님이 말했다. \"그 친구 일로 어찌나 속을 끓이던지, 가엾게도. 그래서 우리가 그 친구를 다시 고국으로 불러들였고, 그에게 작은 특허 자리를 하나 마련해 주었다네. 그 친구한테 한결 잘 맞는 자리지.\" 나는 잭 몰던 군을 익히 아는 터라, 이 이야기로 미루어 그것이 할 일은 별로 없으면서 보수는 꽤 두둑한 자리이리라 짐작했다. 박사님은 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 오락가락 거닐면서, 그 다정한 얼굴을 격려하듯 내 쪽으로 돌린 채, 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 자네의 이 제안 말인데. 나로서야 정말 흐뭇하고 반가운 일이지, 그럼. 하지만 자네, 더 나은 길을 찾을 수 있다고 생각하지 않나? 자넨 우리와 함께 있을 때 두각을 나타냈잖나. 자넨 여러 좋은 일을 할 자질이 있어. 어떤 건물이든 올릴 수 있는 토대를 닦아 두지 않았나. 그런 자네가 인생의 봄날을, 내가 줄 수 있는 이런 변변찮은 일에 바친다는 게 안타깝지 않은가?\"\n\n나는 다시 후끈 달아올라, 두렵게도 황홀한 어조로 내 뜻을 펼치며 내 청을 힘주어 밀어붙였다. 그러면서 나에게는 이미 직업이 있음을 박사님에게 일깨워 드렸다.\n\n\"음, 그래, 그래,\" 박사님이 말했다. \"그건 사실이지. 분명, 자네에게 직업이 있고, 또 실제로 그걸 익히고 있는 중이라면, 사정이 다르지. 하지만, 내 착한 젊은 친구여, 일 년에 칠십 파운드가 대체 얼마나 된다고?\"\n\n\"우리 수입이 갑절로 늘어납니다, 스트롱 박사님.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 세상에!\" 박사님이 대답했다. \"그걸 생각하면! 그렇다고 일 년에 딱 칠십 파운드로 빠듯하게 묶어 두자는 뜻은 아니야. 나는 이렇게 데려다 쓰는 젊은 친구에게는 으레 따로 선물도 챙겨 줄 작정이었거든. 틀림없이,\" 박사님은 여전히 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 나를 오락가락 거닐게 하며 말했다. \"나는 늘 해마다 줄 선물까지 셈에 넣고 있었네.\"\n\n\"제 사랑하는 은사님,\" 내가 말했다 (이제 정말이지 아무 허튼소리도 없이). \"저는 이미 갚을 길 없는 은혜를 선생님께 너무도 많이 졌습니다만—\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야,\" 박사님이 가로막았다. \"그런 말 말게!\"\n\n\"제가 낼 수 있는 시간, 그러니까 아침나절과 저녁나절을 받아 주시고, 그것이 일 년에 칠십 파운드의 값어치가 있다고 여겨 주신다면, 저로서는 이루 말할 수 없는 은혜를 베풀어 주시는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 세상에!\" 박사님이 천진하게 말했다. \"그렇게 적은 걸 그렇게 큰 값에 친다니! 이런, 이런! 그래서 더 나은 길이 생기면, 그리로 가겠나? 자, 약속하지?\" 박사님이 말했다 — 이 말은 그가 늘 우리 소년들의 명예에 자못 엄숙하게 호소하던 방식이었다.\n\n\"약속합니다, 선생님!\" 나는 옛 학창 시절의 어투로 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그렇게 하세.\" 박사님이 내 어깨를 툭 치며 말했는데, 우리가 여전히 오락가락 거니는 동안에도 그 손은 그대로 거기 얹혀 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 저는 스무 배는 더 기쁠 겁니다, 선생님,\" 내가 조금 — 부디 악의 없는 — 아첨을 섞어 말했다. \"제 일이 그 사전 작업이라면요.\"\n\n박사님은 걸음을 멈추더니, 빙긋 웃으며 다시 한번 내 어깨를 툭 치고는, 마치 내가 인간 예지의 가장 깊은 심연까지 꿰뚫어 본 것처럼, 보기에 더없이 흐뭇한 의기양양함으로 외쳤다. \"내 사랑하는 젊은 친구여, 자네가 정곡을 찔렀네. 바로 그 사전일세!\"\n\n그 밖에 무엇일 수 있었겠는가! 그의 주머니는 머릿속만큼이나 사전으로 가득 차 있었다. 사방으로 사전이 삐져나와 있었다. 그가 나에게 말하기를, 학교 일에서 물러난 뒤로 사전 작업이 놀랍도록 진척되었으며, 낮에는 으레 생각하는 모자를 쓰고 거니는 게 버릇인 만큼, 아침저녁으로 일하자는 이 제안만큼 그에게 안성맞춤인 것도 없다고 했다. 잭 몰던 군이 최근 들어 이따금 필경사 노릇을 자청해 주었으나 그 일에 익숙하지 못한 탓에, 그의 원고가 다소 어수선해져 있었다. 하지만 어긋난 부분은 곧 바로잡아, 일이 술술 풀릴 것이라고 했다. 나중에, 우리가 본격적으로 일에 들어갔을 때, 나는 잭 몰던 군이 해 놓은 일이 예상보다 훨씬 더 골칫거리라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 숱한 실수를 저질렀을 뿐 아니라, 박사님의 원고 위에 군인이며 여인의 머리통을 어찌나 많이 그려 놓았던지, 나는 종종 알 수 없는 미궁 속에 빠져들곤 했던 것이다.\n\n박사님은 그 놀라운 대작을 함께 만들어 나가게 되었다는 기대에 무척이나 행복해했고, 우리는 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 시작하기로 정했다. 우리는 매일 아침 두 시간, 매일 밤 두세 시간씩 일하기로 했고, 다만 토요일에는 내가 쉬기로 했다. 일요일에는 물론 나도 쉴 참이었으니, 나는 이 조건이 무척 수월하다고 여겼다.\n\n이렇게 우리 계획이 서로 흡족하게 짜이고 나자, 박사님은 나를 집 안으로 데려가 스트롱 부인에게 인사시켜 주려 했다. 우리는 부인이 박사님의 새 서재에서 그의 책에 먼지를 떨고 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그것은 그가 그 거룩한 애장서들에 대해 다른 누구에게도 결코 허락하지 않던 자유였다.\n\n그들은 나 때문에 아침 식사를 미뤄 두었던 터라, 우리는 함께 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 나는 무슨 기척이 들려오기도 전에, 스트롱 부인의 얼굴에서 누군가 다가오고 있음을 알아챘다. 말을 탄 신사 하나가 문 앞에 이르더니, 마치 제집인 양 말고삐를 팔에 건 채 말을 끌고 작은 마당으로 들어와, 텅 빈 마차 보관소 벽의 고리에 말을 매고는, 채찍을 손에 들고 아침 식사를 하는 응접실로 들어왔다. 잭 몰던 군이었다. 그런데 내 생각에 잭 몰던 군은 인도에 다녀와서도 조금도 나아진 데가 없었다. 다만 곤경의 숲에서 나무를 베어 넘기지 않는 젊은이들에 대해서라면 나는 무자비할 만큼 의로움에 들떠 있던 터라, 내 인상은 그만한 에누리를 두고 받아들여야 할 것이다.\n\n\"잭 군!\" 박사님이 말했다. \"코퍼필드 군일세!\"\n\n잭 몰던 군은 나와 악수했으나, 내가 보기에 그리 따뜻하지는 않았다. 게다가 나른하게 생색내는 태도라, 나는 속으로 몹시 못마땅했다. 그런데 그 나른함이라는 게 통틀어 정말 볼 만한 구경거리였다 — 사촌 누이 애니에게 말을 걸 때만 빼고. \"오늘 아침은 드셨나, 잭 군?\" 박사님이 말했다.\n\n\"저는 아침은 거의 안 먹습니다, 선생님.\" 그가 안락의자에 머리를 뒤로 젖힌 채 대답했다. \"먹으면 따분하더군요.\"\n\n\"오늘 무슨 소식이라도 있나?\" 박사님이 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 없습니다, 선생님.\" 몰던 군이 대답했다. \"북부 지방에서 사람들이 굶주리고 불만에 차 있다는 기사가 있긴 합니다만, 그 사람들이야 늘 어디선가 굶주리고 불만에 차 있는걸요.\"\n\n박사님은 자못 심각한 얼굴이 되더니, 화제를 돌리고 싶다는 듯 말했다. \"그렇다면 소식이 전혀 없는 셈이군. 그리고 무소식이 희소식이라고들 하지.\"\n\n\"신문에 살인 사건에 대한 긴 기사가 실렸습니다, 선생님,\" 몰던 군이 한마디 했다. \"하지만 누군가는 늘 살해당하기 마련이라, 저는 읽지 않았습니다.\"\n\n인류의 온갖 행동과 격정에 대해 무관심을 드러내는 것이, 그 무렵에는, 그 후로 내가 으레 그렇게 여겨지는 것을 지켜본 만큼 그리 대단한 자질로 통하지는 않았던 것 같다. 나는 그것이 정말이지 무척 유행하는 것을 보았다. 어찌나 성공적으로 펼쳐지는지, 나는 차라리 애벌레로 태어났더라면 나았을 법한 멋쟁이 신사 숙녀들을 더러 만나기도 했다. 어쩌면 그때 나에게는 그것이 새로웠던 까닭에 더욱 인상 깊었는지 모르나, 어쨌든 그것이 잭 몰던 군에 대한 내 평가를 높여 주거나 그에 대한 내 신뢰를 굳혀 주는 데 보탬이 되지 않았던 것만은 분명하다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 애니가 오페라에 가고 싶어 할지 물어보러 들렀어.\" 몰던 군이 그녀에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"이번 시즌에 볼 만한 마지막 밤이거든. 게다가 그곳에 꼭 들어 봐야 할 가수가 한 명 있어. 정말 빼어난 가수야. 게다가 어찌나 매력적으로 못생겼는지,\" 다시 나른함 속으로 가라앉으며.\n\n박사님은 늘 젊은 아내를 기쁘게 할 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 흐뭇해하던지라, 부인에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"가야지, 애니. 꼭 가야 해.\"\n\n\"전 안 가는 게 낫겠어요.\" 그녀가 박사님에게 말했다. \"집에 있고 싶어요. 집에 있는 게 훨씬 좋아요.\"\n\n그러더니 사촌 오빠는 쳐다보지도 않고 나에게 말을 건네며, 아그네스에 대해, 아그네스를 만나 볼 수 있을지, 아그네스가 오늘 오지 않을지를 물었다. 그러고는 어찌나 심란해 보이던지, 토스트에 버터를 바르고 있는 박사님조차 어떻게 저토록 빤한 것을 보지 못하는지 나로서는 의아할 지경이었다.\n\n그러나 그는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그는 부인에게 너그럽게 말하기를, 부인은 젊으니 즐기고 놀아야지, 따분한 늙은이 때문에 따분해져서는 안 된다고 했다. 더구나 그는 부인이 그 새 가수의 노래를 모조리 자기에게 불러 주는 것을 듣고 싶다고 했다. 그런데 가지 않고서야 어떻게 그걸 제대로 부르겠느냐는 것이었다. 그리하여 박사님은 한사코 부인을 대신해 약속을 잡았고, 잭 몰던 군은 저녁 식사 때 다시 오기로 했다. 이렇게 매듭이 지어지자, 그는 짐작건대 자기 특허 자리로 갔을 테지만, 어쨌든 무척이나 빈둥대는 모습으로 말을 타고 떠났다.\n\n나는 이튿날 아침, 그녀가 갔었는지 궁금해 알아보고 싶었다. 그녀는 가지 않았고, 사촌 오빠에게 약속을 미루겠다고 런던으로 기별을 보냈으며, 오후에는 아그네스를 만나러 외출했는데, 박사님을 졸라 함께 가게 했다고 한다. 그리고 저녁나절이 무척 좋아서, 둘이 들판을 가로질러 걸어 돌아왔다고 박사님이 나에게 일러 주었다. 그때 나는 궁금했다. 아그네스가 시내에 없었더라도 그녀가 갔을까, 그리고 아그네스가 그녀에게도 어떤 좋은 영향을 끼치고 있는 것은 아닐까 하고!\n\n내 생각에 그녀는 그리 행복해 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그것은 선한 얼굴이거나, 아니면 더없이 거짓된 얼굴이었다. 나는 자주 그녀의 얼굴을 흘끔거렸는데, 우리가 일하는 동안 내내 그녀가 창가에 앉아 있었던 데다, 우리가 일하는 틈틈이 짬짬이 들던 아침 식사도 그녀가 차려 주었기 때문이다. 아홉 시에 내가 떠날 무렵, 그녀는 박사님 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 그의 신발과 각반을 신겨 주고 있었다. 그 낮은 방의 열린 창문 위로 드리운 푸른 잎사귀에서 던져진 부드러운 그늘이 그녀의 얼굴에 어려 있었다. 그리고 나는 민법 박사회관으로 가는 내내, 예전에 박사님이 책을 읽는 동안 그녀가 그를 바라보던 그 모습을 보았던 그 밤을 떠올렸다.\n\n이제 나는 꽤 바빴다. 아침 다섯 시에 일어나, 밤 아홉 시나 열 시에야 집에 돌아왔다. 그러나 나는 그토록 빡빡하게 일하는 데서 무한한 만족을 느꼈고, 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 느릿느릿 걷는 법이 없었으며, 내 몸을 지치게 할수록 도라를 가질 자격을 그만큼 더 쌓는 것이라는 열의에 찬 마음이 들었다. 나는 아직 달라진 내 모습을 도라에게 드러내지 않았는데, 며칠 안에 그녀가 밀스 양을 만나러 오기로 되어 있어, 들려줄 이야기는 죄다 그때까지 미뤄 두었기 때문이다. 다만 편지로 (우리의 모든 연락은 밀스 양을 통해 몰래 오갔다) 그녀에게 들려줄 이야기가 많다고만 알려 두었다. 그러는 동안, 나는 곰 기름을 아껴 쓰고, 향 비누와 라벤더 물은 아주 끊어 버렸으며, 조끼 세 벌은 내 모진 앞날에 너무 사치스럽다며 엄청난 손해를 무릅쓰고 팔아 치웠다.\n\n이 모든 일로도 만족하지 못하고, 무언가 더 하고 싶어 안달이 나서, 나는 트래들스를 보러 갔다. 그는 이제 홀번의 캐슬 가에 있는 어느 집 난간 뒤쪽에 세 들어 살고 있었다. 이미 나와 함께 하이게이트에 두 번이나 다녀왔고 박사님과의 교제도 다시 트게 된 딕 씨를 나는 데리고 갔다.\n\n내가 딕 씨를 데려간 까닭은 이러했다. 그는 고모님의 몰락에 몹시 마음 아파했고, 나만큼 일하는 갤리선 노예나 죄수도 없으리라고 진심으로 믿었기에, 자기는 쓸모 있게 할 일이 하나도 없다며 안달복달하다 기운과 입맛마저 잃기 시작한 것이다. 이런 상태에서 그는 그 어느 때보다 진정서를 끝내지 못할 것 같다고 느꼈다. 그리고 거기에 매달려 애쓰면 애쓸수록, 그 불운한 찰스 일세의 머리통이 그만큼 더 자주 끼어들었다. 나는 그의 병이 더 깊어질까 봐 자못 걱정스러웠다. 그가 쓸모 있다고 믿게끔 무해한 속임수라도 쓰든지, 아니면 (이편이 더 나을 텐데) 그를 정말로 쓸모 있게 할 길을 마련해 주든지 하지 않으면 안 될 것 같았다. 그래서 나는 트래들스가 우리를 도와줄 수 있는지 시험해 보기로 마음먹었다. 가기에 앞서 나는 그동안 일어난 일을 모조리 적어 트래들스에게 보냈고, 트래들스는 자신의 연민과 우정을 담은 더없이 훌륭한 답장을 보내왔다.\n\n우리는 그가 잉크병과 서류를 펼쳐 놓고 열심히 일하고 있는 것을 보았는데, 그 작은 방 한구석의 화분 받침대와 작고 둥근 탁자가 눈에 들어와 마음이 환해졌다. 그는 우리를 다정하게 맞아 주었고, 딕 씨와도 단번에 친해졌다. 딕 씨는 전에 그를 본 적이 분명히 있노라고 우겼고, 우리 둘은 \"그럴 만도 하지요\"라고 맞장구쳐 주었다.\n\n내가 트래들스와 의논해야 할 첫 번째 주제는 이것이었다. 나는 여러 분야에서 두각을 나타낸 많은 이들이 의회의 토론을 기사로 받아쓰는 일로 인생을 시작했다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 트래들스가 자기 희망 중 하나로 신문 일을 나에게 언급한 적이 있던지라, 나는 그 두 가지를 한데 엮어, 편지로 트래들스에게 이 일을 하려면 어떻게 자격을 갖춰야 하는지 알고 싶다고 했다. 이제 트래들스가 알아본 결과를 일러 주기를, 드문 경우를 빼고는, 그 일에 두루 능통해지는 데 필요한 순전히 기계적인 기술의 습득, 다시 말해 속기로 쓰고 읽는 그 비법을 완벽하고 온전하게 익히는 일이, 그 어려움으로 치자면 여섯 개 언어를 통달하는 것과 맞먹는다고 했다. 그리고 그것은 어쩌면 끈기를 발휘하면 몇 해에 걸쳐 이룰 수 있을지도 모른다고 했다. 트래들스는 으레 이쯤이면 이 일이 매듭지어지리라 짐작했다. 그러나 나는 여기에야말로 베어 넘겨야 할 키 큰 나무 몇 그루가 있구나 싶은 마음에, 그 즉시 도끼를 손에 쥐고 이 덤불을 헤치며 도라에게로 나아가기로 작정했다.\n\n\"정말 고맙네, 내 사랑하는 트래들스!\" 내가 말했다. \"내일부터 시작하겠네.\"\n\n트래들스는 놀란 표정을 지었는데, 그도 그럴 만했다. 하지만 그는 아직 내 황홀한 상태를 까맣게 모르고 있었다.\n\n\"이 기술의 좋은 체계가 담긴 책을 한 권 살 거야,\" 내가 말했다. \"할 일이 절반도 안 되는 박사회관에서 그걸 익히겠어. 연습 삼아 우리 법정에서 오가는 진술을 받아 적어 보겠어 — 트래들스, 이 친구야, 내가 기어이 통달하고 말겠어!\"\n\n\"이런, 세상에,\" 트래들스가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 말했다. \"자네가 그렇게 굳센 사람인 줄은 미처 몰랐네, 코퍼필드!\"\n\n그가 어떻게 알았겠는가, 나 자신에게도 새로운 일이었으니. 나는 그 말을 슬쩍 흘려 넘기고, 딕 씨 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"그러니까 말입니다,\" 딕 씨가 간절하게 말했다. \"제가 힘을 좀 쓸 수 있다면요, 트래들스 씨 — 제가 북을 칠 수만 있다면 — 아니, 무엇이든 불 수만 있다면요!\"\n\n가엾은 양반! 나는 그가 마음속으로는 그런 일을 다른 무엇보다 더 좋아했으리라는 것을 거의 의심하지 않는다. 무슨 일이 있어도 빙긋 웃지 않았을 트래들스가 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 선생님은 글씨를 아주 잘 쓰시잖습니까. 자네가 그렇게 말했지, 코퍼필드?\" \"기막히게 잘 쓰시지!\" 내가 말했다. 정말 그러했다. 그는 유난히 정갈한 글씨를 썼다.\n\n\"이렇게 생각해 보시는 건 어떨까요,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"제가 글을 좀 구해다 드리면, 선생님이 그걸 베껴 써 주실 수 있지 않을까요?\"\n\n딕 씨는 미심쩍은 얼굴로 나를 보았다. \"어떤가, 트롯우드?\"\n\n나는 고개를 가로저었다. 딕 씨도 고개를 가로저으며 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"진정서 이야기를 해 주게.\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n나는 딕 씨의 원고에서 찰스 일세를 떼어 놓기가 어렵다는 사정을 트래들스에게 설명해 주었다. 그러는 동안 딕 씨는 자못 공손하고 진지한 얼굴로 트래들스를 바라보며 엄지손가락을 빨고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 말씀드리는 그 글들은, 아시다시피, 이미 다 작성되어 끝난 것들입니다,\" 트래들스가 잠깐 생각하더니 말했다. \"딕 씨가 거기에 무얼 보탤 것도 없지요. 그러면 사정이 달라지지 않을까, 코퍼필드? 어쨌거나, 한번 시도해 보는 것이 좋지 않겠나?\"\n\n이 말이 우리에게 새로운 희망을 주었다. 딕 씨가 의자에 앉아 애타게 우리를 지켜보는 동안, 트래들스와 나는 한쪽에서 머리를 맞대고, 그를 이튿날부터 일에 끌어들일 묘책을 짜냈고, 그것은 의기양양한 성공을 거두었다.\n\n버킹엄 가의 창가에 놓인 탁자 하나에, 우리는 트래들스가 구해 온 일거리를 펼쳐 놓았다 — 어느 통행권에 관한 법률 문서를, 몇 부였는지는 잊었지만 여러 부 베끼는 일이었다 — 그리고 다른 탁자 하나에는 그 위대한 진정서의 아직 끝맺지 못한 마지막 원본을 펼쳐 놓았다. 딕 씨에게 우리가 일러 둔 지시는, 눈앞에 놓인 것을 한 치도 원본에서 벗어나지 말고 그대로 베껴 쓰되, 찰스 일세를 조금이라도 끌어들이고 싶은 마음이 들거든 그 즉시 진정서로 달려가라는 것이었다. 우리는 그에게 이것을 굳게 지키라고 거듭 당부하고는, 그를 지켜보는 일은 고모님에게 맡겼다. 고모님이 나중에 우리에게 일러 주기를, 처음에 그는 케틀드럼을 치는 사람 같아서, 두 가지 사이를 끊임없이 오가며 갈팡질팡했다고 한다. 그러나 이러다 보니 머리가 복잡해지고 지치는 데다, 베낄 것이 눈앞에 빤히 놓여 있는 터라, 그는 곧 차분하고 일하는 사람답게 자리에 앉아, 진정서는 더 한가한 때로 미뤄 두었다고 한다. 한마디로, 비록 우리가 그에게 알맞은 만큼만 일을 시키려고 무척 마음을 썼고, 또 그가 한 주가 시작될 때부터 일을 시작한 것도 아니었지만, 그는 다음 토요일 밤까지 십 실링 구 펜스를 벌었다. 그리고 그가 이 보물을 육 펜스짜리 동전으로 바꾸려고 동네 가게란 가게를 죄다 돌아다니던 일, 그것을 쟁반 위에 하트 모양으로 가지런히 늘어놓고, 두 눈에 기쁨과 자랑의 눈물을 글썽이며 고모님께 가져오던 일을, 나는 살아 있는 동안 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 그는 쓸모 있게 일하게 된 그 순간부터, 마치 상서로운 부적의 힘 아래 놓인 사람 같았다. 그리고 그 토요일 밤, 세상에 행복한 사람이 있었다면, 그것은 고모님을 세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인으로, 나를 가장 놀라운 청년으로 여기던, 그 고마운 양반이었다.\n\n\"이제 굶을 일은 없어, 트롯우드,\" 딕 씨가 한구석에서 나와 악수하며 말했다. \"내가 고모님을 부양할 걸세, 자네!\" 그러더니 마치 열 손가락이 열 개의 은행이라도 되는 양, 손가락을 공중에 활짝 펼쳐 흔들었다.\n\n트래들스와 나, 둘 중에 누가 더 기뻤는지 나로서는 알기 어렵다. \"이런,\" 트래들스가 갑자기 주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 나에게 건네며 말했다. \"미코버 씨를 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭔가!\"\n\n그 편지는 (미코버 씨는 편지 쓸 만한 기회라면 단 한 번도 놓치는 법이 없었다) 나에게 보내진 것으로, \"이너 템플 소속 T. 트래들스 귀하의 호의를 빌려\"라고 적혀 있었다. 그 내용은 이러했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\n무언가 일이 풀렸다는 소식을 받을 마음의 준비가 어쩌면 그대에게 안 되어 있지는 않으리라 보네. 내가 전에 한번 그런 일을 기대하고 있노라고 그대에게 언급했을 법도 하니.\n\n나는 우리 축복받은 섬의 어느 지방 도시(그곳 사회는 농업과 성직의 행복한 어우러짐이라 일컬을 만하네)에, 학식 있는 직업 중 하나와 직접 연을 맺으며 자리를 잡으려 하네. 미코버 부인과 우리 자식들도 나와 함께 갈 걸세. 훗날 우리의 유골은 어느 유서 깊은 건물에 딸린 묘지에서 한데 섞인 채 발견될 것이네. 내가 말하는 그곳은 그 묘지로 하여, 이를테면 중국에서 페루에 이르기까지, 그만한 명성을 얻었다고 해도 좋을 곳이지.\n\n수많은 부침을 — 그래도 부디 비루하지는 않게 겪어 낸 — 저 현대의 바빌론에 작별을 고하면서, 미코버 부인과 나는, 우리 가정생활의 제단에 강한 인연으로 얽힌 한 사람과, 여러 해 동안일 수도, 어쩌면 영영일 수도 있는 이별을 한다는 사실을 마음에서 감출 길이 없네. 그러한 떠남을 앞둔 저녁에, 그대가 우리 공동의 벗인 토머스 트래들스 씨와 함께 지금 우리가 머무는 거처로 와, 그 자리에 어울리는 소망을 함께 나눠 준다면, 그대는 다음과 같은 자에게 더없는 은혜를 베푸는 것이 되리라.\n\n영원히 그대의 것인 이 한 사람, 윌킨스 미코버.\"\n\n나는 미코버 씨가 그 잿더미를 털어 내고, 마침내 정말로 무언가 일이 풀렸다는 것을 알게 되어 기뻤다. 트래들스에게서 그 초대가 곧 저물어 가는 그날 저녁을 가리키는 것임을 듣고, 나는 기꺼이 그 자리를 빛내 드리겠노라고 했다. 그리하여 우리는 함께, 미코버 씨가 모티머 씨라는 이름으로 머물고 있는, 그레이즈 인 가 꼭대기 근처에 자리한 그 숙소로 향했다.\n\n이 숙소의 형편은 어찌나 빠듯하던지, 우리는 이제 여덟아홉 살쯤 된 쌍둥이가 가족 거실의 접이식 침대에서 잠들어 있는 것을 보았다. 그 거실에서 미코버 씨는 세면대 물병에다, 그가 그것으로 이름난 바로 그 흐뭇한 음료를 자기 말로 \"한 통\" 우려내 두었다. 나는 이 자리에서, 미코버 도령과 다시 알고 지내는 기쁨을 누렸는데, 그는 열두세 살쯤 된 장래가 촉망되는 소년으로, 그 또래 젊은이들에게 드물지 않게 나타나는 그 사지의 들썩거림에 무척 시달리고 있었다. 또한 나는 그의 누이인 미코버 양과도 다시 알게 되었는데, 미코버 씨의 말을 빌리자면, 그 아이에게서는 \"그 어미가 불사조처럼 젊음을 되살리고\" 있었다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그대와 트래들스 씨가 보다시피 우리는 이주의 문턱에 있으니, 그러한 처지에 따르기 마련인 자그마한 불편들은 너그러이 봐주시게나.\"\n\n알맞은 대답을 하며 둘레를 둘러보니, 가족의 살림살이가 이미 다 꾸려져 있고, 짐의 양도 결코 압도적일 만큼은 아님이 눈에 들어왔다. 나는 미코버 부인에게 다가오는 변화를 축하했다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"우리 집안일 전부에 대한 당신의 다정한 관심을, 저는 잘 믿고 있답니다. 제 친정 식구들이야 이걸 추방이라 여기든 말든 그건 그들 마음이지요. 하지만 저는 한 사람의 아내요 어미입니다. 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 저버리지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n미코버 부인의 눈길에 호소받은 트래들스가, 진심 어린 마음으로 그 말에 수긍했다.\n\n\"적어도 그것이,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨, 그리고 트래들스 씨, 제가 '나, 엠마는, 그대 윌킨스를 지아비로 맞노라'라는, 돌이킬 수 없는 그 말을 따라 외었을 때 스스로 짊어진 의무에 대한 제 생각이랍니다. 저는 그 전날 밤 손촛불을 켜 놓고 그 혼인 예식문을 처음부터 끝까지 읽어 보았는데, 거기서 제가 끌어낸 결론은, 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 저버릴 수 없다는 것이었어요. 그리고,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"제가 그 예식의 뜻을 잘못 새겼을 수도 있겠지만, 저는 결단코 그러지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 다소 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"당신이 그런 일을 하리라고 누가 기대한다는 건지 나는 영 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"잘 알고 있어요, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨,\" 미코버 부인이 말을 이었다. \"제가 이제 낯선 사람들 틈에 제 운명을 던지려 한다는 것을요. 또한 미코버 씨가 그 사실을 알리려고 더없이 점잖은 말로 편지를 보낸 제 친정 식구들이, 미코버 씨의 그 글에 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳하지 않았다는 것도요. 어쩌면 제가 미신을 믿는 것인지도 모르겠습니다만,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"제가 보기에 미코버 씨는 자기가 쓰는 글의 거의 대부분에 대해 그 어떤 답장도 결코 받지 못할 운명인 것만 같아요. 친정 식구들의 침묵으로 미루어, 제가 내린 결심에 그들이 반대하는 거라고 점쳐 볼 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 저는, 코퍼필드 씨, 설령 친정 아버지 어머니가 아직 살아 계신다 한들, 마땅히 가야 할 길에서 결코 벗어나지 않겠어요.\"\n\n나는 그것이 옳은 방향으로 가는 것이라는 내 의견을 밝혔다. \"성당이 있는 도시에 스스로를 가두는 것이,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 하나의 희생일 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 코퍼필드 씨, 정녕 그것이 저에게 희생이라면, 미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 분에게는 훨씬 더 큰 희생일 거예요.\"\n\n\"오! 성당이 있는 도시로 가시는군요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n세면대 물병에서 우리 모두에게 음료를 따라 주고 있던 미코버 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"캔터베리로 가네. 실은, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 나는 어떤 약정을 맺었는데, 그에 따라 우리 벗 힙에게 — 그의 심복 사무원 자격으로 그를 보좌하고 섬기기로 — 맹세하고 계약한 몸이 되었다네.\"\n\n나는 미코버 씨를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 그는 내 놀라움을 무척이나 즐겼다.\n\n\"그대에게 꼭 말해 두어야겠는데,\" 그가 격식 차린 태도로 말했다. \"미코버 부인의 사무적인 습성과 신중한 제안이, 이 결과를 끌어내는 데 크게 한몫했다네. 미코버 부인이 전에 한번 말한 그 장갑이 광고의 형태로 던져졌고, 그것을 내 벗 힙이 집어 들면서, 서로를 알아보게 된 것이지. 내 벗 힙으로 말할 것 같으면,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"놀랍도록 빈틈없는 사람인데, 나는 그에 대해 가능한 한 온갖 경의를 다해 말하고 싶네. 내 벗 힙은 확정된 보수를 너무 높은 액수로 정하지는 않았으나, 금전적 곤경의 압박에서 벗어나는 일과 관련해서는, 내 봉사의 값어치에 달린 것으로 많은 것을 약속해 주었네. 그리고 나는 바로 그 봉사의 값어치에 내 믿음을 거네. 나에게 우연히 갖춰진 그만한 수완과 지력이라면,\" 미코버 씨가 옛날의 그 점잖은 태도로, 자기를 한껏 낮추는 척 뽐내며 말했다. \"내 벗 힙을 섬기는 데 바쳐질 것이네. 나는 이미 법에 대해 어느 정도 아는 바가 있어 — 민사 소송의 피고로서 말이지 — 그러니 곧장 우리 영국에서 가장 저명하고 빼어난 법학자 한 분의 주해서에 매달릴 작정이네. 내가 말하는 분이 블랙스톤 판사님이라는 건 굳이 덧붙일 필요가 없겠지.\"\n\n이런 말들은, 그리고 사실 그날 저녁에 오간 말들의 대부분은, 미코버 도령이 자기 장화 위에 걸터앉거나, 머리가 헐거워지기라도 한 듯 두 팔로 머리를 감싸 쥐거나, 탁자 밑에서 얼떨결에 트래들스를 걷어차거나, 두 발을 서로 비비적대거나, 자연의 섭리를 거스를 만큼 터무니없는 거리에 두 발을 쭉 뻗어 놓거나, 머리카락을 와인 잔 사이에 늘어뜨린 채 옆으로 드러눕거나, 그 밖에 사회의 공익과 영 어울리지 않는 어떤 다른 모양새로 사지의 들썩거림을 드러내는 것을 미코버 부인이 발견할 때마다, 그리고 미코버 도령이 그러한 발견을 못마땅한 기색으로 받아들일 때마다 끊겼다. 나는 그동안 줄곧, 미코버 씨가 털어놓은 이야기에 어안이 벙벙해, 그것이 대체 무슨 뜻인지 곱씹으며 앉아 있었다. 그러다 미코버 부인이 다시 이야기의 실마리를 이어 가며 내 주의를 끌었다.\n\n\"제가 미코버 씨에게 각별히 조심하라고 당부하는 것은요,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨, 그이가 법의 이 하급 부문에 매진하느라, 끝내 나무 꼭대기까지 오를 힘을 스스로 잃어버리는 일이 없도록 하라는 거예요. 저는 확신해요. 미코버 씨가 자신의 풍부한 자질과 거침없는 언변에 그토록 잘 맞는 직업에 마음을 쏟는다면, 틀림없이 두각을 나타내리라는 것을요. 자, 예를 들면요, 트래들스 씨,\" 미코버 부인이 자못 심오한 태도를 지으며 말했다. \"판사, 아니 대법관이라도 말이에요. 미코버 씨가 받아들인 그런 직책을 맡는다고 해서, 그 사람이 그러한 영전의 울타리 밖으로 밀려나기라도 하나요?\"\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 한마디 했다 — 그러나 트래들스를 향해 캐묻듯 흘끔거리기도 하면서. \"그런 문제들을 따져 볼 시간은 우리 앞에 얼마든지 있소.\"\n\n\"미코버,\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"아니에요! 당신의 인생에서 잘못된 점은, 멀리까지 충분히 내다보지 않는다는 거예요. 당신은, 당신 자신을 위해서는 아니더라도, 적어도 가족에 대한 도리로서, 당신의 능력이 데려다줄 수 있는 지평선의 그 가장 먼 지점까지 한눈에 담아 보아야 마땅해요.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 기침을 하더니, 더없이 흡족한 태도로 펀치를 마셨다 — 여전히 트래들스를 흘끔거리면서, 마치 그의 의견을 듣고 싶다는 듯이.\n\n\"저, 사정을 있는 그대로 말씀드리자면 말입니다, 미코버 부인,\" 트래들스가 부드럽게 진실을 그녀에게 일러 주며 말했다. \"제 말은, 그 진짜 무미건조한 사실 말입니다만—\"\n\n\"바로 그거예요,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 트래들스 씨, 이렇게 중요한 문제에 대해서라면 저야말로 되도록 무미건조하고 곧이곧대로이고 싶답니다.\"\n\n\"—그러니까,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"법의 이 부문은, 설령 미코버 씨가 정식 사무 변호사라 하더라도—\"\n\n\"바로 그래요,\" 미코버 부인이 받았다. (\"윌킨스, 당신 사팔뜨기가 되겠어요, 눈이 도로 안 돌아올 거예요.\")\n\n\"—그것과는,\" 트래들스가 말을 이었다. \"아무 상관이 없습니다. 그런 영전에 오를 자격이 있는 건 오직 법정 변호사뿐인데, 미코버 씨는 법학원에 학생으로 등록해 오 년을 채우지 않고서는 법정 변호사가 될 수 없거든요.\"\n\n\"제가 제대로 따라가고 있나요?\" 미코버 부인이 더없이 상냥한 사무적 태도로 말했다. \"그러니까 제가 이해하기로는, 내 사랑하는 트래들스 씨, 그 기간이 끝나면 미코버 씨가 판사나 대법관이 될 자격이 생긴다는 거지요?\"\n\n\"자격은 생기지요.\" 트래들스가 그 말에 힘주어 강조하며 대답했다.\n\n\"고마워요,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그것으로 충분해요. 사정이 그렇고, 미코버 씨가 이 직무를 맡는다고 해서 아무런 특권도 잃지 않는다면, 제 근심은 가라앉은 셈이에요. 저야 어쩔 수 없이,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"한낱 여자로서 말하는 것입니다만, 저는 늘 미코버 씨가, 제가 친정에서 살 때 아버지가 부르시던 말로 하자면 '법관다운 두뇌'를 지녔다고 여겨 왔답니다. 그리고 이제 미코버 씨가 그 두뇌를 펼쳐 내고 높은 자리를 차지할 수 있는 마당에 들어서기를 바라요.\"\n\n나는 미코버 씨가 그 법관다운 두뇌의 눈으로, 자기가 대법관의 양모 방석 위에 앉아 있는 모습을 보고 있다는 걸 똑똑히 믿었다. 그는 만족스러운 듯 자기 대머리를 쓸어 넘기더니, 짐짓 체념한 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"여보, 우리 운명의 판결을 미리 점치지는 맙시다. 만일 내가 가발을 쓸 운명이라면, 적어도 겉으로는,\" 자기 대머리를 빗대어, \"그 영예를 맞을 채비가 되어 있는 셈이지. 나는,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 머리카락을 아쉬워하지 않네. 어쩌면 어떤 특별한 목적이 있어 그것을 잃은 건지도 모르지. 그건 나도 알 수 없네. 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 나는 내 아들을 교회를 위해 키울 작정이네. 그 아이를 생각하면, 내가 높은 자리에 오를 수 있다면 행복하겠다는 것을 굳이 감추지는 않겠네.\"\n\n\"교회를 위해서요?\" 내가, 여전히 틈틈이 유라이아 힙을 곰곰이 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다네,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그 아이는 두성이 빼어나니, 성가대원으로 시작할 걸세. 우리가 캔터베리에 거처를 두고, 그곳에 연줄이 생기면, 틀림없이 성당 성가대에 자리가 나는 대로 그 기회를 잡을 수 있을 게야.\"\n\n미코버 도령을 다시 보니, 어딘지 자기 목소리가 두 눈썹 뒤에 있기라도 한 듯한 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그리고 그 목소리는 곧, 그가 (노래를 부르든지 잠자리에 들든지 둘 중 하나를 택하라기에) \"딱따구리가 또드락또드락\"을 우리에게 불러 줄 때, 정말로 거기 있는 것처럼 보였다. 이 노래 솜씨에 숱한 칭찬을 늘어놓은 뒤, 우리는 이런저런 이야기에 빠져들었다. 그리고 나는 필사의 각오를 품은 마음이 너무 벅차서 달라진 내 형편을 혼자만 알고 있을 수가 없어, 미코버 씨 부부에게 그것을 털어놓았다. 고모님이 곤경에 처했다는 생각에 두 사람이 얼마나 더없이 기뻐했는지, 그리고 그것이 두 사람을 얼마나 편안하고 다정하게 만들었는지, 나는 이루 말로 다 할 수가 없다.\n\n펀치가 거의 마지막 잔에 이르렀을 때, 나는 트래들스에게로 몸을 돌려, 우리 벗들의 건강과 행복과 새 출발에서의 성공을 빌어 주지 않고서는 헤어져서는 안 된다고 일러 주었다. 나는 미코버 씨에게 잔을 가득 채워 달라고 청하고, 격식을 갖춰 건배를 제안했다. 탁자 너머로 그와 악수하고, 미코버 부인에게는 입을 맞추어, 그 뜻깊은 자리를 기렸다. 트래들스는 첫 번째 것은 나를 따라 했으나, 두 번째 것을 감행할 만큼 오랜 벗은 못 된다고 여겼다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 조끼 양쪽 주머니에 엄지손가락을 하나씩 찔러 넣은 채 자리에서 일어나며 말했다. \"내 젊은 날의 벗 — 이런 표현을 써도 좋다면 — 그리고 내가 존경하는 벗 트래들스 — 그를 그렇게 불러도 된다면 — 이 두 사람은, 미코버 부인과 나 자신과 우리 자식들을 대신해, 그들의 따뜻한 소망에 대해 더없이 뜨겁고 한 점 에누리 없는 말로 감사드리는 것을 허락해 주리라 믿네. 우리를 전혀 새로운 삶으로 데려갈 이주를 앞둔 저녁에,\" 미코버 씨는 마치 그들이 오십만 마일이라도 떠나는 양 말했다. \"내 눈앞에 보이는 이런 두 벗에게 작별의 말 몇 마디를 건네는 것이 마땅하다고 기대할 법도 하지. 하지만 그런 식으로 내가 할 말은, 이미 다 했네. 내가 이제 그 변변찮은 일원이 되려 하는 그 학식 있는 직업을 통해 사회에서 어떤 지위에 오르든, 나는 그것을 욕되게 하지 않으려 애쓸 것이고, 미코버 부인은 틀림없이 그것을 빛내 줄 걸세. 즉시 갚을 작정으로 졌으나 여러 사정이 겹쳐 끝내 갚지 못한 금전적 부채의 일시적인 압박 아래, 나는 내 타고난 본성이 움츠러드는 차림새를 어쩔 수 없이 걸쳐야 했네 — 내가 말하는 것은 안경일세 — 그리고 정당하게 내세울 근거라고는 하나도 없는 가명을 갖다 쓰기도 했지. 그 점에 대해 내가 할 말은 오직 이것뿐이네. 그 음울한 풍경에서 먹구름이 걷히고, 낮의 신께서 다시금 산봉우리 높이 떠오르셨다는 것 말일세. 다가오는 월요일, 오후 네 시 마차가 캔터베리에 닿으면, 내 발은 내 고향 땅을 딛고 설 것이네 — 내 이름은, 미코버일세!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 이 말을 맺으며 다시 자리에 앉더니, 엄숙하게 펀치 두 잔을 잇따라 들이켰다. 그러고는 자못 장중하게 말했다.\n\n\"이 이별이 온전히 마무리되기 전에 한 가지 더 해야 할 일이 있으니, 그것은 정의의 행위를 하나 행하는 것이네. 내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨는, 두 번에 걸쳐, 흔한 표현을 빌리자면, 나의 편의를 위해 환어음에 '자기 이름을 올려' 주었네. 첫 번째 경우, 토머스 트래들스 씨는 — 짧게 말하자면 — 곤경에 내팽개쳐졌네. 두 번째 것의 만기는 아직 돌아오지 않았고. 첫 번째 채무의 액수는,\" 여기서 미코버 씨는 서류를 꼼꼼히 들춰 보았다. \"내 기억에 이십삼 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스 반이었고, 두 번째 것은, 그 거래에 대한 내 기록에 따르면 십팔 파운드 육 실링 이 펜스였네. 이 두 액수를 합하면, 내 계산이 맞다면, 도합 사십일 파운드 십 실링 십일 펜스 반이 되네. 내 벗 코퍼필드가 그 합계를 좀 확인해 주는 친절을 베풀어 주겠나?\"\n\n나는 그렇게 했고, 맞다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"이 대도시와,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨를 떠나면서, 이 채무의 금전적인 부분을 스스로 청산하지 않는다면, 그것은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 내 마음을 짓누를 걸세. 그래서 나는 내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨를 위해, 그 바라던 바를 이루어 줄 증서를 한 장 마련했고, 지금 그것을 손에 들고 있네. 내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨에게 사십일 파운드 십 실링 십일 펜스 반에 대한 내 차용증을 건네는 바이며, 나는 내 도덕적 존엄을 되찾아, 다시 한번 동포 앞에서 허리를 곧추세우고 걸을 수 있게 되었음을 기쁘게 여기네!\"\n\n이 서두(이것이 그를 무척이나 감동시켰다)와 함께, 미코버 씨는 자신의 차용증을 트래들스의 손에 쥐여 주며, 인생의 온갖 일에서 그가 잘되기를 바란다고 했다. 나는 이것이 미코버 씨에게는 돈을 갚은 것과 조금도 다를 바 없을 뿐 아니라, 트래들스 자신도 곰곰이 생각해 볼 틈이 생기기 전까지는 그 차이를 거의 알아채지 못했으리라고 확신한다. 미코버 씨는 이 갸륵한 행위의 힘을 빌려 동포 앞에서 어찌나 허리를 곧추세우고 걸었던지, 우리에게 불을 비추며 아래층으로 안내할 때는 그의 가슴팍이 절반은 더 떡 벌어져 보일 지경이었다. 우리는 양쪽 다 더없이 진심 어린 마음으로 작별했다. 그리고 트래들스를 그의 집 문 앞까지 바래다주고 홀로 집으로 가면서, 나는 골똘히 생각하던 다른 야릇하고 모순된 일들 가운데, 이런 생각이 들었다. 미코버 씨가 그토록 미끄러운 사람이긴 해도, 그가 나에게 단 한 번도 돈을 빌려 달라고 하지 않은 것은, 아마도 나를 그의 어린 하숙생으로 기억하며 품어 온 어떤 가엾이 여기는 마음 덕분이었으리라고. 나라면 그것을 거절할 도덕적 용기가 분명 없었을 것이다. 그리고 그도 그것을 (그의 명예를 위해 적어 두건대) 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었으리라는 것을, 나는 조금도 의심하지 않는다.\n\n# CHAPTER 37. A LITTLE COLD WATER\n\n내 새로운 삶이 시작된 지 일주일이 넘었고, 지금 이 위기가 요구한다고 느낀 그 엄청난 실천적 결심들에 있어서 나는 어느 때보다 단단했다. 나는 여전히 무서운 속도로 걸어 다녔고, 내가 앞으로 나아가고 있다는 막연한 느낌을 품고 있었다. 무엇이든 내 정력을 쏟아붓는 일이라면, 나 자신을 최대한 쥐어짜 내는 것을 하나의 규칙으로 삼았다. 나는 나 자신을 완벽한 제물로 바쳤다. 심지어 채식만 하겠다는 생각까지 잠깐 품었는데, 풀만 먹는 짐승이 되면 도라를 위해 나를 희생하는 셈이 되리라는 막연한 상상에서였다.\n\n지금까지 어린 도라는 내 굳건한 결의를 거의 알아채지 못했다. 내 편지가 그것을 어렴풋이 그림자처럼 비춰 보인 것 말고는 말이다. 그러나 또 한 번 토요일이 돌아왔고, 그 토요일 저녁에 도라는 밀스 양 집에 와 있기로 되어 있었다. 그리고 밀스 씨가 자기 휘스트 클럽에 가고 나면 (응접실 가운데 창문에 내건 새장으로 길거리에 있는 나에게 신호를 보내기로 했다) 나는 그 집에 차를 마시러 가기로 했다.\n\n이 무렵 우리는 버킹엄 거리에 완전히 자리를 잡았고, 그곳에서 딕 씨는 더없는 행복에 젖어 필사 일을 이어 갔다. 고모님은 크럽 부인을 상대로 눈부신 승리를 거두었는데, 밀린 삯을 깨끗이 치러 내보내고, 그녀가 계단에 떡하니 갖다 놓은 첫 번째 물병을 창밖으로 내던지고, 바깥세상에서 새로 고용한 임시 일꾼을 몸소 계단 위아래로 따라다니며 호위한 덕분이었다. 이 강경한 조치들은 크럽 부인의 가슴에 어찌나 큰 공포를 심어 주었던지, 그녀는 고모님이 미쳤다고 단정한 채 제 부엌으로 슬그머니 물러나 버렸다. 고모님은 크럽 부인을 비롯한 그 누구의 의견에도 더없이 무관심했고, 그런 인상을 말리기는커녕 오히려 부추기는 쪽이었던 터라, 한때 그토록 대담하던 크럽 부인은 며칠 사이 어찌나 겁쟁이가 되었던지, 계단에서 고모님과 마주치느니 차라리 문 뒤에 제 육중한 몸을 숨기려고 애쓰거나 — 그러면서도 플란넬 속치마 자락이 넓게 비어져 나오는 건 어쩌지 못했다 — 어두운 구석으로 움츠러들곤 했다. 이것이 고모님에게 어찌나 말할 수 없는 만족을 주었던지, 나는 고모님이 크럽 부인이 어슬렁댈 만한 시간이면 보닛을 머리 꼭대기에 미친 듯이 얹은 채 계단을 오르내리며 어슬렁대는 것을 즐겼다고 믿는다.\n\n고모님은 유난히 깔끔하고 손재주가 좋아서, 우리 집 살림살이에 자질구레한 개선을 어찌나 많이 해 놓았던지, 나는 형편이 더 가난해지기는커녕 오히려 더 부유해진 듯한 느낌이 들 정도였다. 그중에서도 고모님은 식료품 저장실을 내 옷방으로 바꿔 놓았고, 내가 쓸 침대를 하나 사다가 꾸며 주었는데, 낮에는 침대가 책장처럼 보일 수 있는 한도 내에서 영락없는 책장으로 보였다. 나는 고모님이 끊임없이 마음 쓰는 대상이었다. 가엾은 어머니라 할지라도 나를 그보다 더 사랑하거나, 나를 행복하게 해 주려고 그보다 더 골몰하지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n패거티는 이 일들에 한몫 끼도록 허락받은 것을 큰 특권으로 여겼다. 그리고 고모님에 대한 옛날의 경외심 같은 것을 아직 얼마쯤 간직하고 있긴 했어도, 격려와 신뢰의 표시를 어찌나 많이 받았던지, 둘은 더없이 좋은 친구 사이가 되었다. 그러나 이제 (밀스 양 집에 차를 마시러 가기로 한 그 토요일 이야기다) 패거티가 고향으로 돌아가, 햄을 위해 떠맡은 의무를 이행하기 시작해야 할 때가 왔다. \"그럼 잘 가요, 바키스.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"몸조심하고! 내가 당신을 잃는 게 섭섭하리라곤 정말이지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없는데 말이야!\"\n\n나는 패거티를 마차 정거장까지 데려다주고 배웅했다. 그녀는 헤어지면서 울었고, 햄이 그랬던 것처럼 제 오빠를 내 우정에 부탁했다. 그가 그 화창한 오후에 떠난 뒤로 우리는 그에 대해 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 터였다.\n\n\"그리고 이제, 우리 사랑하는 데이비.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"도련님이 견습생으로 있는 동안 쓸 돈이 좀 필요하거나, 아니면 견습이 끝나고 나서 자리를 잡는 데 밑천이 좀 필요하거든 (도련님은 둘 중 하나는, 아니 어쩌면 둘 다 해야 할 거예요, 우리 도련님), 그 돈을 빌려 드리게 해 달라고 부탁할 권리가, 우리 예쁜 아씨가 키운 늙고 어리석은 이 몸만큼 떳떳한 사람이 또 어디 있겠어요!\"\n\n나는 누구에게든 돈을 빌리게 된다면 그건 그녀에게서 빌리겠노라고 대답하는 것 말고는, 야박하게 자존심을 세울 만큼 모질지 못했다. 그 자리에서 큰돈을 덥석 받는 것 다음으로, 이 대답이 내가 해 줄 수 있었던 그 무엇보다도 패거티에게 큰 위안이 되었다고 나는 믿는다.\n\n\"그리고, 도련님!\" 패거티가 속삭였다. \"그 예쁜 꼬마 천사한테 전해 줘요, 단 일 분만이라도 그분을 정말 너무너무 보고 싶었다고요! 그리고 그분이 우리 도련님과 결혼하기 전에, 도련님이 허락만 해 주신다면, 내가 가서 도련님 집을 아주 아름답게 꾸며 드리겠다고요!\"\n\n나는 다른 누구의 손도 그 집에 대지 못하게 하겠노라고 단언했다. 이것이 패거티에게 어찌나 큰 기쁨을 주었던지, 그녀는 기분 좋게 떠났다.\n\n나는 종일 민사 법원에서 갖가지 방법을 동원해 최대한 나 자신을 녹초로 만들었고, 저녁의 약속 시간에 맞춰 밀스 씨네 거리로 향했다. 저녁을 먹고 나면 곯아떨어지기로 악명 높은 밀스 씨는 아직 나가지 않은 상태였고, 가운데 창문에는 새장이 걸려 있지 않았다.\n\n밀스 씨가 어찌나 오래 나를 기다리게 했던지, 나는 클럽이 그에게 지각 벌금을 물리기를 간절히 빌었다. 마침내 그가 나왔다. 그러자 나의 도라가 새장을 거는 것이 보였고, 발코니로 고개를 내밀어 나를 찾다가, 내가 거기 있는 것을 보고는 다시 안으로 뛰어 들어가는 것이 보였다. 그동안 집은 뒤에 남아, 길거리의 거대한 푸줏간 개를 향해 사납게 짖어 댔는데, 그 개는 집을 알약 삼키듯 한입에 꿀꺽할 수도 있었을 것이다.\n\n도라가 나를 맞으러 응접실 문으로 나왔다. 집은 제 으르렁거리는 소리에 제풀에 걸려 넘어지며 허둥지둥 기어 나왔는데, 나를 산적쯤으로 여기는 모양이었다. 그렇게 우리 셋은 더없이 행복하고 다정하게 안으로 들어갔다. 그러나 나는 곧 우리의 기쁨 한가운데에 황폐함을 들이고 말았다 — 일부러 그런 것은 아니고, 그 문제로 머릿속이 가득 차 있었던 탓이다 — 아무런 사전 준비도 없이, 거지를 사랑할 수 있겠느냐고 도라에게 물어버린 것이다.\n\n내 예쁘고 작고 깜짝 놀란 도라! 그녀가 그 단어를 두고 떠올릴 수 있는 거라곤 누런 얼굴에 잠자리 모자를 쓴 사람, 아니면 한 쌍의 목발, 나무 의족, 혹은 술병 받침대를 입에 문 개, 그런 것들뿐이었다. 그래서 그녀는 더없이 사랑스러운 어리둥절함으로 나를 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n\"어떻게 그런 바보 같은 걸 물어볼 수 있어요?\" 도라가 입을 삐죽였다. \"거지를 사랑하라니!\"\n\n\"도라, 내 가장 사랑하는 사람!\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 거지요!\"\n\n\"어떻게 그렇게 어리석을 수가 있어요.\" 도라가 내 손을 찰싹 때리며 대꾸했다. \"거기 앉아서 그런 거짓말이나 하고 있다니. 집을 시켜서 물어 버리게 할 거예요!\"\n\n그녀의 그 어린애 같은 태도는 내게 세상에서 가장 달콤한 것이었지만, 나는 분명하게 말할 필요가 있었기에, 엄숙하게 되풀이했다.\n\n\"도라, 내 생명, 나는 파산한 당신의 데이비드요!\"\n\n\"정말 집을 시켜서 물어 버리게 할 거예요!\" 도라가 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"그렇게 우스꽝스럽게 굴면요.\"\n\n그러나 내가 어찌나 심각한 표정을 짓고 있었던지, 도라는 곱슬머리 흔들기를 멈추고, 떨리는 작은 손을 내 어깨에 얹더니, 처음에는 겁먹은 듯 불안한 표정을 짓다가, 이내 울기 시작했다. 그것은 끔찍했다. 나는 소파 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀를 어루만지며, 제발 내 가슴을 갈가리 찢지 말아 달라고 애원했다. 그러나 한동안 가엾고 작은 도라는 그저 아이고, 어머나! 아이고, 어머나! 하고 외치기만 했다. 게다가, 아, 그녀는 너무도 겁에 질려 있었다! 그리고 줄리아 밀스는 어디 있느냐고! 아, 제발 자기를 줄리아 밀스에게 데려다주고 가 버리라고! 그렇게 외쳐 대는 통에 나는 거의 제정신이 아니었다.\n\n마침내, 간청과 항변의 고통스러운 몸부림 끝에, 나는 도라가 공포에 질린 얼굴로 나를 바라보게 만들었고, 그 표정을 차츰차츰 달래어 마침내 사랑스러운 빛만 남게 했으며, 그녀의 부드럽고 예쁜 뺨이 내 뺨에 기대게 했다. 그러고 나서 나는 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안은 채 그녀에게 말했다. 내가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 너무도 깊이, 너무도 깊이 사랑하는지를. 이제 내가 가난해졌으니 약혼에서 그녀를 풀어 주겠다고 제안하는 것이 옳다고 느꼈음을. 만약 그녀를 잃는다면 나는 결코 그것을 견디거나 회복할 수 없으리라는 것을. 그녀만 두렵지 않다면 나도 가난이 조금도 두렵지 않다는 것을, 그녀가 내 팔에 힘을 주고 내 가슴을 북돋아 주니까. 내가 이미 연인들만이 아는 그런 용기를 가지고 일하고 있다는 것을. 내가 실천적인 사람이 되어 미래를 내다보기 시작했다는 것을. 정직하게 벌어들인 빵 껍질 한 조각이 물려받은 잔칫상보다 훨씬 더 달콤하다는 것을. 그리고 그 밖에도 같은 취지의 많은 말을, 나 자신도 꽤나 놀랄 만큼 격정적인 웅변을 쏟아내며 전했는데, 사실 고모님이 나를 깜짝 놀라게 한 이래로 밤낮없이 그 일을 생각해 온 터였다.\n\n\"당신 마음은 아직도 내 것이오, 사랑하는 도라?\" 내가 황홀하게 말했다. 그녀가 내게 매달리는 것으로 그렇다는 걸 알았기 때문이다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요!\" 도라가 외쳤다. \"오, 그럼요, 전부 당신 거예요. 오, 제발 끔찍하게 굴지 말아요!\"\n\n내가 끔찍하다니! 도라에게!\n\n\"가난하다느니, 힘들게 일한다느니, 그런 얘기는 하지 말아요!\" 도라가 내게 더 바싹 파고들며 말했다. \"오, 제발, 제발!\"\n\n\"내 가장 사랑하는 사람.\" 내가 말했다. \"정직하게 벌어들인 빵 껍질은—\"\n\n\"오, 그래요. 하지만 빵 껍질 얘기는 더 듣고 싶지 않아요!\" 도라가 말했다. \"그리고 집은 매일 열두 시에 양고기 갈비를 먹어야 해요, 안 그러면 죽어 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n나는 그녀의 어린애 같고 사랑스러운 태도에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 나는 집이 늘 하던 대로 어김없이 양고기 갈비를 먹게 될 거라고 도라에게 다정하게 설명해 주었다. 나는 내 노동으로 자립한 우리의 검소한 보금자리를 그림처럼 그려 보였다 — 하이게이트에서 본 그 작은 집을, 그리고 위층 방에 있는 고모님을 스케치하면서.\n\n\"이제 나 끔찍하지 않지요, 도라?\" 내가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요!\" 도라가 외쳤다. \"하지만 당신 고모님은 자기 방에 많이 계셨으면 좋겠어요. 그리고 잔소리하는 늙은이가 아니었으면 좋겠고요!\"\n\n내가 도라를 이전보다 더 사랑하는 것이 가능한 일이라면, 분명 나는 그러했다. 그러나 나는 그녀가 좀 비현실적이라는 것을 느꼈다. 갓 피어난 내 열정을 그녀에게 전하는 것이 그토록 어렵다는 사실은 그 열정에 찬물을 끼얹었다. 나는 한 번 더 시도해 보았다. 그녀가 완전히 제정신을 되찾고, 제 무릎 위에 누운 집의 귀를 돌돌 말고 있을 때, 나는 진지해져서 말했다.\n\n\"내 사람! 내가 뭔가 한마디 해도 될까요?\"\n\n\"오, 제발 실천적으로 굴지 말아요!\" 도라가 어르듯 말했다. \"그건 나를 너무 무섭게 하니까요!\"\n\n\"내 사랑!\" 내가 대꾸했다. \"이 모든 일에 당신을 놀라게 할 건 하나도 없어요. 나는 당신이 이걸 아주 다르게 생각해 주었으면 해요. 나는 이것이 당신에게 힘을 주고, 당신을 북돋아 주기를 바라요, 도라!\"\n\n\"오, 하지만 그건 너무 충격적이에요!\" 도라가 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 사랑, 천만에요. 끈기와 굳건한 인격이 있으면 우리는 이보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 일도 견딜 수 있어요.\" \"하지만 난 굳건함이라곤 조금도 없는걸요.\" 도라가 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"안 그래, 집? 오, 어서 집에게 입 맞추고, 좀 상냥하게 굴어요!\"\n\n그녀가 입맞춤을 받게 하려고 집을 내게 들어 올리고는, 자기 입으로 그 동작을 지휘하면서 제 밝고 발그레한 작은 입을 입맞춤 모양으로 오므렸을 때, 나는 집에게 입 맞추지 않을 도리가 없었다. 그녀는 그 입맞춤이 반드시 집의 코 한가운데에 좌우 대칭으로 행해져야 한다고 우겼다. 나는 그녀가 시키는 대로 했고 — 그러고 나서는 그 순종에 대해 나 자신에게 보상을 내렸다 — 그녀는 얼마나 오랫동안인지 모를 시간 동안 나를 그 진지한 태도에서 홀려 끌어냈다.\n\n\"하지만, 도라, 내 사랑하는 사람!\" 마침내 나는 다시 그 태도를 되찾으며 말했다. \"내가 뭔가 한마디 하려던 참이었어요.\"\n\n작은 두 손을 모아 들어 올린 채 제발 더는 끔찍하게 굴지 말아 달라고 빌고 애원하는 그녀의 모습을 보았다면, 유산 검인 법원의 판사라도 그녀에게 반해 버렸을 것이다.\n\n\"정말이지 그러지 않을게요, 내 사랑!\" 나는 그녀를 안심시켰다. \"하지만, 도라, 내 사랑, 당신이 가끔씩만이라도 생각해 준다면 — 낙담해서 그러란 건 아니에요. 결코 그런 게 아니라! — 그저 가끔씩만 생각해 준다면 — 그냥 스스로 마음을 다잡기 위해서라도 — 당신이 가난한 남자와 약혼한 사이라는 걸—\"\n\n\"하지 말아요, 하지 말아요! 제발 하지 말아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다. \"그건 너무너무 끔찍해요!\"\n\n\"내 영혼, 전혀 그렇지 않아요!\" 내가 명랑하게 말했다. \"당신이 가끔씩 그걸 생각하고, 이따금 당신 아버지의 살림살이를 한번 둘러보고, 작은 습관을 — 이를테면 가계부 적는 습관 같은 걸 — 좀 익히려고 애써 준다면—\"\n\n가엾고 작은 도라는 이 제안을, 흐느낌인지 비명인지 모를 소리를 반쯤 섞어 받아들였다.\n\n\"—그게 나중에 우리한테 아주 쓸모가 있을 거예요.\" 나는 말을 이어 갔다. \"그리고 만약 당신이 약속해 준다면, 책을 조금 — 내가 보내 줄 작은 요리책을 조금 읽어 주겠다고 약속해 준다면, 그건 우리 둘 모두에게 아주 훌륭한 일이 될 거예요. 우리가 살아갈 인생길은, 도라.\" 나는 그 주제에 점점 열을 올리며 말했다. \"지금은 돌투성이에 험하지만, 그걸 평평하게 다지는 건 우리에게 달려 있어요. 우린 앞으로 싸우며 나아가야 해요. 우린 용감해야 해요. 만나야 할 장애물들이 있고, 우린 그것들과 맞서서, 짓밟아 부숴 버려야 해요!\"\n\n나는 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 더없이 열정에 찬 얼굴로 굉장한 기세로 말을 이어 가고 있었다. 그러나 더 나아갈 필요는 전혀 없었다. 나는 이미 충분히 말한 것이다. 또 그 짓을 저지르고 만 것이다. 아, 그녀는 너무도 겁에 질렸다! 아, 줄리아 밀스는 어디 있느냐고! 아, 제발 자기를 줄리아 밀스에게 데려다주고 가 버리라고! 그리하여, 요컨대 나는 완전히 정신이 나가, 응접실을 미친 듯이 헤매며 소리쳤다.\n\n이번엔 내가 그녀를 죽여 버린 줄로만 알았다. 나는 그녀의 얼굴에 물을 뿌렸다. 나는 무릎을 꿇었다. 나는 내 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다. 나는 나 자신을 무자비한 짐승이요 잔인한 야수라고 비난했다. 나는 그녀에게 용서를 빌었다. 나는 제발 눈을 떠 달라고 간청했다. 나는 냄새 맡는 약병을 찾으려고 밀스 양의 반짇고리를 헤집었는데, 마음의 고통에 사로잡힌 나머지 그 대신 상아 바늘통을 집어 들어, 도라에게 온통 바늘을 쏟아 버렸다. 나는 나만큼이나 광란에 빠진 집을 향해 두 주먹을 흔들어 댔다. 나는 할 수 있는 온갖 미친 짓을 다 했고, 밀스 양이 방으로 들어왔을 때 나는 이미 정신줄을 놓은 지 한참이었다.\n\n\"이게 누구 짓이에요?\" 밀스 양이 친구를 부축하며 외쳤다.\n\n나는 대답했다. \"나요, 밀스 양! 내가 한 짓이오! 이 파괴자를 보시오!\" — 뭐 그런 취지의 말을 하고는 — 소파 쿠션에 얼굴을 파묻어 빛으로부터 숨겼다.\n\n처음에 밀스 양은 그것이 다툼이라고, 우리가 사하라 사막의 가장자리에 다다랐다고 생각했다. 그러나 그녀는 곧 사정이 어찌 된 것인지 알아차렸는데, 나의 사랑스럽고 다정한 작은 도라가 그녀를 끌어안고서 내가 \"가난한 노동자\"라고 외쳐 대기 시작했기 때문이다. 그러고는 나를 위해 울고, 나를 끌어안고, 자기 돈을 전부 내게 맡아 달라고 주면 안 되겠느냐고 묻더니, 이내 밀스 양의 목에 매달려, 그 여린 가슴이 무너져 내리기라도 한 듯 흐느꼈다.\n\n밀스 양은 분명 우리에게 축복이 되려고 태어난 사람이었다. 그녀는 몇 마디로 내게서 무슨 일인지를 알아내고, 도라를 달래고, 차츰차츰 그녀에게 내가 노동자가 아니라는 것을 납득시켰다 — 내가 사정을 설명한 방식 때문에 도라는 내가 뱃사람이 되어, 외바퀴 손수레를 밀며 종일 널빤지 위에서 몸을 휘청휘청 가누고 있는 거라고 결론지은 모양이었다 — 그렇게 우리를 화목하게 다시 이어 주었다. 우리가 완전히 진정되고, 도라가 두 눈에 장미수를 좀 바르려고 위층으로 올라가자, 밀스 양이 차를 가져오라고 벨을 울렸다. 뒤이은 그 짬에, 나는 밀스 양에게 그녀가 영원토록 나의 벗이며, 내 심장이 고동을 멈추기 전에는 그녀의 동정심을 결코 잊지 못할 것이라고 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 나는 도라에게 그토록 처참하게 실패한 채로 설명하려 애썼던 것을 밀스 양에게 풀어놓았다. 밀스 양은 일반론적으로 대답하기를, 만족이 깃든 오두막이 차디찬 화려함의 궁전보다 낫고, 사랑이 있는 곳에 모든 것이 있다고 했다.\n\n나는 밀스 양에게, 그것은 참으로 맞는 말이며, 일찍이 어떤 인간도 겪어 보지 못한 그런 사랑으로 도라를 사랑하는 나만큼 그것을 잘 아는 사람이 또 어디 있겠느냐고 말했다. 그러나 밀스 양이 침울하게, 정말이지 어떤 이들의 마음에게는 그것이 사실이라면 다행이겠다고 말하자, 나는 그 말을 남성에 속한 인간들에게로 한정해 주십사 부탁드린다고 설명했다.\n\n그러고 나서 나는 밀스 양에게, 내가 그토록 간절히 하려고 했던 그 제안 — 가계부와 살림살이와 요리책에 관한 그 제안에, 과연 실천적인 가치가 있다고 보는지 없다고 보는지를 말해 달라고 청했다.\n\n밀스 양은 잠시 생각하더니 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 솔직하게 말씀드릴게요. 마음의 고통과 시련은, 어떤 사람에게는 세월을 대신해 주는 법이지요. 그러니 저는 마치 수녀원장이라도 된 듯이 솔직하게 말씀드리겠어요. 안 돼요. 그 제안은 우리 도라에게는 어울리지 않아요. 우리 가장 사랑스러운 도라는 자연이 어여삐 여기는 아이예요. 그 애는 빛이고, 산들거림이고, 기쁨이지요. 솔직히 말씀드리면, 그게 가능하기만 하다면야 좋겠지만, 하지만—\" 그러고는 밀스 양이 고개를 절레절레 저었다.\n\n밀스 양이 끝에 이렇게 인정해 주는 데 용기를 얻어, 나는 그녀에게 물어보았다. 도라를 위해서, 혹시라도 그 애의 마음을 진지한 삶을 위한 그런 준비 쪽으로 살며시 끌어당길 기회가 생긴다면, 그 기회를 놓치지 않고 잡아 주겠느냐고. 밀스 양이 어찌나 선뜻 그러겠노라 대답하던지, 나는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 요리책을 맡아 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그리고 혹시 도라를 겁주지 않으면서 그 책을 슬며시 받아들이게 할 수만 있다면, 그 더없는 수고를 베풀어 주겠느냐고도. 밀스 양은 이 임무도 받아들였다. 다만 그리 자신만만해하지는 않았다.\n\n이윽고 도라가 돌아왔는데, 어찌나 사랑스러운 작은 생명체로 보이던지, 나는 정말이지 이 아이에게 그런 평범하기 짝이 없는 일로 걱정을 끼쳐도 되는 건지 의심스러워졌다. 게다가 그 애는 나를 어찌나 사랑하던지, 또 어찌나 마음을 사로잡던지 (특히 집을 뒷발로 서게 해서 토스트를 받게 하거나, 그러기를 마다한다고 벌을 준답시고 그 코를 뜨거운 찻주전자에다 갖다 대는 시늉을 할 때면 더더욱 그랬다), 나는 그 애를 겁주고 울게 만들었던 일을 떠올릴 때마다, 마치 요정의 정원에 쳐들어간 괴물이라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤에는 기타를 꺼냈다. 그리고 도라는 그 정답고 오래된 프랑스 노래들, 무슨 일이 있어도 도무지 춤추기를 멈출 수 없다는 그 노래들을, 라 라 라, 라 라 라, 하고 불렀고, 그럴수록 나는 아까보다 훨씬 더 큰 괴물이 된 기분이었다.\n\n우리의 즐거움에 찬물을 끼얹은 일은 단 한 번뿐이었는데, 그것은 내가 작별을 고하기 조금 전에 벌어졌다. 밀스 양이 어쩌다 내일 아침 이야기를 슬쩍 꺼냈을 때, 나는 불행히도 그만 입을 놀려, 이제는 부지런히 애써야 하는 처지라 다섯 시에 일어난다고 털어놓고 만 것이다. 도라가 나를 야경꾼쯤으로 여겼는지 어땠는지는 알 수 없으나, 어쨌든 그 말은 그 애에게 큰 충격을 주었고, 그 애는 더는 연주도 노래도 하지 않았다.\n\n내가 작별 인사를 할 때까지도 그 일은 그 애의 마음에 걸려 있었다. 그러더니 그 애는, 마치 내가 인형이라도 되는 양 — 나는 늘 그렇게 느끼곤 했다 — 그 어여쁘게 어르는 말투로 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 다섯 시에 일어나지 마요, 못된 사람. 그건 너무 말도 안 돼요!\"\n\n\"내 사랑,\" 내가 말했다. \"나는 해야 할 일이 있는걸.\"\n\n\"그럼 하지 마요!\" 도라가 받아쳤다. \"왜 해야 하는데요?\"\n\n그 사랑스럽고 작고 놀란 얼굴을 마주하고서는, 가볍고 장난스러운 투가 아니고서는, 우리가 먹고살자면 일을 해야 한다는 말을 도저히 할 수가 없었다.\n\n\"어머! 정말 우스워요!\" 도라가 외쳤다.\n\n\"일을 안 하면 우리가 어떻게 살아, 도라?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게요? 아무렇게나요!\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n그 애는 자기가 그 문제를 완전히 해결했다고 여기는 모양이었고, 그 천진한 마음에서 곧장 우러나온 의기양양한 작은 입맞춤을 나에게 해 주었으니, 나는 큰 재산을 준다 해도 그 애가 자기 대답에 품은 그 흐뭇함을 깨뜨릴 수가 없었다.\n\n그래! 나는 그 애를 사랑했고, 계속해서 사랑했다. 무엇보다도 푹 빠져, 온전히, 남김없이. 하지만 그러면서도 나는 꽤 고되게 일했고, 이제 화로에 올려놓은 쇳덩이를 죄다 벌겋게 달군 채로 바삐 지켰다. 그러다 밤이면 고모님 맞은편에 앉아, 그날 내가 도라를 어떻게 겁주었던가, 그리고 기타 케이스를 들고 이 어려움의 숲을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 헤쳐 나갈 수 있을까를 곰곰이 생각하다가, 급기야 내 머리가 온통 희끗희끗 세어 버리는 것만 같다고 상상하곤 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 38. A DISSOLUTION OF PARTNERSHIP\n\n의회 토론을 받아쓰겠다는 내 결심은 좀처럼 식지 않았다. 그것은 내가 곧장 달구기 시작한 쇠붙이 가운데 하나였고, 줄곧 뜨겁게 데워 두고 두드려 댄 쇠붙이였다. 그 끈기만큼은 나 스스로도 솔직히 감탄할 만했다. 나는 속기라는 고귀한 기술이자 신비의 비법을 정리해 놓은, 권위를 인정받은 교본을 한 권 샀고(십 실링 육 펜스를 치렀다), 그러고는 몇 주 만에 나를 정신 줄을 놓을 지경까지 몰고 간 혼란의 바다에 풍덩 뛰어들었다. 점 하나가 이런 자리에 찍히면 이런 뜻이 되고, 또 저런 자리에 찍히면 전혀 딴판인 다른 뜻이 되는 그 무수한 변주들, 동그라미들이 부리는 기막힌 장난들, 파리 다리처럼 생긴 표시에서 비롯되는 도무지 알 수 없는 결과들, 곡선 하나가 엉뚱한 데 놓였을 때 빚어지는 어마어마한 효과 — 이런 것들이 내 깨어 있는 시간을 괴롭힌 것은 물론이요, 잠든 사이에도 눈앞에 되살아났다. 이 난관들을 더듬더듬, 앞이 안 보이는 채로 헤쳐 나가, 그 자체로 이집트 신전이나 다름없던 알파벳을 겨우 익히고 나자, 이번에는 임의 기호라는 새로운 공포의 행렬이 줄지어 나타났다. 내가 여태껏 겪어 본 가장 횡포한 기호들이었다. 이를테면 거미줄 첫머리처럼 생긴 것이 '기대'를 뜻한다고 우기고, 펜과 잉크로 그린 폭죽 같은 것이 '불리한'이라는 뜻이라고 떼를 쓰는 것이었다. 이 몹쓸 것들을 머릿속에 박아 넣고 나니, 이놈들이 다른 모든 것을 머릿속에서 죄다 몰아내 버렸다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 다시 처음부터 시작하면 이놈들을 잊어버렸고, 이놈들을 다시 주워 담는 사이에는 체계의 다른 조각들을 떨어뜨렸다. 한마디로 거의 애간장이 녹을 노릇이었다.\n\n도라가 없었다면 정말이지 애간장이 녹았을지도 모른다. 그녀야말로 폭풍에 떠밀리는 내 조각배의 버팀목이자 닻이었다. 교본에 그어진 획 하나하나가 난관의 숲에 솟은 옹이투성이 떡갈나무였고, 나는 그것들을 하나하나 어찌나 기운차게 베어 넘겼던지, 서너 달 만에 하원의 내로라하는 명연설가 하나를 상대로 실험을 해 볼 만한 상태에 이르렀다. 그 명연설가가 내가 받아쓰기를 시작하기도 전에 저만치 달아나 버리고, 내 얼간이 같은 연필만 발작이라도 일으킨 양 종이 위를 비틀비틀 헤매도록 내버려 둔 그 광경을 내가 어찌 잊으랴!\n\n이래선 안 될 일이었다. 그건 너무나 분명했다. 나는 너무 높이 날고 있었으니, 이대로는 결코 나아갈 수 없었다. 나는 트래들스에게 조언을 구했다. 그는 내 부족함에 맞춰, 알맞은 속도로, 이따금 멈춰 가며, 자기가 나에게 연설을 불러 주겠노라고 제안했다. 이 우정 어린 도움이 무척이나 고마워 나는 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그리하여 밤이면 밤마다, 거의 매일 밤, 오랫동안, 박사님 댁에서 집으로 돌아온 뒤면 우리는 버킹엄 가에서 일종의 사설 의회를 열었다.\n\n그런 의회는 세상 어디에 가도 다시 볼 수 없을 것이다! 고모님과 딕 씨가 (그때그때) 정부 측이나 야당 측을 맡았고, 트래들스는 엔필드의 명연설집이나 의회 연설 모음집의 도움을 받아 그들을 향해 놀라운 독설을 천둥처럼 퍼부었다. 책장 사이에 손가락을 끼워 자리를 표시하고 오른팔을 머리 위로 휘두르며, 트래들스는 피트 씨, 폭스 씨, 셰리든 씨, 버크 씨, 캐슬레이 경, 시드머스 자작, 또는 캐닝 씨가 되어, 스스로를 가장 격렬한 흥분으로 몰아넣고는 고모님과 딕 씨의 방탕과 부패를 향해 가장 신랄한 규탄을 쏟아 냈다. 그동안 나는 조금 떨어진 자리에 공책을 무릎에 얹고 앉아, 있는 힘 없는 힘 다 짜내어 그의 말을 죽기 살기로 좇았다. 트래들스의 일관성 없음과 무모함은 어떤 진짜 정치가도 따라올 수 없을 정도였다. 그는 일주일 안에 온갖 종류의 정책을 다 지지했고, 온갖 색깔의 깃발을 온갖 돛대에 다 못질해 매달았다. 고모님은 꿈쩍도 않는 재무장관을 빼다 박은 모습으로, 본문이 요구하는 듯싶을 때면 이따금 \"옳소!\" 또는 \"안 되오!\" 또는 \"오!\" 하고 한두 마디씩 끼어들었는데, 그것은 늘 딕 씨(영락없는 시골 신사였다)에게 같은 외침을 우렁차게 따라 하라는 신호였다. 하지만 딕 씨는 의정 활동을 하는 동안 그런 일들로 추궁을 당하고, 그토록 끔찍한 결과에 책임을 지게 되는 통에, 더러는 마음이 영 편치 않아졌다. 내가 보기에 그는, 자기가 정말로 무언가 영국 헌정을 무너뜨리고 나라를 망치는 짓을 저지른 게 아닌가 진심으로 두려워하기 시작한 것 같았다.\n\n우리는 시계가 자정을 가리키고 촛불이 다 타들어 갈 때까지 이런 토론을 거듭하고 또 거듭했다. 이토록 많은 연습이 가져다준 결과는, 머지않아 내가 트래들스를 꽤 잘 따라잡기 시작했다는 것이었으니, 만약 내가 받아쓴 글이 무슨 내용인지 손톱만큼이라도 알아볼 수 있었다면 나는 아주 의기양양했을 것이다. 그러나 받아쓴 것을 나중에 읽는 일로 말하자면, 차라리 어마어마하게 쌓아 둔 찻상자 더미에 적힌 한문 글귀나, 약방의 크고 빨갛고 푸른 병들에 죄다 박혀 있는 금빛 글자들을 베껴 적은 편이 나았을 것이다!\n\n별수 없었다. 되돌아가 처음부터 죄다 다시 시작하는 수밖에. 무척 힘들었지만, 나는 무거운 마음을 안고 되돌아갔고, 달팽이 걸음으로 똑같이 지긋지긋한 땅을 부지런히, 차근차근 다시 갈기 시작했다. 길에 놓인 점 하나하나를 사방으로 꼼꼼히 뜯어보려고 멈춰 서고, 이 종잡을 수 없는 기호들을 어디서 마주치든 한눈에 알아보려고 죽을힘을 다해 애썼다. 나는 사무소에 늘 시간을 어김없이 댔고, 박사님 댁에도 그러했다. 그리고 흔히 하는 말마따나, 정말이지 짐말처럼 일했다. 어느 날, 여느 때처럼 박사회에 갔을 때, 나는 스펜로 씨가 입구에 서서 몹시 심각한 얼굴로 혼잣말을 하고 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 평소 머리가 아프다고 푸념하는 버릇이 있었던 데다 — 워낙 목이 짧았고, 내 진심으로 믿건대 풀을 너무 빳빳하게 먹인 탓이었다 — 그래서 나는 처음엔 그가 머리 쪽이 좀 어떻게 된 게 아닌가 하는 생각에 덜컥 놀랐다. 그러나 그는 곧 내 불안을 거두어 주었다.\n\n그는 평소의 살가움으로 내 \"안녕하세요\"에 답하는 대신, 서먹하고 격식 차린 태도로 나를 바라보고는, 어느 커피집까지 함께 가 달라고 차갑게 청했다. 그 시절 그곳은 세인트 폴 묘지의 작은 아치 길 바로 안쪽에서 박사회로 통하는 문이 하나 나 있었다. 나는 몹시 불편한 심정으로, 마치 내 불안이 봉오리를 틔워 터져 나오기라도 하듯 온몸이 뜨겁게 욱신거리는 채로 그를 따랐다. 길이 좁아서 그를 조금 앞서 가게 했을 때, 나는 그가 유난히 불길하게 느껴지는 거만한 자세로 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 걷는 것을 보았다. 그러자 그가 내 사랑하는 도라에 관해 알아챈 것이 아닌가 하는 불안이 마음을 짓눌렀다.\n\n설령 커피집으로 가는 길에 이 짐작을 못 했더라도, 그를 따라 이 층 방으로 올라가 거기서 머드스톤 양을 발견했을 때 나는 무슨 일인지 모를 수가 없었을 것이다. 그녀는 찬장을 배경 삼아 떡 버티고 앉아 있었는데, 그 찬장 위에는 거꾸로 엎어 놓은 유리잔 몇 개가 레몬을 떠받치고 있었고, 칼과 포크를 꽂아 두는, 온통 모서리와 골진 무늬뿐인 그 별난 상자가 둘 놓여 있었다 — 인류에게는 다행스럽게도, 이제는 자취를 감춘 물건이다.\n\n머드스톤 양은 차디찬 손톱을 나에게 내밀고는, 무섭도록 뻣뻣하게 앉아 있었다. 스펜로 씨는 문을 닫고 나에게 의자를 가리킨 다음, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 섰다.\n\n\"머드스톤 양, 그 손가방에 든 것을 코퍼필드 씨에게 보여 주시겠습니까.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다.\n\n내 어린 시절의 바로 그 강철 잠금쇠 달린 손가방, 무는 입처럼 딱 닫히던 그 가방이 분명했다. 머드스톤 양은 그 딸깍 소리에 맞춰 입술을 꾹 다물며 가방을 열었고 — 그와 동시에 입도 조금 벌리며 — 도라에게 보낸 내 마지막 편지, 한결같은 애정의 표현이 넘쳐흐르는 그 편지를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"이게 당신 글씨가 맞지요, 코퍼필드 씨?\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다.\n\n온몸이 화끈거렸고, 내가 \"맞습니다, 선생님!\" 하고 말했을 때 내 귀에 들린 목소리는 도무지 내 것 같지 않았다.\n\n\"내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면,\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. 그때 머드스톤 양이 손가방에서 더없이 정겨운 파란 리본으로 묶은 편지 한 묶음을 꺼냈다. \"이것들도 당신 펜에서 나온 것이겠지요, 코퍼필드 씨?\"\n\n나는 더없이 쓸쓸한 심정으로 그것들을 그녀에게서 받아 들었다. 그러고는 첫머리에 적힌 \"내 영원히 가장 사랑하는 나만의 도라\", \"내 가장 사랑하는 천사\", \"내 영원히 축복받은 사람\" 따위의 문구를 흘끗 보고는, 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮습니다!\" 내가 기계적으로 그것들을 도로 건네자 스펜로 씨가 차갑게 말했다. \"당신 것을 빼앗지는 않겠소. 머드스톤 양, 계속해 주시지요!\"\n\n그 온순한 인물은 잠시 생각에 잠긴 듯 양탄자를 살펴본 다음, 메마른 거드름을 잔뜩 풍기며 다음과 같이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"고백하건대, 데이비드 코퍼필드와 관련하여 스펜로 양을 얼마간 의심해 왔습니다. 나는 스펜로 양과 데이비드 코퍼필드가 처음 만났을 때 두 사람을 눈여겨보았는데, 그때 받은 인상이 영 마뜩잖았어요. 사람 마음이란 어찌나 타락한 것인지—\"\n\n\"부인,\" 스펜로 씨가 가로막았다. \"사실에만 국한해 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\"\n\n머드스톤 양은 눈을 내리깔고, 이 무례한 끼어듦에 항의라도 하듯 고개를 가로젓더니, 눈살을 찌푸린 위엄을 풍기며 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"사실에만 국한해야 한다면, 되도록 메마르게 진술하지요. 어쩌면 그게 받아들일 만한 진행 방식으로 여겨질 테니까요. 이미 말씀드렸다시피, 선생님, 나는 데이비드 코퍼필드와 관련하여 스펜로 양을 얼마간 의심해 왔습니다. 그 의심을 결정적으로 뒷받침할 증거를 거듭 찾아내려 애썼으나 헛수고였지요. 그래서 스펜로 양의 아버님께는 그 일을 말씀드리지 않고 참아 왔습니다.\" 그녀는 그를 매섭게 쏘아보았다. \"그런 경우 양심껏 의무를 다한 것을 인정해 주려는 마음이 대개 얼마나 박한지 잘 알고 있었으니까요.\"\n\n스펜로 씨는 머드스톤 양의 신사 같은 준엄한 태도에 잔뜩 기가 죽은 듯, 화해를 청하듯 손을 살짝 내저으며 그녀의 매서움을 누그러뜨려 보려 했다.\n\n\"내 오라비의 결혼으로 자리를 비웠다가 노우드로 돌아온 뒤,\" 머드스톤 양은 경멸 어린 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"그리고 스펜로 양이 친구인 밀스 양 댁을 방문하고 돌아온 뒤, 나는 스펜로 양의 태도가 전보다 더 의심할 만한 빌미를 준다고 여겼습니다. 그래서 스펜로 양을 면밀히 지켜보았지요.\"\n\n사랑스럽고 다정한 어린 도라, 이 용의 눈을 그토록 까맣게 모르고 있었다니!\n\n\"그래도,\" 머드스톤 양이 다시 말을 이었다. \"어젯밤까지는 아무 증거도 잡지 못했습니다. 스펜로 양이 친구인 밀스 양에게서 편지를 지나치게 많이 받는다 싶긴 했으나, 밀스 양은 아버님의 전적인 동의 아래 친구가 된 사이였으니,\" 또 한 번 스펜로 씨에게 따끔한 일격이었다. \"내가 끼어들 일은 아니었습니다. 인간 마음의 타고난 타락을 언급하는 것이 허락되지 않는다면, 적어도 잘못 둔 신뢰에 관해서만은 — 언급해도 좋겠지요, 아니, 언급해야겠지요.\"\n\n스펜로 씨는 사과하듯 동의를 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"어제저녁 차를 마신 뒤,\" 머드스톤 양이 말을 이었다. \"나는 그 작은 개가 응접실을 이리저리 펄쩍거리고, 뒹굴고, 으르렁대며 무언가를 물어뜯는 것을 보았습니다. 그래서 스펜로 양에게 말했지요. '도라, 저 개가 입에 문 게 뭐냐? 종이로구나.' 스펜로 양은 곧장 자기 옷자락에 손을 대더니, 별안간 외마디 소리를 지르며 개에게 달려갔어요. 내가 가로막으며 말했습니다. '도라, 얘야, 나한테 맡기렴.'\"\n\n오, 지프, 가엾은 스패니얼아, 그렇다면 이 모든 비참함이 다 네 짓이었구나!\n\n\"스펜로 양은,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"입맞춤이며 반짇고리며 자잘한 패물 따위로 나를 구슬리려 들었지만 — 그건 물론 그냥 넘어가지요. 내가 다가가자 그 작은 개는 소파 밑으로 달아났고, 부지깽이를 동원해 가까스로 끌어냈습니다. 끌어냈을 때도 녀석은 여전히 편지를 입에 물고 있었고, 내가 물릴 위험을 무릅쓰고 그것을 빼앗으려 하자, 어찌나 끈질기게 이 사이에 꽉 물고 있던지 그 편지로 제 몸이 공중에 대롱대롱 매달리는 것마저 견디더군요. 마침내 나는 그것을 손에 넣었습니다. 그것을 다 읽고 나서 나는 스펜로 양에게 그런 편지를 많이 갖고 있지 않으냐고 다그쳤고, 결국 지금 데이비드 코퍼필드의 손에 들린 그 묶음을 그녀에게서 받아 냈습니다.\"\n\n여기서 그녀는 말을 그쳤다. 그러고는 손가방을 다시 딱 닫고 입을 다문 채, 부러질지언정 결코 휘지는 않으리라는 표정으로 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"머드스톤 양의 말을 들었지요.\" 스펜로 씨가 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"코퍼필드 씨, 이에 대해 할 말이 있는지 묻고 싶군요.\"\n\n내 마음속 아름다운 작은 보물이 밤새도록 흐느끼며 울었을 모습 — 그 무렵 외롭고, 겁에 질리고, 비참했을 모습 — 그 돌처럼 매정한 여자에게 용서해 달라고 그토록 애처롭게 빌고 또 빌었을 모습 — 그 입맞춤이며 반짇고리며 패물을 부질없이 내밀었을 모습 — 그토록 처절한 고통에 빠져 있었을, 그것도 죄다 나 때문에 그러했을 모습이 눈앞에 떠올라, 내가 가까스로 그러모았던 알량한 위엄을 송두리째 무너뜨렸다. 애써 감추려 했지만, 한 일 분쯤 내가 부들부들 떨고 있었던 것 같아 부끄럽다.\n\n\"드릴 말씀이 없습니다, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"모든 잘못이 제게 있다는 것 말고는요. 도라가—\"\n\n\"스펜로 양이라고 해 주시지요.\" 그녀의 아버지가 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"—제게 꼬임을 당하고 설득당해,\" 나는 그 차가운 호칭을 꾹 삼키며 말을 이었다. \"이 비밀에 동의한 것이니, 저는 그것을 뼈저리게 후회하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 큰 잘못을 저질렀소, 선생님.\" 스펜로 씨가 벽난로 앞 깔개 위를 왔다 갔다 하며 말했다. 그는 빳빳한 넥타이와 등 때문에 고개 대신 온몸으로 자기 말을 강조했다. \"당신은 떳떳치 못하고 어울리지 않는 짓을 했소, 코퍼필드 씨. 내가 한 신사를 우리 집에 들일 때는, 그가 열아홉이든 스물아홉이든 아흔이든 간에, 신뢰하는 마음으로 들이는 거요. 그가 내 신뢰를 저버린다면, 그건 명예롭지 못한 짓을 저지른 것이오, 코퍼필드 씨.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 느낍니다, 선생님, 맹세코요.\" 내가 대답했다. \"하지만 이전에는 한 번도 그렇게 생각해 본 적이 없습니다. 진심으로, 솔직히, 스펜로 씨, 이전에는 정말 그렇게 생각해 본 적이 없어요. 저는 스펜로 양을 그토록 사랑하기에—\"\n\n\"흥! 헛소리!\" 스펜로 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"내 면전에서 내 딸을 사랑한다는 따위 말은 제발 하지 마시오, 코퍼필드 씨!\"\n\n\"그렇지 않다면 제가 어찌 제 행동을 변명할 수 있겠습니까, 선생님?\" 나는 한껏 겸손하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 당신이 어찌 제 행동을 변명할 수 있겠소, 선생님?\" 스펜로 씨가 깔개 위에서 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"당신 나이와 내 딸의 나이를 헤아려 보았소, 코퍼필드 씨? 내 딸과 나 사이에 있어야 마땅한 신뢰를 허무는 일이 무얼 뜻하는지 헤아려 보았소? 내 딸의 사회적 지위, 내가 그 애의 앞날을 위해 품을지 모를 계획, 그 애와 관련해 내가 가질지 모를 유언상의 뜻을 헤아려 보았소? 무엇 하나라도 헤아려 본 것이 있소, 코퍼필드 씨?\"\n\n\"별로 헤아려 보지 못한 것 같습니다, 선생님.\" 나는 느끼는 그대로 공손하고 침통하게 그에게 말했다. \"하지만 제발 믿어 주십시오. 제 세속적인 처지만큼은 헤아려 보았습니다. 그 처지를 선생님께 설명드렸을 때, 저희는 이미 약혼한 사이였고—\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이오.\" 스펜로 씨가 한 손으로 다른 손을 힘껏 내리치며 말했는데, 그 모습이 내가 여태 본 어느 때보다 펀치 인형을 닮아 있어서 — 절망 속에서도 나는 그것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다 — \"나에게 약혼이니 뭐니 하는 말은 하지 마시오, 코퍼필드 씨!\"\n\n좀처럼 꿈쩍 않던 머드스톤 양이 한 음절로 짤막하게 비웃었다.\n\n\"바뀐 제 처지를 선생님께 설명드렸을 때, 선생님,\" 나는 그가 그토록 못마땅해하는 표현 대신 새 표현으로 바꾸어 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"제가 불행히도 스펜로 양을 끌어들이고 만 이 비밀이 이미 시작된 뒤였습니다. 그 바뀐 처지에 놓인 뒤로, 저는 그것을 나아지게 하려고 신경을 곤두세우고 온 힘을 쏟아부었습니다. 시간이 지나면 반드시 나아지게 만들 자신이 있습니다. 제게 시간을 주시지 않겠습니까 — 얼마가 됐든 좋습니다. 저희는 둘 다 이토록 젊으니까요, 선생님—\"\n\n\"옳은 말이오.\" 스펜로 씨가 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이고 잔뜩 눈살을 찌푸리며 가로막았다. \"둘 다 아주 젊지. 죄다 헛소리요. 이 헛소리를 끝냅시다. 그 편지들을 가져가서 불속에 던지시오. 스펜로 양의 편지는 내가 불에 던지게 나에게 주고. 그리고 앞으로 우리 사이의 왕래는, 당신도 알다시피 이곳 박사회로만 제한될 수밖에 없겠지만, 지난 일은 더 이상 들먹이지 않기로 합시다. 자, 코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 분별이 모자란 사람이 아니오. 이것이 분별 있는 길이오.\"\n\n아니다. 나는 거기에 동의할 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 무척 죄송했지만, 분별보다 더 높은 고려가 있었다. 사랑은 이 세상의 그 어떤 고려보다도 위에 있었고, 나는 도라를 우상처럼 떠받들며 사랑했고, 도라도 나를 사랑했다. 딱 잘라 그렇게 말하지는 않았다. 되도록 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸다. 하지만 그런 뜻을 내비쳤고, 그 점에 대해서는 단호했다. 내가 아주 우스꽝스러워 보이지는 않았던 것 같지만, 내가 단호했던 것만은 분명하다.\n\n\"좋소, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"내 딸에게 내 영향력을 행사해 봐야겠군.\"\n\n머드스톤 양은 한숨도 신음도 아니면서 그 둘을 다 닮은, 길게 끌어 내쉬는 의미심장한 숨소리로, 진작에 그랬어야 했다는 자기 의견을 드러냈다.\n\n\"내 딸에게,\" 이 지원에 힘을 얻은 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"내 영향력을 행사해 봐야겠소. 그 편지들을 가져가지 않겠다는 거요, 코퍼필드 씨?\" 내가 편지들을 탁자 위에 놓아 두었기 때문이다.\n\n그렇다. 나는 그가 잘못이라 여기지 않기를 바라지만, 도무지 머드스톤 양에게서 그것을 받아 갈 수는 없노라고 말했다.\n\n\"나한테서도 못 받겠소?\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다.\n\n못 받겠습니다, 하고 나는 더없이 깊은 공손함으로 대답했다. 선생님께서 주셔도 못 받겠습니다.\n\n\"좋소!\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다.\n\n침묵이 뒤따랐고, 나는 가야 할지 머물러야 할지 마음을 정하지 못했다. 마침내 나는, 어쩌면 물러가는 것이 그의 심정을 가장 잘 헤아리는 길일지 모르겠다고 말할 작정으로 문 쪽을 향해 조용히 발걸음을 옮기고 있었다. 그때 그가 두 손을 — 가까스로 욱여넣을 수 있을 만큼 빠듯한 — 코트 주머니에 찌른 채, 전체로 보아 단연 경건하다고 할 만한 분위기를 풍기며 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 당신도 아마 알고 있겠지만, 나에게 세속적 재산이 아주 없는 것은 아니고, 내 딸은 나에게 가장 가깝고 가장 소중한 혈육이오?\"\n\n나는 부랴부랴, 절박한 사랑에 휩쓸려 빠지고 만 잘못 때문에 그가 저를 돈이나 노리는 사람으로까지 여기지 않으시길 바란다는 취지로 대답했다.\n\n\"그런 뜻으로 그 일을 들먹이는 건 아니오.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"당신이 돈을 노리는 사람이라면 차라리 당신 자신을 위해서도, 우리 모두를 위해서도 더 나을 거요, 코퍼필드 씨 — 내 말은, 당신이 좀 더 신중하고 이 풋내기 같은 헛소리에 덜 휘둘린다면 말이오. 아니, 나는 그저 전혀 다른 관점에서, 내가 내 딸에게 물려줄 재산이 얼마간 있다는 것을 당신도 아마 알고 있으리란 얘기를 하는 거요?\"\n\n나는 분명 그러리라 짐작하고 있었다.\n\n\"그런데 당신도 이곳 박사회에서 날마다 보아 알다시피,\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"사람들이 유언 처리에 관해 갖가지로 도무지 알 수 없고 또 소홀한 행태를 보이지요 — 인간의 일관성 없음에 관한 가장 기이한 본색이 드러나는 주제를 다 통틀어도 아마 이것만 한 것이 없을 거요 — 그런 마당에, 설마 내 유언만큼은 다 마련되어 있지 않으리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없겠지요?\"\n\n나는 동의한다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"나는 결코,\" 스펜로 씨가 경건한 감정을 부쩍 더하며, 발끝과 발뒤꿈치로 번갈아 몸을 가누고 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"내 딸을 위한 합당한 대비가 지금 같은 풋내기 어리석음 따위에 휘둘리도록 내버려 두지 않을 거요. 그건 그저 어리석음일 뿐이오. 한낱 헛소리지. 머지않아 그것은 깃털 하나보다 가벼워질 거요. 하지만 만약 이 어처구니없는 일이 완전히 깨끗이 청산되지 않는다면, 나는 어쩌면 — 어쩌면 — 어느 불안한 순간에, 어리석은 결혼 같은 어떤 잘못된 발걸음이 빚을 결과로부터 그 애를 지키고, 그 애 둘레에 보호막을 둘러야겠다는 마음에 이끌릴지도 모르오. 자, 코퍼필드 씨, 나는 당신이, 인생이라는 책의 그 덮인 페이지를 단 십오 분이라도 다시 펼쳐 보고, 오래전에 정리된 중대한 일들을 단 십오 분이라도 다시 흔들어 놓을 수밖에 없게끔 만들지 않기를 바라오.\"\n\n그에게는 어떤 고요함, 어떤 평온함, 어떤 잔잔한 저녁놀 같은 기운이 감돌고 있어서, 나는 자못 마음이 움직였다. 그는 어찌나 평화롭고 체념한 듯하던지 — 자기 일들을 분명 그토록 완벽한 궤도에 올려놓고 그토록 빈틈없이 매듭지어 둔 듯하여 — 가만히 들여다보고 있노라면 절로 가슴이 뭉클해지는 사람이었다. 이 모든 것에 대한 자기 자신의 깊은 감정에서 그의 눈에 정말로 눈물이 차오르는 것을 본 것만 같다.\n\n하지만 내가 무얼 할 수 있었겠는가? 나는 도라와 내 마음을 부정할 수 없었다. 그가 자기 말을 일주일 동안 곰곰이 생각해 보는 게 좋겠다고 했을 때, 일주일은 못 받겠다고 어찌 말할 수 있었으랴. 그러면서도, 몇 주가 됐든 그것이 나 같은 사랑에 영향을 미칠 리 없다는 것을 어찌 모를 수 있었겠는가?\n\n\"그동안 트롯우드 양이든, 세상 물정 좀 아는 누구든 상의해 보시오.\" 스펜로 씨가 두 손으로 넥타이를 매만지며 말했다. \"일주일을 갖도록 하시오, 코퍼필드 씨.\"\n\n나는 따랐다. 그러고는 풀이 죽고 절망에 빠진 변함없는 마음을 할 수 있는 한 또렷이 드러낸 얼굴로 그 방을 나왔다. 머드스톤 양의 묵직한 눈썹이 문까지 나를 좇았다 — 그녀의 눈이 아니라 눈썹이라고 말하는 까닭은, 그녀의 얼굴에서는 눈썹이 훨씬 더 중요했기 때문이다 — 그리고 그녀는 블런더스톤의 우리 집 거실에서 아침 그 무렵이면 늘 짓던 그 표정 그대로여서, 나는 마치 또다시 공부를 망쳐 쩔쩔매고 있고, 내 마음을 짓누르는 그 무거운 덩어리가 바로 그 끔찍한 옛 철자 교본 — 어린 내 눈에는 안경알처럼 생긴 타원형 목판화가 박혀 있던 그 책 — 인 듯한 착각마저 들 지경이었다.\n\n사무소에 도착해, 두 손으로 늙은 티피 영감과 다른 사람들을 가려 막고, 내 전용 구석 자리 책상에 앉아, 그토록 느닷없이 일어난 이 지진을 곱씹으며 비통한 심정으로 지프를 저주하다가, 나는 도라를 둘러싸고 어찌나 큰 괴로움에 빠져들었던지, 모자를 집어 들고 미친 사람처럼 노우드로 달려가지 않은 것이 신기할 지경이었다. 그들이 그녀를 겁주고 울리는데, 나는 그 곁에서 위로해 줄 수도 없다는 생각이 어찌나 사무치게 괴로웠던지, 나는 그 충동에 떠밀려 스펜로 씨에게 미친 듯한 편지를 썼다. 부디 내 끔찍한 운명의 결과를 그녀에게 떠넘기지 말아 달라고 애원하는 내용이었다. 그녀의 여린 성품을 부디 아껴 달라고 — 가녀린 꽃을 짓밟지 말아 달라고 — 빌었고, 내 기억으로는, 그가 그녀의 아버지가 아니라 무슨 식인귀나 완틀리의 용이라도 되는 양 그를 통틀어 다루었다. 이 편지를 봉해서 그가 돌아오기 전에 그의 책상 위에 놓아 두었고, 그가 들어왔을 때 나는 반쯤 열린 그의 방문 틈으로 그가 그것을 집어 들고 읽는 것을 보았다.\n* 완틀리의 용: 영국 옛 발라드에 나오는 용으로, 무엇이든 닥치는 대로 집어삼키는 괴물.\n\n그는 오전 내내 그것에 대해 아무 말도 없었다. 그러나 오후에 나가기 전에 나를 불러서는, 자기 딸의 행복에 대해 조금도 불안해할 것 없다고 일러 주었다. 그는 그녀에게 그 모든 게 다 헛소리라고 단단히 못 박아 두었고, 더 이상 그녀에게 할 말이 없노라고 했다. 자기는 너그러운 아버지라고 믿으며(실제로도 그러했다), 그러니 나는 그녀 때문에 마음 졸일 필요가 전혀 없다고 했다.\n\n\"당신이 어리석게 굴거나 고집을 부린다면, 코퍼필드 씨,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 한동안 딸을 다시 외국으로 보내야 할 수밖에 없게 될지도 모르오. 하지만 나는 당신을 더 좋게 보고 있소. 며칠 안에 당신이 그보다는 현명해지기를 바라오. 머드스톤 양으로 말하자면,\" 내가 편지에서 그녀를 언급했기 때문이다. \"나는 그 부인의 경계심을 존중하고 또 고맙게 여기지만, 그 부인에게는 이 일을 입에 올리지 말라고 엄히 일러 두었소. 내가 바라는 건 오직, 코퍼필드 씨, 이 일이 잊히는 것뿐이오. 당신이 해야 할 일은 오직, 코퍼필드 씨, 이 일을 잊는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n오직! 밀스 양에게 쓴 쪽지에서 나는 이 말을 비통하게 인용했다. 내가 해야 할 일은 오직, 하고 나는 음울한 빈정거림을 담아 말했다, 도라를 잊는 것뿐이라고. 그게 다라고, 한데 그게 무슨 말이냐고! 나는 밀스 양에게 그날 저녁 나를 만나 달라고 간청했다. 만약 밀스 씨의 허락과 동의 아래 그것이 안 된다면, 짤주기가 놓인 뒤편 부엌에서라도 몰래 만나 달라고 애원했다. 나는 그녀에게, 내 이성이 그 옥좌 위에서 휘청거리고 있으며, 오직 그녀, 밀스 양만이 그것이 폐위되는 것을 막을 수 있노라고 알렸다. 나는 미칠 듯한 그녀의 것, 이라고 서명했다. 그리고 이 글을 짐꾼 편에 부치기 전에 다시 읽어 보면서, 어딘가 미코버 씨의 문체를 빼닮았다는 느낌을 떨칠 수가 없었다.\n\n* 짤주기(맹글): 빨래의 물기를 짜내거나 다림질하듯 펴 주는 옛날의 큰 압착 기구.\n그래도 나는 그것을 보냈다. 밤이 되자 나는 밀스 양이 사는 거리로 가서, 밀스 양의 하녀가 슬그머니 나를 데리러 나와 지하 통로를 거쳐 뒤편 부엌으로 안내할 때까지 이리저리 서성였다. 그 뒤로 나는, 밀스 양이 낭만적이고 신비로운 것을 좋아하는 취향만 아니었다면, 내가 정문으로 들어가 응접실로 안내받는 것을 세상 그 무엇도 막지 못했으리라고 믿을 만한 까닭을 알게 되었다.\n\n뒤편 부엌에서 나는 내 처지에 어울리게 마구 헛소리를 늘어놓았다. 짐작건대 나는 거기에 나 자신을 바보로 만들러 간 셈이었고, 분명히 그러고도 남았다. 밀스 양은 도라에게서 급히 쓴 쪽지를 받았는데, 모든 게 들통났다는 내용과 함께 \"오, 줄리아, 제발 와 줘, 어서, 어서!\" 하는 말이 적혀 있었다. 그러나 밀스 양은 자기가 가도 윗분들이 반겨 줄지 미덥지 못해 아직 가지 않은 터였다. 그래서 우리는 다 함께 사하라 사막에 갇혀 어둠 속을 헤매는 신세였다.\n\n밀스 양은 놀라운 말솜씨를 지녔고, 그것을 쏟아 내기를 즐겼다. 그녀가 내 눈물에 자기 눈물을 보태기는 했지만, 나는 그녀가 우리의 고통 속에서 어딘가 무시무시한 호사를 누리고 있다는 느낌을 떨칠 수 없었다. 말하자면 그녀는 그 고통을 어루만지고, 한껏 부풀려 즐겼다. 그녀는 말하기를, 도라와 나 사이에 깊은 심연이 입을 벌렸고, 사랑만이 그 위에 무지개 다리를 놓을 수 있다고 했다. 사랑은 이 모진 세상에서 마땅히 고통받기 마련이니, 늘 그래 왔고 앞으로도 늘 그러하리라고. 하지만 상관없다고 밀스 양은 말했다. 거미줄에 갇힌 가슴은 끝내 터지고 말 것이며, 그때 사랑은 원수를 갚는다고.\n\n이건 보잘것없는 위로였지만, 밀스 양은 헛된 희망을 부추기려 들지 않았다. 그녀는 나를 전보다 훨씬 더 비참하게 만들었고, 나는 (그리고 더없이 깊은 고마움을 담아 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다) 그녀가 정말로 벗이라고 느꼈다. 우리는 그녀가 이튿날 아침 제일 먼저 도라에게 가서, 눈빛으로든 말로든 내 헌신과 비참함을 어떻게든 그녀에게 전해 줄 방도를 찾기로 다짐했다. 우리는 슬픔에 짓눌린 채 헤어졌다. 그리고 내 생각엔, 밀스 양은 더없이 흡족하게 즐긴 듯했다.\n\n집에 돌아와 나는 고모님께 모든 것을 털어놓았다. 고모님이 아무리 타일러도, 나는 절망에 빠진 채 잠자리에 들었다. 절망에 빠진 채 일어나, 절망에 빠진 채 집을 나섰다. 토요일 아침이었고, 나는 곧장 박사회로 갔다.\n\n우리 사무소 문이 보이는 데까지 왔을 때, 나는 입찰 짐꾼들이 밖에 서서 서로 이야기를 나누고, 대여섯 명의 행인이 닫혀 있는 창문을 멀거니 올려다보고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 나는 걸음을 재촉해, 그들 사이를 헤치고 지나가며 그 표정을 의아해하다가, 허둥지둥 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n서기들은 거기 있었으나 아무도 일을 하고 있지 않았다. 늙은 티피 영감은, 내 생각엔 평생 처음으로, 남의 의자에 걸터앉아 있었고 모자도 걸어 두지 않은 채였다.\n\n\"이건 끔찍한 재앙입니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 내가 들어서자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가요?\" 내가 소리쳤다. \"무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n\"모르십니까?\" 티피가 외쳤고, 나머지 사람들도 죄다 내 둘레로 모여들었다.\n\n\"몰라요!\" 내가 이 얼굴 저 얼굴을 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"스펜로 씨가요.\" 티피가 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 어떻게 됐는데요!\"\n\n\"돌아가셨습니다!\" 서기 하나가 나를 붙드는 순간, 나는 흔들리는 것이 내가 아니라 사무소라고 생각했다. 그들은 나를 의자에 앉히고, 목수건을 풀어 주고, 물을 좀 가져다주었다. 이 모든 일에 시간이 얼마나 걸렸는지 나는 전혀 모르겠다.\n\n\"돌아가셨다고요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\"어제 시내에서 식사를 하시고, 사륜마차를 손수 몰아 내려가셨답니다.\" 티피가 말했다. \"아시다시피 가끔 그러시듯이, 마부는 역마차 편에 먼저 집으로 돌려보내시고요—\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"마차가 그분 없이 집에 돌아온 겁니다. 말들이 마구간 문 앞에 멈춰 섰지요. 마부가 등불을 들고 나가 보니, 마차 안엔 아무도 없었던 거예요.\"\n\n\"말들이 달아났던 겁니까?\"\n\n\"열이 올라 있지 않았어요.\" 티피가 안경을 쓰며 말했다. \"내가 듣기로는, 여느 때 속도로 내려갈 때보다 조금도 더 열이 오른 게 아니었답니다. 고삐는 끊어져 있었지만 땅에 질질 끌린 흔적이 있었고요. 집안이 곧장 발칵 뒤집혀, 셋이 길을 따라 나섰습니다. 그분을 일 마일쯤 떨어진 데서 찾았지요.\"\n\n\"일 마일도 넘었습니다, 티피 씨.\" 한 젊은 서기가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그랬던가? 자네 말이 맞을 거야.\" 티피가 말했다. \"일 마일도 넘는 곳에서 — 교회에서 멀지 않은 데서 — 한쪽은 길가에, 한쪽은 보도에 걸친 채, 엎드린 모습으로요. 발작이 일어나 마차에서 떨어진 건지, 아니면 발작이 닥치기 전에 몸이 안 좋아서 내린 건지 — 심지어 그때 이미 완전히 돌아가셨던 건지조차도 — 의식이 전혀 없었던 것은 분명하지만 — 아무도 모르는 것 같습니다. 설령 숨은 붙어 있었다 해도, 한마디도 못 하신 것만은 확실하고요. 되도록 빨리 의료진을 불렀지만, 다 소용없었답니다.\"\n\n이 소식이 나를 어떤 심정으로 몰아넣었는지 나는 다 말로 옮길 수가 없다. 그토록 갑작스레, 그것도 어느 면에서든 나와 갈등을 빚었던 사람에게 그런 일이 닥쳤다는 충격 — 그가 바로 얼마 전까지 자리하던 방의 소름 끼치는 텅 빈 자리, 그의 의자와 책상이 그를 기다리는 듯하고, 어제 적어 둔 그의 글씨가 유령처럼 남아 있던 그 방 — 그를 그 자리에서 도무지 떼어 낼 수 없는 듯한 느낌, 그래서 문이 열리면 그가 들어올 것만 같은 느낌 — 사무소에 깃든 나른한 정적과 멈춤, 그리고 우리 사람들이 그 일을 입에 올리며 보이던 채울 줄 모르는 입맛, 온종일 사람들이 드나들며 그 화제를 게걸스레 먹어 치우던 광경 — 이런 것들은 누구나 쉽게 알아들을 것이다. 내가 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없는 것은, 내 마음 가장 깊은 구석에서 죽음마저 은근히 질투하고 있었다는 것이다. 그 힘이 도라의 생각 속 내 자리에서 나를 밀어낼 것만 같은 그 느낌. 뭐라 말할 길 없는 옹졸한 방식으로 그녀의 슬픔을 시샘하던 그 마음. 그녀가 남들 앞에서 운다거나, 남들에게 위로받는다는 생각이 나를 안절부절못하게 만들던 그 심정. 다른 모든 때를 통틀어 가장 어울리지 않을 그 시점에, 나 말고는 누구도 그녀에게서 떼어 내고 내가 그녀의 전부가 되고 싶다는, 움켜쥐려 들고 탐욕스럽기까지 한 그 바람.\n\n이런 심정의 혼란 속에서 — 부디 나 혼자만의 것이 아니라 남들도 아는 것이기를 바라지만 — 나는 그날 밤 노우드로 내려갔다. 문간에서 물어보다가 하인 하나에게서 밀스 양이 거기 와 있다는 것을 알고는, 고모님께 부탁해 그녀에게 보내는 편지의 겉봉을 쓰게 하고 안의 글은 내가 썼다. 나는 스펜로 씨의 때 이른 죽음을 더없이 진심으로 애도했고, 그 글을 쓰면서 눈물을 흘렸다. 나는 그녀에게, 만약 도라가 들을 만한 상태라면, 그가 나에게 더없이 다정하고 사려 깊게 말했으며, 그녀의 이름과 더불어 다정함 말고는 아무것도, 단 한마디 책망도 곁들이지 않았노라고 전해 달라고 간청했다. 내가 이렇게 한 것이, 내 이름을 그녀 앞에 들이밀려는 이기심에서였다는 걸 안다. 그러나 나는 그것이 그를 기리는 정당한 일이라고 믿으려 애썼다. 어쩌면 정말 그렇게 믿었는지도 모른다.\n\n이튿날 고모님은 답장으로 몇 줄을 받았다. 겉봉에는 고모님 앞으로, 안에는 나에게 쓴 글이었다. 도라는 슬픔에 짓눌려 있었다. 그리고 그 벗이 나에게 사랑을 전할까 하고 물었을 때, 그녀는 늘 그러듯 그저 \"오, 사랑하는 아빠! 오, 가엾은 아빠!\" 하고 울기만 했다고 한다. 그러나 그녀는 안 된다고 말하지는 않았으니, 나는 그 점을 최대한 부풀려 새겼다.\n\n그 일이 있은 뒤로 줄곧 노우드에 머물던 조킨스 씨가, 며칠 지나 사무소로 왔다. 그와 티피는 한동안 둘이서 방에 틀어박혀 있었고, 이윽고 티피가 문밖으로 고개를 내밀어 나를 손짓으로 불러들였다.\n\n\"오!\" 조킨스 씨가 말했다. \"코퍼필드 씨, 티피 씨와 나는 고인의 책상과 서랍, 그 밖에 그런 보관함들을 살펴보려 합니다. 그분의 개인 서류를 봉인하고 유언장을 찾으려는 거지요. 다른 데서는 그런 게 있다는 흔적이 전혀 없으니까요. 괜찮으시다면 당신이 우리를 거들어 주는 것도 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n나는 우리 도라가 어떤 처지에 놓일지 — 누구의 후견을 받게 될지 따위 — 무어라도 알아내려고 애가 타던 참이라, 이것은 그 실마리가 될 만했다. 우리는 곧장 수색을 시작했다. 조킨스 씨가 서랍과 책상을 열고, 우리 모두가 서류를 꺼냈다. 사무소 서류는 한쪽에, 개인 서류는(수가 많지 않았다) 다른 쪽에 놓았다. 우리는 무척 엄숙했다. 그리고 외따로 떨어진 인장이나 연필집, 반지, 또는 그가 떠오르는 그런 자잘한 물건이 나오면 우리는 목소리를 잔뜩 낮추었다.\n\n우리는 몇 묶음을 봉인했고, 여전히 먼지 속에서 조용히 일을 이어 가던 차에, 조킨스 씨가 우리에게 말했다. 죽은 동업자가 자기에게 쓰던 바로 그 말을, 토씨 하나 안 틀리고 그 죽은 동업자에게 그대로 갖다 붙이면서.\n\n\"스펜로 씨는 정해진 길에서 벗어나게 하기가 무척 어려운 분이었지요. 그분이 어떤 양반이었는지 잘 아시지 않습니까! 내 생각엔 유언장을 안 만드셨지 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 만드신 게 분명한데요!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n두 사람이 다 멈춰 서서 나를 바라보았다. \"제가 그분을 마지막으로 뵌 바로 그날,\" 내가 말했다. \"그분이 직접 저에게, 유언장을 만들어 두었고 자기 일들은 진작에 정리되어 있다고 말씀하셨습니다.\"\n\n조킨스 씨와 늙은 티피는 약속이라도 한 듯 한꺼번에 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"그건 영 불길한 얘긴데요.\" 티피가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 불길하지요.\" 조킨스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"설마 두 분은 의심하시는—\" 내가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이보세요, 코퍼필드 씨!\" 티피가 내 팔에 손을 얹고, 고개를 가로저으며 두 눈을 다 감은 채 말했다. \"당신도 나만큼 오래 박사회에 있어 봤다면, 사람이 이만큼 일관성 없고 이만큼 믿을 수 없는 주제도 없다는 걸 알게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 세상에, 그분이 바로 그 말씀을 하셨다니까요!\" 나는 고집스레 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그거라면 거의 결론을 내린 거나 다름없군요.\" 티피가 한마디 했다. \"내 생각엔 — 유언장은 없습니다.\"\n\n나에게는 놀라운 일로 여겨졌으나, 결국 유언장은 없었던 것으로 드러났다. 그가 남긴 서류로 보건대, 그는 그런 것을 만들 생각조차 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다. 유언상의 뜻이라곤 그 어떤 종류의 암시도, 초안도, 메모도 없었으니까. 그에 못지않게 나를 놀라게 한 것은, 그의 일들이 더없이 엉망인 상태였다는 점이다. 그가 빚진 것이 얼마인지, 갚은 것이 얼마인지, 죽을 때 무엇을 지니고 있었는지를 가려내기가 무척 어려웠다고 한다. 십중팔구 그 자신도 여러 해 동안 이런 문제에 대해 분명한 생각을 가져 본 적이 없었으리라고들 여겼다. 차츰차츰 드러난 바로는, 당시 박사회에서 겉치레와 점잖음을 두고 모든 면에서 한창 치열하던 경쟁 속에서, 그는 그리 많지도 않은 자기 직업 수입을 넘게 써 버렸고, 설령 한때 넉넉했다 하더라도(이는 몹시 의심스러웠지만) 개인 재산을 정말이지 바닥까지 갉아먹었던 것이다. 노우드에서는 가구와 임차권을 처분하는 경매가 열렸다. 그리고 티피는, 내가 그 이야기에 얼마나 마음이 쓰이는지 까맣게 모른 채, 고인의 정당한 빚을 다 갚고 회사에 들어올 떼이거나 받을지 의심스러운 채권 가운데 그의 몫을 제하고 나면, 남은 자산 전부를 다 합쳐도 천 파운드를 쳐주지 않겠다고 나에게 일러 주었다.\n\n이것은 약 여섯 주가 지난 무렵의 일이었다. 그동안 나는 줄곧 고문에 시달렸다. 그리고 밀스 양이, 가슴이 찢어진 내 어린 도라가 내 이야기가 나오면 그저 \"오, 가엾은 아빠! 오, 사랑하는 아빠!\" 할 뿐 다른 말은 한마디도 않는다고 여전히 전해 줄 때면, 나는 정말이지 내 손으로 나 자신을 해치고야 말 것만 같았다. 또한 그녀에게는 스펜로 씨의 미혼 누이인 두 고모 말고는 다른 친척이 없는데, 그들은 퍼트니에 살았고 여러 해 동안 자기 오라비와 그저 어쩌다 한 번 연락할 뿐이었다는 것도 전해 들었다. 둘이 다툰 적이 있어서가 아니라(밀스 양이 일러 주었다), 도라의 세례식 때 자기들은 만찬에 초대받을 자격이 있다고 여겼는데 차나 마시러 오라고 초대받은 일이 있어서, 글로 자기들 의견을 밝히기를, \"모두의 행복을 위해서\" 자기들이 오지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 했다는 것이다. 그 후로 그들은 그들 길을 갔고, 그들의 오라비는 그의 길을 갔다.\n\n이 두 부인이 이제 칩거를 깨고 나타나, 도라를 데려가 퍼트니에서 함께 살자고 제안했다. 도라는 두 사람에게 매달려 울며 외쳤다. \"오, 그래요, 고모님들! 제발 줄리아 밀스와 저와 지프를 퍼트니로 데려가 주세요!\" 그래서 그들은 장례식이 끝나고 머지않아 떠났다.\n\n내가 어떻게 짬을 내어 퍼트니를 맴돌았는지는 나도 정말 모르겠다. 하지만 어떻게든 그 동네를 꽤 자주 어슬렁거리는 데 용케 성공했다. 밀스 양은 우정의 의무를 더 빈틈없이 다하려고 일기를 썼는데, 더러는 그 공유지에서 나를 만나 그것을 읽어 주거나, (그럴 짬이 없으면) 나에게 빌려주곤 했다. 내가 그 기록들을 얼마나 애지중지 간직했던지 — 여기 그 한 자락을 옮겨 적는다!\n\n\"월요일. 내 사랑스러운 도라는 여전히 몹시 풀이 죽어 있음. 두통. 지프가 어여쁘게 윤이 난다고 주의를 끌어 줌. 도라가 지프를 어루만짐. 그리하여 깨어난 추억이 슬픔의 봇물을 열어젖힘. 비탄이 밀려듦. (눈물은 마음의 이슬방울인가? 줄리아 밀스.)\n\n\"화요일. 도라는 기운 없고 신경이 곤두서 있음. 창백한 모습이 아름다움. (우리는 달에게서도 이를 보지 않는가? 줄리아 밀스.) 도라와 줄리아 밀스와 지프, 마차로 바람을 쐼. 지프가 창밖을 내다보며 청소부에게 사납게 짖어 대니, 도라의 얼굴에 미소가 번짐. (생명의 사슬은 이토록 보잘것없는 고리들로 엮여 있구나! 줄리아 밀스.)\n\n\"수요일. 도라는 비교적 명랑함. 어울릴 만한 가락이다 싶어 그녀에게 '저녁 종소리'를 불러 줌. 효과는 달래기는커녕 정반대. 도라가 형언할 수 없이 북받침. 나중에 제 방에서 흐느끼고 있는 것을 발견. 자신과 어린 가젤에 관한 시구를 읊어 줌. 헛수고. 기념비 위의 인내상도 들먹여 봄. (의문. 왜 하필 기념비 위에? 줄리아 밀스.)\n\n\"목요일. 도라는 확실히 나아짐. 밤잠을 더 잘 잠. 뺨에 발그레한 다마스크 빛이 살짝 되돌아옴. 디 시의 이름을 꺼내 보기로 결심. 바람을 쐬는 동안 조심스레 그 이름을 끄집어냄. 도라가 즉시 무너짐. '오, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 줄리아! 오, 나는 못되고 불효한 딸이었어!' 달래고 어루만짐. 무덤 가장자리에 선 디 시의 모습을 상상으로 그려 줌. 도라가 다시 무너짐. '오, 어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아? 오, 나를 어디로든 데려가 줘!' 몹시 놀람. 도라가 실신해 선술집에서 물 한 잔 얻어 옴. (시적인 인연. 문기둥의 격자무늬 간판, 격자무늬 같은 인생사. 아아! 줄리아 밀스.)\n\n\"금요일. 사건의 날. 한 사내가 파란 자루를 들고 부엌에 나타나, '굽을 고치라고 내놓은 아씨 부츠를 가지러 왔다'고 함. 요리사가 답하길, '그런 분부 없었다.' 사내가 따짐. 요리사가 알아보러 물러가며, 사내를 지프와 단둘이 남겨 둠. 요리사가 돌아오니 사내는 여전히 따지다가 결국 가 버림. 지프가 사라짐. 도라가 제정신이 아님. 경찰에 신고함. 사내의 인상착의는 넓적한 코와 다리 난간 같은 두 다리. 사방으로 수색. 지프는 없음. 도라가 슬피 울며 달래지지 않음. 어린 가젤을 다시 들먹임. 적절하나 소용없음. 저녁 무렵 낯선 소년이 찾아옴. 거실로 들임. 넓적한 코는 맞으나 난간 같은 다리는 아님. 1파운드를 원하며 개를 안다고 함. 거듭 채근해도 더는 설명하려 들지 않음. 도라가 1파운드를 내놓자 요리사를 어느 작은 집으로 데려감. 거기서 지프가 탁자 다리에 홀로 묶여 있었음. 도라의 기쁨, 지프가 저녁을 먹는 동안 그 둘레를 빙빙 돎. 이 반가운 변화에 용기를 얻어, 위층에서 디 시 이야기를 꺼냄. 도라가 다시 울며 애처롭게 외침. '오, 하지 마, 하지 마, 하지 마! 가엾은 아빠 말고 다른 걸 생각하다니, 너무 못된 짓이야!' — 지프를 끌어안고 흐느끼다 잠듦. (디 시는 시간의 너른 날개에 자신을 맡겨야 하지 않을까? 줄리아 밀스.)\"\n\n밀스 양과 그녀의 일기는 이 무렵 나의 유일한 위안이었다. 얼마 전까지 도라를 보았던 그녀를 만나는 것, 그녀의 공감 어린 책장 사이로 도라 이름의 첫 글자를 더듬어 보는 것, 그녀 때문에 점점 더 비참해지는 것 — 그것이 나의 유일한 위로였다. 나는 마치 카드로 지은 궁전에서 살아왔는데, 그것이 와르르 무너져 폐허 속에 밀스 양과 나만 남겨진 듯한 느낌이었다. 마치 어떤 험상궂은 마법사가 내 마음속 순결한 여신 둘레에 마법의 원을 그어 놓아서, 그토록 많은 사람을 그토록 먼 거리까지 실어 나를 수 있는 바로 그 억센 날개가 아니고서는 그 무엇으로도 내가 그 안에 들어설 수 없을 것만 같았다!\n\n# CHAPTER 39. WICKFIELD AND HEEP\n\n고모님은, 내 짐작에, 오래 끄는 내 우울 때문에 정말로 마음이 불편해지기 시작하셨던 모양인지, 내가 도버에 가서 세를 놓은 그 오두막이 다 잘 돌아가는지 살펴보고, 같은 세입자와 더 오래 사는 조건으로 계약을 마무리짓고 오기를 바라는 척하셨다. 재닛은 스트롱 부인의 집에 일하러 보내졌고, 그곳에서 나는 그 애를 날마다 보았다. 도버를 떠날 무렵 재닛은, 자기가 받아 온 그 인간 거부 교육의 마지막 마무리로 도선사와 결혼할까 말까 망설이고 있었으나, 끝내 그 모험은 하지 않기로 정했다. 내 생각에 그건 무슨 신념 때문이라기보다, 그저 그 남자가 마음에 들지 않았기 때문이었다.\n\n밀스 양과 헤어지는 것은 마음을 다잡아야 하는 일이었지만, 나는 고모님의 그 핑계에 차라리 기꺼이 따랐다. 그래야 아그네스와 더불어 평온한 몇 시간을 보낼 수 있었으니까. 나는 사흘쯤 자리를 비우는 일을 두고 마음씨 좋은 박사님과 상의했다. 박사님은 내가 그렇게 쉬는 것을 바라셨고 — 사실은 더 쉬기를 바라셨지만 나는 기운이 그걸 견디지 못했다 — 그래서 나는 가기로 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n민법 박사회 쪽 일로 말하자면, 그 방면에서 내 직무를 굳이 꼼꼼히 챙길 까닭이 별로 없었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 우리는 일류 소송 대리인들 사이에서 그리 좋은 평판을 얻지 못하고 있었고, 빠르게 미끄러져 미심쩍은 자리로 떨어지고 있었다. 스펜로 씨가 들어오기 전, 조킨스 씨 밑에서도 사업은 시원찮았고, 비록 새 피가 수혈되고 스펜로 씨가 보여 준 화려한 겉치레 덕에 한동안 살아나기는 했어도, 활동적인 운영자가 갑자기 사라지는 그런 타격을 흔들림 없이 견딜 만큼 단단한 토대 위에 서 있지는 못했다. 사업은 크게 기울었다. 조킨스 씨는 회사 안에서의 명성에도 불구하고 무사태평하고 무능한 사람이었으며, 바깥에서의 평판은 그 명성을 떠받쳐 줄 만한 것이 못 되었다. 나는 이제 그에게 넘겨졌는데, 그가 코담배나 들이마시며 일을 내버려 두는 꼴을 보고 있노라니, 고모님의 천 파운드가 그 어느 때보다 더 아까웠다.\n\n그러나 이게 최악은 아니었다. 민법 박사회 언저리에는 빌붙어 사는 자들과 바깥 사람들이 한 무리 있었는데, 이들은 정작 자기가 소송 대리인은 아니면서 일반 서식 업무에 손을 댔고, 떡고물 한몫을 받는 대가로 이름을 빌려 주는 진짜 대리인을 통해 그 일을 처리했다 — 그런 자들도 꽤 많았다. 우리 회사도 이제는 어떤 조건으로든 일거리가 아쉬웠던 터라, 우리도 이 고결한 무리에 끼어들어, 그 빌붙는 자들과 바깥 사람들에게 미끼를 던져 일감을 우리에게 가져오도록 꾀었다. 우리 모두가 노리는 것은 혼인 허가증과 자잘한 유언 검인이었고, 그것이 가장 짭짤한 벌이였으니, 그것을 두고 경쟁이 어찌나 치열했던지 말로 다 할 수 없었다. 민법 박사회로 들어오는 길목마다 호객꾼과 유인꾼이 진을 치고서, 상복 입은 사람이라면 누구든, 또 행색에 조금이라도 수줍은 구석이 있는 신사라면 누구든 가로채어, 제각기 자기 고용주가 관심을 둔 사무소로 꾀어 오라는 지시를 받고 있었다. 그 지시가 어찌나 잘 지켜졌던지, 나조차도 얼굴이 알려지기 전에 두 번이나 우리 회사의 주된 경쟁사 안으로 떠밀려 들어간 적이 있었다. 이렇게 호객하는 신사들의 이해가 서로 부딪치다 보니 감정이 상하는 일이 잦았고, 그러다 몸싸움까지 벌어졌다. 우리 회사의 주된 유인꾼(예전에 포도주 장사를 했고, 그 뒤에는 공인 중개업에 몸담았던 자다)이 며칠 동안 눈에 시퍼런 멍을 달고 돌아다니는 바람에 민법 박사회가 망신을 당하기도 했다. 이런 끄나풀들 가운데 누구든, 검은 상복 차림의 노부인을 마차에서 정중히 부축해 내려 준 다음, 노부인이 찾는 소송 대리인을 (말로) 죽여 버리고는, 자기 고용주가 바로 그 대리인의 정당한 후계자이자 대리인이라고 둘러대며, 노부인을 (때로는 몹시 감격해하는 노부인을) 자기 고용주의 사무소로 끌고 가는 일쯤은 아무렇지도 않게 여겼다. 이런 식으로 끌려온 포로가 내게도 여럿 넘겨졌다. 혼인 허가증으로 말하자면, 경쟁이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 허가증이 필요한 수줍은 신사로서는 맨 처음 만난 유인꾼에게 그대로 몸을 맡기거나, 아니면 쟁탈전의 대상이 되어 가장 힘센 자의 먹이가 되는 수밖에 없었다. 우리 회사의 한 서기는 바깥 사람이었는데, 이 다툼이 한창일 때면 모자를 쓴 채 앉아 있곤 했다. 누구든 잡혀 들어오면 곧장 뛰쳐나가 대리 판사 앞에서 선서시킬 채비를 해 두려는 것이었다. 이 유인 수법은 내 생각에 오늘날까지도 이어지고 있다. 내가 마지막으로 민법 박사회에 갔을 때도, 흰 앞치마를 두른 점잖고 건장한 사내 하나가 문간에서 내게 와락 달려들더니, 귀에 대고 \"혼인 허가증\"이라고 속삭이며, 하마터면 나를 두 팔로 번쩍 들어 어느 소송 대리인의 사무소로 옮겨 갈 뻔한 것을 간신히 막았다. 이 여담은 이쯤 하고, 도버 이야기로 넘어가자.\n\n오두막은 모든 것이 흡족한 상태였다. 그리고 나는 새 세입자가 고모님의 그 싸움을 물려받아 당나귀들과 끊임없는 전쟁을 벌이고 있더라고 전하여 고모님을 무척이나 기쁘게 해 드릴 수 있었다. 그곳에서 처리할 자잘한 용무를 마치고 하룻밤을 묵은 뒤, 나는 이튿날 이른 아침에 캔터베리로 걸어갔다. 어느덧 다시 겨울이었다. 상쾌하고 차가운 바람 부는 날씨와, 끝없이 펼쳐진 구릉지가 내 희망을 조금은 환하게 밝혀 주었다.\n\n캔터베리에 들어서면서, 나는 그 오래된 거리를 천천히 거닐었는데, 그 차분한 기쁨이 마음을 가라앉히고 가슴을 어루만져 주었다. 옛 간판들이 그대로 있었고, 가게 위에 걸린 옛 이름들도, 그 안에서 일하는 옛 사람들도 그대로였다. 내가 이곳에서 학생으로 지낸 지 어찌나 오래된 것 같던지, 이곳이 이리도 변하지 않았다는 것이 의아할 지경이었다. 그러다 나 자신이 얼마나 변하지 않았는가를 떠올리고서야 고개가 끄덕여졌다. 묘하게도, 내 마음속에서 아그네스와 떼려야 뗄 수 없던 그 고요한 기운이, 그녀가 사는 이 도시 전체에까지 스며 있는 듯했다. 유서 깊은 대성당의 첨탑들, 그리고 그 아련한 울음소리로 완전한 침묵보다 더 깊은 적막을 자아내던 늙은 갈까마귀와 까마귀들, 오래전 쓰러져 부서진 동상들이 빼곡히 박힌 낡은 성문들 — 그 동상들은 그것을 우러러보던 경건한 순례자들처럼 닳고 허물어져 있었다 — 수백 년 세월의 담쟁이가 박공 끝과 무너진 담벼락을 타고 기어오른 고요한 모퉁이들, 오래된 집들, 들판과 과수원과 정원이 어우러진 목가적인 풍경, 그 어디에서든, 그 모든 것에서, 나는 똑같이 더없이 잔잔한 공기를, 똑같이 고요하고 사색적이며 마음을 누그러뜨리는 기운을 느꼈다.\n\n위크필드 씨의 집에 다다라, 일 층 아래쪽 작은 방, 예전에 유라이아 힙이 늘 앉아 있던 그 방에 들어서니, 미코버 씨가 더없이 부지런히 펜을 놀리고 있었다. 그는 법조계 사람처럼 보이는 검은 양복을 차려입고서, 그 비좁은 사무실 안에서 우람하고 커다랗게 버티고 있었다.\n\n미코버 씨는 나를 보고 무척이나 반가워했으나, 한편으로는 조금 당황한 기색이기도 했다. 그는 곧장 나를 유라이아 앞으로 데려가려 했지만, 나는 사양했다.\n\n\"이 집은 예전부터 잘 알잖아요, 기억하시죠.\" 내가 말했다. \"위층은 알아서 찾아갈게요. 법률 일은 좀 마음에 드세요, 미코버 씨?\"\n\n\"이보게, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답했다. \"더 높은 상상력을 지닌 사람에게, 법률 공부의 거슬리는 점은 그것이 끌어안고 있는 자질구레한 세부 사항의 양이라네. 우리가 주고받는 업무 서신에서조차,\" 미코버 씨가 자기가 쓰던 편지 몇 통을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"정신이 어떤 드높은 표현의 경지로 날아오를 자유가 없거든. 그래도 위대한 일이야. 위대한 일이고말고!\"\n\n이어서 그는 자기가 유라이아 힙이 예전에 살던 집의 세입자가 되었다는 것과, 미코버 부인이 자기 집 지붕 아래에서 다시금 나를 맞이하게 되어 무척 기뻐할 거라는 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n\"누추하지.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 친구 힙이 즐겨 쓰는 표현을 빌리자면 말일세. 하지만 그게 더 야심 찬 거처로 가는 디딤돌이 될지도 모르지.\"\n\n나는 그에게, 여태까지 친구 힙이 그를 대하는 태도에 만족할 만한 까닭이 있었느냐고 물었다. 그는 대답하기에 앞서 일어나 문이 꼭 닫혀 있는지 확인하고 나서, 한층 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"이보게 코퍼필드, 금전적 곤경에 짓눌린 사람은, 대다수 사람들 앞에서 불리한 처지에 놓이게 마련이라네. 그 불리함은, 그 곤경 때문에 봉급이 엄밀히 따져 받을 때가 되기도 전에 미리 끌어다 써야 할 지경이 되면 결코 줄어들지 않지. 내가 말할 수 있는 건 이것뿐이야. 굳이 자세히 밝힐 필요 없는 어떤 부탁들에 대해, 내 친구 힙은 그의 머리와 가슴의 명예에 똑같이 빛이 될 만한 방식으로 응해 주었다는 것 말일세.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔 그가 돈에 그리 후한 사람 같지도 않던데요.\" 내가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"천만에!\" 미코버 씨가 무언가에 얽매인 듯한 태도로 말했다. \"나는 내 친구 힙을, 내가 직접 겪은 대로 말하는 것일세.\"\n\n\"그 경험이 그토록 좋은 것이었다니 다행이네요.\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"아주 친절한 말이군, 이보게 코퍼필드.\" 미코버 씨가 말하더니, 콧노래를 흥얼거렸다.\n\n\"위크필드 씨는 자주 보세요?\" 화제를 바꾸려고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"자주는 아니야.\" 미코버 씨가 대수롭지 않게 말했다. \"위크필드 씨는, 내 보기에, 더없이 훌륭한 뜻을 품은 사람이지. 하지만 그분은 — 한마디로, 한물간 사람이라네.\"\n\n\"그분의 동업자가 그분을 그렇게 만들려 드는 게 아닌가 싶습니다만.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이보게 코퍼필드!\" 미코버 씨가 의자 위에서 불편하게 몸을 몇 번 뒤척이고 나서 대꾸했다. \"한마디 하게 해 주게나! 나는 지금 비밀을 다루는 자리에 있네. 나는 지금 신임을 받는 자리에 있어. 어떤 화제들을 입에 올리는 것은, 설령 미코버 부인 자신과라도 (오랜 세월 내 온갖 부침을 함께해 온 동반자이자, 보기 드물게 명석한 지성을 지닌 여인이지만) 이제 내게 맡겨진 직무와 양립할 수 없는 일이라고 나는 생각하게 되었네. 그러니 외람되지만 한 가지 제안하겠네. 우리의 우정 어린 교류에서 — 결코 흐트러지는 일이 없기를 바라네! — 우리 한 가닥 선을 긋세나. 이 선의 한쪽에는,\" 미코버 씨가 사무실 자를 책상 위에 대어 그 선을 그려 보이며 말했다. \"사소한 예외 하나만 빼고 인간 지성의 모든 영역이 들어가네. 다른 한쪽에는, 바로 그 예외가 있지. 다시 말해, 위크필드 힙 상회의 사무와, 거기에 딸리고 결부된 모든 것 말일세. 이 제안을 내 젊은 시절 벗의 한결 냉정한 판단에 맡긴다 해서, 그 벗의 기분을 상하게 하는 건 아니리라 믿네만?\"\n\n미코버 씨에게 일어난 어색한 변화가, 마치 그 새 직무가 몸에 맞지 않는 옷이라도 되는 양 그를 옥죄고 있는 것이 보였지만, 나는 기분 상해할 권리가 없다고 느꼈다. 내가 그렇게 말해 주자 그는 마음이 놓이는 듯했고, 나와 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"나는 매료되었네, 코퍼필드.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"확언컨대, 위크필드 양에게 말이야. 그분은 더없이 빼어난 아가씨라네. 보기 드문 매력과 우아함과 미덕을 갖추셨지. 내 명예를 걸고,\" 미코버 씨가 어딘지 막연하게 손에 입을 맞추고 더없이 점잖은 태도로 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"나는 위크필드 양께 경의를 바치네! 흠!\" \"그건 적어도 반가운 일이네요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 즐거웠던 오후, 우리가 자네와 더불어 행복하게 보냈던 그날, 자네가 D가 가장 좋아하는 글자라고 우리에게 단언하지 않았더라면,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"나는 틀림없이 A가 그 글자였으리라 짐작했을 걸세.\"\n\n우리 모두는 가끔 어떤 느낌에 사로잡힌다. 지금 우리가 하는 말과 행동이, 까마득한 옛적에 이미 말해지고 행해졌던 것 같은 느낌 — 아득한 옛날에 똑같은 얼굴들, 똑같은 사물들, 똑같은 상황에 둘러싸여 있었던 것 같은 느낌 — 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지를, 마치 문득 그것이 기억나기라도 한 듯 완벽하게 알고 있는 것 같은 느낌 말이다. 그가 그 말을 입에 올리기 직전만큼, 내 평생 그 신비로운 느낌을 더 강하게 받은 적은 없었다.\n\n나는 일단 미코버 씨와 작별하면서, 집에 있는 모든 이에게 안부를 잘 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 그가 다시 의자에 앉아 펜을 집어 들고, 더 편하게 글을 쓰려고 빳빳한 깃 속에서 고개를 이리저리 돌리는 모습을 뒤로하고 떠나면서, 나는 그가 새 직무를 맡은 뒤로 그와 나 사이에 무언가가 끼어들어, 예전처럼 서로 가까이 다가서는 것을 막고, 우리 사이의 성격을 완전히 바꿔 버렸음을 똑똑히 알아차렸다.\n\n예스러운 그 응접실에는 아무도 없었으나, 힙 부인이 어딘가에 있다는 흔적은 남아 있었다. 나는 아직 아그네스의 것인 그 방을 들여다보았고, 그녀가 난롯가에서, 자기가 쓰는 아담하고 고풍스러운 책상에 앉아 무언가를 쓰고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n내가 빛을 가리는 바람에 그녀가 고개를 들었다. 그녀의 그 주의 깊은 얼굴에 환한 변화를 불러일으키는 까닭이 되고, 그 다정한 눈길과 환대의 대상이 된다는 것이 어찌나 기쁘던지!\n\n\"아, 아그네스!\" 우리가 나란히 곁에 앉았을 때 내가 말했다. \"요즘 네가 어찌나 보고 싶던지!\"\n\n\"정말?\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"또? 그새?\"\n\n나는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠어, 아그네스. 나는 마땅히 갖춰야 할 어떤 마음의 능력이 모자란 것 같아. 이곳에서 보낸 그 행복한 옛 시절에, 너는 그렇게 늘 나를 위해 생각해 주는 게 버릇이 됐고, 나도 그렇게 자연스레 네게 와서 조언과 힘을 구했으니, 정말이지 나는 그 능력을 익힐 기회를 놓쳐 버린 게 아닌가 싶어.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 아그네스가 환하게 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고 불러야 할지 모르겠어.\" 내가 대답했다. \"내가 진지하고 끈기 있다고는 생각하는데?\"\n\n\"그건 확실해.\" 아그네스가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 참을성도 있고, 아그네스?\" 나는 살짝 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"응.\" 아그네스가 웃으며 대답했다. \"그럭저럭.\"\n\n\"그런데도,\" 내가 말했다. \"나는 이렇게 비참해지고 안달이 나고, 스스로를 다잡는 힘이 이토록 흔들리고 우유부단하니, 분명 뭔가가 모자란 거야 — 뭐랄까 — 일종의 의지할 데가 모자란 거겠지?\"\n\n\"네가 그러고 싶다면 그렇게 불러.\" 아그네스가 말했다.\n\n\"그래!\" 내가 받았다. \"이것 봐! 네가 런던에 오면, 나는 너를 의지하고, 곧바로 목표와 나아갈 길이 생겨. 그러다 거기서 밀려나, 이곳에 오면, 한순간에 사람이 달라진 것 같아. 이 방에 들어왔다고 해서, 나를 괴롭히던 상황이 달라진 건 아니야. 그런데 그 짧은 사이에 어떤 기운이 나를 휘감아 나를 바꿔 놓는단 말이지, 아, 얼마나 좋은 쪽으로 바뀌는지 몰라! 그게 뭘까? 네 비결이 뭐야, 아그네스?\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 숙인 채 불을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"늘 똑같은 얘기지.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 이런 말을 해도 웃지 마. 작은 일에서나 큰일에서나 늘 한결같았어. 옛날에 내가 겪던 고민들은 시시한 것이었고, 지금은 심각한 것이야. 하지만 내가 누이에게서 멀어질 때면 언제나—\"\n\n아그네스가 고개를 들었다 — 어찌나 천사 같은 얼굴이던지! — 그리고 내게 손을 내밀었고, 나는 그 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"처음에 네가 조언하고 인정해 주는 일이 없을 때면 언제나, 아그네스, 나는 정신을 못 차리고 온갖 곤경에 빠져드는 것 같았어. 그러다 끝내 (늘 그래 왔듯이) 네게 오면, 나는 평온과 행복에 이르렀지. 나는 지금 지친 나그네처럼 집에 돌아와, 이렇게 복된 안식의 느낌을 누리고 있어!\"\n\n내가 한 말이 어찌나 가슴 깊이 사무치던지, 어찌나 진심으로 마음을 흔들던지, 목소리가 잠겨 버려, 나는 한 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 울음을 터뜨렸다. 나는 진실을 적고 있다. 우리 가운데 그토록 많은 이의 내면이 그러하듯, 내 안에 어떤 모순과 어긋남이 있었든, 무엇이 그토록 다를 수도, 그토록 더 나을 수도 있었든, 내가 비뚤어지게 제 마음의 소리에서 벗어나 헤맨 일이 무엇이었든, 나는 아무것도 알지 못했다. 다만, 아그네스가 곁에 있다는 안식과 평온을 느낄 때면, 내가 뜨겁게 진심이었다는 것만은 알았다.\n\n그 잔잔한 누이다운 태도로, 그 빛나는 눈으로, 그 다정한 목소리로, 그리고 오래전부터 그녀를 품은 그 집을 내게 더없이 신성한 곳으로 만들어 준 그 사랑스러운 평정으로, 그녀는 곧 나를 이 나약함에서 끌어내, 우리가 마지막으로 만난 뒤로 일어난 모든 일을 털어놓도록 이끌었다.\n\n\"더는 들려줄 게 없어, 아그네스.\" 속내를 다 털어놓고 나서 내가 말했다. \"이제, 나는 너를 의지할 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 나를 의지해선 안 돼, 트롯우드.\" 아그네스가 환한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"다른 누군가를 의지해야지.\"\n\n\"도라를?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"그게 말이야, 아직 얘기 안 했지, 아그네스.\" 내가 조금 멋쩍어하며 말했다. \"도라는 좀 — 죽어도 의지하기 어렵다는 말은 하고 싶지 않아, 그 애는 순수함과 진실 그 자체니까 — 하지만 좀 뭐랄까 — 정말이지 어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠어, 아그네스. 그 애는 겁 많은 작은 아이라, 쉽게 마음이 흔들리고 겁을 먹어. 얼마 전에, 그 애 아버지가 돌아가시기 전에, 내가 그 애에게 말해 두는 게 옳다고 여겨서 — 아니, 어떻게 된 일인지 들어 줄 수 있다면 얘기해 줄게.\"\n\n그래서 나는 아그네스에게, 내가 가난을 고백했던 일이며, 요리책이며, 살림 장부며, 그 밖의 모든 일을 들려주었다.\n\n\"오, 트롯우드!\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 나무랐다. \"딱 너다운 그 성급함이야! 세상에서 자리 잡으려고 애쓰는 일에 진심일 수는 있겠지만, 그렇게 겁 많고 사랑스럽고 세상 물정 모르는 아이한테 그토록 느닷없이 굴 것까진 없잖아. 가엾은 도라!\"\n\n이 대답을 하면서 그녀가 목소리에 담아낸 것만큼 다정하고 너그러운 친절을, 나는 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다. 마치 그녀가 도라를 감탄과 애정으로 끌어안으면서, 그 작은 마음을 떨리게 한 내 성급함을 그 사려 깊은 보호로써 넌지시 나무라는 모습을 본 것만 같았다. 마치 도라가 그 매혹적인 천진함을 한껏 드러내며 아그네스를 어루만지고, 고마워하고, 나에게 항의하듯이 애교를 부리면서, 그 어린아이다운 순수함으로 나를 사랑하는 모습을 본 것만 같았다.\n\n나는 아그네스가 어찌나 고맙던지, 또 어찌나 그녀가 존경스럽던지! 나는 그 두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을, 환한 미래 속에서, 그토록 잘 어울리는 벗으로, 서로가 서로를 그토록 빛내 주는 모습으로 그려 보았다!\n\n\"그럼 난 어떻게 해야 할까, 아그네스?\" 잠시 불을 바라보고 나서 내가 물었다. \"어떻게 하는 게 옳을까?\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"떳떳한 길은, 그 두 분께 편지를 쓰는 거야. 무엇이든 몰래 하는 건 떳떳하지 못한 일이라고 생각하지 않아?\"\n\n\"그래. 네가 그렇게 생각한다면.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"나야 그런 일을 판단할 자격이 모자라지.\" 아그네스가 겸손하게 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"하지만 분명히 느껴 — 한마디로, 네가 그렇게 비밀스럽고 은밀하게 구는 건 너답지 않다는 느낌이 들어.\"\n\n\"네가 나를 너무 높이 봐 줘서 그런 거지, 아무래도 그런 것 같아, 아그네스.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네 천성의 솔직함을 두고 너답다고 하는 거야.\" 그녀가 받았다. \"그러니 나라면 그 두 분께 편지를 쓰겠어. 일어난 일을 되도록 명백하고 솔직하게 다 털어놓겠어. 그리고 가끔 그 댁을 찾아뵙도록 허락해 달라고 청하겠어. 네가 아직 젊고, 인생에서 자리를 잡으려고 애쓰는 중인 걸 생각하면, 그분들이 내거는 어떤 조건에도 기꺼이 따르겠다고 말하는 게 좋을 것 같아. 도라에게 알리지 않은 채로 네 청을 물리치지는 말아 달라고, 그리고 적당한 때가 되었다 싶으면 도라와 의논해 달라고 부탁드리겠어. 너무 격하게 굴거나,\" 아그네스가 부드럽게 말했다. \"지나치게 많은 걸 내세우지는 않겠어. 나라면 내 신의와 끈기를 — 그리고 도라를 믿겠어.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분들이 도라에게 말을 꺼내서 그 애를 또 겁먹게 한다면, 아그네스.\" 내가 말했다. \"그래서 도라가 울기만 하고 나에 대해선 아무 말도 안 한다면!\"\n\n\"그럴 것 같아?\" 아그네스가 여전히 그 다정하고 사려 깊은 얼굴로 물었다.\n\n\"맙소사, 그 앤 새처럼 잘도 놀라거든.\" 내가 말했다. \"그럴 수도 있다고! 아니면 그 두 스펜로 양이 (그런 부류의 나이 든 부인들은 가끔 별난 데가 있으니까) 그런 식으로 말을 건넬 만한 분들이 아닐 수도 있고!\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 트롯우드,\" 아그네스가 그 부드러운 눈을 들어 나를 보며 대답했다. \"그런 건 따지지 않겠어. 어쩌면 이걸 하는 게 옳은지 아닌지만 따져 보는 게 나을지도 몰라. 그래서 옳다면, 하는 거지.\"\n\n나는 더는 그 문제로 망설이지 않았다. 마음이 한결 가벼워진 채로, 그러나 내 과업의 막중한 무게를 깊이 느끼며, 나는 그 오후를 통째로 이 편지의 초안을 다듬는 데 바쳤다. 그 대업을 위해 아그네스가 자기 책상을 내게 내주었다. 그러나 먼저 나는 아래층으로 내려가 위크필드 씨와 유라이아 힙을 만났다.\n\n유라이아는 정원에 새로 지어 회반죽 냄새가 풀풀 나는 사무실을 차지하고 있었는데, 무더기로 쌓인 책과 서류 한가운데서 유난히 비루해 보였다. 그는 여느 때처럼 알랑거리는 태도로 나를 맞이하더니, 미코버 씨에게서 내가 왔다는 말을 못 들은 척했다. 그 시치미를 나는 외람되지만 믿지 않기로 했다. 그는 나를 위크필드 씨의 방으로 안내했는데, 그 방은 예전의 그림자에 지나지 않았다 — 새 동업자의 편의를 위해 온갖 편리한 것들이 다 빠져나가 버린 탓이었다 — 그리고 위크필드 씨와 내가 인사를 나누는 동안, 그는 난롯불 앞에 서서 등을 쬐며, 뼈만 앙상한 손으로 제 턱을 쓱쓱 쓸어 댔다.\n\n\"트롯우드, 캔터베리에 머무는 동안엔 우리 집에 묵겠지?\" 위크필드 씨가 유라이아의 동의를 구하는 듯한 눈길을 흘끗 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 묵을 방이 있을까요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야 하는데, 그 호칭이 워낙 입에 배어서요,\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"기꺼이 도련님이 쓰시던 옛 방을 비워 드리지요, 그게 좋으시다면요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"왜 자네가 불편을 겪어야 하나? 다른 방이 있어. 다른 방이 있다네.\" \"오, 하지만 아시다시피,\" 유라이아가 씩 웃으며 받았다. \"저야 정말 기쁠 텐데요!\"\n\n이야기를 짧게 끝내려고, 나는 다른 방을 쓰거나 아니면 아예 묵지 않겠다고 말했다. 그래서 내가 다른 방을 쓰기로 정해졌고, 나는 저녁 식사 때까지 회사 사람들과 작별하고 다시 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n나는 아그네스 말고는 다른 동무가 없기를 바랐다. 그러나 힙 부인이, 바람이 그렇게 부는 날에는 그 방이 응접실이나 식당보다 류머티즘에 더 좋은 방향이라는 핑계로, 자기와 뜨개질감을 그 방 난롯가로 가져와도 되겠느냐고 청해 둔 터였다. 나는 그녀를 양심의 가책 하나 없이 대성당 꼭대기 첨탑 위에 올려놓고 바람의 처분에 맡겨 버리고 싶을 지경이었지만, 어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕으로 삼아 그녀에게 정답게 인사를 건넸다.\n\n\"황송할 따름이에요, 나리.\" 내가 건강을 묻자 힙 부인이 대답했다. \"하지만 그저 그렇답니다. 자랑할 게 별로 없네요. 우리 유라이아가 세상에 자리 잘 잡은 걸 볼 수만 있다면, 더 바랄 게 없을 것 같아요. 우리 유리가 어때 보여요, 나리?\"\n\n내가 보기엔 그는 여느 때처럼 악당 같아 보였다. 그래서 나는 그에게서 아무 변화도 못 보겠다고 대답했다.\n\n\"아니, 변한 것 같지 않아요?\" 힙 부인이 말했다. \"거기선 황송하지만 생각이 다르다고 말씀드려야겠네요. 좀 야윈 게 안 보여요?\"\n\n\"여느 때보다 더하지는 않은데요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"어머, 그래요!\" 힙 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 나리는 어미의 눈으로 저 애를 보는 게 아니잖아요!\"\n\n그 어미의 눈은, 그것이 내 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그에게는 그토록 다정할지언정 나머지 세상에는 사악한 눈이라고 나는 생각했다. 그리고 내 생각에 그녀와 그녀의 아들은 서로에게 헌신적이었다. 그 눈은 나를 지나쳐 아그네스에게로 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"위크필드 양은 저 애가 시들고 쇠해 가는 게 안 보여요?\" 힙 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨.\" 아그네스가 하던 일을 조용히 이어 가며 말했다. \"부인은 저분을 너무 걱정하세요. 아주 건강하신걸요.\"\n\n힙 부인은 엄청나게 코를 훌쩍이고는 다시 뜨개질을 시작했다.\n\n그녀는 한순간도 손을 멈추거나 우리 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 나는 이른 시각에 도착했으니, 저녁 식사까지 아직 서너 시간이 남아 있었다. 그런데도 그녀는 거기 앉아, 모래시계가 모래를 쏟아 내듯 단조롭게 뜨개바늘을 놀려 댔다. 그녀는 난로 한쪽에 앉았고, 나는 그 앞 책상에 앉았으며, 나에게서 조금 떨어진 반대쪽에 아그네스가 앉아 있었다. 천천히 내 편지를 곱씹다가 눈을 들어 아그네스의 사색에 잠긴 얼굴과 마주칠 때면, 그 얼굴이 환하게, 그 특유의 천사 같은 표정으로 나를 격려하며 빛나는 것이 보였고, 그러면 곧 나는 그 사악한 눈이 나를 지나쳐 그녀에게로 갔다가 다시 내게 돌아왔다가, 슬그머니 뜨개질감으로 떨어지는 것을 느꼈다. 그 뜨개질이 무엇이었는지는, 내가 그 기술을 알지 못하니 모르겠으나, 그것은 그물처럼 보였다. 그리고 그녀가 그 중국 젓가락 같은 뜨개바늘을 놀려 대는 모습은, 난롯불 빛 속에서 보기 흉한 마녀처럼 보였다. 맞은편의 환한 선함에 아직은 가로막혀 있으나, 머지않아 그물을 한 번 던질 채비를 하고 있는 마녀 말이다.\n\n저녁 식사 자리에서도 그녀는 똑같이 깜빡이지 않는 눈으로 감시를 이어 갔다. 저녁 식사가 끝나자 그녀의 아들이 그 일을 넘겨받았고, 위크필드 씨와 그와 나, 셋만 남게 되자, 그는 나를 곁눈질하고 몸을 비비 꼬아 대는 통에 나는 도저히 견디기 어려울 지경이었다. 응접실에서는 또 어미가 뜨개질을 하며 지켜보고 있었다. 아그네스가 노래하고 연주하는 내내, 어미는 피아노 곁에 앉아 있었다. 한번은 그녀가 어떤 발라드를 청하면서, 자기 유리가 (커다란 안락의자에 앉아 하품을 하고 있던 그가) 그 곡을 무척 좋아한다고 했다. 그러고는 틈틈이 그를 돌아보며, 그가 음악에 흠뻑 빠져 있다고 아그네스에게 일러 주었다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 언급하지 않고는 좀처럼 입을 떼는 법이 없었다 — 아니, 한 번이라도 그런 적이 있었는지 의심스럽다. 그것이 그녀에게 맡겨진 임무임이 내게는 분명히 보였다.\n\n이런 일이 잠자리에 들 시각까지 계속되었다. 그 어미와 아들이 두 마리의 커다란 박쥐처럼 온 집안 위에 매달려, 그 흉측한 몰골로 집안을 어둡게 만드는 꼴을 보고 있자니 어찌나 불편하던지, 차라리 뜨개질이고 뭐고 아래층에 그대로 남아 있는 편이 잠자리에 드는 것보다 나을 지경이었다. 나는 거의 한숨도 못 잤다. 이튿날에도 뜨개질과 감시가 다시 시작되어 온종일 이어졌다.\n\n나는 아그네스와 십 분도 이야기할 기회를 얻지 못했다. 가까스로 그녀에게 내 편지를 보여 줄 수 있었을 뿐이다. 나는 그녀에게 함께 산책을 나가자고 제안했지만, 힙 부인이 자꾸만 몸이 더 안 좋다고 하소연하는 통에, 아그네스는 자비를 베풀어 집에 남아 그녀의 말동무가 되어 주었다. 황혼 무렵 나는 혼자 밖으로 나가, 내가 어떻게 해야 할지, 그리고 유라이아 힙이 런던에서 내게 한 말을 더 이상 아그네스에게 숨기는 것이 과연 옳은지를 곰곰이 생각했다. 그 일이 다시금 나를 몹시 괴롭히기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n마을을 완전히 벗어날 만큼 멀리 걷지도 못한 채, 길이 잘 닦인 램스게이트 가도에 들어섰을 때, 뒤에서 누군가가 흙먼지 사이로 나를 불러 세웠다. 그 비척거리는 걸음걸이와 헐렁한 외투는 못 알아볼 수가 없었다. 나는 멈춰 섰고, 유라이아 힙이 다가왔다.\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"걸음이 어찌나 빠르신지!\" 그가 말했다. \"제 다리가 꽤 긴 편인데도, 도련님 덕분에 아주 혼쭐이 났네요.\"\n\n\"어디 가는 길이야?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"도련님하고 같이 가려고요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 옛 지인과 산책하는 즐거움을 허락해 주신다면요.\" 이렇게 말하면서, 그는 비위를 맞추려는 건지 비웃는 건지 모를 몸짓을 한 번 까딱하더니, 내 곁에서 보조를 맞춰 걸었다.\n\n\"유라이아!\" 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 나는 되도록 정중하게 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이아가 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히 말하면 (기분 상해하진 말고), 나는 사람들에게 너무 시달려서, 혼자 걸으려고 나온 거야.\"\n\n그는 나를 곁눈으로 보더니, 그 더없이 짓궂은 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"어머니를 두고 하시는 말씀이군요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 그래, 맞아.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 하지만 아시다시피 저희는 워낙 미천한 사람들이잖아요.\" 그가 받았다. \"그리고 저희 자신의 미천함을 그토록 잘 아는 만큼, 미천하지 않은 자들에게 구석으로 밀려나지 않도록 정말 조심해야 한답니다. 사랑에서는 어떤 술수든 정당하니까요, 나리.\"\n\n그는 커다란 두 손을 턱에 닿을 만큼 치켜들어 살살 비비고는, 나직이 낄낄거렸는데, 인간으로서 지을 수 있는 가장 악의에 찬 비비 원숭이의 표정이 아닐까 싶었다.\n\n\"보세요,\" 그는 여전히 그 불쾌한 식으로 제 몸을 끌어안고 내게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"도련님은 꽤나 위험한 연적이거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 늘 그러셨죠, 아시다시피.\"\n\n\"네가 나 때문에 위크필드 양을 감시하고, 그녀의 집을 집이 아니게 만드는 거냐?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 코퍼필드 도련님! 그거 참 험한 말씀이네요.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"내 뜻을 네가 좋을 대로 어떤 말로 바꿔도 좋아.\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 뭔지 너도 나만큼 잘 알잖아, 유라이아.\"\n\n\"오 아니죠! 도련님이 말로 옮기셔야죠.\" 그가 말했다. \"오, 정말이지! 저로선 도저히 못 하겠어요.\"\n\n\"내가 위크필드 양을 더없이 사랑하는 누이가 아닌 다른 무엇으로 여긴다고 생각하는 거냐?\" 아그네스를 생각해서 그에게 아주 차분하고 조용히 대하려 애쓰며 내가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 대답했다. \"보시다시피 저는 그 물음에 답할 의무가 없죠. 아닐 수도 있고요, 아시다시피. 하지만 또 그런가 하면, 그럴 수도 있는 거고요!\"\n\n그의 표정에 어린, 그리고 속눈썹 그림자 하나 없는 그 그늘 없는 두 눈에 어린 그 비루한 교활함에 견줄 만한 것을, 나는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"좋아 그럼!\" 내가 말했다. \"위크필드 양을 위해서—\"\n\n\"제 아그네스 말이죠!\" 그가 병적이고 모난 몸짓으로 제 몸을 뒤틀며 외쳤다. \"그분을 아그네스라고 불러 주시겠어요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n\"아그네스 위크필드를 위해서 — 하느님의 축복이 그녀에게 있기를!\"\n\n\"그 축복 고맙습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내가 어떤 다른 상황이었다면 차라리 잭 케치한테나 털어놓을까 싶은 이야기를 들려주지.\"\n\n\"누구한테요, 나리?\" 유라이아가 목을 쭉 빼고 손으로 귀를 가리며 말했다.\n\n\"교수형 집행인 말이야.\" 내가 대답했다. \"내가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 어림없는 상대지.\" — 정작 그의 얼굴이야말로 그 비유를 아주 자연스러운 순서로 떠올리게 해 주었지만 말이다. \"나는 다른 아가씨와 약혼했어. 이걸로 네 마음이 풀리길 바란다.\"\n\n\"영혼을 걸고요?\" 유라이아가 말했다.\n\n내가 발끈하여 그가 요구하는 확언을 해 주려는 찰나, 그가 내 손을 덥석 잡더니 꽉 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"오, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 말했다. \"도련님 거실 난롯가에서 잠들어 그토록 폐를 끼쳐 드렸던 그날 밤, 제가 제 속내를 죄다 쏟아 냈을 때 도련님께서도 황송하게 마음을 터놓아 주셨더라면, 저는 결코 도련님을 의심하지 않았을 텐데요. 사정이 이러하니, 어머니는 당장 떼어 놓겠습니다. 그것도 더없이 기쁜 마음으로요. 애정에서 비롯된 조심성은 너그러이 봐주시겠죠? 정말 안타까워요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 도련님이 황송하게도 제게 속내를 터놓아 주지 않으신 게요! 저는 분명 도련님께 온갖 기회를 다 드렸는데 말이죠. 하지만 도련님은 제가 바라는 만큼 황송하게 저를 대해 주신 적이 없어요. 도련님은 제가 도련님을 좋아한 만큼 저를 좋아하신 적이 한 번도 없다는 걸 저도 알아요!\"\n\n이러는 내내 그는 그 축축하고 물고기 같은 손가락으로 내 손을 움켜쥐고 있었고, 나는 점잖게나마 손을 빼내려고 온갖 애를 다 썼다. 그러나 영 헛수고였다. 그는 내 손을 자기 오디색 외투 소매 밑으로 끌어당겼고, 나는 거의 떠밀리다시피 그와 팔짱을 낀 채 걸었다.\n\n\"돌아갈까요?\" 이윽고 유라이아가 나를 마을 쪽으로 휙 돌려세우며 말했다. 마을 위로는 어느새 초저녁 달이 빛나며 멀리 창문들을 은빛으로 물들이고 있었다.\n\n\"이 얘기를 그만두기 전에, 이건 분명히 알아 둬야겠다.\" 꽤 긴 침묵을 깨고 내가 말했다. \"내가 보기에 아그네스 위크필드는 너 따위보다 한참 위에 있고, 저 달 자체만큼이나 네 온갖 야심에서 멀리 떨어져 있어!\"\n\n\"고요하죠! 안 그래요!\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"정말요! 자, 인정하세요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 도련님은 제가 도련님을 좋아한 만큼은 저를 좋아하지 않으셨다는 걸요. 처음부터 줄곧 저를 너무 미천하다고 여기셨겠죠, 지금도요, 안 봐도 뻔하네요?\"\n\n\"나는 미천함을 늘어놓는 걸 좋아하지 않아.\" 내가 받았다. \"다른 무엇을 늘어놓는 것도 마찬가지고.\" \"그것 보세요!\" 유라이아가 달빛 속에서 흐물흐물하고 납빛으로 보이며 말했다. \"제가 모를 줄 아셨어요! 하지만 도련님은 저 같은 처지의 사람이 마땅히 지녀야 할 미천함을 얼마나 가볍게 여기시는지요, 코퍼필드 도련님! 아버지와 저는 둘 다 사내아이들을 위한 자선 학교에서 자랐고, 어머니도 그와 비슷한 공립 자선 시설에서 자랐답니다. 거기서 사람들은 우리에게 미천함을 잔뜩 가르쳤어요 — 아침부터 밤까지, 제가 아는 한 그 외엔 별로 가르친 게 없었죠. 우리는 이 사람에게도 미천해야 하고, 저 사람에게도 미천해야 하고, 여기서 모자를 벗어야 하고, 저기서 절을 해야 하고, 늘 제 분수를 알고 윗사람들 앞에서 굽실거려야 한다고요. 게다가 윗사람도 어찌나 많던지요! 아버지는 미천하게 굴어서 모범생 메달을 받으셨고, 저도 그랬죠. 아버지는 미천하게 굴어서 교회지기가 되셨어요. 점잖은 분들 사이에서 어찌나 행실 바른 사람이라는 평판을 얻으셨던지, 그분들이 기어이 그 자리에 앉히기로 작정하셨던 거예요. '미천하게 굴어라, 유라이아,' 아버지가 제게 말씀하셨죠. '그러면 출세할 거다. 학교에서 너와 나한테 늘 귀에 못이 박이도록 들려준 게 그거 아니냐. 그게 제일 잘 먹히는 거다. 미천하게 굴어라,' 아버지가 말씀하셨죠. '그러면 잘될 거야!' 그런데 정말이지 그게 나쁘지 않게 먹혔어요!\"\n\n거짓 미천함이라는 이 가증스러운 위선이 힙 집안에서 비롯되었을지도 모른다는 생각이, 그때 처음으로 내 머리에 떠올랐다. 나는 그 수확물은 보아 왔으나, 그 씨앗에 대해서는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"제가 아주 어린 소년이었을 때,\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"저는 미천함이 어떤 일을 해내는지 알게 됐고, 거기에 푹 빠졌어요. 굴욕의 떡을 입맛 다시며 먹었죠. 저는 제 배움을 미천한 수준에서 딱 멈추고는, '여기서 멈춰!' 하고 다짐했어요. 도련님이 제게 라틴어를 가르쳐 주겠다고 하셨을 때, 저는 더 잘 알았죠. '사람들은 자기가 너보다 위에 있는 걸 좋아하는 법이다,' 아버지가 말씀하셨거든요. '몸을 낮춰라.' 저는 지금 이 순간까지도 아주 미천하답니다, 코퍼필드 도련님, 하지만 이제 저도 조금은 힘을 갖게 됐어요!\"\n\n그리고 그가 이 모든 말을 한 까닭은 — 달빛 속에서 그의 얼굴을 보며 나는 알았다 — 그가 자기 힘을 써서 스스로 그 빚을 되갚으리라 작정했음을 내게 알리려는 것이었다. 나는 그의 비열함과 교활함과 악의를 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었다. 그러나 이 어린 시절부터의, 이토록 오랜 억압이 얼마나 비루하고 무자비하며 앙심에 찬 영혼을 길러 냈을지를, 이제야 처음으로 온전히 깨달았다.\n\n자기 자신에 대한 그의 이 이야기에는 그나마 한 가지 반가운 결과가 따랐으니, 그가 손을 빼내어 다시 한번 제 턱 밑에서 자기 몸을 끌어안게 되었다는 것이다. 일단 그에게서 떨어지자, 나는 떨어진 채로 있기로 마음먹었다. 우리는 나란히 걸어 돌아오면서, 오는 길에 더는 별말을 하지 않았다. 내가 그에게 털어놓은 이야기 때문에 그의 기분이 들떴는지, 아니면 그가 이 회상에 흠뻑 잠겨 든 탓인지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그의 기분은 무언가의 영향으로 한껏 들떠 있었다. 그는 저녁 식사 자리에서 평소보다 말이 많았다. 어머니에게 (집에 다시 들어선 순간부터 임무에서 풀려난 그녀에게) 자기가 이제 노총각으로 지내기엔 나이가 너무 든 것 아니냐고 물었고, 한번은 아그네스를 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지, 나는 그를 때려눕힐 수만 있다면 가진 것을 죄다 내줘도 좋다고 생각했다.\n\n저녁 식사 후 우리 남자 셋만 남게 되자, 그는 한층 대담한 상태가 되었다. 그는 술을 거의, 아니 전혀 입에 대지 않았다. 그러니 그것은 그저 승리감에 도취된 오만함이었으리라 짐작되는데, 어쩌면 그것을 한껏 드러내 보이고픈 유혹을 내 존재가 부추긴 탓에 더욱 달아올랐는지도 모른다.\n\n나는 어제, 그가 위크필드 씨에게 술을 권해 마시게 하려 드는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그래서 아그네스가 나가면서 내게 던진 눈길의 뜻을 헤아려, 나는 한 잔으로 그쳤고, 그러고는 우리도 그녀를 따라가자고 제안했다. 오늘도 그렇게 하고 싶었지만, 유라이아가 나보다 한발 빨랐다.\n\n\"우리 손님을 자주 뵙지도 못하잖습니까, 나리.\" 그가, 식탁 끝에 그와는 너무도 대조적인 모습으로 앉아 있는 위크필드 씨를 향해 말했다. \"그러니 괜찮으시다면 포도주 한두 잔으로 손님을 환대하면 어떨까 싶은데요. 코퍼필드 씨, 도련님의 건강과 행복을 위하여!\"\n\n나는 그가 식탁 너머로 내민 손을 잡는 시늉이라도 해야 했다. 그러고 나서, 사뭇 다른 감정으로, 나는 그의 동업자인 그 무너진 신사의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"자, 동업자 양반,\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"외람되지만 말씀드리자면 — 자, 이번엔 코퍼필드에게 어울릴 만한 무언가로 한 잔 권해 주시지요!\"\n\n위크필드 씨가 내 고모님을 위하여, 딕 씨를 위하여, 민법 박사회를 위하여, 유라이아를 위하여 잔을 들자고 제안하며, 모든 것을 두 잔씩 마셔 댄 일이며, 자신의 나약함을 의식하면서도 그것에 맞서 헛되이 안간힘을 쓰던 일이며, 유라이아의 처신에 부끄러워하면서도 그를 달래고 싶어 하던 그 갈등이며, 유라이아가 그를 비틀고 뒤틀어 내 앞에서 조롱거리로 떠받들며 드러내던 그 노골적인 의기양양함은, 그냥 넘어가겠다. 그것을 지켜보는 것만으로도 가슴이 메스꺼웠고, 그것을 적으려니 손이 절로 움츠러든다.\n\n\"자, 동업자 양반!\" 마침내 유라이아가 말했다. \"한 잔 더 올리겠습니다. 그런데 이번엔 잔을 가득 채워 주시기를 황송하게 청합니다. 여인들 중에서도 가장 거룩한 분을 위해 들 작정이니까요.\"\n\n그녀의 아버지는 빈 잔을 손에 들고 있었다. 나는 그가 잔을 내려놓고, 그녀를 그토록 빼닮은 초상화를 바라보더니, 손을 이마에 갖다 대고 안락의자 깊숙이 움츠러드는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"제가 그분의 건강을 위해 잔을 들기엔 미천한 인간이지요.\" 유라이아가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 저는 그분을 흠모합니다 — 아니, 숭배합니다.\"\n\n그녀의 아버지의 그 희끗한 머리가 견딜 수 있을 어떤 육체의 고통도, 지금 그가 두 손에 짓눌러 담고 있던 그 마음의 인내보다 더 끔찍하지는 않았으리라.\n\n\"아그네스,\" 유라이아가, 그를 안중에 두지 않는 건지 아니면 자기 행동이 어떤 것인지 모르는 건지, 이렇게 말했다. \"아그네스 위크필드는, 단언컨대, 여인들 중에서도 가장 거룩한 분입니다. 친구들 사이니 터놓고 말해도 되겠지요? 그분의 아버지가 된다는 건 자랑스러운 영예이지만, 그분의 남편이 된다는 건—\"\n\n그녀의 아버지가 식탁에서 벌떡 일어서며 내지른 그 비명을, 다시는 듣지 않게 해 주소서! \"무슨 일이십니까?\" 유라이아가 핏기가 싹 가신 얼굴로 말했다. \"설마 결국 미쳐 버리신 건 아니겠죠, 위크필드 씨? 제가 댁의 아그네스를 제 아그네스로 만들고 싶은 야심이 있다고 한들, 저도 다른 사내만큼 그럴 권리가 있습니다. 아니, 다른 어느 사내보다 더 그럴 권리가 있다고요!\"\n\n나는 두 팔로 위크필드 씨를 끌어안고, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것을 다 들먹이며, 그중에서도 아그네스에 대한 그의 사랑을 가장 많이 들먹이며, 부디 마음을 조금만 가라앉히시라고 애원했다. 그는 한순간 제정신이 아니었다. 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고, 제 머리를 두드리고, 나를 떼어 내려 하고, 제 몸을 내게서 빼내려 하면서, 한마디도 대답하지 않고, 아무도 쳐다보거나 알아보지 못한 채, 자기도 무엇인지 모를 것을 향해 무작정 안간힘을 썼는데, 그 얼굴은 온통 부릅뜨고 일그러져 — 보기에도 끔찍한 광경이었다.\n\n나는 두서없이, 그러나 더없이 간절하게, 부디 이 광기에 자신을 내맡기지 마시고 내 말을 들어 달라고 그에게 빌었다. 아그네스를 생각하시라고, 나를 아그네스와 결부지어 생각하시라고, 아그네스와 내가 어떻게 함께 자라 왔는지, 내가 그녀를 얼마나 존경하고 사랑하는지, 그녀가 그분의 자랑이자 기쁨임을 떠올려 보시라고 애원했다. 나는 어떤 식으로든 그녀의 모습을 그의 앞에 떠올려 주려 애썼다. 심지어 이런 장면을 그녀가 알지 못하도록 막아 줄 만한 굳건함조차 없으시냐고 나무라기까지 했다. 내가 무언가를 해냈는지, 아니면 그의 광기가 제풀에 다한 것인지 모르겠으나, 차츰 그는 덜 몸부림치더니, 나를 바라보기 시작했다 — 처음엔 낯설게, 그러다 눈에 알아보는 빛을 띠고서. 마침내 그가 말했다. \"안다네, 트롯우드! 내 사랑하는 아이와 자네는 — 다 알아! 하지만 저자를 보게!\"\n\n그가 유라이아를 가리켰다. 그는 한쪽 구석에서 핼쑥하게 노려보고 있었는데, 분명 자기 계산이 한참 빗나가 허를 찔린 기색이 역력했다.\n\n\"내 고문자를 보게.\" 그가 말했다. \"저자 앞에서 나는 한 걸음 한 걸음, 이름과 평판을, 평화와 안식을, 집과 가정을 다 내놓았네.\"\n\n\"제가 댁의 이름과 평판을 지켜 드렸고, 댁의 평화와 안식도, 댁의 집과 가정도 지켜 드렸지요.\" 유라이아가 시무룩하고 다급하며 패배한 듯한 타협조로 말했다. \"어리석게 굴지 마세요, 위크필드 씨. 제가 댁이 마음의 준비를 한 것보다 조금 더 나갔다면, 도로 물릴 수도 있죠, 안 그래요? 해될 건 없잖습니까.\"\n\n\"나는 누구에게서나 단순한 동기 하나만을 찾으려 했네.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"그래서 나는 이익이라는 동기로 저자를 내게 묶어 두었다고 흡족해했지. 하지만 저자가 어떤 놈인지 보게나 — 오, 저자가 어떤 놈인지 좀 보라고!\"\n\n\"가능하면 저분 입 좀 막으시는 게 좋을 겁니다, 코퍼필드.\" 유라이아가 그 긴 집게손가락을 내 쪽으로 겨누며 외쳤다. \"저분이 곧 무슨 말을 할 텐데 — 명심하세요! — 나중에 가서 한 걸 후회할 말을요. 그러면 도련님도 그걸 들은 걸 후회하실 테고요!\"\n\n\"무슨 말이든 하고 말겠다!\" 위크필드 씨가 절망에 찬 표정으로 외쳤다. \"내가 자네 손아귀에 있는 마당에, 어찌 온 세상의 손아귀에 들어가지 않을 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"명심하세요! 말씀드립니다!\" 유라이아가 내게 계속 경고하며 말했다. \"저분 입을 막지 않으시면, 도련님은 저분의 친구가 아닙니다! 어찌 온 세상의 손아귀에 들어가지 않을 수 있겠느냐고요, 위크필드 씨? 댁에게 딸이 있기 때문이죠. 댁과 저는 우리가 아는 걸 알고 있죠, 안 그렇습니까? 잠자는 개는 그냥 누워 있게 두는 겁니다 — 누가 그놈을 깨우고 싶겠어요? 저는 아니에요. 제가 더없이 미천하게 굴고 있는 게 안 보이세요? 말씀드리지만, 제가 너무 멀리 나갔다면, 미안합니다. 더 무엇을 바라십니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"오, 트롯우드, 트롯우드!\" 위크필드 씨가 두 손을 쥐어짜며 외쳤다. \"내가 이 집에서 자네를 처음 본 뒤로, 이 지경까지 떨어지다니! 그때도 나는 내리막길에 들어서 있었지만, 그 뒤로 내가 걸어온 그 황량하고 황량한 길이라니! 나약한 탐닉이 나를 망쳤네. 추억에 빠지는 탐닉, 그리고 망각에 빠지는 탐닉 말일세. 내 아이의 어미를 향한 자연스러운 슬픔이 병이 되었고, 내 아이를 향한 자연스러운 사랑이 병이 되었네. 나는 내가 손대는 모든 것을 병들게 했어. 내가 끔찍이 사랑하는 것에 비참함을 안겨 주었지, 나도 알아 — 자네도 알지! 나는 세상에서 한 사람만을 진실로 사랑하면서 나머지는 사랑하지 않아도 된다고 여겼네. 세상을 떠난 한 사람만을 진실로 애도하면서, 애도하는 모든 이의 슬픔에 한몫 끼지 않아도 된다고 여겼지. 그렇게 내 인생의 가르침이 비뚤어지고 만 거야! 나는 병들고 비겁한 내 마음을 파먹었고, 그 마음이 나를 파먹었네. 슬픔에서도 비루하고, 사랑에서도 비루하고, 그 둘의 어두운 면에서 비참하게 달아나는 데서도 비루한 인간, 오, 내가 얼마나 폐허가 되었는지 보게나, 그리고 나를 미워하게, 나를 멀리하게!\"\n\n그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 힘없이 흐느꼈다. 그를 들끓게 했던 흥분이 가시고 있었다. 유라이아가 제 구석에서 걸어 나왔다.\n\n\"내가 어리석은 나머지 저지른 짓을 나는 다 알지 못하네.\" 위크필드 씨가, 마치 내 비난을 만류하려는 듯 두 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"저자가 가장 잘 알겠지.\" 유라이아 힙을 가리키는 말이었다. \"저자는 늘 내 곁에 붙어 내게 속삭여 왔으니까. 저자가 내 목에 매달린 맷돌이라는 게 보이지. 자네는 저자를 내 집에서 마주치고, 저자를 내 사업에서 마주치네. 자네도 방금 전에 저자가 하는 말을 들었지. 내가 더 무슨 말을 할 필요가 있겠나!\"\n\n\"그렇게 많이, 아니 그 절반도, 아니 아무 말도 하실 필요 없었어요.\" 유라이아가 반쯤은 대들고 반쯤은 알랑거리며 한마디 했다. \"포도주만 아니었으면 그렇게까지 받아들이진 않으셨을 거예요. 내일이면 다시 생각이 달라지실 겁니다, 나리. 제가 너무 많은 말을, 아니 본심보다 지나친 말을 했다 한들, 그게 어떻다는 겁니까? 저는 그 말을 고수하지도 않았는걸요!\"\n\n문이 열리고, 아그네스가 얼굴에 핏기 하나 없이 미끄러지듯 들어오더니, 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 차분하게 말했다. \"아빠, 몸이 안 좋으세요. 저랑 가요!\"\n\n그는 무거운 수치심에 짓눌린 듯 그녀의 어깨에 고개를 기댄 채, 그녀와 함께 나갔다. 그녀의 눈이 잠깐 내 눈과 마주쳤을 뿐인데, 나는 그녀가 방금 벌어진 일을 얼마나 많이 알고 있는지 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"그분이 그렇게 거칠게 나오실 줄은 몰랐네요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"하지만 아무것도 아니에요. 내일이면 그분과 화해할 겁니다. 다 그분을 위한 거예요. 저는 황송하게도 그분이 잘되기를 바라거든요.\"\n\n나는 그에게 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 위층의 그 고요한 방으로 올라갔다. 아그네스가 그토록 자주 내 곁에서 함께 책을 보던 그 방이었다. 밤이 깊도록 아무도 내게 다가오지 않았다. 나는 책을 집어 들고 읽어 보려 했다. 시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리가 들렸고, 그래도 나는 내가 무엇을 읽고 있는지도 모른 채 여전히 읽고 있었는데, 그때 아그네스가 나를 건드렸다.\n\n\"내일 아침 일찍 떠날 거잖아, 트롯우드! 지금 작별 인사를 해 두자!\"\n\n그녀는 울고 있었으나, 그때 그녀의 얼굴은 어찌나 차분하고 아름답던지!\n\n\"하느님의 축복이 함께하길!\" 그녀가 내게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"더없이 소중한 아그네스!\" 내가 받았다. \"오늘 밤 일은 입에 올리지 말라고 네가 청하는 걸 알아 — 하지만 정말 아무것도 할 게 없는 거야?\"\n\n\"믿고 의지할 하느님이 계셔!\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 아무것도 못 한단 말이야 — 이렇게 변변찮은 슬픔이나 안고 네게 오는 내가?\"\n\n\"그래서 내 슬픔을 그토록 가볍게 해 주잖아.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"사랑하는 트롯우드, 아무것도 하지 마!\"\n\n\"사랑하는 아그네스,\" 내가 말했다. \"네가 그토록 풍요롭게 지닌 모든 것에서 — 선함, 결단력, 온갖 고귀한 자질에서 — 그토록 빈곤한 내가 너를 의심하거나 이끌려 든다는 건 주제넘은 일이야. 하지만 너도 알지, 내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 또 네게 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지. 너는 잘못된 의무감 때문에 자신을 희생하는 일은 결코 없을 거지, 아그네스?\"\n\n내가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 한순간 동요하며, 그녀는 내게서 두 손을 빼고 한 걸음 물러섰다.\n\n\"그런 생각은 없다고 말해 줘, 사랑하는 아그네스! 누이보다 훨씬 더 소중한 너! 너의 그런 마음 같은, 너의 그런 사랑 같은 더없이 값진 선물을 생각해 봐!\"\n\n오! 오랜, 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤, 나는 그 얼굴이 내 앞에 떠오르는 것을 보았다. 그 찰나의 표정, 놀라지도, 탓하지도, 아쉬워하지도 않던 그 표정과 함께. 오, 오랜, 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤, 나는 그 표정이, 지금처럼, 그 사랑스러운 미소로 잦아드는 것을 보았다. 그 미소로 그녀는, 자신에 대해서는 아무 두려움이 없으니 — 나도 그녀를 두고 두려워할 필요가 없다고 말하고는, 오라버니라는 이름으로 나와 작별하고, 떠나갔다!\n\n아침에 여관 문 앞에서 마차에 올랐을 때, 날은 아직 어두웠다. 막 출발하려 할 무렵에야 동이 트기 시작했는데, 그때 그녀를 생각하며 앉아 있던 내게, 낮과 밤이 뒤섞인 어스름을 뚫고 유라이아의 머리가 마차 옆으로 비척비척 올라왔다.\n\n\"코퍼필드!\" 그가 지붕의 쇠막대에 매달린 채, 까마귀 울음 같은 목소리로 속삭이듯 말했다. \"떠나시기 전에 들으면 기뻐하실 것 같아서요. 우리 사이엔 깨진 게 하나도 없답니다. 저는 벌써 그분 방에 다녀왔고, 우리는 다 매끄럽게 풀었어요. 아, 저는 미천할지언정 그분에게 쓸모 있는 사람이거든요, 아시다시피. 그분은 술에 안 취했을 땐 자기 잇속을 잘 챙기시죠! 그분도 알고 보면 어찌나 호감 가는 분인지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n나는 그가 사과를 했다니 다행이라는 말을 억지로 내뱉었다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요!\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"사람이 미천하면, 아시다시피, 사과쯤이야 뭐가 대수겠어요? 그저 쉬운 일이죠! 그런데요! 짐작건대,\" 그가 몸을 한 번 까딱하며 말했다. \"도련님도 가끔은 배가 익기도 전에 따 본 적이 있으시겠죠, 코퍼필드 도련님?\"\n\n\"그런 적이 있겠지.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 어젯밤에 그걸 했어요.\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"하지만 그것도 곧 익을 겁니다! 그저 손길이 좀 필요할 뿐이죠. 저는 기다릴 수 있거든요!\"\n\n작별 인사를 잔뜩 늘어놓고 나서, 마부가 올라타자 그도 다시 내려갔다. 내가 아는 한, 그는 차가운 아침 공기를 막으려고 무언가를 씹고 있었는지도 모른다. 하지만 그는, 마치 그 배가 벌써 익기라도 한 듯, 또 그것을 맛보며 입맛을 다시기라도 하듯 입을 우물거렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER 40. THE WANDERER\n\n그날 밤 우리는 버킹엄 거리에서, 내가 지난 장에 자세히 적은 집안일들을 두고 아주 심각하게 이야기를 나눴다. 고모님은 그 일에 깊이 마음을 쓰셨고, 그 뒤로 두 시간 넘게 팔짱을 낀 채 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하셨다. 마음이 유난히 어지러울 때면 고모님은 늘 이런 보행 묘기를 한바탕 펼치셨고, 그 어지러움의 정도는 걸음을 얼마나 오래 잇느냐로 늘 가늠할 수 있었다. 이번에는 마음이 어찌나 심란하셨던지 침실 문까지 열어 두고, 침실 벽에서 벽까지 그 폭을 통째로 아우르는 경로를 스스로 마련하실 필요를 느끼셨다. 그리고 디크 씨와 내가 난롯가에 가만히 앉아 있는 동안, 고모님은 그 잰 듯한 길을 따라 들고 나기를 반복하셨는데, 그 걸음걸이가 시계추처럼 한결같이 규칙적이었다.\n\n디크 씨가 자러 들어가 고모님과 나만 남게 되자, 나는 두 노부인에게 보낼 편지를 쓰려고 자리에 앉았다. 그 무렵 고모님은 걷는 데 지쳐, 늘 그러시듯 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고 난롯가에 앉아 계셨다. 그러나 평소처럼 잔을 무릎에 올려 든 채 앉아 계신 게 아니라, 잔을 벽난로 선반에 그냥 내버려 둔 채로, 왼쪽 팔꿈치를 오른팔에 괴고 턱을 왼손에 받친 채, 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 나를 바라보셨다. 하던 일에서 눈을 들 때마다 나는 고모님의 눈길과 마주쳤다. \"난 더없이 다정한 마음인 거야, 얘야.\" 고모님은 고개를 끄덕이며 나를 안심시키곤 하셨다. \"다만 안절부절못하고 마음이 아플 뿐이지!\"\n\n나는 너무 바빠서, 고모님이 잠자리에 드시고 나서야 비로소 그분이 늘 '밤 약물'이라고 부르시던 그것을 벽난로 선반에 입도 안 댄 채 그냥 두고 가신 것을 알아챘다. 이 발견을 알리려고 내가 문을 두드리자, 고모님은 평소보다 한층 더 다정한 태도로 문 앞에 나오셨다. 그러나 \"오늘 밤은 그걸 마실 마음이 없구나, 트롯.\" 이렇게만 말씀하시고는, 고개를 가로젓더니 다시 안으로 들어가셨다.\n\n아침에 고모님은 두 노부인에게 보낼 내 편지를 읽고는 흡족해하셨다. 나는 편지를 부쳤고, 그러고 나서는 답장을 되도록 참을성 있게 기다리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다. 나는 여전히 이렇게 기다리는 상태였고, 그러기를 거의 일주일째였는데, 어느 눈 오는 밤 박사님 댁을 나와 집으로 걸어가던 참이었다.\n\n매섭게 추운 날이었고, 살을 에는 북동풍이 한동안 불어 댔다. 해가 지면서 바람이 잦아들자, 눈이 내리기 시작했다. 큼직큼직한 송이로, 무겁게 차곡차곡 쌓이는 눈이었다고 기억한다. 눈은 두텁게 깔렸다. 바퀴 구르는 소리며 사람들 발걸음 소리가, 마치 거리에 그 두께만큼 깃털을 뿌려 놓기라도 한 듯 잦아들었다.\n\n집으로 가는 가장 빠른 길은 — 그런 밤에 나는 당연히 가장 빠른 길을 택했다 — 세인트 마틴스 레인을 가로지르는 길이었다. 그런데 그 골목길에 이름을 준 교회는 그 무렵 지금보다 트인 곳에 서 있지 못했다. 앞에 빈터가 없었고, 골목은 스트랜드 거리로 굽이쳐 내려갔다. 현관 기둥의 계단을 지나칠 때, 나는 모퉁이에서 어느 여인의 얼굴과 마주쳤다. 그 얼굴은 내 얼굴을 들여다보고, 좁은 골목을 가로질러, 사라졌다. 나는 그 얼굴을 알았다. 어디선가 본 적이 있었다. 하지만 어디서였는지 떠오르지 않았다. 그 얼굴에는 곧장 내 가슴을 친 어떤 연상이 얽혀 있었다. 그러나 그 얼굴이 불쑥 나타났을 때 나는 전혀 다른 생각에 잠겨 있던 터라, 정신이 어지러웠다.\n\n교회 계단에는 한 남자가 몸을 구부린 채 서 있었는데, 매끈한 눈 위에 무슨 짐을 내려놓고 그것을 고쳐 매던 참이었다. 내가 그 여인의 얼굴을 본 것과 그 남자를 본 것은 동시였다. 놀란 나머지 발걸음을 멈추지는 않았던 것 같다. 하지만 어쨌든 내가 계속 걸어가는 사이에 그 남자가 몸을 일으켜 돌아서더니 내 쪽으로 내려왔다. 나는 패거티 씨와 얼굴을 마주하고 섰다!\n\n그제야 나는 그 여인을 떠올렸다. 마사였다. 그날 밤 부엌에서 에밀리가 돈을 건넸던 바로 그 여인. 마사 엔델 — 햄이 나에게 말하기를, 바다에 가라앉은 온갖 보물을 다 준다 해도 패거티 씨는 사랑하는 조카가 그 여인과 나란히 있는 꼴을 보려 하지 않았을 거라던, 바로 그 여인이었다.\n\n우리는 진심으로 악수를 나눴다. 처음에는 둘 다 한마디도 꺼내지 못했다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님!\" 그가 나를 꽉 붙잡으며 말했다. \"도련님을 뵈니 가슴이 다 따뜻해집니다요, 나리. 반갑습니다요, 반가워요!\"\n\n\"반가워요, 오래된 벗이여!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤에 도련님 안부나 여쭤 볼까 하던 참이었습니다요, 나리.\" 그가 말했다. \"헌데 고모님이 도련님하고 같이 사신다는 걸 알고 보니 — 제가 그 아래쪽에 다녀왔거든요, 야머스 쪽에요 — 너무 늦은 시각이지 싶어 겁이 났습죠. 떠나기 전에 아침 일찍 찾아뵐 작정이었습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"또 떠나신다고요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 그가 참을성 있게 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다. \"내일 떠납니다요.\"\n\n\"이번에는 어디로 가시던 길이었나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요!\" 그가 긴 머리카락에서 눈을 털어 내며 대답했다. \"어디든 묵을 데를 찾아 들어가던 참이었습죠.\"\n\n그 시절에는 우리가 서 있던 곳 바로 맞은편에, 골든 크로스의 마구간 마당으로 통하는 옆문이 하나 있었다. 그의 불운과 얽혀 나에게 잊지 못할 그 여인숙 말이다. 나는 그 문간을 가리키고는, 그의 팔에 내 팔을 끼고 함께 길을 건넜다. 마구간 마당 쪽으로는 두세 개의 손님방이 열려 있었다. 그중 한 방을 들여다보니 비어 있고 난롯불이 잘 타고 있어서, 나는 그를 그리로 데리고 들어갔다.\n\n불빛 아래에서 그를 보니, 머리카락이 길고 부스스할 뿐 아니라, 얼굴이 햇볕에 거뭇하게 그을린 게 눈에 띄었다. 머리는 더 세었고, 얼굴과 이마의 주름은 더 깊어졌으며, 온갖 날씨를 헤치며 고생하고 떠돌아다닌 티가 역력했다. 하지만 그는 무척 강건해 보였고, 어떤 것에도 지치지 않는, 굳은 결심에 떠받쳐진 사람 같았다. 내가 속으로 이런 생각을 하는 동안, 그는 모자와 옷에서 눈을 털고, 얼굴에서도 눈을 쓸어 냈다. 우리가 들어온 그 문을 등진 채 탁자를 사이에 두고 내 맞은편에 앉으면서, 그는 다시 거친 손을 내밀어 내 손을 따뜻하게 움켜잡았다.\n\n\"말씀드리겠습니다요, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 다녀온 데를 죄다, 그리고 우리가 들은 얘기를 죄다요. 멀리도 다녔지만, 들은 건 별로 없습니다요. 그래도 다 말씀드리겠습니다요!\"\n\n나는 따뜻한 마실 것을 시키려고 종을 울렸다. 그는 에일보다 독한 것은 마다했다. 그것이 나오고 난롯불에 데워지는 동안, 그는 가만히 앉아 생각에 잠겼다. 그의 얼굴에는 곱고도 묵직한 진중함이 어려 있어, 나는 감히 그 침묵을 깨뜨리지 못했다.\n\n\"그 애가 어릴 적에는요.\" 우리만 남게 된 지 얼마 안 되어 그가 고개를 들며 말했다. \"저한테 바다 얘기를 참 많이 했습니다요. 바다가 짙푸르게 변하고, 햇살 아래 반짝반짝 빛나는 그런 바닷가 얘기를요. 가끔은요, 그 애 아비가 물에 빠져 죽어서 그 애가 그걸 그렇게 골똘히 생각하나 싶었습죠. 모르긴 해도요, 어쩌면 그 애는 믿었던가 — 아니면 바랐던가 봐요 — 아비가 꽃이 늘 흐드러지게 피고 온 땅이 환한 그런 데로 떠밀려 갔다고요.\"\n\n\"어린애다운 공상이었겠지요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"그 애가 — 사라졌을 때요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"저는 속으로 알았습니다요, 그놈이 그 애를 그런 나라들로 데려가리란 걸요. 속으로 알았습죠, 그놈이 그 나라들의 신기한 얘기를 그 애한테 들려주었으리란 걸요. 거기서 그 애가 귀부인이 될 거라는 둥, 그런 식으로 그 애를 꾀어 처음에 제 말에 귀 기울이게 했으리란 걸요. 그놈 어미를 만났을 때, 제가 옳았다는 걸 똑똑히 알았습니다요. 저는 해협을 건너 프랑스로 가서, 하늘에서 뚝 떨어지기라도 한 양 그 땅에 내렸습죠.\"\n\n문이 움직이고 눈발이 안으로 휘몰려 드는 것이 보였다. 문이 조금 더 움직이더니, 어떤 손 하나가 살그머니 끼어들어 문을 열어 두는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"권세 있는 자리에 있는 영국 신사 한 분을 알아냈습죠.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그분께 제 조카를 찾으러 간다고 말씀드렸습니다요. 그분이 제가 길을 다니는 데 필요한 서류들을 마련해 주셨습죠 — 그게 뭐라고 부르는 건지는 제대로 모릅니다만 — 돈도 주려 하셨지만, 다행히 그건 필요가 없어 고맙게 사양했습니다요. 그분이 베풀어 주신 모든 게 정말로 고맙습죠! '내가 자네보다 먼저 편지를 써 두겠네.' 그분이 저한테 말씀하셨습니다요. '그리고 그쪽으로 가는 많은 이들한테 일러둘 테니, 자네가 혼자 멀리 떠돌더라도 여기서 한참 떨어진 곳에서도 많은 사람이 자네를 알아볼 걸세.' 저는 할 수 있는 한 제 고마움을 다 말씀드리고, 프랑스를 가로질러 떠났습니다요.\"\n\n\"혼자서, 걸어서요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"거의 걸어서요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이따금은 장에 가는 사람들 짐수레에 얹혀서요. 가끔은 빈 마차를 얻어 타고요. 하루에도 몇 마일씩 걷는데, 흔히는 친구들을 만나러 가는 어느 가엾은 병사 하나하고 같이였습죠. 저는 그 사람한테 말을 붙일 수도 없었고요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그 사람도 저한테 그랬습죠. 하지만 그래도 우린 그 흙먼지 길에서 서로한테 길동무가 돼 주었습니다요.\"\n\n그의 다정한 말투만으로도 나는 그랬으리란 걸 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"어느 마을이든 닿으면요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"여인숙을 찾아서, 영어를 아는 누군가가 나타날 때까지(거의 누군가는 나타났습죠) 마당에서 기다렸습니다요. 그러면 제가 조카를 찾으러 가는 길이라고 얘기했고, 그러면 그 사람들이 그 집에 어떤 양반네가 묵고 있는지 일러 주었습죠. 저는 그 애 비슷한 사람이 드나드는지 보려고 기다렸습니다요. 에밀리가 아니면 저는 다시 길을 떠났습죠. 차츰차츰, 새 마을이나 그런 데에 닿으면, 가난한 사람들 사이에서 그 사람들이 제 얘기를 안다는 걸 알게 됐습니다요. 그 사람들은 저를 자기네 오두막 문간에 앉히고, 먹고 마실 것을 이것저것 내주고, 잘 데를 일러 주었습죠. 그리고 데이비 도련님, 에밀리 또래 딸을 둔 여인네들 중에는요, 마을 어귀 구세주 십자가 곁에서 저를 기다리고 있다가 저한테 그런 친절을 베풀어 준 이가 한둘이 아니었습니다요. 더러는 그 딸이 죽고 없는 이도 있었습죠. 그 어미들이 저한테 얼마나 잘해 줬는지는 하느님만 아실 겁니다요!\"\n\n문간에 선 것은 마사였다. 나는 그 초췌하게 귀 기울이는 얼굴을 또렷이 보았다. 내가 두려웠던 것은, 그가 고개를 돌려 그 여인까지 보게 되는 일이었다.\n\n\"그 사람들은 자기네 아이들을 — 특히 어린 딸아이들을요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"곧잘 제 무릎에 앉혔습니다요. 밤이 내려앉을 무렵 그 집 문간에 앉아 있는 절 도련님도 여러 번 보셨을지도 모릅죠. 꼭 그 아이들이 제 사랑하는 그 애의 자식들이기라도 한 것처럼요. 오, 내 사랑하는 그 애!\"\n\n갑작스러운 슬픔에 짓눌려 그는 큰 소리로 흐느꼈다. 나는 그가 얼굴 앞에 가져다 댄 그 손 위에 떨리는 내 손을 얹었다. \"고맙습니다요, 나리.\" 그가 말했다. \"신경 쓰지 마십쇼.\"\n\n이윽고 그는 손을 떼어 가슴에 갖다 대더니, 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다. \"그 사람들은 곧잘 저랑 같이 걸어 주었습니다요.\" 그가 말했다. \"아침이면, 한두 마일쯤 제 길을 같이 가 주었습죠. 그리고 헤어질 때 제가 '정말로 고맙습니다! 하느님의 축복이 있기를!' 하면, 그 사람들은 늘 알아듣는 듯이 정답게 대꾸해 주었습니다요. 마침내 저는 바다에 닿았습죠. 저처럼 뱃일하던 사람한테야, 이탈리아까지 일을 거들며 건너가는 게 어렵지 않았다는 건 짐작하실 겝니다요. 거기 닿아서는 전에 하던 대로 떠돌았습죠. 사람들은 한결같이 저한테 잘해 줬고요. 저는 이 마을 저 마을, 어쩌면 그 나라를 통째로 누비고 다녔을 겝니다요. 헌데 그 애가 저 스위스 산속에서 목격됐다는 소식을 들었습죠. 그놈 하인을 아는 누군가가 거기서 그 셋을 다 봤다면서, 그들이 어떻게 다니는지, 어디 있는지를 일러 주었습니다요. 저는 밤낮으로 그 산들을 향해 갔습죠, 데이비 도련님. 제가 가면 갈수록, 그 산들은 자꾸만 저한테서 멀어지는 것 같았습니다요. 그래도 저는 따라잡았고, 그 산들을 넘었습죠. 듣고 온 그 곳 가까이에 이르자, 저는 속으로 이런 생각이 들기 시작했습니다요. '그 애를 보면 난 어떡하지?'\"\n\n귀 기울이는 그 얼굴은 매서운 밤에도 아랑곳없이 여전히 문간에 드리워 있었고, 두 손은 나에게 그 얼굴을 내쫓지 말아 달라고 빌고 — 또 빌고 — 있었다.\n\n\"저는 한 번도 그 애를 의심하지 않았습니다요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"아뇨! 눈곱만큼도요! 그저 그 애가 제 얼굴을 보기만 한다면 — 그저 그 애가 제 목소리를 듣기만 한다면 — 그저 제가 그 애 앞에 가만히 서 있는 것만으로 그 애가 도망쳐 나온 집과 자기가 한때 어떤 아이였는지를 떠올리기만 한다면요 — 설령 그 애가 왕녀가 다 됐다 한들, 제 발 앞에 엎어졌을 겝니다요! 저는 그걸 잘 알았습죠! 꿈속에서 저는 그 애가 '삼촌!' 하고 외치는 소리를 여러 번 들었고, 그 애가 제 앞에서 죽은 듯이 쓰러지는 걸 봤습니다요. 꿈속에서 저는 여러 번 그 애를 일으켜 세우고, '에밀리야, 얘야, 내가 용서를 가지고 왔단다, 너를 집으로 데려가려고 왔어!' 하고 속삭였습죠.\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추고 고개를 가로젓더니, 한숨을 쉬며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이제 그놈은 저한테 아무것도 아니었습니다요. 에밀리가 전부였습죠. 저는 그 애한테 입힐 시골 옷을 한 벌 샀습니다요. 그리고 알았습죠, 일단 찾기만 하면 그 애는 제가 어디로 가든 그 돌투성이 길을 제 곁에서 함께 걸을 거고, 두 번 다시는, 두 번 다시는 저를 떠나지 않으리란 걸요. 그 애한테 그 옷을 입히고, 그 애가 걸친 것을 벗겨 던지고 — 그 애를 다시 제 팔에 끼고 집을 향해 걷고 — 이따금 길에서 멈춰서, 그 애의 멍든 발과, 그보다 더 멍든 마음을 어루만져 낫게 하고 — 이제 제가 생각하는 건 그게 다였습니다요. 그놈은 쳐다보지도 않았을 겝니다요. 헌데, 데이비 도련님, 그게 이뤄질 일이 아니었습죠 — 아직은요! 제가 너무 늦었고, 그들은 떠나 버렸습니다요. 어디로 갔는지는 알 수가 없었습죠. 누구는 여기라 하고, 누구는 저기라 했습니다요. 저는 여기로도 가 보고, 저기로도 가 봤지만, 에밀리를 찾지 못한 채 집으로 돌아왔습죠.\"\n\n\"얼마 전이었나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"한 나흘쯤 됐습니다요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"날이 어두워진 뒤에 그 낡은 배가 눈에 들어왔고, 창에 불빛이 비치고 있었습죠. 가까이 가서 유리창 너머로 들여다보니, 그 충직한 거미지 아주머니가 우리가 정해 둔 대로 난롯가에 혼자 앉아 있는 게 보였습니다요. 제가 '겁먹지 마세요! 댄이에요!' 하고 외치고는 안으로 들어갔습죠. 그 낡은 배가 그토록 낯설게 느껴질 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐습니다요!\" 그는 가슴팍 어느 주머니에서 아주 조심스러운 손길로 작은 종이 꾸러미 하나를 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 편지인지 작은 봉지인지가 두세 개 들어 있었다. 그는 그것을 탁자 위에 올려놓았다.\n\n\"이 첫 번째 게 왔습니다요.\" 그가 그중 하나를 골라내며 말했다. \"제가 떠난 지 일주일도 안 돼서요. 종이 한 장에 싼 오십 파운드짜리 은행권인데, 제 앞으로 부쳐진 채 밤중에 문 밑으로 밀어 넣어져 있었습죠. 그 애가 글씨를 감추려고 했지만, 저한테는 못 감췄습니다요!\"\n\n그는 그 지폐를 더없이 참을성 있고 조심스럽게, 처음과 똑같은 모양으로 다시 접어, 한쪽에 내려놓았다.\n\n\"이건 거미지 아주머니한테 왔습니다요.\" 그가 또 하나를 펼치며 말했다. \"두세 달 전쯤이었습죠.\" 잠시 그것을 들여다보더니, 그는 그것을 나에게 건네며 나직한 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"수고스러우시겠지만 읽어 주십쇼, 나리.\"\n\n나는 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"오, 이 글씨를 보고, 이게 제 못된 손에서 나왔다는 걸 알면 아주머니는 어떤 심정이실까요! 하지만 부디, 부디 — 저를 봐서가 아니라 삼촌의 어지심을 봐서, 아주 짧디짧은 동안만이라도 저한테 마음을 누그러뜨려 주세요! 부디, 제발, 이 비참한 계집애한테 마음을 풀어 주시고, 종잇조각에 적어 주세요. 삼촌이 안녕하신지, 그리고 아주머니들끼리 제 이름을 입에 올리기를 그만두기 전에 삼촌이 저에 대해 뭐라고 하셨는지를요. 그리고 밤에, 제가 옛날에 집으로 돌아오던 그 시각이면, 삼촌이 한때 그토록 애틋하게 사랑하던 사람을 떠올리는 듯한 표정을 지으시는 걸 본 적이 있는지를요. 오, 그걸 생각하면 제 가슴이 미어져요! 저는 아주머니께 무릎 꿇고, 제가 받아 마땅한 만큼 — 정말로, 정말로 받아 마땅하다는 걸 잘 알아요 — 그만큼 저한테 모질게 굴지는 말아 주십사 빌고 또 빌어요. 부디 그토록 다정하고 그토록 어지셔서, 삼촌에 대해 뭐든 좀 적어 보내 주세요. 저를 '꼬마'라고 부르지 않으셔도 돼요. 제가 더럽힌 그 이름으로 부르지 않으셔도 돼요. 하지만 오, 제 고통에 귀 기울이시고, 삼촌에 대해 한마디라도 적어 주실 만큼만이라도 저한테 자비를 베풀어 주세요. 이 세상에서는 제 눈으로 두 번 다시, 두 번 다시 뵐 수 없는 그 삼촌에 대해서요!\n\n사랑하는 아주머니, 만약 아주머니 마음이 저한테 모질다면 — 마땅히 모질겠지요, 알아요 — 하지만, 들어 주세요, 만약 마음이 모질다면, 아주머니, 제가 가장 크게 잘못한 그 사람한테 — 제가 그 아내가 될 뻔했던 그 사람한테 물어봐 주세요. 제 가엾고 가엾은 이 청을 아주 매정하게 물리쳐 버리기 전에요! 만약 그가 너무도 인정이 많아, 제가 읽을 수 있게 뭔가 적어 줘도 좋다고 한다면 — 그럴 거예요, 오, 그럴 거예요, 아주머니가 그저 물어봐 주기만 한다면요, 그는 늘 그토록 용감하고 그토록 잘 용서하는 사람이었으니까요 — 그러면 그에게 전해 주세요(다만 그가 그러마 할 때만요). 밤에 바람이 부는 소리를 들으면, 그게 마치 그와 삼촌을 보고 성이 나서 휙 스쳐 가는 것 같고, 저를 고발하러 하느님께 올라가는 것 같다고요. 그에게 전해 주세요. 만약 제가 내일 죽는다면(오, 제가 죽을 자격이 있다면, 저는 기꺼이 죽을 텐데요!) 마지막 말로 그와 삼촌을 축복하고, 마지막 숨으로 그의 행복한 가정을 위해 기도하겠다고요!\"\n\n이 편지에도 돈이 동봉되어 있었다. 오 파운드였다. 그것 역시 앞서의 돈처럼 손도 안 댄 채였고, 그는 그것을 같은 모양으로 다시 접었다. 답장 주소에 관한 상세한 지시도 덧붙어 있었는데, 그 지시는 여러 사람의 손을 거쳤음을 드러내, 그 애가 숨어 있는 곳에 관해 어떤 그럴듯한 결론에 이르기는 어렵게 만들었지만, 적어도 그 애가 목격됐다고 전해진 바로 그곳에서 편지를 썼을 가능성이 적지 않음을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"답장은 어떻게 보냈나요?\" 나는 패거티 씨에게 물었다.\n\n\"거미지 아주머니가요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"글재주가 변변치 못한지라, 나리, 햄이 친절하게 초안을 잡아 주고, 아주머니가 그걸 베껴 썼습죠. 제가 그 애를 찾으러 떠났다는 것과, 제 작별 인사가 뭐였는지를 그 애한테 일러 주었습니다요.\"\n\n\"손에 든 그것도 또 편지인가요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"돈입니다요, 나리.\" 패거티 씨가 그것을 조금 펼쳐 보이며 말했다. \"보십쇼, 십 파운드입니다요. 그리고 안쪽에 '한 진정한 벗으로부터'라고 적혀 있습죠, 첫 번째 것처럼요. 하지만 첫 번째 건 문 밑으로 넣어졌고, 이건 우편으로 왔습니다요, 그제요. 저는 이 소인을 찍은 데로 그 애를 찾으러 갈 작정입니다요.\"\n\n그가 그것을 나에게 보여 주었다. 어퍼 라인의 한 도시였다. 그는 야머스에서, 그 지방을 아는 외국인 상인 몇몇을 알아냈고, 그들이 종이에 그를 위해 투박한 지도를 그려 주었는데, 그는 그것을 아주 잘 이해할 수 있었다. 그는 그것을 우리 사이의 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓고는, 한 손으로 턱을 괴고 다른 손으로 자기가 갈 길을 짚어 갔다.\n\n나는 햄이 어떻게 지내는지 물었다. 그는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"그놈은 일합니다요.\" 그가 말했다. \"사내대장부가 할 수 있는 만큼 씩씩하게요. 그 일대에서 그놈 이름은 세상 어디 누구 이름만큼이나 좋습죠. 누구든 그놈을 거들려고 선뜻 손을 내밀고, 그놈도 그들을 거들려고 선뜻 손을 내밉니다요, 아시겠습니까. 그놈이 불평하는 건 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없습죠. 하지만 제 누이 생각으로는(우리끼리 얘깁니다만), 그게 그놈을 속 깊이 후벼 팠다는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 친구로군요, 그럴 만도 합니다!\"\n\n\"그놈은 통 자기 앞가림을 안 합니다요, 데이비 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 엄숙하게 속삭이며 말했다. \"어쩐지 제 목숨 따위는 도무지 아랑곳을 안 해요. 험한 날씨에 험한 일에 누가 필요하다 하면, 그놈이 거기 있습죠. 위험이 따르는 고된 임무가 있으면, 그놈이 동료들을 다 제치고 앞장섭니다요. 그러면서도 그놈은 어린애처럼 순합죠. 야머스에서 그놈을 모르는 아이가 하나도 없습니다요.\"\n\n그는 생각에 잠긴 채 편지들을 그러모아, 손으로 매만져 폈다. 그것들을 작은 꾸러미로 묶어, 다시 가슴에 고이 간직했다. 문간에서 그 얼굴은 사라지고 없었다. 나는 여전히 눈발이 안으로 휘몰려 드는 것은 보았으나, 그 밖에는 거기 아무것도 없었다.\n\n\"자!\" 그가 자기 짐 보따리를 바라보며 말했다. \"오늘 밤 도련님을 뵈었으니(그게 저한테 큰 위안이 됩니다요!), 저는 내일 아침 일찌감치 떠나겠습니다요. 제가 여기 가진 게 뭔지 도련님도 보셨습죠.\" 그는 그 작은 꾸러미가 놓인 가슴팍에 손을 얹었다. \"저를 괴롭히는 건 단 하나, 저 돈을 돌려주기도 전에 저한테 무슨 일이 닥칠까 하는 생각뿐입니다요. 만약 제가 죽고, 저게 없어지거나, 도둑맞거나, 다른 식으로 사라져 버려서, 제가 그걸 가로챘다고밖에는 그놈이 알 길이 없게 된다면, 저는 저세상에서도 가만히 있지 못할 겝니다요! 분명 다시 돌아오고 말 겝니다요!\"\n\n그가 일어섰고, 나도 일어섰다. 우리는 밖으로 나가기 전에 다시 한번 서로 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"일만 마일이라도 가겠습니다요.\" 그가 말했다. \"쓰러져 죽을 때까지라도 가서, 저 돈을 그놈 앞에 내려놓겠습니다요. 그러고 나서 제 에밀리를 찾는다면, 저는 만족합죠. 만약 못 찾더라도, 어쩌면 그 애가 언젠가는 듣게 되겠지요. 그 애를 사랑하는 삼촌이 제 목숨을 다하는 그 순간에야 그 애 찾는 일을 그만뒀다는 걸요. 그리고 제가 그 애를 안다면, 바로 그것이 끝내 그 애를 집으로 돌아오게 만들 겝니다요!\"\n\n그가 매서운 밤 속으로 나서자, 나는 그 외로운 형체가 우리 앞으로 휙 스쳐 가는 것을 보았다. 나는 무슨 핑계를 대어 황급히 그를 돌려세우고는, 그 형체가 사라질 때까지 그를 붙들고 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n그는 도버 가도에 있는, 그날 밤 깨끗하고 수수한 잠자리를 분명히 구할 수 있다는 어느 나그네 숙소 이야기를 했다. 나는 그와 함께 웨스트민스터 다리를 건너, 서리 강가에서 그와 헤어졌다. 그가 눈을 헤치며 외로운 여정을 다시 시작하는 모습에, 내 머릿속에는 온 세상이 그에게 경의를 표하느라 숨죽이는 것만 같았다.\n\n나는 여인숙 마당으로 돌아와, 그 얼굴의 기억에 사로잡힌 채 두려운 마음으로 사방을 둘러보며 그것을 찾았다. 거기에 없었다. 눈이 우리가 조금 전 남긴 발자국을 덮어 버려, 새로 찍힌 내 발자국만이 보였다. 그리고 어깨 너머로 돌아보는 사이에, 그것마저도 (눈이 어찌나 세차게 내렸던지) 차츰 지워지기 시작했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 41. DORA’S AUNTS\n\n마침내 두 노부인에게서 답장이 왔다. 두 분은 코퍼필드 씨에게 정중한 인사를 전하면서, 그의 편지를 '양쪽 모두의 행복을 위하여' 가장 신중하게 검토했다고 알려 왔다. 이 표현이 나는 어쩐지 섬뜩하게 느껴졌는데, 앞서 말한 집안 불화와 관련해서 두 분이 이 말을 써먹은 적이 있기 때문이기도 하지만, 내가 (그리고 평생토록) 지켜본 바로는 이런 상투적인 문구란 일종의 불꽃놀이 같아서, 쉽게 쏘아 올릴 수 있고, 본래의 모양에서는 전혀 짐작할 수 없는 온갖 형태와 빛깔로 변하기 마련이기 때문이다. 스펜로 자매분은 덧붙이기를, 코퍼필드 씨의 편지에 담긴 사안에 대해 '편지라는 수단을 통해서는' 의견을 밝히기를 삼가고 싶으나, 코퍼필드 씨가 어느 정해진 날에 (그가 적당하다고 여긴다면 믿을 만한 벗을 한 사람 데리고) 방문하는 호의를 베풀어 준다면, 그 사안을 두고 기꺼이 이야기를 나누겠다고 했다.\n\n이 호의에 코퍼필드 씨는 곧장 정중한 인사와 함께 답장을 보내어, 지정된 시각에 스펜로 자매분을 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠으며, 두 분의 친절한 허락에 따라 이너 템플의 토머스 트래들스 씨를 벗으로 데리고 가겠노라고 했다. 이 서신을 보내고 나서 코퍼필드 씨는 극심한 신경과민과 불안 상태에 빠졌고, 약속한 날이 올 때까지 그대로였다.\n\n이 중대한 고비에서, 밀스 양의 그 값을 매길 수 없는 도움을 빼앗긴 것은 내 불안을 한층 더 키웠다. 그런데 늘 이런저런 일로 나를 성가시게 굴던 밀스 씨가 — 아니, 적어도 나는 그가 그렇게 군다고 느꼈는데, 그게 그거였다 — 인도로 가겠다는 생각을 머리에 박아 넣음으로써 그 행태를 절정에 이르게 했다. 나를 괴롭히려는 것이 아니라면 도대체 왜 인도로 간단 말인가? 하기야 그가 세상의 다른 지역과는 아무런 인연이 없고 오직 그 지역과만 큰 인연이 있긴 했다. 그게 무엇이든 간에 (나 스스로도 황금빛 숄과 코끼리 어금니에 관한 어렴풋한 공상을 품곤 했다) 그는 온전히 인도 무역에 종사하고 있었으며, 젊은 시절 캘커타에 가 본 적이 있고, 이제 다시 그곳으로 나가 주재 동업자 자격으로 일할 작정이었다. 하지만 그것은 나와는 아무 상관이 없었다. 그러나 그에게는 어찌나 큰일이었던지 인도로 떠날 결심을 굳혔고, 줄리아도 함께 떠나기로 했다. 그래서 줄리아는 친척들에게 작별 인사를 하러 시골로 내려갔고, 집은 세를 놓거나 판다는, 그리고 가구는 (탈수기까지 죄다) 감정가에 넘긴다는 광고로 온통 도배되었다. 그러니 여기 또 하나의 지진이 닥쳐, 앞선 충격에서 미처 회복하기도 전에 나는 그 노리개 신세가 되고 만 것이다!\n\n그 중요한 날 어떻게 차려입어야 할지 나는 마음을 여러 갈래로 정하지 못했다. 한껏 돋보이고 싶은 욕심과, 스펜로 자매분의 눈에 내 엄정하고 실속 있는 됨됨이를 깎아내릴 만한 무언가를 걸치지나 않을까 하는 걱정 사이에서 갈팡질팡했던 것이다. 나는 이 두 극단 사이에서 적절한 중간을 맞춰 보려고 애썼다. 고모님은 그 결과를 마음에 들어 했고, 딕 씨는 트래들스와 내가 아래층으로 내려갈 때 행운을 빈다며 자기 신발 한 짝을 우리 뒤로 던졌다.\n\n트래들스가 더없이 훌륭한 친구임을 잘 알고, 그에게 따뜻한 애정을 품고 있으면서도, 그 미묘한 자리에서만큼은 그가 머리카락을 그토록 빳빳하게 세워 빗어 올리는 버릇을 결코 들이지 않았더라면 좋았겠다는 마음을 떨칠 수가 없었다. 그 머리는 그를 깜짝 놀란 사람처럼 — 아니, 벽난로 빗자루 같은 표정이라고 해도 좋을 만큼 — 보이게 만들었는데, 그것이 우리에게 치명적일지도 모른다고 내 불안이 속삭였다.\n\n퍼트니로 걸어가는 동안, 나는 외람되게 그 점을 트래들스에게 꺼내며, 머리카락을 조금만 가라앉혀 준다면 좋겠다고 말했다 —\n\n\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 모자를 벗고 머리카락을 온갖 방향으로 문지르며 말했다. \"그렇게만 된다면 나도 더없이 기쁘겠네. 하지만 안 돼.\"\n\n\"가라앉지를 않는다고?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"무슨 수를 써도 안 가라앉아. 퍼트니까지 내내 그 위에 오십 파운드짜리 추를 얹고 간다 해도, 추를 떼는 순간 도로 곤두서고 말 거야. 자네는 내 머리가 얼마나 고집통인지 모를 걸세, 코퍼필드. 나는 완전히 안달복달하는 고슴도치라니까.\"\n\n솔직히 좀 실망스럽긴 했지만, 그의 사람 좋은 천성에 또 흠뻑 반하고 말았다. 나는 그 좋은 천성을 얼마나 높이 사는지 말해 주었고, 그의 머리카락이 그의 성격에서 고집이란 고집은 죄다 빼내 가 버린 모양이라고, 그에게는 고집이라곤 한 톨도 없으니 말이다, 하고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오!\" 트래들스가 웃으며 받았다. \"정말이지 내 불행한 머리카락은 아주 해묵은 이야기라네. 우리 큰아버지 부인은 이 머리를 참아 내질 못했어. 자길 약 올린다나. 처음 소피에게 반했을 때도 이게 어찌나 걸림돌이 됐던지. 아주 단단히 말일세!\"\n\n\"그 아가씨가 싫어했나?\"\n\n\"그녀는 아니었어.\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"다만 그녀 맏언니가 — 미인이라는 그 언니 말이야 — 이걸 완전히 놀림감으로 삼았던 모양이야. 사실 자매들이 죄다 이걸 보고 웃거든.\"\n\n\"거참 유쾌하구먼!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지.\" 트래들스가 더없이 천진하게 받았다. \"우리한테는 웃음거리지. 자기들끼리 짓궂게 굴기를, 소피가 책상 속에 내 머리카락 한 타래를 넣어 두고, 그게 곤두서지 못하도록 잠금쇠 달린 책에 끼워 두어야 한다고 한다네. 우린 그걸로 웃고 그래.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 이보게 트래들스,\" 내가 말했다. \"자네 경험에서 내가 뭔가 얻을 게 있을지도 모르겠네. 방금 말한 그 아가씨와 약혼할 때, 자네는 그 집안에 정식으로 청혼을 했었나? 이를테면 — 오늘 우리가 겪고 있는 것 같은 그런 일이 있었나?\" 나는 초조하게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 트래들스가 받았는데, 그 사려 깊은 얼굴에 어느새 생각에 잠긴 그늘이 드리워 있었다. \"내 경우에는 꽤나 고통스러운 거래였네, 코퍼필드. 알다시피 소피가 집안에서 워낙 쓸모가 많다 보니, 식구들 누구도 그녀가 시집간다는 생각을 견디질 못하는 거야. 사실 자기들끼리 그녀는 절대 시집 안 보낸다고 못 박아 놓고는, 그녀를 노처녀라고 불렀거든. 그래서 내가 더없이 조심조심 크루러 부인에게 그 얘길 꺼냈을 때 —\"\n\n\"그 어머니 말인가?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그 어머니지.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"호러스 크루러 목사님 사모님 말이야 — 내가 온갖 조심을 다 기울여 크루러 부인에게 그 얘길 꺼냈더니, 그 효과가 어찌나 대단했던지 부인이 비명을 지르며 정신을 잃고 말았네. 그 뒤로 몇 달 동안은 그 얘길 다시 꺼내지도 못했어.\"\n\n\"그래도 결국엔 꺼냈나?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 호러스 목사님이 꺼내셨지.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그분은 더없이 훌륭한 분이고, 모든 면에서 본보기가 되는 분이거든. 그분이 부인에게, 기독교인으로서 마땅히 이 희생을 (더구나 워낙 불확실한 일이니만큼) 받아들이고, 나를 향해 매정한 감정을 품어서는 안 된다고 일깨워 주셨어. 나로 말하자면, 코퍼필드, 맹세코, 그 집안에 대해 완전히 맹금이라도 된 듯한 기분이었다네.\"\n\n\"자매들은 자네 편을 들어 줬으면 좋았겠는데, 트래들스?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그랬다고는 못 하겠네.\" 그가 받았다. \"크루러 부인을 그럭저럭 누그러뜨리고 나니, 이번엔 세라에게 그 얘길 꺼내야 했어. 척추에 뭔가 문제가 있다는 세라 얘길 내가 했던 거, 기억하나?\"\n\n\"잘 기억하지!\"\n\n\"그녀가 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐더군.\" 트래들스가 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 얼굴로 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"그러고는 눈을 감고, 납빛으로 변하더니, 온몸이 빳빳하게 굳어서는, 이틀 동안 찻숟가락으로 떠 넣어 주는 구운 빵과 물 말고는 아무것도 입에 대질 않았어.\"\n\n\"거참 어지간히도 거북한 아가씨로구먼, 트래들스!\" 내가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"오, 그건 좀 양해해 주게, 코퍼필드!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"아주 사랑스러운 아가씨인데, 다만 감정이 워낙 풍부해서 그래. 사실 그 집 식구들이 다 그렇거든. 나중에 소피가 그러는데, 세라를 간호하면서 자기가 겪은 자책감은 무슨 말로도 표현할 수 없다더군. 내 심정으로 미루어 봐도 그게 분명 모질었으리란 걸 알겠어, 코퍼필드. 마치 죄인 같은 심정이었으니까. 세라가 회복되고 나서도, 우린 나머지 여덟 명에게 또 그 얘길 꺼내야 했고, 그게 저마다에게 더없이 애처로운 갖가지 반응을 불러일으켰지. 소피가 가르치는 어린 두 동생은 이제 막 나를 끔찍이 싫어하길 그만뒀을 정도야.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 이제는 다들 그 일을 받아들였으면 좋겠는데, 그렇지?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그-그래, 대체로 보면 단념했다고는 할 수 있겠지.\" 트래들스가 미심쩍은 듯 말했다. \"사실은, 우린 그 얘길 꺼내는 걸 피하거든. 그리고 내 앞날이 불안하고 형편이 변변찮다는 점이 그들에겐 큰 위안이 되고 있어. 우리가 결혼할 땐 통탄스러운 광경이 벌어질 거야. 결혼식이라기보다는 차라리 장례식에 훨씬 가깝겠지. 그리고 식구들은 죄다 내가 그녀를 데려간다고 나를 미워할 거고!\"\n\n그가 정색과 우스개를 뒤섞어 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 나를 바라보던 그 정직한 얼굴은, 실제로 마주했을 때보다 돌이켜 떠올리는 지금 더 깊은 인상으로 남아 있다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그때 나는 극도의 떨림과 산만함에 빠진 나머지 무엇 하나에도 도무지 정신을 붙들 수가 없었기 때문이다. 스펜로 자매분이 사는 집에 다가갈 무렵, 나는 외모며 침착함이며 죄다 형편없이 떨어진 상태여서, 트래들스가 맥주 한 잔이라는 형태의 부드러운 자극제를 권했다. 이웃 술집에서 그것을 들이켜고 나자, 그는 휘청거리는 걸음의 나를 부축해 스펜로 자매분 댁 문 앞까지 데려다주었다.\n\n하녀가 문을 열었을 때, 나는 어쩐지 구경거리로 내걸린 듯한 어렴풋한 느낌이 들었다. 그리고 어찌어찌 청우계가 놓인 현관을 비틀비틀 가로질러, 일 층의 아담한 정원이 내려다보이는 조용하고 자그마한 응접실로 들어선 느낌, 거기 소파에 앉은 느낌, 그리고 모자를 벗자 트래들스의 머리카락이 곤두서는 것을 본 느낌 — 가짜 코담뱃갑 뚜껑을 열면 튀어나오는, 용수철로 만든 그 주제넘은 작은 인형들 가운데 하나처럼. 그리고 벽난로 선반 위에서 구식 시계가 똑딱이는 소리를 들으며, 그것을 내 심장의 발딱거림에 맞춰 보려 애쓰던 느낌 — 도무지 맞춰지지 않았지만. 그리고 방 안을 둘러보며 도라의 흔적을 찾았으나 아무것도 보이지 않던 느낌. 그리고 멀리서 집이 한 번 짖었다가 누군가에게 곧장 입이 틀어막힌 듯하다고 생각한 느낌. 마침내 나는 트래들스를 벽난로 쪽으로 밀어붙인 채, 검은 옷을 입은 두 분의 자그마하고 메마른 노부인에게 어쩔 줄 모르며 절을 하고 있는 나 자신을 발견했는데, 두 분 모두 작고하신 스펜로 씨를 나무껍질이나 가죽으로 빚어 놓은 표본처럼 신기할 만큼 빼닮아 있었다.\n\n\"부디,\" 두 자그마한 부인 가운데 한 분이 말했다. \"앉으시지요.\"\n\n내가 트래들스를 들이받고 넘어지길 멈추고, 고양이가 아닌 무언가 위에 앉고 — 처음 앉은 자리가 그러했다 — 나서야, 나는 시야를 어느 정도 되찾아 다음을 알아차렸다. 스펜로 씨는 분명 집안에서 막내였다는 것, 두 자매 사이에는 여섯 살이나 여덟 살쯤 터울이 진다는 것, 그리고 동생 쪽이 이 회담의 진행을 맡은 듯하다는 것이었다. 내 편지를 손에 쥐고 있는 게 바로 그분이었기 때문인데 — 그 편지가 나에게는 어찌나 낯익으면서도 또 어찌나 묘하게 보이던지! — 그분은 외알 안경 너머로 그것을 들여다보고 있었다. 두 분은 똑같이 차려입었으나, 이 동생 쪽이 언니보다 좀 더 젊은 분위기로 옷을 걸치고 있었다. 어쩌면 주름 장식이나 깃 장식, 브로치나 팔찌, 또는 그런 류의 소소한 것을 살짝 더 달고 있어서, 한결 생기 있어 보였는지도 모른다. 두 분 다 자세가 꼿꼿하고, 격식 차리고, 빈틈없고, 차분하고, 조용했다. 내 편지를 들지 않은 언니 쪽은 두 팔을 가슴 위에 포개어 서로 기대 놓고 있었는데, 마치 우상 같았다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨가 맞으시지요.\" 내 편지를 든 동생 쪽이 트래들스를 향해 말했다.\n\n이것은 끔찍한 시작이었다. 트래들스는 코퍼필드 씨가 나임을 가리켜 보여야 했고, 나는 내가 나임을 주장해야 했으며, 두 분은 트래들스가 코퍼필드 씨라는 미리 품은 생각을 떨쳐 내야 했으니, 통틀어 우리는 꽤나 딱한 처지에 놓인 셈이었다. 그 처지를 더욱 빛내 주려고, 우리는 모두 또렷이 들었다. 집이 짧게 두 번 짖고는 또 한 번 입이 틀어막히는 소리를.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨!\" 편지를 든 동생 쪽이 말했다.\n\n나는 무언가를 했다 — 짐작건대 절을 했을 것이다 — 그러고는 온통 귀를 기울이고 있는데, 언니 쪽이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"제 동생 러비니아가,\" 그분이 말했다. \"이런 종류의 일에 밝으니, 양쪽 모두의 행복을 도모하는 데 가장 알맞다고 저희가 여기는 바를 말씀드릴 거예요.\"\n\n나는 나중에 러비니아 양이 연애 문제에 일가견이 있는 분이라는 것을 알게 되었는데, 옛날에 짧은 휘스트를 즐기던 어떤 피저 씨라는 사람이 있어 그녀에게 마음을 두었다고 짐작되기 때문이었다. 내 개인적인 생각으로는, 이는 순전히 근거 없는 억측이며, 피저는 그런 류의 감정과는 전혀 무관했다 — 내가 들어 본 한, 그는 그런 감정을 그 어떤 식으로도 내비친 적이 없었으니까. 그렇지만 러비니아 양과 클러리사 양은 둘 다 미신처럼 믿고 있었다. 피저가 젊은 시절에 (예순 살쯤에) 술을 너무 마셔 몸을 망가뜨리고, 그것을 다시 바로잡겠다며 배스 광천수를 들이켜다가 그만 이를 무리하게 한 탓에 일찍 꺾이지만 않았다면, 틀림없이 자기 열정을 고백했으리라고. 두 분은 심지어 그가 남모를 사랑 때문에 죽었다고 은근히 의심하기까지 했다. 그렇지만 솔직히 말하자면, 그 집에는 그가 다홍빛 코를 한 채로 그려진 초상화가 걸려 있었는데, 그 모습으로 보아 사랑을 감추느라 속을 끓인 흔적이라곤 도무지 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"우리는,\" 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"이 일의 지난 내력은 들추지 않겠어요. 우리 가엾은 오빠 프랜시스의 죽음으로 그건 다 지워졌으니까요.\"\n\n\"우리는,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"오라버니 프랜시스와 자주 왕래하는 사이는 아니었어요. 하지만 우리 사이에 딱 잘라 갈라서거나 등 돌린 일은 없었지요. 프랜시스는 프랜시스의 길을 갔고, 우리는 우리 길을 갔어요. 우리는 그것이 양쪽 모두의 행복에 보탬이 된다고 여겼지요. 그리고 실제로 그러했어요.\"\n\n자매분은 말할 때마다 몸을 조금씩 앞으로 기울였고, 말을 마치면 고개를 흔들었으며, 입을 다물면 다시 꼿꼿해졌다. 클러리사 양은 결코 팔을 움직이지 않았다. 가끔 손가락으로 팔 위에 가락을 두드리기는 했는데 — 짐작건대 미뉴에트며 행진곡 따위를 — 그러면서도 팔만큼은 결코 움직이지 않았다.\n\n\"우리 조카의 처지는, 아니 그러리라 짐작되는 처지는, 오라버니 프랜시스의 죽음으로 크게 달라졌어요.\" 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"그러니 우리는 조카의 처지에 관한 오라버니의 생각도 달라졌다고 봅니다. 코퍼필드 씨, 우리는 당신이 좋은 자질과 떳떳한 인품을 갖춘 젊은 신사라는 것, 또 우리 조카에게 애정을 품고 있다는 것 — 아니, 애정을 품고 있다고 굳게 믿고 있다는 것 — 을 의심할 까닭이 없어요.\"\n\n나는 기회가 생길 때마다 으레 하던 대로, 내가 도라를 사랑하는 만큼 누구를 사랑한 사람은 세상에 없을 거라고 대답했다. 트래들스가 맞장구치는 중얼거림으로 나를 거들었다.\n\n러비니아 양이 무언가 대꾸를 이어 가려는데, 줄곧 오라버니 프랜시스 얘기를 꺼내고 싶은 충동에 시달리는 듯한 클러리사 양이 또 끼어들었다.\n\n\"도라의 어머니가,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"우리 오라버니 프랜시스와 결혼할 때, 진작에 식탁에 가족이 앉을 자리는 없다고 잘라 말했더라면, 양쪽 모두의 행복을 위해 더 나았을 거예요.\"\n\n\"클러리사 언니,\" 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"그 얘긴 이제 굳이 안 해도 되지 않을까요.\"\n\n\"러비니아 동생,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"그건 이 일에 속하는 얘기야. 네가 말할 자격이 있는 그 부분에 대해서라면 나는 끼어들 생각이 없어. 하지만 이 부분에 대해서는 나도 할 말이 있고 내 의견이 있어. 도라의 어머니가 우리 오라버니 프랜시스와 결혼할 때, 자기 속셈을 솔직하게 밝혔더라면 양쪽 모두의 행복을 위해 더 나았을 거야. 그랬다면 우리도 무엇을 기대해야 할지 알았겠지. 우리도 '아무 때고 저희를 초대하지 말아 주세요'라고 했을 테고, 오해의 소지는 죄다 피할 수 있었을 거야.\"\n\n클러리사 양이 고개를 흔들고 나자, 러비니아 양이 다시 말을 이으며, 또 한 번 외알 안경 너머로 내 편지를 들여다보았다. 그건 그렇고, 두 분 다 작고 반짝이며 동그랗게 빛나는 눈을 가졌는데, 꼭 새 눈 같았다. 두 분은 통틀어 새와 그리 다르지 않았으니, 날카롭고 잽싸고 불쑥불쑥하는 태도며, 카나리아처럼 짧고 말쑥하게 매무새를 가다듬는 모양새가 그러했다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이, 러비니아 양이 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 우리 조카가 받아들인 구혼자 자격으로 이곳을 드나들 수 있게 해 달라고, 제 언니 클러리사와 저에게 허락을 청하셨지요.\"\n\n\"우리 오라버니 프랜시스가,\" 이토록 차분한 것을 두고 폭발이라 부를 수 있다면, 클러리사 양이 또 한 번 폭발하며 말했다. \"법학원 분위기로, 그것도 오직 법학원 분위기로만 자기 둘레를 채우고 싶어 했다면, 우리에게 무슨 권리나 마음이 있어 거기에 토를 달겠어요? 없지요, 분명히. 우리는 누구에게도 억지로 비집고 들어가길 늘 멀리해 왔어요. 하지만 왜 그렇다고 말하지 않았을까요? 오라버니 프랜시스와 그 부인은 그들의 사교 모임을 가지라지요. 제 동생 러비니아와 저는 우리 사교 모임을 가질 테니까요. 그쯤은 우리 스스로 찾을 수 있어요, 그러길 바라요.\"\n\n이 말이 트래들스와 나에게 한 것 같아서, 트래들스도 나도 무언가 대답 비슷한 것을 했다. 트래들스의 말은 들리지 않았다. 나로 말하자면, 그것이 관련된 모두에게 더없이 떳떳한 일이라고 한 것 같다. 그게 무슨 뜻이었는지는 나도 도무지 모르겠다.\n\n\"러비니아 동생,\" 이제 속이 후련해진 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"이어서 하렴, 얘야.\"\n\n러비니아 양이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 제 언니 클러리사와 저는 정말이지 이 편지를 매우 신중하게 검토했어요. 게다가 끝내는 우리 조카에게 보여 주고, 조카와 의논까지 한 끝에야 검토를 마쳤지요. 우리는 당신이 그 애를 무척 좋아한다고 여긴다는 걸 의심하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"여기다니요, 부인.\" 내가 황홀하게 입을 떼었다. \"오! —\"\n\n그러나 클러리사 양이 (꼭 날카로운 카나리아처럼) 나를 쳐다보며, 신탁을 가로막지 말라는 듯이 굴기에, 나는 용서를 빌었다.\n\n\"애정이란,\" 러비니아 양이 동의를 구하듯 언니를 흘끗 보자, 언니가 구절마다 가볍게 고갯짓으로 동의를 표하는 가운데 말했다. \"무르익은 애정, 흠모, 헌신이란, 쉽사리 제 모습을 드러내지 않아요. 그 목소리는 낮지요. 겸손하고 물러나 있으며, 숨어 엎드린 채 기다리고 또 기다려요. 무르익은 열매란 그런 거예요. 때로는 한평생이 미끄러지듯 흘러가도, 그것이 여전히 그늘 속에서 익어 가고 있음을 보게 되지요.\"\n\n물론 그때는 이것이 그녀가 사랑에 꺾인 피저에 대해 겪었다고 짐작되는 경험을 빗댄 말이라는 것을 알지 못했다. 하지만 클러리사 양이 무겁게 고개를 끄덕이는 그 진지함으로 미루어, 이 말에 큰 무게가 실려 있음은 알아챘다.\n\n\"아주 어린 사람들의 가벼운 — 이런 감정에 견주어 나는 이를 가볍다고 부르겠는데 — 마음의 끌림이란,\" 러비니아 양이 이어 갔다. \"바위에 견주면 먼지나 다름없어요. 그것이 과연 오래 갈 만한 것인지, 아니면 무슨 참된 바탕이 있는 것인지 알기가 어렵기에, 제 언니 클러리사와 저는 어떻게 처신해야 할지 무척 망설였어요, 코퍼필드 씨, 그리고 — 씨도요.\"\n\n\"트래들스입니다.\" 자기를 쳐다보는 것을 알아챈 내 친구가 말했다.\n\n\"실례했어요. 이너 템플 소속이시지요?\" 클러리사 양이 또 한 번 내 편지를 흘끗 보며 말했다.\n\n트래들스는 \"바로 그렇습니다\" 하고는, 얼굴이 제법 벌게졌다.\n\n자, 비록 나는 아직 그 어떤 분명한 격려도 받지 못했지만, 두 자그마한 자매분에게서, 특히 러비니아 양에게서, 이 새롭고 풍성한 집안 관심거리를 한껏 즐기는 기색, 이를 최대한 우려먹으려고 자리를 잡는 기색, 이를 어루만지며 귀여워하려는 기색을 본 듯했고, 거기에는 밝고 환한 희망의 한 줄기 빛이 깃들어 있었다. 내가 보기에 러비니아 양은 도라와 나처럼 어린 두 연인을 감독하는 데 남다른 만족을 느낄 듯했고, 클러리사 양도 동생이 우리를 감독하는 모습을 지켜보며, 또 충동이 강하게 일 때마다 자기만의 그 특별한 영역으로 끼어들며, 그에 못지않은 만족을 느낄 듯했다. 이것이 나에게 용기를 주어, 나는 누구에게 말로 다 할 수도, 누가 곧이들을 수도 없을 만큼 도라를 사랑한다고, 내 벗들이 죄다 내가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지 안다고, 고모님이며 아그네스며 트래들스며 나를 아는 사람이라면 누구나 내가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그 사랑이 나를 얼마나 진실하게 만들었는지 안다고, 더없이 열렬하게 항변했다. 그 진실함을 두고 나는 트래들스에게 증언을 청했다. 그러자 트래들스는 마치 의회 토론에라도 뛰어드는 양 불끈 달아올라, 정말이지 훌륭하게 나섰다. 그는 시원시원하고 묵직한 말로, 또 꾸밈없고 사리에 맞으며 실속 있는 태도로 나를 뒷받침했는데, 그것이 보아하니 좋은 인상을 남겼다.\n\n\"외람되나마 말씀드리자면, 저는 이런 일에 약간의 경험이 있는 사람으로서 말씀드리는 겁니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"저 자신도 어떤 아가씨와 약혼한 처지거든요 — 데번셔의 열 자매 가운데 하나인데요 — 그리고 지금으로선 우리 약혼이 깨질 낌새는 전혀 보이지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 한 말을 당신이 뒷받침해 주실 수 있겠군요, 트래들스 씨.\" 분명히 그에게 새로운 흥미를 느끼며 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"겸손하고 물러나 있으며, 기다리고 또 기다리는 그 애정에 대해서 말이에요.\"\n\n\"전적으로요, 부인.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n클러리사 양이 러비니아 양을 바라보며 무겁게 고개를 흔들었다. 러비니아 양은 무언가 깨달은 듯 클러리사 양을 바라보며 작게 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"러비니아 동생,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"내 냄새 맡는 약병을 받으렴.\"\n\n러비니아 양은 향초 식초를 몇 번 들이마시며 기운을 차렸고 — 그동안 트래들스와 나는 무척 근심스럽게 지켜보았다 — 그러고는 다소 가냘프게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제 언니와 저는, 트래들스 씨, 당신 벗 코퍼필드 씨와 우리 조카처럼 아주 어린 사람들의 호감, 아니 호감이라고 상상하는 그 마음에 관해, 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 무척 망설였어요.\"\n\n\"우리 오라버니 프랜시스의 자식이지요.\" 클러리사 양이 한마디 했다. \"오라버니 프랜시스의 부인이 생전에 (물론 자기가 가장 옳다고 여기는 대로 행할 두말없는 권리가 있긴 했지만) 우리 가족을 식탁에 부르는 게 편하다고 여겼더라면, 우리도 지금쯤 오라버니 프랜시스의 자식을 더 잘 알았을 거예요. 러비니아 동생, 계속하렴.\"\n\n러비니아 양은 내 편지를 돌려 겉봉의 글씨가 자기 쪽을 향하게 한 다음, 외알 안경 너머로 그 부분에 적어 둔 단정해 보이는 메모를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"우리가 보기에는,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"트래들스 씨, 이 감정들을 우리 자신의 관찰이라는 시험대에 올려 보는 것이 신중한 처사인 듯해요. 지금으로선 우리는 그 감정에 대해 아무것도 모르고, 거기에 얼마만큼의 진실함이 있는지 판단할 처지도 못 되니까요. 그래서 우리는 코퍼필드 씨의 청을, 그가 이곳을 드나드는 것을 허락하는 선까지는 받아들일 마음이에요.\"\n\n\"친애하는 부인들,\" 내가 어마어마한 불안의 짐을 덜고 외쳤다. \"두 분의 친절을 저는 결코 잊지 않겠습니다!\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 러비니아 양이 이어 갔다. \"하지만 우리는 그 드나듦을, 트래들스 씨, 지금으로선 우리를 찾아오는 것으로 여기고 싶어요. 우리에게 기회가 생길 때까지는, 코퍼필드 씨와 우리 조카 사이의 그 어떤 분명한 약혼도 인정하지 않도록 우리 스스로 경계해야 하니까요 —\"\n\n\"네가 기회를 갖게 될 때까지지, 러비니아 동생.\" 클러리사 양이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다고 하지요.\" 러비니아 양이 한숨과 함께 동의했다. \"내가 그들을 관찰할 기회를 갖게 될 때까지요.\"\n\n\"코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이보다 더 이치에 맞고 사려 깊은 일은 없으리라는 걸, 자네도 분명 느낄 걸세.\"\n\n\"없고말고!\" 내가 외쳤다. \"그 점을 저는 뼈저리게 느낍니다.\"\n\n\"이런 사정인 만큼,\" 러비니아 양이 다시 메모를 들여다보며 말했다. \"그리고 오직 이런 양해 아래서만 그의 드나듦을 허락하는 만큼, 우리는 코퍼필드 씨에게 명예를 걸고 분명한 다짐을 받아야 해요. 우리가 모르는 채로는 그와 우리 조카 사이에 어떤 식의 연락도 오가지 않으리라는 것 말이에요. 우리 조카에 관한 그 어떤 계획도, 먼저 우리에게 알리지 않고서는 도모하지 않으리라는 것도요 —\" \"너에게지, 러비니아 동생.\" 클러리사 양이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그렇다고 하자, 클러리사!\" 러비니아 양이 체념한 듯 동의했다. \"나에게 알리고 — 우리 동의를 얻으리라는 것 말이에요. 우리는 이것을 더없이 분명하고 엄중한 조건으로 삼아야 하며, 어떤 경우에도 어겨서는 안 돼요. 우리가 오늘 코퍼필드 씨더러 믿을 만한 벗을 한 분 데려오시라고 한 것은,\" 트래들스 쪽으로 고개를 까딱하자 그가 절을 했다. \"이 문제에 관해 의심이나 오해가 없도록 하기 위해서였어요. 만약 코퍼필드 씨나, 또는 트래들스 씨, 당신이 이 약속을 하기에 조금이라도 께름칙한 데가 있다면, 부디 시간을 두고 생각해 보시기를 청해요.\"\n\n나는 한껏 황홀한 열기에 휩싸여, 단 한순간도 생각할 필요가 없다고 외쳤다. 나는 더없이 열정적인 태도로 요구된 약속에 나 자신을 옭아맸고, 트래들스에게 증인이 되어 달라고 청했으며, 만약 내가 그 약속에서 조금이라도 비껴난다면 나야말로 더없이 흉악한 인간이라고 스스로를 단죄했다.\n\n\"잠깐!\" 러비니아 양이 손을 들며 말했다. \"우리는 두 분 신사를 맞이하는 기쁨을 누리기에 앞서, 십오 분 동안 두 분만 남겨 두고 이 점을 곰곰이 생각하시도록 하기로 정해 두었어요. 우리가 물러가는 걸 허락해 주시겠지요.\"\n\n생각할 필요가 없다고 말해 봐야 헛일이었다. 두 분은 정해진 시간만큼 물러가겠다고 고집을 부렸다. 그리하여 이 자그마한 새 두 마리는 무척 위엄 있게 폴짝폴짝 나갔고, 나는 남아서 트래들스의 축하를 받으며, 마치 내가 더없이 황홀한 행복의 나라로 옮겨진 듯한 기분에 잠겼다. 정확히 십오 분이 다 지나자, 두 분은 물러갈 때 못지않은 위엄으로 다시 나타났다. 두 분은 마치 자그마한 옷이 가을 낙엽으로 지어지기라도 한 양 바스락거리며 나갔다가, 똑같이 바스락거리며 돌아온 것이다.\n\n그러고서 나는 또 한 번 정해진 조건에 나 자신을 옭아맸다.\n\n\"클러리사 언니,\" 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"나머지는 언니가 맡아요.\"\n\n클러리사 양은 처음으로 팔짱을 풀고, 메모를 집어 들어 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"우리는,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"코퍼필드 씨가 편하시다면, 일요일마다 저녁 식사에 모시는 게 기쁘겠어요. 우리 식사 시간은 세 시예요.\"\n\n나는 절을 했다.\n\"주중에는,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"코퍼필드 씨를 차 시간에 모시는 게 기쁘겠어요. 우리 차 시간은 여섯 시 반이고요.\"\n\n나는 또 절을 했다.\n\n\"일주일에 두 번이에요.\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"다만 보통은 그보다 잦지 않게요.\"\n\n나는 또 절을 했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨 편지에 언급된 트롯우드 양도,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"어쩌면 우리를 찾아 주시겠지요. 왕래가 양쪽 모두의 행복에 더 나을 때라면, 우리는 기꺼이 방문을 받고 또 답방하지요. 왕래가 없는 편이 양쪽 모두의 행복에 더 나을 때라면 (우리 오라버니 프랜시스와 그 식솔의 경우처럼) 그건 또 전혀 다른 얘기지만요.\"\n\n나는 고모님이 두 분과 사귀게 되어 자랑스럽고 기쁘게 여기시리라는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 다만 솔직히 말해, 두 쪽이 그리 흡족하게 어울려 지낼지는 영 자신이 없었다. 이제 조건이 다 매듭지어졌기에, 나는 더없이 따뜻한 태도로 감사의 뜻을 표했다. 그리고 먼저 클러리사 양의 손을, 이어서 러비니아 양의 손을 잡아, 그때마다 그 손을 내 입술에 갖다 댔다.\n\n그러자 러비니아 양이 일어나서, 트래들스 씨에게 잠깐 양해를 구하고는, 나더러 따라오라고 했다. 나는 온통 떨면서 그 말을 따랐고, 다른 방으로 인도되었다. 거기서 나는 내 축복받은 그대가 귀여운 작은 얼굴을 벽에 댄 채 문 뒤에서 두 귀를 틀어막고 있는 것을 보았다. 그리고 집은 머리를 수건으로 싸맨 채 접시 데우는 통 속에 들어 있었다.\n\n오! 검은 옷을 입은 그녀가 어찌나 아름답던지, 그리고 처음에는 또 어찌나 흐느끼며 울던지, 문 뒤에서 좀체 나오려 들지 않던지! 마침내 그녀가 나왔을 때 우리가 서로 얼마나 다정했던지. 그리고 우리가 집을 접시 데우는 통에서 꺼내, 연거푸 재채기를 해 대는 그 녀석을 다시 빛 가운데로 돌려놓고, 셋이 다 함께 다시 만났을 때, 나는 또 얼마나 더없는 행복에 잠겨 있었던지!\n\n\"내 가장 사랑하는 도라! 이제 정말로, 영원히 나만의 그대로구려!\"\n\n\"오, 그러지 마세요!\" 도라가 애원했다. \"제발!\"\n\n\"그대는 영원히 나만의 사람이 아니오, 도라?\"\n\n\"오 그럼요, 당연히 그렇지요!\" 도라가 외쳤다. \"하지만 난 너무 무서운걸요!\"\n\n\"무섭다니, 내 사람아?\"\n\n\"오 그래요! 난 저분이 싫어요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"저분은 왜 안 가는 거죠?\"\n\n\"누구 말이오, 내 생명이여?\"\n\n\"당신 친구분이요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"이건 저분이 상관할 일이 아니잖아요. 어쩜 저렇게 멍청할까요!\"\n\n\"내 사랑!\" (그녀의 어린애 같은 몸짓만큼 사람을 녹이는 것은 없었다.) \"그는 더없이 좋은 사람이라오!\"\n\n\"오, 하지만 우린 그런 더없이 좋은 사람 같은 건 필요 없는걸요!\" 도라가 입을 삐죽였다.\n\n\"여보,\" 내가 타일렀다. \"당신도 곧 그를 잘 알게 되면, 무엇보다 그를 좋아하게 될 거요. 그리고 곧 우리 고모님도 오실 텐데, 고모님도 알게 되면 무엇보다 좋아하게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 제발 고모님은 데려오지 말아요!\" 도라가 겁에 질린 작은 입맞춤을 나에게 하고는 두 손을 모으며 말했다. \"안 돼요. 난 알아요, 그분은 심술궂고, 말썽이나 부리는 늙은이라는 걸! 그분을 여기 오게 하지 말아요, 도디!\" 이는 데이비드를 비틀어 부르는 말이었다.\n\n그때는 타일러 봐야 소용없었다. 그래서 나는 그저 웃고, 감탄하고, 사랑에 흠뻑 빠져 더없이 행복했다. 그녀는 집이 구석에서 뒷다리로 서는 새 재주를 보여 주었는데 — 녀석은 번개가 번쩍하는 정도의 짧은 동안 그러다가 폭삭 주저앉았다 — 만약 러비니아 양이 들어와 나를 데려가지 않았더라면, 나는 트래들스도 까맣게 잊은 채 거기 얼마나 오래 머물렀을지 모른다. 러비니아 양은 도라를 무척 귀여워했고 (그녀는 도라가 자기 그 나이 적 모습과 똑 닮았다고 했는데 — 그렇다면 그동안 어지간히 달라진 모양이다), 도라를 꼭 장난감처럼 다루었다. 나는 도라를 구슬려 트래들스를 만나러 가게 하고 싶었으나, 그 말을 꺼내자 그녀는 제 방으로 달아나 문을 잠가 버렸다. 그래서 나는 그녀 없이 트래들스에게 갔고, 그와 함께 둥실둥실 떠가는 기분으로 걸어 나왔다.\n\n\"이보다 더 흡족할 수가 없네.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그리고 분명 무척 상냥한 노부인들이야. 자네가 나보다 몇 해는 먼저 결혼한다 해도 나는 조금도 놀라지 않을 걸세, 코퍼필드.\"\n\n\"자네의 소피는 무슨 악기라도 다루나, 트래들스?\" 내가 우쭐한 마음에 물었다.\n\n\"피아노라면 어린 동생들에게 가르칠 만큼은 알지.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"노래는 좀 하고?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"음, 가끔 동생들이 풀 죽어 있을 때 기운 좀 북돋워 주려고 발라드를 부르긴 해.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"전문적인 건 아니고.\"\n\n\"기타에 맞춰서 노래하지는 않나?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 천만에!\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"그림은 좀 그리나?\"\n\n\"전혀.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n나는 트래들스에게 도라의 노래를 들려주고, 그녀가 그린 꽃 그림도 몇 점 보여 주겠다고 약속했다. 그는 그러면 무척 좋겠다고 했고, 우리는 팔짱을 끼고 더없이 흐뭇하고 즐거운 기분으로 집에 돌아갔다. 가는 길에 나는 그에게 소피 얘기를 하도록 부추겼고, 그는 그녀에 대한 사랑 어린 믿음으로 그 얘길 했는데, 나는 그게 무척 감탄스러웠다. 나는 속으로 그녀를 도라와 견주며 적잖은 흐뭇함을 느꼈다. 그러나 솔직히 나 자신에게 인정하기를, 그녀 또한 트래들스에게는 더없이 훌륭한 아가씨인 듯하다고 했다.\n\n물론 고모님께는 곧장 회담이 잘 풀린 결과와, 그 과정에서 오간 말과 일을 죄다 알려 드렸다. 고모님은 내가 그토록 행복해하는 것을 보고 기뻐하셨고, 지체 없이 도라의 고모들을 찾아가겠다고 약속하셨다. 그러나 그날 밤 내가 아그네스에게 편지를 쓰는 동안 고모님이 우리 방을 어찌나 오래 왔다 갔다 거니시던지, 나는 고모님이 아침까지 거닐 작정이신가 하는 생각마저 들었다.\n\n아그네스에게 보낸 내 편지는 열렬하고 고마움이 넘치는 것이었는데, 그녀의 조언을 따른 데서 나온 온갖 좋은 결실을 죄다 적었다. 그녀는 돌아오는 우편으로 나에게 답장을 했다. 그녀의 편지는 희망차고, 진지하고, 명랑했다. 그녀는 그때부터 늘 명랑했다.\n\n이제 나는 어느 때보다 두 손이 바빠졌다. 하이게이트로 날마다 오가는 것을 감안하면, 퍼트니는 먼 길이었다. 그런데도 나는 자연히 가능한 한 자주 그곳에 가고 싶었다. 차를 마시러 오라는 제안은 도무지 지키기 어려운 일이어서, 나는 러비니아 양과 타협해, 내가 특권으로 누리는 일요일을 축내지 않으면서 토요일 오후마다 방문해도 좋다는 허락을 받아 냈다. 그리하여 매주의 끝자락은 나에게 더없이 달콤한 시간이었고, 나는 그것을 기다리는 마음으로 나머지 한 주를 견뎌 냈다.\n\n고모님과 도라의 고모들이, 이모저모 따져 보아도 내가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 매끄럽게 잘 지내는 것을 알고 나는 무척 마음이 놓였다. 고모님은 회담이 있은 지 며칠 안 되어 약속한 방문을 했고, 다시 며칠이 더 지나 도라의 고모들이 격식과 절차를 갖추어 고모님을 답방했다. 그 뒤로도 비슷하나 한결 다정한 왕래가 이어졌는데, 대개 서너 주 간격이었다. 내가 알기로 고모님은 도라의 고모들을 무척이나 곤혹스럽게 만들었는데, 마차로 오가는 격식을 깡그리 무시하고 아침 식사 직후나 차 시간 직전 같은 엉뚱한 때에 퍼트니까지 걸어 나가는가 하면, 그 문제에 관한 문명의 통념 따위는 조금도 개의치 않고, 그저 머리에 편한 대로 아무렇게나 보닛을 쓰고 다녔기 때문이다. 그래도 도라의 고모들은 이내 고모님을, 분별이 야무진, 좀 별나고 다소 남성적인 부인으로 여기기로 했다. 그리고 고모님이 이따금 갖가지 예법 문제를 두고 이단스러운 의견을 내뱉어 도라의 고모들의 깃털을 곤두서게 하긴 했어도, 고모님은 나를 워낙 끔찍이 사랑한 나머지 전체의 화목을 위해 자신의 자잘한 별난 점 몇 가지쯤은 기꺼이 희생하셨다.\n\n우리의 작은 무리 가운데 형편에 자기를 맞추기를 한사코 거부한 단 하나의 일원은 집이었다. 녀석은 고모님만 봤다 하면 곧장 입안의 이란 이는 죄다 드러내 보이고, 의자 밑으로 물러나, 쉴 새 없이 으르렁거렸다. 이따금은 고모님이 정말이지 제 감정으로는 도무지 못 견딜 상대라는 듯 구슬프게 울부짖기까지 했다. 녀석에게는 온갖 방법이 다 동원되었다 — 어르고, 야단치고, 찰싹 때리고, 버킹엄 거리로 데려가 보고 (거기서 녀석은 두 고양이에게 단박에 덤벼들어 보는 사람들을 죄다 질겁하게 만들었다). 그러나 녀석은 도무지 고모님과 함께 있는 것을 견뎌 낼 마음을 먹지 못했다. 가끔은 제 반감을 이겨 낸 줄 알고 몇 분쯤 상냥하게 굴다가도, 이내 그 들창코를 쳐들고 어찌나 울부짖던지, 별수 없이 눈을 가려 접시 데우는 통에 가둘 수밖에 없었다. 결국 도라는 문간에 고모님이 왔다는 기별이 오면, 으레 녀석을 수건으로 싸맨 채 거기 가두어 두었다.\n\n이렇게 조용한 흐름에 접어든 뒤로 나를 무척 괴롭힌 것이 하나 있었다. 그것은 도라가 다들 한마음으로 마치 예쁜 장난감이나 노리개처럼 대접받는 것 같다는 점이었다. 차츰 그녀와 허물없어진 고모님은 늘 그녀를 '꼬마 꽃송이'라고 불렀다. 그리고 러비니아 양의 삶의 낙은 그녀의 시중을 들고, 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말아 주고, 장신구를 만들어 주고, 귀여운 아이처럼 다루는 것이었다. 러비니아 양이 하는 일이라면 그 언니도 으레 따라 했다. 나에게는 무척 묘한 노릇이었다. 그런데 다들 도라를 그 나름의 격에 맞춰, 꼭 도라가 집을 그 나름의 격에 맞춰 대하는 것과 똑같이 대하는 것 같았다.\n\n나는 이 일을 두고 도라에게 말해 보기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 하루는 둘이 밖으로 산책을 나갔을 때 (얼마간 지나자 러비니아 양이 우리끼리 산책을 나가도 좋다고 허락해 주었다), 나는 그녀에게, 식구들이 그녀를 좀 다르게 대하도록 해 줬으면 좋겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 말이오, 여보,\" 내가 타일렀다. \"당신은 어린애가 아니잖소.\"\n\n\"저것 봐요!\" 도라가 말했다. \"또 화내려고 그러는 거죠!\"\n\n\"화라니, 내 사랑?\"\n\n\"그분들은 분명 나한테 무척 잘해 주는걸요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그리고 난 무척 행복하고요 —\"\n\n\"그래! 하지만 내 가장 소중한 사람이여!\" 내가 말했다. \"당신은 무척 행복하면서도, 또 사리에 맞게 대접받을 수도 있는 거요.\"\n\n도라는 나를 나무라는 눈빛으로 보았다 — 더없이 예쁜 눈빛이었다! — 그러더니 흐느끼기 시작하며 말하기를, 자기를 좋아하지 않으면서 왜 그토록 자기와 약혼하고 싶어 했느냐고, 그리고 자기를 견딜 수 없다면 지금이라도 왜 떠나지 않느냐고 했다.\n\n그러니 그 뒤에 내가 그녀의 눈물을 입맞춤으로 닦아 주고, 내가 그녀를 얼마나 끔찍이 사랑하는지 말해 주는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가!\n\n\"난 분명 정이 무척 많은 사람이에요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"당신은 나한테 모질게 굴면 안 돼요, 도디!\"\n\n\"모질다니, 내 소중한 사랑이여! 내가 세상 무엇과도 바꾸어 당신에게 모질게 굴 — 아니, 모질게 굴 수나 있겠소!\"\n\n\"그럼 내 흠을 잡지 말아요.\" 도라가 입을 장미 봉오리처럼 오므리며 말했다. \"그럼 나도 착하게 굴게요.\"\n\n이윽고 그녀가 제 입으로 먼저, 언젠가 내가 말했던 그 요리책을 갖다 달라고, 그리고 언젠가 약속했던 대로 가계부 적는 법을 알려 달라고 청하기에, 나는 흠뻑 반하고 말았다. 다음 방문 때 나는 그 책을 가져갔고 (먼저 너무 딱딱해 보이지 않고 좀 더 끌리게끔 예쁘게 제본해 두었다), 우리가 공유지를 거니는 동안 고모님의 낡은 살림 장부를 보여 주었으며, 살림 연습을 하라고 그녀에게 글씨판 한 벌과, 예쁘고 작은 연필집과 심을 담은 갑을 주었다.\n\n그러나 요리책은 도라를 머리 아프게 했고, 숫자는 그녀를 울게 만들었다. 숫자가 도무지 더해지질 않는다나. 그래서 그녀는 숫자를 지워 버리고, 글씨판 온통에 자그마한 꽃다발이며 나와 집의 모습을 그려 넣었다.\n\n그러고서 나는 토요일 오후에 둘이 거닐며, 살림 문제를 말로 가르쳐 보려고 장난스레 시도했다. 이를테면 가끔 푸줏간을 지날 때면 나는 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"자 이보, 우리가 결혼했고, 당신이 저녁상에 올릴 양 어깨살을 사러 간다고 쳐 봐요. 그걸 어떻게 사야 할지 알겠소?\"\n\n내 예쁘고 작은 도라의 얼굴은 시무룩해졌고, 그녀는 또 입을 봉오리처럼 오므렸다. 마치 그보다는 입맞춤으로 내 입을 막아 버리는 편이 훨씬 낫다는 듯이.\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 사야 할지 알겠소, 여보?\" 내가 무척 꿋꿋하게 굴 때면, 아마 이렇게 거듭 물었으리라.\n\n도라는 잠시 생각하더니, 이내 의기양양하게 대답하곤 했다.\n\n\"아니, 그건 푸줏간 주인이 어떻게 팔지 알 텐데, 내가 알 게 뭐예요? 오, 바보 같은 사람아!\"\n\n그래서 한번은 요리책을 염두에 두고 도라에게, 우리가 결혼했는데 내가 맛있는 아일랜드식 스튜가 먹고 싶다고 하면 어떻게 하겠느냐고 물었더니, 그녀는 하녀더러 만들라고 시키겠다고 대답했다. 그러고는 내 팔에 작은 두 손을 마주쳐 손뼉을 치며 어찌나 사랑스럽게 웃던지, 그녀는 여느 때보다 더 매혹스러웠다.\n\n그 결과, 요리책의 주된 쓰임새는 구석에 놓여 집이 그 위에 올라서는 받침이 되는 것이었다. 그래도 도라는, 녀석이 내려오려는 기색도 없이 그 위에 올라선 채 동시에 입에 연필집을 물고 있도록 길들여 놓고는 어찌나 흐뭇해하던지, 나는 그 책을 산 것이 무척 잘한 일이다 싶었다.\n\n그러고서 우리는 다시 기타집과 꽃 그림과, 춤을 절대 멈추지 않겠다는 노래, 타 라 라! 로 돌아갔고, 한 주가 길고 긴 만큼이나 행복했다. 나는 이따금, 러비니아 양에게 슬쩍 일러 줄 용기를 낼 수 있다면 좋겠다고 생각했다 — 내 마음의 그대를 좀 지나치게 노리개처럼 대하신다고 말이다. 그리고 가끔은, 나 또한 그 흔한 잘못에 빠져 그녀를 노리개처럼 대해 왔음을 문득 깨닫고는 화들짝 놀라곤 했다 — 그렇게 자주는 아니었지만.\n\n# CHAPTER 42. MISCHIEF\n\n내가 도라와 그 고모들에게 진 책임감 때문에, 저 가공할 속기와 거기에 딸린 온갖 향상에 얼마나 열심히 매달렸는지를 여기 굳이 적는 것은 — 비록 이 원고가 나 말고는 누구의 눈에도 보일 일이 없다 하더라도 — 내가 할 일이 아닌 것만 같다. 그저 내 인생의 이 시기에 내가 보여 준 끈기에 대하여, 그리고 그때 내 안에서 영글기 시작한, 참을성 있고 꾸준한 기운에 대하여 — 내 성격에 조금이라도 강한 구석이 있다면 바로 이것이 그 강한 부분이라고 나는 안다 — 이미 적어 둔 것에 한 가지만 덧붙이고 싶다. 돌이켜 보면, 거기에서 나는 내 성공의 원천을 찾는다. 나는 세상일에서 무척 운이 좋았다. 나보다 훨씬 더 열심히 일하고도 그 절반만큼도 성공하지 못한 사람이 많다. 하지만 나는 그때 몸에 익힌 시간 엄수와 질서와 부지런함의 습관 없이는, 그리고 한 번에 한 가지 일에만 나를 집중하겠다는 — 그 일의 뒤를 이을 일이 아무리 빨리 발꿈치를 밟아 오더라도 — 그런 결심 없이는, 내가 이룬 일을 결코 이루지 못했을 것이다. 하늘이 아시거니와, 나는 결코 제 자랑하는 마음으로 이것을 쓰는 것이 아니다. 나처럼 한 장 한 장 넘어가며 제 인생을 되짚어 보는 사람이라면, 묵혀 둔 숱한 재능과 흘려보낸 숱한 기회와, 가슴속에서 끊임없이 다투며 자신을 무너뜨리던 숱한 변덕스럽고 비뚤어진 감정을 또렷이 의식하지 않고 넘어가려면, 정말로 어지간히 좋은 사람이라야 할 것이다. 감히 말하건대, 나에게는 함부로 굴리지 않은 타고난 재주가 단 하나도 없다. 내 말뜻은 그저, 인생에서 무엇을 하려고 했든 나는 온 마음을 다해 잘하려고 애썼다는 것, 무엇에 나를 바쳤든 나는 거기에 온전히 나를 바쳤다는 것, 큰 목표에서나 작은 목표에서나 나는 늘 철저히 진심이었다는 것이다. 타고난 재능이든 갈고닦은 재능이든, 그것이 꾸준하고 수수하고 고되게 일하는 자질의 동행을 면제받고도 제 목적을 이룰 수 있다고 나는 한 번도 믿어 본 적이 없다. 이 세상에 그런 식의 성취란 없다. 어떤 행복한 재능과 어떤 운 좋은 기회가 어떤 사람이 올라가는 사다리의 양쪽 기둥을 이룰 수는 있겠지만, 그 사다리의 가로대만큼은 닳고 부대껴도 견딜 재료로 만들어져야 한다. 그리고 철저하고 뜨겁고 참된 진정성을 대신할 것은 없다. 내 온몸을 던질 수 없는 일에는 결코 한 손도 대지 않을 것, 그리고 그것이 무엇이든 내 일을 짐짓 깎아내리는 시늉은 결코 하지 않을 것 — 이것이 내 황금률이었음을 나는 이제 깨닫는다.\n\n내가 방금 격언으로 추려 낸 그 실천 가운데 얼마나 많은 것을 애그니스에게 빚지고 있는지는, 여기서 되풀이하지 않겠다. 내 이야기는 고마운 사랑을 품고 애그니스에게로 나아간다.\n\n애그니스는 보름 동안 박사님 댁에 머물러 다니러 왔다. 위크필드 씨는 박사님의 오랜 벗이었고, 박사님은 그와 이야기를 나누며 그에게 도움이 되어 주고 싶어 했다. 지난번 애그니스가 시내에 왔을 때 이 일이 화제에 올랐고, 이번 방문이 그 결실이었다. 애그니스와 아버지는 함께 왔다. 애그니스가 힙 부인을 위해 동네에 묵을 곳을 구해 주기로 했다는 말을 들었을 때도 나는 그리 놀라지 않았다. 힙 부인은 류머티즘 탓에 공기를 바꿔 줄 필요가 있었고, 그런 사람들과 함께 지낼 수 있다면 더없이 좋아할 터였다. 바로 그 이튿날, 효성스러운 아들답게 유라이어가 자기 갸륵한 어머니를 모셔다가 그곳에 자리를 잡게 했을 때도 나는 놀라지 않았다.\n\n\"보세요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 박사님 정원을 한 바퀴 돌자며 억지로 내 곁에 끼어들어 말했다. \"사람이란 게 사랑을 하면 좀 질투가 나는 법이지요 — 적어도 사랑하는 사람한테서 눈을 떼지 않으려고 애를 쓰게 되니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 지금 누구를 질투하는 건데?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"도련님 덕분에,\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"지금 당장은 딱히 누구랄 것도 없네요 — 적어도 남자는요.\"\n\n“그럼 네가 어떤 여자를 질투한다는 말이냐?”\n\n그는 음흉한 붉은 눈으로 나를 곁눈질하더니 웃었다. \"정말이지, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 말했다. \"— 나리라고 불러야겠지만, 제 몸에 밴 버릇은 너그러이 봐주실 줄 압니다 — 도련님은 어찌나 슬슬 파고드시는지, 코르크 따개처럼 절 끌어내신다니까요! 뭐, 말씀드려도 상관없습니다만,\" 그가 그 물고기 같은 손을 내 손 위에 얹으며 말했다. \"저는 본디 여자한테 살가운 부류가 아니에요, 나리. 스트롱 부인한테도 한 번도 그런 적이 없고요.\"\n\n이제 그의 눈은 내 눈을 지켜보면서 악당 같은 교활함으로 푸르스름하게 보였다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 제가 법률가이긴 합니다만, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 메마른 히죽거림을 띠며 대답했다. \"지금 당장은 그저 말 그대로의 뜻이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 네 그 눈빛은 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 내가 조용히 되받았다.\n\n\"제 눈빛이요? 아이고 참, 코퍼필드, 그건 너무 매서운 수법인데요! 제 눈빛이 무슨 뜻이냐니요?\"\n\n\"그래,\" 내가 말했다. \"네 그 눈빛 말이다.\"\n\n그는 무척 재미있어하는 듯, 제 천성이 허락하는 한껏 마음을 다해 웃었다. 손으로 턱을 몇 번 긁적이고 나서, 그는 눈을 내리깐 채 — 여전히, 아주 느릿느릿 긁으면서 — 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"제가 한낱 미천한 서기였을 적에, 그 부인은 늘 절 깔보았지요. 노상 우리 애그니스를 자기 집으로 이리 오라 저리 가라 불러 대고, 노상 도련님하고는 다정한 벗이 되어 주면서요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 한데 저 자신은 너무 까마득히 낮아서 눈에 들지도 못했답니다.\"\n\n\"그래서?\" 내가 말했다. \"그랬다 치자!\"\n\n\"— 그리고 그분한테도 까마득히 낮았지요.\" 유라이어가 턱을 계속 긁으면서, 사뭇 또렷이, 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 듯한 목소리로 이어 갔다.\n\n\"네가 박사님 앞에 없을 때 박사님이 네 존재 따위를 의식하고 계셨으리라고 짐작할 만큼,\" 내가 말했다. \"박사님을 그것밖에 모르느냐?\"\n\n그는 다시 그 곁눈질로 나를 바라보았고, 더 편하게 긁으려는 듯 턱을 길쭉하게 빼며 대답했다.\n\n\"아이고 저런, 저는 박사님 말씀을 하는 게 아닙니다! 아니에요, 가엾으신 양반! 저는 몰던 씨 얘기를 하는 거예요!\"\n\n내 심장이 가슴속에서 완전히 죽어 버렸다. 그 일에 대한 나의 오랜 의심과 불안, 박사님의 온갖 행복과 평온, 도무지 풀어낼 수 없었던 결백과 묵인의 뒤얽힌 갖가지 가능성, 그 모든 것이 이 작자의 뒤틀어 대는 짓에 휘둘리고 있음을 나는 한순간에 깨달았다.\n\n\"그 양반은 사무소에 들어올 때마다 꼭 절 부려 먹고 이리저리 떠밀지 않고는 못 배겼어요.\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"도련님 식으로 치면 점잖은 신사 나리 가운데 하나였지요! 저야 더없이 온순하고 미천했고요 — 지금도 그렇습니다만. 한데 저는 그런 짓거리가 싫었어요 — 지금도 싫고요!\"\n\n그는 턱 긁기를 멈추고 두 볼을 안으로 빨아들였는데, 그러자 볼이 입 안에서 맞닿을 것만 같았다. 그러면서도 줄곧 곁눈으로 나를 살폈다.\n\n\"그 부인은 도련님 식으로 치면 어여쁜 여인네 가운데 하나지요, 정말요.\" 그가 천천히 얼굴을 본디 모양으로 되돌려 놓고 나서 말을 이었다. \"그리고 저 같은 인간한테는 벗이 되어 줄 마음이 조금도 없는 분이고요, 그건 제가 압니다. 우리 애그니스를 더 높은 부류의 놀음판에 끌어들일 만한 바로 그런 분이에요. 자, 저는 여자한테 살가운 부류가 아닙니다, 코퍼필드 도련님. 하지만 저는 꽤 오래전부터 머리에 눈깔이 박혀 있었거든요. 우리 같은 미천한 것들도 대개는 눈이 있고요 — 그 눈으로 살핍니다.\"\n\n나는 아무것도 모르는 척, 마음이 흔들리지 않은 척 보이려고 애썼지만, 그의 얼굴을 보니 그 노력이 영 신통치 못했음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"자, 저는 짓밟히고 가만있을 생각이 없어요, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 붉은 눈썹이 있었다면 그 자리였을 부분을 악의에 찬 의기양양함으로 치켜올리며 이어 갔다. \"그래서 저는 이 우정을 막으려고 제가 할 수 있는 일을 할 겁니다. 저는 이걸 못마땅하게 여기거든요. 솔직히 말씀드려, 저는 좀 인색하게 따지는 성미라서, 끼어드는 자들은 죄다 떼어 놓고 싶어 한다는 걸 인정하는 데 거리낌이 없습니다. 제가 아는 한, 저는 음모에 당할 위험을 무릅쓸 생각이 없어요.\"\n\n\"내 보기엔, 너는 늘 음모를 꾸미면서, 남들도 다 그러려니 하고 스스로를 속이는 것 같구나.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"하지만 제 동업자 양반 말마따나, 저한테는 동기가 있고, 저는 그걸 이를 악물고 손톱이 빠지도록 파고듭니다. 저는 미천한 인간이라고 너무 함부로 당해서는 안 되거든요. 제 앞길에 사람들이 가로막고 서게 둘 수는 없어요. 정말이지 그 사람들은 수레에서 내려와야 합니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!\"\n\n\"무슨 소린지 모르겠다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 모르시겠어요?\" 그가 특유의 움찔하는 몸짓을 하며 받았다. \"그것참 놀랍네요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 늘 그렇게 빠르신 분이! 다음번엔 좀 더 알아듣기 쉽게 해 보지요. — 저기 문에서 종을 울리고 있는 게 말 위에 앉은 몰던 씨 아닌가요, 나리?\"\n\n\"그 사람 같군.\" 나는 되도록 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n유라이어는 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 두 손을 커다란 혹 같은 무릎 사이에 끼우고 몸을 둘로 접듯 구부려 웃어 댔다. 완벽하게 소리 없는 웃음이었다. 그에게서는 단 한 가지 소리도 새어 나오지 않았다. 나는 그의 역겨운 행태에, 특히 이 마지막 꼴에 어찌나 진저리가 났던지, 인사치레도 없이 돌아서서, 받쳐 줄 것이 없어 휘청거리는 허수아비처럼 정원 한복판에 몸을 접고 선 그를 그대로 두고 와 버렸다.\n\n내가 애그니스를 데리고 도라를 보러 간 것은 그날 저녁이 아니라, 내 기억으로는 그다음다음 날 저녁, 그러니까 일요일이었다. 나는 미리 라비니아 양과 그 방문을 의논해 두었고, 애그니스는 차 마실 시간에 오기로 되어 있었다.\n\n나는 자랑스러움과 불안으로 마음이 들떠 있었다. 내 사랑스러운 어린 약혼녀가 자랑스러웠고, 애그니스가 그 애를 마음에 들어 했으면 하는 불안이 있었다. 퍼트니로 가는 내내, 애그니스는 역마차 안에 타고 나는 바깥에 탄 채로, 나는 내가 너무도 잘 아는 도라의 그 어여쁜 표정 하나하나를 마음속에 그려 보았다. 어떤 때는 그 애가 꼭 이런 때에 짓던 그 표정 그대로였으면 좋겠다고 마음을 정했다가, 또 어떤 때는 저런 다른 때에 짓던 표정이 더 낫지 않을까 망설였다. 그러다 거의 그 일로 안달이 나서 열병에 걸릴 지경이 되었다.\n\n어쨌거나 그 애가 무척 예쁘리라는 데는 조금도 의심이 없었다. 그런데 마침 그날 나는 그 애가 그토록 고와 보인 적이 없는 모습을 보게 되었다. 내가 그 작은 고모들에게 애그니스를 인사시킬 때 그 애는 응접실에 없었고, 수줍게 자리를 피하고 있었다. 이제 나는 그 애를 어디서 찾아야 할지 알았다. 아니나 다를까, 그 애는 여전히 그 칙칙하고 낡은 문 뒤에서 또 두 귀를 틀어막고 있었다.\n\n처음에 그 애는 도무지 나오려 하지 않았다. 그러더니 내 시계로 오 분만 더 있게 해 달라고 사정했다. 마침내 그 애가 응접실로 데려가 달라며 내 팔에 제 팔을 끼웠을 때, 그 매력적인 작은 얼굴은 발그레 상기되어 그토록 예뻐 보인 적이 없었다. 그런데 우리가 방에 들어서면서 그 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리자, 그 애는 그보다 만 배는 더 예뻤다.\n\n도라는 애그니스를 두려워했다. 그 애는 애그니스가 '너무 똑똑하다'는 걸 알고 있다고 나한테 말한 적이 있었다. 하지만 애그니스가 그토록 환하면서도 그토록 진지하고, 그토록 사려 깊고, 그토록 착하게 자기를 바라보는 모습을 보자, 그 애는 반가운 놀라움에 가냘프게 작은 소리를 내지르고는, 다정한 두 팔을 애그니스의 목에 두르고, 그 순진한 뺨을 애그니스의 얼굴에 갖다 댔다.\n\n나는 그토록 행복한 적이 없었다. 그 둘이 나란히 함께 앉는 모습을 보았을 때만큼 기뻤던 적이 없었다. 내 어린 연인이 그 다정한 두 눈을 그토록 자연스럽게 올려다보는 모습을 보았을 때만큼. 애그니스가 그 애에게 보내는 그 부드럽고 아름다운 눈길을 보았을 때만큼.\n\n라비니아 양과 클라리사 양도 그들 나름의 방식으로 내 기쁨을 함께 나누었다. 세상에서 가장 즐거운 찻상이었다. 클라리사 양이 자리를 주관했다. 내가 캐러웨이 씨앗 케이크를 잘라 돌렸다 — 그 작은 자매는 새처럼 씨앗을 쪼아 먹고 설탕을 콕콕 찍어 먹는 걸 좋아했다. 라비니아 양은 마치 우리의 행복한 사랑이 모두 제 솜씨이기라도 한 양 인자하게 후원하는 눈길로 바라보았고, 우리는 우리 자신에게도 서로에게도 더없이 흡족했다.\n\n애그니스의 부드러운 쾌활함은 그들 모두의 마음을 사로잡았다. 도라가 관심을 갖는 모든 것에 보이는 조용한 관심, 집(곧바로 화답한)과 친해지는 방식, 도라가 부끄러워 늘 앉던 내 옆자리로 건너오지 못할 때 보여 준 그 상냥한 솜씨, 도라에게서 발그레한 신뢰의 작은 표시를 한 무더기나 끌어낸 그 수수한 우아함과 편안함 — 그 모든 것이 우리의 모임을 더없이 완전하게 만들어 주는 듯했다.\n\n\"정말 기뻐요.\" 차를 마시고 나서 도라가 말했다. \"절 좋아해 주셔서요. 그러실 줄 몰랐거든요. 그리고 줄리아 밀스도 떠나 버린 지금, 저는 그 어느 때보다도 사랑받고 싶어요.\"\n\n그러고 보니 이걸 미처 말하지 못했다. 밀스 양은 배를 타고 떠나 버렸고, 도라와 나는 그레이브젠드까지 가서 커다란 동인도 무역선에 올라 그녀를 배웅했다. 우리는 점심으로 설탕에 절인 생강과 구아바, 그 밖의 그런 진미들을 먹었다. 그리고 우리는 후갑판 위 접의자에 앉아 우는 밀스 양을 두고 왔는데, 그녀는 커다란 새 일기장을 옆구리에 끼고 있었고, 거기에다 대양을 바라보며 떠오른 독창적인 사색들을 자물쇠로 채워 적어 둘 작정이었다.\n\n애그니스는 내가 자기를 별 볼 일 없는 사람으로 소개했나 보다며 걱정스러워했지만, 도라가 곧장 바로잡았다.\n\n\"어머, 아니에요!\" 그 애가 나를 향해 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"전부 칭찬이었어요. 이이가 언니 의견을 어찌나 대단하게 여기는지, 저는 그게 다 무서울 정도였다니까요.\"\n\n\"제 좋은 평가가, 그분이 아는 어떤 사람들에 대한 그분의 애정을 더 굳게 해 줄 수는 없어요.\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그 사람들이 받을 만한 가치도 없는 거고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 부디 저한테는 주세요.\" 도라가 어리광 부리는 투로 말했다. \"주실 수 있다면요!\"\n\n우리는 도라가 사랑받고 싶어 한다며 즐겁게 웃었고, 도라는 내가 멍청이라고, 어쨌거나 자기는 날 좋아하지 않는다고 했다. 그렇게 짧은 저녁이 거미줄 같은 날개를 달고 훌훌 날아가 버렸다. 마차가 우리를 데리러 올 시각이 가까워지고 있었다. 내가 난로 앞에 혼자 서 있는데, 도라가 내가 떠나기 전에 늘 주던 그 소중한 작은 입맞춤을 해 주려고 살그머니 들어왔다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 도디, 내가 오래전부터 애그니스 언니를 벗으로 두었더라면,\" 도라가 그 반짝이는 눈을 유난히 환하게 빛내며, 작은 오른손으로 내 외투 단추 하나를 무심히 만지작거리며 말했다. \"어쩌면 내가 좀 더 똑똑해졌을까요?\"\n\n\"내 사랑!\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리야!\"\n\n\"그게 말도 안 되는 소리 같아요?\" 도라가 나를 보지도 않고 되물었다. \"정말 그래요?\"\n\"당연하지!\"\n\n\"잊어버렸는데,\" 여전히 단추를 빙글빙글 돌리며 도라가 말했다. \"애그니스 언니가 당신하고 무슨 사이라고 했죠, 이 사랑스럽고 못된 사람?\"\n\n\"피붙이는 아니야.\" 내가 대답했다. \"하지만 우리는 오누이처럼 함께 자랐어.\"\n\n\"당신은 대체 왜 나 같은 사람하고 사랑에 빠졌을까?\" 도라가 내 외투의 또 다른 단추를 만지작거리기 시작하며 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 너를 보고도 사랑하지 않을 수가 없었기 때문이겠지, 도라!\"\n\n\"만약에 당신이 나를 아예 본 적이 없었더라면요.\" 도라가 또 다른 단추로 옮겨 가며 말했다.\n\n\"만약에 우리가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면!\" 내가 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n내 외투 단추 줄을 따라 올라오는 그 작고 보드라운 손과, 내 가슴께에 닿아 늘어진 송이송이 머리채와, 그 무심한 손가락을 따라 살짝 들리는 내리깐 눈의 속눈썹을, 나는 감탄에 잠겨 말없이 바라보며, 그 애가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 궁금해했다. 마침내 그 애가 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보더니, 발끝으로 서서 평소보다 더 사뭇 생각에 잠긴 듯 그 소중한 작은 입맞춤을 해 주고는 — 한 번, 두 번, 세 번 — 방을 나갔다.\n\n그러고 나서 오 분도 안 되어 그들 모두가 함께 돌아왔는데, 그때쯤 도라의 그 평소답지 않던 사색은 말끔히 가셔 있었다. 그 애는 마차가 오기 전에 집에게 온갖 재주를 다 부리게 하겠다며 웃음 띤 얼굴로 단단히 마음먹고 있었다. 그것은 시간이 좀 걸렸는데(재주가 다양해서라기보다는 집이 영 내키지 않아 해서였다), 문간에서 마차 소리가 들렸을 때도 아직 끝나지 않은 채였다. 애그니스와 그 애 사이에 황급하지만 다정한 작별이 있었다. 도라는 애그니스에게 편지를 쓰기로 했고(자기 편지가 어리석어도 신경 쓰지 말라고 했다), 애그니스는 도라에게 편지를 쓰기로 했다. 그러고 둘은 마차 문 앞에서 두 번째로 작별했고, 도라가 라비니아 양의 만류에도 아랑곳없이 한 번 더 뛰어나와 마차 창 너머로 애그니스에게 편지 쓰는 걸 거듭 당부하고, 마부석에 앉은 나를 향해 곱슬머리를 흔들 때 세 번째로 작별했다.\n\n역마차는 우리를 코번트 가든 근처에 내려놓기로 되어 있었고, 거기서 우리는 하이게이트로 가는 다른 역마차로 갈아탈 참이었다. 나는 그 사이의 짧은 도보길이 못 견디게 기다려졌으니, 애그니스가 나에게 도라를 칭찬해 줄 수 있을 터였기 때문이다. 아, 그것은 얼마나 큰 칭찬이었던가! 그 애의 온갖 꾸밈없는 우아함이 가장 잘 드러난 채로, 내가 얻은 그 어여쁜 아이를 내가 더없이 부드럽게 보살피도록, 그 칭찬은 얼마나 사랑스럽고 뜨겁게 당부해 주었던가! 그러면서도 짐짓 그러는 티는 조금도 내지 않으면서, 내가 그 고아 같은 아이를 맡아 지키고 있다는 그 신성한 책임을 얼마나 사려 깊게 일깨워 주었던가!\n\n나는 그 어느 때도, 그날 밤 도라를 사랑한 것만큼 그토록 깊고 참되게 그 애를 사랑한 적이 없었다. 우리가 다시 마차에서 내려, 박사님 댁으로 이어지는 고요한 길을 별빛 아래 걷고 있을 때, 나는 애그니스에게 이게 다 네 덕분이라고 말했다.\n\n\"네가 그 애 곁에 앉아 있을 때,\" 내가 말했다. \"너는 내 수호천사이기도 한 만큼이나 그 애의 수호천사이기도 한 것 같았어. 지금도 그렇게 보여, 애그니스.\"\n\n\"보잘것없는 천사지.\" 애그니스가 받았다. \"하지만 진실한 천사야.\"\n\n내 가슴속으로 곧장 파고드는 그 맑은 목소리에, 나는 자연스레 이렇게 말하게 되었다.\n\n\"너한테만 있는 그 쾌활함이 — 내가 여태 본 그 누구에게도 없는 그 쾌활함이 — 오늘 보니 너무도 되살아나 있어서, 나는 네가 집에서 더 행복해진 게 아닐까 하는 희망을 품기 시작했어.\"\n\n\"내 안에서 더 행복해.\" 그 애가 말했다. \"나는 아주 명랑하고 마음이 가벼워.\"\n\n나는 위를 올려다보는 그 평온한 얼굴을 흘끗 보았고, 그 얼굴을 그토록 고귀해 보이게 한 것은 별빛이라고 생각했다.\n\n\"집에는 아무 변화도 없었어.\" 잠시 뒤 애그니스가 말했다.\n\n\"새삼스레 다시 들먹인 일은 없었고?\" 내가 말했다. \"— 너를 괴롭히고 싶진 않지만, 묻지 않을 수가 없구나 — 우리가 지난번 헤어질 때 이야기했던 그 일 말이야.\"\n\n\"응, 없었어.\" 그 애가 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 그 일을 정말 많이 생각했어.\"\n\n\"그 생각은 좀 덜 해야 해. 내가 결국에는 수수한 사랑과 진실을 믿고 의지한다는 걸 기억해 줘. 나 때문에 걱정하지 마, 트롯우드.\" 잠시 뒤 그 애가 덧붙였다. \"네가 내가 밟을까 봐 두려워하는 그 발걸음을, 나는 결코 내딛지 않을 거야.\"\n\n차분히 곰곰 따져 보던 어느 때든 내가 정말로 그것을 두려워한 적은 없었다고 생각하지만, 그래도 그 애의 진실한 입에서 직접 이 다짐을 듣고 나니 말할 수 없는 안도가 밀려왔다. 나는 그 마음을 진심으로 그 애에게 털어놓았다.\n\n\"그런데 이 방문이 끝나면,\" 내가 말했다. \"— 다시 단둘이 있을 기회가 없을지도 모르니까 — 애그니스, 네가 다시 런던에 오기까지는 얼마나 걸릴 것 같아?\"\n\n\"아마 한참 걸릴 거야.\" 그 애가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 — 아버지를 위해서는 — 집에 있는 게 가장 좋을 것 같아. 앞으로 한동안은 자주 못 만나겠지. 하지만 나는 도라하고 부지런히 편지를 주고받을 거고, 그렇게 해서 우리는 서로 소식을 자주 듣게 될 거야.\"\n\n우리는 이제 박사님 오두막의 작은 안뜰 안에 들어와 있었다. 시각이 늦어 가고 있었다. 스트롱 부인 방 창문에 불이 켜져 있었고, 애그니스는 그쪽을 가리키며 내게 잘 자라고 인사했다.\n\n\"우리 불행이나 근심 때문에 마음 쓰지 마.\" 그 애가 내게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"나는 네 행복보다 더 큰 행복을 어디서도 못 느껴. 혹시라도 네가 나를 도와줄 수 있을 때가 오면, 틀림없이 내가 너한테 부탁할 테니 믿어 줘. 늘 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\" 그 애의 환한 미소 속에서, 그리고 그 명랑한 목소리의 마지막 가락 속에서, 나는 그 애와 함께 있는 내 어린 도라를 다시 보고 듣는 것만 같았다. 나는 사랑과 고마움으로 가슴이 벅찬 채, 한동안 현관 처마 사이로 별들을 바라보며 서 있다가, 천천히 걸어 나왔다. 나는 가까운 점잖은 술집에 잠자리를 잡아 두었고, 막 대문을 나서던 참이었는데, 우연히 고개를 돌렸다가 박사님 서재에 불이 켜진 것을 보았다. 내 도움 없이 박사님이 사전 작업을 하고 계셨던 게 아닐까 하는, 절반쯤 자책 어린 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 정말 그런지 살펴볼 겸, 어쨌든 박사님이 아직 책들 사이에 앉아 계시다면 잘 주무시라는 인사라도 드릴 겸, 나는 발길을 돌려 살그머니 현관을 가로질러 문을 가만히 열고 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n갓을 씌운 등불의 은은한 빛 속에서 내가 맨 먼저 본 사람은, 놀랍게도 유라이어였다. 그는 그 등불 바로 곁에 서서, 해골 같은 한 손을 입에 갖다 대고, 다른 손은 박사님 책상 위에 얹은 채였다. 박사님은 서재 의자에 앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 계셨다. 위크필드 씨는 몹시 괴롭고 비통한 얼굴로, 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채 머뭇거리며 박사님 팔을 만지고 있었다.\n\n순간 나는 박사님이 편찮으신 줄로만 알았다. 그런 생각에 황급히 한 걸음 나아갔다가, 유라이어의 눈과 마주치고서야 무슨 일인지 알아챘다. 나는 물러나려 했지만, 박사님이 나를 붙잡으려는 몸짓을 하셔서 그대로 남았다.\n\n\"어쨌든,\" 유라이어가 그 볼썽사나운 몸을 한 번 비틀며 말했다. \"문은 닫아 두는 게 좋겠지요. 온 동네에 다 알릴 필요는 없으니까요.\"\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그는 내가 열어 둔 문으로 발끝을 세워 다가가 조심스레 닫았다. 그러고는 다시 돌아와 아까의 자리에 섰다. 그의 목소리와 태도에는 동정하는 척하는 열성이 거슬리게 드러나 있었는데, 그것은 그가 꾸며 보일 수 있는 그 어떤 태도보다도 — 적어도 나에게는 — 더 견디기 어려웠다.\n\n\"저는 마땅히 그래야 할 것 같아서요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"도련님과 제가 이미 이야기 나눴던 그 일을 스트롱 박사님께 짚어 드렸습니다. 그런데 도련님은 제 말뜻을 제대로 못 알아들으셨지요?\"\n\n나는 그를 한 번 노려보았을 뿐 다른 대답은 하지 않았다. 그리고 나의 인자하신 옛 스승님께 다가가, 위로와 격려가 되었으면 하는 마음으로 몇 마디 말씀을 드렸다. 박사님은 내가 아주 어린 꼬마였을 적에 늘 그러시던 대로 내 어깨에 손을 얹으셨지만, 그 백발 머리는 들지 않으셨다.\n\n\"도련님이 제 말뜻을 못 알아들으셨으니,\" 유라이어가 똑같이 주제넘은 태도로 말을 이었다. \"이렇게 벗들끼리 모인 자리에서 외람되지만 미천하게나마 말씀드리자면, 제가 스트롱 박사님께 스트롱 부인의 행실에 대해 주의를 환기해 드렸습니다. 정말이지, 코퍼필드, 이렇게 불쾌한 일에 끼어드는 게 저로서는 무척 내키지 않는다는 걸 알아주세요. 하지만 사실 이대로 가다가는 우리 모두가 있어서는 안 될 일에 얽혀 들게 되거든요. 도련님이 제 말뜻을 못 알아들으셨을 때, 제가 하려던 말이 바로 그거였습니다, 나리.\" 지금 그의 곁눈질을 떠올리면, 그때 내가 그의 멱살을 잡고 숨이 끊어지도록 흔들어 대지 않은 것이 의아할 지경이다.\n\n\"제가 제 뜻을 그리 분명히 밝히지 못했나 봅니다.\" 그가 말을 이어 갔다. \"도련님도 그러셨고요. 하긴 그런 화제는 우리 둘 다 멀찍이 피하고 싶었을 테지요. 한데 결국 저는 터놓고 말하기로 마음을 굳혔고, 스트롱 박사님께 말씀드렸습니다 — 뭐라고 하셨나요, 나리?\"\n\n이것은 신음 소리를 내신 박사님을 향한 말이었다. 그 소리는 어떤 사람의 가슴이든 다 울렸을 것 같았지만, 유라이어의 가슴에는 아무 영향도 미치지 못했다.\n\n\"— 스트롱 박사님께 말씀드렸습니다.\" 그가 계속했다. \"몰던 씨와, 스트롱 박사님 부인이신 그 어여쁘고 상냥한 부인이 서로에게 너무 다정하다는 건 누구든 알아볼 수 있다고요. 정말이지 이제는 (지금 우리 모두가 있어서는 안 될 일에 얽혀 들고 있으니) 스트롱 박사님께 말씀드려야 할 때입니다, 몰던 씨가 인도로 떠나기 전에 이미 이 일이 햇빛처럼 누구에게나 훤히 보였다는 것을요. 몰던 씨가 다른 까닭은 전혀 없이 오로지 그것 때문에 핑계를 만들어 돌아왔다는 것을요. 그리고 그가 다른 까닭 없이 오로지 그것 때문에 늘 여기 와 있다는 것을요. 나리께서 들어오셨을 때, 저는 마침 제 동업자 양반한테,\" 그가 그쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"스트롱 박사님께 자기 명예와 양심을 걸고, 오래전부터 정말 이런 의견을 품어 왔는지 아닌지 말씀드려 보라고 청하던 참이었습니다. 자, 위크필드 씨, 나리! 저희한테 말씀해 주시겠습니까? 네입니까, 아니요입니까, 나리? 자, 동업자 양반!\"\n\n\"제발 부탁이오, 박사님,\" 위크필드 씨가 다시 그 머뭇거리는 손을 박사님 팔에 얹으며 말했다. \"내가 품었을지도 모를 어떤 의심에도 너무 무게를 두지 마시오.\"\n\n\"저것 보십시오!\" 유라이어가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 외쳤다. \"얼마나 서글픈 확증입니까, 안 그래요? 저 양반이요! 저런 오랜 벗이! 맙소사, 제가 한낱 저분 사무소 서기에 지나지 않았을 적에도, 코퍼필드, 저는 저분이 이 일로 어쩔 줄 몰라 하시는 걸 한두 번도 아니고 스무 번은 보았어요 — 아주 마음을 졸이셨지요 (그야 아버지로서 지극히 당연한 일이고요. 정말이지 저로서는 저분을 탓할 수 없습니다), 애그니스 아가씨가 있어서는 안 될 일에 얽혀 들고 있다는 생각에요.\"\n\n\"이보게, 스트롱,\" 위크필드 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"내 좋은 벗이여, 굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 누구한테서나 어떤 으뜸가는 동기를 찾으려 들고, 모든 행동을 하나의 좁은 잣대로 재 보려는 것이 내 못된 버릇이었네. 그런 잘못 때문에 내가 품었던 그런 의심에 빠져들었는지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"자네는 의심을 품었어, 위크필드.\" 박사님이 고개를 들지 않은 채 말씀하셨다. \"자네는 의심을 품었어.\"\n\n\"말씀하세요, 동업자 양반.\" 유라이어가 다그쳤다.\n\n\"한때는, 분명히 그랬소.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"나는 — 하느님, 용서하소서 — 자네가 그런 줄로만 알았네.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야!\" 박사님이 더없이 애절한 비탄에 잠긴 목소리로 받으셨다.\n\"한때 나는,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 바람직한 별거를 이루려고 몰던을 외국으로 보내고 싶어 하는 줄로 알았네.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야!\" 박사님이 받으셨다. \"애니에게 기쁨을 주려고, 그 애 어린 시절의 동무에게 무슨 자리라도 마련해 주려던 것뿐이었네. 다른 뜻은 전혀 없었어.\"\n\n\"나도 그런 줄 알게 됐지.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 그렇게 말해 주니 의심할 수가 없더군. 하지만 나는 생각했네 — 부디 그 좁은 해석이 내 고질병 같은 죄였음을 기억해 주게 — 나이 차가 그토록 많이 나는 경우에는—\"\n\n\"보십시오, 바로 저렇게 둘러대는 거지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어가 아첨하면서도 모욕적인 연민을 띠며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"— 저토록 젊고, 저토록 매력이 넘치는 부인이라면, 자네를 향한 존경이 아무리 참되다 한들, 결혼할 때 오로지 세속적인 잇속만으로 마음이 움직였을 수도 있다고 말이네. 두루 좋은 쪽으로 작용했을지도 모를 그 무수한 감정과 사정은 내가 전혀 헤아리지 못했어. 제발 그것만은 기억해 주게!\"\n\n\"얼마나 점잖게 둘러대시는지!\" 유라이어가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"늘 한 가지 관점에서만 그 부인을 바라보았으니.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 자네에게 소중한 그 모든 것을 걸고, 내 오랜 벗이여, 부디 그게 어떤 것이었는지 헤아려 주게. 이제 나는 빠져나갈 길이 없으니 고백할 수밖에 없네—\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 거기서 빠져나갈 길은 없지요, 위크필드 씨, 나리,\" 유라이어가 한마디 했다. \"일이 이 지경에 이른 마당에는요.\"\n\n\"— 내가 정말로,\" 위크필드 씨가 어쩔 줄 몰라 정신없이 동업자를 흘끗거리며 말했다. \"정말로 그 부인을 의심했고, 자네에 대한 도리를 다하지 못한다고 여겼다는 것을. 그리고 이왕 다 말해야 한다면, 나는 이따금 애그니스가 그 부인과 그토록 허물없는 사이로 지내는 것이 — 내가 본 것을, 아니면 내 병든 억측으로 보았다고 여긴 것을 그 애가 보게 될까 봐 — 못마땅했다는 것을. 나는 이 일을 누구에게도 입 밖에 낸 적이 없네. 그 누구도 알게 할 생각이 없었어. 그리고 이 말을 듣는 자네에게는 끔찍한 일이겠지만,\" 위크필드 씨가 완전히 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"이 말을 하는 게 나에게 얼마나 끔찍한지를 자네가 안다면, 나를 가엾게 여겨 줄 걸세!\"\n\n박사님은 그 천성의 더없는 선량함으로 손을 내미셨다. 위크필드 씨는 고개를 숙인 채 그 손을 잠시 자기 손에 쥐고 있었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 유라이어가 갯장어처럼 그 침묵 속으로 몸을 비틀어 들어오며 말했다. \"이게 누구에게나 불쾌함으로 가득한 화제임은 분명합니다. 하지만 이왕 여기까지 온 마당이니, 외람되지만 코퍼필드도 이걸 눈치채고 있었다는 걸 말씀드려야겠네요.\"\n\n나는 그를 홱 돌아보며, 어떻게 감히 나를 끌어들이느냐고 따져 물었다!\n\n\"아이고! 참 친절하시네요, 코퍼필드,\" 유라이어가 온몸을 일렁이며 받았다. \"도련님 성품이 얼마나 다정한지야 우리 모두 알지요. 하지만 일전 그날 밤 제가 도련님께 말을 꺼낸 그 순간, 도련님은 제 말뜻을 아셨잖아요. 제 말뜻을 아셨다는 걸 도련님도 아시면서요, 코퍼필드. 발뺌하지 마세요! 도련님은 좋은 뜻으로 발뺌하시는 거지만, 그러지 마세요, 코퍼필드.\"\n\n나는 그 인자하신 옛 박사님의 온화한 눈길이 한순간 나에게로 향하는 것을 보았고, 나의 오랜 의구심과 기억의 고백이 내 얼굴에 너무도 또렷이 쓰여 있어 도저히 지나칠 수 없으리라는 것을 느꼈다. 길길이 날뛰어 봐야 소용없었다. 나는 그것을 되돌릴 수 없었다. 무슨 말을 한들 이미 한 말을 주워 담을 수는 없었다.\n\n우리는 다시 입을 다물었고, 박사님이 일어나 방을 두세 번 가로질러 걸으실 때까지 그렇게 있었다. 이윽고 박사님은 의자가 놓인 자리로 돌아오셔서, 그 등받이에 몸을 기대고, 이따금 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 대시면서, 내 생각으로는 그 어떤 가식으로도 얻지 못할 명예를 안겨 주는 그 수수한 정직함으로 말씀하셨다.\n\n\"내 잘못이 컸네. 정말이지 내 잘못이 무척 컸다고 생각하네. 나는 내 마음속 깊이 품고 있는 사람을 시련과 — 비방이라 부르겠네, 누구의 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에서 떠올랐을 뿐이라 해도 비방이라 부르겠네 — 그런 비방에 내맡겼어. 나만 아니었다면 그 사람은 결코 그런 일의 대상이 될 리 없었을 텐데.\"\n\n유라이어 힙이 코를 훌쩍이는 듯한 소리를 냈다. 동정을 나타내려는 것이었으리라.\n\n\"나만 아니었다면, 내 애니는,\" 박사님이 말씀하셨다. \"결코 그런 일의 대상이 될 리 없었을 걸세. 여러분, 아시다시피 나는 이제 늙었네. 오늘 밤, 나는 더 살아갈 까닭이 그리 많지 않다는 느낌이 드는구려. 하지만 내 목숨을 — 내 목숨을 걸고 맹세하겠소, 이 대화의 주인공이 된 그 사랑하는 부인의 진실과 명예를 두고서!\"\n\n기사도 정신을 가장 훌륭히 구현한 인물이라 해도, 화가가 일찍이 상상한 가장 잘생기고 가장 낭만적인 형상을 그대로 빚어낸 인물이라 해도, 이 소박한 노박사님보다 더 인상 깊고 더 가슴을 울리는 위엄으로 이 말을 할 수는 없었으리라.\n\n\"하지만 나는 부인할 준비가 되어 있지 않네.\" 박사님이 말을 이으셨다. \"— 아니, 어쩌면 나도 모르게, 어느 정도는 인정할 준비가 되어 있었는지도 모르지 — 내가 본의 아니게 그 부인을 불행한 결혼으로 옭아 넣었을지도 모른다는 것을. 나는 무언가를 살펴 헤아리는 데는 도무지 익숙지 못한 사람이야. 그러니 나이도 처지도 제각각인 여러 사람의 관찰이, 하나같이 너무도 분명히 한 방향을 (그것도 그토록 자연스러운 방향을) 가리키고 있다면, 그게 내 관찰보다 낫다고 믿지 않을 수가 없네.\"\n\n내가 다른 데서도 적었듯이, 나는 종종 박사님이 그 젊은 부인을 대하는 인자한 태도에 감탄하곤 했다. 그러나 이 자리에서 부인을 언급할 때마다 보이신 그 정중한 다정함, 그리고 부인의 결백에 대한 아주 사소한 의심마저 자기에게서 밀쳐 내시던 그 거의 경건하기까지 한 태도는, 내 눈에 그분을 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 드높여 놓았다.\n\n\"나는 그 부인이 아주 어렸을 때 결혼했네.\" 박사님이 말씀하셨다. \"그 사람의 인격이 채 영글기도 전에 내 사람으로 맞아들였지. 그것이 자라난 만큼은, 그것을 빚어 가는 게 내 행복이었네. 나는 그 사람의 아버지를 잘 알았어. 그 사람도 잘 알았고. 나는 그 모든 아름답고 어진 성품을 아끼는 마음에서, 내가 할 수 있는 것을 그 사람에게 가르쳐 주었네. 만약 내가 그 사람의 고마움과 애정을 이용해 (하지만 결코 그럴 뜻은 없었네) 그 사람에게 못할 짓을 했다면, 정말 그랬을까 두렵네만, 나는 마음속으로 그 부인에게 용서를 빌겠네!\"\n\n박사님은 방을 가로질러 걸으셨다가 다시 같은 자리로 돌아오셨다. 그 진심 어린 간절함 속에서, 죽인 목소리가 그러하듯 떨리는 손길로 의자를 움켜쥐신 채.\n\n\"나는 나 자신을, 인생의 위험과 풍파로부터 그 사람을 지켜 줄 피난처로 여겼네. 나이는 우리가 비록 어울리지 않아도, 그 사람이 나와 함께 평온하고 만족스럽게 살아가리라고 스스로를 타일렀어. 내가 그 사람을 자유롭게 놓아줄 날을, 그때도 여전히 젊고 여전히 아름답되 분별만큼은 더 무르익은 그 사람을 놓아줄 그날을, 나는 결코 헤아림에서 빼놓지 않았네 — 아니, 여러분 — 내 진실을 걸고서!\"\n\n그 소박한 모습이 그분의 충심과 너그러움으로 환히 빛나는 듯했다. 그분이 내뱉는 한마디 한마디에는, 그 어떤 다른 우아함으로도 실어 줄 수 없었을 힘이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n\"이 부인과 함께한 내 삶은 무척 행복했네. 오늘 밤까지, 나는 그 사람에게 큰 잘못을 저지른 그날을 끊임없이 축복할 까닭이 있었네.\"\n\n이 말들을 내뱉으면서 점점 더 떨려 가던 박사님의 목소리가 잠시 멎었다. 그러더니 다시 말을 이으셨다.\n\n\"내 꿈에서 한번 깨어나고 보니 — 나는 이런저런 방식으로 평생을 가엾은 몽상가로 살아왔네만 — 그 사람이 옛 동무, 자기와 처지가 같은 그 사람에게 어떤 아쉬운 마음을 품는 것이 얼마나 자연스러운 일인지 알겠네. 그 사람이 그를 어떤 순결한 아쉬움으로, 나만 아니었다면 어땠을까 하는 어떤 죄 없는 생각으로 바라본다는 것은, 안타깝게도 너무도 사실인 듯하네. 내가 보았으나 마음에 새겨 두지 않았던 많은 것들이, 이 견디기 힘든 지난 한 시간 동안 새로운 뜻을 띠고 나에게 되돌아왔어. 하지만 여러분, 그 이상으로는, 그 사랑하는 부인의 이름이 의심의 말 한마디나 숨결 하나와도 결코 엮여서는 안 되네.\"\n\n잠시 그분의 눈에 불이 일고 목소리가 단단해졌다. 잠시 그분은 다시 입을 다무셨다. 이윽고 그분은 아까처럼 말을 이어 가셨다.\n\n\"이제 내게 남은 일은, 내가 불러일으킨 그 불행을 아는 채로, 되도록 순순히 견뎌 내는 것뿐이네. 책망해야 할 사람은 그 사람이지 내가 아니야. 그 사람을 오해로부터, 내 벗들조차 피하지 못했던 그 잔인한 오해로부터 지켜 내는 것이 이제 내 의무가 되었네. 우리가 더 조용히 숨어 살수록, 나는 그 의무를 더 잘 다할 수 있겠지. 그리고 그때가 오면 — 하느님의 자비로운 뜻이라면, 부디 그때가 어서 오기를! — 내 죽음이 그 사람을 굴레에서 풀어 줄 그때가 오면, 나는 더없는 믿음과 사랑으로 그 명예로운 얼굴을 향해 두 눈을 감겠네. 그리고 그때는 아무 슬픔 없이, 그 사람을 더 행복하고 더 환한 나날로 떠나보내겠네.\"\n\n그분의 그 간절함과 선량함이, 그 더없이 수수한 태도로 한껏 꾸며지고 또 그 태도를 한껏 빛내면서, 내 두 눈에 차오르게 한 눈물 때문에 나는 그분을 볼 수가 없었다. 그분은 문 쪽으로 걸어가시면서 이렇게 덧붙이셨다.\n\n\"여러분, 나는 여러분에게 내 마음을 보여 드렸네. 여러분이 그것을 존중해 주리라 믿네. 오늘 밤 우리가 한 말은 두 번 다시 입에 올려서는 안 되네. 위크필드, 오랜 벗의 팔을 빌려 위층까지 좀 부축해 주겠나!\"\n\n위크필드 씨가 서둘러 그분께 다가갔다. 두 사람은 한마디도 주고받지 않고 함께 천천히 방을 나갔고, 유라이어는 그 뒷모습을 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어가 점잖은 척 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이 일이 예상했던 대로 흘러가진 않았네요. 저 늙은 학자 양반이 — 참으로 훌륭한 분이지요! — 벽돌 조각처럼 눈이 멀어 버려서요. 하지만 이 집안은 수레에서 내렸다고 봐야겠지요!\"\n\n그의 목소리를 듣는 것만으로도, 나는 그전에도 없었고 그 뒤로도 없었을 만큼 미친 듯이 격분했다.\n\n\"이 악당아,\" 내가 말했다. \"네 음모에 나를 끌어들이다니 무슨 수작이냐? 어떻게 감히 방금 마치 우리가 함께 의논이라도 했던 것처럼 나를 끌어대느냐, 이 거짓말쟁이 악당아?\"\n\n우리가 얼굴을 맞대고 서 있을 때, 나는 그 얼굴에 슬그머니 떠오른 의기양양함에서, 내가 이미 너무도 분명히 알고 있던 것을 너무도 분명히 보았다. 곧, 그가 나를 비참하게 만들 작정으로 일부러 자기 속내를 나에게 들이밀었고, 바로 이 일에서 나에게 계획된 덫을 놓았다는 것을. 나는 도저히 참을 수가 없었다. 그 길쭉한 볼 전체가 나를 부추기듯 눈앞에 있었고, 나는 손가락이 데기라도 한 듯 얼얼하도록 그 볼을 펼친 손바닥으로 후려쳤다.\n\n그는 그 손을 제 손으로 붙잡았고, 우리는 그렇게 손을 맞잡은 채 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다. 우리는 그렇게 한참을 서 있었다. 그 깊고 붉은 볼에서 내 손가락의 하얀 자국이 사라지고, 그 자리가 더 짙게 붉어지는 것을 내가 볼 수 있을 만큼 오래도록.\n\n\"코퍼필드,\" 마침내 그가 숨 가쁜 목소리로 말했다. \"정신이 나가셨어요?\"\n\n\"너하고는 끝이다.\" 나는 손을 홱 빼내며 말했다. \"이 개 같은 놈, 너에 대해선 더는 아무것도 알고 싶지 않아.\"\n\n\"안 그러실걸요?\" 그가 볼이 아파 그쪽에 손을 갖다 대지 않을 수 없어 하며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그러고 싶어도 못 그러실 거예요. 이건 좀 배은망덕한 짓 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"나는 너를 경멸한다는 걸 그동안 충분히 보여 줬어.\" 내가 말했다. \"지금은 더 분명히 보여 줬고. 네가 네 주위 사람들 모두에게 못된 짓을 다 한다 한들 내가 왜 두려워해야 하지? 너야 늘 그것 말고 다른 짓을 하는 게 있느냐?\"\n\n그는 지금까지 내가 그와 주고받는 말에서 나를 자제하게 했던 그 사정에 대한 이 빗댄 말을 완벽하게 알아들었다. 그날 밤 애그니스에게서 받은 그 다짐이 아니었다면, 그 따귀도, 그 빗댄 말도 내 입에서 나오지 않았으리라 싶다. 하지만 이제 와 그게 무슨 상관이랴.\n\n또 한 번 긴 침묵이 흘렀다. 나를 바라보는 그의 두 눈은, 눈을 추하게 만들 수 있는 온갖 빛깔을 다 띠는 듯했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드,\" 그가 볼에서 손을 떼며 말했다. \"당신은 늘 저한테 맞서 왔어요. 위크필드 씨 댁에서도 늘 저한테 맞서곤 하셨다는 걸 저는 압니다.\"\n\n\"네 멋대로 생각해라.\" 나는 여전히 노여움이 머리끝까지 치민 채 말했다. \"그게 사실이 아니라면, 그만큼 너는 더 가치 있는 인간이겠지.\"\n\n\"그런데도 저는 늘 당신이 좋았어요, 코퍼필드!\" 그가 받아쳤다.\n\n나는 그에게 대꾸할 가치도 못 느꼈다. 그래서 모자를 집어 들고 잠자리로 나가려는데, 그가 나와 문 사이에 끼어들었다.\n\n\"코퍼필드,\" 그가 말했다. \"싸움이란 두 편이 있어야 하는 법이지요. 저는 그 한 편이 되지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"꺼져 버려!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 말 마세요!\" 그가 받았다. \"나중에 후회하실 거라는 걸 저는 압니다. 어떻게 그렇게 못된 심보를 내보이면서 스스로를 저보다 못한 사람으로 깎아내리실 수 있어요? 하지만 저는 당신을 용서합니다.\"\n\n\"네가 나를 용서한다고!\" 나는 경멸을 담아 되뇌었다.\n\n\"용서합니다, 그리고 당신은 어쩔 도리가 없어요.\" 유라이어가 받았다. \"늘 당신의 벗이었던 저를 가서 후려치다니요! 하지만 싸움이란 두 편이 없으면 안 되고, 저는 그 한 편이 되지 않겠습니다. 저는 당신이 마다해도 당신의 벗이 되겠어요. 자, 이제 당신이 뭘 각오해야 하는지 아시겠지요.\"\n\n이 대화를(그의 몫은 무척 느렸고 내 몫은 무척 빨랐다), 이런 때아닌 시각에 집 안 사람들을 깨우지 않도록 낮은 목소리로 이어 가야 한다는 점이 내 화를 가라앉히는 데는 도움이 안 됐다. 그래도 내 격정은 차츰 식어 갔다. 나는 그저, 내가 늘 너에게서 기대해 온 것을, 그리고 여태 한 번도 어긋난 적 없던 그것을 앞으로도 기대하겠노라고만 말하고는, 그가 마치 까 보라고 거기 놓인 커다란 호두라도 되는 양 문을 열어젖히고 집을 나섰다. 하지만 그도 집 밖, 제 어머니의 셋방에서 잤기에, 내가 몇백 야드도 채 못 가서 그가 따라붙었다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 코퍼필드,\" 그가 내 귀에 대고 말했다(나는 고개를 돌리지 않았다). \"당신은 지금 영 그릇된 처지에 놓여 있어요.\" 나는 그게 사실임을 느꼈고, 그래서 더 약이 올랐다. \"이걸 용감한 일로 꾸밀 수도 없고, 용서받지 않을 도리도 없지요. 저는 어머니한테도, 살아 있는 그 누구한테도 이걸 말할 생각이 없습니다. 저는 당신을 용서하기로 단단히 마음먹었어요. 하지만 그토록 미천한 줄 빤히 아는 사람한테 손찌검을 하시다니, 그건 정말 의아하네요!\"\n\n나는 그저 그보다 덜 비열한 인간일 뿐이라고 느꼈다. 그는 나 자신보다도 나를 더 잘 알았다. 그가 맞받아쳤거나 대놓고 나를 약 올렸더라면, 그건 차라리 한숨 돌릴 일이자 내 행동의 변명거리가 되었을 것이다. 하지만 그는 나를 약한 불 위에 올려놓았고, 나는 그 위에서 밤의 절반을 시달리며 누워 있었다.\n\n아침에 내가 밖으로 나왔을 때, 이른 교회 종이 울리고 있었고, 그는 제 어머니와 함께 거닐고 있었다. 그는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 나에게 말을 걸었고, 나도 대꾸하지 않을 수 없었다. 짐작건대 나는 그가 치통을 앓을 만큼 세게 후려쳤던 모양이다. 어쨌거나 그의 얼굴은 검은 비단 손수건으로 동여매여 있었고, 그 위에 모자를 얹은 꼴은 그의 행색을 나아 보이게 하기는커녕 영 딴판이었다. 듣자 하니 그는 월요일 아침에 런던의 어느 치과에 가서 이를 하나 뺐다고 했다. 부디 그게 어금니였기를 바란다.\n\n박사님은 몸이 좀 편찮으시다고 알리고는, 남은 방문 기간 내내 날마다 상당한 시간을 혼자 지내셨다. 애그니스와 그 아버지가 떠난 지 일주일이 지나서야 우리는 평소의 작업을 다시 시작했다. 작업을 다시 시작하기 전날, 박사님은 봉인하지 않은 접힌 쪽지를 손수 나에게 건네셨다. 그것은 나에게 보내는 것이었는데, 다정한 몇 마디로, 그날 밤의 그 일은 결코 다시 입에 올리지 말라는 당부를 담고 있었다. 나는 그 일을 고모님께만 털어놓았고, 다른 누구에게도 말하지 않았다. 그것은 내가 애그니스와 의논할 만한 일이 아니었고, 애그니스 역시 그동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 조금도 눈치채지 못한 게 분명했다.\n\n내가 확신하건대, 그때는 스트롱 부인도 마찬가지였다. 부인에게서 아주 작은 변화라도 보이기까지는 몇 주가 흘렀다. 그것은 바람 한 점 없을 때 끼는 구름처럼 천천히 다가왔다. 처음에 부인은 박사님이 자기에게 말을 건넬 때 보이는 그 부드러운 연민에, 그리고 그 따분하고 단조로운 생활을 덜어 주려고 어머니를 곁에 두면 좋겠다는 박사님의 바람에 어리둥절해하는 듯했다. 우리가 작업을 하고 부인이 곁에 앉아 있을 때면, 나는 종종 부인이 하던 것을 멈추고 그 잊을 수 없는 얼굴로 박사님을 바라보는 것을 보곤 했다. 그 뒤로 나는 이따금 부인이 눈에 눈물이 가득한 채 일어나 방을 나가는 것을 보았다. 차츰 불행한 그늘이 그 아름다움 위에 드리워, 날마다 짙어 갔다. 그 무렵 마클럼 부인은 오두막에 아예 눌러앉아 살았지만, 그녀는 떠들고 또 떠들 뿐, 아무것도 보지 못했다.\n\n한때 박사님 집안의 햇살 같던 애니에게 이 변화가 슬그머니 스며드는 동안, 박사님은 겉모습이 더 늙어 가고 더 무거워지셨다. 하지만 그분의 그 다정한 성미와 그 잔잔한 친절함과 부인을 향한 그 자애로운 염려는, 만약 더 늘어날 여지가 있었다면 더 늘어나 있었다. 부인의 생일날 이른 아침, 우리가 작업을 하는 동안 그녀가 창가에 앉으러 왔을 때(늘 해 오던 일이지만, 이제는 내가 보기에 무척 가슴 아프도록 머뭇거리고 머쓱한 기색으로 그러기 시작했다), 나는 박사님이 두 손으로 그녀의 이마를 감싸고 입을 맞추신 뒤, 더는 머물 수 없을 만큼 마음이 북받쳐 황급히 자리를 뜨시는 모습을 한 번 보았다. 나는 그녀가 박사님이 떠나신 자리에 조각상처럼 가만히 서 있다가, 이윽고 고개를 떨구고 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 뭐라 말할 수 없을 만큼 서글피 우는 것을 보았다.\n\n그 뒤로 이따금, 우리 둘만 남게 되는 틈에 그녀가 나에게라도 무슨 말을 하려 애쓰는 것만 같았다. 하지만 그녀는 끝내 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않았다. 박사님은 늘 그녀가 어머니와 함께 집을 떠나 무슨 놀이에 끼어들 만한 새 계획을 마련하셨다. 그리고 놀이라면 무척 좋아하고 다른 것에는 어찌나 쉽게 싫증을 내는지 모르는 마클럼 부인은, 더없이 흔쾌히 거기에 끼어들어 큰 소리로 그것들을 칭찬했다. 하지만 애니는 기운 없고 불행한 모습으로, 그저 이끄는 대로 따라갈 뿐, 무엇에도 도무지 마음을 두지 않는 듯했다.\n\n나는 어찌 생각해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 고모님도 마찬가지였으니, 분명 그 막막함 속에서 이런저런 때에 도합 백 마일은 걸어 다니셨을 것이다. 무엇보다 기이한 것은, 이 가정의 불행이 자리한 그 은밀한 영역으로 비집고 든 단 하나의 진정한 위안이, 다름 아닌 딕 씨라는 사람의 모습으로 그곳에 비집고 들었다는 점이다.\n\n이 일에 대해 그의 생각이 어떠했는지, 또는 그의 관찰이 어떠했는지를 나는 도무지 설명할 수가 없는데, 감히 말하건대 그 일을 거들어 달라 했어도 그 자신 역시 그러지 못했을 것이다. 하지만 내 학창 시절 이야기에 적어 두었듯이, 박사님을 향한 그의 숭배는 끝이 없었다. 그리고 참된 애정에는 — 그것이 미물 가운데 하나가 사람을 향해 품은 것일 때조차 — 더없이 높은 지성을 저만치 뒤로 따돌리는 어떤 섬세한 통찰이 깃들어 있는 법이다. 딕 씨 안에 있는 이 마음의 지성이라고 불러도 좋을 것에는, 진실의 어떤 밝은 빛줄기 하나가 곧장 내리꽂혔다.\n\n그는 캔터베리에서 박사님 산책로를 오르내리며 거닐던 데 익숙했던 것처럼, 한가한 시간이면 박사님과 함께 정원을 오르내리며 거니는 그 특권을 자랑스레 되찾았다. 그런데 사정이 이렇게 되자마자, 그는 한가한 시간을 죄다(그 시간을 더 늘리려고 더 일찍 일어나기까지 했다) 이 거닒에 바쳤다. 그가 박사님이 그 놀라운 역작인 사전을 자기에게 읽어 주실 때만큼 행복했던 적이 없었다면, 이제는 박사님이 그것을 주머니에서 꺼내 읽기 시작하지 않으면 영 비참해했다. 박사님과 내가 일에 매달려 있을 때면, 그는 이제 스트롱 부인과 함께 오르내리며 거닐고, 부인이 아끼는 꽃을 다듬거나 화단의 잡초를 뽑는 것을 돕는 버릇이 들었다. 감히 말하건대 그는 한 시간에 열 마디도 채 입에 올리지 않았을 것이다. 하지만 그의 그 조용한 관심과 애틋한 얼굴은 두 사람의 가슴에서 곧바로 화답을 얻었다. 저마다 상대도 그를 좋아한다는 것을, 그리고 그가 둘 다 사랑한다는 것을 알았다. 그리하여 그는 다른 누구도 될 수 없는 무엇이 되었으니 — 두 사람을 잇는 다리가 된 것이다.\n\n그가 그 헤아릴 길 없이 지혜로운 얼굴을 하고 박사님과 함께 오르내리며 거닐면서, 사전에 나오는 어려운 말들에 두들겨 맞는 것을 좋아하던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 그가 애니의 뒤를 따라 커다란 물뿌리개를 나르고, 정말이지 장갑이라는 앞발을 낀 채 무릎을 꿇고, 작은 잎사귀들 사이에서 참을성 있게 미세한 일을 하던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 어떤 철학자도 표현하지 못했을 것을, 그가 하는 모든 일에서, 그녀의 벗이 되고 싶다는 그 섬세한 바람으로 표현하던 모습을. 물뿌리개의 구멍이란 구멍에서마다 공감과 믿음과 애정을 쏟아붓던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 그가 불행이 말을 걸어오는 그 더 나은 정신을 잃고 헤매는 일이 결코 없었고, 그 불운한 찰스 왕을 정원에 끌어들이는 일도 결코 없었으며, 그 고마워하는 섬김에서 흔들리는 법도 결코 없었고, 무언가 잘못되어 있다는 자각이나 그것을 바로잡고 싶다는 바람에서 한눈파는 법도 결코 없던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 나는 내 멀쩡한 정신으로 해낸 그 모든 일을 다 따져 보더라도, 그가 정신이 온전치 못한 사람이라는 걸 알고 지냈다는 사실이 정말이지 거의 부끄러워질 지경이다.\n\n\"트롯, 저 사람이 어떤 사람인지는 나 말고는 아무도 몰라!\" 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나눌 때면 고모님이 자랑스레 말씀하시곤 했다. \"딕은 언젠가 이름을 떨치고 말 거야!\"\n\n이 장을 마치기 전에 한 가지 이야기를 더 짚어 두어야겠다. 박사님 댁의 방문이 아직 한창일 무렵, 나는 우편배달부가 아침마다 유라이어 힙 앞으로 두세 통의 편지를 가져오는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그는 한가한 때라 다른 사람들이 돌아간 뒤에도 하이게이트에 남아 있었다. 그리고 그 편지들은 늘 미코버 씨가 사무적인 필치로 겉봉을 쓴 것이었는데, 그는 이제 둥글둥글한 법률가 글씨체를 쓰고 있었다. 나는 이 사소한 단서들로 미루어 미코버 씨가 잘 지내고 있나 보다고 짐작하며 반가워했고, 그래서 이 무렵 그의 사랑스러운 아내에게서 다음과 같은 편지를 받았을 때 무척 놀랐다.\n\n'캔터베리, 월요일 저녁.\n\n'친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 이 편지를 받으시고 분명 놀라시겠지요. 그 내용에는 더더욱 놀라실 테고요. 그리고 제가 간곡히 부탁드리는 절대적인 비밀 엄수의 조건에는 한층 더 놀라실 겁니다. 하지만 아내로서, 또 어머니로서 제 심정이 풀어 놓을 길을 찾고 있는데, 친정 식구들(이미 미코버 씨의 심기를 거스르는 이들이지요)과 상의하고 싶지는 않으니, 제 벗이자 옛 하숙인인 당신만큼 조언을 구하기에 더 나은 사람을 저는 알지 못합니다.\n\n'친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 당신도 아시겠지만, 저와 미코버 씨(저는 결코 그이를 저버리지 않을 거예요) 사이에는 늘 서로를 믿는 마음이 지켜져 왔습니다. 미코버 씨가 이따금 저와 상의도 없이 어음을 끊었거나, 그 빚이 만기가 되는 시점을 두고 저를 오해하게 했을 수는 있어요. 실제로 그런 일이 있었고요. 하지만 대체로 미코버 씨는 애정 어린 품, 곧 자기 아내한테 비밀이 없었고, 잠자리에 들 때면 늘 그날 있었던 일들을 돌이켜 들려주곤 했습니다.\n\n'친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 미코버 씨가 완전히 변해 버렸다고 말씀드리면, 제 심정이 얼마나 사무치게 쓰라릴지 짐작이 가실 거예요. 그이는 입을 닫아 버렸어요. 비밀스러워졌고요. 기쁨도 슬픔도 나누는 동반자에게 — 다시금 자기 아내를 두고 하는 말입니다만 — 그이의 삶은 하나의 수수께끼가 되었어요. 그리고 그이가 아침부터 밤까지 사무소에서 시간을 보낸다는 것만 알 뿐, 이제 저는 그 삶에 대해, 식어 빠진 자두 죽을 두고 철없는 아이들이 부질없는 옛이야기를 읊어 대는 그 남쪽 나라 사내에 대해 아는 것보다도 더 모른다고 말씀드린다면, 그건 흔히들 빠지는 잘못된 표현을 빌려 어김없는 사실을 전하는 셈이겠지요.\n\n'하지만 이게 다가 아니에요. 미코버 씨는 시무룩해졌어요. 모질어졌고요. 큰아들과 큰딸과는 서먹해졌고, 쌍둥이를 자랑스러워하지도 않으며, 우리 식구가 된 지 얼마 안 되는, 아무 죄도 없는 그 갓난것조차 차가운 눈으로 바라봅니다. 우리 살림에 드는 돈은, 한 푼까지 바짝 쥐어짜 줄였는데도, 그이한테서 받아 내기가 여간 어려운 게 아니에요. 심지어 자기를 끝장내 버리겠다는(꼭 그 말 그대로예요) 무시무시한 으름장까지 놓는답니다. 그러면서도 이 사람을 미치게 만드는 그 처사에 대해서는 어떤 설명도 한사코 거절하고요.\n\n'이건 견디기 힘든 일이에요. 가슴이 미어지는 일이고요. 제 변변찮은 힘이 어떤지 아시는 당신이, 이토록 낯선 곤경에서 그 힘을 어떻게 쓰는 것이 가장 좋을지 조언해 주신다면, 이미 제게 베풀어 주신 그 숱한 다정한 은혜에 또 하나의 은혜를 더해 주시는 셈이 될 거예요. 아이들의 사랑과, 아무것도 모른 채 행복한 그 갓난것의 미소를 함께 보내며, 저는 그럼 이만 줄이겠습니다, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨,\n\n'시름에 잠긴 당신의 벗, '엠마 미코버 올림.'\n\n미코버 부인 정도의 경륜을 지닌 아내에게 내가 드릴 만한 권고라곤, 참을성과 다정함으로 미코버 씨를 되돌려 보라는 것밖에 (어차피 그녀라면 어떤 경우든 그러리라는 걸 나는 알았다) 달리 떳떳하게 내놓을 게 없었다. 하지만 이 편지는 나로 하여금 그에 대해 무척 많은 생각에 잠기게 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 43. ANOTHER RETROSPECT\n\n내 인생에서 잊지 못할 한 시절 앞에서, 다시 한번 발걸음을 멈추게 해 다오. 한쪽으로 비켜서서, 그 시절의 환영들이 내 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 모습을 — 나 자신의 그림자를 거느린 채, 어슴푸레한 행렬을 이루며 — 바라보게 해 다오.\n\n몇 주, 몇 달, 계절이 흘러간다. 그것들은 어느 여름날 하루와 어느 겨울 저녁 한때보다 그리 길게 느껴지지도 않는다. 어느 순간엔 도라와 함께 거니는 공유지가 온통 꽃으로 뒤덮여 눈부신 황금 들판을 이루고, 또 어느 순간엔 보이지 않던 히스가 눈 이불 밑에 둔덕과 무더기를 이루며 누워 있다. 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 일요일 산책길을 가로질러 흐르는 강물이 여름 햇살에 반짝이다가, 겨울바람에 잔물결을 일으키다가, 떠다니는 얼음덩이로 두꺼워진다. 그 어떤 강물이 바다를 향해 달려간 것보다도 더 빠르게, 강물은 번득이고, 어두워지고, 굽이쳐 흘러가 버린다.\n\n새 두 마리처럼 자그마한 두 부인의 집에서는 실오라기 하나 달라지지 않는다. 시계는 벽난로 위에서 똑딱거리고, 청우계는 복도에 걸려 있다. 시계도 청우계도 한 번도 맞은 적이 없으나, 우리는 둘 다 경건하게 믿는다.\n\n나는 법적으로 어엿한 성년이 되었다. 스물한 살의 위엄에 이르렀다. 하지만 이것은 누군가에게 떠밀려 얹어지는 종류의 위엄이다. 내가 무엇을 이루었는지 한번 생각해 보자.\n\n나는 그 사납던 속기의 비밀을 길들였다. 그것으로 그럭저럭 괜찮은 수입을 올린다. 그 기술에 관한 모든 분야에서 솜씨를 인정받아 명성이 높고, 다른 열한 사람과 함께 어느 조간신문을 위해 의회의 토론을 받아 적는다. 밤이면 밤마다, 나는 결코 들어맞지 않는 예언, 결코 지켜지지 않는 공언, 그저 사람들을 헷갈리게 할 목적뿐인 해명을 받아 적는다. 나는 말의 늪에서 뒹군다. 그 불운한 여인, 브리타니아는 늘 내 앞에 묶인 닭처럼 놓여 있다. 사무용 펜에 온몸이 줄줄이 꿰뚫리고, 붉은 끈으로 손발이 꽁꽁 묶인 채로. 나는 정치판 무대 뒤를 충분히 들여다봐서 정치 인생의 값어치를 안다. 나는 그 점에서는 완전한 불신자이고, 앞으로도 결코 개종하지 않을 것이다.\n\n내 다정한 옛 친구 트래들스도 같은 일에 손을 대 보았으나, 트래들스의 방식과는 맞지 않았다. 그는 자신의 실패를 두고도 더할 나위 없이 너그럽게 받아들이며, 자기는 늘 스스로 굼뜬 사람이라고 여겨 왔노라고 나에게 일깨워 준다. 그는 같은 신문에 이따금 일거리를 맡는데, 메마른 주제들의 사실을 그러모아 더 풍부한 머리를 가진 사람들이 글로 쓰고 살을 붙이도록 넘기는 일이다. 그는 변호사 자격을 얻었고, 감탄스러운 근면과 극기로 또 백 파운드를 그러모아, 자기가 드나드는 법률 사무실의 부동산 양도 전문 변호사에게 수업료로 바쳤다. 그의 변호사 임명식에서는 아주 뜨거운 포트와인이 어마어마하게 소비되었으니, 그 액수로 따지면 이너 템플은 그 일로 한몫 챙긴 게 분명하지 싶다.\n\n나는 또 다른 길로도 세상에 나왔다. 나는 두렵고 떨리는 마음으로 글쓰기에 손을 댔다. 남몰래 자그마한 것을 하나 써서 어느 잡지에 보냈더니, 그게 잡지에 실렸다. 그 뒤로 나는 용기를 내어 꽤 많은 자질구레한 글을 썼다. 이제는 그것들로 어엿이 원고료를 받는다. 통틀어 보면 나는 살림이 넉넉한 편이라, 내 수입을 왼손 손가락으로 꼽아 헤아릴 때면, 셋째 손가락을 지나 넷째 손가락 가운데 마디까지 이른다.\n\n우리는 버킹엄 거리에서, 내가 처음 열병에 들떴을 적에 눈여겨봐 두었던 집과 아주 가까운, 아담하고 정겨운 작은 오두막으로 이사했다. 그러나 고모님은 (도버의 집을 좋은 값에 파셨다) 여기 머무를 작정은 아니고, 바로 가까이에 있는 더 자그마한 오두막으로 거처를 옮기려 하신다. 이것이 무엇을 뜻하는가? 내 결혼인가? 그렇다!\n\n그렇다! 나는 도라와 결혼하려 한다! 러비니어 양과 클라리사 양도 승낙해 주었다. 카나리아 두 마리가 호들갑을 떤 적이 있다면, 바로 지금 그 두 사람이 그렇다. 러비니어 양은 내 사랑의 혼수 단속을 자청해 맡고서, 끊임없이 갈색 종이로 갑옷 같은 본을 떠 내며, 긴 옷감 꾸러미를 들고 야드 자를 옆구리에 낀 어느 점잖은 젊은이와 의견이 엇갈린다. 가슴에 늘 바늘과 실을 꽂고 다니는 양재사 한 사람이 그 집에 묵으며 일하는데, 내가 보기엔 먹을 때나 마실 때나 잘 때나 골무를 손에서 빼는 법이 없는 것 같다. 그들은 내 사랑을 마네킹으로 삼는다. 늘 그녀를 불러다가 이것저것 입혀 본다. 우리는 저녁에 단 오 분도 단둘이 다정히 있질 못하는데, 어김없이 어떤 주제넘은 여자가 문을 두드리고는 이렇게 말하기 때문이다. \"어머, 도라 아가씨, 죄송하지만 위층으로 좀 올라와 주시겠어요!\"\n\n클라리사 양과 고모님은 도라와 내가 살펴볼 가구를 찾으려고 런던 곳곳을 헤매고 다닌다. 차라리 이런 검사 절차 없이 그냥 물건을 사 버리는 편이 두 분에게 더 나으련만. 왜냐하면 우리가 부엌 난로 가리개와 고기 굽는 가림막을 보러 가면, 도라는 꼭대기에 작은 종들이 달린, 집의 중국식 개집을 보고는 차라리 그게 더 좋다고 하기 때문이다. 그리고 그걸 사고 나서도 집을 새 거처에 길들이는 데는 한참이 걸린다. 들어가거나 나올 때마다 집은 그 작은 종들을 죄다 울려 대며 무섭다고 질겁한다.\n\n패거티가 한몫 거들겠다고 올라와서는 곧장 일에 매달린다. 그녀가 맡은 분야는 모든 것을 닦고 또 닦는 일인 듯하다. 닦을 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든, 끊임없는 마찰로 제 정직한 이마처럼 반들반들 빛이 날 때까지 문지른다. 그리고 바로 이 무렵에, 나는 그녀의 외로운 오라비가 밤이면 어두운 거리를 지나가며, 걸으면서 떠돌이 얼굴들 사이를 살피는 모습을 보기 시작한다. 그런 시각엔 나는 결코 그에게 말을 걸지 않는다. 그의 무거운 모습이 앞으로 지나갈 때, 나는 그가 무엇을 찾고 무엇을 두려워하는지를 너무도 잘 알기 때문이다.\n\n오늘 오후 트래들스가 민사 법원으로 — 시간이 날 때면 나도 아직 형식상 가끔 들르는 곳이다 — 나를 찾아왔을 때, 어째서 그토록 거창한 표정을 짓는 걸까? 어린 시절 내 백일몽이 실현될 날이 코앞에 닥쳤다. 나는 혼인 허가증을 받으러 가는 길이다.\n\n이만한 일을 해내기에는 너무도 자그마한 서류다. 그것이 내 책상 위에 놓여 있는 것을, 트래들스는 반은 감탄하며 반은 경외하며 바라본다. 거기엔 그 달콤하고 오래되고 꿈결 같은 인연으로 맺어진 이름, 데이비드 코퍼필드와 도라 스펜로가 적혀 있다. 그리고 한쪽 귀퉁이엔, 인간사의 온갖 거래에 그토록 자애로이 관심을 기울이는 그 부모 같은 기관, 인지국이 우리 두 사람의 결합을 굽어보고 있다. 또한 캔터베리 대주교가 인쇄된 글자로 우리에게 축복을 빌어 주는데, 그것도 도무지 더는 바랄 수 없을 만큼 헐값에 해 주고 있다.\n\n그렇건만 나는 꿈속에 있다. 허둥대고, 행복하고, 정신없는 꿈속에. 나는 이 일이 정말로 일어나리라는 것을 믿을 수가 없다. 그러면서도, 길에서 스치는 사람마다 내가 모레 결혼한다는 것을 무언가 어렴풋이 알아챘으리라고 믿지 않을 수가 없다. 선서를 하러 내려갔을 때 대리 판사가 나를 알아보고는, 마치 우리 사이에 비밀결사 같은 묵계라도 있는 듯이 손쉽게 일을 처리해 준다. 트래들스는 전혀 필요하지 않으나, 내 전반적인 후원자로서 곁에 서 있다.\n\n\"다음번에 자네가 여기 올 땐 말이야,\" 나는 트래들스에게 말한다. \"자네 자신을 위한 같은 볼일로 오게 되길 바라네. 그리고 그게 어서 이루어지길 바라.\"\n\n\"좋게 빌어 줘서 고맙네, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답한다. \"나도 그러길 바라네. 그녀가 아무리 오래 걸려도 나를 기다려 줄 거라는 것, 그리고 그녀가 정말이지 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라는 것을 안다는 게 큰 위안이지—\"\n\n\"그녀를 역마차 정류장에서 언제 만나기로 했나?\" 내가 묻는다.\n\n\"일곱 시에.\" 트래들스가 자기의 수수하고 낡은 은시계를 들여다보며 말한다. 학창 시절에 물레방아를 만들겠다고 톱니바퀴 하나를 빼낸 적이 있던 바로 그 시계다. \"그쯤이면 위크필드 양이 오는 시각이지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"조금 더 이르네. 그녀는 여덟 시 반이야.\" \"정말이지 말이네,\" 트래들스가 말한다. \"이 일이 이토록 행복하게 마무리될 거라고 생각하니, 마치 내가 결혼하는 것이나 진배없이 기쁘다네. 그리고 정말로, 소피를 이 기쁜 자리에 직접 함께하게 해 주고, 위크필드 양과 더불어 신부 들러리로 청해 준 그 두터운 우정과 배려에는 가장 따뜻한 감사를 드려야 마땅하지. 나는 그걸 더없이 고맙게 여긴다네.\"\n\n나는 그의 말을 들으며 그와 악수한다. 그리고 우리는 이야기를 나누고, 걷고, 저녁을 먹고, 그런 식으로 시간을 보낸다. 그러나 나는 그것이 믿기지 않는다. 무엇 하나 실감 나지 않는다.\n\n소피가 때맞춰 도라의 고모들 집에 도착한다. 그녀는 더없이 호감 가는 얼굴을 지녔는데 — 빼어나게 아름답다고는 할 수 없으나, 더할 나위 없이 사랑스럽다 — 내가 여태껏 본 사람 가운데 가장 상냥하고, 꾸밈없고, 솔직하고, 마음을 사로잡는 이들 중 하나다. 트래들스는 한껏 자랑스러워하며 그녀를 우리에게 소개한다. 그리고 내가 한쪽 구석에서 그의 선택을 축하해 주자, 그는 머리카락 한 올 한 올이 까치발을 디딘 듯 곤두선 채로 시계로 꼬박 십 분 동안 두 손을 비벼 댄다.\n\n나는 캔터베리발 역마차에서 애그니스를 데려왔고, 그녀의 밝고 아름다운 얼굴이 두 번째로 우리 사이에 끼어든다. 애그니스는 트래들스를 무척 좋아하는데, 두 사람이 만나는 모습을 보는 것도, 트래들스가 세상에서 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 아가씨를 그녀에게 인사시키며 으스대는 모습을 지켜보는 것도 더할 나위 없이 즐겁다.\n\n그래도 나는 그것이 믿기지 않는다. 우리는 더없이 흐뭇한 저녁을 보내며 한껏 행복하지만, 나는 아직도 그것이 믿기지 않는다. 도무지 정신을 가다듬을 수가 없다. 행복이 일어나는 족족 그것을 헤아려 짚어 낼 수가 없다. 나는 안개에 휩싸인 듯 붕 뜬 상태에 빠져 있다. 마치 한두 주 전 어느 아침에 아주 일찍 일어난 뒤로, 그 후로 한 번도 잠자리에 든 적이 없는 것만 같다. 어제가 언제였는지조차 가늠이 안 된다. 그 허가증을 호주머니에 넣고 여러 달째 들고 다닌 것만 같다.\n\n이튿날도 마찬가지로, 우리 모두가 떼를 지어 그 집을 — 우리 집을, 도라와 나의 집을 — 보러 갔을 때, 나는 도무지 나 자신을 그 집의 주인으로 여길 수가 없다. 마치 다른 누군가의 허락을 받아 거기 있는 것만 같다. 진짜 주인이 이내 집에 돌아와서는, 나를 보니 반갑다고 말하지나 않을까 하는 생각이 반쯤 든다. 모든 것이 그토록 밝고 새것인, 어쩌면 그리도 아름답고 작은 집인지. 양탄자 위의 꽃들은 방금 꺾어 온 것만 같고, 벽지의 푸른 잎사귀들은 막 돋아난 것만 같고, 티 하나 없는 모슬린 커튼이며, 발그레한 장밋빛 가구며, 파란 리본이 달린 도라의 정원 모자가 — 그녀를 처음 알았을 적에 바로 그런 모자를 쓴 그녀를 내가 얼마나 사랑했던가, 지금도 기억나는구나! — 벌써 자그마한 못에 걸려 있고, 기타 케이스가 한쪽 구석에 발을 딛고 아주 편안히 자리 잡고 있으며, 모두가 이 살림에 비해 너무도 큰 집의 탑 모양 개집에 발이 걸려 넘어진다. 또 한 번 행복한 저녁이, 다른 모든 일과 똑같이 도무지 실감 나지 않는 저녁이 흐르고, 떠나기 전에 나는 슬그머니 그 늘 쓰던 방으로 들어선다. 도라가 거기 없다. 아직 옷 입혀 보는 일이 안 끝난 모양이다. 러비니어 양이 빼꼼히 들여다보고는, 그녀가 곧 올 거라고 알 듯 말 듯한 투로 일러 준다. 그런데도 그녀는 꽤나 오래 걸린다. 하지만 이윽고 나는 문께에서 사각거리는 소리를 듣고, 누군가 똑똑 두드린다.\n\n내가 \"들어와요!\" 하고 말하지만, 누군가 또 똑똑 두드린다.\n\n나는 누구일까 궁금해하며 문으로 간다. 거기서 나는 한 쌍의 반짝이는 눈과 발그레한 얼굴을 마주한다. 그것은 도라의 눈과 얼굴이다. 러비니어 양이 나에게 보여 주려고, 모자까지 갖춰서 내일 입을 옷을 도라에게 입혀 놓은 것이다. 나는 내 작은 아내를 가슴에 끌어안는다. 그러자 러비니어 양이 내가 모자를 흐트러뜨렸다고 조그맣게 비명을 지르고, 도라는 내가 너무 기뻐하니까 한꺼번에 웃다가 운다. 그리고 나는 그 어느 때보다도 더 이것이 믿기지 않는다.\n\n\"이거 예뻐요, 도디?\" 도라가 말한다.\n\n예쁘냐고! 두말하면 잔소리다.\n\n\"그리고 저를 정말 무척 사랑하는 거 맞아요?\" 도라가 말한다.\n\n이 화제는 모자에 너무도 위험천만한지라, 러비니어 양이 또 한 번 조그맣게 비명을 지르고는, 도라는 오로지 바라보기만 해야 하며 결코 손을 대서는 안 된다는 것을 명심하라고 나에게 신신당부한다. 그래서 도라는 일이 분 동안 흐뭇하리만치 어쩔 줄 몰라하는 채로 서서 찬사를 받는다. 그러더니 모자를 벗는데 — 그걸 벗으니 어찌나 자연스러워 보이는지! — 그것을 손에 든 채 달아나 버린다. 그러고는 늘 보던 제 평상복 차림으로 다시 춤추듯 내려와, 집에게 내가 아름답고 작은 아내를 얻은 거냐고, 그리고 이제 자기가 결혼한 걸 용서해 주겠느냐고 묻고는, 처녀로 사는 마지막으로 집을 요리책 위에 올라서게 하려고 무릎을 꿇는다.\n\n나는 그 어느 때보다도 더 믿기지 않는 심정으로, 바로 가까이에 잡아 둔 하숙집으로 돌아간다. 그리고 아침 일찍 일어나, 하이게이트 길로 말을 달려 고모님을 모시러 간다.\n\n나는 고모님이 그토록 차려입은 모습을 본 적이 없다. 고모님은 라벤더빛 비단옷을 입고 흰 모자를 쓰고 계신데, 보기에 놀랍기 그지없다. 재닛이 그분을 단장해 드리고는, 나를 보려고 거기 서 있다. 패거티는 교회에 갈 채비를 마치고, 회랑에서 예식을 지켜볼 작정이다. 제단 앞에서 내 사랑을 나에게 건네줄 딕 씨는 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 말았다. 약속대로 통행료 징수소에서 태워 온 트래들스는, 크림빛과 연파랑이 어우러진 눈부신 차림새를 뽐낸다. 그리고 그와 딕 씨 둘 다, 온통 장갑투성이라는 인상을 전체적으로 풍긴다.\n\n물론 나는 이것이 그러하다는 걸 알기에 보는 것이지, 사실 나는 정신이 나가서 아무것도 보이지 않는 것 같다. 또한 무엇 하나 도무지 믿기지도 않는다. 그래도, 우리가 지붕 없는 마차를 타고 달리는 동안, 이 동화 같은 결혼은 충분히 실감 나서, 거기에 아무 몫도 없이 그저 가게를 쓸어 내고 그날그날의 생업으로 향하는 불운한 사람들에게 묘한 연민 비슷한 것이 차오를 만큼은 된다.\n\n고모님은 가는 내내 내 손을 당신 손에 쥐고 앉아 계신다. 우리가 마차 위 바깥자리에 태우고 온 패거티를 내려 주려고 교회 조금 못 미쳐 멈춰 섰을 때, 고모님은 내 손을 꼭 쥐고 나에게 입을 맞춰 주신다.\n\n\"신의 가호가 있기를, 트롯! 내 친자식인들 너보다 더 사랑스러울 수는 없을 게다. 오늘 아침엔 가엾고 사랑스러운 그 아기 생각이 나는구나.\" \"저도요. 그리고 제가 고모님께 진 모든 신세도요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"쯧, 얘야!\" 고모님이 말씀하시며, 넘쳐흐르는 다정함으로 트래들스에게 손을 내미신다. 그러자 트래들스가 딕 씨에게 손을 내밀고, 딕 씨가 나에게 손을 내밀고, 내가 다시 트래들스에게 손을 내미는데, 그러다 보니 우리는 교회 문에 다다른다.\n\n교회는 분명 충분히 고요하다. 하지만 그것이 나를 가라앉히는 효과로 따지자면, 차라리 한창 돌아가는 증기 동력 직조기라 해도 좋을 정도다. 나는 그러기엔 이미 너무 멀리 가 버렸다.\n\n나머지는 죄다, 어느 정도 갈피를 못 잡는 꿈이다.\n\n그들이 도라와 함께 들어오는 꿈. 신도석 안내인이 우리를 훈련 교관처럼 제단 난간 앞에 정렬시키는 꿈. 그러면서도 나는, 그 와중에도, 어째서 신도석 안내인이란 늘 구할 수 있는 한 가장 못마땅한 여인들이어야 하는지, 혹시 좋은 기분이 옮아 재앙처럼 번지는 것을 막으려는 어떤 종교적 두려움이 있어서, 천국으로 가는 길에 그런 식초 단지 같은 여인들을 세워 두는 것이 꼭 필요하다 여기는 건 아닌지 궁금해하는 꿈.\n\n목사와 서기가 나타나는 꿈. 뱃사공 몇과 다른 사람 몇이 어슬렁어슬렁 들어오는 꿈. 내 뒤에 있던 늙은 뱃사람 하나가 럼주 냄새를 교회 안에 진하게 풍기는 꿈. 예식이 굵직한 목소리로 시작되고, 우리 모두가 아주 정중히 귀를 기울이는 꿈.\n\n준보조 신부 들러리 노릇을 하는 러비니어 양이 맨 먼저 우는 꿈. 그리고 그녀가 (내가 보기엔) 흐느낌으로 피저의 추억에 경의를 표하는 꿈. 클라리사 양이 냄새 맡는 병을 갖다 대는 꿈. 애그니스가 도라를 보살피는 꿈. 고모님이 얼굴에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리면서도 자신을 근엄함의 본보기로 꾸며 보이려 애쓰는 꿈. 자그마한 도라가 몹시 떨면서, 가냘픈 속삭임으로 서약의 응답을 하는 꿈.\n\n우리가 나란히 함께 무릎을 꿇는 꿈. 도라가 점점 덜 떨면서도, 줄곧 애그니스의 손을 꼭 붙잡고 있는 꿈. 예식이 조용하고 엄숙하게 치러지는 꿈. 그것이 끝나자 우리 모두가 사월처럼 웃음과 눈물이 뒤섞인 채로 서로를 바라보는 꿈. 내 젊은 아내가 제의실에서 신경이 북받쳐, 자기의 가엾은 아빠를, 사랑하는 아빠를 부르며 우는 꿈.\n\n그녀가 이내 다시 기운을 차리고, 우리 모두가 둘러서서 혼인 명부에 서명하는 꿈. 내가 패거티를 데려와 서명하게 하려고 회랑으로 올라가는 꿈. 패거티가 한쪽 구석에서 나를 끌어안고, 내 사랑하는 어머니가 결혼하던 모습을 자기가 지켜봤노라고 일러 주는 꿈. 그것이 다 끝나고, 우리가 떠나는 꿈.\n\n내가 사랑스러운 아내를 팔에 끼고서, 반쯤만 보이는 사람들과 설교단들, 기념비들, 신도석들, 세례반들, 오르간들, 그리고 교회 창문들이 자아내는 안개 사이를, 그토록 자랑스럽고 다정하게 통로를 걸어 내려가는 꿈. 그 안개 속에는 그 옛날 고향의 어린 시절 교회와 얽힌 어렴풋한 회상의 기운이 가물가물 떠다니는 것이었다.\n\n우리가 지나갈 때 사람들이, 어쩌면 저리도 젊은 한 쌍인지, 또 어쩌면 저리도 어여쁘고 작은 아내인지 하고 속삭이는 꿈. 돌아가는 마차 안에서 우리 모두가 그토록 흥겹고 수다스러운 꿈. 소피가, 트래들스에게 (내가 그에게 허가증을 맡겼다) 그것을 내놓으라 했을 때, 그가 어떻게든 그걸 잃어버리거나 소매치기를 당할 거라고 굳게 믿었던지라 까무러칠 뻔했다고 우리에게 일러 주는 꿈. 애그니스가 명랑하게 웃는 꿈. 그리고 도라가 애그니스를 어찌나 좋아하는지, 그녀와 떨어지려 하지 않고 여태 그 손을 꼭 잡고 있는 꿈.\n\n먹고 마실, 예쁘고도 든든한 것들이 잔뜩 차려진 아침 잔치가 있는 꿈. 나도 거기에 한몫 끼는데, 다른 어떤 꿈에서나 그러하듯, 그 맛을 조금도 느끼지 못한 채로 먹고 마신다. 말하자면, 나는 사랑과 결혼 말고는 아무것도 먹고 마시지 않으며, 그 음식들도 다른 무엇과 마찬가지로 도무지 믿기지 않는 것이다.\n\n내가 똑같이 꿈결 같은 투로 연설을 하는 꿈. 내가 무슨 말을 하고 싶은지 도무지 모르겠고, 다만 내가 그 말을 아직 하지 않았다는 확신 속에 담길 만한 것밖에는 떠오르지 않는다. 우리가 아주 정답고 소박하게 행복한 (그래도 늘 꿈속에서지만) 꿈. 집이 결혼 케이크를 먹고, 그게 나중에 탈을 일으키는 꿈.\n\n세를 낸 역마 두 마리가 채비를 마치고, 도라가 옷을 갈아입으러 가는 꿈. 고모님과 클라리사 양이 우리와 함께 남고, 우리가 정원을 거니는 꿈. 그리고 아침 잔치에서 도라의 고모들을 두고 제법 일장 연설을 한 고모님이, 스스로 무척 흐뭇해하면서도 한편으로 조금은 우쭐해하는 꿈.\n\n도라가 채비를 마치고, 러비니어 양이 그토록 즐거운 소일거리를 안겨 주던 그 어여쁜 장난감을 잃기 아쉬워 그녀 곁을 맴도는 꿈. 도라가 온갖 자질구레한 것들을 깜박 잊었다는 사실을 잇따라 놀라며 새로 깨닫고, 모두가 그것을 가지러 사방으로 뛰어다니는 꿈.\n\n마침내 도라가 작별 인사를 하기 시작하자, 다들 그녀를 빙 둘러싸는데, 그 화사한 빛깔과 리본들이 마치 한 무더기 꽃밭처럼 보이는 꿈. 내 사랑이 그 꽃들 사이에서 거의 파묻힐 뻔하다가, 웃음과 울음을 한꺼번에 터뜨리며 질투에 찬 내 품으로 빠져나오는 꿈.\n\n내가 (우리와 함께 갈) 집을 안고 가려 하자, 도라가 안 된다고, 자기가 그를 안고 가야 한다고, 안 그러면 이제 자기가 결혼했으니 더는 자기를 좋아하지 않는다고 그가 여겨 마음이 찢어질 거라고 말하는 꿈. 우리가 팔짱을 끼고 걸어가는데, 도라가 멈춰 서서 뒤돌아보며, \"혹시 제가 누구한테든 심술궂거나 고마운 줄 모르게 군 적이 있더라도, 부디 기억하지 말아 줘요!\" 하고 말하고는 울음을 터뜨리는 꿈.\n\n그녀가 작은 손을 흔들고, 우리가 또 한 번 떠나가는 꿈. 그녀가 또 한 번 멈춰 서서 뒤돌아보고는, 애그니스에게로 달려가, 다른 누구보다도 애그니스에게 마지막 입맞춤과 작별 인사를 건네는 꿈.\n\n우리는 함께 마차를 타고 달려가고, 나는 꿈에서 깨어난다. 나는 마침내 그것을 믿는다. 내 곁에 있는 것은 내가 그토록 사랑하는, 사랑스럽고 사랑스러운 내 작은 아내다!\n\n\"이제 행복해요, 이 바보 같은 양반?\" 도라가 말한다. \"그리고 후회하지 않는 거 맞죠?\"\n\n나는 그 시절의 환영들이 내 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 모습을 보려고 한쪽으로 비켜서 있었다. 그것들은 지나가 버렸고, 나는 다시 내 이야기의 여정을 이어 간다.\n\n# CHAPTER 44. OUR HOUSEKEEPING\n\n신혼여행도 끝나고 신부 들러리들도 다 집으로 돌아간 뒤, 도라와 함께 우리 집 작은 거실에 마주 앉아 있는 나 자신을 발견했을 때, 그것은 참으로 묘한 상황이었다. 말하자면 나는, 그 달콤하고도 오래된 일거리 — 사랑을 하는 일 — 에 관한 한 졸지에 실업자가 된 셈이었다.\n\n도라가 늘 거기 있다는 것은 이루 말할 수 없이 신기한 일이었다. 그녀를 보러 일부러 나가지 않아도 된다는 것, 그녀 때문에 마음 졸이며 나를 들볶을 까닭이 없다는 것, 그녀에게 편지를 쓸 일이 없다는 것, 그녀와 단둘이 있을 기회를 궁리하고 꾸밀 필요가 없다는 것이 도무지 믿기지 않았다. 가끔 저녁 무렵, 글을 쓰다가 고개를 들어 맞은편에 앉은 그녀를 보면, 나는 의자에 등을 기대고 이런 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 우리가 이렇게 단둘이, 당연한 듯 함께 있다니 — 더는 누구의 참견거리도 아니고 — 우리 약혼 시절의 그 모든 낭만은 선반에 얹혀 녹이나 슬도록 치워졌으며 — 기쁘게 해 줄 사람이라곤 서로밖에 없고 — 평생토록 서로를 기쁘게 해 줄 뿐이라니, 참으로 이상한 노릇이다, 하고.\n\n토론회가 열려 아주 늦게까지 붙들려 있다가, 집으로 걸어 돌아오면서 도라가 집에 있다는 생각을 떠올리는 것은 어찌나 묘하던지! 처음에는, 내가 저녁을 먹는 동안 그녀가 살그머니 내려와 나에게 말을 거는 것이 얼마나 신기한 일이었던가. 그녀가 머리를 종이로 말아 둔다는 것을 확실히 알게 된 것은 또 얼마나 놀라운 일이었던가. 그녀가 실제로 그렇게 하는 모습을 보는 것은 정말이지 통째로 경이로운 사건이었다!\n\n나와 내 예쁜 도라보다 살림에 대해 더 모르는 어린 새 두 마리가 과연 있었을까 싶다. 물론 우리에게는 하녀가 있었다. 그 여자가 우리 살림을 맡아 주었다. 나는 아직도, 그 여자가 사실은 크럽 부인의 딸이 변장하고 온 게 아닐까 하는 속내를 은근히 품고 있는데, 메리 앤과 함께 우리가 겪은 고생이 그만큼 끔찍했기 때문이다.\n\n그 여자의 이름은 패러건이었다. 우리가 그 여자를 고용할 때, 그 천성은 이름으로 다 담아내기엔 모자랄 정도라는 식으로 소개를 받았다. 그 여자에게는 포고문만큼이나 큼지막한 추천서가 있었는데, 그 문서에 따르자면 내가 들어 본 적 있는 온갖 집안일은 물론, 내가 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 일까지 죄다 해낼 수 있다고 적혀 있었다. 그 여자는 한창나이의 여인으로, 표정이 험상궂었고, (특히 두 팔에) 일종의 영구적인 홍역이나 시뻘건 발진을 달고 사는 사람이었다. 그 여자에게는 근위 기병대에 사촌이 하나 있었는데, 다리가 어찌나 길던지 마치 다른 누군가의 오후 그림자처럼 보였다. 그의 짧은 군복 상의가 그에게 터무니없이 작았던 만큼, 그도 우리 집에 비해 터무니없이 컸다. 그는 워낙 집과 비례가 맞지 않아서, 있어야 할 것보다 우리 오두막을 더 좁아 보이게 만들었다. 게다가 벽이 두껍지 않은 탓에, 그가 우리 집에서 저녁을 보낼 때면 우리는 부엌에서 끊임없이 울리는 으르렁 소리만 듣고도 늘 그가 와 있음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n우리의 보물 같은 하녀는 술도 안 마시고 정직하다고 보증되어 있었다. 그러니 나는 기꺼이 이렇게 믿어 두련다. 우리가 그 여자를 보일러 밑에서 발견했을 때 그것은 발작을 일으킨 것이었으며, 모자란 찻숟가락들은 쓰레기 치우는 인부 탓이었다고 말이다.\n\n그러나 그 여자는 우리 마음을 무섭게 갉아먹었다. 우리는 우리 자신의 미숙함을 절감했으되, 어찌해 볼 도리가 없었다. 그 여자에게 조금이라도 자비심이 있었다면 우리는 그 자비에 매달렸을 테지만, 그 여자는 인정사정없는 사람이라 그런 것이 전혀 없었다. 그 여자가 바로 우리 부부 첫 말다툼의 빌미였다.\n\n\"여보, 내 사랑.\" 어느 날 나는 도라에게 말했다. \"메리 앤이 시간 개념이라는 게 있기는 한 것 같소?\"\n\n\"왜요, 도디?\" 도라가 그림을 그리다 천진하게 고개를 들며 물었다.\n\n\"여보, 다섯 시인데 우리는 네 시에 밥을 먹기로 했으니까 그렇지.\"\n\n도라는 시계를 아쉬운 듯 흘끗 보더니, 시계가 너무 빠른 것 같다고 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"천만에, 여보.\" 나는 회중시계를 들여다보며 말했다. \"오히려 몇 분 느린걸.\"\n\n내 작은 아내는 다가와 내 무릎에 앉아, 그만 조용히 하라고 나를 어르며, 연필로 내 코 한복판에 줄을 죽 그었다. 하지만 그게 아무리 기분 좋은 일이라 해도, 그걸 밥으로 삼아 먹을 수는 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n\"여보, 그러지 말고.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 메리 앤에게 한마디 따끔하게 일러두는 편이 낫지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"어머, 안 돼요, 제발! 난 못 해요, 도디!\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 못 하오, 여보?\" 나는 부드럽게 물었다.\n\n\"아유, 난 이렇게 어리바리한 바보인데,\" 도라가 말했다. \"그 애도 내가 그런 걸 다 안단 말이에요!\"\n\n나는 이런 생각이 메리 앤에 대한 어떤 통제 체계를 세우는 일과는 도무지 어울리지 않는다고 여겨, 살짝 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"어머, 우리 나쁜 도련님 이마에 보기 싫은 주름이 잡혔네!\" 도라가 말했다. 그러고는 여전히 내 무릎에 앉은 채로, 연필로 그 주름을 따라 그렸다. 더 검게 그려지라고 연필을 제 발그레한 입술에 갖다 댄 다음, 부지런한 척 익살스럽게 내 이마를 매만지는데, 나도 모르게 그만 기분이 한껏 좋아졌다.\n\n\"아유, 착하지.\" 도라가 말했다. \"웃으니까 얼굴이 훨씬 예쁘잖아.\" \"하지만, 여보.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼! 제발!\" 도라가 입을 맞추며 외쳤다. \"심술궂은 푸른 수염은 되지 말아요! 진지해지지 말아요!\"\n\n* 푸른 수염: 아내를 여럿 죽인 프랑스 동화 속 잔혹한 남편.\n\"내 소중한 아내여.\" 내가 말했다. \"사람이 때로는 진지해져야 하는 법이오. 자! 이 의자에, 내 바로 옆에 앉아요! 연필 이리 줘요! 자! 이제 우리 사리에 맞게 얘기해 봅시다. 알다시피, 여보\" — 손을 잡으니 어찌나 작은 손이며, 결혼반지는 또 어찌나 앙증맞던지! — \"알다시피, 여보, 밥도 못 먹고 일하러 나가야 한다는 건 그리 편한 노릇이 아니잖소. 자, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"아—아—니요!\" 도라가 가냘프게 대답했다.\n\n\"여보, 왜 그리 떠는 거요!\"\n\n\"당신이 날 야단치려는 걸 다 아니까요.\" 도라가 가엾은 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 사랑, 나는 그저 차근차근 따져 보려는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"어머, 차근차근 따지는 게 야단치는 것보다 더 나빠요!\" 도라가 절망하며 외쳤다. \"난 따짐을 당하려고 결혼한 게 아니에요. 나처럼 가엾고 작은 애를 붙잡고 따질 생각이었다면, 미리 말해 줬어야죠, 이 매정한 도련님!\"\n\n나는 도라를 달래 보려 했지만, 그녀는 얼굴을 돌리고 곱슬머리를 이리저리 흔들며 \"매정해, 매정한 도련님!\" 하고 어찌나 여러 번 되뇌던지, 나는 정말 어찌해야 좋을지 알 수가 없었다. 그래서 어쩔 줄 모른 채 방 안을 몇 번 왔다 갔다 하다가 다시 그녀에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"도라, 내 사랑!\"\n\n\"아니, 난 당신 사랑이 아니에요. 당신은 나랑 결혼한 걸 분명 후회하고 있으니까요. 안 그러면 나랑 따지려 들지 않았을 거예요!\" 도라가 받아쳤다.\n\n나는 이렇게 앞뒤 안 맞는 비난에 어찌나 억울하던지, 도리어 그 억울함이 진지해질 용기를 주었다.\n\n\"자, 나의 도라.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신은 너무 어린애 같고, 말도 안 되는 소리를 하고 있소. 분명히 기억할 거요, 어제 나는 밥을 반쯤 먹다 말고 나가야 했고, 그저께는 덜 익은 송아지 고기를 허겁지겁 먹는 바람에 속이 영 안 좋았다는 걸. 오늘은 아예 밥을 못 먹고 있고 — 아침 먹으려고 얼마나 기다렸는지는 입에 담기도 무서울 지경이오 — 게다가 물도 끓질 않았소. 당신을 나무라려는 게 아니오, 여보. 하지만 이건 편한 살림이 아니란 말이오.\"\n\n\"어머, 매정해, 매정한 도련님, 내가 마뜩잖은 아내라는 거잖아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 나의 도라, 내가 그런 말은 절대 안 한 걸 당신도 알 거 아니오!\"\n\n\"내가 편하지 않다고 했잖아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다. \"나는 살림이 편하지 않다고 했소!\"\n\n\"그게 그거잖아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다. 그리고 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 여기는 게 분명했다. 너무도 서럽게 울었으니까.\n\n나는 예쁜 아내에 대한 사랑으로 가슴이 벅찬 동시에, 차라리 문에다 머리를 박아 버리고 싶은 자책감에 정신이 산란한 채로, 또 한 번 방을 가로질러 서성였다. 그러고는 다시 앉아 말했다.\n\n\"당신을 탓하는 게 아니오, 도라. 우리 둘 다 배워야 할 게 산더미요. 나는 그저, 여보, 당신이 — 정말로 당신이\" (나는 이것만은 포기하지 않기로 작정했다) — \"메리 앤을 단속하는 데 익숙해져야 한다는 걸 보여 주려는 것뿐이오. 그리고 당신 자신을 위해서도, 나를 위해서도, 조금은 알아서 처신해야 한다는 것을.\"\n\n\"어쩜, 정말이지, 당신이 그렇게 배은망덕한 소릴 하다니 놀라워요.\" 도라가 흐느꼈다. \"지난번에 당신이 생선이 좀 먹고 싶다고 했을 때, 내가 깜짝 놀라게 해 주려고 몇십 리나 직접 나가서 주문해 온 걸 다 알면서!\"\n\n\"그건 정말 고마운 일이었소, 내 사랑.\" 내가 말했다. \"어찌나 고맙던지, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 당신이 연어를 사 왔다는 말은 꺼내지도 않으려 했소 — 둘이 먹기엔 너무 컸다는 것도, 값이 일 파운드 육 실링이나 했다는 것도 — 그건 우리 형편에 너무 과한 돈이었지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"당신은 아주 맛있게 먹었잖아요.\" 도라가 흐느꼈다. \"그리고 날 보고 생쥐라고 했잖아요.\"\n\n\"또 그렇게 부르고말고, 내 사랑.\" 나는 대답했다. \"천 번이라도!\"\n\n그러나 나는 이미 도라의 여리고 작은 마음에 상처를 입혔고, 그녀는 도무지 달랠 수가 없었다. 그녀가 흐느끼며 슬퍼하는 모습이 어찌나 가엾던지, 나는 마치 무슨 끔찍한 말을 해서 그녀를 아프게 한 것만 같았다. 나는 황급히 집을 나서야 했고, 늦게까지 밖에 붙들려 있었으며, 밤새도록 비참할 만큼 가책에 시달렸다. 나는 살인자 같은 죄책감을 품은 채, 어마어마한 죄악을 저질렀다는 어렴풋한 느낌에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n집에 돌아온 것은 자정을 두세 시간 넘긴 무렵이었다. 우리 집에서 고모님이 나를 기다리며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일 있어요, 고모님?\" 나는 깜짝 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"아무 일 없다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"앉아라, 앉아. 우리 작은 꽃송이가 기운이 좀 없길래 곁에 있어 줬을 뿐이야. 그게 다란다.\"\n\n나는 손으로 머리를 괴었다. 그리고 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있자니, 내 가장 빛나던 소망을 이룬 지 이토록 얼마 되지도 않아 이렇게나 서글프고 풀이 죽으리라고는 미처 짐작도 못 했던 만큼, 마음이 한층 무겁고 가라앉았다. 그렇게 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있다가, 문득 내 얼굴에 머물러 있던 고모님의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그 눈에는 근심 어린 빛이 서려 있었으나, 이내 말끔히 가셨다.\n\n\"정말이에요, 고모님.\" 내가 말했다. \"도라가 저렇게 속상해하는 걸 생각하니 저도 밤새 영 마음이 안 좋았어요. 하지만 저는 그저 우리 집안일에 대해 다정하고 살갑게 얘기하려던 것뿐이었어요.\"\n\n고모님이 격려하듯 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"인내심을 가져야 한다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"물론이죠. 하늘이 아시겠지만, 저는 억지를 부릴 생각은 없어요, 고모님!\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"하지만 우리 작은 꽃송이는 아주 여린 꽃송이라, 바람도 살살 불어 줘야 하는 법이란다.\"\n\n나는 내 아내를 향한 고모님의 다정함에 마음속으로 고마워했다. 그리고 내가 그렇게 고마워하는 것을 고모님도 알고 계심을 확신했다.\n\n\"고모님은 어떻게 생각하세요.\" 나는 한참 더 난롯불을 바라보다가 말했다. \"이따금 우리 둘을 위해서, 도라에게 살짝 충고하고 조언해 주실 수 있지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"트롯.\" 고모님이 다소 감정이 북받친 듯 대답했다. \"아니다! 나에게 그런 부탁은 하지 마라.\"\n\n그 어조가 어찌나 간곡하던지, 나는 놀라 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"내 살아온 날들을 돌아보면 말이다, 얘야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"지금은 무덤 속에 있는 사람들, 내가 좀 더 다정하게 대할 수도 있었던 사람들이 떠오른단다. 내가 남들의 결혼 실수를 모질게 판단했다면, 그건 내 자신의 결혼을 모질게 판단할 쓰라린 까닭이 있었기 때문일지도 모르지. 그건 그렇다 치자. 나는 오랜 세월 동안 무뚝뚝하고, 칙칙하고, 제멋대로인 여자로 살아왔다. 지금도 그렇고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거야. 하지만 너와 나는 서로에게 좋은 일을 해 왔어, 트롯 — 적어도 너는 나에게 좋은 일을 해 줬다, 얘야. 그러니 이제 와서 우리 사이에 틈이 생겨서는 안 돼.\"\n\n\"우리 사이에 틈이라뇨!\" 내가 외쳤다.\n\n\"얘야, 얘야!\" 고모님이 옷자락을 매만지며 말했다. \"내가 뭐든 끼어들었다가는, 우리 사이에 얼마나 빨리 틈이 벌어질지, 또 내가 우리 작은 꽃송이를 얼마나 불행하게 만들지, 예언자라도 알 수 없는 일이야. 나는 우리 귀여운 것이 나를 좋아하고, 나비처럼 명랑하기를 바란단다. 그 두 번째 결혼 시절의 네 집을 떠올려 보렴. 그리고 방금 네가 넌지시 말한 것 같은 그런 해코지를, 나에게도 그 애에게도 절대로 하지 마라!\"\n\n나는 단번에 고모님 말씀이 옳음을 깨달았다. 그리고 사랑하는 내 아내를 향한 고모님의 그 너그러운 마음이 얼마나 깊은지를 온전히 헤아렸다.\n\n\"아직은 이른 날들이야, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"로마도 하루아침에 세워진 게 아니고, 일 년 만에 세워진 것도 아니지. 너는 네 손으로 자유로이 선택했고\" — 한순간 고모님 얼굴에 구름 한 점이 스쳐 가는 것 같았다 — \"아주 예쁘고 아주 정 많은 사람을 골랐어. 그 애를 (네가 그렇게 골랐듯이) 그 애에게 있는 자질로 가늠하고, 없을지도 모를 자질로 가늠하지 않는 것이 네 의무이자 — 또한 네 기쁨이기도 할 거야 — 물론 그건 나도 안다. 잔소리를 늘어놓으려는 게 아니야. 없는 자질이라면, 네가 할 수 있다면 그 애 안에서 키워 줘야지. 그리고 만약 그러지 못한다면, 얘야,\" 여기서 고모님은 코를 문질렀다. \"그저 그것 없이 지내는 데 익숙해지는 수밖에 없어. 하지만 명심해라, 얘야, 너희 둘의 앞날은 너희 둘 사이의 일이다. 아무도 너희를 도울 수 없어. 너희 스스로 헤쳐 나가야 해. 이게 결혼이라는 거야, 트롯. 그러니 부디 하늘이 너희 두 사람을 축복하시기를, 숲속의 두 어린 아기 같은 너희를!\"\n\n고모님은 발랄하게 그렇게 말씀하시고는, 그 축복을 확인하듯 내게 입을 맞추셨다.\n\n\"자.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내 작은 등불을 켜다오. 그리고 정원 길로 해서 이 보잘것없는 처소까지 나를 바래다주렴.\" 그쪽 방향으로 우리 두 오두막 사이에 통하는 길이 나 있었다. \"돌아오거든 베시 트롯우드의 사랑을 꽃송이에게 전하고. 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도, 트롯, 이 베시를 허수아비로 세울 생각일랑 꿈에도 하지 마라. 거울 속에서 본 적이 있다만, 그 애는 혼자만으로도 충분히 험상궂고 충분히 깡말랐으니까!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 고모님은 손수건으로 머리를 동여맸는데, 이런 때면 늘 그렇게 머리를 보따리처럼 싸매곤 했다. 그리고 나는 고모님을 댁까지 모셔다드렸다. 고모님이 당신 정원에 서서, 돌아가는 내 길을 비추려고 작은 등불을 치켜든 채 서 있을 때, 나를 바라보는 그 눈길에 또다시 근심이 어린 듯했다. 그러나 나는 고모님이 하신 말씀을 곱씹느라 너무도 골몰해 있었고, 도라와 내가 정말로 우리 둘의 앞날을 우리 스스로 헤쳐 나가야 하며 아무도 우리를 도울 수 없다는 — 처음으로, 진실로 — 그 확신에 너무도 깊이 사로잡혀 있던 터라, 그 눈길을 그리 눈여겨보지 못했다.\n\n이제 나 혼자 있게 되자, 도라가 작은 슬리퍼를 신고 살그머니 내려와 나를 맞이했다. 그녀는 내 어깨에 기대어 울며, 내가 매정했고 자기가 못되게 굴었다고 말했다. 나도 사실상 거의 같은 말을 했던 것 같다. 그리고 우리는 화해했고, 이 첫 작은 다툼을 마지막으로 삼기로, 백 년을 살더라도 두 번 다시 그런 일은 없도록 하기로 약속했다.\n\n우리가 다음으로 겪은 가정의 시련은 바로 하인 시험이었다. 메리 앤의 사촌이 우리 집 석탄 광으로 탈영해 들어왔는데, 우리가 기겁할 만큼 놀랍게도, 그의 동료 병사들로 이루어진 분견대가 그를 끌어내어 수갑을 채운 채 줄지어 데려가는 바람에, 우리 앞뜰이 온통 망신살에 뒤덮였다. 이 일에 힘입어 나는 메리 앤을 내보낼 용기를 냈다. 그 여자는 품삯을 받고는 어찌나 순순히 나가던지 나는 의아했는데, 나중에 찻숟가락 일이며, 또 내 허락도 없이 내 이름을 대고 상인들에게서 빌려 쓴 자잘한 돈의 내막을 알고 나서야 그 까닭을 깨달았다. 그 뒤로 키저베리 부인 — 켄티시 타운에서 가장 나이 든 주민으로 알고 있는데, 가정부 일을 다녔으나 그 솜씨에 대한 자신의 구상을 실행하기엔 너무 노쇠한 분이었다 — 이 잠시 거쳐 간 다음, 우리는 또 다른 보물을 들였다. 그녀는 더없이 상냥한 여인이었으나, 으레 쟁반을 든 채 부엌 계단을 오르내리며 굴러떨어지는 것을 일과로 삼았고, 찻그릇을 든 채로는 마치 목욕통에 뛰어들 듯 거실로 곤두박질치다시피 했다. 이 가엾은 여인이 저지른 손실이 너무 막심해 결국 내보낼 수밖에 없었고, 그 뒤로 (사이사이 키저베리 부인을 끼워 가며) 무능한 사람들이 줄줄이 거쳐 갔으며, 그 끝은 점잖아 보이는 어느 젊은 여자였는데, 그녀는 도라의 보닛을 쓰고 그리니치 장터에 놀러 갔다. 그 뒤로는, 고만고만하게 골고루 실패했다는 것 말고는 아무것도 기억나는 게 없다.\n\n우리와 거래하는 사람은 죄다 우리를 속여먹는 것 같았다. 우리가 가게에 나타나는 것은 곧장 상한 물건을 꺼내 오라는 신호였다. 바닷가재를 사면 속이 물로 가득 차 있었다. 고기란 고기는 죄다 질겼고, 빵 덩어리에는 껍질이라 할 만한 게 거의 없었다. 고깃덩이를 어떤 원칙으로 구워야 알맞게, 너무 과하지 않게 익는지 알아보려고, 나는 직접 요리책을 들춰 보았는데, 거기에는 일 파운드당 십오 분씩, 거기에 십오 분쯤 더 잡으면 된다고 정해져 있었다. 그러나 이 원칙은 어떤 묘한 운명의 장난인지 늘 우리에게 통하지 않았고, 우리는 시뻘건 날고기와 새카만 숯덩이 사이의 그 어떤 중간치도 끝내 맞추지 못했다.\n\n이런 실패들을 이루어 내느라 우리가 도리어 줄줄이 성공을 거두는 것보다 훨씬 큰 비용을 치렀다고 믿을 만한 이유가 나에게는 있었다. 상인들의 장부를 들여다보면, 마치 우리가 지하층 바닥을 버터로 깔아 놓고 지낸 게 아닌가 싶을 정도로, 그 물건의 소비량이 어마어마했다. 그 무렵의 소비세 통계에 후추 수요의 증가가 잡혔는지는 모르겠으나, 우리의 행태가 시장에 영향을 주지 않았다면, 분명 여러 집안이 후추 쓰기를 그만두었으리라고 나는 말하겠다. 그리고 무엇보다도 놀라운 사실은, 집 안에 도무지 남아나는 게 없었다는 것이다.\n\n빨래하는 여자가 옷가지를 전당포에 잡혀 놓고는, 뉘우치며 거나하게 취한 꼴로 찾아와 사과하던 일로 말할 것 같으면, 누구에게나 몇 번쯤은 일어날 법한 일이려니 싶다. 굴뚝에 불이 난 것이며, 교구 소방펌프가 출동한 것이며, 교회지기가 위증을 한 것도 마찬가지다. 그러나 짐작건대 우리는 특히나 운이 좋아서, 술을 즐기는 취향을 지닌 하녀를 들였는데, 그 여자는 술집 외상 장부에 도무지 알 수 없는 항목들로 우리 빚을 불려 놓았다. 이를테면 '럼 슈러브 사분의 일 통 (C 부인)', '진과 정향 팔분의 일 통 (C 부인)', '럼과 박하 한 잔 (C 부인)' 따위였다 — 이 괄호는 늘 도라를 가리켰는데, 나중에 해명을 들어 보니, 이 음료들을 죄다 도라가 들이켠 것으로 되어 있다는 것이었다.\n\n우리가 살림에서 처음으로 해낸 큰일 가운데 하나는 트래들스를 초대한 조촐한 만찬이었다. 나는 시내에서 그와 마주쳐, 그날 오후 함께 우리 집까지 걷자고 청했다. 그가 흔쾌히 응하기에, 나는 도라에게 그를 데려가겠다고 편지를 써 보냈다. 날씨가 좋았고, 가는 길에 우리는 내 가정의 행복을 화제로 삼아 이야기를 나누었다. 트래들스는 그 이야기로 가슴이 잔뜩 부풀어, 그런 집에서 소피가 자기를 기다리며 채비하는 모습을 그려 보니, 자기 행복을 완성하는 데 더 바랄 게 아무것도 없겠다고 말했다.\n\n식탁 맞은편에 더 예쁜 작은 아내가 앉아 있기를 바랄 수는 없었지만, 막상 자리에 앉고 보니 공간이 조금만 더 있었으면 하는 바람은 분명히 들었다. 어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠으나, 우리는 단둘뿐인데도 늘 자리가 비좁았고, 그러면서도 물건을 죄다 잃어버릴 만큼은 자리가 넉넉했다. 짐작건대 그것은, 큰길목을 어김없이 가로막는 집의 작은 탑 모양 개집을 빼고는, 무엇 하나 제자리라는 게 없었기 때문인 듯하다. 이날도 트래들스는 그 탑 모양 개집과 기타 가방과 도라의 꽃 그림과 내 책상에 어찌나 둘러싸여 있던지, 나는 그가 과연 나이프와 포크를 쓸 수나 있을지 진지하게 걱정스러웠다. 그러나 그는 특유의 너그러움으로 손사래를 쳤다. \"자리는 바다처럼 넓다네, 코퍼필드! 정말이지, 바다처럼 넓어!\"\n\n또 한 가지 바라는 게 있었으니, 그것은 식사 도중 집이 식탁보 위를 돌아다니도록 부추기는 일이 애당초 없었으면 하는 것이었다. 나는 그 녀석이 거기 올라와 있다는 것 자체가, 설령 발을 소금이나 녹인 버터에 담그는 버릇이 없었더라도, 어딘가 어수선한 일이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 이날 그 녀석은 자기가 트래들스를 막아 내려고 일부러 불려 나온 줄로 여기는 듯했다. 녀석은 내 옛 친구를 향해 짖어 대고, 그의 접시를 향해 짧게 돌진하기를 어찌나 겁 없이 끈질기게 되풀이하던지, 가히 그 녀석이 대화를 독차지했다고 해도 될 정도였다.\n\n그러나 사랑하는 도라가 얼마나 여린 마음의 소유자인지, 또 자기가 아끼는 녀석을 조금이라도 푸대접하면 얼마나 예민하게 굴지를 잘 알았기에, 나는 일절 싫은 내색을 비치지 않았다. 같은 까닭으로, 나는 바닥에 흩어져 옥신각신하는 접시들에 대해서도, 죄다 뒤죽박죽으로 술 취한 듯 보이는 양념병 받침의 꼴사나운 행색에 대해서도, 떠돌아다니는 채소 접시와 주전자들 때문에 트래들스가 한층 더 포위당한 사정에 대해서도 입도 뻥긋하지 않았다. 나는 눈앞에 놓인 삶은 양 다리를 썰기에 앞서 가만히 바라보면서, 우리 집 고깃덩이가 어쩜 이리도 별난 모양인지 — 우리 정육점 주인이 세상에 태어나는 기형 양들을 죄다 도맡아 계약하는 게 아닌가 — 속으로 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그 생각만큼은 혼자 묻어 두었다.\n\n\"여보.\" 나는 도라에게 말했다. \"그 접시에는 뭐가 들었소?\"\n\n도라가 마치 나에게 입맞춤이라도 하고 싶은 듯, 귀엽게 얼굴을 찡긋거리며 나를 꼬드기던 까닭을 나는 도무지 알 수가 없었다.\n\n\"굴이에요, 여보.\" 도라가 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 당신 생각이었소?\" 나는 반색하며 말했다.\n\n\"네—네, 도디.\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"그보다 더 좋은 생각은 없었을 거요!\" 나는 고기 써는 칼과 포크를 내려놓으며 외쳤다. \"트래들스가 세상에서 제일 좋아하는 게 바로 굴이거든!\"\n\n\"네—네, 도디.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그래서 아주 예쁜 작은 통으로 하나 샀어요. 가게 아저씨가 아주 좋은 거라고 했고요. 그런데 나—나, 뭔가 잘못된 것 같아 걱정이에요. 영 이상해 보여서요.\" 여기서 도라가 고개를 절레절레 흔드니, 그 눈에서 다이아몬드 같은 눈물이 반짝였다.\n\n\"두 껍데기가 다 붙어 있을 뿐이오.\" 내가 말했다. \"위쪽 껍데기만 떼어 내요, 여보.\"\n\n\"그런데 안 떨어져요!\" 도라가 안간힘을 쓰면서, 몹시 난처한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"있잖나, 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 명랑하게 접시를 살펴보며 말했다. \"이게 다 그 때문인 것 같은데 — 아주 훌륭한 굴이긴 한데, 내 생각엔 다 그 때문인 것 같아 — 이게 한 번도 까지지 않은 탓인 것 같네.\"\n\n그 굴들은 한 번도 까진 적이 없었다. 게다가 우리에게는 굴 까는 칼도 없었고 — 설령 있었더라도 쓸 줄 몰랐을 것이다. 그래서 우리는 굴은 바라만 보고 양고기를 먹었다. 적어도 익은 만큼은 먹고, 모자란 데는 케이퍼로 메웠다. 내가 말리지 않았더라면, 트래들스는 분명 완전한 야만인 흉내를 내며, 이 만찬이 얼마나 즐거운지를 표하느라 날고기 한 접시를 통째로 먹어 치웠을 것이다. 그러나 나는 우정의 제단에 그런 희생을 바치는 일은 결코 듣지 않으려 했고, 대신 베이컨을 한 접시 내놓았다. 다행스럽게도 식료품 저장실에 식은 베이컨이 마침 남아 있었던 것이다.\n\n내 가엾은 작은 아내는 내가 화가 났을까 봐 어찌나 애를 태우다가, 그렇지 않다는 걸 알고는 또 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 내가 가까스로 억눌렀던 그 언짢음마저 이내 씻은 듯 사라지고, 우리는 즐거운 저녁을 보냈다. 트래들스와 내가 포도주를 한 잔씩 기울이는 동안, 도라는 내 의자에 팔을 얹고 곁에 앉아, 틈만 나면 내 귀에 대고 매정하고 심술궂은 늙은이가 되지 않아 줘서 정말 고맙다고 속삭였다. 이윽고 그녀가 우리에게 차를 끓여 주었는데, 마치 인형 놀이용 찻그릇 한 벌을 가지고 부지런을 떠는 것처럼 그 모습이 어찌나 예쁘던지, 나는 차의 맛 따위에는 까다롭게 굴 생각이 들지 않았다. 그런 다음 트래들스와 나는 크리비지를 한두 판 했다. 그러는 동안 도라가 기타에 맞춰 노래를 부르니, 나에게는 우리의 연애와 결혼이 마치 내 다정한 꿈만 같고, 처음으로 그녀의 목소리에 귀 기울였던 그 밤이 아직 끝나지 않은 것만 같았다.\n\n* 크리비지: 점수판에 못을 꽂아 가며 셈하는 영국식 카드놀이.\n트래들스가 돌아가고, 그를 배웅한 뒤 거실로 들어오니, 아내가 제 의자를 내 의자 바로 옆에 끌어다 놓고 곁에 앉았다. \"정말 미안해요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"도디, 나에게 가르쳐 줄래요?\"\n\n\"먼저 내가 나 자신부터 가르쳐야겠소, 도라.\" 내가 말했다. \"나도 당신만큼이나 형편없으니까, 여보.\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 당신은 배울 수 있잖아요.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"당신은 영리하고, 영리한 사람이니까요!\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리, 생쥐야!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 말이에요.\" 한참 입을 다물고 있던 아내가 다시 입을 열었다. \"꼬박 일 년쯤 시골로 내려가, 아그네스와 함께 살았더라면 좋았을 것 같아요!\"\n\n그녀는 두 손을 내 어깨 위에 모으고 그 위에 턱을 괸 채, 푸른 눈으로 가만히 내 눈을 들여다보고 있었다.\n\n\"왜 그렇소?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그분이라면 나를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 줬을 것 같고, 나도 그분에게서 뭔가 배웠을 것 같아서요.\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"다 때가 되면 될 일이오, 여보. 아그네스는 이 여러 해 동안 아버지를 돌봐야 했다는 걸 잊지 마요. 아주 어린아이였을 적에도, 그녀는 이미 우리가 아는 그 아그네스였소.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 당신더러 불러 달라고 하는 이름으로, 날 불러 줄래요?\" 도라가 꼼짝도 않고 물었다.\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 나는 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"바보 같은 이름이에요.\" 그녀가 잠시 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"어린 아내.\"\n\n나는 웃으며, 어린 아내에게 어쩌다 그렇게 불리고 싶은 마음이 들었느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 내가 둘러 감은 팔에 이끌려 푸른 눈이 내게 좀 더 가까이 다가온 것 말고는 꼼짝도 않은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"바보 같으니, 내 말은 당신이 도라 대신 그 이름을 쓰라는 게 아니에요. 그저 나를 그렇게 생각해 달라는 거예요. 나에게 화가 나려고 할 때면, 속으로 이렇게 말해요. '그저 내 어린 아내일 뿐이야!' 내가 당신을 몹시 실망시킬 때면, 이렇게 말하고요. '저 사람이 한낱 어린 아내밖에 못 될 거라는 건 진작에 알고 있었지!' 내가 마땅히 되어야 할, 하지만 결코 될 수 없을 것 같은 그 모습이 아쉬울 때면, 이렇게 말해요. '그래도 내 어리석은 어린 아내는 나를 사랑하지!' 정말로 사랑하니까요.\"\n\n나는 그때까지 그녀에게 진지하게 굴지 않았다. 지금 이 순간까지 그녀 자신이 진지하다는 걸 미처 몰랐던 것이다. 그러나 이제 내가 진심을 다해 건넨 말에 그녀의 다정한 천성이 어찌나 행복해하던지, 반짝이는 두 눈의 눈물이 채 마르기도 전에 얼굴에는 웃음이 번졌다. 그녀는 이내 정말로 내 어린 아내가 되어, 중국식 작은 집 밖 바닥에 주저앉아, 요전번 못된 짓에 대한 벌로 작은 종을 하나하나 차례로 흔들어 댔다. 그러는 동안 집은 머리만 내민 채 문간에 엎드려 눈을 끔벅거렸는데, 너무 게을러서 약 올림을 당하기조차 귀찮은 모양이었다.\n\n도라의 이 호소는 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 나는 지금 글로 옮기는 그 시절을 돌아본다. 내가 그토록 사랑했던 그 천진한 모습이 과거의 안개와 그늘 밖으로 걸어 나와, 그 다정한 고개를 다시 한번 내게로 돌려 주기를 불러 본다. 그리고 나는 지금도 이렇게 말할 수 있다. 그 한마디 작은 호소가 늘 내 기억 속에 머물러 있었노라고. 어쩌면 나는 그것을 가장 잘 활용하지는 못했을지도 모른다. 나는 젊고 미숙했으니까. 그러나 나는 그 꾸밈없는 간청에 단 한 번도 귀를 닫지 않았다.\n\n도라는 그 직후 나에게, 자기가 훌륭한 살림꾼이 되겠노라고 말했다. 그리하여 그녀는 석판을 닦고, 연필을 깎고, 어마어마하게 큰 가계부를 사고, 집이 찢어 놓은 요리책의 책장을 죄다 바늘과 실로 정성껏 꿰매고, '착해지려고' — 그녀가 그렇게 부르던 대로 — 자못 필사적인 작은 시도를 벌였다. 그러나 숫자들은 예의 그 고집스러운 성미를 부려 — 도무지 더해지질 않았다. 그녀가 가계부에 힘들여 항목을 두세 개 적어 넣고 나면, 집이 그 위를 걸어 다니며 꼬리를 흔들어 죄다 문질러 지워 버렸다. 그녀 자신의 작은 오른손 가운뎃손가락만 뼛속까지 잉크에 절었으니, 내 생각엔 그것만이 유일하게 확실히 얻어 낸 결과였다.\n\n가끔 저녁이면, 내가 집에서 일을 하고 있을 때 — 나는 그 무렵 글을 꽤 많이 썼고, 조금씩 작가로 알려지기 시작하던 참이었다 — 나는 펜을 내려놓고 착해지려 애쓰는 내 어린 아내를 가만히 지켜보곤 했다. 우선 그녀는 그 어마어마하게 큰 가계부를 꺼내 와, 깊은 한숨과 함께 책상에 내려놓았다. 그러고는 어젯밤 집이 알아볼 수 없게 만들어 놓은 자리를 펼치고, 집을 불러내 제 잘못을 들여다보게 했다. 이 일은 으레 집에게 유리하게 흐르는 딴청으로 번졌고, 벌이랍시고 그 녀석 코에 잉크칠이나 좀 하는 것으로 끝나곤 했다. 그런 다음 그녀는 집에게 당장 책상 위에 '사자처럼' 엎드리라고 일렀는데 — 그건 그 녀석의 재주 가운데 하나였으나, 사자와 닮았다고는 도저히 말 못 하겠다 — 녀석이 순한 기분이면 시키는 대로 따랐다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 펜을 집어 들고 쓰기 시작하다가, 펜에 머리카락이 끼어 있는 걸 발견했다. 그러면 다른 펜을 집어 들고 쓰기 시작하다가, 펜이 잉크를 튀긴다는 걸 알았다. 그러면 또 다른 펜을 집어 들고 쓰기 시작하다가, 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"아유, 이건 말하는 펜이라 도디를 방해하겠어!\" 그러고는 가망 없는 일이라며 손을 놓고, 그 가계부로 사자를 짓누르는 시늉을 한 뒤 치워 버렸다.\n\n아니면, 그녀가 아주 차분하고 진지한 마음 상태일 때면, 석판과, 무엇보다도 머리 마는 종이 뭉치처럼 보이는 청구서며 온갖 서류가 담긴 작은 바구니를 앞에 놓고 앉아, 거기서 어떻게든 결과를 뽑아내려 애썼다. 이것저것 매섭게 견주어 보고, 석판에 적었다가 다시 지우고, 왼손 손가락을 앞으로 뒤로 몇 번이고 되풀이해 세어 보다가, 어찌나 약이 오르고 풀이 죽던지, 또 그 표정이 어찌나 서글퍼 보이던지, 그 환한 얼굴이 — 그것도 나 때문에! — 흐려지는 것을 보고 있자면 나는 가슴이 아팠다. 그래서 나는 살그머니 그녀에게 다가가 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"왜 그래, 도라?\"\n\n도라는 절망한 듯 고개를 들고 대답했다. \"이게 도무지 맞아떨어지질 않아요. 머리가 어찌나 아픈지. 그리고 내가 바라는 건 하나도 안 돼요!\"\n\n그러면 나는 말했다. \"자, 우리 같이 해 보자. 내가 보여 줄게, 도라.\"\n\n그러고는 실제로 시범을 보이기 시작하면, 도라는 깊이 귀를 기울였는데 — 어쩌면 오 분쯤은 그랬다. 그러다 그녀는 끔찍하게 지치기 시작해, 내 머리카락을 배배 꼬거나, 내 셔츠 깃을 접어 내려 내 얼굴이 어떻게 보이는지 시험하면서 그 따분한 화제를 가볍게 풀어 버렸다. 내가 잠자코 그 장난을 막고 끈질기게 계속하면, 그녀는 점점 더 갈피를 못 잡으며 어찌나 겁먹고 풀 죽은 표정이 되던지, 내가 처음 그녀의 길로 들어섰을 때의 그 타고난 명랑함이며 그녀가 내 어린 아내라는 사실이 나를 꾸짖듯 떠올랐다. 그러면 나는 연필을 내려놓고 기타를 가져오라고 했다.\n\n나는 할 일이 산더미였고 근심도 많았지만, 바로 그런 마음 씀씀이 때문에 그것들을 혼자 묻어 두었다. 그렇게 한 것이 옳았는지 지금도 도무지 확신이 서지 않으나, 나는 내 어린 아내를 위해 그렇게 했다. 나는 내 가슴속을 더듬어, 그 안의 비밀을 — 내가 그것을 안다면 — 한 점 숨김없이 이 종이에 털어놓는다. 무언가를 예전에 잃었거나 가지지 못했다는 그 오래된 서글픔이, 내 마음 한구석에 얼마쯤 자리 잡고 있었음을 나도 안다. 그러나 그것이 내 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들 정도는 아니었다. 화창한 날 홀로 거닐며, 온 대기가 소년 시절의 황홀로 가득 차 있던 그 여름날들을 떠올릴 때면, 내 꿈이 다 이루어지지는 못했다는 아쉬움이 조금은 들었다. 그러나 나는 그것이, 그 무엇도 지금 이 순간 위에 드리울 수 없는, 과거의 한결 부드러워진 영광이려니 여겼다. 가끔은 잠깐씩, 내 아내가 나의 조언자였더라면, 나를 떠받치고 더 나은 사람으로 이끌어 줄 만한 더 굳건한 인품과 목표를 지녔더라면, 내 주위 어딘가에 있는 듯한 그 빈자리를 채워 줄 힘을 타고났더라면 하고 바라는 마음이 일기도 했다. 그러나 나는 이것이 내 행복의 천상 같은 완성이며, 애당초 그렇게 될 운명이 아니었고 또 결코 그렇게 될 수도 없었던 일이라고 느꼈다.\n\n나는 나이로 따지면 소년 같은 남편이었다. 나는 이 책장에 적힌 것 말고는 그 어떤 다른 슬픔이나 경험의, 사람을 단련시키는 영향을 알지 못했다. 내가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀다면 — 어쩌면 많이 저질렀을 텐데 — 그것은 잘못된 사랑에서, 그리고 지혜가 모자란 탓에서 비롯된 것이었다. 나는 있는 그대로의 진실을 적는다. 이제 와서 그것을 변명한들 나에게 아무 도움도 되지 않을 것이다.\n\n그리하여 나는 우리 삶의 수고와 근심을 혼자 짊어졌고, 거기에 동반자는 없었다. 우리는 그 엉망진창인 살림살이에 관한 한 예전과 다름없이 살았다. 그러나 나는 그런 데 이미 익숙해졌고, 도라가 이제는 좀처럼 속을 끓이지 않는 것을 보니 흐뭇했다. 그녀는 예의 그 어린아이 같은 모습으로 환하고 명랑했으며, 나를 끔찍이 사랑했고, 그 오래된 자질구레한 것들로도 행복해했다.\n\n토론회가 길어질 때면 — 길이로 말하는 것이지 내용으로 말하는 게 아니다, 내용으로 따지면 길지 않은 적이 드물었으니까 — 그리고 내가 집에 늦게 돌아오면, 도라는 내 발소리를 듣고는 결코 가만히 쉬는 법 없이 언제나 아래층으로 내려와 나를 맞이했다. 그토록 애써 자격을 갖춘 그 일거리에 매이지 않은 저녁이면, 그리고 내가 집에서 글쓰기에 몰두해 있으면, 그녀는 아무리 밤이 깊어도 내 곁에 조용히 앉아, 어찌나 입을 다물고 있던지 나는 그녀가 잠든 줄로 여기기 일쑤였다. 그러나 대개 내가 고개를 들면, 앞서 말한 그 고요한 눈길로 나를 바라보고 있는 그녀의 푸른 눈이 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n\"아유, 어쩜 이리 지친 도련님이람!\" 어느 날 밤 책상을 정리하다 그녀의 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"어쩜 이리 지친 아가씨람!\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 더 맞는 말이지. 다음부터는 먼저 자러 가야 해, 여보. 당신한테는 너무 늦은 시각이야.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 날 자러 보내지 말아요!\" 도라가 내 곁으로 다가오며 애원했다. \"제발, 그러지 말아요!\"\n\n\"도라!\" 놀랍게도 그녀는 내 목에 기대어 흐느끼고 있었다. \"몸이 안 좋아, 여보! 행복하지 않은 거야!\"\n\n\"아니에요! 아주 건강하고, 아주 행복해요!\" 도라가 말했다. \"하지만 내가 여기 머물면서 당신이 글 쓰는 걸 지켜봐도 된다고 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"아니, 이 한밤중에 그토록 반짝이는 눈으로 볼 게 뭐 그리 있다고!\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래도 이 눈이 반짝이긴 해요?\" 도라가 웃으며 받았다. \"반짝인다니 정말 기뻐요.\" \"허영덩어리 같으니!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그러나 그것은 허영이 아니었다. 그저 내가 감탄해 주는 것에 대한 순수한 기쁨일 뿐이었다. 그녀가 말해 주기 전에도 나는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n\"이 눈이 예쁘다고 생각하면, 내가 늘 여기 머물면서 당신 글 쓰는 걸 봐도 된다고 해 줘요!\" 도라가 말했다. \"이 눈이 예쁘다고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"아주 예쁘지.\"\n\n\"그럼 늘 여기 머물면서 당신 글 쓰는 걸 보게 해 줘요.\"\n\n\"그래서야 이 눈이 더 반짝여지지는 않을 것 같은데, 도라.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 더 반짝일 거예요! 왜냐면, 이 영리한 도련님아, 당신이 말없는 공상에 푹 빠져 있을 때에도 그러면 나를 잊지 않을 테니까요. 내가 아주, 아주 바보 같은 소리를 — 평소보다 더 바보 같은 소리를 — 해도 괜찮겠어요?\" 도라가 내 어깨 너머로 내 얼굴을 빠끔히 들여다보며 물었다.\n\n\"그 놀라운 게 뭔데?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"펜을 들고 있게 해 줘요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"당신이 그토록 부지런히 일하는 그 숱한 시간 동안 나도 뭔가 할 일이 있었으면 좋겠어요. 펜을 들고 있어도 돼요?\"\n\n내가 그러라고 했을 때 그녀가 보이던 그 예쁜 기쁨을 떠올리면 눈에 눈물이 고인다. 다음번 글을 쓰려고 앉았을 때부터, 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧, 그녀는 예의 그 자리에 여분의 펜 한 묶음을 곁에 두고 앉았다. 내 일과 이렇게 맺어진 것을 그녀가 어찌나 자랑스러워하던지, 또 내가 새 펜이 필요하다고 할 때면 — 나는 아주 자주 그런 척했다 — 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 그 모습에 나는 내 어린 아내를 기쁘게 해 줄 새로운 방법을 떠올렸다. 나는 이따금 원고를 한두 장 베껴 써 줘야 한다는 시늉을 했다. 그러면 도라는 의기양양해졌다. 이 대단한 작업을 위해 그녀가 차리던 채비며, 둘러쓰던 앞치마며, 잉크를 막으려고 부엌에서 빌려 오던 턱받이며, 들이던 시간이며, 마치 집이 다 알아듣기라도 하는 양 그 녀석과 한바탕 웃느라 수도 없이 멈추던 일이며, 끝에 제 이름을 서명하지 않으면 그 작업이 완성되지 않은 거라고 굳게 믿던 것이며, 그것을 마치 학교 습자장처럼 나에게 가져오던 모습이며, 그러고는 내가 칭찬해 주면 내 목을 끌어안던 그 모습은, 다른 사람들에게는 아무리 시시해 보일지언정 나에게는 가슴 뭉클한 추억이다.\n\n그녀는 그 직후 열쇠들을 인수하여, 가느다란 허리에 묶은 작은 바구니에 그 꾸러미를 통째로 담고 짤랑짤랑 집 안을 돌아다녔다. 그 열쇠들이 맞는 곳이 잠겨 있는 일은 좀처럼 없었고, 집의 장난감 구실 말고는 무슨 쓸모가 있는 것도 아니었다 — 그러나 도라가 좋아하니, 그것이 나도 좋았다. 그녀는 이 살림 소꿉놀이로 꽤 많은 일이 이루어진다고 흡족해했으며, 마치 우리가 장난삼아 인형 집 살림을 차린 것처럼 즐거워했다.\n\n그렇게 우리는 살아갔다. 도라는 고모님에게도 나에게 못지않게 살갑게 굴었고, 자기가 고모님을 '심술궂은 늙은이'로 여기며 무서워하던 시절 이야기를 자주 들려주곤 했다. 나는 고모님이 누군가에게 그토록 한결같이 마음을 푸는 것을 본 적이 없었다. 고모님은 집에게 살갑게 굴었다 — 집은 한 번도 응해 주지 않았는데도. 날이면 날마다 기타 소리에 귀를 기울였다 — 음악에는 취미가 없었을 텐데도. 무능한 하인들을 한 번도 몰아붙이지 않았다 — 그 유혹이 분명 어지간히 컸을 텐데도. 도라가 갖고 싶어 한다는 걸 알아낸 것이라면 무엇이든 깜짝 선물로 사 주려고 먼 길을 걸어 다녔다. 그리고 정원 쪽으로 들어왔다가 방에 도라가 없으면, 어김없이 계단 아래에서, 온 집 안에 명랑하게 울려 퍼지는 목소리로 이렇게 외치곤 했다.\n\n\"우리 작은 꽃송이는 어디 있나?\"\n\n# CHAPTER 45. MR. DICK FULFILS MY AUNT’S PREDICTIONS\n\n내가 박사 선생님 곁을 떠난 지도 이제 제법 시간이 흘렀다. 그 동네에 살다 보니 박사 선생님을 자주 뵈었고, 우리는 두세 번쯤 그 댁에 가서 저녁을 먹거나 차를 마셨다. 늙은 군인 아주머니는 박사 선생님 댁 지붕 아래 아주 눌러앉아 있었다. 아주머니는 예전과 조금도 다름없었고, 그 모자 위로는 죽지 않는 나비들이 여전히 너울거렸다.\n\n내가 살아오면서 알게 된 다른 몇몇 어머니들처럼, 마크럼 부인도 자기 딸보다 훨씬 더 노는 것을 좋아했다. 아주머니에게는 즐길 거리가 무척이나 많이 필요했는데, 닳고 닳은 늙은 군인답게, 자기가 좋아하는 일을 찾아 즐기면서도 마치 딸을 위해 헌신하는 척했다. 그러니 애니를 즐겁게 해 주고 싶다는 박사 선생님의 바람은 이 훌륭한 어머니에게 특히나 반가운 것이었고, 아주머니는 박사 선생님의 사려 깊음을 두말없이 칭찬해 마지않았다.\n\n사실 나는, 아주머니가 자기도 모르게 박사 선생님의 상처를 후벼 파고 있었다고 믿어 의심치 않는다. 별다른 뜻 없이 그저, 나이가 무르익으면 으레 따라붙기 마련인 어떤 농익은 경박함과 이기심을 품었을 뿐이지만, 아주머니가 아내의 삶의 짐을 덜어 주려는 박사 선생님의 계획을 그토록 열렬히 추켜세움으로써, 자기가 어린 아내에게 거추장스러운 존재라는 두려움, 그리고 둘 사이에 마음이 통하는 구석이 없다는 두려움을 박사 선생님에게 더욱 굳혀 준 것이라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n\"여보게,\" 어느 날 내가 그 자리에 있을 때 아주머니가 박사 선생님에게 말했다. \"애니가 늘 여기만 틀어박혀 있으면 좀 갑갑할 거라는 건, 자네도 의심할 여지가 없지 않은가.\"\n\n박사 선생님은 인자한 머리를 끄덕였다. \"그 애가 제 어미 나이쯤 되면,\" 마크럼 부인이 부채를 한 번 펄럭이며 말했다. \"그땐 또 다른 얘기지. 나야 점잖은 사람들이랑 카드놀이만 할 수 있다면 감옥에 처넣어도 나오고 싶은 생각이 안 들 거야. 하지만 나는 애니가 아니잖나, 알다시피. 그리고 애니는 제 어미가 아니고.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고요.\" 박사 선생님이 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 사람이야 — 아니, 미안하네!\" 박사 선생님이 손사래를 쳤기 때문이다. \"내 늘 자네 뒤에서 하는 말을 자네 앞에서도 해야겠는데, 자네는 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 사람이야. 하지만 자네는 당연히 — 안 그런가? — 애니와 똑같은 취미나 흥취에 빠져들진 못하잖나?\"\n\n\"못하지요.\" 박사 선생님이 서글픈 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 당연히 못하지.\" 늙은 군인이 받아쳤다. \"예를 들어 자네 그 사전을 보게나. 사전이라는 게 얼마나 쓸모 있는 책인가! 얼마나 꼭 필요한 책이냐고! 단어의 뜻이라니! 존슨 박사인지 그 비슷한 누군가가 없었더라면, 우린 지금 이 순간에도 다리미를 침대 틀이라고 부르고 있었을지도 몰라. 하지만 사전이 — 더구나 한창 만드는 중인 사전이 — 애니의 흥미를 끌 거라고 기대할 순 없잖나, 안 그래?\"\n\n박사 선생님은 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"그래서 내가 자네의 그 깊은 마음씀을 그토록 갸륵하게 여긴다는 거야.\" 마크럼 부인이 접은 부채로 박사 선생님의 어깨를 톡톡 치며 말했다. \"이건 자네가, 나이 든 사람들이 흔히 그러듯, 젊은 어깨에 늙은 머리가 얹혀 있길 바라지 않는다는 뜻이지. 자네는 애니의 성품을 잘 살폈고, 그걸 이해하고 있어. 그게 바로 내가 그렇게나 멋지다고 여기는 점이라네!\"\n\n이런 칭찬 세례를 받자, 침착하고 참을성 많은 스트롱 박사 선생님의 얼굴에마저 어렴풋한 고통의 빛이 스치는 것 같았다.\n\n\"그러니까, 친애하는 박사 선생,\" 늙은 군인이 박사 선생님을 다정하게 몇 번 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"언제 어느 때든 나를 마음껏 부려도 좋네. 자, 내가 자네를 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠다는 걸 알아 두게. 나는 애니를 데리고 오페라며 음악회며 전시회며, 온갖 데를 다 따라나설 준비가 돼 있어. 그리고 자네는 내가 지치는 꼴을 결코 못 볼 걸세. 의무라네, 친애하는 박사 선생, 세상 그 무엇보다 의무가 먼저야!\"\n\n아주머니는 자기 말을 그대로 지켰다. 아주머니는 엄청난 즐거움도 거뜬히 견뎌 내는 부류의 사람이었고, 그 대의를 향한 끈기에서 한 번도 물러서는 법이 없었다. 신문(아주머니는 날마다 두 시간씩, 집에서 제일 푹신한 의자에 떡하니 자리 잡고 앉아 안경 너머로 그것을 처음부터 끝까지 읽었다)을 손에 쥐었다 하면, 애니가 분명 보고 싶어 할 것이 틀림없는 무언가를 어김없이 찾아냈다. 애니가 그런 일에는 진력이 났다고 아무리 항변해도 소용없었다. 어머니의 잔소리는 늘 한결같았다. \"자, 우리 애니, 너도 잘 알 텐데. 그리고 얘야, 내 말해 두는데, 너는 스트롱 박사의 친절에 합당한 보답을 못 하고 있는 거야.\"\n\n이 말은 대개 박사 선생님이 있는 자리에서 나왔고, 애니가 무언가 반대를 했다가도 그것을 거두어들이게 만드는 주된 빌미가 되는 것 같았다. 하지만 대개 애니는 어머니에게 순순히 따랐고, 늙은 군인이 가자는 데로 갔다.\n\n이제는 몰던 씨가 그들과 함께 가는 일이 거의 없었다. 가끔은 고모님과 도라가 함께 가자는 청을 받아 그 초대에 응하기도 했다. 가끔은 도라만 부르기도 했다. 한때는 도라가 거기 가는 것이 마음 불편했던 적도 있었다. 그러나 예전 그날 밤 박사 선생님의 서재에서 있었던 일을 곱씹어 본 뒤로, 내 미심쩍은 마음에도 변화가 생겼다. 나는 박사 선생님이 옳다고 믿었고, 더 나쁜 의심 따위는 품지 않았다.\n\n고모님은 가끔 나와 단둘이 있게 되면 코를 문지르며, 도무지 영문을 모르겠다고 말했다. 그분들이 좀 더 행복했으면 좋겠다고, 우리의 그 군인 양반(고모님은 늙은 군인을 늘 그렇게 불렀다)이 사태를 조금도 나아지게 하는 것 같지 않다고 했다. 고모님은 한술 더 떠 이런 의견을 내놓았다. \"우리 군인 양반이 그 나비들을 싹둑 잘라서, 오월제 때 굴뚝 청소부들한테나 줘 버린다면, 그게 그 양반 쪽에서 뭔가 분별 있는 일을 시작하는 신호처럼 보일 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n하지만 고모님이 한결같이 기대를 거는 사람은 딕 씨였다. 저 사람 머릿속에는 분명 어떤 생각이 들어 있다고, 고모님은 말했다. 다만 그 생각을 어느 한 구석에 단단히 몰아넣을 수만 있다면 — 그게 저 사람한테는 큰 난관이었지만 — 어떤 비범한 방식으로 두각을 나타낼 거라는 것이었다.\n\n이 예언을 까맣게 모른 채, 딕 씨는 박사 선생님과 스트롱 부인에 관해서라면 정확히 똑같은 자리를 계속 지키고 있었다. 앞으로 나아가지도, 뒤로 물러서지도 않는 것 같았다. 마치 건물처럼 애초의 토대 위에 그대로 눌러앉은 듯했다. 그리고 솔직히 고백하건대, 딕 씨가 언젠가 움직일 거라는 내 믿음은, 그가 건물이었을 경우와 별반 다를 게 없었다.\n\n그런데 어느 날 밤, 내가 결혼한 지 몇 달쯤 됐을 무렵, 딕 씨가 응접실로 머리를 쑥 들이밀었다. 나는 거기서 혼자 글을 쓰고 있었다(도라는 고모님과 함께 그 두 마리 작은 새와 차를 마시러 나가고 없었다). 딕 씨는 의미심장하게 기침을 한 번 하고는 말했다.\n\n\"트롯우드, 자네한테 폐를 끼치지 않고 잠깐 얘기 좀 할 수 있을까 모르겠네?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 딕 씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"들어오세요!\"\n\n\"트롯우드.\" 딕 씨가 나와 악수를 하고 나서, 손가락을 콧등에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"앉기 전에 한마디 짚고 넘어가고 싶은 게 있네. 자네, 자네 고모님 알지?\"\n\n\"조금은요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"그분은 이 세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인일세!\"\n\n자기 안에 그 말이 잔뜩 장전돼 있기라도 했던 것처럼 그것을 탕 쏘아 내고는, 딕 씨는 여느 때보다 더 진지하게 자리에 앉아 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 얘야.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"자네한테 질문 하나를 던지겠네.\"\n\n\"얼마든지 하세요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 나를 뭐라고 생각하나?\" 딕 씨가 팔짱을 끼며 물었다.\n\n\"정다운 오랜 친구죠.\" 내가 말했다. \"고맙네, 트롯우드.\" 딕 씨가 웃으며, 무척 기뻐서 몸을 쭉 뻗어 나와 악수를 하려고 손을 내밀며 대답했다. \"하지만 내 말은 말이야, 얘야,\" 다시 진지함을 되찾으며 자기 이마를 짚었다. \"이쪽으로 봐서 자네는 나를 뭐라고 생각하느냔 말이야.\"\n\n나는 어떻게 대답해야 할지 난감했지만, 그가 한마디로 거들어 주었다.\n\n\"모자라다고?\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 내가 우물쭈물 대답했다. \"좀 그런 편이죠.\"\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 딕 씨가 외쳤는데, 내 대답에 아주 홀딱 반한 듯했다. \"그러니까, 트롯우드, 그 누군지 알지, 그 사람 머릿속에서 골칫거리를 좀 끄집어내서, 자네도 알다시피 그걸 어디다 집어넣었을 때 말이야, 거기엔 — \" 딕 씨는 두 손을 서로 맞대고 아주 빠르게 여러 번 빙글빙글 돌리더니, 와락 부딪쳐서는, 뒤죽박죽 엉킨 모양을 나타내려는 듯 서로 위로 아래로 마구 비벼 댔다. \"어쩐 일인지 나한테도 그런 비슷한 일이 일어났던 거야. 응?\"\n\n내가 그를 향해 고개를 끄덕이자, 그도 마주 끄덕였다.\n\n\"한마디로 말해서, 얘야,\" 딕 씨가 목소리를 속삭이듯 낮추며 말했다. \"나는 단순한 사람이야.\"\n\n나는 그 결론을 좀 누그러뜨려 보려 했지만, 그가 나를 막았다.\n\n\"맞아, 단순해! 그분은 안 그런 척하지. 그분은 그 말을 들으려고도 안 해. 하지만 나는 단순한 사람이야. 내가 잘 안다고. 그분이 내 편이 되어 주지 않았더라면, 나는 갇혀서 이 오랜 세월을 침울하게 살았을 걸세. 하지만 내가 그분을 보살펴 드릴 거야! 나는 필사해서 번 돈은 한 푼도 안 써. 그걸 상자에 넣어 두지. 유언장도 만들어 놨어. 그걸 죄다 그분께 남길 거야. 그분은 부자가 될 걸세 — 귀족처럼 떵떵거리며 살 거라고!\"\n\n딕 씨는 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 눈가를 닦았다. 그러고는 그것을 무척 정성스레 접어, 두 손바닥 사이에 끼워 반반하게 누른 다음, 주머니에 집어넣었다. 마치 고모님을 그 손수건과 함께 갈무리해 넣는 것 같았다.\n\n\"자, 자네는 학자야, 트롯우드.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"자네는 훌륭한 학자야. 박사 선생님이 얼마나 학식 깊은 분인지, 얼마나 위대한 분인지 자네는 알지. 그분이 늘 나를 얼마나 높이 대접해 주셨는지도 알 거야. 자기 지혜를 뽐내지 않으셔. 겸손하고, 또 겸손해서 — 단순하고 아무것도 모르는 가엾은 딕한테까지도 몸을 낮추신다고. 연이 하늘 위에, 종달새들 사이에 떠 있을 때, 나는 종잇조각에 그분 이름을 적어서 연줄을 따라 위로 올려 보냈지. 연이 그걸 기꺼이 받아 갔다네, 트롯우드. 그러니 하늘이 그 덕에 더 환해졌지.\"\n\n박사 선생님이 우리의 가장 깊은 존경과 가장 높은 흠모를 받을 만한 분이라고 내가 진심을 다해 말하자, 그는 무척 기뻐했다.\n\n\"그리고 그분의 아름다운 부인은 별이야.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"반짝이는 별이지. 나는 그분이 빛나는 걸 봤어, 트롯우드. 하지만,\" 의자를 더 가까이 끌어당기고, 한 손을 내 무릎에 얹으며 — \"먹구름이야, 트롯우드 — 먹구름.\"\n\n나는 그의 얼굴에 어린 근심에 답하듯, 똑같은 표정을 내 얼굴에 담고 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"무슨 먹구름이지?\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n그가 어찌나 애타게 내 얼굴을 들여다보며 알고 싶어 안달하던지, 나는 마치 아이에게 무언가를 설명해 주듯, 천천히 또박또박 대답하려고 무진 애를 썼다.\n\n\"두 분 사이에 무언가 불행한 골이 패었어요.\" 내가 대답했다. \"무언가 안타까운 이유로 마음이 멀어진 거죠. 비밀이에요. 어쩌면 두 분의 나이 차이와 떼어 놓을 수 없는 일인지도 모르고요. 어쩌면 거의 아무것도 아닌 데서 자라난 것인지도 모르죠.\"\n\n문장 하나하나를 사려 깊게 끄덕여 가며 헤아리던 딕 씨는, 내가 말을 마치자 잠시 멈추더니, 눈은 내 얼굴에, 손은 내 무릎에 둔 채로 앉아 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"박사 선생님이 부인한테 화가 나신 게 아니지, 트롯우드?\" 한참 있다가 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요. 부인께 헌신하고 계시죠.\"\n\n\"그럼, 알아냈어, 얘야!\" 딕 씨가 말했다.\n\n그가 느닷없이 의기양양해져서 내 무릎을 탁 치고는, 눈썹을 올릴 수 있는 데까지 한껏 치켜올린 채 의자에 등을 기대는 바람에, 나는 그가 그 어느 때보다 더 정신이 나갔다고 생각했다. 그러더니 또 갑작스레 진지해져서, 아까처럼 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 — 먼저 손수건을, 그것이 정말로 고모님을 대신하기라도 하는 듯 공손히 꺼내고는 — 말했다.\n\n\"세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인이야, 트롯우드. 그런데 그분은 왜 일을 바로잡으려고 아무것도 안 했을까?\"\n\n\"그렇게 끼어들기엔 너무 미묘하고 어려운 일이니까요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"훌륭한 학자야.\" 딕 씨가 손가락으로 나를 가리키며 말했다. \"그럼 그분은 왜 아무것도 안 했지?\"\n\n\"같은 이유에서죠.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼, 알아냈어, 얘야!\" 딕 씨가 말했다. 그러고는 아까보다 더 의기양양하게 내 앞에 우뚝 서서, 고개를 끄덕이고 제 가슴을 거듭 쳐 댔는데, 어찌나 끄덕이고 쳐 대던지 몸에서 숨이 다 빠져나갈 지경이 아닌가 싶을 정도였다.\n\n\"무언가에 홀린 가엾은 사람이,\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"얼간이가, 마음이 모자란 사람이 — 바로 여기 있는 이 사람 말이야!\" 다시 제 가슴을 치며 — \"놀라운 사람들이 못 하는 일을 해낼 수도 있는 법이지. 내가 두 분을 화해시킬 거야, 얘야. 내가 해 볼게. 두 분은 나를 탓하지 않을 거야. 두 분은 나한테 시비를 걸지 않을 거야. 내가 무슨 일을 하든, 그게 틀린 일이라 해도 두 분은 개의치 않을 거라고. 나는 그저 딕 씨일 뿐이니까. 그리고 누가 딕 따위를 마음에 두겠어? 딕은 아무것도 아닌데! 후우!\" 그는 자기 자신을 훅 불어 날려 버리기라도 하듯, 가볍게, 깔보듯 입김을 한 번 내뿜었다.\n\n그가 자기 비밀스러운 계획을 거기까지 풀어놓은 것이 다행이었으니, 마차가 작은 정원 문 앞에 멈추는 소리가 들리며 고모님과 도라가 집으로 돌아왔기 때문이다.\n\n\"한마디도 하지 마, 얘야!\" 그가 속삭이며 말을 이었다. \"모든 탓은 딕한테 다 떠넘기게 — 단순한 딕, 미친 딕 말이야. 얼마 전부터 내가 그걸 알아 가고 있다는 느낌이 들었는데, 이제 알아냈어. 자네가 나한테 해 준 말을 듣고 나니, 정말 알아낸 게 틀림없어. 좋아!\" 딕 씨는 그 일에 관해 더는 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않았다. 다만 그 후 삼십 분 동안(고모님의 마음을 어지간히도 어지럽혀 가며) 나에게 절대적인 함구령을 내리려고 제 몸을 영락없는 전신기로 만들어 댔다.\n\n뜻밖에도, 나는 그 일에 관해 두세 주쯤 아무 말도 듣지 못했다. 그의 노력의 결과가 무척이나 궁금했는데도 말이다. 그가 다다른 결론에서, 나는 묘하게 번득이는 분별력을 알아챘다 — 따뜻한 마음씨야 그가 늘 보여 주던 것이니 굳이 말할 것도 없고. 마침내 나는, 그토록 들떠 있고 진득하지 못한 그의 마음 상태로 보아, 그가 자기 뜻을 잊어버렸거나 아니면 단념해 버렸으리라고 믿기 시작했다.\n\n어느 청명한 저녁, 도라가 나가기 싫어하기에, 고모님과 나는 박사 선생님 댁까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 올라갔다. 때는 가을이라, 저녁 공기를 어지럽힐 토론도 없었다. 그리고 나는 기억한다, 발밑에 밟히는 낙엽에서 블런더스톤의 우리 집 정원 같은 냄새가 풍기던 것을, 그리고 그 오래된 불행했던 느낌이 한숨짓는 바람결을 타고 스쳐 지나가는 듯하던 것을.\n\n우리가 그 댁에 다다랐을 때는 땅거미가 내리고 있었다. 스트롱 부인은 마침 정원에서 나오던 참이었는데, 거기서는 딕 씨가 아직 칼을 들고 분주히, 정원사가 말뚝을 깎아 뾰족하게 만드는 일을 거들며 어정거리고 있었다. 박사 선생님은 서재에서 누군가와 일을 보는 중이었지만, 손님이 곧 갈 테니 남아서 만나고 가시라고 스트롱 부인이 청했다. 우리는 부인을 따라 응접실로 들어가, 어두워져 가는 창가에 앉았다. 우리처럼 오랜 벗이자 이웃인 사람들이 오갈 때 격식 따위는 차린 적이 없었다.\n\n여기 앉은 지 몇 분 되지 않아, 늘 무언가로 한바탕 부산을 떨곤 하던 마크럼 부인이, 신문을 손에 들고 헐레벌떡 들어오더니 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"어머나 세상에, 애니야, 서재에 손님이 와 있다는 걸 왜 나한테 말 안 했니!\"\n\n\"엄마.\" 애니가 차분히 대답했다. \"엄마가 그걸 알고 싶어 하실 줄 제가 어떻게 알았겠어요?\"\n\n\"알고 싶어 했냐고!\" 마크럼 부인이 소파에 털썩 주저앉으며 말했다. \"내 평생 그렇게 놀란 적이 없다!\"\n\n\"그럼 서재에 들어가 보셨어요, 엄마?\" 애니가 물었다.\n\n\"들어가 봤다마다, 얘야!\" 부인이 힘주어 대답했다. \"정말로 들어가 봤지! 그 사랑스러운 양반이 — 트롯우드 양과 데이비드, 내 심정을 좀 헤아려 주게나 — 유언장을 쓰고 있는 현장을 딱 맞닥뜨렸지 뭐냐.\"\n\n부인의 딸이 창가에서 홱 돌아보았다.\n\n\"바로 그 현장을, 우리 애니야,\" 마크럼 부인이 신문을 식탁보처럼 무릎에 쫙 펼치고 그 위를 손으로 토닥거리며 거듭 말했다. \"유언장을, 그 마지막 유언과 유증을 작성하고 있는 현장을 말이야. 그 사랑스러운 양반의 앞날을 내다보는 마음과 깊은 정이라니! 어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 내 너한테 꼭 얘기해야겠다. 그 양반을 위해서라도 정말이지 얘기해야겠어 — 그이는 더없이 사랑스러운 사람이니까 말이야! — 어쩌다 그랬는지 들어 보렴. 트롯우드 양도 아마 알 거야, 이 집에선 신문을 읽느라 눈알이 머리에서 빠져나올 지경이 되도록 죽어라 애써야 비로소 촛불 한 자루 켜 준다는 걸. 그리고 이 집엔 서재에 있는 의자 하나 빼고는, 내가 말하는 그 '읽는다'는 걸 할 만한 의자가 없다는 것도. 그래서 서재로 갔더니, 불빛이 보이지 뭐냐. 문을 열었지. 그 사랑스러운 박사 양반과 함께 보아하니 법 쪽 일을 하는 사람 둘이 있었고, 세 사람이 다 탁자 앞에 서 있었어. 그 사랑스러운 박사 양반은 펜을 손에 쥐고 말이야. '그러니까 이 문서는,' 박사 양반이 말하더라 — 애니야, 얘야, 그 말 한마디 한마디를 잘 새겨들으렴 — '그러니까 이 문서는 말입니다, 여러분, 내가 스트롱 부인에게 품은 신뢰를 그저 명시할 뿐이며, 그녀에게 모든 것을 아무 조건 없이 넘긴다는 뜻이지요?' 그 법 쪽 사람 하나가 대답하더라. '그리고 그녀에게 모든 것을 아무 조건 없이 넘긴다는 뜻입니다.' 그 말에, 어미의 본능적인 심정으로, 내가 '아이고 맙소사, 실례했습니다!' 하고는, 문턱에 발이 걸려 휘청거리며 식료품 저장실이 있는 그 작은 뒷복도로 빠져나왔단다.\"\n\n스트롱 부인이 창문을 열고 베란다로 나가, 거기 기둥에 기대어 섰다.\n\n\"하지만, 트롯우드 양, 그리고 데이비드, 이거야말로 기운이 솟는 일 아니냐,\" 마크럼 부인이 무심결에 눈으로 딸을 좇으며 말했다. \"스트롱 박사 정도 연배에, 이런 일을 해낼 만한 굳은 마음을 지닌 사람을 보다니 말이야. 이건 내 말이 옳았다는 걸 보여 주는 거야. 스트롱 박사가 나한테 더없이 영광스러운 방문을 해서 애니를 두고 사랑을 고백하고 청혼을 했을 때, 내가 애니한테 그랬거든. '얘야, 너한테 마땅한 살림 밑천이라는 점에서 보자면, 스트롱 박사는 자기가 약속하는 것보다 더 많은 걸 해 줄 거다, 내 보기엔 그건 추호도 의심할 여지가 없어.'\"\n\n이때 초인종이 울리고, 손님들이 나가는 발소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"다 끝난 게 틀림없어.\" 늙은 군인이 귀를 기울이고 나서 말했다. \"그 사랑스러운 양반이 서명하고 봉인하고 넘겨줬으니, 이제 마음이 놓이겠지. 그럴 만도 하지! 참으로 대단한 마음이야! 애니야, 얘야, 나는 신문을 들고 서재로 가야겠다. 소식거리가 없으면 영 못 견디는 가엾은 사람이라서 말이야. 트롯우드 양, 데이비드, 어서들 와서 박사 양반을 만나 보게나.\"\n\n우리가 부인을 따라 서재로 갈 때, 나는 딕 씨가 방의 그늘진 구석에 서서 칼을 접고 있는 것을 알아챘다. 또 우리 군인 양반을 향한 못마땅함을 점잖게 푸느라, 고모님이 가는 길에 코를 사납게 문질러 대는 것도 알아챘다. 그러나 누가 먼저 서재에 들어갔는지, 마크럼 부인이 어떻게 순식간에 안락의자에 자리를 잡았는지, 또 어쩌다가 고모님과 내가 문가에 단둘이 남게 됐는지(고모님의 눈이 내 눈보다 빨라서 나를 붙들어 세운 것이 아니라면 말이다)는, 설령 한때 알았다 한들 지금은 다 잊어버렸다. 하지만 이것만은 안다. 우리가 박사 선생님보다 먼저 그분을 보았다는 것을. 그분이 자기가 좋아하던 2절판 책들 사이, 탁자 앞에 앉아, 손에 머리를 차분히 괴고 있던 것을. 바로 그 순간, 스트롱 부인이 창백하게 떨며 미끄러지듯 들어오는 것을 우리가 보았다는 것을. 딕 씨가 자기 팔로 부인을 부축하고 있었다는 것을. 그가 다른 한 손을 박사 선생님의 팔에 얹자, 박사 선생님이 멍한 얼굴로 고개를 들었다는 것을. 박사 선생님이 머리를 움직이자, 그 아내가 그분 발치에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 두 손을 애원하듯 치켜든 채, 내가 결코 잊은 적 없는 그 잊지 못할 눈빛으로 그분의 얼굴을 지그시 응시했다는 것을. 이 광경을 보고 마크럼 부인이 신문을 떨어뜨리더니, 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 무엇보다도, 마치 '경악호'라는 이름이 붙을 배를 위해 만든 뱃머리 조각상처럼 멀거니 바라보았다는 것을.\n\n박사 선생님의 그 부드러운 태도와 놀란 기색, 그 아내의 애원하는 자세에 어린 위엄, 딕 씨의 다정한 근심, 그리고 고모님이 \"저 사람 미쳤어!\"라고 혼잣말하던 그 진지함(그가 저 사람을 구해 냈다는 그 비참한 처지를 의기양양하게 드러내던 말)을 — 이 일을 글로 적는 지금, 나는 그것을 기억한다기보다 차라리 보고 듣는 것만 같다.\n\n\"박사 선생님!\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"뭐가 잘못된 겁니까? 여기 좀 보세요!\"\n\n\"애니!\" 박사 선생님이 외쳤다. \"내 발치에 무릎 꿇지 마오, 여보!\"\n\n\"아니에요!\" 부인이 말했다. \"제발 부탁이니 아무도 이 방을 떠나지 말아 주세요! 오, 내 남편이자 아버지여, 이 기나긴 침묵을 깨뜨려 주세요. 우리 사이를 갈라놓은 것이 무엇인지, 우리 둘 다 알게 해 주세요!\"\n\n이때쯤 말할 기운을 되찾고, 가문의 자존심과 어미로서의 분노로 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 듯한 마크럼 부인이 여기서 소리쳤다. \"애니, 당장 일어나라. 그렇게 제 몸을 낮춰서 네 식구들 모두를 욕보이지 마. 당장 이 자리에서 내가 정신 줄 놓는 꼴을 보고 싶은 게 아니라면 말이야!\"\n\n\"엄마!\" 애니가 대답했다. \"저한테 말을 낭비하지 마세요. 제 호소는 제 남편을 향한 거예요. 그러니 여기서는 엄마조차도 아무것도 아니에요.\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니라고!\" 마크럼 부인이 외쳤다. \"내가, 아무것도 아니라니! 저 애가 제정신이 아니구나. 누구 나한테 물 한 잔만 가져다주게!\"\n\n나는 박사 선생님과 그 아내에게 온통 마음을 빼앗겨, 이 요청에 조금도 신경 쓰지 못했다. 그리고 다른 누구에게도 그 말은 아무 인상을 주지 못했다. 그래서 마크럼 부인은 헐떡이고, 멀거니 바라보고, 부채질만 했다.\n\n\"애니!\" 박사 선생님이 다정하게 그녀의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"여보! 세월이 흐르는 가운데 우리 부부의 삶에 어쩔 수 없는 변화가 찾아온 것이라면, 그건 당신 탓이 아니오. 잘못은 내게 있소, 오직 내게만. 당신을 향한 내 애정과 흠모와 존경에는 아무런 변함이 없소. 나는 당신을 행복하게 해 주고 싶소. 나는 진심으로 당신을 사랑하고 받드오. 일어나오, 애니, 제발!\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 일어나지 않았다. 잠시 그를 바라보던 그녀는, 그에게 더 바짝 몸을 낮추어, 한쪽 팔을 그의 무릎 위에 걸치고, 그 위에 머리를 떨군 채 말했다.\n\n\"이 일에서 저를 위해, 혹은 제 남편을 위해 한마디라도 해 줄 벗이 여기 있다면, 제 마음이 이따금 저에게 속삭이던 어떤 의심에 목소리를 실어 줄 벗이 여기 있다면, 제 남편을 존경하거나 저를 한 번이라도 아껴 준 적이 있고, 우리 사이를 중재하는 데 도움이 될 만한 것을, 그것이 무엇이든, 알고 있는 벗이 여기 있다면, 부디 그 벗에게 간청합니다, 말씀해 주세요!\"\n\n깊은 침묵이 흘렀다. 괴로운 머뭇거림이 잠시 이어진 끝에, 내가 그 침묵을 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"스트롱 부인.\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 알고 있는 것이 하나 있습니다. 그것을 감춰 달라고 스트롱 박사 선생님께서 간곡히 부탁하셔서, 오늘 밤까지 감추고 있었습니다. 하지만 이제는, 그것을 더 감추는 것이 잘못된 믿음이자 잘못된 배려가 될 때가 왔다고 저는 믿습니다. 그리고 부인의 호소가 저를 그분의 당부에서 풀어 주는군요.\"\n\n부인이 잠시 내 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸고, 나는 내가 옳았음을 알았다. 그 얼굴이 내게 준 확신이 설령 덜 미더운 것이었다 해도, 나는 그 간청을 도저히 뿌리치지 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"우리의 앞날의 평화가,\" 부인이 말했다. \"당신 손에 달려 있을지도 모릅니다. 당신이 무엇 하나 숨기지 않으리라 믿고, 그것을 당신께 온전히 맡깁니다. 당신이든 그 누구든 제게 무슨 말을 해도, 제 남편의 고귀한 마음을 오직 한 가지 빛으로밖에는 비추지 못하리라는 걸 저는 미리 알고 있습니다. 그것이 저를 어떻게 건드리는 것처럼 당신께 보이든, 그건 개의치 마세요. 제 일은 제가 직접, 남편 앞에서, 그리고 그다음엔 하느님 앞에서 말하겠습니다.\"\n\n이렇듯 간절히 청을 받았기에, 나는 박사 선생님에게 허락을 구하지도 않고, 유라이아 힙의 그 추악함을 약간 누그러뜨린 것 말고는 진실을 조금도 굽히지 않은 채, 바로 그 방에서 그날 밤 있었던 일을 있는 그대로 이야기했다. 이야기가 이어지는 내내 마크럼 부인이 멀거니 바라보던 모습과, 가끔 그것을 끊고 끼어들던 새되고 날카로운 탄성은 이루 다 말로 할 수가 없다.\n\n내가 이야기를 마쳤을 때, 애니는 앞서 말한 대로 고개를 숙인 채로 잠시 말없이 가만있었다. 이윽고 그녀는 박사 선생님의 손을(그분은 우리가 방에 들어왔을 때와 똑같은 자세로 앉아 있었다) 잡아, 자기 가슴에 꼭 눌러 대고 입을 맞추었다. 딕 씨가 그녀를 살그머니 일으켜 세웠다. 그녀는 말을 시작할 때, 그에게 기대어 선 채, 남편을 내려다보았다 — 한 번도 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채로.\n\n\"결혼한 뒤로 제 마음속에 있던 모든 것을,\" 그녀가 낮고, 순종적이고, 다정한 목소리로 말했다. \"당신 앞에 낱낱이 풀어놓겠어요. 지금 제가 아는 것을 안 이상, 단 하나라도 숨긴 채로는 도저히 살아갈 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"아니오, 애니.\" 박사 선생님이 부드럽게 말했다. \"나는 당신을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없소, 내 아이여. 그럴 필요 없소. 정말로 그럴 필요 없소, 여보.\"\n\n\"꼭 그래야 해요.\" 그녀가 같은 투로 대답했다. \"해마다, 날마다 점점 더 사랑하고 우러러 온, 너그러움과 진실의 화신이신 그분 앞에 제 마음을 통째로 열어 보여야 해요. 하늘이 아실 거예요!\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 마크럼 부인이 끼어들었다. \"나한테 분별이라는 게 조금이라도 있다면 —\"\n\n(\"자네한텐 그런 게 없어, 이 훼방꾼아.\" 고모님이 분개한 목소리로 속삭이듯 말했다.) \" — 이런 시시콜콜한 얘기까지 다 늘어놓을 필요는 없다고 한마디 해야겠다.\"\n\n\"그건 제 남편 말고는 아무도 판단할 수 없어요, 엄마.\" 애니가 남편의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. \"그리고 남편은 제 말을 들어 줄 거예요. 제가 무슨 말을 해서 엄마 마음을 아프게 한다면, 용서하세요. 저는 그 아픔을, 누구보다 먼저, 자주, 그리고 오래 견뎌 왔으니까요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 별꼴이야!\" 마크럼 부인이 숨을 헐떡였다.\n\n\"제가 아주 어렸을 때,\" 애니가 말했다. \"그야말로 어린아이였을 때, 어떤 종류든 앎이라는 것과 처음 맺은 인연은, 한 분의 인내심 깊은 벗이자 스승과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것이었어요 — 돌아가신 제 아버지의 벗이자, 제겐 늘 소중한 분이셨죠. 제가 아는 것 가운데, 그분을 떠올리지 않고 떠올릴 수 있는 건 하나도 없어요. 그분은 제 마음에 처음으로 보물을 채워 주셨고, 그 모든 것에 자신의 인품을 새겨 넣으셨어요. 만약 제가 그것을 다른 누구의 손에서 받았더라면, 지금처럼 제게 좋은 것이 되지는 못했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"제 어미는 아무것도 아니라는 거구나!\" 마크럼 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그게 아니에요, 엄마.\" 애니가 말했다. \"저는 그분이 어떤 분이었는지를 있는 그대로 말씀드리는 거예요. 꼭 그래야만 해요. 제가 자라는 동안에도 그분은 여전히 같은 자리에 계셨어요. 저는 그분의 관심이 자랑스러웠어요. 깊이, 살뜰하게, 고마운 마음으로 그분께 매여 있었죠. 저는 그분을 우러러보았어요, 뭐라 표현하기도 어렵지만 — 아버지처럼, 길잡이처럼, 그분의 칭찬은 다른 어떤 칭찬과도 다른, 온 세상을 다 의심하게 되더라도 그분만은 믿고 마음을 털어놓을 수 있는 그런 분으로요. 엄마도 아시죠, 엄마가 갑자기 그분을 제 앞에 연인으로 내세웠을 때, 제가 얼마나 어리고 세상 물정 모르는 아이였는지를요.\"\n\n\"내 그 얘기는 여기 있는 누구한테든 적어도 쉰 번은 했다!\" 마크럼 부인이 말했다.\n\n(\"그럼 제발 좀 입 다물고, 더는 그 얘기 꺼내지 말게나!\" 고모님이 중얼거렸다.)\n\n\"그건 너무도 큰 변화였고, 너무도 큰 상실이라, 처음엔 그렇게 느껴졌어요.\" 애니가 여전히 같은 눈빛과 같은 목소리를 지키며 말했다. \"그래서 마음이 흔들리고 괴로웠죠. 저는 그저 어린 소녀였으니까요. 그토록 오랫동안 우러러보던 그분의 자리가 그렇게 크게 바뀌니, 제 생각엔 서글펐던 것 같아요. 하지만 그 무엇도 그분을 예전의 그분으로 되돌려 놓을 순 없었어요. 그리고 그분이 저를 그토록 합당한 사람으로 여겨 주신다는 게 자랑스러웠죠. 그래서 우리는 결혼했어요.\" \" — 캔터베리의 세인트 앨피지 성당에서 말이야.\" 마크럼 부인이 한마디 거들었다.\n\n(\"저 여편네 진짜!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"도무지 조용히 있질 못하는군!\")\n\n\"저는 한 번도,\" 애니가 얼굴빛을 붉히며 말을 이었다. \"제 남편이 제게 가져다줄 세속적인 이득 따위를 생각해 본 적이 없어요. 제 어린 마음은 그분을 향한 흠모로 가득 차서, 그런 천박한 셈속이 끼어들 틈이 없었어요. 엄마, 이 말을 하는 저를 용서하세요. 그렇게 잔인한 의심으로 누군가가 저를, 그리고 그분을 욕보일 수 있다는 생각을 제 머릿속에 처음 심어 준 건 바로 엄마였어요.\"\n\n\"내가!\" 마크럼 부인이 소리쳤다.\n\n(\"아, 그래! 바로 자네지!\" 고모님이 한마디 했다. \"그건 부채질로도 못 날려 보내, 우리 군인 양반!\")\n\n\"그게 제 새 삶의 첫 번째 불행이었어요.\" 애니가 말했다. \"제가 겪은 모든 불행한 순간의 첫 빌미였죠. 그런 순간들이 요즘 들어 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많아졌어요. 하지만 — 너그러우신 제 남편이여! — 당신이 짐작하시는 그런 이유 때문은 아니에요. 제 마음속에는, 그 어떤 힘으로도 당신과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 생각도, 추억도, 희망도 단 하나뿐이니까요!\"\n\n그녀는 눈을 들어 두 손을 맞잡았는데, 내가 보기엔 그 어떤 천사 못지않게 아름답고 진실해 보였다. 박사 선생님은 이제부터 줄곧, 그녀가 자기를 바라보던 그 한결같음으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"엄마에게는 아무 잘못이 없어요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"엄마가 자신을 위해 당신을 졸랐던 적은 한 번도 없으니까요. 그리고 그 의도에 있어서는 어느 모로 보나 엄마에게 아무 잘못이 없다고 저는 확신해요 — 하지만 제 이름으로 그토록 많은 끈질긴 요구가 당신께 밀려드는 것을, 제 이름이 그렇게 흥정거리가 되는 것을, 당신이 얼마나 너그러우신지를, 그리고 당신의 안녕을 무척이나 마음 쓰던 위크필드 씨가 그것을 얼마나 못마땅해하던지를 보았을 때 — 제 다정함이 돈에 팔려, 그것도 세상 모든 사람 가운데 하필 당신께 팔려 넘어갔다는 그 비열한 의심에 제가 내던져졌다는 첫 깨달음이, 마치 까닭 없는 치욕처럼 제게 덮쳐 왔어요. 그리고 저는 당신마저 그 치욕에 끌어들이고 만 거예요. 이 두려움과 괴로움을 늘 마음에 짊어진 채, 그러면서도 제 영혼 깊이로는, 결혼하던 그날 제가 제 삶의 사랑과 명예에 면류관을 씌웠다는 걸 알고 있던 것이 — 어떤 심정이었는지 당신께 말로 다 할 수가 없어요. 엄마는 짐작조차 못 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"가족을 보살펴 준 대가로 받는 고마움이 고작 이런 거구나!\" 마크럼 부인이 눈물을 흘리며 외쳤다. \"차라리 내가 터키 사람이었으면 좋겠다!\"\n\n(\"나야말로 진심으로 그러길 바라네 — 그것도 자네 그 나라에 가서 말이야!\" 고모님이 말했다.)\n\n\"바로 그 무렵 엄마는 제 사촌 몰던 일로 가장 안달복달하셨어요. 저는 그를 좋아했었죠.\" 그녀는 부드럽게, 그러나 조금의 머뭇거림도 없이 말했다. \"아주 많이요. 우리는 한때 어린 연인 사이였어요. 사정이 다르게 흘러가지 않았더라면, 저는 그를 정말로 사랑한다고 스스로를 설득하고 말았을지도 몰라요. 그래서 그와 결혼해 더없이 비참하게 살았을지도 모르죠. 결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없으니까요.\"\n\n나는 그 뒤에 이어지는 말에 골똘히 귀 기울이면서도, 마치 그 말들에 무언가 각별한 뜻이, 아니면 내가 헤아릴 수 없는 어떤 기묘한 적용이 담겨 있기라도 한 듯, 그 말을 곱씹었다. '결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없다' — '결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없다.'\n\n\"우리 사이엔,\" 애니가 말했다. \"함께 나눌 만한 게 아무것도 없어요. 아무것도 없다는 걸 안 지 오래됐죠. 설령 제가 그토록 많은 게 아니라 그저 이 한 가지만으로 제 남편께 감사한다 해도, 저는 그분께 감사할 거예요 — 다스려지지 않은 제 마음의 그 첫 잘못된 충동에서 저를 구해 주신 것을 말이에요.\"\n\n그녀는 박사 선생님 앞에 꼼짝도 않고 서서, 나를 전율케 하는 간절함으로 말했다. 그러면서도 그 목소리는 아까와 똑같이 잔잔했다.\n\n\"그가, 저를 위해 그토록 아낌없이 베풀어 주신 당신의 후한 베풂의 대상이 되기를 기다리고 있었을 때, 그리고 제가 억지로 뒤집어쓴 그 돈에 팔린 모습 때문에 괴로워하고 있었을 때, 저는 그가 차라리 제 힘으로 살길을 헤쳐 나갔더라면 더 떳떳했으리라고 생각했어요. 제가 그였다면, 거의 어떤 고생을 무릅쓰고서라도 그렇게 해 보려 했을 거라고요. 하지만 그가 인도로 떠나던 그날 밤 전까지는, 저는 그를 그보다 더 나쁘게 여기지는 않았어요. 그날 밤, 저는 그가 거짓되고 배은망덕한 마음을 지녔다는 걸 알았어요. 그때 저는 위크필드 씨가 저를 살피던 그 눈길에 숨은 이중의 뜻을 보았어요. 그리고 처음으로, 제 삶에 그늘을 드리운 그 어두운 의심을 알아차렸죠.\"\n\n\"의심이라니, 애니!\" 박사 선생님이 말했다. \"아니, 아니, 아니오!\"\n\n\"당신 마음속엔 그런 게 없었다는 거, 저도 알아요, 여보!\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그날 밤 제가 당신께 가서 제 모든 부끄러움과 슬픔의 짐을 내려놓으려 했을 때, 그리고 당신의 지붕 아래서, 당신이 저를 사랑하는 마음에 은혜를 베풀어 주신 제 친척 가운데 하나가, 설령 제가 그가 여기던 그 나약하고 돈에 눈먼 계집이었다 한들 결코 입에 담아선 안 될 말을 제게 했다는 걸 털어놓아야 한다는 걸 알았을 때 — 그 이야기를 입에 올리는 것만으로도 묻어나는 그 더러움에 제 마음이 진저리를 쳤어요. 그 말은 제 입술에서 죽어 버렸고, 그 순간부터 지금까지 단 한 번도 그 입술을 넘은 적이 없어요.\"\n\n마크럼 부인이 짤막한 신음을 내뱉으며 안락의자에 등을 기대고는, 다시는 나오지 않을 사람처럼 부채 뒤로 몸을 숨겼다.\n\n\"그때부터 저는, 당신이 있는 자리가 아니고서는, 그와 단 한마디도 주고받은 적이 없어요. 그것도 오직 이런 해명을 피하기 위해 어쩔 수 없을 때뿐이었죠. 그가 이곳에서 자기 처지가 어떤 것인지를 제 입을 통해 알게 된 지도 여러 해가 흘렀어요. 당신이 그의 출세를 위해 남몰래 베풀어 주신 친절들, 그러다 저를 놀라게 하고 기쁘게 해 주려고 제게 털어놓으신 그 친절들이, 짐작하시겠지만, 제 비밀의 불행과 무게를 더 무겁게 했을 뿐이었어요.\"\n\n박사 선생님이 한사코 막으려 했지만, 그녀는 그분 발치에 살며시 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 눈물 어린 눈으로 그분의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"아직 제게 아무 말씀 마세요! 조금만 더 말하게 해 주세요! 옳든 그르든, 만약 이 일을 다시 하게 된다 해도, 저는 똑같이 했을 거라고 생각해요. 그 오랜 정과 함께 당신께 헌신한다는 게 어떤 것인지, 누군가가 그토록 모질게도 제 마음의 진실이 흥정거리로 팔려 넘어갔다고 짐작할 수 있다는 걸 알게 되는 게, 그리고 그 믿음을 굳혀 주는 겉모습들에 둘러싸이는 게 어떤 것인지, 당신은 결코 모르실 거예요. 저는 너무 어렸고, 의논할 사람도 없었어요. 당신에 관한 모든 일에서, 엄마와 저 사이엔 넓은 골이 있었죠. 제가 겪은 그 멸시를 감추며 제 안으로 움츠러들었다면, 그건 제가 당신을 그토록 존경했고, 당신도 저를 존경해 주시기를 그토록 바랐기 때문이에요!\"\n\n\"애니, 내 순결한 마음이여!\" 박사 선생님이 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 아이여!\"\n\n\"조금만 더요! 정말 몇 마디만 더요! 저는 늘 생각했어요, 당신이 결혼할 수 있었던 수많은 여자가 있었을 텐데, 그 여자들이라면 당신께 이런 짐과 골칫거리를 안기지 않고, 당신의 집을 더 떳떳한 보금자리로 만들어 주었을 텐데, 하고요. 저는 차라리 당신의 제자로, 아니 거의 당신의 아이로 남아 있는 편이 나았으리라고 두려워하곤 했어요. 제가 당신의 학식과 지혜에 너무도 어울리지 않는 사람이라고 늘 겁이 났죠. 이 모든 것이 저를 제 안으로 움츠러들게 했다면(정말 그랬어요), 그 털어놓아야 할 것을 품고 있었을 때조차, 그건 여전히 제가 당신을 그토록 존경했고, 언젠가 당신도 저를 존경해 주시기를 바랐기 때문이에요.\"\n\n\"그날은 이미 오래전부터 환히 빛나고 있었소, 애니.\" 박사 선생님이 말했다. \"그리고 그 뒤에 올 긴 밤은 오직 하나뿐일 거요, 여보.\"\n\n\"한마디만 더요! 그 뒤로 저는 마음먹었어요 — 굳게 마음먹고, 스스로에게 다짐했어요 — 당신이 그토록 잘해 주신 사람의 그 못난 됨됨이를 안다는 그 무게를, 통째로 혼자 짊어지겠다고요. 그리고 이제 마지막 한마디예요, 더없이 소중하고 가장 좋은 벗이여! 제가 그토록 큰 아픔과 슬픔으로 지켜보았고, 때로는 제 오랜 불안 탓으로, 또 때로는 진실에 더 가까운, 마음속에 맴도는 어떤 짐작 탓으로 돌리곤 했던, 요즘 당신께 일어난 그 변화의 까닭이 오늘 밤 밝혀졌어요. 그리고 어쩌다 보니 저는 오늘 밤, 그 오해 속에서조차 당신이 저를 얼마나 고귀하게 믿어 주셨는지 그 깊이를 온전히 알게 됐어요. 제가 그 보답으로 바칠 어떤 사랑과 정성도, 저를 당신의 그 값을 매길 수 없는 믿음에 합당한 사람으로 만들어 주리라고는 바라지 않아요. 하지만 이 모든 깨달음이 제 안에 생생한 지금, 저는 아버지처럼 우러르고, 남편처럼 사랑하고, 어린 시절엔 벗으로서 거룩하게 여겼던 이 소중한 얼굴을 향해 눈을 들어, 엄숙히 선언할 수 있어요. 저는 그 가장 가벼운 생각 속에서조차 당신을 한 번도 저버린 적이 없으며, 당신께 마땅히 바쳐야 할 사랑과 정절에서 단 한 번도 흔들린 적이 없다고요!\"\n\n그녀는 두 팔로 박사 선생님의 목을 감싸 안았고, 그분은 그 위로 고개를 숙여, 자신의 백발을 그녀의 짙은 갈색 머리채에 뒤섞었다.\n\n\"오, 저를 당신 가슴에 꼭 안아 주세요, 여보! 저를 결코 내치지 마세요! 우리 사이에 어긋남이 있다는 생각도, 말도 하지 마세요. 그런 건 없으니까요. 제 그 많은 부족함 말고는요. 한 해 한 해 지날수록 저는 이 사실을 더 잘 알게 됐어요, 당신을 점점 더 우러러보게 되면서요. 오, 저를 당신 가슴에 안아 주세요, 여보. 제 사랑은 반석 위에 세워졌고, 그래서 영원하니까요!\"\n\n뒤이은 침묵 속에, 고모님은 조금도 서두르는 기색 없이 진중하게 딕 씨에게 걸어가더니, 그를 와락 끌어안고 쪽 소리 나게 입을 맞추었다. 그렇게 한 것은 딕 씨의 체면을 봐서 참으로 다행한 일이었으니, 바로 그 순간 그가 기쁨을 적절히 드러내는 표현으로 한쪽 다리로 서려고 채비하는 것을 내가 분명히 보았다고 확신하기 때문이다.\n\n\"자네는 정말 비범한 사람일세, 딕!\" 고모님이 두말없는 칭찬의 기색으로 말했다. \"그러니 절대 다른 사람인 척하지 말게, 내가 더 잘 아니까!\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 고모님은 딕 씨의 소매를 잡아끌며 나에게 고갯짓을 했다. 그리하여 우리 셋은 조용히 방에서 살그머니 빠져나와 그 자리를 떠났다.\n\n\"어쨌거나 우리 군인 양반한테는 이걸로 끝장이 난 셈이지.\" 고모님이 집으로 돌아오는 길에 말했다. \"다른 기뻐할 일이 아무것도 없다 해도, 이것만으로도 오늘 밤은 발 뻗고 잘 수 있겠어!\"\n\n\"그분이 무척이나 북받쳐 하시던데요.\" 딕 씨가 깊이 가엾어하며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 자네 악어가 북받쳐 하는 거 본 적 있나?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 악어를 본 적이 있는지 모르겠는데요.\" 딕 씨가 점잖게 대답했다.\n\n\"그 늙은 짐승만 아니었으면 애당초 아무 탈도 없었을 거야.\" 고모님이 힘주어 말했다. \"어떤 어미들은 딸이 결혼하고 나면 좀 내버려 두고, 그렇게 극성맞게 끼고돌지 않았으면 정말 좋겠어. 그런 어미들은 가엾은 젊은 여자 하나를 세상에 내보내 준 대가로 받을 수 있는 유일한 보답이 — 아이고 맙소사, 그 애가 세상에 내보내 달라고 졸랐거나, 나오고 싶어 안달이라도 했던 것처럼 말이지! — 그 애를 다시 들들 볶아서 세상 밖으로 몰아낼 완전한 권리라고 여기는 모양이야. 무슨 생각을 그렇게 하나, 트롯?\"\n\n나는 오갔던 그 모든 말을 생각하고 있었다. 내 머릿속에는 거기서 쓰인 몇몇 표현이 여전히 맴돌고 있었다. '결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없다.' '다스려지지 않은 마음의 그 첫 잘못된 충동.' '제 사랑은 반석 위에 세워졌어요.' 그러나 우리는 어느새 집에 다다랐다. 밟힌 낙엽이 발밑에 깔려 있었고, 가을바람이 불고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 46. INTELLIGENCE\n\n결혼한 지 한 일 년쯤 지났을 무렵이었던 것 같다. 날짜에 관한 한 내 기억은 영 미덥지 못하니 그쯤으로 믿어 둘 수밖에 없다. 어느 저녁, 나는 혼자 산책을 마치고 돌아오던 길에, 그 무렵 쓰고 있던 책을 골똘히 생각하고 있었다. 꾸준히 매달린 만큼 내 성공도 차곡차곡 쌓여 갔고, 나는 그때 첫 소설을 붙들고 있었다. 그러다 스티어포스 부인의 집 앞을 지나게 되었다. 그 동네에 사는 동안 나는 그 집 앞을 자주 지나다녔는데, 다른 길을 고를 수 있을 때는 한 번도 그쪽으로 가지 않았다. 그러나 멀리 빙 돌지 않고서는 다른 길을 찾기가 쉽지 않을 때도 더러 있어서, 결국 따지고 보면 그 길을 꽤 자주 지난 셈이었다.\n\n나는 걸음을 빨리하며 그 집을 흘끗 보는 것 말고는 한 번도 더 눈여겨본 적이 없었다. 그 집은 한결같이 음침하고 칙칙했다. 가장 좋은 방들은 어느 것도 길에 면해 있지 않았고, 좁고 묵직한 틀을 두른 구식 창문들은 어떤 상황에서도 명랑해 보인 적이 없었으며, 늘 꽉 닫힌 채 차양을 내리고 있어 몹시 을씨년스러웠다. 작은 포석 깔린 안뜰을 가로질러 한 번도 쓰인 적 없는 출입구로 이어지는 지붕 덮인 통로가 있었다. 그리고 다른 것들과는 어긋나게, 차양에 가려지지 않은 유일한 둥근 계단 창이 하나 있었는데, 그것 역시 똑같이 비어 있는 듯한 멍한 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그 집 전체에서 불빛을 본 기억이 나는 없다. 만약 내가 우연히 지나가는 행인이었다면, 그 안에 자식 없는 누군가가 죽어 누워 있다고 짐작했을 것이다. 다행히 그 집을 전혀 모르는 채로 변함없는 그 모습을 자주 보았다면, 나는 분명 온갖 기발한 상상을 떠올리며 공상을 즐겼을 것이다.\n\n그러나 사정이 사정인 만큼, 나는 되도록 그 집을 떠올리지 않으려 했다. 하지만 내 몸은 그 집을 지나쳐 버릴 수 있어도, 내 마음은 그러지 못했다. 그 집은 으레 길고 긴 상념의 행렬을 깨워 놓았다. 내가 말하는 바로 그날 저녁에는, 어린 시절의 기억과 훗날의 공상, 채 영글지도 못한 희망의 유령들, 어렴풋이 보이고 어렴풋이 헤아려지는 실망의 부서진 그림자들, 그리고 그 무렵 내 생각을 분주하게 만들던 일에 따라붙기 마련인 경험과 상상의 뒤섞임이 한데 어우러져 떠올랐고, 그래서 그것은 여느 때보다 더 많은 것을 일깨웠다. 나는 걷는 내내 깊은 명상에 빠져들었고, 옆에서 들려온 목소리에 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n게다가 그것은 여자 목소리였다. 나는 곧 그것이 스티어포스 부인의 어린 응접실 하녀임을 떠올렸다. 예전에는 모자에 파란 리본을 달고 다니던 아이였다. 이제는 그 리본을 떼어 냈는데, 짐작건대 달라진 집안 분위기에 맞추려는 것이었으리라. 지금은 칙칙한 갈색의 처량한 나비매듭 한두 개만 달고 있었다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 선생님. 죄송하지만 안으로 드셔서 다틀 아가씨와 말씀 좀 나누어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"다틀 양이 나를 부르라고 너를 보냈느냐?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 아니에요, 선생님. 하지만 마찬가지예요. 다틀 아가씨가 하루 이틀 전에 선생님이 지나가시는 걸 보셨거든요. 그래서 저더러 계단에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있다가, 선생님이 또 지나가시거든 안으로 모시고 와서 말씀을 나누시게 하라고 하셨어요.\"\n\n나는 발길을 돌렸고, 가는 동안 안내하는 아이에게 스티어포스 부인은 어떠시냐고 물었다. 아이는 마님이 편찮으셔서 방에만 많이 계신다고 했다.\n\n집에 다다르자 나는 정원에 있는 다틀 양에게로 안내되었고, 내가 왔음을 직접 알리도록 그 자리에 남겨졌다. 그녀는 일종의 테라스 한쪽 끝에 놓인 자리에 앉아, 저 아래 거대한 도시를 내려다보고 있었다. 음울한 저녁이었고, 하늘에는 불그죽죽한 빛이 어려 있었다. 저 멀리 풍경이 잔뜩 찌푸린 듯 보이고, 그 침침한 노을 속에 여기저기 큼직한 윤곽들이 불쑥불쑥 솟아오르는 것을 바라보면서, 나는 그것이 이 사나운 여인을 떠올리기에 그리 어울리지 않는 동반자는 아니라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n그녀는 내가 다가오는 것을 보고 잠깐 일어서서 나를 맞았다. 그때 나는 그녀가 지난번에 보았을 때보다 한층 더 핏기 없고 야위었다고 느꼈다. 번뜩이는 두 눈은 여전히 더 형형했고, 흉터는 여전히 더 또렷했다.\n\n우리의 만남은 다정하지 않았다. 지난번에 우리는 성을 내며 헤어졌다. 그녀에게는 굳이 감추려 들지 않는 경멸의 기색이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"저더러 할 말이 있으시다고 들었습니다, 다틀 양.\" 내가 그녀 곁에 서서, 한 손을 자리 등받이에 얹은 채, 앉으라는 손짓을 사양하며 말했다.\n\n\"그러시지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래, 그 계집은 찾았나요?\"\n\"못 찾았습니다.\"\n\n\"그런데도 도망을 쳤다니!\"\n\n나를 바라보는 그녀의 얇은 입술이 달싹이는 것이 보였다. 마치 그 입술이 그 아이에게 비난을 한껏 퍼붓고 싶어 안달이 난 것 같았다.\n\n\"도망을 쳤다고요?\" 내가 되물었다.\n\n\"그래요! 그 사람한테서요.\" 그녀가 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"못 찾는다면, 어쩌면 영영 못 찾을지도 모르죠. 죽었을지도 모르고요!\"\n\n내 눈길을 받아 내며 으스대듯 드러내던 그 잔인함은, 내가 이제껏 본 그 어떤 얼굴에서도 그런 식으로 드러나는 것을 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"그 아이가 죽기를 바라는 것은,\" 내가 말했다. \"같은 여자가 그 아이에게 베풀 수 있는 가장 다정한 소망일지도 모르겠군요. 세월이 당신을 그토록 누그러뜨려 놓아서 다행입니다, 다틀 양.\"\n\n그녀는 굳이 대꾸하는 수고를 마다하고, 또 한 번 비웃음을 흘리며 나를 돌아보고는 말했다.\n\n\"이 훌륭하고 크게 상처 입은 아가씨의 벗들이 당신의 벗들이시죠. 당신은 그들의 투사이고, 그들의 권리를 주장하시고요. 그 계집에 대해 알려진 것을 알고 싶으세요?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 험상궂은 미소를 지으며 일어나, 가까이에 있던, 잔디밭과 채소밭을 가르는 호랑가시나무 울타리 쪽으로 몇 걸음 다가가더니, 더 큰 목소리로 말했다. \"이리 와!\" 마치 무슨 더러운 짐승이라도 부르는 듯한 투였다.\n\n\"물론 이 자리에서 과시하듯 투사 노릇을 하거나 복수를 벌이는 일은 삼가시겠지요, 코퍼필드 씨?\" 그녀가 어깨너머로 나를 돌아보며 아까와 똑같은 표정으로 말했다.\n\n나는 그녀가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지도 모른 채 고개를 숙였다. 그녀는 다시 \"이리 와!\" 하고 부르더니, 점잖은 리티머 씨를 거느리고 돌아왔다. 리티머 씨는 여전히 점잖음을 조금도 잃지 않은 채 나에게 인사를 하고는 그녀의 뒤에 가서 섰다. 그녀가 우리 사이의 자리에 비스듬히 기대어 나를 바라보던 그 사악한 우아함, 의기양양함의 기색에는, 이상하게도 어딘가 여성적이고 매혹적인 무엇이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 전설 속 잔인한 공주에게나 어울릴 법한 것이었다.\n\n\"자.\" 그녀가 그를 흘끗도 보지 않은 채 위압적으로 말하며, 욱신거리는 그 오래된 상처를 어루만졌다. 어쩌면 이번만큼은 고통이 아니라 쾌감으로 그랬는지도 모른다. \"코퍼필드 씨에게 그 도망 이야기를 들려 드려.\"\n\n\"제임스 도련님과 저는, 마님—\"\n\n\"나한테 말 걸지 마!\" 그녀가 눈살을 찌푸리며 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"제임스 도련님과 저는, 선생님—\"\n\n\"저한테도 말 걸지 마시지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n리티머 씨는 조금도 흐트러지지 않고, 가볍게 머리를 조아려 우리에게 가장 흡족한 것이라면 무엇이든 자기에게도 가장 흡족하다는 뜻을 내비치고는, 다시 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"제임스 도련님과 저는 그 젊은 여자가 야머스를 떠나 도련님의 보호 아래 들어온 뒤로 줄곧 함께 외국에 나가 있었습니다. 여러 곳을 다녔고, 이국땅을 많이 구경했습니다. 프랑스, 스위스, 이탈리아, 사실상 거의 모든 곳을 다 다녔습니다.\"\n\n그는 마치 그 자리 등받이를 향해 말하기라도 하듯 그쪽을 바라보았고, 마치 소리 안 나는 피아노 건반을 짚어 화음을 내듯, 두 손으로 그 등받이를 부드럽게 두드렸다.\n\n\"제임스 도련님은 그 젊은 여자에게 자못 유별나게 빠져들었습니다. 그래서 한동안은, 제가 도련님을 모신 이래로 본 적이 없을 만큼 마음을 잡고 차분하게 지내셨습니다. 그 젊은 여자는 무척 가르치기 좋은 사람이라 외국어도 배워 익혔고, 도무지 같은 시골 출신이라고는 알아볼 수 없을 정도였습니다. 가는 곳마다 많은 사람의 찬사를 받는 것을 저도 눈여겨보았습니다.\"\n\n다틀 양이 한 손을 옆구리에 갖다 댔다. 나는 그가 그녀를 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 혼자 살짝 미소 짓는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"정말로, 그 젊은 여자는 대단한 찬사를 받았습니다. 옷차림이며, 바닷바람과 햇볕이며, 그토록 떠받들어졌던 것이며, 이것저것 온갖 사정이 어우러진 덕에, 그 여자의 매력이 정말이지 뭇사람의 눈길을 끌었던 겁니다.\"\n\n그는 잠깐 말을 멈췄다. 그녀의 두 눈은 먼 풍경 위를 불안하게 헤맸고, 그녀는 그 부산한 입을 멈추려는 듯 아랫입술을 깨물었다.\n\n리티머 씨는 등받이에서 두 손을 거두어 한 손을 다른 손 안에 포개고, 한쪽 다리에 몸을 싣고 자세를 고치고는, 눈을 내리깐 채, 점잖은 머리를 조금 앞으로, 또 조금 옆으로 기울이며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그 젊은 여자는 한동안 이런 식으로 지냈는데, 이따금 기분이 가라앉곤 했습니다. 그러다 제 생각에 그 가라앉은 기분이며 그런 종류의 변덕을 자꾸 부려 대는 통에 제임스 도련님이 슬슬 싫증을 내기 시작하셨고, 사정이 그리 편치 않게 되었습니다. 도련님은 다시 안절부절못하기 시작하셨고요. 도련님이 안절부절못할수록, 그 여자는 더 나빠졌습니다. 그래서 제 처지로 말하자면, 두 사람 사이에서 참으로 어려운 시간을 보냈다고 말씀드릴 수밖에 없습니다. 그래도 여기서 한 번 꿰매고, 저기서 한 번 메우고, 그렇게 거듭하면서, 통틀어 누구도 예상치 못했을 만큼 오래 끌었던 것은 분명합니다.\"\n\n그녀는 먼 데에서 눈길을 거두어 다시 나를 바라보았는데, 아까의 그 기색 그대로였다. 리티머 씨는 손으로 입을 가리고 점잖게 짧은 헛기침으로 목을 가다듬더니, 다리를 바꿔 짚고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"마침내, 통틀어 말다툼과 비난이 꽤 오갔던 어느 날 아침, 제임스 도련님은 우리가 별장을 두고 있던 나폴리 근방에서 떠나셨습니다(그 젊은 여자가 바다를 무척 좋아했거든요). 그러고는 하루 이틀 안에 돌아오겠다는 핑계를 대고, 관련된 모두의 행복을 위해서 도련님이—\" 여기서 또 한 번 짧은 기침으로 말이 끊겼다. \"—떠나셨다는 것을 제가 알려 주는 일을 떠맡기셨습니다. 하지만 제임스 도련님이 참으로 더없이 떳떳하게 처신하셨다는 점은 말씀드려야겠습니다. 도련님은 그 젊은 여자가 아주 점잖은 사람과 결혼하면 어떻겠느냐고 제안하셨으니까요. 그 사람은 지난 일을 다 눈감아 줄 마음의 준비가 충분히 되어 있었고, 적어도 그 젊은 여자가 정상적인 길로 바랄 수 있었던 그 누구 못지않게 괜찮은 사람이었습니다. 그 여자의 집안이 워낙 미천했으니까요.\"\n\n그는 다시 다리를 바꿔 짚고 입술을 축였다. 나는 그 악당이 자기 자신을 두고 하는 말이라고 확신했고, 다틀 양의 얼굴에 비친 표정에서 내 확신이 그대로 반영된 것을 보았다.\n\n\"이것 또한 제가 전하도록 맡은 일이었습니다. 저는 제임스 도련님을 그 곤경에서 벗어나게 해 드리고, 도련님과, 도련님 때문에 그토록 많은 고생을 하신 자애로우신 어머님 사이의 화목을 되찾아 드리기 위해서라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 하려 했습니다. 그래서 그 일을 떠맡았던 겁니다. 도련님이 떠나셨다는 사실을 제가 알리자, 정신을 차린 그 젊은 여자가 부린 행패는 도무지 상상을 뛰어넘는 것이었습니다. 완전히 미쳐 날뛰어서 억지로 붙들어야 했고, 그러지 않으면 칼이라도 잡거나 바다로 뛰어들었을 테고, 그러지도 못하면 대리석 바닥에 제 머리를 짓찧었을 겁니다.\"\n\n다틀 양은 자리에 등을 기대고 얼굴에 환희의 빛을 띤 채, 이자가 내뱉은 말들을 거의 어루만지는 듯했다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 맡은 일의 두 번째 부분으로 넘어가자,\" 리티머 씨가 두 손을 불편하게 비비며 말했다. \"누구든 그래도 그것만큼은 적어도 친절한 뜻으로 여겨 고마워하리라 짐작했을 그 대목으로 넘어가자, 그 젊은 여자는 본색을 드러냈습니다. 그토록 막돼먹은 사람은 저도 본 적이 없습니다. 그 행실은 놀라울 만큼 못됐습니다. 그 여자에게는 나무토막이나 돌멩이만큼의 고마움도, 정도, 참을성도, 분별도 없었습니다. 제가 미리 경계하고 있지 않았다면, 그 여자가 제 피를 보고야 말았으리라고 저는 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 나는 그 아이가 더 마음에 드는군요.\" 내가 분개하여 말했다.\n\n리티머 씨는 \"그러십니까, 선생님? 하지만 선생님은 아직 젊으시지요!\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯 고개를 숙이고는, 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n\"요컨대 한동안은, 그 여자 가까이에서 제 몸이든 남의 몸이든 해칠 수 있는 모든 것을 치워 버리고, 그 여자를 꽉 가둬 둘 필요가 있었습니다. 그런데도 그 여자는 밤중에 빠져나갔습니다. 제가 직접 못질을 해 두었던 창문 격자를 비틀어 부수고, 그 아래로 뻗어 있던 포도 덩굴을 타고 내려가서는, 제가 알기로 그 뒤로 다시는 보이지도, 소식이 들리지도 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 죽었겠지.\" 다틀 양이 미소를 띠며 말했는데, 마치 그 망가진 여자의 시신을 발로 걷어찰 수도 있다는 듯한 투였다.\n\n\"물에 빠져 죽었을지도 모릅니다, 아가씨.\" 리티머 씨가 누군가에게 말을 걸 구실을 붙잡으며 대답했다. \"충분히 그럴 만합니다. 아니면 뱃사람들이나 그 아낙들, 아이들의 도움을 받았을 수도 있고요. 그 여자는 천한 무리들과 어울리기를 좋아해서, 해변에서 그들과 이야기하고 그들의 배 곁에 앉아 있는 일이 다반사였습니다, 다틀 아가씨. 제임스 도련님이 자리를 비우면 하루 종일 그러는 것을 제가 본 적도 있습니다. 한번은 그 여자가 아이들에게 자기가 뱃사람의 딸이며, 옛날 제 고향에서는 자기도 그 애들처럼 해변을 쏘다녔노라고 말한 것을 알고 도련님이 영 못마땅해하셨던 일도 있고요.\"\n\n오, 에밀리! 가엾은 미인이여! 그녀가 저 머나먼 바닷가에, 자기가 순결하던 시절의 자기와 똑같은 아이들 틈에 앉아, 가난한 사내의 아내였더라면 자기를 어머니라 불렀을 법한 그 작은 목소리들에, 그리고 영원토록 \"두 번 다시는!\" 하고 외치는 바다의 그 거대한 목소리에 귀 기울이는 그림이, 내 눈앞에 얼마나 또렷이 떠올랐던가!\n\n\"더 이상 어찌해 볼 도리가 없다는 게 분명해지자, 다틀 아가씨—\"\n\n\"내가 나한테 말 걸지 말랬잖아?\" 그녀가 매서운 경멸을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨가 저한테 말을 거셨잖습니까, 아가씨.\" 그가 대답했다. \"용서하십시오. 하지만 따르는 것이 제 직분이라서요.\"\n\n\"네 직분이나 해.\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"이야기나 끝내고, 가!\"\n\n\"더 이상 어찌해 볼 도리가 없다는 게 분명해지자,\" 그가 한없는 점잖음과 순종하는 인사로 말했다. \"그 여자를 도무지 찾을 수 없다는 게 분명해지자, 저는 도련님께 편지를 보내기로 약속해 둔 곳으로 가서, 그간 있었던 일을 알렸습니다. 그 일로 우리 사이에 말이 오갔고, 저는 제 인격을 지키기 위해서라도 도련님 곁을 떠나는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈습니다. 저는 제임스 도련님에게서 많은 것을 견딜 수 있었고, 또 견뎌 왔습니다. 하지만 도련님은 저를 너무 심하게 모욕하셨습니다. 저를 상처 입히셨어요. 저는 도련님과 어머님 사이의 불행한 불화를, 또 그로 인해 어머님 마음이 얼마나 애를 태우실지를 알았기에, 외람되이 영국 본국으로 돌아와 알리는—\"\n\n\"내가 그에게 돈을 치렀고요.\" 다틀 양이 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 마님—제가 아는 것을 알리는 일을 했습니다. 그 밖에 더 드릴 말씀은,\" 리티머 씨가 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"없는 것 같습니다. 저는 지금 일자리가 없는 처지이니, 점잖은 자리를 하나 만날 수 있다면 기쁘겠습니다.\"\n\n다틀 양은 내가 묻고 싶은 것이 있는지 알아보려는 듯 나를 흘끗 보았다. 마침 내 마음에 떠오른 것이 있어, 나는 이렇게 대답 삼아 말했다.\n\n\"나는 이—작자에게서 알고 싶은 게 있소.\" 나는 도저히 그보다 누그러진 말은 입에 올릴 수가 없었다. \"이 사람들이 집에서 그 아이에게 보낸 편지를 가로챈 것인지, 아니면 그 아이가 그 편지를 받았다고 이 사람이 짐작하는지를 말이오.\"\n\n그는 차분하고 잠잠하게, 눈길을 땅에 고정한 채, 오른손 손가락 끝마다 왼손 손가락 끝을 하나하나 섬세하게 맞댄 채 서 있었다.\n\n다틀 양이 멸시하듯 고개를 그에게로 돌렸다.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 아가씨.\" 그가 멍한 상태에서 깨어나며 말했다. \"아무리 아가씨께 순종한다 해도, 저는 하인일망정 제 처지가 있습니다. 코퍼필드 씨와 아가씨는 서로 다른 분이시고요. 코퍼필드 씨가 저에게서 무언가를 알고 싶으시다면, 외람되지만 코퍼필드 씨께서 저에게 직접 물으실 수 있다는 점을 일깨워 드리는 바입니다. 저에게는 지켜야 할 인격이 있으니까요.\"\n\n나는 잠깐 나 자신과 씨름한 끝에, 그에게 눈길을 돌리고 말했다. \"내 질문은 자네도 들었네. 원한다면 그게 자네에게 던진 것이라고 여기게. 자네는 어떻게 대답하겠나?\"\n\n\"선생님.\" 그가 그 섬세한 손가락 끝을 이따금 떼었다 다시 붙이며 대답했다. \"제 대답에는 단서를 달 수밖에 없습니다. 제임스 도련님의 신임을 그 어머님께 저버리는 것과, 선생님께 저버리는 것은 서로 다른 행동이니까요. 제 생각에, 제임스 도련님이 기분을 가라앉히고 불쾌함을 더할 만한 편지를 받게끔 부추기셨을 리는 없습니다. 하지만 그 이상은, 선생님, 더 들어가는 것을 삼가고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 다인가요?\" 다틀 양이 나에게 물었다.\n\n나는 더 할 말이 없다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. \"다만,\" 그가 자리를 뜨려는 것을 보고 내가 덧붙였다. \"이 못된 이야기에서 이 작자가 맡은 역할을 나는 똑똑히 알고 있고, 그 아이를 어릴 적부터 아버지처럼 돌봐 온 그 정직한 분에게 이 사실을 알릴 작정이니, 이 사람은 사람들 앞에 너무 나돌아 다니지 않는 게 좋을 거라고 일러 두겠소.\"\n\n내가 말을 꺼내자 그는 곧장 멈춰 서서, 여느 때처럼 침착한 태도로 귀 기울였다.\n\n\"감사합니다, 선생님. 하지만 선생님, 외람되지만 한 말씀 드리자면, 이 나라에는 노예도 노예 부리는 자도 없으며, 사람이 제멋대로 법을 손에 쥐는 일은 허락되지 않습니다. 만약 그런 짓을 한다면, 제 생각에 그것은 남보다 자기 자신에게 더 위태로운 일이지요. 그러니 결과적으로 말씀드리자면, 저는 제가 가고 싶은 곳이라면 어디든 가는 데 전혀 두려움이 없습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n그러고는 그가 정중하게 인사를 하고, 다틀 양에게도 또 한 번 인사를 하고는, 그가 들어왔던 호랑가시나무 울타리의 아치문을 통해 가 버렸다. 다틀 양과 나는 잠시 말없이 서로를 바라보았다. 그녀의 태도는 그자를 불러냈을 때와 조금도 다르지 않았다.\n\n\"그가 덧붙이기로는,\" 그녀가 입술을 천천히 비틀며 말했다. \"자기가 듣기로 자기 주인이 스페인 연안을 항해 중이고, 이 일이 끝나면 물릴 때까지 뱃놀이 취미를 마음껏 채우러 떠난다고 하더군요. 하지만 이건 당신에게 아무 흥미도 없는 얘기겠죠. 이 거만한 두 사람, 어머니와 아들 사이에는 전보다 더 깊은 골이 패었고, 그 골이 메워질 가망도 거의 없어요. 둘은 본바탕이 똑같고, 세월이 갈수록 서로 더 고집스럽고 더 안하무인이 되어 가니까요. 이것도 당신한테는 아무 흥미 없는 얘기겠지만, 이게 내가 하려는 말의 실마리예요. 당신이 천사로 떠받드는 이 악마, 그러니까 그가 갯벌 진창에서 건져 올린 이 천한 계집 말이에요.\" 그녀가 검은 두 눈을 나에게 가득 쏟고 격정에 찬 손가락을 치켜들며 말을 이었다. \"어쩌면 살아 있을지도 모르죠. 천한 것들은 좀처럼 죽지 않는 법이라고 나는 믿거든요. 만약 살아 있다면, 당신은 그토록 값진 진주가 발견되어 잘 간수되기를 바라겠죠. 우리도 그걸 바라요. 그가 어쩌다 다시 그 계집의 먹잇감이 되지 않도록 말이에요. 거기까지는, 우리가 한 가지 이해로 뭉친 셈이죠. 그래서 그토록 천박한 인간이 느낄 수 있는 온갖 해코지라도 그 계집에게 퍼붓고 싶은 나 같은 사람이, 당신을 불러서 당신이 들은 얘기를 듣게 한 거예요.\"\n\n나는 그녀의 얼굴빛이 변하는 것을 보고, 누군가 내 뒤에서 다가오고 있음을 알았다. 스티어포스 부인이었다. 그녀는 예전보다 더 쌀쌀하게, 또 그 옛날의 위엄을 한층 더 부풀린 태도로 나에게 손을 내밀었다. 하지만 그러면서도 나는—그리고 그 점에 마음이 뭉클했는데—그녀가 자기 아들을 향한 내 옛 사랑을 결코 지울 수 없는 기억으로 간직하고 있음을 알아챘다. 그녀는 몹시 변해 있었다. 그 곱던 자태는 한참 덜 꼿꼿했고, 잘생긴 얼굴에는 깊은 주름이 패었으며, 머리는 거의 하얗게 세어 있었다. 그러나 자리에 앉았을 때, 그녀는 여전히 아름다운 부인이었다. 그 고고한 눈빛이 깃든 형형한 눈을, 학창 시절 내 꿈속에서도 한 줄기 빛이 되어 주던 그 눈을, 나는 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨가 모든 걸 들었느냐, 로자?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"리티머가 직접 하는 얘기도 들었고?\"\n\n\"네. 어머님이 왜 그러길 바라셨는지도 제가 말씀드렸어요.\" \"착하구나. 선생, 나는 자네의 옛 벗과 약간의 서신을 주고받은 적이 있네.\" 그녀가 나에게 말했다. \"하지만 그것으로 그 애의 의무감이나 천륜에 대한 도리가 되살아나지는 않았네. 그러니 이 일에서 내가 바라는 것은 로자가 말한 것 외에는 아무것도 없네. 만약, 자네가 여기 데려온 그 점잖은 사람의(나는 그 사람이 안됐다고 생각하네—그 이상은 말할 수 없지만) 마음을 덜어 줄 어떤 방법을 통해서, 내 아들이 음흉한 적의 덫에 또다시 빠지는 일을 면할 수 있다면, 그것으로 좋네!\"\n\n그녀는 몸을 곧추세우고, 저 멀리 앞만을 똑바로 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"부인.\" 내가 정중하게 말했다. \"잘 알겠습니다. 단언컨대 저는 부인의 뜻을 비뚤어지게 해석할 마음이 조금도 없습니다. 하지만 이 상처 입은 가족을 어릴 적부터 알아 온 사람으로서, 부인께라도 이 말씀만은 드려야겠습니다. 만약 그토록 깊이 짓밟힌 그 아이가 잔인하게 속아 넘어간 것이 아니라고, 그리고 지금 부인의 아드님 손에서 물 한 잔 받아 마시느니 차라리 백 번을 죽으려 하지 않을 거라고 여기신다면, 부인은 무서운 착각을 품고 계신 겁니다.\"\n\n\"됐다, 로자, 됐어!\" 다른 여자가 끼어들려 하자 스티어포스 부인이 말했다. \"아무래도 좋다. 그냥 두어라. 선생, 자네 결혼했다고 들었네만?\"\n\n나는 결혼한 지 얼마 되었다고 대답했다.\n\n\"잘 지내고 있고? 내가 조용히 사는 터라 듣는 것이 거의 없네만, 자네 이름이 점점 알려지기 시작했다고 알고 있네.\"\n\n\"운이 무척 좋았습니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"제 이름이 약간의 칭찬과 함께 오르내리고 있더군요.\"\n\n\"어머니는 안 계신가?\" 누그러진 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"네, 안 계십니다.\"\n\"안됐구먼.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"어머니가 계셨다면 자네를 자랑스러워하셨을 텐데. 잘 가게!\"\n\n나는 그녀가 위엄 있고 굽힐 줄 모르는 태도로 내미는 손을 잡았는데, 그 손은 마치 그녀의 가슴속이 평온하기라도 한 듯 내 손안에서 차분했다. 그녀의 자존심은 그 맥박마저도 잠재울 수 있는 듯했고, 그 얼굴 앞에 잔잔한 장막을 드리워, 그녀는 그 너머로 저 멀리 앞만을 똑바로 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n내가 테라스를 따라 그들에게서 멀어져 가는 동안, 나는 두 사람이 얼마나 미동도 없이 앉아 그 풍경을 응시하고 있는지, 그리고 그 풍경이 얼마나 그들 둘레로 짙어지며 죄어드는지를 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다. 저 멀리 도시에서는 여기저기 이른 등불이 깜박이는 것이 보였고, 동쪽 하늘에는 그 불그죽죽한 빛이 여전히 어른거렸다. 그러나 그 사이에 끼어든 너른 골짜기의 대부분에서는 안개가 바다처럼 피어올랐고, 그것이 어둠과 뒤섞여 마치 차오르는 물이 그들을 에워싸려는 것처럼 보였다. 나에게는 이것을 기억하고, 두려운 마음으로 떠올릴 까닭이 있다. 내가 그 두 사람을 다시 보기 전에, 사나운 바다가 그들의 발치까지 차올랐으니까.\n\n이렇게 전해 들은 이야기를 곰곰이 되새기면서, 나는 그것을 패거티 씨에게 알리는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈다. 이튿날 저녁, 나는 그를 찾아 런던으로 들어갔다. 그는 조카딸을 되찾겠다는 그 하나의 목표를 눈앞에 두고 늘 이곳저곳을 떠돌았지만, 다른 어느 곳보다 런던에 머무는 일이 많았다. 이제 나는 깊은 밤에 그가 거리를 따라 지나가며, 그런 야심한 시각에 바깥을 어슬렁거리는 몇 안 되는 사람들 틈에서 차마 마주치기 두려운 그것을 찾아 헤매는 모습을 자주, 또 자주 보아 왔다.\n\n그는 헝거퍼드 시장의 작은 잡화점 위층에 셋방을 얻어 지내고 있었다. 이 가게는 내가 한두 번 이야기한 적이 있는데, 그가 그 자비로운 임무를 위해 처음 길을 나섰던 곳이다. 나는 그쪽으로 발길을 잡았다. 그를 찾으니, 집안사람들에게서 그가 아직 나가지 않았으며 위층 방에 있을 거라는 말을 들었다.\n\n그는 화초 몇 그루를 둔 창가에서 책을 읽으며 앉아 있었다. 방은 무척 단정하고 가지런했다. 나는 한눈에, 이 방이 늘 그녀를 맞이할 채비를 갖춘 채로 정돈되어 있으며, 그가 밖에 나갈 때마다 어쩌면 그녀를 집으로 데려올 수 있을지도 모른다고 생각하며 나선다는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 내가 문을 두드리는 소리도 듣지 못한 채, 내가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고서야 비로소 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님! 고맙습니다, 도련님! 이렇게 찾아 주시다니 진심으로 고맙습니다요! 앉으세요. 어서 오세요, 도련님!\"\n\n\"패거티 씨.\" 그가 건네주는 의자에 앉으며 내가 말했다. \"너무 큰 기대는 마세요! 소식을 좀 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"에밀리 소식인가요!\"\n그는 초조하게 손을 입에 갖다 대고, 얼굴이 하얗게 질리며 두 눈을 내 눈에 박았다.\n\n\"그 애가 어디 있는지는 알 수 없습니다. 하지만 그 사람과 함께 있지는 않아요.\"\n\n그는 자리에 앉아 나를 골똘히 바라보며, 내가 들려줄 이야기를 깊은 침묵 속에 귀 기울여 들었다. 그가 차츰 내 눈에서 눈길을 거두어 아래를 내려다보며 이마를 손에 괴고 앉았을 때, 그 얼굴의 참을성 어린 무게가 나에게 안겨 준 위엄, 아니 아름다움마저도 나는 또렷이 기억한다. 그는 한 번도 말을 끊지 않고, 처음부터 끝까지 완전히 미동도 없이 있었다. 그는 그 이야기 속에서 오로지 그녀의 모습만을 좇으며, 다른 모든 형상은 아무것도 아니라는 듯 그냥 흘려보내는 것 같았다.\n\n내가 이야기를 마치자, 그는 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 여전히 입을 다물고 있었다. 나는 잠시 창밖을 내다보며 화초들에 마음을 두었다.\n\n\"이 일을 어떻게 받아들이시나요, 데이비 도련님?\" 마침내 그가 물었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 그 애가 살아 있는 것 같습니다.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 모르겠습니다요. 어쩌면 처음 받은 충격이 너무 모질어서, 미친 듯이 어쩔 줄 모르다가—! 그 애가 늘 입에 올리던 그 푸른 바닷물 말이에요. 그게 제 무덤이 될 거라서, 그 애가 그토록 여러 해 그 생각을 했던 걸까요!\"\n\n그는 낮고 겁먹은 목소리로 이렇게 혼잣말하듯 중얼거리며, 그 작은 방을 가로질러 걸었다.\n\n\"그렇지만요,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"데이비 도련님, 저는 그 애가 살아 있다고 너무도 굳게 믿어 왔습니다요—깨어 있을 때나 잠들었을 때나, 제가 그 애를 찾아내리라는 게 너무도 참된 일이라고 알고 있었습니다요—그 믿음에 이끌리고, 그 믿음에 버텨 왔습니다요—그러니 제가 속았을 리가 없다고 믿습니다요. 아니에요! 에밀리는 살아 있어요!\"\n\n그는 손을 탁자 위에 굳게 내려놓고, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 단호한 표정으로 다잡았다.\n\n\"제 조카딸 에밀리는 살아 있습니다요, 도련님!\" 그가 흔들림 없이 말했다. \"그게 어디서 오는 건지, 어떻게 그런 건지는 모르겠습니다요만, 그 애가 살아 있다고, 누가 저한테 일러 주는 것만 같습니다요!\"\n\n그는 이 말을 하면서 거의 영감에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 보였다. 나는 그가 온전히 나에게 마음을 기울일 수 있을 때까지 잠시 기다렸다가, 어젯밤 내 머릿속에 떠오른, 미리 해 두면 좋을 듯한 조치를 설명하기 시작했다.\n\n\"자, 나의 소중한 벗이여—\" 내가 입을 뗐다.\n\n\"고맙습니다요, 고맙습니다요, 친절하신 도련님.\" 그가 두 손으로 내 손을 꼭 움켜쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"만약 그 애가 런던으로 길을 잡는다면 말입니다—그럴 법도 하지요. 이 거대한 도시만큼 제 몸을 쉽게 숨겨 버릴 수 있는 데가 어디 있겠습니까. 게다가 집으로 돌아가지 않을 거라면, 그 애가 바라는 거라곤 제 몸을 잃어버리고 숨기는 것 말고 또 뭐가 있겠습니까—\"\n\n\"그 애는 집에 안 갈 겁니다요.\" 그가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 끼어들었다. \"제 발로 떠난 거였다면 모를까, 사정이 그렇질 못하니까요, 도련님.\"\n\n\"만약 그 애가 여기로 온다면,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 세상 그 누구보다 그 애를 찾아낼 가능성이 큰 사람이 한 명, 여기 있다고 저는 믿습니다. 기억하세요—제 말을 굳게 들으세요—당신의 그 큰 목표를 떠올리세요!—마사를 기억하시나요?\"\n\n\"우리 고장의 그 애요?\"\n\n그의 얼굴 말고는 다른 대답이 필요 없었다.\n\n\"그 애가 런던에 있는 걸 아세요?\"\n\n\"길거리에서 본 적이 있습니다요.\" 그가 몸서리를 치며 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 모르실 겁니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"에밀리가 집을 떠나기 한참 전부터, 햄의 도움을 받아 그 애에게 자비를 베풀었다는 걸요. 또 우리가 어느 날 밤 만나서, 길 건너 저쪽 방에서 함께 이야기를 나눌 때, 그 애가 문간에서 엿들었다는 것도요.\"\n\n\"데이비 도련님!\" 그가 깜짝 놀라 대꾸했다. \"그날 밤, 눈이 그토록 모질게 내리던 그날 밤이요?\"\n\n\"바로 그날 밤이에요. 저는 그 뒤로 그 애를 한 번도 보지 못했어요. 당신과 헤어진 뒤에 그 애에게 말을 걸려고 되돌아갔지만, 그 애는 이미 사라지고 없었어요. 그때는 그 애 이야기를 당신께 꺼내고 싶지 않았고, 지금도 그렇습니다. 하지만 제가 말하는 사람이 바로 그 애고, 우리가 그 애와 연락을 해 봐야 한다고 생각합니다. 무슨 말인지 아시겠어요?\"\n\n\"너무 잘 알겠습니다요, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. 우리는 거의 속삭임에 가깝게 목소리를 낮추었고, 그 어조로 계속 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그 애를 본 적이 있다고 하셨죠. 그 애를 찾을 수 있을 것 같으세요? 저로서는 그저 운에 기대는 수밖에 없거든요.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 데이비 도련님, 어디를 찾아봐야 할지 알 것 같습니다요.\"\n\n\"날이 어둡네요. 이왕 함께 있으니, 지금 나가서 오늘 밤에 그 애를 찾아볼까요?\"\n\n그는 그러자고 하고, 나를 따라나설 채비를 했다. 나는 그가 무얼 하는지 눈여겨보지 않는 척했지만, 그가 얼마나 정성스레 그 작은 방을 매만지는지를 보았다. 그는 초 한 자루와 불 붙일 도구를 챙겨 두고, 침대를 매만지고, 마지막으로 서랍에서 그녀의 옷 한 벌(그녀가 그 옷을 입은 것을 본 기억이 난다)을 다른 옷가지 몇 점과 함께 곱게 개어진 채로 꺼내고, 모자도 하나 꺼내어 의자 위에 놓아두었다. 그는 이 옷들에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 나도 그랬다. 그것들은 분명, 수없이 많은 밤 동안 그곳에서 그녀를 기다려 왔으리라.\n\n\"한때는요, 데이비 도련님,\" 우리가 계단을 내려오면서 그가 말했다. \"저는 이 마사라는 애를 우리 에밀리 발밑의 흙먼지나 진배없다고 여겼습니다요. 하느님, 용서하소서, 이제는 사정이 달라졌습니다요!\"\n\n길을 가는 동안, 한편으로는 그가 계속 말을 잇게 하려고, 한편으로는 내 마음을 좀 놓으려고, 나는 그에게 햄의 안부를 물었다. 그는 예전과 거의 똑같은 말로, 햄은 그대로라고, \"제 한 몸 도무지 돌볼 생각도 없이 그렇게 목숨을 깎아 먹으면서도, 한 번도 투덜대는 법 없이 모두에게 사랑받는다\"고 대답했다.\n\n나는 그에게, 그들이 겪은 불행의 원인에 대해 햄이 어떤 심정일 것 같으냐고 물었다. 그것이 위태롭다고 여기는지? 이를테면, 햄이 스티어포스와 마주치기라도 하면 어찌할 것 같으냐고?\n\n\"저도 모르겠습니다요, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"여러 번 생각해 봤습니다요만, 도무지 짐작이 가질 않습니다요, 아무리 해도요.\"\n\n나는 그녀가 떠난 이튿날 아침, 우리 셋이 모두 해변에 나가 있던 일을 그에게 일깨워 주었다. \"기억하세요?\" 내가 말했다. \"그 애가 사나운 눈빛으로 바다를 내다보며 '그게 끝장날 날'에 대해 말하던 거요?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 기억하고말고요!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이었던 것 같으세요?\"\n\n\"데이비 도련님,\" 그가 대답했다. \"저도 그 물음을 제 자신한테 숱하게 던져 봤습니다요만, 도무지 답을 찾지 못했습니다요. 그리고 한 가지 묘한 게 있는데요—그 애가 그토록 싹싹한데도, 저는 차마 그 애 속을 떠보려는 마음이 영 편치가 않습니다요. 그 애는 저한테 더없이 고분고분한 말 말고는 한 마디도 한 적이 없고, 이제 와 딴 식으로 말을 꺼낼 것 같지도 않습니다요만, 그 생각들이 가라앉아 있는 그 애 마음속은 결코 얕은 물이 아닙니다요. 깊어요, 도련님, 저는 그 바닥이 보이질 않습니다요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 가끔 저도 불안하게 만들더군요.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다요, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"장담컨대, 그 애가 무모하게 구는 것보다 그게 더 그렇습니다요. 둘 다 그 애가 변한 탓이긴 하지만요. 그 애가 어떤 상황에서든 폭력을 휘두를 사람이라고는 생각지 않습니다요만, 그래도 저는 그 둘이 서로 떨어져 있게 되길 바랍니다요.\"\n\n우리는 템플 바를 지나 시내로 들어와 있었다. 이제는 더 말을 나누지 않고, 그는 내 곁에서 걸으며 그 헌신적인 삶의 하나뿐인 목표에 온전히 자기 자신을 내맡긴 채, 무리 속에서도 그의 모습을 외따로 보이게 했을 그 고요한 정신의 집중으로 걸어갔다. 우리가 블랙프라이어스 다리에서 멀지 않은 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그가 고개를 돌려 길 건너편을 스치듯 지나가는 외로운 여인의 모습을 가리켰다. 나는 그것이 우리가 찾던 바로 그 사람임을 단번에 알아보았다.\n\n우리는 길을 건너 그녀를 향해 다가가고 있었는데, 문득 이런 생각이 들었다. 사람들에게서 떨어진 더 조용한 곳에서, 남의 눈에 덜 띌 만한 데서 말을 건넨다면, 그녀가 그 잃어버린 아이에게 같은 여자로서 더 마음을 쓰게 될지도 모른다는 것이었다. 그래서 나는 동행에게 아직 그녀에게 말을 걸지 말고 따라가자고 일렀다. 여기에는 그녀가 어디로 가는지 알고 싶다는, 나 자신도 또렷이 알 수 없는 바람도 한몫했다.\n\n그가 동의해서, 우리는 거리를 두고 뒤를 밟았다. 결코 그녀를 시야에서 놓치지 않으면서도, 그녀가 자주 두리번거리는 통에 너무 가까이 다가가지는 않으려 애썼다. 한번은 그녀가 악단의 연주를 들으려고 멈춰 섰고, 그러자 우리도 멈춰 섰다.\n\n그녀는 한참을 걸어갔다. 우리도 계속 걸어갔다. 그녀가 길을 잡아 가는 모양새로 보아, 어딘가 정해진 목적지로 향하고 있는 것이 분명했다. 이것과, 그녀가 번잡한 거리를 벗어나지 않는다는 것, 그리고 짐작건대 누군가를 그렇게 몰래 뒤쫓는 일에 깃든 야릇한 매혹이 어우러져, 나는 처음의 작정을 그대로 밀고 나갔다. 마침내 그녀는 소음과 인파가 사라진 칙칙하고 어두운 거리로 접어들었다. 그래서 나는 \"이제 말을 걸어도 되겠어요\" 하고 말했고, 우리는 걸음을 다그쳐 그녀의 뒤를 쫓았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 47. MARTHA\n\n우리는 이제 웨스트민스터까지 내려와 있었다. 그녀가 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 것과 마주친 터라, 우리는 발길을 돌려 뒤를 밟았다. 웨스트민스터 사원은 그녀가 큰길의 불빛과 소음에서 벗어나는 지점이었다. 그녀는 다리 쪽으로 향하는 인파와 다리에서 빠져나오는 인파, 그 두 물결에서 풀려나자 어찌나 빨리 걸음을 옮기던지, 갈림길에서 방향을 틀며 벌어진 거리까지 더해져, 우리가 그녀를 따라잡았을 무렵에는 이미 밀뱅크 옆 좁은 강가 골목에 들어서 있었다. 바로 그 순간, 그녀는 등 뒤로 바짝 따라붙는 발소리를 피하려는 듯 길을 가로질렀고, 뒤도 돌아보지 않은 채 한층 더 빠르게 걸어갔다.\n\n수레 몇 대가 밤을 나려고 들어와 있던 칙칙한 출입문 너머로 강물이 언뜻 비치자, 내 발은 그 자리에 멈춰 서는 것 같았다. 나는 말없이 동행을 건드렸고, 우리 둘 다 그녀를 따라 길을 건너지 않은 채, 길 맞은편을 따라 걸었다. 집들의 그늘 속에 최대한 조용히 몸을 숨기면서도, 그녀와의 거리는 아주 바짝 좁히고서.\n\n내가 이 글을 쓰는 지금도 그렇지만, 그 낮게 깔린 골목 끝에는 다 허물어져 가는 작은 나무 건물이 하나 있었는데, 아마도 더는 쓰지 않는 낡은 나루터집이었을 것이다. 그 위치는 바로 골목이 끝나고, 길이 늘어선 집들과 강 사이로 뻗기 시작하는 지점이었다. 그녀는 이곳에 다다라 물을 보자마자, 목적지에 도착하기라도 한 듯 멈춰 섰다. 그러더니 이내 강가를 따라 천천히 걸으며 물을 뚫어지게 들여다보았다.\n\n여기까지 오는 내내, 나는 그녀가 어느 집으로 향하는 줄로만 알았다. 사실, 그 집이 어떤 식으로든 잃어버린 그 아이와 이어져 있을지 모른다는 막연한 희망까지 품고 있었다. 그러나 출입문 너머로 어둑하게 한 번 비친 그 강물이, 그녀가 더는 가지 않으리라는 것을 본능적으로 일러 주었다.\n\n그 일대는 그 무렵 황량하기 그지없었다. 밤이면 런던 어느 동네 못지않게 짓누르듯 답답하고, 슬프고, 외로운 곳이었다. 텅 빈 거대한 감옥 가까이로 음울하게 버려진 길에는 부두도 집도 없었다. 느릿한 도랑은 감옥 담벼락에다 진흙을 게워 냈다. 거친 풀과 무성한 잡초가 그 부근의 질척한 땅을 온통 뒤덮으며 어지러이 뻗어 있었다. 한쪽에서는, 불길하게 짓기 시작했다가 끝내 완성되지 못한 집들의 잔해가 썩어 가고 있었다. 또 다른 쪽에서는, 어느 투기꾼이 긁어모아 쌓아 놓은 녹슨 쇠붙이 괴물들 — 증기 보일러, 바퀴, 크랭크, 파이프, 화로, 외륜, 닻, 잠수종, 풍차 날개, 그 밖에 내가 무엇인지도 모를 온갖 기괴한 물건들 — 이 땅을 어지럽히며 흙먼지 속에 나뒹굴고 있었는데, 비 오는 날이면 제 무게로 흙 속에 가라앉아, 마치 헛되이 제 몸을 감추려는 듯한 꼴이었다. 강가에 늘어선 갖가지 불 뿜는 공장들의 쨍그랑 소리와 번쩍이는 불빛은, 밤이면 굴뚝에서 쏟아져 나오는 무겁고 끊이지 않는 연기만 빼고 모든 것을 어지럽혔다. 미끈거리는 빈틈과 둑길이 낡은 나무 말뚝 사이로 굽이쳤는데, 그 말뚝에는 초록빛 머리카락 같은 병든 물질이 들러붙어 있었고, 물에 빠져 죽은 사람을 찾는다고 현상금을 내건 작년 전단의 너덜너덜한 조각들이 만조선 위에서 펄럭였다. 그 길은 진창과 질척한 수렁을 지나 썰물 진 물가로 내려갔다. 대역병이 돌던 시절 죽은 자들을 묻으려고 파 놓은 구덩이 가운데 하나가 이 근처에 있었다는 이야기가 전해 왔다. 그리고 그곳에서부터 무언가 시들게 하는 기운이 온 동네로 퍼져 나온 듯했다. 아니면, 더럽혀진 강물이 넘쳐흘러, 이 일대가 차츰 그런 악몽 같은 꼴로 썩어 들어간 것처럼 보이기도 했다.\n\n마치 그 강이 토해 내어 썩고 문드러지도록 내버린 쓰레기의 한 조각이기라도 하듯, 우리가 뒤쫓아 온 그 아이는 강가로 비척비척 내려가, 이 밤 풍경 한복판에 외롭고도 가만히 선 채 물을 바라보았다.\n\n진흙에 좌초된 보트와 거룻배 몇 척이 있었던 덕분에, 우리는 들키지 않고 그녀에게서 몇 야드 안까지 다가갈 수 있었다. 나는 패거티 씨에게 그 자리에 그대로 있으라고 손짓하고, 그늘에서 빠져나와 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 나는 그 외로운 모습에 다가가면서 떨지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 작정하고 걸어온 길의 그 음울한 끝, 그리고 쇠다리의 동굴 같은 그늘 거의 안쪽에 서서, 거센 물살에 비뚜름하게 비친 불빛을 바라보던 그 모습이, 내 안에 두려움을 불러일으켰기 때문이다.\n\n내 생각에 그녀는 혼잣말을 하고 있었던 것 같다. 물을 들여다보는 데 온통 정신이 팔려 있었으나, 분명 어깨에서 숄이 흘러내린 채였고, 그 숄로 두 손을 어수선하고 얼떨떨하게 감싸고 있었는데, 깨어 있는 사람이라기보다 몽유병 환자의 몸짓에 가까웠다. 나는 안다, 그리고 결코 잊지 못한다 — 내가 그녀의 팔을 움켜잡기 전까지, 그 사나운 몸짓에는 그녀가 바로 내 눈앞에서 물속으로 가라앉지 않으리라는 보장이 조금도 없었다는 것을.\n\n바로 그 순간 나는 \"마사!\" 하고 불렀다.\n\n그녀는 겁에 질려 비명을 지르며, 어찌나 거센 힘으로 나에게 맞서던지, 나 혼자였다면 과연 붙잡을 수 있었을지 의심스러울 정도였다. 그러나 내 손보다 더 센 손이 그녀에게 얹혔다. 그녀가 겁먹은 눈을 들어 그것이 누구의 손인지 알아본 순간, 그녀는 단 한 번 더 몸부림치다가 우리 둘 사이로 풀썩 주저앉았다. 우리는 그녀를 물가에서 마른 돌이 있는 데로 옮겨, 거기 눕혔는데, 그녀는 울며 신음했다. 잠시 후 그녀는 두 손으로 비참한 머리를 감싼 채 돌 틈에 주저앉았다.\n\n\"아, 강물!\" 그녀가 격렬하게 외쳤다. \"아, 저 강물!\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿!\" 내가 말했다. \"마음을 가라앉혀요.\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 여전히 같은 말만 되풀이하며, \"아, 저 강물!\"이라고 자꾸만 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"저게 나 같다는 걸 알아요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"내가 저기에 속한다는 걸 알아요. 저게 나 같은 것들의 마땅한 짝이라는 걸 안다고요! 저것도 시골에서 흘러나와요, 한때는 아무런 해도 없던 곳에서 — 그러다 음침한 거리를 기어 다니며, 더럽혀지고 비참해지고 — 그러다 내 인생처럼, 늘 뒤숭숭한 큰 바다로 흘러가요 — 그래서 나도 저것과 함께 가야만 한다는 느낌이 들어요!\" 나는 그 말투에서만큼 절망이라는 것이 무엇인지를 절절히 안 적이 없다.\n\n\"저것에서 벗어날 수가 없어요. 잊을 수가 없어요. 밤낮으로 나를 따라다녀요. 이 세상에서 내가 어울릴 만한 단 하나, 또는 나에게 어울리는 단 하나예요. 아, 저 무서운 강물!\"\n\n말없이 미동도 없이 그녀를 바라보는 내 동행의 얼굴에서, 만약 내가 그 조카딸의 사연을 아무것도 몰랐더라도 그 사연을 능히 읽어 낼 수 있었으리라는 생각이 내 머릿속을 스쳤다. 나는 그림에서든 현실에서든, 공포와 연민이 그토록 인상 깊게 뒤섞인 모습을 본 적이 없었다. 그는 곧 쓰러질 듯 떨었다. 그리고 그의 손은 — 그 모습이 걱정스러워 내 손으로 만져 보았는데 — 죽은 사람처럼 차가웠다.\n\n\"지금 제정신이 아니에요.\" 나는 그에게 속삭였다. \"조금만 지나면 다른 말을 할 거예요.\"\n\n그가 무어라 대답하려 했는지는 모르겠다. 그는 입을 무어라 달싹이며, 자기가 말을 한 줄로 여기는 듯했으나, 실은 뻗은 손으로 그녀를 가리켰을 뿐이었다.\n\n이제 그녀에게 또 한 차례 울음이 터져 나왔고, 그녀는 다시 한번 돌 틈에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 굴욕과 파멸의 엎드린 형상으로 우리 앞에 누웠다. 이 상태가 지나가기 전에는 어떤 희망을 품고 그녀에게 말을 건넬 수 없음을 알기에, 나는 그가 그녀를 일으키려 할 때 감히 그를 말렸고, 우리는 그녀가 좀 더 잠잠해질 때까지 말없이 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"마사.\" 그러고 나서 나는 몸을 숙여 그녀가 일어서도록 도우며 말했다. 그녀는 어디론가 가려는 듯 일어서고 싶어 하는 것 같았으나, 몸이 약해서 보트에 기댔다. \"지금 나와 함께 있는 이분이 누구인지 알겠어요?\"\n\n그녀는 가냘프게 \"네.\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 우리가 먼 길을 당신 뒤를 밟아 왔다는 걸 알아요?\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 저었다. 그녀는 그도 나도 보지 않고, 겸허한 자세로 서서, 한 손에는 보닛과 숄을 쥐었으되 그것들을 의식하지도 못하는 듯했고, 다른 한 손은 주먹을 쥐어 이마를 짓누르고 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 그토록 마음을 쓰던 그 일에 대해 — 부디 하늘이 그것을 기억해 주기를! — 그 눈 내리던 밤의 그 일에 대해 이야기를 나눌 만큼 마음이 가라앉았나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n그녀는 다시 흐느끼기 시작하더니, 자기를 문 앞에서 내쫓지 않은 데 대해 나에게 무어라 알아들을 수 없는 감사의 말을 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"제 자신을 위해서는 아무 말도 하고 싶지 않아요.\" 잠시 후 그녀가 말했다. \"전 나쁜 년이에요, 전 버려진 몸이고요. 저에겐 아무 희망도 없어요. 하지만 저분께 전해 주세요, 선생님.\" 그녀는 그에게서 움츠러들며 물러나 있었다. \"저를 너무 모질게 여기시지 않는다면요, 제가 어떤 식으로도 저분의 불행의 원인이 된 적은 결코 없다고요.\" \"그건 한 번도 당신 탓으로 돌려진 적이 없어요.\" 나는 그녀의 간절함에 진심으로 응답하며 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그 애가 저를 그토록 가엾이 여겨 주던 그날 밤 부엌으로 들어오셨던 분이 바로 선생님이었지요.\" 그녀가 갈라진 목소리로 말했다. \"그 애는 제게 어찌나 다정했던지요. 남들처럼 저를 보고 움츠러들지도 않고, 그토록 친절히 도와줬어요! 그게 선생님이셨나요?\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 애에게 제가 무슨 잘못이라도 저질렀다는 마음의 짐이 있었다면,\" 그녀는 무시무시한 표정으로 강물을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"전 진작에 저 강물 속에 있었을 거예요. 거기에 제가 한 자락이라도 끼어 있었다면, 단 하룻밤 겨울밤도 강물에서 멀리 떨어져 견디지 못했을 거예요!\"\n\n\"그 애가 달아난 까닭은 너무도 잘 알려져 있어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신은 거기에 아무런 잘못이 없다고, 우린 진심으로 믿어요 — 우린 알아요.\"\n\n\"아, 제 마음이 조금만 더 착했더라면, 전 그 애에게 훨씬 더 좋은 사람이 될 수도 있었을 텐데!\" 그녀가 더없이 처량한 회한에 잠겨 외쳤다. \"그 애는 늘 제게 잘해 줬으니까요! 그 애는 저에게 즐겁고 옳은 말이 아니면 단 한마디도 한 적이 없어요. 제가 어떤 인간인지 그토록 잘 알면서, 제가 그 애를 저 같은 인간으로 만들려고 들었을 리가 있겠어요? 인생을 값지게 해 주는 모든 것을 잃었을 때, 제 온갖 생각 가운데 가장 끔찍한 것은 제가 그 애와 영영 갈라섰다는 거였어요!\"\n\n패거티 씨는 한 손을 보트 뱃전에 얹고 눈을 내리깐 채 서 있다가, 비어 있던 다른 손을 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔다.\n\n\"그리고 그 눈 내리던 밤보다 앞서 무슨 일이 있었는지를 우리 고장 사람한테서 들었을 때,\" 마사가 외쳤다. \"제 마음에 가장 쓰라린 생각은, 사람들이 그 애가 한때 저와 어울려 다녔던 걸 기억해 내고는, 제가 그 애를 타락시켰다고 떠들어 댈 거라는 거였어요! 하늘이 알아요, 전 그 애의 깨끗한 이름을 되돌릴 수만 있다면 죽기라도 했을 거예요!\"\n\n오랫동안 자제라는 것을 해 본 적이 없는 터라, 회한과 비탄의 그 찌르는 듯한 고뇌는 보기에 처참했다.\n\n\"죽는 거야 별것도 아니었겠죠 — 뭐라고 해야 할까요? — 전 살아 냈을 거예요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"전 그 비참한 거리에서 늙도록 살아 냈을 거예요 — 어둠 속에서 사람들의 외면을 받으며 떠돌고 — 그 으스스하게 늘어선 집들 위로 동이 트는 걸 보면서, 한때 그 똑같은 해가 내 방으로 비쳐 들어 나를 깨워 주던 걸 떠올리며 — 전 그 애를 구할 수만 있다면 그런 일조차 했을 거예요!\"\n\n그녀는 돌 위로 주저앉아, 양손에 돌을 한 줌씩 움켜쥐고는, 그것을 갈아 으스러뜨리기라도 할 듯 꽉 쥐었다. 그녀는 끊임없이 새로운 자세로 몸을 비틀었다. 두 팔을 뻣뻣하게 세우고, 그것을 얼굴 앞에서 배배 꼬았는데, 마치 그나마 거기 있는 작은 빛마저 두 눈에서 가리려는 듯했고, 머리를 떨구었는데, 마치 견딜 수 없는 기억으로 그것이 무겁기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n\"전 대체 어떻게 해야 할까요!\" 그녀가 그렇게 절망과 싸우며 말했다. \"이대로 어떻게 살아가나요, 제 자신에게는 외로운 저주이고, 제가 가까이 가는 모두에게는 살아 있는 치욕인데요!\" 갑자기 그녀가 내 동행에게 돌아섰다. \"저를 짓밟으세요, 저를 죽이세요! 그 애가 당신의 자랑이던 시절엔, 제가 길에서 그 애를 스치기만 했어도 그 애를 더럽혔다고 여기셨을 거예요. 당신은 제 입에서 나오는 단 한마디도 믿지 못하시겠죠 — 어찌 믿으시겠어요? 지금이라도 그 애와 제가 말 한마디라도 주고받는다면, 그건 당신에게 불타는 수치일 거예요. 전 불평하지 않아요. 그 애와 제가 같다고 말하는 것도 아니에요 — 우리 사이엔 멀고도 먼 거리가 있다는 걸 알아요. 다만 이 한 가지, 제 머리 위에 온갖 죄와 비참함을 다 인 채로 말할게요, 전 영혼을 다해 그 애에게 고마워하고, 그 애를 사랑해요. 아, 무언가를 사랑할 제 능력이 죄다 닳아 없어졌다고는 여기지 말아 주세요! 온 세상이 그러듯 저를 내던지세요. 제가 이런 인간이라는 것 때문에, 한때 그 애를 알았다는 것 때문에 저를 죽이세요. 하지만 저에 대해 그런 생각만은 하지 말아 주세요!\"\n\n그녀가 이렇게 애원하는 동안, 그는 사납고 넋 나간 모습으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그리고 그녀가 입을 다물자, 그는 부드럽게 그녀를 일으켜 세웠다.\n\n\"마사,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"내가 너를 심판하다니 하늘이 막을 일이지. 다른 사람도 아닌 이 내가 그런 짓을 하다니 하늘이 막을 일이고말고, 얘야! 네가 그럴 법하다고 여기지만, 시간이 흐르는 동안 나한테 닥친 변화가 어떤 건지 넌 그 절반도 몰라. 그래!\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 다시 이었다. \"여기 이 신사 양반하고 내가 너한테 말을 걸고 싶어 한 게 어찌 된 영문인지 넌 모를 게다. 우리 앞에 무엇이 놓여 있는지 넌 모를 게야. 자, 들어 봐라!\"\n\n그가 그녀에게 미치는 영향력은 완벽했다. 그녀는 그의 눈을 마주하기가 두렵기라도 한 듯 움츠러든 채 그 앞에 섰다. 그러나 그 격정에 찬 슬픔만은 아주 잠잠해지고 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"네가,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그 눈이 몹시 퍼붓던 밤, 데이비 도련님하고 나 사이에 오간 얘기를 조금이라도 들었다면, 내가 그동안 — 안 가 본 데 없이 — 내 사랑하는 조카딸을 찾아다녔다는 걸 알 게다. 내 사랑하는 조카딸 말이다.\" 그가 굳건하게 되풀이했다. \"전보다 지금이 그 애가 나한테 더 소중하니까 말이다, 마사.\"\n\n그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러나 그 밖에는 가만히 있었다.\n\n\"내가 그 애한테 들었다.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"네가 일찍이 아비도 어미도 잃고, 거친 뱃사람 식으로나마 그 빈자리를 채워 줄 벗 하나 없었다고 말이다. 너한테 그런 벗이 하나 있었더라면, 시간이 흐르면서 그 사람을 따르게 됐으리라는 걸 너도 짐작할 게다. 그리고 내 조카딸은 나한테 딸이나 다름없었다는 것도.\"\n\n그녀가 말없이 떨고 있자, 그는 바닥에서 숄을 집어 들어 정성스레 그녀의 어깨에 둘러 주었다.\n\n\"그러니,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 안다 — 그 애가 나를 다시 한번 볼 수만 있다면 나하고 함께 이 세상 끝까지라도 가리라는 것을. 그리고 나를 보지 않으려고 이 세상 끝까지라도 달아나리라는 것도. 그 애가 내 사랑을 의심할 까닭이야 없고, 의심하지도 않지만 — 의심하지 않지만,\" 그는 자기 말이 옳다는 조용한 확신으로 되풀이했다. \"수치심이 끼어들어, 우리 사이를 가로막고 있으니 말이다.\"\n\n나는 그가 자신을 풀어내는 그 꾸밈없고 묵직한 말 한마디 한마디에서, 그가 이 한 가지 주제를, 그것이 드러내는 온갖 면면을 빠짐없이 곱씹어 왔다는 새로운 증거를 읽었다.\n\n\"우리 짐작으로는,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"여기 데이비 도련님하고 내 짐작으로는, 그 애가 언젠가 외롭고 가엾은 제 발길을 런던으로 향할 것 같다. 우린 믿는다 — 데이비 도련님도, 나도, 우리 모두가 — 네가 그 애한테 닥친 모든 일에 대해, 아직 태어나지 않은 아기만큼이나 아무 죄가 없다는 걸. 너는 그 애가 너한테 다정하고, 친절하고, 상냥했다고 말했지. 그 애에게 복을 빈다, 난 그 애가 그런 줄 알고 있었어! 그 애가 누구에게나 늘 그랬다는 걸 난 알고 있었다. 너는 그 애한테 고마워하고, 그 애를 사랑하지. 그 애를 찾도록 우리를 힘껏 도와다오, 그러면 하늘이 너에게 갚아 주실 게다!\"\n\n그녀는 그가 한 말을 미심쩍어하기라도 하는 듯, 황급히, 그리고 처음으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저를 믿어 주신다고요?\" 그녀가 놀라움에 잠긴 낮은 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"온전히, 거리낌 없이!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그 애를 찾게 되거든 말을 걸고, 제게 나눠 줄 거처가 조금이라도 있거든 그 애를 거두고, 그런 다음 그 애 모르게 당신께 와서 당신을 그 애에게 데려다드리라는 거지요?\" 그녀가 다급히 물었다.\n\n우리는 함께 대답했다. \"그래요!\"\n\n그녀는 눈을 들어, 이 일에 자신을 바치겠노라고, 뜨겁고 충실하게 그리하겠노라고 엄숙히 다짐했다. 한 가닥 희망이라도 남아 있는 한, 결코 흔들리지 않고, 결코 곁길로 새지 않고, 결코 손을 놓지 않겠노라고. 만약 그 다짐에 충실하지 못한다면, 지금 자기를 악이 깃들지 않은 무언가에 붙들어 매 주는 이 삶의 목표가 자기에게서 떠나갈 때, 자기를 그날 밤 강가에 섰을 때보다 — 그게 가능하다면 — 더 처량하고 더 절망스럽게 내버려 두기를. 그리하여 사람의 도움이든 하늘의 도움이든 모든 도움이 자기를 영영 저버리기를.\n\n그녀는 숨소리보다 높게 목소리를 올리지도 않았고, 우리에게 말을 건네지도 않았으며, 이 말을 밤하늘을 향해 했다. 그러고는 음울한 강물을 바라보며 더없이 고요하게 서 있었다.\n\n우리는 이제 우리가 아는 것을 모두 그녀에게 말해 주는 것이 좋겠다고 판단했고, 나는 그것을 길게 들려주었다. 그녀는 잔뜩 귀를 기울여 들었는데, 얼굴은 자주 바뀌었으되, 그 온갖 변하는 표정 속에는 한결같은 결의가 깃들어 있었다. 그녀의 두 눈에는 이따금 눈물이 차올랐으나, 그 눈물을 억눌렀다. 마치 그 영혼이 송두리째 달라지기라도 한 듯, 그녀는 아무리 차분해도 지나치지 않다는 듯했다.\n\n이야기가 다 끝나자 그녀는, 만일 일이 생기면 우리와 어디로 연락하면 되느냐고 물었다. 길가 흐릿한 등불 아래에서, 나는 수첩 한 장에 우리 두 사람의 주소를 적어, 그것을 찢어 그녀에게 주었고, 그녀는 그것을 가엾은 가슴팍에 넣었다. 나는 그녀 자신은 어디 사느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 잠시 뜸을 들이더니, 한곳에 오래 머무는 법이 없다고 했다. 모르는 편이 낫다고.\n\n패거티 씨가 나에게 속삭여 일러 준 것은, 이미 나도 떠올린 바였다. 나는 지갑을 꺼냈으나, 그녀에게 어떤 돈도 받게 할 수가 없었고, 다음에라도 받겠다는 약속조차 받아 낼 수가 없었다. 나는 그녀에게, 패거티 씨가 처지로 보아 결코 가난하다고는 할 수 없는 사람이라는 것, 그리고 그녀가 제 한 몸 살림에 기대어 이 수색에 나선다는 생각이 우리 둘 다를 충격에 빠뜨린다는 것을 일러 주었다. 그녀는 한결같이 버텼다. 이 한 가지에서만큼은, 그가 그녀에게 미치는 영향력도 나의 것과 똑같이 아무런 힘을 쓰지 못했다. 그녀는 그에게 고마워하면서도 꿈쩍하지 않았다.\n\n\"일거리야 구하면 되죠.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"해 볼게요.\"\n\n\"적어도 해 보기 전까지만이라도, 도움을 좀 받아요.\" 내가 받아 말했다.\n\n\"돈을 받고서는 제가 약속한 일을 할 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"굶어 죽는다 해도 그 돈은 받지 못해요. 저에게 돈을 주신다는 건, 당신의 믿음을 도로 거두는 것이고, 당신이 저에게 주신 그 목표를 도로 빼앗는 것이며, 저를 강물에서 건져 주는 단 하나의 확실한 것을 도로 앗아 가는 거예요.\"\n\n\"우리 모두가 그분의 무서운 때에 그 앞에 서야 할, 그 위대한 심판자의 이름으로,\" 내가 말했다. \"그 끔찍한 생각을 떨쳐 버려요! 우리 모두는 마음만 먹으면 무언가 좋은 일을 할 수 있어요.\"\n\n그녀는 떨었고, 입술을 떨었으며, 대답할 때는 얼굴이 더 창백해졌다.\n\n\"어쩌면 가엾은 한 인간을 회개하도록 구원하려는 마음이 당신들 가슴에 심어진 건지도 모르죠. 그렇게 생각하기가 두려워요. 너무 분에 넘치는 것 같아서요. 만약 저한테서 조금이라도 좋은 일이 나온다면, 저도 희망을 품기 시작할지 몰라요. 지금껏 제 행실에서 나온 건 해악밖에 없었으니까요. 당신들이 저에게 애써 보라고 무언가를 맡겨 주신 덕분에, 오랜만에 처음으로, 이 비참한 목숨을 두고 누군가의 믿음을 받게 됐어요. 그 이상은 저도 모르고, 더 할 말도 없어요.\"\n\n그녀는 다시, 흐르기 시작한 눈물을 억눌렀다. 그러고는 떨리는 손을 내밀어, 마치 그에게 무슨 치유의 힘이라도 깃들어 있는 듯 패거티 씨를 한 번 만지더니, 황량한 길을 따라 떠나갔다. 그녀는 병을 앓았던 모양인데, 아마 오래 그랬을 것이다. 좀 더 가까이서 살펴볼 기회에, 나는 그녀가 야위고 초췌하며, 푹 꺼진 두 눈이 궁핍과 인고를 말해 주고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n우리는 얼마쯤 거리를 두고 그녀를 뒤따랐는데, 길이 같은 방향이었던 까닭이다. 그렇게 환하고 사람 많은 거리로 다시 돌아올 때까지. 나는 그녀의 다짐을 어찌나 굳게 믿었던지, 그 시점에 패거티 씨에게, 그녀를 더 따라가는 것은 처음부터 그녀를 못 미더워하는 것처럼 보이지 않겠느냐고 물었다. 그도 같은 생각이었고, 똑같이 그녀를 믿었으므로, 우리는 그녀가 제 갈 길을 가도록 두고, 우리는 우리 길로, 하이게이트 쪽으로 향했다. 그는 한참을 나와 함께 걸어 주었다. 그리고 이 새로운 시도가 잘되기를 비는 기도와 함께 우리가 헤어질 때, 그에게는 내가 어렵잖게 읽어 낼 수 있는, 새롭고도 깊은 연민이 어려 있었다.\n\n집에 다다랐을 때는 한밤중이었다. 나는 우리 집 대문께에 이르러, 세인트폴 대성당의 묵직한 종소리에 귀를 기울이며 서 있었다. 사방에서 울리는 무수한 시계 종소리 틈에 그 소리가 섞여 나에게 실려 온 것 같았기 때문이다. 그런데 고모님 오두막의 문이 열려 있고, 현관의 희미한 불빛이 길 건너까지 새어 나오는 것을 보고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n고모님이 또 옛날의 그 불안 가운데 하나에 빠져들어, 멀리서 일어나는 가상의 화재가 번져 가는 것을 지켜보고 계신 건 아닐까 싶어, 나는 고모님께 말씀을 드리러 갔다. 그러다 한 남자가 고모님의 작은 정원에 서 있는 것을 보고 나는 무척이나 놀랐다.\n\n그는 손에 잔과 병을 들고 술을 마시는 참이었다. 나는 바깥의 우거진 잎사귀들 틈에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 비록 가려져 있긴 했어도 이제 달이 떠 있었던 까닭이다. 그리고 나는 그 남자를 알아보았다. 한때 딕 씨의 헛것이려니 여겼던, 그리고 한 번은 시내 거리에서 고모님과 함께 마주쳤던 바로 그 남자였다.\n\n그는 술뿐 아니라 음식도 먹고 있었는데, 허기진 듯 게걸스럽게 먹는 듯했다. 그는 또 오두막을 두고 마치 처음 보는 것처럼 신기해하는 눈치였다. 그는 몸을 숙여 병을 바닥에 내려놓고는, 창문들을 올려다보고 사방을 둘러보았다. 그러면서도 어딘가 은밀하고 조바심 나는 기색이, 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶어 안달하는 듯했다.\n\n복도의 불빛이 잠깐 가려지더니, 고모님이 나오셨다. 고모님은 마음이 동요한 채로, 그의 손에 돈을 세어 쥐여 주었다. 나는 그 짤랑거리는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"이까짓 게 무슨 소용이오?\" 그가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"더는 줄 게 없어요.\" 고모님이 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 못 가겠소.\" 그가 말했다. \"자! 도로 가져가시오!\"\n\n\"못된 사람 같으니.\" 고모님이 크게 격해진 채로 받아쳤다. \"어떻게 나를 이렇게 부려먹을 수가 있어요? 한데 내가 왜 이런 걸 묻는담? 그건 당신이 내가 얼마나 약한지를 알고 있기 때문이지! 당신의 발걸음에서 영영 자유로워지려면, 당신을 마땅한 업보에 내버려 두는 것 말고 내가 무얼 할 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 나를 마땅한 업보에 내버려 두지 않는 거요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나한테 왜냐고 묻다니!\" 고모님이 받아쳤다. \"당신은 대체 무슨 심보를 가진 사람이람!\"\n\n그는 침울하게 돈을 짤랑거리며 고개를 가로젓고 서 있다가, 마침내 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그럼, 나한테 줄 게 이게 다란 말이오?\"\n\n\"이게 내가 줄 수 있는 전부예요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 손해를 봐서 예전보다 가난해졌다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요. 그렇게 말해 줬고요. 그걸 받았으면, 어째서 나한테 당신을 한순간이라도 더 바라보며 당신이 어떤 꼴이 됐는지 보아야 하는 고통을 주는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그 말이라면, 내 꼴이 어지간히 초라해지긴 했지.\" 그가 말했다. \"올빼미 같은 신세로 살고 있으니.\"\n\n\"당신은 내가 가진 거의 모든 걸 나한테서 벗겨 갔어요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"당신은 몇 해고 몇 해고 내 마음을 온 세상에 닫아걸게 만들었어요. 당신은 나를 거짓되게, 배은망덕하게, 모질게 대했어요. 가요, 그리고 뉘우쳐요. 당신이 나한테 저지른 길고도 긴 잘못의 목록에다 새로운 잘못을 더 보태지 말고요!\"\n\n\"하!\" 그가 받았다. \"다 그럴듯한 소리지 — 그래! 당분간은 내 할 수 있는 대로 해 봐야겠지, 뭐.\"\n\n자기도 모르게, 그는 고모님의 분에 찬 눈물에 머쓱해진 듯, 어슬렁어슬렁 정원에서 나왔다. 나는 마치 막 도착하기라도 한 양 두세 걸음 빠르게 내디뎌, 대문께에서 그와 마주쳤고, 그가 나오는 사이에 안으로 들어갔다. 우리는 스쳐 지나며 서로를 빈틈없이, 그리고 조금도 호의 없이 훑어보았다.\n\n\"고모님,\" 내가 다급히 말했다. \"이 남자가 또 고모님을 겁주고 있어요! 제가 저 사람한테 말 좀 하게 해 주세요. 누구예요?\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 고모님이 내 팔을 붙들며 대답했다. \"들어가자, 그리고 십 분 동안은 나한테 말 걸지 말고.\"\n\n우리는 고모님의 작은 거실에 앉았다. 고모님은 옛날에 쓰던 둥근 초록빛 부채 뒤로 물러나 앉았는데, 그 부채는 의자 등받이에 나사로 고정되어 있었다. 고모님은 한 십오 분쯤 이따금 눈물을 훔쳤다. 그러더니 부채 뒤에서 나와, 내 곁에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"트롯,\" 고모님이 차분하게 말했다. \"그 사람은 내 남편이란다.\"\n\n\"고모님 남편이라고요? 전 돌아가신 줄로만 알았는데요!\"\n\n\"나한테는 죽은 사람이지.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"하지만 살아 있단다.\"\n\n나는 말없이 놀란 채로 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"베시 트롯우드가 그런 애틋한 정 같은 것에 어울릴 만한 위인으로는 안 보이겠지.\" 고모님이 침착하게 말했다. \"하지만 트롯, 한때는 이 베시 트롯우드가 그 남자를 더없이 송두리째 믿던 시절이 있었단다. 그를 사랑하던 시절이 있었지, 트롯, 아주 깊이. 그를 위해서라면 내주지 못할 애정과 정성의 증표가 없던 시절이 있었어. 그는 그 보답으로 내 재산을 거덜 내고, 내 마음마저 거의 부숴 놓았지. 그래서 나는 그런 종류의 감정이란 감정은 죄다, 단 한 번에 영영, 무덤에 묻고, 흙을 메우고, 평평하게 다져 버렸단다.\"\n\n\"고모님, 사랑하는 고모님!\"\n\n\"나는 그를 떠났다.\" 고모님이 늘 하던 대로 자기 손을 내 손등에 얹으며 말을 이었다. \"너그럽게 떠나 줬어. 이제 이렇게 세월이 흐른 마당이니 말할 수 있다만, 트롯, 나는 그를 너그럽게 떠났단다. 그는 나한테 어찌나 모질게 굴었던지, 나로서는 손쉬운 조건으로 갈라설 수도 있었지. 하지만 나는 그러지 않았어. 그는 내가 준 것을 금세 물 위로 던진 조약돌처럼 흥청망청 날려 버리고, 점점 더 밑바닥으로 가라앉아, 내 짐작엔 딴 여자랑 결혼했고, 협잡꾼에 노름꾼에 사기꾼이 됐단다. 지금 그가 어떤 꼴인지는 너도 보았지. 하지만 내가 그와 결혼할 무렵엔 풍채 좋은 남자였단다.\" 고모님이 옛 자부심과 흠모의 메아리를 그 말투에 실어 말했다. \"그리고 나는 그를 믿었지 — 내가 바보였어! — 명예의 화신쯤으로 말이다!\"\n\n고모님은 내 손을 한번 꼭 쥐고는, 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"그는 이제 나한테 아무것도 아니다, 트롯 — 아무것도 아닌 것보다도 못해. 하지만 그가 자기 잘못으로 벌을 받느니 (이 나라에서 어슬렁거리고 다니다가는 그렇게 될 테니), 그가 다시 나타날 때마다 그가 떠나도록 내 형편에 넘치는 돈을 쥐여 준단다. 그와 결혼할 때 나는 바보였지. 그리고 그 일에 관한 한 나는 어찌나 구제 불능의 바보인지, 한때 내가 그를 어떤 사람이라고 믿었던가, 그 기억을 봐서라도, 이 부질없는 환상의 그림자나마 모질게 다뤄지는 꼴은 차마 못 보겠더구나. 나는 진심이었으니까, 트롯, 어느 여자가 진심이었던 적이 있다면 바로 나만큼은 말이다.\"\n\n고모님은 무거운 한숨과 함께 이 이야기를 매듭짓고, 옷매무새를 가다듬었다.\n\n\"자, 얘야!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이제 너도 처음과 가운데와 끝까지, 그 모든 사연을 다 알게 됐구나. 우리 둘은 이 이야기를 두 번 다시 서로 입에 올리지 말자꾸나. 너도 물론 다른 누구한테든 이 얘기를 꺼내지 말고. 이건 나의 부루퉁하고 케케묵은 이야기이니, 우리끼리만 간직하자, 트롯!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 48. DOMESTIC\n\n나는 신문 일을 한 치 어긋남 없이 제때 해내면서도 거기에 방해받지 않도록 내 책에 매달려 부지런히 썼다. 그렇게 책이 세상에 나왔고, 큰 성공을 거두었다. 귀에 쟁쟁하게 울리는 칭찬에 나는 정신을 잃을 만큼 들뜨지는 않았다. 물론 그 칭찬에 예민하게 마음이 가 닿기는 했고, 내 작품을 두고 누구보다도 내가 더 높이 평가했으리라는 것은 거의 의심할 여지가 없다. 사람의 본성을 지켜보면서 내가 늘 깨달은 바가 있는데, 자기를 믿을 만한 그럴듯한 근거를 가진 사람은 결코 남들이 자기를 믿게 하려고 그들 면전에서 제 자랑을 떠벌리지 않는다는 것이다. 그래서 나는 바로 그 자존심 덕분에 겸손함을 지켰고, 칭찬을 받으면 받을수록 그 칭찬에 더 어울리는 사람이 되려고 애썼다.\n\n이 기록은 다른 모든 본질적인 면에서는 내 기억을 글로 옮긴 것이지만, 내가 쓴 소설들의 내력을 좇는 것이 그 목적은 아니다. 소설은 스스로 제 모습을 드러내니, 나는 그것들을 그것들에게 맡겨 둔다. 내가 이따금 그것들을 언급한다면, 그것은 오로지 내 성장 과정의 한 부분으로서일 뿐이다.\n\n이 무렵 천성과 우연이 나를 작가로 만들어 놓았다고 믿을 만한 근거가 어느 정도 생겼기에, 나는 자신감을 품고 내 천직을 좇았다. 그런 확신이 없었더라면 나는 분명 그 길을 내버려 두고 다른 일에 내 힘을 쏟았을 것이다. 천성과 우연이 나를 정말로 무엇으로 만들어 놓았는지를 알아내려 애썼을 테고, 다른 무엇도 아닌 바로 그것이 되려 했을 것이다. 나는 신문에도, 다른 데에도 글을 써서 꽤 잘 풀려 가고 있었으니, 이번 새로운 성공을 거두고 나자 그 따분한 토론에서 벗어날 자격쯤은 마땅히 있다고 여겼다. 그래서 어느 기쁜 밤, 나는 마지막으로 의회의 백파이프 소리를 받아 적었고, 그 뒤로는 그 소리를 다시는 듣지 않았다. 그래도 회기 내내 신문 속에서 그 낡은 웅웅거림을 여전히 알아보기는 하는데, 이렇다 할 변화 하나 없이 (아마 그 양이 더 늘어났다는 것 말고는) 그대로다.\n\n지금 내가 쓰고 있는 것은, 내가 결혼한 지 한 일 년 반쯤 되었을 무렵의 이야기다. 이런저런 방식으로 여러 번 실험해 본 끝에, 우리는 살림이란 것을 글러 먹은 일로 여기고 포기해 버렸다. 집은 알아서 굴러갔고, 우리는 사환 아이 하나를 두었다. 이 하인의 주된 임무는 요리사와 다투는 것이었는데, 그 점에서 그는 영락없는 휘팅턴이었다 — 다만 고양이도 없고, 런던 시장이 될 가망이라곤 손톱만큼도 없는 휘팅턴이었지만.\n\n* 휘팅턴: 고양이 덕에 출세하여 런던 시장이 되었다는 전설 속 인물 딕 휘팅턴을 가리킴.\n내가 보기에 그 아이는 냄비 뚜껑이 우박처럼 쏟아지는 속에서 사는 듯했다. 그의 삶 전체가 한바탕 드잡이였다. 그는 영 어울리지 않는 순간마다 — 가령 우리가 조촐한 만찬 자리를 벌이거나 저녁에 친구 몇이 와 있을 때 — 살려 달라고 비명을 질러 댔고, 쇠붙이가 뒤따라 날아오는 와중에 부엌에서 굴러 나오곤 했다. 우리는 그를 떼어 내고 싶었지만, 그는 우리에게 어찌나 정이 깊었던지 도무지 떠나려 하지 않았다. 그는 눈물이 헤픈 아이였고, 관계를 끊자는 기미만 비쳐도 어찌나 가엾게 통곡을 터뜨리던지, 우리는 그를 데리고 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그에게는 어머니도 없었고 — 내가 찾아낸 한, 친척이라 할 만한 것이 아무것도 없었다 — 누이 하나만 있었는데, 그 누이는 우리가 동생을 떠맡자마자 미국으로 줄행랑을 쳐 버렸다. 그래서 그는 끔찍한 어린 도깨비처럼 우리 집에 눌러앉게 되었다. 그는 제 불운한 처지를 생생하게 알아차리고 있어서, 늘 윗도리 소맷자락으로 눈을 비비거나, 작은 손수건의 맨 끄트머리 한 귀퉁이에 코를 풀려고 몸을 숙이곤 했는데, 그 손수건은 절대 호주머니에서 통째로 꺼내는 법이 없이 늘 아껴 가며 감춰 두었다.\n\n이 불운한 사환 아이는, 불길한 시각에 연봉 육 파운드 십 실링으로 고용된 터였는데, 나에게는 끊임없는 골칫거리의 근원이었다. 나는 그가 자라는 것을 — 그는 강낭콩처럼 쑥쑥 자랐다 — 그가 면도를 시작할 날을, 심지어 머리가 벗어지거나 희끗희끗해질 날을 떠올리며 괴로운 불안에 사로잡혀 지켜보았다. 그를 떼어 낼 가망은 도무지 보이지 않았다. 그래서 나는 미래로 나 자신을 던져 보며, 그가 노인이 되면 얼마나 골칫거리일까 곰곰이 생각하곤 했다.\n\n이 불행한 녀석이 내 곤경을 풀어 줄 방식을, 나는 꿈에도 짐작하지 못했다. 그는 도라의 시계를 훔쳤는데, 그 시계는 우리 것이 다 그렇듯 따로 정해진 자리가 없던 물건이었다. 그는 그것을 돈으로 바꿔, 그 돈을 (그는 늘 줏대 없는 아이였으니) 런던과 억스브리지 사이를 마차 바깥자리에 앉아 쉴 새 없이 오르내리는 데 다 써 버렸다. 내 기억으로 그는 열다섯 번째 여행을 마쳤을 때 보 스트리트로 끌려갔는데, 그때 그의 몸에서 사 실링 육 펜스와, 불 줄도 모르는 중고 피리 하나가 나왔다.\n\n만약 그가 뉘우치지만 않았더라면 그 사건과 그 뒷일은 나에게 훨씬 덜 거북했을 것이다. 하지만 그는 정말이지 깊이 뉘우쳤고, 그것도 묘한 방식으로 — 한꺼번에가 아니라 할부로 뉘우쳤다. 이를테면 이런 식이었다. 내가 그를 상대로 법정에 서야 했던 다음 날, 그는 지하실의 광주리 하나에 관해 어떤 폭로를 했는데, 우리는 그것이 포도주로 가득 찬 줄로만 알고 있었으나 안에는 빈 병과 코르크 마개밖에 없었다. 우리는 그가 이제 속을 다 털어놓고, 요리사에 대해 아는 가장 나쁜 일까지 다 일러바쳤거니 했다. 그러나 하루 이틀쯤 지나자 그의 양심이 새로이 뜨끔해졌는지, 요리사에게 어린 딸이 하나 있어 그 애가 매일 새벽 우리 빵을 가져갔다는 것과, 또 자기 자신은 우유 배달부에게 석탄을 대 주도록 매수당했다는 사실을 폭로했다. 다시 이삼 일이 더 지나자, 나는 그가 부엌 잡동사니 속에서 소 등심 덩어리가, 그리고 누더기 자루 속에서 침대 시트가 발견되도록 단서를 댔다는 소식을 당국으로부터 전해 들었다. 그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 전혀 새로운 방향으로 터져 나와, 술집 심부름꾼 녀석이 우리 집에 강도질을 들 뜻을 품고 있더라는 것을 자기가 알고 있노라 자백했고, 그 녀석은 즉시 붙잡혀 갔다. 나는 이렇게 봉변을 당하는 처지가 너무도 부끄러워, 그가 입을 다물어만 준다면 얼마든 돈을 주었을 것이고, 그를 도망치게 놓아주는 대가로라면 두둑한 뇌물이라도 내놓았을 것이다. 사건에서 더 약 오르는 대목은, 그가 이런 내 심정은 짐작도 못 한 채, 새로운 폭로를 할 때마다 자기가 나에게 빚을 갚는 셈이라고 — 더 나아가 나에게 은혜를 잔뜩 베푸는 셈이라고 여겼다는 것이다.\n\n마침내 나는, 새로운 정보를 들고 경찰 사자가 다가오는 게 보일 때마다, 나 자신이 줄행랑을 쳤다. 그래서 그가 재판을 받고 유형에 처해질 때까지 숨어 사는 신세로 지냈다. 그러고 나서도 그는 가만히 있지 못하고 줄곧 우리에게 편지를 써 보냈다. 떠나기 전에 도라를 몹시도 보고 싶어 한 나머지, 도라가 면회를 갔다가, 쇠창살 안에 들어선 순간 그만 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다. 한마디로 나는 그가 국외로 추방되어, (나중에 들으니) 어딘가 '내륙 깊숙한 곳'에서 양치기가 될 때까지 단 하루도 마음 편할 날이 없었다. 그곳이 지리상 어디인지는 나도 전혀 모른다.\n\n이 모든 일은 나를 어떤 진지한 성찰로 이끌었고, 우리의 잘못을 새로운 측면에서 보여 주었다. 어느 날 저녁, 도라를 향한 애틋한 마음에도 불구하고 나는 끝내 그것을 그녀에게 털어놓지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"여보,\" 내가 말했다. \"우리에게 체계도 관리도 없는 탓에 우리 자신만이 아니라 (그건 이제 우리도 익숙해졌지) 다른 사람들까지 끌어들인다고 생각하면 정말 마음이 아파.\"\n\n\"당신 한참을 잠자코 있더니, 이제 또 짜증을 내려는 거죠!\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"아니야, 여보, 정말 아니야! 내 말이 무슨 뜻인지 설명하게 해 줘.\"\n\n\"난 알고 싶지 않은 것 같아요.\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 난 당신이 알았으면 해, 여보. 집을 내려놓아.\"\n\n도라는 집의 코를 내 코에 갖다 대고는 \"워!\" 하고 외쳐 내 진지함을 쫓아 버리려 했다. 그러나 뜻대로 되지 않자, 집을 탑 모양 개집으로 들여보내고는, 두 손을 포갠 채 더없이 체념한 듯한 자그마한 표정으로 나를 빤히 바라보며 앉았다.\n\n\"실은 말이야, 여보,\" 내가 입을 뗐다. \"우리한테는 전염성이 있어. 우리 주위 사람들을 죄다 물들인단 말이야.\"\n\n만약 도라의 얼굴이 나에게 일러 주지 않았더라면, 나는 이런 비유적인 투로 계속 늘어놓았을지도 모른다. 도라는 온 힘을 다해 궁리하고 있었던 것이다 — 내가 이 우리의 못된 상태를 두고 무슨 새로운 종류의 예방접종이나 다른 의학적 치료법을 제안하려는 게 아닐까 하고. 그래서 나는 말을 멈추고, 내 뜻을 좀 더 알아듣기 쉽게 풀었다.\n\n\"내 사랑, 그저 우리가 좀 더 조심하기를 배우지 못한 탓에 돈과 안락을, 때로는 마음의 평화까지 잃는다는 것만이 아니야,\" 내가 말했다. \"우리 밑에서 일하거나 우리와 거래하는 사람을 죄다 망쳐 놓는 중대한 책임까지 떠안게 된다는 거야. 잘못이 어느 한쪽에만 있는 게 아니라, 이 사람들이 다 못되게 변해 가는 건 우리 자신이 그리 잘하지 못하기 때문이 아닐까, 슬슬 그런 게 두려워지기 시작했어.\"\n\n\"어머나, 무슨 그런 누명을 씌워요,\" 도라가 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 외쳤다. \"내가 금시계 훔치는 걸 본 적이라도 있다는 듯이! 어머나!\"\n\n\"이봐, 여보,\" 나는 타일렀다. \"말도 안 되는 소리 하지 마! 누가 금시계 얘길 눈곱만큼이라도 꺼냈다고 그래?\"\n\n\"당신이 그랬잖아요.\" 도라가 받아쳤다. \"그랬다는 거 알면서. 내가 잘하지 못했다고 하면서, 나를 그 애한테 빗댔잖아요.\"\n\n\"누구한테?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그 사환 아이한테요.\" 도라가 흐느꼈다. \"어쩜 그렇게 잔인해요, 당신을 사랑하는 아내를 유배된 사환 아이한테 빗대다니! 왜 결혼하기 전에 나에 대한 당신 속내를 말해 주지 않았어요? 왜 말 안 했어요, 이 매정한 양반아, 내가 유배된 사환 아이보다 더 못한 사람이라고 확신한다고? 어쩜 나를 그렇게 끔찍하게 생각해요! 아아, 세상에!\"\n\n\"자, 도라, 여보,\" 나는 그녀가 눈에 갖다 댄 손수건을 살며시 치우려 애쓰며 말했다. \"이건 당신답지 않게 우스꽝스러울 뿐 아니라 아주 잘못된 생각이야. 무엇보다도 그건 사실이 아니야.\"\n\n\"당신은 늘 그 애가 거짓말쟁이라고 했잖아요.\" 도라가 흐느꼈다. \"그런데 이제 나한테도 똑같은 말을 하다니! 아아, 어쩌면 좋아! 어쩌면 좋아!\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 여보,\" 나는 되받았다. \"정말이지 부탁이니 분별 있게 좀 굴고, 내가 한 말, 지금 하는 말을 잘 들어 봐. 도라, 우리가 부리는 사람들에게 우리 도리를 다하는 법을 배우지 않으면, 그들도 결코 우리에게 제 도리를 다하는 법을 배우지 못할 거야. 우리가 사람들에게 결코 줘서는 안 될 잘못을 저지를 기회를 주고 있는 게 아닐까 두려워. 설령 우리가 이 모든 살림살이에서 지금처럼 느슨한 게 우리 선택이라 해도 — 사실 그렇지도 않지만 — 설령 우리가 그걸 좋아하고 그렇게 사는 게 즐겁다 해도 — 그렇지도 않지만 — 그래도 우리에게 이런 식으로 계속할 권리는 없다고 나는 확신해. 우리는 분명히 사람들을 타락시키고 있어. 우린 그걸 생각해야 해. 나는 그 생각을 떨칠 수가 없어, 도라. 도무지 떨쳐 버릴 수 없는 생각이고, 때로는 그게 나를 몹시 불안하게 만들어. 자, 여보, 그게 다야. 이제 그만. 바보같이 굴지 마!\"\n\n도라는 한참 동안 내가 손수건을 치우도록 놔두지 않았다. 그녀는 손수건 뒤에서 흐느끼며 중얼거리기를, 그렇게 불안하다면 애초에 왜 결혼을 했느냐고 했다. 왜 교회에 가기 바로 전날에라도, 내가 불안할 걸 알고 있으니 차라리 결혼하지 않는 게 낫겠다고 말하지 않았느냐고 했다. 나를 견뎌 낼 수 없다면 왜 자기를 퍼트니의 고모들에게나, 아니면 인도의 줄리아 밀스에게 보내 버리지 않느냐고도 했다. 줄리아라면 자기를 기쁘게 맞아 줄 것이고, 유배된 사환 아이라고 부르지도 않을 거라고 — 줄리아는 한 번도 자기를 그딴 식으로 부른 적이 없다고 했다. 한마디로 도라는 어찌나 슬퍼하던지, 또 그 슬퍼하는 모습으로 나를 어찌나 슬프게 하던지, 나는 아무리 부드럽게 한들 이런 식의 시도를 되풀이해 봐야 소용없겠다는 것을, 무언가 다른 길을 택해야겠다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n남은 다른 길이 무엇이었던가? 그녀의 '마음을 빚어' 보는 것? 이것은 그럴듯하고 희망찬 울림을 가진 흔한 말이었기에, 나는 도라의 마음을 빚어 보기로 결심했다.\n\n나는 곧장 시작했다. 도라가 한껏 어린애처럼 굴어서 그저 비위를 맞춰 주는 게 한없이 더 좋을 때에도, 나는 짐짓 근엄해지려 애썼다 — 그래서 그녀를 당황하게 했고, 나 자신마저 당황하게 했다. 나는 내 머릿속을 채우고 있는 주제들에 관해 그녀에게 이야기했고, 셰익스피어를 읽어 주었다 — 그래서 그녀를 지칠 대로 지치게 만들었다. 나는 짐짓 아주 무심한 척 그녀에게 쓸모 있는 정보 부스러기나 건전한 의견을 자잘하게 던져 주는 버릇을 들였다 — 그러면 내가 그것을 터뜨릴 때마다 그녀는 마치 폭죽이라도 터진 듯 화들짝 놀라며 물러섰다. 아무리 우연인 척, 자연스러운 척 내 어린 아내의 마음을 빚어 보려 애써도, 그녀가 늘 본능적으로 내 속셈을 알아채고는 더없이 날카로운 불안에 사로잡힌다는 것을 나는 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다. 특히 그녀가 셰익스피어를 끔찍한 사람으로 여긴다는 것만은 나에게 분명했다. 마음을 빚는 일은 더디기 짝이 없게 진행되었다.\n\n나는 트래들스를 본인도 모르게 이 일에 끌어들여, 그가 우리를 보러 올 때마다 도라를 간접적으로 교화할 셈으로 그를 향해 내 지뢰를 터뜨렸다. 이런 식으로 내가 트래들스에게 베푼 실용적 지혜의 양은 엄청났고, 질도 최상급이었다. 그러나 그것이 도라에게 미친 영향이라곤, 그녀의 기분을 가라앉히고, 다음은 자기 차례일 거라는 두려움에 늘 안절부절못하게 만든 것뿐이었다. 나는 어느새 내가 학교 선생, 덫, 함정 같은 존재가 되어, 도라라는 파리를 노리는 거미 노릇을 하며, 늘 구멍에서 불쑥 튀어나와 그녀를 한없이 어지럽히고 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n그래도 나는 이 중간 단계 너머로, 도라와 나 사이에 완벽한 공감이 이루어지고, 내가 그녀의 마음을 내 마음에 흡족하도록 '빚어' 놓을 그날을 내다보며, 몇 달이나 끈기 있게 버텼다. 그러나 마침내 깨닫고 보니, 그 오랜 시간 내내 나는 결의로 온몸의 가시를 잔뜩 곤두세운 호저나 고슴도치 같은 꼴이었으면서도 이뤄 놓은 것이 하나도 없었다. 그러자 어쩌면 도라의 마음은 이미 다 빚어져 있는 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n곰곰이 더 따져 보니 그럴 법한 일로 여겨졌기에, 나는 그 계획을 — 행동으로 옮길 때보다 말로 할 때 훨씬 그럴듯해 보였던 그 계획을 — 단념했다. 이제부터는 내 어린 아내에 만족하고, 어떤 방법으로도 그녀를 다른 무엇으로 바꾸려 들지 않기로 마음먹었다. 혼자서 슬기롭고 신중한 척하는 데에도, 내 사랑하는 사람이 옴짝달싹 못 하는 모습을 지켜보는 데에도 진절머리가 났다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 위해 예쁜 귀걸이 한 쌍을, 집을 위해 목걸이 하나를 사 들고, 어느 날 집으로 돌아가 다정한 사람이 되기로 했다.\n\n도라는 그 자그마한 선물에 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르며 흥겹게 나에게 입을 맞췄다. 그러나 우리 사이에는, 아무리 희미할지언정 그늘이 하나 있었고, 나는 그것이 거기 있어서는 안 된다고 단단히 마음을 굳혔다. 어디엔가 그런 그늘이 꼭 있어야만 한다면, 나는 앞으로 그것을 내 가슴속에만 간직하리라.\n\n나는 소파에 앉은 아내 곁에 앉아, 귀걸이를 그녀의 귀에 달아 주었다. 그러고 나서 요즘 우리가 예전만큼 좋은 동무가 되지 못한 것 같아 걱정인데, 그게 다 내 잘못이라고 말했다. 진심으로 그렇게 느꼈고, 또 사실이 그러했다.\n\n\"실은 말이야, 도라, 내 사랑,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 슬기로운 사람이 되어 보려고 애써 왔어.\"\n\n\"그리고 나까지 슬기롭게 만들려고요.\" 도라가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"그렇죠, 두디?\"\n\n치켜올린 두 눈썹의 그 예쁜 물음에, 나는 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 답하고는, 살짝 벌어진 입술에 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"그래 봐야 손톱만큼도 소용없어요.\" 도라가 고개를 절레절레 흔드는 바람에 귀걸이가 다시 짤랑거렸다. \"내가 얼마나 하찮은 사람인지, 처음부터 당신이 나를 뭐라고 불러 주길 바랐는지 잘 알잖아요. 그렇게 해 주지 못한다면, 당신은 영영 나를 좋아하지 못할까 봐 걱정이에요. 정말로 가끔, 차라리 이러는 편이 나았을 거라고 생각하지 않아요 —\"\n\n\"뭘 하는 편이, 여보?\" 그녀가 더는 말을 잇지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요!\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니라고?\" 내가 되물었다.\n그녀는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고는 웃음을 터뜨리며, 자기가 좋아하는 별명대로 스스로를 거위라고 부르고는, 곱슬머리를 어찌나 풍성하게 늘어뜨려 내 어깨에 얼굴을 묻던지, 그것을 헤치고 얼굴을 보는 것이 여간 큰일이 아니었다.\n\n\"내 어린 아내의 마음을 빚으려 애쓰느니, 차라리 아무것도 하지 않는 편이 나았을 거라고 생각하지 않느냐고?\" 나는 나 자신을 비웃으며 말했다. \"그게 묻고 싶은 거야? 그래, 정말이지 그래.\"\n\n\"그게 당신이 애써 온 일이었어요?\" 도라가 외쳤다. \"어머, 못된 사람 같으니!\"\n\n\"하지만 다시는 그러지 않을 거야.\" 내가 말했다. \"나는 지금 그대로의 그녀를 끔찍이 사랑하니까.\"\n\n\"거짓말 빼고 — 정말로요?\" 도라가 나에게 더 바짝 파고들며 물었다.\n\n\"오랫동안 나에게 그토록 소중했던 것을,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 왜 바꾸려 들겠어! 너는 타고난 네 모습일 때보다 더 멋질 수가 없어, 사랑스러운 도라. 우리는 이제 잘난 척하는 실험 따위는 그만두고, 옛날 우리 방식으로 돌아가 행복하게 지내자.\"\n\n\"행복하게 지내요!\" 도라가 받아쳤다. \"그래요! 하루 종일! 그리고 가끔 일이 아주 쪼끔 틀어져도 신경 쓰지 않을 거죠?\"\n\n\"안 쓰지, 안 써.\" 내가 말했다. \"우린 그저 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다하면 돼.\"\n\n\"그리고 우리가 다른 사람들을 못되게 만든다는 말은 다시 안 할 거죠,\" 도라가 어리광을 부렸다. \"그렇죠? 그건 정말이지 너무 끔찍하게 짜증 나는 소리란 말이에요!\"\n\n\"안 해, 안 해.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 불편한 것보다는 어리석은 편이 더 낫죠, 그렇죠?\" 도라가 말했다.\n\n\"세상 그 무엇보다, 타고난 도라 그대로인 편이 낫지.\"\n\n\"세상이라니! 아, 두디, 세상은 넓은 곳이잖아요!\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 흔들고는, 기쁨에 빛나는 두 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보며, 나에게 입을 맞추고, 까르르 즐겁게 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 집의 새 목걸이를 채워 주려고 깡충 뛰어가 버렸다.\n\n이렇게 도라를 조금이라도 바꿔 보려던 나의 마지막 시도는 막을 내렸다. 그것을 시도하면서 나는 불행했다. 나 홀로 슬기로운 척하는 것을 견딜 수 없었고, 또 그것을 나의 어린 아내로서 일찍이 나에게 매달리던 그녀의 모습과 도무지 조화시킬 수 없었다. 나는 조용한 방식으로 우리 살림을 나 스스로 나아지게 할 수 있는 만큼 해 보기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 내가 아무리 애를 써 봐야 그 결과는 보잘것없으리라는 것을, 그러지 않으려면 다시 거미로 전락해 영영 잠복해 기다리는 신세가 되리라는 것을 나는 내다보았다.\n\n그리고 내가 앞서 말한 그늘, 다시는 우리 사이에 두지 않고 온전히 내 가슴 위에만 얹어 두기로 한 그 그늘은 어찌 되었던가? 그것은 어떻게 드리워졌던가?\n\n그 오래된 불행한 느낌이 내 삶에 스며들었다. 변했다 해도 그저 더 깊어졌을 뿐이었고, 여전히 종잡을 수 없는 채로, 마치 밤중에 어렴풋이 들려오는 한 가락 구슬픈 음악처럼 나에게 말을 걸어왔다. 나는 아내를 끔찍이 사랑했고, 행복했다. 그러나 한때 내가 어렴풋이 그려 보았던 그 행복은 내가 누리는 행복이 아니었으며, 늘 무언가 모자란 데가 있었다.\n\n내 마음을 이 종이 위에 비추어 내겠다고 나 자신과 맺은 약속을 지키고자, 나는 다시 그것을 면밀히 들여다보며 그 비밀을 빛 가운데로 끄집어낸다. 내가 아쉬워했던 것을, 나는 여전히 — 늘 — 젊은 날의 환상이 빚어낸 한낱 꿈이었다고 여겼다. 결코 이루어질 수 없는 것, 모든 사람이 그러하듯 어느 정도 자연스러운 아픔과 더불어 그것이 그러하다는 것을 이제 깨달아 가는 중이라고 말이다. 그러나 내 아내가 나를 더 도와줄 수 있었더라면, 나에게 동무가 없던 그 숱한 생각들을 함께 나눠 줄 수 있었더라면 나에게 더 나았으리라는 것, 또 그럴 수도 있었으리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었다.\n\n서로 화해할 수 없는 이 두 결론 사이에서 — 하나는 내가 느끼는 것이 누구에게나 흔한, 피할 수 없는 일이라는 것, 다른 하나는 그것이 나에게만 고유한 일이며 달라질 수도 있었으리라는 것 — 나는 둘이 서로 어긋난다는 또렷한 자각도 없이 묘하게 갈팡질팡했다. 이루어질 수 없는 젊은 날의 부질없는 꿈을 떠올리면, 어느새 자라나 벗어 버린, 어른이 되기 직전의 더 나은 시절이 떠올랐다. 그러면 정든 옛집에서 아그네스와 함께 보낸 그 흡족하던 날들이, 죽은 자들의 망령처럼 내 앞에 솟아올랐다 — 다른 세상에서라면 어떤 식으로든 되살아날지 모르나, 이승에서는 다시는 소생할 수 없는 망령처럼.\n\n이따금 이런 생각이 머릿속에 떠오르곤 했다. 도라와 내가 서로를 영영 몰랐더라면 어떤 일이 벌어졌을까, 혹은 벌어졌을까? 그러나 그녀는 내 존재와 너무도 한 몸으로 엮여 있어서, 그것은 온갖 부질없는 공상 가운데서도 가장 부질없는 것이었고, 이내 허공에 떠다니는 거미줄처럼 내 손길과 시야 너머로 솟아올라 사라져 버리곤 했다.\n\n나는 언제나 그녀를 사랑했다. 내가 여기 적고 있는 이 느낌은 내 마음 가장 깊은 구석에서 잠들었다가, 반쯤 깨어났다가, 다시 잠들곤 했다. 그것이 내 안에 있다는 흔적은 겉으로 드러나지 않았으며, 그것이 내가 한 말이나 행동에 무슨 영향을 끼쳤는지 나는 알지 못한다. 우리의 자잘한 근심거리며 내 모든 계획의 무게는 모두 내가 짊어졌고, 도라는 펜을 들었다. 그리고 우리 둘 다 우리 몫이 형편에 맞게 잘 나누어졌다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 진심으로 나를 좋아했고, 나를 자랑스러워했다. 아그네스가 도라에게 보내는 편지에 몇 마디 진심 어린 말을 적어 — 나의 옛 친구들이 나날이 높아 가는 내 명성을 전해 듣고, 또 내 책을 마치 내가 그 내용을 직접 말해 주기라도 하는 양 읽으면서 품은 그 자랑스러움과 관심을 적어 — 보내면, 도라는 빛나는 두 눈에 기쁨의 눈물을 글썽이며 그것을 나에게 소리 내어 읽어 주고는, 나더러 정답고 똑똑하고 유명한 양반이라고 했다.\n\n\"단련되지 못한 마음이 처음으로 잘못 내디딘 충동.\" 스트롱 부인의 그 말이 이 무렵 나에게 끊임없이 되살아났다. 거의 늘 내 마음속에 자리하고 있었다. 나는 밤중에 종종 그 말과 함께 잠에서 깼고, 심지어 꿈속에서 그 말이 집 벽에 새겨진 것을 읽은 기억까지 난다. 이제 나는 알았기 때문이다 — 내 마음이 처음 도라를 사랑했을 때 그 마음은 단련되지 못한 것이었고, 만약 단련된 마음이었더라면 결혼한 뒤에, 그 은밀한 경험 속에서 느꼈던 것을 결코 느끼지 못했으리라는 것을.\n\n\"마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 결혼에 큰 어긋남은 없다.\" 그 말도 나는 기억했다. 나는 도라를 나에게 맞추려 애써 보았으나, 그것이 불가능하다는 것을 알았다. 이제 남은 것은 나를 도라에게 맞추는 일이었다 — 내가 나눌 수 있는 것을 그녀와 나누며 행복해하고, 내가 짊어져야 할 것을 내 어깨에 지고서도 여전히 행복해하는 일이었다. 이것이 내가 생각이란 것을 하기 시작했을 때 내 마음을 데려가려 한 단련이었다. 그것은 나의 둘째 해를 첫해보다 훨씬 행복하게 만들어 주었고, 더더욱 좋았던 것은, 도라의 삶을 온통 햇살로 채워 주었다는 점이다.\n\n그러나 그해가 저물어 갈수록 도라는 몸이 튼튼하지 못했다. 나는 나보다 더 부드러운 손길이 그녀의 성품을 빚는 일을 도와주기를, 그녀의 가슴에 안긴 아기의 미소가 나의 어린 아내를 한 여인으로 바꾸어 주기를 바랐다. 그러나 그렇게 될 운명이 아니었다. 그 어린 영혼은 자그마한 감옥의 문턱에서 한순간 파닥이더니, 갇혀 있었다는 것조차 모른 채 날개를 펴고 날아가 버렸다.\n\n\"내가 다시 예전처럼 뛰어다닐 수 있게 되면, 고모님,\" 도라가 말했다. \"집을 막 달리게 할 거예요. 요즘 영 느려 터지고 게을러졌거든요.\"\n\n\"내 보기엔 말이다, 얘야,\" 고모님이 그녀 곁에서 조용히 바느질을 하며 말했다. \"저 녀석은 그것보다 더 고약한 병이 든 게야. 나이라는 병이지, 도라.\"\n\n\"저 애가 늙었다고 생각하세요?\" 도라가 놀라 물었다. \"어머, 집이 늙었다니, 어쩜 그렇게 이상한 일이 다 있어요!\"\n\n\"그건 우리 모두가 살아가면서 걸리게 마련인 병이란다, 우리 아가,\" 고모님이 명랑하게 말했다. \"나도 예전보다 그 병에서 더 자유로워졌다고는 말 못 하지, 정말로.\"\n\n\"하지만 집이,\" 도라가 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"우리 조그만 집이까지! 아아, 가엾어라!\"\n\n\"내 보기엔 아직 한참 더 살 것 같구나, 우리 꽃송이,\" 고모님이 도라의 뺨을 토닥이며 말했다. 도라는 집을 보려고 긴 의자에서 몸을 내밀고 있었는데, 집은 거기에 응답하느라 뒷다리로 일어서서, 머리와 어깨로 기어오르려고 천식 걸린 듯 헐떡이며 이런저런 시도를 하다가 번번이 제풀에 주저앉았다. \"올겨울엔 저 녀석 집에 플란넬 천 한 조각을 깔아 줘야겠다. 그러면 봄에 꽃들이 필 무렵엔 다시 아주 팔팔해져서 나온대도 놀랍지 않을 게야. 저 작은 강아지 녀석!\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"고양이만큼 목숨이 여럿이고 그 목숨을 죄다 잃기 직전이라 해도, 마지막 숨을 내쉬면서까지 나한테 짖어 댈 녀석이라니까, 정말로!\"\n\n도라가 집을 소파 위로 끌어 올려 주었는데, 거기서 집은 정말이지 어찌나 사납게 고모님에게 대들던지, 똑바로 서 있지도 못하고 옆으로 비틀거리며 짖어 댔다. 고모님이 그를 바라보면 바라볼수록 그는 더욱 그녀를 나무랐다. 고모님이 요즘 들어 안경을 쓰기 시작했는데, 무슨 알 수 없는 까닭에서인지 그는 그 안경알을 자기에 대한 모욕으로 여겼던 것이다.\n\n도라는 한참을 구슬려 가며 집을 자기 곁에 눕혔다. 그가 잠잠해지자, 그녀는 그의 긴 귀 한쪽을 손으로 자꾸자꾸 쓸어내리며, 골똘히 되뇌었다. \"우리 조그만 집이까지! 아아, 가엾어라!\"\n\n\"폐는 멀쩡하구먼,\" 고모님이 쾌활하게 말했다. \"싫어하는 것 미워하는 기운도 전혀 시들지 않았고. 틀림없이 앞으로 여러 해는 더 살 게다. 하지만 함께 달릴 강아지가 갖고 싶다면, 우리 작은 꽃송이야, 저 녀석은 너무 호강하며 살아서 그건 안 되겠으니, 내가 한 마리 구해 주마.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 고모님,\" 도라가 가냘프게 말했다. \"하지만 제발 그러지 마세요!\"\n\n\"안 된다고?\" 고모님이 안경을 벗으며 말했다.\n\n\"저는 집 말고 다른 강아지는 못 키워요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그건 집한테 너무 못된 일이잖아요! 게다가 저는 집 말고 다른 강아지하곤 그렇게 친구가 될 수 없어요. 다른 강아지는 제가 결혼하기 전의 저를 알지 못했을 테고, 두디가 우리 집에 처음 왔을 때 짖어 주지도 않았을 테니까요. 집 말고 다른 강아지는 도무지 마음이 안 갈 것 같아요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고!\" 고모님이 다시 그녀의 뺨을 토닥이며 말했다. \"네 말이 옳다.\"\n\n\"화나신 거 아니죠.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 이렇게 여린 우리 아가가 다 있나!\" 고모님이 다정하게 그녀에게 몸을 숙이며 외쳤다. \"내가 화를 내다니, 그런 생각을 다 하다니!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요, 정말로 그렇게 생각한 건 아니에요.\" 도라가 받아쳤다. \"그저 조금 피곤해서, 잠깐 바보같이 군 거예요 — 저야 늘 바보 같은 조그만 사람이지만, 집 얘기를 하니까 더 바보가 된 거예요. 집은 저한테 일어난 모든 일을 다 겪으면서 저를 알아 왔잖아요, 그렇지, 집? 그런데 저 애가 좀 변했다고 해서 저 애를 푸대접한다는 건 견딜 수가 없어요 — 그렇지, 집?\"\n\n집은 제 주인 곁에 더 바짝 파고들어, 나른하게 그녀의 손을 핥았다.\n\n\"너 그렇게 늙진 않았지, 집, 그렇지? 벌써 주인을 두고 떠나 버릴 만큼은 아니지?\" 도라가 말했다. \"우리 조금 더 오래 서로 동무가 되어 줄 수 있겠지!\"\n\n내 어여쁜 도라! 이튿날 일요일에 그녀가 저녁 식사 자리에 내려와, 그 늙은 트래들스를 (그는 일요일마다 늘 우리와 함께 식사했다) 보고 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 우리는 그녀가 며칠만 지나면 '예전처럼 뛰어다니게' 되리라 여겼다. 그러나 며칠만 더 기다려 보라고들 했고, 그러고는 또 며칠만 더 기다려 보라고들 했다. 그래도 그녀는 뛰지도 걷지도 못했다. 그녀는 무척 예뻐 보였고, 무척 명랑했다. 그러나 집의 둘레를 돌며 춤출 때면 그토록 날래던 그 자그마한 두 발은, 이제 둔하게 멈춰 있었다.\n\n나는 아침마다 그녀를 안아 아래층으로 데리고 내려가고, 밤마다 안아 위층으로 데리고 올라가기 시작했다. 그러는 동안 그녀는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고는, 내가 무슨 내기라도 걸고 하는 양 웃어 댔다. 집은 우리 둘레를 짖으며 깡충깡충 뛰다가 앞장서 나아가서는, 우리가 따라오는지 보려고 층계참에서 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 뒤를 돌아보곤 했다. 고모님은, 더없이 훌륭하고 더없이 쾌활한 간병인답게, 숄과 베개를 한 무더기 움직이며 우리 뒤를 터덜터덜 따라왔다. 딕 씨는 촛불 드는 자리를 이 세상 그 누구에게도 결코 내주려 하지 않았다. 트래들스는 곧잘 계단 아래에 서서 그 광경을 지켜보며, 도라가 세상에서 제일 사랑하는 아가씨에게 보내는 장난스러운 전갈을 맡아 주곤 했다. 우리는 그것을 자못 흥겨운 행렬로 만들었고, 그 가운데서 나의 어린 아내가 가장 흥겨운 사람이었다.\n\n그러나 이따금, 그녀를 안아 올리면서 두 팔에 안긴 그녀가 더 가벼워졌다는 것을 느낄 때면, 죽은 듯한 텅 빈 느낌이 나를 덮쳐 왔다. 마치 아직 보이지 않는 어떤 얼어붙은 땅에 다가가고 있어, 그것이 내 삶을 마비시키는 것만 같았다. 나는 그 느낌을 어떤 이름으로 부르거나 나 자신과 마주 보고 따져 보기를 애써 피했다. 그러던 어느 날 밤, 그 느낌이 몹시도 강하게 나를 짓누르고, 고모님이 \"잘 자라, 우리 작은 꽃송이\"라는 작별 인사를 남기고 그녀 곁을 떠났을 때, 나는 홀로 책상 앞에 앉아 생각해 보려 했다. 아아, 그것은 얼마나 불길한 이름이었던가, 그리고 그 꽃송이는 가지에 핀 채로 어찌 그리도 시들어 갔던가!\n\n# CHAPTER 49. I AM INVOLVED IN MYSTERY\n\n내가 어느 날 아침 우편으로 받은 편지는 다음과 같았는데, 캔터베리에서 부친 것으로, 박사회관에 있는 나에게로 온 것이었다. 나는 다소 놀라며 이 편지를 읽었다.\n\n\"친애하는 선생께,\n저 개인의 힘으로는 어찌할 수 없는 사정들이, 상당한 세월에 걸쳐, 그 친밀함을 끊어 놓았으니, 이 친밀함이란 직무에 매인 와중에 저에게 허락된 한정된 기회 가운데, 기억의 프리즘 빛깔로 물든 지난날의 정경과 사건들을 돌이켜 볼 적마다 저에게 늘 안겨 주었던 것이요, 앞으로도 늘 안겨 줄 수밖에 없는 것으로, 결코 흔치 않은 종류의 흐뭇한 정서를 일으켜 주는 것입니다. 친애하는 선생, 이 사실에다가, 선생의 재능이 선생을 끌어올려 놓은 그 빼어난 지위까지 더해지고 보니, 저는 감히 제 젊은 시절의 벗을 코퍼필드라는 그 정겨운 호칭으로 부르고자 하는 자유를 함부로 넘보지 못하게 됩니다! 제가 외람되이 입에 올리는 그 이름이, 우리 집안의 문서고 가운데 (저는 우리 옛 하숙인들과 관련하여 미코버 부인이 보존하고 있는 기록을 가리키는 것입니다) 애정에 다다른 개인적 존경의 정과 더불어 언제까지나 소중히 간직되리라는 것을 아는 것만으로 충분합니다.\n\n자신의 본래 과오와, 상서롭지 못한 사건들의 우연한 결합으로 말미암아, 좌초한 배(이토록 바다 냄새 짙은 명칭을 제 몸에 갖다 붙이는 것을 허락해 주신다면 말입니다)와 같은 처지에 놓인 한 사람이, 지금 펜을 들어 선생께 글을 올리거니와—거듭 말씀드리거니와, 이런 처지에 놓인 사람으로서, 찬사의 언어나 축하의 언어를 늘어놓는 것은 가당치 않은 일입니다. 그런 것은 더 유능하고 더 정결한 손에 맡기겠습니다.\n\n선생의 더 중요한 일과가 혹시라도 이 못난 글자들을 여기까지 더듬어 내려오는 것을 허락한다면—이는 사정이 어찌 되느냐에 따라 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠습니다만—선생은 자연스레, 그렇다면 대체 무슨 목적으로 제가 이 글월을 적고 있는지 물으실 것입니다. 그 물음이 지당하다는 데에 제가 온전히 따른다는 것을 말씀드리며, 그것을 풀어 나가겠습니다. 다만 그것이 금전과 관련된 목적은 아니라는 것을 먼저 밝혀 둡니다.\n\n제 안에 혹시라도 존재할지 모르는, 벼락을 휘두르거나 집어삼키며 복수하는 화염을 어느 방향으로든 향하게 할 잠재된 능력을 더 직접적으로 언급하지는 않더라도, 지나가는 말로 이 정도는 말씀드리는 것이 허락되겠지요. 저의 가장 빛나던 환상은 영영 흩어져 버렸고—저의 평화는 산산이 깨졌으며 즐길 수 있는 힘은 무너졌고—저의 심장은 더는 제자리에 있지 않으며—저는 더 이상 동포 앞에서 떳떳이 허리를 펴고 걷지 못한다는 것입니다. 꽃 속에 좀이 들었습니다. 잔은 가장자리까지 쓰디씁니다. 벌레가 제 일을 하고 있으니, 머지않아 그 제물을 처리해 버릴 것입니다. 빠르면 빠를수록 좋겠지요. 그러나 옆길로 새지는 않겠습니다.\n미코버 부인의 영향력으로도—그것이 여인이자 아내이자 어머니라는 삼중의 역할로 발휘된다 한들—누그러뜨릴 수 없는 곳에 이를 만큼 유난히 고통스러운 정신적 처지에 놓인 저는, 잠시 저 자신에게서 달아나, 사십팔 시간의 말미를 내어, 지난날 즐거움을 누리던 대도시의 어떤 정경들을 다시 찾아보고자 합니다. 가정의 평온과 마음의 안식이 깃든 여러 안식처 가운데서도, 제 발길은 자연스레 킹스벤치 감옥을 향할 것입니다. 모레 저녁 일곱 시 정각에, 민사 절차로 사람을 가두는 그곳의 남쪽 담장 바깥에 제가 (하느님의 뜻이라면) 있으리라는 것을 말씀드림으로써, 이 서신을 통한 전달의 목적은 이루어진 셈입니다.\n\n저는 옛 벗인 코퍼필드 씨께, 또는 옛 벗인 이너템플의 토머스 트래들스 씨께—그분이 아직 살아 계셔서 만날 수 있다면 말입니다만—황송하게도 저를 만나 주시어, (가능한 한) 지난 옛 시절의 관계를 다시 이어 주십사 청할 자격이 제게 있다고는 느끼지 않습니다. 다만 제가 일러 드린 그 시각과 장소에 가면, 다음과 같이 남아 있는, 폐허가 된 자취를 보실 수 있으리라는 한마디를 던져 둘 따름입니다.\n\n'무너진 '탑의 '잔해로 '남아 있는, '윌킨스 미코버.\n\n추신. 위 내용에다, 미코버 부인은 저의 의도를 은밀히 알고 있지 않다는 사실을 덧붙여 두는 것이 좋을 듯합니다.\"\n\n나는 그 편지를 몇 번이고 거듭 읽었다. 미코버 씨 특유의 거창한 문장 솜씨를 충분히 감안하고, 또 그가 가능한 경우든 불가능한 경우든 가리지 않고 책상에 앉아 긴 편지를 써 내려가며 느끼는 그 유별난 흥취를 헤아려 보더라도, 나는 여전히 이 빙빙 둘러대는 글의 밑바닥에 뭔가 중요한 것이 숨어 있다고 믿었다. 나는 편지를 내려놓고 곰곰이 생각해 보았다가, 다시 집어 들어 한 번 더 읽었다. 그렇게 여전히 그 뜻을 좇고 있던 참에, 내가 한창 어쩔 줄 몰라 하고 있을 때 트래들스가 나를 찾아왔다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 내가 말했다. \"자네를 만나서 이보다 더 반가운 적이 없었네. 정말이지 더없이 알맞은 때에 자네의 그 차분한 판단을 빌려주러 와 주었군. 트래들스, 내가 미코버 씨한테서 아주 묘한 편지를 한 통 받았다네.\"\n\n\"설마?\" 트래들스가 소리쳤다. \"정말인가? 그런데 나도 미코버 부인한테서 한 통 받았다네!\"\n\n그러면서 트래들스는—걸어오느라 얼굴이 발갛게 달아오르고, 운동과 흥분이 한데 어우러진 탓에 머리카락이 마치 유쾌한 유령이라도 본 것처럼 곤두선 채로—제 편지를 꺼내어 나와 맞바꾸었다. 나는 그가 미코버 씨의 편지 한복판으로 빠져드는 것을 지켜보다가, \"'벼락을 휘두르거나, 집어삼키며 복수하는 화염을 어느 방향으로든 향하게 할!' 이런 세상에, 코퍼필드!\"라고 말하며 치켜올리는 그 눈썹에 나도 눈썹을 치켜올려 화답한 다음, 미코버 부인의 서신을 읽어 내려가기 시작했다.\n\n편지는 이러했다.\n\n\"토머스 트래들스 씨께 깊은 안부를 전하오며, 만약 그분께서 지난날 그분과 잘 알고 지내는 행복을 누렸던 한 사람을 아직 기억하신다면, 그분의 한가한 시간 가운데 잠시만 얻을 수 있을까요? 제가 만약 거의 정신이 나가기 직전의 처지가 아니었다면, T. T. 씨의 친절을 이렇게 함부로 침범하지 않았으리라는 것을 분명히 말씀드립니다.\n\n저로서는 입에 올리기조차 가슴 아픈 일이지만, (전에는 그토록 가정적이던) 미코버 씨가 아내와 가족에게서 멀어져 버린 것이, 제가 이 불행한 호소를 트래들스 씨께 올리며 부디 너그러이 헤아려 주십사 매달리게 된 까닭입니다. 미코버 씨의 행동이 어떻게 달라졌는지, 그 거칠어짐과 그 난폭함을 T. 씨께서는 짐작조차 못 하실 것입니다. 그것이 차츰차츰 심해지더니, 이제는 정신이 어긋나 버린 듯한 모습을 띠게 되었습니다. 트래들스 씨께 분명히 말씀드리거니와, 어떤 발작이 일어나지 않고 지나가는 날이 거의 하루도 없습니다. 미코버 씨가 자기 자신을 악마에게 팔아넘겼다고 우겨 대는 소리를 듣는 것이 이제는 익숙해졌다고 말씀드리면, T. 씨께서는 그때 제 심정이 어떠한지 굳이 그려 보라고는 하지 않으실 것입니다. 비밀과 은밀함이 오래도록 그이의 주된 특성이 되어, 오래도록 그 무한하던 신뢰를 밀어내 버렸습니다. 아주 사소한 자극에도—심지어 저녁으로 무얼 드시고 싶으냐고 묻는 것만으로도—그이는 헤어지자는 소망을 입에 올립니다. 어젯밤에는, '레몬스터너'라는 이 고장 사탕을 사겠다고 쌍둥이가 어린애답게 이 펜스만 달라고 졸랐더니, 그이는 쌍둥이에게 굴 까는 칼을 들이댔습니다!\n\n이런 시시콜콜한 사정을 늘어놓는 저를 트래들스 씨께서 부디 참고 견디어 주시기를 간청합니다. 이런 것들이 없으면, T. 씨께서는 정말이지 가슴이 찢어지는 듯한 제 처지를 어렴풋이나마 짐작하기조차 어려우실 테니까요.\n\n이제 제 편지의 본뜻을 감히 T. 씨께 털어놓아도 될까요? 이제 그분의 다정한 헤아림에 저를 내맡겨도 될까요? 아, 그럼요, 저는 그분의 마음씨를 잘 아니까요!\n\n애정에서 비롯된 날카로운 눈은, 더구나 여자의 것이라면, 쉽사리 멀지 않습니다. 미코버 씨는 런던으로 가려 합니다. 오늘 아침 식사 전에 그이가 더 행복했던 시절에 쓰던 작은 갈색 여행 가방에 붙일 행선지 쪽지를 쓰면서 자기 글씨를 애써 가리려 했지만, 부부간의 근심에서 비롯된 매서운 매의 눈은 거기에 'd, o, n'이 또렷이 적혀 있는 것을 알아챘습니다. 그 마차의 웨스트엔드 종착지는 골든크로스입니다. 감히 T. 씨께 제 길 잃은 남편을 만나, 그이를 타일러 주십사 간절히 빌어도 될까요? 감히 T. 씨께 미코버 씨와 그 괴로워하는 가족 사이에 끼어들어 주십사 청해도 될까요? 아, 아니요, 그건 너무 지나친 부탁이겠지요!\n\n만약 코퍼필드 씨가 세상에 이름조차 알려지지 않은 한 사람을 아직 기억하신다면, T. 씨께서 부디 저의 변함없는 안부와 그와 같은 간청을 그분께 전해 주시겠는지요? 어떤 경우든, 그분께서는 이 글을 철저히 비밀로 여기시어, 미코버 씨가 있는 자리에서는 아무리 에둘러서라도 결코 입에 올리지 않으시는 자비를 베풀어 주시리라 믿습니다. 만약 T. 씨께서 이 편지에 답을 보내 주신다면 (그럴 가능성은 거의 없다고 느끼지 않을 수 없습니다만), 캔터베리 우체국, M. E. 앞으로 부치시는 편지가, 극심한 비탄에 잠겨 이렇게 서명하는 사람에게 곧장 부치는 것보다는 덜 고통스러운 결과를 가져올 것입니다.\n\n토머스 트래들스 씨를 공경하는 벗이자 탄원자,\n'에마 미코버.\"\n\n\"그 편지를 어떻게 생각하나?\" 내가 두 번 읽고 나자 트래들스가 나에게 눈길을 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"자넨 저쪽 편지를 어떻게 생각하나?\" 내가 말했다. 그는 여전히 이마를 찌푸린 채 그것을 읽고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이야, 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"이 둘을 합쳐 보면, 미코버 씨 내외가 평소 편지에 담는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 뜻이 있는 것 같아—하지만 그게 뭔지는 모르겠네. 둘 다 진심으로 쓴 거고, 서로 짜고 쓴 게 아닌 건 의심할 여지가 없어. 가엾기도 하지!\" 그는 이제 미코버 부인의 편지를 두고 하는 말이었는데, 우리는 나란히 서서 두 편지를 견주어 보고 있었다. \"어쨌든 부인에게 답장을 써서, 우리가 틀림없이 미코버 씨를 만나겠다고 알려 주는 게 자비로운 일이겠지.\"\n\n나는 더욱 선뜻 이 말에 동의했는데, 이제 와서 부인의 지난번 편지를 다소 가볍게 넘겨 버린 것이 스스로 부끄러웠기 때문이다. 앞서 제때 말했듯이 그 편지는 그때 나에게 적잖이 생각거리를 던져 주었으나, 내 일에 골몰해 있던 데다, 그 집안에 대한 경험과, 그 뒤로 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 탓에, 차츰 그 일을 마음에서 떨쳐 버리고 만 것이었다. 나는 미코버 씨네를 자주 떠올리긴 했지만, 주로 그들이 캔터베리에서 어떤 '금전적 채무'를 쌓아 가고 있을까 궁금해하거나, 미코버 씨가 유라이아 히프의 서기가 되었을 때 나를 얼마나 어색하게 대했는지를 되새기는 정도였다.\n\n어쨌든 나는 이제 미코버 부인에게 위로의 편지를 우리 두 사람 이름으로 써서, 둘 다 서명했다. 그것을 부치러 시내로 걸어 들어가면서, 트래들스와 나는 오랫동안 이야기를 나누고 여러 가지 짐작을 늘어놓았는데, 여기서 굳이 다시 옮길 필요는 없겠다. 오후에는 고모님까지 의논에 끌어들였지만, 우리가 내린 단 하나의 확실한 결론은 미코버 씨와의 약속을 아주 정확하게 지키자는 것뿐이었다.\n\n우리는 약속한 장소에 정해진 시각보다 십오 분이나 일찍 나타났는데도, 미코버 씨가 이미 그곳에 와 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 팔짱을 끼고 담장을 마주한 채 서서, 그 꼭대기의 쇠창살을 바라보고 있었는데, 마치 그것이 젊은 시절 자기에게 그늘을 드리워 주던 나무들의 얽힌 가지이기라도 한 양 감상에 젖은 표정이었다.\n\n우리가 다가가 인사를 건네자, 그의 태도는 예전보다 어딘가 더 어수선하고, 어딘가 덜 점잖았다. 그는 이번 나들이를 위해 법률가다운 검은 정장을 벗어 두고, 옛날의 그 프록코트와 꼭 끼는 바지를 입고 있었으나, 옛 풍채까지 완전히 갖춘 것은 아니었다. 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안 그는 차츰 그 풍채를 더 많이 되찾아 갔지만, 그의 외알 안경조차 예전만큼 편안하게 매달려 있지 못한 듯했고, 셔츠 깃은 여전히 그 위풍당당한 크기였으되 어쩐지 풀이 죽어 늘어져 있었다.\n\n\"두 분!\" 처음 인사를 주고받은 뒤 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"두 분은 어려울 때 찾아 주신 벗이요, 참된 벗입니다. 실제로 존재하는 코퍼필드 부인과, 장차 존재할 트래들스 부인의 신체적 안녕에 관하여 안부를 여쭙는 것을 허락해 주시지요—다시 말해, 제 벗 트래들스 씨가 기쁠 때나 슬플 때나 함께할 그 연모의 대상과 아직 맺어지지 않았다는 것을 전제로 말입니다만.\"\n\n우리는 그의 정중함에 사례하고 적당한 대답을 했다. 그러자 그는 우리의 시선을 담장 쪽으로 돌리며, \"단언컨대, 두 분,\" 하고 운을 떼기 시작했는데, 내가 그 격식 차린 말투에 이의를 제기하며, 부디 옛날처럼 우리에게 말씀해 주십사 청했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 군,\" 그가 내 손을 꼭 쥐며 대답했다. \"자네의 그 따뜻함에 나는 압도되네. 한때 인간이라 불리던 사원의 부서진 한 조각을—이렇게 표현하는 것이 허락된다면 말이네만—이토록 맞아 주는 것은, 우리 공통의 본성에 명예가 되는 마음씨를 일러 주는 것일세. 내가 막 말하려던 것은, 내 삶에서 가장 행복했던 시간 가운데 얼마가 덧없이 흘러갔던 그 고요한 자리를 다시 보고 있다는 것이었네.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 미코버 부인 덕분에 그렇게 행복했겠지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"부인은 잘 지내시는지요?\"\n\n\"고맙네.\" 이 말에 얼굴이 흐려지며 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"그저 그렇다네. 그리고 여기가 말일세,\" 미코버 씨가 슬프게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"바로 벤치 감옥이라네! 여러 해가 돌고 도는 가운데 처음으로, 금전적 채무라는 그 짓누르는 압박이 날마다 통로를 비키지 않으려 들며 졸라 대는 목소리들로 떠들썩하게 알려지지 않았던 곳이지. 어떤 채권자가 매달려 두드릴 문고리도 없었고, 사람을 직접 붙들어 절차를 밟을 필요도 없었으며, 수감자들은 그저 문간에 거두어 들여졌을 뿐인 곳이라네! 두 분,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"저 벽돌 건물 꼭대기의 쇠창살 그림자가 산책로의 자갈밭 위에 드리워졌을 때, 나는 내 아이들이 그 복잡한 무늬의 미로를 누비며 어두운 자국을 피해 다니는 것을 보았다네. 나는 이곳의 돌 하나하나에 다 익숙했지. 내가 약한 모습을 보이더라도, 두 분은 나를 어떻게 너그러이 봐주어야 할지 아실 걸세.\"\n\n\"그 시절 이후로 우리 모두 인생에서 앞으로 나아갔지요, 미코버 씨.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 군,\" 미코버 씨가 쓰디쓰게 대답했다. \"내가 저 안식처의 수감자였을 적에는, 동포의 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 볼 수 있었고, 그자가 나를 욕보이면 그 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박을 수도 있었네. 동포와 나는 이제 더 이상 그런 영광스러운 사이가 아닐세!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 풀이 죽은 모습으로 그 건물에서 돌아서더니, 한쪽으로는 내가 내민 팔을, 다른 한쪽으로는 트래들스가 내민 팔을 받아 끼고, 우리 둘 사이에 끼여 걸어갔다.\n\n\"무덤으로 가는 길에는 말일세,\" 미코버 씨가 어깨 너머로 애틋하게 돌아보며 말했다. \"그런 소망을 품는 것이 불경한 일만 아니라면, 차라리 결코 지나오지 않았더라면 싶은 이정표들이 더러 있다네. 내 파란만장한 인생에서 저 벤치 감옥이 바로 그런 것이지.\"\n\n\"아, 기운이 많이 가라앉으셨군요, 미코버 씨.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다네, 선생.\" 미코버 씨가 끼어들며 말했다.\n\n\"바라건대,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"법이 싫어지셔서 그런 건 아니길 바랍니다—아시다시피 저 자신도 법률가니까요.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"우리 친구 히프는 어떻게 지냅니까, 미코버 씨?\" 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 내가 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 군,\" 미코버 씨가 잔뜩 흥분한 상태로 터져 나오며 새파랗게 질려 대답했다. \"자네가 내 고용주를 자네의 친구로 여겨 안부를 묻는 거라면, 나는 그게 안타깝네. 그를 나의 친구로 여겨 묻는 거라면, 나는 거기에 비웃음을 흘릴 뿐일세. 어떤 자격으로 내 고용주의 안부를 묻든 간에, 자네에게 무례가 되지 않기를 바라며, 내 대답은 이것으로 그치겠네—그자의 건강 상태가 어떠하든, 그자의 몰골은 여우 같다는 것이지. 악마 같다고는 차마 말하지 않더라도 말일세. 사사로운 한 개인으로서, 나는 직무상의 자격으로 나를 절망의 끝자락까지 몰아붙인 이 화제를 더 이상 좇지 않기를 자네가 허락해 주리라 믿네.\"\n\n나는 그를 이토록 들끓게 만든 화제를 무심코 건드린 것을 미안하다고 했다. \"여쭤도 될까요,\" 내가 말했다. \"같은 실수를 되풀이할 위험 없이 말입니다만, 제 옛 친구분이신 위크필드 씨와 위크필드 양은 어떻게 지내시는지요?\"\n\n\"위크필드 양은,\" 이번에는 얼굴이 벌게지며 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"늘 그렇듯이, 본보기이자 빛나는 귀감일세. 코퍼필드 군, 그 아가씨는 이 비참한 삶 속에서 단 하나뿐인 별빛 같은 자리라네. 그 젊은 아가씨에 대한 나의 존경과, 그 인품에 대한 나의 흠모와, 그 사랑과 진실과 선량함에 대한 나의 헌신이라니!—나를,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"어디 골목으로 좀 데려가 주게, 정말이지 지금 내 심정으로는 도저히 이걸 감당할 수가 없네!\"\n\n우리는 그를 좁은 골목으로 데리고 들어갔고, 거기서 그는 주머니 손수건을 꺼내 들고 담장에 등을 기댄 채 섰다. 만약 내가 트래들스만큼이나 심각한 표정으로 그를 바라보았다면, 그는 우리와 함께 있는 것이 조금도 기운 나는 일이 아니라고 여겼으리라.\n\n\"이것이 내 운명일세,\" 미코버 씨가 진심으로 흐느끼며 말했는데, 그러면서도 어딘가 점잖게 처신한다는 옛 표정의 그림자를 여전히 띠고 있었다. \"두 분, 우리 본성의 더 고운 감정들이 도리어 나에게 책망이 되어 버린 것이 내 운명이라네. 위크필드 양에 대한 나의 경의가, 내 가슴속에서는 화살 떼가 되어 박힌다네. 부디 괜찮다면, 나를 그냥 내버려 두고 떠도는 부랑자처럼 이 땅을 떠돌게 해 주게. 벌레가 머지않아 내 일을 후딱 끝내 줄 테니.\"\n\n우리는 이 절절한 호소에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 그가 주머니 손수건을 도로 집어넣고, 셔츠 깃을 추켜올리고, 혹시 그를 지켜보고 있을지 모를 주변 사람들을 속이려고 모자를 잔뜩 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓴 채 콧노래를 흥얼거릴 때까지 곁에 서 있었다. 그러고 나서 나는—그를 아직 놓쳐 버리면 무엇을 잃게 될지 몰랐기에—그가 하이게이트까지 마차를 타고 와 준다면, 내 고모님께 그를 소개해 드리는 것이 나로서는 큰 기쁨이겠고, 잠자리도 마련되어 있다고 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"미코버 씨, 직접 펀치를 한 잔 타 주시고,\" 내가 말했다. \"마음에 담고 계신 것이 무엇이든 더 즐거운 추억거리 속에서 잊어버리시지요.\"\n\n\"아니면, 벗들에게 무언가를 털어놓는 편이 마음을 더 가볍게 해 드릴 것 같으면, 그것을 저희에게 말씀해 주셔도 됩니다, 미코버 씨.\" 트래들스가 신중하게 말했다.\n\n\"두 분,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"마음대로 나를 다루시게! 나는 깊은 바다 위에 떠 있는 한 가닥 지푸라기요, 코끼리들에게 사방으로 내던져지는 신세라네—실례했네, 원소들이라고 했어야 하는데 말이지.\"\n\n우리는 다시 팔짱을 끼고 걸어갔고, 마침 막 떠나려는 마차를 발견했으며, 가는 길에 아무런 어려움도 겪지 않고 하이게이트에 다다랐다. 나는 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 하는 것이 가장 나을지 마음이 몹시 불안하고 갈피를 잡지 못했는데—트래들스도 보아하니 마찬가지였다. 미코버 씨는 대체로 깊은 우울 속에 잠겨 있었다. 그는 이따금 기운을 차려 보려 애쓰며 노래 한 자락의 끝부분을 흥얼거리기도 했으나, 모자를 잔뜩 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓰고 셔츠 깃을 눈까지 추켜올린 우스꽝스러운 꼴 탓에, 다시 깊은 우수에 빠져드는 그 모습은 더욱 사무치게 다가올 뿐이었다.\n\n우리는 도라가 몸이 좋지 않은 탓에, 내 집이 아니라 고모님 댁으로 갔다. 고모님은 사람을 보내 부르자 모습을 드러내, 미코버 씨를 더없이 정중하고 따뜻하게 맞아 주었다. 미코버 씨는 고모님의 손등에 입을 맞추고는 창가로 물러나, 주머니 손수건을 꺼내 들고 속으로 자기 자신과 한바탕 씨름을 벌였다.\n\n딕 씨도 집에 있었다. 그는 천성이 워낙에 마음이 편치 않아 보이는 사람이라면 누구에게나 더없이 가엾어하는 데다, 그런 사람을 워낙 잽싸게 알아채는 터라, 오 분 사이에 적어도 대여섯 번은 미코버 씨와 악수를 했다. 곤경에 빠진 미코버 씨에게는, 낯선 사람이 보여 주는 이런 따뜻함이 어찌나 사무치게 다가왔던지, 그는 악수를 할 때마다 그저 \"선생, 저를 압도하시는군요!\" 하고 말할 따름이었다. 그 말에 딕 씨는 어찌나 흐뭇했던지, 전보다 더 힘차게 또다시 악수를 해 댔다.\n\n\"이 신사분의 다정함이 말입니다,\" 미코버 씨가 고모님께 말했다. \"부인, 우리 좀 더 거친 국민적 운동 경기의 어휘에서 비유 하나를 따오는 것을 허락해 주신다면—저를 그야말로 때려눕히는군요. 복잡하게 뒤엉킨 곤혹과 불안의 짐과 씨름하고 있는 사람에게, 이런 환대는 정말이지 견디기 힘든 시련입니다.\"\n\n\"내 친구 딕 씨는,\" 고모님이 자랑스럽게 대답했다. \"여느 사람이 아니랍니다.\"\n\n\"그 점은 저도 확신합니다.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"선생!\" 딕 씨가 또다시 그와 악수를 하고 있었기 때문이다. \"선생의 그 따뜻함을 저는 깊이 느낍니다!\"\n\n\"몸은 좀 어떠십니까?\" 딕 씨가 근심스러운 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"그저 그렇습니다, 선생.\" 미코버 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다.\n\n\"기운을 내셔야 합니다.\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 되도록 편안하게 지내셔야지요.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 이 다정한 말과, 딕 씨의 손이 또다시 자기 손안에 들어와 있는 것에 그만 완전히 무너지고 말았다. \"다채로운 인생의 파노라마 속에서,\" 그가 말했다. \"이따금 오아시스를 만나는 것이 제 운명이었습니다만, 지금 이것처럼 이토록 푸르고 이토록 샘솟는 오아시스는 결코 만난 적이 없습니다!\"\n\n다른 때 같았으면 나는 이 말에 재미있어했을 것이다. 그러나 나는 우리 모두가 거북하고 불안하다는 것을 느끼고 있었고, 무언가를 털어놓고 싶어 하는 기색과 아무것도 털어놓지 않으려는 기색 사이에서 갈팡질팡하는 미코버 씨를 어찌나 애가 타게 지켜보았던지, 그야말로 안절부절못하는 지경이었다. 트래들스는 의자 끄트머리에 걸터앉아, 두 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고, 머리카락은 어느 때보다 더 도드라지게 곤두선 채, 바닥과 미코버 씨를 번갈아 빤히 바라볼 뿐, 한마디 끼어들 엄두조차 내지 못했다. 고모님은, 내가 보기에 그 빈틈없는 관찰력을 온통 새 손님에게 쏟고 있었으되, 우리 둘 누구보다도 정신을 더 야무지게 부려, 그를 대화 속에 붙들어 두고는, 그가 좋든 싫든 말을 하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다.\n\n\"미코버 씨, 댁은 제 조카의 아주 오랜 벗이시지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"진작에 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸더라면 좋았을 것을요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"저야말로 좀 더 일찍 부인을 알게 되는 영광을 누렸더라면 싶습니다. 저도 지금 부인께서 보시는 이런 난파선 같은 꼴이 늘 그러했던 것은 아니랍니다.\"\n\n\"미코버 부인과 가족분들도 안녕하시기를 바랍니다, 선생.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n미코버 씨가 고개를 숙였다. \"그들도 잘 지냅니다, 부인,\" 그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 자포자기한 듯 말했다. \"이방인이자 버림받은 자들이 바랄 수 있는 만큼은요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 선생!\" 고모님이 그 특유의 퉁명스러운 투로 소리쳤다. \"대체 무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"제 가족의 생계가 말입니다, 부인,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"저울 위에서 위태롭게 흔들리고 있습니다. 제 고용주가—\"\n\n여기서 미코버 씨는 약 올리듯 말을 뚝 끊더니, 내가 일러 준 대로 그의 앞에 차려 놓은 레몬들을, 펀치를 만들 때 쓰는 다른 온갖 도구들과 함께 껍질을 벗기기 시작했다.\n\n\"고용주가 말이지요, 그래서요.\" 딕 씨가 살며시 일깨워 주려는 듯 그의 팔을 툭 치며 말했다.\n\n\"이보십시오, 선생,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"일깨워 주시니, 고맙습니다.\" 두 사람은 또다시 악수를 했다. \"제 고용주가 말입니다, 부인—히프 씨가—언젠가 저에게 이런 말을 하는 은혜를 베풀었지요. 만약 제가 그와의 계약에 딸린 봉급이라는 보수를 받지 못했더라면, 저는 아마도 시골을 떠도는 약장수가 되어, 칼날을 삼키고 집어삼키는 불을 먹고 있었을 거라고 말입니다. 제가 보기에 사정이 달라질 것 같지도 않으니, 여전히 제 아이들은 몸을 뒤트는 곡예로 입에 풀칠하는 신세로 전락하고, 미코버 부인은 손풍금을 켜서 그 부자연스러운 재주를 거들게 될 가능성이 큽니다.\"\n\n미코버 씨는 칼을 아무렇게나, 그러나 의미심장하게 한 번 휘둘러, 이런 공연들이 자기가 죽고 난 뒤에 벌어지리라 예상된다는 것을 내비치더니, 다시 자포자기한 기색으로 껍질을 벗기기 시작했다.\n\n고모님은 늘 곁에 두는 작은 둥근 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 그를 유심히 바라보았다. 그가 자진해서 할 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않은 어떤 폭로를 함정에 빠뜨려 끌어내는 것을 나는 못마땅하게 여겼지만, 그럼에도 이 대목에서 그를 붙들고 캐물었을 것이다—내가 본, 그가 벌이고 있던 그 괴상한 짓들만 아니었다면 말이다. 그중에서도 가장 눈에 띄는 것을 꼽자면, 레몬 껍질을 주전자에 넣고, 설탕을 양초 심지 자르는 접시에 붓고, 독한 술을 빈 물병에 따르고, 촛대에서 끓는 물을 따르려고 천연덕스럽게 시도하는 것 따위였다. 나는 어떤 고비가 코앞에 닥쳤음을 알아챘고, 과연 그것이 닥쳤다. 그는 자기 재료와 도구를 죄다 와장창 한데 부딪치게 한 뒤, 의자에서 일어나, 주머니 손수건을 꺼내 들고는, 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"코퍼필드 군,\" 미코버 씨가 손수건 뒤에서 말했다. \"이 일은, 무엇보다도, 평온한 마음과 자존심을 필요로 하는 일이라네. 나는 이걸 해낼 수가 없네. 도저히 어림없는 일이야.\"\n\n\"미코버 씨,\" 내가 말했다. \"무슨 일입니까? 부디 속 시원히 말씀해 주세요. 여기는 다 벗들뿐이지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"벗들뿐이라고, 선생!\" 미코버 씨가 되뇌었다. 그러자 그가 그동안 담아 두었던 모든 것이 봇물 터지듯 그에게서 쏟아져 나왔다. \"맙소사, 내 마음 상태가 이 지경이 된 것은, 무엇보다도 바로 내가 벗들 사이에 있기 때문이라네. 무슨 일이냐고, 두 분? 무슨 일이 아니냐고 물어야 하지 않겠나? 악행이 바로 그 일일세. 비열함이 바로 그 일이야. 기만과 사기와 음모가 바로 그 일이지. 그리고 그 흉악한 무더기 전체의 이름이 바로—히프일세!\"\n\n고모님은 손뼉을 쳤고, 우리는 모두 무엇에 홀린 사람들처럼 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"이제 씨름은 끝났네!\" 미코버 씨가 주머니 손수건을 거칠게 휘두르며, 마치 인간의 힘을 넘어서는 난관 속에서 헤엄을 치기라도 하는 양 이따금 두 팔을 활짝 내뻗으며 말했다. \"나는 더 이상 이런 삶을 이어 가지 않겠네. 나는 삶을 견딜 만하게 해 주는 모든 것에서 동떨어진 비참한 존재일세. 나는 그 지옥 같은 악당의 종살이 속에서 금기에 묶여 있었네. 내 아내를 돌려주게, 내 가족을 돌려주게, 지금 내 발에 신긴 장화를 신고 돌아다니는 그 하찮은 놈 대신 미코버를 되돌려 놓아 주게. 그리고 내일 당장 칼을 삼키라 명해 보게, 그러면 나는 해 보이겠네. 입맛까지 다시며 말일세!\"\n\n나는 평생 그토록 펄펄 끓어오르는 사람을 본 적이 없다. 우리가 뭔가 이치에 닿는 데까지 이르도록 그를 진정시키려 애썼지만, 그는 점점 더 뜨거워질 뿐 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"나는 그 누구의 손도 잡지 않겠네.\" 미코버 씨가 숨을 헐떡이고 씩씩거리며, 또 찬물과 싸우는 사람처럼 그렇게 흐느끼며 말했다. \"내가—그—그 가증스러운—뱀—히프를—산산조각으로—날려 버리기 전까지는! 나는 그 누구의 환대도 받지 않겠네—내가—그—저버림받은 악당—히프—위에—베수비오 화산을—폭발시키기—전까지는! 이 지붕 아래에서—먹을 것이라니—특히 펀치라니—그건—나를—목 막히게 할—걸세—내가—먼저—그—끝도 없는 사기꾼이자 거짓말쟁이—히프의—머리통에서—눈알을—후벼 파 버리지—않는 한은! 나는—그—그 누구도 알은체하지 않고—아무 말도 하지 않고—그 어디에도 머물지 않겠네—내가—그 비할 데 없고 죽지도 않을 위선자이자 위증자—히프를—찾아낼 수 없는—티끌로—짓이겨 버리기—전까지는!\"\n\n나는 정말이지 미코버 씨가 그 자리에서 죽어 버리지나 않을까 어느 정도 겁이 났다. 그가 이 횡설수설하는 문장들을 헤치고 나아가는 모양새며, 히프라는 이름 가까이에 이를 때마다 그 이름을 향해 기를 쓰고 나아가, 까무러칠 듯한 상태로 거기에 달려들어, 거의 경탄스럽다 할 만큼의 격렬함으로 그것을 토해 내는 그 꼴은 무시무시했다. 그러나 이제, 그가 김을 펄펄 내뿜으며 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 있어야 할 자리에 있을 리 없는 온갖 빛깔이 다 떠오른 얼굴로 우리를 바라보았을 때, 그리고 끝없이 줄줄이 이어지는 덩어리들이 다급하게 그의 목구멍을 타고 올라와 거기서 이마로 튀어 오르는 듯했을 때, 그는 그야말로 마지막 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 몰골이었다. 나는 그를 도우러 가려 했지만, 그는 나를 손짓으로 물리치며 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"안 되네, 코퍼필드!—아무 말도—그—위크필드 양이—그 천하의 악당—히프가—입힌—상처에서—바로잡힐—때까지는!\" (이 말이 다가오는 것을 느낄 때 그 안에 불어넣어 주던 그 놀라운 기운이 아니었다면, 그는 세 마디도 잇지 못했으리라고 나는 확신한다.) \"온 세상에—그—깨뜨릴 수 없는 비밀로—그—예외 없이—이날로부터 일주일 뒤—그—아침 식사 때—그—모두가 모인 자리에서—고모님까지 포함해서—그리고 더없이 다정한 신사분까지—캔터베리의 그 호텔로 와 주게—그—거기서—미코버 부인과 내가—다 함께 옛 노래를 부르고—그리고—그—그 못 봐줄 악당을—히프를—까발리겠네! 더 할 말도—그—없고—설득도 듣지 않겠네—당장 가야겠네—견딜 수 없네—그—사람들 곁에 있는 것을—그 충성스럽고 파멸할 운명의 배신자—히프의—뒤를 쫓아서!\"\n\n그를 여태껏 버티게 해 준 그 마법의 단어를, 이전의 그 모든 안간힘을 능가하는 기세로 마지막으로 한 번 더 되뇌고는, 미코버 씨는 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 우리를 흥분과 희망과 경탄의 도가니 속에 남겨 둔 채로 말인데, 그 바람에 우리도 그와 별반 다를 바 없는 꼴이 되고 말았다. 그러나 그런 와중에도 편지 쓰기를 향한 그의 열정은 도저히 억누를 수 없을 만큼 강해서, 우리가 아직 흥분과 희망과 경탄의 한가운데에 있는 동안, 그가 들러 적은 다음과 같은 목가적인 쪽지가 이웃 선술집에서 나에게 전해졌다.\n\n'극비이자 친전. \"친애하는 선생께,\n\n저의 아까 그 흥분에 대하여, 선생을 통하여, 선생의 훌륭하신 고모님께 사죄의 말씀을 전할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 청합니다. 오래도록 억눌려 있던 화산이 속에서 부글거리다 터져 나온 것은, 말로 그려 내기보다 마음으로 헤아리기가 더 쉬운 내면의 다툼이 빚어낸 결과였습니다.\n\n이날로부터 일주일 뒤 아침, 캔터베리의 그 대중 접객소에서의 약속을 제가 그런대로 알아들으실 만하게 전했기를 바랍니다. 그곳은 일찍이 미코버 부인과 제가, 트위드강 너머에서 자라난 저 불멸의 세리를 노래한 그 널리 알려진 가락에 선생의 목소리와 우리의 목소리를 한데 모으는 영광을 누렸던 자리이지요.\n\n저로 하여금 비로소 동포를 마주 볼 수 있게 해 줄 그 의무를 다하고, 그 속죄의 행위를 이루고 나면, 저는 더 이상 세상에 알려지지 않을 것입니다. 저는 그저, 만인이 모여드는 그곳에 묻히기만을 바랄 따름입니다. 그곳은,\n\n저마다 좁은 방 안에 영영 누운 채로, 마을의 투박한 옛 조상들이 잠든 곳이니,\n'—다음과 같은 단출한 비명과 함께 말입니다.\n\n'윌킨스 미코버.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 50. Mr. PEGGOTTY’S DREAM COMES TRUE\n\n그러는 사이 몇 달이 흘렀다. 강둑에서 마사를 만난 그날 이후로 말이다. 그 후로 나는 마사를 한 번도 본 적이 없었지만, 마사는 여러 번 패거티 아저씨와 연락을 주고받았다. 그 열성적인 개입에서 얻은 것은 아무것도 없었다. 아저씨의 이야기로 미루어 보아도, 단 한순간이나마 에밀리의 행방을 짚어 줄 실마리를 붙잡았다고는 보이지 않았다. 솔직히 고백하면, 나는 에밀리를 되찾으리란 희망을 차츰 잃기 시작했고, 그 애가 이미 죽었으리라는 생각 속으로 갈수록 깊이 가라앉아 갔다.\n\n그러나 아저씨의 확신은 조금도 흔들리지 않았다. 내가 아는 한 — 그분의 정직한 마음은 나에게 훤히 들여다보였다고 믿는다 — 그분은 에밀리를 찾아내고야 말겠다는 엄숙한 확신 속에서 두 번 다시 흔들리지 않았다. 그분의 인내는 결코 지치는 법이 없었다. 언젠가 그 굳건한 믿음이 단 한 번의 타격으로 산산이 부서지는 날이 오면 그것이 그분에게 얼마나 큰 고통일까 싶어 나는 마음이 떨렸지만, 그 믿음에는 어딘가 신성한 데가 있었고, 그분의 고결한 천성의 가장 맑은 깊은 곳에 닻을 내리고 있음을 가슴 저미게 드러내고 있었다. 그래서 내가 그분에게 품은 존경과 흠모는 날마다 더 높아만 갔다.\n\n그분의 믿음은 그저 바라기만 하고 그 이상은 하지 않는 게으른 믿음이 아니었다. 그분은 평생을 억센 행동으로 살아온 사람이었고, 도움이 필요한 모든 일에서 자기 몫을 성실하게 해내고 스스로를 도와야 한다는 것을 알았다. 옛 배에 불이 켜져 있지 않을지도 모른다는 어쩌다 든 불안 때문에, 그분이 한밤중에 길을 나서 야머스까지 걸어간 적도 있었다. 신문에서 에밀리에게 해당될 법한 무언가를 읽고는 지팡이를 집어 들고 칠팔십 마일이나 되는 길을 떠나는 것도 보았다. 다틀 양이 들려준 이야기를 듣고 난 뒤에는 배를 타고 나폴리까지 갔다가 돌아오기도 했다. 그 모든 여정을 그분은 거칠게, 고생스럽게 치러 냈다. 에밀리를 찾는 날을 위해 돈을 아끼겠다는 마음을 늘 굳게 지켰기 때문이다. 이 길고 긴 추적 내내, 나는 그분이 한탄하는 소리를 들은 적이 없었다. 그분이 지쳤다거나 풀이 죽었다고 말하는 것도 들은 적이 없었다.\n\n도라는 우리가 결혼한 뒤로 그분을 여러 번 만났고, 무척 좋아했다. 지금도 그분의 모습이 눈앞에 선하다. 거친 모자를 손에 쥔 채 도라의 소파 곁에 서 있고, 나의 어린 아내가 푸른 두 눈을 들어 수줍은 경이로움을 담아 그분의 얼굴을 올려다보던 모습 말이다. 저녁 무렵, 황혼 즈음에 그분이 나와 이야기를 나누러 오면, 나는 종종 그분을 꾀어 정원에서 함께 천천히 거닐며 파이프 담배를 피우게 하곤 했다. 그러면 텅 비어 버린 그분의 옛집, 어린 시절 내 눈에 비쳤던 그 집의 아늑한 정취 — 난롯불이 타오르고 바람이 집 둘레에서 신음하던 어느 저녁의 그 풍경이 더없이 생생하게 마음속에 떠올랐다.\n\n어느 저녁, 바로 그 시각에, 그분은 어젯밤 거처에서 나왔을 때 마사가 근처에서 기다리고 있더라고, 그리고 자기를 다시 만나기 전까지는 무슨 일이 있어도 런던을 떠나지 말아 달라고 부탁하더라고 말했다.\n\n\"이유는 말했나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"물어봤지유, 데이비 도련님.\" 그분이 대답했다. \"그치만 그 애는 워낙 말이 없어서, 그저 내 약속만 받고는 가 버립디다.\"\n\n\"언제쯤 다시 만나게 될지는 말했고요?\" 내가 캐물었다.\n\n\"아니유, 데이비 도련님.\" 그분은 손을 얼굴 위로 생각에 잠긴 듯 쓸어내리며 대답했다. \"그것도 물어봤지유. 그치만 그건 (그 애 말로는) 자기도 알 수 없는 거라고 합디다.\"\n\n가는 실에 매달린 희망으로 그분을 부추기는 일을 나는 오래전에 그만두었던 터라, 이 소식에 대해서도 곧 마사를 만나게 되리라 본다는 말 외에는 아무런 토를 달지 않았다. 그것이 내 안에 불러일으킨 갖은 추측은 나 혼자만 간직했고, 그나마도 무척 희미한 것이었다.\n\n그로부터 보름쯤 지난 어느 저녁, 나는 정원을 홀로 거닐고 있었다. 나는 그 저녁을 또렷이 기억한다. 미코버 씨의 그 마음 졸이던 한 주 가운데 둘째 날이었다. 종일 비가 내렸고, 공기에는 축축한 기운이 감돌았다. 나뭇잎은 무성하게 우거진 채 물기를 머금어 묵직했다. 그러나 비는 그쳤고, 하늘은 여전히 어두웠으며, 희망에 찬 새들은 명랑하게 지저귀고 있었다. 내가 정원을 이리저리 거니는 동안 황혼이 내 둘레로 내려앉기 시작하자 새들의 작은 노랫소리도 잦아들었다. 그리고 시골의 그런 저녁에만 깃드는 그 독특한 고요가 사방을 채웠다. 이따금 가지에서 물방울이 똑똑 떨어지는 것 말고는, 가장 여린 나무들조차 미동도 없었다.\n\n우리 오두막 옆으로는 격자 울타리와 담쟁이덩굴이 이루는 자그마한 초록의 길이 나 있었는데, 내가 거닐던 정원에서 그 사이로 집 앞 길이 내다보였다. 이런저런 생각에 잠겨 있다가, 마침 나는 그쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다. 그러자 그 너머에 수수한 망토를 두른 사람 하나가 서 있는 것이 보였다. 그 사람은 나를 향해 간절히 몸을 기울이며 손짓하고 있었다.\n\n\"마사!\" 내가 그쪽으로 다가가며 말했다.\n\n\"저랑 같이 가 주실 수 있어요?\" 마사가 흥분한 듯 떨리는 목소리로 속삭이듯 물었다. \"아저씨 댁에 갔는데 집에 안 계셨어요. 어디로 오시면 되는지 제 손으로 적어서 탁자에 두고 왔어요. 곧 돌아오신다더군요. 아저씨께 전할 소식이 있어요. 지금 바로 가 주실 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n내 대답은, 곧장 대문 밖으로 나서는 것이었다. 마사는 손으로 황급히 몸짓을 했는데, 마치 참아 달라고, 조용히 해 달라고 사정하는 듯했다. 그러고는 런던 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 차림새로 보아 거기서부터 부지런히 걸어온 모양이었다.\n\n나는 그곳이 우리가 갈 곳이냐고 물었다. 마사가 조금 전과 똑같이 황급한 몸짓으로 그렇다고 끄덕이자, 나는 마침 지나가던 빈 마차를 세웠고, 우리는 그 안에 올라탔다. 마부에게 어디로 몰면 되느냐고 묻자, 마사는 대답했다. \"골든 광장 근처 아무 데나요! 그리고 빨리요!\" 그러더니 한쪽 구석으로 움츠러들어, 떨리는 한 손을 얼굴 앞에 대고, 다른 손으로는 아까와 같은 몸짓을 했다. 마치 어떤 목소리도 견딜 수 없다는 듯이.\n\n나는 이제 몹시 마음이 어지러워, 희망과 두려움이 엇갈려 번쩍이는 빛에 눈이 부신 채로, 무슨 설명이라도 듣고 싶어 마사를 바라보았다. 그러나 마사가 조용히 있기를 얼마나 간절히 바라는지를 보았고, 또 이런 때에는 그것이 나 자신의 본능이기도 하다고 느꼈기에, 나는 굳이 그 침묵을 깨려 들지 않았다. 우리는 한마디도 나누지 않은 채 나아갔다. 이따금 마사는 창밖을 흘끗거렸는데, 마치 우리가 더디게 가고 있다고 여기는 듯했지만, 실은 빠르게 가고 있었다. 그것 말고는 처음 모습 그대로였다.\n\n우리는 마사가 말한 광장의 어느 입구에서 내렸다. 나는 마차에게 거기서 기다리라고 일렀는데, 혹시 다시 쓸 일이 있을지 몰라서였다. 마사는 내 팔에 손을 얹고는, 그 동네에 몇 군데 있는 음침한 거리 가운데 한 곳으로 나를 서둘러 데려갔다. 그 거리의 집들은 한때 한 가족씩 들어 살던 번듯한 살림집이었으나, 지금은, 그리고 오래전부터, 방마다 세를 놓는 초라한 셋집으로 영락해 버린 곳이었다. 그 가운데 한 집의 열린 문으로 들어서더니, 마사는 내 팔을 놓고는 공동 계단을 따라 자기를 뒤따르라고 손짓했다. 그 계단은 마치 큰 거리로 흘러드는 샛물줄기 같았다.\n\n집 안은 세든 사람들로 바글바글했다. 우리가 올라가는 동안 방문들이 열리고 사람들이 고개를 내밀었다. 계단에서는 내려오는 다른 사람들과 마주쳤다. 안으로 들어서기 전, 바깥에서 올려다보았을 때 나는 여자들과 아이들이 화분 너머로 창가에 기대어 있는 것을 보았다. 우리가 그들의 호기심을 끈 모양이었는지, 문밖을 내다보던 이들이 대개 그 사람들이었다. 널찍한 판자벽 계단에는 어떤 검은 나무로 만든 묵직한 난간이 달려 있었고, 문 위에는 깎아 새긴 과일과 꽃으로 꾸민 처마 장식이 있었으며, 창가에는 넓은 자리가 마련되어 있었다. 그러나 지난날의 영화를 보여 주는 이 모든 자취는 비참하게 썩고 더럽혀져 있었다. 부패와 습기와 세월이 마룻바닥을 약하게 만들어 놓아, 곳곳이 부실하고 심지어 위태롭기까지 했다. 이 쇠락해 가는 골조에 새 피를 불어넣어 보려는 시도가 있었던 듯, 군데군데 값비싼 옛 목공품을 흔한 전나무 판자로 때워 놓은 것이 눈에 띄었다. 그러나 그것은 마치 몰락한 늙은 귀족과 천민 거지의 결혼 같아서, 어울리지 않는 이 짝의 두 편이 서로에게서 움츠러들고 있었다. 계단의 뒤편 창문 가운데 몇몇은 어둡게 가려졌거나 아예 막혀 있었다. 남아 있는 창에는 유리라곤 거의 없었다. 그리고 탁한 공기가 늘 들어오기만 하고 나가지는 못하는 듯한 그 부스러진 창틀 사이로, 나는 또 다른 유리 없는 창들 너머 비슷한 처지의 다른 집들을 보았고, 어지러운 눈으로 비참한 안마당을 내려다보았다. 그곳은 이 저택의 공동 쓰레기 더미였다.\n\n우리는 집 꼭대기 층까지 올라갔다. 도중에 두세 번, 나는 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 우리 앞서 올라가는 어떤 여자의 치맛자락을 본 듯했다. 우리와 지붕 사이의 마지막 계단을 오르려고 몸을 돌렸을 때, 우리는 그 사람이 어느 문 앞에서 잠시 멈춰 서 있는 모습을 온전히 보게 되었다. 이윽고 그 사람은 손잡이를 돌리고 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"이게 뭐죠!\" 마사가 속삭이듯 말했다. \"저 여자가 제 방으로 들어갔어요. 저는 모르는 사람인데!\"\n\n나는 그 여자를 알았다. 나는 놀라움 속에서 그 여자를 알아보았다. 다틀 양이었다.\n\n나는 짤막한 몇 마디로, 전에 본 적 있는 부인이라는 뜻의 말을 안내자에게 건넸다. 그 말이 채 끝나기도 전에, 우리는 그 방 안에서 다틀 양의 목소리를 들었다. 다만 우리가 선 자리에서는 무슨 말을 하는지까지는 들리지 않았다. 마사는 놀란 얼굴로 아까의 그 몸짓을 되풀이하며, 나를 살며시 계단 위로 이끌었다. 그러고는 자물쇠가 없는 듯한 작은 뒷문을 손끝으로 밀어 열고, 천장이 낮게 비스듬히 기운, 벽장이나 다름없는 작고 텅 빈 다락방으로 나를 데려갔다. 이 방과, 마사가 자기 방이라고 한 그 방 사이에는 자그마한 통하는 문이 하나 있었는데, 반쯤 열린 채였다. 우리는 여기서 멈췄다. 올라오느라 숨이 가빴다. 마사는 내 입술에 손을 가볍게 댔다. 건너편 방에서 내가 볼 수 있는 것이라곤, 방이 제법 넓다는 것, 침대가 하나 있다는 것, 그리고 벽에 흔한 배 그림 몇 점이 걸려 있다는 것뿐이었다. 다틀 양도, 그녀가 말을 건네는 상대도 나는 볼 수 없었다. 내 동행은 더더욱 볼 수 없었으니, 자리는 내 쪽이 가장 나았던 것이다. 한동안 죽은 듯한 침묵이 흘렀다. 마사는 한 손을 내 입술에 댄 채, 다른 손을 들어 귀 기울이는 자세를 취했다.\n\n\"그 애가 집에 없든 말든 나에겐 별일 아니지.\" 로사 다틀이 거만하게 말했다. \"난 그 애 따위는 모르니까. 내가 보러 온 건 너야.\"\n\n\"저요?\" 부드러운 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n그 목소리가 들리는 순간, 전율이 내 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다. 에밀리의 목소리였던 것이다!\n\n\"그래.\" 다틀 양이 받았다. \"널 보러 왔어. 뭐? 그렇게 많은 일을 저지른 그 낯짝이 부끄럽지도 않은 모양이지?\"\n\n그 말투에 담긴 단호하고 가차 없는 증오, 그 차갑고 매서운 날카로움, 그리고 억눌린 분노가, 마치 환한 빛 속에 선 그녀를 직접 본 것처럼 내 눈앞에 그녀를 그려 냈다. 나는 번뜩이는 검은 두 눈과 격정에 야윈 몸을 보았다. 그리고 그 흉터를, 입술을 가르며 지나가는 하얀 자국을 보았다. 그녀가 말할 때마다 그것이 떨리고 꿈틀거렸다.\n\n\"난 보러 왔어.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제임스 스티어포스가 노리갯감으로 삼은 계집을. 그놈과 함께 달아나, 제 고향에서 가장 천한 자들의 입방아에 오르내리는 계집을. 제임스 스티어포스 같은 사내들의 뻔뻔하고 요란하고 닳고 닳은 동무를. 난 그런 물건이 어떻게 생겨 먹었는지 알고 싶었어.\"\n\n옷자락 스치는 소리가 났다. 마치 이 야유 세례를 받던 불행한 처녀가 문 쪽으로 달려가려는 듯했고, 말하던 여자가 잽싸게 그 앞을 가로막은 듯했다. 그러고는 잠깐 정적이 이어졌다.\n\n다틀 양이 다시 입을 열었을 때, 그것은 앙다문 잇새로 나오는 소리였고, 발로 바닥을 쾅 구르는 소리가 함께였다.\n\n\"거기 가만있어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 이 집 사람들한테, 온 거리에 네 정체를 떠벌릴 테니까! 날 피해 달아나려 들면, 머리채를 잡아서라도 붙들고, 길바닥의 돌멩이까지 일으켜 너에게 들이댈 거야!\"\n\n겁에 질린 웅얼거림만이 내 귀에 닿은 유일한 대답이었다. 정적이 뒤따랐다. 나는 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 이 만남을 끝내고 싶은 마음이 아무리 간절해도, 나에게는 나설 권리가 없다고 느꼈다. 에밀리를 만나 되찾는 일은 오직 패거티 아저씨만의 몫이었다. 아저씨는 영영 안 오시는 걸까? 나는 애가 타서 생각했다.\n\n\"그래!\" 로사 다틀이 경멸 어린 웃음을 흘리며 말했다. \"이제야 그 애 꼴을 보는구나! 흥, 그 가냘프고 짐짓 꾸민 정숙함과 푹 수그린 그 머리에 넘어가다니, 그놈도 참 못난 위인이었어!\"\n\n\"오, 제발, 저를 좀 봐주세요!\" 에밀리가 외쳤다. \"당신이 누구든, 제 가엾은 사연을 아시잖아요. 제발, 당신도 봐줌을 받고 싶으시거든, 저를 좀 봐주세요!\"\n\n\"내가 봐줌을 받고 싶다고!\" 상대가 사납게 받아쳤다. \"너와 나 사이에 도대체 무슨 공통점이 있다고 생각하는 거지!\"\n\n\"우리가 같은 여자라는 것밖에는요.\" 에밀리가 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그게,\" 로사 다틀이 말했다. \"너처럼 추악한 것이 내세우기엔 너무도 강력한 권리라서, 만약 내 가슴에 너에 대한 멸시와 혐오 말고 다른 감정이 조금이라도 있었다면, 그 권리가 그걸 꽁꽁 얼려 버렸을 거다. 우리가 같은 여자라니! 너는 우리 여자들의 자랑거리로구나!\"\n\n\"이런 대접을 받아 마땅하다는 거 알아요.\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"그렇지만 너무 끔찍해요! 부인, 부인, 제가 얼마나 괴로워했는지, 제가 얼마나 나락에 떨어졌는지 헤아려 주세요! 오, 마사, 돌아와! 오, 집으로, 집으로!\"\n\n다틀 양은 문이 보이는 위치의 의자에 앉아, 마치 에밀리가 자기 앞 바닥에 웅크리고 있기라도 한 듯 아래를 내려다보았다. 이제 그녀가 나와 빛 사이에 자리해 있어서, 나는 비웃듯 말려 올라간 그녀의 입술과, 한곳에 매섭게 고정된 잔인한 두 눈을, 탐욕스러운 승리감을 띤 그 눈을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"내 말 잘 들어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 그 거짓 수작은 네 어수룩한 봉들한테나 써먹어. 눈물 따위로 날 움직일 수 있을 것 같아? 네 웃음으로 날 호릴 수 없는 것만큼이나 어림없는 짓이야, 이 돈에 팔려 온 노예야.\"\n\n\"오, 저에게 자비를 좀 베풀어 주세요!\" 에밀리가 부르짖었다. \"저에게 동정을 좀 보여 주세요. 안 그러면 저는 미쳐서 죽고 말 거예요!\"\n\n\"그건 네 죄에 비하면 대수로운 벌도 아니지.\" 로사 다틀이 말했다. \"네가 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 알기나 해? 네가 쑥대밭으로 만들어 놓은 그 집을 한 번이라도 떠올려 보긴 해?\"\n\n\"오, 그걸 떠올리지 않는 밤이나 낮이 단 한순간이라도 있는 줄 아세요!\" 에밀리가 부르짖었다. 이제 나는 겨우 그 애를 볼 수 있었다. 무릎을 꿇은 채 머리를 뒤로 젖히고, 창백한 얼굴을 위로 향하고, 두 손을 미친 듯이 맞잡아 앞으로 내밀고, 머리카락은 사방으로 흘러내려 있었다. \"깨어 있을 때나 잠들었을 때나, 그게 내 눈앞에 떠오르지 않은 순간이 단 일 분이라도 있었던 줄 아세요? 영영, 영영 그 집에 등을 돌리던 그 잃어버린 날들의 모습 그대로 말이에요! 오, 집으로, 집으로! 오, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아저씨, 제가 선한 길에서 떨어져 나갔을 때 아저씨의 사랑이 저에게 얼마나 큰 고통을 줄지 진작 아셨더라면, 아무리 그 사랑이 깊더라도 저에게 그토록 변함없이 베풀지는 않으셨을 거예요. 오히려 살면서 단 한 번이라도 저에게 화를 내 주셨더라면, 저는 조금이나마 위안을 얻었을 텐데! 저에겐 위안이라곤 없어요, 하나도, 이 세상에 위안이라곤 하나도 없어요, 다들 늘 저를 그토록 아껴 주셨으니까요!\" 그 애는 의자에 앉은 도도한 사람 앞에 엎드려, 그 치맛자락이라도 붙들어 보려고 애원하듯 안간힘을 썼다.\n\n로사 다틀은 놋쇠로 빚은 형상처럼 꿈쩍도 않고 그 애를 내려다보고 앉아 있었다. 그녀의 입술은 꽉 다물려 있었는데, 마치 자기 자신을 단단히 억눌러야 한다는 것을 — 나는 진심으로 믿는 바를 쓰는 것이다 — 알고 있는 듯했다. 안 그러면 그 아름다운 자태를 발로 차 버리고 싶은 충동에 사로잡힐 것이기 때문이었다. 나는 그녀를 똑똑히 보았다. 그녀의 얼굴과 성격의 모든 힘이 그 표정 하나에 짜내어진 듯했다. 아저씨는 영영 안 오시는 걸까?\n\n\"이 땅벌레들의 가련한 허영이라니!\" 그녀는 가슴속에 끓어오르는 분노를 어느 정도 다스려 말을 꺼낼 만큼 자제력을 되찾자 이렇게 말했다. \"네 집이라고! 내가 그런 데 신경이나 쓸 줄 알아? 돈이면, 그것도 넉넉히 치르면 메우지 못할 손해를 네가 그 천한 곳에 끼칠 수 있다고 생각해? 네 집이라고! 너는 네 집의 장사 밑천 가운데 하나였고, 네 집안사람들이 다루던 다른 팔 물건처럼 사고팔린 거야.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 아니에요!\" 에밀리가 부르짖었다. \"저에 대해선 무슨 말이든 하세요. 하지만 제 치욕과 수치를 제가 이미 짊어진 것보다 더, 당신 못지않게 떳떳한 사람들에게 들씌우지는 말아 주세요! 당신이 귀부인이라면 그분들을 존중해 주세요. 저에게 베풀 자비가 없으시더라도요.\"\n\n\"내가 말하는 건,\" 그녀는 이 호소엔 눈길 한 번 줄 가치도 없다는 듯, 에밀리의 손길이 닿아 더럽혀질세라 제 옷자락을 끌어당기며 말했다. \"내가 말하는 건 그자의 집이야. 내가 사는 곳 말이야. 자,\" 그녀는 경멸 어린 웃음을 띠고 손을 뻗으며, 엎드린 처녀를 내려다보며 말했다. \"여기 귀부인 어머니와 신사 아들 사이를 갈라놓을 만한 훌륭한 빌미가 있구나. 너 같은 건 부엌데기로도 들이지 않았을 그 집에 슬픔을 안기고, 분노와 한탄과 비난을 불러일으킨 빌미가. 물가에서 주워 올린 이 더러운 오물 조각이, 한때 귀하게 떠받들어지다가, 다시 제자리로 내동댕이쳐진 거야!\"\n\n\"아니에요! 아니에요!\" 에밀리가 두 손을 맞잡으며 부르짖었다. \"그이가 처음 제 앞에 나타났을 때 — 차라리 그날이 밝지 않았더라면, 그이가 무덤으로 실려 가는 저를 만났더라면! — 저는 당신이나 어느 귀부인 못지않게 정숙하게 자랐고, 당신이나 세상 어느 귀부인이 맞을 수 있는 그 누구 못지않게 착한 사내의 아내가 될 참이었어요. 당신이 그이의 집에 살며 그이를 안다면, 그이가 약하고 허영심 많은 처녀에게 어떤 힘을 휘두를 수 있는지도 아마 아시겠지요. 저는 변명하려는 게 아니에요. 하지만 저는 잘 알아요, 그이도 잘 알아요. 아니면 죽음을 맞이할 때, 마음이 그 일로 괴로워질 때 알게 되겠지요. 그이가 저를 속이려고 온갖 수를 다 썼다는 것을, 그리고 제가 그이를 믿고, 의지하고, 사랑했다는 것을요!\"\n\n로사 다틀은 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 뒷걸음질을 쳤다. 그러더니 뒷걸음질하면서 에밀리를 후려쳤는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 악의에 차고, 격정으로 일그러지고 시커멓게 변했던지, 나는 하마터면 두 사람 사이로 몸을 던질 뻔했다. 겨냥 없이 휘두른 그 손길은 허공을 갈랐을 뿐이었다. 이제 그녀는 헐떡이며 서서, 자기가 드러낼 수 있는 최고의 혐오를 담아 에밀리를 노려보았고, 분노와 멸시로 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있었다. 나는 그런 광경은 일찍이 본 적이 없었고, 앞으로도 두 번 다시 볼 수 없으리라 생각했다.\n\n\"네가 그놈을 사랑한다고? 네가?\" 그녀가 부르짖었다. 움켜쥔 손이, 마치 그 분노의 대상을 찌를 무기 하나만 있으면 된다는 듯 부들부들 떨렸다.\n\n에밀리는 내 시야 밖으로 움츠러들어 있었다. 아무 대답도 없었다.\n\n\"그걸 나한테 지껄여?\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그 수치스러운 입으로? 이런 것들에게 어째서 채찍질을 안 하는 거지? 내가 명령을 내릴 수만 있다면, 이 계집을 채찍으로 때려죽이게 할 텐데.\"\n\n그러고도 남았으리라, 나는 의심치 않는다. 그 사나운 표정이 가시지 않는 한, 나는 그녀에게 고문대조차 맡기지 않았을 것이다. 그녀는 천천히, 아주 천천히 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 마치 에밀리가 신들과 사람들 앞에 내놓인 수치의 구경거리라도 되는 양 손으로 그 애를 가리켰다.\n\n\"저 계집이 사랑을 한다고!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저 썩은 고깃덩이가! 그리고 그놈이 자기를 아꼈었다고 나한테 말하다니. 하, 하! 이런 장사치들이란 거짓말쟁이가 따로 없다니까!\"\n\n그녀의 조롱은 노골적인 분노보다 더 끔찍했다. 둘 중 하나를 고르라면, 나는 차라리 그 분노의 대상이 되는 편이 훨씬 나았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 그 조롱을 풀어 놓은 것은 한순간뿐이었다. 그녀는 그것을 다시 사슬로 묶어 두었고, 그것이 속을 아무리 갈가리 찢어 놓을지언정, 기어이 제 안에 가두어 다스렸다.\n\n\"내가 여기 온 건, 이 순결한 사랑의 샘물 같은 것아,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"처음에 말했듯이, 너 같은 물건이 어떻게 생겨 먹었는지 보러 온 거야. 궁금했거든. 이제 만족스러워. 또 한 가지 일러 둘 게 있어서야. 너는 어서 빨리 그 집으로 찾아가, 너를 기다리는, 네 돈이면 위로받을 그 갸륵한 사람들 틈에 머리를 처박는 게 상책이라는 걸. 돈이 다 떨어지면, 또 믿고, 의지하고, 사랑하면 되는 거 아니겠어, 그치! 난 너를 제 수명을 다 산 부서진 노리개, 빛바래 내던져진 하찮은 번쩍이쯤으로 여겼어. 그런데 알고 보니 네가 진짜 금이고, 어엿한 귀부인이고, 사랑과 신뢰로 가득 찬 싱그러운 마음을 지닌, 모진 대접을 받은 순진한 아이였다니 — 네 꼬락서니가 딱 그렇고, 네 사연하고도 아주 잘 들어맞는구나! — 그러니 한마디 더 해 둬야겠어. 잘 들어. 난 말한 건 반드시 하니까. 듣고 있어, 이 요정 같은 것아? 내가 말한 건, 기어이 할 작정이야!\"\n\n분노가 또 한 번 그녀를 사로잡았지만, 그것은 발작처럼 그녀의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나가고, 그녀를 미소 짓는 채로 남겨 두었다.\n\n\"몸을 숨겨.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그 집이 아니거든 어디든. 손 닿지 않는 어딘가에 있어. 어디 이름 없는 삶 속에 — 아니, 그보다도 이름 없는 죽음 속에 있으면 더 좋고. 네 그 사랑 넘치는 마음이 터져 버리지 않을 거라면, 그걸 잠재울 방도를 아직도 못 찾았다니 놀랍구나! 나는 그런 방도가 더러 있다고 들었어. 쉽게 찾을 수 있을걸.\"\n\n여기서 에밀리의 나직한 울음소리가 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. 그녀는 말을 멈추고, 그것이 음악이라도 되는 양 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"내가 좀 별난 성미인지도 모르지.\" 로사 다틀이 말을 이었다. \"하지만 난 네가 들이쉬는 공기 속에선 숨을 편히 쉴 수가 없어. 메스꺼워. 그러니 난 그 공기를 깨끗이 치울 거야. 너를 말끔히 몰아내 버리겠어. 내일도 네가 여기 살고 있으면, 이 공동 계단에 네 사연과 네 정체를 떠벌릴 테다. 이 집엔 점잖은 여자들이 산다더군. 너 같은 빛이 그들 틈에 섞여 숨어 있다니 안타까운 노릇이지. 여기를 떠나, 이 도시 어디에서든 네 본래의 정체 말고 다른 행세로 몸을 숨기려 든다면 (본래의 정체로라면야 내가 방해 않을 테니 얼마든지 그러렴), 네 은신처를 알게 되는 즉시 똑같이 해 주마. 얼마 전까지 네 손을 잡아 보려 안달하던 어느 신사 양반의 도움을 받고 있으니, 그쯤은 거뜬할 거야.\"\n\n아저씨는 영영, 영영 안 오시는 걸까? 이걸 얼마나 더 견뎌야 하나? 얼마나 더 견딜 수 있을까? \"오, 어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아!\" 가엾은 에밀리가 부르짖었다. 그 목소리는 아무리 모진 마음이라도 움직일 만했다고, 나는 생각했다. 그러나 로사 다틀의 미소에는 누그러짐이라곤 없었다. \"어쩌면, 어쩌면 좋아, 난 어찌해야 하나!\"\n\n\"어찌하다니?\" 상대가 받았다. \"네 그 추억 속에서 행복하게 살려무나! 제임스 스티어포스의 다정함을 되새기는 데 네 평생을 바치든가 — 그자가 너를 제 하인의 아내로 만들어 줬을 거 아니냐, 안 그래? — 아니면 너를 제 선물로 받아 주려던 그 올곧고 갸륵한 위인에게 고마워하는 데 바치든가. 그것도 아니면, 그 자랑스러운 추억들과, 네 미덕에 대한 자부심과, 그 미덕이 사람 꼴을 한 만물의 눈에 너를 끌어올려 준 그 명예로운 자리가 너를 지탱해 주지 못한다면, 그 착한 사내와 결혼해서 그자가 너에게 베풀어 주는 은덕 속에서 행복하렴. 이것도 저것도 안 되거든, 죽어 버려! 그런 죽음을 위해, 그런 절망을 위해 마련된 문간이며 쓰레기 더미가 있으니 — 하나 골라잡아, 천국으로 날아가 버리렴!\"\n\n멀리 계단 위에서 발소리가 들렸다. 나는 그것을 알았다. 나는 확신했다. 아저씨의 발소리였다, 하느님 감사합니다!\n\n그녀는 이 말을 하면서 천천히 문 앞에서 비켜나더니, 내 시야 밖으로 사라졌다.\n\n\"하지만 명심해!\" 그녀가 나가려고 다른 쪽 문을 열며, 천천히 그리고 매섭게 덧붙였다. \"나는, 나에게 있는 이유들과 내가 품은 증오 때문에, 네가 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 아주 물러나거나 그 예쁜 가면을 벗어 버리지 않는 한, 너를 기어이 내쫓기로 작정했다. 이게 내가 하려던 말이야. 그리고 내가 말한 건, 기어이 할 작정이고!\"\n\n계단 위의 발소리가 가까이 — 더 가까이 — 내려가는 그녀를 스쳐 지나 — 방 안으로 와락 들이닥쳤다!\n\n\"아저씨!\"\n\n그 말에 뒤이어 무시무시한 외침이 터져 나왔다. 나는 잠시 멈칫했다가 안을 들여다보았고, 아저씨가 정신을 잃은 그 애의 몸을 두 팔로 받쳐 안고 있는 것을 보았다. 아저씨는 몇 초간 그 얼굴을 들여다보더니, 몸을 숙여 입을 맞추고는 — 오, 얼마나 다정하게! — 손수건을 그 얼굴 위에 덮었다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님,\" 얼굴이 가려지자, 아저씨가 낮고 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"내 꿈이 이뤄진 걸 하늘에 계신 아버지께 감사드립니다! 당신의 뜻대로 나를 내 귀한 것에게로 인도해 주신 걸 진심으로 감사드려유!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 아저씨는 그 애를 두 팔로 들어 안았다. 그리고 가려진 얼굴을 제 가슴에 뉘어 자기 쪽으로 향하게 한 채, 미동도 없이 정신을 잃은 그 애를 안고 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 51. THE BEGINNING OF A LONGER JOURNEY\n\n이튿날 아침 아직 이른 시각에, 나는 정원에서 고모님과 함께 거닐고 있었는데 — 고모님은 사랑하는 도라를 워낙 극진히 돌보느라 요즈음은 그 밖의 운동이라곤 거의 하지 않으셨다 — 그때 패거티 씨가 나와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다는 전갈을 받았다. 내가 대문 쪽으로 걸어가자 그는 정원으로 들어와 중간쯤에서 나를 맞았다. 그러고는, 자기가 깊이 존경하는 고모님을 볼 때면 늘 그러듯이 모자를 벗어 머리를 드러냈다. 나는 간밤에 일어난 일을 죄다 고모님께 말씀드려 둔 터였다. 고모님은 한마디 말도 없이 정겨운 낯빛으로 다가와 그와 악수를 나누고 그의 팔을 토닥였다. 어찌나 뜻이 또렷이 담겼던지, 굳이 입을 열 필요조차 없었다. 패거티 씨는 고모님이 천 마디를 한 것이나 다름없이 그 마음을 똑똑히 헤아렸다.\n\n\"난 이제 안으로 들어가 봐야겠다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"곧 일어날 테니 우리 어린 꽃송이를 보살펴야지.\"\n\n\"제가 여기 와 있다고 그러시는 건 아니지요, 마님?\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"제 정신이 오늘 아침 새둥지 뒤지러 나간 게 아니라면\" — 패거티 씨가 하려던 말은 새둥지 뒤지기, 곧 정신이 딴 데 팔렸다는 뜻이었다 — \"혹시 저 때문에 자리를 비키시려는 거 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"자네가 할 얘기가 있을 텐데, 나의 좋은 벗이여.\" 고모님이 받았다. \"내가 없는 편이 더 편할 거야.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 마님.\" 패거티 씨가 대답했다. \"제 수다를 마다하지 않으신다면, 여기 그냥 계셔 주시는 게 저는 고맙겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 고모님이 짤막하게 너그러운 투로 말했다. \"그렇다면야 당연히 있어야지!\"\n\n그래서 고모님은 패거티 씨의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고, 정원 끝에 있는 잎이 우거진 작은 정자로 함께 걸어갔다. 거기서 고모님은 긴 의자에 앉았고, 나는 그 곁에 앉았다. 패거티 씨를 위한 자리도 있었지만 그는 작은 시골풍 탁자에 손을 짚고 서 있기를 택했다. 그가 입을 떼기 전에 잠시 자기 모자를 내려다보며 서 있는 동안, 나는 그의 억센 손이 얼마나 강한 힘과 굳센 성품을 드러내는지, 또 그 손이 정직한 이마와 쇠처럼 희끗한 머리칼에 얼마나 미덥고 든든한 동무인지를 새삼 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"어젯밤에 제가 그 가엾은 자식을 데려왔습니다.\" 패거티 씨가 우리에게 눈을 들며 이야기를 시작했다. \"오랫동안 그 애를 기다리고 그 애를 위해 채비를 해 둔 제 거처로요. 그 애가 저를 제대로 알아보기까지 몇 시간이나 걸렸습니다. 그러다 알아보고는, 제 발치에 무릎을 꿇고서, 기도라도 올리듯이, 어쩌다 일이 이 지경까지 왔는지를 저한테 들려주었지요. 믿어 주십시오, 집에서 그토록 명랑하게 듣던 그 애 목소리를 다시 듣고 — 또 우리 구주께서 복된 손으로 글을 쓰시던 그 흙먼지 속에 엎드리듯 비참하게 무릎 꿇은 그 애를 보고 — 저는 온통 감사한 마음 한가운데서도 가슴을 찌르는 아픔을 느꼈습니다.\"\n\n그는 왜 그러는지 숨기려는 기색도 없이 소맷자락으로 얼굴을 쓱 훔치고는 목청을 가다듬었다.\n\n\"하지만 그런 마음이 오래가지는 않았습니다. 그 애를 되찾았으니까요. 그저 그 애를 되찾았다는 생각만 하면 그 아픔은 사라졌지요. 사실 제가 지금 왜 이런 얘기까지 꺼내는지 모르겠습니다. 조금 전까지만 해도 제 얘기를 할 마음은 없었거든요. 그런데 너무 자연스레 솟구쳐서, 저도 모르게 그만 거기에 끌려가 버렸네요.\"\n\n\"자넨 자기를 비울 줄 아는 사람이야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그러니 마땅한 보답을 받을 게야.\"\n\n패거티 씨는 나뭇잎 그림자가 얼굴을 가로질러 어른거리는 가운데, 고모님의 좋은 평가에 화답하듯 놀란 듯이 고개를 한 번 숙여 보이더니, 놓았던 이야기의 실마리를 다시 잡았다.\n\n\"제 에밀리가 도망쳐 나온 건,\" 그가 잠깐 매서운 분노에 사로잡혀 말했다. \"데이비 도련님이 본 그 얼룩무늬 뱀 같은 놈한테 갇혀 있던 그 집에서였습니다 — 그 도련님 얘기는 죄다 사실이고, 부디 하느님이 그놈을 벌하시기를! — 그 애는 밤에 도망쳤습니다. 별이 잔뜩 빛나던 어두운 밤이었지요. 그 애는 제정신이 아니었습니다. 옛날 배가 거기 있으리라 믿고 바닷가를 따라 내달리면서, 자기가 지나가니 우리더러 얼굴을 돌리라고 소리쳤다더군요. 자기 울부짖는 소리가 마치 딴사람 목소리처럼 들렸다고요. 뾰족한 돌과 바위에 살갗을 베이면서도, 제 몸이 바위라도 된 양 아무것도 못 느꼈답니다. 그렇게 한참을 달리는데 눈앞엔 불길이 일렁이고 귓속엔 굉음이 울렸다지요. 그러다 갑자기 — 그 애 느낌으로는 그랬다는 겁니다, 아시겠지요 — 비 내리고 바람 부는 날이 밝았고, 보니 자기는 바닷가 돌무더기 밑에 누워 있고, 웬 여인네가 그 고장 말로 자기한테 무슨 일이 그리 크게 잘못된 거냐고 묻고 있더랍니다.\"\n\n그는 자기가 이야기하는 모든 장면을 눈으로 보고 있었다. 말하는 동안 그것이 그의 앞을 어찌나 생생하게 스쳐 지나갔던지, 그 절절한 진정 속에서 그가 묘사하는 광경이 내가 글로 표현할 수 있는 것보다 더 또렷하게 내게 와닿았다. 한참 세월이 흐른 지금 이렇게 글을 쓰면서도, 그때 내가 정말로 그 장면 속에 함께 있었던 게 아닌가 하는 생각을 떨치기 어렵다. 그만큼 그 광경들은 놀라우리만치 생생하게 내게 새겨져 있다.\n\n\"무거워 감기던 에밀리의 두 눈이 그 여인네를 좀 더 또렷이 보게 되자,\" 패거티 씨가 이어 갔다. \"그 애는 자기가 바닷가에서 자주 말을 섞던 사람들 가운데 하나라는 걸 알아봤습니다. 아까도 말씀드렸듯이 그 애가 그 밤에 한참을 달리긴 했지만, 그 전에도 여러 차례 멀리까지 헤매고 다녔거든요. 어떤 때는 걸어서, 어떤 때는 배나 마차로요. 그래서 해안을 따라 수십 리에 걸쳐 그 고장 일대를 다 꿰고 있었답니다. 이 여인네는 젊은 새댁이라 제 자식은 아직 없었지만, 머지않아 하나 낳을 참이었다지요. 부디 그 아이가 평생토록 그 여인에게 기쁨이 되고, 위안이 되고, 자랑이 되기를 하늘에 비나이다! 그 여인이 늙었을 때 자식이 어미를 사랑하고 효성스럽게 모시며, 마지막 가는 길까지 보살펴 드리기를. 이승에서도 저승에서도 그 여인에게 천사가 되어 주기를!\"\n\n\"아멘!\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"그 여인네는 좀 수줍고 풀 죽은 성미라서,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"처음에 에밀리가 아이들하고 얘기를 나눌 때면, 좀 떨어진 데 앉아 물레질이며 무슨 일이며를 하고 있었답니다. 그런데 에밀리가 그 여인네를 눈여겨보고는 다가가 말을 걸었지요. 그 젊은 여인네도 아이들을 무척 좋아하던 터라, 둘은 금세 친해졌습니다. 어찌나 친해졌는지, 에밀리가 그 길로 지날 때면 그 여인네는 늘 에밀리한테 꽃을 주었답니다. 무슨 일이 그리 크게 잘못된 거냐고 이제 물어 온 사람이 바로 이 여인네였습니다. 에밀리가 사정을 말하자, 그 여인네는 — 그 애를 집으로 데려갔습니다. 정말로요. 집으로 데려갔어요.\" 패거티 씨가 얼굴을 감싸며 말했다.\n\n그는 에밀리가 떠나던 그 밤 이후로 내가 보아 온 어떤 일보다도 이 친절한 행동에 더 깊이 마음이 흔들린 듯했다. 고모님과 나는 그를 방해하려 들지 않았다.\n\n\"짐작하시겠지만 그건 자그마한 오두막이었지요.\" 그가 이윽고 말했다. \"그래도 그 여인네는 그 안에 에밀리가 머물 자리를 마련해 줬습니다 — 남편은 바다에 나가 있었거든요 — 그리고 그 일을 비밀로 했고, 가까이 사는 이웃이라야 몇 안 됐지만 그 사람들한테도 입조심을 시켰지요. 에밀리는 열병이 들어 몹시 앓았는데, 저로서는 참 이상한 게 — 학식 있는 분들한테야 그리 이상한 일이 아닐는지도 모르지만 — 그 고장 말이 머릿속에서 싹 사라져 버리고, 제 고향 말밖에 못 하게 됐다는 겁니다. 아무도 못 알아듣는 말이었지요. 그 애는 꿈결처럼 어렴풋이 기억한답니다. 거기 누워 줄곧 제 고향 말로 떠들면서, 옛날 배가 만의 바로 다음 곶 근처에 있으리라 믿고, 사람을 보내 자기가 죽어 간다고 전해 달라고, 단 한마디라도 좋으니 용서한다는 말을 받아다 달라고 빌고 애원했다고요. 거의 내내, 그 애 생각으로는 — 어떤 때는 제가 방금 말씀드린 그놈이 창문 밑에 숨어 자기를 노린다 싶고, 어떤 때는 자기를 이 지경으로 만든 놈이 방 안에 있다 싶었답니다. 그래서 그 착한 젊은 여인네한테 자기를 내치지 말라고 울며 매달리면서도, 동시에 그 여인이 알아듣지 못한다는 걸 알았고, 자기가 끌려가게 될까 봐 두려워했다지요. 눈앞엔 여전히 불길이 일렁이고 귓속엔 굉음이 울렸으며, 오늘도 어제도 내일도 없이, 살면서 있었던 일, 있을 수 있었던 일, 한 번도 없었던 일, 결코 있을 수 없었던 일이 죄다 한꺼번에 들이닥쳐서, 또렷한 것도 반가운 것도 하나 없었답니다. 그런데도 그 애는 그걸 두고 노래하고 웃었다는 거예요! 이게 얼마나 오래갔는지는 모르겠습니다. 그러다 잠이 찾아왔고, 그 잠 속에서, 제 본래 모습보다 몇 곱절 억셌던 그 애가 가장 어린 갓난아이만큼이나 가녀린 상태로 떨어졌답니다.\"\n\n여기서 그는 자기가 들려준 이야기의 두려움에서 한숨 돌리려는 듯 말을 멈추었다. 잠깐 입을 다물고 있다가, 그는 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그 애가 깨어났을 때는 화창한 오후였답니다. 어찌나 고요한지, 밀물도 썰물도 없는 그 푸른 바다가 기슭에 찰랑이는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다지요. 처음엔 자기가 집에서 어느 일요일 아침을 맞은 줄 알았답니다. 그런데 창가에 보이는 포도 덩굴잎이며 그 너머 언덕들이 집이 아니어서, 그게 아니라고 일러 주더래요. 그러다 그 벗이 들어와 침대 곁을 지켜 주었고, 그제야 그 애는 옛날 배가 만의 다음 곶 근처에 있는 게 아니라 멀리 떨어져 있다는 걸 깨달았답니다. 자기가 어디 있는지, 왜 거기 있는지도요. 그러고는 그 착한 젊은 여인네 품에 안겨 울음을 터뜨렸지요. 부디 지금쯤 그 여인의 아기가 그 품에 누워, 예쁜 두 눈으로 어미를 기쁘게 해 주고 있기를!\"\n\n그는 눈물을 쏟지 않고는 에밀리의 이 착한 벗을 입에 올릴 수가 없었다. 참아 보려 했지만 헛수고였다. 그 여인을 축복하려 애쓰다가 그는 또다시 무너지고 말았다.\n\n\"그 덕분에 우리 에밀리가 나아졌습니다.\" 나로서는 함께 마음 아파하지 않고는 차마 볼 수 없는 격정에 잠겼다가 그가 다시 입을 열었다. 고모님으로 말하자면, 진심을 다해 울고 있었다. \"그 덕에 에밀리가 나아졌고, 차차 회복되기 시작했습니다. 하지만 그 고장 말은 머릿속에서 깡그리 사라져 버려서, 그 애는 손짓으로 뜻을 전할 수밖에 없었지요. 그렇게 날이 갈수록 더디지만 분명하게 좋아져 가면서, 흔한 물건들의 이름을 — 난생처음 들어 보는 듯한 이름들을 — 익히려고 애썼답니다. 그러던 어느 저녁, 그 애가 창가에 앉아 바닷가에서 노는 어린 계집아이를 바라보고 있었지요. 그런데 그 아이가 별안간 손을 내밀며, 영어로 옮기면 이런 말을 했답니다. '어부의 딸님, 여기 조개 있어요!' — 아셔야 할 게, 거기서는 처음에 그 고장 풍습대로 에밀리를 '예쁜 아씨'라고 불렀는데, 그 애가 대신 '어부의 딸'이라 불러 달라고 가르쳐 줬거든요. 그 아이가 느닷없이 '어부의 딸님, 여기 조개 있어요!' 하니까, 그제야 에밀리가 그 말을 알아들었고, 울음을 터뜨리며 대꾸했답니다. 그러자 모든 게 한꺼번에 되살아났다는 거예요!\"\n\n\"에밀리가 다시 기운을 차린 뒤,\" 또 한 차례 짧은 침묵 끝에 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그 애는 그 착한 젊은이를 떠나 제 나라로 돌아갈 궁리를 했습니다. 그때쯤 남편이 집에 돌아와 있었지요. 부부가 함께 그 애를 레그혼으로 가는 작은 무역선에 태워 줬고, 거기서 다시 프랑스로 건너가게 했답니다. 그 애한테 돈이 좀 있긴 했지만, 그 사람들이 베푼 것에 견주면 그들이 받은 건 쥐꼬리만큼도 못 됐지요. 그 사람들이 그토록 가난한데도, 저는 차라리 그게 다행이다 싶습니다! 그들이 베푼 것은 좀먹지도 녹슬지도 않고, 도둑이 뚫고 들어와 훔쳐 가지도 못하는 곳에 고이 쌓여 있을 테니까요. 데이비 도련님, 그건 이 세상 어떤 보물보다 오래갈 겁니다.\n\n에밀리는 프랑스에 닿았고, 항구의 어느 여관에서 여행하는 부인들의 시중을 드는 일자리를 얻었습니다. 그런데 거기로 어느 날 그 뱀 같은 놈이 찾아왔지요 — 그놈이 절대 제 곁에 얼씬도 하지 않기를. 안 그러면 제가 무슨 짓을 저지를지 저도 모릅니다! — 그놈이 자기를 보기도 전에 그놈을 본 순간, 그 애는 온갖 두려움과 광기가 되살아나서, 그놈이 내쉬는 숨결 앞에서 도망쳐 버렸습니다. 그렇게 영국으로 건너와 도버에 내렸지요.\n\n언제부터 그 애 가슴이 무너지기 시작했는지는,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"저도 똑똑히는 모르겠습니다. 다만 영국으로 오는 내내 그 애는 그리운 제 집으로 돌아올 작정이었답니다. 영국에 닿자마자 집 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸지요. 그런데 용서받지 못할까 봐 두렵고, 손가락질당할까 봐 두렵고, 우리 중 누군가가 자기 때문에 죽었을까 봐 두렵고, 그 밖에 온갖 게 두려워서, 거의 떠밀리다시피 가던 길에서 발길을 돌리고 말았답니다. '아저씨, 아저씨' 하고 그 애가 저한테 그러더군요. '찢기고 피 흘리는 제 가슴이 그토록 간절히 하고 싶어 하던 일을 할 자격이 없을까 봐, 그게 무엇보다 무서운 두려움이었어요! 밤중에 그 옛날 문턱까지 기어가서 거기에 입 맞추고, 죄 많은 제 얼굴을 거기 묻고, 아침이면 거기서 죽은 채 발견되게 해 달라고 기도로 가슴이 미어지던 바로 그때, 저는 발길을 돌리고 말았어요.'\n\n그 애는,\" 패거티 씨가 두려움에 사로잡힌 속삭임으로 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"런던으로 갔습니다. 그 애가 — 평생 한 번도 본 적 없는 그곳으로 — 혼자서 — 한 푼도 없이 — 어리고 — 그토록 예쁜 채로 — 런던으로 갔습니다. 그 막막한 곳에 발을 디딘 거의 그 순간, 그 애는 (자기 딴엔) 벗을 하나 만났다고 믿었지요. 점잖아 보이는 여자가 다가와, 그 애가 어려서부터 익힌 바느질 얘기며, 그 일감을 넉넉히 구해 주겠다는 얘기며, 하룻밤 묵을 곳 얘기며, 이튿날 저와 집안 식구들에 대해 몰래 알아봐 주겠다는 얘기를 했답니다. 제 자식이,\" 그가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨릴 만큼 벅찬 감사를 담아 소리 높여 말했다. \"제가 말로도 생각으로도 다 담을 수 없는 그 무엇의 벼랑 끝에 서 있을 때 — 마사가, 제 약속을 지킨 마사가, 그 애를 구해 냈습니다.\"\n\n나는 기쁨의 외침을 참을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님!\" 그가 그 억센 손으로 내 손을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"마사 얘기를 저한테 처음 꺼내 주신 게 도련님이었지요. 고맙습니다, 도련님! 마사는 진심이었습니다. 자기가 쓰라리게 겪어 알게 된 덕에, 어디를 지켜봐야 하고 무얼 해야 하는지를 알고 있었지요. 그리고 그대로 해냈습니다. 그 위에 주님이 계셨고요! 마사는 핏기 없이 황급한 얼굴로, 잠든 에밀리에게 다가갔답니다. 그러고는 '죽음보다 더한 데서 일어나, 나랑 같이 가자!' 하고 말했지요. 그 집 사람들이 그 애를 막으려 들었지만, 차라리 바다를 막는 게 빨랐을 겁니다. '내게서 물러서.' 마사가 말했답니다. '나는 파헤쳐진 무덤 곁에서 이 애를 불러내는 유령이야!' 마사는 에밀리한테 제가 그 애를 봤다고, 제가 그 애를 사랑하고 용서한다는 걸 안다고 말해 줬습니다. 그러고는 서둘러 그 애한테 옷을 둘러 입히고, 정신을 잃고 떨고 있는 그 애를 제 팔에 안아 들었지요. 그 사람들이 뭐라고 하든, 귀가 없는 사람처럼 아랑곳하지 않았답니다. 오직 제 자식만 살피며 그 사람들 틈을 걸어 나와, 그 캄캄한 파멸의 구덩이에서 깊은 밤중에 그 애를 무사히 끌어냈습니다!\n\n마사는 에밀리를 보살폈습니다.\" 내 손을 놓고 자기 손을 들썩이는 가슴에 얹으며 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"지쳐 쓰러져, 이따금 헛소리를 하며 누워 있는 우리 에밀리를 이튿날 늦게까지 곁에서 돌봐 줬지요. 그런 다음 저를 찾아 나섰고, 또 도련님을 찾아 나섰습니다. 마사는 자기가 왜 나왔는지를 에밀리한테 말하지 않았답니다. 그 애 가슴이 또 무너져서 어디 숨어 버릴 생각을 할까 봐서요. 그 모진 여자가 어떻게 그 애가 거기 있는 걸 알았는지는 저도 모릅니다. 제가 그토록 말씀드린 그놈이 그들이 거기 들어가는 걸 우연히 본 건지, 아니면 (제 생각엔 이쪽이 더 그럴 법한데) 그 여자한테서 들은 건지, 저는 그리 따져 묻지 않습니다. 제 조카는 되찾았으니까요.\n\n밤새도록,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"에밀리하고 저는 함께 있었습니다. (그 시간을 생각하면) 가슴 찢어지는 눈물 사이로 그 애가 말로 한 건 얼마 안 됩니다. 제 화롯가에서 어엿한 여인으로 자라난 그 애의 그리운 얼굴도 그만큼밖에 못 봤지요. 하지만 밤새도록 그 애 두 팔이 제 목을 감싸고, 그 애 머리가 여기 기대 있었습니다. 그리고 우리는 이제 영영 서로를 믿고 의지할 수 있다는 걸 똑똑히 압니다.\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추었고, 탁자에 얹은 그의 손은 사자라도 굴복시킬 만한 결의를 담은 채 완벽한 고요 속에 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"나에게 한 줄기 빛이 비쳤더랬지, 트롯.\" 고모님이 눈물을 닦으며 말했다. \"내가 네 누이동생 베시 트롯우드의 대모가 되겠다고 마음먹었을 때 말이다. 그 애는 끝내 나를 실망시켰지만. 그런데 그다음으로는, 저 착한 젊은이의 아기의 대모가 되는 일만큼 나를 기쁘게 할 일이 거의 없겠구나!\"\n\n패거티 씨는 고모님의 마음을 알아들었다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였지만, 고모님이 칭찬한 그 여인에 대해서는 차마 말로 한마디도 꺼낼 자신이 없었다. 우리는 모두 입을 다문 채 저마다 제 상념에 잠겨 있었다 (고모님은 눈물을 닦다가, 발작하듯 흐느끼다가, 또 웃음을 터뜨리며 자기를 바보라 부르고 있었다). 그러다 내가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"앞날에 대해서는 마음을 완전히 정하셨겠지요, 나의 좋은 벗이여?\" 내가 패거티 씨에게 말했다. \"굳이 여쭤볼 것도 없겠지만요.\"\n\n\"완전히 정했습니다, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"에밀리한테도 말해 뒀고요. 여기서 멀리 떨어진, 드넓은 나라들이 있지 않습니까. 우리 앞날은 저 바다 건너에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"두 사람이 함께 이민을 가려나 봐요, 고모님.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다!\" 패거티 씨가 희망 어린 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"오스트레일리아에서는 아무도 제 귀한 자식을 손가락질하지 못합니다. 우린 거기서 새 삶을 시작할 겁니다!\"\n\n나는 떠날 시기를 어느 정도 정해 두었느냐고 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 일찍 부두에 나가 봤습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"배편을 알아보려고요. 지금부터 한 여섯 주, 아니면 두 달쯤 뒤에 떠나는 배가 한 척 있더군요 — 오늘 아침에 그 배를 봤습니다 — 올라가 봤고요 — 우린 그 배로 떠날 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"단둘이서요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 데이비 도련님!\" 그가 대답했다. \"보시다시피 제 누이는 도련님하고 도련님 식구들을 그토록 끔찍이 아끼는 데다, 제 나라 생각밖에 할 줄 모르는 사람이라, 데려간다는 게 도무지 도리가 아니지요. 게다가 누이가 맡아 돌봐야 할 사람이 하나 있지 않습니까, 데이비 도련님. 그 사람을 잊어선 안 되지요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 햄!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"보시다시피 제 착한 누이가 그 애 집을 돌봐 주는데, 그 애도 누이를 잘 따른답니다, 마님.\" 고모님이 더 잘 알아들으시도록 패거티 씨가 설명했다. \"그 애가 다른 사람한테는 도무지 입을 떼지 못할 것 같을 때도, 누이한테는 차분한 마음으로 앉아서 이런저런 얘기를 한답니다. 가엾은 녀석!\" 패거티 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그 애한테 남은 게 얼마 없으니, 그나마 가진 그 얼마를 내줄 형편도 못 되지요!\"\n\n\"거미지 부인은요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 정말이지 거미지 부인 일을 두고 제가 어지간히 고민을 했습니다.\" 패거티 씨가 곤혹스러운 낯빛으로 대답했는데, 그 낯빛은 이야기를 이어 갈수록 차츰 풀렸다. \"보시다시피, 거미지 부인이 그 영감 생각에 빠지면, 영 함께 있을 만한 사람이 못 됩니다. 우리끼리 얘긴데요, 데이비 도련님 — 그리고 마님 — 거미지 부인이 훌쩍훌쩍 울기 시작하면,\" — 운다는 뜻의 우리 고향 옛말이다 — \"그 영감을 모르는 사람들 눈에는 짜증 부리는 거로 보이기 십상이지요. 저야 그 영감을 알았고,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그 영감의 좋은 점도 알았으니까 부인을 이해합니다만, 다른 사람들이야 다 그럴 수는 없지요 — 당연히 그럴 수가 없잖습니까!\"\n\n고모님과 나는 둘 다 그렇겠다고 수긍했다.\n\n\"그러니,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"제 누이가 어쩌면 — 꼭 그러리란 건 아니지만, 어쩌면 — 거미지 부인 때문에 이따금 자잘한 골치를 앓을지도 모릅니다. 그래서 거미지 부인을 그들과 한데 묶어 둘 생각은 없고, 부인 혼자서 제 살림을 꾸려 갈 만한 '보금자리'를 마련해 줄 작정입니다.\" ('보금자리'는 그 사투리로 집을 뜻하고, '살림을 꾸린다'는 건 생계를 댄다는 뜻이다.) \"그러자면,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"제가 떠나기 전에 부인한테 생활비를 떼어 줘서, 그럭저럭 편히 지내게 해 줄 작정입니다. 부인은 더없이 신실한 사람이지요. 물론 그 나이에, 외롭고 의지가지없는 처지에, 그 착한 노인네더러 배 위에서 이리저리 부대끼고, 멀고 낯선 나라의 숲이며 황야를 헤매라고 할 수야 없지요. 그래서 부인은 그렇게 할 작정입니다.\"\n\n그는 누구 하나 잊지 않았다. 그는 자기 자신을 빼고는 모두의 처지와 노고를 헤아렸다.\n\n\"에밀리는,\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"저랑 함께 지낼 겁니다 — 가엾은 것, 평안과 안식이 절실히 필요하니까요! — 우리가 항해를 떠날 때까지요. 그 애는 만들어야 할 그 옷들을 지을 테고요. 그러다 보면, 거칠지만 사랑 깊은 제 아저씨 곁에 다시 있게 되면서, 그 애의 괴로움이 실제보다 더 먼 옛일처럼 느껴지기 시작하기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n고모님은 그 바람에 동감한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였고, 패거티 씨는 크게 흐뭇해했다.\n\n\"한 가지 더 있습니다, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣어, 전에 본 적 있는 그 작은 종이 꾸러미를 점잖게 꺼내 탁자 위에 펼치며 말했다. \"여기 이 지폐들 말입니다 — 오십 파운드하고 십 파운드요. 여기에다 그 애가 떠날 때 가져갔던 돈을 보태고 싶습니다. 그 애한테 그 일을 물어봐서 (왜 묻는지는 말하지 않고요) 합산해 뒀지요. 저야 배운 게 없으니, 죄송하지만 이게 맞는지 좀 봐 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n그는 자기 학식 없음을 사과하듯 종이 한 장을 내게 건네고는, 내가 그것을 살펴보는 동안 나를 지켜보았다. 셈은 정확했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 그것을 도로 받으며 말했다. \"이 돈을, 도련님이 보시기에 별 탈이 없다면, 데이비 도련님, 제가 떠나기 바로 전에 그놈 앞으로 봉투에 넣어 두렵니다. 그리고 그걸 다시 그놈 어미 앞으로 된 다른 봉투에 넣어 두고요. 그 어미한테는, 지금 도련님께 말씀드리는 만큼만 짤막하게, 이게 무엇의 대가인지를 적어 두겠습니다. 그리고 저는 이미 떠나서, 돌려받을 수 없게 됐다고요.\"\n\n나는 그렇게 하는 것이 옳을 것 같다고 — 그가 그것을 옳다고 느끼는 만큼, 옳으리라 확신한다고 말해 주었다.\n\n\"한 가지 더 있다고 말씀드렸지요.\" 그가 작은 꾸러미를 다시 묶어 주머니에 넣고 나서, 점잖은 미소를 띠고 말을 이었다. \"그런데 사실 두 가지였습니다. 오늘 아침 집을 나설 때만 해도, 그토록 감사한 일이 일어났다는 걸 제가 직접 햄한테 가서 전할 수 있을지 마음이 영 자신이 없었습니다. 그래서 나가 있는 동안 편지를 한 통 써서 우체국에 넣어 뒀지요. 일이 다 이렇게 됐다고, 그리고 내일 거기로 내려가 처리해야 할 자잘한 일들을 마무리하고, 어쩌면 야머스에 작별 인사를 하게 될 거라고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 저더러 같이 가 달라는 말씀인가요?\" 그가 무언가 말하지 않은 게 있음을 알아채고 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 친절을 베풀어 주실 수 있다면요, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"도련님 얼굴을 보면 그 사람들 기운이 좀 날 것 같아서요.\"\n\n내 어린 도라가 기분이 좋은 데다, 내가 다녀오기를 무척 바라는 것을 — 그 일을 함께 의논해 보고 알았는데 — 알았기에, 나는 그의 바람대로 기꺼이 동행하기로 약속했다. 그리하여 이튿날 아침, 우리는 야머스행 역마차에 올라, 다시 그 옛 길을 따라 달려갔다.\n\n밤에 그 낯익은 거리를 지나는데 — 패거티 씨는 내가 아무리 말려도 기어이 내 가방을 들었다 — 나는 오머와 조럼의 가게 안을 흘끗 들여다보았고, 거기서 옛 친구 오머 씨가 파이프를 피우고 있는 것을 보았다. 패거티 씨가 누이와 햄을 처음 만나는 자리에 내가 끼어 있기가 영 마음에 걸려, 오머 씨를 핑계 삼아 뒤에 좀 처졌다.\n\n\"이렇게 오랜만인데, 오머 씨는 어떻게 지내세요?\" 안으로 들어서며 내가 말했다.\n\n그는 나를 더 잘 보려고 파이프 연기를 손으로 휘저어 흩고는, 이내 나를 알아보고 무척 반가워했다.\n\n\"이런 영광스러운 방문에 마땅히 일어나서 인사를 드려야겠지만, 손님.\" 그가 말했다. \"다리가 영 시원찮아서, 이렇게 굴려 다니는 신세랍니다. 그래도 다리하고 숨 말고는, 사람이 누릴 수 있는 만큼 정정하다고, 고맙게도 그렇게 말씀드릴 수 있지요.\"\n\n나는 그의 흡족한 표정과 좋은 기분을 두고 축하의 말을 건넸는데, 그제야 그의 안락의자에 바퀴가 달려 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"기발한 물건이지요, 안 그래요?\" 그가 내 시선이 향하는 데를 따라가며, 팔로 팔걸이를 쓱쓱 닦으며 물었다. \"깃털처럼 가볍게 굴러가고, 역마차만큼이나 곧게 나아간답니다. 아이고, 우리 어린 미니가 — 내 손녀딸 말이오, 미니의 자식이지요 — 그 작은 힘으로 등받이를 밀면, 휙 하고 나아가는데, 그렇게 영리하고 신나는 광경은 좀처럼 보기 힘들 거요! 게다가 말이오 — 파이프 피우기에 이만큼 좋은 의자가 또 없답니다.\"\n\n나는 오머 씨처럼 무슨 일이든 좋게 받아들이고 거기서 즐거움을 찾아내는 착한 노인을 본 적이 없다. 그는 마치 자기 의자며, 천식이며, 시원찮은 다리가 죄다 파이프의 즐거움을 한층 돋우기 위한 어느 위대한 발명의 갈래들이라도 되는 양 환하게 빛나고 있었다.\n\n\"내 장담하는데, 이 의자에 앉아서 세상을 더 많이 본다오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"이 의자 밖에서 보던 것보다 말이오. 하루에도 얼마나 많은 사람이 들여다보며 한담을 나누고 가는지, 놀라실 거요. 정말이라니까! 이 의자를 들이고 나서는 신문에 실린 게 예전보다 두 배는 많아 보인답니다. 일반 독서로 말하자면, 아이고, 어찌나 많이 읽어 치우는지! 내가 그게 정말 다행이다 싶은 거요, 알겠소? 만약 눈이 탈이 났더라면 어쩔 뻔했겠소? 귀가 탈이 났더라면 또 어쩔 뻔했고? 그게 다리니까, 그게 무슨 대수겠소? 아니, 다리야 쓸 때면 그저 숨이나 더 가쁘게 했을 뿐인걸. 그리고 이제 거리에 나가거나 모래밭에 내려가고 싶으면, 조럼네 막내 도제 딕을 부르기만 하면 돼요. 그러면 런던 시장님처럼 내 마차를 타고 휙 나간다오.\"\n\n여기서 그는 웃다가 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이 되었다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 오머 씨가 다시 파이프를 물며 말했다. \"사람이란 기름진 데와 살코기를 함께 받아들여야 하는 법이오. 이 세상살이에서 사람이 마음먹어야 할 게 바로 그거지요. 조럼이 장사를 아주 잘한답니다. 기막히게 잘해요!\"\n\n\"그것참 반가운 소식이네요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았소.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"게다가 조럼하고 미니는 한 쌍의 발렌타인 같다오. 사람이 그 이상 무얼 바라겠소? 그에 비하면 다리 따위가 뭐 대수겠소!\"\n\n파이프를 피우며 앉아 자기 다리를 그토록 깔보는 그 모습은, 내가 여태껏 만나 본 가장 흐뭇한 별남 가운데 하나였다.\n\n\"그래, 내가 일반 독서에 빠진 사이, 손님은 일반 저술에 빠지셨구려, 안 그래요?\" 오머 씨가 나를 흠모하듯 훑어보며 말했다. \"참으로 사랑스러운 작품이었소! 그 속에 담긴 표현들이라니! 한 글자도 빠짐없이 읽었다오 — 한 글자도요. 그런데 졸음이 오기는커녕! 전혀 안 왔지 뭐요!\"\n\n나는 웃으며 흡족하다고 말했지만, 솔직히 그가 이 두 가지를 한데 묶어 말하는 게 의미심장하게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"내 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그 책을 탁자 위에 올려놓고 겉을 바라볼 때면, 따로따로 세 권으로 야무지게 묶인 그 책을 — 하나, 둘, 셋 — 한때 내가 손님 댁과 연을 맺는 영광을 누렸구나 생각하면, 어찌나 우쭐한지 모른다오. 아이고, 이제 그것도 한참 된 일이지요, 안 그래요? 저 블런더스톤에서 말이오. 어여쁜 작은 손님 한 분을 또 다른 손님 곁에 나란히 모셨던 그때 말입니다. 그리고 그때는 손님도 아주 작은 손님이셨지요. 아이고, 저런, 저런!\"\n\n나는 에밀리 이야기를 꺼내며 화제를 돌렸다. 그가 늘 에밀리에게 얼마나 마음을 써 왔는지, 또 얼마나 다정하게 대해 왔는지를 내가 잊지 않고 있다고 일러 준 뒤, 마사의 도움으로 에밀리가 아저씨 품으로 돌아오게 된 자초지종을 대강 들려주었다. 이 노인이 기뻐하리라는 걸 알았기 때문이다. 그는 더없이 골똘히 귀를 기울이더니, 내 말이 끝나자 감정에 겨워 말했다.\n\n\"정말 기쁘구려, 손님! 요 며칠 들은 소식 중에 가장 반가운 소식이오. 아이고, 저런, 저런! 그런데 그 불쌍한 처녀, 마사를 위해서는 이제 무얼 해 줄 작정이오?\"\n\n\"어제부터 제 마음에 줄곧 걸려 있던 대목을 짚으시네요.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 아직은 그 일에 대해 알려 드릴 게 없습니다, 오머 씨. 패거티 씨가 그 얘기를 꺼내지 않았는데, 제가 먼저 입에 올리기가 조심스러워서요. 분명 그분이 그 일을 잊지는 않았을 겁니다. 사심 없고 선한 일이라면 그분은 무엇 하나 잊는 법이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 말이오,\" 오머 씨가 끊었던 말을 다시 이어 받으며 말했다. \"무슨 일을 하든, 나도 한몫 끼고 싶구려. 손님이 보기에 옳다 싶은 일이라면 내 몫으로 얼마든 적어 두고, 알려 주시오. 나는 도무지 그 처녀가 죄다 못된 애라고는 생각할 수 없었는데, 그렇지 않다는 걸 알게 되어 기쁘오. 내 딸 미니도 그럴 거요. 젊은 여자들이란 어떤 일에선 영 종잡을 수 없는 데가 있어요 — 그 애 어미가 꼭 그랬지요 — 그래도 마음만은 곱고 따뜻하답니다. 마사 일에 대해서는 미니가 죄다 겉치레로 그러는 거요. 어째서 그 애가 겉치레할 필요를 느끼는지는 나도 뭐라 말 못 하겠소. 하지만 그게 다 겉치레라니까요, 아무렴. 남몰래는 무슨 친절이든 베풀 애지요. 그러니 손님이 보기에 옳다 싶은 만큼 내 몫으로 적어 주시겠소? 그리고 어디로 보내면 될지 몇 자 적어 보내 주시오. 아이고,\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"사람이란 게 삶의 두 끝이 맞닿는 그런 나이에 이르러서, 제아무리 정정하다 한들 두 번째로 일종의 유모차에 실려 굴려 다니는 신세가 되면 말이오, 친절을 베풀 수 있을 때 베푸는 걸 더없이 기뻐해야 하는 법이오. 그런 게 잔뜩 필요하지요. 그리고 딱히 내 얘기만 하는 건 아니라오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"왜냐하면, 손님, 내가 보기엔, 우리는 나이가 몇이든 다 함께 언덕 밑바닥을 향해 내려가고 있는 거요. 시간이란 게 단 한순간도 멈춰 서는 법이 없으니까. 그러니 우리 늘 친절을 베풀고, 더없이 기뻐합시다. 암, 그렇고말고요!\"\n\n그는 파이프에서 재를 톡톡 털어 내더니, 의자 등받이에 그것을 받쳐 두라고 일부러 만들어 둔 받침에 파이프를 올려놓았다.\n\n\"에밀리의 사촌 말이오, 그 애와 결혼하기로 돼 있던 그 사람 말입니다.\" 오머 씨가 힘없이 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"야머스에서 둘도 없이 훌륭한 청년이지요! 저녁이면 가끔 와서 한 시간씩 나한테 이야기도 해 주고 글도 읽어 준답니다. 그게 친절이라는 거지요, 암! 그 사람 한평생이 다 친절이라오.\"\n\n\"마침 그분을 만나러 가는 길입니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요?\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"내가 정정하더라고, 안부 전한다고 말해 주시오. 미니하고 조럼은 무도회에 갔다오. 집에 있었으면 나만큼이나 손님을 보고 자랑스러워했을 텐데. 미니는 통 바깥출입을 안 하려 든답니다, '아버지 때문에'라면서 말이오. 그래서 오늘 밤엔 내가 으름장을 놨지요, 네가 안 나가면 나는 여섯 시에 자 버리겠다고. 그 덕분에,\" 오머 씨가 제 꾀가 통한 게 우스워 제 몸과 의자를 들썩이며 웃었다. \"미니하고 조럼이 무도회에 간 거라오.\"\n\n나는 그와 악수를 나누고 안녕히 주무시라고 인사했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"내 어린 코끼리를 안 보고 가시면, 둘도 없는 구경거리를 놓치는 거요. 그런 구경거리는 본 적 없을걸! 미니!\" 위층 어디선가 작고 낭랑한 목소리가 대답했다. \"가요, 할아버지!\" 그러더니 길고 곱슬곱슬한 아마빛 머리칼을 늘어뜨린 예쁜 여자아이가 이내 가게로 달려 들어왔다.\n\n\"이게 내 어린 코끼리랍니다, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 아이를 어루만지며 말했다. \"샴 종이지요, 손님. 자, 어린 코끼리야!\"\n\n어린 코끼리가 거실 문을 활짝 열어 주는 바람에, 나는 요즈음 그 방이 위층으로 옮기기 힘든 오머 씨를 위한 침실로 바뀌었다는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그러고는 아이는 그 예쁜 이마를 감추듯, 긴 머리칼을 헝클어뜨리며 오머 씨의 의자 등받이에 머리를 들이밀었다.\n\n\"코끼리란 게 말이오, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. \"목표물을 향해 갈 때면 머리로 들이받는답니다. 하나, 코끼리야. 둘. 셋!\"\n\n이 신호에 어린 코끼리는, 그 작은 짐승치고는 거의 놀랍다 할 만한 솜씨로, 오머 씨를 태운 채 의자를 홱 돌리더니, 문설주에 한 번 부딪치는 일도 없이 와르르 거실 안으로 밀고 들어갔다. 오머 씨는 그 묘기를 말할 수 없이 즐기며, 마치 그것이 제 한평생 노고의 의기양양한 결실이기라도 한 양, 나아가는 길에 나를 돌아보았다.\n\n마을을 한 바퀴 거닐고 나서 나는 햄의 집으로 갔다. 패거티는 이제 아주 이리로 옮겨 와 살고 있었다. 그리고 자기 집은 운송업에서 바키스 씨의 뒤를 이은 사람에게 세놓았는데, 그 사람은 영업권과 짐수레와 말값을 그녀에게 후하게 쳐주었다. 내 생각에는 바키스 씨가 몰던 바로 그 굼뜬 말이 아직도 일하고 있는 듯했다.\n\n나는 그들이 깔끔한 부엌에 있는 것을 보았는데, 패거티 씨가 손수 옛날 배에서 데려온 거미지 부인도 함께 있었다. 다른 누가 권했더라면 부인이 제 자리를 버리고 따라나섰을 성싶지 않다. 그는 식구들에게 모든 것을 다 일러둔 게 분명했다. 패거티와 거미지 부인은 둘 다 앞치마를 눈에 대고 있었고, 햄은 막 '바닷가나 한 바퀴 돌고 오겠다'며 나간 참이었다. 그는 이내 집으로 돌아와 나를 보고 무척 반가워했다. 그리고 내가 거기 있어 준 덕에 식구들 모두가 조금이나마 마음이 나아졌기를 바란다. 우리는 어느 정도 명랑한 기색을 띠며, 패거티 씨가 새 나라에서 부자가 될 일이며, 그가 편지에 적어 보낼 갖가지 신기한 이야기들에 대해 이야기를 나누었다. 우리는 에밀리의 이름을 입에 올리지는 않았지만, 한 번 이상 에둘러 그 애를 언급했다. 그 자리에서 가장 평온한 사람은 햄이었다.\n\n하지만 패거티가, 탁자 위에 악어 책이 나를 위해 준비된 채 놓여 있는 작은 방으로 촛불을 들고 나를 안내하면서 들려준 말로는, 그는 늘 그 모양이라고 했다. 그녀는 (울먹이며 내게 말하기를) 그가 가슴이 무너진 거라고 믿었다. 그는 다정한 만큼이나 용기가 넘쳤고, 그 일대 어느 조선소의 어느 배 목수보다 더 부지런하고 솜씨 좋게 일했지만 말이다. 저녁이면 가끔, 그가 그 배 집에서 보낸 옛 시절 이야기를 꺼낼 때가 있다고 그녀는 말했다. 그럴 때면 그는 에밀리를 어린아이로 입에 올렸다. 하지만 결코 그 애를 한 여인으로는 입에 올리지 않았다.\n\n나는 그의 얼굴에서, 그가 나와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶어 한다는 것을 읽었다고 생각했다. 그래서 이튿날 저녁, 그가 일을 마치고 돌아올 때 그의 길목에 서 있기로 마음먹었다. 이렇게 혼자 결심하고 나서 나는 잠이 들었다. 그날 밤, 그 숱한 밤을 통틀어 처음으로, 창가의 촛불이 치워졌고, 패거티 씨는 옛날 배 안에서 그 옛 해먹에 누워 흔들렸으며, 바람은 그 옛 소리로 그의 머리맡을 맴돌며 속삭였다.\n\n이튿날 그는 온종일 고깃배와 어구를 처분하고, 자기한테 쓸모 있겠다 싶은 자잘한 살림살이를 챙겨 짐수레 편으로 런던에 부치고, 나머지는 떠나보내거나 거미지 부인에게 나눠 주는 일로 바빴다. 부인은 온종일 그의 곁에 있었다. 나는 그 옛 장소가 자물쇠로 잠기기 전에 한 번 더 보고 싶은 서글픈 마음에, 저녁에 거기서 그들과 만나기로 약속했다. 다만 햄을 먼저 만날 수 있도록 일정을 짰다.\n\n그가 어디서 일하는지 알았으니 그의 길목에 서기는 어렵지 않았다. 나는 그가 가로지를 것을 아는 한적한 모래밭 어귀에서 그를 만나, 그가 정말로 원한다면 나와 한가로이 이야기할 수 있도록 그와 함께 발길을 돌렸다. 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 기색을 내가 잘못 읽은 게 아니었다. 우리가 함께 얼마 걷지 않았을 때, 그가 나를 보지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님, 그 애를 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"잠깐, 그 애가 혼절해 있을 때 봤을 뿐이야.\" 내가 나직이 대답했다.\n\n우리는 조금 더 걸었고, 그가 말했다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님, 도련님은 그 애를 보실 것 같으세요?\"\n\n\"아마 그 애한테는 너무 괴로운 일일 거야.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"저도 그 생각을 했습니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그렇겠지요, 도련님, 그렇겠지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 햄,\" 내가 부드럽게 말했다. \"혹시 내가 그 애한테 직접 전할 수 없을 경우, 자네를 대신해 글로 적어 보낼 만한 게 있다면, 또 나를 통해 그 애한테 알리고 싶은 게 있다면, 내가 그걸 거룩한 부탁으로 여기고 맡겠네.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그러시겠지요. 고맙습니다, 도련님, 참으로 친절하십니다! 말로든 글로든 전하고 싶은 게 하나 있는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\"\n\n우리는 말없이 조금 더 걸었고, 이윽고 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"제가 그 애를 용서한다는 게 아닙니다. 그건 그리 대수로운 게 아니에요. 그보다는, 제 마음을 그 애한테 억지로 들이민 걸 용서해 달라고 그 애한테 비는 겁니다. 이따금 이런 생각이 들거든요. 제가 그 애한테서 결혼하겠다는 약속을 받아 내지만 않았더라도, 도련님, 그 애가 저를 벗으로서 그토록 믿고 따랐으니, 제 마음속에서 무엇이 그리 들끓고 있는지 저한테 털어놓고 의논해 줬을 테고, 그랬다면 제가 그 애를 구할 수도 있었을 텐데 하고요.\"\n\n나는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. \"그게 다인가?\" \"아직 하나 더 있습니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"제가 말로 할 수만 있다면요, 데이비 도련님.\"\n\n우리는 여태 걸은 것보다 더 멀리 걷고서야, 그가 다시 입을 열었다. 내가 줄표로 나타낼 멈춤의 순간에 그가 울고 있던 것은 아니었다. 그는 그저 아주 또박또박 말하려고 마음을 추스르고 있었을 뿐이다.\n\n\"저는 그 애를 사랑했고 — 그 애에 대한 기억을 사랑합니다 — 너무 깊이요 — 그래서 제 스스로 행복한 사람인 척 그 애가 믿게 만들 수가 없습니다. 저는 오직 — 그 애를 잊어야만 — 행복할 수 있을 텐데 — 제가 그랬다는 말을 그 애가 듣게 되는 건 도저히 견딜 수 없을 것 같습니다. 하지만 그토록 학식이 깊으신 도련님이, 무슨 말을 좀 생각해 내셔서, 제가 크게 상처받지 않았다고 그 애가 믿게 해 주실 수 있다면요. 여전히 그 애를 사랑하고, 그 애를 위해 슬퍼하고 있다고요. 제가 삶에 지친 게 아니라, 다만 사악한 자들이 더는 괴롭히지 못하고 지친 자들이 안식을 얻는 그곳에서, 아무 허물 없이 그 애를 다시 만나기를 바라고 있다고 그 애가 믿게 할 만한 말 무엇이든요. 그 애의 슬픈 마음을 달래 주면서도, 제가 언젠가 결혼할 수 있으리라거나, 그 누구라도 그 애가 제게 그러했던 만큼이 될 수 있으리라고는 그 애가 생각지 않게 할 만한 말 무엇이든요. 그런 말을 그 애한테 전해 주십사고 — 그토록 소중했던 그 애를 위한 제 기도와 함께 — 부탁드리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n나는 그의 사내다운 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥐고, 내가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 그 일을 맡겠노라고 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이렇게 저를 만나러 와 주셔서 고맙습니다. 아저씨와 함께 내려와 주셔서 고맙고요. 데이비 도련님, 저는 잘 압니다. 비록 제 고모님이 그들이 떠나기 전에 런던으로 가서 다시 한번 한자리에 모이게 되겠지만, 저는 아저씨를 다시 못 볼 것 같다는 걸요. 그리될 것 같다는 게 똑똑히 느껴집니다. 우리끼리 그렇게 말하지는 않습니다만, 그리될 거고, 또 그게 낫지요. 도련님이 아저씨를 마지막으로 보실 때 — 그 마지막 순간에 — 아저씨한테 전해 주시겠습니까? 아저씨가 늘 친아버지 이상으로 대해 주신 이 고아의, 더없이 사랑 깊은 효성과 감사를요?\"\n\n이것도 나는 꼭 그러겠노라고 약속했다.\n\n\"다시 한번 고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 진심으로 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"도련님이 어디로 가시는지 압니다. 안녕히 가십시오!\"\n\n마치 자기는 그 옛 장소에 들어갈 수 없노라고 내게 일러 주기라도 하듯 가볍게 손을 한 번 흔들고는, 그는 발길을 돌렸다. 달빛 아래 황량한 벌판을 가로질러 가는 그의 모습을 바라보고 있노라니, 그가 바다 위에 비친 은빛 띠 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 그것을 바라보며 걸어가다가, 이윽고 저 멀리 한 점 그림자가 되는 것이 보였다.\n\n내가 다가갔을 때 그 배 집의 문은 열려 있었다. 안으로 들어서니, 낡은 궤짝 하나만 빼고 가구가 죄다 치워져 있었는데, 그 궤짝 위에 거미지 부인이 무릎에 바구니를 얹은 채 앉아 패거티 씨를 바라보고 있었다. 그는 거친 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 난로 안에서 사위어 가는 몇 점의 잉걸불을 물끄러미 들여다보고 있었다. 그러다 내가 들어서자 희망에 찬 얼굴로 고개를 들고, 명랑한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"약속대로, 여기에 작별 인사를 하러 오셨군요, 그렇지요, 데이비 도련님?\" 그가 촛불을 집어 들며 말했다. \"이제 텅 비었지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" \"정말 시간을 알차게 쓰셨네요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 저희가 놀고 있지는 않았지요, 도련님. 거미지 부인이 마치 — 거미지 부인이 마치 뭐처럼 일했다고 해야 할지 모르겠네요.\" 충분히 흡족할 만한 비유가 떠오르지 않아 막막한 듯, 패거티 씨가 부인을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n거미지 부인은 바구니에 몸을 기댄 채 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"바로 저게 도련님이 에밀리하고 같이 앉곤 하시던 그 궤짝입니다!\" 패거티 씨가 속삭이듯 말했다. \"저건 맨 마지막에 제가 가지고 떠날 작정이에요. 그리고 여기가 도련님의 그 작은 침실이었지요, 보세요, 데이비 도련님! 오늘 밤은 마음이 바랄 만큼이나 휑하군요!\"\n\n정말이지 바람은 잔잔했어도 어딘가 엄숙한 소리를 냈고, 버려진 집 둘레를 속삭이듯 흐느끼며 맴도는 그 소리가 무척이나 구슬펐다. 굴 껍데기 테를 두른 작은 거울까지, 모든 것이 다 사라지고 없었다. 나는 집에 그 첫 번째 큰 변화가 일어나던 무렵, 이곳에 누워 있던 내 모습을 떠올렸다. 나를 홀딱 반하게 했던 그 파란 눈의 아이를 떠올렸다. 그리고 스티어포스를 떠올렸다. 그러자 그가 바로 가까이에 있어, 어느 모퉁이에서든 마주칠지 모른다는, 어리석고도 두려운 망상이 나를 사로잡았다.\n\n\"한참 걸릴 것 같습니다.\" 패거티 씨가 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"이 배가 새 임자를 만나기까지는요. 이 동네 사람들은 이제 이 배를 불길하게 여기거든요!\"\n\n\"이 동네 누가 주인인가요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"읍내의 돛대 만드는 사람입니다.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 그 사람한테 열쇠를 넘겨줄 작정이에요.\"\n\n우리는 다른 작은 방을 들여다보고는, 궤짝에 앉아 있는 거미지 부인에게로 돌아왔다. 패거티 씨가 촛불을 벽난로 선반에 올려놓고는, 촛불을 끄기 전에 궤짝을 문밖으로 내가려고 부인에게 일어나 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"댄..\" 거미지 부인이 별안간 바구니를 내팽개치고 그의 팔에 매달리며 말했다. \"내 소중한 댄, 내가 이 집에서 하는 작별 인사는 이거예요. 나를 두고 가면 안 돼요. 날 뒤에 남겨 두고 갈 생각일랑 말아요, 댄! 아이고, 절대 그러지 말아요!\"\n\n패거티 씨는 화들짝 놀라, 잠에서 막 깨어난 사람처럼, 거미지 부인을 보았다가 나를 보고, 다시 나를 보았다가 거미지 부인을 보았다.\n\n\"그러지 말아요, 더없이 소중한 댄, 그러지 말아요!\" 거미지 부인이 간절하게 외쳤다. \"나를 데려가 줘요, 댄, 나도 당신하고 에밀리하고 같이 데려가 줘요! 변함없이 진실되게 당신네 하인이 될게요. 당신이 가는 그 고장에 노예가 있다면, 기꺼이 당신한테 매인 노예 한 사람이 되어도 좋아요. 그래도 행복할 거예요. 그러니 나를 두고 가지 말아요, 댄, 그래 줘요 제발!\"\n\n\"이 착한 사람아.\" 패거티 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그게 얼마나 긴 항해인지, 또 얼마나 고된 삶인지 모르고 하는 소리야!\" \"아니, 알아요, 댄! 짐작할 수 있다고요!\" 거미지 부인이 외쳤다. \"하지만 이 지붕 아래서 하는 내 작별 인사는 이거예요. 나를 데려가지 않으면, 나는 구빈원에 들어가 죽을 거예요. 나는 땅도 팔 수 있고, 댄. 일도 할 수 있어요. 고생도 견딜 수 있고요. 이젠 다정하고 참을성 있게 굴 수도 있어요 — 당신 생각보다 훨씬요, 댄, 한 번만 시험해 보면 알 거예요. 굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 그 생활비엔 손도 안 댈 거예요, 댄 패거티. 하지만 한 번만 허락해 준다면, 나는 당신하고 에밀리를 따라 세상 끝까지라도 갈 거예요! 나도 알아요. 내가 외롭고 의지가지없는 신세라고 당신이 여기는 걸 알아요. 하지만 소중한 당신, 이젠 더는 그렇지 않아요! 내가 여기 이토록 오래 앉아, 지켜보면서, 당신의 시련을 곱씹는 동안 나한테도 좋은 게 좀 생긴 거라고요. 데이비 도련님, 저 대신 좀 말해 주세요! 저는 저 사람 성미도, 에밀리 성미도 알고, 두 사람의 슬픔도 알아서, 이따금 그들에게 위안이 되어 줄 수도 있고, 언제까지나 그들을 위해 애쓸 수도 있어요! 댄, 소중한 댄, 나도 당신하고 같이 가게 해 줘요!\"\n\n그러고서 거미지 부인은 그의 손을 잡아, 그가 마땅히 받을 만한, 헌신과 감사가 어린 소박한 황홀경 속에서, 투박하면서도 애틋한 정과 가련함을 담아 그 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n우리는 궤짝을 밖으로 내가고, 촛불을 끄고, 문을 바깥에서 단단히 잠근 다음, 그 옛 배를 꼭 닫아 두고 떠났다. 흐린 밤하늘 아래 한 점 검은 얼룩으로. 이튿날, 우리가 역마차 바깥자리에 앉아 런던으로 돌아가는데, 거미지 부인과 부인의 바구니는 뒷자리에 실려 있었고, 거미지 부인은 행복했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 52. I ASSIST AT AN EXPLOSION\n\n미코버 씨가 그토록 알쏭달쏭하게 정해 둔 날이 이제 스물네 시간도 채 남지 않았을 무렵, 고모님과 나는 어떻게 일을 처리할지 의논했다. 고모님이 도라 곁을 떠나기를 몹시 꺼렸기 때문이다. 아! 이제 나는 얼마나 가뿐하게 도라를 안고 계단을 오르내렸던가!\n\n미코버 씨가 고모님이 꼭 참석해야 한다고 못 박았는데도, 우리는 고모님은 집에 남고 디크 씨와 내가 대신 가도록 일을 꾸미려는 쪽으로 기울었다. 한마디로, 우리는 그렇게 하기로 마음을 정했는데, 그러자 도라가 또다시 우리를 흔들어 놓았다. 무슨 핑계를 대든 고모님이 뒤에 남는다면, 자기는 평생 자기 자신을 용서하지 못할 거고, 자기 나쁜 남편도 결코 용서하지 못할 거라고 선언한 것이다.\n\n\"고모님하고는 말도 안 할 거예요.\" 도라가 고모님을 향해 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"심술쟁이가 될 거예요! 집프가 하루 종일 고모님한테 짖게 만들 거예요. 안 가시면, 고모님이 정말로 못된 늙은이라고 단단히 믿어 버릴 거라고요!\"\n\n\"쯧, 우리 꽃송이!\" 고모님이 웃었다. \"넌 내가 없으면 못 견딘다는 거, 잘 알면서!\"\n\n\"아니요, 견딜 수 있어요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"고모님은 저한테 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없어요. 하루 종일 저 때문에 계단을 오르내려 주지도 않잖아요. 곁에 앉아서, 도디가 신발이 다 닳고 먼지투성이가 됐을 적 이야기를 들려주지도 않고요 — 아, 그 가엾고 조그만 꼬마! 고모님은 저를 기쁘게 해 줄 일은 하나도 안 하잖아요, 안 그래요, 고모님?\" 도라는 고모님이 그 말을 진담으로 받아들일까 봐, 부랴부랴 고모님에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"맞아요, 다 해 주시죠! 농담이에요!\"\n\n\"하지만, 고모님.\" 도라가 어리광 부리듯 말했다. \"이제 들어 보세요. 꼭 가셔야 해요. 제 뜻대로 될 때까지 고모님을 졸라 댈 거예요. 우리 못된 남편이 고모님을 안 가게 하면, 그 사람 인생을 아주 들들 볶을 거예요. 제가 얼마나 심술궂게 굴 텐데요 — 집프도 그럴 거고요! 안 가시면, 착하게 다녀올걸 하고 두고두고, 정말 오래오래 후회하실 거예요. 게다가,\" 도라가 머리를 뒤로 넘기며, 고모님과 나를 의아한 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"왜 두 분이 다 가시면 안 되는 거죠? 저 정말로 그렇게 아프지 않아요. 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 무슨 소리니!\" 고모님이 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 그런 생각을!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네! 제가 어리석은 꼬마라는 거 알아요!\" 도라가 우리 둘을 천천히 번갈아 보다가, 소파에 누운 채 우리에게 입 맞추려고 예쁜 입술을 내밀며 말했다. \"그럼, 두 분 다 가셔야 해요. 안 그러면 저는 두 분을 안 믿을 거고, 그러면 울어 버릴 거예요!\"\n\n고모님의 얼굴에서, 이제 마음이 누그러지기 시작하는 것이 보였고, 도라도 그것을 알아채자 다시 환해졌다.\n\n\"두 분이 돌아오시면 들려줄 이야기가 어찌나 많을지, 제가 알아듣는 데만도 적어도 일주일은 걸릴 거예요!\" 도라가 말했다. \"왜냐면 거기에 무슨 사무적인 일이 끼어 있으면, 한참 동안 못 알아들을 게 뻔하거든요. 그리고 분명 무슨 사무적인 일이 끼어 있을 거고요! 게다가 셈할 게 있기라도 하면, 언제 풀어낼지 모르겠고요. 그동안 우리 나쁜 남편은 내내 어찌나 비참한 얼굴을 하고 있을 텐데요. 자! 이제 가실 거죠, 네? 딱 하룻밤만 비우시는 거고, 비우신 동안엔 집프가 저를 지켜 줄 거예요. 도디가 가시기 전에 저를 위층으로 안아다 줄 거고, 두 분이 돌아오실 때까지 저는 다시 안 내려올 거예요. 그리고 아그네스한테 제가 보내는 무섭게 야단치는 편지를 가져다주세요. 한 번도 우리 보러 안 왔으니까요!\"\n\n우리는 더 의논할 것도 없이, 둘 다 가기로, 그리고 도라는 어리광 받는 게 좋아서 좀 아픈 척하는 작은 사기꾼이라고 의견을 모았다. 도라는 무척 기뻐하며 한껏 들떴다. 그리고 우리 넷, 그러니까 고모님, 디크 씨, 트래들스, 나는 그날 밤 도버행 우편 마차를 타고 캔터베리로 내려갔다.\n\n미코버 씨가 자기를 기다려 달라고 청한 호텔에 한밤중에 어렵사리 들어가서, 나는 편지 한 통을 발견했다. 그가 아침 아홉 시 반 정각에 나타나겠다는 내용이었다. 그러고 나서 우리는 그 거북한 시각에, 묵은 수프와 마구간 냄새가 우러난 용액에 오랜 세월 절여진 듯한 비좁은 통로들을 거쳐, 부들부들 떨며 각자의 침대로 갔다.\n\n이른 아침, 나는 정겹고 오래된 고요한 거리를 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐며, 다시 한번 유서 깊은 성문과 교회의 그림자 속으로 스며들었다. 까마귀들이 대성당 첨탑 둘레를 빙빙 떠다녔다. 그 첨탑들은 변함없이 길게 뻗은 기름진 들판과 그 정겨운 시내를 굽어보며, 이 땅에 변화라는 것은 도무지 없다는 듯 환한 아침 공기를 가르고 있었다. 그러나 종소리가 울리자, 그 소리는 만물의 변화를 서글피 일러 주었다. 종 자신의 늙음을, 그리고 내 어여쁜 도라의 젊음을. 그리고 종소리의 울림이, 안쪽에 걸린 흑태자의 녹슨 갑옷을 뚫고 웅웅거리다가, 시간의 깊은 물 위에 떠도는 티끌이 되어, 물에 번지는 동그라미처럼 허공으로 사라지는 동안, 살고 사랑하고 죽어 간, 결코 늙지 않는 수많은 이들을 일러 주었다.\n* 흑태자: 14세기 잉글랜드 왕 에드워드 3세의 맏아들 에드워드를 가리키는 별칭. 검은 갑옷을 입었다 하여 그렇게 불렸다.\n\n나는 길모퉁이에서 그 오래된 집을 바라보았으나, 더 가까이 가지는 않았다. 들켜서, 내가 도우러 온 계획에 본의 아니게 해를 끼칠까 봐서였다. 이른 아침 햇살이 그 집 박공과 격자창에 비스듬히 내려앉아 황금빛으로 어루만지고 있었다. 그 오래된 평화의 빛줄기 몇 가닥이 내 마음마저 어루만지는 듯했다.\n\n나는 한 시간 남짓 들판을 거닐다가, 그새 간밤의 잠을 떨쳐 낸 큰길을 따라 돌아왔다. 가게에서 부산을 떠는 사람들 가운데, 나의 옛 원수인 푸줏간 사내가 보였다. 이제는 목 긴 장화를 신고 갓난아기까지 거느린, 제 가게를 차린 어엿한 주인이었다. 그는 아기를 어르고 있었는데, 사회의 자애로운 일원처럼 보였다.\n\n우리는 아침을 먹으려고 자리에 앉았을 때 다들 몹시 초조하고 안달이 났다. 아홉 시 반이 점점 가까워질수록, 미코버 씨를 기다리는 우리의 안절부절못함도 커져 갔다. 마침내 우리는 식사에 마음을 쓰는 척조차 그만두었는데, 디크 씨를 빼고는 처음부터 그저 형식에 불과한 식사였다. 고모님은 방 안을 서성였다. 트래들스는 소파에 앉아 두 눈은 천장에 둔 채 신문을 읽는 척했고, 나는 창밖을 내다보며 미코버 씨가 오는지 미리 알리려 했다. 오래 지켜볼 것도 없었다. 그 반 시각을 알리는 첫 종소리가 울리자, 그가 거리에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"저기 오네요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그런데 법률가 차림이 아니에요!\"\n\n고모님은 보닛 끈을 묶고(아침 먹으러 내려올 때부터 쓰고 있었다), 마치 단호하고 한 치도 물러섬 없는 일이라면 무엇이든 맞설 채비를 한 사람처럼 숄을 둘렀다. 트래들스는 결연한 기색으로 코트 단추를 채웠다. 디크 씨는 이 가공할 차림새들에 마음이 어수선했지만, 자기도 흉내 내야겠다고 느꼈는지, 두 손으로 모자를 잡아 있는 힘껏 단단히 귀까지 푹 눌러썼다가, 미코버 씨를 맞이하려고 곧바로 다시 벗었다.\n\n\"신사 여러분, 그리고 부인.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"안녕하십니까! 친애하는 선생님,\" 그와 격렬하게 악수를 나눈 디크 씨를 향해. \"정말 친절하십니다.\"\n\n\"아침은 드셨소?\" 디크 씨가 말했다. \"갈비 한 점 드시오!\"\n\n\"천만에요, 친절하신 선생님!\" 미코버 씨가 종을 울리러 가는 그를 가로막으며 외쳤다. \"식욕과 저는, 딕슨 씨, 오래전부터 서로 모르는 사이가 되었답니다.\"\n\n딕슨 씨는 새 이름이 어찌나 마음에 들었는지, 또 미코버 씨가 그 이름을 붙여 준 것이 어찌나 고마운 일로 여겨졌는지, 그와 또 한 번 악수를 하며 다소 어린아이처럼 웃었다.\n\n\"디크.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"정신 차려요!\"\n\n디크 씨는 얼굴을 붉히며 제정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"자, 그럼.\" 고모님이 장갑을 끼며 미코버 씨에게 말했다. \"당신이 원하시면 언제든, 우리는 베수비오 화산이든 뭐든 맞설 채비가 되어 있어요.\"\n\n\"부인.\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"곧 분화를 목격하시리라 믿습니다. 트래들스 씨, 우리가 함께 연락을 주고받아 왔다는 사실을 여기서 밝혀도 좋다는 허락을 주신 걸로 압니다만?\"\n\n\"틀림없는 사실이라네, 코퍼필드.\" 내가 놀라서 쳐다보자 트래들스가 말했다. \"미코버 씨가 자기가 구상하는 일에 관해 내게 상의했고, 나는 내 판단껏 최선을 다해 조언했네.\"\n\n\"제 생각이 틀리지 않았다면, 트래들스 씨.\" 미코버 씨가 말을 이었다. \"제가 구상하는 것은 중대한 성격의 폭로입니다.\"\n\n\"지극히 그렇지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 부인 그리고 신사 여러분.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"이런 상황에서, 부디 잠시만 한 사람의 지시에 따라 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까? 그자는 인간 본성의 물가에 떠밀려 온 표류물이자 부랑자로 여겨지는 것 말고는 달리 봐줄 구석이 없는 자이지만, 그래도 개인적 과오와 갖가지 상황이 겹쳐 누적된 힘에 짓눌려 본래의 모습이 으스러졌을 뿐, 여전히 여러분과 같은 인간입니다.\"\n\n\"우리는 당신을 온전히 믿습니다, 미코버 씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 하라는 대로 하지요.\"\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨.\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"현 시점에서 당신의 믿음은 헛되이 베풀어진 것이 아닙니다. 부디 시계로 오 분만 먼저 출발하도록 허락해 주십시오. 그런 다음, 제가 봉급쟁이로 일하는 위크필드 앤드 힙 사무소로, 위크필드 양을 찾아 오신 여러분을 맞이하겠습니다.\"\n\n고모님과 나는 트래들스를 쳐다보았고, 그는 고개를 끄덕여 찬성했다.\n\n\"지금으로서는 더 드릴 말씀이 없습니다.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다.\n\n그러고는, 나로서는 한없이 놀랍게도, 그가 우리 모두를 아우르는 넓은 절을 하더니 사라져 버렸다. 그 태도는 더없이 서먹했고, 얼굴은 더없이 창백했다.\n\n내가 설명을 바라며 쳐다보자 트래들스는 그저 빙긋 웃으며 (머리카락이 정수리에서 빳빳하게 곤두선 채) 고개를 가로저을 뿐이었다. 그래서 나는 시계를 꺼내, 마지막 방편으로 오 분을 세었다. 고모님도 손에 자기 시계를 들고 똑같이 했다. 시간이 다 되자, 트래들스가 고모님에게 팔을 내밀었고, 우리는 가는 내내 한마디도 하지 않고 다 함께 그 오래된 집으로 갔다.\n\n미코버 씨는 일 층 탑루 사무실의 책상에 앉아, 글을 쓰거나 쓰는 척하느라 여념이 없었다. 큼직한 사무용 자가 그의 조끼에 꽂혀 있었는데, 잘 숨기지 못해서, 그 자가 한 자 남짓 가슴팍에서 삐죽 튀어나와, 무슨 새로운 종류의 셔츠 주름 장식처럼 보였다.\n\n내가 말을 꺼내야 할 것 같아서, 나는 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 미코버 씨?\"\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨.\" 미코버 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"건강하신 모습이길 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"위크필드 양은 댁에 계신가요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"위크필드 씨는 류머티즘열로 자리에 누워 편찮으십니다, 선생님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"하지만 위크필드 양은 옛 친구분들을 분명 반갑게 맞으시리라 믿습니다. 들어오시겠습니까, 선생님?\"\n\n그는 우리를 앞서 식당으로 데려갔다 — 내가 이 집에서 맨 처음 들어가 본 방이었다 — 그러고는 위크필드 씨가 예전에 쓰던 사무실 문을 활짝 열어젖히며, 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"트롯우드 양, 데이비드 코퍼필드 씨, 토머스 트래들스 씨, 그리고 딕슨 씨!\"\n\n나는 그 일격 이후로 유라이아 힙을 본 적이 없었다. 우리의 방문이 그를 놀라게 한 것이 역력했다. 우리 자신도 놀랐던 만큼이나 더더욱 그랬으리라 싶다. 그는 눈썹을 한데 모으지 않았는데, 모을 만한 눈썹이랄 게 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 작은 두 눈이 거의 감기다시피 할 만큼 눈살을 찌푸렸고, 흉측한 손을 황급히 턱으로 가져가는 모습이 어떤 당혹이나 놀람을 드러냈다. 이건 우리가 그의 방으로 막 들어서는 찰나, 내가 고모님 어깨 너머로 그를 흘끗 보았을 때뿐이었다. 잠시 후, 그는 여느 때처럼 알랑거리고 비굴해졌다.\n\n\"어이쿠, 이거 참.\" 그가 말했다. \"이거 정말 뜻밖의 기쁨이로군요! 말하자면, 세인트 폴 둘레의 벗들을 한꺼번에 다 모시다니, 바라지도 못한 호사올시다! 코퍼필드 씨, 건강하신 모습이길 바라고요 — 외람되이 이렇게 표현해도 된다면 — 당신의 벗 되시는 분들에게, 그렇든 아니든, 늘 다정하신 모습이길 바랍니다. 코퍼필드 부인은요, 선생님, 차도가 있으시길 바랍니다. 요즘 부인의 상태가 좋지 않다는 소식에 우리도 정말이지 어찌나 마음을 졸였는지요.\"\n\n그에게 내 손을 잡히는 게 부끄러웠지만, 달리 어쩌면 좋을지 알 수가 없었다.\n\n\"제가 보잘것없는 서기로 트롯우드 양의 조랑말을 붙들고 있던 때랑은, 이 사무실도 변했지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 유라이아가 더없이 역겨운 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"하지만 저는 안 변했습니다, 트롯우드 양.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 선생.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"솔직히 말하자면, 당신은 어린 시절의 그 싹수에 꽤나 한결같다고 생각하는데. 그게 당신에게 무슨 위안이 된다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 유라이아가 볼썽사납게 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"좋게 봐주셔서요! 미코버, 아그네스 아씨한테 알리라고 일러 — 그리고 어머니한테도. 어머니는 이분들을 보면 정신이 다 나가실 거요!\" 유라이아가 의자를 갖다 놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"바쁘지 않으시오, 힙 씨?\" 트래들스가 말했다. 우리를 동시에 살피면서도 피하던 그 교활하고 붉은 눈이, 트래들스의 눈과 우연히 마주쳤다.\n\n\"아니요, 트래들스 씨.\" 유라이아가 공무용 자리로 돌아가 앉으며, 뼈만 앙상한 두 손을 손바닥끼리 맞붙여 앙상한 두 무릎 사이에 끼우고 쥐어짜며 대답했다. \"바랐던 만큼은 아니지요. 하지만 변호사며, 상어며, 거머리는 좀처럼 만족을 모르는 법 아닙니까! 그렇다고 저랑 미코버 손이 한가하다는 건 아닙니다, 대체로 양손에 일이 그득하지요. 위크필드 씨가 무슨 일이든 도무지 감당하기 어려우신 탓이라서요, 선생님. 하지만 그분을 위해 일하는 건 의무이자 즐거움이올시다, 분명히요. 트래들스 씨는 위크필드 씨와 가깝게 지내신 적이 없으시지요? 제가 직접 뵌 영광은 딱 한 번뿐인 걸로 압니다만?\"\n\n\"네, 위크필드 씨와 가깝게 지낸 적은 없습니다.\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"그랬더라면 진작에 당신을 찾아뵈었을지도 모르지요, 힙 씨.\"\n\n이 대답의 어조에는 무언가가 있어서, 유라이아는 매우 음흉하고 의심스러운 표정으로 그 말을 한 사람을 다시 쳐다보았다. 그러나 사람 좋은 얼굴에, 꾸밈없는 태도에, 곤두선 머리카락을 한 트래들스밖에 보이지 않자, 그는 그 의심을 떨쳐 버리며, 온몸을, 특히 목구멍을 한 번 홱 비틀고는 대답했다.\n\n\"안타깝군요, 트래들스 씨. 그분을 가까이 두셨다면 우리 모두처럼 흠모하셨을 텐데요. 그분의 사소한 결점들이 오히려 더 정겹게 느껴지셨을 거고요. 하지만 제 동업자를 멋들어지게 칭송하는 걸 들어 보고 싶으시거든, 코퍼필드한테 물어보시라고 권하지요. 그 집안 이야기라면 저 친구가 아주 자신 있어 하는 화제거든요, 들어 보신 적 없으시다면 말입니다.\"\n\n내가 그 치하를(어쨌든 그래야 했다면) 부인할 새도 없이, 미코버 씨가 안내하는 아그네스가 들어왔다. 그녀는 평소만큼 차분하지는 못한 것 같았고, 불안과 피로를 겪은 것이 역력했다. 그러나 그녀의 진심 어린 다정함과 고요한 아름다움은, 그 때문에 더 부드러운 광채로 빛났다.\n\n유라이아가 그녀가 우리에게 인사하는 동안 그녀를 지켜보는 것이 보였다. 그는 선한 정령을 노려보는 추악하고 반항적인 마귀를 떠올리게 했다. 그러는 사이, 미코버 씨와 트래들스 사이에 어떤 가벼운 신호가 오갔고, 나 말고는 아무도 못 본 채, 트래들스가 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"기다리지 마, 미코버.\" 유라이아가 말했다.\n\n미코버 씨는 가슴팍의 자에 한 손을 얹은 채 문 앞에 꼿꼿이 서서, 자기와 같은 인간 하나를, 더구나 그 인간이 자기 고용주인 자를, 더없이 또렷하게 응시하고 있었다.\n\n\"뭘 기다리는 거야?\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"미코버! 기다리지 말라고 한 말 못 들었어?\"\n\n\"들었지!\" 꿈쩍도 않는 미코버 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 왜 기다리는 거야?\" 유라이아가 말했다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 나는 — 한마디로, 그러기로 택했으니까.\" 미코버 씨가 버럭 대답했다.\n\n유라이아의 두 뺨에서 핏기가 가셨고, 온몸에 깔린 붉은 기가 아직 희미하게 어린, 병색 짙은 창백함이 그 뺨을 뒤덮었다. 그는 미코버 씨를 유심히 쳐다보았는데, 얼굴의 모든 근육이 가쁘고 빠르게 숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"넌 세상이 다 아는 방탕한 놈이야.\" 그가 억지로 웃음을 지어 보이며 말했다. \"그러니 너 때문에 어쩔 수 없이 널 내쫓게 될까 봐 걱정이로구나. 썩 꺼져! 이따가 너랑 얘기하지.\"\n\n\"이 땅 위에, 내가 이미 너무 많은 말을 섞은 악당이 하나 있다면,\" 미코버 씨가 별안간 다시, 더없이 격렬하게 터져 나오며 말했다. \"그 악당의 이름은 바로 — 힙이다!\"\n\n유라이아는 얻어맞거나 쏘이기라도 한 듯 뒤로 물러섰다. 그러더니 그 얼굴이 지을 수 있는 가장 어둡고 사악한 표정으로 우리를 천천히 둘러보며, 한층 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"오호! 이거 음모로구나! 너희가 미리 짜고 여기서 만난 거야! 내 서기랑 짜고 한통속으로 노는 거지, 그렇지, 코퍼필드? 자, 조심해. 이래 봤자 아무것도 얻지 못할 테니까. 너랑 나는 서로를 잘 알아. 우리 사이엔 정이라곤 없지. 넌 여기 처음 발 들인 순간부터 늘 콧대 높은 강아지였고, 내가 출세한 걸 시기하지, 안 그래? 나한테 음모 따위 꾸미지 마. 내가 맞불을 놓을 테니! 미코버, 넌 꺼져. 이따 너랑 얘기하지.\"\n\n\"미코버 씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"이 작자에게 갑작스러운 변화가 일어났습니다. 한 가지 점에서 진실을 말했다는 그 유별난 변화 말고도 여러 면에서요. 그건 이자가 막다른 골목에 몰렸다는 증거입니다. 받아 마땅한 대로 다뤄 주십시오!\"\n\n\"너희들 참 별난 작자들이로구나, 안 그래?\" 유라이아가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로, 이마에 송골송골 맺힌 끈끈한 땀을 길고 비쩍 마른 손으로 닦아 내며 말했다. \"사회의 쓰레기나 다름없는 내 서기를 매수해서 — 너 자신도 그랬잖아, 코퍼필드, 너도 알지, 누가 너한테 자비를 베풀기 전까진 말이야 — 거짓말로 날 헐뜯다니? 트롯우드 양, 이 짓 그만두는 게 좋을 거요. 안 그러면 당신 남편 일을 당신 마음에 안 들 만큼 짧게 끝장내 줄 테니까. 내가 당신 사연을 직업상 공짜로 알게 된 줄 아쇼, 노부인! 위크필드 양, 아버지를 조금이라도 사랑한다면, 저 패거리에 끼지 않는 게 좋을 거요. 그러면 내가 그분을 파멸시킬 테니까. 자, 어디! 너희 중 몇몇은 이미 내 써레 밑에 깔려 있어. 그게 너희 위로 지나가기 전에 두 번 생각해. 두 번 생각하라고, 너, 미코버, 으스러지기 싫거든. 내 충고하건대 썩 꺼져서 이따 이야기나 듣지 그래, 이 멍청아! 물러설 시간 있을 때 말이야. 어머니는 어딨지?\" 그가 트래들스가 없어진 것을 별안간 알아챈 듯 화들짝 놀라며, 종 줄을 잡아당기며 말했다. \"남의 집에서 별짓을 다 하는군!\"\n\n\"힙 부인은 여기 계십니다, 선생.\" 트래들스가 그 갸륵한 아들의 갸륵한 어머니를 데리고 돌아오며 말했다. \"외람되이 제가 부인께 제 소개를 해 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"네가 뭔데 네 소개를 해?\" 유라이아가 쏘아붙였다. \"그리고 여기서 뭘 원하는 거야?\"\n\n\"저는 위크필드 씨의 대리인이자 친구입니다, 선생.\" 트래들스가 차분하고 사무적인 투로 말했다. \"그리고 그분이 모든 일에서 그분을 대신해 처리하라고 주신 위임장이 제 주머니에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그 늙은 멍청이가 술에 절어 노망이 든 거지.\" 유라이아가 아까보다 더 추악해진 얼굴로 말했다. \"그걸 사기로 받아 낸 거야!\"\n\n\"무언가를 사기로 받아 냈지요, 나도 압니다.\" 트래들스가 조용히 대꾸했다. \"당신도 알고요, 힙 씨. 괜찮으시다면 그 문제는 미코버 씨에게 맡기지요.\"\n\n\"유리—!\" 힙 부인이 불안한 몸짓으로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"어머니는 입 다물어요.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"말이 적을수록 탈도 적은 법이니까.\"\n\n\"하지만, 우리 유리—\"\n\n\"입 다물고 나한테 맡기라니까요, 어머니?\"\n\n그의 비굴함이 거짓이고, 그의 모든 가식이 음흉하고 속이 빈 것임은 진작부터 알고 있었지만, 이제 가면을 벗어 던진 그를 보고서야, 나는 그 위선의 깊이를 제대로 헤아렸다. 가면이 더는 쓸모없음을 알아챈 순간 그것을 내던지는 그 갑작스러움, 그가 드러낸 악의와 오만과 증오, 이 순간에조차 자기가 저지른 악행에 의기양양해하며 짓던 곁눈질 — 게다가 이 내내 그는 절박했고, 우리를 이길 방도를 찾느라 막다른 골목에 몰려 있기도 했다 — 이 모든 것이 내가 그에게서 겪은 경험과 완벽하게 들어맞았는데도, 그토록 오래 그를 알아 왔고 그토록 진심으로 싫어한 나마저, 처음엔 깜짝 놀라고 말았다.\n\n그가 우리를 하나하나 노려보며 서 있을 때 나에게 던진 그 눈초리에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠다. 그가 나를 미워한다는 건 늘 알고 있었고, 그의 뺨에 남은 내 손자국도 기억하고 있었으니까. 그러나 그의 눈길이 아그네스에게 옮겨 갔을 때, 그녀를 휘두르던 자기 힘이 손가락 사이로 빠져나가는 것을 느끼며 그가 품은 분노, 그리고 그 좌절 속에서, 그가 결코 진가를 알아보지도 마음 쓰지도 못할 미덕을 지닌 사람을 탐내게 만든 그 가증스러운 욕정의 표출 — 그것을 본 나는, 그녀가 그런 사내의 눈길이 닿는 곳에서 단 한 시간이라도 살아왔다는 생각만으로도 몸서리가 쳐졌다.\n\n얼굴 아랫부분을 한참 문지르고, 흉측한 손가락들 너머로 그 못된 눈으로 우리를 한참 쳐다보더니, 그는 나에게 다시 한번, 반은 징징대고 반은 욕설을 퍼붓는 투로 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"이게 정당하다고 생각하는 거지, 어, 코퍼필드, 그렇게 명예니 뭐니 하는 걸 그토록 자랑스러워하는 네가, 내 집을 살금살금 기웃거리며 내 서기랑 엿듣고 다니는 게? 이게 나였다면 놀라지도 않았을 거야. 난 나 자신을 신사라고 내세우지도 않으니까(하긴 미코버 말로는 너처럼 거리를 떠돈 적은 한 번도 없지만). 그런데 다른 사람도 아닌 네가! 게다가 이런 짓을 하면서 무섭지도 않아? 내가 그 대가로 무슨 짓을 할지는 조금도 생각 안 해? 음모니 뭐니 해서 네 발등에 불 떨어질 일은? 좋아. 두고 보자고! 이름이 뭐였더라, 당신, 어떤 문제를 미코버한테 맡긴다고 했지. 자, 저기 당신네 중재인 있잖아. 어디 한번 말을 시켜 보시지? 보아하니 자기 대본은 다 외운 모양인데.\"\n\n자기 말이 나에게도, 우리 누구에게도 아무 효과가 없는 걸 보고서, 그는 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 한쪽 넓적한 발을 다른 다리에 비비 꼰 채, 책상 모서리에 걸터앉아, 다음에 무슨 일이 닥칠지 끈질기게 기다렸다.\n\n미코버 씨는, 그때까지 내가 그의 격정을 천신만고 끝에 억눌러 온 터였고, 두 번째 음절까지는 가지도 못한 채 \"악다—악당!\"의 첫 음절만 거듭 끼워 넣던 참이었는데, 이제 앞으로 와락 뛰쳐나오며 가슴팍에서 자를 빼 들고(아무래도 방어용 무기로 삼으려는 듯했다), 주머니에서 큼직한 편지 모양으로 접힌 대형 문서 한 장을 꺼냈다. 옛날 그 멋들어진 손짓으로 이 꾸러미를 펼쳐, 마치 그 문장의 작법에 예술적 감탄이라도 품은 듯 내용을 한번 훑어보더니, 이렇게 읽어 내려가기 시작했다.\n\n\"'친애하는 트롯우드 양, 그리고 신사 여러분—'\"\n\n\"맙소사, 저 양반 좀 봐!\" 고모님이 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"사형감인 일이라도, 편지는 한 연(連)씩 써 댈 양반이라니까!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 그 말을 듣지 못한 채 계속했다.\n\n\"'아마도 일찍이 존재한 가장 완벽한 악당을 고발하고자 여러분 앞에 나서면서,'\" 미코버 씨는 편지에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 유령의 곤봉처럼 자로 유라이아 힙을 가리켰다. \"'저 자신을 위한 배려는 일절 청하지 않습니다. 요람에 있을 적부터, 갚을 길 없는 금전 채무의 희생양이었던 저는, 늘 비천한 처지의 노리개이자 장난감이었습니다. 치욕, 궁핍, 절망, 광기가, 한꺼번에 혹은 따로따로, 제 인생 행로의 길동무였습니다.'\"\n\n미코버 씨가 자신을 이 음울한 재난들의 먹잇감이라고 묘사하는 그 감칠맛은, 편지를 읽어 내려가는 그 힘찬 어조에나 견줄 만했다. 그리고 정말 어느 문장을 제대로 한 방 먹였다 싶을 때, 머리를 한 번 끄덕여 그 문장에 바치는 일종의 경의에나 비길 만했다.\n\n\"'치욕과 궁핍과 절망과 광기가 켜켜이 쌓인 가운데, 저는 명목상으로는 위크필드 앤드 — 힙이라는 명칭으로 운영되나, 실제로는 — 힙 혼자 좌지우지하는 그 회사의 사무소 — 우리의 발랄한 이웃 갈리아 사람이라면 뷔로라 부를 — 에 들어갔습니다. 힙, 오로지 힙만이, 그 기계의 태엽입니다. 힙, 오로지 힙만이, 위조범이자 사기꾼입니다.'\"\n\n이 말에 흰빛보다 푸른빛이 더 도는 얼굴이 된 유라이아가, 편지를 갈기갈기 찢으려는 듯 와락 달려들었다. 미코버 씨는 솜씨인지 운인지 완벽한 기적을 부려, 다가오는 그의 손마디를 자로 후려쳐 오른손을 못 쓰게 만들었다. 그 손은 손목에서 뚝 꺾여, 부러지기라도 한 듯 축 늘어졌다. 그 일격은 나무토막을 후려친 듯한 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"이 빌어먹을 놈!\" 유라이아가 통증에 새로운 몸짓으로 비틀거리며 말했다. \"이 빚은 꼭 갚아 주마.\"\n\n\"한 번만 더 나한테 다가와 봐, 이 — 이 — 이 치욕덩어리 힙아.\" 미코버 씨가 헐떡이며 말했다. \"네 머리통이 사람의 머리통이거든, 내가 그걸 박살 내 주마. 덤벼, 덤벼 봐!\"\n\n미코버 씨가 자로 큰칼 막기 자세를 취하며 \"덤벼!\" 하고 외치고, 트래들스와 내가 그를 구석으로 밀어 넣어도 그가 처넣어 줄 때마다 한사코 다시 뛰쳐나오던 그 광경보다 더 우스꽝스러운 건 평생 본 적이 없는 것 같다 — 그 순간조차 나는 그게 우습다는 걸 느끼고 있었다.\n\n그의 적은 한참 동안 다친 손을 부여잡고 혼잣말로 중얼거리더니, 천천히 목도리를 풀어 손을 싸맸다. 그러고는 그것을 성한 손에 쥔 채, 뚱한 얼굴을 떨군 채로 책상에 걸터앉았다.\n\n미코버 씨는 충분히 가라앉자 편지를 계속 읽어 나갔다.\n\n\"'제가 — 힙의 일을 맡는 대가로 받기로 한 봉급은,'\" 그 단어 앞에서는 늘 한 박자 쉬고, 놀랄 만큼 힘차게 그 말을 내뱉었다. \"'주급 이십이 실링 육 펜스라는 쥐꼬리만 한 푼돈 말고는 정해진 바가 없었습니다. 나머지는 제 직무 수행의 가치에 달려 있었습니다. 달리, 더 적나라하게 말하자면, 제 천성의 비열함, 제 동기의 탐욕, 제 가족의 가난, 그리고 저와 — 힙 사이의 전반적인 도덕적(아니, 차라리 부도덕적) 유사성에 달려 있었습니다. 미코버 부인과, 시들었으나 자라나는 우리 가족을 부양하기 위해, 제가 곧 — 힙에게 — 돈을 미리 빌려 달라고 간청해야 하는 처지가 되었음을 굳이 말할 필요가 있을까요? 이런 처지가 — 힙에 의해 — 미리 예견된 것이었음을 말할 필요가 있을까요? 그 가불금이 이 나라의 법률 제도가 인정하는 차용증서며 그 밖의 비슷한 증서로 담보되었음을? 그리하여 제가, 그가 저를 잡으려고 쳐 둔 거미줄에 얽혀 들었음을?'\"\n\n이 불행한 처지를 묘사하면서 미코버 씨가 자기 서간 솜씨를 즐기는 모양새는, 정작 그 현실이 그에게 안겨 주었을 어떤 고통이나 불안마저 능가하는 듯했다. 그는 읽어 나갔다.\n\n\"'바로 그때부터 — 힙은 — 자기의 그 사악한 사업을 처리하는 데 꼭 필요한 만큼만 제게 속내를 털어놓는 호의를 베풀기 시작했습니다. 바로 그때부터 저는, 셰익스피어식으로 표현하자면, 시들고 야위고 말라 가기 시작했습니다. 제 직무가 끊임없이 동원되는 데가, 사무를 위조하고, 제가 W 씨라 칭하려는 어떤 인물을 미혹하는 일임을 알게 되었습니다. 그 W 씨는 온갖 수단으로 기만당하고, 무지 속에 갇히고, 농락당했습니다. 그러면서도 내내, 그 악당 — 힙은 — 그토록 모진 학대를 당한 그 신사에게 한없는 감사와 한없는 우정을 공언하고 있었습니다. 이것만으로도 충분히 고약했지만, 그 철학자다운 덴마크인이, 엘리자베스 시대의 그 빛나는 보배를 돋보이게 하는 보편적 적용성으로써 말했듯, 더 고약한 것이 남아 있습니다!'\"\n\n미코버 씨는 이렇게 인용으로 말끔하게 마무리 지은 것에 어찌나 흡족했는지, 읽던 자리를 놓쳤다는 핑계로, 자신과 우리에게 그 문장을 두 번째로 낭독하는 호사를 누렸다.\n\n\"'본 서신의 범위 안에서,'\" 그가 계속 읽어 나갔다. \"'제가 W 씨라 명명한 인물에게 영향을 끼친, 그리고 제가 암묵적으로 동조한 당사자였던 갖가지 소소한 비행을(다른 곳에 준비되어 있긴 합니다만) 상세히 나열할 생각은 없습니다. 봉급이냐 무봉급이냐, 빵집이냐 무빵집이냐, 생존이냐 비생존이냐 하는 제 안의 갈등이 끝났을 때, 제 목적은, 그 기회를 활용해, — 힙이 — 그 신사에게 끼친 심대한 잘못과 손해를 저지른 중대한 비행을 밝혀내고 폭로하는 것이었습니다. 안에서 조용히 타이르는 충고자와, 그에 못지않게 마음을 울리며 호소하는 바깥의 충고자 — 간략히 W 양이라 칭하겠습니다 — 에게 자극받아, 저는 결코 수월치 않은 은밀한 조사라는 과업에 착수하여, 제가 알고 정보를 입수하고 믿는 바로는, 열두 달이 넘는 기간에 걸쳐 그것을 이어 왔습니다.'\"\n\n그는 이 대목을 마치 의회 법령이라도 읽듯이 읽었고, 그 말들의 울림에 위풍당당하게 활기를 되찾는 듯했다.\n\n\"'— 힙에 대한 제 고발은,'\" 그가 만일을 대비해 자를 왼팔 밑 편한 위치로 끌어당기고 그를 흘끗 보며 읽어 나갔다. \"'다음과 같습니다.'\"\n\n우리 모두가 숨을 죽였던 것 같다. 유라이아도 분명 숨을 죽였다.\n\n\"'첫째,'\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"'W 씨의 사무 능력과 기억력이, 제가 굳이 들어가 따질 필요도 마땅함도 없는 까닭으로 인해, 약해지고 흐려졌을 때, — 힙은 — 모든 공식 거래를 의도적으로 헝클어뜨리고 복잡하게 만들었습니다. W 씨가 사무에 임하기에 가장 부적합할 때면, — 힙은 — 늘 곁에 붙어 그를 억지로 사무에 임하게 했습니다. 그는 그런 상황에서 W 씨의 서명을 중요한 문서에 받아 냈는데, 그것들을 아무 중요치 않은 다른 문서라고 둘러댔습니다. 그는 W 씨를 꾀어, 이런 식으로 십이 파운드 육 실링 십사, 이 펜스 구에 이르는 특정 신탁 자금 한 건을 인출할 권한을 자기에게 주도록 했고, 그것을 이미 처리되었거나 실은 존재한 적도 없는 가짜 사무 경비와 결손을 메우는 데 썼습니다. 그는 이 모든 과정에, 그것이 W 씨 자신의 부정한 의도에서 비롯되어 W 씨 자신의 부정한 행위로 이루어진 것 같은 외양을 입혔고, 그 이후로 줄곧 그것을 빌미로 그를 괴롭히고 옭아맸습니다.'\"\n\n\"이걸 증명해 봐, 이 코퍼필드야!\" 유라이아가 머리를 위협적으로 흔들며 말했다. \"다 때가 되면 알게 될 거야!\"\n\n\"— 힙에게 물어보세요, 트래들스 씨, 위크필드 씨 다음에 그 집에 살았던 사람 아닙니까.\" 미코버 씨가 편지에서 잠시 손을 떼며 말했다. \"물어보시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그 멍청이가 직접 살았지 — 지금도 거기 살고.\" 유라이아가 깔보듯 말했다.\n\n\"— 힙에게 물어보세요, 그 집에서 수첩을 가지고 있던 적이 있는지.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"물어보시겠습니까?\"\n\n유라이아의 비쩍 마른 손이 턱을 긁다 말고 저도 모르게 멈추는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"아니면 물어보세요.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그가 그 집에서 수첩을 태운 적이 있는지를요. 만일 그렇다고 하면서 그 재가 어디 있느냐고 묻거든, 윌킨스 미코버에게 알아보라고 하세요. 그러면 자기한테 전혀 이로울 게 없는 소식을 듣게 될 테니까요!\"\n\n미코버 씨가 이 말을 의기양양하게 멋들어진 손짓으로 내뱉자, 그 어머니가 잔뜩 겁먹는 강력한 효과가 났다. 어머니는 몹시 동요하며 부르짖었다.\n\n\"유리, 유리! 비굴하게 굽혀서 타협하렴, 얘야!\"\n\n\"어머니!\" 그가 쏘아붙였다. \"가만히 좀 있을래요? 겁에 질려서 자기가 무슨 말을, 무슨 뜻으로 하는지도 모르면서. 비굴하게라니!\" 그가 나를 쳐다보며 으르렁대듯 되풀이했다. \"내가 저들 중 몇몇은 진작부터 한참 동안 비굴하게 굽실거리게 만들어 왔어, 나 자신은 비굴했어도 말이지!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 점잖게 턱을 크라바트에 추슬러 넣고는, 곧 자기 작문을 이어 나갔다.\n\n\"'둘째. 힙은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 제가 알고 정보를 입수하고 믿는 바로는—'\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 안 통할 거야.\" 유라이아가 안도하며 중얼거렸다. \"어머니, 가만히 좀 있어요.\"\n\n\"통하고도, 끝내 당신을 끝장낼 무언가를 마련하도록 애써 보지요, 선생, 머지않아서요.\" 미코버 씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"'둘째. 힙은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 제가 알고 정보를 입수하고 믿는 바로는, 여러 장부 기재와 책자와 문서에 W 씨의 서명을 조직적으로 위조했습니다. 그리고 한 건에 있어서는 명백히 그리하였으며, 그것은 제가 증명할 수 있습니다. 즉, 다음과 같은 방식으로, 다시 말하면 이러합니다.'\"\n\n또다시 미코버 씨는 이렇게 말을 격식 있게 켜켜이 쌓아 올리는 데서 감칠맛을 느꼈는데, 그것은 그의 경우엔 우스꽝스럽게 드러났을망정, 말하건대 결코 그만의 별난 버릇은 아니었다. 나는 살아오면서 숱한 사람들에게서 그것을 보아 왔다. 그건 일반적인 법칙인 듯싶다. 이를테면 법정 선서를 할 때, 진술인들은 하나의 뜻을 표현하려고 멋진 말 여러 개를 잇달아 늘어놓는 대목에 이르면 무척이나 즐거워하는 것 같다. 자기는 무엇무엇을 철저히 혐오하고, 증오하고, 배격한다는 식으로 말이다. 옛날의 저주문들도 같은 원리로 감칠맛이 났다. 우리는 말의 횡포를 운운하지만, 우리 자신도 말을 횡포하게 부려 먹기를 좋아한다. 우리는 큰 행사가 있을 때 우리 시중을 들 군더더기 말들을 잔뜩 거느리기를 즐긴다. 그래야 중해 보이고 듣기에도 그럴싸하다고 여기는 것이다. 우리가 의전 행사 때 입는 제복의 의미에 그리 까다롭지 않고 그저 화려하고 수만 많으면 되듯이, 말의 의미나 필요성도, 그 말이 그저 거창하게 줄지어 늘어서기만 한다면, 부차적인 고려 사항일 뿐이다. 그리고 개인이 제복을 너무 거창하게 차려입어 곤경에 빠지듯, 또는 노예가 수가 너무 많아지면 주인에게 들고일어나듯, 너무 많은 말의 종자(從者)를 거느린 탓에 숱한 큰 곤경에 빠졌고 또 앞으로 더 큰 곤경에 빠질 어느 나라를 나는 댈 수 있을 것 같다.\n\n미코버 씨는 거의 입맛을 다시며 읽어 나갔다.\n\n\"'즉, 다음과 같은 방식으로, 다시 말하면 이러합니다. W 씨가 병약하여, 그의 사망이 어떤 사실의 발각으로 이어지고, W 가문에 대한 — 힙의 — 지배력의 몰락으로 이어질 개연성의 범위 안에 있었으므로 — 즉, 저 윌킨스 미코버, 아래에 서명한 본인이 추정하건대 — 그의 딸의 효성을 은밀히 부추겨 동업 사무에 대한 일체의 조사가 영영 이뤄지지 못하게 막지 않는 한 — 상기 — 힙은 — W 씨가 발행한 것처럼 꾸민 차용 증서 한 장을, 앞서 언급한 십이 파운드 육 실링 십사, 이 펜스 구에 이자를 더한 금액으로, 곁에 마련해 두는 것이 상책이라 여겼습니다. 그 증서에는 그 돈이 W 씨의 불명예를 막기 위해 — 힙이 — W 씨에게 빌려준 것이라 적혀 있으나, 실은 그 돈을 그가 빌려준 적이 결코 없으며, 게다가 진작에 채워 넣어진 것입니다. 이 증서에 W 씨가 작성하고 윌킨스 미코버가 입회 서명한 것처럼 되어 있는 서명들은 — 힙이 — 위조한 것입니다. 저는 그의 필체와 수첩에서, W 씨 서명을 비슷하게 흉내 낸 것 여러 점을 제 손에 가지고 있는데, 여기저기 불에 그슬렸으나 누구든 알아볼 수 있을 정도입니다. 저는 그러한 문서에 입회 서명한 적이 결코 없습니다. 그리고 저는 그 증서 자체를 제 손에 가지고 있습니다.'\" 유라이아 힙이 흠칫하며 주머니에서 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내 어느 서랍을 열다가, 별안간 자기가 뭘 하고 있는지 깨닫고는, 들여다보지도 않은 채 다시 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"'그리고 저는 그 증서를,'\" 미코버 씨가 마치 설교의 본문이라도 되는 양 두리번거리며 다시 읽었다. \"'제 손에 가지고 있습니다 — 그러니까, 이 글을 쓴 오늘 이른 아침까지는 가지고 있었으나, 그 후 트래들스 씨에게 넘겼습니다.'\"\n\n\"틀림없는 사실입니다.\" 트래들스가 동의했다.\n\n\"유리, 유리!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"비굴하게 굽혀서 타협하렴. 우리 아들은 분명 비굴하게 굽힐 거예요, 여러분, 생각할 시간만 주신다면요. 코퍼필드 씨, 저 애가 늘 아주 비굴했다는 거, 분명 아시잖아요, 선생님!\"\n\n아들은 쓸모없다고 내던진 그 낡은 수법에, 어머니는 여전히 매달리고 있었으니, 참으로 묘한 일이었다.\n\n\"어머니.\" 그가 손을 싸맨 손수건을 짜증스럽게 한 번 깨물며 말했다. \"차라리 장전한 총이나 가져다 나한테 쏘는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 난 널 사랑한단다, 유리.\" 힙 부인이 외쳤다. 나는 그녀가 정말로 그를 사랑했음을, 또 아무리 이상해 보일지언정 그가 그녀를 사랑했음을 의심치 않는다. 하긴 둘은 천생연분인 한 쌍이긴 했다. \"그리고 난 네가 신사분들을 자극하면서 더 큰 위험에 빠지는 걸 차마 못 듣겠구나. 저 신사분이 위층에서 일이 들통났다고 말해 줬을 때, 내가 처음부터 그랬어. 네가 비굴하게 굽혀서 잘못을 바로잡을 거라고 내가 보증한다고. 아, 보세요, 제가 이렇게 비굴하잖아요, 여러분, 저 애 말은 신경 쓰지 마세요!\"\n\n\"아니, 저기 코퍼필드 있잖아요, 어머니.\" 그가 자기의 모든 적의가 향한 나에게, 이 발각의 주모자인 나에게 비쩍 마른 손가락을 겨누며 성나게 되받았다. 나는 그 오해를 굳이 풀어 주지 않았다. \"저기 코퍼필드 말이에요, 어머니가 함부로 지껄인 것보다 말을 좀 줄이라고 어머니한테 백 파운드라도 줬을 사람이라고요!\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없잖니, 유리.\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"네가 그렇게 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 위험 속으로 달려드는 꼴을 두고 볼 수가 없구나. 늘 그래 왔듯이 비굴하게 굽히는 게 낫지.\"\n\n그는 잠시 동안 손수건을 깨물고 있다가, 나를 향해 인상을 쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"더 내놓을 게 뭐가 있어? 있으면 어디 계속해 봐. 왜 날 쳐다보는 거야?\"\n\n미코버 씨는 곧장 편지를 다시 집어 들었다. 그토록 흡족해하던 공연으로 되돌아가게 되어 반가운 기색이었다.\n\n\"'셋째. 그리고 마지막. 저는 이제, — 힙의 — 거짓 장부와 — 힙의 — 진짜 비망록을 통해, 그것도 일부 훼손된 그 수첩(미코버 부인이 우리가 현재의 거처를 차지하면서, 집 안 난로에서 태운 재를 받는 통, 즉 함에서 우연히 발견했을 당시에는 제가 그 내용을 이해할 수 없었습니다)으로 시작하여, 다음을 증명할 수 있는 처지에 있습니다. 즉, 불행한 W 씨의 약점과 결점과, 심지어 미덕과, 부모로서의 애정과, 명예심까지가, 수년에 걸쳐 — 힙에 의해 — 농락당하고 그의 비열한 목적에 맞게 비틀렸다는 것을. W 씨가 수년에 걸쳐, 상상할 수 있는 온갖 방식으로 기만당하고 약탈당하여, 그 탐욕스럽고 거짓되고 게걸스러운 — 힙의 — 금전적 치부에 이바지했다는 것을. — 힙의 — 골몰한 목적이, 이득 다음으로, W 씨와 W 양을(후자에 관한 그의 더 음흉한 속셈에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠습니다) 완전히 자기 손아귀에 넣는 것이었다는 것을. 불과 몇 달 전에 완수된 그의 마지막 행각이, W 씨를 꾀어 동업 지분 포기 증서를, 심지어 자기 집 가구 자체에 대한 매도 증서까지 작성하게 한 것이었다는 것을. — 힙이 — 매년 사분기 결산일 네 차례에 어김없이 또박또박 지급하기로 한 일정 연금을 대가로 말입니다. 이 그물이 — W 씨가 관리인으로 있는 재산에 관한, 그가 무모하고 그릇된 투기에 손을 대어 도덕적으로나 법적으로나 책임져야 할 돈이 수중에 없었을지도 모를 시기의, 놀라우면서도 거짓으로 꾸며진 회계 보고에서 시작하여 — 실은 — 힙에게서 — 나온 돈을, 또는 그러한 투기 따위를 핑계로 — 힙이 — 사기로 가로채거나 W 씨 본인에게서 떼어먹은 돈을, 엄청난 이자를 붙여 빌려주는 척하는 행위로 이어지고 — 양심 없는 온갖 협잡의 잡다한 목록으로 영속화되어 — 점점 두꺼워진 끝에, 불행한 W 씨는 그 너머의 세상을 볼 수 없게 되었습니다. 처지로 보나, 다른 모든 희망으로 보나, 명예로 보나, 스스로 믿기에 파산한 그가, 유일하게 의지한 것은 사람의 탈을 쓴 괴물이었으니,'\" — 미코버 씨는 이 새로운 표현의 묘미를 한껏 살렸다 — \"'그자는 자기를 그에게 없어서는 안 될 존재로 만들어, 그의 파멸을 이룬 것입니다. 이 모든 것을 제가 증명하겠습니다. 어쩌면 훨씬 더 많은 것을요!'\"\n\n나는 내 곁에서 반은 기쁨에, 반은 슬픔에 잠겨 울고 있던 아그네스에게 몇 마디를 속삭였다. 미코버 씨가 다 끝낸 줄 알고, 우리 사이에 무언가 움직임이 일었다. 그러자 그가 더없이 엄숙하게 \"양해해 주십시오.\" 하고는, 더없이 가라앉은 기분과 더없이 강렬한 즐거움이 뒤섞인 채로, 편지의 맺음말로 나아갔다.\n\n\"'이제 저는 글을 맺습니다. 제게 남은 일이라곤 이 고발들을 입증하는 것, 그러고는 박복한 제 식구와 더불어, 우리가 한낱 짐이 되는 듯한 이 풍경에서 사라지는 것뿐입니다. 그건 곧 이루어질 일입니다. 우리 갓난아기가, 우리 식구 가운데 가장 연약한 일원인 만큼, 먼저 영양실조로 숨질 것이고, 다음 차례로 우리 쌍둥이가 뒤따르리라고 어렵잖이 짐작할 수 있습니다. 그렇게 되라죠! 저로 말하자면, 저의 캔터베리 순례가 이미 많은 일을 했고, 민사 소송에 따른 투옥과 궁핍이 머지않아 더 많은 일을 할 것입니다. 부디, 이 조사의 — 더없이 보잘것없는 성과들을, 고된 생업의 압박 속에, 사람을 갉아먹는 곤궁한 두려움 아래, 동틀 녘에, 이슬 맺힌 저녁에, 밤의 그늘 속에서, 굳이 악마라 부를 것도 없는 자의 감시하는 눈길 아래, 더디게나마 짜 맞춰 온 그 노고와 위험이 — 그것이 완성되었을 때 그것을 옳은 쓸모로 돌리려는 부모 된 가난의 분투와 어우러져 — 제 화장용 장작더미에 달콤한 물 몇 방울을 뿌려 주는 격이 되기를 바랍니다. 저는 그 이상은 바라지 않습니다. 부디, 제가 감히 견줄 엄두도 못 낼 어느 용감하고 걸출한 해군 영웅에 대해 그러했듯이, 저에 대해서도 다만 공정하게, 제가 한 일은, 돈벌이나 사사로운 목적을 마다하고 했노라고 말해 주시기를 바랄 뿐입니다.\n\n영국과 가정과 미녀를 위하여.\n\n언제나 변함없이, 운운, 운운, 윌킨스 미코버.'\"\n\n미코버 씨는 몹시 감격하면서도 여전히 강렬하게 자기 흥에 겨워, 편지를 접어, 고모님이 간직하고 싶어 할 만한 물건이라는 듯 절을 하며 건넸다.\n\n오래전 처음 방문했을 때 보았듯이, 그 방에는 철제 금고가 하나 있었다. 열쇠는 거기 꽂혀 있었다. 어떤 다급한 의심이 유라이아를 후려친 듯했다. 그는 미코버 씨를 한 번 흘끗 보더니, 그쪽으로 가서 철컹 소리가 나게 문을 와락 열어젖혔다. 텅 비어 있었다.\n\n\"장부들 어디 갔어?\" 그가 끔찍한 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"어떤 도둑놈이 장부를 훔쳐 갔어!\"\n\n미코버 씨가 자로 자기 몸을 톡톡 쳤다. \"내가 가져갔지, 늘 하던 대로 네게서 열쇠를 받았을 때 — 다만 조금 일찍 받아서 — 오늘 아침에 열었지.\"\n\n\"불안해할 것 없습니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그것들은 제 손에 들어왔습니다. 제가 말씀드린 권한에 따라 잘 보관하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"장물을 받아 챙기는 거야, 어?\" 유라이아가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이런 상황에서는,\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"그렇습니다.\"\n\n그토록 깊이 침묵하며 주의 깊게 지켜보던 고모님이, 유라이아 힙에게 와락 달려들어 두 손으로 그의 멱살을 움켜쥐는 것을 보았을 때, 내 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가!\n\n\"내가 뭘 원하는지 알지?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"구속복이겠지요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n* 구속복: 정신 이상자나 난폭한 사람이 팔을 못 쓰게 묶는 옷.\n\"아니. 내 재산이오!\" 고모님이 되받았다. \"아그네스야, 얘야, 그게 정말로 네 아버지 손에 날아간 줄로 믿고 있는 동안에는, 나는 그것이 투자용으로 여기 맡겨졌다는 걸 한마디도 — 얘야, 트롯에게조차 한마디도 안 했다, 저 애도 알다시피 — 입 밖에 내지 않았다. 하지만 이제 이 작자한테 그 책임이 있다는 걸 알았으니, 그걸 받아 내고 말겠다! 트롯, 이리 와서 저자한테서 그걸 빼앗아라!\"\n\n고모님이 그 순간, 그가 자기 재산을 목도리 속에 감춰 두기라도 한 줄로 여겼는지는 정말이지 알 수 없으나, 어쨌든 정말 그런 줄 아는 사람처럼 그것을 잡아당긴 것만은 분명했다. 나는 얼른 둘 사이에 끼어들어, 그가 부당하게 손에 넣은 모든 것을 한 푼도 빠짐없이 토해 내도록 우리 모두가 단단히 챙기겠노라고 고모님을 안심시켰다. 이 말과, 잠깐의 생각 끝에 고모님은 진정되었다. 그러나 고모님은 자기가 한 일에 조금도 당황하지 않았고(보닛에 대해서는 그렇게 말 못 하겠지만), 침착하게 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n지난 몇 분 동안, 힙 부인은 아들에게 \"비굴하게 굽히라\"고 아우성쳤고, 우리 모두에게 차례차례 무릎을 꿇으며 더없이 터무니없는 약속들을 늘어놓고 있었다. 아들은 어머니를 자기 의자에 앉히고, 곁에 뚱하니 서서, 거칠지는 않게 한 손으로 어머니 팔을 붙든 채, 험악한 눈초리로 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 어떻게 했으면 좋겠어?\"\n\n\"무엇을 해야 하는지 내가 말해 주지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"저 코퍼필드는 혀가 없나?\" 유라이아가 중얼거렸다. \"누가 저놈 혀를 잘라 갔다고 거짓말 안 하고 말해 줄 수만 있다면, 내가 당신을 위해 어지간한 건 다 해 주겠는데.\"\n\n\"우리 유라이아는 비굴하게 굽힐 작정이에요!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"저 애 말은 신경 쓰지 마세요, 친절하신 여러분!\"\n\n\"해야 할 것은,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"이것입니다. 첫째, 우리가 들은 그 지분 포기 증서를, 지금 — 여기서 — 나에게 넘겨야 합니다.\"\n\n\"내가 그걸 안 가지고 있다면?\" 그가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은 가지고 있지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그러니, 알다시피, 그렇게 가정하진 않겠습니다.\" 그리고 내 옛 학교 친구의 명석한 머리와, 꾸밈없고 끈기 있고 실속 있는 양식(良識)을 내가 진정으로 제대로 평가한 건 이때가 처음이었음을 고백하지 않을 수 없다. \"그다음,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"당신은 그 탐욕으로 손에 넣은 모든 것을 토해 내고, 마지막 한 푼까지 되돌려 줄 채비를 해야 합니다. 모든 동업 장부와 서류는 우리 손에 남겨 둬야 하고, 당신의 모든 장부와 서류, 두 종류의 모든 금전 계정과 증권도 마찬가지입니다. 한마디로, 여기 있는 모든 것을요.\"\n\n\"꼭 그래야 하나? 난 그건 모르겠는데.\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"그건 생각할 시간이 좀 필요해.\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"하지만 그동안, 모든 것이 우리가 만족할 만큼 처리될 때까지, 우리는 이 물건들을 우리 손에 두겠습니다. 그리고 당신에게 청하건대 — 한마디로, 강제하건대 — 당신 방에만 머물며 누구와도 연락하지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"안 그럴 거야!\" 유라이아가 욕설을 내뱉으며 말했다.\n\n\"메이드스톤 감옥이 더 안전한 구금 장소지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그리고 법이 우리의 권리를 바로잡는 데는 더 오래 걸릴지도 모르고, 당신만큼 완벽하게 바로잡아 주지 못할지도 모르지만, 당신을 벌하리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없습니다. 어허, 그건 당신도 나만큼 잘 알지 않습니까! 코퍼필드, 길드홀로 가서 관리 두 사람만 데려와 주겠나?\"\n\n여기서 힙 부인이 다시 터져 나와, 무릎을 꿇고 아그네스에게 자기네 편을 들어 달라고 울며 매달렸다. 아들은 아주 비굴하고, 다 사실이며, 아들이 우리가 원하는 대로 하지 않으면 자기가 하겠다는 둥, 같은 취지의 말을 잔뜩 외쳐 댔다. 사랑하는 아들에 대한 두려움으로 반쯤 제정신이 아니었던 것이다. 그에게 조금이라도 배짱이 있었다면 무슨 짓을 했을까 묻는 것은, 똥개에게 호랑이의 기상이 있었다면 무슨 짓을 했을까 묻는 것과 같으리라. 그는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 겁쟁이였다. 그리고 그 비루한 인생의 어느 때 못지않게, 뚱한 표정과 굴욕감을 통해 자신의 비겁한 본성을 드러냈다.\n\n\"그만!\" 그가 나에게 으르렁대며, 화끈거리는 얼굴을 손으로 닦았다. \"어머니, 시끄러운 소리 좀 그쳐요. 좋아! 저 증서 가져가라고 해. 가서 가져와요!\"\n\n\"부인을 좀 도와드리시지요, 디크 씨.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n자기 소임을 자랑스러워하며, 또 그것을 잘 알아듣고서, 디크 씨는 양치기 개가 양을 따라가듯 그녀를 따라갔다. 하지만 힙 부인은 그를 별로 애먹이지 않았으니, 증서를 가지고 돌아왔을 뿐 아니라, 그것이 든 상자까지 가져왔는데, 거기서 우리는 은행 통장과, 나중에 쓸모 있게 된 다른 서류 몇 가지를 발견했다.\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 이것이 들어오자 트래들스가 말했다. \"자, 힙 씨, 이제 방으로 물러가서 생각하셔도 됩니다. 특히, 부디 잘 새겨 두세요. 여기 있는 모두를 대신하여 내가 당신에게 선언하건대, 해야 할 일은 단 한 가지뿐이고, 그것은 내가 설명한 그대로이며, 지체 없이 이뤄져야 한다는 것을요.\"\n\n유라이아는 땅바닥에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 손을 턱에 댄 채로 방을 가로질러 발을 질질 끌며 가다가, 문 앞에서 멈춰 서서 말했다.\n\n\"코퍼필드, 난 늘 널 미워해 왔어. 넌 늘 벼락출세한 놈이었고, 늘 나한테 맞서 왔지.\"\n\n\"전에도 한 번 말했던 것 같은데,\" 내가 말했다. \"탐욕과 교활함으로 온 세상에 맞서 온 건 바로 너야. 앞으로 이걸 곰곰이 새겨 두면 너한테 득이 될지도 모르지. 도가 지나쳐 제 꾀에 제가 넘어가지 않은 탐욕과 교활함은 이 세상에 일찍이 없었다는 걸 말이야. 그건 죽음만큼이나 확실한 일이지.\"\n\n\"아니면, 학교에서(내가 그 숱한 비굴함을 주워 배운 바로 그 학교지) 가르치던 것만큼이나 확실하겠지. 아홉 시부터 열한 시까진 노동이 저주라고 가르치고, 열한 시부터 한 시까진 그게 축복이고 즐거움이고 위엄이고 뭐고 다 된다고 가르치던 거 말이야, 어?\" 그가 비웃으며 말했다. \"넌 그자들만큼이나 앞뒤 안 맞게 설교를 늘어놓는군. 비굴함이 안 통한다고? 내 생각엔, 그게 없었으면 나의 그 신사 동업자를 구워삶지 못했을걸. — 미코버, 이 늙은 깡패야, 너한텐 꼭 갚아 주마!\"\n\n미코버 씨는 그와 그의 쭉 뻗은 손가락 따위 더없이 당당하게 무시하며, 그가 문밖으로 슬그머니 빠져나갈 때까지 가슴팍을 한껏 부풀려 보이고는, 나에게로 몸을 돌려, \"자기와 미코버 부인 사이에 상호 신뢰가 회복되는 것을 목격하는\" 만족을 베풀어 주겠노라고 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 좌중 전체에게, 그 가슴 뭉클한 광경을 함께 음미해 보자고 청했다.\n\n\"오랫동안 미코버 부인과 저 사이에 드리워져 있던 장막이, 이제 걷혔습니다.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그리하여 제 아이들과 그들의 창조주가 다시 한번 대등한 처지에서 마주할 수 있게 되었습니다.\"\n\n우리 모두 그에게 무척 고마웠고, 어수선하고 어지러운 마음이 허락하는 한, 다들 그 고마움을 보이고 싶어 했으니, 아마 다 함께 갔을 것이다. 다만 아그네스가, 아직 희망의 동틀 녘 이상은 감당하지 못하는 아버지에게 돌아가야 했고, 또 누군가는 유라이아를 안전하게 붙잡아 두어야 했다. 그래서 트래들스가 뒤엣일을 위해 남았다가 곧 디크 씨와 교대하기로 했다. 그리고 디크 씨와 고모님과 나는 미코버 씨와 함께 집으로 갔다. 내가 그토록 많은 신세를 진 그 사랑스러운 처녀와 황급히 헤어지면서, 그녀가 아마도 그날 아침 — 그녀의 더 굳건한 결심에도 불구하고 — 무엇으로부터 구원받았는지를 생각하니, 나를 미코버 씨와 알게 해 준 내 어린 시절의 그 모진 불행들에 진심으로 감사한 마음이 들었다.\n\n그의 집은 멀지 않았다. 그리고 길로 난 문이 곧장 거실로 통해 있어서, 그가 자기만의 그 다급한 기세로 안으로 와락 뛰어들자, 우리는 단숨에 그 가족의 품 한복판에 서 있게 되었다. 미코버 씨가 \"에마! 내 생명이여!\" 하고 외치며 미코버 부인의 품으로 달려들었다. 미코버 부인은 비명을 지르며 미코버 씨를 끌어안았다. 미코버 부인이 나에게 보낸 지난번 편지 속의 그 모르는 손님을 어르고 있던 미코버 양은, 적잖이 감격했다. 그 모르는 손님은 깡충깡충 뛰었다. 쌍둥이는 거추장스럽지만 천진한 갖가지 몸짓으로 기쁨을 드러냈다. 일찌감치 좌절에 마음이 비뚤어진 듯하고 표정도 시무룩해진 미코버 도령은, 더 나은 감정에 굴복하여 엉엉 울었다.\n\n\"에마!\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 마음에서 먹구름이 걷혔소. 한때 우리 사이에 그토록 오래 지켜졌던 상호 신뢰가 회복되었으니, 다시는 끊기는 일이 없을 거요. 자, 가난이여, 어서 오라!\" 미코버 씨가 눈물을 흘리며 외쳤다. \"비참함이여 어서 오라, 집 없음이여 어서 오라, 굶주림과 누더기와 비바람과 거지 신세여 어서 오라! 상호 신뢰가 끝까지 우리를 떠받쳐 주리니!\"\n\n이런 말들과 함께, 미코버 씨는 미코버 부인을 의자에 앉히고, 온 식구를 빙 돌아가며 끌어안았다. 그리고 내가 보기엔 도무지 반가울 게 없을 갖가지 암담한 앞날을 반긴 다음, 식구들에게 캔터베리 거리로 나가 합창이나 하자고 청했다. 달리 그들을 부지할 방도가 없다는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 미코버 부인이 벅찬 감정에 그만 까무러쳐 버려서, 합창을 갖출 생각을 하기에 앞서, 제일 먼저 할 일은 그녀를 깨우는 것이었다. 고모님과 미코버 씨가 그 일을 했다. 그러고 나서 고모님이 소개되었고, 미코버 부인은 나를 알아보았다.\n\n\"용서하세요, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨.\" 가엾은 부인이 나에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제가 몸이 튼튼치 못한 데다, 미코버 씨와 저 사이의 지난번 오해가 풀리고 나니 처음엔 너무 벅찼답니다.\"\n\n\"이게 댁의 식구가 다인가요, 부인?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"지금으로서는 더는 없습니다.\" 미코버 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"아이고, 그 말이 아니라요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내 말은, 이 아이들이 다 댁의 자식이냐는 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인.\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"틀림없는 사실입니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 저 맏아드님 말인데요,\" 고모님이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"무얼 시키며 키우셨나요?\"\n\n\"제가 여기 왔을 때 품었던 희망은,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"윌킨스를 교회에 들여보내는 것이었습니다. 아니, 좀 더 엄밀히 말하자면, 성가대에 넣는 것이라 해야겠군요. 그러나 이 도시가 마땅히 그토록 이름난 그 유서 깊은 대성당에는 테너 자리가 비어 있지 않았습니다. 그래서 이 아이는 — 한마디로, 성스러운 건물보다는 술집에서 노래하는 버릇이 들고 말았습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 저 애는 마음씨가 착해요.\" 미코버 부인이 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지, 여보.\" 미코버 씨가 맞장구쳤다. \"저 애가 유독 마음씨가 착하다는 거야 그렇겠지만, 저 애가 그 착한 마음을 어떤 방향으로든 실천에 옮기는 건 아직 본 적이 없구려.\"\n\n미코버 도령의 시무룩한 표정이 다시 돌아왔고, 그는 다소 발끈하며, 자기더러 어쩌라는 거냐고 따져 물었다. 자기가 새로 태어난 게 아니듯, 목수나 마차 도장공으로 태어나기라도 했느냐고? 옆 거리로 가서 약방을 차릴 수 있느냐고? 다음 순회 재판소로 달려가 자기가 변호사라고 선언할 수 있느냐고? 오페라 극장에 힘으로 밀고 들어가 폭력으로 성공할 수 있느냐고? 무언가를 익히도록 키워지지 않고서야, 무슨 일을 할 수 있느냐고?\n\n고모님은 잠시 곰곰이 생각하더니 말했다.\n\n\"미코버 씨, 어찌 한 번도 이민에 생각을 돌려 보지 않으셨는지 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"부인.\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"그건 제 젊은 시절의 꿈이었고, 좀 더 무르익은 나이의 헛된 갈망이었습니다.\" 그건 그렇고, 그가 평생 그런 걸 생각해 본 적이 없다는 걸 나는 확신한다.\n\n\"그래요?\" 고모님이 나를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"아니, 두 분과 댁 식구에게, 지금 이민을 떠나신다면 그게 얼마나 좋은 일이겠어요, 미코버 부부.\"\n\n\"자본이지요, 부인, 자본이요.\" 미코버 씨가 침울하게 역설했다.\n\n\"그게 가장 큰, 아니 유일한 어려움이랍니다, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨.\" 그의 아내가 동의했다.\n\n\"자본이라뇨?\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"하지만 당신들은 우리에게 큰 신세를 — 큰 신세를 지웠다고 해야겠지요, 분명 이 불길에서 많은 게 나올 테니까 — 그러니 우리가 자본을 마련해 주는 것의 절반만큼이라도 좋은 일을, 당신들을 위해 무엇을 더 해 줄 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"그걸 거저 받을 수는 없습니다.\" 미코버 씨가 열의와 활기에 가득 차 말했다. \"하지만 충분한 액수를, 이를테면 연 오 퍼센트 이자로, 제 개인 책임 아래 — 이를테면 무슨 일이 생길 시간을 벌도록 각각 십이 개월, 십팔 개월, 이십사 개월짜리 어음으로 — 미리 빌릴 수만 있다면—\"\n\n\"빌릴 수 있느냐고요? 당신이 원하는 조건대로 빌릴 수 있고말고요, 빌려 드리지요.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"말씀만 하시면요. 자, 이제 두 분 다 이걸 생각해 보세요. 데이비드가 아는 사람 몇이 머잖아 오스트레일리아로 떠나요. 두 분이 가기로 정하시면, 왜 같은 배로 못 가겠어요? 서로 도울 수도 있고요. 자, 이제 이걸 생각해 보세요, 미코버 부부. 시간을 갖고, 잘 따져 보세요.\"\n\n\"꼭 한 가지, 친애하는 부인, 여쭙고 싶은 게 있는데요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"기후가, 제가 알기로는, 건강에 좋다지요?\"\n\n\"세상에서 제일 좋답니다!\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요.\" 미코버 부인이 대꾸했다. \"그럼 제 질문이 나옵니다. 자, 그 나라 사정이, 미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 사람이 사회적 지위에서 출세할 기회를 공정하게 누릴 만한 곳인가요? 지금 당장, 그이가 총독이라든가 그런 자리를 넘볼 수 있겠느냐고는 묻지 않겠습니다. 하지만 그이의 재능이 — 그거면 충분하고도 남으니까요 — 스스로 펼쳐지고, 제 나름의 발전을 이룰, 합당한 길이 열려 있을까요?\"\n\n\"어디에도 그보다 나은 길은 없지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"처신을 바르게 하고 부지런한 사람한테는요.\"\n\n\"처신을 바르게 하고,\" 미코버 부인이 더없이 또렷한 사무적 태도로 되풀이했다. \"부지런한 사람한테요. 바로 그거예요. 오스트레일리아가 미코버 씨의 정당한 활동 무대라는 게 제겐 분명해졌어요!\"\n\n\"제가 확신하건대, 친애하는 부인.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그곳이, 지금 형편으로는, 저와 제 식구를 위한 땅, 유일한 땅이며, 그 해안에서 무언가 비범한 일이 생길 것입니다. 그곳은 — 비교적 말해서 — 거리도 멀지 않지요. 그리고 부인의 제안에 담긴 친절에는 마땅히 감사드려야겠지만, 분명히 말씀드리건대 그 부분은 그저 형식에 불과합니다.\"\n\n그가 어떻게 단숨에, 행운을 내다보는 더없이 낙천적인 사람이 되었는지를 내가 어찌 잊을 수 있으랴. 또 미코버 부인이 곧이어 캥거루의 습성에 대해 늘어놓던 그 이야기를 어찌 잊으랴! 장날의 그 캔터베리 거리를 떠올리면서, 우리와 함께 걸어 돌아오던 그를 떠올리지 않을 도리가 어디 있으랴. 그는 짐짓 취한 대담하고 떠돌이다운 태도로, 그 땅에 잠시 머무는 나그네의 정처 없는 버릇을 드러내 보였고, 황소들이 지나가자 오스트레일리아 농부의 눈길로 그것들을 바라보았던 것이다!\n\n# CHAPTER 53. ANOTHER RETROSPECT\n\n다시 한번 멈춰야겠다. 오, 나의 어린 아내여, 내 기억 앞을 흐르는 무리 속에 한 모습이 있어, 고요하고 잔잔하게, 그 순결한 사랑과 어린아이 같은 아름다움으로 이렇게 말한다. 멈춰서 나를 생각해 줘요 — 작은 꽃송이가 땅으로 팔랑팔랑 떨어지는 모습을 돌아봐 줘요!\n\n나는 그렇게 한다. 다른 모든 것은 흐릿해지고, 스러져 사라진다. 나는 다시 도라와 함께, 우리 오두막에 있다. 그녀가 앓은 지 얼마나 됐는지 나는 모른다. 마음으로는 그것이 너무도 익숙해져서, 그 시간을 헤아릴 수가 없다. 주나 달로 따지면 정말로 긴 시간은 아니다. 하지만 내 처지와 경험으로는, 지치고 또 지치는 긴 세월이다.\n\n이제 사람들은 나에게 \"며칠만 더 기다려 보라\"는 말을 그만두었다. 나는 어렴풋이, 내 어린 아내가 옛 친구 집과 함께 햇살 속을 달리는 모습을 다시 보게 될 그날이 영영 밝지 않을지도 모른다는 두려움을 품기 시작했다.\n\n집은, 말하자면 갑작스레, 무척 늙어 버렸다. 어쩌면 그 녀석은 제 주인에게서 자기를 활기차게 하고 젊어지게 했던 무언가를 잃은 것인지도 모른다. 하지만 녀석은 풀이 죽어 시들시들하고, 눈은 침침해졌고, 다리에는 힘이 없다. 그리고 고모님은 녀석이 더 이상 자기에게 대들지 않는 것을 안타까워한다. 녀석은 도라의 침대 위에 누운 채로 — 고모님은 침대 곁에 앉아 있는데 — 고모님 가까이 슬금슬금 다가가, 그 손을 부드럽게 핥는다.\n\n도라는 우리를 바라보며 미소 띤 채 누워 있고, 아름답고, 성급하거나 불평하는 말은 한마디도 내뱉지 않는다. 그녀는 우리가 자기에게 무척 잘해 준다고 말한다. 자상하던 옛 소년이 스스로를 지치게 만들고 있다는 걸 안다고, 고모님은 한숨도 못 자면서도 늘 깨어 있고, 부지런하고, 다정하다고 말한다. 가끔, 그 작은 새 같은 부인들이 그녀를 보러 온다. 그러면 우리는 우리 결혼식 날과, 그 행복했던 시절을 이야기한다.\n\n조용하고, 그늘지고, 가지런한 방에 앉아, 내 어린 아내의 푸른 두 눈이 나를 향하고, 그 작은 손가락들이 내 손을 감싸 휘감을 때 — 내 삶에, 아니 안팎의 온 삶에, 이 얼마나 이상한 안식과 멈춤이 깃든 것 같은지! 나는 이렇게 수없이 많은 시간을 앉아 있다. 하지만 그 모든 시간 가운데, 세 번이 내 마음속에 가장 생생하게 떠오른다.\n\n아침이다. 고모님의 손길로 곱게 단장한 도라가, 자기 예쁜 머리카락이 아직도 베개 위에서 어떻게 곱슬곱슬 말리는지, 또 얼마나 길고 윤기 나는지, 그리고 자기가 쓰고 있는 그 망에 그것을 느슨하게 모아 두는 걸 얼마나 좋아하는지를 나에게 보여 준다.\n\n\"이제 이걸 뽐내려는 게 아니에요, 짓궂은 양반.\" 내가 미소 짓자 그녀가 말한다. \"당신이 이게 참 아름답다고 생각한다고 늘 말했으니까요. 그리고 내가 당신을 처음 마음에 두기 시작했을 때, 거울을 들여다보면서 당신이 이 머리카락 한 타래를 갖고 싶어 무척 좋아할까 궁금해하곤 했으니까요. 아아, 내가 한 타래 줬을 때 당신은 얼마나 바보 같았는지 몰라요, 두디!\"\n\n\"그건 당신이, 내가 준 꽃을 그리고 있던 날이었지, 도라. 그리고 내가 당신에게 얼마나 사랑에 빠졌는지를 털어놓던 날이었고.\"\n\n\"아아! 하지만 그땐 차마 말하기 싫었어요.\" 도라가 말한다. \"내가 그 꽃을 보며 얼마나 울었는지를요. 당신이 정말로 나를 좋아한다고 믿었으니까요! 내가 예전처럼 다시 뛰어다닐 수 있게 되면, 두디, 우리가 그렇게 바보 같은 한 쌍이었던 그 장소들을 보러 가요, 그래 줄래요? 그리고 옛날에 걷던 길도 좀 걷고요? 그리고 가엾은 아빠도 잊지 말고요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그러자, 그리고 행복한 날들을 보내자. 그러니 어서 빨리 나아야 해, 여보.\"\n\n\"오, 곧 그렇게 될 거예요! 나 정말 훨씬 좋아졌어요, 당신은 모를 거예요!\"\n\n저녁이다. 나는 같은 의자에, 같은 침대 곁에 앉아 있고, 같은 얼굴이 나를 향하고 있다. 우리는 말없이 있었고, 그녀의 얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있다. 이제 나는 내 가벼운 짐을 안고 계단을 오르내리지 않는다. 그녀는 온종일 여기 누워 있다.\n\n\"두디!\"\n\n\"내 사랑 도라!\"\n\n\"얼마 전에 당신이 위크필드 씨가 편찮으시다고 했잖아요, 그 얘기 들은 뒤에 내가 하려는 말을 당신이 터무니없다고 여기지 않을 거죠? 나, 애그니스를 보고 싶어요. 무척이나 그녀를 보고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그녀에게 편지를 쓰마, 여보.\"\n\n\"그래 줄래요?\"\n\n\"당장에.\"\n\n\"참 착하고 다정한 양반이에요! 두디, 나를 당신 팔에 기대게 해 줘요. 정말이에요, 여보, 이건 변덕이 아니에요. 바보 같은 공상도 아니고요. 나, 정말이지 무척이나, 그녀를 보고 싶어요!\"\n\n\"나도 알아. 내가 그렇게 전하기만 하면, 그녀는 틀림없이 올 거야.\"\n\n\"이제 아래층에 내려가면 무척 외롭죠?\" 도라가 내 목에 팔을 두른 채 속삭인다.\n\n\"어찌 안 그러겠어, 내 사랑, 당신의 빈 의자를 보는데?\"\n\n\"내 빈 의자!\" 그녀는 말없이 한동안 나에게 매달려 있다. \"그러면 당신은 정말로 내가 그리워요, 두디?\" 나를 올려다보며, 환하게 미소 지으며. \"이 가엾고, 덜렁대고, 멍청한 나조차도요?\"\n\n\"내 마음, 이 세상에 내가 그토록 그리워할 사람이 또 어디 있겠어?\"\n\n\"오, 여보! 나 너무 기뻐요, 그런데 또 너무 슬퍼요!\" 나에게 더 바짝 파고들며, 두 팔로 나를 감싸 안으며. 그녀는 웃다가 흐느끼고, 그러다 잠잠해지더니, 아주 행복해한다.\n\n\"아주요!\" 그녀가 말한다. \"그저 애그니스에게 내 깊은 사랑을 전해 주고, 내가 정말, 정말로 그녀를 무척 보고 싶어 한다고 말해 줘요. 그러면 나는 더 바랄 게 아무것도 없어요.\"\n\n\"다시 건강해지는 것만 빼면 말이지, 도라.\"\n\n\"아아, 두디! 가끔 이런 생각이 들어요 — 알잖아요, 난 늘 어리석은 어린것이었으니까! — 그런 날은 영영 오지 않을 거라는 생각이요!\"\n\n\"그런 말 마, 도라! 가장 사랑하는 사람, 그렇게 생각하지 마!\"\n\n\"안 할게요, 그럴 수만 있다면요, 두디. 하지만 나는 무척 행복해요. 내 사랑하는 양반이 어린 아내의 빈 의자 앞에서 혼자 그토록 외롭더라도요!\"\n\n밤이다. 나는 여전히 그녀와 함께 있다. 애그니스가 도착해, 꼬박 하루 낮과 한 저녁을 우리 곁에 머물렀다. 그녀와 고모님과 나는 아침부터 다 함께 도라 곁에 앉아 있었다. 우리는 말을 많이 하지 않았지만, 도라는 더없이 흡족하고 명랑했다. 이제 우리는 단둘이다.\n\n이제 나는, 내 어린 아내가 머지않아 나를 떠나리라는 것을 알고 있는 걸까? 사람들은 나에게 그렇게 말했다. 내 생각에 이미 없던 사실을 그들이 새로 알려 준 것은 아니다 — 하지만 내가 그 진실을 마음 깊이 받아들였는지는 도무지 확신이 서지 않는다. 나는 그것을 도저히 감당할 수가 없다. 오늘 나는 여러 번 혼자 물러나 울었다. 산 자와 죽은 자의 이별 앞에서 눈물 흘리셨던 그분을 나는 떠올렸다. 그 자비롭고 가엾이 여기는 모든 이야기를 곱씹어 보았다. 나는 체념하려, 스스로를 달래려 애썼다. 그리고 그것은, 바라건대, 어설프게나마 해냈는지도 모른다. 하지만 내 마음속에 굳게 자리잡힐 수 없는 단 하나는, 끝이 정녕 오고야 만다는 사실이다. 나는 그녀의 손을 내 손에 쥐고, 그녀의 마음을 내 마음에 품고, 나를 향한 그녀의 사랑이 온 힘을 다해 살아 있음을 본다. 나는 그녀가 살아남으리라는 창백하게 어른거리는 믿음의 그림자를 차마 떨쳐 낼 수가 없다.\n\n\"당신에게 할 말이 있어요, 두디. 요즘 자주 하고 싶었던 말을 하려고요. 괜찮죠?\" 부드러운 눈길로.\n\n\"괜찮다니, 내 사랑?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 당신이 어떻게 생각할지, 아니면 가끔 어떻게 생각했을지 모르겠으니까요. 어쩌면 당신도 똑같은 생각을 자주 했을지 몰라요. 두디, 여보, 나는 내가 너무 어렸던 게 아닌가 싶어요.\"\n\n나는 그녀 곁 베개에 얼굴을 묻고, 그녀는 내 두 눈을 들여다보며, 아주 부드럽게 말한다. 그녀가 말을 이어 갈수록, 나는 가슴이 찢어지는 마음으로, 그녀가 자기 자신을 이미 지나간 사람처럼 이야기하고 있음을 느낀다.\n\n\"나는, 여보, 내가 너무 어렸던 게 아닌가 싶어요. 나이만 두고 하는 말이 아니에요, 경험이며, 생각이며, 모든 것이요. 나는 정말 어리석은 어린것이었어요! 우리가 그저 소년 소녀처럼 서로 사랑하다가 잊어버렸더라면 더 나았을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어요. 나는 아내가 될 만한 사람이 아니었다는 생각이 들기 시작했어요.\"\n\n나는 눈물을 참으려 애쓰며 대답한다. \"오, 도라, 내 사랑, 당신은 내가 남편 될 만큼은 충분했어!\"\n\n\"모르겠어요.\" 옛날처럼 곱슬머리를 살래살래 흔들며. \"어쩌면요! 하지만 내가 결혼할 자질이 더 있었더라면, 당신도 더 그렇게 만들었을지 몰라요. 게다가 당신은 무척 영리한 사람인데, 나는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었으니까요.\"\n\n\"우리는 무척 행복했잖아, 사랑스러운 도라.\"\n\n\"나는 무척 행복했어요, 정말로요. 하지만 세월이 흐르면, 내 사랑하는 양반은 어린 아내에게 싫증이 났을 거예요. 그녀는 점점 더 그에게 어울리는 짝이 못 되었을 테고요. 그는 자기 가정에 부족한 것이 무엇인지 점점 더 절실히 느꼈을 거예요. 그녀는 나아지지 않았을 테고요. 이대로가 더 나아요.\"\n\n\"오, 도라, 가장 사랑하는, 가장 사랑하는 사람, 나에게 그렇게 말하지 마. 한마디 한마디가 다 꾸짖음 같아!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 한 음절도 그렇지 않아요!\" 그녀가 나에게 입 맞추며 대답한다. \"오, 여보, 당신은 그런 말을 들을 까닭이 한 번도 없었어요. 그리고 나는 당신을 너무 깊이 사랑해서, 진심으로 당신에게 꾸짖는 말 한마디 할 수 없었어요 — 그게 내가 가진 유일한 미덕이었죠, 예쁜 것 말고는요 — 아니면 당신이 나를 그렇게 여겨 줬던 거고요. 아래층은 외롭죠, 두디?\"\n\n\"무척! 무척!\"\n\n\"울지 마요! 내 의자는 거기 있어요?\"\n\n\"그 옛날 자리에 있지.\"\n\n\"오, 내 가엾은 양반이 어쩜 저렇게 우는지! 쉿, 쉿! 자, 나에게 한 가지만 약속해 줘요. 나, 애그니스와 이야기하고 싶어요. 아래층에 내려가거든, 애그니스에게 그렇게 말하고, 그녀를 나에게 올려보내 줘요. 그리고 내가 그녀와 이야기하는 동안에는, 아무도 오지 않게 해 줘요 — 고모님조차도요. 나는 애그니스와 단둘이서 이야기하고 싶어요. 애그니스와, 아주 단둘이서요.\"\n\n나는 그렇게 하겠노라고, 당장 그러겠노라고 약속한다. 하지만 슬픔에 겨워 그녀 곁을 떠날 수가 없다.\n\n\"이대로가 더 낫다고 내가 말했죠!\" 그녀가 두 팔로 나를 끌어안은 채 속삭인다. \"오, 두디, 세월이 더 흘렀더라도, 당신은 지금보다 어린 아내를 더 사랑할 수는 없었을 거예요. 그리고 세월이 더 흘렀더라면, 그녀는 당신을 너무도 애먹이고 실망시켜서, 당신이 그녀를 지금의 절반만큼도 사랑할 수 없었을지 몰라요! 나는 내가 너무 어리고 어리석었다는 걸 알아요. 이대로가 훨씬 나아요!\"\n\n내가 거실로 들어가니 애그니스가 아래층에 있다. 나는 그녀에게 그 말을 전한다. 그녀는 나를 집과 단둘이 남겨 둔 채 사라진다.\n\n집의 중국식 개집이 난로 곁에 있고, 녀석은 그 안에서 플란넬 잠자리에 누워, 보채듯 잠들려 애쓰고 있다. 밝은 달이 높이, 맑게 떠 있다. 밤을 내다보는 사이, 내 눈물은 줄줄 흘러내리고, 다스리지 못한 내 마음은 무겁게, 무겁게 매를 맞는다.\n\n나는 난롯가에 앉아, 결혼 이후로 내가 키워 온 그 모든 은밀한 감정들을 눈먼 회한과 함께 곱씹는다. 나와 도라 사이의 사소한 일 하나하나를 떠올리며, 그 사소한 것들이 모여 삶의 총합을 이룬다는 진실을 느낀다. 내 기억의 바다에서 끝없이 떠오르는 것은, 내가 처음 알았던 그 사랑스러운 아이의 모습이다. 내 젊은 사랑과 그녀 자신의 사랑으로 곱게 빛나고, 그러한 사랑이 풍성히 머금은 온갖 매력으로 단장한 모습이다. 정녕, 우리가 소년 소녀처럼 서로 사랑하다가 잊어버렸더라면 더 나았을까? 다스리지 못한 마음이여, 대답하라!\n\n시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지 나는 모른다. 내 어린 아내의 옛 동무가 나를 깨워 부를 때까지는. 녀석은 아까보다 더 안절부절못하며, 제 집에서 기어 나와 나를 바라보다가, 문 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가, 위층으로 올라가고 싶다고 낑낑댄다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 안 돼, 집! 오늘 밤은 안 돼!\"\n\n녀석은 아주 천천히 나에게 돌아와, 내 손을 핥고, 침침한 두 눈을 들어 내 얼굴을 본다.\n\n\"오, 집! 어쩌면, 다시는 못 보겠지!\"\n\n녀석은 내 발치에 엎드려, 잠이라도 들려는 듯 몸을 쭉 뻗더니, 구슬프게 한 번 끙 소리를 내고는, 숨을 거둔다.\n\n\"오, 애그니스! 봐요, 봐요, 여기를!\" — 연민과 슬픔으로 가득 찬 그 얼굴, 그 빗물처럼 쏟아지는 눈물, 나를 향한 그 말 없는 끔찍한 호소, 하늘을 향해 엄숙히 치켜든 그 손!\n\n\"애그니스?\"\n\n다 끝났다. 어둠이 내 두 눈 앞으로 밀려든다. 그리고 한동안, 모든 것이 내 기억에서 지워진다.\n\n# CHAPTER 54. Mr. MICAWBER’S TRANSACTIONS\n\n지금은 슬픔의 무게에 짓눌린 내 마음의 상태를 풀어놓을 자리가 아니다. 나는 미래가 내 앞에 벽처럼 가로막혀 버렸다고, 내 삶의 활력과 의욕이 끝장나 버렸다고, 무덤 말고는 어디서도 안식을 찾을 수 없으리라고 생각하기에 이르렀다. 그렇게 생각하기에 이르렀다고 나는 말하지만, 그것은 슬픔의 첫 충격 속에서가 아니었다. 그런 마음은 천천히 자라났다. 내가 이제부터 이야기할 사건들이 내 주위로 빽빽이 몰려들어, 처음에는 내 고통을 헝클어 놓고 끝내는 그것을 부풀려 놓지 않았더라면, 나는 어쩌면 (그럴 법하지는 않다고 보지만) 곧장 그런 상태로 떨어졌을지도 모른다. 하지만 실제로는, 내가 내 비탄을 온전히 깨닫기까지 한동안 틈이 있었다. 그 틈 동안 나는 그 비탄의 가장 날카로운 아픔이 이미 지나갔다고 여기기까지 했다. 영원히 막을 내린 그 애틋한 이야기 속에서 가장 순결하고 아름다운 모든 것에 마음을 기대어 스스로를 달랠 수 있던 틈이었다.\n\n내가 외국으로 떠나야 한다는 이야기가 처음 나온 것이 언제였는지, 변화와 여행 속에서 마음의 평화를 되찾아야 한다는 것이 우리 사이에서 어떻게 합의되었는지, 나는 지금도 또렷이 모른다. 그 슬픔의 시간에 우리가 생각하고, 말하고, 행한 모든 것에 아그네스의 마음이 어찌나 두루 스며 있었던지, 나는 그 계획을 그녀의 영향에 돌려도 좋으리라고 짐작한다. 그러나 그녀의 영향은 어찌나 조용했던지, 나는 그 이상은 알지 못한다.\n\n그리고 이제, 정말로, 나는 이런 생각을 하기 시작했다. 오래전 내가 그녀를 교회의 스테인드글라스 창과 한데 묶어 떠올리던 그 일이, 때가 무르익어 닥칠 그 참사 속에서 그녀가 나에게 어떤 존재가 될지를 예언처럼 미리 비춰 준 것이, 어느새 내 마음속으로 길을 찾아 들어왔던 거라고. 그 모든 슬픔 속에서, 그녀가 손을 치켜든 채 내 앞에 서 있던, 결코 잊지 못할 그 순간부터, 그녀는 내 외로운 집 안의 성스러운 존재 같았다. 죽음의 천사가 그곳에 내려앉았을 때, 내 어린 아내는 미소를 머금은 채 그녀의 가슴에 안겨 잠들었다 — 내가 그 말을 견딜 수 있게 되었을 때 사람들이 그렇게 일러 주었다. 혼절했다가 처음 깨어났을 때 내가 의식한 것은, 그녀의 가엾어하는 눈물이었고, 희망과 평안을 담은 그녀의 말이었으며, 천국에 더 가까운 더 맑은 세계에서 내려오기라도 한 듯 다스려지지 않은 내 마음 위로 굽어, 그 아픔을 부드럽게 어루만지는 그녀의 다정한 얼굴이었다.\n\n이야기를 계속하자.\n\n나는 외국으로 떠나기로 되어 있었다. 그것은 처음부터 우리 사이에서 정해져 있었던 듯하다. 세상을 떠난 내 아내의 스러질 수 있는 모든 것을 이제 흙이 덮고 있는 지금, 나는 미코버 씨가 '히프의 최종적 분쇄'라고 부르던 일과 이주민들의 출발만을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n내 곤경의 시절에 가장 살뜰하고 헌신적인 벗이었던 트래들스의 청에 따라, 우리는 캔터베리로 돌아갔다. 고모님과 아그네스와 나, 이렇게 말이다. 우리는 약속대로 곧장 미코버 씨의 집으로 갔는데, 우리가 폭탄 같은 자리를 함께한 이래로 내 벗은 그 집과 위크필드 씨 댁에서 줄곧 일을 해 오고 있었다. 가엾은 미코버 부인은 내가 검은 상복을 입고 들어서는 것을 보자 눈에 띄게 마음이 흔들렸다. 미코버 부인의 마음속에는 그 숱한 세월의 빚 독촉에도 닳아 없어지지 않은 착한 구석이 꽤 많이 남아 있었다.\n\n\"자, 미코버 씨, 미코버 부인.\" 우리가 자리에 앉은 뒤 고모님이 처음 건넨 인사였다. \"내가 말한 그 이주 제안, 생각들은 좀 해 보셨나요?\"\n\n\"부인,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"미코버 부인과 부인의 보잘것없는 종, 그리고 거기에 우리 아이들까지 더하여, 다 함께 그리고 저마다 다다른 결론을 표현하자면, 저로서는 어느 이름난 시인의 말씨를 빌려, 우리의 거룻배는 물가에 있고 우리의 큰 배는 바다 위에 있다고 답하는 것보다 더 나은 방도를 찾지 못하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그거예요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 현명한 결정에서 나는 온갖 좋은 일을 점쳐 봅니다.\"\n\n\"부인, 저희에게 큰 영광을 베푸시는군요.\" 그가 받았다. 그러더니 그는 비망록을 들여다보았다. \"사업이라는 대양에 우리의 가냘픈 카누를 띄우게 해 줄 금전적 도움에 관해서는, 그 중대한 사업상의 요점을 다시 생각해 보았습니다. 그리하여 제 약속어음을 — 그런 증권에 적용되는 여러 의회법이 저마다 요구하는 액수의 인지에 끊어 발행하는 것은 굳이 따로 정할 것도 없는 일입니다만 — 십팔 개월, 이십사 개월, 삼십 개월짜리로 내놓을 것을 제안드리고자 합니다. 제가 애초에 제출한 안은 십이 개월, 십팔 개월, 이십사 개월이었습니다만, 그런 식으로 짜면 무언가가 — 무언가가 — 충분히 생겨날 만큼의 시간이 모자랄까 봐 염려가 됩니다. 우리는,\" 미코버 씨가, 마치 그 방이 잘 일군 수백 에이커의 땅이라도 되는 양 둘러보며 말했다. \"첫 채무가 만기에 이르렀을 때, 추수에 성공하지 못했을 수도, 또는 거둔 것을 미처 들이지 못했을 수도 있습니다. 우리가 비옥하게 들끓는 흙과 씨름하게 될 운명에 처할, 우리 식민 영토의 그 지역에서는, 일손을 구하기가 이따금 어렵다고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"좋으신 대로 정하세요, 선생님.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"미코버 부인과 저는 우리의 벗이자 후원자 되시는 분들의 그토록 사려 깊은 친절을 깊이 느끼고 있습니다. 제가 바라는 바는, 더없이 사업가답고, 더없이 정확하게 처신하는 것입니다. 이제 막 우리가 펼치려는 것처럼 완전히 새로운 페이지를 넘기면서, 또 지금 우리가 하고 있는 것처럼 예사롭지 않은 큰 도약을 위해 한껏 뒤로 물러서면서, 이 약정들이 사내와 사내 사이의 일로서 마무리되는 것은, 제 자존심의 측면에서도 중요하거니와 제 아들에게도 본보기가 됩니다.\"\n\n미코버 씨가 이 마지막 구절에 어떤 의미를 담았는지 나는 모르겠다. 누가 지금이나 예전이나 거기에 의미를 담은 적이 있는지도 모르겠다. 그러나 그는 그 말을 유난히 음미하는 듯했고, 인상적인 헛기침과 함께 \"사내와 사내 사이의 일로서\"라고 되뇌었다.\n\n\"제가 제안하는 것은,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"어음입니다. 상업계에 편리한 물건이지요. 제가 알기로 우리가 본디 이것을 빚진 상대는 유대인들인데, 그들은 그 후로 줄곧 어음과 지나치게 깊이 얽혀 온 듯합니다 — 어음은 양도가 가능하니까요. 하지만 채무 증서든 그 밖의 어떤 형태의 담보든 그쪽을 더 원하신다면, 저는 기꺼이 그런 증서를 작성하겠습니다. 사내와 사내 사이의 일로서요.\"\n\n고모님은, 양쪽 다 무엇에든 기꺼이 동의하려는 마당이니, 이 점을 매듭짓는 데 어려움이 없으리라 여긴다고 말했다. 미코버 씨도 같은 생각이었다.\n\n\"우리의 가정적 준비에 관해서 말씀드리자면, 부인,\" 미코버 씨가 약간 자랑스러운 기색으로 말했다. \"이제 우리가 스스로 바쳐 나아가기로 한 운명을 맞이하기 위한 준비를 보고드리겠습니다. 제 맏딸은 소젖 짜는 과정을 — 그것을 과정이라 부를 수 있다면요 — 익히려고 매일 아침 다섯 시에 이웃의 한 시설에 나가 있습니다. 어린 자식들은 이 도시의 더 가난한 구역에서 기르는 돼지며 가금의 습성을, 사정이 허락하는 한 가까이서 관찰하라고 일러 두었는데, 그 일로 두 차례나 자칫 수레에 깔릴 뻔한 채로 집에 실려 온 적이 있습니다. 저 자신은 지난 한 주 동안 빵 굽는 기술에 얼마간 주의를 기울였고, 제 아들 윌킨스는 지팡이를 들고 나가 소를 몰았습니다 — 소를 맡은 거친 일꾼들이 그쪽으로 자진해서 봉사하는 것을 허락해 줄 때 말이지요. 다만 인간 본성의 명예를 위해 유감스럽게 말씀드리건대, 그런 허락은 자주 있지 않았습니다. 대개는 욕설과 함께 그만두라는 경고를 받았으니까요.\"\n\n\"정말 다 잘하셨네요.\" 고모님이 북돋우듯 말했다. \"미코버 부인도 분명 바쁘셨겠지요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 미코버 부인이 사업가다운 태도로 받았다. \"솔직히 고백하건대, 저는 경작이나 가축에 곧바로 닿는 일에는 적극적으로 매달리지 못했습니다. 낯선 해안에 가면 그 두 가지가 다 제 손길을 요구하리라는 것은 잘 알고 있습니다만 말입니다. 가정의 본분에서 떼어 낼 수 있었던 짬은, 저는 친정 식구들에게 제법 긴 편지를 띄우는 데 바쳤습니다. 솔직히 제게는 이렇게 여겨지거든요, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨,\" 미코버 부인은, 처음에는 누구를 향해 이야기를 시작하든, 아마도 오랜 버릇에서 늘 나에게로 되돌아오곤 했는데, 이렇게 말했다. \"지난 일은 망각 속에 묻어야 할 때가, 우리 친정이 미코버 씨의 손을 잡고 미코버 씨가 우리 친정의 손을 잡아야 할 때가, 사자가 양과 함께 누워야 할 때가, 그리고 우리 친정이 미코버 씨와 화목해져야 할 때가 왔다고 말입니다.\"\n\n나도 그렇게 생각한다고 말했다.\n\n\"적어도, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 이것이 제가 이 문제를 바라보는 관점입니다.\" 미코버 부인이 이어 갔다. \"제가 친정에서 아빠 엄마와 함께 살 때, 우리의 좁은 식구들 사이에서 무슨 안건이 논의될라치면 아빠는 으레 이렇게 물으셨답니다. '우리 에마는 이 문제를 어떤 관점에서 보느냐?' 우리 아빠가 저를 지나치게 편애하셨다는 건 저도 압니다. 그래도 미코버 씨와 우리 친정 사이에 줄곧 이어져 온 그 얼음장 같은 냉기 같은 문제라면, 비록 잘못된 것일지언정 저로서는 어쩔 수 없이 의견 하나는 갖게 마련이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼요. 당연히 그러시겠지요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 미코버 부인이 맞장구쳤다. \"이제, 제 결론이 틀렸을 수도 있습니다. 틀렸을 가능성이 아주 큽니다만, 제 개인적인 인상으로는, 우리 친정과 미코버 씨 사이의 그 깊은 골은, 미코버 씨가 금전적 융통을 요구할 것이라는 우리 친정 쪽의 우려에서 비롯된 것으로 더듬어 갈 수 있다고 봅니다. 저는 이렇게 생각하지 않을 수가 없어요,\" 미코버 부인이 깊은 통찰의 기색으로 말했다. \"우리 친정 식구 가운데에는, 미코버 씨가 자기들 이름을 청해 올까 봐 걱정해 온 이들이 있다고 말입니다. 우리 아이들에게 세례 때 붙여 달라는 것이 아니라, 환어음에 적어 넣어 금융 시장에서 양도하자는 그런 이름 말이지요.\"\n\n미코버 부인이 마치 여태 아무도 거기에 생각이 미친 적이 없다는 듯 이 발견을 알릴 때 짓던 그 꿰뚫는 듯한 표정은, 고모님을 적잖이 놀라게 한 모양이었다. 고모님은 불쑥 대꾸했다. \"글쎄요, 부인, 따지고 보면, 부인 말씀이 옳다 해도 이상할 게 없겠네요!\"\n\n\"미코버 씨는 이제 막, 그토록 오래 그를 옭아매 온 금전의 족쇄를 벗어 던지고,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그의 능력을 펼칠 충분한 활동 폭이 있는 나라에서 — 제가 보기에 이건 대단히 중요한 일입니다, 미코버 씨의 능력은 유난히 넓은 공간을 필요로 하니까요 — 새로운 길을 시작하려는 참입니다. 그러니 제게는, 우리 친정이 앞으로 나서서 이 일을 기려야 마땅하게 여겨집니다. 제가 보고 싶은 것은, 우리 친정의 비용으로 베푸는 흥겨운 잔치 자리에서 미코버 씨와 우리 친정이 만나는 모습입니다. 그 자리에서 우리 친정의 어느 이름난 어른이 미코버 씨의 건강과 번영을 위해 축배를 들면, 미코버 씨도 자기 포부를 펼쳐 보일 기회를 얻을 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 약간 발끈하여 말했다. \"이참에 분명히 밝혀 두는 편이 나을 듯하오. 만약 내가 그 모인 무리 앞에서 내 포부를 펼친다면, 그건 십중팔구 듣기에 거북한 것이 되고 말 거요. 내 인상으로 당신네 친정 식구들은, 통틀어 말하면 건방진 속물들이고, 하나하나 따지면 누그러질 줄 모르는 악당들이니까.\"\n\n\"미코버,\" 미코버 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"아니에요! 당신은 한 번도 그들을 이해한 적이 없고, 그들도 한 번도 당신을 이해한 적이 없어요.\"\n\n미코버 씨가 헛기침을 했다.\n\n\"그들은 한 번도 당신을 이해한 적이 없어요, 미코버.\" 그의 아내가 말했다. \"그럴 능력이 없는 건지도 모르지요. 만약 그렇다면, 그건 그들의 불행이에요. 나는 그들의 불행을 가엾이 여길 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"정말 미안하오, 사랑하는 에마,\" 미코버 씨가 누그러지며 말했다. \"조금이라도, 아주 멀게나마, 거친 말로 보일 만한 표현에 휩쓸리고 만 것 말이오. 내가 말하고 싶은 건 단지, 당신네 친정이 나서서 호의를 베풀어 주지 않더라도 — 한마디로 작별 삼아 차갑게 어깨를 한 번 떠밀어 주는 것만으로도 — 나는 외국으로 떠날 수 있다는 것, 그리고 따지고 보면, 그쪽에서 가속을 좀 얻느니 차라리 내가 가진 만큼의 힘으로 영국을 떠나는 편이 낫겠다는 것이오. 그래도, 여보, 만약 그들이 황송하게도 당신 편지에 답장이라도 보내 준다면 — 우리 둘의 경험으로 보아 그럴 가능성은 거의 없습니다만 — 당신의 바람을 가로막을 마음은 결코 없소.\"\n\n이렇게 문제가 화기애애하게 마무리되자, 미코버 씨는 미코버 부인에게 팔을 내밀고, 트래들스 앞 탁자에 쌓인 책과 서류 더미를 한번 흘끗 보더니, 우리끼리 있게 자리를 비켜 주겠다고 말했다. 그러고는 둘이 격식을 차려 물러갔다.\n\n\"친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 그들이 가 버린 뒤 트래들스가 의자에 등을 기대고, 눈이 벌게지고 머리카락이 온갖 모양으로 헝클어질 만큼 애정 어린 눈으로 나를 보며 말했다. \"사업 이야기로 자네를 번거롭게 하는 걸 변명하진 않겠네. 자네가 여기에 깊이 마음을 두고 있는 줄 알고, 또 이게 자네 생각을 다른 데로 돌려 줄 수도 있으니까. 이보게, 자네 너무 지친 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"이젠 괜찮아.\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 내가 말했다. \"누구보다도 우리 고모님을 생각해야지. 고모님이 얼마나 애써 주셨는지 자네도 알잖나.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그렇고말고.\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"누가 그걸 잊을 수 있겠나!\"\n\n\"하지만 그것도 다가 아니라네.\" 내가 말했다. \"지난 보름 동안 무슨 새로운 근심이 고모님을 괴롭혔는지, 날마다 런던을 들락날락하셨어. 몇 번은 이른 아침에 나가셔서 저녁까지 돌아오지 않으셨고. 어젯밤엔, 트래들스, 이 여행을 앞두고도, 거의 자정이 다 되어서야 집에 돌아오셨다네. 자네도 고모님이 남을 얼마나 배려하시는지 알잖나. 무슨 일로 그토록 마음을 졸이시는지 나한테 말씀을 안 하셔.\"\n\n고모님은 몹시 창백한 얼굴에 깊은 주름을 새긴 채, 내 말이 끝날 때까지 꼼짝없이 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 흐르는 눈물 몇 방울이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 고모님은 내 손 위에 자기 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니다, 트롯. 아무것도 아니야. 다시는 그런 일 없을 게다. 차차 알게 될 거야. 자 아그네스, 얘야, 우리 이 일들이나 마저 보자꾸나.\"\n\n\"미코버 씨에게는 공정하게 이 말은 해 둬야겠는데,\" 트래들스가 입을 열었다. \"자기 자신을 위해서는 별 신통한 성과를 못 내는 사람처럼 보여도, 남을 위해 일할 때는 그야말로 지칠 줄을 모르는 사람일세. 그런 친구는 본 적이 없어. 늘 이런 식으로 살아온 거라면, 그 양반은 지금쯤 사실상 이백 살은 됐어야 할 거야. 줄곧 스스로를 들끓게 몰아넣던 그 열기며, 밤낮없이 서류와 책 더미 속을 정신없이 다급하게 파고들던 그 태도며, 게다가 이 집과 위크필드 씨 댁 사이를 오가며 나한테 써 보낸 그 엄청난 양의 편지며 — 맞은편에 앉아 있어서 훨씬 쉽게 말로 하면 될 때도 곧잘 탁자 너머로 편지를 써 보내곤 했지 — 정말이지 보통 일이 아니야.\"\n\n\"편지라니!\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"그 양반은 꿈도 편지로 꾸는 모양이지!\"\n\n\"디크 씨도 말이야,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"기적 같은 일을 해냈다네! 유라이어 히프 감시하는 일에서 — 내가 본 것 중 그보다 더한 감시는 없을 만큼 단단히 붙잡고 있었지 — 풀려나자마자, 위크필드 씨에게 온몸을 바쳐 매달리기 시작했어. 그리고 우리가 벌이던 조사에 정말이지 도움이 되고자 하던 그 열성이며, 서류를 뽑아내고, 베끼고, 가져오고, 나르는 데서 보여 준 그 실속 있는 쓸모란, 우리한테 여간 힘이 되지 않았다네.\"\n\n\"디크는 아주 비범한 사람이야.\" 고모님이 소리쳤다. \"내가 늘 그렇게 말했지. 트롯, 너도 알잖니.\"\n\n\"기쁘게 말씀드릴 게 있습니다, 위크필드 양.\" 트래들스가 더없이 섬세하면서도 더없이 진지하게 이어 갔다. \"그쪽이 자리를 비운 동안 위크필드 씨가 상당히 좋아지셨습니다. 그토록 오래 그를 옥죄어 온 그 짐과, 그가 그 아래서 살아온 그 무서운 두려움에서 벗어나, 지금은 거의 딴사람 같습니다. 때로는, 특정한 사업상의 사안에 기억과 주의를 모으는 그 손상된 능력마저 상당히 회복되어서, 그가 없었다면 정말 어려웠을, 어쩌면 가망 없었을 몇몇 일들을 또렷이 밝히는 데 우리를 도와주셨습니다. 하지만 제가 해야 할 일은 결론에 이르는 것이지요. 결론이야 충분히 짧으니까요. 제가 눈여겨본 그 모든 희망찬 정황들을 시시콜콜 늘어놓는 게 아니라요. 안 그러면 이야기가 끝이 없을 테니까요.\" 그의 천연덕스러운 태도와 사람 좋은 소박함을 보면, 그가 이 말을 하는 것은 우리에게 용기를 북돋워 주고 아그네스가 자기 아버지 이야기를 좀 더 마음 놓고 들을 수 있게 하려는 것임이 훤히 들여다보였다. 그렇다고 해서 그 말이 덜 흐뭇하지는 않았다.\n\n\"자, 어디 보자.\" 탁자 위 서류들을 뒤지며 트래들스가 말했다. \"우선 우리 손에 있는 자금을 헤아리고, 첫째로는 본의 아닌 혼란의, 둘째로는 고의적인 혼란과 위조의 그 어마어마한 덩어리를 정리하고 나니, 이제는 위크필드 씨가 자기 사업과 대리 위탁을 깨끗이 청산하고도 어떠한 부족이나 횡령도 없음을 분명히 보일 수 있다고 보입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 하느님 감사합니다!\" 아그네스가 간절하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그의 생계 밑천으로 남게 될 잉여금은 — 이렇게 말하는 마당에 집은 팔린다고 잡고서 하는 말입니다만 — 너무도 적어서, 아마 다 합해도 몇백 파운드를 넘지 못할 겁니다. 그러니, 위크필드 양, 어쩌면 그분이 그토록 오래 수입 관리인을 맡아 온 그 토지의 대리권만큼은 그대로 지니시는 게 어떨지 생각해 보는 게 상책일지도 모르겠습니다. 이제 자유로운 몸이니, 친구들이 조언을 드릴 수도 있겠지요. 위크필드 양 본인이라든가 — 코퍼필드라든가 — 저라든가 —\"\n\n\"저도 그건 생각해 봤어요, 트롯우드.\" 아그네스가 나를 보며 말했다. \"그리고 제가 느끼기에 그건 안 될 일이고, 그래서는 안 됩니다. 제가 이토록 고맙고 이토록 많은 신세를 진 친구분의 권유라 하더라도요.\"\n\n\"권한다고는 말하지 않겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그저 한번 짚어 드리는 것이 옳다고 여겼을 뿐입니다. 그 이상은 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 기쁩니다.\" 아그네스가 차분하게 대답했다. \"우리가 같은 생각이라는 희망을, 거의 확신을 주시니까요. 친애하는 트래들스 씨, 그리고 친애하는 트롯우드, 아빠가 명예를 지키며 한번 자유로워지신다면, 제가 무얼 더 바라겠어요! 저는 늘 갈망해 왔어요, 아빠를 옭아매던 그 굴레에서 벗어나게 해 드릴 수만 있다면, 제가 아빠께 진 사랑과 보살핌을 아주 조금이라도 되돌려 드리고, 제 삶을 아빠께 바치겠다고요. 여러 해 동안 그것이 제 바람의 가장 높은 꼭대기였어요. 우리의 앞날을 제가 떠맡는 것이, 다음으로 큰 행복이 될 거예요 — 아빠가 모든 위탁과 책임에서 벗어나시는 그 일에 버금가는, 제가 알 수 있는 그다음 행복 말이에요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 할지는 생각해 봤니, 아그네스?\"\n\n\"여러 번요! 두렵지 않아요, 친애하는 트롯우드. 저는 잘되리라 확신해요. 여기엔 저를 알고 저를 따뜻하게 여겨 주는 사람이 아주 많아서, 저는 확신해요. 저를 못 미더워하지 마세요. 우리한테 필요한 건 많지 않아요. 정든 옛집을 세 얻어 학교를 열면, 저는 쓸모도 있고 행복도 할 거예요.\"\n\n쾌활한 그녀의 목소리에 담긴 그 잔잔한 열정이, 먼저 정든 옛집 그 자체를, 그다음에는 나의 외로운 집을 어찌나 생생히 되살려 놓던지, 내 가슴은 벅차올라 말이 나오지 않았다. 트래들스는 한동안 서류들을 바쁘게 뒤지는 척했다.\n\n\"다음은, 트롯우드 양,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그쪽의 그 재산 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 선생님.\" 고모님이 한숨지었다. \"그 일로 내가 할 말이라곤, 사라진 거라면 견디겠고, 안 사라진 거라면 되찾으면 기쁘겠다는 것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"그건 본디, 제 생각엔, 콘솔 공채로 팔천 파운드였지요?\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요!\" 고모님이 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 따져 본 바로는 오천밖에 행방을 못 짚겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 어리둥절한 기색으로 말했다.\n\n\"— 천이라는 말인가요,\" 고모님이 유달리 침착하게 물었다. \"아니면 파운드?\"\n\n\"오천 파운드입니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 있던 전부예요.\" 고모님이 받았다. \"삼천은 내가 직접 팔았지요. 천은 네 변호사 수습 계약비로 냈고, 트롯, 얘야. 나머지 이천은 내가 갖고 있어요. 나머지를 잃었을 때, 나는 그 돈 얘기는 입 밖에 안 내고 만일에 대비해 몰래 간직해 두는 게 현명하겠다 싶었지. 트롯, 나는 네가 그 시련을 어떻게 헤쳐 나오는지 보고 싶었어. 그런데 너는 보란 듯이 헤쳐 나왔지 — 끈기 있고, 제 힘으로 서고, 제 욕심을 누르면서! 디크도 그랬고. 나한테 말 걸지 마라, 신경이 좀 흔들리는구나!\"\n\n팔짱을 낀 채 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 모습만 보면 아무도 그렇게 생각지 못했을 테지만, 고모님은 놀라운 자제력을 지니고 있었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 기쁘게 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 기쁨으로 환하게 빛나며 외쳤다. \"우리가 그 돈을 몽땅 되찾았습니다!\"\n\n\"아무도 나한테 축하 같은 거 하지 마요!\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"어떻게 된 거죠, 선생님?\"\n\n\"그쪽은 그 돈을 위크필드 씨가 잘못 빼돌렸다고 믿으셨지요?\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"당연히 그렇게 믿었지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그래서 쉽게 입을 다물었던 거고. 아그네스, 한마디도 하지 마라!\"\n\n\"그런데 사실은,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그 돈은 그분이 그쪽에게서 받은 관리 권한에 의거해 팔린 게 맞습니다. 다만 누구 손으로 팔렸는지, 누구의 실제 서명으로 팔렸는지는 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요. 나중에 그 악당이 위크필드 씨에게는, 다른 부족분과 곤란을 드러나지 않게 덮으려고 (포괄적인 지시를 받았다면서) 자기가 그 돈을 챙긴 것이라고 둘러댔고, 숫자로 그렇게 증명까지 해 보였습니다. 위크필드 씨는 그자의 손아귀에서 어찌나 무력하고 어쩔 줄 몰랐던지, 나중에는 자기도 없는 줄 뻔히 아는 가짜 원금에 대한 이자 명목으로 그쪽에게 몇 차례 돈을 치렀고, 그렇게 불행히도 스스로 그 사기의 한패가 되고 말았습니다.\"\n\n\"그러고는 끝내 죄를 제가 다 뒤집어썼지요.\" 고모님이 덧붙였다. \"그러고는 나한테 정신 나간 편지를 써 보냈어요. 자기가 강도질을 했네, 듣도 보도 못한 못된 짓을 했네 하면서 죄를 죄다 제게 씌우더군요. 그래서 나는 어느 날 이른 아침 그를 찾아가, 촛불을 가져오라 해서 그 편지를 태워 버리고, 그에게 일렀지요. 언젠가 나와 자신을 바로잡을 수 있거든 그렇게 하고, 못 하겠거든 딸을 위해서라도 입을 다물고 있으라고요. — 누구든 나한테 말 걸면, 나는 이 집을 나가 버리겠어요!\"\n\n우리는 모두 조용히 있었다. 아그네스는 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"자, 친애하는 친구,\" 잠시 뒤 고모님이 말했다. \"그래서 정말로 그자에게서 그 돈을 도로 짜냈다는 건가요?\"\n\n\"실은 말입니다,\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"미코버 씨가 그자를 어찌나 빈틈없이 몰아붙였던지, 그리고 옛 논점 하나가 막히면 늘 새 논점을 여럿 들고 채비하고 있어서, 그자가 우리한테서 빠져나갈 수가 없었습니다. 가장 놀라운 사실은, 그자가 이 돈을 거머쥔 것이, 도를 넘어선 그자의 탐욕을 채우려는 것보다도, 사실은 코퍼필드에 대한 증오 때문이었다고 저는 정말로 봅니다. 그자가 저한테 대놓고 그렇게 말했어요. 코퍼필드를 방해하거나 해코지할 수만 있다면 이만한 돈쯤 기꺼이 썼을 거라고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"하!\" 고모님이 생각에 잠겨 눈살을 찌푸리며 아그네스를 흘끗 보았다. \"그래서 그자는 어찌 됐나요?\"\n\n\"모릅니다. 여기를 떠났습니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"내내 악을 쓰고, 애원하고, 죄다 까발리던 제 어미와 함께요. 런던행 야간 마차편으로 떠났는데, 그자에 대해 더는 아는 게 없습니다. 다만 헤어질 때 저한테 보인 그 악의만큼은 대단했습니다. 그자는 자기가 저한테 진 빚이 미코버 씨에게 진 것에 못지않다고 여기는 모양이었는데, 저는 (그자한테도 그렇게 말했지만) 그걸 그야말로 칭찬으로 받아들입니다.\"\n\n\"그자한테 돈이 좀 있을 것 같나, 트래들스?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아 이런, 그럼, 있고말고지.\" 그가 진지하게 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"이런저런 방법으로 꽤 챙겨 넣었을 거라고 봐. 하지만 말이야, 코퍼필드, 자네한테 그자의 행적을 지켜볼 기회가 있다면 알게 될 거야. 돈이 있다고 그런 인간이 못된 짓을 안 할 리는 없다는 걸. 그자는 워낙 위선이 뼛속까지 사무친 인간이라, 무슨 목적을 좇든 비뚤어진 길로밖에 못 좇아. 그게 스스로에게 씌운 겉치레 굴레에 대한 그자의 유일한 보상이거든. 늘 어떤 시시한 목적을 향해 땅바닥을 기어 다니면서, 그자는 길목에 놓인 모든 것을 잔뜩 부풀려 보고, 그래서 아무리 무심하게라도 자기와 그것 사이에 끼어드는 사람은 누구든 미워하고 의심하지. 그러니 그 비뚤어진 행로는, 사소한 이유로든 아무 이유 없이든, 언제든 더 비뚤어지게 마련이야. 그걸 알려면,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"여기서 그자가 걸어온 내력만 짚어 봐도 충분하지.\"\n\n\"비열함이 괴물 같은 자야!\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 저는 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"마음만 먹으면 누구나 꽤 비열해질 수 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 미코버 씨 이야기로 넘어가서.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"그게, 정말이지,\" 트래들스가 명랑하게 말했다. \"저는 다시 한번 미코버 씨를 한껏 칭찬해 드려야겠습니다. 그분이 그토록 오래 그렇게 참고 견디지 않았더라면, 우리는 입에 올릴 만한 일은 아무것도 해내지 못했을 겁니다. 그리고 미코버 씨가 입을 다물어 주는 대가로 유라이어 히프 본인과 어떤 거래를 맺을 수도 있었을지를 헤아려 보면, 그분이 오로지 옳음을 위해 옳게 처신했다는 점은 우리가 인정해야 한다고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각해요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그분께 얼마나 드리실 건가요?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n\"아! 거기에 이르기 전에 말입니다,\" 트래들스가 약간 머쓱해하며 말했다. \"이 어려운 일을 이렇게 법에 어긋나게 매듭짓느라 — 처음부터 끝까지 완전히 법에 어긋나는 일이지요 — 두 가지를 (제 뜻대로 죄다 밀어붙일 수가 없어서) 빼놓는 게 신중하겠다 싶었던 것 같습니다. 미코버 씨가 받은 선금에 대해 그자에게 써 준 그 차용증서 따위 말입니다 —\"\n\n\"그건! 갚아야지요.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 하지만 언제 그게 청구될지, 또 그게 어디 있는지를 모르겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 받았다. \"그리고 제 짐작으로는, 지금부터 출발할 때까지 미코버 씨는 줄곧 체포되거나 강제 집행을 당할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 줄곧 다시 풀어 주고, 강제 집행에서 빼내 와야지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"다 합해서 얼마인가요?\"\n\n\"그게, 미코버 씨가 그 거래들을 — 본인은 거래라고 부릅니다만 — 거창한 격식을 차려 장부에 적어 두었더군요.\" 트래들스가 빙그레 웃으며 받았다. \"그분 계산으로는 그 액수가 백삼 파운드 오 실링입니다.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 그 액수까지 포함해서 그분께 얼마를 드릴까요?\" 고모님이 말했다. \"아그네스, 얘야, 그걸 어떻게 나눌지는 너랑 나랑 나중에 얘기하자. 얼마면 좋을까요? 오백 파운드?\"\n\n이 말에 트래들스와 나는 둘 다 동시에 끼어들었다. 우리는 둘 다 현금으로는 소액을 드리고, 유라이어 쪽 청구가 들어오는 대로 미코버 씨에게 따로 조건을 달지 않고 갚아 주자고 권했다. 우리는 그 가족에게 뱃삯과 채비 비용, 그리고 백 파운드를 마련해 주자고 제안했다. 또한 선금 상환에 관한 미코버 씨의 약정은 진지하게 받아들여 두자고 했는데, 그 책임 아래 있다고 스스로 여기는 편이 그에게 이로울 수도 있기 때문이었다. 여기에 나는, 믿을 만한 사람임을 내가 아는 패거티 씨에게 그의 성품과 내력을 어느 정도 설명해 두고, 다시 백 파운드를 더 융통해 줄지 여부는 패거티 씨의 재량에 조용히 맡기자는 제안을 보탰다. 나아가 나는, 정당하다고 느껴지거나 적절하다고 여겨지는 만큼만 패거티 씨의 사연을 미코버 씨에게 털어놓음으로써 미코버 씨가 패거티 씨에게 관심을 갖게 하고, 두 사람이 서로에게 보탬이 되도록 이어 주려 애써 보자고 제안했다. 우리는 모두 이 견해에 흔쾌히 뜻을 모았다. 그리고 곧장 한마디 덧붙이자면, 당사자들 본인도 얼마 뒤 더없이 좋은 뜻과 화목함으로 거기에 동의했다.\n\n트래들스가 다시 고모님 쪽을 불안하게 흘끗거리는 것을 보고, 나는 그가 앞서 넌지시 말한 그 두 번째이자 마지막 사안을 그에게 일깨워 주었다.\n\n\"자네와 자네 고모님께서 양해해 주시게, 코퍼필드. 내가 무척 두려워하는 대로 아무래도 아픈 화제를 건드리게 될 것 같으니 말이야.\" 트래들스가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"하지만 이건 기억에 되살려 드리는 게 필요하다고 봐. 미코버 씨가 그 잊지 못할 폭로를 하던 날, 유라이어 히프가 자네 고모님의 — 남편을 두고 위협조의 말을 흘렸거든.\"\n\n고모님은 그 뻣뻣한 자세와 겉으로의 침착함을 그대로 지킨 채, 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다.\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그건 그저 아무 뜻 없는 무례였을지도 모르지요?\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 고모님이 받았다.\n\n\"정말로 — 실례입니다만 — 그런 사람이 있고, 그것도 그자가 얼마간 손아귀에 쥐고 있는 사람이었다는 건가요?\" 트래들스가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n\"그래요, 친애하는 친구.\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n트래들스는 눈에 띄게 얼굴이 길어진 채, 자기로서는 이 문제에 손을 댈 수가 없었다고 설명했다. 그것은 미코버 씨의 채무와 같은 운명을 겪어, 그가 맺은 조건 안에 포함되지 못했고, 우리는 이제 유라이어 히프에 대해 아무런 권한도 없으며, 그자가 우리에게, 또는 우리 중 누구에게든 해코지나 골칫거리를 끼칠 수 있다면 틀림없이 그렇게 하리라는 것이었다.\n\n고모님은 조용히 있었다. 그러다 또다시 흐르는 눈물 몇 방울이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. \"당신 말이 옳아요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 얘기를 꺼내 준 건 참 사려 깊은 일이었어요.\"\n\n\"제가 — 아니면 코퍼필드가 — 무얼 좀 할 수 있을까요?\" 트래들스가 부드럽게 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 없어요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"정말 여러 번 고마워요. 트롯, 얘야, 헛된 협박이란다! 미코버 씨 부부를 다시 들어오게 하자. 그리고 너희들 누구도 나한테 말 걸지 마라!\" 그러면서 고모님은 옷매무새를 매만지고, 꼿꼿한 자세로 앉아 문 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 미코버 씨, 미코버 부인!\" 그들이 들어서자 고모님이 말했다. \"우리가 두 분의 이주 문제를 의논하고 있었답니다. 두 분을 그토록 오래 방 밖에 붙들어 둔 것은 거듭 사과드려요. 우리가 제안하는 약정이 무엇인지 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n고모님이 그것을 풀어 설명하니, 그 가족은 — 그때는 아이들까지 다 함께 있었는데 — 더할 나위 없이 흡족해했다. 그리고 그것은 모든 어음 거래의 첫 단계에 나타나는 미코버 씨의 그 어김없는 버릇을 어찌나 일깨웠던지, 그가 그 자리에서 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 약속어음에 붙일 인지를 사러 뛰쳐나가는 것을 도무지 말릴 수가 없었다. 그러나 그의 기쁨은 별안간 제동이 걸렸다. 오 분도 채 안 되어 그가 보안관 관리에게 붙들린 채 돌아와, 눈물을 쏟으며 모든 게 끝장났다고 우리에게 알렸기 때문이다. 우리는 이 일에 — 물론 유라이어 히프의 짓이었다 — 단단히 대비하고 있던 터라 곧 돈을 치렀고, 다시 오 분 뒤 미코버 씨는 탁자에 앉아 더없는 기쁨의 표정으로 인지를 채워 넣고 있었다. 그것은 오직 그렇게 마음에 꼭 드는 일, 또는 펀치를 만드는 일만이 그의 빛나는 얼굴에 온전히 가득 채워 줄 수 있는 표정이었다. 그가 예술가의 흥취로 인지에 매달려, 그것을 그림처럼 어루만지고, 곁눈질로 들여다보고, 날짜며 액수를 수첩에 묵직하게 적어 두고, 다 채운 뒤에는 그 귀한 값어치를 한껏 음미하며 들여다보는 모습은, 그야말로 볼만한 광경이었다.\n\n\"자, 선생님, 제가 한마디 권해도 좋다면, 선생님이 하실 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은요,\" 고모님이 그를 잠자코 지켜보다가 말했다. \"그 일을 다시는 영영 입에 올리지 않겠다고 맹세하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"그런 맹세를 미래의 처녀처럼 깨끗한 페이지에 새겨 넣을 작정입니다. 미코버 부인이 증언해 줄 것입니다. 저는 믿습니다,\" 미코버 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"제 아들 윌킨스가, 불행한 제 아비의 피를 독으로 물들인 그 뱀들을 손으로 다루느니 차라리 제 주먹을 불 속에 넣는 편이 한없이 낫다는 것을, 두고두고 마음에 새기기를 말입니다!\" 깊은 감회에 젖어 한순간에 절망의 화신으로 변한 미코버 씨는, 그 뱀들을 음울한 혐오의 눈길로 (그 안에는 방금 전 그가 그것들에 보내던 감탄이 채 가시지 않은 채였다) 바라보더니, 그것들을 접어 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n이로써 그날 저녁의 일은 마무리되었다. 우리는 슬픔과 피로에 지쳐 있었고, 고모님과 나는 이튿날 런던으로 돌아가기로 되어 있었다. 미코버 씨 가족은 가재도구를 중개상에게 팔아넘긴 뒤 우리를 뒤따라오기로 했고, 위크필드 씨의 일은 트래들스의 지휘 아래 되도록 빨리 매듭짓기로 했으며, 그 정리가 진행되는 동안 아그네스도 런던으로 오기로 했다. 우리는 그 옛집에서 하룻밤을 보냈는데, 히프 모자가 사라지고 나니 마치 병이 씻겨 나간 듯했다. 그리고 나는 난파해 떠돌다 집에 돌아온 나그네처럼 내 옛 방에 누웠다.\n\n이튿날 우리는 — 내 집이 아니라 — 고모님 댁으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 옛날처럼 잠자리에 들기 전 고모님과 단둘이 앉았을 때, 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"트롯, 요즘 내 마음을 짓누르던 게 무엇인지 정말로 알고 싶으냐?\"\n\n\"정말 알고 싶어요, 고모님. 제가 함께 나눌 수 없는 슬픔이나 근심을 고모님이 안고 계신 게 못마땅하게 느껴진 때가 있었다면, 바로 지금이에요.\"\n\n\"넌 이미 슬픔을 겪을 만큼 겪었잖니, 얘야.\" 고모님이 다정하게 말했다. \"거기에 내 자잘한 근심까지 보태지 않더라도 말이다. 트롯, 너한테 뭔가를 숨긴 데에 다른 뜻이 있었을 리 없어.\"\n\n\"저도 그건 잘 알아요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그러니 이제 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"내일 아침에 나랑 잠깐 마차 타고 같이 나가 주겠니?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n\"물론이죠.\"\n\n\"아홉 시에.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그때 말해 주마, 얘야.\"\n\n그리하여 아홉 시에 우리는 작은 사륜마차를 타고 나가 런던으로 향했다. 우리는 거리를 한참 달려, 큰 병원 가운데 하나에 이르렀다. 그 건물 바로 곁에 수수한 영구차 한 대가 서 있었다. 마부가 고모님을 알아보고는, 창가에서 고모님이 손짓하자 그에 따라 천천히 차를 몰기 시작했고, 우리는 그 뒤를 따라갔다.\n\n\"이제 알겠지, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 사람이 갔단다!\"\n\n\"병원에서 돌아가셨어요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n고모님은 내 곁에 꼼짝없이 앉아 있었다. 그러나 나는 또다시 그 얼굴에 흐르는 눈물을 보았다.\n\n\"전에도 한 번 거기 있었지.\" 이윽고 고모님이 말했다. \"오래 앓았어 — 부서지고 망가진 사람이었지, 이 숱한 세월 동안. 이번 마지막 병에서 제 처지를 깨닫고는, 나를 불러 달라고 하더구나. 그때는 미안해했어. 무척 미안해했지.\"\n\n\"고모님이 가셨던 거군요, 알겠어요.\"\n\n\"갔지. 그 뒤로 그 사람 곁에 꽤 오래 있어 주었단다.\"\n\n\"우리가 캔터베리로 떠나기 전날 밤에 돌아가신 건가요?\" 내가 말했다. 고모님이 고개를 끄덕였다. \"이제 아무도 그 사람을 해칠 수 없어.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그건 헛된 협박이었지.\"\n\n우리는 시내를 벗어나 혼지의 교회 묘지로 마차를 몰았다. \"거리보다는 여기가 낫지.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 사람은 여기서 태어났거든.\"\n\n우리는 마차에서 내렸다. 그러고는 그 수수한 관을 따라, 내가 잘 기억하는 한 구석으로 갔고, 거기서 관을 흙에 맡기는 예배가 낭독되었다.\n\n\"오늘로 삼십육 년 전이로구나, 얘야.\" 우리가 마차로 걸어 돌아가는 동안 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 결혼한 날이. 하느님, 우리 모두를 용서하소서!\" 우리는 말없이 자리에 앉았다. 그렇게 고모님은 내 손을 잡은 채 오랫동안 내 곁에 앉아 있었다. 마침내 고모님은 별안간 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그 사람과 결혼할 때만 해도 그이는 잘생긴 남자였단다, 트롯 — 그런데 어찌나 딱하게 변했던지!\"\n\n그것은 오래가지 않았다. 눈물로 한바탕 마음을 덜어 내고 나자, 고모님은 곧 평정을 되찾았고, 심지어 명랑해지기까지 했다. 신경이 좀 흔들렸던 거라고, 안 그랬으면 그렇게 무너지지는 않았을 거라고 고모님은 말했다. 하느님, 우리 모두를 용서하소서!\n\n그렇게 우리는 하이게이트의 고모님 작은 오두막으로 마차를 몰아 돌아왔는데, 거기서 미코버 씨가 그날 아침 우편으로 부쳐 온 다음의 짧은 쪽지를 받았다.\n\n'캔터베리,\n\n'금요일.\n\n'친애하는 부인, 그리고 코퍼필드 군에게,\n\n'요사이 지평선 위로 어렴풋이 떠오르던 그 아름다운 약속의 땅이 다시금 뚫을 수 없는 안개에 휩싸여, 운명이 봉인된 한 표류하는 가련한 자의 눈앞에서 영영 거두어졌소!\n\n'또 한 장의 영장이 (웨스트민스터의 국왕 폐하 고등 왕좌 재판소에서) 또 한 건의 소송, 즉 히프 대 미코버 사건으로 발부되었고, 그 사건의 피고는 이 관할 구역에 법적 권한을 지닌 보안관의 먹잇감이 되었소.\n'이제 그날이요, 이제 그 시각이라,\n\n싸움의 진영이 음산하게 다가오니,\n보라, 오만한 에드워드의 군세가 밀려온다 —\n\n쇠사슬과 노예살이를!\n'그것에, 그리고 머지않은 종말에 넘겨진 채 (정신의 고문이란 어느 한계를 넘어서는 견딜 수 없는 법인데, 그 한계에 내가 다다랐음을 나는 느끼오) 나의 행로는 끝이 났소. 부디 평안하시기를, 평안하시기를! 훗날 어느 길손이, 호기심에서, 부디 동정이 섞이지 않았기를 바라지는 않겠소만, 이 도시에서 채무자들에게 배정된 그 갇힌 자리를 찾았을 때, 녹슨 못으로 벽에 새겨진 글자를 더듬으며, 부디 곰곰이 헤아려 주기를 나는 믿소,\n\n'그 흐릿한 머리글자,\n\n'W. M. 을.\n'추신. 우리 공동의 벗인 토머스 트래들스 씨가 (그는 아직 우리 곁을 떠나지 않았고, 더없이 좋아 보이오) 트롯우드 양의 고귀한 이름으로 빚과 비용을 갚아 주었음을 알리고자 이 편지를 다시 펴오. 그리하여 나와 내 가족은 이 세상 더없는 행복의 꼭대기에 올라 있소.'\n\n# CHAPTER 55. TEMPEST\n\n이제 나는 내 인생의 한 사건에 다가선다. 너무도 또렷하게 새겨졌고, 너무도 끔찍하며, 이 책에서 그 앞에 펼쳐진 모든 일과 무한히 다양한 끈으로 얽혀 있어서, 이야기를 시작한 순간부터 글을 써 나가는 동안 그것이 점점 더 크게 자라나는 것을 나는 보아 왔다. 마치 평원 위에 우뚝 선 거대한 탑처럼, 그것은 내 어린 시절의 자잘한 일들에까지 미리 그림자를 드리웠다.\n\n그 일이 일어난 뒤로 여러 해 동안 나는 그것을 자주 꿈에 보았다. 어찌나 생생하게 그 인상에 사로잡혀 화들짝 깨어났던지, 고요한 밤 내 조용한 방 안에서 그 광란이 여태 휘몰아치는 듯했다. 지금 이 시각까지도, 비록 길고 들쭉날쭉한 간격을 두고서나마, 나는 이따금 그것을 꿈꾼다. 그것과 사나운 바람, 또는 바닷가를 스치듯 입에 올리는 가장 가벼운 한마디 사이에는 어떤 연결고리가 있는데, 내 마음이 의식하는 그 어떤 연결보다도 강하다. 그 일이 일어난 광경을 내가 똑똑히 보는 만큼, 그대로 적어 보려 한다. 나는 그것을 기억해 내는 게 아니라, 일어나는 그대로 본다. 그것이 내 눈앞에서 다시 벌어지고 있으니까.\n\n이민선이 출항할 날이 빠르게 다가오자, 마음씨 고운 늙은 유모가 (우리가 처음 다시 만났을 때 나를 보고 거의 가슴이 찢어질 듯했던 사람이다) 런던으로 올라왔다. 나는 그녀와, 그녀의 오빠와, 미코버 식구들과 (그들은 늘 함께 어울려 다녔다) 줄곧 같이 지냈다. 그러나 에밀리만큼은 한 번도 보지 못했다.\n\n날짜가 코앞에 닥친 어느 날 저녁, 나는 패거티와 그녀의 오빠와만 단둘이 아니 셋이 있었다. 우리 이야기는 햄에게로 흘러갔다. 그녀는 햄이 얼마나 다정하게 작별 인사를 건넸는지, 또 얼마나 사내답고 조용하게 처신했는지를 우리에게 들려주었다. 무엇보다, 가장 마음이 시달릴 거라고 그녀가 믿었던 요즈음에 더더욱 그랬다고 했다. 그 다정한 여인은 이 이야깃거리에 결코 싫증 내는 법이 없었다. 그리고 햄과 그토록 가까이 지내 온 그녀가 들려주는 숱한 사례를 듣는 우리의 관심도, 그것을 들려주는 그녀의 마음만큼이나 컸다.\n\n그 무렵 고모님과 나는 하이게이트의 오두막 두 채를 비우는 중이었다. 나는 외국으로 떠날 작정이었고, 고모님은 도버의 집으로 돌아갈 참이었다. 우리는 코번트 가든에 임시 거처를 두고 있었다. 그날 저녁 이야기를 마치고 그곳으로 걸어 돌아오면서, 내가 마지막으로 야머스에 갔을 때 햄과 나 사이에 오갔던 말을 곰곰이 떠올리다가, 처음에 세웠던 계획 — 배 위에서 그녀의 외삼촌과 작별할 때 에밀리에게 편지 한 통을 남기겠다는 — 이 마음에서 흔들렸고, 지금 그녀에게 써 보내는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 들었다. 내 글을 받고 나면 그녀가 불행한 연인에게 작별의 한마디라도 나를 통해 전하고 싶어 할지 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 나는 그 기회를 그녀에게 주어야 마땅했다.\n\n그래서 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 내 방에 앉아 그녀에게 편지를 썼다. 나는 햄을 만났다는 것, 그리고 햄이 이 종이 위 제자리에 이미 적어 둔 그 말을 그녀에게 전해 달라고 내게 부탁했다는 것을 말했다. 나는 그 말을 고스란히 되풀이했다. 설령 내게 그럴 권리가 있었더라도, 거기에 군말을 보탤 필요는 없었다. 그 깊은 진심과 선량함은 나든 그 누구든 꾸밀 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 나는 아침에 부쳐지도록 편지를 따로 빼 두었고, 패거티 씨에게 그것을 그녀에게 전해 달라고 청하는 짧은 글도 함께 적었다. 그러고는 동틀 무렵에야 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n그때 나는 내가 아는 것보다 더 쇠약해져 있었다. 해가 떠오를 때까지 잠들지 못한 탓에, 이튿날은 늦도록 누워 있었고 잠을 자고도 개운치 않았다. 나는 머리맡에 말없이 와 서 있는 고모님의 기척에 잠에서 깼다. 잠결에 그것을 느꼈는데, 우리 모두 그런 것은 그렇게 느끼는 모양이다.\n\n\"트롯, 얘야,\" 내가 눈을 뜨자 고모님이 말했다. \"도무지 너를 깨울 마음이 안 나더구나. 패거티 씨가 와 계신다. 올라오라고 할까?\"\n\n나는 그러라고 대답했고, 그는 곧 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님,\" 우리가 악수를 나눈 뒤 그가 말했다. \"에밀리한테 도련님 편지를 줬구먼유. 그랬더니 그 애가 이걸 써서는, 도련님더러 읽어 봐 주십사 부탁하더라구유. 그래서 거기 거리낄 게 없거든, 부디 맡아 주십사 하던데유.\"\n\n\"읽어 봤어요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그는 슬프게 고개를 끄덕였다. 나는 편지를 펼쳐서 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"보내 주신 말씀 받았어요. 아, 제게 베풀어 주신 그 선하고 복된 친절을 무어라 글로 적어 고마움을 전할 수 있을까요!\n\n저는 그 말씀을 가슴에 꼭 품었어요. 죽을 때까지 간직할 거예요. 날카로운 가시 같지만, 그래도 그렇게 위안이 돼요. 저는 그 말씀을 두고 기도했어요, 아, 정말 많이도 기도했어요. 당신이 어떤 분인지, 외삼촌이 어떤 분인지를 헤아릴 때면, 하느님이 어떤 분이실지를 떠올리며 그분께 울며 매달릴 수 있게 돼요.\n\n영원히 안녕히. 이제, 내 소중한 분, 내 벗이여, 이 세상에서 영원히 안녕히. 다른 세상에서, 혹 제가 용서받는다면, 어린아이로 깨어나 당신께 갈 수 있을지도 몰라요. 온갖 고마움과 축복을 빌어요. 영원토록, 안녕히.\"\n\n눈물로 얼룩진 이것이 그 편지였다.\n\n\"도련님이 거기 거리낄 게 없다고 하시고, 또 부디 맡아 주시겠다고, 그 애한테 전해도 될깝쇼, 데이비 도련님?\" 내가 다 읽고 나자 패거티 씨가 말했다.\n\"두말하면 잔소리지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그런데 — 한 가지 생각하는 게 있어요 —\"\n\n\"네, 데이비 도련님?\"\n\n\"생각하는데,\" 내가 말했다. \"다시 한 번 야머스에 내려갔다 와야겠어요. 배가 떠나기 전에 다녀올 시간은 넉넉하고도 남아요. 외톨이로 지내는 그 사람 생각이 자꾸만 머릿속을 떠나지 않아요. 그녀가 직접 쓴 이 편지를 바로 지금 그의 손에 쥐여 주고, 또 헤어지는 그 순간에 그녀에게 그가 편지를 받았노라고 당신이 전해 줄 수 있게 한다면, 그건 두 사람 모두에게 베푸는 친절일 거예요. 나는 그의 부탁을 엄숙히 받아들였어요, 마음씨 고운 그 친구의 부탁을요. 그러니 아무리 빈틈없이 해내도 지나치지 않아요. 여행쯤은 내게 아무것도 아니에요. 나는 마음이 안절부절못하니, 몸을 움직이는 편이 차라리 나을 거예요. 오늘 밤에 내려가겠어요.\"\n\n그는 애써 나를 말리려 들었으나, 나는 그도 속으로는 나와 같은 마음임을 알았다. 그리고 이것은, 만일 내가 결심을 굳힐 확인이 필요했다면, 바로 그런 효과를 냈을 것이다. 그는 내 부탁대로 역마차 사무소에 다녀와서, 우편마차 마부석 옆자리를 잡아 주었다. 저녁에 나는 그 마차를 타고, 그토록 많은 부침을 겪으며 오갔던 그 길을 따라 출발했다.\n\n\"이게 말이오,\" 런던을 벗어난 첫 정거장에서 내가 마부에게 물었다. \"참 별난 하늘 아니오? 이런 하늘은 본 기억이 없는데.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇소 — 이만한 건 처음이오.\" 그가 대답했다. \"바람이오, 손님. 머지않아 바다에서 사달이 날 게요, 내 짐작엔.\"\n\n그것은 음울한 뒤죽박죽이었다 — 군데군데 축축한 땔감에서 피어오르는 연기 빛깔로 얼룩진 — 날아다니는 구름이 기막히게 거대한 더미로 솟구쳐, 땅속 가장 깊은 골짜기 바닥까지의 깊이보다 구름 속의 높이가 더 까마득해 보였고, 그 사이로 거친 달이 곤두박질치듯 빠져들었다. 마치 자연의 법칙이 두려이 흐트러진 가운데 길을 잃고 겁에 질리기라도 한 듯이. 하루 종일 바람이 불었는데, 그때 더 거세지면서 유난히 큰 소리를 내고 있었다. 한 시간이 더 지나자 바람은 한결 사나워졌고, 하늘은 더욱 흐려졌으며, 세차게 불어 댔다.\n\n그러나 밤이 깊어 갈수록 구름이 한데 모여들어 하늘 전체를 빽빽이 뒤덮자, 그때는 칠흑같이 어두웠고, 바람은 점점 더, 점점 더 사납게 몰아치기 시작했다. 그것은 줄곧 거세져, 우리 말들이 좀처럼 바람을 마주하지 못할 지경에 이르렀다. 밤의 캄캄한 무렵 (때는 구월 하순이라 밤이 짧지 않았다)에 여러 번 선두의 말들이 몸을 돌리거나 딱 멈춰 섰고, 우리는 마차가 뒤집히지나 않을까 자주 심각하게 두려워했다. 휘몰아치는 빗줄기가 이 폭풍 앞에 들이닥쳤는데, 마치 강철 화살이 소나기처럼 쏟아지는 듯했다. 그럴 때, 나무나 바람막이 담 따위 몸을 피할 데가 조금이라도 있으면, 도무지 그 싸움을 더 이어 갈 수 없어 우리는 멈춰 서기를 청했다.\n\n날이 밝자 바람은 점점 더, 점점 더 거세게 불었다. 나는 야머스에 있을 때 뱃사람들이 대포처럼 바람이 분다고 하는 말을 들은 적이 있었으나, 이런 바람은, 아니 이에 견줄 만한 무엇도 일찍이 알지 못했다. 우리는 입스위치에 다다랐다 — 런던에서 십 마일 벗어난 뒤로 한 치 한 치 길을 싸워 가며 와야 했던 탓에 몹시 늦은 시각이었다 — 그리고 굴뚝이 무너질까 두려워 밤중에 잠자리에서 일어난 사람들이 장터에 한 무리 모여 있는 것을 보았다. 그중 몇몇은 우리가 말을 바꾸는 동안 여관 마당에 모여서, 높은 교회 탑에서 커다란 납판이 뜯겨 나가 골목길로 내동댕이쳐져 그 길이 막혀 버렸다는 이야기를 들려주었다. 또 어떤 이들은, 이웃 마을에서 들어온 시골 사람들이 큰 나무들이 뿌리째 뽑혀 나뒹구는 모습이며, 낟가리가 통째로 길과 들판에 흩뿌려진 광경을 보았다고 전했다. 그래도 폭풍은 누그러질 줄 모르고, 오히려 더 사납게 불어 댔다.\n\n우리가 안간힘을 다해 바다 쪽으로, 이 거대한 바람이 곧장 뭍을 향해 불어오는 그 바다 쪽으로 점점 더 다가갈수록, 그 위력은 점점 더 무시무시해졌다. 바다가 보이기 한참 전부터 그 물보라가 우리 입술에 닿았고, 짠 비를 우리에게 뿌려 댔다. 야머스에 잇닿은 평평한 시골 땅 수 마일, 수 마일에 걸쳐 물이 넘쳐 났다. 그리고 못이며 웅덩이마다 제 둑을 후려쳤고, 우리 쪽으로 묵직하게 밀려드는 작은 물결을 세차게 일으켰다. 바다가 시야에 들어왔을 때, 출렁이는 심연 위로 이따금 솟구쳐 지평선에 걸린 파도는, 탑과 건물이 늘어선 또 다른 해안을 언뜻언뜻 보는 것 같았다. 마침내 우리가 마을 안으로 들어서자, 사람들이 죄다 비스듬히 몸을 기울이고 머리카락을 흩날리며 문간으로 나와서, 이런 밤을 뚫고 들어온 우편마차를 신기해했다.\n\n나는 그 옛 여관에 들었다가, 바다를 보러 내려갔다. 모래와 해초와 흩날리는 물거품 얼룩으로 어지러운 거리를 비틀비틀 걸으며, 떨어지는 슬레이트와 기와가 무서워, 바람이 사납게 휘몰아치는 모퉁이에서는 마주치는 사람들을 붙잡으면서. 해변 가까이 다가가니, 뱃사람들뿐 아니라 마을 사람들 절반이 건물 뒤에 숨어 있는 것이 보였다. 더러는 이따금 폭풍의 광란을 무릅쓰고 바다 쪽을 내다보았다가, 갈지자로 되돌아오려다 제 방향에서 완전히 밀려나기도 했다.\n\n이 무리에 끼어들고 보니, 청어잡이 배나 굴잡이 배를 타고 나간 남편을 둔 여인들이 통곡하고 있었다. 그 배들이 어디 안전한 데로 미처 피하기도 전에 가라앉았으리라고 여길 까닭이 너무도 많았기 때문이다. 사람들 틈에는 머리가 희끗희끗한 늙은 뱃사람들도 있어서, 바다에서 하늘로 눈을 옮기며 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 서로 무어라 중얼거렸다. 들뜨고 불안한 선주들도 있었다. 아이들은 한데 옹송그려서, 어른들 얼굴을 빤히 올려다보았다. 심지어 건장한 뱃사람들조차 동요하고 불안해하며, 몸을 피한 자리 뒤에서 마치 적을 살피기라도 하듯 바다를 향해 망원경을 겨누었다.\n\n눈을 멀게 하는 바람과, 날아드는 돌과 모래와, 그 끔찍한 굉음의 소용돌이 속에서, 잠깐이나마 틈을 내어 바라볼 수 있었던 그 거대한 바다 자체가 나를 아연실색하게 만들었다. 높다란 물의 벽이 굽이쳐 밀려와, 그 절정에서 부서져 포말이 되어 무너질 때면, 그중 가장 작은 것 하나라도 마을을 통째로 삼켜 버릴 것만 같았다. 물러가는 파도가 쉰 목소리로 으르렁대며 휩쓸려 나갈 때면, 마치 땅을 파헤치려는 속셈이라도 있는 듯 해변에 깊은 동굴을 파내는 것 같았다. 머리가 허연 너울 몇몇이 우레처럼 밀려들다가 뭍에 닿기도 전에 산산조각 부서질 때면, 방금까지 한 덩어리였던 그 파편 하나하나가 제 분노의 온 위력에 사로잡힌 듯, 또 다른 괴물의 몸뚱이로 합쳐지려고 내달렸다. 굽이치는 언덕이 골짜기로 바뀌고, 굽이치는 골짜기는 (이따금 외로운 폭풍새 한 마리가 그 사이를 스쳐 갔다) 언덕으로 치솟았다. 물의 덩어리가 우르릉 소리를 내며 해변을 부르르 떨게 하고 뒤흔들었다. 모든 형상이 생겨나기 무섭게 어지러이 굴러가, 제 모습과 자리를 바꾸고, 또 다른 모습과 자리를 후려쳐 쫓아냈다. 지평선 위 탑과 건물이 늘어선 그 환상의 해안이 솟았다 가라앉았다 했다. 구름은 빠르고 두텁게 내리덮였다. 나는 온 자연이 찢기고 솟구치는 광경을 보는 것만 같았다.\n\n이 잊지 못할 바람이 — 그곳에서는 여태 그 해안에 불어닥친 가장 거센 바람으로 기억되고 있다 — 한데 불러 모은 사람들 틈에서 햄을 찾지 못하자, 나는 그의 집으로 가는 길을 더듬었다. 집은 잠겨 있었고, 두드려도 아무도 대답하지 않기에, 나는 뒷길과 골목을 돌아 그가 일하던 작업장으로 갔다. 거기서 나는 그가 자기 솜씨가 필요한 어떤 급한 배 수리 일이 생겨 그것을 처리하러 로스토프트로 갔으나, 이튿날 아침 넉넉한 시간에 돌아올 거라는 말을 들었다.\n\n나는 여관으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 씻고 옷을 갈아입고 잠을 청해 보았으나 헛수고였고, 그러는 사이 오후 다섯 시가 되었다. 휴게실 난롯가에 앉은 지 오 분도 안 되어, 불을 쑤시러 온 웨이터가 말을 붙일 핑계 삼아, 몇 마일 떨어진 곳에서 석탄 운반선 두 척이 선원 전원과 함께 가라앉았다고 했다. 그리고 다른 배 몇 척도 만 어귀에서 안간힘을 쓰며, 안절부절못하는 가운데 뭍에서 떨어지려고 애쓰는 것이 목격되었다고 했다. 어젯밤 같은 밤이 또 닥치면, 그들에게, 그리고 가엾은 뱃사람들 모두에게 부디 자비가 있기를, 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 마음이 몹시 가라앉았다. 무척이나 외로웠다. 그리고 햄이 거기 없다는 사실에, 상황에 어울리지 않을 만큼 큰 불안을 느꼈다. 나는 얼마나 깊이인지도 모른 채 최근의 일들에 심각하게 흔들려 있었고, 사나운 바람에 오래 노출된 탓에 머릿속이 어수선했다. 내 생각과 기억이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽이었던지, 시간과 거리를 또렷이 가늠하던 능력을 잃어버렸다. 그래서 만일 내가 마을로 나갔다면, 그 순간 분명 런던에 있을 게 틀림없다고 알고 있는 누군가와 마주쳤더라도, 내 생각엔, 그리 놀라지 않았을 듯하다. 말하자면 이런 점에서 내 마음에는 묘한 부주의가 깃들어 있었다. 그러면서도 그 마음은, 그 장소가 자연히 일깨우는 온갖 추억으로 분주하기도 했다. 그리고 그 추억들은 유난히 또렷하고 생생했다.\n\n이런 상태에서, 배들에 관한 웨이터의 음울한 소식은, 내 의지의 어떤 노력도 없이, 곧장 햄에 대한 내 불안과 이어졌다. 나는 그가 로스토프트에서 바닷길로 돌아오다가 목숨을 잃을지 모른다는 예감에 사로잡혔다는 확신이 들었다. 이것이 내 안에서 어찌나 강하게 자라났던지, 나는 저녁을 들기 전에 작업장으로 다시 가서, 그가 바닷길로 돌아오려 할 가능성이 조금이라도 있다고 보는지 배 만드는 사람에게 물어봐야겠다고 마음먹었다. 만일 그가 그럴 법하다고 여길 만한 빌미를 조금이라도 준다면, 나는 로스토프트로 건너가 그를 데리고 와서 그 일을 막겠다고 다짐했다.\n\n나는 서둘러 저녁을 시켜 놓고, 작업장으로 되돌아갔다. 늦지 않게 갔으니 다행이었다. 배 만드는 사람이 손에 등불을 들고 작업장 문을 잠그던 참이었으니까. 그는 내가 그 질문을 던지자 한껏 웃어 보이며, 걱정할 것 없다고 했다. 제정신인 사람이든 아니든, 이런 폭풍 같은 바람 속에 배를 띄울 사람은 없다고, 더더구나 천성이 뱃사람으로 태어난 햄 패거티라면 어림없다고 했다.\n\n이 점을 미리 잘 알고 있었던 터라, 그러면서도 떠밀리듯 한 그 짓이 정말이지 부끄럽게 느껴진 채로, 나는 여관으로 돌아왔다. 그런 바람이 더 거세질 수 있다면, 내 생각엔 정말로 거세지고 있었다. 울부짖고 으르렁대는 소리, 문과 창문이 덜컹대는 소리, 굴뚝 속에서 우르릉대는 소리, 나를 품어 주는 바로 그 집이 통째로 흔들리는 듯한 느낌, 그리고 바다의 어마어마한 아우성이 아침보다 한층 더 무시무시했다. 그러나 이제는 거기에 더해 짙은 어둠까지 깔려서, 그것이 폭풍에 새로운 공포를, 실재하는 공포와 허깨비 같은 공포를 함께 입혀 놓았다.\n\n먹을 수도, 가만히 앉아 있을 수도, 무엇 하나에 진득이 매달릴 수도 없었다. 내 안의 무언가가 바깥의 폭풍에 희미하게 화답하며, 내 기억의 밑바닥을 들쑤셔 그 속에 소란을 일으켰다. 그런데도, 우르릉대는 바다와 함께 거칠게 내달리는 내 모든 생각의 혼란 속에서 — 그 폭풍과, 햄에 대한 내 불안이 언제나 맨 앞에 자리하고 있었다.\n\n내 저녁상은 거의 손도 대지 않은 채 물려졌고, 나는 포도주 한두 잔으로 기운을 차려 보려 했다. 헛일이었다. 나는 난롯가에서 멍하니 선잠에 빠져들었으나, 바깥의 아우성도, 내가 있는 곳도 의식을 놓치지는 않았다. 그 둘 다 새롭고 형언할 수 없는 공포에 가려지더니, 내가 깨어났을 때 — 아니, 의자에 나를 옭아매던 그 무기력을 떨쳐 냈을 때 — 온몸이 까닭도 알 수 없고 정체도 모를 두려움에 부르르 떨렸다.\n\n나는 이리저리 서성거렸고, 낡은 지명 사전을 읽어 보려 했으며, 그 끔찍한 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 불 속에서 얼굴들과 광경과 형체들을 들여다보았다. 마침내, 벽에 걸린 시계가 아무 동요 없이 째깍거리는 그 한결같은 소리가 어찌나 나를 괴롭히던지, 나는 잠자리에 들기로 마음먹었다.\n\n이런 밤에, 여관 하인 몇이 의논 끝에 아침까지 함께 깨어 있기로 했다는 말을 들으니 마음이 놓였다. 나는 몹시 지치고 무거운 몸으로 잠자리에 들었으나, 막상 눕고 나니 그런 느낌이 마법처럼 죄다 사라지고, 나는 모든 감각이 또렷이 곤두선 채로 말똥말똥 깨어 있었다.\n\n몇 시간이고 나는 그렇게 누워 바람과 물소리에 귀를 기울였다. 어느 순간엔 바다 저편에서 비명이 들리는 듯했고, 또 어느 순간엔 신호용 대포 쏘는 소리가 또렷이 들리는 듯했으며, 또 어느 순간엔 마을의 집들이 무너지는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 나는 몇 번이나 일어나 밖을 내다보았으나, 켜 둔 채로 둔 희미한 촛불이 창유리에 비친 모습과, 캄캄한 허공에서 나를 들여다보는 내 자신의 초췌한 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다.\n\n마침내 내 안절부절못함이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 나는 황급히 옷을 걸치고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 들보에 베이컨과 양파 두름이 걸려 있는 것이 어슴푸레 보이는 그 널찍한 부엌에서, 야경을 서는 사람들이 큰 굴뚝에서 일부러 떨어뜨려 문 가까이 끌어다 놓은 탁자를 둘러싸고 갖가지 자세로 옹기종기 모여 있었다. 앞치마로 귀를 틀어막고 두 눈은 문에 못 박은 한 예쁜 처녀가, 내가 나타나자 유령인 줄 알고 비명을 질렀다. 그러나 나머지 사람들은 좀 더 침착해서, 일행이 하나 늘어난 것을 반겼다. 한 사내가 그들이 나누던 화제를 들먹이며, 가라앉은 석탄 운반선 선원들의 영혼이 이 폭풍 속에 떠돌고 있다고 생각하는지 내게 물었다.\n\n나는 거기서, 짐작건대, 두 시간쯤 머물렀다. 한 번은 작업장 문을 열고 텅 빈 거리를 내다보았다. 모래와 해초와 거품 조각들이 휘몰려 지나갔다. 그리고 나는 도움을 청한 뒤에야 다시 문을 닫고 바람에 맞서 단단히 잠글 수 있었다.\n\n마침내 내 외딴 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 그 방엔 어두운 적막이 감돌았다. 그러나 이제는 지친 터라, 다시 잠자리에 들자, 탑에서 — 그리고 벼랑 아래로 — 떨어지듯 깊은 잠 속으로 빠져들었다. 오랜 시간 동안, 다른 곳, 갖가지 장면 속에 있는 꿈을 꾸면서도, 꿈속에선 늘 바람이 불고 있었다는 느낌이 든다. 마침내 나는 현실에 매달린 그 가냘픈 끈마저 놓쳤고, 두 소중한 벗과 — 그러나 그들이 누구였는지는 모르겠다 — 함께, 우르릉대는 포격 소리 속에서 어느 마을을 포위 공격하는 데 가담해 있었다.\n\n대포의 우레가 어찌나 크고 끊임없던지, 나는 그토록 듣고 싶었던 무언가를 도무지 들을 수가 없다가, 안간힘을 다 쓰고서야 잠에서 깼다. 환한 대낮이었다 — 여덟아홉 시쯤. 포대 대신 폭풍이 휘몰아치고 있었고, 누군가 내 방문을 두드리며 부르고 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 내가 외쳤다.\n\n\"난파선이오! 바로 코앞에!\"\n\n나는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 무슨 난파선이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"스페인인지 포르투갈인지에서 온 스쿠너요, 과일이랑 포도주를 싣고요. 서두르시오, 손님, 보고 싶으시거든! 해변에선 다들 저 배가 당장이라도 산산조각 날 거라고 하오.\"\n\n흥분한 목소리가 계단을 따라 떠들썩하게 멀어졌다. 나는 되도록 빨리 옷을 둘러 입고, 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n벌써 숱한 사람들이 나보다 앞서서, 모두 한 방향으로, 해변을 향해 달려가고 있었다. 나도 같은 길로 달려 꽤 많은 사람을 앞질렀고, 이내 사나운 바다를 마주하게 되었다.\n\n이 무렵 바람은 어쩌면 조금 잦아들었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은, 내가 꿈에서 들었던 포격 소리가 수백 문 가운데 예닐곱 문이 잠잠해진 정도로 줄어든 것이나 다름없을 만큼 미미했다. 그러나 바다는, 밤새도록 더 들끓은 것이 얹혀, 내가 마지막으로 보았을 때보다 한없이 더 무시무시했다. 그때 보였던 모든 모습이 죄다 부풀어 오른 표정을 띠고 있었다. 파도가 솟구치는 높이며, 서로를 넘겨다보며 서로를 짓누르고 끝없는 무리를 이루어 굽이쳐 밀려드는 그 광경은 더없이 소름 끼쳤다. 바람과 파도 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않는 데다, 인파와, 형언할 수 없는 혼란과, 날씨에 맞서 버티려는 내 첫 숨 가쁜 안간힘 속에서, 나는 어찌나 정신이 없던지 난파선을 찾아 바다를 내다보았으나 거대한 파도의 포말 진 꼭대기 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 내 옆에 선, 옷도 채 못 갖춰 입은 한 뱃사람이 (문신으로 새긴 화살이 같은 방향을 가리키고 있는) 맨팔로 왼쪽을 가리켰다. 그제야, 아, 위대하신 하느님, 나는 그것을 보았다, 우리 코앞에 바짝 다가와 있는 것을!\n\n돛대 하나가 갑판에서 육칠 피트쯤 되는 자리에서 뚝 부러져 뱃전 너머로 늘어진 채, 돛과 밧줄이 뒤엉킨 미로 속에 얽혀 있었다. 배가 흔들리고 부딪칠 때마다 — 한순간도 쉬지 않고, 도무지 상상조차 못 할 만큼 사납게 그랬다 — 그 부서진 잔해가 뱃전을 뚫고 들어올 듯이 후려쳤다. 그때 이미 이 난파의 한 부분을 잘라 내려는 안간힘이 펼쳐지고 있었다. 옆구리를 드러낸 채 흔들리던 배가 우리 쪽으로 돌아설 때, 나는 사람들이 도끼를 들고 일하는 모습을 또렷이 알아보았는데, 그중에서도 긴 곱슬머리를 한 활기찬 한 사람이 나머지 가운데 유독 눈에 띄었다. 그러나 바로 그 순간, 바람과 물소리 너머로도 들릴 만큼 큰 비명이 해변에서 터져 나왔다. 바다가 흔들리는 난파선 위를 휩쓸며 깨끗이 한 차례 휘몰아쳐, 사람들과 활대와 통과 널빤지와 뱃전 난간과, 그런 장난감 같은 것들을 한 무더기로 들끓는 물결 속으로 쓸어 갔다.\n\n두 번째 돛대는 아직 서 있었는데, 찢긴 돛 누더기와, 끊어진 밧줄들이 이리저리 거칠게 펄럭이며 어지러이 뒤엉켜 있었다. 배가 한 차례 부딪쳤다가, 그러고는 밀려 올라가 다시 부딪쳤다고, 아까 그 뱃사람이 내 귀에 쉰 목소리로 말했다. 나는 그가 배가 한가운데서 두 동강 나고 있다고 덧붙인 것으로 알아들었고, 그럴 만하다고 쉬이 짐작했다. 그 흔들림과 부딪침이 어떤 사람의 솜씨로도 오래 견디기엔 너무도 어마어마했으니까. 그가 말하는 사이, 해변에서 또 한 차례 큰 연민의 비명이 일었다. 네 사람이 난파선과 함께 심연에서 솟아올라, 남은 돛대의 밧줄에 매달려 있었다. 그 맨 꼭대기에는, 곱슬머리를 한 활기찬 그 사람이 있었다.\n\n배에는 종이 하나 달려 있었다. 그리고 배가 미쳐 날뛰는 절망에 빠진 짐승처럼 흔들리고 부딪칠 때마다 — 옆으로 거의 자빠지듯 뭍을 향해 돌아설 때면 갑판 전체를 우리에게 다 드러내 보였다가, 사납게 솟구쳐 넘어가 바다를 향해 돌아설 때면 용골 말고는 아무것도 안 보였다 — 그 종이 울렸다. 그리고 그 소리는, 저 불행한 사람들의 조종(弔鐘) 소리는, 바람을 타고 우리 쪽으로 실려 왔다. 우리는 또 한 번 배를 놓쳤고, 배는 또 한 번 솟아올랐다. 두 사람이 사라지고 없었다. 해변의 비통함이 더해 갔다. 사내들은 신음하며 두 손을 맞잡았고, 여인들은 비명을 지르며 얼굴을 돌렸다. 더러는 도움이 닿을 수 없는 곳에서 도와 달라 외치며 해변을 따라 미친 듯이 오르내렸다. 나도 그중 하나가 되어, 내가 아는 한 무리의 뱃사람들에게, 저 길 잃은 두 사람이 우리 눈앞에서 죽어 가게 두지 말아 달라고 미친 듯이 애원하고 있었다.\n\n그들은 동요한 기색으로 내게 무어라 알려 주려 했다 — 나는 그게 어떤 말이었는지 모르겠다, 겨우 들리는 토막마저 알아들을 만큼 마음이 가라앉아 있지 못했으니까 — 한 시간 전에 구명정이 용감하게 사람들을 태워 나갔으나 아무것도 할 수 없었다는 것, 그리고 밧줄 하나 끌고 물속을 헤치며 나아가 뭍과 연락을 잇겠다고 나설 만큼 무모한 사람은 아무도 없으니, 이제 더 해 볼 것이 남지 않았다는 것이었다. 그때 나는 해변의 사람들을 어떤 새로운 감정이 동요시키는 것을 알아챘고, 그들이 갈라서며, 햄이 그 사이를 헤치고 앞으로 나오는 것을 보았다.\n\n나는 그에게 달려갔다 — 내가 아는 한, 도와 달라는 호소를 되풀이하려고. 그러나 너무도 낯설고 끔찍한 광경에 넋이 나가 있던 와중에도, 그의 얼굴에 어린 결심과, 바다를 내다보는 그 눈빛이 — 에밀리가 달아난 이튿날 아침과 관련해 내가 기억하던 바로 그 눈빛이 — 그가 처한 위험을 깨닫게 했다. 나는 두 팔로 그를 붙들고, 방금까지 이야기를 나누던 그 사람들에게 부디 그의 말을 듣지 말라고, 살인을 저지르지 말라고, 그를 저 모래에서 한 발짝도 움직이게 하지 말라고 애원했다.\n\n해변에서 또 한 차례 비명이 일었다. 난파선을 보니, 그 모진 돛이 후려치고 또 후려쳐, 두 사람 중 아래쪽 사람을 쳐서 떨어뜨리고, 의기양양하게 솟구쳐 돛대에 홀로 남은 그 활기찬 사람 둘레로 펄럭였다.\n\n그런 광경 앞에서, 그리고 이미 거기 모인 사람 절반을 이끄는 데 익숙한, 차분하게 절망한 그 사람의 결심 앞에서, 차라리 나는 바람에게 호소하는 편이 더 가망 있었을 것이다. \"데이비 도련님,\" 그가 쾌활하게 내 두 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"지가 갈 때가 됐으면, 된 거지유. 안 됐으면, 기다릴 거구유. 위에 계신 주님이 도련님께 복 주시고, 모두에게 복 주시기를! 친구들, 채비해 주게! 나 나갈 테니!\"\n\n나는 떠밀려 나갔으나, 모질지는 않게, 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳으로 밀려나, 둘러싼 사람들이 나를 거기 붙들어 두었다. 내가 어렴풋이 알아듣기로, 그가 도움을 받든 안 받든 기어이 나갈 작정이니, 그의 안전을 위한 채비를 맡은 이들을 내가 성가시게 하면 그 채비를 위태롭게 할 뿐이라는 것이었다. 내가 무어라 대답했는지, 그들이 무어라 받아쳤는지 나는 모르겠다. 그러나 나는 해변에서 사람들이 분주히 움직이는 것을, 거기 놓인 양묘기에서 밧줄을 들고 사내들이 달려가, 그를 내게서 가려 버린 사람들의 동그라미 속으로 파고드는 것을 보았다. 그러고는 그가 홀로 서 있는 것을 보았다, 뱃사람의 작업복과 바지 차림으로. 밧줄 한 가닥을 손에 쥐었거나 손목에 감고, 또 한 가닥은 몸에 둘렀으며, 가장 솜씨 좋은 사람 몇이 조금 떨어진 데서 그 뒤쪽 밧줄을 붙들었는데, 그 밧줄은 그가 직접 발치 모래밭에 느슨히 풀어 놓은 것이었다.\n\n내 서투른 눈에도, 난파선은 부서져 가고 있었다. 나는 배가 한가운데서 갈라지고 있다는 것, 그리고 돛대에 홀로 매달린 그 사람의 목숨이 실낱에 걸려 있다는 것을 알아보았다. 그래도 그는 거기 매달려 있었다. 그는 색다른 빨간 모자를 쓰고 있었는데 — 뱃사람의 모자 같지는 않고, 좀 더 고운 빛깔이었다 — 그를 파멸에서 갈라놓은 몇 안 되는 휘청대는 널빤지가 출렁이며 불룩 솟고, 미리 울리는 그의 조종 소리가 울려 퍼지는 가운데, 우리 모두는 그가 그 모자를 흔드는 것을 보았다. 나는 지금 그가 그러는 것을 보았고, 그 몸짓이 한때 소중했던 한 벗에 대한 옛 기억을 내 마음에 불러일으켰을 때, 나는 정신이 나가 버리는 줄 알았다.\n\n햄은 홀로 서서, 등 뒤로는 숨죽인 침묵을, 앞으로는 폭풍을 둔 채, 바다를 지켜보았다. 그러다 크게 물러나는 파도가 일자, 그는 제 몸에 단단히 둘러맨 밧줄을 붙든 사람들을 한 번 흘끗 돌아보고는, 그 파도를 따라 뛰어들었다. 그러고는 순식간에 물과 맞붙어 싸웠다. 언덕과 함께 솟구치고, 골짜기와 함께 떨어지고, 포말 아래로 사라졌다가, 다시 뭍으로 당겨졌다. 사람들은 황급히 밧줄을 끌어당겼다.\n\n그는 다쳐 있었다. 내가 선 자리에서도 그의 얼굴에 피가 보였다. 그러나 그는 그런 것엔 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그는 자기를 좀 더 자유롭게 풀어 달라고 사람들에게 황급히 무어라 지시하는 듯했고 — 적어도 그의 팔짓으로 보아 그랬다 — 이내 아까처럼 떠나갔다.\n\n그리고 이제 그는 난파선을 향해 나아갔다. 언덕과 함께 솟구치고, 골짜기와 함께 떨어지고, 거친 포말 아래로 사라지고, 뭍 쪽으로 떠밀렸다가, 배 쪽으로 떠밀려 가며, 안간힘을 다해 용감하게 헤쳐 나갔다. 거리는 아무것도 아니었으나, 바다와 바람의 위력이 그 싸움을 목숨을 건 것으로 만들었다. 마침내 그는 난파선에 다가갔다. 어찌나 가까웠던지, 그 힘찬 손짓 한 번만 더하면 거기 매달릴 참이었는데 — 배 너머에서 뭍을 향해 밀려오던, 높다랗고 시퍼런, 거대한 산비탈 같은 물이, 그가 거기로 힘껏 솟구쳐 뛰어든 듯하더니, 배가 사라져 버렸다!\n\n밧줄을 끌어당기던 자리로 달려가다가, 나는 바다에 소용돌이치는 파편 몇 조각을 보았다. 마치 통 하나가 부서지기라도 한 것처럼. 모두의 얼굴에 경악이 어렸다. 사람들이 그를 바로 내 발치로 끌어왔다 — 정신을 잃은 채 — 숨이 끊긴 채. 그는 가장 가까운 집으로 옮겨졌다. 이제 아무도 나를 막지 않았기에, 나는 그의 곁에 머물며, 온갖 소생술을 펼치는 동안 분주히 거들었다. 그러나 그는 그 거대한 파도에 맞아 죽은 것이었고, 그의 너그러운 심장은 영영 멎어 버렸다.\n\n희망이 버려지고 모든 것이 끝났을 때, 내가 침대 곁에 앉아 있자니, 에밀리와 내가 어린아이였던 시절부터, 그리고 그 후로 줄곧 나를 알아 온 한 어부가 문간에서 내 이름을 속삭였다.\n\n\"손님,\" 그가 말했다. 풍상에 찌든 그의 얼굴에 눈물이 솟구쳤고, 그 얼굴은 떨리는 입술과 더불어 잿빛으로 새파랬다. \"저쪽으로 좀 와 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n내게 되살아났던 그 옛 기억이, 그의 눈빛 속에 있었다. 나는 공포에 질려, 그가 나를 부축하려고 내민 팔에 기대며 물었다.\n\n\"시신이 떠밀려 왔소?\"\n\n그가 말했다. \"예.\"\n\n\"내가 아는 사람이오?\" 그제야 내가 물었다.\n\n그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n다만 그는 나를 해변으로 이끌었다. 그리고 그녀와 내가, 두 아이가 조개껍데기를 줍던 그 해변의 한 자리에서 — 어젯밤 무너져 내린 그 낡은 배의 좀 더 가벼운 파편들이 바람에 흩뿌려진 그 자리에서 — 그가 저버렸던 그 보금자리의 잔해 가운데서 — 나는 그가, 학교에서 그가 누워 있는 모습을 내가 자주 보았던 그대로, 한쪽 팔에 머리를 베고 누워 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 56. THE NEW WOUND, AND THE OLD\n\n아, 스티어포스여, 우리가 마지막으로 함께 이야기를 나누던 그 시각, 그것이 우리의 작별 시각이리라고는 꿈에도 몰랐던 그때, 굳이 그런 말을 할 필요는 없었다. \"나를 가장 좋은 모습으로 기억해 줘!\"라고 말할 필요는 없었다. 나는 늘 그렇게 해 왔으니까. 그리고 이 광경을 마주한 지금, 내가 그 마음을 바꿀 수 있겠는가!\n\n사람들이 손으로 드는 들것을 가져와 그를 거기 눕히고 깃발로 덮은 다음, 들어 올려 집들이 있는 쪽으로 옮겼다. 그를 옮기는 사람들은 모두 그를 알았고, 그와 함께 배를 타고 바다로 나가 본 적이 있었으며, 그가 쾌활하고 대담한 모습을 보아 온 이들이었다. 그들은 사나운 포효 한가운데를, 그 모든 소동 속의 정적 한 토막을 가르며 그를 옮겼다. 그러고는 이미 죽음이 와 있는 그 오두막으로 그를 데려갔다.\n\n하지만 들것을 문지방에 내려놓았을 때, 사람들은 서로를, 그리고 나를 바라보며 수군거렸다. 나는 그 까닭을 알았다. 그를 똑같이 고요한 방 안에 함께 눕히는 것이 옳지 않다고 느낀 것이었다.\n\n우리는 읍내로 들어가, 그 짐을 여관으로 옮겼다. 마음을 조금이나마 가다듬을 수 있게 되자마자, 나는 조램을 불러, 밤사이에 그를 런던으로 옮길 탈것을 마련해 달라고 부탁했다. 그를 보살피는 일과, 그의 어머니에게 그를 맞을 마음의 준비를 시키는 그 모진 임무가 오직 나에게만 맡겨질 수밖에 없다는 것을 알았다. 그리고 나는 그 임무를 할 수 있는 한 충실히 해내고 싶었다.\n\n나는 읍내를 떠날 때 사람들의 호기심이 덜할까 싶어 밤을 골라 길을 나섰다. 그러나 내가 내 임무를 실은 것을 뒤따르게 하고 마차에 올라 마당을 빠져나온 것이 거의 자정 무렵이었는데도, 많은 사람들이 기다리고 있었다. 읍내를 따라 군데군데, 심지어 길을 조금 벗어난 곳에서도 더 많은 이들이 보였다. 그러나 마침내 내 둘레에는 황량한 밤과 텅 빈 들판, 그리고 내 젊은 날 우정의 재만이 남았다.\n\n낙엽이 향기를 풍기고, 노랗고 붉고 갈색인 아름다운 빛깔의 잎들이 아직도 나무에 잔뜩 매달려 그 사이로 햇살이 비치던, 무르익은 가을날 정오 무렵, 나는 하이게이트에 다다랐다. 마지막 일 마일은 걸으면서, 내가 해야 할 일을 곰곰이 생각했다. 밤새 나를 뒤따라온 마차는 앞으로 나아가라는 분부를 기다리도록 남겨 두었다.\n\n가까이 다가가서 보니, 그 집은 예전과 꼭 같았다. 덧창은 하나도 올라가 있지 않았고, 쓰지 않는 문으로 이어지는 지붕 덮인 통로가 딸린 음침한 포석 깔린 안마당에는 생기라곤 아무런 기색도 없었다. 바람은 완전히 잦아들었고, 아무것도 움직이지 않았다.\n\n나는 처음에는 대문 종을 울릴 용기가 나지 않았다. 그리고 막상 종을 울렸을 때, 그 종소리 자체에 내 용건이 고스란히 담겨 있는 것만 같았다. 자그마한 응접실 하녀가 손에 열쇠를 들고 나왔는데, 대문을 따면서 나를 골똘히 바라보더니 말했다.\n\n\"실례합니다만, 나리. 어디 편찮으세요?\"\n\n\"마음이 몹시 흔들렸고, 또 지쳤다네.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이라도 있나요, 나리?— 제임스 도련님이—\"\n\"쉿!\" 내가 말했다. \"그래, 일이 좀 생겼어. 스티어포스 부인께 알려 드려야 하는 일이라네. 댁에 계시는가?\"\n\n하녀는 조심스럽게 대답하기를, 마님께서는 요즘 마차를 타고 나가시는 일조차 거의 없고, 방 안에만 계시며, 손님도 만나지 않으시지만 저는 만나 주실 거라고 했다. 마님은 일어나 계시고, 다틀 양이 곁에 있다고 했다. 위층에 어떤 말씀을 전해 올릴까요?\n\n나는 하녀에게 행동거지를 각별히 조심하고, 그저 내 명함만 들여보내고 내가 기다린다고만 전하라고 단단히 일러 둔 다음, 하녀가 돌아올 때까지 객실에(우리는 어느새 그곳에 이르러 있었다) 앉아 있었다. 예전의 그 정겨운, 사람 손길이 깃든 분위기는 사라졌고, 덧문은 반쯤 닫혀 있었다. 하프는 오래오래 쓰지 않은 채였다. 소년 시절의 그의 초상화가 거기 있었다. 그의 어머니가 그의 편지를 간직해 둔 장식장이 거기 있었다. 나는 부인이 지금도 그 편지들을 읽는지, 앞으로 또 읽기나 할지 궁금했다.\n\n집 안이 어찌나 고요한지 위층에서 하녀의 가벼운 발걸음 소리가 다 들렸다. 하녀는 내려와서 전갈을 가져왔는데, 스티어포스 부인은 몸이 성치 않아 내려올 수 없으나, 침실에 있는 것을 양해해 주신다면 기꺼이 저를 만나겠다는 뜻이었다. 잠시 후 나는 부인 앞에 섰다.\n\n부인은 자기 방이 아니라 그의 방에 있었다. 나는 물론 부인이 그를 추억하느라 그 방을 차지하고 지내게 된 것이며, 그녀를 둘러싼 그의 옛 놀이며 재주를 보여 주는 그 숱한 흔적들이 그가 남겨 둔 그대로 거기 남아 있는 것도 같은 까닭에서임을 알 수 있었다. 그러나 부인은 나를 맞으면서도, 자기 침실은 분위기가 병약한 몸에 맞지 않아 거기서 나와 있는 것이라고 중얼거렸고, 그 위엄 어린 표정으로 진실에 대한 일말의 의심마저 물리쳤다.\n\n부인의 의자 곁에는 여느 때처럼 로자 다틀이 있었다. 그 검은 두 눈이 나에게 머무는 첫 순간부터, 나는 그녀가 내가 흉한 소식을 전하러 온 사람임을 알아챘다는 것을 알았다. 바로 그 순간 흉터가 도드라졌다. 그녀는 자기 얼굴이 스티어포스 부인의 눈에 들지 않도록 의자 뒤로 한 걸음 물러서더니, 결코 흔들리지도, 움츠러들지도 않는 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선으로 나를 살폈다.\n\n\"상복을 입으신 걸 보니 안타깝군요, 나리.\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"불행히도 저는 홀아비가 되었습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그토록 큰 상실을 겪기엔 아주 젊으신데요.\" 부인이 받았다. \"참 안된 일입니다. 참 안된 일이에요. 부디 세월이 위로가 되어 주길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"부디 세월이,\" 부인을 바라보며 내가 말했다. \"우리 모두에게 위로가 되어 주길 바랍니다. 친애하는 스티어포스 부인, 우리는 가장 무거운 불행을 겪을 때 모두 그것에 기댈 수밖에 없지요.\"\n\n내 태도의 간곡함과 두 눈에 고인 눈물이 부인을 놀라게 했다. 부인의 온갖 생각이 흐름을 멈추고 방향을 바꾸는 듯했다.\n\n나는 그의 이름을 부드럽게 입에 올리며 목소리를 다잡으려 했으나, 목소리가 떨렸다. 부인은 그 이름을 낮은 소리로 두세 번 혼자 되뇌었다. 그러더니 나를 향해, 애써 차분함을 갖추고 말했다.\n\n\"내 아들이 아픕니다.\"\n\n\"그 애를 보았소?\"\n\n\"보았습니다.\"\n\n\"화해는 했고?\"\n\n나는 그렇다고도 할 수 없었고, 아니라고도 할 수 없었다. 부인은 로자 다틀이 자기 팔꿈치 곁에 서 있던 자리 쪽으로 고개를 살짝 돌렸고, 바로 그 순간 나는 입술만 움직여 로자에게 말했다. \"죽었어요!\"\n\n스티어포스 부인이 뒤를 돌아보다가, 아직 받아들일 준비가 안 된 사실이 또렷이 적힌 것을 읽어 버리는 일이 없도록, 나는 재빨리 부인의 시선을 마주했다. 그러나 나는 로자 다틀이 절망과 공포에 사무쳐 두 손을 허공으로 격렬하게 치켜드는 것을, 이어 그 두 손을 제 얼굴에 갖다 대는 것을 이미 보고 말았다.\n\n그 잘생긴 부인은—어찌나 닮았는지, 아아, 어찌나 닮았는지!—나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 한 손을 이마에 갖다 댔다. 나는 부인에게 부디 진정하고, 내가 전할 이야기를 견딜 마음의 준비를 하라고 간청했다. 그러나 차라리 울라고 애원하는 편이 나았을 것이다. 부인은 돌로 깎은 형상처럼 앉아 있었으니까.\n\n\"제가 지난번 여기 왔을 때,\" 나는 더듬거렸다. \"다틀 양이 그가 여기저기 배를 타고 다닌다고 했지요. 그저께 밤 바다는 끔찍했습니다. 만약 그가 그날 밤 바다에 있었고, 사람들 말처럼 위험한 해안 가까이에 있었다면, 그리고 사람들이 본 그 배가 정말로 그 배라면—\"\n\n\"로자!\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다. \"이리 오너라!\"\n\n로자는 다가왔으나, 거기엔 어떤 연민도 다정함도 없었다. 그를 낳은 어머니와 마주하는 그녀의 두 눈은 불처럼 번득였고, 이내 그녀는 소름 끼치는 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"자,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이제 당신 자존심은 풀렸나요, 미친 노인네? 이제 그가 당신에게 속죄했나요—제 목숨으로! 듣고 있어요?— 그 애의 목숨으로!\"\n\n스티어포스 부인은 의자에 빳빳이 기댄 채 쓰러지듯 주저앉아, 신음 말고는 아무 소리도 내지 못하고, 두 눈을 크게 뜬 채 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래요!\" 로자가 격렬하게 제 가슴을 치며 외쳤다. \"날 봐요! 신음하고, 끙끙대면서 날 보라고요! 여길 봐요!\" 흉터를 치며 외쳤다. \"당신 죽은 아들의 솜씨를!\"\n\n어머니가 이따금 토해 내는 그 신음이 내 가슴을 후벼 팠다. 늘 똑같았다. 늘 말이 되지 못하고 짓눌린 소리였다. 늘 머리를 가누지 못하고 움직이는 몸짓이 따랐으나, 얼굴빛은 전혀 변하지 않았다. 늘 굳어 버린 입과 다문 잇새에서 새어 나왔으니, 마치 턱이 잠겨 버리고 얼굴이 고통 속에 얼어붙은 듯했다.\n\n\"그가 이런 짓을 한 게 언제였는지 기억나요?\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그가 당신 천성을 물려받고, 당신이 그의 자만과 격정을 떠받들어 키운 탓에, 이런 짓을 저질러 나를 평생 흉하게 만든 게 언제였는지 기억나요? 날 봐요, 죽는 날까지 그의 분노로 낙인찍힌 이 몸을. 당신이 그를 그렇게 만들어 놓고선 신음하고 끙끙대요!\"\n\n\"다틀 양.\" 내가 그녀에게 애원했다. \"제발 부탁이니—\"\n\n\"나는 말하고 말 거예요!\" 그녀가 번갯불 같은 눈으로 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"당신은 입 다물어요! 날 보라고요, 거만하고 거짓된 아들을 둔 거만한 어머니! 그를 길러 낸 것을 신음하고, 그를 타락시킨 것을 신음하고, 그를 잃은 것을 신음하고, 내가 그를 잃은 것을 신음해요!\"\n\n그녀는 주먹을 불끈 쥐었고, 격정이 그 야위고 지친 몸을 조금씩 갉아먹어 가기라도 하듯 온몸을 부르르 떨었다.\n\n\"당신이, 그 애의 제멋대로인 고집에 분개한다고요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"당신이, 그 애의 오만한 성미에 상처받았다고요! 머리가 희끗해진 당신이, 그 두 가지를 못마땅해하면서, 정작 그 애를 낳으면서 그 둘을 만들어 준 그 천성을 들이밀고 있는 거예요! 요람에서부터 그를 길러 지금의 그로 만들어 놓고, 마땅히 되어야 했을 모습을 짓밟아 버린 게 바로 당신이라고요! 그래, 이제 그 오랜 세월의 수고에 대한 보답을 받은 건가요?\"\n\n\"오, 다틀 양, 부끄러운 줄 아세요! 오, 너무 잔인하군요!\"\n\n\"내 말하지만,\" 그녀가 받았다. \"나는 이 사람한테 말하고 말 거예요. 내가 여기 서 있는 한, 이 세상 그 어떤 힘도 나를 막지 못해요! 내가 이 숱한 세월을 입 다물고 살았는데, 지금 와서 말도 못 한단 말인가요? 나는 당신이 그를 사랑한 것보다 더 그를 사랑했어요!\" 부인을 사납게 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"나는 그를 사랑하면서도 아무런 보답도 바라지 않을 수 있었어요. 내가 그의 아내였더라면, 일 년에 사랑한다는 한마디를 듣자고 그의 변덕에 노예가 되었을 거예요. 그랬을 거라고요. 그걸 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 어디 있겠어요? 당신은 사람을 들들 볶고, 거만하고, 까다롭고, 이기적이었어요. 내 사랑은 헌신적이었을 거예요—당신의 그 하잘것없는 칭얼거림 따위는 발밑에 짓밟아 버렸을 거라고요!\"\n\n번득이는 두 눈으로, 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 하기라도 하는 양 땅을 쾅쾅 굴렀다.\n\n\"여길 봐요!\" 그녀가 가차 없는 손길로 다시 흉터를 치며 말했다. \"그가 자기가 저지른 짓을 더 잘 헤아릴 만큼 자라자, 그걸 깨닫고 뉘우쳤어요! 나는 그에게 노래를 불러 주고, 이야기를 나누고, 그가 하는 모든 일에 내가 품은 열정을 보여 주고, 그가 가장 흥미로워하는 것이라면 무엇이든 애써 익혀 그만큼의 지식을 갖추었어요. 그렇게 나는 그를 사로잡았어요. 그가 가장 싱그럽고 가장 진실했던 시절에, 그는 나를 사랑했어요. 그래요, 정말이에요! 당신이 무심한 한마디로 내쳐지던 그 숱한 순간에, 그는 나를 제 가슴에 품었어요!\"\n\n그녀는 그 광기—실로 광기에 가까웠다—의 한복판에서, 사람을 비웃는 듯한 자긍심을 담아 그렇게 말했으나, 한편으로는 그것을 애틋이 떠올리는 기색이 있었으니, 그 안에서 한결 부드러운 감정의 사그라들던 불씨가 한순간 되살아났다.\n\n\"나는 추락했어요—진작 알았어야 했지만, 그 풋풋한 구애에 홀려 버린 탓이었지요—인형 하나로, 한가한 시간을 때울 노리개 하나로 추락했어요. 그의 변덕스러운 기분이 일 때마다 내던져졌다가 다시 집어 들려지고, 또 가지고 놀려지는 노리개로요. 그가 싫증을 내자, 나도 싫증이 났어요. 그의 마음이 식어 가자, 나는 내가 쥔 어떤 힘도 더 키워 보려 하지 않았으니, 그가 어쩔 수 없이 나를 아내로 맞아야 할 처지가 됐다 한들 그와 결혼하려 들지 않았을 것과 마찬가지였어요. 우리는 말 한마디 없이 서로에게서 멀어졌어요. 어쩌면 당신도 그걸 보고서 안타까워하지 않았겠지요. 그 후로 나는 당신네 두 사람 사이에 놓인, 그저 흉하게 망가진 가구 한 점이었어요. 눈도 없고, 귀도 없고, 감정도 없고, 추억도 없는. 신음한다고요? 당신이 그를 만들어 놓은 걸 신음해요. 당신의 사랑 때문이 아니라. 다시 말하지만, 한때는 내가 당신이 그를 사랑한 것보다 더 그를 사랑한 시절이 있었어요!\"\n\n그녀는 번뜩이는 성난 두 눈으로 그 크게 뜬 멍한 시선과 굳어 버린 얼굴을 마주하고 섰다. 그리고 신음이 되풀이될 때에도, 마치 그 얼굴이 한 폭의 그림이기라도 한 듯 조금도 누그러지지 않았다.\n\n\"다틀 양.\" 내가 말했다. \"이 비탄에 잠긴 어머니를 가엾이 여기지 못할 만큼 그토록 모질 수 있다면—\"\n\n\"나를 가엾이 여겨 주는 사람은 누구죠?\" 그녀가 날카롭게 받아쳤다. \"이건 저 여자가 뿌린 씨예요. 오늘 거두는 수확을 두고 저 여자더러 실컷 신음하라고 해요!\"\n\n\"그리고 그에게 허물이 있었다 해도—\" 내가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"허물이라고요!\" 그녀가 격정에 찬 눈물을 쏟으며 외쳤다. \"누가 감히 그를 헐뜯어요? 그는 자기가 굽히고 들어가 사귀던 그런 친구들 수백만 명을 합친 것보다 더 값진 영혼을 지녔어요!\"\n\n\"나만큼 그를 사랑한 사람도, 나만큼 그를 애틋이 기억하는 사람도 없을 겁니다.\" 내가 대답했다. \"내가 하려던 말은, 만약 당신이 그의 어머니를 가엾이 여기지 못한다면, 아니면 그의 허물이—당신은 그 허물을 두고 모질게 말해 왔지요—\"\n\n\"거짓말이에요.\" 그녀가 검은 머리카락을 쥐어뜯으며 외쳤다. \"나는 그를 사랑했어요!\"\n\n\"—만약 그의 허물을,\" 내가 말을 이었다. \"이런 순간에 당신의 기억에서 떨쳐 낼 수 없다면, 저 모습을, 한 번도 본 적 없는 사람을 대하듯 바라보고서라도, 부디 얼마간 도움의 손길을 베풀어 주세요!\"\n\n이러는 내내, 그 모습은 변함이 없었고, 변할 수도 없어 보였다. 미동도 없이, 굳은 채로, 멍하니 응시하면서, 이따금 똑같이 말 없는 신음을 흘리며, 똑같이 머리를 가누지 못하고 움직였으나, 그 밖에 살아 있다는 다른 기색은 보이지 않았다. 다틀 양이 갑자기 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 옷매무새를 풀기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신한테 저주가 내리길!\" 그녀가 분노와 비탄이 뒤섞인 표정으로 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"당신이 여기 온 건 불길한 순간이었어요! 당신한테 저주가 내리길! 가요!\"\n\n방을 나선 뒤, 나는 하인들에게 한시바삐 알리려고 종을 울리러 서둘러 되돌아갔다. 그때 그녀는 그 미동도 없는 형상을 두 팔로 끌어안고, 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채로, 그 위로 흐느끼며 입을 맞추고, 부르고, 어린아이를 어르듯 제 가슴에 안고 앞뒤로 흔들면서, 잠든 감각을 일깨우려고 온갖 다정한 수단을 다 써 보고 있었다. 이제는 그녀를 두고 떠나는 것이 두렵지 않았기에, 나는 소리 없이 발길을 돌렸다. 그리고 나가면서 온 집안에 알렸다.\n\n그날 늦게, 나는 다시 돌아갔고, 우리는 그를 그의 어머니 방에 눕혔다. 부인은 여전히 그대로라고 사람들이 일러 주었다. 다틀 양은 한시도 곁을 떠나지 않았고, 의사들이 와서 시중을 들었으며, 온갖 방도를 다 써 보았으나, 부인은 이따금 내는 낮은 소리 말고는 조각상처럼 누워 있었다.\n\n나는 음울한 집 안을 돌아다니며 창마다 빛을 가렸다. 그가 누워 있는 방의 창은 맨 마지막에 가렸다. 나는 납덩이처럼 차가운 그 손을 들어 올려 내 가슴에 갖다 댔다. 그러자 온 세상이 죽음과 침묵뿐인 듯했고, 오직 그의 어머니가 흘리는 신음 소리만이 그 침묵을 깨뜨릴 따름이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 57. THE EMIGRANTS\n\n이런 감정의 충격에 나 자신을 내맡기기 전에, 해야 할 일이 하나 더 있었다. 그것은 떠나는 사람들에게 일어난 일을 숨기는 것, 그래서 그들이 아무것도 모르는 행복 속에서 항해를 떠나게 하는 것이었다. 여기에는 한시도 지체할 틈이 없었다.\n\n나는 바로 그날 밤 마이코버 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가, 패거티 씨와 얼마 전의 참사에 관한 소식 사이를 가로막는 일을 그에게 맡겼다. 그는 그 일을 기꺼이 떠맡았고, 그런 조심성이 없으면 패거티 씨에게 소식이 닿을지도 모를 신문이란 신문은 죄다 중간에서 가로채겠다고 했다.\n\n\"혹시라도 그 소식이 그분께 뚫고 들어간다면 말입니다, 선생.\" 마이코버 씨가 제 가슴을 탁 치며 말했다. \"그건 먼저 이 몸뚱이를 지나가야 할 겁니다!\"\n\n내가 짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 마이코버 씨가 새로운 사회 형편에 자기를 맞추는 과정에서 대담한 해적 같은 풍모를 얻었다는 점이다. 완전히 무법자라고는 할 수 없으되, 방어적이면서도 민첩한 기색이었다. 누가 보아도 그가 황야의 자식으로, 문명의 울타리 밖에서 사는 데 오래 익숙해진 사람이라, 이제 곧 제 고향의 거친 들판으로 돌아가려는 참이라고 여겼을 법했다.\n\n그는 여러 가지 채비 가운데에서도 기름먹인 방수복 한 벌을 빠짐없이 갖추고, 챙이 아주 낮고 겉에 역청이나 뱃밥을 발라 메운 밀짚모자 하나를 마련해 두었다. 이 거친 옷차림에, 흔한 뱃사람의 망원경을 팔에 끼고, 궂은 날씨를 살피기라도 하듯 하늘을 향해 눈을 치켜뜨는 약삭빠른 시늉까지 더하니, 그는 그 나름의 방식으로 패거티 씨보다 훨씬 더 뱃사람다웠다. 그의 온 식구가, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 전투 준비를 마친 상태였다. 마이코버 부인은 더없이 꽉 끼고 한 치의 타협도 없는 보닛을 턱 밑에서 단단히 묶어 쓰고, 숄로는 (고모님이 나를 처음 거두었을 때 나를 동여맸던 것처럼) 몸을 보따리처럼 칭칭 동여매어 등 뒤 허리께에서 단단한 매듭으로 여민 모습이었다. 마이코버 양도 같은 방식으로 폭풍우에 대비해 빈틈없이 채비를 갖추었고, 몸에 거추장스러운 것이라곤 하나도 없었다. 마이코버 도련님은 건지 셔츠와 내가 본 것 중 가장 텁수룩한 선원 작업복에 파묻혀 거의 보이지도 않았다. 어린아이들은 통조림 고기처럼 물 한 방울 새지 않는 껍데기에 꽁꽁 싸여 있었다. 마이코버 씨와 그 맏아들은 둘 다 소맷부리를 손목까지 헐겁게 걷어 올렸는데, 어느 방향으로든 일손을 보탤 채비가 되어 있는 모양새요, 신호만 떨어지면 곧장 \"갑판으로!\" 하고 뛰어오르거나 \"어기—여차—어기!\" 하고 소리를 지를 태세였다.\n\n이렇게 차린 그들을, 트래들스와 나는 해 질 녘에, 그 무렵 헝거포드 계단이라 불리던 나무 층계 위에 모여, 자기네 짐 일부를 실은 배가 떠나가는 모습을 지켜보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 나는 트래들스에게 그 끔찍한 사건을 이미 일러두었고, 그는 큰 충격을 받았다. 그러나 그것을 비밀로 묻어 두는 것이 마땅하다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었고, 그래서 그는 이 마지막 수고를 거들러 와 준 것이었다. 마이코버 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가 그의 약속을 받아 낸 것이 바로 이곳이었다.\n\n마이코버 식구는 그 무렵 계단 바로 곁에 있던, 작고 더럽고 곧 허물어질 듯한 선술집에 묵고 있었는데, 그 집의 비죽 튀어나온 나무 방들은 강물 위로 매달려 있었다. 이민을 떠나는 식구로서 헝거포드 일대에서 적잖은 구경거리가 된 그들은 어찌나 많은 구경꾼을 끌어모았던지, 우리는 그들의 방으로 피신하게 되어 차라리 다행이었다. 위층 나무 방 가운데 하나였는데, 그 아래로 밀물이 흐르고 있었다. 고모님과 애그니스가 거기 있으면서, 아이들을 위해 옷가지 쪽으로 조금이나마 더 살뜰한 채비를 부지런히 마련하고 있었다. 패거티는 그동안 숱한 세월을 살아남은 그 무덤덤한 낡은 반짇고리며 줄자며 밀초 토막을 앞에 놓고, 조용히 거들고 있었다.\n\n그녀의 물음에 답하기란 쉽지 않았다. 마이코버 씨가 패거티 씨를 데리고 들어왔을 때, 내가 편지를 전했고 만사가 잘되었다고 그에게 귓속말하기란 더욱 어려웠다. 그러나 나는 두 가지를 다 해냈고, 그들을 기쁘게 했다. 혹시라도 내 심정의 흔적이 조금이라도 드러났다면, 내 자신의 슬픔만으로도 그것을 충분히 둘러댈 수 있었으리라.\n\n\"그래, 배는 언제 떠나나요, 마이코버 씨?\" 고모님이 물었다.\n\n마이코버 씨는 고모님이든 자기 아내든 조금씩 마음의 준비를 시켜 두어야겠다고 여겼는지, 어제 짐작했던 것보다 더 빨리 떠난다고 말했다.\n\n\"보트가 소식을 전해 줬나 보군요?\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그러니까 배가 떠나는 게—\"\n\n\"부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"내일 아침 일곱 시 전에는 반드시 승선해 있어야 한다는 통지를 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"아이고머니나!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그거 빠르군. 그게 항해상 확실한 사실인가요, 패거티 씨?\" \"그렇습죠, 부인. 배는 저 밀물을 타고 강을 따라 내려갈 겝니다. 데이비 도련님하고 지 누이가 그담 날 오후에 그레이브젠드에서 배에 올라타면, 떠나는 우리 마지막 모습을 보게 될 거예유.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하고말고요,\" 내가 말했다. \"꼭 그러겠습니다!\"\n\n\"그때까지, 그리고 우리가 바다로 나갈 때까지,\" 마이코버 씨가 나에게 알겠다는 눈짓을 보내며 말했다. \"패거티 씨와 나는 우리 살림 세간을 함께 빈틈없이 이중으로 지켜볼 작정입니다. 에마, 여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 그 특유의 당당한 방식으로 헛기침을 하며 말했다. \"내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨가 어찌나 친절하신지, 우리 마음속에서 옛 잉글랜드의 구운 쇠고기와 유독 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 얽혀 있는 그 음료를 적당량 빚는 데 필요한 재료를 주문할 특권을 자기에게 달라고 내 귀에 청해 왔구려. 내가 말하는 것은—한마디로, 펀치라오. 평상시라면 트롯우드 양과 위크필드 양의 너그러움을 구하기를 망설였겠지만—\"\n\n\"제 입장에서만 말씀드리자면,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"더없는 기쁨으로 당신들 모두의 행복과 성공을 빌며 마시겠어요, 마이코버 씨.\"\n\n\"저도요!\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n마이코버 씨는 곧장 술청으로 내려갔는데, 거기서는 마치 제집처럼 편안해 보였다. 그리고 때맞춰 김이 모락모락 나는 주전자를 들고 돌아왔다. 그가 제 접칼로 손수 레몬 껍질을 벗기고 있었다는 것을 나는 알아차리지 않을 수 없었는데, 실전에 나선 개척자의 칼답게 그 길이가 한 자쯤 되었고, 그는 그것을 다소 으스대는 기색이 없지 않게 제 외투 소매에다 쓱쓱 닦았다. 마이코버 부인과 그 집안의 손위 두 사람도 비슷하게 어마어마한 연장을 갖추고 있었으며, 어린아이들은 저마다 나무 숟가락을 튼튼한 끈으로 제 몸에 매달고 있었다. 이렇게 배 위 생활과 오지 생활을 미리 내다보는 마음에서, 마이코버 씨는 방 안 선반 가득 술잔이 있었으니 마음만 먹으면 손쉽게 그랬을 텐데도, 마이코버 부인과 맏아들과 맏딸에게 술잔에 펀치를 따라 주는 대신, 흉측하기 짝이 없는 작은 양철 단지들에 차례로 따라 돌렸다. 그리고 그가 제 전용 한 파인트짜리 단지로 펀치를 마시고, 그날 밤이 다 끝날 무렵 그것을 제 주머니에 챙겨 넣을 때만큼 무언가를 그렇게 즐기는 모습을 나는 본 적이 없다.\n\n\"옛 고국의 사치품들은,\" 마이코버 씨가 그것들을 내던지는 데서 절절한 만족감을 느끼며 말했다. \"우리가 버립니다. 숲속의 주민들이야 자유의 땅이 누리는 세련됨에 끼어들기를 어찌 바라겠습니까.\"\n\n이때 사내아이 하나가 들어와 마이코버 씨를 아래층에서 찾는다고 했다.\n\n\"예감이 들어요,\" 마이코버 부인이 양철 단지를 내려놓으며 말했다. \"우리 집안 식구일 거예요!\"\n\n\"만약 그렇다면, 여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 그 화제에 대해 늘 그러듯 별안간 발끈하며 말했다. \"당신 집안 식구라는 자가—그게 남자든 여자든 무엇이든 간에—꽤 오랫동안 우릴 기다리게 했으니, 이제 그 식구분께서는 내 편할 때까지 좀 기다리셔도 되겠구려.\"\n\n\"마이코버,\" 그의 아내가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이런 때에—\"\n\n\"'사소한 허물마다 일일이 따지고 드는 것은 온당치 못하도다!'\" 마이코버 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"에마, 내가 책망을 받아 마땅하오.\"\n\n\"손해를 본 건, 마이코버,\" 그의 아내가 말했다. \"우리 집안이지, 당신이 아니에요. 우리 집안 식구가 마침내 지난날 제 처신 탓에 스스로 자초한 그 결핍을 깨닫고, 이제 화해의 손길을 내밀고자 한다면, 그것을 매정하게 뿌리치지는 말아 주세요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 그가 대답했다. \"그럼 그러리다!\"\n\n\"그들을 위해서가 아니라면, 나를 위해서라도요, 마이코버.\" 그의 아내가 말했다.\n\n\"에마,\" 그가 대답했다. \"이런 순간에는, 그렇게 보면 거역할 도리가 없구려. 지금조차도 당신 집안의 목을 부둥켜안겠노라고 똑똑히 맹세하지는 못하겠으나, 지금 와 있는 당신 집안 식구만큼은 그 따뜻한 정이 나 때문에 얼어붙는 일은 없도록 하리다.\"\n\n마이코버 씨는 자리를 떴고, 잠시 동안 돌아오지 않았다. 그러는 사이 마이코버 부인은 그와 그 식구 사이에 말다툼이 벌어지지나 않았을까 하는 걱정에서 영 자유롭지 못했다. 마침내 아까 그 사내아이가 다시 나타나, 연필로 쓰고 법률 문서처럼 '힙 대 마이코버'라는 표제를 단 쪽지 하나를 나에게 건넸다. 이 문서로 미루어 나는, 마이코버 씨가 또 체포되어 '절망의 마지막 발작에 빠졌으며, 부디 칼과 한 파인트짜리 단지를 이 심부름꾼 편에 보내 달라, 감옥에서 자기 생애의 짧은 나머지 동안 요긴하게 쓰일지도 모른다'고 청했음을 알게 되었다. 그는 또한 마지막 우정의 표시로, 자기 식구를 교구 구빈원으로 데려다주고, 그런 인간이 일찍이 살았었다는 사실을 잊어 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n물론 나는 그 사내아이와 함께 내려가 돈을 치름으로써 이 쪽지에 답했고, 거기서 마이코버 씨가 한쪽 구석에 앉아, 자기를 붙잡아 들인 보안관 집행관을 음울하게 노려보고 있는 것을 발견했다. 풀려나자 그는 더없이 격렬하게 나를 껴안았고, 그 거래를 제 수첩에 적어 넣었는데—내 기억으로는, 내가 총액을 셈하면서 깜빡 빠뜨린 반 페니 하나를 두고 무척이나 꼼꼼하게 굴었다.\n\n이 중대한 수첩은 그에게 또 다른 거래 하나를 때맞춰 일깨워 주었다. 위층 방으로 돌아오자 (거기서 그는 자기가 자리를 비운 것을 두고 자기로서는 어쩔 수 없는 사정 탓이었다고 둘러댔다), 그는 수첩에서 작게 접힌 큼지막한 종이 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 정성껏 풀어낸 긴 계산식들이 빼곡히 적혀 있었다. 흘끗 본 것만으로도 나는, 학교 산수 공책 바깥에서 그런 셈을 본 적이 없다고 말할 수 있을 정도였다. 알고 보니 그것들은 그가 '원금 사십일 파운드 십 실링 십일 펜스 반'이라 부르는 금액에 대해 여러 기간에 걸쳐 복리를 계산한 것이었다. 이것들을 꼼꼼히 따져 보고, 자기 재산을 정교하게 어림한 끝에, 그는 그날부터 헤아려 이태 하고도 열다섯 달 하고도 열나흘에 이르는 복리를 더한 금액을 나타내는 그 합계를 고르기로 결론을 내렸다. 이것을 위해 그는 더없이 깔끔하게 약속어음을 작성했고, 그 자리에서 그것을 트래들스에게 넘겨주면서 (사람 대 사람으로서) 자기 빚을 전액 청산했노라고, 거듭 고마움을 표하며 말했다.\n\n\"아직도 예감이 들어요,\" 마이코버 부인이 시름에 잠겨 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"우리가 마지막으로 떠나기 전에 우리 집안 식구가 배 위에 나타날 거라고요.\"\n\n마이코버 씨도 그 일에 대해 분명 제 나름의 예감이 있었으나, 그것을 양철 단지에 담아 꿀꺽 삼켜 버렸다.\n\n\"항해 도중에 본국으로 편지를 보낼 기회가 조금이라도 생기거든, 마이코버 부인,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"꼭 소식을 전해 주셔야 해요, 아시겠지요.\"\n\n\"고마우신 트롯우드 양,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"누군가 우리 소식을 기다린다고 생각하는 것만으로도 더없이 기쁠 따름이에요. 빠짐없이 편지를 올리겠어요. 코퍼필드 씨도, 오래되고 허물없는 벗으로서, 쌍둥이가 아직 철도 들기 전부터 자기를 알던 사람한테서 이따금 소식 받는 것을 마다하지 않으시리라 믿어요.\"\n\n나는 그녀에게 편지 쓸 기회가 생길 때마다 소식 듣기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n\"하느님의 뜻이라면, 그런 기회는 숱하게 있을 겁니다.\" 마이코버 씨가 말했다. \"요즘 바다는 그야말로 배들의 함대지요. 건너가는 동안 숱한 배와 마주치지 않을 도리가 없습니다. 그저 한번 건너는 것일 뿐이지요.\" 마이코버 씨가 외알 안경을 만지작거리며 말했다. \"그저 한번 건너는 것일 뿐. 그 거리란 순전히 상상 속에나 있는 것입니다.\"\n\n지금 와서 생각하면 참 묘한 일이지만, 또 그것이 얼마나 마이코버 씨다운지 모르겠다. 그는 런던에서 캔터베리로 갈 때는 세상 끝 가장 먼 데까지 떠나는 사람처럼 말하더니, 막상 잉글랜드에서 오스트레일리아로 가게 되자 마치 해협 건너로 잠깐 나들이라도 가는 듯이 말했으니 말이다.\n\n\"항해하는 동안 나는 애써서,\" 마이코버 씨가 말했다. \"이따금 그들에게 옛이야기를 풀어 줄 것이고, 내 아들 윌킨스의 노래도 부디 취사장 화롯가에서 환영받기를 바랍니다. 마이코버 부인도 뱃멀미를 떨치고 발이 갑판에 익으면—이 표현에 관습상 무례함이 없기를 바랍니다만—틀림없이 그들에게 '꼬마 태플린'을 불러 줄 겁니다. 쇠돌고래며 돌고래가 우리 뱃머리를 가로질러 자주 보이리라 믿고, 우현이든 좌현이든 가릴 것 없이 흥미로운 것들이 끊임없이 눈에 띄겠지요. 한마디로,\" 마이코버 씨가 그 옛 점잖은 풍모로 말했다. \"갑판 위아래로 죄다 어찌나 흥미진진할지, 큰돛대 망루에 올라간 망꾼이 '육지다!' 하고 외칠 때쯤이면, 우리는 어지간히 깜짝 놀라 있을 겁니다!\"\n\n그러면서 그는 마치 이미 그 항해를 다 마치고 최고위 해군 당국 앞에서 일등 시험에 합격이라도 한 양, 제 작은 양철 단지의 내용물을 호기롭게 들이켰다.\n\n\"제가 무엇보다 바라는 것은요, 고마우신 코퍼필드 씨,\" 마이코버 부인이 말했다. \"우리 집안의 어느 가지에서든 옛 고국에서 다시 살아가게 되는 거예요. 인상 쓰지 말아요, 마이코버! 지금은 내 친정 식구 얘기가 아니라, 우리 손주들의 손주 얘기를 하는 거예요. 어린 묘목이 아무리 기운차게 자란다 해도,\" 마이코버 부인이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"나는 그 모태가 된 나무를 잊을 수가 없어요. 우리 후손이 높은 지위와 부를 이루게 되면, 솔직히 나는 그 재산이 브리타니아의 금고로 흘러들기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 말했다. \"브리타니아는 제 운에 맡겨야지요. 내 분명히 말하건대, 그 나라가 나를 위해 해 준 게 별로 없으니, 나로서는 그 일에 딱히 바라는 바도 없소.\"\n\n\"마이코버,\" 마이코버 부인이 받아쳤다. \"그 점에서는, 당신이 틀렸어요. 당신은, 마이코버, 이 머나먼 땅으로 나가는 거예요. 당신 자신과 앨비언 사이의 인연을 약화시키러 가는 게 아니라, 강화하러 가는 거라고요.\"\n\n\"문제의 그 인연이란, 여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 받아 말했다. \"거듭 말하지만, 또 다른 인연을 맺는 일에 내가 조금이라도 예민해질 만큼, 나에게 그런 개인적 신세의 짐을 지운 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"마이코버,\" 마이코버 부인이 받아쳤다. \"그 점에서, 다시 말하지만, 당신이 틀렸어요. 당신은 당신의 힘을 모르고 있어요, 마이코버. 바로 그 힘이야말로, 지금 당신이 내디디려는 이 한 걸음에서조차, 당신 자신과 앨비언 사이의 인연을 더욱 굳건히 다져 줄 거예요.\"\n\n마이코버 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 눈썹을 치켜올린 채, 마이코버 부인이 펼쳐 놓는 견해를 반은 받아들이고 반은 물리치면서도, 그 선견지명만큼은 절절히 느끼고 있었다.\n\n\"고마우신 코퍼필드 씨,\" 마이코버 부인이 말했다. \"나는 마이코버 씨가 자기 위치를 자각하기를 바라요. 마이코버 씨가 배에 오르는 그 순간부터 자기 위치를 자각하는 것이, 내가 보기엔 더없이 중요해요. 나에 대한 당신의 오랜 앎이, 고마우신 코퍼필드 씨, 내가 마이코버 씨처럼 낙천적인 기질이 아니라는 걸 일러 주었을 거예요. 내 기질은, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 지극히 현실적이지요. 나는 이것이 긴 항해라는 걸 알아요. 숱한 결핍과 불편이 따르리라는 것도 알아요. 그런 사실들에 눈감을 수는 없지요. 하지만 나는 또한 마이코버 씨가 어떤 사람인지도 알아요. 마이코버 씨 안에 잠재된 힘을 알지요. 그러니 나는 마이코버 씨가 자기 위치를 자각하는 것이 무엇보다 중요하다고 여기는 거예요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"어쩌면 한마디 해도 될까 하는데, 지금 이 순간 내가 정말로 내 위치를 자각하고 있을 가능성도 영 없지는 않다오.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 아니에요, 마이코버.\" 그녀가 받아 말했다. \"온전히는 아니지요. 고마우신 코퍼필드 씨, 마이코버 씨의 경우는 흔한 경우가 아니에요. 마이코버 씨가 먼 나라로 가는 건, 다름 아니라 난생처음으로 온전히 이해받고 인정받기 위해서지요. 나는 마이코버 씨가 저 배의 뱃머리에 떡 버티고 서서, 단호하게 이렇게 말하길 바라요. '내가 정복하러 온 나라가 바로 이곳이다! 너에게 영예가 있느냐? 너에게 부가 있느냐? 너에게 짭짤한 금전적 보수가 나오는 자리가 있느냐? 죄다 가져오너라. 그건 다 내 것이다!'\"\n\n마이코버 씨는 우리 모두를 흘끗 둘러보며, 이 생각에 제법 일리가 있다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n\"내 말뜻이 통한다면, 나는 마이코버 씨가,\" 마이코버 부인이 논리를 따지는 어조로 말했다. \"제 운명의 카이사르가 되길 바라요. 그것이, 고마우신 코퍼필드 씨, 내가 보기엔 그분의 참된 위치예요. 이 항해의 첫 순간부터, 나는 마이코버 씨가 저 배의 뱃머리에 서서 이렇게 말하길 바라요. '지체는 그만하면 됐다. 실망도 그만하면 됐다. 쪼들리는 형편도 그만하면 됐다. 그건 옛 고국에서의 일이었다. 여기는 새 나라다. 너의 보상을 내놓아라. 어서 가져오너라!'\"\n\n마이코버 씨는 마치 그때 이미 뱃머리 선수상 위에 떡 버티고 서 있기라도 한 듯, 단호하게 두 팔을 팔짱 꼈다.\n\n\"그리고 그렇게 하면,\" 마이코버 부인이 말했다. \"자기 위치를 자각하면 말이에요—마이코버 씨가 영국과의 인연을 약화시키는 게 아니라 강화하리라고 말한다면, 내가 틀린 건가요? 저 반구에서 중요한 공인이 한 사람 솟아오르는데, 그 영향력이 본국에서 느껴지지 않으리라고 나에게 말할 수 있을까요? 오스트레일리아에서 재능과 권력의 지팡이를 휘두르는 마이코버 씨가 잉글랜드에서는 아무것도 아니리라고 내가 어리석게 상상이나 할 수 있을까요? 나야 한낱 여자에 불과하지만, 그런 터무니없는 나약함에 빠진다면, 나는 나 자신과 우리 아버지께 부끄러운 사람이 되고 말 거예요.\"\n\n자기 논리가 반박할 수 없는 것이라는 마이코버 부인의 확신은, 그녀의 어조에 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 듯한 도덕적 드높음을 실어 주었다.\n\n\"그러니 더더욱,\" 마이코버 부인이 말했다. \"나는 언젠가 우리가 다시 그 모태의 땅에서 살게 되기를 바라는 거예요. 마이코버 씨는 어쩌면—나 자신조차 숨길 수 없는 게, 십중팔구 마이코버 씨는—역사의 한 페이지가 될 거예요. 그렇다면 그분은 마땅히, 자기를 낳아 놓고도 일자리는 주지 않았던 그 나라에서 기려져야 마땅하지요!\"\n\n\"여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 말했다. \"당신의 그 애정에 내가 감동하지 않을 도리가 없구려. 나는 언제나 당신의 분별에 기꺼이 따른다오. 될 일은—될 것이오. 우리 후손이 쌓아 올릴 부의 한 자락이라도 내 고국에 돌아가는 것을, 내가 어찌 인색하게 아까워하겠소!\"\n\n\"그거 좋군요,\" 고모님이 패거티 씨를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그럼 나는 당신들 모두를 위해 내 마음을 담아 마시겠어요. 온갖 복과 성공이 당신들과 함께하기를!\"\n\n패거티 씨는 양쪽 무릎에 하나씩 어르고 있던 두 아이를 내려놓고는, 마이코버 부부와 함께 답례로 우리 모두를 위해 잔을 들었다. 그가 마이코버네 사람들과 동지처럼 다정하게 손을 맞잡고, 그 구릿빛 얼굴이 미소로 환하게 빛날 때, 나는 그가 어디로 가든 제 길을 헤쳐 나가고, 좋은 평판을 얻고, 사랑받으리라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n어린아이들조차 저마다 나무 숟가락을 마이코버 씨의 단지에 담갔다가 그 내용물로 우리를 위해 건배하도록 일러 받았다. 이것이 끝나자, 고모님과 애그니스가 일어서서 이민자들과 작별을 나누었다. 슬픈 작별이었다. 모두가 울고 있었다. 아이들은 끝까지 애그니스에게 매달렸다. 그리고 우리는 가엾은 마이코버 부인을 더없이 비통한 상태로 남겨 둔 채 떠났는데, 그녀는 가물거리는 촛불 곁에서 흐느끼며 울고 있었으니, 강에서 바라보면 그 방이 마치 처량한 등대처럼 보였으리라.\n\n이튿날 아침 나는 그들이 잘 떠나는지 보러 다시 내려갔다. 그들은 새벽 다섯 시도 되기 전에 보트를 타고 떠난 뒤였다. 그런 이별이 만들어 내는 빈자리가 어떤 것인지, 그것은 나에게 놀라운 본보기였다. 내가 그들을 그 허물어져 가는 선술집이며 나무 층계와 연관 지은 것은 겨우 어젯밤부터였는데도, 그들이 떠나고 나니 둘 다 황량하고 버림받은 것처럼 보였으니 말이다.\n\n그 다음 날 오후, 옛 유모와 나는 그레이브젠드로 내려갔다. 우리는 그 배가 강 위에, 무수한 보트에 둘러싸여 떠 있는 것을 발견했다. 순풍이 불고 있었고, 돛대 꼭대기에는 출항 신호가 걸려 있었다. 나는 곧장 보트 한 척을 빌려 배를 향해 저어 갔고, 그 배를 중심으로 소용돌이치는 자그마한 혼잡을 헤치고 나아가 갑판에 올랐다.\n\n패거티 씨가 갑판에서 우리를 기다리고 있었다. 그는 마이코버 씨가 조금 전 힙의 소송으로 또 (그리고 마지막으로) 체포되었으나, 내가 그에게 부탁해 둔 대로 자기가 그 돈을 치렀다고 일러 주었고, 나는 그 돈을 다시 갚아 주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 우리를 갑판 아래로 데려갔다. 거기서, 그가 혹시 무슨 일이 벌어졌다는 소문을 들은 것은 아닐까 하고 내가 아직 품고 있던 미련 같은 두려움은, 마이코버 씨가 어둠 속에서 나와 다정하고도 감싸 주는 태도로 그의 팔을 붙잡고는, 그저께 밤 이래로 둘이 한순간도 떨어진 적이 거의 없다고 나에게 일러 주는 바람에 말끔히 가셨다.\n\n나에게는 너무도 낯선 광경이요, 어찌나 비좁고 어두운지, 처음에는 거의 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다. 그러나 차츰차츰, 내 눈이 그 어스름에 익숙해지면서 시야가 또렷해졌고, 나는 마치 오스타데의 그림 속에 서 있는 듯했다. 배의 큰 들보며 선체며 고리못 사이로, 이민자들의 침상이며 궤짝이며 보따리며 통이며 잡다한 짐 더미가 — 여기저기 매달린 등불이, 또 다른 곳에서는 통풍통이나 승강구로 새어 드는 누런 햇살이 비추는 가운데 — 빽빽이 들어찬 사람들 무리가, 새로 사귀고, 서로 작별하고, 이야기하고, 웃고, 울고, 먹고 마시고 있었다. 어떤 이들은 벌써 제 몫인 몇 자(尺)의 공간을 차지하고 자리를 잡아, 단출한 살림을 정돈해 놓고 어린아이들을 등받이 없는 의자나 자그마한 안락의자에 앉혀 두었으며, 또 어떤 이들은 쉴 자리를 찾지 못해 낙담한 채 정처 없이 헤매고 있었다. 살아온 날이 겨우 한두 주밖에 안 되는 갓난아기에서부터, 앞으로 남은 날이 한두 주밖에 안 돼 보이는 등 굽은 노인들까지, 또 잉글랜드의 흙을 제 장화에 그대로 묻혀 나르는 농부에서부터, 그 나라의 검댕과 연기를 제 살갗에 견본으로 묻혀 가는 대장장이까지, 온갖 나이와 직업이 그 비좁은 중갑판의 한 뼘 공간에 빼곡히 들어차 있는 듯했다.\n\n이곳을 죽 둘러보던 내 눈에, 열린 현창 곁에 마이코버네 아이 하나를 가까이 두고 앉아 있는, 에밀리를 닮은 형체 하나가 보이는 듯했다. 처음 내 눈길을 끈 것은, 또 다른 형체가 그 사람에게 입맞춤하며 작별하는 모습이었다. 그 형체가 그 어수선함 사이를 차분히 미끄러져 멀어져 가는 모습이 나에게 — 애그니스를 떠올리게 했다! 그러나 그 빠른 움직임과 혼란 속에서, 또 나 자신의 어수선한 마음속에서, 나는 그것을 다시 놓쳐 버렸다. 그러고는 그저, 방문객은 모두 배에서 내리라는 경고가 떨어진 그때가 되었다는 것, 내 유모가 내 곁 궤짝에 앉아 울고 있다는 것, 그리고 거미지 부인이 검은 옷을 입고 허리를 굽힌 어떤 젊은 여인의 도움을 받아, 패거티 씨의 짐을 부지런히 정돈하고 있다는 것만 알 따름이었다.\n\n\"마지막으로 헐 말씀이 있으세유, 데이비 도련님?\" 그가 말했다. \"갈라서기 전에 잊어뿐 게 하나라도 있남유?\"\n\n\"하나 있어요!\" 내가 말했다. \"마사!\"\n\n그는 내가 말한 그 젊은 여인의 어깨를 건드렸고, 마사가 내 앞에 섰다.\n\n\"하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를, 당신은 참 좋은 분이에요!\" 내가 외쳤다. \"마사를 데려가시는군요!\"\n\n마사가 왈칵 눈물을 터뜨리며 그를 대신해 대답했다. 그때 나는 더 말을 잇지 못한 채 그의 손을 꽉 부여잡았다. 내가 일찍이 어떤 사람을 사랑하고 우러른 적이 있다면, 나는 영혼 깊은 곳에서 그 사람을 사랑하고 우러렀다.\n\n배에서는 낯선 이들이 빠르게 빠져나가고 있었다. 나에게 닥칠 가장 혹독한 시련이 아직 남아 있었다. 나는 그에게, 이제 세상을 떠난 그 고결한 영혼이 작별 인사로 전해 달라며 나에게 맡긴 말을 일러 주었다. 그것은 그를 깊이 뒤흔들었다. 그러나 그가 답례로, 이제는 듣지 못하는 그 귀들을 향한 숱한 애정과 회한의 말을 나에게 맡겼을 때, 그는 나를 더욱 뒤흔들어 놓았다.\n\n때가 되었다. 나는 그를 껴안고, 울고 있는 유모를 내 팔에 부축한 채 서둘러 그곳을 떠났다. 갑판에서 나는 가엾은 마이코버 부인과 작별했다. 그녀는 그 순간에도 정신없이 제 식구를 두리번두리번 찾고 있었고, 나에게 남긴 마지막 말은 자기는 결코 마이코버 씨를 버리지 않으리라는 것이었다.\n\n우리는 뱃전을 넘어 우리 보트로 내려가, 조금 떨어진 곳에 머무르며 그 배가 항로를 따라 실려 가는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그때 바다는 잔잔했고, 노을은 눈부셨다. 배는 우리와 그 붉은 빛 사이에 떠 있었고, 가느다란 밧줄과 활대 하나하나까지 그 타오르는 노을을 배경으로 또렷이 보였다. 그 영광스러운 배가, 발그레 물든 물 위에 고요히 떠서, 배 안의 모든 생명이 뱃전에 빽빽이 몰려나와 한순간 모자를 벗고 말없이 모여 선 그 광경만큼, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 애달프고, 그토록 희망에 찬 광경을, 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없다.\n\n말없는 것도 한순간뿐이었다. 돛이 바람을 받아 솟구치고 배가 움직이기 시작하자, 모든 보트에서 세 번의 우렁찬 환호가 터져 나왔고, 배 위의 사람들이 그 환호를 받아 메아리쳐 되돌렸으며, 그것이 다시 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다. 그 소리를 듣고, 모자와 손수건이 물결치듯 흔들리는 것을 바라보았을 때, 내 가슴은 터질 듯했다—그리고 그때 나는 그녀를 보았다!\n\n그때 나는 그녀를, 그 삼촌의 곁에서, 그의 어깨에 기대어 떨고 있는 그녀를 보았다. 그가 간절한 손짓으로 우리를 가리켰고, 그녀는 우리를 보고는 나에게 마지막 작별의 손짓을 보냈다. 그래, 에밀리야, 아름답고도 시들어 가는 너야, 멍든 네 가슴이 품을 수 있는 더없는 믿음으로 그에게 매달리렴. 그 또한 제 크나큰 사랑의 온 힘을 다해 너에게 매달려 왔으니!\n\n장밋빛 노을에 둘러싸여, 갑판 높은 곳에 둘이 따로 떨어져 서서, 그녀는 그에게 매달리고 그는 그녀를 감싸 안은 채, 두 사람은 엄숙히 멀어져 갔다. 우리가 노 저어 뭍에 닿았을 때, 켄트의 언덕들 위로 밤이 내려앉아 있었다—그리고 내 위로도 어둑하게 내려앉았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 58. ABSENCE\n\n내 위로 길고 음울한 밤이 내려앉았다. 수많은 희망과 수많은 그리운 추억, 수많은 잘못과 수많은 부질없는 슬픔과 후회의 유령들이 그 밤을 떠돌았다.\n\n나는 영국을 떠났다. 그때까지도 내가 견뎌야 할 충격이 얼마나 큰지 알지 못한 채. 나는 내게 소중한 모든 이를 두고 떠났다. 그러면서 나는 그 충격을 이미 견뎌 냈고, 그것은 지나간 일이라고 믿었다. 전장에서 치명상을 입고도 자기가 맞았는지조차 거의 알아채지 못하는 병사처럼, 나는 길들지 않은 내 마음과 홀로 남았을 때, 그 마음이 맞서 싸워야 할 상처를 전혀 가늠하지 못했다.\n\n그 깨달음은 빠르게가 아니라 조금씩, 한 톨 한 톨 쌓이며 내게 다가왔다. 외국으로 떠나며 품었던 황량한 느낌은 시간이 갈수록 깊어지고 넓어졌다. 처음에는 그저 묵직한 상실감과 슬픔뿐이어서, 그 안에서 다른 것은 거의 구별할 수가 없었다. 그러다 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 더디게, 그것은 내가 잃어버린 모든 것 — 사랑, 우정, 관심 — 에 대한, 그리고 산산이 부서진 모든 것 — 나의 첫 신뢰, 나의 첫 애정, 내 인생의 그 허공에 떠 있던 성채 전부 — 에 대한, 그리고 남은 모든 것 — 폐허가 된 텅 빈 황무지, 내 둘레로 끊김 없이 어두운 지평선까지 너르게 펼쳐진 그것 — 에 대한 가망 없는 자각으로 변해 갔다.\n\n내 슬픔이 이기적인 것이었다 해도, 나는 그런 줄을 몰랐다. 나는 그 활짝 핀 세상에서 너무도 어린 나이에 떠나간 내 어린 아내를 애도했다. 오래전 내 사랑과 흠모를 얻었듯, 수천 명의 사랑과 흠모를 얻을 수도 있었을 그를 애도했다. 사나운 바다에서 안식을 찾은 그 부서진 마음을 애도했다. 그리고 내가 어렸을 적 밤바람이 부는 소리를 들었던 그 소박한 집의, 이제는 흩어져 떠도는 잔해들을 애도했다.\n\n내가 빠져든 그 차곡차곡 쌓인 슬픔에서, 마침내 나는 다시는 헤어날 가망이 없었다. 나는 이곳저곳을 떠돌았고, 어디를 가든 내 짐을 짊어지고 다녔다. 이제 나는 그 무게를 고스란히 느꼈다. 그 아래 짓눌려 고개를 떨군 채, 이 짐은 결코 가벼워질 수 없으리라고 마음속으로 되뇌었다.\n\n이 의기소침이 가장 심했을 때, 나는 내가 죽을 것이라고 믿었다. 어떤 때는 고향에서 죽고 싶다는 생각이 들어, 빨리 그곳에 닿으려고 실제로 가던 길을 되돌리기도 했다. 또 어떤 때는 더 멀리 나아가, 무엇을 찾는지도 모른 채 도시에서 도시로 옮겨 다니며, 무엇을 뒤에 두고 떠나려는지도 모른 채 떠나려 애썼다.\n\n내가 지나온 마음의 그 모든 고단한 고통의 국면을 하나하나 되짚는 것은 내 힘으로 할 수 없는 일이다. 어떤 꿈은 불완전하고 어렴풋하게밖에 그려 낼 수 없는 법인데, 내 인생의 이 시기를 억지로라도 돌아보려 하면, 나는 꼭 그런 꿈을 떠올리는 것만 같다. 나는 이국의 낯선 도시들, 궁전과 대성당과 신전, 그림과 성채와 무덤, 환상적인 거리들 — 역사와 환상이 오래 깃들어 온 그 자리들 — 사이를 지나가는 내 모습을 마치 꿈꾸는 사람처럼 본다. 그 모든 것 사이로 내 고통스러운 짐을 짊어지고, 눈앞에서 스러져 가는 사물들을 거의 의식하지도 못한 채 지나가는 모습을. 깊은 슬픔에 잠기는 것 말고는 모든 것에 무심한 상태, 그것이 길들지 않은 내 마음 위에 내려앉은 밤이었다. 거기서 눈을 들어 — 마침내 그렇게 했으니, 하늘에 감사하리라! — 그 길고 슬프고 비참한 꿈에서 새벽으로 눈을 들게 해 다오.\n\n여러 달 동안 나는 점점 더 어두워지는 그 구름을 마음에 인 채 여행했다. 고향으로 돌아가지 않으려는 어떤 어렴풋한 이유들이 — 그때 내 안에서 좀 더 또렷한 표현을 얻으려고 헛되이 몸부림치던 그 이유들이 — 나를 순례의 길에 묶어 두었다. 어떤 때는 어디에도 멈추지 못하고 안절부절 이곳저곳을 옮겨 다녔고, 어떤 때는 한 자리에 오래 머물기도 했다. 나에게는 어디서도 아무런 목적도, 나를 떠받쳐 줄 영혼도 없었다.\n\n나는 스위스에 있었다. 이탈리아에서 나와, 알프스의 큰 고개 하나를 넘어온 뒤로, 안내인 하나를 데리고 산속 샛길을 떠돌고 있었다. 그 두려운 적막이 내 마음에 무언가 말을 건넸다 한들, 나는 그것을 알아채지 못했다. 나는 무시무시한 높이와 벼랑에서, 으르렁대는 급류에서, 얼음과 눈의 황무지에서 숭고함과 경이를 보았다. 하지만 그때까지 그것들은 내게 다른 것은 아무것도 가르쳐 주지 못했다.\n\n어느 날 저녁, 해 지기 전에 나는 쉬어 갈 어느 골짜기로 내려갔다. 산허리를 따라 굽이굽이 난 길을 타고 그곳으로 내려가는 동안, 저 멀리 아래에서 골짜기가 반짝이는 것을 보았는데, 오랫동안 느껴 보지 못했던 어떤 아름다움과 고요의 감각이, 그 평화가 일깨운 어떤 누그러뜨리는 기운이, 내 가슴속에서 희미하게 움직였던 것 같다. 나는 한 번 걸음을 멈췄던 것을 기억한다. 짓누르기만 하지도, 아주 절망스럽기만 하지도 않은 어떤 슬픔과 함께. 내 안에서 무언가 더 나은 변화가 가능할지도 모른다고 거의 바라기까지 했던 것을 기억한다.\n\n저녁 해가 골짜기를 영원한 구름처럼 에워싼 저 먼 눈 덮인 봉우리를 비추고 있을 때, 나는 그 골짜기로 들어섰다. 작은 마을이 자리한 협곡을 이루는 산자락은 풍성한 초록으로 덮여 있었고, 그 부드러운 초목 위로 높이는 검은 전나무 숲이 자라, 겨울 눈더미를 쐐기처럼 가르며 눈사태를 막아서고 있었다. 그 위로는 험준한 비탈이 줄줄이 이어졌으니, 잿빛 바위와 빛나는 얼음, 그리고 매끄러운 초록빛 점점이 박힌 목초지가, 모두 그 꼭대기를 덮은 눈과 차츰 어우러졌다. 산허리 여기저기 점점이, 그 작은 점 하나하나가 하나의 집인데, 외딴 통나무 오두막들이 박혀 있었다. 우뚝 솟은 높이에 짓눌려 어찌나 작아 보이던지, 장난감이라기에도 너무 작아 보였다. 골짜기에 옹기종기 모인 마을도 마찬가지였으니, 그 마을에는 시냇물을 가로지르는 나무다리가 놓여 있었고, 시냇물은 부서진 바위 위로 굴러떨어지며 나무들 사이로 으르렁대며 흘러갔다. 고요한 공기 속에 멀리서 노랫소리가 들려왔다 — 양치기들의 목소리였다. 그러나 밝은 저녁 구름 한 조각이 산허리 중턱을 떠가고 있어서, 나는 그 노래가 저기서 들려오는 것이며 이 세상의 음악이 아니라고 거의 믿을 뻔했다. 별안간, 이 고요 속에서, 위대한 자연이 내게 말을 건넸다. 그리고 내 지친 머리를 풀밭에 누이고, 도라가 죽은 뒤로 아직 한 번도 울어 보지 못한 만큼 울도록 나를 달래 주었다!\n\n나는 불과 몇 분 전에 나를 기다리고 있던 편지 꾸러미 하나를 받은 참이었고, 저녁이 차려지는 동안 그것을 읽으려고 마을 밖으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나온 것이었다. 다른 꾸러미들은 나를 비껴갔고, 나는 오랫동안 한 통도 받지 못했었다. 나는 잘 있다, 어디어디에 도착했다 하는 한두 줄을 넘기고는, 집을 떠난 뒤로 편지 한 통 쓸 만한 꿋꿋함도 한결같음도 없었다.\n\n그 꾸러미가 내 손에 있었다. 나는 그것을 열어, 애그니스의 글씨를 읽었다.\n\n그녀는 행복하고 쓸모 있는 사람으로, 바라던 대로 잘 지내고 있었다. 자기 자신에 대해 내게 한 말은 그것이 전부였다. 나머지는 모두 나에 관한 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 내게 아무 충고도 하지 않았다. 어떤 의무도 강요하지 않았다. 다만 그녀 특유의 그 뜨거운 태도로, 나를 향한 자기 믿음이 어떤 것인지를 들려주었을 뿐이다. 그녀는 (이렇게 말했다) 나 같은 천성이 어떻게 고난을 좋은 것으로 바꿔 놓을지 안다고 했다. 시련과 격정이 어떻게 그 천성을 드높이고 굳세게 할지 안다고 했다. 내가 겪은 슬픔을 통해, 내 모든 결심이 더 단단하고 더 높은 방향을 얻게 될 것이라고 그녀는 확신했다. 내 명성을 그토록 자랑스러워하고 그것이 더 커지기를 그토록 고대하던 그녀는, 내가 계속 정진하리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 내 안에서는 슬픔이 나약함이 될 수 없으며 도리어 힘이 될 수밖에 없음을 그녀는 알았다. 어린 시절의 인내가 지금의 나를 만드는 데 제 몫을 했듯, 더 큰 재앙들은 나를 다그쳐 지금의 나보다 한층 더 나은 사람으로 만들 것이며, 그리하여 그 재앙들이 나를 가르쳤듯, 나도 남을 가르치게 되리라고 했다. 그녀는 나의 순결한 사랑을 데려가 안식에 들게 하신 하느님께 나를 의탁했다. 그리고 그 누이 같은 정으로 언제나 나를 아꼈고, 내가 어디로 가든 늘 내 곁에 있었으며, 내가 이미 이룬 것을 자랑스러워하되, 내가 앞으로 이루도록 남겨진 것을 한없이 더 자랑스러워했다.\n\n나는 그 편지를 가슴에 품고, 한 시간 전의 나는 어떠했던가 생각했다! 노랫소리가 잦아드는 것을 듣고, 고요한 저녁 구름이 흐려지는 것을, 골짜기의 온갖 빛깔이 스러지는 것을, 산봉우리의 황금빛 눈이 창백한 밤하늘의 아득한 한 부분이 되어 가는 것을 보면서도, 밤이 내 마음에서 물러가고 그 모든 그림자가 걷히는 것을 느꼈을 때, 그때 내가 그녀에게 품은 사랑은 — 그 순간부터 그 어느 때보다 내게 소중해진 그 사랑은 — 이름 붙일 길이 없었다.\n\n나는 그녀의 편지를 여러 번 읽었다. 잠들기 전에 그녀에게 편지를 썼다. 나는 그녀의 도움이 절실히 필요했노라고, 그녀가 없으면 나는 그녀가 여기는 그런 사람이 아니며 한 번도 그런 적이 없었노라고, 하지만 그녀가 내게 그런 사람이 되라고 일깨워 주니, 나도 애써 보겠노라고 적었다.\n\n나는 정말로 애썼다. 석 달이 더 지나면, 내 슬픔이 시작된 지 일 년이 되는 셈이었다. 나는 그 석 달이 다 지나기 전까지는 아무 결심도 세우지 않되, 그저 애쓰기로 마음먹었다. 그동안 내내 나는 그 골짜기와 그 언저리에서 지냈다.\n\n그 석 달이 지나자, 나는 얼마간 더 고향을 떠나 있기로 결심했다. 그 저녁의 기억 속에서 점점 더 소중해지고 있던 스위스에 당분간 자리를 잡기로, 다시 펜을 들기로, 일하기로 결심했다.\n\n나는 애그니스가 일러 준 그곳으로 겸허히 발길을 돌렸다. 나는 자연을 찾았고, 결코 헛되이 찾지 않았다. 그리고 얼마 전까지 움츠러들며 피했던 사람에 대한 관심을 다시 내 가슴에 받아들였다. 오래지 않아 나는 야머스에서만큼이나 많은 벗을 그 골짜기에 두게 되었다. 겨울이 닥치기 전에 그곳을 떠나 제네바로 갔다가 봄에 다시 돌아왔을 때, 비록 영어로 전해진 것은 아니었으나 그들의 따뜻한 인사는 내게 정겨운 고향의 소리로 들렸다.\n\n나는 이른 아침부터 밤늦게까지, 참을성 있게, 부지런히 일했다. 나는 내 경험에서 — 멀지 않은 곳에서 — 자라난 어떤 목적을 담아 이야기 하나를 썼고, 그것을 트래들에게 보냈다. 그러자 그가 나에게 아주 유리한 조건으로 그 책의 출간을 주선해 주었다. 그리고 우연히 마주친 여행객들에게서, 점점 커져 가는 내 명성의 소식이 들려오기 시작했다. 얼마간 쉬며 기분을 바꾼 뒤, 나는 다시 옛날의 그 열정적인 방식으로, 나를 강하게 사로잡은 새로운 구상에 매달려 일했다. 이 작업을 밀고 나아갈수록 나는 그것을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈고, 잘 해내려고 온 힘을 끌어모았다. 이것이 나의 세 번째 소설이었다. 절반도 채 쓰지 못했을 때, 쉬는 틈에 나는 고향으로 돌아가는 일을 생각하게 되었다.\n\n오랫동안 나는 참을성 있게 공부하고 일하면서도, 한편으로 몸을 튼튼히 하는 운동을 몸에 익혀 왔다. 영국을 떠날 무렵 심하게 상했던 건강은 완전히 회복되었다. 나는 많은 것을 보았다. 여러 나라를 다녔고, 내 지식의 곳간도 한결 늘렸기를 바란다.\n\n이제 나는 이 떠나 있던 기간에 관해, 여기서 떠올릴 필요가 있다고 여기는 것을 모두 떠올렸다 — 한 가지만 빼고. 여기까지 나는 내 생각을 어느 하나도 숨길 뜻 없이 적어 왔다. 다른 데서도 말했듯, 이 이야기는 글로 적은 내 기억이니까. 나는 내 마음의 가장 은밀한 물줄기만큼은 따로 떼어 두었다가, 맨 마지막에 풀어내고 싶었다. 이제 그것을 적는다. 나는 내 마음의 신비를 완전히 꿰뚫어 볼 수가 없어서, 내가 언제부터 가장 이르고 가장 빛나던 내 희망을 애그니스에게 걸었을지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작했는지를 알지 못한다. 내 슬픔의 어느 단계에서, 제멋대로였던 소년 시절에 내가 그녀의 사랑이라는 보물을 내던져 버렸다는 그 생각과 처음 맞물렸는지도 말할 수 없다. 어쩌면 나는 그 아득한 생각의 어떤 속삭임을, 일찍이 내가 어렴풋이 느껴 왔던 그 오래된 불행한 상실 속에서, 끝내 이룰 수 없는 무언가에 대한 결핍 속에서 이미 들었던 것인지도 모른다. 하지만 그 생각이 새로운 자책과 새로운 후회가 되어 내 마음으로 밀려든 것은, 내가 이 세상에 그토록 슬프고 외롭게 남겨졌을 때였다.\n\n만약 그 무렵 내가 그녀와 자주 함께 있었더라면, 나는 내 황량함이 가져온 나약함에 못 이겨 이 마음을 들키고 말았을 것이다. 처음 영국을 떠나 있어야겠다는 충동에 사로잡혔을 때, 내가 어렴풋이 두려워한 것이 바로 그것이었다. 나는 그녀의 누이 같은 정을 손톱만큼이라도 잃는 것을 견딜 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 마음을 들켰다면, 나는 우리 사이에 여태껏 없던 어떤 거북함을 심어 놓고 말았을 것이다.\n\n나는 잊을 수가 없었다. 지금 그녀가 나를 바라보는 그 감정이, 다름 아닌 나 스스로의 자유로운 선택과 행로 속에서 자라난 것임을. 만약 그녀가 한때 나를 다른 종류의 사랑으로 사랑한 적이 있었다 해도 — 그리고 나는 때때로 그녀가 그랬을 법한 시절이 있었으리라 생각했다 — 그것을 내던져 버린 것은 나였다는 것을. 우리 둘이 그저 어린아이였을 적에, 그녀를 내 거친 공상과는 한참 동떨어진 사람으로 여기는 데 익숙해졌었다는 것은, 이제 와 아무것도 아니었다. 나는 내 뜨거운 애정을 다른 대상에게 쏟아부었다. 그리고 내가 할 수도 있었을 일을, 나는 하지 않았다. 애그니스가 내게 어떤 사람인가는, 나와 그녀 자신의 고결한 마음이 함께 만들어 낸 것이었다.\n\n내 안에서 차츰차츰 일어나던 그 변화의 처음 무렵, 나 자신을 좀 더 잘 이해하고 좀 더 나은 사람이 되려고 애쓰던 때에, 나는 어떤 막연한 시험의 시기를 거쳐, 잘못으로 얼룩진 과거를 지워 버리고 그녀와 결혼하는 복을 누릴 수 있을지도 모를 어느 날을 흘끗 그려 보기는 했다. 그러나 세월이 흐를수록 이 어렴풋한 전망은 빛이 바래더니, 내게서 떠나갔다. 만약 그녀가 그때 나를 사랑한 적이 있었다면, 나는 그녀를 더욱더 신성하게 여겨야 마땅했다. 내가 그녀에게 털어놓았던 속내들을, 내 방황하는 마음을 그녀가 알고 있었다는 것을, 내 벗이자 누이가 되기 위해 그녀가 치렀을 희생을, 그리고 그녀가 거둔 그 승리를 떠올리면서. 그녀가 나를 한 번도 사랑한 적이 없었다면, 그녀가 이제 와 나를 사랑하리라고 어찌 믿을 수 있겠는가?\n\n나는 그녀의 한결같음과 꿋꿋함에 견주어 늘 내 나약함을 느껴 왔는데, 이제는 그것을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈다. 오래전 내가 그녀에게 더 어울리는 사람이었더라면 내가 그녀에게, 또 그녀가 내게 어떤 사이가 되었을지 모르나, 지금의 나는 그렇지 못했고, 그녀도 그렇지 않았다. 그 시절은 지나가 버렸다. 나는 그것을 흘려보냈고, 마땅한 대가로 그녀를 잃은 것이다.\n\n이런 갈등 속에서 내가 무척이나 괴로웠다는 것, 그것들이 나를 불행과 회한으로 가득 채웠다는 것, 그러면서도 내게는 나를 떠받쳐 주는 어떤 감각이 있었다는 것 — 곧 희망이 밝고 싱그러웠을 때 내가 경솔히 등 돌렸던 그 사랑스러운 여인에게, 그 희망이 시들어 버린 지금 다시 손을 내밀려는 생각만큼은 부끄러움과 함께 나 자신에게서 멀리 떼어 놓는 것이 옳고 명예로운 도리로서 내게 요구된다는 그 감각 말이다. 그리고 이 헤아림이야말로 그녀에 관한 내 모든 생각의 뿌리에 자리하고 있었다는 것 — 이 모두가 한결같이 사실이다. 나는 이제 내가 그녀를 사랑한다는 것을, 그녀에게 온 마음을 바치고 있다는 것을 나 자신에게 감추려 들지 않았다. 다만 이제는 너무 늦었으며, 오래도록 이어져 온 우리 사이는 흔들림 없이 그대로 두어야 한다는 확신을 내 가슴에 단단히 새겼다.\n\n나는 내 도라가, 우리를 시험하지 않도록 운명 지어진 그 세월 속에 무슨 일이 일어났을 수 있었을지를 내게 어렴풋이 그려 보였던 것을, 자주 그리고 깊이 곱씹었다. 결코 일어나지 않는 일도, 그 미치는 영향에서는 이루어진 일 못지않게 우리에게 생생한 현실인 경우가 많다는 것을 나는 헤아려 보았다. 그녀가 말한 바로 그 세월이, 이제 나를 바로잡아 주는 현실이 되어 있었다. 그리고 우리가 가장 어리석던 시절에 갈라섰다 하더라도, 언젠가는, 어쩌면 조금 더 늦게나마, 역시 현실이 되었을 것이다. 나는 나와 애그니스 사이에 있었을 수도 있었던 것을, 나를 더 자제할 줄 알고, 더 굳건하며, 나 자신과 내 결점과 잘못을 더 또렷이 의식하는 사람으로 만드는 수단으로 바꾸어 보려 애썼다. 그리하여 그랬을 수도 있었으리라는 그 헤아림을 거쳐, 나는 그것이 결코 이루어질 수 없다는 확신에 다다랐다.\n\n이런 것들이, 그 온갖 혼란과 모순과 더불어, 내가 떠나던 때로부터 삼 년 뒤 고향으로 돌아오던 때까지, 내 마음속에서 끊임없이 자리를 옮기는 모래수렁이었다. 그 이민선이 떠난 지 삼 년이 흘렀다. 바로 그 해 질 무렵, 바로 그 자리에서, 나는 나를 고향으로 실어다 주는 우편선 갑판에 서서, 일찍이 그 배의 그림자가 비치는 것을 보았던 그 장밋빛 물결을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n삼 년. 다 모아 놓고 보면 길었으되, 지나가는 동안에는 짧기만 했다. 그리고 고향은 내게 무척이나 소중했고, 애그니스 또한 그러했다 — 하지만 그녀는 내 사람이 아니었다 — 그녀는 결코 내 사람이 될 수 없었다. 그럴 수도 있었으련만, 그것은 이미 지나간 일이었다!\n\n# CHAPTER 59. RETURN\n\n내가 런던에 발을 디딘 것은 어느 겨울 같은 가을 저녁이었다. 사방은 어두웠고 비가 내렸는데, 나는 일 분 만에 일 년 동안 본 것보다 더 많은 안개와 진창을 보았다. 세관에서 기념비까지 걸어가고 나서야 마차 한 대를 잡았다. 불어난 도랑을 굽어보는 그 집들의 정면조차 나에게는 오랜 벗처럼 다가왔지만, 그것들이 무척이나 꾀죄죄한 벗이라는 사실만큼은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n나는 자주 느껴 왔다 — 다들 그러리라 싶은데 — 익숙한 곳을 떠난다는 것이 그곳에 변화가 닥치라는 신호이기라도 한 것 같다고. 마차 창밖을 내다보다가, 백 년 동안 페인트공이나 목수나 벽돌공의 손길 한 번 닿지 않고 버텨 온 피시스트리트힐의 어느 낡은 집이 내가 없는 사이 헐려 버린 것을 알아챘을 때, 그리고 예부터 불결하고 불편하기로 유명하던 이웃 거리에 지금 배수로가 놓이고 길이 넓혀지고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 세인트폴 대성당마저도 더 늙어 보이지 않을까 반쯤 기대하게 되었다.\n\n내 친구들의 처지에 일어난 몇 가지 변화에 대해서는 마음의 준비가 되어 있었다. 고모님은 오래전에 도버에 다시 자리를 잡으셨고, 트래들스는 내가 떠난 바로 그다음 학기부터 변호사로서 자그마한 사건들을 맡기 시작했다. 그는 이제 그레이스인에 사무실을 두고 있었고, 마지막 편지에서 머지않아 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨와 맺어질 희망이 없지 않다고 나에게 알려 왔다.\n\n그들은 내가 크리스마스 전에는 집에 돌아오리라 기대하고 있었지만, 이렇게 빨리 돌아올 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다. 그들을 깜짝 놀라게 하는 기쁨을 누리려고 일부러 그들을 속였던 것이다. 그런데도 나는 어찌나 심사가 뒤틀렸던지, 아무런 환영도 받지 못한 채 안개 낀 거리를 홀로 말없이 덜컹덜컹 지나가는 것이 못내 서운하고 쓸쓸하게 느껴졌다.\n\n그래도 명랑한 불빛을 단 낯익은 가게들이 나를 어느 정도 위로해 주었고, 그레이스인 커피하우스 문 앞에서 내려섰을 무렵에는 기운을 되찾고 있었다. 그곳은 처음에 내가 골든크로스에 묵었던 그토록 다른 시절을 떠올리게 했고, 그 뒤로 일어난 변화들을 일깨워 주었다. 하지만 그것은 자연스러운 일이었다.\n\n\"인 안에서 트래들스 씨가 어디 사시는지 아나?\" 나는 커피룸 난롯가에서 몸을 녹이며 종업원에게 물었다.\n\n\"홀번코트입니다, 손님. 이 호예요.\"\n\n\"트래들스 씨는 변호사들 사이에서 명성이 오르고 있는 분이지, 아마?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 손님,\" 종업원이 대답했다. \"아마 그러실 겁니다, 손님. 하지만 저는 잘 모르겠는데요.\"\n\n이 종업원은 중년에 깡마른 사람이었는데, 더 권위 있는 종업원에게 도움을 구하듯 눈길을 보냈다. 그쪽은 살집이 두둑하고 위풍당당한 노인으로, 턱이 겹으로 접히고 검은 반바지에 양말을 신고 있었으며, 커피룸 끝에 있는 교회 관리인 좌석 같은 자리에서 나왔다. 그곳에서 그는 현금 상자며 인명부며 변호사 명부며 그 밖의 책과 서류들과 더불어 지내고 있었다.\n\n\"트래들스 씨 말입니다.\" 깡마른 종업원이 말했다. \"코트 이 호요.\"\n\n위풍당당한 종업원은 손을 저어 그를 물리고는, 점잖게 나에게로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"내가 물어본 것은,\" 나는 말했다. \"코트 이 호에 사는 트래들스 씨가 변호사들 사이에서 명성이 오르고 있지 않으냐는 거였네.\"\n\n\"그분 이름은 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\" 종업원이 굵직하고 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n나는 트래들스를 대신해 사과라도 하고 싶은 심정이었다.\n\n\"젊은 분이지요, 분명?\" 그 위엄 어린 종업원이 나에게 엄한 눈길을 박으며 말했다. \"인에 들어온 지는 얼마나 됐습니까?\"\n\n\"삼 년 안쪽일세.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n종업원은 — 짐작건대 그 교회 관리인 좌석에서 사십 년은 살았을 법한 사람이었다 — 그토록 시시한 화제는 더 이어 갈 마음이 없는 듯했다. 그는 저녁으로 무엇을 들겠느냐고 나에게 물었다.\n\n나는 내가 다시 영국에 돌아왔음을 실감했고, 트래들스의 처지를 생각하니 정말이지 풀이 죽었다. 그에게는 도무지 희망이 없어 보였다. 나는 얌전하게 생선 한 토막과 스테이크 하나를 주문하고는, 난로 앞에 서서 그의 무명세에 잠겨 골똘히 생각했다.\n\n수석 종업원을 눈으로 좇으면서, 나는 그가 차츰차츰 자라 마침내 지금과 같은 꽃이 된 그 정원이라는 곳이 출세하기에 어지간히 힘든 곳이구나 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그곳에는 그토록 인습에 얽매이고, 고집스럽고, 오래 자리 잡고, 엄숙하고, 늙수그레한 기운이 감돌았다. 나는 방 안을 둘러보았다. 모래를 깐 바닥은, 의심할 바 없이, 수석 종업원이 소년이던 시절에도 — 그에게 소년 시절이라는 게 있었다면 말이지만, 그럴 성싶지는 않았다 — 정확히 똑같은 방식으로 모래가 깔려 있었으리라. 또 번들거리는 탁자들에는 잔물결 하나 없는 낡은 마호가니의 깊은 속에 내 모습이 비쳤고, 다듬고 닦은 데 흠 하나 없는 등불들이며, 깔끔한 놋쇠 봉으로 칸막이 좌석을 아늑하게 두른 편안한 초록빛 커튼들이며, 환하게 타오르는 두 개의 큼직한 석탄 난로며, 아래에 값비싼 묵은 포트와인 통이 있다는 사실을 의식하기라도 한 듯 떡 벌어진 술병들이 줄지어 늘어서 있었다. 그러니 영국도, 법조계도, 정말이지 단숨에 들이쳐 점령하기는 무척 어렵겠구나 싶었다. 나는 젖은 옷을 갈아입으려고 내 침실로 올라갔다. 그 낡은 판벽으로 둘러싸인 널따란 방은 — 인으로 통하는 아치 길 위에 있었던 걸로 기억한다 — 그 어마어마한 넓이며, 진중하고 거대한 네 기둥 침대며, 굽힐 줄 모르는 위엄을 풍기는 서랍장들이, 죄다 한통속이 되어 트래들스의 앞날을, 아니 그런 당돌한 젊은이라면 누구든 그 앞날을 엄하게 노려보고 있는 것만 같았다. 나는 다시 내려와 저녁을 들었다. 그런데 느릿느릿 흐르는 식사의 안락함과, 그 자리의 정연한 침묵마저도 — 장기 휴정기가 아직 끝나지 않아 손님이라곤 없었다 — 트래들스의 무모함과 앞으로 이십 년간 입에 풀칠이나마 할 가망이 거의 없음을 웅변하는 듯했다.\n\n나는 떠난 이래 이런 광경을 본 적이 없었으니, 그것은 내 친구에 대한 희망을 완전히 꺾어 놓았다. 수석 종업원은 나에게 진력이 난 모양이었다. 그는 더는 내 곁에 오지 않고, 긴 각반을 찬 어느 노신사에게 전념했다. 그 노신사를 맞으려고 특별한 포트와인 한 파인트가 마치 제 발로 술 창고에서 걸어 나온 듯했는데, 그가 따로 주문을 하지도 않았기 때문이다. 두 번째 종업원이 나에게 속삭이듯 알려 주기를, 이 노신사는 광장에 사는 은퇴한 부동산 양도 전문 변호사로, 엄청난 돈을 가졌으며 그것을 자기 빨래하는 여자의 딸에게 물려줄 거라는 소문이 있다고 했다. 또한 그는 책상 서랍 안에 은식기 한 벌을 두었는데 오래 처박아 두어 죄다 녹이 슬었다는 소문도 있으나, 정작 그의 방에서 숟가락 한 개나 포크 한 개라도 사람 눈으로 본 사람은 한 명도 없다는 것이었다. 이쯤 되니 나는 트래들스를 아주 가망 없는 사람으로 단념했고, 내 마음속으로는 그에게 희망이 없다고 못 박아 버렸다.\n\n그렇지만 그 정다운 옛 벗이 몹시 보고 싶어서, 나는 수석 종업원의 눈에 결코 좋게 비치지 않을 모양새로 저녁을 후딱 해치우고는 뒷문으로 서둘러 빠져나왔다. 코트 이 호는 금세 닿았다. 문기둥에 새겨진 글귀가 트래들스 씨는 맨 위층에 방을 쓰고 있노라고 일러 주기에, 나는 계단을 올라갔다. 그것은 망가져 가는 낡은 계단으로, 층계참마다 곤봉 머리처럼 생긴 작은 기름 심지가 더러운 유리로 만든 작은 감방 속에서 가물가물 죽어 가며 희미하게 비추고 있었다.\n\n비틀비틀 계단을 오르는 동안, 나는 유쾌한 웃음소리가 들리는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그것은 사무 변호사나 법정 변호사의 웃음도, 사무 변호사의 서기나 법정 변호사의 서기의 웃음도 아닌, 두세 명의 명랑한 아가씨들의 웃음이었다. 그런데 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이다가, 마침 그레이스인의 존경하는 협회가 널빤지 한 장을 빠뜨려 놓은 구멍에 발을 디디는 바람에, 제법 요란한 소리를 내며 넘어지고 말았다. 다시 발을 추슬렀을 때는 사방이 고요했다.\n\n남은 길은 더 조심조심 더듬어 가는데, '트래들스 씨'라고 칠해진 바깥문이 열려 있는 것을 발견하자 내 심장은 쿵쿵 뛰었다. 나는 문을 두드렸다. 안에서 한바탕 부산하게 들썩이는 소리가 났을 뿐 다른 기척은 없었다. 그래서 나는 다시 두드렸다.\n\n자그마하고 약삭빨라 보이는 사내아이가 — 반은 사환이요 반은 서기인 듯한 — 나타났는데, 숨을 몹시 헐떡이면서도 그 사실을 법으로 입증해 보라는 듯 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"트래들스 씨 안에 계시느냐?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 손님. 하지만 일하시는 중인데요.\"\n\n\"내가 그분을 좀 만나야겠다.\"\n\n잠깐 나를 살펴보더니, 약삭빨라 보이는 그 아이는 나를 들여보내기로 마음먹은 모양이었다. 그러려고 문을 더 활짝 열고는, 먼저 옷장만 한 작은 현관으로, 다음에는 자그마한 거실로 나를 들였다. 그곳에서 나는 옛 벗과 마주했는데(그 역시 숨을 헐떡이고 있었다), 그는 탁자에 앉아 서류 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었다.\n\n\"세상에!\" 트래들스가 고개를 들며 소리쳤다. \"코퍼필드잖아!\" 그러고는 내 품으로 달려들었고, 나는 그를 꼭 끌어안았다.\n\n\"다들 잘 지내나, 친애하는 트래들스?\"\n\n\"다들 잘 지내네, 친애하고 친애하는 코퍼필드, 좋은 소식뿐이라네!\"\n\n우리는 둘 다 기뻐서 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n\"이 사람아,\" 트래들스가 흥분해서 머리카락을 헝클어뜨리며 말했는데, 그것은 더없이 쓸데없는 짓이었다. \"내 가장 친애하는 코퍼필드, 오래도록 잃었던, 더없이 반가운 친구여, 자네를 보니 얼마나 기쁜지! 어쩜 이리 까맣게 탔나! 정말 기뻐! 내 목숨과 명예를 걸고, 나는 이토록 기뻐한 적이 없네, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 결코 없었어!\"\n\n나도 내 감정을 표현할 길이 없기는 마찬가지였다. 처음에는 도무지 입이 떨어지지 않았다.\n\n\"이 사람아!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"게다가 이렇게 유명해지다니! 내 자랑스러운 코퍼필드! 어이쿠 세상에, 도대체 언제 왔고, 어디서 왔으며, 그동안 무얼 하고 지냈나?\"\n\n자기가 하는 말 어느 것에도 대답을 기다릴 새 없이, 트래들스는 나를 난롯가 안락의자에 털썩 앉혀 놓고는, 줄곧 한 손으로는 부젓가락을 들고 격렬하게 불을 쑤셔 댔고, 다른 손으로는 그것이 무슨 외투라도 되는 양 황당한 착각에 빠져 내 목도리를 잡아당겼다. 그는 부젓가락도 내려놓지 않은 채 다시 나를 끌어안았고, 나도 그를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 둘 다 웃다가, 둘 다 눈물을 훔치다가, 둘 다 자리에 앉아 난로를 사이에 두고 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"생각해 보게,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"자네가 분명 집에 거의 다 와 가고 있었을 텐데, 이 친애하는 옛 친구야, 그 예식에는 오지 못했다니!\"\n\n\"무슨 예식 말인가, 친애하는 트래들스?\"\n\n\"어이쿠 세상에!\" 트래들스가 예의 그 버릇대로 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 소리쳤다. \"내 지난번 편지를 못 받았나?\"\n\n\"무슨 예식 얘기가 적혀 있었다면, 분명 못 받았네.\"\n\n\"아니, 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 두 손으로 머리카락을 곧추세웠다가 그 손을 내 무릎에 얹으며 말했다. \"나 결혼했다네!\"\n\n\"결혼!\" 나는 기뻐서 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이런 세상에, 그렇다네!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"호러스 목사님 주례로 — 소피와 — 저 아래 데번셔에서 말이야. 아니, 이 친구야, 그 사람 지금 창문 커튼 뒤에 있다네! 자 보게!\"\n\n놀랍게도, 바로 그 순간 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨가 웃으며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 숨어 있던 자리에서 나왔다. 그렇게 명랑하고, 상냥하고, 정직하고, 행복하고, 환하게 빛나는 신부를 세상은 본 적이 없으리라고 나는 믿는다(그 자리에서 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다). 나는 오랜 벗이 마땅히 그러하듯 그녀에게 입을 맞추었고, 진심을 다해 두 사람의 행복을 빌었다.\n\n\"이런,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"이 얼마나 즐거운 재회인가! 자넨 정말 끔찍이도 까맣게 탔네그려, 친애하는 코퍼필드! 아이고 맙소사, 난 어찌나 행복한지!\"\n\n\"나도 그렇다네.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"저야말로 정말 행복한걸요!\" 얼굴을 붉히며 웃던 소피가 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 다들 더할 나위 없이 행복해!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그 애들마저도 행복하다네. 이런, 내가 그 애들을 깜빡했구먼!\"\n\n\"깜빡?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 애들 말이야.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"소피의 자매들이지. 우리 집에 와서 묵고 있다네. 런던 구경을 좀 하러 왔어. 사실은, 그러니까 — 코퍼필드, 계단에서 넘어진 게 자넨가?\"\n\n\"맞네.\" 내가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그러면, 자네가 계단에서 넘어졌을 때,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"나는 그 애들이랑 한창 뛰놀고 있었다네. 실은 우리가 '구석 술래잡기'를 하고 있었어. 그런데 그게 웨스트민스터홀에선 어림없는 짓이고, 의뢰인 눈에라도 띄면 도무지 변호사답게 보이지 않을 테니, 그 애들이 부랴부랴 달아났지. 그래서 지금은 — 틀림없이 엿듣고 있을 걸세.\" 트래들스가 다른 방 문 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 미안하군.\" 나는 다시 웃으며 말했다. \"그렇게 흩어지게 만들었으니.\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 트래들스가 무척 기뻐하며 받았다. \"자네가 노크한 뒤에 그 애들이 달아났다가, 머리에서 떨어진 빗을 주우러 다시 뛰어 돌아오고, 그렇게 정신없이 법석을 떠는 꼴을 봤더라면, 그런 말은 못 했을 걸세. 여보, 그 애들 좀 데려와 주겠소?\"\n\n소피가 종종걸음으로 가자, 옆방에서 그녀를 한바탕 웃음소리로 맞이하는 것이 들렸다.\n\n\"정말 음악 같지 않은가, 친애하는 코퍼필드?\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"듣기에 참 좋아. 이 낡은 방들을 환하게 밝혀 준다네. 평생을 홀로 살아온 가엾은 노총각에게는, 알다시피, 그야말로 더없이 감미롭지. 매혹적이야. 가엾은 것들, 그 애들은 소피를 잃어 큰 상실을 겪었거든 — 장담컨대, 코퍼필드, 소피는 지금도, 예나 지금이나 변함없이, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라네! — 그러니 그 애들이 저렇게 기운찬 모습을 보니 이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇하다네. 아가씨들이랑 함께 지내는 건 참으로 즐거운 일이야, 코퍼필드. 변호사답지는 않지만, 무척 즐거워.\"\n\n그가 살짝 말을 더듬는 것을 보고, 또 마음 착한 그가 방금 한 말로 나에게 어떤 아픔을 줄까 봐 두려워하고 있음을 알아챈 나는, 그의 말에 진심으로 맞장구쳐 주었다. 그것이 그를 눈에 띄게 안심시키고 무척 기쁘게 한 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"하지만 그건 그렇고,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"사실대로 말하자면, 우리 살림살이는 통째로 변호사답지가 못하다네, 친애하는 코퍼필드. 소피가 여기 있는 것조차 변호사답지 않지. 그래도 우리에겐 달리 거처가 없어. 우린 조각배를 타고 바다로 나선 셈이지만, 거친 풍파를 견딜 각오는 단단히 되어 있다네. 게다가 소피는 살림 솜씨가 비상한 사람이야! 저 애들을 어떻게 다 욱여넣었는지 알면 자넨 깜짝 놀랄 걸세. 정말이지 어떻게 그게 가능한지 나도 잘 모르겠다니까!\"\n\n\"아가씨들이 여럿 와 있나?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"맏이인, 그 미인이 와 있다네.\" 트래들스가 나직이 속내를 털어놓는 목소리로 말했다. \"캐럴라인이지. 그리고 세라도 와 있어 — 알다시피 척추에 무슨 탈이 있다고 자네한테 말했던 그 애 말이야. 훨씬 좋아졌다네! 그리고 소피가 가르친 막내 둘도 함께 있어. 그리고 루이자도 와 있고.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 내가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그런데 이 방 전체가 — 그러니까 이 사무실 말일세 — 고작 방 세 칸뿐인데, 소피가 그 애들 자리를 기막히게 잘 마련해 줘서, 다들 더없이 편안하게 잔다네. 저 방에 셋.\" 트래들스가 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다. \"저 방에 둘이지.\"\n\n나는 트래들스 부부에게 남은 자리는 어디인가 싶어 둘러보지 않을 수 없었다. 트래들스는 내 마음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"음!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"방금 말했듯이 우린 거친 풍파를 견딜 각오가 돼 있어서, 지난주에는 여기 바닥에 임시 잠자리를 마련했다네. 그래도 지붕 밑에 작은 방이 하나 있어 — 올라가 보면 참 아담한 방이지 — 소피가 나를 놀래 주려고 손수 도배를 했다네. 그게 지금 우리 방이야. 집시 소굴 같은 그럴듯한 작은 방이지. 거기서는 전망도 제법이라네.\"\n\n\"드디어 자네가 행복한 결혼을 이루었구먼, 친애하는 트래들스!\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 얼마나 기쁜지!\"\n\n\"고맙네, 친애하는 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 다시 한번 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"그래, 나는 더없이 행복하다네. 자네 보게, 저기 자네의 옛 친구가 있지.\" 트래들스가 화분과 받침대를 향해 의기양양하게 고갯짓하며 말했다. \"그리고 저기 대리석 상판을 얹은 탁자도 있고! 나머지 가구들은 죄다 수수하고 실용적이라는 걸 알 수 있을 걸세. 그리고 은식기로 말하자면, 어이쿠, 우린 찻숟가락 하나도 없다네.\"\n\n\"앞으로 다 벌어들일 거란 말이지?\" 내가 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇다네.\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"다 벌어들일 거야. 물론 찻숟가락 비슷한 건 있긴 하지. 차를 저어야 하니까. 하지만 그건 백랍 합금이라네.\"\n\n\"은은 나중에 들어올 때 더 빛나는 법이지.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 우리가 하는 말이 그거라네!\" 트래들스가 소리쳤다. \"그게 말이야, 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 그가 다시 나직하게 속내를 털어놓는 어조로 말했다. \"내가 '도 데임. 자입스 대 위그지엘' 사건에서 변론을 펼친 뒤에 — 그게 변호사들 사이에서 나한테 큰 도움이 됐는데 — 데번셔로 내려가서, 호러스 목사님과 단둘이 진지한 이야기를 나눴다네. 나는 소피가 — 장담컨대, 코퍼필드, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라네! —\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그럴 거야!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그렇다네!\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"그런데 내가 본론에서 벗어나고 있는 것 같군. 내가 호러스 목사님 얘기를 했던가?\"\n\n\"이러이러한 사실에 힘주어 말했다고 했네 —\"\n\n\"맞아! 소피와 내가 오랫동안 약혼한 사이였다는 사실, 그리고 소피가 부모님 허락을 얻어 나를 기꺼이 받아들이려 했다는 사실에 힘주어 말했지 — 한마디로,\" 트래들스가 예의 그 솔직한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"지금의 이 백랍 합금 살림 처지에서 말일세. 좋아. 그래서 나는 호러스 목사님께 제안했다네 — 그분은 더없이 훌륭한 성직자이시거든, 코퍼필드, 주교가 되셔야 마땅한 분이지. 적어도 쪼들리지 않고 살아갈 만큼은 가지셔야 할 분이야 — 만약 내가 일 년 안에, 그러니까 이백오십 파운드라는 고비를 넘길 수 있고, 이듬해에는 그만큼이나 그보다 나은 형편으로 갈 길이 제법 환히 보이며, 게다가 이런 작은 거처 하나쯤은 그럭저럭 꾸밀 수 있다면, 그렇다면 그때, 그런 경우에는 소피와 내가 맺어지게 해 달라고 말이야. 나는 외람되게도 우리가 여러 해 동안 참고 기다려 왔다는 점을 말씀드렸고, 또 소피가 집안에서 유난히 쓸모 있는 사람이라는 사정이 그 다정한 부모님으로 하여금 소피가 제 인생을 꾸리는 것을 막는 구실이 되어선 안 된다고 했다네 — 무슨 말인지 알겠나?\"\n\n\"당연히 그래선 안 되지.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자네가 그리 생각해 주니 기쁘네, 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"호러스 목사님을 탓하려는 건 결코 아니지만, 나는 정말이지 그런 경우에 부모며 형제며 하는 사람들이 때때로 좀 이기적인 데가 있다고 생각하거든. 아무튼! 나는 또한 내 가장 간절한 소망은 그 집안에 보탬이 되는 것이라고, 그리고 내가 세상에서 자리를 잡고 만에 하나 그분께 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면 — 호러스 목사님 말일세 —\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻인지 알겠네.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"— 아니면 크루얼러 부인께 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면, 그 아가씨들에게 아비 노릇을 해 주는 것이야말로 내 소원을 더없이 만족시키는 일이 되리라고 말씀드렸다네. 그분은 더없이 훌륭한 태도로, 내 마음을 무척이나 흐뭇하게 해 주는 답을 주셨고, 이 일에 크루얼러 부인의 승낙을 받아 내겠노라 약속하셨지. 그분들은 부인 때문에 무척 끔찍한 시간을 보냈다네. 그게 부인의 다리에서 시작해 가슴으로, 다음엔 머리로 치밀어 올랐거든 —\"\n\n\"무엇이 치밀어 올랐단 말인가?\" 내가 물었다.\n\"부인의 슬픔 말이야.\" 트래들스가 자못 진지한 낯빛으로 대답했다. \"부인의 감정 전반이지. 전에 한번 말했듯이, 부인은 무척 뛰어난 분이지만 사지를 못 쓰신다네. 부인을 괴롭히는 일이 생기면, 그게 보통은 다리에 자리를 잡는데, 이번에는 가슴으로 올라갔다가, 다음엔 머리로 올라가서, 한마디로 온몸으로 더없이 끔찍하게 퍼져 나갔지. 그래도 그분들이 쉼 없는 정성과 사랑으로 부인을 거기서 끌어내 주셔서, 우리는 어제로 육 주 전에 결혼식을 올렸다네. 코퍼필드, 온 가족이 사방에서 울고불고 정신을 잃는 꼴을 보았을 때 내가 얼마나 괴물 같은 심정이었는지 자넨 짐작도 못 할 걸세! 크루얼러 부인은 우리가 떠나기 전에는 나를 차마 보지 못하셨다네 — 그땐 자기 아이를 빼앗아 갔다고 나를 용서하지 못하셨지 — 하지만 마음씨가 고운 분이라 그 뒤로는 용서해 주셨어. 바로 오늘 아침에도 부인께 더없이 정다운 편지를 받았다네.\"\n\n\"그러니 한마디로, 친애하는 친구여,\" 내가 말했다. \"자넨 누릴 자격이 있는 만큼 행복을 느끼고 있는 게로군!\"\n\n\"오! 그건 자네가 날 편애해서 그런 거지!\" 트래들스가 웃었다. \"하지만, 정말이지, 나는 더없이 부러운 처지에 있다네. 열심히 일하고, 게걸스럽게 법을 공부하지. 매일 아침 다섯 시에 일어나는데, 조금도 힘들지 않아. 낮에는 그 애들을 감춰 두고, 저녁에는 함께 즐겁게 보낸다네. 그리고 정말이지 그 애들이 화요일에 집으로 돌아간다니 못내 아쉬워 — 그날이 미가엘 축일 학기 첫날 바로 전날이거든. 그런데 마침,\" 트래들스가 속내 털어놓던 이야기를 멈추고 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그 애들이 왔구먼! 코퍼필드 씨, 이쪽은 크루얼러 양 — 세라 양 — 루이자 양 — 그리고 마거릿과 루시라네!\"\n\n그들은 그야말로 장미 한 둥지였다. 어찌나 싱그럽고 발랄해 보이던지. 다들 예뻤고, 캐럴라인 양은 무척 아름다웠다. 하지만 소피의 환한 얼굴에는 그보다 더 나은, 사랑스럽고 명랑하며 난롯가의 온기 같은 품성이 깃들어 있어, 내 친구가 잘 골랐음을 나에게 확신시켜 주었다. 우리는 다 함께 난로 둘레에 둘러앉았다. 그동안 그 약삭빠른 사내아이는 — 이제 보니 서류를 치우느라 숨이 찼던 모양이다 — 서류를 다시 치우고는 찻그릇을 내왔다. 그러고 나서 그 아이는 우리 등 뒤로 바깥문을 쾅 닫으며 잠자리에 들러 물러갔다. 트래들스 부인은 살림하는 사람 특유의 눈빛에서 더없는 기쁨과 차분함을 뿜어내며 차를 끓이고는, 난롯가 한구석에 앉아 조용히 토스트를 구웠다.\n\n토스트를 구우면서 부인이 나에게 들려준 바로는, 애그니스를 만났다고 했다. '톰'이 신혼여행으로 부인을 켄트로 데려갔는데, 거기서 우리 고모님도 만났다는 것이었다. 고모님도 애그니스도 다들 잘 지내고 있고, 모두가 내 얘기밖에 하지 않았다고 했다. '톰'은 내가 떠나 있던 그 오랜 세월 내내 단 한 번도 나를 마음에서 놓은 적이 없었다고, 부인은 정말로 그렇게 믿고 있었다. '톰'은 무슨 일에서든 권위 그 자체였다. '톰'은 분명 그녀의 삶의 우상이었다. 어떤 소동에도 그 받침대 위에서 흔들리는 일이 없고, 무슨 일이 닥치든 늘 믿음의 대상이며, 온 마음의 신앙을 다해 떠받드는 존재였다.\n\n그녀와 트래들스가 그 미인에게 보이는 깍듯함은 나를 무척 흐뭇하게 했다. 그게 썩 합당하다고 생각했는지는 모르겠지만, 무척 흐뭇한 일이라고, 그리고 본질적으로 그들 성품의 일부라고 여겼다. 트래들스가 아직 벌어들이지 못한 그 찻숟가락들을 한순간이라도 아쉬워한 적이 있다면, 그것은 틀림없이 그 미인에게 차를 건넬 때였으리라. 그의 마음씨 고운 아내가 누군가를 상대로 자기주장을 내세운 적이 있다면, 그것은 오직 그녀가 그 미인의 언니였기 때문이었으리라 나는 확신한다. 그 미인에게서 내가 본, 다소 응석받이에 변덕스러운 태도의 자그마한 기색들을 트래들스 부부는 명백히 그녀의 타고난 권리이자 천부의 자질로 여겼다. 그녀가 여왕벌로 태어나고 그들이 일벌이었다 한들, 그보다 더 만족스럽게 받아들이지는 못했으리라.\n\n하지만 자기를 잊은 그들의 모습이 나를 매료시켰다. 이 아가씨들에 대한 그들의 자랑스러움, 그리고 그 모든 변덕에 자신들을 내맡기는 그들의 모습은, 내가 보고 싶었던 그들 자신의 가치에 대한 더없이 흐뭇하고 자그마한 증거였다. 그날 저녁 내내 트래들스가 '여보'라는 소리를 한 번이라도 들었다면, 그리고 이것 좀 가져오라, 저것 좀 갖다 놓아라, 이것 좀 집어 들어라, 저것 좀 내려놓아라, 무엇 좀 찾아라, 무엇 좀 가져오라는 부탁을 받았다면, 그는 처제들 가운데 누군가에게 한 시간에 적어도 열두 번은 그런 부탁을 받았다. 또한 그들은 소피 없이는 아무것도 하지 못했다. 누군가의 머리가 흘러내리면, 그것을 올려 줄 사람은 소피밖에 없었다. 누군가가 어떤 곡조가 어떻게 가는지 잊어버리면, 그 곡조를 바르게 흥얼거려 줄 사람은 소피밖에 없었다. 누군가가 데번셔의 어떤 지명을 떠올리고 싶어 하면, 그것을 아는 사람은 소피뿐이었다. 집에 편지를 써 보낼 일이 생기면, 아침 식사 전에 미덥게 그 편지를 쓸 수 있는 사람은 소피 한 사람뿐이었다. 누군가가 뜨개질을 하다 막히면, 그 막힌 데를 바로잡아 줄 사람은 소피밖에 없었다. 그들은 이곳의 완전한 안주인들이었고, 소피와 트래들스는 그들의 시중을 들었다. 소피가 한창때 몇 명의 아이를 돌보았을지 나로서는 짐작도 못 하겠지만, 영어로 아이에게 불러 준 온갖 노래를 죄다 안다는 점에서 유명한 사람인 듯했다. 그녀는 세상에서 가장 맑고 자그마한 목소리로 주문받은 노래를 수십 곡씩 잇달아 불러 주었으니(자매마다 저마다 다른 곡을 시켰고, 미인은 대개 맨 마지막에 끼어들었다), 나는 그만 완전히 매혹되고 말았다. 무엇보다도 좋았던 것은, 그렇게 요구를 늘어놓는 와중에도 자매들 모두가 소피와 트래들스 두 사람에게 깊은 다정함과 존경을 품고 있다는 점이었다. 정말이지, 내가 작별을 고하고 트래들스가 나를 커피하우스까지 바래다주려고 나설 때, 나는 그토록 고집스러운 머리카락이, 아니 그 어떤 머리카락이든, 그렇게 쏟아지는 입맞춤의 소나기 속에서 마구 뒤엉키는 것을 본 적이 없다고 생각했다.\n\n대체로 그것은, 돌아와서 트래들스에게 잘 자라고 인사한 뒤에도 오래도록 즐거이 곱씹지 않을 수 없는 광경이었다. 그 시들어 버린 그레이스인의 맨 위층 사무실에서 천 송이 장미가 피어나는 것을 보았다 한들, 그처럼 절반만큼이라도 그곳을 환하게 밝혀 주지는 못했으리라. 메마른 법률 문구점들과 사무 변호사 사무실들 틈에 자리한 그 데번셔 아가씨들이라는 발상, 그리고 가루분과 양피지, 붉은 끈, 먼지 앉은 봉인지, 잉크병, 소장과 초안 용지, 판례집, 영장, 소장, 비용 청구서가 빚어내는 그 음산한 분위기 속의 차와 토스트와 동요라는 발상은, 마치 술탄의 그 유명한 가족이 사무 변호사 명부에 올라 말하는 새와 노래하는 나무와 황금빛 물을 그레이스인 홀로 가져왔다고 내가 꿈꾼 것이나 다름없이 유쾌한 공상으로 다가왔다. 어쩐 일인지, 나는 트래들스에게 밤 인사를 하고 커피하우스로 돌아온 무렵에는, 그에 대한 내 낙담이 크게 달라져 있는 것을 깨달았다. 나는 영국의 그 숱한 수석 종업원들이 무어라 분부하든, 그가 잘 풀려 나가리라는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n느긋하게 그를 생각해 보려고 커피룸 난로 가운데 하나 앞으로 의자를 끌어다 놓고 보니, 나는 차츰 그의 행복을 헤아리던 데서 벗어나, 벌겋게 타는 석탄 속에서 앞날을 더듬어 보고, 또 그것들이 부서지고 모양을 바꾸는 모습을 따라 내 인생에 새겨진 주요한 부침과 이별들을 떠올리게 되었다. 삼 년 전 영국을 떠난 뒤로 나는 석탄 불을 본 적이 없었다. 비록 장작 불이라면 숱하게 지켜봐 왔지만 말이다. 그것이 허옇게 재로 부스러져 난로 위 깃털 같은 잿더미에 섞이는 모습을, 낙담에 빠진 나에게 그것은 내 죽어 버린 희망의 모습을 자못 어울리게 그려 보이곤 했다.\n\n이제 나는 지난날을 떠올릴 수 있었다. 침울하게나마, 그러나 쓰라리지는 않게. 그리고 씩씩한 마음으로 앞날을 헤아릴 수 있었다. 가장 참된 의미에서의 가정은, 이제 나에게 더는 없었다. 내가 더 애틋한 사랑을 불어넣어 줄 수도 있었을 그 사람을, 나는 내 누이가 되도록 가르쳐 버렸다. 그녀는 결혼할 테고, 그 다정함을 새로이 차지할 사람들이 생길 테며, 그러는 동안에도 내 가슴속에 자라난 그녀를 향한 사랑은 영영 알지 못하리라. 내가 무모한 열정의 대가를 치르는 것은 마땅한 일이었다. 내가 뿌린 것을, 내가 거두는 것이었다.\n\n나는 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그리고 내가 정말로 이 일에 마음을 단단히 길들였는지, 굳세게 이를 견뎌 낼 수 있을지, 그녀가 내 가정에서 차분히 차지해 온 자리를 나도 차분히 그녀의 가정에서 지켜 낼 수 있을지 헤아리고 있는데 — 문득 내 눈길이 어느 얼굴에 멎어 있는 것을 깨달았다. 그 얼굴은 어린 시절의 기억과 얽혀 있어, 마치 불 속에서 떠오른 것만 같았다.\n\n이 이야기의 맨 첫 장에서 내가 그 친절한 도움을 입었던 자그마한 칠립 의사 선생님이, 맞은편 구석의 그늘 속에 앉아 신문을 읽고 있었다. 이 무렵 그는 나이가 제법 들어 있었다. 하지만 온화하고 유순하며 차분한 자그마한 사람이라 그런지 어찌나 곱게 늙었던지, 그 순간 그의 모습은 우리 집 거실에 앉아 내가 태어나기를 기다리던 시절의 모습 그대로인 것 같았다.\n\n칠립 선생님은 육칠 년 전에 블런더스톤을 떠났고, 나는 그 뒤로 그를 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 그는 자그마한 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울인 채, 팔꿈치 옆에 따끈한 셰리주 니거스 한 잔을 두고 평온히 신문을 들여다보고 있었다. 그의 태도가 어찌나 더없이 고분고분하던지, 신문을 읽는 외람됨을 두고 그 신문에게마저 사과하는 듯이 보였다.\n\n나는 그가 앉아 있는 데로 걸어가, \"안녕하십니까, 칠립 선생님?\" 하고 말했다.\n\n낯선 사람의 이 뜻밖의 말 걸기에 그는 몹시 허둥대더니, 예의 그 느릿한 말투로 대답했다. \"감사합니다, 손님, 참 친절하시군요. 고맙습니다, 손님. 손님께서도 안녕하시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"저를 기억 못 하시는군요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 더없이 유순하게 웃으며, 나를 살피느라 고개를 절레절레 흔들면서 대답했다. \"어쩐지 손님 얼굴에 낯익은 무언가가 있는 듯한 느낌이 들기는 하는데요, 손님. 도무지 성함이 떠오르질 않는군요, 정말로.\"\n\n\"하지만 선생님은 제가 제 이름을 알기 한참 전부터 그걸 알고 계셨답니다.\" 내가 받았다.\n\n\"제가 정말 그랬습니까, 손님?\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"혹시 제가, 손님, 그러니까 — 그때 거들어 드리는 영광을 누렸던 건 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이런 세상에!\" 칠립 선생님이 소리쳤다. \"하지만 그때 이후로 손님은 무척 많이 변하셨겠지요, 손님?\"\n\n\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"저, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"제가 어쩔 수 없이 성함을 여쭙는 무례를 범하더라도 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n내 이름을 일러 주자, 그는 정말로 감동했다. 그는 나와 한바탕 악수까지 했는데 — 그에게는 격렬한 행동이었다. 그가 늘 하는 식이라면 자기 엉덩이께에서 한두 치쯤 앞으로 미적지근한 생선용 칼 같은 손을 슬며시 내밀고는, 누구든 그것을 덥석 잡으면 더없이 당황하는 기색을 보이는 것이었으니까. 지금도 그는 손을 빼낼 수 있게 되자마자 외투 주머니에 손을 집어넣고, 그 손을 무사히 도로 거둬들이고 나서야 안심하는 듯했다.\n\n\"이런, 손님!\" 칠립 선생님이 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울인 채 나를 살피며 말했다. \"그러니까 코퍼필드 씨로군요, 그렇지요? 저런, 손님, 외람되게도 좀 더 가까이 들여다봤더라면 알아봤을 텐데요. 손님은 돌아가신 부친과 무척 닮으셨거든요, 손님.\"\n\n\"저는 아버지를 뵙는 기쁨을 한 번도 누리지 못했답니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"지당하신 말씀입니다, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 달래는 듯한 어조로 말했다. \"그리고 그것은, 여러모로, 무척이나 애석한 일이었지요! 저희도, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 다시 자그마한 머리를 천천히 흔들며 말했다. \"저희 시골 동네에서도 손님의 명성을 모르지 않습니다. 여기는 무척 흥분되는 곳이겠지요, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 집게손가락으로 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"참으로 고된 일이라 여기실 게 틀림없습니다, 손님!\"\n\n\"요즘은 어느 동네에 계십니까?\" 나는 그의 곁에 자리를 잡으며 물었다.\n\n\"베리세인트에드먼즈에서 몇 마일 안쪽에 자리 잡았습니다, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"집사람이 그 동네에서 부친 유언에 따라 자그마한 재산을 물려받게 되어서, 제가 그 아래쪽에서 병원을 하나 사들였지요. 손님께서 들으시면 기뻐하실 텐데, 거기서 잘 지내고 있답니다. 제 딸아이는 이제 제법 키 큰 아가씨로 자라고 있답니다, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 자그마한 머리를 또 한 번 자그맣게 흔들며 말했다. \"바로 지난주에도 애 엄마가 그 애 원피스 단을 두 단이나 내려 주었지요. 세월이란 게 그런 거지요, 손님, 안 그렇습니까!\"\n\n이렇게 술회하면서 그 자그마한 사람이 이제 빈 잔을 입에 갖다 대기에, 나는 그에게 잔을 다시 채우자고 권하면서 나도 한 잔 더 들어 같이 어울리겠노라 했다. \"글쎄요, 손님,\" 그가 예의 그 느릿한 말투로 받았다. \"제 주량을 넘는 일입니다만, 손님과 이야기 나누는 즐거움을 차마 마다하지 못하겠군요. 손님께서 홍역을 앓으실 때 제가 돌봐 드리는 영광을 누린 게 바로 어제 일만 같습니다. 손님은 홍역을 멋지게 이겨 내셨지요, 손님!\"\n\n나는 이 칭찬을 받아들이고는 니거스를 주문했고, 그것은 곧 나왔다. \"정말 보기 드문 호사로군요!\" 칠립 선생님이 잔을 저으며 말했다. \"하지만 이렇게 각별한 자리를 거역할 수가 없어서요. 손님은 가족이 없으신가요, 손님?\"\n\n나는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"손님께서 얼마 전에 상을 당하셨다는 건 알고 있었습니다, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"손님의 장인 누이에게서 들었지요. 거참 보통내기가 아닌 분이더군요, 손님?\"\n\n\"음, 그렇지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"보통내기가 아니고말고요. 그 사람을 어디서 보셨습니까, 칠립 선생님?\"\n\n\"손님께선 모르시나요, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 더없이 평온한 미소를 지으며 받았다. \"손님의 장인이 다시 제 이웃이 됐다는 걸요?\"\n\"몰랐습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그렇답니다, 손님!\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"그 동네 젊은 아가씨와 결혼했는데, 자그마한 재산을 제법 가진 사람이었지요, 가엾게도. — 그런데 손님, 지금 이 두뇌 활동 말입니다만, 손님은 이게 피곤하게 느껴지지 않으십니까?\" 칠립 선생님이 감탄에 젖은 울새처럼 나를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n나는 그 질문을 흘려 넘기고 머드스톤 남매 얘기로 돌아갔다. \"그 사람이 재혼한 건 저도 알고 있었습니다. 그 집안을 진찰하십니까?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"규칙적으로는 아닙니다. 불려 간 적은 있지요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"머드스톤 씨와 그 누이는 골상학으로 따지면 완고함을 관장하는 부위가 무척 발달해 있더군요, 손님.\"\n\n내가 어찌나 의미심장한 표정으로 대꾸했던지, 칠립 선생님은 그 표정과 니거스에 힘입어 머리를 몇 차례 짧게 흔들고는 생각에 잠긴 듯 외쳤다. \"아아, 이런! 우리가 옛 시절을 기억하는군요, 코퍼필드 씨!\"\n\n\"그 남매는 여전히 옛 행실대로 살고 있나 보군요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 대답했다. \"의사라는 사람은 워낙 여러 집안을 드나드니, 제 직업 말고는 눈도 귀도 가져선 안 되겠지요. 그래도 한마디 해야겠는데, 그분들은 무척 가혹합니다, 손님. 이승에 대해서나 저승에 대해서나 둘 다요.\"\n\n\"저승은 그들과 별 상관없이 다스려질 테니 걱정 없을 겁니다.\" 내가 받았다. \"이승에 대해선 그들이 어떻게 하고 있습니까?\"\n\n칠립 선생님은 고개를 흔들고, 니거스를 젓더니, 한 모금 홀짝였다.\n\n\"매력적인 부인이었답니다, 손님!\" 그가 애처로운 투로 말했다.\n\n\"지금의 머드스톤 부인 말입니까?\"\n\n\"정말 매력적인 부인이었지요, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"더없이 상냥한, 그야말로 상냥할 수 있는 만큼 상냥한 분이었습니다! 우리 집사람 생각으론, 그 부인의 기개가 결혼한 뒤로 완전히 꺾여 버려서, 거의 우울증에 미칠 지경이 됐다는군요. 그리고 여자들이란,\" 칠립 선생님이 조심스레 말했다. \"관찰력이 대단하지요, 손님.\"\n\n\"그 가증스러운 틀에 맞춰 그 부인을 억누르고 꺾어 버리려 했겠지요, 하느님 도우소서!\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 그렇게 돼 버린 거고요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 손님, 처음에는 격렬한 다툼이 있었답니다, 정말로요.\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"하지만 이제 그 부인은 완전히 그림자가 됐지요. 외람된 말씀일지 모르나, 손님, 은밀히 말씀드리자면, 그 누이가 거들러 온 뒤로 오누이가 둘이서 그 부인을 거의 백치 상태로 만들어 버렸답니다.\"\n\n나는 그 말을 쉬이 믿을 수 있다고 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"저는 서슴없이 말씀드릴 수 있습니다, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 니거스를 또 한 모금 들이켜 기운을 차리며 말했다. \"이건 손님과 저 둘만의 얘기입니다만, 그 부인의 어머니는 그 일로 돌아가신 거나 다름없다고요 — 아니, 그 횡포와 우울과 시달림이 머드스톤 부인을 거의 백치로 만들어 버렸다고 말입니다. 결혼 전에는 발랄한 젊은 여자였지요, 손님. 그런데 그들의 우울함과 엄혹함이 그 여자를 망가뜨린 겁니다. 이제 그들은 그 부인을 데리고 다니는 게, 남편과 시누이라기보단 마치 간수 같답니다. 그게 바로 지난주에 우리 집사람이 저한테 한 말이지요. 그리고 정말이지, 손님, 여자들은 관찰력이 대단하답니다. 우리 집사람부터가 대단한 관찰자고요!\"\n\n\"그자는 아직도 음울하게 자기가 (이런 데 그 말을 갖다 붙이려니 부끄럽습니다만) 신앙심 깊다고 떠벌리고 다닙니까?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"손님께서 앞질러 말씀하시는군요, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 평소답지 않게 들이켠 자극에 눈꺼풀이 제법 벌게진 채 말했다. \"우리 집사람이 한 가장 인상 깊은 말 가운데 하나거든요. 우리 집사람이,\" 그가 더없이 차분하고 느릿한 투로 이었다. \"머드스톤 씨가 자기 자신의 형상을 세워 놓고 그걸 신성이라 부른다고 짚어 줬을 때, 저는 정말 깜짝 놀랐답니다. 우리 집사람이 그렇게 말했을 때, 손님, 정말이지 깃펜 깃털로 살짝 밀기만 해도 제가 벌렁 나자빠졌을 겁니다. 여자들은 관찰력이 대단하지요, 손님?\"\n\n\"본능적으로 그렇지요.\" 내가 말했고, 그는 더없이 기뻐했다.\n\n\"제 견해에 그런 지지를 받게 되어 무척 기쁩니다, 손님.\" 그가 받았다. \"정말이지, 제가 의학과 무관한 견해를 감히 내놓는 일은 흔치 않거든요. 머드스톤 씨는 가끔 대중 연설을 하는데, 사람들 말이 — 한마디로, 손님, 우리 집사람 말로는 — 그자가 요즘 더 음흉한 폭군이 될수록, 그자의 교리는 더 흉포해진다는군요.\"\n\n\"우리 집사람 말이 더없이 옳다고 봅니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 집사람은 이런 말까지 한답니다,\" 더없이 유순한 그 자그마한 사람이 한껏 기운을 얻어 이었다. \"그런 자들이 자기네 신앙이라고 잘못 일컫는 것은, 사실은 제 못된 심사와 오만을 쏟아 내는 배출구일 뿐이라고요. 그리고 그거 아십니까, 손님,\" 그가 부드럽게 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울이며 이었다. \"저는 신약 성서 어디에서도 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양에 대한 근거를 찾지 못하겠더군요.\"\n\n\"저도 한 번도 못 찾았습니다!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"한편으로, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"그들은 무척 미움을 받고 있답니다. 그런데 그들은 자기네를 싫어하는 사람은 누구든 거리낌 없이 지옥에 떨어지라고 단죄하니, 정말이지 우리 동네에선 지옥행이 어지간히 흥하고 있는 셈이지요! 하지만 우리 집사람 말마따나, 손님, 그들은 끊임없는 벌을 받고 있는 겁니다. 안으로만 향해 제 마음을 파먹고 사는데, 그 마음이라는 게 영 못된 먹이거든요. 그런데 손님, 그 두뇌 말씀입니다만, 다시 거기로 돌아가는 걸 양해해 주신다면요. 손님은 그걸 너무 많은 흥분에 내맡기시는 것 아닙니까, 손님?\"\n\n니거스를 들이켠 칠립 선생님 자신의 두뇌가 흥분한 틈을 타, 나는 어렵잖게 그의 관심을 이 화제에서 그 자신의 일로 돌려놓았다. 그러자 그는 이후 삼십 분 동안 제법 수다스러워졌는데, 다른 여러 소식 가운데서도, 자기가 지금 그레이스인 커피하우스에 와 있는 것은 정신 질환 위원회에 의학 소견을 제출하기 위해서이며, 그 환자는 과음으로 정신이 이상해진 사람이라는 것을 나에게 알려 주었다. \"그리고 정말이지, 손님,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그런 자리에선 무척 떨린답니다. 이른바 윽박지름을 당하는 건 도저히 못 견디겠어요, 손님. 그러면 완전히 기가 꺾여 버리거든요. 그거 아십니까, 손님이 태어나신 날 밤, 제가 그 무시무시한 부인을 다루던 기개를 되찾는 데 한참이나 걸렸답니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\"\n\n나는 그날 밤의 그 용에게로, 바로 우리 고모님께로 내일 아침 일찍 내려갈 참이며, 고모님을 좀 더 잘 알게 되면 그도 익히 알게 되겠지만, 고모님은 더없이 다정하고 훌륭한 부인 가운데 한 분이라고 그에게 말해 주었다. 그가 언젠가 고모님을 다시 볼지도 모른다는 생각만으로도 그는 겁에 질리는 듯했다. 그는 핏기 없는 자그마한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"정말 그러신가요, 손님? 정말요?\" 그러더니 거의 곧장 촛불을 청해서는, 다른 데서는 도무지 안심이 안 된다는 듯 잠자리에 들었다. 그가 니거스에 정말로 비틀거린 것은 아니었다. 하지만 짐작건대, 고모님이 보닛으로 그를 후려쳤던 그 크나큰 실망의 밤 이래로, 그의 평온하고 자그마한 맥박이 일 분에 두세 번쯤은 더 뛰었으리라.\n\n나도 녹초가 되어 자정에 잠자리에 들었다. 이튿날은 도버행 마차에서 보냈고, 고모님이 차를 들고 계실 때(이젠 안경을 쓰고 계셨다) 그 옛 거실로 무사히 멀쩡하게 뛰어들었다. 그러자 고모님과 딕 씨, 그리고 가정부 노릇을 하고 있던 정다운 패거티가 두 팔을 활짝 벌리고 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 나를 맞아 주었다. 우리가 차분히 이야기를 나누기 시작했을 때, 고모님은 내가 칠립 선생님과 마주친 이야기며 그가 고모님을 그토록 두려운 기억으로 간직하고 있다는 이야기에 무척 즐거워하셨다. 그리고 고모님도 패거티도, 내 가엾은 어머니의 두 번째 남편이며 '그 살인자 같은 누이년'에 대해 할 말이 무척 많았다 — 그 누이로 말하자면, 그 어떤 고통이나 형벌을 들이댄들 고모님이 결코 기독교식 이름이든 제대로 된 이름이든, 아니 그 어떤 호칭이든 붙여 줄 리 없었을 거라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n# CHAPTER 60. AGNES\n\n이모와 나는 단둘이 남게 되자 밤이 깊도록 이야기를 나누었다. 이민을 떠난 사람들이 본국으로 편지를 보낼 때면 한결같이 밝고 희망찬 내용만 적어 보냈다는 것, 미코버 씨가 사람과 사람 사이의 거래답게 그토록 격식을 차리며 입에 올리던 그 '금전상의 채무' 명목으로 실제로 자잘한 액수의 돈을 여러 차례 송금해 왔다는 것, 도버로 돌아온 제닛이 다시 이모의 시중을 들다가 마침내 잘나가는 선술집 주인과 혼인함으로써 사내라는 족속을 영영 끊겠다던 선언을 실행에 옮겼다는 것, 그리고 이모 또한 그 신부를 거들고 부추기며 결혼식에 직접 참석해 식을 빛내 줌으로써 그 거룩한 신조에 마지막으로 자기 도장을 찍었다는 것 — 이런 것들이 우리가 나눈 화제였는데, 그동안 받은 편지를 통해 나는 이미 어지간히 알고 있던 이야기였다. 늘 그렇듯 딕 씨도 빠지지 않았다. 이모는 딕 씨가 손에 잡히는 것이라면 무엇이든 끊임없이 베껴 적는 일에 몰두하면서, 그 일하는 시늉으로 찰스 일세 왕을 점잖은 거리 밖에 붙들어 둔다는 것, 단조로운 속박 속에서 시들어 가는 대신 자유롭고 행복하게 지내는 것이 이모의 삶에서 가장 큰 기쁨이자 보람 가운데 하나라는 것, 그리고 (새삼스러운 결론이지만) 딕 씨가 어떤 사람인지를 온전히 알아볼 수 있는 사람은 이모 자신밖에 없다는 것을 알려 주었다.\n\n\"그런데 말이다, 트롯,\" 우리가 옛날처럼 난롯가에 앉아 있는데 이모가 내 손등을 토닥이며 말했다. \"넌 언제 캔터베리에 가 볼 작정이냐?\"\n\n\"말을 한 마리 빌려서 내일 아침에 타고 가 보려고요, 이모. 이모가 저랑 같이 가실 게 아니라면요.\"\n\n\"싫다!\" 이모가 특유의 짧고 퉁명스러운 투로 말했다. \"난 그냥 여기 있을란다.\"\n\n그러면 말을 타고 가겠노라고 나는 말했다. 이모가 아닌 다른 사람을 만나러 가는 길이었다면 오늘 캔터베리를 지나면서 그냥 지나칠 수는 없었을 것이라고 했다.\n\n이모는 흐뭇해하면서도 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"쯧, 트롯. 이 늙은 뼈다귀가 내일까지야 못 버티겠니!\" 그러고는 내가 생각에 잠긴 채 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 동안 다시 한번 내 손을 가만히 토닥여 주었다.\n\n생각에 잠겼다고 한 것은, 내가 이곳에 다시 와서 애그니스와 이토록 가까이 있으면서, 그토록 오래 마음을 짓눌러 온 그 회한이 되살아나지 않을 수 없었기 때문이다. 어린 시절이 송두리째 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있을 때 미처 배우지 못했던 것을 뒤늦게 깨우쳐 주는, 한결 누그러진 회한이었을지 모르나, 그래도 회한은 회한이었다. \"아, 트롯,\" 이모가 다시 한번 내게 말하는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 이제는 그 뜻을 전보다 더 잘 알 수 있었다 — \"눈먼 것아, 눈먼 것아, 눈먼 것아!\"\n\n우리 둘은 몇 분 동안 말없이 앉아 있었다. 내가 눈을 들었을 때, 이모가 나를 가만히 지켜보고 있는 것을 보았다. 어쩌면 이모는 내 마음의 흐름을 좇고 있었는지도 모른다. 한때는 그토록 제멋대로였던 그 마음도 이제는 따라가기 쉬워 보였으니 말이다.\n\n\"가 보면 그 애 아버지가 백발노인이 되어 있을 게다.\" 이모가 말했다. \"그 밖의 모든 점에서는 더 나은 사람이 됐지만 말이야 — 새사람이 됐지. 그리고 이제는 인간의 온갖 관심사며 기쁨이며 슬픔을, 그 가엾고 작은 한 치짜리 자로 죄다 재 보려 들지도 않을 거다. 내 말 믿으렴, 얘야, 그런 것들을 그런 식으로 잴 수 있으려면 아주 많이 쪼그라들어야 하는 법이거든.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇겠지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 애를 보면,\" 이모가 말을 이었다. \"언제나 그래 왔듯이 착하고, 아름답고, 진실하고, 사심 없는 모습 그대로일 게다. 트롯, 내가 그보다 더 높은 칭찬을 알았더라면, 그 애한테 다 갖다 바쳤을 거야.\"\n\n그 애에게 그보다 더 높은 칭찬은 없었고, 나에게 그보다 더 큰 질책도 없었다. 아, 나는 어쩌다가 그렇게 멀리 헤매고 다녔던가!\n\n\"제 곁에 두고 가르치는 어린 계집애들을 자기처럼 길러 낸다면,\" 이모가 어찌나 간절한지 눈에 눈물까지 그렁그렁한 채로 말했다. \"하늘은 아실 게다, 그 애의 삶이 참으로 보람되게 쓰이리라는 걸! 그 애가 그날 말했던 대로, 쓸모 있고 행복하게 말이야! 그 애가 쓸모 있고 행복하지 않을 도리가 어디 있겠니!\"\n\n\"애그니스한테 혹시—\" 나는 말을 한다기보다 소리 내어 생각하고 있었다.\n\n\"왜? 응? 혹시 뭐?\" 이모가 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"연인이라도 있나요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"한 무더기지.\" 이모가 일종의 분개 어린 자랑스러움을 담아 외쳤다. \"네가 떠나 있는 동안에, 얘야, 그 애는 스무 번도 더 시집갈 수 있었을 거다!\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그렇고말고요. 하지만 그 애한테 어울릴 만한 연인이 있느냐는 거예요. 애그니스라면 그만 못한 사람한테는 마음을 줄 수 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n이모는 턱을 손에 괸 채 잠시 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 천천히 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보며 말했다.\n\n\"내 짐작엔 그 애가 마음을 둔 사람이 있는 것 같다, 트롯.\"\n\n\"잘되어 가는 사이인가요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"트롯,\" 이모가 무겁게 대꾸했다. \"나도 모르겠다. 사실 이만큼 말할 자격도 내겐 없어. 그 애가 내게 털어놓은 적은 한 번도 없지만, 그저 짐작이 그렇다는 거야.\"\n\n이모는 너무도 골똘하고 근심스럽게 나를 바라보아서 (나는 이모가 떠는 것까지 보았다), 이모가 조금 전 내 속마음을 좇고 있었다는 것을 이제 그 어느 때보다도 절실히 느꼈다. 나는 그 숱한 낮과 밤을 거치며, 그 숱한 마음속 갈등을 거치며 다져 온 결심을 모조리 끌어모았다.\n\n\"만약 정말 그렇다면,\" 내가 입을 떼었다. \"그리고 저는 그렇기를 바라는데—\"\n\n\"정말 그런지 어떤지 난 모른다.\" 이모가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"내 짐작에 휘둘리면 안 돼. 이건 너만 알고 있어야 한다. 어쩌면 아주 어렴풋한 짐작일 뿐이야. 나는 입을 놀릴 자격이 없어.\"\n\n\"만약 정말 그렇다면,\" 나는 거듭 말했다. \"애그니스가 때가 되면 제게 말해 줄 거예요. 제가 그토록 많은 걸 털어놓은 누이라면, 이모, 저한테 마음을 털어놓기를 꺼리지는 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n이모는 나에게 눈길을 돌렸던 때처럼 천천히 내게서 눈을 거두더니, 생각에 잠긴 채 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다. 이윽고 다른 손을 내 어깨에 얹었다. 그렇게 우리 둘은 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않은 채, 그날 밤 헤어질 때까지 지난날을 들여다보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n나는 이른 아침에 말을 타고 옛 학창 시절의 무대로 떠났다. 나 자신을 이겨 내고 있다는 희망 속에서도, 그렇게 곧 다시 그 애의 얼굴을 보게 되리라는 기대 앞에서도, 아직 마음이 온전히 행복하다고는 말할 수 없었다.\n\n낯익은 길은 금세 지나쳤고, 나는 돌멩이 하나하나가 내겐 한 소년의 이야기책이던 그 고요한 거리로 들어섰다. 나는 걸어서 그 옛집으로 갔다가, 들어서기엔 가슴이 너무 벅차 발길을 돌렸다. 다시 돌아와, 지나는 길에 그 작은 탑 모양 방의 낮은 창문 너머를 들여다보았는데 — 처음에는 유라이아 힙이, 나중에는 미코버 씨가 늘 앉아 있던 그 방이었다 — 이제는 작은 거실이 되어 있고 사무실은 없었다. 그 밖에는, 차분하고 오래된 그 집이 깨끗하고 정돈된 모습 그대로, 내가 처음 보았을 때와 조금도 다름이 없었다. 나는 문을 열어 준 새 하녀에게, 외국에 있는 한 친구의 부탁으로 위크필드 양을 뵈러 온 신사가 와 있다고 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 그러자 나는 그 점잖고 오래된 계단을 따라 (내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 계단 단을 조심하라는 당부와 함께) 조금도 변하지 않은 응접실로 안내받았다. 애그니스와 내가 함께 읽던 책들이 그 선반에 그대로 꽂혀 있었고, 내가 여러 밤 동안 공부에 매달리던 책상도 여전히 탁자의 그 옛 모서리에 놓여 있었다. 힙 부자가 있던 동안 슬그머니 끼어들었던 사소한 변화들은 죄다 다시 제자리로 돌아와 있었다. 모든 것이 그 행복하던 시절 그대로였다.\n\n나는 창가에 서서, 오래된 거리 건너편 집들을 바라보며, 처음 이곳에 왔을 때 비 내리는 오후면 그 집들을 지켜보던 일을 떠올렸다. 어느 창문에든 누군가 나타나면 그 사람에 대해 이런저런 짐작을 해 보고, 그들이 계단을 오르내리는 모습을 눈으로 좇곤 했던 일을. 그러는 동안 여자들은 나막신을 딸깍거리며 보도를 걸어갔고, 흐린 비는 빗금을 그으며 내려, 저쪽 빗물받이 홈통에서 쏟아져 길바닥으로 흘러내렸다. 그 비 내리는 저녁이면, 땅거미가 질 무렵 떠돌이들이 마을로 들어와 막대 끝에 보따리를 매달아 어깨에 늘어뜨린 채 절뚝거리며 지나가는 모습을 지켜보던 그 느낌이, 그때처럼 축축한 흙냄새와 젖은 나뭇잎과 들장미 냄새를 가득 품고서, 그리고 내 고단한 여정길에 내 몸을 스치던 바로 그 바람의 감촉을 품고서, 생생하게 되살아났다.\n\n판자벽에 난 작은 문이 열리는 바람에 나는 흠칫 놀라 돌아섰다. 그 애가 내게로 다가오는데, 아름답고 잔잔한 두 눈이 내 눈과 마주쳤다. 그 애는 걸음을 멈추고 한 손을 가슴에 얹었고, 나는 두 팔로 그 애를 끌어안았다.\n\n\"애그니스! 사랑하는 내 누이! 너무 갑작스럽게 들이닥쳤구나.\"\n\n\"아니야, 아니야! 널 보니 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라, 트롯우드!\"\n\n\"사랑하는 애그니스, 너를 다시 보게 되다니 내겐 정말 큰 행복이야!\"\n\n나는 그 애를 가슴에 끌어안았고, 우리 둘은 잠시 말이 없었다. 이윽고 우리는 나란히 자리에 앉았다. 천사 같은 그 얼굴이, 깨어 있을 때나 잠들어 있을 때나 몇 해 동안 꿈꿔 온 바로 그 환대를 담고 나를 향했다.\n\n그 애는 너무도 진실했고, 너무도 아름다웠고, 너무도 착했다 — 나는 그 애에게 그토록 많은 빚을 지고 있었고, 그 애가 내겐 너무도 소중해서, 내가 느끼는 것을 무어라 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다. 나는 그 애에게 축복을 빌어 보려 했고, 고마움을 전해 보려 했고, (편지로 자주 그랬듯이) 그 애가 내게 어떤 영향을 끼쳐 왔는지 말해 보려 했다. 그러나 내 온갖 애씀은 헛수고였다. 내 사랑도 내 기쁨도 입을 떼지 못했다.\n\n그 애는 특유의 다정한 차분함으로 내 동요를 가라앉혔다. 우리가 헤어지던 때로 나를 데려가, 여러 차례 남몰래 찾아가 만났다는 에밀리 이야기를 들려주었고, 도라의 무덤 이야기를 다정하게 꺼냈다. 그 고귀한 마음의 빗나감 없는 직감으로, 그 애는 내 기억의 현(絃)을 어찌나 부드럽고 조화롭게 어루만지던지, 내 안에서 거슬리게 울리는 음이 하나도 없었다. 나는 그 슬프고도 아득한 음악에 귀 기울이며, 그것이 불러일으키는 어떤 것에서도 움츠러들고 싶지 않았다. 그 모든 것에 어우러진 것이 바로 그 애 자신, 내 삶의 더 나은 천사였으니, 내가 어찌 움츠러들 수 있었겠는가?\n\n\"그런데 너는, 애그니스,\" 이윽고 내가 말했다. \"네 이야기를 좀 들려줘. 이렇게 오랜 세월이 흐르는 동안, 너는 네 삶에 대해선 거의 한마디도 안 했잖아!\"\n\n\"무슨 이야기를 하면 될까?\" 그 애가 환한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"아버지는 잘 지내셔. 보다시피 우리는 이렇게 우리 집에서 조용히 지내고 있어. 근심거리도 다 가라앉았고, 우리 집도 우리에게 되돌아왔지. 그것만 알면, 사랑하는 트롯우드, 넌 모든 걸 다 아는 셈이야.\"\n\n\"모든 걸 다, 애그니스?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그 애는 얼굴에 조금 떨리는 의아함을 띤 채 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"다른 건 정말 없어, 누이?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n조금 전 가셨던 그 애의 핏기가 다시 돌아왔다가, 다시 가셨다. 그 애는 미소를 지었는데, 내 보기엔 어딘가 잔잔한 슬픔이 어린 미소였다. 그러고는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n나는 이모가 넌지시 비친 그것으로 그 애를 이끌어 보려 했다. 그런 속내를 듣는 것이 내겐 견디기 힘들 만큼 아픈 일일지언정, 나는 내 마음을 다스리고 그 애에게 마땅한 도리를 다해야 했으니까. 그러나 그 애가 불편해하는 것을 보고, 나는 그냥 넘어갔다.\n\n\"할 일이 많지, 사랑하는 애그니스?\"\n\n\"내 학교 말이니?\" 그 애가 환한 차분함을 그대로 간직한 채 다시 고개를 들며 말했다.\n\n\"응. 고된 일이잖아, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"그 일이 너무 즐거워서,\" 그 애가 대답했다. \"고되다는 말로 부르는 게 오히려 고마움을 모르는 짓 같아.\"\n\n\"너에게 좋은 일치고 어려운 일은 없으니까.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그 애의 핏기가 다시 한번 돌았다 가셨다. 그리고 그 애가 고개를 숙이는데, 나는 또다시 그 슬픈 미소를 보았다.\n\n\"기다렸다가 아버지를 만나 봐.\" 애그니스가 밝게 말했다. \"그리고 우리랑 하루 보내고. 어쩌면 네 방에서 자고 가도 되고? 우린 늘 그 방을 네 방이라고 불러.\"\n\n밤이 되면 이모 댁으로 말을 타고 돌아가겠다고 약속한 터라 그럴 수는 없었다. 그러나 하루를 거기서 보내는 것은 기꺼이 그러겠노라고 했다.\n\n\"난 잠시 갇힌 몸 신세가 되어야 해.\" 애그니스가 말했다. \"하지만 여기 옛 책들이 있잖아, 트롯우드, 옛 악보도 있고.\"\n\n\"옛 꽃들까지 다 여기 있네.\" 나는 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. \"아니면 그 옛날과 같은 종류든지.\"\n\n\"내 즐거움을 하나 찾았어.\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"네가 떠나 있는 동안, 우리가 어린아이였을 적 그대로 모든 걸 간직해 두는 일 말이야. 그때 우린 참 행복했던 것 같거든.\"\n\n\"하늘이 아실 거야, 우리가 그랬다는 걸!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 내 오라비를 떠올리게 하는 사소한 것 하나하나가,\" 애그니스가 다정한 두 눈을 환하게 내게로 돌리며 말했다. \"반가운 벗이 되어 주었어. 이것까지도 말이야.\" 그러면서 여전히 옆구리에 매달려 있는, 열쇠가 가득한 작은 바구니를 내게 보여 주었다. \"어쩐지 옛 가락 같은 걸 짤랑짤랑 울리는 것 같지 뭐야!\"\n\n그 애는 다시 미소 짓더니, 들어왔던 그 문으로 나갔다.\n\n이 누이로서의 정을 종교처럼 정성껏 지키는 것이 내가 할 일이었다. 그것은 내가 나 자신에게 남겨 둔 전부였고, 하나의 보물이었다. 그것이 내게 주어지게 한 그 거룩한 신뢰와 관계의 기틀을 내가 한 번이라도 흔든다면, 그것은 잃어버린 것이 되어 영영 되찾을 수 없으리라. 나는 이것을 흔들림 없이 내 앞에 세워 두었다. 그 애를 사랑하면 할수록, 나는 더더욱 이것을 잊지 말아야 했다.\n\n나는 거리를 걸었다. 그러다가 내 옛 원수였던 푸줏간 사내를 다시 보았는데 — 이제는 경관이 되어, 가게에 곤봉을 걸어 두고 있었다 — 그와 한판 붙었던 곳으로 내려가 보았다. 거기서 셰퍼드 양과 큰 라킨스 양, 그리고 그 시절의 부질없던 온갖 사랑과 호감과 반감을 곰곰이 떠올렸다. 그 시절에서 살아남은 것은 애그니스밖에 없는 듯했다. 언제나 내 머리 위의 별이던 그 애는, 더 밝고 더 높이 떠 있었다.\n\n내가 돌아왔을 때, 위크필드 씨는 마을에서 한두 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 있는 자기 정원에서 집으로 돌아와 있었다. 그는 요즘 거의 매일 그 정원 일에 몰두하고 있었다. 나는 그가 이모가 묘사한 그대로의 모습임을 알았다. 우리는 예닐곱 명쯤 되는 어린 계집애들과 함께 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 그는 벽에 걸린 그 잘생긴 초상화의 그림자에 지나지 않아 보였다.\n\n내 기억 속 그 고요한 터에 예전부터 깃들어 있던 잔잔함과 평화가, 다시 그곳을 가득 채웠다. 저녁 식사가 끝나자, 위크필드 씨는 술을 들지 않았고 나도 마시고 싶지 않아서, 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다. 그곳에서 애그니스와 그 애가 돌보는 어린 계집애들이 노래하고 연주하고 바느질을 했다. 차를 마신 뒤 아이들은 우리 곁을 떠났고, 우리 셋은 함께 앉아 지나간 날들에 대해 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n\"그 시절 내가 맡은 몫에는,\" 위크필드 씨가 백발을 가로저으며 말했다. \"후회할 거리가 참 많네 — 깊은 후회, 깊은 뉘우침이 말이야, 트롯우드, 자네도 잘 알다시피. 하지만 그걸 내 힘으로 지울 수 있다 해도, 나는 지우지 않으련다.\"\n\n그 곁에 있는 얼굴을 바라보니, 그 말이 쉬이 믿어졌다.\n\n\"그것을 지운다면,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그토록 한결같은 인내와 헌신, 그토록 변함없는 충실함, 그토록 지극한 자식의 사랑까지 함께 지우는 셈이 될 테니 말이야. 그건 잊어서는 안 되지, 안 되고말고! 내 자신을 잊는 한이 있더라도.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 알겠습니다, 어르신.\" 나는 부드럽게 말했다. \"저는 그것을 — 언제나 그것을 — 우러르고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 아무도 모른다네, 자네조차도,\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"저 애가 얼마나 많은 일을 해냈는지, 얼마나 많은 걸 견뎌 냈는지, 얼마나 모질게 애써 왔는지를 말이야. 사랑하는 애그니스!\"\n\n그 애는 그를 멈추게 하려고 애원하듯 그의 팔에 손을 얹고 있었다. 그리고 새파랗게, 너무도 새파랗게 질려 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 그가 한숨을 내쉬며 말했는데, 그제야 나는 알았다, 그가 이모에게 들은 그 일과 관련해 그 애가 이미 견뎌 냈거나 아직 견뎌야 할 어떤 시련을 떨쳐 버리고 있다는 것을. \"그래! 트롯우드, 나는 자네한테 저 애 어미 이야기를 한 적이 없지. 누가 한 적이 있던가?\"\n\n\"없습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"별것 아니야 — 비록 그 고통은 컸다만. 그 사람은 자기 아버지의 뜻을 거스르고 나와 결혼했고, 아버지는 그 사람을 내쳤다네. 그 사람은 내 애그니스가 이 세상에 나오기 전에, 아버지에게 용서를 빌었어. 그는 무척 모진 사람이었고, 그 사람의 어머니는 진작에 세상을 떠난 뒤였지. 그는 딸을 뿌리쳤어. 딸의 가슴을 찢어 놓았지.\"\n\n애그니스는 그의 어깨에 기대어, 가만히 한 팔을 그의 목에 둘렀다.\n\n\"그 사람은 정 많고 다정한 마음을 지녔는데,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 마음이 찢어진 거야. 나는 그 여린 천성을 아주 잘 알고 있었네. 내가 모른다면 아무도 알 수 없었지. 그 사람은 나를 끔찍이 사랑했지만, 한 번도 행복하지 못했어. 늘 남몰래 이 괴로움에 시달렸지. 그러다가 아버지의 그 마지막 뿌리침을 당할 무렵 — 그게 처음도 아니었어, 몇 번이나 거듭된 일이었지 — 몸이 약하고 마음이 짓눌린 채로, 시들시들 야위어 가다가 세상을 떠났다네. 그 사람은 두 주밖에 안 된 애그니스를 내게 남겼고, 자네가 처음 왔을 때 보았던 그 흰머리도 함께 남겼지.\" 그는 애그니스의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 내 자식을 향한 내 사랑은 병든 사랑이었지만, 그 무렵 내 정신은 온통 병들어 있었으니까. 그 이야긴 더 하지 않으마. 트롯우드, 나는 내 이야기를 하는 게 아니라, 저 애 어미와 저 애의 이야기를 하는 거야. 내가 어떤 사람인지, 또는 어떤 사람이었는지에 대해 자네한테 실마리 하나만 던져 주면, 자네는 그걸 풀어낼 게야, 나는 알고 있어. 애그니스가 어떤 아이인지는 굳이 말할 것도 없지. 나는 늘 저 애의 됨됨이에서 그 가엾은 어미의 사연을 어느 정도 읽어 왔다네. 그래서 이렇게 큰 변화들을 다 겪고 우리 셋이 다시 함께 모인 오늘 밤, 자네에게 그 이야기를 들려주는 걸세. 이제 다 말했네.\"\n\n수그린 그의 머리와, 그 애의 천사 같은 얼굴과 자식의 효성은, 그 이야기로 말미암아 예전에는 없던 한층 더 애틋한 의미를 띠게 되었다. 우리가 재회한 이 밤을 표시할 무언가가 내게 필요했다면, 나는 바로 이 안에서 그것을 찾았을 것이다.\n\n이윽고 애그니스는 아버지 곁에서 일어나, 가만히 자기 피아노로 가더니, 그곳에서 우리가 자주 귀 기울이곤 했던 옛 곡들을 몇 곡 연주했다.\n\n\"또 어디 멀리 떠날 생각이 있는 거야?\" 내가 곁에 서 있는데 애그니스가 물었다.\n\n\"내 누이는 그 일을 어떻게 생각하는데?\"\n\n\"안 그러길 바라.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 나도 그럴 생각은 없어, 애그니스.\"\n\n\"네가 물으니 하는 말인데, 너는 떠나지 말아야 한다고 생각해, 트롯우드.\" 그 애가 부드럽게 말했다. \"날로 높아지는 네 명성과 성공은 네가 좋은 일을 할 힘을 더 크게 해 주잖아. 그리고 설령 내가 내 오라비를 떠나보낼 수 있다 해도,\" 두 눈을 내게 둔 채로 말했다. \"아마 세상은 너를 떠나보낼 수 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"지금의 나라는 사람은 네가 만든 거야, 애그니스. 그러니 네가 제일 잘 알겠지.\"\n\n\"내가 너를 만들었다고, 트롯우드?\"\n\n\"그래! 애그니스, 사랑하는 내 누이!\" 나는 그 애에게 몸을 숙이며 말했다. \"오늘 만났을 때 너에게 하려던 말이 있어, 도라가 죽은 뒤로 줄곧 내 마음속에 자리 잡고 있던 거야. 기억하니, 네가 우리 작은 방으로 내게 내려와서 — 위를 가리키던 거, 애그니스?\"\n\n\"아, 트롯우드!\" 그 애가 두 눈에 눈물을 가득 담은 채 대답했다. \"그토록 다정하고, 그토록 믿음직스럽고, 그토록 어렸지! 내가 그걸 어떻게 잊을 수 있겠어?\"\n\n\"그때의 네 모습 그대로, 나는 그 뒤로 자주 생각해 왔는데, 너는 내게 늘 그런 존재였어. 언제나 위를 가리키며, 애그니스. 언제나 나를 더 나은 것으로 이끌며. 언제나 나를 더 높은 것으로 향하게 하며!\"\n\n그 애는 그저 고개를 가로저었다. 그 애의 눈물 너머로 나는 또다시 그 슬프고 잔잔한 미소를 보았다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 그 일이 너무 고마워, 애그니스, 너에게 너무 매여 있어서, 내 마음속 이 정에는 붙일 이름조차 없어. 나는 네가 알아주었으면 해, 어떻게 말해야 할지는 모르겠지만, 내 평생토록 나는 너를 우러를 거라는 걸, 그리고 지나간 그 어둠 속을 헤쳐 오는 동안 그래 왔듯이 너의 인도를 받을 거라는 걸 말이야. 무슨 일이 닥치든, 네가 어떤 새로운 인연을 맺든, 우리 사이에 어떤 변화가 끼어들든, 나는 지금처럼, 그리고 늘 그래 왔듯이, 언제나 너를 바라보고 너를 사랑할 거야. 너는 늘 그래 왔듯이 언제나 내 위안이자 의지처가 되어 줄 거야. 내가 죽는 날까지, 사랑하는 내 누이여, 나는 언제나 내 앞에서 위를 가리키는 너를 보게 될 거야!\"\n\n그 애는 내 손에 자기 손을 얹고는, 내가 그 애를 그 진가보다 한참 지나치게 추켜세우긴 했지만, 그래도 내가 자랑스럽고 내가 한 말이 자랑스럽다고 했다. 그러고는 나에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 다시 가만히 연주를 이어 갔다. \"있잖아, 애그니스, 오늘 밤 내가 들은 이야기 말이야,\" 내가 말했다. \"이상하게도 내가 너를 처음 보았을 때 너를 바라보던 그 느낌의 한 자락인 것 같아 — 내가 그 거친 학창 시절에 네 곁에 앉아 있던 그때의 느낌 말이야.\"\n\n\"내게 어머니가 없다는 걸 네가 알고 있었으니까,\" 그 애가 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"나한테 다정한 마음이 든 거지.\"\n\n\"그것만이 아니야, 애그니스. 나는 거의 이 사연을 알고 있기라도 했던 것처럼, 너를 둘러싼 무언가 알 수 없이 부드럽고 누그러진 기운이 있다는 걸 알았어. 다른 사람에게라면 슬픔이 되었을 무언가가 (이제 그것이 슬픔이었다는 걸 알겠지만), 너에게서는 슬픔이 아니던 그런 것 말이야.\"\n\n그 애는 여전히 나를 바라보며 가만히 연주를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"내가 이런 상상을 품고 있다고 비웃을 거니, 애그니스?\"\n\n\"아니!\"\n\n\"아니면 내가, 그때조차도 정말로 이렇게 느꼈다고 말하면 어떨까. 너라면 어떤 좌절 속에서도 변함없이 다정할 수 있고, 네 목숨이 다하는 그 순간까지 결코 그러기를 그치지 않으리라고 말이야 — 그런 꿈같은 소릴 비웃을 거니?\"\n\n\"아, 아니야! 아, 아니야!\"\n\n한순간, 괴로움이 어린 그림자가 그 애의 얼굴을 스쳤다. 그러나 그것이 내게 안긴 놀라움이 채 가시기도 전에 그림자는 사라졌고, 그 애는 다시 연주를 이어 가며 특유의 그 차분한 미소로 나를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n내가 적막한 밤길을 말을 타고 돌아오는 동안, 바람이 가만있지 못하는 기억처럼 내 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다. 나는 이 일을 곱씹으며, 그 애가 행복하지 않은 게 아닐까 두려워했다. 나도 행복하지 않았다. 그러나 여기까지, 나는 지난날에 충실히 도장을 찍어 마무리해 두었으며, 위를 가리키는 그 애를 떠올리며, 내 머리 위 저 하늘을 가리키는 모습으로 그 애를 그렸다. 다가올 그 신비 속에서, 나는 언젠가 이 땅에서는 알 수 없는 사랑으로 그 애를 사랑하고, 이곳에서 그 애를 사랑했을 때 내 안에서 어떤 싸움이 벌어졌는지를 그 애에게 말해 줄 수 있으리라.\n\n# CHAPTER 61. I AM SHOWN TWO INTERESTING PENITENTS\n\n한동안 — 어쨌든 내 책이 완성될 때까지, 그것은 여러 달이 걸릴 일이었으나 — 나는 도버에 있는 고모님 댁에 거처를 정했다. 그리고 그 지붕이 처음으로 나를 품어 주었을 때 바다 위의 달을 내다보던 바로 그 창가에 앉아, 나는 조용히 내 일을 이어 나갔다.\n\n내 소설은 그 흐름이 어쩌다 내 이야기의 진행과 맞물릴 때에만 언급하기로 한 뜻에 따라, 나는 내 예술의 포부며 환희, 불안, 승리에는 발을 들이지 않겠다. 내가 진정 온 마음을 다해 거기에 헌신했고, 내 영혼의 모든 기력을 거기에 쏟았다는 것은 이미 말했다. 내가 쓴 책들에 조금이라도 값어치가 있다면, 그 나머지는 그 책들이 채워 줄 것이다. 그렇지 않다면 나는 헛되이 쓴 셈일 테고, 그 나머지는 아무에게도 흥미를 끌지 못할 것이다.\n\n이따금 나는 런던에 갔다. 그곳 삶의 무리 속에 나를 묻으러 가거나, 아니면 어떤 사무적인 일을 트래들스와 의논하러 갔다. 그는 내가 자리를 비운 동안 더없이 건전한 판단으로 내 일을 처리해 주었고, 내 세상살이는 잘 풀려 가고 있었다. 내 이름이 알려지기 시작하면서 내가 전혀 모르는 사람들로부터 어마어마한 양의 편지가 쏟아져 들어오는데 — 거의 다 별것 아닌 일에다, 답하기는 몹시 까다로운 것들이었다 — 나는 트래들스와 합의해 그의 사무실 문에 내 이름을 써 붙이기로 했다. 거기로 그 구역을 도는 헌신적인 우편배달부가 내 앞으로 온 편지를 부셸 단위로 날라다 주었고, 거기서 나는 틈틈이, 봉급 없는 내무대신처럼 그 편지들과 씨름했다.\n\n이런 편지들 가운데 이따금 한 통씩, 민사 법원 언저리를 늘 어슬렁거리는 수많은 부외자(部外者) 가운데 하나가 보내온 친절한 제안이 끼어 있곤 했다. 내가 변호사가 되기 위해 남은 절차만 밟아 준다면 내 이름을 간판 삼아 일을 하고, 그 수익의 일정 비율을 나에게 떼어 주겠다는 것이었다. 그러나 나는 이런 제안들을 거절했다. 이미 그런 식의 그림자 영업자들이 수두룩하다는 것을 알고 있었던 데다가, 민사 법원은 내가 굳이 더 망쳐 놓지 않아도 이미 충분히 형편없다고 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n내 이름이 트래들스의 문에 활짝 피어났을 무렵, 그 집 처녀들은 이미 고향으로 돌아간 뒤였다. 약삭빠른 사환 아이는 온종일, 마치 소피라는 사람은 들어 본 적도 없다는 듯한 낯빛으로 굴었는데, 정작 소피는 뒷방에 틀어박혀 바느질을 하다가 이따금 펌프 하나가 놓인 그을음투성이 손바닥만 한 정원을 내려다보곤 했다. 하지만 나는 거기서 늘 그녀를 보았다. 한결같이 밝은 살림꾼인 그녀를. 그녀는 낯선 발소리가 계단을 올라오지 않을 때면 곧잘 데번셔 민요를 흥얼거렸고, 그 가락으로 약삭빠른 사환 아이가 제 골방에서 부려 대는 날카로운 기세를 무디게 만들었다.\n\n처음에 나는 왜 소피가 그렇게 자주 습자책에 글씨를 쓰고 있는지, 그리고 내가 나타나면 왜 늘 그것을 덮어 부랴부랴 탁자 서랍 속으로 밀어 넣는지 궁금했다. 그러나 그 비밀은 곧 드러났다. 어느 날 트래들스가 (진눈깨비를 맞으며 법정에서 막 돌아온 참이었다) 책상에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내더니, 이 필체를 어떻게 보느냐고 나에게 물었다.\n\n\"어머, 안 돼요, 톰!\" 난롯불 앞에서 그의 슬리퍼를 데우고 있던 소피가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여보,\" 톰이 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르며 받았다. \"왜 안 돼? 이 글씨 어떤가, 코퍼필드?\"\n\n\"굉장히 법률가답고 격식 있는데.\" 내가 말했다. \"이렇게 딱딱한 필체는 본 적이 없는 것 같아.\"\n\n\"여자 손글씨 같지는 않지, 그렇지?\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"여자라니!\" 나는 되받았다. \"벽돌하고 회반죽이 차라리 여자 손글씨에 더 가깝겠네!\"\n\n트래들스는 황홀하게 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 그게 소피의 글씨라고 알려 주었다. 소피가 머잖아 그에게 필사 서기가 필요해질 거라고, 그러면 자기가 그 서기가 되겠노라고 굳게 맹세하고 다짐했다는 것, 그래서 본보기를 따라 이 필체를 익혔다는 것, 그리고 시간당 — 몇 절(folio)인지는 잊었지만 — 그만큼을 척척 베껴 낼 수 있다는 것이었다. 소피는 이 모든 이야기를 내가 듣게 되자 몹시 부끄러워하면서, '톰'이 판사가 되면 이렇게 떠벌리지는 못할 거라고 말했다. 그러자 '톰'은 그것을 부인하며, 어떤 처지에 놓이든 늘 한결같이 그것을 자랑스러워할 거라고 단언했다.\n\n\"정말이지 더없이 착하고 사랑스러운 아내야, 트래들스.\" 그녀가 웃으며 자리를 뜬 뒤에 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이보게, 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"그녀는 둘도 없이, 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 여자야! 이 집을 꾸려 가는 솜씨며, 그 빈틈없음, 살림 지식, 알뜰함, 그리고 정돈하는 솜씨 하며, 그 명랑함이라니, 코퍼필드!\"\n\n\"정말이지 자네가 그녀를 칭찬할 만하군!\" 내가 받았다. \"자네는 행복한 사람이야. 내 믿기로 자네들은 스스로를, 또 서로를, 세상에서 가장 행복한 두 사람으로 만들고 있어.\"\n\n\"우리가 가장 행복한 두 사람인 건 분명해.\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"그건 어쨌든 나도 인정하네. 아이고, 이 어두컴컴한 아침마다 그녀가 촛불을 켜고 일어나 하루 일을 챙기느라 분주히 움직이고, 법학원 사무원들이 출근하기도 전에 장을 보러 나가고, 날씨 따위는 아랑곳 않고, 가장 수수한 재료로 더없이 근사한 자그마한 저녁을 궁리해 내고, 푸딩이며 파이를 만들고, 모든 것을 제자리에 두고, 늘 그렇게 단정하고 곱게 차려입고, 아무리 늦은 밤이라도 나와 함께 깨어 앉아 있고, 언제나 다정하고 북돋아 주며, 그 모든 게 다 나를 위한 것이라니, 정말이지 가끔은 믿기지가 않아, 코퍼필드!\"\n\n그는 그녀가 데워 놓았던 바로 그 슬리퍼마저 애틋하게 여기며 발을 꿰더니, 흐뭇하게 두 발을 난로 가리개 위로 쭉 뻗었다.\n\n\"정말이지 가끔은 믿기지가 않아.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"게다가 우리 낙(樂)이라니! 아이고, 돈은 거의 안 드는데 그게 어찌나 근사한지! 우리가 저녁에 이렇게 집에 있으면서 바깥문을 닫고, 저 커튼을 — 저것도 그녀가 만든 거야 — 치면, 이보다 더 아늑한 데가 어디 있겠나? 날이 좋아서 저녁에 산책하러 나가면, 거리는 우리에게 즐길 거리로 넘쳐 나. 우리는 보석상의 번쩍이는 진열창을 들여다보지. 그러면 나는 소피에게, 형편만 된다면 사 주고 싶은 다이아몬드 눈을 박은 뱀 장신구가 — 하얀 새틴 받침 위에 똬리를 튼 것들 가운데 — 어느 것인지 가리켜 보여. 그러면 소피는 나에게, 자기가 형편만 된다면 사 주고 싶은 금시계가 — 뚜껑이 달리고 보석이 박히고 무늬가 새겨진 데다 수평 레버 탈진기에 온갖 장치가 든 것들 가운데 — 어느 것인지 가리켜 보여 주지. 그러고는 둘 다 형편만 된다면 둘 다 갖고 싶은 숟가락이며 포크, 생선 뒤집개, 버터 칼, 설탕 집게를 골라. 그러다 정말로, 마치 그걸 다 손에 넣기라도 한 것처럼 흐뭇하게 자리를 떠! 그러다 광장이며 큰길로 어슬렁어슬렁 걷다가 세놓는다는 집이 보이면, 가끔 그걸 올려다보면서, 내가 판사가 된다면 저 집은 어떨까, 하고 말하지. 그러고는 그 집을 이리저리 나눠 보는 거야 — 우리 방은 이 방, 처녀들 방은 저 방, 그런 식으로 — 그러다 마침내 흡족하게 결론을 내려. 경우에 따라, 저 집이면 되겠다, 아니면 안 되겠다, 하고 말이야. 가끔은 반값에 극장 일등석 아래 평토간에 들어가는데 — 내 생각에는 그 냄새만으로도 그 돈값을 하고도 남아 — 거기서 우리는 연극을 마음껏 즐기지. 소피는 그 한마디 한마디를 다 곧이곧대로 믿고, 나도 그래. 걸어서 집에 돌아오는 길에는, 어쩌면 음식점에서 뭘 조금 사거나 생선 가게에서 작은 바닷가재를 한 마리 사 와서, 여기서 근사한 야식을 차려 놓고, 우리가 본 것을 두고 도란도란 이야기를 나누지. 자, 보게, 코퍼필드, 내가 대법관이라면 이런 건 할 수가 없잖나!\"\n\n'자네가 무엇이 되든, 트래들스, 자네는 분명 무언가 즐겁고 정겨운 일을 해낼 사람이지.' 나는 속으로 생각했다. \"그건 그렇고,\" 나는 소리 내어 말했다. \"이제 해골 그림 같은 건 안 그리겠지?\"\n\n\"실은,\" 트래들스가 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고 답했다. \"안 그린다고는 차마 못 하겠네, 코퍼필드. 일전에 왕좌 재판소 뒷줄 어디쯤에 펜을 손에 쥐고 앉아 있다가, 그 솜씨를 아직 간직하고 있는지 한번 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 불쑥 들었거든. 그래서 안됐지만, 책상 가장자리에 해골이 하나 — 가발을 쓴 채로 — 남아 있다네.\"\n\n우리 둘 다 실컷 웃고 난 뒤, 트래들스는 난롯불을 미소 띤 눈으로 바라보며, 그 너그러운 말투로 이렇게 마무리했다. \"크리클 영감!\"\n\n\"여기 그 늙은 — 악당한테서 온 편지가 있어.\" 내가 말했다. 트래들스가 그토록 선뜻 제 손으로 그를 용서하려는 것을 보니, 그가 트래들스를 그렇게 두들겨 패던 일을 용서할 마음이 그 어느 때보다도 들지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n\"교장 크리클한테서?\" 트래들스가 외쳤다. \"설마!\"\n\n\"내 명성과 재산이 일어서면서 나에게 끌려드는 사람들, 그러니까 자기들은 늘 나를 무척 아꼈노라고 새삼 발견해 내는 사람들 가운데,\" 나는 편지를 훑어보며 말했다. \"바로 그 크리클이 끼어 있어. 그는 이제 교장이 아니야, 트래들스. 은퇴했어. 미들섹스 치안판사가 됐지.\"\n\n트래들스가 그 말에 놀랄 줄 알았는데, 그는 전혀 놀라지 않았다.\n\n\"그가 어쩌다 미들섹스 치안판사가 됐다고 생각해?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아이고 이 사람아!\" 트래들스가 답했다. \"그 물음에 답하기는 아주 어렵겠는걸. 어쩌면 누군가에게 표를 줬거나, 누군가에게 돈을 빌려줬거나, 누군가한테 뭘 사 줬거나, 그게 아니면 누군가에게 신세를 갚게 해 줬거나, 누군가를 위해 뒷일을 봐줬겠지. 그 누군가가, 주(州) 부지사한테 그를 치안위원으로 천거하게 만든 또 다른 누군가를 알고 있었을 테고.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 그가 치안위원직에 있는 건 사실이야.\" 내가 말했다. \"그가 여기 나에게 편지를 써 보냈는데, 단 하나뿐인 참된 감옥 규율 체계가 어떻게 돌아가는지를 기꺼이 나에게 보여 주고 싶다는군. 진실되고 영원한 회심자와 참회자를 만들어 내는, 누구도 토를 달 수 없는 유일한 방법 말이야 — 자네도 알다시피, 그건 독방 감금이지. 어떻게 생각해?\"\n\n\"그 체계 말인가?\" 트래들스가 정색하며 물었다.\n\n\"아니. 내가 그 제안을 받아들이고, 자네가 나와 함께 가는 거 말이야.\"\n\n\"반대하지 않네.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 하겠다고 답장을 쓰지. 자네도 기억하겠지 (우리가 받은 대접은 차치하고라도) 바로 그 크리클이 제 아들을 문밖으로 내쫓던 일, 그리고 제 아내와 딸을 어떤 식으로 살게 했는지를?\"\n\n\"똑똑히 기억하지.\" 트래들스가 말했다.\n\n\"그런데도 그의 편지를 읽어 보면, 그가 온갖 중죄 목록에 다 걸려 유죄 선고를 받은 죄수들에게는 더없이 인정 많은 사람이라는 걸 알게 될 거야.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 그 인정이 다른 어떤 부류의 피조물에게까지 미치는 흔적은 도무지 찾을 수가 없더군.\"\n\n트래들스는 어깨를 으쓱했을 뿐, 조금도 놀라지 않았다. 나는 그가 놀라리라 기대하지도 않았고, 나 자신도 놀라지 않았다. 그러지 않았다면 그런 식의 실생활 풍자를 내가 그리 많이 보아 오지 못했다는 뜻이 되었으리라. 우리는 방문 시간을 잡았고, 나는 그날 저녁 그에 맞춰 크리클 씨에게 편지를 썼다.\n\n정해진 날 — 아마 그 이튿날이었던 것 같은데, 아무래도 상관없다 — 트래들스와 나는 크리클 씨가 권세를 부리는 그 감옥으로 갔다. 어마어마한 돈을 들여 지은, 거대하고 육중한 건물이었다. 정문으로 다가가면서 나는 이런 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었다. 만약 어떤 얼빠진 사람이 이 건물에 든 돈의 절반만이라도 어린이를 위한 실업 학교나 마땅히 도움받을 자격이 있는 노인을 위한 보호소를 짓는 데 쓰자고 제안했다면, 이 나라에서 얼마나 큰 소동이 일었을까.\n\n바벨탑의 일 층쯤에나 있었을 법한, 그만큼 육중하게 지어진 사무실에서, 우리는 옛 교장 앞에 안내되었다. 그는 두세 명쯤 되는 좀 더 활동적인 부류의 치안판사들과, 그들이 데려온 방문객 몇 명으로 이루어진 무리 가운데 한 사람이었다. 그는 지나간 세월 동안 내 정신을 빚어 왔고 늘 나를 애틋이 사랑해 온 사람인 양 나를 맞이했다. 내가 트래들스를 소개하자, 크리클 씨는 같은 식으로, 다만 한 등급 덜한 정도로, 자기가 늘 트래들스의 길잡이요 철학자요 벗이었노라는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 우리의 존경스러운 스승은 한참 더 늙었고, 외모는 나아진 데가 없었다. 얼굴은 여전히 시뻘겋고, 두 눈은 여전히 작은 데다 전보다 좀 더 움푹 들어가 있었다. 내가 기억하는, 그 듬성듬성하고 축축해 보이던 잿빛 머리카락은 거의 다 사라졌고, 벗어진 머리에 굵게 도드라진 핏줄들은 보기에 조금도 나아진 데가 없었다.\n\n이 신사들 사이에 오간 대화로 미루어 보자면, 이 세상에서 정당하게 따져 봐야 할 일이라고는 어떤 비용을 치르더라도 죄수들에게 더없는 안락을 베푸는 것뿐이고, 이 드넓은 땅 위에서 감옥 문 바깥에서 해야 할 일은 아무것도 없는 듯했다. 그런 대화가 오간 뒤에 우리는 시찰을 시작했다. 마침 저녁 식사 때여서, 우리는 먼저 커다란 부엌으로 들어갔다. 그곳에서는 죄수마다 (저마다 제 감방으로 가져다줄) 저녁이 따로따로, 시계 장치 같은 규칙성과 정확성으로 차려지고 있었다. 나는 트래들스에게 가만히 말했다. 이렇게 고급스러운 푸짐한 식사와, 빈민은 말할 것도 없고 군인이며 뱃사람, 막일꾼, 다시 말해 정직하게 일하는 공동체의 대다수가 먹는 식사 사이에 뚜렷한 대조가 있다는 걸 누구라도 떠올리기는 할까, 그들 가운데 이 절반만큼이라도 잘 먹는 사람은 오백 명에 하나도 없는데, 하고. 그러나 나는 그 '체계'가 잘 먹는 것을 요구한다는 것을 알게 되었다. 한마디로, 이 체계 이야기를 한 번에 끝내 두자면, 나는 이 문제에서나 다른 모든 문제에서나 '그 체계'가 모든 의심을 잠재우고 모든 모순을 처리해 버린다는 것을 알았다. 누구도 '그 체계' 말고 따져 봐야 할 다른 체계가 있다는 생각은 손톱만큼도 품고 있지 않은 듯했다.\n\n웅장한 복도 몇 군데를 지나면서, 나는 크리클 씨와 그의 벗들에게, 이 모든 것을 다스리고 무엇이든 짓누르는 이 체계의 주된 이점이 무엇이라고들 여기느냐고 물었다. 알고 보니 그것은 죄수들을 완벽히 격리하는 것 — 그래서 그곳에 갇힌 사람은 누구도 다른 사람에 대해 아무것도 알지 못하게 하는 것 — 과, 죄수들을 건전한 마음 상태로 끌어내려 진실된 통회와 참회로 이끄는 것이었다.\n\n그런데 우리가 개개인의 감방을 방문하기 시작하고, 그 감방들이 늘어선 복도를 지나며, 예배당에 가는 방식이며 그런 것들을 설명 듣게 되었을 때, 나에게는 죄수들이 서로에 대해 꽤 많은 것을 알고 있을 가능성이, 그리고 그들끼리 제법 완벽한 교류 체계를 꾸려 가고 있을 가능성이 다분해 보였다. 이 글을 쓰는 지금, 그것이 사실이었음이 입증된 것으로 나는 알고 있다. 그러나 그때 그런 의심을 조금이라도 내비치는 것은 이 체계에 대한 노골적인 모독이었을 테니, 나는 그 대신 참회를 되도록 부지런히 찾아보았다.\n\n그리고 여기서도 나는 크게 미심쩍었다. 나는 참회의 형식에서, 양복점 진열창에 걸린 외투며 조끼의 형식에서 내가 바깥에 두고 온 것만큼이나 판에 박힌 유행을 발견했다. 나는 엄청난 양의 공언(公言)을 발견했는데, 그 성격이 좀처럼 변하지 않았다. 심지어 (이것이 나로서는 몹시 수상쩍었는데) 그 말투조차 거의 변하지 않았다. 나는 손닿지 않는 곳의 포도밭을 통째로 깎아내리는 여우를 숱하게 발견했지만, 막상 한 송이가 손 닿는 자리에 있을 때 믿을 만한 여우는 거의 보지 못했다. 무엇보다도 나는, 가장 떠벌리는 자들이 가장 큰 관심의 대상이 된다는 것을 발견했다. 그리고 그들의 자만심, 허영심, 자극에 대한 갈증, 그리고 속임수를 즐기는 성향이 (그들 가운데 다수는 그 내력이 보여 주듯 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼 이런 성향을 지니고 있었다) 하나같이 이런 공언을 부추겼고, 또 그 공언으로 죄다 충족되었다.\n\n그렇긴 해도, 우리가 이리저리 오가는 동안 어떤 27번에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 거듭 들었던지 — 그가 총애받는 자였고, 정말이지 모범 죄수로 보였다 — 나는 27번을 직접 볼 때까지는 판단을 미루기로 했다. 28번도, 내가 들은 바로는, 유난히 빛나는 별이었으나, 27번의 비범한 광채에 그 영광이 다소 빛바래는 것이 그의 불운이었다. 나는 27번에 관해 어찌나 많은 이야기를 들었던지 — 그가 주위 모든 사람에게 건네는 경건한 훈계며, 그가 어머니에게 (그가 보기에 매우 위태로운 상태에 있는 듯한 어머니에게) 끊임없이 써 보낸다는 그 아름다운 편지들 이야기를 — 그를 보고 싶어 안달이 날 지경이었다.\n\n27번이 마지막을 장식할 효과로 따로 남겨져 있었던 탓에, 나는 한동안 안달을 억눌러야 했다. 그러나 마침내 우리는 그의 감방 문 앞에 이르렀다. 크리클 씨가 그 문에 난 작은 구멍으로 들여다보더니, 더없는 감탄에 휩싸여, 그가 찬송가집을 읽고 있노라고 우리에게 알렸다.\n\n27번이 찬송가집을 읽는 것을 보려고 즉각 머리들이 어찌나 우르르 몰려들었던지, 그 작은 구멍이 여섯이고 일곱이고 겹친 머리들로 막혀 버렸다. 이 불편을 덜어 주고, 우리가 27번과 그 온전한 순결함 속에서 이야기를 나눌 기회를 주려고, 크리클 씨는 감방 문을 열게 하고 27번을 복도로 나오라고 청하게 했다. 그렇게 하니 — 트래들스와 나는, 회심했다는 이 27번에게서, 놀랍게도 누구를 보았겠는가, 다름 아닌 유라이아 힙이었다!\n\n그는 곧바로 우리를 알아보았고, 옛날 그 몸을 비비 꼬는 몸짓 그대로 밖으로 나오며 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 코퍼필드 씨? 안녕하십니까, 트래들스 씨?\"\n\n이렇게 알아보는 것에 일행은 다들 감탄했다. 내가 보기에는 그가 거만을 떨지 않고 우리를 알은체했다는 데에 다들 감동한 듯했다.\n\n\"그래, 27번,\" 크리클 씨가 애잔하게 그를 우러러보며 말했다. \"오늘은 자네 상태가 어떤가?\"\n\n\"저는 아주 미천합니다, 나리!\" 유라이아 힙이 답했다.\n\n\"자네는 늘 그렇지, 27번.\" 크리클 씨가 말했다.\n\n여기서 또 다른 신사가 지극한 근심에 잠겨 물었다. \"정말 편안한가?\"\n\n\"네, 고맙습니다, 나리!\" 유라이아 힙이 그쪽을 바라보며 말했다. \"바깥에 있을 때보다 여기가 훨씬 더 편안합니다. 이제 제 어리석음이 보입니다, 나리. 그래서 제가 편안한 겁니다.\"\n\n여러 신사가 크게 감동했다. 그리고 세 번째 질문자가 앞으로 비집고 나오더니, 북받치는 감정으로 물었다. \"쇠고기는 어떤가?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 유라이아가 이 목소리가 들려온 새로운 방향을 흘끔 보며 답했다. \"어제 것은 제가 바라는 것보다 좀 질겼습니다만, 견디는 것이 제 도리지요. 저는 어리석음을 저질렀습니다, 여러분.\" 유라이아가 온순한 미소를 띠고 좌중을 둘러보며 말했다. \"그러니 불평 없이 그 대가를 치러야 마땅합니다.\" 27번의 천상에 닿은 마음 상태에 대한 흡족함과, 그에게 불평거리를 안겨 준 납품업자에 대한 분개가 (크리클 씨는 그것을 즉시 메모했다) 반반씩 섞인 웅성거림이 가라앉자, 27번은 우리 한가운데 서 있었는데, 마치 자기가 더없이 공덕 높은 박물관에서 으뜸가는 공덕의 전시물이라도 된 듯한 기색이었다. 풋내기인 우리에게 한꺼번에 넘치는 빛이 쏟아질 수 있도록, 28번을 내보내라는 명령이 떨어졌다.\n\n나는 이미 어찌나 놀란 상태였던지, 리티머 씨가 좋은 책을 읽으며 걸어 나왔을 때에도 그저 일종의 체념 어린 경이로움만 느꼈을 뿐이었다!\n\n\"28번,\" 그때까지 한마디도 하지 않았던 안경 쓴 신사가 말했다. \"여보게, 자네가 지난주에 코코아를 두고 불평했는데. 그 뒤로는 어떤가?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 리티머 씨가 말했다. \"더 잘 타게 됐습니다. 외람되이 말씀드리자면, 나리, 거기 넣고 끓이는 우유가 그리 진짜는 아닌 듯합니다만, 런던에서는 우유에 섞음질이 심하고, 순수한 상태의 물건을 구하기가 어렵다는 것을 저도 알고 있습니다, 나리.\"\n\n내가 보기에 안경 쓴 신사는 자기네 28번을 크리클 씨의 27번에 맞서 내세우는 듯했다. 둘 다 제 사람을 손수 떠맡고 있었으니 말이다.\n\n\"자네 마음 상태는 어떤가, 28번?\" 안경 쓴 질문자가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 리티머 씨가 답했다. \"이제 제 어리석음이 보입니다, 나리. 저는 옛 동무들의 죄를 떠올리면 마음이 무척 괴롭습니다, 나리. 그러나 그들도 용서받을 수 있기를 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"자네 자신은 아주 행복한가?\" 질문자가 북돋듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"황송합니다, 나리.\" 리티머 씨가 답했다. \"더없이 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"지금 마음에 걸리는 것이 조금이라도 있는가?\" 질문자가 말했다. \"있다면 말해 보게, 28번.\"\n\n\"나리,\" 리티머 씨가 고개는 들지 않은 채 말했다. \"제 눈이 저를 속인 게 아니라면, 이 자리에 제 지난 삶에서 저를 알던 신사 한 분이 계십니다. 그분이 아셔 두면 유익할 듯합니다, 나리. 제 지난 어리석음은 오로지 젊은 분들을 모시며 분별없는 삶을 산 데서, 그리고 그분들에게 이끌려, 제 힘으로는 떨쳐 내지 못한 약점에 빠져든 데서 비롯되었다는 것을요. 그 신사분이 이를 경계로 삼으시기를, 그리고 저의 거리낌 없는 말에 노여워하지 마시기를 바랍니다, 나리. 다 그분을 위한 것이니까요. 저는 제 지난 어리석음을 자각하고 있습니다. 그분도 자기가 가담했던 그 모든 사악함과 죄를 뉘우치시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n여러 신사가 저마다 한 손으로 두 눈을 가리는 것이 내 눈에 들어왔는데, 마치 방금 교회에 들어선 사람들 같았다.\n\n\"그건 자네에게 영예로운 일일세, 28번.\" 질문자가 답했다. \"자네라면 그러리라 기대했지. 그 밖에 또 있는가?\"\n\n\"나리,\" 리티머 씨가 눈썹은 살짝 치켜올렸으되 눈은 들지 않은 채 답했다. \"방탕한 길로 빠져든 어떤 젊은 여자가 있었습니다. 제가 구하려 애썼으나 끝내 건져 내지 못했지요, 나리. 그 신사분이, 혹 그럴 힘이 있으시다면, 그 젊은 여자에게 제 말을 전해 주시기를 청합니다. 저에게 보인 그 못된 행실을 제가 용서하노라고, 그리고 그녀에게 회개를 촉구한다고요 — 그분께서 그리 너그러이 해 주신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그대가 가리키는 그 신사분도,\" 질문자가 답했다. \"자네가 그토록 온당하게 한 말에 우리 모두가 그렇듯 깊이 느끼는 바가 있으리라는 데에 나는 의심이 없네, 28번. 자네를 더 붙들어 두지 않겠네.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 리티머 씨가 말했다. \"여러분, 좋은 하루 되시기를 빕니다. 그리고 여러분과 여러분의 가족도 부디 자신의 사악함을 깨닫고 고치시기를 바랍니다!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 28번은, 유라이아와 한 번 눈길을 주고받은 뒤에 물러갔다 — 마치 둘이 어떤 소통의 매개를 통해 서로 아주 모르는 사이는 아니라는 듯이. 그리고 그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 그가 더없이 훌륭한 사람이요 참으로 아름다운 사례라는 웅성거림이 무리 사이로 퍼져 나갔다.\n\n\"자, 27번,\" 크리클 씨가 자기 사람을 데리고 텅 빈 무대에 올라서듯 말했다. \"누가 자네를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이 무엇이라도 있는가? 있다면 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"미천하게나마 청하옵건대, 나리,\" 유라이아가 그 악의에 찬 머리를 홱 비틀며 답했다. \"어머니에게 다시 편지를 쓰게 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 허락하겠네.\" 크리클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리! 저는 어머니가 걱정됩니다. 어머니가 무사하지 못할까 봐 두렵습니다.\"\n\n누군가가 경솔하게 무엇으로부터 무사하지 못하냐고 물었다. 그러나 \"쉿!\" 하는, 못마땅해하는 속삭임이 일었다.\n\n\"영혼의 구원을 두고 드리는 말씀입니다, 나리.\" 유라이아가 그 목소리 쪽으로 몸을 비틀며 답했다. \"어머니도 제 상태에 이르렀으면 합니다. 제가 여기 오지 않았더라면 지금 이 상태에 결코 이르지 못했을 겁니다. 어머니도 여기 왔더라면 좋았을 텐데요. 다들 붙잡혀서 여기로 끌려오면 모두에게 더 나을 텐데요.\"\n\n이 견해는 그지없는 만족을 안겨 주었다 — 내 생각에는, 지금껏 오간 그 무엇보다도 큰 만족을.\n\n\"제가 여기 오기 전에는,\" 유라이아가, 할 수만 있다면 우리가 속한 그 바깥세상을 통째로 시들게 해 버릴 듯한 눈초리로 우리를 흘끔 훔쳐보며 말했다. \"저는 어리석음에 빠져 있었습니다. 그러나 이제 제 어리석음을 깨닫습니다. 바깥에는 죄가 가득합니다. 어머니 안에도 죄가 가득합니다. 여기 말고는 어디나 온통 죄밖에 없습니다.\"\n\n\"완전히 달라졌나?\" 크리클 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아이고, 그럼요, 나리!\" 이 가망 넘치는 참회자가 외쳤다.\n\n\"바깥으로 나간다면 다시 빠져들지는 않겠지?\" 또 다른 누군가가 물었다.\n\n\"아이고, 처-언만에요, 나리!\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 크리클 씨가 말했다. \"이거 참 흐뭇하군. 자네가 코퍼필드 씨에게 말을 건넸는데, 27번. 그에게 더 하고 싶은 말이 있는가?\"\n\n\"코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 제가 여기 와서 달라지기 한참 전부터 저를 아셨지요.\" 유라이아가 나를 바라보며 말했는데, 그토록 악랄한 눈빛은, 그의 얼굴에서조차도, 나는 본 적이 없었다. \"당신은 제가, 어리석음에도 불구하고, 거만한 자들 사이에서 미천하고 사나운 자들 사이에서 온순했던 그 시절의 저를 아셨지요 — 코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 저에게 사나우셨습니다. 한번은 제 얼굴을 후려치셨지요, 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n좌중에 동정이 일었다. 분개한 눈길 여럿이 나를 향했다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 당신을 용서합니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 유라이아가, 자기의 용서하는 천성을 더없이 불경하고 끔찍한 비유의 소재로 삼으며 말했는데, 그 비유는 여기 옮기지 않겠다. \"저는 모두를 용서합니다. 앙심을 품는 것은 저에게 어울리지 않으니까요. 저는 기꺼이 당신을 용서합니다. 그리고 앞으로는 당신이 그 격정을 다스리시기를 바랍니다. 위필드 씨도, 위필드 양도, 그 죄 많은 무리 모두도 뉘우치기를 바랍니다. 당신은 시련을 겪으셨고, 그것이 당신에게 약이 되기를 바랍니다. 하지만 차라리 여기 오셨더라면 더 나았을 겁니다. 위필드 씨도 여기 오셨더라면 더 나았을 테고, 위필드 양도 마찬가지였을 겁니다. 코퍼필드 씨, 제가 당신께, 그리고 여러분 모두께 빌어 드릴 수 있는 가장 좋은 소원은, 여러분이 붙잡혀 여기로 끌려오는 것입니다. 제 지난 어리석음과 지금의 제 상태를 떠올리면, 그것이 여러분께 가장 좋으리라는 것을 저는 확신합니다. 저는 여기 끌려오지 못한 모든 이가 가엾습니다!\"\n\n그는 자그마한 찬사의 합창 속에서 슬그머니 제 감방으로 기어 들어갔다. 그가 안에 갇히자 트래들스도 나도 크게 마음이 놓였다.\n\n이 참회에서 한 가지 특징적인 면은, 이 두 사람이 도대체 무슨 짓을 저질러 여기 와 있는지 내가 굳이 물어봐야 했다는 점이다. 그것이야말로 그들이 입에 올릴 말이 가장 없는 일인 듯했다. 나는 두 간수 가운데 한 사람에게 물어보았다. 그들의 얼굴에 어린 어떤 은밀한 기색으로 미루어, 나는 그들이 이 모든 소동이 무슨 값어치인지를 꽤 잘 알고 있으리라 짐작했다.\n\n\"혹시 아십니까,\" 우리가 복도를 따라 걸으며 내가 물었다. \"27번의 마지막 '어리석음'이 무슨 중죄였는지?\"\n\n대답인즉 은행 사건이라는 것이었다.\n\n\"잉글랜드 은행을 상대로 한 사기였습니까?\" 내가 물었다. \"그렇습니다, 나리. 사기에 위조에 공모였지요. 그자와 몇몇이서 말입니다. 그자가 나머지를 끌어들였고요. 거액을 노린 치밀한 음모였습니다. 형량은 종신 유형(流刑)이고요. 27번은 그 무리 중에서도 가장 약은 새여서, 하마터면 제 몸은 안전하게 빼낼 뻔했습니다만, 끝내 그러지는 못했지요. 은행이 아슬아슬하게 그자의 꼬리를 붙잡았거든요 — 정말 간발의 차로요.\"\n\n\"28번의 죄는 아십니까?\"\n\n\"28번은요,\" 내 정보원이 내내 낮은 목소리로 말하며, 이 흠 없는 성자들에 대한 이런 불경한 언급을 크리클 일당에게 들키지 않으려고 복도를 걷는 내내 어깨 너머를 살피며 답했다. \"28번도 (역시 유형입니다) 어느 자리에 들어가서, 외국으로 떠나기 전날 밤에 젊은 주인의 돈과 귀중품을 한 이백오십 파운드어치쯤 털었습니다. 저는 이 사건이 특히 기억에 남습니다, 그자가 난쟁이한테 붙잡혔거든요.\"\n\n\"뭐한테요?\"\n\n\"자그마한 여자한테요. 이름은 잊어버렸습니다만?\"\n\n\"마우처 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다! 그자가 추격을 따돌리고 아마빛 가발에 구레나룻까지 붙이고, 평생 본 적 없을 만큼 완벽한 변장을 하고 미국으로 가려던 참이었지요. 그런데 그 자그마한 여자가 마침 사우샘프턴에 와 있다가 길을 걸어가는 그자와 마주쳤습니다 — 그 날카로운 눈으로 단박에 그자를 가려내고 — 그자의 두 다리 사이로 뛰어들어 넘어뜨리고는 — 죽음의 신처럼 악착같이 그자를 붙들고 늘어졌지요.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 마우처 양!\" 내가 외쳤다.\n\n\"나리도 보셨더라면 그렇게 말씀하셨을 겁니다. 제가 그랬듯이, 재판에서 그 여자가 증인석 의자에 올라서 있는 걸 보셨더라면요.\" 내 벗이 말했다. \"그자는 붙잡힐 때 그 여자의 얼굴을 쫙 갈라놓고 더없이 잔인하게 두들겨 팼습니다만, 여자는 그자가 갇힐 때까지 결코 잡은 손을 놓지 않았습니다. 어찌나 단단히 붙들고 늘어졌던지, 사실 경관들이 둘을 함께 데려갈 수밖에 없었지요. 그 여자는 더없이 당찬 태도로 증언을 했고, 재판관들에게 크게 칭찬받았으며, 제 숙소까지 환호를 받으며 돌아갔습니다. 그 여자는 법정에서, 그자가 삼손이라 해도 (그자에 대해 자기가 아는 것이 있으니) 혼자서라도 붙잡았을 거라고 말했습니다. 그리고 제 믿기로는 정말 그랬을 겁니다!\"\n\n나도 같은 생각이었고, 그 점에서 나는 마우처 양을 깊이 우러르게 되었다.\n\n이제 우리는 볼 것은 다 본 셈이었다. 존경하올 크리클 씨 같은 사람에게, 27번과 28번이 완벽히 한결같고 조금도 달라지지 않았다는 것을, 그러니까 그때의 그들 모습이 곧 늘 그들의 모습이었다는 것을, 그 위선적인 악당들이야말로 이런 곳에서 그런 식의 공언을 하기에 딱 알맞은 자들이라는 것을, 그들이 그 공언의 시장 가치를 — 유형에 처해질 때 그것이 곧바로 자기들에게 가져다줄 이득을 — 우리 못지않게 잘 알고 있다는 것을, 한마디로 이 모든 것이 썩어 빠지고 속 빈, 가슴 아플 만큼 시사적인 짓거리라는 것을 일러 줘 봤자 헛수고였으리라. 우리는 그들을 그들의 체계와 그들 자신에게 내맡기고, 어리둥절한 채로 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"어쩌면 잘된 일인지도 몰라, 트래들스.\" 내가 말했다. \"엉터리 취미일수록 호되게 몰아붙이는 게 말이야. 그래야 더 빨리 죽도록 내달려 끝나 버릴 테니.\"\n\n\"그러길 바라네.\" 트래들스가 답했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 62. A LIGHT SHINES ON MY WAY\n\n내 인생이 크리스마스 무렵으로 한 바퀴 돌아왔다. 집에 돌아온 지 두 달이 넘었다. 그동안 나는 애그니스를 자주 만났다. 사람들이 아무리 큰 목소리로 나를 격려하고, 그 격려가 내 안에 아무리 뜨거운 감정과 노력을 불러일으켰다 한들, 그녀가 던지는 가장 가벼운 칭찬 한마디는 그 무엇과도 다르게 내 귀에 들어왔다.\n\n적어도 일주일에 한 번, 어떤 때는 그보다 더 자주, 나는 그곳까지 말을 타고 가서 저녁 시간을 보냈다. 보통은 밤에 말을 타고 돌아왔다. 그 오래된 서글픈 감정이 이제 늘 내 곁을 맴돌았기 때문이다. 그녀와 헤어질 때면 가장 사무치게 밀려왔다. 그래서 나는 지친 채 잠 못 이루며 지난날을 헤매거나 비참한 꿈에 시달리기보다는, 차라리 몸을 일으켜 밖으로 나서는 편이 마음 편했다. 거칠고 슬픈 숱한 밤들의 가장 긴 자락을 나는 그 말 위에서 흘려보냈다. 가는 길에, 오래 떨어져 있는 동안 나를 사로잡았던 생각들을 되살려 가면서.\n\n아니, 그 생각들의 메아리에 귀를 기울였다고 말하는 편이 진실을 더 잘 담아낼 것이다. 그 생각들은 아득히 먼 곳에서 내게 말을 건넸다. 나는 그것들을 멀찍이 밀어 두고, 내게 주어진 피할 수 없는 자리를 받아들였다. 내가 쓴 글을 애그니스에게 읽어 줄 때, 귀 기울이는 그녀의 얼굴을 바라볼 때, 그녀를 웃기거나 울릴 때, 그리고 내가 살아가는 그 상상의 세계에서 벌어지는 어슴푸레한 사건들을 두고 그녀가 어찌나 진지하게 따뜻한 목소리로 말하는지 들을 때면, 내 운명이 어떠했을 수도 있었을까 하고 생각했다. 그러나 그저 생각만 했을 뿐이다. 도라와 결혼한 뒤에, 내 아내가 어떤 사람이었으면 좋았을까 하고 생각만 했던 것처럼.\n\n애그니스를 향한 내 도리, 그녀는 그토록 깊은 사랑으로 나를 사랑했는데, 만약 내가 그 마음을 어지럽힌다면 그것은 더없이 이기적이고 옹졸하게 그녀를 짓밟는 일이 될 테고, 그 상처는 결코 되돌릴 수 없으리라. 내 운명은 내 손으로 빚어 낸 것이고, 한때 앞뒤 없이 마음을 쏟아 손에 넣은 것을 얻은 처지이니, 나에게는 투덜댈 권리가 없으며 견뎌야 한다는 무르익은 확신, 이것이 내가 느낀 것이자 배운 것의 전부였다. 그러나 나는 그녀를 사랑했다. 그리고 이제는 언젠가 먼 훗날, 떳떳하게 그 사랑을 고백할 수 있는 날을 어렴풋이 그려 보는 것조차 내게 얼마쯤 위안이 되었다. 이 모든 일이 다 지나가고, 내가 이렇게 말할 수 있는 날을. \"애그니스, 내가 집에 돌아왔을 때 이미 이랬소. 그리고 이제 나는 늙었고, 그 후로 단 한 번도 다른 사람을 사랑한 적이 없소!\"\n\n그녀는 단 한 번도 자신이 달라졌다는 기색을 내게 보이지 않았다. 늘 내게 그러했던 모습 그대로, 그녀는 여전히 그러했다. 조금도 변하지 않은 채로.\n\n내가 돌아온 그날 밤 이후로, 이 일과 관련해서 고모님과 나 사이에는 무언가가 있었다. 그것을 무슨 거리낌이라거나 화제를 피하는 것이라고 부르기보다는, 우리가 그 일을 함께 떠올리되 그 생각을 말로 빚어내지는 않는다는 무언의 양해라고 하는 편이 맞겠다. 우리는 옛 버릇대로 밤마다 난롯가에 앉았는데, 그럴 때면 종종 그 흐름에 빠져들곤 했다. 마치 거리낌 없이 입 밖에 낸 것처럼, 자연스럽게, 그리고 서로가 또렷이 알아차리면서. 그러나 우리는 깨지지 않는 침묵을 지켰다. 나는 믿었다. 그녀가 그날 밤 내 생각을 읽었거나 어느 정도 읽었으리라고, 그리고 내가 왜 내 생각을 더 또렷이 드러내지 않는지를 그녀가 온전히 헤아리고 있으리라고.\n\n이렇게 크리스마스 무렵이 다가왔는데도 애그니스가 새로이 내게 속내를 털어놓지 않자, 여러 번 내 마음속에 떠올랐던 의심 하나가 무겁게 나를 짓누르기 시작했다. 그녀가 내 가슴속 참모습을 꿰뚫어 보고 있어서, 내게 고통을 줄까 봐 두려운 마음에 스스로를 억누르고 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 의심이었다. 만약 그렇다면, 내 희생은 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀에 대한 가장 명백한 내 도리를 다하지 못한 셈이고, 내가 움츠러들며 피해 온 그 옹졸한 짓거리를 시시각각 저지르고 있는 셈이었다. 나는 이 일을 한 점 의심도 없이 바로잡기로 결심했다. 우리 사이에 그런 장벽이 가로놓여 있다면, 단호한 손으로 당장 그것을 허물어 버리기로.\n\n그날은, 내가 이 일을 두고두고 기억할 까닭이 얼마나 깊은지! 춥고 매서운 겨울날이었다. 몇 시간 전에 눈이 내렸고, 깊이 쌓이지는 않았으나 땅 위에 꽝꽝 얼어붙어 있었다. 창밖 저 멀리 바다 위로는 북녘에서 바람이 거칠게 불어왔다. 나는 그 바람이 스위스의 저 황량한 설산 위를, 그 무렵에는 사람의 발길이 닿을 수 없는 그곳을 휩쓸어 가는 모습을 떠올리고 있었다. 그리고 어느 쪽이 더 외로울까 곰곰이 따져 보고 있었다. 그 호젓한 산자락일까, 아니면 인적 없는 바다일까.\n\n\"오늘도 말 타고 가니, 트롯?\" 고모님이 문틈으로 고개를 들이밀며 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"캔터베리에 갈 거예요. 말 타기 좋은 날이네요.\"\n\n\"네 말도 그렇게 생각하면 좋으련만.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"지금은 저기 문 앞에 서서 고개랑 귀를 푹 떨구고 있는 게, 제 마구간이 더 낫다고 여기는 모양이던데.\"\n\n덧붙이자면, 고모님은 내 말이 그 금지된 땅에 들어서는 것은 봐주었으나, 당나귀들에 대해서만큼은 조금도 누그러지지 않았다.\n\n\"곧 기운이 펄펄 날 거예요!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"어쨌거나 그 주인한테는 말 타는 게 약이 되겠지.\" 고모님이 내 책상 위의 종이들을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"아이고, 얘야, 너 여기서 참 많은 시간을 보내는구나! 내가 한창 책을 읽던 시절에는, 그걸 쓰는 게 이리 고된 일인 줄은 미처 몰랐다.\"\n\n\"읽는 것도 어떤 때는 어지간히 고된 일인걸요.\" 내가 받았다. \"쓰는 거로 말하자면, 그 나름의 매력이 있어요, 고모님.\"\n\n\"아하! 알겠다!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"야망에다, 인정받고 싶은 마음에다, 공감에다, 뭐 그런 것들이 한참 더 있겠지? 그래, 어서 가 보거라!\"\n\n\"혹시 더 아시는 게 있으세요.\" 내가 그녀 앞에 차분히 서서 말했다. 고모님은 내 어깨를 토닥이고는 내 의자에 앉아 있었다. \"애그니스의 그 사람 얘기 말이에요.\"\n\n고모님은 대답하기에 앞서 잠시 내 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"있는 것 같다, 트롯.\"\n\n\"짐작하신 게 굳어지셨어요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그런 것 같구나, 트롯.\"\n\n고모님은 어찌나 한결같이 나를 바라보던지, 그 애정 속에 어쩐지 의구심인지 연민인지 조마조마함 같은 것이 어려 있어서, 나는 그녀에게 더없이 환한 얼굴을 보여 주려고 더 굳은 결심을 끌어모았다.\n\n\"게다가 말이다, 트롯—\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n\"네!\"\n\n\"애그니스가 곧 결혼할 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"하느님의 축복이 그 애에게 깃들기를!\" 내가 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"그 애에게 축복을!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그리고 그 신랑에게도!\"\n\n나는 그 말을 따라 외고는, 고모님과 작별하고 가벼운 걸음으로 아래층에 내려와 말에 올라 떠났다. 내가 마음먹은 일을 해야 할 까닭이 전보다 더 커진 것이다.\n\n그 겨울날의 말타기를 나는 어찌 이리 또렷이 기억하는지! 바람에 풀잎에서 떨려 나와 내 얼굴을 가로질러 날아드는 얼음 알갱이들, 땅 위에 가락을 두드리는 말발굽의 딱딱한 또각또각 소리, 빳빳하게 갈아엎은 흙, 산들바람이 휘저을 때마다 백악 채굴터에서 가볍게 소용돌이치는 눈더미, 마른 묵은 풀을 실은 짐마차를 끌고 언덕 꼭대기에서 숨을 고르며 방울을 가락 맞춰 흔들어 대던 김 모락모락 나는 말 떼, 그리고 거대한 석판 위에 그려 놓기라도 한 듯 어두운 하늘을 등지고 누운 다운랜드의 허옇게 덮인 비탈과 굽이들!\n\n애그니스는 혼자 있었다. 어린 여자아이들은 이제 다들 제 집으로 돌아갔고, 그녀는 난롯가에 홀로 앉아 책을 읽고 있었다. 내가 들어서는 것을 보자 그녀는 책을 내려놓았다. 그러고는 여느 때처럼 나를 맞이한 뒤, 반짇고리를 들고 와 그 고풍스러운 창문 가운데 하나에 앉았다.\n\n나는 창가 자리에 그녀 곁에 앉았고, 우리는 내가 지금 하고 있는 일이며, 그게 언제쯤 끝날지며, 지난번에 들른 뒤로 내가 얼마나 진척을 보았는지를 이야기했다. 애그니스는 무척 명랑했다. 그러더니 웃으며, 내가 곧 너무 유명해져서 이런 이야기는 나누기 어려워질 거라고 점쳤다.\n\n\"그러니까 보다시피 난 지금 이 순간을 한껏 누리는 거예요.\" 애그니스가 말했다. \"그럴 수 있을 때 당신이랑 이야기를 나누면서요.\"\n\n내가 그녀의 아름다운 얼굴을 바라보며 그녀가 일감에 정신을 쏟는 모습을 지켜보고 있는데, 그녀가 그 부드럽고 맑은 눈을 들어 올리더니, 내가 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"오늘 무슨 생각에 잠겨 있군요, 트롯우드!\"\n\n\"애그니스, 무슨 생각인지 말해 줄까요? 그 얘기를 하려고 왔어요.\"\n\n그녀는 우리가 무언가 진지하게 의논할 때면 늘 그러했듯이 일감을 옆으로 치워 놓고, 온 마음을 다해 내게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"사랑하는 애그니스, 내가 당신에게 진실하다는 걸 의심하나요?\"\n\n\"아니요!\" 그녀가 놀란 눈빛으로 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 당신에게 늘 그랬던 그 모습 그대로라는 걸 의심하나요?\"\n\n\"아니요!\" 그녀가 아까처럼 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 집에 돌아왔을 때, 당신에게 진 그 감사의 빚이 얼마나 깊은지, 그리고 당신을 향한 내 마음이 얼마나 뜨거운지 말하려 애썼던 거, 기억해요, 더없이 소중한 애그니스?\"\n\n\"기억해요.\" 그녀가 부드럽게 말했다. \"아주 잘 기억해요.\"\n\n\"당신에겐 비밀이 하나 있죠.\" 내가 말했다. \"그 비밀을 나도 나누게 해 줘요, 애그니스.\"\n\n그녀는 눈을 내리깔며 떨었다.\n\n\"내가 알아채지 못할 리가 없었어요. 설령 듣지 못했더라도요. 그런데 당신 입이 아닌 다른 사람의 입으로 들었다는 게 이상하긴 하지만요, 애그니스, 당신이 사랑이라는 보물을 바친 누군가가 있다는 걸요. 당신의 행복과 그토록 깊이 맞닿은 일에서 나를 밀어내지 말아요! 당신이 말한 대로, 그리고 내가 알기에도 그래도 되는 대로, 정말 나를 믿어 준다면, 다른 무엇보다 바로 이 일에서, 내가 당신의 벗이 되고 오라비가 되게 해 줘요!\"\n\n호소하는 듯한, 거의 나무라는 듯한 눈빛으로 그녀는 창가에서 일어섰다. 그러고는 어디로 가는지도 모르는 사람처럼 황급히 방을 가로지르더니, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고, 내 가슴을 후벼 파는 그런 눈물을 터뜨렸다.\n\n그런데도 그 눈물은 내 안의 무언가를 깨워, 내 가슴에 어떤 약속을 가져다주었다. 왜인지도 모른 채, 그 눈물은 내 기억 속에 그토록 또렷이 박혀 있는 그 조용하고 서글픈 미소와 한데 어우러졌고, 두려움이나 슬픔보다는 희망으로 나를 더 크게 뒤흔들었다.\n\n\"애그니스! 누이! 더없이 소중한 사람! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거죠?\"\n\n\"나가게 해 줘요, 트롯우드. 몸이 좀 안 좋아요. 제정신이 아니에요. 나중에 얘기할게요. 다른 때에요. 편지로 쓸게요. 지금은 내게 말 걸지 말아요. 제발! 제발!\"\n\n나는 지난날 그 밤에 그녀에게 말을 걸었을 때, 자기 사랑은 보답을 바라지 않는다고 그녀가 했던 말을 떠올려 보려 했다. 한순간에 샅샅이 뒤져야 할 세상이 통째로 펼쳐진 것만 같았다. \"애그니스, 당신이 이러는 걸 차마 볼 수가 없어요. 더구나 그게 나 때문이라고 생각하니. 더없이 소중한 사람, 내게 이 세상 그 무엇보다 소중한 사람, 당신이 불행하다면 그 불행을 나도 나누게 해 줘요. 당신에게 도움이나 조언이 필요하다면, 내가 그걸 주도록 애쓰게 해 줘요. 정말 당신 가슴에 무거운 짐이 있다면, 내가 그걸 덜어 주도록 애쓰게 해 줘요. 이제 내가 누구를 위해 사나요, 애그니스, 당신을 위해서가 아니라면!\"\n\n\"오, 나를 좀 봐줘요! 제정신이 아니에요! 다른 때에요!\" 내가 알아들을 수 있는 말은 그게 전부였다.\n\n나를 잘못된 길로 이끄는 것은 이기적인 착각일까? 아니면 한번 희망의 실마리를 붙잡고 나니, 감히 떠올려 본 적도 없던 무언가가 내게 열리고 있는 것일까?\n\n\"더 말해야겠어요. 당신을 이렇게 그냥 보낼 수는 없어요! 제발, 애그니스, 이 숱한 세월이 흐른 끝에, 그 세월과 함께 오고 간 그 모든 일을 겪은 끝에, 우리 서로를 오해하지는 맙시다! 솔직히 말해야겠어요. 혹시라도 당신 마음에, 당신이 베풀 그 행복을 내가 시샘하리라는 생각이, 당신 스스로 고른 더 소중한 보호자에게 당신을 내어주지 못하리라는 생각이, 한발 물러선 내 자리에서 당신의 기쁨을 흐뭇하게 지켜보지 못하리라는 생각이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 떨쳐 버려요. 난 그런 의심을 받을 사람이 아니니까요! 나는 그저 헛되이 괴로워한 게 아니에요. 당신도 그저 헛되이 나를 가르친 게 아니에요. 당신을 향한 내 마음에는 사사로운 이기심이 한 점도 섞여 있지 않아요.\"\n\n이제 그녀는 잠잠해졌다. 잠시 뒤, 그녀는 창백한 얼굴을 내게로 돌리더니, 군데군데 끊기긴 했으나 아주 또렷한 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"나를 향한 당신의 순수한 우정에 대한 도리로 말하는 거예요, 트롯우드. 그 우정은, 정말이지, 나도 의심하지 않아요. 당신이 잘못 알고 있다고요. 그 이상은 말할 수 없어요. 세월이 흐르는 동안 더러 도움과 조언이 필요했을 때, 그것들은 내게 찾아와 주었어요. 더러 불행했을 때, 그 감정은 지나가 버렸어요. 가슴에 무거운 짐을 진 적이 있다 해도, 누군가 그걸 덜어 주었어요. 내게 비밀이 있다면, 그건 새로운 게 아니에요. 그리고 당신이 짐작하는 그런 것도 아니에요. 나는 그걸 드러낼 수도, 나눌 수도 없어요. 그건 오랫동안 내 것이었고, 앞으로도 내 것으로 남아야 해요.\"\n\n\"애그니스! 가지 말아요! 잠깐만!\"\n\n그녀가 떠나려 했으나 나는 그녀를 붙들었다. 나는 한 팔로 그녀의 허리를 감았다. '세월이 흐르는 동안!' '그건 새로운 게 아니에요!' 새로운 생각과 희망이 내 마음속을 소용돌이쳤고, 내 인생의 온갖 빛깔이 바뀌어 가고 있었다.\n\n\"더없이 소중한 애그니스! 내가 그토록 우러르고 떠받드는 사람, 그토록 한결같이 사랑하는 사람! 오늘 이곳에 올 때만 해도, 나는 그 무엇도 이 고백을 내게서 끄집어낼 수 없으리라 여겼어요. 우리가 늙을 때까지, 평생을 이 마음을 가슴속에만 묻어 둘 수 있으리라 여겼어요. 하지만, 애그니스, 만약 내게 정말로, 언젠가 당신을 누이보다 더한, 누이와는 한참 다른 무언가로 부를 수 있으리라는 갓 태어난 희망이 조금이라도 있다면—\"\n\n그녀의 눈물이 후두두 떨어졌다. 그러나 그것은 조금 전에 흘리던 눈물과는 달랐고, 나는 그 눈물 속에서 내 희망이 환히 밝아 오는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"애그니스! 늘 나를 이끌어 준 길잡이이자 더없이 든든한 버팀목! 우리가 여기서 함께 자라던 시절에, 당신이 나를 덜 챙기고 자신을 좀 더 챙겼더라면, 내 그 철없는 마음은 결코 당신에게서 떠돌지 않았을 거예요. 하지만 당신은 나보다 너무도 나은 사람이었고, 소년 시절의 온갖 희망과 좌절 속에서 내게 너무도 없어서는 안 될 사람이어서, 당신을 의지하고 매사에 당신에게 기대는 일이 어느새 제2의 천성이 되어 버렸어요. 한동안은 지금 내가 당신을 사랑하는 그 첫째가는, 더 큰 천성을 밀어내 버릴 만큼요!\"\n\n여전히 울고 있었으나, 슬픈 울음이 아니었다. 기쁨의 눈물이었다! 그리고 내 품에 안겨 있었다. 한 번도 안겨 본 적 없던 모습으로, 결코 안길 일이 없으리라 여겼던 모습으로!\n\n\"내가 도라를 사랑했을 때, 애그니스, 당신도 알다시피 애틋하게 사랑했을 때—\"\n\n\"네!\" 그녀가 간절하게 외쳤다. \"그걸 알게 되어 기뻐요!\"\n\n\"내가 그녀를 사랑했을 때, 그때조차도, 당신의 공감이 없었다면 내 사랑은 온전치 못했을 거예요. 나는 그 공감을 받았고, 그래서 내 사랑은 완전해졌어요. 그리고 그녀를 잃었을 때, 애그니스, 당신이 없었다면 나는 또 어찌 되었을까요!\"\n\n내 품에 더 바싹, 내 가슴에 더 가까이, 그녀의 떨리는 손이 내 어깨에 얹히고, 눈물 너머로 빛나는 그 사랑스러운 눈이 내 눈을 마주했다!\n\n\"나는 당신을 사랑하며 떠났어요, 사랑하는 애그니스. 당신을 사랑하며 멀리 머물렀어요. 당신을 사랑하며 집으로 돌아왔어요!\"\n\n그리고 이제 나는 내가 겪어 온 갈등과, 내가 다다른 결론을 그녀에게 들려주려 애썼다. 내 마음을 그녀 앞에 참되게, 그리고 송두리째 펼쳐 보이려 애썼다. 내가 어떻게 나 자신과 그녀를 더 잘 알게 되었기를 바랐는지, 그 더 깊은 깨달음이 가져다준 것에 어떻게 스스로를 내맡겼는지, 그리고 바로 그날 이곳에 올 때조차 그 깨달음에 충실하고자 어떻게 왔는지를 보여 주려 애썼다. 만약 그녀가 정말로 나를 사랑하여 나를 남편으로 맞이할 수 있다면(내가 말했다), 그것은 내가 무슨 자격이 있어서가 아니라, 오직 그녀를 향한 내 사랑의 진실함과, 그 사랑이 지금의 모습으로 무르익기까지 겪은 시련 덕분일 뿐이며, 바로 그래서 내가 이 마음을 드러내는 것이라고. 그리고 오, 애그니스, 그 순간 바로 그대의 진실한 눈 속에서도, 내 아이 같던 아내의 영혼이 나를 굽어보며 잘했다고 말하고 있었으니, 그대를 통하여, 채 피어 보지도 못한 채 시들어 버린 그 꽃송이에 대한 더없이 애틋한 추억으로 나를 이끌고 있었다!\n\n\"나는 너무나 복받은 사람이에요, 트롯우드. 가슴이 벅차서 터질 것만 같아요. 그런데 꼭 해야 할 말이 하나 있어요.\"\n\n\"더없이 소중한 사람, 무슨 말인가요?\"\n그녀는 그 부드러운 두 손을 내 어깨에 얹고, 차분히 내 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 말인지, 이제 알겠어요?\"\n\n\"무슨 말일지 감히 짐작이 안 돼요. 말해 줘요, 사랑하는 사람.\"\n\n\"나는 평생토록 당신을 사랑해 왔어요!\"\n\n오, 우리는 행복했다, 우리는 행복했다! 우리의 눈물은 우리가 이렇게 되기까지 헤쳐 온 시련(그녀의 시련이 훨씬 더 컸다) 때문이 아니라, 이렇게 되어, 다시는 갈라서지 않으리라는 그 황홀함 때문이었다!\n\n그 겨울 저녁, 우리는 들판을 함께 거닐었다. 우리 안에 깃든 그 복된 고요를 서리 어린 공기마저 함께 나누는 것만 같았다. 우리가 그렇게 거니는 동안 초저녁 별들이 빛나기 시작했고, 그 별들을 올려다보며 우리는 이 평온함으로 우리를 이끌어 주신 하느님께 감사를 드렸다.\n\n밤이 되어 달이 빛날 때, 우리는 그 고풍스러운 옛 창가에 함께 섰다. 애그니스는 그 고요한 눈을 들어 달을 올려다보았고, 나는 그녀의 시선을 따라갔다. 그때 내 마음속에는 아득히 먼 길이 펼쳐졌다. 그 길을 힘겹게 걸어가는, 누덕누덕 해진 옷에 여독에 찌든 한 소년이 보였다. 버림받고 내팽개쳐진 그 소년이, 언젠가 지금 내 가슴에 맞닿아 뛰고 있는 이 심장마저 제 것이라 부르게 될 터였다.\n\n이튿날 우리가 고모님 앞에 나타났을 때는 거의 저녁 먹을 무렵이었다. 고모님은 위층 내 서재에 있다고 패거티가 말했다. 그 서재를 나를 위해 늘 준비해 두고 가지런히 정돈해 두는 것이 패거티의 자랑거리였다. 우리가 가 보니 고모님은 안경을 쓴 채 난롯가에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"아이고 세상에!\" 고모님이 어스름 속을 들여다보며 말했다. \"집에 데려온 이 사람은 누구냐?\"\n\n\"애그니스예요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n처음에는 아무 말도 하지 않기로 미리 짜 둔 터라, 고모님은 적잖이 당황했다. 내가 \"애그니스\"라고 말하자 고모님은 내게 기대 어린 눈길을 휙 던졌다. 그러나 내가 여느 때와 다름없어 보이자, 절망한 듯 안경을 벗어 그것으로 코를 문질렀다.\n\n그래도 고모님은 애그니스를 정답게 맞이했다. 우리는 곧 아래층의 불 켜진 거실에서 저녁을 들었다. 고모님은 나를 다시 한번 살펴보려고 두세 번 안경을 썼다가, 그때마다 실망한 듯 다시 벗어 그것으로 코를 문질렀다. 이를 좋지 않은 징조로 여기던 딕 씨가 적잖이 마음을 졸였다.\n\n\"참, 그런데 고모님.\" 저녁을 들고 나서 내가 말했다. \"고모님이 말씀해 주신 그 얘기를 애그니스한테 했어요.\"\n\n\"그렇담, 트롯.\" 고모님이 새빨개지며 말했다. \"넌 잘못한 거다. 약속을 어겼어.\"\n\n\"화내신 건 아니죠, 고모님? 안 그러실 거예요. 애그니스가 그 어떤 사람을 향한 마음 때문에 불행한 게 아니라는 걸 아시면요.\"\n\n\"무슨 헛소리, 말도 안 되는 소리!\" 고모님이 말했다.\n\n고모님이 짜증 난 듯 보여서, 나는 그 짜증을 단번에 잘라 버리는 게 상책이겠다 싶었다. 나는 애그니스를 한 팔로 안아 고모님 의자 뒤로 데려갔고, 우리 둘은 함께 고모님 위로 몸을 숙였다. 고모님은 두 손을 한 번 탁 마주치고 안경 너머로 한 번 바라보더니, 그 자리에서 발작하듯 까무러쳤다. 내가 아는 한 처음이자 단 한 번뿐인 일이었다.\n\n그 발작에 패거티가 달려왔다. 고모님은 정신을 차리자마자 패거티에게 달려들어, 이 어리석은 늙은이라고 부르면서 있는 힘껏 끌어안았다. 그러고 나서 딕 씨를 끌어안았는데(딕 씨는 무척 영광스러워하면서도 적잖이 놀랐다), 그다음에야 두 사람에게 까닭을 일러 주었다. 그리하여 우리는 다 함께 행복했다.\n\n지난번 나와 짧게 나눈 그 대화에서 고모님이 갸륵한 거짓말을 둘러댄 것인지, 아니면 정말로 내 마음 상태를 잘못 알았던 것인지는 끝내 알아낼 수 없었다. 애그니스가 곧 결혼할 거라고 내게 말해 준 것이면 충분하다고 고모님은 말했다. 그리고 그게 얼마나 들어맞는 말이었는지는 이제 내가 누구보다 잘 알지 않느냐고.\n\n우리는 두 주가 채 못 되어 결혼했다. 트래들스와 소피, 그리고 스트롱 박사 부부가 우리의 조촐한 결혼식에 온 손님의 전부였다. 우리는 그들을 기쁨에 겨운 채로 두고, 함께 마차를 타고 떠났다. 내 품에 꼭 안긴 채로, 나는 내가 품어 온 모든 값진 열망의 샘을 안고 있었다. 나 자신의 중심이요, 내 인생의 둘레요, 내 사람이요, 내 아내인 그녀를. 그녀를 향한 내 사랑은 반석 위에 세워진 것이었다!\n\n\"더없이 소중한 남편!\" 애그니스가 말했다. \"이제 당신을 그 이름으로 부를 수 있게 되었으니, 한 가지 더 말할 게 있어요.\"\n\n\"들려줘요, 사랑하는 사람.\"\n\n\"도라가 세상을 떠나던 그 밤에서 비롯된 거예요. 그녀가 나를 데려오라고 당신을 보냈잖아요.\"\n\n\"그랬죠.\"\n\n\"그녀가 내게 무언가를 남긴다고 말했어요. 그게 뭐였을지 짐작이 가요?\"\n\n나는 짐작할 수 있을 것 같았다. 나는 그토록 오래 나를 사랑해 온 아내를 내 곁으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"그녀가 내게 마지막 부탁을 하고, 마지막 당부를 남긴다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"그게—\"\n\n\"오직 나만이 이 빈자리를 채워 달라는 거였어요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 애그니스는 내 가슴에 머리를 묻고 울었다. 우리는 그토록 행복했건만, 나도 그녀와 함께 울었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 63. A VISITOR\n\n내가 적어 두기로 마음먹은 이야기는 거의 다 끝나 간다. 그러나 아직 내 기억 속에 또렷이 남아, 떠올릴 때마다 즐거운 마음으로 머무르게 되는 한 사건이 남아 있다. 이것이 없다면 내가 짜 온 이야기의 그물에서 한 가닥 실이 풀린 채 끝을 맺게 될 것이다.\n\n나는 명성과 재산에서 앞으로 나아갔고, 가정의 기쁨은 더없이 완전했으며, 행복한 결혼 생활을 십 년이나 누렸다. 어느 봄날 밤, 애그니스와 나는 런던 우리 집 난롯가에 앉아 있었고, 우리 아이들 셋이 방 안에서 놀고 있었는데, 그때 누군가 낯선 사람이 나를 만나고 싶어 한다는 전갈이 왔다.\n\n볼일이 있어 온 것이냐고 물었더니 아니라고 대답했단다. 그저 나를 보는 즐거움 때문에 왔으며, 먼 길을 왔다고 했다. 하인의 말로는, 나이 든 노인인데 농부처럼 보인다고 했다.\n\n이 말이 아이들에게는 어쩐지 알쏭달쏭하게 들린 데다, 애그니스가 늘 들려주던, 모두를 미워하는 망토 두른 심술궂은 늙은 요정이 등장하는 이야기의 첫머리와 비슷했던 터라, 작은 소동이 일었다. 우리 아들 하나는 화를 피하려는 듯 어머니 무릎에 머리를 파묻었고, 어린 애그니스(우리 맏이다)는 제 인형을 의자에 앉혀 자기 대신 세워 두고는, 다음에 무슨 일이 일어날지 보려고 황금빛 곱슬머리 뭉치를 창문 커튼 사이로 쑥 내밀었다.\n\n\"이리로 모셔 와라!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n이윽고 들어서면서 어두운 문간에 잠시 멈춰 선, 정정하고 머리가 희끗희끗한 노인이 나타났다. 어린 애그니스가 그 모습에 끌려 그를 모셔 오려고 달려갔는데, 내가 미처 그의 얼굴을 또렷이 보기도 전에, 아내가 벌떡 일어나 기쁘면서도 들뜬 목소리로 외쳤다. 패거티 아저씨라고!\n\n정말 패거티 아저씨였다. 이제는 노인이 되었으나, 혈색 좋고 다부지며 정정한 노년이었다. 처음의 벅찬 감정이 가라앉고, 그가 아이들을 무릎에 앉힌 채 난롯불을 받으며 앉았을 때, 내 눈에 비친 그는 내가 여태껏 본 그 어떤 노인보다도 기운차고 튼튼했으며, 게다가 멋졌다.\n\n\"데이비 도련님.\" 그가 말했다. 옛 이름이 옛 말투에 실려 어찌나 자연스럽게 내 귀에 와닿던지! \"데이비 도련님, 이렇게 다시 도련님을, 그것도 도련님의 어여쁜 마님과 함께 뵈니 참으로 기쁜 순간입니다요!\"\n\n\"정말로 기쁜 순간이오, 오랜 벗이여!\" 내가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 여기 이 예쁜 것들 좀 보십쇼.\" 패거티 아저씨가 말했다. \"여기 이 꽃송이들을 좀 보세요! 아니, 데이비 도련님, 제가 처음 도련님을 뵈었을 때 도련님은 여기 이 제일 작은 아이만 한 키였잖습니까! 에밀리도 그만큼밖에 안 컸고, 우리 가엾은 그 녀석도 한낱 어린애였을 때 말입니다요!\"\n\n\"그때 이후로 세월은 아저씨보다 나를 더 많이 바꿔 놓았소.\" 내가 말했다. \"그건 그렇고, 이 사랑스러운 개구쟁이들은 어서 재웁시다. 그리고 잉글랜드에서 이 집 말고는 어디서도 아저씨를 묵게 할 수 없으니, 짐을 어디로 가지러 보내면 될지 말해 주시오(그 먼 길을 함께 다닌 낡은 검정 가방도 그중에 있는지 궁금하구려!). 그런 다음 야머스식 그로그주 한잔 기울이면서 십 년 치 소식을 들어 봅시다!\"\n\n\"혼자 오셨어요?\" 애그니스가 물었다.\n\n\"예, 마님.\" 그가 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"아주 혼자입니다요.\"\n\n우리는 그를 어떻게 맞이해야 흡족할지 몰라 둘 사이에 앉혔다. 그 정겹고 익숙한 목소리에 다시 귀 기울이기 시작하자, 나는 그가 아직도 사랑하는 조카딸을 찾아 머나먼 여정을 헤매고 있는 것만 같은 착각이 들었다.\n\n\"건너오기엔 어마어마한 물길이지요.\" 패거티 아저씨가 말했다. \"그것도 고작 사 주쯤 머물려고 말입니다. 하지만 물이란(특히 짠물은) 저한테는 천성처럼 익숙한 거고, 벗은 소중하니, 그래서 제가 여기 온 겁니다요. ―이거 운율이 맞네요.\" 패거티 아저씨는 스스로 그걸 깨닫고 놀라며 말했다. \"그럴 작정은 아니었는데도 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그 수천 마일 길을 그렇게 빨리 되돌아가신다고요?\" 애그니스가 물었다.\n\n\"예, 마님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"떠나오기 전에 에밀리한테 약속했거든요. 보세요, 해가 거듭될수록 저도 젊어지는 건 아니잖습니까. 그러니 그때 배를 타지 않았더라면 아마 영영 못 했을 겁니다. 그리고 늘 마음에 걸렸던 게, 제가 너무 늙어 버리기 전에 데이비 도련님과 마님의 어여쁘고 활짝 피어난 모습을, 그 결혼의 행복 속에서 한번 뵈러 와야겠다는 거였지요.\"\n\n그는 우리를 바라보았는데, 아무리 봐도 그 눈이 우리를 다 담지 못해 못내 아쉬운 듯했다. 애그니스가 웃으며 그가 우리를 더 잘 볼 수 있도록 흐트러진 희끗한 머리칼 몇 가닥을 쓸어 넘겨 주었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 들려주세요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그동안 어떻게 지내셨는지 죄다요.\"\n\n\"저희가 어떻게 지냈는지는 말입니다, 데이비 도련님,\" 그가 받았다. \"금방 이야기됩니다. 저흰 그럭저럭이 아니라, 잘됐어요. 늘 번창했지요. 마땅히 일해야 할 만큼 일했고, 처음 얼마간은 좀 고되게 살았을지 몰라도, 늘 번창했습니다. 양 치는 일이며, 가축 치는 일이며, 이런저런 일이며 해서, 더 바랄 데 없이 잘 지내고 있어요. 어쩐지 저희한테 복이 내린 것 같습디다.\" 패거티 아저씨가 경건하게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"저흰 그저 번창하기만 했답니다. 그러니까, 길게 보면요. 어제가 아니면 오늘, 오늘이 아니면 내일, 그렇게요.\"\n\n\"그래서 에밀리는요?\" 애그니스와 내가 동시에 물었다.\n\n\"에밀리는요,\" 그가 말했다. \"마님이 그 애 곁을 떠나신 뒤로―저희가 오지에 자리를 잡고 나서, 밤마다 칸막이 천 너머에서 그 애가 기도하는 소리를 들을 때면 늘 마님 이름이 들렸지요―그리고 그 애와 제가, 그 눈부신 노을 속에서 데이비 도련님을 마지막으로 뵌 뒤로는, 처음엔 어찌나 풀이 죽었던지, 만약 그 애가 데이비 도련님이 그토록 자상하고 사려 깊게 저희한테 숨겨 주신 일을 그때 알았더라면, 제 생각엔 그대로 시들어 버렸을 겁니다요. 그런데 배 안에 병든 가난한 사람들이 좀 있어서 그 애가 그들을 돌봤고, 또 우리 일행 중에 아이들이 있어서 그들도 돌봤지요. 그렇게 바빠지고 좋은 일을 하다 보니, 그게 그 애한테 힘이 됐답니다.\"\n\n\"그 애가 그걸 처음 안 게 언제였소?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"제가 그 소식을 듣고 나서도 그 애한텐 숨겼지요.\" 패거티 아저씨가 말했다. \"거의 한 해 가까이요. 그 무렵 저흰 외딴곳에 살았는데, 더없이 아름다운 나무들에 둘러싸여 있었고, 장미가 우리 오두막 지붕까지 덮고 있었지요. 어느 날 제가 밭에 나가 일하고 있는데, 잉글랜드 우리 노퍽인가 서퍽인가(어느 쪽인지는 똑똑히 기억이 안 납니다)에서 온 나그네 하나가 지나가지 뭡니까. 물론 저흰 그를 들이고 먹고 마실 것을 주며 반갑게 맞이했지요. 그곳 이주민들은 다들 그렇게 합니다요, 식민지 어디서나요. 그 사람이 낡은 신문 한 장을 가지고 있었는데, 거기에 그 폭풍에 관한 다른 기사도 활자로 실려 있었어요. 그렇게 그 애가 알게 된 겁니다. 제가 밤에 집에 돌아오니, 그 애가 이미 알고 있더군요.\"\n\n이 말을 하면서 그는 목소리를 낮췄고, 내가 그토록 잘 기억하는 그 엄숙함이 그의 얼굴에 번졌다.\n\n\"그 일로 그 애가 많이 변했나요?\" 우리가 물었다.\n\n\"예, 한참 동안요.\" 그가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"어쩌면 지금 이 순간까지도요. 하지만 제 생각엔 그 외로움이 그 애한테 약이 된 것 같습니다요. 그리고 닭 치는 일이며 그런 잔일거리가 많아서, 거기에 마음을 쏟다 보니 이겨 낸 거지요. 궁금하네요.\" 그가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"지금 제 에밀리를 보시면, 데이비 도련님, 도련님이 그 애를 알아보실 수 있을지 말입니다!\"\n\n\"그렇게나 변했소?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"저야 모르지요. 날마다 보니 모르겠어요. 그런데 가끔은 그런 생각이 들기도 합니다. 가냘픈 몸매에,\" 패거티 아저씨가 난롯불을 바라보며 말했다. \"어쩐지 수척하고, 부드럽고 슬픔 어린 파란 눈에, 가녀린 얼굴, 살짝 숙인 예쁜 머리, 조용한 목소리와 몸가짐―거의 수줍은 듯한. 그게 에밀리입니다요!\"\n\n우리는 그가 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며 앉아 있는 모습을 말없이 지켜보았다.\n\n\"더러는 그러더군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애 정이 그릇된 사람한테 잘못 갔다고요. 또 더러는 그 애 혼사가 죽음으로 깨졌다고 하고요. 어찌 된 영문인지는 아무도 모르지요. 그 애는 좋은 혼처에 시집갈 기회가 수없이 많았지만, '아저씨,' 그 애가 저한테 그래요, '그건 영영 지나가 버린 일이에요' 하고요. 저랑 있을 땐 명랑하다가도, 남이 곁에 있으면 뒤로 물러나지요. 멀리까지라도 마다 않고 가서 아이를 가르치거나, 병자를 보살피거나, 어느 처녀의 혼사를 거들어 주는 친절을 베푸는 걸 좋아하고요(그렇게 거든 혼사가 숱하지만, 정작 제 혼사는 한 번도 못 봤지요). 아저씨를 끔찍이 사랑하고, 참을성 있고, 젊은이든 늙은이든 다 그 애를 좋아하고, 무슨 어려움이 있는 사람이면 죄다 그 애를 찾는답니다. 그게 에밀리입니다요!\"\n\n그가 손으로 얼굴을 쓸어내리더니, 반쯤 삼킨 한숨을 내쉬며 난롯불에서 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"마사는 아직 함께 지내오?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"마사는요,\" 그가 대답했다. \"둘째 해에 결혼했습니다, 데이비 도련님. 어느 젊은이가, 농장 일꾼이었는데, 제 주인의 짐마차를 끌고 장에 가는 길에 우리 곁을 지나갔지요―갔다 오는 데 오백 마일이 넘는 길이었어요―그 젊은이가 그 애한테 아내가 되어 달라고 청혼을 하더니(그곳엔 아내 될 여자가 아주 귀하거든요), 둘이서 오지에 따로 살림을 차리겠다고 했지요. 그 애가 저한테, 그 사람한테 자기 지난 이야기를 다 일러 달라더군요. 제가 그리했지요. 둘은 결혼했고, 지금은 사백 마일 안짝에 자기들 목소리와 지저귀는 새소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않는 곳에서 산답니다.\"\n\n\"거미지 아주머니는요?\" 내가 운을 떼었다.\n\n이건 건드리기 좋은 화제였다. 패거티 아저씨가 별안간 너털웃음을 터뜨리며, 그 오래전 난파선 보트에서 신이 날 때면 으레 그러던 버릇대로 두 손으로 다리를 위아래로 문질러 댔으니까.\n\n\"이걸 믿으시겠습니까!\" 그가 말했다. \"아니, 글쎄, 그 아주머니한테까지 청혼하는 사람이 다 있었지 뭡니까! 식민지에 정착하려던 어느 배 요리사가 거미지 아주머니한테 청혼을 했더라니까요, 데이비 도련님, 이게 거짓말이면 제가 사람이 아닙니다요―이보다 더 정직하게는 말 못 하겠습니다!\"\n\n나는 애그니스가 그토록 웃는 모습을 본 적이 없다. 패거티 아저씨가 별안간 보인 이 황홀한 신명이 그녀에게 어찌나 즐거웠던지, 그녀는 도무지 웃음을 멈추질 못했다. 그녀가 웃으면 웃을수록 나도 따라 웃게 됐고, 그럴수록 패거티 아저씨의 신명은 더 커져서, 그는 더욱더 다리를 문질러 댔다.\n\n\"그래서 거미지 아주머니가 뭐라고 합디까?\" 가까스로 웃음을 가다듬고서 내가 물었다.\n\n\"믿으실는지 모르겠습니다만,\" 패거티 아저씨가 대답했다. \"거미지 아주머니가, '고맙수다, 정말 황송하우, 하지만 이 나이에 내 처지를 바꿀 생각은 없수' 하고 말하는 대신에, 곁에 놓여 있던 양동이를 집어 들더니 그 배 요리사 머리에 폭 씌워 버리지 뭡니까. 그 사람이 살려 달라고 고래고래 소리를 지르길래, 제가 들어가서 구해 줬지요.\"\n\n패거티 아저씨가 너털웃음을 터뜨렸고, 애그니스와 나도 함께 어울렸다.\n\n\"그래도 이 착한 사람을 위해 이건 꼭 말해야겠습니다.\" 우리가 다 웃다 지쳤을 때, 그가 얼굴을 훔치며 다시 말을 이었다. \"그 아주머니는 저희한테 해 주겠다고 한 건 죄다, 아니 그 이상으로 해 줬어요. 데이비 도련님, 그 아주머니는 이 세상에 숨 쉬고 사는 그 누구보다도 기꺼이 돕고, 더없이 미덥고, 더없이 진실되게 거드는 여자입니다요. 식민지가 온통 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있고 저희가 거기 처음 발 디뎠을 때조차도, 저는 그 아주머니가 단 일 분이라도 '외롭고 쓸쓸하다'고 하는 걸 본 적이 없어요. 그리고 '영감님' 생각일랑은, 정말이지, 잉글랜드를 떠난 뒤로는 한 번도 한 적이 없다니까요!\"\n\n\"자, 마지막으로, 그렇다고 결코 덜 중요한 건 아닌데, 미코버 씨는 어떻소?\" 내가 말했다. \"그분은 여기서 진 빚을 죄다 갚았소―심지어 트래들스한테 진 어음까지도요, 내 사랑 애그니스, 당신도 기억하지요―그러니 그분이 잘 지내고 있으리라고 봐도 무방하겠지요. 그래, 그분의 가장 최근 소식은 어떻소?\"\n\n패거티 아저씨가 빙긋 웃으며 가슴팍 안주머니에 손을 넣더니, 납작하게 접은 종이 꾸러미를 꺼냈다. 그러고는 거기서 아주 조심스럽게 야릇하게 생긴 작은 신문 한 장을 끄집어냈다.\n\n\"아셔야 할 게 있는데요, 데이비 도련님,\" 그가 말했다. \"저희가 형편이 워낙 좋아져서 이제 오지를 떠나, 곧장 돌아 나와 포트 미들베이 항구로 갔답니다. 거기는 저희가 '읍내'라고 부를 만한 데가 있거든요.\"\n\n\"미코버 씨가 아저씨네 근처 오지에 있었소?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그럼요, 그렇고말고요.\" 패거티 아저씨가 말했다. \"그것도 아주 발 벗고 나섰지요. 그분만큼 발 벗고 나서서 일하는 신사는 다시없을 겁니다. 데이비 도련님, 저는 그분의 그 벗어진 머리가 땡볕에 어찌나 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리던지, 그러다 녹아내리지나 않을까 싶었다니까요. 그런데 이제 그분이 치안 판사랍니다.\"\n\n\"치안 판사라고요, 정말?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n패거티 아저씨가 신문의 어느 한 단락을 가리켰고, 나는 거기 〈포트 미들베이 타임스〉에서 다음과 같이 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n\"우리의 저명한 동향 이주민이자 읍민이신, 포트 미들베이 지구 치안 판사 윌킨스 미코버 님을 위한 공개 만찬이 어제 호텔 대연회실에서 성대히 열렸으며, 연회실은 발 디딜 틈 없이 가득 찼다. 복도와 계단에 늘어선 사람들을 제외하고도, 한자리에서 만찬을 든 인원이 무려 마흔일곱 명을 밑돌지 않았으리라 추산된다. 포트 미들베이의 미모와 멋과 명사들이, 이토록 마땅히 존경받고, 이토록 높은 재능을 지녔으며, 이토록 두루 사랑받는 분께 경의를 표하고자 구름같이 모여들었다. 멜 박사(포트 미들베이 식민지 세일럼하우스 문법 학교)께서 좌장을 맡으셨고, 그 오른편에 저명한 귀빈이 앉으셨다. 상보를 거두고 〈논 노비스〉 합창(아름답게 연주되었으며, 그 가운데서 재능 넘치는 아마추어 윌킨스 미코버 이세 님의 종소리 같은 음색을 분간하기란 어렵지 않았다)이 끝난 뒤, 으레 올리는 충성과 애국의 건배가 차례로 제창되어 열렬한 환호 속에 받아들여졌다. 이어 멜 박사께서 감정이 북받치는 연설로 이렇게 건배를 제안하셨다. '우리의 저명한 귀빈, 우리 읍의 자랑이신 분. 부디 더 나은 자리로 영전하시는 일 말고는 결코 우리 곁을 떠나지 마시며, 우리 가운데서 거두신 성공이 너무도 커서 더는 영전하실 수조차 없게 되기를!' 이 건배가 받은 환호는 이루 다 말로 옮길 수 없다. 그것은 마치 대양의 물결처럼 거듭거듭 솟구쳤다 잦아들었다. 마침내 모두가 잠잠해지자, 윌킨스 미코버 님께서 답사를 하시려 자리에서 일어나셨다. 우리 신문사의 자원이 비교적 아직 미비한 지금 형편으로는, 우리의 저명한 읍민이 펼치신 그 세련되고 더없이 화려한 답사의 유려하게 흐르는 문장들을 일일이 좇으려는 시도는 부디 삼가고자 한다! 다만 이것이 웅변의 걸작이었다는 것, 그리고 특히 당신 자신의 성공적인 이력을 그 근원까지 거슬러 짚으시며, 청중 가운데 젊은 축에게 결코 갚을 수 없는 금전적 채무라는 암초에 빠지지 말라고 경계하신 대목들이, 그 자리에 있던 가장 사내다운 이의 눈에도 눈물이 맺히게 했다는 것만 일러 두면 족하리라. 나머지 건배는 멜 박사, 미코버 부인(미모의 여인들이 한 무리 의자 위에 올라서서 이 흐뭇한 광경을 지켜보는 동시에 그 자리를 빛내고 있던 옆문에서, 우아하게 고개 숙여 답례하셨다), 리저 베그스 부인(전 미코버 양), 멜 부인, 윌킨스 미코버 이세 님(연설로는 답례를 드릴 수 없으니, 허락해 주신다면 노래로 대신하겠노라고 익살스레 말하여 좌중을 웃음바다로 만드셨다), 미코버 부인 일가(모국에서 익히 알려진 분들임은 새삼 말할 필요도 없다) 등등을 위해 올려졌다. 행사가 끝나자 식탁들이 마치 마법이라도 부린 듯 치워져 무도장이 마련되었다. 해님이 떠날 시간임을 알릴 때까지 흥겹게 춤판을 벌인 무도의 여신 테르프시코레의 숭배자들 가운데, 윌킨스 미코버 이세 님과 멜 박사의 넷째 따님이신 사랑스럽고 교양 넘치는 헬레나 양이 유독 돋보였다.\"\n\n나는 멜 박사라는 이름으로 눈길을 되돌리고 있었다. 한때 내 미들섹스 치안 판사 밑에서 박봉에 시달리던 가엾은 보조 교사였던 멜 선생이, 이렇게 더 행복한 처지에 놓인 것을 알게 되어 기뻤기 때문이다. 그때 패거티 아저씨가 신문의 다른 부분을 가리켰고, 내 눈은 내 이름 위에 가 멈췄으며, 나는 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\"데이비드 코퍼필드 님께,\n\n저명한 작가이신 그분께.\n\n친애하는 선생께,\n문명 세계의 상당수 사람들이 이제 그 모습을 마음속에 익히 그려 보는, 그 이목구비를 제가 두 눈으로 직접 뜯어볼 기회를 가져 본 지도 여러 해가 흘렀습니다.\n\n그러나 친애하는 선생, 비록 제가 어찌할 수 없는 여러 사정의 힘에 떠밀려 젊은 시절의 벗이자 동무와 직접 어울릴 기회로부터 멀어지긴 했으되, 저는 그가 드높이 날아오르는 모습을 한시도 잊은 적이 없습니다. 또한 저는,\n\n비록 우리 사이에 바닷물이 굽이쳐 포효할지라도,\n\n(번스) 그가 우리 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 지적 향연에 동참하는 일에서 가로막힌 적도 없었습니다.\n\n그러므로 저는, 우리가 서로 존경하고 흠모하는 한 인물이 이곳을 떠나는 마당에, 친애하는 선생, 이 공개적인 기회를 빌려 선생께 감사를 드리지 않을 수 없습니다. 저 자신을 대신하여, 그리고 감히 덧붙이건대 포트 미들베이 주민 전체를 대신하여, 선생이 그 기쁨을 베풀어 주시는 일꾼이신 데 대하여 말입니다.\n\n계속 나아가소서, 친애하는 선생! 선생은 이곳에서 알려지지 않은 분이 아니며, 인정받지 못하는 분도 아닙니다. 비록 '멀리 떨어져' 있으나, 우리는 '벗 없는' 처지도, '우울한' 처지도, (감히 덧붙이건대) '굼뜬' 처지도 아닙니다. 계속 나아가소서, 친애하는 선생, 그 독수리의 항로를 따라! 포트 미들베이 주민들은 적어도 그 비상을 우러러보기를 갈망할 수 있으니, 기쁨으로, 즐거움으로, 가르침으로 말입니다!\n\n이 지구의 한구석에서 선생을 향해 우러러 들린 눈들 가운데에는, 그 눈이 빛과 생명을 지니는 한, 언제까지나\n윌킨스 미코버,\n\n치안 판사에게\n속한\n\n눈이\n있을 것입니다.\"\n\n신문의 나머지 내용을 훑어보다가, 나는 미코버 씨가 그 신문의 부지런하고 존경받는 기고가라는 것을 알게 되었다. 같은 신문에 다리를 다룬 그의 또 다른 편지가 실려 있었고, 머지않아 '상당한 보충을 더하여' 산뜻한 한 권으로 재출간될 예정인 그의 비슷한 편지 모음집 광고도 있었으며, 내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면 사설마저 그의 글이었다.\n\n패거티 아저씨가 우리와 함께 머무는 동안, 우리는 여러 다른 저녁에도 미코버 씨 이야기를 많이 나누었다. 그는 머무는 내내 우리와 함께 지냈는데―내 생각엔 한 달이 채 안 되었던 것 같다―그의 누이와 내 고모님도 그를 보러 런던으로 왔다. 애그니스와 나는 그가 배를 타고 떠날 때 선상에서 그와 작별했다. 그리고 우리는 이 세상에서 다시는 그와 헤어지지 않을 것이다.\n\n그러나 떠나기 전에, 그는 나와 함께 야머스로 가서, 내가 햄을 기리려고 교회 묘지에 세워 둔 작은 비석을 보았다. 그의 청에 따라 그 수수한 비문을 베껴 적고 있는데, 나는 그가 몸을 숙여 무덤에서 풀 한 줌과 흙 한 줌을 뜨는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"에밀리한테 주려고요.\" 그가 그것을 가슴팍에 품으며 말했다. \"약속했거든요, 데이비 도련님.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 64. A LAST RETROSPECT\n\n이제 내가 쓴 이야기는 끝난다. 나는 다시 한번 — 마지막으로 — 이 책장을 덮기 전에 뒤를 돌아본다.\n\n나는 애그니스를 곁에 두고 인생의 길을 걸어가는 나 자신을 본다. 우리를 둘러싼 우리 아이들과 우리 친구들이 보인다. 그리고 길을 걸어가는 나에게 결코 무심하지 않은 수많은 목소리의 울림이 들린다.\n\n스쳐 지나가는 무리 가운데 나에게 가장 또렷한 얼굴은 누구일까? 보라, 바로 이 얼굴들이다. 내가 내 생각에게 그 물음을 던지는 순간, 모두가 나를 향해 돌아선다!\n\n여기 우리 고모님이 계신다. 도수 높은 안경을 끼고, 여든 살이 넘은 노부인이 되었으되, 여전히 허리가 꼿꼿하고, 한겨울 날씨에도 한 번에 육 마일을 거뜬히 걷는 든든한 보행자다.\n\n언제나 고모님과 함께, 여기 패거티가 온다. 내 착하고 나이 든 유모도 마찬가지로 안경을 끼고, 밤이면 등불에 바짝 붙어 바느질하는 버릇이 들었는데, 그러면서도 밀랍 양초 한 토막과, 작은 집 모양 자와, 뚜껑에 성 바울 대성당 그림이 그려진 반짇고리 없이는 결코 자리에 앉는 법이 없다.\n\n어린 시절 내가 새들이 사과 대신 왜 저 뺨과 팔을 쪼지 않을까 궁금해했을 만큼 단단하고 붉던 패거티의 뺨과 팔은 이제 쪼글쪼글해졌다. 그리고 한때 얼굴에서 그 둘레를 온통 어둡게 물들이던 두 눈도 빛이 흐려졌다(그래도 여전히 반짝거리기는 한다). 하지만 옛날에 내가 호주머니 속 육두구 강판을 떠올리게 했던 그 거친 집게손가락만큼은 조금도 변하지 않았다. 우리 막내가 고모님에게서 패거티에게로 비틀비틀 걸어가며 그 손가락을 붙잡으려고 하는 모습을 볼 때면, 나는 내가 겨우 걷기 시작했던 시절, 우리 집 그 작은 거실을 떠올린다. 고모님의 오랜 실망은 이제 바로잡혔다. 고모님은 살아 숨 쉬는 진짜 베시 트롯우드의 대모가 되었으니까. 그리고 (그다음 차례인) 도라는 고모님이 그 아이를 버릇없게 키운다고 말한다.\n\n패거티의 호주머니에는 뭔가 불룩한 것이 들어 있다. 그것은 다름 아닌 악어 책인데, 이제는 책장이 여기저기 찢기고 다시 꿰매져 제법 너덜너덜해진 상태지만, 패거티는 그것을 귀한 유물처럼 아이들에게 보여 준다. 악어 이야기 속에서 나를 올려다보는 내 어린 시절의 얼굴을 보는 일도, 그것을 통해 내 오랜 지인인 셰필드의 브룩스 씨를 떠올리게 되는 일도, 나에게는 참으로 신기하게 느껴진다.\n\n이번 여름 방학 동안, 내 아들들 틈에서, 나는 거대한 연을 만들어 그것이 공중에 뜬 것을 바라보며, 말로 다할 수 없는 기쁨에 젖어 있는 한 노인을 본다. 그는 황홀하게 나를 맞이하더니, 연신 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 찡긋거리며 속삭인다. \"트롯우드, 자네 들으면 기뻐할 걸세. 달리 할 일이 없을 때 진정서를 마저 끝낼 작정이라네. 그리고 자네 고모님은 세상에서 가장 비범한 여인이시지, 선생!\"\n\n지팡이에 의지해 몸을 가누고, 옛날의 자존심과 아름다움이 어렴풋이 남은 얼굴을, 투덜대고 멍하니 안달하며 떠도는 마음과 힘없이 싸우게 하는, 저 허리 굽은 부인은 누구일까? 그녀는 어느 정원에 있다. 그리고 그 곁에는 입술에 하얀 흉터가 있는, 날카롭고 가무잡잡하며 시든 여인이 서 있다. 두 사람이 무슨 말을 나누는지 들어 보자.\n\n\"로자, 이 신사분 이름을 깜빡했구나.\"\n\n로자가 그녀에게 몸을 굽혀 외친다. \"코퍼필드 씨예요.\"\n\n\"만나서 반가워요, 선생. 상복을 입으셨군요, 안됐어요. 부디 세월이 당신에게 너그럽기를 바라요.\"\n\n성마른 시중꾼이 그녀를 나무라며, 내가 상복을 입은 게 아니라고 일러 주고, 다시 보라고 채근하며, 정신을 차리게 하려 애쓴다.\n\n\"내 아들을 만나 봤지요, 선생.\" 노부인이 말한다. \"둘이 화해했나요?\"\n\n그녀는 나를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 한 손을 이마에 갖다 대고 신음한다. 그러더니 갑자기 끔찍한 목소리로 외친다. \"로자, 이리 와. 그 애가 죽었어!\" 로자는 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고, 어르다가 다투기를 번갈아 한다. 어느 때는 사납게, \"내가 당신보다 그분을 더 사랑했어요!\"라고 쏘아붙이고 — 또 어느 때는 아픈 아이를 달래듯 자기 가슴에 기대어 잠들게 어른다. 나는 그렇게 두 사람을 두고 떠난다. 언제 가도 두 사람은 그 모습 그대로다. 그렇게 두 사람은 해가 가고 또 가도록 세월을 흘려보낸다.\n\n인도에서 본국을 향해 돛을 올리고 돌아오는 저 배는 무슨 배이며, 커다란 귀가 축 늘어진, 으르렁대는 늙은 스코틀랜드 부자에게 시집간 저 영국 부인은 누구일까? 설마 줄리아 밀스일까?\n\n과연 줄리아 밀스다. 짜증스럽고 우아하게 차려입고, 황금 쟁반에 명함과 편지를 받쳐 나르는 흑인 하인과, 머리에 화사한 손수건을 두르고 아마포 옷을 걸친 채 화장실에서 점심 식사를 시중드는 구릿빛 여인을 거느리고 있다. 그러나 줄리아는 요즘 일기를 쓰지 않는다. '애정의 만가'도 결코 부르지 않는다. 햇볕에 그은 가죽을 뒤집어쓴 누런 곰 같은 그 늙은 스코틀랜드 부자와 끊임없이 다툰다. 줄리아는 목까지 돈에 푹 잠겨, 오직 돈 이야기만 하고 돈 생각만 한다. 나는 사하라 사막 시절의 그녀가 더 좋았다.\n\n어쩌면 이곳이 바로 사하라 사막일지도 모른다! 줄리아에게 으리으리한 저택과, 막강한 손님들과, 날마다 호화로운 만찬이 있다 한들, 나는 그녀 곁에서 푸르게 자라는 것을 보지 못하니까. 열매를 맺거나 꽃을 피울 만한 것이라곤 하나도 없다. 줄리아가 '사교계'라 부르는 것이 내 눈에 들어온다. 그 가운데에는 자기 특권직에 올라앉아 그 자리를 마련해 준 손을 비웃으며, 나에게 박사님을 두고 \"참으로 매력적으로 고풍스럽다\"고 말하는 잭 몰던 씨가 있다. 그러나 줄리아여, 사교계라는 것이 그처럼 속 빈 신사 숙녀를 가리키는 이름이고, 그 교양이라는 것이 인류를 앞으로 나아가게 하거나 뒤로 물러서게 할 그 어떤 것에도 짐짓 무관심한 태도를 뜻한다면, 우리는 바로 그 사하라 사막에서 길을 잃은 게 틀림없으니, 어서 빠져나갈 길을 찾는 편이 나으리라.\n\n그리고 보라, 박사님이 계신다. 언제나 우리의 좋은 벗인 그분은, 사전 편찬에 매달려(어디쯤인가 디(D) 자 언저리에 와 있다) 자기 집과 아내 속에서 행복하다. 그리고 옛 군인 부인도 계신데, 형편이 상당히 쪼그라들었고, 지난날과 달리 이제는 결코 그리 큰 영향력을 부리지 못한다!\n\n법조 거리의 사무실에서 분주한 모습으로 일하고, (대머리가 아닌 부분의) 머리카락은 법조인 가발에 끊임없이 쓸려 그 어느 때보다 더 제멋대로 뻗친 채인, 내 정다운 옛 친구 트래들스를 나는 훗날 다시 만난다. 그의 책상은 두툼한 서류 더미로 뒤덮여 있다. 나는 둘레를 둘러보며 말한다.\n\n\"이보게 트래들스, 지금 소피가 자네 서기로 일했다면, 손이 모자라 쩔쩔맸겠는걸!\"\n\n\"그렇게 말해도 되겠네, 사랑하는 코퍼필드! 하지만 홀번 코트 시절도 더없이 좋은 날들이었지! 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"소피가 자네더러 언젠가 판사가 될 거라고 말하던 그 시절 말인가? 하지만 그땐 온 동네에 소문난 얘기는 아니었지!\"\n\n\"어쨌든,\" 트래들스가 말한다. \"내가 정말 판사가 된다면—\" \"이런, 자네도 알잖나, 틀림없이 될 거라는 걸.\"\n\n\"좋아, 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 내가 판사가 되면, 전에 그러겠다고 했듯이, 그 이야기를 들려주겠네.\"\n\n우리는 팔짱을 끼고 걸어간다. 나는 트래들스 집에서 가족 만찬을 함께 할 참이다. 오늘은 소피의 생일이다. 그리고 가는 길에 트래들스는 자기가 누려 온 행운에 대해 나에게 이야기를 풀어놓는다.\n\n\"정말이지, 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 나는 내가 마음속에 가장 간절히 바라던 일을 다 이룰 수 있었다네. 호러스 목사님은 연봉 사백오십 파운드짜리 그 성직록 자리로 승진하셨고, 우리 집 두 아들은 더없이 훌륭한 교육을 받으며 착실한 학생이자 좋은 청년으로 두각을 나타내고 있고, 딸아이 셋은 아주 편안하게 시집을 갔고, 셋은 우리와 함께 살고, 또 셋은 크루얼러 부인이 돌아가신 뒤로 호러스 목사님 댁 살림을 맡고 있다네. 그리고 모두가 행복하지.\"\n\n\"다만—\" 내가 슬쩍 운을 뗀다.\n\n\"미인 아가씨만 빼고 말이지.\" 트래들스가 말한다. \"그래. 그 애가 그런 떠돌이 같은 자와 결혼한 건 참으로 불운한 일이었어. 하지만 그자에게는 그 애를 사로잡은 어떤 호기로움과 번지르르함이 있었지. 그래도 이제는 그 애를 무사히 우리 집으로 데려왔고, 그자도 떨쳐 냈으니, 다시 기운을 북돋워 줘야지.\"\n\n트래들스의 집은, 저녁 산책길에 그와 소피가 이러쿵저러쿵 나눠 가지곤 하던 바로 그런 집 가운데 하나다 — 아니, 어쩌면 정말 그 집일 수도 있다. 큰 집이다. 그러나 트래들스는 서류를 자기 화장실에 두고, 장화도 그 서류와 함께 둔다. 그리고 그와 소피는 제일 좋은 침실들을 미인 아가씨와 딸아이들에게 내주고, 자기들은 위층 방으로 비집고 들어간다. 이 집에는 남는 방이 없다. 이런저런 까닭으로 내가 셀 수 없을 만큼 많은 '딸아이들'이 여기 와 있고, 늘 여기 있기 때문이다. 우리가 안으로 들어서면, 한 무리의 아이들이 문간으로 우르르 달려 나와, 트래들스가 숨이 찰 때까지 이리저리 끌고 다니며 입을 맞추게 한다. 여기, 영영 눌러앉은 가엾은 미인 아가씨가 있는데, 어린 딸을 둔 과부다. 여기, 소피의 생일 만찬 자리에는, 시집간 딸 셋이 저마다 남편을 데리고 와 있고, 그 남편 가운데 하나의 동생과, 또 다른 남편의 사촌과, 또 다른 남편의 누이가 있는데, 그 누이는 아무래도 그 사촌과 약혼한 사이인 듯하다. 트래들스는, 옛날 그대로 한결같이 소박하고 꾸밈없는 사람이라, 큰 식탁 끄트머리에 족장처럼 앉아 있다. 그리고 소피는 식탁 윗자리에서, 결코 영국제 합금 그릇 따위로 번쩍이지는 않는 그 정겨운 공간 너머로 그를 향해 환하게 웃음을 보낸다.\n* 영국제 합금: 주석에 안티몬과 구리를 섞어 만든 값싼 식기용 금속.\n\n이제 나는 내 일을 마무리하며, 아직도 더 머물고 싶은 마음을 억누른다. 그러자 이 얼굴들은 차츰 희미해진다. 그러나 한 얼굴만은, 내가 다른 모든 것을 보게 해 주는 천상의 빛처럼 나를 비추며, 그 모든 얼굴 위에, 그 모든 얼굴 너머에 있다. 그리고 그 얼굴은 남는다.\n\n나는 고개를 돌려, 그 아름다운 평온 속에 깃든 그 얼굴이 내 곁에 있는 것을 본다.\n\n내 등불은 가물가물 타들어 가고, 나는 밤이 깊도록 글을 써 왔다. 그러나 그 사람 없이는 내가 아무것도 아닐, 그 정다운 존재가 내 곁을 지켜 준다.\n\n오 애그니스, 오 내 영혼이여, 내가 정말로 내 삶을 마감하는 그날에도 부디 그대의 얼굴이 내 곁에 있기를. 지금 내가 떨쳐 보내는 저 그림자들처럼 현실이 내게서 스러져 갈 때에도, 부디 내가 위를 가리키며 내 곁에 선 그대를 여전히 찾을 수 있기를!";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-766"]=[[["Whether I shall turn out to be the hero of my own life, or whether that station will be held by anybody else, these pages must show.", "내가 내 인생의 주인공이 될지, 아니면 그 자리를 다른 누군가가 차지할지는 이 책이 보여 줄 것이다."], ["To begin my life with the beginning of my life, I record that I was born (as I have been informed and believe) on a Friday, at twelve o’clock at night.", "내 인생을 처음부터 이야기하자면, 나는 (전해 들어서 그렇게 믿고 있는데) 어느 금요일 밤 열두 시에 태어났다."], ["It was remarked that the clock began to strike, and I began to cry, simultaneously.", "시계가 종을 치기 시작하는 것과 내가 울음을 터뜨린 것이 동시였다는 말이 있었다."]], [["In consideration of the day and hour of my birth, it was declared by the nurse, and by some sage women in the neighbourhood who had taken a lively interest in me several months before there was any possibility of our becoming personally acquainted, first, that I was destined to be unlucky in life; and secondly, that I was privileged to see ghosts and spirits; both these gifts inevitably attaching, as they believed, to all unlucky infants of either gender, born towards the small hours on a Friday night.", "내가 태어난 날짜와 시각을 두고, 산파와 동네의 지혜로운 아낙 몇몇이 — 우리가 서로 얼굴을 익힐 가능성이 생기기도 한참 전부터 나에게 깊은 관심을 보였던 분들이다 — 이렇게 단언했다. 첫째, 나는 평생 운이 없을 팔자라는 것, 둘째, 나에게는 유령과 혼령을 볼 특권이 주어졌다는 것이다. 이 두 가지 재능은, 그들의 믿음에 따르면, 금요일 밤 자정 무렵에 태어난 불운한 아기라면 남자든 여자든 반드시 따라붙는 것이라고 했다."]], [["I need say nothing here, on the first head, because nothing can show better than my history whether that prediction was verified or falsified by the result.", "첫째 항목에 대해서는 여기서 굳이 할 말이 없다. 그 예언이 결과로써 들어맞았는지 빗나갔는지는 내 인생 이야기보다 더 잘 보여 줄 것이 없을 테니까."], ["On the second branch of the question, I will only remark, that unless I ran through that part of my inheritance while I was still a baby, I have not come into it yet.", "둘째 항목에 대해서는 이것만 말해 두겠다. 내가 아기였을 적에 그 유산을 이미 다 써 버린 게 아니라면, 나는 아직 그것을 물려받지 못했다."], ["But I do not at all complain of having been kept out of this property; and if anybody else should be in the present enjoyment of it, he is heartily welcome to keep it.", "그렇다고 이 재산을 받지 못한 것을 조금도 불평하지는 않는다. 혹시 다른 누군가가 지금 그것을 누리고 있다면, 진심으로 마음껏 가지라고 권하고 싶다."]], [["I was born with a caul, which was advertised for sale, in the newspapers, at the low price of fifteen guineas.", "나는 양막을 쓰고 태어났는데, 이것이 신문에 헐값인 십오 기니에 매물로 광고로 나왔다."], ["Whether sea-going people were short of money about that time, or were short of faith and preferred cork jackets, I don’t know; all I know is, that there was but one solitary bidding, and that was from an attorney connected with the bill-broking business, who offered two pounds in cash, and the balance in sherry, but declined to be guaranteed from drowning on any higher bargain.", "그 무렵 뱃사람들에게 돈이 없었던 건지, 아니면 믿음이 모자라 코르크 구명조끼를 더 좋아했던 건지 나는 모른다. 내가 아는 거라곤 단 한 건의 입찰밖에 없었다는 것뿐인데, 그것도 어음 중개업과 연줄이 있는 변호사 하나가 현금 이 파운드에 나머지는 셰리주로 치르겠다고 제안하면서, 그보다 높은 값으로는 물에 빠져 죽지 않을 보증을 받지 않겠다고 거절한 것이었다."], ["Consequently the advertisement was withdrawn at a dead loss--for as to sherry, my poor dear mother’s own sherry was in the market then--and ten years afterwards, the caul was put up in a raffle down in our part of the country, to fifty members at half-a-crown a head, the winner to spend five shillings.", "그래서 광고는 완전한 손해를 보고 내려졌다 — 셰리주로 말하자면, 그 무렵 내 가엾은 어머니의 셰리주가 시장에 나와 있던 참이었으니까 — 그리고 십 년이 지난 뒤, 그 양막은 우리 고장에서 한 사람당 반 크라운씩 받고 오십 명을 모아 추첨에 부쳐졌고, 당첨된 사람은 오 실링을 더 내기로 되어 있었다."], ["I was present myself, and I remember to have felt quite uncomfortable and confused, at a part of myself being disposed of in that way.", "나도 그 자리에 있었는데, 내 몸의 일부가 그런 식으로 처분되는 것이 영 거북하고 어리둥절하게 느껴졌던 기억이 난다."], ["The caul was won, I recollect, by an old lady with a hand-basket, who, very reluctantly, produced from it the stipulated five shillings, all in halfpence, and twopence halfpenny short--as it took an immense time and a great waste of arithmetic, to endeavour without any effect to prove to her.", "양막은, 내 기억으로는, 손바구니를 든 어느 노부인이 차지했는데, 그 노부인은 몹시 마지못해 바구니에서 정해진 오 실링을 꺼냈으되, 그것도 죄다 반 페니짜리 동전으로, 그나마 이 펜스 반이 모자랐다 — 그 사실을 노부인에게 입증하려고 무진 시간과 산수를 허비했지만 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["It is a fact which will be long remembered as remarkable down there, that she was never drowned, but died triumphantly in bed, at ninety-two.", "그 노부인이 끝내 물에 빠져 죽지 않고, 아흔두 살에 침대에서 의기양양하게 숨을 거두었다는 것은 그 고장에서 오래도록 기억될 놀라운 사실이다."], ["I have understood that it was, to the last, her proudest boast, that she never had been on the water in her life, except upon a bridge; and that over her tea (to which she was extremely partial) she, to the last, expressed her indignation at the impiety of mariners and others, who had the presumption to go ‘meandering’ about the world.", "들리는 말에 따르면, 노부인은 죽는 날까지 자기 평생 다리 위 말고는 단 한 번도 물 위에 오른 적이 없다는 것을 가장 자랑스러운 자랑거리로 삼았다고 한다. 그리고 차(그녀가 무척이나 좋아했던 음료다)를 마시면서, 죽는 날까지, 감히 세상을 '싸돌아다니는' 뱃사람이며 다른 사람들의 불경함에 분노를 토했다고 한다."], ["It was in vain to represent to her that some conveniences, tea perhaps included, resulted from this objectionable practice.", "그 못마땅한 행위 덕분에 차를 비롯한 몇몇 편의를 누리게 된 것이라고 아무리 설명해도 헛수고였다."], ["She always returned, with greater emphasis and with an instinctive knowledge of the strength of her objection, ‘Let us have no meandering.’", "노부인은 늘, 더 힘주어, 그리고 자기 반대가 얼마나 굳건한지를 본능으로 알고 있는 듯이 이렇게 받아쳤다. \"싸돌아다니는 짓 따위는 하지 맙시다.\""]], [["Not to meander myself, at present, I will go back to my birth.", "나 자신도 지금 싸돌아다니지 않으려면, 다시 내가 태어난 이야기로 돌아가야겠다."]], [["I was born at Blunderstone, in Suffolk, or ‘there by’, as they say in Scotland.", "나는 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서, 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나 '그 언저리'에서 태어났다. 나는 유복자였다. 아버지의 두 눈이 이 세상의 빛을 향해 감긴 지 여섯 달 만에 내 두 눈이 그 빛을 향해 열렸다."], ["I was a posthumous child.", "아버지가 나를 한 번도 보지 못했다는 사실을 떠올리면 지금도 어딘가 묘한 기분이 든다."], ["My father’s eyes had closed upon the light of this world six months, when mine opened on it.", "그리고 더 묘한 것은, 교회 묘지에 선 아버지의 하얀 묘비를 두고 어린 시절 처음 품었던 어렴풋한 기억이다. 우리 집 작은 거실은 난롯불과 촛불로 따뜻하고 환한데, 그 묘비만 캄캄한 밤 홀로 바깥에 누워 있다는 생각에 나는 형언할 수 없는 연민을 느끼곤 했다."], ["There is something strange to me, even now, in the reflection that he never saw me; and something stranger yet in the shadowy remembrance that I have of my first childish associations with his white grave-stone in the churchyard, and of the indefinable compassion I used to feel for it lying out alone there in the dark night, when our little parlour was warm and bright with fire and candle, and the doors of our house were--almost cruelly, it seemed to me sometimes--bolted and locked against it.", "게다가 우리 집 문은 — 때로는 거의 매정하다 싶을 만큼 — 그 묘비를 막아 빗장을 지르고 자물쇠로 잠겨 있었다. * 유복자: 아버지가 죽은 뒤에 태어난 자식. * 기니: 옛 영국 화폐 단위로 일 파운드 일 실링에 해당함."]], [["An aunt of my father’s, and consequently a great-aunt of mine, of whom I shall have more to relate by and by, was the principal magnate of our family.", "아버지의 고모, 따라서 나에게는 대고모뻘 되는 분이, 우리 집안의 가장 큰 어른이었다. 이분에 대해서는 차차 더 이야기할 일이 많을 것이다."], ["Miss Trotwood, or Miss Betsey, as my poor mother always called her, when she sufficiently overcame her dread of this formidable personage to mention her at all (which was seldom), had been married to a husband younger than herself, who was very handsome, except in the sense of the homely adage, ‘handsome is, that handsome does’--for he was strongly suspected of having beaten Miss Betsey, and even of having once, on a disputed question of supplies, made some hasty but determined arrangements to throw her out of a two pair of stairs’ window.", "트롯우드 양, 또는 내 가엾은 어머니가 늘 부르던 대로 베시 양은, 이 만만찮은 인물을 입에 올릴 정도로 두려움을 가까스로 이겨 냈을 때 (그런 일은 드물었다) 그렇게 불렸는데, 자기보다 나이 어린 남편과 결혼했었다. 그 남편은 아주 잘생겼으되, '잘난 사람은 잘난 일을 한다'는 흔한 속담의 뜻으로 따지면 결코 잘난 사람이 아니었다 — 그가 베시 양을 때렸다는 의심이 강하게 돌았고, 심지어 한번은 생활비 문제로 다투다가 그녀를 이 층 창밖으로 내던지려고 성급하지만 단호한 채비를 한 적이 있다는 소문까지 있었기 때문이다."], ["These evidences of an incompatibility of temper induced Miss Betsey to pay him off, and effect a separation by mutual consent.", "이렇게 성미가 맞지 않는다는 증거들이 쌓이자 베시 양은 그에게 돈을 쥐여 주고 합의하에 갈라서기로 했다."], ["He went to India with his capital, and there, according to a wild legend in our family, he was once seen riding on an elephant, in company with a Baboon; but I think it must have been a Baboo--or a Begum.", "남편은 자기 몫의 돈을 챙겨 인도로 떠났고, 우리 집안에 떠도는 황당한 전설에 따르면, 그곳에서 한번은 비비를 거느리고 코끼리를 타고 다니는 모습이 목격되었다고 한다. 하지만 내 생각에 그건 비비가 아니라 인도 신사였거나 — 아니면 인도 귀부인이었지 싶다."], ["Anyhow, from India tidings of his death reached home, within ten years.", "어쨌거나 그가 죽었다는 소식이 인도에서 본국으로 전해진 것은 십 년이 채 안 되어서였다."], ["How they affected my aunt, nobody knew; for immediately upon the separation, she took her maiden name again, bought a cottage in a hamlet on the sea-coast a long way off, established herself there as a single woman with one servant, and was understood to live secluded, ever afterwards, in an inflexible retirement.", "그 소식이 고모님에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 갈라서자마자 고모님은 처녀 적 성을 다시 쓰기 시작했고, 멀리 떨어진 바닷가 외딴 마을에 작은 오두막을 사서, 그곳에 하녀 하나만 두고 독신녀로 자리를 잡았으며, 그 후로는 줄곧 굽힐 줄 모르는 은둔 속에 외따로 산다고 전해졌다."]], [["My father had once been a favourite of hers, I believe; but she was mortally affronted by his marriage, on the ground that my mother was ‘a wax doll’.", "아버지는 한때 고모님의 귀여움을 받았던 모양이다. 그러나 고모님은 아버지의 결혼에 죽도록 모욕을 느꼈는데, 내 어머니가 '밀랍 인형'이라는 이유에서였다."], ["She had never seen my mother, but she knew her to be not yet twenty.", "고모님은 어머니를 한 번도 본 적이 없었으나, 어머니가 아직 스무 살도 안 됐다는 것은 알고 있었다."], ["My father and Miss Betsey never met again.", "아버지와 베시 양은 두 번 다시 만나지 않았다."], ["He was double my mother’s age when he married, and of but a delicate constitution.", "아버지는 결혼할 때 어머니보다 나이가 곱절이었고, 몸도 그리 튼튼하지 못했다."], ["He died a year afterwards, and, as I have said, six months before I came into the world.", "그리고 일 년 뒤에 세상을 떠났는데, 앞서 말했듯이 내가 세상에 나오기 여섯 달 전이었다."]], [["This was the state of matters, on the afternoon of, what I may be excused for calling, that eventful and important Friday.", "그 운명적이고 중요한 금요일이라고 불러도 봐 줄 만한 날의 오후, 사정은 이러했다."], ["I can make no claim therefore to have known, at that time, how matters stood; or to have any remembrance, founded on the evidence of my own senses, of what follows.", "그러니 나는 그때 사정이 어떠했는지를 그 자리에서 알고 있었다고 주장할 수 없고, 이제부터 이야기할 일들을 내 감각의 증거에 기대어 기억한다고 내세울 수도 없다."]], [["My mother was sitting by the fire, but poorly in health, and very low in spirits, looking at it through her tears, and desponding heavily about herself and the fatherless little stranger, who was already welcomed by some grosses of prophetic pins, in a drawer upstairs, to a world not at all excited on the subject of his arrival; my mother, I say, was sitting by the fire, that bright, windy March afternoon, very timid and sad, and very doubtful of ever coming alive out of the trial that was before her, when, lifting her eyes as she dried them, to the window opposite, she saw a strange lady coming up the garden.", "어머니는 난롯가에 앉아 있었는데, 몸은 약했고 마음은 한없이 가라앉아, 눈물 너머로 불을 바라보며 자기 자신과 아비 없는 어린 손님 — 위층 서랍 속 점지의 핀 몇 그로스가 벌써 환영하고 있던 그 손님 — 을 생각하며 무겁게 절망하고 있었다. 그 손님이 도착하는 일에 세상은 조금도 들떠 있지 않았다. 다시 말하건대 어머니는, 바람 부는 화창한 삼월 오후에, 난롯가에 앉아, 몹시 겁먹고 슬픈 채로, 앞에 놓인 시련에서 과연 살아 나올 수 있을지 깊이 의심하고 있었다. 그러다 눈물을 닦으며 맞은편 창문으로 눈을 들었을 때, 낯선 부인이 정원을 가로질러 다가오는 것을 보았다."]], [["My mother had a sure foreboding at the second glance, that it was Miss Betsey.", "어머니는 두 번째로 흘끗 본 순간, 그분이 베시 양이라는 확실한 예감이 들었다."], ["The setting sun was glowing on the strange lady, over the garden-fence, and she came walking up to the door with a fell rigidity of figure and composure of countenance that could have belonged to nobody else.", "정원 울타리 너머로 지는 햇살이 그 낯선 부인을 붉게 비추고 있었고, 그녀는 다른 누구의 것일 수도 없는 무시무시할 만큼 꼿꼿한 자태와 침착한 낯빛으로 문을 향해 걸어 올라왔다."]], [["When she reached the house, she gave another proof of her identity.", "집에 다다랐을 때, 부인은 자기가 누구인지를 또 한 번 증명해 보였다."], ["My father had often hinted that she seldom conducted herself like any ordinary Christian; and now, instead of ringing the bell, she came and looked in at that identical window, pressing the end of her nose against the glass to that extent, that my poor dear mother used to say it became perfectly flat and white in a moment.", "아버지는 그분이 좀처럼 여느 신실한 사람처럼 처신하지 않는다고 종종 넌지시 말하곤 했는데, 과연 그녀는 초인종을 울리는 대신, 바로 그 창문 앞으로 와서 안을 들여다보았다. 어찌나 코끝을 유리에 바짝 눌렀던지, 내 가엾은 어머니는 그 코가 순식간에 납작하고 하얗게 변했다고 늘 말하곤 했다."]], [["She gave my mother such a turn, that I have always been convinced I am indebted to Miss Betsey for having been born on a Friday.", "부인이 어머니를 어찌나 놀라게 했던지, 나는 내가 금요일에 태어난 것이 베시 양 덕분이라고 늘 굳게 믿어 왔다."]], [["My mother had left her chair in her agitation, and gone behind it in the corner.", "어머니는 너무 놀란 나머지 의자에서 일어나 그 뒤편 구석으로 가 버렸다."], ["Miss Betsey, looking round the room, slowly and inquiringly, began on the other side, and carried her eyes on, like a Saracen’s Head in a Dutch clock, until they reached my mother.", "베시 양은 천천히 캐묻듯이 방 안을 둘러보았는데, 반대편에서 시작해, 네덜란드제 시계의 사라센 머리 인형처럼 눈동자를 빙 돌려 가다가, 마침내 어머니에게 이르렀다."], ["Then she made a frown and a gesture to my mother, like one who was accustomed to be obeyed, to come and open the door.", "그러고는 명령에 복종하는 데 익숙한 사람답게, 와서 문을 열라는 듯 어머니에게 눈살을 찌푸리며 손짓했다."], ["My mother went.", "어머니는 갔다."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드 부인이시겠지?\""], ["David Copperfield, I think,’ said Miss Betsey; the emphasis referring, perhaps, to my mother’s mourning weeds, and her condition.", "베시 양이 말했다. 그 말투에 실린 강조는 아마도 어머니의 상복과 그 처지를 가리키는 것이었으리라."]], [["‘Yes,’ said my mother, faintly.", "\"네.\" 어머니가 가냘프게 대답했다."]], [["‘Miss Trotwood,’ said the visitor.", "\"트롯우드 양이오.\" 손님이 말했다."], ["‘You have heard of her, I dare say?’", "\"내 얘기는 들어 봤겠지?\""]], [["My mother answered she had had that pleasure.", "어머니는 그런 영광을 누렸노라고 대답했다."], ["And she had a disagreeable consciousness of not appearing to imply that it had been an overpowering pleasure.", "그러면서, 그 영광이 그리 압도적인 것이었다는 뜻으로 들리지 않는 것 같아 마음이 영 불편했다."]], [["‘Now you see her,’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"이제 직접 보는구먼.\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["My mother bent her head, and begged her to walk in.", "어머니는 고개를 숙이고 안으로 드시라고 청했다."]], [["They went into the parlour my mother had come from, the fire in the best room on the other side of the passage not being lighted--not having been lighted, indeed, since my father’s funeral; and when they were both seated, and Miss Betsey said nothing, my mother, after vainly trying to restrain herself, began to cry.", "두 사람은 어머니가 나왔던 거실로 들어갔다. 복도 건너편 제일 좋은 방의 난로에는 불이 지펴져 있지 않았으니 — 사실 아버지의 장례식 이후로 한 번도 불을 지핀 적이 없었다 — 그래서였다. 둘이 다 자리에 앉았는데 베시 양이 아무 말도 하지 않자, 어머니는 애써 참아 보았지만 끝내 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["‘Oh tut, tut, tut!’ said Miss Betsey, in a hurry.", "\"어허, 쯧, 쯧, 쯧!\" 베시 양이 황급히 말했다."], ["‘Don’t do that!", "\"그러지 마!"], ["Come, come!’", "자, 자!\""]], [["My mother couldn’t help it notwithstanding, so she cried until she had had her cry out.", "그래도 어머니는 어쩔 수가 없어서, 실컷 울고 나서야 그쳤다."]], [["‘Take off your cap, child,’ said Miss Betsey, ‘and let me see you.’", "\"모자를 벗어 봐, 얘야.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"얼굴 좀 보게.\""]], [["My mother was too much afraid of her to refuse compliance with this odd request, if she had any disposition to do so.", "어머니는 설령 거절하고 싶은 마음이 있었더라도 그분이 너무 무서워서, 이 별난 부탁을 거역하지 못했다."], ["Therefore she did as she was told, and did it with such nervous hands that her hair (which was luxuriant and beautiful) fell all about her face.", "그래서 시키는 대로 했는데, 손이 어찌나 떨렸던지 머리카락이 (숱이 많고 아름다웠다) 온 얼굴로 흘러내렸다."]], [["‘Why, bless my heart!’ exclaimed Miss Betsey.", "\"어머나, 세상에!\" 베시 양이 소리쳤다."], ["‘You are a very Baby!’", "\"넌 완전 아기로구나!\""]], [["My mother was, no doubt, unusually youthful in appearance even for her years; she hung her head, as if it were her fault, poor thing, and said, sobbing, that indeed she was afraid she was but a childish widow, and would be but a childish mother if she lived.", "어머니는 분명 제 나이치고도 유난히 앳돼 보였다. 어머니는 그것이 제 잘못이라도 되는 양 고개를 떨구더니, 흐느끼며 말하기를, 정말이지 자기는 어린애 같은 과부일 뿐이고, 살아남는다 해도 어린애 같은 어미밖에 못 될까 봐 두렵다고 했다."], ["In a short pause which ensued, she had a fancy that she felt Miss Betsey touch her hair, and that with no ungentle hand; but, looking at her, in her timid hope, she found that lady sitting with the skirt of her dress tucked up, her hands folded on one knee, and her feet upon the fender, frowning at the fire.", "잠깐 침묵이 흐르는 사이, 어머니는 베시 양이 자기 머리카락을 만지는 듯한, 그것도 결코 무뚝뚝하지 않은 손길로 만지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그러나 겁먹은 채로 한 가닥 희망을 품고 그분을 올려다보았을 때, 그 부인은 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고 두 손을 한쪽 무릎 위에 포갠 채, 두 발을 난로 가리개에 올려놓고 불을 향해 눈살을 찌푸리고 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘In the name of Heaven,’ said Miss Betsey, suddenly, ‘why Rookery?’", "\"맙소사, 도대체,\" 베시 양이 불쑥 말했다. \"왜 까마귀 둥지냐?\""]], [["‘Do you mean the house, ma’am?’ asked my mother.", "\"집 말씀이세요, 부인?\" 어머니가 물었다."]], [["‘Why Rookery?’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"왜 까마귀 둥지냐고.\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["‘Cookery would have been more to the purpose, if you had had any practical ideas of life, either of you.’", "\"너희 둘 중 누구라도 살림에 대한 실속 있는 생각이 조금이라도 있었다면, 차라리 부엌살림이 더 어울렸을 텐데.\""]], [["‘The name was Mr. Copperfield’s choice,’ returned my mother.", "\"그 이름은 코퍼필드 씨가 고른 거예요.\" 어머니가 대답했다."], ["‘When he bought the house, he liked to think that there were rooks about it.’", "\"그이가 이 집을 살 때, 둘레에 까마귀가 산다고 생각하는 게 좋다고 했어요.\""]], [["The evening wind made such a disturbance just now, among some tall old elm-trees at the bottom of the garden, that neither my mother nor Miss Betsey could forbear glancing that way.", "마침 그때 저녁 바람이 정원 끝에 선 키 큰 늙은 느릅나무들 사이를 어찌나 휘저어 놓던지, 어머니도 베시 양도 그쪽을 흘끗거리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["As the elms bent to one another, like giants who were whispering secrets, and after a few seconds of such repose, fell into a violent flurry, tossing their wild arms about, as if their late confidences were really too wicked for their peace of mind, some weatherbeaten ragged old rooks’-nests, burdening their higher branches, swung like wrecks upon a stormy sea.", "느릅나무들은 비밀을 속삭이는 거인들처럼 서로에게 몸을 기울이다가, 그렇게 몇 초쯤 잠잠하더니, 갑자기 격렬하게 술렁이며 사나운 팔을 마구 휘둘러 댔다. 마치 방금 주고받은 속내가 마음의 평화를 깨뜨릴 만큼 너무도 사악하기라도 한 듯이. 그 높은 가지에 매달려 무게를 더하던 비바람에 닳고 해진 낡은 까마귀 둥지 몇 개가, 폭풍우 치는 바다 위 난파선처럼 흔들렸다."]], [["‘Where are the birds?’ asked Miss Betsey.", "\"새들은 어디 있나?\" 베시 양이 물었다."]], [["‘The--?’ My mother had been thinking of something else.", "\"네에—?\" 어머니는 다른 생각에 잠겨 있던 참이었다."]], [["‘The rooks--what has become of them?’ asked Miss Betsey.", "\"까마귀들 말이야 — 다들 어디로 갔느냔 말이야.\" 베시 양이 물었다."]], [["‘There have not been any since we have lived here,’ said my mother.", "\"우리가 여기 산 뒤로는 한 마리도 없었어요.\""], ["‘We thought--Mr.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["Copperfield thought--it was quite a large rookery; but the nests were very old ones, and the birds have deserted them a long while.’", "\"우린 생각했어요 — 코퍼필드 씨는 생각했지요 — 꽤 큰 까마귀 둥지라고요. 하지만 둥지가 아주 오래된 거라, 새들은 진작에 그걸 버리고 떠난 거였어요.\""]], [["‘David Copperfield all over!’ cried Miss Betsey.", "\"영락없이 데이비드 코퍼필드로구나!\" 베시 양이 소리쳤다."], ["‘David Copperfield from head to foot!", "\"머리끝부터 발끝까지 데이비드 코퍼필드야!"], ["Calls a house a rookery when there’s not a rook near it, and takes the birds on trust, because he sees the nests!’", "까마귀 한 마리 얼씬 안 하는데 집을 까마귀 둥지라 부르고, 둥지가 보인다고 새들이 거기 있겠거니 곧이곧대로 믿어 버리다니!\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨는,\""], ["Copperfield,’ returned my mother, ‘is dead, and if you dare to speak unkindly of him to me--’", "어머니가 받아쳤다. \"이미 세상을 떠난 사람이에요. 그러니 그이를 두고 제 앞에서 함부로 험담하시려거든—\""]], [["My poor dear mother, I suppose, had some momentary intention of committing an assault and battery upon my aunt, who could easily have settled her with one hand, even if my mother had been in far better training for such an encounter than she was that evening.", "내 가엾은 어머니는, 짐작건대, 한순간 고모님에게 폭력이라도 휘두를 작정이었던 듯하다. 그러나 고모님은 한 손만으로도 어머니를 쉽게 제압했을 테고, 설령 그날 저녁 어머니가 그런 맞대결에 훨씬 잘 단련된 상태였다 해도 마찬가지였을 것이다."], ["But it passed with the action of rising from her chair; and she sat down again very meekly, and fainted.", "하지만 그 기세는 의자에서 몸을 일으키는 동작과 함께 스러졌고, 어머니는 다시 아주 얌전히 주저앉더니, 그대로 정신을 잃었다."]], [["When she came to herself, or when Miss Betsey had restored her, whichever it was, she found the latter standing at the window.", "어머니가 스스로 정신을 차렸는지, 아니면 베시 양이 깨운 것인지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 어머니가 깨어났을 때 그분은 창가에 서 있었다."], ["The twilight was by this time shading down into darkness; and dimly as they saw each other, they could not have done that without the aid of the fire.", "그 무렵 황혼은 이미 어둠으로 짙어 가고 있었다. 두 사람이 서로를 어렴풋이나마 알아볼 수 있었던 것도 난롯불의 도움이 없었다면 불가능했을 것이다."]], [["‘Well?’ said Miss Betsey, coming back to her chair, as if she had only been taking a casual look at the prospect; ‘and when do you expect--’", "\"자?\" 그저 무심코 바깥 경치나 둘러본 것처럼 베시 양이 의자로 돌아오며 말했다. \"그래, 언제쯤 나올—\""]], [["‘I am all in a tremble,’ faltered my mother.", "\"온몸이 다 떨려요.\" 어머니가 더듬거렸다."], ["‘I don’t know what’s the matter. I shall die, I am sure!’", "\"무슨 일인지 모르겠어요. 저는 분명 죽을 거예요, 틀림없어요!\""]], [["‘No, no, no,’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["‘Have some tea.’", "\"차나 좀 마셔.\""]], [["‘Oh dear me, dear me, do you think it will do me any good?’ cried my mother in a helpless manner.", "\"아이고 저런, 저런, 그게 제게 조금이라도 도움이 될까요?\" 어머니가 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Of course it will,’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"당연히 되지.\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["‘It’s nothing but fancy.", "\"다 마음 탓이야."], ["What do you call your girl?’", "너희 집 계집애 이름이 뭐냐?\""]], [["‘I don’t know that it will be a girl, yet, ma’am,’ said my mother innocently.", "\"아직 그게 여자애일지 어떨지 모르겠는데요, 부인.\" 어머니가 천진하게 말했다."]], [["‘Bless the Baby!’ exclaimed Miss Betsey, unconsciously quoting the second sentiment of the pincushion in the drawer upstairs, but applying it to my mother instead of me, ‘I don’t mean that.", "\"아이고 이 아기야!\" 베시 양이 외쳤는데, 자기도 모르게 위층 서랍 속 바늘겨레의 두 번째 글귀를 인용한 셈이었으나, 나 대신 어머니에게 갖다 붙인 것이었다. \"그 얘기가 아니라."], ["I mean your servant-girl.’", "너희 집 하녀 말이야.\""]], [["‘Peggotty,’ said my mother.", "\"패거티예요.\" 어머니가 말했다."]], [["‘Peggotty!’ repeated Miss Betsey, with some indignation.", "\"패거티!\" 베시 양이 다소 분개하며 되받았다."], ["‘Do you mean to say, child, that any human being has gone into a Christian church, and got herself named Peggotty?’ ‘It’s her surname,’ said my mother, faintly. ‘Mr.", "\"얘야, 너 지금, 멀쩡한 사람이 신실한 교회에 들어가서 패거티라는 이름을 받았다고 말하는 게냐?\" \"그건 그 애 성이에요.\" 어머니가 가냘프게 말했다."], ["Copperfield called her by it, because her Christian name was the same as mine.’", "\"그 애 세례명이 제 이름과 같아서, 코퍼필드 씨가 성으로 불렀어요.\""]], [["‘Here! Peggotty!’ cried Miss Betsey, opening the parlour door. ‘Tea.", "\"이봐! 패거티!\" 베시 양이 거실 문을 열며 소리쳤다. \"차."], ["Your mistress is a little unwell.", "네 주인마님이 몸이 좀 안 좋으시다."], ["Don’t dawdle.’", "꾸물대지 마.\""]], [["Having issued this mandate with as much potentiality as if she had been a recognized authority in the house ever since it had been a house, and having looked out to confront the amazed Peggotty coming along the passage with a candle at the sound of a strange voice, Miss Betsey shut the door again, and sat down as before: with her feet on the fender, the skirt of her dress tucked up, and her hands folded on one knee.", "집이 집으로 세워진 이래 줄곧 그 집의 공인된 권위자였기라도 한 듯한 위세로 이 명령을 내리고는, 낯선 목소리에 촛불을 들고 복도를 따라 어리둥절하게 다가오는 패거티와 맞닥뜨리려고 밖을 한번 내다본 다음, 베시 양은 다시 문을 닫고 아까처럼 앉았다. 두 발은 난로 가리개에 올리고, 치맛자락은 걷어 올리고, 두 손은 한쪽 무릎 위에 포갠 채로."]], [["‘You were speaking about its being a girl,’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"여자애일 거라는 얘기를 하던 참이지.\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["‘I have no doubt it will be a girl. I have a presentiment that it must be a girl.", "\"난 그게 여자애일 거라고 조금도 의심치 않아. 여자애일 거라는 예감이 들거든."], ["Now child, from the moment of the birth of this girl--’", "자 얘야, 이 여자애가 태어나는 그 순간부터—\""]], [["‘Perhaps boy,’ my mother took the liberty of putting in.", "\"어쩌면 사내애일지도요.\" 어머니가 외람되이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘I tell you I have a presentiment that it must be a girl,’ returned Miss Betsey.", "\"내가 여자애일 거라는 예감이 든다고 했잖니.\" 베시 양이 되받았다."], ["‘Don’t contradict.", "\"토 달지 마."], ["From the moment of this girl’s birth, child, I intend to be her friend.", "얘야, 이 여자애가 태어나는 그 순간부터, 나는 그 애의 벗이 되어 줄 작정이다."], ["I intend to be her godmother, and I beg you’ll call her Betsey Trotwood Copperfield.", "그 애의 대모가 되어 줄 작정이야. 그러니 그 애를 베시 트롯우드 코퍼필드라고 불러 주렴."], ["There must be no mistakes in life with THIS Betsey Trotwood.", "이 베시 트롯우드만큼은 인생에서 실수가 있어선 안 돼."], ["There must be no trifling with HER affections, poor dear.", "가엾은 것, 그 애의 마음을 가지고 함부로 장난쳐서도 안 되고."], ["She must be well brought up, and well guarded from reposing any foolish confidences where they are not deserved.", "잘 길러야 하고, 받을 자격도 없는 데다가 어리석은 정을 함부로 쏟지 않도록 잘 지켜 줘야 해."], ["I must make that MY care.’", "그건 내가 맡아야 할 일이야.\""]], [["There was a twitch of Miss Betsey’s head, after each of these sentences, as if her own old wrongs were working within her, and she repressed any plainer reference to them by strong constraint.", "이런 말을 한마디씩 내뱉을 때마다 베시 양은 머리를 한 번씩 씰룩거렸는데, 마치 자기가 옛날에 당했던 억울한 일들이 속에서 다시 들끓는데, 그것을 노골적으로 입에 올리지 않으려 안간힘으로 억누르는 듯했다."], ["So my mother suspected, at least, as she observed her by the low glimmer of the fire: too much scared by Miss Betsey, too uneasy in herself, and too subdued and bewildered altogether, to observe anything very clearly, or to know what to say.", "적어도 어머니는 그렇게 짐작했다. 어머니는 낮게 깜박이는 난롯불 빛에 기대어 그분을 살폈는데, 베시 양이 너무 무섭고, 제 처지가 너무 불안하고, 또 온통 주눅 들고 어리둥절해 있던 탓에, 무엇 하나 또렷이 살피지도, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지도 못했다."]], [["‘And was David good to you, child?’ asked Miss Betsey, when she had been silent for a little while, and these motions of her head had gradually ceased.", "\"그래, 데이비드가 너한테 잘해 줬니, 얘야?\" 잠시 침묵하다가, 머리를 씰룩이던 동작이 차츰 잦아들었을 때 베시 양이 물었다."], ["‘Were you comfortable together?’", "\"둘이 같이 편안하게 지냈어?\""]], [["‘We were very happy,’ said my mother. ‘Mr.", "\"우린 무척 행복했어요.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["Copperfield was only too good to me.’", "\"코퍼필드 씨는 저한테 너무도 잘해 줬어요.\""]], [["‘What, he spoilt you, I suppose?’ returned Miss Betsey.", "\"뭐, 그러니까 널 응석받이로 만들어 놨다는 거지?\" 베시 양이 되받았다."]], [["‘For being quite alone and dependent on myself in this rough world again, yes, I fear he did indeed,’ sobbed my mother.", "\"이 거친 세상에 다시 홀로 남아 제 한 몸을 제가 건사해야 하는 처지로 보자면, 네, 정말로 그랬던 게 아닌가 싶어요.\" 어머니가 흐느끼며 말했다."]], [["‘Well!", "\"자!"], ["Don’t cry!’ said Miss Betsey.", "울지 마!\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["‘You were not equally matched, child--if any two people can be equally matched--and so I asked the question. You were an orphan, weren’t you?’ ‘Yes.’", "\"너희는 짝이 잘 맞지 않았어, 얘야 — 세상에 짝이 잘 맞는 두 사람이라는 게 있기나 하다면 말이지만 — 그래서 물어본 거야. 너 고아였지?\" \"네.\""]], [["‘And a governess?’", "\"가정교사였고?\""]], [["‘I was nursery-governess in a family where Mr. Copperfield came to visit. Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨가 드나들던 집에서 보모 겸 가정교사로 있었어요."], ["Copperfield was very kind to me, and took a great deal of notice of me, and paid me a good deal of attention, and at last proposed to me.", "코퍼필드 씨가 저한테 무척 다정했고, 저를 많이 눈여겨봐 주고, 마음도 많이 써 주다가, 마침내 청혼을 했어요."], ["And I accepted him.", "그래서 받아들였고요."], ["And so we were married,’ said my mother simply.", "그렇게 우린 결혼했어요.\" 어머니가 담담하게 말했다."]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["Poor Baby!’ mused Miss Betsey, with her frown still bent upon the fire.", "가엾은 아기 같으니!\" 베시 양은 여전히 불을 향해 눈살을 찌푸린 채 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."], ["‘Do you know anything?’", "\"넌 뭐 아는 게 있긴 하니?\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, ma’am,’ faltered my mother.", "\"네? 무슨 말씀이신지요, 부인.\" 어머니가 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘About keeping house, for instance,’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"이를테면 살림하는 거 말이야.\" 베시 양이 말했다."]], [["‘Not much, I fear,’ returned my mother.", "\"많이는 못해요, 그게.\" 어머니가 대답했다."], ["‘Not so much as I could wish.", "\"제가 바라는 만큼은요."], ["But Mr. Copperfield was teaching me--’", "하지만 코퍼필드 씨가 가르쳐 주고 있었—\""]], [["(‘Much he knew about it himself!’) said Miss Betsey in a parenthesis.", "(\"자기는 그런 걸 뭐 그리 많이 알았다고!\") 베시 양이 곁다리로 한마디 했다."], ["--‘And I hope I should have improved, being very anxious to learn, and he very patient to teach me, if the great misfortune of his death’--my mother broke down again here, and could get no farther.", "\"—저도 무척 배우고 싶었고 그이도 무척 참을성 있게 가르쳐 줬으니, 그이가 죽는 그 큰 불행만 없었다면 분명 나아졌을 거예요.\" 여기서 어머니는 또 무너져 내려, 더는 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["‘Well, well!’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"그래, 그래!\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["--‘I kept my housekeeping-book regularly, and balanced it with Mr. Copperfield every night,’ cried my mother in another burst of distress, and breaking down again.", "\"—가계부도 꼬박꼬박 적었고, 매일 밤 코퍼필드 씨하고 같이 셈을 맞춰 봤어요.\" 어머니가 또 한바탕 슬픔을 터뜨리며 외치다가 또 무너졌다."]], [["‘Well, well!’ said Miss Betsey.", "\"그래, 그래!\" 베시 양이 말했다."], ["‘Don’t cry any more.’ --‘And I am sure we never had a word of difference respecting it, except when Mr.", "\"더는 울지 마.\" \"—그리고 그 일로 우린 정말이지 단 한마디도 다툰 적이 없어요."], ["Copperfield objected to my threes and fives being too much like each other, or to my putting curly tails to my sevens and nines,’ resumed my mother in another burst, and breaking down again.", "그저 코퍼필드 씨가 제 삼 자랑 오 자가 너무 비슷하다고 한다거나, 칠 자랑 구 자에 꼬리를 배배 꼬아 붙인다고 한 적 말고는요.\" 어머니가 또 한바탕 터뜨리며 말하다가 또 무너졌다."]], [["‘You’ll make yourself ill,’ said Miss Betsey, ‘and you know that will not be good either for you or for my god-daughter. Come!", "\"너 그러다 병나겠다.\" 베시 양이 말했다. \"그게 너한테도, 내 대녀한테도 좋을 게 없다는 걸 너도 알잖니. 자!"], ["You mustn’t do it!’", "그러면 안 돼!\""]], [["This argument had some share in quieting my mother, though her increasing indisposition had a larger one.", "이 말도 어머니를 가라앉히는 데 얼마쯤 몫을 했지만, 점점 심해지는 진통이 훨씬 큰 몫을 했다."], ["There was an interval of silence, only broken by Miss Betsey’s occasionally ejaculating ‘Ha!’ as she sat with her feet upon the fender.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 베시 양이 두 발을 난로 가리개에 올린 채 이따금 \"하!\" 하고 내뱉는 소리만이 그 정적을 깰 뿐이었다."]], [["‘David had bought an annuity for himself with his money, I know,’ said she, by and by.", "\"데이비드가 제 돈으로 연금을 들어 뒀다는 건 나도 알아.\" 이윽고 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘What did he do for you?’", "\"널 위해선 뭘 해 뒀니?\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨는,\""], ["Copperfield,’ said my mother, answering with some difficulty, ‘was so considerate and good as to secure the reversion of a part of it to me.’", "어머니가 힘겹게 대답했다. \"참 사려 깊고 마음씨 고운 사람이라, 그 연금의 일부를 제게 물려주도록 해 뒀어요.\""]], [["‘How much?’ asked Miss Betsey.", "\"얼마나?\" 베시 양이 물었다."]], [["‘A hundred and five pounds a year,’ said my mother.", "\"한 해에 백오 파운드요.\" 어머니가 말했다."]], [["‘He might have done worse,’ said my aunt.", "\"그만하면 더 못한 짓도 할 수 있었을 텐데.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["The word was appropriate to the moment.", "* 연금: 일정 금액을 미리 내고 살아 있는 동안 또는 정해진 기간에 정기적으로 일정액을 받는 제도. * 위자료·유산 따위로 받는 정기 수입."], ["My mother was so much worse that Peggotty, coming in with the teaboard and candles, and seeing at a glance how ill she was,--as Miss Betsey might have done sooner if there had been light enough,--conveyed her upstairs to her own room with all speed; and immediately dispatched Ham Peggotty, her nephew, who had been for some days past secreted in the house, unknown to my mother, as a special messenger in case of emergency, to fetch the nurse and doctor.", "그 말은 그 순간에 꼭 들어맞았다. 어머니가 너무도 위중해진 탓에, 차 쟁반과 촛불을 들고 들어온 패거티가, 한눈에 마님이 얼마나 위독한지를 알아채고는 — 빛만 충분했더라면 베시 양도 진작 알아챘을 것이다 — 부랴부랴 어머니를 위층 방으로 옮겼다. 그러고는 며칠 전부터 어머니 몰래 집 안에 숨겨 두었던 — 만일의 비상사태에 대비한 특별 심부름꾼으로 둔 — 조카 햄 패거티를 곧장 보내, 산파와 의사를 불러오게 했다."]], [["Those allied powers were considerably astonished, when they arrived within a few minutes of each other, to find an unknown lady of portentous appearance, sitting before the fire, with her bonnet tied over her left arm, stopping her ears with jewellers’ cotton.", "이 두 협력자는 몇 분 간격으로 도착해서는, 난로 앞에 보닛을 왼팔에 걸쳐 묶은 채로 앉아, 보석상에서 쓰는 솜으로 두 귀를 틀어막고 있는, 심상찮은 행색의 낯선 부인을 발견하고는 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["Peggotty knowing nothing about her, and my mother saying nothing about her, she was quite a mystery in the parlour; and the fact of her having a magazine of jewellers’ cotton in her pocket, and sticking the article in her ears in that way, did not detract from the solemnity of her presence.", "패거티는 그 부인에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 어머니도 그녀에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 그녀는 거실 안에서 완전한 수수께끼였다. 게다가 주머니에 보석상 솜을 잔뜩 쟁여 두고는 그것을 그런 식으로 귀에 쑤셔 박고 있다는 사실도, 그녀가 풍기는 엄숙한 분위기를 조금도 덜어 주지 못했다."]], [["The doctor having been upstairs and come down again, and having satisfied himself, I suppose, that there was a probability of this unknown lady and himself having to sit there, face to face, for some hours, laid himself out to be polite and social. He was the meekest of his sex, the mildest of little men.", "의사는 위층에 올라갔다 다시 내려와서는, 짐작건대 이 낯선 부인과 자기가 몇 시간이고 마주 앉아 있어야 할 가능성이 크다는 것을 알아채고는, 정중하고 붙임성 있게 굴기로 작정한 모양이었다. 그는 같은 남자 중에서도 가장 온순한 사람이었고, 자그마한 남자들 가운데서도 가장 부드러운 사람이었다."], ["He sidled in and out of a room, to take up the less space.", "그는 자리를 덜 차지하려고 몸을 모로 비틀며 방을 드나들었다."], ["He walked as softly as the Ghost in Hamlet, and more slowly.", "그는 햄릿에 나오는 유령처럼 사뿐사뿐, 그보다 더 느릿느릿 걸었다."], ["He carried his head on one side, partly in modest depreciation of himself, partly in modest propitiation of everybody else.", "그는 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울이고 다녔는데, 한편으로는 겸손하게 자기를 낮추느라, 또 한편으로는 겸손하게 남들의 비위를 맞추느라 그랬다."], ["It is nothing to say that he hadn’t a word to throw at a dog.", "그가 개한테조차 던질 말 한마디가 없었다고 하면 약과다."], ["He couldn’t have thrown a word at a mad dog.", "그는 미친개한테도 말 한마디 던지지 못했을 사람이다."], ["He might have offered him one gently, or half a one, or a fragment of one; for he spoke as slowly as he walked; but he wouldn’t have been rude to him, and he couldn’t have been quick with him, for any earthly consideration.", "어쩌면 한마디쯤 부드럽게 건넸거나, 반 마디나 한마디 조각쯤 건넸을지는 몰라도 — 그는 걸음만큼이나 말도 느릿느릿했으니까 — 무슨 일이 있어도 미친개한테조차 무례하게 굴지 못했을 것이고, 어떤 세상의 사정을 들이대도 그를 다그칠 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Chillip, looking mildly at my aunt with his head on one side, and making her a little bow, said, in allusion to the jewellers’ cotton, as he softly touched his left ear:", "칠립 씨는 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 고모님을 부드럽게 바라보며 가볍게 고개를 숙여 인사하더니, 자기 왼쪽 귀를 살며시 만지며 그 보석상 솜을 빗대어 말했다."]], [["‘Some local irritation, ma’am?’", "\"어디 좀 거북하신 데라도 있으신가요, 부인?\""]], [["‘What!’ replied my aunt, pulling the cotton out of one ear like a cork.", "\"뭐라고!\" 고모님이 한쪽 귀에서 솜을 코르크 마개 뽑듯 쑥 빼내며 대꾸했다."]], [["Mr. Chillip was so alarmed by her abruptness--as he told my mother afterwards--that it was a mercy he didn’t lose his presence of mind.", "칠립 씨는 그 퉁명스러움에 어찌나 놀랐던지 — 나중에 어머니에게 그렇게 말했다 — 침착함을 잃지 않은 게 천만다행이라고 했다."], ["But he repeated sweetly:", "그래도 그는 상냥하게 다시 물었다."]], [["‘Some local irritation, ma’am?’", "\"어디 좀 거북하신 데라도 있으신가요, 부인?\""]], [["‘Nonsense!’ replied my aunt, and corked herself again, at one blow.", "\"쓸데없는 소리!\" 고모님이 대꾸하고는, 단번에 다시 귀를 틀어막았다."]], [["Mr. Chillip could do nothing after this, but sit and look at her feebly, as she sat and looked at the fire, until he was called upstairs again.", "이렇게 되니 칠립 씨로서는 별수 없이, 그저 앉아서 그녀가 불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 동안 그녀를 맥없이 바라보고 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그러다 다시 위층에서 부름을 받았다."], ["After some quarter of an hour’s absence, he returned.", "십오 분쯤 자리를 비웠다가 그는 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Well?’ said my aunt, taking the cotton out of the ear nearest to him.", "\"어떻소?\" 고모님이 그에게 가까운 쪽 귀에서 솜을 빼며 물었다."]], [["‘Well, ma’am,’ returned Mr.", "\"네, 부인,\" 칠립 씨가 대답했다."], ["Chillip, ‘we are--we are progressing slowly, ma’am.’", "\"저희가 — 저희가 더디게, 더디게 진척되고 있습니다, 부인.\""]], [["‘Ba--a--ah!’ said my aunt, with a perfect shake on the contemptuous interjection.", "\"체에—에—엣!\" 고모님이, 그 경멸 어린 감탄사를 떨림까지 완벽하게 실어 내뱉었다."], ["And corked herself as before.", "그러고는 또 아까처럼 귀를 틀어막았다."]], [["Really--really--as Mr. Chillip told my mother, he was almost shocked; speaking in a professional point of view alone, he was almost shocked.", "칠립 씨가 어머니에게 말하기를, 정말이지 — 정말이지 — 그는 거의 충격을 받았다고 했다. 순전히 직업적인 견지에서만 보아도, 그는 거의 충격을 받았다는 것이었다."], ["But he sat and looked at her, notwithstanding, for nearly two hours, as she sat looking at the fire, until he was again called out.", "그래도 그는 그녀가 불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 동안 거의 두 시간을 앉아서 그녀를 바라보고 있다가, 다시 불려 나갔다."], ["After another absence, he again returned.", "또 한참 자리를 비웠다가 그는 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Well?’ said my aunt, taking out the cotton on that side again.", "\"어떻소?\" 고모님이 그쪽 귀에서 다시 솜을 빼며 물었다."]], [["‘Well, ma’am,’ returned Mr.", "\"네, 부인,\" 칠립 씨가 대답했다."], ["Chillip, ‘we are--we are progressing slowly, ma’am.’", "\"저희가 — 저희가 더디게, 더디게 진척되고 있습니다, 부인.\""]], [["‘Ya--a--ah!’ said my aunt.", "\"이야—아—앗!\""], ["With such a snarl at him, that Mr. Chillip absolutely could not bear it.", "고모님이 어찌나 으르렁대며 쏘아붙였던지, 칠립 씨는 정말이지 도저히 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["It was really calculated to break his spirit, he said afterwards.", "그건 정말 사람 기를 꺾어 놓으려고 작정한 거나 다름없었다고, 그는 나중에 말했다."], ["He preferred to go and sit upon the stairs, in the dark and a strong draught, until he was again sent for.", "그래서 그는 차라리 계단에 나가 앉아, 어둠 속에서 거센 외풍을 맞으며, 다시 부름을 받을 때까지 기다리는 편을 택했다."]], [["Ham Peggotty, who went to the national school, and was a very dragon at his catechism, and who may therefore be regarded as a credible witness, reported next day, that happening to peep in at the parlour-door an hour after this, he was instantly descried by Miss Betsey, then walking to and fro in a state of agitation, and pounced upon before he could make his escape.", "햄 패거티는 국민학교에 다녔고 교리문답이라면 둘째가라면 서러울 정도였으니, 그런 만큼 믿을 만한 증인으로 봐도 좋을 것이다. 그가 이튿날 전한 바에 따르면, 이 일이 있고 한 시간쯤 뒤 마침 거실 문 안을 빼꼼 들여다보았다가, 그때 안절부절못하며 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하던 베시 양에게 곧장 들켜, 미처 달아나기도 전에 덥석 붙잡히고 말았다고 한다."], ["That there were now occasional sounds of feet and voices overhead which he inferred the cotton did not exclude, from the circumstance of his evidently being clutched by the lady as a victim on whom to expend her superabundant agitation when the sounds were loudest.", "그리고 그때쯤 위층에서 이따금 발소리며 사람 목소리가 들려왔는데, 그 소리가 가장 클 때마다 부인이 넘쳐흐르는 동요를 쏟아 낼 제물로 삼는 양 자기를 꽉 움켜쥐는 걸로 미루어, 솜으로도 그 소리까지 막지는 못한 모양이라고 했다."], ["That, marching him constantly up and down by the collar (as if he had been taking too much laudanum), she, at those times, shook him, rumpled his hair, made light of his linen, stopped his ears as if she confounded them with her own, and otherwise tousled and maltreated him.", "또 부인은 (그가 마치 아편을 과하게 먹기라도 한 듯이) 멱살을 잡고 줄곧 그를 위아래로 끌고 다니면서, 그럴 때마다 그를 흔들어 대고, 머리카락을 헝클어뜨리고, 셔츠를 함부로 다루고, 마치 자기 귀와 헷갈리기라도 한 듯 그의 귀를 틀어막고, 그 밖에도 이리저리 쥐어뜯고 들들 볶았다고 한다."], ["This was in part confirmed by his aunt, who saw him at half past twelve o’clock, soon after his release, and affirmed that he was then as red as I was.", "이 일은 그의 이모가 어느 정도 사실임을 확인해 주었는데, 그가 풀려난 직후인 열두 시 반에 그를 본 이모는, 그때 그의 얼굴이 나만큼이나 새빨갰다고 단언했다."]], [["The mild Mr. Chillip could not possibly bear malice at such a time, if at any time.", "온순한 칠립 씨는, 설령 다른 때라면 모를까, 이런 때에 도무지 앙심을 품을 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["He sidled into the parlour as soon as he was at liberty, and said to my aunt in his meekest manner:", "그는 풀려나자마자 몸을 모로 비틀며 거실로 들어와, 더없이 온순한 태도로 고모님에게 말했다."]], [["‘Well, ma’am, I am happy to congratulate you.’", "\"네, 부인, 기쁜 마음으로 축하드립니다.\""]], [["‘What upon?’ said my aunt, sharply.", "\"뭘 축하한다는 거요?\" 고모님이 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["Mr. Chillip was fluttered again, by the extreme severity of my aunt’s manner; so he made her a little bow and gave her a little smile, to mollify her.", "칠립 씨는 고모님의 지극히 매서운 태도에 또다시 안절부절못하더니, 그녀를 누그러뜨리려고 가볍게 고개를 숙이고 살짝 미소를 지어 보였다."]], [["‘Mercy on the man, what’s he doing!’ cried my aunt, impatiently.", "\"맙소사, 이 양반이 대체 뭘 하는 거야!\" 고모님이 조바심을 내며 외쳤다."], ["‘Can’t he speak?’", "\"말도 못해?\""]], [["‘Be calm, my dear ma’am,’ said Mr. Chillip, in his softest accents.", "\"진정하세요, 부인.\" 칠립 씨가 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘There is no longer any occasion for uneasiness, ma’am. Be calm.’", "\"이제 더는 마음 졸이실 일이 없습니다, 부인. 진정하세요.\""]], [["It has since been considered almost a miracle that my aunt didn’t shake him, and shake what he had to say, out of him.", "훗날 사람들은 이때 고모님이 칠립 씨를 붙잡고 흔들어, 그가 할 말까지 흔들어 빼내지 않은 것이 거의 기적이라고 여겼다."], ["She only shook her own head at him, but in a way that made him quail.", "고모님은 그저 그를 향해 자기 머리를 한 번 흔들었을 뿐인데, 그 모양새가 그를 움찔하게 만들었다."]], [["‘Well, ma’am,’ resumed Mr.", "\"네, 부인,\""], ["Chillip, as soon as he had courage, ‘I am happy to congratulate you.", "칠립 씨가 용기를 내자마자 말을 이었다. \"기쁜 마음으로 축하드립니다."], ["All is now over, ma’am, and well over.’", "이제 다 끝났습니다, 부인. 무사히 끝났어요.\""]], [["During the five minutes or so that Mr. Chillip devoted to the delivery of this oration, my aunt eyed him narrowly.", "칠립 씨가 이 일장 연설을 늘어놓는 데 바친 오 분쯤 되는 시간 동안, 고모님은 그를 빤히 뜯어보았다."]], [["‘How is she?’ said my aunt, folding her arms with her bonnet still tied on one of them.", "\"그 앤 어떻소?\" 보닛을 여전히 한쪽 팔에 걸친 채 두 팔을 포개며 고모님이 물었다."]], [["‘Well, ma’am, she will soon be quite comfortable, I hope,’ returned Mr.", "\"네, 부인, 곧 아주 편안해지실 겁니다, 그러길 바랍니다.\""], ["Chillip. ‘Quite as comfortable as we can expect a young mother to be, under these melancholy domestic circumstances.", "칠립 씨가 대답했다. \"이런 침울한 집안 사정에서 젊은 어머니가 누릴 수 있을 만큼은 편안해지실 거예요."], ["There cannot be any objection to your seeing her presently, ma’am. It may do her good.’", "이제 곧 만나 보셔도 아무 문제 없습니다, 부인. 만나 보시면 그분에게 도움이 될지도 모르고요.\""]], [["‘And SHE.", "\"그래서 그 앤."], ["How is SHE?’ said my aunt, sharply.", "그 앤 어떻소?\" 고모님이 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["Mr. Chillip laid his head a little more on one side, and looked at my aunt like an amiable bird.", "칠립 씨는 고개를 좀 더 한쪽으로 기울이며, 다정한 새처럼 고모님을 바라보았다."]], [["‘The baby,’ said my aunt.", "\"아기 말이야.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘How is she?’", "\"그 앤 어떻소?\""]], [["‘Ma’am,’ returned Mr.", "\"부인,\" 칠립 씨가 대답했다."], ["Chillip, ‘I apprehended you had known. It’s a boy.’", "\"이미 아신 줄 알았습니다. 사내아이입니다.\""]], [["My aunt said never a word, but took her bonnet by the strings, in the manner of a sling, aimed a blow at Mr. Chillip’s head with it, put it on bent, walked out, and never came back.", "고모님은 한마디도 하지 않고, 보닛 끈을 마치 투석기 끈처럼 거머쥐더니, 그것으로 칠립 씨 머리를 향해 한 방 후려갈기고는, 찌그러진 채로 머리에 눌러쓰고, 밖으로 걸어 나가, 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않았다."], ["She vanished like a discontented fairy; or like one of those supernatural beings, whom it was popularly supposed I was entitled to see; and never came back any more.", "그녀는 심술 난 요정처럼, 아니면 세상 사람들이 으레 내가 볼 자격이 있다고 여겼던 그 초자연적인 존재들 가운데 하나처럼 홀연히 사라졌고, 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않았다."]], [["No.", "그렇다."], ["I lay in my basket, and my mother lay in her bed; but Betsey Trotwood Copperfield was for ever in the land of dreams and shadows, the tremendous region whence I had so lately travelled; and the light upon the window of our room shone out upon the earthly bourne of all such travellers, and the mound above the ashes and the dust that once was he, without whom I had never been.", "나는 바구니에 누워 있었고, 어머니는 침대에 누워 있었다. 그러나 베시 트롯우드 코퍼필드는 영원히 꿈과 그림자의 나라, 내가 그토록 얼마 전에 떠나온 그 어마어마한 영역에 머무르게 되었다. 그리고 우리 방 창문에 비친 불빛은, 그 모든 나그네가 이르는 이승의 종착지를 비추었고, 한때 그였던 — 그가 없었더라면 나 또한 결코 없었을 — 재와 흙 위에 솟은 무덤을 비추고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 2. I OBSERVE", "# CHAPTER 2. I OBSERVE"]], [["The first objects that assume a distinct presence before me, as I look far back, into the blank of my infancy, are my mother with her pretty hair and youthful shape, and Peggotty with no shape at all, and eyes so dark that they seemed to darken their whole neighbourhood in her face, and cheeks and arms so hard and red that I wondered the birds didn’t peck her in preference to apples.", "저 멀리, 내 갓난 시절의 텅 빈 자리를 돌아볼 때, 가장 먼저 또렷한 모습으로 떠오르는 것은 예쁜 머리카락과 앳된 몸매를 가진 어머니, 그리고 몸매라곤 도무지 찾아볼 수 없는 패거티다. 패거티의 눈은 어찌나 검던지 얼굴에서 그 둘레까지 죄다 어둡게 물들이는 것 같았고, 두 뺨과 두 팔은 어찌나 단단하고 붉던지 새들이 사과 대신 그녀를 쪼아 먹지 않는 게 신기할 정도였다."]], [["I believe I can remember these two at a little distance apart, dwarfed to my sight by stooping down or kneeling on the floor, and I going unsteadily from the one to the other.", "내 생각에 나는 그 두 사람이 조금 떨어져 서 있던 모습을 기억할 수 있다. 둘 다 몸을 숙이거나 마룻바닥에 무릎을 꿇어 내 눈높이에 맞추느라 작아 보였고, 나는 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 비틀비틀 걸어가곤 했다."], ["I have an impression on my mind which I cannot distinguish from actual remembrance, of the touch of Peggotty’s forefinger as she used to hold it out to me, and of its being roughened by needlework, like a pocket nutmeg-grater.", "패거티가 나에게 내밀던 집게손가락의 감촉이, 마치 호주머니 속 육두구 강판처럼 바느질로 거칠어져 있던 그 느낌이 내 마음에 또렷이 남아 있는데, 나는 그것을 실제 기억과 구별해 낼 수가 없다."]], [["This may be fancy, though I think the memory of most of us can go farther back into such times than many of us suppose; just as I believe the power of observation in numbers of very young children to be quite wonderful for its closeness and accuracy.", "이게 그저 상상일지도 모르겠다. 그러나 나는 우리 대부분의 기억이 흔히들 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 먼 옛날까지 거슬러 올라갈 수 있다고 믿는다. 마찬가지로 나는 아주 어린 아이들 상당수의 관찰력이 그 세밀함과 정확함에서 자못 놀랍다고 믿는다."], ["Indeed, I think that most grown men who are remarkable in this respect, may with greater propriety be said not to have lost the faculty, than to have acquired it; the rather, as I generally observe such men to retain a certain freshness, and gentleness, and capacity of being pleased, which are also an inheritance they have preserved from their childhood.", "정말이지 내 생각에, 이 점에서 두드러진 어른들 대부분은 그 능력을 얻었다기보다 잃지 않은 것이라고 말하는 편이 더 맞을 듯하다. 더구나 내가 보기에 그런 사람들은 어떤 싱그러움과 부드러움, 그리고 무언가에 기뻐할 줄 아는 능력을 간직하고 있는데, 그것 또한 어린 시절에서 고스란히 지켜 온 유산이기 때문이다."]], [["I might have a misgiving that I am ‘meandering’ in stopping to say this, but that it brings me to remark that I build these conclusions, in part upon my own experience of myself; and if it should appear from anything I may set down in this narrative that I was a child of close observation, or that as a man I have a strong memory of my childhood, I undoubtedly lay claim to both of these characteristics.", "이런 말을 하려고 멈춰 서는 것이 '싸돌아다니는' 짓은 아닐까 싶어 마음이 켕기지만, 그래도 이 말 덕분에 나는 이런 결론들을 일부나마 나 자신에 대한 경험에 기대어 세운다는 점을 짚어 둘 수 있다. 그리고 이 이야기에서 내가 적어 내려갈 무언가를 통해, 내가 세밀한 관찰력을 지닌 아이였다거나, 어른이 되어서도 어린 시절을 또렷이 기억한다는 사실이 드러난다면, 나는 그 두 가지 특성 모두를 분명히 내 것이라 주장하겠다."]], [["Looking back, as I was saying, into the blank of my infancy, the first objects I can remember as standing out by themselves from a confusion of things, are my mother and Peggotty.", "앞서 말했듯이 내 갓난 시절의 텅 빈 자리를 돌아볼 때, 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉킨 것들 사이에서 저 홀로 또렷이 떠오르는 첫 번째 모습은 어머니와 패거티다."], ["What else do I remember?", "또 무엇이 기억날까?"], ["Let me see.", "어디 보자."]], [["There comes out of the cloud, our house--not new to me, but quite familiar, in its earliest remembrance.", "안개 속에서 우리 집이 떠오른다. 가장 이른 기억 속에서도 그 집은 낯설지 않고 아주 친숙하다. 일 층에는 뒤뜰로 통하는 패거티의 부엌이 있다."], ["On the ground-floor is Peggotty’s kitchen, opening into a back yard; with a pigeon-house on a pole, in the centre, without any pigeons in it; a great dog-kennel in a corner, without any dog; and a quantity of fowls that look terribly tall to me, walking about, in a menacing and ferocious manner.", "뜰 한가운데에는 장대 위에 비둘기 집이 얹혀 있는데, 비둘기는 한 마리도 없다. 한쪽 구석에는 커다란 개집이 있는데, 개는 한 마리도 없다. 그리고 닭들이 잔뜩 있는데, 내 눈에는 그것들이 무시무시하게 커 보였고, 위협하듯 사납게 돌아다녔다."], ["There is one cock who gets upon a post to crow, and seems to take particular notice of me as I look at him through the kitchen window, who makes me shiver, he is so fierce.", "수탉 한 마리가 말뚝에 올라가 울었는데, 내가 부엌 창문으로 그놈을 바라보면 마치 나를 콕 집어 노려보는 듯해서, 그 사나운 기세에 나는 부르르 몸을 떨었다."], ["Of the geese outside the side-gate who come waddling after me with their long necks stretched out when I go that way, I dream at night: as a man environed by wild beasts might dream of lions.", "옆문 밖의 거위들은 내가 그쪽으로 가면 긴 목을 쭉 빼고 뒤뚱뒤뚱 쫓아왔는데, 나는 밤마다 그놈들 꿈을 꾸었다. 들짐승에 둘러싸인 사람이 사자 꿈을 꾸듯이."]], [["Here is a long passage--what an enormous perspective I make of it!--leading from Peggotty’s kitchen to the front door.", "여기 긴 복도가 있다. 내가 얼마나 어마어마하게 까마득한 거리로 그것을 그려 내는지! 그 복도는 패거티의 부엌에서 현관문까지 이어진다."], ["A dark store-room opens out of it, and that is a place to be run past at night; for I don’t know what may be among those tubs and jars and old tea-chests, when there is nobody in there with a dimly-burning light, letting a mouldy air come out of the door, in which there is the smell of soap, pickles, pepper, candles, and coffee, all at one whiff.", "그 복도에서 어두운 저장실 하나가 열리는데, 밤이면 그 앞을 후다닥 뛰어 지나가야 하는 곳이다. 안에 사람이 없고 희미한 불빛 하나도 없을 때면, 그 통이며 단지며 낡은 차 상자 사이에 무엇이 도사리고 있을지 알 수 없기 때문이다. 문틈으로는 곰팡내가 새어 나오는데, 그 안에는 비누, 절임, 후추, 양초, 커피 냄새가 단숨에 한꺼번에 풍긴다."], ["Then there are the two parlours: the parlour in which we sit of an evening, my mother and I and Peggotty--for Peggotty is quite our companion, when her work is done and we are alone--and the best parlour where we sit on a Sunday; grandly, but not so comfortably.", "그다음에는 거실이 둘 있다. 하나는 저녁이면 우리가 함께 앉아 있는 거실로, 어머니와 나와 패거티가 거기 있다. 일이 끝나고 우리끼리만 남으면 패거티는 어엿한 우리 동무가 되기 때문이다."], ["There is something of a doleful air about that room to me, for Peggotty has told me--I don’t know when, but apparently ages ago--about my father’s funeral, and the company having their black cloaks put on.", "다른 하나는 일요일에 앉는 제일 좋은 거실인데, 그럴듯하지만 그리 편하지는 않다. 그 방은 나에게 어쩐지 음울한 느낌이 도는데, 패거티가 — 언제였는지는 모르지만 아무래도 까마득한 옛날인 듯하다 — 아버지의 장례식 이야기며, 손님들이 검은 외투를 두르고 있었다는 이야기를 해 주었기 때문이다."], ["One Sunday night my mother reads to Peggotty and me in there, how Lazarus was raised up from the dead.", "어느 일요일 밤, 어머니가 그 방에서 패거티와 나에게 나사로가 죽음에서 다시 살아난 이야기를 읽어 주었다."], ["And I am so frightened that they are afterwards obliged to take me out of bed, and show me the quiet churchyard out of the bedroom window, with the dead all lying in their graves at rest, below the solemn moon.", "나는 어찌나 무서웠던지, 결국 두 사람이 나를 침대에서 끌어내, 침실 창문으로 고요한 교회 묘지를 보여 주어야 했다. 엄숙한 달빛 아래, 죽은 이들이 모두 제 무덤 속에 편히 누워 있는 그 묘지를."]], [["There is nothing half so green that I know anywhere, as the grass of that churchyard; nothing half so shady as its trees; nothing half so quiet as its tombstones.", "내가 아는 그 어디에도 그 교회 묘지의 풀만큼 푸른 것은 없고, 그곳 나무들만큼 그늘진 것도 없으며, 그곳 묘비들만큼 고요한 것도 없다."], ["The sheep are feeding there, when I kneel up, early in the morning, in my little bed in a closet within my mother’s room, to look out at it; and I see the red light shining on the sun-dial, and think within myself, ‘Is the sun-dial glad, I wonder, that it can tell the time again?’", "이른 아침, 어머니 방 안의 골방에 놓인 내 작은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 무릎을 꿇고 그곳을 내다보면, 양 떼가 풀을 뜯고 있다. 그리고 해시계 위로 붉은 햇살이 비치는 것을 보며 나는 속으로 생각한다. '해시계는 다시 시간을 알려 줄 수 있어서 기쁠까?'"]], [["Here is our pew in the church.", "여기 교회에 우리 자리가 있다."], ["What a high-backed pew!", "등받이가 어찌나 높은 자리인지!"], ["With a window near it, out of which our house can be seen, and IS seen many times during the morning’s service, by Peggotty, who likes to make herself as sure as she can that it’s not being robbed, or is not in flames.", "그 곁에는 창문이 있어 우리 집이 내다보이는데, 패거티는 아침 예배 내내 몇 번이고 그 창으로 우리 집을 내다본다. 집에 도둑이 들지는 않았는지, 불이 나지는 않았는지 가능한 한 확실히 해 두고 싶어서다."], ["But though Peggotty’s eye wanders, she is much offended if mine does, and frowns to me, as I stand upon the seat, that I am to look at the clergyman.", "그런데 패거티는 제 눈은 여기저기 돌리면서도, 내 눈이 그러면 몹시 못마땅해하며, 자리에 올라선 나에게 목사님을 보라고 눈살을 찌푸린다. 하지만 나는 줄곧 목사님만 볼 수는 없다."], ["But I can’t always look at him--I know him without that white thing on, and I am afraid of his wondering why I stare so, and perhaps stopping the service to inquire--and what am I to do?", "그 하얀 옷을 걸치지 않은 모습으로도 나는 그분을 알아보는데, 내가 왜 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보는지 그분이 의아해하다가, 어쩌면 예배를 멈추고 물어볼까 봐 겁이 난다. 그러니 나는 어쩌면 좋단 말인가?"], ["It’s a dreadful thing to gape, but I must do something.", "입을 헤벌리고 있는 건 끔찍한 짓이지만, 무엇이든 하긴 해야 한다. 어머니를 본다."], ["I look at my mother, but she pretends not to see me.", "그런데 어머니는 나를 못 본 척한다. 통로에 있는 사내애를 본다."], ["I look at a boy in the aisle, and he makes faces at me.", "그런데 그 애는 나에게 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지어 보인다."], ["I look at the sunlight coming in at the open door through the porch, and there I see a stray sheep--I don’t mean a sinner, but mutton--half making up his mind to come into the church.", "현관을 지나 열린 문으로 들어오는 햇살을 본다. 그러자 거기서 길 잃은 양 한 마리가 — 죄인을 말하는 게 아니라 진짜 양고기 말이다 — 교회 안으로 들어올까 말까 망설이는 모습이 보인다."], ["I feel that if I looked at him any longer, I might be tempted to say something out loud; and what would become of me then!", "내가 그놈을 더 오래 쳐다봤다간 큰 소리로 무슨 말을 내뱉고 싶어질 것만 같다. 그러면 나는 또 어떻게 되겠는가!"], ["I look up at the monumental tablets on the wall, and try to think of Mr. Bodgers late of this parish, and what the feelings of Mrs. Bodgers must have been, when affliction sore, long time Mr.", "벽에 걸린 기념 명판을 올려다보며, 이 교구에 살다 가신 보저스 씨를 떠올려 보려 애쓰고, 보저스 씨가 오랜 세월 모진 병고를 견디고 의사들이 다 헛수고였을 때, 보저스 부인의 심정이 어떠했을지 헤아려 본다."], ["Bodgers bore, and physicians were in vain. I wonder whether they called in Mr. Chillip, and he was in vain; and if so, how he likes to be reminded of it once a week.", "그분들이 칠립 의사 선생님을 불렀는데도 그분이 헛수고였던 건 아닐까, 그리고 만약 그랬다면, 일주일에 한 번씩 그 일을 떠올려야 하는 게 그분에게 어떤 기분일까 궁금해한다."], ["I look from Mr. Chillip, in his Sunday neckcloth, to the pulpit; and think what a good place it would be to play in, and what a castle it would make, with another boy coming up the stairs to attack it, and having the velvet cushion with the tassels thrown down on his head.", "일요일용 목도리를 두른 칠립 의사 선생님에게서 설교단으로 눈을 옮기며, 거기가 놀기엔 얼마나 좋은 곳일까, 또 다른 사내애가 계단을 올라와 공격해 오면 그 위에서 술 달린 벨벳 방석을 그 애 머리에 떨어뜨리는, 얼마나 멋진 성채가 될까 하고 생각한다."], ["In time my eyes gradually shut up; and, from seeming to hear the clergyman singing a drowsy song in the heat, I hear nothing, until I fall off the seat with a crash, and am taken out, more dead than alive, by Peggotty.", "이윽고 내 두 눈은 차츰차츰 감기고, 목사님이 그 열기 속에서 졸음 겨운 노래를 부르는 것처럼 들리는가 싶더니, 마침내 아무 소리도 들리지 않다가, 쿵 하고 자리에서 굴러떨어진 뒤에야, 산 것도 죽은 것도 아닌 꼴로 패거티에게 들려 밖으로 나간다."]], [["And now I see the outside of our house, with the latticed bedroom-windows standing open to let in the sweet-smelling air, and the ragged old rooks’-nests still dangling in the elm-trees at the bottom of the front garden.", "이제 우리 집 바깥이 보인다. 격자무늬 침실 창문들은 향긋한 바람을 들이려 활짝 열려 있고, 앞뜰 끝의 느릅나무들에는 너덜너덜한 낡은 까마귀 둥지들이 여전히 매달려 있다. 이제 나는 빈 비둘기 집과 개집이 있는 뜰 너머, 뒤뜰 정원에 있다."], ["Now I am in the garden at the back, beyond the yard where the empty pigeon-house and dog-kennel are--a very preserve of butterflies, as I remember it, with a high fence, and a gate and padlock; where the fruit clusters on the trees, riper and richer than fruit has ever been since, in any other garden, and where my mother gathers some in a basket, while I stand by, bolting furtive gooseberries, and trying to look unmoved.", "내 기억으로 그곳은 나비들의 보금자리나 다름없었는데, 높은 울타리에 문이 달리고 자물쇠가 채워져 있었다. 나무마다 열매가 송이송이 매달려 있었는데, 그 뒤로 다른 어느 정원에서 본 어떤 열매보다도 더 무르익고 탐스러웠다. 어머니가 그 열매 몇 개를 바구니에 따 담는 동안, 나는 그 곁에 서서, 까치밥나무 열매를 몰래 입에 털어 넣으며, 시치미를 뚝 떼려고 애를 쓴다."], ["A great wind rises, and the summer is gone in a moment.", "큰바람이 일더니, 여름은 순식간에 가 버린다."], ["We are playing in the winter twilight, dancing about the parlour.", "우리는 겨울 황혼 속에서 거실을 빙빙 돌며 춤추듯 놀고 있다."], ["When my mother is out of breath and rests herself in an elbow-chair, I watch her winding her bright curls round her fingers, and straitening her waist, and nobody knows better than I do that she likes to look so well, and is proud of being so pretty.", "어머니가 숨이 차서 안락의자에 기대 쉴 때면, 나는 어머니가 반짝이는 곱슬머리를 손가락에 돌돌 감고 허리를 곧게 펴는 모습을 지켜본다. 어머니가 그렇게 예쁘게 보이는 걸 좋아하고, 그토록 어여쁜 것을 자랑스러워한다는 사실은, 세상에 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 없다."]], [["That is among my very earliest impressions.", "그것이 내 가장 이른 기억 가운데 하나다."], ["That, and a sense that we were both a little afraid of Peggotty, and submitted ourselves in most things to her direction, were among the first opinions--if they may be so called--that I ever derived from what I saw.", "그것, 그리고 우리 둘 다 패거티를 조금은 무서워하면서 거의 모든 일에서 그녀의 지시에 따랐다는 느낌, 그것이 내가 본 것에서 끌어낸 — 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 — 최초의 견해 가운데 하나였다."]], [["Peggotty and I were sitting one night by the parlour fire, alone.", "어느 날 밤, 패거티와 나는 단둘이 거실 난롯가에 앉아 있었다."], ["I had been reading to Peggotty about crocodiles.", "나는 패거티에게 악어 이야기를 읽어 주던 참이었다."], ["I must have read very perspicuously, or the poor soul must have been deeply interested, for I remember she had a cloudy impression, after I had done, that they were a sort of vegetable.", "내가 무척 또렷하게 읽었거나, 아니면 그 가엾은 사람이 깊이 빠져들었던 게 분명한데, 다 읽고 났을 때 패거티가 악어를 무슨 채소 같은 것으로 어렴풋이 알아들었다는 게 기억나기 때문이다."], ["I was tired of reading, and dead sleepy; but having leave, as a high treat, to sit up until my mother came home from spending the evening at a neighbour’s, I would rather have died upon my post (of course) than have gone to bed.", "나는 읽기에 지쳐 죽도록 졸렸다. 그래도 특별한 호사로, 어머니가 이웃집에서 저녁을 보내고 돌아올 때까지 자지 않고 앉아 있어도 좋다는 허락을 받았으니, 나는 (당연히) 침대로 가느니 차라리 내 자리에서 죽기를 택했을 것이다. 졸음이 한껏 차오른 그 무렵, 패거티는 부풀어 올라 어마어마하게 커지는 것 같았다."], ["I had reached that stage of sleepiness when Peggotty seemed to swell and grow immensely large.", "나는 두 집게손가락으로 눈꺼풀을 떠받쳐 억지로 뜨고, 일하느라 앉아 있는 그녀를 끈질기게 바라보았다."], ["I propped my eyelids open with my two forefingers, and looked perseveringly at her as she sat at work; at the little bit of wax-candle she kept for her thread--how old it looked, being so wrinkled in all directions!--at the little house with a thatched roof, where the yard-measure lived; at her work-box with a sliding lid, with a view of St. Paul’s Cathedral (with a pink dome) painted on the top; at the brass thimble on her finger; at herself, whom I thought lovely.", "그녀가 실에 쓰려고 챙겨 둔 작은 밀랍 토막을 — 사방으로 어찌나 쪼글쪼글한지 어찌나 낡아 보이던지! — 그리고 줄자가 사는 초가지붕 얹은 작은 집을, 뚜껑에 (분홍빛 둥근 지붕을 얹은) 세인트폴 대성당이 그려진 채 미닫이뚜껑이 달린 반짇고리를, 손가락에 낀 놋쇠 골무를, 그리고 내가 사랑스럽다 여기던 그녀 자신을 바라보았다."], ["I felt so sleepy, that I knew if I lost sight of anything for a moment, I was gone.", "어찌나 졸렸던지, 잠깐이라도 무언가에서 눈을 떼면 나는 끝장이라는 걸 알았다."]], [["‘Peggotty,’ says I, suddenly, ‘were you ever married?’", "\"패거티.\" 나는 불쑥 말했다. \"결혼해 본 적 있어?\""]], [["‘Lord, Master Davy,’ replied Peggotty.", "\"어머나, 데이비 도련님.\" 패거티가 대답했다."], ["‘What’s put marriage in your head?’", "\"결혼은 또 무슨 바람이 불어서 머릿속에 들어온 거예요?\""]], [["She answered with such a start, that it quite awoke me. And then she stopped in her work, and looked at me, with her needle drawn out to its thread’s length.", "어찌나 화들짝 놀라며 대답하던지, 나는 그만 잠이 확 달아났다. 그러고는 일손을 멈추고, 바늘을 실 길이만큼 쭉 빼낸 채로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["‘But WERE you ever married, Peggotty?’ says I.", "\"근데 진짜 결혼해 본 적 있어, 패거티?\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘You are a very handsome woman, an’t you?’", "\"패거티는 아주 예쁜 여자잖아, 안 그래?\""]], [["I thought her in a different style from my mother, certainly; but of another school of beauty, I considered her a perfect example.", "물론 나는 그녀가 어머니와는 다른 결의 미인이라고 여겼다. 하지만 또 다른 부류의 아름다움으로 치면, 나는 그녀를 더없이 완벽한 본보기로 생각했다."], ["There was a red velvet footstool in the best parlour, on which my mother had painted a nosegay.", "제일 좋은 거실에는 붉은 벨벳 발받침이 하나 있었는데, 그 위에 어머니가 꽃다발을 그려 놓았다."], ["The ground-work of that stool, and Peggotty’s complexion appeared to me to be one and the same thing.", "그 발받침의 바탕천과 패거티의 살빛은, 내 눈에 똑같은 것으로 보였다."], ["The stool was smooth, and Peggotty was rough, but that made no difference.", "발받침은 매끈하고 패거티는 거칠었지만, 그런 건 아무 상관도 없었다."]], [["‘Me handsome, Davy!’ said Peggotty.", "\"내가 예쁘다고, 데이비?\" 패거티가 말했다."], ["‘Lawk, no, my dear!", "\"어머나, 아니에요, 도련님!"], ["But what put marriage in your head?’", "그런데 결혼은 또 무슨 바람이 불어서 머릿속에 들어온 거예요?\""]], [["‘I don’t know!--You mustn’t marry more than one person at a time, may you, Peggotty?’", "\"몰라! 근데 한 번에 두 사람하고 결혼하면 안 되는 거지, 안 그래, 패거티?\""]], [["‘Certainly not,’ says Peggotty, with the promptest decision.", "\"당연히 안 되지요.\" 패거티가 한 치의 망설임도 없이 단호하게 말했다."]], [["‘But if you marry a person, and the person dies, why then you may marry another person, mayn’t you, Peggotty?’", "\"근데 어떤 사람하고 결혼했는데 그 사람이 죽으면, 그럼 다른 사람하고 또 결혼해도 되는 거지, 안 그래, 패거티?\""]], [["‘YOU MAY,’ says Peggotty, ‘if you choose, my dear.", "\"그래도 돼요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"도련님 마음만 정해지면요."], ["That’s a matter of opinion.’", "그건 사람마다 생각하기 나름이에요.\""]], [["‘But what is your opinion, Peggotty?’ said I.", "\"근데 패거티 생각은 어때, 패거티?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["I asked her, and looked curiously at her, because she looked so curiously at me.", "내가 그렇게 물으며 그녀를 궁금하다는 듯 바라본 것은, 그녀가 나를 어찌나 궁금하다는 듯 바라보던지 그래서였다."]], [["‘My opinion is,’ said Peggotty, taking her eyes from me, after a little indecision and going on with her work, ‘that I never was married myself, Master Davy, and that I don’t expect to be.", "\"내 생각은요,\" 패거티가 잠깐 머뭇거리다가 나에게서 눈을 떼고 다시 일을 이어 가며 말했다. \"나는 한 번도 결혼한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 할 일이 없을 거라는 거예요, 데이비 도련님."], ["That’s all I know about the subject.’", "이 일에 대해 내가 아는 건 그게 다예요.\""]], [["‘You an’t cross, I suppose, Peggotty, are you?’ said I, after sitting quiet for a minute.", "\"패거티, 화난 건 아니지, 응?\" 잠시 가만히 앉아 있다가 내가 말했다."]], [["I really thought she was, she had been so short with me; but I was quite mistaken: for she laid aside her work (which was a stocking of her own), and opening her arms wide, took my curly head within them, and gave it a good squeeze.", "나는 정말 그녀가 화난 줄 알았다. 나에게 어찌나 퉁명스럽게 굴던지. 그러나 그건 완전히 내 착각이었다."], ["I know it was a good squeeze, because, being very plump, whenever she made any little exertion after she was dressed, some of the buttons on the back of her gown flew off.", "패거티가 일감을 (자기 양말이었다) 내려놓고, 두 팔을 활짝 벌려 내 곱슬머리를 그 안에 안고는, 꽉 끌어안아 주었기 때문이다. 그게 꽉 끌어안은 거였다는 건 내가 안다. 그녀는 워낙 통통해서, 옷을 다 입은 뒤에 조금이라도 힘을 쓰면, 가운 등판의 단추 몇 개가 튕겨 나가곤 했기 때문이다."], ["And I recollect two bursting to the opposite side of the parlour, while she was hugging me.", "그리고 나는 그녀가 나를 껴안는 동안 단추 두 개가 거실 맞은편으로 튀어 날아간 걸 기억한다."]], [["‘Now let me hear some more about the Crorkindills,’ said Peggotty, who was not quite right in the name yet, ‘for I an’t heard half enough.’", "\"자, 그 악악어 얘기 좀 더 들려줘 봐요.\" 아직 이름을 제대로 못 외운 패거티가 말했다. \"반도 못 들은 것 같으니까.\""]], [["I couldn’t quite understand why Peggotty looked so queer, or why she was so ready to go back to the crocodiles.", "나는 패거티가 왜 그렇게 묘한 표정을 짓는지, 왜 그렇게 선뜻 악어 이야기로 돌아가려 하는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었다."], ["However, we returned to those monsters, with fresh wakefulness on my part, and we left their eggs in the sand for the sun to hatch; and we ran away from them, and baffled them by constantly turning, which they were unable to do quickly, on account of their unwieldy make; and we went into the water after them, as natives, and put sharp pieces of timber down their throats; and in short we ran the whole crocodile gauntlet.", "그래도 우리는 그 괴물들에게로 돌아갔고, 나는 정신이 새로 말짱해진 채로 그놈들의 알을 햇볕에 부화하라고 모래밭에 남겨 두었다. 그리고 우리는 그놈들에게서 달아나, 끊임없이 방향을 휙휙 틀어 그놈들을 따돌렸는데, 그놈들은 몸뚱이가 둔해서 빨리 돌지 못했다. 또 우리는 원주민이 되어 그놈들을 따라 물속으로 들어가, 그놈들 목구멍에 날카로운 나무토막을 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["I did, at least; but I had my doubts of Peggotty, who was thoughtfully sticking her needle into various parts of her face and arms, all the time.", "한마디로 우리는 악어와 관련된 온갖 모험을 다 헤쳐 나갔다. 적어도 나는 그랬다. 그러나 패거티는 그러는 내내 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 제 얼굴과 팔 여기저기에 바늘을 콕콕 찌르고 있었으니, 나는 그녀까지 그랬는지는 의심스럽다."]], [["We had exhausted the crocodiles, and begun with the alligators, when the garden-bell rang.", "악어를 다 끝내고 악어붙이로 막 넘어가려는데, 정원의 종이 울렸다. 우리는 문으로 나갔다."], ["We went out to the door; and there was my mother, looking unusually pretty, I thought, and with her a gentleman with beautiful black hair and whiskers, who had walked home with us from church last Sunday.", "거기 어머니가 있었는데, 내 눈엔 유난히 예뻐 보였다. 그리고 어머니 곁에는 아름다운 검은 머리와 구레나룻을 기른 신사가 있었는데, 지난 일요일에 교회에서 우리와 함께 집까지 걸어온 사람이었다."]], [["As my mother stooped down on the threshold to take me in her arms and kiss me, the gentleman said I was a more highly privileged little fellow than a monarch--or something like that; for my later understanding comes, I am sensible, to my aid here.", "어머니가 문지방에서 몸을 숙여 나를 두 팔로 안고 입을 맞추자, 그 신사가 나더러 임금님보다도 더 큰 복을 받은 꼬마라고 — 뭐 그 비슷한 말을 했다. 여기서는 훗날 자라서 깨달은 것이 내 기억을 거든다는 걸 나도 안다."]], [["‘What does that mean?’ I asked him, over her shoulder.", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\" 나는 어머니 어깨 너머로 그에게 물었다."]], [["He patted me on the head; but somehow, I didn’t like him or his deep voice, and I was jealous that his hand should touch my mother’s in touching me--which it did.", "그가 내 머리를 토닥였다. 그런데 어쩐지 나는 그도, 그 굵은 목소리도 마음에 들지 않았고, 나를 만지면서 그의 손이 어머니의 손에 닿는 것이 — 실제로 닿았다 — 질투가 났다."], ["I put it away, as well as I could.", "나는 할 수 있는 한 그 손을 떼어 냈다."]], [["‘Oh, Davy!’ remonstrated my mother.", "\"어머나, 데이비!\" 어머니가 나무랐다."]], [["‘Dear boy!’ said the gentleman.", "\"착하기도 하지!\" 신사가 말했다."], ["‘I cannot wonder at his devotion!’", "\"이렇게 엄마를 끔찍이 위하는 게 어찌 놀랍지 않겠습니까!\""]], [["I never saw such a beautiful colour on my mother’s face before.", "나는 어머니 얼굴에 그렇게 고운 빛이 도는 걸 그전엔 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["She gently chid me for being rude; and, keeping me close to her shawl, turned to thank the gentleman for taking so much trouble as to bring her home.", "어머니는 내가 버릇없게 군다고 부드럽게 나무라고는, 나를 제 숄에 바싹 끌어안은 채, 집까지 데려다주는 수고를 해 준 신사에게 고맙다는 인사를 하려고 돌아섰다."], ["She put out her hand to him as she spoke, and, as he met it with his own, she glanced, I thought, at me.", "그렇게 말하며 어머니가 그에게 손을 내밀자, 그가 자기 손으로 그 손을 맞잡았는데, 그 순간 어머니가 나를 흘끗 보았던 것 같다."]], [["‘Let us say “good night”, my fine boy,’ said the gentleman, when he had bent his head--I saw him!--over my mother’s little glove.", "\"그럼 '잘 자거라' 인사하자꾸나, 꼬마 신사.\" 신사가 — 나는 보았다! — 어머니의 작은 장갑 위로 고개를 숙이고 나서 말했다."]], [["‘Good night!’ said I.", "\"안녕히 가세요!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Come!", "\"자!"], ["Let us be the best friends in the world!’ said the gentleman, laughing.", "우리 세상에서 둘도 없는 친구가 되자꾸나!\" 신사가 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘Shake hands!’", "\"악수하자!\""]], [["My right hand was in my mother’s left, so I gave him the other.", "내 오른손은 어머니의 왼손에 쥐여 있어서, 나는 그에게 다른 손을 내밀었다."]], [["‘Why, that’s the Wrong hand, Davy!’ laughed the gentleman.", "\"이런, 그건 틀린 손이잖니, 데이비!\" 신사가 웃었다."]], [["My mother drew my right hand forward, but I was resolved, for my former reason, not to give it him, and I did not.", "어머니가 내 오른손을 앞으로 끌어 주었지만, 나는 앞서 말한 그 까닭으로 그 손을 주지 않기로 마음먹은 터라, 끝내 주지 않았다."], ["I gave him the other, and he shook it heartily, and said I was a brave fellow, and went away.", "나는 그에게 다른 손을 내밀었고, 그는 그 손을 힘껏 흔들며 나더러 용감한 녀석이라고 하고는 가 버렸다."]], [["At this minute I see him turn round in the garden, and give us a last look with his ill-omened black eyes, before the door was shut.", "지금도 나는 그가 문이 닫히기 전에 정원에서 몸을 돌려, 그 불길한 검은 눈으로 우리를 마지막으로 한 번 바라보던 모습이 보인다."]], [["Peggotty, who had not said a word or moved a finger, secured the fastenings instantly, and we all went into the parlour.", "한마디도 하지 않고 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않던 패거티는 곧장 빗장을 단단히 채웠고, 우리는 모두 거실로 들어갔다."], ["My mother, contrary to her usual habit, instead of coming to the elbow-chair by the fire, remained at the other end of the room, and sat singing to herself.", "어머니는 여느 때 버릇과 달리 난롯가 안락의자로 오지 않고, 방 저편 끝에 머물러 혼자 노래를 흥얼거리며 앉아 있었다."], ["--‘Hope you have had a pleasant evening, ma’am,’ said Peggotty, standing as stiff as a barrel in the centre of the room, with a candlestick in her hand.", "\"마님, 즐거운 저녁 보내셨길 바라요.\" 손에 촛대를 든 채 방 한가운데에 술통처럼 빳빳이 선 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘Much obliged to you, Peggotty,’ returned my mother, in a cheerful voice, ‘I have had a VERY pleasant evening.’", "\"정말 고마워, 패거티.\" 어머니가 명랑한 목소리로 받았다. \"아주 즐거운 저녁이었어.\""]], [["‘A stranger or so makes an agreeable change,’ suggested Peggotty.", "\"낯선 손님이라도 좀 오면 기분 전환이 되긴 하지요.\" 패거티가 넌지시 말했다."]], [["‘A very agreeable change, indeed,’ returned my mother.", "\"정말 기분 좋은 전환이었지.\" 어머니가 받았다."]], [["Peggotty continuing to stand motionless in the middle of the room, and my mother resuming her singing, I fell asleep, though I was not so sound asleep but that I could hear voices, without hearing what they said.", "패거티가 계속 방 한가운데 꼼짝 않고 서 있고 어머니가 다시 노래를 흥얼거리는 사이, 나는 잠이 들었다. 그래도 아주 깊이 잠든 건 아니어서, 무슨 말인지는 알아듣지 못해도 목소리는 들을 수 있었다."], ["When I half awoke from this uncomfortable doze, I found Peggotty and my mother both in tears, and both talking.", "이 불편한 선잠에서 반쯤 깨어났을 때, 나는 패거티와 어머니 둘 다 눈물을 흘리며 이야기하고 있는 걸 보았다."]], [["‘Not such a one as this, Mr. Copperfield wouldn’t have liked,’ said Peggotty.", "\"이런 사람은, 코퍼필드 나리께서 결코 마음에 들어 하지 않으셨을 거예요.\" 패거티가 말했다."], ["‘That I say, and that I swear!’", "\"그건 제가 단언하고, 맹세까지 할 수 있어요!\""]], [["‘Good Heavens!’ cried my mother, ‘you’ll drive me mad!", "\"맙소사!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"너 때문에 미치겠어!"], ["Was ever any poor girl so ill-used by her servants as I am!", "세상에 나만큼 하인한테 못된 꼴 당하는 가엾은 계집애가 또 있을까!"], ["Why do I do myself the injustice of calling myself a girl? Have I never been married, Peggotty?’", "아니, 나 자신을 계집애라 부르다니, 내가 나한테 무슨 부당한 짓을 하는 거람? 나 결혼한 적 없니, 패거티?\""]], [["‘God knows you have, ma’am,’ returned Peggotty. ‘Then, how can you dare,’ said my mother--‘you know I don’t mean how can you dare, Peggotty, but how can you have the heart--to make me so uncomfortable and say such bitter things to me, when you are well aware that I haven’t, out of this place, a single friend to turn to?’", "\"하느님은 아시지요, 마님은 결혼하셨어요.\" 패거티가 받았다. \"그럼 어떻게 네가 감히,\" 어머니가 말했다. \"ㅡ감히라고 한 게 무슨 뜻인지 너도 알지, 패거티. 내 말은, 어떻게 네가 그런 모진 말로 날 이렇게 불편하게 만들 마음이 드느냔 말이야. 이 동네 말고는 기댈 친구 하나 없다는 걸 빤히 알면서!\""]], [["‘The more’s the reason,’ returned Peggotty, ‘for saying that it won’t do. No!", "\"바로 그러니까,\" 패거티가 받았다. \"그건 안 될 일이라고 말씀드리는 거예요. 안 돼요!"], ["That it won’t do. No!", "그건 절대 안 돼요. 안 됩니다!"], ["No price could make it do.", "그 어떤 값을 쳐도 안 될 일이에요."], ["No!’--I thought Peggotty would have thrown the candlestick away, she was so emphatic with it.", "안 돼요!\" 패거티가 어찌나 촛대를 들고 힘주어 말하던지, 나는 그녀가 그 촛대를 내던질 줄 알았다."]], [["‘How can you be so aggravating,’ said my mother, shedding more tears than before, ‘as to talk in such an unjust manner!", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 사람 속을 긁어 놓니,\" 어머니가 아까보다 더 많은 눈물을 쏟으며 말했다. \"그렇게 부당하게 말을 하다니!"], ["How can you go on as if it was all settled and arranged, Peggotty, when I tell you over and over again, you cruel thing, that beyond the commonest civilities nothing has passed!", "어떻게 모든 게 다 정해지고 짜여진 일인 양 그러는 거야, 패거티. 이 모진 것아, 내가 몇 번이고 말하잖니, 그저 흔한 예의범절 말고는 아무 일도 없었다고!"], ["You talk of admiration. What am I to do?", "흠모니 뭐니 하는데, 그럼 난 어쩌란 말이야?"], ["If people are so silly as to indulge the sentiment, is it my fault?", "사람들이 어리석게 그런 마음을 품는다면, 그게 내 잘못이니?"], ["What am I to do, I ask you?", "내가 어쩌면 좋겠어, 응?"], ["Would you wish me to shave my head and black my face, or disfigure myself with a burn, or a scald, or something of that sort?", "내가 머리를 박박 밀고 얼굴을 시커멓게 칠하고, 아니면 데이거나 끓는 물에 데어 흉측하게라도 망가뜨려야 속이 시원하겠니?"], ["I dare say you would, Peggotty.", "그래, 너는 그러길 바라겠지, 패거티."], ["I dare say you’d quite enjoy it.’", "너는 그러면 아주 신날 거야.\""]], [["Peggotty seemed to take this aspersion very much to heart, I thought.", "내가 보기에 패거티는 이 비난을 몹시 마음에 담아 두는 듯했다."]], [["‘And my dear boy,’ cried my mother, coming to the elbow-chair in which I was, and caressing me, ‘my own little Davy!", "\"그리고 우리 귀한 아들아,\" 어머니가 내가 앉아 있던 안락의자로 다가와 나를 어루만지며 외쳤다. \"내 사랑하는 어린 데이비!"], ["Is it to be hinted to me that I am wanting in affection for my precious treasure, the dearest little fellow that ever was!’", "내가 우리 귀한 보물, 세상에 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 우리 꼬마한테 정이 모자란다고, 그런 소리가 내 귀에 들려야 한단 말이니!\""]], [["‘Nobody never went and hinted no such a thing,’ said Peggotty.", "\"누가 언제 그런 말 한 적도 없는데요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘You did, Peggotty!’ returned my mother.", "\"네가 했잖아, 패거티!\" 어머니가 받았다. \"네가 한 거 너도 알잖니."], ["‘You know you did.", "이 매정한 것아, 네가 한 말에서 달리 뭘 더 미루어 짐작하란 말이니?"], ["What else was it possible to infer from what you said, you unkind creature, when you know as well as I do, that on his account only last quarter I wouldn’t buy myself a new parasol, though that old green one is frayed the whole way up, and the fringe is perfectly mangy?", "너도 나만큼 잘 알면서. 이 애 때문에 지난 분기에는 새 양산도 안 샀잖아. 그 낡은 초록색 양산은 위쪽까지 죄다 올이 풀리고 술 장식도 다 빠져 너덜너덜한데도 말이야."], ["You know it is, Peggotty.", "너도 알잖아, 패거티."], ["You can’t deny it.’ Then, turning affectionately to me, with her cheek against mine, ‘Am I a naughty mama to you, Davy?", "부인 못 하지.\" 그러더니 다정하게 나에게로 돌아서서, 제 뺨을 내 뺨에 대고 말했다. \"내가 너한테 못된 엄마니, 데이비?"], ["Am I a nasty, cruel, selfish, bad mama?", "내가 고약하고, 모질고, 제멋대로에, 나쁜 엄마야? 그렇다고 말해 봐, 아가."], ["Say I am, my child; say “yes”, dear boy, and Peggotty will love you; and Peggotty’s love is a great deal better than mine, Davy.", "'응'이라고 말해, 우리 아들. 그러면 패거티가 널 사랑해 줄 거야. 패거티의 사랑이 내 사랑보다 훨씬 나으니까, 데이비."], ["I don’t love you at all, do I?’", "난 널 조금도 사랑하지 않지, 그렇지?\""]], [["At this, we all fell a-crying together.", "이 말에 우리는 다 같이 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["I think I was the loudest of the party, but I am sure we were all sincere about it.", "우리 중에서 내가 가장 큰 소리로 울었던 것 같은데, 우리 모두 진심으로 운 것만은 분명하다."], ["I was quite heart-broken myself, and am afraid that in the first transports of wounded tenderness I called Peggotty a ‘Beast’.", "나 자신도 가슴이 찢어질 듯했는데, 상처받은 애틋함이 처음 북받쳐 오른 통에 패거티를 '짐승'이라 불렀던 게 아닌가 싶다."], ["That honest creature was in deep affliction, I remember, and must have become quite buttonless on the occasion; for a little volley of those explosives went off, when, after having made it up with my mother, she kneeled down by the elbow-chair, and made it up with me.", "그 정직한 사람은, 내 기억으로, 깊은 슬픔에 빠져 있었고, 그 바람에 단추가 죄다 떨어져 나갔을 게 분명하다. 어머니와 화해한 뒤 안락의자 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 나와도 화해할 때, 그 작은 폭탄들이 한바탕 터져 나갔으니까."]], [["We went to bed greatly dejected.", "우리는 몹시 풀이 죽은 채로 잠자리에 들었다."], ["My sobs kept waking me, for a long time; and when one very strong sob quite hoisted me up in bed, I found my mother sitting on the coverlet, and leaning over me.", "내 흐느낌은 한참 동안 나를 거듭 깨웠다. 그러다 유난히 큰 흐느낌 하나가 나를 침대에서 벌떡 일으켜 세웠을 때, 나는 어머니가 이불 위에 앉아 나에게 몸을 숙이고 있는 걸 보았다."], ["I fell asleep in her arms, after that, and slept soundly.", "그러고 나서 나는 어머니 품에 안겨 잠이 들었고, 곤히 잤다."]], [["Whether it was the following Sunday when I saw the gentleman again, or whether there was any greater lapse of time before he reappeared, I cannot recall.", "그 신사를 다시 본 것이 그다음 일요일이었는지, 아니면 그가 다시 나타나기까지 더 긴 시간이 흘렀는지, 나는 떠올리지 못한다."], ["I don’t profess to be clear about dates.", "날짜를 또렷이 안다고 우길 생각은 없다."], ["But there he was, in church, and he walked home with us afterwards.", "어쨌든 그는 교회에 있었고, 예배가 끝난 뒤 우리와 함께 집까지 걸어왔다."], ["He came in, too, to look at a famous geranium we had, in the parlour-window.", "그는 거실 창가에 둔 우리 집의 멋진 제라늄을 보겠다며 안으로 들어오기까지 했다."], ["It did not appear to me that he took much notice of it, but before he went he asked my mother to give him a bit of the blossom.", "내 보기엔 그가 그 꽃에 별 관심을 두는 것 같지 않았는데, 가기 전에 어머니에게 그 꽃송이를 한 조각 달라고 했다."], ["She begged him to choose it for himself, but he refused to do that--I could not understand why--so she plucked it for him, and gave it into his hand.", "어머니가 직접 골라 보라고 권했지만, 그는 한사코 마다했다. 나는 왜 그러는지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그래서 어머니가 그에게 꺾어서 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["He said he would never, never part with it any more; and I thought he must be quite a fool not to know that it would fall to pieces in a day or two.", "그는 이 꽃을 절대로, 절대로 손에서 놓지 않겠다고 했는데, 나는 그게 하루 이틀이면 바스러져 버린다는 걸 모르다니 어지간한 바보로구나 싶었다."]], [["Peggotty began to be less with us, of an evening, than she had always been.", "패거티는 저녁이면 늘 그래 왔던 것보다 우리와 함께 있는 시간이 점점 줄어들었다."], ["My mother deferred to her very much--more than usual, it occurred to me--and we were all three excellent friends; still we were different from what we used to be, and were not so comfortable among ourselves.", "어머니는 패거티를 무척이나 — 내가 보기엔 평소보다 더 — 깍듯이 대했고, 우리 셋은 더없이 좋은 친구였다. 그래도 우리는 예전 같지 않았고, 우리끼리 있어도 그리 편치가 않았다."], ["Sometimes I fancied that Peggotty perhaps objected to my mother’s wearing all the pretty dresses she had in her drawers, or to her going so often to visit at that neighbour’s; but I couldn’t, to my satisfaction, make out how it was.", "나는 이따금 패거티가 어머니가 서랍 속 예쁜 옷들을 죄다 꺼내 입는 것이나, 그 이웃집에 자주 마실 가는 것을 못마땅해하는 게 아닐까 짐작했지만, 그게 어찌 된 영문인지 속 시원히 알아낼 수는 없었다."]], [["Gradually, I became used to seeing the gentleman with the black whiskers.", "차츰 나는 그 검은 구레나룻 신사를 보는 데 익숙해졌다."], ["I liked him no better than at first, and had the same uneasy jealousy of him; but if I had any reason for it beyond a child’s instinctive dislike, and a general idea that Peggotty and I could make much of my mother without any help, it certainly was not THE reason that I might have found if I had been older.", "그래도 처음보다 그가 더 좋아지지는 않았고, 여전히 그를 향한 그 불편한 질투를 느꼈다. 하지만 어린아이의 본능적인 반감과, 패거티와 나 둘이면 누구의 도움 없이도 어머니를 떠받들 수 있다는 막연한 생각 말고 거기에 다른 까닭이 있었다 해도, 그건 결코 내가 좀 더 컸다면 찾아냈을 법한 그 까닭은 아니었다."], ["No such thing came into my mind, or near it.", "그런 생각은 내 마음에 떠오른 적도, 근처에 얼씬한 적도 없었다."], ["I could observe, in little pieces, as it were; but as to making a net of a number of these pieces, and catching anybody in it, that was, as yet, beyond me.", "나는 말하자면 자잘한 조각들을 하나하나 관찰할 수는 있었다. 그러나 그 여러 조각들로 그물을 엮어 누군가를 거기 옭아넣는 일은, 아직 나에겐 능력 밖이었다."]], [["One autumn morning I was with my mother in the front garden, when Mr. Murdstone--I knew him by that name now--came by, on horseback.", "어느 가을 아침, 나는 앞뜰에서 어머니와 함께 있었는데, 머드스톤 씨가 — 이제 나는 그를 그 이름으로 알았다 — 말을 타고 지나갔다."], ["He reined up his horse to salute my mother, and said he was going to Lowestoft to see some friends who were there with a yacht, and merrily proposed to take me on the saddle before him if I would like the ride.", "그는 말고삐를 당겨 어머니에게 인사하고는, 요트를 타고 거기 와 있는 친구들을 만나러 로스토프트에 가는 길이라고 하더니, 나더러 말 타는 게 좋으면 자기 앞쪽 안장에 태워 주겠다고 명랑하게 제안했다."]], [["The air was so clear and pleasant, and the horse seemed to like the idea of the ride so much himself, as he stood snorting and pawing at the garden-gate, that I had a great desire to go.", "공기가 어찌나 맑고 상쾌하던지, 그리고 말도 정원 문 앞에서 콧김을 내뿜고 발을 구르며 그 나들이를 무척 반기는 것 같아서, 나는 가고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다."], ["So I was sent upstairs to Peggotty to be made spruce; and in the meantime Mr. Murdstone dismounted, and, with his horse’s bridle drawn over his arm, walked slowly up and down on the outer side of the sweetbriar fence, while my mother walked slowly up and down on the inner to keep him company.", "그래서 나는 패거티에게 올라가 말끔하게 단장을 했고, 그동안 머드스톤 씨는 말에서 내려, 고삐를 팔에 걸친 채 들장미 울타리 바깥쪽을 천천히 오르락내리락 거닐었고, 어머니는 안쪽에서 그와 짝을 맞춰 천천히 오르락내리락 거닐었다."], ["I recollect Peggotty and I peeping out at them from my little window; I recollect how closely they seemed to be examining the sweetbriar between them, as they strolled along; and how, from being in a perfectly angelic temper, Peggotty turned cross in a moment, and brushed my hair the wrong way, excessively hard.", "나는 패거티와 내가 내 작은 창문으로 두 사람을 엿보던 게 기억난다. 두 사람이 거니는 동안 사이에 놓인 들장미를 어찌나 골똘히 살피는 것 같던지, 그리고 더없이 천사 같던 패거티가 순식간에 뾰로통해져서, 내 머리를 거꾸로, 그것도 사납도록 세게 빗질하던 게 기억난다."]], [["Mr. Murdstone and I were soon off, and trotting along on the green turf by the side of the road.", "머드스톤 씨와 나는 곧 길을 나서, 길가 푸른 잔디 위를 달가닥달가닥 달렸다."], ["He held me quite easily with one arm, and I don’t think I was restless usually; but I could not make up my mind to sit in front of him without turning my head sometimes, and looking up in his face.", "그는 한쪽 팔만으로도 나를 거뜬히 붙들었는데, 나는 평소엔 가만히 못 있는 아이가 아니었던 것 같은데도, 그의 앞에 앉아 이따금 고개를 돌려 그의 얼굴을 올려다보지 않고는 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["He had that kind of shallow black eye--I want a better word to express an eye that has no depth in it to be looked into--which, when it is abstracted, seems from some peculiarity of light to be disfigured, for a moment at a time, by a cast.", "그는 그런 부류의 얕은 검은 눈을 가지고 있었는데 — 들여다볼 깊이가 없는 눈을 가리키기엔 더 나은 말이 있으면 좋겠다 — 그 눈은 멍하니 딴생각에 잠기면, 빛의 어떤 묘한 조화 탓인지, 한순간씩 사팔뜨기처럼 일그러져 보였다."], ["Several times when I glanced at him, I observed that appearance with a sort of awe, and wondered what he was thinking about so closely.", "그를 흘끗거릴 때마다 나는 그 모습을 일종의 두려움을 품고 바라보며, 그가 무슨 생각을 그토록 골똘히 하는지 궁금해했다."], ["His hair and whiskers were blacker and thicker, looked at so near, than even I had given them credit for being.", "이렇게 가까이서 보니 그의 머리와 구레나룻은 내가 여태 짐작했던 것보다도 더 검고 더 짙었다."], ["A squareness about the lower part of his face, and the dotted indication of the strong black beard he shaved close every day, reminded me of the wax-work that had travelled into our neighbourhood some half-a-year before.", "얼굴 아래쪽의 각진 윤곽과, 날마다 바짝 깎아 내는 짙고 억센 수염이 점점이 비치는 자국은, 한 반년쯤 전에 우리 동네로 흘러들었던 밀랍 인형을 떠올리게 했다."], ["This, his regular eyebrows, and the rich white, and black, and brown, of his complexion--confound his complexion, and his memory!--made me think him, in spite of my misgivings, a very handsome man.", "이것, 그리고 그의 반듯한 눈썹과, 희고 검고 갈색이 어우러진 짙은 살빛은 — 그 살빛도, 그 기억도 다 망할 것! — 나로 하여금, 그 켕기는 마음에도 불구하고, 그를 아주 잘생긴 남자라고 여기게 했다."], ["I have no doubt that my poor dear mother thought him so too.", "내 가엾고 사랑하는 어머니도 그를 그렇게 여겼으리라는 데에는 나는 한 점 의심이 없다."]], [["We went to an hotel by the sea, where two gentlemen were smoking cigars in a room by themselves.", "우리는 바닷가의 어느 호텔로 갔는데, 거기서 신사 둘이 한 방에 따로 들어앉아 시가를 피우고 있었다."], ["Each of them was lying on at least four chairs, and had a large rough jacket on.", "둘 다 의자를 적어도 네 개씩 깔고 드러누워, 크고 거친 재킷을 걸치고 있었다."], ["In a corner was a heap of coats and boat-cloaks, and a flag, all bundled up together.", "한쪽 구석에는 외투며 뱃사람 망토며 깃발이 한데 뒤엉켜 수북이 쌓여 있었다."]], [["They both rolled on to their feet in an untidy sort of manner, when we came in, and said, ‘Halloa, Murdstone!", "우리가 들어가자 둘 다 부스스 몸을 일으키더니 말했다. \"어이, 머드스톤!"], ["We thought you were dead!’", "우린 자네가 죽은 줄 알았네!\""]], [["‘Not yet,’ said Mr. Murdstone.", "\"아직은 아니라네.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And who’s this shaver?’ said one of the gentlemen, taking hold of me.", "\"근데 이 꼬마는 누군가?\" 신사 하나가 나를 붙잡으며 말했다."]], [["‘That’s Davy,’ returned Mr. Murdstone.", "\"데이비라네.\" 머드스톤 씨가 받았다."]], [["‘Davy who?’ said the gentleman.", "\"데이비 누구?\" 신사가 말했다."], ["‘Jones?’", "\"존스인가?\""]], [["‘Copperfield,’ said Mr. Murdstone.", "\"코퍼필드라네.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Bewitching Mrs. Copperfield’s encumbrance?’ cried the gentleman.", "그 매혹적인 코퍼필드 부인의 짐짝 말인가?\" 신사가 외쳤다."], ["‘The pretty little widow?’", "\"그 어여쁜 젊은 과부 말일세?\""]], [["‘Quinion,’ said Mr.", "\"퀴니언,\""], ["Murdstone, ‘take care, if you please.", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"제발 말조심하게."], ["Somebody’s sharp.’", "영리한 녀석이 있으니.\""]], [["‘Who is?’ asked the gentleman, laughing.", "\"누가?\" 신사가 웃으며 물었다."], ["I looked up, quickly; being curious to know.", "나는 궁금해서 얼른 고개를 들었다."]], [["‘Only Brooks of Sheffield,’ said Mr. Murdstone.", "\"셰필드의 브룩스 말일세.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다."]], [["I was quite relieved to find that it was only Brooks of Sheffield; for, at first, I really thought it was I.", "나는 그게 그저 셰필드의 브룩스라는 걸 알고 무척 마음을 놓았다. 처음엔 그게 정말 나인 줄 알았으니까."]], [["There seemed to be something very comical in the reputation of Mr.", "셰필드의 브룩스 씨라는 평판에는 무언가 몹시 우스운 구석이 있는 모양이었다."], ["Brooks of Sheffield, for both the gentlemen laughed heartily when he was mentioned, and Mr. Murdstone was a good deal amused also.", "그 이름이 나오자 두 신사가 다 같이 껄껄 웃었고, 머드스톤 씨도 자못 재미있어했으니까."], ["After some laughing, the gentleman whom he had called Quinion, said:", "한바탕 웃고 나서, 그가 퀴니언이라고 부른 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘And what is the opinion of Brooks of Sheffield, in reference to the projected business?’", "\"그래, 그 계획 중인 일에 대해서 셰필드의 브룩스 씨 의견은 어떻다나?\""]], [["‘Why, I don’t know that Brooks understands much about it at present,’ replied Mr.", "\"글쎄, 브룩스가 지금은 그 일을 잘 이해하는 것 같진 않네만,\""], ["Murdstone; ‘but he is not generally favourable, I believe.’", "머드스톤 씨가 받았다. \"내 보기에 대체로 탐탁잖아하는 눈치라네.\""]], [["There was more laughter at this, and Mr. Quinion said he would ring the bell for some sherry in which to drink to Brooks.", "이 말에 또 웃음이 터졌고, 퀴니언 씨는 브룩스를 위해 건배할 셰리주를 가져오라고 종을 울리겠다고 했다."], ["This he did; and when the wine came, he made me have a little, with a biscuit, and, before I drank it, stand up and say, ‘Confusion to Brooks of Sheffield!’ The toast was received with great applause, and such hearty laughter that it made me laugh too; at which they laughed the more.", "그가 그렇게 했고, 술이 나오자 그는 나에게도 비스킷과 함께 조금 마시게 하고는, 마시기 전에 일어서서 \"셰필드의 브룩스에게 파멸을!\" 하고 외치게 했다. 이 건배는 우레와 같은 박수와, 어찌나 호탕한 웃음으로 맞아졌던지 나까지 웃음이 나왔고, 그러자 그들은 더 크게 웃었다."], ["In short, we quite enjoyed ourselves.", "한마디로 우리는 아주 즐거운 시간을 보냈다."]], [["We walked about on the cliff after that, and sat on the grass, and looked at things through a telescope--I could make out nothing myself when it was put to my eye, but I pretended I could--and then we came back to the hotel to an early dinner.", "그 뒤에 우리는 절벽 위를 거닐다가, 풀밭에 앉아, 망원경으로 이것저것 구경했다. 망원경을 내 눈에 대어 주었을 때 나는 아무것도 알아볼 수 없었지만, 알아보는 척했다. 그러고 나서 우리는 호텔로 돌아와 이른 저녁을 먹었다."], ["All the time we were out, the two gentlemen smoked incessantly--which, I thought, if I might judge from the smell of their rough coats, they must have been doing, ever since the coats had first come home from the tailor’s.", "우리가 밖에 나가 있는 내내 두 신사는 쉴 새 없이 담배를 피워 댔는데, 그 거친 재킷 냄새로 미루어 짐작하자면, 그 재킷이 처음 양복점에서 나온 그날부터 줄곧 그래 온 게 분명했다."], ["I must not forget that we went on board the yacht, where they all three descended into the cabin, and were busy with some papers.", "우리가 요트에 올라탔던 일도 빠뜨려선 안 되겠다. 거기서 셋은 모두 선실로 내려가 무슨 서류를 들고 분주했다."], ["I saw them quite hard at work, when I looked down through the open skylight.", "열린 천창으로 내려다보니 그들이 한창 열심히 일하는 게 보였다."], ["They left me, during this time, with a very nice man with a very large head of red hair and a very small shiny hat upon it, who had got a cross-barred shirt or waistcoat on, with ‘Skylark’ in capital letters across the chest.", "그들은 그동안 나를 아주 친절한 사람에게 맡겨 두었는데, 그 사람은 머리가 아주 크고 붉은 머리카락이 수북했으며, 그 위에 아주 작고 반들거리는 모자를 얹고 있었다. 그는 격자무늬 셔츠인지 조끼인지를 입었는데, 가슴팍에 대문자로 '스카이라크'라고 적혀 있었다. 나는 그게 그의 이름인 줄 알았다."], ["I thought it was his name; and that as he lived on board ship and hadn’t a street door to put his name on, he put it there instead; but when I called him Mr. Skylark, he said it meant the vessel.", "그가 배 위에서 사니까 제 이름을 내걸 길거리 대문이 없어서, 대신 거기 적어 둔 거려니 했다. 그런데 내가 그를 스카이라크 씨라고 부르자, 그는 그게 배 이름이라고 했다."]], [["I observed all day that Mr. Murdstone was graver and steadier than the two gentlemen.", "나는 하루 종일 머드스톤 씨가 두 신사보다 더 무겁고 진중하다는 걸 눈여겨보았다."], ["They were very gay and careless.", "그 둘은 아주 들떠 있고 무심했다."], ["They joked freely with one another, but seldom with him. It appeared to me that he was more clever and cold than they were, and that they regarded him with something of my own feeling.", "그들은 서로 거리낌 없이 농을 주고받았지만, 그에게는 좀처럼 그러지 않았다. 내 보기에 그는 그들보다 더 영리하고 차가웠고, 그들도 나와 비슷한 감정으로 그를 대하는 것 같았다."], ["I remarked that, once or twice when Mr. Quinion was talking, he looked at Mr. Murdstone sideways, as if to make sure of his not being displeased; and that once when Mr.", "나는 한두 번, 퀴니언 씨가 이야기하다가, 그가 언짢아하지는 않는지 확인하려는 듯 머드스톤 씨를 곁눈질하는 걸 보았다."], ["Passnidge (the other gentleman) was in high spirits, he trod upon his foot, and gave him a secret caution with his eyes, to observe Mr. Murdstone, who was sitting stern and silent.", "또 한 번은 패스니지 씨(다른 신사다)가 한껏 들떠 있을 때, 퀴니언 씨가 그의 발을 밟고는, 엄숙히 입을 다물고 앉아 있는 머드스톤 씨를 잘 살피라고 눈짓으로 슬며시 주의를 주는 것도 보았다."], ["Nor do I recollect that Mr. Murdstone laughed at all that day, except at the Sheffield joke--and that, by the by, was his own.", "그리고 그날 머드스톤 씨가 웃은 적이 있었던가 싶은데, 셰필드 농담 때 빼고는 도무지 기억나지 않는다. 그나저나 그 농담은 본디 그가 만든 것이었다."]], [["We went home early in the evening.", "우리는 저녁 일찍 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["It was a very fine evening, and my mother and he had another stroll by the sweetbriar, while I was sent in to get my tea.", "아주 좋은 저녁이었고, 어머니와 그는 들장미 곁을 또 한 번 거닐었으며, 그동안 나는 차를 마시러 안으로 들여보내졌다."], ["When he was gone, my mother asked me all about the day I had had, and what they had said and done.", "그가 떠난 뒤, 어머니는 그날 하루를 어떻게 보냈는지, 그들이 무슨 말을 하고 무얼 했는지 나에게 죄다 캐물었다."], ["I mentioned what they had said about her, and she laughed, and told me they were impudent fellows who talked nonsense--but I knew it pleased her.", "나는 그들이 어머니에 대해 한 말을 들려주었고, 어머니는 웃으며 그들이 허튼소리나 늘어놓는 뻔뻔한 자들이라고 했다. 하지만 나는 그게 어머니를 기쁘게 했다는 걸 알았다."], ["I knew it quite as well as I know it now.", "지금 아는 것만큼이나 똑똑히 알았다."], ["I took the opportunity of asking if she was at all acquainted with Mr. Brooks of Sheffield, but she answered No, only she supposed he must be a manufacturer in the knife and fork way.", "나는 이참에 어머니가 셰필드의 브룩스 씨를 조금이라도 아는지 물어보았는데, 어머니는 아니라며, 다만 그가 나이프와 포크 만드는 일을 하는 제조업자이려니 짐작할 뿐이라고 대답했다."]], [["Can I say of her face--altered as I have reason to remember it, perished as I know it is--that it is gone, when here it comes before me at this instant, as distinct as any face that I may choose to look on in a crowded street?", "내가 어머니의 얼굴을 두고 — 내가 떠올릴 수밖에 없는 그 변해 버린 얼굴, 이제는 스러져 버린 줄 아는 그 얼굴을 두고 — 그것이 사라졌다고 말할 수 있을까? 지금 이 순간, 붐비는 거리에서 내가 골라 바라볼 어떤 얼굴 못지않게 또렷이 내 앞에 떠오르는데?"], ["Can I say of her innocent and girlish beauty, that it faded, and was no more, when its breath falls on my cheek now, as it fell that night?", "내가 어머니의 그 순결하고 소녀 같은 아름다움을 두고, 그것이 시들어 더는 없다고 말할 수 있을까? 그날 밤 그랬듯이 지금도 그 숨결이 내 뺨에 와 닿는데?"], ["Can I say she ever changed, when my remembrance brings her back to life, thus only; and, truer to its loving youth than I have been, or man ever is, still holds fast what it cherished then?", "내가 어머니가 변했다고 말할 수 있을까? 내 기억이 오직 이렇게 어머니를 되살려 내는데. 그리고 내가 그래 왔던 것보다, 또 누구라도 그러는 것보다 그 사랑스러운 젊음에 더 진실하게, 그 시절 소중히 품었던 것을 여태 굳게 붙들고 있는데."]], [["I write of her just as she was when I had gone to bed after this talk, and she came to bid me good night. She kneeled down playfully by the side of the bed, and laying her chin upon her hands, and laughing, said:", "나는 어머니를, 이 이야기를 나눈 뒤 내가 잠자리에 들고 어머니가 잘 자라는 인사를 하러 왔을 때 그 모습 그대로 적는다. 어머니는 장난스럽게 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 두 손에 턱을 괴고 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘What was it they said, Davy?", "\"그 사람들이 뭐라고 했다고, 데이비?"], ["Tell me again.", "다시 말해 봐."], ["I can’t believe it.’", "도무지 못 믿겠으니까.\""]], [["‘“Bewitching--”’ I began.", "\"'매혹적인—'\" 내가 입을 떼었다."]], [["My mother put her hands upon my lips to stop me.", "어머니가 두 손을 내 입술에 대어 막았다."]], [["‘It was never bewitching,’ she said, laughing.", "\"매혹적이라니, 그럴 리가.\" 어머니가 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘It never could have been bewitching, Davy. Now I know it wasn’t!’", "\"그게 매혹적이었을 리 없어, 데이비. 자, 이제 알겠다, 그런 게 아니었어!\""]], [["‘Yes, it was.", "\"맞아, 그랬어."], ["“Bewitching Mrs. Copperfield”,’ I repeated stoutly.", "'매혹적인 코퍼필드 부인.'\" 나는 꿋꿋이 되풀이했다."], ["‘And, “pretty.”’", "\"그리고 '어여쁘다'고도.\""]], [["‘No, no, it was never pretty.", "\"아니야, 아니야, 어여쁘다니 그럴 리 없어."], ["Not pretty,’ interposed my mother, laying her fingers on my lips again.", "어여쁘긴.\" 어머니가 다시 내 입술에 손가락을 대며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Yes it was.", "\"맞아, 그랬어."], ["“Pretty little widow.”’", "'어여쁜 젊은 과부.'\""]], [["‘What foolish, impudent creatures!’ cried my mother, laughing and covering her face.", "\"어쩜 그렇게 어리석고 뻔뻔한 사람들이람!\" 어머니가 웃으며 얼굴을 가리고 외쳤다."], ["‘What ridiculous men!", "\"어쩜 그런 우스꽝스러운 사람들이!"], ["An’t they?", "안 그러니?"], ["Davy dear--’", "데이비야—\""]], [["‘Well, Ma.’", "\"네, 엄마.\""]], [["‘Don’t tell Peggotty; she might be angry with them.", "\"패거티한테는 말하지 마. 그 애가 그 사람들한테 화낼지도 모르니까."], ["I am dreadfully angry with them myself; but I would rather Peggotty didn’t know.’", "나도 그 사람들한테 끔찍이 화가 나지만, 그래도 패거티는 모르는 게 낫겠어.\""]], [["I promised, of course; and we kissed one another over and over again, and I soon fell fast asleep.", "나는 물론 그러겠다고 약속했고, 우리는 몇 번이고 거듭 입을 맞추었으며, 나는 이내 곤히 잠들었다."]], [["It seems to me, at this distance of time, as if it were the next day when Peggotty broached the striking and adventurous proposition I am about to mention; but it was probably about two months afterwards.", "이렇게 오랜 세월이 지난 지금 돌이켜 보면, 패거티가 이제 이야기할 그 놀랍고도 모험 가득한 제안을 꺼낸 것이 바로 그다음 날 같지만, 아마 두어 달쯤 뒤였을 것이다."]], [["We were sitting as before, one evening (when my mother was out as before), in company with the stocking and the yard-measure, and the bit of wax, and the box with St.", "어느 날 저녁, 우리는 (어머니가 또 외출한 사이) 여느 때처럼 양말과 줄자와 밀랍 토막과, 뚜껑에 세인트폴 대성당이 그려진 반짇고리와, 악어 책을 곁에 두고 앉아 있었다."], ["Paul’s on the lid, and the crocodile book, when Peggotty, after looking at me several times, and opening her mouth as if she were going to speak, without doing it--which I thought was merely gaping, or I should have been rather alarmed--said coaxingly:", "그때 패거티가 나를 몇 번이나 바라보고, 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 입을 벌렸다가는 끝내 다물기를 거듭하더니 — 나는 그게 그냥 하품인 줄 알았으니 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 적잖이 놀랐을 것이다 — 살살 구슬리듯 말했다."]], [["‘Master Davy, how should you like to go along with me and spend a fortnight at my brother’s at Yarmouth?", "\"데이비 도련님, 저랑 같이 야머스에 있는 우리 오라버니 댁에 가서 두 주쯤 지내다 오면 어떨까요?"], ["Wouldn’t that be a treat?’", "그거 참 신나지 않겠어요?\""]], [["‘Is your brother an agreeable man, Peggotty?’ I inquired, provisionally.", "\"패거티네 오라버니는 좋은 사람이야, 패거티?\" 나는 일단 따져 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, what an agreeable man he is!’ cried Peggotty, holding up her hands.", "\"어머나, 얼마나 좋은 사람인데요!\" 패거티가 두 손을 번쩍 들며 외쳤다."], ["‘Then there’s the sea; and the boats and ships; and the fishermen; and the beach; and Am to play with--’", "\"게다가 바다도 있고요. 보트랑 배도 있고, 어부들도 있고, 모래밭도 있고, 그리고 함하고 같이 놀 수도—\""]], [["Peggotty meant her nephew Ham, mentioned in my first chapter; but she spoke of him as a morsel of English Grammar.", "패거티가 말한 건 첫 장에서 이야기한 조카 함이었다. 그런데 그 이름을 어찌나 짧게 끊어 말하던지 꼭 영문법 토막 같았다."]], [["I was flushed by her summary of delights, and replied that it would indeed be a treat, but what would my mother say?", "나는 그녀가 줄줄이 늘어놓은 즐거움에 얼굴이 화끈 달아올라, 정말 신날 것 같다고 대답하면서도, 어머니가 뭐라고 할까 싶다고 했다."]], [["‘Why then I’ll as good as bet a guinea,’ said Peggotty, intent upon my face, ‘that she’ll let us go.", "\"그럼 내 일 기니를 걸어도 좋아요,\" 패거티가 내 얼굴을 골똘히 들여다보며 말했다. \"마님이 우릴 보내 주실 거예요."], ["I’ll ask her, if you like, as soon as ever she comes home.", "도련님만 좋다면, 마님이 돌아오시는 대로 바로 여쭤볼게요."], ["There now!’", "자, 어때요!\""]], [["‘But what’s she to do while we’re away?’ said I, putting my small elbows on the table to argue the point.", "\"근데 우리가 가 있는 동안 엄만 어떡해?\" 나는 그 점을 따져 보려고 작은 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴며 말했다."], ["‘She can’t live by herself.’", "\"엄만 혼자 못 사는데.\""]], [["If Peggotty were looking for a hole, all of a sudden, in the heel of that stocking, it must have been a very little one indeed, and not worth darning.", "만약 패거티가 그 순간 양말 뒤꿈치에 난 구멍을 찾고 있던 거라면, 그건 기워 봐야 별 소용도 없을 만큼 아주 작은 구멍이었을 게 분명하다."]], [["‘I say! Peggotty!", "\"있잖아! 패거티!"], ["She can’t live by herself, you know.’", "엄만 혼자 못 산다니까.\""]], [["‘Oh, bless you!’ said Peggotty, looking at me again at last.", "\"어머나, 세상에!\" 마침내 패거티가 다시 나를 보며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t you know?", "\"도련님은 모르세요?"], ["She’s going to stay for a fortnight with Mrs. Grayper. Mrs.", "마님은 그레이퍼 부인 댁에서 두 주 동안 지내실 거예요."], ["Grayper’s going to have a lot of company.’", "그레이퍼 부인 댁에 손님이 많이 온대요.\""]], [["Oh! If that was it, I was quite ready to go.", "아! 그게 그렇다면야, 나는 갈 채비가 다 된 셈이었다."], ["I waited, in the utmost impatience, until my mother came home from Mrs. Grayper’s (for it was that identical neighbour), to ascertain if we could get leave to carry out this great idea.", "나는 어머니가 그레이퍼 부인 댁에서 (바로 그 이웃이었다) 돌아올 때까지, 이 위대한 계획을 실행에 옮길 허락을 받을 수 있을지 알아보려고 애가 타게 기다렸다."], ["Without being nearly so much surprised as I had expected, my mother entered into it readily; and it was all arranged that night, and my board and lodging during the visit were to be paid for.", "어머니는 내가 예상했던 만큼 그리 놀라지도 않고 선뜻 찬성했고, 모든 것이 그날 밤 매듭지어졌으며, 머무는 동안의 내 숙식비도 치르기로 했다."]], [["The day soon came for our going.", "떠날 날이 곧 다가왔다."], ["It was such an early day that it came soon, even to me, who was in a fever of expectation, and half afraid that an earthquake or a fiery mountain, or some other great convulsion of nature, might interpose to stop the expedition.", "그토록 가까운 날이라, 기대로 들떠 안달이 나서, 지진이나 불타는 산, 아니면 무슨 어마어마한 천재지변이라도 끼어들어 이 원정을 가로막지나 않을까 반쯤 겁먹고 있던 나에게도 그날은 금세 왔다."], ["We were to go in a carrier’s cart, which departed in the morning after breakfast.", "우리는 짐마차를 타고 가기로 했는데, 아침 식사 뒤에 출발하는 마차였다."], ["I would have given any money to have been allowed to wrap myself up over-night, and sleep in my hat and boots.", "나는 전날 밤부터 옷을 둘둘 차려입고, 모자와 장화까지 신은 채로 자도 좋다는 허락만 받을 수 있다면, 얼마든지 돈을 냈을 것이다."]], [["It touches me nearly now, although I tell it lightly, to recollect how eager I was to leave my happy home; to think how little I suspected what I did leave for ever.", "지금 이 일을 가볍게 이야기하고 있지만, 내가 그 행복한 보금자리를 떠나려고 얼마나 안달했는지를, 내가 영영 두고 떠나는 것이 무엇인지를 얼마나 까맣게 몰랐는지를 떠올리면, 지금도 가슴이 저민다."]], [["I am glad to recollect that when the carrier’s cart was at the gate, and my mother stood there kissing me, a grateful fondness for her and for the old place I had never turned my back upon before, made me cry.", "짐마차가 문 앞에 와 있고, 어머니가 거기 서서 나에게 입을 맞출 때, 어머니에 대한, 그리고 한 번도 등진 적 없던 그 정든 집에 대한 고마운 애틋함이 북받쳐 내가 울었던 것을 떠올리면 마음이 놓인다."], ["I am glad to know that my mother cried too, and that I felt her heart beat against mine.", "어머니도 울었다는 것, 그리고 어머니의 심장이 내 심장에 맞대어 뛰는 걸 내가 느꼈다는 것을 떠올리면 마음이 놓인다."]], [["I am glad to recollect that when the carrier began to move, my mother ran out at the gate, and called to him to stop, that she might kiss me once more.", "짐마차가 막 움직이기 시작했을 때, 어머니가 문밖으로 뛰어나와 마차를 세우라고 외쳤던 것을 떠올리면 마음이 놓인다. 나에게 한 번 더 입 맞추려고."], ["I am glad to dwell upon the earnestness and love with which she lifted up her face to mine, and did so.", "어머니가 얼마나 간절하고 애틋하게 제 얼굴을 내 얼굴로 들어 올려, 그렇게 입을 맞추었는지를 곱씹으면 마음이 놓인다."]], [["As we left her standing in the road, Mr. Murdstone came up to where she was, and seemed to expostulate with her for being so moved.", "우리가 어머니를 길에 남겨 두고 떠날 때, 머드스톤 씨가 어머니가 선 자리로 다가오더니, 그렇게 마음을 쏟는다고 어머니를 나무라는 듯했다."], ["I was looking back round the awning of the cart, and wondered what business it was of his.", "나는 마차 차양 너머로 뒤를 돌아보며, 그게 무슨 자기 일이라고 저러나 싶었다."], ["Peggotty, who was also looking back on the other side, seemed anything but satisfied; as the face she brought back in the cart denoted.", "반대편에서 함께 뒤를 돌아보던 패거티도, 마차로 다시 돌려놓은 그 얼굴로 미루어 보아, 영 못마땅한 기색이었다."]], [["I sat looking at Peggotty for some time, in a reverie on this supposititious case: whether, if she were employed to lose me like the boy in the fairy tale, I should be able to track my way home again by the buttons she would shed.", "나는 한참 동안 패거티를 바라보며, 이런 가정에 잠겨 공상에 빠졌다. 만약 동화 속 사내애처럼 그녀가 나를 내다 버리는 일을 맡는다면, 그녀가 흘리고 다닐 단추를 따라 내가 다시 집으로 길을 더듬어 찾아올 수 있을까 하는 공상에."]], [["# CHAPTER 3. I HAVE A CHANGE", "# CHAPTER 3. I HAVE A CHANGE"]], [["The carrier’s horse was the laziest horse in the world, I should hope, and shuffled along, with his head down, as if he liked to keep people waiting to whom the packages were directed.", "짐꾼의 말은 내가 보기에 세상에서 가장 게으른 말이었지 싶다. 그 말은 머리를 푹 숙인 채 발을 질질 끌며 걸었는데, 마치 짐을 받을 사람들을 일부러 기다리게 하는 걸 즐기기라도 하는 듯했다."], ["I fancied, indeed, that he sometimes chuckled audibly over this reflection, but the carrier said he was only troubled with a cough.", "사실 나는 그 말이 이따금 그런 생각에 들떠 소리 내어 킥킥거리는 것 같기도 했지만, 짐꾼은 그저 기침을 좀 앓을 뿐이라고 했다."], ["The carrier had a way of keeping his head down, like his horse, and of drooping sleepily forward as he drove, with one of his arms on each of his knees.", "짐꾼도 제 말처럼 머리를 푹 숙이는 버릇이 있었고, 마차를 몰 때면 두 팔을 양쪽 무릎에 하나씩 얹은 채 졸린 듯 앞으로 축 늘어졌다."], ["I say ‘drove’, but it struck me that the cart would have gone to Yarmouth quite as well without him, for the horse did all that; and as to conversation, he had no idea of it but whistling.", "'몰았다'고는 했지만, 내가 보기에 그 사람이 없어도 마차는 똑같이 야머스까지 잘 갔을 것이다. 그 일은 죄다 말이 했으니까. 그리고 대화로 말하자면, 짐꾼은 휘파람 부는 것 말고는 그런 걸 할 줄도 몰랐다."]], [["Peggotty had a basket of refreshments on her knee, which would have lasted us out handsomely, if we had been going to London by the same conveyance.", "패거티는 먹을거리가 담긴 바구니를 무릎에 올려놓고 있었는데, 우리가 이 마차로 런던까지 갔더라도 넉넉히 버틸 만한 양이었다."], ["We ate a good deal, and slept a good deal.", "우리는 꽤 많이 먹었고, 꽤 많이 잤다."], ["Peggotty always went to sleep with her chin upon the handle of the basket, her hold of which never relaxed; and I could not have believed unless I had heard her do it, that one defenceless woman could have snored so much.", "패거티는 늘 바구니 손잡이에 턱을 괸 채 잠들었는데, 그 손잡이를 쥔 손은 한 번도 풀어진 적이 없었다. 그리고 직접 듣지 않았더라면, 무방비 상태의 여인 하나가 그렇게 요란하게 코를 골 수 있다는 걸 결코 믿지 못했을 것이다."]], [["We made so many deviations up and down lanes, and were such a long time delivering a bedstead at a public-house, and calling at other places, that I was quite tired, and very glad, when we saw Yarmouth.", "우리는 골목을 따라 위로 아래로 어찌나 많이 돌아갔던지, 게다가 어느 선술집에 침대틀 하나를 내려놓는 데 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 또 다른 곳들에도 들렀던 탓에, 나는 완전히 지쳐 버렸고, 야머스가 눈에 들어왔을 때 무척 기뻤다."], ["It looked rather spongy and soppy, I thought, as I carried my eye over the great dull waste that lay across the river; and I could not help wondering, if the world were really as round as my geography book said, how any part of it came to be so flat.", "강 건너편에 펼쳐진 그 크고 황량한 벌판으로 눈길을 던지며, 나는 그곳이 어쩐지 물기 머금어 질척하고 흐물흐물해 보인다고 생각했다. 그리고 세상이 정말로 내 지리책 말처럼 둥글다면, 어떻게 그 일부분이 이토록 평평할 수 있는지 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But I reflected that Yarmouth might be situated at one of the poles; which would account for it.", "하지만 곰곰이 생각해 보니 야머스는 극지방 어느 한 곳에 자리한 것인지도 몰랐고, 그렇다면 설명이 되긴 했다."]], [["As we drew a little nearer, and saw the whole adjacent prospect lying a straight low line under the sky, I hinted to Peggotty that a mound or so might have improved it; and also that if the land had been a little more separated from the sea, and the town and the tide had not been quite so much mixed up, like toast and water, it would have been nicer.", "조금 더 가까이 다가가, 둘레의 온 풍경이 하늘 아래 곧고 나지막한 선을 그리며 누워 있는 것을 보자, 나는 패거티에게 둔덕이라도 몇 개 있었으면 좀 나아 보였을 거라고 슬쩍 말했다. 그리고 땅과 바다가 조금만 더 떨어져 있고, 마을과 밀물이 토스트와 물처럼 그렇게까지 뒤섞여 있지 않았더라면 더 보기 좋았을 거라고도 했다."], ["But Peggotty said, with greater emphasis than usual, that we must take things as we found them, and that, for her part, she was proud to call herself a Yarmouth Bloater.", "그러나 패거티는 평소보다 더 힘주어, 세상일은 있는 그대로 받아들여야 하는 법이며, 자기로 말하자면 스스로를 야머스 청어라 부르는 것이 자랑스럽다고 했다."]], [["When we got into the street (which was strange enough to me) and smelt the fish, and pitch, and oakum, and tar, and saw the sailors walking about, and the carts jingling up and down over the stones, I felt that I had done so busy a place an injustice; and said as much to Peggotty, who heard my expressions of delight with great complacency, and told me it was well known (I suppose to those who had the good fortune to be born Bloaters) that Yarmouth was, upon the whole, the finest place in the universe.", "우리가 (나에게는 무척 낯설었던) 거리로 들어서서 생선과 송진과 뱃밥과 타르 냄새를 맡고, 뱃사람들이 이리저리 걸어 다니는 것과 짐수레들이 자갈길 위로 덜그럭거리며 오르내리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 이렇게 부산한 곳을 내가 너무 박하게 봤다는 느낌이 들었다. 그래서 패거티에게 그렇게 말했더니, 패거티는 내 감탄을 무척 흐뭇하게 들으며, 야머스가 전체적으로 보아 온 우주에서 가장 멋진 곳이라는 건 (청어로 태어나는 행운을 누린 사람들 사이에서는) 익히 알려진 사실이라고 했다."]], [["‘Here’s my Am!’ screamed Peggotty, ‘growed out of knowledge!’", "\"우리 햄이 저기 있네!\" 패거티가 비명을 질렀다. \"몰라보게 컸어!\""]], [["He was waiting for us, in fact, at the public-house; and asked me how I found myself, like an old acquaintance.", "햄은 정말로 선술집에서 우리를 기다리고 있었는데, 오랜 친구라도 되는 양 내게 그동안 어떻게 지냈느냐고 물었다."], ["I did not feel, at first, that I knew him as well as he knew me, because he had never come to our house since the night I was born, and naturally he had the advantage of me.", "나는 처음엔 그가 나를 아는 만큼 내가 그를 잘 안다는 느낌이 들지 않았다. 그는 내가 태어난 날 밤 이후로 우리 집에 한 번도 온 적이 없었으니, 당연히 그가 나보다 유리한 처지였던 것이다."], ["But our intimacy was much advanced by his taking me on his back to carry me home.", "하지만 그가 나를 등에 업어 집까지 데려가면서 우리의 친밀함은 부쩍 깊어졌다."], ["He was, now, a huge, strong fellow of six feet high, broad in proportion, and round-shouldered; but with a simpering boy’s face and curly light hair that gave him quite a sheepish look.", "그는 이제 키가 육 척에 그에 걸맞게 떡 벌어진, 어깨가 둥글게 굽은 거대하고 힘센 사내였다. 그러나 실실 웃는 듯한 소년 같은 얼굴에 곱슬곱슬한 연한 머리카락이 그를 영락없이 어수룩해 보이게 했다."], ["He was dressed in a canvas jacket, and a pair of such very stiff trousers that they would have stood quite as well alone, without any legs in them.", "그는 캔버스 천 윗옷에, 어찌나 빳빳한지 다리를 넣지 않아도 혼자서 그대로 서 있을 법한 바지를 입고 있었다."], ["And you couldn’t so properly have said he wore a hat, as that he was covered in a-top, like an old building, with something pitchy.", "그리고 그가 모자를 썼다기보다는, 낡은 건물처럼 머리 꼭대기가 뭔가 송진 같은 것으로 덮여 있었다고 하는 편이 더 맞을 것이다."]], [["Ham carrying me on his back and a small box of ours under his arm, and Peggotty carrying another small box of ours, we turned down lanes bestrewn with bits of chips and little hillocks of sand, and went past gas-works, rope-walks, boat-builders’ yards, shipwrights’ yards, ship-breakers’ yards, caulkers’ yards, riggers’ lofts, smiths’ forges, and a great litter of such places, until we came out upon the dull waste I had already seen at a distance; when Ham said,", "햄이 나를 등에 업고 우리 작은 상자 하나를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 패거티가 또 다른 작은 상자를 들고서, 우리는 나무 부스러기와 자그마한 모래 둔덕이 흩뿌려진 골목길로 접어들었다. 그러고는 가스 공장과 밧줄 공장, 보트 만드는 작업장, 배 짓는 작업장, 배 부수는 작업장, 뱃밥 메우는 작업장, 삭구 다루는 다락, 대장간, 그리고 그 비슷한 곳들이 잔뜩 어질러진 사이를 지나, 마침내 내가 멀리서 이미 보았던 그 황량한 벌판으로 나왔다. 그러자 햄이 말했다."]], [["‘Yon’s our house, Mas’r Davy!’", "\"저게 우리 집이에요, 데이비 도련님!\""]], [["I looked in all directions, as far as I could stare over the wilderness, and away at the sea, and away at the river, but no house could I make out.", "나는 그 벌판 위로 눈에 닿는 데까지 사방을 둘러보고, 멀리 바다 쪽도 보고, 멀리 강 쪽도 보았지만, 집이라고는 도무지 찾아낼 수가 없었다."], ["There was a black barge, or some other kind of superannuated boat, not far off, high and dry on the ground, with an iron funnel sticking out of it for a chimney and smoking very cosily; but nothing else in the way of a habitation that was visible to me.", "멀지 않은 곳에 검은 거룻배, 아니 무슨 한물간 배 같은 것이 땅 위에 높직이 마른 채로 놓여 있었고, 거기서 굴뚝 삼은 쇠 연통이 삐죽 솟아 무척 아늑하게 연기를 피워 올리고 있었다. 그러나 사람이 사는 곳이라고 할 만한 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 내 눈에 보이지 않았다."]], [["‘That’s not it?’ said I.", "\"저건 아니지?\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘That ship-looking thing?’", "\"저 배처럼 생긴 거 말이야?\""]], [["‘That’s it, Mas’r Davy,’ returned Ham.", "\"저게 맞아요, 데이비 도련님.\" 햄이 대답했다."]], [["If it had been Aladdin’s palace, roc’s egg and all, I suppose I could not have been more charmed with the romantic idea of living in it.", "설령 그것이 알라딘의 궁전이고, 거기 로크 새의 알까지 다 있었다 한들, 나는 그 안에 산다는 낭만적인 생각에 그보다 더 홀딱 반할 수는 없었지 싶다."], ["There was a delightful door cut in the side, and it was roofed in, and there were little windows in it; but the wonderful charm of it was, that it was a real boat which had no doubt been upon the water hundreds of times, and which had never been intended to be lived in, on dry land.", "옆구리에 잘라 낸 멋진 문이 하나 있었고, 지붕이 덮여 있었으며, 자그마한 창문들도 달려 있었다. 그러나 그 놀라운 매력은, 그것이 분명 수백 번은 물 위에 떠 있었을 진짜 배이며, 마른 땅 위에서 사람이 살라고 만든 물건이 결코 아니라는 데 있었다."], ["That was the captivation of it to me.", "바로 그것이 나를 사로잡았다."], ["If it had ever been meant to be lived in, I might have thought it small, or inconvenient, or lonely; but never having been designed for any such use, it became a perfect abode.", "만약 그것이 애초에 사람 살라고 지은 것이었다면, 나는 비좁다거나 불편하다거나 외롭다거나 했을지도 모른다. 그러나 그런 쓰임새로 만든 것이 전혀 아니었기에, 그것은 더없이 완벽한 보금자리가 되었다."]], [["It was beautifully clean inside, and as tidy as possible.", "안쪽은 아름답도록 깨끗했고, 더할 나위 없이 정갈했다."], ["There was a table, and a Dutch clock, and a chest of drawers, and on the chest of drawers there was a tea-tray with a painting on it of a lady with a parasol, taking a walk with a military-looking child who was trundling a hoop.", "탁자가 하나, 네덜란드제 시계가 하나, 서랍장이 하나 있었고, 서랍장 위에는 차 쟁반이 하나 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에는 양산을 든 부인이 굴렁쇠를 굴리는 군인처럼 차려입은 아이와 함께 산책하는 그림이 그려져 있었다."], ["The tray was kept from tumbling down, by a bible; and the tray, if it had tumbled down, would have smashed a quantity of cups and saucers and a teapot that were grouped around the book.", "그 쟁반이 굴러떨어지지 않도록 받쳐 주는 것은 성경책 한 권이었다. 만약 쟁반이 굴러떨어졌더라면, 그 책 둘레에 모여 있던 찻잔과 받침 접시 한 무더기와 찻주전자가 죄다 박살이 났을 것이다."], ["On the walls there were some common coloured pictures, framed and glazed, of scripture subjects; such as I have never seen since in the hands of pedlars, without seeing the whole interior of Peggotty’s brother’s house again, at one view.", "벽에는 성경 이야기를 그린, 액자에 끼우고 유리를 댄 흔한 색채화 몇 점이 걸려 있었다. 그 뒤로 행상들 손에 든 그런 그림을 볼 때마다, 나는 어김없이 패거티 오라버니네 집 안 전체를 한눈에 다시 떠올리게 되곤 했다."], ["Abraham in red going to sacrifice Isaac in blue, and Daniel in yellow cast into a den of green lions, were the most prominent of these.", "그중에서도 빨간 옷을 입고 파란 옷을 입은 이삭을 제물로 바치러 가는 아브라함, 그리고 노란 옷을 입고 초록빛 사자 굴에 던져진 다니엘이 가장 눈에 띄었다."], ["Over the little mantelshelf, was a picture of the ‘Sarah Jane’ lugger, built at Sunderland, with a real little wooden stern stuck on to it; a work of art, combining composition with carpentry, which I considered to be one of the most enviable possessions that the world could afford.", "자그마한 벽난로 선반 위에는 선덜랜드에서 만든 '세라 제인'호 외돛배 그림이 걸려 있었는데, 거기에는 진짜 나무로 만든 작은 고물이 떡하니 붙어 있었다. 그것은 그림과 목공을 한데 어우른 예술품으로, 나는 세상이 베풀 수 있는 가장 부러운 소유물 가운데 하나라고 여겼다."], ["There were some hooks in the beams of the ceiling, the use of which I did not divine then; and some lockers and boxes and conveniences of that sort, which served for seats and eked out the chairs.", "천장 들보에는 갈고리 몇 개가 박혀 있었는데, 그때는 그 쓰임새를 짐작도 못 했다. 그리고 의자 노릇을 하며 모자라는 의자를 메워 주는 사물함이며 상자며 그런 따위의 편의용 물건들도 몇 개 있었다."]], [["All this I saw in the first glance after I crossed the threshold--child-like, according to my theory--and then Peggotty opened a little door and showed me my bedroom.", "문턱을 넘자마자 첫눈에 이 모든 것을 다 보았는데 — 내 지론대로 아이답게 말이다 — 그러고 나서 패거티가 작은 문 하나를 열고 내 침실을 보여 주었다."], ["It was the completest and most desirable bedroom ever seen--in the stern of the vessel; with a little window, where the rudder used to go through; a little looking-glass, just the right height for me, nailed against the wall, and framed with oyster-shells; a little bed, which there was just room enough to get into; and a nosegay of seaweed in a blue mug on the table.", "그것은 여태 본 가운데 가장 완벽하고 더없이 탐나는 침실로, 배의 고물 쪽에 있었다. 예전에 방향타가 지나던 자리에는 자그마한 창문이 하나 있었고, 딱 내 키에 맞는 작은 거울이 굴 껍데기로 테를 둘러 벽에 못 박혀 있었으며, 겨우 들어가 누울 만한 작은 침대가 하나, 그리고 탁자 위 파란 머그잔에는 해초를 꽂은 꽃다발이 놓여 있었다."], ["The walls were whitewashed as white as milk, and the patchwork counterpane made my eyes quite ache with its brightness.", "벽은 우유처럼 새하얗게 회칠이 되어 있었고, 조각보를 이은 침대보는 어찌나 화사한지 눈이 다 아릴 지경이었다."], ["One thing I particularly noticed in this delightful house, was the smell of fish; which was so searching, that when I took out my pocket-handkerchief to wipe my nose, I found it smelt exactly as if it had wrapped up a lobster.", "이 즐거운 집에서 내가 특히 눈여겨본 것 한 가지는 생선 냄새였는데, 어찌나 속속들이 배어들었던지, 코를 닦으려고 주머니 손수건을 꺼냈을 때 영락없이 바닷가재라도 싸 두었던 것처럼 그 냄새가 났다."], ["On my imparting this discovery in confidence to Peggotty, she informed me that her brother dealt in lobsters, crabs, and crawfish; and I afterwards found that a heap of these creatures, in a state of wonderful conglomeration with one another, and never leaving off pinching whatever they laid hold of, were usually to be found in a little wooden outhouse where the pots and kettles were kept.", "이 발견을 패거티에게 가만히 털어놓았더니, 패거티는 제 오라버니가 바닷가재며 게며 가재 따위를 다룬다고 알려 주었다. 그리고 나는 나중에야, 그 짐승들이 한 무더기로 서로 놀랍도록 뒤엉킨 채, 무엇이든 붙잡으면 쉴 새 없이 집게로 집어 대면서, 냄비며 솥을 보관하는 작은 나무 헛간 안에 으레 모여 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["We were welcomed by a very civil woman in a white apron, whom I had seen curtseying at the door when I was on Ham’s back, about a quarter of a mile off.", "우리는 하얀 앞치마를 두른 무척 예의 바른 아낙의 환대를 받았는데, 내가 햄의 등에 업혀 사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 있을 때 문간에서 무릎을 굽혀 인사하던 그 여인이었다."], ["Likewise by a most beautiful little girl (or I thought her so) with a necklace of blue beads on, who wouldn’t let me kiss her when I offered to, but ran away and hid herself.", "또 무척 어여쁜 (적어도 내 눈에는 그렇게 보인) 어린 여자아이도 우리를 맞아 주었는데, 파란 구슬 목걸이를 하고 있었고, 내가 입맞춤을 하려 했더니 마다하며 달아나 숨어 버렸다."], ["By and by, when we had dined in a sumptuous manner off boiled dabs, melted butter, and potatoes, with a chop for me, a hairy man with a very good-natured face came home.", "이윽고 우리가 삶은 가자미와 녹인 버터, 감자로, 게다가 나에게는 따로 고기 토막까지 곁들여 호화롭게 식사를 마쳤을 무렵, 무척 사람 좋아 보이는 얼굴에 털북숭이인 남자가 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["As he called Peggotty ‘Lass’, and gave her a hearty smack on the cheek, I had no doubt, from the general propriety of her conduct, that he was her brother; and so he turned out--being presently introduced to me as Mr. Peggotty, the master of the house.", "그가 패거티를 '아가씨'라 부르며 뺨에 정겹게 쪽 입을 맞추는 데다, 패거티가 줄곧 점잖게 처신하는 것으로 미루어, 나는 그가 패거티의 오라버니임을 의심치 않았다. 과연 그러했으니, 이내 그는 이 집의 주인 패거티 씨라고 내게 소개되었다."]], [["‘Glad to see you, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"만나서 반가워요, 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty. ‘You’ll find us rough, sir, but you’ll find us ready.’", "\"여긴 거칠다고 느낄 거예요, 도련님. 하지만 채비만큼은 야무지다고 느낄 거예요.\""]], [["I thanked him, and replied that I was sure I should be happy in such a delightful place.", "나는 고맙다고 인사하고는, 이렇게 즐거운 곳에서라면 틀림없이 행복할 것 같다고 대답했다."]], [["‘How’s your Ma, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"어머님은 어떠세요, 도련님?\""], ["Peggotty. ‘Did you leave her pretty jolly?’", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"꽤 명랑하게 잘 계시던가요?\""]], [["I gave Mr. Peggotty to understand that she was as jolly as I could wish, and that she desired her compliments--which was a polite fiction on my part.", "나는 어머니가 더 바랄 나위 없이 명랑하시며, 안부를 전하더라고 패거티 씨에게 일러 주었다 — 사실은 내 쪽에서 점잖게 지어낸 거짓말이었다."]], [["‘I’m much obleeged to her, I’m sure,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 고마우신 말씀이네요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty. ‘Well, sir, if you can make out here, fur a fortnut, ‘long wi’ her,’ nodding at his sister, ‘and Ham, and little Em’ly, we shall be proud of your company.’", "\"그럼, 도련님, 여기서 보름쯤, 우리 누이랑,\" 누이동생을 향해 고갯짓을 하며, \"그리고 햄이랑 어린 에밀리랑 같이 잘 지내 주신다면, 우린 도련님과 함께한 걸 자랑스레 여길 거예요.\""]], [["Having done the honours of his house in this hospitable manner, Mr. Peggotty went out to wash himself in a kettleful of hot water, remarking that ‘cold would never get his muck off’.", "이렇게 손님 대접하는 인사치레를 후하게 마치고 나서, 패거티 씨는 솥 가득한 뜨거운 물로 몸을 씻으러 밖으로 나가며, \"찬물로는 이 때가 절대 안 벗겨진다\"고 한마디 했다."], ["He soon returned, greatly improved in appearance; but so rubicund, that I couldn’t help thinking his face had this in common with the lobsters, crabs, and crawfish,--that it went into the hot water very black, and came out very red.", "그는 곧 몰라보게 말끔해진 모습으로 돌아왔는데, 어찌나 벌건지, 나는 그의 얼굴이 바닷가재며 게며 가재와 한 가지 닮은 점이 있다는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었다 — 새카만 채로 뜨거운 물에 들어갔다가 시뻘게져 나온다는 점 말이다."]], [["After tea, when the door was shut and all was made snug (the nights being cold and misty now), it seemed to me the most delicious retreat that the imagination of man could conceive.", "차를 마신 뒤, 문이 닫히고 모든 것이 아늑해지자 (이제 밤은 춥고 안개가 끼었다), 그곳은 인간의 상상이 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 황홀한 은신처처럼 느껴졌다."], ["To hear the wind getting up out at sea, to know that the fog was creeping over the desolate flat outside, and to look at the fire, and think that there was no house near but this one, and this one a boat, was like enchantment.", "바다 저편에서 바람이 일어나는 소리를 듣고, 바깥 황량한 벌판 위로 안개가 슬금슬금 기어드는 것을 알면서, 난롯불을 바라보며, 이 집 말고는 근처에 집 한 채 없고, 게다가 이 집이 배라는 걸 떠올리는 것은 마치 마법 같았다."], ["Little Em’ly had overcome her shyness, and was sitting by my side upon the lowest and least of the lockers, which was just large enough for us two, and just fitted into the chimney corner. Mrs.", "어린 에밀리는 수줍음을 떨쳐 내고, 가장 낮고 가장 작은 사물함 위에 내 곁에 앉아 있었는데, 그것은 딱 우리 둘이 앉을 만한 크기였고 벽난로 모퉁이에 꼭 맞게 들어앉아 있었다."], ["Peggotty with the white apron, was knitting on the opposite side of the fire.", "하얀 앞치마를 두른 거미지 부인은 난로 맞은편에서 뜨개질을 하고 있었다."], ["Peggotty at her needlework was as much at home with St. Paul’s and the bit of wax-candle, as if they had never known any other roof.", "패거티는 바느질거리를 앞에 두고서, 성 바울 성당이 그려진 반짇고리와 밀랍 양초 토막을 마치 다른 지붕 아래선 한 번도 본 적 없다는 듯이 정겹게 다루고 있었다."], ["Ham, who had been giving me my first lesson in all-fours, was trying to recollect a scheme of telling fortunes with the dirty cards, and was printing off fishy impressions of his thumb on all the cards he turned. Mr. Peggotty was smoking his pipe.", "햄은 내게 카드놀이 첫 수업을 막 가르쳐 주던 참인데, 이번엔 더러운 카드로 점치는 법을 떠올려 보려 애쓰면서, 뒤집는 카드마다 생선 냄새 밴 엄지 자국을 꾹꾹 찍어 대고 있었다. 패거티 씨는 파이프 담배를 피우고 있었다."], ["I felt it was a time for conversation and confidence.", "나는 이제 이야기를 나누며 흉금을 털어놓을 때라고 느꼈다. \"패거티 아저씨!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Peggotty!’ says I.", "\"네, 도련님.\""], ["‘Sir,’ says he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["‘Did you give your son the name of Ham, because you lived in a sort of ark?’", "\"아들 이름을 햄이라고 지은 건, 일종의 방주 같은 데 살아서 그런 거예요?\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty seemed to think it a deep idea, but answered:", "패거티 씨는 그것이 꽤 심오한 생각이라고 여기는 듯했으나, 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["‘No, sir.", "\"아뇨, 도련님."], ["I never giv him no name.’", "그 애한테 이름은 내가 안 지었어요.\""]], [["‘Who gave him that name, then?’ said I, putting question number two of the catechism to Mr. Peggotty.", "\"그럼 누가 그 이름을 지었어요?\" 나는 패거티 씨에게 문답 둘째 항목을 던지며 물었다."]], [["‘Why, sir, his father giv it him,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"아, 그게요, 도련님, 그 애 아버지가 지었어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I thought you were his father!’", "\"아저씨가 그 애 아버지인 줄 알았는데요!\""]], [["‘My brother Joe was his father,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"내 동생 조가 그 애 아버지였지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Dead, Mr. Peggotty?’ I hinted, after a respectful pause.", "\"돌아가셨어요, 패거티 아저씨?\" 나는 정중하게 한 박자 쉬고 나서 슬쩍 물었다."]], [["‘Drowndead,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"물에 빠져 돌아갔어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["I was very much surprised that Mr. Peggotty was not Ham’s father, and began to wonder whether I was mistaken about his relationship to anybody else there.", "나는 패거티 씨가 햄의 아버지가 아니라는 데 무척 놀랐고, 혹시 거기 있는 다른 사람들과의 관계도 내가 잘못 알고 있는 게 아닐까 의아해지기 시작했다."], ["I was so curious to know, that I made up my mind to have it out with Mr. Peggotty.", "너무도 알고 싶었던 나머지, 나는 패거티 씨에게 끝까지 캐물어 보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["‘Little Em’ly,’ I said, glancing at her.", "\"어린 에밀리는,\" 내가 에밀리를 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["‘She is your daughter, isn’t she, Mr. Peggotty?’", "\"아저씨 딸이죠, 그렇죠, 패거티 아저씨?\""]], [["‘No, sir. My brother-in-law, Tom, was her father.’", "\"아뇨, 도련님. 내 처남 톰이 그 애 아버지였어요.\""]], [["I couldn’t help it.", "나는 어쩔 수가 없었다."], ["‘--Dead, Mr. Peggotty?’ I hinted, after another respectful silence.", "\"돌아가셨어요, 패거티 아저씨?\" 나는 또 한 번 정중하게 침묵을 둔 뒤에 슬쩍 물었다."]], [["‘Drowndead,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"물에 빠져 돌아갔어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["I felt the difficulty of resuming the subject, but had not got to the bottom of it yet, and must get to the bottom somehow. So I said:", "나는 그 화제를 다시 꺼내기가 거북하다고 느꼈으나, 아직 바닥까지 파헤치지 못했으니, 어떻게든 끝장을 봐야 했다. 그래서 나는 말했다."]], [["‘Haven’t you ANY children, Mr. Peggotty?’", "\"아저씨는 자식이 하나도 없어요, 패거티 아저씨?\""]], [["‘No, master,’ he answered with a short laugh.", "\"없어요, 도련님.\" 그가 짤막하게 웃으며 대답했다."], ["‘I’m a bacheldore.’", "\"난 총각이에요.\""]], [["‘A bachelor!’ I said, astonished.", "\"총각이라뇨!\" 나는 깜짝 놀라 말했다."], ["‘Why, who’s that, Mr. Peggotty?’ pointing to the person in the apron who was knitting.", "\"그럼 저분은 누구예요, 패거티 아저씨?\" 앞치마를 두른 채 뜨개질하는 사람을 가리키며."]], [["‘That’s Missis Gummidge,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"저 사람은 거미지 부인이에요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Gummidge, Mr. Peggotty?’", "\"거미지요, 패거티 아저씨?\""]], [["But at this point Peggotty--I mean my own peculiar Peggotty--made such impressive motions to me not to ask any more questions, that I could only sit and look at all the silent company, until it was time to go to bed.", "그러나 이 대목에서 패거티가 — 그러니까 내 특별한 패거티 말이다 — 더는 아무것도 묻지 말라고 어찌나 단단히 손짓을 해 보였던지, 나는 그저 앉아서 그 말없는 일행을 다 둘러보다가 잠자리에 들 시간을 맞을 수밖에 없었다."], ["Then, in the privacy of my own little cabin, she informed me that Ham and Em’ly were an orphan nephew and niece, whom my host had at different times adopted in their childhood, when they were left destitute: and that Mrs. Gummidge was the widow of his partner in a boat, who had died very poor.", "그러고 나서 내 작은 선실에서 단둘이 있을 때, 패거티는 햄과 에밀리가 고아가 된 조카들로, 둘이 어릴 적 빈털터리로 남겨졌을 때 우리 집주인이 이런저런 때에 거두어 들였다고 알려 주었다. 그리고 거미지 부인은 그의 배 동업자였던 사람의 미망인인데, 그 사람이 무척 가난하게 죽었다고 했다."], ["He was but a poor man himself, said Peggotty, but as good as gold and as true as steel--those were her similes.", "패거티 말로는, 그 자신도 가난한 사람일 뿐이지만, 황금처럼 착하고 강철처럼 진실하다고 했다 — 그것이 패거티가 든 비유였다."], ["The only subject, she informed me, on which he ever showed a violent temper or swore an oath, was this generosity of his; and if it were ever referred to, by any one of them, he struck the table a heavy blow with his right hand (had split it on one such occasion), and swore a dreadful oath that he would be ‘Gormed’ if he didn’t cut and run for good, if it was ever mentioned again.", "그가 사납게 성을 내거나 욕설을 내뱉는 유일한 화제는 바로 이 베푸는 마음씨라고 했다. 만약 누구라도 그 일을 입에 올리면, 그는 오른손으로 탁자를 쾅 내리치고는 (한번은 그러다 탁자를 쪼개기도 했다), 그 얘기가 또다시 나오면 영영 뛰쳐나가 버릴 거라고, 안 그러면 자기가 '고름드'가 되겠다며 무시무시한 맹세를 했다."], ["It appeared, in answer to my inquiries, that nobody had the least idea of the etymology of this terrible verb passive to be gormed; but that they all regarded it as constituting a most solemn imprecation.", "내가 캐물어 보니, 이 무서운 수동태 동사 '고름드가 되다'의 어원을 아는 사람은 아무도 없는 듯했으나, 다들 그것을 더없이 엄숙한 저주의 말로 여기고 있었다."]], [["I was very sensible of my entertainer’s goodness, and listened to the women’s going to bed in another little crib like mine at the opposite end of the boat, and to him and Ham hanging up two hammocks for themselves on the hooks I had noticed in the roof, in a very luxurious state of mind, enhanced by my being sleepy.", "나는 나를 맞아 준 주인의 착한 마음씨를 깊이 느끼면서, 여자들이 배 반대쪽 끝에 있는, 내 것과 같은 작은 침대로 자러 가는 소리며, 패거티 씨와 햄이 내가 눈여겨봤던 천장 갈고리에 제 해먹 두 개를 매다는 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 무척 호사스러운 기분이었고, 졸음이 밀려와 그 기분은 한층 더 짙어졌다."], ["As slumber gradually stole upon me, I heard the wind howling out at sea and coming on across the flat so fiercely, that I had a lazy apprehension of the great deep rising in the night.", "잠이 차츰 나를 덮쳐 올 때, 나는 바다 저편에서 바람이 울부짖으며 벌판을 가로질러 어찌나 사납게 몰려오는지 듣고서, 밤사이 저 깊은 바다가 솟구쳐 오르지나 않을까 하는 막연한 두려움이 들었다."], ["But I bethought myself that I was in a boat, after all; and that a man like Mr. Peggotty was not a bad person to have on board if anything did happen.", "그러나 어쨌든 나는 배 안에 있고, 만에 하나 무슨 일이 벌어지더라도 패거티 씨 같은 사람이 함께 타고 있다면 결코 나쁘지 않다고 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["Nothing happened, however, worse than morning.", "그러나 아침 말고는 더 나쁜 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Almost as soon as it shone upon the oyster-shell frame of my mirror I was out of bed, and out with little Em’ly, picking up stones upon the beach.", "굴 껍데기 테를 두른 내 거울 위로 아침 햇살이 비치자마자, 나는 침대에서 빠져나와 어린 에밀리와 함께 밖으로 나가, 바닷가에서 돌멩이를 주웠다."]], [["‘You’re quite a sailor, I suppose?’ I said to Em’ly.", "\"넌 영락없는 뱃사람이겠지?\" 내가 에밀리에게 말했다."], ["I don’t know that I supposed anything of the kind, but I felt it an act of gallantry to say something; and a shining sail close to us made such a pretty little image of itself, at the moment, in her bright eye, that it came into my head to say this.", "딱히 그런 생각이 들었던 건 아니지만, 무언가 한마디 건네는 것이 신사다운 행동이라고 느꼈다. 게다가 바로 그 순간, 가까이서 반짝이는 돛 하나가 에밀리의 빛나는 눈동자 속에 자그마하고 예쁜 제 모습을 비추는 바람에, 이런 말이 문득 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["‘No,’ replied Em’ly, shaking her head, ‘I’m afraid of the sea.’", "\"아니.\" 에밀리가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"난 바다가 무서워.\""]], [["‘Afraid!’ I said, with a becoming air of boldness, and looking very big at the mighty ocean.", "\"무섭다고!\" 나는 제법 대담한 척하며, 저 드넓은 대양을 향해 한껏 거드름을 피우는 눈길을 던지며 말했다."], ["‘I an’t!’", "\"난 안 그런데!\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["but it’s cruel,’ said Em’ly.", "하지만 바다는 잔인해.\" 에밀리가 말했다."], ["‘I have seen it very cruel to some of our men. I have seen it tear a boat as big as our house, all to pieces.’", "\"난 바다가 우리 마을 사람들 몇몇한테 얼마나 잔인했는지 봤어. 우리 집만 한 배를 산산조각 내는 것도 봤는걸.\""]], [["‘I hope it wasn’t the boat that--’", "\"그게 설마 그 배는—\""]], [["‘That father was drownded in?’ said Em’ly. ‘No.", "\"우리 아버지가 빠져 죽은 배냐고?\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"아니."], ["Not that one, I never see that boat.’", "그 배는 아니야. 난 그 배는 한 번도 못 봤어.\""]], [["‘Nor him?’ I asked her.", "\"아버지도?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["Little Em’ly shook her head. ‘Not to remember!’", "어린 에밀리는 고개를 저었다. \"기억에 남을 만큼은 못 봤어!\""]], [["Here was a coincidence!", "이런 우연이 다 있나!"], ["I immediately went into an explanation how I had never seen my own father; and how my mother and I had always lived by ourselves in the happiest state imaginable, and lived so then, and always meant to live so; and how my father’s grave was in the churchyard near our house, and shaded by a tree, beneath the boughs of which I had walked and heard the birds sing many a pleasant morning.", "나는 곧장 설명을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 나도 내 아버지를 한 번도 본 적이 없다는 것, 어머니와 나는 늘 단둘이서 더없이 행복하게 살아왔고 지금도 그렇게 살며 앞으로도 죽 그렇게 살 작정이라는 것, 그리고 내 아버지 무덤은 우리 집 가까운 교회 묘지에 있는데, 나무 그늘에 가려져 있고, 나는 즐거운 아침마다 그 가지 밑을 거닐며 새소리를 들었다는 것을 말이다."], ["But there were some differences between Em’ly’s orphanhood and mine, it appeared.", "그러나 에밀리의 고아 신세와 내 처지 사이에는 몇 가지 다른 점이 있는 듯했다."], ["She had lost her mother before her father; and where her father’s grave was no one knew, except that it was somewhere in the depths of the sea.", "에밀리는 아버지보다 어머니를 먼저 잃었고, 아버지 무덤이 어디 있는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 다만 그 깊은 바닷속 어딘가라는 것만 알 뿐이었다."]], [["‘Besides,’ said Em’ly, as she looked about for shells and pebbles, ‘your father was a gentleman and your mother is a lady; and my father was a fisherman and my mother was a fisherman’s daughter, and my uncle Dan is a fisherman.’", "\"게다가,\" 에밀리가 조개껍데기와 조약돌을 찾아 두리번거리며 말했다. \"네 아버지는 신사였고 네 어머니는 귀부인이잖아. 우리 아버지는 어부였고 우리 어머니는 어부의 딸이었고, 우리 댄 아저씨도 어부야.\""]], [["‘Dan is Mr. Peggotty, is he?’ said I.", "\"댄이 패거티 아저씨지?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Uncle Dan--yonder,’ answered Em’ly, nodding at the boat-house.", "\"댄 아저씨 — 저기 계신.\" 에밀리가 그 배 모양 집을 향해 고갯짓하며 대답했다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"응."], ["I mean him.", "그분 말이야."], ["He must be very good, I should think?’", "무척 좋은 분 같은데, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘Good?’ said Em’ly.", "\"좋다고?\" 에밀리가 말했다."], ["‘If I was ever to be a lady, I’d give him a sky-blue coat with diamond buttons, nankeen trousers, a red velvet waistcoat, a cocked hat, a large gold watch, a silver pipe, and a box of money.’", "\"내가 만약 귀부인이 된다면, 아저씨한테 다이아몬드 단추가 달린 하늘색 외투랑, 무명 바지랑, 빨간 벨벳 조끼랑, 삼각모자랑, 커다란 금시계랑, 은 파이프랑, 돈 상자를 드릴 거야.\""]], [["I said I had no doubt that Mr. Peggotty well deserved these treasures.", "나는 패거티 씨가 그런 보물들을 받을 자격이 충분하다는 데 의심의 여지가 없다고 말했다."], ["I must acknowledge that I felt it difficult to picture him quite at his ease in the raiment proposed for him by his grateful little niece, and that I was particularly doubtful of the policy of the cocked hat; but I kept these sentiments to myself.", "솔직히 고백하건대, 고마워하는 어린 조카가 골라 준 그 차림새를 걸친 그가 마음 편히 있는 모습은 도무지 그려지지 않았고, 특히 삼각모자가 과연 현명한 선택일지 영 미심쩍었지만, 이런 생각은 혼자 속으로만 간직했다."]], [["Little Em’ly had stopped and looked up at the sky in her enumeration of these articles, as if they were a glorious vision.", "어린 에밀리는 이 물건들을 하나하나 늘어놓다 말고 걸음을 멈추고는, 그것들이 무슨 찬란한 환영이라도 되는 양 하늘을 올려다보았다."], ["We went on again, picking up shells and pebbles.", "우리는 다시 걸음을 옮기며 조개껍데기와 조약돌을 주웠다."]], [["‘You would like to be a lady?’ I said.", "\"넌 귀부인이 되고 싶구나?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Emily looked at me, and laughed and nodded ‘yes’.", "에밀리는 나를 보고 웃으며 \"응\" 하고 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘I should like it very much.", "\"난 정말 그렇게 되고 싶어."], ["We would all be gentlefolks together, then.", "그러면 우리 다 같이 양반이 되는 거잖아."], ["Me, and uncle, and Ham, and Mrs. Gummidge.", "나랑, 아저씨랑, 햄이랑, 거미지 부인이랑."], ["We wouldn’t mind then, when there comes stormy weather.---Not for our own sakes, I mean.", "그럼 사나운 날씨가 와도 무섭지 않을 거야. 우리 자신을 위해서라는 게 아니라 말이야."], ["We would for the poor fishermen’s, to be sure, and we’d help ‘em with money when they come to any hurt.’ This seemed to me to be a very satisfactory and therefore not at all improbable picture.", "물론 가엾은 어부들을 위해서 무섭긴 하겠지. 그래서 그 사람들이 무슨 일이라도 당하면 우리가 돈으로 도와줄 거야.\" 이것은 나에게 무척 만족스러운, 따라서 전혀 있을 법하지 않은 그림은 아니라고 여겨졌다."], ["I expressed my pleasure in the contemplation of it, and little Em’ly was emboldened to say, shyly,", "나는 그 그림을 그려 보는 것이 즐겁다고 말했고, 그러자 어린 에밀리는 용기를 내어 수줍게 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t you think you are afraid of the sea, now?’", "\"넌 이제 바다가 무섭지 않은 것 같아?\""]], [["It was quiet enough to reassure me, but I have no doubt if I had seen a moderately large wave come tumbling in, I should have taken to my heels, with an awful recollection of her drowned relations.", "바다는 충분히 잔잔해서 나를 안심시킬 만했지만, 만약 제법 큰 파도가 굽이치며 밀려오는 것을 봤더라면, 에밀리의 물에 빠져 죽은 친척들을 무섭게 떠올리며 줄행랑을 쳤을 게 분명하다. 그래도 나는 \"안 무서워\""], ["However, I said ‘No,’ and I added, ‘You don’t seem to be either, though you say you are,’--for she was walking much too near the brink of a sort of old jetty or wooden causeway we had strolled upon, and I was afraid of her falling over.", "하고 말했고, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"넌 무섭다면서, 정작 무서워하는 것 같지가 않은걸.\" 에밀리가 우리가 거닐던 낡은 부두인지 나무 둑길인지 모를 그 가장자리에 너무 바짝 붙어 걷고 있어서, 나는 그녀가 떨어질까 봐 겁이 났던 것이다."]], [["‘I’m not afraid in this way,’ said little Em’ly.", "\"난 그런 식으로는 안 무서워.\" 어린 에밀리가 말했다."], ["‘But I wake when it blows, and tremble to think of Uncle Dan and Ham and believe I hear ‘em crying out for help.", "\"하지만 바람이 불면 잠이 깨고, 댄 아저씨랑 햄이 생각나서 벌벌 떨려. 두 사람이 살려 달라고 울부짖는 소리가 들리는 것 같거든."], ["That’s why I should like so much to be a lady.", "그래서 귀부인이 그렇게나 되고 싶은 거야."], ["But I’m not afraid in this way. Not a bit.", "하지만 그런 식으로는 안 무서워. 조금도."], ["Look here!’", "자, 이거 봐!\""]], [["She started from my side, and ran along a jagged timber which protruded from the place we stood upon, and overhung the deep water at some height, without the least defence.", "에밀리는 내 곁에서 훌쩍 뛰쳐나가, 우리가 서 있던 곳에서 삐죽 튀어나온 들쭉날쭉한 통나무를 따라 달려갔는데, 그 통나무는 깊은 물 위로 제법 높이 매달려 있는 데다 아무런 난간 하나 없었다."], ["The incident is so impressed on my remembrance, that if I were a draughtsman I could draw its form here, I dare say, accurately as it was that day, and little Em’ly springing forward to her destruction (as it appeared to me), with a look that I have never forgotten, directed far out to sea.", "그 광경이 내 기억에 어찌나 깊이 새겨졌던지, 내가 화가였다면 지금 여기에 그날 그대로의 모습을 정확하게 그려 낼 수 있을 것이다. 어린 에밀리가 (내 눈에는 그렇게 보였는데) 제 파멸을 향해 앞으로 훌쩍 뛰어들며, 저 멀리 바다 쪽을 향한, 내가 결코 잊지 못할 표정을 짓고 있는 모습을 말이다."]], [["The light, bold, fluttering little figure turned and came back safe to me, and I soon laughed at my fears, and at the cry I had uttered; fruitlessly in any case, for there was no one near.", "그 가뿐하고 대담하고 팔랑거리는 작은 모습은 돌아서서 무사히 내게 되돌아왔고, 나는 이내 내 두려움을, 그리고 내가 질렀던 비명을 비웃었다. 어차피 헛된 비명이었으니, 근처에는 아무도 없었던 것이다."], ["But there have been times since, in my manhood, many times there have been, when I have thought, Is it possible, among the possibilities of hidden things, that in the sudden rashness of the child and her wild look so far off, there was any merciful attraction of her into danger, any tempting her towards him permitted on the part of her dead father, that her life might have a chance of ending that day?", "그러나 그 뒤로, 어른이 된 다음, 나는 여러 번, 정말 여러 번 이런 생각을 해 보았다. 숨겨진 일들의 온갖 가능성 가운데, 그 아이의 느닷없는 무모함과 저 멀리 던진 그 거친 눈빛 속에, 죽은 아버지가 딸을 위험 쪽으로 자비롭게 끌어당기는 어떤 힘이, 그 애를 자기 곁으로 유혹하도록 허락된 어떤 손길이 있어서, 그 애의 목숨이 그날로 끝날 기회를 얻은 것은 아니었을까 하고."], ["There has been a time since when I have wondered whether, if the life before her could have been revealed to me at a glance, and so revealed as that a child could fully comprehend it, and if her preservation could have depended on a motion of my hand, I ought to have held it up to save her.", "그 뒤로 나는 또 이런 의문을 품은 적도 있다. 만약 그 애 앞에 놓인 삶이 한눈에 내게 드러났더라면, 그것도 어린아이가 온전히 이해할 만큼 드러났더라면, 그리고 그 애의 목숨이 내 손짓 하나에 달려 있었더라면, 나는 과연 그 애를 살리려고 손을 들었어야 했을까 하고."], ["There has been a time since--I do not say it lasted long, but it has been--when I have asked myself the question, would it have been better for little Em’ly to have had the waters close above her head that morning in my sight; and when I have answered Yes, it would have been.", "그 뒤로 나는 또 이런 자문을 한 적도 있다 — 오래 가지는 않았다고 해 두자, 하지만 그런 적이 있었다 — 그날 아침 내가 보는 앞에서 그 물이 어린 에밀리의 머리 위로 덮쳤더라면 그 애한테 차라리 나았을까, 하고. 그리고 나는 그렇다고, 차라리 나았을 거라고 답한 적도 있다."]], [["This may be premature.", "이건 너무 이른 이야기인지도 모른다."], ["I have set it down too soon, perhaps.", "어쩌면 너무 일찍 적어 버렸는지도 모른다."], ["But let it stand.", "하지만 그대로 두기로 한다."]], [["We strolled a long way, and loaded ourselves with things that we thought curious, and put some stranded starfish carefully back into the water--I hardly know enough of the race at this moment to be quite certain whether they had reason to feel obliged to us for doing so, or the reverse--and then made our way home to Mr. Peggotty’s dwelling.", "우리는 멀리까지 거닐며, 신기하다 싶은 것들을 잔뜩 짊어졌고, 물가에 떠밀려 온 불가사리 몇 마리를 조심스레 물속에 도로 넣어 주었다 — 지금도 나는 그 종족에 대해 잘 알지 못해, 우리가 그렇게 해 준 것을 그것들이 고맙게 여길 까닭이 있었는지 아니면 그 반대였는지 도무지 확실히 알 수 없다 — 그러고 나서 패거티 씨네 집으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["We stopped under the lee of the lobster-outhouse to exchange an innocent kiss, and went in to breakfast glowing with health and pleasure.", "우리는 바닷가재 헛간의 바람 그늘 아래 멈춰 서서 순수한 입맞춤을 한 번 나누고는, 건강과 즐거움으로 발그레 달아오른 채 아침을 먹으러 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘Like two young mavishes,’ Mr.", "\"꼭 어린 개똥지빠귀 두 마리 같구먼.\""], ["Peggotty said.", "패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["I knew this meant, in our local dialect, like two young thrushes, and received it as a compliment.", "나는 이 말이 우리 고장 사투리로 어린 티티새 두 마리 같다는 뜻임을 알았기에, 그것을 칭찬으로 받아들였다."]], [["Of course I was in love with little Em’ly.", "물론 나는 어린 에밀리에게 사랑에 빠져 있었다."], ["I am sure I loved that baby quite as truly, quite as tenderly, with greater purity and more disinterestedness, than can enter into the best love of a later time of life, high and ennobling as it is.", "나는 그 어린것을, 훗날의 그 어떤 더없이 고귀하고 숭고한 사랑에도 깃들 수 없을 만큼, 참되게, 다정하게, 더 순수하고 더 사심 없이 사랑했다고 확신한다."], ["I am sure my fancy raised up something round that blue-eyed mite of a child, which etherealized, and made a very angel of her.", "나는 내 환상이 그 파란 눈동자의 자그마한 아이 둘레에 무언가를 피워 올려, 그 애를 영묘하게 만들고 완연한 천사로 빚어냈다고 확신한다."], ["If, any sunny forenoon, she had spread a little pair of wings and flown away before my eyes, I don’t think I should have regarded it as much more than I had had reason to expect.", "햇살 좋은 어느 오전에, 그 애가 작은 날개 한 쌍을 펼쳐 내 눈앞에서 훌쩍 날아가 버렸더라도, 나는 그것을 능히 예상했던 일 이상으로 여기지는 않았을 것 같다."]], [["We used to walk about that dim old flat at Yarmouth in a loving manner, hours and hours.", "우리는 야머스의 그 어슴푸레하고 오래된 벌판을 정답게 돌아다니곤 했는데, 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 그랬다."], ["The days sported by us, as if Time had not grown up himself yet, but were a child too, and always at play.", "나날은 우리 곁을 깡충거리며 지나갔는데, 마치 '시간'이라는 녀석마저 아직 다 자라지 않은 채, 자기도 하나의 아이가 되어 늘 노는 데만 정신이 팔려 있는 듯했다."], ["I told Em’ly I adored her, and that unless she confessed she adored me I should be reduced to the necessity of killing myself with a sword. She said she did, and I have no doubt she did.", "나는 에밀리에게 너를 흠모한다고, 네가 나를 흠모한다고 고백하지 않으면 칼로 자결할 수밖에 없는 처지에 내몰릴 거라고 말했다. 에밀리는 흠모한다고 했고, 나는 그 애가 정말 그랬다고 믿어 의심치 않는다."]], [["As to any sense of inequality, or youthfulness, or other difficulty in our way, little Em’ly and I had no such trouble, because we had no future.", "처지가 서로 맞지 않는다거나, 너무 어리다거나, 그 밖에 우리 앞을 가로막을 무슨 어려움이 있다거나 하는 생각으로 말하자면, 어린 에밀리와 나에게는 그런 고민이 전혀 없었다."], ["We made no more provision for growing older, than we did for growing younger. We were the admiration of Mrs.", "우리에게는 미래라는 것이 없었으니까. 우리는 나이를 더 먹을 준비 따위, 더 어려질 준비를 하지 않는 것만큼이나 하지 않았다."], ["Gummidge and Peggotty, who used to whisper of an evening when we sat, lovingly, on our little locker side by side, ‘Lor!", "우리는 거미지 부인과 패거티의 감탄거리였는데, 두 사람은 우리가 저녁이면 작은 사물함 위에 나란히 정답게 앉아 있을 때면 이렇게 속삭이곤 했다. \"어머나!"], ["wasn’t it beautiful!’ Mr.", "어쩜 저렇게 예쁠까!\""], ["Peggotty smiled at us from behind his pipe, and Ham grinned all the evening and did nothing else.", "패거티 씨는 파이프 너머로 우리를 보며 빙그레 웃었고, 햄은 저녁 내내 싱글거리기만 할 뿐 다른 일은 아무것도 하지 않았다."], ["They had something of the sort of pleasure in us, I suppose, that they might have had in a pretty toy, or a pocket model of the Colosseum.", "그 사람들은 우리에게서, 예쁜 장난감이나 콜로세움 손바닥 모형에서 느낄 법한 그런 즐거움을 얻었던 것 같다."]], [["I soon found out that Mrs. Gummidge did not always make herself so agreeable as she might have been expected to do, under the circumstances of her residence with Mr.", "나는 거미지 부인이, 패거티 씨와 함께 사는 그 처지로 미루어 으레 기대됨 직한 만큼 늘 상냥하게 구는 것은 아님을 곧 알아차렸다."], ["Peggotty. Mrs. Gummidge’s was rather a fretful disposition, and she whimpered more sometimes than was comfortable for other parties in so small an establishment.", "거미지 부인은 다소 짜증을 잘 내는 성미여서, 이렇게 자그마한 살림에서 다른 식구들이 편치 않을 만큼 이따금 훌쩍이곤 했다."], ["I was very sorry for her; but there were moments when it would have been more agreeable, I thought, if Mrs. Gummidge had had a convenient apartment of her own to retire to, and had stopped there until her spirits revived.", "나는 그 부인이 무척 안쓰러웠다. 그러나 거미지 부인에게 물러가 쉴 만한 제 방이 따로 있어서, 기분이 풀릴 때까지 거기 들어가 있어 준다면 한결 좋겠다 싶은 순간들도 있었다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty went occasionally to a public-house called The Willing Mind.", "패거티 씨는 이따금 '윌링 마인드'라는 선술집에 갔다."], ["I discovered this, by his being out on the second or third evening of our visit, and by Mrs. Gummidge’s looking up at the Dutch clock, between eight and nine, and saying he was there, and that, what was more, she had known in the morning he would go there.", "나는 우리가 머문 둘째인가 셋째 날 저녁에 그가 밖에 나가 있고, 거미지 부인이 여덟 시에서 아홉 시 사이에 네덜란드제 시계를 올려다보며 그가 거기 가 있다고, 게다가 아침부터 그가 거기 갈 줄 알고 있었다고 말하는 것을 보고서 이 사실을 알아챘다."]], [["Mrs. Gummidge had been in a low state all day, and had burst into tears in the forenoon, when the fire smoked.", "거미지 부인은 그날 하루 종일 기분이 가라앉아 있었는데, 오전에는 난로에서 연기가 나자 와락 눈물을 터뜨렸다."], ["‘I am a lone lorn creetur’,’ were Mrs.", "\"난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세야.\""], ["Gummidge’s words, when that unpleasant occurrence took place, ‘and everythink goes contrary with me.’", "그 언짢은 일이 벌어졌을 때 거미지 부인이 한 말이다. \"뭐든 죄다 내 뜻과는 어긋나게만 돌아간다니까.\""]], [["‘Oh, it’ll soon leave off,’ said Peggotty--I again mean our Peggotty--‘and besides, you know, it’s not more disagreeable to you than to us.’", "\"아유, 곧 그칠 거예요.\" 패거티가 말했다 — 다시 말하건대 우리 패거티 말이다 — \"게다가, 아시잖아요, 그게 우리보다 부인한테 더 언짢은 일도 아니잖아요.\""]], [["‘I feel it more,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"난 더 사무치게 느껴.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다."]], [["It was a very cold day, with cutting blasts of wind. Mrs.", "무척 추운 날이었고, 살을 에는 바람이 몰아쳤다."], ["Gummidge’s peculiar corner of the fireside seemed to me to be the warmest and snuggest in the place, as her chair was certainly the easiest, but it didn’t suit her that day at all.", "거미지 부인이 차지한 난롯가 그 특별한 모퉁이는 내가 보기에 그 집에서 가장 따뜻하고 아늑한 자리였고, 그 의자도 분명 가장 편한 의자였지만, 그날따라 그녀에게는 도무지 맞지 않았다."], ["She was constantly complaining of the cold, and of its occasioning a visitation in her back which she called ‘the creeps’.", "그녀는 끊임없이 춥다고, 그 추위 때문에 등줄기에 '소름'이라고 부르는 게 끼친다고 투덜댔다."], ["At last she shed tears on that subject, and said again that she was ‘a lone lorn creetur’ and everythink went contrary with her’.", "마침내 그녀는 그 일을 두고 눈물을 흘리며, 자기는 '외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세'고 뭐든 죄다 자기 뜻과 어긋나게만 돌아간다고 또 말했다."]], [["‘It is certainly very cold,’ said Peggotty.", "\"확실히 무척 춥긴 하네요.\" 패거티가 말했다."], ["‘Everybody must feel it so.’", "\"다들 그렇게 느낄 거예요.\""]], [["‘I feel it more than other people,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"난 다른 사람들보다 더 사무치게 느껴.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다."]], [["So at dinner; when Mrs.", "저녁 식사 때도 그러했다."], ["Gummidge was always helped immediately after me, to whom the preference was given as a visitor of distinction.", "거미지 부인은 늘 나 다음으로 곧장 음식을 받았는데, 나에게는 귀한 손님이라고 우선권을 주었던 것이다."], ["The fish were small and bony, and the potatoes were a little burnt.", "생선은 작고 가시가 많았고, 감자는 좀 탔다."], ["We all acknowledged that we felt this something of a disappointment; but Mrs. Gummidge said she felt it more than we did, and shed tears again, and made that former declaration with great bitterness.", "우리 모두 이것이 다소 실망스럽다고 인정했다. 그러나 거미지 부인은 자기는 우리보다 더 사무치게 느낀다고 하면서, 또다시 눈물을 흘리고는 아까 그 말을 몹시 쓰라리게 되뇌었다."]], [["Accordingly, when Mr. Peggotty came home about nine o’clock, this unfortunate Mrs. Gummidge was knitting in her corner, in a very wretched and miserable condition.", "그리하여 패거티 씨가 아홉 시쯤 집에 돌아왔을 때, 이 가엾은 거미지 부인은 제 모퉁이에서 무척 비참하고 처량한 꼴로 뜨개질을 하고 있었다."], ["Peggotty had been working cheerfully.", "패거티는 명랑하게 일하고 있었다."], ["Ham had been patching up a great pair of waterboots; and I, with little Em’ly by my side, had been reading to them. Mrs.", "햄은 커다란 방수 장화 한 켤레를 깁고 있었고, 나는 어린 에밀리를 곁에 두고 그들에게 책을 읽어 주고 있었다."], ["Gummidge had never made any other remark than a forlorn sigh, and had never raised her eyes since tea.", "거미지 부인은 처량한 한숨 말고는 그 어떤 말도 하지 않았고, 차를 마신 뒤로는 한 번도 눈을 들지 않았다."]], [["‘Well, Mates,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 동무들,\""], ["Peggotty, taking his seat, ‘and how are you?’", "패거티 씨가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"다들 어떻게 지냈나?\""]], [["We all said something, or looked something, to welcome him, except Mrs. Gummidge, who only shook her head over her knitting.", "우리는 저마다 무어라 한마디씩 하거나 무어라 눈짓을 보내며 그를 맞았지만, 거미지 부인만은 뜨개질감 위로 그저 고개만 가로저을 뿐이었다."]], [["‘What’s amiss?’ said Mr.", "\"왜 그래?\""], ["Peggotty, with a clap of his hands.", "패거티 씨가 손뼉을 짝 치며 말했다."], ["‘Cheer up, old Mawther!’ (Mr. Peggotty meant old girl.)", "\"기운 내요, 할멈!\" (패거티 씨는 '할망구'라는 뜻으로 한 말이었다.)"]], [["Mrs. Gummidge did not appear to be able to cheer up.", "거미지 부인은 도무지 기운을 낼 수 없는 모양이었다."], ["She took out an old black silk handkerchief and wiped her eyes; but instead of putting it in her pocket, kept it out, and wiped them again, and still kept it out, ready for use.", "그녀는 낡은 검은 비단 손수건을 꺼내 눈을 닦았다. 그러나 그것을 주머니에 도로 넣는 대신 손에 쥐고 있다가 다시 눈을 닦았고, 또 쓰려는 듯 그대로 손에 들고 있었다."]], [["‘What’s amiss, dame?’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"왜 그래, 부인?\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing,’ returned Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 거미지 부인이 대답했다."], ["‘You’ve come from The Willing Mind, Dan’l?’", "\"윌링 마인드에서 오시는 길이죠, 댄 양반?\""]], [["‘Why yes, I’ve took a short spell at The Willing Mind tonight,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"아 그래요, 오늘 밤 윌링 마인드에서 잠깐 한잔하고 왔지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I’m sorry I should drive you there,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"내가 당신을 거기로 내모는 것 같아 미안하네요.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Drive!", "\"내몰긴!"], ["I don’t want no driving,’ returned Mr.", "누가 날 내몰 것도 없어요.\""], ["Peggotty with an honest laugh.", "패거티 씨가 솔직하게 웃으며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I only go too ready.’", "\"내가 너무 기꺼이 갈 뿐이지.\""]], [["‘Very ready,’ said Mrs.", "\"무척 기꺼이도 가시지요.\""], ["Gummidge, shaking her head, and wiping her eyes.", "거미지 부인이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 눈을 닦았다."], ["‘Yes, yes, very ready.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 무척 기꺼이."], ["I am sorry it should be along of me that you’re so ready.’", "당신이 그렇게 기꺼이 가는 게 나 때문인 것 같아 미안해요.\""]], [["‘Along o’ you!", "\"당신 때문이라니!"], ["It an’t along o’ you!’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "당신 때문이 아니에요!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Don’t ye believe a bit on it.’", "\"그런 말은 한 톨도 믿지 말아요.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, it is,’ cried Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요, 맞아요.\""], ["‘I know what I am.", "거미지 부인이 외쳤다."], ["I know that I am a lone lorn creetur’, and not only that everythink goes contrary with me, but that I go contrary with everybody.", "\"내가 어떤 사람인지는 내가 알아요. 난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세고, 뭐든 죄다 내 뜻과 어긋날 뿐만 아니라, 나 자신이 모두와 어긋나게 군다는 것도 알아요."], ["Yes, yes. I feel more than other people do, and I show it more.", "그래요, 그래요. 난 다른 사람들보다 더 사무치게 느끼고, 그걸 더 드러내요."], ["It’s my misfortun’.’", "그게 내 불행이지요.\""]], [["I really couldn’t help thinking, as I sat taking in all this, that the misfortune extended to some other members of that family besides Mrs. Gummidge.", "이 모든 이야기를 앉아서 듣고 있자니, 그 불행이 거미지 부인 말고도 그 집안 다른 식구들에게까지 미친다는 생각이 정말이지 들지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But Mr. Peggotty made no such retort, only answering with another entreaty to Mrs. Gummidge to cheer up.", "그러나 패거티 씨는 그런 핀잔은 한마디도 하지 않고, 그저 거미지 부인에게 기운을 내라고 다시 한번 간청할 따름이었다."]], [["‘I an’t what I could wish myself to be,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"난 내가 바라는 그런 사람이 못 돼요.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다."], ["‘I am far from it.", "\"그러기엔 한참 멀었지요."], ["I know what I am. My troubles has made me contrary.", "내가 어떤 사람인지는 내가 알아요. 내 고생이 날 이렇게 비뚤어지게 만든 거예요."], ["I feel my troubles, and they make me contrary.", "난 내 고생을 사무치게 느끼고, 그게 날 비뚤어지게 해요."], ["I wish I didn’t feel ‘em, but I do.", "안 느꼈으면 좋으련만, 난 느껴요."], ["I wish I could be hardened to ‘em, but I an’t.", "거기에 무뎌질 수 있으면 좋으련만, 난 못 그래요."], ["I make the house uncomfortable.", "내가 이 집을 불편하게 만들어요."], ["I don’t wonder at it.", "그래도 난 놀랍지가 않아요."], ["I’ve made your sister so all day, and Master Davy.’", "오늘 하루 종일 당신 누이를 그렇게 만들었고, 데이비 도련님도요.\""]], [["Here I was suddenly melted, and roared out, ‘No, you haven’t, Mrs.", "이 대목에서 나는 갑자기 마음이 녹아내려, 몹시 괴로운 심정으로 버럭 소리쳤다."], ["Gummidge,’ in great mental distress.", "\"아니에요, 그러지 않으셨어요, 거미지 부인.\""]], [["‘It’s far from right that I should do it,’ said Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"내가 이러는 건 도리에 한참 어긋나는 일이에요.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다."], ["‘It an’t a fit return.", "\"이건 마땅한 보답이 못 되지요."], ["I had better go into the house and die.", "차라리 구빈원에 들어가 죽는 게 낫겠어요."], ["I am a lone lorn creetur’, and had much better not make myself contrary here.", "난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세니, 여기서 비뚤어지게 굴지 않는 게 훨씬 낫지요."], ["If thinks must go contrary with me, and I must go contrary myself, let me go contrary in my parish.", "일이 죄다 내 뜻과 어긋나야 하고, 나 자신도 비뚤어지게 굴어야 한다면, 차라리 내 고향 교구에서 비뚤어지게 굴게 해 줘요."], ["Dan’l, I’d better go into the house, and die and be a riddance!’", "댄 양반, 차라리 구빈원에 들어가 죽어서 짐이나 덜어 주는 게 낫겠어요!\""]], [["Mrs. Gummidge retired with these words, and betook herself to bed.", "거미지 부인은 이 말을 남기고 물러가 잠자리에 들었다."], ["When she was gone, Mr. Peggotty, who had not exhibited a trace of any feeling but the profoundest sympathy, looked round upon us, and nodding his head with a lively expression of that sentiment still animating his face, said in a whisper:", "그녀가 가고 나자, 더없이 깊은 연민 말고는 어떤 감정도 내비치지 않던 패거티 씨가 우리를 둘러보더니, 그 연민이 여전히 얼굴에 생생히 어린 채로 고개를 끄덕이며 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["‘She’s been thinking of the old ‘un!’", "\"옛사람 생각을 하고 있었던 게야!\""]], [["I did not quite understand what old one Mrs. Gummidge was supposed to have fixed her mind upon, until Peggotty, on seeing me to bed, explained that it was the late Mr. Gummidge; and that her brother always took that for a received truth on such occasions, and that it always had a moving effect upon him.", "거미지 부인이 도대체 어떤 옛사람에게 마음을 두고 있었다는 건지 나는 잘 이해하지 못했는데, 패거티가 나를 재워 주며, 그것이 작고한 거미지 씨를 가리키는 말이고, 자기 오라버니는 그럴 때면 늘 그것을 당연한 사실로 받아들이며, 그것이 늘 오라버니의 마음을 짠하게 한다고 설명해 주었다."], ["Some time after he was in his hammock that night, I heard him myself repeat to Ham, ‘Poor thing!", "그날 밤 그가 해먹에 누운 뒤 얼마쯤 지나, 나는 그가 햄에게 \"가엾은 사람!"], ["She’s been thinking of the old ‘un!’ And whenever Mrs.", "옛사람 생각을 하고 있었던 거야!\" 하고 되뇌는 소리를 직접 들었다."], ["Gummidge was overcome in a similar manner during the remainder of our stay (which happened some few times), he always said the same thing in extenuation of the circumstance, and always with the tenderest commiseration.", "그리고 우리가 머무는 나머지 동안 거미지 부인이 비슷하게 북받칠 때마다 (그런 일이 몇 번 있었다), 그는 늘 그 상황을 두둔하며 똑같은 말을 했고, 늘 더없이 다정한 연민을 담아 그렇게 했다."]], [["So the fortnight slipped away, varied by nothing but the variation of the tide, which altered Mr.", "이렇게 보름은 흘러갔는데, 밀물 썰물의 변화 말고는 달라지는 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["Peggotty’s times of going out and coming in, and altered Ham’s engagements also.", "그 조수가 패거티 씨가 나가고 들어오는 시간을 바꾸었고, 햄의 일거리도 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["When the latter was unemployed, he sometimes walked with us to show us the boats and ships, and once or twice he took us for a row.", "햄은 일이 없을 때면 이따금 우리와 함께 거닐며 보트며 배를 구경시켜 주었고, 한두 번은 우리를 노 젓는 배에 태워 주기도 했다."], ["I don’t know why one slight set of impressions should be more particularly associated with a place than another, though I believe this obtains with most people, in reference especially to the associations of their childhood.", "어째서 어느 한 가지 자그마한 인상의 묶음이 다른 것보다 유독 어떤 장소와 더 끈끈하게 얽히는지 나는 모르겠으나, 특히 어린 시절의 기억과 관련해서는 대다수 사람들이 다 그러리라 믿는다."], ["I never hear the name, or read the name, of Yarmouth, but I am reminded of a certain Sunday morning on the beach, the bells ringing for church, little Em’ly leaning on my shoulder, Ham lazily dropping stones into the water, and the sun, away at sea, just breaking through the heavy mist, and showing us the ships, like their own shadows.", "나는 야머스라는 이름을 듣거나 읽을 때마다, 어김없이 어느 일요일 아침의 바닷가가 떠오른다. 교회를 알리는 종이 울리고, 어린 에밀리는 내 어깨에 기대어 있고, 햄은 나른하게 물속으로 돌멩이를 떨어뜨리고, 저 멀리 바다에서는 해가 막 짙은 안개를 뚫고 나와, 마치 제 그림자 같은 배들을 우리에게 보여 주던 그 광경 말이다."]], [["At last the day came for going home.", "마침내 집으로 돌아갈 날이 왔다."], ["I bore up against the separation from Mr. Peggotty and Mrs. Gummidge, but my agony of mind at leaving little Em’ly was piercing.", "나는 패거티 씨와 거미지 부인과 헤어지는 것은 그럭저럭 견뎌 냈지만, 어린 에밀리를 두고 떠나는 마음의 괴로움은 가슴을 후벼 팠다."], ["We went arm-in-arm to the public-house where the carrier put up, and I promised, on the road, to write to her.", "우리는 짐꾼이 묵는 선술집까지 팔짱을 끼고 걸었고, 나는 가는 길에 에밀리에게 편지를 쓰겠다고 약속했다."], ["(I redeemed that promise afterwards, in characters larger than those in which apartments are usually announced in manuscript, as being to let.) We were greatly overcome at parting; and if ever, in my life, I have had a void made in my heart, I had one made that day.", "(나는 나중에 그 약속을 지켰는데, 흔히 셋방 내놓는다고 손으로 써 붙이는 글씨보다 더 큼지막한 글자로 써 보냈다.) 우리는 헤어지면서 무척 북받쳐 올랐다. 그리고 내 평생 가슴에 구멍이 뻥 뚫린 적이 있었다면, 바로 그날 하나 뚫린 것이었다."]], [["Now, all the time I had been on my visit, I had been ungrateful to my home again, and had thought little or nothing about it.", "그런데 가만 보니, 내가 이 집을 떠나 와 있는 내내, 나는 다시 내 집에 무심했고, 그곳을 거의, 아니 전혀 떠올리지 않았다."], ["But I was no sooner turned towards it, than my reproachful young conscience seemed to point that way with a ready finger; and I felt, all the more for the sinking of my spirits, that it was my nest, and that my mother was my comforter and friend.", "그러나 발길을 집 쪽으로 돌리기가 무섭게, 나를 책망하는 어린 양심이 냉큼 손가락을 들어 그쪽을 가리키는 듯했다. 그리고 기운이 가라앉을수록 더더욱, 나는 그곳이 내 둥지이며, 어머니가 나를 달래 주는 사람이자 벗이라는 것을 사무치게 느꼈다."]], [["This gained upon me as we went along; so that the nearer we drew, the more familiar the objects became that we passed, the more excited I was to get there, and to run into her arms.", "이런 마음은 우리가 길을 갈수록 점점 더 커졌다. 그래서 가까이 다가갈수록, 지나치는 사물들이 더 낯익어질수록, 나는 어서 그곳에 닿아 어머니 품으로 달려들고 싶어 더욱 들떴다."], ["But Peggotty, instead of sharing in those transports, tried to check them (though very kindly), and looked confused and out of sorts.", "그러나 패거티는 그런 들뜬 마음을 함께 나누기는커녕 (무척 다정하게나마) 그것을 누그러뜨리려 했고, 어딘가 혼란스럽고 영 마음이 불편해 보였다."]], [["Blunderstone Rookery would come, however, in spite of her, when the carrier’s horse pleased--and did.", "그래도 블런더스톤 까마귀 둥지는, 짐꾼의 말이 내켜만 한다면 패거티가 어떻게 하든 다가오게 마련이었고 — 과연 다가왔다."], ["How well I recollect it, on a cold grey afternoon, with a dull sky, threatening rain!", "음울한 하늘이 비를 머금어 잔뜩 위협하던 어느 춥고 잿빛인 오후의 그 광경을, 나는 어찌나 또렷이 기억하는지!"]], [["The door opened, and I looked, half laughing and half crying in my pleasant agitation, for my mother.", "문이 열렸고, 나는 즐거운 설렘에 반쯤 웃고 반쯤 울며 어머니를 찾아 둘러보았다."], ["It was not she, but a strange servant.", "그러나 어머니가 아니라 낯선 하녀였다."]], [["‘Why, Peggotty!’ I said, ruefully, ‘isn’t she come home?’", "\"어머, 패거티!\" 나는 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"어머니가 집에 안 돌아오셨어?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, Master Davy,’ said Peggotty.", "\"아녜요, 아녜요, 데이비 도련님.\" 패거티가 말했다."], ["‘She’s come home.", "\"마님은 돌아오셨어요."], ["Wait a bit, Master Davy, and I’ll--I’ll tell you something.’", "잠깐만요, 데이비 도련님, 제가 — 제가 도련님한테 할 말이 있어요.\""]], [["Between her agitation, and her natural awkwardness in getting out of the cart, Peggotty was making a most extraordinary festoon of herself, but I felt too blank and strange to tell her so.", "흥분한 데다 본디 마차에서 내리는 것이 영 굼떴던 탓에, 패거티는 제 몸을 어찌나 우스꽝스럽게 비비 꼬았는지 마치 화환이라도 만드는 것 같았다. 그러나 나는 너무 멍하고 이상한 기분이라 그 말을 해 줄 수가 없었다."], ["When she had got down, she took me by the hand; led me, wondering, into the kitchen; and shut the door.", "패거티는 내려서더니 내 손을 잡고, 어리둥절해하는 나를 부엌으로 이끈 다음, 문을 닫았다."]], [["‘Peggotty!’ said I, quite frightened.", "\"패거티!\" 나는 잔뜩 겁에 질려 말했다."], ["‘What’s the matter?’", "\"무슨 일이야?\""]], [["‘Nothing’s the matter, bless you, Master Davy dear!’ she answered, assuming an air of sprightliness.", "\"아무 일도 없어요, 우리 귀한 데이비 도련님, 안심하세요!\" 패거티가 짐짓 쾌활한 척하며 대답했다."]], [["‘Something’s the matter, I’m sure. Where’s mama?’", "\"무슨 일이 있는 게 분명해. 어머니는 어디 계셔?\""]], [["‘Where’s mama, Master Davy?’ repeated Peggotty.", "\"마님이 어디 계시냐고요, 데이비 도련님?\" 패거티가 되뇌었다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"응."], ["Why hasn’t she come out to the gate, and what have we come in here for?", "왜 대문까지 안 나오셨고, 우린 왜 여기 들어온 거야? 아, 패거티!\""], ["Oh, Peggotty!’ My eyes were full, and I felt as if I were going to tumble down.", "내 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했고, 금방이라도 폭삭 주저앉을 것만 같았다."]], [["‘Bless the precious boy!’ cried Peggotty, taking hold of me.", "\"아이고 우리 가여운 도련님!\" 패거티가 나를 와락 붙들며 외쳤다."], ["‘What is it? Speak, my pet!’", "\"왜 그래요? 말해 봐요, 우리 귀염둥이!\""]], [["‘Not dead, too!", "\"어머니도 돌아가신 거 아니지!"], ["Oh, she’s not dead, Peggotty?’", "아, 어머니가 돌아가신 건 아니지, 패거티?\""]], [["Peggotty cried out No! with an astonishing volume of voice; and then sat down, and began to pant, and said I had given her a turn.", "패거티는 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 큰 목소리로 아니라고 외쳤다. 그러고는 주저앉아 헐떡거리기 시작하더니, 내가 자기를 깜짝 놀라게 했다고 했다."]], [["I gave her a hug to take away the turn, or to give her another turn in the right direction, and then stood before her, looking at her in anxious inquiry.", "나는 그 놀란 가슴을 가라앉혀 주려고, 아니면 옳은 방향으로 다시 한번 놀라게 해 주려고 패거티를 꼭 끌어안았고, 그러고 나서 그녀 앞에 서서 애타게 묻는 눈빛으로 바라보았다."]], [["‘You see, dear, I should have told you before now,’ said Peggotty, ‘but I hadn’t an opportunity.", "\"있잖아요, 도련님, 진작에 말씀드렸어야 했는데,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"기회가 없었어요."], ["I ought to have made it, perhaps, but I couldn’t azackly’--that was always the substitute for exactly, in Peggotty’s militia of words--‘bring my mind to it.’", "어쩌면 일부러라도 기회를 만들었어야 했는데, 도무지 정짝히는\"— 이것은 패거티의 어수룩한 어휘 부대에서 늘 '정확히'를 대신하던 말이었다 —\"마음을 못 다잡겠더라고요.\""]], [["‘Go on, Peggotty,’ said I, more frightened than before.", "\"어서 말해, 패거티.\" 나는 아까보다 더 겁에 질려 말했다."]], [["‘Master Davy,’ said Peggotty, untying her bonnet with a shaking hand, and speaking in a breathless sort of way.", "\"데이비 도련님,\" 패거티가 떨리는 손으로 모자를 풀며, 숨이 가쁜 듯한 말투로 말했다. \"어떻게 된 것 같아요?"], ["‘What do you think? You have got a Pa!’", "도련님한테 아버지가 생겼어요!\""]], [["I trembled, and turned white.", "나는 부르르 떨며 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["Something--I don’t know what, or how--connected with the grave in the churchyard, and the raising of the dead, seemed to strike me like an unwholesome wind.", "무언가가 — 무엇인지, 어떻게 그런 건지는 모르겠으나 — 교회 묘지의 그 무덤과, 죽은 자가 되살아나는 일과 얽혀, 마치 불길한 바람처럼 나를 후려치는 것 같았다."]], [["‘A new one,’ said Peggotty.", "\"새 아버지요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘A new one?’ I repeated.", "\"새 아버지?\" 내가 되뇌었다."]], [["Peggotty gave a gasp, as if she were swallowing something that was very hard, and, putting out her hand, said:", "패거티는 무척 딱딱한 무언가를 삼키기라도 하는 듯 숨을 헉 들이켜더니, 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["‘Come and see him.’", "\"가서 만나 봐요.\""]], [["‘I don’t want to see him.’", "\"만나기 싫어.\""]], [["--‘And your mama,’ said Peggotty.", "\"그리고 어머님도요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["I ceased to draw back, and we went straight to the best parlour, where she left me.", "나는 더는 뒷걸음치지 않았고, 우리는 곧장 제일 좋은 거실로 갔는데, 패거티는 거기서 나를 두고 가 버렸다."], ["On one side of the fire, sat my mother; on the other, Mr. Murdstone.", "난로 한쪽에는 어머니가 앉아 있었고, 다른 한쪽에는 머드스톤 씨가 앉아 있었다."], ["My mother dropped her work, and arose hurriedly, but timidly I thought.", "어머니는 하던 일감을 떨어뜨리고 황급히 일어났지만, 내가 보기엔 어딘가 주뼛거리는 듯했다."]], [["‘Now, Clara my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 클라라, 여보.\""], ["Murdstone. ‘Recollect!", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다."], ["control yourself, always control yourself!", "\"잊지 말아요! 자제해요, 늘 자제하란 말이오!"], ["Davy boy, how do you do?’", "데이비, 잘 있었니?\""]], [["I gave him my hand.", "나는 그에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["After a moment of suspense, I went and kissed my mother: she kissed me, patted me gently on the shoulder, and sat down again to her work.", "잠시 머뭇거린 끝에, 나는 다가가 어머니에게 입을 맞췄다. 어머니도 나에게 입을 맞추고는, 내 어깨를 가볍게 토닥인 다음, 다시 앉아 일감을 들었다."], ["I could not look at her, I could not look at him, I knew quite well that he was looking at us both; and I turned to the window and looked out there, at some shrubs that were drooping their heads in the cold.", "나는 어머니를 차마 볼 수 없었고, 그를 차마 볼 수 없었다. 나는 그가 우리 둘을 빤히 바라보고 있다는 것을 너무도 잘 알았다. 그래서 나는 창가로 돌아서서, 추위에 고개를 떨군 관목 몇 그루를 내다보았다."]], [["As soon as I could creep away, I crept upstairs.", "나는 슬그머니 빠져나올 수 있게 되자마자, 살금살금 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["My old dear bedroom was changed, and I was to lie a long way off.", "정답던 내 옛 침실은 달라져 있었고, 나는 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 자야 했다."], ["I rambled downstairs to find anything that was like itself, so altered it all seemed; and roamed into the yard.", "모든 것이 어찌나 달라져 보이던지, 나는 예전 그대로인 것이 무엇이라도 없을까 하고 아래층을 헤매다가, 마당으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나갔다."], ["I very soon started back from there, for the empty dog-kennel was filled up with a great dog--deep mouthed and black-haired like Him--and he was very angry at the sight of me, and sprang out to get at me.", "그러나 나는 이내 거기서 화들짝 물러서고 말았으니, 텅 비어 있던 개집이 커다란 개 한 마리로 채워져 있었기 때문이다 — 그놈은 입이 떡 벌어지고 털이 새카만 것이 꼭 그를 닮았는데 — 그놈은 나를 보자 몹시 성을 내며, 나를 물려고 펄쩍 뛰어나왔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 4. I FALL INTO DISGRACE", "# CHAPTER 4. I FALL INTO DISGRACE"]], [["If the room to which my bed was removed were a sentient thing that could give evidence, I might appeal to it at this day--who sleeps there now, I wonder!--to bear witness for me what a heavy heart I carried to it.", "내 침대가 옮겨진 그 방이, 만일 증언할 수 있는 감각을 지닌 존재였다면, 나는 오늘 이 순간에도 그 방에 호소했을 것이다 — 지금은 그곳에서 누가 자고 있을까, 문득 궁금하다! — 내가 얼마나 무거운 마음을 안고 그리로 올라갔는지 증언해 달라고 말이다."], ["I went up there, hearing the dog in the yard bark after me all the way while I climbed the stairs; and, looking as blank and strange upon the room as the room looked upon me, sat down with my small hands crossed, and thought.", "나는 계단을 오르는 내내 마당의 개가 등 뒤에서 짖어 대는 소리를 들으며 그 방으로 올라갔다. 그러고는 방이 나를 멍하니 낯설게 바라보는 만큼이나 나도 그 방을 멍하니 낯설게 바라보며, 작은 두 손을 포갠 채 주저앉아 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["I thought of the oddest things.", "나는 더없이 엉뚱한 것들을 떠올렸다."], ["Of the shape of the room, of the cracks in the ceiling, of the paper on the walls, of the flaws in the window-glass making ripples and dimples on the prospect, of the washing-stand being rickety on its three legs, and having a discontented something about it, which reminded me of Mrs. Gummidge under the influence of the old one.", "방의 모양, 천장에 난 금, 벽에 발린 종이, 창유리의 흠집이 바깥 풍경에 잔물결과 오목한 자국을 만들어 내는 모양, 세면대가 세 다리로 비틀비틀 서 있는 꼴, 그리고 거기에 깃든 어딘가 불만스러운 기운 — 그것이 '옛것'에 사로잡힌 거미지 부인을 떠올리게 했다."], ["I was crying all the time, but, except that I was conscious of being cold and dejected, I am sure I never thought why I cried.", "나는 내내 울고 있었지만, 춥고 풀이 죽었다는 것만 느꼈을 뿐, 왜 우는지는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었던 게 분명하다."], ["At last in my desolation I began to consider that I was dreadfully in love with little Em’ly, and had been torn away from her to come here where no one seemed to want me, or to care about me, half as much as she did.", "마침내 그 쓸쓸함 속에서 나는, 내가 어린 에밀리를 끔찍이도 사랑하고 있는데, 그 애만큼 나를 원하거나 아껴 주는 이가 아무도 없어 보이는 이곳으로 오느라 그 애에게서 떨어져 나온 거라고 곱씹기 시작했다."], ["This made such a very miserable piece of business of it, that I rolled myself up in a corner of the counterpane, and cried myself to sleep.", "그렇게 생각하니 이 모든 일이 어찌나 비참하게 여겨졌던지, 나는 침대보 한 귀퉁이에 몸을 둘둘 말고 울다가 잠이 들었다."]], [["I was awoke by somebody saying ‘Here he is!’ and uncovering my hot head.", "\"여기 있어요!\" 하는 누군가의 목소리에, 그리고 내 화끈거리는 머리를 덮은 이불이 걷히는 바람에 나는 잠에서 깼다."], ["My mother and Peggotty had come to look for me, and it was one of them who had done it.", "어머니와 패거티가 나를 찾으러 온 것이었고, 이불을 걷은 사람은 둘 중 하나였다."]], [["‘Davy,’ said my mother.", "\"데이비.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘What’s the matter?’", "\"무슨 일이니?\""]], [["I thought it was very strange that she should ask me, and answered, ‘Nothing.’ I turned over on my face, I recollect, to hide my trembling lip, which answered her with greater truth.", "나는 어머니가 그걸 묻는다는 게 무척 이상하게 느껴져서 \"아무것도 아니에요\" 하고 대답했다. 떨리는 입술을 감추려고 얼굴을 돌려 엎드렸던 게 기억난다."], ["‘Davy,’ said my mother.", "그 입술이야말로 어머니에게 더 진실한 대답을 하고 있었으니까. \"데이비.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘Davy, my child!’", "\"데이비, 내 아가!\""]], [["I dare say no words she could have uttered would have affected me so much, then, as her calling me her child.", "그때 어머니가 했을 어떤 말도, 나를 '내 아가'라고 불러 준 그 말만큼 나를 크게 흔들어 놓지는 못했을 것이다."], ["I hid my tears in the bedclothes, and pressed her from me with my hand, when she would have raised me up.", "나는 이불 속에 눈물을 감추었고, 어머니가 나를 일으키려 하자 손으로 그녀를 밀어냈다."]], [["‘This is your doing, Peggotty, you cruel thing!’ said my mother.", "\"이게 다 네 짓이야, 패거티, 이 모진 것아!\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘I have no doubt at all about it.", "\"조금도 의심하지 않아."], ["How can you reconcile it to your conscience, I wonder, to prejudice my own boy against me, or against anybody who is dear to me?", "도대체 어떻게 네 양심이 그걸 견디는지 모르겠구나. 내 아들을 내게서, 아니 내가 아끼는 누구에게서든 등 돌리게 만들다니."], ["What do you mean by it, Peggotty?’", "무슨 속셈이야, 패거티?\""]], [["Poor Peggotty lifted up her hands and eyes, and only answered, in a sort of paraphrase of the grace I usually repeated after dinner, ‘Lord forgive you, Mrs.", "가엾은 패거티는 두 손과 두 눈을 치켜들고는, 내가 저녁 식사 뒤에 늘 따라 외우던 감사 기도를 일종의 풀어 쓴 말투로 이렇게만 대답했다. \"주님께서 부인을 용서하시길요, 코퍼필드 부인."], ["Copperfield, and for what you have said this minute, may you never be truly sorry!’", "그리고 방금 하신 그 말씀을, 부디 진심으로 후회하실 일이 결코 없기를요!\""]], [["‘It’s enough to distract me,’ cried my mother.", "\"정말 미쳐 버리겠네.\" 어머니가 외쳤다."], ["‘In my honeymoon, too, when my most inveterate enemy might relent, one would think, and not envy me a little peace of mind and happiness.", "\"그것도 신혼 중에 말이야. 아무리 지독한 원수라도 마음을 누그러뜨릴 만한 때인데, 내가 조금이라도 마음 편하고 행복한 걸 시샘하지 않을 법한 때인데."], ["Davy, you naughty boy!", "데이비, 이 못된 녀석!"], ["Peggotty, you savage creature!", "패거티, 이 매정한 인간아!"], ["Oh, dear me!’ cried my mother, turning from one of us to the other, in her pettish wilful manner, ‘what a troublesome world this is, when one has the most right to expect it to be as agreeable as possible!’", "아이고, 세상에!\" 어머니는 토라진 제멋대로의 태도로 우리 둘을 번갈아 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"세상이 가장 즐겁기를 바랄 자격이 있을 때, 어쩌면 이렇게 골치 아픈 세상인지!\""]], [["I felt the touch of a hand that I knew was neither hers nor Peggotty’s, and slipped to my feet at the bed-side.", "나는 어머니의 손도 패거티의 손도 아니라는 걸 단번에 알 수 있는 어떤 손길을 느꼈고, 침대 옆으로 미끄러져 내려와 두 발로 섰다."], ["It was Mr. Murdstone’s hand, and he kept it on my arm as he said:", "그것은 머드스톤 씨의 손이었는데, 그는 그 손을 내 팔에 얹은 채 말했다."]], [["‘What’s this?", "\"이게 무슨 짓이오?"], ["Clara, my love, have you forgotten?--Firmness, my dear!’", "클라라, 여보, 잊었소? — 단호함이오, 여보!\""]], [["‘I am very sorry, Edward,’ said my mother.", "\"정말 미안해요, 에드워드.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘I meant to be very good, but I am so uncomfortable.’", "\"정말 잘하려고 했는데, 마음이 너무 불편해서요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ he answered.", "\"그래요!\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘That’s a bad hearing, so soon, Clara.’", "\"이렇게 빨리 그런 소릴 듣다니, 영 좋지 않은 징조로구려, 클라라.\""]], [["‘I say it’s very hard I should be made so now,’ returned my mother, pouting; ‘and it is--very hard--isn’t it?’", "\"지금 이런 사람이 돼야 한다는 게 너무 가혹하다는 거예요.\" 어머니가 입술을 삐죽이며 받아쳤다. \"그래요 — 너무 가혹하잖아요 — 안 그래요?\""]], [["He drew her to him, whispered in her ear, and kissed her.", "그는 어머니를 끌어당겨 귀에 대고 속삭이고는 입을 맞추었다."], ["I knew as well, when I saw my mother’s head lean down upon his shoulder, and her arm touch his neck--I knew as well that he could mould her pliant nature into any form he chose, as I know, now, that he did it.", "어머니의 머리가 그의 어깨로 기울고, 그녀의 팔이 그의 목에 닿는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그가 어머니의 무른 천성을 자기 마음대로 빚어낼 수 있다는 걸 똑똑히 알았다 — 지금 그가 실제로 그렇게 했다는 걸 아는 것만큼이나 똑똑히."]], [["‘Go you below, my love,’ said Mr.", "\"당신은 아래로 내려가요, 여보.\""], ["Murdstone. ‘David and I will come down, together.", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"데이비드하고 나는 같이 내려가리다. 이봐.\""], ["My friend,’ turning a darkening face on Peggotty, when he had watched my mother out, and dismissed her with a nod and a smile; ‘do you know your mistress’s name?’", "어머니가 나가는 것을 지켜보고 고갯짓과 미소로 그녀를 내려보낸 뒤, 그는 어두워지는 얼굴을 패거티에게 돌리며 말했다. \"당신 주인마님의 이름을 아나?\""]], [["‘She has been my mistress a long time, sir,’ answered Peggotty, ‘I ought to know it.’ ‘That’s true,’ he answered.", "\"오래도록 제 주인마님이셨으니까요, 나리.\" 패거티가 대답했다. \"당연히 알지요.\" \"맞아.\""], ["‘But I thought I heard you, as I came upstairs, address her by a name that is not hers.", "그가 대답했다. \"그런데 내가 계단을 올라오면서 들으니, 자네가 마님을 마님 것이 아닌 이름으로 부르는 것 같던데."], ["She has taken mine, you know. Will you remember that?’", "마님은 내 이름을 쓰게 됐다는 걸 자네도 알 텐데. 그걸 기억해 두겠나?\""]], [["Peggotty, with some uneasy glances at me, curtseyed herself out of the room without replying; seeing, I suppose, that she was expected to go, and had no excuse for remaining.", "패거티는 나를 불안하게 흘끔거리면서, 아무 대답도 없이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 방을 나갔다. 짐작건대 자기가 나가 주기를 바라는 눈치인데 남아 있을 핑계가 없다는 걸 알아챈 것이리라."], ["When we two were left alone, he shut the door, and sitting on a chair, and holding me standing before him, looked steadily into my eyes. I felt my own attracted, no less steadily, to his.", "우리 둘만 남게 되자 그는 문을 닫더니, 의자에 앉아 나를 자기 앞에 세워 둔 채로 내 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다. 나는 내 눈도 그에 못지않게 똑바로 그의 눈에 이끌리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["As I recall our being opposed thus, face to face, I seem again to hear my heart beat fast and high.", "우리가 그렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 마주 섰던 일을 떠올리면, 지금도 내 심장이 빠르고 거세게 뛰는 소리가 다시 들리는 듯하다."]], [["‘David,’ he said, making his lips thin, by pressing them together, ‘if I have an obstinate horse or dog to deal with, what do you think I do?’", "\"데이비드.\" 그는 입술을 꾹 눌러 얇게 만들며 말했다. \"내가 고집 센 말이나 개를 다뤄야 할 때, 내가 어떻게 한다고 생각하느냐?\""]], [["‘I don’t know.’", "\"모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘I beat him.’", "\"때린다.\""]], [["I had answered in a kind of breathless whisper, but I felt, in my silence, that my breath was shorter now.", "나는 숨이 막힌 듯한 속삭임으로 대답했지만, 침묵 속에서 이제 내 숨이 더 가빠졌다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["‘I make him wince, and smart.", "\"움찔하고 따끔하게 만들지."], ["I say to myself, “I’ll conquer that fellow”; and if it were to cost him all the blood he had, I should do it.", "나는 속으로 이렇게 말한다. '저놈을 굴복시키고 말겠다.' 그러려면 그놈의 피를 죄다 쏟게 하는 한이 있어도 나는 해낸다."], ["What is that upon your face?’", "네 얼굴에 그게 뭐냐?\""]], [["‘Dirt,’ I said.", "\"먼지예요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["He knew it was the mark of tears as well as I.", "그는 나만큼이나 그것이 눈물 자국이라는 걸 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But if he had asked the question twenty times, each time with twenty blows, I believe my baby heart would have burst before I would have told him so.", "하지만 그가 그 질문을 스무 번 던지면서 매번 스무 대씩 때렸다 해도, 나는 그렇다고 말하느니 차라리 어린 가슴이 터져 버렸을 거라고 믿는다."]], [["‘You have a good deal of intelligence for a little fellow,’ he said, with a grave smile that belonged to him, ‘and you understood me very well, I see.", "\"꼬마치고는 머리가 제법 도는구나.\" 그는 자기 특유의 근엄한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"그리고 내 말을 아주 잘 알아들은 모양이로구나."], ["Wash that face, sir, and come down with me.’", "얼굴 씻어라, 이놈아. 그리고 나하고 같이 내려가자.\""]], [["He pointed to the washing-stand, which I had made out to be like Mrs. Gummidge, and motioned me with his head to obey him directly.", "그는 내가 거미지 부인 같다고 여겼던 그 세면대를 가리키며, 당장 시키는 대로 하라고 고갯짓으로 명령했다."], ["I had little doubt then, and I have less doubt now, that he would have knocked me down without the least compunction, if I had hesitated.", "만일 내가 머뭇거렸다면 그는 눈곱만큼의 거리낌도 없이 나를 때려눕혔으리라는 걸, 그때도 거의 의심하지 않았고 지금은 더더욱 의심하지 않는다."]], [["‘Clara, my dear,’ he said, when I had done his bidding, and he walked me into the parlour, with his hand still on my arm; ‘you will not be made uncomfortable any more, I hope.", "\"클라라, 여보.\" 내가 그가 시킨 대로 하자, 그는 여전히 내 팔에 손을 얹은 채 나를 데리고 거실로 들어가며 말했다. \"이제 더는 마음 불편할 일이 없기를 바라오."], ["We shall soon improve our youthful humours.’", "우리는 머지않아 이 어린것의 성미를 고쳐 놓을 테니까.\""]], [["God help me, I might have been improved for my whole life, I might have been made another creature perhaps, for life, by a kind word at that season.", "하느님 맙소사, 그 시절에 다정한 말 한마디만 있었더라면 나는 평생 더 나은 사람이 되었을지도, 어쩌면 평생 다른 사람이 되었을지도 모른다."], ["A word of encouragement and explanation, of pity for my childish ignorance, of welcome home, of reassurance to me that it was home, might have made me dutiful to him in my heart henceforth, instead of in my hypocritical outside, and might have made me respect instead of hate him.", "격려와 설명의 말 한마디, 내 어린아이다운 무지를 가엾이 여기는 말 한마디, 집에 온 걸 반기는 말 한마디, 여기가 집이라고 안심시켜 주는 말 한마디만 있었더라면, 나는 이후로 겉으로만 위선적으로 따르는 대신 마음으로 그에게 순종했을지도 모르고, 그를 미워하는 대신 존경했을지도 모른다."], ["I thought my mother was sorry to see me standing in the room so scared and strange, and that, presently, when I stole to a chair, she followed me with her eyes more sorrowfully still--missing, perhaps, some freedom in my childish tread--but the word was not spoken, and the time for it was gone.", "어머니는 내가 그렇게 겁먹고 낯설어하며 방에 서 있는 것을 보고 안타까워하는 듯했고, 이윽고 내가 슬그머니 의자로 가자 더욱 슬픈 눈으로 나를 좇았는데 — 어쩌면 내 어린아이다운 발걸음에서 사라진 어떤 자유로움을 아쉬워한 것이리라 — 그러나 그 말은 끝내 나오지 않았고, 그럴 때는 지나가 버렸다."]], [["We dined alone, we three together.", "우리는 셋이서만 식사를 했다."], ["He seemed to be very fond of my mother--I am afraid I liked him none the better for that--and she was very fond of him.", "그는 어머니를 무척 사랑하는 듯 보였고 — 그래서 내가 그를 조금이라도 더 좋아하게 됐다고는 도무지 말 못 하겠다 — 어머니도 그를 무척 사랑했다."], ["I gathered from what they said, that an elder sister of his was coming to stay with them, and that she was expected that evening.", "둘이 나누는 말에서, 그의 손위 누이가 함께 지내러 올 거라는 것, 그리고 그날 저녁 도착할 예정이라는 걸 알게 됐다."], ["I am not certain whether I found out then, or afterwards, that, without being actively concerned in any business, he had some share in, or some annual charge upon the profits of, a wine-merchant’s house in London, with which his family had been connected from his great-grandfather’s time, and in which his sister had a similar interest; but I may mention it in this place, whether or no.", "그가 어떤 사업에 직접 손을 대지는 않았으되, 그의 집안이 증조할아버지 대부터 인연을 맺어 온 런던의 어느 포도주 상회에서 이익의 일부를 받거나 해마다 일정한 몫을 챙기고 있었고, 그의 누이도 거기에 비슷한 지분을 갖고 있었다는 것은 — 내가 그때 알았는지 나중에 알았는지는 확실치 않지만 — 어쨌든 여기서 짚어 두어도 무방하겠다."]], [["After dinner, when we were sitting by the fire, and I was meditating an escape to Peggotty without having the hardihood to slip away, lest it should offend the master of the house, a coach drove up to the garden-gate and he went out to receive the visitor.", "식사가 끝나고 우리가 난롯가에 앉아 있을 때, 나는 집주인의 심기를 거스를까 봐 슬쩍 빠져나갈 배짱은 없으면서도 패거티에게로 달아날 궁리를 하고 있었는데, 그때 마차 한 대가 정원 문 앞에 와 멎었고 그는 손님을 맞으러 나갔다."], ["My mother followed him.", "어머니가 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["I was timidly following her, when she turned round at the parlour door, in the dusk, and taking me in her embrace as she had been used to do, whispered me to love my new father and be obedient to him.", "나도 머뭇머뭇 어머니를 따라가는데, 어스름 속에서 어머니가 거실 문께에서 돌아서더니, 예전에 늘 그러던 것처럼 나를 품에 안고서, 새 아버지를 사랑하고 그의 말을 잘 들으라고 속삭였다."], ["She did this hurriedly and secretly, as if it were wrong, but tenderly; and, putting out her hand behind her, held mine in it, until we came near to where he was standing in the garden, where she let mine go, and drew hers through his arm.", "어머니는 그것이 잘못된 일이기라도 한 듯 황급히, 몰래, 그러나 다정하게 그렇게 했다. 그러고는 한 손을 등 뒤로 내밀어 내 손을 잡았는데, 정원에 그가 서 있는 곳 가까이 다다를 때까지 그렇게 쥐고 있다가, 거기서 내 손을 놓고 자기 손을 그의 팔에 끼웠다."]], [["It was Miss Murdstone who was arrived, and a gloomy-looking lady she was; dark, like her brother, whom she greatly resembled in face and voice; and with very heavy eyebrows, nearly meeting over her large nose, as if, being disabled by the wrongs of her sex from wearing whiskers, she had carried them to that account.", "도착한 사람은 머드스톤 양이었는데, 음울한 인상의 여자였다. 자기 오빠처럼 가무잡잡한 것이, 얼굴이며 목소리며 무척 닮았다. 게다가 큼지막한 코 위에서 거의 맞붙을 만큼 짙은 눈썹을 가졌는데, 마치 여자라는 약점 탓에 구레나룻을 기르지 못하니 그 몫까지 눈썹으로 채워 넣은 듯했다."], ["She brought with her two uncompromising hard black boxes, with her initials on the lids in hard brass nails.", "그녀는 단단한 놋쇠 못으로 뚜껑에 자기 머리글자를 박은, 타협이라곤 모를 단단하고 시커먼 상자 두 개를 가져왔다."], ["When she paid the coachman she took her money out of a hard steel purse, and she kept the purse in a very jail of a bag which hung upon her arm by a heavy chain, and shut up like a bite.", "마부에게 삯을 치를 때는 단단한 강철 지갑에서 돈을 꺼냈고, 그 지갑은 영락없이 감옥 같은 가방 속에 넣어 두었는데, 그 가방은 묵직한 사슬로 그녀의 팔에 매달려 있었고 짐승이 콱 무는 것처럼 딱 닫혔다."], ["I had never, at that time, seen such a metallic lady altogether as Miss Murdstone was.", "그 당시 나는 머드스톤 양만큼 통째로 쇠붙이 같은 부인은 본 적이 없었다."]], [["She was brought into the parlour with many tokens of welcome, and there formally recognized my mother as a new and near relation.", "그녀는 온갖 환영의 인사를 받으며 거실로 안내됐고, 거기서 정식으로 내 어머니를 새롭고 가까운 친척으로 인정했다."], ["Then she looked at me, and said:", "그러더니 나를 보고 말했다."]], [["‘Is that your boy, sister-in-law?’", "\"쟤가 올케 아들이오?\""]], [["My mother acknowledged me.", "어머니는 그렇다고 나를 인정했다."]], [["‘Generally speaking,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘I don’t like boys.", "\"대체로 말해서,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"나는 사내애들을 안 좋아해."], ["How d’ye do, boy?’", "잘 있었니, 얘?\""]], [["Under these encouraging circumstances, I replied that I was very well, and that I hoped she was the same; with such an indifferent grace, that Miss Murdstone disposed of me in two words:", "이런 격려가 넘치는 분위기 속에서, 나는 잘 지냈고 그쪽도 그러시길 바란다고 대답했는데, 어찌나 무덤덤하고 시큰둥했던지 머드스톤 양은 두 마디로 나를 처분해 버렸다."]], [["‘Wants manner!’", "\"버르장머리가 없군!\""]], [["Having uttered which, with great distinctness, she begged the favour of being shown to her room, which became to me from that time forth a place of awe and dread, wherein the two black boxes were never seen open or known to be left unlocked, and where (for I peeped in once or twice when she was out) numerous little steel fetters and rivets, with which Miss Murdstone embellished herself when she was dressed, generally hung upon the looking-glass in formidable array.", "그 말을 또박또박 내뱉고 나서, 그녀는 자기 방으로 안내해 주기를 청했다. 그 방은 그때부터 내게 두려움과 공포의 장소가 되었는데, 그 시커먼 상자 두 개는 한 번도 열린 채로 보인 적이 없었고 자물쇠가 풀린 채 놓여 있던 적도 없었으며, (그녀가 나간 사이 한두 번 들여다본 적이 있는데) 거기에는 머드스톤 양이 차려입을 때 몸을 치장하는 자그마한 강철 족쇄와 대갈못 따위가 거울 위에 으리으리하게 줄줄이 걸려 있었다."]], [["As well as I could make out, she had come for good, and had no intention of ever going again.", "내가 알아챈 바로는, 그녀는 아예 눌러앉으러 온 것이었고 다시 떠날 생각은 조금도 없었다."], ["She began to ‘help’ my mother next morning, and was in and out of the store-closet all day, putting things to rights, and making havoc in the old arrangements.", "그녀는 이튿날 아침부터 어머니를 '거들기' 시작했고, 하루 종일 식료품 창고를 들락거리며 물건들을 제자리에 놓는답시고 예전의 정리 방식을 죄다 뒤엎어 놓았다."], ["Almost the first remarkable thing I observed in Miss Murdstone was, her being constantly haunted by a suspicion that the servants had a man secreted somewhere on the premises.", "머드스톤 양에게서 처음으로 알아챈 눈에 띄는 점은, 하인들이 집 안 어딘가에 사내 하나를 숨겨 놓았다는 의심에 끊임없이 사로잡혀 있다는 것이었다."], ["Under the influence of this delusion, she dived into the coal-cellar at the most untimely hours, and scarcely ever opened the door of a dark cupboard without clapping it to again, in the belief that she had got him.", "이 망상에 사로잡혀 그녀는 더없이 엉뚱한 시각에 석탄 창고로 뛰어들었고, 어두컴컴한 벽장 문을 열 때마다 거의 매번, 드디어 그놈을 잡았다는 믿음으로 문을 도로 쾅 닫곤 했다."]], [["Though there was nothing very airy about Miss Murdstone, she was a perfect Lark in point of getting up.", "머드스톤 양에게 가뿐한 구석이라곤 전혀 없었지만, 일찍 일어나는 데서만큼은 영락없는 종달새였다."], ["She was up (and, as I believe to this hour, looking for that man) before anybody in the house was stirring.", "그녀는 집 안 누구도 꼼지락거리기 전에 (그리고 지금까지도 나는 그녀가 그 사내를 찾고 있었다고 믿는다) 일어나 있었다."], ["Peggotty gave it as her opinion that she even slept with one eye open; but I could not concur in this idea; for I tried it myself after hearing the suggestion thrown out, and found it couldn’t be done.", "패거티는 그녀가 한쪽 눈을 뜨고 잔다는 게 자기 생각이라고 말했지만, 나는 그 말에 동의할 수 없었다. 그 얘기를 듣고 나도 직접 해 봤는데, 도저히 안 되는 일이었기 때문이다."]], [["On the very first morning after her arrival she was up and ringing her bell at cock-crow.", "도착한 바로 다음 날 아침, 그녀는 닭이 울 무렵 벌써 일어나 종을 울리고 있었다."], ["When my mother came down to breakfast and was going to make the tea, Miss Murdstone gave her a kind of peck on the cheek, which was her nearest approach to a kiss, and said:", "어머니가 아침을 먹으러 내려와 차를 끓이려 하자, 머드스톤 양은 입맞춤에 가장 가까운 행동이랍시고 어머니 뺨을 콕 쪼는 시늉을 하고는 말했다."]], [["‘Now, Clara, my dear, I am come here, you know, to relieve you of all the trouble I can.", "\"자, 클라라, 새아씨, 알다시피 나는 새아씨 수고를 덜어 줄 수 있는 데까진 다 덜어 주려고 여기 온 거예요."], ["You’re much too pretty and thoughtless’--my mother blushed but laughed, and seemed not to dislike this character--‘to have any duties imposed upon you that can be undertaken by me.", "새아씨는 너무 곱고 생각이 없어서\" — 어머니는 얼굴을 붉히면서도 웃었고, 그런 평가를 싫어하지 않는 듯했다 — \"내가 맡을 수 있는 집안일을 떠맡길 수가 없어요."], ["If you’ll be so good as give me your keys, my dear, I’ll attend to all this sort of thing in future.’", "미안하지만 새아씨, 열쇠를 내게 주면, 앞으로 이런 일은 다 내가 챙길게요.\""]], [["From that time, Miss Murdstone kept the keys in her own little jail all day, and under her pillow all night, and my mother had no more to do with them than I had.", "그때부터 머드스톤 양은 낮에는 그 작은 감옥 같은 가방에, 밤에는 자기 베개 밑에 열쇠를 간직했고, 어머니는 나만큼이나 그 열쇠와 아무런 상관이 없게 되었다."]], [["My mother did not suffer her authority to pass from her without a shadow of protest.", "어머니가 자기 권한을 손에서 놓으면서 일말의 항의 그림자조차 없었던 건 아니다."], ["One night when Miss Murdstone had been developing certain household plans to her brother, of which he signified his approbation, my mother suddenly began to cry, and said she thought she might have been consulted.", "어느 날 밤 머드스톤 양이 자기 오빠에게 어떤 살림 계획들을 늘어놓고 그가 그것에 찬성의 뜻을 보이자, 어머니가 별안간 울음을 터뜨리며 자기와 의논했어도 됐을 거라고 말했다."]], [["‘Clara!’ said Mr.", "\"클라라!\""], ["Murdstone sternly. ‘Clara!", "머드스톤 씨가 엄하게 말했다. \"클라라!"], ["I wonder at you.’", "당신이 놀랍구려.\""]], [["‘Oh, it’s very well to say you wonder, Edward!’ cried my mother, ‘and it’s very well for you to talk about firmness, but you wouldn’t like it yourself.’", "\"오, 놀랍다는 말이야 하기 좋죠, 에드워드!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"단호함이 어쩌고 떠드는 것도 당신한테야 좋겠죠. 하지만 당신이라면 그걸 좋아하지 않을걸요.\""]], [["Firmness, I may observe, was the grand quality on which both Mr. and Miss Murdstone took their stand.", "여기서 짚어 두자면, 단호함이야말로 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양 둘 다 떠받드는 으뜸가는 미덕이었다."], ["However I might have expressed my comprehension of it at that time, if I had been called upon, I nevertheless did clearly comprehend in my own way, that it was another name for tyranny; and for a certain gloomy, arrogant, devil’s humour, that was in them both.", "그때 누가 물었다면 내가 그것을 어떻게 풀어 말했을지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 나는 내 나름으로 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 그것은 폭정의 다른 이름이며, 그 둘 모두에게 깃든 음울하고 오만한, 악마 같은 심술의 다른 이름이라는 것을."], ["The creed, as I should state it now, was this. Mr.", "그 신조를 지금 내가 정리하자면 이렇다. 머드스톤 씨는 단호하다."], ["Murdstone was firm; nobody in his world was to be so firm as Mr.", "그의 세상에서 머드스톤 씨만큼 단호한 사람은 없어야 한다."], ["Murdstone; nobody else in his world was to be firm at all, for everybody was to be bent to his firmness.", "그의 세상에서 다른 누구도 결코 단호해서는 안 된다. 모든 이가 그의 단호함 앞에 굽혀야 하니까."], ["Miss Murdstone was an exception.", "머드스톤 양은 예외였다."], ["She might be firm, but only by relationship, and in an inferior and tributary degree.", "그녀는 단호할 수 있었으나, 오직 혈연의 자격으로, 그것도 낮고 종속된 정도로만 그러했다."], ["My mother was another exception.", "어머니는 또 다른 예외였다."], ["She might be firm, and must be; but only in bearing their firmness, and firmly believing there was no other firmness upon earth.", "어머니도 단호할 수 있었고 또 단호해야 했지만, 오직 그들의 단호함을 견뎌 내는 일에서만, 그리고 이 세상에 다른 단호함이란 없다고 굳게 믿는 일에서만 그러했다."]], [["‘It’s very hard,’ said my mother, ‘that in my own house--’", "\"너무하잖아요.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"내 집에서—\""]], [["‘My own house?’ repeated Mr. Murdstone.", "\"내 집이라고?\" 머드스톤 씨가 되받았다."], ["‘Clara!’", "\"클라라!\""]], [["‘OUR own house, I mean,’ faltered my mother, evidently frightened--‘I hope you must know what I mean, Edward--it’s very hard that in YOUR own house I may not have a word to say about domestic matters.", "\"우리 집 말이에요.\" 어머니가 또렷이 겁에 질려 더듬거렸다. \"내가 무슨 말 하는지 알 거예요, 에드워드 — 당신 집에서 내가 집안일에 대해 한마디도 못 한다는 게 너무하다는 거죠."], ["I am sure I managed very well before we were married.", "결혼하기 전엔 내가 얼마나 잘 꾸려 나갔는데요."], ["There’s evidence,’ said my mother, sobbing; ‘ask Peggotty if I didn’t do very well when I wasn’t interfered with!’", "증거도 있어요.\" 어머니가 흐느끼며 말했다. \"패거티한테 물어봐요, 아무도 참견하지 않을 때 내가 얼마나 잘했는지!\""]], [["‘Edward,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘let there be an end of this.", "\"에드워드.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"이쯤에서 끝냅시다."], ["I go tomorrow.’", "나는 내일 떠나겠어요.\""]], [["‘Jane Murdstone,’ said her brother, ‘be silent!", "\"제인 머드스톤.\" 그녀의 오빠가 말했다. \"잠자코 있어!"], ["How dare you to insinuate that you don’t know my character better than your words imply?’", "네 말투가 풍기는 것처럼 네가 내 성격을 그것밖에 모른다고 넌지시 흘리다니, 어디 감히!\""]], [["‘I am sure,’ my poor mother went on, at a grievous disadvantage, and with many tears, ‘I don’t want anybody to go.", "\"정말이지,\" 가엾은 어머니가 한심한 처지로, 눈물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 말을 이었다. \"나는 누구도 떠나길 바라지 않아요."], ["I should be very miserable and unhappy if anybody was to go. I don’t ask much.", "누구라도 떠난다면 난 정말 비참하고 불행할 거예요. 많은 걸 바라는 게 아니에요."], ["I am not unreasonable.", "무리한 사람이 아니라고요."], ["I only want to be consulted sometimes.", "그저 가끔 의논 좀 했으면 하는 것뿐이에요."], ["I am very much obliged to anybody who assists me, and I only want to be consulted as a mere form, sometimes.", "날 도와주는 사람한테는 정말 고마워하고 있어요. 그저 가끔, 형식으로라도 의논 좀 했으면 하는 거예요."], ["I thought you were pleased, once, with my being a little inexperienced and girlish, Edward--I am sure you said so--but you seem to hate me for it now, you are so severe.’", "에드워드, 한때 당신은 내가 좀 미숙하고 계집애 같은 걸 좋아한다고 했잖아요 — 분명 그렇게 말했잖아요 — 그런데 이제는 그걸로 날 미워하는 것 같아요, 당신이 어찌나 매정한지.\""]], [["‘Edward,’ said Miss Murdstone, again, ‘let there be an end of this.", "\"에드워드.\" 머드스톤 양이 다시 말했다. \"이쯤에서 끝냅시다."], ["I go tomorrow.’", "나는 내일 떠나겠어요.\""]], [["‘Jane Murdstone,’ thundered Mr. Murdstone.", "\"제인 머드스톤.\" 머드스톤 씨가 천둥 치듯 호통쳤다."], ["‘Will you be silent?", "\"잠자코 못 있겠냐?"], ["How dare you?’", "어디 감히?\""]], [["Miss Murdstone made a jail-delivery of her pocket-handkerchief, and held it before her eyes.", "머드스톤 양은 자기 손수건을 감옥에서 풀어 주듯 꺼내 두 눈 앞에 갖다 댔다."]], [["‘Clara,’ he continued, looking at my mother, ‘you surprise me! You astound me!", "\"클라라,\" 그가 어머니를 바라보며 말을 이었다."], ["Yes, I had a satisfaction in the thought of marrying an inexperienced and artless person, and forming her character, and infusing into it some amount of that firmness and decision of which it stood in need.", "\"당신은 나를 놀라게 하는구려! 아연실색하게 만들어! 그래, 나는 미숙하고 꾸밈없는 사람과 결혼해서, 그 성품을 빚어내고, 거기에 필요한 단호함과 결단력을 얼마간 불어넣겠다는 생각에 흐뭇해했소."], ["But when Jane Murdstone is kind enough to come to my assistance in this endeavour, and to assume, for my sake, a condition something like a housekeeper’s, and when she meets with a base return--’", "그런데 제인 머드스톤이 친절하게도 이 일을 거들어 주러 와서, 나를 위해 가정부나 다름없는 처지를 떠맡아 줬는데, 그 보답으로 이런 비열한 대접을 받다니—\""]], [["‘Oh, pray, pray, Edward,’ cried my mother, ‘don’t accuse me of being ungrateful.", "\"오, 제발, 제발요, 에드워드.\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"내가 은혜를 모른다고 몰아붙이지 말아요."], ["I am sure I am not ungrateful.", "정말이지 난 은혜를 모르는 사람이 아니에요."], ["No one ever said I was before.", "전엔 누구도 나더러 그렇다고 한 적 없어요."], ["I have many faults, but not that. Oh, don’t, my dear!’", "내게 흠이 많지만, 그건 아니에요. 오, 제발 그러지 말아요, 여보!\""]], [["‘When Jane Murdstone meets, I say,’ he went on, after waiting until my mother was silent, ‘with a base return, that feeling of mine is chilled and altered.’", "\"내 말은, 제인 머드스톤이,\" 그는 어머니가 잠잠해질 때까지 기다렸다가 말을 이었다. \"이런 비열한 대접을 받으니, 내 그 마음이 싸늘히 식어 변해 버린다는 거요.\""]], [["‘Don’t, my love, say that!’ implored my mother very piteously.", "\"제발, 여보, 그런 말 말아요!\" 어머니가 가엾기 그지없이 애원했다."], ["‘Oh, don’t, Edward!", "\"오, 그러지 말아요, 에드워드!"], ["I can’t bear to hear it.", "그 말은 차마 못 듣겠어요."], ["Whatever I am, I am affectionate.", "내가 어떤 사람이든, 나는 정이 깊은 사람이에요."], ["I know I am affectionate.", "내가 정이 깊다는 거 나도 알아요."], ["I wouldn’t say it, if I wasn’t sure that I am. Ask Peggotty.", "확신이 없다면 이런 말 안 했을 거예요. 패거티한테 물어봐요."], ["I am sure she’ll tell you I’m affectionate.’", "분명 내가 정이 깊다고 말해 줄 거예요.\""]], [["‘There is no extent of mere weakness, Clara,’ said Mr.", "\"한낱 나약함이란 건, 클라라,\" 머드스톤 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Murdstone in reply, ‘that can have the least weight with me.", "\"아무리 끝없이 늘어놓아도 내겐 눈곱만큼의 무게도 없소."], ["You lose breath.’", "숨만 낭비할 뿐이지.\""]], [["‘Pray let us be friends,’ said my mother, ‘I couldn’t live under coldness or unkindness.", "\"제발 우리 좋게 지내요.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"냉랭함이나 매정함 속에선 도저히 못 살겠어요."], ["I am so sorry.", "정말 미안해요."], ["I have a great many defects, I know, and it’s very good of you, Edward, with your strength of mind, to endeavour to correct them for me.", "내게 흠이 아주 많다는 거 나도 알아요. 그리고 에드워드, 당신은 그 굳센 마음으로 날 위해 그걸 고쳐 주려고 애쓰니 참 고마워요."], ["Jane, I don’t object to anything.", "제인, 나는 아무것도 반대하지 않아요."], ["I should be quite broken-hearted if you thought of leaving--’ My mother was too much overcome to go on.", "당신이 떠날 생각을 한다면 난 가슴이 찢어질 거예요—\" 어머니는 너무 북받쳐 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["‘Jane Murdstone,’ said Mr.", "\"제인 머드스톤.\""], ["Murdstone to his sister, ‘any harsh words between us are, I hope, uncommon.", "머드스톤 씨가 누이에게 말했다. \"우리 사이에 모진 말이 오가는 건, 바라건대, 드문 일이지."], ["It is not my fault that so unusual an occurrence has taken place tonight.", "오늘 밤 이렇게 별난 일이 벌어진 건 내 잘못이 아니야."], ["I was betrayed into it by another.", "다른 사람 때문에 거기 휘말린 거지."], ["Nor is it your fault.", "그리고 네 잘못도 아니야."], ["You were betrayed into it by another.", "너도 다른 사람 때문에 거기 휘말린 거니까."], ["Let us both try to forget it.", "우리 둘 다 그 일을 잊도록 애써 보자."], ["And as this,’ he added, after these magnanimous words, ‘is not a fit scene for the boy--David, go to bed!’", "그리고 이건,\" 그는 이 너그러운 말을 마친 뒤 덧붙였다. \"애한테 보일 만한 장면이 아니지 — 데이비드, 가서 자거라!\""]], [["I could hardly find the door, through the tears that stood in my eyes.", "두 눈에 그렁그렁 고인 눈물 너머로 문을 거의 찾을 수가 없었다."], ["I was so sorry for my mother’s distress; but I groped my way out, and groped my way up to my room in the dark, without even having the heart to say good night to Peggotty, or to get a candle from her.", "어머니가 그토록 괴로워하는 것이 너무도 가여웠다. 하지만 나는 더듬더듬 길을 찾아 나가, 어둠 속에서 더듬더듬 내 방까지 올라갔다. 패거티에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넬 마음도, 그녀에게서 촛불 하나 얻어 올 마음도 나지 않았다."], ["When her coming up to look for me, an hour or so afterwards, awoke me, she said that my mother had gone to bed poorly, and that Mr. and Miss Murdstone were sitting alone.", "한 시간쯤 지나 그녀가 나를 찾으러 올라온 바람에 잠에서 깼을 때, 그녀는 어머니가 몸이 좋지 않아 잠자리에 들었고, 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양만 단둘이 앉아 있다고 말했다."]], [["Going down next morning rather earlier than usual, I paused outside the parlour door, on hearing my mother’s voice.", "이튿날 아침 여느 때보다 좀 일찍 내려가던 나는 어머니의 목소리가 들려서 거실 문밖에 멈춰 섰다."], ["She was very earnestly and humbly entreating Miss Murdstone’s pardon, which that lady granted, and a perfect reconciliation took place.", "어머니는 무척 간절하고 비굴하게 머드스톤 양에게 용서를 빌고 있었고, 그 부인은 그것을 받아들였으며, 그렇게 완벽한 화해가 이루어졌다."], ["I never knew my mother afterwards to give an opinion on any matter, without first appealing to Miss Murdstone, or without having first ascertained by some sure means, what Miss Murdstone’s opinion was; and I never saw Miss Murdstone, when out of temper (she was infirm that way), move her hand towards her bag as if she were going to take out the keys and offer to resign them to my mother, without seeing that my mother was in a terrible fright.", "그 후로 어머니가 무슨 일에든 의견을 내는 걸 나는 한 번도 보지 못했다. 먼저 머드스톤 양에게 여쭙거나, 아니면 어떤 확실한 수단으로 머드스톤 양의 의견이 무엇인지 미리 알아내지 않고서는. 또 머드스톤 양이 심기가 틀어졌을 때 (그녀는 그런 쪽으로 병약했다) 열쇠를 꺼내 어머니에게 도로 내놓으려는 듯 가방으로 손을 뻗는 걸 볼 때마다, 나는 어김없이 어머니가 무섭게 겁먹는 모습을 보았다."]], [["The gloomy taint that was in the Murdstone blood, darkened the Murdstone religion, which was austere and wrathful.", "머드스톤 집안의 피에 흐르는 음울한 기운은 머드스톤 집안의 종교를 어둡게 물들였고, 그 종교는 가혹하고 노여움에 차 있었다."], ["I have thought, since, that its assuming that character was a necessary consequence of Mr.", "그것이 그런 성격을 띠게 된 것은 머드스톤 씨의 단호함에서 비롯된 필연적 결과였다는 생각이 그 후로 들었다."], ["Murdstone’s firmness, which wouldn’t allow him to let anybody off from the utmost weight of the severest penalties he could find any excuse for.", "그 단호함은, 핑계를 댈 수 있는 한 가장 혹독한 벌의 가장 무거운 무게를 그 누구에게서도 면제해 주는 법이 없었으니까."], ["Be this as it may, I well remember the tremendous visages with which we used to go to church, and the changed air of the place.", "어찌 됐든, 우리가 교회에 갈 때 짓던 그 무시무시한 얼굴들과, 그곳의 달라진 분위기가 똑똑히 기억난다."], ["Again, the dreaded Sunday comes round, and I file into the old pew first, like a guarded captive brought to a condemned service.", "또다시, 두려운 일요일이 돌아온다. 나는 마치 사형 집행 예배에 끌려온 감시받는 포로처럼 맨 먼저 낡은 신도석으로 줄지어 들어간다."], ["Again, Miss Murdstone, in a black velvet gown, that looks as if it had been made out of a pall, follows close upon me; then my mother; then her husband.", "또다시, 상여 덮개로 지은 듯한 검은 우단 가운 차림의 머드스톤 양이 내 바로 뒤를 바짝 따른다. 그다음이 어머니, 그다음이 그녀의 남편이다."], ["There is no Peggotty now, as in the old time.", "옛날과 달리 이제 패거티는 없다."], ["Again, I listen to Miss Murdstone mumbling the responses, and emphasizing all the dread words with a cruel relish.", "또다시, 나는 머드스톤 양이 응답 기도를 우물거리며 무시무시한 단어마다 잔인한 쾌감으로 힘주어 발음하는 소리를 듣는다."], ["Again, I see her dark eyes roll round the church when she says ‘miserable sinners’, as if she were calling all the congregation names.", "또다시, 나는 그녀가 '비참한 죄인들' 하고 말할 때 마치 온 신도를 싸잡아 욕하기라도 하듯 그 검은 눈을 교회 안에 휘휘 굴리는 것을 본다."], ["Again, I catch rare glimpses of my mother, moving her lips timidly between the two, with one of them muttering at each ear like low thunder.", "또다시, 나는 두 사람 사이에서 어머니가 머뭇머뭇 입술을 달싹이는 모습을 가끔 흘끗 잡아내는데, 그 두 사람은 양쪽 귀에 대고 낮은 천둥처럼 중얼대고 있다."], ["Again, I wonder with a sudden fear whether it is likely that our good old clergyman can be wrong, and Mr. and Miss Murdstone right, and that all the angels in Heaven can be destroying angels.", "또다시, 나는 우리의 인자한 노목사님이 틀리고 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 옳을 수가, 그리고 천국의 모든 천사가 멸하는 천사일 수가 과연 있을까, 하는 갑작스러운 두려움에 휩싸인다."], ["Again, if I move a finger or relax a muscle of my face, Miss Murdstone pokes me with her prayer-book, and makes my side ache.", "또다시, 내가 손가락 하나라도 까딱하거나 얼굴 근육 하나라도 풀면, 머드스톤 양이 기도서로 나를 콕 찔러 옆구리를 아프게 한다."]], [["Yes, and again, as we walk home, I note some neighbours looking at my mother and at me, and whispering.", "그래, 또다시, 집으로 걸어가는 길에 나는 이웃 몇몇이 어머니와 나를 바라보며 수군대는 것을 알아챈다."], ["Again, as the three go on arm-in-arm, and I linger behind alone, I follow some of those looks, and wonder if my mother’s step be really not so light as I have seen it, and if the gaiety of her beauty be really almost worried away.", "또다시, 세 사람이 팔짱을 끼고 앞서 가고 나 혼자 뒤에 처져 따라갈 때, 나는 그 시선 몇몇을 좇으며, 어머니의 발걸음이 정말 내가 보아 온 만큼 가볍지 않은 건지, 그 아름다움의 발랄함이 정말 거의 시들어 버린 건지 궁금해한다."], ["Again, I wonder whether any of the neighbours call to mind, as I do, how we used to walk home together, she and I; and I wonder stupidly about that, all the dreary dismal day.", "또다시, 나는 그 이웃 누군가도 나처럼, 예전에 어머니와 내가 함께 집으로 걸어가던 모습을 떠올릴까 궁금해한다. 그리고 그 음울하고 쓸쓸한 하루 내내, 나는 그것을 멍청하게 궁금해한다."]], [["There had been some talk on occasions of my going to boarding-school. Mr.", "이따금 나를 기숙학교에 보내자는 이야기가 오갔다."], ["and Miss Murdstone had originated it, and my mother had of course agreed with them.", "머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 그걸 꺼냈고, 물론 어머니도 그들에게 동의했다."], ["Nothing, however, was concluded on the subject yet.", "하지만 아직 그 문제로 결론이 난 건 없었다."], ["In the meantime, I learnt lessons at home. Shall I ever forget those lessons!", "그동안 나는 집에서 공부했다. 그 공부 시간을 내가 어찌 잊을 수 있을까!"], ["They were presided over nominally by my mother, but really by Mr. Murdstone and his sister, who were always present, and found them a favourable occasion for giving my mother lessons in that miscalled firmness, which was the bane of both our lives.", "명목상으로는 어머니가 주관했지만 실제로는 머드스톤 씨와 그의 누이가 주관했는데, 두 사람은 늘 자리를 지키면서 그 시간을, 우리 둘의 인생을 망쳐 놓은 그 잘못 이름 붙은 단호함을 어머니에게 가르치는 좋은 기회로 삼았다."], ["I believe I was kept at home for that purpose. I had been apt enough to learn, and willing enough, when my mother and I had lived alone together.", "나는 바로 그 목적 때문에 집에 붙들려 있었던 거라고 믿는다. 어머니와 나 단둘이 살 때만 해도 나는 배움에 제법 재주가 있었고 의욕도 넘쳤다."], ["I can faintly remember learning the alphabet at her knee.", "어머니 무릎에 앉아 알파벳을 배우던 게 어렴풋이 떠오른다."], ["To this day, when I look upon the fat black letters in the primer, the puzzling novelty of their shapes, and the easy good-nature of O and Q and S, seem to present themselves again before me as they used to do.", "지금도 입문서의 통통하고 시커먼 글자들을 들여다보면, 그 모양의 알쏭달쏭한 새로움과, O며 Q며 S의 너그럽고 인심 좋은 생김새가, 예전 그대로 다시 눈앞에 떠오르는 듯하다."], ["But they recall no feeling of disgust or reluctance. On the contrary, I seem to have walked along a path of flowers as far as the crocodile-book, and to have been cheered by the gentleness of my mother’s voice and manner all the way.", "그러나 그 글자들은 역겨움이나 거부감 같은 감정은 조금도 불러일으키지 않는다. 오히려 나는 악어 나오는 책에 이르기까지 꽃길을 걸어왔던 것 같고, 그 길 내내 어머니의 다정한 목소리와 태도에 힘을 얻었던 것 같다."], ["But these solemn lessons which succeeded those, I remember as the death-blow of my peace, and a grievous daily drudgery and misery.", "하지만 그 뒤를 이은 이 엄숙한 공부 시간은, 내 평화의 숨통을 끊어 놓은 일격으로, 그리고 매일같이 이어지는 괴롭고 고된 비참함으로 기억된다."], ["They were very long, very numerous, very hard--perfectly unintelligible, some of them, to me--and I was generally as much bewildered by them as I believe my poor mother was herself.", "그것은 무척 길고, 무척 많고, 무척 어려웠으며 — 그중 몇몇은 내겐 도무지 알아먹을 수 없는 것이었다 — 나는 대개 그것 때문에 어쩔 줄 몰라 했는데, 가엾은 어머니 본인도 그랬으리라 믿는다."]], [["Let me remember how it used to be, and bring one morning back again.", "그때가 어떠했는지 떠올려 보고, 어느 아침 하나를 다시 불러와 보겠다."]], [["I come into the second-best parlour after breakfast, with my books, and an exercise-book, and a slate.", "나는 아침을 먹고 나서 책 몇 권과 연습장 하나, 석판 하나를 들고 둘째로 좋은 거실로 들어선다."], ["My mother is ready for me at her writing-desk, but not half so ready as Mr. Murdstone in his easy-chair by the window (though he pretends to be reading a book), or as Miss Murdstone, sitting near my mother stringing steel beads.", "어머니는 글 쓰는 책상 앞에서 나를 맞을 채비를 하고 있지만, 창가 안락의자에 앉은 머드스톤 씨(책을 읽는 척하고는 있다)나, 어머니 곁에 앉아 강철 구슬을 꿰고 있는 머드스톤 양만큼 단단히 채비하고 있지는 않다."], ["The very sight of these two has such an influence over me, that I begin to feel the words I have been at infinite pains to get into my head, all sliding away, and going I don’t know where.", "이 두 사람을 보기만 해도 어찌나 영향을 받는지, 나는 무진 애를 써서 머릿속에 집어넣어 둔 단어들이 죄다 미끄러져 어디론가 사라져 버리는 걸 느끼기 시작한다."], ["I wonder where they do go, by the by?", "그나저나 그것들은 대체 어디로 가는 걸까?"]], [["I hand the first book to my mother.", "나는 첫 번째 책을 어머니에게 건넨다."], ["Perhaps it is a grammar, perhaps a history, or geography.", "어쩌면 문법책일 수도, 어쩌면 역사책이나 지리책일 수도 있다."], ["I take a last drowning look at the page as I give it into her hand, and start off aloud at a racing pace while I have got it fresh.", "책을 어머니 손에 넘기면서 나는 물에 빠지는 사람처럼 그 쪽을 마지막으로 한 번 더 훑고는, 머릿속에 생생할 때 냅다 소리 내어 내달리기 시작한다."], ["I trip over a word. Mr.", "한 단어에서 발이 걸린다."], ["Murdstone looks up.", "머드스톤 씨가 눈을 든다."], ["I trip over another word.", "또 한 단어에서 발이 걸린다."], ["Miss Murdstone looks up.", "머드스톤 양이 눈을 든다."], ["I redden, tumble over half-a-dozen words, and stop.", "나는 얼굴이 빨개진 채로 대여섯 단어를 곤두박질치다가 멈춰 버린다."], ["I think my mother would show me the book if she dared, but she does not dare, and she says softly:", "어머니는 용기만 있다면 내게 책을 보여 주고 싶어 하겠지만, 그럴 용기가 없어서, 다만 나직이 말한다."]], [["‘Oh, Davy, Davy!’", "\"오, 데이비, 데이비!\""]], [["‘Now, Clara,’ says Mr.", "\"자, 클라라,\""], ["Murdstone, ‘be firm with the boy.", "머드스톤 씨가 말한다. \"그 애한테 단호하게 대해요."], ["Don’t say, “Oh, Davy, Davy!”", "'오, 데이비, 데이비!' 같은 소리 하지 말고."], ["That’s childish.", "그건 어린애 같은 짓이오."], ["He knows his lesson, or he does not know it.’", "그 애는 외웠거나, 못 외웠거나 둘 중 하나니까.\""]], [["‘He does NOT know it,’ Miss Murdstone interposes awfully.", "\"못 외웠어요.\" 머드스톤 양이 으스스하게 끼어든다."]], [["‘I am really afraid he does not,’ says my mother.", "\"정말이지 못 외운 게 아닐까 걱정이에요.\" 어머니가 말한다."]], [["‘Then, you see, Clara,’ returns Miss Murdstone, ‘you should just give him the book back, and make him know it.’", "\"그렇다면, 봐요, 클라라,\" 머드스톤 양이 받는다. \"그냥 책을 도로 돌려주고 외우게 만들면 되잖아요.\""]], [["‘Yes, certainly,’ says my mother; ‘that is what I intend to do, my dear Jane.", "\"네, 그럼요.\" 어머니가 말한다. \"그렇게 할 작정이에요, 제인."], ["Now, Davy, try once more, and don’t be stupid.’", "자, 데이비, 한 번 더 해 봐. 멍청하게 굴지 말고.\""]], [["I obey the first clause of the injunction by trying once more, but am not so successful with the second, for I am very stupid.", "나는 한 번 더 해 보는 것으로 그 명령의 첫째 항목엔 따르지만, 둘째 항목엔 그리 성공하지 못한다."], ["I tumble down before I get to the old place, at a point where I was all right before, and stop to think. But I can’t think about the lesson.", "나는 정말 멍청하니까. 아까는 멀쩡히 넘어갔던 대목에서, 옛 자리에 이르기도 전에 곤두박질치고는 생각하려고 멈춰 선다."], ["I think of the number of yards of net in Miss Murdstone’s cap, or of the price of Mr. Murdstone’s dressing-gown, or any such ridiculous problem that I have no business with, and don’t want to have anything at all to do with. Mr.", "그러나 나는 그 공부에 대해 도무지 생각할 수가 없다. 머드스톤 양의 모자에 달린 그물이 몇 야드나 될까, 머드스톤 씨의 실내복 값이 얼마일까, 따위, 나와는 아무 상관도 없고 조금도 엮이고 싶지 않은 그런 터무니없는 문제만 생각한다."], ["Murdstone makes a movement of impatience which I have been expecting for a long time.", "머드스톤 씨가 내가 오래전부터 예상해 온 그 짜증 섞인 몸짓을 한다."], ["Miss Murdstone does the same.", "머드스톤 양도 똑같이 한다."], ["My mother glances submissively at them, shuts the book, and lays it by as an arrear to be worked out when my other tasks are done.", "어머니는 순순히 그들을 흘끗 보고는 책을 덮어, 다른 과제가 끝난 뒤에 해치워야 할 밀린 빚으로 한쪽에 치워 둔다."]], [["There is a pile of these arrears very soon, and it swells like a rolling snowball.", "이런 밀린 빚이 금세 한 무더기가 되어, 굴러가는 눈덩이처럼 불어난다."], ["The bigger it gets, the more stupid I get.", "그게 커질수록 나는 더 멍청해진다."], ["The case is so hopeless, and I feel that I am wallowing in such a bog of nonsense, that I give up all idea of getting out, and abandon myself to my fate.", "사정이 어찌나 절망적인지, 나는 이런 헛소리의 수렁에서 허우적대고 있다는 느낌에, 빠져나갈 생각일랑 죄다 포기하고 내 운명에 나를 맡겨 버린다."], ["The despairing way in which my mother and I look at each other, as I blunder on, is truly melancholy.", "내가 비척대며 더듬어 나가는 동안 어머니와 내가 서로를 바라보는 그 절망스러운 모습은 정말이지 처량하기 그지없다."], ["But the greatest effect in these miserable lessons is when my mother (thinking nobody is observing her) tries to give me the cue by the motion of her lips.", "하지만 이 비참한 공부 시간에 가장 큰 파장을 일으키는 건, 어머니가 (아무도 자기를 안 본다고 여기고) 입술의 움직임으로 내게 실마리를 주려고 할 때다."], ["At that instant, Miss Murdstone, who has been lying in wait for nothing else all along, says in a deep warning voice:", "바로 그 순간, 줄곧 다른 건 아랑곳없이 오로지 그것만 노리고 도사리고 있던 머드스톤 양이, 묵직한 경고의 목소리로 말한다."]], [["‘Clara!’", "\"클라라!\""]], [["My mother starts, colours, and smiles faintly. Mr.", "어머니는 흠칫하며 얼굴을 붉히고 희미하게 미소 짓는다."], ["Murdstone comes out of his chair, takes the book, throws it at me or boxes my ears with it, and turns me out of the room by the shoulders.", "머드스톤 씨가 의자에서 일어나 책을 집어 들고는, 나한테 던지거나 그걸로 내 귀싸대기를 후려치고, 두 어깨를 잡아 나를 방 밖으로 내쫓는다."]], [["Even when the lessons are done, the worst is yet to happen, in the shape of an appalling sum.", "공부가 끝난 뒤에도, 가장 끔찍한 것은 아직 닥치지 않았으니, 그건 바로 소름 끼치는 산수 문제의 모습으로 온다."], ["This is invented for me, and delivered to me orally by Mr.", "이건 나를 위해 지어내져, 머드스톤 씨가 입으로 불러 주는데, 이렇게 시작된다."], ["Murdstone, and begins, ‘If I go into a cheesemonger’s shop, and buy five thousand double-Gloucester cheeses at fourpence-halfpenny each, present payment’--at which I see Miss Murdstone secretly overjoyed.", "\"내가 치즈 가게에 들어가서 더블 글로스터 치즈 오천 개를 한 개에 사 펜스 반 페니씩 주고 현금으로 산다면—\" 이 대목에서 나는 머드스톤 양이 몰래 좋아 어쩔 줄 모르는 걸 본다."], ["I pore over these cheeses without any result or enlightenment until dinner-time, when, having made a Mulatto of myself by getting the dirt of the slate into the pores of my skin, I have a slice of bread to help me out with the cheeses, and am considered in disgrace for the rest of the evening.", "나는 이 치즈들을 두고 아무런 성과도 깨달음도 없이 저녁 먹을 때까지 끙끙대는데, 그쯤 되면 석판의 때를 살갗 땀구멍에 묻혀 물라토 꼴이 되어 있고, 그 치즈를 헤쳐 나가는 데 보탬이 되라고 빵 한 조각을 받으며, 그날 밤 내내 망신스러운 신세로 여겨진다."]], [["It seems to me, at this distance of time, as if my unfortunate studies generally took this course.", "이렇게 세월이 흐른 지금 돌이켜 보면, 내 불운한 공부는 대체로 이런 식으로 흘러간 것 같다."], ["I could have done very well if I had been without the Murdstones; but the influence of the Murdstones upon me was like the fascination of two snakes on a wretched young bird.", "머드스톤 남매만 없었다면 나는 아주 잘했을 텐데, 그들이 내게 미친 영향은 가엾은 어린 새 한 마리를 사로잡는 두 마리 뱀의 흘림 같은 것이었다. 어쩌다 오전을 그럭저럭 봐줄 만하게 넘긴다 해도 얻는 거라곤 저녁밥뿐이었다."], ["Even when I did get through the morning with tolerable credit, there was not much gained but dinner; for Miss Murdstone never could endure to see me untasked, and if I rashly made any show of being unemployed, called her brother’s attention to me by saying, ‘Clara, my dear, there’s nothing like work--give your boy an exercise’; which caused me to be clapped down to some new labour, there and then.", "머드스톤 양은 내가 일을 안 하고 있는 꼴을 도무지 못 봐줬으니까. 내가 경솔하게 한가한 티라도 낼라치면, 그녀는 \"클라라, 새아씨, 일만 한 게 없어요 — 애한테 연습 문제 좀 줘요\" 하고 말하며 오빠의 시선을 내게 끌어왔고, 그러면 나는 그 자리에서 곧장 새로운 노동에 처박혔다."], ["As to any recreation with other children of my age, I had very little of that; for the gloomy theology of the Murdstones made all children out to be a swarm of little vipers (though there WAS a child once set in the midst of the Disciples), and held that they contaminated one another.", "내 또래 다른 아이들과 노는 일로 말하자면, 나는 그런 걸 거의 누리지 못했다. 머드스톤 남매의 음울한 신학은 모든 아이를 작은 독사 떼로 몰아붙였고 (예전에 한 아이가 제자들 한가운데에 세워진 적도 있건만), 아이들끼리는 서로를 더럽힌다고 여겼으니까."]], [["The natural result of this treatment, continued, I suppose, for some six months or more, was to make me sullen, dull, and dogged.", "이런 대접이 짐작건대 여섯 달 남짓 이어진 자연스러운 결과로, 나는 시무룩하고 굼뜨고 고집스러운 아이가 되었다."], ["I was not made the less so by my sense of being daily more and more shut out and alienated from my mother.", "게다가 날이 갈수록 점점 더 어머니에게서 떼어지고 멀어진다는 느낌이 나를 더더욱 그렇게 만들었다."], ["I believe I should have been almost stupefied but for one circumstance.", "한 가지 사정만 아니었다면 나는 거의 얼이 빠져 버렸을 거라고 믿는다."]], [["It was this. My father had left a small collection of books in a little room upstairs, to which I had access (for it adjoined my own) and which nobody else in our house ever troubled.", "그건 이런 것이었다. 아버지는 위층 작은 방에 자그마한 책 모음을 남겨 두었는데, 그 방은 (내 방과 붙어 있어서) 내가 드나들 수 있었고 우리 집의 다른 누구도 거기엔 신경 쓰지 않았다."], ["From that blessed little room, Roderick Random, Peregrine Pickle, Humphrey Clinker, Tom Jones, the Vicar of Wakefield, Don Quixote, Gil Blas, and Robinson Crusoe, came out, a glorious host, to keep me company.", "그 축복받은 작은 방에서, 로더릭 랜덤, 페러그린 피클, 험프리 클링커, 톰 존스, 웨이크필드의 목사, 돈키호테, 질 블라스, 로빈슨 크루소가 영광스러운 무리를 이루어 나와서 내 벗이 되어 주었다."], ["They kept alive my fancy, and my hope of something beyond that place and time,--they, and the Arabian Nights, and the Tales of the Genii,--and did me no harm; for whatever harm was in some of them was not there for me; I knew nothing of it.", "그 책들은, 그리고 아라비안나이트와 정령 이야기는, 내 공상과, 그곳과 그때 너머의 무언가에 대한 희망을 살아 있게 해 주었고, 내게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았다. 그중 몇몇에 해로운 게 들어 있었다 한들, 그건 내게는 거기 있는 게 아니었으니까."], ["It is astonishing to me now, how I found time, in the midst of my porings and blunderings over heavier themes, to read those books as I did.", "나는 그런 건 전혀 몰랐다. 더 무거운 주제들을 끙끙대며 더듬는 와중에 어떻게 그 책들을 그렇게 읽어 낼 시간을 냈는지, 지금 생각하면 놀랍기만 하다."], ["It is curious to me how I could ever have consoled myself under my small troubles (which were great troubles to me), by impersonating my favourite characters in them--as I did--and by putting Mr. and Miss Murdstone into all the bad ones--which I did too.", "내 자그마한 괴로움들 (내게는 큰 괴로움이었다) 속에서, 그 책들 속 좋아하는 인물이 되어 — 실제로 나는 그렇게 했다 — 또 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양을 온갖 악당 역에 집어넣어 — 이것도 실제로 했다 — 어떻게 스스로를 위로할 수 있었는지, 지금 생각하면 신기하다."], ["I have been Tom Jones (a child’s Tom Jones, a harmless creature) for a week together.", "나는 일주일 내내 톰 존스 (어린아이판 톰 존스, 해 끼칠 줄 모르는 녀석이었다)가 되어 보기도 했다."], ["I have sustained my own idea of Roderick Random for a month at a stretch, I verily believe.", "한 달을 내리 로더릭 랜덤이라는 내 나름의 상을 지켜 나간 적도 있다고, 정말이지 나는 믿는다."], ["I had a greedy relish for a few volumes of Voyages and Travels--I forget what, now--that were on those shelves; and for days and days I can remember to have gone about my region of our house, armed with the centre-piece out of an old set of boot-trees--the perfect realization of Captain Somebody, of the Royal British Navy, in danger of being beset by savages, and resolved to sell his life at a great price.", "그 선반에 있던 항해기며 여행기 몇 권 — 지금은 무엇이었는지 잊었다 — 을 나는 게걸스레 탐했다. 그리고 날이면 날마다 우리 집 내 구역을 헌 구두골 한 벌에서 빼낸 가운데 토막으로 무장하고 돌아다니던 게 기억나는데 — 영국 왕립 해군 소속 아무개 함장이, 야만인들에게 둘러싸일 위기에 처해 제 목숨을 비싼 값에 팔아넘기기로 결심한 모습을 완벽하게 구현한 것이었다."], ["The Captain never lost dignity, from having his ears boxed with the Latin Grammar.", "그 함장은 라틴어 문법책으로 귀싸대기를 얻어맞고도 결코 위엄을 잃지 않았다."], ["I did; but the Captain was a Captain and a hero, in despite of all the grammars of all the languages in the world, dead or alive.", "나는 잃었다. 하지만 함장은, 세상의 살아 있든 죽었든 그 어떤 언어의 그 어떤 문법책에도 아랑곳없이, 함장이자 영웅이었다."]], [["This was my only and my constant comfort.", "이것이 나의 유일하고도 한결같은 위안이었다."], ["When I think of it, the picture always rises in my mind, of a summer evening, the boys at play in the churchyard, and I sitting on my bed, reading as if for life.", "이걸 떠올리면 늘 어떤 그림 하나가 머릿속에 떠오른다. 어느 여름 저녁, 아이들은 교회 묘지에서 놀고, 나는 침대에 앉아 목숨이라도 걸린 듯 책을 읽는 그림이다."], ["Every barn in the neighbourhood, every stone in the church, and every foot of the churchyard, had some association of its own, in my mind, connected with these books, and stood for some locality made famous in them.", "이웃의 헛간 하나하나, 교회의 돌 하나하나, 교회 묘지의 한 뼘 한 뼘이, 내 마음속에서는 이 책들과 얽힌 저마다의 사연을 지니고 있었고, 그 책들 속에서 이름을 떨친 어느 고장을 대신하고 있었다."], ["I have seen Tom Pipes go climbing up the church-steeple; I have watched Strap, with the knapsack on his back, stopping to rest himself upon the wicket-gate; and I know that Commodore Trunnion held that club with Mr. Pickle, in the parlour of our little village alehouse.", "나는 톰 파이프스가 교회 첨탑을 기어오르는 걸 보았고, 스트랩이 등에 배낭을 진 채 쪽문에 멈춰 서서 한숨 돌리는 걸 지켜보았으며, 트러니언 함장이 우리 작은 마을 선술집 거실에서 피클 씨와 그 곤봉을 두고 한판 벌인 것을 안다."]], [["The reader now understands, as well as I do, what I was when I came to that point of my youthful history to which I am now coming again.", "이제 독자도 나만큼이나 잘 알 것이다. 내가 다시 이르고 있는 내 어린 시절 이야기의 그 대목에 다다랐을 때 내가 어떤 아이였는지를."]], [["One morning when I went into the parlour with my books, I found my mother looking anxious, Miss Murdstone looking firm, and Mr. Murdstone binding something round the bottom of a cane--a lithe and limber cane, which he left off binding when I came in, and poised and switched in the air.", "어느 날 아침 책을 들고 거실로 들어가니, 어머니는 불안한 얼굴이고, 머드스톤 양은 단호한 얼굴이며, 머드스톤 씨는 회초리 끝에 무언가를 칭칭 감고 있었다 — 낭창낭창 잘 휘는 회초리였는데, 그는 내가 들어오자 감던 손을 멈추고 그것을 허공에 가늠하며 휙휙 휘둘렀다."]], [["‘I tell you, Clara,’ said Mr.", "\"내 말하건대, 클라라,\""], ["Murdstone, ‘I have been often flogged myself.’", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"나도 어릴 때 매를 자주 맞았소.\""]], [["‘To be sure; of course,’ said Miss Murdstone.", "\"그럼요, 당연하죠.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly, my dear Jane,’ faltered my mother, meekly.", "\"물론이죠, 제인.\" 어머니가 고분고분 더듬거렸다."], ["‘But--but do you think it did Edward good?’", "\"하지만 — 하지만 그게 에드워드한테 도움이 됐다고 생각해요?\""]], [["‘Do you think it did Edward harm, Clara?’ asked Mr. Murdstone, gravely.", "\"그게 에드워드한테 해가 됐다고 생각하오, 클라라?\" 머드스톤 씨가 근엄하게 물었다."]], [["‘That’s the point,’ said his sister.", "\"바로 그게 요점이에요.\" 그의 누이가 말했다."]], [["To this my mother returned, ‘Certainly, my dear Jane,’ and said no more.", "이에 어머니는 \"물론이죠, 제인\" 하고 답하고는 더는 말이 없었다."]], [["I felt apprehensive that I was personally interested in this dialogue, and sought Mr. Murdstone’s eye as it lighted on mine.", "나는 이 대화에 내가 직접 걸려 있는 게 아닐까 불안한 마음이 들어, 내게 와 닿는 머드스톤 씨의 눈을 찾았다."]], [["‘Now, David,’ he said--and I saw that cast again as he said it--‘you must be far more careful today than usual.’ He gave the cane another poise, and another switch; and having finished his preparation of it, laid it down beside him, with an impressive look, and took up his book.", "\"자, 데이비드,\" 그가 말했다 — 그가 그 말을 하면서 또 그 눈초리를 던지는 걸 나는 보았다 — \"오늘은 평소보다 훨씬 더 조심해야 할 거다.\" 그는 회초리를 다시 한 번 가늠하고, 다시 한 번 휙 휘둘렀다. 그렇게 손질을 마치고는, 의미심장한 표정으로 그것을 자기 옆에 내려놓고 책을 집어 들었다."]], [["This was a good freshener to my presence of mind, as a beginning.", "이것은 그 시작으로, 내 정신을 번쩍 들게 하기에 딱 좋았다."], ["I felt the words of my lessons slipping off, not one by one, or line by line, but by the entire page; I tried to lay hold of them; but they seemed, if I may so express it, to have put skates on, and to skim away from me with a smoothness there was no checking.", "나는 공부한 단어들이 하나씩, 또는 한 줄씩이 아니라 통째로 한 쪽씩 미끄러져 달아나는 걸 느꼈다. 붙잡으려 애썼지만, 그것들은, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 스케이트를 신은 듯 거침없이 매끄럽게 내게서 스르르 미끄러져 나가, 도무지 멈출 길이 없었다."]], [["We began badly, and went on worse.", "우리는 출발이 나빴고, 갈수록 더 나빠졌다."], ["I had come in with an idea of distinguishing myself rather, conceiving that I was very well prepared; but it turned out to be quite a mistake.", "나는 채비를 아주 잘해 왔다고 여겨, 오히려 좀 두각을 나타내 보겠다는 생각으로 들어왔지만, 그건 완전한 착각으로 드러났다."], ["Book after book was added to the heap of failures, Miss Murdstone being firmly watchful of us all the time.", "머드스톤 양이 줄곧 우리 모두를 단호히 감시하는 가운데, 책이 한 권 또 한 권 실패의 더미에 보태졌다."], ["And when we came at last to the five thousand cheeses (canes he made it that day, I remember), my mother burst out crying.", "그리고 마침내 그 오천 개의 치즈에 (그날은 회초리로 바꿔 냈던 게 기억난다) 이르렀을 때, 어머니가 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Clara!’ said Miss Murdstone, in her warning voice.", "\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 경고하는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I am not quite well, my dear Jane, I think,’ said my mother.", "\"나 몸이 좀 안 좋은 것 같아요, 제인.\" 어머니가 말했다."]], [["I saw him wink, solemnly, at his sister, as he rose and said, taking up the cane:", "그가 일어서서 회초리를 집어 들며 누이에게 근엄하게 눈을 찡긋하는 걸 나는 보았다."]], [["‘Why, Jane, we can hardly expect Clara to bear, with perfect firmness, the worry and torment that David has occasioned her today.", "\"이런, 제인, 클라라가 데이비드 때문에 오늘 겪은 그 골치와 시달림을, 완벽한 단호함으로 견디기를 바라긴 어렵잖아."], ["That would be stoical. Clara is greatly strengthened and improved, but we can hardly expect so much from her.", "그건 금욕주의자나 할 일이지. 클라라가 무척 굳세지고 나아지긴 했지만, 그렇게까지 바랄 순 없어."], ["David, you and I will go upstairs, boy.’", "데이비드, 너와 나는 위층으로 가자, 이놈아.\""]], [["As he took me out at the door, my mother ran towards us.", "그가 나를 문밖으로 데리고 나가자 어머니가 우리 쪽으로 달려왔다."], ["Miss Murdstone said, ‘Clara!", "머드스톤 양이 \"클라라!"], ["are you a perfect fool?’ and interfered.", "당신 완전 바보예요?\" 하고 말하며 가로막았다."], ["I saw my mother stop her ears then, and I heard her crying.", "그때 어머니가 두 귀를 틀어막는 걸 나는 보았고, 어머니가 우는 소리를 들었다."]], [["He walked me up to my room slowly and gravely--I am certain he had a delight in that formal parade of executing justice--and when we got there, suddenly twisted my head under his arm.", "그는 나를 천천히, 근엄하게 내 방으로 데려갔는데 — 나는 그가 그렇게 격식을 갖춰 정의를 집행하는 행렬을 즐겼다고 확신한다 — 거기 다다르자 별안간 내 머리를 자기 겨드랑이 밑으로 비틀어 처박았다."]], [["‘Mr. Murdstone!", "\"머드스톤 씨! 나리!\""], ["Sir!’ I cried to him. ‘Don’t!", "나는 그에게 외쳤다. \"그러지 마세요!"], ["Pray don’t beat me!", "제발 때리지 마세요!"], ["I have tried to learn, sir, but I can’t learn while you and Miss Murdstone are by.", "저 배우려고 애썼어요, 나리. 하지만 나리와 머드스톤 양이 곁에 있으면 배울 수가 없어요."], ["I can’t indeed!’", "정말 못 배우겠어요!\""]], [["‘Can’t you, indeed, David?’ he said.", "\"정말 못 배우겠다고, 데이비드?\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘We’ll try that.’", "\"어디 한번 보자.\""]], [["He had my head as in a vice, but I twined round him somehow, and stopped him for a moment, entreating him not to beat me.", "그는 내 머리를 죔쇠처럼 꽉 쥐고 있었지만, 나는 어떻게든 그에게 몸을 휘감아 잠시 그를 멈춰 세우고는, 때리지 말아 달라고 애원했다."], ["It was only a moment that I stopped him, for he cut me heavily an instant afterwards, and in the same instant I caught the hand with which he held me in my mouth, between my teeth, and bit it through.", "그를 멈춰 세운 건 단 한순간뿐이었으니, 바로 다음 순간 그가 나를 호되게 내리쳤고, 그와 동시에 나는 그가 나를 붙들고 있던 그 손을 내 입속에 넣고 이로 콱 물어 뚫어 버렸다."], ["It sets my teeth on edge to think of it.", "그걸 생각하면 지금도 이가 다 시리다."]], [["He beat me then, as if he would have beaten me to death.", "그러자 그는 나를 때려죽이기라도 할 듯이 때렸다."], ["Above all the noise we made, I heard them running up the stairs, and crying out--I heard my mother crying out--and Peggotty.", "우리가 내는 그 온갖 소란 너머로, 사람들이 계단을 뛰어 올라오며 소리치는 게 들렸다 — 어머니가 소리치는 게 들렸고 — 패거티도."], ["Then he was gone; and the door was locked outside; and I was lying, fevered and hot, and torn, and sore, and raging in my puny way, upon the floor.", "그러더니 그는 가 버렸고, 문은 밖에서 잠겼고, 나는 열에 들떠 화끈거리고, 찢기고, 쓰라리고, 내 보잘것없는 방식으로 분노에 차서 바닥에 널브러져 있었다."]], [["How well I recollect, when I became quiet, what an unnatural stillness seemed to reign through the whole house! How well I remember, when my smart and passion began to cool, how wicked I began to feel!", "내가 잠잠해졌을 때, 온 집 안에 어찌나 부자연스러운 고요가 감돌던지, 지금도 똑똑히 기억난다! 쓰라림과 격정이 가라앉기 시작하면서, 내가 얼마나 못된 짓을 했다는 기분이 들기 시작했는지, 지금도 똑똑히 기억난다!"]], [["I sat listening for a long while, but there was not a sound.", "나는 오랫동안 앉아서 귀를 기울였지만, 아무 소리도 나지 않았다."], ["I crawled up from the floor, and saw my face in the glass, so swollen, red, and ugly that it almost frightened me.", "나는 바닥에서 기어 올라와 거울에 비친 내 얼굴을 보았는데, 어찌나 퉁퉁 붓고 벌겋고 흉측한지 나 자신조차 거의 겁이 날 지경이었다."], ["My stripes were sore and stiff, and made me cry afresh, when I moved; but they were nothing to the guilt I felt.", "매 맞은 자국들이 쓰라리고 뻣뻣해서, 몸을 움직일 때마다 새삼 울음이 났다. 하지만 그것도 내가 느끼는 죄책감에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."], ["It lay heavier on my breast than if I had been a most atrocious criminal, I dare say.", "그 죄책감은, 감히 말하건대, 내가 세상에서 가장 극악무도한 죄인이었다 한들 그럴까 싶을 만큼 내 가슴을 무겁게 짓눌렀다."]], [["It had begun to grow dark, and I had shut the window (I had been lying, for the most part, with my head upon the sill, by turns crying, dozing, and looking listlessly out), when the key was turned, and Miss Murdstone came in with some bread and meat, and milk.", "날이 어둑해지기 시작해서 나는 창문을 닫았는데 (나는 거의 내내 창턱에 머리를 얹고 누워, 번갈아 가며 울다가 졸다가 멍하니 바깥을 내다보고 있었다), 그때 열쇠가 돌아가더니 머드스톤 양이 빵과 고기 약간, 그리고 우유를 들고 들어왔다."], ["These she put down upon the table without a word, glaring at me the while with exemplary firmness, and then retired, locking the door after her.", "그녀는 모범적인 단호함으로 나를 노려보며 그것들을 말없이 탁자 위에 내려놓고는, 도로 나가서 문을 잠갔다."]], [["Long after it was dark I sat there, wondering whether anybody else would come.", "날이 어두워진 뒤로도 한참을, 나는 거기 앉아 또 누가 오지 않을까 하고 생각했다."], ["When this appeared improbable for that night, I undressed, and went to bed; and, there, I began to wonder fearfully what would be done to me. Whether it was a criminal act that I had committed?", "그날 밤은 그럴 가망이 없어 보이자, 나는 옷을 벗고 잠자리에 들었다. 그러고는 거기서, 내게 무슨 일이 닥칠까 두려워하며 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다."], ["Whether I should be taken into custody, and sent to prison?", "내가 저지른 게 범죄 행위였을까? 나는 체포되어 감옥에 보내질까?"], ["Whether I was at all in danger of being hanged?", "나는 교수형에 처해질 위험이라도 있는 걸까?"]], [["I never shall forget the waking, next morning; the being cheerful and fresh for the first moment, and then the being weighed down by the stale and dismal oppression of remembrance.", "이튿날 아침 잠에서 깨던 일을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 처음 한순간은 명랑하고 상쾌하다가, 이내 케케묵고 음울한 기억의 짓누름에 짓눌리던 그 느낌을."], ["Miss Murdstone reappeared before I was out of bed; told me, in so many words, that I was free to walk in the garden for half an hour and no longer; and retired, leaving the door open, that I might avail myself of that permission.", "머드스톤 양은 내가 침대에서 나오기도 전에 다시 나타나, 똑똑히 말하기를, 정원을 반 시간 동안 거닐어도 좋지만 그 이상은 안 된다고 했다. 그러고는 내가 그 허락을 누릴 수 있도록 문을 열어 둔 채로 나갔다."]], [["I did so, and did so every morning of my imprisonment, which lasted five days.", "나는 그렇게 했고, 닷새간 이어진 내 유폐 생활 동안 아침마다 그렇게 했다."], ["If I could have seen my mother alone, I should have gone down on my knees to her and besought her forgiveness; but I saw no one, Miss Murdstone excepted, during the whole time--except at evening prayers in the parlour; to which I was escorted by Miss Murdstone after everybody else was placed; where I was stationed, a young outlaw, all alone by myself near the door; and whence I was solemnly conducted by my jailer, before any one arose from the devotional posture.", "어머니를 혼자 만날 수만 있었다면, 나는 어머니 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 용서를 빌었을 것이다. 하지만 그 내내 머드스톤 양 말고는 아무도 보지 못했다 — 거실의 저녁 기도 시간만 빼고는. 그 시간엔 다른 사람들이 다 자리를 잡은 뒤에 머드스톤 양이 나를 거기까지 데려다 놓았는데, 나는 어린 무법자가 되어, 문 가까이에 나 혼자만 외따로 세워졌다."], ["I only observed that my mother was as far off from me as she could be, and kept her face another way so that I never saw it; and that Mr. Murdstone’s hand was bound up in a large linen wrapper.", "그리고 누구도 경건한 자세에서 일어나기 전에, 내 간수의 손에 이끌려 엄숙하게 끌려 나왔다. 다만 내가 알아챈 건, 어머니가 나에게서 되도록 멀리 떨어져 있었고, 얼굴을 딴 데로 돌려 내가 끝내 그 얼굴을 보지 못했다는 것, 그리고 머드스톤 씨의 손이 커다란 아마포 붕대로 동여매여 있었다는 것뿐이었다."]], [["The length of those five days I can convey no idea of to any one.", "그 닷새가 얼마나 길었는지, 나는 누구에게도 도무지 짐작시킬 수가 없다. 그것은 내 기억 속에서 여러 해의 자리를 차지하고 있다. 내 귀에 들려오는 집 안의 온갖 일에 내가 귀 기울이던 모습 — 종소리, 문이 열리고 닫히는 소리, 도란거리는 목소리, 계단의 발소리."], ["They occupy the place of years in my remembrance.", "바깥에서 들려오는 웃음소리나 휘파람 소리, 노랫소리는, 외롭고 망신스러운 내게 그 무엇보다 음울하게 느껴졌다."], ["The way in which I listened to all the incidents of the house that made themselves audible to me; the ringing of bells, the opening and shutting of doors, the murmuring of voices, the footsteps on the stairs; to any laughing, whistling, or singing, outside, which seemed more dismal than anything else to me in my solitude and disgrace--the uncertain pace of the hours, especially at night, when I would wake thinking it was morning, and find that the family were not yet gone to bed, and that all the length of night had yet to come--the depressed dreams and nightmares I had--the return of day, noon, afternoon, evening, when the boys played in the churchyard, and I watched them from a distance within the room, being ashamed to show myself at the window lest they should know I was a prisoner--the strange sensation of never hearing myself speak--the fleeting intervals of something like cheerfulness, which came with eating and drinking, and went away with it--the setting in of rain one evening, with a fresh smell, and its coming down faster and faster between me and the church, until it and gathering night seemed to quench me in gloom, and fear, and remorse--all this appears to have gone round and round for years instead of days, it is so vividly and strongly stamped on my remembrance.", "시간이 흐르는 그 종잡을 수 없는 속도 — 특히 밤이면, 아침인 줄 알고 깼다가 식구들이 아직 잠자리에도 들지 않았고 그 긴 밤이 통째로 아직 남았다는 걸 알게 되곤 했다. 내가 꾼 가라앉은 꿈과 악몽들. 낮이, 정오가, 오후가, 저녁이 돌아오던 일, 아이들이 교회 묘지에서 놀 때면 나는 내가 갇혔다는 걸 그 애들이 알까 봐 창가에 모습을 드러내기가 부끄러워서 방 안에서 멀찍이 그 애들을 지켜보았다."], ["On the last night of my restraint, I was awakened by hearing my own name spoken in a whisper.", "내 목소리를 한 번도 들을 일 없다는 그 묘한 느낌. 먹고 마실 때 함께 찾아왔다가 그것과 함께 떠나 버리던, 명랑함 비슷한 것의 덧없는 순간들. 어느 날 저녁 비가 내리기 시작하던 일, 상큼한 냄새와 함께 나와 교회 사이로 점점 더 세차게 쏟아져 내리더니, 마침내 그 비와 짙어 가는 밤이 나를 음울함과 두려움과 회한 속에 잠겨 꺼뜨려 버리는 듯하던 일 — 이 모든 것이 며칠이 아니라 여러 해 동안 빙글빙글 돌아간 것처럼 여겨지니, 그만큼 내 기억에 또렷하고 강렬하게 새겨져 있다."], ["I started up in bed, and putting out my arms in the dark, said:", "갇혀 지내던 마지막 밤, 나는 누군가 내 이름을 속삭이는 소리에 잠에서 깼다. 나는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 어둠 속으로 두 팔을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["‘Is that you, Peggotty?’", "\"너야, 패거티?\""]], [["There was no immediate answer, but presently I heard my name again, in a tone so very mysterious and awful, that I think I should have gone into a fit, if it had not occurred to me that it must have come through the keyhole.", "곧바로 대답은 없었지만, 이내 또 한 번 내 이름이 들렸는데, 어찌나 으스스하고 무시무시한 어조였던지, 그게 분명 열쇠 구멍을 통해 들려온 거라는 생각이 떠오르지 않았다면 나는 발작이라도 일으켰을 것이다."]], [["I groped my way to the door, and putting my own lips to the keyhole, whispered: ‘Is that you, Peggotty dear?’", "나는 더듬더듬 문께로 가서, 내 입술을 열쇠 구멍에 대고 속삭였다. \"너야, 패거티?\""]], [["‘Yes, my own precious Davy,’ she replied.", "\"그래, 내 소중한 데이비.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘Be as soft as a mouse, or the Cat’ll hear us.’", "\"쥐새끼처럼 살살 해, 안 그러면 고양이가 우릴 들을 거야.\""]], [["I understood this to mean Miss Murdstone, and was sensible of the urgency of the case; her room being close by.", "나는 이것이 머드스톤 양을 가리키는 말임을 알아들었고, 사정이 다급하다는 걸 절감했다. 그녀의 방이 바로 옆이었으니까."]], [["‘How’s mama, dear Peggotty?", "\"엄마는 어때, 패거티?"], ["Is she very angry with me?’", "나한테 많이 화났어?\""]], [["I could hear Peggotty crying softly on her side of the keyhole, as I was doing on mine, before she answered. ‘No.", "내가 내 쪽 열쇠 구멍에서 그랬듯, 패거티가 자기 쪽 열쇠 구멍에서 가만가만 우는 소리가 들리더니, 이윽고 그녀가 대답했다. \"아니."], ["Not very.’", "많이는 아냐.\""]], [["‘What is going to be done with me, Peggotty dear?", "\"나 어떻게 되는 거야, 패거티?"], ["Do you know?’", "너 알아?\""]], [["‘School.", "\"학교."], ["Near London,’ was Peggotty’s answer.", "런던 근처.\" 패거티의 대답이었다."], ["I was obliged to get her to repeat it, for she spoke it the first time quite down my throat, in consequence of my having forgotten to take my mouth away from the keyhole and put my ear there; and though her words tickled me a good deal, I didn’t hear them.", "나는 그녀에게 다시 말해 달라고 해야 했는데, 처음엔 그녀가 그걸 곧장 내 목구멍 속으로 말해 버렸기 때문이다. 내가 입을 떼고 귀를 갖다 대는 걸 깜빡한 탓이었다. 그래서 그 말이 무척 간지러웠을망정, 나는 알아듣지 못했다."]], [["‘When, Peggotty?’", "\"언제, 패거티?\""]], [["‘Tomorrow.’", "\"내일.\""]], [["‘Is that the reason why Miss Murdstone took the clothes out of my drawers?’ which she had done, though I have forgotten to mention it.", "\"그래서 머드스톤 양이 내 서랍에서 옷을 죄다 꺼낸 거야?\" 그녀가 그랬는데, 내가 미처 말하는 걸 잊었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Peggotty.", "\"응.\" 패거티가 말했다."], ["‘Box.’", "\"상자에.\""]], [["‘Shan’t I see mama?’", "\"엄마는 못 봐?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Peggotty.", "\"봐.\" 패거티가 말했다."], ["‘Morning.’", "\"아침에.\""]], [["Then Peggotty fitted her mouth close to the keyhole, and delivered these words through it with as much feeling and earnestness as a keyhole has ever been the medium of communicating, I will venture to assert: shooting in each broken little sentence in a convulsive little burst of its own.", "그러고는 패거티가 열쇠 구멍에 입을 바짝 갖다 대고, 이 말들을 그 구멍을 통해 내뱉었는데, 일찍이 어떤 열쇠 구멍이 무언가를 전하는 매개가 되어 본 적이 없으리만치 그렇게 절절하고 진심 어린 마음을 담아서였다고, 나는 감히 단언한다. 끊어진 짤막한 문장 하나하나를 저마다 발작하듯 짧게 터뜨려 가며."]], [["‘Davy, dear. If I ain’t been azackly as intimate with you.", "\"데이비, 얘야. 내가 너랑. 요즘. 예전만큼."], ["Lately, as I used to be.", "가깝게 못 지냈다면."], ["It ain’t because I don’t love you.", "그건 내가 널 사랑하지 않아서가 아니야."], ["Just as well and more, my pretty poppet.", "예전만큼, 아니 그보다 더, 내 어여쁜 강아지야."], ["It’s because I thought it better for you.", "그건 그게 너한테 더 낫다고 생각해서야."], ["And for someone else besides.", "그리고 또 다른 누구한테도."], ["Davy, my darling, are you listening? Can you hear?’", "데이비, 내 사랑, 듣고 있니? 들려?\""]], [["‘Ye-ye-ye-yes, Peggotty!’ I sobbed.", "\"으-으-응, 패거티!\" 나는 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘My own!’ said Peggotty, with infinite compassion.", "\"내 새끼!\" 패거티가 한없는 연민을 담아 말했다."], ["‘What I want to say, is.", "\"내가 하고 싶은 말은."], ["That you must never forget me.", "넌 날 절대 잊으면 안 된다는 거야."], ["For I’ll never forget you.", "나도 절대 널 안 잊을 테니까."], ["And I’ll take as much care of your mama, Davy.", "그리고 데이비, 내가 네 엄마를 정성껏 보살필게."], ["As ever I took of you.", "너를 보살핀 만큼이나."], ["And I won’t leave her.", "절대 곁을 안 떠날 거야."], ["The day may come when she’ll be glad to lay her poor head. On her stupid, cross old Peggotty’s arm again.", "언젠가 네 엄마가 그 가엾은 머리를. 다시 멍청하고 까탈스러운 늙은 패거티의 팔에 기대고 싶어 할 날이 올지도 몰라."], ["And I’ll write to you, my dear.", "그리고 네게 편지할게, 얘야."], ["Though I ain’t no scholar.", "내가 배운 건 없지만."], ["And I’ll--I’ll--’ Peggotty fell to kissing the keyhole, as she couldn’t kiss me.", "그리고 난, 난—\" 패거티는 나에게 입맞출 수 없으니 열쇠 구멍에 입을 맞추기 시작했다."]], [["‘Thank you, dear Peggotty!’ said I.", "\"고마워, 패거티!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Oh, thank you! Thank you!", "\"오, 고마워! 고마워!"], ["Will you promise me one thing, Peggotty?", "한 가지만 약속해 줄래, 패거티?"], ["Will you write and tell Mr. Peggotty and little Em’ly, and Mrs. Gummidge and Ham, that I am not so bad as they might suppose, and that I sent ‘em all my love--especially to little Em’ly?", "패거티 아저씨랑 어린 에밀리한테, 그리고 거미지 부인이랑 햄한테 편지해서, 내가 그분들이 생각하실 만큼 그렇게 나쁜 애가 아니라고, 모두에게 내 사랑을 전한다고 — 특히 어린 에밀리한테 — 그렇게 써 줄래?"], ["Will you, if you please, Peggotty?’", "그래 줄래, 패거티, 부탁이야?\""]], [["The kind soul promised, and we both of us kissed the keyhole with the greatest affection--I patted it with my hand, I recollect, as if it had been her honest face--and parted.", "마음씨 고운 그 사람은 약속해 주었고, 우리 둘은 더없이 다정하게 열쇠 구멍에 입을 맞추었다 — 나는 그것이 그녀의 정직한 얼굴이기라도 한 듯 손으로 토닥였던 기억이 난다 — 그리고 헤어졌다."], ["From that night there grew up in my breast a feeling for Peggotty which I cannot very well define.", "그날 밤부터 내 가슴속에는 패거티를 향한, 무어라 잘 정의할 수 없는 감정이 자라났다."], ["She did not replace my mother; no one could do that; but she came into a vacancy in my heart, which closed upon her, and I felt towards her something I have never felt for any other human being.", "그녀가 어머니를 대신한 건 아니었다. 누구도 그럴 순 없었다. 하지만 그녀는 내 마음속 어떤 빈자리로 들어와 그 자리를 채우며 닫혔고, 나는 그녀에게 다른 어떤 인간에게도 느껴 본 적 없는 무언가를 느꼈다."], ["It was a sort of comical affection, too; and yet if she had died, I cannot think what I should have done, or how I should have acted out the tragedy it would have been to me.", "그것은 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 정이기도 했다. 그러면서도 만약 그녀가 죽었다면, 나는 내가 어떻게 했을지, 그것이 내게 되었을 그 비극을 어떻게 견뎌 냈을지 도무지 모르겠다."]], [["In the morning Miss Murdstone appeared as usual, and told me I was going to school; which was not altogether such news to me as she supposed.", "아침에 머드스톤 양이 여느 때처럼 나타나, 내가 학교에 가게 됐다고 말했는데, 그녀가 짐작한 것만큼 내게 전혀 새로운 소식은 아니었다."], ["She also informed me that when I was dressed, I was to come downstairs into the parlour, and have my breakfast.", "그녀는 또, 옷을 다 입거든 아래층 거실로 내려와 아침을 먹으라고 일러 주었다."], ["There, I found my mother, very pale and with red eyes: into whose arms I ran, and begged her pardon from my suffering soul.", "거기서 나는 몹시 창백하고 눈이 빨개진 어머니를 보았다. 나는 그 품으로 달려들어, 괴로워하는 내 영혼을 다해 용서를 빌었다."]], [["‘Oh, Davy!’ she said.", "\"오, 데이비!\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘That you could hurt anyone I love!", "\"어떻게 내가 사랑하는 사람을 다치게 할 수가 있니!"], ["Try to be better, pray to be better!", "더 착해지려고 애써 다오, 더 착해지게 해 달라고 기도해 다오!"], ["I forgive you; but I am so grieved, Davy, that you should have such bad passions in your heart.’", "너를 용서하마. 하지만, 데이비, 네 마음속에 그런 못된 격정이 있다는 게 나는 너무도 슬프구나.\""]], [["They had persuaded her that I was a wicked fellow, and she was more sorry for that than for my going away.", "그들은 어머니에게 내가 못된 녀석이라고 믿게 만들어 놓았고, 어머니는 내가 떠나는 것보다 그 일을 더 슬퍼했다."], ["I felt it sorely. I tried to eat my parting breakfast, but my tears dropped upon my bread-and-butter, and trickled into my tea.", "나는 그게 사무치게 아팠다. 작별의 아침밥을 먹어 보려 했지만, 눈물이 버터 바른 빵 위로 뚝뚝 떨어지고 찻잔 속으로 주르르 흘러내렸다."], ["I saw my mother look at me sometimes, and then glance at the watchful Miss Murdstone, and than look down, or look away.", "어머니가 가끔 나를 바라보다가, 감시하는 머드스톤 양을 흘끔 보고는, 고개를 떨구거나 눈길을 딴 데로 돌리는 걸 나는 보았다."]], [["‘Master Copperfield’s box there!’ said Miss Murdstone, when wheels were heard at the gate.", "\"코퍼필드 도련님 상자 저기 있어요!\" 대문께에서 바퀴 소리가 들리자 머드스톤 양이 말했다."]], [["I looked for Peggotty, but it was not she; neither she nor Mr. Murdstone appeared.", "나는 패거티를 찾았지만, 그녀가 아니었다. 그녀도 머드스톤 씨도 나타나지 않았다."], ["My former acquaintance, the carrier, was at the door.", "내 옛 지인인 짐마차꾼이 문 앞에 와 있었다."], ["The box was taken out to his cart, and lifted in.", "상자가 그의 수레로 옮겨져 실렸다."]], [["‘Clara!’ said Miss Murdstone, in her warning note.", "\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 경고하는 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘Ready, my dear Jane,’ returned my mother.", "\"준비됐어요, 제인.\" 어머니가 대답했다."], ["‘Good-bye, Davy. You are going for your own good.", "\"잘 가렴, 데이비. 네가 잘되라고 가는 거야."], ["Good-bye, my child.", "잘 가, 내 아가."], ["You will come home in the holidays, and be a better boy.’", "방학 때 집에 와서, 더 착한 아이가 되어 다오.\""]], [["‘Clara!’ Miss Murdstone repeated.", "\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Certainly, my dear Jane,’ replied my mother, who was holding me.", "\"그럼요, 제인.\" 나를 안고 있던 어머니가 대답했다."], ["‘I forgive you, my dear boy. God bless you!’", "\"널 용서한다, 내 사랑하는 아들아. 하느님이 너를 지켜 주시길!\""]], [["‘Clara!’ Miss Murdstone repeated.", "\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 되풀이했다."]], [["Miss Murdstone was good enough to take me out to the cart, and to say on the way that she hoped I would repent, before I came to a bad end; and then I got into the cart, and the lazy horse walked off with it.", "머드스톤 양은 친절하게도 나를 수레까지 데려다주었고, 가는 길에, 내가 비참한 끝장을 보기 전에 부디 뉘우치길 바란다고 말했다. 그러고 나서 나는 수레에 올랐고, 게으른 말이 그걸 끌고 터덜터덜 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER 5. I AM SENT AWAY FROM HOME", "# CHAPTER 5. I AM SENT AWAY FROM HOME"]], [["We might have gone about half a mile, and my pocket-handkerchief was quite wet through, when the carrier stopped short.", "우리가 한 반 마일쯤 갔을 무렵, 내 손수건은 흠뻑 젖어 있었는데, 그때 마부가 갑자기 멈춰 섰다."], ["Looking out to ascertain for what, I saw, to My amazement, Peggotty burst from a hedge and climb into the cart.", "무슨 일인가 싶어 밖을 내다보니, 놀랍게도 패거티가 산울타리 사이에서 불쑥 튀어나와 마차로 기어 올라왔다."], ["She took me in both her arms, and squeezed me to her stays until the pressure on my nose was extremely painful, though I never thought of that till afterwards when I found it very tender.", "패거티는 두 팔로 나를 끌어안고 자기 코르셋에 어찌나 꽉 눌러 댔는지 코가 몹시 아팠는데, 정작 그것을 알아챈 건 나중에 코끝이 아릿한 걸 느꼈을 때였다."], ["Not a single word did Peggotty speak.", "패거티는 단 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Releasing one of her arms, she put it down in her pocket to the elbow, and brought out some paper bags of cakes which she crammed into my pockets, and a purse which she put into my hand, but not one word did she say.", "한쪽 팔을 풀더니 팔꿈치까지 주머니에 깊숙이 찔러 넣어 종이봉지에 든 과자 몇 개를 꺼내 내 주머니에 가득 쑤셔 넣고, 지갑 하나를 내 손에 쥐여 주었지만, 그러는 내내 한마디도 없었다."], ["After another and a final squeeze with both arms, she got down from the cart and ran away; and, my belief is, and has always been, without a solitary button on her gown.", "두 팔로 마지막으로 한 번 더 꽉 안아 주고는 마차에서 내려 달아났는데, 내 믿음으로는, 그리고 줄곧 그렇게 믿어 온 바로는, 그때 패거티 옷에는 단추가 단 한 개도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["I picked up one, of several that were rolling about, and treasured it as a keepsake for a long time.", "여기저기 굴러다니던 여러 단추 가운데 하나를 주웠는데, 나는 그것을 오래도록 기념물로 소중히 간직했다."]], [["The carrier looked at me, as if to inquire if she were coming back.", "마부는 패거티가 다시 올 건지 묻기라도 하듯 나를 바라보았다."], ["I shook my head, and said I thought not.", "나는 고개를 저으며 안 올 것 같다고 말했다."], ["‘Then come up,’ said the carrier to the lazy horse; who came up accordingly.", "\"그럼 가자.\" 마부가 게으른 말에게 말했다. 그러자 말은 그 말대로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Having by this time cried as much as I possibly could, I began to think it was of no use crying any more, especially as neither Roderick Random, nor that Captain in the Royal British Navy, had ever cried, that I could remember, in trying situations.", "이쯤 되니 울 수 있는 만큼 다 울고 나서, 더 울어 봐야 아무 소용없다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 더구나 로더릭 랜덤도, 영국 왕립 해군의 그 함장도, 내 기억에 따르면 어려운 처지에 놓여서 운 적이 한 번도 없었으니 말이다."], ["The carrier, seeing me in this resolution, proposed that my pocket-handkerchief should be spread upon the horse’s back to dry.", "마부는 내가 이렇게 마음을 다잡은 것을 보고는, 손수건을 말리게 말 등에 펴 놓자고 했다."], ["I thanked him, and assented; and particularly small it looked, under those circumstances.", "나는 고맙다고 하고 그러자고 했다. 그렇게 펼쳐 놓고 보니 손수건은 유난히 작아 보였다."]], [["I had now leisure to examine the purse.", "이제 지갑을 살펴볼 여유가 생겼다."], ["It was a stiff leather purse, with a snap, and had three bright shillings in it, which Peggotty had evidently polished up with whitening, for my greater delight.", "그것은 똑딱단추가 달린 빳빳한 가죽 지갑이었는데, 안에는 반짝이는 일 실링짜리 동전 세 개가 들어 있었다. 패거티가 나를 더 기쁘게 해 주려고 백묵 가루로 닦아 윤을 낸 게 분명했다."], ["But its most precious contents were two half-crowns folded together in a bit of paper, on which was written, in my mother’s hand, ‘For Davy.", "그러나 그 안에서 가장 값진 것은 종잇조각에 함께 접혀 있던 반 크라운 동전 두 개였는데, 그 종이에는 어머니의 글씨로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"데이비에게."], ["With my love.’ I was so overcome by this, that I asked the carrier to be so good as to reach me my pocket-handkerchief again; but he said he thought I had better do without it, and I thought I really had, so I wiped my eyes on my sleeve and stopped myself.", "사랑을 담아.\" 나는 이것에 너무 북받쳐서, 마부에게 미안하지만 손수건을 다시 건네 달라고 부탁했다. 그러나 마부는 그냥 손수건 없이 견디는 게 낫겠다고 했고, 나도 정말 그게 낫겠다 싶어서, 소매로 눈을 닦고 울음을 그쳤다."]], [["For good, too; though, in consequence of my previous emotions, I was still occasionally seized with a stormy sob.", "그것도 아주 그쳤다. 다만 앞서 북받친 감정 탓에, 이따금 폭풍 같은 흐느낌이 불쑥 솟구치곤 했다."], ["After we had jogged on for some little time, I asked the carrier if he was going all the way.", "한동안 덜컹덜컹 달리고 나서, 나는 마부에게 끝까지 가느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘All the way where?’ inquired the carrier.", "\"끝까지 어디?\" 마부가 물었다."]], [["‘There,’ I said.", "\"거기요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Where’s there?’ inquired the carrier.", "\"거기가 어딘데?\" 마부가 물었다."]], [["‘Near London,’ I said.", "\"런던 근처요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Why that horse,’ said the carrier, jerking the rein to point him out, ‘would be deader than pork afore he got over half the ground.’", "\"아니, 이 말로는,\" 마부가 말을 가리키려고 고삐를 홱 당기며 말했다. \"절반도 못 가서 돼지고기보다 더 죽어 버릴걸.\""]], [["‘Are you only going to Yarmouth then?’ I asked.", "\"그럼 야머스까지만 가시는 거예요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘That’s about it,’ said the carrier.", "\"그쯤이지.\" 마부가 말했다."], ["‘And there I shall take you to the stage-cutch, and the stage-cutch that’ll take you to--wherever it is.’", "\"거기 가서 너를 역마차에 태워 줄 거고, 그 역마차가 너를 데려다줄 거다 — 거기가 어디든 말이야.\""]], [["As this was a great deal for the carrier (whose name was Mr. Barkis) to say--he being, as I observed in a former chapter, of a phlegmatic temperament, and not at all conversational--I offered him a cake as a mark of attention, which he ate at one gulp, exactly like an elephant, and which made no more impression on his big face than it would have done on an elephant’s.", "이건 마부치고는 (그의 이름은 바키스 씨였다) 엄청나게 많은 말이었는데 — 앞 장에서 말했듯이 그는 무던하고 굼뜬 성미라 도무지 말수가 없는 사람이었으니 — 나는 정성의 표시로 과자 하나를 건넸고, 그는 그것을 영락없이 코끼리처럼 한입에 꿀꺽 삼켰는데, 그 큰 얼굴에 남긴 인상은 코끼리 얼굴에 남겼을 정도밖에 안 되었다."]], [["‘Did SHE make ‘em, now?’ said Mr.", "\"이거, 그 여자가 만든 거냐?\""], ["Barkis, always leaning forward, in his slouching way, on the footboard of the cart with an arm on each knee.", "바키스 씨가 늘 그러듯 구부정한 자세로 마차 발판 위로 몸을 앞으로 기울이며, 두 무릎에 한쪽씩 팔을 얹고 물었다."]], [["‘Peggotty, do you mean, sir?’", "\"패거티 말씀이세요, 아저씨?\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"그래!\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Her.’", "\"그 여자.\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["She makes all our pastry, and does all our cooking.’", "우리 집 빵이며 과자는 죄다 그 사람이 만들고, 요리도 다 해요.\""]], [["‘Do she though?’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"그래, 정말?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."], ["He made up his mouth as if to whistle, but he didn’t whistle.", "그는 휘파람이라도 불 것처럼 입을 모았지만, 휘파람을 불지는 않았다."], ["He sat looking at the horse’s ears, as if he saw something new there; and sat so, for a considerable time. By and by, he said:", "그러고는 말의 두 귀에서 무슨 새로운 거라도 보이는 듯이 그것을 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 한참을 그렇게 앉아 있다가, 이윽고 그가 말했다."]], [["‘No sweethearts, I b’lieve?’", "\"애인은 없겠지, 아마?\""]], [["‘Sweetmeats did you say, Mr.", "\"단것 말인가요, 바키스 아저씨?\""], ["Barkis?’ For I thought he wanted something else to eat, and had pointedly alluded to that description of refreshment.", "나는 그가 뭔가 더 먹을 것을 원하면서, 그 종류의 군것질거리를 콕 집어 넌지시 말하는 거라고 생각했다. * 영어로 'sweethearts(애인)'와 'sweetmeats(단것)'의 발음이 비슷해서 데이비드가 잘못 알아들은 것이다."]], [["‘Hearts,’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"애인 말이야.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Sweet hearts; no person walks with her!’", "\"애인 말이다. 그 여자랑 같이 다니는 사람이 없느냔 말이야!\""]], [["‘With Peggotty?’", "\"패거티하고요?\""]], [["‘Ah!’ he said.", "\"그래!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Her.’", "\"그 여자.\""]], [["‘Oh, no.", "\"아, 없어요."], ["She never had a sweetheart.’", "그 사람은 애인을 가져 본 적이 한 번도 없어요.\""]], [["‘Didn’t she, though!’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"그래, 정말 없어!\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["Again he made up his mouth to whistle, and again he didn’t whistle, but sat looking at the horse’s ears.", "그는 다시 휘파람을 불려고 입을 모았지만, 또 휘파람을 불지 않고, 말의 두 귀만 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘So she makes,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서 그 여자가,\""], ["Barkis, after a long interval of reflection, ‘all the apple parsties, and doos all the cooking, do she?’", "바키스 씨가 한참 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"사과 파이를 다 만들고, 요리도 다 한단 말이지, 그렇지?\""]], [["I replied that such was the fact.", "나는 정말 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Well.", "\"흠."], ["I’ll tell you what,’ said Mr. Barkis.", "그럼 내 말 들어 봐라.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘P’raps you might be writin’ to her?’", "\"혹시 너 그 여자한테 편지를 쓸지도 모르겠구나?\""]], [["‘I shall certainly write to her,’ I rejoined.", "\"그럼요, 꼭 쓸 거예요.\" 내가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ he said, slowly turning his eyes towards me. ‘Well!", "\"그래!\" 그가 천천히 나를 향해 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"그럼!"], ["If you was writin’ to her, p’raps you’d recollect to say that Barkis was willin’; would you?’", "혹시 그 여자한테 편지를 쓰거든, 바키스가 마음 있다고 잊지 말고 전해 줄 수 있겠냐?\""]], [["‘That Barkis is willing,’ I repeated, innocently.", "\"바키스가 마음 있다고요.\" 내가 천진하게 따라 말했다."], ["‘Is that all the message?’", "\"그게 전할 말 전부예요?\""]], [["‘Ye-es,’ he said, considering. ‘Ye-es.", "\"그래—.\" 그가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"그래—."], ["Barkis is willin’.’", "바키스가 마음 있다고.\""]], [["‘But you will be at Blunderstone again tomorrow, Mr.", "\"하지만 아저씨, 내일이면 다시 블런더스톤에 가시잖아요.\""], ["Barkis,’ I said, faltering a little at the idea of my being far away from it then, and could give your own message so much better.’", "그때쯤이면 나는 거기서 멀리 떨어져 있을 거라는 생각에 목소리가 조금 떨렸지만 나는 말했다. \"그럼 직접 전하시는 게 훨씬 더 나을 텐데요.\""]], [["As he repudiated this suggestion, however, with a jerk of his head, and once more confirmed his previous request by saying, with profound gravity, ‘Barkis is willin’. That’s the message,’ I readily undertook its transmission.", "그러나 그가 고개를 홱 저어 이 제안을 물리치고, 더없이 진지한 얼굴로 \"바키스가 마음 있다고. 그게 전할 말이야\"라고 말하며 앞서 한 부탁을 다시 한번 못 박자, 나는 기꺼이 전해 주겠노라 떠맡았다."], ["While I was waiting for the coach in the hotel at Yarmouth that very afternoon, I procured a sheet of paper and an inkstand, and wrote a note to Peggotty, which ran thus: ‘My dear Peggotty.", "바로 그날 오후 야머스의 여관에서 역마차를 기다리는 동안, 나는 종이 한 장과 잉크병을 구해서 패거티에게 짤막한 편지를 썼는데, 내용은 이러했다. \"사랑하는 패거티에게. 나는 여기 무사히 도착했어."], ["I have come here safe.", "바키스가 마음 있대."], ["Barkis is willing. My love to mama.", "엄마한테 사랑한다고 전해 줘."], ["Yours affectionately. P.S.", "사랑을 담아. 추신."], ["He says he particularly wants you to know--BARKIS IS WILLING.’", "바키스 아저씨가 특히 이걸 꼭 알려 달래 — 바키스가 마음 있대.\""]], [["When I had taken this commission on myself prospectively, Mr. Barkis relapsed into perfect silence; and I, feeling quite worn out by all that had happened lately, lay down on a sack in the cart and fell asleep.", "이 심부름을 앞으로 떠맡기로 하고 나자, 바키스 씨는 완전한 침묵으로 다시 빠져들었고, 나는 요즘 일어난 온갖 일로 완전히 녹초가 되어, 마차 안 자루 위에 드러누워 잠들어 버렸다."], ["I slept soundly until we got to Yarmouth; which was so entirely new and strange to me in the inn-yard to which we drove, that I at once abandoned a latent hope I had had of meeting with some of Mr. Peggotty’s family there, perhaps even with little Em’ly herself.", "야머스에 다다를 때까지 나는 푹 잤다. 마차가 들어선 여관 마당은 나에게 어찌나 온통 낯설고 새로웠던지, 거기서 혹시 패거티 씨네 식구 누군가를, 어쩌면 어린 에밀리까지 만날지도 모른다고 남몰래 품었던 희망을 나는 단번에 접어 버렸다."]], [["The coach was in the yard, shining very much all over, but without any horses to it as yet; and it looked in that state as if nothing was more unlikely than its ever going to London.", "역마차는 마당에 있었는데, 온통 번쩍번쩍 빛났으나 아직 말이 매여 있지 않았다. 그 꼴을 보니 이 마차가 런던으로 간다는 게 도무지 있을 법하지 않은 일처럼 보였다."], ["I was thinking this, and wondering what would ultimately become of my box, which Mr. Barkis had put down on the yard-pavement by the pole (he having driven up the yard to turn his cart), and also what would ultimately become of me, when a lady looked out of a bow-window where some fowls and joints of meat were hanging up, and said:", "내가 이런 생각에 잠겨, 바키스 씨가 (마차를 돌리려고 마당 안으로 몰고 들어온 터라) 마당 포석 위 끌채 옆에 내려놓은 내 상자가 결국 어찌 될까, 또 나는 결국 어찌 될까 궁금해하고 있을 때, 닭이며 고깃덩이가 걸려 있는 활처럼 굽은 창문에서 한 부인이 내다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Is that the little gentleman from Blunderstone?’", "\"블런더스톤에서 온 꼬마 신사가 너냐?\""]], [["‘Yes, ma’am,’ I said.", "\"네, 부인.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘What name?’ inquired the lady.", "\"이름이 뭐냐?\" 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘Copperfield, ma’am,’ I said.", "\"코퍼필드예요, 부인.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘That won’t do,’ returned the lady.", "\"그건 안 되는데.\" 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["‘Nobody’s dinner is paid for here, in that name.’", "\"그런 이름으로는 여기 점심값을 치른 사람이 없어.\""]], [["‘Is it Murdstone, ma’am?’ I said.", "\"머드스톤 아닐까요, 부인?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘If you’re Master Murdstone,’ said the lady, ‘why do you go and give another name, first?’", "\"네가 머드스톤 도련님이라면,\" 부인이 말했다. \"왜 처음에 딴 이름을 대고 그러느냐?\""]], [["I explained to the lady how it was, who than rang a bell, and called out, ‘William!", "나는 어찌 된 일인지 부인에게 설명했고, 부인은 종을 울리며 외쳤다. \"윌리엄!"], ["show the coffee-room!’ upon which a waiter came running out of a kitchen on the opposite side of the yard to show it, and seemed a good deal surprised when he was only to show it to me.", "커피실로 안내해!\" 그러자 마당 맞은편 부엌에서 웨이터 한 명이 안내하러 뛰어나왔는데, 안내할 상대가 고작 나뿐이라는 것을 알고는 적잖이 놀란 눈치였다."]], [["It was a large long room with some large maps in it.", "그곳은 커다란 지도 몇 장이 걸린, 길고 널찍한 방이었다."], ["I doubt if I could have felt much stranger if the maps had been real foreign countries, and I cast away in the middle of them.", "만약 그 지도들이 진짜 외국 땅이고 내가 그 한복판에 내던져진 거라 해도, 그보다 더 낯설지는 않았을 성싶다."], ["I felt it was taking a liberty to sit down, with my cap in my hand, on the corner of the chair nearest the door; and when the waiter laid a cloth on purpose for me, and put a set of castors on it, I think I must have turned red all over with modesty.", "모자를 손에 든 채, 문에서 가장 가까운 의자 귀퉁이에 앉는 것조차 내겐 외람된 짓 같았다. 그래서 웨이터가 일부러 나를 위해 식탁보를 깔고 그 위에 양념병 한 벌을 놓았을 때, 나는 부끄러움에 온몸이 새빨개졌을 것이다."]], [["He brought me some chops, and vegetables, and took the covers off in such a bouncing manner that I was afraid I must have given him some offence.", "웨이터가 양고기 토막과 채소를 가져와서 어찌나 거칠게 덮개를 홱 벗기던지, 나는 내가 그의 비위를 거스른 건 아닌가 겁이 났다."], ["But he greatly relieved my mind by putting a chair for me at the table, and saying, very affably, ‘Now, six-foot! come on!’", "하지만 그가 식탁에 내 의자를 놓아 주며 아주 싹싹하게 \"자, 키다리 양반! 어서!\" 하고 말하자, 내 마음은 한결 놓였다."]], [["I thanked him, and took my seat at the board; but found it extremely difficult to handle my knife and fork with anything like dexterity, or to avoid splashing myself with the gravy, while he was standing opposite, staring so hard, and making me blush in the most dreadful manner every time I caught his eye.", "* 키다리 양반(six-foot): 키가 6피트나 되는 어른을 부르듯 농담조로 어린 데이비드를 부른 것이다. 나는 고맙다고 하고 식탁에 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 그가 맞은편에 서서 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지, 그리고 그와 눈이 마주칠 때마다 끔찍하리만치 얼굴이 빨개지던지, 나이프와 포크를 솜씨 있게 다루기도, 옷에 고기 국물을 튀기지 않기도 몹시 힘들었다."], ["After watching me into the second chop, he said:", "그는 내가 두 번째 양고기 토막을 먹기 시작할 때까지 지켜보다가 말했다."]], [["‘There’s half a pint of ale for you.", "\"맥주 반 파인트 시켜 둔 게 있어요."], ["Will you have it now?’", "지금 드실래요?\""]], [["I thanked him and said, ‘Yes.’ Upon which he poured it out of a jug into a large tumbler, and held it up against the light, and made it look beautiful.", "나는 고맙다고 하고 \"네\"라고 말했다. 그러자 그는 주전자에서 큼직한 잔에 맥주를 따라, 빛에 비춰 들어 보이며 그것을 근사하게 보이도록 만들었다."]], [["‘My eye!’ he said.", "\"이야!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘It seems a good deal, don’t it?’", "\"꽤 많아 보이죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘It does seem a good deal,’ I answered with a smile.", "\"정말 많아 보이네요.\" 나는 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["For it was quite delightful to me, to find him so pleasant.", "그가 이렇게 싹싹하게 구는 게 내겐 무척 기뻤다."], ["He was a twinkling-eyed, pimple-faced man, with his hair standing upright all over his head; and as he stood with one arm a-kimbo, holding up the glass to the light with the other hand, he looked quite friendly.", "그는 눈을 반짝거리고 얼굴에 여드름이 난 사내였는데, 머리카락이 온통 곤두서 있었다. 한 손은 옆구리에 척 얹고 다른 손으로 잔을 빛에 비춰 든 채 서 있는 그 모습은 자못 다정해 보였다."]], [["‘There was a gentleman here, yesterday,’ he said--‘a stout gentleman, by the name of Topsawyer--perhaps you know him?’", "\"어제 여기 한 신사가 왔었거든요.\" 그가 말했다. \"톱소여라는 이름의 뚱뚱한 신사인데 — 혹시 아세요?\""]], [["‘No,’ I said, ‘I don’t think--’", "\"아니요.\" 내가 말했다. \"잘 모르겠는—\""]], [["‘In breeches and gaiters, broad-brimmed hat, grey coat, speckled choker,’ said the waiter.", "\"반바지에 각반을 차고, 챙 넓은 모자에, 잿빛 외투, 얼룩무늬 목도리를 했어요.\" 웨이터가 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ I said bashfully, ‘I haven’t the pleasure--’", "\"아니요.\" 내가 수줍게 말했다. \"그런 분은 영광스럽게도 알지 못—\""]], [["‘He came in here,’ said the waiter, looking at the light through the tumbler, ‘ordered a glass of this ale--WOULD order it--I told him not--drank it, and fell dead.", "\"그분이 여기 들어와서,\" 웨이터가 잔 너머로 빛을 바라보며 말했다. \"이 맥주를 한 잔 시켰어요 — 기어이 시키더라고요 — 제가 그러지 말라고 했는데도 — 마셨고, 그대로 푹 쓰러져 죽었지 뭐예요."], ["It was too old for him.", "그분한테는 너무 독한 거였어요."], ["It oughtn’t to be drawn; that’s the fact.’", "따라서는 안 되는 거였죠. 사실이 그래요.\""]], [["I was very much shocked to hear of this melancholy accident, and said I thought I had better have some water.", "나는 이 서글픈 사고 이야기에 몹시 충격을 받아, 그냥 물이나 좀 마시는 게 낫겠다고 말했다."]], [["‘Why you see,’ said the waiter, still looking at the light through the tumbler, with one of his eyes shut up, ‘our people don’t like things being ordered and left.", "\"그게 말이죠,\" 웨이터가 한쪽 눈을 찡그린 채 여전히 잔 너머로 빛을 바라보며 말했다. \"여기 사람들은 시켜 놓고 안 마시는 걸 안 좋아하거든요."], ["It offends ‘em.", "기분 나빠해요."], ["But I’ll drink it, if you like.", "하지만 원하신다면 제가 마실게요."], ["I’m used to it, and use is everything.", "저는 익숙하니까요, 익숙한 게 전부죠."], ["I don’t think it’ll hurt me, if I throw my head back, and take it off quick.", "머리를 뒤로 젖히고 단숨에 들이켜면 저한테는 별 탈 없을 거예요."], ["Shall I?’", "그럴까요?\""]], [["I replied that he would much oblige me by drinking it, if he thought he could do it safely, but by no means otherwise.", "나는 안전하게 마실 수 있다고 생각한다면 그렇게 해 주면 정말 고맙겠지만, 그렇지 않다면 절대 안 된다고 대답했다."], ["When he did throw his head back, and take it off quick, I had a horrible fear, I confess, of seeing him meet the fate of the lamented Mr. Topsawyer, and fall lifeless on the carpet.", "그가 정말로 머리를 뒤로 젖히고 단숨에 들이켤 때, 솔직히 고백하건대, 나는 그가 애통한 톱소여 씨와 똑같은 운명을 맞아 양탄자 위에 푹 고꾸라져 죽지나 않을까 끔찍하게 겁이 났다."], ["But it didn’t hurt him. On the contrary, I thought he seemed the fresher for it.", "하지만 그에게는 아무 탈이 없었다. 오히려 그는 그걸 마시고 한결 생기가 도는 것 같았다."]], [["‘What have we got here?’ he said, putting a fork into my dish.", "\"여기 이건 뭐죠?\" 그가 내 접시에 포크를 찔러 넣으며 말했다."], ["‘Not chops?’\n‘Chops,’ I said.", "\"양고기 토막 아니에요?\""]], [["‘Lord bless my soul!’ he exclaimed, ‘I didn’t know they were chops.", "\"양고기 토막이에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"아이고, 맙소사!\""], ["Why, a chop’s the very thing to take off the bad effects of that beer!", "그가 외쳤다. \"양고기 토막인 줄 몰랐네요. 아니, 양고기 토막이야말로 그 맥주의 나쁜 기운을 가시게 해 주는 바로 그거잖아요!"], ["Ain’t it lucky?’", "운이 좋지 않아요?\""]], [["So he took a chop by the bone in one hand, and a potato in the other, and ate away with a very good appetite, to my extreme satisfaction.", "그러더니 그는 한 손에 뼈를 잡아 양고기 토막 하나를 들고, 다른 손에 감자 하나를 들고, 아주 맛있게 먹어 치웠는데, 그게 나는 더없이 흐뭇했다."], ["He afterwards took another chop, and another potato; and after that, another chop and another potato.", "그는 뒤이어 또 양고기 토막 하나에 감자 하나를 먹고, 그러고 나서 또 양고기 토막 하나에 감자 하나를 먹었다."], ["When we had done, he brought me a pudding, and having set it before me, seemed to ruminate, and to become absent in his mind for some moments.", "다 먹고 나자 그는 나에게 푸딩을 가져왔는데, 그것을 내 앞에 놓고는 무슨 생각에 잠긴 듯, 잠시 멍하니 넋이 나간 것 같았다."]], [["‘How’s the pie?’ he said, rousing himself.", "\"파이 맛이 어때요?\" 그가 정신을 차리며 말했다."]], [["‘It’s a pudding,’ I made answer.", "\"푸딩인데요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Pudding!’ he exclaimed.", "\"푸딩!\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["‘Why, bless me, so it is!", "\"아니, 세상에, 정말 그러네! 이런!\""], ["What!’ looking at it nearer.", "그가 더 가까이 들여다보았다."], ["‘You don’t mean to say it’s a batter-pudding!’", "\"이거 설마 반죽 푸딩이라는 건 아니겠죠!\""]], [["‘Yes, it is indeed.’", "\"네, 정말 그래요.\""]], [["‘Why, a batter-pudding,’ he said, taking up a table-spoon, ‘is my favourite pudding!", "\"아니, 반죽 푸딩은,\" 그가 큰 숟가락을 집어 들며 말했다. \"제가 제일 좋아하는 푸딩이라고요!"], ["Ain’t that lucky?", "운이 좋지 않아요?"], ["Come on, little ‘un, and let’s see who’ll get most.’", "자, 꼬마 친구, 누가 더 많이 먹나 한번 봅시다.\""]], [["The waiter certainly got most. He entreated me more than once to come in and win, but what with his table-spoon to my tea-spoon, his dispatch to my dispatch, and his appetite to my appetite, I was left far behind at the first mouthful, and had no chance with him.", "웨이터가 더 많이 먹은 건 두말할 나위가 없었다. 그는 한 번 이상 나더러 어서 덤벼서 이겨 보라고 부추겼지만, 그의 큰 숟가락에 내 찻숟가락, 그의 잽싼 손놀림에 내 손놀림, 그의 식욕에 내 식욕으로는, 첫 한입에서 이미 한참 뒤처져 그를 당해 낼 재간이 없었다."], ["I never saw anyone enjoy a pudding so much, I think; and he laughed, when it was all gone, as if his enjoyment of it lasted still.", "푸딩을 그렇게 맛있게 먹는 사람은 그때껏 본 적이 없는 것 같다. 그리고 푸딩이 다 없어졌을 때, 그는 마치 그 즐거움이 아직도 이어지는 듯이 웃었다."]], [["Finding him so very friendly and companionable, it was then that I asked for the pen and ink and paper, to write to Peggotty.", "그가 이렇게 무척 다정하고 붙임성 있는 것을 보고, 그제야 나는 패거티에게 편지를 쓰려고 펜과 잉크와 종이를 달라고 했다."], ["He not only brought it immediately, but was good enough to look over me while I wrote the letter.", "그는 그것을 당장 가져다주었을 뿐 아니라, 친절하게도 내가 편지를 쓰는 동안 곁에서 들여다봐 주기까지 했다."], ["When I had finished it, he asked me where I was going to school.", "내가 편지를 다 쓰자, 그는 내가 어느 학교로 가느냐고 물었다."]], [["I said, ‘Near London,’ which was all I knew.", "나는 \"런던 근처요\"라고 했는데, 그게 내가 아는 전부였다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아이고! 저런!\""], ["my eye!’ he said, looking very low-spirited, ‘I am sorry for that.’", "그가 몹시 풀 죽은 얼굴로 말했다. \"그거 안됐네요.\""]], [["‘Why?’ I asked him.", "\"왜요?\" 내가 그에게 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, Lord!’ he said, shaking his head, ‘that’s the school where they broke the boy’s ribs--two ribs--a little boy he was.", "\"아이고, 맙소사!\" 그가 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다. \"거기가 바로 애 갈비뼈를 부러뜨린 그 학교잖아요 — 갈비뼈 두 대를 — 조그만 사내애였는데."], ["I should say he was--let me see--how old are you, about?’", "그 애가 — 가만, 그러니까 — 너는 몇 살쯤 되니, 대략?\""]], [["I told him between eight and nine.", "나는 여덟 살에서 아홉 살 사이라고 말해 주었다."]], [["‘That’s just his age,’ he said.", "\"꼭 그 애 나이로구나.\""], ["‘He was eight years and six months old when they broke his first rib; eight years and eight months old when they broke his second, and did for him.’", "그가 말했다. \"걔는 여덟 살하고 여섯 달일 때 첫 번째 갈비뼈가 부러졌고, 여덟 살하고 여덟 달일 때 두 번째 갈비뼈가 부러져서 끝장이 났지.\""]], [["I could not disguise from myself, or from the waiter, that this was an uncomfortable coincidence, and inquired how it was done.", "이게 영 꺼림칙한 우연의 일치라는 사실을, 나는 나 자신에게도 웨이터에게도 숨길 수가 없어서, 어쩌다 그렇게 됐느냐고 물었다."], ["His answer was not cheering to my spirits, for it consisted of two dismal words, ‘With whopping.’", "그의 대답은 내 마음을 도무지 달래 주지 못했으니, 그것은 음울한 한마디 — \"두들겨 패서요\"였다."]], [["The blowing of the coach-horn in the yard was a seasonable diversion, which made me get up and hesitatingly inquire, in the mingled pride and diffidence of having a purse (which I took out of my pocket), if there were anything to pay.", "마당에서 마차 나팔 소리가 울린 것은 때마침 알맞은 기분 전환거리였는데, 그 덕에 나는 자리에서 일어나, 지갑을 (주머니에서 꺼내며) 가졌다는 자부심과 머뭇거림이 뒤섞인 채로, 치를 게 있느냐고 우물쭈물 물었다."]], [["‘There’s a sheet of letter-paper,’ he returned.", "\"편지지 한 장이 있죠.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘Did you ever buy a sheet of letter-paper?’", "\"편지지 한 장 사 보신 적 있어요?\""]], [["I could not remember that I ever had.", "나는 사 본 적이 있는지 도무지 기억나지 않았다."]], [["‘It’s dear,’ he said, ‘on account of the duty.", "\"비싸요.\" 그가 말했다. \"세금 때문에요."], ["Threepence.", "삼 펜스예요."], ["That’s the way we’re taxed in this country.", "이 나라에서는 이런 식으로 세금을 매기거든요."], ["There’s nothing else, except the waiter.", "그것 말고는 없어요, 웨이터 빼고는요."], ["Never mind the ink. I lose by that.’", "잉크는 신경 쓰지 마세요. 그건 제가 손해 보는 거니까요.\""]], [["‘What should you--what should I--how much ought I to--what would it be right to pay the waiter, if you please?’ I stammered, blushing.", "\"제가 — 그러니까 제가 — 웨이터한테 얼마나 — 얼마를 드리는 게 맞을까요, 괜찮으시다면?\" 나는 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘If I hadn’t a family, and that family hadn’t the cowpock,’ said the waiter, ‘I wouldn’t take a sixpence.", "\"저한테 식구가 없고, 그 식구가 마마에 안 걸렸다면,\" 웨이터가 말했다. \"육 펜스도 안 받을 거예요."], ["If I didn’t support a aged pairint, and a lovely sister,’--here the waiter was greatly agitated--‘I wouldn’t take a farthing.", "제가 늙은 부모님하고 사랑스러운 누이를 부양하지 않는다면,\" — 여기서 웨이터는 몹시 격해졌다 — \"한 푼도 안 받을 거예요."], ["If I had a good place, and was treated well here, I should beg acceptance of a trifle, instead of taking of it.", "제게 좋은 일자리가 있고 여기서 대접을 잘 받는다면, 받기는커녕 한 푼이라도 받아 달라고 빌어야 마땅하죠."], ["But I live on broken wittles--and I sleep on the coals’--here the waiter burst into tears.", "하지만 저는 먹다 남은 음식으로 연명하고 — 석탄 위에서 잔답니다\" — 여기서 웨이터는 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["I was very much concerned for his misfortunes, and felt that any recognition short of ninepence would be mere brutality and hardness of heart.", "나는 그의 불행이 몹시 안쓰러워서, 구 펜스에 못 미치는 어떤 사례도 그저 잔인하고 모진 짓일 뿐이라는 생각이 들었다."], ["Therefore I gave him one of my three bright shillings, which he received with much humility and veneration, and spun up with his thumb, directly afterwards, to try the goodness of.", "그래서 나는 반짝이는 일 실링짜리 동전 세 개 중 하나를 그에게 주었고, 그는 그것을 몹시 황송하고 공손한 태도로 받더니, 곧바로 엄지손가락으로 휙 튕겨 진짜인지 확인했다."]], [["It was a little disconcerting to me, to find, when I was being helped up behind the coach, that I was supposed to have eaten all the dinner without any assistance.", "마차 뒤에 올라타도록 부축을 받을 때, 그 점심을 누구의 도움도 없이 나 혼자 다 먹어 치운 걸로 여겨지고 있다는 걸 알고 나는 좀 당황스러웠다."], ["I discovered this, from overhearing the lady in the bow-window say to the guard, ‘Take care of that child, George, or he’ll burst!’ and from observing that the women-servants who were about the place came out to look and giggle at me as a young phenomenon.", "활처럼 굽은 창문의 부인이 차장에게 \"조지, 저 애 잘 지켜봐, 안 그러면 배가 터질 거야!\" 하고 말하는 것을 엿듣고, 또 그곳에 있던 하녀들이 나를 어린 진귀한 구경거리라도 되는 양 보고 키득거리려고 나오는 것을 보고 나서야 알아챈 것이다."], ["My unfortunate friend the waiter, who had quite recovered his spirits, did not appear to be disturbed by this, but joined in the general admiration without being at all confused.", "한결 기운을 차린 나의 불운한 친구 웨이터는 이 일에 조금도 동요하는 기색 없이, 전혀 멋쩍어하지 않고 다 함께 감탄하는 데 끼어들었다."], ["If I had any doubt of him, I suppose this half awakened it; but I am inclined to believe that with the simple confidence of a child, and the natural reliance of a child upon superior years (qualities I am very sorry any children should prematurely change for worldly wisdom), I had no serious mistrust of him on the whole, even then.", "만약 그에게 의심이 들었다면, 아마 이 일이 그 의심을 어렴풋이 일깨웠을 테지만, 내 생각엔, 아이 특유의 순진한 믿음과 손윗사람에 대한 아이 특유의 자연스러운 의지로 (아이들이 이런 자질을 너무 일찍 세상 물정에 밝은 약삭빠름과 맞바꾸는 건 정말 안타까운 일이다), 그때조차도 나는 그를 대체로 크게 미심쩍어하지는 않았던 것 같다."]], [["I felt it rather hard, I must own, to be made, without deserving it, the subject of jokes between the coachman and guard as to the coach drawing heavy behind, on account of my sitting there, and as to the greater expediency of my travelling by waggon.", "솔직히 고백하건대, 받아도 싼 짓을 한 것도 아닌데, 내가 거기 앉아 있는 탓에 마차 뒤쪽이 무거워졌다느니, 차라리 짐마차로 가는 게 더 나을 거라느니 하는 마부와 차장 사이의 농담거리가 된 것은 좀 견디기 힘들었다."], ["The story of my supposed appetite getting wind among the outside passengers, they were merry upon it likewise; and asked me whether I was going to be paid for, at school, as two brothers or three, and whether I was contracted for, or went upon the regular terms; with other pleasant questions.", "내가 먹어 치웠다는 그 식욕 이야기가 바깥쪽 손님들 사이에 퍼지자, 그들도 그걸로 흥겨워하며, 학교에서 내 식비를 형제 두 사람 몫으로 내느냐 세 사람 몫으로 내느냐, 정찰제로 계약했느냐 보통 요금으로 다니느냐 따위 유쾌한 질문을 던져 댔다."], ["But the worst of it was, that I knew I should be ashamed to eat anything, when an opportunity offered, and that, after a rather light dinner, I should remain hungry all night--for I had left my cakes behind, at the hotel, in my hurry.", "하지만 그중에서도 최악은, 기회가 와도 부끄러워서 아무것도 못 먹을 게 뻔하다는 것, 그래서 점심을 다소 가볍게 먹은 탓에 밤새 굶주린 채로 있게 되리라는 것이었다 — 서두르는 바람에 과자를 여관에 두고 와 버렸으니까."], ["My apprehensions were realized.", "내 걱정은 그대로 들어맞았다."], ["When we stopped for supper I couldn’t muster courage to take any, though I should have liked it very much, but sat by the fire and said I didn’t want anything.", "저녁을 먹으려고 멈춰 섰을 때, 나는 무척 먹고 싶었으면서도 도무지 무엇이든 먹을 용기를 내지 못해, 불가에 앉아 아무것도 안 먹겠다고만 했다."], ["This did not save me from more jokes, either; for a husky-voiced gentleman with a rough face, who had been eating out of a sandwich-box nearly all the way, except when he had been drinking out of a bottle, said I was like a boa-constrictor who took enough at one meal to last him a long time; after which, he actually brought a rash out upon himself with boiled beef.", "그렇다고 더한 농담을 면한 것도 아니었으니, 거의 내내 샌드위치 통에서 뭔가를 꺼내 먹다가 이따금 병에서 술을 들이켜던, 거친 얼굴에 목소리가 쉰 한 신사가, 나더러 한 끼에 오래 버틸 만큼 잔뜩 먹어 두는 보아 구렁이 같다고 했다. 그러고는 정작 자기가 삶은 쇠고기를 먹고 두드러기가 돋았다."]], [["We had started from Yarmouth at three o’clock in the afternoon, and we were due in London about eight next morning.", "우리는 오후 세 시에 야머스를 출발했고, 이튿날 아침 여덟 시쯤 런던에 닿을 예정이었다."], ["It was Mid-summer weather, and the evening was very pleasant.", "한여름 날씨라 저녁은 무척 상쾌했다."], ["When we passed through a village, I pictured to myself what the insides of the houses were like, and what the inhabitants were about; and when boys came running after us, and got up behind and swung there for a little way, I wondered whether their fathers were alive, and whether they were happy at home.", "마을을 지날 때면, 나는 집 안이 어떤 모습일지, 사람들은 무엇을 하고 있을지 마음속에 그려 보았다. 그리고 사내애들이 우리를 뒤쫓아 달려와 마차 뒤에 올라타 잠깐씩 매달려 갈 때면, 그 애들의 아버지는 살아 있을까, 그 애들은 집에서 행복할까 궁금해했다."], ["I had plenty to think of, therefore, besides my mind running continually on the kind of place I was going to--which was an awful speculation.", "그러니 내가 가고 있는 곳이 어떤 데일까 끊임없이 마음을 졸이는 것 말고도 — 그건 무시무시한 상상이었다 — 생각할 거리가 많았다."], ["Sometimes, I remember, I resigned myself to thoughts of home and Peggotty; and to endeavouring, in a confused blind way, to recall how I had felt, and what sort of boy I used to be, before I bit Mr.", "가끔, 기억하건대, 나는 집과 패거티 생각에 몸을 맡기기도 했다. 그리고 어수선하고 막막한 심정으로, 내가 머드스톤 씨를 물기 전에 어떤 기분이었는지, 어떤 아이였는지 떠올려 보려 애쓰기도 했다."], ["Murdstone: which I couldn’t satisfy myself about by any means, I seemed to have bitten him in such a remote antiquity.", "하지만 그건 도무지 만족스럽게 떠오르지 않았으니, 그를 문 것이 까마득히 먼 옛날 일처럼 느껴졌기 때문이다."]], [["The night was not so pleasant as the evening, for it got chilly; and being put between two gentlemen (the rough-faced one and another) to prevent my tumbling off the coach, I was nearly smothered by their falling asleep, and completely blocking me up.", "밤은 저녁만큼 상쾌하지 않았다. 쌀쌀해졌기 때문이다. 게다가 마차에서 굴러떨어지지 않게 두 신사 (거친 얼굴의 그 신사와 또 다른 신사) 사이에 끼인 탓에, 그들이 잠들어 나를 완전히 틀어막는 바람에 나는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["They squeezed me so hard sometimes, that I could not help crying out, ‘Oh!", "그들은 가끔 나를 어찌나 세게 짓누르던지, 나는 \"아! 제발요!\""], ["If you please!’--which they didn’t like at all, because it woke them.", "하고 외치지 않을 수가 없었는데 — 그러면 그들은 잠이 깨는 통에 그걸 영 싫어했다."], ["Opposite me was an elderly lady in a great fur cloak, who looked in the dark more like a haystack than a lady, she was wrapped up to such a degree.", "내 맞은편에는 커다란 모피 외투를 두른 나이 든 부인이 있었는데, 어찌나 칭칭 싸맸던지 어둠 속에서는 부인이라기보다 건초 더미처럼 보였다."], ["This lady had a basket with her, and she hadn’t known what to do with it, for a long time, until she found that on account of my legs being short, it could go underneath me.", "이 부인은 바구니 하나를 가지고 있었는데, 오랫동안 그걸 어찌해야 할지 몰라 하다가, 내 다리가 짧으니 그 바구니를 내 밑에 넣을 수 있다는 걸 알아냈다."], ["It cramped and hurt me so, that it made me perfectly miserable; but if I moved in the least, and made a glass that was in the basket rattle against something else (as it was sure to do), she gave me the cruellest poke with her foot, and said, ‘Come, don’t YOU fidget.", "그게 나를 어찌나 옥죄고 아프게 하던지 나는 완전히 비참한 꼴이 됐다. 그런데 내가 조금이라도 움직여서 바구니 안의 유리잔이 다른 무언가에 부딪쳐 달그락 소리를 내면 (틀림없이 그런 소리가 났다), 부인은 발로 나를 더없이 모질게 쿡 찌르며 말했다. \"이봐, 가만 좀 있어."], ["YOUR bones are young enough, I’m sure!’", "네 뼈는 분명 아직 젊잖니!\""]], [["At last the sun rose, and then my companions seemed to sleep easier.", "마침내 해가 떴고, 그제야 동행들은 좀 편히 자는 듯했다."], ["The difficulties under which they had laboured all night, and which had found utterance in the most terrific gasps and snorts, are not to be conceived.", "그들이 밤새 시달리며 더없이 끔찍한 헐떡임과 콧방귀로 토해 낸 그 고생이란 이루 말할 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["As the sun got higher, their sleep became lighter, and so they gradually one by one awoke.", "해가 높이 솟을수록 그들의 잠은 얕아졌고, 그렇게 하나씩 차례로 잠에서 깨어났다."], ["I recollect being very much surprised by the feint everybody made, then, of not having been to sleep at all, and by the uncommon indignation with which everyone repelled the charge.", "그때 다들 잠 따위는 한숨도 안 잔 척 시치미를 뚝 떼는 것에, 그리고 그런 혐의를 받으면 저마다 유난스레 발끈 화를 내는 것에 내가 무척 놀랐던 기억이 난다."], ["I labour under the same kind of astonishment to this day, having invariably observed that of all human weaknesses, the one to which our common nature is the least disposed to confess (I cannot imagine why) is the weakness of having gone to sleep in a coach.", "나는 오늘날까지도 똑같은 종류의 놀라움에 빠져 있으니, 인간의 온갖 약점 가운데 우리 인지상정이 가장 인정하기 싫어하는 것이 (왜 그런지는 도무지 모르겠지만) 바로 마차 안에서 잠들었다는 약점임을 한결같이 보아 왔기 때문이다."]], [["What an amazing place London was to me when I saw it in the distance, and how I believed all the adventures of all my favourite heroes to be constantly enacting and re-enacting there, and how I vaguely made it out in my own mind to be fuller of wonders and wickedness than all the cities of the earth, I need not stop here to relate.", "멀리서 런던을 바라보았을 때 그곳이 나에게 얼마나 놀라운 곳이었는지, 내가 가장 좋아하는 영웅들의 온갖 모험이 죄다 그곳에서 끊임없이 펼쳐지고 또 펼쳐지고 있으리라고 얼마나 굳게 믿었는지, 그곳이 이 세상 어떤 도시보다도 경이와 사악함으로 가득 찬 곳이라고 내 마음속에서 얼마나 어렴풋이 그려 냈는지는 여기서 굳이 멈춰 이야기할 필요가 없겠다."], ["We approached it by degrees, and got, in due time, to the inn in the Whitechapel district, for which we were bound.", "우리는 조금씩 런던에 다가가, 때맞춰 목적지인 화이트채플 구역의 여관에 다다랐다."], ["I forget whether it was the Blue Bull, or the Blue Boar; but I know it was the Blue Something, and that its likeness was painted up on the back of the coach.", "그게 블루 불이었는지 블루 보어였는지는 잊어버렸지만, '블루 무엇'이었다는 것, 그리고 그 그림이 마차 뒤에 그려져 있었다는 것만은 기억난다. * 블루 불, 블루 보어: 각각 '푸른 황소', '푸른 멧돼지'라는 뜻의 여관 이름이다."]], [["The guard’s eye lighted on me as he was getting down, and he said at the booking-office door:", "차장이 마차에서 내리다가 나에게 눈길이 닿자, 그는 매표소 문 앞에서 말했다."]], [["‘Is there anybody here for a yoongster booked in the name of Murdstone, from Bloonderstone, Sooffolk, to be left till called for?’", "\"여기, 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 머드스톤이란 이름으로 예약하고, 데리러 올 때까지 맡겨 두라고 한 꼬맹이를 찾으러 온 사람 있소?\""]], [["Nobody answered.", "아무도 대답하지 않았다."]], [["‘Try Copperfield, if you please, sir,’ said I, looking helplessly down.", "\"코퍼필드로 한번 찾아 주세요, 아저씨.\" 내가 막막하게 고개를 숙인 채 말했다."]], [["‘Is there anybody here for a yoongster, booked in the name of Murdstone, from Bloonderstone, Sooffolk, but owning to the name of Copperfield, to be left till called for?’ said the guard. ‘Come!", "\"여기, 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 머드스톤이란 이름으로 예약했는데, 코퍼필드란 이름이라고 우기는 꼬맹이를, 데리러 올 때까지 맡겨 두라고 한 거 찾으러 온 사람 있소?\" 차장이 말했다. \"이봐요!"], ["IS there anybody?’", "누구 없소?\""]], [["No. There was nobody.", "없었다. 아무도 없었다."], ["I looked anxiously around; but the inquiry made no impression on any of the bystanders, if I except a man in gaiters, with one eye, who suggested that they had better put a brass collar round my neck, and tie me up in the stable.", "나는 불안하게 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그 물음은 구경꾼 누구에게도 아무 인상을 남기지 못했으니, 외눈에 각반을 찬 사내 하나만 빼고는 그랬다. 그 사내는 차라리 내 목에 놋쇠 목줄을 채워서 마구간에 묶어 두는 게 낫겠다고 한마디 했다."]], [["A ladder was brought, and I got down after the lady, who was like a haystack: not daring to stir, until her basket was removed.", "사다리가 와서, 나는 건초 더미 같던 그 부인 다음에 내렸는데, 부인의 바구니가 치워질 때까지 감히 꼼짝도 못 했다."], ["The coach was clear of passengers by that time, the luggage was very soon cleared out, the horses had been taken out before the luggage, and now the coach itself was wheeled and backed off by some hostlers, out of the way. Still, nobody appeared, to claim the dusty youngster from Blunderstone, Suffolk.", "그 무렵 마차에서 승객은 모두 빠져나갔고, 짐도 곧 다 치워졌으며, 말은 짐보다 먼저 풀려 나갔고, 이제 마차 자체도 마부들이 굴려서 뒤로 빼내 한쪽으로 치워 버렸다. 그래도 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 온 먼지투성이 꼬맹이를 찾으러 나타나는 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["More solitary than Robinson Crusoe, who had nobody to look at him and see that he was solitary, I went into the booking-office, and, by invitation of the clerk on duty, passed behind the counter, and sat down on the scale at which they weighed the luggage.", "자기가 외톨이라는 걸 보아 줄 사람조차 없었던 로빈슨 크루소보다도 더 외로운 신세로, 나는 매표소 안으로 들어갔고, 당번 직원이 권하기에 계산대 뒤로 가서 짐 무게를 다는 저울 위에 앉았다."], ["Here, as I sat looking at the parcels, packages, and books, and inhaling the smell of stables (ever since associated with that morning), a procession of most tremendous considerations began to march through my mind.", "거기 앉아 소포며 꾸러미며 장부 들을 바라보고, 마구간 냄새를 들이마시며 (그 냄새는 그 후로 줄곧 그날 아침과 한데 얽혀 떠오른다), 더없이 무시무시한 온갖 상념이 내 마음속을 줄지어 행진하기 시작했다."], ["Supposing nobody should ever fetch me, how long would they consent to keep me there? Would they keep me long enough to spend seven shillings?", "만약 아무도 영영 나를 데리러 오지 않으면, 저 사람들은 나를 얼마나 오래 여기 두는 데 동의해 줄까? 칠 실링을 다 쓸 만큼 오래 두어 줄까?"], ["Should I sleep at night in one of those wooden bins, with the other luggage, and wash myself at the pump in the yard in the morning; or should I be turned out every night, and expected to come again to be left till called for, when the office opened next day?", "밤에는 다른 짐들과 함께 저 나무통 같은 칸 가운데 하나에서 자고, 아침에는 마당 펌프에서 몸을 씻어야 하나? 아니면 매일 밤 쫓겨났다가, 이튿날 매표소 문이 열리면 다시 와서 데리러 올 때까지 맡겨져 있어야 하나?"], ["Supposing there was no mistake in the case, and Mr. Murdstone had devised this plan to get rid of me, what should I do?", "만약 이게 착오 같은 게 아니라, 머드스톤 씨가 나를 떼어 버리려고 이 계획을 꾸민 거라면, 나는 어찌해야 하나?"], ["If they allowed me to remain there until my seven shillings were spent, I couldn’t hope to remain there when I began to starve.", "칠 실링을 다 쓸 때까지 여기 머무르게 해 준다 해도, 막상 굶주리기 시작하면 여기 더 있기를 바랄 수는 없을 것이다."], ["That would obviously be inconvenient and unpleasant to the customers, besides entailing on the Blue Whatever-it-was, the risk of funeral expenses.", "그건 손님들에게 분명 불편하고 거북한 일일 테고, 게다가 그 블루 무엇이라는 여관에 장례 비용까지 떠안길 위험이 있을 테니까."], ["If I started off at once, and tried to walk back home, how could I ever find my way, how could I ever hope to walk so far, how could I make sure of anyone but Peggotty, even if I got back?", "만약 당장 떠나서 걸어서 집으로 돌아가려 한다면, 어떻게 길을 찾으며, 그 먼 길을 걸어갈 엄두를 어찌 내며, 설령 돌아간들 패거티 말고 누구를 믿을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["If I found out the nearest proper authorities, and offered myself to go for a soldier, or a sailor, I was such a little fellow that it was most likely they wouldn’t take me in.", "만약 가장 가까운 관청을 찾아내 군인이나 뱃사람으로 가겠다고 나선다 해도, 나는 워낙 조그만 녀석이라 십중팔구 받아 주지 않을 것이다."], ["These thoughts, and a hundred other such thoughts, turned me burning hot, and made me giddy with apprehension and dismay.", "이런 생각, 그리고 이와 비슷한 수백 가지 생각에 나는 온몸이 후끈 달아오르고, 두려움과 절망으로 머리가 어질어질했다."], ["I was in the height of my fever when a man entered and whispered to the clerk, who presently slanted me off the scale, and pushed me over to him, as if I were weighed, bought, delivered, and paid for.", "한창 이런 열병에 빠져 있을 때, 한 사내가 들어와 직원에게 뭐라고 속삭였고, 직원은 곧 나를 저울에서 미끄러뜨려 그 사내 쪽으로 떠밀었는데, 마치 내가 무게를 달아 팔리고, 넘겨지고, 값이 치러진 물건이라도 되는 것 같았다."]], [["As I went out of the office, hand in hand with this new acquaintance, I stole a look at him.", "이 새로 알게 된 사람과 손을 맞잡고 매표소를 나서면서, 나는 그를 몰래 흘끔 살폈다."], ["He was a gaunt, sallow young man, with hollow cheeks, and a chin almost as black as Mr.", "그는 핼쑥하고 누르께한 얼굴빛의 젊은이로, 두 볼이 움푹 꺼졌고, 턱은 머드스톤 씨만큼이나 거뭇했다."], ["Murdstone’s; but there the likeness ended, for his whiskers were shaved off, and his hair, instead of being glossy, was rusty and dry.", "그러나 닮은 점은 거기까지였으니, 그는 구레나룻을 밀어 버렸고, 머리카락도 윤기가 흐르기는커녕 푸석푸석하고 메말라 있었다."], ["He was dressed in a suit of black clothes which were rather rusty and dry too, and rather short in the sleeves and legs; and he had a white neck-kerchief on, that was not over-clean.", "그는 검은 양복 한 벌을 입고 있었는데, 그것 역시 꽤 낡고 메마른 데다, 소매와 바짓단이 좀 짧았다. 그리고 그리 깨끗하달 수 없는 흰 목도리를 두르고 있었다."], ["I did not, and do not, suppose that this neck-kerchief was all the linen he wore, but it was all he showed or gave any hint of.", "나는 이 목도리가 그가 걸친 속옷의 전부라고 그때도 생각지 않았고 지금도 생각지 않지만, 어쨌든 그가 내보이거나 그런 낌새라도 풍긴 것은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["‘You’re the new boy?’ he said.", "\"네가 새로 온 애냐?\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Yes, sir,’ I said.", "\"네, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["I supposed I was.", "나는 그런가 보다 했다."], ["I didn’t know.", "나도 잘 몰랐다."]], [["‘I’m one of the masters at Salem House,’ he said.", "\"나는 세일럼 하우스의 선생들 중 하나다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["I made him a bow and felt very much overawed.", "나는 그에게 고개 숙여 인사하면서 몹시 주눅이 들었다."], ["I was so ashamed to allude to a commonplace thing like my box, to a scholar and a master at Salem House, that we had gone some little distance from the yard before I had the hardihood to mention it.", "세일럼 하우스의 학자이자 선생님에게 내 상자 따위 같은 시시한 것을 입에 올리기가 어찌나 부끄럽던지, 마당에서 좀 떨어진 데까지 가고 나서야 가까스로 용기를 내어 그 얘기를 꺼냈다."], ["We turned back, on my humbly insinuating that it might be useful to me hereafter; and he told the clerk that the carrier had instructions to call for it at noon.", "그것이 나중에 내게 쓸모가 있을지도 모른다고 겸손하게 슬쩍 비추자, 우리는 발길을 되돌렸다. 그는 직원에게, 마부가 정오에 그것을 가지러 오라는 지시를 받았다고 일러 두었다."]], [["‘If you please, sir,’ I said, when we had accomplished about the same distance as before, ‘is it far?’", "\"저, 선생님,\" 우리가 아까와 비슷한 거리를 걸어왔을 때 내가 말했다. \"거기까지 먼가요?\""]], [["‘It’s down by Blackheath,’ he said.", "\"블랙히스 쪽에 있단다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Is that far, sir?’ I diffidently asked.", "\"그게 먼가요, 선생님?\" 내가 조심스레 물었다."]], [["‘It’s a good step,’ he said.", "\"제법 걸어야 하지.\""], ["‘We shall go by the stage-coach.", "그가 말했다. \"역마차로 갈 거다."], ["It’s about six miles.’", "한 육 마일쯤 돼.\""]], [["I was so faint and tired, that the idea of holding out for six miles more, was too much for me.", "나는 어찌나 기진맥진하고 지쳤던지, 육 마일을 더 버틴다는 생각만으로도 견디기 힘들었다."], ["I took heart to tell him that I had had nothing all night, and that if he would allow me to buy something to eat, I should be very much obliged to him.", "나는 용기를 내어 그에게, 밤새 아무것도 먹지 못했다고, 그러니 뭔가 먹을 것을 사게 허락해 주신다면 정말 고맙겠다고 말했다."], ["He appeared surprised at this--I see him stop and look at me now--and after considering for a few moments, said he wanted to call on an old person who lived not far off, and that the best way would be for me to buy some bread, or whatever I liked best that was wholesome, and make my breakfast at her house, where we could get some milk.", "그는 이 말에 놀란 듯했다 — 지금도 그가 걸음을 멈추고 나를 바라보던 모습이 눈에 선하다 — 그러고는 잠시 생각하더니, 멀지 않은 곳에 사는 어떤 노인을 찾아갈 참이라며, 가장 좋은 방법은 내가 빵이든, 몸에 해롭지 않으면서 제일 먹고 싶은 것이든 사 가지고, 그 노인 집에서 아침을 먹는 거라고, 거기서 우유도 좀 얻을 수 있다고 했다."]], [["Accordingly we looked in at a baker’s window, and after I had made a series of proposals to buy everything that was bilious in the shop, and he had rejected them one by one, we decided in favour of a nice little loaf of brown bread, which cost me threepence.", "그래서 우리는 빵집 진열창을 들여다보았는데, 내가 가게 안의 느끼한 것들을 죄다 사겠다고 줄줄이 제안하고, 그가 그것을 하나씩 하나씩 물리친 끝에, 우리는 삼 펜스짜리 갈색 빵 한 덩이를 사기로 했다."], ["Then, at a grocer’s shop, we bought an egg and a slice of streaky bacon; which still left what I thought a good deal of change, out of the second of the bright shillings, and made me consider London a very cheap place.", "그러고 나서 식료품점에서 달걀 하나와 비계가 섞인 베이컨 한 조각을 샀는데, 그러고도 반짝이는 일 실링짜리 동전 두 번째 것에서 내 생각에 꽤 많은 거스름돈이 남아서, 나는 런던이 무척 값싼 곳이라고 여기게 되었다."], ["These provisions laid in, we went on through a great noise and uproar that confused my weary head beyond description, and over a bridge which, no doubt, was London Bridge (indeed I think he told me so, but I was half asleep), until we came to the poor person’s house, which was a part of some alms-houses, as I knew by their look, and by an inscription on a stone over the gate which said they were established for twenty-five poor women.", "이 먹을거리를 챙겨 넣고, 우리는 내 지친 머리를 이루 말할 수 없이 어지럽히는 엄청난 소음과 소란을 뚫고 나아가, 분명 런던 다리였을 다리를 (실은 그가 그렇게 말해 준 것 같은데, 나는 반쯤 졸고 있었다) 건너서, 마침내 그 가난한 노인의 집에 다다랐다. 그곳은 한눈에 봐도, 그리고 대문 위 돌에 새겨진, 가난한 여인 스물다섯 명을 위해 세워졌다는 글귀로 보아도, 자선 양로원의 한 채였다."]], [["The Master at Salem House lifted the latch of one of a number of little black doors that were all alike, and had each a little diamond-paned window on one side, and another little diamond--paned window above; and we went into the little house of one of these poor old women, who was blowing a fire to make a little saucepan boil.", "세일럼 하우스의 선생님은 죄다 똑같이 생긴 자그마한 검은 문 여러 개 가운데 하나의 걸쇠를 들어 올렸는데, 그 문들은 저마다 한쪽에 마름모꼴 격자창이 하나, 그 위에 또 마름모꼴 격자창이 하나 달려 있었다. 우리는 그 가난한 노파들 중 한 사람의 작은 집으로 들어갔는데, 노파는 작은 냄비를 끓이려고 불을 후후 불고 있었다."], ["On seeing the master enter, the old woman stopped with the bellows on her knee, and said something that I thought sounded like ‘My Charley!’ but on seeing me come in too, she got up, and rubbing her hands made a confused sort of half curtsey.", "선생님이 들어오는 것을 보자 노파는 풀무를 무릎에 얹은 채로 멈추고, 내 귀에는 \"우리 찰리!\"처럼 들린 무슨 말을 했다. 그러나 나까지 들어오는 것을 보고는 일어나서, 두 손을 비비며 어정쩡하게 어설픈 무릎인사를 했다."]], [["‘Can you cook this young gentleman’s breakfast for him, if you please?’ said the Master at Salem House.", "\"괜찮으시면 이 어린 신사분 아침을 좀 차려 주시겠어요?\" 세일럼 하우스의 선생님이 말했다."]], [["‘Can I?’ said the old woman.", "\"되고말고!\" 노파가 말했다."], ["‘Yes can I, sure!’", "\"암, 되고말고요!\""]], [["‘How’s Mrs.", "\"오늘 피비슨 부인은 좀 어떠세요?\""], ["Fibbitson today?’ said the Master, looking at another old woman in a large chair by the fire, who was such a bundle of clothes that I feel grateful to this hour for not having sat upon her by mistake.", "선생님이, 불가의 커다란 의자에 앉은 또 다른 노파를 바라보며 말했다. 그 노파는 옷가지를 한 덩어리로 둘둘 뭉쳐 놓은 꼴이라, 잘못해서 그 위에 깔고 앉지 않은 게 지금 이 순간까지도 고마울 지경이었다."]], [["‘Ah, she’s poorly,’ said the first old woman.", "\"아휴, 그이는 시름시름해요.\""], ["‘It’s one of her bad days.", "첫 번째 노파가 말했다."], ["If the fire was to go out, through any accident, I verily believe she’d go out too, and never come to life again.’", "\"오늘은 안 좋은 날 중 하루라서요. 어쩌다 불이라도 꺼지면, 정말이지 그이도 같이 꺼져서 다시는 살아나지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["As they looked at her, I looked at her also.", "그들이 그 노파를 바라보자, 나도 그 노파를 바라보았다."], ["Although it was a warm day, she seemed to think of nothing but the fire.", "따뜻한 날이었는데도, 그 노파는 오로지 불 생각밖에 없는 듯했다."], ["I fancied she was jealous even of the saucepan on it; and I have reason to know that she took its impressment into the service of boiling my egg and broiling my bacon, in dudgeon; for I saw her, with my own discomfited eyes, shake her fist at me once, when those culinary operations were going on, and no one else was looking.", "나는 그 노파가 불에 올려놓은 냄비마저도 시샘하는 게 아닌가 싶었다. 그리고 내 달걀을 삶고 베이컨을 굽는 일에 그 냄비가 징발된 것을 노파가 못마땅해했다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 있으니, 내 두 눈으로, 그 부엌일이 한창이고 아무도 보지 않을 때 노파가 나를 향해 한 번 주먹을 흔들어 대는 것을 똑똑히 보았기 때문이다."], ["The sun streamed in at the little window, but she sat with her own back and the back of the large chair towards it, screening the fire as if she were sedulously keeping IT warm, instead of it keeping her warm, and watching it in a most distrustful manner.", "햇살이 작은 창으로 쏟아져 들어왔지만, 노파는 자기 등과 커다란 의자 등받이를 그쪽으로 돌린 채 앉아, 마치 불이 자기를 따뜻하게 해 주는 게 아니라 자기가 부지런히 불을 따뜻하게 지켜 주기라도 하는 양 불을 가리고서, 더없이 미심쩍은 눈초리로 불을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["The completion of the preparations for my breakfast, by relieving the fire, gave her such extreme joy that she laughed aloud--and a very unmelodious laugh she had, I must say.", "내 아침 채비가 마무리되어 불이 풀려나자, 노파는 어찌나 기뻤던지 소리 내어 웃었는데 — 그 웃음소리가 도무지 곱지 않았다고 말해 두어야겠다."]], [["I sat down to my brown loaf, my egg, and my rasher of bacon, with a basin of milk besides, and made a most delicious meal.", "나는 갈색 빵 덩이와 달걀, 베이컨 한 조각, 거기에 우유 한 사발까지 차려진 앞에 앉아, 더없이 맛있는 식사를 했다."], ["While I was yet in the full enjoyment of it, the old woman of the house said to the Master:", "내가 아직 한창 그것을 즐기고 있을 때, 그 집 노파가 선생님에게 말했다."]], [["‘Have you got your flute with you?’", "\"플루트 가져왔수?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ he returned.", "\"네.\" 그가 대답했다."]], [["‘Have a blow at it,’ said the old woman, coaxingly. ‘Do!’", "\"한 곡 불어 봐요.\" 노파가 어르듯이 말했다. \"어서!\""]], [["The Master, upon this, put his hand underneath the skirts of his coat, and brought out his flute in three pieces, which he screwed together, and began immediately to play.", "이 말에 선생님은 외투 자락 밑으로 손을 넣어 세 토막으로 된 플루트를 꺼내, 그것들을 돌려 맞추더니, 곧장 불기 시작했다."], ["My impression is, after many years of consideration, that there never can have been anybody in the world who played worse.", "여러 해 곰곰이 생각해 본 끝에 내가 받은 인상은, 이 세상에 그보다 더 못 부는 사람은 결코 있을 수 없으리라는 것이다."], ["He made the most dismal sounds I have ever heard produced by any means, natural or artificial.", "그는 자연적이든 인위적이든 어떤 수단으로 낸 것 중에서도 내가 들어 본 가장 음울한 소리를 냈다."], ["I don’t know what the tunes were--if there were such things in the performance at all, which I doubt--but the influence of the strain upon me was, first, to make me think of all my sorrows until I could hardly keep my tears back; then to take away my appetite; and lastly, to make me so sleepy that I couldn’t keep my eyes open.", "그 곡조가 무엇이었는지 나는 모른다 — 애당초 그 연주에 곡조라는 게 있었기나 했는지도 의심스럽지만 — 어쨌든 그 가락이 내게 미친 영향은, 처음에는 내 온갖 슬픔을 떠올리게 해서 눈물을 참기 힘들 지경으로 만들었고, 다음에는 식욕을 가시게 했으며, 마지막에는 어찌나 졸음이 쏟아지던지 눈을 뜨고 있을 수가 없게 만들었다."], ["They begin to close again, and I begin to nod, as the recollection rises fresh upon me.", "그 기억이 새삼 생생히 떠오르는 지금도, 다시 눈이 감기고 고개가 끄덕끄덕 졸린다."], ["Once more the little room, with its open corner cupboard, and its square-backed chairs, and its angular little staircase leading to the room above, and its three peacock’s feathers displayed over the mantelpiece--I remember wondering when I first went in, what that peacock would have thought if he had known what his finery was doomed to come to--fades from before me, and I nod, and sleep.", "또다시 그 작은 방이 — 모서리에 열린 찬장이며, 등받이가 네모난 의자들이며, 위층 방으로 이어지는 모난 작은 계단이며, 벽난로 선반 위에 펼쳐 놓은 공작 깃털 세 개가 (처음 들어갔을 때, 만약 그 공작이 제 화려한 깃털이 이런 신세로 전락할 줄 알았더라면 무슨 생각을 했을까 궁금해했던 기억이 난다) 있던 그 방이 — 내 눈앞에서 흐려지고, 나는 고개를 끄덕이며 잠든다."], ["The flute becomes inaudible, the wheels of the coach are heard instead, and I am on my journey.", "플루트 소리는 들리지 않게 되고, 그 대신 마차 바퀴 소리가 들리며, 나는 여행길에 올라 있다."], ["The coach jolts, I wake with a start, and the flute has come back again, and the Master at Salem House is sitting with his legs crossed, playing it dolefully, while the old woman of the house looks on delighted.", "마차가 덜컹하고, 나는 화들짝 깨어나고, 플루트 소리는 다시 돌아와 있으며, 세일럼 하우스의 선생님은 다리를 꼰 채 앉아 구슬프게 그것을 불고, 그 집 노파는 황홀해하며 바라보고 있다."], ["She fades in her turn, and he fades, and all fades, and there is no flute, no Master, no Salem House, no David Copperfield, no anything but heavy sleep.", "이번에는 노파가 흐려지고, 그가 흐려지고, 모든 것이 흐려지더니, 플루트도 없고, 선생님도 없고, 세일럼 하우스도 없고, 데이비드 코퍼필드도 없고, 깊은 잠 말고는 아무것도 없게 된다."]], [["I dreamed, I thought, that once while he was blowing into this dismal flute, the old woman of the house, who had gone nearer and nearer to him in her ecstatic admiration, leaned over the back of his chair and gave him an affectionate squeeze round the neck, which stopped his playing for a moment.", "나는 이런 꿈을 꾼 것 같았다. 그가 이 음울한 플루트를 불고 있던 어느 순간, 황홀한 감탄에 젖어 그에게 점점 더 가까이 다가가던 그 집 노파가 의자 등받이 너머로 몸을 기울여 그의 목을 다정하게 꼭 끌어안았고, 그 바람에 그의 연주가 잠깐 멎었다는 꿈을. 나는 그때, 혹은 바로 그 직후에, 잠과 깸 사이의 어중간한 상태에 있었다."], ["I was in the middle state between sleeping and waking, either then or immediately afterwards; for, as he resumed--it was a real fact that he had stopped playing--I saw and heard the same old woman ask Mrs. Fibbitson if it wasn’t delicious (meaning the flute), to which Mrs.", "왜냐하면 그가 다시 연주를 시작했을 때 — 그가 연주를 멈췄던 것은 실제 있었던 일이다 — 나는 그 똑같은 노파가 피비슨 부인에게 (플루트를 가리키며) 황홀하지 않으냐고 묻는 것을 보고 들었고, 그러자 피비슨 부인이 \"아무렴, 아무렴! 그렇고말고!\""], ["Fibbitson replied, ‘Ay, ay! yes!’ and nodded at the fire: to which, I am persuaded, she gave the credit of the whole performance.", "하고 대답하며 불을 향해 고개를 끄덕이는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 나는 그 노파가 이 모든 연주의 공을 불에 돌렸다고 확신한다."]], [["When I seemed to have been dozing a long while, the Master at Salem House unscrewed his flute into the three pieces, put them up as before, and took me away.", "내가 오랫동안 꾸벅꾸벅 존 것 같았을 즈음, 세일럼 하우스의 선생님은 플루트를 세 토막으로 돌려 풀어 아까처럼 챙겨 넣고, 나를 데리고 떠났다."], ["We found the coach very near at hand, and got upon the roof; but I was so dead sleepy, that when we stopped on the road to take up somebody else, they put me inside where there were no passengers, and where I slept profoundly, until I found the coach going at a footpace up a steep hill among green leaves.", "우리는 아주 가까운 데서 마차를 찾아 지붕 위에 올라탔다. 그러나 나는 죽은 듯이 졸려서, 도중에 다른 사람을 태우려고 멈춰 섰을 때, 사람들이 손님이 아무도 없는 안쪽에 나를 들여놓았고, 나는 그 안에서 깊이 잠들었다가, 마차가 푸른 잎새들 사이로 가파른 언덕을 걸음 속도로 오르고 있을 때에야 정신을 차렸다."], ["Presently, it stopped, and had come to its destination.", "이윽고 마차가 멈췄고, 목적지에 다다른 것이었다."]], [["A short walk brought us--I mean the Master and me--to Salem House, which was enclosed with a high brick wall, and looked very dull.", "조금 걷자 우리는 — 그러니까 선생님과 나는 — 세일럼 하우스에 이르렀는데, 그곳은 높다란 벽돌 담으로 둘러싸여 있어 무척 음침해 보였다."], ["Over a door in this wall was a board with SALEM HOUSE upon it; and through a grating in this door we were surveyed when we rang the bell by a surly face, which I found, on the door being opened, belonged to a stout man with a bull-neck, a wooden leg, overhanging temples, and his hair cut close all round his head.", "이 담의 문 위에는 '세일럼 하우스'라고 쓰인 간판이 걸려 있었고, 우리가 종을 울리자 이 문의 쇠창살 너머로 어떤 무뚝뚝한 얼굴이 우리를 살펴보았는데, 문이 열리고 보니 그 얼굴은 황소 같은 목에, 나무 의족을 하고, 관자놀이가 툭 튀어나오고, 머리는 빙 둘러 짧게 깎은 건장한 사내의 것이었다."]], [["‘The new boy,’ said the Master.", "\"새로 온 애요.\" 선생님이 말했다."]], [["The man with the wooden leg eyed me all over--it didn’t take long, for there was not much of me--and locked the gate behind us, and took out the key.", "나무 의족을 한 사내는 나를 위아래로 훑어보았는데 — 나라는 게 별로 볼 것도 없어서 오래 걸리지는 않았다 — 우리 뒤로 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 빼냈다."], ["We were going up to the house, among some dark heavy trees, when he called after my conductor.", "우리가 어둡고 묵직한 나무들 사이로 집을 향해 올라가고 있을 때, 그가 내 인솔자를 뒤에서 불렀다."], ["‘Hallo!’", "\"이봐요!\""]], [["We looked back, and he was standing at the door of a little lodge, where he lived, with a pair of boots in his hand.", "우리가 뒤돌아보니, 그는 자기가 사는 작은 수위실 문 앞에 부츠 한 켤레를 손에 들고 서 있었다."]], [["‘Here!", "\"여기!"], ["The cobbler’s been,’ he said, ‘since you’ve been out, Mr.", "구두장이가 다녀갔소.\" 그가 말했다."], ["Mell, and he says he can’t mend ‘em any more.", "\"당신 나가 있는 동안에요, 멜 선생."], ["He says there ain’t a bit of the original boot left, and he wonders you expect it.’", "이젠 더 못 고치겠다는데. 본래 부츠는 한 조각도 안 남았다고, 당신이 그걸 바라는 게 신기하다더군.\""]], [["With these words he threw the boots towards Mr. Mell, who went back a few paces to pick them up, and looked at them (very disconsolately, I was afraid), as we went on together.", "이 말과 함께 그는 부츠를 멜 선생 쪽으로 휙 던졌고, 멜 선생은 몇 걸음 되돌아가 그것을 주워, 우리가 함께 걸어가는 동안 (몹시 풀이 죽은 채로, 내겐 그렇게 보였다) 그것을 바라보았다."], ["I observed then, for the first time, that the boots he had on were a good deal the worse for wear, and that his stocking was just breaking out in one place, like a bud.", "그제야 나는 처음으로, 그가 신고 있는 부츠가 꽤 닳아 빠졌다는 것, 그리고 그의 양말이 한 군데에서 마치 꽃봉오리처럼 막 터져 나오고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["Salem House was a square brick building with wings; of a bare and unfurnished appearance.", "세일럼 하우스는 양 날개가 달린 네모난 벽돌 건물이었는데, 휑하고 휘휑한 모습이었다."], ["All about it was so very quiet, that I said to Mr. Mell I supposed the boys were out; but he seemed surprised at my not knowing that it was holiday-time.", "그 둘레가 어찌나 고요하던지, 나는 멜 선생에게 아이들이 다 나가 있나 보다고 말했다."], ["That all the boys were at their several homes. That Mr.", "그러나 그는 지금이 방학 때라는 걸 내가 모른다는 사실에 놀란 듯했다."], ["Creakle, the proprietor, was down by the sea-side with Mrs. and Miss Creakle; and that I was sent in holiday-time as a punishment for my misdoing, all of which he explained to me as we went along.", "아이들이 모두 저마다 집에 가 있다는 것, 학교 주인인 크리클 선생은 크리클 부인과 크리클 양과 함께 바닷가에 가 있다는 것, 그리고 내가 잘못을 저지른 벌로 방학 때 보내졌다는 것을, 그는 우리가 걸어가는 동안 죄다 설명해 주었다."]], [["I gazed upon the schoolroom into which he took me, as the most forlorn and desolate place I had ever seen. I see it now.", "그가 나를 데리고 들어간 교실을, 나는 그때껏 본 가운데 가장 쓸쓸하고 황량한 곳이라며 바라보았다. 지금도 눈에 선하다."], ["A long room with three long rows of desks, and six of forms, and bristling all round with pegs for hats and slates.", "길쭉한 책상이 세 줄, 긴 의자가 여섯 줄 놓인 기다란 방인데, 사방으로 모자와 석판을 거는 못이 빙 둘러 삐죽삐죽 돋아 있었다."], ["Scraps of old copy-books and exercises litter the dirty floor. Some silkworms’ houses, made of the same materials, are scattered over the desks.", "너덜너덜한 옛 습자 공책과 연습장 조각들이 지저분한 바닥에 어지러이 널려 있었다. 같은 재료로 만든 누에 집 몇 개가 책상 위에 흩어져 있었다."], ["Two miserable little white mice, left behind by their owner, are running up and down in a fusty castle made of pasteboard and wire, looking in all the corners with their red eyes for anything to eat.", "주인이 두고 간 가엾은 흰 생쥐 두 마리가, 판지와 철사로 만든 곰팡내 나는 성채 안을 위아래로 오르내리며, 빨간 눈으로 먹을 게 없나 구석구석 살피고 있었다."], ["A bird, in a cage very little bigger than himself, makes a mournful rattle now and then in hopping on his perch, two inches high, or dropping from it; but neither sings nor chirps.", "새장 하나는 그 안의 새보다 별로 크지도 않았는데, 그 새는 이따금 두 인치 높이의 횃대에 폴짝 뛰어오르거나 거기서 뛰어내리면서 구슬픈 소리를 달그락 냈지만, 노래하지도 지저귀지도 않았다."], ["There is a strange unwholesome smell upon the room, like mildewed corduroys, sweet apples wanting air, and rotten books.", "그 방에는 곰팡이 핀 코듀로이며, 바람을 못 쐰 단 사과며, 썩은 책 냄새 같은, 이상하고 퀴퀴한 냄새가 배어 있었다."], ["There could not well be more ink splashed about it, if it had been roofless from its first construction, and the skies had rained, snowed, hailed, and blown ink through the varying seasons of the year.", "그곳에 잉크가 그보다 더 튀어 있을 수는 없었으니, 마치 처음 지어질 때부터 지붕이 없어, 일 년 사철 내내 하늘에서 잉크가 비처럼, 눈처럼, 우박처럼 쏟아지고 잉크 바람이 분 것만 같았다."]], [["Mr. Mell having left me while he took his irreparable boots upstairs, I went softly to the upper end of the room, observing all this as I crept along.", "멜 선생이 고칠 수 없게 된 부츠를 들고 위층으로 올라가며 나를 두고 간 사이, 나는 방의 위쪽 끝으로 살금살금 걸어가, 기어가듯 다가가면서 이 모든 것을 살펴보았다."], ["Suddenly I came upon a pasteboard placard, beautifully written, which was lying on the desk, and bore these words: ‘TAKE CARE OF HIM.", "그러다가 문득, 책상 위에 놓여 있던 한 판지 푯말과 마주쳤는데, 거기에는 아름다운 글씨로 이런 말이 적혀 있었다. \"이 아이를 조심할 것."], ["HE BITES.’", "무는 버릇이 있음.\""]], [["I got upon the desk immediately, apprehensive of at least a great dog underneath.", "나는 적어도 책상 밑에 커다란 개가 있겠거니 겁이 나서, 곧장 책상 위로 올라갔다."], ["But, though I looked all round with anxious eyes, I could see nothing of him.", "그러나 불안한 눈으로 사방을 둘러봐도, 개라고는 도무지 보이지 않았다."], ["I was still engaged in peering about, when Mr. Mell came back, and asked me what I did up there?", "내가 아직도 두리번거리고 있을 때, 멜 선생이 돌아와서 거기서 뭘 하느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ says I, ‘if you please, I’m looking for the dog.’", "\"죄송해요, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"저, 개를 찾고 있는데요.\""]], [["‘Dog?’ he says.", "\"개?\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘What dog?’", "\"무슨 개?\""]], [["‘Isn’t it a dog, sir?’", "\"개 아닌가요, 선생님?\""]], [["‘Isn’t what a dog?’", "\"뭐가 개라는 거냐?\""]], [["‘That’s to be taken care of, sir; that bites.’", "\"조심하라는 거요, 선생님. 문다는 거요.\""]], [["‘No, Copperfield,’ says he, gravely, ‘that’s not a dog.", "\"아니다, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 진지하게 말했다. \"그건 개가 아니야."], ["That’s a boy.", "그건 아이다."], ["My instructions are, Copperfield, to put this placard on your back.", "코퍼필드, 내가 받은 지시는 이 푯말을 네 등에 붙이라는 거다."], ["I am sorry to make such a beginning with you, but I must do it.’ With that he took me down, and tied the placard, which was neatly constructed for the purpose, on my shoulders like a knapsack; and wherever I went, afterwards, I had the consolation of carrying it.", "너와 이런 식으로 시작하게 되어 미안하지만, 나는 그렇게 해야만 한다.\" 그러면서 그는 나를 책상에서 내려놓고, 그 목적에 맞게 깔끔하게 만들어진 그 푯말을, 배낭처럼 내 어깨에 묶었다. 그리고 그 후로 어디를 가든, 나는 그것을 짊어지고 다니는 위안을 누리게 되었다."]], [["What I suffered from that placard, nobody can imagine. Whether it was possible for people to see me or not, I always fancied that somebody was reading it.", "그 푯말 때문에 내가 어떤 고통을 겪었는지는 아무도 상상하지 못할 것이다. 사람들이 나를 볼 수 있든 없든, 나는 늘 누군가 그걸 읽고 있다는 상상에 시달렸다."], ["It was no relief to turn round and find nobody; for wherever my back was, there I imagined somebody always to be.", "뒤를 돌아봐 아무도 없다는 걸 알아도 마음이 놓이지 않았으니, 내 등이 가는 곳마다 거기에는 늘 누군가 있으리라고 상상했기 때문이다."], ["That cruel man with the wooden leg aggravated my sufferings.", "그 나무 의족을 한 잔인한 사내는 내 고통을 더 키웠다."], ["He was in authority; and if he ever saw me leaning against a tree, or a wall, or the house, he roared out from his lodge door in a stupendous voice, ‘Hallo, you sir!", "그는 권한을 쥐고 있었는데, 내가 나무나 담벼락이나 건물에 기대 있는 걸 볼 때면 언제든 자기 수위실 문에서 어마어마한 목소리로 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"이봐, 거기 너! 너 코퍼필드!"], ["You Copperfield!", "그 표를 잘 보이게 내놓지 않으면 일러바칠 테다!\""], ["Show that badge conspicuous, or I’ll report you!’ The playground was a bare gravelled yard, open to all the back of the house and the offices; and I knew that the servants read it, and the butcher read it, and the baker read it; that everybody, in a word, who came backwards and forwards to the house, of a morning when I was ordered to walk there, read that I was to be taken care of, for I bit, I recollect that I positively began to have a dread of myself, as a kind of wild boy who did bite.", "운동장은 휑한 자갈 마당으로, 집 뒤편과 부속 건물들이 죄다 내다보였다. 그래서 나는 하인들도 그걸 읽고, 푸주한도 그걸 읽고, 빵장수도 그걸 읽는다는 것을, 한마디로 내가 거기서 걸으라는 명을 받은 아침에 그 집을 드나드는 사람이라면 누구나, 내가 무니까 조심해야 한다고 적힌 그것을 읽는다는 것을 알았다. 기억하건대 나는, 정말로 무는 버릇이 있는 일종의 야생 소년이라며, 나 자신을 두려워하기 시작했다."]], [["There was an old door in this playground, on which the boys had a custom of carving their names.", "이 운동장에는 낡은 문이 하나 있었는데, 아이들은 거기에 자기 이름을 새기는 풍습이 있었다."], ["It was completely covered with such inscriptions.", "그 문은 그런 새김 글씨로 빈틈없이 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["In my dread of the end of the vacation and their coming back, I could not read a boy’s name, without inquiring in what tone and with what emphasis HE would read, ‘Take care of him.", "방학이 끝나고 아이들이 돌아올 일이 두려운 나머지, 나는 한 아이의 이름을 읽으면서, 그 애가 어떤 말투로, 어떤 강세를 실어 \"이 아이를 조심할 것. 무는 버릇이 있음\"을 읽을까 떠올려 보지 않고는 견딜 수 없었다."], ["He bites.’ There was one boy--a certain J. Steerforth--who cut his name very deep and very often, who, I conceived, would read it in a rather strong voice, and afterwards pull my hair.", "한 아이는 — J. 스티어포스라는 아이는 — 자기 이름을 아주 깊게, 아주 자주 새겨 놓았는데, 나는 그 애가 그것을 다소 우렁찬 목소리로 읽고 나서 내 머리카락을 잡아당기리라고 생각했다."], ["There was another boy, one Tommy Traddles, who I dreaded would make game of it, and pretend to be dreadfully frightened of me.", "또 다른 아이로 토미 트래들스라는 애가 있었는데, 나는 그 애가 그것을 가지고 장난치며 나를 끔찍이 무서워하는 척하지나 않을까 겁이 났다."], ["There was a third, George Demple, who I fancied would sing it.", "세 번째로 조지 뎀플이라는 애가 있었는데, 나는 그 애가 그것을 노래로 부를 것 같았다."], ["I have looked, a little shrinking creature, at that door, until the owners of all the names--there were five-and-forty of them in the school then, Mr.", "나는 잔뜩 움츠러든 작은 존재가 되어 그 문을 바라보았는데, 마침내 그 모든 이름의 주인들이 — 멜 선생 말로는 그때 학교에 마흔다섯 명이 있었다 — 다 함께 환호하며 나를 따돌리고, 저마다 제 방식대로 \"이 아이를 조심할 것."], ["Mell said--seemed to send me to Coventry by general acclamation, and to cry out, each in his own way, ‘Take care of him. He bites!’", "무는 버릇이 있음!\" 하고 외쳐 대는 것만 같았다."]], [["It was the same with the places at the desks and forms.", "책상과 의자의 자리들도 마찬가지였다."], ["It was the same with the groves of deserted bedsteads I peeped at, on my way to, and when I was in, my own bed.", "내 침대로 가는 길에, 그리고 내 침대에 누워서 슬쩍 들여다본, 텅 빈 침대들이 늘어선 그 숲도 마찬가지였다. 기억하건대 나는 밤이면 밤마다 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["I remember dreaming night after night, of being with my mother as she used to be, or of going to a party at Mr. Peggotty’s, or of travelling outside the stage-coach, or of dining again with my unfortunate friend the waiter, and in all these circumstances making people scream and stare, by the unhappy disclosure that I had nothing on but my little night-shirt, and that placard.", "예전 모습 그대로인 어머니와 함께 있는 꿈, 패거티 씨 집 잔치에 가는 꿈, 역마차 바깥쪽에 타고 가는 꿈, 나의 불운한 친구 웨이터와 다시 식사하는 꿈을. 그런데 이 모든 상황에서, 나는 내가 조그만 잠옷 하나에 그 푯말 말고는 아무것도 걸치지 않았다는 불행한 사실을 들통내는 바람에, 사람들이 비명을 지르며 빤히 쳐다보게 만들었다."]], [["In the monotony of my life, and in my constant apprehension of the re-opening of the school, it was such an insupportable affliction!", "단조로운 나의 일상 속에서, 그리고 학교가 다시 열린다는 끊임없는 두려움 속에서, 그것은 정말이지 견딜 수 없는 괴로움이었다!"], ["I had long tasks every day to do with Mr.", "나는 날마다 멜 선생과 함께 긴 과제를 해야 했다."], ["Mell; but I did them, there being no Mr. and Miss Murdstone here, and got through them without disgrace.", "그러나 여기에는 머드스톤 씨도 머드스톤 양도 없었기에, 나는 그 과제를 해냈고, 망신당하는 일 없이 마쳤다."], ["Before, and after them, I walked about--supervised, as I have mentioned, by the man with the wooden leg.", "과제 전후로 나는 이리저리 거닐었는데 — 앞서 말했듯 나무 의족을 한 사내의 감시를 받으며 그랬다."], ["How vividly I call to mind the damp about the house, the green cracked flagstones in the court, an old leaky water-butt, and the discoloured trunks of some of the grim trees, which seemed to have dripped more in the rain than other trees, and to have blown less in the sun!", "집 둘레의 축축함, 안마당의 금이 가고 푸르스름한 포석들, 물이 새는 낡은 빗물통, 그리고 음산한 나무들 중 몇 그루의 색이 바랜 줄기 — 다른 나무들보다 비를 더 많이 맞아 물이 뚝뚝 떨어지고, 햇볕은 덜 쬔 듯한 그 줄기들이 얼마나 생생하게 떠오르는지!"], ["At one we dined, Mr. Mell and I, at the upper end of a long bare dining-room, full of deal tables, and smelling of fat.", "한 시가 되면 멜 선생과 나는 식사를 했는데, 전나무 식탁이 가득 들어차고 비계 냄새가 풍기는 길쭉하고 휑한 식당의 위쪽 끝에서였다."], ["Then, we had more tasks until tea, which Mr. Mell drank out of a blue teacup, and I out of a tin pot.", "그러고 나서 차 마실 시간까지 또 과제를 했는데, 차는 멜 선생은 파란 찻잔으로, 나는 양철 주전자로 마셨다."], ["All day long, and until seven or eight in the evening, Mr. Mell, at his own detached desk in the schoolroom, worked hard with pen, ink, ruler, books, and writing-paper, making out the bills (as I found) for last half-year.", "온종일, 그리고 저녁 일고여덟 시까지, 멜 선생은 교실 안 자기만의 외딴 책상에서, 펜과 잉크와 자와 책과 종이를 가지고 부지런히 일했는데, (내가 알아낸 바로는) 지난 학기 청구서를 작성하는 것이었다."], ["When he had put up his things for the night he took out his flute, and blew at it, until I almost thought he would gradually blow his whole being into the large hole at the top, and ooze away at the keys.", "밤에 물건들을 챙겨 넣고 나면 그는 플루트를 꺼내 불었는데, 어찌나 부는지 나는 거의, 그가 차츰차츰 자기 온 존재를 그 꼭대기 큰 구멍 속으로 불어 넣어, 음 구멍들로 스르르 새어 나가 버리는 게 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들었다."]], [["I picture my small self in the dimly-lighted rooms, sitting with my head upon my hand, listening to the doleful performance of Mr. Mell, and conning tomorrow’s lessons.", "희미한 불빛 아래 방에서, 한 손에 머리를 괴고 앉아, 멜 선생의 구슬픈 연주에 귀 기울이며, 내일 수업을 외우고 있는 어린 내 모습이 그려진다."], ["I picture myself with my books shut up, still listening to the doleful performance of Mr. Mell, and listening through it to what used to be at home, and to the blowing of the wind on Yarmouth flats, and feeling very sad and solitary.", "책을 덮어 놓고도, 여전히 멜 선생의 구슬픈 연주에 귀 기울이며, 그 소리 너머로 예전 집에 있던 것들에, 야머스 갯벌에 불던 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이면서, 몹시 슬프고 외로워하던 내 모습이 그려진다."], ["I picture myself going up to bed, among the unused rooms, and sitting on my bed-side crying for a comfortable word from Peggotty.", "아무도 쓰지 않는 방들 사이로 잠자리에 올라가, 침대 곁에 걸터앉아 패거티의 다정한 말 한마디가 그리워 울던 내 모습이 그려진다."], ["I picture myself coming downstairs in the morning, and looking through a long ghastly gash of a staircase window at the school-bell hanging on the top of an out-house with a weathercock above it; and dreading the time when it shall ring J.", "아침이면 아래층으로 내려와, 길쭉하고 으스스하게 째진 틈 같은 계단 창문 너머로, 풍향계가 얹힌 헛간 꼭대기에 매달린 학교 종을 바라보던 내 모습이 그려진다. 그러면서 그 종이 J."], ["Steerforth and the rest to work: which is only second, in my foreboding apprehensions, to the time when the man with the wooden leg shall unlock the rusty gate to give admission to the awful Mr. Creakle.", "스티어포스와 나머지 아이들을 공부하라고 울려 댈 때를 두려워하던 내 모습이. 그 두려움은 나의 불길한 걱정 속에서, 나무 의족을 한 사내가 녹슨 문을 열어 무시무시한 크리클 선생을 들여보낼 때를 두려워하는 것에 버금가는 것이었다."], ["I cannot think I was a very dangerous character in any of these aspects, but in all of them I carried the same warning on my back.", "이 어느 면으로 보나 내가 그리 위험한 인물이었다고는 생각할 수 없지만, 그 모든 면에서 나는 같은 경고문을 등에 짊어지고 다녔다."]], [["Mr. Mell never said much to me, but he was never harsh to me.", "멜 선생은 나에게 말을 많이 거는 법이 없었지만, 결코 모질게 대하지도 않았다."], ["I suppose we were company to each other, without talking.", "우리는 말없이도 서로에게 동무가 되어 주었던 셈이다."], ["I forgot to mention that he would talk to himself sometimes, and grin, and clench his fist, and grind his teeth, and pull his hair in an unaccountable manner.", "깜빡 말하지 못했는데, 그는 가끔 혼잣말을 하며 씩 웃고, 주먹을 불끈 쥐고, 이를 갈고, 영문 모를 모습으로 머리카락을 쥐어뜯곤 했다."], ["But he had these peculiarities: and at first they frightened me, though I soon got used to them.", "하지만 그에게는 이런 별난 버릇들이 있었고, 처음에는 그것들이 나를 겁먹게 했으나, 나는 이내 거기에 익숙해졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER 6. I ENLARGE MY CIRCLE OF ACQUAINTANCE", "# CHAPTER 6. I ENLARGE MY CIRCLE OF ACQUAINTANCE"]], [["I HAD led this life about a month, when the man with the wooden leg began to stump about with a mop and a bucket of water, from which I inferred that preparations were making to receive Mr.", "이런 생활을 한 달쯤 했을 무렵, 나무다리를 단 사내가 대걸레와 물통을 들고 쿵쿵 돌아다니기 시작했고, 나는 거기서 크리클 선생님과 학생들을 맞이할 채비가 시작되었구나 하고 짐작했다."], ["Creakle and the boys.", "내 짐작은 틀리지 않았다."], ["I was not mistaken; for the mop came into the schoolroom before long, and turned out Mr. Mell and me, who lived where we could, and got on how we could, for some days, during which we were always in the way of two or three young women, who had rarely shown themselves before, and were so continually in the midst of dust that I sneezed almost as much as if Salem House had been a great snuff-box.", "머잖아 대걸레가 교실로 들이닥쳐 멜 선생님과 나를 쫓아냈고, 우리는 며칠 동안 머물 수 있는 곳에 머물고 지낼 수 있는 대로 지냈는데, 그동안 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않던 젊은 여자 두세 명이 늘 우리 앞을 가로막고 있었고, 그들은 어찌나 줄곧 먼지 한가운데 있던지 나는 세일럼 하우스가 무슨 커다란 코담뱃갑이라도 되는 양 줄기차게 재채기를 해 댔다."]], [["One day I was informed by Mr. Mell that Mr. Creakle would be home that evening.", "어느 날 멜 선생님에게서 크리클 선생님이 그날 저녁에 돌아오신다는 말을 들었다."], ["In the evening, after tea, I heard that he was come.", "저녁에 차를 마시고 난 뒤, 나는 그분이 도착했다는 소식을 들었다."], ["Before bedtime, I was fetched by the man with the wooden leg to appear before him.", "잠자리에 들기 전에, 나는 나무다리를 단 사내에게 이끌려 그분 앞에 불려 갔다."]], [["Mr. Creakle’s part of the house was a good deal more comfortable than ours, and he had a snug bit of garden that looked pleasant after the dusty playground, which was such a desert in miniature, that I thought no one but a camel, or a dromedary, could have felt at home in it.", "크리클 선생님이 쓰는 집 쪽은 우리 쪽보다 훨씬 안락했고, 그분에게는 아담하고 아늑한 정원이 딸려 있었는데, 그것은 먼지투성이 운동장 다음이라 더욱 보기 좋았다. 그 운동장은 어찌나 작은 사막 같던지, 나는 낙타나 단봉낙타가 아니고서는 그 안에서 편안할 사람이 없겠다고 생각했다."], ["It seemed to me a bold thing even to take notice that the passage looked comfortable, as I went on my way, trembling, to Mr.", "떨면서 크리클 선생님이 계신 곳으로 가는 길에, 복도가 안락해 보인다는 것을 알아챈 것조차 내게는 어쩐지 대담한 짓처럼 느껴졌다."], ["Creakle’s presence: which so abashed me, when I was ushered into it, that I hardly saw Mrs. Creakle or Miss Creakle (who were both there, in the parlour), or anything but Mr. Creakle, a stout gentleman with a bunch of watch-chain and seals, in an arm-chair, with a tumbler and bottle beside him.", "그래서 안으로 안내받아 들어갔을 때 나는 어찌나 주눅이 들었던지, (둘 다 거실에 있던) 크리클 부인이나 크리클 양은 거의 보이지도 않았고, 시곗줄과 인장이 한 다발 달린, 안락의자에 앉아 곁에 텀블러와 술병을 둔 뚱뚱한 신사 크리클 선생님 말고는 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았다."]], [["‘So!’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"그래!\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘This is the young gentleman whose teeth are to be filed!", "\"이놈이 그 이빨을 줄로 갈아 줘야 한다는 어린 신사로구먼!"], ["Turn him round.’", "돌려 봐.\""]], [["The wooden-legged man turned me about so as to exhibit the placard; and having afforded time for a full survey of it, turned me about again, with my face to Mr. Creakle, and posted himself at Mr. Creakle’s side. Mr.", "나무다리를 단 사내가 나를 빙 돌려 등에 붙은 팻말을 내보였고, 그것을 찬찬히 살펴볼 시간을 충분히 준 다음, 다시 나를 돌려 크리클 선생님 쪽으로 얼굴을 향하게 하고는, 자기는 크리클 선생님 곁에 가서 섰다."], ["Creakle’s face was fiery, and his eyes were small, and deep in his head; he had thick veins in his forehead, a little nose, and a large chin.", "크리클 선생님의 얼굴은 불그죽죽했고, 두 눈은 작고 움푹 들어가 있었다. 이마에는 굵은 핏줄이 서 있고, 코는 자그마하고, 턱은 큼지막했다."], ["He was bald on the top of his head; and had some thin wet-looking hair that was just turning grey, brushed across each temple, so that the two sides interlaced on his forehead.", "정수리는 벗어졌고, 막 희끗희끗해지기 시작한 가늘고 축축해 보이는 머리카락 몇 가닥을 양쪽 관자놀이 너머로 빗어 넘겨, 양쪽이 이마에서 서로 엇갈려 있었다."], ["But the circumstance about him which impressed me most, was, that he had no voice, but spoke in a whisper.", "그러나 그분에게서 나에게 가장 강한 인상을 남긴 점은, 목소리가 없어서 속삭이듯 말한다는 것이었다."], ["The exertion this cost him, or the consciousness of talking in that feeble way, made his angry face so much more angry, and his thick veins so much thicker, when he spoke, that I am not surprised, on looking back, at this peculiarity striking me as his chief one.", "그렇게 말하느라 들이는 안간힘 때문인지, 아니면 그토록 힘없는 식으로 말한다는 의식 때문인지, 그분이 입을 열 때면 성난 얼굴은 한층 더 성나 보였고 굵은 핏줄은 한층 더 굵어졌으니, 지금 돌이켜 봐도 이 별난 점이 그분의 가장 큰 특징으로 내게 박힌 것이 놀랍지 않다. \"자,\""], ["‘Now,’ said Mr. Creakle.", "크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘What’s the report of this boy?’", "\"이놈에 대한 보고는 뭐냐?\""]], [["‘There’s nothing against him yet,’ returned the man with the wooden leg.", "\"아직은 이놈에게 불리한 게 없습니다.\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 대답했다."], ["‘There has been no opportunity.’", "\"그럴 기회가 없었으니까요.\""]], [["I thought Mr. Creakle was disappointed. I thought Mrs.", "내 생각에 크리클 선생님은 실망한 것 같았다."], ["and Miss Creakle (at whom I now glanced for the first time, and who were, both, thin and quiet) were not disappointed.", "그리고 (그제야 처음으로 흘끗 본, 둘 다 마르고 조용한) 크리클 부인과 크리클 양은 실망하지 않은 것 같았다."]], [["‘Come here, sir!’ said Mr. Creakle, beckoning to me.", "\"이리 오너라, 이놈아!\" 크리클 선생님이 나에게 손짓하며 말했다."]], [["‘Come here!’ said the man with the wooden leg, repeating the gesture.", "\"이리 와!\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 그 손짓을 따라 하며 말했다."]], [["‘I have the happiness of knowing your father-in-law,’ whispered Mr.", "\"나는 다행히 네 양아버지를 잘 안다.\""], ["Creakle, taking me by the ear; ‘and a worthy man he is, and a man of a strong character.", "크리클 선생님이 내 귀를 잡으며 속삭였다. \"참 훌륭한 분이고, 강단 있는 분이지."], ["He knows me, and I know him.", "그분은 나를 알고, 나는 그분을 알아."], ["Do YOU know me? Hey?’ said Mr.", "너는 나를 아느냐? 응?\""], ["Creakle, pinching my ear with ferocious playfulness.", "크리클 선생님이 사납게 장난치듯 내 귀를 비틀며 말했다."]], [["‘Not yet, sir,’ I said, flinching with the pain.", "\"아직 모릅니다, 선생님.\" 나는 아파서 움찔하며 말했다."]], [["‘Not yet? Hey?’ repeated Mr.", "\"아직 몰라? 응?\" 크리클 선생님이 되받았다."], ["Creakle. ‘But you will soon. Hey?’", "\"하지만 곧 알게 될 거다. 응?\""]], [["‘You will soon. Hey?’ repeated the man with the wooden leg.", "\"곧 알게 될 거다. 응?\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 되풀이했다."], ["I afterwards found that he generally acted, with his strong voice, as Mr. Creakle’s interpreter to the boys.", "나중에 알게 된 일이지만, 그는 보통 자기의 우렁찬 목소리로 학생들에게 크리클 선생님의 말을 통역하는 역할을 했다."]], [["I was very much frightened, and said, I hoped so, if he pleased.", "나는 몹시 겁이 나서, 부디 그러길 바란다고 말했다."], ["I felt, all this while, as if my ear were blazing; he pinched it so hard.", "그러는 내내 귀가 활활 타오르는 것만 같았다. 그분이 어찌나 세게 꼬집었던지."]], [["‘I’ll tell you what I am,’ whispered Mr.", "\"내가 어떤 사람인지 일러 주마.\""], ["Creakle, letting it go at last, with a screw at parting that brought the water into my eyes. ‘I’m a Tartar.’", "크리클 선생님이 마침내 귀를 놓아주며 속삭였는데, 헤어지면서 한 번 비트는 통에 내 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌았다. \"나는 타타르 사람이야.\""]], [["‘A Tartar,’ said the man with the wooden leg.", "\"타타르 사람이지.\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘When I say I’ll do a thing, I do it,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 어떤 일을 하겠다고 하면, 나는 그걸 한다.\""], ["Creakle; ‘and when I say I will have a thing done, I will have it done.’", "크리클 선생님이 말했다. \"그리고 어떤 일을 시키겠다고 하면, 나는 기어이 그걸 시키지.\""]], [["‘--Will have a thing done, I will have it done,’ repeated the man with the wooden leg.", "\"—어떤 일을 시키겠다고 하면, 기어이 그걸 시키지.\" 나무다리를 단 사내가 되풀이했다."]], [["‘I am a determined character,’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"나는 마음먹은 대로 하는 사람이야.\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘That’s what I am.", "\"그게 나라는 사람이다."], ["I do my duty. That’s what I do.", "나는 내 할 일을 한다."], ["My flesh and blood’--he looked at Mrs. Creakle as he said this--‘when it rises against me, is not my flesh and blood.", "그게 내가 하는 일이야. 내 살붙이라도\"—이 말을 하면서 그분은 크리클 부인을 바라보았다—\"나에게 거역하면, 그건 내 살붙이가 아니야."], ["I discard it.", "나는 내쳐 버려."], ["Has that fellow’--to the man with the wooden leg--‘been here again?’", "그 녀석이\"—나무다리를 단 사내에게—\"또 여기 왔던 거냐?\""]], [["‘No,’ was the answer.", "\"아니요.\" 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["‘No,’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"아니라고.\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘He knows better.", "\"그놈은 분수를 알지."], ["He knows me.", "나를 잘 알아."], ["Let him keep away.", "얼씬도 못 하게 둬."], ["I say let him keep away,’ said Mr.", "얼씬도 못 하게 두라고 했다.\""], ["Creakle, striking his hand upon the table, and looking at Mrs.", "크리클 선생님이 손으로 탁자를 내리치며, 크리클 부인을 바라보면서 말했다."], ["Creakle, ‘for he knows me.", "\"그놈은 나를 잘 아니까."], ["Now you have begun to know me too, my young friend, and you may go.", "자, 너도 이제 나를 좀 알기 시작했겠다, 어린 친구야, 그만 가도 좋아."], ["Take him away.’", "데리고 나가.\""]], [["I was very glad to be ordered away, for Mrs.", "나는 가도 좋다는 명령이 무척 반가웠다."], ["and Miss Creakle were both wiping their eyes, and I felt as uncomfortable for them as I did for myself.", "크리클 부인과 크리클 양이 둘 다 눈물을 훔치고 있어서, 나는 나 자신만큼이나 그분들이 안쓰러워 마음이 불편했기 때문이다."], ["But I had a petition on my mind which concerned me so nearly, that I couldn’t help saying, though I wondered at my own courage:", "하지만 나에게는 나와 너무도 깊이 얽힌 청이 하나 마음에 걸려 있어서, 내 스스로도 그 용기에 놀라면서도, 끝내 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘If you please, sir--’", "\"저, 선생님—\""]], [["Mr. Creakle whispered, ‘Hah!", "크리클 선생님이 속삭였다. \"허!"], ["What’s this?’ and bent his eyes upon me, as if he would have burnt me up with them.", "이게 뭐냐?\" 그러고는 두 눈으로 나를 태워 버리기라도 할 듯 노려보았다."]], [["‘If you please, sir,’ I faltered, ‘if I might be allowed (I am very sorry indeed, sir, for what I did) to take this writing off, before the boys come back--’", "\"저, 선생님,\" 나는 더듬거렸다. \"혹시 허락해 주신다면 (제가 한 짓에 대해 정말로 죄송합니다, 선생님), 다른 애들이 돌아오기 전에 이 글씨를 떼 버릴 수 있게—\""]], [["Whether Mr. Creakle was in earnest, or whether he only did it to frighten me, I don’t know, but he made a burst out of his chair, before which I precipitately retreated, without waiting for the escort of the man with the wooden leg, and never once stopped until I reached my own bedroom, where, finding I was not pursued, I went to bed, as it was time, and lay quaking, for a couple of hours.", "크리클 선생님이 진심이었는지, 아니면 그저 나를 겁주려고 그런 것인지는 모르겠으나, 그분은 의자에서 벌떡 몸을 일으켰고, 나는 그 앞에서 나무다리를 단 사내의 호위를 기다릴 새도 없이 부랴부랴 도망쳐, 내 침실에 다다를 때까지 단 한 번도 멈추지 않았다. 그곳에서 뒤따라오는 사람이 없는 것을 확인하고는, 마침 잠잘 시각이라 잠자리에 들어, 두어 시간을 벌벌 떨며 누워 있었다."]], [["Next morning Mr. Sharp came back. Mr.", "이튿날 아침 샤프 선생님이 돌아왔다."], ["Sharp was the first master, and superior to Mr. Mell. Mr.", "샤프 선생님은 수석 선생님으로, 멜 선생님보다 윗자리였다."], ["Mell took his meals with the boys, but Mr. Sharp dined and supped at Mr. Creakle’s table.", "멜 선생님은 학생들과 함께 끼니를 먹었지만, 샤프 선생님은 크리클 선생님의 식탁에서 점심과 저녁을 들었다."], ["He was a limp, delicate-looking gentleman, I thought, with a good deal of nose, and a way of carrying his head on one side, as if it were a little too heavy for him.", "내가 보기에 그분은 흐느적거리고 가냘파 보이는 신사로, 코가 꽤 컸고, 머리가 자기에게 좀 무겁기라도 한 양 한쪽으로 기울이고 다니는 버릇이 있었다."], ["His hair was very smooth and wavy; but I was informed by the very first boy who came back that it was a wig (a second-hand one HE said), and that Mr. Sharp went out every Saturday afternoon to get it curled.", "머리카락은 아주 매끈하고 곱슬곱슬했지만, 맨 먼저 돌아온 한 학생에게서 그게 가발이라는 (게다가 그 애 말로는 헌 가발이라는) 것과, 샤프 선생님이 토요일 오후마다 그것을 말러 나간다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["It was no other than Tommy Traddles who gave me this piece of intelligence.", "이 소식을 내게 일러 준 사람은 다름 아닌 토미 트래들스였다. 그가 맨 먼저 돌아온 학생이었다. 그는 자기 이름을 정문 오른쪽 모퉁이, 맨 위 빗장 위에서 찾을 수 있을 거라고 일러 주는 것으로 자기소개를 대신했다."], ["He was the first boy who returned.", "그래서 내가 \"트래들스?\""], ["He introduced himself by informing me that I should find his name on the right-hand corner of the gate, over the top-bolt; upon that I said, ‘Traddles?’ to which he replied, ‘The same,’ and then he asked me for a full account of myself and family.", "하고 묻자, 그는 \"바로 그거야\" 하고 대답하더니, 이내 나와 우리 집안에 대해 죄다 이야기해 보라고 했다."]], [["It was a happy circumstance for me that Traddles came back first.", "트래들스가 맨 먼저 돌아온 것은 나에게 다행스러운 일이었다."], ["He enjoyed my placard so much, that he saved me from the embarrassment of either disclosure or concealment, by presenting me to every other boy who came back, great or small, immediately on his arrival, in this form of introduction, ‘Look here!", "그는 내 팻말을 어찌나 재미있어했던지, 돌아오는 학생마다, 크든 작든, 도착하자마자 곧장 나를 이런 식으로 소개함으로써, 나는 사실을 밝혀야 할지 숨겨야 할지 하는 곤혹스러움에서 벗어날 수 있었다. \"이거 봐!"], ["Here’s a game!’ Happily, too, the greater part of the boys came back low-spirited, and were not so boisterous at my expense as I had expected.", "여기 재미난 게 있어!\" 게다가 다행스럽게도 학생들 대부분은 풀이 죽은 채 돌아왔고, 내 예상만큼 나를 가지고 떠들썩하게 굴지는 않았다."], ["Some of them certainly did dance about me like wild Indians, and the greater part could not resist the temptation of pretending that I was a dog, and patting and soothing me, lest I should bite, and saying, ‘Lie down, sir!’ and calling me Towzer.", "분명 몇몇은 야만 인디언처럼 내 주위에서 춤을 추었고, 대부분은 내가 개라도 되는 척하며 내가 물까 봐 토닥토닥 달래는 시늉을 하고는 \"엎드려!\" 하면서 나를 타우저라고 부르는 유혹을 떨치지 못했다."], ["This was naturally confusing, among so many strangers, and cost me some tears, but on the whole it was much better than I had anticipated.", "그 많은 낯선 사람들 틈에서 이것은 당연히 어리둥절한 노릇이었고 눈물도 좀 흘리게 했지만, 그래도 전체적으로는 내가 각오했던 것보다 훨씬 나았다."]], [["I was not considered as being formally received into the school, however, until J.", "* 타우저: 영국에서 개에게 흔히 붙이던 이름. 그렇지만 J."], ["Steerforth arrived. Before this boy, who was reputed to be a great scholar, and was very good-looking, and at least half-a-dozen years my senior, I was carried as before a magistrate.", "스티어포스가 도착하기 전까지는, 나는 이 학교에 정식으로 받아들여졌다고 여겨지지 않았다. 대단한 학자로 소문난 데다 아주 잘생기고 나보다 적어도 예닐곱 살은 위인 이 학생 앞으로, 나는 마치 치안판사 앞에 끌려가듯 인도되었다."], ["He inquired, under a shed in the playground, into the particulars of my punishment, and was pleased to express his opinion that it was ‘a jolly shame’; for which I became bound to him ever afterwards.", "그는 운동장의 헛간 아래에서 내가 받은 벌의 자초지종을 캐물었고, 그것이 \"참 고약한 짓\"이라는 의견을 기꺼이 밝혀 주었다. 그 일로 나는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그에게 마음으로 매이게 되었다."]], [["‘What money have you got, Copperfield?’ he said, walking aside with me when he had disposed of my affair in these terms.", "\"너 돈 얼마나 있어, 코퍼필드?\" 이런 식으로 내 일을 마무리 짓고 나서, 그가 나를 한쪽으로 데려가 걸으며 말했다."], ["I told him seven shillings.", "나는 칠 실링이라고 말했다."]], [["‘You had better give it to me to take care of,’ he said.", "\"나한테 맡겨서 간수하게 하는 게 좋을 거야.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘At least, you can if you like.", "\"뭐, 네가 원하면 말이지."], ["You needn’t if you don’t like.’", "싫으면 안 해도 되고.\""]], [["I hastened to comply with his friendly suggestion, and opening Peggotty’s purse, turned it upside down into his hand.", "나는 부랴부랴 그 다정한 제안을 따랐고, 패거티의 지갑을 열어 그의 손바닥에 거꾸로 탈탈 쏟았다."]], [["‘Do you want to spend anything now?’ he asked me.", "\"지금 뭐 사고 싶은 거 있어?\" 그가 나에게 물었다."]], [["‘No thank you,’ I replied.", "\"아니, 괜찮아.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘You can, if you like, you know,’ said Steerforth.", "\"원하면 사도 돼, 알지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Say the word.’", "\"말만 해.\""]], [["‘No, thank you, sir,’ I repeated.", "\"아니, 괜찮아.\" 나는 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Perhaps you’d like to spend a couple of shillings or so, in a bottle of currant wine by and by, up in the bedroom?’ said Steerforth.", "\"그럼 이따가 침실에 올라가서 까치밥나무주 한 병에다 한 두어 실링쯤 쓰는 건 어때?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘You belong to my bedroom, I find.’", "\"보니까 너 우리 침실 식구더라.\""]], [["It certainly had not occurred to me before, but I said, Yes, I should like that.", "전에는 미처 생각해 본 적도 없었지만, 나는 그래, 그러고 싶다고 말했다."]], [["‘Very good,’ said Steerforth.", "\"아주 좋아.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘You’ll be glad to spend another shilling or so, in almond cakes, I dare say?’", "\"아몬드 과자에도 한 실링쯤 더 쓰면 좋겠지, 안 그래?\""]], [["I said, Yes, I should like that, too.", "나는 그래, 그것도 좋겠다고 말했다."]], [["‘And another shilling or so in biscuits, and another in fruit, eh?’ said Steerforth.", "\"비스킷에 또 한 실링쯤, 과일에 또 한 실링쯤, 어때?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘I say, young Copperfield, you’re going it!’", "\"야, 어린 코퍼필드, 너 제대로 한턱내는구나!\""]], [["I smiled because he smiled, but I was a little troubled in my mind, too.", "그가 웃기에 나도 웃었지만, 마음 한구석은 조금 켕겼다."]], [["‘Well!’ said Steerforth.", "\"뭐!\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘We must make it stretch as far as we can; that’s all.", "\"할 수 있는 데까지 늘려 쓰면 되지, 그뿐이야."], ["I’ll do the best in my power for you.", "내가 너를 위해 힘닿는 데까지 해 볼게."], ["I can go out when I like, and I’ll smuggle the prog in.’ With these words he put the money in his pocket, and kindly told me not to make myself uneasy; he would take care it should be all right.", "나는 내키면 언제든 밖에 나갈 수 있으니까, 먹을 걸 몰래 들여올게.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 돈을 자기 주머니에 넣고는, 마음 졸이지 말라고, 다 잘되게 자기가 알아서 챙기겠다고 다정하게 일러 주었다. 그는 정말로 약속을 지켰다."], ["He was as good as his word, if that were all right which I had a secret misgiving was nearly all wrong--for I feared it was a waste of my mother’s two half-crowns--though I had preserved the piece of paper they were wrapped in: which was a precious saving.", "사실 나는 그게 대부분 잘못된 일이 아닐까 남몰래 께름칙해하면서도—어머니의 반 크라운짜리 두 닢을 헛되이 쓰는 게 아닐까 걱정스러웠으니까—그래도 그게 잘된 일이라고 친다면 말이다. 그래도 나는 그 돈을 쌌던 종잇조각만큼은 고이 간직해 두었으니, 그건 값진 절약이었다."], ["When we went upstairs to bed, he produced the whole seven shillings’ worth, and laid it out on my bed in the moonlight, saying:", "잠자리에 들려고 이층으로 올라갔을 때, 그는 칠 실링어치를 죄다 꺼내 달빛 아래 내 침대 위에 펼쳐 놓으며 말했다."]], [["‘There you are, young Copperfield, and a royal spread you’ve got.’", "\"자, 어린 코퍼필드, 임금님 잔칫상이 다 차려졌다.\""]], [["I couldn’t think of doing the honours of the feast, at my time of life, while he was by; my hand shook at the very thought of it.", "그가 곁에 있는데, 내 나이로는 도저히 잔치의 주인 노릇을 할 엄두가 나지 않았다. 그 생각만 해도 손이 다 떨렸다."], ["I begged him to do me the favour of presiding; and my request being seconded by the other boys who were in that room, he acceded to it, and sat upon my pillow, handing round the viands--with perfect fairness, I must say--and dispensing the currant wine in a little glass without a foot, which was his own property.", "나는 부디 좌장 노릇을 맡아 달라고 그에게 청했고, 그 방에 있던 다른 학생들이 내 청을 거들자, 그는 그것을 받아들여 내 베개 위에 앉아 음식을 나누어 주었다—참으로 공평하게, 이건 꼭 말해 두어야겠다—까치밥나무주는 자기 것인 굽 없는 작은 잔에 따라 돌렸다."], ["As to me, I sat on his left hand, and the rest were grouped about us, on the nearest beds and on the floor.", "나로 말하자면 그의 왼편에 앉았고, 나머지 애들은 가장 가까운 침대와 바닥에 무리 지어 우리 둘레에 둘러앉았다."]], [["How well I recollect our sitting there, talking in whispers; or their talking, and my respectfully listening, I ought rather to say; the moonlight falling a little way into the room, through the window, painting a pale window on the floor, and the greater part of us in shadow, except when Steerforth dipped a match into a phosphorus-box, when he wanted to look for anything on the board, and shed a blue glare over us that was gone directly!", "우리가 거기 앉아 소곤소곤 이야기를 나누던 그 정경이, 아니 그들이 이야기하고 내가 공손히 귀 기울이던 그 정경이 — 이렇게 말하는 편이 옳겠다 — 지금도 얼마나 또렷이 떠오르는지! 달빛이 창문을 통해 방 안으로 조금 흘러들어 바닥에 희끄무레한 창문 하나를 그려 놓았고, 우리는 대부분 어둠 속에 잠겨 있었다. 다만 스티어포스가 상 위에서 무언가를 찾으려고 성냥을 인광 통에 담갔다 그을 때면, 우리 위로 푸르스름한 섬광이 번쩍였다가 이내 사라지곤 했다."], ["A certain mysterious feeling, consequent on the darkness, the secrecy of the revel, and the whisper in which everything was said, steals over me again, and I listen to all they tell me with a vague feeling of solemnity and awe, which makes me glad that they are all so near, and frightens me (though I feign to laugh) when Traddles pretends to see a ghost in the corner.", "어둠과, 그 잔치의 은밀함과, 모든 말을 속삭이듯 주고받은 데서 비롯된 어떤 야릇한 기분이 다시금 내게 스며들어, 나는 어렴풋한 엄숙함과 외경심에 잠긴 채 그들이 들려주는 이야기에 모두 귀를 기울인다. 그 기분은 그들이 다들 이토록 가까이 있는 것을 반갑게 하면서도, 트래들스가 구석에 유령이 보이는 척할 때면 (나는 웃는 척하지만) 나를 겁먹게 만든다."]], [["I heard all kinds of things about the school and all belonging to it.", "나는 학교와 거기 딸린 온갖 것에 관해 별의별 이야기를 다 들었다."], ["I heard that Mr. Creakle had not preferred his claim to being a Tartar without reason; that he was the sternest and most severe of masters; that he laid about him, right and left, every day of his life, charging in among the boys like a trooper, and slashing away, unmercifully.", "크리클 선생님이 자기를 타타르 사람이라고 내세운 데에 까닭이 없는 게 아니라는 것, 그분이 선생들 가운데 가장 모질고 가장 매서운 사람이라는 것, 살아 있는 날마다 좌우로 후려치며 기병처럼 학생들 한복판으로 돌격해 인정사정없이 매질을 해 댄다는 것을 들었다."], ["That he knew nothing himself, but the art of slashing, being more ignorant (J.", "그분 자신은 매질하는 재주 말고는 아무것도 모르고, (J."], ["Steerforth said) than the lowest boy in the school; that he had been, a good many years ago, a small hop-dealer in the Borough, and had taken to the schooling business after being bankrupt in hops, and making away with Mrs.", "스티어포스의 말로는) 학교에서 제일 못한 학생보다도 더 무식하다는 것, 여러 해 전에는 버러에서 홉을 파는 자그마한 장수였는데, 홉 장사로 파산하고 크리클 부인의 돈을 다 들어먹은 뒤 학교 사업에 손을 댔다는 것도 들었다."], ["Creakle’s money. With a good deal more of that sort, which I wondered how they knew.", "그밖에도 그런 이야기가 한참 더 나왔는데, 그들이 그걸 어떻게 알았을까 신기했다. * 버러: 런던 템스강 남쪽의 한 지구."]], [["I heard that the man with the wooden leg, whose name was Tungay, was an obstinate barbarian who had formerly assisted in the hop business, but had come into the scholastic line with Mr.", "나무다리를 단 사내는 이름이 텅게이인데, 고집불통의 야만인으로, 전에는 홉 장사를 거들었으나 크리클 선생님을 따라 학교 일에 들어왔다는 이야기를 들었다."], ["Creakle, in consequence, as was supposed among the boys, of his having broken his leg in Mr. Creakle’s service, and having done a deal of dishonest work for him, and knowing his secrets.", "학생들 사이의 짐작으로는, 그가 크리클 선생님 밑에서 일하다 다리가 부러진 데다, 그분을 위해 떳떳지 못한 일을 숱하게 해 주었고 그분의 비밀을 쥐고 있기 때문이라고 했다."], ["I heard that with the single exception of Mr. Creakle, Tungay considered the whole establishment, masters and boys, as his natural enemies, and that the only delight of his life was to be sour and malicious.", "텅게이는 오직 크리클 선생님 한 사람만 빼고는, 선생이든 학생이든 학교 전체를 자기의 타고난 원수로 여겼으며, 그의 인생에서 유일한 낙은 심술궂고 못되게 구는 것이라는 이야기도 들었다."], ["I heard that Mr. Creakle had a son, who had not been Tungay’s friend, and who, assisting in the school, had once held some remonstrance with his father on an occasion when its discipline was very cruelly exercised, and was supposed, besides, to have protested against his father’s usage of his mother.", "크리클 선생님에게 아들이 하나 있는데, 텅게이와는 사이가 좋지 않았고, 학교 일을 거들다가 한번은 학교의 체벌이 너무도 잔인하게 행해진 일을 두고 아버지에게 항의한 적이 있으며, 게다가 아버지가 어머니를 대하는 방식에 대해서도 들고일어났다고 짐작된다는 이야기도 들었다."], ["I heard that Mr. Creakle had turned him out of doors, in consequence; and that Mrs. and Miss Creakle had been in a sad way, ever since.", "그 일로 크리클 선생님이 아들을 집 밖으로 내쫓았고, 그 뒤로 크리클 부인과 크리클 양이 줄곧 가엾은 처지에 놓여 있다는 이야기도 들었다."]], [["But the greatest wonder that I heard of Mr. Creakle was, there being one boy in the school on whom he never ventured to lay a hand, and that boy being J.", "그러나 크리클 선생님에 관해 들은 것 가운데 가장 놀라운 일은, 학교에 그분이 감히 손찌검할 엄두를 내지 못하는 학생이 하나 있다는 것, 그리고 그 학생이 바로 J."], ["Steerforth. Steerforth himself confirmed this when it was stated, and said that he should like to begin to see him do it.", "스티어포스라는 것이었다. 그 말이 나왔을 때 스티어포스 자신이 그것을 인정하면서, 어디 한번 그가 손대는 꼴을 보고 싶다고 했다."], ["On being asked by a mild boy (not me) how he would proceed if he did begin to see him do it, he dipped a match into his phosphorus-box on purpose to shed a glare over his reply, and said he would commence by knocking him down with a blow on the forehead from the seven-and-sixpenny ink-bottle that was always on the mantelpiece.", "한 온순한 학생이 (내가 아니다) 만약 정말로 그분이 손대는 꼴을 보게 되면 어떻게 할 거냐고 묻자, 그는 일부러 성냥을 인광 통에 담갔다 그어 자기 대답 위로 섬광을 비추면서, 늘 벽난로 선반 위에 놓여 있는 칠 실링 육 펜스짜리 잉크병으로 그분의 이마를 한 방 갈겨 쓰러뜨리는 것으로 시작하겠다고 말했다."], ["We sat in the dark for some time, breathless.", "우리는 한동안 숨을 죽이고 어둠 속에 앉아 있었다."]], [["I heard that Mr. Sharp and Mr. Mell were both supposed to be wretchedly paid; and that when there was hot and cold meat for dinner at Mr.", "샤프 선생님과 멜 선생님은 둘 다 형편없는 박봉에 시달리는 것으로 여겨진다는 이야기를 들었다."], ["Creakle’s table, Mr. Sharp was always expected to say he preferred cold; which was again corroborated by J.", "크리클 선생님의 식탁에 더운 고기와 식은 고기가 함께 나오면, 샤프 선생님은 늘 식은 고기가 더 좋다고 말하기를 기대받는다는 것이었는데, 이것 또한 유일한 거실 기숙생인 J. 스티어포스가 사실이라고 확인해 주었다."], ["Steerforth, the only parlour-boarder. I heard that Mr.", "샤프 선생님의 가발이 머리에 맞지 않는다는 것, 그리고 그것을 두고 그렇게 \"거들먹거릴\""], ["Sharp’s wig didn’t fit him; and that he needn’t be so ‘bounceable’--somebody else said ‘bumptious’--about it, because his own red hair was very plainly to be seen behind.", "것은—누군가는 \"잘난 척할\" 것은이라고 했다—없다는 것도 들었다. 뒤로는 그분의 빨간 머리가 빤히 다 보였으니까."]], [["I heard that one boy, who was a coal-merchant’s son, came as a set-off against the coal-bill, and was called, on that account, ‘Exchange or Barter’--a name selected from the arithmetic book as expressing this arrangement.", "한 학생은 석탄 상인의 아들인데, 석탄 외상값을 갚는 셈 치고 들어왔다 하여, 그 까닭으로 \"교환 또는 물물거래\"라고 불린다는 이야기를 들었다—이 거래를 나타낸다 하여 산수 책에서 골라낸 별명이었다."], ["I heard that the table beer was a robbery of parents, and the pudding an imposition.", "식탁에 나오는 맥주는 부모들에게서 돈을 뜯어내는 강도질이고, 푸딩은 속임수라는 것도 들었다."], ["I heard that Miss Creakle was regarded by the school in general as being in love with Steerforth; and I am sure, as I sat in the dark, thinking of his nice voice, and his fine face, and his easy manner, and his curling hair, I thought it very likely.", "크리클 양은 학교 전체에서 스티어포스에게 반한 것으로 여겨진다는 이야기도 들었는데, 어둠 속에 앉아 그의 고운 목소리며 멋진 얼굴이며 느긋한 몸가짐이며 곱슬곱슬한 머리카락을 떠올려 보니, 나도 정말 그럴 법하다는 생각이 들었다."], ["I heard that Mr. Mell was not a bad sort of fellow, but hadn’t a sixpence to bless himself with; and that there was no doubt that old Mrs.", "멜 선생님은 나쁜 사람은 아니지만 제 몸뚱이 하나 건사할 육 펜스도 없으며, 그분의 어머니인 늙은 멜 부인도 욥처럼 가난한 것이 틀림없다는 이야기도 들었다."], ["Mell, his mother, was as poor as job. I thought of my breakfast then, and what had sounded like ‘My Charley!’ but I was, I am glad to remember, as mute as a mouse about it.", "그때 나는 그 아침 식사와, \"내 찰리!\"처럼 들렸던 그 말을 떠올렸지만, 다행히 기억하건대, 나는 그것에 대해 쥐 죽은 듯 입을 다물고 있었다. * 욥처럼 가난한: 성경 「욥기」의 주인공 욥이 모든 재산을 잃은 데서 나온, 몹시 가난하다는 뜻의 영어 관용 표현."]], [["The hearing of all this, and a good deal more, outlasted the banquet some time. The greater part of the guests had gone to bed as soon as the eating and drinking were over; and we, who had remained whispering and listening half-undressed, at last betook ourselves to bed, too.", "이 모든 이야기와, 그밖에도 한참 더 많은 이야기를 듣느라, 잔치가 끝난 뒤로도 한동안 시간이 흘렀다. 손님들 대부분은 먹고 마시는 일이 끝나자마자 잠자리에 들었고, 옷을 반쯤 벗은 채 속닥이며 귀 기울이던 우리도 마침내 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["‘Good night, young Copperfield,’ said Steerforth.", "\"잘 자, 어린 코퍼필드.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘I’ll take care of you.’ ‘You’re very kind,’ I gratefully returned.", "\"내가 너 챙겨 줄게.\" \"정말 친절하구나.\" 나는 고마워하며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I am very much obliged to you.’", "\"참 고마워.\""]], [["‘You haven’t got a sister, have you?’ said Steerforth, yawning.", "\"너 누이는 없지, 그렇지?\" 스티어포스가 하품하며 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ I answered.", "\"응, 없어.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘That’s a pity,’ said Steerforth.", "\"그거 안됐네.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘If you had had one, I should think she would have been a pretty, timid, little, bright-eyed sort of girl.", "\"만약 누이가 있었다면, 분명 예쁘고 수줍음 많고 자그마하고 눈이 초롱초롱한 여자애였을 것 같은데."], ["I should have liked to know her.", "알고 지내면 좋았을걸."], ["Good night, young Copperfield.’", "잘 자, 어린 코퍼필드.\""]], [["‘Good night, sir,’ I replied.", "\"안녕히 주무세요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["I thought of him very much after I went to bed, and raised myself, I recollect, to look at him where he lay in the moonlight, with his handsome face turned up, and his head reclining easily on his arm.", "나는 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 그를 자꾸 떠올렸고, 기억하건대 몸을 일으켜, 달빛 속에 누워 잘생긴 얼굴을 위로 향한 채 팔을 베고 편안히 머리를 기댄 그를 바라보았다."], ["He was a person of great power in my eyes; that was, of course, the reason of my mind running on him.", "그는 내 눈에 굉장한 힘을 지닌 사람이었다. 물론 그래서 내 마음이 줄곧 그에게로 향한 것이었다."], ["No veiled future dimly glanced upon him in the moonbeams.", "달빛 속에서 그에게 어슴푸레 비치는 베일에 싸인 미래 따위는 없었다."], ["There was no shadowy picture of his footsteps, in the garden that I dreamed of walking in all night.", "내가 밤새 거닐던 꿈속의 그 정원에, 그의 발자국이 남긴 어두운 그림자 같은 것도 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 7. MY ‘FIRST HALF’ AT SALEM HOUSE", "# CHAPTER 7. MY ‘FIRST HALF’ AT SALEM HOUSE"]], [["School began in earnest next day.", "이튿날부터 학교 수업이 본격적으로 시작되었다."], ["A profound impression was made upon me, I remember, by the roar of voices in the schoolroom suddenly becoming hushed as death when Mr. Creakle entered after breakfast, and stood in the doorway looking round upon us like a giant in a story-book surveying his captives.", "그날 아침을 먹고 나서 크리클 선생님이 들어와 문간에 서서, 이야기책 속 거인이 제 포로들을 둘러보듯 우리를 죽 훑어볼 때, 교실을 가득 메우던 떠들썩한 목소리가 순식간에 죽음처럼 잦아들던 그 광경이 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼던 것이 지금도 기억난다."]], [["Tungay stood at Mr. Creakle’s elbow.", "텅게이는 크리클 선생님 팔꿈치 옆에 버티고 섰다."], ["He had no occasion, I thought, to cry out ‘Silence!’ so ferociously, for the boys were all struck speechless and motionless.", "굳이 그렇게 사납게 \"조용히!\" 하고 외칠 까닭도 없어 보였다. 아이들은 죄다 말문이 막히고 몸이 굳어 버렸으니까."]], [["Mr. Creakle was seen to speak, and Tungay was heard, to this effect.", "크리클 선생님이 입을 움직이는 게 보였고, 텅게이의 목소리가 이렇게 들려왔다."]], [["‘Now, boys, this is a new half.", "\"자, 너희들, 이번엔 새 학기다."], ["Take care what you’re about, in this new half.", "이 새 학기 동안 처신 똑똑히 해."], ["Come fresh up to the lessons, I advise you, for I come fresh up to the punishment. I won’t flinch.", "수업에 산뜻하게 임하라고 충고해 둔다, 나는 매질에 산뜻하게 임할 테니까. 나는 절대 봐주지 않아."], ["It will be of no use your rubbing yourselves; you won’t rub the marks out that I shall give you.", "비벼 봐야 소용없다. 내가 남길 자국은 아무리 비벼도 안 지워질 테니까."], ["Now get to work, every boy!’", "자, 일들 시작해, 한 놈도 빠짐없이!\""]], [["When this dreadful exordium was over, and Tungay had stumped out again, Mr. Creakle came to where I sat, and told me that if I were famous for biting, he was famous for biting, too.", "이 무시무시한 첫머리 연설이 끝나고 텅게이가 다시 쿵쿵거리며 나가 버리자, 크리클 선생님은 내가 앉은 자리로 다가와, 내가 무는 것으로 유명하다면 자기도 무는 것으로 유명하다고 했다."], ["He then showed me the cane, and asked me what I thought of THAT, for a tooth?", "그러고는 회초리를 내보이며, 이것을 이빨로 치면 어떻겠느냐고 물었다."], ["Was it a sharp tooth, hey?", "날카로운 이빨이지 않으냐, 응?"], ["Was it a double tooth, hey?", "어금니지 않으냐, 응?"], ["Had it a deep prong, hey?", "뿌리가 깊지 않으냐, 응?"], ["Did it bite, hey? Did it bite?", "잘 물지 않으냐, 응? 물지?"], ["At every question he gave me a fleshy cut with it that made me writhe; so I was very soon made free of Salem House (as Steerforth said), and was very soon in tears also.", "그러면서 한 마디 물을 때마다 나에게 살을 파고드는 매질을 한 대씩 안겨, 나는 몸부림쳤다. 그렇게 해서 나는 (스티어포스 말마따나) 곧장 세일럼 하우스의 정식 일원이 되었고, 또한 곧장 눈물범벅이 되었다."]], [["Not that I mean to say these were special marks of distinction, which only I received.", "이것이 나만 받은 특별한 표창 같은 것이었다는 말은 결코 아니다."], ["On the contrary, a large majority of the boys (especially the smaller ones) were visited with similar instances of notice, as Mr.", "오히려 크리클 선생님이 교실을 한 바퀴 도는 동안, 아이들 대다수가 (특히 어린 축들이) 그와 비슷한 관심의 사례를 받았다."], ["Creakle made the round of the schoolroom. Half the establishment was writhing and crying, before the day’s work began; and how much of it had writhed and cried before the day’s work was over, I am really afraid to recollect, lest I should seem to exaggerate.", "그날 일과가 시작되기도 전에 학교의 절반이 몸부림치며 울고 있었고, 일과가 끝날 무렵에는 그 가운데 얼마나 많은 아이가 몸부림치며 울었는지는, 행여 과장하는 것처럼 보일까 봐 떠올리기조차 솔직히 두렵다."]], [["I should think there never can have been a man who enjoyed his profession more than Mr.", "크리클 선생님만큼 제 직업을 즐긴 사람은 세상에 다시없으리라 싶다."], ["Creakle did. He had a delight in cutting at the boys, which was like the satisfaction of a craving appetite.", "그는 아이들을 후려치는 데서, 갈망하던 식욕을 채울 때 같은 만족을 느꼈다."], ["I am confident that he couldn’t resist a chubby boy, especially; that there was a fascination in such a subject, which made him restless in his mind, until he had scored and marked him for the day.", "나는 그가 특히 통통한 아이를 보면 도저히 참지 못했다고 확신한다. 그런 아이에게는 묘한 끌림이 있어서, 그날 그 아이를 후려쳐 자국을 남겨 놓기 전까지는 마음이 영 안절부절못했던 것이다."], ["I was chubby myself, and ought to know.", "나 자신이 통통한 편이었으니, 잘 안다."], ["I am sure when I think of the fellow now, my blood rises against him with the disinterested indignation I should feel if I could have known all about him without having ever been in his power; but it rises hotly, because I know him to have been an incapable brute, who had no more right to be possessed of the great trust he held, than to be Lord High Admiral, or Commander-in-Chief--in either of which capacities it is probable that he would have done infinitely less mischief.", "지금 그 작자를 떠올리면, 그의 손아귀에 한 번도 든 적 없이 그에 관한 모든 것을 알게 되었더라도 느꼈을 법한 사심 없는 분노로 피가 끓어오른다. 하지만 그 피가 뜨겁게 끓는 까닭은, 그가 무능한 짐승이었음을 내가 알기 때문이다. 그는 자기가 거머쥔 그 막중한 직무를 맡을 자격이 해군 대제독이나 총사령관 자리를 맡을 자격만큼도 없는 사람이었으니 — 차라리 그 두 자리 중 하나라도 맡았더라면 끼친 해악이 훨씬 적었을 것이다."]], [["Miserable little propitiators of a remorseless Idol, how abject we were to him!", "가차 없는 우상 앞에 빌붙던 가엾고 자그마한 아첨꾼들, 우리는 그자에게 얼마나 비굴했던가!"], ["What a launch in life I think it now, on looking back, to be so mean and servile to a man of such parts and pretensions!", "이제 돌이켜 보면, 그만한 재주와 허세를 지닌 인물에게 그토록 비열하고 굽실거리며 굴었다는 것이 인생의 출발로서 참 한심하기 짝이 없다는 생각이 든다."]], [["Here I sit at the desk again, watching his eye--humbly watching his eye, as he rules a ciphering-book for another victim whose hands have just been flattened by that identical ruler, and who is trying to wipe the sting out with a pocket-handkerchief.", "여기 나는 다시 책상 앞에 앉아 그의 눈치를 살핀다. 굽실거리며 그의 눈치를 살핀다. 그는 또 다른 희생양을 위해 산수 공책에 선을 긋고 있는데, 그 아이는 방금 바로 그 자로 손바닥을 납작하게 얻어맞고는 손수건으로 얼얼한 통증을 닦아 내려 애쓰는 중이다."], ["I have plenty to do.", "나는 할 일이 잔뜩 있다."], ["I don’t watch his eye in idleness, but because I am morbidly attracted to it, in a dread desire to know what he will do next, and whether it will be my turn to suffer, or somebody else’s.", "한가해서 그의 눈을 살피는 게 아니라, 다음엔 그가 무슨 짓을 할지, 당할 차례가 나인지 다른 누구인지 알고 싶은 두려운 욕망에 병적으로 끌려서다."], ["A lane of small boys beyond me, with the same interest in his eye, watch it too.", "내 너머로 죽 늘어선 어린 아이들도 그의 눈에 같은 관심을 품고 함께 살핀다."], ["I think he knows it, though he pretends he don’t.", "그도 그걸 알고 있는 것 같다. 모르는 척하지만 말이다."], ["He makes dreadful mouths as he rules the ciphering-book; and now he throws his eye sideways down our lane, and we all droop over our books and tremble.", "그는 산수 공책에 줄을 그으면서 무시무시하게 입을 씰룩거린다. 그러다 이제 곁눈질로 우리 줄을 죽 훑어 내려가면, 우리는 모두 책 위로 고개를 떨군 채 부들부들 떤다."], ["A moment afterwards we are again eyeing him.", "잠시 뒤 우리는 다시 그를 곁눈질하고 있다."], ["An unhappy culprit, found guilty of imperfect exercise, approaches at his command.", "연습 문제를 제대로 못 풀었다고 유죄 판결을 받은 불행한 죄인 하나가 그의 명령에 다가온다."], ["The culprit falters excuses, and professes a determination to do better tomorrow. Mr.", "죄인은 더듬더듬 변명을 늘어놓고, 내일은 더 잘하겠노라 다짐한다."], ["Creakle cuts a joke before he beats him, and we laugh at it,--miserable little dogs, we laugh, with our visages as white as ashes, and our hearts sinking into our boots.", "크리클 선생님은 때리기 전에 농담을 한마디 던지고, 우리는 그 농담에 웃는다. 가엾고 자그마한 강아지들인 우리는, 잿빛으로 핏기 가신 얼굴에, 심장은 장화 속으로 가라앉은 채로, 그 농담에 웃는다."]], [["Here I sit at the desk again, on a drowsy summer afternoon.", "여기 나는 다시 책상 앞에 앉아 있다. 나른한 여름 오후다."], ["A buzz and hum go up around me, as if the boys were so many bluebottles. A cloggy sensation of the lukewarm fat of meat is upon me (we dined an hour or two ago), and my head is as heavy as so much lead.", "마치 아이들이 떼 지은 쉬파리라도 되는 양, 윙윙거리고 웅웅거리는 소리가 내 둘레로 피어오른다. (한두 시간 전에 점심을 먹었으니) 미적지근한 고기 기름이 명치께를 짓누르는 느낌이고, 머리는 납덩이처럼 무겁다."], ["I would give the world to go to sleep.", "잠들 수만 있다면 온 세상이라도 내주겠다."], ["I sit with my eye on Mr. Creakle, blinking at him like a young owl; when sleep overpowers me for a minute, he still looms through my slumber, ruling those ciphering-books, until he softly comes behind me and wakes me to plainer perception of him, with a red ridge across my back.", "나는 크리클 선생님에게 눈을 박은 채 새끼 올빼미처럼 눈을 끔벅인다. 잠이 일 분쯤 나를 짓눌러도, 그는 여전히 내 선잠 속에 어른거리며 그 산수 공책에 줄을 긋다가, 마침내 슬그머니 내 뒤로 다가와, 등줄기에 벌건 골을 하나 그어 놓으며 나를 깨워 그를 더 또렷이 알아보게 한다."]], [["Here I am in the playground, with my eye still fascinated by him, though I can’t see him.", "여기 나는 운동장에 있다. 그가 보이지는 않지만 내 눈은 여전히 그에게 홀려 있다."], ["The window at a little distance from which I know he is having his dinner, stands for him, and I eye that instead.", "조금 떨어진 곳에 그가 식사를 하고 있는 창문이 있고, 그 창문이 그를 대신하므로, 나는 그 대신 그 창문에 눈을 둔다."], ["If he shows his face near it, mine assumes an imploring and submissive expression.", "그가 그 근처에 얼굴을 내밀면, 내 얼굴은 애원하며 복종하는 표정을 짓는다."], ["If he looks out through the glass, the boldest boy (Steerforth excepted) stops in the middle of a shout or yell, and becomes contemplative.", "그가 유리 너머로 내다보면, 가장 대담한 아이도 (스티어포스만 빼고) 소리를 지르거나 고함치다 말고 뚝 멈춰 깊은 사색에 잠긴다."], ["One day, Traddles (the most unfortunate boy in the world) breaks that window accidentally, with a ball.", "어느 날, 트래들스가 (세상에서 가장 운 없는 아이다) 어쩌다 공으로 그 창문을 깨뜨렸다."], ["I shudder at this moment with the tremendous sensation of seeing it done, and feeling that the ball has bounded on to Mr. Creakle’s sacred head.", "나는 그 일이 벌어지는 것을 보았던, 그리고 그 공이 크리클 선생님의 신성한 머리통 위로 튀어 올랐겠구나 느꼈던 그 어마어마한 충격에, 지금 이 순간에도 몸서리가 쳐진다."]], [["Poor Traddles!", "가엾은 트래들스!"], ["In a tight sky-blue suit that made his arms and legs like German sausages, or roly-poly puddings, he was the merriest and most miserable of all the boys.", "꽉 끼는 하늘색 양복을 입어 팔다리가 마치 독일 소시지나 둥글둥글한 푸딩처럼 보이던 그 아이는, 모든 아이 가운데 가장 명랑하면서도 가장 비참했다. 그는 늘 회초리를 맞았다."], ["He was always being caned--I think he was caned every day that half-year, except one holiday Monday when he was only ruler’d on both hands--and was always going to write to his uncle about it, and never did.", "그 학기 동안 하루도 빠짐없이 매를 맞았던 것 같은데, 딱 한 번 어느 공휴일이던 월요일에만 양손에 자로 맞고 넘어갔지 싶다. 그리고 그는 늘 그 일을 삼촌에게 편지로 일러바치겠다고 했지만, 한 번도 쓰지 않았다."], ["After laying his head on the desk for a little while, he would cheer up, somehow, begin to laugh again, and draw skeletons all over his slate, before his eyes were dry.", "잠시 책상에 머리를 처박고 있다가도, 그는 어떻게든 기운을 차려 다시 웃기 시작했고, 눈물이 채 마르기도 전에 석판 가득 해골을 그려 댔다."], ["I used at first to wonder what comfort Traddles found in drawing skeletons; and for some time looked upon him as a sort of hermit, who reminded himself by those symbols of mortality that caning couldn’t last for ever.", "나는 처음엔 트래들스가 해골을 그리는 데서 무슨 위안을 얻는지 궁금했다. 한동안은 그를 일종의 은둔자로 여겼다. 그 죽음의 상징들로 매질이란 영원히 가지 않으리란 걸 스스로 일깨우는 것이려니 했던 것이다."], ["But I believe he only did it because they were easy, and didn’t want any features.", "하지만 나는 그가 그저 해골이 그리기 쉽고, 이목구비가 필요 없어서 그렸을 뿐이라고 믿는다."]], [["He was very honourable, Traddles was, and held it as a solemn duty in the boys to stand by one another.", "트래들스는 무척 의리 있는 아이였고, 아이들끼리 서로 감싸 주는 것을 엄숙한 의무로 여겼다."], ["He suffered for this on several occasions; and particularly once, when Steerforth laughed in church, and the Beadle thought it was Traddles, and took him out.", "그는 이 때문에 여러 번 고초를 겪었다. 특히 한번은 스티어포스가 교회에서 웃었는데, 교회지기가 트래들스가 웃은 줄로 알고 그를 끌어냈다."], ["I see him now, going away in custody, despised by the congregation.", "신도들의 멸시를 받으며 붙들려 나가던 그의 모습이 지금도 눈에 선하다."], ["He never said who was the real offender, though he smarted for it next day, and was imprisoned so many hours that he came forth with a whole churchyard-full of skeletons swarming all over his Latin Dictionary.", "그는 이튿날 그 일로 따끔하게 매를 맞고, 라틴어 사전 위에 온 교회 묘지를 가득 채울 만큼 해골이 우글거릴 정도로 오랜 시간 갇혀 있다 나왔으면서도, 끝내 진짜 범인이 누구인지 발설하지 않았다."], ["But he had his reward.", "하지만 그는 보답을 받았다."], ["Steerforth said there was nothing of the sneak in Traddles, and we all felt that to be the highest praise.", "스티어포스가 트래들스에게는 고자질쟁이 기질이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없다고 했고, 우리는 모두 그것이 더없이 높은 칭찬이라 여겼다."], ["For my part, I could have gone through a good deal (though I was much less brave than Traddles, and nothing like so old) to have won such a recompense.", "나로 말하자면, 그런 보답을 얻을 수만 있었다면 (트래들스보다 훨씬 덜 용감하고 나이도 한참 어렸지만) 어지간한 고생쯤은 기꺼이 견뎌 냈을 것이다."]], [["To see Steerforth walk to church before us, arm-in-arm with Miss Creakle, was one of the great sights of my life.", "스티어포스가 우리보다 앞장서서 크리클 양과 팔짱을 끼고 교회로 걸어가는 모습은 내 인생의 큰 구경거리 가운데 하나였다."], ["I didn’t think Miss Creakle equal to little Em’ly in point of beauty, and I didn’t love her (I didn’t dare); but I thought her a young lady of extraordinary attractions, and in point of gentility not to be surpassed.", "나는 크리클 양이 아름다움으로 치면 꼬마 에밀리에 미치지 못한다고 생각했고, 그녀를 사랑하지도 않았다 (감히 그러지도 못했다). 하지만 그녀가 비범한 매력을 지닌 아가씨이고, 기품으로 말하자면 따를 자가 없다고 여겼다."], ["When Steerforth, in white trousers, carried her parasol for her, I felt proud to know him; and believed that she could not choose but adore him with all her heart. Mr.", "흰 바지를 입은 스티어포스가 그녀의 양산을 들어 줄 때면, 나는 그를 안다는 것이 자랑스러웠고, 그녀가 온 마음을 다해 그를 흠모하지 않을 수 없으리라 믿었다."], ["Sharp and Mr. Mell were both notable personages in my eyes; but Steerforth was to them what the sun was to two stars.", "샤프 선생님과 멜 선생님도 내 눈에는 둘 다 대단한 인물이었지만, 스티어포스는 그 둘에게 마치 두 별 곁의 해 같은 존재였다."]], [["Steerforth continued his protection of me, and proved a very useful friend; since nobody dared to annoy one whom he honoured with his countenance. He couldn’t--or at all events he didn’t--defend me from Mr.", "스티어포스는 계속해서 나를 보호해 주었고, 아주 쓸모 있는 친구임을 입증했다. 그가 얼굴을 알아 주며 아껴 주는 아이를 감히 괴롭히려는 자가 아무도 없었기 때문이다."], ["Creakle, who was very severe with me; but whenever I had been treated worse than usual, he always told me that I wanted a little of his pluck, and that he wouldn’t have stood it himself; which I felt he intended for encouragement, and considered to be very kind of him.", "그는 나에게 몹시 가혹했던 크리클 선생님으로부터는 나를 지켜 주지 못했다 — 아니, 적어도 지켜 주지 않았다. 하지만 내가 여느 때보다 심하게 당하고 나면, 그는 늘 나에게 자기 배짱을 조금 본받을 필요가 있다고, 자기 같으면 그런 꼴을 가만두지 않았으리라 말했다. 나는 그것이 나를 격려하려는 뜻임을 느꼈고, 무척 다정한 말이라 여겼다."], ["There was one advantage, and only one that I know of, in Mr.", "크리클 선생님의 가혹함에는 내가 아는 한 단 하나의 이점이 있었다."], ["Creakle’s severity. He found my placard in his way when he came up or down behind the form on which I sat, and wanted to make a cut at me in passing; for this reason it was soon taken off, and I saw it no more.", "그가 내가 앉은 의자 뒤로 오르락내리락하다가 지나가는 길에 나를 한 대 후려치려 할 때면 내 등에 붙은 푯말이 거치적거렸던 것이다. 그래서 그 푯말은 곧 떼어졌고, 나는 두 번 다시 그것을 보지 못했다."]], [["An accidental circumstance cemented the intimacy between Steerforth and me, in a manner that inspired me with great pride and satisfaction, though it sometimes led to inconvenience.", "우연한 사건 하나가 스티어포스와 나 사이의 친분을 단단히 굳혀 주었는데, 이는 나에게 큰 자부심과 만족을 안겨 주었으나, 때로는 곤란을 불러오기도 했다."], ["It happened on one occasion, when he was doing me the honour of talking to me in the playground, that I hazarded the observation that something or somebody--I forget what now--was like something or somebody in Peregrine Pickle.", "그 일은, 어느 날 그가 운동장에서 나에게 말을 걸어 주는 영광을 베풀던 참에 일어났다. 나는 무엇 혹은 누군가가 — 지금은 무엇이었는지 잊었다 — 『페레그린 피클』에 나오는 무엇 혹은 누군가와 비슷하다는 소견을 슬쩍 내놓았다."], ["He said nothing at the time; but when I was going to bed at night, asked me if I had got that book?", "그는 그때는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 밤에 내가 잠자리에 들려 할 때 그 책을 가지고 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["I told him no, and explained how it was that I had read it, and all those other books of which I have made mention.", "나는 아니라고 대답하고, 어떻게 해서 그 책을, 그리고 앞서 말한 다른 책들을 다 읽게 되었는지 설명했다."]], [["‘And do you recollect them?’ Steerforth said.", "\"그래서 그걸 다 기억하고 있어?\" 스티어포스가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh yes,’ I replied; I had a good memory, and I believed I recollected them very well.", "\"응, 그럼.\" 나는 대답했다. 나는 기억력이 좋았고, 그 책들을 아주 잘 기억하고 있다고 믿었다."]], [["‘Then I tell you what, young Copperfield,’ said Steerforth, ‘you shall tell ‘em to me.", "\"그럼 이렇게 하자, 꼬마 코퍼필드.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"네가 나한테 그걸 얘기해 주는 거야."], ["I can’t get to sleep very early at night, and I generally wake rather early in the morning.", "나는 밤에 일찍 잠들지를 못하고, 아침엔 대개 좀 일찍 깨거든."], ["We’ll go over ‘em one after another.", "한 편씩 차례로 짚어 나가자."], ["We’ll make some regular Arabian Nights of it.’", "우리만의 그럴듯한 『천일야화』를 만드는 거야.\""]], [["I felt extremely flattered by this arrangement, and we commenced carrying it into execution that very evening.", "나는 이 약속에 무척 우쭐해졌고, 바로 그날 저녁부터 실행에 옮기기 시작했다."], ["What ravages I committed on my favourite authors in the course of my interpretation of them, I am not in a condition to say, and should be very unwilling to know; but I had a profound faith in them, and I had, to the best of my belief, a simple, earnest manner of narrating what I did narrate; and these qualities went a long way.", "내가 그 책들을 풀어내는 과정에서 내가 좋아하는 작가들에게 얼마나 큰 만행을 저질렀는지는 말할 처지가 못 되고, 알고 싶지도 않다. 하지만 나는 그 책들을 깊이 믿었고, 적어도 내 생각에는, 내가 들려주는 이야기를 소박하고 진지한 태도로 풀어 갔다. 그리고 이 자질들이 큰 몫을 했다."]], [["The drawback was, that I was often sleepy at night, or out of spirits and indisposed to resume the story; and then it was rather hard work, and it must be done; for to disappoint or to displease Steerforth was of course out of the question.", "골칫거리는, 내가 밤에 자주 졸리거나, 기운이 없어 이야기를 다시 이어 갈 마음이 나지 않는다는 것이었다. 그럴 때면 꽤나 고역이었지만 그래도 해내야만 했다. 스티어포스를 실망시키거나 언짢게 하는 일은 당연히 있을 수 없었으니까."], ["In the morning, too, when I felt weary, and should have enjoyed another hour’s repose very much, it was a tiresome thing to be roused, like the Sultana Scheherazade, and forced into a long story before the getting-up bell rang; but Steerforth was resolute; and as he explained to me, in return, my sums and exercises, and anything in my tasks that was too hard for me, I was no loser by the transaction.", "아침에도, 몸이 노곤해서 한 시간쯤 더 자면 더없이 좋았을 때, 술탄의 왕비 셰에라자드처럼 깨워져 기상종이 울리기도 전에 긴 이야기를 억지로 늘어놓아야 하는 것은 성가신 노릇이었다. 그러나 스티어포스는 단호했고, 그 대가로 나에게 산수며 연습 문제며 내가 풀기 너무 어려운 과제 따위를 설명해 주었으므로, 이 거래에서 나는 손해 본 것이 없었다."], ["Let me do myself justice, however.", "하지만 나에게도 공정을 기해야겠다."], ["I was moved by no interested or selfish motive, nor was I moved by fear of him.", "나는 사사로운 이해나 이기심에 끌려 움직인 것이 아니었고, 그가 두려워서 움직인 것도 아니었다."], ["I admired and loved him, and his approval was return enough.", "나는 그를 흠모하고 사랑했으며, 그의 인정만으로도 충분한 보답이 되었다."], ["It was so precious to me that I look back on these trifles, now, with an aching heart.", "그것이 어찌나 소중했던지, 이제 이 사소한 일들을 돌이켜 보면 가슴이 아려 온다."]], [["Steerforth was considerate, too; and showed his consideration, in one particular instance, in an unflinching manner that was a little tantalizing, I suspect, to poor Traddles and the rest.", "스티어포스는 또한 자상한 데가 있었고, 한 가지 일에서는 그 자상함을 굽힘 없이 드러냈는데, 짐작건대 가엾은 트래들스를 비롯한 나머지 아이들에게는 좀 약 오르는 노릇이었으리라."], ["Peggotty’s promised letter--what a comfortable letter it was!--arrived before ‘the half’ was many weeks old; and with it a cake in a perfect nest of oranges, and two bottles of cowslip wine.", "패거티가 보내 주마 약속했던 편지가 — 어찌나 마음 푸근한 편지였던지! — 학기가 시작된 지 몇 주 되지 않아 도착했다. 그 편지와 함께, 오렌지로 완벽하게 둥지를 틀어 놓은 한가운데 케이크 하나와 앵초주 두 병이 들어 있었다."], ["This treasure, as in duty bound, I laid at the feet of Steerforth, and begged him to dispense.", "나는 도리에 따라 이 보물을 스티어포스의 발치에 바치고, 나누어 주십사 부탁했다."]], [["‘Now, I’ll tell you what, young Copperfield,’ said he: ‘the wine shall be kept to wet your whistle when you are story-telling.’", "\"자, 이렇게 하자, 꼬마 코퍼필드.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 술은 네가 이야기를 들려줄 때 목을 축이라고 남겨 두는 거야.\""]], [["I blushed at the idea, and begged him, in my modesty, not to think of it.", "나는 그 말에 얼굴이 붉어졌고, 겸손하게 그러지 말라고 부탁했다."], ["But he said he had observed I was sometimes hoarse--a little roopy was his exact expression--and it should be, every drop, devoted to the purpose he had mentioned.", "그러나 그는 내 목소리가 이따금 쉰다는 걸 — '약간 컬컬하다'는 게 그의 정확한 표현이었다 — 눈여겨봤다며, 한 방울도 빠짐없이 그 용도에 바치겠다고 했다."], ["Accordingly, it was locked up in his box, and drawn off by himself in a phial, and administered to me through a piece of quill in the cork, when I was supposed to be in want of a restorative.", "그리하여 술은 그의 상자에 잠겨 보관되었고, 그가 직접 작은 약병에 따라 두었다가, 내가 기력을 보충해야 할 성싶을 때면 코르크 마개에 꽂은 깃대 한 토막을 통해 나에게 먹여 주었다."], ["Sometimes, to make it a more sovereign specific, he was so kind as to squeeze orange juice into it, or to stir it up with ginger, or dissolve a peppermint drop in it; and although I cannot assert that the flavour was improved by these experiments, or that it was exactly the compound one would have chosen for a stomachic, the last thing at night and the first thing in the morning, I drank it gratefully and was very sensible of his attention.", "때로는 더 만병통치약처럼 만들겠다고 친절하게도 거기에 오렌지즙을 짜 넣거나, 생강을 휘저어 넣거나, 박하 사탕을 하나 녹여 넣기도 했다. 이런 실험으로 맛이 나아졌다거나, 밤에 자기 직전과 아침에 일어나 맨 먼저 마실 위장약으로 딱히 골랐을 법한 조합이었다고 장담할 수는 없지만, 나는 고맙게 그것을 마셨고 그의 배려를 절절히 느꼈다."]], [["We seem, to me, to have been months over Peregrine, and months more over the other stories.", "내 느낌으로는 『페레그린』을 끝내는 데만도 몇 달이 걸렸고, 다른 이야기들에는 또 몇 달이 더 걸린 것 같다."], ["The institution never flagged for want of a story, I am certain; and the wine lasted out almost as well as the matter.", "우리 모임이 이야깃거리가 모자라 시들해지는 일은 결코 없었다고 확신한다. 술도 이야깃거리에 거의 맞먹게 오래갔다."], ["Poor Traddles--I never think of that boy but with a strange disposition to laugh, and with tears in my eyes--was a sort of chorus, in general; and affected to be convulsed with mirth at the comic parts, and to be overcome with fear when there was any passage of an alarming character in the narrative.", "가엾은 트래들스는 — 그 아이를 떠올릴 때면 나는 늘 웃고 싶은 묘한 충동이 일면서도 눈에는 눈물이 고인다 — 대개 일종의 합창단 노릇을 했다. 익살스러운 대목에서는 웃겨 죽겠다는 듯 몸을 비틀어 댔고, 이야기에 무시무시한 대목이 나오면 겁에 질린 시늉을 했다."], ["This rather put me out, very often.", "이게 나를 자주 당황하게 했다."], ["It was a great jest of his, I recollect, to pretend that he couldn’t keep his teeth from chattering, whenever mention was made of an Alguazill in connexion with the adventures of Gil Blas; and I remember that when Gil Blas met the captain of the robbers in Madrid, this unlucky joker counterfeited such an ague of terror, that he was overheard by Mr. Creakle, who was prowling about the passage, and handsomely flogged for disorderly conduct in the bedroom.", "기억나기로, 『질 블라스』의 모험과 관련해 포졸이 등장할 때마다 그는 이가 딱딱 부딪치는 걸 못 참겠다는 시늉을 즐겨 했다. 그리고 질 블라스가 마드리드에서 도적 두목을 만나는 대목에서, 이 운 없는 익살꾼이 어찌나 학질에 걸린 듯 부들부들 떠는 시늉을 했던지, 복도를 어슬렁거리던 크리클 선생님 귀에 들리고 말았고, 침실에서 소란을 피웠다는 죄목으로 멋들어지게 매를 맞았다."], ["Whatever I had within me that was romantic and dreamy, was encouraged by so much story-telling in the dark; and in that respect the pursuit may not have been very profitable to me.", "내 안에 있던 낭만적이고 몽상적인 기질이 어떤 것이었든, 어둠 속에서 그토록 많은 이야기를 들려주는 동안 더욱 부추겨졌다. 그런 점에서 이 일은 나에게 그리 이로운 것이 아니었을지도 모른다."], ["But the being cherished as a kind of plaything in my room, and the consciousness that this accomplishment of mine was bruited about among the boys, and attracted a good deal of notice to me though I was the youngest there, stimulated me to exertion.", "하지만 내 방에서 일종의 노리갯감처럼 귀여움을 받고, 이 재주가 아이들 사이에 소문이 나서, 비록 내가 거기서 가장 어렸는데도 적잖은 주목을 받고 있다는 의식이 나를 분발하게 만들었다."], ["In a school carried on by sheer cruelty, whether it is presided over by a dunce or not, there is not likely to be much learnt.", "순전히 잔혹함으로만 굴러가는 학교에서는, 우두머리가 멍청이든 아니든, 배우는 것이 많기 어렵다."], ["I believe our boys were, generally, as ignorant a set as any schoolboys in existence; they were too much troubled and knocked about to learn; they could no more do that to advantage, than any one can do anything to advantage in a life of constant misfortune, torment, and worry.", "우리 아이들은 대체로 세상의 어느 학생들 못지않게 무지한 무리였다고 나는 믿는다. 그들은 너무 시달리고 두들겨 맞아서 배울 수가 없었다. 끊임없는 불행과 고통과 근심으로 점철된 삶 속에서 무슨 일을 제대로 해낼 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 그들도 공부를 제대로 해낼 수 없었던 것이다."], ["But my little vanity, and Steerforth’s help, urged me on somehow; and without saving me from much, if anything, in the way of punishment, made me, for the time I was there, an exception to the general body, insomuch that I did steadily pick up some crumbs of knowledge.", "하지만 나의 자그마한 허영심과 스티어포스의 도움이 어떻게든 나를 떠밀었고, 벌받는 일에서 나를 별로, 아니 거의 구해 주지는 못했어도, 적어도 내가 거기 머무는 동안만큼은 나를 전체 무리 가운데 예외로 만들어 주어서, 나는 꾸준히 지식의 부스러기를 조금씩 주워 모을 수 있었다."]], [["In this I was much assisted by Mr. Mell, who had a liking for me that I am grateful to remember.", "이 일에서 나는 멜 선생님의 도움을 많이 받았는데, 그분은 나를 아껴 주었고 나는 그것을 고맙게 기억한다."], ["It always gave me pain to observe that Steerforth treated him with systematic disparagement, and seldom lost an occasion of wounding his feelings, or inducing others to do so.", "스티어포스가 그분을 두고 늘 깔보듯 대하고, 그분의 마음에 상처를 주거나 남들로 하여금 그러게 하는 기회를 좀처럼 놓치지 않는 것을 볼 때면 나는 늘 마음이 아팠다."], ["This troubled me the more for a long time, because I had soon told Steerforth, from whom I could no more keep such a secret, than I could keep a cake or any other tangible possession, about the two old women Mr. Mell had taken me to see; and I was always afraid that Steerforth would let it out, and twit him with it.", "나는 케이크나 다른 어떤 손에 잡히는 물건도 숨기지 못하는 것처럼 그런 비밀도 스티어포스에게는 숨길 수 없어서, 멜 선생님이 나를 데려가 보여 준 두 노부인 이야기를 진작 그에게 털어놓았던 터라, 이 일이 오랫동안 더더욱 나를 괴롭혔다. 나는 스티어포스가 그 일을 발설해서 그분을 놀려 댈까 봐 늘 두려웠다."]], [["We little thought, any one of us, I dare say, when I ate my breakfast that first morning, and went to sleep under the shadow of the peacock’s feathers to the sound of the flute, what consequences would come of the introduction into those alms-houses of my insignificant person.", "그 첫날 아침 내가 아침을 먹고, 피리 소리에 맞춰 공작 깃털 그늘 아래서 잠이 들 때만 해도, 우리 가운데 그 누구도 짐작하지 못했으리라. 보잘것없는 내가 그 양로원에 발을 들인 일이 어떤 결과를 불러올지 말이다."], ["But the visit had its unforeseen consequences; and of a serious sort, too, in their way.", "하지만 그 방문에는 예기치 못한 결과가 따랐고, 그것도 나름대로 자못 심각한 종류의 것이었다."]], [["One day when Mr. Creakle kept the house from indisposition, which naturally diffused a lively joy through the school, there was a good deal of noise in the course of the morning’s work.", "어느 날 크리클 선생님이 몸이 안 좋아 집에 누워 있었는데, 이는 당연하게도 학교 전체에 생기 넘치는 기쁨을 퍼뜨렸고, 그날 오전 일과 내내 소란이 자못 컸다."], ["The great relief and satisfaction experienced by the boys made them difficult to manage; and though the dreaded Tungay brought his wooden leg in twice or thrice, and took notes of the principal offenders’ names, no great impression was made by it, as they were pretty sure of getting into trouble tomorrow, do what they would, and thought it wise, no doubt, to enjoy themselves today.", "아이들은 크나큰 해방감과 만족감에 들떠서 다루기가 힘들었다. 무서운 텅게이가 나무다리를 끌고 두세 번 들어와 주모자 아이들의 이름을 적어 갔지만 별 효과가 없었다. 어차피 무슨 짓을 하든 내일이면 십중팔구 곤란에 처할 게 뻔했으니, 오늘은 실컷 즐기는 게 현명하다고 다들 여겼던 게 분명하다."]], [["It was, properly, a half-holiday; being Saturday.", "그날은 토요일이라 원래는 반공일이었다."], ["But as the noise in the playground would have disturbed Mr. Creakle, and the weather was not favourable for going out walking, we were ordered into school in the afternoon, and set some lighter tasks than usual, which were made for the occasion.", "하지만 운동장에서 떠드는 소리가 크리클 선생님을 방해할 테고, 날씨도 산책 나가기에 좋지 않아서, 우리는 오후에 교실로 들어가라는 명령을 받았고, 이날을 위해 마련된, 평소보다 가벼운 과제를 받았다."], ["It was the day of the week on which Mr. Sharp went out to get his wig curled; so Mr.", "그날은 샤프 선생님이 가발을 곱슬곱슬 말러 나가는 요일이었다."], ["Mell, who always did the drudgery, whatever it was, kept school by himself.", "그래서 무슨 궂은일이든 늘 도맡던 멜 선생님이 혼자 교실을 맡았다."], ["If I could associate the idea of a bull or a bear with anyone so mild as Mr. Mell, I should think of him, in connexion with that afternoon when the uproar was at its height, as of one of those animals, baited by a thousand dogs.", "멜 선생님처럼 온순한 사람에게 황소나 곰의 형상을 갖다 붙일 수 있다면, 소란이 절정에 달했던 그날 오후의 그분을, 천 마리 개에게 물어뜯기는 그런 짐승 가운데 하나로 떠올렸을 것이다."], ["I recall him bending his aching head, supported on his bony hand, over the book on his desk, and wretchedly endeavouring to get on with his tiresome work, amidst an uproar that might have made the Speaker of the House of Commons giddy.", "나는 그분이 욱신거리는 머리를 앙상한 손으로 받친 채 책상 위 책 쪽으로 숙이고, 하원 의장조차 어지럼증을 느꼈을 법한 소란 속에서, 그 지긋지긋한 일을 비참하게 이어 가려 애쓰던 모습이 떠오른다. 아이들은 제자리에서 들락날락하며 다른 아이들과 술래잡기를 했다."], ["Boys started in and out of their places, playing at puss in the corner with other boys; there were laughing boys, singing boys, talking boys, dancing boys, howling boys; boys shuffled with their feet, boys whirled about him, grinning, making faces, mimicking him behind his back and before his eyes; mimicking his poverty, his boots, his coat, his mother, everything belonging to him that they should have had consideration for.", "웃는 아이, 노래하는 아이, 떠드는 아이, 춤추는 아이, 울부짖는 아이들이 있었다. 아이들은 발을 질질 끌었고, 그분 주위를 빙빙 돌며 히죽거리고, 얼굴을 찡그리고, 등 뒤에서 또 면전에서 그분을 흉내 냈다. 그분의 가난을, 그분의 장화를, 그분의 외투를, 그분의 어머니를, 그분에게 딸린 것이라면 마땅히 헤아려 줬어야 할 모든 것을 흉내 냈다."]], [["‘Silence!’ cried Mr. Mell, suddenly rising up, and striking his desk with the book.", "\"조용히!\" 멜 선생님이 별안간 벌떡 일어서며 책으로 책상을 내리쳐 외쳤다."], ["‘What does this mean!", "\"이게 무슨 짓이냐!"], ["It’s impossible to bear it.", "도저히 못 참겠다."], ["It’s maddening.", "미칠 노릇이구나."], ["How can you do it to me, boys?’", "너희가 어떻게 나한테 이럴 수 있느냐?\""]], [["It was my book that he struck his desk with; and as I stood beside him, following his eye as it glanced round the room, I saw the boys all stop, some suddenly surprised, some half afraid, and some sorry perhaps.", "그분이 책상을 내리친 것은 내 책이었다. 그분 곁에 서서, 방 안을 휙 둘러보는 그분의 눈길을 따라가던 나는, 아이들이 모두 멈추는 것을 보았다. 더러는 갑작스레 놀라고, 더러는 반쯤 겁먹고, 더러는 어쩌면 미안해하는 듯했다."]], [["Steerforth’s place was at the bottom of the school, at the opposite end of the long room.", "스티어포스의 자리는 교실 맨 끝, 그 긴 방의 반대쪽 끝이었다."], ["He was lounging with his back against the wall, and his hands in his pockets, and looked at Mr. Mell with his mouth shut up as if he were whistling, when Mr. Mell looked at him.", "그는 벽에 등을 기대고 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 빈둥거리고 있었는데, 멜 선생님이 그를 쳐다보았을 때, 마치 휘파람이라도 부는 양 입을 꾹 다물고 멜 선생님을 마주 보았다."]], [["‘Silence, Mr. Steerforth!’ said Mr. Mell.", "\"조용히 해, 스티어포스 군!\" 멜 선생님이 말했다."]], [["‘Silence yourself,’ said Steerforth, turning red.", "\"선생님이나 조용히 하시죠.\" 스티어포스가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["‘Whom are you talking to?’", "\"누구한테 하는 말입니까?\""]], [["‘Sit down,’ said Mr. Mell.", "\"앉아.\" 멜 선생님이 말했다."]], [["‘Sit down yourself,’ said Steerforth, ‘and mind your business.’", "\"선생님이나 앉으시죠.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그리고 선생님 일이나 신경 쓰세요.\""]], [["There was a titter, and some applause; but Mr.", "낄낄거리는 소리와 약간의 박수가 일었다."], ["Mell was so white, that silence immediately succeeded; and one boy, who had darted out behind him to imitate his mother again, changed his mind, and pretended to want a pen mended.", "하지만 멜 선생님의 얼굴이 어찌나 하얗게 질렸던지 곧장 정적이 뒤따랐고, 그분 등 뒤로 잽싸게 튀어나가 그분 어머니를 또 흉내 내려던 한 아이는 마음을 바꿔 펜을 고쳐 달라는 척했다."]], [["‘If you think, Steerforth,’ said Mr.", "\"스티어포스 군,\" 멜 선생님이 말했다."], ["Mell, ‘that I am not acquainted with the power you can establish over any mind here’--he laid his hand, without considering what he did (as I supposed), upon my head--‘or that I have not observed you, within a few minutes, urging your juniors on to every sort of outrage against me, you are mistaken.’", "\"자네가 여기 누구의 마음이든 휘어잡을 수 있는 그 힘을 내가 모른다고 생각한다면\" — 그분은 (내 짐작에는) 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채 한 손을 내 머리에 얹었다 — \"또는 자네가 불과 몇 분 사이에 후배들을 부추겨 온갖 방식으로 나에게 행패를 부리게 하는 것을 내가 보지 못했다고 생각한다면, 그건 큰 착각이야.\""]], [["‘I don’t give myself the trouble of thinking at all about you,’ said Steerforth, coolly; ‘so I’m not mistaken, as it happens.’", "\"저는 선생님에 대해 생각하는 수고 따위는 아예 하지 않습니다.\" 스티어포스가 차갑게 말했다. \"그러니 마침 저는 착각한 게 없네요.\""]], [["‘And when you make use of your position of favouritism here, sir,’ pursued Mr. Mell, with his lip trembling very much, ‘to insult a gentleman--’", "\"그리고 자네가 여기서 총애받는 처지를 이용해서, 한 신사를 모욕하기에 이른다면—\" 멜 선생님이 입술을 몹시 떨며 말을 이었다."]], [["‘A what?--where is he?’ said Steerforth.", "\"신사라니요? 어디 있는데요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["Here somebody cried out, ‘Shame, J. Steerforth!", "이때 누군가가 외쳤다. \"부끄러운 줄 알아, 제이 스티어포스!"], ["Too bad!’ It was Traddles; whom Mr.", "너무하잖아!\" 트래들스였다."], ["Mell instantly discomfited by bidding him hold his tongue.", "멜 선생님은 곧장 그에게 입 다물라고 일러 그의 기를 꺾어 놓았다."], ["--‘To insult one who is not fortunate in life, sir, and who never gave you the least offence, and the many reasons for not insulting whom you are old enough and wise enough to understand,’ said Mr.", "\"인생에 운이 따라 주지 않고, 자네에게 한 번도 털끝만 한 해를 끼친 적이 없으며, 모욕해선 안 될 까닭이 숱하게 있는 사람을 — 그 까닭을 자네는 이해할 만큼 나이도 충분하고 지각도 충분하건만 — 모욕한다면,\""], ["Mell, with his lips trembling more and more, ‘you commit a mean and base action.", "멜 선생님이 입술을 점점 더 심하게 떨며 말했다. \"자네는 비열하고 천박한 짓을 저지르는 거야."], ["You can sit down or stand up as you please, sir.", "앉든 서든 자네 마음대로 하게, 군."], ["Copperfield, go on.’", "코퍼필드, 계속해라.\""]], [["‘Young Copperfield,’ said Steerforth, coming forward up the room, ‘stop a bit.", "\"꼬마 코퍼필드,\" 스티어포스가 교실 위쪽으로 걸어 나오며 말했다. \"잠깐 멈춰라."], ["I tell you what, Mr. Mell, once for all.", "멜 선생님, 단번에 분명히 말해 두죠."], ["When you take the liberty of calling me mean or base, or anything of that sort, you are an impudent beggar.", "선생님이 멋대로 나를 비열하다느니 천박하다느니, 그런 따위로 부른다면, 선생님은 뻔뻔스러운 거지예요."], ["You are always a beggar, you know; but when you do that, you are an impudent beggar.’", "선생님은 언제나 거지이긴 하죠. 하지만 그런 식으로 나오면, 선생님은 뻔뻔스러운 거지인 겁니다.\""]], [["I am not clear whether he was going to strike Mr. Mell, or Mr. Mell was going to strike him, or there was any such intention on either side.", "그가 멜 선생님을 치려던 것인지, 멜 선생님이 그를 치려던 것인지, 아니면 어느 쪽에도 그런 의도가 있긴 했던 것인지 나는 잘 모르겠다."], ["I saw a rigidity come upon the whole school as if they had been turned into stone, and found Mr. Creakle in the midst of us, with Tungay at his side, and Mrs. and Miss Creakle looking in at the door as if they were frightened. Mr.", "나는 학교 전체가 마치 돌로 변한 듯 뻣뻣하게 굳는 것을 보았고, 어느새 우리 한가운데 크리클 선생님이 텅게이를 옆에 거느리고 서 있는 것을, 그리고 크리클 부인과 크리클 양이 겁에 질린 듯 문간에서 들여다보고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["Mell, with his elbows on his desk and his face in his hands, sat, for some moments, quite still.", "멜 선생님은 두 팔꿈치를 책상에 짚고 얼굴을 두 손에 묻은 채, 한동안 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Mr. Mell,’ said Mr.", "\"멜 선생,\""], ["Creakle, shaking him by the arm; and his whisper was so audible now, that Tungay felt it unnecessary to repeat his words; ‘you have not forgotten yourself, I hope?’", "크리클 선생님이 그분 팔을 잡고 흔들며 말했다. 그의 속삭임은 이제 또렷이 들려서, 텅게이도 굳이 그 말을 되풀이할 필요를 느끼지 못했다. \"제정신을 잃은 건 아니길 바라오?\""]], [["‘No, sir, no,’ returned the Master, showing his face, and shaking his head, and rubbing his hands in great agitation. ‘No, sir. No.", "\"아니요, 선생님, 아닙니다.\" 멜 선생님이 얼굴을 들고, 고개를 흔들며, 몹시 동요한 채 두 손을 비비며 대답했다."], ["I have remembered myself, I--no, Mr. Creakle, I have not forgotten myself, I--I have remembered myself, sir.", "\"아닙니다, 선생님. 아니에요. 저는 제정신을 차렸습니다, 저는 — 아니요, 크리클 선생님, 저는 제정신을 잃지 않았습니다, 저는 — 저는 제정신을 차렸어요, 선생님."], ["I--I--could wish you had remembered me a little sooner, Mr.", "저는 — 저는 — 선생님이 저를 조금만 더 일찍 떠올려 주셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요, 크리클 선생님."], ["Creakle. It--it--would have been more kind, sir, more just, sir.", "그게 — 그게 — 더 친절한 처사였을 겁니다, 선생님, 더 공정한 처사였을 거고요, 선생님."], ["It would have saved me something, sir.’", "그랬으면 제가 무언가는 면했을 텐데요, 선생님.\""]], [["Mr. Creakle, looking hard at Mr. Mell, put his hand on Tungay’s shoulder, and got his feet upon the form close by, and sat upon the desk.", "크리클 선생님은 멜 선생님을 매섭게 노려보며 텅게이의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 두 발을 바로 옆 의자에 올린 채 책상 위에 걸터앉았다."], ["After still looking hard at Mr. Mell from his throne, as he shook his head, and rubbed his hands, and remained in the same state of agitation, Mr. Creakle turned to Steerforth, and said:", "그 옥좌에서 여전히 멜 선생님을 매섭게 노려보며, 멜 선생님이 고개를 흔들고 두 손을 비비며 여전히 똑같이 동요한 상태로 있는 동안, 크리클 선생님은 스티어포스를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘Now, sir, as he don’t condescend to tell me, what is this?’", "\"자, 군, 저 사람이 황송하게도 나한테 말해 주려 들지 않으니, 이게 다 무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["Steerforth evaded the question for a little while; looking in scorn and anger on his opponent, and remaining silent.", "스티어포스는 잠시 그 질문을 피했다. 멸시와 분노에 찬 눈으로 자기 상대를 바라보며 입을 다물었다."], ["I could not help thinking even in that interval, I remember, what a noble fellow he was in appearance, and how homely and plain Mr. Mell looked opposed to him.", "나는 그 짧은 순간에도, 그가 겉모습으로 얼마나 고귀한 친구로 보이는지, 그에 맞선 멜 선생님이 얼마나 초라하고 볼품없어 보이는지 생각하지 않을 수 없었던 것이 기억난다."]], [["‘What did he mean by talking about favourites, then?’ said Steerforth at length.", "\"그럼 저 사람이 총애 어쩌고 한 건 무슨 뜻이었답니까?\" 마침내 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Favourites?’ repeated Mr. Creakle, with the veins in his forehead swelling quickly.", "\"총애?\" 크리클 선생님이 이마의 핏줄을 순식간에 부풀리며 되받았다."], ["‘Who talked about favourites?’", "\"누가 총애 얘기를 했단 말이냐?\""]], [["‘He did,’ said Steerforth.", "\"저 사람이요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘And pray, what did you mean by that, sir?’ demanded Mr.", "\"한데, 도대체, 자네 그건 무슨 뜻으로 한 말이오, 선생?\""], ["Creakle, turning angrily on his assistant.", "크리클 선생님이 자기 조교를 향해 성을 내며 따져 물었다."]], [["‘I meant, Mr.", "\"제 말은요, 크리클 선생님,\""], ["Creakle,’ he returned in a low voice, ‘as I said; that no pupil had a right to avail himself of his position of favouritism to degrade me.’", "그분이 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. \"방금 말한 그대로입니다. 어떤 학생도 자기가 총애받는 처지를 이용해 저를 깎아내릴 권리는 없다는 거지요.\""]], [["‘To degrade YOU?’ said Mr.", "\"자네를 깎아내려?\""], ["Creakle. ‘My stars!", "크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["But give me leave to ask you, Mr. What’s-your-name’; and here Mr.", "\"이런 별일을 다 보겠나! 한데 한 가지 물어봅시다, 그 이름이 뭐라더라 선생.\""], ["Creakle folded his arms, cane and all, upon his chest, and made such a knot of his brows that his little eyes were hardly visible below them; ‘whether, when you talk about favourites, you showed proper respect to me?", "여기서 크리클 선생님은 회초리까지 끼고 두 팔을 가슴에 척 포개더니, 눈썹을 어찌나 잔뜩 찡그렸던지 그 아래로 작은 두 눈이 거의 보이지 않을 지경이었다. \"자네가 총애 어쩌고 떠들 때, 나한테는 마땅한 존경을 보였소?"], ["To me, sir,’ said Mr.", "나한테 말이오, 선생,\""], ["Creakle, darting his head at him suddenly, and drawing it back again, ‘the principal of this establishment, and your employer.’", "크리클 선생님이 별안간 머리를 그분 쪽으로 쑥 내밀었다가 다시 거두며 말했다. \"이 학교의 교장이자 자네 고용주인 나한테.\""]], [["‘It was not judicious, sir, I am willing to admit,’ said Mr. Mell.", "\"분별 있는 처신은 아니었습니다, 선생님, 인정합니다.\" 멜 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘I should not have done so, if I had been cool.’", "\"제가 냉정했더라면 그러지 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["Here Steerforth struck in.", "이때 스티어포스가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Then he said I was mean, and then he said I was base, and then I called him a beggar.", "\"그러더니 저 사람이 나더러 비열하다고 했고, 또 천박하다고 했고, 그래서 내가 저 사람을 거지라고 불렀습니다."], ["If I had been cool, perhaps I shouldn’t have called him a beggar.", "내가 냉정했더라면 어쩌면 거지라고 부르지 않았을지도 모르죠."], ["But I did, and I am ready to take the consequences of it.’", "하지만 나는 그렇게 불렀고, 그 대가는 기꺼이 치를 각오가 되어 있습니다.\""]], [["Without considering, perhaps, whether there were any consequences to be taken, I felt quite in a glow at this gallant speech.", "치러야 할 대가라는 게 있기나 한지는 미처 생각해 보지도 않은 채, 나는 이 늠름한 일갈에 온몸이 후끈 달아올랐다."], ["It made an impression on the boys too, for there was a low stir among them, though no one spoke a word.", "그것은 아이들에게도 인상을 남겼다. 아무도 한마디 입을 떼지는 않았지만, 그들 사이에 낮은 술렁임이 일었으니까."]], [["‘I am surprised, Steerforth--although your candour does you honour,’ said Mr.", "\"놀랍구나, 스티어포스 — 네 솔직함은 명예로운 것이지만,\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["Creakle, ‘does you honour, certainly--I am surprised, Steerforth, I must say, that you should attach such an epithet to any person employed and paid in Salem House, sir.’", "\"분명, 명예로운 것이지만 — 놀랍다, 스티어포스, 정말이지, 세일럼 하우스에 고용되어 봉급을 받는 사람에게 네가 그런 호칭을 갖다 붙이다니 말이다, 군.\""]], [["Steerforth gave a short laugh.", "스티어포스는 짧게 웃었다."]], [["‘That’s not an answer, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"그건 답이 아니다, 군,\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["Creakle, ‘to my remark.", "\"내 말에 대한 답이 아니야."], ["I expect more than that from you, Steerforth.’", "나는 너한테 그 이상을 기대한다, 스티어포스.\""]], [["If Mr. Mell looked homely, in my eyes, before the handsome boy, it would be quite impossible to say how homely Mr.", "내 눈에 멜 선생님이 그 잘생긴 아이 앞에서 초라해 보였다면, 크리클 선생님은 얼마나 초라해 보였는지 도저히 말로 다 할 수 없다."], ["Creakle looked. ‘Let him deny it,’ said Steerforth.", "\"저 사람더러 부인해 보라고 하시죠.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Deny that he is a beggar, Steerforth?’ cried Mr. Creakle.", "\"저 사람이 거지라는 걸 부인하라고, 스티어포스?\" 크리클 선생님이 외쳤다."], ["‘Why, where does he go a-begging?’", "\"아니, 저 사람이 어디 가서 동냥을 한단 말이냐?\""]], [["‘If he is not a beggar himself, his near relation’s one,’ said Steerforth.", "\"저 사람 본인이 거지가 아니라면, 가까운 친척이 거지예요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘It’s all the same.’", "\"그게 그거죠.\""]], [["He glanced at me, and Mr. Mell’s hand gently patted me upon the shoulder.", "그가 나를 흘끗 쳐다보았고, 멜 선생님의 손이 내 어깨를 가만히 토닥였다."], ["I looked up with a flush upon my face and remorse in my heart, but Mr. Mell’s eyes were fixed on Steerforth.", "나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르고 가슴엔 가책이 차오른 채 고개를 들었는데, 멜 선생님의 눈은 스티어포스에게 고정되어 있었다."], ["He continued to pat me kindly on the shoulder, but he looked at him.", "그분은 내 어깨를 다정하게 계속 토닥였지만, 시선은 스티어포스를 향했다."]], [["‘Since you expect me, Mr.", "\"크리클 선생님이 제가 스스로를 변호하길 바라시고,\""], ["Creakle, to justify myself,’ said Steerforth, ‘and to say what I mean,--what I have to say is, that his mother lives on charity in an alms-house.’", "스티어포스가 말했다. \"제 말뜻을 밝히길 바라시니 — 제가 할 말은, 저 사람 어머니가 양로원에서 자선에 기대어 산다는 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Mell still looked at him, and still patted me kindly on the shoulder, and said to himself, in a whisper, if I heard right: ‘Yes, I thought so.’", "멜 선생님은 여전히 그를 바라보며, 여전히 내 어깨를 다정하게 토닥이면서, 내가 제대로 들었다면, 속삭이듯 혼잣말을 했다. \"그래, 그럴 줄 알았어.\""]], [["Mr. Creakle turned to his assistant, with a severe frown and laboured politeness:", "크리클 선생님이 매서운 눈살과 억지스러운 정중함으로 자기 조교를 돌아보았다."]], [["‘Now, you hear what this gentleman says, Mr. Mell.", "\"자, 이 도련님이 하는 말을 들었을 거요, 멜 선생."], ["Have the goodness, if you please, to set him right before the assembled school.’", "부디 모쪼록, 모인 학교 학생들 앞에서 이 도련님 말을 바로잡아 주시오.\""]], [["‘He is right, sir, without correction,’ returned Mr.", "\"바로잡을 것도 없이 도련님 말이 맞습니다, 선생님.\""], ["Mell, in the midst of a dead silence; ‘what he has said is true.’", "멜 선생님이 쥐 죽은 듯한 정적 속에서 대답했다. \"저 학생이 한 말은 사실입니다.\""]], [["‘Be so good then as declare publicly, will you,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 부디, 공개적으로 밝혀 주겠소,\""], ["Creakle, putting his head on one side, and rolling his eyes round the school, ‘whether it ever came to my knowledge until this moment?’", "크리클 선생님이 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 두 눈을 굴려 학교를 휘둘러보며 말했다. \"이 일이 이 순간까지 단 한 번이라도 내 귀에 들어온 적이 있었는지를 말이오?\""]], [["‘I believe not directly,’ he returned.", "\"직접적으로는 없었지 싶습니다.\" 그분이 대답했다."]], [["‘Why, you know not,’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"이거 봐, 모를 리가 없잖소,\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘Don’t you, man?’", "\"안 그렇소, 선생?\""]], [["‘I apprehend you never supposed my worldly circumstances to be very good,’ replied the assistant.", "\"선생님은 제 형편이 그리 넉넉하다고는 한 번도 여기지 않으셨으리라 봅니다.\" 조교가 대답했다."], ["‘You know what my position is, and always has been, here.’", "\"여기서 제 처지가 어떤지, 또 줄곧 어떠했는지 잘 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["‘I apprehend, if you come to that,’ said Mr.", "\"그 얘기를 꺼내자면 말이오,\""], ["Creakle, with his veins swelling again bigger than ever, ‘that you’ve been in a wrong position altogether, and mistook this for a charity school. Mr.", "크리클 선생님이 핏줄을 그 어느 때보다 더 굵게 부풀리며 말했다. \"내가 보기에 자네는 애초부터 처지를 잘못 잡은 거고, 여길 자선 학교로 착각한 거요."], ["Mell, we’ll part, if you please. The sooner the better.’", "멜 선생, 그럼 갈라섭시다, 괜찮다면. 빠를수록 좋소.\""]], [["‘There is no time,’ answered Mr.", "\"지금만 한 때가,\" 멜 선생님이 자리에서 일어서며 대답했다."], ["Mell, rising, ‘like the present.’", "\"어디 또 있겠습니까.\""]], [["‘Sir, to you!’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"잘 가시오, 선생!\" 크리클 선생님이 말했다."]], [["‘I take my leave of you, Mr. Creakle, and all of you,’ said Mr.", "\"작별을 고합니다, 크리클 선생님, 그리고 여러분 모두에게도.\""], ["Mell, glancing round the room, and again patting me gently on the shoulders.", "멜 선생님이 방 안을 둘러보고는, 다시 한번 내 어깨를 가만히 토닥이며 말했다."], ["‘James Steerforth, the best wish I can leave you is that you may come to be ashamed of what you have done today.", "\"제임스 스티어포스, 내가 자네에게 남길 수 있는 가장 좋은 바람은, 자네가 오늘 저지른 일을 언젠가 부끄러워하게 되는 것이네."], ["At present I would prefer to see you anything rather than a friend, to me, or to anyone in whom I feel an interest.’", "지금으로선, 나에게든, 내가 마음 쓰는 그 누구에게든, 자네가 친구가 되는 것 말고 다른 무엇이라도 되는 편이 차라리 낫겠네.\""]], [["Once more he laid his hand upon my shoulder; and then taking his flute and a few books from his desk, and leaving the key in it for his successor, he went out of the school, with his property under his arm. Mr.", "그분은 다시 한번 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 그러고는 책상에서 피리와 책 몇 권을 챙기고, 후임자를 위해 책상에 열쇠를 꽂아 둔 채, 제 짐을 옆구리에 끼고 교실을 나갔다."], ["Creakle then made a speech, through Tungay, in which he thanked Steerforth for asserting (though perhaps too warmly) the independence and respectability of Salem House; and which he wound up by shaking hands with Steerforth, while we gave three cheers--I did not quite know what for, but I supposed for Steerforth, and so joined in them ardently, though I felt miserable. Mr.", "그러자 크리클 선생님은 텅게이를 통해 한바탕 연설을 했는데, 세일럼 하우스의 독립성과 명예를 (어쩌면 너무 열을 올리긴 했어도) 지켜 세운 스티어포스에게 고맙다고 했고, 스티어포스와 악수를 나누며 연설을 끝맺었다. 그동안 우리는 만세 삼창을 했다. 나는 무엇을 위한 만세인지 잘 몰랐지만, 스티어포스를 위한 것이려니 짐작하고 비참한 심정이면서도 열렬히 함께 외쳤다."], ["Creakle then caned Tommy Traddles for being discovered in tears, instead of cheers, on account of Mr. Mell’s departure; and went back to his sofa, or his bed, or wherever he had come from.", "그러고 나서 크리클 선생님은 멜 선생님이 떠난 일을 두고 만세 대신 눈물을 흘리다 들킨 토미 트래들스를 회초리로 때린 뒤, 자기 소파로든 침대로든, 그가 나온 그 어디로든 돌아갔다."]], [["We were left to ourselves now, and looked very blank, I recollect, on one another.", "이제 우리끼리만 남게 되자, 우리는 멍한 얼굴로 서로를 바라보았던 것이 기억난다."], ["For myself, I felt so much self-reproach and contrition for my part in what had happened, that nothing would have enabled me to keep back my tears but the fear that Steerforth, who often looked at me, I saw, might think it unfriendly--or, I should rather say, considering our relative ages, and the feeling with which I regarded him, undutiful--if I showed the emotion which distressed me.", "나로 말하자면, 그날 벌어진 일에서 내가 한 몫에 대해 자책과 뉘우침이 어찌나 컸던지, 스티어포스가 — 내가 보기에 그는 나를 자주 쳐다보았다 — 내가 나를 짓누르던 그 감정을 드러내면 그것을 우정에 어긋난다고, 아니, 우리의 나이 차이와 내가 그를 우러르던 마음을 헤아리자면 도리에 어긋난다고 여길까 봐 두려운 것 말고는, 그 무엇도 내 눈물을 막아 줄 수 없었다."], ["He was very angry with Traddles, and said he was glad he had caught it.", "그는 트래들스에게 몹시 화가 나서, 그가 매를 맞아 고소하다고 했다."]], [["Poor Traddles, who had passed the stage of lying with his head upon the desk, and was relieving himself as usual with a burst of skeletons, said he didn’t care. Mr. Mell was ill-used.", "가엾은 트래들스는, 책상에 머리를 처박는 단계는 이미 넘어서서 여느 때처럼 해골을 한바탕 그려 대며 마음을 달래는 중이었는데, 자기는 상관없다고 했다. 멜 선생님이 부당한 대접을 받았다고 했다."]], [["‘Who has ill-used him, you girl?’ said Steerforth.", "\"누가 그분을 부당하게 대했다는 거야, 이 계집애 같은 녀석아?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, you have,’ returned Traddles.", "\"아니, 네가 그랬잖아.\" 트래들스가 되받았다."]], [["‘What have I done?’ said Steerforth.", "\"내가 뭘 어쨌는데?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘What have you done?’ retorted Traddles.", "\"네가 뭘 어쨌냐고?\" 트래들스가 쏘아붙였다."], ["‘Hurt his feelings, and lost him his situation.’", "\"그분 마음에 상처를 주고, 그분 일자리까지 잃게 했잖아.\""]], [["‘His feelings?’ repeated Steerforth disdainfully.", "\"그분 마음?\" 스티어포스가 경멸하듯 되뇌었다."], ["‘His feelings will soon get the better of it, I’ll be bound.", "\"그분 마음쯤이야 곧 추슬러질 게 뻔하다, 장담하지."], ["His feelings are not like yours, Miss Traddles.", "그분 마음은 너 같은 거랑은 다르거든, 트래들스 아가씨."], ["As to his situation--which was a precious one, wasn’t it?--do you suppose I am not going to write home, and take care that he gets some money?", "그분 일자리로 말하자면 — 그게 뭐 대단한 자리이기라도 했냐? — 내가 집에 편지를 써서 그분이 돈푼이라도 받게끔 손쓰지 않을 것 같아?"], ["Polly?’", "폴리 양?\""]], [["We thought this intention very noble in Steerforth, whose mother was a widow, and rich, and would do almost anything, it was said, that he asked her.", "우리는 스티어포스의 이 마음 씀씀이를 무척 고귀하게 여겼다. 그의 어머니는 과부에 부자였고, 그가 부탁하는 거라면 거의 무엇이든 들어준다는 말이 있었으니까."], ["We were all extremely glad to see Traddles so put down, and exalted Steerforth to the skies: especially when he told us, as he condescended to do, that what he had done had been done expressly for us, and for our cause; and that he had conferred a great boon upon us by unselfishly doing it.", "우리는 트래들스가 그렇게 코가 납작해진 것이 너무도 통쾌했고, 스티어포스를 하늘 높이 떠받들었다. 특히 그가 황송하게도 우리에게, 자기가 한 일은 다 우리를 위해, 우리의 대의를 위해 한 것이며, 사심 없이 그 일을 함으로써 우리에게 큰 은혜를 베푼 것이라고 말해 주자 더욱 그랬다."], ["But I must say that when I was going on with a story in the dark that night, Mr. Mell’s old flute seemed more than once to sound mournfully in my ears; and that when at last Steerforth was tired, and I lay down in my bed, I fancied it playing so sorrowfully somewhere, that I was quite wretched.", "하지만 솔직히 말하지 않을 수 없다. 그날 밤 어둠 속에서 이야기를 이어 가던 중에 멜 선생님의 그 낡은 피리가 내 귓가에 한두 번 구슬프게 울리는 것 같았고, 마침내 스티어포스가 지쳐서 내가 잠자리에 누웠을 때, 어디선가 그 피리가 어찌나 서글프게 연주되는 것만 같던지, 나는 도저히 비참한 심정을 가눌 수 없었다."]], [["I soon forgot him in the contemplation of Steerforth, who, in an easy amateur way, and without any book (he seemed to me to know everything by heart), took some of his classes until a new master was found.", "나는 곧 스티어포스를 바라보며 그를 잊어버렸다. 새 선생님을 구할 때까지, 스티어포스는 책도 한 권 없이 (나에게는 그가 모든 걸 외우고 있는 것처럼 보였다) 느긋한 아마추어 솜씨로 몇몇 반을 맡아 가르쳤다."], ["The new master came from a grammar school; and before he entered on his duties, dined in the parlour one day, to be introduced to Steerforth.", "새 선생님은 어느 문법 학교에서 왔는데, 부임하기 전에 스티어포스에게 소개되려고 어느 날 응접실에서 식사를 했다."], ["Steerforth approved of him highly, and told us he was a Brick.", "스티어포스는 그를 무척 마음에 들어 했고, 우리에게 그가 '벽돌' 같은 사람이라고 했다."], ["Without exactly understanding what learned distinction was meant by this, I respected him greatly for it, and had no doubt whatever of his superior knowledge: though he never took the pains with me--not that I was anybody--that Mr. Mell had taken.", "이게 무슨 학식 높은 구별을 뜻하는지 정확히는 몰랐지만, 나는 그 말 때문에 그를 크게 존경했고, 그가 더 뛰어난 지식을 갖췄음을 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 비록 그는 멜 선생님이 나에게 기울였던 만큼의 수고를 — 내가 뭐 대단한 사람이라서가 아니지만 — 나에게 한 번도 기울이지 않았지만 말이다."]], [["There was only one other event in this half-year, out of the daily school-life, that made an impression upon me which still survives. It survives for many reasons.", "이 학기 동안, 날마다 되풀이되던 학교생활 말고, 지금까지도 가시지 않은 인상을 나에게 남긴 사건이 딱 하나 더 있었다. 그것이 가시지 않는 데에는 여러 까닭이 있다."]], [["One afternoon, when we were all harassed into a state of dire confusion, and Mr. Creakle was laying about him dreadfully, Tungay came in, and called out in his usual strong way: ‘Visitors for Copperfield!’", "어느 날 오후, 우리가 모두 시달릴 대로 시달려 끔찍한 혼란에 빠져 있고, 크리클 선생님이 무시무시하게 사방을 후려쳐 대고 있을 때, 텅게이가 들어와 여느 때처럼 우렁찬 소리로 외쳤다. \"코퍼필드한테 손님이오!\""]], [["A few words were interchanged between him and Mr. Creakle, as, who the visitors were, and what room they were to be shown into; and then I, who had, according to custom, stood up on the announcement being made, and felt quite faint with astonishment, was told to go by the back stairs and get a clean frill on, before I repaired to the dining-room.", "손님이 누구이며 어느 방으로 안내해야 할지를 두고 그와 크리클 선생님 사이에 몇 마디가 오갔다. 그러고 나서, 관례대로 호명이 떨어지자 벌떡 일어서서 놀라움에 정신이 아득해져 있던 나는, 식당으로 가기 전에 뒤쪽 계단으로 가서 깨끗한 옷깃을 달고 오라는 말을 들었다."], ["These orders I obeyed, in such a flutter and hurry of my young spirits as I had never known before; and when I got to the parlour door, and the thought came into my head that it might be my mother--I had only thought of Mr. or Miss Murdstone until then--I drew back my hand from the lock, and stopped to have a sob before I went in.", "나는 그 명령에 따랐는데, 어린 마음이 그토록 떨리고 다급하게 두근거린 적은 그전에 없었다. 그리고 응접실 문에 다다라, 어쩌면 어머니일지도 모른다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐을 때 — 그때까지 나는 머드스톤 씨나 머드스톤 양만 떠올리고 있었던 것이다 — 나는 문고리에서 손을 거두고 잠시 멈춰 서서 한 번 흐느낀 뒤에야 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["At first I saw nobody; but feeling a pressure against the door, I looked round it, and there, to my amazement, were Mr. Peggotty and Ham, ducking at me with their hats, and squeezing one another against the wall.", "처음엔 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그런데 문에 무언가 눌리는 느낌이 들어 문 뒤를 돌아보니, 놀랍게도 거기에 패거티 씨와 햄이 모자를 들어 나에게 꾸벅거리며, 서로를 벽에 밀어붙이고 있었다."], ["I could not help laughing; but it was much more in the pleasure of seeing them, than at the appearance they made.", "나는 웃지 않을 수 없었다. 하지만 그건 그들의 꼬락서니가 우스워서라기보다는 그들을 보는 기쁨이 훨씬 컸기 때문이었다."], ["We shook hands in a very cordial way; and I laughed and laughed, until I pulled out my pocket-handkerchief and wiped my eyes.", "우리는 무척 정겹게 악수를 나누었고, 나는 웃고 또 웃다가 결국 손수건을 꺼내 눈을 닦았다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty (who never shut his mouth once, I remember, during the visit) showed great concern when he saw me do this, and nudged Ham to say something.", "패거티 씨는 (그 방문 내내 입을 한 번도 다물지 않았던 게 기억난다) 내가 그러는 것을 보고 몹시 걱정스러워하며, 햄을 슬쩍 찔러 뭐라도 말하라고 했다."]], [["‘Cheer up, Mas’r Davy bor’!’ said Ham, in his simpering way.", "\"기운 내요, 데이비 도련님!\" 햄이 헤벌쭉 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘Why, how you have growed!’", "\"아니, 어쩜 이렇게 컸대요!\""]], [["‘Am I grown?’ I said, drying my eyes.", "\"내가 컸어?\" 나는 눈을 닦으며 말했다."], ["I was not crying at anything in particular that I know of; but somehow it made me cry, to see old friends.", "딱히 무슨 일로 운 것도 아니었는데, 어쩐지 옛 친구들을 보니 눈물이 났다."]], [["‘Growed, Mas’r Davy bor’?", "\"컸지요, 데이비 도련님?"], ["Ain’t he growed!’ said Ham.", "안 컸나, 이거!\" 햄이 말했다."]], [["‘Ain’t he growed!’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"안 컸나, 이거!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["They made me laugh again by laughing at each other, and then we all three laughed until I was in danger of crying again.", "그들이 서로를 보고 웃어 대는 통에 나는 다시 웃음이 터졌고, 그러다 우리 셋은 내가 또 울 뻔할 만큼 웃어 댔다."]], [["‘Do you know how mama is, Mr.", "\"엄마는 잘 계세요, 패거티 씨?\""], ["Peggotty?’ I said. ‘And how my dear, dear, old Peggotty is?’", "내가 말했다. \"그리고 우리 정답고 정다운, 그리운 패거티는요?\""]], [["‘Oncommon,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"기차게 잘 있지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And little Em’ly, and Mrs. Gummidge?’", "\"그럼 꼬마 에밀리랑 거미지 부인은요?\""]], [["‘On--common,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"기—차게 잘 있어요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["There was a silence. Mr.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Peggotty, to relieve it, took two prodigious lobsters, and an enormous crab, and a large canvas bag of shrimps, out of his pockets, and piled them up in Ham’s arms.", "패거티 씨는 그 침묵을 메우려고 주머니에서 어마어마하게 큰 바닷가재 두 마리와 엄청난 게 한 마리, 그리고 새우가 가득 든 커다란 천 자루를 꺼내, 햄의 두 팔에 차곡차곡 쌓아 올렸다."]], [["‘You see,’ said Mr.", "\"실은 말이죠,\""], ["Peggotty, ‘knowing as you was partial to a little relish with your wittles when you was along with us, we took the liberty.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"도련님이 우리랑 같이 있을 적에 끼니에 곁들이는 군것질을 좋아했던 게 생각나서, 외람되지만 좀 가져왔지요."], ["The old Mawther biled ‘em, she did. Mrs.", "그 늙은 아낙이 삶았어요, 그랬다고요."], ["Gummidge biled ‘em. Yes,’ said Mr.", "거미지 부인이 삶았지요. 그렇소,\""], ["Peggotty, slowly, who I thought appeared to stick to the subject on account of having no other subject ready, ‘Mrs.", "패거티 씨가 천천히 말했는데, 내 생각에 그는 달리 꺼낼 화제가 마땅찮아서 이 화제에 매달리는 듯했다."], ["Gummidge, I do assure you, she biled ‘em.’", "\"거미지 부인이, 내 장담하는데, 그분이 삶았다고요.\""]], [["I expressed my thanks; and Mr. Peggotty, after looking at Ham, who stood smiling sheepishly over the shellfish, without making any attempt to help him, said:", "내가 고맙다는 인사를 하자, 패거티 씨는 조개붙이를 안고 멋쩍게 웃으며 서 있는, 그러나 그를 거들 생각은 도통 안 하는 햄을 한번 쳐다보더니 말했다."]], [["‘We come, you see, the wind and tide making in our favour, in one of our Yarmouth lugs to Gravesen’.", "\"보다시피, 바람도 물때도 우리한테 맞춰 줘서, 우리 야머스 거룻배 한 척을 타고 그레이브젠드까지 왔답니다."], ["My sister she wrote to me the name of this here place, and wrote to me as if ever I chanced to come to Gravesen’, I was to come over and inquire for Mas’r Davy and give her dooty, humbly wishing him well and reporting of the fam’ly as they was oncommon toe-be-sure.", "누이가 나한테 이 동네 이름을 편지에 적어 보냈는데, 혹시라도 내가 그레이브젠드에 들를 일이 생기거든 건너가서 데이비 도련님을 찾아 안부를 전하고, 부디 잘 지내시길 바라며 식구들도 죄다 기차게 잘 있다고 알려 드리라 했지요. 꼬마 에밀리는 말이죠, 내가 돌아가면 누이한테 편지를 쓸 거예요."], ["Little Em’ly, you see, she’ll write to my sister when I go back, as I see you and as you was similarly oncommon, and so we make it quite a merry-go-rounder.’", "내가 도련님을 만났는데 도련님도 마찬가지로 기차게 잘 있더라고요. 그래서 우린 이걸 아주 빙글빙글 도는 회전목마처럼 만드는 셈이지요.\""]], [["I was obliged to consider a little before I understood what Mr. Peggotty meant by this figure, expressive of a complete circle of intelligence.", "나는 소식이 한 바퀴 빙 도는 것을 나타낸 패거티 씨의 그 표현을 알아듣기까지 잠시 생각을 좀 해야 했다."], ["I then thanked him heartily; and said, with a consciousness of reddening, that I supposed little Em’ly was altered too, since we used to pick up shells and pebbles on the beach?", "그러고 나서 나는 진심으로 고맙다고 했고, 얼굴이 붉어지는 걸 의식하면서, 우리가 해변에서 조가비와 조약돌을 줍던 그 시절과는 꼬마 에밀리도 달라졌겠지요, 하고 말했다."]], [["‘She’s getting to be a woman, that’s wot she’s getting to be,’ said Mr.", "\"걘 여자가 다 되어 가고 있지요, 그게 걔가 되어 가는 거예요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty. ‘Ask HIM.’ He meant Ham, who beamed with delight and assent over the bag of shrimps.", "\"쟤한테 물어봐요.\" 그가 가리킨 건 햄이었는데, 햄은 새우 자루 위로 기쁨과 동의에 겨워 함박웃음을 지었다."]], [["‘Her pretty face!’ said Mr.", "\"그 예쁜 얼굴이라니!\""], ["Peggotty, with his own shining like a light.", "패거티 씨가 제 얼굴을 등불처럼 환히 빛내며 말했다."]], [["‘Her learning!’ said Ham.", "\"걔 공부 솜씨라니!\" 햄이 말했다."]], [["‘Her writing!’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"걔 글씨라니!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Why it’s as black as jet!", "\"아, 그게 흑옥처럼 새까맣다니까요!"], ["And so large it is, you might see it anywheres.’", "게다가 얼마나 큼지막한지, 어디서든 다 보일 정도라오.\""]], [["It was perfectly delightful to behold with what enthusiasm Mr. Peggotty became inspired when he thought of his little favourite.", "패거티 씨가 그 어린 귀염둥이를 떠올리며 어찌나 신명이 솟던지, 보고 있자니 더없이 흐뭇했다. 그가 지금도 내 앞에 서 있는 듯하다."], ["He stands before me again, his bluff hairy face irradiating with a joyful love and pride, for which I can find no description.", "텁수룩하니 거친 얼굴이 기쁜 사랑과 자랑으로 환히 빛나는데, 나는 그것을 뭐라 형용할 길이 없다."], ["His honest eyes fire up, and sparkle, as if their depths were stirred by something bright.", "그의 정직한 두 눈이 마치 깊은 속에서 무언가 환한 것이 일렁이기라도 하듯 불꽃처럼 타오르며 반짝인다."], ["His broad chest heaves with pleasure.", "그의 떡 벌어진 가슴이 기쁨으로 들썩인다."], ["His strong loose hands clench themselves, in his earnestness; and he emphasizes what he says with a right arm that shows, in my pigmy view, like a sledge-hammer.", "그의 억세고 헐거운 두 손이 진심에 겨워 불끈 쥐어진다. 그리고 그는 자기가 하는 말을, 내 난쟁이 같은 눈에는 큰 쇠망치처럼 보이는 오른팔을 휘저으며 힘주어 강조한다."]], [["Ham was quite as earnest as he.", "햄도 그 못지않게 진지했다. 짐작건대 그들은 에밀리에 관해 훨씬 더 많은 이야기를 했을 텐데, 스티어포스가 뜻밖에 들어오는 바람에 머쓱해지고 말았다."], ["I dare say they would have said much more about her, if they had not been abashed by the unexpected coming in of Steerforth, who, seeing me in a corner speaking with two strangers, stopped in a song he was singing, and said: ‘I didn’t know you were here, young Copperfield!’ (for it was not the usual visiting room) and crossed by us on his way out.", "스티어포스는 내가 한구석에서 낯선 두 사람과 이야기하는 것을 보고는, 부르던 노래를 뚝 그치고 말했다. \"여기 있는 줄은 몰랐네, 꼬마 코퍼필드!\" (여기는 여느 때 손님을 맞는 방이 아니었으니까) 그러고는 나가는 길에 우리 곁을 가로질러 갔다."]], [["I am not sure whether it was in the pride of having such a friend as Steerforth, or in the desire to explain to him how I came to have such a friend as Mr.", "스티어포스 같은 친구가 있다는 자부심에서였는지, 아니면 어쩌다 패거티 씨 같은 친구를 두게 되었는지를 그에게 설명하고 싶은 마음에서였는지는 잘 모르겠다."], ["Peggotty, that I called to him as he was going away.", "어쨌든 나는 그가 떠나려는 참에 그를 불러 세웠다."], ["But I said, modestly--Good Heaven, how it all comes back to me this long time afterwards--!", "그리고 나는 겸손하게 말했다 — 아아, 이 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에 그 모든 것이 어찌 이리 생생히 되살아나는지! —"]], [["‘Don’t go, Steerforth, if you please.", "\"가지 마, 스티어포스, 괜찮다면 말이야."], ["These are two Yarmouth boatmen--very kind, good people--who are relations of my nurse, and have come from Gravesend to see me.’", "이분들은 야머스에서 온 뱃사람 두 분인데 — 무척 친절하고 좋은 분들이야 — 우리 유모의 친척이고, 나를 보려고 그레이브젠드에서 오셨어.\""]], [["‘Aye, aye?’ said Steerforth, returning.", "\"아, 그래?\" 스티어포스가 되돌아오며 말했다."], ["‘I am glad to see them.", "\"두 분을 뵙게 되어 반갑습니다."], ["How are you both?’", "두 분 다 안녕하세요?\""]], [["There was an ease in his manner--a gay and light manner it was, but not swaggering--which I still believe to have borne a kind of enchantment with it.", "그의 태도에는 어떤 편안함이 있었다 — 명랑하고 가뿐한 태도였지만 거들먹거리는 데가 전혀 없었다 — 나는 그것이 일종의 마법을 품고 있었다고 지금도 믿는다."], ["I still believe him, in virtue of this carriage, his animal spirits, his delightful voice, his handsome face and figure, and, for aught I know, of some inborn power of attraction besides (which I think a few people possess), to have carried a spell with him to which it was a natural weakness to yield, and which not many persons could withstand.", "나는 지금도 믿는다. 이 몸가짐과 넘치는 생기, 듣기 좋은 목소리, 잘생긴 얼굴과 체격, 그리고 내가 알지 못하는 어떤 타고난 매력의 힘까지 (몇몇 사람은 그런 것을 지녔다고 나는 생각한다) 더해져, 그가 어떤 주문을 지니고 다녔으며, 거기에 굴복하는 것은 자연스러운 약점이었고 좀처럼 거역할 수 있는 사람이 많지 않았으리라고 말이다."], ["I could not but see how pleased they were with him, and how they seemed to open their hearts to him in a moment.", "나는 그들이 그를 얼마나 마음에 들어 하는지, 또 어떻게 한순간에 그에게 마음을 활짝 여는지를 보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘You must let them know at home, if you please, Mr.", "\"부디 그 편지를 보내실 때, 패거티 씨,\""], ["Peggotty,’ I said, ‘when that letter is sent, that Mr.", "내가 말했다. \"집에 알려 주세요."], ["Steerforth is very kind to me, and that I don’t know what I should ever do here without him.’", "스티어포스 씨가 나한테 무척 잘해 준다고, 그가 없으면 여기서 어떻게 지냈을지 모르겠다고요.\""]], [["‘Nonsense!’ said Steerforth, laughing.", "\"무슨 소리.\" 스티어포스가 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘You mustn’t tell them anything of the sort.’", "\"그런 건 절대 알리면 안 돼.\""]], [["‘And if Mr. Steerforth ever comes into Norfolk or Suffolk, Mr.", "\"그리고 스티어포스 씨가 혹시라도 노퍽이나 서퍽에 오게 되면, 패거티 씨,\""], ["Peggotty,’ I said, ‘while I am there, you may depend upon it I shall bring him to Yarmouth, if he will let me, to see your house.", "내가 말했다. \"내가 거기 있을 때라면, 장담하는데, 본인만 허락한다면 야머스로 데려가서 댁을 보여 드릴게요."], ["You never saw such a good house, Steerforth.", "너 이런 멋진 집은 본 적 없을걸, 스티어포스."], ["It’s made out of a boat!’", "배로 만든 집이거든!\""]], [["‘Made out of a boat, is it?’ said Steerforth.", "\"배로 만들었다고?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘It’s the right sort of a house for such a thorough-built boatman.’", "\"뼛속까지 뱃사람인 분에게 딱 어울리는 집이로군.\""]], [["‘So ‘tis, sir, so ‘tis, sir,’ said Ham, grinning.", "\"그렇지요, 도련님, 그렇고말고요, 도련님.\""], ["‘You’re right, young gen’l’m’n!", "햄이 싱글거리며 말했다. \"맞아요, 젊은 양반!"], ["Mas’r Davy bor’, gen’l’m’n’s right.", "데이비 도련님, 이 양반 말이 옳아요."], ["A thorough-built boatman!", "뼛속까지 뱃사람이라니! 허, 허!"], ["Hor, hor! That’s what he is, too!’", "바로 그게 이 양반이지요, 정말!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty was no less pleased than his nephew, though his modesty forbade him to claim a personal compliment so vociferously.", "패거티 씨도 조카 못지않게 흐뭇해했지만, 겸손한 성미라 그렇게 떠들썩하게 자기 칭찬을 받아들이지는 못했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ he said, bowing and chuckling, and tucking in the ends of his neckerchief at his breast: ‘I thankee, sir, I thankee!", "\"음, 그게, 도련님,\" 그가 꾸벅꾸벅 절을 하고 껄껄 웃으며, 목도리 끝자락을 가슴께로 여며 넣으며 말했다. \"고맙소이다, 도련님, 고맙소!"], ["I do my endeavours in my line of life, sir.’", "나야 내 분야에서 그저 애쓸 따름이지요, 도련님.\""]], [["‘The best of men can do no more, Mr. Peggotty,’ said Steerforth.", "\"세상 가장 훌륭한 사람도 그 이상은 못 하는 법이죠, 패거티 씨.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["He had got his name already.", "그는 벌써 그의 이름을 알고 있었다."]], [["‘I’ll pound it, it’s wot you do yourself, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"내 장담컨대, 그건 도련님 본인이 하는 일이지요, 도련님.\""], ["Peggotty, shaking his head, ‘and wot you do well--right well! I thankee, sir.", "패거티 씨가 고개를 흔들며 말했다. \"게다가 아주 잘 해내고요 — 아주 잘!"], ["I’m obleeged to you, sir, for your welcoming manner of me.", "고맙소이다, 도련님. 이렇게 나를 반갑게 맞아 줘서 고마워요, 도련님."], ["I’m rough, sir, but I’m ready--least ways, I hope I’m ready, you unnerstand.", "나야 거칠지만, 그래도 준비된 사람이오 — 적어도, 준비된 사람이길 바라지요, 무슨 말인지 알겠죠."], ["My house ain’t much for to see, sir, but it’s hearty at your service if ever you should come along with Mas’r Davy to see it.", "우리 집이야 뭐 볼 게 별로 없소이다만, 도련님이 행여 데이비 도련님이랑 같이 보러 오게 되거든 진심으로 맞이하리다."], ["I’m a reg’lar Dodman, I am,’ said Mr.", "나야 천상 굼벵이라서,\""], ["Peggotty, by which he meant snail, and this was in allusion to his being slow to go, for he had attempted to go after every sentence, and had somehow or other come back again; ‘but I wish you both well, and I wish you happy!’", "패거티 씨가 말했는데, 이건 달팽이를 뜻하는 거였고, 그가 갈 듯 갈 듯 떠나지 못하는 걸 빗댄 말이었다. 실은 그는 말끝마다 가려고 했다가 어찌어찌 도로 돌아오곤 했으니까. \"어쨌거나 두 분 다 잘 지내시길, 행복하시길 바라오!\""]], [["Ham echoed this sentiment, and we parted with them in the heartiest manner.", "햄도 이 말에 맞장구를 쳤고, 우리는 더없이 정겹게 그들과 작별했다."], ["I was almost tempted that evening to tell Steerforth about pretty little Em’ly, but I was too timid of mentioning her name, and too much afraid of his laughing at me.", "그날 저녁 나는 예쁜 꼬마 에밀리 이야기를 스티어포스에게 하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았지만, 그녀의 이름을 입에 올리기가 너무 수줍었고, 그가 나를 놀려 댈까 봐 너무 겁이 났다."], ["I remember that I thought a good deal, and in an uneasy sort of way, about Mr. Peggotty having said that she was getting on to be a woman; but I decided that was nonsense.", "패거티 씨가 그녀가 여자가 다 되어 간다고 한 말이, 어쩐지 마음 한구석을 영 불편하게 휘저어 놓아 나는 한참을 곱씹었던 것이 기억난다. 하지만 나는 그게 다 부질없는 소리라고 결론지었다."]], [["We transported the shellfish, or the ‘relish’ as Mr. Peggotty had modestly called it, up into our room unobserved, and made a great supper that evening.", "우리는 조개붙이를, 아니 패거티 씨가 겸손하게 일컬은 대로 그 '곁들이 거리'를 아무도 모르게 우리 방으로 날라다, 그날 저녁 거나하게 잔치를 벌였다."], ["But Traddles couldn’t get happily out of it.", "하지만 트래들스는 그 일에서 무사히 빠져나오질 못했다."], ["He was too unfortunate even to come through a supper like anybody else.", "그는 남들처럼 평범하게 잔치 한 끼를 치르고 넘어갈 정도의 운조차 없었다."], ["He was taken ill in the night--quite prostrate he was--in consequence of Crab; and after being drugged with black draughts and blue pills, to an extent which Demple (whose father was a doctor) said was enough to undermine a horse’s constitution, received a caning and six chapters of Greek Testament for refusing to confess.", "그는 한밤중에 게 때문에 탈이 나서 — 아주 녹초가 되도록 앓았다 — 그리고 (아버지가 의사인) 뎀플이 말로는 말 한 마리의 기력도 거덜낼 만한 양이라던 검은 물약과 파란 알약을 잔뜩 처방받아 먹은 뒤에도, 자백하기를 거부했다는 이유로 회초리찜질과 그리스어 신약 여섯 장을 받았다."]], [["The rest of the half-year is a jumble in my recollection of the daily strife and struggle of our lives; of the waning summer and the changing season; of the frosty mornings when we were rung out of bed, and the cold, cold smell of the dark nights when we were rung into bed again; of the evening schoolroom dimly lighted and indifferently warmed, and the morning schoolroom which was nothing but a great shivering-machine; of the alternation of boiled beef with roast beef, and boiled mutton with roast mutton; of clods of bread-and-butter, dog’s-eared lesson-books, cracked slates, tear-blotted copy-books, canings, rulerings, hair-cuttings, rainy Sundays, suet-puddings, and a dirty atmosphere of ink, surrounding all.", "학기의 나머지는 내 기억 속에 뒤죽박죽 엉켜 있다. 날마다 이어지던 우리 삶의 다툼과 몸부림이며, 기울어 가던 여름과 바뀌어 가던 계절이며, 종소리에 침대에서 끌려 나오던 서리 내린 아침들과, 종소리에 다시 침대로 떠밀려 들어가던 캄캄한 밤의 그 차디차고 차디찬 냄새며, 침침하게 불 밝히고 미적지근하게 데워지던 저녁 교실과, 그저 거대한 오들오들 떨림 기계나 다름없던 아침 교실이며, 삶은 소고기와 구운 소고기, 삶은 양고기와 구운 양고기가 번갈아 나오던 것이며, 버터 발린 빵 덩이며, 귀퉁이가 개 귀처럼 접힌 교과서며, 금 간 석판이며, 눈물 자국 얼룩진 습자 공책이며, 회초리질이며, 자질이며, 머리 깎이던 일이며, 비 오던 일요일들이며, 쇠기름 푸딩이며, 그리고 이 모든 것을 휘감던 잉크 냄새 자욱한 지저분한 공기가 한데 엉켜 있다."]], [["I well remember though, how the distant idea of the holidays, after seeming for an immense time to be a stationary speck, began to come towards us, and to grow and grow.", "그래도 또렷이 기억난다. 멀찍이 있던 방학이라는 관념이, 어마어마하게 오랫동안 꼼짝 않는 점 하나로만 보이다가, 마침내 우리 쪽으로 다가오기 시작해, 점점 더 커지고 또 커지던 것을."], ["How from counting months, we came to weeks, and then to days; and how I then began to be afraid that I should not be sent for and when I learnt from Steerforth that I had been sent for, and was certainly to go home, had dim forebodings that I might break my leg first.", "어떻게 우리가 달을 세다가 주를 세고, 다시 날을 세게 되었는지를. 그러다 어떻게 나는 행여 데리러 오는 사람이 없을까 봐 겁이 나기 시작했고, 스티어포스에게서 나를 데리러 사람이 왔으며 분명히 집에 가게 된다는 말을 들었을 때는, 그 전에 다리라도 부러지면 어쩌나 하는 어슴푸레한 불길한 예감에 사로잡혔는지를."], ["How the breaking-up day changed its place fast, at last, from the week after next to next week, this week, the day after tomorrow, tomorrow, today, tonight--when I was inside the Yarmouth mail, and going home.", "어떻게 종업식 날이 마침내 빠르게 자리를 옮겨, 다다음 주에서 다음 주로, 이번 주로, 모레로, 내일로, 오늘로, 오늘 밤으로 바짝 다가왔는지를 — 그리하여 나는 야머스행 우편 마차 안에 올라, 집으로 가고 있었다."]], [["I had many a broken sleep inside the Yarmouth mail, and many an incoherent dream of all these things.", "나는 야머스행 우편 마차 안에서 잠을 여러 번 설쳤고, 이 모든 일에 관한 두서없는 꿈을 숱하게 꾸었다."], ["But when I awoke at intervals, the ground outside the window was not the playground of Salem House, and the sound in my ears was not the sound of Mr. Creakle giving it to Traddles, but the sound of the coachman touching up the horses.", "하지만 이따금 깨어 보면, 창밖의 땅은 세일럼 하우스의 운동장이 아니었고, 귓가에 울리는 소리도 크리클 선생님이 트래들스를 후려치는 소리가 아니라, 마부가 말들을 채찍질해 다그치는 소리였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 8. MY HOLIDAYS. ESPECIALLY ONE HAPPY AFTERNOON", "# CHAPTER 8. MY HOLIDAYS. ESPECIALLY ONE HAPPY AFTERNOON"]], [["When we arrived before day at the inn where the mail stopped, which was not the inn where my friend the waiter lived, I was shown up to a nice little bedroom, with DOLPHIN painted on the door.", "우편 마차가 멈추는 여관에 동이 트기 전에 도착했는데, 그 여관은 내 친구인 종업원이 일하던 곳은 아니었다. 나는 문에 '돌고래'라고 칠해진 아담하고 좋은 침실로 안내받았다."], ["Very cold I was, I know, notwithstanding the hot tea they had given me before a large fire downstairs; and very glad I was to turn into the Dolphin’s bed, pull the Dolphin’s blankets round my head, and go to sleep.", "아래층에서 큰 난롯불 앞에 앉아 뜨거운 차를 마셨는데도 몸이 어찌나 차가웠던지 지금도 기억이 난다. 그래서 나는 돌고래의 침대로 기어들어 돌고래의 담요를 머리까지 끌어당기고 잠드는 게 더없이 반가웠다."]], [["Mr. Barkis the carrier was to call for me in the morning at nine o’clock.", "짐마차꾼 바키스 씨가 아침 아홉 시에 나를 데리러 오기로 되어 있었다."], ["I got up at eight, a little giddy from the shortness of my night’s rest, and was ready for him before the appointed time.", "나는 여덟 시에 일어났는데, 밤잠이 짧았던 탓에 머리가 좀 어질어질했고, 약속 시간이 되기도 전에 채비를 마쳤다."], ["He received me exactly as if not five minutes had elapsed since we were last together, and I had only been into the hotel to get change for sixpence, or something of that sort.", "바키스 씨는 우리가 마지막으로 함께한 뒤로 오 분도 지나지 않은 듯이, 그러니까 내가 그저 육 펜스짜리를 잔돈으로 바꾸려고 여관에 잠깐 들렀다 나온 것쯤으로 여기는 듯이 나를 맞았다."]], [["As soon as I and my box were in the cart, and the carrier seated, the lazy horse walked away with us all at his accustomed pace.", "나와 내 상자가 마차에 실리고 짐마차꾼도 자리에 앉자마자, 게으른 말은 늘 그렇듯이 익숙한 걸음으로 우리 모두를 끌고 천천히 걸어 나갔다."]], [["‘You look very well, Mr. Barkis,’ I said, thinking he would like to know it.", "\"안색이 아주 좋아 보이세요, 바키스 씨.\" 나는 그가 그 말을 알면 좋아하겠거니 싶어 말을 건넸다."]], [["Mr. Barkis rubbed his cheek with his cuff, and then looked at his cuff as if he expected to find some of the bloom upon it; but made no other acknowledgement of the compliment.", "바키스 씨는 소맷부리로 뺨을 문지르고는, 거기에 혈색이 좀 묻어났기를 바라기라도 한 것처럼 소맷부리를 들여다보았다. 그러나 그 칭찬에 대해 그 밖에 다른 반응은 보이지 않았다."]], [["‘I gave your message, Mr.", "\"바키스 씨 말씀 전했어요.\""], ["Barkis,’ I said: ‘I wrote to Peggotty.’", "내가 말했다. \"패거티에게 편지를 썼거든요.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"아!\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Barkis seemed gruff, and answered drily.", "바키스 씨는 무뚝뚝해 보였고, 대답도 메마르게 했다."]], [["‘Wasn’t it right, Mr. Barkis?’ I asked, after a little hesitation.", "\"제대로 안 된 거였나요, 바키스 씨?\" 나는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, no,’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"뭐, 그게 그렇다네.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Not the message?’", "\"전갈이 잘못됐어요?\""]], [["‘The message was right enough, perhaps,’ said Mr.", "\"전갈은 그런대로 괜찮았을 거야.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."], ["Barkis; ‘but it come to an end there.’", "\"그런데 거기서 그냥 끝나 버렸지 뭐냐.\""]], [["Not understanding what he meant, I repeated inquisitively: ‘Came to an end, Mr. Barkis?’", "나는 그 말뜻을 알아듣지 못해서, 궁금한 마음에 되물었다. \"끝나 버렸다고요, 바키스 씨?\""]], [["‘Nothing come of it,’ he explained, looking at me sideways.", "\"아무 일도 안 일어났단 말이야.\" 그가 나를 곁눈질하며 설명했다."], ["‘No answer.’", "\"답이 없었어.\""]], [["‘There was an answer expected, was there, Mr.", "\"답을 기다리셨던 거예요, 바키스 씨?\""], ["Barkis?’ said I, opening my eyes.", "내가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 말했다."], ["For this was a new light to me.", "이건 나에게 새로운 사실이었으니까."]], [["‘When a man says he’s willin’,’ said Mr.", "\"사내가 그럴 마음이 있다고 말할 때는,\""], ["Barkis, turning his glance slowly on me again, ‘it’s as much as to say, that man’s a-waitin’ for a answer.’", "바키스 씨가 다시 천천히 나에게 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"그건 그 사내가 답을 기다린다는 뜻이나 다름없는 거야.\""]], [["‘Well, Mr. Barkis?’", "\"그래서요, 바키스 씨?\""]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서,\""], ["Barkis, carrying his eyes back to his horse’s ears; ‘that man’s been a-waitin’ for a answer ever since.’", "바키스 씨가 눈길을 다시 말의 두 귀로 돌리며 말했다. \"그 사내는 그때부터 줄곧 답을 기다리고 있었던 거지.\""]], [["‘Have you told her so, Mr. Barkis?’", "\"그 사람한테 그렇게 말해 주셨어요, 바키스 씨?\""]], [["‘No--no,’ growled Mr.", "\"아니 — 아니야.\""], ["Barkis, reflecting about it. ‘I ain’t got no call to go and tell her so.", "바키스 씨가 그 일을 곰곰이 되짚으며 으르렁거리듯 말했다. \"내가 가서 그렇게 말할 까닭이 없잖아."], ["I never said six words to her myself, I ain’t a-goin’ to tell her so.’", "내가 그 여자한테 직접 여섯 마디도 안 해 봤는데, 그렇게 말할 수야 없지.\""]], [["‘Would you like me to do it, Mr.", "\"제가 대신 해 드릴까요, 바키스 씨?\""], ["Barkis?’ said I, doubtfully.", "나는 미덥잖다는 듯이 말했다."], ["‘You might tell her, if you would,’ said Mr.", "\"네가 해 줄 수 있다면,\""], ["Barkis, with another slow look at me, ‘that Barkis was a-waitin’ for a answer.", "바키스 씨가 다시 한번 나를 천천히 바라보며 말했다. \"바키스가 답을 기다린다고 말해 주렴."], ["Says you--what name is it?’", "너 — 그 여자 이름이 뭐랬지?\""]], [["‘Her name?’", "\"그 사람 이름요?\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr. Barkis, with a nod of his head.", "\"아!\" 바키스 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["‘Peggotty.’", "\"패거티예요.\""]], [["‘Chrisen name?", "\"세례명이냐?"], ["Or nat’ral name?’ said Mr. Barkis.", "아니면 그냥 부르는 이름이냐?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, it’s not her Christian name.", "\"아, 그건 세례명이 아니에요."], ["Her Christian name is Clara.’", "세례명은 클라라예요.\""]], [["‘Is it though?’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"그래?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["He seemed to find an immense fund of reflection in this circumstance, and sat pondering and inwardly whistling for some time.", "그는 이 사실에서 무궁무진한 생각거리를 발견한 듯, 한동안 가만히 골똘히 앉아 속으로 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["‘Well!’ he resumed at length.", "\"좋아!\" 마침내 그가 다시 입을 열었다."], ["‘Says you, “Peggotty!", "\"이렇게 말하는 거야. '패거티!"], ["Barkis is waitin’ for a answer.”", "바키스가 답을 기다려요.'"], ["Says she, perhaps, “Answer to what?”", "그럼 그 여자가, 아마, '무슨 답요?' 하고 묻겠지."], ["Says you, “To what I told you.”", "그럼 네가, '내가 전한 그 말에 대한 답요.' 하는 거야."], ["“What is that?” says she.", "'그게 뭔데요?' 하고 그 여자가 묻거든."], ["“Barkis is willin’,” says you.’", "'바키스는 그럴 마음이 있어요.' 하고 네가 말하는 거지.\""]], [["This extremely artful suggestion Mr. Barkis accompanied with a nudge of his elbow that gave me quite a stitch in my side.", "이 지극히 교묘한 제안을 하면서 바키스 씨는 팔꿈치로 나를 쿡 찔렀는데, 어찌나 세게 찔렀던지 옆구리가 다 결릴 지경이었다."], ["After that, he slouched over his horse in his usual manner; and made no other reference to the subject except, half an hour afterwards, taking a piece of chalk from his pocket, and writing up, inside the tilt of the cart, ‘Clara Peggotty’--apparently as a private memorandum.", "그러고는 평소처럼 말 위로 구부정하게 몸을 숙였다. 그러고는 그 일에 대해 더는 말을 꺼내지 않다가, 삼십 분쯤 지난 뒤에 주머니에서 분필 한 조각을 꺼내, 마차 포장 안쪽에다 '클라라 패거티'라고 적어 넣었다 — 아무래도 자기만의 비망록인 듯했다."]], [["Ah, what a strange feeling it was to be going home when it was not home, and to find that every object I looked at, reminded me of the happy old home, which was like a dream I could never dream again!", "아아, 집이 아닌 곳으로 집에 가는 기분이라니, 그 얼마나 묘한 느낌이었던가. 게다가 눈에 들어오는 것마다 행복했던 옛집을 떠올리게 하는데, 그 옛집은 두 번 다시 꿀 수 없는 꿈만 같았다!"], ["The days when my mother and I and Peggotty were all in all to one another, and there was no one to come between us, rose up before me so sorrowfully on the road, that I am not sure I was glad to be there--not sure but that I would rather have remained away, and forgotten it in Steerforth’s company.", "어머니와 나와 패거티가 서로에게 전부였고, 우리 사이에 끼어들 사람이 아무도 없던 그 시절이 길 위에서 어찌나 서글프게 떠오르던지, 나는 거기 가는 게 정말 기뻤는지조차 확신할 수 없었다 — 차라리 멀리 떨어진 채로 스티어포스와 어울리며 그 일을 잊고 지내는 편이 낫지 않았을까 싶기도 했다. 하지만 나는 거기 있었다."], ["But there I was; and soon I was at our house, where the bare old elm-trees wrung their many hands in the bleak wintry air, and shreds of the old rooks’-nests drifted away upon the wind.", "그리고 곧 우리 집에 다다랐는데, 헐벗은 늙은 느릅나무들이 황량한 겨울 공기 속에서 수많은 손을 비틀어 짜고 있었고, 낡은 까마귀 둥지의 부스러기들이 바람에 흩날려 떠가고 있었다."]], [["The carrier put my box down at the garden-gate, and left me.", "짐마차꾼은 정원 문 앞에 내 상자를 내려놓고 떠났다."], ["I walked along the path towards the house, glancing at the windows, and fearing at every step to see Mr. Murdstone or Miss Murdstone lowering out of one of them.", "나는 창문들을 흘끔거리며 집을 향해 난 길을 걸었는데, 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 머드스톤 씨나 머드스톤 양이 그 창문 어딘가에서 잔뜩 인상을 쓰고 내다보지나 않을까 겁이 났다."], ["No face appeared, however; and being come to the house, and knowing how to open the door, before dark, without knocking, I went in with a quiet, timid step.", "그러나 아무 얼굴도 나타나지 않았다. 집에 이르렀고, 날이 어두워지기 전이라 노크하지 않고도 문 여는 법을 알았기에, 나는 조용하고 머뭇거리는 걸음으로 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["God knows how infantine the memory may have been, that was awakened within me by the sound of my mother’s voice in the old parlour, when I set foot in the hall. She was singing in a low tone.", "복도에 발을 들여놓았을 때, 옛 거실에서 들려온 어머니의 목소리가 내 안에서 일깨운 그 기억이 얼마나 어린 시절의 것이었는지는 하느님만 아실 것이다. 어머니는 나직한 목소리로 노래를 부르고 있었다."], ["I think I must have lain in her arms, and heard her singing so to me when I was but a baby.", "내가 갓난아기였을 적에 분명 어머니 품에 안겨 그렇게 나에게 불러 주는 노래를 들었던 게 틀림없다."], ["The strain was new to me, and yet it was so old that it filled my heart brim-full; like a friend come back from a long absence.", "그 가락은 나에게 낯설었으나, 또 한편으로는 너무도 오래된 것이어서 내 가슴을 가득 채워 넘치게 했다. 마치 오랜만에 돌아온 벗처럼."]], [["I believed, from the solitary and thoughtful way in which my mother murmured her song, that she was alone.", "어머니가 외롭고 생각에 잠긴 듯한 투로 흥얼거리는 것으로 보아, 나는 어머니가 혼자라고 믿었다."], ["And I went softly into the room.", "그래서 나는 살그머니 방으로 들어갔다."], ["She was sitting by the fire, suckling an infant, whose tiny hand she held against her neck.", "어머니는 난롯가에 앉아 갓난아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었는데, 그 작은 손을 자기 목에 갖다 대고 있었다."], ["Her eyes were looking down upon its face, and she sat singing to it.", "어머니의 눈은 아기의 얼굴을 내려다보고 있었고, 어머니는 그 아이에게 노래를 불러 주고 있었다."], ["I was so far right, that she had no other companion.", "어머니에게 다른 동무가 없다는 점에서, 내 짐작은 들어맞은 셈이었다."]], [["I spoke to her, and she started, and cried out.", "내가 말을 건네자 어머니는 깜짝 놀라 외마디 소리를 질렀다."], ["But seeing me, she called me her dear Davy, her own boy!", "그러나 나를 보고는, 사랑스러운 데이비, 내 아들이라고 부르며!"], ["and coming half across the room to meet me, kneeled down upon the ground and kissed me, and laid my head down on her bosom near the little creature that was nestling there, and put its hand to my lips.", "나를 맞으러 방을 가로질러 절반쯤 다가오더니, 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 나에게 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 거기 파고들어 있던 작은 생명체 가까이 내 머리를 자기 가슴에 기대게 하고, 그 아기의 손을 내 입술에 갖다 댔다."]], [["I wish I had died. I wish I had died then, with that feeling in my heart!", "차라리 그때 죽었더라면 좋았을 것을. 그 느낌을 가슴에 품은 채 그때 죽었더라면!"], ["I should have been more fit for Heaven than I ever have been since.", "그랬다면 나는 그 뒤로 살아온 어느 때보다도 천국에 더 어울리는 사람이었을 텐데."]], [["‘He is your brother,’ said my mother, fondling me.", "\"이 애가 네 동생이란다.\" 어머니가 나를 어루만지며 말했다."], ["‘Davy, my pretty boy!", "\"데이비, 내 예쁜 아들!"], ["My poor child!’ Then she kissed me more and more, and clasped me round the neck.", "가엾은 내 아가!\" 그러고는 자꾸자꾸 입을 맞추며 내 목을 끌어안았다."], ["This she was doing when Peggotty came running in, and bounced down on the ground beside us, and went mad about us both for a quarter of an hour.", "어머니가 그러고 있을 때 패거티가 달려 들어와, 우리 곁 바닥에 털썩 주저앉더니, 십오 분 동안이나 우리 둘을 두고 정신없이 좋아했다."]], [["It seemed that I had not been expected so soon, the carrier being much before his usual time.", "내가 이렇게 일찍 올 줄은 아무도 몰랐던 모양인데, 짐마차꾼이 평소보다 훨씬 일찍 도착한 탓이었다."], ["It seemed, too, that Mr. and Miss Murdstone had gone out upon a visit in the neighbourhood, and would not return before night.", "또한 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양은 이웃에 다니러 나가서 밤이 되기 전에는 돌아오지 않을 거라고 했다. 나는 이런 행운은 꿈에도 바라지 않았다."], ["I had never hoped for this. I had never thought it possible that we three could be together undisturbed, once more; and I felt, for the time, as if the old days were come back.", "우리 셋이 다시 한번 아무 방해 없이 함께 있을 수 있으리라고는 생각조차 못 했었다. 그래서 나는 그 순간만큼은 옛 시절이 되돌아온 것만 같았다."]], [["We dined together by the fireside.", "우리는 난롯가에서 함께 저녁을 먹었다."], ["Peggotty was in attendance to wait upon us, but my mother wouldn’t let her do it, and made her dine with us.", "패거티가 우리 시중을 들려고 곁에 섰지만, 어머니는 그렇게 하도록 두지 않고 함께 먹게 했다."], ["I had my own old plate, with a brown view of a man-of-war in full sail upon it, which Peggotty had hoarded somewhere all the time I had been away, and would not have had broken, she said, for a hundred pounds.", "내 앞에는 내 옛 접시가 놓였는데, 돛을 활짝 편 군함이 갈색으로 그려진 그 접시는, 내가 떠나 있던 내내 패거티가 어딘가에 고이 간직해 둔 것이었다. 백 파운드를 준대도 그걸 깨뜨리지는 않았을 거라고 패거티는 말했다."], ["I had my own old mug with David on it, and my own old little knife and fork that wouldn’t cut.", "내 앞에는 데이비드라고 적힌 내 옛 머그잔과, 잘 잘리지도 않는 내 옛 작은 나이프와 포크가 놓여 있었다."]], [["While we were at table, I thought it a favourable occasion to tell Peggotty about Mr. Barkis, who, before I had finished what I had to tell her, began to laugh, and throw her apron over her face.", "식탁에 앉아 있는 동안, 나는 패거티에게 바키스 씨 이야기를 들려주기 딱 좋은 때라고 생각했는데, 내 이야기가 채 끝나기도 전에 패거티는 웃음을 터뜨리며 앞치마를 끌어다 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["‘Peggotty,’ said my mother.", "\"패거티.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘What’s the matter?’", "\"무슨 일이야?\""]], [["Peggotty only laughed the more, and held her apron tight over her face when my mother tried to pull it away, and sat as if her head were in a bag.", "패거티는 더욱 웃기만 할 뿐이었고, 어머니가 앞치마를 잡아당기려 하자 더욱 꼭 끌어다 얼굴을 가린 채, 머리에 자루라도 뒤집어쓴 사람처럼 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘What are you doing, you stupid creature?’ said my mother, laughing.", "\"무슨 짓이야, 이 어리석은 사람아?\" 어머니가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, drat the man!’ cried Peggotty.", "\"아유, 그놈의 남자!\" 패거티가 소리쳤다."], ["‘He wants to marry me.’", "\"저더러 결혼하자잖아요.\""]], [["‘It would be a very good match for you; wouldn’t it?’ said my mother.", "\"너한테는 아주 좋은 짝이지 않니?\" 어머니가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh! I don’t know,’ said Peggotty.", "\"아이고! 모르겠어요.\""], ["‘Don’t ask me.", "패거티가 말했다."], ["I wouldn’t have him if he was made of gold.", "\"묻지 마세요. 그 사람이 금으로 만들어졌대도 저는 싫어요."], ["Nor I wouldn’t have anybody.’", "아니, 누구라도 싫다고요.\""]], [["‘Then, why don’t you tell him so, you ridiculous thing?’ said my mother.", "\"그럼 왜 그렇게 말해 주지 않는 게냐, 이 우스운 사람아?\" 어머니가 말했다."]], [["‘Tell him so,’ retorted Peggotty, looking out of her apron.", "\"그렇게 말해 주라고요.\" 패거티가 앞치마 밖으로 내다보며 받아쳤다."], ["‘He has never said a word to me about it.", "\"그 사람은 저한테 그런 얘기를 한마디도 한 적이 없어요."], ["He knows better. If he was to make so bold as say a word to me, I should slap his face.’", "그럴 줄 모르는 사람도 아니고요. 만약 뻔뻔스럽게 저한테 한마디라도 했다간, 제가 따귀를 올려붙였을 거예요.\""]], [["Her own was as red as ever I saw it, or any other face, I think; but she only covered it again, for a few moments at a time, when she was taken with a violent fit of laughter; and after two or three of those attacks, went on with her dinner.", "그러는 패거티의 얼굴은 내가 본 어떤 얼굴보다도, 아니 그 누구의 얼굴보다도 시뻘게져 있었다. 하지만 패거티는 격렬한 웃음 발작이 도질 때마다 잠깐씩만 다시 얼굴을 가렸고, 그런 발작을 두세 번 더 겪고 나서는 다시 저녁을 먹었다."]], [["I remarked that my mother, though she smiled when Peggotty looked at her, became more serious and thoughtful.", "나는 어머니가 패거티를 볼 때면 미소를 짓다가도, 점점 더 진지하고 생각에 잠긴 표정이 되는 걸 알아챘다."], ["I had seen at first that she was changed.", "어머니가 변했다는 건 처음부터 느꼈었다."], ["Her face was very pretty still, but it looked careworn, and too delicate; and her hand was so thin and white that it seemed to me to be almost transparent.", "어머니의 얼굴은 여전히 무척 예뻤지만 근심에 찌든 듯 보였고, 너무 가냘파 보였다. 어머니의 손은 어찌나 가늘고 하얀지, 나에게는 거의 투명해 보일 지경이었다."], ["But the change to which I now refer was superadded to this: it was in her manner, which became anxious and fluttered.", "그러나 지금 내가 말하는 변화는 이런 것 위에 더해진 것이었다. 그것은 어머니의 태도에 있었는데, 불안하고 안절부절못하게 변해 있었다."], ["At last she said, putting out her hand, and laying it affectionately on the hand of her old servant,", "마침내 어머니는 손을 뻗어, 오랜 하녀의 손 위에 다정하게 자기 손을 포개며 말했다."]], [["‘Peggotty, dear, you are not going to be married?’", "\"패거티, 얘야, 너 설마 결혼하려는 건 아니지?\""]], [["‘Me, ma’am?’ returned Peggotty, staring.", "\"제가요, 마님?\" 패거티가 빤히 쳐다보며 되물었다."], ["‘Lord bless you, no!’", "\"에구머니나, 아니에요!\""]], [["‘Not just yet?’ said my mother, tenderly.", "\"아직은 아닌 거지?\" 어머니가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["‘Never!’ cried Peggotty.", "\"절대로요!\" 패거티가 외쳤다."]], [["My mother took her hand, and said:", "어머니는 패거티의 손을 잡고 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t leave me, Peggotty.", "\"날 떠나지 마, 패거티."], ["Stay with me. It will not be for long, perhaps.", "나하고 함께 있어 줘. 아마 그리 오래는 아닐 거야."], ["What should I ever do without you!’", "너 없이 내가 어떻게 살겠니!\""]], [["‘Me leave you, my precious!’ cried Peggotty.", "\"제가 마님을 떠나다니요, 소중한 마님!\" 패거티가 외쳤다."], ["‘Not for all the world and his wife.", "\"이 세상을 다 준대도, 그 마누라까지 다 준대도 그럴 일 없어요."], ["Why, what’s put that in your silly little head?’--For Peggotty had been used of old to talk to my mother sometimes like a child.", "아니, 그런 어리석은 생각이 그 작은 머릿속에 어쩌다 들어왔대요?\" — 패거티는 예전부터 가끔 어머니를 어린애 대하듯 말하곤 했으니까."]], [["But my mother made no answer, except to thank her, and Peggotty went running on in her own fashion.", "그러나 어머니는 고맙다는 말 외에는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 패거티는 제 식대로 말을 줄줄 이어 갔다."]], [["‘Me leave you?", "\"제가 마님을 떠난다고요?"], ["I think I see myself.", "어디 그런 꼴 좀 봐야겠네요."], ["Peggotty go away from you?", "패거티가 마님 곁을 떠난다고요?"], ["I should like to catch her at it!", "그런 짓 하는 꼴 한번 잡아 보고 싶네요!"], ["No, no, no,’ said Peggotty, shaking her head, and folding her arms; ‘not she, my dear.", "아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.\" 패거티가 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 팔짱을 끼며 말했다."], ["It isn’t that there ain’t some Cats that would be well enough pleased if she did, but they sha’n’t be pleased.", "\"그런 일은 없어요, 마님. 그렇게 되면 좋아라 할 고양이 같은 것들이 없는 건 아니지만, 그것들 좋은 일은 안 시켜 줄 거예요."], ["They shall be aggravated.", "외려 약이 바짝 오르게 해 줄 거예요."], ["I’ll stay with you till I am a cross cranky old woman.", "저는 심술궂고 까탈스러운 늙은이가 될 때까지 마님 곁에 있을 거예요."], ["And when I’m too deaf, and too lame, and too blind, and too mumbly for want of teeth, to be of any use at all, even to be found fault with, than I shall go to my Davy, and ask him to take me in.’", "그러다 너무 귀가 멀고, 너무 다리를 절고, 너무 눈이 안 보이고, 이가 다 빠져 너무 우물거려서 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없어지면, 그러니까 잔소리 들을 거리조차 못 되면, 그땐 우리 데이비한테 가서 좀 거둬 달라고 할 거예요.\""]], [["‘And, Peggotty,’ says I, ‘I shall be glad to see you, and I’ll make you as welcome as a queen.’", "\"그럼요, 패거티.\" 내가 말했다. \"그땐 내가 정말 반갑게 맞을게. 여왕님처럼 떠받들어 줄게.\""]], [["‘Bless your dear heart!’ cried Peggotty.", "\"고마운 우리 도련님!\" 패거티가 외쳤다."], ["‘I know you will!’ And she kissed me beforehand, in grateful acknowledgement of my hospitality.", "\"그러실 줄 알았어요!\" 그러고는 내 환대에 고마운 마음을 표하느라 미리 나에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["After that, she covered her head up with her apron again and had another laugh about Mr. Barkis.", "그런 다음 다시 앞치마로 머리를 가리고 바키스 씨 이야기에 또 한바탕 웃었다."], ["After that, she took the baby out of its little cradle, and nursed it.", "그런 다음 작은 요람에서 아기를 꺼내 어르며 돌보았다."], ["After that, she cleared the dinner table; after that, came in with another cap on, and her work-box, and the yard-measure, and the bit of wax-candle, all just the same as ever.", "그런 다음 저녁 식탁을 치웠고, 그런 다음 다른 모자를 쓰고, 반짇고리와 자, 밀랍 양초 토막을 들고 들어왔다 — 죄다 예전과 조금도 다름이 없었다."]], [["We sat round the fire, and talked delightfully.", "우리는 난롯가에 둘러앉아 즐겁게 이야기를 나누었다."], ["I told them what a hard master Mr. Creakle was, and they pitied me very much.", "나는 크리클 선생이 얼마나 모진 선생인지를 들려주었고, 둘은 나를 몹시 가엾게 여겼다."], ["I told them what a fine fellow Steerforth was, and what a patron of mine, and Peggotty said she would walk a score of miles to see him.", "나는 스티어포스가 얼마나 훌륭한 친구이며 나를 얼마나 잘 봐주는지를 들려주었고, 패거티는 그를 한번 보려면 이십 마일이라도 걷겠다고 했다."], ["I took the little baby in my arms when it was awake, and nursed it lovingly.", "나는 아기가 깨어 있을 때 두 팔로 안아 다정하게 어르고 돌봤다."], ["When it was asleep again, I crept close to my mother’s side according to my old custom, broken now a long time, and sat with my arms embracing her waist, and my little red cheek on her shoulder, and once more felt her beautiful hair drooping over me--like an angel’s wing as I used to think, I recollect--and was very happy indeed.", "아기가 다시 잠들자, 나는 오랫동안 끊겼던 옛 버릇대로 어머니 곁에 바짝 다가가, 두 팔로 어머니 허리를 감싸고, 작은 붉은 뺨을 어머니 어깨에 댄 채 앉았다. 그러자 또 한 번 어머니의 아름다운 머리카락이 내 위로 드리워지는 게 느껴졌다 — 예전에 내가 늘 천사의 날개 같다고 생각했던 그 머리카락이, 지금도 그렇게 기억난다 — 나는 정말이지 더없이 행복했다."]], [["While I sat thus, looking at the fire, and seeing pictures in the red-hot coals, I almost believed that I had never been away; that Mr.", "그렇게 앉아 난롯불을 바라보며 새빨갛게 단 숯덩이 속에서 그림들을 보고 있노라니, 나는 내가 한 번도 떠난 적이 없었던 것만 같았다."], ["and Miss Murdstone were such pictures, and would vanish when the fire got low; and that there was nothing real in all that I remembered, save my mother, Peggotty, and I.", "머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양도 그저 그런 그림에 지나지 않아서 불이 사그라지면 사라져 버릴 것 같았고, 내가 기억하는 그 모든 것 가운데 어머니와 패거티와 나 말고는 진짜인 게 하나도 없는 것만 같았다."]], [["Peggotty darned away at a stocking as long as she could see, and then sat with it drawn on her left hand like a glove, and her needle in her right, ready to take another stitch whenever there was a blaze.", "패거티는 눈이 보이는 한 줄곧 양말을 깁다가, 더는 보이지 않게 되자 그것을 장갑처럼 왼손에 끼우고, 오른손에는 바늘을 쥔 채, 불꽃이 일렁일 때마다 한 땀씩 더 뜨려고 채비하고 앉아 있었다."], ["I cannot conceive whose stockings they can have been that Peggotty was always darning, or where such an unfailing supply of stockings in want of darning can have come from.", "패거티가 늘 깁고 있던 그 양말이 도대체 누구의 것이었는지, 또 그렇게 깁기를 기다리는 양말이 어디서 그토록 끊이지 않고 공급되었는지 나는 도무지 알 수가 없다."], ["From my earliest infancy she seems to have been always employed in that class of needlework, and never by any chance in any other.", "내가 갓난아기였을 적부터 패거티는 늘 그런 종류의 바느질에만 매달려 있었고, 어쩌다가라도 다른 바느질을 하는 법이 없었던 것 같다."]], [["‘I wonder,’ said Peggotty, who was sometimes seized with a fit of wondering on some most unexpected topic, ‘what’s become of Davy’s great-aunt?’ ‘Lor, Peggotty!’ observed my mother, rousing herself from a reverie, ‘what nonsense you talk!’", "\"궁금하네.\" 패거티가 말했는데, 그녀는 가끔 가장 뜻밖의 화제를 두고 궁금증 발작에 사로잡히곤 했다. \"데이비 도련님의 대고모님은 어떻게 되셨을까?\" \"원, 패거티!\" 어머니가 깊은 생각에서 깨어나며 말했다. \"무슨 그런 쓸데없는 소릴 하니!\""]], [["‘Well, but I really do wonder, ma’am,’ said Peggotty.", "\"하지만 마님, 정말 궁금한걸요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘What can have put such a person in your head?’ inquired my mother.", "\"어쩌다 그런 사람이 네 머릿속에 떠올랐대?\" 어머니가 물었다."], ["‘Is there nobody else in the world to come there?’", "\"이 세상에 거기 떠올릴 사람이 그렇게 없니?\""]], [["‘I don’t know how it is,’ said Peggotty, ‘unless it’s on account of being stupid, but my head never can pick and choose its people.", "\"어떻게 된 노릇인지 저도 모르겠어요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"제가 멍청해서 그런가, 제 머리는 사람을 골라 떠올릴 줄을 몰라요."], ["They come and they go, and they don’t come and they don’t go, just as they like.", "멋대로 왔다가 가고, 멋대로 안 왔다가 안 가고 그러는걸요."], ["I wonder what’s become of her?’", "그분은 어떻게 되셨을까요?\""]], [["‘How absurd you are, Peggotty!’ returned my mother.", "\"넌 어쩜 그렇게 엉뚱하니, 패거티!\" 어머니가 받아쳤다."], ["‘One would suppose you wanted a second visit from her.’", "\"꼭 그분이 한 번 더 찾아오기라도 바라는 것 같구나.\""]], [["‘Lord forbid!’ cried Peggotty.", "\"아이고, 그런 일은 없어야지요!\" 패거티가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Well then, don’t talk about such uncomfortable things, there’s a good soul,’ said my mother.", "\"그럼 그런 거북한 얘긴 하지 마, 착한 사람아.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘Miss Betsey is shut up in her cottage by the sea, no doubt, and will remain there.", "\"베시 양은 분명 바닷가 오두막에 틀어박혀 계실 테고, 거기 그대로 계실 거야."], ["At all events, she is not likely ever to trouble us again.’", "어쨌든 다시는 우리를 성가시게 할 일은 없을 거다.\""]], [["‘No!’ mused Peggotty.", "\"그렇겠죠!\""], ["‘No, that ain’t likely at all.---I wonder, if she was to die, whether she’d leave Davy anything?’", "패거티가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"그래요, 그럴 리야 없겠죠. — 그런데 만약 그분이 돌아가시면, 데이비 도련님한테 뭐라도 남겨 주실까요?\""]], [["‘Good gracious me, Peggotty,’ returned my mother, ‘what a nonsensical woman you are!", "\"맙소사, 패거티.\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"넌 어쩜 그렇게 말도 안 되는 소릴 하니!"], ["when you know that she took offence at the poor dear boy’s ever being born at all.’", "그분이 이 가엾은 아이가 세상에 태어났다는 사실 자체에 노여워하셨다는 걸 뻔히 알면서.\""]], [["‘I suppose she wouldn’t be inclined to forgive him now,’ hinted Peggotty.", "\"이제는 도련님을 용서하실 마음이 없으시겠죠.\" 패거티가 넌지시 비쳤다."]], [["‘Why should she be inclined to forgive him now?’ said my mother, rather sharply.", "\"이제 와서 왜 이 애를 용서하실 마음이 생기겠니?\" 어머니가 다소 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["‘Now that he’s got a brother, I mean,’ said Peggotty.", "\"이제 동생이 생겼으니 하는 말이에요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["My mother immediately began to cry, and wondered how Peggotty dared to say such a thing.", "어머니는 곧장 울음을 터뜨리더니, 패거티가 어떻게 감히 그런 말을 할 수 있느냐고 따졌다."]], [["‘As if this poor little innocent in its cradle had ever done any harm to you or anybody else, you jealous thing!’ said she.", "\"요람 속의 이 가엾고 죄 없는 것이 너한테든 누구한테든 무슨 해라도 끼쳤다는 거니, 이 샘 많은 사람아!\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘You had much better go and marry Mr. Barkis, the carrier.", "\"차라리 짐마차꾼 바키스 씨한테 시집이나 가는 게 훨씬 낫겠다."], ["Why don’t you?’", "왜 안 가는 거야?\""]], [["‘I should make Miss Murdstone happy, if I was to,’ said Peggotty.", "\"제가 그러면 머드스톤 양이 좋아하겠죠.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘What a bad disposition you have, Peggotty!’ returned my mother.", "\"넌 어쩜 그렇게 심보가 고약하니, 패거티!\" 어머니가 받아쳤다."], ["‘You are as jealous of Miss Murdstone as it is possible for a ridiculous creature to be.", "\"넌 머드스톤 양을 우스운 사람이 시샘할 수 있는 한 최대로 시샘하고 있어."], ["You want to keep the keys yourself, and give out all the things, I suppose?", "네가 직접 열쇠를 쥐고, 모든 걸 네가 내주고 싶은 거지? 안 그래?"], ["I shouldn’t be surprised if you did.", "정말 그래도 난 놀라지 않을 거야."], ["When you know that she only does it out of kindness and the best intentions!", "그분이 다 친절한 마음에서, 더없이 좋은 뜻으로 그러시는 걸 뻔히 알면서!"], ["You know she does, Peggotty--you know it well.’", "너도 알잖아, 패거티 — 잘 알면서.\""]], [["Peggotty muttered something to the effect of ‘Bother the best intentions!’ and something else to the effect that there was a little too much of the best intentions going on.", "패거티는 \"좋은 뜻은 무슨 놈의 좋은 뜻!\" 비슷한 말을 중얼거렸고, 그 좋은 뜻이라는 게 좀 지나치게 넘쳐 난다는 식의 말도 덧붙였다."]], [["‘I know what you mean, you cross thing,’ said my mother.", "\"네 속을 다 알아, 이 심술궂은 사람아.\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["‘I understand you, Peggotty, perfectly.", "\"네 마음을 환히 꿰뚫고 있다고, 패거티."], ["You know I do, and I wonder you don’t colour up like fire. But one point at a time.", "내가 그런다는 걸 너도 알 테고, 어째서 네가 불처럼 얼굴이 달아오르지 않는지 이상할 지경이다."], ["Miss Murdstone is the point now, Peggotty, and you sha’n’t escape from it.", "하지만 한 번에 한 가지씩만 하자. 지금은 머드스톤 양 얘기야, 패거티."], ["Haven’t you heard her say, over and over again, that she thinks I am too thoughtless and too--a--a--’", "넌 거기서 빠져나갈 생각 마라. 그분이 입버릇처럼 하시는 말씀 못 들었니? 내가 너무 경솔하고 너무 — 어 — 어 —\""]], [["‘Pretty,’ suggested Peggotty.", "\"예쁘다고요.\" 패거티가 거들었다."]], [["‘Well,’ returned my mother, half laughing, ‘and if she is so silly as to say so, can I be blamed for it?’", "\"뭐,\" 어머니가 반쯤 웃으며 받았다. \"그분이 그렇게 어리석게 그런 말씀을 하신다 한들, 그게 내 탓이겠니?\""]], [["‘No one says you can,’ said Peggotty.", "\"아무도 마님 탓이라고는 안 해요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘No, I should hope not, indeed!’ returned my mother.", "\"그래, 정말 그래야지!\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"그분이 그래서 나를 위해 온갖 수고를 덜어 주고 싶어 하신다고 입버릇처럼 말씀하시는 거 못 들었니?"], ["‘Haven’t you heard her say, over and over again, that on this account she wished to spare me a great deal of trouble, which she thinks I am not suited for, and which I really don’t know myself that I AM suited for; and isn’t she up early and late, and going to and fro continually--and doesn’t she do all sorts of things, and grope into all sorts of places, coal-holes and pantries and I don’t know where, that can’t be very agreeable--and do you mean to insinuate that there is not a sort of devotion in that?’", "내가 그런 일에 어울리지 않는다고 여기시고, 나도 정말이지 내가 그런 일에 어울리는지 어떤지 모르겠는데 말이야. 그분은 아침저녁으로 부지런히 움직이고, 쉴 새 없이 이리저리 오가시지 않니 — 온갖 일을 다 하시고, 석탄 광이며 식료품 저장실이며 어딘지도 모를 온갖 곳을 다 뒤지고 다니시잖아. 결코 유쾌할 리 없는 일들을 말이야 — 그런데 거기에 일종의 헌신이 없다고 넌 넌지시 비꼬려는 거니?\""]], [["‘I don’t insinuate at all,’ said Peggotty.", "\"전 비꼬는 거 하나도 없어요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘You do, Peggotty,’ returned my mother.", "\"넌 비꼬는 거야, 패거티.\" 어머니가 받아쳤다."], ["‘You never do anything else, except your work.", "\"넌 네 일 말고는 늘 그것밖에 안 하잖아."], ["You are always insinuating.", "늘 비꼰다고."], ["You revel in it.", "거기에 신이 나서."], ["And when you talk of Mr. Murdstone’s good intentions--’", "그리고 머드스톤 씨의 좋은 뜻을 두고 네가 말할 때면 —\""]], [["‘I never talked of ‘em,’ said Peggotty.", "\"전 그분 얘긴 한마디도 안 했어요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘No, Peggotty,’ returned my mother, ‘but you insinuated.", "\"그래, 패거티.\" 어머니가 받아쳤다. \"하지만 넌 넌지시 비꼬았어."], ["That’s what I told you just now.", "방금 내가 그렇게 말했잖니."], ["That’s the worst of you.", "그게 너의 가장 못된 버릇이야."], ["You WILL insinuate.", "넌 기어이 비꼰다니까."], ["I said, at the moment, that I understood you, and you see I did.", "내가 아까도 네 속을 다 안다고 했고, 봐라, 정말 알았잖아."], ["When you talk of Mr. Murdstone’s good intentions, and pretend to slight them (for I don’t believe you really do, in your heart, Peggotty), you must be as well convinced as I am how good they are, and how they actuate him in everything.", "머드스톤 씨의 좋은 뜻을 두고 네가 말하면서 그걸 깔보는 척할 때 (네 마음속으로는 정말로 그러는 건 아니라고 믿지만, 패거티), 너도 나만큼이나 그 뜻이 얼마나 좋은지, 그 뜻이 그분을 매사에 어떻게 움직이는지 잘 알고 있을 거야."], ["If he seems to have been at all stern with a certain person, Peggotty--you understand, and so I am sure does Davy, that I am not alluding to anybody present--it is solely because he is satisfied that it is for a certain person’s benefit.", "그분이 어떤 사람에게 다소 엄하게 구신 것처럼 보였다면, 패거티 — 너도 알 테고, 데이비도 분명 알 텐데, 내가 지금 여기 있는 누구를 두고 하는 말이 아니라는 걸 — 그건 오로지 그게 어떤 사람을 위하는 길이라고 확신하시기 때문이야."], ["He naturally loves a certain person, on my account; and acts solely for a certain person’s good.", "그분은 나 때문에 자연히 어떤 사람을 사랑하시고, 오로지 어떤 사람을 위해서만 행동하시는 거야. 그 일을 판단하는 데는 나보다 그분이 훨씬 나아."], ["He is better able to judge of it than I am; for I very well know that I am a weak, light, girlish creature, and that he is a firm, grave, serious man.", "나야 약하고 가볍고 계집애 같은 사람이고, 그분은 굳건하고 진중하고 진지한 분이라는 걸 잘 아니까. 그리고 그분은,\" 어머니가, 그 다정한 천성에서 우러난 눈물을 얼굴에 살그머니 흘리며 말했다."], ["And he takes,’ said my mother, with the tears which were engendered in her affectionate nature, stealing down her face, ‘he takes great pains with me; and I ought to be very thankful to him, and very submissive to him even in my thoughts; and when I am not, Peggotty, I worry and condemn myself, and feel doubtful of my own heart, and don’t know what to do.’", "\"나를 위해 무던히 애를 써 주셔. 그러니 나는 그분께 무척 감사해야 하고, 마음속으로까지도 그분께 순종해야 해. 그런데 그러지 못할 때면, 패거티, 나는 마음을 졸이며 나 자신을 탓하고, 내 마음을 의심하면서, 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어.\""]], [["Peggotty sat with her chin on the foot of the stocking, looking silently at the fire.", "패거티는 양말 발끝에 턱을 괸 채, 말없이 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘There, Peggotty,’ said my mother, changing her tone, ‘don’t let us fall out with one another, for I couldn’t bear it.", "\"자, 패거티.\" 어머니가 어조를 바꾸어 말했다."], ["You are my true friend, I know, if I have any in the world.", "\"우리 서로 다투지는 말자. 난 그걸 견딜 수가 없어."], ["When I call you a ridiculous creature, or a vexatious thing, or anything of that sort, Peggotty, I only mean that you are my true friend, and always have been, ever since the night when Mr.", "이 세상에 나한테 진정한 벗이 하나라도 있다면, 그게 너라는 걸 안다. 내가 너를 우스운 사람이라느니, 골치 아픈 사람이라느니, 그런 비슷한 말로 부를 때, 패거티, 그건 다만 네가 내 진정한 벗이라는 뜻일 뿐이야."], ["Copperfield first brought me home here, and you came out to the gate to meet me.’", "코퍼필드 씨가 나를 처음 이 집으로 데려오던 날 밤, 네가 문간으로 마중 나왔던 그때부터 줄곧 그래 왔다는 뜻이야.\""]], [["Peggotty was not slow to respond, and ratify the treaty of friendship by giving me one of her best hugs.", "패거티는 지체 없이 응답하여, 나를 한 번 힘껏 끌어안는 것으로 우정의 조약을 비준했다."], ["I think I had some glimpses of the real character of this conversation at the time; but I am sure, now, that the good creature originated it, and took her part in it, merely that my mother might comfort herself with the little contradictory summary in which she had indulged.", "그때도 나는 이 대화의 진짜 속내를 얼마간 어렴풋이 알아챘던 것 같다. 하지만 이제는 분명히 안다. 그 착한 사람이 이 대화를 먼저 꺼내고 거기서 제 몫을 맡았던 것은, 오로지 어머니가 스스로 즐겨 늘어놓은 그 조그맣고 앞뒤 안 맞는 결론으로 마음의 위안을 얻게 하려는 것이었음을."], ["The design was efficacious; for I remember that my mother seemed more at ease during the rest of the evening, and that Peggotty observed her less.", "그 뜻은 효험이 있었다. 그날 저녁 내내 어머니가 한결 마음 편해 보였고, 패거티도 어머니를 덜 살피는 듯했던 게 기억나니까."]], [["When we had had our tea, and the ashes were thrown up, and the candles snuffed, I read Peggotty a chapter out of the Crocodile Book, in remembrance of old times--she took it out of her pocket: I don’t know whether she had kept it there ever since--and then we talked about Salem House, which brought me round again to Steerforth, who was my great subject.", "차를 다 마시고, 재를 긁어 올리고, 촛불 심지를 다듬고 나서, 나는 옛 시절을 추억하며 패거티에게 악어 책에서 한 장을 읽어 주었다 — 패거티가 주머니에서 그 책을 꺼냈는데, 그동안 줄곧 거기 넣어 두었던 건지는 모르겠다 — 그러고 나서 우리는 세일럼 하우스 이야기를 했고, 그러다 보니 다시 스티어포스 이야기로 돌아오게 되었는데, 그는 내 단골 화제였다."], ["We were very happy; and that evening, as the last of its race, and destined evermore to close that volume of my life, will never pass out of my memory.", "우리는 무척 행복했다. 그리고 그날 저녁은, 그런 저녁의 마지막이자 내 인생의 그 권(卷)을 영영 닫게끔 정해진 저녁으로서, 내 기억에서 결코 사라지지 않을 것이다."]], [["It was almost ten o’clock before we heard the sound of wheels.", "마차 바퀴 소리가 들린 것은 거의 열 시가 다 되어서였다."], ["We all got up then; and my mother said hurriedly that, as it was so late, and Mr. and Miss Murdstone approved of early hours for young people, perhaps I had better go to bed.", "그러자 우리는 다들 일어섰다. 어머니는 시간이 너무 늦었고, 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 젊은 사람은 일찍 자는 게 좋다고 여기시니, 내가 가서 자는 편이 낫겠다고 황급히 말했다."], ["I kissed her, and went upstairs with my candle directly, before they came in.", "나는 어머니에게 입을 맞추고는, 그들이 들어오기 전에 곧장 촛불을 들고 이층으로 올라갔다."], ["It appeared to my childish fancy, as I ascended to the bedroom where I had been imprisoned, that they brought a cold blast of air into the house which blew away the old familiar feeling like a feather.", "예전에 갇혀 있던 그 침실로 올라가던 중에, 어린 마음에 이런 느낌이 들었다. 그들이 집 안으로 차가운 바람 한 줄기를 몰고 들어와, 그 옛날의 정겨운 느낌을 깃털처럼 날려 버리는 것 같다고."]], [["I felt uncomfortable about going down to breakfast in the morning, as I had never set eyes on Mr. Murdstone since the day when I committed my memorable offence.", "아침에 내려가 아침을 먹는 게 영 마음이 편치 않았는데, 내가 그 잊지 못할 잘못을 저지른 날 이후로 머드스톤 씨를 한 번도 마주친 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["However, as it must be done, I went down, after two or three false starts half-way, and as many runs back on tiptoe to my own room, and presented myself in the parlour.", "그래도 안 할 수는 없는 노릇이라, 나는 계단 중간까지 갔다가 두세 번 멈칫 되돌아오고, 발끝으로 또 그만큼 내 방으로 도로 뛰어가기를 거듭한 끝에, 거실에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["He was standing before the fire with his back to it, while Miss Murdstone made the tea.", "그는 난로를 등진 채 그 앞에 서 있었고, 머드스톤 양은 차를 끓이고 있었다."], ["He looked at me steadily as I entered, but made no sign of recognition whatever.", "내가 들어서자 그는 나를 빤히 쳐다보았으나, 아는 척하는 기색은 조금도 없었다."], ["I went up to him, after a moment of confusion, and said: ‘I beg your pardon, sir.", "나는 잠깐 어쩔 줄 몰라 하다가 그에게 다가가 말했다. \"죄송합니다, 아저씨."], ["I am very sorry for what I did, and I hope you will forgive me.’", "제가 한 일을 무척 뉘우치고 있어요. 부디 용서해 주세요.\""]], [["‘I am glad to hear you are sorry, David,’ he replied.", "\"네가 뉘우친다니 다행이구나, 데이비드.\" 그가 대답했다."]], [["The hand he gave me was the hand I had bitten.", "그가 나에게 내민 손은 내가 물어뜯었던 그 손이었다."], ["I could not restrain my eye from resting for an instant on a red spot upon it; but it was not so red as I turned, when I met that sinister expression in his face.", "나는 거기 난 붉은 자국에 한순간 눈길이 머무는 걸 어쩌지 못했다. 하지만 그 얼굴에 떠오른 음험한 표정과 마주쳐 내 얼굴이 시뻘게졌을 때, 그 손의 자국은 그만큼 붉지는 않았다."]], [["‘How do you do, ma’am?’ I said to Miss Murdstone.", "\"안녕하세요, 아주머니?\" 나는 머드스톤 양에게 말했다."]], [["‘Ah, dear me!’ sighed Miss Murdstone, giving me the tea-caddy scoop instead of her fingers.", "\"아유, 저런!\" 머드스톤 양이 한숨을 쉬며, 손가락 대신 차통 숟가락을 나에게 내밀었다."], ["‘How long are the holidays?’", "\"방학이 며칠이라더냐?\""]], [["‘A month, ma’am.’", "\"한 달이에요, 아주머니.\""]], [["‘Counting from when?’", "\"언제부터 치는데?\""]], [["‘From today, ma’am.’", "\"오늘부터요, 아주머니.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Miss Murdstone.", "\"오!\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다."], ["‘Then here’s one day off.’", "\"그럼 하루가 지나간 거구나.\""]], [["She kept a calendar of the holidays in this way, and every morning checked a day off in exactly the same manner.", "그녀는 이런 식으로 방학 달력을 매기며, 매일 아침 똑같은 방식으로 하루씩 지워 나갔다."], ["She did it gloomily until she came to ten, but when she got into two figures she became more hopeful, and, as the time advanced, even jocular.", "열흘째가 될 때까지는 우울하게 지워 나갔지만, 두 자릿수로 들어서면 한결 기대에 부풀었고, 날이 갈수록 심지어 농담까지 던졌다."]], [["It was on this very first day that I had the misfortune to throw her, though she was not subject to such weakness in general, into a state of violent consternation.", "평소에는 그런 약한 모습을 보이는 사람이 아니었건만, 바로 이 첫날 나는 불행히도 그녀를 격렬한 경악 상태에 빠뜨리고 말았다."], ["I came into the room where she and my mother were sitting; and the baby (who was only a few weeks old) being on my mother’s lap, I took it very carefully in my arms.", "나는 그녀와 어머니가 앉아 있던 방으로 들어갔는데, 아기(태어난 지 겨우 몇 주밖에 안 된)가 어머니 무릎에 있어서, 나는 그 아기를 아주 조심스럽게 두 팔로 안았다."], ["Suddenly Miss Murdstone gave such a scream that I all but dropped it.", "그 순간 머드스톤 양이 어찌나 비명을 질러 댔던지, 나는 하마터면 아기를 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다."]], [["‘My dear Jane!’ cried my mother.", "\"제인, 얘!\" 어머니가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Good heavens, Clara, do you see?’ exclaimed Miss Murdstone.", "\"맙소사, 클라라, 저게 보여?\" 머드스톤 양이 부르짖었다."]], [["‘See what, my dear Jane?’ said my mother; ‘where?’", "\"뭐가 보인다는 거야, 제인?\" 어머니가 말했다. \"어디?\""]], [["‘He’s got it!’ cried Miss Murdstone.", "\"저 애가 안았어!\" 머드스톤 양이 외쳤다."], ["‘The boy has got the baby!’", "\"저 애가 아기를 안았다고!\""]], [["She was limp with horror; but stiffened herself to make a dart at me, and take it out of my arms.", "그녀는 공포에 질려 맥이 풀렸지만, 몸을 다잡고 나에게 달려들어 내 팔에서 아기를 빼앗아 갔다."], ["Then, she turned faint; and was so very ill that they were obliged to give her cherry brandy.", "그러고는 정신이 아득해지더니, 어찌나 심하게 아파했던지 사람들은 그녀에게 체리 브랜디를 먹여야 했다."], ["I was solemnly interdicted by her, on her recovery, from touching my brother any more on any pretence whatever; and my poor mother, who, I could see, wished otherwise, meekly confirmed the interdict, by saying: ‘No doubt you are right, my dear Jane.’", "그녀는 정신을 차리고 나서, 어떤 핑계로든 내 동생을 두 번 다시 만지지 말라고 나에게 엄숙히 명했다. 그리고 내 가엾은 어머니는, 내가 보기엔 그러지 않기를 바랐으면서도, \"네 말이 맞아, 제인.\" 하고 말하며 그 금령을 순순히 못 박았다."]], [["On another occasion, when we three were together, this same dear baby--it was truly dear to me, for our mother’s sake--was the innocent occasion of Miss Murdstone’s going into a passion.", "또 한 번은 우리 셋이 함께 있을 때, 바로 이 사랑스러운 아기가 — 어머니 때문에라도 나에게는 정말로 사랑스러웠던 — 머드스톤 양이 발끈하는 죄 없는 빌미가 되었다."], ["My mother, who had been looking at its eyes as it lay upon her lap, said:", "어머니는 아기를 무릎에 뉘어 놓고 그 눈을 들여다보다가 말했다."]], [["‘Davy!", "\"데이비!"], ["come here!’ and looked at mine.", "이리 와 보렴!\" 그러고는 내 눈을 들여다보았다."]], [["I saw Miss Murdstone lay her beads down.", "나는 머드스톤 양이 묵주를 내려놓는 걸 보았다."]], [["‘I declare,’ said my mother, gently, ‘they are exactly alike.", "\"정말이지,\" 어머니가 부드럽게 말했다. \"둘이 똑 닮았어."], ["I suppose they are mine.", "내 눈을 닮은 것 같아."], ["I think they are the colour of mine.", "색깔이 내 눈빛 같아."], ["But they are wonderfully alike.’", "그런데 정말 놀랍도록 닮았네.\""]], [["‘What are you talking about, Clara?’ said Miss Murdstone.", "\"무슨 소릴 하는 거야, 클라라?\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Jane,’ faltered my mother, a little abashed by the harsh tone of this inquiry, ‘I find that the baby’s eyes and Davy’s are exactly alike.’", "\"제인,\" 어머니가 이 매서운 물음투에 다소 주눅이 들어 더듬거렸다. \"아기 눈하고 데이비 눈이 똑 닮은 걸 알았다는 거야.\""]], [["‘Clara!’ said Miss Murdstone, rising angrily, ‘you are a positive fool sometimes.’", "\"클라라!\" 머드스톤 양이 성을 내며 일어섰다. \"넌 가끔 영락없는 바보로구나.\""]], [["‘My dear Jane,’ remonstrated my mother.", "\"제인, 얘.\" 어머니가 항변했다."]], [["‘A positive fool,’ said Miss Murdstone.", "\"영락없는 바보야.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다."], ["‘Who else could compare my brother’s baby with your boy?", "\"그 누가 우리 오라버니 아기를 네 아들 따위와 견준단 말이냐?"], ["They are not at all alike.", "둘은 조금도 안 닮았어."], ["They are exactly unlike.", "정반대로 다르지."], ["They are utterly dissimilar in all respects.", "모든 면에서 완전히 딴판이야."], ["I hope they will ever remain so.", "앞으로도 영영 그러기를 바란다."], ["I will not sit here, and hear such comparisons made.’ With that she stalked out, and made the door bang after her.", "난 여기 앉아서 그런 비교나 듣고 있진 않겠어.\" 그 말과 함께 그녀는 성큼성큼 걸어 나가, 문을 쾅 닫아 버렸다."]], [["In short, I was not a favourite with Miss Murdstone.", "요컨대, 나는 머드스톤 양의 귀염을 받는 아이가 아니었다."], ["In short, I was not a favourite there with anybody, not even with myself; for those who did like me could not show it, and those who did not, showed it so plainly that I had a sensitive consciousness of always appearing constrained, boorish, and dull.", "요컨대, 나는 거기서 그 누구의 귀염도, 심지어 나 자신의 귀염조차 받지 못했다. 나를 좋아하는 사람들은 그걸 드러낼 수 없었고, 나를 좋아하지 않는 사람들은 어찌나 노골적으로 드러내던지, 나는 늘 내가 거북하고 촌스럽고 굼떠 보이는 것 같다는 걸 예민하게 의식하고 있었으니까."]], [["I felt that I made them as uncomfortable as they made me. If I came into the room where they were, and they were talking together and my mother seemed cheerful, an anxious cloud would steal over her face from the moment of my entrance.", "나는 내가 그들을 거북하게 하는 만큼 그들도 나를 거북하게 한다고 느꼈다. 내가 그들이 함께 이야기를 나누고 있는 방에 들어서면, 어머니가 즐거워 보이다가도 내가 들어서는 순간부터 그 얼굴에 근심의 그늘이 슬며시 드리워졌다."], ["If Mr. Murdstone were in his best humour, I checked him.", "머드스톤 씨가 기분이 더없이 좋을 때면 나는 그 기분을 꺾어 놓았다."], ["If Miss Murdstone were in her worst, I intensified it.", "머드스톤 양이 기분이 더없이 나쁠 때면 나는 그걸 더 부추겼다."], ["I had perception enough to know that my mother was the victim always; that she was afraid to speak to me or to be kind to me, lest she should give them some offence by her manner of doing so, and receive a lecture afterwards; that she was not only ceaselessly afraid of her own offending, but of my offending, and uneasily watched their looks if I only moved.", "나는 어머니가 늘 희생양이라는 걸 알아챌 만큼은 눈치가 있었다. 어머니는 나에게 말을 걸거나 다정하게 대했다가, 그 태도가 그들에게 무슨 거슬림을 주어 나중에 잔소리를 들을까 봐 그러기를 두려워했다. 어머니는 끊임없이 자기가 잘못할까 봐 두려워했을 뿐 아니라 내가 잘못할까 봐도 두려워했고, 내가 조금만 움직여도 불안하게 그들의 눈치를 살폈다."], ["Therefore I resolved to keep myself as much out of their way as I could; and many a wintry hour did I hear the church clock strike, when I was sitting in my cheerless bedroom, wrapped in my little great-coat, poring over a book.", "그래서 나는 되도록 그들의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 마음먹었고, 음울한 내 침실에 앉아 작은 외투를 두른 채 책에 코를 박고 있노라면, 교회 종이 울리는 소리를 겨울날 몇 시간이고 듣곤 했다."]], [["In the evening, sometimes, I went and sat with Peggotty in the kitchen.", "저녁이면 나는 가끔 부엌에 가서 패거티와 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["There I was comfortable, and not afraid of being myself.", "거기서는 마음이 편했고, 나 자신으로 있는 게 두렵지 않았다."], ["But neither of these resources was approved of in the parlour.", "그러나 이 두 가지 도피처는 거실에서 허락되지 않았다."], ["The tormenting humour which was dominant there stopped them both.", "거기를 지배하는 그 괴롭히기 좋아하는 심술이 둘 다 가로막았다."], ["I was still held to be necessary to my poor mother’s training, and, as one of her trials, could not be suffered to absent myself.", "나는 여전히 내 가엾은 어머니의 훈육에 꼭 필요한 존재로, 또 어머니의 시련 가운데 하나로 여겨졌으니, 자리를 비우는 게 용납되지 않았던 것이다."]], [["‘David,’ said Mr.", "\"데이비드.\""], ["Murdstone, one day after dinner when I was going to leave the room as usual; ‘I am sorry to observe that you are of a sullen disposition.’", "어느 날 저녁을 먹고 나서 내가 늘 그렇듯 방을 나가려 하자 머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"네가 시무룩한 성미를 지녔다는 걸 알게 되어 유감이구나.\""]], [["‘As sulky as a bear!’ said Miss Murdstone.", "\"곰처럼 뚱한 게야!\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다."]], [["I stood still, and hung my head.", "나는 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["‘Now, David,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 데이비드.\""], ["Murdstone, ‘a sullen obdurate disposition is, of all tempers, the worst.’", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"시무룩하고 고집스러운 성미야말로 모든 기질 가운데 가장 못된 것이다.\""]], [["‘And the boy’s is, of all such dispositions that ever I have seen,’ remarked his sister, ‘the most confirmed and stubborn.", "\"게다가 이 애 성미는, 내가 본 그런 모든 성미 가운데,\" 그의 누이가 거들었다. \"가장 뿌리 깊고 고집불통이지."], ["I think, my dear Clara, even you must observe it?’", "클라라, 너조차도 이쯤은 알아챌 거다, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, my dear Jane,’ said my mother, ‘but are you quite sure--I am certain you’ll excuse me, my dear Jane--that you understand Davy?’", "\"미안하지만, 제인.\" 어머니가 말했다. \"정말 확실해 — 분명 날 이해해 줄 거야, 제인 — 네가 데이비를 제대로 알고 있다고 말이야?\""]], [["‘I should be somewhat ashamed of myself, Clara,’ returned Miss Murdstone, ‘if I could not understand the boy, or any boy.", "\"내가 이 애나 다른 어떤 애를 이해 못 한다면, 클라라, 나 자신이 좀 부끄러울 거다.\" 머드스톤 양이 받아쳤다."], ["I don’t profess to be profound; but I do lay claim to common sense.’", "\"내가 무슨 깊은 사람인 척하는 건 아니지만, 상식만큼은 갖췄다고 자부한다.\""]], [["‘No doubt, my dear Jane,’ returned my mother, ‘your understanding is very vigorous--’", "\"물론이지, 제인.\" 어머니가 받았다. \"네 이해력이야 무척 빈틈없지 —\""]], [["‘Oh dear, no!", "\"아이고, 천만에!"], ["Pray don’t say that, Clara,’ interposed Miss Murdstone, angrily.", "제발 그런 소린 마, 클라라.\" 머드스톤 양이 성을 내며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘But I am sure it is,’ resumed my mother; ‘and everybody knows it is.", "\"하지만 정말 그런걸.\" 어머니가 다시 말했다. \"다들 그런 줄 알아."], ["I profit so much by it myself, in many ways--at least I ought to--that no one can be more convinced of it than myself; and therefore I speak with great diffidence, my dear Jane, I assure you.’", "나야말로 여러모로 거기서 큰 덕을 보고 있고 — 적어도 봐야 마땅하지 — 그러니 나만큼 그걸 절실히 아는 사람도 없을 거야. 그래서 정말이지 무척 조심스럽게 말하는 거야, 제인.\""]], [["‘We’ll say I don’t understand the boy, Clara,’ returned Miss Murdstone, arranging the little fetters on her wrists.", "\"내가 이 애를 이해 못 한다고 치자, 클라라.\" 머드스톤 양이 손목에 찬 작은 족쇄 같은 장신구를 매만지며 받아쳤다."], ["‘We’ll agree, if you please, that I don’t understand him at all.", "\"네가 원한다면, 내가 이 애를 도무지 이해 못 한다고 해 두자."], ["He is much too deep for me.", "이 애는 나한테 너무 속이 깊으니까."], ["But perhaps my brother’s penetration may enable him to have some insight into his character.", "하지만 어쩌면 우리 오라버니의 통찰력이라면 이 애의 성격을 얼마간 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있을지도 모르지."], ["And I believe my brother was speaking on the subject when we--not very decently--interrupted him.’", "그리고 우리가 — 그리 점잖지 못하게 — 끼어들었을 때, 마침 오라버니가 그 얘길 하고 있었던 것 같은데.\""]], [["‘I think, Clara,’ said Mr.", "\"내 생각엔, 클라라.\""], ["Murdstone, in a low grave voice, ‘that there may be better and more dispassionate judges of such a question than you.’", "머드스톤 씨가 낮고 진중한 목소리로 말했다. \"그런 문제를 두고는 너보다 더 낫고 더 냉정한 판단자가 있을 듯하구나.\""]], [["‘Edward,’ replied my mother, timidly, ‘you are a far better judge of all questions than I pretend to be.", "\"에드워드.\" 어머니가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"당신은 모든 문제에서 제가 감히 견줄 수도 없을 만큼 훨씬 나은 판단자예요."], ["Both you and Jane are.", "당신도 제인도 다 그래요."], ["I only said--’", "저는 그저 —\""]], [["‘You only said something weak and inconsiderate,’ he replied.", "\"당신은 그저 어리석고 경솔한 말을 했을 뿐이야.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘Try not to do it again, my dear Clara, and keep a watch upon yourself.’", "\"다시는 그러지 않도록 애쓰고, 자신을 잘 단속해, 클라라.\""]], [["My mother’s lips moved, as if she answered ‘Yes, my dear Edward,’ but she said nothing aloud.", "어머니의 입술이, '네, 에드워드.' 하고 대답하듯 달싹였지만, 입 밖으로는 아무 말도 내지 않았다."]], [["‘I was sorry, David, I remarked,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 유감이라고 했지, 데이비드.\""], ["Murdstone, turning his head and his eyes stiffly towards me, ‘to observe that you are of a sullen disposition.", "머드스톤 씨가 고개와 두 눈을 뻣뻣하게 나에게로 돌리며 말했다. \"네가 시무룩한 성미를 지녔다는 걸 알게 되어 말이다."], ["This is not a character that I can suffer to develop itself beneath my eyes without an effort at improvement.", "이건 내 눈앞에서 고쳐 보려는 노력도 없이 멋대로 자라도록 두고 볼 수 있는 성격이 아니다."], ["You must endeavour, sir, to change it.", "너는 그걸 바꾸도록 애써야 한다."], ["We must endeavour to change it for you.’", "우리도 너를 위해 그걸 바꾸도록 애써야 하고.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ I faltered.", "\"죄송해요, 아저씨.\" 나는 더듬거렸다."], ["‘I have never meant to be sullen since I came back.’", "\"돌아온 뒤로 시무룩하게 굴 생각은 한 번도 없었어요.\""]], [["‘Don’t take refuge in a lie, sir!’ he returned so fiercely, that I saw my mother involuntarily put out her trembling hand as if to interpose between us.", "\"거짓말 뒤에 숨지 마라!\" 그가 어찌나 사납게 받아치던지, 어머니가 우리 사이에 끼어들기라도 하려는 듯 떨리는 손을 저도 모르게 내미는 게 보였다."], ["‘You have withdrawn yourself in your sullenness to your own room.", "\"너는 시무룩한 심사로 네 방에 틀어박혔어."], ["You have kept your own room when you ought to have been here.", "여기 있어야 마땅할 때 네 방에만 처박혀 있었지."], ["You know now, once for all, that I require you to be here, and not there.", "이제 단단히 알아 둬라. 나는 네가 거기가 아니라 여기 있기를 바란다."], ["Further, that I require you to bring obedience here.", "게다가 나는 네가 여기에 순종을 들고 오기를 바라."], ["You know me, David. I will have it done.’", "너는 나를 알지, 데이비드. 나는 기어이 이 일을 시킬 거야.\""]], [["Miss Murdstone gave a hoarse chuckle.", "머드스톤 양이 쉰 목소리로 낄낄거렸다."]], [["‘I will have a respectful, prompt, and ready bearing towards myself,’ he continued, ‘and towards Jane Murdstone, and towards your mother.", "\"나는 나 자신에 대해, 그리고 제인 머드스톤에 대해, 그리고 네 어머니에 대해, 공손하고 즉각적이며 선뜻한 태도를 보길 바란다.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["I will not have this room shunned as if it were infected, at the pleasure of a child. Sit down.’", "\"나는 이 방을 무슨 병이라도 옮은 곳인 양 어린애 멋대로 피해 다니는 꼴을 두고 보지 않겠다. 앉아라.\""]], [["He ordered me like a dog, and I obeyed like a dog.", "그는 개를 부리듯 나에게 명령했고, 나는 개처럼 따랐다."]], [["‘One thing more,’ he said.", "\"한 가지 더.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘I observe that you have an attachment to low and common company.", "\"보아하니 너는 천하고 상스러운 무리를 좋아하는 모양이더구나."], ["You are not to associate with servants.", "너는 하인들과 어울려선 안 된다."], ["The kitchen will not improve you, in the many respects in which you need improvement.", "부엌은, 네가 고쳐야 할 그 숱한 면에서 너를 조금도 나아지게 하지 못해."], ["Of the woman who abets you, I say nothing--since you, Clara,’ addressing my mother in a lower voice, ‘from old associations and long-established fancies, have a weakness respecting her which is not yet overcome.’", "너를 부추기는 그 여자에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠다 — 클라라, 너는,\" 그가 어머니를 향해 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"오랜 인연과 뿌리 깊은 정 때문에 그 여자를 두고 아직 다 떨쳐 내지 못한 약한 마음이 있으니까.\""]], [["‘A most unaccountable delusion it is!’ cried Miss Murdstone.", "\"참으로 알다가도 모를 망상이지요!\" 머드스톤 양이 외쳤다."]], [["‘I only say,’ he resumed, addressing me, ‘that I disapprove of your preferring such company as Mistress Peggotty, and that it is to be abandoned.", "\"나는 다만 이 말만 하겠다.\" 그가 나를 향해 다시 말했다. \"나는 네가 패거티 부인 같은 무리를 좋아하는 걸 못마땅하게 여기며, 그건 그만두어야 한다."], ["Now, David, you understand me, and you know what will be the consequence if you fail to obey me to the letter.’", "자, 데이비드, 너는 내 말을 알아들었고, 내 말을 한 자도 어김없이 따르지 않으면 어떤 결과가 따를지도 안다.\""]], [["I knew well--better perhaps than he thought, as far as my poor mother was concerned--and I obeyed him to the letter.", "나는 잘 알았다 — 내 가엾은 어머니에 관한 한, 어쩌면 그가 생각하는 것보다 더 잘 알았다 — 그래서 나는 그의 말을 한 자도 어김없이 따랐다."], ["I retreated to my own room no more; I took refuge with Peggotty no more; but sat wearily in the parlour day after day, looking forward to night, and bedtime.", "나는 두 번 다시 내 방으로 물러가지 않았고, 두 번 다시 패거티에게로 피신하지 않았다. 그저 날마다 지치도록 거실에 앉아, 밤이 오기를, 잠자리에 들 시간이 오기를 손꼽아 기다렸다."]], [["What irksome constraint I underwent, sitting in the same attitude hours upon hours, afraid to move an arm or a leg lest Miss Murdstone should complain (as she did on the least pretence) of my restlessness, and afraid to move an eye lest she should light on some look of dislike or scrutiny that would find new cause for complaint in mine!", "내가 견뎌야 했던 그 진절머리 나는 속박이라니. 몇 시간이고 똑같은 자세로 앉아, 팔 하나 다리 하나 움직였다간 머드스톤 양이 (그녀는 아주 사소한 핑곗거리에도 그랬다) 내가 안절부절못한다고 트집 잡을까 봐 두렵고, 눈 한 번 굴렸다간 내 눈에서 새로운 트집거리를 찾아낼 만한 싫증이나 의심의 빛이 그녀 눈에 떠오를까 봐 두려웠으니!"], ["What intolerable dulness to sit listening to the ticking of the clock; and watching Miss Murdstone’s little shiny steel beads as she strung them; and wondering whether she would ever be married, and if so, to what sort of unhappy man; and counting the divisions in the moulding of the chimney-piece; and wandering away, with my eyes, to the ceiling, among the curls and corkscrews in the paper on the wall!", "시계가 째깍거리는 소리에 귀 기울이고, 머드스톤 양이 자그마한 반짝이는 강철 구슬을 꿰는 걸 지켜보며, 그녀가 과연 시집을 가긴 갈지, 간다면 어떤 불행한 사내한테 갈지 궁금해하고, 벽난로 선반 쇠시리의 칸들을 헤아리고, 벽지에 그려진 곱슬곱슬하고 나선으로 꼬인 무늬들 사이로 천장까지 눈길을 떠돌리며 앉아 있는 그 견딜 수 없는 따분함이라니!"]], [["What walks I took alone, down muddy lanes, in the bad winter weather, carrying that parlour, and Mr.", "궂은 겨울 날씨에 진흙투성이 골목길을 따라 나는 또 얼마나 많이 홀로 걸어 다녔던가."], ["and Miss Murdstone in it, everywhere: a monstrous load that I was obliged to bear, a daymare that there was no possibility of breaking in, a weight that brooded on my wits, and blunted them!", "그 거실을, 그리고 그 안의 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양을 어디로든 짊어지고서. 그것은 내가 짊어질 수밖에 없는 끔찍한 짐이었고, 도무지 깨고 나올 길이 없는 한낮의 악몽이었으며, 내 정신 위에 내려앉아 그것을 무디게 만드는 무게였다!"]], [["What meals I had in silence and embarrassment, always feeling that there were a knife and fork too many, and that mine; an appetite too many, and that mine; a plate and chair too many, and those mine; a somebody too many, and that I!", "내가 먹은 그 식사들은 또 얼마나 침묵과 거북함 속이었던가. 나이프와 포크가 하나 너무 많은데 그게 바로 내 것이고, 식욕이 하나 너무 많은데 그게 바로 내 것이며, 접시와 의자가 하나 너무 많은데 그게 바로 내 것이고, 누군가 하나가 너무 많은데 그게 바로 나라는 느낌을 늘 받으면서!"]], [["What evenings, when the candles came, and I was expected to employ myself, but, not daring to read an entertaining book, pored over some hard-headed, harder-hearted treatise on arithmetic; when the tables of weights and measures set themselves to tunes, as ‘Rule Britannia’, or ‘Away with Melancholy’; when they wouldn’t stand still to be learnt, but would go threading my grandmother’s needle through my unfortunate head, in at one ear and out at the other! What yawns and dozes I lapsed into, in spite of all my care; what starts I came out of concealed sleeps with; what answers I never got, to little observations that I rarely made; what a blank space I seemed, which everybody overlooked, and yet was in everybody’s way; what a heavy relief it was to hear Miss Murdstone hail the first stroke of nine at night, and order me to bed!", "촛불이 들어오고 나도 무언가 일거리를 찾아야 할 그 저녁들은 또 어땠던가. 재미난 책은 감히 읽지도 못하고, 머리가 딱딱하고 마음은 더 딱딱한 산수 책 따위를 들여다보고 있노라면, 도량형 표가 저절로 '룰 브리타니아'며 '우울아 물러가라' 같은 곡조에 맞춰 노래를 불러 댔다. 그것들은 외워지려고 가만히 있어 주기는커녕, 우리 할머니의 바늘귀에 실을 꿰듯 내 가엾은 머릿속을 한쪽 귀로 들어왔다 다른 쪽 귀로 빠져나가며 줄줄이 꿰뚫고 지나가는 것이었다! 그토록 조심을 했건만 나는 또 얼마나 자주 하품을 하고 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았던가. 몰래 빠져든 잠에서 또 얼마나 화들짝 깨어났던가. 내가 어쩌다 한마디씩 던지는 사소한 말에 돌아오는 대답은 또 얼마나 없었던가. 나는 모두가 못 본 척하면서도 모두에게 거치적거리는, 텅 빈 공백 같은 존재였다. 밤 아홉 시 첫 종소리를 머드스톤 양이 반기며 나에게 잠자리에 들라고 명할 때, 그게 또 얼마나 묵직한 안도였던가!"]], [["Thus the holidays lagged away, until the morning came when Miss Murdstone said: ‘Here’s the last day off!’ and gave me the closing cup of tea of the vacation.", "이렇게 방학은 더디 흘러갔고, 마침내 머드스톤 양이 \"오늘이 마지막 하루구나!\" 하고 말하며 방학의 마지막 찻잔을 나에게 건네는 아침이 찾아왔다."]], [["I was not sorry to go.", "나는 떠나는 게 서운하지 않았다."], ["I had lapsed into a stupid state; but I was recovering a little and looking forward to Steerforth, albeit Mr. Creakle loomed behind him.", "나는 멍청한 상태로 가라앉아 있었지만, 조금씩 기운을 차리며 스티어포스를 만날 일을 기대하고 있었다. 비록 그 뒤로 크리클 선생이 어른거리기는 했지만."], ["Again Mr. Barkis appeared at the gate, and again Miss Murdstone in her warning voice, said: ‘Clara!’ when my mother bent over me, to bid me farewell.", "다시 바키스 씨가 문 앞에 나타났고, 어머니가 작별 인사를 하려고 내 위로 몸을 굽혔을 때, 다시 머드스톤 양이 경고하는 목소리로 \"클라라!\" 하고 말했다."]], [["I kissed her, and my baby brother, and was very sorry then; but not sorry to go away, for the gulf between us was there, and the parting was there, every day.", "나는 어머니와 갓난 동생에게 입을 맞추었고, 그제야 무척 서글펐다. 하지만 떠나는 게 서운하지는 않았다. 우리 사이에는 날마다 그 깊은 골이 가로놓여 있었고, 날마다 그 이별이 있었으니까."], ["And it is not so much the embrace she gave me, that lives in my mind, though it was as fervent as could be, as what followed the embrace.", "그리고 내 마음속에 살아 있는 것은 어머니가 나를 안아 준 그 포옹이라기보다 — 그것은 더없이 뜨거웠지만 — 그 포옹 뒤에 이어진 광경이다."]], [["I was in the carrier’s cart when I heard her calling to me.", "내가 짐마차에 올라타 있을 때, 어머니가 나를 부르는 소리가 들렸다."], ["I looked out, and she stood at the garden-gate alone, holding her baby up in her arms for me to see.", "내다보니, 어머니가 정원 문간에 홀로 서서, 나에게 보여 주려고 아기를 두 팔로 높이 안아 들고 있었다."], ["It was cold still weather; and not a hair of her head, nor a fold of her dress, was stirred, as she looked intently at me, holding up her child.", "춥고 고요한 날씨였다. 어머니가 아기를 안아 든 채 나를 뚫어지게 바라볼 때, 그 머리카락 한 올도, 옷자락 한 주름도 까딱하지 않았다."]], [["So I lost her. So I saw her afterwards, in my sleep at school--a silent presence near my bed--looking at me with the same intent face--holding up her baby in her arms.", "그렇게 나는 어머니를 잃었다. 그 뒤로 나는 학교에서 잠들 때면 그렇게 어머니를 보았다 — 내 침대 곁의 말 없는 존재로 — 똑같이 뚫어지게 바라보는 그 얼굴로 — 두 팔에 아기를 높이 안아 든 채로."]], [["# CHAPTER 9. I HAVE A MEMORABLE BIRTHDAY", "# CHAPTER 9. I HAVE A MEMORABLE BIRTHDAY"]], [["I PASS over all that happened at school, until the anniversary of my birthday came round in March.", "학교에서 일어난 일은 삼월에 내 생일 기념일이 다시 돌아올 때까지 죄다 건너뛰겠다."], ["Except that Steerforth was more to be admired than ever, I remember nothing.", "스티어포스가 그 어느 때보다 더 우러러볼 만한 사람이었다는 것 말고는 아무것도 기억나지 않는다."], ["He was going away at the end of the half-year, if not sooner, and was more spirited and independent than before in my eyes, and therefore more engaging than before; but beyond this I remember nothing.", "그는 학기 말, 어쩌면 그보다 더 일찍 떠날 예정이었고, 내 눈에는 전보다 더 기개 넘치고 자립심 강한 사람으로 비쳤으며, 그래서 전보다 더 매력적이었다. 하지만 그것 말고는 아무것도 떠오르지 않는다."], ["The great remembrance by which that time is marked in my mind, seems to have swallowed up all lesser recollections, and to exist alone.", "그 무렵을 내 마음에 새겨 놓은 그 거대한 기억이, 자잘한 기억들을 모조리 삼켜 버리고 홀로 남아 버린 듯하다."]], [["It is even difficult for me to believe that there was a gap of full two months between my return to Salem House and the arrival of that birthday.", "세일럼 하우스로 돌아온 때와 그 생일이 찾아온 때 사이에 꼬박 두 달이라는 틈이 있었다는 것조차 나로서는 믿기 어렵다."], ["I can only understand that the fact was so, because I know it must have been so; otherwise I should feel convinced that there was no interval, and that the one occasion trod upon the other’s heels.", "그것이 사실임을 받아들일 수 있는 유일한 까닭은, 틀림없이 그러했음을 내가 알기 때문이다. 그렇지 않았다면, 그 사이에 아무런 간격도 없었으며, 한 사건이 다른 사건의 뒤꿈치를 곧장 밟고 따라온 것이라고 확신했으리라."]], [["How well I recollect the kind of day it was!", "그날이 어떤 날이었는지 나는 어찌나 또렷이 기억하는지! 나는 그곳에 자욱이 깔린 안개 냄새를 맡는다."], ["I smell the fog that hung about the place; I see the hoar frost, ghostly, through it; I feel my rimy hair fall clammy on my cheek; I look along the dim perspective of the schoolroom, with a sputtering candle here and there to light up the foggy morning, and the breath of the boys wreathing and smoking in the raw cold as they blow upon their fingers, and tap their feet upon the floor.", "그 안개 사이로 유령처럼 어른거리는 흰 서리를 본다. 서리 맞은 내 머리카락이 축축하게 뺨에 늘어지는 것을 느낀다. 흐릿하게 길게 뻗은 교실 풍경을 따라 눈길을 던지면, 안개 낀 아침을 밝히려고 여기저기 촛불이 지지직거리고, 사내아이들이 손가락에 입김을 호호 불며 바닥을 발로 톡톡 구를 때 그 입김이 살을 에는 추위 속에 김처럼 서려 피어오른다."], ["It was after breakfast, and we had been summoned in from the playground, when Mr.", "아침 식사가 끝나고, 운동장에서 호출되어 들어왔을 때였다."], ["Sharp entered and said:", "샤프 선생님이 들어와 말했다."]], [["‘David Copperfield is to go into the parlour.’", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드는 응접실로 들어가도록.\""]], [["I expected a hamper from Peggotty, and brightened at the order.", "나는 패거티가 보낸 꾸러미를 기대하며, 그 명령에 얼굴이 환해졌다."], ["Some of the boys about me put in their claim not to be forgotten in the distribution of the good things, as I got out of my seat with great alacrity.", "내가 잽싸게 자리에서 일어나자, 곁에 있던 몇몇 아이들이 그 좋은 것들을 나눌 때 자기들도 빼놓지 말라고 미리 한마디씩 청을 넣었다."]], [["‘Don’t hurry, David,’ said Mr. Sharp.", "\"서두르지 마라, 데이비드.\" 샤프 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘There’s time enough, my boy, don’t hurry.’", "\"시간은 넉넉하니까, 얘야, 서두르지 마라.\""]], [["I might have been surprised by the feeling tone in which he spoke, if I had given it a thought; but I gave it none until afterwards.", "내가 만약 그 말투에 담긴 안쓰러운 어조를 잠깐이라도 곱씹어 보았더라면 어쩌면 놀랐을지도 모른다. 그러나 나는 나중에야 그것을 헤아렸다."], ["I hurried away to the parlour; and there I found Mr.", "나는 응접실로 부랴부랴 달려갔다."], ["Creakle, sitting at his breakfast with the cane and a newspaper before him, and Mrs. Creakle with an opened letter in her hand.", "거기서 크리클 교장 선생님이 회초리와 신문을 앞에 놓고 아침을 들고 있었고, 크리클 부인은 뜯어 본 편지 한 통을 손에 쥐고 있었다."], ["But no hamper.", "하지만 꾸러미는 없었다."]], [["‘David Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드.\""], ["Creakle, leading me to a sofa, and sitting down beside me.", "크리클 부인이 나를 소파로 이끌어 옆에 앉히며 말했다."], ["‘I want to speak to you very particularly.", "\"너에게 아주 긴히 할 말이 있단다."], ["I have something to tell you, my child.’", "너에게 들려줄 게 있어, 얘야.\""]], [["Mr. Creakle, at whom of course I looked, shook his head without looking at me, and stopped up a sigh with a very large piece of buttered toast.", "내가 당연히 눈길을 던졌던 크리클 교장 선생님은, 나를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 고개를 가로젓고는, 버터 바른 토스트를 큼지막하게 한 입 베어 물어 한숨을 틀어막았다."]], [["‘You are too young to know how the world changes every day,’ said Mrs. Creakle, ‘and how the people in it pass away.", "\"넌 세상이 날마다 어떻게 변하는지, 그 안의 사람들이 어떻게 세상을 떠나가는지 알기엔 너무 어리지.\" 크리클 부인이 말했다."], ["But we all have to learn it, David; some of us when we are young, some of us when we are old, some of us at all times of our lives.’", "\"하지만 우리는 모두 그것을 배워야 한단다, 데이비드. 누구는 젊을 때, 누구는 늙어서, 또 누구는 평생 어느 때고 말이야.\""]], [["I looked at her earnestly.", "나는 그분을 골똘히 바라보았다."]], [["‘When you came away from home at the end of the vacation,’ said Mrs.", "\"방학이 끝나고 집에서 떠나올 때,\" 잠시 뜸을 들이고 나서 크리클 부인이 말했다."], ["Creakle, after a pause, ‘were they all well?’ After another pause, ‘Was your mama well?’", "\"다들 잘 지냈니?\" 다시 한 번 뜸을 들이고는, \"네 엄마는 건강하셨고?\""]], [["I trembled without distinctly knowing why, and still looked at her earnestly, making no attempt to answer.", "나는 까닭도 분명히 모른 채 몸을 떨었고, 여전히 그분을 골똘히 바라보기만 할 뿐 대답하려는 시도조차 하지 못했다."]], [["‘Because,’ said she, ‘I grieve to tell you that I hear this morning your mama is very ill.’", "\"왜냐하면,\" 그분이 말했다. \"이런 말을 전하게 되어 가슴이 아프지만, 오늘 아침에 네 엄마가 몹시 편찮으시다는 소식을 들었단다.\""]], [["A mist rose between Mrs. Creakle and me, and her figure seemed to move in it for an instant.", "크리클 부인과 나 사이로 안개가 피어올랐고, 그 속에서 그분의 모습이 한순간 일렁이는 것 같았다."], ["Then I felt the burning tears run down my face, and it was steady again.", "그러다 뜨거운 눈물이 내 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 느꼈고, 그러자 다시 모든 것이 또렷해졌다."]], [["‘She is very dangerously ill,’ she added.", "\"위중하실 정도로 편찮으시단다.\" 그분이 덧붙였다."]], [["I knew all now.", "이제 나는 다 알았다."]], [["‘She is dead.’", "\"돌아가셨어.\""]], [["There was no need to tell me so.", "굳이 그렇게 말해 줄 필요도 없었다."], ["I had already broken out into a desolate cry, and felt an orphan in the wide world.", "나는 이미 처량한 울음을 터뜨렸고, 이 넓은 세상에 홀로 남은 고아가 된 기분이었다."]], [["She was very kind to me.", "그분은 나에게 무척 다정했다."], ["She kept me there all day, and left me alone sometimes; and I cried, and wore myself to sleep, and awoke and cried again.", "그분은 나를 하루 종일 거기 머물게 하면서, 때때로 혼자 있게 내버려 두었다. 나는 울다가 지쳐 잠들었고, 깨어나서는 또 울었다."], ["When I could cry no more, I began to think; and then the oppression on my breast was heaviest, and my grief a dull pain that there was no ease for.", "더 이상 울 수 없게 되자 나는 생각에 잠기기 시작했다. 그러자 가슴을 짓누르는 무게가 가장 무거워졌고, 내 슬픔은 달랠 길 없는 둔한 통증이 되었다."]], [["And yet my thoughts were idle; not intent on the calamity that weighed upon my heart, but idly loitering near it.", "그런데도 내 생각은 부질없이 떠돌기만 했다. 가슴을 짓누르는 재앙에 골똘히 매달리지 못하고, 그 언저리를 하릴없이 어슬렁거렸다."], ["I thought of our house shut up and hushed.", "나는 문이 닫히고 적막에 잠긴 우리 집을 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of the little baby, who, Mrs. Creakle said, had been pining away for some time, and who, they believed, would die too.", "크리클 부인이 한동안 시름시름 앓아 왔다던, 그래서 사람들이 그 아이마저 죽을 거라 여긴다던 그 어린 아기를 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of my father’s grave in the churchyard, by our house, and of my mother lying there beneath the tree I knew so well.", "우리 집 곁 교회 묘지에 있는 아버지의 무덤을, 그리고 내가 그토록 잘 아는 그 나무 아래 누워 계실 어머니를 떠올렸다."], ["I stood upon a chair when I was left alone, and looked into the glass to see how red my eyes were, and how sorrowful my face.", "혼자 남겨졌을 때 나는 의자 위에 올라가 거울을 들여다보며 내 눈이 얼마나 빨갛게 부었는지, 내 얼굴이 얼마나 슬퍼 보이는지를 살폈다."], ["I considered, after some hours were gone, if my tears were really hard to flow now, as they seemed to be, what, in connexion with my loss, it would affect me most to think of when I drew near home--for I was going home to the funeral.", "몇 시간이 흐른 뒤, 나는 곰곰이 생각해 보았다. 지금 보이는 것처럼 정말로 눈물이 잘 나오지 않는다면, 집에 가까이 다가갈 때 내가 잃은 것과 관련해 무엇을 떠올리는 것이 나를 가장 마음 아프게 할까 하고 말이다 — 나는 장례식 때문에 집으로 가는 길이었으니까."], ["I am sensible of having felt that a dignity attached to me among the rest of the boys, and that I was important in my affliction.", "나는 다른 아이들 사이에서 나에게 어떤 위엄이 깃든 듯한 느낌을, 그리고 내가 그 슬픔 덕분에 중요한 사람이 된 듯한 느낌을 받았던 것을 분명히 기억한다."]], [["If ever child were stricken with sincere grief, I was.", "세상에 진실로 슬픔에 짓눌린 아이가 있다면 그게 바로 나였다."], ["But I remember that this importance was a kind of satisfaction to me, when I walked in the playground that afternoon while the boys were in school.", "하지만 그날 오후 다른 아이들이 수업을 받는 동안 내가 운동장을 거닐 때, 그 중요한 사람이 된 느낌이 일종의 만족감으로 다가왔던 것을 나는 기억한다."], ["When I saw them glancing at me out of the windows, as they went up to their classes, I felt distinguished, and looked more melancholy, and walked slower.", "아이들이 교실로 올라가며 창문 너머로 나를 흘끔거리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 내가 남다른 사람이 된 듯한 기분이 들어 더욱 우울한 표정을 짓고 더 느릿느릿 걸었다."], ["When school was over, and they came out and spoke to me, I felt it rather good in myself not to be proud to any of them, and to take exactly the same notice of them all, as before.", "수업이 끝나 아이들이 나와 나에게 말을 걸었을 때, 나는 누구에게도 으스대지 않고 예전과 똑같이 모두를 한결같이 대하는 내 모습이 제법 갸륵하게 여겨졌다."]], [["I was to go home next night; not by the mail, but by the heavy night-coach, which was called the Farmer, and was principally used by country-people travelling short intermediate distances upon the road.", "나는 이튿날 밤에 집으로 갈 예정이었다. 우편 마차가 아니라, '농부'라고 불리는 묵직한 야간 합승 마차였는데, 주로 길에서 가까운 중간 거리를 오가는 시골 사람들이 이용하는 마차였다."], ["We had no story-telling that evening, and Traddles insisted on lending me his pillow.", "그날 저녁에는 이야기 들려주기도 없었고, 트래들스는 자기 베개를 나에게 빌려주겠다고 우겼다."], ["I don’t know what good he thought it would do me, for I had one of my own: but it was all he had to lend, poor fellow, except a sheet of letter-paper full of skeletons; and that he gave me at parting, as a soother of my sorrows and a contribution to my peace of mind.", "나도 내 베개가 있었으니 그게 무슨 도움이 될 거라 여겼는지 모르겠지만, 가엾은 녀석에게는 그것밖에 빌려줄 게 없었다 — 해골이 잔뜩 그려진 편지지 한 장 말고는. 그것마저 그는 헤어질 때 나에게 건네주었는데, 내 슬픔을 달래 주고 내 마음에 평안을 보태 주려는 뜻에서였다."]], [["I left Salem House upon the morrow afternoon.", "나는 이튿날 오후 세일럼 하우스를 떠났다."], ["I little thought then that I left it, never to return. We travelled very slowly all night, and did not get into Yarmouth before nine or ten o’clock in the morning.", "그때 나는 그곳을 영영 다시 돌아오지 못할 채로 떠나는 줄은 까맣게 몰랐다. 우리는 밤새 아주 느릿느릿 달렸고, 야머스에 들어선 것은 아침 아홉 시인가 열 시가 되어서였다."], ["I looked out for Mr. Barkis, but he was not there; and instead of him a fat, short-winded, merry-looking, little old man in black, with rusty little bunches of ribbons at the knees of his breeches, black stockings, and a broad-brimmed hat, came puffing up to the coach window, and said:", "나는 바키스 씨를 찾아 두리번거렸지만 그는 없었다. 그 대신 까만 옷을 입은, 뚱뚱하고 숨이 가쁘며 쾌활해 보이는 자그마한 노인이 — 반바지 무릎께에 녹슨 듯한 리본 다발을 자그맣게 달고, 검은 양말에 챙 넓은 모자를 쓰고 — 헐떡거리며 마차 창문께로 다가와 말했다."]], [["‘Master Copperfield?’", "\"코퍼필드 도련님?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"네, 그런데요.\""]], [["‘Will you come with me, young sir, if you please,’ he said, opening the door, ‘and I shall have the pleasure of taking you home.’", "\"괜찮으시다면 저와 함께 가시지요, 도련님.\" 그가 문을 열며 말했다. \"제가 도련님을 집까지 모셔다드리는 기쁨을 누리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["I put my hand in his, wondering who he was, and we walked away to a shop in a narrow street, on which was written OMER, DRAPER, TAILOR, HABERDASHER, FUNERAL FURNISHER, &c.", "나는 이 사람이 누구인가 궁금해하며 그의 손에 내 손을 맡겼고, 우리는 좁은 거리에 있는 어느 가게로 걸어갔다. 가게에는 '오머. 포목상, 양복점, 잡화상, 장의용품 일체 등'이라고 쓰여 있었다."], ["It was a close and stifling little shop; full of all sorts of clothing, made and unmade, including one window full of beaver-hats and bonnets.", "답답하고 숨 막히는 자그마한 가게였는데, 다 지은 것과 짓다 만 것을 가리지 않고 온갖 옷가지가 가득했고, 진열창 하나는 비버 가죽 모자와 보닛으로 빼곡했다."], ["We went into a little back-parlour behind the shop, where we found three young women at work on a quantity of black materials, which were heaped upon the table, and little bits and cuttings of which were littered all over the floor.", "우리는 가게 뒤편의 작은 거실로 들어갔다. 거기서 젊은 여자 셋이 검은 천 무더기를 가지고 일하고 있었는데, 천은 탁자 위에 산더미처럼 쌓여 있었고, 자투리와 잘린 조각들이 온 바닥에 어지러이 널려 있었다."], ["There was a good fire in the room, and a breathless smell of warm black crape--I did not know what the smell was then, but I know now.", "방에는 불이 활활 타오르고 있었고, 따뜻한 검은 크레이프 천에서 풍기는 숨 막히는 냄새가 가득했다 — 그때는 그게 무슨 냄새인지 몰랐지만 지금은 안다."]], [["The three young women, who appeared to be very industrious and comfortable, raised their heads to look at me, and then went on with their work.", "매우 부지런하고 편안해 보이는 그 젊은 여자 셋은 고개를 들어 나를 한번 보고는, 다시 하던 일을 이어 갔다."], ["Stitch, stitch, stitch.", "바느질, 바느질, 바느질."], ["At the same time there came from a workshop across a little yard outside the window, a regular sound of hammering that kept a kind of tune: RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, without any variation.", "그러는 동안 창밖 작은 마당 건너편 작업장에서, 가락을 맞추는 듯한 규칙적인 망치질 소리가 들려왔다. 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 한 치의 변화도 없이."]], [["‘Well,’ said my conductor to one of the three young women.", "\"그래.\" 나를 데려온 사람이 젊은 여자 셋 가운데 하나에게 말했다."], ["‘How do you get on, Minnie?’", "\"어떻게 돼 가니, 미니?\""]], [["‘We shall be ready by the trying-on time,’ she replied gaily, without looking up.", "\"가봉할 시간까지는 다 될 거예요.\" 그 여자가 고개도 들지 않고 명랑하게 대답했다."], ["‘Don’t you be afraid, father.’", "\"걱정 마세요, 아버지.\""]], [["Mr. Omer took off his broad-brimmed hat, and sat down and panted.", "오머 씨는 챙 넓은 모자를 벗고 자리에 앉아 헐떡였다."], ["He was so fat that he was obliged to pant some time before he could say:", "어찌나 뚱뚱한지, 한참을 헐떡인 뒤에야 비로소 말을 할 수 있었다."]], [["‘That’s right.’", "\"그래야지.\""]], [["‘Father!’ said Minnie, playfully.", "\"아버지!\" 미니가 장난스럽게 말했다."], ["‘What a porpoise you do grow!’", "\"어쩜 그렇게 돌고래처럼 점점 불어나세요!\""]], [["‘Well, I don’t know how it is, my dear,’ he replied, considering about it.", "\"글쎄다, 어쩌다 이렇게 됐는지 모르겠구나, 얘야.\" 그가 곰곰 따져 보며 대답했다."], ["‘I am rather so.’", "\"내가 영 이렇구나.\""]], [["‘You are such a comfortable man, you see,’ said Minnie.", "\"아버진 워낙 마음이 느긋하시잖아요.\" 미니가 말했다."], ["‘You take things so easy.’", "\"뭐든 그렇게 편하게 받아들이시니까요.\""]], [["‘No use taking ‘em otherwise, my dear,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"달리 받아들여 봐야 소용없잖니, 얘야.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, indeed,’ returned his daughter.", "\"그럼요, 정말 그래요.\" 딸이 받아쳤다."], ["‘We are all pretty gay here, thank Heaven!", "\"여긴 다들 꽤나 명랑하답니다, 하느님 감사합니다!"], ["Ain’t we, father?’", "안 그래요, 아버지?\""]], [["‘I hope so, my dear,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"그러길 바란다, 얘야.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘As I have got my breath now, I think I’ll measure this young scholar.", "\"이제 숨도 좀 돌렸으니, 이 어린 학생 치수를 재 볼까 싶구나."], ["Would you walk into the shop, Master Copperfield?’", "가게로 들어와 주시겠어요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\""]], [["I preceded Mr.", "나는 그분의 청에 따라 오머 씨 앞서서 들어갔다."], ["Omer, in compliance with his request; and after showing me a roll of cloth which he said was extra super, and too good mourning for anything short of parents, he took my various dimensions, and put them down in a book.", "그분은 나에게 천 한 두루마리를 보여 주며 이건 특상품인데, 부모상이 아니고서는 쓰기 아까울 만큼 좋은 상복감이라고 했다. 그러고는 내 몸의 이런저런 치수를 재어 장부에 적었다."], ["While he was recording them he called my attention to his stock in trade, and to certain fashions which he said had ‘just come up’, and to certain other fashions which he said had ‘just gone out’.", "치수를 적어 가면서 그분은 자기 가게 물건들에 내 눈길을 끌어, '막 유행하기 시작한' 어떤 방식과 '막 유행이 지나간' 또 다른 어떤 방식들을 가리켜 보였다."]], [["‘And by that sort of thing we very often lose a little mint of money,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"그런 것 때문에 우리는 종종 적잖은 돈을 손해 보지요.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘But fashions are like human beings.", "\"하지만 유행이란 사람과 같답니다."], ["They come in, nobody knows when, why, or how; and they go out, nobody knows when, why, or how.", "언제, 왜, 어떻게 들어오는지 아무도 모르고, 또 언제, 왜, 어떻게 사라지는지도 아무도 모르지요."], ["Everything is like life, in my opinion, if you look at it in that point of view.’", "그런 관점에서 보면, 제 생각엔 세상 모든 게 다 삶과 같답니다.\""]], [["I was too sorrowful to discuss the question, which would possibly have been beyond me under any circumstances; and Mr.", "나는 그 문제를 두고 따져 볼 만큼 마음에 여유가 없었고, 어차피 어떤 처지에서든 나로서는 헤아리기 벅찬 이야기였을 것이다."], ["Omer took me back into the parlour, breathing with some difficulty on the way.", "오머 씨는 나를 다시 거실로 데려갔는데, 가는 길에 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬었다."]], [["He then called down a little break-neck range of steps behind a door: ‘Bring up that tea and bread-and-butter!’ which, after some time, during which I sat looking about me and thinking, and listening to the stitching in the room and the tune that was being hammered across the yard, appeared on a tray, and turned out to be for me.", "그러더니 그분은 문 뒤편의 가파른 계단 아래로 대고 소리쳤다. \"차하고 버터 바른 빵 좀 올려 보내!\" 한참이 지나는 동안 나는 앉아서 둘레를 둘러보고 이런저런 생각에 잠긴 채, 방 안의 바느질 소리와 마당 건너편에서 망치로 두드리는 가락 소리에 귀를 기울였는데, 이윽고 그것이 쟁반에 담겨 나타났고, 알고 보니 나를 위한 것이었다."]], [["‘I have been acquainted with you,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 자네를 알고 지낸 지가 말이지,\""], ["Omer, after watching me for some minutes, during which I had not made much impression on the breakfast, for the black things destroyed my appetite, ‘I have been acquainted with you a long time, my young friend.’", "오머 씨가 몇 분간 나를 지켜본 뒤에 말했다. 그동안 나는 아침을 거의 입에 대지 못했는데, 검은 천 때문에 입맛이 싹 가셨기 때문이다. \"내가 자네를 알고 지낸 지가 아주 오래됐다네, 젊은 친구.\""]], [["‘Have you, sir?’", "\"그러셨어요?\""]], [["‘All your life,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"자네가 태어난 뒤로 줄곧이지.\""], ["‘I may say before it.", "오머 씨가 말했다."], ["I knew your father before you.", "\"태어나기 전부터라고도 할 수 있겠군."], ["He was five foot nine and a half, and he lays in five-and-twen-ty foot of ground.’", "난 자네보다 먼저 자네 아버지를 알았으니까. 그분은 키가 다섯 자 아홉 치 반이셨는데, 지금은 스—물다섯 자 땅속에 누워 계시지.\""]], [["‘RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat,’ across the yard.", "\"탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁.\" 마당 건너편에서."]], [["‘He lays in five and twen-ty foot of ground, if he lays in a fraction,’ said Mr.", "\"한 치도 어김없이 스—물다섯 자 땅속에 누워 계신다네.\" 오머 씨가 정답게 말했다."], ["Omer, pleasantly. ‘It was either his request or her direction, I forget which.’", "\"그분이 부탁하신 건지, 아니면 부인이 지시하신 건지, 어느 쪽인지는 잊어버렸군.\""]], [["‘Do you know how my little brother is, sir?’ I inquired.", "\"제 어린 동생은 어떤지 아세요, 아저씨?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["Mr. Omer shook his head.", "오머 씨는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat, RAT--tat-tat.’", "\"탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁, 탁—탁탁.\""]], [["‘He is in his mother’s arms,’ said he.", "\"그 아이는 제 어머니 품에 안겨 있다네.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, poor little fellow! Is he dead?’", "\"아, 가엾은 것! 죽은 거예요?\""]], [["‘Don’t mind it more than you can help,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Yes.", "\"되도록 너무 마음 쓰지 말게.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"그래."], ["The baby’s dead.’", "아기는 죽었어.\""]], [["My wounds broke out afresh at this intelligence.", "이 소식에 내 상처가 다시 터져 벌어졌다."], ["I left the scarcely-tasted breakfast, and went and rested my head on another table, in a corner of the little room, which Minnie hastily cleared, lest I should spot the mourning that was lying there with my tears.", "나는 거의 입도 대지 못한 아침을 그대로 두고, 작은 방 한쪽 구석의 다른 탁자로 가서 그 위에 머리를 기댔다. 미니는 거기 놓인 상복에 내 눈물이 얼룩질까 봐 황급히 치워 주었다."], ["She was a pretty, good-natured girl, and put my hair away from my eyes with a soft, kind touch; but she was very cheerful at having nearly finished her work and being in good time, and was so different from me!", "그 여자는 예쁘고 마음씨 고운 처녀여서, 부드럽고 다정한 손길로 내 눈을 가린 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겨 주었다. 하지만 일을 거의 끝내고 제때에 맞추게 되었다며 무척 들떠 있어서, 나와는 너무도 딴판이었다!"]], [["Presently the tune left off, and a good-looking young fellow came across the yard into the room.", "이윽고 가락 소리가 멎더니, 잘생긴 젊은 사내가 마당을 가로질러 방으로 들어왔다."], ["He had a hammer in his hand, and his mouth was full of little nails, which he was obliged to take out before he could speak.", "손에는 망치를 들었고, 입안 가득 작은 못을 물고 있어서 말을 하려면 먼저 그것들을 빼내야 했다."]], [["‘Well, Joram!’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"그래, 조람!\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘How do you get on?’", "\"어떻게 돼 가나?\""]], [["‘All right,’ said Joram.", "\"다 됐습니다.\" 조람이 말했다."], ["‘Done, sir.’", "\"끝냈어요, 사장님.\""]], [["Minnie coloured a little, and the other two girls smiled at one another.", "미니가 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔고, 다른 두 처녀가 서로를 보며 빙긋 웃었다."]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["you were at it by candle-light last night, when I was at the club, then?", "그럼 내가 클럽에 가 있던 어젯밤에 자네 촛불 켜 놓고 그걸 했단 말인가? 그랬어?\""], ["Were you?’ said Mr. Omer, shutting up one eye.", "오머 씨가 한쪽 눈을 찡긋 감으며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Joram.", "\"네.\" 조람이 말했다."], ["‘As you said we could make a little trip of it, and go over together, if it was done, Minnie and me--and you.’", "\"일이 끝나면 우리끼리 짧은 나들이라도 해서, 다 같이 다녀와도 좋다고 하셨잖아요. 미니랑 저랑 — 그리고 사장님까지요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I thought you were going to leave me out altogether,’ said Mr. Omer, laughing till he coughed.", "난 자네가 날 아예 빼놓을 줄 알았지.\" 오머 씨가 기침이 날 만큼 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘--As you was so good as to say that,’ resumed the young man, ‘why I turned to with a will, you see.", "\"— 사장님이 그렇게 좋은 말씀을 해 주셨으니,\" 젊은 사내가 말을 이었다. \"그래서 제가 신이 나서 달려든 거지요, 아시겠어요?"], ["Will you give me your opinion of it?’", "한번 보시고 어떤지 말씀해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘I will,’ said Mr. Omer, rising.", "\"그러지.\" 오머 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"얘야.\""], ["‘My dear’; and he stopped and turned to me: ‘would you like to see your--’", "그러다 그분이 말을 멈추고 나를 돌아보았다. \"혹시 네 — 보고 싶니—\""]], [["‘No, father,’ Minnie interposed.", "\"안 돼요, 아버지.\" 미니가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘I thought it might be agreeable, my dear,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"마음이 놓일까 싶어서 그랬다, 얘야.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘But perhaps you’re right.’", "\"하지만 네 말이 맞을지도 모르겠구나.\""]], [["I can’t say how I knew it was my dear, dear mother’s coffin that they went to look at.", "그들이 보러 간 것이 내 사랑하는, 그리운 어머니의 관이라는 걸 내가 어떻게 알았는지는 말로 다 못 하겠다."], ["I had never heard one making; I had never seen one that I know of.--but it came into my mind what the noise was, while it was going on; and when the young man entered, I am sure I knew what he had been doing.", "나는 관 만드는 소리를 들어 본 적도 없었고, 내가 아는 한 관을 본 적도 없었다. 하지만 그 소리가 나는 동안, 그게 무슨 소리인지 문득 마음에 떠올랐다. 그리고 그 젊은 사내가 들어왔을 때, 나는 그가 무슨 일을 하고 있었는지 분명히 알아챘다."]], [["The work being now finished, the two girls, whose names I had not heard, brushed the shreds and threads from their dresses, and went into the shop to put that to rights, and wait for customers.", "이제 일이 끝나자, 이름은 듣지 못한 두 처녀가 옷에 묻은 천 부스러기와 실밥을 털어 내고, 가게로 나가 그쪽을 정돈하고 손님을 기다렸다."], ["Minnie stayed behind to fold up what they had made, and pack it in two baskets.", "미니는 뒤에 남아 그들이 지은 것을 개켜서 바구니 두 개에 챙겨 넣었다."], ["This she did upon her knees, humming a lively little tune the while.", "무릎을 꿇고 그 일을 하면서, 흥겨운 짧은 가락을 흥얼거렸다."], ["Joram, who I had no doubt was her lover, came in and stole a kiss from her while she was busy (he didn’t appear to mind me, at all), and said her father was gone for the chaise, and he must make haste and get himself ready.", "분명 그녀의 연인일 조람이 들어와, 그녀가 바삐 일하는 틈에 살짝 입을 맞추고는 (나는 전혀 안중에도 없는 듯했다), 아버지가 마차를 가지러 가셨으니 어서 채비를 해야겠다고 말했다."], ["Then he went out again; and then she put her thimble and scissors in her pocket, and stuck a needle threaded with black thread neatly in the bosom of her gown, and put on her outer clothing smartly, at a little glass behind the door, in which I saw the reflection of her pleased face.", "그러고는 다시 나갔다. 그러자 미니는 골무와 가위를 주머니에 넣고, 검은 실을 꿴 바늘을 옷 가슴팍에 가지런히 꽂은 다음, 문 뒤의 작은 거울 앞에서 잽싸게 겉옷을 걸쳤는데, 그 거울 속에 비친 그녀의 흡족한 얼굴이 나에게도 보였다."]], [["All this I observed, sitting at the table in the corner with my head leaning on my hand, and my thoughts running on very different things.", "이 모든 것을 나는 구석 탁자에 앉아 손에 머리를 괴고 지켜보았으며, 내 생각은 전혀 다른 데로 치닫고 있었다."], ["The chaise soon came round to the front of the shop, and the baskets being put in first, I was put in next, and those three followed.", "곧 마차가 가게 앞으로 돌아왔고, 바구니들이 먼저 실리고, 그다음에 내가 태워졌으며, 그 세 사람이 뒤따라 올랐다."], ["I remember it as a kind of half chaise-cart, half pianoforte-van, painted of a sombre colour, and drawn by a black horse with a long tail.", "내 기억으로 그것은 반쯤은 짐마차, 반쯤은 피아노 운반차 같은 것으로, 침침한 빛깔로 칠해져 있었고, 꼬리가 긴 검은 말이 끌었다."], ["There was plenty of room for us all.", "우리 모두가 타고도 자리가 넉넉했다."]], [["I do not think I have ever experienced so strange a feeling in my life (I am wiser now, perhaps) as that of being with them, remembering how they had been employed, and seeing them enjoy the ride.", "내 평생 그토록 기묘한 느낌을 겪어 본 적이 있나 싶다 (이제는 어쩌면 좀 더 철이 들었지만). 그들이 무슨 일을 했었는지를 떠올리면서, 그들과 함께 있으며 그들이 마차 나들이를 즐기는 모습을 바라보는 느낌 말이다."], ["I was not angry with them; I was more afraid of them, as if I were cast away among creatures with whom I had no community of nature.", "나는 그들에게 화가 나지는 않았다. 오히려 그들이 무서웠는데, 마치 나와는 본성이 전혀 다른 생명체들 가운데 내던져진 것만 같았다."], ["They were very cheerful.", "그들은 무척 명랑했다."], ["The old man sat in front to drive, and the two young people sat behind him, and whenever he spoke to them leaned forward, the one on one side of his chubby face and the other on the other, and made a great deal of him.", "노인은 앞자리에 앉아 마차를 몰았고, 두 젊은이는 그 뒤에 앉아, 그가 말을 걸 때마다 통통한 그의 얼굴 양쪽으로 하나는 이쪽에서, 하나는 저쪽에서 몸을 기울이며 그를 한껏 떠받들었다."], ["They would have talked to me too, but I held back, and moped in my corner; scared by their love-making and hilarity, though it was far from boisterous, and almost wondering that no judgement came upon them for their hardness of heart.", "그들은 나에게도 말을 걸려 했지만, 나는 뒤로 물러나 구석에서 시무룩이 웅크렸다. 그들의 사랑놀음과 들뜬 웃음이 — 결코 요란스럽지는 않았으나 — 나를 겁먹게 했고, 그토록 모진 마음에도 아무런 천벌이 내리지 않는 것이 거의 의아하게 느껴졌다."]], [["So, when they stopped to bait the horse, and ate and drank and enjoyed themselves, I could touch nothing that they touched, but kept my fast unbroken.", "그래서 그들이 말에게 먹이를 주려고 멈춰 서서 먹고 마시며 흥겹게 즐길 때에도, 나는 그들이 손댄 것에는 아무것도 손대지 못하고 단식을 깨뜨리지 않았다."], ["So, when we reached home, I dropped out of the chaise behind, as quickly as possible, that I might not be in their company before those solemn windows, looking blindly on me like closed eyes once bright.", "그래서 집에 다다랐을 때에도, 나는 최대한 잽싸게 마차 뒤로 내려서, 한때 빛나던 두 눈이 감긴 것처럼 멀거니 나를 내려다보는 그 엄숙한 창문들 앞에서 그들과 함께 있지 않으려 했다."], ["And oh, how little need I had had to think what would move me to tears when I came back--seeing the window of my mother’s room, and next it that which, in the better time, was mine!", "아, 그리고 돌아왔을 때 무엇이 나를 울리게 할까 따위를 굳이 생각할 필요가 어디 있었던가 — 어머니 방의 창문을, 그리고 그 옆에, 좋았던 시절 내 방이었던 창문을 보게 되었으니!"]], [["I was in Peggotty’s arms before I got to the door, and she took me into the house.", "나는 문에 채 닿기도 전에 패거티의 품에 안겼고, 패거티는 나를 집 안으로 데려갔다."], ["Her grief burst out when she first saw me; but she controlled it soon, and spoke in whispers, and walked softly, as if the dead could be disturbed.", "그녀는 나를 처음 보았을 때 슬픔이 북받쳐 터져 나왔지만, 곧 그것을 억누르고 속삭이듯 말하며, 마치 죽은 이를 깨우기라도 할까 봐 살금살금 걸었다."], ["She had not been in bed, I found, for a long time.", "알고 보니 그녀는 오랫동안 잠자리에 들지 못한 채였다."], ["She sat up at night still, and watched.", "밤이면 여전히 자지 않고 앉아 지켰다."], ["As long as her poor dear pretty was above the ground, she said, she would never desert her.", "가엾고 사랑스러운 자기 아씨가 땅 위에 머무는 한, 결코 곁을 떠나지 않겠노라고 그녀는 말했다."]], [["Mr. Murdstone took no heed of me when I went into the parlour where he was, but sat by the fireside, weeping silently, and pondering in his elbow-chair.", "머드스톤 씨는 내가 그가 있는 거실로 들어갔을 때 나에게 눈길 한 번 주지 않고, 난롯가에 앉아 소리 없이 눈물을 흘리며 안락의자에 잠겨 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Miss Murdstone, who was busy at her writing-desk, which was covered with letters and papers, gave me her cold finger-nails, and asked me, in an iron whisper, if I had been measured for my mourning.", "편지와 서류로 뒤덮인 책상에서 부지런히 일하고 있던 머드스톤 양은 차가운 손톱을 나에게 내밀더니, 쇳소리 같은 속삭임으로 상복 치수를 다 쟀느냐고 물었다."]], [["I said: ‘Yes.’", "나는 대답했다. \"네.\""]], [["‘And your shirts,’ said Miss Murdstone; ‘have you brought ‘em home?’", "\"셔츠는,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"집으로 가져왔니?\""]], [["‘Yes, ma’am. I have brought home all my clothes.’", "\"네, 아주머니. 제 옷은 전부 다 가져왔어요.\""]], [["This was all the consolation that her firmness administered to me.", "이것이 그녀의 그 야무진 태도가 나에게 베푼 위로의 전부였다."], ["I do not doubt that she had a choice pleasure in exhibiting what she called her self-command, and her firmness, and her strength of mind, and her common sense, and the whole diabolical catalogue of her unamiable qualities, on such an occasion.", "그녀가 이런 상황에서 자신이 자제력이라 부르는 것, 그리고 야무짐과 굳은 의지, 분별력, 또 그 밖에 정나미 떨어지는 온갖 악마 같은 자질을 늘어놓아 보이는 데서 각별한 즐거움을 느꼈으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["She was particularly proud of her turn for business; and she showed it now in reducing everything to pen and ink, and being moved by nothing.", "그녀는 특히 자기가 일을 야무지게 처리하는 솜씨를 자랑스러워했고, 지금도 모든 것을 펜과 잉크로 처리하며 무슨 일에도 흔들리지 않는 모습으로 그것을 뽐냈다."], ["All the rest of that day, and from morning to night afterwards, she sat at that desk, scratching composedly with a hard pen, speaking in the same imperturbable whisper to everybody; never relaxing a muscle of her face, or softening a tone of her voice, or appearing with an atom of her dress astray.", "그날 내내, 그리고 그 후로도 아침부터 밤까지, 그녀는 그 책상에 앉아 딱딱한 펜으로 차분하게 사각사각 써 댔고, 누구에게나 한결같이 흔들림 없는 속삭임으로 말했으며, 얼굴 근육 하나 풀어지지 않고, 목소리 한 가락 누그러지지 않으며, 옷매무새 한 올 흐트러진 채로 나타나는 법이 없었다."]], [["Her brother took a book sometimes, but never read it that I saw.", "그녀의 오빠는 가끔 책을 집어 들었지만, 내가 본 한 그것을 읽은 적은 없었다."], ["He would open it and look at it as if he were reading, but would remain for a whole hour without turning the leaf, and then put it down and walk to and fro in the room.", "책을 펴서 마치 읽는 것처럼 들여다보면서도, 한 시간 내내 책장 한 장 넘기지 않은 채 있다가, 이내 책을 내려놓고 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["I used to sit with folded hands watching him, and counting his footsteps, hour after hour. He very seldom spoke to her, and never to me.", "나는 두 손을 모은 채 앉아, 몇 시간이고 그를 지켜보며 그의 발걸음을 헤아리곤 했다. 그는 그녀에게 좀처럼 말을 걸지 않았고, 나에게는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["He seemed to be the only restless thing, except the clocks, in the whole motionless house.", "그는 이 미동조차 없는 집 안에서, 시계들 말고는 유일하게 안절부절못하는 존재인 듯했다."]], [["In these days before the funeral, I saw but little of Peggotty, except that, in passing up or down stairs, I always found her close to the room where my mother and her baby lay, and except that she came to me every night, and sat by my bed’s head while I went to sleep.", "장례식 전의 그 며칠 동안, 나는 패거티를 거의 보지 못했다. 다만 계단을 오르내릴 때면 언제나 어머니와 아기가 누워 있는 방 가까이에 그녀가 있는 것을 보았고, 또 밤마다 그녀가 내게 와서 내가 잠들 때까지 침대 머리맡에 앉아 있었던 것은 예외였다."], ["A day or two before the burial--I think it was a day or two before, but I am conscious of confusion in my mind about that heavy time, with nothing to mark its progress--she took me into the room.", "매장하기 하루나 이틀 전에 — 하루나 이틀 전이었던 것 같은데, 그 무거운 시간에 대해서는 나아간 자취를 짚을 만한 표시가 아무것도 없어서 내 기억이 뒤죽박죽이라는 걸 안다 — 그녀가 나를 그 방으로 데려갔다."], ["I only recollect that underneath some white covering on the bed, with a beautiful cleanliness and freshness all around it, there seemed to me to lie embodied the solemn stillness that was in the house; and that when she would have turned the cover gently back, I cried: ‘Oh no!", "내가 기억하는 것이라곤, 침대 위 어떤 흰 덮개 아래, 둘레가 온통 아름답게 정갈하고 산뜻한 가운데, 이 집을 감돌던 그 엄숙한 정적이 형체를 입고 누워 있는 듯했다는 것뿐이다. 그리고 그녀가 그 덮개를 살며시 젖히려 했을 때, 나는 \"아, 안 돼요! 아, 안 돼요!\""], ["oh no!’ and held her hand.", "하고 외치며 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["If the funeral had been yesterday, I could not recollect it better.", "장례식이 어제 일이었다 해도 이보다 더 또렷이 기억할 수는 없을 것이다."], ["The very air of the best parlour, when I went in at the door, the bright condition of the fire, the shining of the wine in the decanters, the patterns of the glasses and plates, the faint sweet smell of cake, the odour of Miss Murdstone’s dress, and our black clothes. Mr. Chillip is in the room, and comes to speak to me.", "제일 좋은 거실의 그 공기, 문으로 들어설 때의 그 환한 난롯불, 디캔터 속 포도주의 반짝임, 잔과 접시의 무늬, 어렴풋이 달콤한 케이크 냄새, 머드스톤 양의 옷에서 풍기던 냄새, 그리고 우리의 검은 옷. 칠립 의사 선생님이 방 안에 있다가 나에게 말을 걸러 온다."]], [["‘And how is Master David?’ he says, kindly.", "\"데이비드 도련님은 좀 어떤가요?\" 그가 다정하게 묻는다."]], [["I cannot tell him very well.", "나는 그에게 뭐라 제대로 대답하지 못한다."], ["I give him my hand, which he holds in his.", "나는 그에게 손을 내밀고, 그는 그 손을 제 손에 쥐어 준다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ says Mr.", "\"이런, 저런!\""], ["Chillip, meekly smiling, with something shining in his eye.", "칠립 의사 선생님이 눈에 무언가 반짝이는 것을 띤 채 온화하게 미소 지으며 말한다."], ["‘Our little friends grow up around us.", "\"우리 어린 친구들이 우리 곁에서 무럭무럭 자라나는군요."], ["They grow out of our knowledge, ma’am?’ This is to Miss Murdstone, who makes no reply.", "어느새 우리가 못 알아볼 만큼 커 버리지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 이건 머드스톤 양에게 한 말인데, 그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않는다."]], [["‘There is a great improvement here, ma’am?’ says Mr. Chillip.", "\"이 댁 도련님이 부쩍 자랐지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 칠립 의사 선생님이 말한다."]], [["Miss Murdstone merely answers with a frown and a formal bend: Mr.", "머드스톤 양은 그저 눈살을 찌푸리고 형식적으로 고개를 까딱하는 것으로 답할 뿐이다."], ["Chillip, discomfited, goes into a corner, keeping me with him, and opens his mouth no more.", "무안해진 칠립 의사 선생님은 나를 데리고 한쪽 구석으로 가더니, 더는 입을 열지 않는다."]], [["I remark this, because I remark everything that happens, not because I care about myself, or have done since I came home.", "내가 이런 것을 일일이 짚어 두는 까닭은, 내가 일어나는 모든 일을 낱낱이 짚기 때문이지, 나 자신을 챙겨서가 아니다."], ["And now the bell begins to sound, and Mr.", "집에 돌아온 뒤로 나는 나 자신 따위를 챙긴 적이 없었다."], ["Omer and another come to make us ready. As Peggotty was wont to tell me, long ago, the followers of my father to the same grave were made ready in the same room.", "이제 종소리가 울리기 시작하고, 오머 씨와 또 한 사람이 우리에게 채비를 갖춰 주러 온다. 오래전 패거티가 곧잘 들려주었듯이, 아버지를 같은 무덤으로 따라간 사람들도 이 같은 방에서 채비를 갖췄었다."]], [["There are Mr. Murdstone, our neighbour Mr.", "머드스톤 씨와 우리 이웃 그레이퍼 씨, 칠립 의사 선생님, 그리고 내가 있다."], ["Grayper, Mr. Chillip, and I.", "우리가 문을 나서자, 운구하는 사람들과 그들이 짊어진 짐이 정원에 있다."], ["When we go out to the door, the Bearers and their load are in the garden; and they move before us down the path, and past the elms, and through the gate, and into the churchyard, where I have so often heard the birds sing on a summer morning.", "그들은 우리 앞서서 오솔길을 따라, 느릅나무들을 지나, 문을 통과해, 교회 묘지로 들어선다. 여름 아침이면 새들이 노래하는 소리를 내가 그토록 자주 듣곤 했던 곳이다."]], [["We stand around the grave.", "우리는 무덤 둘레에 둘러선다."], ["The day seems different to me from every other day, and the light not of the same colour--of a sadder colour.", "그날은 다른 모든 날과 달라 보이고, 햇빛도 여느 때와 같은 빛깔이 아니다 — 더 슬픈 빛깔이다. 이제 엄숙한 정적이 감도는데, 흙 속에 잠든 것과 함께 우리가 집에서 가져온 정적이다. 우리가 맨머리로 서 있는 동안, 나는 탁 트인 바깥에서 아득히 들려오면서도 또렷하고 분명한 목사님의 목소리를 듣는다."], ["Now there is a solemn hush, which we have brought from home with what is resting in the mould; and while we stand bareheaded, I hear the voice of the clergyman, sounding remote in the open air, and yet distinct and plain, saying: ‘I am the Resurrection and the Life, saith the Lord!’ Then I hear sobs; and, standing apart among the lookers-on, I see that good and faithful servant, whom of all the people upon earth I love the best, and unto whom my childish heart is certain that the Lord will one day say: ‘Well done.’", "\"나는 부활이요 생명이니, 주께서 이르시되!\" 이윽고 흐느낌이 들리고, 지켜보는 사람들 가운데 외따로 떨어져 서서, 나는 이 땅 위의 누구보다도 내가 가장 사랑하며, 언젠가 주께서 \"착하고 충성된 종아, 잘하였도다\" 하고 말씀해 주시리라고 내 어린 마음이 확신하는, 그 착하고 충실한 종을 본다."]], [["There are many faces that I know, among the little crowd; faces that I knew in church, when mine was always wondering there; faces that first saw my mother, when she came to the village in her youthful bloom.", "자그마한 무리 속에는 내가 아는 얼굴들이 많다. 내가 늘 두리번거리던 시절 교회에서 보았던 얼굴들, 어머니가 젊고 꽃다운 시절 이 마을로 왔을 때 어머니를 처음 본 얼굴들."], ["I do not mind them--I mind nothing but my grief--and yet I see and know them all; and even in the background, far away, see Minnie looking on, and her eye glancing on her sweetheart, who is near me.", "나는 그들에게 마음 쓰지 않는다 — 내 슬픔 말고는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않는다 — 그런데도 나는 그들 모두를 보고 알아본다. 멀리 뒤편에서, 미니가 지켜보고 있는 것마저, 그리고 내 곁에 있는 자기 연인에게 흘긋 던지는 그녀의 눈길마저 본다."]], [["It is over, and the earth is filled in, and we turn to come away.", "다 끝나고, 흙이 메워지고, 우리는 돌아서서 떠나려 한다. 우리 앞에는 우리 집이 서 있다."], ["Before us stands our house, so pretty and unchanged, so linked in my mind with the young idea of what is gone, that all my sorrow has been nothing to the sorrow it calls forth.", "그토록 예쁘고 변함없으며, 이제는 사라진 그 어린 시절의 생각과 내 마음속에 너무도 단단히 얽혀 있어서, 지금껏 내가 겪은 슬픔이 그것이 불러일으키는 슬픔에 비하면 아무것도 아닌 것만 같다."], ["But they take me on; and Mr. Chillip talks to me; and when we get home, puts some water to my lips; and when I ask his leave to go up to my room, dismisses me with the gentleness of a woman.", "하지만 사람들이 나를 데려가고, 칠립 의사 선생님이 나에게 말을 건다. 집에 다다르자 그는 내 입술에 물을 적셔 주고, 내가 내 방으로 올라가게 해 달라고 청하자, 여인 같은 다정함으로 나를 보내 준다."]], [["All this, I say, is yesterday’s event.", "다시 말하건대, 이 모든 것이 어제의 일이다."], ["Events of later date have floated from me to the shore where all forgotten things will reappear, but this stands like a high rock in the ocean.", "그 뒤에 일어난 일들은 잊힌 모든 것이 다시 떠오르는 그 기슭으로 나에게서 떠내려가 버렸지만, 이 일만은 바다 한가운데 우뚝 솟은 높은 바위처럼 서 있다."]], [["I knew that Peggotty would come to me in my room.", "나는 패거티가 내 방으로 올 것임을 알고 있었다."], ["The Sabbath stillness of the time (the day was so like Sunday!", "그 무렵의 안식일 같은 정적이 (그날은 너무도 일요일 같았다!"], ["I have forgotten that) was suited to us both.", "그걸 깜빡했구나) 우리 둘에게 꼭 어울렸다."], ["She sat down by my side upon my little bed; and holding my hand, and sometimes putting it to her lips, and sometimes smoothing it with hers, as she might have comforted my little brother, told me, in her way, all that she had to tell concerning what had happened.", "그녀는 내 작은 침대로 와서 내 곁에 앉았다. 그러고는 내 손을 잡고, 때로는 그 손을 입술에 갖다 대고, 때로는 자기 손으로 쓸어 주면서, 마치 내 어린 동생을 달래기라도 하듯이, 일어난 일에 관해 들려줄 것을 모두 자기 나름의 방식으로 나에게 이야기해 주었다."]], [["‘She was never well,’ said Peggotty, ‘for a long time.", "\"아씨는 오랫동안,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"내내 편찮으셨단다."], ["She was uncertain in her mind, and not happy.", "마음이 갈피를 못 잡고, 행복하지가 않으셨어."], ["When her baby was born, I thought at first she would get better, but she was more delicate, and sunk a little every day.", "아기가 태어났을 때 난 처음엔 좀 나아지시려나 했는데, 더 여위어 가시면서 날마다 조금씩 가라앉으셨지."], ["She used to like to sit alone before her baby came, and then she cried; but afterwards she used to sing to it--so soft, that I once thought, when I heard her, it was like a voice up in the air, that was rising away.", "아기가 태어나기 전엔 혼자 앉아 계시기를 좋아하셨는데, 그럴 때면 우셨단다. 하지만 그 뒤엔 아기에게 노래를 불러 주셨지 — 어찌나 부드럽던지, 한번은 그 소리를 들으면서, 마치 저 하늘 위로 솟아오르며 멀어져 가는 목소리 같다고 생각했어."]], [["‘I think she got to be more timid, and more frightened-like, of late; and that a hard word was like a blow to her.", "\"요즘 들어선 더 겁이 많아지고, 더 무서움을 타시는 것 같았어. 모진 말 한마디가 매를 맞는 것이나 다름없으셨지."], ["But she was always the same to me.", "하지만 나한텐 늘 한결같으셨단다."], ["She never changed to her foolish Peggotty, didn’t my sweet girl.’", "내 사랑스러운 아씨는, 어리석은 자기 패거티한테는 결코 변함이 없으셨어.\""]], [["Here Peggotty stopped, and softly beat upon my hand a little while.", "여기서 패거티는 말을 멈추고, 잠시 내 손을 가만가만 토닥였다."]], [["‘The last time that I saw her like her own old self, was the night when you came home, my dear.", "\"아씨가 예전의 그 모습 그대로인 걸 마지막으로 본 건, 얘야, 네가 집에 돌아온 날 밤이었어."], ["The day you went away, she said to me, “I never shall see my pretty darling again.", "네가 떠나던 날, 아씨가 나한테 그러셨지. '나는 내 어여쁜 보물을 다시는 못 볼 거야."], ["Something tells me so, that tells the truth, I know.”", "무언가가 나한테 그렇게 일러 줘. 그게 진실을 말하고 있다는 걸 난 알아.'"]], [["‘She tried to hold up after that; and many a time, when they told her she was thoughtless and light-hearted, made believe to be so; but it was all a bygone then.", "\"그 뒤로 아씨는 버텨 보려 애쓰셨어. 사람들이 아씨더러 철없고 속없이 들떠 있다고 할 때마다, 여러 번 그런 척 꾸며 보이기도 하셨지. 하지만 그건 이미 다 지난 일이었어."], ["She never told her husband what she had told me--she was afraid of saying it to anybody else--till one night, a little more than a week before it happened, when she said to him: “My dear, I think I am dying.”", "아씨는 나한테 말한 걸 남편한텐 끝내 털어놓지 않으셨어 — 다른 누구한테도 그 말을 하기가 두려우셨던 거야 — 그러다 일이 벌어지기 한 주하고도 며칠 전 어느 날 밤, 마침내 그이한테 이러셨대. '여보, 나 죽어 가는 것 같아요.'"]], [["‘“It’s off my mind now, Peggotty,”", "\"'이제 마음에서 내려놓았어, 패거티.'"], ["she told me, when I laid her in her bed that night.", "그날 밤 내가 아씨를 침대에 뉘어 드릴 때 아씨가 나한테 그러셨어."], ["“He will believe it more and more, poor fellow, every day for a few days to come; and then it will be past.", "'저이는, 가엾은 사람, 앞으로 며칠 동안 날마다 그 말을 점점 더 믿게 될 거야. 그러고 나면 다 지나가겠지."], ["I am very tired.", "난 무척 지쳤어."], ["If this is sleep, sit by me while I sleep: don’t leave me.", "이게 잠이라면, 내가 자는 동안 내 곁에 앉아 있어 줘. 날 떠나지 마."], ["God bless both my children!", "하느님, 제 두 아이를 축복하소서!"], ["God protect and keep my fatherless boy!”", "하느님, 아비 없는 제 아들을 지키고 보살펴 주소서!'"]], [["‘I never left her afterwards,’ said Peggotty.", "\"그 뒤로 난 결코 아씨 곁을 떠나지 않았단다.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"아씨는 아래층 그 두 사람한테 종종 말을 거셨어 — 그들을 사랑하셨으니까."], ["‘She often talked to them two downstairs--for she loved them; she couldn’t bear not to love anyone who was about her--but when they went away from her bed-side, she always turned to me, as if there was rest where Peggotty was, and never fell asleep in any other way.", "곁에 있는 누구든 사랑하지 않고는 못 견디는 분이셨거든 — 하지만 그들이 침대 머리맡에서 물러가면, 아씨는 늘 나에게로 돌아서셨어. 마치 패거티가 있는 곳에 안식이 있다는 듯이 말이야. 그러고는 다른 어떤 식으로도 잠들지 않으셨지."]], [["‘On the last night, in the evening, she kissed me, and said: “If my baby should die too, Peggotty, please let them lay him in my arms, and bury us together.”", "\"마지막 날 밤, 저녁이 되자 아씨는 나에게 입을 맞추며 그러셨어. '내 아기마저 죽거든, 패거티, 부디 그 애를 내 품에 안겨서 우리를 함께 묻어 줘.' (그대로 되었단다."], ["(It was done; for the poor lamb lived but a day beyond her.) “Let my dearest boy go with us to our resting-place,”", "가엾은 어린양은 아씨보다 단 하루를 더 살았을 뿐이거든.) '내 사랑하는 아들도 우리가 쉴 곳까지 함께 가게 해 줘.' 아씨가 그러셨어."], ["she said, “and tell him that his mother, when she lay here, blessed him not once, but a thousand times.”’", "'그리고 그 애한테 전해 줘. 제 어미가 여기 누워서 그 애를 축복했다고. 한 번이 아니라 천 번을.'\""]], [["Another silence followed this, and another gentle beating on my hand.", "이 말 뒤에 또 한 차례 침묵이 흘렀고, 내 손을 가만가만 토닥이는 손길이 또 한 번 이어졌다."]], [["‘It was pretty far in the night,’ said Peggotty, ‘when she asked me for some drink; and when she had taken it, gave me such a patient smile, the dear!--so beautiful!", "\"밤이 꽤 깊었을 때,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"아씨가 나한테 마실 것을 좀 달라고 하셨어. 그러고는 그걸 받아 드시고 나서, 그 다정한 분이, 그렇게도 참을성 있는 미소를 나한테 지어 보이셨단다 — 어찌나 아름답던지!"]], [["‘Daybreak had come, and the sun was rising, when she said to me, how kind and considerate Mr.", "\"동이 트고 해가 떠오를 무렵, 아씨가 나한테 그러셨어."], ["Copperfield had always been to her, and how he had borne with her, and told her, when she doubted herself, that a loving heart was better and stronger than wisdom, and that he was a happy man in hers.", "코퍼필드 어른이 늘 얼마나 다정하고 자상하셨는지, 그이가 아씨를 얼마나 너그러이 받아 주셨는지, 그리고 아씨가 자신을 못 미더워할 때면 그이가 이르셨다는구나. 사랑하는 마음이 지혜보다 더 낫고 더 강한 거라고, 그리고 자기는 아씨의 그 마음 덕분에 행복한 사람이라고. '패거티, 내 사랑하는 사람,'"], ["“Peggotty, my dear,”", "그때 아씨가 그러셨어."], ["she said then, “put me nearer to you,”", "'날 너한테 더 가까이 붙여 줘.'"], ["for she was very weak.", "아씨가 무척 쇠약하셨거든."], ["“Lay your good arm underneath my neck,”", "'네 그 좋은 팔을 내 목 밑에 받쳐 주고,'"], ["she said, “and turn me to you, for your face is going far off, and I want it to be near.”", "아씨가 그러셨어. '날 너 쪽으로 돌려 줘. 네 얼굴이 점점 멀어져 가는데, 난 그게 가까이 있었으면 좋겠어.'"], ["I put it as she asked; and oh Davy!", "난 아씨가 청하신 대로 해 드렸지."], ["the time had come when my first parting words to you were true--when she was glad to lay her poor head on her stupid cross old Peggotty’s arm--and she died like a child that had gone to sleep!’", "아, 데이비, 그러자 내가 너랑 처음 헤어지면서 했던 그 말이 참말이 되는 순간이 온 거야 — 아씨가 그 가엾은 머리를, 어리석고 심술궂은 늙은 패거티의 팔에 기꺼이 기대시던 그 순간이 말이야 — 그러고는 아씨는 잠든 어린아이처럼 숨을 거두셨단다!\""]], [["Thus ended Peggotty’s narration.", "이렇게 패거티의 이야기가 끝났다."], ["From the moment of my knowing of the death of my mother, the idea of her as she had been of late had vanished from me.", "어머니가 돌아가셨다는 것을 안 그 순간부터, 요즘의 어머니 모습은 내게서 사라져 버렸다."], ["I remembered her, from that instant, only as the young mother of my earliest impressions, who had been used to wind her bright curls round and round her finger, and to dance with me at twilight in the parlour.", "그 순간부터 나는 오직 내 가장 어린 시절의 기억 속 젊은 어머니로만 그분을 떠올렸다. 빛나는 곱슬머리를 손가락에 돌돌 감곤 하고, 황혼 녘이면 거실에서 나와 함께 춤을 추던 그 어머니로."], ["What Peggotty had told me now, was so far from bringing me back to the later period, that it rooted the earlier image in my mind.", "패거티가 방금 나에게 들려준 이야기는, 나를 그 뒷날의 시절로 되돌려 놓기는커녕, 오히려 그 더 이른 시절의 모습을 내 마음에 더욱 깊이 뿌리내리게 했다."], ["It may be curious, but it is true.", "묘하게 들릴지 모르지만 사실이다."], ["In her death she winged her way back to her calm untroubled youth, and cancelled all the rest.", "어머니는 죽음 속에서 평온하고 근심 없던 젊은 날로 날개를 펴 되돌아갔고, 그 밖의 모든 것을 지워 버렸다."]], [["The mother who lay in the grave, was the mother of my infancy; the little creature in her arms, was myself, as I had once been, hushed for ever on her bosom.", "무덤 속에 누운 어머니는 내 갓난 시절의 어머니였다. 그 품에 안긴 자그마한 것은, 한때의 나, 그분의 가슴에 안겨 영원히 잠든 나 자신이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 10. I BECOME NEGLECTED, AND AM PROVIDED FOR", "# CHAPTER 10. I BECOME NEGLECTED, AND AM PROVIDED FOR"]], [["The first act of business Miss Murdstone performed when the day of the solemnity was over, and light was freely admitted into the house, was to give Peggotty a month’s warning.", "머드스톤 양은 장례라는 엄숙한 의식의 날이 끝나고 집 안에 빛이 거리낌 없이 들어오게 되자, 맨 먼저 한 일이 패거티에게 한 달 뒤에 나가라고 통보한 것이었다."], ["Much as Peggotty would have disliked such a service, I believe she would have retained it, for my sake, in preference to the best upon earth.", "패거티가 그런 일자리를 아무리 싫어했더라도, 나를 위해서라면 세상에서 가장 좋은 자리보다 그 자리를 지켰을 거라고 나는 믿는다."], ["She told me we must part, and told me why; and we condoled with one another, in all sincerity.", "패거티는 우리가 헤어져야 한다고, 그 까닭이 무엇인지 나에게 말해 주었다. 그리고 우리는 진심을 다해 서로를 위로했다."]], [["As to me or my future, not a word was said, or a step taken.", "나에 대해서나 내 앞날에 대해서는 단 한마디 말도, 단 한 걸음의 조치도 없었다."], ["Happy they would have been, I dare say, if they could have dismissed me at a month’s warning too.", "감히 말하건대, 나까지 한 달 통보로 내보낼 수 있었다면 그들은 더없이 기뻐했을 것이다."], ["I mustered courage once, to ask Miss Murdstone when I was going back to school; and she answered dryly, she believed I was not going back at all.", "나는 한 번 용기를 내어 머드스톤 양에게 학교로 언제 돌아가느냐고 물어보았다. 그러자 그녀는 무뚝뚝하게, 내가 아예 돌아가지 않을 것 같다고 대답했다."], ["I was told nothing more.", "그 이상은 아무것도 듣지 못했다."], ["I was very anxious to know what was going to be done with me, and so was Peggotty; but neither she nor I could pick up any information on the subject.", "나는 내가 어떻게 될 것인지 무척 알고 싶었고, 패거티도 마찬가지였지만, 그녀도 나도 그 일에 대해서는 어떤 정보도 주워 모을 수가 없었다."]], [["There was one change in my condition, which, while it relieved me of a great deal of present uneasiness, might have made me, if I had been capable of considering it closely, yet more uncomfortable about the future.", "내 처지에 한 가지 변화가 있었는데, 그것은 당장의 거북함을 크게 덜어 주기는 했으나, 내가 그것을 곰곰이 따져 볼 줄 아는 아이였다면 오히려 앞날에 대해 더 마음을 졸이게 만들었을지도 모른다."], ["It was this. The constraint that had been put upon me, was quite abandoned.", "그것은 이러했다. 나에게 씌워졌던 속박이 깡그리 사라진 것이다."], ["I was so far from being required to keep my dull post in the parlour, that on several occasions, when I took my seat there, Miss Murdstone frowned to me to go away.", "거실에서 따분한 자리를 지키도록 요구받기는커녕, 여러 번은 내가 거기 앉으려 하면 머드스톤 양이 눈살을 찌푸리며 나가라고 손짓했다."], ["I was so far from being warned off from Peggotty’s society, that, provided I was not in Mr. Murdstone’s, I was never sought out or inquired for.", "패거티와 어울리지 말라는 경고를 받기는커녕, 머드스톤 씨 곁에만 있지 않으면 나를 찾거나 내 안부를 묻는 사람도 없었다."], ["At first I was in daily dread of his taking my education in hand again, or of Miss Murdstone’s devoting herself to it; but I soon began to think that such fears were groundless, and that all I had to anticipate was neglect.", "처음에는 그가 다시 내 교육을 손에 쥐거나, 머드스톤 양이 거기에 매달릴까 봐 날마다 겁이 났다. 그러나 곧 그런 두려움은 근거가 없으며, 내가 예상해야 할 것은 오로지 방치뿐이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["I do not conceive that this discovery gave me much pain then.", "이런 사실을 깨달은 것이 그때 나에게 큰 고통을 주었다고는 생각하지 않는다."], ["I was still giddy with the shock of my mother’s death, and in a kind of stunned state as to all tributary things.", "나는 아직 어머니의 죽음이라는 충격에 어지러웠고, 거기에 딸린 모든 일에 대해 일종의 멍한 상태였다. 사실, 가끔 한가할 때면 이런 가능성들을 이리저리 그려 보던 기억이 난다."], ["I can recollect, indeed, to have speculated, at odd times, on the possibility of my not being taught any more, or cared for any more; and growing up to be a shabby, moody man, lounging an idle life away, about the village; as well as on the feasibility of my getting rid of this picture by going away somewhere, like the hero in a story, to seek my fortune: but these were transient visions, daydreams I sat looking at sometimes, as if they were faintly painted or written on the wall of my room, and which, as they melted away, left the wall blank again.", "내가 더는 무엇도 배우지 못하고, 누구의 보살핌도 받지 못한 채, 후줄근하고 우울한 사내로 자라나, 마을 언저리에서 빈둥빈둥 게으른 삶을 흘려보내는 모습. 그런가 하면, 이야기 속 주인공처럼 어딘가로 떠나 내 운을 찾아 나섬으로써 이 그림을 떨쳐 버릴 수 있을지 가늠해 보기도 했다. 그러나 그것들은 덧없는 환영이었고, 마치 내 방 벽에 희미하게 그려지거나 적혀 있기라도 한 듯 내가 가끔 앉아서 바라보던 백일몽이었으며, 그것들이 녹아 사라지면 벽은 다시 텅 비곤 했다."]], [["‘Peggotty,’ I said in a thoughtful whisper, one evening, when I was warming my hands at the kitchen fire, ‘Mr.", "\"패거티.\" 어느 저녁, 부엌 난롯불에 손을 쬐다가 나는 생각에 잠긴 채 속삭였다."], ["Murdstone likes me less than he used to.", "\"머드스톤 씨는 예전보다 날 덜 좋아해."], ["He never liked me much, Peggotty; but he would rather not even see me now, if he can help it.’", "원래 날 별로 안 좋아했지만, 패거티, 지금은 할 수만 있으면 날 보기조차 싫어해.\""]], [["‘Perhaps it’s his sorrow,’ said Peggotty, stroking my hair.", "\"슬픔 때문인지도 모르지.\" 패거티가 내 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했다."]], [["‘I am sure, Peggotty, I am sorry too.", "\"정말이지 패거티, 나도 슬퍼."], ["If I believed it was his sorrow, I should not think of it at all.", "그게 그분의 슬픔 때문이라고 믿는다면, 난 그런 건 아예 신경도 안 쓸 거야."], ["But it’s not that; oh, no, it’s not that.’", "하지만 그게 아니야. 아, 아니야, 그건 아니야.\""]], [["‘How do you know it’s not that?’ said Peggotty, after a silence.", "\"그게 아닌 걸 어떻게 알아?\" 잠시 가만히 있다가 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, his sorrow is another and quite a different thing.", "\"아, 그분의 슬픔은 또 다른, 아주 별개의 거야."], ["He is sorry at this moment, sitting by the fireside with Miss Murdstone; but if I was to go in, Peggotty, he would be something besides.’", "그분은 지금 이 순간 머드스톤 양이랑 난롯가에 앉아서 슬퍼하고 있겠지. 하지만 내가 들어가면, 패거티, 그분은 슬픔 말고 또 다른 게 될걸.\""]], [["‘What would he be?’ said Peggotty.", "\"뭐가 되는데?\" 패거티가 물었다."]], [["‘Angry,’ I answered, with an involuntary imitation of his dark frown.", "\"화가 나는 거지.\" 나는 나도 모르게 그의 어두운 찌푸림을 흉내 내며 대답했다."], ["‘If he was only sorry, he wouldn’t look at me as he does.", "\"그분이 그저 슬프기만 하다면, 날 저런 눈으로 보진 않을 거야."], ["I am only sorry, and it makes me feel kinder.’", "난 그냥 슬프기만 한데, 그래서 마음이 더 다정해지거든.\""]], [["Peggotty said nothing for a little while; and I warmed my hands, as silent as she.", "패거티는 한동안 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 나는 그녀처럼 말없이 손을 쬐었다."]], [["‘Davy,’ she said at length.", "\"데이비.\" 마침내 그녀가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Yes, Peggotty?’ ‘I have tried, my dear, all ways I could think of--all the ways there are, and all the ways there ain’t, in short--to get a suitable service here, in Blunderstone; but there’s no such a thing, my love.’", "\"응, 패거티?\" \"얘야, 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 방법으로 애써 봤단다. 다시 말해서 있는 방법이란 방법, 없는 방법까지 죄다 동원해서, 여기 블런더스톤에서 마땅한 일자리를 구해 보려 했어. 하지만 그런 건 없구나, 우리 아가.\""]], [["‘And what do you mean to do, Peggotty,’ says I, wistfully.", "\"그럼 어쩔 셈이야, 패거티.\" 나는 애틋하게 물었다."], ["‘Do you mean to go and seek your fortune?’", "\"운을 찾아 떠날 작정이야?\""]], [["‘I expect I shall be forced to go to Yarmouth,’ replied Peggotty, ‘and live there.’", "\"아무래도 야머스로 가야 할 것 같다.\" 패거티가 대답했다. \"거기서 사는 거지.\""]], [["‘You might have gone farther off,’ I said, brightening a little, ‘and been as bad as lost.", "\"더 멀리 갈 수도 있었으니까.\" 나는 조금 환해지며 말했다. \"그랬으면 영영 잃어버린 거나 마찬가지였을 거야."], ["I shall see you sometimes, my dear old Peggotty, there.", "거기선 가끔 널 볼 수 있겠네, 정겨운 우리 패거티."], ["You won’t be quite at the other end of the world, will you?’", "세상 저쪽 끝까지 가는 건 아니지, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘Contrary ways, please God!’ cried Peggotty, with great animation.", "\"천만에, 하느님 덕분이지!\" 패거티가 잔뜩 신이 나서 외쳤다."], ["‘As long as you are here, my pet, I shall come over every week of my life to see you.", "\"네가 여기 있는 한, 우리 아가, 난 평생 매주 한 번씩 널 보러 건너올 거야."], ["One day, every week of my life!’", "살아 있는 동안 일주일에 하루씩, 꼭!\""]], [["I felt a great weight taken off my mind by this promise: but even this was not all, for Peggotty went on to say:", "이 약속에 나는 마음에서 큰 짐 하나가 내려가는 걸 느꼈다. 하지만 그게 다가 아니었으니, 패거티가 말을 이었다."]], [["‘I’m a-going, Davy, you see, to my brother’s, first, for another fortnight’s visit--just till I have had time to look about me, and get to be something like myself again.", "\"데이비, 있잖니, 난 우선 우리 오라버니한테로 가서 또 두 주쯤 머물 거야. 둘레를 좀 살펴보고, 다시 예전의 나로 돌아갈 짬을 가질 때까지만."], ["Now, I have been thinking that perhaps, as they don’t want you here at present, you might be let to go along with me.’", "그런데 내가 생각해 봤는데 말이야, 지금 여기서 널 필요로 하지 않으니, 어쩌면 너도 나랑 같이 가도록 해 줄지 몰라.\""]], [["If anything, short of being in a different relation to every one about me, Peggotty excepted, could have given me a sense of pleasure at that time, it would have been this project of all others.", "패거티만 빼고 내 둘레의 모든 사람과 전혀 다른 관계에 놓이는 일을 제외하고, 그 무렵 나에게 기쁨을 줄 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 다른 무엇보다도 바로 이 계획이었을 것이다."], ["The idea of being again surrounded by those honest faces, shining welcome on me; of renewing the peacefulness of the sweet Sunday morning, when the bells were ringing, the stones dropping in the water, and the shadowy ships breaking through the mist; of roaming up and down with little Em’ly, telling her my troubles, and finding charms against them in the shells and pebbles on the beach; made a calm in my heart.", "그 정직한 얼굴들에 다시 둘러싸여 환한 환영을 받는 일, 종이 울리고 돌멩이가 물에 떨어지고 어슴푸레한 배들이 안개를 뚫고 나오던 그 달콤한 일요일 아침의 평온을 되살리는 일, 어린 에밀리와 위아래로 거닐며 내 시름을 털어놓고 바닷가의 조개와 조약돌에서 그 시름을 막아 줄 부적을 찾는 일을 떠올리니, 마음에 고요가 깃들었다."], ["It was ruffled next moment, to be sure, by a doubt of Miss Murdstone’s giving her consent; but even that was set at rest soon, for she came out to take an evening grope in the store-closet while we were yet in conversation, and Peggotty, with a boldness that amazed me, broached the topic on the spot.", "물론 다음 순간 머드스톤 양이 허락해 줄까 하는 의심에 그 고요가 흔들리기는 했다. 하지만 그것조차 곧 가라앉았으니, 우리가 아직 이야기를 나누고 있을 때 그녀가 저녁 무렵 저장실을 더듬어 보려고 나왔고, 패거티가 나를 놀라게 할 만큼 대담하게 그 자리에서 이 화제를 꺼냈기 때문이다."]], [["‘The boy will be idle there,’ said Miss Murdstone, looking into a pickle-jar, ‘and idleness is the root of all evil.", "\"그 애는 거기 가면 빈둥거릴 거야.\" 머드스톤 양이 피클 단지 속을 들여다보며 말했다. \"그리고 게으름은 모든 악의 뿌리지."], ["But, to be sure, he would be idle here--or anywhere, in my opinion.’", "하지만 분명, 그 애는 여기서도 빈둥거릴 테니까. 내가 보기엔 어디서든 마찬가지야.\""]], [["Peggotty had an angry answer ready, I could see; but she swallowed it for my sake, and remained silent.", "패거티가 발끈한 대답을 준비하고 있는 게 보였다. 그러나 그녀는 나를 위해 그것을 꿀꺽 삼키고 잠자코 있었다."]], [["‘Humph!’ said Miss Murdstone, still keeping her eye on the pickles; ‘it is of more importance than anything else--it is of paramount importance--that my brother should not be disturbed or made uncomfortable.", "\"흥!\" 머드스톤 양이 여전히 피클에 눈을 둔 채 말했다. \"무엇보다 중요한 건, 그 무엇보다도 으뜸으로 중요한 건, 내 오라버니가 방해받거나 불편해지지 않는 거야."], ["I suppose I had better say yes.’", "그래을 거라고 해 두는 게 낫겠지.\""]], [["I thanked her, without making any demonstration of joy, lest it should induce her to withdraw her assent.", "나는 기쁜 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않고 그녀에게 고맙다고 했다. 자칫 그것이 그녀로 하여금 승낙을 거두게 만들까 봐서였다."], ["Nor could I help thinking this a prudent course, since she looked at me out of the pickle-jar, with as great an access of sourness as if her black eyes had absorbed its contents.", "그게 신중한 처사였다고 생각하지 않을 수가 없었으니, 그녀가 피클 단지 속에서 나를 쳐다보는데, 마치 그 검은 두 눈이 단지 속 내용물을 죄다 빨아들이기라도 한 듯 한층 시큼한 낯빛이었기 때문이다."], ["However, the permission was given, and was never retracted; for when the month was out, Peggotty and I were ready to depart.", "어쨌든 허락은 떨어졌고, 결코 번복되지 않았다. 한 달이 지났을 때, 패거티와 나는 떠날 채비를 마쳤다."]], [["Mr. Barkis came into the house for Peggotty’s boxes.", "바키스 씨가 패거티의 짐 상자를 가지러 집으로 들어왔다."], ["I had never known him to pass the garden-gate before, but on this occasion he came into the house.", "그가 정원 문을 넘어 들어오는 건 그때까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었는데, 이번에는 집 안까지 들어온 것이다."], ["And he gave me a look as he shouldered the largest box and went out, which I thought had meaning in it, if meaning could ever be said to find its way into Mr. Barkis’s visage.", "그리고 가장 큰 상자를 어깨에 짊어지고 나가면서 나에게 어떤 눈길을 던졌는데, 만일 의미라는 것이 바키스 씨의 얼굴에 길을 찾아들 수 있다고 칠 수 있다면, 거기엔 뭔가 의미가 담겨 있다고 나는 생각했다."]], [["Peggotty was naturally in low spirits at leaving what had been her home so many years, and where the two strong attachments of her life--for my mother and myself--had been formed.", "패거티는 그토록 오랜 세월 자기 집이었던 곳, 그리고 평생의 두 가지 깊은 정 — 어머니와 나에 대한 정 — 이 맺어진 그곳을 떠나면서 당연히 마음이 가라앉아 있었다."], ["She had been walking in the churchyard, too, very early; and she got into the cart, and sat in it with her handkerchief at her eyes.", "그녀는 아주 이른 아침에 교회 묘지를 거닐기도 했다. 그러고는 마차에 올라 손수건을 눈에 댄 채 앉아 있었다."]], [["So long as she remained in this condition, Mr. Barkis gave no sign of life whatever.", "그녀가 그런 상태로 있는 동안, 바키스 씨는 살아 있다는 기색을 전혀 내비치지 않았다."], ["He sat in his usual place and attitude like a great stuffed figure.", "그는 여느 때의 자리에 여느 때의 자세로, 커다란 박제 인형처럼 앉아 있었다."], ["But when she began to look about her, and to speak to me, he nodded his head and grinned several times.", "그러나 그녀가 둘레를 살피며 나에게 말을 걸기 시작하자, 그는 고개를 끄덕이며 몇 번이나 히죽거렸다."], ["I have not the least notion at whom, or what he meant by it.", "누구를 향한 것인지, 무슨 뜻인지는 도무지 알 수 없었다."]], [["‘It’s a beautiful day, Mr. Barkis!’ I said, as an act of politeness.", "\"날씨 참 좋네요, 바키스 씨!\" 나는 예의상 말했다."]], [["‘It ain’t bad,’ said Mr.", "\"나쁘진 않지.\""], ["Barkis, who generally qualified his speech, and rarely committed himself.", "바키스 씨가 말했는데, 그는 으레 말끝을 흐리며 좀처럼 단정을 짓지 않았다."]], [["‘Peggotty is quite comfortable now, Mr.", "\"패거티는 이제 꽤 편안한 것 같아요, 바키스 씨.\""], ["Barkis,’ I remarked, for his satisfaction.", "나는 그를 흐뭇하게 해 주려고 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Is she, though?’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"정말 그런가?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["After reflecting about it, with a sagacious air, Mr. Barkis eyed her, and said:", "바키스 씨는 그 말을 곰곰이 따져 보는 듯 지혜로운 표정을 짓더니, 그녀를 흘끗 보며 말했다."]], [["‘ARE you pretty comfortable?’", "\"댁은 꽤 편안한가?\""]], [["Peggotty laughed, and answered in the affirmative.", "패거티가 웃으면서 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘But really and truly, you know. Are you?’ growled Mr.", "\"하지만 정말로, 진짜로 말이야. 그런가?\""], ["Barkis, sliding nearer to her on the seat, and nudging her with his elbow. ‘Are you?", "바키스 씨가 으르렁대듯 말하며 좌석에서 그녀 쪽으로 미끄러져 다가와 팔꿈치로 쿡 찔렀다. \"그래?"], ["Really and truly pretty comfortable? Are you? Eh?’", "정말로 진짜로 꽤 편안한가? 그래? 응?\""]], [["At each of these inquiries Mr. Barkis shuffled nearer to her, and gave her another nudge; so that at last we were all crowded together in the left-hand corner of the cart, and I was so squeezed that I could hardly bear it.", "이렇게 물을 때마다 바키스 씨는 그녀 쪽으로 슬금슬금 더 다가와 또 한 번씩 쿡 찔렀다. 그래서 마침내 우리는 모두 마차 왼쪽 구석으로 한데 몰리고 말았고, 나는 어찌나 짓눌렸던지 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다."]], [["Peggotty calling his attention to my sufferings, Mr. Barkis gave me a little more room at once, and got away by degrees.", "패거티가 내 고통에 그의 주의를 돌리자, 바키스 씨는 곧 나에게 조금 더 자리를 내주고 차츰차츰 물러났다."], ["But I could not help observing that he seemed to think he had hit upon a wonderful expedient for expressing himself in a neat, agreeable, and pointed manner, without the inconvenience of inventing conversation. He manifestly chuckled over it for some time.", "하지만 그가 대화를 새로 짜내는 수고 없이도, 말끔하고 흐뭇하고 콕 집어 자기 마음을 표현할 기막힌 방편을 찾아냈다고 여기는 듯한 것을 나는 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 한동안 그것을 떠올리며 쿡쿡 웃는 게 분명했다."], ["By and by he turned to Peggotty again, and repeating, ‘Are you pretty comfortable though?’ bore down upon us as before, until the breath was nearly edged out of my body.", "이윽고 그는 다시 패거티 쪽으로 돌아서서 \"그래도 꽤 편안한가?\" 하고 되풀이하며, 아까처럼 우리를 짓눌러 와서, 내 몸에서 숨이 거의 빠져나갈 지경이었다."], ["By and by he made another descent upon us with the same inquiry, and the same result.", "이윽고 그는 똑같은 질문과 똑같은 결과로 또 한 번 우리를 덮쳤다."], ["At length, I got up whenever I saw him coming, and standing on the foot-board, pretended to look at the prospect; after which I did very well.", "마침내 나는 그가 다가오는 걸 볼 때마다 일어나서 발판에 올라서서 바깥 경치를 보는 척했다. 그 뒤로는 아주 잘 지냈다."]], [["He was so polite as to stop at a public-house, expressly on our account, and entertain us with broiled mutton and beer.", "그는 친절하게도 오로지 우리를 위해 선술집에 들러, 구운 양고기와 맥주를 대접했다."], ["Even when Peggotty was in the act of drinking, he was seized with one of those approaches, and almost choked her.", "패거티가 한창 마시고 있을 때조차 그는 그 발작 같은 접근 중 하나를 일으켜 그녀를 거의 사레들게 했다."], ["But as we drew nearer to the end of our journey, he had more to do and less time for gallantry; and when we got on Yarmouth pavement, we were all too much shaken and jolted, I apprehend, to have any leisure for anything else.", "그러나 우리가 여정의 끝에 가까워질수록 그는 할 일이 많아지고 사근사근할 짬이 줄어들었다. 야머스의 포장도로에 들어섰을 때, 우리는 모두 너무 흔들리고 덜컹거려서, 다른 무엇에 마음 쓸 여유가 없었던 것 같다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty and Ham waited for us at the old place.", "패거티 씨와 햄이 늘 만나던 그 자리에서 우리를 기다리고 있었다."], ["They received me and Peggotty in an affectionate manner, and shook hands with Mr. Barkis, who, with his hat on the very back of his head, and a shame-faced leer upon his countenance, and pervading his very legs, presented but a vacant appearance, I thought.", "그들은 나와 패거티를 다정하게 맞이하고, 바키스 씨와 악수를 했는데, 바키스 씨는 모자를 뒤통수에 한껏 걸친 채, 멋쩍은 곁눈질이 얼굴에, 아니 다리까지 온통 퍼진 모습으로, 내가 보기엔 그저 멍한 행색이었다."], ["They each took one of Peggotty’s trunks, and we were going away, when Mr. Barkis solemnly made a sign to me with his forefinger to come under an archway.", "두 사람이 패거티의 짐 가방을 하나씩 들었고, 우리가 막 떠나려는데, 바키스 씨가 엄숙하게 집게손가락으로 나에게 신호를 보내 어느 아치 길 아래로 오라고 했다."]], [["‘I say,’ growled Mr.", "\"있잖아.\""], ["Barkis, ‘it was all right.’", "바키스 씨가 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"다 잘됐어.\""]], [["I looked up into his face, and answered, with an attempt to be very profound: ‘Oh!’", "나는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 무척 심오해 보이려고 애쓰며 대답했다. \"아!\""]], [["‘It didn’t come to a end there,’ said Mr. Barkis, nodding confidentially.", "\"거기서 끝난 게 아니야.\" 바키스 씨가 은밀하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘It was all right.’", "\"다 잘됐다고.\""]], [["Again I answered, ‘Oh!’", "나는 또 대답했다. \"아!\""]], [["‘You know who was willin’,’ said my friend.", "\"누가 마음 있었는지 너도 알지.\" 내 친구가 말했다."], ["‘It was Barkis, and Barkis only.’", "\"바키스였어, 바키스뿐이었지.\""]], [["‘It’s all right,’ said Mr.", "나는 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다."], ["Barkis, shaking hands; ‘I’m a friend of your’n.", "\"다 잘됐어.\" 바키스 씨가 악수하며 말했다. \"난 네 편이야."], ["You made it all right, first.", "네가 먼저 다 잘되게 해 줬잖아."], ["It’s all right.’", "다 잘됐어.\""]], [["In his attempts to be particularly lucid, Mr. Barkis was so extremely mysterious, that I might have stood looking in his face for an hour, and most assuredly should have got as much information out of it as out of the face of a clock that had stopped, but for Peggotty’s calling me away.", "유독 명료하게 말하려는 노력 속에서, 바키스 씨는 어찌나 지독히 알쏭달쏭했던지, 내가 한 시간을 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 서 있었더라도, 멈춰 버린 시계의 얼굴에서 얻는 만큼의 정보밖에 얻지 못했을 게 틀림없었으나, 패거티가 나를 불러서 데려갔다."], ["As we were going along, she asked me what he had said; and I told her he had said it was all right.", "우리가 걸어가는데 그녀가 그가 무슨 말을 했느냐고 물었다. 나는 다 잘됐다고 했다고 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Like his impudence,’ said Peggotty, ‘but I don’t mind that!", "\"뻔뻔하기는.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그래도 난 그게 싫지 않아!"], ["Davy dear, what should you think if I was to think of being married?’", "데이비, 얘야, 내가 결혼할 생각을 한다면 넌 어떨 것 같으냐?\""]], [["‘Why--I suppose you would like me as much then, Peggotty, as you do now?’ I returned, after a little consideration.", "\"음, 그럼 패거티, 넌 그때도 지금만큼 날 좋아해 줄 거지?\" 나는 잠깐 생각한 뒤에 되물었다."]], [["Greatly to the astonishment of the passengers in the street, as well as of her relations going on before, the good soul was obliged to stop and embrace me on the spot, with many protestations of her unalterable love.", "거리의 행인들도, 앞서 걸어가던 그녀의 친척들도 몹시 놀랐는데, 이 착한 사람이 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 나를 끌어안고는, 변치 않는 사랑을 거듭거듭 다짐할 수밖에 없었기 때문이다."]], [["‘Tell me what should you say, darling?’ she asked again, when this was over, and we were walking on.", "\"넌 뭐라고 할지 말해 봐, 우리 아가.\" 그 일이 끝나고 우리가 다시 걸어가는데 그녀가 또 물었다."]], [["‘If you were thinking of being married--to Mr. Barkis, Peggotty?’", "\"네가 결혼할 생각을 한다면 — 바키스 씨하고, 패거티?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Peggotty.", "\"그래.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘I should think it would be a very good thing.", "\"난 그게 아주 좋은 일이라고 생각할 거야."], ["For then you know, Peggotty, you would always have the horse and cart to bring you over to see me, and could come for nothing, and be sure of coming.’", "그러면 있잖아, 패거티, 넌 늘 말과 마차가 있어서 날 보러 건너올 수 있고, 공짜로 올 수 있고, 틀림없이 올 수 있을 테니까.\""]], [["‘The sense of the dear!’ cried Peggotty.", "\"아이고 우리 똑똑한 것!\" 패거티가 외쳤다."], ["‘What I have been thinking of, this month back!", "\"내가 지난 한 달 내내 생각하던 게 바로 그거란다!"], ["Yes, my precious; and I think I should be more independent altogether, you see; let alone my working with a better heart in my own house, than I could in anybody else’s now.", "그래, 우리 보배. 그리고 내 생각엔 나도 통틀어 더 떳떳해질 것 같아, 알겠니. 남의집살이를 하는 것보다 내 집에서 더 즐거운 마음으로 일할 수 있는 건 말할 것도 없고."], ["I don’t know what I might be fit for, now, as a servant to a stranger.", "지금 와서 내가 낯선 사람 밑에서 하녀 노릇이나 제대로 할 수 있을지도 모르겠고. 게다가 늘 우리 예쁜이가 쉬는 곳 가까이 있을 테고.\""], ["And I shall be always near my pretty’s resting-place,’ said Peggotty, musing, ‘and be able to see it when I like; and when I lie down to rest, I may be laid not far off from my darling girl!’", "패거티가 골똘히 잠겨 말했다. \"보고 싶을 때 볼 수도 있고. 그리고 나도 쉬러 누울 때면, 우리 귀여운 아씨에게서 멀지 않은 데 묻힐 수 있겠지!\""]], [["We neither of us said anything for a little while.", "우리는 둘 다 한동안 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘But I wouldn’t so much as give it another thought,’ said Peggotty, cheerily ‘if my Davy was anyways against it--not if I had been asked in church thirty times three times over, and was wearing out the ring in my pocket.’", "\"하지만 우리 데이비가 조금이라도 반대한다면 난 두 번 다시 생각도 안 할 거야.\" 패거티가 명랑하게 말했다. \"교회에서 서른 번씩 세 번을 결혼 예고를 했고, 주머니 속 반지가 닳도록 끼고 있다 해도 말이지.\""]], [["‘Look at me, Peggotty,’ I replied; ‘and see if I am not really glad, and don’t truly wish it!’ As indeed I did, with all my heart.", "\"날 봐, 패거티.\" 나는 대답했다. \"내가 정말로 기뻐하는지, 진심으로 그걸 바라는지 봐!\" 정말이지 나는 온 마음을 다해 그랬다."]], [["‘Well, my life,’ said Peggotty, giving me a squeeze, ‘I have thought of it night and day, every way I can, and I hope the right way; but I’ll think of it again, and speak to my brother about it, and in the meantime we’ll keep it to ourselves, Davy, you and me.", "\"그래, 내 목숨 같은 것아.\" 패거티가 나를 꼭 껴안으며 말했다. \"난 밤낮으로, 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 방법으로 생각해 봤고, 부디 옳은 방법이길 바라."], ["Barkis is a good plain creature,’ said Peggotty, ‘and if I tried to do my duty by him, I think it would be my fault if I wasn’t--if I wasn’t pretty comfortable,’ said Peggotty, laughing heartily.", "하지만 다시 한번 생각해 보고, 오라버니한테도 얘기할 거야. 그동안은 우리끼리만 알자꾸나, 데이비, 너랑 나랑. 바키스는 착하고 순박한 사람이야.\""], ["This quotation from Mr.", "패거티가 말했다. \"그리고 내가 그 사람한테 도리를 다하려고 애쓴다면, 내가 — 내가 꽤 편안하지 않다면, 그건 내 잘못일 거야.\""], ["Barkis was so appropriate, and tickled us both so much, that we laughed again and again, and were quite in a pleasant humour when we came within view of Mr. Peggotty’s cottage.", "패거티가 신나게 웃으며 말했다. 바키스 씨의 이 말을 따 온 게 어찌나 안성맞춤이었던지, 우리는 둘 다 어찌나 우스웠던지, 거듭거듭 웃었고, 패거티 씨의 오두막이 시야에 들어올 무렵에는 아주 유쾌한 기분이 되어 있었다."]], [["It looked just the same, except that it may, perhaps, have shrunk a little in my eyes; and Mrs. Gummidge was waiting at the door as if she had stood there ever since.", "그곳은 예전과 똑같아 보였는데, 다만 어쩌면 내 눈에 조금 작아진 것 같았다. 그리고 거미지 부인은 문간에서 마치 그때부터 줄곧 거기 서 있었던 것처럼 기다리고 있었다."], ["All within was the same, down to the seaweed in the blue mug in my bedroom.", "안은 죄다 그대로였으니, 내 침실의 파란 머그잔에 든 해초까지도 그러했다."], ["I went into the out-house to look about me; and the very same lobsters, crabs, and crawfish possessed by the same desire to pinch the world in general, appeared to be in the same state of conglomeration in the same old corner.", "나는 둘레를 살펴보러 헛간으로 들어갔다. 그러자 세상을 통째로 집게로 집어 보고 싶어 하는 그 똑같은 욕망에 사로잡힌 바로 그 가재며 게며 새우들이, 그 옛 구석에서 그대로 한데 엉겨 있는 모습이었다."]], [["But there was no little Em’ly to be seen, so I asked Mr. Peggotty where she was.", "그러나 어린 에밀리는 보이지 않았다. 그래서 나는 패거티 씨에게 그 애가 어디 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘She’s at school, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"학교에 갔어요, 도련님.\""], ["Peggotty, wiping the heat consequent on the porterage of Peggotty’s box from his forehead; ‘she’ll be home,’ looking at the Dutch clock, ‘in from twenty minutes to half-an-hour’s time.", "패거티 씨가 패거티의 짐 상자를 나른 끝에 이마에 솟은 열기를 닦으며 말했다. \"집에 올 거예요,\" 그는 네덜란드제 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["We all on us feel the loss of her, bless ye!’", "\"이제 한 이십 분에서 반 시간쯤 있으면요. 우리 다 그 애가 없어서 허전하답니다, 거참!\""]], [["Mrs. Gummidge moaned.", "거미지 부인이 끙 신음했다."]], [["‘Cheer up, Mawther!’ cried Mr. Peggotty.", "\"기운 내요, 마더!\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘I feel it more than anybody else,’ said Mrs.", "\"난 누구보다도 더 허전해요.\""], ["Gummidge; ‘I’m a lone lorn creetur’, and she used to be a’most the only thing that didn’t go contrary with me.’", "거미지 부인이 말했다. \"난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세인데, 그 애는 내 뜻을 거스르지 않던 거의 유일한 존재였거든요.\""]], [["Mrs. Gummidge, whimpering and shaking her head, applied herself to blowing the fire. Mr.", "거미지 부인은 훌쩍거리며 고개를 절레절레 흔들더니, 불 피우는 일에 매달렸다. 패거티 씨는 그녀가 그렇게 바삐 움직이는 동안 우리를 둘러보며, 손으로 입가를 가리고 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["Peggotty, looking round upon us while she was so engaged, said in a low voice, which he shaded with his hand: ‘The old ‘un!’ From this I rightly conjectured that no improvement had taken place since my last visit in the state of Mrs. Gummidge’s spirits.", "\"저 노인네!\" 이로써 나는 지난번 방문 이후로 거미지 부인의 심기 상태에 아무 진전이 없었음을 옳게 짐작했다."]], [["Now, the whole place was, or it should have been, quite as delightful a place as ever; and yet it did not impress me in the same way.", "그런데 그곳은 죄다, 적어도 응당, 예나 다름없이 즐거운 곳이었을 텐데도, 나에게는 예전 같은 인상을 주지 못했다."], ["I felt rather disappointed with it.", "나는 오히려 좀 실망스러웠다."], ["Perhaps it was because little Em’ly was not at home.", "어쩌면 어린 에밀리가 집에 없어서였는지도 모른다."], ["I knew the way by which she would come, and presently found myself strolling along the path to meet her.", "나는 그 애가 올 길을 알고 있었고, 곧 그 애를 마중하러 오솔길을 따라 거니는 나 자신을 발견했다."]], [["A figure appeared in the distance before long, and I soon knew it to be Em’ly, who was a little creature still in stature, though she was grown.", "이윽고 저 멀리 한 모습이 나타났고, 나는 곧 그것이 에밀리임을 알아보았는데, 그 애는 자라긴 했어도 여전히 키가 자그마한 아이였다."], ["But when she drew nearer, and I saw her blue eyes looking bluer, and her dimpled face looking brighter, and her whole self prettier and gayer, a curious feeling came over me that made me pretend not to know her, and pass by as if I were looking at something a long way off.", "그러나 그 애가 더 가까이 다가와, 그 파란 두 눈이 더 파랗게 보이고, 보조개 진 얼굴이 더 환해 보이고, 온 모습이 더 예쁘고 명랑해 보이자, 묘한 기분이 나를 사로잡아서, 나는 그 애를 못 본 척하며 멀리 무언가를 바라보는 양 지나쳐 갔다."], ["I have done such a thing since in later life, or I am mistaken.", "그런 짓을 나는 훗날 인생에서도 한 적이 있다. 내 착각이 아니라면."]], [["Little Em’ly didn’t care a bit.", "어린 에밀리는 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["She saw me well enough; but instead of turning round and calling after me, ran away laughing.", "그 애는 나를 똑똑히 보았으면서도, 돌아서서 나를 부르기는커녕 웃으며 달아났다."], ["This obliged me to run after her, and she ran so fast that we were very near the cottage before I caught her.", "그 바람에 나는 그 애를 쫓아가야 했는데, 그 애가 어찌나 빨리 달리던지 내가 그 애를 붙잡았을 때는 오두막에 거의 다 와 있었다."]], [["‘Oh, it’s you, is it?’ said little Em’ly.", "\"어머, 너였구나, 그렇지?\" 어린 에밀리가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, you knew who it was, Em’ly,’ said I.", "\"아니, 누군지 너도 알았잖아, 에밀리.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘And didn’t YOU know who it was?’ said Em’ly.", "\"그럼 넌 누군지 몰랐어?\" 에밀리가 말했다."], ["I was going to kiss her, but she covered her cherry lips with her hands, and said she wasn’t a baby now, and ran away, laughing more than ever, into the house.", "나는 그 애에게 입을 맞추려 했지만, 그 애는 두 손으로 앵두 같은 입술을 가리고는, 자기는 이제 아기가 아니라고 말하더니, 어느 때보다 더 웃으면서 집으로 달아났다."]], [["She seemed to delight in teasing me, which was a change in her I wondered at very much.", "그 애는 나를 놀리는 게 즐거운 듯했는데, 그건 내가 무척 의아하게 여긴 변화였다."], ["The tea table was ready, and our little locker was put out in its old place, but instead of coming to sit by me, she went and bestowed her company upon that grumbling Mrs.", "차탁이 차려졌고, 우리의 작은 사물함이 그 옛 자리에 놓였지만, 그 애는 내 곁에 와 앉기는커녕, 저 투덜대는 거미지 부인에게 가서 함께 있어 주었다."], ["Gummidge: and on Mr. Peggotty’s inquiring why, rumpled her hair all over her face to hide it, and could do nothing but laugh.", "그리고 패거티 씨가 왜 그러느냐고 묻자, 머리카락을 온통 얼굴에 흐트러뜨려 그것을 감추며, 그저 웃기만 할 뿐이었다."]], [["‘A little puss, it is!’ said Mr. Peggotty, patting her with his great hand.", "\"요 깜찍한 것!\" 패거티 씨가 큼직한 손으로 그 애를 토닥이며 말했다."]], [["‘So sh’ is! so sh’ is!’ cried Ham.", "\"그럼요! 그럼요!\" 햄이 외쳤다."], ["‘Mas’r Davy bor’, so sh’ is!’ and he sat and chuckled at her for some time, in a state of mingled admiration and delight, that made his face a burning red.", "\"데이비 도련님, 그럼요!\" 그러고는 앉아서 한동안 그 애를 보며 쿡쿡 웃었는데, 감탄과 기쁨이 뒤섞인 상태라 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아올랐다."]], [["Little Em’ly was spoiled by them all, in fact; and by no one more than Mr. Peggotty himself, whom she could have coaxed into anything, by only going and laying her cheek against his rough whisker.", "사실 어린 에밀리는 그들 모두에게 응석받이로 길러졌고, 그 누구보다도 패거티 씨가 그러했는데, 그 애는 그저 가서 그의 까칠한 구레나룻에 뺨을 갖다 대기만 하면 무슨 일이든 그를 구슬릴 수 있었다."], ["That was my opinion, at least, when I saw her do it; and I held Mr.", "적어도 그 애가 그러는 걸 보았을 때 내 생각은 그러했다."], ["Peggotty to be thoroughly in the right.", "그리고 나는 패거티 씨가 전적으로 옳다고 여겼다."], ["But she was so affectionate and sweet-natured, and had such a pleasant manner of being both sly and shy at once, that she captivated me more than ever.", "하지만 그 애는 어찌나 다정하고 마음씨가 곱던지, 또 능청맞으면서도 수줍어하는 그 사랑스러운 태도를 한꺼번에 지녔던지, 나를 어느 때보다 더 사로잡았다."]], [["She was tender-hearted, too; for when, as we sat round the fire after tea, an allusion was made by Mr. Peggotty over his pipe to the loss I had sustained, the tears stood in her eyes, and she looked at me so kindly across the table, that I felt quite thankful to her.", "그 애는 마음씨도 여렸다. 차를 마신 뒤 우리가 난롯가에 둘러앉았을 때, 패거티 씨가 파이프를 물고 내가 겪은 상실을 넌지시 언급하자, 그 애의 두 눈에 눈물이 고였고, 식탁 너머로 나를 어찌나 다정하게 바라보던지, 나는 그 애에게 무척 고마운 마음이 들었다."]], [["‘Ah!’ said Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Peggotty, taking up her curls, and running them over his hand like water, ‘here’s another orphan, you see, sir.", "패거티 씨가 그 애의 곱슬머리를 집어 들어 물처럼 손 위로 주르륵 흘려보내며 말했다. \"여기 또 한 명의 고아가 있지요, 도련님."], ["And here,’ said Mr.", "그리고 여기,\""], ["Peggotty, giving Ham a backhanded knock in the chest, ‘is another of ‘em, though he don’t look much like it.’", "패거티 씨가 햄의 가슴팍을 손등으로 툭 치며 말했다. \"여기 또 한 명이 있는데, 그렇게는 안 보이지만 말입니다.\""]], [["‘If I had you for my guardian, Mr.", "\"제가 패거티 씨를 후견인으로 두었다면,\""], ["Peggotty,’ said I, shaking my head, ‘I don’t think I should FEEL much like it.’", "나는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그렇게 느껴지지는 않을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["‘Well said, Mas’r Davy bor’!’ cried Ham, in an ecstasy. ‘Hoorah!", "\"옳지, 데이비 도련님!\" 햄이 황홀해하며 외쳤다. \"만세!"], ["Well said!", "옳은 말씀이오!"], ["Nor more you wouldn’t! Hor!", "정말 그렇게 안 느껴질 거요! 하! 하!\""], ["Hor!’--Here he returned Mr. Peggotty’s back-hander, and little Em’ly got up and kissed Mr. Peggotty.", "여기서 그는 패거티 씨의 손등 펀치를 되갚았고, 어린 에밀리는 일어나서 패거티 씨에게 입을 맞췄다."], ["‘And how’s your friend, sir?’ said Mr. Peggotty to me.", "\"그래, 도련님 친구는 잘 있나요?\" 패거티 씨가 나에게 물었다."]], [["‘Steerforth?’ said I.", "\"스티어포스요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘That’s the name!’ cried Mr. Peggotty, turning to Ham.", "\"그 이름이오!\" 패거티 씨가 햄을 돌아보며 외쳤다."], ["‘I knowed it was something in our way.’", "\"우리 식하고 비슷한 거더라니까.\""]], [["‘You said it was Rudderford,’ observed Ham, laughing.", "\"러더포드라고 하셨잖아요.\" 햄이 웃으며 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Well!’ retorted Mr.", "\"흥!\" 패거티 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["Peggotty. ‘And ye steer with a rudder, don’t ye?", "\"그래도 키는 러더, 키로 배를 모는 거 아니냐? 그리 멀지 않아."], ["It ain’t fur off. How is he, sir?’", "그래, 그 친구는 잘 있어요, 도련님?\""]], [["‘He was very well indeed when I came away, Mr. Peggotty.’", "\"제가 떠나올 때는 정말 잘 있었어요, 패거티 씨.\""]], [["‘There’s a friend!’ said Mr. Peggotty, stretching out his pipe.", "\"그게 친구지!\" 패거티 씨가 파이프를 쭉 내밀며 말했다."], ["‘There’s a friend, if you talk of friends!", "\"친구 얘기라면 그게 진짜 친구지!"], ["Why, Lord love my heart alive, if it ain’t a treat to look at him!’", "아이고 맙소사, 그 친구를 보는 것만으로도 눈호강이 따로 없으니!\""]], [["‘He is very handsome, is he not?’ said I, my heart warming with this praise.", "\"그 애는 아주 잘생겼어요, 그렇죠?\" 내가 이 칭찬에 가슴이 따뜻해지며 말했다."]], [["‘Handsome!’ cried Mr. Peggotty.", "\"잘생겼고말고!\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘He stands up to you like--like a--why I don’t know what he don’t stand up to you like.", "\"그 친구는 도련님 앞에 떡 버티고 서면 — 마치 — 아니, 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠네. 어쨌든 떡 버티고 선단 말이오."], ["He’s so bold!’", "어찌나 당찬지!\""]], [["‘Yes!", "\"네!"], ["That’s just his character,’ said I.", "그게 바로 그 애 성격이에요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘He’s as brave as a lion, and you can’t think how frank he is, Mr. Peggotty.’", "\"그 앤 사자처럼 용감하고, 또 얼마나 솔직한지 패거티 씨는 짐작도 못 하실 거예요.\""]], [["‘And I do suppose, now,’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 내가 짐작하기로,\""], ["Peggotty, looking at me through the smoke of his pipe, ‘that in the way of book-larning he’d take the wind out of a’most anything.’", "패거티 씨가 파이프 연기 너머로 나를 보며 말했다. \"책 공부로 치면 그 친구는 거의 뭐든 다 앞지를 거 같은데.\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said I, delighted; ‘he knows everything.", "\"맞아요.\" 내가 기뻐하며 말했다. \"그 앤 뭐든 다 알아요."], ["He is astonishingly clever.’", "놀랄 만큼 똑똑하죠.\""]], [["‘There’s a friend!’ murmured Mr. Peggotty, with a grave toss of his head.", "\"그게 친구지!\" 패거티 씨가 진지하게 고개를 한 번 끄덕이며 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘Nothing seems to cost him any trouble,’ said I.", "\"그 앤 무슨 일이든 힘들이지 않는 것 같아요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘He knows a task if he only looks at it.", "\"과제도 한 번 훑어보기만 하면 알아요."], ["He is the best cricketer you ever saw. He will give you almost as many men as you like at draughts, and beat you easily.’", "그 앤 여태껏 본 적 없는 최고의 크리켓 선수예요. 체커 놀이도 원하는 만큼 말을 양보해 주고도 거뜬히 이긴다고요.\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty gave his head another toss, as much as to say: ‘Of course he will.’", "패거티 씨가 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕였는데, \"당연히 그렇겠지\" 하는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["‘He is such a speaker,’ I pursued, ‘that he can win anybody over; and I don’t know what you’d say if you were to hear him sing, Mr.", "\"그 앤 말솜씨가 어찌나 좋은지,\" 내가 이어 갔다. \"누구든 다 자기편으로 끌어들일 수 있어요."], ["Peggotty.’", "그리고 그 애가 노래하는 걸 들으시면 뭐라고 하실지 모르겠네요, 패거티 씨.\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty gave his head another toss, as much as to say: ‘I have no doubt of it.’", "패거티 씨가 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕였는데, \"그야 의심할 여지가 없지\" 하는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["‘Then, he’s such a generous, fine, noble fellow,’ said I, quite carried away by my favourite theme, ‘that it’s hardly possible to give him as much praise as he deserves.", "\"게다가 그 앤 어찌나 너그럽고 훌륭하고 고결한 친구인지,\" 내가 가장 좋아하는 주제에 푹 빠져 말했다. \"받을 만한 칭찬을 다 해 주기조차 어려워요."], ["I am sure I can never feel thankful enough for the generosity with which he has protected me, so much younger and lower in the school than himself.’", "나보다 훨씬 어리고 학교에서도 한참 아래인 나를 그 애가 너그럽게 지켜 줬으니, 아무리 고마워해도 모자랄 거예요.\""]], [["I was running on, very fast indeed, when my eyes rested on little Em’ly’s face, which was bent forward over the table, listening with the deepest attention, her breath held, her blue eyes sparkling like jewels, and the colour mantling in her cheeks.", "내가 정말이지 신나게 떠들어 대다가, 문득 어린 에밀리의 얼굴에 눈길이 멎었다. 그 애는 식탁 위로 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채, 숨을 죽이고, 파란 두 눈을 보석처럼 반짝이고, 두 뺨에 발그레한 빛을 띠고서, 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 듣고 있었다."], ["She looked so extraordinarily earnest and pretty, that I stopped in a sort of wonder; and they all observed her at the same time, for as I stopped, they laughed and looked at her.", "그 애가 어찌나 유난히 진지하고 예뻐 보였던지, 나는 일종의 놀라움에 말을 뚝 멈췄다. 그러자 모두가 동시에 그 애를 바라보았으니, 내가 멈추자 그들이 웃으며 그 애를 쳐다보았던 것이다."]], [["‘Em’ly is like me,’ said Peggotty, ‘and would like to see him.’", "\"에밀리는 나랑 똑같아서,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그 친구를 보고 싶을 거야.\""]], [["Em’ly was confused by our all observing her, and hung down her head, and her face was covered with blushes.", "에밀리는 우리가 모두 자기를 바라보자 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 고개를 떨구었고, 얼굴은 발갛게 물들었다."], ["Glancing up presently through her stray curls, and seeing that we were all looking at her still (I am sure I, for one, could have looked at her for hours), she ran away, and kept away till it was nearly bedtime.", "이윽고 흘러내린 곱슬머리 사이로 흘끗 올려다보고는, 우리가 여전히 자기를 보고 있는 걸 알아채자(맹세컨대, 적어도 나는 몇 시간이라도 그 애를 바라볼 수 있었을 것이다), 그 애는 달아나서 잠자리에 들 때가 거의 다 되도록 모습을 보이지 않았다."]], [["I lay down in the old little bed in the stern of the boat, and the wind came moaning on across the flat as it had done before.", "나는 배의 고물 쪽에 있는 그 옛 작은 침대에 누웠고, 바람이 예전처럼 평지를 가로질러 끙끙대며 불어왔다."], ["But I could not help fancying, now, that it moaned of those who were gone; and instead of thinking that the sea might rise in the night and float the boat away, I thought of the sea that had risen, since I last heard those sounds, and drowned my happy home.", "그러나 이제 나는 그 바람이 떠나간 사람들을 두고 끙끙대는 거라는 상상을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그리고 밤사이 바닷물이 차올라 배를 둥둥 떠내려가게 할지도 모른다고 생각하는 대신, 내가 지난번에 그 소리를 들은 이후로 차올라서 내 행복한 보금자리를 삼켜 버린 그 바다를 생각했다."], ["I recollect, as the wind and water began to sound fainter in my ears, putting a short clause into my prayers, petitioning that I might grow up to marry little Em’ly, and so dropping lovingly asleep.", "바람과 물소리가 내 귀에 차츰 희미해질 무렵, 내가 자라서 어린 에밀리와 결혼하게 해 달라고 비는 짧은 구절을 기도에 끼워 넣고는, 사랑스럽게 잠에 빠져들었던 기억이 난다."]], [["The days passed pretty much as they had passed before, except--it was a great exception--that little Em’ly and I seldom wandered on the beach now.", "날들은 예전과 거의 다름없이 흘러갔다. 다만 — 이건 큰 예외였는데 — 어린 에밀리와 내가 이제는 좀처럼 바닷가를 거닐지 않는다는 점이 달랐다."], ["She had tasks to learn, and needle-work to do; and was absent during a great part of each day.", "그 애는 배워야 할 과제와 바느질거리가 있어서, 매일 하루의 상당 부분을 자리를 비웠다."], ["But I felt that we should not have had those old wanderings, even if it had been otherwise.", "하지만 사정이 그렇지 않았더라도 우리가 그 옛 거닒을 다시 누리지는 못했으리라는 걸 나는 느꼈다."], ["Wild and full of childish whims as Em’ly was, she was more of a little woman than I had supposed.", "에밀리는 자유분방하고 아이다운 변덕이 가득했어도, 내가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 작은 어른이었다."], ["She seemed to have got a great distance away from me, in little more than a year.", "그 애는 일 년 남짓 만에 나로부터 멀찍이 떨어져 버린 듯했다."], ["She liked me, but she laughed at me, and tormented me; and when I went to meet her, stole home another way, and was laughing at the door when I came back, disappointed.", "그 애는 나를 좋아했지만, 나를 비웃고 나를 괴롭혔다. 내가 그 애를 마중하러 가면 다른 길로 몰래 집에 와 버렸고, 내가 실망해서 돌아오면 문간에서 웃고 있었다."], ["The best times were when she sat quietly at work in the doorway, and I sat on the wooden step at her feet, reading to her.", "가장 좋은 때는 그 애가 문간에서 조용히 일감을 들고 앉아 있고, 내가 그 발치 나무 계단에 앉아 그 애에게 책을 읽어 줄 때였다."], ["It seems to me, at this hour, that I have never seen such sunlight as on those bright April afternoons; that I have never seen such a sunny little figure as I used to see, sitting in the doorway of the old boat; that I have never beheld such sky, such water, such glorified ships sailing away into golden air.", "지금 이 순간 내게는, 그 화창한 사월 오후만큼 눈부신 햇살을 본 적이 없는 것만 같고, 그 옛 배의 문간에 앉아 있던 그 햇살 같은 자그마한 모습만큼 햇살 어린 형상을 본 적이 없는 것만 같고, 그토록 아름다운 하늘과 물과, 황금빛 대기 속으로 미끄러져 떠나가던 그 영광스러운 배들을 본 적이 없는 것만 같다."]], [["On the very first evening after our arrival, Mr. Barkis appeared in an exceedingly vacant and awkward condition, and with a bundle of oranges tied up in a handkerchief.", "우리가 도착한 바로 그 첫날 저녁, 바키스 씨가 지극히 멍하고 어색한 모습으로, 손수건에 싸맨 오렌지 꾸러미를 들고 나타났다."], ["As he made no allusion of any kind to this property, he was supposed to have left it behind him by accident when he went away; until Ham, running after him to restore it, came back with the information that it was intended for Peggotty.", "그가 이 물건에 대해 어떤 언급도 하지 않았으므로, 떠날 때 깜빡 잊고 두고 간 거라고 다들 여겼다. 그런데 햄이 그것을 돌려주려고 그를 뒤쫓아 갔다가, 그게 패거티에게 주려던 거라는 소식을 가지고 돌아왔다."], ["After that occasion he appeared every evening at exactly the same hour, and always with a little bundle, to which he never alluded, and which he regularly put behind the door and left there.", "그 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 매일 저녁 어김없이 똑같은 시각에, 늘 작은 꾸러미를 들고 나타났는데, 그것에 대해 결코 언급하지 않고, 으레 문 뒤에 놓아두고 가곤 했다."], ["These offerings of affection were of a most various and eccentric description.", "이 애정의 선물들은 더없이 다채롭고 별난 종류였다."], ["Among them I remember a double set of pigs’ trotters, a huge pin-cushion, half a bushel or so of apples, a pair of jet earrings, some Spanish onions, a box of dominoes, a canary bird and cage, and a leg of pickled pork.", "그중에서 내가 기억하는 것으로는 돼지 족발 두 벌, 커다란 바늘꽂이, 사과 반 부셀쯤, 흑옥 귀고리 한 쌍, 스페인 양파 몇 개, 도미노 한 통, 새장에 든 카나리아 한 마리, 그리고 절인 돼지 다리 하나가 있었다."]], [["Mr. Barkis’s wooing, as I remember it, was altogether of a peculiar kind.", "내가 기억하기로, 바키스 씨의 구애는 도무지 별스러운 종류였다."], ["He very seldom said anything; but would sit by the fire in much the same attitude as he sat in his cart, and stare heavily at Peggotty, who was opposite.", "그는 좀처럼 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그저 자기 마차에 앉아 있을 때와 거의 똑같은 자세로 난롯가에 앉아, 맞은편에 있는 패거티를 묵직하게 바라보곤 했다."], ["One night, being, as I suppose, inspired by love, he made a dart at the bit of wax-candle she kept for her thread, and put it in his waistcoat-pocket and carried it off.", "어느 밤, 짐작건대 사랑에 사로잡혔는지, 그는 그녀가 실에 쓰려고 두었던 밀랍 양초 토막에 달려들어, 그것을 조끼 주머니에 넣고 가져가 버렸다."], ["After that, his great delight was to produce it when it was wanted, sticking to the lining of his pocket, in a partially melted state, and pocket it again when it was done with.", "그 뒤로 그의 큰 즐거움은, 양초가 필요할 때면 주머니 안감에 들러붙어 반쯤 녹은 그것을 꺼내 보였다가, 다 쓰고 나면 다시 주머니에 넣는 것이었다."], ["He seemed to enjoy himself very much, and not to feel at all called upon to talk.", "그는 무척 즐거워하는 듯했고, 말을 해야 한다는 의무감은 전혀 느끼지 않는 듯했다."], ["Even when he took Peggotty out for a walk on the flats, he had no uneasiness on that head, I believe; contenting himself with now and then asking her if she was pretty comfortable; and I remember that sometimes, after he was gone, Peggotty would throw her apron over her face, and laugh for half-an-hour.", "패거티를 데리고 평지로 산책을 나갈 때조차, 내 생각에 그는 그 점에 대해 아무 불안이 없었다. 이따금 그녀에게 꽤 편안하냐고 물어보는 것으로 만족했으니까. 그리고 그가 가고 나면 패거티가 앞치마를 얼굴에 뒤집어쓰고 반 시간이나 웃곤 했던 기억이 난다."], ["Indeed, we were all more or less amused, except that miserable Mrs. Gummidge, whose courtship would appear to have been of an exactly parallel nature, she was so continually reminded by these transactions of the old one.", "정말이지 우리는 모두 어느 정도 재미있어했는데, 다만 저 가엾은 거미지 부인만은 예외였으니, 그녀의 구애도 꼭 이와 똑같은 식이었던 모양이라, 이런 일들을 볼 때마다 끊임없이 옛 구애가 떠올랐기 때문이다."]], [["At length, when the term of my visit was nearly expired, it was given out that Peggotty and Mr. Barkis were going to make a day’s holiday together, and that little Em’ly and I were to accompany them.", "마침내 내 방문 기간이 거의 끝나갈 무렵, 패거티와 바키스 씨가 함께 하루 휴가를 보내기로 했고, 어린 에밀리와 나도 그들과 동행하기로 했다는 발표가 나왔다."], ["I had but a broken sleep the night before, in anticipation of the pleasure of a whole day with Em’ly.", "나는 에밀리와 온종일을 보낼 기쁨을 미리 그리느라 그 전날 밤 잠을 설쳤다."], ["We were all astir betimes in the morning; and while we were yet at breakfast, Mr. Barkis appeared in the distance, driving a chaise-cart towards the object of his affections.", "우리는 모두 아침 일찍 부산을 떨었다. 그리고 우리가 아직 아침을 먹고 있는데, 바키스 씨가 저 멀리서 사랑하는 사람을 향해 이륜마차를 몰고 나타났다."]], [["Peggotty was dressed as usual, in her neat and quiet mourning; but Mr.", "패거티는 늘 그렇듯 단정하고 차분한 상복 차림이었다."], ["Barkis bloomed in a new blue coat, of which the tailor had given him such good measure, that the cuffs would have rendered gloves unnecessary in the coldest weather, while the collar was so high that it pushed his hair up on end on the top of his head.", "그러나 바키스 씨는 새 파란색 외투를 입고 활짝 피어 있었는데, 재단사가 어찌나 넉넉하게 치수를 잡았던지, 그 소맷부리는 아무리 추운 날씨에도 장갑이 필요 없을 정도였고, 옷깃은 어찌나 높던지 머리카락을 머리 꼭대기로 곧추세워 밀어 올릴 지경이었다."], ["His bright buttons, too, were of the largest size.", "게다가 그의 반짝이는 단추들도 가장 큰 치수였다."], ["Rendered complete by drab pantaloons and a buff waistcoat, I thought Mr. Barkis a phenomenon of respectability.", "거기에 황갈색 바지와 담황색 조끼로 완성된 차림을 보니, 나는 바키스 씨가 점잖음의 화신 같다고 생각했다."]], [["When we were all in a bustle outside the door, I found that Mr. Peggotty was prepared with an old shoe, which was to be thrown after us for luck, and which he offered to Mrs. Gummidge for that purpose.", "우리가 모두 문밖에서 부산을 떨고 있을 때, 나는 패거티 씨가 낡은 신발 한 짝을 준비해 둔 것을 알게 되었는데, 행운을 빌며 우리 뒤로 던질 신발이었고, 그가 거미지 부인에게 그 일을 맡아 달라고 내밀었다."]], [["‘No. It had better be done by somebody else, Dan’l,’ said Mrs.", "\"싫어요. 그건 다른 사람이 하는 게 낫겠어요, 댄얼.\""], ["Gummidge. ‘I’m a lone lorn creetur’ myself, and everythink that reminds me of creetur’s that ain’t lone and lorn, goes contrary with me.’", "거미지 부인이 말했다. \"난 외롭고 쓸쓸한 신세인데, 외롭지도 쓸쓸하지도 않은 사람들을 떠올리게 하는 건 죄다 내 뜻을 거스른답니다.\""]], [["‘Come, old gal!’ cried Mr. Peggotty.", "\"이봐요, 할멈!\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Take and heave it.’", "\"어서 집어 들고 던져요.\""]], [["‘No, Dan’l,’ returned Mrs. Gummidge, whimpering and shaking her head.", "\"싫어요, 댄얼.\" 거미지 부인이 훌쩍거리며 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 대답했다."], ["‘If I felt less, I could do more.", "\"내가 덜 느꼈으면 더 할 수 있을 텐데."], ["You don’t feel like me, Dan’l; thinks don’t go contrary with you, nor you with them; you had better do it yourself.’", "당신은 나처럼 느끼지 않잖아요, 댄얼. 당신한테는 일이 거스르지도 않고, 당신도 일을 거스르지 않으니까. 당신이 직접 하는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["But here Peggotty, who had been going about from one to another in a hurried way, kissing everybody, called out from the cart, in which we all were by this time (Em’ly and I on two little chairs, side by side), that Mrs. Gummidge must do it.", "그런데 이때 패거티가, 그동안 부산하게 이 사람 저 사람을 오가며 모두에게 입을 맞추다가, 이제 우리가 모두 올라타 있던 마차에서(에밀리와 나는 작은 의자 두 개에 나란히 앉아 있었다) 거미지 부인이 꼭 그 일을 해야 한다고 소리쳤다. 그래서 거미지 부인이 그 일을 했다."], ["So Mrs. Gummidge did it; and, I am sorry to relate, cast a damp upon the festive character of our departure, by immediately bursting into tears, and sinking subdued into the arms of Ham, with the declaration that she knowed she was a burden, and had better be carried to the House at once.", "그런데 안타깝게도 그녀는 곧장 울음을 터뜨리고는, 자기는 짐덩어리인 줄 알고 있으니 당장 구빈원으로 데려가는 게 낫겠다고 선언하며 햄의 품에 풀썩 무너져 안기는 바람에, 우리 출발의 잔치 같은 분위기에 찬물을 끼얹고 말았다. 그건 정말이지 그럴듯한 생각이라, 햄이 실행에 옮겨도 좋았겠다고 나는 진심으로 여겼다."], ["Which I really thought was a sensible idea, that Ham might have acted on.", "* 구빈원: 가난한 사람을 수용해 돌보던 옛 영국의 시설."]], [["Away we went, however, on our holiday excursion; and the first thing we did was to stop at a church, where Mr. Barkis tied the horse to some rails, and went in with Peggotty, leaving little Em’ly and me alone in the chaise.", "그래도 우리는 휴가 나들이를 떠났다. 우리가 맨 처음 한 일은 어느 교회 앞에 멈춰 선 것이었는데, 거기서 바키스 씨는 말을 난간에 매어 두고 패거티와 함께 안으로 들어가, 어린 에밀리와 나만 마차에 남겨 두었다."], ["I took that occasion to put my arm round Em’ly’s waist, and propose that as I was going away so very soon now, we should determine to be very affectionate to one another, and very happy, all day.", "나는 그 틈을 타서 에밀리의 허리에 팔을 두르고, 이제 곧 내가 떠날 테니, 우리 둘이 하루 종일 서로 무척 다정하고 무척 행복하게 지내기로 다짐하자고 제안했다."], ["Little Em’ly consenting, and allowing me to kiss her, I became desperate; informing her, I recollect, that I never could love another, and that I was prepared to shed the blood of anybody who should aspire to her affections.", "어린 에밀리가 동의하며 나에게 입맞춤을 허락하자, 나는 무모해져서, 결코 다른 누구도 사랑할 수 없으며, 누구든 그 애의 사랑을 넘본다면 그자의 피를 흘리게 할 각오가 되어 있다고 일러 준 기억이 난다."]], [["How merry little Em’ly made herself about it!", "어린 에밀리는 그 일을 두고 얼마나 즐거워했던지!"], ["With what a demure assumption of being immensely older and wiser than I, the fairy little woman said I was ‘a silly boy’; and then laughed so charmingly that I forgot the pain of being called by that disparaging name, in the pleasure of looking at her.", "나보다 한없이 나이 많고 슬기로운 척 새침하게 굴면서, 그 요정 같은 작은 여인은 내가 \"바보 같은 사내애\"라고 말했다. 그러고는 어찌나 사랑스럽게 웃던지, 나는 그 깎아내리는 이름으로 불린 아픔을, 그 애를 바라보는 기쁨 속에 까맣게 잊고 말았다."]], [["Mr. Barkis and Peggotty were a good while in the church, but came out at last, and then we drove away into the country.", "바키스 씨와 패거티는 교회 안에 꽤 오래 있다가 마침내 나왔고, 그러고 나서 우리는 시골 쪽으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["As we were going along, Mr. Barkis turned to me, and said, with a wink,--by the by, I should hardly have thought, before, that he could wink:", "가는 길에 바키스 씨가 나를 돌아보며 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. 그런데 말이지, 그가 윙크를 할 수 있는 사람일 거라곤 그전에는 도무지 생각지 못했다."]], [["‘What name was it as I wrote up in the cart?’", "\"내가 마차에 써 붙였던 이름이 뭐였더라?\""]], [["‘Clara Peggotty,’ I answered.", "\"클라라 패거티요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘What name would it be as I should write up now, if there was a tilt here?’", "\"여기에 차양이 있다면, 지금 써 붙일 이름은 뭐가 되겠어?\""]], [["‘Clara Peggotty, again?’ I suggested.", "\"또 클라라 패거티요?\" 내가 넘겨짚었다."]], [["‘Clara Peggotty BARKIS!’ he returned, and burst into a roar of laughter that shook the chaise.", "\"클라라 패거티 바키스지!\" 그가 받아치더니, 마차가 다 흔들리도록 와하하 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["In a word, they were married, and had gone into the church for no other purpose.", "한마디로, 두 사람은 결혼했고, 다른 목적 없이 오직 그 일을 위해 교회에 들어갔던 것이다."], ["Peggotty was resolved that it should be quietly done; and the clerk had given her away, and there had been no witnesses of the ceremony.", "패거티는 그 일을 조용히 치르기로 마음먹었던 터라, 교회 서기가 그녀를 신랑에게 인도했고, 그 예식에는 증인도 없었다."], ["She was a little confused when Mr. Barkis made this abrupt announcement of their union, and could not hug me enough in token of her unimpaired affection; but she soon became herself again, and said she was very glad it was over.", "바키스 씨가 두 사람의 결합을 이렇게 불쑥 발표하자 그녀는 잠시 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며, 변함없는 애정의 표시로 나를 아무리 끌어안아도 모자라 했다. 하지만 그녀는 곧 본래의 모습으로 돌아와, 일이 끝나서 무척 기쁘다고 말했다."]], [["We drove to a little inn in a by-road, where we were expected, and where we had a very comfortable dinner, and passed the day with great satisfaction.", "우리는 샛길에 있는 작은 여관으로 마차를 몰았는데, 우리를 기다리고 있던 그곳에서 아주 푸짐한 점심을 먹고, 더없이 흡족하게 하루를 보냈다."], ["If Peggotty had been married every day for the last ten years, she could hardly have been more at her ease about it; it made no sort of difference in her: she was just the same as ever, and went out for a stroll with little Em’ly and me before tea, while Mr. Barkis philosophically smoked his pipe, and enjoyed himself, I suppose, with the contemplation of his happiness.", "패거티가 지난 십 년 동안 날마다 결혼을 했다 한들, 이보다 더 결혼을 대수롭지 않게 여길 수는 없었을 것이다. 그녀에게는 아무런 변화도 없었으니, 그녀는 여전히 예전 그대로였고, 차를 마시기 전에 어린 에밀리와 나를 데리고 산책을 나갔으며, 그동안 바키스 씨는 철학자처럼 파이프를 피우며, 짐작건대 자기 행복을 음미하며 즐거워했다."], ["If so, it sharpened his appetite; for I distinctly call to mind that, although he had eaten a good deal of pork and greens at dinner, and had finished off with a fowl or two, he was obliged to have cold boiled bacon for tea, and disposed of a large quantity without any emotion.", "만일 그랬다면 그것이 그의 식욕을 돋우었던 모양이다. 분명히 기억하는데, 그는 점심때 돼지고기와 채소를 잔뜩 먹고 닭 한두 마리로 마무리까지 했는데도, 차를 마실 때 또 차가운 삶은 베이컨을 먹어야 했고, 아무런 감흥 없이 어마어마한 양을 해치웠으니 말이다."]], [["I have often thought, since, what an odd, innocent, out-of-the-way kind of wedding it must have been!", "그 뒤로 나는 그게 얼마나 별나고 순박하고 색다른 결혼식이었을까 하고 자주 생각하곤 했다! 우리는 어두워진 직후 다시 마차에 올라, 별들을 올려다보며 별 이야기를 나누면서 아늑하게 돌아왔다."], ["We got into the chaise again soon after dark, and drove cosily back, looking up at the stars, and talking about them.", "나는 별에 관한 으뜸가는 해설자였고, 바키스 씨의 마음을 놀라울 정도로 활짝 열어 주었다. 나는 내가 아는 걸 죄다 일러 주었는데, 내가 어쩌다 마음먹고 전해 주는 거라면 그는 무엇이든 곧이곧대로 믿었을 것이다."], ["I was their chief exponent, and opened Mr. Barkis’s mind to an amazing extent.", "그는 내 재능을 깊이 떠받들고 있어서, 바로 그 자리에서 내가 듣는 데서 자기 아내에게 내가 \"어린 로셔스\"라고 알려 주었다."], ["I told him all I knew, but he would have believed anything I might have taken it into my head to impart to him; for he had a profound veneration for my abilities, and informed his wife in my hearing, on that very occasion, that I was ‘a young Roeshus’--by which I think he meant prodigy.", "그가 한 말은 신동이라는 뜻이었지 싶다. * 로셔스: 고대 로마의 이름난 희극 배우. 뛰어난 재능을 가진 사람을 빗대는 말로 쓰였다."]], [["When we had exhausted the subject of the stars, or rather when I had exhausted the mental faculties of Mr. Barkis, little Em’ly and I made a cloak of an old wrapper, and sat under it for the rest of the journey.", "별 이야기가 다 동나자, 아니 정확히 말하면 내가 바키스 씨의 지력을 다 동내고 나자, 어린 에밀리와 나는 낡은 덮개를 망토 삼아, 남은 여정 동안 그 밑에 들어가 앉았다. 아, 나는 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했던가!"], ["Ah, how I loved her! What happiness (I thought) if we were married, and were going away anywhere to live among the trees and in the fields, never growing older, never growing wiser, children ever, rambling hand in hand through sunshine and among flowery meadows, laying down our heads on moss at night, in a sweet sleep of purity and peace, and buried by the birds when we were dead!", "우리가 결혼해서 어딘가로 떠나 나무들 사이에서, 들판에서 살며, 결코 나이도 들지 않고, 결코 영악해지지도 않고, 영원히 아이인 채로, 손에 손을 잡고 햇살 속을, 꽃 핀 풀밭 사이를 거닐고, 밤이면 이끼에 머리를 누이고 순수하고 평화로운 단잠에 들고, 우리가 죽으면 새들이 우리를 묻어 준다면 (나는 그렇게 생각했다) 얼마나 행복할까!"], ["Some such picture, with no real world in it, bright with the light of our innocence, and vague as the stars afar off, was in my mind all the way.", "현실 세계라곤 없고, 우리 순결의 빛으로 환하고, 저 멀리 별처럼 아련한 그런 어떤 그림이 가는 내내 내 마음속에 있었다."], ["I am glad to think there were two such guileless hearts at Peggotty’s marriage as little Em’ly’s and mine.", "패거티의 결혼식에 어린 에밀리와 나의 것 같은 그렇게 순진무구한 두 마음이 있었다는 걸 생각하면 나는 기쁘다."], ["I am glad to think the Loves and Graces took such airy forms in its homely procession.", "그 소박한 행렬 속에서 사랑과 우아의 정령들이 그토록 가뿐한 모습을 띠었다는 걸 생각하면 나는 기쁘다."]], [["Well, we came to the old boat again in good time at night; and there Mr.", "자, 우리는 밤이 늦지 않게 그 옛 배에 다시 다다랐다."], ["and Mrs. Barkis bade us good-bye, and drove away snugly to their own home.", "그리고 거기서 바키스 부부는 우리에게 작별을 고하고, 아늑하게 자기네 집으로 마차를 몰고 갔다."], ["I felt then, for the first time, that I had lost Peggotty.", "그제야 나는 처음으로, 내가 패거티를 잃었다는 걸 느꼈다."], ["I should have gone to bed with a sore heart indeed under any other roof but that which sheltered little Em’ly’s head.", "어린 에밀리의 머리를 지붕 아래 품어 주는 그 집이 아닌 다른 어떤 지붕 아래였다면, 나는 정말이지 쓰라린 마음으로 잠자리에 들었을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty and Ham knew what was in my thoughts as well as I did, and were ready with some supper and their hospitable faces to drive it away.", "패거티 씨와 햄은 내 마음속에 무엇이 들었는지 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었고, 그것을 쫓아 줄 야식과 살가운 얼굴을 준비해 두고 있었다."], ["Little Em’ly came and sat beside me on the locker for the only time in all that visit; and it was altogether a wonderful close to a wonderful day.", "어린 에밀리가 와서, 그 방문 내내 단 한 번, 사물함 위 내 곁에 앉았다. 그것은 더없이 멋진 하루를 마무리하는 더없이 멋진 끝맺음이었다."]], [["It was a night tide; and soon after we went to bed, Mr. Peggotty and Ham went out to fish.", "만조의 밤이었다. 그래서 우리가 잠자리에 든 직후, 패거티 씨와 햄이 고기잡이를 하러 나갔다."], ["I felt very brave at being left alone in the solitary house, the protector of Em’ly and Mrs. Gummidge, and only wished that a lion or a serpent, or any ill-disposed monster, would make an attack upon us, that I might destroy him, and cover myself with glory.", "나는 그 외딴집에 혼자 남아 에밀리와 거미지 부인의 보호자가 된 것에 무척 용감해진 기분이었고, 그저 사자나 뱀이나 무슨 못된 괴물이라도 우리를 공격해 와서, 내가 그것을 무찌르고 영광에 휩싸였으면 하고 바랄 뿐이었다."], ["But as nothing of the sort happened to be walking about on Yarmouth flats that night, I provided the best substitute I could by dreaming of dragons until morning.", "하지만 그날 밤 야머스 평지를 그런 게 어슬렁대고 다닐 리가 없었으므로, 나는 그 대신 할 수 있는 최선의 것을 마련했으니, 아침이 올 때까지 용들 꿈을 꾼 것이다."]], [["With morning came Peggotty; who called to me, as usual, under my window as if Mr. Barkis the carrier had been from first to last a dream too.", "아침과 함께 패거티가 왔다. 그녀는 여느 때처럼 내 창문 아래에서 나를 불렀는데, 마치 마부 바키스 씨가 처음부터 끝까지 한낱 꿈이기라도 했던 듯했다."], ["After breakfast she took me to her own home, and a beautiful little home it was.", "아침을 먹고 나서 그녀는 나를 자기 집으로 데려갔는데, 그건 참으로 아름다운 작은 보금자리였다."], ["Of all the moveables in it, I must have been impressed by a certain old bureau of some dark wood in the parlour (the tile-floored kitchen was the general sitting-room), with a retreating top which opened, let down, and became a desk, within which was a large quarto edition of Foxe’s Book of Martyrs.", "그 안의 온갖 세간 중에서, 거실(타일을 깐 부엌이 거실 노릇을 했다)에 있던 짙은 색 나무로 만든 어떤 낡은 책상에 나는 깊은 인상을 받았던 게 틀림없다. 그 책상은 윗부분이 뒤로 물러나며 열려서 아래로 내려뜨리면 쓰는 책상이 되었는데, 그 안에는 폭스의 『순교자 열전』 사절판 큰 책이 들어 있었다."], ["This precious volume, of which I do not recollect one word, I immediately discovered and immediately applied myself to; and I never visited the house afterwards, but I kneeled on a chair, opened the casket where this gem was enshrined, spread my arms over the desk, and fell to devouring the book afresh.", "한마디도 기억나지 않는 이 귀한 책을 나는 곧장 발견하고 곧장 거기에 빠져들었다. 그 뒤로 그 집을 찾을 때마다 나는 어김없이 의자에 무릎을 꿇고, 이 보석이 모셔진 함을 열고, 책상 위에 두 팔을 펼치고는, 그 책을 다시 게걸스레 파먹기 시작했다."], ["I was chiefly edified, I am afraid, by the pictures, which were numerous, and represented all kinds of dismal horrors; but the Martyrs and Peggotty’s house have been inseparable in my mind ever since, and are now.", "안타깝게도 내가 주로 감화받은 건 그림이었는데, 그 그림들은 수도 많았고 온갖 음울한 공포를 그리고 있었다. 하지만 그 순교자들과 패거티의 집은 그 이후로 내 마음속에서 떼려야 뗄 수 없게 되었고, 지금도 그러하다."]], [["I took leave of Mr. Peggotty, and Ham, and Mrs.", "나는 그날 패거티 씨와 햄과 거미지 부인과 어린 에밀리에게 작별을 고했다."], ["Gummidge, and little Em’ly, that day; and passed the night at Peggotty’s, in a little room in the roof (with the Crocodile Book on a shelf by the bed’s head) which was to be always mine, Peggotty said, and should always be kept for me in exactly the same state.", "그리고 패거티네 집, 지붕 아래 작은 방(머리맡 선반에는 그 악어 책이 놓여 있었다)에서 밤을 보냈는데, 그 방은 패거티 말로는 늘 내 것이 될 것이고, 늘 꼭 같은 상태로 나를 위해 간직해 두겠노라 했다."]], [["‘Young or old, Davy dear, as long as I am alive and have this house over my head,’ said Peggotty, ‘you shall find it as if I expected you here directly minute.", "\"젊든 늙든, 데이비, 내가 살아 있고 이 집이 내 머리 위에 있는 한,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"넌 마치 내가 당장 이 순간 널 기다리고 있기라도 한 것처럼 이 방을 보게 될 거야."], ["I shall keep it every day, as I used to keep your old little room, my darling; and if you was to go to China, you might think of it as being kept just the same, all the time you were away.’", "내가 네 옛 작은 방을 간직하던 것처럼, 우리 아가, 날마다 이 방을 간직할 거란다. 그러니 네가 중국에 간다 해도, 떠나 있는 내내 이 방이 꼭 같이 간직되고 있다고 생각하면 돼.\""]], [["I felt the truth and constancy of my dear old nurse, with all my heart, and thanked her as well as I could.", "나는 사랑하는 우리 옛 유모의 진심과 한결같음을 온 마음으로 느꼈고, 할 수 있는 한 잘 고마움을 전했다."], ["That was not very well, for she spoke to me thus, with her arms round my neck, in the morning, and I was going home in the morning, and I went home in the morning, with herself and Mr.", "그게 그리 잘되지는 않았는데, 그녀가 아침에 두 팔로 내 목을 감싼 채 그렇게 말했고, 나도 아침에 집으로 가는 길이었고, 결국 나는 아침에 그녀와 바키스 씨와 함께 마차를 타고 집으로 갔기 때문이다."], ["Barkis in the cart. They left me at the gate, not easily or lightly; and it was a strange sight to me to see the cart go on, taking Peggotty away, and leaving me under the old elm-trees looking at the house, in which there was no face to look on mine with love or liking any more.", "그들은 문 앞에서 나를 내려놓았는데, 쉽사리도 가벼이도 그러지 못했다. 그리고 마차가 패거티를 싣고 떠나가며, 나를 그 옛 느릅나무들 아래 남겨 두고, 이제 더는 사랑이나 호의로 내 얼굴을 마주 봐 줄 얼굴 하나 없는 그 집을 바라보게 하는 광경은, 나에게 참 묘한 풍경이었다."]], [["And now I fell into a state of neglect, which I cannot look back upon without compassion.", "이제 나는 방치된 상태에 빠져들었는데, 그것은 지금 돌이켜 보면 연민 없이는 떠올릴 수 없는 일이다."], ["I fell at once into a solitary condition,--apart from all friendly notice, apart from the society of all other boys of my own age, apart from all companionship but my own spiritless thoughts,--which seems to cast its gloom upon this paper as I write.", "나는 단숨에 외톨이 처지에 빠지고 말았다. 다정한 관심에서도 동떨어지고, 또래 사내아이들 모두의 무리에서도 동떨어지고, 내 기죽은 생각 말고는 어떤 벗에서도 동떨어진 그 처지는, 내가 이 글을 쓰는 지금에도 이 종이 위에 그 어둠을 드리우는 것만 같다."]], [["What would I have given, to have been sent to the hardest school that ever was kept!--to have been taught something, anyhow, anywhere!", "여태껏 세워진 그 어떤 가장 혹독한 학교에라도 보내졌더라면, 나는 무엇이든 내주었을 것이다! 어떻게든, 어디서든, 무엇이라도 배웠더라면!"], ["No such hope dawned upon me.", "그런 희망은 나에게 동터 오지 않았다."], ["They disliked me; and they sullenly, sternly, steadily, overlooked me.", "그들은 나를 싫어했다. 그리고 시무룩하게, 매정하게, 한결같이 나를 못 본 체했다."], ["I think Mr. Murdstone’s means were straitened at about this time; but it is little to the purpose.", "내 생각에 머드스톤 씨의 형편은 그 무렵 쪼들리고 있었던 듯하다. 하지만 그건 별로 상관없는 일이다."], ["He could not bear me; and in putting me from him he tried, as I believe, to put away the notion that I had any claim upon him--and succeeded.", "그는 나를 견딜 수 없어 했고, 나를 곁에서 떼어 놓음으로써, 내가 그에게 무슨 권리라도 주장할 거라는 생각을 떨쳐 버리려 했던 거라고 나는 믿는다. 그리고 그는 성공했다."]], [["I was not actively ill-used.", "나는 적극적으로 학대당하지는 않았다."], ["I was not beaten, or starved; but the wrong that was done to me had no intervals of relenting, and was done in a systematic, passionless manner.", "매를 맞거나 굶주리지도 않았다. 하지만 나에게 가해진 부당함에는 누그러지는 틈이라곤 없었고, 그것은 조직적이고 무덤덤한 방식으로 행해졌다."], ["Day after day, week after week, month after month, I was coldly neglected.", "날이면 날마다, 주가 가고 또 주가 가고, 달이 가고 또 달이 가도록, 나는 차갑게 방치되었다."], ["I wonder sometimes, when I think of it, what they would have done if I had been taken with an illness; whether I should have lain down in my lonely room, and languished through it in my usual solitary way, or whether anybody would have helped me out.", "그것을 생각할 때면 나는 가끔 궁금해진다. 내가 병에라도 걸렸다면 그들은 어찌했을까. 내가 그 외로운 방에 드러누워 여느 때의 그 외톨이 신세로 병을 앓아 넘겼을지, 아니면 누구라도 나를 헤어나게 도와주었을지."]], [["When Mr. and Miss Murdstone were at home, I took my meals with them; in their absence, I ate and drank by myself.", "머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 집에 있을 때면 나는 그들과 함께 식사를 했고, 그들이 없을 때면 혼자서 먹고 마셨다."], ["At all times I lounged about the house and neighbourhood quite disregarded, except that they were jealous of my making any friends: thinking, perhaps, that if I did, I might complain to someone. For this reason, though Mr.", "어느 때든 나는 집과 동네를 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채 어슬렁댔는데, 다만 그들은 내가 친구를 사귀는 것만은 시샘했다. 어쩌면 내가 친구를 사귀면 누군가에게 하소연할까 봐 그랬는지도 모른다."], ["Chillip often asked me to go and see him (he was a widower, having, some years before that, lost a little small light-haired wife, whom I can just remember connecting in my own thoughts with a pale tortoise-shell cat), it was but seldom that I enjoyed the happiness of passing an afternoon in his closet of a surgery; reading some book that was new to me, with the smell of the whole Pharmacopoeia coming up my nose, or pounding something in a mortar under his mild directions.", "그런 까닭에, 칠립 씨가 자주 나에게 놀러 오라고 청했는데도(그는 홀아비였는데, 몇 해 전에 머리 빛깔이 옅은 자그마하고 가냘픈 아내를 잃었고, 나는 그녀를 떠올릴 때면 어렴풋이 창백한 거북등무늬 고양이와 연관 짓곤 했다), 나는 그의 골방 같은 진료실에서 오후 한나절을 보내는 행복을 좀처럼 누리지 못했다. 그 진료실에서는 약전에 실린 온갖 약 냄새가 코를 찌르는 가운데 나에게 새로운 책을 읽거나, 그의 부드러운 지시에 따라 절구에 무언가를 빻곤 했던 것이다. * 약전: 약품의 종류·품질·제법 따위를 규정한 책."]], [["For the same reason, added no doubt to the old dislike of her, I was seldom allowed to visit Peggotty.", "같은 까닭에, 게다가 분명 그녀가 예전부터 나를 싫어한 탓도 보태어져, 나는 패거티를 만나러 가는 것이 좀처럼 허락되지 않았다."], ["Faithful to her promise, she either came to see me, or met me somewhere near, once every week, and never empty-handed; but many and bitter were the disappointments I had, in being refused permission to pay a visit to her at her house.", "패거티는 약속에 충실하여, 일주일에 한 번씩 나를 보러 오거나 우리 집 근처 어딘가에서 나를 만났고, 결코 빈손으로 온 적이 없었다. 하지만 그녀의 집으로 찾아가는 게 허락되지 않아 겪은 실망은 많고도 쓰라렸다. 그래도 아주 가끔, 오랜 간격을 두고 거기 가도 좋다는 허락이 떨어지기도 했다."], ["Some few times, however, at long intervals, I was allowed to go there; and then I found out that Mr.", "그러면 나는 바키스 씨가 다소 구두쇠라는 걸, 아니 패거티가 곱게 둘러 말한 대로 \"좀 인색하다는\""], ["Barkis was something of a miser, or as Peggotty dutifully expressed it, was ‘a little near’, and kept a heap of money in a box under his bed, which he pretended was only full of coats and trousers.", "걸, 그리고 침대 밑 상자에 돈을 한 무더기 쟁여 두고는 외투랑 바지만 가득하다고 시치미를 뗀다는 걸 알게 되었다. 그의 재물은 이 궤짝 속에서 어찌나 끈질기게 점잔 빼며 숨어 있던지, 아주 적은 액수조차 꾀를 부려야만 꾀어낼 수 있었다."], ["In this coffer, his riches hid themselves with such a tenacious modesty, that the smallest instalments could only be tempted out by artifice; so that Peggotty had to prepare a long and elaborate scheme, a very Gunpowder Plot, for every Saturday’s expenses.", "그래서 패거티는 토요일마다 쓸 돈을 마련하느라 길고 정교한 계략, 그야말로 화약 음모 같은 것을 짜야 했다. * 화약 음모: 1605년 영국에서 가톨릭 세력이 국회의사당을 폭파하려다 발각된 사건. 치밀한 모의를 빗대는 말로 쓰인다."]], [["All this time I was so conscious of the waste of any promise I had given, and of my being utterly neglected, that I should have been perfectly miserable, I have no doubt, but for the old books.", "이 모든 시간 동안 나는 내가 받은 어떤 약속도 헛되이 흘러가고 있다는 것, 그리고 내가 철저히 방치되고 있다는 것을 너무도 절절히 느끼고 있어서, 그 옛 책들이 없었더라면 의심할 여지 없이 완전히 비참했을 것이다."], ["They were my only comfort; and I was as true to them as they were to me, and read them over and over I don’t know how many times more.", "그 책들은 내 유일한 위안이었다. 나는 책들이 나에게 그러했듯 책들에게 충실했고, 몇 번이나 더인지 셀 수도 없을 만큼 그것들을 읽고 또 읽었다."]], [["I now approach a period of my life, which I can never lose the remembrance of, while I remember anything: and the recollection of which has often, without my invocation, come before me like a ghost, and haunted happier times.", "이제 나는 내 인생에서, 무엇이든 기억하는 한 결코 그 기억을 잃을 수 없는 한 시기에 다가선다. 그 기억은 내가 부르지 않아도 종종 유령처럼 내 앞에 나타나, 더 행복한 시절을 따라다니며 괴롭혔다."]], [["I had been out, one day, loitering somewhere, in the listless, meditative manner that my way of life engendered, when, turning the corner of a lane near our house, I came upon Mr.", "어느 날 나는 밖에 나가서, 내 생활방식이 빚어낸 그 멍하고 골똘한 태도로 어딘가를 어슬렁거리고 있었는데, 우리 집 근처 골목 모퉁이를 돌다가 어떤 신사와 함께 걷고 있는 머드스톤 씨와 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["Murdstone walking with a gentleman. I was confused, and was going by them, when the gentleman cried:", "나는 당황해서 그들을 지나치려 했는데, 그 신사가 소리쳤다. \"이런! 브룩스 아니냐!\""]], [["‘What!", "\"아닌데요, 선생님."], ["Brooks!’\n‘No, sir, David Copperfield,’ I said.", "데이비드 코퍼필드인데요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t tell me.", "\"그런 소리 말아라."], ["You are Brooks,’ said the gentleman.", "너는 브룩스야.\" 신사가 말했다."], ["‘You are Brooks of Sheffield.", "\"너는 셰필드의 브룩스다."], ["That’s your name.’", "그게 네 이름이지.\""]], [["At these words, I observed the gentleman more attentively.", "이 말에 나는 그 신사를 더 유심히 살펴보았다."], ["His laugh coming to my remembrance too, I knew him to be Mr.", "그의 웃음소리도 떠올라서, 나는 그가 퀴니언 씨임을 알아보았다."], ["Quinion, whom I had gone over to Lowestoft with Mr. Murdstone to see, before--it is no matter--I need not recall when.", "전에 — 언제인지는 상관없다, 굳이 떠올릴 필요는 없다 — 내가 머드스톤 씨와 함께 만나러 로스토프트까지 갔던 그 사람이었다."]], [["‘And how do you get on, and where are you being educated, Brooks?’ said Mr. Quinion.", "\"그래, 잘 지내냐, 어디서 교육받고 있느냐, 브룩스?\" 퀴니언 씨가 말했다."]], [["He had put his hand upon my shoulder, and turned me about, to walk with them.", "그는 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 나를 돌려세워, 함께 걷게 했다."], ["I did not know what to reply, and glanced dubiously at Mr. Murdstone.", "나는 무어라 대답해야 할지 몰라서, 머드스톤 씨를 미심쩍게 흘끗 보았다."]], [["‘He is at home at present,’ said the latter.", "\"이 애는 지금 집에 있소.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다."], ["‘He is not being educated anywhere.", "\"어디서도 교육받고 있지 않소."], ["I don’t know what to do with him.", "어찌해야 할지 모르겠소."], ["He is a difficult subject.’", "다루기 까다로운 녀석이라.\""]], [["That old, double look was on me for a moment; and then his eyes darkened with a frown, as it turned, in its aversion, elsewhere.", "그 옛, 그 두 겹의 눈길이 한순간 내게 머물렀다. 그러더니 그 눈길은 못마땅한 듯 다른 데로 돌려지면서, 두 눈이 찌푸림과 함께 어두워졌다."]], [["‘Humph!’ said Mr. Quinion, looking at us both, I thought.", "\"흠!\" 퀴니언 씨가 내 생각엔 우리 둘을 번갈아 보며 말했다."], ["‘Fine weather!’", "\"날씨 좋군!\""]], [["Silence ensued, and I was considering how I could best disengage my shoulder from his hand, and go away, when he said:", "침묵이 이어졌고, 나는 어떻게 하면 그의 손에서 어깨를 빼내어 가 버릴 수 있을까 궁리하고 있었는데, 그때 그가 말했다."]], [["‘I suppose you are a pretty sharp fellow still? Eh, Brooks?’", "\"넌 아직 꽤 영리한 녀석이렷다? 응, 브룩스?\""]], [["‘Aye! He is sharp enough,’ said Mr.", "\"그래요! 영리하기는 충분히 영리하지요.\""], ["Murdstone, impatiently.", "머드스톤 씨가 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["‘You had better let him go.", "\"그만 보내 주는 게 낫겠소."], ["He will not thank you for troubling him.’", "이 녀석은 귀찮게 군다고 당신한테 고마워할 녀석이 아니오.\""]], [["On this hint, Mr. Quinion released me, and I made the best of my way home.", "이 눈치에 퀴니언 씨가 나를 놓아주었고, 나는 부리나케 집으로 향했다."], ["Looking back as I turned into the front garden, I saw Mr. Murdstone leaning against the wicket of the churchyard, and Mr.", "앞뜰로 접어들며 뒤를 돌아보니, 머드스톤 씨가 교회 묘지 쪽문에 기대어 있고 퀴니언 씨가 그에게 이야기를 하고 있었다."], ["Quinion talking to him. They were both looking after me, and I felt that they were speaking of me.", "두 사람 다 내 뒷모습을 지켜보고 있었고, 나는 그들이 내 이야기를 하고 있다는 걸 느꼈다."]], [["Mr. Quinion lay at our house that night.", "퀴니언 씨는 그날 밤 우리 집에서 묵었다."], ["After breakfast, the next morning, I had put my chair away, and was going out of the room, when Mr.", "이튿날 아침 식사를 마치고 내가 의자를 치우고 방을 나가려는데, 머드스톤 씨가 나를 다시 불렀다."], ["Murdstone called me back. He then gravely repaired to another table, where his sister sat herself at her desk. Mr.", "그러더니 그는 엄숙하게 다른 탁자로 가는데, 거기에는 그의 누이가 자기 책상 앞에 자리 잡고 앉아 있었다."], ["Quinion, with his hands in his pockets, stood looking out of window; and I stood looking at them all.", "퀴니언 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 창밖을 내다보며 서 있었고, 나는 서서 그들 모두를 바라보았다."]], [["‘David,’ said Mr.", "\"데이비드,\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다."], ["Murdstone, ‘to the young this is a world for action; not for moping and droning in.’ --‘As you do,’ added his sister.", "\"젊은이에게 이 세상은 행동하는 무대이지, 의기소침해서 빈둥대며 우물쭈물할 곳이 아니다.\" \"네가 하는 것처럼 말이지.\" 그의 누이가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Jane Murdstone, leave it to me, if you please.", "\"제인 머드스톤, 부디 나한테 맡겨 주시오."], ["I say, David, to the young this is a world for action, and not for moping and droning in.", "데이비드, 내 말은, 젊은이에게 이 세상은 행동하는 무대이지, 의기소침해서 빈둥대며 우물쭈물할 곳이 아니라는 거다."], ["It is especially so for a young boy of your disposition, which requires a great deal of correcting; and to which no greater service can be done than to force it to conform to the ways of the working world, and to bend it and break it.’", "너처럼 단단히 고쳐야 할 기질을 가진 어린 녀석한테는 특히 그렇지. 그런 기질에 해 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 일은, 그것을 일하는 세상의 방식에 따르도록 억지로 밀어붙이고, 휘어 꺾어 놓는 것이다.\""]], [["‘For stubbornness won’t do here,’ said his sister ‘What it wants is, to be crushed.", "\"고집은 여기서 안 통하니까.\" 그의 누이가 말했다. \"필요한 건 그걸 짓뭉개는 거야."], ["And crushed it must be. Shall be, too!’", "짓뭉개야 해. 그리고 짓뭉개고말고!\""]], [["He gave her a look, half in remonstrance, half in approval, and went on:", "그는 나무람 반, 칭찬 반의 눈길을 그녀에게 던지고는 말을 이었다."]], [["‘I suppose you know, David, that I am not rich.", "\"데이비드, 너도 알겠지만 나는 부자가 아니다."], ["At any rate, you know it now.", "어쨌든 이제는 알겠지."], ["You have received some considerable education already.", "너는 이미 상당한 교육을 받았다. 교육에는 돈이 든다."], ["Education is costly; and even if it were not, and I could afford it, I am of opinion that it would not be at all advantageous to you to be kept at school.", "설령 돈이 들지 않고 내가 감당할 수 있다 해도, 너를 계속 학교에 두는 게 너에게 조금도 이로울 게 없다는 게 내 의견이다."], ["What is before you, is a fight with the world; and the sooner you begin it, the better.’", "네 앞에 놓인 것은 세상과의 한판 싸움이고, 그걸 일찍 시작할수록 좋다.\""]], [["I think it occurred to me that I had already begun it, in my poor way: but it occurs to me now, whether or no.", "내 생각엔 나도 이미 내 딴에는 그 싸움을 시작했다는 게 그때 머릿속을 스쳤던 것 같다. 하지만 어쨌거나 그건 지금 떠오르는 생각이다."]], [["‘You have heard the “counting-house”", "\"너도 '경리부'라는 말을 가끔 들어 봤겠지.\""], ["mentioned sometimes,’ said Mr. Murdstone.", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘The counting-house, sir?’ I repeated.", "\"경리부요, 선생님?\" 내가 되물었다."], ["‘Of Murdstone and Grinby, in the wine trade,’ he replied.", "\"포도주 거래를 하는 머드스톤 앤드 그린비 상회 말이다.\" 그가 대답했다."]], [["I suppose I looked uncertain, for he went on hastily:", "내가 어리둥절해 보였던 모양인지, 그가 서둘러 말을 이었다."]], [["‘You have heard the “counting-house” mentioned, or the business, or the cellars, or the wharf, or something about it.’", "\"'경리부'라는 말이나, 그 사업이나, 창고나, 부두나, 뭐 그런 걸 들어 본 적 있겠지.\""]], [["‘I think I have heard the business mentioned, sir,’ I said, remembering what I vaguely knew of his and his sister’s resources.", "\"그 사업 얘기는 들어 본 것 같아요, 선생님.\" 나는 그와 그의 누이의 재산에 대해 어렴풋이 알던 바를 떠올리며 말했다."], ["‘But I don’t know when.’", "\"하지만 언제인지는 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘It does not matter when,’ he returned. ‘Mr.", "\"언제든 상관없다.\" 그가 받았다."], ["Quinion manages that business.’", "\"그 사업은 퀴니언 씨가 운영한다.\""]], [["I glanced at the latter deferentially as he stood looking out of window.", "나는 창밖을 내다보며 서 있는 그를 공손히 흘끗 보았다."]], [["‘Mr. Quinion suggests that it gives employment to some other boys, and that he sees no reason why it shouldn’t, on the same terms, give employment to you.’", "\"퀴니언 씨가 제안하기를, 그 사업이 다른 사내아이 몇몇에게 일자리를 주고 있는데, 같은 조건으로 너에게도 일자리를 주지 못할 까닭이 없다는 거다.\""]], [["‘He having,’ Mr.", "\"이 애한테,\""], ["Quinion observed in a low voice, and half turning round, ‘no other prospect, Murdstone.’", "퀴니언 씨가 반쯤 몸을 돌리며 낮은 목소리로 한마디 했다. \"달리 앞길이 없으니 말이오, 머드스톤.\""]], [["Mr. Murdstone, with an impatient, even an angry gesture, resumed, without noticing what he had said:", "머드스톤 씨는 짜증 섞인, 아니 화난 듯한 몸짓으로, 그가 한 말은 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Those terms are, that you will earn enough for yourself to provide for your eating and drinking, and pocket-money.", "\"그 조건이란, 네가 먹고 마실 것과 용돈을 스스로 충당할 만큼 벌어야 한다는 거다."], ["Your lodging (which I have arranged for) will be paid by me.", "네 숙소는(내가 마련해 두었다) 내가 대 주마."], ["So will your washing--’", "빨래 삯도 그렇고—\""]], [["‘--Which will be kept down to my estimate,’ said his sister.", "\"—그건 내가 정한 액수 안에서 맞출 거고요.\" 그의 누이가 말했다."]], [["‘Your clothes will be looked after for you, too,’ said Mr.", "\"네 옷가지도 너 대신 챙겨 줄 거다.\""], ["Murdstone; ‘as you will not be able, yet awhile, to get them for yourself.", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"당분간은 네가 스스로 옷을 마련할 수 없을 테니까."], ["So you are now going to London, David, with Mr. Quinion, to begin the world on your own account.’", "그러니 데이비드, 너는 이제 퀴니언 씨와 함께 런던으로 가서, 네 힘으로 세상살이를 시작하는 거다.\""]], [["‘In short, you are provided for,’ observed his sister; ‘and will please to do your duty.’", "\"한마디로, 네 살길이 마련된 거다.\" 그의 누이가 한마디 했다. \"그러니 부디 네 본분을 다하도록 해라.\""]], [["Though I quite understood that the purpose of this announcement was to get rid of me, I have no distinct remembrance whether it pleased or frightened me.", "이 발표의 목적이 나를 떼어 내려는 것임은 똑똑히 알았으나, 그것이 나를 기쁘게 했는지 두렵게 했는지는 또렷한 기억이 없다."], ["My impression is, that I was in a state of confusion about it, and, oscillating between the two points, touched neither.", "내 짐작에 나는 그 일을 두고 어리둥절한 상태였고, 두 마음 사이를 오락가락하느라 어느 쪽에도 가닿지 못했던 것 같다."], ["Nor had I much time for the clearing of my thoughts, as Mr. Quinion was to go upon the morrow.", "게다가 퀴니언 씨가 이튿날 떠날 참이었으므로, 생각을 정리할 시간도 그리 많지 않았다."]], [["Behold me, on the morrow, in a much-worn little white hat, with a black crape round it for my mother, a black jacket, and a pair of hard, stiff corduroy trousers--which Miss Murdstone considered the best armour for the legs in that fight with the world which was now to come off.", "자, 보라, 이튿날 나는 닳고 닳은 작은 흰 모자에 어머니를 기리는 검은 상장을 두르고, 검은 윗옷에, 빳빳하고 뻣뻣한 코르덴 바지 한 벌을 입고 있는데 — 머드스톤 양은 이제 벌어질 그 세상과의 한판 싸움에서 다리를 지킬 최고의 갑옷이 그거라고 여겼다."], ["Behold me so attired, and with my little worldly all before me in a small trunk, sitting, a lone lorn child (as Mrs. Gummidge might have said), in the post-chaise that was carrying Mr. Quinion to the London coach at Yarmouth!", "보라, 그렇게 차려입고, 내 보잘것없는 전 재산을 작은 짐 가방에 담아 앞에 둔 채, (거미지 부인이라면 말했을 법한) 외롭고 쓸쓸한 아이가 되어, 퀴니언 씨를 야머스의 런던행 역마차까지 태워다 주는 우편 마차에 앉아 있는 나를!"], ["See, how our house and church are lessening in the distance; how the grave beneath the tree is blotted out by intervening objects; how the spire points upwards from my old playground no more, and the sky is empty!", "보라, 우리 집과 교회가 멀어지며 작아지는 걸. 나무 아래 그 무덤이 가로막는 것들에 가려 지워지는 걸. 첨탑이 내 옛 놀이터에서 더는 하늘을 가리키지 않고, 하늘이 텅 비어 가는 걸!"]], [["# CHAPTER 11. I BEGIN LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT, AND DON’T LIKE IT", "# CHAPTER 11. I BEGIN LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT, AND DON’T LIKE IT"]], [["I know enough of the world now, to have almost lost the capacity of being much surprised by anything; but it is matter of some surprise to me, even now, that I can have been so easily thrown away at such an age.", "이제 나는 세상을 충분히 겪어, 무슨 일에든 크게 놀라는 능력을 거의 잃어버렸다. 그렇지만 지금도 어딘가 놀라운 것은, 내가 그토록 어린 나이에 그렇게 쉽게 내팽개쳐질 수 있었다는 사실이다."], ["A child of excellent abilities, and with strong powers of observation, quick, eager, delicate, and soon hurt bodily or mentally, it seems wonderful to me that nobody should have made any sign in my behalf.", "능력이 뛰어나고 관찰력이 예리하며, 영민하고 열심이며, 섬세해서 몸이든 마음이든 쉬이 상처받는 아이였는데, 나를 위해 누구 하나 나서서 손짓 한 번 해 주지 않았다는 것이 나로서는 도무지 믿기지 않는다."], ["But none was made; and I became, at ten years old, a little labouring hind in the service of Murdstone and Grinby.", "그러나 아무도 그러지 않았고, 나는 열 살 나이에 머드스톤과 그린비 상회에서 종처럼 일하는 어린 일꾼이 되었다."]], [["Murdstone and Grinby’s warehouse was at the waterside.", "머드스톤과 그린비의 창고는 강가에 있었다."], ["It was down in Blackfriars.", "블랙프라이어스 아래쪽이었다."], ["Modern improvements have altered the place; but it was the last house at the bottom of a narrow street, curving down hill to the river, with some stairs at the end, where people took boat.", "요즘 들어 손을 본 통에 그 자리는 달라졌지만, 그곳은 강을 향해 비탈을 따라 굽이쳐 내려가는 좁은 거리의 맨 끝 집이었고, 거리 끝에는 사람들이 배를 타는 계단이 몇 개 있었다."], ["It was a crazy old house with a wharf of its own, abutting on the water when the tide was in, and on the mud when the tide was out, and literally overrun with rats.", "그것은 제 부두를 거느린 낡고 다 쓰러져 가는 집이었는데, 물이 들어오면 부두는 물에 맞닿고, 물이 빠지면 진흙에 맞닿았으며, 말 그대로 쥐가 들끓었다."], ["Its panelled rooms, discoloured with the dirt and smoke of a hundred years, I dare say; its decaying floors and staircase; the squeaking and scuffling of the old grey rats down in the cellars; and the dirt and rottenness of the place; are things, not of many years ago, in my mind, but of the present instant.", "백 년 묵은 때와 연기에 거뭇해진 판자벽 방들, 썩어 가는 마룻바닥과 계단, 지하실 아래에서 늙은 잿빛 쥐들이 찍찍대며 부스럭거리는 소리, 그곳의 더러움과 썩은 내음 — 이런 것들은 내 마음속에서 여러 해 전의 일이 아니라 바로 지금 이 순간의 일이다."], ["They are all before me, just as they were in the evil hour when I went among them for the first time, with my trembling hand in Mr. Quinion’s.", "그것들이 모두 내 눈앞에 있다. 퀴니언 씨 손에 떨리는 내 손을 맡긴 채 처음으로 그들 사이에 들어섰던 그 불행한 시각에 그러했던 그대로."]], [["Murdstone and Grinby’s trade was among a good many kinds of people, but an important branch of it was the supply of wines and spirits to certain packet ships.", "머드스톤과 그린비의 거래처는 온갖 부류의 사람들이었으나, 그 중 중요한 한 갈래는 어떤 정기 우편선들에 포도주와 독한 술을 대는 일이었다."], ["I forget now where they chiefly went, but I think there were some among them that made voyages both to the East and West Indies.", "지금은 그 배들이 주로 어디로 갔는지 잊어버렸지만, 그중 몇 척은 동인도와 서인도를 오가며 항해했던 것 같다."], ["I know that a great many empty bottles were one of the consequences of this traffic, and that certain men and boys were employed to examine them against the light, and reject those that were flawed, and to rinse and wash them.", "이 거래가 빚어내는 결과 가운데 하나가 어마어마하게 많은 빈 병이었다는 것, 그리고 어떤 어른들과 소년들이 그 병을 빛에 비추어 살피고, 흠 있는 것을 골라내고, 헹구고 씻는 일에 부려졌다는 것은 내가 안다."], ["When the empty bottles ran short, there were labels to be pasted on full ones, or corks to be fitted to them, or seals to be put upon the corks, or finished bottles to be packed in casks.", "빈 병이 부족할 때면 가득 찬 병에 상표를 붙이거나, 코르크 마개를 끼우거나, 마개에 봉인을 찍거나, 완성된 병을 통에 담는 일을 했다."], ["All this work was my work, and of the boys employed upon it I was one.", "이 모든 일이 내 일이었고, 그 일에 부려진 소년들 가운데 나도 하나였다."]], [["There were three or four of us, counting me.", "나까지 셈해서 서넛이 있었다."], ["My working place was established in a corner of the warehouse, where Mr. Quinion could see me, when he chose to stand up on the bottom rail of his stool in the counting-house, and look at me through a window above the desk.", "내 작업 자리는 창고 한 귀퉁이에 마련되었는데, 퀴니언 씨가 회계실에서 자기 걸상의 맨 아래 가로대에 올라서서, 책상 위쪽 창문 너머로 나를 들여다보고 싶을 때면 볼 수 있는 자리였다."], ["Hither, on the first morning of my so auspiciously beginning life on my own account, the oldest of the regular boys was summoned to show me my business.", "그토록 상서롭게 제 힘으로 인생을 시작한 첫날 아침, 정식으로 일하는 소년들 중 가장 나이 많은 아이가 이리로 불려 와 내게 일을 가르쳐 주게 되었다."], ["His name was Mick Walker, and he wore a ragged apron and a paper cap.", "그 아이 이름은 믹 워커였고, 누덕누덕한 앞치마를 두르고 종이 모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["He informed me that his father was a bargeman, and walked, in a black velvet head-dress, in the Lord Mayor’s Show.", "그 아이는 제 아버지가 거룻배 사공이며, 검은 벨벳 머리 장식을 쓰고 시장 행렬에 걸어 나간다고 일러 주었다."], ["He also informed me that our principal associate would be another boy whom he introduced by the--to me--extraordinary name of Mealy Potatoes.", "또 우리의 주된 동료가 될 또 한 아이를 소개해 주었는데, 그 아이를 — 내게는 — 기묘하기 짝이 없는 이름인 '밀리 포테이토'라고 불렀다."], ["I discovered, however, that this youth had not been christened by that name, but that it had been bestowed upon him in the warehouse, on account of his complexion, which was pale or mealy.", "하지만 나는 곧, 그 소년이 그런 이름으로 세례를 받은 것이 아니라, 창백하다 못해 흙빛인 그 낯빛 때문에 창고에서 그 이름을 얻은 것임을 알게 되었다."], ["Mealy’s father was a waterman, who had the additional distinction of being a fireman, and was engaged as such at one of the large theatres; where some young relation of Mealy’s--I think his little sister--did Imps in the Pantomimes.", "밀리의 아버지는 나룻배 사공이었는데, 거기에 더해 소방수라는 명예까지 누리고 있어서, 어느 큰 극장에 그런 일로 고용되어 있었다. 그곳에서는 밀리의 어린 친척 하나가 — 내 생각에 그 애의 여동생인 듯한데 — 무언극에서 꼬마 도깨비 역을 맡았다."]], [["No words can express the secret agony of my soul as I sunk into this companionship; compared these henceforth everyday associates with those of my happier childhood--not to say with Steerforth, Traddles, and the rest of those boys; and felt my hopes of growing up to be a learned and distinguished man, crushed in my bosom.", "이런 무리 속으로 가라앉으며, 이제부터 날마다 함께할 이 동무들을 더 행복했던 어린 시절의 벗들과 — 스티어포스나 트래들스, 그 밖의 다른 소년들은 말할 것도 없고 — 견주어 보던 그때, 학식 있고 명망 높은 어른으로 자라리라는 내 희망이 가슴속에서 짓이겨지는 것을 느끼던 그 순간, 내 영혼의 은밀한 고통은 어떤 말로도 다 담을 수 없다."], ["The deep remembrance of the sense I had, of being utterly without hope now; of the shame I felt in my position; of the misery it was to my young heart to believe that day by day what I had learned, and thought, and delighted in, and raised my fancy and my emulation up by, would pass away from me, little by little, never to be brought back any more; cannot be written.", "이제 나에게는 아무 희망도 남지 않았다는 그 느낌, 내 처지가 부끄럽다는 그 느낌, 그리고 내가 배우고 생각하고 즐거워하며 그것으로 내 상상과 분발심을 끌어올렸던 모든 것이 날이 갈수록 조금씩 내게서 떠나가, 두 번 다시 되돌아오지 않으리라 믿는 것이 어린 가슴에 얼마나 비참한 일이었던지 — 그 깊은 기억은 글로 옮길 수가 없다."], ["As often as Mick Walker went away in the course of that forenoon, I mingled my tears with the water in which I was washing the bottles; and sobbed as if there were a flaw in my own breast, and it were in danger of bursting.", "그날 오전 내내 믹 워커가 자리를 비울 때마다, 나는 병을 씻던 물에 눈물을 섞었고, 마치 내 가슴속에 흠이 하나 나 있어 금방이라도 터져 버릴 것처럼 흐느꼈다."]], [["The counting-house clock was at half past twelve, and there was general preparation for going to dinner, when Mr. Quinion tapped at the counting-house window, and beckoned to me to go in.", "회계실 시계가 열두 시 반을 가리키고, 다들 점심을 먹으러 갈 채비를 하던 참에, 퀴니언 씨가 회계실 창문을 톡톡 두드리며 내게 들어오라고 손짓했다."], ["I went in, and found there a stoutish, middle-aged person, in a brown surtout and black tights and shoes, with no more hair upon his head (which was a large one, and very shining) than there is upon an egg, and with a very extensive face, which he turned full upon me.", "내가 들어가 보니, 갈색 프록코트에 검은 타이츠와 신발을 신은, 다부진 중년 사내가 있었다. 머리(크고 무척이나 번들거렸다)에는 달걀에 난 털만큼도 머리카락이 없었고, 무척이나 넓은 얼굴을 나를 향해 정면으로 돌렸다."], ["His clothes were shabby, but he had an imposing shirt-collar on.", "옷차림은 추레했으나, 위풍당당한 셔츠 깃을 달고 있었다."], ["He carried a jaunty sort of a stick, with a large pair of rusty tassels to it; and a quizzing-glass hung outside his coat,--for ornament, I afterwards found, as he very seldom looked through it, and couldn’t see anything when he did.", "그는 큼직하게 녹슨 술 한 쌍이 달린, 멋을 부린 듯한 지팡이를 들고 있었고, 외알 안경을 코트 바깥에 늘어뜨리고 있었다 — 나중에 알게 되었지만 그건 장식용이었다. 그가 그 안경을 들여다보는 일은 거의 없었고, 들여다본들 아무것도 보지 못했으니까."]], [["‘This,’ said Mr.", "\"이 아이입니다.\""], ["Quinion, in allusion to myself, ‘is he.’", "퀴니언 씨가 나를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["‘This,’ said the stranger, with a certain condescending roll in his voice, and a certain indescribable air of doing something genteel, which impressed me very much, ‘is Master Copperfield.", "\"이분이,\" 그 낯선 사내가, 어딘가 거들먹거리는 듯한 가락을 목소리에 실으며, 또 형언하기 어려운 점잖은 일을 해내는 듯한 태도를 풍기며 — 그 태도에 나는 무척 감명을 받았다 — 말했다. \"코퍼필드 도련님이로군요."], ["I hope I see you well, sir?’", "건강하시지요, 도련님?\""]], [["I said I was very well, and hoped he was.", "나는 아주 잘 있다고, 그리고 그분도 그러시기를 바란다고 대답했다."], ["I was sufficiently ill at ease, Heaven knows; but it was not in my nature to complain much at that time of my life, so I said I was very well, and hoped he was.", "하느님은 아시겠지만, 나는 마음이 영 편치 못했다. 하지만 그 시절 내 본성은 그리 자주 푸념을 늘어놓는 것이 아니었으므로, 나는 아주 잘 있다고, 그리고 그분도 그러시기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["‘I am,’ said the stranger, ‘thank Heaven, quite well.", "\"나는,\" 그 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"하느님 덕분에, 아주 잘 있소이다."], ["I have received a letter from Mr.", "머드스톤 씨에게서 편지를 한 통 받았는데, 거기서 말하기를, 지금 비어 있는 우리 집 뒤편의 한 방을 — 요컨대 세를 놓으려는 — 요컨대,\""], ["Murdstone, in which he mentions that he would desire me to receive into an apartment in the rear of my house, which is at present unoccupied--and is, in short, to be let as a--in short,’ said the stranger, with a smile and in a burst of confidence, ‘as a bedroom--the young beginner whom I have now the pleasure to--’ and the stranger waved his hand, and settled his chin in his shirt-collar.", "그 낯선 사내가 미소를 지으며, 갑자기 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다. \"침실로 말이오 — 그 방에, 지금 내가 이렇게 알게 되어 기쁜 이 어린 신참을 받아 주었으면 하더군요.\" 그러더니 낯선 사내는 한 손을 휘저으며 턱을 셔츠 깃에 파묻었다."]], [["‘This is Mr. Micawber,’ said Mr.", "\"이분이 미코버 씨란다.\""], ["Quinion to me.", "퀴니언 씨가 내게 말했다."]], [["‘Ahem!’ said the stranger, ‘that is my name.’", "\"에헴!\" 그 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"그게 내 이름이오.\""]], [["‘Mr. Micawber,’ said Mr.", "\"미코버 씨는,\" 퀴니언 씨가 말했다."], ["Quinion, ‘is known to Mr.", "\"머드스톤 씨와 아는 사이지."], ["Murdstone. He takes orders for us on commission, when he can get any.", "우리 회사 주문을 수수료를 받고 따 오시는데, 따 올 수 있을 때에 한해서다."], ["He has been written to by Mr. Murdstone, on the subject of your lodgings, and he will receive you as a lodger.’", "머드스톤 씨가 네 하숙 문제로 편지를 보내셨고, 미코버 씨가 너를 하숙생으로 받아 주실 거야.\""]], [["‘My address,’ said Mr.", "\"내 주소는,\""], ["Micawber, ‘is Windsor Terrace, City Road.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"시티 로드의 윈저 테라스요."], ["I--in short,’ said Mr.", "나는 — 요컨대,\""], ["Micawber, with the same genteel air, and in another burst of confidence--‘I live there.’", "미코버 씨가 아까와 똑같이 점잖은 태도로, 또 한 번 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다. \"내가 거기 산다오.\""]], [["I made him a bow.", "나는 그에게 고개 숙여 인사했다."]], [["‘Under the impression,’ said Mr. Micawber, ‘that your peregrinations in this metropolis have not as yet been extensive, and that you might have some difficulty in penetrating the arcana of the Modern Babylon in the direction of the City Road,--in short,’ said Mr.", "\"이 큰 도시에서 도련님의 발걸음이 아직 그리 멀리까지 미치지 못했으리라는 짐작에서,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, in another burst of confidence, ‘that you might lose yourself--I shall be happy to call this evening, and install you in the knowledge of the nearest way.’", "\"또 시티 로드 방향으로 이 현대판 바빌론의 깊은 속내를 헤쳐 나가는 데 다소 어려움을 겪을지도 모른다는 짐작에서 — 요컨대,\" 미코버 씨가 또 한 번 속내를 털어놓듯 말했다. \"도련님이 길을 잃을지도 모른다는 짐작에서, 오늘 저녁에 들러서 가장 가까운 길을 일러 드릴 수 있다면 기쁘겠소이다.\""]], [["I thanked him with all my heart, for it was friendly in him to offer to take that trouble.", "나는 진심으로 고맙다고 인사했다. 그렇게 수고를 마다 않고 나서 주겠다니, 참으로 다정한 일이었으니까."]], [["‘At what hour,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼 몇 시쯤에,\""], ["Micawber, ‘shall I--’", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내가 —\""]], [["‘At about eight,’ said Mr. Quinion.", "\"여덟 시쯤이요.\" 퀴니언 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘At about eight,’ said Mr. Micawber.", "\"여덟 시쯤이라.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I beg to wish you good day, Mr.", "\"그럼 안녕히 계시오, 퀴니언 씨."], ["Quinion. I will intrude no longer.’", "더는 폐를 끼치지 않겠소이다.\""]], [["So he put on his hat, and went out with his cane under his arm: very upright, and humming a tune when he was clear of the counting-house.", "그러고는 모자를 쓰고 지팡이를 옆구리에 낀 채 밖으로 나갔다. 몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세우고, 회계실에서 멀어지자 콧노래를 흥얼거리면서."]], [["Mr. Quinion then formally engaged me to be as useful as I could in the warehouse of Murdstone and Grinby, at a salary, I think, of six shillings a week.", "그러고 나서 퀴니언 씨는 머드스톤과 그린비 창고에서 나더러 할 수 있는 한 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 달라고 정식으로 일을 맡겼는데, 보수는 주급 육 실링이었던 것 같다."], ["I am not clear whether it was six or seven.", "육 실링이었는지 칠 실링이었는지 분명치 않다."], ["I am inclined to believe, from my uncertainty on this head, that it was six at first and seven afterwards.", "이 대목이 헷갈리는 걸 보면, 처음에는 육 실링이었다가 나중에 칠 실링이 된 게 아닐까 싶다."], ["He paid me a week down (from his own pocket, I believe), and I gave Mealy sixpence out of it to get my trunk carried to Windsor Terrace that night: it being too heavy for my strength, small as it was.", "그는 일주일 치를 미리 내주었는데(내 짐작에 자기 주머니에서였다), 나는 거기서 육 펜스를 밀리에게 주어 그날 밤 내 트렁크를 윈저 테라스까지 날라다 달라고 했다. 트렁크가 작긴 했어도 내 힘으로 들기에는 너무 무거웠으니까."], ["I paid sixpence more for my dinner, which was a meat pie and a turn at a neighbouring pump; and passed the hour which was allowed for that meal, in walking about the streets.", "나는 점심값으로 육 펜스를 더 썼는데, 점심은 고기 파이 한 조각에다가 근처 펌프 물 한 모금이었다. 그리고 그 끼니에 허락된 한 시간을 거리를 이리저리 거닐며 보냈다."]], [["At the appointed time in the evening, Mr. Micawber reappeared.", "저녁 약속 시각에 미코버 씨가 다시 나타났다."], ["I washed my hands and face, to do the greater honour to his gentility, and we walked to our house, as I suppose I must now call it, together; Mr.", "나는 그의 점잖음에 좀 더 예를 갖추려고 손과 얼굴을 씻었고, 우리는 함께 우리 집을 — 이제는 그렇게 불러야 할 것 같다 — 향해 걸었다."], ["Micawber impressing the name of streets, and the shapes of corner houses upon me, as we went along, that I might find my way back, easily, in the morning.", "미코버 씨는 가는 길에 거리 이름과 모퉁이 집들의 생김새를 내게 새겨 주었는데, 아침에 내가 쉽게 길을 되짚어 갈 수 있도록 하기 위해서였다."]], [["Arrived at this house in Windsor Terrace (which I noticed was shabby like himself, but also, like himself, made all the show it could), he presented me to Mrs.", "윈저 테라스의 이 집에 다다랐을 때(나는 그 집이 그 사람처럼 추레하면서도, 그 사람처럼 할 수 있는 한 멋을 한껏 부려 놓았다는 것을 알아챘다), 그는 나를 미코버 부인에게 소개했다."], ["Micawber, a thin and faded lady, not at all young, who was sitting in the parlour (the first floor was altogether unfurnished, and the blinds were kept down to delude the neighbours), with a baby at her breast.", "부인은 야위고 빛바랜, 조금도 젊지 않은 여인이었는데, 응접실에 앉아(이 층은 아예 가구 하나 없었고, 이웃들 눈을 속이려고 차양을 내려 두었다) 아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었다."], ["This baby was one of twins; and I may remark here that I hardly ever, in all my experience of the family, saw both the twins detached from Mrs. Micawber at the same time.", "이 아기는 쌍둥이 중 하나였다. 그리고 여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 그 집을 드나든 내내, 쌍둥이 둘 다 미코버 부인에게서 떨어져 있는 모습은 거의 본 적이 없다."], ["One of them was always taking refreshment.", "둘 중 하나는 늘 무언가를 빨고 있었다."]], [["There were two other children; Master Micawber, aged about four, and Miss Micawber, aged about three.", "다른 아이도 둘 있었다. 네 살쯤 된 미코버 도련님과 세 살쯤 된 미코버 양이었다."], ["These, and a dark-complexioned young woman, with a habit of snorting, who was servant to the family, and informed me, before half an hour had expired, that she was ‘a Orfling’, and came from St. Luke’s workhouse, in the neighbourhood, completed the establishment.", "이 아이들과, 콧방귀 뀌는 버릇이 있는 가무잡잡한 젊은 여자 — 그 집 하녀였는데, 채 삼십 분도 지나기 전에 자기가 '고아'이며 근처 세인트 루크 구빈원에서 왔노라고 내게 알려 주었다 — 이렇게 해서 그 집 식구가 다 모였다."], ["My room was at the top of the house, at the back: a close chamber; stencilled all over with an ornament which my young imagination represented as a blue muffin; and very scantily furnished.", "내 방은 집 꼭대기, 뒤쪽에 있었다. 갑갑한 방이었고, 온 벽에 어떤 무늬가 본을 떠 찍혀 있었는데, 내 어린 상상에는 그것이 파란 머핀처럼 보였다. 그리고 가구는 무척 빈약했다."]], [["‘I never thought,’ said Mrs.", "\"나는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없어요.\""], ["Micawber, when she came up, twin and all, to show me the apartment, and sat down to take breath, ‘before I was married, when I lived with papa and mama, that I should ever find it necessary to take a lodger. But Mr.", "미코버 부인이 쌍둥이까지 안고 올라와 그 방을 보여 주고는, 숨을 돌리려고 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"결혼하기 전, 아빠 엄마와 함께 살 적에는, 하숙을 칠 필요가 생기리라고는 말이에요."], ["Micawber being in difficulties, all considerations of private feeling must give way.’", "하지만 미코버 씨가 곤경에 처했으니, 사사로운 감정 따위는 다 접어 둘 수밖에요.\""]], [["I said: ‘Yes, ma’am.’", "나는 말했다. \"네, 부인.\""]], [["‘Mr. Micawber’s difficulties are almost overwhelming just at present,’ said Mrs.", "\"미코버 씨의 곤경은 지금 거의 감당할 수 없는 지경이에요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber; ‘and whether it is possible to bring him through them, I don’t know.", "\"그이를 거기서 빠져나오게 할 수 있을지 어떨지, 나는 모르겠어요."], ["When I lived at home with papa and mama, I really should have hardly understood what the word meant, in the sense in which I now employ it, but experientia does it,--as papa used to say.’", "아빠 엄마와 함께 집에 살 적에는, 지금 내가 쓰는 뜻으로 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 사실 거의 알지도 못했을 거예요. 하지만 경험이 그렇게 만들지요 — 아빠가 늘 하시던 말씀처럼요.\""]], [["I cannot satisfy myself whether she told me that Mr. Micawber had been an officer in the Marines, or whether I have imagined it.", "부인이 미코버 씨가 해병대 장교였다고 내게 말한 것인지, 아니면 내가 지어낸 상상인지는 나도 영 알 수가 없다."], ["I only know that I believe to this hour that he WAS in the Marines once upon a time, without knowing why.", "다만 나는 지금 이 순간까지도, 까닭은 모르겠지만, 그가 한때 해병대에 있었다고 믿고 있다는 것만은 안다."], ["He was a sort of town traveller for a number of miscellaneous houses, now; but made little or nothing of it, I am afraid.", "그는 지금 이런저런 잡다한 가게들을 도는 일종의 외판원이었으나, 그 일로는 아무것도 못 벌거나 거의 못 벌었던 듯하다."]], [["‘If Mr. Micawber’s creditors will not give him time,’ said Mrs.", "\"미코버 씨의 채권자들이 그이에게 시간을 주지 않겠다면,\""], ["Micawber, ‘they must take the consequences; and the sooner they bring it to an issue the better.", "미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그 결과는 그 사람들이 감당해야지요. 게다가 빨리 매듭지을수록 좋고요."], ["Blood cannot be obtained from a stone, neither can anything on account be obtained at present (not to mention law expenses) from Mr. Micawber.’", "돌에서 피를 짜낼 수 없듯이, 지금 미코버 씨에게서는 외상값이라곤 한 푼도(소송 비용은 말할 것도 없고) 받아 낼 수가 없으니까요.\""]], [["I never can quite understand whether my precocious self-dependence confused Mrs. Micawber in reference to my age, or whether she was so full of the subject that she would have talked about it to the very twins if there had been nobody else to communicate with, but this was the strain in which she began, and she went on accordingly all the time I knew her.", "내가 또래답지 않게 일찍 제 앞가림을 하는 것이 내 나이를 두고 미코버 부인을 헷갈리게 한 것인지, 아니면 부인이 그 일에 너무 골몰한 나머지 말 상대가 달리 아무도 없었다면 쌍둥이한테라도 그 얘기를 늘어놓았을 것인지, 나는 도무지 알 수가 없다. 어쨌든 부인은 이런 가락으로 이야기를 시작했고, 내가 부인을 알고 지낸 내내 그런 식으로 이어 갔다."]], [["Poor Mrs. Micawber!", "가엾은 미코버 부인!"], ["She said she had tried to exert herself, and so, I have no doubt, she had.", "부인은 자기도 애를 써 보았노라고 말했고, 나도 부인이 정말 애를 썼으리라 믿어 의심치 않는다."], ["The centre of the street door was perfectly covered with a great brass-plate, on which was engraved ‘Mrs.", "현관문 한복판에는 큼직한 놋쇠 명패가 떡하니 붙어 있었는데, 거기에는 '미코버 부인 영양 기숙 학원'이라고 새겨져 있었다."], ["Micawber’s Boarding Establishment for Young Ladies’: but I never found that any young lady had ever been to school there; or that any young lady ever came, or proposed to come; or that the least preparation was ever made to receive any young lady.", "그러나 나는 그 학원에 다닌 영양이 단 한 명이라도 있었다는 것을, 또는 영양이 한 명이라도 왔거나 오겠다고 했다는 것을, 또는 영양을 맞을 채비를 손톱만큼이라도 갖췄다는 것을 끝내 본 적이 없다."], ["The only visitors I ever saw, or heard of, were creditors.", "내가 보았거나 들은 방문객이라곤 채권자들뿐이었다."], ["THEY used to come at all hours, and some of them were quite ferocious.", "그자들은 아무 때나 찾아왔고, 그중 몇몇은 아주 사나웠다."], ["One dirty-faced man, I think he was a boot-maker, used to edge himself into the passage as early as seven o’clock in the morning, and call up the stairs to Mr. Micawber--‘Come!", "더러운 얼굴의 한 사내는, 내 생각에 구두장이였던 듯한데, 아침 일곱 시도 안 되어 복도에 비집고 들어와서는 계단 위쪽 미코버 씨를 향해 소리치곤 했다 — \"이봐요!"], ["You ain’t out yet, you know.", "아직 안 나왔구먼, 알지요."], ["Pay us, will you? Don’t hide, you know; that’s mean.", "돈을 갚으시오, 응? 숨지 마요, 알겠소."], ["I wouldn’t be mean if I was you. Pay us, will you?", "그건 비겁한 짓이오. 내가 당신이라면 비겁하게 굴진 않겠소."], ["You just pay us, d’ye hear?", "돈을 갚으시오, 응?"], ["Come!’ Receiving no answer to these taunts, he would mount in his wrath to the words ‘swindlers’ and ‘robbers’; and these being ineffectual too, would sometimes go to the extremity of crossing the street, and roaring up at the windows of the second floor, where he knew Mr. Micawber was.", "그냥 갚으면 돼요, 알겠소? 이봐요!\" 이런 비아냥에 아무 대답이 없으면, 그자는 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 '사기꾼'이니 '도둑놈'이니 하는 말까지 끌어 올렸고, 그래도 통하지 않으면 때로는 길 건너편까지 가서, 미코버 씨가 있는 줄 빤히 아는 이 층 창문을 향해 고래고래 소리를 질러 댔다."], ["At these times, Mr. Micawber would be transported with grief and mortification, even to the length (as I was once made aware by a scream from his wife) of making motions at himself with a razor; but within half-an-hour afterwards, he would polish up his shoes with extraordinary pains, and go out, humming a tune with a greater air of gentility than ever. Mrs.", "그럴 때면 미코버 씨는 슬픔과 굴욕에 사로잡혀, (한번은 부인의 비명 소리로 알게 되었듯이) 면도칼을 자기 목에 갖다 대는 시늉을 할 지경에까지 이르렀다. 그러나 그러고 나서 반 시간도 채 안 되어, 그는 유난히 공들여 구두를 닦고는, 그 어느 때보다도 점잖은 태도로 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 밖으로 나가곤 했다."], ["Micawber was quite as elastic. I have known her to be thrown into fainting fits by the king’s taxes at three o’clock, and to eat lamb chops, breaded, and drink warm ale (paid for with two tea-spoons that had gone to the pawnbroker’s) at four.", "미코버 부인도 꼭 그만큼이나 회복이 빨랐다. 나는 부인이 세 시에 국세 때문에 까무러쳐 쓰러졌다가, 네 시에는 빵가루를 입힌 양고기 갈비를 먹고 따뜻한 에일을 마시는(전당포에 잡힌 찻숟가락 두 개로 마련한 돈이었다) 것을 보기도 했다."], ["On one occasion, when an execution had just been put in, coming home through some chance as early as six o’clock, I saw her lying (of course with a twin) under the grate in a swoon, with her hair all torn about her face; but I never knew her more cheerful than she was, that very same night, over a veal cutlet before the kitchen fire, telling me stories about her papa and mama, and the company they used to keep.", "한번은 압류 집행이 막 들어왔을 때, 우연히 여섯 시라는 이른 시각에 집에 돌아왔더니, 부인이 머리카락을 죄다 얼굴로 흐트러뜨린 채(물론 쌍둥이 하나를 안고) 난로 아래에서 까무러쳐 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 하지만 바로 그날 밤, 부엌 난롯불 앞에서 송아지 고기 커틀릿을 앞에 놓고, 자기 아빠 엄마와 그분들이 어울리던 사람들 이야기를 내게 들려주던 부인보다 더 명랑한 부인을 나는 본 적이 없다."]], [["In this house, and with this family, I passed my leisure time.", "이 집에서, 이 식구와 함께, 나는 일 없는 시간을 보냈다."], ["My own exclusive breakfast of a penny loaf and a pennyworth of milk, I provided myself.", "오로지 나만의 아침거리인 일 페니짜리 빵 한 덩이와 일 페니어치 우유는 내가 직접 마련했다."], ["I kept another small loaf, and a modicum of cheese, on a particular shelf of a particular cupboard, to make my supper on when I came back at night.", "밤에 돌아와 저녁으로 먹을 또 다른 작은 빵 한 덩이와 치즈 한 조각은, 어느 찬장의 어느 선반에 따로 두었다."], ["This made a hole in the six or seven shillings, I know well; and I was out at the warehouse all day, and had to support myself on that money all the week.", "이렇게 하니 육 실링인지 칠 실링인지 하는 돈에 구멍이 났다, 잘 알고 있다. 게다가 나는 온종일 창고에 나가 있었고, 그 돈으로 한 주를 내내 먹고살아야 했다."], ["From Monday morning until Saturday night, I had no advice, no counsel, no encouragement, no consolation, no assistance, no support, of any kind, from anyone, that I can call to mind, as I hope to go to heaven!", "월요일 아침부터 토요일 밤까지, 나는 그 누구에게서도 어떤 종류의 충고도, 조언도, 격려도, 위로도, 도움도, 지지도 — 내가 기억하는 한 — 받지 못했다, 천국에 가기를 바라는 마음을 걸고 맹세하건대!"]], [["I was so young and childish, and so little qualified--how could I be otherwise?--to undertake the whole charge of my own existence, that often, in going to Murdstone and Grinby’s, of a morning, I could not resist the stale pastry put out for sale at half-price at the pastrycooks’ doors, and spent in that the money I should have kept for my dinner.", "나는 너무도 어리고 철없었으며, 내 존재 전부를 떠맡기에는 — 어찌 그러지 않을 수 있었으랴 — 너무도 자격이 모자랐던 터라, 아침에 머드스톤과 그린비로 가는 길에, 빵집 문 앞에 반값으로 내놓은 묵은 과자를 차마 그냥 지나치지 못하고, 점심값으로 아껴 둬야 할 돈을 거기다 써 버리기 일쑤였다."], ["Then, I went without my dinner, or bought a roll or a slice of pudding.", "그러면 점심을 거르거나, 둥근 빵 하나나 푸딩 한 조각을 샀다."], ["I remember two pudding shops, between which I was divided, according to my finances.", "내 형편에 따라 마음이 갈리던 푸딩 가게 둘이 기억난다."], ["One was in a court close to St. Martin’s Church--at the back of the church,--which is now removed altogether.", "하나는 세인트 마틴 교회 가까운 골목 안에 — 교회 뒤편에 — 있었는데, 지금은 아예 사라지고 없다."], ["The pudding at that shop was made of currants, and was rather a special pudding, but was dear, twopennyworth not being larger than a pennyworth of more ordinary pudding.", "그 가게 푸딩은 건포도로 만든 것으로, 꽤 특별한 푸딩이었지만 값이 비싸서, 이 페니어치가 보통 푸딩 일 페니어치만 한 크기밖에 안 됐다."], ["A good shop for the latter was in the Strand--somewhere in that part which has been rebuilt since.", "보통 푸딩을 사기에 좋은 가게는 스트랜드에 — 그 뒤로 새로 지어 올린 어딘가에 — 있었다."], ["It was a stout pale pudding, heavy and flabby, and with great flat raisins in it, stuck in whole at wide distances apart.", "그건 묵직하고 흐물흐물한, 두툼하고 허연 푸딩으로, 큼직하고 납작한 건포도가 통째로 멀찍멀찍 박혀 있었다."], ["It came up hot at about my time every day, and many a day did I dine off it.", "그것은 날마다 거의 내가 가는 시각쯤에 따끈하게 나왔고, 나는 그것으로 점심을 때운 날이 숱하게 많았다."], ["When I dined regularly and handsomely, I had a saveloy and a penny loaf, or a fourpenny plate of red beef from a cook’s shop; or a plate of bread and cheese and a glass of beer, from a miserable old public-house opposite our place of business, called the Lion, or the Lion and something else that I have forgotten.", "제대로, 풍족하게 점심을 먹을 때면, 나는 소시지 하나에 일 페니짜리 빵을 곁들이거나, 음식점에서 사 페니짜리 소고기 한 접시를 먹었다. 또는 우리 일터 맞은편에 있는, '라이언', 아니면 '라이언'에다가 내가 잊어버린 무슨 이름이 붙은, 초라하고 낡은 술집에서 빵과 치즈 한 접시에 맥주 한 잔을 먹기도 했다."], ["Once, I remember carrying my own bread (which I had brought from home in the morning) under my arm, wrapped in a piece of paper, like a book, and going to a famous alamode beef-house near Drury Lane, and ordering a ‘small plate’ of that delicacy to eat with it.", "한번은, 아침에 집에서 가져온 빵을 책처럼 종이에 싸서 옆구리에 끼고, 드루리 레인 근처의 이름난 알라모드 소고기 집에 가서, 그 빵과 함께 먹으려고 그 별미를 '작은 접시'로 하나 주문했던 기억이 난다."], ["What the waiter thought of such a strange little apparition coming in all alone, I don’t know; but I can see him now, staring at me as I ate my dinner, and bringing up the other waiter to look.", "그렇게 혼자 들어온 이 기묘한 꼬마 도깨비를 종업원이 어떻게 생각했는지는 모르겠다. 하지만 내가 점심을 먹는 동안 그가 나를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 다른 종업원까지 데려와 구경시키던 모습이 지금도 눈에 선하다."], ["I gave him a halfpenny for himself, and I wish he hadn’t taken it.", "나는 그에게 팁으로 반 페니를 주었는데, 그가 그걸 받지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을."]], [["We had half-an-hour, I think, for tea.", "차를 마시는 시간은, 내 생각에 반 시간쯤이었다."], ["When I had money enough, I used to get half-a-pint of ready-made coffee and a slice of bread and butter.", "돈이 넉넉할 때면 나는 미리 타 놓은 커피 반 파인트에다 버터 바른 빵 한 조각을 사 먹곤 했다."], ["When I had none, I used to look at a venison shop in Fleet Street; or I have strolled, at such a time, as far as Covent Garden Market, and stared at the pineapples.", "돈이 없을 때면 플리트 거리의 사슴고기 가게를 들여다보거나, 그런 시각에 코번트 가든 시장까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가 파인애플을 멀거니 바라보곤 했다."], ["I was fond of wandering about the Adelphi, because it was a mysterious place, with those dark arches.", "나는 어둑한 아치들이 있어 신비로운 곳이었던 애덜파이 일대를 쏘다니기를 좋아했다."], ["I see myself emerging one evening from some of these arches, on a little public-house close to the river, with an open space before it, where some coal-heavers were dancing; to look at whom I sat down upon a bench.", "어느 저녁, 이 아치들 가운데 하나에서 빠져나와, 강 가까이에 있는 작은 술집 앞에 다다른 내 모습이 보인다. 술집 앞에는 넓은 공터가 있었고, 거기서 석탄 짐꾼 몇이 춤을 추고 있었다. 나는 그것을 구경하려고 긴 의자에 앉았다."], ["I wonder what they thought of me!", "그자들이 나를 어떻게 생각했을지 궁금하다!"]], [["I was such a child, and so little, that frequently when I went into the bar of a strange public-house for a glass of ale or porter, to moisten what I had had for dinner, they were afraid to give it me.", "나는 어찌나 어린애였고 어찌나 작았던지, 점심으로 먹은 것을 적시려고 처음 가 보는 술집 바에 들어가 에일이나 포터 한 잔을 청하면, 그 사람들이 겁이 나서 내게 술을 안 내주는 일이 잦았다."], ["I remember one hot evening I went into the bar of a public-house, and said to the landlord: ‘What is your best--your very best--ale a glass?’ For it was a special occasion.", "어느 무더운 저녁, 내가 어느 술집 바에 들어가 주인에게 이렇게 말했던 기억이 난다. \"여기서 가장 좋은 — 정말 가장 좋은 — 에일이 한 잔에 얼마요?\" 특별한 날이었으니까."], ["I don’t know what.", "무슨 날이었는지는 모르겠다."], ["It may have been my birthday.", "어쩌면 내 생일이었을지도 모른다."]], [["‘Twopence-halfpenny,’ says the landlord, ‘is the price of the Genuine Stunning ale.’", "\"이 펜스 반이,\" 주인이 말한다. \"정품 끝내주는 에일 값이지.\""]], [["‘Then,’ says I, producing the money, ‘just draw me a glass of the Genuine Stunning, if you please, with a good head to it.’", "\"그럼,\" 내가 돈을 꺼내며 말한다. \"그 정품 끝내주는 걸로 한 잔 따라 주시오, 거품을 듬뿍 얹어서요.\""]], [["The landlord looked at me in return over the bar, from head to foot, with a strange smile on his face; and instead of drawing the beer, looked round the screen and said something to his wife.", "주인은 그 답으로, 바 너머로 나를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며, 얼굴에 야릇한 미소를 띠었다. 그러고는 맥주를 따르는 대신, 칸막이 뒤를 돌아보며 제 아내에게 뭐라고 말했다."], ["She came out from behind it, with her work in her hand, and joined him in surveying me.", "아내는 일감을 손에 든 채 칸막이 뒤에서 나와, 그와 함께 나를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다."], ["Here we stand, all three, before me now.", "셋이 다 지금 내 눈앞에 서 있다."], ["The landlord in his shirt-sleeves, leaning against the bar window-frame; his wife looking over the little half-door; and I, in some confusion, looking up at them from outside the partition.", "셔츠 바람으로 바의 창틀에 기댄 주인, 작은 반쪽짜리 문 너머로 넘겨다보는 그 아내, 그리고 다소 어리둥절한 채로 칸막이 바깥에서 그들을 올려다보는 나."], ["They asked me a good many questions; as, what my name was, how old I was, where I lived, how I was employed, and how I came there.", "그들은 내게 이런저런 질문을 많이 했다. 이름은 무엇인지, 나이는 몇인지, 어디 사는지, 무슨 일을 하는지, 어쩌다 여기 오게 됐는지."], ["To all of which, that I might commit nobody, I invented, I am afraid, appropriate answers.", "나는 누구에게도 해가 가지 않도록, 미안하지만, 그 모든 물음에 그럴듯한 대답을 지어냈다."], ["They served me with the ale, though I suspect it was not the Genuine Stunning; and the landlord’s wife, opening the little half-door of the bar, and bending down, gave me my money back, and gave me a kiss that was half admiring and half compassionate, but all womanly and good, I am sure.", "그들은 내게 에일을 내주었는데, 그게 정품 끝내주는 에일은 아니었지 싶다. 그리고 주인의 아내는 바의 작은 반쪽 문을 열고 몸을 굽혀, 내 돈을 돌려주고, 입을 맞춰 주었다. 반은 감탄이 담기고 반은 가엾어하는, 그러나 모두가 여인다운, 선한 입맞춤이었다, 분명히."]], [["I know I do not exaggerate, unconsciously and unintentionally, the scantiness of my resources or the difficulties of my life.", "나는 내 형편의 궁색함이나 내 삶의 고달픔을 나도 모르게, 본의 아니게 과장하고 있지는 않다고 안다."], ["I know that if a shilling were given me by Mr. Quinion at any time, I spent it in a dinner or a tea.", "퀴니언 씨가 어쩌다 내게 일 실링을 주면, 나는 그것을 점심이나 차를 마시는 데 써 버렸다는 것을 안다."], ["I know that I worked, from morning until night, with common men and boys, a shabby child.", "추레한 아이로, 아침부터 밤까지 천한 어른들과 소년들 틈에서 일했다는 것을 안다."], ["I know that I lounged about the streets, insufficiently and unsatisfactorily fed.", "변변히, 흡족히 먹지도 못한 채 거리를 어슬렁거렸다는 것을 안다."], ["I know that, but for the mercy of God, I might easily have been, for any care that was taken of me, a little robber or a little vagabond.", "그리고 하느님의 자비가 아니었다면, 나를 돌보는 손길이라곤 없었으니, 나는 자칫 어린 도둑이나 어린 부랑아가 되기 십상이었으리라는 것을 안다."]], [["Yet I held some station at Murdstone and Grinby’s too.", "그래도 나는 머드스톤과 그린비에서 나름의 자리를 지켰다."], ["Besides that Mr. Quinion did what a careless man so occupied, and dealing with a thing so anomalous, could, to treat me as one upon a different footing from the rest, I never said, to man or boy, how it was that I came to be there, or gave the least indication of being sorry that I was there.", "퀴니언 씨가 그토록 바쁘고 무심한 사람으로서, 게다가 그토록 비정상적인 일을 다루면서도, 나를 나머지 아이들과는 다른 처지에 놓인 사람으로 대해 주려고 할 수 있는 만큼은 해 준 것도 있었거니와, 나는 그 누구에게도, 어른에게든 소년에게든, 내가 어쩌다 거기 오게 됐는지를 말한 적이 없었고, 거기 있는 것을 서글퍼하는 기색을 손톱만큼도 내비치지 않았다."], ["That I suffered in secret, and that I suffered exquisitely, no one ever knew but I.", "내가 남몰래 괴로워했다는 것, 그것도 사무치게 괴로워했다는 것은 나 말고는 아무도 몰랐다."], ["How much I suffered, it is, as I have said already, utterly beyond my power to tell.", "내가 얼마나 괴로웠는지는, 앞서 이미 말했듯이, 도무지 내 힘으로 다 말할 수가 없다."], ["But I kept my own counsel, and I did my work.", "그러나 나는 속내를 혼자 간직했고, 내 일을 했다."], ["I knew from the first, that, if I could not do my work as well as any of the rest, I could not hold myself above slight and contempt.", "처음부터 나는, 내가 다른 누구만큼 일을 잘 해내지 못하면, 멸시와 업신여김을 면할 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["I soon became at least as expeditious and as skilful as either of the other boys.", "나는 곧 적어도 다른 소년들 못지않게 날래고 능숙해졌다."], ["Though perfectly familiar with them, my conduct and manner were different enough from theirs to place a space between us.", "그들과 아주 격의 없이 지냈으면서도, 내 행동거지와 태도는 그들의 것과는 사뭇 달라서 우리 사이에 간격이 생겼다."], ["They and the men generally spoke of me as ‘the little gent’, or ‘the young Suffolker.’ A certain man named Gregory, who was foreman of the packers, and another named Tipp, who was the carman, and wore a red jacket, used to address me sometimes as ‘David’: but I think it was mostly when we were very confidential, and when I had made some efforts to entertain them, over our work, with some results of the old readings; which were fast perishing out of my remembrance.", "그들과 어른들은 대개 나를 '꼬마 신사'나 '서퍽 도련님'이라고 불렀다. 짐 싸는 일의 십장인 그레고리라는 사내와, 짐마차꾼으로 붉은 윗옷을 입고 다니던 팁이라는 사내는 가끔 나를 '데이비드'라고 부르곤 했다. 하지만 그건 대개 우리가 아주 허물없이 굴 때, 그리고 내가 일하는 틈틈이 옛날에 읽은 책 이야기 — 내 기억에서 빠르게 사라져 가고 있던 것들 — 로 그들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 애쓴 끝에 그러했던 것 같다."], ["Mealy Potatoes uprose once, and rebelled against my being so distinguished; but Mick Walker settled him in no time.", "한번은 밀리 포테이토가 들고일어나 내가 그렇게 따로 대접받는 것에 반기를 들었으나, 믹 워커가 그를 단숨에 잠재워 버렸다."]], [["My rescue from this kind of existence I considered quite hopeless, and abandoned, as such, altogether.", "나는 이런 삶에서 벗어날 길은 영 가망이 없다고 여겼고, 가망 없는 것으로 아예 단념해 버렸다."], ["I am solemnly convinced that I never for one hour was reconciled to it, or was otherwise than miserably unhappy; but I bore it; and even to Peggotty, partly for the love of her and partly for shame, never in any letter (though many passed between us) revealed the truth.", "단 한 시간도 이 삶에 마음을 붙인 적이 없었고, 비참하게 불행한 것 외에 다른 적이 없었음을 나는 엄숙히 확신한다. 그러나 나는 그것을 견뎠다. 그리고 패거티에게조차, 한편으로는 그녀를 사랑하는 마음에서, 한편으로는 부끄러움에서, 그 어떤 편지에서도(우리 사이에 편지가 숱하게 오갔지만) 진실을 결코 털어놓지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Micawber’s difficulties were an addition to the distressed state of my mind.", "미코버 씨의 곤경은 내 시름겨운 마음에 또 하나의 짐을 보탰다."], ["In my forlorn state I became quite attached to the family, and used to walk about, busy with Mrs. Micawber’s calculations of ways and means, and heavy with the weight of Mr. Micawber’s debts.", "외롭고 처량한 처지였던 나는 그 식구와 무척 정이 들어, 미코버 부인의 살림 셈에 골몰하고, 미코버 씨의 빚 무게에 짓눌린 채 이리저리 거닐곤 했다."], ["On a Saturday night, which was my grand treat,--partly because it was a great thing to walk home with six or seven shillings in my pocket, looking into the shops and thinking what such a sum would buy, and partly because I went home early,--Mrs. Micawber would make the most heart-rending confidences to me; also on a Sunday morning, when I mixed the portion of tea or coffee I had bought over-night, in a little shaving-pot, and sat late at my breakfast.", "토요일 밤은 나에게 더없는 즐거움이었는데 — 한편으로는 주머니에 육 실링인지 칠 실링인지를 넣고 집까지 걸어가며, 가게들을 들여다보고 그만한 돈이면 무엇을 살 수 있을까 헤아려 보는 것이 대단한 일이었고, 한편으로는 일찍 집에 들어갔기 때문이었다 — 그런 토요일 밤이면 미코버 부인은 가슴이 미어지는 속내를 내게 털어놓곤 했다. 일요일 아침에도 그랬는데, 그때면 나는 전날 밤 사 둔 차나 커피를 작은 면도용 단지에 타서, 느지막이 앉아 아침을 먹었다."], ["It was nothing at all unusual for Mr. Micawber to sob violently at the beginning of one of these Saturday night conversations, and sing about Jack’s delight being his lovely Nan, towards the end of it.", "미코버 씨가 이런 토요일 밤 이야기를 시작할 무렵에는 격하게 흐느끼다가, 끝날 무렵에는 '잭의 기쁨은 그의 사랑스러운 낸'이라는 노래를 부르는 것은 조금도 별난 일이 아니었다."], ["I have known him come home to supper with a flood of tears, and a declaration that nothing was now left but a jail; and go to bed making a calculation of the expense of putting bow-windows to the house, ‘in case anything turned up’, which was his favourite expression. And Mrs.", "나는 그가 눈물을 펑펑 쏟으며 저녁을 먹으러 돌아와서는 이제 남은 건 감옥뿐이라고 단언하더니, 잠자리에 들 무렵에는 '혹시 무슨 수가 생길 경우에' 대비해 집에 내닫이창을 다는 비용을 셈하는 — '혹시 무슨 수가 생기면'은 그가 즐겨 쓰던 말이었다 — 것을 본 적도 있다."], ["Micawber was just the same.", "그리고 미코버 부인도 똑같았다."]], [["A curious equality of friendship, originating, I suppose, in our respective circumstances, sprung up between me and these people, notwithstanding the ludicrous disparity in our years.", "우리 사이의 나이 차가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 컸음에도, 나와 이 사람들 사이에는 묘하게 대등한 우정이 싹텄는데, 짐작건대 서로의 처지에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["But I never allowed myself to be prevailed upon to accept any invitation to eat and drink with them out of their stock (knowing that they got on badly with the butcher and baker, and had often not too much for themselves), until Mrs. Micawber took me into her entire confidence.", "그러나 나는 그들이 자기네 양식에서 덜어 함께 먹고 마시자고 권할 때마다, 한 번도 거기 넘어가지 않았다(푸줏간이며 빵집과 사이가 좋지 않아 자기네 먹을 것도 넉넉지 못한 적이 많다는 것을 알았으니까). 미코버 부인이 내게 속내를 죄다 털어놓기 전까지는 그랬다."], ["This she did one evening as follows:", "부인은 어느 저녁 이렇게 털어놓았다."]], [["‘Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"코퍼필드 도련님,\""], ["Micawber, ‘I make no stranger of you, and therefore do not hesitate to say that Mr. Micawber’s difficulties are coming to a crisis.’", "미코버 부인이 말했다. \"나는 도련님을 남으로 여기지 않으니, 미코버 씨의 곤경이 고비에 이르렀다는 말을 망설임 없이 하겠어요.\""]], [["It made me very miserable to hear it, and I looked at Mrs. Micawber’s red eyes with the utmost sympathy.", "그 말을 들으니 무척이나 비참한 심정이 되어, 나는 더없는 연민을 담아 미코버 부인의 빨개진 눈을 바라보았다."]], [["‘With the exception of the heel of a Dutch cheese--which is not adapted to the wants of a young family’--said Mrs.", "\"네덜란드 치즈 끄트머리 한 조각만 빼면 — 그것도 어린 식구들 입에는 안 맞는 것이지만요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘there is really not a scrap of anything in the larder.", "\"정말이지 식료품 저장실에 먹을 것이라곤 부스러기 하나 없어요."], ["I was accustomed to speak of the larder when I lived with papa and mama, and I use the word almost unconsciously.", "아빠 엄마와 함께 살 적에는 식료품 저장실이라는 말을 입에 달고 살았던지라, 거의 무심코 그 말을 쓰게 되네요."], ["What I mean to express is, that there is nothing to eat in the house.’", "내가 하려는 말은, 집에 먹을 게 아무것도 없다는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ I said, in great concern.", "\"아이고 저런!\" 나는 몹시 걱정스러워 말했다."]], [["I had two or three shillings of my week’s money in my pocket--from which I presume that it must have been on a Wednesday night when we held this conversation--and I hastily produced them, and with heartfelt emotion begged Mrs. Micawber to accept of them as a loan.", "내 주머니에는 그 주 봉급에서 남은 이삼 실링이 있었는데 — 이걸 보면 우리가 이 이야기를 나눈 건 수요일 밤이었던 모양이다 — 나는 얼른 그것을 꺼내, 진심에서 우러나는 마음으로 미코버 부인에게 빌려 드릴 테니 받아 달라고 간청했다."], ["But that lady, kissing me, and making me put them back in my pocket, replied that she couldn’t think of it.", "그러나 부인은 내게 입을 맞추고는 그것을 도로 주머니에 넣게 하면서, 그건 생각조차 할 수 없는 일이라고 대답했다."]], [["‘No, my dear Master Copperfield,’ said she, ‘far be it from my thoughts!", "\"아니에요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 부인이 말했다. \"내 머릿속엔 그런 생각이 전혀 없어요!"], ["But you have a discretion beyond your years, and can render me another kind of service, if you will; and a service I will thankfully accept of.’", "하지만 도련님은 나이답지 않게 분별이 있으니, 마음만 있다면 내게 다른 종류의 도움을 줄 수 있어요. 그런 도움이라면 나도 고맙게 받겠고요.\""]], [["I begged Mrs. Micawber to name it.", "나는 미코버 부인에게 그게 무엇인지 말해 달라고 청했다."]], [["‘I have parted with the plate myself,’ said Mrs. Micawber.", "\"식기는 내가 직접 처분했어요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Six tea, two salt, and a pair of sugars, I have at different times borrowed money on, in secret, with my own hands.", "\"찻숟가락 여섯, 소금 숟가락 둘, 설탕 숟가락 한 쌍을, 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 남몰래, 내 손으로 직접 돈을 빌려 잡혔지요."], ["But the twins are a great tie; and to me, with my recollections, of papa and mama, these transactions are very painful.", "하지만 쌍둥이가 발목을 단단히 잡으니, 게다가 아빠 엄마에 대한 추억이 있는 나로서는, 이런 거래가 무척이나 가슴 아파요."], ["There are still a few trifles that we could part with. Mr.", "그래도 우리가 처분할 만한 자질구레한 물건이 아직 몇 가지 남아 있어요."], ["Micawber’s feelings would never allow him to dispose of them; and Clickett’--this was the girl from the workhouse--‘being of a vulgar mind, would take painful liberties if so much confidence was reposed in her.", "미코버 씨의 마음으로는 그걸 내다 팔 엄두가 도무지 안 날 테고, 클리킷은 — 이게 구빈원에서 온 그 여자아이였다 — 마음 씀씀이가 천박해서, 그렇게 큰일을 맡겼다가는 주제넘게 굴어 속을 썩일 거예요."], ["Master Copperfield, if I might ask you--’", "코퍼필드 도련님, 부탁드려도 될까요 —\""]], [["I understood Mrs. Micawber now, and begged her to make use of me to any extent.", "나는 이제 미코버 부인의 뜻을 알아채고, 얼마든지 나를 부려 달라고 청했다."], ["I began to dispose of the more portable articles of property that very evening; and went out on a similar expedition almost every morning, before I went to Murdstone and Grinby’s.", "나는 바로 그날 저녁부터 들고 다니기 좋은 물건들을 처분하기 시작했고, 거의 매일 아침, 머드스톤과 그린비로 가기 전에 비슷한 심부름을 하러 나갔다."]], [["Mr. Micawber had a few books on a little chiffonier, which he called the library; and those went first.", "미코버 씨에게는 작은 장식장 위에 책이 몇 권 있었는데, 그는 그것을 서재라고 불렀다. 그것들이 먼저 나갔다."], ["I carried them, one after another, to a bookstall in the City Road--one part of which, near our house, was almost all bookstalls and bird shops then--and sold them for whatever they would bring.", "나는 그 책들을 한 권 한 권, 시티 로드에 있는 어느 헌책 노점으로 날라다가 — 우리 집 가까운 그 일대는 그때 거의 다 헌책 노점과 새 가게뿐이었다 — 값나가는 대로 팔았다."], ["The keeper of this bookstall, who lived in a little house behind it, used to get tipsy every night, and to be violently scolded by his wife every morning.", "이 노점 주인은 그 뒤편 작은 집에 살았는데, 밤마다 곤드레만드레 취했고 아침마다 제 아내에게 사납게 닦달을 당했다."], ["More than once, when I went there early, I had audience of him in a turn-up bedstead, with a cut in his forehead or a black eye, bearing witness to his excesses over-night (I am afraid he was quarrelsome in his drink), and he, with a shaking hand, endeavouring to find the needful shillings in one or other of the pockets of his clothes, which lay upon the floor, while his wife, with a baby in her arms and her shoes down at heel, never left off rating him.", "내가 일찍 그곳에 갔을 때, 접이식 침대에 누운 그를 만나는 일이 한두 번이 아니었다. 이마에 베인 상처가 있거나 눈가가 시퍼렇게 멍든 채로, 전날 밤의 과음을 증언하고 있었고(술이 들어가면 시비를 거는 버릇이 있었던 듯하다), 그는 떨리는 손으로 마룻바닥에 널브러진 옷가지의 이 주머니 저 주머니를 뒤져 필요한 실링을 찾으려 애쓰는 동안, 아기를 품에 안고 신발 뒤축이 닳아빠진 그의 아내는 끊임없이 그를 들들 볶아 댔다."], ["Sometimes he had lost his money, and then he would ask me to call again; but his wife had always got some--had taken his, I dare say, while he was drunk--and secretly completed the bargain on the stairs, as we went down together.", "어떤 때는 그가 돈을 잃어버려서, 다시 들르라고 내게 부탁하기도 했다. 하지만 그의 아내는 늘 얼마간 돈을 갖고 있었고 — 분명히 그가 취한 사이에 그의 돈을 챙겨 둔 것이리라 — 우리가 함께 계단을 내려갈 때 몰래 그 계단에서 거래를 마무리 지었다."], ["At the pawnbroker’s shop, too, I began to be very well known.", "전당포에서도 나는 꽤 낯이 익은 손님이 되기 시작했다."], ["The principal gentleman who officiated behind the counter, took a good deal of notice of me; and often got me, I recollect, to decline a Latin noun or adjective, or to conjugate a Latin verb, in his ear, while he transacted my business.", "계산대 뒤를 맡은 주된 신사는 나를 무척 눈여겨보았고, 내 거래를 처리하는 동안 종종 나더러 라틴어 명사나 형용사를 변화시켜 보라거나 라틴어 동사를 활용시켜 제 귀에 들려 달라고 시키곤 했던 기억이 난다."], ["After all these occasions Mrs. Micawber made a little treat, which was generally a supper; and there was a peculiar relish in these meals which I well remember.", "이 모든 일을 치르고 나면 미코버 부인은 작은 잔치를 벌였는데, 대개는 저녁상이었고, 이 끼니에는 내가 지금도 또렷이 기억하는 별스러운 맛이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["At last Mr. Micawber’s difficulties came to a crisis, and he was arrested early one morning, and carried over to the King’s Bench Prison in the Borough.", "마침내 미코버 씨의 곤경이 고비에 이르렀고, 그는 어느 날 아침 일찍 붙잡혀 자치구의 킹스 벤치 감옥으로 끌려갔다."], ["He told me, as he went out of the house, that the God of day had now gone down upon him--and I really thought his heart was broken and mine too.", "그는 집을 나서면서 내게, 이제 자기에게서 한낮의 해가 졌노라고 말했다 — 그리고 나는 정말로 그의 가슴이 무너졌고, 내 가슴도 무너졌다고 생각했다."], ["But I heard, afterwards, that he was seen to play a lively game at skittles, before noon.", "그러나 나중에 들으니, 그는 한낮이 되기도 전에 신나게 구주희 놀이를 하더라는 것이었다."]], [["On the first Sunday after he was taken there, I was to go and see him, and have dinner with him.", "그가 거기 끌려간 뒤 처음 맞는 일요일, 나는 그를 보러 가서 함께 점심을 먹기로 했다."], ["I was to ask my way to such a place, and just short of that place I should see such another place, and just short of that I should see a yard, which I was to cross, and keep straight on until I saw a turnkey.", "어디어디로 가는 길을 묻고, 그 자리에 거의 다다르면 또 다른 어디어디가 보일 것이며, 그 자리에 거의 다다르면 마당이 하나 보일 텐데, 그 마당을 가로질러 곧장 가다 보면 간수가 보일 거라고 했다."], ["All this I did; and when at last I did see a turnkey (poor little fellow that I was!), and thought how, when Roderick Random was in a debtors’ prison, there was a man there with nothing on him but an old rug, the turnkey swam before my dimmed eyes and my beating heart.", "나는 이 모든 것을 그대로 따랐다. 그리고 마침내 간수가 정말로 눈에 들어왔을 때(가엾고 작은 나였다!), 로더릭 랜덤이 채무자 감옥에 있을 적에 거기 낡은 깔개 한 장만 걸친 사내가 있었다는 대목을 떠올리자, 흐려진 내 두 눈과 두근거리는 가슴 앞에서 간수의 모습이 일렁거렸다."]], [["Mr. Micawber was waiting for me within the gate, and we went up to his room (top story but one), and cried very much.", "미코버 씨가 정문 안쪽에서 나를 기다리고 있었고, 우리는 그의 방으로(꼭대기 바로 아래층이었다) 올라가 한참을 펑펑 울었다."], ["He solemnly conjured me, I remember, to take warning by his fate; and to observe that if a man had twenty pounds a-year for his income, and spent nineteen pounds nineteen shillings and sixpence, he would be happy, but that if he spent twenty pounds one he would be miserable.", "그가 엄숙하게 나더러 자기의 운명을 경계 삼으라고, 그리고 한 사람의 한 해 수입이 이십 파운드인데 십구 파운드 십구 실링 육 펜스를 쓰면 행복하겠지만, 이십 파운드 일 실링을 쓰면 비참해진다는 것을 명심하라고 당부하던 기억이 난다."], ["After which he borrowed a shilling of me for porter, gave me a written order on Mrs. Micawber for the amount, and put away his pocket-handkerchief, and cheered up.", "그러고 나서 그는 포터 값으로 내게 일 실링을 빌려, 그 액수만큼 미코버 부인에게 지급하라는 증서를 써 주더니, 손수건을 집어넣고 기운을 차렸다."]], [["We sat before a little fire, with two bricks put within the rusted grate, one on each side, to prevent its burning too many coals; until another debtor, who shared the room with Mr. Micawber, came in from the bakehouse with the loin of mutton which was our joint-stock repast.", "우리는 녹슨 화격자에 벽돌 두 장을 양쪽으로 하나씩 끼워 둔 — 석탄이 너무 많이 타들어 가는 것을 막으려는 것이었다 — 작은 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있었다. 그러다 미코버 씨와 방을 함께 쓰는 또 다른 채무자가 빵 굽는 데서 양고기 허릿살을 가지고 들어왔는데, 그것이 우리의 공동 식사였다."], ["Then I was sent up to ‘Captain Hopkins’ in the room overhead, with Mr. Micawber’s compliments, and I was his young friend, and would Captain Hopkins lend me a knife and fork.", "그러고 나서 나는 미코버 씨의 안부 인사를 전하고, 내가 그의 어린 친구인데, 홉킨스 대위께서 나이프와 포크를 빌려 주실 수 있겠느냐고 여쭈러, 바로 위층 방에 있는 '홉킨스 대위'에게 보내졌다."]], [["Captain Hopkins lent me the knife and fork, with his compliments to Mr.", "홉킨스 대위는 미코버 씨에게 안부를 전하며 나이프와 포크를 빌려 주었다."], ["Micawber. There was a very dirty lady in his little room, and two wan girls, his daughters, with shock heads of hair.", "그의 작은 방에는 무척 지저분한 부인과, 텁수룩한 머리를 한 그의 두 딸인 핼쑥한 소녀 둘이 있었다."], ["I thought it was better to borrow Captain Hopkins’s knife and fork, than Captain Hopkins’s comb.", "나는 홉킨스 대위의 빗을 빌리기보다는 홉킨스 대위의 나이프와 포크를 빌리는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."], ["The Captain himself was in the last extremity of shabbiness, with large whiskers, and an old, old brown great-coat with no other coat below it.", "대위 자신은 추레하기가 극에 달한 차림이었는데, 커다란 구레나룻에다, 그 밑에 다른 코트라곤 없이 아주 오래되고 낡은 갈색 외투를 걸치고 있었다."], ["I saw his bed rolled up in a corner; and what plates and dishes and pots he had, on a shelf; and I divined (God knows how) that though the two girls with the shock heads of hair were Captain Hopkins’s children, the dirty lady was not married to Captain Hopkins.", "한구석에 둘둘 말아 놓은 그의 침대가 보였고, 선반에는 그가 가진 접시며 그릇이며 항아리가 놓여 있었다. 그리고 나는 (어떻게 그랬는지는 하느님만 아시리라) 텁수룩한 머리의 두 소녀는 홉킨스 대위의 딸이지만, 그 지저분한 부인은 홉킨스 대위와 결혼한 사이가 아니라는 것을 알아챘다."], ["My timid station on his threshold was not occupied more than a couple of minutes at most; but I came down again with all this in my knowledge, as surely as the knife and fork were in my hand.", "그의 문지방에 겁먹은 채 서 있던 시간은 길어야 이삼 분에 지나지 않았으나, 나는 손에 나이프와 포크가 들려 있는 것만큼이나 확실하게, 이 모든 것을 알아챈 채로 다시 내려왔다."]], [["There was something gipsy-like and agreeable in the dinner, after all.", "그래도 그 점심에는 어딘가 집시 같고 흐뭇한 데가 있었다."], ["I took back Captain Hopkins’s knife and fork early in the afternoon, and went home to comfort Mrs.", "나는 오후 일찍 홉킨스 대위의 나이프와 포크를 돌려주고, 내 방문 이야기를 들려주어 미코버 부인을 위로하려고 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["Micawber with an account of my visit. She fainted when she saw me return, and made a little jug of egg-hot afterwards to console us while we talked it over.", "부인은 내가 돌아오는 것을 보자 까무러쳤고, 그러고 나서는 우리가 그 일을 두고 이야기하는 동안 마음을 달래려고 달걀을 푼 따끈한 술 한 단지를 만들었다."]], [["I don’t know how the household furniture came to be sold for the family benefit, or who sold it, except that I did not.", "집안 살림살이가 어쩌다가 가족을 위해 팔리게 됐는지, 또 누가 그것을 팔았는지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그걸 판 사람이 나는 아니다."], ["Sold it was, however, and carried away in a van; except the bed, a few chairs, and the kitchen table.", "그러나 살림은 팔렸고, 침대와 의자 몇 개, 부엌 탁자만 빼고는 짐마차에 실려 나갔다."], ["With these possessions we encamped, as it were, in the two parlours of the emptied house in Windsor Terrace; Mrs.", "우리는 이 세간을 가지고, 말하자면 텅 빈 윈저 테라스 집의 응접실 두 칸에 진을 쳤다."], ["Micawber, the children, the Orfling, and myself; and lived in those rooms night and day.", "미코버 부인과 아이들, 그 고아 소녀, 그리고 나, 이렇게 그 방에서 밤낮없이 지냈다."], ["I have no idea for how long, though it seems to me for a long time. At last Mrs.", "그게 얼마 동안이었는지는 모르겠지만, 내게는 꽤 오랜 시간으로 느껴진다."], ["Micawber resolved to move into the prison, where Mr. Micawber had now secured a room to himself.", "마침내 미코버 부인은 감옥으로 옮겨 가기로 마음먹었다. 미코버 씨가 이제 거기서 자기 혼자 쓸 방을 하나 마련해 두었던 것이다."], ["So I took the key of the house to the landlord, who was very glad to get it; and the beds were sent over to the King’s Bench, except mine, for which a little room was hired outside the walls in the neighbourhood of that Institution, very much to my satisfaction, since the Micawbers and I had become too used to one another, in our troubles, to part.", "그래서 나는 집 열쇠를 집주인에게 갖다주었고, 집주인은 그것을 돌려받게 되어 무척 기뻐했다. 침대들은 내 것만 빼고 모두 킹스 벤치로 보내졌는데, 내 침대를 위해서는 그 시설 근처 담장 바깥에 작은 방 하나를 빌렸다. 미코버 식구와 나는 그 고생을 함께 겪으며 서로 너무 익숙해진 터라 떨어지기가 싫었으니, 이는 내게 무척 흡족한 일이었다."], ["The Orfling was likewise accommodated with an inexpensive lodging in the same neighbourhood.", "그 고아 소녀도 마찬가지로 같은 동네에 값싼 하숙을 마련했다."], ["Mine was a quiet back-garret with a sloping roof, commanding a pleasant prospect of a timberyard; and when I took possession of it, with the reflection that Mr. Micawber’s troubles had come to a crisis at last, I thought it quite a paradise.", "내 방은 비스듬한 지붕이 덮인 조용한 뒤채 다락방이었는데, 목재 하치장이 시원하게 내다보였다. 그 방을 차지하면서, 미코버 씨의 시련이 드디어 고비를 넘겼다는 생각을 곱씹으니, 나는 그곳이 아주 천국 같다고 여겼다."]], [["All this time I was working at Murdstone and Grinby’s in the same common way, and with the same common companions, and with the same sense of unmerited degradation as at first.", "이 내내 나는 머드스톤과 그린비에서 처음과 똑같이 천한 일을, 똑같이 천한 동무들과, 그리고 까닭 없이 추락했다는 똑같은 느낌을 안고 일하고 있었다."], ["But I never, happily for me no doubt, made a single acquaintance, or spoke to any of the many boys whom I saw daily in going to the warehouse, in coming from it, and in prowling about the streets at meal-times.", "그러나 나에게는 다행스럽게도, 나는 단 한 명의 친구도 사귀지 않았고, 창고로 오가는 길에서나 끼니때 거리를 어슬렁거릴 때 날마다 마주치던 그 숱한 소년들 가운데 그 누구와도 말을 섞지 않았다."], ["I led the same secretly unhappy life; but I led it in the same lonely, self-reliant manner.", "나는 여전히 남몰래 불행한 삶을 살았다. 그러나 여전히 외롭게, 제 힘에만 기대어 그렇게 살았다."], ["The only changes I am conscious of are, firstly, that I had grown more shabby, and secondly, that I was now relieved of much of the weight of Mr. and Mrs.", "내가 의식하는 변화라곤 단 두 가지뿐이다. 첫째, 내 차림이 더 추레해졌다는 것, 둘째, 이제 미코버 부부의 시름이라는 무거운 짐을 상당히 덜게 됐다는 것이다."], ["Micawber’s cares; for some relatives or friends had engaged to help them at their present pass, and they lived more comfortably in the prison than they had lived for a long while out of it.", "어떤 친척인지 친구인지가 지금의 이 고비를 넘기도록 그들을 돕기로 했고, 그들은 오랫동안 감옥 밖에서 살던 것보다 감옥 안에서 더 편히 지냈으니까."], ["I used to breakfast with them now, in virtue of some arrangement, of which I have forgotten the details.", "나는 이제 어떤 약조 덕분에 그들과 함께 아침을 먹곤 했는데, 그 약조의 자세한 내용은 잊어버렸다."], ["I forget, too, at what hour the gates were opened in the morning, admitting of my going in; but I know that I was often up at six o’clock, and that my favourite lounging-place in the interval was old London Bridge, where I was wont to sit in one of the stone recesses, watching the people going by, or to look over the balustrades at the sun shining in the water, and lighting up the golden flame on the top of the Monument.", "아침에 몇 시에 문이 열려 내가 들어갈 수 있었는지도 잊어버렸다. 다만 나는 자주 여섯 시에 일어났다는 것, 그리고 그 짬에 내가 가장 즐겨 어슬렁거리던 곳이 낡은 런던 다리였다는 것은 안다. 거기서 나는 돌로 움푹 파인 자리 가운데 한 곳에 앉아 오가는 사람들을 바라보거나, 난간 너머로 물에 비쳐 반짝이는 햇살이 모뉴먼트 꼭대기의 황금빛 불길을 밝히는 것을 내려다보곤 했다."], ["The Orfling met me here sometimes, to be told some astonishing fictions respecting the wharves and the Tower; of which I can say no more than that I hope I believed them myself.", "그 고아 소녀가 가끔 여기서 나를 만나, 부두와 런던탑에 얽힌 놀라운 꾸민 이야기들을 들려달라 하곤 했는데, 그에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 나 자신도 그 이야기들을 믿었기를 바란다는 것뿐이다."], ["In the evening I used to go back to the prison, and walk up and down the parade with Mr. Micawber; or play casino with Mrs. Micawber, and hear reminiscences of her papa and mama.", "저녁이면 나는 감옥으로 돌아가, 미코버 씨와 함께 산책로를 오르락내리락 거닐거나, 미코버 부인과 카지노 놀이를 하며 그녀의 아빠 엄마에 대한 추억담을 듣곤 했다."], ["Whether Mr. Murdstone knew where I was, I am unable to say.", "머드스톤 씨가 내가 어디 있는지 알았는지는 나도 말할 수 없다."], ["I never told them at Murdstone and Grinby’s.", "나는 머드스톤과 그린비에서는 그 일을 절대로 말하지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Micawber’s affairs, although past their crisis, were very much involved by reason of a certain ‘Deed’, of which I used to hear a great deal, and which I suppose, now, to have been some former composition with his creditors, though I was so far from being clear about it then, that I am conscious of having confounded it with those demoniacal parchments which are held to have, once upon a time, obtained to a great extent in Germany.", "미코버 씨의 일은, 고비는 넘겼다 해도, 어떤 '증서' 때문에 무척이나 뒤얽혀 있었다."], ["At last this document appeared to be got out of the way, somehow; at all events it ceased to be the rock-ahead it had been; and Mrs.", "나는 그 얘기를 숱하게 들었는데, 지금 짐작건대 그것은 예전에 채권자들과 맺은 어떤 화해 약정이었던 듯하다. 하지만 그때는 그게 무언지 도무지 알 수가 없어서, 옛날에 독일에 한때 널리 퍼졌다는 그 악마와 맺은 양피지 계약서와 그것을 헷갈렸던 기억이 난다."], ["Micawber informed me that ‘her family’ had decided that Mr. Micawber should apply for his release under the Insolvent Debtors Act, which would set him free, she expected, in about six weeks.", "마침내 이 서류가 어떻게든 처리된 모양이었고, 어쨌든 그것이 앞을 가로막던 암초이기를 그쳤다. 그리고 미코버 부인이 내게 알려 주기를, '자기 집안'에서 미코버 씨가 파산 채무자법에 따라 석방 신청을 하기로 결정했다는 것이었는데, 그러면 그가 한 육 주쯤 뒤에는 풀려나리라고 부인은 기대했다."]], [["‘And then,’ said Mr.", "\"그러면 그때는,\""], ["Micawber, who was present, ‘I have no doubt I shall, please Heaven, begin to be beforehand with the world, and to live in a perfectly new manner, if--in short, if anything turns up.’", "그 자리에 있던 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"틀림없이, 하느님이 도우시면, 세상에 한발 앞서기 시작해서, 아주 새로운 방식으로 살아갈 수 있을 게요. 만약 — 요컨대, 무슨 수가 생기기만 한다면 말이오.\""]], [["By way of going in for anything that might be on the cards, I call to mind that Mr. Micawber, about this time, composed a petition to the House of Commons, praying for an alteration in the law of imprisonment for debt.", "무슨 수든 생길 만한 것에 기대를 거는 셈으로, 미코버 씨가 이 무렵 채무자 투옥에 관한 법을 고쳐 달라고 간청하는 청원서를 하원 앞으로 지었던 기억이 난다."], ["I set down this remembrance here, because it is an instance to myself of the manner in which I fitted my old books to my altered life, and made stories for myself, out of the streets, and out of men and women; and how some main points in the character I shall unconsciously develop, I suppose, in writing my life, were gradually forming all this while.", "내가 이 기억을 여기 적어 두는 것은, 이것이 내가 옛날에 읽은 책들을 달라진 내 삶에 어떻게 끼워 맞췄는지, 거리와 남녀들에게서 어떻게 나 자신을 위한 이야기를 지어냈는지, 그리고 내가 내 인생을 쓰면서 나도 모르게 펼쳐 보일 성격의 어떤 중요한 대목들이 이 내내 어떻게 조금씩 빚어지고 있었는지를 나 스스로에게 보여 주는 한 본보기이기 때문이다."]], [["There was a club in the prison, in which Mr. Micawber, as a gentleman, was a great authority. Mr.", "감옥 안에는 클럽이 하나 있었는데, 신사인 미코버 씨는 거기서 대단한 권위자였다."], ["Micawber had stated his idea of this petition to the club, and the club had strongly approved of the same.", "미코버 씨가 클럽에 이 청원서에 대한 자기 생각을 밝히자, 클럽은 그것을 강력히 찬성했다."], ["Wherefore Mr. Micawber (who was a thoroughly good-natured man, and as active a creature about everything but his own affairs as ever existed, and never so happy as when he was busy about something that could never be of any profit to him) set to work at the petition, invented it, engrossed it on an immense sheet of paper, spread it out on a table, and appointed a time for all the club, and all within the walls if they chose, to come up to his room and sign it.", "그리하여 미코버 씨는(천성이 더없이 선량한 사람이었고, 자기 일만 빼면 무슨 일에든 세상에 둘도 없이 부지런한 위인이었으며, 자기에게 아무 득도 될 수 없는 무언가에 바삐 매달릴 때보다 더 행복한 적이 없는 사람이었다) 청원서 작업에 착수하여, 그것을 지어내고, 어마어마하게 큰 종이에 정성껏 옮겨 적은 다음, 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓고, 클럽 사람 전부, 그리고 원하는 사람이라면 담장 안 누구라도 자기 방으로 올라와 거기 서명하도록 시각을 정해 두었다."]], [["When I heard of this approaching ceremony, I was so anxious to see them all come in, one after another, though I knew the greater part of them already, and they me, that I got an hour’s leave of absence from Murdstone and Grinby’s, and established myself in a corner for that purpose.", "이 다가오는 의식 이야기를 들었을 때, 나는 그들이 한 사람씩 차례로 들어오는 모습을 어찌나 보고 싶었던지 — 비록 그들 대부분을 이미 알고 있었고 그들도 나를 알고 있었지만 — 그 일을 위해 머드스톤과 그린비에서 한 시간 외출 허가를 받아, 한 귀퉁이에 자리를 잡았다."], ["As many of the principal members of the club as could be got into the small room without filling it, supported Mr. Micawber in front of the petition, while my old friend Captain Hopkins (who had washed himself, to do honour to so solemn an occasion) stationed himself close to it, to read it to all who were unacquainted with its contents.", "그 작은 방을 꽉 채우지 않으면서 들어올 수 있는 만큼의 클럽 주요 회원들이 청원서 앞에 미코버 씨를 둘러싸고 받쳐 섰고, 내 옛 친구 홉킨스 대위는(이토록 엄숙한 자리를 빛내려고 몸을 씻은 채로) 그 곁에 바짝 자리를 잡고 서서, 내용을 모르는 모든 이에게 그것을 읽어 주었다. 그러고 나서 문이 활짝 열리고, 일반 재소자들이 긴 줄을 지어 들어오기 시작했다."], ["The door was then thrown open, and the general population began to come in, in a long file: several waiting outside, while one entered, affixed his signature, and went out.", "한 사람이 들어와 서명하고 나가는 동안 여럿이 바깥에서 기다렸다. 차례차례 들어오는 모든 사람에게 홉킨스 대위는 말했다. \"읽어 보셨소?\""], ["To everybody in succession, Captain Hopkins said: ‘Have you read it?’--‘No.’---‘Would you like to hear it read?’ If he weakly showed the least disposition to hear it, Captain Hopkins, in a loud sonorous voice, gave him every word of it.", "— \"아니요.\" — \"읽어 드릴까요?\" 그자가 조금이라도 들어 볼 뜻을 살짝 비치는 어수룩함을 보이기만 하면, 홉킨스 대위는 우렁차고 낭랑한 목소리로 그것을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 읽어 주었다."], ["The Captain would have read it twenty thousand times, if twenty thousand people would have heard him, one by one.", "만 명이 한 사람씩 차례로 들어 주기만 한다면, 대위는 그것을 이만 번이라도 읽었을 것이다."], ["I remember a certain luscious roll he gave to such phrases as ‘The people’s representatives in Parliament assembled,’ ‘Your petitioners therefore humbly approach your honourable house,’ ‘His gracious Majesty’s unfortunate subjects,’ as if the words were something real in his mouth, and delicious to taste; Mr.", "'의회에 모인 국민의 대표 여러분', '하여 청원인들은 삼가 귀 명예로운 의회에 다가서나이다', '인자하신 폐하의 불운한 신민들' 같은 구절들을 그가 어찌나 감칠맛 나게 굴려 댔던지, 마치 그 말들이 그의 입속에서 무슨 실체라도 되고 음미할수록 맛깔스럽기라도 한 듯하던 기억이 난다."], ["Micawber, meanwhile, listening with a little of an author’s vanity, and contemplating (not severely) the spikes on the opposite wall.", "그러는 동안 미코버 씨는 작가다운 허영심을 조금 풍기며 귀를 기울였고, 맞은편 벽의 (그리 모질지 않은 눈길로) 쇠창살 끝을 물끄러미 바라보고 있었다."]], [["As I walked to and fro daily between Southwark and Blackfriars, and lounged about at meal-times in obscure streets, the stones of which may, for anything I know, be worn at this moment by my childish feet, I wonder how many of these people were wanting in the crowd that used to come filing before me in review again, to the echo of Captain Hopkins’s voice!", "내가 날마다 사우스워크와 블랙프라이어스 사이를 오가며, 끼니때면 어두컴컴한 거리들을 — 내가 알기로 그 자리의 돌은 어쩌면 지금 이 순간에도 그 시절 내 어린 발길에 닳고 있을지 모른다 — 어슬렁거리던 그때, 홉킨스 대위의 목소리가 메아리치는 가운데 내 앞에 다시 줄지어 행진하듯 지나가던 그 사람들 중에 몇이나 그 무리에 끼어 있지 않았을까 싶다!"], ["When my thoughts go back, now, to that slow agony of my youth, I wonder how much of the histories I invented for such people hangs like a mist of fancy over well-remembered facts!", "이제 내 생각이 내 젊은 날의 그 더디고 고통스러운 시절로 돌아갈 때면, 그런 사람들을 두고 내가 지어낸 이야기들이 또렷이 기억나는 사실들 위에 얼마나 짙은 환상의 안개처럼 드리워져 있는지 새삼 놀랍다!"], ["When I tread the old ground, I do not wonder that I seem to see and pity, going on before me, an innocent romantic boy, making his imaginative world out of such strange experiences and sordid things!", "그 옛 자리를 다시 밟으면, 그토록 기묘한 경험들과 추레한 일들로 제 상상의 세계를 빚어 가며 내 앞에서 걸어가는 천진하고 몽상에 빠진 한 소년이 보이고, 또 그 아이가 가엾게 여겨지는 것도 조금도 이상하지 않다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 12. LIKING LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT NO BETTER, I FORM A GREAT RESOLUTION", "# CHAPTER 12. LIKING LIFE ON MY OWN ACCOUNT NO BETTER, I FORM A GREAT RESOLUTION"]], [["In due time, Mr. Micawber’s petition was ripe for hearing; and that gentleman was ordered to be discharged under the Act, to my great joy.", "때가 무르익어, 미코버 씨의 청원이 심리를 받을 만하게 되었고, 그리하여 그분은 채무자 구제법에 따라 풀려나라는 명을 받았으니, 나는 더없이 기뻤다."], ["His creditors were not implacable; and Mrs. Micawber informed me that even the revengeful boot-maker had declared in open court that he bore him no malice, but that when money was owing to him he liked to be paid.", "채권자들도 매정하지는 않았다. 미코버 부인이 알려 준 바로는, 그토록 앙심을 품었던 구둣방 주인조차 공개된 법정에서, 자기는 그분에게 아무런 악의도 없으며 다만 돈을 받을 게 있으면 받고 싶을 뿐이라고 단언했다는 것이었다."], ["He said he thought it was human nature.", "그 사람은 그게 인지상정이라고 생각한다고 했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber returned to the King’s Bench when his case was over, as some fees were to be settled, and some formalities observed, before he could be actually released.", "미코버 씨는 자기 사건이 끝나자 다시 킹스 벤치로 돌아갔는데, 실제로 풀려나기 전에 몇 가지 수수료를 정산하고 몇 가지 절차를 밟아야 했기 때문이다."], ["The club received him with transport, and held an harmonic meeting that evening in his honour; while Mrs.", "동아리는 그분을 열렬히 맞아들였고, 그날 저녁 그분을 기리는 합창 모임을 열었다."], ["Micawber and I had a lamb’s fry in private, surrounded by the sleeping family.", "그동안 미코버 부인과 나는, 잠든 식구들에 둘러싸인 채, 단둘이서 양 내장 볶음을 먹었다."]], [["‘On such an occasion I will give you, Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"이런 날에는 말이에요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘in a little more flip,’ for we had been having some already, ‘the memory of my papa and mama.’", "\"플립을 조금만 더 곁들여서요.\" 우리는 이미 얼마쯤 마시고 있던 참이었다. \"제 아빠와 엄마를 추모하며 한잔 올리겠어요.\""]], [["‘Are they dead, ma’am?’ I inquired, after drinking the toast in a wine-glass.", "\"그분들은 돌아가셨나요, 부인?\" 나는 와인 잔으로 그 건배를 들이켠 뒤에 여쭈었다."]], [["‘My mama departed this life,’ said Mrs.", "\"제 엄마는 이 세상을 떠나셨어요.\""], ["Micawber, ‘before Mr.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber’s difficulties commenced, or at least before they became pressing.", "\"미코버 씨의 곤경이 시작되기 전에, 아니 적어도 그게 다급해지기 전에요."], ["My papa lived to bail Mr. Micawber several times, and then expired, regretted by a numerous circle.’", "제 아빠는 미코버 씨의 보석금을 여러 번 대 주실 만큼 오래 사셨다가, 그러고는 숨을 거두셨는데, 수많은 분이 애석해했지요.\""]], [["Mrs. Micawber shook her head, and dropped a pious tear upon the twin who happened to be in hand.", "미코버 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 마침 품에 안고 있던 쌍둥이 위로 경건한 눈물 한 방울을 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["As I could hardly hope for a more favourable opportunity of putting a question in which I had a near interest, I said to Mrs. Micawber:", "나로서는 나에게 절실한 관심사를 여쭤보기에 이보다 더 좋은 기회를 좀처럼 바랄 수 없었으므로, 미코버 부인에게 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘May I ask, ma’am, what you and Mr.", "\"여쭤봐도 될까요, 부인."], ["Micawber intend to do, now that Mr. Micawber is out of his difficulties, and at liberty?", "이제 미코버 씨가 곤경에서 벗어나 자유로워지셨으니, 부인과 미코버 씨는 앞으로 어쩌실 작정이세요?"], ["Have you settled yet?’", "벌써 정하셨나요?\""]], [["‘My family,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리 친정 식구들은,\""], ["Micawber, who always said those two words with an air, though I never could discover who came under the denomination, ‘my family are of opinion that Mr.", "미코버 부인이 말했다. 부인은 늘 그 '친정 식구들'이라는 말을 거드름을 잔뜩 실어서 했는데, 나는 도대체 누가 그 이름에 들어가는지 끝내 알아내지 못했다."], ["Micawber should quit London, and exert his talents in the country. Mr.", "\"우리 친정 식구들은 미코버 씨가 런던을 떠나 시골에서 그 재능을 발휘해야 한다는 의견이에요."], ["Micawber is a man of great talent, Master Copperfield.’", "미코버 씨는 큰 재능을 지닌 분이거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\""]], [["I said I was sure of that.", "나는 그 점은 정말 그렇다고 말했다."]], [["‘Of great talent,’ repeated Mrs. Micawber.", "\"대단한 재능이지요.\" 미코버 부인이 거듭 말했다."], ["‘My family are of opinion, that, with a little interest, something might be done for a man of his ability in the Custom House.", "\"우리 친정 식구들은, 연줄을 조금만 쓰면 그분만큼 능력 있는 사람을 위해 세관에서 무슨 자리든 마련할 수 있으리라는 의견이에요."], ["The influence of my family being local, it is their wish that Mr. Micawber should go down to Plymouth.", "우리 친정 식구들의 영향력이 그 지방에 있어서, 미코버 씨가 플리머스로 내려가기를 바라고 있어요."], ["They think it indispensable that he should be upon the spot.’", "그분이 반드시 현장에 있어야 한다고들 생각하거든요.\""]], [["‘That he may be ready?’ I suggested.", "\"준비를 갖추고 계실 수 있게요?\" 내가 한마디 거들었다."]], [["‘Exactly,’ returned Mrs. Micawber.", "\"바로 그거예요.\" 미코버 부인이 받았다."], ["‘That he may be ready--in case of anything turning up.’", "\"준비를 갖추고 계실 수 있게요 — 무슨 수가 생길 경우를 대비해서요.\""]], [["‘And do you go too, ma’am?’", "\"그러면 부인도 함께 가시나요?\""]], [["The events of the day, in combination with the twins, if not with the flip, had made Mrs. Micawber hysterical, and she shed tears as she replied:", "그날 있었던 일들이, 플립까지는 몰라도 쌍둥이와 어우러져 미코버 부인을 격해지게 만든 모양인지, 부인은 눈물을 흘리며 대답했다."]], [["‘I never will desert Mr. Micawber. Mr.", "\"저는 결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 거예요."], ["Micawber may have concealed his difficulties from me in the first instance, but his sanguine temper may have led him to expect that he would overcome them.", "미코버 씨가 처음에는 곤경을 저한테 숨겼을지도 몰라요. 하지만 그분의 낙천적인 성품 탓에, 그걸 능히 헤쳐 나가리라 기대하셨던 거겠지요."], ["The pearl necklace and bracelets which I inherited from mama, have been disposed of for less than half their value; and the set of coral, which was the wedding gift of my papa, has been actually thrown away for nothing.", "엄마한테 물려받은 진주 목걸이와 팔찌는 그 값의 절반도 못 받고 처분해 버렸어요. 그리고 아빠가 결혼 선물로 주신 산호 세트는, 실은 한 푼도 못 받고 거저 내던지듯 팔아 버렸고요."], ["But I never will desert Mr. Micawber.", "그래도 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 거예요. 절대로요!\""], ["No!’ cried Mrs. Micawber, more affected than before, ‘I never will do it!", "미코버 부인은 아까보다 더 격해져서 외쳤다. \"저는 절대로 그러지 않을 거예요!"], ["It’s of no use asking me!’", "저한테 물어봐야 소용없어요!\""]], [["I felt quite uncomfortable--as if Mrs. Micawber supposed I had asked her to do anything of the sort!--and sat looking at her in alarm.", "나는 무척이나 거북했다 — 마치 미코버 부인이, 내가 그런 짓을 하라고 자기한테 요구하기라도 한 줄로 여기는 것 같아서 말이다! — 그래서 깜짝 놀라 부인을 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Mr. Micawber has his faults.", "\"미코버 씨한테도 허물은 있어요."], ["I do not deny that he is improvident.", "그분이 앞날을 헤아릴 줄 모른다는 건 부인하지 않겠어요. 그분이 자기 형편과 빚을 둘 다 저한테 깜깜하게 숨겨 왔다는 것도 부인하지 않아요.\""], ["I do not deny that he has kept me in the dark as to his resources and his liabilities both,’ she went on, looking at the wall; ‘but I never will desert Mr. Micawber!’", "부인은 벽을 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"하지만 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["Mrs. Micawber having now raised her voice into a perfect scream, I was so frightened that I ran off to the club-room, and disturbed Mr.", "미코버 부인이 이제 목소리를 완전히 비명에 가깝게 높이는 바람에, 나는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 동아리 방으로 뛰어갔다."], ["Micawber in the act of presiding at a long table, and leading the chorus of", "그리고 긴 식탁에서 좌장 노릇을 하며 합창을 이끌고 있던 미코버 씨를 방해하고 말았다."]], [["Gee up, Dobbin, Gee ho, Dobbin, Gee up, Dobbin, Gee up, and gee ho--o--o!", "이랴, 도빈, 워, 도빈, 이랴, 도빈, 이랴, 그리고 워어어!"]], [["--with the tidings that Mrs. Micawber was in an alarming state, upon which he immediately burst into tears, and came away with me with his waistcoat full of the heads and tails of shrimps, of which he had been partaking.", "— 그러니까 미코버 부인의 상태가 위태롭다는 소식을 전한 것이었고, 그 말에 그분은 곧장 울음을 터뜨리더니, 먹다 만 새우의 머리와 꼬리로 조끼를 잔뜩 채운 채 나를 따라 나왔다."]], [["‘Emma, my angel!’ cried Mr.", "\"엠마, 내 천사!\""], ["Micawber, running into the room; ‘what is the matter?’", "미코버 씨가 방으로 뛰어들며 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이오?\""]], [["‘I never will desert you, Micawber!’ she exclaimed.", "\"저는 결코 당신을 버리지 않을 거예요, 미코버!\" 부인이 부르짖었다."]], [["‘My life!’ said Mr. Micawber, taking her in his arms.", "\"내 생명!\" 미코버 씨가 부인을 품에 안으며 말했다."], ["‘I am perfectly aware of it.’", "\"나도 잘 알고 있소.\""]], [["‘He is the parent of my children!", "\"이이는 내 아이들의 부모예요!"], ["He is the father of my twins!", "내 쌍둥이의 아빠라고요!"], ["He is the husband of my affections,’ cried Mrs.", "내가 사랑하는 남편이에요.\" 미코버 부인이 발버둥 치며 외쳤다."], ["Micawber, struggling; ‘and I ne--ver--will--desert Mr. Micawber!’", "\"그러니 저는 결—코—미코버 씨를—버리지—않을 거예요!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber was so deeply affected by this proof of her devotion (as to me, I was dissolved in tears), that he hung over her in a passionate manner, imploring her to look up, and to be calm.", "미코버 씨는 부인이 보여 준 이 헌신의 증거에 어찌나 깊이 감동했던지 (나로 말하자면 눈물에 흠뻑 녹아 버렸다), 격정에 사로잡혀 부인 위로 몸을 굽힌 채, 고개를 들어 마음을 가라앉히라고 애원했다."], ["But the more he asked Mrs. Micawber to look up, the more she fixed her eyes on nothing; and the more he asked her to compose herself, the more she wouldn’t.", "그러나 미코버 씨가 고개를 들라고 청할수록 부인은 더더욱 허공에다 눈길을 박았고, 마음을 가다듬으라고 청할수록 더더욱 가다듬으려 들지 않았다."], ["Consequently Mr. Micawber was soon so overcome, that he mingled his tears with hers and mine; until he begged me to do him the favour of taking a chair on the staircase, while he got her into bed.", "그 결과 미코버 씨는 이내 너무도 북받쳐서, 자기 눈물을 부인과 나의 눈물에 한데 섞어 버렸다. 그러다 끝내, 자기가 부인을 침대에 눕히는 동안 계단에 의자를 놓고 좀 앉아 있어 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라고 나에게 부탁했다."], ["I would have taken my leave for the night, but he would not hear of my doing that until the strangers’ bell should ring.", "나는 그만 밤 인사를 하고 떠나려 했으나, 그분은 면회객을 알리는 종이 울리기 전에는 내가 떠나는 것을 한사코 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["So I sat at the staircase window, until he came out with another chair and joined me.", "그래서 나는 계단 창가에 앉아 있었는데, 마침내 그분이 의자를 하나 더 들고 나와 내 곁에 앉았다."]], [["‘How is Mrs. Micawber now, sir?’ I said.", "\"미코버 부인은 이제 좀 어떠세요, 아저씨?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Very low,’ said Mr.", "\"몹시 가라앉아 있어.\""], ["Micawber, shaking his head; ‘reaction.", "미코버 씨가 고개를 흔들며 말했다."], ["Ah, this has been a dreadful day!", "\"반동이지. 아, 오늘은 끔찍한 하루였어!"], ["We stand alone now--everything is gone from us!’", "우린 이제 외톨이야 — 모든 게 우리한테서 사라져 버렸어!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber pressed my hand, and groaned, and afterwards shed tears.", "미코버 씨는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는 신음하더니, 이윽고 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["I was greatly touched, and disappointed too, for I had expected that we should be quite gay on this happy and long-looked-for occasion. But Mr.", "나는 크게 마음이 움직였고, 한편으로는 실망도 했다. 이렇게 기쁘고 오래도록 기다려 온 날이니만큼 우리가 무척 흥겨우리라 기대했기 때문이다."], ["and Mrs. Micawber were so used to their old difficulties, I think, that they felt quite shipwrecked when they came to consider that they were released from them.", "그러나 미코버 부부는, 내 생각에, 묵은 곤경에 어찌나 익숙해져 있었던지, 막상 그 곤경에서 풀려났다는 것을 곱씹어 보니 도리어 완전히 난파당한 기분이 들었던 모양이다."], ["All their elasticity was departed, and I never saw them half so wretched as on this night; insomuch that when the bell rang, and Mr.", "두 사람의 그 탄력은 죄다 사라져 버렸고, 나는 이날 밤만큼 그들이 비참해하는 모습을 그 절반이라도 본 적이 없었다."], ["Micawber walked with me to the lodge, and parted from me there with a blessing, I felt quite afraid to leave him by himself, he was so profoundly miserable.", "그래서 종이 울리고 미코버 씨가 나와 함께 정문까지 걸어 나와 거기서 축복을 건네며 작별할 때, 나는 그분이 너무도 깊이 처량해 보여, 그분을 홀로 두고 떠나기가 차마 두려웠다."]], [["But through all the confusion and lowness of spirits in which we had been, so unexpectedly to me, involved, I plainly discerned that Mr. and Mrs. Micawber and their family were going away from London, and that a parting between us was near at hand.", "그러나 나로서는 너무도 뜻밖에 휩쓸려 든 그 모든 혼란과 가라앉은 마음을 헤치고서도, 나는 미코버 부부와 그 식구들이 런던을 떠나려 한다는 것과, 우리 사이의 이별이 코앞에 닥쳤다는 것을 또렷이 알아차렸다."], ["It was in my walk home that night, and in the sleepless hours which followed when I lay in bed, that the thought first occurred to me--though I don’t know how it came into my head--which afterwards shaped itself into a settled resolution.", "그날 밤 집으로 걸어 돌아오는 길에, 그리고 그 뒤 침대에 누워 잠 못 이루던 시각에, 나에게 처음으로 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐는데 — 그게 어떻게 머릿속에 들어왔는지는 모르겠으나 — 나중에 그 생각은 굳은 결심으로 모양을 갖추었다."]], [["I had grown to be so accustomed to the Micawbers, and had been so intimate with them in their distresses, and was so utterly friendless without them, that the prospect of being thrown upon some new shift for a lodging, and going once more among unknown people, was like being that moment turned adrift into my present life, with such a knowledge of it ready made as experience had given me.", "나는 미코버 부부에게 너무도 익숙해진 데다, 그들의 곤경 속에서 무척이나 가깝게 지냈고, 그들이 없으면 완전히 의지가지없는 처지였기에, 새로 거처를 마련하느라 또 한 번 궁리를 짜내고 다시금 낯선 사람들 틈으로 들어가야 한다는 전망은, 마치 그 순간 다시 지금의 이 삶 속으로 정처 없이 내던져지는 것만 같았다 — 그것도 경험이 안겨 준, 이미 알 만큼 다 아는 그 삶에 대한 지식을 고스란히 챙겨 든 채로 말이다."], ["All the sensitive feelings it wounded so cruelly, all the shame and misery it kept alive within my breast, became more poignant as I thought of this; and I determined that the life was unendurable.", "그 삶이 그토록 모질게 할퀴어 놓은 온갖 예민한 감정, 그 삶이 내 가슴속에 살려 둔 온갖 수치와 비참함은, 이 생각을 곱씹을수록 더 사무치게 다가왔다. 그리하여 나는 이런 삶은 도저히 견딜 수 없다고 단단히 마음먹었다."]], [["That there was no hope of escape from it, unless the escape was my own act, I knew quite well. I rarely heard from Miss Murdstone, and never from Mr.", "이 삶에서 벗어날 길은, 그 탈출이 내 손으로 직접 이루어지지 않는 한 아무런 희망이 없다는 것을, 나는 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 머드스톤 양에게서는 좀처럼 소식이 오지 않았고, 머드스톤 씨한테서는 단 한 번도 오지 않았다."], ["Murdstone: but two or three parcels of made or mended clothes had come up for me, consigned to Mr. Quinion, and in each there was a scrap of paper to the effect that J. M.", "그러나 새로 짓거나 손본 옷가지 꾸러미가 두세 차례 키니언 씨 앞으로 부쳐져 나에게 왔는데, 그 꾸러미마다 쪽지가 한 장씩 들어 있었으니, 그 내용인즉슨 J."], ["trusted D. C.", "M.은 D."], ["was applying himself to business, and devoting himself wholly to his duties--not the least hint of my ever being anything else than the common drudge into which I was fast settling down.", "C.가 일에 매진하고 본분에 전적으로 헌신하기를 바란다는 것이었다 — 내가 빠르게 굳어져 가는 그 흔해 빠진 막일꾼 말고 무언가 다른 사람이 될 수 있으리라는 기미는 손톱만큼도 없었다."]], [["The very next day showed me, while my mind was in the first agitation of what it had conceived, that Mrs. Micawber had not spoken of their going away without warrant.", "바로 그 이튿날, 내 마음이 막 품은 생각으로 처음 들끓고 있던 참에, 미코버 부인이 떠난다고 한 말이 빈말이 아니었음이 드러났다."], ["They took a lodging in the house where I lived, for a week; at the expiration of which time they were to start for Plymouth. Mr.", "그들은 내가 사는 집에 일주일짜리 셋방을 얻었고, 그 기한이 다하면 플리머스로 출발할 예정이었다."], ["Micawber himself came down to the counting-house, in the afternoon, to tell Mr. Quinion that he must relinquish me on the day of his departure, and to give me a high character, which I am sure I deserved.", "미코버 씨는 그날 오후 직접 회계사무소로 내려와, 자기가 떠나는 날 나를 놓아주어야 한다고 키니언 씨에게 일렀고, 나에 대해 후한 평을 해 주었는데, 나는 분명 그럴 자격이 있다고 믿는다."], ["And Mr. Quinion, calling in Tipp the carman, who was a married man, and had a room to let, quartered me prospectively on him--by our mutual consent, as he had every reason to think; for I said nothing, though my resolution was now taken.", "그러자 키니언 씨는, 결혼한 몸으로 세놓을 방이 있던 마부 팁을 불러들여, 나를 앞으로 그 사람한테 묵게끔 정해 두었다 — 그로서는 우리 양쪽이 합의한 것으로 여길 만한 온갖 이유가 있었으니까. 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 내 결심은 이미 굳어진 뒤였다."]], [["I passed my evenings with Mr. and Mrs. Micawber, during the remaining term of our residence under the same roof; and I think we became fonder of one another as the time went on.", "한 지붕 아래 머무는 남은 기간 동안, 나는 미코버 부부와 더불어 저녁 시간을 보냈고, 시간이 흐를수록 우리는 서로를 더욱 아끼게 된 것 같다."], ["On the last Sunday, they invited me to dinner; and we had a loin of pork and apple sauce, and a pudding.", "마지막 일요일, 두 분은 나를 저녁 식사에 초대했고, 우리는 돼지 등심에 사과 소스를 곁들이고 푸딩까지 먹었다."], ["I had bought a spotted wooden horse over-night as a parting gift to little Wilkins Micawber--that was the boy--and a doll for little Emma.", "나는 전날 밤 어린 윌킨스 미코버 — 그게 사내아이였다 — 에게 줄 작별 선물로 얼룩무늬 나무 말을 사 두었고, 어린 엠마에게 줄 인형도 사 두었다."], ["I had also bestowed a shilling on the Orfling, who was about to be disbanded.", "그리고 곧 해고될 처지였던 그 고아 하녀에게도 일 실링을 쥐여 주었다."]], [["We had a very pleasant day, though we were all in a tender state about our approaching separation.", "우리는 다가오는 헤어짐 때문에 다들 마음이 애틋한 가운데에서도, 무척 즐거운 하루를 보냈다."]], [["‘I shall never, Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"저는 결코, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘revert to the period when Mr. Micawber was in difficulties, without thinking of you.", "\"미코버 씨가 곤경에 처했던 그 시절을 떠올릴 때마다, 도련님 생각을 안 할 수가 없을 거예요."], ["Your conduct has always been of the most delicate and obliging description. You have never been a lodger.", "도련님의 처신은 늘 더없이 섬세하고 자상했어요. 도련님은 한 번도 그저 세 든 사람이었던 적이 없어요."], ["You have been a friend.’", "도련님은 우리 친구였어요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber; ‘Copperfield,’ for so he had been accustomed to call me, of late, ‘has a heart to feel for the distresses of his fellow-creatures when they are behind a cloud, and a head to plan, and a hand to--in short, a general ability to dispose of such available property as could be made away with.’", "\"코퍼필드는,\" 요즘 들어 그분은 나를 그렇게 부르는 데 익숙해져 있었다. \"동료 인간들이 먹구름 뒤에 가려 곤경에 빠졌을 때 그 처지를 함께 아파할 줄 아는 마음을 지녔고, 궁리할 줄 아는 머리를 지녔고, 또 — 한마디로, 처분할 수 있는 재산이라면 무엇이든 잘 처리할 줄 아는 두루 갖춘 능력을 지녔지.\""]], [["I expressed my sense of this commendation, and said I was very sorry we were going to lose one another.", "나는 이 칭찬에 대한 고마움을 표하며, 우리가 서로 헤어지게 되어 무척 안타깝다고 말했다."]], [["‘My dear young friend,’ said Mr.", "\"내 사랑하는 젊은 친구,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘I am older than you; a man of some experience in life, and--and of some experience, in short, in difficulties, generally speaking.", "\"나는 자네보다 나이가 위고, 세상살이에 어느 정도 경험이 있는 사람이고, 또 — 또 한마디로, 곤경에 관해서라면 대체로 보아 어느 정도 경험이 있는 사람이지."], ["At present, and until something turns up (which I am, I may say, hourly expecting), I have nothing to bestow but advice.", "지금으로선, 무슨 수가 생길 때까지는 (솔직히 나는 시시각각 그걸 고대하고 있다네) 자네한테 베풀 거라곤 충고밖에 없네."], ["Still my advice is so far worth taking, that--in short, that I have never taken it myself, and am the’--here Mr.", "그래도 내 충고는 새겨들을 만한 값어치가 있는데, 그건 — 한마디로, 내가 정작 그걸 한 번도 스스로 따른 적이 없어서, 그래서 이렇게 — \""], ["Micawber, who had been beaming and smiling, all over his head and face, up to the present moment, checked himself and frowned--‘the miserable wretch you behold.’", "여기서 미코버 씨는, 그 순간까지 머리며 얼굴 전체가 환히 빛나며 미소 짓고 있다가, 문득 멈칫하더니 인상을 찌푸렸다. \"— 자네가 지금 보고 있는 이 비참한 가련뱅이가 된 거라네.\""]], [["‘My dear Micawber!’ urged his wife.", "\"여보, 미코버!\" 부인이 다그쳤다."]], [["‘I say,’ returned Mr.", "\"하긴,\""], ["Micawber, quite forgetting himself, and smiling again, ‘the miserable wretch you behold.", "미코버 씨가 그새 또 자기를 잊고 다시 미소 지으며 받았다. \"자네가 지금 보고 있는 비참한 가련뱅이지."], ["My advice is, never do tomorrow what you can do today.", "내 충고는 이거야. 오늘 할 수 있는 일을 결코 내일로 미루지 말게."], ["Procrastination is the thief of time. Collar him!’", "미루는 버릇은 시간을 훔쳐 가는 도둑이야. 그놈의 멱살을 잡게!\""]], [["‘My poor papa’s maxim,’ Mrs.", "\"우리 가엾은 아빠의 좌우명이지요.\""], ["Micawber observed.", "미코버 부인이 한마디 덧붙였다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘your papa was very well in his way, and Heaven forbid that I should disparage him.", "\"당신 아버님은 그 나름으로 참 훌륭한 분이셨고, 내가 그분을 깎아내린다는 건 천부당만부당한 일이오."], ["Take him for all in all, we ne’er shall--in short, make the acquaintance, probably, of anybody else possessing, at his time of life, the same legs for gaiters, and able to read the same description of print, without spectacles.", "모든 점을 다 따져 보아도, 우리는 두 번 다시 — 한마디로, 그분 연세에 그만한 다리로 각반을 차고, 안경도 없이 그만한 활자를 읽어 낼 만한 사람을 아마 두 번 다시 만나지 못할 거요."], ["But he applied that maxim to our marriage, my dear; and that was so far prematurely entered into, in consequence, that I never recovered the expense.’ Mr.", "하지만 그분은 그 좌우명을 우리 결혼에 적용하셨고, 그 바람에 우리 결혼이 너무 일찍 치러진 셈이라, 그 비용을 나는 끝내 만회하지 못했지.\""], ["Micawber looked aside at Mrs. Micawber, and added: ‘Not that I am sorry for it.", "미코버 씨는 곁눈으로 미코버 부인을 흘끗 보고는 덧붙였다. \"그렇다고 그걸 후회한다는 건 아니오."], ["Quite the contrary, my love.’ After which, he was grave for a minute or so.", "오히려 정반대요, 내 사랑.\" 그러고는 일 분쯤 자못 엄숙해졌다."]], [["‘My other piece of advice, Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 자네한테 줄 또 한 가지 충고는, 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, ‘you know.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Annual income twenty pounds, annual expenditure nineteen nineteen and six, result happiness.", "\"자네도 알고 있는 거지. 연 수입 이십 파운드에 연 지출 십구 파운드 십구 실링 육 펜스면, 결과는 행복이야."], ["Annual income twenty pounds, annual expenditure twenty pounds ought and six, result misery.", "연 수입 이십 파운드에 연 지출 이십 파운드와 육 펜스면, 결과는 비참함이지."], ["The blossom is blighted, the leaf is withered, the god of day goes down upon the dreary scene, and--and in short you are for ever floored. As I am!’", "꽃봉오리는 시들고, 잎은 마르고, 한낮의 신은 그 황량한 풍경 위로 저물어 가고, 또 — 한마디로 자네는 영영 나가떨어지는 거야. 지금 나처럼 말일세!\""]], [["To make his example the more impressive, Mr. Micawber drank a glass of punch with an air of great enjoyment and satisfaction, and whistled the College Hornpipe.", "자기 본보기를 한층 더 절실하게 새겨 주려고, 미코버 씨는 더없이 흡족하고 만족스러운 표정으로 펀치 한 잔을 들이켜고는, 칼리지 혼파이프 곡을 휘파람으로 불었다."]], [["I did not fail to assure him that I would store these precepts in my mind, though indeed I had no need to do so, for, at the time, they affected me visibly.", "나는 이 가르침을 마음속에 잘 간직하겠노라고 그분에게 빠짐없이 다짐했는데, 실은 그럴 필요도 없었던 것이, 그때 그 말은 눈에 띌 만큼 나를 뭉클하게 했기 때문이다."], ["Next morning I met the whole family at the coach office, and saw them, with a desolate heart, take their places outside, at the back.", "이튿날 아침 나는 역마차 사무소에서 온 식구를 만나, 가슴이 휑한 채로, 그들이 마차 바깥 뒷자리에 오르는 모습을 지켜보았다."]], [["‘Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"코퍼필드 도련님,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘God bless you!", "\"하느님의 가호가 있기를!"], ["I never can forget all that, you know, and I never would if I could.’", "저는 결코 그 모든 일을 잊을 수 없을 거예요, 알지요. 잊을 수 있다 해도 결코 잊지 않을 거고요.\""]], [["‘Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, ‘farewell!", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Every happiness and prosperity!", "\"잘 가게! 온갖 행복과 번영이 함께하기를!"], ["If, in the progress of revolving years, I could persuade myself that my blighted destiny had been a warning to you, I should feel that I had not occupied another man’s place in existence altogether in vain.", "만일 세월이 굽이굽이 흐르는 가운데, 망가져 버린 내 운명이 자네한테 하나의 경고가 되었노라고 스스로 믿을 수 있게 된다면, 나는 이 세상에서 남의 자리를 차지하고 산 것이 아주 헛되지만은 않았다고 느끼게 될 걸세."], ["In case of anything turning up (of which I am rather confident), I shall be extremely happy if it should be in my power to improve your prospects.’", "무슨 수가 생기는 경우에는 (나는 꽤 그리되리라 믿네만), 내 힘으로 자네의 앞날을 밝혀 줄 수 있다면 더없이 기쁠 거야.\""]], [["I think, as Mrs. Micawber sat at the back of the coach, with the children, and I stood in the road looking wistfully at them, a mist cleared from her eyes, and she saw what a little creature I really was.", "미코버 부인이 아이들과 함께 마차 뒷자리에 앉아 있고, 내가 길에 서서 그들을 애틋하게 바라보고 있는 동안, 부인의 눈에서 안개 같은 것이 걷히면서, 부인은 내가 정말로 얼마나 어린것인지를 깨달았던 것 같다."], ["I think so, because she beckoned to me to climb up, with quite a new and motherly expression in her face, and put her arm round my neck, and gave me just such a kiss as she might have given to her own boy.", "내가 그렇게 생각하는 까닭은, 부인이 더없이 새롭고 어머니다운 표정을 얼굴에 띤 채 나에게 올라오라고 손짓하더니, 내 목을 한 팔로 감싸 안고, 마치 제 친아들에게나 해 줄 법한 입맞춤을 나에게 해 주었기 때문이다."], ["I had barely time to get down again before the coach started, and I could hardly see the family for the handkerchiefs they waved.", "마차가 출발하기 전에 나는 가까스로 다시 내려올 틈밖에 없었고, 그들이 흔들어 대는 손수건 때문에 그 식구들이 거의 보이지도 않았다."], ["It was gone in a minute.", "마차는 순식간에 사라졌다."], ["The Orfling and I stood looking vacantly at each other in the middle of the road, and then shook hands and said good-bye; she going back, I suppose, to St. Luke’s workhouse, as I went to begin my weary day at Murdstone and Grinby’s.", "그 고아 하녀와 나는 길 한복판에 서서 멍하니 서로를 바라보다가, 이윽고 악수를 하고 작별 인사를 나누었다. 그 애는, 내 짐작에, 세인트 루크 구빈원으로 돌아갔고, 나는 머드스톤과 그린비 상회에서 또 하루의 고단한 일과를 시작하러 갔다."]], [["But with no intention of passing many more weary days there. No.", "그러나 거기서 고단한 날을 더 많이 보낼 생각은 조금도 없었다. 천만에."], ["I had resolved to run away.---To go, by some means or other, down into the country, to the only relation I had in the world, and tell my story to my aunt, Miss Betsey.", "나는 달아나기로 결심한 터였다. — 어떻게 해서든 시골로 내려가, 이 세상에 단 하나뿐인 친척인 고모, 베시 양을 찾아가, 내 사연을 털어놓기로 말이다."], ["I have already observed that I don’t know how this desperate idea came into my brain.", "이 절박한 생각이 어떻게 내 머릿속에 들어왔는지 모르겠다는 말은 앞서도 했다."], ["But, once there, it remained there; and hardened into a purpose than which I have never entertained a more determined purpose in my life.", "그러나 일단 거기 자리 잡고 나니, 그것은 그대로 머물러 있었고, 내 평생 단 한 번도 품어 본 적 없을 만큼 굳센 결심으로 단단히 굳어졌다."], ["I am far from sure that I believed there was anything hopeful in it, but my mind was thoroughly made up that it must be carried into execution.", "그 안에 무슨 희망이 있다고 믿었던 것 같지는 도무지 않으나, 어쨌든 그것은 반드시 실행에 옮겨야 한다고 내 마음은 완전히 정해져 있었다."]], [["Again, and again, and a hundred times again, since the night when the thought had first occurred to me and banished sleep, I had gone over that old story of my poor mother’s about my birth, which it had been one of my great delights in the old time to hear her tell, and which I knew by heart.", "그 생각이 처음 떠올라 내 잠을 쫓아내 버린 그날 밤 이후로, 나는 거듭, 거듭, 그리고 백 번도 더, 내 출생에 얽힌 가엾은 어머니의 그 옛날이야기를 되짚어 보았다. 그 이야기는 옛 시절 어머니가 들려주는 것을 듣는 것이 내 큰 즐거움 가운데 하나였고, 나는 그것을 줄줄 외고 있었다."], ["My aunt walked into that story, and walked out of it, a dread and awful personage; but there was one little trait in her behaviour which I liked to dwell on, and which gave me some faint shadow of encouragement.", "고모는 그 이야기 속으로 걸어 들어왔다가 다시 걸어 나갔는데, 무섭고도 위압적인 인물이었다. 그러나 고모의 처신 가운데 내가 곱씹기 좋아하는, 그래서 나에게 어렴풋한 격려의 그림자를 안겨 주는 자그마한 면모가 하나 있었다."], ["I could not forget how my mother had thought that she felt her touch her pretty hair with no ungentle hand; and though it might have been altogether my mother’s fancy, and might have had no foundation whatever in fact, I made a little picture, out of it, of my terrible aunt relenting towards the girlish beauty that I recollected so well and loved so much, which softened the whole narrative.", "어머니가, 고모가 자기의 고운 머리카락을 결코 무뚝뚝하지 않은 손길로 만지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다고 했던 것을 나는 잊을 수 없었다. 그것이 온전히 어머니의 착각이었을 수도 있고, 사실에 아무런 근거도 없는 것이었을 수도 있다. 그래도 나는 그것을 바탕으로, 내가 그토록 또렷이 기억하고 그토록 사랑했던 그 앳된 아름다움을 향해 마음이 누그러지는 무서운 고모의 모습을 자그맣게 하나 그려 보았는데, 그 그림이 그 이야기 전체를 부드럽게 만들어 주었다."], ["It is very possible that it had been in my mind a long time, and had gradually engendered my determination.", "그것이 오랫동안 내 마음속에 자리하여, 차츰차츰 내 결심을 키워 냈으리라는 것도 충분히 있을 법한 일이다."]], [["As I did not even know where Miss Betsey lived, I wrote a long letter to Peggotty, and asked her, incidentally, if she remembered; pretending that I had heard of such a lady living at a certain place I named at random, and had a curiosity to know if it were the same.", "나는 베시 양이 어디에 사는지조차 몰랐으므로, 패거티에게 긴 편지를 써서, 지나가는 말인 양 그 주소를 기억하느냐고 물었다. 어느 부인이 — 그러면서 내가 아무렇게나 지어낸 어떤 곳을 댔다 — 거기 산다는 얘기를 들었는데, 그게 같은 분인지 궁금해서 그런다는 시늉을 한 것이다."], ["In the course of that letter, I told Peggotty that I had a particular occasion for half a guinea; and that if she could lend me that sum until I could repay it, I should be very much obliged to her, and would tell her afterwards what I had wanted it for.", "그 편지에서 나는 패거티에게, 반 기니가 꼭 필요한 특별한 사정이 생겼다고, 갚을 수 있을 때까지 그 돈을 빌려준다면 무척 고맙겠으며, 그게 무엇에 쓸 돈인지는 나중에 알려 주겠노라고 했다."]], [["Peggotty’s answer soon arrived, and was, as usual, full of affectionate devotion.", "패거티의 답장은 곧 도착했고, 여느 때처럼 애정 어린 정성으로 가득했다."], ["She enclosed the half guinea (I was afraid she must have had a world of trouble to get it out of Mr. Barkis’s box), and told me that Miss Betsey lived near Dover, but whether at Dover itself, at Hythe, Sandgate, or Folkestone, she could not say.", "그녀는 반 기니를 동봉했고 (바키스 씨의 돈궤에서 그걸 꺼내느라 그녀가 얼마나 애를 먹었을지 나는 마음이 쓰였다), 베시 양이 도버 근처에 산다고, 다만 도버 시내인지, 하이드인지, 샌드게이트인지, 폴크스턴인지는 알 수 없다고 알려 주었다."], ["One of our men, however, informing me on my asking him about these places, that they were all close together, I deemed this enough for my object, and resolved to set out at the end of that week.", "그런데 우리 일꾼 하나가, 내가 그 지명들을 물어보자 거기가 죄다 서로 가까이 붙어 있다고 일러 주었으므로, 나는 내 목적에는 이만하면 충분하다 여기고, 그 주가 끝날 무렵에 떠나기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Being a very honest little creature, and unwilling to disgrace the memory I was going to leave behind me at Murdstone and Grinby’s, I considered myself bound to remain until Saturday night; and, as I had been paid a week’s wages in advance when I first came there, not to present myself in the counting-house at the usual hour, to receive my stipend.", "나는 무척 정직한 어린것인 데다, 머드스톤과 그린비 상회에 남기고 떠날 내 평판을 더럽히고 싶지 않았으므로, 토요일 밤까지는 남아 있는 것이 도리라고 여겼다. 그리고 처음 그곳에 왔을 때 일주일 치 품삯을 미리 받았던 터라, 평소 시각에 회계사무소에 들어가 봉급을 받지는 않기로 했다."], ["For this express reason, I had borrowed the half-guinea, that I might not be without a fund for my travelling-expenses.", "바로 그 까닭에 나는 반 기니를 빌렸던 것이니, 여비로 쓸 밑천이 한 푼도 없는 신세가 되지 않으려는 것이었다."], ["Accordingly, when the Saturday night came, and we were all waiting in the warehouse to be paid, and Tipp the carman, who always took precedence, went in first to draw his money, I shook Mick Walker by the hand; asked him, when it came to his turn to be paid, to say to Mr.", "그리하여 토요일 밤이 와서, 우리 모두 창고에서 품삯을 받으려고 기다리고 있을 때, 늘 맨 앞 차례인 마부 팁이 먼저 들어가 자기 돈을 받자, 나는 믹 워커와 악수를 나누었다. 그리고 그의 차례가 되어 돈을 받게 되거든, 내가 짐 상자를 팁의 집으로 옮기러 갔다고 키니언 씨에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Quinion that I had gone to move my box to Tipp’s; and, bidding a last good night to Mealy Potatoes, ran away.", "그러고는 가루 감자에게 마지막 밤 인사를 건네고, 달아났다."]], [["My box was at my old lodging, over the water, and I had written a direction for it on the back of one of our address cards that we nailed on the casks: ‘Master David, to be left till called for, at the Coach Office, Dover.’ This I had in my pocket ready to put on the box, after I should have got it out of the house; and as I went towards my lodging, I looked about me for someone who would help me to carry it to the booking-office.", "내 짐 상자는 강 건너 옛 하숙집에 있었는데, 나는 우리가 술통에 못으로 박아 두던 주소 카드 한 장의 뒷면에다 그 짐의 행선지를 적어 두었다. \"데이비드 도련님, 찾으러 올 때까지 보관 요망, 도버 역마차 사무소.\" 나는 짐을 집에서 꺼낸 뒤에 거기다 붙이려고 그것을 호주머니에 챙겨 두었고, 하숙집을 향해 가면서, 그것을 운임 접수소까지 날라 줄 누군가를 두리번거리며 찾았다."]], [["There was a long-legged young man with a very little empty donkey-cart, standing near the Obelisk, in the Blackfriars Road, whose eye I caught as I was going by, and who, addressing me as ‘Sixpenn’orth of bad ha’pence,’ hoped ‘I should know him agin to swear to’--in allusion, I have no doubt, to my staring at him.", "블랙프라이어스 로드의 오벨리스크 근처에, 텅 빈 자그마한 당나귀 수레를 세워 둔 다리 긴 젊은이가 하나 있었는데, 내가 지나가는 길에 그와 눈이 마주쳤다. 그는 나를 두고 \"엿 같은 반 페니짜리 육 펜스어치\"라고 부르더니, \"내 얼굴을 잘 봐 두었다가 나중에 증언이라도 할 셈이냐\"고 빈정거렸다 — 틀림없이 내가 자기를 빤히 쳐다본 것을 두고 한 말이었다."], ["I stopped to assure him that I had not done so in bad manners, but uncertain whether he might or might not like a job.", "나는 걸음을 멈추고, 무례하게 굴려던 게 아니라고 그에게 분명히 말해 두었는데, 다만 그가 일감을 좋아할지 어떨지 몰라서였다."]], [["‘Wot job?’ said the long-legged young man.", "\"무슨 일감?\" 다리 긴 젊은이가 말했다."]], [["‘To move a box,’ I answered.", "\"짐 상자 하나 옮기는 일이요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Wot box?’ said the long-legged young man.", "\"무슨 상자?\" 다리 긴 젊은이가 말했다."]], [["I told him mine, which was down that street there, and which I wanted him to take to the Dover coach office for sixpence.", "나는 그게 내 거라고, 저쪽 저 거리에 있는데, 그걸 도버 역마차 사무소까지 육 펜스에 옮겨 주었으면 한다고 말했다."]], [["‘Done with you for a tanner!’ said the long-legged young man, and directly got upon his cart, which was nothing but a large wooden tray on wheels, and rattled away at such a rate, that it was as much as I could do to keep pace with the donkey.", "\"육 펜스에 합시다!\" 다리 긴 젊은이가 말하더니, 곧장 자기 수레에 올라탔는데, 그건 바퀴 달린 커다란 나무 쟁반에 지나지 않았다. 그러고는 어찌나 빠르게 덜컹덜컹 달려 나가던지, 나는 당나귀를 따라잡느라 할 수 있는 데까지 안간힘을 다 써야 했다."]], [["There was a defiant manner about this young man, and particularly about the way in which he chewed straw as he spoke to me, that I did not much like; as the bargain was made, however, I took him upstairs to the room I was leaving, and we brought the box down, and put it on his cart.", "이 젊은이에게는 어딘가 시비조의 태도가 있었고, 특히 나에게 말을 하면서 짚을 질겅질겅 씹는 그 꼴이 나는 그리 마음에 들지 않았다. 그래도 흥정은 이미 끝났으므로, 나는 그를 내가 떠나려는 그 방까지 데리고 올라가, 둘이서 짐 상자를 들고 내려와 그의 수레에 실었다."], ["Now, I was unwilling to put the direction-card on there, lest any of my landlord’s family should fathom what I was doing, and detain me; so I said to the young man that I would be glad if he would stop for a minute, when he came to the dead-wall of the King’s Bench prison.", "그런데 나는 행선지 카드를 거기다 붙이기가 꺼려졌으니, 하숙집 주인 식구 누군가가 내가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알아채고 나를 붙잡을까 봐서였다. 그래서 나는 그 젊은이에게, 킹스 벤치 감옥의 막다른 담장에 이르거든 잠깐만 멈춰 주면 고맙겠다고 말했다."], ["The words were no sooner out of my mouth, than he rattled away as if he, my box, the cart, and the donkey, were all equally mad; and I was quite out of breath with running and calling after him, when I caught him at the place appointed.", "그런데 그 말이 입에서 떨어지기가 무섭게, 그는 마치 자기도, 내 짐도, 수레도, 당나귀도 죄다 똑같이 미쳐 버리기라도 한 듯이 덜컹덜컹 내달았다. 그래서 나는 약속한 그 자리에서 그를 따라잡았을 무렵에는, 뒤를 쫓으며 불러 대느라 완전히 숨이 턱에 차 있었다."]], [["Being much flushed and excited, I tumbled my half-guinea out of my pocket in pulling the card out.", "너무나 얼굴이 달아오르고 흥분한 나머지, 나는 카드를 꺼내려다 호주머니에서 반 기니를 굴러떨어뜨리고 말았다."], ["I put it in my mouth for safety, and though my hands trembled a good deal, had just tied the card on very much to my satisfaction, when I felt myself violently chucked under the chin by the long-legged young man, and saw my half-guinea fly out of my mouth into his hand.", "나는 안전하게 두려고 그것을 입에 물었는데, 손이 자못 떨리기는 했어도 카드를 아주 흡족하게 막 동여맨 참에, 별안간 그 다리 긴 젊은이에게 턱밑을 사납게 후려쳐 올림을 당했고, 내 반 기니가 입에서 튕겨 나가 그의 손바닥으로 날아드는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Wot!’ said the young man, seizing me by my jacket collar, with a frightful grin.", "\"뭐야!\" 젊은이가 소름 끼치는 히죽한 웃음을 띠며 내 윗도리 깃을 움켜쥐고 말했다."], ["‘This is a pollis case, is it?", "\"이거 순경한테 끌고 갈 일이로구먼, 응?"], ["You’re a-going to bolt, are you?", "너 지금 튀려는 거지, 그렇지?"], ["Come to the pollis, you young warmin, come to the pollis!’", "순경한테 가자, 이 어린 도둑놈아, 순경한테 가!\""]], [["‘You give me my money back, if you please,’ said I, very much frightened; ‘and leave me alone.’", "\"제발 제 돈이나 돌려주세요.\" 내가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 말했다. \"그리고 저 좀 내버려 두세요.\""]], [["‘Come to the pollis!’ said the young man.", "\"순경한테 가!\" 젊은이가 말했다."], ["‘You shall prove it yourn to the pollis.’", "\"그게 네 거라는 걸 순경한테 증명해 봐.\""]], [["‘Give me my box and money, will you,’ I cried, bursting into tears.", "\"제 상자랑 돈을 돌려주세요, 네?\" 나는 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."]], [["The young man still replied: ‘Come to the pollis!’ and was dragging me against the donkey in a violent manner, as if there were any affinity between that animal and a magistrate, when he changed his mind, jumped into the cart, sat upon my box, and, exclaiming that he would drive to the pollis straight, rattled away harder than ever.", "젊은이는 여전히 \"순경한테 가!\"라고만 받았고, 마치 그 짐승과 치안판사 사이에 무슨 연이라도 있다는 듯이 나를 당나귀 쪽으로 사납게 끌고 갔다. 그러더니 마음을 바꿔 수레에 훌쩍 올라타, 내 짐 상자 위에 걸터앉고는, 곧장 순경한테 몰고 가겠다고 외치며 어느 때보다도 더 거세게 덜컹덜컹 달아났다."]], [["I ran after him as fast as I could, but I had no breath to call out with, and should not have dared to call out, now, if I had.", "나는 있는 힘껏 그를 뒤쫓았으나, 소리쳐 부를 숨조차 없었고, 설령 숨이 있었더라도 이제는 감히 소리쳐 부르지도 못했을 것이다."], ["I narrowly escaped being run over, twenty times at least, in half a mile.", "반 마일을 가는 동안 적어도 스무 번은 수레에 깔릴 뻔한 것을 아슬아슬하게 모면했다."], ["Now I lost him, now I saw him, now I lost him, now I was cut at with a whip, now shouted at, now down in the mud, now up again, now running into somebody’s arms, now running headlong at a post.", "그를 놓쳤다가, 그가 보였다가, 다시 놓쳤다가, 채찍에 후려쳐지다가, 고함을 들었다가, 진창에 처박혔다가, 다시 일어섰다가, 누군가의 품으로 뛰어들었다가, 기둥을 향해 곤두박질치듯 달려들었다가 했다."], ["At length, confused by fright and heat, and doubting whether half London might not by this time be turning out for my apprehension, I left the young man to go where he would with my box and money; and, panting and crying, but never stopping, faced about for Greenwich, which I had understood was on the Dover Road: taking very little more out of the world, towards the retreat of my aunt, Miss Betsey, than I had brought into it, on the night when my arrival gave her so much umbrage.", "마침내 두려움과 열기로 정신이 아뜩해지고, 어쩌면 이쯤 되면 런던의 절반이 나를 붙잡으러 몰려나온 게 아닐까 의심스러워진 나는, 그 젊은이가 내 짐과 돈을 가지고 어디로든 가 버리도록 내버려 두었다. 그리고 헐떡이며 울면서도 결코 멈추지 않은 채, 도버 가도에 있다고 들은 그리니치를 향해 방향을 틀었다. 고모인 베시 양의 은신처를 향해 떠나는 마당에, 내가 세상에서 챙겨 가는 것이라곤, 내가 도착하던 그날 밤 고모를 그토록 언짢게 만들며 이 세상에 가지고 들어왔던 것보다 조금도 더 많지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 13. THE SEQUEL OF MY RESOLUTION", "# CHAPTER 13. THE SEQUEL OF MY RESOLUTION"]], [["For anything I know, I may have had some wild idea of running all the way to Dover, when I gave up the pursuit of the young man with the donkey-cart, and started for Greenwich.", "당나귀 수레를 끄는 그 젊은이를 뒤쫓는 것을 포기하고 그리니치를 향해 걸음을 옮길 무렵, 어쩌면 나는 도버까지 내내 달려갈 작정이라는 엉뚱한 생각을 품고 있었는지도 모른다."], ["My scattered senses were soon collected as to that point, if I had; for I came to a stop in the Kent Road, at a terrace with a piece of water before it, and a great foolish image in the middle, blowing a dry shell.", "설령 그런 마음이 있었다 해도, 그 점에 대한 흐트러진 정신은 곧 제자리를 찾았다. 켄트 로드의 어느 테라스 앞에서 내가 걸음을 멈췄기 때문이다. 그 앞에는 물웅덩이가 있었고, 한가운데에는 마른 소라 껍데기를 부는, 크고 멍청해 보이는 조각상 하나가 서 있었다."], ["Here I sat down on a doorstep, quite spent and exhausted with the efforts I had already made, and with hardly breath enough to cry for the loss of my box and half-guinea.", "나는 거기 문간 계단에 주저앉았다. 이미 쏟아부은 안간힘에 완전히 지치고 기진맥진해서, 내 상자와 반 기니를 잃어버린 것을 두고 울 숨조차 제대로 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["It was by this time dark; I heard the clocks strike ten, as I sat resting.", "이 무렵엔 이미 날이 어두웠다. 앉아서 쉬는 동안 시계가 열 시를 치는 소리를 들었다."], ["But it was a summer night, fortunately, and fine weather.", "그래도 다행히 여름밤이었고 날씨도 좋았다."], ["When I had recovered my breath, and had got rid of a stifling sensation in my throat, I rose up and went on.", "숨을 좀 돌리고 목에 걸린 듯 답답한 느낌을 떨쳐 내고 나서, 나는 몸을 일으켜 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["In the midst of my distress, I had no notion of going back.", "그렇게 비참한 처지에 놓였어도 돌아갈 생각은 조금도 없었다."], ["I doubt if I should have had any, though there had been a Swiss snow-drift in the Kent Road.", "설령 켄트 로드에 스위스의 눈보라가 몰아쳤다 해도 돌아갈 마음은 들지 않았을 것이다."]], [["But my standing possessed of only three-halfpence in the world (and I am sure I wonder how they came to be left in my pocket on a Saturday night!) troubled me none the less because I went on.", "하지만 이 세상에서 내가 가진 것이라곤 고작 일 페니 반밖에 없다는 사실은(토요일 밤에 그것이 어떻게 내 주머니에 남아 있었는지는 지금도 정말 신기하다!) 걸음을 옮기는 와중에도 어김없이 나를 괴롭혔다."], ["I began to picture to myself, as a scrap of newspaper intelligence, my being found dead in a day or two, under some hedge; and I trudged on miserably, though as fast as I could, until I happened to pass a little shop, where it was written up that ladies’ and gentlemen’s wardrobes were bought, and that the best price was given for rags, bones, and kitchen-stuff.", "나는 하루나 이틀 뒤에 어느 울타리 밑에서 죽은 채 발견되는 내 모습을, 신문 한 귀퉁이에 실린 짤막한 소식처럼 머릿속에 그리기 시작했다. 그리고 비참한 심정으로, 그래도 할 수 있는 한 빨리 터덜터덜 걷다가, 우연히 작은 가게 하나를 지나치게 되었다. 그곳에는 신사 숙녀의 헌옷을 사들이며, 넝마와 뼈와 부엌 부산물에 가장 좋은 값을 쳐 준다고 적혀 있었다."], ["The master of this shop was sitting at the door in his shirt-sleeves, smoking; and as there were a great many coats and pairs of trousers dangling from the low ceiling, and only two feeble candles burning inside to show what they were, I fancied that he looked like a man of a revengeful disposition, who had hung all his enemies, and was enjoying himself.", "이 가게 주인은 셔츠 차림으로 문간에 앉아 담배를 피우고 있었다. 낮은 천장에는 외투며 바지가 잔뜩 매달려 있었고, 그것들이 무엇인지 비춰 줄 거라곤 안에서 가물거리는 촛불 두 개뿐이었으므로, 나는 그가 마치 원수들을 모조리 목매달아 놓고 흐뭇해하는, 복수심 가득한 사내처럼 보인다고 생각했다."]], [["My late experiences with Mr. and Mrs. Micawber suggested to me that here might be a means of keeping off the wolf for a little while.", "미코버 씨 내외와 얼마 전 함께 지내며 겪은 일이, 여기서라면 잠시나마 굶주림이라는 늑대를 물리칠 수단이 생길지도 모른다는 생각을 떠올리게 했다."], ["I went up the next by-street, took off my waistcoat, rolled it neatly under my arm, and came back to the shop door.", "나는 다음 골목길로 들어가 조끼를 벗어 겨드랑이에 단정히 말아 끼고는 다시 가게 문 앞으로 돌아왔다."]], [["‘If you please, sir,’ I said, ‘I am to sell this for a fair price.’", "\"실례합니다, 아저씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"이걸 적당한 값에 팔려고요.\""]], [["Mr.", "돌로비 씨는 — 적어도 가게 문 위에 적힌 이름은 돌로비였다 — 조끼를 받아 들고, 파이프를 문기둥에 거꾸로 세워 놓더니, 가게 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Dolloby--Dolloby was the name over the shop door, at least--took the waistcoat, stood his pipe on its head, against the door-post, went into the shop, followed by me, snuffed the two candles with his fingers, spread the waistcoat on the counter, and looked at it there, held it up against the light, and looked at it there, and ultimately said:", "나도 뒤를 따라 들어갔다. 그는 손가락으로 촛불 두 개의 심지를 다듬은 다음, 조끼를 계산대 위에 펼쳐 놓고 거기서 들여다보고, 다시 불빛에 비춰 들여다보더니, 마침내 입을 열었다."]], [["‘What do you call a price, now, for this here little weskit?’", "\"자, 이 자그마한 조끼에 얼마쯤 부르겠냐?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아이고!"], ["you know best, sir,’ I returned modestly.", "아저씨가 더 잘 아시잖아요.\" 나는 겸손하게 대꾸했다."]], [["‘I can’t be buyer and seller too,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 사는 사람이면서 파는 사람까지 될 순 없잖냐.\""], ["Dolloby. ‘Put a price on this here little weskit.’", "돌로비 씨가 말했다. \"이 자그마한 조끼에 값을 매겨 봐.\""]], [["‘Would eighteenpence be?’--I hinted, after some hesitation.", "\"십팔 펜스쯤이면—?\" 나는 한참 망설인 끝에 슬며시 운을 띄웠다."]], [["Mr. Dolloby rolled it up again, and gave it me back.", "돌로비 씨는 조끼를 다시 둘둘 말아 내게 돌려주었다."], ["‘I should rob my family,’ he said, ‘if I was to offer ninepence for it.’", "\"이걸 구 펜스에 사겠다고 하면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 내 식구 등쳐 먹는 짓이지.\""]], [["This was a disagreeable way of putting the business; because it imposed upon me, a perfect stranger, the unpleasantness of asking Mr. Dolloby to rob his family on my account.", "이렇게 흥정을 거는 방식은 영 못마땅했다. 생판 모르는 남인 나에게, 나 때문에 돌로비 씨더러 식구를 등쳐 먹어 달라고 부탁해야 하는 거북한 처지를 떠안기는 셈이었으니까."], ["My circumstances being so very pressing, however, I said I would take ninepence for it, if he pleased. Mr. Dolloby, not without some grumbling, gave ninepence.", "그러나 워낙 사정이 급박했던 터라, 나는 정 그러시다면 구 펜스에 넘기겠다고 말했다. 돌로비 씨는 투덜거리지 않은 것은 아니었으나 구 펜스를 내주었다."], ["I wished him good night, and walked out of the shop the richer by that sum, and the poorer by a waistcoat.", "나는 안녕히 계시라고 인사하고는, 그 액수만큼 부자가 되고 조끼 하나만큼 가난해진 채 가게를 나섰다."], ["But when I buttoned my jacket, that was not much.", "그래도 윗도리 단추를 채우고 보니 그리 큰 차이는 아니었다."], ["Indeed, I foresaw pretty clearly that my jacket would go next, and that I should have to make the best of my way to Dover in a shirt and a pair of trousers, and might deem myself lucky if I got there even in that trim.", "사실 나는 다음 차례로 윗도리마저 떠날 것이고, 결국 셔츠와 바지 차림으로 어떻게든 도버까지 가야 하리란 것을, 그런 꼴로라도 그곳에 닿는다면 그나마 운이 좋은 거라고 여겨야 하리란 것을 꽤 또렷이 내다보고 있었다."], ["But my mind did not run so much on this as might be supposed.", "하지만 내 마음이 거기에만 그리 골몰해 있던 건 아니었다."], ["Beyond a general impression of the distance before me, and of the young man with the donkey-cart having used me cruelly, I think I had no very urgent sense of my difficulties when I once again set off with my ninepence in my pocket.", "앞에 놓인 길이 멀다는 막연한 느낌과, 당나귀 수레를 끄는 그 젊은이가 나를 모질게 대했다는 생각 말고는, 구 펜스를 주머니에 넣고 다시 길을 나설 무렵 내 어려움이 그리 절박하게 느껴지지는 않았던 것 같다."]], [["A plan had occurred to me for passing the night, which I was going to carry into execution.", "밤을 보낼 한 가지 방도가 떠올랐고, 나는 그것을 실행에 옮길 참이었다."], ["This was, to lie behind the wall at the back of my old school, in a corner where there used to be a haystack.", "그건 옛 학교 뒤편 담장 너머, 예전에 건초더미가 있던 한쪽 구석에 누워 자는 것이었다."], ["I imagined it would be a kind of company to have the boys, and the bedroom where I used to tell the stories, so near me: although the boys would know nothing of my being there, and the bedroom would yield me no shelter.", "아이들과, 내가 옛날에 이야기를 들려주던 침실이 이렇게 가까이 있다는 것이 일종의 벗이 되어 주리라는 생각이 들었다. 비록 아이들은 내가 거기 있는 줄 전혀 모를 테고, 그 침실이 나에게 잠자리를 내어 줄 리도 없었지만."]], [["I had had a hard day’s work, and was pretty well jaded when I came climbing out, at last, upon the level of Blackheath.", "하루 종일 힘겹게 걸은 터라, 마침내 기어오르듯 블랙히스의 평지에 올라섰을 무렵엔 몸이 거의 녹초가 되어 있었다."], ["It cost me some trouble to find out Salem House; but I found it, and I found a haystack in the corner, and I lay down by it; having first walked round the wall, and looked up at the windows, and seen that all was dark and silent within.", "세일럼 하우스를 찾는 데 적잖이 애를 먹었다. 하지만 끝내 찾아냈고, 구석에 있는 건초더미도 찾아냈으며, 그 곁에 몸을 뉘었다. 먼저 담장을 한 바퀴 돌며 창문들을 올려다보고, 안이 온통 캄캄하고 고요한 것을 확인한 뒤였다."], ["Never shall I forget the lonely sensation of first lying down, without a roof above my head!", "머리 위에 지붕 하나 없이 처음 자리에 누웠을 때의 그 외로운 느낌을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다!"]], [["Sleep came upon me as it came on many other outcasts, against whom house-doors were locked, and house-dogs barked, that night--and I dreamed of lying on my old school-bed, talking to the boys in my room; and found myself sitting upright, with Steerforth’s name upon my lips, looking wildly at the stars that were glistening and glimmering above me.", "그날 밤, 집집마다 문이 잠기고 집집의 개가 짖어 대며 쫓아낸 수많은 떠돌이들에게 그러했듯, 나에게도 잠이 찾아왔다. 그리고 나는 옛 학교 침대에 누워 방 안 아이들과 이야기하는 꿈을 꾸었다. 그러다 정신을 차려 보니 나는 몸을 똑바로 일으켜 앉아, 입술에 스티어포스의 이름을 올린 채, 머리 위에서 반짝이며 가물거리는 별들을 넋 나간 듯 올려다보고 있었다."], ["When I remembered where I was at that untimely hour, a feeling stole upon me that made me get up, afraid of I don’t know what, and walk about.", "이 한밤중에 내가 어디에 있는지를 떠올리자, 무언가 알 수 없는 두려움에 자리에서 일어나 서성거리게 만드는 어떤 기분이 슬며시 나를 사로잡았다."], ["But the fainter glimmering of the stars, and the pale light in the sky where the day was coming, reassured me: and my eyes being very heavy, I lay down again and slept--though with a knowledge in my sleep that it was cold--until the warm beams of the sun, and the ringing of the getting-up bell at Salem House, awoke me.", "하지만 점점 희미해지는 별빛과, 동이 트는 쪽 하늘의 창백한 빛이 나를 다시 안심시켜 주었다. 두 눈이 무척이나 무거웠던 터라, 나는 다시 자리에 누워 잠들었다. 잠결에도 춥다는 것은 알고 있었지만, 따뜻한 햇살과 세일럼 하우스의 기상 종소리가 나를 깨울 때까지 잤다."], ["If I could have hoped that Steerforth was there, I would have lurked about until he came out alone; but I knew he must have left long since.", "만약 스티어포스가 거기 있으리라 기대할 수 있었다면, 나는 그가 혼자 나올 때까지 근처에 숨어 있었을 것이다. 하지만 그가 진작에 떠났으리란 것을 나는 알고 있었다."], ["Traddles still remained, perhaps, but it was very doubtful; and I had not sufficient confidence in his discretion or good luck, however strong my reliance was on his good nature, to wish to trust him with my situation.", "트래들스는 어쩌면 아직 남아 있을지도 몰랐으나, 그것조차 무척 미심쩍었다. 게다가 그의 착한 마음씨만큼은 굳게 믿었지만, 내 처지를 털어놓을 만큼 그의 입단속이나 운수를 믿을 자신은 없었다."], ["So I crept away from the wall as Mr. Creakle’s boys were getting up, and struck into the long dusty track which I had first known to be the Dover Road when I was one of them, and when I little expected that any eyes would ever see me the wayfarer I was now, upon it.", "그래서 크리클 씨의 학생들이 일어날 무렵, 나는 담장에서 슬그머니 빠져나와, 내가 그 학생들 중 하나였을 적에 도버 로드라고 처음 알게 된 그 길고 먼지투성이 길로 접어들었다. 그때만 해도 누군가의 눈이 지금처럼 이 길 위를 떠도는 나그네가 된 나를 보게 되리라곤 거의 상상도 못 했던 그 길로."]], [["What a different Sunday morning from the old Sunday morning at Yarmouth!", "야머스에서 맞던 옛 일요일 아침과는 얼마나 다른 일요일 아침이던지!"], ["In due time I heard the church-bells ringing, as I plodded on; and I met people who were going to church; and I passed a church or two where the congregation were inside, and the sound of singing came out into the sunshine, while the beadle sat and cooled himself in the shade of the porch, or stood beneath the yew-tree, with his hand to his forehead, glowering at me going by.", "터덜터덜 걷는 동안 때맞춰 교회 종소리가 들려왔다. 교회로 가는 사람들과 마주쳤고, 신도들이 안에 들어차 있는 교회를 한두 곳 지나치기도 했는데, 햇살 속으로 찬송 소리가 흘러나오는 동안 교회지기는 현관 그늘에 앉아 더위를 식히거나, 주목 아래 서서 이마에 손을 얹고 지나가는 나를 노려보았다."], ["But the peace and rest of the old Sunday morning were on everything, except me.", "하지만 옛 일요일 아침의 그 평화와 안식은, 나를 빼고는 만물에 깃들어 있었다."], ["That was the difference.", "그게 바로 차이였다."], ["I felt quite wicked in my dirt and dust, with my tangled hair.", "흙먼지를 뒤집어쓴 데다 머리카락은 엉겨 붙은 내 꼴이, 나 자신이 영락없는 죄인처럼 느껴졌다."], ["But for the quiet picture I had conjured up, of my mother in her youth and beauty, weeping by the fire, and my aunt relenting to her, I hardly think I should have had the courage to go on until next day. But it always went before me, and I followed.", "젊고 아름다운 시절의 어머니가 난롯가에서 울고, 고모님이 그런 어머니에게 마음을 누그러뜨리는, 내가 마음속에 그려 낸 그 고요한 그림이 아니었다면, 다음 날까지 걸어 나아갈 용기를 도무지 내지 못했을 것 같다. 하지만 그 그림은 늘 내 앞에서 길잡이가 되어 주었고, 나는 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["I got, that Sunday, through three-and-twenty miles on the straight road, though not very easily, for I was new to that kind of toil.", "그 일요일에 나는 곧게 뻗은 길로 이십삼 마일을 걸었다. 그리 수월하지는 않았는데, 그런 고생에는 영 서툴렀기 때문이다."], ["I see myself, as evening closes in, coming over the bridge at Rochester, footsore and tired, and eating bread that I had bought for supper.", "저녁이 깃들 무렵, 발은 부르트고 몸은 지친 채 로체스터의 다리를 건너오며, 저녁거리로 사 둔 빵을 먹는 내 모습이 지금도 눈에 선하다."], ["One or two little houses, with the notice, ‘Lodgings for Travellers’, hanging out, had tempted me; but I was afraid of spending the few pence I had, and was even more afraid of the vicious looks of the trampers I had met or overtaken.", "'여행자 숙소'라는 안내문을 내건 자그마한 집 한두 채가 나를 솔깃하게 했지만, 가진 몇 푼을 쓰기가 두려웠고, 길에서 마주치거나 따라잡은 떠돌이들의 험상궂은 낯빛은 그보다 더 무서웠다. 그래서 나는 하늘 말고는 어떤 지붕도 구하지 않았다."], ["I sought no shelter, therefore, but the sky; and toiling into Chatham,--which, in that night’s aspect, is a mere dream of chalk, and drawbridges, and mastless ships in a muddy river, roofed like Noah’s arks,--crept, at last, upon a sort of grass-grown battery overhanging a lane, where a sentry was walking to and fro.", "그리고 채텀으로 힘겹게 걸어 들어갔다 — 그날 밤의 모습으로 보자면, 채텀은 그저 백악과 도개교와, 진흙탕 강물 위에 노아의 방주처럼 지붕을 인 돛대 없는 배들이 어우러진 한바탕 꿈만 같았다 — 그러다 마침내 어느 골목길 위로 비스듬히 솟은, 풀이 무성한 포대 같은 곳으로 기어 올라갔는데, 거기서는 보초 하나가 이리저리 오가고 있었다."], ["Here I lay down, near a cannon; and, happy in the society of the sentry’s footsteps, though he knew no more of my being above him than the boys at Salem House had known of my lying by the wall, slept soundly until morning.", "나는 거기 대포 하나 곁에 몸을 뉘었다. 세일럼 하우스의 아이들이 담장 옆에 누운 나를 몰랐듯, 보초도 자기 위쪽에 내가 있는 줄 전혀 몰랐지만, 그 보초의 발소리를 벗 삼으니 마음이 흐뭇해져서, 아침까지 곤히 잤다."]], [["Very stiff and sore of foot I was in the morning, and quite dazed by the beating of drums and marching of troops, which seemed to hem me in on every side when I went down towards the long narrow street.", "아침에 일어나니 몸은 뻣뻣하고 발은 욱신거렸다. 좁고 긴 거리 쪽으로 내려갈 때 사방에서 나를 에워싸는 듯한 북소리와 군대의 행진에 정신마저 멍해졌다."], ["Feeling that I could go but a very little way that day, if I were to reserve any strength for getting to my journey’s end, I resolved to make the sale of my jacket its principal business.", "여정의 끝까지 갈 기력을 조금이라도 남겨 두려면 그날은 아주 조금밖에 걷지 못하겠다는 생각이 들어, 나는 윗도리를 파는 일을 그날의 주된 과업으로 삼기로 마음먹었다."], ["Accordingly, I took the jacket off, that I might learn to do without it; and carrying it under my arm, began a tour of inspection of the various slop-shops.", "그래서 윗도리 없이 지내는 데 익숙해지려고 그것을 벗어 겨드랑이에 끼고는, 여러 헌옷 가게를 둘러보러 나섰다."]], [["It was a likely place to sell a jacket in; for the dealers in second-hand clothes were numerous, and were, generally speaking, on the look-out for customers at their shop doors.", "윗도리를 팔기에는 그럴듯한 곳이었다. 헌옷 장수가 수두룩했고, 대개들 가게 문 앞에서 손님을 노리고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But as most of them had, hanging up among their stock, an officer’s coat or two, epaulettes and all, I was rendered timid by the costly nature of their dealings, and walked about for a long time without offering my merchandise to anyone.", "하지만 그들 대부분이 사들인 물건들 틈에 견장까지 달린 장교 외투를 한두 벌씩 매달아 놓은 터라, 나는 그들 거래의 값비싼 성격에 주눅이 들어, 한참 동안 누구에게도 내 물건을 내밀지 못한 채 서성였다."]], [["This modesty of mine directed my attention to the marine-store shops, and such shops as Mr. Dolloby’s, in preference to the regular dealers.", "이런 소심함 때문에 나는 정식 옷 장수보다는 선박용 잡화점이나 돌로비 씨네 같은 가게로 눈길을 돌리게 되었다."], ["At last I found one that I thought looked promising, at the corner of a dirty lane, ending in an enclosure full of stinging-nettles, against the palings of which some second-hand sailors’ clothes, that seemed to have overflowed the shop, were fluttering among some cots, and rusty guns, and oilskin hats, and certain trays full of so many old rusty keys of so many sizes that they seemed various enough to open all the doors in the world.", "마침내 그럴듯해 보이는 곳을 한 군데 찾았는데, 더러운 골목 모퉁이에 있었다. 골목 끝은 쐐기풀이 가득한 공터로 막혀 있었고, 그 울타리에는 가게에서 흘러넘친 듯한 헌 뱃사람 옷가지가 간이침대며 녹슨 총이며 방수모 따위와 함께 나부끼고 있었다. 또 어떤 쟁반에는 어찌나 크기가 제각각인 낡고 녹슨 열쇠가 잔뜩 담겨 있던지, 세상 모든 문을 다 열 수 있을 만큼 종류가 다양해 보였다."]], [["Into this shop, which was low and small, and which was darkened rather than lighted by a little window, overhung with clothes, and was descended into by some steps, I went with a palpitating heart; which was not relieved when an ugly old man, with the lower part of his face all covered with a stubbly grey beard, rushed out of a dirty den behind it, and seized me by the hair of my head.", "이 가게는 낮고 작았으며, 옷가지가 잔뜩 드리워진 작은 창문 하나가 빛을 들이기는커녕 오히려 가게를 어둡게 만들었고, 안으로 들어가려면 계단을 몇 개 내려가야 했다. 나는 두근거리는 가슴을 안고 그 안으로 들어갔는데, 뒤편의 더러운 굴 같은 방에서 추레한 잿빛 수염으로 얼굴 아랫부분을 온통 뒤덮은 흉측한 노인이 뛰쳐나와 내 머리카락을 움켜쥐었을 때 그 두근거림은 조금도 가라앉지 않았다."], ["He was a dreadful old man to look at, in a filthy flannel waistcoat, and smelling terribly of rum.", "보기만 해도 끔찍한 노인이었다. 더러운 플란넬 조끼를 입고, 럼주 냄새를 지독하게 풍겼다."], ["His bedstead, covered with a tumbled and ragged piece of patchwork, was in the den he had come from, where another little window showed a prospect of more stinging-nettles, and a lame donkey.", "그가 나온 굴 같은 방 안에는, 헝클어지고 너덜너덜한 누비이불을 덮은 침대가 있었고, 거기 또 다른 작은 창문으로는 쐐기풀이 더 많이 우거진 풍경과 절름발이 당나귀 한 마리가 내다보였다."]], [["‘Oh, what do you want?’ grinned this old man, in a fierce, monotonous whine.", "\"오, 뭘 원하는 게냐?\" 이 노인이 사납고 단조로운 콧소리로 이를 드러내며 으르렁댔다."], ["‘Oh, my eyes and limbs, what do you want?", "\"오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야, 뭘 원하는 게냐?"], ["Oh, my lungs and liver, what do you want?", "오, 내 허파와 간이야, 뭘 원하는 게냐?"], ["Oh, goroo, goroo!’", "오, 고루, 고루!\""]], [["I was so much dismayed by these words, and particularly by the repetition of the last unknown one, which was a kind of rattle in his throat, that I could make no answer; hereupon the old man, still holding me by the hair, repeated:", "이 말에, 특히 마지막의 알 수 없는 그 한마디 — 목구멍에서 그르렁대는 소리 같은 것 — 를 거듭 내뱉는 통에 어찌나 질겁했던지, 나는 아무 대답도 할 수 없었다. 그러자 노인은 여전히 내 머리카락을 움켜쥔 채 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Oh, what do you want?", "\"오, 뭘 원하는 게냐?"], ["Oh, my eyes and limbs, what do you want?", "오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야, 뭘 원하는 게냐?"], ["Oh, my lungs and liver, what do you want?", "오, 내 허파와 간이야, 뭘 원하는 게냐?"], ["Oh, goroo!’--which he screwed out of himself, with an energy that made his eyes start in his head.", "오, 고루!\" 그는 두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 만큼 안간힘을 쥐어짜며 그 말을 비틀어 냈다."]], [["‘I wanted to know,’ I said, trembling, ‘if you would buy a jacket.’", "\"알고 싶은 게 있어서요.\" 나는 떨면서 말했다. \"윗도리를 사 주실까 해서요.\""]], [["‘Oh, let’s see the jacket!’ cried the old man.", "\"오, 어디 윗도리 좀 보자!\" 노인이 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh, my heart on fire, show the jacket to us!", "\"오, 내 가슴팍에 불이 붙는구나, 어서 그 윗도리를 보여 다오!"], ["Oh, my eyes and limbs, bring the jacket out!’", "오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야, 그 윗도리를 꺼내 보거라!\""]], [["With that he took his trembling hands, which were like the claws of a great bird, out of my hair; and put on a pair of spectacles, not at all ornamental to his inflamed eyes.", "그러면서 그는 커다란 새의 발톱 같은, 부들부들 떨리는 두 손을 내 머리카락에서 떼더니, 충혈된 두 눈에 전혀 어울리지 않는 안경 하나를 걸쳤다."]], [["‘Oh, how much for the jacket?’ cried the old man, after examining it.", "\"오, 윗도리에 얼마를 쳐 줄까?\" 노인이 그것을 살펴본 뒤 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh--goroo!--how much for the jacket?’", "\"오 — 고루! — 윗도리에 얼마면 되겠냐?\""]], [["‘Half-a-crown,’ I answered, recovering myself.", "\"반 크라운이요.\" 나는 정신을 추스르며 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, my lungs and liver,’ cried the old man, ‘no!", "\"오, 내 허파와 간이야,\" 노인이 외쳤다. \"안 돼!"], ["Oh, my eyes, no!", "오, 내 눈깔아, 안 돼!"], ["Oh, my limbs, no!", "오, 내 팔다리야, 안 돼!"], ["Eighteenpence. Goroo!’", "십팔 펜스다. 고루!\""]], [["Every time he uttered this ejaculation, his eyes seemed to be in danger of starting out; and every sentence he spoke, he delivered in a sort of tune, always exactly the same, and more like a gust of wind, which begins low, mounts up high, and falls again, than any other comparison I can find for it.", "이 외마디를 내뱉을 때마다 그의 두 눈은 금방이라도 튀어나올 듯했다. 그리고 그가 하는 말은 한마디 한마디가 일종의 가락에 실려 나왔는데, 늘 토씨 하나 틀림없이 똑같았고, 내가 달리 견줄 만한 게 없으니 굳이 비유하자면 낮게 시작했다가 높이 솟구치고 다시 잦아드는 한 줄기 바람결 같았다."]], [["‘Well,’ said I, glad to have closed the bargain, ‘I’ll take eighteenpence.’", "\"좋아요.\" 나는 흥정을 마무리 짓게 되어 반가운 마음에 말했다. \"십팔 펜스에 할게요.\""]], [["‘Oh, my liver!’ cried the old man, throwing the jacket on a shelf.", "\"오, 내 간이야!\" 노인이 윗도리를 선반에 내던지며 외쳤다. \"가게에서 나가!"], ["‘Get out of the shop!", "오, 내 허파야, 가게에서 나가!"], ["Oh, my lungs, get out of the shop!", "오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야 — 고루!"], ["Oh, my eyes and limbs--goroo!--don’t ask for money; make it an exchange.’ I never was so frightened in my life, before or since; but I told him humbly that I wanted money, and that nothing else was of any use to me, but that I would wait for it, as he desired, outside, and had no wish to hurry him.", "— 돈은 달라고 하지 마라, 물물교환으로 하자.\" 내 평생 그때만큼, 그 전에도 그 후에도 그렇게 겁먹은 적은 없었다. 하지만 나는 공손히, 돈이 필요하며 다른 것은 나에게 아무 쓸모가 없다고, 하지만 바깥에서 그가 원하는 대로 기다리겠으며 그를 재촉할 마음은 없다고 말했다."], ["So I went outside, and sat down in the shade in a corner.", "그래서 나는 밖으로 나가 한쪽 구석 그늘에 앉았다."], ["And I sat there so many hours, that the shade became sunlight, and the sunlight became shade again, and still I sat there waiting for the money.", "그리고 어찌나 오래 거기 앉아 있었던지, 그늘이 볕으로 변하고 그 볕이 다시 그늘로 변하도록, 나는 그저 돈을 기다리며 그 자리에 앉아 있었다."]], [["There never was such another drunken madman in that line of business, I hope.", "그 장사판에 그런 술 취한 미치광이가 또 있으리라고는, 부디 없기를 바란다."], ["That he was well known in the neighbourhood, and enjoyed the reputation of having sold himself to the devil, I soon understood from the visits he received from the boys, who continually came skirmishing about the shop, shouting that legend, and calling to him to bring out his gold.", "그가 동네에서 유명짜한 인물이고, 악마에게 제 몸을 팔아넘겼다는 평판을 누리고 있다는 것을, 나는 곧 아이들의 출몰로 알아챘다. 아이들이 끊임없이 가게 주위로 몰려와 싸움을 걸 듯 그 전설을 외쳐 대며, 어서 금을 꺼내 보이라고 노인에게 소리쳤기 때문이다."], ["‘You ain’t poor, you know, Charley, as you pretend.", "\"찰리, 네가 꾸미는 것처럼 그렇게 가난한 게 아니잖아."], ["Bring out your gold.", "금 좀 꺼내 봐."], ["Bring out some of the gold you sold yourself to the devil for. Come!", "악마한테 몸 팔고 받은 금 좀 내놔 보라고. 어서!"], ["It’s in the lining of the mattress, Charley.", "그거 매트리스 안감 속에 있잖아, 찰리."], ["Rip it open and let’s have some!’ This, and many offers to lend him a knife for the purpose, exasperated him to such a degree, that the whole day was a succession of rushes on his part, and flights on the part of the boys.", "죽 찢어서 좀 보여 줘!\" 이런 말과 함께, 그러라고 칼을 빌려주겠다는 제안이 수두룩하게 쏟아지자, 노인은 어찌나 약이 올랐던지 하루 종일 그가 달려들면 아이들이 달아나는 일이 끝없이 되풀이되었다."], ["Sometimes in his rage he would take me for one of them, and come at me, mouthing as if he were going to tear me in pieces; then, remembering me, just in time, would dive into the shop, and lie upon his bed, as I thought from the sound of his voice, yelling in a frantic way, to his own windy tune, the ‘Death of Nelson’; with an Oh!", "더러는 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 나를 그 아이들 중 하나로 착각하고, 마치 나를 갈가리 찢어 놓을 듯 입을 우물거리며 덤벼들기도 했다. 그러다 아슬아슬하게 나를 알아보고는 가게 안으로 뛰어들어가, 목소리로 미루어 짐작하건대 침대에 드러누운 채, 제 특유의 그 바람 같은 가락에 실어 미친 듯이 「넬슨의 죽음」을 악써 부르곤 했다."], ["before every line, and innumerable Goroos interspersed. As if this were not bad enough for me, the boys, connecting me with the establishment, on account of the patience and perseverance with which I sat outside, half-dressed, pelted me, and used me very ill all day.", "매 행 앞에 \"오!\"를 붙이고, 그 사이사이에 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 \"고루\"를 끼워 가면서. 이것만으로도 나에게는 충분히 고약한 일이었거늘, 아이들은 내가 옷도 변변히 못 걸친 채 바깥에 끈질기게 앉아 버티는 꼴을 보고 나를 그 가게와 한패로 엮어서는, 하루 종일 나에게 돌팔매질을 하며 몹시 못살게 굴었다."]], [["He made many attempts to induce me to consent to an exchange; at one time coming out with a fishing-rod, at another with a fiddle, at another with a cocked hat, at another with a flute.", "노인은 물물교환에 응하도록 나를 꾀려고 갖은 시도를 했다. 한 번은 낚싯대를 들고 나오고, 또 한 번은 바이올린을, 또 한 번은 삼각모를, 또 한 번은 플루트를 들고 나왔다."], ["But I resisted all these overtures, and sat there in desperation; each time asking him, with tears in my eyes, for my money or my jacket.", "하지만 나는 그 모든 제안을 뿌리치고 절망 속에 그 자리에 앉아, 그때마다 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈으로 내 돈이 아니면 윗도리를 돌려 달라고 졸랐다."], ["At last he began to pay me in halfpence at a time; and was full two hours getting by easy stages to a shilling.", "마침내 그는 반 페니씩 끊어서 돈을 치르기 시작했는데, 야금야금 단계를 밟아 한 실링을 채우는 데 꼬박 두 시간이 걸렸다."]], [["‘Oh, my eyes and limbs!’ he then cried, peeping hideously out of the shop, after a long pause, ‘will you go for twopence more?’", "\"오, 내 눈깔과 팔다리야!\" 한참 뜸을 들인 뒤, 노인이 가게에서 흉측하게 내다보며 외쳤다. \"이 펜스만 깎고 갈 텐가?\""]], [["‘I can’t,’ I said; ‘I shall be starved.’", "\"못 깎아요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그럼 굶어 죽어요.\""]], [["‘Oh, my lungs and liver, will you go for threepence?’", "\"오, 내 허파와 간이야, 삼 펜스 깎고 갈 텐가?\""]], [["‘I would go for nothing, if I could,’ I said, ‘but I want the money badly.’", "\"그럴 수만 있다면 한 푼도 안 받고 가겠지만,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 돈이 정말 절실해요.\""]], [["‘Oh, go-roo!’ (it is really impossible to express how he twisted this ejaculation out of himself, as he peeped round the door-post at me, showing nothing but his crafty old head); ‘will you go for fourpence?’", "\"오, 고-루!\" (문기둥 너머로 교활한 늙은 머리통만 빼꼼 내밀고 나를 엿보며, 이 외마디를 제 몸에서 어떻게 비틀어 냈는지는 정말이지 글로 옮길 길이 없다.) \"사 펜스 깎고 갈 텐가?\""]], [["I was so faint and weary that I closed with this offer; and taking the money out of his claw, not without trembling, went away more hungry and thirsty than I had ever been, a little before sunset.", "나는 너무도 기진하고 지친 터라 이 제안을 받아들였다. 그러고는 떨지 않을 수 없는 손길로 그의 발톱에서 돈을 받아 들고, 해가 지기 조금 전, 그 어느 때보다도 굶주리고 목마른 채로 자리를 떴다."], ["But at an expense of threepence I soon refreshed myself completely; and, being in better spirits then, limped seven miles upon my road.", "하지만 삼 펜스를 들여 나는 곧 기운을 말끔히 되찾았다. 그렇게 한결 좋아진 기분으로, 다리를 절뚝이며 칠 마일을 더 걸었다."]], [["My bed at night was under another haystack, where I rested comfortably, after having washed my blistered feet in a stream, and dressed them as well as I was able, with some cool leaves.", "그날 밤 내 잠자리는 또 다른 건초더미 아래였다. 시냇물에 물집 잡힌 발을 씻고, 시원한 잎사귀 몇 장으로 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 싸맨 뒤, 나는 그곳에서 편안히 쉬었다."], ["When I took the road again next morning, I found that it lay through a succession of hop-grounds and orchards.", "이튿날 아침 다시 길을 나섰을 때, 그 길이 홉밭과 과수원을 줄줄이 지나간다는 것을 알았다."], ["It was sufficiently late in the year for the orchards to be ruddy with ripe apples; and in a few places the hop-pickers were already at work.", "과수원이 잘 익은 사과로 발갛게 물들 만큼 한 해가 깊어 있었고, 몇몇 곳에서는 홉을 따는 사람들이 벌써 일을 하고 있었다."], ["I thought it all extremely beautiful, and made up my mind to sleep among the hops that night: imagining some cheerful companionship in the long perspectives of poles, with the graceful leaves twining round them.", "나는 그 모든 풍경이 더없이 아름답다고 생각했고, 그날 밤은 홉밭에서 자기로 마음먹었다. 길게 늘어선 장대들과 그것을 우아하게 휘감은 잎사귀들 사이에서 어떤 정겨운 벗을 그려 보면서."]], [["The trampers were worse than ever that day, and inspired me with a dread that is yet quite fresh in my mind.", "그날은 떠돌이들이 어느 때보다 더 험악해서, 지금도 생생하게 떠오르는 공포를 내게 심어 주었다."], ["Some of them were most ferocious-looking ruffians, who stared at me as I went by; and stopped, perhaps, and called after me to come back and speak to them, and when I took to my heels, stoned me.", "그중 몇몇은 가장 흉포해 보이는 불한당이었는데, 내가 지나가면 나를 노려보다가, 어쩌면 멈춰 서서 돌아와 말 좀 하자고 불러 댔고, 내가 줄행랑을 치면 돌을 던졌다."], ["I recollect one young fellow--a tinker, I suppose, from his wallet and brazier--who had a woman with him, and who faced about and stared at me thus; and then roared to me in such a tremendous voice to come back, that I halted and looked round.", "어떤 젊은 사내가 기억난다 — 봇짐과 화로로 미루어 땜장이였지 싶다 — 여자 하나를 데리고 다니던 그 사내는 홱 돌아서서 그렇게 나를 노려보더니, 어찌나 무시무시한 목소리로 돌아오라고 호통을 쳤던지, 나는 걸음을 멈추고 돌아보고 말았다."]], [["‘Come here, when you’re called,’ said the tinker, ‘or I’ll rip your young body open.’", "\"부르면 이리 와.\" 땜장이가 말했다. \"안 그러면 네 어린 배때기를 갈라 버릴 테다.\""]], [["I thought it best to go back.", "돌아가는 게 상책이라고 나는 생각했다."], ["As I drew nearer to them, trying to propitiate the tinker by my looks, I observed that the woman had a black eye.", "그들에게 가까이 다가가며, 표정으로나마 땜장이의 비위를 맞추려 애쓰다가, 나는 여자의 한쪽 눈이 멍들어 있는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘Where are you going?’ said the tinker, gripping the bosom of my shirt with his blackened hand.", "\"어디 가는 길이냐?\" 땜장이가 시커먼 손으로 내 셔츠 앞섶을 움켜쥐며 말했다."]], [["‘I am going to Dover,’ I said.", "\"도버에 가요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Where do you come from?’ asked the tinker, giving his hand another turn in my shirt, to hold me more securely.", "\"어디서 오는 길이냐?\" 땜장이가 나를 더 단단히 붙잡으려고 셔츠를 한 번 더 비틀어 쥐며 물었다."]], [["‘I come from London,’ I said.", "\"런던에서 와요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘What lay are you upon?’ asked the tinker.", "\"무슨 수작을 부리는 거냐?\" 땜장이가 물었다."], ["‘Are you a prig?’", "\"도둑놈이냐?\""]], [["‘N-no,’ I said.", "\"아—아니에요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Ain’t you, by G--?", "\"아니라고, 빌어먹을?"], ["If you make a brag of your honesty to me,’ said the tinker, ‘I’ll knock your brains out.’", "내 앞에서 정직한 척 으스대다간,\" 땜장이가 말했다. \"네 골통을 박살 내 버릴 테다.\""]], [["With his disengaged hand he made a menace of striking me, and then looked at me from head to foot.", "그는 비어 있는 손으로 나를 때리는 시늉을 하더니, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 나를 훑어보았다."]], [["‘Have you got the price of a pint of beer about you?’ said the tinker.", "\"맥주 한 잔 살 돈은 몸에 지녔겠지?\" 땜장이가 말했다."], ["‘If you have, out with it, afore I take it away!’", "\"있으면 빼앗기기 전에 어서 내놔!\""]], [["I should certainly have produced it, but that I met the woman’s look, and saw her very slightly shake her head, and form ‘No!’ with her lips.", "나는 틀림없이 돈을 꺼냈을 것이다. 그런데 그 여자의 눈길과 마주쳤고, 그녀가 아주 살짝 고개를 가로젓더니 입술로 \"안 돼!\" 하는 모양을 짓는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘I am very poor,’ I said, attempting to smile, ‘and have got no money.’", "\"전 아주 가난해서,\" 나는 애써 웃어 보이며 말했다. \"돈이 한 푼도 없어요.\""]], [["‘Why, what do you mean?’ said the tinker, looking so sternly at me, that I almost feared he saw the money in my pocket.", "\"뭐라고, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 땜장이가 어찌나 매섭게 나를 노려보던지, 나는 그가 내 주머니 속 돈을 꿰뚫어 본 게 아닐까 겁이 날 지경이었다."]], [["‘Sir!’ I stammered.", "\"아저씨!\" 내가 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘What do you mean,’ said the tinker, ‘by wearing my brother’s silk handkerchief!", "\"무슨 뜻이냐,\" 땜장이가 말했다. \"우리 형 비단 손수건을 두르고 다니다니!"], ["Give it over here!’ And he had mine off my neck in a moment, and tossed it to the woman.", "이리 내놔!\" 그러고는 순식간에 내 목에서 손수건을 잡아채 여자에게 던졌다."]], [["The woman burst into a fit of laughter, as if she thought this a joke, and tossed it back to me, nodded once, as slightly as before, and made the word ‘Go!’ with her lips.", "여자는 이게 무슨 농담이라도 되는 양 까르르 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 그것을 도로 나에게 던지고는, 아까처럼 살짝 한 번 고개를 끄덕이고 입술로 \"가!\" 하는 모양을 지었다."], ["Before I could obey, however, the tinker seized the handkerchief out of my hand with a roughness that threw me away like a feather, and putting it loosely round his own neck, turned upon the woman with an oath, and knocked her down.", "하지만 내가 그 말을 따르기도 전에, 땜장이는 어찌나 거칠게 내 손에서 손수건을 낚아챘던지 나를 깃털처럼 내동댕이쳤고, 그것을 제 목에 헐겁게 두르더니 욕설을 내뱉으며 여자에게 돌아서서 그녀를 후려쳐 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["I never shall forget seeing her fall backward on the hard road, and lie there with her bonnet tumbled off, and her hair all whitened in the dust; nor, when I looked back from a distance, seeing her sitting on the pathway, which was a bank by the roadside, wiping the blood from her face with a corner of her shawl, while he went on ahead.", "그녀가 딱딱한 길바닥에 발랑 나자빠져, 보닛이 벗겨진 채 머리카락이 온통 먼지에 허옇게 뒤덮인 채로 누워 있던 모습을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 또 멀리서 돌아보았을 때, 길가 둑이던 그 보도에 그녀가 주저앉아 숄 귀퉁이로 얼굴의 피를 닦고 있는데, 그 사내는 앞장서서 가 버리던 그 모습도."]], [["This adventure frightened me so, that, afterwards, when I saw any of these people coming, I turned back until I could find a hiding-place, where I remained until they had gone out of sight; which happened so often, that I was very seriously delayed.", "이 일에 어찌나 겁을 먹었던지, 그 뒤로 나는 그런 사람들이 다가오는 게 보이면, 숨을 곳을 찾을 때까지 되돌아가서 그들이 시야에서 사라질 때까지 거기 머물렀다. 그런 일이 하도 잦아서 길이 몹시 더뎌졌다."], ["But under this difficulty, as under all the other difficulties of my journey, I seemed to be sustained and led on by my fanciful picture of my mother in her youth, before I came into the world.", "하지만 이 어려움 속에서도, 여정의 다른 온갖 어려움 속에서와 마찬가지로, 내가 이 세상에 나오기 전, 젊은 시절 어머니의 모습을 그린 그 마음속 그림이 나를 떠받치고 이끌어 주는 것 같았다."], ["It always kept me company.", "그 그림은 늘 나와 함께였다."], ["It was there, among the hops, when I lay down to sleep; it was with me on my waking in the morning; it went before me all day.", "잠을 청하려 누웠을 때 홉밭 사이에도 그것이 있었고, 아침에 깨어날 때도 나와 함께였으며, 하루 종일 내 앞에서 길잡이가 되어 주었다."], ["I have associated it, ever since, with the sunny street of Canterbury, dozing as it were in the hot light; and with the sight of its old houses and gateways, and the stately, grey Cathedral, with the rooks sailing round the towers.", "그 뒤로 나는 그 그림을, 무더운 햇살 속에서 마치 졸고 있는 듯한 캔터베리의 양지바른 거리와, 그곳의 오래된 집들과 성문들, 그리고 탑 둘레를 떼까마귀가 맴도는 위풍당당한 잿빛 대성당의 광경과 함께 떠올리게 되었다."], ["When I came, at last, upon the bare, wide downs near Dover, it relieved the solitary aspect of the scene with hope; and not until I reached that first great aim of my journey, and actually set foot in the town itself, on the sixth day of my flight, did it desert me.", "마침내 도버 근처의 휑하니 너른 구릉지에 이르렀을 때, 그 그림은 그 적막한 풍경에 희망을 보태 주었다. 그리고 내 여정의 그 첫 번째 큰 목표지에 닿아, 도주 엿새째에 마침내 그 마을에 발을 들여놓고 나서야 비로소 그 그림은 나를 떠났다."], ["But then, strange to say, when I stood with my ragged shoes, and my dusty, sunburnt, half-clothed figure, in the place so long desired, it seemed to vanish like a dream, and to leave me helpless and dispirited.", "하지만 그때, 참 이상하게도, 너덜너덜한 신발과 먼지투성이에 햇볕에 그을린, 옷도 변변히 못 걸친 몰골로, 그토록 오래 바라던 그곳에 막상 서고 보니, 그 그림은 꿈처럼 스러져 버리고, 나를 어쩔 줄 모르고 풀 죽은 채로 내버려 두는 것만 같았다."]], [["I inquired about my aunt among the boatmen first, and received various answers.", "나는 먼저 뱃사람들에게 고모님에 대해 물어보았는데, 갖가지 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["One said she lived in the South Foreland Light, and had singed her whiskers by doing so; another, that she was made fast to the great buoy outside the harbour, and could only be visited at half-tide; a third, that she was locked up in Maidstone jail for child-stealing; a fourth, that she was seen to mount a broom in the last high wind, and make direct for Calais.", "어떤 이는 고모님이 사우스 포어랜드 등대에 살면서 그 바람에 구레나룻을 그슬렸다고 했고, 다른 이는 항구 바깥 큰 부표에 매여 있어서 썰물 때나 찾아갈 수 있다고 했으며, 또 다른 이는 아이를 훔친 죄로 메이드스톤 감옥에 갇혀 있다고 했고, 네 번째 사람은 지난번 거센 바람 때 빗자루에 올라타 곧장 칼레로 날아가는 걸 봤다고 했다."], ["The fly-drivers, among whom I inquired next, were equally jocose and equally disrespectful; and the shopkeepers, not liking my appearance, generally replied, without hearing what I had to say, that they had got nothing for me.", "다음으로 물어본 합승마차 마부들도 하나같이 익살맞고 하나같이 무례했다. 가게 주인들은 내 행색이 마음에 들지 않았던지, 대개는 내 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않고 너한테 줄 건 아무것도 없다고 대꾸했다."], ["I felt more miserable and destitute than I had done at any period of my running away.", "나는 도망쳐 나온 이래 어느 때보다도 비참하고 막막한 기분이 들었다."], ["My money was all gone, I had nothing left to dispose of; I was hungry, thirsty, and worn out; and seemed as distant from my end as if I had remained in London.", "돈은 다 떨어졌고, 처분할 것이라곤 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다. 배고프고, 목마르고, 기진맥진했다. 그리고 마치 런던에 그대로 머물러 있었던 것처럼, 목적지가 아득히 멀게만 느껴졌다."]], [["The morning had worn away in these inquiries, and I was sitting on the step of an empty shop at a street corner, near the market-place, deliberating upon wandering towards those other places which had been mentioned, when a fly-driver, coming by with his carriage, dropped a horsecloth.", "이렇게 묻고 다니는 사이 아침나절이 다 가 버렸고, 나는 장터 근처 길모퉁이의 빈 가게 계단에 앉아, 사람들이 말한 그 다른 곳들로 발길을 옮겨 볼까 궁리하고 있었다. 그때 합승마차 한 대가 지나가다가 마부가 말 덮개를 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Something good-natured in the man’s face, as I handed it up, encouraged me to ask him if he could tell me where Miss Trotwood lived; though I had asked the question so often, that it almost died upon my lips.", "내가 그것을 집어 건네자, 그 사내의 얼굴에 어린 어딘가 선량한 기색에 용기를 얻어, 나는 혹시 트롯우드 양이 어디 사는지 아느냐고 물어보았다. 워낙 여러 번 던진 질문이라, 그 말이 입술에서 거의 사그라들 지경이었지만."]], [["‘Trotwood,’ said he.", "\"트롯우드라.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Let me see.", "\"어디 보자."], ["I know the name, too. Old lady?’", "그 이름은 나도 알지. 나이 든 부인인가?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ I said, ‘rather.’", "\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"꽤 그러세요.\""]], [["‘Pretty stiff in the back?’ said he, making himself upright.", "\"등이 꽤 꼿꼿하고?\" 그가 몸을 똑바로 세워 보이며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ I said.", "\"네.\""], ["‘I should think it very likely.’", "내가 말했다. \"아마 그러실 거예요.\""]], [["‘Carries a bag?’ said he--‘bag with a good deal of room in it--is gruffish, and comes down upon you, sharp?’", "\"가방을 들고 다니지?\" 그가 말했다. \"속이 넉넉한 가방 말이야 — 좀 무뚝뚝하고, 사람을 매섭게 몰아붙이고?\""]], [["My heart sank within me as I acknowledged the undoubted accuracy of this description.", "이 묘사가 의심할 여지 없이 정확하다는 것을 인정하면서, 내 가슴은 철렁 내려앉았다."]], [["‘Why then, I tell you what,’ said he.", "\"그렇다면 말이지, 내 일러 주마.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘If you go up there,’ pointing with his whip towards the heights, ‘and keep right on till you come to some houses facing the sea, I think you’ll hear of her.", "\"저 위로 올라가서,\" 채찍으로 언덕배기를 가리키며 그가 말했다. \"바다를 마주 보고 선 집들이 나올 때까지 곧장 가면, 거기서 그분 소식을 들을 수 있을 게다."], ["My opinion is she won’t stand anything, so here’s a penny for you.’", "내 생각에 그분은 아무것도 그냥 봐주는 법이 없을 테니, 옜다, 너 일 페니 가져라.\""]], [["I accepted the gift thankfully, and bought a loaf with it.", "나는 고맙게 그 선물을 받아 그것으로 빵 한 덩이를 샀다."], ["Dispatching this refreshment by the way, I went in the direction my friend had indicated, and walked on a good distance without coming to the houses he had mentioned.", "이 요깃거리를 가는 길에 해치우며, 나는 그 사람이 일러 준 방향으로 한참을 걸었지만 그가 말한 집들은 좀처럼 나오지 않았다."], ["At length I saw some before me; and approaching them, went into a little shop (it was what we used to call a general shop, at home), and inquired if they could have the goodness to tell me where Miss Trotwood lived.", "마침내 저 앞에 집 몇 채가 보였고, 가까이 다가가 작은 가게로 들어갔다(우리 고향에서 흔히 만물상이라고 부르던 곳이었다). 그러고는 혹시 트롯우드 양이 어디 사는지 일러 주실 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["I addressed myself to a man behind the counter, who was weighing some rice for a young woman; but the latter, taking the inquiry to herself, turned round quickly.", "나는 계산대 뒤에 있는 남자에게 말을 건넸는데, 그는 한 젊은 여자에게 줄 쌀을 달고 있었다. 그런데 그 여자가 이 질문을 자기에게 한 것으로 여기고는 홱 돌아섰다."]], [["‘My mistress?’ she said.", "\"우리 마님 말이니?\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘What do you want with her, boy?’", "\"그분한테 무슨 볼일이 있어, 얘?\""]], [["‘I want,’ I replied, ‘to speak to her, if you please.’", "\"저는,\" 내가 대답했다. \"괜찮으시다면, 그분께 말씀을 좀 드리고 싶어서요.\""]], [["‘To beg of her, you mean,’ retorted the damsel.", "\"구걸하려는 거겠지.\" 그 처녀가 쏘아붙였다."]], [["‘No,’ I said, ‘indeed.’ But suddenly remembering that in truth I came for no other purpose, I held my peace in confusion, and felt my face burn.", "\"아니에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"정말이에요.\" 하지만 사실 내가 다른 목적으로 온 게 아니라는 것이 문득 떠올라, 나는 당황한 채 입을 다물었고, 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["My aunt’s handmaid, as I supposed she was from what she had said, put her rice in a little basket and walked out of the shop; telling me that I could follow her, if I wanted to know where Miss Trotwood lived.", "말투로 미루어 고모님네 하녀이지 싶은 그 여자는, 쌀을 작은 바구니에 담더니 가게 밖으로 걸어 나가며, 트롯우드 양이 어디 사는지 알고 싶으면 자기를 따라오라고 했다."], ["I needed no second permission; though I was by this time in such a state of consternation and agitation, that my legs shook under me.", "두 번 권할 것도 없었다. 그 무렵 나는 어찌나 놀라고 떨렸던지 다리가 후들거릴 지경이었지만."], ["I followed the young woman, and we soon came to a very neat little cottage with cheerful bow-windows: in front of it, a small square gravelled court or garden full of flowers, carefully tended, and smelling deliciously.", "나는 그 젊은 여자를 따라갔고, 우리는 곧 산뜻한 활꼴 창이 달린 아주 아담한 작은 오두막에 이르렀다. 그 앞에는 자그마한 네모난 자갈 마당, 아니 정원이 있었는데, 정성껏 가꾼 꽃이 가득해서 향기가 그윽했다."]], [["‘This is Miss Trotwood’s,’ said the young woman.", "\"여기가 트롯우드 양 댁이야.\" 그 젊은 여자가 말했다. \"자, 이제 알았지."], ["‘Now you know; and that’s all I have got to say.’ With which words she hurried into the house, as if to shake off the responsibility of my appearance; and left me standing at the garden-gate, looking disconsolately over the top of it towards the parlour window, where a muslin curtain partly undrawn in the middle, a large round green screen or fan fastened on to the windowsill, a small table, and a great chair, suggested to me that my aunt might be at that moment seated in awful state.", "내가 할 말은 이게 다야.\" 그러고는 마치 내가 나타난 책임을 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 서둘러 집 안으로 들어가 버렸고, 나는 정원 문 앞에 서서, 그 너머로 거실 창문 쪽을 시무룩하게 바라보았다. 가운데가 반쯤 걷힌 모슬린 커튼과, 창턱에 고정된 크고 둥근 초록색 가리개, 작은 탁자, 커다란 의자가, 어쩌면 지금 이 순간 고모님이 위풍당당하게 거기 앉아 계실지도 모른다는 생각을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["My shoes were by this time in a woeful condition.", "이 무렵 내 신발은 처참한 꼴이었다."], ["The soles had shed themselves bit by bit, and the upper leathers had broken and burst until the very shape and form of shoes had departed from them.", "밑창은 야금야금 떨어져 나갔고, 가죽 윗부분은 터지고 갈라져 신발의 형체조차 사라지고 없었다."], ["My hat (which had served me for a night-cap, too) was so crushed and bent, that no old battered handleless saucepan on a dunghill need have been ashamed to vie with it.", "모자는(밤에는 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자 노릇도 했다) 어찌나 찌그러지고 뒤틀렸던지, 거름더미 위에 나뒹구는 손잡이 떨어진 낡은 냄비라도 그것과 견주는 걸 부끄러워할 필요는 없을 정도였다."], ["My shirt and trousers, stained with heat, dew, grass, and the Kentish soil on which I had slept--and torn besides--might have frightened the birds from my aunt’s garden, as I stood at the gate.", "더위와 이슬과 풀물, 그리고 내가 자고 일어난 켄트의 흙으로 얼룩진 데다 군데군데 찢어지기까지 한 내 셔츠와 바지는, 내가 정원 문 앞에 선 채로 고모님의 정원에서 새들을 쫓아 버렸을지도 모를 지경이었다."], ["My hair had known no comb or brush since I left London.", "머리카락은 런던을 떠난 뒤로 빗이나 솔이라곤 구경도 못 했다."], ["My face, neck, and hands, from unaccustomed exposure to the air and sun, were burnt to a berry-brown.", "얼굴과 목과 손은, 익숙지 않게 바깥 공기와 햇볕에 노출된 탓에 산딸기처럼 가무잡잡하게 타 있었다."], ["From head to foot I was powdered almost as white with chalk and dust, as if I had come out of a lime-kiln.", "머리끝부터 발끝까지 백악과 먼지를 어찌나 뒤집어썼던지 거의 허옇게 분칠을 한 듯, 마치 석회 가마에서 막 기어 나온 꼴이었다."], ["In this plight, and with a strong consciousness of it, I waited to introduce myself to, and make my first impression on, my formidable aunt.", "이런 몰골로, 그 몰골을 뼈저리게 의식하면서, 나는 나의 만만찮은 고모님께 나를 소개하고 첫인상을 남길 순간을 기다렸다."]], [["The unbroken stillness of the parlour window leading me to infer, after a while, that she was not there, I lifted up my eyes to the window above it, where I saw a florid, pleasant-looking gentleman, with a grey head, who shut up one eye in a grotesque manner, nodded his head at me several times, shook it at me as often, laughed, and went away.", "거실 창문이 줄곧 미동조차 없는 것으로 미루어 한참 만에 고모님이 거기 안 계시는구나 짐작하고, 나는 그 위층 창문으로 눈을 들어 올렸다. 거기 혈색 좋고 인상이 서글서글한, 머리가 희끗한 신사가 보였는데, 그는 괴상한 모양새로 한쪽 눈을 찡긋 감더니, 나를 향해 몇 번이나 고개를 끄덕이고, 또 그만큼이나 고개를 가로젓고는, 웃음을 터뜨리더니 사라져 버렸다."]], [["I had been discomposed enough before; but I was so much the more discomposed by this unexpected behaviour, that I was on the point of slinking off, to think how I had best proceed, when there came out of the house a lady with her handkerchief tied over her cap, and a pair of gardening gloves on her hands, wearing a gardening pocket like a toll-man’s apron, and carrying a great knife.", "그렇잖아도 잔뜩 마음이 흐트러져 있던 참인데, 이 뜻밖의 행동에 마음이 한층 더 어수선해져서, 나는 어떻게 하는 게 상책일지 곰곰이 따져 보려고 슬그머니 자리를 뜰까 하던 차였다. 그때 집에서 한 부인이 나왔다. 모자 위에 손수건을 동여매고, 두 손에는 정원용 장갑을 끼고, 통행료 받는 사람의 앞치마 같은 정원용 주머니를 두르고, 커다란 칼 하나를 든 채였다."], ["I knew her immediately to be Miss Betsey, for she came stalking out of the house exactly as my poor mother had so often described her stalking up our garden at Blunderstone Rookery.", "나는 그분이 베시 양이라는 것을 단박에 알아보았다. 가엾은 어머니가 그토록 자주 들려주던, 블런더스톤 루커리의 우리 집 정원을 성큼성큼 걸어 올라오던 바로 그 모습 그대로, 그녀는 집에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나왔기 때문이다."]], [["‘Go away!’ said Miss Betsey, shaking her head, and making a distant chop in the air with her knife.", "\"저리 가!\" 베시 양이 고개를 가로저으며, 칼로 허공을 향해 멀찍이 한 번 내리치는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"가 버려!"], ["‘Go along! No boys here!’", "여기 사내애는 안 받아!\""]], [["I watched her, with my heart at my lips, as she marched to a corner of her garden, and stooped to dig up some little root there.", "나는 가슴이 입까지 차오른 듯한 심정으로, 그녀가 정원 한쪽 구석으로 성큼성큼 가더니 거기 있는 작은 뿌리를 캐려고 몸을 숙이는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Then, without a scrap of courage, but with a great deal of desperation, I went softly in and stood beside her, touching her with my finger.", "그러고는 용기라곤 한 톨도 없이, 그러나 절박함만은 한가득 안고, 나는 살그머니 안으로 들어가 그녀 곁에 서서 손가락으로 그녀를 슬쩍 건드렸다."]], [["‘If you please, ma’am,’ I began.", "\"실례합니다, 부인.\" 내가 입을 떼었다."]], [["She started and looked up.", "그녀가 흠칫하며 고개를 들었다."]], [["‘If you please, aunt.’", "\"실례합니다, 고모님.\""]], [["‘EH?’ exclaimed Miss Betsey, in a tone of amazement I have never heard approached.", "\"어엉?\" 베시 양이 외쳤다. 내가 평생 그에 견줄 만한 것조차 들어 본 적 없는 경악에 찬 어조였다."]], [["‘If you please, aunt, I am your nephew.’", "\"실례지만, 고모님, 저는 고모님의 조카예요.\""]], [["‘Oh, Lord!’ said my aunt.", "\"아이고, 맙소사!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["And sat flat down in the garden-path.", "그러고는 정원 오솔길에 털썩 주저앉았다."]], [["‘I am David Copperfield, of Blunderstone, in Suffolk--where you came, on the night when I was born, and saw my dear mama.", "\"저는 데이비드 코퍼필드예요. 서퍽주 블런더스톤에서 왔어요 — 제가 태어나던 날 밤에 고모님이 오셔서, 제 사랑하는 엄마를 만나셨던 그곳이요."], ["I have been very unhappy since she died.", "엄마가 돌아가신 뒤로 저는 무척 불행했어요."], ["I have been slighted, and taught nothing, and thrown upon myself, and put to work not fit for me.", "천대받고, 아무것도 못 배우고, 혼자 내팽개쳐지고, 저한테 맞지도 않는 일에 내몰렸어요. 그래서 고모님께 도망쳐 온 거예요. 길을 처음 나설 때 가진 걸 다 털리고, 여기까지 줄곧 걸어왔어요."], ["It made me run away to you.", "여정을 시작한 뒤로 침대에서 자 본 적이 한 번도 없어요.\""], ["I was robbed at first setting out, and have walked all the way, and have never slept in a bed since I began the journey.’ Here my self-support gave way all at once; and with a movement of my hands, intended to show her my ragged state, and call it to witness that I had suffered something, I broke into a passion of crying, which I suppose had been pent up within me all the week.", "여기서 나를 지탱하던 힘이 한꺼번에 무너져 내렸다. 나는 내 너덜너덜한 몰골을 그녀에게 보여 주려는, 그리고 내가 무언가 고생을 했음을 그것으로 증언하려는 듯 두 손을 한 번 휘젓고는, 한바탕 격렬한 울음을 터뜨렸다. 일주일 내내 가슴속에 꾹꾹 눌러 담아 두었던 울음이지 싶었다."]], [["My aunt, with every sort of expression but wonder discharged from her countenance, sat on the gravel, staring at me, until I began to cry; when she got up in a great hurry, collared me, and took me into the parlour.", "고모님은 놀라움을 빼고는 온갖 표정이 다 사라진 얼굴로, 자갈밭에 주저앉아 나를 빤히 바라보다가, 내가 울음을 터뜨리자 황급히 일어나 내 멱살을 잡고 거실로 데리고 들어갔다."], ["Her first proceeding there was to unlock a tall press, bring out several bottles, and pour some of the contents of each into my mouth.", "거기서 고모님이 맨 처음 한 일은 키 큰 찬장을 열어 병 몇 개를 꺼내, 그 안의 내용물을 조금씩 내 입에 부어 넣는 것이었다."], ["I think they must have been taken out at random, for I am sure I tasted aniseed water, anchovy sauce, and salad dressing.", "짐작건대 그것들은 손에 잡히는 대로 꺼낸 게 틀림없는데, 내가 아니스 향수와 멸치 소스와 샐러드 드레싱 맛을 본 게 분명하기 때문이다."], ["When she had administered these restoratives, as I was still quite hysterical, and unable to control my sobs, she put me on the sofa, with a shawl under my head, and the handkerchief from her own head under my feet, lest I should sully the cover; and then, sitting herself down behind the green fan or screen I have already mentioned, so that I could not see her face, ejaculated at intervals, ‘Mercy on us!’ letting those exclamations off like minute guns.", "이런 회복제들을 먹이고 나서도 내가 여전히 흐느낌을 가누지 못하고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하자, 고모님은 나를 소파에 눕히고 머리 밑에는 숄을 받쳐 주었으며, 내가 덮개를 더럽힐까 봐 발밑에는 자기 머리에 둘렀던 손수건을 깔아 주었다. 그러고는 앞서 말한 그 초록색 부채, 아니 가리개 뒤에 자기 몸을 앉혀 내가 자기 얼굴을 볼 수 없게 하더니, 이따금씩 \"어이구, 맙소사!\" 하고 외마디를 터뜨렸는데, 그 소리가 마치 일 분마다 한 발씩 쏘는 예포 같았다."]], [["After a time she rang the bell. ‘Janet,’ said my aunt, when her servant came in.", "얼마 뒤 고모님이 종을 울렸다. \"재닛.\" 하녀가 들어오자 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Go upstairs, give my compliments to Mr. Dick, and say I wish to speak to him.’", "\"위층에 올라가서 딕 씨께 내 안부를 전하고, 내가 얘기 좀 하고 싶다고 말씀드려라.\""]], [["Janet looked a little surprised to see me lying stiffly on the sofa (I was afraid to move lest it should be displeasing to my aunt), but went on her errand.", "재닛은 내가 소파에 뻣뻣하게 누워 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀란 눈치였지만(나는 행여 고모님 심기를 거스를까 봐 움직이기가 두려웠다), 시킨 심부름을 하러 갔다."], ["My aunt, with her hands behind her, walked up and down the room, until the gentleman who had squinted at me from the upper window came in laughing.", "고모님은 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌린 채 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었고, 그러다 위층 창문에서 나에게 곁눈질하던 그 신사가 웃으며 들어왔다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"딕 씨.\""], ["Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘don’t be a fool, because nobody can be more discreet than you can, when you choose.", "고모님이 말했다. \"바보처럼 굴지 마세요. 마음만 먹으면 댁만큼 분별 있는 사람도 없잖아요."], ["We all know that.", "그건 우리 모두 알아요."], ["So don’t be a fool, whatever you are.’", "그러니 당신이 어떤 사람이든 간에, 바보처럼 굴진 마세요.\""]], [["The gentleman was serious immediately, and looked at me, I thought, as if he would entreat me to say nothing about the window.", "신사는 곧바로 진지해지더니, 내가 보기엔 마치 그 창문 일은 아무 말 말아 달라고 나에게 애원하는 듯한 표정으로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Mr. Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘you have heard me mention David Copperfield?", "\"딕 씨.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["Now don’t pretend not to have a memory, because you and I know better.’", "\"내가 데이비드 코퍼필드 얘기 하는 거 들으셨죠? 자, 기억 안 나는 척하지 마세요. 당신이나 나나 다 아는 사실이니까.\""]], [["‘David Copperfield?’ said Mr. Dick, who did not appear to me to remember much about it.", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드요?\" 딕 씨가 말했는데, 내가 보기엔 그것에 대해 별로 기억하는 것 같지 않았다."], ["‘David Copperfield?", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드요?"], ["Oh yes, to be sure.", "아, 그럼요, 물론이죠."], ["David, certainly.’", "데이비드, 당연하죠.\""]], [["‘Well,’ said my aunt, ‘this is his boy--his son.", "\"그런데,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 애가 그 사람 아들이에요 — 그 사람 자식이요."], ["He would be as like his father as it’s possible to be, if he was not so like his mother, too.’", "어쩌면 이렇게 제 어미를 빼닮지만 않았어도, 영락없이 제 아비랑 똑같았을 텐데.\""]], [["‘His son?’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"그 사람 아들이라고요?\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["‘David’s son? Indeed!’", "\"데이비드의 아들이요? 저런!\""]], [["‘Yes,’ pursued my aunt, ‘and he has done a pretty piece of business.", "\"그래요.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"게다가 이 애가 일을 아주 멋들어지게 저질렀지 뭐예요."], ["He has run away. Ah!", "글쎄 도망을 쳤다잖아요. 아!"], ["His sister, Betsey Trotwood, never would have run away.’ My aunt shook her head firmly, confident in the character and behaviour of the girl who never was born.", "그 애 누이, 베시 트롯우드라면 절대 도망치지 않았을 텐데.\" 고모님은 단호하게 고개를 가로저었다. 끝내 태어나지 않은 그 여자아이의 됨됨이와 행실을 굳게 확신하면서."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you think she wouldn’t have run away?’ said Mr. Dick.", "그 애는 도망치지 않았을 거라고 생각하세요?\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Bless and save the man,’ exclaimed my aunt, sharply, ‘how he talks!", "\"하느님 맙소사, 이 양반 좀 봐.\" 고모님이 매섭게 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 소리람!"], ["Don’t I know she wouldn’t? She would have lived with her god-mother, and we should have been devoted to one another.", "그 애가 도망 안 쳤을 거란 걸 내가 모를까 봐요? 그 애는 자기 대모랑 같이 살았을 거고, 우리 둘은 서로에게 끔찍이도 정을 쏟았을 거예요."], ["Where, in the name of wonder, should his sister, Betsey Trotwood, have run from, or to?’", "도대체, 세상에, 그 애 누이 베시 트롯우드가 어디서, 어디로 도망을 친단 말이에요?\""]], [["‘Nowhere,’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"아무 데도요.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well then,’ returned my aunt, softened by the reply, ‘how can you pretend to be wool-gathering, Dick, when you are as sharp as a surgeon’s lancet?", "\"그럼 그렇지.\" 고모님은 그 대답에 마음이 누그러져서 말했다. \"그런데 딕, 당신은 외과의의 침처럼 예리한 사람이면서, 어쩜 그렇게 정신 나간 척을 한답니까?"], ["Now, here you see young David Copperfield, and the question I put to you is, what shall I do with him?’", "자, 여기 어린 데이비드 코퍼필드가 있어요. 내가 당신에게 묻고 싶은 건, 내가 이 애를 어떻게 하면 좋겠느냐는 거예요.\""]], [["‘What shall you do with him?’ said Mr.", "\"이 애를 어떻게 하느냐고요?\""], ["Dick, feebly, scratching his head. ‘Oh! do with him?’", "딕 씨가 머리를 긁적이며 맥없이 말했다. \"오! 이 애를 어떻게 하느냐고요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said my aunt, with a grave look, and her forefinger held up. ‘Come!", "\"그래요.\" 고모님이 자못 진지한 표정으로, 집게손가락을 치켜들고 말했다. \"어서요!"], ["I want some very sound advice.’", "정말로 쓸 만한 조언이 필요해요.\""]], [["‘Why, if I was you,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄, 내가 댁이라면,\" 딕 씨가 골똘히 생각하며, 멍하니 나를 바라보면서 말했다."], ["Dick, considering, and looking vacantly at me, ‘I should--’ The contemplation of me seemed to inspire him with a sudden idea, and he added, briskly, ‘I should wash him!’", "\"나라면—\" 나를 가만히 들여다보던 것이 그에게 문득 어떤 생각을 불어넣은 듯, 그는 활기차게 덧붙였다. \"나라면 이 애를 씻기겠어요!\""]], [["‘Janet,’ said my aunt, turning round with a quiet triumph, which I did not then understand, ‘Mr.", "\"재닛.\" 고모님이, 그때는 내가 미처 이해하지 못한 어떤 조용한 의기양양함을 띠고 돌아서며 말했다."], ["Dick sets us all right.", "\"딕 씨가 우리 모두를 바로잡아 주는구나."], ["Heat the bath!’", "목욕물을 데워라!\""]], [["Although I was deeply interested in this dialogue, I could not help observing my aunt, Mr. Dick, and Janet, while it was in progress, and completing a survey I had already been engaged in making of the room.", "이 대화에 깊이 빠져 있긴 했지만, 나는 그 와중에도 고모님과 딕 씨와 재닛을 유심히 살피지 않을 수 없었고, 또 진작부터 하고 있던 방 안 둘러보기도 마저 끝냈다."]], [["My aunt was a tall, hard-featured lady, but by no means ill-looking.", "고모님은 키가 크고 이목구비가 단단한 부인이었으나, 결코 못생긴 것은 아니었다."], ["There was an inflexibility in her face, in her voice, in her gait and carriage, amply sufficient to account for the effect she had made upon a gentle creature like my mother; but her features were rather handsome than otherwise, though unbending and austere.", "그 얼굴에도, 목소리에도, 걸음걸이와 몸가짐에도 굽힐 줄 모르는 강단이 서려 있어서, 우리 어머니처럼 여린 사람에게 그분이 끼친 영향력을 넉넉히 설명하고도 남았다. 하지만 그 이목구비는, 비록 빳빳하고 근엄하기는 해도, 못났다기보다 오히려 잘난 편이었다."], ["I particularly noticed that she had a very quick, bright eye.", "나는 특히 그분의 눈이 무척 빠르고 초롱초롱하다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Her hair, which was grey, was arranged in two plain divisions, under what I believe would be called a mob-cap; I mean a cap, much more common then than now, with side-pieces fastening under the chin.", "잿빛 머리카락은 두 갈래로 단정하게 가른 채, 내 생각엔 머리쓰개라고 부를 만한 것 아래로 정리되어 있었다. 그러니까 지금보다 그 시절에 훨씬 흔하던, 양옆 자락을 턱밑에서 여미는 모자 말이다."], ["Her dress was of a lavender colour, and perfectly neat; but scantily made, as if she desired to be as little encumbered as possible.", "옷은 연보라색에 더없이 단정했으나, 되도록 거추장스러운 게 없기를 바라기라도 한 듯 천을 아껴 지은 것이었다."], ["I remember that I thought it, in form, more like a riding-habit with the superfluous skirt cut off, than anything else.", "그 모양새가 무엇보다 군더더기 치맛자락을 잘라 낸 승마복에 가깝다고 생각했던 기억이 난다."], ["She wore at her side a gentleman’s gold watch, if I might judge from its size and make, with an appropriate chain and seals; she had some linen at her throat not unlike a shirt-collar, and things at her wrists like little shirt-wristbands.", "옆구리에는 신사용 금시계를 차고 있었는데, 크기와 만듦새로 미루어 보건대 그러했고, 거기 어울리는 줄과 인장도 달려 있었다. 목에는 셔츠 깃과 다르지 않은 아마포를 둘렀고, 손목에는 작은 셔츠 소맷부리 같은 것을 차고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Dick, as I have already said, was grey-headed, and florid: I should have said all about him, in saying so, had not his head been curiously bowed--not by age; it reminded me of one of Mr.", "딕 씨는, 앞서 말했듯이 머리가 희끗하고 혈색이 좋았다. 그것만 말하면 그에 대해 할 말을 다 한 셈이었을 텐데, 그의 머리가 묘하게 수그러져 있지만 않았더라도 그랬을 것이다 — 나이 탓은 아니었다."], ["Creakle’s boys’ heads after a beating--and his grey eyes prominent and large, with a strange kind of watery brightness in them that made me, in combination with his vacant manner, his submission to my aunt, and his childish delight when she praised him, suspect him of being a little mad; though, if he were mad, how he came to be there puzzled me extremely.", "그 모습은 매를 맞은 뒤의 크리클 씨네 학생들 머리통을 떠올리게 했다 — 게다가 그의 잿빛 눈은 툭 불거지고 큼직했으며, 그 안에는 묘하게 촉촉한 광채가 어려 있었다. 그 광채가 그의 멍한 태도, 고모님에게 고분고분 따르는 모습, 고모님이 칭찬해 줄 때의 어린애 같은 기쁨과 어우러져, 나로 하여금 그가 좀 정신이 이상한 게 아닌가 의심하게 만들었다. 하지만 그가 정말 미친 사람이라면 어떻게 여기 와 있게 된 건지가 나를 몹시 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["He was dressed like any other ordinary gentleman, in a loose grey morning coat and waistcoat, and white trousers; and had his watch in his fob, and his money in his pockets: which he rattled as if he were very proud of it.", "그는 여느 평범한 신사와 다름없이, 헐렁한 잿빛 실내 외투에 조끼, 흰 바지를 차려입었고, 시계는 호주머니에, 돈은 주머니에 넣어 두었는데, 그 돈을 마치 무척이나 자랑스러워하듯 짤랑짤랑 흔들어 댔다."]], [["Janet was a pretty blooming girl, of about nineteen or twenty, and a perfect picture of neatness.", "재닛은 열아홉이나 스무 살쯤 된 예쁘장하고 발랄한 처녀로, 단정함의 본보기였다."], ["Though I made no further observation of her at the moment, I may mention here what I did not discover until afterwards, namely, that she was one of a series of protegees whom my aunt had taken into her service expressly to educate in a renouncement of mankind, and who had generally completed their abjuration by marrying the baker.", "그 순간엔 그녀를 더 눈여겨보지 않았지만, 여기서 나중에야 알게 된 사실 하나를 말해 두어야겠다. 그러니까 그녀는, 고모님이 일부러 인류를 멀리하는 법을 가르치려고 거두어들인 일련의 보호자녀 중 하나였는데, 이들은 대개 빵집 주인과 결혼함으로써 그 결심을 매듭짓곤 했다."]], [["The room was as neat as Janet or my aunt.", "방은 재닛이나 고모님만큼이나 단정했다."], ["As I laid down my pen, a moment since, to think of it, the air from the sea came blowing in again, mixed with the perfume of the flowers; and I saw the old-fashioned furniture brightly rubbed and polished, my aunt’s inviolable chair and table by the round green fan in the bow-window, the drugget-covered carpet, the cat, the kettle-holder, the two canaries, the old china, the punchbowl full of dried rose-leaves, the tall press guarding all sorts of bottles and pots, and, wonderfully out of keeping with the rest, my dusty self upon the sofa, taking note of everything.", "조금 전, 그 방을 떠올리려고 펜을 내려놓는데, 또다시 바다에서 불어오는 바람이 꽃향기를 실어 안으로 밀려들었다. 나는 환하게 닦고 윤을 낸 고풍스러운 가구며, 활꼴 창가의 둥근 초록 부채 곁에 놓인, 누구도 범할 수 없는 고모님의 의자와 탁자며, 거친 모직물을 깐 카펫이며, 고양이며, 주전자 받침이며, 카나리아 두 마리며, 오래된 도자기며, 말린 장미 잎이 가득한 펀치 그릇이며, 온갖 병과 단지를 지키고 선 키 큰 찬장이며, 그리고 나머지 풍경과는 놀랍도록 어울리지 않게, 소파 위에 앉아 모든 것을 눈여겨보던 먼지투성이의 나 자신을 보았다."]], [["Janet had gone away to get the bath ready, when my aunt, to my great alarm, became in one moment rigid with indignation, and had hardly voice to cry out, ‘Janet! Donkeys!’", "재닛이 목욕물을 준비하러 나간 사이, 무척이나 놀랍게도, 고모님은 한순간에 분노로 뻣뻣하게 굳더니, 가까스로 목소리를 짜내어 외쳤다. \"재닛! 당나귀들이야!\""]], [["Upon which, Janet came running up the stairs as if the house were in flames, darted out on a little piece of green in front, and warned off two saddle-donkeys, lady-ridden, that had presumed to set hoof upon it; while my aunt, rushing out of the house, seized the bridle of a third animal laden with a bestriding child, turned him, led him forth from those sacred precincts, and boxed the ears of the unlucky urchin in attendance who had dared to profane that hallowed ground.", "그러자 재닛이 집에 불이라도 난 듯 계단을 뛰어 올라와, 앞쪽의 작은 풀밭으로 쏜살같이 달려 나가, 감히 거기에 발굽을 들이민, 부인들이 올라탄 안장 얹은 당나귀 두 마리를 쫓아 버렸다. 그사이 고모님은 집에서 뛰쳐나가, 어린아이를 등에 태운 세 번째 짐승의 고삐를 움켜쥐고는, 녀석을 돌려세워 그 신성한 구역에서 끌어낸 다음, 그 거룩한 땅을 더럽힌 시중꾼 불량배 녀석의 따귀를 갈겼다."]], [["To this hour I don’t know whether my aunt had any lawful right of way over that patch of green; but she had settled it in her own mind that she had, and it was all the same to her.", "지금 이 순간까지도 나는 고모님이 그 풀밭에 대해 어떤 합법적인 통행권을 가졌는지 어쨌는지 알지 못한다. 하지만 고모님은 자기 마음속으로 그렇다고 못 박아 두었고, 그녀에게는 그게 다 매한가지였다."], ["The one great outrage of her life, demanding to be constantly avenged, was the passage of a donkey over that immaculate spot.", "평생 끊임없이 복수해야 마땅한, 그녀 인생의 하나뿐인 크나큰 분노거리는, 그 티 없이 깨끗한 자리를 당나귀가 지나가는 일이었다."], ["In whatever occupation she was engaged, however interesting to her the conversation in which she was taking part, a donkey turned the current of her ideas in a moment, and she was upon him straight.", "무슨 일에 몰두해 있든, 자기가 한몫 끼어든 대화가 아무리 흥미롭든, 당나귀 한 마리면 순식간에 그녀의 생각의 물줄기를 돌려놓았고, 그녀는 곧장 녀석에게 달려들었다."], ["Jugs of water, and watering-pots, were kept in secret places ready to be discharged on the offending boys; sticks were laid in ambush behind the door; sallies were made at all hours; and incessant war prevailed.", "물 주전자와 물뿌리개가 비밀스러운 곳곳에 숨겨져 있다가 못된 짓을 한 아이들에게 쏟아부어졌고, 막대기들이 문 뒤에 매복해 있었으며, 시도 때도 없이 기습이 감행되어, 끊임없는 전쟁이 벌어졌다."], ["Perhaps this was an agreeable excitement to the donkey-boys; or perhaps the more sagacious of the donkeys, understanding how the case stood, delighted with constitutional obstinacy in coming that way.", "어쩌면 이것이 당나귀 모는 아이들에게는 즐거운 자극거리였는지도 모른다. 아니면 사정을 좀 더 영리하게 알아챈 당나귀들이, 타고난 고집으로 일부러 그 길로 오는 것을 즐겼는지도 모를 일이다."], ["I only know that there were three alarms before the bath was ready; and that on the occasion of the last and most desperate of all, I saw my aunt engage, single-handed, with a sandy-headed lad of fifteen, and bump his sandy head against her own gate, before he seemed to comprehend what was the matter.", "내가 아는 것이라곤, 목욕물이 준비되기까지 세 번이나 비상사태가 벌어졌다는 것, 그리고 그 마지막이자 가장 격렬했던 비상사태 때, 고모님이 모래빛 머리의 열다섯 살짜리 녀석을 혼자서 상대해, 그 녀석이 무슨 영문인지 미처 깨닫기도 전에 모래빛 머리통을 자기 집 대문에 쾅 박는 것을 내가 보았다는 것뿐이다. 이런 방해들이 나에게는 더더욱 우스꽝스러웠다."], ["These interruptions were of the more ridiculous to me, because she was giving me broth out of a table-spoon at the time (having firmly persuaded herself that I was actually starving, and must receive nourishment at first in very small quantities), and, while my mouth was yet open to receive the spoon, she would put it back into the basin, cry ‘Janet!", "그때 고모님이 나에게 큰 숟가락으로 수프를 떠먹이던 참이었기 때문이다(내가 정말로 굶주리고 있어서 처음에는 아주 조금씩만 영양분을 줘야 한다고 그녀는 굳게 믿어 버린 터였다). 그러다 내 입이 아직 숟가락을 받으려고 벌어져 있는데도, 고모님은 그 숟가락을 도로 그릇에 놓고는 \"재닛! 당나귀들이야!\""], ["Donkeys!’ and go out to the assault.", "하고 외치며 공격에 나서곤 했다."]], [["The bath was a great comfort.", "목욕은 큰 위안이 되었다."], ["For I began to be sensible of acute pains in my limbs from lying out in the fields, and was now so tired and low that I could hardly keep myself awake for five minutes together.", "들판에서 노숙한 탓에 팔다리가 욱신욱신 쑤셔 오기 시작했고, 게다가 이제는 너무 지치고 기운이 빠져서 오 분도 채 깨어 있기가 힘들 지경이었으니까."], ["When I had bathed, they (I mean my aunt and Janet) enrobed me in a shirt and a pair of trousers belonging to Mr.", "목욕을 마치자 그들은(고모님과 재닛 말이다) 딕 씨의 셔츠와 바지를 나에게 입히고, 큼지막한 숄로 두세 겹 칭칭 동여맸다."], ["Dick, and tied me up in two or three great shawls. What sort of bundle I looked like, I don’t know, but I felt a very hot one.", "내가 무슨 보따리 꼴로 보였는지는 모르겠지만, 어쨌든 무척 더운 보따리였던 것만은 분명하다."], ["Feeling also very faint and drowsy, I soon lay down on the sofa again and fell asleep.", "게다가 몹시 어질어질하고 졸려서, 나는 곧 다시 소파에 누워 잠이 들고 말았다."]], [["It might have been a dream, originating in the fancy which had occupied my mind so long, but I awoke with the impression that my aunt had come and bent over me, and had put my hair away from my face, and laid my head more comfortably, and had then stood looking at me.", "그건 어쩌면, 그토록 오래 내 마음을 사로잡고 있던 그 상상에서 비롯된 꿈이었는지도 모른다. 하지만 나는 고모님이 다가와 내 위로 몸을 굽히고, 내 얼굴에서 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겨 주고, 내 머리를 더 편안하게 받쳐 준 다음, 가만히 서서 나를 바라보았다는 인상을 받으며 잠에서 깼다. \"예쁜 녀석\""], ["The words, ‘Pretty fellow,’ or ‘Poor fellow,’ seemed to be in my ears, too; but certainly there was nothing else, when I awoke, to lead me to believe that they had been uttered by my aunt, who sat in the bow-window gazing at the sea from behind the green fan, which was mounted on a kind of swivel, and turned any way.", "또는 \"가엾은 녀석\"이라는 말도 내 귀에 맴도는 것 같았다. 하지만 깨어났을 때, 그 말을 고모님이 했다고 믿게 할 만한 것은 그 밖에 아무것도 없었다. 고모님은 활꼴 창가에 앉아, 일종의 회전축에 달려 어느 쪽으로든 돌아가는 그 초록 부채 뒤에서 바다를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["We dined soon after I awoke, off a roast fowl and a pudding; I sitting at table, not unlike a trussed bird myself, and moving my arms with considerable difficulty.", "내가 깨어난 직후 우리는 구운 닭고기와 푸딩으로 저녁을 먹었다. 나는 식탁에 앉았는데, 묶어 놓은 새 한 마리와 다를 바 없는 꼴로, 팔을 움직이기조차 여간 힘들지 않았다."], ["But as my aunt had swathed me up, I made no complaint of being inconvenienced.", "하지만 고모님이 나를 칭칭 싸매 놓은 터라, 나는 불편하다는 불평 한마디 하지 않았다."], ["All this time I was deeply anxious to know what she was going to do with me; but she took her dinner in profound silence, except when she occasionally fixed her eyes on me sitting opposite, and said, ‘Mercy upon us!’ which did not by any means relieve my anxiety.", "그러는 내내 나는 고모님이 나를 어떻게 할 작정인지 무척이나 알고 싶었다. 하지만 고모님은 깊은 침묵 속에 식사를 했고, 다만 이따금씩 맞은편에 앉은 나에게 눈길을 고정하고는 \"어이구, 맙소사!\" 하고 내뱉을 뿐이었는데, 그것은 내 불안을 결코 조금도 덜어 주지 못했다."]], [["The cloth being drawn, and some sherry put upon the table (of which I had a glass), my aunt sent up for Mr.", "식탁보가 걷히고 셰리주가 식탁에 올려진 뒤(나도 한 잔 마셨다), 고모님은 다시 딕 씨를 불러오라고 사람을 올려보냈다."], ["Dick again, who joined us, and looked as wise as he could when she requested him to attend to my story, which she elicited from me, gradually, by a course of questions.", "딕 씨가 합류하자, 고모님은 그에게 내 이야기에 귀 기울여 달라고 청했고, 잇따른 질문을 통해 그 이야기를 나에게서 차근차근 끌어냈는데, 그러는 동안 딕 씨는 할 수 있는 한 현명해 보이는 표정을 지었다."], ["During my recital, she kept her eyes on Mr. Dick, who I thought would have gone to sleep but for that, and who, whensoever he lapsed into a smile, was checked by a frown from my aunt.", "내가 이야기하는 동안 고모님은 줄곧 딕 씨에게 눈길을 두었는데, 그게 아니었다면 딕 씨는 잠들어 버렸지 싶었다. 게다가 그는 어쩌다 슬며시 미소를 지을라치면 어김없이 고모님의 찌푸린 눈살에 제지당했다."]], [["‘Whatever possessed that poor unfortunate Baby, that she must go and be married again,’ said my aunt, when I had finished, ‘I can’t conceive.’", "\"그 가엾고 불행한 아기가, 도대체 무슨 마가 끼었기에 가서 또 결혼을 했단 말인지,\" 내 이야기가 끝나자 고모님이 말했다. \"나는 도무지 알 수가 없네.\""]], [["‘Perhaps she fell in love with her second husband,’ Mr. Dick suggested.", "\"어쩌면 두 번째 남편을 사랑하게 됐던 거겠죠.\" 딕 씨가 넌지시 말했다."]], [["‘Fell in love!’ repeated my aunt.", "\"사랑에 빠져!\" 고모님이 되받았다."], ["‘What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 소리예요?"], ["What business had she to do it?’", "그 애가 무슨 권리로 그런 짓을 해요?\""]], [["‘Perhaps,’ Mr.", "\"어쩌면,\""], ["Dick simpered, after thinking a little, ‘she did it for pleasure.’", "딕 씨가 잠시 생각하더니 헤벌쭉 웃으며 말했다. \"재미 삼아 그랬는지도 모르죠.\""]], [["‘Pleasure, indeed!’ replied my aunt.", "\"재미라니, 참도!\" 고모님이 대꾸했다."], ["‘A mighty pleasure for the poor Baby to fix her simple faith upon any dog of a fellow, certain to ill-use her in some way or other.", "\"그 가엾은 아기가 이런저런 식으로 자기를 못살게 굴 게 뻔한, 개 같은 작자 따위에게 그 순진한 믿음을 갖다 바친 게 무슨 대단한 재밋거리란 말이에요."], ["What did she propose to herself, I should like to know!", "도대체 그 애가 자기 딴엔 뭘 어쩌자는 속셈이었는지, 정말 알고 싶네요!"], ["She had had one husband. She had seen David Copperfield out of the world, who was always running after wax dolls from his cradle.", "그 애는 이미 남편이 하나 있었어요. 요람에 누웠을 적부터 늘 밀랍 인형이나 졸졸 쫓아다니던 데이비드 코퍼필드를 저세상으로 떠나보냈고요."], ["She had got a baby--oh, there were a pair of babies when she gave birth to this child sitting here, that Friday night!--and what more did she want?’", "게다가 아기도 하나 있었어요 — 아니, 그 금요일 밤에 여기 앉은 이 애를 낳았을 때, 거기엔 아기가 한 쌍이나 있었지! — 그런데 도대체 뭘 더 바란 거예요?\""]], [["Mr. Dick secretly shook his head at me, as if he thought there was no getting over this.", "딕 씨가 나를 향해 슬그머니 고개를 가로저었다. 마치 이건 도무지 어쩔 도리가 없는 일이라고 여기는 듯이."]], [["‘She couldn’t even have a baby like anybody else,’ said my aunt.", "\"그 애는 남들처럼 평범하게 아기 하나 낳는 것조차 못 했다니까요.\""], ["‘Where was this child’s sister, Betsey Trotwood?", "고모님이 말했다. \"이 애의 누이 베시 트롯우드는 어디 있느냐고요?"], ["Not forthcoming.", "코빼기도 안 비쳤어요."], ["Don’t tell me!’", "더는 말도 마세요!\""]], [["Mr. Dick seemed quite frightened.", "딕 씨는 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯했다."]], [["‘That little man of a doctor, with his head on one side,’ said my aunt, ‘Jellips, or whatever his name was, what was he about?", "\"머리를 한쪽으로 갸우뚱 기울인 그 자그마한 의사 양반,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"젤립스인가 뭔가 하는 이름이었는데, 그 작자는 도대체 뭘 하고 있었답니까?"], ["All he could do, was to say to me, like a robin redbreast--as he is--“It’s a boy.” A boy!", "그자가 한 거라곤 고작, 꼭 울새처럼 — 실제로 그렇게 생겼지만 — 나한테 이렇게 말한 게 다예요. '사내아이로군요.'"], ["Yah, the imbecility of the whole set of ‘em!’", "사내아이라니! 흥, 그 작자들이란 죄다 어쩜 그리 멍청한지!\""]], [["The heartiness of the ejaculation startled Mr. Dick exceedingly; and me, too, if I am to tell the truth.", "이 외마디에 실린 열기에 딕 씨는 화들짝 놀랐고, 솔직히 말하면 나도 그랬다."]], [["‘And then, as if this was not enough, and she had not stood sufficiently in the light of this child’s sister, Betsey Trotwood,’ said my aunt, ‘she marries a second time--goes and marries a Murderer--or a man with a name like it--and stands in THIS child’s light!", "\"게다가 그것도 모자라, 이 애의 누이 베시 트롯우드의 앞길을 가로막은 것으로도 충분치 않았던지,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 애는 두 번째로 결혼을 해 버렸어요 — 가서 살인자랑, 아니면 그 비슷한 이름을 가진 작자랑 결혼해서는, 이번엔 이 애의 앞길까지 가로막았지 뭐예요!"], ["And the natural consequence is, as anybody but a baby might have foreseen, that he prowls and wanders.", "그래서 당연한 결과로, 아기가 아닌 다음에야 누구라도 내다봤을 일이지만, 이 애가 이렇게 떠돌아다니며 헤매게 된 거예요."], ["He’s as like Cain before he was grown up, as he can be.’", "다 자라기 전의 카인이랑 어쩜 이리 똑같은지 모르겠네.\""]], [["Mr. Dick looked hard at me, as if to identify me in this character.", "딕 씨는 그 인물이 정말 나인지 확인이라도 하려는 듯 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["‘And then there’s that woman with the Pagan name,’ said my aunt, ‘that Peggotty, she goes and gets married next.", "\"게다가 그 이교도 이름을 가진 여자 말이에요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 패거티라는 여자도 가서 다음 차례로 결혼을 해 버렸어요."], ["Because she has not seen enough of the evil attending such things, she goes and gets married next, as the child relates.", "그런 일에 따르는 화근을 충분히 보지 못해서인지, 글쎄 이 애 말로는 다음으로 가서 결혼을 했다잖아요. 나는 그저 바랄 뿐이에요.\""], ["I only hope,’ said my aunt, shaking her head, ‘that her husband is one of those Poker husbands who abound in the newspapers, and will beat her well with one.’", "고모님이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그 여자 남편이라는 작자가, 신문에 차고 넘치는 부지깽이 남편들 중 하나라서, 부지깽이로 그 여자를 흠씬 두들겨 패 주기를.\""]], [["I could not bear to hear my old nurse so decried, and made the subject of such a wish.", "내 옛 유모가 그렇게 헐뜯기고, 그런 악담의 대상이 되는 것을 차마 들을 수가 없었다."], ["I told my aunt that indeed she was mistaken.", "나는 고모님께, 정말이지 그건 오해라고 말했다."], ["That Peggotty was the best, the truest, the most faithful, most devoted, and most self-denying friend and servant in the world; who had ever loved me dearly, who had ever loved my mother dearly; who had held my mother’s dying head upon her arm, on whose face my mother had imprinted her last grateful kiss.", "패거티는 세상에서 가장 착하고, 가장 진실되고, 가장 충직하고, 가장 헌신적이며, 가장 자기를 희생할 줄 아는 벗이자 하녀로, 늘 나를 끔찍이 아껴 주었고, 늘 우리 어머니를 끔찍이 아껴 주었으며, 어머니가 숨을 거둘 때 그 머리를 자기 팔로 받쳐 주었고, 그 얼굴에 어머니가 마지막 감사의 입맞춤을 남긴 사람이라고."], ["And my remembrance of them both, choking me, I broke down as I was trying to say that her home was my home, and that all she had was mine, and that I would have gone to her for shelter, but for her humble station, which made me fear that I might bring some trouble on her--I broke down, I say, as I was trying to say so, and laid my face in my hands upon the table.", "그러다 그 두 사람에 대한 기억이 북받쳐 목이 메었고, 패거티의 집이 곧 내 집이며, 그녀가 가진 모든 것이 곧 내 것이고, 그녀의 보잘것없는 처지가, 행여 그녀에게 누를 끼칠까 두렵게 만들지만 않았어도 나는 그녀에게 몸을 의탁하러 갔을 거라고 말하려는 찰나 — 그 말을 하려던 찰나, 나는 그만 무너져 내렸고, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 식탁에 엎드렸다."]], [["‘Well, well!’ said my aunt, ‘the child is right to stand by those who have stood by him--Janet!", "\"그래, 그래!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"자기 편이 되어 준 사람들 편을 드는 건 옳은 일이지 — 재닛!"], ["Donkeys!’", "당나귀들이야!\""]], [["I thoroughly believe that but for those unfortunate donkeys, we should have come to a good understanding; for my aunt had laid her hand on my shoulder, and the impulse was upon me, thus emboldened, to embrace her and beseech her protection.", "그 불운한 당나귀들만 아니었다면, 우리는 틀림없이 마음이 잘 통하게 되었으리라고 나는 굳게 믿는다. 고모님이 내 어깨에 손을 얹었고, 나는 그렇게 용기를 얻어, 그녀를 와락 끌어안고 보살펴 달라고 애원하고 싶은 충동이 일었기 때문이다."], ["But the interruption, and the disorder she was thrown into by the struggle outside, put an end to all softer ideas for the present, and kept my aunt indignantly declaiming to Mr.", "하지만 그 방해와, 바깥의 옥신각신으로 그녀가 빠져든 혼란이 당분간 모든 부드러운 생각에 종지부를 찍었고, 고모님은 차 마실 시간이 될 때까지 딕 씨에게 분개하며 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다."], ["Dick about her determination to appeal for redress to the laws of her country, and to bring actions for trespass against the whole donkey proprietorship of Dover, until tea-time.", "나라의 법에 호소해 배상을 받아 내고, 도버의 당나귀 주인이란 주인은 죄다 무단 침입으로 고소하겠노라는 자신의 결심에 대해서."]], [["After tea, we sat at the window--on the look-out, as I imagined, from my aunt’s sharp expression of face, for more invaders--until dusk, when Janet set candles, and a backgammon-board, on the table, and pulled down the blinds.", "차를 마신 뒤, 우리는 창가에 앉아 있었다 — 고모님의 매서운 표정으로 미루어 짐작하건대, 또 다른 침입자들을 망보면서 — 그렇게 땅거미가 질 때까지. 그 무렵 재닛이 식탁에 촛불과 주사위놀이판을 올려놓고 블라인드를 내렸다."]], [["‘Now, Mr.", "\"자, 딕 씨.\""], ["Dick,’ said my aunt, with her grave look, and her forefinger up as before, ‘I am going to ask you another question.", "고모님이 자못 진지한 표정으로, 아까처럼 집게손가락을 치켜들고 말했다. \"내가 또 한 가지 물어볼 게 있어요."], ["Look at this child.’", "이 애를 봐요.\""]], [["‘David’s son?’ said Mr.", "\"데이비드의 아들이요?\""], ["Dick, with an attentive, puzzled face.", "딕 씨가 주의 깊으면서도 어리둥절한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["‘Exactly so,’ returned my aunt.", "\"바로 그래요.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다."], ["‘What would you do with him, now?’", "\"이 애를 이제 어떻게 하면 좋겠어요?\""]], [["‘Do with David’s son?’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"데이비드의 아들을 어떻게 하느냐고요?\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ay,’ replied my aunt, ‘with David’s son.’", "\"그래요.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"데이비드의 아들 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Mr. Dick. ‘Yes.", "\"오!\" 딕 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지."], ["Do with--I should put him to bed.’", "어떻게 하느냐면 — 나라면 이 애를 재우겠어요.\""]], [["‘Janet!’ cried my aunt, with the same complacent triumph that I had remarked before. ‘Mr.", "\"재닛!\" 고모님이, 앞서 내가 눈여겨봤던 것과 똑같은 흐뭇한 의기양양함을 띠고 외쳤다."], ["Dick sets us all right. If the bed is ready, we’ll take him up to it.’", "\"딕 씨가 우리 모두를 바로잡아 주는구나. 잠자리가 준비됐거든, 이 애를 데리고 올라가자.\""]], [["Janet reporting it to be quite ready, I was taken up to it; kindly, but in some sort like a prisoner; my aunt going in front and Janet bringing up the rear.", "재닛이 잠자리가 다 준비됐다고 알리자, 나는 위층으로 데려가졌다. 다정하게, 하지만 어딘가 죄수와 같은 모양새로. 고모님이 앞장서고 재닛이 뒤를 따랐다."], ["The only circumstance which gave me any new hope, was my aunt’s stopping on the stairs to inquire about a smell of fire that was prevalent there; and janet’s replying that she had been making tinder down in the kitchen, of my old shirt.", "나에게 무언가 새로운 희망을 안겨 준 단 하나의 정황은, 고모님이 계단에서 걸음을 멈추고 거기 자욱하게 퍼진 무언가 타는 냄새에 대해 물어본 일이었다. 재닛은 부엌에서 내 헌 셔츠로 부싯깃을 만들던 참이라고 대답했다."], ["But there were no other clothes in my room than the odd heap of things I wore; and when I was left there, with a little taper which my aunt forewarned me would burn exactly five minutes, I heard them lock my door on the outside.", "하지만 내 방에는 내가 걸친 그 잡동사니 더미 말고는 다른 옷가지가 없었다. 그리고 정확히 오 분만 타들어 갈 거라고 고모님이 미리 일러 준 자그마한 양초 하나와 함께 그 방에 홀로 남겨졌을 때, 나는 그들이 바깥에서 내 방문을 잠그는 소리를 들었다."], ["Turning these things over in my mind I deemed it possible that my aunt, who could know nothing of me, might suspect I had a habit of running away, and took precautions, on that account, to have me in safe keeping.", "이런저런 일들을 마음속으로 곱씹어 보다가, 나는 나에 대해 아무것도 알 리 없는 고모님이, 어쩌면 내게 도망치는 버릇이 있지 않을까 의심하고, 그래서 나를 안전하게 단속해 두려고 그런 조치를 취한 게 아닐까 싶었다."]], [["The room was a pleasant one, at the top of the house, overlooking the sea, on which the moon was shining brilliantly.", "그 방은 집의 맨 위층에 자리한, 기분 좋은 방이었다. 바다가 내려다보였고, 그 위로 달이 휘영청 빛나고 있었다."], ["After I had said my prayers, and the candle had burnt out, I remember how I still sat looking at the moonlight on the water, as if I could hope to read my fortune in it, as in a bright book; or to see my mother with her child, coming from Heaven, along that shining path, to look upon me as she had looked when I last saw her sweet face.", "기도를 마치고 촛불이 다 타 버린 뒤에도, 나는 마치 밝은 책을 읽듯 그 물 위에서 내 운명을 읽어 낼 수 있기라도 한 양, 아니면 어머니가 아기를 안고 천국에서 그 반짝이는 길을 따라 내려와, 내가 마지막으로 보았던 그 사랑스러운 얼굴 그대로 나를 굽어봐 주기라도 할 양, 물 위의 달빛을 하염없이 바라보며 앉아 있던 기억이 난다."], ["I remember how the solemn feeling with which at length I turned my eyes away, yielded to the sensation of gratitude and rest which the sight of the white-curtained bed--and how much more the lying softly down upon it, nestling in the snow-white sheets!--inspired.", "마침내 그 광경에서 눈을 돌렸을 때 나를 사로잡았던 그 엄숙한 기분이, 흰 커튼이 드리워진 침대를 바라보며 — 아니, 그보다 훨씬 더, 그 위에 살그머니 몸을 누이고 눈처럼 새하얀 시트 속에 폭 파묻혔을 때 — 차오른 그 고마움과 안식의 감각에 자리를 내어 주던 것도 기억난다."], ["I remember how I thought of all the solitary places under the night sky where I had slept, and how I prayed that I never might be houseless any more, and never might forget the houseless.", "밤하늘 아래, 내가 잠들었던 그 모든 외로운 곳들을 떠올리며, 다시는 집 없는 신세가 되지 않기를, 그리고 집 없는 이들을 결코 잊지 않기를 기도하던 것도 기억난다."], ["I remember how I seemed to float, then, down the melancholy glory of that track upon the sea, away into the world of dreams.", "그러고는 그때 내가, 바다 위 그 길의 구슬프도록 찬란한 빛을 따라 둥실 떠내려가, 꿈의 세계 속으로 아스라이 흘러가는 것만 같던 그 느낌도 기억난다."]], [["# CHAPTER 14. MY AUNT MAKES UP HER MIND ABOUT ME", "# CHAPTER 14. MY AUNT MAKES UP HER MIND ABOUT ME"]], [["On going down in the morning, I found my aunt musing so profoundly over the breakfast table, with her elbow on the tray, that the contents of the urn had overflowed the teapot and were laying the whole table-cloth under water, when my entrance put her meditations to flight.", "아침에 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 고모님이 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 쟁반 위로 어찌나 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있던지, 차 끓이는 통의 물이 찻주전자를 넘쳐흘러 식탁보를 온통 물바다로 만들어 놓은 참이었는데, 내가 들어서자 그제야 고모님의 사색이 흩어졌다."], ["I felt sure that I had been the subject of her reflections, and was more than ever anxious to know her intentions towards me. Yet I dared not express my anxiety, lest it should give her offence.", "나는 내가 고모님의 생각거리였다고 확신했고, 나를 어떻게 할 작정인지 그 어느 때보다 간절히 알고 싶었다. 그렇지만 행여 고모님의 비위를 거스를까 봐 그 조바심을 입 밖에 낼 엄두는 나지 않았다."]], [["My eyes, however, not being so much under control as my tongue, were attracted towards my aunt very often during breakfast.", "하지만 내 두 눈은 혀만큼 단속이 되지 않아서, 아침을 먹는 내내 자꾸만 고모님 쪽으로 끌려갔다."], ["I never could look at her for a few moments together but I found her looking at me--in an odd thoughtful manner, as if I were an immense way off, instead of being on the other side of the small round table.", "잠깐이라도 고모님을 바라볼라치면 어김없이 고모님도 나를 바라보고 있는 것이었다 — 그 작고 둥근 식탁 맞은편이 아니라 까마득히 먼 곳에라도 있는 사람을 보듯, 묘하게 생각에 잠긴 눈빛으로."], ["When she had finished her breakfast, my aunt very deliberately leaned back in her chair, knitted her brows, folded her arms, and contemplated me at her leisure, with such a fixedness of attention that I was quite overpowered by embarrassment.", "아침을 다 드시고 나자, 고모님은 아주 천천히 의자에 등을 기대고, 눈살을 찌푸리고, 팔짱을 끼고, 어찌나 한곳에 못 박힌 듯 골똘한 눈길로 느긋하게 나를 뜯어보던지, 나는 거북함에 그만 짓눌릴 지경이었다."], ["Not having as yet finished my own breakfast, I attempted to hide my confusion by proceeding with it; but my knife tumbled over my fork, my fork tripped up my knife, I chipped bits of bacon a surprising height into the air instead of cutting them for my own eating, and choked myself with my tea, which persisted in going the wrong way instead of the right one, until I gave in altogether, and sat blushing under my aunt’s close scrutiny.", "나는 아직 내 아침을 다 먹지 못한 터라, 식사를 계속하는 척하며 당황한 기색을 감춰 보려 했다. 그러나 내 나이프가 포크 위로 엎어지고, 포크가 나이프에 걸려 넘어지고, 먹으려고 자르기는커녕 베이컨 조각을 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 높이 공중으로 튕겨 올리고, 차마저 제대로 넘어가지 않고 자꾸만 엉뚱한 데로 넘어가 사레가 들리는 통에, 나는 결국 모든 걸 포기하고, 고모님의 빈틈없는 눈초리 아래 얼굴만 붉힌 채 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Hallo!’ said my aunt, after a long time.", "\"이봐!\" 한참 만에 고모님이 말했다."]], [["I looked up, and met her sharp bright glance respectfully.", "나는 고개를 들어, 날카롭게 빛나는 그 눈길을 공손히 마주했다."]], [["‘I have written to him,’ said my aunt. ‘To--?’", "\"그 사람한테 편지를 보냈다.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘To your father-in-law,’ said my aunt.", "\"누구한테—요?\" \"네 의붓아버지한테 말이다.\""], ["‘I have sent him a letter that I’ll trouble him to attend to, or he and I will fall out, I can tell him!’", "고모님이 말했다. \"내가 단단히 신경 써서 답하라고 편지를 보냈으니, 안 그러면 그자랑 나랑 한판 붙는 수밖에 없다고, 그렇게 일러 두었지!\""]], [["‘Does he know where I am, aunt?’ I inquired, alarmed.", "\"제가 어디 있는지 그 사람이 알아요, 고모님?\" 나는 겁이 나서 물었다."]], [["‘I have told him,’ said my aunt, with a nod.", "\"내가 알려 줬다.\" 고모님이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["‘Shall I--be--given up to him?’ I faltered.", "\"저를—그 사람한테—넘겨주실 건가요?\" 나는 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said my aunt.", "\"모르겠다.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘We shall see.’", "\"두고 봐야지.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I can’t think what I shall do,’ I exclaimed, ‘if I have to go back to Mr.", "머드스톤 씨한테 다시 돌아가야 한다면, 저는 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠어요.\""], ["Murdstone!’", "나는 외쳤다."]], [["‘I don’t know anything about it,’ said my aunt, shaking her head.", "\"나도 그건 아무것도 모른다.\" 고모님이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."], ["‘I can’t say, I am sure.", "\"정말이지 뭐라 말을 못 하겠구나."], ["We shall see.’", "두고 봐야지.\""]], [["My spirits sank under these words, and I became very downcast and heavy of heart.", "이 말에 내 마음은 가라앉았고, 나는 몹시 풀이 죽어 가슴이 무거워졌다."], ["My aunt, without appearing to take much heed of me, put on a coarse apron with a bib, which she took out of the press; washed up the teacups with her own hands; and, when everything was washed and set in the tray again, and the cloth folded and put on the top of the whole, rang for Janet to remove it.", "고모님은 나에게 별로 신경 쓰는 기색도 없이, 찬장에서 꺼낸 가슴받이가 달린 거친 앞치마를 두르고, 손수 찻잔을 씻었다. 그리고 모든 것을 다 씻어 쟁반에 도로 정돈하고, 식탁보를 개어 그 위에 얹은 다음, 종을 울려 재닛을 불러 치우게 했다."], ["She next swept up the crumbs with a little broom (putting on a pair of gloves first), until there did not appear to be one microscopic speck left on the carpet; next dusted and arranged the room, which was dusted and arranged to a hair’s breadth already.", "그다음에는 (먼저 장갑부터 한 켤레 끼고) 작은 빗자루로 부스러기를 쓸어, 양탄자 위에 티끌 하나 남지 않은 듯 보일 때까지 쓸었다. 그러고는 이미 머리카락 한 올만큼의 빈틈도 없이 먼지를 털고 정돈해 둔 방을, 다시 먼지를 털고 정돈했다."], ["When all these tasks were performed to her satisfaction, she took off the gloves and apron, folded them up, put them in the particular corner of the press from which they had been taken, brought out her work-box to her own table in the open window, and sat down, with the green fan between her and the light, to work.", "이 모든 일을 흡족할 만큼 마치고 나자, 고모님은 장갑과 앞치마를 벗어 개키고, 그것들을 꺼냈던 찬장의 그 특정한 구석에 도로 넣은 뒤, 활짝 열린 창가 자기 탁자로 반짇고리를 가져와 자리에 앉았다. 빛과 자기 사이에 초록색 부채를 놓고, 바느질을 시작했다."]], [["‘I wish you’d go upstairs,’ said my aunt, as she threaded her needle, ‘and give my compliments to Mr.", "\"네가 위층에 좀 올라가 줬으면 한다.\" 고모님이 바늘에 실을 꿰며 말했다."], ["Dick, and I’ll be glad to know how he gets on with his Memorial.’", "\"그래서 딕 씨에게 내 안부를 전하고, 진정서 작업이 어떻게 되어 가는지 좀 알아봐 다오.\""]], [["I rose with all alacrity, to acquit myself of this commission.", "나는 이 심부름을 해내려고 냉큼 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["‘I suppose,’ said my aunt, eyeing me as narrowly as she had eyed the needle in threading it, ‘you think Mr.", "\"내 짐작에는,\" 바늘에 실을 꿸 때 그 바늘을 살피던 만큼이나 빈틈없는 눈으로 나를 살피며 고모님이 말했다."], ["Dick a short name, eh?’", "\"네가 딕 씨를 짧은 이름이라고 여기는 모양인데, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘I thought it was rather a short name, yesterday,’ I confessed.", "\"어제 좀 짧은 이름이라고 생각하긴 했어요.\" 나는 털어놓았다."]], [["‘You are not to suppose that he hasn’t got a longer name, if he chose to use it,’ said my aunt, with a loftier air. ‘Babley--Mr.", "\"그 사람이 더 긴 이름이 없어서 그러는 줄 알면 안 된다, 본인이 쓰고 싶기만 하면 얼마든지 쓸 수 있는 이름이 있으니까.\" 고모님이 한결 거만한 태도로 말했다."], ["Richard Babley--that’s the gentleman’s true name.’", "\"배블리—리처드 배블리 씨—그게 그분의 진짜 이름이야.\""]], [["I was going to suggest, with a modest sense of my youth and the familiarity I had been already guilty of, that I had better give him the full benefit of that name, when my aunt went on to say:", "나는 내 어린 나이와 이미 저지른 무람없음을 겸허히 의식하며, 그분께 그 이름을 온전히 다 불러 드리는 게 낫겠다고 슬며시 말해 볼 참이었는데, 고모님이 말을 이었다."]], [["‘But don’t you call him by it, whatever you do.", "\"하지만 무슨 일이 있어도 그 이름으로는 부르지 마라."], ["He can’t bear his name.", "그 사람은 자기 이름을 견디질 못해."], ["That’s a peculiarity of his.", "그게 그 사람의 별난 점이지."], ["Though I don’t know that it’s much of a peculiarity, either; for he has been ill-used enough, by some that bear it, to have a mortal antipathy for it, Heaven knows. Mr.", "하긴 그게 그리 별난 점이랄 것도 없는 게, 그 이름을 가진 몇몇 사람한테 어찌나 모질게 당했던지, 그 이름을 죽도록 싫어할 만도 하다는 걸 하늘은 아실 게다."], ["Dick is his name here, and everywhere else, now--if he ever went anywhere else, which he don’t.", "여기서나 다른 어디서나, 이제 그 사람 이름은 딕 씨야—하긴 다른 데를 갈 일이라곤 없는 사람이지만."], ["So take care, child, you don’t call him anything BUT Mr. Dick.’", "그러니 얘야, 조심해서 그 사람을 오직 딕 씨라고만 부르거라.\""]], [["I promised to obey, and went upstairs with my message; thinking, as I went, that if Mr.", "나는 그러겠노라 약속하고, 그 전갈을 가지고 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Dick had been working at his Memorial long, at the same rate as I had seen him working at it, through the open door, when I came down, he was probably getting on very well indeed.", "올라가면서 생각하기를, 아까 내가 내려올 때 열린 문틈으로 보았던 그 속도로 딕 씨가 오랫동안 진정서에 매달려 왔다면, 작업이 어지간히 잘 진척되고 있을 게 틀림없다 싶었다."], ["I found him still driving at it with a long pen, and his head almost laid upon the paper.", "가 보니 딕 씨는 여전히 긴 펜으로 진정서를 몰아붙이고 있었는데, 머리가 거의 종이에 닿을 지경이었다."], ["He was so intent upon it, that I had ample leisure to observe the large paper kite in a corner, the confusion of bundles of manuscript, the number of pens, and, above all, the quantity of ink (which he seemed to have in, in half-gallon jars by the dozen), before he observed my being present.", "어찌나 골똘했던지, 그가 내가 와 있는 걸 알아채기 전까지, 나는 한쪽 구석의 커다란 종이 연이며, 어수선하게 쌓인 원고 뭉치며, 그 많은 펜이며, 무엇보다 그 엄청난 잉크 양(반 갤런들이 단지로 열 몇 개씩 들여놓은 듯했다)을 느긋이 살펴볼 짬이 충분했다."]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["Phoebus!’ said Mr.", "포이보스로구나!\""], ["Dick, laying down his pen.", "딕 씨가 펜을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"세상은 어떻게 돌아가나?"], ["‘How does the world go?", "내가 한마디 해 주마.\""], ["I’ll tell you what,’ he added, in a lower tone, ‘I shouldn’t wish it to be mentioned, but it’s a--’ here he beckoned to me, and put his lips close to my ear--‘it’s a mad world.", "그가 목소리를 낮춰 덧붙였다. \"남한테 떠벌리고 싶진 않다만, 이건 말이야—\" 여기서 그는 나에게 손짓하더니 입술을 내 귀에 바짝 갖다 댔다."], ["Mad as Bedlam, boy!’ said Mr.", "\"미친 세상이야. 정신병원처럼 미쳤다고, 얘야!\""], ["Dick, taking snuff from a round box on the table, and laughing heartily.", "딕 씨는 탁자 위 둥근 통에서 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 그렇게 말하고는 호탕하게 웃었다."]], [["Without presuming to give my opinion on this question, I delivered my message.", "이 문제에 대해 감히 내 의견을 내놓을 처지는 못 되었으므로, 나는 전갈만 전했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr.", "\"그래.\""], ["Dick, in answer, ‘my compliments to her, and I--I believe I have made a start. I think I have made a start,’ said Mr.", "딕 씨가 대답으로 말했다. \"고모님께 내 안부를 전해 다오, 그리고 나는—나는 이제 첫발을 뗀 것 같다."], ["Dick, passing his hand among his grey hair, and casting anything but a confident look at his manuscript.", "첫발을 뗀 것 같아.\" 딕 씨는 희끗한 머리칼 사이로 손을 쓸어 넣으며, 자기 원고를 향해 도무지 자신 없는 눈길을 던지며 말했다."], ["‘You have been to school?’", "\"너 학교에 다녀 봤지?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ I answered; ‘for a short time.’", "\"네, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"잠깐 다녔어요.\""]], [["‘Do you recollect the date,’ said Mr.", "\"너 그 날짜를 기억하느냐,\""], ["Dick, looking earnestly at me, and taking up his pen to note it down, ‘when King Charles the First had his head cut off?’ I said I believed it happened in the year sixteen hundred and forty-nine.", "딕 씨가 나를 진지하게 바라보더니, 받아 적으려고 펜을 집어 들며 말했다. \"찰스 일 세 임금이 목이 잘린 날 말이다.\" 나는 그게 천육백사십구 년에 일어난 일로 알고 있다고 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ returned Mr. Dick, scratching his ear with his pen, and looking dubiously at me.", "\"흠.\" 딕 씨가 펜으로 귀를 긁으며, 미심쩍은 눈으로 나를 보며 대꾸했다."], ["‘So the books say; but I don’t see how that can be.", "\"책에는 그렇게 나와 있지. 하지만 나는 어떻게 그럴 수 있는지 모르겠어."], ["Because, if it was so long ago, how could the people about him have made that mistake of putting some of the trouble out of his head, after it was taken off, into mine?’", "만약 그게 그렇게 오래전 일이라면, 그 사람 머리가 잘린 다음에, 그 머릿속에 있던 골칫거리 얼마를 그 곁에 있던 사람들이 어떻게 내 머릿속으로 옮겨 넣는 실수를 저지를 수 있었겠느냐?\""]], [["I was very much surprised by the inquiry; but could give no information on this point.", "나는 그 물음에 무척 놀랐으나, 이 점에 대해서는 아무런 정보도 줄 수가 없었다."]], [["‘It’s very strange,’ said Mr.", "\"참 이상한 일이야.\""], ["Dick, with a despondent look upon his papers, and with his hand among his hair again, ‘that I never can get that quite right.", "딕 씨가 자기 서류를 풀 죽은 눈으로 내려다보며, 다시 머리칼 사이로 손을 넣고 말했다. \"나는 도무지 그걸 제대로 맞춰 볼 수가 없어."], ["I never can make that perfectly clear.", "그걸 완벽하게 깔끔히 풀어내질 못하겠단 말이야."], ["But no matter, no matter!’ he said cheerfully, and rousing himself, ‘there’s time enough!", "하지만 괜찮아, 괜찮아!\" 그는 기운을 차려 명랑하게 말했다. \"시간은 넉넉하니까!"], ["My compliments to Miss Trotwood, I am getting on very well indeed.’", "트롯우드 양께 안부 전해 다오, 일은 아주 잘되어 가고 있다고.\""]], [["I was going away, when he directed my attention to the kite.", "내가 막 나가려는데, 그가 연 쪽으로 내 눈길을 끌었다."]], [["‘What do you think of that for a kite?’ he said.", "\"저 연 어떠냐?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["I answered that it was a beautiful one.", "나는 멋진 연이라고 대답했다."], ["I should think it must have been as much as seven feet high.", "내 짐작에 그 높이가 칠 피트는 족히 되었을 것 같았다."]], [["‘I made it.", "\"내가 만들었지."], ["We’ll go and fly it, you and I,’ said Mr. Dick.", "우리 함께 가서 날려 보자, 너랑 나랑.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Do you see this?’", "\"이게 보이느냐?\""]], [["He showed me that it was covered with manuscript, very closely and laboriously written; but so plainly, that as I looked along the lines, I thought I saw some allusion to King Charles the First’s head again, in one or two places.", "그는 연이 원고로 뒤덮여 있는 걸 나에게 보여 주었는데, 아주 빽빽하고 공들여 쓴 글씨였으나, 어찌나 또렷한지 줄을 따라 읽어 가다 보니, 한두 군데에서 또 찰스 일 세 임금의 머리를 넌지시 가리키는 대목이 보이는 듯싶었다."]], [["‘There’s plenty of string,’ said Mr.", "\"실이 넉넉히 있어.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["Dick, ‘and when it flies high, it takes the facts a long way.", "\"그래서 연이 높이 날면, 사실들을 멀리까지 실어 나르지."], ["That’s my manner of diffusing ‘em.", "그게 내가 사실들을 퍼뜨리는 방식이란다."], ["I don’t know where they may come down.", "어디로 떨어질지는 나도 몰라."], ["It’s according to circumstances, and the wind, and so forth; but I take my chance of that.’", "형편이며, 바람이며, 그런 것들에 달렸으니까. 하지만 나는 거기다 운을 걸어 보는 거지.\""]], [["His face was so very mild and pleasant, and had something so reverend in it, though it was hale and hearty, that I was not sure but that he was having a good-humoured jest with me.", "그의 얼굴이 어찌나 온순하고 흐뭇하던지, 그리고 정정하고 활기차면서도 어딘가 우러러볼 만한 데가 있던지, 나는 그가 나를 데리고 짓궂지 않게 농담을 주고받는 건 아닌가 싶기도 했다."], ["So I laughed, and he laughed, and we parted the best friends possible.", "그래서 내가 웃자 그도 웃었고, 우리는 더없이 좋은 친구가 되어 헤어졌다."]], [["‘Well, child,’ said my aunt, when I went downstairs.", "\"그래, 얘야.\" 내가 아래층으로 내려가자 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘And what of Mr. Dick, this morning?’", "\"오늘 아침 딕 씨는 어떻든?\""]], [["I informed her that he sent his compliments, and was getting on very well indeed.", "나는 그분이 안부를 전했고, 일이 아주 잘되어 가고 있다고 알려 드렸다."]], [["‘What do you think of him?’ said my aunt.", "\"그 사람을 어떻게 생각하느냐?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["I had some shadowy idea of endeavouring to evade the question, by replying that I thought him a very nice gentleman; but my aunt was not to be so put off, for she laid her work down in her lap, and said, folding her hands upon it:", "나는 아주 좋은 분이라고 생각한다고 대답하여 그 물음을 슬쩍 피해 볼까 하는 어렴풋한 궁리를 했으나, 고모님은 그렇게 넘어갈 분이 아니어서, 바느질감을 무릎에 내려놓고 그 위에 두 손을 포개며 말했다."]], [["‘Come!", "\"자!"], ["Your sister Betsey Trotwood would have told me what she thought of anyone, directly.", "네 누이 베시 트롯우드라면 누구든 어떻게 생각하는지 당장 나한테 말했을 거다."], ["Be as like your sister as you can, and speak out!’", "되도록 네 누이처럼, 솔직히 말해 보거라!\""]], [["‘Is he--is Mr.", "\"그분은—딕 씨는—제가 몰라서 여쭙는 건데요, 고모님—그럼 그분이 좀 정신이 온전치 못하신 건가요?\""], ["Dick--I ask because I don’t know, aunt--is he at all out of his mind, then?’ I stammered; for I felt I was on dangerous ground.", "나는 더듬거렸다. 내가 위태로운 땅을 밟고 있다는 걸 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["‘Not a morsel,’ said my aunt.", "\"털끝만큼도 아니다.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ I observed faintly.", "\"아, 그렇군요!\" 나는 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["‘If there is anything in the world,’ said my aunt, with great decision and force of manner, ‘that Mr.", "\"이 세상에서 딕 씨가 아닌 게 한 가지 있다면,\" 고모님이 아주 단호하고 힘찬 태도로 말했다."], ["Dick is not, it’s that.’", "\"그게 바로 그거다.\""]], [["I had nothing better to offer, than another timid, ‘Oh, indeed!’", "나는 또 한 번 머뭇머뭇 \"아, 그렇군요!\" 하는 말밖에 더 나은 대꾸를 내놓지 못했다."]], [["‘He has been CALLED mad,’ said my aunt.", "\"그 사람이 미쳤다는 소리를 듣긴 했지.\""], ["‘I have a selfish pleasure in saying he has been called mad, or I should not have had the benefit of his society and advice for these last ten years and upwards--in fact, ever since your sister, Betsey Trotwood, disappointed me.’", "고모님이 말했다. \"내가 그 사람이 미쳤다는 소리를 들었다고 말하는 데에는 이기적인 즐거움이 있단다. 안 그랬으면 지난 십 년 넘게 그 사람과 함께 지내며 그 충고를 누리는 복을 못 누렸을 테니까—사실, 네 누이 베시 트롯우드가 나를 실망시킨 그때 이후로 줄곧 말이다.\""]], [["‘So long as that?’ I said.", "\"그렇게 오래됐어요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘And nice people they were, who had the audacity to call him mad,’ pursued my aunt. ‘Mr.", "\"그 사람을 미쳤다고 떠벌릴 만큼 뻔뻔했던 작자들이, 또 그렇게 점잖은 사람들이었지.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다."], ["Dick is a sort of distant connexion of mine--it doesn’t matter how; I needn’t enter into that.", "\"딕 씨는 나하고 먼 친척뻘 되는 사람인데—어떻게 되는지는 상관없으니, 그것까진 들어갈 필요 없고."], ["If it hadn’t been for me, his own brother would have shut him up for life.", "만약 내가 아니었으면, 친형이라는 작자가 그 사람을 평생 가둬 놓았을 게다."], ["That’s all.’", "그게 다야.\""]], [["I am afraid it was hypocritical in me, but seeing that my aunt felt strongly on the subject, I tried to look as if I felt strongly too.", "내가 위선을 떤 건지도 모르겠지만, 고모님이 이 일에 강한 감정을 품고 있는 걸 보고, 나도 강한 감정을 느끼는 척 애써 보였다."]], [["‘A proud fool!’ said my aunt.", "\"오만한 멍청이 같으니!\""], ["‘Because his brother was a little eccentric--though he is not half so eccentric as a good many people--he didn’t like to have him visible about his house, and sent him away to some private asylum-place: though he had been left to his particular care by their deceased father, who thought him almost a natural.", "고모님이 말했다. \"자기 동생이 좀 별나다고—세상의 수많은 사람들 절반만큼도 안 별난데도—집 안에 그 사람이 어른거리는 걸 싫어해서, 어디 사설 요양원 같은 데로 내쫓아 버렸어. 돌아가신 자기네 아버지가, 그 애가 거의 천치나 다름없다고 여겨, 특별히 잘 보살피라고 그 형한테 맡겨 두었는데도 말이다."], ["And a wise man he must have been to think so!", "그렇게 생각했다니, 그 아버지도 어지간히 현명한 양반이었나 보지!"], ["Mad himself, no doubt.’", "본인이야말로 미친 게 분명해.\""]], [["Again, as my aunt looked quite convinced, I endeavoured to look quite convinced also.", "또다시, 고모님이 아주 확신에 차 보였으므로, 나도 아주 확신하는 양 애써 보였다."]], [["‘So I stepped in,’ said my aunt, ‘and made him an offer.", "\"그래서 내가 끼어들었지.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그래서 그 사람한테 제안을 했어."], ["I said, “Your brother’s sane--a great deal more sane than you are, or ever will be, it is to be hoped.", "내가 그랬다. '당신 동생은 멀쩡해—당신보다, 또 당신이 앞으로 될 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 멀쩡해, 그러길 바라야겠지만."], ["Let him have his little income, and come and live with me.", "그 사람 몫의 자그마한 수입은 그대로 두고, 나한테 와서 살게 해 줘요."], ["I am not afraid of him, I am not proud, I am ready to take care of him, and shall not ill-treat him as some people (besides the asylum-folks) have done.”", "나는 그 사람이 무섭지 않고, 잘난 척할 것도 없고, 그 사람을 보살필 준비가 됐고, 몇몇 사람들(요양원 사람들 말고도)이 그랬던 것처럼 그 사람을 함부로 대하지도 않을 테니.'"], ["After a good deal of squabbling,’ said my aunt, ‘I got him; and he has been here ever since.", "한참을 옥신각신한 끝에,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 그 사람을 데려왔지."], ["He is the most friendly and amenable creature in existence; and as for advice!--But nobody knows what that man’s mind is, except myself.’", "그 뒤로 줄곧 여기 있는 거야. 그 사람은 세상에 둘도 없이 다정하고 고분고분한 사람이란다. 충고로 말할 것 같으면!—하지만 그 사람 머릿속이 어떤지는 나 말고는 아무도 몰라.\""]], [["My aunt smoothed her dress and shook her head, as if she smoothed defiance of the whole world out of the one, and shook it out of the other.", "고모님은 옷자락을 매만지고 고개를 한 번 흔들었는데, 마치 옷자락에서는 온 세상에 맞서는 도전을 매만져 펴 버리고, 머리에서는 그걸 떨쳐 버리기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["‘He had a favourite sister,’ said my aunt, ‘a good creature, and very kind to him.", "\"그 사람한테는 아끼는 누이가 하나 있었어.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["But she did what they all do--took a husband.", "\"착한 사람이었고, 그 사람한테 무척 잘해 줬지."], ["And HE did what they all do--made her wretched.", "그런데 그 여자도 다들 하는 짓을 했어—남편을 얻은 거야."], ["It had such an effect upon the mind of Mr. Dick (that’s not madness, I hope!) that, combined with his fear of his brother, and his sense of his unkindness, it threw him into a fever.", "그리고 그 남편이라는 작자도 다들 하는 짓을 했지—그 여자를 비참하게 만든 거야. 그게 딕 씨의 마음에 어찌나 영향을 끼쳤던지(이건 미친 게 아니길 바란다!), 그 일이 형에 대한 두려움과 형이 모질게 굴었다는 생각과 겹치면서, 그 사람을 열병으로 몰아넣었어."], ["That was before he came to me, but the recollection of it is oppressive to him even now.", "그게 나한테 오기 전 일이지만, 그 기억은 지금도 그 사람을 짓누르고 있어."], ["Did he say anything to you about King Charles the First, child?’", "얘야, 그 사람이 너한테 찰스 일 세 임금에 대해 무슨 말을 하더냐?\""]], [["‘Yes, aunt.’", "\"네, 고모님.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose as if she were a little vexed.", "\"아!\" 고모님이 약간 짜증이 난 듯 코를 문지르며 말했다."], ["‘That’s his allegorical way of expressing it.", "\"그게 그 사람이 그 일을 빗대어 표현하는 방식이야."], ["He connects his illness with great disturbance and agitation, naturally, and that’s the figure, or the simile, or whatever it’s called, which he chooses to use.", "그 사람은 자기 병을 어떤 큰 소동이며 동요와 연결 짓는데, 그건 당연한 일이고, 그래서 그 비유든, 그 직유든, 뭐라고 부르든 간에, 그걸 골라 쓰는 거란다."], ["And why shouldn’t he, if he thinks proper!’", "본인이 그게 옳다고 여긴다면, 그러지 못할 게 뭐가 있겠니!\""]], [["I said: ‘Certainly, aunt.’", "나는 말했다. \"그럼요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘It’s not a business-like way of speaking,’ said my aunt, ‘nor a worldly way.", "\"그게 사무적인 말투는 아니지.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"세속적인 말투도 아니고."], ["I am aware of that; and that’s the reason why I insist upon it, that there shan’t be a word about it in his Memorial.’", "나도 그건 안다. 그래서 내가 우기는 거야, 그 사람 진정서에는 그 얘기가 한마디도 들어가선 안 된다고 말이다.\""]], [["‘Is it a Memorial about his own history that he is writing, aunt?’", "\"그분이 쓰고 계신 게 자기 신상에 관한 진정서예요, 고모님?\""]], [["‘Yes, child,’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose again.", "\"그렇단다, 얘야.\" 고모님이 다시 코를 문지르며 말했다."], ["‘He is memorializing the Lord Chancellor, or the Lord Somebody or other--one of those people, at all events, who are paid to be memorialized--about his affairs.", "\"그 사람은 대법관님인지, 무슨 무슨 경인지—하여간 진정을 받는 대가로 봉급을 받는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나한테, 자기 사정을 진정하고 있는 거야."], ["I suppose it will go in, one of these days.", "머잖아 그게 들어가긴 하겠지."], ["He hasn’t been able to draw it up yet, without introducing that mode of expressing himself; but it don’t signify; it keeps him employed.’", "아직은 그런 식의 표현을 끼워 넣지 않고서는 작성을 못 하고 있다만, 그건 상관없어. 그 일이 그 사람을 바쁘게 해 주니까.\""]], [["In fact, I found out afterwards that Mr. Dick had been for upwards of ten years endeavouring to keep King Charles the First out of the Memorial; but he had been constantly getting into it, and was there now.", "실은, 나중에 알게 된 것인데, 딕 씨는 십 년 넘게 찰스 일 세 임금을 진정서에서 빼내려고 애써 왔으나, 그 임금은 줄곧 슬그머니 끼어들었고, 지금도 그 안에 들어 있었다."]], [["‘I say again,’ said my aunt, ‘nobody knows what that man’s mind is except myself; and he’s the most amenable and friendly creature in existence.", "\"내가 다시 말하지만,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 사람 머릿속이 어떤지는 나 말고는 아무도 몰라."], ["If he likes to fly a kite sometimes, what of that! Franklin used to fly a kite.", "그리고 그 사람은 세상에 둘도 없이 고분고분하고 다정한 사람이야. 가끔 연을 날리고 싶어 한들, 그게 뭐 어떻단 말이냐!"], ["He was a Quaker, or something of that sort, if I am not mistaken.", "프랭클린도 연을 날리곤 했어. 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그 사람은 퀘이커교도였든지 그 비슷한 거였지."], ["And a Quaker flying a kite is a much more ridiculous object than anybody else.’", "그리고 퀘이커교도가 연을 날리는 게, 다른 누가 그러는 것보다 훨씬 더 우스꽝스러운 광경이고 말이다.\""]], [["If I could have supposed that my aunt had recounted these particulars for my especial behoof, and as a piece of confidence in me, I should have felt very much distinguished, and should have augured favourably from such a mark of her good opinion.", "만약 고모님이 이런 시시콜콜한 사정을 오로지 나를 위해, 또 나를 믿고 털어놓는 것이라고 생각할 수 있었다면, 나는 무척 영광스러웠을 테고, 그렇게 나를 좋게 보아 주는 표시에서 길조를 점쳤을 것이다."], ["But I could hardly help observing that she had launched into them, chiefly because the question was raised in her own mind, and with very little reference to me, though she had addressed herself to me in the absence of anybody else.", "그러나 고모님이 이 이야기에 빠져든 것은, 비록 곁에 다른 사람이 없어서 나를 상대로 말을 건넨 것이긴 해도, 주로 그 물음이 고모님 자신의 마음속에서 떠올랐기 때문이지 나하고는 별 상관이 없다는 것을, 나는 알아채지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["At the same time, I must say that the generosity of her championship of poor harmless Mr. Dick, not only inspired my young breast with some selfish hope for myself, but warmed it unselfishly towards her.", "그러는 한편, 한마디 해 두자면, 가엾고 해롭잖은 딕 씨를 너그러이 두둔하는 고모님의 그 마음씨는, 내 어린 가슴에 나 자신을 위한 어떤 이기적인 희망을 불어넣었을 뿐 아니라, 고모님을 향해서도 사심 없는 따스함으로 데워 주었다."], ["I believe that I began to know that there was something about my aunt, notwithstanding her many eccentricities and odd humours, to be honoured and trusted in.", "나는 비로소, 고모님의 그 숱한 별스러움과 괴팍한 변덕에도 불구하고, 고모님에게는 존경하고 믿을 만한 무언가가 있다는 걸 알기 시작한 것 같았다."], ["Though she was just as sharp that day as on the day before, and was in and out about the donkeys just as often, and was thrown into a tremendous state of indignation, when a young man, going by, ogled Janet at a window (which was one of the gravest misdemeanours that could be committed against my aunt’s dignity), she seemed to me to command more of my respect, if not less of my fear.", "그날도 고모님은 어제만큼이나 매서웠고, 당나귀 때문에 들락날락하기를 똑같이 잦게 했고, 어떤 젊은 사내가 지나가다 창가의 재닛에게 추파를 던지자(이는 고모님의 위엄에 대한 가장 중대한 위반 행위 가운데 하나였다) 무시무시한 분노에 휩싸였지만, 그래도 나에게는, 두려움이 줄어든 건 아닐지언정, 더 큰 존경을 자아내는 분으로 보였다."]], [["The anxiety I underwent, in the interval which necessarily elapsed before a reply could be received to her letter to Mr.", "머드스톤 씨에게 보낸 편지의 답장이 도착하기까지 자연히 흘러야 했던 그 사이에, 내가 겪은 불안은 이루 말할 수 없었다."], ["Murdstone, was extreme; but I made an endeavour to suppress it, and to be as agreeable as I could in a quiet way, both to my aunt and Mr. Dick.", "그러나 나는 그것을 억누르려 애썼고, 조용한 방식으로나마 고모님과 딕 씨 두 분 모두에게 되도록 싹싹하게 굴려고 했다."], ["The latter and I would have gone out to fly the great kite; but that I had still no other clothes than the anything but ornamental garments with which I had been decorated on the first day, and which confined me to the house, except for an hour after dark, when my aunt, for my health’s sake, paraded me up and down on the cliff outside, before going to bed.", "딕 씨와 나는 그 커다란 연을 날리러 밖에 나가고 싶었지만, 나에게는 첫날 입혀진 그 도무지 볼품없는 옷가지밖에 다른 옷이 없어, 잠자리에 들기 전 고모님이 내 건강을 위해 한 시간씩 바깥 절벽 위로 나를 데리고 거니는 어둑한 때를 빼고는, 줄곧 집 안에 갇혀 있었다."], ["At length the reply from Mr. Murdstone came, and my aunt informed me, to my infinite terror, that he was coming to speak to her herself on the next day.", "마침내 머드스톤 씨의 답장이 왔고, 고모님은 나에게, 이루 말할 수 없는 두려움을 안기며, 그가 이튿날 직접 자기를 만나러 온다고 알려 주었다."], ["On the next day, still bundled up in my curious habiliments, I sat counting the time, flushed and heated by the conflict of sinking hopes and rising fears within me; and waiting to be startled by the sight of the gloomy face, whose non-arrival startled me every minute.", "이튿날, 여전히 그 괴상한 옷가지에 둘둘 싸인 채, 나는 가라앉는 희망과 솟아오르는 두려움이 안에서 다투는 통에 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채로, 시간을 헤아리며 앉아 있었다. 그리고 그 음울한 얼굴이 나타나면 깜짝 놀랄 줄 알면서 기다렸는데, 그자가 나타나지 않는 그 한순간 한순간이 도리어 나를 놀라게 했다."]], [["My aunt was a little more imperious and stern than usual, but I observed no other token of her preparing herself to receive the visitor so much dreaded by me.", "고모님은 평소보다 조금 더 위압적이고 근엄했으나, 내가 그토록 두려워하는 손님을 맞을 채비를 한다는 다른 기색은 보이지 않았다."], ["She sat at work in the window, and I sat by, with my thoughts running astray on all possible and impossible results of Mr. Murdstone’s visit, until pretty late in the afternoon.", "고모님은 창가에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있었고, 나는 그 곁에 앉아, 머드스톤 씨의 방문이 가져올 가능한 결과며 불가능한 결과며 온갖 것에 마음이 갈팡질팡한 채로, 오후도 꽤 늦은 시각까지 그러고 있었다."], ["Our dinner had been indefinitely postponed; but it was growing so late, that my aunt had ordered it to be got ready, when she gave a sudden alarm of donkeys, and to my consternation and amazement, I beheld Miss Murdstone, on a side-saddle, ride deliberately over the sacred piece of green, and stop in front of the house, looking about her.", "저녁 식사는 기약 없이 미뤄진 참이었으나, 시간이 어찌나 늦어졌던지 고모님이 식사를 준비하라고 일러 두었을 무렵, 갑자기 당나귀가 나타났다고 경보를 울렸다. 그러고는, 나의 경악과 놀라움 속에, 나는 머드스톤 양이 여자용 안장에 올라타고 그 신성한 풀밭을 태연히 가로질러 와서는, 집 앞에 멈춰 서서 둘레를 둘러보는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Go along with you!’ cried my aunt, shaking her head and her fist at the window.", "\"썩 꺼지지 못해!\" 고모님이 창문을 향해 고개를 흔들고 주먹을 휘두르며 소리쳤다."], ["‘You have no business there.", "\"거기 들어올 일 없어."], ["How dare you trespass? Go along! Oh!", "어디 함부로 침범이야? 썩 가! 아!"], ["you bold-faced thing!’", "이 뻔뻔한 것이!\""]], [["My aunt was so exasperated by the coolness with which Miss Murdstone looked about her, that I really believe she was motionless, and unable for the moment to dart out according to custom.", "머드스톤 양이 둘레를 둘러보는 그 태연함에 고모님은 어찌나 분통이 터졌던지, 정말이지 그 순간만큼은 평소처럼 뛰쳐나가지 못하고 그대로 굳어 버린 게 아닌가 싶었다."], ["I seized the opportunity to inform her who it was; and that the gentleman now coming near the offender (for the way up was very steep, and he had dropped behind), was Mr. Murdstone himself.", "나는 그 틈을 타, 저 사람이 누구인지, 그리고 지금 그 침입자에게 다가오는 신사(올라오는 길이 몹시 가팔라서 그가 뒤처져 있었다)가 바로 머드스톤 씨라고 고모님께 알려 드렸다."]], [["‘I don’t care who it is!’ cried my aunt, still shaking her head and gesticulating anything but welcome from the bow-window.", "\"그게 누구든 난 상관없어!\" 고모님이 여전히 고개를 흔들며, 활 모양 퇴창에서 도무지 환영이라고는 할 수 없는 손짓을 해 대며 소리쳤다. \"침범당하지 않을 테야."], ["‘I won’t be trespassed upon.", "절대로 안 돼. 썩 꺼져!"], ["I won’t allow it. Go away!", "재닛, 저놈을 돌려세워."], ["Janet, turn him round.", "끌고 가 버려!\""], ["Lead him off!’ and I saw, from behind my aunt, a sort of hurried battle-piece, in which the donkey stood resisting everybody, with all his four legs planted different ways, while Janet tried to pull him round by the bridle, Mr. Murdstone tried to lead him on, Miss Murdstone struck at Janet with a parasol, and several boys, who had come to see the engagement, shouted vigorously.", "그러자 고모님 등 뒤에서, 나는 일종의 급박한 전투 장면을 보았다. 당나귀는 네 다리를 제각각 다른 방향으로 떡 버틴 채 모두에게 버티고 섰고, 재닛은 고삐를 잡아 돌려세우려 했고, 머드스톤 씨는 끌고 나아가려 했고, 머드스톤 양은 양산으로 재닛을 후려쳤고, 이 한판 싸움을 구경하러 몰려든 사내아이 몇몇은 기운차게 소리를 질러 댔다."], ["But my aunt, suddenly descrying among them the young malefactor who was the donkey’s guardian, and who was one of the most inveterate offenders against her, though hardly in his teens, rushed out to the scene of action, pounced upon him, captured him, dragged him, with his jacket over his head, and his heels grinding the ground, into the garden, and, calling upon Janet to fetch the constables and justices, that he might be taken, tried, and executed on the spot, held him at bay there.", "그러나 고모님은, 그 무리 가운데 갑자기 그 당나귀의 보호자인 어린 악당을 알아보았는데, 그놈은 아직 열 살 남짓밖에 안 됐는데도 고모님에게 가장 뿌리 깊은 위반자 가운데 하나였던지라, 싸움터로 쏜살같이 달려 나가 그놈을 덮쳤다. 그러고는 그놈을 붙잡아, 윗도리를 머리 위로 뒤집어씌운 채, 발꿈치가 땅에 질질 끌리도록 정원으로 끌고 들어와, 재닛더러 순경과 치안판사를 데려와 그놈을 잡아다 그 자리에서 재판해 처형할 수 있게 하라고 외치며, 거기서 그놈을 옴짝달싹 못 하게 붙들었다."], ["This part of the business, however, did not last long; for the young rascal, being expert at a variety of feints and dodges, of which my aunt had no conception, soon went whooping away, leaving some deep impressions of his nailed boots in the flower-beds, and taking his donkey in triumph with him.", "그러나 이 대목은 오래가지 않았으니, 그 어린 악동은 고모님이 짐작도 못 하는 갖가지 속임수와 잔꾀에 능했던지라, 이내 휘파람을 불며 달아나면서, 화단에 못 박힌 장화 자국을 몇 군데 깊이 남기고, 자기 당나귀를 의기양양하게 끌고 가 버렸다."]], [["Miss Murdstone, during the latter portion of the contest, had dismounted, and was now waiting with her brother at the bottom of the steps, until my aunt should be at leisure to receive them.", "머드스톤 양은 싸움이 뒷부분에 이를 무렵 말에서 내려, 이제 고모님이 자기들을 맞이할 여유가 생길 때까지 오라비와 함께 층계 아래에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["My aunt, a little ruffled by the combat, marched past them into the house, with great dignity, and took no notice of their presence, until they were announced by Janet.", "고모님은 싸움으로 조금 헝클어진 채로, 대단한 위엄을 차리고 그들 곁을 지나 집 안으로 행진해 들어가서는, 재닛이 그들의 방문을 알릴 때까지 그들이 있는 줄도 모르는 체했다."]], [["‘Shall I go away, aunt?’ I asked, trembling.", "\"제가 나가 있을까요, 고모님?\" 나는 떨면서 물었다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ said my aunt.", "\"아니다, 얘야.\""], ["‘Certainly not!’ With which she pushed me into a corner near her, and fenced Me in with a chair, as if it were a prison or a bar of justice.", "고모님이 말했다. \"절대 안 돼!\" 그러면서 고모님은 나를 자기 곁 한 구석으로 밀어 넣고, 마치 그곳이 감옥이나 피고석이라도 되는 양 의자로 나를 둘러막았다."], ["This position I continued to occupy during the whole interview, and from it I now saw Mr. and Miss Murdstone enter the room.", "나는 면담이 진행되는 내내 이 자리를 지켰고, 이 자리에서 나는 이제 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양이 방으로 들어서는 걸 보았다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said my aunt, ‘I was not aware at first to whom I had the pleasure of objecting.", "\"아!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 처음에는 누구한테 항의하는 기쁨을 누리는 건지 미처 몰랐군요."], ["But I don’t allow anybody to ride over that turf. I make no exceptions.", "하지만 나는 누구든 저 풀밭 위로 말을 타고 지나가는 걸 그냥 두지 않아요. 예외는 없어요."], ["I don’t allow anybody to do it.’", "누구한테도 그러게 두지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Your regulation is rather awkward to strangers,’ said Miss Murdstone.", "\"댁의 규칙이 낯선 사람한테는 꽤 거북하군요.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다."]], [["‘Is it!’ said my aunt.", "\"그래요!\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Murdstone seemed afraid of a renewal of hostilities, and interposing began:", "머드스톤 씨는 적대 행위가 다시 불붙을까 두려운 듯, 끼어들어 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Miss Trotwood!’", "\"트롯우드 양!\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ observed my aunt with a keen look.", "\"실례합니다만,\" 고모님이 날카로운 눈빛으로 말했다."], ["‘You are the Mr. Murdstone who married the widow of my late nephew, David Copperfield, of Blunderstone Rookery!--Though why Rookery, I don’t know!’", "\"당신이, 우리 죽은 조카 데이비드 코퍼필드, 그러니까 블런더스톤 까마귀 둥지의 그 사람의 미망인과 결혼한 그 머드스톤 씨로군요!—그런데 왜 까마귀 둥지인지는 나도 모르겠지만!\""]], [["‘I am,’ said Mr. Murdstone.", "\"맞습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘You’ll excuse my saying, sir,’ returned my aunt, ‘that I think it would have been a much better and happier thing if you had left that poor child alone.’", "\"이런 말씀 드려 미안합니다만, 선생.\" 고모님이 받아쳤다. \"당신이 그 가엾은 아이를 그냥 내버려 뒀더라면 훨씬 더 좋고 행복한 일이 됐을 거라고 생각해요.\""]], [["‘I so far agree with what Miss Trotwood has remarked,’ observed Miss Murdstone, bridling, ‘that I consider our lamented Clara to have been, in all essential respects, a mere child.’", "\"트롯우드 양께서 말씀하신 바에 그만큼은 저도 동의합니다.\" 머드스톤 양이 거만하게 고개를 곧추세우며 말했다. \"저는 우리의 애통한 클라라가, 모든 본질적인 면에서, 한낱 어린애였다고 보거든요.\""]], [["‘It is a comfort to you and me, ma’am,’ said my aunt, ‘who are getting on in life, and are not likely to be made unhappy by our personal attractions, that nobody can say the same of us.’", "\"그건 우리 같은 사람들한테는 위안이 되겠군요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나이가 들어 가는 데다, 제 미모로 누굴 불행하게 만들 일도 없을 우리한테는, 우리를 두고는 아무도 그런 소리를 할 수 없을 테니 말이에요.\""]], [["‘No doubt!’ returned Miss Murdstone, though, I thought, not with a very ready or gracious assent.", "\"물론이지요!\" 머드스톤 양이 대꾸했으나, 내 생각엔 그리 선뜻 우러나오거나 우아한 동의는 아니었다."], ["‘And it certainly might have been, as you say, a better and happier thing for my brother if he had never entered into such a marriage.", "\"그리고 정말이지, 말씀대로, 제 오라비가 그런 결혼에 발을 들이지 않았더라면 더 좋고 행복한 일이 됐을 겁니다."], ["I have always been of that opinion.’", "저는 늘 그렇게 생각해 왔어요.\""]], [["‘I have no doubt you have,’ said my aunt.", "\"당연히 그러셨겠지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"재닛,\""], ["‘Janet,’ ringing the bell, ‘my compliments to Mr. Dick, and beg him to come down.’", "종을 울리며, \"딕 씨께 내 안부를 전하고, 좀 내려와 달라고 청해 다오.\""]], [["Until he came, my aunt sat perfectly upright and stiff, frowning at the wall. When he came, my aunt performed the ceremony of introduction.", "그가 내려올 때까지, 고모님은 더없이 꼿꼿하고 뻣뻣하게 앉아, 벽을 노려보고 있었다. 그가 오자, 고모님은 소개 절차를 치렀다."]], [["‘Mr. Dick.", "\"딕 씨예요."], ["An old and intimate friend.", "오래된 절친한 벗이지요. 그분의 판단을,\""], ["On whose judgement,’ said my aunt, with emphasis, as an admonition to Mr. Dick, who was biting his forefinger and looking rather foolish, ‘I rely.’", "고모님이, 검지를 깨물며 다소 멍청한 표정을 짓고 있던 딕 씨에게 일러 두려는 듯, 힘주어 말했다. \"나는 믿습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Dick took his finger out of his mouth, on this hint, and stood among the group, with a grave and attentive expression of face.", "이 귀띔에 딕 씨는 입에서 손가락을 빼고, 진지하고 주의 깊은 표정을 지으며 무리 가운데 섰다."]], [["My aunt inclined her head to Mr. Murdstone, who went on:", "고모님이 머드스톤 씨를 향해 고개를 까딱하자, 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Miss Trotwood: on the receipt of your letter, I considered it an act of greater justice to myself, and perhaps of more respect to you--’", "\"트롯우드 양. 당신의 편지를 받고, 저는 그것이 저 자신에 대한 더 큰 정의이며, 어쩌면 당신에 대한 더 큰 예의라고 여겨—\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ said my aunt, still eyeing him keenly.", "\"고맙군요.\" 고모님이 여전히 날카롭게 그를 노려보며 말했다."], ["‘You needn’t mind me.’", "\"나는 신경 쓸 것 없어요.\""]], [["‘To answer it in person, however inconvenient the journey,’ pursued Mr.", "\"여정이 아무리 불편할지언정, 편지로가 아니라 직접 만나 답하는 게 옳다고 여겼습니다.\""], ["Murdstone, ‘rather than by letter.", "머드스톤 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["This unhappy boy who has run away from his friends and his occupation--’", "\"제 벗들과 제 일자리에서 달아난 이 가엾은 아이는—\""]], [["‘And whose appearance,’ interposed his sister, directing general attention to me in my indefinable costume, ‘is perfectly scandalous and disgraceful.’", "\"그리고 이 아이의 꼴이,\" 그의 누이가 형언할 수 없는 옷차림의 나를 가리키며 끼어들었다. \"참으로 망측하고 창피스럽기 짝이 없지요.\""]], [["‘Jane Murdstone,’ said her brother, ‘have the goodness not to interrupt me.", "\"제인 머드스톤,\" 그의 오라비가 말했다. \"내 말을 끊지 말아 주면 고맙겠다."], ["This unhappy boy, Miss Trotwood, has been the occasion of much domestic trouble and uneasiness; both during the lifetime of my late dear wife, and since.", "이 가엾은 아이는, 트롯우드 양, 적잖은 집안의 말썽과 골칫거리의 원인이 돼 왔습니다. 내 사랑하는 죽은 아내가 살아 있는 동안에도, 그 뒤로도요."], ["He has a sullen, rebellious spirit; a violent temper; and an untoward, intractable disposition.", "이 아이는 음침하고 반항적인 기질에, 사나운 성미에, 비뚤어지고 다루기 힘든 성품을 지녔습니다."], ["Both my sister and myself have endeavoured to correct his vices, but ineffectually.", "제 누이와 저는 이 아이의 악덕을 바로잡으려 애썼으나, 헛수고였지요."], ["And I have felt--we both have felt, I may say; my sister being fully in my confidence--that it is right you should receive this grave and dispassionate assurance from our lips.’", "그리고 저는 느꼈습니다—우리 둘 다 느꼈다고 해도 좋겠군요, 제 누이는 제 속을 온전히 알고 있으니—이 진중하고 냉정한 단언을 우리 입으로 당신께 들려 드리는 게 옳다고 말입니다.\""]], [["‘It can hardly be necessary for me to confirm anything stated by my brother,’ said Miss Murdstone; ‘but I beg to observe, that, of all the boys in the world, I believe this is the worst boy.’", "\"제 오라비가 말한 것을 제가 굳이 확인해 드릴 필요는 없겠지요.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"하지만 한마디 짚어 두자면, 세상의 모든 사내아이 가운데, 저는 이 아이가 가장 못된 아이라고 봅니다.\""]], [["‘Strong!’ said my aunt, shortly.", "\"심한 말이군!\" 고모님이 짧게 말했다."]], [["‘But not at all too strong for the facts,’ returned Miss Murdstone.", "\"하지만 그 사실에 비하면 조금도 심한 말이 아니지요.\" 머드스톤 양이 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Ha!’ said my aunt.", "\"하!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Well, sir?’", "\"그래서요, 선생?\""]], [["‘I have my own opinions,’ resumed Mr. Murdstone, whose face darkened more and more, the more he and my aunt observed each other, which they did very narrowly, ‘as to the best mode of bringing him up; they are founded, in part, on my knowledge of him, and in part on my knowledge of my own means and resources.", "\"저에게는 이 아이를 기르는 최선의 방법에 관한 제 나름의 견해가 있습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 다시 말을 이었는데, 그와 고모님이 서로를 빤히 뜯어볼수록 그의 얼굴은 점점 더 어두워졌다."], ["I am responsible for them to myself, I act upon them, and I say no more about them.", "\"그 견해는 일부는 이 아이에 대한 제 앎에, 일부는 제 형편과 재산에 대한 제 앎에 바탕을 둔 것입니다."], ["It is enough that I place this boy under the eye of a friend of my own, in a respectable business; that it does not please him; that he runs away from it; makes himself a common vagabond about the country; and comes here, in rags, to appeal to you, Miss Trotwood.", "저는 그 견해에 대해 제 자신에게 책임이 있고, 그에 따라 행동하며, 그에 대해 더는 말하지 않겠습니다. 제가 이 아이를 점잖은 사업에 종사하는 제 벗의 눈 아래 맡겨 두었고, 이 아이는 그게 마음에 들지 않아, 그곳에서 달아나, 시골을 떠돌며 한낱 부랑아가 되고, 누더기를 걸치고 여기까지 와서 당신께 호소하고 있다, 그것으로 충분합니다."], ["I wish to set before you, honourably, the exact consequences--so far as they are within my knowledge--of your abetting him in this appeal.’", "저는 당신이 이 호소를 부추긴 데서 비롯될 정확한 결과를—제가 아는 한도 안에서—당신께 떳떳이 말씀드리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘But about the respectable business first,’ said my aunt.", "\"하지만 그 점잖은 사업부터 짚어 봅시다.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘If he had been your own boy, you would have put him to it, just the same, I suppose?’", "\"이 아이가 당신의 친자식이었더라도, 마찬가지로 그 일을 시켰을 거라고 보는데요, 아닌가요?\""]], [["‘If he had been my brother’s own boy,’ returned Miss Murdstone, striking in, ‘his character, I trust, would have been altogether different.’", "\"이 아이가 제 오라비의 친자식이었다면,\" 머드스톤 양이 끼어들며 말했다. \"그 성품이 아주 달랐을 거라고 저는 믿습니다.\""]], [["‘Or if the poor child, his mother, had been alive, he would still have gone into the respectable business, would he?’ said my aunt.", "\"아니면 이 가엾은 아이의 어머니가 아직 살아 있었더라도, 이 아이는 그 점잖은 사업에 들어갔을 텐가요, 그래요?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘I believe,’ said Mr.", "\"제 생각에는,\""], ["Murdstone, with an inclination of his head, ‘that Clara would have disputed nothing which myself and my sister Jane Murdstone were agreed was for the best.’", "머드스톤 씨가 고개를 까딱이며 말했다. \"저와 제 누이 제인 머드스톤이 가장 좋다고 합의한 것이라면, 클라라는 무엇 하나 따지지 않았을 겁니다.\""]], [["Miss Murdstone confirmed this with an audible murmur.", "머드스톤 양이 들릴 만한 중얼거림으로 이 말을 확인해 주었다."]], [["‘Humph!’ said my aunt.", "\"흥!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Unfortunate baby!’", "\"불쌍한 아기!\""]], [["Mr. Dick, who had been rattling his money all this time, was rattling it so loudly now, that my aunt felt it necessary to check him with a look, before saying:", "이 내내 자기 돈을 짤랑거리던 딕 씨가, 이제 어찌나 요란하게 짤랑거리던지, 고모님은 다음 말을 꺼내기 전에 눈짓으로 그를 말려야겠다고 느꼈다."]], [["‘The poor child’s annuity died with her?’", "\"그 가엾은 아이의 연금은 그 애가 죽으면서 함께 끝났겠지요?\""]], [["‘Died with her,’ replied Mr. Murdstone.", "\"그 사람과 함께 끝났습니다.\" 머드스톤 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘And there was no settlement of the little property--the house and garden--the what’s-its-name Rookery without any rooks in it--upon her boy?’", "\"그리고 그 자그마한 재산—집과 정원—까마귀 한 마리 없는데 까마귀 둥지라나 뭐라나 하는 그것을, 그 애의 아들 몫으로 남기는 합의 같은 건 없었고요?\""]], [["‘It had been left to her, unconditionally, by her first husband,’ Mr.", "\"그것은 그 사람의 첫 남편이 아무 조건 없이 그 사람에게 남긴 것이었습니다.\""], ["Murdstone began, when my aunt caught him up with the greatest irascibility and impatience.", "머드스톤 씨가 입을 열자, 고모님이 더없이 발끈하며 못 참겠다는 듯 말허리를 잘랐다."]], [["‘Good Lord, man, there’s no occasion to say that.", "\"맙소사, 이 양반아, 그런 말은 할 것도 없어요."], ["Left to her unconditionally!", "아무 조건 없이 남기다니!"], ["I think I see David Copperfield looking forward to any condition of any sort or kind, though it stared him point-blank in the face!", "데이비드 코퍼필드가 무슨 조건이든, 어떤 종류의 조건이든 미리 내다봤을 것 같소, 그게 눈앞에 빤히 들이닥쳤더라도 말이오!"], ["Of course it was left to her unconditionally.", "당연히 아무 조건 없이 남겼겠지."], ["But when she married again--when she took that most disastrous step of marrying you, in short,’ said my aunt, ‘to be plain--did no one put in a word for the boy at that time?’", "하지만 그 애가 재혼했을 때—요컨대, 당신과 결혼하는 그 더없이 끔찍한 걸음을 내디뎠을 때,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"솔직히 말해서—그때 그 애 아들을 위해 한마디 거들어 준 사람은 아무도 없었소?\""]], [["‘My late wife loved her second husband, ma’am,’ said Mr.", "\"제 죽은 아내는 두 번째 남편을 사랑했고, 부인,\""], ["Murdstone, ‘and trusted implicitly in him.’", "머드스톤 씨가 말했다. \"그를 온전히 믿었습니다.\""]], [["‘Your late wife, sir, was a most unworldly, most unhappy, most unfortunate baby,’ returned my aunt, shaking her head at him.", "\"당신의 죽은 아내는, 선생, 더없이 세상 물정 모르고, 더없이 불행하고, 더없이 불운한 아기였소.\" 고모님이 그를 향해 고개를 흔들며 받아쳤다."], ["‘That’s what she was.", "\"그게 그 애였소."], ["And now, what have you got to say next?’", "자, 그래서 그다음엔 또 무슨 할 말이 있소?\""]], [["‘Merely this, Miss Trotwood,’ he returned.", "\"그저 이겁니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 그가 대꾸했다."], ["‘I am here to take David back--to take him back unconditionally, to dispose of him as I think proper, and to deal with him as I think right.", "\"저는 데이비드를 도로 데려가려고 여기 왔습니다—아무 조건 없이 데려가서, 제가 옳다고 여기는 대로 처분하고, 제가 옳다고 여기는 대로 다루려고 말입니다."], ["I am not here to make any promise, or give any pledge to anybody.", "저는 누구에게도 무슨 약속을 하거나 무슨 다짐을 하려고 온 게 아닙니다."], ["You may possibly have some idea, Miss Trotwood, of abetting him in his running away, and in his complaints to you.", "트롯우드 양, 당신은 어쩌면 이 아이가 달아난 것이며 당신께 호소한 것을 부추길 생각을 품고 있을지도 모르겠습니다."], ["Your manner, which I must say does not seem intended to propitiate, induces me to think it possible.", "당신의 태도가, 솔직히 말해 화해를 꾀하는 것으로는 도무지 보이지 않으니, 그럴 법도 하다는 생각이 듭니다."], ["Now I must caution you that if you abet him once, you abet him for good and all; if you step in between him and me, now, you must step in, Miss Trotwood, for ever.", "자, 미리 경고해 두는데, 당신이 이 아이를 한번 부추기면, 영영 부추기는 셈입니다. 지금 당신이 이 아이와 저 사이에 끼어든다면, 트롯우드 양, 당신은 영원히 끼어들어야 합니다."], ["I cannot trifle, or be trifled with. I am here, for the first and last time, to take him away.", "저는 장난칠 줄도 모르고, 저를 가지고 장난치게 둘 사람도 아닙니다. 저는 처음이자 마지막으로 이 아이를 데려가려고 여기 왔습니다."], ["Is he ready to go? If he is not--and you tell me he is not; on any pretence; it is indifferent to me what--my doors are shut against him henceforth, and yours, I take it for granted, are open to him.’", "이 아이는 갈 준비가 됐습니까? 만약 그렇지 않다면—당신이 그렇지 않다고 한다면, 무슨 핑계로든, 무슨 핑계인지는 제게 상관없습니다만—제 문은 이제부터 이 아이에게 닫혀 있을 테고, 당신 문은, 당연히, 이 아이에게 열려 있겠지요.\""]], [["To this address, my aunt had listened with the closest attention, sitting perfectly upright, with her hands folded on one knee, and looking grimly on the speaker.", "이 말에, 고모님은 더없이 꼿꼿이 앉아, 두 손을 한쪽 무릎에 포갠 채, 말하는 이를 험상궂게 노려보며 빈틈없이 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["When he had finished, she turned her eyes so as to command Miss Murdstone, without otherwise disturbing her attitude, and said:", "그가 말을 마치자, 고모님은 자세는 전혀 흩뜨리지 않고 눈만 돌려 머드스톤 양을 똑바로 겨누며 말했다."]], [["‘Well, ma’am, have YOU got anything to remark?’", "\"자, 부인, 당신은 뭐 할 말이 있소?\""]], [["‘Indeed, Miss Trotwood,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘all that I could say has been so well said by my brother, and all that I know to be the fact has been so plainly stated by him, that I have nothing to add except my thanks for your politeness.", "\"정말이지, 트롯우드 양.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"제가 할 수 있는 말은 제 오라비가 죄다 훌륭하게 해 주었고, 제가 사실로 아는 바도 다 그가 똑똑히 밝혔으니, 저는 당신의 정중함에 감사드린다는 말 외에 덧붙일 게 없군요."], ["For your very great politeness, I am sure,’ said Miss Murdstone; with an irony which no more affected my aunt, than it discomposed the cannon I had slept by at Chatham.", "정말이지 그 대단한 정중함에 말이에요.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했는데, 그 빈정거림은, 내가 채텀에서 그 곁에 잠들었던 대포를 흔들지 못했던 만큼이나 고모님을 흔들지 못했다."]], [["‘And what does the boy say?’ said my aunt.", "\"그래, 이 아이는 뭐라고 하느냐?\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Are you ready to go, David?’", "\"갈 준비가 됐느냐, 데이비드?\""]], [["I answered no, and entreated her not to let me go.", "나는 싫다고 대답하며, 제발 저를 보내지 말아 달라고 고모님께 간청했다."], ["I said that neither Mr. nor Miss Murdstone had ever liked me, or had ever been kind to me.", "나는 머드스톤 씨도 머드스톤 양도 나를 좋아한 적이 없고, 나에게 다정했던 적도 없다고 말했다."], ["That they had made my mama, who always loved me dearly, unhappy about me, and that I knew it well, and that Peggotty knew it.", "늘 나를 끔찍이 사랑했던 우리 엄마를 그들이 나 때문에 불행하게 만들었고, 나는 그걸 잘 알고 있으며, 패거티도 알고 있다고 말했다."], ["I said that I had been more miserable than I thought anybody could believe, who only knew how young I was.", "나는 내가 얼마나 어린지만 아는 사람이라면 누구도 믿지 못할 만큼 비참했다고 말했다."], ["And I begged and prayed my aunt--I forget in what terms now, but I remember that they affected me very much then--to befriend and protect me, for my father’s sake.", "그리고 나는 고모님께, 우리 아버지를 봐서라도 나를 벗 삼아 지켜 달라고 빌고 또 빌었다—지금은 그게 무슨 말이었는지 잊었지만, 그때는 그 말들이 나 자신을 몹시 북받치게 했던 게 기억난다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"딕 씨,\""], ["Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘what shall I do with this child?’", "고모님이 말했다. \"이 아이를 어쩌면 좋겠어요?\""]], [["Mr. Dick considered, hesitated, brightened, and rejoined, ‘Have him measured for a suit of clothes directly.’", "딕 씨는 곰곰 생각하고, 머뭇거리고, 얼굴이 환해지더니, 대꾸했다. \"당장 옷 한 벌 맞추게 치수를 재게 하시죠.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"딕 씨,\" 고모님이 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["Dick,’ said my aunt triumphantly, ‘give me your hand, for your common sense is invaluable.’ Having shaken it with great cordiality, she pulled me towards her and said to Mr. Murdstone:", "\"손 좀 줘 봐요, 당신의 그 상식은 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼 귀하니까.\" 고모님은 더없이 다정하게 그 손을 흔들고 나서, 나를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 머드스톤 씨에게 말했다."]], [["‘You can go when you like; I’ll take my chance with the boy.", "\"가고 싶을 때 가시오. 나는 이 아이에게 운을 걸어 보겠소."], ["If he’s all you say he is, at least I can do as much for him then, as you have done.", "이 아이가 당신 말 그대로라면, 그때는 적어도 당신이 한 만큼은 나도 이 아이를 위해 해 줄 수 있을 테니."], ["But I don’t believe a word of it.’", "하지만 나는 그 말을 한마디도 안 믿어요.\""]], [["‘Miss Trotwood,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"트롯우드 양,\""], ["Murdstone, shrugging his shoulders, as he rose, ‘if you were a gentleman--’", "머드스톤 씨가 일어서며 어깨를 으쓱하고 대꾸했다. \"당신이 신사였더라면—\""]], [["‘Bah!", "\"흥!"], ["Stuff and nonsense!’ said my aunt.", "헛소리에 어림없는 소리!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Don’t talk to me!’", "\"나한테 말 걸지 말아요!\""]], [["‘How exquisitely polite!’ exclaimed Miss Murdstone, rising.", "\"어쩌면 이리도 절묘하게 공손하실까!\" 머드스톤 양이 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["‘Overpowering, really!’", "\"정말이지, 압도적이네요!\""]], [["‘Do you think I don’t know,’ said my aunt, turning a deaf ear to the sister, and continuing to address the brother, and to shake her head at him with infinite expression, ‘what kind of life you must have led that poor, unhappy, misdirected baby?", "\"내가 모를 줄 아시오,\" 고모님이 그 누이는 들은 척도 않고, 계속 그 오라비를 향해 말하며, 한없는 표정을 담아 그를 향해 고개를 흔들며 말했다. \"당신이 그 가엾고 불행하고 그릇 이끌린 아기를 어떤 삶으로 몰아넣었을지를?"], ["Do you think I don’t know what a woeful day it was for the soft little creature when you first came in her way--smirking and making great eyes at her, I’ll be bound, as if you couldn’t say boh! to a goose!’", "그 여리고 자그마한 사람이 당신과 처음 마주친 게 얼마나 비통한 날이었을지를 내가 모를 줄 아시오?—거위한테 '워이!' 소리 한 번 못 칠 사람인 양, 능글맞게 웃으며 그 애한테 눈웃음을 쳐 댔겠지, 내 장담하건대!\""]], [["‘I never heard anything so elegant!’ said Miss Murdstone.", "\"이렇게 우아한 말은 난생처음 듣네!\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다."]], [["‘Do you think I can’t understand you as well as if I had seen you,’ pursued my aunt, ‘now that I DO see and hear you--which, I tell you candidly, is anything but a pleasure to me?", "\"내가 당신을 직접 본 것처럼 훤히 꿰뚫고 있다는 걸 모를 줄 아시오,\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"이제 정말로 당신을 보고 듣고 있는 마당에—솔직히 말하건대, 그건 나한테 도무지 기쁜 일이 아니지만 말이오?"], ["Oh yes, bless us!", "아, 그렇고말고, 맙소사!"], ["who so smooth and silky as Mr. Murdstone at first!", "처음엔 머드스톤 씨만큼 매끄럽고 비단결 같은 사람이 어디 있었겠소!"], ["The poor, benighted innocent had never seen such a man.", "그 가엾고 어리석은 순진한 사람은 그런 남자를 본 적이 없었지."], ["He was made of sweetness.", "당신은 달콤함 그 자체였어."], ["He worshipped her.", "당신은 그 애를 떠받들었지."], ["He doted on her boy--tenderly doted on him!", "그 애의 아들을 끔찍이 아꼈고—한없이 다정하게 아꼈고말고!"], ["He was to be another father to him, and they were all to live together in a garden of roses, weren’t they? Ugh!", "당신은 그 애한테 또 하나의 아버지가 되어 줄 거였고, 다들 장미 정원에서 오순도순 함께 살 거였지, 안 그랬소? 으윽!"], ["Get along with you, do!’ said my aunt.", "썩 꺼지시오, 어서!\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘I never heard anything like this person in my life!’ exclaimed Miss Murdstone.", "\"내 평생 이런 사람은 처음 봐!\" 머드스톤 양이 외쳤다."]], [["‘And when you had made sure of the poor little fool,’ said my aunt--‘God forgive me that I should call her so, and she gone where YOU won’t go in a hurry--because you had not done wrong enough to her and hers, you must begin to train her, must you?", "\"그리고 그 가엾은 어린 바보를 손에 넣었다 확신하고 나자,\" 고모님이 말했다—\"그 애를 그렇게 부르다니 하느님 용서하소서, 게다가 그 애는 당신이 서둘러 갈 일 없는 그곳으로 가 버렸는데—당신은 그 애와 그 애의 식구한테 아직 충분히 못된 짓을 못 했다 싶어, 그 애를 길들이기 시작해야만 했지, 안 그렇소?"], ["begin to break her, like a poor caged bird, and wear her deluded life away, in teaching her to sing YOUR notes?’", "가엾은 새장 속 새처럼 그 애를 꺾어 버리고, 당신의 가락을 노래하라고 가르치며 그 속아 넘어간 목숨을 시들어 가게 해야만 했지?\""]], [["‘This is either insanity or intoxication,’ said Miss Murdstone, in a perfect agony at not being able to turn the current of my aunt’s address towards herself; ‘and my suspicion is that it’s intoxication.’", "\"이건 미친 거 아니면 취한 거야.\" 머드스톤 양이 고모님의 말줄기를 자기 쪽으로 돌리지 못해 완전히 안달이 나서 말했다. \"내 짐작엔 취한 게 분명해.\""]], [["Miss Betsey, without taking the least notice of the interruption, continued to address herself to Mr. Murdstone as if there had been no such thing.", "베시 아주머니는 이 끼어듦에는 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳없이, 그런 일이라곤 없었던 양 계속 머드스톤 씨만 상대로 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Murdstone,’ she said, shaking her finger at him, ‘you were a tyrant to the simple baby, and you broke her heart.", "\"머드스톤 씨,\" 고모님이 그를 향해 손가락질하며 말했다."], ["She was a loving baby--I know that; I knew it, years before you ever saw her--and through the best part of her weakness you gave her the wounds she died of.", "\"당신은 그 순진한 아기한테 폭군이었고, 그 애의 가슴을 찢어 놓았소. 그 애는 사랑이 넘치는 아기였소—나는 그걸 알아요. 당신이 그 애를 보기 몇 해 전부터 알고 있었지—그리고 당신은 그 애의 가장 여린 약점을 파고들어, 그 애가 그 때문에 죽은 그 상처를 입혔소."], ["There is the truth for your comfort, however you like it.", "당신 좋을 대로 받아들이든 말든, 위안 삼으라고 일러 주는 진실이오."], ["And you and your instruments may make the most of it.’", "당신과 당신의 도구들이나 그걸 실컷 우려먹든지.\""]], [["‘Allow me to inquire, Miss Trotwood,’ interposed Miss Murdstone, ‘whom you are pleased to call, in a choice of words in which I am not experienced, my brother’s instruments?’", "\"한 가지 여쭙겠는데요, 트롯우드 양.\" 머드스톤 양이 끼어들었다. \"제가 익숙지 않은 그 골라 쓰신 말솜씨로, 누구를 제 오라비의 도구라고 부르시는 건지요?\""]], [["‘It was clear enough, as I have told you, years before YOU ever saw her--and why, in the mysterious dispensations of Providence, you ever did see her, is more than humanity can comprehend--it was clear enough that the poor soft little thing would marry somebody, at some time or other; but I did hope it wouldn’t have been as bad as it has turned out.", "\"내가 말했듯이, 당신이 그 애를 보기 몇 해 전부터 빤한 일이었소—그리고 섭리의 그 알 수 없는 작정 속에서 당신이 대체 어쩌다 그 애를 보게 됐는지는, 사람의 머리로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 일이지만—그 가엾고 여린 자그마한 사람이 언젠가는 누군가와 결혼하리라는 건 빤한 일이었소. 하지만 나는 일이 이렇게까지 고약하게 풀리지는 않기를 바랐단 말이오. 그때였소, 머드스톤 씨, 그 애가 여기서 이 아이를 낳은 게,\""], ["That was the time, Mr.", "고모님이 말했다."], ["Murdstone, when she gave birth to her boy here,’ said my aunt; ‘to the poor child you sometimes tormented her through afterwards, which is a disagreeable remembrance and makes the sight of him odious now.", "\"당신이 나중에 그 애를 괴롭힐 때 그 빌미로 삼았던 이 가엾은 아이를 말이오. 그건 떠올리기조차 거북한 기억이라, 이제 이 아이를 보는 것마저 역겹게 만드는구려."], ["Aye, aye!", "그래, 그래!"], ["you needn’t wince!’ said my aunt.", "움찔할 것 없소!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘I know it’s true without that.’", "\"그러지 않아도 그게 사실이라는 건 나도 아니까.\""]], [["He had stood by the door, all this while, observant of her with a smile upon his face, though his black eyebrows were heavily contracted.", "그는 이 내내 문가에 서서, 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 고모님을 지켜보고 있었으나, 그 검은 눈썹은 무겁게 찌푸려져 있었다."], ["I remarked now, that, though the smile was on his face still, his colour had gone in a moment, and he seemed to breathe as if he had been running.", "나는 이제, 미소는 여전히 그 얼굴에 떠 있었지만, 그의 핏기가 한순간에 가셨고, 마치 달음박질이라도 친 사람처럼 숨을 몰아쉬는 듯한 걸 알아챘다."]], [["‘Good day, sir,’ said my aunt, ‘and good-bye!", "\"안녕히 가시오, 선생.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["Good day to you, too, ma’am,’ said my aunt, turning suddenly upon his sister.", "\"그리고 잘 가시오! 당신도 안녕히 가시고, 부인.\" 고모님이 갑자기 그 누이에게로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["‘Let me see you ride a donkey over my green again, and as sure as you have a head upon your shoulders, I’ll knock your bonnet off, and tread upon it!’", "\"다시 한번 내 풀밭 위로 당나귀를 타고 지나가기만 해 봐, 당신 어깨 위에 머리가 붙어 있는 게 확실한 만큼이나 확실하게, 내 당신 모자를 후려쳐 떨어뜨리고 그걸 짓밟아 줄 테니!\""]], [["It would require a painter, and no common painter too, to depict my aunt’s face as she delivered herself of this very unexpected sentiment, and Miss Murdstone’s face as she heard it.", "이 너무도 뜻밖의 선언을 내뱉을 때의 고모님 얼굴과, 그것을 들을 때의 머드스톤 양 얼굴을 그려 내려면, 화가가, 그것도 여간한 화가가 아니라 대단한 화가가 필요할 것이다."], ["But the manner of the speech, no less than the matter, was so fiery, that Miss Murdstone, without a word in answer, discreetly put her arm through her brother’s, and walked haughtily out of the cottage; my aunt remaining in the window looking after them; prepared, I have no doubt, in case of the donkey’s reappearance, to carry her threat into instant execution.", "그러나 그 말씨는 그 내용 못지않게 어찌나 불같았던지, 머드스톤 양은 대꾸 한마디 없이, 슬며시 오라비의 팔짱을 끼고 거만하게 오두막을 나섰다. 고모님은 창가에 남아 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보았는데, 당나귀가 다시 나타날 경우에 대비해, 그 위협을 즉시 실행에 옮길 채비를 하고 있었으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다."]], [["No attempt at defiance being made, however, her face gradually relaxed, and became so pleasant, that I was emboldened to kiss and thank her; which I did with great heartiness, and with both my arms clasped round her neck. I then shook hands with Mr.", "그러나 아무런 도전의 기미도 보이지 않자, 고모님의 얼굴은 차츰 풀어져 어찌나 흐뭇해졌던지, 나는 용기를 내어 고모님께 입을 맞추고 감사를 드렸다. 나는 두 팔을 고모님 목에 둘러 감고, 더없이 진심으로 그렇게 했다."], ["Dick, who shook hands with me a great many times, and hailed this happy close of the proceedings with repeated bursts of laughter.", "그러고 나서 나는 딕 씨와 악수를 나눴는데, 딕 씨는 나와 몇 번이고 악수하며, 이 즐거운 결말을 거듭거듭 웃음을 터뜨리며 반겨 주었다."]], [["‘You’ll consider yourself guardian, jointly with me, of this child, Mr.", "\"딕 씨, 당신이 나와 함께 이 아이의 공동 후견인이 되어 주는 걸로 알겠어요.\""], ["Dick,’ said my aunt.", "고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘I shall be delighted,’ said Mr.", "\"저야 더없이 기쁘지요.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["Dick, ‘to be the guardian of David’s son.’", "\"데이비드 아들의 후견인이 된다니요.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ returned my aunt, ‘that’s settled.", "\"좋아요.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"그럼 그걸로 정해졌고."], ["I have been thinking, do you know, Mr. Dick, that I might call him Trotwood?’", "내가 생각해 봤는데 말이에요, 딕 씨, 이 아이를 트롯우드라고 부르면 어떨까 해요.\""]], [["‘Certainly, certainly.", "\"그럼요, 그럼요."], ["Call him Trotwood, certainly,’ said Mr. Dick.", "트롯우드라고 부르세요, 그럼요.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["‘David’s son’s Trotwood.’", "\"데이비드 아들은 트롯우드.\""]], [["‘Trotwood Copperfield, you mean,’ returned my aunt.", "\"트롯우드 코퍼필드라는 뜻이에요.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Yes, to be sure. Yes.", "\"네, 그렇고말고요. 네."], ["Trotwood Copperfield,’ said Mr. Dick, a little abashed.", "트롯우드 코퍼필드.\" 딕 씨가 조금 머쓱해하며 말했다."]], [["My aunt took so kindly to the notion, that some ready-made clothes, which were purchased for me that afternoon, were marked ‘Trotwood Copperfield’, in her own handwriting, and in indelible marking-ink, before I put them on; and it was settled that all the other clothes which were ordered to be made for me (a complete outfit was bespoke that afternoon) should be marked in the same way.", "고모님은 이 생각이 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 그날 오후에 나를 위해 사 온 기성복 몇 벌에, 내가 그것을 입기도 전에, 고모님이 손수, 지워지지 않는 표시용 잉크로, '트롯우드 코퍼필드'라고 표시를 해 두었다. 그리고 나를 위해 새로 짓기로 주문한 다른 옷가지들(그날 오후에 한 벌 빠짐없이 갖춰진 차림새를 통째로 맞췄다)도 죄다 같은 식으로 표시하기로 정해졌다."]], [["Thus I began my new life, in a new name, and with everything new about me.", "이리하여 나는 새 이름으로, 온통 새로운 것들에 둘러싸여 새 삶을 시작했다."], ["Now that the state of doubt was over, I felt, for many days, like one in a dream.", "이제 막막함의 시간이 끝나고 나니, 나는 여러 날 동안 꿈을 꾸는 사람 같았다."], ["I never thought that I had a curious couple of guardians, in my aunt and Mr. Dick.", "나는 고모님과 딕 씨라는, 한 쌍의 진기한 후견인을 두었다는 생각 따위는 한 번도 하지 않았다."], ["I never thought of anything about myself, distinctly.", "나는 나 자신에 관해서도 무엇 하나 또렷이 생각하지 않았다."], ["The two things clearest in my mind were, that a remoteness had come upon the old Blunderstone life--which seemed to lie in the haze of an immeasurable distance; and that a curtain had for ever fallen on my life at Murdstone and Grinby’s.", "내 마음속에 가장 또렷한 두 가지는, 옛 블런더스톤 시절이 아득히 멀어져—헤아릴 수 없는 거리의 안개 속에 누워 있는 듯했다는 것, 그리고 머드스톤 앤드 그린비에서의 내 삶 위로 영영 막이 내려졌다는 것이었다."], ["No one has ever raised that curtain since.", "그 후로 아무도 그 막을 걷어 올린 적이 없다."], ["I have lifted it for a moment, even in this narrative, with a reluctant hand, and dropped it gladly.", "나는 이 이야기 속에서조차, 마지못한 손길로 잠깐 그것을 들어 올렸다가, 기꺼이 도로 내려놓았다."], ["The remembrance of that life is fraught with so much pain to me, with so much mental suffering and want of hope, that I have never had the courage even to examine how long I was doomed to lead it.", "그 삶의 기억은 나에게 너무도 많은 고통과, 너무도 많은 마음의 괴로움과 절망으로 가득 차 있어서, 나는 내가 얼마나 오래 그 삶을 살도록 운명 지어졌었는지 헤아려 볼 엄두조차 한 번도 내지 못했다."], ["Whether it lasted for a year, or more, or less, I do not know.", "그것이 일 년 동안 이어졌는지, 그보다 길었는지, 짧았는지, 나는 모른다."], ["I only know that it was, and ceased to be; and that I have written, and there I leave it.", "다만 그것이 있었고, 그쳤다는 것, 그리고 내가 그것을 적었으니, 이제 거기 그대로 두려 한다는 것만 알 뿐이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 15. I MAKE ANOTHER BEGINNING", "# CHAPTER 15. I MAKE ANOTHER BEGINNING"]], [["Mr. Dick and I soon became the best of friends, and very often, when his day’s work was done, went out together to fly the great kite.", "딕 씨와 나는 곧 둘도 없는 벗이 되었고, 그가 하루 일과를 마치고 나면 우리는 함께 밖으로 나가 그 커다란 연을 날리곤 했다."], ["Every day of his life he had a long sitting at the Memorial, which never made the least progress, however hard he laboured, for King Charles the First always strayed into it, sooner or later, and then it was thrown aside, and another one begun.", "그는 날마다 진정서 앞에 오래도록 앉아 있었지만, 아무리 애를 써도 그 일은 조금도 진척이 없었다. 찰스 일세 임금님이 늦든 빠르든 늘 그 안으로 슬그머니 끼어들었고, 그러면 그 종이는 한쪽으로 내던져지고 새로운 종이가 시작되었기 때문이다."], ["The patience and hope with which he bore these perpetual disappointments, the mild perception he had that there was something wrong about King Charles the First, the feeble efforts he made to keep him out, and the certainty with which he came in, and tumbled the Memorial out of all shape, made a deep impression on me.", "끊임없이 되풀이되는 이 좌절을 견디는 그의 인내와 희망, 찰스 일세 임금님에게 뭔가 잘못된 구석이 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 느끼는 그 헤아림, 임금님을 들어오지 못하게 막으려는 그 가냘픈 안간힘, 그러고도 임금님이 어김없이 들어와 진정서를 온통 엉망으로 헝클어 놓는 그 확실함은 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["What Mr. Dick supposed would come of the Memorial, if it were completed; where he thought it was to go, or what he thought it was to do; he knew no more than anybody else, I believe.", "만일 진정서가 완성된다면 거기서 무엇이 나올 것이라고 딕 씨가 여겼는지, 그것이 어디로 가야 한다고 생각했는지, 또 그것이 무슨 일을 해야 한다고 여겼는지는, 내 생각에 그 자신도 다른 누구 못지않게 전혀 몰랐다."], ["Nor was it at all necessary that he should trouble himself with such questions, for if anything were certain under the sun, it was certain that the Memorial never would be finished.", "그렇다고 그가 그런 물음으로 골머리를 앓을 필요도 전혀 없었으니, 이 하늘 아래 무엇 하나가 확실하다면, 그 진정서는 결코 완성되지 못하리라는 것이야말로 확실했기 때문이다."], ["It was quite an affecting sight, I used to think, to see him with the kite when it was up a great height in the air.", "연이 공중 까마득히 높이 떠올랐을 때 그것을 붙들고 선 그의 모습은, 내 생각에 정말이지 가슴 뭉클한 광경이었다."], ["What he had told me, in his room, about his belief in its disseminating the statements pasted on it, which were nothing but old leaves of abortive Memorials, might have been a fancy with him sometimes; but not when he was out, looking up at the kite in the sky, and feeling it pull and tug at his hand.", "연에 붙여 놓은 글귀들 — 그것은 미완으로 끝난 진정서의 낡은 종이쪽지에 지나지 않았다 — 이 그렇게 세상에 널리 퍼진다고 믿는다며 자기 방에서 나에게 들려준 이야기는, 어쩌면 평소엔 그저 그의 공상이었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그가 밖에 나와 하늘의 연을 올려다보며, 연이 그의 손을 잡아당기고 끌어채는 것을 느낄 때만큼은 그렇지 않았다."], ["He never looked so serene as he did then.", "그는 그때만큼 마음이 평온해 보인 적이 없었다."], ["I used to fancy, as I sat by him of an evening, on a green slope, and saw him watch the kite high in the quiet air, that it lifted his mind out of its confusion, and bore it (such was my boyish thought) into the skies.", "저녁이면 푸른 비탈에 그의 곁에 앉아, 고요한 하늘 높이 뜬 연을 바라보는 그를 지켜보면서, 나는 그 연이 그의 어지러운 마음을 들어 올려 (이것이 어린 내가 품은 생각이었다) 하늘로 데려가는 거라고 곧잘 상상하곤 했다."], ["As he wound the string in and it came lower and lower down out of the beautiful light, until it fluttered to the ground, and lay there like a dead thing, he seemed to wake gradually out of a dream; and I remember to have seen him take it up, and look about him in a lost way, as if they had both come down together, so that I pitied him with all my heart.", "그가 연줄을 감아들이면 연은 그 아름다운 빛 속에서 점점 아래로 아래로 내려와, 마침내 땅으로 펄럭이며 떨어져 죽은 것처럼 누워 버렸는데, 그러면 그는 차츰 꿈에서 깨어나는 듯 보였다. 그리고 내가 본 기억으로는, 그는 연을 집어 들고 마치 둘이 함께 떨어지기라도 한 양 넋 나간 얼굴로 둘레를 두리번거리곤 했으니, 나는 진심으로 그가 가엾었다."]], [["While I advanced in friendship and intimacy with Mr. Dick, I did not go backward in the favour of his staunch friend, my aunt.", "딕 씨와의 우정과 친밀함이 깊어지는 동안, 나는 그의 든든한 벗인 고모님의 사랑에서도 뒤처지지 않았다."], ["She took so kindly to me, that, in the course of a few weeks, she shortened my adopted name of Trotwood into Trot; and even encouraged me to hope, that if I went on as I had begun, I might take equal rank in her affections with my sister Betsey Trotwood.", "고모님은 어찌나 나를 살갑게 여겼던지, 몇 주가 지나는 사이에 트롯우드라는 내 양자 이름을 트롯으로 줄여 불렀고, 심지어 내가 시작한 대로 계속해 나간다면 죽은 베시 트롯우드 누이와 똑같은 자리를 자기 마음속에서 차지할 수 있을 거라는 희망까지 품게 해 주었다."]], [["‘Trot,’ said my aunt one evening, when the backgammon-board was placed as usual for herself and Mr.", "\"트롯,\" 어느 날 저녁, 여느 때처럼 고모님과 딕 씨를 위해 주사위 놀이판이 차려졌을 때 고모님이 말했다."], ["Dick, ‘we must not forget your education.’", "\"네 교육을 잊으면 안 되지.\""]], [["This was my only subject of anxiety, and I felt quite delighted by her referring to it.", "이것은 내가 유일하게 마음 졸이던 일이었던지라, 고모님이 그 이야기를 꺼내자 나는 무척 기뻤다."]], [["‘Should you like to go to school at Canterbury?’ said my aunt.", "\"캔터베리에 있는 학교에 다니고 싶니?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["I replied that I should like it very much, as it was so near her.", "나는 그 학교가 고모님과 아주 가까우니 정말 다니고 싶다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Good,’ said my aunt.", "\"좋아,\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Should you like to go tomorrow?’", "\"내일 가고 싶으냐?\""]], [["Being already no stranger to the general rapidity of my aunt’s evolutions, I was not surprised by the suddenness of the proposal, and said: ‘Yes.’", "이미 고모님의 일 처리가 대체로 얼마나 잽싼지 익히 알던 터라, 나는 그 느닷없는 제안에도 놀라지 않고 \"네.\"라고 말했다."]], [["‘Good,’ said my aunt again.", "\"좋아,\" 고모님이 다시 말했다."], ["‘Janet, hire the grey pony and chaise tomorrow morning at ten o’clock, and pack up Master Trotwood’s clothes tonight.’", "\"재닛, 내일 아침 열 시에 회색 조랑말과 마차를 빌리고, 트롯우드 도련님 옷가지를 오늘 밤에 챙겨 두어라.\""]], [["I was greatly elated by these orders; but my heart smote me for my selfishness, when I witnessed their effect on Mr.", "이 명령에 나는 무척 들떴다. 그러나 그 명령이 딕 씨에게 미친 영향을 보자 내 이기심에 가슴이 찔렸다."], ["Dick, who was so low-spirited at the prospect of our separation, and played so ill in consequence, that my aunt, after giving him several admonitory raps on the knuckles with her dice-box, shut up the board, and declined to play with him any more.", "그는 우리가 헤어진다는 생각에 어찌나 풀이 죽었던지 놀이도 형편없이 해 버렸고, 그러자 고모님은 주사위 통으로 그의 손가락 마디를 몇 번 톡톡 나무라듯 친 뒤, 판을 접고 더는 그와 놀이하기를 마다했다."], ["But, on hearing from my aunt that I should sometimes come over on a Saturday, and that he could sometimes come and see me on a Wednesday, he revived; and vowed to make another kite for those occasions, of proportions greatly surpassing the present one.", "그러나 내가 가끔 토요일에 들르고, 그도 가끔 수요일에 나를 보러 올 수 있다는 말을 고모님에게 듣자, 그는 기운을 되찾았다. 그러면서 그런 날을 위해 지금 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 새 연을 만들겠노라 다짐했다."], ["In the morning he was downhearted again, and would have sustained himself by giving me all the money he had in his possession, gold and silver too, if my aunt had not interposed, and limited the gift to five shillings, which, at his earnest petition, were afterwards increased to ten.", "아침이 되자 그는 다시 시무룩해졌고, 자기가 가진 돈을 죄다 — 금화고 은화고 할 것 없이 — 나에게 줌으로써 마음을 다잡으려 했으나, 고모님이 끼어들어 그 선물을 오 실링으로 제한했다. 그러나 그 돈은 그가 간곡히 청한 끝에 나중에 십 실링으로 늘어났다."], ["We parted at the garden-gate in a most affectionate manner, and Mr. Dick did not go into the house until my aunt had driven me out of sight of it.", "우리는 정원 문에서 더없이 다정하게 작별했고, 딕 씨는 고모님이 마차를 몰아 내가 집에서 보이지 않게 될 때까지 안으로 들어가지 않았다."]], [["My aunt, who was perfectly indifferent to public opinion, drove the grey pony through Dover in a masterly manner; sitting high and stiff like a state coachman, keeping a steady eye upon him wherever he went, and making a point of not letting him have his own way in any respect.", "세상의 평판 따위는 도통 개의치 않는 고모님은 회색 조랑말을 능숙하게 몰아 도버를 지나갔다. 마치 궁정 마부처럼 높직하고 꼿꼿하게 앉아, 조랑말이 어디로 가든 한결같이 매서운 눈으로 지켜보았고, 어떤 점에서도 제멋대로 굴게 두지 않으려 단단히 마음먹은 채였다."], ["When we came into the country road, she permitted him to relax a little, however; and looking at me down in a valley of cushion by her side, asked me whether I was happy?", "그래도 시골길로 접어들자 고모님은 조랑말이 조금쯤 느슨해지도록 허락했고, 곁에 방석 골짜기에 파묻혀 앉은 나를 내려다보며 행복하냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Very happy indeed, thank you, aunt,’ I said.", "\"네, 정말 행복해요, 고모님, 고맙습니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["She was much gratified; and both her hands being occupied, patted me on the head with her whip.", "고모님은 무척 흐뭇해했다. 그러나 두 손이 다 막혀 있던 터라, 채찍으로 내 머리를 토닥였다."]], [["‘Is it a large school, aunt?’ I asked.", "\"큰 학교인가요, 고모님?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, I don’t know,’ said my aunt.", "\"글쎄, 나도 잘 모르겠다.\""], ["‘We are going to Mr. Wickfield’s first.’", "고모님이 말했다. \"우선 위크필드 씨한테 들를 거야.\""]], [["‘Does he keep a school?’ I asked.", "\"그분이 학교를 운영하나요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘No, Trot,’ said my aunt.", "\"아니다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘He keeps an office.’", "\"사무소를 운영하지.\""]], [["I asked for no more information about Mr. Wickfield, as she offered none, and we conversed on other subjects until we came to Canterbury, where, as it was market-day, my aunt had a great opportunity of insinuating the grey pony among carts, baskets, vegetables, and huckster’s goods.", "고모님이 더는 알려 주지 않기에 나도 위크필드 씨에 대해 더 묻지 않았고, 우리는 캔터베리에 닿을 때까지 다른 이야기를 나누었다. 마침 장날이라 고모님은 짐수레며 바구니며 채소며 행상꾼의 물건 사이로 회색 조랑말을 비집어 넣을 절호의 기회를 잡았다."], ["The hair-breadth turns and twists we made, drew down upon us a variety of speeches from the people standing about, which were not always complimentary; but my aunt drove on with perfect indifference, and I dare say would have taken her own way with as much coolness through an enemy’s country.", "우리가 머리카락 한 올 너비로 아슬아슬하게 틀고 꺾는 통에, 둘레에 서 있던 사람들에게서 갖가지 한마디씩이 쏟아졌는데, 늘 칭찬은 아니었다. 그러나 고모님은 더없이 태연하게 마차를 몰았고, 감히 말하건대 적의 나라를 가로지른다 해도 그만큼 침착하게 제 길을 갔을 것이다."]], [["At length we stopped before a very old house bulging out over the road; a house with long low lattice-windows bulging out still farther, and beams with carved heads on the ends bulging out too, so that I fancied the whole house was leaning forward, trying to see who was passing on the narrow pavement below.", "마침내 우리는 길 위로 불룩 튀어나온 아주 오래된 집 앞에 멈췄다. 길고 낮은 격자창이 한층 더 불룩 튀어나오고, 끝에 머리 모양이 새겨진 들보들도 불룩 튀어나온 집이라, 나는 집 전체가 앞으로 몸을 기울여 저 아래 좁은 길을 지나가는 이가 누구인지 들여다보려 애쓰는 것만 같았다."], ["It was quite spotless in its cleanliness.", "집은 흠 하나 없이 깨끗했다."], ["The old-fashioned brass knocker on the low arched door, ornamented with carved garlands of fruit and flowers, twinkled like a star; the two stone steps descending to the door were as white as if they had been covered with fair linen; and all the angles and corners, and carvings and mouldings, and quaint little panes of glass, and quainter little windows, though as old as the hills, were as pure as any snow that ever fell upon the hills.", "낮은 아치형 문에 달린 구식 놋쇠 문고리는 과일과 꽃 모양 화환이 새겨진 채 별처럼 반짝였고, 문으로 내려가는 두 단의 돌계단은 마치 고운 아마포를 덮어 놓은 듯 새하얬다. 그리고 모든 모서리와 귀퉁이, 조각과 장식 쇠시리, 예스러운 작은 유리 조각들과 한층 더 예스러운 작은 창문들은, 산만큼이나 오래되었으되 그 산에 내린 어떤 눈보다도 깨끗했다."]], [["When the pony-chaise stopped at the door, and my eyes were intent upon the house, I saw a cadaverous face appear at a small window on the ground floor (in a little round tower that formed one side of the house), and quickly disappear.", "조랑말 마차가 문 앞에 멈추고 내 눈이 그 집에 빠져 있을 때, 일 층의 작은 창문에서 (집 한쪽을 이루는 작고 둥근 탑에 난 창이었다) 송장 같은 얼굴 하나가 나타났다가 잽싸게 사라졌다."], ["The low arched door then opened, and the face came out.", "이윽고 낮은 아치형 문이 열리더니 그 얼굴이 밖으로 나왔다."], ["It was quite as cadaverous as it had looked in the window, though in the grain of it there was that tinge of red which is sometimes to be observed in the skins of red-haired people.", "그 얼굴은 창문에서 보였던 것과 똑같이 송장 같았는데, 다만 그 살결에는 머리카락이 붉은 사람의 피부에서 이따금 볼 수 있는 그 불그스름한 기운이 어려 있었다."], ["It belonged to a red-haired person--a youth of fifteen, as I take it now, but looking much older--whose hair was cropped as close as the closest stubble; who had hardly any eyebrows, and no eyelashes, and eyes of a red-brown, so unsheltered and unshaded, that I remember wondering how he went to sleep.", "그것은 붉은 머리의 사람 — 지금 헤아려 보면 열다섯 살쯤 된 소년이었으나 훨씬 나이 들어 보였다 — 의 얼굴이었다. 그의 머리카락은 가장 짧게 벤 그루터기만큼 바짝 깎여 있었고, 눈썹은 거의 없다시피 했으며, 속눈썹은 아예 없고, 적갈색 두 눈은 어찌나 가려 주는 것도 그늘져 주는 것도 없던지, 나는 저 사람이 도대체 어떻게 잠드는지 의아했던 기억이 난다."], ["He was high-shouldered and bony; dressed in decent black, with a white wisp of a neckcloth; buttoned up to the throat; and had a long, lank, skeleton hand, which particularly attracted my attention, as he stood at the pony’s head, rubbing his chin with it, and looking up at us in the chaise.", "그는 어깨가 추켜올라가고 뼈만 앙상했으며, 점잖은 검정 옷을 입고 흰 헝겊 조각 같은 목수건을 목까지 단추로 채우고 있었다. 그리고 길고 비쩍 마른 해골 같은 손을 지녔는데, 조랑말 머리맡에 서서 그 손으로 턱을 문지르며 마차에 탄 우리를 올려다보던 그 모습이 유난히 내 눈길을 끌었다."]], [["‘Is Mr. Wickfield at home, Uriah Heep?’ said my aunt.", "\"위크필드 씨 계신가, 유라이아 힙?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"위크필드 씨는 댁에 계십니다, 부인.\""], ["Wickfield’s at home, ma’am,’ said Uriah Heep, ‘if you’ll please to walk in there’--pointing with his long hand to the room he meant.", "유라이아 힙이 그 길쭉한 손으로 자기가 가리키는 방을 짚으며 말했다. \"괜찮으시면 저쪽으로 드시지요.\""]], [["We got out; and leaving him to hold the pony, went into a long low parlour looking towards the street, from the window of which I caught a glimpse, as I went in, of Uriah Heep breathing into the pony’s nostrils, and immediately covering them with his hand, as if he were putting some spell upon him.", "우리는 마차에서 내렸고, 그에게 조랑말을 맡긴 채 거리 쪽으로 난 길고 낮은 응접실로 들어갔다. 들어가면서 나는 그 방 창문 너머로, 유라이아 힙이 조랑말의 콧구멍에 숨을 불어넣고는 마치 무슨 주문이라도 거는 양 곧바로 손으로 콧구멍을 덮는 모습을 흘끗 보았다."], ["Opposite to the tall old chimney-piece were two portraits: one of a gentleman with grey hair (though not by any means an old man) and black eyebrows, who was looking over some papers tied together with red tape; the other, of a lady, with a very placid and sweet expression of face, who was looking at me.", "키 큰 낡은 벽난로 선반 맞은편에는 초상화 두 점이 걸려 있었다. 하나는 회색 머리(그렇다고 결코 노인은 아니었다)에 검은 눈썹을 한 신사로, 붉은 끈으로 묶은 서류 뭉치를 들여다보고 있었다. 다른 하나는 더없이 차분하고 다정한 표정의 부인으로, 나를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["I believe I was turning about in search of Uriah’s picture, when, a door at the farther end of the room opening, a gentleman entered, at sight of whom I turned to the first-mentioned portrait again, to make quite sure that it had not come out of its frame.", "유라이아의 초상화를 찾으려고 둘레를 두리번거리던 참이었던 것 같은데, 그때 방 저쪽 끝의 문이 열리며 한 신사가 들어왔다. 그를 보자 나는 앞서 말한 그 초상화를 다시 돌아보며, 그림이 액자에서 빠져나온 게 아닌지 확인하려 했다."], ["But it was stationary; and as the gentleman advanced into the light, I saw that he was some years older than when he had had his picture painted.", "그러나 그림은 그대로 걸려 있었고, 신사가 빛 속으로 들어서자 나는 그가 초상화를 그렸을 때보다 몇 살 더 늙었다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘Miss Betsey Trotwood,’ said the gentleman, ‘pray walk in.", "\"베시 트롯우드 양,\" 신사가 말했다. \"어서 드시지요."], ["I was engaged for a moment, but you’ll excuse my being busy.", "잠깐 손이 묶여 있었으니, 바쁜 것을 부디 양해해 주십시오."], ["You know my motive.", "제 까닭은 아실 겁니다."], ["I have but one in life.’", "저에게는 평생 단 하나뿐인 까닭이지요.\""]], [["Miss Betsey thanked him, and we went into his room, which was furnished as an office, with books, papers, tin boxes, and so forth.", "베시 양은 그에게 고맙다고 인사했고, 우리는 그의 방으로 들어갔다. 그 방은 책과 서류, 양철 상자 따위로 사무실처럼 꾸며져 있었다."], ["It looked into a garden, and had an iron safe let into the wall; so immediately over the mantelshelf, that I wondered, as I sat down, how the sweeps got round it when they swept the chimney.", "정원이 내다보였고, 벽에 무쇠 금고가 박혀 있었는데, 벽난로 선반 바로 위에 박혀 있어서 나는 자리에 앉으면서 굴뚝 청소부가 굴뚝을 쓸 때 저걸 어떻게 빙 돌아가는지 의아했다."]], [["‘Well, Miss Trotwood,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 트롯우드 양,\""], ["Wickfield; for I soon found that it was he, and that he was a lawyer, and steward of the estates of a rich gentleman of the county; ‘what wind blows you here?", "위크필드 씨가 말했다. 그가 위크필드 씨이며, 변호사이고, 이 고장 어느 부유한 신사의 영지 관리인이라는 것을 나는 곧 알게 되었다. \"무슨 바람이 불어 여기까지 오셨습니까?"], ["Not an ill wind, I hope?’", "나쁜 바람은 아니겠지요?\""]], [["‘No,’ replied my aunt.", "\"아닐세,\" 고모님이 대답했다."], ["‘I have not come for any law.’", "\"법률 문제로 온 게 아니야.\""]], [["‘That’s right, ma’am,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"잘하셨습니다, 부인.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You had better come for anything else.’ His hair was quite white now, though his eyebrows were still black.", "\"다른 무슨 일로든 오시는 편이 낫지요.\" 그의 머리카락은 이제 완전히 하얗게 셌지만 눈썹은 여전히 검었다."], ["He had a very agreeable face, and, I thought, was handsome.", "그는 무척 호감 가는 얼굴이었고, 내 생각에는 잘생긴 편이었다."], ["There was a certain richness in his complexion, which I had been long accustomed, under Peggotty’s tuition, to connect with port wine; and I fancied it was in his voice too, and referred his growing corpulency to the same cause.", "그의 혈색에는 어떤 불그레한 윤기가 돌았는데, 나는 패거티의 가르침 덕에 그것을 포트와인과 결부 짓는 데 오래도록 익숙해져 있었다. 그리고 그의 목소리에도 그런 기운이 어려 있다는 느낌이 들어, 점점 불어 가는 그의 살집까지 같은 까닭으로 돌렸다."], ["He was very cleanly dressed, in a blue coat, striped waistcoat, and nankeen trousers; and his fine frilled shirt and cambric neckcloth looked unusually soft and white, reminding my strolling fancy (I call to mind) of the plumage on the breast of a swan.", "그는 파란 코트에 줄무늬 조끼, 무명 바지 차림으로 아주 깔끔하게 차려입었고, 고운 주름 장식 셔츠와 삼베 목수건은 유난히 부드럽고 하얘서, 떠도는 내 공상에 (지금 떠오르는데) 백조의 가슴 깃털을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["‘This is my nephew,’ said my aunt.", "\"이 아이가 내 조카일세.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Wasn’t aware you had one, Miss Trotwood,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"조카가 있으신 줄은 몰랐습니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘My grand-nephew, that is to say,’ observed my aunt.", "\"그러니까, 내 종손이지.\" 고모님이 일렀다."]], [["‘Wasn’t aware you had a grand-nephew, I give you my word,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"종손이 있으신 줄도 정말이지 몰랐습니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I have adopted him,’ said my aunt, with a wave of her hand, importing that his knowledge and his ignorance were all one to her, ‘and I have brought him here, to put to a school where he may be thoroughly well taught, and well treated.", "\"내가 양자로 들였네.\" 고모님이 손을 한번 내저으며 말했는데, 그가 알든 모르든 자기에게는 매한가지라는 뜻이었다. \"그래서 이 아이를 데려왔어."], ["Now tell me where that school is, and what it is, and all about it.’", "제대로 잘 가르치고 잘 대접해 줄 학교에 넣으려고 말이야. 자, 그런 학교가 어디 있는지, 어떤 곳인지, 죄다 일러 주게나.\""]], [["‘Before I can advise you properly,’ said Mr.", "\"제대로 조언을 드리기 전에,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield--‘the old question, you know. What’s your motive in this?’", "\"그 해묵은 물음이 있지요. 이번 일에 부인의 까닭은 무엇입니까?\""]], [["‘Deuce take the man!’ exclaimed my aunt.", "\"이 사람이 정말!\" 고모님이 외쳤다."], ["‘Always fishing for motives, when they’re on the surface!", "\"까닭이 빤히 드러나 있는데도 늘 까닭을 캐려 드는군!"], ["Why, to make the child happy and useful.’", "아니, 이 아이를 행복하고 쓸모 있게 만들려는 거지.\""]], [["‘It must be a mixed motive, I think,’ said Mr.", "\"제 생각에는 까닭이 뒤섞여 있는 듯한데요.\""], ["Wickfield, shaking his head and smiling incredulously.", "위크필드 씨가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 못 미덥다는 듯이 웃었다."]], [["‘A mixed fiddlestick,’ returned my aunt.", "\"뒤섞이긴 무슨 얼어 죽을.\" 고모님이 받아쳤다."], ["‘You claim to have one plain motive in all you do yourself.", "\"자네는 자기가 하는 일마다 단 하나의 빤한 까닭이 있다고 주장하지."], ["You don’t suppose, I hope, that you are the only plain dealer in the world?’", "설마 세상에서 자네만이 속이 빤히 들여다보이는 사람이라고 여기는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘Ay, but I have only one motive in life, Miss Trotwood,’ he rejoined, smiling.", "\"아 그렇지만, 저는 평생 단 하나의 까닭뿐입니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 그가 웃으며 응수했다."], ["‘Other people have dozens, scores, hundreds.", "\"다른 사람들은 까닭이 열도 되고 스물도 되고 백도 되지요."], ["I have only one.", "저는 단 하나뿐입니다."], ["There’s the difference.", "거기에 차이가 있지요."], ["However, that’s beside the question.", "어쨌거나, 그건 논점에서 벗어난 얘기군요."], ["The best school?", "가장 좋은 학교 말입니까?"], ["Whatever the motive, you want the best?’", "까닭이야 어떻든, 가장 좋은 곳을 원하시는 거지요?\""]], [["My aunt nodded assent.", "고모님이 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다."]], [["‘At the best we have,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 고장에서 가장 좋은 학교라 해도,\""], ["Wickfield, considering, ‘your nephew couldn’t board just now.’", "위크필드 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"조카분이 당장은 거기서 기숙할 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["‘But he could board somewhere else, I suppose?’ suggested my aunt.", "\"하지만 다른 어딘가에서는 기숙할 수 있겠지?\" 고모님이 넌지시 물었다."]], [["Mr. Wickfield thought I could.", "위크필드 씨는 내가 그럴 수 있으리라 보았다."], ["After a little discussion, he proposed to take my aunt to the school, that she might see it and judge for herself; also, to take her, with the same object, to two or three houses where he thought I could be boarded.", "잠깐 의논한 끝에, 그는 고모님을 학교로 데려가 직접 보고 판단하게 하고, 또 같은 목적으로 내가 기숙할 만하다고 여겨지는 두세 집으로 안내하겠다고 제안했다."], ["My aunt embracing the proposal, we were all three going out together, when he stopped and said:", "고모님이 그 제안을 받아들여, 우리 셋이 함께 나서려는데, 그가 걸음을 멈추고 말했다."]], [["‘Our little friend here might have some motive, perhaps, for objecting to the arrangements.", "\"여기 우리 어린 친구가 그 결정에 반대할 만한 까닭이 좀 있을지도 모르겠군요."], ["I think we had better leave him behind?’", "이 아이는 두고 가는 편이 낫지 않을까요?\""]], [["My aunt seemed disposed to contest the point; but to facilitate matters I said I would gladly remain behind, if they pleased; and returned into Mr.", "고모님은 그 점을 두고 따져 볼 마음인 듯했지만, 나는 일을 매끄럽게 하려고, 괜찮으시다면 기꺼이 남아 있겠다고 말한 뒤, 위크필드 씨의 사무실로 되돌아갔다."], ["Wickfield’s office, where I sat down again, in the chair I had first occupied, to await their return.", "그리고 처음 앉았던 그 의자에 다시 앉아 두 사람이 돌아오기를 기다렸다."]], [["It so happened that this chair was opposite a narrow passage, which ended in the little circular room where I had seen Uriah Heep’s pale face looking out of the window.", "마침 이 의자는 좁은 통로와 마주하고 있었는데, 그 통로 끝에는 유라이아 힙의 창백한 얼굴이 창밖을 내다보던 작은 원형 방이 있었다."], ["Uriah, having taken the pony to a neighbouring stable, was at work at a desk in this room, which had a brass frame on the top to hang paper upon, and on which the writing he was making a copy of was then hanging.", "유라이아는 조랑말을 이웃 마구간에 맡기고 와서, 그 방의 책상에 앉아 일하고 있었다. 책상 위쪽에는 종이를 걸어 두는 놋쇠 틀이 있었고, 거기에 그가 베껴 쓰고 있는 글이 걸려 있었다."], ["Though his face was towards me, I thought, for some time, the writing being between us, that he could not see me; but looking that way more attentively, it made me uncomfortable to observe that, every now and then, his sleepless eyes would come below the writing, like two red suns, and stealthily stare at me for I dare say a whole minute at a time, during which his pen went, or pretended to go, as cleverly as ever.", "그의 얼굴이 나를 향해 있었지만, 한동안 나는 우리 사이에 그 글이 걸려 있으니 그가 나를 보지 못하리라 여겼다. 그러나 그쪽을 더 유심히 들여다보다가, 그의 잠들지 않는 두 눈이 이따금 그 글 아래로 두 개의 붉은 태양처럼 내려와서, 감히 말하건대 한 번에 일 분 내내 나를 슬그머니 빤히 노려보곤 한다는 것을 알아채고는 마음이 영 불편해졌다. 그러는 동안에도 그의 펜은 여느 때처럼 능란하게 움직였다 — 아니, 움직이는 척했다."], ["I made several attempts to get out of their way--such as standing on a chair to look at a map on the other side of the room, and poring over the columns of a Kentish newspaper--but they always attracted me back again; and whenever I looked towards those two red suns, I was sure to find them, either just rising or just setting.", "나는 그 눈길을 피하려고 여러 번 애를 썼다 — 의자에 올라서서 방 반대편의 지도를 들여다본다든지, 켄트 지방 신문의 기사 단을 골똘히 읽는다든지 하면서 말이다 — 그러나 그 눈은 늘 나를 도로 끌어당겼고, 내가 그 두 개의 붉은 태양 쪽으로 눈길을 줄 때마다 어김없이 그것은 막 떠오르거나 막 지는 참이었다."]], [["At length, much to my relief, my aunt and Mr. Wickfield came back, after a pretty long absence.", "마침내, 더없이 마음이 놓이게도, 꽤 오래 자리를 비웠던 고모님과 위크필드 씨가 돌아왔다."], ["They were not so successful as I could have wished; for though the advantages of the school were undeniable, my aunt had not approved of any of the boarding-houses proposed for me.", "그러나 그들은 내가 바라던 만큼 성과를 거두지는 못했다. 학교의 이점은 부인할 수 없었으나, 고모님이 나를 위해 제안된 기숙집 가운데 어느 한 곳도 마음에 들어 하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["‘It’s very unfortunate,’ said my aunt.", "\"이거 참 안됐구나.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘I don’t know what to do, Trot.’", "\"어찌해야 할지 모르겠다, 트롯.\""]], [["‘It does happen unfortunately,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"공교롭게 일이 그리되었군요.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["‘But I’ll tell you what you can do, Miss Trotwood.’", "\"하지만 어찌하면 좋을지 일러 드리지요, 트롯우드 양.\""]], [["‘What’s that?’ inquired my aunt.", "\"그게 뭔가?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["‘Leave your nephew here, for the present.", "\"조카분을 당분간 여기 두십시오."], ["He’s a quiet fellow.", "조용한 아이입니다."], ["He won’t disturb me at all.", "저를 조금도 방해하지 않을 거예요."], ["It’s a capital house for study.", "공부하기에는 더없이 좋은 집이지요."], ["As quiet as a monastery, and almost as roomy.", "수도원만큼 조용하고, 거의 그만큼 넓습니다."], ["Leave him here.’", "여기 두십시오.\""]], [["My aunt evidently liked the offer, though she was delicate of accepting it. So did I.", "고모님은 그 제안이 마음에 드는 게 분명했지만, 선뜻 받아들이기를 조심스러워했다."], ["‘Come, Miss Trotwood,’ said Mr.", "나도 마찬가지였다."], ["Wickfield. ‘This is the way out of the difficulty.", "\"자요, 트롯우드 양,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["It’s only a temporary arrangement, you know.", "\"이게 난처함을 푸는 방법입니다."], ["If it don’t act well, or don’t quite accord with our mutual convenience, he can easily go to the right-about.", "어디까지나 임시방편이니까요. 잘 풀리지 않거나 서로의 형편에 딱 맞지 않으면, 아이는 손쉽게 방향을 틀면 됩니다."], ["There will be time to find some better place for him in the meanwhile.", "그동안 더 나은 곳을 찾을 시간도 있을 거고요."], ["You had better determine to leave him here for the present!’", "당분간은 여기 두기로 마음을 정하시는 편이 낫습니다!\""]], [["‘I am very much obliged to you,’ said my aunt; ‘and so is he, I see; but--’", "\"정말 고맙네.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"보아하니 이 아이도 그렇고. 하지만—\""]], [["‘Come!", "\"자요!"], ["I know what you mean,’ cried Mr.", "무슨 말씀이신지 알겠습니다.\""], ["Wickfield. ‘You shall not be oppressed by the receipt of favours, Miss Trotwood.", "위크필드 씨가 외쳤다. \"신세를 진다는 부담에 짓눌리실 필요 없습니다, 트롯우드 양."], ["You may pay for him, if you like.", "원하시면 아이 몫의 비용을 치르셔도 됩니다."], ["We won’t be hard about terms, but you shall pay if you will.’", "조건을 두고 야박하게 굴진 않겠지만, 정 원하신다면 치르십시오.\""]], [["‘On that understanding,’ said my aunt, ‘though it doesn’t lessen the real obligation, I shall be very glad to leave him.’", "\"그렇게 한다면,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"비록 실제로 진 신세가 가벼워지진 않겠지만, 기꺼이 이 아이를 맡기겠네.\""]], [["‘Then come and see my little housekeeper,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"그럼 우리 집 어린 살림꾼을 보러 가시지요.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."]], [["We accordingly went up a wonderful old staircase; with a balustrade so broad that we might have gone up that, almost as easily; and into a shady old drawing-room, lighted by some three or four of the quaint windows I had looked up at from the street: which had old oak seats in them, that seemed to have come of the same trees as the shining oak floor, and the great beams in the ceiling.", "그래서 우리는 놀라운 옛 계단을 올라갔다. 난간이 어찌나 널찍했던지 그 위로도 거의 그만큼 쉽게 올라갈 수 있을 듯했다. 그러고는 그늘진 옛 응접실로 들어갔는데, 내가 거리에서 올려다보았던 그 예스러운 창문 서너 개로 빛이 들어왔다."], ["It was a prettily furnished room, with a piano and some lively furniture in red and green, and some flowers.", "그 창에는 오래된 떡갈나무 의자가 붙어 있었는데, 반들거리는 떡갈나무 마룻바닥이며 천장의 굵직한 들보와 같은 나무에서 나온 것만 같았다."], ["It seemed to be all old nooks and corners; and in every nook and corner there was some queer little table, or cupboard, or bookcase, or seat, or something or other, that made me think there was not such another good corner in the room; until I looked at the next one, and found it equal to it, if not better.", "피아노 한 대와 빨강과 초록의 화사한 가구 몇 점, 그리고 꽃 몇 송이가 놓인, 곱게 꾸민 방이었다. 온통 오래된 후미진 구석과 모퉁이로 이루어진 듯했고, 어느 후미진 구석이며 모퉁이마다 별난 작은 탁자며 찬장이며 책장이며 의자며 그 비슷한 무엇인가가 있어서, 나는 이 방에 이만한 좋은 구석은 다시 없겠다고 여기곤 했다. 그러다 다음 구석을 보면, 그것이 앞의 것과 맞먹거나, 어쩌면 더 낫다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["On everything there was the same air of retirement and cleanliness that marked the house outside.", "모든 것에는 이 집 바깥을 물들이던 것과 똑같은, 한적함과 깨끗함의 기운이 어려 있었다."]], [["Mr. Wickfield tapped at a door in a corner of the panelled wall, and a girl of about my own age came quickly out and kissed him.", "위크필드 씨가 판자벽 한쪽 구석의 문을 톡톡 두드리자, 나와 또래쯤 되는 소녀가 잽싸게 나와 그에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["On her face, I saw immediately the placid and sweet expression of the lady whose picture had looked at me downstairs.", "나는 그 얼굴에서, 아래층에서 나를 바라보던 그 부인 초상화의 차분하고 다정한 표정을 단번에 알아보았다."], ["It seemed to my imagination as if the portrait had grown womanly, and the original remained a child.", "마치 그 초상화는 여인으로 자라났는데 그 실물은 아직 아이로 남아 있는 것만 같았다."], ["Although her face was quite bright and happy, there was a tranquillity about it, and about her--a quiet, good, calm spirit--that I never have forgotten; that I shall never forget.", "그녀의 얼굴은 더없이 밝고 행복했지만, 그 얼굴에는, 그리고 그녀 전체에는 어떤 평온함이 — 조용하고 선량하며 잔잔한 마음씨가 — 깃들어 있었으니, 나는 그것을 결코 잊은 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 잊지 못할 것이다."], ["This was his little housekeeper, his daughter Agnes, Mr. Wickfield said.", "위크필드 씨는 이 아이가 자기 집의 어린 살림꾼, 자기 딸 애그니스라고 말했다."], ["When I heard how he said it, and saw how he held her hand, I guessed what the one motive of his life was.", "그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣고, 그가 딸의 손을 잡는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 그의 평생 단 하나뿐인 까닭이 무엇인지 알아챘다."]], [["She had a little basket-trifle hanging at her side, with keys in it; and she looked as staid and as discreet a housekeeper as the old house could have.", "그녀는 옆구리에 열쇠가 든 작은 바구니 같은 것을 매달고 있었는데, 이 오래된 집이 둘 만한 살림꾼 가운데 더없이 차분하고 사려 깊어 보였다."], ["She listened to her father as he told her about me, with a pleasant face; and when he had concluded, proposed to my aunt that we should go upstairs and see my room.", "그녀는 아버지가 나에 대해 이야기하는 것을 환한 얼굴로 들었고, 아버지의 말이 끝나자 고모님에게 위층으로 올라가 내 방을 보자고 청했다."], ["We all went together, she before us: and a glorious old room it was, with more oak beams, and diamond panes; and the broad balustrade going all the way up to it.", "우리는 그녀를 앞세우고 모두 함께 올라갔다. 떡갈나무 들보가 더 많고 마름모꼴 유리창이 달린, 으리으리한 옛 방이었다. 그 넓은 난간이 그 방까지 죽 이어져 있었다."]], [["I cannot call to mind where or when, in my childhood, I had seen a stained glass window in a church.", "어린 시절 어느 교회에서 색유리 창을 보았는지, 그게 어디였고 언제였는지는 도무지 떠오르지 않는다."], ["Nor do I recollect its subject.", "그 그림이 무엇이었는지도 기억나지 않는다."], ["But I know that when I saw her turn round, in the grave light of the old staircase, and wait for us, above, I thought of that window; and I associated something of its tranquil brightness with Agnes Wickfield ever afterwards.", "그러나 그녀가 그 오래된 계단의 엄숙한 빛 속에서 몸을 돌려, 위쪽에서 우리를 기다리는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 그 창을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그 후로 줄곧 나는 그 창의 고요한 환함 같은 무엇인가를 애그니스 위크필드와 결부시켰다."]], [["My aunt was as happy as I was, in the arrangement made for me; and we went down to the drawing-room again, well pleased and gratified.", "나를 위해 마련된 이 일에 고모님도 나만큼 행복해했고, 우리는 흐뭇하고 만족스러운 마음으로 다시 응접실로 내려왔다."], ["As she would not hear of staying to dinner, lest she should by any chance fail to arrive at home with the grey pony before dark; and as I apprehend Mr.", "고모님은 어떻게든 날이 어두워지기 전에 회색 조랑말을 몰아 집에 닿지 못할까 봐, 저녁 식사를 하고 가라는 권유는 들으려고도 하지 않았다."], ["Wickfield knew her too well to argue any point with her; some lunch was provided for her there, and Agnes went back to her governess, and Mr. Wickfield to his office.", "그리고 내 짐작에 위크필드 씨도 고모님을 너무 잘 알아 어떤 점에서도 굳이 다투려 하지 않았으므로, 그 자리에서 고모님을 위해 가벼운 점심이 차려졌고, 애그니스는 가정교사에게 돌아갔으며 위크필드 씨는 자기 사무실로 돌아갔다."], ["So we were left to take leave of one another without any restraint.", "그리하여 우리는 아무런 거리낌 없이 서로 작별 인사를 나눌 수 있게 되었다."]], [["She told me that everything would be arranged for me by Mr. Wickfield, and that I should want for nothing, and gave me the kindest words and the best advice.", "고모님은 모든 일이 위크필드 씨를 통해 마련될 것이고, 내가 무엇 하나 아쉬운 게 없을 거라고 말하며, 더없이 다정한 말과 더없이 좋은 충고를 건넸다."]], [["‘Trot,’ said my aunt in conclusion, ‘be a credit to yourself, to me, and Mr.", "\"트롯,\" 고모님이 마지막으로 말했다. \"너 자신과 나와 딕 씨에게 떳떳한 사람이 되어라."], ["Dick, and Heaven be with you!’", "하늘이 너와 함께하기를!\""]], [["I was greatly overcome, and could only thank her, again and again, and send my love to Mr. Dick.", "나는 가슴이 북받쳐, 그저 거듭거듭 고모님에게 고맙다는 말밖에 못 했고, 딕 씨에게도 안부를 전해 달라고 했다."]], [["‘Never,’ said my aunt, ‘be mean in anything; never be false; never be cruel.", "\"무슨 일이든,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"절대 비열하게 굴지 마라."], ["Avoid those three vices, Trot, and I can always be hopeful of you.’", "절대 거짓되지 마라. 절대 모질게 굴지 마라. 그 세 가지 악덕을 피한다면, 트롯, 나는 너에게 늘 희망을 걸 수 있을 게다.\""]], [["I promised, as well as I could, that I would not abuse her kindness or forget her admonition.", "나는 할 수 있는 한, 고모님의 친절을 저버리지도 그 가르침을 잊지도 않겠노라 약속했다."]], [["‘The pony’s at the door,’ said my aunt, ‘and I am off!", "\"조랑말이 문 앞에 와 있구나.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["Stay here.’ With these words she embraced me hastily, and went out of the room, shutting the door after her.", "\"나는 간다! 너는 여기 있어라.\" 이 말과 함께 고모님은 황급히 나를 끌어안고는 방을 나가, 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["At first I was startled by so abrupt a departure, and almost feared I had displeased her; but when I looked into the street, and saw how dejectedly she got into the chaise, and drove away without looking up, I understood her better and did not do her that injustice.", "처음에는 그토록 갑작스러운 떠남에 깜짝 놀라, 행여 내가 고모님을 언짢게 한 게 아닌가 두려운 마음마저 들었다. 그러나 거리를 내다보니, 고모님이 풀 죽은 모습으로 마차에 올라, 한 번도 고개를 들지 않은 채 떠나가는 것을 보고는, 고모님을 더 잘 헤아리게 되어 그런 부당한 오해를 거두었다."]], [["By five o’clock, which was Mr. Wickfield’s dinner-hour, I had mustered up my spirits again, and was ready for my knife and fork.", "위크필드 씨의 저녁 식사 시각인 다섯 시가 되자, 나는 다시 기운을 차려 칼과 포크를 들 채비가 되었다."], ["The cloth was only laid for us two; but Agnes was waiting in the drawing-room before dinner, went down with her father, and sat opposite to him at table.", "식탁보는 우리 둘 몫만 깔려 있었지만, 애그니스는 식사 전에 응접실에서 기다리고 있다가 아버지와 함께 내려와 식탁에서 아버지와 마주 보고 앉았다."], ["I doubted whether he could have dined without her.", "나는 그가 과연 딸 없이 식사를 할 수 있을지 의심스러웠다."]], [["We did not stay there, after dinner, but came upstairs into the drawing-room again: in one snug corner of which, Agnes set glasses for her father, and a decanter of port wine.", "우리는 식사 뒤에 그곳에 머물지 않고 다시 위층 응접실로 올라갔다. 그 방의 아늑한 한쪽 구석에 애그니스는 아버지를 위해 잔과 포트와인 한 병을 차려 놓았다."], ["I thought he would have missed its usual flavour, if it had been put there for him by any other hands.", "나는 만일 다른 누군가의 손으로 그것이 놓였더라면 그가 늘 즐기던 그 맛을 느끼지 못했으리라 생각했다."]], [["There he sat, taking his wine, and taking a good deal of it, for two hours; while Agnes played on the piano, worked, and talked to him and me.", "그는 거기 앉아 두 시간 동안 술을 마셨는데, 꽤 많이 마셨다. 그동안 애그니스는 피아노를 치고, 바느질을 하고, 아버지와 나에게 말을 건넸다."], ["He was, for the most part, gay and cheerful with us; but sometimes his eyes rested on her, and he fell into a brooding state, and was silent.", "그는 대체로 우리와 흥겹고 명랑하게 지냈다. 그러나 이따금 그의 눈길이 딸에게 머물면, 그는 깊은 시름에 잠겨 말이 없어지곤 했다."], ["She always observed this quickly, I thought, and always roused him with a question or caress.", "내 생각에 그녀는 늘 그것을 재빨리 알아채고, 늘 물음이나 다정한 몸짓으로 그를 일깨웠다."], ["Then he came out of his meditation, and drank more wine.", "그러면 그는 깊은 생각에서 빠져나와 술을 더 마셨다."]], [["Agnes made the tea, and presided over it; and the time passed away after it, as after dinner, until she went to bed; when her father took her in his arms and kissed her, and, she being gone, ordered candles in his office.", "애그니스가 차를 우려 자리를 보살폈고, 그 뒤로는 저녁 식사 뒤와 마찬가지로 그녀가 잠자리에 들 때까지 시간이 흘러갔다. 그녀가 잠자리에 들 때, 아버지는 딸을 품에 안고 입을 맞추었으며, 딸이 가고 나자 자기 사무실에 촛불을 켜라고 일렀다."], ["Then I went to bed too.", "그러고 나서 나도 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["But in the course of the evening I had rambled down to the door, and a little way along the street, that I might have another peep at the old houses, and the grey Cathedral; and might think of my coming through that old city on my journey, and of my passing the very house I lived in, without knowing it.", "그러나 저녁나절 어느 무렵, 나는 슬슬 문간까지 내려갔다가 거리를 따라 조금 더 걸어 나갔다. 그 오래된 집들과 회색 대성당을 다시 한번 들여다보고, 또 길을 떠나 그 옛 도시를 지나오던 일을, 그리고 내가 지금 살게 된 바로 그 집을 그런 줄도 모르고 스쳐 지나갔던 일을 떠올려 보고 싶어서였다."], ["As I came back, I saw Uriah Heep shutting up the office; and feeling friendly towards everybody, went in and spoke to him, and at parting, gave him my hand.", "돌아오는 길에 나는 유라이아 힙이 사무실 문을 닫는 것을 보았는데, 누구에게나 살가운 마음이 들던 터라 안으로 들어가 그에게 말을 건넸고, 헤어질 때 손을 내밀어 악수를 청했다."], ["But oh, what a clammy hand his was!", "그런데 아아, 그의 손이 어찌나 축축하던지!"], ["as ghostly to the touch as to the sight!", "보기에 으스스한 만큼이나 만져 보아도 으스스했다!"], ["I rubbed mine afterwards, to warm it, AND TO RUB HIS OFF.", "나는 그 뒤로 내 손을 따뜻하게 데우려고, 그리고 그의 손길을 문질러 없애려고 한참을 비벼 댔다."]], [["It was such an uncomfortable hand, that, when I went to my room, it was still cold and wet upon my memory.", "어찌나 거북한 손이었던지, 방으로 돌아온 뒤에도 그것은 내 기억 속에 여전히 차갑고 축축하게 남아 있었다."], ["Leaning out of the window, and seeing one of the faces on the beam-ends looking at me sideways, I fancied it was Uriah Heep got up there somehow, and shut him out in a hurry.", "창밖으로 몸을 내밀다가, 들보 끝에 새겨진 머리 하나가 나를 곁눈질로 바라보는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그것이 어떻게든 거기 올라가 있는 유라이아 힙이라는 망상에 사로잡혀 황급히 창을 닫아 그를 가두어 버렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER 16. I AM A NEW BOY IN MORE SENSES THAN ONE", "# CHAPTER 16. I AM A NEW BOY IN MORE SENSES THAN ONE"]], [["Next morning, after breakfast, I entered on school life again. I went, accompanied by Mr.", "이튿날 아침, 식사를 마치고 나는 다시 학교생활에 발을 들였다."], ["Wickfield, to the scene of my future studies--a grave building in a courtyard, with a learned air about it that seemed very well suited to the stray rooks and jackdaws who came down from the Cathedral towers to walk with a clerkly bearing on the grass-plot--and was introduced to my new master, Doctor Strong.", "위크필드 씨와 함께, 앞으로 내가 공부할 곳으로 갔다. 안마당에 들어선 묵직한 건물이었는데, 학식 있는 분위기가 풍겨 나서, 대성당 탑에서 내려와 잔디밭 위를 점잖은 서기처럼 거니는 떠돌이 까마귀와 갈까마귀에게 썩 잘 어울려 보였다. 그곳에서 나는 새 선생님인 스트롱 박사님을 소개받았다."]], [["Doctor Strong looked almost as rusty, to my thinking, as the tall iron rails and gates outside the house; and almost as stiff and heavy as the great stone urns that flanked them, and were set up, on the top of the red-brick wall, at regular distances all round the court, like sublimated skittles, for Time to play at.", "스트롱 박사님은 내 눈에, 집 바깥에 늘어선 키 큰 쇠 난간이며 대문만큼이나 녹슬어 보였다. 또한 그 양옆에 선 커다란 돌 항아리들만큼이나 뻣뻣하고 묵직해 보였는데, 그 항아리들은 붉은 벽돌담 위에, 안마당을 빙 둘러 일정한 간격으로 세워져 있어서, 마치 세월이 가지고 놀라고 갖다 놓은 거룩한 볼링핀 같았다. 박사님은 서재에 (스트롱 박사님 말이다) 계셨는데, 옷은 그다지 잘 솔질되어 있지 않았고, 머리도 그다지 잘 빗겨 있지 않았다."], ["He was in his library (I mean Doctor Strong was), with his clothes not particularly well brushed, and his hair not particularly well combed; his knee-smalls unbraced; his long black gaiters unbuttoned; and his shoes yawning like two caverns on the hearth-rug.", "무릎 아래 바지의 끈은 풀려 있었고, 긴 검정 각반은 단추가 채워지지 않았으며, 두 신발은 난로 깔개 위에서 두 개의 동굴처럼 하품하듯 벌어져 있었다. 박사님은 나를 향해, 윤기 없는 눈길을 돌렸는데, 그 눈은 옛날 블런더스톤 교회 묘지에서 풀을 뜯고 무덤들 위로 비틀거리며 넘어지곤 하던, 오래도록 잊고 있던 눈먼 늙은 말을 떠올리게 했다."], ["Turning upon me a lustreless eye, that reminded me of a long-forgotten blind old horse who once used to crop the grass, and tumble over the graves, in Blunderstone churchyard, he said he was glad to see me: and then he gave me his hand; which I didn’t know what to do with, as it did nothing for itself.", "박사님은 나를 만나서 기쁘다고 말했다. 그러고는 나에게 손을 내밀었는데, 나는 그 손을 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 손이 스스로 아무것도 하지 않았으니까."]], [["But, sitting at work, not far from Doctor Strong, was a very pretty young lady--whom he called Annie, and who was his daughter, I supposed--who got me out of my difficulty by kneeling down to put Doctor Strong’s shoes on, and button his gaiters, which she did with great cheerfulness and quickness.", "그런데 스트롱 박사님에게서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에, 무언가 일을 하며 앉아 있는 아주 어여쁜 젊은 부인이 있었다. 박사님은 그녀를 애니라고 불렀는데, 나는 그분이 박사님의 딸이려니 짐작했다. 그분이 무릎을 꿇고 스트롱 박사님의 신발을 신겨 주고 각반의 단추를 채워 주면서 — 무척 명랑하고 잽싼 손길로 그렇게 하면서 — 나를 그 곤란에서 구해 주었다."], ["When she had finished, and we were going out to the schoolroom, I was much surprised to hear Mr. Wickfield, in bidding her good morning, address her as ‘Mrs.", "그분이 일을 마치고, 우리가 교실로 나가려는데, 위크필드 씨가 그분에게 안녕히 계시라고 인사하면서 '스트롱 부인'이라고 부르는 것을 듣고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["Strong’; and I was wondering could she be Doctor Strong’s son’s wife, or could she be Mrs. Doctor Strong, when Doctor Strong himself unconsciously enlightened me.", "그분이 스트롱 박사님 아들의 아내인가, 아니면 스트롱 박사 부인인가 하고 어리둥절해하던 차에, 스트롱 박사님이 직접 무심코 그 의문을 풀어 주었다."]], [["‘By the by, Wickfield,’ he said, stopping in a passage with his hand on my shoulder; ‘you have not found any suitable provision for my wife’s cousin yet?’", "\"그건 그렇고, 위크필드,\" 박사님이 복도에서 걸음을 멈추고 내 어깨에 손을 얹은 채 말했다. \"내 아내의 사촌 자리는 아직 적당한 데를 못 찾았나?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Mr.", "\"못 찾았습니다.\""], ["Wickfield. ‘No. Not yet.’", "위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"예. 아직요.\""]], [["‘I could wish it done as soon as it can be done, Wickfield,’ said Doctor Strong, ‘for Jack Maldon is needy, and idle; and of those two bad things, worse things sometimes come. What does Doctor Watts say,’ he added, looking at me, and moving his head to the time of his quotation, ‘“Satan finds some mischief still, for idle hands to do.”’", "\"되도록 빨리 해결되면 좋겠는데, 위크필드,\" 스트롱 박사님이 말했다. \"잭 맬던이 가진 것은 없는데다 빈둥거리니 말이야. 그리고 그 두 가지 나쁜 것에서 더 나쁜 것들이 가끔 생겨나거든. 와츠 박사가 뭐라고 했더라,\" 박사님은 나를 보며, 인용에 맞춰 고개를 까딱이며 덧붙였다. \"'사탄은 노는 손에 시킬 못된 짓을 늘 찾아낸다.'\""]], [["‘Egad, Doctor,’ returned Mr.", "\"맙소사, 박사님,\""], ["Wickfield, ‘if Doctor Watts knew mankind, he might have written, with as much truth, “Satan finds some mischief still, for busy hands to do.”", "위크필드 씨가 받아쳤다. \"와츠 박사가 사람을 좀 알았더라면, 똑같이 맞는 말로 이렇게 썼을 겁니다. '사탄은 바쁜 손에 시킬 못된 짓을 늘 찾아낸다.'"], ["The busy people achieve their full share of mischief in the world, you may rely upon it.", "바쁜 사람들이야말로 이 세상에서 못된 짓의 제 몫을 톡톡히 해낸다니까요, 그건 믿어도 좋습니다."], ["What have the people been about, who have been the busiest in getting money, and in getting power, this century or two? No mischief?’", "지난 한두 세기 동안, 돈을 긁어모으고 권력을 쥐는 데 누구보다 바빴던 사람들이 도대체 무슨 짓을 해 왔습니까? 못된 짓 하나 안 했나요?\""]], [["‘Jack Maldon will never be very busy in getting either, I expect,’ said Doctor Strong, rubbing his chin thoughtfully.", "\"잭 맬던은 그 둘 중 어느 것을 쥐는 데도 그다지 바쁠 일은 없을 것 같네만.\" 스트롱 박사님이 생각에 잠겨 턱을 문지르며 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps not,’ said Mr.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요.\""], ["Wickfield; ‘and you bring me back to the question, with an apology for digressing.", "위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 옆길로 샌 걸 사과드리면서, 다시 본론으로 돌아오게 해 주시는군요."], ["No, I have not been able to dispose of Mr.", "예, 아직 잭 맬던 씨의 자리를 마련하지 못했습니다."], ["Jack Maldon yet.", "제 생각에는,\""], ["I believe,’ he said this with some hesitation, ‘I penetrate your motive, and it makes the thing more difficult.’", "그는 다소 머뭇거리며 이렇게 말했다. \"박사님의 속뜻을 제가 짐작하고 있는데, 그것 때문에 일이 더 어려워집니다.\""]], [["‘My motive,’ returned Doctor Strong, ‘is to make some suitable provision for a cousin, and an old playfellow, of Annie’s.’", "\"내 속뜻은,\" 스트롱 박사님이 받았다. \"애니의 사촌이자 옛 소꿉동무에게 적당한 자리를 마련해 주려는 것뿐일세.\""]], [["‘Yes, I know,’ said Mr.", "\"예, 압니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield; ‘at home or abroad.’", "\"국내에서든 외국에서든 말이지요.\""]], [["‘Aye!’ replied the Doctor, apparently wondering why he emphasized those words so much.", "\"그렇지!\" 박사님이, 위크필드 씨가 어째서 그 말에 그토록 힘을 주는지 의아해하는 듯이 대답했다."], ["‘At home or abroad.’", "\"국내에서든 외국에서든.\""]], [["‘Your own expression, you know,’ said Mr.", "\"박사님이 직접 하신 표현이잖습니까.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield. ‘Or abroad.’", "\"아니면 외국에서든, 하고요.\""]], [["‘Surely,’ the Doctor answered.", "\"그렇고말고.\" 박사님이 대답했다."], ["‘Surely.", "\"그렇고말고."], ["One or other.’", "둘 중 하나지.\""]], [["‘One or other?", "\"둘 중 하나라고요?"], ["Have you no choice?’ asked Mr. Wickfield.", "마음에 둔 쪽이 없으십니까?\" 위크필드 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ returned the Doctor.", "\"없네.\" 박사님이 대답했다."]], [["‘No?’ with astonishment.", "\"없다고요?\" 그는 놀라서 되물었다."]], [["‘Not the least.’", "\"조금도 없어.\""]], [["‘No motive,’ said Mr.", "\"외국이지 국내가 아니라고 두실 이유가,\""], ["Wickfield, ‘for meaning abroad, and not at home?’", "위크필드 씨가 말했다. \"전혀 없으시단 말씀이지요?\""]], [["‘No,’ returned the Doctor.", "\"없네.\" 박사님이 대답했다."]], [["‘I am bound to believe you, and of course I do believe you,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"믿을 수밖에 없고, 물론 믿습니다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It might have simplified my office very much, if I had known it before. But I confess I entertained another impression.’", "\"이걸 진작 알았더라면 제 일이 한결 단순해졌을 텐데요. 하지만 솔직히 말씀드려, 저는 다른 인상을 품고 있었습니다.\""]], [["Doctor Strong regarded him with a puzzled and doubting look, which almost immediately subsided into a smile that gave me great encouragement; for it was full of amiability and sweetness, and there was a simplicity in it, and indeed in his whole manner, when the studious, pondering frost upon it was got through, very attractive and hopeful to a young scholar like me.", "스트롱 박사님은 어리둥절하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 그를 바라보았는데, 그 눈길은 거의 곧바로 미소로 가라앉았다. 그 미소에 나는 크게 용기를 얻었다. 그것은 다정함과 부드러움으로 가득했고, 거기에는 — 그리고 사실 박사님의 태도 전체에는 — 학구적이고 생각에 잠긴 서릿발 같은 기운을 뚫고 나오면, 나 같은 어린 학생에게는 무척 매력적이고 희망을 주는 소박함이 있었다."], ["Repeating ‘no’, and ‘not the least’, and other short assurances to the same purport, Doctor Strong jogged on before us, at a queer, uneven pace; and we followed: Mr.", "\"없다\", \"조금도 없어\", 그리고 같은 뜻의 짤막한 다짐들을 거듭하면서, 스트롱 박사님은 묘하게 들쭉날쭉한 걸음으로 우리 앞을 종종걸음 쳐 나아갔고, 우리는 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Wickfield, looking grave, I observed, and shaking his head to himself, without knowing that I saw him.", "위크필드 씨는, 내가 보기에, 자기가 보이는 줄도 모른 채, 심각한 얼굴로 혼자 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 있었다."]], [["The schoolroom was a pretty large hall, on the quietest side of the house, confronted by the stately stare of some half-dozen of the great urns, and commanding a peep of an old secluded garden belonging to the Doctor, where the peaches were ripening on the sunny south wall.", "교실은 꽤 큰 강당이었는데, 집에서 가장 조용한 쪽에 있었고, 커다란 항아리 대여섯 개가 위엄 있게 노려보는 정면을 마주하고 있었으며, 박사님 소유의 한적한 옛 정원이 얼핏 들여다보였다. 그 정원에서는 햇살이 드는 남쪽 담벼락에서 복숭아가 익어 가고 있었다."], ["There were two great aloes, in tubs, on the turf outside the windows; the broad hard leaves of which plant (looking as if they were made of painted tin) have ever since, by association, been symbolical to me of silence and retirement.", "창문 바깥 잔디밭에는 통에 심긴 커다란 알로에 두 그루가 있었는데, 그 식물의 넓고 단단한 잎사귀는 (마치 색칠한 양철로 만든 것처럼 보였다) 그 뒤로 줄곧, 연상 작용으로 인해, 나에게는 침묵과 은둔의 상징이 되었다."], ["About five-and-twenty boys were studiously engaged at their books when we went in, but they rose to give the Doctor good morning, and remained standing when they saw Mr. Wickfield and me.", "우리가 들어갔을 때 스물다섯 명쯤 되는 아이들이 책에 열중하고 있었는데, 박사님께 아침 인사를 하려고 일어섰고, 위크필드 씨와 나를 보고는 그대로 서 있었다."]], [["‘A new boy, young gentlemen,’ said the Doctor; ‘Trotwood Copperfield.’", "\"새로 온 학생이다, 신사 여러분.\" 박사님이 말했다. \"트롯우드 코퍼필드다.\""]], [["One Adams, who was the head-boy, then stepped out of his place and welcomed me.", "그러자 우두머리 학생인 애덤스라는 아이가 제자리에서 나와 나를 맞아 주었다."], ["He looked like a young clergyman, in his white cravat, but he was very affable and good-humoured; and he showed me my place, and presented me to the masters, in a gentlemanly way that would have put me at my ease, if anything could.", "하얀 넥타이를 매고 있어 젊은 목사님처럼 보였지만, 무척 상냥하고 쾌활했다. 그는 내 자리를 알려 주고, 선생님들께 나를 소개해 주었는데, 그 신사다운 태도는, 무엇이 됐든 나를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있는 것이 있었다면, 분명히 나를 편안하게 해 주었을 것이다."]], [["It seemed to me so long, however, since I had been among such boys, or among any companions of my own age, except Mick Walker and Mealy Potatoes, that I felt as strange as ever I have done in my life.", "하지만 나에게는, 이런 아이들 사이에, 혹은 믹 워커와 멀리 포테이토를 빼고는 내 또래 동무들 사이에 있어 본 것이 어찌나 까마득하게 느껴지던지, 내 평생 그 어느 때 못지않게 어색했다."], ["I was so conscious of having passed through scenes of which they could have no knowledge, and of having acquired experiences foreign to my age, appearance, and condition as one of them, that I half believed it was an imposture to come there as an ordinary little schoolboy.", "나는 이 아이들로서는 알 길이 없는 일들을 겪어 왔고, 그들 가운데 한 명으로서의 내 나이와 외모와 처지에 어울리지 않는 경험을 쌓아 왔다는 것을 너무도 또렷이 의식한 나머지, 평범한 꼬마 학생으로 여기 온 것이 사기가 아닌가 하고 반쯤 믿을 지경이었다."], ["I had become, in the Murdstone and Grinby time, however short or long it may have been, so unused to the sports and games of boys, that I knew I was awkward and inexperienced in the commonest things belonging to them.", "머드스톤앤그린비 시절을 거치면서, 그것이 길었든 짧았든, 나는 아이들의 놀이와 게임에 어찌나 서툴러졌던지, 그들에게 속한 가장 흔한 것들에서조차 내가 어설프고 경험이 없다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Whatever I had learnt, had so slipped away from me in the sordid cares of my life from day to night, that now, when I was examined about what I knew, I knew nothing, and was put into the lowest form of the school.", "무엇을 배웠든 간에, 아침부터 밤까지 누추한 걱정거리에 시달리며 사는 동안 죄다 빠져나가 버려서, 이제 내가 아는 것을 시험받았을 때 나는 아무것도 몰랐고, 학교에서 가장 낮은 반에 들어갔다."], ["But, troubled as I was, by my want of boyish skill, and of book-learning too, I was made infinitely more uncomfortable by the consideration, that, in what I did know, I was much farther removed from my companions than in what I did not.", "그러나 아이다운 재주가 없고 책 공부도 부족해 마음이 괴로웠지만, 그보다 훨씬 더 나를 불편하게 한 것은, 내가 아는 것에서 오히려 모르는 것에서보다 동무들과 한참 더 동떨어져 있다는 생각이었다. 내 머릿속은 이런 생각으로 가득 찼다."], ["My mind ran upon what they would think, if they knew of my familiar acquaintance with the King’s Bench Prison?", "내가 왕립 재판소 감옥과 그렇게 가깝게 지냈다는 것을 이 아이들이 안다면 어떻게 생각할까?"], ["Was there anything about me which would reveal my proceedings in connexion with the Micawber family--all those pawnings, and sellings, and suppers--in spite of myself?", "나도 모르게, 미코버 일가와 얽혀 벌인 일들 — 그 모든 전당포 출입이며, 물건을 팔아 치운 일이며, 야식들 — 을 드러낼 무언가가 내게 묻어 있지 않을까?"], ["Suppose some of the boys had seen me coming through Canterbury, wayworn and ragged, and should find me out?", "행여 어떤 아이가 내가 길에 지치고 누더기를 걸친 채 캔터베리를 지나오는 것을 보았고, 그 사실을 알아채면 어쩌나?"], ["What would they say, who made so light of money, if they could know how I had scraped my halfpence together, for the purchase of my daily saveloy and beer, or my slices of pudding?", "돈을 그토록 우습게 여기는 이 아이들이, 내가 날마다 먹을 소시지며 맥주며, 혹은 푸딩 몇 조각을 사려고 어떻게 반 페니씩 긁어모았는지 알게 된다면 뭐라고 할까?"], ["How would it affect them, who were so innocent of London life, and London streets, to discover how knowing I was (and was ashamed to be) in some of the meanest phases of both?", "런던 생활과 런던 거리에 대해 그토록 순진한 이 아이들이, 그 두 가지의 가장 천한 모습 몇 가지에 내가 얼마나 훤한지를 (그리고 그게 부끄러웠는데) 알게 된다면 어떤 마음이 들까?"], ["All this ran in my head so much, on that first day at Doctor Strong’s, that I felt distrustful of my slightest look and gesture; shrunk within myself whensoever I was approached by one of my new schoolfellows; and hurried off the minute school was over, afraid of committing myself in my response to any friendly notice or advance.", "스트롱 박사님 학교에서 보낸 그 첫날, 이 모든 것이 어찌나 머릿속을 맴돌던지, 나는 내 사소한 눈빛 하나 몸짓 하나도 믿지 못했다. 새 동무 가운데 누군가가 다가올 때마다 나는 움츠러들었고, 다정한 인사나 다가옴에 대답하다가 내 정체를 드러낼까 두려워, 수업이 끝나기가 무섭게 서둘러 빠져나왔다."]], [["But there was such an influence in Mr. Wickfield’s old house, that when I knocked at it, with my new school-books under my arm, I began to feel my uneasiness softening away.", "하지만 위크필드 씨의 오래된 집에는 어떤 힘이 깃들어 있어서, 새 교과서를 옆구리에 끼고 그 집 문을 두드릴 때면, 나는 마음의 불편함이 누그러지는 것을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["As I went up to my airy old room, the grave shadow of the staircase seemed to fall upon my doubts and fears, and to make the past more indistinct.", "바람 잘 통하는 내 오래된 방으로 올라갈 때면, 계단의 묵직한 그림자가 내 의심과 두려움 위로 드리워, 지난날을 더 어렴풋하게 만들어 주는 것 같았다."], ["I sat there, sturdily conning my books, until dinner-time (we were out of school for good at three); and went down, hopeful of becoming a passable sort of boy yet.", "나는 거기 앉아 저녁 식사 시간까지 (우리는 세 시면 완전히 학교에서 풀려났다) 꿋꿋이 책을 외웠고, 그래도 그럭저럭 봐 줄 만한 아이가 될 수 있으리라는 희망을 안고 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["Agnes was in the drawing-room, waiting for her father, who was detained by someone in his office.", "애그니스는 응접실에서 아버지를 기다리고 있었는데, 사무실에서 누군가에게 붙잡혀 있었던 것이다."], ["She met me with her pleasant smile, and asked me how I liked the school.", "애그니스는 상냥한 미소로 나를 맞으며, 학교가 어떠냐고 물었다."], ["I told her I should like it very much, I hoped; but I was a little strange to it at first.", "나는 무척 마음에 들 것 같다고, 그러기를 바란다고 대답했다. 하지만 처음엔 조금 낯설다고 했다."]], [["‘You have never been to school,’ I said, ‘have you?’ ‘Oh yes!", "\"너는 학교에 가 본 적이 없지,\" 내가 말했다. \"그렇지?\""], ["Every day.’", "\"아니, 가 봤어! 날마다 가는걸.\""]], [["‘Ah, but you mean here, at your own home?’", "\"아, 하지만 그건 여기, 너희 집에서 말이지?\""]], [["‘Papa couldn’t spare me to go anywhere else,’ she answered, smiling and shaking her head.", "\"아빠가 나를 다른 데 보낼 수 없으셨거든.\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 고개를 가로저었다."], ["‘His housekeeper must be in his house, you know.’", "\"아빠 집에는 살림 맡을 사람이 있어야 하잖아, 알다시피.\""]], [["‘He is very fond of you, I am sure,’ I said.", "\"아버지가 너를 무척 아끼시는구나, 분명히.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["She nodded ‘Yes,’ and went to the door to listen for his coming up, that she might meet him on the stairs.", "애그니스는 \"응\" 하고 고개를 끄덕이더니, 아버지가 올라오는 소리를 들으려고 문가로 가서 계단에서 마주치려 했다."], ["But, as he was not there, she came back again.", "하지만 아버지가 와 있지 않자, 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Mama has been dead ever since I was born,’ she said, in her quiet way.", "\"엄마는 내가 태어나면서부터 돌아가셨어.\" 애그니스가 그 차분한 투로 말했다."], ["‘I only know her picture, downstairs.", "\"나는 아래층에 있는 그림으로만 엄마를 알아."], ["I saw you looking at it yesterday.", "어제 네가 그걸 보고 있는 걸 봤어."], ["Did you think whose it was?’", "누구 그림인지 생각해 봤니?\""]], [["I told her yes, because it was so like herself.", "나는 그렇다고, 너랑 너무 닮았기 때문이라고 대답했다."]], [["‘Papa says so, too,’ said Agnes, pleased. ‘Hark!", "\"아빠도 그렇게 말씀하셔.\" 애그니스가 기뻐하며 말했다. \"쉿!"], ["That’s papa now!’", "저게 지금 아빠야!\""]], [["Her bright calm face lighted up with pleasure as she went to meet him, and as they came in, hand in hand.", "애그니스가 아버지를 맞으러 가서 손을 잡고 함께 들어올 때, 그 환하고 차분한 얼굴이 기쁨으로 밝아졌다."], ["He greeted me cordially; and told me I should certainly be happy under Doctor Strong, who was one of the gentlest of men.", "아버지는 나를 다정하게 맞아 주며, 스트롱 박사님 밑에서 분명히 행복할 거라고, 그분은 더없이 온화한 사람 중 하나라고 말해 주었다."]], [["‘There may be some, perhaps--I don’t know that there are--who abuse his kindness,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"어쩌면 더러는 — 그런 사람이 있는지는 모르겠지만 — 그분의 친절을 악용하는 자도 있을 거다.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Never be one of those, Trotwood, in anything.", "\"트롯우드, 무슨 일에서든 그런 자가 되지 말거라."], ["He is the least suspicious of mankind; and whether that’s a merit, or whether it’s a blemish, it deserves consideration in all dealings with the Doctor, great or small.’", "그분은 사람을 가장 의심할 줄 모르는 분이야. 그게 미덕인지 흠인지는 모르겠다만, 그분과의 거래에서는, 크든 작든, 늘 그 점을 헤아려야 한다.\""]], [["He spoke, I thought, as if he were weary, or dissatisfied with something; but I did not pursue the question in my mind, for dinner was just then announced, and we went down and took the same seats as before.", "내가 보기에 그는 지쳤거나, 무언가에 만족하지 못하는 사람처럼 말했다. 하지만 나는 마음속으로 그 의문을 더 파고들지 않았다. 바로 그때 저녁 식사가 알려졌고, 우리는 아래층으로 내려가 전과 같은 자리에 앉았기 때문이다."]], [["We had scarcely done so, when Uriah Heep put in his red head and his lank hand at the door, and said:", "우리가 막 자리에 앉기가 무섭게, 유라이어 힙이 그 붉은 머리와 비쩍 마른 손을 문틈으로 들이밀고 말했다."]], [["‘Here’s Mr. Maldon begs the favour of a word, sir.’", "\"맬던 씨가 한 말씀 청하십니다, 어르신.\""]], [["‘I am but this moment quit of Mr.", "\"맬던 씨와는 방금 막 헤어졌네만.\""], ["Maldon,’ said his master.", "그의 주인이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ returned Uriah; ‘but Mr.", "\"예, 어르신.\" 유라이어가 대답했다."], ["Maldon has come back, and he begs the favour of a word.’", "\"하지만 맬던 씨가 다시 오셔서, 한 말씀 청하십니다.\""]], [["As he held the door open with his hand, Uriah looked at me, and looked at Agnes, and looked at the dishes, and looked at the plates, and looked at every object in the room, I thought,--yet seemed to look at nothing; he made such an appearance all the while of keeping his red eyes dutifully on his master.", "문을 손으로 잡아 열어 둔 채로, 유라이어는 나를 보고, 애그니스를 보고, 음식 접시들을 보고, 그릇들을 보고, 방 안의 모든 것을 다 보는 것 같았다. 그런데도 아무것도 보지 않는 것 같았다. 그러면서도 줄곧, 그 붉은 눈을 충실하게 주인에게 두고 있는 듯한 모양새를 하고 있었다."], ["‘I beg your pardon.", "\"죄송합니다."], ["It’s only to say, on reflection,’ observed a voice behind Uriah, as Uriah’s head was pushed away, and the speaker’s substituted--‘pray excuse me for this intrusion--that as it seems I have no choice in the matter, the sooner I go abroad the better.", "다만 다시 생각해 보고 드리는 말씀은,\" 유라이어 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 들리더니, 유라이어의 머리가 밀려나고 그 자리에 말하는 사람의 머리가 들어섰다. \"이렇게 불쑥 들어온 것을 부디 용서하십시오만 — 보아하니 이 일에 제게 선택의 여지가 없는 것 같으니, 빨리 외국에 나갈수록 좋겠다는 겁니다."], ["My cousin Annie did say, when we talked of it, that she liked to have her friends within reach rather than to have them banished, and the old Doctor--’", "제 사촌 애니가, 이 이야기를 할 때, 친구들을 멀리 내쫓느니 가까이 두는 편이 좋다고 했고, 그 늙은 박사님도—\""]], [["‘Doctor Strong, was that?’ Mr.", "\"스트롱 박사님 말씀이오?\""], ["Wickfield interposed, gravely.", "위크필드 씨가 심각하게 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Doctor Strong, of course,’ returned the other; ‘I call him the old Doctor; it’s all the same, you know.’", "\"물론 스트롱 박사님이지요.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"제가 그분을 늙은 박사님이라고 부르는 겁니다. 다 같은 말 아닙니까, 아시다시피.\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ returned Mr. Wickfield.", "\"나는 모르겠소.\" 위크필드 씨가 받았다."]], [["‘Well, Doctor Strong,’ said the other--‘Doctor Strong was of the same mind, I believed.", "\"그럼, 스트롱 박사님 말입니다.\" 상대가 말했다. \"스트롱 박사님도 같은 생각이신 줄 알았지요."], ["But as it appears from the course you take with me he has changed his mind, why there’s no more to be said, except that the sooner I am off, the better.", "한데 당신이 저를 대하는 태도로 보아하니 박사님이 마음을 바꾸신 모양이니, 더 할 말이 없습니다. 빨리 떠날수록 좋다는 것 말고는요."], ["Therefore, I thought I’d come back and say, that the sooner I am off the better.", "그래서 다시 돌아와서, 빨리 떠날수록 좋겠다고 말씀드리려고 한 겁니다."], ["When a plunge is to be made into the water, it’s of no use lingering on the bank.’", "물에 풍덩 뛰어들어야 한다면, 강둑에서 꾸물거려 봐야 소용없으니까요.\""]], [["‘There shall be as little lingering as possible, in your case, Mr.", "\"맬던 씨, 당신 경우에는 꾸물거릴 일이 되도록 없을 거요, 그건 믿어도 좋소.\""], ["Maldon, you may depend upon it,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "위크필드 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Thank’ee,’ said the other.", "\"고맙습니다.\" 상대가 말했다."], ["‘Much obliged.", "\"정말 감사합니다."], ["I don’t want to look a gift-horse in the mouth, which is not a gracious thing to do; otherwise, I dare say, my cousin Annie could easily arrange it in her own way.", "받은 말의 입속까지 들여다볼 생각은 없습니다, 그건 점잖지 못한 짓이니까요. 안 그랬으면, 감히 말하건대, 제 사촌 애니가 자기 식대로 쉽게 처리해 줄 수도 있었을 텐데요."], ["I suppose Annie would only have to say to the old Doctor--’", "제 생각에 애니가 그 늙은 박사님께 그저 한마디만 하면—\""]], [["‘Meaning that Mrs. Strong would only have to say to her husband--do I follow you?’ said Mr.", "\"스트롱 부인이 그저 자기 남편에게 한마디만 하면 된다는 뜻이오 — 내가 제대로 알아들은 거요?\""], ["Wickfield.", "위크필드 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Quite so,’ returned the other, ‘--would only have to say, that she wanted such and such a thing to be so and so; and it would be so and so, as a matter of course.’", "\"바로 그렇지요.\" 상대가 대답했다. \"— 이러이러한 일이 이렇게 됐으면 좋겠다고 한마디만 하면, 당연히 그렇게 될 거라는 거지요.\""]], [["‘And why as a matter of course, Mr. Maldon?’ asked Mr.", "\"그런데 어째서 당연하다는 거요, 맬던 씨?\""], ["Wickfield, sedately eating his dinner.", "위크필드 씨가 차분히 식사를 하며 물었다."]], [["‘Why, because Annie’s a charming young girl, and the old Doctor--Doctor Strong, I mean--is not quite a charming young boy,’ said Mr.", "\"왜냐하면 애니는 매력적인 젊은 아가씨이고, 그 늙은 박사님은 — 스트롱 박사님 말입니다 — 그다지 매력적인 젊은 청년이 아니니까요.\""], ["Jack Maldon, laughing.", "잭 맬던 씨가 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘No offence to anybody, Mr. Wickfield.", "\"누구를 흉보려는 건 아닙니다, 위크필드 씨."], ["I only mean that I suppose some compensation is fair and reasonable in that sort of marriage.’", "그저 그런 종류의 결혼에서는 어느 정도의 보상이 공정하고 합당하지 않겠나 하는 거지요.\""]], [["‘Compensation to the lady, sir?’ asked Mr. Wickfield gravely.", "\"부인에 대한 보상 말이오, 선생?\" 위크필드 씨가 심각하게 물었다."]], [["‘To the lady, sir,’ Mr. Jack Maldon answered, laughing.", "\"부인에 대한 보상이지요, 선생.\""], ["But appearing to remark that Mr.", "잭 맬던 씨가 웃으며 대답했다."], ["Wickfield went on with his dinner in the same sedate, immovable manner, and that there was no hope of making him relax a muscle of his face, he added: ‘However, I have said what I came to say, and, with another apology for this intrusion, I may take myself off.", "하지만 위크필드 씨가 여전히 차분하고 흔들림 없는 태도로 식사를 계속하며, 얼굴 근육 하나 풀 기색이 없다는 것을 알아챈 듯, 그가 덧붙였다. \"어쨌든, 하려고 온 말은 다 했으니, 이렇게 불쑥 들어온 것을 다시 한번 사과드리고, 이만 물러가겠습니다."], ["Of course I shall observe your directions, in considering the matter as one to be arranged between you and me solely, and not to be referred to, up at the Doctor’s.’", "물론 이 일은 오로지 당신과 저 사이에서만 처리할 문제로 여기고, 박사님 댁에서는 입에 올리지 말라는 당신 지시를 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Have you dined?’ asked Mr.", "\"식사는 하셨소?\""], ["Wickfield, with a motion of his hand towards the table.", "위크필드 씨가 식탁 쪽으로 손짓하며 물었다."]], [["‘Thank’ee.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["I am going to dine,’ said Mr.", "저는 식사를 하려고 합니다.\""], ["Maldon, ‘with my cousin Annie.", "맬던 씨가 말했다. \"제 사촌 애니랑요."], ["Good-bye!’", "안녕히 계십시오!\""]], [["Mr. Wickfield, without rising, looked after him thoughtfully as he went out.", "위크필드 씨는 일어나지 않은 채, 그가 나가는 것을 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다."], ["He was rather a shallow sort of young gentleman, I thought, with a handsome face, a rapid utterance, and a confident, bold air.", "내가 보기에 그는 다소 얄팍한 부류의 젊은 신사로, 잘생긴 얼굴에, 말이 빨랐고, 자신만만하고 대담한 태도를 지니고 있었다."], ["And this was the first I ever saw of Mr.", "이것이 내가 잭 맬던 씨를 처음 본 순간이었다."], ["Jack Maldon; whom I had not expected to see so soon, when I heard the Doctor speak of him that morning.", "그날 아침 박사님이 그를 입에 올렸을 때만 해도, 이렇게 빨리 보게 될 줄은 몰랐다."]], [["When we had dined, we went upstairs again, where everything went on exactly as on the previous day.", "식사를 마치고 우리는 다시 위층으로 올라갔는데, 모든 일이 전날과 똑같이 흘러갔다."], ["Agnes set the glasses and decanters in the same corner, and Mr. Wickfield sat down to drink, and drank a good deal.", "애그니스는 술잔과 유리병을 같은 구석에 놓았고, 위크필드 씨는 앉아서 술을 마셨고, 꽤 많이 마셨다."], ["Agnes played the piano to him, sat by him, and worked and talked, and played some games at dominoes with me.", "애그니스는 아버지에게 피아노를 쳐 드리고, 곁에 앉아 일을 하고 이야기를 나누었으며, 나와 도미노 놀이를 몇 판 했다."], ["In good time she made tea; and afterwards, when I brought down my books, looked into them, and showed me what she knew of them (which was no slight matter, though she said it was), and what was the best way to learn and understand them.", "때가 되자 애그니스는 차를 끓였고, 그 뒤 내가 책을 들고 내려오자, 들여다보더니, 자기가 그것에 대해 아는 것을 (스스로는 별것 아니라고 했지만, 결코 사소한 게 아니었다) 보여 주었고, 그것을 배우고 이해하는 가장 좋은 방법을 알려 주었다."], ["I see her, with her modest, orderly, placid manner, and I hear her beautiful calm voice, as I write these words.", "이 글을 쓰는 지금도 나는, 그 겸손하고 단정하고 차분한 태도의 애그니스를 보고, 그 아름답고 잔잔한 목소리를 듣는다."], ["The influence for all good, which she came to exercise over me at a later time, begins already to descend upon my breast.", "훗날 애그니스가 나에게 미치게 된 그 온갖 선한 영향이, 벌써 내 가슴 위로 내려앉기 시작한다."], ["I love little Em’ly, and I don’t love Agnes--no, not at all in that way--but I feel that there are goodness, peace, and truth, wherever Agnes is; and that the soft light of the coloured window in the church, seen long ago, falls on her always, and on me when I am near her, and on everything around.", "나는 어린 에밀리를 사랑하고, 애그니스는 사랑하지 않는다 — 아니, 결코 그런 식으로는 아니다 — 그러나 애그니스가 있는 곳이라면 어디든 선함과 평화와 진실이 있다는 것을 나는 느낀다. 그리고 오래전에 보았던, 교회의 색유리창에서 비쳐 들던 그 부드러운 빛이, 늘 애그니스에게, 그리고 내가 애그니스 곁에 있을 때면 나에게도, 또 그 둘레의 모든 것에 내려앉는다는 것을."]], [["The time having come for her withdrawal for the night, and she having left us, I gave Mr. Wickfield my hand, preparatory to going away myself.", "애그니스가 밤이 깊어 물러갈 시간이 되어 자리를 뜨자, 나도 그만 가려고 위크필드 씨에게 손을 내밀었다. 하지만 그가 나를 붙들고 말했다."], ["But he checked me and said: ‘Should you like to stay with us, Trotwood, or to go elsewhere?’", "\"트롯우드, 우리와 함께 지내는 게 좋으냐, 아니면 다른 데로 가는 게 좋으냐?\""]], [["‘To stay,’ I answered, quickly.", "\"함께 지내겠습니다.\" 나는 얼른 대답했다."]], [["‘You are sure?’", "\"정말이냐?\""]], [["‘If you please. If I may!’", "\"괜찮으시다면요. 그래도 된다면요!\""]], [["‘Why, it’s but a dull life that we lead here, boy, I am afraid,’ he said.", "\"그런데 말이다, 여기서 우리가 사는 게 좀 따분한 생활이라, 그게 걱정이구나, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Not more dull for me than Agnes, sir.", "\"애그니스보다 더 따분하진 않을 거예요, 어르신."], ["Not dull at all!’", "조금도 따분하지 않아요!\""]], [["‘Than Agnes,’ he repeated, walking slowly to the great chimney-piece, and leaning against it.", "\"애그니스보다라.\" 그가 천천히 커다란 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 걸어가 거기 기대며 되뇌었다."], ["‘Than Agnes!’", "\"애그니스보다!\""]], [["He had drank wine that evening (or I fancied it), until his eyes were bloodshot.", "그날 저녁 그는 술을 마신 터라 (아니면 내 착각이었는지) 눈이 충혈되어 있었다."], ["Not that I could see them now, for they were cast down, and shaded by his hand; but I had noticed them a little while before.", "지금은 눈을 내리깔고 손으로 가리고 있어 보이지 않았지만, 조금 전에 나는 그 눈을 보았던 것이다."]], [["‘Now I wonder,’ he muttered, ‘whether my Agnes tires of me.", "\"이제 와 궁금하구나,\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"내 애그니스가 나에게 싫증이 났는지."], ["When should I ever tire of her!", "내가 그 애한테 싫증 날 일이 어디 있겠느냐만!"], ["But that’s different, that’s quite different.’", "하지만 그건 다르지, 그건 아주 다른 얘기야.\""]], [["He was musing, not speaking to me; so I remained quiet.", "그는 혼자 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 것이지, 나에게 말하는 게 아니었다. 그래서 나는 가만히 있었다."]], [["‘A dull old house,’ he said, ‘and a monotonous life; but I must have her near me.", "\"따분하고 낡은 집,\" 그가 말했다. \"단조로운 생활."], ["I must keep her near me.", "하지만 나는 그 애를 곁에 두어야 해."], ["If the thought that I may die and leave my darling, or that my darling may die and leave me, comes like a spectre, to distress my happiest hours, and is only to be drowned in--’", "그 애를 곁에 두어야 한다고. 내가 죽어 내 사랑하는 딸을 두고 떠날지도 모른다는, 또는 내 사랑하는 딸이 죽어 나를 두고 떠날지도 모른다는 생각이, 유령처럼 찾아와, 내 가장 행복한 시간을 괴롭히고, 오직 술에 빠져서만 가라앉힐 수—\""]], [["He did not supply the word; but pacing slowly to the place where he had sat, and mechanically going through the action of pouring wine from the empty decanter, set it down and paced back again.", "그는 그 단어를 채우지 않았다. 그러나 천천히 자기가 앉았던 자리로 걸어가, 빈 유리병에서 술을 따르는 동작을 기계적으로 흉내 내더니, 병을 내려놓고 다시 걸어 돌아왔다."]], [["‘If it is miserable to bear, when she is here,’ he said, ‘what would it be, and she away?", "\"그 애가 여기 있는데도 견디기 비참하다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애가 떠나고 나면 어떻겠는가?"], ["No, no, no. I cannot try that.’", "안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 그건 시도조차 할 수 없어.\""]], [["He leaned against the chimney-piece, brooding so long that I could not decide whether to run the risk of disturbing him by going, or to remain quietly where I was, until he should come out of his reverie.", "그는 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채 어찌나 오래 골똘히 잠겨 있던지, 나는 가서 그를 방해하는 위험을 무릅쓸지, 아니면 그가 몽상에서 깨어날 때까지 가만히 제자리에 있을지 결정할 수가 없었다."], ["At length he aroused himself, and looked about the room until his eyes encountered mine.", "마침내 그가 정신을 차리고 방 안을 둘러보다가, 그 눈길이 내 눈과 마주쳤다."]], [["‘Stay with us, Trotwood, eh?’ he said in his usual manner, and as if he were answering something I had just said.", "\"우리와 함께 지내자고, 트롯우드, 응?\" 그가 평소의 투로, 마치 내가 방금 한 말에 대답하기라도 하듯 말했다."], ["‘I am glad of it.", "\"그거 반갑구나."], ["You are company to us both.", "너는 우리 둘에게 좋은 벗이다."], ["It is wholesome to have you here.", "네가 여기 있는 건 이로운 일이야."], ["Wholesome for me, wholesome for Agnes, wholesome perhaps for all of us.’", "나에게도 이롭고, 애그니스에게도 이롭고, 어쩌면 우리 모두에게 이롭지.\""]], [["‘I am sure it is for me, sir,’ I said.", "\"저에게는 분명히 그래요, 어르신.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I am so glad to be here.’", "\"여기 있게 돼서 정말 기뻐요.\""]], [["‘That’s a fine fellow!’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"그것참 기특한 녀석이구나!\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["‘As long as you are glad to be here, you shall stay here.’ He shook hands with me upon it, and clapped me on the back; and told me that when I had anything to do at night after Agnes had left us, or when I wished to read for my own pleasure, I was free to come down to his room, if he were there and if I desired it for company’s sake, and to sit with him.", "\"네가 여기 있어서 기쁜 한, 너는 여기 머물러도 좋다.\" 그는 그 다짐의 표시로 나와 악수를 하고, 내 등을 토닥였다. 그러고는, 애그니스가 자리를 뜬 뒤 밤에 할 일이 있거나, 내 즐거움을 위해 책을 읽고 싶을 때면, 그가 자기 방에 있고 또 내가 벗 삼아 함께 있고 싶다면, 언제든 내려와 그와 함께 앉아 있어도 좋다고 했다."], ["I thanked him for his consideration; and, as he went down soon afterwards, and I was not tired, went down too, with a book in my hand, to avail myself, for half-an-hour, of his permission.", "나는 그 배려에 고마워했다. 그리고 그가 곧이어 아래층으로 내려갔고 나도 피곤하지 않았기에, 책을 한 권 손에 들고 나도 내려가, 삼십 분쯤 그의 허락을 누려 볼 참이었다."]], [["But, seeing a light in the little round office, and immediately feeling myself attracted towards Uriah Heep, who had a sort of fascination for me, I went in there instead.", "그런데 작고 둥근 사무실에 불이 켜져 있는 것을 보고, 또 곧바로 유라이어 힙에게 끌리는 것을 느껴 — 그에게는 나를 묘하게 사로잡는 데가 있었다 — 그 대신 그 안으로 들어갔다."], ["I found Uriah reading a great fat book, with such demonstrative attention, that his lank forefinger followed up every line as he read, and made clammy tracks along the page (or so I fully believed) like a snail.", "유라이어는 두툼하고 커다란 책을 읽고 있었는데, 어찌나 티 나게 열중해 있던지, 비쩍 마른 집게손가락이 읽는 줄마다 따라가며, 종이 위에 달팽이처럼 끈적끈적한 자국을 남기고 있었다 (적어도 나는 그렇게 굳게 믿었다)."]], [["‘You are working late tonight, Uriah,’ says I.", "\"오늘 밤 늦게까지 일하는구나, 유라이어.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, Master Copperfield,’ says Uriah.", "\"예, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다."]], [["As I was getting on the stool opposite, to talk to him more conveniently, I observed that he had not such a thing as a smile about him, and that he could only widen his mouth and make two hard creases down his cheeks, one on each side, to stand for one.", "그에게 더 편히 이야기를 건네려고 맞은편 걸상에 올라앉다가, 나는 그에게 미소라는 것이 없다는 것을 알아챘다. 그가 할 수 있는 거라곤 입을 옆으로 벌리고 양쪽 뺨에 딱딱한 주름을 하나씩 두 줄 잡아, 그것으로 미소를 대신하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["‘I am not doing office-work, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah.", "\"사무 일을 하는 건 아닙니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다."]], [["‘What work, then?’ I asked.", "\"그럼 무슨 일인데?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I am improving my legal knowledge, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah.", "\"제 법률 지식을 갈고닦는 중이지요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다."], ["‘I am going through Tidd’s Practice.", "\"티드의 『법률 실무』를 훑어보고 있습니다."], ["Oh, what a writer Mr. Tidd is, Master Copperfield!’", "아, 티드 씨는 정말 대단한 저술가예요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["My stool was such a tower of observation, that as I watched him reading on again, after this rapturous exclamation, and following up the lines with his forefinger, I observed that his nostrils, which were thin and pointed, with sharp dints in them, had a singular and most uncomfortable way of expanding and contracting themselves--that they seemed to twinkle instead of his eyes, which hardly ever twinkled at all.", "내 걸상은 어찌나 좋은 관찰 망루였던지, 그가 이 황홀한 감탄을 토한 뒤 다시 책을 읽으며 집게손가락으로 줄을 따라가는 것을 지켜보다가, 나는 그의 콧구멍이 — 얇고 뾰족했으며, 날카로운 홈이 패어 있었다 — 묘하고도 몹시 불쾌하게 벌어졌다 오므라졌다 하는 버릇이 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 좀처럼 반짝이는 법이 없는 그의 눈을 대신해, 콧구멍이 반짝이는 것 같았다."]], [["‘I suppose you are quite a great lawyer?’ I said, after looking at him for some time.", "\"넌 꽤 대단한 변호사인 모양이구나?\" 한참 그를 바라보다가 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Me, Master Copperfield?’ said Uriah.", "\"제가요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\" 유라이어가 말했다."], ["‘Oh, no! I’m a very umble person.’", "\"아이고, 천만에요! 저는 아주 미천한 사람인걸요.\""]], [["It was no fancy of mine about his hands, I observed; for he frequently ground the palms against each other as if to squeeze them dry and warm, besides often wiping them, in a stealthy way, on his pocket-handkerchief.", "그의 손에 대한 내 생각이 결코 헛것이 아니었음을, 나는 알아챘다. 그는 자주 손바닥을 맞비벼 짜내듯 말리고 데우려는 듯했고, 게다가 슬그머니 손수건에 손을 닦아 대곤 했다."]], [["‘I am well aware that I am the umblest person going,’ said Uriah Heep, modestly; ‘let the other be where he may.", "\"제가 둘러봐도 가장 미천한 사람이라는 걸 잘 알지요.\" 유라이어 힙이 겸손하게 말했다. \"다른 누가 어디에 있든 간에요."], ["My mother is likewise a very umble person.", "제 어머니 역시 아주 미천한 사람입니다."], ["We live in a numble abode, Master Copperfield, but have much to be thankful for.", "저희는 보잘것없는 거처에서 삽니다, 코퍼필드 도련님. 하지만 감사할 일이 많지요."], ["My father’s former calling was umble.", "제 아버지가 예전에 하셨던 일도 미천했습니다."], ["He was a sexton.’", "교회 묘지기였거든요.\""]], [["‘What is he now?’ I asked.", "\"지금은 뭘 하시는데?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘He is a partaker of glory at present, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah Heep.", "\"지금은 영광에 동참하고 계십니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어 힙이 말했다."], ["‘But we have much to be thankful for.", "\"하지만 저희는 감사할 일이 많아요."], ["How much have I to be thankful for in living with Mr. Wickfield!’", "위크필드 씨와 함께 살게 된 것만 해도 제가 얼마나 감사할 일인지요!\""]], [["I asked Uriah if he had been with Mr. Wickfield long?", "나는 유라이어에게 위크필드 씨와 함께한 지 오래되었느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘I have been with him, going on four year, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah; shutting up his book, after carefully marking the place where he had left off.", "\"함께한 지 사 년째로 접어듭니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가, 읽다 만 곳을 조심스레 표시한 다음 책을 덮으며 말했다."], ["‘Since a year after my father’s death.", "\"제 아버지가 돌아가시고 일 년 뒤부터지요."], ["How much have I to be thankful for, in that!", "그것만 해도 제가 얼마나 감사할 일인지요!"], ["How much have I to be thankful for, in Mr. Wickfield’s kind intention to give me my articles, which would otherwise not lay within the umble means of mother and self!’", "위크필드 씨가 친절하게도 저에게 견습 계약서를 마련해 주시려는 그 뜻만 해도 제가 얼마나 감사할 일인지요. 안 그랬으면 어머니와 저의 미천한 형편으로는 어림도 없었을 텐데요!\""]], [["‘Then, when your articled time is over, you’ll be a regular lawyer, I suppose?’ said I.", "\"그럼, 견습 기간이 끝나면 너는 정식 변호사가 되겠구나?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘With the blessing of Providence, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah.", "\"하느님의 은총이 있다면요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 대답했다."]], [["‘Perhaps you’ll be a partner in Mr.", "\"언젠가는 네가 위크필드 씨의 사업에 동업자가 될지도 모르겠네.\""], ["Wickfield’s business, one of these days,’ I said, to make myself agreeable; ‘and it will be Wickfield and Heep, or Heep late Wickfield.’", "나는 그를 기쁘게 해 주려고 말했다. \"그래서 '위크필드앤힙'이나 '힙, 전 위크필드'가 되는 거지.\""]], [["‘Oh no, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, shaking his head, ‘I am much too umble for that!’", "\"아이고, 천만에요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다. \"저는 그러기엔 너무나 미천한걸요!\""]], [["He certainly did look uncommonly like the carved face on the beam outside my window, as he sat, in his humility, eyeing me sideways, with his mouth widened, and the creases in his cheeks.", "그가 그렇게, 그 미천함 속에서, 입을 옆으로 벌리고 뺨에 주름을 잡은 채 나를 곁눈질하며 앉아 있는 모습은, 정말이지 내 방 창문 밖 들보에 새겨진 얼굴과 유난히 닮아 보였다."]], [["‘Mr. Wickfield is a most excellent man, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah.", "\"위크필드 씨는 더없이 훌륭한 분입니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다."], ["‘If you have known him long, you know it, I am sure, much better than I can inform you.’", "\"도련님이 그분을 오래 아셨다면, 제가 알려 드릴 것도 없이, 분명 저보다 훨씬 잘 아시겠지요.\""]], [["I replied that I was certain he was; but that I had not known him long myself, though he was a friend of my aunt’s.", "나는 그분이 훌륭한 분인 게 분명하다고 대답했다. 하지만 나 자신은 그분이 우리 고모님의 친구이긴 해도, 오래 안 사이는 아니라고 했다."]], [["‘Oh, indeed, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah.", "\"아, 그렇군요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다."], ["‘Your aunt is a sweet lady, Master Copperfield!’", "\"도련님의 고모님은 참 좋은 분이시지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["He had a way of writhing when he wanted to express enthusiasm, which was very ugly; and which diverted my attention from the compliment he had paid my relation, to the snaky twistings of his throat and body.", "그는 열의를 나타내고 싶을 때면 몸을 비비 꼬는 버릇이 있었는데, 그게 무척 보기 흉했다. 그 탓에 나는, 그가 우리 친척에게 한 칭찬에서 그 뱀 같은 목과 몸뚱이의 뒤틀림으로 주의가 옮겨 가 버렸다."]], [["‘A sweet lady, Master Copperfield!’ said Uriah Heep.", "\"참 좋은 분이세요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어 힙이 말했다."], ["‘She has a great admiration for Miss Agnes, Master Copperfield, I believe?’", "\"고모님이 애그니스 아가씨를 무척 흠모하신다지요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제가 알기로는?\""]], [["I said, ‘Yes,’ boldly; not that I knew anything about it, Heaven forgive me!", "나는 \"응\" 하고 당돌하게 대답했다. 그것에 대해 아는 게 전혀 없으면서 말이다, 하느님, 용서하소서!"]], [["‘I hope you have, too, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah.", "\"도련님도 그러시기를 바랍니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 말했다."], ["‘But I am sure you must have.’", "\"하지만 분명히 그러시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Everybody must have,’ I returned.", "\"누구든 그럴 수밖에 없지.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, thank you, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah Heep, ‘for that remark!", "\"아, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 유라이어 힙이 말했다."], ["It is so true! Umble as I am, I know it is so true!", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니! 정말 맞는 말씀이에요!"], ["Oh, thank you, Master Copperfield!’ He writhed himself quite off his stool in the excitement of his feelings, and, being off, began to make arrangements for going home.", "미천한 저지만, 저도 그게 맞는 말이라는 걸 알지요! 아, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그는 감정이 북받쳐 걸상에서 몸을 배배 꼬며 내려오더니, 일단 내려오자, 집에 갈 채비를 하기 시작했다."]], [["‘Mother will be expecting me,’ he said, referring to a pale, inexpressive-faced watch in his pocket, ‘and getting uneasy; for though we are very umble, Master Copperfield, we are much attached to one another.", "\"어머니가 저를 기다리실 거예요.\" 그가 주머니에서 핼쑥하고 표정 없는 얼굴을 한 회중시계를 꺼내 보며 말했다. \"그리고 마음을 졸이시겠지요."], ["If you would come and see us, any afternoon, and take a cup of tea at our lowly dwelling, mother would be as proud of your company as I should be.’", "저희가 아주 미천하긴 해도, 코퍼필드 도련님, 서로 무척 정이 깊거든요. 언제 오후에라도 저희를 찾아오셔서, 저희 보잘것없는 거처에서 차라도 한 잔 드신다면, 제가 그렇듯 어머니도 도련님이 와 주신 걸 자랑스러워하실 거예요.\""]], [["I said I should be glad to come.", "나는 기꺼이 가겠다고 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, putting his book away upon the shelf--‘I suppose you stop here, some time, Master Copperfield?’", "\"고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이어가 책을 선반에 도로 꽂으며 대답했다. \"도련님은 한동안 여기 머무르시는 거지요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\""]], [["I said I was going to be brought up there, I believed, as long as I remained at school.", "나는 학교에 다니는 동안에는 줄곧 거기서 자라게 될 것 같다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, indeed!’ exclaimed Uriah.", "\"아, 그렇군요!\" 유라이어가 소리쳤다."], ["‘I should think YOU would come into the business at last, Master Copperfield!’", "\"제 생각엔 도련님이 결국 이 사업에 들어오시게 될 것 같은데요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["I protested that I had no views of that sort, and that no such scheme was entertained in my behalf by anybody; but Uriah insisted on blandly replying to all my assurances, ‘Oh, yes, Master Copperfield, I should think you would, indeed!’ and, ‘Oh, indeed, Master Copperfield, I should think you would, certainly!’ over and over again.", "나는 그런 생각은 전혀 없으며, 누구도 나를 두고 그런 계획을 품고 있지 않다고 항변했다. 그러나 유라이어는 내 모든 다짐에 대해 능청스럽게, \"아, 그럼요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제 생각엔 정말 그러실 것 같아요!\", \"아, 그렇고말고요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제 생각엔 분명히 그러실 거예요!\""], ["Being, at last, ready to leave the office for the night, he asked me if it would suit my convenience to have the light put out; and on my answering ‘Yes,’ instantly extinguished it.", "하고 자꾸만 되풀이해 대꾸했다. 마침내 밤이 깊어 사무실을 떠날 채비를 마치자, 그는 불을 꺼도 괜찮으시겠냐고 나에게 물었고, 내가 \"그래\" 하고 대답하자 즉시 불을 껐다."], ["After shaking hands with me--his hand felt like a fish, in the dark--he opened the door into the street a very little, and crept out, and shut it, leaving me to grope my way back into the house: which cost me some trouble and a fall over his stool.", "나와 악수를 한 다음 — 어둠 속에서 그의 손은 마치 물고기 같은 느낌이었다 — 그는 거리로 난 문을 아주 조금 열고, 살금살금 빠져나가, 문을 닫았다. 나만 남겨 두고서, 더듬더듬 집 안으로 돌아가게 한 것인데, 그게 꽤나 애를 먹였고, 그의 걸상에 걸려 넘어지기까지 했다."], ["This was the proximate cause, I suppose, of my dreaming about him, for what appeared to me to be half the night; and dreaming, among other things, that he had launched Mr. Peggotty’s house on a piratical expedition, with a black flag at the masthead, bearing the inscription ‘Tidd’s Practice’, under which diabolical ensign he was carrying me and little Em’ly to the Spanish Main, to be drowned.", "짐작건대 이것이 그를 꿈에서 보게 된 직접적인 원인이었을 텐데, 나에게는 밤의 절반쯤은 되어 보이는 동안 내내 꿈을 꾸었다. 무엇보다, 꿈에서 그가 패거티 씨의 집을 띄워 해적 원정에 나서게 했는데, 돛대 꼭대기에는 '티드의 법률 실무'라고 새긴 검은 깃발이 걸려 있었고, 그 악마 같은 깃발 아래서 그가 나와 어린 에밀리를 익사시키려고 스페인령 해안으로 실어 가는 꿈이었다."]], [["I got a little the better of my uneasiness when I went to school next day, and a good deal the better next day, and so shook it off by degrees, that in less than a fortnight I was quite at home, and happy, among my new companions.", "이튿날 학교에 갔을 때 내 불안은 조금 가셨고, 그다음 날에는 훨씬 더 가셨다. 그렇게 차차 그것을 떨쳐 내, 채 보름도 지나지 않아 나는 새 동무들 사이에서 아주 편안하고 행복해졌다."], ["I was awkward enough in their games, and backward enough in their studies; but custom would improve me in the first respect, I hoped, and hard work in the second.", "그들의 놀이에는 충분히 어설펐고, 공부에서도 충분히 뒤처졌다. 하지만 첫째 점에서는 습관이 나를 나아지게 해 주리라 기대했고, 둘째 점에서는 노력이 그래 주리라 기대했다."], ["Accordingly, I went to work very hard, both in play and in earnest, and gained great commendation.", "그리하여 나는 놀이에서든 진지한 공부에서든 무척 열심히 매달려, 큰 칭찬을 받았다."], ["And, in a very little while, the Murdstone and Grinby life became so strange to me that I hardly believed in it, while my present life grew so familiar, that I seemed to have been leading it a long time.", "그리고 아주 짧은 사이에, 머드스톤앤그린비 시절은 어찌나 낯설어졌던지 그것이 있었다는 게 좀처럼 믿기지 않을 지경이었고, 반면에 지금의 생활은 어찌나 익숙해졌던지 오래도록 그 생활을 해 온 것만 같았다."]], [["Doctor Strong’s was an excellent school; as different from Mr.", "스트롱 박사님의 학교는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 학교였다."], ["Creakle’s as good is from evil. It was very gravely and decorously ordered, and on a sound system; with an appeal, in everything, to the honour and good faith of the boys, and an avowed intention to rely on their possession of those qualities unless they proved themselves unworthy of it, which worked wonders.", "선과 악만큼이나 크리클 씨의 학교와는 딴판이었다. 무척 진중하고 예의 바르게 운영되었고, 건전한 체계 위에 서 있었으며, 매사에 학생들의 명예와 신의에 호소했고, 학생들이 스스로 그럴 자격이 없음을 입증하지 않는 한 그들이 그런 자질을 지녔음을 믿겠다는 뜻을 떳떳이 밝혔는데, 그것이 놀라운 효과를 냈다."], ["We all felt that we had a part in the management of the place, and in sustaining its character and dignity.", "우리 모두는 이 학교를 꾸려 가고, 그 명예와 품위를 지키는 데 우리 몫이 있다고 느꼈다."], ["Hence, we soon became warmly attached to it--I am sure I did for one, and I never knew, in all my time, of any other boy being otherwise--and learnt with a good will, desiring to do it credit.", "그래서 우리는 곧 그곳에 따뜻하게 정이 들었다 — 적어도 나만큼은 분명히 그랬고, 내가 다니는 동안 다르게 구는 아이는 한 명도 본 적이 없다 — 그리고 학교의 명예를 드높이고자 기꺼운 마음으로 배웠다."], ["We had noble games out of hours, and plenty of liberty; but even then, as I remember, we were well spoken of in the town, and rarely did any disgrace, by our appearance or manner, to the reputation of Doctor Strong and Doctor Strong’s boys.", "수업이 없는 시간에는 멋진 놀이를 했고, 자유도 넘쳤다. 하지만 그때조차도, 내 기억으로는, 우리는 마을에서 좋은 평판을 들었고, 우리의 행색이나 태도로 스트롱 박사님과 스트롱 박사님 학생들의 명성에 먹칠을 하는 일은 거의 없었다."]], [["Some of the higher scholars boarded in the Doctor’s house, and through them I learned, at second hand, some particulars of the Doctor’s history--as, how he had not yet been married twelve months to the beautiful young lady I had seen in the study, whom he had married for love; for she had not a sixpence, and had a world of poor relations (so our fellows said) ready to swarm the Doctor out of house and home.", "상급생 가운데 몇몇은 박사님 댁에서 하숙을 했는데, 그들을 통해 나는 박사님 내력의 몇 가지 자세한 사정을 간접적으로 알게 되었다. 이를테면, 박사님이 서재에서 본 그 아름다운 젊은 부인과 결혼한 지 아직 일 년도 안 되었고, 사랑해서 결혼한 것이라는 것 — 부인에게는 단돈 육 펜스도 없었고, 가난한 친척이 세상에 가득해서 (우리 아이들 말로는) 박사님을 집 밖으로 떼 지어 몰아낼 판이었으니까. 또한 박사님이 늘 생각에 잠긴 듯한 태도를 보이는 것은, 노상 그리스어 어근을 찾는 일에 매달려 있기 때문이라는 것."], ["Also, how the Doctor’s cogitating manner was attributable to his being always engaged in looking out for Greek roots; which, in my innocence and ignorance, I supposed to be a botanical furor on the Doctor’s part, especially as he always looked at the ground when he walked about, until I understood that they were roots of words, with a view to a new Dictionary which he had in contemplation.", "나는 순진하고 무지한 탓에, 그게 박사님의 식물학적 열병인 줄로만 알았다. 특히 박사님이 거닐 때면 늘 땅바닥을 내려다보았기 때문이다. 그것이 박사님이 구상 중인 새 사전을 위한, 낱말의 어근이라는 것을 알게 되기 전까지는 말이다."], ["Adams, our head-boy, who had a turn for mathematics, had made a calculation, I was informed, of the time this Dictionary would take in completing, on the Doctor’s plan, and at the Doctor’s rate of going.", "수학에 소질이 있던 우리 우두머리 학생 애덤스가, 들리는 말로는, 박사님 계획대로, 또 박사님의 진행 속도로, 이 사전을 완성하는 데 걸릴 시간을 계산해 보았다고 했다."], ["He considered that it might be done in one thousand six hundred and forty-nine years, counting from the Doctor’s last, or sixty-second, birthday.", "그의 계산으로는, 박사님의 지난 예순두 번째 생일부터 세어, 천육백사십구 년이 걸릴 거라고 했다."]], [["But the Doctor himself was the idol of the whole school: and it must have been a badly composed school if he had been anything else, for he was the kindest of men; with a simple faith in him that might have touched the stone hearts of the very urns upon the wall.", "하지만 박사님 자신은 학교 전체의 우상이었다. 그가 그렇지 않았다면 형편없이 짜인 학교였을 게 틀림없는데, 그는 더없이 친절한 사람이었고, 그 안에는, 담장 위 항아리들의 돌처럼 굳은 마음마저 움직일 만한 순진한 믿음이 깃들어 있었으니까."], ["As he walked up and down that part of the courtyard which was at the side of the house, with the stray rooks and jackdaws looking after him with their heads cocked slyly, as if they knew how much more knowing they were in worldly affairs than he, if any sort of vagabond could only get near enough to his creaking shoes to attract his attention to one sentence of a tale of distress, that vagabond was made for the next two days.", "그가 집 옆쪽 안마당을 거닐 때면, 떠돌이 까마귀와 갈까마귀들이 고개를 음흉하게 갸웃하며 그를 좇아보았는데, 마치 세상일에 관해서라면 자기들이 그보다 얼마나 더 약은지를 안다는 듯했다. 그럴 때, 어떤 부랑자든 그의 삐걱대는 신발 가까이 다가가, 가련한 사연 한 토막에 그의 관심을 끌기만 하면, 그 부랑자는 이틀은 팔자가 폈다. 이는 그 집에서 어찌나 유명했던지, 선생들과 우두머리 학생들은 이 침입자들을 모서리에서 가로채고, 창문으로 나가, 박사님이 그 존재를 알아채기 전에 안마당에서 내쫓느라 애를 썼다."], ["It was so notorious in the house, that the masters and head-boys took pains to cut these marauders off at angles, and to get out of windows, and turn them out of the courtyard, before they could make the Doctor aware of their presence; which was sometimes happily effected within a few yards of him, without his knowing anything of the matter, as he jogged to and fro.", "박사님이 종종걸음으로 오락가락하는 동안, 더러는 그가 까맣게 모른 채로, 그의 코앞 몇 발짝 안에서 일이 다행히도 처리되곤 했다. 자기 영역 바깥에서, 보호받지 못할 때면, 그는 털 깎으러 온 사람 앞의 영락없는 양이었다. 그는 자기 다리에서 각반을 벗어서까지 내줄 사람이었다."], ["Outside his own domain, and unprotected, he was a very sheep for the shearers. He would have taken his gaiters off his legs, to give away.", "사실, 우리 사이에는 이런 이야기가 떠돌았다 (무슨 근거였는지는 도통 모르고, 알았던 적도 없지만, 하도 오랜 세월 믿어 와서 이젠 사실이라고 꽤 확신하게 되었다)."], ["In fact, there was a story current among us (I have no idea, and never had, on what authority, but I have believed it for so many years that I feel quite certain it is true), that on a frosty day, one winter-time, he actually did bestow his gaiters on a beggar-woman, who occasioned some scandal in the neighbourhood by exhibiting a fine infant from door to door, wrapped in those garments, which were universally recognized, being as well known in the vicinity as the Cathedral.", "어느 겨울, 서리 내린 날, 박사님이 정말로 자기 각반을 어떤 거지 여인에게 내주었다는 것이다. 그 여인은 잘생긴 갓난아기를 그 각반으로 싸서 이 집 저 집 보여 주며 다녀, 동네에 적잖은 소동을 일으켰다는데, 그 각반은 누구나 다 알아보았다. 대성당만큼이나 동네에서 익히 알려진 물건이었으니까."], ["The legend added that the only person who did not identify them was the Doctor himself, who, when they were shortly afterwards displayed at the door of a little second-hand shop of no very good repute, where such things were taken in exchange for gin, was more than once observed to handle them approvingly, as if admiring some curious novelty in the pattern, and considering them an improvement on his own.", "전설은 이렇게 덧붙였다. 그 각반을 알아보지 못한 단 한 사람이 바로 박사님 자신이었는데, 얼마 뒤 그 각반이, 이런 물건을 진을 받고 바꿔 주는 평판이 그리 좋지 않은 어느 작은 중고품 가게 문 앞에 내걸렸을 때, 박사님이 그것을 흐뭇하게 만지작거리는 모습이 여러 번 목격되었다는 것이다. 마치 무늬의 진귀한 새로움에 감탄하며, 자기 것보다 더 나은 물건이라 여기는 듯이."]], [["It was very pleasant to see the Doctor with his pretty young wife.", "박사님이 그 어여쁜 젊은 아내와 함께 있는 모습은 보기에 무척 흐뭇했다."], ["He had a fatherly, benignant way of showing his fondness for her, which seemed in itself to express a good man.", "그는 아내에 대한 애정을 드러낼 때 아버지처럼 자애로운 태도를 보였는데, 그것만으로도 그가 선한 사람임을 말해 주는 듯했다."], ["I often saw them walking in the garden where the peaches were, and I sometimes had a nearer observation of them in the study or the parlour.", "나는 복숭아가 있는 정원에서 그들이 거니는 것을 자주 보았고, 서재나 응접실에서 더 가까이서 그들을 살펴볼 때도 더러 있었다."], ["She appeared to me to take great care of the Doctor, and to like him very much, though I never thought her vitally interested in the Dictionary: some cumbrous fragments of which work the Doctor always carried in his pockets, and in the lining of his hat, and generally seemed to be expounding to her as they walked about.", "부인은 내 눈에 박사님을 무척 살뜰히 보살피고, 그를 무척 좋아하는 듯이 보였다. 다만 사전에 절실히 관심이 있다고는 결코 생각하지 않았다. 박사님은 그 사전의 거추장스러운 토막들을 늘 호주머니며 모자 안감 속에 넣고 다녔고, 거닐면서 으레 부인에게 그것을 풀어 설명하는 것 같았다."]], [["I saw a good deal of Mrs. Strong, both because she had taken a liking for me on the morning of my introduction to the Doctor, and was always afterwards kind to me, and interested in me; and because she was very fond of Agnes, and was often backwards and forwards at our house.", "나는 스트롱 부인을 자주 보았는데, 부인이 내가 박사님께 소개받던 그날 아침에 나를 마음에 들어 했고, 그 후로 늘 나에게 친절하며 관심을 가져 주었기 때문이기도 했고, 또 부인이 애그니스를 무척 좋아해서 우리 집에 자주 드나들었기 때문이기도 했다."], ["There was a curious constraint between her and Mr. Wickfield, I thought (of whom she seemed to be afraid), that never wore off.", "내가 보기에 부인과 위크필드 씨 사이에는 묘한 어색함이 있었는데 (부인은 그를 무서워하는 듯했다), 그것이 좀처럼 가시지 않았다."], ["When she came there of an evening, she always shrunk from accepting his escort home, and ran away with me instead.", "부인이 저녁에 우리 집에 왔을 때면, 늘 그가 집까지 바래다주겠다는 것을 마다하고, 대신 나와 함께 달아나곤 했다."], ["And sometimes, as we were running gaily across the Cathedral yard together, expecting to meet nobody, we would meet Mr. Jack Maldon, who was always surprised to see us.", "그리고 때때로, 우리가 아무도 마주치지 않으리라 여기며 대성당 안마당을 함께 명랑하게 달려갈 때면, 잭 맬던 씨와 마주치곤 했는데, 그는 늘 우리를 보고 놀라워했다."]], [["Mrs. Strong’s mama was a lady I took great delight in.", "스트롱 부인의 어머니는 내가 무척 좋아한 부인이었다."], ["Her name was Mrs. Markleham; but our boys used to call her the Old Soldier, on account of her generalship, and the skill with which she marshalled great forces of relations against the Doctor.", "이름은 마클럼 부인이었는데, 우리 아이들은 그분을 '노병'이라고 부르곤 했다. 통솔력이 대단하고, 박사님에 맞서 친척의 대군을 거느리는 솜씨가 능란했기 때문이다."], ["She was a little, sharp-eyed woman, who used to wear, when she was dressed, one unchangeable cap, ornamented with some artificial flowers, and two artificial butterflies supposed to be hovering above the flowers.", "작은 키에 눈매가 날카로운 부인으로, 차려입을 때면 한 가지 변함없는 모자를 썼는데, 거기엔 조화 몇 송이가 장식되어 있고, 그 꽃 위에 떠 있는 듯한 조화 나비 두 마리가 달려 있었다."], ["There was a superstition among us that this cap had come from France, and could only originate in the workmanship of that ingenious nation: but all I certainly know about it, is, that it always made its appearance of an evening, wheresoever Mrs. Markleham made HER appearance; that it was carried about to friendly meetings in a Hindoo basket; that the butterflies had the gift of trembling constantly; and that they improved the shining hours at Doctor Strong’s expense, like busy bees.", "우리 사이에는 이 모자가 프랑스에서 온 것이고, 그 솜씨 좋은 나라 사람들의 손끝에서나 나올 법한 것이라는 미신이 떠돌았다. 하지만 내가 그것에 대해 확실히 아는 거라곤, 마클럼 부인이 어디에 나타나든 그 모자도 으레 저녁이면 나타났다는 것, 그것이 힌두식 바구니에 담겨 친목 모임에 들려 다녔다는 것, 그 나비들에게는 끊임없이 떨리는 재주가 있었다는 것, 그리고 그것들이 부지런한 벌처럼 빛나는 시간을 알차게 보냈다는 것 — 스트롱 박사님의 돈으로 — 뿐이다."]], [["I observed the Old Soldier--not to adopt the name disrespectfully--to pretty good advantage, on a night which is made memorable to me by something else I shall relate.", "나는 이 노병을 — 무례하게 그렇게 부르려는 건 아니다 — 어느 날 밤 꽤 잘 살펴보게 되었는데, 그날 밤은 내가 따로 이야기할 어떤 일 때문에 나에게 잊지 못할 밤이 되었다."], ["It was the night of a little party at the Doctor’s, which was given on the occasion of Mr.", "잭 맬던 씨가 인도로 떠나는 것을 기념해 박사님 댁에서 열린 작은 모임의 밤이었다."], ["Jack Maldon’s departure for India, whither he was going as a cadet, or something of that kind: Mr. Wickfield having at length arranged the business.", "그는 사관후보생인지 뭔지로 인도에 가는 길이었는데, 위크필드 씨가 마침내 그 일을 마무리 지어 주었던 것이다."], ["It happened to be the Doctor’s birthday, too.", "마침 그날은 박사님의 생일이기도 했다."], ["We had had a holiday, had made presents to him in the morning, had made a speech to him through the head-boy, and had cheered him until we were hoarse, and until he had shed tears.", "우리는 휴일을 보냈고, 아침에 박사님께 선물을 드렸고, 우두머리 학생을 통해 박사님께 축사를 했으며, 목이 쉬고 박사님이 눈물을 흘릴 때까지 만세를 외쳤다."], ["And now, in the evening, Mr. Wickfield, Agnes, and I, went to have tea with him in his private capacity.", "그리고 이제, 저녁에 위크필드 씨와 애그니스와 나는 사사로이 박사님과 함께 차를 마시러 갔다."]], [["Mr. Jack Maldon was there, before us. Mrs.", "잭 맬던 씨는 우리보다 먼저 와 있었다."], ["Strong, dressed in white, with cherry-coloured ribbons, was playing the piano, when we went in; and he was leaning over her to turn the leaves.", "스트롱 부인은 흰옷에 앵두빛 리본을 두르고서 우리가 들어갔을 때 피아노를 치고 있었고, 그는 부인 위로 몸을 기울여 악보를 넘겨 주고 있었다."], ["The clear red and white of her complexion was not so blooming and flower-like as usual, I thought, when she turned round; but she looked very pretty, Wonderfully pretty.", "부인이 고개를 돌렸을 때, 그 또렷한 붉고 흰 낯빛이 여느 때처럼 화사하게 꽃 같지는 않다고 나는 생각했다. 하지만 부인은 무척 어여뻤다. 놀랍도록 어여뻤다."]], [["‘I have forgotten, Doctor,’ said Mrs.", "\"제가 깜빡했네요, 박사님.\""], ["Strong’s mama, when we were seated, ‘to pay you the compliments of the day--though they are, as you may suppose, very far from being mere compliments in my case.", "우리가 자리에 앉자 스트롱 부인의 어머니가 말했다. \"생신 인사를 드리는 걸요 — 물론 짐작하시겠지만, 제 경우엔 그게 그저 입에 발린 인사와는 한참 거리가 멀지요."], ["Allow me to wish you many happy returns.’", "앞으로 행복한 생신을 많이 맞으시기를 빌게 해 주세요.\""]], [["‘I thank you, ma’am,’ replied the Doctor.", "\"고맙습니다, 부인.\" 박사님이 대답했다."]], [["‘Many, many, many, happy returns,’ said the Old Soldier.", "\"많이, 많이, 많이 맞으세요, 행복한 생신을.\" 노병이 말했다."], ["‘Not only for your own sake, but for Annie’s, and John Maldon’s, and many other people’s.", "\"박사님 자신을 위해서만이 아니라, 애니를 위해서도, 존 맬던을 위해서도, 다른 많은 사람을 위해서도요."], ["It seems but yesterday to me, John, when you were a little creature, a head shorter than Master Copperfield, making baby love to Annie behind the gooseberry bushes in the back-garden.’", "나에겐 엊그제 일만 같구나, 존, 네가 자그마한 꼬마였을 때, 코퍼필드 도련님보다 머리 하나는 작았을 때, 뒤뜰 구스베리 덤불 뒤에서 애니에게 어린애 같은 사랑을 속삭이던 게 말이다.\""]], [["‘My dear mama,’ said Mrs.", "\"엄마, 어머나.\" 스트롱 부인이 말했다."], ["Strong, ‘never mind that now.’", "\"이제 와서 그 얘긴 그만하세요.\""]], [["‘Annie, don’t be absurd,’ returned her mother.", "\"애니, 우습게 굴지 마라.\" 어머니가 받았다."], ["‘If you are to blush to hear of such things now you are an old married woman, when are you not to blush to hear of them?’", "\"이제 늙은 유부녀가 다 되어서 그런 얘기를 듣고 얼굴을 붉힐 거라면, 대체 언제는 얼굴 안 붉히고 듣겠다는 게냐?\""]], [["‘Old?’ exclaimed Mr. Jack Maldon.", "\"늙은?\" 잭 맬던 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Annie? Come!’", "\"애니가요? 어이쿠!\""]], [["‘Yes, John,’ returned the Soldier.", "\"그래, 존.\" 노병이 받았다."], ["‘Virtually, an old married woman.", "\"사실상 늙은 유부녀지."], ["Although not old by years--for when did you ever hear me say, or who has ever heard me say, that a girl of twenty was old by years!--your cousin is the wife of the Doctor, and, as such, what I have described her.", "나이로야 늙지 않았지 — 내가 언제 스무 살 처녀를 두고 나이로 늙었다고 말한 적이 있더냐, 그런 말을 들은 사람이 누가 있어! — 하지만 네 사촌은 박사님의 아내이고, 그런 만큼, 내가 말한 그대로다."], ["It is well for you, John, that your cousin is the wife of the Doctor.", "너에겐 다행한 일이지, 존, 네 사촌이 박사님의 아내라는 게."], ["You have found in him an influential and kind friend, who will be kinder yet, I venture to predict, if you deserve it.", "너는 그분에게서 영향력 있고 친절한 벗을 얻은 거고, 네가 그럴 자격이 있다면 더욱 친절해지시리라고, 내 감히 점쳐 본다."], ["I have no false pride.", "나는 헛된 자존심 따위 없어."], ["I never hesitate to admit, frankly, that there are some members of our family who want a friend.", "우리 집안 식구 중에 벗이 필요한 이들이 더러 있다는 걸 솔직히 인정하는 데 한 번도 망설인 적이 없지."], ["You were one yourself, before your cousin’s influence raised up one for you.’", "너도 한때 그중 하나였잖느냐, 네 사촌의 영향력이 너에게 벗을 마련해 주기 전까지는.\""]], [["The Doctor, in the goodness of his heart, waved his hand as if to make light of it, and save Mr. Jack Maldon from any further reminder.", "박사님은, 그 너그러운 마음으로, 별일 아니라는 듯, 또 잭 맬던 씨에게 더는 그 일을 들먹이지 않도록 손을 내저었다."], ["But Mrs. Markleham changed her chair for one next the Doctor’s, and putting her fan on his coat-sleeve, said:", "하지만 마클럼 부인은 자리를 박사님 옆자리로 옮기더니, 부채를 그의 윗도리 소매에 얹으며 말했다."]], [["‘No, really, my dear Doctor, you must excuse me if I appear to dwell on this rather, because I feel so very strongly.", "\"아니에요, 정말로, 박사님, 제가 이 얘기를 좀 자꾸 곱씹는 것처럼 보여도 양해해 주세요, 워낙 절절히 느껴서 그러니까요."], ["I call it quite my monomania, it is such a subject of mine.", "이건 정말 제 광적인 집착이라고 할까요, 제가 그렇게 좋아하는 주제거든요."], ["You are a blessing to us.", "박사님은 저희에게 축복이에요."], ["You really are a Boon, you know.’", "정말이지 박사님은 하늘이 주신 선물이세요, 아시잖아요.\""]], [["‘Nonsense, nonsense,’ said the Doctor.", "\"별말씀을, 별말씀을.\" 박사님이 말했다."]], [["‘No, no, I beg your pardon,’ retorted the Old Soldier.", "\"아니에요, 아니, 죄송하지만요.\""], ["‘With nobody present, but our dear and confidential friend Mr. Wickfield, I cannot consent to be put down.", "노병이 받았다. \"여기 우리 소중하고 막역한 벗인 위크필드 씨밖에 없는 자리에서, 제가 입을 다물 수는 없지요."], ["I shall begin to assert the privileges of a mother-in-law, if you go on like that, and scold you.", "박사님이 계속 그러시면, 저는 장모의 권리를 내세우기 시작할 거예요, 그리고 박사님을 야단칠 거고요."], ["I am perfectly honest and outspoken.", "저는 더없이 정직하고 거침이 없는 사람이에요."], ["What I am saying, is what I said when you first overpowered me with surprise--you remember how surprised I was?--by proposing for Annie.", "지금 제가 하는 말은, 박사님이 애니에게 청혼해서 저를 놀라움으로 압도하셨을 때 — 제가 얼마나 놀랐는지 기억하시죠? — 그때 제가 했던 말 그대로예요."], ["Not that there was anything so very much out of the way, in the mere fact of the proposal--it would be ridiculous to say that!--but because, you having known her poor father, and having known her from a baby six months old, I hadn’t thought of you in such a light at all, or indeed as a marrying man in any way,--simply that, you know.’", "청혼했다는 그 사실 자체에 그렇게 엄청나게 뜻밖인 게 있었다는 건 아니에요 — 그렇게 말하면 우습지요! — 다만 박사님이 그 애의 가엾은 아버지를 아셨고, 그 애가 생후 여섯 달 갓난아기 때부터 알아 오셨으니, 박사님을 그런 눈으로는 전혀 생각해 본 적이 없었던 거예요, 아니 결혼할 사람이라고도 도통 생각 못 했지요 — 그저 그뿐이에요, 아시잖아요.\""]], [["‘Aye, aye,’ returned the Doctor, good-humouredly.", "\"그래요, 그래요.\" 박사님이 사람 좋게 받았다."], ["‘Never mind.’", "\"괘념치 마세요.\""]], [["‘But I DO mind,’ said the Old Soldier, laying her fan upon his lips.", "\"하지만 저는 괘념해요.\" 노병이 부채를 그의 입술에 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["‘I mind very much.", "\"아주 많이 괘념하지요."], ["I recall these things that I may be contradicted if I am wrong. Well!", "제가 틀렸다면 반박당하라고, 이런 일들을 끄집어내는 거예요. 자!"], ["Then I spoke to Annie, and I told her what had happened.", "그래서 제가 애니에게 말을 꺼냈고, 무슨 일이 있었는지 일러 줬어요."], ["I said, “My dear, here’s Doctor Strong has positively been and made you the subject of a handsome declaration and an offer.”", "'얘야, 글쎄 스트롱 박사님이 분명히 너를 두고 점잖게 마음을 밝히시고 청혼을 하셨단다.' 제가 조금이라도 그걸 밀어붙였나요? 아니에요. 저는 말했지요."], ["Did I press it in the least? No.", "'자 애니, 지금 당장 솔직히 말해 보렴."], ["I said, “Now, Annie, tell me the truth this moment; is your heart free?” “Mama,”", "네 마음에 둔 사람이 없니?' '엄마,' 그 애가 울면서 말하더군요, '저는 너무 어려요'"], ["she said crying, “I am extremely young”--which was perfectly true--“and I hardly know if I have a heart at all.”", "— 그건 더없이 맞는 말이었지요 — '그리고 제게 마음이라는 게 있기는 한지도 잘 모르겠어요.'"], ["“Then, my dear,”", "'그럼, 얘야,'"], ["I said, “you may rely upon it, it’s free.", "제가 말했지요, '믿어도 좋다, 네 마음은 자유로운 거란다."], ["At all events, my love,”", "어쨌거나, 우리 사랑하는 딸아,'"], ["said I, “Doctor Strong is in an agitated state of mind, and must be answered.", "제가 말했지요, '스트롱 박사님이 마음이 어지러운 상태시고, 대답을 들으셔야 해."], ["He cannot be kept in his present state of suspense.”", "지금 같은 애타는 상태로 두실 수는 없어.' '엄마,'"], ["“Mama,” said Annie, still crying, “would he be unhappy without me?", "애니가 여전히 울면서 말하더군요, '제가 없으면 그분이 불행하실까요?"], ["If he would, I honour and respect him so much, that I think I will have him.”", "그러시다면, 저는 그분을 무척 우러르고 존경하니까, 그분과 결혼해야겠어요.'"], ["So it was settled.", "그렇게 정해진 거예요."], ["And then, and not till then, I said to Annie, “Annie, Doctor Strong will not only be your husband, but he will represent your late father: he will represent the head of our family, he will represent the wisdom and station, and I may say the means, of our family; and will be, in short, a Boon to it.”", "그러고 나서야, 그제야 비로소, 제가 애니에게 말했지요. '애니, 스트롱 박사님은 네 남편이 되어 주실 뿐 아니라, 네 돌아가신 아버지를 대신하실 거다. 우리 집안의 가장을 대신하실 거고, 우리 집안의 지혜와 지위를, 그리고 말하자면, 재력을 대신하실 거야."], ["I used the word at the time, and I have used it again, today.", "한마디로, 우리 집안에 하늘이 주신 선물이 되실 게다.' 저는 그때 그 말을 썼고, 오늘 다시 그 말을 썼지요."], ["If I have any merit it is consistency.’", "저에게 무슨 미덕이 있다면, 그건 한결같음이에요.\""]], [["The daughter had sat quite silent and still during this speech, with her eyes fixed on the ground; her cousin standing near her, and looking on the ground too.", "이 연설이 이어지는 내내 딸은 눈을 땅바닥에 박은 채 아무 말 없이 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그 사촌은 곁에 서서 역시 땅바닥을 바라보고 있었다."], ["She now said very softly, in a trembling voice:", "이제 부인이 떨리는 목소리로 아주 나직이 말했다."]], [["‘Mama, I hope you have finished?’ ‘No, my dear Annie,’ returned the Old Soldier, ‘I have not quite finished.", "\"엄마, 다 끝나신 거죠?\" \"아니, 우리 애니,\" 노병이 받았다."], ["Since you ask me, my love, I reply that I have not.", "\"아직 다 못 끝냈단다. 네가 물어보니, 얘야, 다 못 끝냈다고 대답하마."], ["I complain that you really are a little unnatural towards your own family; and, as it is of no use complaining to you. I mean to complain to your husband.", "나는 네가 정말이지 네 친정 식구들한테 좀 매정하다고 불평하는 거란다. 그리고 너한테 불평해 봐야 소용없으니, 네 남편한테 불평할 작정이다."], ["Now, my dear Doctor, do look at that silly wife of yours.’", "자, 박사님, 저 어리석은 부인 좀 보세요.\""]], [["As the Doctor turned his kind face, with its smile of simplicity and gentleness, towards her, she drooped her head more.", "박사님이 그 친절한 얼굴을, 순진하고 온화한 미소를 띤 채, 부인 쪽으로 돌리자, 부인은 고개를 더 떨구었다."], ["I noticed that Mr. Wickfield looked at her steadily.", "나는 위크필드 씨가 부인을 가만히 응시하는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘When I happened to say to that naughty thing, the other day,’ pursued her mother, shaking her head and her fan at her, playfully, ‘that there was a family circumstance she might mention to you--indeed, I think, was bound to mention--she said, that to mention it was to ask a favour; and that, as you were too generous, and as for her to ask was always to have, she wouldn’t.’", "\"내가 며칠 전에 저 못된 것한테 우연히, 박사님께 말씀드릴 만한 — 정말이지, 마땅히 말씀드려야 한다고 생각하는 — 집안일이 하나 있다고 했더니,\" 어머니가 부인을 향해 장난스레 고개와 부채를 흔들며 이어 갔다. \"글쎄 저 애가, 그걸 말씀드리는 건 부탁드리는 거나 마찬가지고, 박사님이 워낙 너그러우셔서 저 애가 부탁하면 늘 들어주시니, 그래서 말씀 안 드리겠다지 뭐예요.\""]], [["‘Annie, my dear,’ said the Doctor.", "\"애니, 우리 딸.\" 박사님이 말했다."], ["‘That was wrong.", "\"그건 잘못이오."], ["It robbed me of a pleasure.’", "그건 나에게서 기쁨을 빼앗는 일이야.\""]], [["‘Almost the very words I said to her!’ exclaimed her mother.", "\"제가 저 애한테 한 말이랑 거의 토씨 하나 안 틀려요!\""], ["‘Now really, another time, when I know what she would tell you but for this reason, and won’t, I have a great mind, my dear Doctor, to tell you myself.’", "어머니가 외쳤다. \"자 정말로, 다음번에, 저 애가 이런 이유로 박사님께 말씀 안 드리려는 게 뭔지 제가 알게 되면, 박사님, 제가 직접 말씀드릴 마음이 굴뚝같네요.\""]], [["‘I shall be glad if you will,’ returned the Doctor.", "\"그래 주시면 기쁘겠습니다.\" 박사님이 대답했다."]], [["‘Shall I?’", "\"그럴까요?\""]], [["‘Certainly.’", "\"물론이지요.\""]], [["‘Well, then, I will!’ said the Old Soldier.", "\"그럼, 그렇게 하지요!\" 노병이 말했다."], ["‘That’s a bargain.’ And having, I suppose, carried her point, she tapped the Doctor’s hand several times with her fan (which she kissed first), and returned triumphantly to her former station.", "\"약속한 거예요.\" 그러고는, 짐작건대 자기 뜻을 관철한 셈이어서, 박사님의 손을 부채로 여러 번 가볍게 두드린 다음 (먼저 부채에 입을 맞추고서), 의기양양하게 원래 자리로 돌아갔다."]], [["Some more company coming in, among whom were the two masters and Adams, the talk became general; and it naturally turned on Mr. Jack Maldon, and his voyage, and the country he was going to, and his various plans and prospects.", "손님 몇이 더 들어왔는데, 그중에는 선생 둘과 애덤스도 있었다. 이야기는 자연히 잭 맬던 씨와 그의 항해, 그가 갈 나라, 그리고 그의 갖가지 계획과 전망 쪽으로 흘러갔다."], ["He was to leave that night, after supper, in a post-chaise, for Gravesend; where the ship, in which he was to make the voyage, lay; and was to be gone--unless he came home on leave, or for his health--I don’t know how many years.", "그는 그날 밤 저녁을 먹은 뒤, 역마차를 타고 그레이브젠드로 떠날 예정이었다. 그가 항해할 배가 거기 정박해 있었던 것이다. 그리고 — 휴가로 본국에 돌아오거나 건강 때문에 오는 게 아니라면 — 몇 년이 될지 모를 만큼 가 있을 작정이었다."], ["I recollect it was settled by general consent that India was quite a misrepresented country, and had nothing objectionable in it, but a tiger or two, and a little heat in the warm part of the day.", "내 기억으로는, 인도라는 나라가 꽤나 오해받는 나라이고, 호랑이 한두 마리와 한낮 더운 때의 약간의 더위 말고는 거슬릴 만한 게 없다는 데에 모두가 입을 모았다."], ["For my own part, I looked on Mr. Jack Maldon as a modern Sindbad, and pictured him the bosom friend of all the Rajahs in the East, sitting under canopies, smoking curly golden pipes--a mile long, if they could be straightened out.", "나로 말하면, 잭 맬던 씨를 현대판 신드바드로 여기고, 그가 동방의 온갖 왕족과 막역한 벗이 되어, 천막 아래 앉아, 똘똘 말린 황금 담뱃대를 — 곧게 펴면 일 마일은 됨 직한 — 빠는 모습을 그려 보았다."]], [["Mrs. Strong was a very pretty singer: as I knew, who often heard her singing by herself.", "스트롱 부인은 노래를 무척 잘했다. 부인이 혼자 노래하는 것을 자주 들었던 나는 잘 알았다."], ["But, whether she was afraid of singing before people, or was out of voice that evening, it was certain that she couldn’t sing at all.", "하지만 사람들 앞에서 노래하기가 두려웠던 건지, 아니면 그날 저녁 목 상태가 좋지 않았던 건지, 어쨌든 부인은 영 노래를 부르지 못했다."], ["She tried a duet, once, with her cousin Maldon, but could not so much as begin; and afterwards, when she tried to sing by herself, although she began sweetly, her voice died away on a sudden, and left her quite distressed, with her head hanging down over the keys.", "한번은 사촌 맬던과 이중창을 해 보려 했지만, 시작조차 하지 못했다. 그러고는, 혼자 불러 보려 했을 때도, 곱게 시작하다가, 갑자기 목소리가 잦아들어, 건반 위로 고개를 떨군 채 몹시 괴로워했다."], ["The good Doctor said she was nervous, and, to relieve her, proposed a round game at cards; of which he knew as much as of the art of playing the trombone.", "마음씨 좋은 박사님은 부인이 긴장한 거라며, 부인을 달래 주려고 카드 돌림 놀이를 하자고 했다. 박사님은 트롬본 연주술만큼이나 카드에 대해 잘 알았지만 말이다."], ["But I remarked that the Old Soldier took him into custody directly, for her partner; and instructed him, as the first preliminary of initiation, to give her all the silver he had in his pocket.", "그런데 나는 노병이 곧장 그를 자기 짝으로 잡아채는 것을 보았다. 그리고 입문의 첫 절차랍시고, 박사님에게 호주머니에 있는 은화를 죄다 자기한테 내놓으라고 가르쳤다."]], [["We had a merry game, not made the less merry by the Doctor’s mistakes, of which he committed an innumerable quantity, in spite of the watchfulness of the butterflies, and to their great aggravation. Mrs.", "우리는 즐겁게 놀았는데, 박사님의 실수 덕에 놀이가 한결 더 즐거워졌다. 나비들의 감시에도 아랑곳없이, 또 그 나비들을 크게 약 올리며, 박사님은 셀 수 없이 많은 실수를 저질렀다."], ["Strong had declined to play, on the ground of not feeling very well; and her cousin Maldon had excused himself because he had some packing to do.", "스트롱 부인은 몸이 영 좋지 않다는 이유로 놀이를 마다했고, 그 사촌 맬던도 짐을 좀 싸야 한다며 빠졌다."], ["When he had done it, however, he returned, and they sat together, talking, on the sofa.", "하지만 짐을 다 싸자, 그는 돌아와서 부인과 함께 소파에 앉아 이야기를 나누었다."], ["From time to time she came and looked over the Doctor’s hand, and told him what to play.", "이따금 부인은 와서 박사님의 패를 들여다보고, 무엇을 낼지 일러 주었다."], ["She was very pale, as she bent over him, and I thought her finger trembled as she pointed out the cards; but the Doctor was quite happy in her attention, and took no notice of this, if it were so.", "박사님 위로 몸을 숙일 때 부인은 무척 핼쑥했고, 카드를 가리키는 손가락이 떨리는 것 같았다. 하지만 박사님은 부인의 보살핌에 그저 흐뭇해할 뿐, 설령 그랬다 하더라도 그것을 알아채지 못했다."]], [["At supper, we were hardly so gay. Everyone appeared to feel that a parting of that sort was an awkward thing, and that the nearer it approached, the more awkward it was. Mr.", "저녁 식사 때 우리는 그다지 흥겹지 못했다. 그런 종류의 작별이 거북한 일이고, 그것이 가까워질수록 더 거북해진다는 것을, 다들 느끼는 듯했다."], ["Jack Maldon tried to be very talkative, but was not at his ease, and made matters worse.", "잭 맬던 씨는 무척 말이 많은 척했지만, 편치 못한 기색이었고, 일을 더 그르쳤다."], ["And they were not improved, as it appeared to me, by the Old Soldier: who continually recalled passages of Mr. Jack Maldon’s youth.", "게다가 내가 보기에, 노병 탓에 분위기가 나아지지 않았다. 그녀가 자꾸만 잭 맬던 씨의 어린 시절 일들을 들추어냈기 때문이다."]], [["The Doctor, however, who felt, I am sure, that he was making everybody happy, was well pleased, and had no suspicion but that we were all at the utmost height of enjoyment.", "하지만 박사님은, 분명히 자기가 모두를 즐겁게 해 주고 있다고 느꼈는지, 무척 흡족해하며, 우리 모두가 더없이 즐거움의 절정에 있다고 믿어 의심치 않았다."]], [["‘Annie, my dear,’ said he, looking at his watch, and filling his glass, ‘it is past your cousin Jack’s time, and we must not detain him, since time and tide--both concerned in this case--wait for no man. Mr.", "\"애니, 우리 딸.\" 박사님이 회중시계를 보고 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"네 사촌 잭이 떠날 시간이 지났구나."], ["Jack Maldon, you have a long voyage, and a strange country, before you; but many men have had both, and many men will have both, to the end of time.", "그를 붙잡아 두어선 안 되지, 세월과 조수는 — 이 경우엔 둘 다 관련이 있는데 — 사람을 기다려 주지 않으니까. 잭 맬던 씨, 자네 앞에는 긴 항해와 낯선 나라가 놓여 있네."], ["The winds you are going to tempt, have wafted thousands upon thousands to fortune, and brought thousands upon thousands happily back.’", "하지만 많은 이들이 그 둘을 겪었고, 세상 끝나는 날까지 많은 이들이 또 그 둘을 겪을 걸세. 자네가 도전하려는 그 바람은 수천수만 명을 부귀로 실어 갔고, 또 수천수만 명을 무사히 데려왔다네.\""]], [["‘It’s an affecting thing,’ said Mrs.", "\"가슴 뭉클한 일이에요.\""], ["Markleham--‘however it’s viewed, it’s affecting, to see a fine young man one has known from an infant, going away to the other end of the world, leaving all he knows behind, and not knowing what’s before him.", "마클럼 부인이 말했다. \"어떻게 봐도, 가슴 뭉클하지요, 갓난아기 때부터 알아 온 훌륭한 청년이, 세상 저쪽 끝으로 떠나가고, 아는 것 다 뒤에 두고, 앞일은 모르는 채로 가는 걸 보는 게 말이에요."], ["A young man really well deserves constant support and patronage,’ looking at the Doctor, ‘who makes such sacrifices.’", "그런 희생을 치르는 청년이라면,\" 박사님을 바라보며, \"정말이지 변함없는 지원과 후원을 받을 자격이 있지요.\""]], [["‘Time will go fast with you, Mr.", "\"자네에게도 세월은 빨리 흐를 걸세, 잭 맬던 씨.\""], ["Jack Maldon,’ pursued the Doctor, ‘and fast with all of us.", "박사님이 이어 갔다. \"우리 모두에게도 빨리 흐르고."], ["Some of us can hardly expect, perhaps, in the natural course of things, to greet you on your return.", "어쩌면 우리 중 누군가는, 자연의 순리대로라면, 자네가 돌아올 때 맞아 줄 수 있으리라고 기대하기 어려울지도 모르지."], ["The next best thing is to hope to do it, and that’s my case.", "그다음으로 좋은 것은 그러기를 바라는 것이고, 내가 바로 그렇다네."], ["I shall not weary you with good advice.", "좋은 충고로 자네를 지치게 하진 않겠네."], ["You have long had a good model before you, in your cousin Annie.", "자네에겐 오랫동안 좋은 본보기가 곁에 있었으니, 자네 사촌 애니 말일세."], ["Imitate her virtues as nearly as you can.’", "그 사람의 미덕을 되도록 가까이 본받게나.\""]], [["Mrs. Markleham fanned herself, and shook her head.", "마클럼 부인은 부채질을 하며 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘Farewell, Mr.", "\"잘 가게, 잭.\""], ["Jack,’ said the Doctor, standing up; on which we all stood up.", "박사님이 일어서며 말했고, 그러자 우리도 모두 일어섰다."], ["‘A prosperous voyage out, a thriving career abroad, and a happy return home!’", "\"나가는 길의 순조로운 항해, 외국에서의 번창하는 앞길, 그리고 행복한 귀향을 비네!\""]], [["We all drank the toast, and all shook hands with Mr. Jack Maldon; after which he hastily took leave of the ladies who were there, and hurried to the door, where he was received, as he got into the chaise, with a tremendous broadside of cheers discharged by our boys, who had assembled on the lawn for the purpose.", "우리는 모두 그 축배를 들고, 모두 잭 맬던 씨와 악수를 했다. 그런 다음 그는 거기 있던 부인들에게 황급히 작별을 고하고, 서둘러 문으로 나갔는데, 마차에 오르는 그를, 그러려고 잔디밭에 모여 있던 우리 아이들이 우레 같은 만세 일제사격으로 맞아 주었다."], ["Running in among them to swell the ranks, I was very near the chaise when it rolled away; and I had a lively impression made upon me, in the midst of the noise and dust, of having seen Mr. Jack Maldon rattle past with an agitated face, and something cherry-coloured in his hand.", "나도 그 무리에 끼어 대열을 불리려고 달려간 터라, 마차가 굴러갈 때 마차 가까이에 있었다. 그 소란과 먼지 속에서, 나는 잭 맬던 씨가 어지러운 얼굴로, 손에 앵두빛 무언가를 쥐고 덜컹덜컹 지나가는 것을 본 듯한 생생한 인상을 받았다."]], [["After another broadside for the Doctor, and another for the Doctor’s wife, the boys dispersed, and I went back into the house, where I found the guests all standing in a group about the Doctor, discussing how Mr. Jack Maldon had gone away, and how he had borne it, and how he had felt it, and all the rest of it.", "박사님을 위한 만세 일제사격이 한 번 더, 그리고 박사님 부인을 위한 만세가 또 한 번 터진 뒤, 아이들은 흩어졌고, 나는 집 안으로 돌아갔다. 들어가 보니 손님들이 모두 박사님 둘레에 무리 지어 서서, 잭 맬던 씨가 어떻게 떠났는지, 그가 그것을 어떻게 견뎠는지, 어떻게 느꼈는지, 그 밖의 이런저런 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["In the midst of these remarks, Mrs. Markleham cried: ‘Where’s Annie?’", "이런 이야기가 한창일 때, 마클럼 부인이 외쳤다. \"애니는 어디 있지?\""]], [["No Annie was there; and when they called to her, no Annie replied.", "애니는 거기 없었다. 그리고 사람들이 부인을 불러도, 애니는 대답이 없었다."], ["But all pressing out of the room, in a crowd, to see what was the matter, we found her lying on the hall floor.", "하지만 무슨 일인가 싶어 다들 무리 지어 방에서 우르르 몰려나가 보니, 부인이 현관 마룻바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["There was great alarm at first, until it was found that she was in a swoon, and that the swoon was yielding to the usual means of recovery; when the Doctor, who had lifted her head upon his knee, put her curls aside with his hand, and said, looking around:", "처음엔 다들 크게 놀랐지만, 부인이 까무러친 것이고, 그 까무러침이 흔히 쓰는 방법으로 곧 풀리고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그러자 박사님이 부인의 머리를 자기 무릎 위로 받쳐 올리고, 손으로 부인의 곱슬머리를 쓸어 넘기며, 둘레를 둘러보고 말했다."]], [["‘Poor Annie!", "\"가엾은 애니!"], ["She’s so faithful and tender-hearted!", "워낙 정이 깊고 마음이 여리니!"], ["It’s the parting from her old playfellow and friend--her favourite cousin--that has done this. Ah!", "옛 소꿉동무이자 벗이었던 — 가장 아끼던 사촌과 — 헤어진 게 이렇게 만든 거야. 아! 안됐구나!"], ["It’s a pity! I am very sorry!’", "내가 다 가슴이 아파!\""]], [["When she opened her eyes, and saw where she was, and that we were all standing about her, she arose with assistance: turning her head, as she did so, to lay it on the Doctor’s shoulder--or to hide it, I don’t know which.", "부인이 눈을 떠서, 자기가 어디 있는지, 또 우리가 모두 둘레에 서 있다는 것을 알아챘을 때, 부축을 받아 일어났다. 일어나면서, 부인은 고개를 돌려 박사님의 어깨에 기댔다 — 아니면 얼굴을 묻으려는 것이었는지, 어느 쪽인지는 모르겠다."], ["We went into the drawing-room, to leave her with the Doctor and her mother; but she said, it seemed, that she was better than she had been since morning, and that she would rather be brought among us; so they brought her in, looking very white and weak, I thought, and sat her on a sofa.", "우리는 부인을 박사님과 그 어머니에게 맡겨 두려고 응접실로 들어갔다. 그런데 부인은, 들리는 말로는, 아침 이래로 어느 때보다 몸이 나아졌으니, 차라리 우리 사이에 끼고 싶다고 했단다. 그래서 다들 부인을 데리고 들어왔는데, 내가 보기에 무척 핼쑥하고 기운 없는 모습이었고, 부인을 소파에 앉혔다."]], [["‘Annie, my dear,’ said her mother, doing something to her dress.", "\"애니, 우리 딸.\" 어머니가 부인의 옷매무새를 손보며 말했다."], ["‘See here!", "\"이것 보렴!"], ["You have lost a bow. Will anybody be so good as find a ribbon; a cherry-coloured ribbon?’", "너 리본 하나를 잃어버렸구나. 누가 좀 찾아 주시겠어요, 리본을, 앵두빛 리본을요?\""]], [["It was the one she had worn at her bosom.", "그것은 부인이 가슴께에 달고 있던 것이었다."], ["We all looked for it; I myself looked everywhere, I am certain--but nobody could find it.", "우리는 모두 그것을 찾았다. 나 자신도 분명히 사방을 다 뒤졌다 — 하지만 아무도 찾지 못했다."]], [["‘Do you recollect where you had it last, Annie?’ said her mother.", "\"애니, 마지막으로 그걸 어디서 봤는지 기억나니?\" 어머니가 말했다."]], [["I wondered how I could have thought she looked white, or anything but burning red, when she answered that she had had it safe, a little while ago, she thought, but it was not worth looking for.", "부인이, 조금 전까지만 해도 분명히 잘 달고 있었던 것 같은데, 굳이 찾을 것까지는 없다고 대답했을 때, 나는 내가 어떻게 부인이 핼쑥하다고, 아니 활활 타듯 붉은 것 말고 다른 무엇이라고 생각할 수 있었던 건지 의아했다."]], [["Nevertheless, it was looked for again, and still not found.", "그런데도 그것을 다시 찾았지만, 여전히 못 찾았다."], ["She entreated that there might be no more searching; but it was still sought for, in a desultory way, until she was quite well, and the company took their departure.", "부인은 제발 더는 찾지 말아 달라고 간청했다. 하지만 부인이 완전히 회복되고 손님들이 떠날 때까지, 그것은 띄엄띄엄 계속 찾아졌다."]], [["We walked very slowly home, Mr. Wickfield, Agnes, and I--Agnes and I admiring the moonlight, and Mr.", "우리는 무척 천천히 집으로 걸어갔다, 위크필드 씨와 애그니스와 나는."], ["Wickfield scarcely raising his eyes from the ground. When we, at last, reached our own door, Agnes discovered that she had left her little reticule behind.", "애그니스와 나는 달빛을 감탄하며 보았고, 위크필드 씨는 땅바닥에서 좀처럼 눈을 떼지 않았다. 마침내 우리 집 문 앞에 다다랐을 때, 애그니스는 자기가 작은 손가방을 두고 왔다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Delighted to be of any service to her, I ran back to fetch it.", "애그니스에게 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있어 기뻤던 나는, 그것을 가지러 달려 돌아갔다."]], [["I went into the supper-room where it had been left, which was deserted and dark.", "나는 그것을 두고 온 저녁 식사 방으로 들어갔는데, 사람 하나 없이 캄캄했다."], ["But a door of communication between that and the Doctor’s study, where there was a light, being open, I passed on there, to say what I wanted, and to get a candle.", "하지만 그 방과 불이 켜진 박사님 서재 사이의 연결문이 열려 있어서, 나는 내가 온 까닭을 말하고 촛불을 얻으려고 그쪽으로 갔다."]], [["The Doctor was sitting in his easy-chair by the fireside, and his young wife was on a stool at his feet.", "박사님은 난롯가의 안락의자에 앉아 있었고, 그 젊은 아내는 박사님 발치의 걸상에 앉아 있었다."], ["The Doctor, with a complacent smile, was reading aloud some manuscript explanation or statement of a theory out of that interminable Dictionary, and she was looking up at him.", "박사님은 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며, 그 끝도 없는 사전에서 어떤 이론에 대한 필사된 설명인지 진술인지를 소리 내어 읽고 있었고, 부인은 박사님을 올려다보고 있었다."], ["But with such a face as I never saw.", "하지만 내가 일찍이 본 적 없는 그런 얼굴로."], ["It was so beautiful in its form, it was so ashy pale, it was so fixed in its abstraction, it was so full of a wild, sleep-walking, dreamy horror of I don’t know what.", "그 얼굴은 생김새가 너무도 아름다웠고, 잿빛으로 너무도 핼쑥했고, 너무도 넋이 빠진 채 굳어 있었고, 무언지 모를 것에 대한, 사납고, 몽유병자 같고, 꿈결 같은 공포로 너무도 가득했다."], ["The eyes were wide open, and her brown hair fell in two rich clusters on her shoulders, and on her white dress, disordered by the want of the lost ribbon.", "두 눈은 활짝 열려 있었고, 갈색 머리카락은 잃어버린 리본이 없어 흐트러진 채로, 두 갈래 풍성한 다발이 되어 어깨와 흰 드레스 위로 흘러내려 있었다."], ["Distinctly as I recollect her look, I cannot say of what it was expressive, I cannot even say of what it is expressive to me now, rising again before my older judgement.", "그 표정을 또렷이 기억하면서도, 나는 그것이 무엇을 나타냈는지 말할 수 없다. 이제 와, 더 나이 든 내 판단 앞에 다시 떠오르는 그것이 나에게 무엇을 나타내는지조차 말할 수 없다."], ["Penitence, humiliation, shame, pride, love, and trustfulness--I see them all; and in them all, I see that horror of I don’t know what.", "뉘우침, 굴욕, 수치, 자존심, 사랑, 그리고 믿음 — 나는 그것을 다 본다. 그리고 그 모든 것 속에서, 무언지 모를 것에 대한 그 공포를 본다."]], [["My entrance, and my saying what I wanted, roused her.", "내가 들어가서 내가 온 까닭을 말하자, 부인이 정신을 차렸다."], ["It disturbed the Doctor too, for when I went back to replace the candle I had taken from the table, he was patting her head, in his fatherly way, and saying he was a merciless drone to let her tempt him into reading on; and he would have her go to bed.", "그것은 박사님도 놀라게 했는데, 내가 식탁에서 가져갔던 촛불을 도로 갖다 놓으려고 돌아갔을 때, 박사님은 그 아버지 같은 태도로 부인의 머리를 쓰다듬으며, 자기가 인정머리 없는 수벌이라 부인이 자기를 꾀어 계속 읽게 만들도록 내버려 두었다고, 이제 부인을 자러 보내야겠다고 말하고 있었다."]], [["But she asked him, in a rapid, urgent manner, to let her stay--to let her feel assured (I heard her murmur some broken words to this effect) that she was in his confidence that night.", "하지만 부인은 빠르고 다급한 투로, 자기를 머무르게 해 달라고 — 그날 밤 자기가 박사님의 믿음을 받고 있다는 것을 확신할 수 있게 해 달라고 (부인이 이런 뜻으로 띄엄띄엄 중얼거리는 것을 나는 들었다) 박사님에게 청했다."], ["And, as she turned again towards him, after glancing at me as I left the room and went out at the door, I saw her cross her hands upon his knee, and look up at him with the same face, something quieted, as he resumed his reading.", "그리고, 내가 방을 나가 문밖으로 나설 때 나를 흘끗 본 다음, 부인이 다시 박사님 쪽으로 돌아설 때, 나는 부인이 두 손을 박사님 무릎 위에 포개고, 좀 가라앉기는 했으나 아까와 똑같은 그 얼굴로, 박사님이 다시 읽기를 이어 갈 때 그를 올려다보는 것을 보았다."]], [["It made a great impression on me, and I remembered it a long time afterwards; as I shall have occasion to narrate when the time comes.", "그것은 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼고, 나는 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤에도 그것을 기억했다. 때가 되면 이야기할 기회가 있을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 17. SOMEBODY TURNS UP", "# CHAPTER 17. SOMEBODY TURNS UP"]], [["It has not occurred to me to mention Peggotty since I ran away; but, of course, I wrote her a letter almost as soon as I was housed at Dover, and another, and a longer letter, containing all particulars fully related, when my aunt took me formally under her protection.", "내가 도망친 뒤로 패거티 이야기를 꺼낼 겨를이 없었다. 하지만 물론, 도버에 자리를 잡자마자 나는 그녀에게 편지를 한 통 썼고, 고모님이 나를 정식으로 보호하에 두었을 때 또 한 통, 모든 자초지종을 빠짐없이 적은 더 긴 편지를 보냈다."], ["On my being settled at Doctor Strong’s I wrote to her again, detailing my happy condition and prospects.", "스트롱 박사 학교에 자리를 잡고서는 다시 편지를 써서 내 행복한 처지와 앞날을 상세히 알렸다."], ["I never could have derived anything like the pleasure from spending the money Mr. Dick had given me, that I felt in sending a gold half-guinea to Peggotty, per post, enclosed in this last letter, to discharge the sum I had borrowed of her: in which epistle, not before, I mentioned about the young man with the donkey-cart.", "딕 씨가 준 돈을 쓰는 데서는, 이 마지막 편지에 동봉해 그녀에게 빌렸던 돈을 갚으려고 우편으로 반 기니짜리 금화를 부치면서 느낀 기쁨에 견줄 만한 즐거움을 결코 끌어낼 수 없었으리라. 바로 그 편지에서, 그 전이 아니라, 나는 당나귀 수레를 끌던 젊은이 이야기를 적었다."]], [["To these communications Peggotty replied as promptly, if not as concisely, as a merchant’s clerk.", "이런 소식에 패거티는, 간결하지는 않았으되, 상인의 점원만큼이나 즉각 답장을 보냈다."], ["Her utmost powers of expression (which were certainly not great in ink) were exhausted in the attempt to write what she felt on the subject of my journey.", "내 여정에 대해 자기가 느낀 바를 적어 보려는 시도에 그녀의 온갖 표현력이 (잉크로 적는 솜씨가 대단치 않았던 것은 분명하지만) 죄다 소진되었다."], ["Four sides of incoherent and interjectional beginnings of sentences, that had no end, except blots, were inadequate to afford her any relief.", "끝도 없이 — 잉크 얼룩 말고는 — 두서없고 감탄사뿐인 문장들의 첫머리가 종이 넉 면을 가득 채웠으나, 그녀에게 아무런 위안이 되어 주지 못했다."], ["But the blots were more expressive to me than the best composition; for they showed me that Peggotty had been crying all over the paper, and what could I have desired more?", "그러나 그 얼룩들이 나에게는 어떤 명문보다도 더 많은 것을 말해 주었다. 패거티가 종이가 다 젖도록 펑펑 울었다는 것을 보여 주었으니, 그보다 더 무엇을 바랐겠는가?"]], [["I made out, without much difficulty, that she could not take quite kindly to my aunt yet.", "나는 그녀가 아직 고모님을 썩 살갑게 받아들이지 못한다는 것을 별 어려움 없이 알아챘다."], ["The notice was too short after so long a prepossession the other way.", "그토록 오랫동안 정반대로 마음을 굳혀 온 터라, 새 사정을 받아들이기에는 통보가 너무 갑작스러웠던 것이다."], ["We never knew a person, she wrote; but to think that Miss Betsey should seem to be so different from what she had been thought to be, was a Moral!--that was her word.", "그녀는 사람 속은 도무지 알 수 없다고 썼다. 베시 양이라는 분이 사람들이 여태 알고 있던 모습과 그토록 달라 보일 수 있다니, 그건 정말 교훈이라고! — 그게 그녀가 쓴 말이었다."], ["She was evidently still afraid of Miss Betsey, for she sent her grateful duty to her but timidly; and she was evidently afraid of me, too, and entertained the probability of my running away again soon: if I might judge from the repeated hints she threw out, that the coach-fare to Yarmouth was always to be had of her for the asking.", "그녀는 분명 아직도 베시 양을 두려워하고 있었으니, 베시 양께 감사 인사를 전하면서도 잔뜩 움츠러든 채였다. 그리고 나 또한 두려워하는 게 분명했으니, 내가 머지않아 또 도망칠 가능성을 마음에 품고 있었던 것이다. 야머스까지 가는 마차 삯은 언제든 말만 하면 그녀에게서 받을 수 있다고 거듭거듭 흘린 암시로 미루어 보면 말이다."]], [["She gave me one piece of intelligence which affected me very much, namely, that there had been a sale of the furniture at our old home, and that Mr. and Miss Murdstone were gone away, and the house was shut up, to be let or sold.", "그녀는 나를 몹시 마음 아프게 한 소식을 하나 전해 주었다. 다름 아니라, 우리 옛집의 가구가 경매로 팔렸고, 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양은 떠나 버렸으며, 집은 세를 놓거나 팔려고 문을 닫아걸었다는 것이었다."], ["God knows I had no part in it while they remained there, but it pained me to think of the dear old place as altogether abandoned; of the weeds growing tall in the garden, and the fallen leaves lying thick and wet upon the paths.", "하느님은 아실 테지만, 그들이 거기 살던 동안에는 나에게 그 집에 대한 아무런 몫도 없었다. 하지만 그 정든 옛집이 그렇게 완전히 버려졌다고 생각하니 마음이 아팠다. 정원에는 잡초가 키 높이 자라고, 오솔길에는 떨어진 잎들이 젖은 채 두껍게 쌓여 있을 것을 생각하니."], ["I imagined how the winds of winter would howl round it, how the cold rain would beat upon the window-glass, how the moon would make ghosts on the walls of the empty rooms, watching their solitude all night.", "나는 상상해 보았다. 겨울바람이 그 집을 휘돌며 울부짖고, 차가운 빗줄기가 창유리를 두드리고, 달빛이 텅 빈 방들의 벽에 유령을 그리며 밤새도록 그 적막을 지켜보는 모습을."], ["I thought afresh of the grave in the churchyard, underneath the tree: and it seemed as if the house were dead too, now, and all connected with my father and mother were faded away.", "나는 교회 묘지의, 나무 밑에 있는 그 무덤을 새삼 떠올렸다. 그러자 이제는 그 집마저 죽어 버린 것 같았고, 아버지와 어머니에 얽힌 모든 것이 다 빛바래 사라져 버린 듯했다."]], [["There was no other news in Peggotty’s letters. Mr.", "패거티의 편지에 다른 소식은 없었다."], ["Barkis was an excellent husband, she said, though still a little near; but we all had our faults, and she had plenty (though I am sure I don’t know what they were); and he sent his duty, and my little bedroom was always ready for me. Mr.", "바키스 씨는 더없이 훌륭한 남편이라고 그녀는 적었다. 비록 아직도 조금 인색하긴 하지만 말이다. 하지만 누구에게나 결점은 있는 법이고, 그녀에게도 결점이 많다고 했다(도대체 그게 뭔지 나는 도무지 모르겠지만)."], ["Peggotty was well, and Ham was well, and Mrs. Gummidge was but poorly, and little Em’ly wouldn’t send her love, but said that Peggotty might send it, if she liked.", "그리고 그가 안부를 전한다고 했고, 내 작은 침실은 언제나 나를 위해 마련되어 있다고 했다. 패거티 씨는 잘 지내고, 햄도 잘 지내고, 거미지 부인은 그저 그렇고, 어린 에밀리는 사랑을 전하지 않겠다지만 패거티가 원한다면 대신 전해도 좋다고 했단다."]], [["All this intelligence I dutifully imparted to my aunt, only reserving to myself the mention of little Em’ly, to whom I instinctively felt that she would not very tenderly incline.", "이 모든 소식을 나는 고모님께 빠짐없이 전해 드렸다. 다만 어린 에밀리 이야기만은 나 혼자 간직했으니, 본능적으로 고모님이 그 아이에게 그리 다정하게 마음을 기울이지 않으리라는 느낌이 들었던 것이다."], ["While I was yet new at Doctor Strong’s, she made several excursions over to Canterbury to see me, and always at unseasonable hours: with the view, I suppose, of taking me by surprise.", "내가 아직 스트롱 박사 학교에 갓 들어갔을 무렵, 고모님은 나를 보러 캔터베리까지 몇 차례 행차하셨는데, 늘 엉뚱한 시간에 나타나셨다. 짐작건대 나를 불시에 덮칠 셈이었던 것이다."], ["But, finding me well employed, and bearing a good character, and hearing on all hands that I rose fast in the school, she soon discontinued these visits.", "그러나 내가 공부에 충실하고 평판도 좋으며, 사방에서 학교에서 빠르게 두각을 나타낸다는 소리를 듣고서, 고모님은 곧 이 방문을 그만두셨다."], ["I saw her on a Saturday, every third or fourth week, when I went over to Dover for a treat; and I saw Mr. Dick every alternate Wednesday, when he arrived by stage-coach at noon, to stay until next morning.", "나는 서너 주에 한 번씩 토요일이면 도버로 건너가 대접을 받으며 고모님을 뵈었고, 격주 수요일마다 딕 씨를 뵈었는데, 그는 정오에 역마차로 도착해 이튿날 아침까지 머물렀다."]], [["On these occasions Mr. Dick never travelled without a leathern writing-desk, containing a supply of stationery and the Memorial; in relation to which document he had a notion that time was beginning to press now, and that it really must be got out of hand.", "이런 때면 딕 씨는 가죽 필기 책상을 빼놓고는 결코 다니지 않았는데, 그 안에는 문구류와 진정서가 한가득 들어 있었다. 그 문서에 관해서는, 이제는 시간이 촉박해지기 시작했으니 정말이지 서둘러 마무리 지어야 한다는 생각을 품고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Dick was very partial to gingerbread.", "딕 씨는 생강빵을 무척 좋아했다."], ["To render his visits the more agreeable, my aunt had instructed me to open a credit for him at a cake shop, which was hampered with the stipulation that he should not be served with more than one shilling’s-worth in the course of any one day.", "그의 방문을 한결 더 즐겁게 해 주려고, 고모님은 나에게 어느 과자 가게에 그가 외상으로 살 수 있도록 미리 일러 두라고 시키셨는데, 다만 하루에 일 실링어치를 넘게는 사 주지 못한다는 단서가 붙어 있었다."], ["This, and the reference of all his little bills at the county inn where he slept, to my aunt, before they were paid, induced me to suspect that he was only allowed to rattle his money, and not to spend it.", "이 일과 함께, 그가 묵는 마을 여관의 자잘한 청구서를 죄다 갚기 전에 고모님께 확인받게 한 것을 보고, 나는 그가 그저 돈을 짤랑거리며 가지고 다니도록만 허락받았을 뿐 쓰지는 못하는 게 아닐까 하고 의심하게 되었다."], ["I found on further investigation that this was so, or at least there was an agreement between him and my aunt that he should account to her for all his disbursements.", "더 알아보니 과연 그러했다. 아니, 적어도 그와 고모님 사이에는 그가 쓴 모든 비용을 고모님께 정산해 보고하기로 한 합의가 있었다."], ["As he had no idea of deceiving her, and always desired to please her, he was thus made chary of launching into expense.", "그는 고모님을 속일 마음이 조금도 없었고 늘 고모님을 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶어 했으니, 그렇게 해서 함부로 돈을 쓰지 않도록 조심하게 된 것이다."], ["On this point, as well as on all other possible points, Mr. Dick was convinced that my aunt was the wisest and most wonderful of women; as he repeatedly told me with infinite secrecy, and always in a whisper.", "이 점에 관해서도, 또 생각할 수 있는 다른 모든 점에 관해서도, 딕 씨는 고모님이야말로 세상에서 가장 지혜롭고 가장 놀라운 여인이라고 확신하고 있었다. 그가 나에게 끝없이 은밀하게, 그것도 늘 속삭이는 목소리로 거듭 일러 주었듯이 말이다."]], [["‘Trotwood,’ said Mr.", "\"트롯우드,\""], ["Dick, with an air of mystery, after imparting this confidence to me, one Wednesday; ‘who’s the man that hides near our house and frightens her?’", "어느 수요일, 이 비밀을 나에게 털어놓고 나서 딕 씨가 수수께끼 같은 표정으로 말했다. \"우리 집 근처에 숨어서 고모님을 겁주는 그 남자는 누구냐?\""]], [["‘Frightens my aunt, sir?’", "\"고모님을 겁준다고요, 선생님?\""]], [["Mr.", "딕 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["Dick nodded.", "\"난 그 무엇도 고모님을 겁줄 순 없을 거라고 생각했단다.\" 그가 말했다. \"왜냐면 고모님은 — \""], ["‘I thought nothing would have frightened her,’ he said, ‘for she’s--’ here he whispered softly, ‘don’t mention it--the wisest and most wonderful of women.’ Having said which, he drew back, to observe the effect which this description of her made upon me.", "여기서 그는 나직이 속삭였다. \"이건 말하지 마라 — 세상에서 가장 지혜롭고 가장 놀라운 여인이니까.\" 이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 이 묘사가 나에게 어떤 효과를 일으키는지 보려고 몸을 뒤로 물렸다."]], [["‘The first time he came,’ said Mr.", "\"그자가 처음 온 게,\""], ["Dick, ‘was--let me see--sixteen hundred and forty-nine was the date of King Charles’s execution.", "딕 씨가 말했다. \"그게 — 가만있자 — 천육백사십구 년이 찰스 왕이 처형당한 해였지."], ["I think you said sixteen hundred and forty-nine?’", "네가 천육백사십구 년이라고 했던 것 같은데?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["‘I don’t know how it can be,’ said Mr.", "\"어떻게 그럴 수 있는지 모르겠구나.\""], ["Dick, sorely puzzled and shaking his head.", "딕 씨가 몹시 어리둥절해하며 고개를 절레절레 저으며 말했다."], ["‘I don’t think I am as old as that.’", "\"내가 그렇게까지 나이를 먹은 것 같진 않은데.\""]], [["‘Was it in that year that the man appeared, sir?’ I asked.", "\"그 남자가 나타난 게 그해였나요, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, really’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄, 정말이지,\""], ["Dick, ‘I don’t see how it can have been in that year, Trotwood.", "딕 씨가 말했다. \"그게 그해였을 리가 없을 것 같구나, 트롯우드."], ["Did you get that date out of history?’", "너 그 날짜를 역사책에서 알아냈니?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["‘I suppose history never lies, does it?’ said Mr. Dick, with a gleam of hope.", "\"역사책은 결코 거짓말을 하지 않겠지, 그렇지?\" 딕 씨가 한 줄기 희망을 반짝이며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh dear, no, sir!’ I replied, most decisively.", "\"아이고, 그럼요, 선생님!\" 나는 더없이 단호하게 대답했다."], ["I was ingenuous and young, and I thought so.", "나는 순진하고 어렸으니, 정말 그렇게 믿었다."]], [["‘I can’t make it out,’ said Mr. Dick, shaking his head.", "\"도무지 모르겠구나.\" 딕 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘There’s something wrong, somewhere.", "\"어딘가 뭔가가 잘못됐어."], ["However, it was very soon after the mistake was made of putting some of the trouble out of King Charles’s head into my head, that the man first came.", "어쨌든, 찰스 왕 머릿속에 있던 골칫거리를 얼마쯤 내 머릿속에 옮겨 넣는 실수가 저질러지고 나서 얼마 지나지 않아 그 남자가 처음 왔단다."], ["I was walking out with Miss Trotwood after tea, just at dark, and there he was, close to our house.’", "차를 마시고 나서 막 어두워질 무렵에 트롯우드 양과 함께 산책을 나갔는데, 거기 그자가 있었어, 우리 집 바로 옆에.\""]], [["‘Walking about?’ I inquired.", "\"서성거리고 있던가요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Walking about?’ repeated Mr. Dick.", "\"서성거리고 있었느냐고?\" 딕 씨가 되뇌었다."], ["‘Let me see, I must recollect a bit.", "\"가만있자, 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠구나."], ["N-no, no; he was not walking about.’", "아 — 아니, 아니야. 서성거리고 있던 건 아니었어.\""]], [["I asked, as the shortest way to get at it, what he WAS doing.", "나는 그 답에 이르는 가장 빠른 길로, 그렇다면 그자가 무엇을 하고 있었느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Well, he wasn’t there at all,’ said Mr.", "\"음, 그자는 거기 전혀 없었어.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["Dick, ‘until he came up behind her, and whispered.", "\"고모님 뒤로 다가와 속삭이기 전까지는 말이다."], ["Then she turned round and fainted, and I stood still and looked at him, and he walked away; but that he should have been hiding ever since (in the ground or somewhere), is the most extraordinary thing!’", "그러자 고모님이 돌아서더니 정신을 잃었고, 나는 가만히 서서 그자를 바라보았는데, 그자는 걸어가 버렸어. 그런데 그 뒤로 줄곧 (땅속이든 어디든) 숨어 있었다는 게 가장 기이한 일이야!\""]], [["‘HAS he been hiding ever since?’ I asked.", "\"그 뒤로 줄곧 숨어 있었다고요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘To be sure he has,’ retorted Mr. Dick, nodding his head gravely.", "\"그럼, 당연하지.\" 딕 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 고개를 끄덕이며 받았다."], ["‘Never came out, till last night!", "\"어젯밤까지 한 번도 안 나타났어!"], ["We were walking last night, and he came up behind her again, and I knew him again.’", "어젯밤에 우리가 산책을 하고 있는데, 그자가 또 고모님 뒤로 다가왔고, 나는 그자를 또 알아봤단다.\""]], [["‘And did he frighten my aunt again?’", "\"그래서 고모님을 또 겁주던가요?\""]], [["‘All of a shiver,’ said Mr.", "\"온통 부들부들 떠셨지.\""], ["Dick, counterfeiting that affection and making his teeth chatter.", "딕 씨가 그 모습을 흉내 내며 이를 딱딱 부딪쳐 보이며 말했다."], ["‘Held by the palings. Cried.", "\"울타리를 붙들고. 우셨어."], ["But, Trotwood, come here,’ getting me close to him, that he might whisper very softly; ‘why did she give him money, boy, in the moonlight?’", "그런데, 트롯우드, 이리 와 보렴.\" 그가 아주 나직이 속삭일 수 있도록 나를 바짝 끌어당겼다. \"왜 고모님이 그자에게 돈을 줬을까, 얘야, 그것도 달빛 아래서?\""]], [["‘He was a beggar, perhaps.’", "\"거지였나 보지요, 아마.\""]], [["Mr.", "딕 씨는 그 짐작을 완전히 일축하듯 고개를 저었다."], ["Dick shook his head, as utterly renouncing the suggestion; and having replied a great many times, and with great confidence, ‘No beggar, no beggar, no beggar, sir!’ went on to say, that from his window he had afterwards, and late at night, seen my aunt give this person money outside the garden rails in the moonlight, who then slunk away--into the ground again, as he thought probable--and was seen no more: while my aunt came hurriedly and secretly back into the house, and had, even that morning, been quite different from her usual self; which preyed on Mr.", "그러고는 아주 자신만만하게 여러 번 \"거지 아니야, 거지 아니야, 거지 아니야, 선생!\" 하고 대답하고 나서, 그 뒤에 밤늦게 자기 방 창문으로, 고모님이 달빛 아래 정원 울타리 밖에서 그자에게 돈을 주는 것을 보았다고 말을 이었다. 그자는 그러고 나서 슬그머니 사라져 — 그가 보기엔 아마도 또 땅속으로 — 두 번 다시 보이지 않았고, 한편 고모님은 황급히, 몰래 집으로 돌아왔으며, 바로 그날 아침까지도 평소의 모습과는 사뭇 달랐다는 것이었다."], ["Dick’s mind.", "그게 딕 씨의 마음을 짓누르고 있었다."]], [["I had not the least belief, in the outset of this story, that the unknown was anything but a delusion of Mr.", "이 이야기를 처음 들었을 때 나는 그 정체 모를 자가 딕 씨의 망상에 지나지 않으며, 그에게 그토록 많은 골칫거리를 안겨 준 그 불운한 왕의 일족 가운데 하나일 뿐이라고 조금도 의심치 않았다."], ["Dick’s, and one of the line of that ill-fated Prince who occasioned him so much difficulty; but after some reflection I began to entertain the question whether an attempt, or threat of an attempt, might have been twice made to take poor Mr. Dick himself from under my aunt’s protection, and whether my aunt, the strength of whose kind feeling towards him I knew from herself, might have been induced to pay a price for his peace and quiet.", "그러나 좀 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 가엾은 딕 씨 본인을 고모님의 보호 아래서 떼어 내려는 시도, 혹은 시도하겠다는 위협이 두 번쯤 있었던 것은 아닐까, 그리고 그를 향한 다정한 정이 얼마나 깊은지 고모님 본인에게서 직접 들어 알고 있던 터라, 고모님이 그의 평온과 안녕을 위해 값을 치르도록 떠밀린 것은 아닐까 하는 의문을 품기 시작했다. 나는 이미 딕 씨에게 무척 정이 들었고 그의 안녕을 몹시 염려하던 터라, 내 두려움이 이 짐작에 힘을 실어 주었다."], ["As I was already much attached to Mr. Dick, and very solicitous for his welfare, my fears favoured this supposition; and for a long time his Wednesday hardly ever came round, without my entertaining a misgiving that he would not be on the coach-box as usual.", "그래서 오랫동안 수요일이 돌아올 때마다 그가 여느 때처럼 마차 마부석에 앉아 있지 않으면 어쩌나 하는 불안을 거의 매번 품지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그는 언제나 거기 나타났으니, 희끗희끗한 머리에 웃음을 띠고 행복한 얼굴로 말이다."], ["There he always appeared, however, grey-headed, laughing, and happy; and he never had anything more to tell of the man who could frighten my aunt.", "그리고 고모님을 겁줄 수 있는 그 남자에 대해서는 더 이상 들려줄 이야기가 없었다."]], [["These Wednesdays were the happiest days of Mr.", "이 수요일들은 딕 씨의 인생에서 가장 행복한 날들이었다."], ["Dick’s life; they were far from being the least happy of mine.", "나에게도 결코 가장 덜 행복한 날들이 아니었다."], ["He soon became known to every boy in the school; and though he never took an active part in any game but kite-flying, was as deeply interested in all our sports as anyone among us.", "그는 곧 학교의 모든 아이에게 알려졌다. 그리고 비록 연날리기 말고는 어떤 놀이에도 직접 끼어들지 않았으나, 우리 가운데 누구 못지않게 우리 놀이 전부에 깊이 빠져들었다."], ["How often have I seen him, intent upon a match at marbles or pegtop, looking on with a face of unutterable interest, and hardly breathing at the critical times!", "구슬치기나 팽이 시합에 정신을 쏟은 채, 형언할 수 없는 흥미가 가득한 얼굴로 지켜보며, 결정적인 순간이면 숨조차 제대로 못 쉬던 그의 모습을 나는 얼마나 자주 보았던가!"], ["How often, at hare and hounds, have I seen him mounted on a little knoll, cheering the whole field on to action, and waving his hat above his grey head, oblivious of King Charles the Martyr’s head, and all belonging to it!", "토끼와 사냥개 놀이를 할 때면 작은 둔덕 위에 올라서서 운동장 전체를 향해 어서 뛰라고 응원하며, 찰스 순교왕의 머리며 그에 얽힌 모든 것을 까맣게 잊은 채, 희끗희끗한 머리 위로 모자를 휘젓던 그의 모습을 또 얼마나 자주 보았던가!"], ["How many a summer hour have I known to be but blissful minutes to him in the cricket-field!", "크리켓 경기장에서 보낸 여름날의 숱한 시간이 그에게는 그저 더없이 행복한 몇 분에 지나지 않았음을 나는 알고 있다!"], ["How many winter days have I seen him, standing blue-nosed, in the snow and east wind, looking at the boys going down the long slide, and clapping his worsted gloves in rapture!", "눈과 동풍 속에 코끝이 파래진 채 서서, 긴 빙판 미끄럼을 타고 내려가는 아이들을 바라보며, 털장갑 낀 두 손을 황홀하게 마주치던 그의 모습을 나는 또 얼마나 많은 겨울날에 보았던가!"]], [["He was an universal favourite, and his ingenuity in little things was transcendent.", "그는 모두의 사랑을 한 몸에 받았고, 자잘한 일에서 그의 손재주는 따를 자가 없었다."], ["He could cut oranges into such devices as none of us had an idea of.", "그는 우리로서는 짐작도 못 할 갖가지 모양으로 오렌지를 깎아 낼 줄 알았다."], ["He could make a boat out of anything, from a skewer upwards.", "꼬치 하나에서부터 시작해 무엇으로든 배를 만들 줄 알았다."], ["He could turn cramp-bones into chessmen; fashion Roman chariots from old court cards; make spoked wheels out of cotton reels, and bird-cages of old wire.", "동물 발목뼈를 체스 말로 바꾸고, 낡은 카드짝으로 로마식 전차를 빚어내고, 실패로 바큇살 달린 바퀴를 만들고, 낡은 철사로 새장을 만들 줄 알았다."], ["But he was greatest of all, perhaps, in the articles of string and straw; with which we were all persuaded he could do anything that could be done by hands.", "하지만 아마도 그가 가장 뛰어난 분야는 노끈과 지푸라기였으니, 손으로 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 그가 그것으로 해낼 수 있다고 우리 모두 믿어 의심치 않았다."]], [["Mr. Dick’s renown was not long confined to us.", "딕 씨의 명성은 오래지 않아 우리에게만 머무르지 않았다."], ["After a few Wednesdays, Doctor Strong himself made some inquiries of me about him, and I told him all my aunt had told me; which interested the Doctor so much that he requested, on the occasion of his next visit, to be presented to him.", "몇 번의 수요일이 지난 뒤, 스트롱 박사가 직접 나에게 그에 관해 이것저것 물어왔다. 나는 고모님이 들려준 이야기를 전부 전해 드렸고, 박사는 그 이야기에 어찌나 흥미를 느꼈던지 다음번 방문 때 그를 소개해 달라고 청했다."], ["This ceremony I performed; and the Doctor begging Mr.", "나는 이 의식을 치러 주었다."], ["Dick, whensoever he should not find me at the coach office, to come on there, and rest himself until our morning’s work was over, it soon passed into a custom for Mr. Dick to come on as a matter of course, and, if we were a little late, as often happened on a Wednesday, to walk about the courtyard, waiting for me.", "그러자 박사는 딕 씨에게, 마차 사무소에서 나를 만나지 못하거든 언제든 그리로 와서 우리 오전 수업이 끝날 때까지 쉬고 있으라고 권했고, 그리하여 딕 씨가 으레 그리로 오는 것이 곧 관습이 되었다. 그리고 수요일마다 흔히 그랬듯이 우리가 조금 늦으면, 그는 안뜰을 거닐며 나를 기다렸다."], ["Here he made the acquaintance of the Doctor’s beautiful young wife (paler than formerly, all this time; more rarely seen by me or anyone, I think; and not so gay, but not less beautiful), and so became more and more familiar by degrees, until, at last, he would come into the school and wait.", "여기서 그는 박사의 아름답고 젊은 아내와 알게 되었는데(이 무렵 그녀는 예전보다 더 창백했고, 내 보기에 나든 누구든 그녀를 보는 일이 더 드물어졌으며, 예전만큼 명랑하지도 않았으나 그렇다고 덜 아름다운 것도 아니었다), 그렇게 차츰차츰 더 가까워지더니, 마침내 학교 안까지 들어와 기다리게 되었다."], ["He always sat in a particular corner, on a particular stool, which was called ‘Dick’, after him; here he would sit, with his grey head bent forward, attentively listening to whatever might be going on, with a profound veneration for the learning he had never been able to acquire.", "그는 늘 어느 특정한 구석의, 어느 특정한 걸상에 앉았는데, 그 걸상은 그의 이름을 따 '딕'이라고 불렸다. 여기 앉아 그는 희끗희끗한 머리를 앞으로 숙인 채, 무슨 일이 벌어지든 귀 기울여 들으며, 끝내 익히지 못한 학문에 깊은 경외를 품곤 했다."]], [["This veneration Mr. Dick extended to the Doctor, whom he thought the most subtle and accomplished philosopher of any age.", "이 경외를 딕 씨는 박사에게까지 넓혔으니, 그는 박사를 어느 시대를 통틀어 가장 명민하고 가장 뛰어난 철학자라고 여겼다."], ["It was long before Mr. Dick ever spoke to him otherwise than bareheaded; and even when he and the Doctor had struck up quite a friendship, and would walk together by the hour, on that side of the courtyard which was known among us as The Doctor’s Walk, Mr.", "한참 동안 딕 씨는 모자를 벗지 않고서는 결코 박사에게 말을 건네지 못했다. 심지어 그와 박사가 제법 친한 사이가 되어, 우리 사이에서 '박사의 산책로'라고 불리던 안뜰 한쪽을 몇 시간이고 함께 거닐 때조차, 딕 씨는 지혜와 학식에 대한 존경을 드러내려고 이따금 모자를 벗어 들곤 했다."], ["Dick would pull off his hat at intervals to show his respect for wisdom and knowledge. How it ever came about that the Doctor began to read out scraps of the famous Dictionary, in these walks, I never knew; perhaps he felt it all the same, at first, as reading to himself.", "이 산책 중에 박사가 그 유명한 사전의 토막들을 소리 내어 읽기 시작한 것이 어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 나는 끝내 알지 못했다. 어쩌면 박사는 처음엔 그것이 혼자 읽는 것과 다를 바 없다고 느꼈는지도 모른다."], ["However, it passed into a custom too; and Mr.", "어쨌거나 그것 역시 관습이 되었다."], ["Dick, listening with a face shining with pride and pleasure, in his heart of hearts believed the Dictionary to be the most delightful book in the world.", "그리고 딕 씨는 자랑스러움과 즐거움으로 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 귀 기울여 들으며, 마음속 깊은 곳에서 그 사전이야말로 세상에서 가장 즐거운 책이라고 믿었다."]], [["As I think of them going up and down before those schoolroom windows--the Doctor reading with his complacent smile, an occasional flourish of the manuscript, or grave motion of his head; and Mr. Dick listening, enchained by interest, with his poor wits calmly wandering God knows where, upon the wings of hard words--I think of it as one of the pleasantest things, in a quiet way, that I have ever seen.", "그 두 사람이 교실 창문 앞을 오르락내리락하는 모습을 떠올리면 — 박사는 흡족한 미소를 띤 채 이따금 원고를 한 번 휘저어 보이거나 자못 진중하게 고개를 끄덕이며 읽고, 딕 씨는 흥미에 사로잡혀 귀 기울이는데, 그 가엾은 정신은 어려운 낱말들의 날개를 타고 하느님만 아실 어딘가로 평온히 떠돌고 있으니 — 나는 그것이, 잔잔한 방식으로, 내가 여태껏 본 가장 기분 좋은 광경 가운데 하나라고 여긴다."], ["I feel as if they might go walking to and fro for ever, and the world might somehow be the better for it--as if a thousand things it makes a noise about, were not one half so good for it, or me.", "두 사람이 영원토록 그렇게 오락가락 거닐어도 좋을 것 같고, 그러면 어쩐지 세상이 조금은 더 나아질 것 같은 느낌이 든다 — 세상이 떠들썩하게 떠받드는 천 가지 일들이, 세상에든 나에게든 그 절반만큼도 좋지 않은 것 같은 느낌이."]], [["Agnes was one of Mr. Dick’s friends, very soon; and in often coming to the house, he made acquaintance with Uriah.", "아그네스도 머지않아 딕 씨의 벗 가운데 하나가 되었다. 그리고 그는 그 집에 자주 드나들면서 유라이어와도 알게 되었다."], ["The friendship between himself and me increased continually, and it was maintained on this odd footing: that, while Mr.", "그와 나 사이의 우정은 끊임없이 깊어졌는데, 그 우정은 이런 묘한 토대 위에 유지되었다."], ["Dick came professedly to look after me as my guardian, he always consulted me in any little matter of doubt that arose, and invariably guided himself by my advice; not only having a high respect for my native sagacity, but considering that I inherited a good deal from my aunt.", "즉, 딕 씨는 내 보호자로서 나를 보살피겠다고 표방하며 찾아왔으면서도, 무슨 자잘한 의심거리가 생기면 늘 나에게 의논했고, 한결같이 내 조언을 길잡이로 삼았던 것이다. 내 타고난 슬기를 높이 산 데다, 내가 고모님에게서 상당히 많은 것을 물려받았다고 여긴 까닭이었다."]], [["One Thursday morning, when I was about to walk with Mr.", "어느 목요일 아침, 학교로 돌아가기 전에 딕 씨와 함께 호텔에서 마차 사무소까지 걸어가려던 참이었는데(아침을 먹기 전에 한 시간 수업이 있었다), 거리에서 유라이어를 만났다."], ["Dick from the hotel to the coach office before going back to school (for we had an hour’s school before breakfast), I met Uriah in the street, who reminded me of the promise I had made to take tea with himself and his mother: adding, with a writhe, ‘But I didn’t expect you to keep it, Master Copperfield, we’re so very umble.’", "그는 내가 자기와 자기 어머니와 함께 차를 마시러 오겠다고 한 약속을 일깨우면서, 몸을 배배 꼬며 덧붙였다. \"하지만 코퍼필드 도련님이 그 약속을 지키실 거라곤 기대 안 했어요. 저희가 워낙 비천한 사람들이니까요.\""]], [["I really had not yet been able to make up my mind whether I liked Uriah or detested him; and I was very doubtful about it still, as I stood looking him in the face in the street.", "나는 사실 아직도 내가 유라이어를 좋아하는지 싫어하는지 마음을 정하지 못한 상태였다. 그래서 거리에서 그의 얼굴을 마주 보고 선 채로도 여전히 몹시 망설여졌다."], ["But I felt it quite an affront to be supposed proud, and said I only wanted to be asked.", "하지만 내가 거만한 사람으로 여겨지는 것이 무척 모욕스럽게 느껴졌고, 그래서 그저 청해 주기만을 바랐을 뿐이라고 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, if that’s all, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘and it really isn’t our umbleness that prevents you, will you come this evening?", "\"아, 그게 다라면, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"그리고 정말로 저희의 비천함 때문에 못 오시는 게 아니라면, 오늘 저녁에 와 주시겠어요?"], ["But if it is our umbleness, I hope you won’t mind owning to it, Master Copperfield; for we are well aware of our condition.’", "하지만 만약 저희의 비천함 때문이라면, 그걸 솔직히 인정해 주셔도 괜찮습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님. 저희는 저희 처지를 잘 알고 있으니까요.\""]], [["I said I would mention it to Mr. Wickfield, and if he approved, as I had no doubt he would, I would come with pleasure.", "나는 위크필드 씨에게 말해 보겠다고, 그리고 그분이 허락하신다면 — 틀림없이 허락하시겠지만 — 기꺼이 가겠다고 했다."], ["So, at six o’clock that evening, which was one of the early office evenings, I announced myself as ready, to Uriah.", "그래서 그날 저녁 여섯 시, 사무소가 일찍 문 닫는 날 가운데 하루였는데, 나는 유라이어에게 준비가 됐다고 알렸다."]], [["‘Mother will be proud, indeed,’ he said, as we walked away together.", "\"어머니가 정말 자랑스러워하실 겁니다.\" 우리가 함께 걸어가는데 그가 말했다."], ["‘Or she would be proud, if it wasn’t sinful, Master Copperfield.’", "\"아니, 자랑이 죄가 아니라면 자랑스러워하셨을 거예요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\""]], [["‘Yet you didn’t mind supposing I was proud this morning,’ I returned.", "\"그런데 오늘 아침엔 나를 거만하다고 짐작하면서도 아무렇지 않았잖아.\" 내가 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Oh dear, no, Master Copperfield!’ returned Uriah.", "\"아이고, 천만에요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어가 받았다."], ["‘Oh, believe me, no! Such a thought never came into my head!", "\"오, 믿어 주세요, 절대 아닙니다! 그런 생각은 제 머릿속에 떠오른 적도 없어요!"], ["I shouldn’t have deemed it at all proud if you had thought US too umble for you.", "도련님이 저희를 너무 비천해서 상대 못 할 사람들이라고 여기셨더라도, 저는 그걸 조금도 거만하다고 생각하지 않았을 거예요."], ["Because we are so very umble.’", "저희가 워낙 비천한 사람들이니까요.\""]], [["‘Have you been studying much law lately?’ I asked, to change the subject.", "\"요즘 법 공부 많이 했어?\" 화제를 돌리려고 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, Master Copperfield,’ he said, with an air of self-denial, ‘my reading is hardly to be called study.", "\"아, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 자신을 한껏 낮추는 투로 말했다. \"제 독서는 공부라고 부를 만한 것도 못 됩니다."], ["I have passed an hour or two in the evening, sometimes, with Mr. Tidd.’", "저녁에 가끔 티드 선생 책으로 한두 시간 보낼 뿐이지요.\""]], [["‘Rather hard, I suppose?’ said I.", "\"꽤 어렵겠지, 아마?\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘He is hard to me sometimes,’ returned Uriah.", "\"저한테는 가끔 어렵습니다.\" 유라이어가 받았다."], ["‘But I don’t know what he might be to a gifted person.’", "\"하지만 재능 있는 분에게는 그게 어떨지 저야 알 수 없지요.\""]], [["After beating a little tune on his chin as he walked on, with the two forefingers of his skeleton right hand, he added:", "걸어가면서 해골 같은 오른손의 두 집게손가락으로 턱에 가락을 잠깐 두드리더니, 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["‘There are expressions, you see, Master Copperfield--Latin words and terms--in Mr. Tidd, that are trying to a reader of my umble attainments.’", "\"보시다시피, 티드 선생 책에는 — 라틴어 낱말이며 용어들이 — 저처럼 비천한 학식을 가진 독자에게는 버거운 표현들이 있거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\""]], [["‘Would you like to be taught Latin?’ I said briskly.", "\"라틴어를 배우고 싶어?\" 내가 활기차게 말했다."], ["‘I will teach it you with pleasure, as I learn it.’", "\"내가 배우는 대로 기꺼이 가르쳐 줄게.\""]], [["‘Oh, thank you, Master Copperfield,’ he answered, shaking his head.", "\"오, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["‘I am sure it’s very kind of you to make the offer, but I am much too umble to accept it.’", "\"그렇게 제안해 주시다니 정말 친절하신 건 분명하지만, 저는 너무 비천해서 받아들일 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["‘What nonsense, Uriah!’", "\"무슨 말도 안 되는 소리야, 유라이어!\""]], [["‘Oh, indeed you must excuse me, Master Copperfield!", "\"오, 정말이지 양해해 주셔야 합니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!"], ["I am greatly obliged, and I should like it of all things, I assure you; but I am far too umble.", "정말 감사하고, 무엇보다도 그러고 싶지만, 정말입니다, 저는 너무나 비천하거든요."], ["There are people enough to tread upon me in my lowly state, without my doing outrage to their feelings by possessing learning.", "제 미천한 처지에 저를 짓밟을 사람들이 이미 차고 넘치는데, 제가 학식까지 갖춰서 그들의 심기를 거스를 것까지야 없지요."], ["Learning ain’t for me.", "학식은 제 몫이 아닙니다."], ["A person like myself had better not aspire.", "저 같은 사람은 분수에 넘치는 걸 바라지 않는 게 낫습니다."], ["If he is to get on in life, he must get on umbly, Master Copperfield!’", "세상에서 출세를 하려거든, 비천하게 출세해야지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["I never saw his mouth so wide, or the creases in his cheeks so deep, as when he delivered himself of these sentiments: shaking his head all the time, and writhing modestly.", "이런 속내를 토해 낼 때만큼 그의 입이 그토록 크게 벌어지고 두 뺨의 주름이 그토록 깊게 패는 것을 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 내내 고개를 절레절레 저으며, 겸손하게 몸을 배배 꼬면서 말이다."]], [["‘I think you are wrong, Uriah,’ I said.", "\"내 생각엔 네가 틀린 것 같아, 유라이어.\""], ["‘I dare say there are several things that I could teach you, if you would like to learn them.’", "내가 말했다. \"분명 내가 너에게 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 게 여러 가지 있을 텐데, 네가 배우고 싶다면 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh, I don’t doubt that, Master Copperfield,’ he answered; ‘not in the least.", "\"오, 그건 의심치 않습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"조금도 의심치 않아요."], ["But not being umble yourself, you don’t judge well, perhaps, for them that are.", "하지만 도련님은 비천하지 않으시니, 비천한 사람들 처지를 잘은 헤아리지 못하실지도 모르지요."], ["I won’t provoke my betters with knowledge, thank you.", "저는 학식으로 윗분들의 심기를 거스르지 않겠습니다, 고맙지만요."], ["I’m much too umble.", "저는 너무나 비천하거든요."], ["Here is my umble dwelling, Master Copperfield!’", "여기가 제 비천한 거처입니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["We entered a low, old-fashioned room, walked straight into from the street, and found there Mrs. Heep, who was the dead image of Uriah, only short.", "우리는 거리에서 곧장 들어가게 되어 있는, 천장 낮은 구식 방으로 들어섰고, 거기서 힙 부인을 만났는데, 그녀는 유라이어를 빼다 박은 모습이었으나 다만 키가 작았다."], ["She received me with the utmost humility, and apologized to me for giving her son a kiss, observing that, lowly as they were, they had their natural affections, which they hoped would give no offence to anyone.", "그녀는 더없이 비천한 태도로 나를 맞이하고, 아들에게 입을 맞춘 것을 나에게 사과하면서, 자기들이 아무리 미천해도 타고난 정은 있는 법이니 그것이 부디 누구에게도 거슬리지 않기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["It was a perfectly decent room, half parlour and half kitchen, but not at all a snug room.", "거기는 더없이 단정한 방으로, 절반은 응접실이고 절반은 부엌이었으나, 결코 아늑한 방은 아니었다."], ["The tea-things were set upon the table, and the kettle was boiling on the hob.", "차 도구가 식탁 위에 차려져 있고, 주전자는 화덕 받침 위에서 끓고 있었다."], ["There was a chest of drawers with an escritoire top, for Uriah to read or write at of an evening; there was Uriah’s blue bag lying down and vomiting papers; there was a company of Uriah’s books commanded by Mr. Tidd; there was a corner cupboard: and there were the usual articles of furniture.", "유라이어가 저녁에 읽거나 쓸 수 있도록 윗면이 책상으로 된 서랍장이 있었고, 유라이어의 파란 가방이 종이를 토해 내듯 입을 벌린 채 널브러져 있었으며, 티드 선생이 지휘하는 유라이어의 책 한 무리가 있었고, 구석에는 찬장이, 그리고 여느 가구들이 있었다."], ["I don’t remember that any individual object had a bare, pinched, spare look; but I do remember that the whole place had.", "어느 한 물건이 따로 헐벗고 옹색하고 빈약해 보였던 기억은 없으나, 그 방 전체가 그러했던 것만은 똑똑히 기억난다."]], [["It was perhaps a part of Mrs. Heep’s humility, that she still wore weeds.", "힙 부인이 아직도 상복을 입고 있던 것도 어쩌면 그녀의 비천함의 한 부분이었으리라."], ["Notwithstanding the lapse of time that had occurred since Mr. Heep’s decease, she still wore weeds.", "힙 씨가 세상을 떠난 지 적잖은 세월이 흘렀는데도, 그녀는 여전히 상복을 입고 있었다."], ["I think there was some compromise in the cap; but otherwise she was as weedy as in the early days of her mourning.", "모자에는 얼마쯤 타협이 있었던 듯하나, 그 밖에는 상을 막 당했던 초기 시절과 다름없이 온통 상복 차림이었다."]], [["‘This is a day to be remembered, my Uriah, I am sure,’ said Mrs.", "\"오늘은 두고두고 기억할 날이에요, 우리 유라이어, 정말이지.\""], ["Heep, making the tea, ‘when Master Copperfield pays us a visit.’", "차를 우리며 힙 부인이 말했다. \"코퍼필드 도련님이 우리를 찾아 주시다니 말이야.\""]], [["‘I said you’d think so, mother,’ said Uriah.", "\"제가 그렇게 생각하실 거라고 했잖아요, 어머니.\" 유라이어가 말했다."]], [["‘If I could have wished father to remain among us for any reason,’ said Mrs.", "\"무슨 이유로든 아버지가 우리 곁에 남아 계시기를 바랄 수 있었다면,\" 힙 부인이 말했다."], ["Heep, ‘it would have been, that he might have known his company this afternoon.’", "\"그건 오늘 오후의 이 귀한 손님을 그이가 알아뵐 수 있게 하기 위해서였을 거예요.\""]], [["I felt embarrassed by these compliments; but I was sensible, too, of being entertained as an honoured guest, and I thought Mrs. Heep an agreeable woman.", "이런 찬사에 나는 거북함을 느꼈으나, 그러면서도 귀한 손님으로 대접받고 있다는 것이 느껴졌고, 힙 부인이 상냥한 여인이라고 생각했다."]], [["‘My Uriah,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리 유라이어는,\""], ["Heep, ‘has looked forward to this, sir, a long while.", "힙 부인이 말했다. \"이날을 오래도록 손꼽아 기다렸답니다, 도련님."], ["He had his fears that our umbleness stood in the way, and I joined in them myself.", "저희의 비천함이 걸림돌이 될까 봐 그 애도 걱정했고, 저도 그 걱정에 함께했지요."], ["Umble we are, umble we have been, umble we shall ever be,’ said Mrs. Heep.", "저희는 비천하고, 비천해 왔으며, 앞으로도 영영 비천할 거예요.\" 힙 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘I am sure you have no occasion to be so, ma’am,’ I said, ‘unless you like.’", "\"굳이 그러실 필요는 없다고 생각합니다, 부인.\" 내가 말했다. \"원하지 않으신다면요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir,’ retorted Mrs. Heep.", "\"고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 힙 부인이 받았다."], ["‘We know our station and are thankful in it.’", "\"저희는 저희 분수를 알고, 그 안에서 감사하며 지냅니다.\""]], [["I found that Mrs. Heep gradually got nearer to me, and that Uriah gradually got opposite to me, and that they respectfully plied me with the choicest of the eatables on the table.", "문득 보니 힙 부인은 차츰차츰 나에게 가까이 다가오고, 유라이어는 차츰차츰 내 맞은편으로 자리를 옮겼으며, 두 사람은 공손하게 식탁 위의 가장 좋은 먹을거리를 나에게 자꾸 권했다."], ["There was nothing particularly choice there, to be sure; but I took the will for the deed, and felt that they were very attentive.", "사실 거기 특별히 좋은 거라곤 없었지만, 나는 그 마음만으로도 고맙게 받아들였고, 두 사람이 무척 정성스럽다고 느꼈다."], ["Presently they began to talk about aunts, and then I told them about mine; and about fathers and mothers, and then I told them about mine; and then Mrs.", "이윽고 그들은 고모 이야기를 꺼냈고, 그러자 나는 우리 고모님 이야기를 했다. 또 아버지와 어머니 이야기가 나오자, 나는 우리 부모님 이야기를 했다."], ["Heep began to talk about fathers-in-law, and then I began to tell her about mine--but stopped, because my aunt had advised me to observe a silence on that subject.", "그러자 힙 부인이 의붓아버지 이야기를 꺼냈고, 나는 우리 의붓아버지 이야기를 시작했으나 — 멈추었다. 고모님이 그 일에 대해서는 입을 다물라고 충고하셨기 때문이다."], ["A tender young cork, however, would have had no more chance against a pair of corkscrews, or a tender young tooth against a pair of dentists, or a little shuttlecock against two battledores, than I had against Uriah and Mrs. Heep.", "그러나 연하고 어린 코르크 마개가 한 쌍의 코르크 마개뽑이를 당해 낼 재간이 없고, 연하고 어린 이가 한 쌍의 치과 의사를 당해 낼 재간이 없으며, 작은 깃털공이 두 개의 라켓을 당해 낼 재간이 없듯이, 나도 유라이어와 힙 부인을 당해 낼 재간이 없었다."], ["They did just what they liked with me; and wormed things out of me that I had no desire to tell, with a certainty I blush to think of, the more especially, as in my juvenile frankness, I took some credit to myself for being so confidential and felt that I was quite the patron of my two respectful entertainers.", "그들은 나를 제멋대로 주물렀고, 내가 털어놓을 마음이 조금도 없던 것들을, 떠올리면 얼굴이 붉어질 만큼 능숙하게 캐냈다. 더구나 어린아이다운 솔직함에 빠진 나는, 이렇게 속을 다 터놓는 것을 스스로 제법 대견하게 여기며, 나야말로 이 공손한 두 접대꾼의 후원자라도 된 듯이 느꼈으니, 더더욱 그러했다."]], [["They were very fond of one another: that was certain.", "그들은 서로를 무척 아꼈으니, 그것만은 분명했다."], ["I take it, that had its effect upon me, as a touch of nature; but the skill with which the one followed up whatever the other said, was a touch of art which I was still less proof against.", "짐작건대 그것이 인지상정의 정취로서 나에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤을 것이다. 하지만 한쪽이 무슨 말을 하면 다른 쪽이 그것을 받아 이어 가는 그 솜씨는, 내가 한층 더 당해 낼 수 없는 기교의 정취였다."], ["When there was nothing more to be got out of me about myself (for on the Murdstone and Grinby life, and on my journey, I was dumb), they began about Mr. Wickfield and Agnes. Uriah threw the ball to Mrs.", "나 자신에 관해 더 캐낼 것이 없어지자(머드스톤 앤드 그린비 시절과 내 여정에 대해서는 나는 입을 꾹 다물었으니), 그들은 위크필드 씨와 아그네스 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."], ["Heep, Mrs. Heep caught it and threw it back to Uriah, Uriah kept it up a little while, then sent it back to Mrs. Heep, and so they went on tossing it about until I had no idea who had got it, and was quite bewildered.", "유라이어가 힙 부인에게 공을 던지면, 힙 부인이 그것을 받아 유라이어에게 되던지고, 유라이어가 잠시 받아쳤다가 다시 힙 부인에게 보내고, 그렇게 두 사람이 공을 주거니 받거니 하는 통에, 나는 이제 누가 그 공을 쥐고 있는지조차 알 수 없어 완전히 어리둥절해졌다."], ["The ball itself was always changing too. Now it was Mr.", "공 자체도 줄곧 바뀌었다."], ["Wickfield, now Agnes, now the excellence of Mr. Wickfield, now my admiration of Agnes; now the extent of Mr. Wickfield’s business and resources, now our domestic life after dinner; now, the wine that Mr.", "어느 땐 위크필드 씨였다가, 어느 땐 아그네스였다가, 어느 땐 위크필드 씨의 훌륭함이었다가, 어느 땐 아그네스를 향한 내 흠모였다가, 어느 땐 위크필드 씨의 사업과 재산의 규모였다가, 어느 땐 저녁 식사 뒤 우리 집안의 가정생활이었다가, 어느 땐 위크필드 씨가 마시는 술, 그가 그것을 마시는 이유, 그가 그토록 많이 마시는 것이 안타깝다는 이야기였다가, 어느 땐 이것, 어느 땐 저것, 그러다 한꺼번에 모든 것이 되었다."], ["Wickfield took, the reason why he took it, and the pity that it was he took so much; now one thing, now another, then everything at once; and all the time, without appearing to speak very often, or to do anything but sometimes encourage them a little, for fear they should be overcome by their humility and the honour of my company, I found myself perpetually letting out something or other that I had no business to let out and seeing the effect of it in the twinkling of Uriah’s dinted nostrils.", "그리고 그 내내, 자주 말하는 것처럼도, 또 그들이 자신들의 비천함과 내가 함께해 주는 영광에 짓눌릴까 봐 이따금 조금 격려해 주는 것 말고는 무언가를 하는 것처럼도 보이지 않으면서, 나는 끊임없이 무언가, 발설해선 안 될 무언가를 흘리고 있었고, 그 효과가 유라이어의 우묵 팬 콧구멍이 씰룩이는 데서 드러나는 것을 지켜보았다."]], [["I had begun to be a little uncomfortable, and to wish myself well out of the visit, when a figure coming down the street passed the door--it stood open to air the room, which was warm, the weather being close for the time of year--came back again, looked in, and walked in, exclaiming loudly, ‘Copperfield!", "이쯤 되자 나는 조금 거북해지기 시작했고, 이 방문에서 어서 벗어나고 싶어지던 참이었는데, 거리를 내려오던 한 사람이 문 앞을 지나갔다 — 문은 방을 환기하려고 열려 있었으니, 그해의 그 시기치고는 날이 후텁지근해 방 안이 따뜻했던 것이다 — 그러더니 그 사람이 되돌아와 안을 들여다보고는, 안으로 걸어 들어오며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"코퍼필드!"], ["Is it possible?’", "이게 웬일인가?\""]], [["It was Mr. Micawber!", "미코버 씨였다!"], ["It was Mr. Micawber, with his eye-glass, and his walking-stick, and his shirt-collar, and his genteel air, and the condescending roll in his voice, all complete!", "미코버 씨가, 외알 안경에, 지팡이에, 셔츠 깃에, 그 점잖은 풍채에, 거들먹거리듯 굴리는 목소리까지, 죄다 갖추고 나타난 것이다!"]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"이보게 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, putting out his hand, ‘this is indeed a meeting which is calculated to impress the mind with a sense of the instability and uncertainty of all human--in short, it is a most extraordinary meeting.", "미코버 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"이거야말로 인간 만사의 덧없음과 불확실함을 마음에 새겨 주기에 꼭 알맞은 만남일세 — 한마디로, 더없이 기이한 만남이야."], ["Walking along the street, reflecting upon the probability of something turning up (of which I am at present rather sanguine), I find a young but valued friend turn up, who is connected with the most eventful period of my life; I may say, with the turning-point of my existence.", "거리를 걸으며 뭔가 풀릴 일이 생기지 않을까(이 점에 대해서는 지금 나는 제법 낙관하고 있다네) 곰곰 생각하던 차에, 젊되 귀한 벗이 떡하니 나타나는데, 내 인생에서 가장 파란만장했던 시절과 — 아니, 내 존재의 전환점과 인연이 닿은 친구가 말이야."], ["Copperfield, my dear fellow, how do you do?’", "코퍼필드, 이 친구야, 그래 잘 지냈는가?\""]], [["I cannot say--I really cannot say--that I was glad to see Mr.", "미코버 씨를 거기서 보게 되어 기뻤다고는, 정말이지 말할 수 없다."], ["Micawber there; but I was glad to see him too, and shook hands with him, heartily, inquiring how Mrs. Micawber was.", "그래도 그를 본 것이 반갑기는 했으니, 나는 그와 진심으로 악수를 나누고 미코버 부인은 안녕하시냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Thank you,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙네.\""], ["Micawber, waving his hand as of old, and settling his chin in his shirt-collar.", "미코버 씨가 예전처럼 손을 한 번 휘젓고 셔츠 깃에 턱을 묻으며 말했다."], ["‘She is tolerably convalescent.", "\"그럭저럭 회복되어 가는 중이지."], ["The twins no longer derive their sustenance from Nature’s founts--in short,’ said Mr.", "쌍둥이는 이제 자연의 샘에서 양식을 길어 먹지 않게 되었어 — 한마디로,\""], ["Micawber, in one of his bursts of confidence, ‘they are weaned--and Mrs. Micawber is, at present, my travelling companion.", "미코버 씨가 특유의 속내를 한바탕 털어놓는 투로 말했다. \"젖을 뗐다네 — 그래서 미코버 부인이 지금은 내 여행의 길벗이지."], ["She will be rejoiced, Copperfield, to renew her acquaintance with one who has proved himself in all respects a worthy minister at the sacred altar of friendship.’", "부인도 무척 기뻐할 걸세, 코퍼필드, 모든 면에서 우정이라는 신성한 제단의 합당한 사제임을 스스로 증명해 보인 이와 다시 정을 나누게 되었으니 말이야.\""]], [["I said I should be delighted to see her.", "나는 부인을 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁘겠다고 했다."]], [["‘You are very good,’ said Mr. Micawber.", "\"참으로 고마운 사람일세.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber then smiled, settled his chin again, and looked about him.", "그러더니 미코버 씨는 빙긋 웃고, 다시 턱을 깃에 묻은 다음, 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["‘I have discovered my friend Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 내 벗 코퍼필드를 발견했는데,\""], ["Micawber genteelly, and without addressing himself particularly to anyone, ‘not in solitude, but partaking of a social meal in company with a widow lady, and one who is apparently her offspring--in short,’ said Mr.", "미코버 씨가 점잖게, 누구를 콕 집어 말하는 것도 아니게 말했다. \"혼자가 아니라, 한 과부댁과, 그리고 보아하니 그 자제인 듯한 이와 더불어 정겨운 식사를 함께하고 있더란 말이지 — 한마디로,\""], ["Micawber, in another of his bursts of confidence, ‘her son.", "미코버 씨가 또 한 번 속내를 한바탕 털어놓는 투로 말했다."], ["I shall esteem it an honour to be presented.’", "\"그 아드님과 함께. 소개를 받게 된다면 영광으로 알겠네.\""]], [["I could do no less, under these circumstances, than make Mr. Micawber known to Uriah Heep and his mother; which I accordingly did.", "이런 상황에서 나로서는 미코버 씨를 유라이어 힙과 그의 어머니에게 소개해 주는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었고, 그래서 그렇게 했다."], ["As they abased themselves before him, Mr. Micawber took a seat, and waved his hand in his most courtly manner.", "그들이 그의 앞에서 한껏 몸을 낮추는 동안, 미코버 씨는 자리에 앉아 더없이 정중한 태도로 손을 한 번 휘저었다."]], [["‘Any friend of my friend Copperfield’s,’ said Mr.", "\"내 벗 코퍼필드의 벗이라면,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘has a personal claim upon myself.’", "\"누구든 나 자신에게도 친구일 권리가 있는 셈이지요.\""]], [["‘We are too umble, sir,’ said Mrs. Heep, ‘my son and me, to be the friends of Master Copperfield.", "\"저희는 코퍼필드 도련님의 벗이 되기에는 너무나 비천한 사람들입니다, 선생님.\" 힙 부인이 말했다."], ["He has been so good as take his tea with us, and we are thankful to him for his company, also to you, sir, for your notice.’", "\"제 아들과 저는요. 도련님이 저희와 함께 차를 들어 주시는 친절을 베푸셨으니, 함께해 주신 데 대해서도, 또 선생님이 이렇게 알은체해 주신 데 대해서도 감사할 따름이지요.\""]], [["‘Ma’am,’ returned Mr.", "\"부인,\""], ["Micawber, with a bow, ‘you are very obliging: and what are you doing, Copperfield?", "미코버 씨가 허리를 굽히며 받았다. \"참으로 친절하십니다. 그래, 자네는 요즘 뭘 하고 지내나, 코퍼필드?"], ["Still in the wine trade?’", "아직도 포도주 장사인가?\""]], [["I was excessively anxious to get Mr. Micawber away; and replied, with my hat in my hand, and a very red face, I have no doubt, that I was a pupil at Doctor Strong’s.", "나는 미코버 씨를 어서 거기서 떼어 내고 싶어 안달이 나서, 모자를 손에 든 채, 분명 얼굴이 새빨개진 채로, 나는 스트롱 박사 학교의 학생이라고 대답했다."]], [["‘A pupil?’ said Mr.", "\"학생이라고?\""], ["Micawber, raising his eyebrows.", "미코버 씨가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다."], ["‘I am extremely happy to hear it.", "\"그것참 듣던 중 반가운 소릴세."], ["Although a mind like my friend Copperfield’s’--to Uriah and Mrs. Heep--‘does not require that cultivation which, without his knowledge of men and things, it would require, still it is a rich soil teeming with latent vegetation--in short,’ said Mr.", "비록 내 벗 코퍼필드 같은 정신은\" — 유라이어와 힙 부인을 향해 — \"사람과 세상에 대한 그의 식견이 없었더라면 필요했을 그런 도야까지야 필요 없겠지만, 그래도 그것은 잠재된 초목으로 가득한 비옥한 토양이니 — 한마디로,\""], ["Micawber, smiling, in another burst of confidence, ‘it is an intellect capable of getting up the classics to any extent.’", "미코버 씨가 빙긋 웃으며 또 한 번 속내를 한바탕 털어놓는 투로 말했다. \"고전을 얼마든지 깊이 익힐 수 있는 지성이란 말이지.\""]], [["Uriah, with his long hands slowly twining over one another, made a ghastly writhe from the waist upwards, to express his concurrence in this estimation of me.", "유라이어는 그 긴 두 손을 천천히 서로 비비 꼬며, 나에 대한 이 평가에 동의한다는 뜻으로 허리 위쪽을 섬뜩하게 배배 꼬았다."]], [["‘Shall we go and see Mrs.", "\"미코버 부인을 뵈러 가실까요, 선생님?\""], ["Micawber, sir?’ I said, to get Mr. Micawber away.", "미코버 씨를 떼어 내려고 내가 말했다."]], [["‘If you will do her that favour, Copperfield,’ replied Mr. Micawber, rising.", "\"부인에게 그런 호의를 베풀어 주겠다면야, 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 일어서며 받았다."], ["‘I have no scruple in saying, in the presence of our friends here, that I am a man who has, for some years, contended against the pressure of pecuniary difficulties.’ I knew he was certain to say something of this kind; he always would be so boastful about his difficulties.", "\"여기 우리 벗들 앞에서 거리낌 없이 말하건대, 나는 여러 해 동안 금전적 곤경의 압박과 맞서 싸워 온 사람일세.\" 그가 이런 류의 말을 꼭 하리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었다. 그는 늘 자기 곤경을 두고 그렇게 으스대곤 했으니까."], ["‘Sometimes I have risen superior to my difficulties.", "\"어떤 때는 내가 곤경을 딛고 우뚝 일어섰지."], ["Sometimes my difficulties have--in short, have floored me.", "또 어떤 때는 곤경이 — 한마디로, 나를 때려눕혔어."], ["There have been times when I have administered a succession of facers to them; there have been times when they have been too many for me, and I have given in, and said to Mrs.", "내가 곤경에 연거푸 정통으로 한 방씩 먹인 때도 있었고, 곤경이 나에게 너무 벅차서 두 손 들고 미코버 부인에게 카토의 말을 빌려 이렇게 말한 때도 있었지."], ["Micawber, in the words of Cato, “Plato, thou reasonest well. It’s all up now.", "'플라톤이여, 그대의 추론은 옳도다. 이제 다 끝났구나."], ["I can show fight no more.”", "더는 싸울 수 없도다.'"], ["But at no time of my life,’ said Mr.", "하지만 내 평생 어느 때에도,\""], ["Micawber, ‘have I enjoyed a higher degree of satisfaction than in pouring my griefs (if I may describe difficulties, chiefly arising out of warrants of attorney and promissory notes at two and four months, by that word) into the bosom of my friend Copperfield.’", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 슬픔을(주로 위임장이며 두 달짜리, 넉 달짜리 약속어음에서 비롯된 곤경을 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 말일세) 내 벗 코퍼필드의 가슴에 쏟아붓던 그때만큼 더 큰 만족을 누린 적이 없었네.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber closed this handsome tribute by saying, ‘Mr. Heep!", "미코버 씨는 이 멋들어진 헌사를 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"힙 군!"], ["Good evening. Mrs. Heep!", "잘 있게나. 힙 부인!"], ["Your servant,’ and then walking out with me in his most fashionable manner, making a good deal of noise on the pavement with his shoes, and humming a tune as we went.", "안녕히 계십시오.\" 그러고는 더없이 멋들어진 태도로 나와 함께 걸어 나가, 구두로 보도를 요란하게 울리며 콧노래까지 흥얼거렸다."]], [["It was a little inn where Mr. Micawber put up, and he occupied a little room in it, partitioned off from the commercial room, and strongly flavoured with tobacco-smoke.", "미코버 씨가 묵는 곳은 자그마한 여관이었고, 그는 그 안의 작은 방을 차지하고 있었는데, 영업실에서 칸막이로 막아 놓은 그 방은 담배 연기 냄새가 짙게 배어 있었다."], ["I think it was over the kitchen, because a warm greasy smell appeared to come up through the chinks in the floor, and there was a flabby perspiration on the walls.", "바닥 틈새로 따뜻하고 기름진 냄새가 올라오는 듯한 것을 보면 부엌 위에 있는 방이지 싶었고, 벽에는 끈적한 땀이 배어나 있었다."], ["I know it was near the bar, on account of the smell of spirits and jingling of glasses.", "술 냄새와 유리잔 짤랑거리는 소리로 미루어 술집 바로 옆이라는 것도 알 수 있었다."], ["Here, recumbent on a small sofa, underneath a picture of a race-horse, with her head close to the fire, and her feet pushing the mustard off the dumb-waiter at the other end of the room, was Mrs.", "여기, 경주마 그림 아래 작은 소파에 비스듬히 기대어, 머리는 난로에 바짝 붙이고, 두 발로는 방 저쪽 끝의 식기 운반대에서 겨자를 밀어내고 있는 이가, 바로 미코버 부인이었다."], ["Micawber, to whom Mr. Micawber entered first, saying, ‘My dear, allow me to introduce to you a pupil of Doctor Strong’s.’", "미코버 씨는 먼저 들어가 부인에게 말했다. \"여보, 스트롱 박사 학교의 학생을 소개하리다.\""]], [["I noticed, by the by, that although Mr. Micawber was just as much confused as ever about my age and standing, he always remembered, as a genteel thing, that I was a pupil of Doctor Strong’s.", "말이 나온 김에 짚어 두자면, 미코버 씨는 내 나이와 처지에 대해 여전히 예전과 다름없이 헷갈려 했으나, 내가 스트롱 박사 학교의 학생이라는 것만은 점잖은 일이라며 늘 잊지 않고 기억했다."]], [["Mrs. Micawber was amazed, but very glad to see me.", "미코버 부인은 깜짝 놀라면서도 나를 보고 무척 반가워했다."], ["I was very glad to see her too, and, after an affectionate greeting on both sides, sat down on the small sofa near her.", "나도 부인을 보고 무척 반가웠고, 양쪽이 다정하게 인사를 나눈 뒤, 나는 부인 곁의 작은 소파에 앉았다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘if you will mention to Copperfield what our present position is, which I have no doubt he will like to know, I will go and look at the paper the while, and see whether anything turns up among the advertisements.’", "\"우리의 지금 처지가 어떤지 코퍼필드에게 좀 들려주구려. 이 친구도 분명 알고 싶어 할 테니. 나는 그동안 신문이나 좀 들여다보면서 광고들 가운데 뭔가 풀릴 일이 있나 살펴보겠소.\""]], [["‘I thought you were at Plymouth, ma’am,’ I said to Mrs. Micawber, as he went out.", "\"전 두 분이 플리머스에 계신 줄 알았어요, 부인.\" 그가 나가자 내가 미코버 부인에게 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Master Copperfield,’ she replied, ‘we went to Plymouth.’", "\"코퍼필드 도련님,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"우리는 플리머스에 갔었지요.\""]], [["‘To be on the spot,’ I hinted.", "\"현장에 자리 잡으시려고요.\" 내가 넌지시 비쳤다."]], [["‘Just so,’ said Mrs. Micawber.", "\"바로 그래요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["‘To be on the spot. But, the truth is, talent is not wanted in the Custom House.", "\"현장에 자리를 잡으려고요. 하지만 사실은, 세관에서는 재능을 원하지 않더군요."], ["The local influence of my family was quite unavailing to obtain any employment in that department, for a man of Mr.", "미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 사람에게 그 부서에서 일자리 하나 얻어 주는 데 우리 집안의 지역 영향력이라곤 도무지 쓸모가 없었어요."], ["Micawber’s abilities. They would rather NOT have a man of Mr.", "그들은 차라리 미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 사람을 두고 싶어 하지 않아요."], ["Micawber’s abilities. He would only show the deficiency of the others.", "그이는 다른 사람들의 모자람만 드러내 보일 테니까요. 그뿐 아니라,\""], ["Apart from which,’ said Mrs.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘I will not disguise from you, my dear Master Copperfield, that when that branch of my family which is settled in Plymouth, became aware that Mr. Micawber was accompanied by myself, and by little Wilkins and his sister, and by the twins, they did not receive him with that ardour which he might have expected, being so newly released from captivity.", "\"도련님께는 숨기지 않겠어요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 플리머스에 자리 잡은 우리 집안의 그 갈래가, 미코버 씨가 저와 어린 윌킨스와 그 누이, 그리고 쌍둥이를 데리고 왔다는 것을 알고는, 갓 구금에서 풀려난 그이가 기대했음 직한 그런 열의로 그이를 맞아 주지 않더란 말이에요. 사실은,\""], ["In fact,’ said Mrs. Micawber, lowering her voice,--‘this is between ourselves--our reception was cool.’", "미코버 부인이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"이건 우리끼리 얘기지만 — 우리가 받은 대접은 냉랭했어요.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ I said.", "\"저런!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mrs. Micawber.", "\"그래요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["‘It is truly painful to contemplate mankind in such an aspect, Master Copperfield, but our reception was, decidedly, cool.", "\"인간을 그런 면에서 바라보자니 참으로 마음 아픈 일이지요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 하지만 우리가 받은 대접은 확실히 냉랭했어요."], ["There is no doubt about it.", "그건 의심할 여지가 없지요."], ["In fact, that branch of my family which is settled in Plymouth became quite personal to Mr. Micawber, before we had been there a week.’", "사실, 플리머스에 자리 잡은 우리 집안의 그 갈래는, 우리가 거기 간 지 일주일도 채 안 되어 미코버 씨에게 아주 노골적으로 인신공격을 하기 시작했어요.\""]], [["I said, and thought, that they ought to be ashamed of themselves.", "나는 그들이 부끄러운 줄 알아야 한다고 말했고, 또 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["‘Still, so it was,’ continued Mrs. Micawber.", "\"어쨌든 그렇게 됐지요.\" 미코버 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["‘Under such circumstances, what could a man of Mr. Micawber’s spirit do?", "\"그런 상황에서 미코버 씨 같은 기개를 지닌 사람이 무얼 할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["But one obvious course was left.", "한 가지 빤한 길밖에 없었지요."], ["To borrow, of that branch of my family, the money to return to London, and to return at any sacrifice.’", "우리 집안의 그 갈래에게서 런던으로 돌아갈 돈을 빌려, 어떤 희생을 치르고서라도 돌아오는 것 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Then you all came back again, ma’am?’ I said.", "\"그래서 다들 다시 돌아오셨군요, 부인?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘We all came back again,’ replied Mrs. Micawber.", "\"우리 모두 다시 돌아왔지요.\" 미코버 부인이 대답했다."], ["‘Since then, I have consulted other branches of my family on the course which it is most expedient for Mr. Micawber to take--for I maintain that he must take some course, Master Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"그 뒤로 나는 미코버 씨가 취하기에 가장 마땅한 길에 대해 우리 집안의 다른 갈래들에게 의논했어요 — 나는 그이가 어떤 길이든 택해야 한다고 주장하니까요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\""], ["Micawber, argumentatively.", "미코버 부인이 따지듯이 말했다."], ["‘It is clear that a family of six, not including a domestic, cannot live upon air.’", "\"하인을 빼고도 여섯이나 되는 식구가 허공만 먹고 살 수는 없는 노릇이 분명하잖아요.\""]], [["‘Certainly, ma’am,’ said I.", "\"그렇고말고요, 부인.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘The opinion of those other branches of my family,’ pursued Mrs. Micawber, ‘is, that Mr.", "\"우리 집안의 그 다른 갈래들의 의견은,\" 미코버 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Micawber should immediately turn his attention to coals.’", "\"미코버 씨가 당장 석탄에 관심을 돌려야 한다는 거예요.\""]], [["‘To what, ma’am?’", "\"무엇에요, 부인?\""]], [["‘To coals,’ said Mrs. Micawber.", "\"석탄이요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["‘To the coal trade. Mr.", "\"석탄 장사요."], ["Micawber was induced to think, on inquiry, that there might be an opening for a man of his talent in the Medway Coal Trade.", "미코버 씨는 알아본 끝에, 메드웨이 석탄 사업에 그이의 재능을 지닌 사람에게 길이 열려 있을지도 모른다는 생각을 품게 됐지요."], ["Then, as Mr. Micawber very properly said, the first step to be taken clearly was, to come and see the Medway.", "그래서, 미코버 씨가 아주 지당하게 말했듯이, 가장 먼저 밟아야 할 단계는 분명 가서 메드웨이를 직접 보는 것이었어요."], ["Which we came and saw.", "그래서 우리는 가서 봤지요."], ["I say “we”, Master Copperfield; for I never will,’ said Mrs.", "내가 '우리'라고 하는 건요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 나는 결코,\""], ["Micawber with emotion, ‘I never will desert Mr. Micawber.’", "미코버 부인이 북받쳐 말했다. \"결코 미코버 씨를 버리지 않을 것이기 때문이에요.\""]], [["I murmured my admiration and approbation.", "나는 감탄과 찬동을 나직이 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘We came,’ repeated Mrs.", "\"우리는 가서,\""], ["Micawber, ‘and saw the Medway.", "미코버 부인이 되풀이했다."], ["My opinion of the coal trade on that river is, that it may require talent, but that it certainly requires capital. Talent, Mr.", "\"메드웨이를 봤어요. 그 강의 석탄 사업에 대한 내 의견은, 그게 재능을 필요로 할지는 몰라도 자본은 분명히 필요로 한다는 거예요."], ["Micawber has; capital, Mr. Micawber has not.", "재능이라면 미코버 씨가 가졌지요."], ["We saw, I think, the greater part of the Medway; and that is my individual conclusion.", "자본은, 미코버 씨에게 없고요. 우리는 메드웨이의 대부분을 본 것 같아요."], ["Being so near here, Mr.", "그게 내 개인적인 결론이지요."], ["Micawber was of opinion that it would be rash not to come on, and see the Cathedral.", "여기서 이렇게 가까우니, 미코버 씨는 여기까지 와서 대성당을 보지 않는 건 경솔한 일일 거라는 의견이었어요."], ["Firstly, on account of its being so well worth seeing, and our never having seen it; and secondly, on account of the great probability of something turning up in a cathedral town.", "첫째로는, 그게 그토록 볼 만한 가치가 있는데 우리가 한 번도 본 적이 없었기 때문이고, 둘째로는, 대성당이 있는 도시에서는 뭔가 풀릴 일이 생길 가능성이 크기 때문이지요."], ["We have been here,’ said Mrs.", "우리가 여기 온 지,\""], ["Micawber, ‘three days.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Nothing has, as yet, turned up; and it may not surprise you, my dear Master Copperfield, so much as it would a stranger, to know that we are at present waiting for a remittance from London, to discharge our pecuniary obligations at this hotel.", "\"사흘이 됐어요. 아직까지 풀린 일은 아무것도 없고요. 그리고, 코퍼필드 도련님, 우리가 지금 이 호텔에서 진 빚을 갚으려고 런던에서 올 송금을 기다리고 있다는 것은, 낯선 사람만큼 도련님을 그리 놀라게 하지는 않을 거예요."], ["Until the arrival of that remittance,’ said Mrs.", "그 송금이 도착하기 전까지는,\""], ["Micawber with much feeling, ‘I am cut off from my home (I allude to lodgings in Pentonville), from my boy and girl, and from my twins.’", "미코버 부인이 자못 감정에 북받쳐 말했다. \"나는 내 집(펜턴빌의 하숙을 두고 하는 말이에요)과, 내 아들딸과, 내 쌍둥이로부터 떨어져 있는 셈이지요.\""]], [["I felt the utmost sympathy for Mr. and Mrs. Micawber in this anxious extremity, and said as much to Mr.", "이 애타는 궁지에 빠진 미코버 부부에게 나는 더없는 동정을 느꼈고, 마침 돌아온 미코버 씨에게 그 마음을 그대로 전하며, 그저 내게 돈이 넉넉히 있어서 두 분에게 필요한 액수를 빌려드릴 수 있다면 좋으련만 하고 덧붙였다."], ["Micawber, who now returned: adding that I only wished I had money enough, to lend them the amount they needed. Mr.", "미코버 씨의 대답은 그의 어지러운 심정을 드러내는 것이었다. 그는 나와 악수하며 말했다."], ["Micawber’s answer expressed the disturbance of his mind.", "\"코퍼필드, 자네는 진정한 친구일세."], ["He said, shaking hands with me, ‘Copperfield, you are a true friend; but when the worst comes to the worst, no man is without a friend who is possessed of shaving materials.’ At this dreadful hint Mrs. Micawber threw her arms round Mr.", "하지만 최악의 사태가 닥쳐도, 면도 도구를 가진 사람에게는 친구가 없는 법이 없지.\" 이 끔찍한 암시에 미코버 부인은 두 팔로 미코버 씨의 목을 끌어안고 부디 진정하라고 애원했다."], ["Micawber’s neck and entreated him to be calm. He wept; but so far recovered, almost immediately, as to ring the bell for the waiter, and bespeak a hot kidney pudding and a plate of shrimps for breakfast in the morning.", "그는 눈물을 흘렸으나, 거의 곧바로 정신을 추슬러, 종을 울려 종업원을 부르고는 이튿날 아침 식사로 뜨거운 콩팥 푸딩 하나와 새우 한 접시를 주문했다."]], [["When I took my leave of them, they both pressed me so much to come and dine before they went away, that I could not refuse.", "내가 작별을 고하자, 두 사람은 떠나기 전에 꼭 와서 함께 식사하자고 하도 졸라 대는 통에, 나는 거절할 수가 없었다."], ["But, as I knew I could not come next day, when I should have a good deal to prepare in the evening, Mr. Micawber arranged that he would call at Doctor Strong’s in the course of the morning (having a presentiment that the remittance would arrive by that post), and propose the day after, if it would suit me better.", "하지만 이튿날은 저녁에 준비할 일이 많아 갈 수 없을 것을 알았으므로, 미코버 씨는 오전 중에 스트롱 박사 학교로 나를 찾아와(그 우편으로 송금이 도착하리라는 예감이 든다면서), 내게 형편이 더 나으면 모레로 정하자고 제안하기로 했다."], ["Accordingly I was called out of school next forenoon, and found Mr. Micawber in the parlour; who had called to say that the dinner would take place as proposed.", "그리하여 이튿날 오전 나는 수업 중에 불려 나갔고, 응접실에서 미코버 씨를 만났는데, 그는 식사를 제안한 대로 그날 하기로 하자고 말하러 온 것이었다."], ["When I asked him if the remittance had come, he pressed my hand and departed.", "송금이 왔느냐고 물으니, 그는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는 떠나갔다."]], [["As I was looking out of window that same evening, it surprised me, and made me rather uneasy, to see Mr.", "바로 그날 저녁, 창밖을 내다보고 있던 나는, 미코버 씨와 유라이어 힙이 팔짱을 끼고 지나가는 것을 보고 깜짝 놀랐으며 적잖이 마음이 불편해졌다."], ["Micawber and Uriah Heep walk past, arm in arm: Uriah humbly sensible of the honour that was done him, and Mr. Micawber taking a bland delight in extending his patronage to Uriah.", "유라이어는 자기에게 베풀어진 영광을 비천하게 의식하고 있었고, 미코버 씨는 유라이어에게 후원의 손길을 뻗는 데 흐뭇한 기쁨을 느끼고 있었다."], ["But I was still more surprised, when I went to the little hotel next day at the appointed dinner-hour, which was four o’clock, to find, from what Mr. Micawber said, that he had gone home with Uriah, and had drunk brandy-and-water at Mrs. Heep’s.", "그러나 이튿날 약속한 식사 시간인 네 시에 그 작은 호텔로 갔을 때, 미코버 씨의 말로 미루어 그가 유라이어와 함께 그 집으로 가서 힙 부인 댁에서 브랜디에 물을 타 마셨다는 것을 알고, 나는 더더욱 놀랐다."]], [["‘And I’ll tell you what, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서 말인데 말이야, 이보게 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, ‘your friend Heep is a young fellow who might be attorney-general.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"자네 벗 힙은 장차 검찰총장이 될 만한 젊은이일세."], ["If I had known that young man, at the period when my difficulties came to a crisis, all I can say is, that I believe my creditors would have been a great deal better managed than they were.’", "내가 내 곤경이 막바지에 이르렀던 그 시절에 저 젊은이를 알았더라면, 한마디로 말해, 내 채권자들이 실제보다 훨씬 더 잘 다뤄졌으리라고 나는 믿네.\""]], [["I hardly understood how this could have been, seeing that Mr. Micawber had paid them nothing at all as it was; but I did not like to ask.", "미코버 씨가 사실상 채권자들에게 한 푼도 갚지 않은 마당에, 어떻게 그럴 수 있었다는 건지 나는 도무지 이해할 수 없었다."], ["Neither did I like to say, that I hoped he had not been too communicative to Uriah; or to inquire if they had talked much about me. I was afraid of hurting Mr.", "하지만 굳이 묻고 싶지는 않았다. 또한 그가 유라이어에게 너무 속을 다 털어놓지 않았기를 바란다고 말하고 싶지도, 그들이 내 이야기를 많이 했는지 물어보고 싶지도 않았다."], ["Micawber’s feelings, or, at all events, Mrs. Micawber’s, she being very sensitive; but I was uncomfortable about it, too, and often thought about it afterwards.", "미코버 씨의 마음을, 아니 적어도 미코버 부인의 마음을 상하게 할까 봐 두려웠으니, 부인이 무척 예민한 사람이었기 때문이다. 하지만 그 일이 마음에 걸렸고, 그 뒤로도 자주 그 생각이 났다."]], [["We had a beautiful little dinner.", "우리는 멋진 자그마한 만찬을 들었다."], ["Quite an elegant dish of fish; the kidney-end of a loin of veal, roasted; fried sausage-meat; a partridge, and a pudding.", "우아한 생선 요리 한 접시에, 송아지 허릿살의 콩팥 쪽을 구운 것, 소시지 고기 튀김, 자고새 한 마리, 그리고 푸딩이 있었다."], ["There was wine, and there was strong ale; and after dinner Mrs. Micawber made us a bowl of hot punch with her own hands.", "포도주도 있고 독한 에일도 있었으며, 식사 뒤에는 미코버 부인이 손수 뜨거운 펀치를 한 사발 만들어 주었다."]], [["Mr. Micawber was uncommonly convivial.", "미코버 씨는 유난히 흥겨웠다."], ["I never saw him such good company. He made his face shine with the punch, so that it looked as if it had been varnished all over.", "그토록 좋은 말벗인 그를 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 그는 펀치로 얼굴을 번들번들 빛냈는데, 마치 온통 니스 칠이라도 한 것처럼 보였다."], ["He got cheerfully sentimental about the town, and proposed success to it; observing that Mrs. Micawber and himself had been made extremely snug and comfortable there and that he never should forget the agreeable hours they had passed in Canterbury.", "그는 이 도시를 두고 즐겁게 감상에 젖어 도시의 번영을 위해 건배를 제안하면서, 미코버 부인과 자기가 여기서 더없이 아늑하고 편안하게 지냈으며 캔터베리에서 보낸 즐거운 시간을 결코 잊지 못할 거라고 했다."], ["He proposed me afterwards; and he, and Mrs. Micawber, and I, took a review of our past acquaintance, in the course of which we sold the property all over again.", "이어서 그는 나를 위해 건배했고, 그와 미코버 부인과 나는 지난 우리 인연을 돌이켜 보았는데, 그러는 가운데 우리는 그 가재도구들을 또 한 번 죄다 팔아 치웠다."], ["Then I proposed Mrs. Micawber: or, at least, said, modestly, ‘If you’ll allow me, Mrs.", "그다음에는 내가 미코버 부인을 위해 건배를 제안했다. 아니, 적어도 나는 점잖게 이렇게 말했다."], ["Micawber, I shall now have the pleasure of drinking your health, ma’am.’ On which Mr.", "\"괜찮으시다면, 미코버 부인, 이제 부인의 건강을 위해 잔을 들 영광을 누리고 싶습니다.\""], ["Micawber delivered an eulogium on Mrs. Micawber’s character, and said she had ever been his guide, philosopher, and friend, and that he would recommend me, when I came to a marrying time of life, to marry such another woman, if such another woman could be found.", "그러자 미코버 씨가 미코버 부인의 인품에 대한 찬사를 늘어놓으며, 부인이야말로 한결같이 자기의 길잡이요 철학자요 벗이었다고 했고, 내가 장가들 나이가 되거든 이런 또 한 사람의 여인을, 그런 여인을 찾을 수만 있다면, 꼭 아내로 맞으라고 권한다고 했다."]], [["As the punch disappeared, Mr. Micawber became still more friendly and convivial. Mrs.", "펀치가 줄어들수록 미코버 씨는 한층 더 다정하고 흥겨워졌다."], ["Micawber’s spirits becoming elevated, too, we sang ‘Auld Lang Syne’.", "미코버 부인의 기분도 한껏 들떠, 우리는 '올드 랭 사인'을 불렀다."], ["When we came to ‘Here’s a hand, my trusty frere’, we all joined hands round the table; and when we declared we would ‘take a right gude Willie Waught’, and hadn’t the least idea what it meant, we were really affected.", "'여기 내 손이 있네, 나의 미더운 벗이여'라는 대목에 이르렀을 때 우리는 모두 식탁을 빙 둘러 손을 맞잡았고, '우리 한 잔 흠뻑 들이켜세'라고 외칠 때는, 그게 무슨 뜻인지는 도무지 알지 못하면서도, 정말로 가슴이 뭉클해졌다."]], [["In a word, I never saw anybody so thoroughly jovial as Mr. Micawber was, down to the very last moment of the evening, when I took a hearty farewell of himself and his amiable wife.", "한마디로, 그날 저녁의 마지막 순간, 내가 그와 그의 사랑스러운 아내에게 진심 어린 작별을 고할 때까지, 미코버 씨만큼 더없이 유쾌한 사람을 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없다."], ["Consequently, I was not prepared, at seven o’clock next morning, to receive the following communication, dated half past nine in the evening; a quarter of an hour after I had left him:--", "따라서 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 다음과 같은 편지를 받게 되리라고는 미처 마음의 준비를 하지 못했으니, 그 편지에 적힌 날짜는 전날 저녁 아홉 시 반, 그러니까 내가 그와 헤어진 지 십오 분 뒤였다. \"나의 친애하는 젊은 벗,"]], [["‘The die is cast--all is over.", "주사위는 던져졌네 — 모든 게 끝났어."], ["Hiding the ravages of care with a sickly mask of mirth, I have not informed you, this evening, that there is no hope of the remittance!", "병색 도는 명랑함의 가면으로 근심의 상흔을 감춘 채, 나는 오늘 저녁 자네에게 송금이 가망 없다는 사실을 알리지 못했네!"], ["Under these circumstances, alike humiliating to endure, humiliating to contemplate, and humiliating to relate, I have discharged the pecuniary liability contracted at this establishment, by giving a note of hand, made payable fourteen days after date, at my residence, Pentonville, London.", "이런 상황에서 — 견디기에도 굴욕스럽고, 헤아리기에도 굴욕스럽고, 입에 담기에도 한결같이 굴욕스러운 — 나는 이 업소에서 진 금전적 빚을, 발행일로부터 십사 일 뒤에 내 거처인 런던 펜턴빌에서 지급하기로 한 약속어음 한 장을 써 주어 청산했네."], ["When it becomes due, it will not be taken up.", "그 어음이 만기가 되어도, 결제되지 못할 걸세."], ["The result is destruction. The bolt is impending, and the tree must fall.", "그 결과는 파멸이지. 벼락은 머리 위에 드리워져 있고, 나무는 쓰러질 수밖에 없네."]], [["‘Let the wretched man who now addresses you, my dear Copperfield, be a beacon to you through life.", "지금 자네에게 이 글을 쓰는 이 가련한 사내가, 부디 자네에게 평생의 등불이 되기를 바라네, 친애하는 코퍼필드."], ["He writes with that intention, and in that hope.", "그런 뜻으로, 그런 희망으로 이 글을 쓰는 걸세."], ["If he could think himself of so much use, one gleam of day might, by possibility, penetrate into the cheerless dungeon of his remaining existence--though his longevity is, at present (to say the least of it), extremely problematical.", "만약 그가 자신을 그토록 쓸모 있는 존재로 여길 수 있다면, 어쩌면 그의 남은 삶의 음울한 토굴 속으로 한 줄기 햇살이라도 스며들지 모르지 — 하기야 그의 수명이라는 것도 지금으로선(아무리 좋게 말해도) 극히 불확실하다네만."]], [["‘This is the last communication, my dear Copperfield, you will ever receive", "이것이 자네가 받게 될 마지막 편지일세, 친애하는 코퍼필드,\n거지가 되어 버림받은 자,"]], [["‘From\n‘The", "윌킨스 미코버"]], [["‘Beggared Outcast,", "로부터.\""]], [["I was so shocked by the contents of this heart-rending letter, that I ran off directly towards the little hotel with the intention of taking it on my way to Doctor Strong’s, and trying to soothe Mr.", "이 가슴 찢어지는 편지의 내용에 어찌나 충격을 받았던지, 나는 스트롱 박사 학교로 가는 길에 그 작은 호텔에 들러 위로의 말 한마디로 미코버 씨를 달래 보려고 곧장 그리로 달려갔다."], ["Micawber with a word of comfort. But, half-way there, I met the London coach with Mr. and Mrs.", "그러나 절반쯤 갔을 때 런던행 마차와 마주쳤는데, 그 뒷자리에 미코버 부부가 타고 있었다."], ["Micawber up behind; Mr. Micawber, the very picture of tranquil enjoyment, smiling at Mrs. Micawber’s conversation, eating walnuts out of a paper bag, with a bottle sticking out of his breast pocket.", "미코버 씨는 그야말로 평온한 만족의 화신처럼, 미코버 부인의 이야기에 미소 지으며, 종이봉투에서 호두를 꺼내 먹고 있었고, 가슴 주머니에서는 술병 하나가 삐죽 튀어나와 있었다."], ["As they did not see me, I thought it best, all things considered, not to see them.", "그들이 나를 보지 못했으므로, 나는 이모저모 따져 보아 그들을 못 본 척하는 게 상책이라고 생각했다."], ["So, with a great weight taken off my mind, I turned into a by-street that was the nearest way to school, and felt, upon the whole, relieved that they were gone; though I still liked them very much, nevertheless.", "그래서, 마음의 큰 짐을 던 채, 나는 학교로 가는 가장 가까운 길인 샛길로 접어들었고, 대체로 그들이 떠나 버려 홀가분하다고 느꼈다. 그러면서도 여전히 그들이 무척 좋았지만 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 18. A RETROSPECT", "# CHAPTER 18. A RETROSPECT"]], [["My school-days!", "내 학창 시절!"], ["The silent gliding on of my existence--the unseen, unfelt progress of my life--from childhood up to youth!", "어린 시절부터 청년기에 이르기까지, 소리 없이 미끄러져 흘러간 내 존재 — 보이지도 않고 느껴지지도 않게 나아간 내 삶이여!"], ["Let me think, as I look back upon that flowing water, now a dry channel overgrown with leaves, whether there are any marks along its course, by which I can remember how it ran.", "이제는 메말라 잎으로 뒤덮인 물길이 되어 버린 그 흐르던 강물을 되돌아보며, 그 흐름이 어떠했는지를 기억해 낼 만한 표지가 그 물줄기를 따라 어딘가 남아 있는지 한번 헤아려 보자."]], [["A moment, and I occupy my place in the Cathedral, where we all went together, every Sunday morning, assembling first at school for that purpose.", "한순간, 나는 성당 안 내 자리에 앉아 있다. 우리는 일요일 아침마다 다 같이 그곳에 갔는데, 그러려고 먼저 학교에 모이곤 했다."], ["The earthy smell, the sunless air, the sensation of the world being shut out, the resounding of the organ through the black and white arched galleries and aisles, are wings that take me back, and hold me hovering above those days, in a half-sleeping and half-waking dream.", "흙냄새, 햇빛 한 줄기 들지 않는 공기, 세상이 바깥에 차단되어 있다는 느낌, 검고 흰 아치형 회랑과 통로를 따라 울려 퍼지는 오르간 소리 — 이것들은 나를 그 시절로 데려가는 날개가 되어, 반쯤 잠들고 반쯤 깨어 있는 꿈속에서 그 나날 위를 맴돌게 붙잡아 둔다."]], [["I am not the last boy in the school.", "나는 학교에서 꼴찌가 아니다."], ["I have risen in a few months, over several heads.", "몇 달 사이에 여러 아이를 제치고 위로 올라섰다."], ["But the first boy seems to me a mighty creature, dwelling afar off, whose giddy height is unattainable.", "그러나 일등 하는 아이는 내게 까마득히 먼 곳에 사는 막강한 존재로 보였고, 그 아찔한 높이는 도저히 닿을 수 없는 것 같았다."], ["Agnes says ‘No,’ but I say ‘Yes,’ and tell her that she little thinks what stores of knowledge have been mastered by the wonderful Being, at whose place she thinks I, even I, weak aspirant, may arrive in time.", "애그니스는 \"아니야\"라고 했지만 나는 \"맞아\"라고 우기며, 그 놀라운 존재가 얼마나 많은 지식을 통달했는지 너는 짐작도 못 한다고 말했다. 그런데도 애그니스는 나, 그래, 이 보잘것없는 지망생인 나조차도 언젠가는 그 자리에 이를 수 있으리라 여겼다."], ["He is not my private friend and public patron, as Steerforth was, but I hold him in a reverential respect.", "그는 스티어포스가 그랬던 것처럼 나만의 친구이자 공공연한 후원자는 아니었으나, 나는 그를 경외에 가까운 마음으로 우러러보았다."], ["I chiefly wonder what he’ll be, when he leaves Doctor Strong’s, and what mankind will do to maintain any place against him.", "무엇보다 나는 그가 스트롱 박사 학교를 떠나면 어떤 사람이 될지, 그리고 그에게 맞서 어떤 자리를 지키려면 인류가 무슨 수를 써야 할지가 궁금했다."]], [["But who is this that breaks upon me?", "그런데 이렇게 불쑥 내 마음에 끼어드는 이 사람은 누구인가?"], ["This is Miss Shepherd, whom I love.", "바로 셰퍼드 양인데, 나는 그녀를 사랑한다."]], [["Miss Shepherd is a boarder at the Misses Nettingalls’ establishment.", "셰퍼드 양은 네팅걸 자매가 운영하는 기숙학교의 기숙생이다."], ["I adore Miss Shepherd.", "나는 셰퍼드 양을 흠모한다."], ["She is a little girl, in a spencer, with a round face and curly flaxen hair. The Misses Nettingalls’ young ladies come to the Cathedral too.", "그녀는 짧은 웃옷을 걸친 자그마한 소녀로, 둥근 얼굴에 곱슬곱슬한 아마빛 머리카락을 지녔다. 네팅걸 자매네 아가씨들도 성당에 온다."], ["I cannot look upon my book, for I must look upon Miss Shepherd.", "나는 책에 눈을 둘 수가 없으니, 셰퍼드 양만 바라봐야 하기 때문이다."], ["When the choristers chaunt, I hear Miss Shepherd.", "성가대가 성가를 부를 때면 나는 셰퍼드 양의 목소리만 듣는다."], ["In the service I mentally insert Miss Shepherd’s name--I put her in among the Royal Family.", "예배 중에 나는 마음속으로 셰퍼드 양의 이름을 끼워 넣어 — 그녀를 왕족들 틈에 넣어 둔다."], ["At home, in my own room, I am sometimes moved to cry out, ‘Oh, Miss Shepherd!’ in a transport of love.", "집에서, 내 방에 혼자 있을 때면, 사랑에 겨운 황홀경에 빠져 이따금 \"아, 셰퍼드 양!\" 하고 소리치고 싶어진다."]], [["For some time, I am doubtful of Miss Shepherd’s feelings, but, at length, Fate being propitious, we meet at the dancing-school.", "한동안 나는 셰퍼드 양의 마음을 확신하지 못하다가, 마침내 운명이 우리 편이 되어 무용 학원에서 만난다."], ["I have Miss Shepherd for my partner.", "셰퍼드 양이 내 짝이 된다."], ["I touch Miss Shepherd’s glove, and feel a thrill go up the right arm of my jacket, and come out at my hair.", "셰퍼드 양의 장갑에 손이 닿는 순간, 짜릿한 떨림이 내 윗옷 오른팔을 타고 올라와 머리카락 끝으로 빠져나가는 게 느껴진다."], ["I say nothing to Miss Shepherd, but we understand each other.", "나는 셰퍼드 양에게 아무 말도 하지 않지만, 우리는 서로를 알아챈다."], ["Miss Shepherd and myself live but to be united.", "셰퍼드 양과 나는 오직 맺어지기 위해서만 산다."]], [["Why do I secretly give Miss Shepherd twelve Brazil nuts for a present, I wonder?", "나는 왜 셰퍼드 양에게 브라질너트 열두 알을 몰래 선물로 주는 걸까, 정말 모르겠다."], ["They are not expressive of affection, they are difficult to pack into a parcel of any regular shape, they are hard to crack, even in room doors, and they are oily when cracked; yet I feel that they are appropriate to Miss Shepherd.", "그것은 애정을 드러내는 물건도 아니고, 반듯한 모양으로 꾸러미에 싸기도 어렵고, 방문틈에 끼워서도 깨기 힘들며, 깨고 나면 기름이 잔뜩 묻어난다. 그런데도 나는 그것이 셰퍼드 양에게 딱 어울린다고 느낀다."], ["Soft, seedy biscuits, also, I bestow upon Miss Shepherd; and oranges innumerable.", "부드럽고 씨가 박힌 비스킷도 셰퍼드 양에게 갖다 바치고, 오렌지는 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 안긴다."], ["Once, I kiss Miss Shepherd in the cloak-room. Ecstasy!", "한번은 외투 보관실에서 셰퍼드 양에게 입을 맞춘다. 황홀하다!"], ["What are my agony and indignation next day, when I hear a flying rumour that the Misses Nettingall have stood Miss Shepherd in the stocks for turning in her toes!", "그런데 이튿날, 셰퍼드 양이 안짱걸음을 걷는다고 네팅걸 자매가 그녀를 발에 차꼬를 채워 세워 두었다는 소문이 퍼져 나가니, 그날 내가 느낀 고통과 분노는 또 어떠한가!"]], [["Miss Shepherd being the one pervading theme and vision of my life, how do I ever come to break with her? I can’t conceive.", "셰퍼드 양은 내 삶을 온통 휘감은 단 하나의 주제이자 환영인데, 도대체 어쩌다 나는 그녀와 갈라서게 되는 걸까? 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없다."], ["And yet a coolness grows between Miss Shepherd and myself.", "그런데도 셰퍼드 양과 나 사이에는 점점 냉랭한 기운이 자라난다."], ["Whispers reach me of Miss Shepherd having said she wished I wouldn’t stare so, and having avowed a preference for Master Jones--for Jones!", "셰퍼드 양이 내가 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보지 않았으면 좋겠다고 말했다는 둥, 존스 군을 더 좋아한다고 털어놓았다는 둥 하는 소문이 내 귀에 들려온다 — 그것도 존스를!"], ["a boy of no merit whatever!", "아무런 장점도 없는 녀석을!"], ["The gulf between me and Miss Shepherd widens.", "나와 셰퍼드 양 사이의 골은 점점 깊어진다."], ["At last, one day, I meet the Misses Nettingalls’ establishment out walking.", "마침내 어느 날, 나는 산책을 나온 네팅걸 자매네 학생들과 마주친다."], ["Miss Shepherd makes a face as she goes by, and laughs to her companion. All is over.", "셰퍼드 양은 지나가면서 얼굴을 찡그리더니 동무에게 까르르 웃는다. 모든 것이 끝났다."], ["The devotion of a life--it seems a life, it is all the same--is at an end; Miss Shepherd comes out of the morning service, and the Royal Family know her no more.", "한평생의 헌신이 — 한평생처럼 느껴지니 어차피 마찬가지다 — 막을 내렸다. 셰퍼드 양은 아침 예배에서 걸어 나오고, 왕족들은 이제 그녀를 알지 못한다."]], [["I am higher in the school, and no one breaks my peace.", "나는 학교에서 더 윗자리로 올라가고, 아무도 내 평온을 깨뜨리지 못한다."], ["I am not at all polite, now, to the Misses Nettingalls’ young ladies, and shouldn’t dote on any of them, if they were twice as many and twenty times as beautiful.", "이제 나는 네팅걸 자매네 아가씨들에게 조금도 예의를 차리지 않으며, 그들이 지금의 곱절로 많고 스무 배로 아름답다 한들 누구 하나에게도 빠지지 않을 것이다."], ["I think the dancing-school a tiresome affair, and wonder why the girls can’t dance by themselves and leave us alone.", "나는 무용 학원이 따분한 일이라 여기고, 여자애들이 자기들끼리 춤추고 우리를 가만히 내버려 두면 안 되나 싶어 의아해한다."], ["I am growing great in Latin verses, and neglect the laces of my boots.", "나는 라틴어 운문에 점점 두각을 나타내고, 부츠 끈 묶는 일 따위는 등한시한다."], ["Doctor Strong refers to me in public as a promising young scholar. Mr.", "스트롱 박사는 여러 사람 앞에서 나를 두고 장래가 촉망되는 젊은 학자라고 일컫는다."], ["Dick is wild with joy, and my aunt remits me a guinea by the next post.", "딕 씨는 기뻐서 어쩔 줄 모르고, 고모님은 바로 다음 우편으로 나에게 일 기니를 부쳐 준다."]], [["The shade of a young butcher rises, like the apparition of an armed head in Macbeth.", "한 젊은 푸주한의 그림자가, 「맥베스」에 나오는 투구 쓴 머리의 환영처럼 떠오른다."], ["Who is this young butcher?", "이 젊은 푸주한은 누구인가?"], ["He is the terror of the youth of Canterbury.", "그는 캔터베리 청소년들의 공포다."], ["There is a vague belief abroad, that the beef suet with which he anoints his hair gives him unnatural strength, and that he is a match for a man.", "그가 머리에 바르는 쇠기름이 그에게 초자연적인 힘을 주어 어른과도 맞먹는다는 막연한 믿음이 널리 퍼져 있다."], ["He is a broad-faced, bull-necked, young butcher, with rough red cheeks, an ill-conditioned mind, and an injurious tongue.", "그는 얼굴이 넓적하고 황소처럼 목이 굵은 젊은 푸주한으로, 두 뺨은 거칠게 붉고, 심보는 고약하며, 혀는 사람을 해코지한다."], ["His main use of this tongue, is, to disparage Doctor Strong’s young gentlemen.", "이 혀의 주된 쓰임새는 스트롱 박사의 젊은 신사들을 깎아내리는 것이다."], ["He says, publicly, that if they want anything he’ll give it ‘em.", "그는 공공연히 떠벌리기를, 그자들이 뭔가 원한다면 자기가 톡톡히 안겨 주겠다고 한다."], ["He names individuals among them (myself included), whom he could undertake to settle with one hand, and the other tied behind him.", "그러면서 그자들 가운데 몇몇(나도 포함해서)을 콕 집어, 한 손은 등 뒤로 묶은 채 다른 한 손만으로도 처리해 버릴 수 있다고 떠든다."], ["He waylays the smaller boys to punch their unprotected heads, and calls challenges after me in the open streets.", "그는 어린 학생들을 길목에서 기다렸다가 무방비한 머리통을 쥐어박고, 훤한 거리에서 내 뒤꽁무니에 대고 한판 붙자고 소리친다."], ["For these sufficient reasons I resolve to fight the butcher.", "이런 충분한 이유들 때문에 나는 그 푸주한과 한판 붙기로 결심한다."]], [["It is a summer evening, down in a green hollow, at the corner of a wall.", "어느 여름 저녁, 담장 모퉁이의 풀 우거진 움푹한 곳에서다."], ["I meet the butcher by appointment.", "나는 약속대로 그 푸주한을 만난다."], ["I am attended by a select body of our boys; the butcher, by two other butchers, a young publican, and a sweep. The preliminaries are adjusted, and the butcher and myself stand face to face.", "내 곁에는 우리 학교에서 가려 뽑은 아이들 한 무리가 따라붙고, 푸주한 곁에는 다른 푸주한 둘, 젊은 술집 주인 하나, 그리고 굴뚝 청소부 하나가 따른다. 준비가 갖추어지고, 푸주한과 나는 얼굴을 맞대고 선다."], ["In a moment the butcher lights ten thousand candles out of my left eyebrow.", "한순간에 푸주한이 내 왼쪽 눈썹에서 촛불 만 개를 피워 낸다."], ["In another moment, I don’t know where the wall is, or where I am, or where anybody is.", "또 한순간, 나는 담장이 어디 있는지, 내가 어디 있는지, 누가 어디 있는지조차 알 수 없다."], ["I hardly know which is myself and which the butcher, we are always in such a tangle and tussle, knocking about upon the trodden grass.", "우리는 짓밟힌 풀밭 위를 뒹굴며 줄곧 한데 뒤엉켜 맞붙어 싸우는 통에, 나는 어느 쪽이 나고 어느 쪽이 푸주한인지도 거의 분간하지 못한다."], ["Sometimes I see the butcher, bloody but confident; sometimes I see nothing, and sit gasping on my second’s knee; sometimes I go in at the butcher madly, and cut my knuckles open against his face, without appearing to discompose him at all.", "어떤 때는 피투성이지만 의기양양한 푸주한이 보이고, 어떤 때는 아무것도 보이지 않아 내 후원자의 무릎에 앉아 숨을 헐떡이며, 어떤 때는 미친 듯이 푸주한에게 달려들었다가 그의 얼굴에 주먹 마디가 까져 터지지만 그는 눈 하나 까딱하지 않는 듯하다."], ["At last I awake, very queer about the head, as from a giddy sleep, and see the butcher walking off, congratulated by the two other butchers and the sweep and publican, and putting on his coat as he goes; from which I augur, justly, that the victory is his.", "마침내 나는, 아찔한 잠에서 깨어나듯 머리가 몹시 멍한 채로 정신을 차리고, 푸주한이 다른 두 푸주한과 굴뚝 청소부, 술집 주인의 축하를 받으며 가는 길에 외투를 걸치고 걸어가 버리는 모습을 본다. 그 모습에서 나는, 옳게도, 승리는 그의 것이라고 헤아린다."]], [["I am taken home in a sad plight, and I have beef-steaks put to my eyes, and am rubbed with vinegar and brandy, and find a great puffy place bursting out on my upper lip, which swells immoderately.", "나는 처참한 몰골로 집에 실려 가, 두 눈에 비프스테이크를 갖다 대고, 식초와 브랜디로 문질러진 채, 윗입술에 크게 부풀어 오른 곳이 터져 나와 보기 흉할 만큼 부어오른 것을 발견한다."], ["For three or four days I remain at home, a very ill-looking subject, with a green shade over my eyes; and I should be very dull, but that Agnes is a sister to me, and condoles with me, and reads to me, and makes the time light and happy.", "사나흘 동안 나는 눈에 초록 가리개를 쓰고 몹시 흉한 꼴로 집에 머무른다. 무척이나 따분했을 테지만, 애그니스가 나에게 누이처럼 굴어 위로해 주고, 책을 읽어 주며, 시간을 가볍고 즐겁게 만들어 주니 그렇지 않다. 애그니스는 늘, 언제나, 내 속내를 온전히 받아 주는 사람이다."], ["Agnes has my confidence completely, always; I tell her all about the butcher, and the wrongs he has heaped upon me; she thinks I couldn’t have done otherwise than fight the butcher, while she shrinks and trembles at my having fought him.", "나는 그녀에게 푸주한 이야기며 그가 나에게 안긴 온갖 부당한 일을 죄다 털어놓는다. 그녀는 내가 그 푸주한과 싸우는 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었으리라 여기면서도, 내가 그와 싸웠다는 사실에 몸을 움츠리며 떤다."]], [["Time has stolen on unobserved, for Adams is not the head-boy in the days that are come now, nor has he been this many and many a day.", "세월은 어느새 슬그머니 흘러갔다. 이제 와서는 애덤스가 더는 우두머리 학생이 아니고, 그런 지도 벌써 오래되었으니 말이다."], ["Adams has left the school so long, that when he comes back, on a visit to Doctor Strong, there are not many there, besides myself, who know him.", "애덤스가 학교를 떠난 지 너무 오래라, 그가 스트롱 박사를 찾아 학교에 들렀을 때 나 말고는 그를 알아보는 이가 거의 없다."], ["Adams is going to be called to the bar almost directly, and is to be an advocate, and to wear a wig.", "애덤스는 머지않아 변호사 자격을 얻어 법정 변호사가 되고 가발을 쓰게 될 참이다."], ["I am surprised to find him a meeker man than I had thought, and less imposing in appearance.", "나는 그가 내 짐작보다 더 온순한 사람이고 풍채도 덜 위풍당당해진 것에 놀란다."], ["He has not staggered the world yet, either; for it goes on (as well as I can make out) pretty much the same as if he had never joined it.", "게다가 그는 아직 세상을 뒤흔들지도 못했다. 세상은 (내가 헤아릴 수 있는 한) 그가 거기에 끼어든 적이 없는 것이나 거의 다름없이 굴러가고 있으니까."]], [["A blank, through which the warriors of poetry and history march on in stately hosts that seem to have no end--and what comes next!", "시와 역사 속의 용사들이 끝이 없을 듯한 위풍당당한 대열을 이루어 행군하는 텅 빈 공백 — 그리고 다음에 무엇이 오는가!"], ["I am the head-boy, now!", "이제 나는 우두머리 학생이다!"], ["I look down on the line of boys below me, with a condescending interest in such of them as bring to my mind the boy I was myself, when I first came there.", "나는 내 아래로 늘어선 아이들의 줄을 내려다보면서, 그 가운데 내가 처음 이곳에 왔을 적의 나 자신을 떠올리게 하는 아이들에게는 너그러이 관심을 보낸다."], ["That little fellow seems to be no part of me; I remember him as something left behind upon the road of life--as something I have passed, rather than have actually been--and almost think of him as of someone else.", "그 자그마한 녀석은 이제 나의 한 부분 같지가 않다. 나는 그를 인생의 길 위에 남겨 두고 온 무언가로 — 실제로 내가 그였다기보다는 내가 지나쳐 온 무언가로 기억하며, 거의 다른 누군가를 떠올리듯 그를 떠올린다."]], [["And the little girl I saw on that first day at Mr. Wickfield’s, where is she?", "그리고 위크필드 씨 댁에서 첫날 보았던 그 자그마한 소녀는 어디로 갔을까? 그 아이도 사라졌다."], ["Gone also. In her stead, the perfect likeness of the picture, a child likeness no more, moves about the house; and Agnes--my sweet sister, as I call her in my thoughts, my counsellor and friend, the better angel of the lives of all who come within her calm, good, self-denying influence--is quite a woman.", "그 자리에는 그림과 똑 닮은, 더는 아이의 모습이 아닌 모습이 집 안을 거닐고 있다. 그리고 애그니스는 — 마음속으로 내가 사랑스러운 누이라 부르는, 나의 조언자이자 벗이며, 차분하고 선하며 자기를 잊는 그 영향력 안에 들어오는 모든 이의 삶에 더 나은 천사가 되어 주는 그녀는 — 어엿한 여인이 되었다."]], [["What other changes have come upon me, besides the changes in my growth and looks, and in the knowledge I have garnered all this while?", "내 키와 외모가 자라고, 그동안 내가 쌓아 온 지식이 늘어난 것 말고, 또 어떤 변화가 나에게 찾아왔을까?"], ["I wear a gold watch and chain, a ring upon my little finger, and a long-tailed coat; and I use a great deal of bear’s grease--which, taken in conjunction with the ring, looks bad.", "나는 금시계와 시곗줄을 차고, 새끼손가락에 반지를 끼며, 긴 자락의 외투를 입는다. 그리고 곰 기름을 잔뜩 바르는데 — 이건 반지와 함께 놓고 보면 영 볼썽사납다."], ["Am I in love again? I am. I worship the eldest Miss Larkins.", "내가 또 사랑에 빠진 건가? 그렇다. 나는 라킨스 맏딸을 떠받든다."]], [["The eldest Miss Larkins is not a little girl.", "라킨스 맏딸은 자그마한 소녀가 아니다."], ["She is a tall, dark, black-eyed, fine figure of a woman.", "키가 크고 가무잡잡하며 검은 눈을 지닌, 훤칠한 몸매의 여인이다."], ["The eldest Miss Larkins is not a chicken; for the youngest Miss Larkins is not that, and the eldest must be three or four years older.", "라킨스 맏딸은 새파란 풋내기가 아니다. 라킨스 막내딸도 풋내기가 아닌데, 맏딸은 그보다 서너 살은 위가 분명하니까."], ["Perhaps the eldest Miss Larkins may be about thirty.", "어쩌면 라킨스 맏딸은 서른 살쯤 됐을지도 모른다."], ["My passion for her is beyond all bounds.", "그녀를 향한 내 열정은 그 어떤 경계도 넘어선다."]], [["The eldest Miss Larkins knows officers.", "라킨스 맏딸은 장교들과 알고 지낸다."], ["It is an awful thing to bear. I see them speaking to her in the street.", "견디기 끔찍한 일이다. 나는 그들이 거리에서 그녀에게 말을 거는 모습을 본다."], ["I see them cross the way to meet her, when her bonnet (she has a bright taste in bonnets) is seen coming down the pavement, accompanied by her sister’s bonnet.", "그녀의 보닛이 (그녀는 보닛 고르는 안목이 산뜻하다) 언니의 보닛을 데리고 인도를 따라 내려오는 게 보이면, 그들이 그녀를 맞으려고 길을 건너오는 모습을 본다."], ["She laughs and talks, and seems to like it.", "그녀는 웃고 떠들며, 그것을 즐기는 듯하다."], ["I spend a good deal of my own spare time in walking up and down to meet her.", "나는 내 한가한 시간을 적잖이 그녀를 만나려고 이리저리 걸어 다니는 데 쓴다."], ["If I can bow to her once in the day (I know her to bow to, knowing Mr. Larkins), I am happier. I deserve a bow now and then.", "하루에 한 번이라도 그녀에게 인사를 건넬 수 있으면 (나는 라킨스 씨와 아는 사이라, 그녀와도 인사를 나눌 정도의 사이다) 나는 더 행복하다."], ["The raging agonies I suffer on the night of the Race Ball, where I know the eldest Miss Larkins will be dancing with the military, ought to have some compensation, if there be even-handed justice in the world.", "나는 가끔은 인사를 받을 자격이 있다. 라킨스 맏딸이 군인들과 춤출 게 뻔한 경마 무도회가 열리는 그 밤에 내가 겪는 미칠 듯한 고통은, 세상에 공평한 정의라는 게 있다면 마땅히 무언가로 보상받아야 한다."]], [["My passion takes away my appetite, and makes me wear my newest silk neckerchief continually.", "내 열정은 식욕을 앗아 가고, 가장 새것인 비단 목도리를 줄곧 두르게 만든다."], ["I have no relief but in putting on my best clothes, and having my boots cleaned over and over again.", "가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고 부츠를 닦고 또 닦는 것 말고는 위안이 없다."], ["I seem, then, to be worthier of the eldest Miss Larkins.", "그러면 나는 라킨스 맏딸에게 좀 더 어울리는 사람이 된 듯하다."], ["Everything that belongs to her, or is connected with her, is precious to me. Mr.", "그녀에게 속한 것, 그녀와 이어진 것이라면 무엇이든 나에게는 귀하다."], ["Larkins (a gruff old gentleman with a double chin, and one of his eyes immovable in his head) is fraught with interest to me.", "라킨스 씨는 (이중턱에 한쪽 눈은 머릿속에 박힌 듯 꼼짝하지 않는, 무뚝뚝한 노신사다) 나에게 흥미로운 사람이다."], ["When I can’t meet his daughter, I go where I am likely to meet him.", "그의 딸을 만날 수 없을 때면, 나는 그를 만날 만한 곳으로 간다."], ["To say ‘How do you do, Mr.", "\"안녕하십니까, 라킨스 씨?"], ["Larkins? Are the young ladies and all the family quite well?’ seems so pointed, that I blush.", "아가씨들과 온 가족이 다들 평안하신지요?\"라고 말하는 게 어찌나 속이 빤히 들여다보이는지, 나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른다."]], [["I think continually about my age.", "나는 끊임없이 내 나이를 곱씹는다."], ["Say I am seventeen, and say that seventeen is young for the eldest Miss Larkins, what of that?", "내가 열일곱 살이라 치고, 열일곱이 라킨스 맏딸에게 어리다 친들, 그게 뭐 어떻단 말인가?"], ["Besides, I shall be one-and-twenty in no time almost.", "게다가 나는 곧 스물한 살이 될 텐데."], ["I regularly take walks outside Mr. Larkins’s house in the evening, though it cuts me to the heart to see the officers go in, or to hear them up in the drawing-room, where the eldest Miss Larkins plays the harp.", "나는 저녁마다 으레 라킨스 씨네 집 밖을 거니는데, 장교들이 안으로 들어가는 걸 보거나, 라킨스 맏딸이 하프를 켜는 응접실에서 그들의 소리가 들려오면 가슴이 미어진다."], ["I even walk, on two or three occasions, in a sickly, spoony manner, round and round the house after the family are gone to bed, wondering which is the eldest Miss Larkins’s chamber (and pitching, I dare say now, on Mr.", "심지어 나는 두세 번쯤은, 식구들이 다 잠자리에 든 뒤에 병이라도 든 듯 얼빠진 꼴로 집 둘레를 빙빙 돌면서, 어느 방이 라킨스 맏딸의 침실일까 헤아려 본다(지금 와 생각하면 아마 라킨스 씨의 방을 골랐을 게 뻔하지만)."], ["Larkins’s instead); wishing that a fire would burst out; that the assembled crowd would stand appalled; that I, dashing through them with a ladder, might rear it against her window, save her in my arms, go back for something she had left behind, and perish in the flames.", "그러면서 불이 확 났으면, 모여든 사람들이 겁에 질려 우두커니 섰으면, 그 틈으로 내가 사다리를 들고 뛰어들어 그녀의 창문에 그것을 걸치고, 내 품에 그녀를 안아 구해 내고, 그녀가 두고 온 무언가를 가지러 다시 들어갔다가 불길 속에 스러질 수 있으면 하고 빈다."], ["For I am generally disinterested in my love, and think I could be content to make a figure before Miss Larkins, and expire.", "나는 사랑에 있어 대체로 사심이 없는 사람이라, 라킨스 양 앞에서 멋진 모습 한번 보이고 죽을 수 있다면 그것으로 흡족하리라 여기기 때문이다."]], [["Generally, but not always.", "대체로 그렇지만, 늘 그런 건 아니다."], ["Sometimes brighter visions rise before me.", "가끔은 더 환한 환상이 내 앞에 떠오른다."], ["When I dress (the occupation of two hours), for a great ball given at the Larkins’s (the anticipation of three weeks), I indulge my fancy with pleasing images.", "라킨스 씨네서 열리는 성대한 무도회를 위해 (삼 주 동안 고대해 온 일이다) 옷을 차려입을 때면 (두 시간이나 걸리는 일이다), 나는 흐뭇한 그림들에 상상을 마음껏 풀어 놓는다."], ["I picture myself taking courage to make a declaration to Miss Larkins.", "나는 용기를 내어 라킨스 양에게 사랑을 고백하는 내 모습을 그려 본다."], ["I picture Miss Larkins sinking her head upon my shoulder, and saying, ‘Oh, Mr. Copperfield, can I believe my ears!’ I picture Mr.", "라킨스 양이 내 어깨에 고개를 묻으며 \"어머, 코퍼필드 씨, 제 귀를 믿어도 되나요!\"라고 말하는 모습을 그려 본다."], ["Larkins waiting on me next morning, and saying, ‘My dear Copperfield, my daughter has told me all.", "라킨스 씨가 이튿날 아침 나를 찾아와 \"코퍼필드 군, 딸아이가 다 이야기했네. 나이는 문제 될 게 없어."], ["Youth is no objection.", "여기 이만 파운드가 있네."], ["Here are twenty thousand pounds.", "부디 행복하게!\"라고 말하는 모습을 그려 본다."], ["Be happy!’ I picture my aunt relenting, and blessing us; and Mr. Dick and Doctor Strong being present at the marriage ceremony.", "고모님이 마음을 누그러뜨리고 우리를 축복해 주는 모습, 그리고 딕 씨와 스트롱 박사가 결혼식에 와 주는 모습도 그려 본다."], ["I am a sensible fellow, I believe--I believe, on looking back, I mean--and modest I am sure; but all this goes on notwithstanding.", "나는 분별 있는 녀석이라고 믿는다 — 돌이켜 보건대 그랬다고 믿는다는 뜻이다 — 게다가 겸손하기도 한 게 분명하다. 그런데도 이런 일은 어김없이 펼쳐진다."], ["I repair to the enchanted house, where there are lights, chattering, music, flowers, officers (I am sorry to see), and the eldest Miss Larkins, a blaze of beauty.", "나는 그 마법에 걸린 집으로 가는데, 그곳에는 불빛과 재잘거림, 음악, 꽃, 장교들 (보기 딱하게도), 그리고 눈부신 아름다움의 화신인 라킨스 맏딸이 있다."], ["She is dressed in blue, with blue flowers in her hair--forget-me-nots--as if SHE had any need to wear forget-me-nots.", "그녀는 파란 옷을 입고 머리에는 파란 꽃을 — 물망초를 — 꽂았는데, 마치 그녀가 물망초를 꽂아야 할 까닭이라도 있는 것처럼."], ["It is the first really grown-up party that I have ever been invited to, and I am a little uncomfortable; for I appear not to belong to anybody, and nobody appears to have anything to say to me, except Mr.", "이번이 내가 초대받은 첫 어른들의 진짜 파티라, 나는 조금 거북하다. 누구에게도 속하지 않은 듯하고, 라킨스 씨 말고는 아무도 나에게 할 말이 없는 듯하니 말이다."], ["Larkins, who asks me how my schoolfellows are, which he needn’t do, as I have not come there to be insulted.", "그런데 라킨스 씨는 내 학교 친구들이 어떻게 지내느냐고 묻는데, 나는 모욕당하러 거기 온 게 아니니 그런 건 묻지 않아도 될 일이다."]], [["But after I have stood in the doorway for some time, and feasted my eyes upon the goddess of my heart, she approaches me--she, the eldest Miss Larkins!--and asks me pleasantly, if I dance?", "그러나 한동안 문간에 서서 내 마음의 여신을 실컷 눈에 담고 있노라니, 그녀가 — 그녀가, 라킨스 맏딸이! — 나에게 다가와, 춤을 추느냐고 상냥하게 묻는다."]], [["I stammer, with a bow, ‘With you, Miss Larkins.’", "나는 고개를 숙이며 더듬더듬 말한다. \"당신과라면요, 라킨스 양.\""]], [["‘With no one else?’ inquires Miss Larkins.", "\"다른 사람과는 안 추고요?\" 라킨스 양이 묻는다."]], [["‘I should have no pleasure in dancing with anyone else.’", "\"다른 누구와 춤춰도 즐겁지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["Miss Larkins laughs and blushes (or I think she blushes), and says, ‘Next time but one, I shall be very glad.’", "라킨스 양은 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고 (아니, 붉힌 것 같다고 생각하고), 이렇게 말한다. \"다다음 곡에 추면 정말 좋겠네요.\""]], [["The time arrives.", "그 차례가 온다."], ["‘It is a waltz, I think,’ Miss Larkins doubtfully observes, when I present myself.", "\"이건 왈츠인 것 같은데요.\" 내가 다가서자 라킨스 양이 미심쩍은 듯 말한다."], ["‘Do you waltz? If not, Captain Bailey--’", "\"왈츠 추실 줄 알아요? 못 추시면 베일리 대위가—\""]], [["But I do waltz (pretty well, too, as it happens), and I take Miss Larkins out.", "그러나 나는 왈츠를 출 줄 안다 (게다가 마침 꽤 잘 추기까지 한다). 그래서 나는 라킨스 양을 데리고 나간다."], ["I take her sternly from the side of Captain Bailey.", "나는 그녀를 베일리 대위 곁에서 단호하게 데려온다."], ["He is wretched, I have no doubt; but he is nothing to me.", "그는 비참할 게 틀림없다. 하지만 그가 나와 무슨 상관이랴."], ["I have been wretched, too.", "나도 비참했었으니까."], ["I waltz with the eldest Miss Larkins!", "내가 라킨스 맏딸과 왈츠를 추다니!"], ["I don’t know where, among whom, or how long.", "나는 어디서, 누구들 틈에서, 얼마나 오래 췄는지도 모르겠다."], ["I only know that I swim about in space, with a blue angel, in a state of blissful delirium, until I find myself alone with her in a little room, resting on a sofa.", "그저 내가 파란 천사와 함께 더없이 행복한 황홀경에 빠져 허공을 떠다니다가, 어느 작은 방에서 그녀와 단둘이 소파에 기대어 쉬고 있는 나를 발견했다는 것만 안다."], ["She admires a flower (pink camellia japonica, price half-a-crown), in my button-hole. I give it her, and say:", "그녀가 내 단춧구멍에 꽂힌 꽃(분홍 동백, 값은 반 크라운)을 보고 감탄한다. 나는 그것을 그녀에게 건네며 말한다."]], [["‘I ask an inestimable price for it, Miss Larkins.’", "\"그 값으로 헤아릴 수 없는 것을 청합니다, 라킨스 양.\""]], [["‘Indeed!", "\"어머나!"], ["What is that?’ returns Miss Larkins.", "그게 뭔데요?\" 라킨스 양이 되묻는다."]], [["‘A flower of yours, that I may treasure it as a miser does gold.’", "\"당신의 꽃 한 송이입니다. 구두쇠가 황금을 끌어안듯 그것을 소중히 간직하게요.\""]], [["‘You’re a bold boy,’ says Miss Larkins.", "\"대담한 소년이군요.\" 라킨스 양이 말한다."], ["‘There.’", "\"자, 여기요.\""]], [["She gives it me, not displeased; and I put it to my lips, and then into my breast.", "그녀는 싫지 않은 기색으로 나에게 꽃을 건넨다. 나는 그것을 입술에 갖다 댄 다음 가슴팍에 품는다."], ["Miss Larkins, laughing, draws her hand through my arm, and says, ‘Now take me back to Captain Bailey.’", "라킨스 양은 웃으며 내 팔에 자기 손을 끼우고는 말한다. \"이제 베일리 대위에게 데려다주세요.\""]], [["I am lost in the recollection of this delicious interview, and the waltz, when she comes to me again, with a plain elderly gentleman who has been playing whist all night, upon her arm, and says:", "이 달콤한 만남과 그 왈츠를 떠올리느라 정신을 놓고 있는데, 그녀가 밤새 휘스트만 친 수수한 노신사 하나를 팔짱 끼고 나에게 다시 다가와 말한다."]], [["‘Oh! here is my bold friend! Mr.", "\"어머! 여기 제 대담한 친구가 있네요!"], ["Chestle wants to know you, Mr. Copperfield.’", "체슬 씨가 당신과 알고 지내고 싶어 하세요, 코퍼필드 씨.\""]], [["I feel at once that he is a friend of the family, and am much gratified.", "나는 그가 이 집안의 친구라는 것을 단박에 알아채고, 무척 흐뭇해한다."]], [["‘I admire your taste, sir,’ says Mr. Chestle.", "\"자네 안목에 감탄했네, 젊은이.\" 체슬 씨가 말한다."], ["‘It does you credit.", "\"보는 눈이 있어."], ["I suppose you don’t take much interest in hops; but I am a pretty large grower myself; and if you ever like to come over to our neighbourhood--neighbourhood of Ashford--and take a run about our place,--we shall be glad for you to stop as long as you like.’", "자네야 홉 따위엔 별로 관심이 없겠지만, 나는 제법 크게 홉을 재배하는 사람이라네. 혹시 우리 동네 — 애시퍼드 근방 — 에 한번 들러 우리 농장을 둘러보고 싶거든 언제든 오게. 머물고 싶은 만큼 머물러도 우린 반가울 테니.\""]], [["I thank Mr. Chestle warmly, and shake hands.", "나는 체슬 씨에게 진심으로 고맙다고 인사하며 악수를 나눈다."], ["I think I am in a happy dream.", "나는 행복한 꿈속에 있는 것만 같다."], ["I waltz with the eldest Miss Larkins once again.", "나는 라킨스 맏딸과 또 한 번 왈츠를 춘다."], ["She says I waltz so well! I go home in a state of unspeakable bliss, and waltz in imagination, all night long, with my arm round the blue waist of my dear divinity.", "그녀는 내가 춤을 어찌나 잘 추는지 모르겠다고 말한다! 나는 이루 말할 수 없는 더없는 행복에 젖어 집으로 가고, 밤새도록 상상 속에서 내 사랑스러운 여신의 파란 허리에 팔을 두르고 왈츠를 춘다."], ["For some days afterwards, I am lost in rapturous reflections; but I neither see her in the street, nor when I call.", "그 뒤로 며칠 동안 나는 황홀한 회상에 빠져 지낸다. 그러나 거리에서도 그녀를 보지 못하고, 찾아가도 그녀를 만나지 못한다."], ["I am imperfectly consoled for this disappointment by the sacred pledge, the perished flower.", "이 실망에 대한 위안은 그 신성한 정표, 시들어 버린 꽃뿐이라 어딘가 미진하다."]], [["‘Trotwood,’ says Agnes, one day after dinner.", "\"트롯우드.\" 어느 날 저녁 식사를 마친 뒤 애그니스가 말한다."], ["‘Who do you think is going to be married tomorrow? Someone you admire.’", "\"내일 누가 결혼하는지 알아? 네가 좋아하는 사람이야.\""]], [["‘Not you, I suppose, Agnes?’", "\"설마 너는 아니겠지, 애그니스?\""]], [["‘Not me!’ raising her cheerful face from the music she is copying.", "\"내가 아니지!\" 그녀가 베껴 적던 악보에서 명랑한 얼굴을 든다."], ["‘Do you hear him, Papa?--The eldest Miss Larkins.’", "\"아버지, 이 사람 좀 보세요. — 라킨스 맏딸이야.\""]], [["‘To--to Captain Bailey?’ I have just enough power to ask.", "\"베, 베일리 대위한테?\" 나는 가까스로 그만큼 물을 힘만 낸다."]], [["‘No; to no Captain. To Mr.", "\"아니, 무슨 대위가 아니야."], ["Chestle, a hop-grower.’", "홉 재배하는 체슬 씨한테.\""]], [["I am terribly dejected for about a week or two.", "나는 한 일이 주쯤 끔찍하게 풀이 죽어 지낸다."], ["I take off my ring, I wear my worst clothes, I use no bear’s grease, and I frequently lament over the late Miss Larkins’s faded flower.", "나는 반지를 빼고, 가장 허름한 옷을 입고, 곰 기름도 바르지 않고, 고인이 된 라킨스 양의 시든 꽃을 두고 자주 한탄한다."], ["Being, by that time, rather tired of this kind of life, and having received new provocation from the butcher, I throw the flower away, go out with the butcher, and gloriously defeat him.", "그러다 그 무렵에는 이런 식의 삶에 다소 진력이 나고, 게다가 그 푸주한에게서 새로운 도발까지 받았던 터라, 나는 꽃을 내던지고 푸주한과 한판 붙으러 나가 그를 보란 듯이 때려눕힌다."]], [["This, and the resumption of my ring, as well as of the bear’s grease in moderation, are the last marks I can discern, now, in my progress to seventeen.", "이것, 그리고 반지를 다시 끼고 곰 기름도 적당히 다시 바르기 시작한 것이, 이제 와 내가 열일곱 살로 나아가던 길에서 분간해 낼 수 있는 마지막 표지들이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 19. I LOOK ABOUT ME, AND MAKE A DISCOVERY", "# CHAPTER 19. I LOOK ABOUT ME, AND MAKE A DISCOVERY"]], [["I am doubtful whether I was at heart glad or sorry, when my school-days drew to an end, and the time came for my leaving Doctor Strong’s.", "학교생활이 막바지에 다다라 스트롱 박사님 곁을 떠날 때가 되었을 때, 마음 깊은 곳에서 기뻤는지 슬펐는지 나조차 잘 모르겠다."], ["I had been very happy there, I had a great attachment for the Doctor, and I was eminent and distinguished in that little world.", "나는 그곳에서 무척 행복했고, 박사님을 깊이 따랐으며, 그 작은 세계에서 손꼽히는 두드러진 학생이었다. 이런 이유로 떠나는 것이 서운했다."], ["For these reasons I was sorry to go; but for other reasons, unsubstantial enough, I was glad.", "하지만 다른 이유로는, 그리 알맹이 없는 이유들이었으되, 기쁘기도 했다."], ["Misty ideas of being a young man at my own disposal, of the importance attaching to a young man at his own disposal, of the wonderful things to be seen and done by that magnificent animal, and the wonderful effects he could not fail to make upon society, lured me away.", "내 마음대로 할 수 있는 어엿한 청년이 된다는 것, 제 앞가림을 하는 청년에게 따라붙는 위엄, 그 당당한 존재가 보고 또 해낼 놀라운 일들, 그리고 그가 세상에 틀림없이 일으킬 놀라운 파장 — 이런 어렴풋한 상상들이 나를 꾀어냈다."], ["So powerful were these visionary considerations in my boyish mind, that I seem, according to my present way of thinking, to have left school without natural regret.", "이 헛된 공상이 내 어린 마음을 어찌나 강하게 사로잡았던지, 지금 돌이켜 생각해 보면 나는 학교를 떠나면서도 마땅히 느껴야 할 아쉬움을 거의 느끼지 못했던 것 같다."], ["The separation has not made the impression on me, that other separations have.", "이 이별은 다른 이별들이 내게 남긴 것 같은 자국을 남기지 않았다."], ["I try in vain to recall how I felt about it, and what its circumstances were; but it is not momentous in my recollection.", "그때 내가 어떤 기분이었는지, 그 사정이 어떠했는지 떠올려 보려 애써도 헛수고였다. 그 일은 내 기억 속에서 그리 대단치 않았던 것이다."], ["I suppose the opening prospect confused me. I know that my juvenile experiences went for little or nothing then; and that life was more like a great fairy story, which I was just about to begin to read, than anything else.", "짐작건대 눈앞에 펼쳐지는 전망이 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 모양이다. 그때 내 어린 시절의 경험은 거의 아무런 무게도 지니지 못했다는 것, 그리고 그 무렵 인생이란 무엇보다도, 막 첫 장을 넘기려는 한 편의 거대한 동화 같았다는 것만은 분명히 안다."]], [["My aunt and I had held many grave deliberations on the calling to which I should be devoted.", "고모님과 나는 내가 무슨 일에 몸담아야 할지를 두고 진지한 의논을 여러 차례 나누었다."], ["For a year or more I had endeavoured to find a satisfactory answer to her often-repeated question, ‘What I would like to be?’ But I had no particular liking, that I could discover, for anything.", "일 년 남짓, 나는 고모님이 거듭 던지는 \"넌 뭐가 되고 싶으냐?\"라는 물음에 흡족한 대답을 찾으려 애썼다. 하지만 아무리 살펴봐도 나에게는 딱히 마음 끌리는 것이 없었다."], ["If I could have been inspired with a knowledge of the science of navigation, taken the command of a fast-sailing expedition, and gone round the world on a triumphant voyage of discovery, I think I might have considered myself completely suited.", "만일 항해술이라는 학문을 깨치도록 영감을 받아, 날쌘 탐험선의 지휘를 맡고, 의기양양한 발견의 항해로 세계를 한 바퀴 돌 수 있었다면, 나는 내가 완벽히 어울리는 자리를 찾았다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["But, in the absence of any such miraculous provision, my desire was to apply myself to some pursuit that would not lie too heavily upon her purse; and to do my duty in it, whatever it might be.", "그러나 그런 기적 같은 길이 마련되어 있지 않은 마당에, 내 바람이라곤 고모님의 지갑에 너무 무거운 짐을 지우지 않을 만한 일에 매달려, 그것이 무엇이든 제 몫을 다하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Mr. Dick had regularly assisted at our councils, with a meditative and sage demeanour.", "딕 씨도 사색에 잠긴 듯 점잖은 태도로 우리 회의에 꼬박꼬박 함께했다. 그는 딱 한 번 말고는 의견을 낸 적이 없었다."], ["He never made a suggestion but once; and on that occasion (I don’t know what put it in his head), he suddenly proposed that I should be ‘a Brazier’.", "그 한 번 (무엇이 그의 머릿속에 그런 생각을 집어넣었는지는 모르겠다), 그는 느닷없이 내가 '놋쇠장이'가 되어야 한다고 제안했다."], ["My aunt received this proposal so very ungraciously, that he never ventured on a second; but ever afterwards confined himself to looking watchfully at her for her suggestions, and rattling his money.", "고모님이 이 제안을 어찌나 쌀쌀맞게 받아넘겼던지, 그는 두 번 다시 입을 떼지 못했고, 그 후로는 줄곧 고모님의 의견을 기다리며 그녀를 조심스레 바라보다가, 주머니 속 동전을 짤랑거리는 일에만 매달렸다."]], [["‘Trot, I tell you what, my dear,’ said my aunt, one morning in the Christmas season when I left school: ‘as this knotty point is still unsettled, and as we must not make a mistake in our decision if we can help it, I think we had better take a little breathing-time.", "\"트롯, 내 얘기 좀 들어 봐라, 얘야.\" 내가 학교를 떠나던 크리스마스 무렵의 어느 아침, 고모님이 말했다. \"이 까다로운 문제가 아직도 풀리지 않았고, 또 우리가 결정에 실수를 하지 않으려면, 잠시 숨 좀 돌리는 게 낫겠다 싶구나."], ["In the meanwhile, you must try to look at it from a new point of view, and not as a schoolboy.’", "그동안 너는 이 일을 새로운 눈으로, 학생이 아닌 사람의 눈으로 바라보도록 애써 보거라.\""]], [["‘I will, aunt.’", "\"그럴게요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘It has occurred to me,’ pursued my aunt, ‘that a little change, and a glimpse of life out of doors, may be useful in helping you to know your own mind, and form a cooler judgement.", "\"문득 이런 생각이 들더구나.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"기분 전환을 좀 하고, 바깥세상을 잠깐 엿보는 게 네 마음을 알아차리고 더 차분한 판단을 내리는 데 도움이 될 거라고 말이야."], ["Suppose you were to go down into the old part of the country again, for instance, and see that--that out-of-the-way woman with the savagest of names,’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose, for she could never thoroughly forgive Peggotty for being so called.", "이를테면 네가 다시 그 옛 고장으로 내려가서, 그 — 그 야만스럽기 짝이 없는 이름을 가진 외딴 데 사는 여자나 한번 보고 오면 어떻겠니.\" 고모님은 이렇게 말하며 코를 문질렀다. 패거티가 그런 이름으로 불린다는 걸 끝내 온전히 용서하지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["‘Of all things in the world, aunt, I should like it best!’", "\"세상 무엇보다도, 고모님, 저는 그게 제일 좋아요!\""]], [["‘Well,’ said my aunt, ‘that’s lucky, for I should like it too.", "\"그래.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"다행이구나, 나도 그게 좋으니까."], ["But it’s natural and rational that you should like it.", "하지만 네가 그걸 좋아하는 건 자연스럽고 이치에 맞는 일이지."], ["And I am very well persuaded that whatever you do, Trot, will always be natural and rational.’", "그리고 나는 네가 무슨 일을 하든, 트롯, 늘 자연스럽고 이치에 맞게 하리라고 굳게 믿는다.\""]], [["‘I hope so, aunt.’", "\"그러길 바라요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘Your sister, Betsey Trotwood,’ said my aunt, ‘would have been as natural and rational a girl as ever breathed.", "\"네 누이동생, 베시 트롯우드는 말이다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 세상에 숨 쉬어 본 그 어떤 여자애 못지않게 자연스럽고 이치에 맞는 아이였을 게다."], ["You’ll be worthy of her, won’t you?’", "너는 그 애에게 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 될 거지, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘I hope I shall be worthy of YOU, aunt.", "\"저는 고모님께 부끄럽지 않은 사람이 되길 바라요."], ["That will be enough for me.’", "그것만으로도 저에겐 충분해요.\""]], [["‘It’s a mercy that poor dear baby of a mother of yours didn’t live,’ said my aunt, looking at me approvingly, ‘or she’d have been so vain of her boy by this time, that her soft little head would have been completely turned, if there was anything of it left to turn.’ (My aunt always excused any weakness of her own in my behalf, by transferring it in this way to my poor mother.) ‘Bless me, Trotwood, how you do remind me of her!’", "\"네 그 가엾고 어린애 같던 엄마가 오래 살지 못한 건 차라리 다행이었어.\" 고모님이 흐뭇하게 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"안 그랬으면 지금쯤 제 아들이 자랑스러워 어쩔 줄 모르다가, 그 여리고 작은 머리가 — 돌아갈 머리가 조금이라도 남아 있었다면 — 완전히 돌아 버렸을 테니까.\" (고모님은 늘 자신의 약한 구석을 이렇게 내 가엾은 어머니에게 떠넘기는 식으로 나를 위해 변명하곤 했다.) \"어머나 세상에, 트롯우드, 넌 어쩜 그렇게 그 애를 닮았니!\""]], [["‘Pleasantly, I hope, aunt?’ said I.", "\"기분 좋게 닮았길 바라요, 고모님?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘He’s as like her, Dick,’ said my aunt, emphatically, ‘he’s as like her, as she was that afternoon before she began to fret--bless my heart, he’s as like her, as he can look at me out of his two eyes!’", "\"이 애가 그 애를 얼마나 닮았는지, 딕.\" 고모님이 힘주어 말했다. \"이 애가 그 애를 닮은 게, 꼭 그 애가 속을 끓이기 시작하기 전 그날 오후 같다니까 — 어머나 세상에, 이 애가 그 두 눈으로 나를 바라보는 게 그 애를 쏙 빼닮았어!\""]], [["‘Is he indeed?’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"정말 그렇습니까?\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And he’s like David, too,’ said my aunt, decisively.", "\"게다가 데이비드도 닮았어.\" 고모님이 단정 짓듯 말했다."]], [["‘He is very like David!’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"데이비드를 정말 많이 닮았네요!\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘But what I want you to be, Trot,’ resumed my aunt, ‘--I don’t mean physically, but morally; you are very well physically--is, a firm fellow.", "\"하지만 내가 네게 바라는 건 말이다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 다시 입을 열었다. \"몸을 두고 하는 말이 아니라 마음을 두고 하는 말이야."], ["A fine firm fellow, with a will of your own.", "몸이야 아주 멀쩡하니까. 내가 바라는 건, 네가 단단한 사내가 되는 거야."], ["With resolution,’ said my aunt, shaking her cap at me, and clenching her hand.", "제 뜻을 지닌 멋지고 단단한 사내 말이다. 결단력을 갖춘,\" 고모님은 나를 향해 모자를 흔들고 주먹을 불끈 쥐며 말했다."], ["‘With determination.", "\"각오를 갖춘."], ["With character, Trot--with strength of character that is not to be influenced, except on good reason, by anybody, or by anything.", "줏대를 갖춘 사람, 트롯 — 그 누구에게도, 그 무엇에도, 정당한 이유가 아니고서는 흔들리지 않는 굳센 줏대를 갖춘 사람이 되는 거야."], ["That’s what I want you to be.", "그게 내가 네게 바라는 거다."], ["That’s what your father and mother might both have been, Heaven knows, and been the better for it.’", "그게 네 아버지도 네 어머니도 둘 다 될 수 있었던 모습이고, 하늘은 아시지, 그랬더라면 둘 다 훨씬 나았을 게다.\""]], [["I intimated that I hoped I should be what she described.", "나는 고모님이 그려 보인 그런 사람이 되길 바란다고 넌지시 일렀다."]], [["‘That you may begin, in a small way, to have a reliance upon yourself, and to act for yourself,’ said my aunt, ‘I shall send you upon your trip, alone.", "\"네가 작은 일에서부터 스스로를 믿고, 스스로 행동하기 시작하도록,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나는 너를 이번 여행에 혼자 보낼 작정이다."], ["I did think, once, of Mr. Dick’s going with you; but, on second thoughts, I shall keep him to take care of me.’", "한때는 딕 씨를 너와 함께 보낼까도 생각했지만, 다시 생각해 보니 그분은 나를 돌보도록 곁에 두어야겠구나.\""]], [["Mr. Dick, for a moment, looked a little disappointed; until the honour and dignity of having to take care of the most wonderful woman in the world, restored the sunshine to his face.", "딕 씨는 한순간 조금 실망한 듯 보였다. 그러나 세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인을 돌봐야 한다는 명예와 위엄이 그의 얼굴에 다시 햇살을 되돌려 놓았다."]], [["‘Besides,’ said my aunt, ‘there’s the Memorial--’", "\"게다가,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그 진정서도 있고—\""]], [["‘Oh, certainly,’ said Mr.", "\"오, 물론이지요.\""], ["Dick, in a hurry, ‘I intend, Trotwood, to get that done immediately--it really must be done immediately!", "딕 씨가 황급히 말했다. \"트롯우드, 나는 그걸 당장 끝낼 작정이야 — 정말 당장 끝내야 하거든!"], ["And then it will go in, you know--and then--’ said Mr.", "그러고 나면 그게 들어갈 거고, 알다시피 — 그러고 나면—\""], ["Dick, after checking himself, and pausing a long time, ‘there’ll be a pretty kettle of fish!’", "딕 씨는 말을 멈추고 한참을 뜸 들이더니 이렇게 말했다. \"한바탕 난리가 나겠지!\""]], [["In pursuance of my aunt’s kind scheme, I was shortly afterwards fitted out with a handsome purse of money, and a portmanteau, and tenderly dismissed upon my expedition.", "고모님의 다정한 계획에 따라, 나는 머지않아 두둑한 돈주머니와 여행 가방을 갖추고서, 다정한 배웅을 받으며 길을 떠났다."], ["At parting, my aunt gave me some good advice, and a good many kisses; and said that as her object was that I should look about me, and should think a little, she would recommend me to stay a few days in London, if I liked it, either on my way down into Suffolk, or in coming back.", "헤어질 때 고모님은 나에게 좋은 충고 몇 마디와 입맞춤을 잔뜩 해 주었다. 그러면서 자신의 목적은 내가 주위를 둘러보고 조금이나마 생각해 보는 데 있으니, 마음 내키면 서퍽으로 내려가는 길에든 돌아오는 길에든 런던에서 며칠 묵어도 좋다고 일러 주었다."], ["In a word, I was at liberty to do what I would, for three weeks or a month; and no other conditions were imposed upon my freedom than the before-mentioned thinking and looking about me, and a pledge to write three times a week and faithfully report myself.", "한마디로, 나는 세 주에서 한 달가량 내가 하고 싶은 대로 할 자유를 얻은 셈이었다. 그 자유에 붙은 조건이라곤 앞서 말한 대로 생각하고 주위를 둘러보는 것, 그리고 일주일에 세 번씩 편지를 써서 내 소식을 빠짐없이 전하겠다는 약속뿐이었다."]], [["I went to Canterbury first, that I might take leave of Agnes and Mr. Wickfield (my old room in whose house I had not yet relinquished), and also of the good Doctor.", "나는 먼저 캔터베리로 갔다. 애그니스와 위크필드 씨 (그 집에 있던 내 옛 방을 나는 아직 내놓지 않은 채였다)에게, 그리고 마음씨 고운 박사님에게도 작별을 고하기 위해서였다."], ["Agnes was very glad to see me, and told me that the house had not been like itself since I had left it.", "애그니스는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐하면서, 내가 떠난 뒤로 그 집이 예전 같지 않았다고 말했다."]], [["‘I am sure I am not like myself when I am away,’ said I.", "\"멀리 떨어져 있으면 저도 제가 아닌 것만 같아요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I seem to want my right hand, when I miss you.", "\"당신이 곁에 없으면 오른손을 잃은 것 같은 기분이에요."], ["Though that’s not saying much; for there’s no head in my right hand, and no heart.", "하긴 그건 별 대단한 말도 아니죠. 제 오른손에는 머리도 없고 마음도 없으니까요."], ["Everyone who knows you, consults with you, and is guided by you, Agnes.’", "당신을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 당신에게 의논하고, 당신을 길잡이로 삼잖아요, 애그니스.\""]], [["‘Everyone who knows me, spoils me, I believe,’ she answered, smiling.", "\"저를 아는 사람들은 다들 저를 응석받이로 만드는 것 같은데요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["‘No. It’s because you are like no one else.", "\"아니에요. 그건 당신이 다른 누구와도 같지 않기 때문이에요."], ["You are so good, and so sweet-tempered.", "당신은 그렇게 착하고, 마음씨가 곱잖아요."], ["You have such a gentle nature, and you are always right.’", "그렇게 부드러운 천성을 지녔고, 늘 옳고요.\""]], [["‘You talk,’ said Agnes, breaking into a pleasant laugh, as she sat at work, ‘as if I were the late Miss Larkins.’", "\"당신 말투를 들으니,\" 애그니스가 일감을 손에 쥔 채 즐겁게 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"꼭 제가 옛날의 라킨스 양이라도 된 것 같네요.\""]], [["‘Come!", "\"이런!"], ["It’s not fair to abuse my confidence,’ I answered, reddening at the recollection of my blue enslaver.", "제 속을 털어놓은 걸 그렇게 놀려먹는 건 너무하잖아요.\" 나는 그 파란 옷을 입은 나의 정복자를 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 대답했다."], ["‘But I shall confide in you, just the same, Agnes.", "\"그래도 저는 여전히 당신에게 속을 털어놓을 거예요, 애그니스."], ["I can never grow out of that.", "저는 그 버릇에서 절대 벗어나지 못할 거예요."], ["Whenever I fall into trouble, or fall in love, I shall always tell you, if you’ll let me--even when I come to fall in love in earnest.’", "곤경에 빠질 때나 사랑에 빠질 때나, 당신이 허락만 해 준다면 저는 늘 당신에게 말할 거예요 — 언젠가 정말 진지하게 사랑에 빠지게 되더라도요.\""]], [["‘Why, you have always been in earnest!’ said Agnes, laughing again.", "\"어머, 당신은 늘 진지했잖아요!\" 애그니스가 다시 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["that was as a child, or a schoolboy,’ said I, laughing in my turn, not without being a little shame-faced.", "그건 어린애였을 때, 아니면 학생이었을 때 얘기죠.\" 나도 따라 웃으며, 조금 멋쩍은 기색을 감추지 못한 채 말했다."], ["‘Times are altering now, and I suppose I shall be in a terrible state of earnestness one day or other.", "\"이제 시절이 달라지고 있으니, 언젠가는 저도 무섭도록 진지한 상태에 빠지겠죠."], ["My wonder is, that you are not in earnest yourself, by this time, Agnes.’", "정작 제가 궁금한 건, 애그니스, 어째서 당신은 이때껏 진지해지지 않았느냐는 거예요.\""]], [["Agnes laughed again, and shook her head.", "애그니스는 또 한 번 웃으며 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘Oh, I know you are not!’ said I, ‘because if you had been you would have told me.", "\"오, 저는 당신이 그렇지 않다는 걸 알아요!\" 내가 말했다. \"왜냐하면 만일 그랬다면 당신은 저에게 말했을 테니까요."], ["Or at least’--for I saw a faint blush in her face, ‘you would have let me find it out for myself.", "아니면 적어도,\" — 그녀의 얼굴에 어렴풋이 홍조가 스치는 것을 보았기에 — \"제가 스스로 알아차리게 내버려 두었을 테니까요."], ["But there is no one that I know of, who deserves to love you, Agnes.", "하지만 제가 아는 한, 당신의 사랑을 받을 자격이 있는 사람은 아무도 없어요, 애그니스."], ["Someone of a nobler character, and more worthy altogether than anyone I have ever seen here, must rise up, before I give my consent.", "제가 여기서 본 그 누구보다도 더 고귀한 인품을 지니고, 모든 면에서 더 자격 있는 누군가가 나타나야만 저도 허락을 할 거예요."], ["In the time to come, I shall have a wary eye on all admirers; and shall exact a great deal from the successful one, I assure you.’", "앞으로 저는 당신을 흠모하는 자들을 모두 빈틈없이 지켜볼 거고, 그 행운의 주인공에게는 아주 많은 걸 요구할 거예요, 정말이에요.\""]], [["We had gone on, so far, in a mixture of confidential jest and earnest, that had long grown naturally out of our familiar relations, begun as mere children.", "여기까지 우리는, 그저 어린애로 시작해 오랫동안 자연스레 자라난 정다운 사이에서 우러나온, 속내를 터놓는 농담과 진심이 뒤섞인 말을 주고받았다."], ["But Agnes, now suddenly lifting up her eyes to mine, and speaking in a different manner, said:", "그런데 애그니스가 갑자기 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보더니, 사뭇 다른 투로 말했다."]], [["‘Trotwood, there is something that I want to ask you, and that I may not have another opportunity of asking for a long time, perhaps--something I would ask, I think, of no one else.", "\"트롯우드, 당신에게 묻고 싶은 게 하나 있는데, 어쩌면 앞으로 오랫동안 다시 물을 기회가 없을지도 몰라요 — 제 생각엔 다른 누구에게도 묻지 못할 그런 거예요."], ["Have you observed any gradual alteration in Papa?’", "아버지에게서 조금씩 일어나는 어떤 변화를 눈치챈 적 있나요?\""]], [["I had observed it, and had often wondered whether she had too.", "나는 그걸 눈치채고 있었고, 그녀도 그런지 종종 궁금했던 터였다."], ["I must have shown as much, now, in my face; for her eyes were in a moment cast down, and I saw tears in them.", "지금 내 얼굴에 그 마음이 그대로 드러났던 게 분명하다. 그녀의 눈이 이내 아래로 떨궈졌고, 나는 그 눈에 고인 눈물을 보았다."]], [["‘Tell me what it is,’ she said, in a low voice.", "\"그게 무엇인지 말해 줘요.\" 그녀가 나직한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I think--shall I be quite plain, Agnes, liking him so much?’", "\"제 생각엔 — 솔직히 다 털어놔도 될까요, 애그니스, 그분을 이토록 좋아하는 제가?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ she said.", "\"네.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["‘I think he does himself no good by the habit that has increased upon him since I first came here. He is often very nervous--or I fancy so.’", "\"제 생각엔, 그분이 제가 여기 처음 온 뒤로 점점 심해진 그 버릇 때문에 스스로를 해치고 계신 것 같아요. 그분은 자주 몹시 신경이 곤두서 있어요 — 아니면 제 착각일까요.\""]], [["‘It is not fancy,’ said Agnes, shaking her head.", "\"착각이 아니에요.\" 애그니스가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["‘His hand trembles, his speech is not plain, and his eyes look wild.", "\"그분은 손을 떨고, 말이 또렷하지 않고, 눈빛이 사나워져요."], ["I have remarked that at those times, and when he is least like himself, he is most certain to be wanted on some business.’", "제가 눈여겨봤는데, 바로 그런 때, 그러니까 그분이 가장 평소답지 않을 때, 어김없이 무슨 일 때문에 그분을 찾는 사람이 있더군요.\""]], [["‘By Uriah,’ said Agnes.", "\"유라이아가요.\" 애그니스가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes; and the sense of being unfit for it, or of not having understood it, or of having shown his condition in spite of himself, seems to make him so uneasy, that next day he is worse, and next day worse, and so he becomes jaded and haggard.", "\"네. 그래서 그 일을 감당할 수 없다는 느낌, 혹은 그 일을 제대로 이해하지 못했다는 느낌, 혹은 자기도 모르게 자기 상태를 드러내고 말았다는 느낌이 그분을 어찌나 불안하게 만드는지, 이튿날이면 더 나빠지고, 그다음 날이면 또 더 나빠져서, 결국 그분은 기진맥진하고 초췌해지는 거예요."], ["Do not be alarmed by what I say, Agnes, but in this state I saw him, only the other evening, lay down his head upon his desk, and shed tears like a child.’", "제 말에 놀라지 말아요, 애그니스, 하지만 바로 며칠 전 저녁에도 그런 상태에 빠진 그분이 책상에 머리를 묻고 어린애처럼 눈물을 흘리는 걸 봤어요.\""]], [["Her hand passed softly before my lips while I was yet speaking, and in a moment she had met her father at the door of the room, and was hanging on his shoulder.", "내가 아직 말을 다 끝내기도 전에 그녀의 손이 부드럽게 내 입술 앞을 스쳐 지나갔고, 다음 순간 그녀는 방문께에서 아버지를 맞이하며 그의 어깨에 매달려 있었다. 두 사람이 함께 나를 바라볼 때 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을, 나는 무척 가슴 뭉클하게 느꼈다."], ["The expression of her face, as they both looked towards me, I felt to be very touching.", "그 아름다운 눈빛에는 아버지를 향한 그토록 깊은 사랑과, 그분의 온갖 애정과 보살핌에 대한 고마움이 담겨 있었다."], ["There was such deep fondness for him, and gratitude to him for all his love and care, in her beautiful look; and there was such a fervent appeal to me to deal tenderly by him, even in my inmost thoughts, and to let no harsh construction find any place against him; she was, at once, so proud of him and devoted to him, yet so compassionate and sorry, and so reliant upon me to be so, too; that nothing she could have said would have expressed more to me, or moved me more.", "또한 거기에는, 내 마음속 가장 깊은 생각에서까지 부디 아버지를 다정하게 대해 주고, 그분에게 어떤 모진 해석도 들이대지 말아 달라는 간절한 호소가 담겨 있었다. 그녀는 그 순간 아버지를 그토록 자랑스러워하면서도 헌신적이었고, 그러면서도 그토록 안쓰러워하고 애달파했으며, 또 나 역시 그러하리라 그토록 믿고 의지하고 있었다. 그러니 그녀가 어떤 말을 했더라도 그 눈빛보다 더 많은 것을 나에게 전하거나, 나를 더 깊이 움직이지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["We were to drink tea at the Doctor’s.", "우리는 박사님 댁에서 차를 마시기로 되어 있었다."], ["We went there at the usual hour; and round the study fireside found the Doctor, and his young wife, and her mother.", "우리는 여느 때처럼 그 시각에 그리로 갔고, 서재 난롯가에 둘러앉은 박사님과 젊은 부인, 그리고 그녀의 어머니를 만났다."], ["The Doctor, who made as much of my going away as if I were going to China, received me as an honoured guest; and called for a log of wood to be thrown on the fire, that he might see the face of his old pupil reddening in the blaze.", "박사님은 내가 마치 중국에라도 가는 양 내 떠남을 크게 떠들썩하게 여기며, 나를 귀한 손님으로 맞이했다. 그러고는 옛 제자의 얼굴이 불길에 발갛게 물드는 것을 보고 싶다며, 난로에 장작 한 토막을 더 넣으라고 일렀다."]], [["‘I shall not see many more new faces in Trotwood’s stead, Wickfield,’ said the Doctor, warming his hands; ‘I am getting lazy, and want ease.", "\"트롯우드를 대신할 새 얼굴을 이제 많이는 못 볼 걸세, 위크필드.\" 박사님이 두 손을 녹이며 말했다. \"나도 점점 게을러지고, 편한 게 좋아지는구먼."], ["I shall relinquish all my young people in another six months, and lead a quieter life.’", "앞으로 여섯 달만 지나면 내 젊은 제자들을 다 내려놓고 더 조용한 삶을 살 작정이야.\""]], [["‘You have said so, any time these ten years, Doctor,’ Mr. Wickfield answered.", "\"십 년째 그 말씀만 하고 계십니다, 박사님.\" 위크필드 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘But now I mean to do it,’ returned the Doctor.", "\"하지만 이번엔 정말 그럴 작정이야.\" 박사님이 되받았다."], ["‘My first master will succeed me--I am in earnest at last--so you’ll soon have to arrange our contracts, and to bind us firmly to them, like a couple of knaves.’", "\"내 수석 교사가 내 자리를 물려받을 걸세 — 드디어 진심이라네 — 그러니 자네도 곧 우리 계약을 마련해서, 우리 두 사람을 한 쌍의 악당처럼 단단히 묶어 두어야 할 거야.\""]], [["‘And to take care,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 잘 살피셔야지요.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield, ‘that you’re not imposed on, eh?", "\"박사님이 손해 보지 않도록 말입니다, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["As you certainly would be, in any contract you should make for yourself. Well!", "박사님 혼자 계약을 맺으셨다간 틀림없이 손해를 보실 테니까요. 좋습니다!"], ["I am ready. There are worse tasks than that, in my calling.’", "저는 준비됐습니다. 제 직업에서 그보다 고약한 일도 많으니까요.\""]], [["‘I shall have nothing to think of then,’ said the Doctor, with a smile, ‘but my Dictionary; and this other contract-bargain--Annie.’", "\"그러면 나는 그때 가서 생각할 거라곤 아무것도 없겠군.\" 박사님이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내 사전, 그리고 이 또 다른 계약 거래 — 애니뿐일 테니.\""]], [["As Mr. Wickfield glanced towards her, sitting at the tea table by Agnes, she seemed to me to avoid his look with such unwonted hesitation and timidity, that his attention became fixed upon her, as if something were suggested to his thoughts.", "위크필드 씨가 애그니스 곁에서 찻상에 앉아 있는 애니 쪽으로 눈길을 던지자, 그녀는 평소답지 않은 머뭇거림과 수줍음으로 그의 시선을 피하는 듯했다. 그러자 그의 관심이 그녀에게 못 박히듯 쏠렸는데, 마치 무언가가 그의 생각을 건드린 것만 같았다."]], [["‘There is a post come in from India, I observe,’ he said, after a short silence.", "\"인도에서 우편물이 한 통 온 모양이더군요.\" 그가 잠시 침묵하다가 말했다."]], [["‘By the by!", "\"참, 그렇지!"], ["and letters from Mr. Jack Maldon!’ said the Doctor.", "잭 맬든 씨에게서 온 편지들이라네!\" 박사님이 말했다."]], [["‘Indeed!’ ‘Poor dear Jack!’ said Mrs.", "\"그렇습니까!\" \"가엾은 우리 잭!\""], ["Markleham, shaking her head.", "마클럼 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘That trying climate!--like living, they tell me, on a sand-heap, underneath a burning-glass!", "\"그 견디기 힘든 기후라니! — 듣자 하니, 활활 타는 볼록 렌즈 밑에서 모래더미 위에 사는 거나 매한가지라더군요!"], ["He looked strong, but he wasn’t.", "그 애가 튼튼해 보여도 실은 그렇지 못했어요."], ["My dear Doctor, it was his spirit, not his constitution, that he ventured on so boldly.", "사랑하는 박사님, 그 애가 그토록 대담하게 나선 건 그 애의 정신이지 체질이 아니었어요."], ["Annie, my dear, I am sure you must perfectly recollect that your cousin never was strong--not what can be called ROBUST, you know,’ said Mrs.", "애니야, 사랑하는 내 딸아, 너도 네 사촌이 한 번도 튼튼한 적이 없었다는 걸 — 흔히 말하는 그 강건한 축에는 끼지 못했다는 걸 똑똑히 기억할 거야.\""], ["Markleham, with emphasis, and looking round upon us generally, ‘--from the time when my daughter and himself were children together, and walking about, arm-in-arm, the livelong day.’", "마클럼 부인은 우리 모두를 둘러보며 힘주어 말했다. \"내 딸과 그 애가 어린 시절을 함께 보내며 온종일 팔짱을 끼고 쏘다니던 그때부터 말이지.\""]], [["Annie, thus addressed, made no reply.", "이렇게 지목당한 애니는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Do I gather from what you say, ma’am, that Mr.", "\"부인 말씀을 들으니, 맬든 씨가 편찮으신 모양이군요?\""], ["Maldon is ill?’ asked Mr. Wickfield.", "위크필드 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Ill!’ replied the Old Soldier.", "\"편찮다고요!\" 노병이 대답했다."], ["‘My dear sir, he’s all sorts of things.’", "\"이보세요, 선생, 그 애는 별의별 병을 다 앓고 있답니다.\""]], [["‘Except well?’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"건강한 것만 빼고요?\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Except well, indeed!’ said the Old Soldier.", "\"정말이지, 건강한 것만 빼고요!\" 노병이 말했다."], ["‘He has had dreadful strokes of the sun, no doubt, and jungle fevers and agues, and every kind of thing you can mention.", "\"틀림없이 무시무시한 일사병을 여러 번 앓았을 테고, 정글 열병에 학질에, 그 밖에 입에 올릴 수 있는 온갖 병을 다 앓았을 거예요."], ["As to his liver,’ said the Old Soldier resignedly, ‘that, of course, he gave up altogether, when he first went out!’", "간으로 말하자면,\" 노병이 체념한 듯 말했다. \"그야 물론, 그곳에 처음 발을 디뎠을 때 이미 완전히 손을 놓아 버렸지요!\""]], [["‘Does he say all this?’ asked Mr. Wickfield.", "\"그가 이 모든 걸 직접 그렇게 말하던가요?\" 위크필드 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Say? My dear sir,’ returned Mrs.", "\"말하느냐고요? 이보세요, 선생.\""], ["Markleham, shaking her head and her fan, ‘you little know my poor Jack Maldon when you ask that question. Say?", "마클럼 부인이 고개와 부채를 함께 내저으며 되받았다. \"그런 질문을 하시는 걸 보니 우리 가엾은 잭 맬든을 도통 모르시는군요. 말하느냐고요? 천만에요."], ["Not he. You might drag him at the heels of four wild horses first.’", "야생마 네 마리 발꿈치에 매달아 끌고 다녀도 그 애 입에서는 그런 말 안 나올 거예요.\""]], [["‘Mama!’ said Mrs. Strong.", "\"엄마!\" 스트롱 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Annie, my dear,’ returned her mother, ‘once for all, I must really beg that you will not interfere with me, unless it is to confirm what I say.", "\"애니야, 사랑하는 딸아.\" 그녀의 어머니가 되받았다. \"내 진심으로 부탁하는데, 이번 한 번만큼은 내가 하는 말을 거들 게 아니라면 제발 끼어들지 말아 다오."], ["You know as well as I do that your cousin Maldon would be dragged at the heels of any number of wild horses--why should I confine myself to four!", "너도 나만큼이나 잘 알지 않니, 네 사촌 맬든이라면 야생마가 몇 마리든 그 발꿈치에 매달려 끌려다닐 사람이라는 걸 — 그러니 내가 왜 굳이 네 마리로만 한정하겠니!"], ["I WON’T confine myself to four--eight, sixteen, two-and-thirty, rather than say anything calculated to overturn the Doctor’s plans.’", "나는 네 마리로 한정 안 할 거야 — 여덟 마리든, 열여섯 마리든, 서른두 마리든, 박사님의 계획을 뒤엎을 만한 말은 한마디도 하느니 차라리 그게 낫지.\""]], [["‘Wickfield’s plans,’ said the Doctor, stroking his face, and looking penitently at his adviser.", "\"위크필드의 계획이지요.\" 박사님이 얼굴을 쓸어내리며, 자신의 조언자를 미안한 듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["‘That is to say, our joint plans for him.", "\"그러니까, 그 애를 위한 우리 둘의 공동 계획 말입니다."], ["I said myself, abroad or at home.’", "나야 본디 외국이든 본국이든 상관없다고 했으니까요.\""]], [["‘And I said’ added Mr.", "\"그리고 저는,\""], ["Wickfield gravely, ‘abroad.", "위크필드 씨가 무겁게 덧붙였다."], ["I was the means of sending him abroad.", "\"외국이라고 했지요. 그를 외국으로 보낸 건 저였습니다."], ["It’s my responsibility.’", "그건 제 책임입니다.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Responsibility!’ said the Old Soldier.", "책임이라니요!\" 노병이 말했다."], ["‘Everything was done for the best, my dear Mr.", "\"모든 게 다 잘되라고 한 일이었어요, 사랑하는 위크필드 씨."], ["Wickfield; everything was done for the kindest and best, we know.", "모든 게 더없이 친절하고 좋은 뜻에서 한 일이었다는 걸 우리 다 알잖아요."], ["But if the dear fellow can’t live there, he can’t live there.", "하지만 그 가여운 애가 거기서 못 산다면, 못 사는 거예요."], ["And if he can’t live there, he’ll die there, sooner than he’ll overturn the Doctor’s plans.", "그리고 거기서 못 산다면, 박사님의 계획을 뒤엎느니 차라리 거기서 죽고 말 거예요."], ["I know him,’ said the Old Soldier, fanning herself, in a sort of calm prophetic agony, ‘and I know he’ll die there, sooner than he’ll overturn the Doctor’s plans.’", "나는 그 애를 잘 알아요.\" 노병은 일종의 차분한 예언자적 비탄에 잠겨 부채질을 하며 말했다. \"그래서 알아요, 그 애는 박사님의 계획을 뒤엎느니 차라리 거기서 죽을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Well, well, ma’am,’ said the Doctor cheerfully, ‘I am not bigoted to my plans, and I can overturn them myself.", "\"자, 자, 부인.\" 박사님이 쾌활하게 말했다. \"나는 내 계획에 그리 집착하지 않으니, 내 손으로 얼마든지 뒤엎을 수 있어요."], ["I can substitute some other plans.", "다른 계획으로 바꾸면 그만이지요."], ["If Mr. Jack Maldon comes home on account of ill health, he must not be allowed to go back, and we must endeavour to make some more suitable and fortunate provision for him in this country.’", "만일 잭 맬든 씨가 건강 때문에 본국으로 돌아온다면, 다시 돌려보내선 안 되고, 우리는 이 나라에서 그에게 더 알맞고 복된 자리를 마련해 주도록 애써야 할 겁니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Markleham was so overcome by this generous speech--which, I need not say, she had not at all expected or led up to--that she could only tell the Doctor it was like himself, and go several times through that operation of kissing the sticks of her fan, and then tapping his hand with it.", "마클럼 부인은 이 너그러운 말에 — 두말할 것 없이, 그녀가 전혀 기대하지도 유도하지도 않은 말이었기에 — 어찌나 감격했던지, 그저 박사님답다고 말할 수밖에 없었다. 그러고는 부챗살에 입을 맞추었다가 그것으로 그의 손을 톡톡 두드리는 동작을 여러 차례 되풀이했다."], ["After which she gently chid her daughter Annie, for not being more demonstrative when such kindnesses were showered, for her sake, on her old playfellow; and entertained us with some particulars concerning other deserving members of her family, whom it was desirable to set on their deserving legs.", "그런 다음 자기 딸 애니를 가만히 나무랐는데, 자기를 봐서 옛 소꿉친구에게 이런 친절이 쏟아지는데도 더 살갑게 굴지 않는다는 이유에서였다. 그러더니 자기 집안에서 마땅히 도움을 받아 제 발로 일어서게 해 줄 만한 다른 갸륵한 식구들에 관한 이런저런 이야기로 우리를 즐겁게 해 주었다."]], [["All this time, her daughter Annie never once spoke, or lifted up her eyes.", "이러는 내내, 그녀의 딸 애니는 단 한 번도 입을 떼거나 눈을 들지 않았다."], ["All this time, Mr. Wickfield had his glance upon her as she sat by his own daughter’s side.", "이러는 내내, 위크필드 씨는 제 딸 곁에 앉은 그녀에게서 눈길을 떼지 않았다."], ["It appeared to me that he never thought of being observed by anyone; but was so intent upon her, and upon his own thoughts in connexion with her, as to be quite absorbed.", "내가 보기에 그는 누군가 자기를 지켜본다는 생각은 조금도 하지 못한 채, 그녀에게, 그리고 그녀와 얽힌 자신의 생각에 어찌나 골몰했던지 완전히 빠져든 듯했다."], ["He now asked what Mr. Jack Maldon had actually written in reference to himself, and to whom he had written?", "이제 그는 잭 맬든 씨가 자기 자신에 관해 실제로 무어라 썼는지, 또 누구에게 썼는지 물었다."]], [["‘Why, here,’ said Mrs.", "\"아니, 여기 있잖아요.\""], ["Markleham, taking a letter from the chimney-piece above the Doctor’s head, ‘the dear fellow says to the Doctor himself--where is it?", "마클럼 부인이 박사님 머리 위 벽난로 선반에서 편지 한 통을 집어 들며 말했다. \"그 가여운 애가 박사님께 직접 이렇게 썼어요 — 어디 보자? 오!"], ["Oh!--“I am sorry to inform you that my health is suffering severely, and that I fear I may be reduced to the necessity of returning home for a time, as the only hope of restoration.”", "'제 건강이 심하게 나빠지고 있음을 알려 드리게 되어 송구합니다. 회복할 유일한 희망으로, 한동안 본국으로 돌아가야 할 처지에 몰리지 않을까 두렵습니다.'"], ["That’s pretty plain, poor fellow!", "가여운 것, 이건 꽤 분명하죠!"], ["His only hope of restoration!", "회복할 유일한 희망이라니!"], ["But Annie’s letter is plainer still.", "그런데 애니에게 보낸 편지는 더더욱 분명해요."], ["Annie, show me that letter again.’", "애니야, 그 편지를 다시 보여 다오.\""]], [["‘Not now, mama,’ she pleaded in a low tone.", "\"지금은 안 돼요, 엄마.\" 그녀가 나직한 목소리로 사정했다."]], [["‘My dear, you absolutely are, on some subjects, one of the most ridiculous persons in the world,’ returned her mother, ‘and perhaps the most unnatural to the claims of your own family.", "\"얘야, 너는 정말이지 어떤 일에 있어선 세상에서 가장 우스꽝스러운 사람 중 하나야.\" 그녀의 어머니가 되받았다. \"그리고 어쩌면 제 집안의 도리에 가장 매정한 사람일지도 모르고."], ["We never should have heard of the letter at all, I believe, unless I had asked for it myself.", "내가 직접 달라고 하지 않았더라면, 그 편지가 있다는 것조차 우리는 끝내 듣지도 못했을 게다."], ["Do you call that confidence, my love, towards Doctor Strong?", "얘야, 그걸 스트롱 박사님께 대한 신뢰라고 부를 수 있겠니?"], ["I am surprised.", "어이가 없구나."], ["You ought to know better.’", "너는 더 잘 알아야 할 텐데.\""]], [["The letter was reluctantly produced; and as I handed it to the old lady, I saw how the unwilling hand from which I took it, trembled.", "편지는 마지못해 꺼내졌다. 그리고 내가 그것을 노부인에게 건넬 때, 내가 받아 든 그 내키지 않는 손이 어떻게 떨리는지를 나는 보았다."]], [["‘Now let us see,’ said Mrs.", "\"자 어디 보자.\""], ["Markleham, putting her glass to her eye, ‘where the passage is.", "마클럼 부인이 안경을 눈에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"그 대목이 어디 있더라."], ["“The remembrance of old times, my dearest Annie”--and so forth--it’s not there.", "'지난날의 추억이, 더없이 사랑하는 애니'— 어쩌고저쩌고 — 거긴 아니고."], ["“The amiable old Proctor”--who’s he?", "'인자하신 늙은 대리인'— 이 사람이 누구지?"], ["Dear me, Annie, how illegibly your cousin Maldon writes, and how stupid I am!", "어머나, 애니야, 네 사촌 맬든은 어쩜 글씨를 이렇게 알아보기 힘들게 쓰니, 그리고 나는 또 왜 이리 멍청하니!"], ["“Doctor,” of course. Ah!", "물론 '박사님'이지. 아!"], ["amiable indeed!’ Here she left off, to kiss her fan again, and shake it at the Doctor, who was looking at us in a state of placid satisfaction.", "정말이지 인자하시고말고!\" 여기서 그녀는 말을 멈추고 다시 부채에 입을 맞춘 다음, 잔잔한 만족감에 잠겨 우리를 바라보고 있던 박사님을 향해 부채를 흔들었다."], ["‘Now I have found it.", "\"자, 이제 찾았다."], ["“You may not be surprised to hear, Annie,”--no, to be sure, knowing that he never was really strong; what did I say just now?--“that I have undergone so much in this distant place, as to have decided to leave it at all hazards; on sick leave, if I can; on total resignation, if that is not to be obtained.", "'애니, 자네는 이 소식을 듣고도 놀라지 않을지 모르겠네'— 그래, 그렇고말고, 그 애가 정말 튼튼한 적이 없었다는 걸 알았으니까. 내가 방금 뭐라고 했더라? — '내가 이 머나먼 곳에서 어찌나 많은 걸 견뎌 왔는지, 이제 무슨 수를 써서라도 이곳을 떠나기로 마음먹었다네."], ["What I have endured, and do endure here, is insupportable.”", "가능하면 병가로, 그게 안 된다면 아예 사직으로라도."], ["And but for the promptitude of that best of creatures,’ said Mrs.", "내가 이곳에서 견뎌 왔고 또 견디고 있는 것은 도저히 참을 수 없는 것이라네.'"], ["Markleham, telegraphing the Doctor as before, and refolding the letter, ‘it would be insupportable to me to think of.’", "그리고 그 더없이 훌륭한 분의 발 빠른 처사가 아니었다면,\" 마클럼 부인이 아까처럼 박사님에게 신호를 보내며 편지를 다시 접고 말했다. \"생각만 해도 저로선 도저히 참을 수 없는 일이었을 거예요.\""]], [["Mr. Wickfield said not one word, though the old lady looked to him as if for his commentary on this intelligence; but sat severely silent, with his eyes fixed on the ground.", "위크필드 씨는, 비록 노부인이 이 소식에 대한 그의 논평이라도 기다리는 양 그를 바라보았지만, 단 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그저 눈을 땅에 박은 채 굳은 듯 침묵하고 앉아 있었다."], ["Long after the subject was dismissed, and other topics occupied us, he remained so; seldom raising his eyes, unless to rest them for a moment, with a thoughtful frown, upon the Doctor, or his wife, or both.", "그 화제가 끝나고 다른 이야깃거리로 자리가 채워진 한참 뒤까지도 그는 그대로였다. 이따금 생각에 잠긴 채 눈살을 찌푸리며, 박사님이나 그의 부인, 혹은 두 사람 모두에게 잠깐 눈길을 머무는 것 말고는, 좀처럼 눈을 들지 않았다."]], [["The Doctor was very fond of music.", "박사님은 음악을 무척 좋아했다."], ["Agnes sang with great sweetness and expression, and so did Mrs. Strong.", "애그니스는 더없이 곱고 풍부한 감정을 담아 노래했고, 스트롱 부인도 그러했다."], ["They sang together, and played duets together, and we had quite a little concert.", "두 사람은 함께 노래하고 함께 이중주를 연주했으며, 우리는 자그마한 음악회를 연 셈이었다."], ["But I remarked two things: first, that though Annie soon recovered her composure, and was quite herself, there was a blank between her and Mr. Wickfield which separated them wholly from each other; secondly, that Mr.", "그러나 나는 두 가지를 눈여겨봤다. 첫째, 애니가 이내 평정을 되찾아 완전히 제 모습으로 돌아왔는데도, 그녀와 위크필드 씨 사이에는 둘을 완전히 갈라놓는 어떤 빈틈이 가로놓여 있다는 것이었다."], ["Wickfield seemed to dislike the intimacy between her and Agnes, and to watch it with uneasiness.", "둘째, 위크필드 씨가 그녀와 애그니스 사이의 친밀함을 못마땅해하면서, 불안한 눈으로 그것을 지켜보는 듯하다는 것이었다."], ["And now, I must confess, the recollection of what I had seen on that night when Mr. Maldon went away, first began to return upon me with a meaning it had never had, and to trouble me.", "그리고 이제, 고백하건대, 맬든 씨가 떠나던 그날 밤 내가 본 광경에 대한 기억이, 그때껏 한 번도 지니지 못했던 의미를 품은 채 처음으로 내게 되살아나 나를 괴롭히기 시작했다."], ["The innocent beauty of her face was not as innocent to me as it had been; I mistrusted the natural grace and charm of her manner; and when I looked at Agnes by her side, and thought how good and true Agnes was, suspicions arose within me that it was an ill-assorted friendship.", "그녀 얼굴의 순결한 아름다움은 예전만큼 순결하게 다가오지 않았다. 나는 그녀의 자연스러운 우아함과 매력을 의심했고, 그녀 곁에 앉은 애그니스를 바라보며 애그니스가 얼마나 착하고 참된지를 생각하노라니, 이것이 어울리지 않는 우정이 아닐까 하는 의심이 마음속에 솟아올랐다."]], [["She was so happy in it herself, however, and the other was so happy too, that they made the evening fly away as if it were but an hour.", "그러나 정작 그녀 자신은 그 우정 속에서 그토록 행복했고, 다른 한 사람도 그토록 행복했기에, 두 사람은 그날 저녁을 마치 한 시간처럼 훌쩍 흘려보냈다."], ["It closed in an incident which I well remember.", "그날 저녁은 내가 또렷이 기억하는 한 가지 사건으로 막을 내렸다."], ["They were taking leave of each other, and Agnes was going to embrace her and kiss her, when Mr. Wickfield stepped between them, as if by accident, and drew Agnes quickly away.", "두 사람이 서로 작별을 고하고, 애그니스가 그녀를 끌어안고 입을 맞추려는 참이었는데, 위크필드 씨가 마치 우연인 듯 둘 사이로 끼어들더니 애그니스를 재빨리 끌어당겼다."], ["Then I saw, as though all the intervening time had been cancelled, and I were still standing in the doorway on the night of the departure, the expression of that night in the face of Mrs. Strong, as it confronted his.", "그때 나는, 마치 그사이의 모든 시간이 지워지고 내가 아직도 그 떠나던 밤의 문간에 서 있기라도 한 듯이, 그 밤에 보았던 그 표정이 스트롱 부인의 얼굴에, 그의 얼굴을 마주한 그 얼굴에 떠오르는 것을 보았다."]], [["I cannot say what an impression this made upon me, or how impossible I found it, when I thought of her afterwards, to separate her from this look, and remember her face in its innocent loveliness again. It haunted me when I got home.", "이것이 내게 얼마나 깊은 인상을 남겼는지, 또 나중에 그녀를 떠올릴 때 그녀를 이 표정에서 떼어 내어 그 순결하고 사랑스러운 얼굴로 다시 기억하기가 얼마나 불가능했는지, 나는 말로 다 할 수가 없다. 집에 돌아온 뒤에도 그 표정이 나를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다."], ["I seemed to have left the Doctor’s roof with a dark cloud lowering on it.", "나는 어두운 먹구름이 낮게 드리운 박사님 댁 지붕을 뒤로하고 떠나온 것만 같았다."], ["The reverence that I had for his grey head, was mingled with commiseration for his faith in those who were treacherous to him, and with resentment against those who injured him.", "그분의 희끗한 머리에 대해 내가 품었던 경외심에는, 자신을 배신하는 자들을 믿는 그 모습에 대한 가여움과, 그분을 해치는 자들에 대한 분노가 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["The impending shadow of a great affliction, and a great disgrace that had no distinct form in it yet, fell like a stain upon the quiet place where I had worked and played as a boy, and did it a cruel wrong.", "닥쳐오는 크나큰 고통의 그림자, 그리고 아직 뚜렷한 형체조차 갖추지 못한 크나큰 치욕의 그림자가, 내가 소년 시절에 일하고 뛰놀던 그 고요한 곳에 얼룩처럼 내려앉아 그곳에 모진 상처를 입혔다."], ["I had no pleasure in thinking, any more, of the grave old broad-leaved aloe-trees, which remained shut up in themselves a hundred years together, and of the trim smooth grass-plot, and the stone urns, and the Doctor’s walk, and the congenial sound of the Cathedral bell hovering above them all.", "백 년을 줄곧 제 안에 틀어박혀 있던 그 근엄하고 잎 넓은 늙은 알로에 나무들도, 단정하게 손질된 매끈한 잔디밭도, 돌 항아리들도, 박사님의 산책로도, 그 모든 것 위를 떠도는 대성당 종소리의 정겨운 울림도, 더는 떠올려도 즐겁지 않았다."], ["It was as if the tranquil sanctuary of my boyhood had been sacked before my face, and its peace and honour given to the winds.", "그것은 마치 내 소년 시절의 고요한 성소가 내 눈앞에서 약탈당하고, 그 평화와 명예가 바람결에 흩어져 버린 것만 같았다."]], [["But morning brought with it my parting from the old house, which Agnes had filled with her influence; and that occupied my mind sufficiently.", "그러나 아침이 밝자 애그니스의 손길이 구석구석 배어 있는 그 오래된 집과 작별할 일이 다가왔고, 그것만으로도 내 마음은 충분히 채워졌다."], ["I should be there again soon, no doubt; I might sleep again--perhaps often--in my old room; but the days of my inhabiting there were gone, and the old time was past.", "머지않아 다시 그곳에 올 것이 분명했고, 어쩌면 내 옛 방에서 다시, 어쩌면 자주 잘 수 있을 터였다. 하지만 그곳에 살던 날들은 지나갔고, 그 옛 시절은 끝나 버린 것이었다."], ["I was heavier at heart when I packed up such of my books and clothes as still remained there to be sent to Dover, than I cared to show to Uriah Heep; who was so officious to help me, that I uncharitably thought him mighty glad that I was going.", "도버로 부치려고 그곳에 아직 남아 있던 책과 옷가지를 챙길 때, 내 마음은 유라이아 힙에게 차마 드러내고 싶지 않을 만큼 무거웠다. 그는 나를 어찌나 부산스레 도왔던지, 나는 박정하게도 그가 내 떠남을 엄청나게 반기는 게 아닐까 하고 생각했다."]], [["I got away from Agnes and her father, somehow, with an indifferent show of being very manly, and took my seat upon the box of the London coach.", "나는 어찌어찌, 자못 사내다운 척 무심한 태도를 꾸며 가며 애그니스와 그 아버지에게서 빠져나와, 런던행 마차의 마부석에 올라앉았다."], ["I was so softened and forgiving, going through the town, that I had half a mind to nod to my old enemy the butcher, and throw him five shillings to drink.", "시내를 지나는 동안 내 마음이 어찌나 누그러지고 너그러워졌던지, 옛 원수인 푸줏간 주인에게 고개라도 까딱하며 술이나 한잔하라고 오 실링을 던져 줄까 하는 생각이 반쯤 들 정도였다."], ["But he looked such a very obdurate butcher as he stood scraping the great block in the shop, and moreover, his appearance was so little improved by the loss of a front tooth which I had knocked out, that I thought it best to make no advances.", "하지만 가게에서 커다란 도마를 박박 긁어 대며 서 있는 그의 모습이 어찌나 지독하게 고집스러운 푸줏간 주인 같았던지, 게다가 내가 부러뜨려 놓은 앞니 하나가 빠진 탓에 그 몰골이 조금도 나아지지 않았던지라, 나는 아예 다가서지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 마음먹었다."]], [["The main object on my mind, I remember, when we got fairly on the road, was to appear as old as possible to the coachman, and to speak extremely gruff.", "마차가 길에 제대로 접어들었을 때, 내 마음을 사로잡은 가장 큰 목적은, 기억하건대, 마부에게 되도록 나이 들어 보이는 것과 몹시 무뚝뚝하게 말하는 것이었다."], ["The latter point I achieved at great personal inconvenience; but I stuck to it, because I felt it was a grown-up sort of thing.", "뒤엣것은 적잖이 몸이 거북했지만 그래도 끝까지 밀고 나갔으니, 그게 어른스러운 짓이라고 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["‘You are going through, sir?’ said the coachman.", "\"끝까지 가십니까, 선생님?\" 마부가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, William,’ I said, condescendingly (I knew him); ‘I am going to London.", "\"그래, 윌리엄.\" 나는 짐짓 거들먹거리며 말했다 (나는 그를 알고 있었다)."], ["I shall go down into Suffolk afterwards.’", "\"런던까지 가네. 그다음엔 서퍽으로 내려갈 거야.\""]], [["‘Shooting, sir?’ said the coachman.", "\"사냥하시려고요, 선생님?\" 마부가 말했다."]], [["He knew as well as I did that it was just as likely, at that time of year, I was going down there whaling; but I felt complimented, too.", "이맘때라면 내가 거기 고래잡이를 하러 내려가는 거나 매한가지로 그럴듯하지 않다는 걸 그도 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었다. 그래도 나는 우쭐한 기분이 들었다."]], [["‘I don’t know,’ I said, pretending to be undecided, ‘whether I shall take a shot or not.’ ‘Birds is got wery shy, I’m told,’ said William.", "\"글쎄,\" 나는 아직 정하지 못한 척하며 말했다. \"한 방 쏠지 말지는 모르겠군.\" \"요즘 새들이 어지간히 겁이 많아졌다더군요.\" 윌리엄이 말했다."]], [["‘So I understand,’ said I.", "\"나도 그렇게 들었네.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Is Suffolk your county, sir?’ asked William.", "\"서퍽이 선생님 고향이십니까?\" 윌리엄이 물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ I said, with some importance.", "\"그래.\" 나는 제법 무게를 잡으며 말했다."], ["‘Suffolk’s my county.’", "\"서퍽이 내 고향이지.\""]], [["‘I’m told the dumplings is uncommon fine down there,’ said William.", "\"거기 경단이 유난히 맛있다던데요.\" 윌리엄이 말했다."]], [["I was not aware of it myself, but I felt it necessary to uphold the institutions of my county, and to evince a familiarity with them; so I shook my head, as much as to say, ‘I believe you!’", "나 자신은 그런 줄도 몰랐으나, 내 고향의 명물을 떠받들고 거기에 익숙한 척해야 할 것 같아서, 나는 \"그렇고말고!\" 하는 양 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘And the Punches,’ said William.", "\"그리고 그 펀치 말입니다.\""], ["‘There’s cattle!", "윌리엄이 말했다."], ["A Suffolk Punch, when he’s a good un, is worth his weight in gold.", "\"그게 바로 명마지요! 서퍽 펀치는 잘난 놈일 때는 제 몸무게만큼의 금값을 한답니다."], ["Did you ever breed any Suffolk Punches yourself, sir?’", "선생님은 서퍽 펀치를 직접 길러 보신 적 있으십니까?\""]], [["‘N-no,’ I said, ‘not exactly.’", "\"아, 아니.\" 내가 말했다. \"딱히 그렇진 않네.\""]], [["‘Here’s a gen’lm’n behind me, I’ll pound it,’ said William, ‘as has bred ‘em by wholesale.’", "\"제 뒤에 앉으신 신사분 말입니다, 제가 장담하는데,\" 윌리엄이 말했다. \"그놈들을 도매로 길러 내신 분이지요.\""]], [["The gentleman spoken of was a gentleman with a very unpromising squint, and a prominent chin, who had a tall white hat on with a narrow flat brim, and whose close-fitting drab trousers seemed to button all the way up outside his legs from his boots to his hips.", "말한 그 신사는 영 미덥지 못하게 사팔뜨기 눈에 턱이 툭 튀어나온 사람으로, 챙이 좁고 납작한 키 큰 흰 모자를 쓰고 있었으며, 몸에 딱 붙는 누런 바지는 장화에서부터 엉덩이까지 다리 바깥쪽을 따라 온통 단추로 채워진 듯했다."], ["His chin was cocked over the coachman’s shoulder, so near to me, that his breath quite tickled the back of my head; and as I looked at him, he leered at the leaders with the eye with which he didn’t squint, in a very knowing manner.", "그의 턱이 마부의 어깨 너머로 쑥 내밀어져 내게 어찌나 가까웠던지, 그 숨결이 내 뒤통수를 간질일 정도였다. 그리고 내가 그를 바라보자, 그는 사팔뜨기가 아닌 쪽 눈으로 매우 능청스럽게 앞에 선 말들을 곁눈질했다."]], [["‘Ain’t you?’ asked William.", "\"안 그렇습니까?\" 윌리엄이 물었다."]], [["‘Ain’t I what?’ said the gentleman behind.", "\"안 그렇다니, 뭐가?\" 뒤에 앉은 신사가 말했다."]], [["‘Bred them Suffolk Punches by wholesale?’", "\"그 서퍽 펀치들을 도매로 길러 내지 않으셨냐고요?\""]], [["‘I should think so,’ said the gentleman.", "\"그런 셈이지.\" 신사가 말했다."], ["‘There ain’t no sort of orse that I ain’t bred, and no sort of dorg.", "\"내가 안 길러 본 말이라곤 없고, 안 길러 본 개라곤 없으니까."], ["Orses and dorgs is some men’s fancy.", "말과 개는 어떤 사람들에겐 그저 취미지."], ["They’re wittles and drink to me--lodging, wife, and children--reading, writing, and Arithmetic--snuff, tobacker, and sleep.’", "나한테는 그게 먹을 거고 마실 거야 — 잠자리고, 마누라고, 자식이고 — 읽기고, 쓰기고, 셈이고 — 코담배고, 담배고, 잠이라네.\""]], [["‘That ain’t a sort of man to see sitting behind a coach-box, is it though?’ said William in my ear, as he handled the reins.", "\"마차 뒷자리에 앉아 있을 분 같지는 않죠, 안 그렇습니까?\" 윌리엄이 고삐를 다루며 내 귀에 대고 말했다."]], [["I construed this remark into an indication of a wish that he should have my place, so I blushingly offered to resign it.", "나는 이 말을 그가 내 자리를 차지하고 싶어 하는 뜻으로 받아들여, 얼굴을 붉히며 자리를 내드리겠다고 했다."]], [["‘Well, if you don’t mind, sir,’ said William, ‘I think it would be more correct.’", "\"뭐, 선생님만 괜찮으시다면요.\" 윌리엄이 말했다. \"그게 더 격에 맞을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["I have always considered this as the first fall I had in life.", "나는 늘 이 일을 내 인생에서 겪은 첫 번째 추락이라 여겨 왔다."], ["When I booked my place at the coach office I had had ‘Box Seat’ written against the entry, and had given the book-keeper half-a-crown.", "마차 사무소에서 자리를 예약할 때 나는 장부의 그 항목에 '마부석'이라고 적게 하고 사무원에게 반 크라운을 쥐여 줬었다."], ["I was got up in a special great-coat and shawl, expressly to do honour to that distinguished eminence; had glorified myself upon it a good deal; and had felt that I was a credit to the coach.", "그 빛나는 높은 자리에 어울리는 영예를 갖추려고 특별히 좋은 외투에 목도리까지 두르고 한껏 차려입었었고, 그 위에 올라앉아 잔뜩 우쭐댔으며, 내가 이 마차의 자랑거리라고 느꼈었다."], ["And here, in the very first stage, I was supplanted by a shabby man with a squint, who had no other merit than smelling like a livery-stables, and being able to walk across me, more like a fly than a human being, while the horses were at a canter!", "그런데 여기 첫 번째 정거장에서, 마구간 냄새를 풍기고 말들이 구보로 달리는 와중에도 사람이라기보다 파리처럼 내 몸 위로 척척 건너다닐 줄 아는 것 말고는 아무 잘난 점도 없는, 사팔뜨기에 추레한 사내에게 자리를 빼앗긴 것이다!"]], [["A distrust of myself, which has often beset me in life on small occasions, when it would have been better away, was assuredly not stopped in its growth by this little incident outside the Canterbury coach.", "작은 일에서, 차라리 없는 편이 나았을 때조차 내 인생 내내 곧잘 나를 따라다니던 그 자기 불신이, 이 캔터베리 마차 위에서 벌어진 이 사소한 사건으로 자라기를 멈추기는커녕 도리어 부추겨진 것만은 분명했다."], ["It was in vain to take refuge in gruffness of speech.", "무뚝뚝한 말투로 도망쳐 봐야 헛일이었다."], ["I spoke from the pit of my stomach for the rest of the journey, but I felt completely extinguished, and dreadfully young.", "나는 남은 여정 내내 배 속 깊은 데서 끌어올린 목소리로 말했지만, 완전히 꺼져 버린 기분이었고, 끔찍하게 어리게만 느껴졌다."]], [["It was curious and interesting, nevertheless, to be sitting up there behind four horses: well educated, well dressed, and with plenty of money in my pocket; and to look out for the places where I had slept on my weary journey.", "그래도 네 마리 말 뒤에 그렇게 올라앉아 있는 것은 묘하고도 흥미진진했다. 잘 배우고, 잘 차려입고, 주머니에는 돈이 두둑한 채로 말이다. 그리고 그 고단한 여정에서 내가 잠을 청했던 곳들을 찾아보는 것도 그러했다."], ["I had abundant occupation for my thoughts, in every conspicuous landmark on the road.", "길가의 눈에 띄는 표지마다 내 생각을 골똘히 붙들어 둘 거리가 넘쳐났다."], ["When I looked down at the trampers whom we passed, and saw that well-remembered style of face turned up, I felt as if the tinker’s blackened hand were in the bosom of my shirt again.", "우리가 지나치는 떠돌이들을 내려다보다가 그 낯익은 얼굴 생김새가 위로 쳐들리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 마치 그 땜장이의 시커먼 손이 다시 내 셔츠 가슴팍에 들어오기라도 한 듯한 기분이 들었다."], ["When we clattered through the narrow street of Chatham, and I caught a glimpse, in passing, of the lane where the old monster lived who had bought my jacket, I stretched my neck eagerly to look for the place where I had sat, in the sun and in the shade, waiting for my money.", "채텀의 좁은 거리를 덜컹덜컹 지나면서, 내 윗도리를 사 갔던 그 늙은 괴물이 살던 골목을 스쳐 가며 흘끗 보았을 때, 나는 햇볕 아래서, 또 그늘 속에서 내 돈을 기다리며 앉아 있던 그 자리를 찾으려고 간절히 목을 길게 뺐다."], ["When we came, at last, within a stage of London, and passed the veritable Salem House where Mr. Creakle had laid about him with a heavy hand, I would have given all I had, for lawful permission to get down and thrash him, and let all the boys out like so many caged sparrows.", "마침내 런던까지 한 정거장을 남겨 두고, 크리클 선생이 그 무거운 손으로 닥치는 대로 매질을 해 대던 바로 그 세일럼 학원을 지나칠 때, 나는 정당하게 내려가서 그를 흠씬 두들겨 패고, 새장에 갇힌 참새 떼처럼 모든 아이들을 풀어 줄 허락만 받을 수 있다면 내가 가진 전부라도 내놓았을 것이다."]], [["We went to the Golden Cross at Charing Cross, then a mouldy sort of establishment in a close neighbourhood.", "우리는 채링 크로스의 골든 크로스로 갔는데, 그 무렵 그곳은 비좁은 동네에 자리 잡은 곰팡내 나는 여관이었다."], ["A waiter showed me into the coffee-room; and a chambermaid introduced me to my small bedchamber, which smelt like a hackney-coach, and was shut up like a family vault.", "종업원이 나를 휴게실로 안내했고, 객실 하녀가 나를 내 작은 침실로 들여보냈는데, 그 방은 삯마차 같은 냄새가 났고 가족 납골당처럼 꼭꼭 닫혀 있었다."], ["I was still painfully conscious of my youth, for nobody stood in any awe of me at all: the chambermaid being utterly indifferent to my opinions on any subject, and the waiter being familiar with me, and offering advice to my inexperience.", "나는 여전히 내 어린 나이가 뼈저리게 의식되었으니, 누구 하나 나를 조금도 어려워하지 않았기 때문이다. 객실 하녀는 어떤 문제에 대한 내 의견에도 통 관심이 없었고, 종업원은 나를 허물없이 대하며 풋내기인 나에게 충고를 늘어놓았다."]], [["‘Well now,’ said the waiter, in a tone of confidence, ‘what would you like for dinner?", "\"자, 그럼,\" 종업원이 속을 터놓는 투로 말했다. \"저녁은 무얼 드시겠습니까?"], ["Young gentlemen likes poultry in general: have a fowl!’", "젊은 신사분들은 대개 새고기를 좋아하시던데요. 닭 한 마리 드시지요!\""]], [["I told him, as majestically as I could, that I wasn’t in the humour for a fowl.", "나는 되도록 위엄을 갖춰, 닭고기는 당기지 않는다고 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Ain’t you?’ said the waiter.", "\"안 당기십니까?\" 종업원이 말했다."], ["‘Young gentlemen is generally tired of beef and mutton: have a weal cutlet!’", "\"젊은 신사분들은 대개 쇠고기랑 양고기엔 물려 하시던데요. 송아지 커틀릿 하나 드시지요!\""]], [["I assented to this proposal, in default of being able to suggest anything else.", "나는 달리 무어라 떠올릴 수가 없어서 이 제안에 응했다."]], [["‘Do you care for taters?’ said the waiter, with an insinuating smile, and his head on one side.", "\"감자는 좋아하십니까?\" 종업원이 알랑거리는 미소를 지으며 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 말했다."], ["‘Young gentlemen generally has been overdosed with taters.’", "\"젊은 신사분들은 대개 감자를 너무 많이 드셔서 질리시던데요.\""]], [["I commanded him, in my deepest voice, to order a veal cutlet and potatoes, and all things fitting; and to inquire at the bar if there were any letters for Trotwood Copperfield, Esquire--which I knew there were not, and couldn’t be, but thought it manly to appear to expect.", "나는 더없이 굵은 목소리로 그에게 송아지 커틀릿과 감자, 그리고 거기에 어울리는 것을 모두 주문하라고, 그리고 카운터에 가서 트롯우드 코퍼필드 님 앞으로 온 편지가 있는지 물어보라고 명령했다 — 그런 편지가 없다는 것도, 있을 수가 없다는 것도 알았지만, 그걸 기다리는 척하는 게 사내다워 보일 것 같았다."]], [["He soon came back to say that there were none (at which I was much surprised) and began to lay the cloth for my dinner in a box by the fire.", "그는 곧 돌아와서 편지는 한 통도 없다고 말했고 (나는 무척 놀란 척했다), 난롯가 칸막이 자리에 내 저녁상을 차리기 시작했다."], ["While he was so engaged, he asked me what I would take with it; and on my replying ‘Half a pint of sherry,’ thought it a favourable opportunity, I am afraid, to extract that measure of wine from the stale leavings at the bottoms of several small decanters.", "그러면서 그는 곁들여 무얼 들겠느냐고 물었는데, 내가 \"셰리주 반 파인트\"라고 대답하자, 미안한 말이지만 그는 이때를 틈타 자그마한 술병 여러 개 바닥에 남은 김빠진 찌꺼기를 그만큼 모아 따라낸 듯하다."], ["I am of this opinion, because, while I was reading the newspaper, I observed him behind a low wooden partition, which was his private apartment, very busy pouring out of a number of those vessels into one, like a chemist and druggist making up a prescription.", "내가 이렇게 짐작하는 까닭은, 신문을 읽다가 그가 자기만의 공간인 낮은 나무 칸막이 뒤에서, 마치 약제사가 처방을 조제하듯 그 여러 병에서 한 병으로 분주히 따라 붓는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 게다가 술이 나왔을 때 나는 그 술이 김빠졌다고 느꼈다."], ["When the wine came, too, I thought it flat; and it certainly had more English crumbs in it, than were to be expected in a foreign wine in anything like a pure state, but I was bashful enough to drink it, and say nothing.", "그리고 그 술에는, 그런대로 순수한 상태의 외국산 포도주에서 기대할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 많은 영국 빵 부스러기가 들어 있었던 게 분명했다. 하지만 나는 너무 수줍은 나머지 군말 없이 그것을 마셔 버렸다."]], [["Being then in a pleasant frame of mind (from which I infer that poisoning is not always disagreeable in some stages of the process), I resolved to go to the play.", "그러고서 기분 좋은 상태에 빠진 채 (이로 미루어, 독을 먹는 과정도 어떤 단계에서는 늘 불쾌하기만 한 것은 아닌 모양이다), 나는 연극을 보러 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["It was Covent Garden Theatre that I chose; and there, from the back of a centre box, I saw Julius Caesar and the new Pantomime.", "내가 고른 것은 코번트 가든 극장이었고, 거기 가운데 박스석 뒤편에서 나는 「율리우스 카이사르」와 새로 나온 무언극을 보았다."], ["To have all those noble Romans alive before me, and walking in and out for my entertainment, instead of being the stern taskmasters they had been at school, was a most novel and delightful effect.", "그 고귀한 로마인들이 모두 내 눈앞에 살아 움직이며, 학교에서처럼 엄한 감독관이 아니라 나를 즐겁게 해 주려고 들락날락하는 모습은 더없이 새롭고 기쁜 광경이었다."], ["But the mingled reality and mystery of the whole show, the influence upon me of the poetry, the lights, the music, the company, the smooth stupendous changes of glittering and brilliant scenery, were so dazzling, and opened up such illimitable regions of delight, that when I came out into the rainy street, at twelve o’clock at night, I felt as if I had come from the clouds, where I had been leading a romantic life for ages, to a bawling, splashing, link-lighted, umbrella-struggling, hackney-coach-jostling, patten-clinking, muddy, miserable world.", "그러나 그 모든 공연에 뒤섞인 현실감과 신비, 시와 조명과 음악과 사람들이 나에게 미친 영향, 그리고 반짝이고 눈부신 무대 배경이 매끄럽게 바뀌는 어마어마한 광경이 어찌나 황홀했던지, 또 그것이 끝없는 환희의 세계를 어찌나 활짝 열어젖혔던지, 밤 열두 시에 비 내리는 거리로 나섰을 때 나는 마치 오랜 세월 낭만적인 삶을 살아온 구름 속에서, 고함치고, 물 튀기고, 가로등 불빛이 어른거리고, 우산끼리 부딪치고, 삯마차가 비집고 다니고, 나막신이 딸각거리는, 진창에 빠진 비참한 세상으로 떨어져 내린 것만 같았다."]], [["I had emerged by another door, and stood in the street for a little while, as if I really were a stranger upon earth: but the unceremonious pushing and hustling that I received, soon recalled me to myself, and put me in the road back to the hotel; whither I went, revolving the glorious vision all the way; and where, after some porter and oysters, I sat revolving it still, at past one o’clock, with my eyes on the coffee-room fire.", "나는 다른 문으로 빠져나와, 정말로 이 땅 위의 이방인이라도 된 양 잠시 거리에 서 있었다. 하지만 나를 향한 거침없는 떠밀림과 밀침에 곧 정신을 차려, 여관으로 돌아가는 길에 올랐다. 그 길 내내 그 찬란한 환상을 되새기며 여관으로 갔고, 거기서 흑맥주와 굴을 좀 먹은 뒤에도, 새벽 한 시가 넘도록 휴게실 난롯불에 눈을 둔 채 여전히 그 환상을 되새기며 앉아 있었다."]], [["I was so filled with the play, and with the past--for it was, in a manner, like a shining transparency, through which I saw my earlier life moving along--that I don’t know when the figure of a handsome well-formed young man dressed with a tasteful easy negligence which I have reason to remember very well, became a real presence to me.", "나는 그 연극으로, 또 지난날로 어찌나 가슴이 가득 찼던지 — 그것은 어떤 면에서, 그것을 통해 내 지난 삶이 흘러가는 것이 비쳐 보이는 빛나는 투명막 같았기에 — 잘생기고 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 멋스럽고 편안한 무심함으로 차려입은 한 젊은이의 모습이 — 그 차림새를 나는 아주 잘 기억할 만한 까닭이 있다 — 언제부터 내게 실제 모습으로 다가왔는지 알 수가 없다."], ["But I recollect being conscious of his company without having noticed his coming in--and my still sitting, musing, over the coffee-room fire.", "다만 나는 그가 들어오는 것을 알아채지도 못한 채 그가 곁에 있음을 느끼고 있었다는 것, 그리고 내가 여전히 휴게실 난롯불 앞에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다는 것만은 기억난다."]], [["At last I rose to go to bed, much to the relief of the sleepy waiter, who had got the fidgets in his legs, and was twisting them, and hitting them, and putting them through all kinds of contortions in his small pantry.", "마침내 나는 자리에서 일어나 잠자리에 들려고 했는데, 졸음에 겨운 종업원에게는 여간 다행이 아니었다. 그는 다리가 근질근질해서, 그 좁은 식기실에서 다리를 비틀고, 두드리고, 온갖 모양으로 배배 꼬아 대던 참이었다."], ["In going towards the door, I passed the person who had come in, and saw him plainly.", "문 쪽으로 가다가 나는 아까 들어온 그 사람을 스쳐 지나며 그를 또렷이 보았다."], ["I turned directly, came back, and looked again.", "나는 곧장 돌아서서, 다시 다가가 한 번 더 그를 보았다."], ["He did not know me, but I knew him in a moment.", "그는 나를 알아보지 못했지만, 나는 단번에 그를 알아보았다."]], [["At another time I might have wanted the confidence or the decision to speak to him, and might have put it off until next day, and might have lost him.", "다른 때였다면 나는 그에게 말을 걸 자신감이나 결단력이 모자라, 그것을 이튿날로 미루다가 그를 놓쳐 버렸을지도 모른다."], ["But, in the then condition of my mind, where the play was still running high, his former protection of me appeared so deserving of my gratitude, and my old love for him overflowed my breast so freshly and spontaneously, that I went up to him at once, with a fast-beating heart, and said:", "그러나 연극의 흥분이 아직도 한껏 끓어오르던 그때 내 마음 상태에서는, 지난날 그가 나를 보살펴 준 일이 어찌나 내 고마움을 받아 마땅하게 여겨졌던지, 또 그를 향한 나의 옛 사랑이 어찌나 싱그럽고 절로 내 가슴에 차올랐던지, 나는 콩닥거리는 심장을 안고 그에게 곧장 다가가 말했다."]], [["‘Steerforth!", "\"스티어포스!"], ["won’t you speak to me?’", "나한테 말 좀 안 걸어 줄래?\""]], [["He looked at me--just as he used to look, sometimes--but I saw no recognition in his face.", "그는 나를 바라보았다 — 가끔 나를 바라보곤 하던 바로 그 눈빛으로 — 하지만 그의 얼굴에서 나를 알아본 기색은 찾을 수 없었다."]], [["‘You don’t remember me, I am afraid,’ said I.", "\"날 기억 못 하는 모양이구나.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘My God!’ he suddenly exclaimed.", "\"맙소사!\" 그가 별안간 소리쳤다."], ["‘It’s little Copperfield!’", "\"꼬마 코퍼필드잖아!\""]], [["I grasped him by both hands, and could not let them go.", "나는 두 손으로 그의 손을 움켜쥐고는 도저히 놓을 수가 없었다."], ["But for very shame, and the fear that it might displease him, I could have held him round the neck and cried.", "부끄러움과, 혹시 그가 싫어할지 모른다는 두려움만 아니었다면, 나는 그의 목을 끌어안고 울었을 것이다."]], [["‘I never, never, never was so glad!", "\"이렇게, 이렇게, 이렇게 기뻤던 적이 없어!"], ["My dear Steerforth, I am so overjoyed to see you!’", "사랑하는 스티어포스, 널 만나서 정말 너무 기뻐!\""]], [["‘And I am rejoiced to see you, too!’ he said, shaking my hands heartily.", "\"나도 너를 만나서 기뻐!\" 그가 내 손을 다정하게 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘Why, Copperfield, old boy, don’t be overpowered!’ And yet he was glad, too, I thought, to see how the delight I had in meeting him affected me.", "\"이런, 코퍼필드, 이 친구야, 너무 그러지 마!\" 그러면서도 그는, 그를 만난 내 기쁨이 나를 이토록 사로잡은 모습을 보고 자기도 기뻐하는 것 같았다."]], [["I brushed away the tears that my utmost resolution had not been able to keep back, and I made a clumsy laugh of it, and we sat down together, side by side.", "나는 아무리 안간힘을 써도 끝내 참지 못하고 흘러나온 눈물을 닦아 내고는, 어설피 웃어넘겼다. 그러고서 우리는 나란히 함께 자리에 앉았다."]], [["‘Why, how do you come to be here?’ said Steerforth, clapping me on the shoulder.", "\"그런데, 어쩌다 여기 있게 된 거야?\" 스티어포스가 내 어깨를 툭 치며 말했다."]], [["‘I came here by the Canterbury coach, today.", "\"오늘 캔터베리 마차로 여기 왔어."], ["I have been adopted by an aunt down in that part of the country, and have just finished my education there. How do YOU come to be here, Steerforth?’", "그 고장에 사는 고모님이 나를 거두셨거든, 그래서 거기서 막 학업을 마쳤어. 너는 어쩌다 여기 있게 된 거야, 스티어포스?\""]], [["‘Well, I am what they call an Oxford man,’ he returned; ‘that is to say, I get bored to death down there, periodically--and I am on my way now to my mother’s.", "\"음, 나는 사람들이 말하는 옥스퍼드 사람이지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그러니까, 나는 주기적으로 거기서 죽을 만큼 따분해진단 말이야 — 그래서 지금 어머니 댁으로 가는 길이야."], ["You’re a devilish amiable-looking fellow, Copperfield.", "너 참 지독히도 상냥해 보이는 친구로구나, 코퍼필드."], ["Just what you used to be, now I look at you!", "지금 다시 보니, 딱 옛날 그대로야!"], ["Not altered in the least!’", "조금도 안 변했어!\""]], [["‘I knew you immediately,’ I said; ‘but you are more easily remembered.’", "\"나는 너를 단번에 알아봤어.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 너야 더 쉽게 기억할 수 있지.\""]], [["He laughed as he ran his hand through the clustering curls of his hair, and said gaily:", "그는 곱슬곱슬 뭉친 머리칼을 손으로 쓸어 넘기며 웃더니, 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, I am on an expedition of duty. My mother lives a little way out of town; and the roads being in a beastly condition, and our house tedious enough, I remained here tonight instead of going on.", "\"그래, 나는 의무를 다하러 가는 원정 중이야. 어머니가 시내에서 좀 떨어진 곳에 사시는데, 길이 지독한 데다 우리 집이 어지간히 따분해서, 오늘 밤은 곧장 가지 않고 여기 묵기로 했어."], ["I have not been in town half-a-dozen hours, and those I have been dozing and grumbling away at the play.’", "시내에 들어온 지 여섯 시간도 채 안 됐는데, 그것도 연극을 보면서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 투덜대며 다 흘려보냈지.\""]], [["‘I have been at the play, too,’ said I.", "\"나도 연극을 보고 왔어.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘At Covent Garden.", "\"코번트 가든에서."], ["What a delightful and magnificent entertainment, Steerforth!’", "정말 즐겁고 으리으리한 구경거리였어, 스티어포스!\""]], [["Steerforth laughed heartily.", "스티어포스가 호탕하게 웃었다."]], [["‘My dear young Davy,’ he said, clapping me on the shoulder again, ‘you are a very Daisy.", "\"내 사랑스러운 어린 데이비.\" 그가 다시 내 어깨를 툭 치며 말했다. \"너는 영락없는 데이지로구나."], ["The daisy of the field, at sunrise, is not fresher than you are.", "해 뜰 녘 들판의 데이지도 너보다 싱그럽진 않을 거야."], ["I have been at Covent Garden, too, and there never was a more miserable business.", "나도 코번트 가든에 갔었는데, 그렇게 형편없는 공연은 또 없었어."], ["Holloa, you sir!’", "어이, 자네!\""]], [["This was addressed to the waiter, who had been very attentive to our recognition, at a distance, and now came forward deferentially.", "이것은 우리가 서로를 알아보는 광경을 멀찍이서 무척 눈여겨보다가, 이제 공손하게 앞으로 다가온 종업원에게 한 말이었다."]], [["‘Where have you put my friend, Mr. Copperfield?’ said Steerforth.", "\"내 친구 코퍼필드 씨를 어디에 모셨나?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon, sir?’", "\"네, 무슨 말씀이신지요, 손님?\""]], [["‘Where does he sleep?", "\"그가 어디서 자느냐고."], ["What’s his number?", "방 번호가 몇 번이야?"], ["You know what I mean,’ said Steerforth.", "내 말 무슨 뜻인지 알잖아.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ said the waiter, with an apologetic air. ‘Mr.", "\"아, 네, 손님.\" 종업원이 미안한 기색으로 말했다."], ["Copperfield is at present in forty-four, sir.’", "\"코퍼필드 씨는 지금 사십사 호에 계십니다, 손님.\""]], [["‘And what the devil do you mean,’ retorted Steerforth, ‘by putting Mr.", "\"그래서 대체 무슨 생각으로,\" 스티어포스가 쏘아붙였다."], ["Copperfield into a little loft over a stable?’", "\"코퍼필드 씨를 마구간 위 자그마한 다락방에 처박은 거지?\""]], [["‘Why, you see we wasn’t aware, sir,’ returned the waiter, still apologetically, ‘as Mr.", "\"그게, 저희가 미처 몰랐지 뭡니까, 손님.\" 종업원이 여전히 미안해하며 대답했다."], ["Copperfield was anyways particular. We can give Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨가 그런 걸 까다롭게 여기시는 분인 줄을요."], ["Copperfield seventy-two, sir, if it would be preferred.", "원하신다면 코퍼필드 씨께 칠십이 호를 드릴 수 있습니다, 손님."], ["Next you, sir.’", "손님 바로 옆방으로요.\""]], [["‘Of course it would be preferred,’ said Steerforth.", "\"당연히 그게 낫지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘And do it at once.’ The waiter immediately withdrew to make the exchange.", "\"그것도 당장.\" 종업원은 곧바로 물러가 방을 바꾸었다."], ["Steerforth, very much amused at my having been put into forty-four, laughed again, and clapped me on the shoulder again, and invited me to breakfast with him next morning at ten o’clock--an invitation I was only too proud and happy to accept.", "스티어포스는 내가 사십사 호에 처박혔던 일이 무척이나 재미있다는 듯 또 한 번 웃고는, 또 한 번 내 어깨를 툭 치더니, 이튿날 아침 열 시에 자기와 함께 아침을 먹자고 청했다 — 나로서는 더없이 자랑스럽고 기쁜 마음으로 받아들인 청이었다."], ["It being now pretty late, we took our candles and went upstairs, where we parted with friendly heartiness at his door, and where I found my new room a great improvement on my old one, it not being at all musty, and having an immense four-post bedstead in it, which was quite a little landed estate.", "시각이 이제 꽤 늦은지라, 우리는 저마다 촛불을 들고 위층으로 올라가, 그의 방문 앞에서 다정하고 살가운 인사를 나누며 헤어졌다. 새 방은 옛 방보다 훨씬 나았으니, 곰팡내라곤 전혀 없었고, 그 안에는 작은 영지나 다름없는 어마어마한 사주식 침대가 놓여 있었다."], ["Here, among pillows enough for six, I soon fell asleep in a blissful condition, and dreamed of ancient Rome, Steerforth, and friendship, until the early morning coaches, rumbling out of the archway underneath, made me dream of thunder and the gods.", "여섯 사람이 베고도 남을 베개들 틈에서 나는 이내 더없이 행복한 기분으로 잠이 들어, 옛 로마와 스티어포스와 우정을 꿈꾸었다. 그러다 새벽 마차들이 바로 아래 아치문을 빠져나가며 우르릉거리는 소리에, 나는 천둥과 신들을 꿈꾸기 시작했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 20. STEERFORTH’S HOME", "# CHAPTER 20. STEERFORTH’S HOME"]], [["When the chambermaid tapped at my door at eight o’clock, and informed me that my shaving-water was outside, I felt severely the having no occasion for it, and blushed in my bed.", "여덟 시에 객실 하녀가 내 방문을 똑똑 두드리며 면도용 물을 문밖에 놓아 두었다고 알려 주었을 때, 나는 그 물이 쓸데없다는 사실이 뼈저리게 느껴져 침대 속에서 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다."], ["The suspicion that she laughed too, when she said it, preyed upon my mind all the time I was dressing; and gave me, I was conscious, a sneaking and guilty air when I passed her on the staircase, as I was going down to breakfast.", "그 말을 하면서 하녀도 웃은 것 같다는 의심이, 옷을 입는 내내 마음을 갉아먹었다. 아침을 먹으러 내려가다가 계단에서 그녀를 지나칠 때, 나는 내가 봐도 슬그머니 죄지은 듯한 기색을 띠고 있었다."], ["I was so sensitively aware, indeed, of being younger than I could have wished, that for some time I could not make up my mind to pass her at all, under the ignoble circumstances of the case; but, hearing her there with a broom, stood peeping out of window at King Charles on horseback, surrounded by a maze of hackney-coaches, and looking anything but regal in a drizzling rain and a dark-brown fog, until I was admonished by the waiter that the gentleman was waiting for me.", "사실 나는 바라는 것보다 어리다는 사실을 어찌나 예민하게 의식했던지, 이런 한심한 처지에서는 한동안 그녀를 지나칠 엄두조차 내지 못했다. 그러다 그녀가 거기서 빗자루를 들고 있는 소리를 듣고는, 창밖으로 말을 탄 찰스 왕을 내다보며 서 있었는데, 그 동상은 삯마차들이 빚어내는 미로 같은 행렬에 둘러싸여, 부슬부슬 내리는 비와 검누런 안개 속에서 도무지 위엄이라곤 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그러다 마침내 종업원이 그 신사분이 나를 기다리고 계신다고 일러 주었다."]], [["It was not in the coffee-room that I found Steerforth expecting me, but in a snug private apartment, red-curtained and Turkey-carpeted, where the fire burnt bright, and a fine hot breakfast was set forth on a table covered with a clean cloth; and a cheerful miniature of the room, the fire, the breakfast, Steerforth, and all, was shining in the little round mirror over the sideboard.", "스티어포스가 나를 기다리던 곳은 커피룸이 아니라, 붉은 휘장을 드리우고 터키산 양탄자를 깐 아늑한 독실이었는데, 그곳은 난롯불이 환히 타오르고, 깨끗한 식탁보를 씌운 탁자 위에 김이 모락모락 나는 훌륭한 아침상이 차려져 있었다. 그리고 그 방과 난롯불과 아침상과 스티어포스와 그 모든 것이, 찬장 위에 걸린 작고 둥근 거울 속에 정겨운 축소판으로 빛나고 있었다."], ["I was rather bashful at first, Steerforth being so self-possessed, and elegant, and superior to me in all respects (age included); but his easy patronage soon put that to rights, and made me quite at home.", "처음에는 좀 수줍었는데, 스티어포스가 어찌나 침착하고 우아하며 모든 면에서 (나이까지 포함해) 나보다 윗길이었던지 그랬다. 하지만 그의 거리낌 없는 보살핌이 이내 그런 마음을 바로잡아 주어, 나는 완전히 마음을 놓았다."], ["I could not enough admire the change he had wrought in the Golden Cross; or compare the dull forlorn state I had held yesterday, with this morning’s comfort and this morning’s entertainment.", "그가 골든 크로스 여관에 일으킨 변화에 나는 아무리 감탄해도 모자랐고, 어제 내가 겪었던 따분하고 쓸쓸한 처지를 오늘 아침의 안락함과 즐거움에 견주어 보아도 그랬다."], ["As to the waiter’s familiarity, it was quenched as if it had never been.", "종업원의 그 허물없던 태도로 말하자면, 마치 처음부터 없었던 양 싹 사라져 버렸다."], ["He attended on us, as I may say, in sackcloth and ashes.", "그는, 말하자면, 베옷을 걸치고 재를 뒤집어쓴 죄인처럼 우리 시중을 들었다."]], [["‘Now, Copperfield,’ said Steerforth, when we were alone, ‘I should like to hear what you are doing, and where you are going, and all about you.", "\"자, 코퍼필드.\" 둘만 남게 되자 스티어포스가 말했다. \"네가 뭘 하고 있는지, 어디로 가는지, 너에 관한 모든 걸 듣고 싶구나."], ["I feel as if you were my property.’ Glowing with pleasure to find that he had still this interest in me, I told him how my aunt had proposed the little expedition that I had before me, and whither it tended.", "꼭 네가 내 소유물 같은 기분이 들거든.\" 그가 여전히 나에게 이런 관심을 품고 있다는 사실에 나는 기쁨으로 달아올라, 고모님이 내 앞에 놓인 이 작은 여행을 제안하셨다는 것과 그 여행이 어디로 향하는지를 들려주었다."]], [["‘As you are in no hurry, then,’ said Steerforth, ‘come home with me to Highgate, and stay a day or two.", "\"그럼, 급할 게 없다면,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"나랑 같이 하이게이트 우리 집으로 가서 하루 이틀 묵고 가."], ["You will be pleased with my mother--she is a little vain and prosy about me, but that you can forgive her--and she will be pleased with you.’", "우리 어머니가 마음에 들 거야 — 어머니는 내 얘기라면 좀 우쭐대고 장황하시지만, 그쯤은 너도 봐 줄 수 있을 테고 — 어머니도 너를 마음에 들어 하실 거야.\""]], [["‘I should like to be as sure of that, as you are kind enough to say you are,’ I answered, smiling.", "\"네가 친절하게 확신하는 만큼 나도 확신할 수 있으면 좋겠어.\" 나는 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Steerforth, ‘everyone who likes me, has a claim on her that is sure to be acknowledged.’", "\"오!\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"나를 좋아하는 사람이라면 누구든 어머니에게 인정받을 자격이 있고, 그건 어김없이 인정받게 돼 있어.\""]], [["‘Then I think I shall be a favourite,’ said I.", "\"그럼 내가 귀여움을 받겠는걸.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Good!’ said Steerforth.", "\"좋았어!\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Come and prove it.", "\"와서 증명해 봐."], ["We will go and see the lions for an hour or two--it’s something to have a fresh fellow like you to show them to, Copperfield--and then we’ll journey out to Highgate by the coach.’", "한두 시간쯤 명소들을 구경하자고 — 너처럼 신선한 친구한테 그것들을 보여 주는 것도 제법 괜찮은 일이야, 코퍼필드 — 그러고 나서 역마차를 타고 하이게이트로 가는 거야.\""]], [["I could hardly believe but that I was in a dream, and that I should wake presently in number forty-four, to the solitary box in the coffee-room and the familiar waiter again.", "이게 꿈이 아닌가 싶었다. 곧 깨어나 보면 다시 사십사 호실에, 커피룸의 외딴 칸막이 자리에, 그 허물없던 종업원이 있을 것만 같았다."], ["After I had written to my aunt and told her of my fortunate meeting with my admired old schoolfellow, and my acceptance of his invitation, we went out in a hackney-chariot, and saw a Panorama and some other sights, and took a walk through the Museum, where I could not help observing how much Steerforth knew, on an infinite variety of subjects, and of how little account he seemed to make his knowledge.", "고모님께 편지를 써서, 흠모하던 옛 학교 친구를 운 좋게 만난 일과 그의 초대를 받아들였다는 소식을 전한 뒤, 우리는 삯마차를 타고 나가 파노라마며 다른 구경거리를 보았고, 박물관을 거닐었는데, 거기서 나는 스티어포스가 온갖 분야에 대해 얼마나 많이 알고 있는지, 그러면서도 자기 지식을 얼마나 대수롭지 않게 여기는지 새삼 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘You’ll take a high degree at college, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘if you have not done so already; and they will have good reason to be proud of you.’", "\"넌 대학에서 높은 학위를 받을 거야, 스티어포스.\" 내가 말했다. \"아직 안 받았다면 말이지. 학교에서도 너를 자랑스러워할 충분한 이유가 있을 거야.\""]], [["‘I take a degree!’ cried Steerforth. ‘Not I!", "\"내가 학위를 받는다고!\" 스티어포스가 소리쳤다. \"천만에!"], ["my dear Daisy--will you mind my calling you Daisy?’", "사랑하는 데이지 — 너를 데이지라고 불러도 괜찮겠지?\""]], [["‘Not at all!’ said I.", "\"전혀 상관없어!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘That’s a good fellow!", "\"역시 좋은 녀석이야!"], ["My dear Daisy,’ said Steerforth, laughing.", "사랑하는 데이지.\" 스티어포스가 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘I have not the least desire or intention to distinguish myself in that way.", "\"나는 그런 식으로 두각을 나타내고 싶은 마음도, 그럴 뜻도 조금도 없어."], ["I have done quite sufficient for my purpose.", "내 목적에 필요한 만큼은 이미 다 했어."], ["I find that I am heavy company enough for myself as I am.’", "나는 이대로도 나 자신에게 충분히 버거운 길동무거든.\""]], [["‘But the fame--’ I was beginning.", "\"하지만 명성이—\" 내가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["‘You romantic Daisy!’ said Steerforth, laughing still more heartily: ‘why should I trouble myself, that a parcel of heavy-headed fellows may gape and hold up their hands?", "\"이 낭만적인 데이지 녀석아!\" 스티어포스가 한결 더 신나게 웃으며 말했다. \"왜 내가 머리 둔한 녀석들 한 무더기가 입을 헤벌리고 두 손을 치켜들게 하려고 애를 써야 하지?"], ["Let them do it at some other man.", "그런 건 다른 사람한테나 가서 해 보라지."], ["There’s fame for him, and he’s welcome to it.’", "명성이라면 그 사람 몫이고, 얼마든지 가지라고 해.\""]], [["I was abashed at having made so great a mistake, and was glad to change the subject.", "이렇게 큰 실수를 저질렀다는 게 부끄러워서, 나는 얼른 화제를 바꾸게 되어 다행이었다."], ["Fortunately it was not difficult to do, for Steerforth could always pass from one subject to another with a carelessness and lightness that were his own.", "다행히 그건 어렵지 않은 일이었는데, 스티어포스는 늘 그 사람 특유의 무심함과 가벼움으로 이 화제에서 저 화제로 옮겨 다닐 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Lunch succeeded to our sight-seeing, and the short winter day wore away so fast, that it was dusk when the stage-coach stopped with us at an old brick house at Highgate on the summit of the hill.", "구경을 마친 뒤 점심을 먹었고, 짧은 겨울 하루가 어찌나 빨리 저물던지, 역마차가 우리를 태운 채 언덕 꼭대기 하이게이트의 오래된 벽돌집 앞에 멈춰 섰을 때는 이미 땅거미가 지고 있었다."], ["An elderly lady, though not very far advanced in years, with a proud carriage and a handsome face, was in the doorway as we alighted; and greeting Steerforth as ‘My dearest James,’ folded him in her arms.", "나이가 그리 많지는 않으나 지긋한 한 부인이, 자긍심 어린 자태와 아름다운 얼굴로 우리가 내릴 때 문간에 서 있었다. 그녀는 스티어포스를 \"내 사랑하는 제임스\"라고 부르며 두 팔로 끌어안았다."], ["To this lady he presented me as his mother, and she gave me a stately welcome.", "스티어포스는 그 부인에게 나를 어머니라고 소개했고, 그녀는 나에게 위엄 있는 환영의 인사를 건넸다."]], [["It was a genteel old-fashioned house, very quiet and orderly.", "품격 있고 고풍스러운 집으로, 아주 조용하고 정돈되어 있었다."], ["From the windows of my room I saw all London lying in the distance like a great vapour, with here and there some lights twinkling through it.", "내 방 창문에서는 런던 전체가 멀리 거대한 수증기처럼 누워 있고, 그 사이로 군데군데 불빛이 깜박이는 게 보였다."], ["I had only time, in dressing, to glance at the solid furniture, the framed pieces of work (done, I supposed, by Steerforth’s mother when she was a girl), and some pictures in crayons of ladies with powdered hair and bodices, coming and going on the walls, as the newly-kindled fire crackled and sputtered, when I was called to dinner.", "옷을 갈아입으면서, 막 지핀 난롯불이 탁탁 튀며 타오르는 동안, 묵직한 가구들과 액자에 든 수예품들 (짐작건대 스티어포스의 어머니가 소녀 시절에 만든 것이리라), 그리고 머리에 분을 바르고 코르셋을 한 부인들을 크레용으로 그린 그림 몇 점이 벽 위에서 어른어른 떠올랐다 사라지는 것을 흘끗 볼 짬밖에 없었는데, 그때 저녁 식사하러 오라는 부름을 받았다."]], [["There was a second lady in the dining-room, of a slight short figure, dark, and not agreeable to look at, but with some appearance of good looks too, who attracted my attention: perhaps because I had not expected to see her; perhaps because I found myself sitting opposite to her; perhaps because of something really remarkable in her.", "식당에는 또 다른 부인이 있었는데, 호리호리하고 작은 몸집에, 가무잡잡하고 보기에 그리 곱지는 않으나 어딘가 미인다운 구석도 있어서 내 주의를 끌었다. 어쩌면 그녀를 보리라고 예상하지 못했기 때문일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 내가 그녀의 맞은편에 앉게 되었기 때문일 수도 있으며, 어쩌면 그녀에게 정말로 눈에 띄는 무언가가 있었기 때문일 수도 있다."], ["She had black hair and eager black eyes, and was thin, and had a scar upon her lip.", "그녀는 검은 머리에 형형한 검은 눈을 지녔고, 야위었으며, 입술에 흉터가 하나 있었다."], ["It was an old scar--I should rather call it seam, for it was not discoloured, and had healed years ago--which had once cut through her mouth, downward towards the chin, but was now barely visible across the table, except above and on her upper lip, the shape of which it had altered.", "오래된 흉터였다 — 차라리 흉이라고 부르는 편이 맞겠는데, 변색되지도 않았고 오래전에 아문 자국이었기 때문이다 — 한때 그녀의 입을 가르며 아래로 턱을 향해 내려간 상처였으나, 이제는 식탁 너머로는 거의 보이지 않았다. 다만 윗입술 위쪽으로, 그 입술 모양을 바꿔 놓은 부분만은 예외였다."], ["I concluded in my own mind that she was about thirty years of age, and that she wished to be married.", "나는 속으로 그녀가 서른 살쯤 되었으며 결혼하고 싶어 하리라고 결론지었다."], ["She was a little dilapidated--like a house--with having been so long to let; yet had, as I have said, an appearance of good looks.", "그녀는 너무 오래 세놓인 집처럼 — 어딘가 조금 낡고 허름했으나 — 그래도 앞서 말했듯 미인다운 데가 있었다."], ["Her thinness seemed to be the effect of some wasting fire within her, which found a vent in her gaunt eyes.", "그 야윈 모습은 그녀 안에서 무언가를 태우는 불길이 빚어낸 결과 같았는데, 그 불길은 그녀의 퀭한 두 눈에서 출구를 찾고 있었다."]], [["She was introduced as Miss Dartle, and both Steerforth and his mother called her Rosa.", "그녀는 다틀 양으로 소개되었고, 스티어포스도 그의 어머니도 그녀를 로자라고 불렀다."], ["I found that she lived there, and had been for a long time Mrs. Steerforth’s companion.", "알고 보니 그녀는 이 집에 살았으며, 오래도록 스티어포스 부인의 말벗 노릇을 해 왔다."], ["It appeared to me that she never said anything she wanted to say, outright; but hinted it, and made a great deal more of it by this practice.", "내가 보기에 그녀는 하고 싶은 말을 결코 대놓고 하는 법이 없었다. 대신 슬쩍 암시를 던졌고, 그런 방식으로 그 말을 훨씬 더 크게 부풀렸다."], ["For example, when Mrs. Steerforth observed, more in jest than earnest, that she feared her son led but a wild life at college, Miss Dartle put in thus:", "이를테면 스티어포스 부인이 진담 반 농담 반으로, 아들이 대학에서 방탕하게 지내지나 않을까 걱정이라고 말했을 때, 다틀 양은 이렇게 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Oh, really?", "\"어머, 정말요?"], ["You know how ignorant I am, and that I only ask for information, but isn’t it always so?", "제가 얼마나 무지한지 아시잖아요, 그저 알고 싶어서 여쭤보는 거예요, 하지만 늘 그렇지 않나요?"], ["I thought that kind of life was on all hands understood to be--eh?’ ‘It is education for a very grave profession, if you mean that, Rosa,’ Mrs.", "그런 생활은 누구나 다 그렇게 이해하는 줄 알았는데 — 아닌가요?\" \"그게 아주 진지한 직업을 위한 수업이라는 뜻이라면 맞아, 로자.\""], ["Steerforth answered with some coldness.", "스티어포스 부인이 다소 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh! Yes!", "\"오! 네!"], ["That’s very true,’ returned Miss Dartle.", "정말 맞는 말씀이에요.\" 다틀 양이 받았다."], ["‘But isn’t it, though?--I want to be put right, if I am wrong--isn’t it, really?’", "\"하지만 그래도 그렇지 않나요? — 제가 틀렸다면 바로잡아 주세요 — 정말 그렇지 않나요?\""]], [["‘Really what?’ said Mrs. Steerforth.", "\"정말 뭐가 그렇다는 게냐?\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh! You mean it’s not!’ returned Miss Dartle.", "\"오! 그렇지 않다는 말씀이군요!\""], ["‘Well, I’m very glad to hear it! Now, I know what to do!", "다틀 양이 받았다. \"어머, 그 말씀을 들으니 정말 기뻐요!"], ["That’s the advantage of asking.", "이제 어떻게 해야 할지 알겠어요!"], ["I shall never allow people to talk before me about wastefulness and profligacy, and so forth, in connexion with that life, any more.’", "이게 바로 여쭤보는 것의 이점이지요. 앞으로는 그런 생활과 관련해서 낭비니 방탕이니 하는 말을 제 앞에서 하도록 절대 두지 않겠어요.\""]], [["‘And you will be right,’ said Mrs. Steerforth.", "\"그래야 옳지.\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다."], ["‘My son’s tutor is a conscientious gentleman; and if I had not implicit reliance on my son, I should have reliance on him.’", "\"내 아들의 가정교사는 양심적인 신사야. 내가 아들을 전적으로 믿지 못한다 하더라도, 그분만큼은 믿을 거다.\""]], [["‘Should you?’ said Miss Dartle. ‘Dear me!", "\"그러세요?\" 다틀 양이 말했다. \"어머나!"], ["Conscientious, is he?", "양심적이라고요?"], ["Really conscientious, now?’", "정말로 양심적인가요?\""]], [["‘Yes, I am convinced of it,’ said Mrs. Steerforth.", "\"그래, 나는 확신한다.\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘How very nice!’ exclaimed Miss Dartle.", "\"어쩜 그리 훌륭할까요!\" 다틀 양이 외쳤다."], ["‘What a comfort!", "\"얼마나 마음 놓이는지!"], ["Really conscientious?", "정말로 양심적이라고요?"], ["Then he’s not--but of course he can’t be, if he’s really conscientious.", "그렇다면 그분은 — 하지만 정말로 양심적이라면 당연히 그럴 리 없겠지요."], ["Well, I shall be quite happy in my opinion of him, from this time.", "그럼 이제부터 저는 그분에 대해 아주 흐뭇한 생각을 품게 되겠네요."], ["You can’t think how it elevates him in my opinion, to know for certain that he’s really conscientious!’", "그분이 정말로 양심적이라는 걸 확실히 아니까 제 마음속에서 얼마나 그분이 높아지는지 모르실 거예요!\""]], [["Her own views of every question, and her correction of everything that was said to which she was opposed, Miss Dartle insinuated in the same way: sometimes, I could not conceal from myself, with great power, though in contradiction even of Steerforth.", "다틀 양은 온갖 문제에 대한 자기 견해며, 자기가 반대하는 모든 말에 대한 자기 나름의 정정을, 이런 식으로 슬며시 흘렸다. 때로는, 나도 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었는데, 상대가 스티어포스라 해도 그에 맞서면서까지 대단한 위력을 발휘했다."], ["An instance happened before dinner was done. Mrs.", "그런 일이 저녁 식사가 끝나기 전에 한 차례 벌어졌다."], ["Steerforth speaking to me about my intention of going down into Suffolk, I said at hazard how glad I should be, if Steerforth would only go there with me; and explaining to him that I was going to see my old nurse, and Mr. Peggotty’s family, I reminded him of the boatman whom he had seen at school.", "스티어포스 부인이 서퍽으로 내려가려는 내 계획에 대해 말을 꺼내자, 나는 스티어포스가 나와 함께 그곳에 가 주기만 한다면 얼마나 기쁠지 모르겠다고 무심코 말했고, 내가 옛 유모와 패거티 씨네 식구를 보러 가는 길이라고 설명하면서, 학창 시절에 그가 본 적 있는 그 뱃사람을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["That bluff fellow!’ said Steerforth.", "그 우락부락한 친구!\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘He had a son with him, hadn’t he?’", "\"아들을 하나 데리고 있지 않았나?\""]], [["‘No.", "\"아니."], ["That was his nephew,’ I replied; ‘whom he adopted, though, as a son.", "그건 조카였어.\" 내가 대답했다. \"그래도 아들처럼 거두었지."], ["He has a very pretty little niece too, whom he adopted as a daughter.", "아주 예쁘장한 어린 조카딸도 하나 있는데, 딸처럼 거두었어."], ["In short, his house--or rather his boat, for he lives in one, on dry land--is full of people who are objects of his generosity and kindness.", "한마디로 그 사람 집은 — 아니, 차라리 배라고 해야겠지, 마른 땅 위에 놓인 배에서 사니까 — 그 사람의 너그러움과 친절을 입은 사람들로 가득해."], ["You would be delighted to see that household.’", "그 집안을 보면 너도 흐뭇할 거야.\""]], [["‘Should I?’ said Steerforth.", "\"그럴까?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Well, I think I should.", "\"음, 그럴 것 같긴 해."], ["I must see what can be done.", "어떻게 할 수 있을지 알아봐야겠어."], ["It would be worth a journey (not to mention the pleasure of a journey with you, Daisy), to see that sort of people together, and to make one of ‘em.’", "그런 종류의 사람들이 한데 모여 있는 걸 보고, 그 틈에 끼어 보는 건 (너와 함께하는 여행의 즐거움은 말할 것도 없고, 데이지) 한번 가 볼 만한 일이겠어.\""]], [["My heart leaped with a new hope of pleasure.", "새로운 기쁨의 희망에 내 가슴이 뛰었다."], ["But it was in reference to the tone in which he had spoken of ‘that sort of people’, that Miss Dartle, whose sparkling eyes had been watchful of us, now broke in again.", "그러나 그가 \"그런 종류의 사람들\"이라고 말한 그 어투를 두고, 줄곧 반짝이는 눈으로 우리를 지켜보던 다틀 양이 또다시 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Oh, but, really?", "\"어머, 하지만 정말요?"], ["Do tell me.", "좀 말씀해 주세요."], ["Are they, though?’ she said.", "그 사람들이 정말 그런가요?\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["‘Are they what?", "\"그 사람들이 뭐가 그렇다는 거죠?"], ["And are who what?’ said Steerforth.", "누가 뭐가 그렇다는 거예요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘That sort of people.---Are they really animals and clods, and beings of another order?", "\"그런 종류의 사람들 말이에요. — 그들이 정말로 짐승이나 흙덩이 같은, 다른 부류의 존재들인가요?"], ["I want to know SO much.’", "너무너무 알고 싶어요.\""]], [["‘Why, there’s a pretty wide separation between them and us,’ said Steerforth, with indifference.", "\"음, 그들과 우리 사이에는 꽤 넓은 간극이 있지.\" 스티어포스가 무심히 말했다."], ["‘They are not to be expected to be as sensitive as we are.", "\"그들이 우리만큼 예민하기를 바랄 수는 없어."], ["Their delicacy is not to be shocked, or hurt easily.", "그들의 섬세함은 쉬이 충격받거나 상처 입을 만한 게 아니거든."], ["They are wonderfully virtuous, I dare say--some people contend for that, at least; and I am sure I don’t want to contradict them--but they have not very fine natures, and they may be thankful that, like their coarse rough skins, they are not easily wounded.’", "아마 놀랄 만큼 덕성스럽긴 할 거야 — 적어도 어떤 사람들은 그렇게 우기지. 나도 그걸 반박할 생각은 전혀 없고 — 하지만 그들은 그리 고운 천성을 지니지 못했고, 거칠고 투박한 살갗처럼 쉬이 다치지 않는다는 걸 오히려 고맙게 여겨야 할 거야.\""]], [["‘Really!’ said Miss Dartle.", "\"정말요!\" 다틀 양이 말했다."], ["‘Well, I don’t know, now, when I have been better pleased than to hear that.", "\"어머, 글쎄요, 그 말을 들은 것만큼 기뻤던 적이 또 있었나 모르겠어요."], ["It’s so consoling!", "어쩜 그리 위안이 되는지!"], ["It’s such a delight to know that, when they suffer, they don’t feel!", "그들이 고통받을 때 아무것도 못 느낀다는 걸 알게 되니 얼마나 기쁜지!"], ["Sometimes I have been quite uneasy for that sort of people; but now I shall just dismiss the idea of them, altogether.", "가끔 저는 그런 종류의 사람들 때문에 꽤 마음이 편치 않았는데, 이제 그런 생각은 깨끗이 떨쳐 버려야겠어요."], ["Live and learn. I had my doubts, I confess, but now they’re cleared up.", "살다 보면 배우는군요. 솔직히 저도 의심이 들긴 했는데, 이제 다 풀렸어요."], ["I didn’t know, and now I do know, and that shows the advantage of asking--don’t it?’", "몰랐던 걸 이제 알게 됐으니, 이것만 봐도 여쭤보는 게 이롭다는 걸 알 수 있죠 — 안 그래요?\""]], [["I believed that Steerforth had said what he had, in jest, or to draw Miss Dartle out; and I expected him to say as much when she was gone, and we two were sitting before the fire.", "나는 스티어포스가 그런 말을 농담으로, 또는 다틀 양의 속내를 끌어내려고 했으리라 믿었다. 그래서 그녀가 가고 우리 둘만 난롯가에 앉게 되면 그가 그렇게 말하리라 기대했다."], ["But he merely asked me what I thought of her.", "그러나 그는 그저 그녀를 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물을 뿐이었다."]], [["‘She is very clever, is she not?’ I asked.", "\"무척 영리한 사람이지, 안 그래?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Clever!", "\"영리하다고!"], ["She brings everything to a grindstone,’ said Steerforth, and sharpens it, as she has sharpened her own face and figure these years past.", "그 여자는 뭐든 숫돌에 갖다 대고,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"갈아 버려."], ["She has worn herself away by constant sharpening.", "지난 몇 년간 자기 얼굴과 몸매를 갈아 댄 것처럼 말이야. 끊임없이 갈아 대느라 자기 자신을 닳아 없애 버린 거지."], ["She is all edge.’", "온통 날만 서 있어.\""]], [["‘What a remarkable scar that is upon her lip!’ I said.", "\"입술에 난 저 흉터, 참 눈에 띄던데!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Steerforth’s face fell, and he paused a moment.", "스티어포스의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 그는 잠시 말을 멈췄다."]], [["‘Why, the fact is,’ he returned, ‘I did that.’", "\"음, 사실은,\" 그가 받았다. \"내가 저렇게 만든 거야.\""]], [["‘By an unfortunate accident!’", "\"불행한 사고로!\""]], [["‘No. I was a young boy, and she exasperated me, and I threw a hammer at her.", "\"아니야. 나는 어린 소년이었고, 저 여자가 나를 약 올렸지."], ["A promising young angel I must have been!’ I was deeply sorry to have touched on such a painful theme, but that was useless now.", "그래서 망치를 던졌어. 어지간히 될성부른 어린 천사였던 셈이지!\" 나는 이런 가슴 아픈 화제를 건드린 게 몹시 미안했으나, 이제는 소용없는 일이었다."]], [["‘She has borne the mark ever since, as you see,’ said Steerforth; ‘and she’ll bear it to her grave, if she ever rests in one--though I can hardly believe she will ever rest anywhere.", "\"보다시피 저 여자는 그 후로 줄곧 저 자국을 지니고 있어.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그리고 무덤에 들어가서도 지니고 있을 거야, 언젠가 무덤에서 쉴 수나 있다면 말이지 — 하긴 저 여자가 어디서든 쉴 수 있으리라곤 도무지 믿기지 않지만."], ["She was the motherless child of a sort of cousin of my father’s.", "저 여자는 우리 아버지의 사촌뻘 되는 어떤 분의, 어미 없는 자식이었어."], ["He died one day. My mother, who was then a widow, brought her here to be company to her.", "그분이 어느 날 돌아가셨지. 그때 과부였던 우리 어머니가 말벗 삼으려고 저 여자를 이리로 데려왔어."], ["She has a couple of thousand pounds of her own, and saves the interest of it every year, to add to the principal.", "저 여자한테는 제 몫으로 이천 파운드쯤이 있는데, 해마다 그 이자를 아껴 모아서 원금에 보태지."], ["There’s the history of Miss Rosa Dartle for you.’", "자, 이게 로자 다틀 양의 내력이야.\""]], [["‘And I have no doubt she loves you like a brother?’ said I.", "\"그래도 저분은 분명 너를 오라비처럼 사랑할 거야?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Humph!’ retorted Steerforth, looking at the fire.", "\"흥!\" 스티어포스가 난롯불을 바라보며 받아쳤다."], ["‘Some brothers are not loved over much; and some love--but help yourself, Copperfield!", "\"어떤 오라비들은 그리 많이 사랑받지 못하고, 또 어떤 사랑은 — 자, 마음껏 들어, 코퍼필드!"], ["We’ll drink the daisies of the field, in compliment to you; and the lilies of the valley that toil not, neither do they spin, in compliment to me--the more shame for me!’ A moody smile that had overspread his features cleared off as he said this merrily, and he was his own frank, winning self again.", "들판의 데이지꽃을 위해 마시자, 너를 기리며. 그리고 수고도 길쌈도 하지 않는 골짜기의 백합을 위해서도 마시자, 나를 기리며 — 그건 나로서는 더 부끄러운 일이지만!\" 그의 이목구비에 번졌던 침울한 미소가 이 말을 명랑하게 내뱉는 사이 걷혔고, 그는 다시 그 솔직하고 사람을 사로잡는 본래의 모습으로 돌아왔다."]], [["I could not help glancing at the scar with a painful interest when we went in to tea.", "차를 마시러 들어갔을 때, 나는 가슴 아픈 관심으로 그 흉터를 흘끔거리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["It was not long before I observed that it was the most susceptible part of her face, and that, when she turned pale, that mark altered first, and became a dull, lead-coloured streak, lengthening out to its full extent, like a mark in invisible ink brought to the fire.", "이내 나는 그것이 그녀 얼굴에서 가장 예민한 부분이며, 그녀의 얼굴이 창백해지면 그 자국이 가장 먼저 변해, 마치 불에 쬐어 드러난 투명 잉크 자국처럼, 칙칙한 납빛 줄로 길게 본래 길이만큼 늘어진다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["There was a little altercation between her and Steerforth about a cast of the dice at backgammon--when I thought her, for one moment, in a storm of rage; and then I saw it start forth like the old writing on the wall.", "백개먼 주사위 던지기를 두고 그녀와 스티어포스 사이에 작은 말다툼이 일었는데 — 한순간 그녀가 분노의 폭풍에 휩싸였다고 느껴졌다. 그러자 그 자국이 마치 옛날 벽에 떠올랐던 글씨처럼 불쑥 솟아나는 것을 나는 보았다."]], [["It was no matter of wonder to me to find Mrs. Steerforth devoted to her son.", "스티어포스 부인이 아들에게 푹 빠져 있다는 걸 알게 된 건 나에게 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었다."], ["She seemed to be able to speak or think about nothing else.", "그녀는 아들 말고는 아무것도 말하거나 생각할 수 없는 사람 같았다."], ["She showed me his picture as an infant, in a locket, with some of his baby-hair in it; she showed me his picture as he had been when I first knew him; and she wore at her breast his picture as he was now.", "그녀는 나에게 아들이 갓난아기였을 적의 그림을, 그 안에 아기 적 머리카락 몇 올을 넣은 로켓에 담아 보여 주었고, 내가 처음 알았을 무렵의 그림도 보여 주었으며, 지금 모습을 담은 그림은 가슴에 달고 있었다."], ["All the letters he had ever written to her, she kept in a cabinet near her own chair by the fire; and she would have read me some of them, and I should have been very glad to hear them too, if he had not interposed, and coaxed her out of the design.", "아들이 그동안 그녀에게 쓴 편지란 편지는 죄다 난롯가 자기 의자 옆 작은 장 안에 간직해 두었는데, 만일 그가 끼어들어 어머니를 어르고 달래 그 마음을 돌려놓지 않았더라면, 그녀는 그중 몇 통을 나에게 읽어 주었을 것이고, 나도 그 편지들을 무척 기쁘게 들었을 것이다."]], [["‘It was at Mr. Creakle’s, my son tells me, that you first became acquainted,’ said Mrs.", "\"내 아들 말로는, 두 사람이 처음 알게 된 게 크리클 씨 학교에서였다더구나.\""], ["Steerforth, as she and I were talking at one table, while they played backgammon at another.", "스티어포스 부인이, 저쪽 탁자에서 두 사람이 백개먼을 두는 동안 이쪽 탁자에서 나와 이야기를 나누며 말했다."], ["‘Indeed, I recollect his speaking, at that time, of a pupil younger than himself who had taken his fancy there; but your name, as you may suppose, has not lived in my memory.’", "\"그러고 보니 그 무렵 아들이, 그곳에서 마음에 든 자기보다 어린 학생 하나에 대해 말하던 게 기억나는구나. 하지만 짐작하겠지만, 네 이름까지는 내 기억에 남아 있지 않았단다.\""]], [["‘He was very generous and noble to me in those days, I assure you, ma’am,’ said I, ‘and I stood in need of such a friend.", "\"그 시절 그 친구는 저에게 참으로 너그럽고 고매했답니다, 부인.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 저에게는 그런 친구가 절실했지요."], ["I should have been quite crushed without him.’", "그 친구가 없었다면 저는 완전히 짓눌려 버렸을 거예요.\""]], [["‘He is always generous and noble,’ said Mrs. Steerforth, proudly.", "\"그 애는 언제나 너그럽고 고매하지.\" 스티어포스 부인이 자랑스럽게 말했다."]], [["I subscribed to this with all my heart, God knows.", "나는 진심으로 그 말에 동의했고, 그건 하느님도 아신다."], ["She knew I did; for the stateliness of her manner already abated towards me, except when she spoke in praise of him, and then her air was always lofty.", "그녀도 내가 그런다는 것을 알았다. 그녀의 위엄 어린 태도가 나를 대할 때는 이미 누그러져 있었기 때문이다. 다만 그녀가 아들을 칭찬할 때만은 예외였는데, 그럴 때면 그 기색이 늘 도도해졌다."]], [["‘It was not a fit school generally for my son,’ said she; ‘far from it; but there were particular circumstances to be considered at the time, of more importance even than that selection.", "\"그 학교가 대체로 내 아들에게 어울리는 학교는 아니었지.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"어울리기는커녕."], ["My son’s high spirit made it desirable that he should be placed with some man who felt its superiority, and would be content to bow himself before it; and we found such a man there.’", "하지만 그 무렵에는 학교 선택보다도 더 중요하게 고려해야 할 특별한 사정들이 있었단다. 내 아들의 드높은 기상 탓에, 그 우월함을 알아보고 그 앞에 기꺼이 머리 숙일 만한 사람 밑에 두는 것이 바람직했는데, 우리는 그런 사람을 그곳에서 찾아냈던 거야.\""]], [["I knew that, knowing the fellow. And yet I did not despise him the more for it, but thought it a redeeming quality in him if he could be allowed any grace for not resisting one so irresistible as Steerforth.", "나는 그자를 아니까, 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았다. 그래도 그 때문에 그자를 더 경멸하지는 않았고, 오히려 스티어포스처럼 거역할 수 없는 사람에게 맞서지 않은 점에 조금이나마 면죄부를 줄 수 있다면, 그것은 그자의 한 가지 미덕이라고 여겼다."]], [["‘My son’s great capacity was tempted on, there, by a feeling of voluntary emulation and conscious pride,’ the fond lady went on to say.", "\"내 아들의 큰 재능은, 그곳에서 자발적인 경쟁심과 스스로에 대한 긍지로 한껏 북돋워졌단다.\" 그 다정한 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["‘He would have risen against all constraint; but he found himself the monarch of the place, and he haughtily determined to be worthy of his station.", "\"그 애는 어떤 속박에도 들고일어났을 테지. 그러나 그곳에서 자기가 그 세계의 군주임을 깨닫고는, 자기 지위에 걸맞은 사람이 되기로 도도하게 마음먹은 거야."], ["It was like himself.’", "그 애다운 일이지.\""]], [["I echoed, with all my heart and soul, that it was like himself.", "나는 온 마음과 영혼을 다해, 정말 그 친구다운 일이라고 맞장구쳤다."]], [["‘So my son took, of his own will, and on no compulsion, to the course in which he can always, when it is his pleasure, outstrip every competitor,’ she pursued.", "\"그렇게 내 아들은, 제 뜻으로, 누가 시켜서가 아니라, 마음만 먹으면 언제든 어떤 경쟁자도 앞지를 수 있는 그 길로 나아간 거야.\""], ["‘My son informs me, Mr.", "그녀가 계속했다."], ["Copperfield, that you were quite devoted to him, and that when you met yesterday you made yourself known to him with tears of joy.", "\"내 아들이 그러는데, 코퍼필드 군, 자네가 그 애를 무척 따랐고, 어제 만났을 때 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 그 애에게 자네를 알렸다더구나."], ["I should be an affected woman if I made any pretence of being surprised by my son’s inspiring such emotions; but I cannot be indifferent to anyone who is so sensible of his merit, and I am very glad to see you here, and can assure you that he feels an unusual friendship for you, and that you may rely on his protection.’", "내 아들이 그런 감정을 불러일으킨 것을 두고 놀란 척이라도 한다면 나는 가식적인 여자가 되겠지. 하지만 그 애의 됨됨이를 그토록 알아주는 사람이라면 누구든 나는 무심할 수가 없단다. 그러니 자네가 이곳에 와 주어 무척 기쁘고, 단언컨대 그 애가 자네에게 남다른 우정을 느끼고 있으니, 자네는 그 애의 보살핌에 기댈 수 있을 거야.\""]], [["Miss Dartle played backgammon as eagerly as she did everything else.", "다틀 양은 무슨 일이든 그랬듯 백개먼도 열심히 두었다."], ["If I had seen her, first, at the board, I should have fancied that her figure had got thin, and her eyes had got large, over that pursuit, and no other in the world.", "만일 내가 그녀를 게임판 앞에서 처음 보았더라면, 세상 다른 무엇도 아닌 오로지 그 놀이에 빠져 몸이 야위고 눈이 커졌으리라 짐작했을 것이다."], ["But I am very much mistaken if she missed a word of this, or lost a look of mine as I received it with the utmost pleasure, and honoured by Mrs. Steerforth’s confidence, felt older than I had done since I left Canterbury.", "그러나 그녀가 이 대화 가운데 단 한마디라도 놓쳤다거나, 내가 더없는 기쁨으로 그 말을 받아들이며 스티어포스 부인의 신뢰에 우쭐해져 캔터베리를 떠난 이래 가장 어른이 된 듯한 기분을 느낄 때 내 표정 하나라도 빠뜨렸다면, 그건 내가 크게 잘못 본 것이리라."]], [["When the evening was pretty far spent, and a tray of glasses and decanters came in, Steerforth promised, over the fire, that he would seriously think of going down into the country with me.", "저녁이 꽤 깊어, 유리잔과 술병을 얹은 쟁반이 들어왔을 때, 스티어포스는 난롯가에서 나와 함께 시골에 내려가는 일을 진지하게 생각해 보겠노라 약속했다."], ["There was no hurry, he said; a week hence would do; and his mother hospitably said the same.", "서두를 것 없다고 그가 말했다. 일주일쯤 뒤면 된다고. 그의 어머니도 인심 좋게 같은 말을 했다."], ["While we were talking, he more than once called me Daisy; which brought Miss Dartle out again.", "이야기를 나누는 동안 그는 한 번 이상 나를 데이지라고 불렀는데, 그 바람에 다틀 양이 또다시 끼어들었다."]], [["‘But really, Mr.", "\"하지만 정말요, 코퍼필드 군.\""], ["Copperfield,’ she asked, ‘is it a nickname?", "그녀가 물었다. \"그게 별명인가요?"], ["And why does he give it you?", "그리고 왜 그 애가 자네에게 그런 별명을 붙였죠?"], ["Is it--eh?--because he thinks you young and innocent?", "그게 — 그러니까 — 자네가 어리고 순진하다고 여겨서인가요?"], ["I am so stupid in these things.’", "저는 이런 일에 어찌나 둔한지.\""]], [["I coloured in replying that I believed it was.", "나는 얼굴을 붉히며, 아마 그래서일 거라고 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Miss Dartle.", "\"오!\" 다틀 양이 말했다."], ["‘Now I am glad to know that!", "\"이제 그걸 알게 되어 기뻐요!"], ["I ask for information, and I am glad to know it.", "알고 싶어서 여쭤보는 건데, 알게 되니 기쁘네요."], ["He thinks you young and innocent; and so you are his friend.", "그 애는 자네를 어리고 순진하다 여기고, 그래서 자네가 그 애의 벗인 거군요."], ["Well, that’s quite delightful!’", "어머, 참 흐뭇한 일이에요!\""]], [["She went to bed soon after this, and Mrs. Steerforth retired too.", "이 일이 있고 얼마 안 되어 그녀는 잠자리에 들었고, 스티어포스 부인도 물러갔다."], ["Steerforth and I, after lingering for half-an-hour over the fire, talking about Traddles and all the rest of them at old Salem House, went upstairs together.", "스티어포스와 나는 난롯가에서 반 시간쯤 머뭇거리며 옛 세일럼 하우스의 트래들스며 다른 친구들 모두에 대해 이야기하다가, 함께 이 층으로 올라갔다."], ["Steerforth’s room was next to mine, and I went in to look at it.", "스티어포스의 방은 내 방 옆이었는데, 나는 한번 들여다보러 들어갔다."], ["It was a picture of comfort, full of easy-chairs, cushions and footstools, worked by his mother’s hand, and with no sort of thing omitted that could help to render it complete.", "그곳은 안락함 그 자체를 그린 그림 같았다. 어머니의 손으로 수놓은 안락의자며 방석이며 발받침이 가득했고, 그 방을 완벽하게 만드는 데 보탬이 될 만한 것이라면 무엇 하나 빠진 게 없었다."], ["Finally, her handsome features looked down on her darling from a portrait on the wall, as if it were even something to her that her likeness should watch him while he slept.", "그리고 마지막으로, 벽에 걸린 초상화 속 그녀의 아름다운 이목구비가 사랑하는 아들을 내려다보고 있었는데, 마치 제 그림이나마 아들이 잠든 동안 지켜보는 것이 그녀에게 무언가 의미라도 되는 듯했다."]], [["I found the fire burning clear enough in my room by this time, and the curtains drawn before the windows and round the bed, giving it a very snug appearance.", "그 무렵 내 방의 난롯불은 제법 환히 타오르고 있었고, 창마다 그리고 침대 둘레마다 휘장이 드리워져 방이 무척 아늑해 보였다."], ["I sat down in a great chair upon the hearth to meditate on my happiness; and had enjoyed the contemplation of it for some time, when I found a likeness of Miss Dartle looking eagerly at me from above the chimney-piece.", "나는 난로 앞 큰 의자에 앉아 내 행복을 곱씹었고, 한동안 그 흐뭇한 사색에 잠겨 있었는데, 그러다 문득 다틀 양의 초상이 벽난로 위에서 나를 형형하게 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했다."]], [["It was a startling likeness, and necessarily had a startling look.", "깜짝 놀랄 만큼 닮은 초상이었고, 따라서 어쩔 수 없이 깜짝 놀랄 만한 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["The painter hadn’t made the scar, but I made it; and there it was, coming and going; now confined to the upper lip as I had seen it at dinner, and now showing the whole extent of the wound inflicted by the hammer, as I had seen it when she was passionate.", "화가가 그 흉터를 그려 넣은 것은 아니었으나, 내가 그것을 그려 넣었다. 그래서 그 자국이 거기에 있어, 떠올랐다 사라지기를 거듭했다. 어떤 때는 저녁 식사 자리에서 보았던 대로 윗입술에만 머물러 있다가, 어떤 때는 그녀가 격앙되었을 때 보았던 대로 망치가 낸 상처의 온 길이를 그대로 드러냈다."]], [["I wondered peevishly why they couldn’t put her anywhere else instead of quartering her on me.", "나는 짜증스럽게도, 왜 하필 그녀를 다른 데 두지 않고 내 방에 떠넘겼을까 의아했다."], ["To get rid of her, I undressed quickly, extinguished my light, and went to bed.", "그녀에게서 벗어나려고 나는 얼른 옷을 벗고, 불을 끄고, 잠자리에 들었다."], ["But, as I fell asleep, I could not forget that she was still there looking, ‘Is it really, though?", "그러나 잠이 드는 순간에도, 그녀가 여전히 거기서 \"그런데 정말 그런가요? 알고 싶어요\""], ["I want to know’; and when I awoke in the night, I found that I was uneasily asking all sorts of people in my dreams whether it really was or not--without knowing what I meant.", "하고 바라보고 있다는 것을 잊을 수가 없었다. 그리고 밤중에 깨어났을 때, 나는 꿈속에서 온갖 사람들에게 그게 정말 그런지 아닌지를 불안하게 묻고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다 — 무슨 뜻으로 그러는지도 모른 채."]], [["# CHAPTER 21. LITTLE EM’LY", "# CHAPTER 21. LITTLE EM’LY"]], [["There was a servant in that house, a man who, I understood, was usually with Steerforth, and had come into his service at the University, who was in appearance a pattern of respectability.", "그 집에는 하인이 하나 있었는데, 내가 알기로 평소 스티어포스를 따라다니는 사내로, 대학 시절부터 그의 시중을 들기 시작했으며, 겉모습이 점잖음의 본보기 같았다."], ["I believe there never existed in his station a more respectable-looking man.", "그 신분에서 그보다 더 점잖아 보이는 사람은 일찍이 없었으리라 싶다."], ["He was taciturn, soft-footed, very quiet in his manner, deferential, observant, always at hand when wanted, and never near when not wanted; but his great claim to consideration was his respectability.", "그는 말수가 적고, 발소리가 부드러웠으며, 몸가짐이 무척 조용하고, 깍듯하고, 빈틈없이 살피며, 필요할 때면 언제나 가까이 있고, 필요 없을 때는 결코 곁에 얼씬하지 않았다. 그러나 그가 사람들의 존중을 받는 가장 큰 까닭은 바로 그 점잖음이었다."], ["He had not a pliant face, he had rather a stiff neck, rather a tight smooth head with short hair clinging to it at the sides, a soft way of speaking, with a peculiar habit of whispering the letter S so distinctly, that he seemed to use it oftener than any other man; but every peculiarity that he had he made respectable.", "그는 잘 휘는 얼굴이 아니라 오히려 뻣뻣한 목을 가졌고, 다소 빳빳하고 매끈한 머리에는 짧은 머리카락이 양옆으로 달라붙어 있었으며, 말투가 부드러운 데다가, S 음을 어찌나 또렷하게 속삭이는 묘한 버릇이 있던지 다른 어떤 사람보다도 그 글자를 더 자주 쓰는 것처럼 들렸다. 하지만 그는 자기가 지닌 온갖 특이한 점을 죄다 점잖아 보이게 만들었다."], ["If his nose had been upside-down, he would have made that respectable.", "코가 거꾸로 붙어 있었다 해도 그는 그것마저 점잖아 보이게 만들었을 것이다."], ["He surrounded himself with an atmosphere of respectability, and walked secure in it.", "그는 점잖음의 분위기로 자기 둘레를 감싸고, 그 안에서 든든하게 걸어 다녔다."], ["It would have been next to impossible to suspect him of anything wrong, he was so thoroughly respectable.", "그가 무언가 잘못을 저질렀으리라고 의심하기란 거의 불가능에 가까웠다. 그만큼 그는 철저하게 점잖았다."], ["Nobody could have thought of putting him in a livery, he was so highly respectable.", "그에게 하인 제복을 입힐 생각을 할 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그만큼 그는 더없이 점잖았다."], ["To have imposed any derogatory work upon him, would have been to inflict a wanton insult on the feelings of a most respectable man.", "그에게 천한 일을 떠맡긴다는 것은, 더없이 점잖은 사람의 감정에 까닭 없는 모욕을 안기는 짓이나 마찬가지였을 것이다."], ["And of this, I noticed--the women-servants in the household were so intuitively conscious, that they always did such work themselves, and generally while he read the paper by the pantry fire.", "그래서 나는 알아차렸는데 — 이 집 하녀들은 그 사실을 어찌나 본능으로 꿰뚫고 있던지, 그런 일은 늘 자기들이 직접 했고, 대개는 그가 식료품실 난롯가에서 신문을 읽는 동안 해치웠다."]], [["Such a self-contained man I never saw.", "그토록 자기 안에 갇혀 사는 사람을 나는 본 적이 없다."], ["But in that quality, as in every other he possessed, he only seemed to be the more respectable.", "그러나 그 자질도, 그가 지닌 다른 온갖 자질과 마찬가지로, 그를 더욱 점잖아 보이게만 할 뿐이었다."], ["Even the fact that no one knew his Christian name, seemed to form a part of his respectability.", "그의 세례명을 아무도 모른다는 사실조차도 그의 점잖음의 한 부분을 이루는 듯했다."], ["Nothing could be objected against his surname, Littimer, by which he was known.", "그가 세상에 알려진 성, 리티머에 대해서는 아무도 트집을 잡을 수 없었다."], ["Peter might have been hanged, or Tom transported; but Littimer was perfectly respectable.", "피터라면 교수형을 당했을 수도 있고, 톰이라면 유배를 갔을 수도 있겠지만, 리티머는 완벽하게 점잖았다."]], [["It was occasioned, I suppose, by the reverend nature of respectability in the abstract, but I felt particularly young in this man’s presence.", "추상으로 따진 점잖음이라는 것의 거룩한 성질 탓이었지 싶은데, 나는 이 사내 앞에 있으면 유난히 어리게 느껴졌다."], ["How old he was himself, I could not guess--and that again went to his credit on the same score; for in the calmness of respectability he might have numbered fifty years as well as thirty.", "그 자신이 몇 살인지는 짐작조차 할 수 없었는데 — 그 점 역시 같은 이유로 그의 신용을 높여 주었다. 점잖음의 차분함 속에서라면 그는 쉰 살이라 해도, 서른 살이라 해도 어울렸을 테니까."]], [["Littimer was in my room in the morning before I was up, to bring me that reproachful shaving-water, and to put out my clothes.", "아침이면 리티머는 내가 일어나기도 전에 내 방에 들어와, 그 책망하는 듯한 면도용 물을 가져다 놓고 옷을 챙겨 두었다."], ["When I undrew the curtains and looked out of bed, I saw him, in an equable temperature of respectability, unaffected by the east wind of January, and not even breathing frostily, standing my boots right and left in the first dancing position, and blowing specks of dust off my coat as he laid it down like a baby.", "내가 침대에서 커튼을 걷고 밖을 내다보면, 그는 일월의 동풍에도 아랑곳없이 점잖음의 한결같은 온도 속에서, 입김 한 번 서리지 않고 서서, 내 장화를 발레의 첫 동작처럼 좌우로 가지런히 세워 놓고, 내 외투를 갓난아기 뉘듯 눕히며 거기에 앉은 먼지 티끌을 후후 불어 내고 있었다."]], [["I gave him good morning, and asked him what o’clock it was. He took out of his pocket the most respectable hunting-watch I ever saw, and preventing the spring with his thumb from opening far, looked in at the face as if he were consulting an oracular oyster, shut it up again, and said, if I pleased, it was half past eight.", "내가 좋은 아침이라 인사하며 몇 시냐고 물으면, 그는 주머니에서 내가 본 것 가운데 가장 점잖은 회중시계를 꺼내, 용수철이 너무 활짝 열리지 않도록 엄지로 누른 채, 마치 신탁을 내리는 굴이라도 들여다보듯 시계판을 살피더니, 다시 탁 닫고는, 괜찮으시다면 여덟 시 반이라고 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Steerforth will be glad to hear how you have rested, sir.’", "\"스티어포스 도련님께서 도련님이 잘 주무셨는지 들으시면 기뻐하실 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ said I, ‘very well indeed.", "\"고맙네.\" 내가 말했다. \"아주 잘 잤다네."], ["Is Mr. Steerforth quite well?’", "스티어포스 군은 잘 있나?\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir, Mr.", "\"감사합니다, 도련님."], ["Steerforth is tolerably well.’ Another of his characteristics--no use of superlatives.", "스티어포스 도련님은 그런대로 잘 계십니다.\" 이것도 그의 특징 가운데 하나였다 — 최상급은 절대 쓰지 않는다는 것."], ["A cool calm medium always.", "언제나 서늘하고 차분한 중간이었다."]], [["‘Is there anything more I can have the honour of doing for you, sir?", "\"제가 더 해 드릴 영광스러운 일이 있겠습니까, 도련님?"], ["The warning-bell will ring at nine; the family take breakfast at half past nine.’", "예고 종은 아홉 시에 울립니다. 식구분들은 아홉 시 반에 아침을 드십니다.\""]], [["‘Nothing, I thank you.’", "\"없네, 고맙네.\""]], [["‘I thank YOU, sir, if you please’; and with that, and with a little inclination of his head when he passed the bed-side, as an apology for correcting me, he went out, shutting the door as delicately as if I had just fallen into a sweet sleep on which my life depended.", "\"제가 도리어 감사드립니다, 도련님, 괜찮으시다면요.\" 그러고는 침대 곁을 지나며 나를 바로잡은 것에 대한 사과인 양 고개를 살짝 숙이고는, 마치 내가 목숨이 달린 달콤한 잠에 막 빠져들기라도 한 듯 더없이 조심스럽게 문을 닫으며 나갔다."]], [["Every morning we held exactly this conversation: never any more, and never any less: and yet, invariably, however far I might have been lifted out of myself over-night, and advanced towards maturer years, by Steerforth’s companionship, or Mrs. Steerforth’s confidence, or Miss Dartle’s conversation, in the presence of this most respectable man I became, as our smaller poets sing, ‘a boy again’.", "매일 아침 우리는 한 치도 더하지 않고 한 치도 덜하지 않은, 꼭 이만큼의 대화를 나누었다. 그런데도, 전날 밤 스티어포스와 어울리며, 혹은 스티어포스 부인의 신임을 받으며, 혹은 다틀 양과 이야기를 나누며 내 자신을 한껏 벗어나 더 어른스러운 나이로 나아갔다 한들, 이 더없이 점잖은 사내 앞에 서기만 하면, 우리네 군소 시인들이 노래하듯 나는 어김없이 '다시 소년'이 되고 말았다."]], [["He got horses for us; and Steerforth, who knew everything, gave me lessons in riding.", "그는 우리를 위해 말을 구해 왔고, 모르는 게 없는 스티어포스는 나에게 승마를 가르쳐 주었다."], ["He provided foils for us, and Steerforth gave me lessons in fencing--gloves, and I began, of the same master, to improve in boxing.", "그는 우리에게 펜싱용 검을 마련해 주었고, 스티어포스는 펜싱을 가르쳐 주었다 — 권투 장갑도 마련해 주어, 나는 같은 스승에게서 권투 솜씨를 늘리기 시작했다."], ["It gave me no manner of concern that Steerforth should find me a novice in these sciences, but I never could bear to show my want of skill before the respectable Littimer.", "이런 기술에서 스티어포스가 나를 풋내기로 여기는 것은 조금도 거리낄 게 없었으나, 점잖은 리티머 앞에서 내 서투른 솜씨를 드러내는 것만은 도무지 견딜 수 없었다."], ["I had no reason to believe that Littimer understood such arts himself; he never led me to suppose anything of the kind, by so much as the vibration of one of his respectable eyelashes; yet whenever he was by, while we were practising, I felt myself the greenest and most inexperienced of mortals.", "리티머 자신이 그런 기술을 안다고 믿을 만한 근거는 없었다. 그는 자기의 점잖은 속눈썹 하나 떨어 보이는 일 없이, 그런 짐작을 하게 만든 적이 없었다. 그런데도 우리가 연습할 때 그가 곁에 있기만 하면, 나는 내가 세상에서 가장 풋내기에다 가장 미숙한 인간처럼 느껴졌다."]], [["I am particular about this man, because he made a particular effect on me at that time, and because of what took place thereafter.", "내가 이 사내에 대해 이렇게 시시콜콜 이야기하는 것은, 그가 그 무렵 나에게 유난히 강한 인상을 남겼기 때문이고, 또 그 뒤에 벌어진 일 때문이기도 하다."]], [["The week passed away in a most delightful manner.", "그 한 주는 더없이 즐겁게 흘러갔다."], ["It passed rapidly, as may be supposed, to one entranced as I was; and yet it gave me so many occasions for knowing Steerforth better, and admiring him more in a thousand respects, that at its close I seemed to have been with him for a much longer time.", "짐작하다시피, 나처럼 넋이 나간 사람에게 시간은 쏜살같이 지나갔다. 그런데도 그 한 주는 스티어포스를 더 깊이 알고 천 가지 면에서 그를 더 우러르게 할 기회를 어찌나 많이 안겨 주었던지, 한 주가 끝날 무렵에는 훨씬 오래 그와 함께 지낸 것만 같았다."], ["A dashing way he had of treating me like a plaything, was more agreeable to me than any behaviour he could have adopted.", "그가 나를 장난감처럼 다루는 그 호기로운 방식은, 그가 취할 수 있었던 다른 어떤 태도보다도 나에게 흐뭇했다."], ["It reminded me of our old acquaintance; it seemed the natural sequel of it; it showed me that he was unchanged; it relieved me of any uneasiness I might have felt, in comparing my merits with his, and measuring my claims upon his friendship by any equal standard; above all, it was a familiar, unrestrained, affectionate demeanour that he used towards no one else.", "그것은 우리의 오랜 사이를 떠올리게 했고, 그 사이의 자연스러운 연장처럼 느껴졌으며, 그가 조금도 변하지 않았음을 보여 주었고, 내 장점을 그의 장점과 견주거나 우리 우정에 대한 내 몫을 무슨 대등한 잣대로 재면서 품었을 법한 어떤 거북함도 덜어 주었다. 무엇보다도 그것은 그가 다른 누구에게도 보이지 않는, 친근하고 거리낌 없고 다정한 태도였다."], ["As he had treated me at school differently from all the rest, I joyfully believed that he treated me in life unlike any other friend he had.", "학교에서 그가 나를 다른 누구와도 다르게 대했듯이, 나는 그가 인생에서도 다른 어떤 친구와도 다르게 나를 대한다고 기쁘게 믿었다."], ["I believed that I was nearer to his heart than any other friend, and my own heart warmed with attachment to him.", "나는 내가 다른 어떤 친구보다도 그의 마음에 더 가깝다고 믿었고, 내 마음은 그에 대한 애착으로 따뜻하게 데워졌다."], ["He made up his mind to go with me into the country, and the day arrived for our departure.", "그는 나와 함께 시골로 가기로 마음을 굳혔고, 마침내 우리가 떠날 날이 왔다."], ["He had been doubtful at first whether to take Littimer or not, but decided to leave him at home.", "처음에 그는 리티머를 데려갈지 말지 망설였으나, 집에 두고 가기로 정했다."], ["The respectable creature, satisfied with his lot whatever it was, arranged our portmanteaux on the little carriage that was to take us into London, as if they were intended to defy the shocks of ages, and received my modestly proffered donation with perfect tranquillity.", "제 처지가 어떻든 만족하는 그 점잖은 사내는, 우리를 런던까지 데려다줄 작은 마차에 우리 여행 가방을 마치 백 년 세월의 충격에도 끄떡없도록 만들기라도 하듯 차곡차곡 실었고, 내가 조심스레 건넨 사례를 더없이 평온하게 받았다."]], [["We bade adieu to Mrs. Steerforth and Miss Dartle, with many thanks on my part, and much kindness on the devoted mother’s.", "우리는 스티어포스 부인과 다틀 양에게 작별을 고했는데, 내 쪽에서는 거듭 고마움을 전했고, 그 헌신적인 어머니 쪽에서는 갖은 친절을 베풀었다."], ["The last thing I saw was Littimer’s unruffled eye; fraught, as I fancied, with the silent conviction that I was very young indeed.", "마지막으로 내 눈에 들어온 것은 흐트러짐 없는 리티머의 눈이었다. 내가 보기에 그 눈에는, 너는 정말이지 아주 어리다는 말없는 확신이 가득 차 있었다."]], [["What I felt, in returning so auspiciously to the old familiar places, I shall not endeavour to describe.", "그토록 상서롭게 옛 정든 곳으로 돌아가면서 내가 무엇을 느꼈는지는 굳이 적어 보려 하지 않겠다."], ["We went down by the Mail. I was so concerned, I recollect, even for the honour of Yarmouth, that when Steerforth said, as we drove through its dark streets to the inn, that, as well as he could make out, it was a good, queer, out-of-the-way kind of hole, I was highly pleased.", "우리는 우편 마차를 타고 내려갔다. 기억하건대 나는 야머스의 체면을 두고도 어찌나 마음을 쓰던지, 마차를 타고 어두운 거리를 지나 여관으로 가는 길에 스티어포스가 자기가 보기엔 여긴 괜찮은, 별나고, 외진 구석 같은 곳이라고 말하자 몹시 기뻤다."], ["We went to bed on our arrival (I observed a pair of dirty shoes and gaiters in connexion with my old friend the Dolphin as we passed that door), and breakfasted late in the morning.", "도착하자마자 우리는 잠자리에 들었고 (그 문을 지나칠 때 내 옛 친구 돌핀 여관과 관련된 더러운 신발 한 켤레와 각반이 눈에 띄었다), 아침에는 늦게 식사를 했다."], ["Steerforth, who was in great spirits, had been strolling about the beach before I was up, and had made acquaintance, he said, with half the boatmen in the place.", "기분이 한껏 들떠 있던 스티어포스는 내가 일어나기도 전에 바닷가를 거닐었고, 이곳 뱃사람의 절반과 안면을 텄다고 했다."], ["Moreover, he had seen, in the distance, what he was sure must be the identical house of Mr. Peggotty, with smoke coming out of the chimney; and had had a great mind, he told me, to walk in and swear he was myself grown out of knowledge.", "게다가 멀리서 굴뚝에서 연기가 피어오르는, 틀림없이 페거티 씨의 바로 그 집이 분명한 곳을 보았다면서, 안으로 쑥 들어가 자기가 몰라보게 자란 나라고 우겨 볼까 하는 생각이 굴뚝같았노라고 말했다."]], [["‘When do you propose to introduce me there, Daisy?’ he said.", "\"언제 거기로 나를 데려갈 생각인가, 데이지?\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘I am at your disposal.", "\"나야 자네 뜻대로 따르겠네."], ["Make your own arrangements.’", "자네가 알아서 정하게.\""]], [["‘Why, I was thinking that this evening would be a good time, Steerforth, when they are all sitting round the fire.", "\"글쎄, 오늘 저녁이 좋을 것 같아, 스티어포스. 다들 난롯가에 둘러앉아 있을 때 말이야."], ["I should like you to see it when it’s snug, it’s such a curious place.’", "아늑할 때 자네가 봤으면 좋겠어. 참 묘한 곳이거든.\""]], [["‘So be it!’ returned Steerforth.", "\"그럼 그렇게 하지!\" 스티어포스가 받았다."], ["‘This evening.’", "\"오늘 저녁.\""]], [["‘I shall not give them any notice that we are here, you know,’ said I, delighted.", "\"우리가 왔다는 걸 미리 알리지는 않을 거야, 알겠나.\" 내가 신이 나서 말했다."], ["‘We must take them by surprise.’", "\"깜짝 놀라게 해 줘야지.\""]], [["‘Oh, of course!", "\"오, 물론이지!"], ["It’s no fun,’ said Steerforth, ‘unless we take them by surprise.", "깜짝 놀라게 하지 않으면,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"무슨 재미가 있겠나."], ["Let us see the natives in their aboriginal condition.’", "원주민들이 천연 그대로 있는 꼴을 한번 보자고.\""]], [["‘Though they ARE that sort of people that you mentioned,’ I returned.", "\"비록 그 사람들이 자네가 말했던 그런 부류이긴 하지만 말이야.\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘Aha! What!", "\"아하! 뭐라고!"], ["you recollect my skirmishes with Rosa, do you?’ he exclaimed with a quick look.", "자네 내가 로자랑 옥신각신한 걸 기억하는군, 그렇지?\" 그가 휙 눈길을 던지며 외쳤다."], ["‘Confound the girl, I am half afraid of her.", "\"그 계집애, 빌어먹을, 나는 그 애가 반쯤 무섭다네."], ["She’s like a goblin to me.", "나한테는 도깨비 같단 말이야."], ["But never mind her.", "하지만 그 애 얘긴 됐고."], ["Now what are you going to do?", "자, 자네 이제 뭘 할 텐가?"], ["You are going to see your nurse, I suppose?’", "유모를 만나러 가겠지, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘Why, yes,’ I said, ‘I must see Peggotty first of all.’", "\"응, 그래.\" 내가 말했다. \"무엇보다 먼저 페거티를 만나야지.\""]], [["‘Well,’ replied Steerforth, looking at his watch.", "\"좋아.\" 스티어포스가 시계를 보며 대답했다."], ["‘Suppose I deliver you up to be cried over for a couple of hours.", "\"내가 자네를 두어 시간쯤 실컷 눈물바다에 내맡겨 줄까."], ["Is that long enough?’", "그 정도면 넉넉한가?\""]], [["I answered, laughing, that I thought we might get through it in that time, but that he must come also; for he would find that his renown had preceded him, and that he was almost as great a personage as I was.", "나는 웃으며, 그 시간이면 그 일을 다 치를 수 있을 것 같다고, 하지만 자네도 와야 한다고 대답했다. 가 보면 자네의 명성이 이미 자네보다 먼저 당도해 있을 테고, 자네도 나만큼이나 대단한 인물이라는 걸 알게 될 테니까."]], [["‘I’ll come anywhere you like,’ said Steerforth, ‘or do anything you like.", "\"자네가 가라는 데면 어디든 가겠네.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"자네가 시키는 거면 뭐든 하겠어."], ["Tell me where to come to; and in two hours I’ll produce myself in any state you please, sentimental or comical.’", "어디로 가면 되는지 일러 주게. 두 시간 뒤에 자네가 원하는 모습으로, 감상적이든 익살맞든, 나타나겠네.\""]], [["I gave him minute directions for finding the residence of Mr. Barkis, carrier to Blunderstone and elsewhere; and, on this understanding, went out alone.", "나는 그에게 블런더스톤을 비롯한 여러 곳을 다니는 짐마차꾼 바키스 씨의 거처를 찾아오는 길을 시시콜콜 일러 준 다음, 이렇게 약속하고 혼자 나섰다."], ["There was a sharp bracing air; the ground was dry; the sea was crisp and clear; the sun was diffusing abundance of light, if not much warmth; and everything was fresh and lively.", "살갗을 에는 듯 상쾌한 바람이 불었고, 땅은 메말랐으며, 바다는 또렷하고 맑았다. 햇살은 온기는 그리 많지 않아도 빛만은 넉넉히 뿌리고 있었고, 모든 것이 싱그럽고 생기가 넘쳤다."], ["I was so fresh and lively myself, in the pleasure of being there, that I could have stopped the people in the streets and shaken hands with them.", "나 자신도 그곳에 있다는 기쁨에 어찌나 싱그럽고 생기가 넘치던지, 거리에서 사람들을 붙잡고 악수라도 청하고 싶을 지경이었다."]], [["The streets looked small, of course.", "물론 거리는 작아 보였다."], ["The streets that we have only seen as children always do, I believe, when we go back to them. But I had forgotten nothing in them, and found nothing changed, until I came to Mr.", "어릴 때만 보았던 거리란 다시 가 보면 으레 그런 법이지 싶다. 그러나 나는 그 거리에서 무엇 하나 잊지 않았고, 무엇 하나 바뀐 것을 찾지 못했다 — 오머 씨의 가게에 이르기 전까지는."], ["Omer’s shop. OMER AND Joram was now written up, where OMER used to be; but the inscription, DRAPER, TAILOR, HABERDASHER, FUNERAL FURNISHER, &c., remained as it was.", "예전에 그냥 '오머'라고만 적혀 있던 자리에 이제는 '오머와 조램'이라 적혀 있었으나, '포목상, 양복점, 잡화상, 장의용품 일체' 운운하는 문구는 옛날 그대로였다."]], [["My footsteps seemed to tend so naturally to the shop door, after I had read these words from over the way, that I went across the road and looked in.", "길 건너편에서 이 글귀를 읽고 나자, 내 발걸음은 어찌나 자연스럽게 가게 문 쪽으로 향하던지, 나는 길을 건너 안을 들여다보았다."], ["There was a pretty woman at the back of the shop, dancing a little child in her arms, while another little fellow clung to her apron.", "가게 안쪽에 어여쁜 여인이 어린 아기를 품에 안고 어르고 있었고, 또 다른 어린 녀석 하나가 그녀의 앞치마에 매달려 있었다."], ["I had no difficulty in recognizing either Minnie or Minnie’s children.", "미니인지, 미니의 아이들인지 알아보기는 조금도 어렵지 않았다."], ["The glass door of the parlour was not open; but in the workshop across the yard I could faintly hear the old tune playing, as if it had never left off.", "거실로 통하는 유리문은 열려 있지 않았으나, 마당 건너편 작업장에서는 마치 한 번도 멈춘 적 없다는 듯 그 옛 가락이 희미하게 들려왔다."]], [["‘Is Mr. Omer at home?’ said I, entering.", "\"오머 씨 댁에 계신가요?\""], ["‘I should like to see him, for a moment, if he is.’", "내가 들어서며 말했다. \"계시다면 잠깐 뵙고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh yes, sir, he is at home,’ said Minnie; ‘the weather don’t suit his asthma out of doors.", "\"아, 네, 손님, 안에 계세요.\" 미니가 말했다. \"이 날씨가 아버지 천식에 안 좋아서 바깥에는 못 나가시거든요."], ["Joe, call your grandfather!’", "조, 가서 할아버지 불러 오렴!\""]], [["The little fellow, who was holding her apron, gave such a lusty shout, that the sound of it made him bashful, and he buried his face in her skirts, to her great admiration.", "그녀의 앞치마를 붙잡고 있던 어린 녀석이 어찌나 우렁차게 소리를 지르던지, 제 소리에 제가 부끄러워져서는 어머니 치맛자락에 얼굴을 파묻었고, 어머니는 그 모습을 흐뭇하게 바라보았다."], ["I heard a heavy puffing and blowing coming towards us, and soon Mr. Omer, shorter-winded than of yore, but not much older-looking, stood before me.", "묵직하게 헐떡이며 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬는 소리가 우리 쪽으로 다가오더니, 이내 오머 씨가 내 앞에 섰다. 예전보다 숨은 더 가빠졌으되 얼굴은 그리 늙어 보이지 않았다."]], [["‘Servant, sir,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"어서 오십시오, 손님.\""], ["‘What can I do for you, sir?’ ‘You can shake hands with me, Mr.", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"무엇을 도와드릴까요, 손님?\""], ["Omer, if you please,’ said I, putting out my own.", "\"괜찮으시다면 저와 악수나 한번 해 주십시오, 오머 씨.\""], ["‘You were very good-natured to me once, when I am afraid I didn’t show that I thought so.’", "내가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"예전에 저에게 무척 잘해 주셨는데, 그때 제가 고마운 줄도 모르는 것처럼 굴었던 것 같아서요.\""]], [["‘Was I though?’ returned the old man.", "\"내가 그랬던가요?\" 노인이 받았다."], ["‘I’m glad to hear it, but I don’t remember when.", "\"그렇다니 반갑소만, 언제였는지는 기억이 안 나는구려."], ["Are you sure it was me?’", "정말 나였소?\" \"틀림없습니다.\""]], [["‘Quite.’\n‘I think my memory has got as short as my breath,’ said Mr.", "\"내 기억력도 이젠 내 숨처럼 짧아진 모양이오.\""], ["Omer, looking at me and shaking his head; ‘for I don’t remember you.’", "오머 씨가 나를 바라보며 고개를 가로저었다. \"도무지 손님이 기억나질 않으니 말이오.\""]], [["‘Don’t you remember your coming to the coach to meet me, and my having breakfast here, and our riding out to Blunderstone together: you, and I, and Mrs.", "\"저를 마중하러 역마차까지 나와 주셨던 거, 제가 여기서 아침을 먹었던 거, 그리고 우리가 다 같이 블런더스톤까지 마차를 타고 갔던 거 기억 안 나세요?"], ["Joram, and Mr. Joram too--who wasn’t her husband then?’", "어르신하고, 저하고, 조램 부인하고, 조램 씨도요 — 그때는 부인의 남편이 아니었지만요.\""]], [["‘Why, Lord bless my soul!’ exclaimed Mr.", "\"아니, 이런, 세상에!\""], ["Omer, after being thrown by his surprise into a fit of coughing, ‘you don’t say so!", "오머 씨가 깜짝 놀라 기침을 한바탕 쏟아낸 끝에 외쳤다. \"설마! 미니야, 얘야, 기억나니?"], ["Minnie, my dear, you recollect? Dear me, yes; the party was a lady, I think?’", "저런, 그래, 그 일행에 부인이 한 분 계셨던 것 같은데, 아닌가?\""]], [["‘My mother,’ I rejoined.", "\"제 어머니셨지요.\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘To--be--sure,’ said Mr.", "\"옳—거니,\""], ["Omer, touching my waistcoat with his forefinger, ‘and there was a little child too!", "오머 씨가 집게손가락으로 내 조끼를 톡 건드리며 말했다. \"그리고 어린 아기도 하나 있었지!"], ["There was two parties.", "일행이 둘이었어."], ["The little party was laid along with the other party.", "그 어린 일행은 그 다른 일행과 함께 누였지."], ["Over at Blunderstone it was, of course. Dear me!", "물론 저기 블런더스톤에서 말이오. 저런, 저런!"], ["And how have you been since?’", "그래, 그동안 잘 지냈소?\""]], [["Very well, I thanked him, as I hoped he had been too.", "아주 잘 지냈다고, 어르신도 그러셨기를 바란다고 고마움을 전했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["nothing to grumble at, you know,’ said Mr. Omer.", "뭐 투덜댈 거야 없지요.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I find my breath gets short, but it seldom gets longer as a man gets older.", "\"숨이 짧아지긴 하는데, 사람이 늙는다고 숨이 길어지는 법은 좀처럼 없으니까."], ["I take it as it comes, and make the most of it.", "오는 대로 받아들이고, 그저 최대한 누리는 거지."], ["That’s the best way, ain’t it?’", "그게 제일 좋은 방법 아니겠소?\""]], [["Mr. Omer coughed again, in consequence of laughing, and was assisted out of his fit by his daughter, who now stood close beside us, dancing her smallest child on the counter.", "오머 씨는 웃다가 또 기침을 했고, 이제는 우리 곁에 바짝 다가서서 막내를 계산대 위에 올려 어르고 있던 딸이 그가 기침 발작에서 벗어나도록 거들었다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"저런!\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Yes, to be sure.", "\"그래, 옳거니."], ["Two parties!", "일행이 둘이었지!"], ["Why, in that very ride, if you’ll believe me, the day was named for my Minnie to marry Joram. “Do name it, sir,”", "그게 말이오, 글쎄 그 마차 길에서, 믿거나 말거나, 우리 미니가 조램과 혼인할 날을 잡았다오. '날을 정하시지요, 어르신.'"], ["says Joram.", "조램이 그러기에."], ["“Yes, do, father,”", "'네, 그래요, 아버지.'"], ["says Minnie.", "미니가 그러더군."], ["And now he’s come into the business.", "그래서 이제 그 사람도 이 일을 함께 하게 됐지."], ["And look here! The youngest!’", "그리고 자, 보시오! 막내요!\""]], [["Minnie laughed, and stroked her banded hair upon her temples, as her father put one of his fat fingers into the hand of the child she was dancing on the counter.", "미니가 웃으며 관자놀이께로 곱게 빗어 넘긴 머리를 쓰다듬는 사이, 아버지는 계산대 위에서 어르고 있던 아이의 손에 자기 통통한 손가락 하나를 쥐여 주었다."]], [["‘Two parties, of course!’ said Mr.", "\"물론, 일행이 둘이었지!\""], ["Omer, nodding his head retrospectively.", "오머 씨가 옛일을 돌이키며 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["‘Ex-actly so!", "\"바—로 그랬다오!"], ["And Joram’s at work, at this minute, on a grey one with silver nails, not this measurement’--the measurement of the dancing child upon the counter--‘by a good two inches.---Will you take something?’", "그리고 조램이 지금 이 순간에도, 은못을 박은 회색 관을 짜고 있는데, 그게 이만한 치수는 아니고\" — 계산대 위에서 어르던 아이의 치수를 가리키며 — \"넉넉히 두 치는 더 크지. 뭐라도 좀 드시겠소?\""]], [["I thanked him, but declined.", "고맙지만 사양했다."]], [["‘Let me see,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"가만 보자.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Barkis’s the carrier’s wife--Peggotty’s the boatman’s sister--she had something to do with your family?", "\"바키스, 그 짐마차꾼의 아내 말이오 — 페거티, 그 뱃사람의 누이 — 손님네 집안과 무슨 인연이 있었지?"], ["She was in service there, sure?’", "거기서 일했던 게 분명한데?\""]], [["My answering in the affirmative gave him great satisfaction.", "내가 그렇다고 대답하자 그는 무척 흡족해했다."]], [["‘I believe my breath will get long next, my memory’s getting so much so,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"이러다 내 숨도 다시 길어지지 싶소. 기억력이 이렇게 좋아지는 걸 보니.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Well, sir, we’ve got a young relation of hers here, under articles to us, that has as elegant a taste in the dress-making business--I assure you I don’t believe there’s a Duchess in England can touch her.’", "\"그런데 말이오, 손님, 그 사람의 어린 친척 하나가 도제로 우리 가게에 와 있는데, 옷 짓는 솜씨의 안목이 어찌나 곱던지 — 정말이지 영국 안에 그 애를 따를 공작 부인이 있을까 싶소.\""]], [["‘Not little Em’ly?’ said I, involuntarily.", "\"꼬마 에밀리 말씀인가요?\" 나도 모르게 입에서 나왔다."]], [["‘Em’ly’s her name,’ said Mr.", "\"에밀리가 그 애 이름이오.\""], ["Omer, ‘and she’s little too.", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 꼬마는 꼬마지."], ["But if you’ll believe me, she has such a face of her own that half the women in this town are mad against her.’", "한데 믿거나 말거나, 그 애 얼굴이 어찌나 제 빛깔이 곱던지 이 동네 여자들 절반이 그 애한테 이를 갈고 있다오.\""]], [["‘Nonsense, father!’ cried Minnie.", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 아버지!\" 미니가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"얘야,\""], ["Omer, ‘I don’t say it’s the case with you,’ winking at me, ‘but I say that half the women in Yarmouth--ah!", "오머 씨가 나에게 눈짓을 하며 말했다. \"너를 두고 하는 말은 아니다만, 야머스 여자들 절반이 — 아니!"], ["and in five mile round--are mad against that girl.’", "오 마일 둘레의 여자들까지 — 그 애한테 이를 갈고 있다는 말이다.\""]], [["‘Then she should have kept to her own station in life, father,’ said Minnie, ‘and not have given them any hold to talk about her, and then they couldn’t have done it.’", "\"그러면 그 애가 제 분수에 맞게 처신했어야지요, 아버지.\" 미니가 말했다. \"수군댈 빌미를 주지 말았어야지요. 그랬으면 사람들이 그러지도 못했을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Couldn’t have done it, my dear!’ retorted Mr. Omer.", "\"그러지도 못했을 거라고, 얘야!\" 오머 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Couldn’t have done it!", "\"그러지도 못했을 거라니!"], ["Is that YOUR knowledge of life?", "그게 네가 아는 세상사냐?"], ["What is there that any woman couldn’t do, that she shouldn’t do--especially on the subject of another woman’s good looks?’", "여자가 못 할 일이 어디 있느냐, 안 해야 마땅한 일이라도 — 더구나 다른 여자의 미모를 두고서야?\""]], [["I really thought it was all over with Mr. Omer, after he had uttered this libellous pleasantry.", "이 험담 섞인 농담을 내뱉고 난 뒤, 나는 오머 씨가 정말 숨이 넘어가는 줄 알았다."], ["He coughed to that extent, and his breath eluded all his attempts to recover it with that obstinacy, that I fully expected to see his head go down behind the counter, and his little black breeches, with the rusty little bunches of ribbons at the knees, come quivering up in a last ineffectual struggle.", "어찌나 기침을 해 대던지, 그리고 숨이 어찌나 끈질기게 그의 온갖 노력을 비껴가며 돌아오질 않던지, 나는 그의 머리가 계산대 뒤로 폭 꺼지고, 무릎께에 녹슨 듯한 리본 매듭을 단 그 작고 검은 반바지가 마지막 헛된 발버둥을 치며 바르르 떨려 올라오는 광경을 영락없이 보게 되리라 여겼다."], ["At length, however, he got better, though he still panted hard, and was so exhausted that he was obliged to sit on the stool of the shop-desk.", "하지만 결국 그는 좀 나아졌다. 여전히 거칠게 헐떡이긴 했고 어찌나 기진했던지 가게 책상 걸상에 주저앉을 수밖에 없었지만."]], [["‘You see,’ he said, wiping his head, and breathing with difficulty, ‘she hasn’t taken much to any companions here; she hasn’t taken kindly to any particular acquaintances and friends, not to mention sweethearts.", "\"그러니까 말이오.\" 그가 머리를 훔치며 힘겹게 숨을 몰아쉬었다. \"그 애가 여기서 또래들과 그리 어울리질 않았소."], ["In consequence, an ill-natured story got about, that Em’ly wanted to be a lady.", "딱히 친한 동무나 벗을 두지도 않았고, 정인은 더 말할 것도 없지."], ["Now my opinion is, that it came into circulation principally on account of her sometimes saying, at the school, that if she was a lady she would like to do so-and-so for her uncle--don’t you see?--and buy him such-and-such fine things.’", "그러다 보니 에밀리가 귀부인 행세를 하고 싶어 한다는 심술궂은 소문이 돌았다오. 한데 내 생각엔, 그 애가 학교에서 이따금 한 말 탓에 그 소문이 주로 퍼진 듯하오 — 자기가 귀부인이라면 외삼촌을 위해 이러저러한 일을 해 주고 싶다 — 무슨 말인지 아시겠소? — 이러저러한 좋은 물건을 사 드리고 싶다, 뭐 그런 말 말이오.\""]], [["‘I assure you, Mr.", "\"정말이지요, 오머 씨, 그 애가 저한테도 그런 말을 했답니다.\""], ["Omer, she has said so to me,’ I returned eagerly, ‘when we were both children.’", "내가 진지하게 받았다. \"둘 다 어린아이였을 때 말이에요.\""]], [["Mr. Omer nodded his head and rubbed his chin.", "오머 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 턱을 문질렀다."], ["‘Just so. Then out of a very little, she could dress herself, you see, better than most others could out of a deal, and that made things unpleasant.", "\"바로 그거요. 게다가 그 애는 아주 적은 것으로도, 보시오, 남들이 많이 들여서 차려입는 것보다 더 잘 차려입을 줄 알았으니, 그게 또 일을 거북하게 만들었지."], ["Moreover, she was rather what might be called wayward--I’ll go so far as to say what I should call wayward myself,’ said Mr.", "더구나 그 애는 좀 변덕스러운 데가 있었는데 — 내가 변덕스럽다고까지 말하겠소.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["Omer; ‘didn’t know her own mind quite--a little spoiled--and couldn’t, at first, exactly bind herself down.", "\"제 마음을 제가 잘 모른달까 — 좀 응석받이였달까 — 처음엔 도무지 제 몸을 다잡질 못했지."], ["No more than that was ever said against her, Minnie?’", "그 애를 두고 그 이상 험한 말이 나온 적은 없었지, 미니?\""]], [["‘No, father,’ said Mrs. Joram.", "\"네, 아버지.\" 조램 부인이 말했다."], ["‘That’s the worst, I believe.’", "\"그게 제일 심한 거예요, 아마.\""]], [["‘So when she got a situation,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서 그 애가 일자리를 얻었는데,\""], ["Omer, ‘to keep a fractious old lady company, they didn’t very well agree, and she didn’t stop.", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"성마른 노부인의 말동무를 하는 자리였소. 한데 둘이 영 맞질 않아서, 그 애가 오래 못 붙어 있었지."], ["At last she came here, apprenticed for three years.", "마침내 여기로 와서 삼 년 도제로 들어왔다오."], ["Nearly two of ‘em are over, and she has been as good a girl as ever was.", "그게 거의 이 년이 지났는데, 그동안 둘도 없이 착한 애였소. 여섯 사람 몫은 되지!"], ["Worth any six! Minnie, is she worth any six, now?’", "미니, 그 애가 지금 여섯 사람 몫은 되지 않니?\""]], [["‘Yes, father,’ replied Minnie.", "\"네, 아버지.\" 미니가 대답했다."], ["‘Never say I detracted from her!’", "\"제가 그 애를 깎아내렸다는 말은 절대 마세요!\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"아주 좋아.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘That’s right.", "\"그래야지."], ["And so, young gentleman,’ he added, after a few moments’ further rubbing of his chin, ‘that you may not consider me long-winded as well as short-breathed, I believe that’s all about it.’", "그러니 젊은 양반,\" 그가 잠시 턱을 더 문지른 뒤 덧붙였다. \"내가 숨이 짧은 데다 말까지 길다 여기지 않도록, 그 애 얘긴 이쯤이면 다 한 것 같소.\""]], [["As they had spoken in a subdued tone, while speaking of Em’ly, I had no doubt that she was near.", "에밀리 이야기를 할 때 두 사람이 낮은 목소리로 말하기에, 나는 그 애가 가까이 있으리라 믿어 의심치 않았다."], ["On my asking now, if that were not so, Mr. Omer nodded yes, and nodded towards the door of the parlour.", "그래서 이제 그렇지 않으냐고 묻자, 오머 씨는 그렇다고 끄덕이며 거실 문 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다."], ["My hurried inquiry if I might peep in, was answered with a free permission; and, looking through the glass, I saw her sitting at her work.", "안을 살짝 들여다봐도 되겠냐고 다급하게 묻자 선뜻 허락이 떨어졌다. 유리문 너머로 보니, 그 애가 앉아서 일을 하고 있었다."], ["I saw her, a most beautiful little creature, with the cloudless blue eyes, that had looked into my childish heart, turned laughingly upon another child of Minnie’s who was playing near her; with enough of wilfulness in her bright face to justify what I had heard; with much of the old capricious coyness lurking in it; but with nothing in her pretty looks, I am sure, but what was meant for goodness and for happiness, and what was on a good and happy course.", "보니, 더없이 어여쁜 작은 아가씨가, 일찍이 내 어린 마음속을 들여다보았던 그 구름 한 점 없는 푸른 눈을, 곁에서 놀던 미니의 또 다른 아이에게 웃으며 돌리고 있었다. 그 환한 얼굴에는 내가 들은 말을 수긍할 만큼의 고집스러움이 어려 있었고, 옛날의 그 변덕스러운 수줍음도 적잖이 깃들어 있었다. 그러나 그 어여쁜 얼굴에는, 분명히 말하건대, 선함과 행복을 위한 것, 그리고 선하고 행복한 길 위에 있는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다."]], [["The tune across the yard that seemed as if it never had left off--alas!", "마당 건너에서 한 번도 멈춘 적 없는 듯 들려오던 그 가락은 — 아아!"], ["it was the tune that never DOES leave off--was beating, softly, all the while.", "그것은 결코 멈추지 않는 가락이었다 — 내내 나직이 박자를 두드리고 있었다."]], [["‘Wouldn’t you like to step in,’ said Mr.", "\"한번 들어가서,\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["Omer, ‘and speak to her? Walk in and speak to her, sir!", "\"그 애한테 말이라도 건네 보지 않겠소? 들어가서 말을 거시오, 손님!"], ["Make yourself at home!’", "편하게 하시구려!\""]], [["I was too bashful to do so then--I was afraid of confusing her, and I was no less afraid of confusing myself.--but I informed myself of the hour at which she left of an evening, in order that our visit might be timed accordingly; and taking leave of Mr.", "그때 나는 너무 부끄러워서 그러지 못했다 — 그 애를 당황하게 할까 봐 두려웠고, 나 자신을 당황하게 할까 봐 그에 못지않게 두려웠다 — 그래서 그저 그 애가 저녁에 몇 시에 일을 마치는지를 알아 두어, 우리 방문 시각을 거기에 맞추기로 했다."], ["Omer, and his pretty daughter, and her little children, went away to my dear old Peggotty’s.", "그러고는 오머 씨와 그의 어여쁜 딸과 그 어린아이들에게 작별을 고하고, 정든 페거티에게로 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Here she was, in the tiled kitchen, cooking dinner!", "페거티는 거기, 타일 깔린 부엌에서, 저녁을 짓고 있었다!"], ["The moment I knocked at the door she opened it, and asked me what I pleased to want.", "내가 문을 두드리자마자 그녀는 문을 열더니, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."], ["I looked at her with a smile, but she gave me no smile in return.", "나는 미소를 띠고 그녀를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 답례로 미소를 보내지 않았다."], ["I had never ceased to write to her, but it must have been seven years since we had met.", "나는 그녀에게 편지 쓰는 일을 한 번도 그친 적이 없었으나, 우리가 직접 만나지 못한 지는 칠 년쯤 되었을 것이다."]], [["‘Is Mr. Barkis at home, ma’am?’ I said, feigning to speak roughly to her.", "\"바키스 씨 댁에 계신가요, 아주머니?\" 내가 일부러 퉁명스레 말하는 척하며 물었다."]], [["‘He’s at home, sir,’ returned Peggotty, ‘but he’s bad abed with the rheumatics.’", "\"댁에 계세요, 손님.\" 페거티가 받았다. \"한데 류머티즘으로 자리보전을 하고 계셔서요.\""]], [["‘Don’t he go over to Blunderstone now?’ I asked.", "\"요즘은 블런더스톤에 안 건너가시나요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘When he’s well he do,’ she answered.", "\"몸이 성하면 가시죠.\" 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["‘Do YOU ever go there, Mrs. Barkis?’", "\"아주머니는 거기 가 보신 적 있나요, 바키스 부인?\""]], [["She looked at me more attentively, and I noticed a quick movement of her hands towards each other.", "그녀가 나를 더 유심히 바라보았고, 나는 그녀의 두 손이 잽싸게 서로를 향해 모아지는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘Because I want to ask a question about a house there, that they call the--what is it?--the Rookery,’ said I.", "\"실은 거기 있는 어떤 집에 대해 여쭤볼 게 있어서요. 그 집을 뭐라고 부르더라 — 까마귀 둥지라고 했던가.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["She took a step backward, and put out her hands in an undecided frightened way, as if to keep me off.", "그녀는 한 걸음 뒤로 물러서더니, 마치 나를 막으려는 듯 머뭇머뭇 겁먹은 손길로 두 손을 내밀었다."]], [["‘Peggotty!’ I cried to her.", "\"페거티!\" 내가 그녀에게 외쳤다."]], [["She cried, ‘My darling boy!’ and we both burst into tears, and were locked in one another’s arms.", "그녀가 \"내 귀여운 도련님!\" 하고 부르짖었고, 우리 둘은 그대로 울음을 터뜨리며 서로의 품에 꼭 끌어안겼다."]], [["What extravagances she committed; what laughing and crying over me; what pride she showed, what joy, what sorrow that she whose pride and joy I might have been, could never hold me in a fond embrace; I have not the heart to tell.", "그녀가 얼마나 호들갑을 떨었는지, 나를 두고 얼마나 웃고 또 울었는지, 얼마나 자랑스러워했는지, 얼마나 기뻐하고, 또 자기가 자랑이요 기쁨으로 삼았을 법한 그 아이를 한 번도 품에 다정히 안아 주지 못했다는 사실에 얼마나 슬퍼했는지는, 차마 다 적을 마음이 나지 않는다."], ["I was troubled with no misgiving that it was young in me to respond to her emotions.", "그녀의 감정에 화답하는 것이 어린애 같은 짓이라는 꺼림칙함은 조금도 들지 않았다."], ["I had never laughed and cried in all my life, I dare say--not even to her--more freely than I did that morning.", "단언컨대 나는 평생, 그날 아침만큼 마음껏 웃고 운 적이 없었다 — 그녀에게조차도."]], [["‘Barkis will be so glad,’ said Peggotty, wiping her eyes with her apron, ‘that it’ll do him more good than pints of liniment.", "\"바키스가 무척 기뻐할 거예요.\" 페거티가 앞치마로 눈물을 훔치며 말했다. \"도련님 오신 게 연고를 몇 파인트씩 바르는 것보다 더 그이한테 약이 될 거예요."], ["May I go and tell him you are here?", "가서 도련님 오셨다고 알려도 될까요?"], ["Will you come up and see him, my dear?’", "올라가서 그이를 좀 봐 주시겠어요, 도련님?\""]], [["Of course I would.", "물론 그러겠노라 했다."], ["But Peggotty could not get out of the room as easily as she meant to, for as often as she got to the door and looked round at me, she came back again to have another laugh and another cry upon my shoulder.", "그러나 페거티는 마음먹은 만큼 쉬이 방을 나서지 못했는데, 문께에 이르러 나를 돌아볼 때마다 다시 돌아와 내 어깨에 기대 또 한 번 웃고 또 한 번 울었기 때문이다."], ["At last, to make the matter easier, I went upstairs with her; and having waited outside for a minute, while she said a word of preparation to Mr. Barkis, presented myself before that invalid.", "결국 일을 수월하게 하려고 나는 그녀와 함께 위층으로 올라갔고, 그녀가 바키스 씨에게 마음의 채비를 시키려고 몇 마디 하는 동안 잠깐 밖에서 기다린 뒤, 그 병자 앞에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["He received me with absolute enthusiasm.", "그는 그야말로 열렬하게 나를 맞았다."], ["He was too rheumatic to be shaken hands with, but he begged me to shake the tassel on the top of his nightcap, which I did most cordially.", "류머티즘이 심해 악수는 할 수 없었으나, 그는 자기 나이트캡 꼭대기에 달린 술을 흔들어 달라고 청했고, 나는 더없이 다정하게 그것을 흔들어 주었다."], ["When I sat down by the side of the bed, he said that it did him a world of good to feel as if he was driving me on the Blunderstone road again. As he lay in bed, face upward, and so covered, with that exception, that he seemed to be nothing but a face--like a conventional cherubim--he looked the queerest object I ever beheld.", "침대 옆에 앉자 그는, 다시 나를 태우고 블런더스톤 길을 모는 것 같은 기분이 들어 더없이 좋다고 했다. 그는 침대에 얼굴을 위로 향하고 누워 있었는데, 그 얼굴만 빼고는 어찌나 꽁꽁 덮여 있던지 마치 얼굴 하나만 달랑 있는 것처럼 보여서 — 흔히 그리는 천사 그림 같았다 — 내가 본 것 가운데 가장 우스꽝스러운 물건처럼 보였다."]], [["‘What name was it, as I wrote up in the cart, sir?’ said Mr. Barkis, with a slow rheumatic smile.", "\"제가 짐마차에 써 붙였던 이름이 뭐였더라, 도련님?\" 바키스 씨가 류머티즘으로 굳은 얼굴에 느릿한 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["‘Ah! Mr. Barkis, we had some grave talks about that matter, hadn’t we?’", "\"아! 바키스 씨, 우리 그 일을 두고 자못 진지한 이야기를 나눴었지요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘I was willin’ a long time, sir?’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"제가 오랫동안 마음이 있었지요, 도련님?\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘A long time,’ said I.", "\"오랫동안 그러셨죠.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘And I don’t regret it,’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"그리고 나는 후회 안 합니다.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Do you remember what you told me once, about her making all the apple parsties and doing all the cooking?’", "\"전에 도련님이 나한테 했던 말 기억하시오? 그 사람이 사과 파이를 다 굽고 요리도 도맡아 한다고 했던 거 말이오.\""]], [["‘Yes, very well,’ I returned.", "\"네, 아주 잘 기억하지요.\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘It was as true,’ said Mr.", "\"그게 다 사실이었소.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."], ["Barkis, ‘as turnips is. It was as true,’ said Mr.", "\"순무가 진짜인 것만큼이나 사실이었지. 그게 다 사실이었소.\""], ["Barkis, nodding his nightcap, which was his only means of emphasis, ‘as taxes is.", "바키스 씨가 자기로서는 유일한 강조 수단인 나이트캡을 끄덕이며 말했다. \"세금이 진짜인 것만큼이나 사실이었지."], ["And nothing’s truer than them.’", "그리고 그것들만큼 확실한 건 없잖소.\""]], [["Mr. Barkis turned his eyes upon me, as if for my assent to this result of his reflections in bed; and I gave it.", "바키스 씨는, 침대에 누워 곱씹은 이 결론에 내 동의를 구하기라도 하듯 나에게 눈길을 돌렸고, 나는 동의해 주었다."]], [["‘Nothing’s truer than them,’ repeated Mr.", "\"그것들만큼 확실한 건 없지.\" 바키스 씨가 되뇌었다."], ["Barkis; ‘a man as poor as I am, finds that out in his mind when he’s laid up.", "\"나처럼 가난한 사람은, 자리보전을 하고 누워 있으면 마음속으로 그걸 깨닫는 법이오."], ["I’m a very poor man, sir!’", "나는 아주 가난한 사람이라오, 도련님!\""]], [["‘I am sorry to hear it, Mr. Barkis.’", "\"그렇다니 안타깝습니다, 바키스 씨.\""]], [["‘A very poor man, indeed I am,’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"참으로 가난한 사람이지요, 정말이오.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["Here his right hand came slowly and feebly from under the bedclothes, and with a purposeless uncertain grasp took hold of a stick which was loosely tied to the side of the bed.", "이때 그의 오른손이 이불 밑에서 느릿하고 힘없이 빠져나오더니, 정처 없고 미덥지 못한 손길로 침대 옆에 헐겁게 매여 있던 막대기 하나를 거머쥐었다."], ["After some poking about with this instrument, in the course of which his face assumed a variety of distracted expressions, Mr. Barkis poked it against a box, an end of which had been visible to me all the time.", "이 도구로 한참을 이리저리 쑤셔 대는 사이 그의 얼굴에는 갖가지 산만한 표정이 떠올랐는데, 마침내 바키스 씨는 그 막대기로, 내내 한쪽 끝이 내 눈에 보였던 상자 하나를 쿡 찔렀다."], ["Then his face became composed.", "그러자 그의 얼굴이 차분해졌다."]], [["‘Old clothes,’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"헌 옷가지요.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said I.", "\"오!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I wish it was Money, sir,’ said Mr. Barkis.", "\"돈이면 좋으련만, 도련님.\" 바키스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I wish it was, indeed,’ said I.", "\"정말 그러면 좋겠네요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘But it AIN’T,’ said Mr.", "\"한데 그게 아니란 말이오.\""], ["Barkis, opening both his eyes as wide as he possibly could.", "바키스 씨가 두 눈을 있는 힘껏 크게 뜨며 말했다."]], [["I expressed myself quite sure of that, and Mr. Barkis, turning his eyes more gently to his wife, said:", "나는 그 말이 정말 확실하다고 했고, 바키스 씨는 한결 부드러운 눈길을 아내에게 돌리며 말했다."]], [["‘She’s the usefullest and best of women, C. P. Barkis.", "\"이 사람은 세상에서 가장 쓸모 있고 가장 착한 여자요, C. P."], ["All the praise that anyone can give to C. P. Barkis, she deserves, and more!", "바키스 말이오. 누가 C. P. 바키스한테 어떤 칭찬을 해도, 이 사람은 그 칭찬을 받아 마땅하고, 그 이상도 받을 만하지!"], ["My dear, you’ll get a dinner today, for company; something good to eat and drink, will you?’", "여보, 오늘은 손님이 오셨으니 저녁을 좀 잘 차려 주구려. 뭐 맛난 거 먹고 마실 거 말이오, 응?\""]], [["I should have protested against this unnecessary demonstration in my honour, but that I saw Peggotty, on the opposite side of the bed, extremely anxious I should not. So I held my peace.", "나는 나를 위한 이 불필요한 야단법석에 한사코 사양하려 했으나, 침대 맞은편에 선 페거티가 내가 그러지 않기를 몹시 바라는 눈치라 잠자코 있었다."]], [["‘I have got a trifle of money somewhere about me, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 어딘가에 돈을 좀 갖고 있긴 한데, 여보.\""], ["Barkis, ‘but I’m a little tired. If you and Mr.", "바키스 씨가 말했다. \"내가 좀 피곤해서."], ["David will leave me for a short nap, I’ll try and find it when I wake.’", "당신하고 데이비드 도련님이 잠깐 한숨 자게 나가 주면, 깨서 한번 찾아보리다.\""]], [["We left the room, in compliance with this request. When we got outside the door, Peggotty informed me that Mr.", "우리는 이 청에 따라 방을 나섰다. 문밖에 나오자 페거티가 일러 주기를, 바키스 씨가 이제는 예전보다 '좀 더 인색해져서'"], ["Barkis, being now ‘a little nearer’ than he used to be, always resorted to this same device before producing a single coin from his store; and that he endured unheard-of agonies in crawling out of bed alone, and taking it from that unlucky box.", "자기 돈주머니에서 동전 한 닢을 꺼낼 때마다 늘 똑같은 이 수법을 쓴다고 했다. 그리고 혼자 침대에서 기어 나와 그 망할 상자에서 돈을 꺼낼 때면 이루 말할 수 없는 고통을 견딘다고 했다."], ["In effect, we presently heard him uttering suppressed groans of the most dismal nature, as this magpie proceeding racked him in every joint; but while Peggotty’s eyes were full of compassion for him, she said his generous impulse would do him good, and it was better not to check it.", "과연 우리는 이내, 까치 같은 그 짓이 그의 마디마디를 쥐어짜는 통에 그가 더없이 처량하게 신음을 억누르는 소리를 들었다. 그러나 페거티의 눈에는 그를 향한 연민이 가득했어도, 그녀는 그의 너그러운 충동이 그에게 약이 될 테니 막지 않는 편이 낫다고 했다."], ["So he groaned on, until he had got into bed again, suffering, I have no doubt, a martyrdom; and then called us in, pretending to have just woke up from a refreshing sleep, and to produce a guinea from under his pillow.", "그래서 그는 다시 침대에 기어들 때까지 줄곧 신음을 토했다 — 의심할 바 없이 순교자의 고난을 겪으며. 그러고는 우리를 불러들여, 마치 개운한 잠에서 막 깬 척하면서 베개 밑에서 일 기니를 꺼내 보였다."], ["His satisfaction in which happy imposition on us, and in having preserved the impenetrable secret of the box, appeared to be a sufficient compensation to him for all his tortures.", "우리를 이렇게 감쪽같이 속여 넘긴 것과, 상자의 뚫을 수 없는 비밀을 지켜 낸 것에 대한 그의 흡족함은, 그가 겪은 온갖 고통에 대한 넉넉한 보상이 되는 듯했다."]], [["I prepared Peggotty for Steerforth’s arrival and it was not long before he came.", "나는 스티어포스가 오리라고 페거티에게 미리 일러 두었고, 머지않아 그가 왔다."], ["I am persuaded she knew no difference between his having been a personal benefactor of hers, and a kind friend to me, and that she would have received him with the utmost gratitude and devotion in any case.", "나는 확신하건대, 페거티는 그가 자기에게 직접 은혜를 베푼 사람인지, 아니면 그저 나에게 좋은 친구인지를 구분하지 못했고, 어느 쪽이었더라도 더없는 고마움과 정성으로 그를 맞이했을 것이다."], ["But his easy, spirited good humour; his genial manner, his handsome looks, his natural gift of adapting himself to whomsoever he pleased, and making direct, when he cared to do it, to the main point of interest in anybody’s heart; bound her to him wholly in five minutes. His manner to me, alone, would have won her.", "그러나 그의 거리낌 없고 활기찬 쾌활함, 다정한 몸가짐, 잘생긴 외모, 누구에게든 제 마음대로 자신을 맞추는 타고난 재주, 그리고 마음만 먹으면 누구의 가슴속에서든 곧장 가장 관심 가는 대목으로 파고드는 솜씨가, 단 오 분 만에 그녀를 그에게 온통 사로잡았다. 나를 대하는 그의 태도만으로도 그녀의 마음을 얻기에 충분했을 것이다."], ["But, through all these causes combined, I sincerely believe she had a kind of adoration for him before he left the house that night.", "그러나 이 모든 것이 한데 어우러진 덕분에, 그가 그날 밤 그 집을 나서기도 전에 그녀가 그에게 일종의 흠모를 품게 되었다고 나는 진심으로 믿는다."]], [["He stayed there with me to dinner--if I were to say willingly, I should not half express how readily and gaily.", "그는 거기서 나와 함께 저녁을 먹으려고 머물렀다 — 기꺼이 그랬다고 말한다면, 그가 얼마나 선뜻, 얼마나 흥겹게 그랬는지를 절반도 못 담아내는 셈이다."], ["He went into Mr. Barkis’s room like light and air, brightening and refreshing it as if he were healthy weather.", "그는 빛과 공기처럼 바키스 씨의 방으로 들어가, 마치 자신이 화창한 날씨라도 되는 양 그 방을 환하게 밝히고 산뜻하게 했다."], ["There was no noise, no effort, no consciousness, in anything he did; but in everything an indescribable lightness, a seeming impossibility of doing anything else, or doing anything better, which was so graceful, so natural, and agreeable, that it overcomes me, even now, in the remembrance.", "그가 하는 일에는 소란도, 애씀도, 의식적인 꾸밈도 없었다. 다만 모든 것에 형언할 수 없는 가벼움이, 달리는 도무지 할 수 없을 것 같은, 더 잘할 수도 없을 것 같은 그 무엇이 깃들어 있었는데, 그것이 어찌나 우아하고 자연스럽고 흐뭇하던지, 지금 돌이켜 보아도 나는 그 기억에 압도되고 만다."]], [["We made merry in the little parlour, where the Book of Martyrs, unthumbed since my time, was laid out upon the desk as of old, and where I now turned over its terrific pictures, remembering the old sensations they had awakened, but not feeling them.", "우리는 그 작은 거실에서 즐겁게 놀았다. 내가 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 손때가 묻지 않은 듯한 『순교자 열전』이 옛날처럼 책상 위에 놓여 있었고, 나는 이제 그 끔찍한 삽화들을 한 장 한 장 넘기며 옛날 그것들이 불러일으켰던 그 감정들을 떠올렸으나, 정작 그 감정이 일지는 않았다."], ["When Peggotty spoke of what she called my room, and of its being ready for me at night, and of her hoping I would occupy it, before I could so much as look at Steerforth, hesitating, he was possessed of the whole case.", "페거티가 자기 말로 내 방이라 부르는 방 이야기를 꺼내며, 밤에 그 방이 채비되어 있을 거라고, 내가 거기서 묵어 주기를 바란다고 했을 때, 내가 미처 머뭇거리는 눈길로 스티어포스를 쳐다볼 새도 없이 그가 사정을 죄다 알아챘다."]], [["‘Of course,’ he said.", "\"물론이지요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘You’ll sleep here, while we stay, and I shall sleep at the hotel.’", "\"여기 머무는 동안 자네는 여기서 자게. 나는 여관에서 잘 테니.\""]], [["‘But to bring you so far,’ I returned, ‘and to separate, seems bad companionship, Steerforth.’", "\"하지만 자네를 이렇게 멀리까지 데려와 놓고,\" 내가 받았다. \"따로 떨어져 자는 건 우정에 어긋나는 일 같아, 스티어포스.\""]], [["‘Why, in the name of Heaven, where do you naturally belong?’ he said.", "\"아니, 도대체, 자네가 본디 있어야 할 곳이 어디겠나?\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘What is “seems”, compared to that?’ It was settled at once.", "\"그것에 비하면 '같아'가 다 뭔가?\" 그렇게 단번에 정해졌다."]], [["He maintained all his delightful qualities to the last, until we started forth, at eight o’clock, for Mr.", "그는 여덟 시에 우리가 페거티 씨의 배를 향해 길을 나설 때까지 그 사랑스러운 자질들을 끝끝내 잃지 않았다."], ["Peggotty’s boat. Indeed, they were more and more brightly exhibited as the hours went on; for I thought even then, and I have no doubt now, that the consciousness of success in his determination to please, inspired him with a new delicacy of perception, and made it, subtle as it was, more easy to him.", "아니, 시간이 흐를수록 그것들은 더욱더 환하게 드러났다. 그때도 나는 생각했고 지금도 의심치 않거니와, 남을 즐겁게 하겠다는 결심이 성공을 거두고 있다는 자각이 그에게 새로운 섬세한 감각을 불어넣어, 본디도 은근했던 그 솜씨를 한결 수월하게 만들어 주었기 때문이다."], ["If anyone had told me, then, that all this was a brilliant game, played for the excitement of the moment, for the employment of high spirits, in the thoughtless love of superiority, in a mere wasteful careless course of winning what was worthless to him, and next minute thrown away--I say, if anyone had told me such a lie that night, I wonder in what manner of receiving it my indignation would have found a vent!", "만약 그때 누군가 나에게, 이 모든 것이 한순간의 흥을 위해, 넘치는 혈기를 쏟을 데를 찾으려고, 우월감에 젖은 생각 없는 애착으로, 그에게는 하찮은 것을 헛되이 함부로 얻어 내고는 다음 순간 내팽개치는 그저 그런 짓에 불과한, 눈부신 노름이라고 말했다면 — 그러니까, 그날 밤 누군가 나에게 그런 거짓말을 했다면, 내 분노가 어떤 식으로 터져 나왔을지 궁금하다!"], ["Probably only in an increase, had that been possible, of the romantic feelings of fidelity and friendship with which I walked beside him, over the dark wintry sands towards the old boat; the wind sighing around us even more mournfully, than it had sighed and moaned upon the night when I first darkened Mr. Peggotty’s door.", "아마도, 그게 가능했다면, 어두운 겨울 모래밭을 건너 그 낡은 배를 향해 그와 나란히 걸으며 내 마음속에 차오르던 충직함과 우정의 그 낭만적인 감정이 더욱 짙어지는 것으로밖에는 터지지 않았으리라. 내가 처음 페거티 씨의 문간을 어둑하게 가렸던 그날 밤보다도 바람은 우리 둘레에서 한층 더 구슬프게 흐느끼고 있었다."]], [["‘This is a wild kind of place, Steerforth, is it not?’", "\"여긴 좀 거친 데지, 스티어포스, 안 그런가?\""]], [["‘Dismal enough in the dark,’ he said: ‘and the sea roars as if it were hungry for us.", "\"어둠 속에서는 꽤나 음산하군.\" 그가 말했다. \"게다가 바다는 우리를 집어삼키려고 굶주리기라도 한 듯 으르렁대고 말이야."], ["Is that the boat, where I see a light yonder?’ ‘That’s the boat,’ said I.", "저기 불빛이 보이는 데가 그 배인가?\" \"저게 그 배야.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘And it’s the same I saw this morning,’ he returned.", "\"내가 오늘 아침에 본 바로 그거로군.\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘I came straight to it, by instinct, I suppose.’", "\"내가 곧장 그리로 갔던 건, 본능이었던 모양이야.\""]], [["We said no more as we approached the light, but made softly for the door.", "불빛에 다가가는 동안 우리는 더는 말을 나누지 않고, 살그머니 문께로 향했다."], ["I laid my hand upon the latch; and whispering Steerforth to keep close to me, went in.", "나는 빗장에 손을 얹었고, 스티어포스에게 나에게 바짝 붙으라고 속삭이고는 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["A murmur of voices had been audible on the outside, and, at the moment of our entrance, a clapping of hands: which latter noise, I was surprised to see, proceeded from the generally disconsolate Mrs. Gummidge.", "바깥에서도 두런두런 말소리가 들려왔는데, 우리가 들어서는 바로 그 순간 손뼉 치는 소리가 났다. 그런데 놀랍게도 그 손뼉 소리는 늘 풀이 죽어 있던 거미지 부인에게서 나는 것이었다."], ["But Mrs. Gummidge was not the only person there who was unusually excited. Mr.", "그러나 거기서 유난히 들떠 있던 사람은 거미지 부인만이 아니었다."], ["Peggotty, his face lighted up with uncommon satisfaction, and laughing with all his might, held his rough arms wide open, as if for little Em’ly to run into them; Ham, with a mixed expression in his face of admiration, exultation, and a lumbering sort of bashfulness that sat upon him very well, held little Em’ly by the hand, as if he were presenting her to Mr.", "페거티 씨는 보기 드문 흡족함으로 얼굴이 환하게 빛난 채 힘껏 웃으며, 꼬마 에밀리가 뛰어들기라도 하라는 듯 거친 두 팔을 활짝 벌리고 있었다. 햄은 흠모와 환희와, 그에게 무척 잘 어울리는 어딘가 굼뜬 듯한 수줍음이 뒤섞인 표정으로, 마치 에밀리를 페거티 씨에게 내보이기라도 하듯 꼬마 에밀리의 손을 잡고 있었다."], ["Peggotty; little Em’ly herself, blushing and shy, but delighted with Mr. Peggotty’s delight, as her joyous eyes expressed, was stopped by our entrance (for she saw us first) in the very act of springing from Ham to nestle in Mr. Peggotty’s embrace.", "꼬마 에밀리는 얼굴을 붉히며 수줍어하면서도, 그 기쁨에 찬 눈이 말해 주듯 페거티 씨가 기뻐하는 것이 기뻐서, 햄에게서 폴짝 뛰어 페거티 씨의 품에 안기려던 바로 그 순간 우리가 들어오는 바람에 (그녀가 우리를 제일 먼저 보았다) 멈칫했다."], ["In the first glimpse we had of them all, and at the moment of our passing from the dark cold night into the warm light room, this was the way in which they were all employed: Mrs.", "어둡고 차가운 밤에서 따뜻하고 환한 방으로 막 들어서던 그 찰나, 우리가 그들 모두를 처음 흘끗 본 광경 속에서 다들 이렇게 한창이었다."], ["Gummidge in the background, clapping her hands like a madwoman.", "거미지 부인은 뒤편에서 미친 여자처럼 손뼉을 쳐 대고 있었다."]], [["The little picture was so instantaneously dissolved by our going in, that one might have doubted whether it had ever been.", "그 작은 그림은 우리가 들어서자마자 어찌나 순식간에 흩어지던지, 그런 것이 정말 있기나 했나 싶을 정도였다."], ["I was in the midst of the astonished family, face to face with Mr. Peggotty, and holding out my hand to him, when Ham shouted:", "어느새 나는 깜짝 놀란 식구들 한가운데서 페거티 씨와 얼굴을 마주한 채 그에게 손을 내밀고 있었고, 그때 햄이 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy!", "\"데이비 도련님!"], ["It’s Mas’r Davy!’", "데이비 도련님이여!\""]], [["In a moment we were all shaking hands with one another, and asking one another how we did, and telling one another how glad we were to meet, and all talking at once. Mr.", "순식간에 우리는 모두 서로 악수를 나누고, 어떻게 지냈느냐고 서로 묻고, 만나서 얼마나 반가운지 서로 떠들어 대며, 다들 한꺼번에 떠들고 있었다."], ["Peggotty was so proud and overjoyed to see us, that he did not know what to say or do, but kept over and over again shaking hands with me, and then with Steerforth, and then with me, and then ruffling his shaggy hair all over his head, and laughing with such glee and triumph, that it was a treat to see him.", "페거티 씨는 우리를 보고 어찌나 우쭐하고 기쁘던지 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 몰라, 나와 악수를 했다가, 다음엔 스티어포스와, 또 다음엔 나와, 거듭거듭 악수를 하고는, 그러다 텁수룩한 머리를 머리통 위로 마구 헝클어뜨리며, 어찌나 흐뭇하고 의기양양하게 웃어 대던지 보는 것만으로도 흐뭇했다."]], [["‘Why, that you two gent’lmen--gent’lmen growed--should come to this here roof tonight, of all nights in my life,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 두 분 신사 양반이 — 어엿한 신사로 자라셔서 — 하필 내 평생 다른 어느 밤도 아닌 오늘 밤에 이 지붕 밑에 찾아오시다니,\""], ["Peggotty, ‘is such a thing as never happened afore, I do rightly believe!", "페거티 씨가 말했다. \"이런 일은 일찍이 없었지요, 내 똑똑히 믿거니와!"], ["Em’ly, my darling, come here!", "에밀리, 내 귀염둥아, 이리 온!"], ["Come here, my little witch!", "이리 오너라, 우리 꼬마 마녀야!"], ["There’s Mas’r Davy’s friend, my dear!", "데이비 도련님의 친구분이시다, 얘야!"], ["There’s the gent’lman as you’ve heerd on, Em’ly.", "네가 얘기로 들었던 그 신사 양반이시다, 에밀리."], ["He comes to see you, along with Mas’r Davy, on the brightest night of your uncle’s life as ever was or will be, Gorm the t’other one, and horroar for it!’", "데이비 도련님하고 같이 너를 보러 오셨단다, 네 외삼촌 평생에 이제껏 없었고 앞으로도 없을 가장 환한 밤에 말이다. 저기 또 한 분 잘 보아라, 만세로다!\""]], [["After delivering this speech all in a breath, and with extraordinary animation and pleasure, Mr. Peggotty put one of his large hands rapturously on each side of his niece’s face, and kissing it a dozen times, laid it with a gentle pride and love upon his broad chest, and patted it as if his hand had been a lady’s.", "이 말을 단숨에, 더없는 신명과 기쁨으로 쏟아 낸 뒤, 페거티 씨는 큼지막한 두 손을 황홀하게 조카딸의 두 뺨에 갖다 대고, 그 얼굴에 열두 번이나 입을 맞추더니, 점잖은 자랑과 사랑을 담아 자기 떡 벌어진 가슴에 그 얼굴을 갖다 대고는, 마치 그 손이 귀부인의 손이라도 되는 양 토닥였다."], ["Then he let her go; and as she ran into the little chamber where I used to sleep, looked round upon us, quite hot and out of breath with his uncommon satisfaction.", "그러고는 그 애를 놓아주었다. 그 애가 내가 자던 그 작은 방으로 달려가자, 그는 보기 드문 흡족함에 후끈 달아올라 숨을 헐떡이며 우리를 둘러보았다."]], [["‘If you two gent’lmen--gent’lmen growed now, and such gent’lmen--’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"두 분 신사 양반이 — 이제는 어엿하게 자란 신사 양반들, 그것도 이런 신사 양반들이 —\" 페거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘So th’ are, so th’ are!’ cried Ham. ‘Well said!", "\"그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요!\" 햄이 외쳤다. \"옳거니!"], ["So th’ are.", "그렇고말고요."], ["Mas’r Davy bor’--gent’lmen growed--so th’ are!’", "데이비 도련님 말이여 — 어엿하게 자라신 신사 양반 — 그렇고말고요!\""]], [["‘If you two gent’lmen, gent’lmen growed,’ said Mr.", "\"두 분 신사 양반, 어엿하게 자란 신사 양반들이,\""], ["Peggotty, ‘don’t ex-cuse me for being in a state of mind, when you understand matters, I’ll arks your pardon.", "페거티 씨가 말했다. \"사정을 아시고 나서, 내가 이렇게 들떠 있는 걸 너그러이 봐주지 않으신다면, 용서를 빌어야겠지요."], ["Em’ly, my dear!--She knows I’m a going to tell,’ here his delight broke out again, ‘and has made off.", "에밀리, 얘야! — 저 애는 내가 이야기하려는 걸 알고,\" 여기서 그의 기쁨이 다시 터져 나왔다."], ["Would you be so good as look arter her, Mawther, for a minute?’", "\"달아나 버렸구먼. 잠깐 저 애 좀 봐 주시겠소, 아주머니?\""]], [["Mrs. Gummidge nodded and disappeared.", "거미지 부인이 고개를 끄덕이고는 사라졌다."]], [["‘If this ain’t,’ said Mr.", "\"만약 이게,\""], ["Peggotty, sitting down among us by the fire, ‘the brightest night o’ my life, I’m a shellfish--biled too--and more I can’t say.", "페거티 씨가 난롯가에 우리 틈에 끼어 앉으며 말했다. \"내 평생 가장 환한 밤이 아니라면, 나는 조개요 — 그것도 삶은 조개 — 그 이상은 더 할 말이 없소."], ["This here little Em’ly, sir,’ in a low voice to Steerforth, ‘--her as you see a blushing here just now--’", "이 꼬마 에밀리가 말이오, 손님,\" 스티어포스에게 나직이 말했다. \"아까 여기서 얼굴 붉히는 걸 보신 그 애가 —\""]], [["Steerforth only nodded; but with such a pleased expression of interest, and of participation in Mr. Peggotty’s feelings, that the latter answered him as if he had spoken.", "스티어포스는 그저 고개만 끄덕였으나, 어찌나 흐뭇하게 관심을 보이는 표정으로, 페거티 씨의 감정에 함께 어우러지는 낯빛으로 끄덕이던지, 페거티 씨는 그가 말이라도 한 양 대꾸했다."]], [["‘To be sure,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"그렇고말고요.\" 페거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘That’s her, and so she is.", "\"그게 그 애요, 바로 그렇답니다."], ["Thankee, sir.’", "고맙습니다, 손님.\""]], [["Ham nodded to me several times, as if he would have said so too.", "햄도 나에게 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕여 보였는데, 마치 자기도 그렇게 말하고 싶다는 듯했다."]], [["‘This here little Em’ly of ours,’ said Mr.", "\"우리 이 꼬마 에밀리는 말이오,\" 페거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘has been, in our house, what I suppose (I’m a ignorant man, but that’s my belief) no one but a little bright-eyed creetur can be in a house.", "\"우리 집에서, 내 짐작에 (나는 무지렁이지만, 그래도 내 믿음으론) 눈망울 초롱초롱한 어린 것 말고는 누구도 한 집안에서 될 수 없는 그런 존재였소. 그 애는 내 자식이 아니오."], ["She ain’t my child; I never had one; but I couldn’t love her more. You understand!", "나는 자식을 둔 적이 없으니까. 하지만 그 애를 이보다 더 사랑할 수는 없을 게요. 아시겠소!"], ["I couldn’t do it!’", "더는 못 한단 말이오!\""]], [["‘I quite understand,’ said Steerforth.", "\"잘 알겠습니다.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘I know you do, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"손님이 아시는 줄 압니다, 손님.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘and thankee again.", "페거티 씨가 받았다. \"다시 한번 고맙소."], ["Mas’r Davy, he can remember what she was; you may judge for your own self what she is; but neither of you can’t fully know what she has been, is, and will be, to my loving art.", "데이비 도련님은 저 애가 어땠는지 기억하시고, 손님은 저 애가 지금 어떤지를 손수 가늠하실 수 있겠지요. 하지만 두 분 다, 저 애가 내 사랑하는 가슴에 어떤 존재였고, 지금 어떤 존재이며, 또 앞으로 어떤 존재일지는 온전히 알 수 없으실 게요."], ["I am rough, sir,’ said Mr.", "나는 거칠지요, 손님.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘I am as rough as a Sea Porkypine; but no one, unless, mayhap, it is a woman, can know, I think, what our little Em’ly is to me.", "페거티 씨가 말했다. \"바다 고슴도치만큼이나 거친 사람이지만, 우리 꼬마 에밀리가 나한테 어떤 존재인지는, 어쩌면 여자라면 모를까, 아무도 알 수 없을 거라 생각하오."], ["And betwixt ourselves,’ sinking his voice lower yet, ‘that woman’s name ain’t Missis Gummidge neither, though she has a world of merits.’ Mr.", "그리고 우리끼리 얘기지만,\" 목소리를 한층 더 낮추며, \"그 여자란 거미지 부인은 아니라오. 그 사람도 장점이야 수두룩하지만 말이오.\""], ["Peggotty ruffled his hair again, with both hands, as a further preparation for what he was going to say, and went on, with a hand upon each of his knees:", "페거티 씨는 자기가 하려는 말에 대한 채비라도 더 하려는 듯 두 손으로 다시 머리를 헝클어뜨리고는, 두 무릎에 한 손씩 얹고 말을 이었다."]], [["‘There was a certain person as had know’d our Em’ly, from the time when her father was drownded; as had seen her constant; when a babby, when a young gal, when a woman.", "\"우리 에밀리가 제 아비를 물에 잃었을 때부터 그 애를 알아 온 사람이 하나 있소. 줄곧 그 애를 봐 온 사람이지. 갓난아기 때도, 어린 계집애 때도, 다 큰 처녀가 됐을 때도."], ["Not much of a person to look at, he warn’t,’ said Mr.", "보기에 대단한 사람은 아니었소.\" 페거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘something o’ my own build--rough--a good deal o’ the sou’-wester in him--wery salt--but, on the whole, a honest sort of a chap, with his art in the right place.’", "\"나하고 비슷한 몸피에 — 거칠고 — 남서풍 비바람에 절은 데가 많고 — 짭짤하게 짠내 나는 — 하지만 통틀어 보면 정직한 녀석이라, 마음 둘 데에 마음을 둔 사람이었소.\""]], [["I thought I had never seen Ham grin to anything like the extent to which he sat grinning at us now.", "내 생각에 나는 햄이 지금 우리를 향해 앉아 씩 웃는 그 정도로 입이 찢어지게 웃는 모습을 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."]], [["‘What does this here blessed tarpaulin go and do,’ said Mr.", "\"이 복받은 방수포 같은 녀석이 가서 뭘 했느냐 하면 말이오,\""], ["Peggotty, with his face one high noon of enjoyment, ‘but he loses that there art of his to our little Em’ly.", "페거티 씨가 얼굴 가득 한낮 같은 환한 즐거움을 띠며 말했다. \"그 마음을 우리 꼬마 에밀리한테 빼앗겨 버렸지 뭐요."], ["He follers her about, he makes hisself a sort o’ servant to her, he loses in a great measure his relish for his wittles, and in the long-run he makes it clear to me wot’s amiss.", "그 애를 졸졸 따라다니고, 그 애한테 종이라도 된 양 굴고, 밥맛까지 크게 잃어버리더니, 마침내 나한테 뭐가 잘못됐는지를 분명히 일러 주더란 말이오."], ["Now I could wish myself, you see, that our little Em’ly was in a fair way of being married.", "자, 나로서야, 보다시피, 우리 꼬마 에밀리가 어엿하게 시집갈 길에 들어섰으면 하는 바람이지요."], ["I could wish to see her, at all ewents, under articles to a honest man as had a right to defend her.", "어찌 됐든 그 애를 지켜 줄 권리가 있는 정직한 사내한테 그 애가 매인 모습을 보고 싶었소."], ["I don’t know how long I may live, or how soon I may die; but I know that if I was capsized, any night, in a gale of wind in Yarmouth Roads here, and was to see the town-lights shining for the last time over the rollers as I couldn’t make no head against, I could go down quieter for thinking “There’s a man ashore there, iron-true to my little Em’ly, God bless her, and no wrong can touch my Em’ly while so be as that man lives.”’", "내가 얼마나 더 살지, 언제 죽을지 나야 모르오. 하지만 만약 내가 어느 밤, 여기 야머스 앞바다에서 거센 바람에 배가 뒤집혀, 도무지 거슬러 나아갈 수 없는 그 물결 너머로 마지막으로 마을의 불빛이 반짝이는 걸 본다 해도, '저기 뭍에 우리 꼬마 에밀리한테 쇠처럼 진실한 사내가 하나 있다, 하느님께서 그 애를 보살피시기를, 그 사내가 살아 있는 한 우리 에밀리한테는 아무 해도 닿지 못하리라' 이렇게 생각하며 한결 마음 편히 가라앉을 수 있다는 건 알지요.\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty, in simple earnestness, waved his right arm, as if he were waving it at the town-lights for the last time, and then, exchanging a nod with Ham, whose eye he caught, proceeded as before.", "페거티 씨는 꾸밈없는 진심으로 오른팔을 휘저었는데, 마치 마을의 불빛을 향해 마지막으로 손을 흔드는 것 같았다. 그러고는 눈이 마주친 햄과 고갯짓을 주고받은 뒤, 아까처럼 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Well!", "\"자!"], ["I counsels him to speak to Em’ly.", "나는 그 녀석한테 에밀리한테 말을 해 보라고 일렀소."], ["He’s big enough, but he’s bashfuller than a little un, and he don’t like.", "덩치는 멀쩡한데, 어린것보다도 더 부끄럼을 타서, 도무지 내켜 하질 않더란 말이오."], ["So I speak. “What! Him!”", "그래서 내가 말을 했지. '뭐라고요!"], ["says Em’ly.", "저이를요!'"], ["“Him that I’ve know’d so intimate so many years, and like so much.", "에밀리가 그러더군. '여러 해 그렇게 살갑게 알고 지내고, 그렇게 좋아하던 저이를요."], ["Oh, Uncle!", "아, 외삼촌!"], ["I never can have him.", "나는 절대 저이랑은 안 돼요."], ["He’s such a good fellow!”", "저이는 그렇게 좋은 사람인걸요!'"], ["I gives her a kiss, and I says no more to her than, “My dear, you’re right to speak out, you’re to choose for yourself, you’re as free as a little bird.”", "나는 그 애한테 입을 맞추고, 그저 이렇게만 말했소. '얘야, 솔직하게 말하길 잘했다, 네 일은 네가 고르는 거다, 너는 작은 새처럼 자유로운 몸이란다.'"], ["Then I aways to him, and I says, “I wish it could have been so, but it can’t.", "그러고는 그 녀석한테 가서 말했지. '그렇게 됐으면 했다만, 그게 안 되겠다."], ["But you can both be as you was, and wot I say to you is, Be as you was with her, like a man.”", "그래도 너희 둘은 예전 그대로 지낼 수 있어, 내가 너한테 하는 말은, 사내답게 그 애하고 예전처럼 지내라는 거다.'"], ["He says to me, a-shaking of my hand, “I will!” he says.", "그러자 그 녀석이 내 손을 흔들며 말하더군. '그러겠어요!' 그렇게요."], ["And he was--honourable and manful--for two year going on, and we was just the same at home here as afore.’", "그리고 그 녀석은 그랬소 — 떳떳하고 사내답게 — 꼬박 이 년 동안이나. 그래서 우리는 여기 집에서 예전과 똑같이 지냈지요.\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty’s face, which had varied in its expression with the various stages of his narrative, now resumed all its former triumphant delight, as he laid a hand upon my knee and a hand upon Steerforth’s (previously wetting them both, for the greater emphasis of the action), and divided the following speech between us:", "페거티 씨의 얼굴은 이야기가 굽이굽이 흐르는 동안 표정이 갖가지로 바뀌었는데, 이제 내 무릎에 한 손을, 스티어포스의 무릎에 한 손을 얹으며 (행동에 한층 힘을 싣느라 두 손에 먼저 침까지 발랐다) 다시 아까처럼 의기양양한 기쁨을 온통 되찾고는, 다음 말을 우리 둘에게 나누어 건넸다."]], [["‘All of a sudden, one evening--as it might be tonight--comes little Em’ly from her work, and him with her!", "\"한데 난데없이, 어느 날 저녁 — 바로 오늘 밤 같은 날이었지요 — 꼬마 에밀리가 일터에서 돌아오는데, 그 녀석이 같이 오는 거요! 그게 뭐 대수냐 하시겠지."], ["There ain’t so much in that, you’ll say. No, because he takes care on her, like a brother, arter dark, and indeed afore dark, and at all times.", "그렇소, 그 녀석은 날이 저문 뒤면, 아니 저물기 전에도, 언제든 오라비처럼 그 애를 챙기니까. 한데 이 방수포 같은 녀석이 그 애 손을 덥석 잡고는, 기뻐서 나한테 소리치는 거요."], ["But this tarpaulin chap, he takes hold of her hand, and he cries out to me, joyful, “Look here!", "'여기 좀 보세요! 이이가 제 작은 아내가 될 사람이에요!' 그러자 그 애가, 반쯤은 대담하고 반쯤은 수줍게, 반쯤은 웃고 반쯤은 울면서 말하더군."], ["This is to be my little wife!”", "'네, 외삼촌! 괜찮으시다면요.'"], ["And she says, half bold and half shy, and half a laughing and half a crying, “Yes, Uncle!", "— 괜찮으냐고!\" 페거티 씨가 그 말을 생각하며 황홀해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 외쳤다."], ["If you please.”--If I please!’ cried Mr.", "\"원, 내가 뭐 다른 걸 할 사람이라고!"], ["Peggotty, rolling his head in an ecstasy at the idea; ‘Lord, as if I should do anythink else!--“If you please, I am steadier now, and I have thought better of it, and I’ll be as good a little wife as I can to him, for he’s a dear, good fellow!”", "— '괜찮으시다면요, 저도 이젠 마음이 더 다잡혔고, 곰곰 다시 생각해 봤어요. 저이한테 될 수 있는 한 좋은 작은 아내가 될게요. 저이는 다정하고 착한 사람이니까요!'"], ["Then Missis Gummidge, she claps her hands like a play, and you come in.", "그러니까 거미지 아주머니가 연극에서처럼 손뼉을 짝짝 치는데, 마침 두 분이 들어오신 거요. 자!"], ["Theer! the murder’s out!’ said Mr.", "이제 다 털어놓았소!\" 페거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty--‘You come in!", "\"두 분이 들어오셨지!"], ["It took place this here present hour; and here’s the man that’ll marry her, the minute she’s out of her time.’", "바로 이 순간에 그 일이 벌어진 거요. 그리고 여기 이 사내가, 그 애가 도제 기한을 마치는 그날로 그 애와 혼인할 사람이오.\""]], [["Ham staggered, as well he might, under the blow Mr. Peggotty dealt him in his unbounded joy, as a mark of confidence and friendship; but feeling called upon to say something to us, he said, with much faltering and great difficulty:", "햄은, 그럴 만도 했지만, 페거티 씨가 끝없는 기쁨에 겨워 믿음과 우정의 표시로 후려친 그 한 방에 휘청거렸다. 그러나 우리에게 무언가 말을 해야겠다는 생각이 든 듯, 그는 몹시 더듬거리며 무던히 애를 써서 말했다."]], [["‘She warn’t no higher than you was, Mas’r Davy--when you first come--when I thought what she’d grow up to be.", "\"그 앤 도련님만큼도 안 됐었지요, 데이비 도련님 — 도련님이 처음 오셨을 때 말이여 — 그때 나는 그 애가 자라서 어찌 될까 생각했더랬소."], ["I see her grown up--gent’lmen--like a flower.", "그 애가 자라는 걸 봤지요 — 신사 양반들 — 꽃처럼요."], ["I’d lay down my life for her--Mas’r Davy--Oh! most content and cheerful!", "나는 그 애를 위해서라면 목숨도 내놓을 수 있소 — 데이비 도련님 — 아! 더없이 흐뭇하고 기쁘게요!"], ["She’s more to me--gent’lmen--than--she’s all to me that ever I can want, and more than ever I--than ever I could say. I--I love her true.", "그 앤 나한테 — 신사 양반들 — 그 무엇보다도 — 그 애는 나한테 내가 바랄 수 있는 전부고, 그 이상이라, 내가 — 내가 도무지 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼이오."], ["There ain’t a gent’lman in all the land--nor yet sailing upon all the sea--that can love his lady more than I love her, though there’s many a common man--would say better--what he meant.’", "나는 — 나는 그 애를 진심으로 사랑하오. 이 온 땅에 — 아니, 온 바다 위를 떠도는 누구를 데려와도 — 나만큼 제 색시를 사랑하는 신사 양반은 없을 거요. 비록 흔한 뱃사람들 가운데 — 제 속마음을 — 더 잘 말할 사람이야 많겠지만 말이오.\""]], [["I thought it affecting to see such a sturdy fellow as Ham was now, trembling in the strength of what he felt for the pretty little creature who had won his heart.", "햄처럼 다부진 사내가, 제 마음을 사로잡은 그 어여쁜 작은 아가씨를 향한 감정의 힘에 못 이겨 떨고 있는 모습을 보는 것은 가슴 뭉클한 일이었다."], ["I thought the simple confidence reposed in us by Mr. Peggotty and by himself, was, in itself, affecting.", "페거티 씨와 햄이 우리에게 보여 준 그 꾸밈없는 신뢰 자체가 가슴 뭉클했다."], ["I was affected by the story altogether.", "나는 그 이야기 전체에 마음이 뭉클해졌다."], ["How far my emotions were influenced by the recollections of my childhood, I don’t know.", "내 감정이 어릴 적 추억에 얼마나 휘둘렸는지는 모르겠다."], ["Whether I had come there with any lingering fancy that I was still to love little Em’ly, I don’t know.", "내가 여전히 꼬마 에밀리를 사랑해야 한다는 어떤 미련 같은 마음을 품고 그곳에 갔던 것인지도 모르겠다."], ["I know that I was filled with pleasure by all this; but, at first, with an indescribably sensitive pleasure, that a very little would have changed to pain.", "다만 이 모든 일로 내 가슴이 기쁨으로 가득 찼다는 것은 안다. 그러나 처음에는, 형언할 수 없이 예민한 기쁨이라, 아주 사소한 일에도 금세 아픔으로 변해 버릴 그런 기쁨이었다."]], [["Therefore, if it had depended upon me to touch the prevailing chord among them with any skill, I should have made a poor hand of it.", "그러니, 그들 사이에 가득한 그 정조를 어떤 솜씨로 건드리는 일이 나에게 달려 있었다면, 나는 일을 서투르게 망쳤을 것이다."], ["But it depended upon Steerforth; and he did it with such address, that in a few minutes we were all as easy and as happy as it was possible to be.", "그러나 그것은 스티어포스에게 달려 있었고, 그는 어찌나 능란하게 해내던지, 몇 분 만에 우리는 더없이 편안하고 더없이 행복한 사이가 되었다."]], [["‘Mr. Peggotty,’ he said, ‘you are a thoroughly good fellow, and deserve to be as happy as you are tonight.", "\"페거티 씨,\" 그가 말했다."], ["My hand upon it! Ham, I give you joy, my boy.", "\"당신은 더없이 좋은 분이시고, 오늘 밤 누리시는 만큼 행복할 자격이 있으십니다."], ["My hand upon that, too!", "자, 제 손을 잡으시지요!"], ["Daisy, stir the fire, and make it a brisk one! and Mr.", "햄, 자네에게 기쁨을 비네, 이 친구야. 여기에도 제 손을 잡게!"], ["Peggotty, unless you can induce your gentle niece to come back (for whom I vacate this seat in the corner), I shall go.", "데이지, 불을 좀 쑤셔서 활활 타오르게 해 주게! 그리고 페거티 씨, 당신의 얌전한 조카따님을 다시 데려오도록 구슬려 주시지 않는다면 (저는 이 구석 자리를 그분께 비워 드리겠습니다) 저는 그만 가 보겠습니다."], ["Any gap at your fireside on such a night--such a gap least of all--I wouldn’t make, for the wealth of the Indies!’", "이런 밤에 당신네 난롯가에 빈자리가 생기는 건 — 더구나 이런 빈자리는 더더욱 — 인도의 온갖 재물을 준다 해도 저는 만들지 않겠습니다!\""]], [["So Mr. Peggotty went into my old room to fetch little Em’ly.", "그래서 페거티 씨가 꼬마 에밀리를 데려오려고 내 옛 방으로 들어갔다."], ["At first little Em’ly didn’t like to come, and then Ham went.", "처음에 꼬마 에밀리는 나오기 싫어했고, 그러자 이번엔 햄이 갔다."], ["Presently they brought her to the fireside, very much confused, and very shy,--but she soon became more assured when she found how gently and respectfully Steerforth spoke to her; how skilfully he avoided anything that would embarrass her; how he talked to Mr.", "이윽고 두 사람이 그 애를 난롯가로 데려왔는데, 몹시 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 무척 수줍어했다."], ["Peggotty of boats, and ships, and tides, and fish; how he referred to me about the time when he had seen Mr. Peggotty at Salem House; how delighted he was with the boat and all belonging to it; how lightly and easily he carried on, until he brought us, by degrees, into a charmed circle, and we were all talking away without any reserve.", "그러나 스티어포스가 자기에게 얼마나 다정하고 깍듯하게 말을 건네는지, 자기를 난처하게 할 만한 것은 얼마나 솜씨 좋게 피해 가는지, 페거티 씨에게 배며 선박이며 조수며 물고기 이야기를 어떻게 풀어 가는지, 그가 세일럼 하우스에서 페거티 씨를 만났던 그때를 두고 나에게 어떻게 말을 거는지, 그가 그 배와 그에 딸린 모든 것을 얼마나 좋아하는지, 그가 얼마나 가뿐하고 수월하게 이야기를 이어 가다가, 차츰차츰 우리를 홀린 듯한 한 무리로 만들어, 마침내 우리 모두가 아무 거리낌 없이 이야기꽃을 피우게 되는지를 알게 되자, 그 애도 이내 한결 마음을 놓았다."]], [["Em’ly, indeed, said little all the evening; but she looked, and listened, and her face got animated, and she was charming.", "사실 에밀리는 그날 저녁 내내 말은 거의 없었다. 그러나 바라보고, 귀 기울이는 사이 그 얼굴에 생기가 돌았고, 그 애는 사랑스러웠다."], ["Steerforth told a story of a dismal shipwreck (which arose out of his talk with Mr. Peggotty), as if he saw it all before him--and little Em’ly’s eyes were fastened on him all the time, as if she saw it too.", "스티어포스는 (페거티 씨와 나눈 이야기에서 나온) 음산한 난파 이야기를 마치 눈앞에 그 광경이 다 펼쳐지기라도 한 듯 들려주었는데 — 꼬마 에밀리의 눈은 마치 자기도 그 광경을 보고 있는 듯 내내 그에게 박혀 있었다."], ["He told us a merry adventure of his own, as a relief to that, with as much gaiety as if the narrative were as fresh to him as it was to us--and little Em’ly laughed until the boat rang with the musical sounds, and we all laughed (Steerforth too), in irresistible sympathy with what was so pleasant and light-hearted.", "그는 그 분위기를 누그러뜨리려고 자기가 겪은 유쾌한 모험담을, 그 이야기가 우리에게처럼 자기에게도 갓 겪은 일인 양 흥겹게 들려주었고 — 꼬마 에밀리는 배 안이 그 가락 같은 웃음소리로 울리도록 깔깔 웃었으며, 우리도 모두 (스티어포스까지도) 그 즐겁고 가뿐한 분위기에 끌려 어쩔 수 없이 함께 웃었다."], ["He got Mr. Peggotty to sing, or rather to roar, ‘When the stormy winds do blow, do blow, do blow’; and he sang a sailor’s song himself, so pathetically and beautifully, that I could have almost fancied that the real wind creeping sorrowfully round the house, and murmuring low through our unbroken silence, was there to listen.", "그는 페거티 씨에게 '거센 바람이 불 때, 불 때, 불 때'라는 노래를 부르게, 아니 외치게 했고, 자기도 뱃사람의 노래 한 가락을 어찌나 애절하고 아름답게 부르던지, 나는 슬프게 집 둘레를 기어 다니며 우리의 끊김 없는 침묵 사이로 나직이 흐느끼는 그 진짜 바람마저도, 그 노래를 들으러 거기 와 있는 것이 아닐까 하는 생각이 들 지경이었다."]], [["As to Mrs. Gummidge, he roused that victim of despondency with a success never attained by anyone else (so Mr. Peggotty informed me), since the decease of the old one.", "거미지 부인으로 말하자면, 그는 (페거티 씨가 일러 준 바로는) 옛 영감이 세상을 뜬 뒤로 누구도 거두지 못한 성공을 거두며 그 낙담의 화신을 흥겹게 만들어 놓았다."], ["He left her so little leisure for being miserable, that she said next day she thought she must have been bewitched.", "그가 그녀에게 비참할 겨를을 어찌나 안 주던지, 그녀는 이튿날 자기가 무슨 흘림에라도 걸렸던 게 틀림없다고 말했다."]], [["But he set up no monopoly of the general attention, or the conversation.", "그러나 그는 모두의 관심이나 대화를 독차지하지는 않았다."], ["When little Em’ly grew more courageous, and talked (but still bashfully) across the fire to me, of our old wanderings upon the beach, to pick up shells and pebbles; and when I asked her if she recollected how I used to be devoted to her; and when we both laughed and reddened, casting these looks back on the pleasant old times, so unreal to look at now; he was silent and attentive, and observed us thoughtfully.", "꼬마 에밀리가 점점 용기를 내어, 난로 너머로 (그래도 여전히 수줍게) 나에게, 조개껍데기며 자갈을 주우러 바닷가를 거닐던 우리 옛 시절 이야기를 건넸을 때, 그리고 내가 그 애에게 내가 그 애한테 얼마나 푹 빠져 있었는지 기억하느냐고 물었을 때, 그리고 우리 둘이 함께 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고, 지금 보면 그토록 비현실적인 그 정겨운 옛 시절을 돌아보던 그때, 그는 잠자코 귀 기울이며 우리를 곰곰이 지켜보았다."], ["She sat, at this time, and all the evening, on the old locker in her old little corner by the fire--Ham beside her, where I used to sit.", "그 애는 이때도, 그리고 저녁 내내, 난롯가의 작고 익숙한 제 구석에 놓인 그 낡은 사물함 위에 앉아 있었다 — 햄은 그 곁, 예전에 내가 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["I could not satisfy myself whether it was in her own little tormenting way, or in a maidenly reserve before us, that she kept quite close to the wall, and away from him; but I observed that she did so, all the evening.", "그 애가 자기 특유의 그 짓궂은 버릇으로 그러는 것인지, 아니면 우리 앞이라 처녀다운 수줍음으로 그러는 것인지 나는 도무지 가늠할 수 없었으나, 그 애는 벽에 바짝 붙어 햄에게서 떨어져 있었고, 나는 그 애가 저녁 내내 그러는 것을 눈여겨보았다."]], [["As I remember, it was almost midnight when we took our leave.", "내 기억으로는, 우리가 작별할 무렵에는 거의 자정이었다."], ["We had had some biscuit and dried fish for supper, and Steerforth had produced from his pocket a full flask of Hollands, which we men (I may say we men, now, without a blush) had emptied.", "우리는 저녁으로 비스킷과 말린 생선을 좀 먹었고, 스티어포스가 주머니에서 네덜란드 진이 가득 든 술병을 꺼내, 우리 사내들이 (이제는 얼굴 붉히지 않고 우리 사내들이라 말해도 되겠다) 그것을 비웠다."], ["We parted merrily; and as they all stood crowded round the door to light us as far as they could upon our road, I saw the sweet blue eyes of little Em’ly peeping after us, from behind Ham, and heard her soft voice calling to us to be careful how we went.", "우리는 흥겹게 헤어졌다. 그들 모두가 우리가 길을 가는 데 될 수 있는 한 멀리까지 비추어 주려고 문께에 빙 둘러 모여 섰을 때, 나는 꼬마 에밀리의 그 어여쁜 푸른 눈이 햄의 등 뒤에서 우리를 살며시 좇는 것을 보았고, 조심해서 가라고 우리에게 외치는 그 애의 부드러운 목소리를 들었다."]], [["‘A most engaging little Beauty!’ said Steerforth, taking my arm. ‘Well!", "\"여간 사랑스러운 작은 미인이 아니로군!\" 스티어포스가 내 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"그래!"], ["It’s a quaint place, and they are quaint company, and it’s quite a new sensation to mix with them.’", "묘한 곳에, 묘한 사람들이야. 저런 사람들과 어울리는 건 꽤 새로운 기분이군.\""]], [["‘How fortunate we are, too,’ I returned, ‘to have arrived to witness their happiness in that intended marriage!", "\"우리가 운이 참 좋기도 하지.\" 내가 받았다. \"이렇게 와서, 그 약속된 혼인으로 행복해하는 모습을 다 보게 됐으니 말이야!"], ["I never saw people so happy.", "그렇게 행복한 사람들은 처음 봤어."], ["How delightful to see it, and to be made the sharers in their honest joy, as we have been!’", "그걸 보고, 또 우리가 그들의 꾸밈없는 기쁨을 함께 나누게 되다니, 얼마나 흐뭇한 일인지!\""]], [["‘That’s rather a chuckle-headed fellow for the girl; isn’t he?’ said Steerforth.", "\"그 처녀한테는 좀 멍청한 친구지, 안 그런가?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["He had been so hearty with him, and with them all, that I felt a shock in this unexpected and cold reply.", "그는 그에게도, 또 그들 모두에게도 그토록 다정했던 터라, 나는 이 뜻밖의 싸늘한 대답에 충격을 받았다."], ["But turning quickly upon him, and seeing a laugh in his eyes, I answered, much relieved:", "그러나 휙 그를 돌아보고 그의 눈에 어린 웃음을 보고는, 한결 마음이 놓여 대답했다."]], [["‘Ah, Steerforth!", "\"아, 스티어포스!"], ["It’s well for you to joke about the poor! You may skirmish with Miss Dartle, or try to hide your sympathies in jest from me, but I know better.", "자네야 가난한 사람들을 두고 농담할 처지가 되니 좋겠네! 다틀 양과 옥신각신하든, 농담으로 나한테 자네 속정을 감추려 들든, 나는 다 알아."], ["When I see how perfectly you understand them, how exquisitely you can enter into happiness like this plain fisherman’s, or humour a love like my old nurse’s, I know that there is not a joy or sorrow, not an emotion, of such people, that can be indifferent to you.", "자네가 저 사람들을 얼마나 완벽하게 이해하는지, 저 소박한 어부의 행복 같은 데에 얼마나 절묘하게 녹아드는지, 우리 옛 유모의 사랑 같은 걸 얼마나 잘 어루만지는지를 보면, 저런 사람들의 기쁨이든 슬픔이든, 어떤 감정 하나도 자네한테 아무래도 좋은 것은 없다는 걸 알 수 있거든."], ["And I admire and love you for it, Steerforth, twenty times the more!’", "그래서 나는 그 점 때문에 자네를 스무 배는 더 우러르고 사랑하는 거야, 스티어포스!\""]], [["He stopped, and, looking in my face, said, ‘Daisy, I believe you are in earnest, and are good.", "그가 걸음을 멈추고 내 얼굴을 들여다보더니 말했다. \"데이지, 자네 진심인 것 같군, 자네는 참 착해."], ["I wish we all were!’ Next moment he was gaily singing Mr. Peggotty’s song, as we walked at a round pace back to Yarmouth.", "우리 모두가 다 그랬으면!\" 다음 순간 그는 흥겹게 페거티 씨의 노래를 부르고 있었고, 우리는 빠른 걸음으로 야머스를 향해 되돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 22. SOME OLD SCENES, AND SOME NEW PEOPLE", "# CHAPTER 22. SOME OLD SCENES, AND SOME NEW PEOPLE"]], [["Steerforth and I stayed for more than a fortnight in that part of the country.", "스티어포스와 나는 그 고장에서 이 주가 넘도록 머물렀다."], ["We were very much together, I need not say; but occasionally we were asunder for some hours at a time.", "우리가 거의 늘 붙어 다녔다는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지만, 가끔은 몇 시간씩 따로 떨어져 지내기도 했다."], ["He was a good sailor, and I was but an indifferent one; and when he went out boating with Mr. Peggotty, which was a favourite amusement of his, I generally remained ashore.", "그는 솜씨 좋은 뱃사람이었고 나는 그저 그런 풋내기였다. 그래서 그가 패거티 아저씨와 함께 배를 타러 나갈 때면 — 그건 그가 즐겨 하던 놀이였다 — 나는 대개 뭍에 남았다."], ["My occupation of Peggotty’s spare-room put a constraint upon me, from which he was free: for, knowing how assiduously she attended on Mr.", "나는 패거티의 손님방을 쓰고 있던 터라 거기에 매여 있었지만, 그는 그런 데서 자유로웠다."], ["Barkis all day, I did not like to remain out late at night; whereas Steerforth, lying at the Inn, had nothing to consult but his own humour.", "패거티가 온종일 바키스 씨를 얼마나 정성껏 돌보는지 잘 알았기에, 나는 밤늦게까지 밖에 있고 싶지 않았다."], ["Thus it came about, that I heard of his making little treats for the fishermen at Mr.", "반면에 여관에 묵던 스티어포스는 제 기분 말고는 살필 것이 없었다."], ["Peggotty’s house of call, ‘The Willing Mind’, after I was in bed, and of his being afloat, wrapped in fishermen’s clothes, whole moonlight nights, and coming back when the morning tide was at flood.", "그렇게 해서, 내가 잠자리에 든 뒤에 그가 패거티 아저씨네 단골 술집 '윌링 마인드'에서 어부들에게 자그마한 술자리를 베풀었다는 이야기며, 어부 옷을 걸치고 달빛이 환한 밤을 통째로 바다 위에서 보내다가 아침 만조 때에야 돌아왔다는 이야기를 듣게 되었다."], ["By this time, however, I knew that his restless nature and bold spirits delighted to find a vent in rough toil and hard weather, as in any other means of excitement that presented itself freshly to him; so none of his proceedings surprised me.", "하지만 그 무렵 나는, 그의 가만있지 못하는 천성과 대담한 기질이 다른 새로운 자극거리에서 그러듯 거친 노동과 사나운 날씨 속에서 분출구를 찾기를 즐긴다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러니 그의 어떤 행동도 나를 놀라게 하지 않았다."]], [["Another cause of our being sometimes apart, was, that I had naturally an interest in going over to Blunderstone, and revisiting the old familiar scenes of my childhood; while Steerforth, after being there once, had naturally no great interest in going there again.", "우리가 가끔 떨어져 지낸 또 다른 까닭은, 나로서는 당연히 블런더스톤으로 건너가 어린 시절의 정든 옛 풍경을 다시 찾아보고 싶은 마음이 있었던 반면, 스티어포스는 거기에 한 번 가 본 뒤로는 두 번 다시 갈 흥미가 별로 없었다는 데 있었다."], ["Hence, on three or four days that I can at once recall, we went our several ways after an early breakfast, and met again at a late dinner.", "그래서 지금 당장 떠올릴 수 있는 사나흘 동안은, 우리는 이른 아침을 먹고 나서 제각기 갈 길로 흩어졌다가 늦은 저녁 식사 자리에서 다시 만났다."], ["I had no idea how he employed his time in the interval, beyond a general knowledge that he was very popular in the place, and had twenty means of actively diverting himself where another man might not have found one.", "그가 그 사이의 시간을 어떻게 보내는지 나는 알 수 없었고, 다만 그가 그 고장에서 무척 인기가 있어서, 다른 사람이라면 한 가지도 못 찾았을 자리에서 그는 스무 가지 방법으로 활기차게 즐긴다는 것만 어렴풋이 알 뿐이었다."]], [["For my own part, my occupation in my solitary pilgrimages was to recall every yard of the old road as I went along it, and to haunt the old spots, of which I never tired.", "나로 말하자면, 나 홀로 떠나는 순례에서 내가 하던 일은, 옛길을 따라 걸으며 한 자 한 자 그 길을 되새기고, 아무리 보아도 싫증 나지 않던 옛 자리들을 두루 찾아 헤매는 것이었다."], ["I haunted them, as my memory had often done, and lingered among them as my younger thoughts had lingered when I was far away.", "나는 그곳들을, 기억이 종종 그러했듯이 찾아 헤맸고, 멀리 떨어져 지내던 시절 어린 마음이 머물렀던 것처럼 그곳들 사이에 오래 머물렀다."], ["The grave beneath the tree, where both my parents lay--on which I had looked out, when it was my father’s only, with such curious feelings of compassion, and by which I had stood, so desolate, when it was opened to receive my pretty mother and her baby--the grave which Peggotty’s own faithful care had ever since kept neat, and made a garden of, I walked near, by the hour.", "나무 아래, 부모님이 함께 누운 무덤 — 아버지만 누워 있을 적에는 묘한 연민의 마음으로 바라보았고, 예쁜 어머니와 그 아기를 받아들이려 파헤쳐졌을 때에는 너무도 쓸쓸히 그 곁에 서 있던 무덤, 그 뒤로 줄곧 패거티의 한결같은 손길이 단정하게 가꾸어 작은 정원처럼 만들어 놓은 무덤 곁을, 나는 몇 시간이고 거닐었다."], ["It lay a little off the churchyard path, in a quiet corner, not so far removed but I could read the names upon the stone as I walked to and fro, startled by the sound of the church-bell when it struck the hour, for it was like a departed voice to me.", "그 무덤은 교회 묘지의 오솔길에서 살짝 비켜난 조용한 구석에 있었으되, 그렇게까지 멀지는 않아서 나는 오가며 비석에 새겨진 이름들을 읽을 수 있었다. 교회 종이 시각을 알리며 울릴 때면 나는 흠칫 놀라곤 했는데, 그 소리가 내게는 마치 떠나간 이의 목소리처럼 들렸기 때문이다."], ["My reflections at these times were always associated with the figure I was to make in life, and the distinguished things I was to do.", "그런 때 내 머릿속을 채우던 생각은 늘, 내가 인생에서 이루어 낼 모습과 내가 해낼 빛나는 일들에 관한 것이었다."], ["My echoing footsteps went to no other tune, but were as constant to that as if I had come home to build my castles in the air at a living mother’s side.", "내 발소리는 오로지 그 한 가락에 맞춰 울려 퍼졌으니, 마치 살아 계신 어머니 곁으로 집에 돌아와 허공에 성을 쌓기라도 하듯 한결같았다."]], [["There were great changes in my old home.", "내 옛집은 크게 달라져 있었다."], ["The ragged nests, so long deserted by the rooks, were gone; and the trees were lopped and topped out of their remembered shapes.", "까마귀들이 오랫동안 버려 둔 너덜너덜한 둥지들은 사라졌고, 나무들은 가지가 잘리고 우듬지가 베여 내가 기억하던 모습을 잃어버렸다."], ["The garden had run wild, and half the windows of the house were shut up.", "정원은 제멋대로 황폐해졌고, 집의 창문은 절반쯤 닫혀 있었다."], ["It was occupied, but only by a poor lunatic gentleman, and the people who took care of him.", "집에는 사람이 살았지만, 가엾은 미친 신사 하나와 그를 돌보는 사람들이 전부였다."], ["He was always sitting at my little window, looking out into the churchyard; and I wondered whether his rambling thoughts ever went upon any of the fancies that used to occupy mine, on the rosy mornings when I peeped out of that same little window in my night-clothes, and saw the sheep quietly feeding in the light of the rising sun.", "그 신사는 늘 내 작은 창가에 앉아 교회 묘지를 내다보았다. 나는 그의 두서없는 생각이, 옛날 내가 잠옷 차림으로 바로 그 작은 창문에서 밖을 내다보며 떠오르는 햇살 속에 양들이 조용히 풀을 뜯는 모습을 보던 장밋빛 아침마다 내 머릿속을 채우던 그런 공상 가운데 어느 하나라도 떠돈 적이 있을까 궁금했다."]], [["Our old neighbours, Mr. and Mrs. Grayper, were gone to South America, and the rain had made its way through the roof of their empty house, and stained the outer walls. Mr.", "우리 옛 이웃이던 그레이퍼 부부는 남아메리카로 떠나 버렸고, 빗물이 빈집의 지붕을 뚫고 새어 들어 바깥벽을 얼룩지게 해 놓았다."], ["Chillip was married again to a tall, raw-boned, high-nosed wife; and they had a weazen little baby, with a heavy head that it couldn’t hold up, and two weak staring eyes, with which it seemed to be always wondering why it had ever been born.", "칠립 의사 선생님은 키가 크고 뼈마디가 굵으며 코가 높은 부인과 재혼했다. 부부에게는 빼빼 마른 어린 아기가 하나 있었는데, 무거운 머리를 가누지 못하고 흐릿한 두 눈을 멀거니 뜬 채, 마치 자기가 도대체 왜 태어났는지 늘 궁금해하는 것만 같았다."]], [["It was with a singular jumble of sadness and pleasure that I used to linger about my native place, until the reddening winter sun admonished me that it was time to start on my returning walk.", "붉어지는 겨울 해가 이제 돌아갈 채비를 하라고 일러 줄 때까지, 나는 묘하게 뒤섞인 슬픔과 즐거움을 안고 내 고향 땅을 서성이곤 했다."], ["But, when the place was left behind, and especially when Steerforth and I were happily seated over our dinner by a blazing fire, it was delicious to think of having been there.", "그러나 그곳을 등 뒤로 남겨 두고 나서, 특히 스티어포스와 내가 활활 타오르는 난롯불 곁에 앉아 즐겁게 저녁을 먹을 때면, 거기에 다녀왔다는 생각이 더없이 달콤했다."], ["So it was, though in a softened degree, when I went to my neat room at night; and, turning over the leaves of the crocodile-book (which was always there, upon a little table), remembered with a grateful heart how blest I was in having such a friend as Steerforth, such a friend as Peggotty, and such a substitute for what I had lost as my excellent and generous aunt.", "비록 누그러진 정도이긴 했으나, 밤에 내 단정한 방으로 돌아가, 늘 작은 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 악어 그림책을 뒤적이며, 스티어포스 같은 벗과 패거티 같은 벗, 그리고 내가 잃은 것을 메워 주는 훌륭하고 너그러운 고모님이 있으니 내가 얼마나 복받은 사람인가를 고마운 마음으로 떠올릴 때도 그러했다."]], [["My nearest way to Yarmouth, in coming back from these long walks, was by a ferry.", "이 긴 산책에서 돌아올 때 야머스로 가는 가장 가까운 길은 나룻배를 타는 것이었다."], ["It landed me on the flat between the town and the sea, which I could make straight across, and so save myself a considerable circuit by the high road. Mr.", "그것은 나를 마을과 바다 사이 평평한 벌판에 내려놓았는데, 거기를 곧장 가로지르면 큰길을 따라 빙 도는 상당한 거리를 줄일 수 있었다."], ["Peggotty’s house being on that waste-place, and not a hundred yards out of my track, I always looked in as I went by.", "패거티 아저씨네 집이 바로 그 황량한 벌판에 있었고, 내가 가는 길에서 백 야드도 떨어져 있지 않았기에, 나는 지나갈 때마다 늘 안을 들여다보았다."], ["Steerforth was pretty sure to be there expecting me, and we went on together through the frosty air and gathering fog towards the twinkling lights of the town.", "스티어포스는 거기서 나를 기다리고 있을 게 거의 틀림없었고, 그러면 우리는 서리 내린 공기와 자욱이 끼어 오는 안개를 헤치고 마을의 반짝이는 불빛을 향해 함께 걸어갔다."]], [["One dark evening, when I was later than usual--for I had, that day, been making my parting visit to Blunderstone, as we were now about to return home--I found him alone in Mr. Peggotty’s house, sitting thoughtfully before the fire.", "어느 어두운 저녁, 평소보다 늦은 때였다 — 그날 나는 이제 곧 집으로 돌아갈 참이라 블런더스톤에 작별 인사를 다녀온 터였다 — 패거티 아저씨네 집에 들어가 보니 그가 혼자 난롯불 앞에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He was so intent upon his own reflections that he was quite unconscious of my approach.", "그는 자기 생각에 어찌나 깊이 빠져 있었던지 내가 다가가는 것을 전혀 알아채지 못했다."], ["This, indeed, he might easily have been if he had been less absorbed, for footsteps fell noiselessly on the sandy ground outside; but even my entrance failed to rouse him.", "사실 바깥의 모래땅 위에서는 발소리가 소리 없이 떨어졌으니, 그가 덜 몰두해 있었더라도 알아채지 못하기 쉬웠을 것이다. 하지만 내가 안으로 들어섰는데도 그는 깨어나지 못했다."], ["I was standing close to him, looking at him; and still, with a heavy brow, he was lost in his meditations.", "나는 그의 바로 곁에 서서 그를 바라보았다. 그래도 그는 미간을 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 깊은 생각 속에 빠져 있었다."]], [["He gave such a start when I put my hand upon his shoulder, that he made me start too.", "내가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹자 그가 어찌나 화들짝 놀랐던지, 나까지 덩달아 놀라고 말았다."]], [["‘You come upon me,’ he said, almost angrily, ‘like a reproachful ghost!’", "\"자네는 마치,\" 그가 거의 성난 듯이 말했다. \"사람을 나무라는 유령처럼 불쑥 나타나는군!\""]], [["‘I was obliged to announce myself, somehow,’ I replied.", "\"어떻게든 내가 왔다는 걸 알려야 했으니까.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘Have I called you down from the stars?’", "\"내가 자네를 별나라에서 불러 내리기라도 했나?\""]], [["‘No,’ he answered. ‘No.’", "\"아니.\" 그가 대답했다. \"아니야.\""]], [["‘Up from anywhere, then?’ said I, taking my seat near him.", "\"그럼 어딘가에서 끌어 올린 건가?\" 내가 그의 곁에 자리를 잡으며 말했다."]], [["‘I was looking at the pictures in the fire,’ he returned.", "\"난 불 속의 그림들을 보고 있었네.\" 그가 받았다."]], [["‘But you are spoiling them for me,’ said I, as he stirred it quickly with a piece of burning wood, striking out of it a train of red-hot sparks that went careering up the little chimney, and roaring out into the air.", "\"그런데 자네가 그걸 내게서 망쳐 버리고 있군.\" 그가 타고 있던 장작개비로 불을 빠르게 쑤셔, 시뻘건 불똥 한 줄기를 튀겨 내고, 그것들이 작은 굴뚝을 타고 솟구쳐 올라 허공으로 으르렁대며 빠져나가는 것을 보며 내가 말했다."]], [["‘You would not have seen them,’ he returned.", "\"자네는 그걸 못 봤을 거야.\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘I detest this mongrel time, neither day nor night.", "\"난 이 어중간한 시간이 질색이야. 낮도 밤도 아닌 이 시간이."], ["How late you are! Where have you been?’", "자넨 어찌 이리 늦었나! 어디 갔다 왔어?\""]], [["‘I have been taking leave of my usual walk,’ said I.", "\"늘 다니던 산책길에 작별을 고하고 왔지.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘And I have been sitting here,’ said Steerforth, glancing round the room, ‘thinking that all the people we found so glad on the night of our coming down, might--to judge from the present wasted air of the place--be dispersed, or dead, or come to I don’t know what harm.", "\"그리고 나는 여기 앉아서,\" 스티어포스가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다. \"우리가 처음 내려오던 날 밤에 그토록 반가워하던 사람들이 모두 — 지금 이 집의 휑한 분위기로 미루어 보자면 — 뿔뿔이 흩어졌거나, 죽었거나, 아니면 무슨 화를 당했는지도 모르겠다는 생각을 하던 참이었네."], ["David, I wish to God I had had a judicious father these last twenty years!’", "데이비드, 지난 이십 년 동안 내게 사리 분별 밝은 아버지가 있었더라면 얼마나 좋았을까!\""]], [["‘My dear Steerforth, what is the matter?’", "\"이보게 스티어포스, 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘I wish with all my soul I had been better guided!’ he exclaimed.", "\"내가 더 잘 인도받았더라면 얼마나 좋았을까, 온 마음으로 그렇게 바라네!\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["‘I wish with all my soul I could guide myself better!’", "\"내가 나 자신을 더 잘 이끌 수 있었더라면, 온 마음으로 그렇게 바라!\""]], [["There was a passionate dejection in his manner that quite amazed me.", "그의 태도에는 나를 어리둥절하게 할 만큼 격정에 찬 의기소침함이 어려 있었다."], ["He was more unlike himself than I could have supposed possible.", "그는 내가 가능하리라 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 더 그답지 않았다."]], [["‘It would be better to be this poor Peggotty, or his lout of a nephew,’ he said, getting up and leaning moodily against the chimney-piece, with his face towards the fire, ‘than to be myself, twenty times richer and twenty times wiser, and be the torment to myself that I have been, in this Devil’s bark of a boat, within the last half-hour!’", "\"차라리 이 가엾은 패거티가 되거나, 그 미련한 조카가 되는 편이 나았을 거야.\" 그가 일어나서 음울하게 난로 선반에 몸을 기대고 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 향한 채 말했다. \"나 자신이 되는 것보다는 말이야. 스무 배는 더 부유하고 스무 배는 더 영리한 이 나 자신, 지난 반 시간 동안 이 빌어먹을 배의 껍데기 속에서 스스로를 괴롭혀 온 이 나보다는!\""]], [["I was so confounded by the alteration in him, that at first I could only observe him in silence, as he stood leaning his head upon his hand, and looking gloomily down at the fire.", "나는 그의 그 변화에 어찌나 당황했던지, 처음에는 그저 말없이 그를 바라보기만 했다. 그는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 서서 음울하게 불을 내려다보고 있었다."], ["At length I begged him, with all the earnestness I felt, to tell me what had occurred to cross him so unusually, and to let me sympathize with him, if I could not hope to advise him.", "마침내 나는 내가 느끼는 온 진심을 담아, 무엇이 그를 그토록 유난히 어지럽혔는지 말해 달라고, 충고는 못 해 줄지언정 함께 아파할 수는 있게 해 달라고 간청했다."], ["Before I had well concluded, he began to laugh--fretfully at first, but soon with returning gaiety.", "내가 말을 채 맺기도 전에 그는 웃기 시작했는데, 처음에는 신경질적으로 웃다가, 이내 본래의 명랑함을 되찾으며 웃었다."]], [["‘Tut, it’s nothing, Daisy!", "\"쳇, 아무것도 아니야, 데이지!"], ["nothing!’ he replied.", "아무것도 아니라고!\""], ["‘I told you at the inn in London, I am heavy company for myself, sometimes.", "그가 대답했다. \"런던 여관에서도 말했잖아, 가끔 나는 나 자신에게 버거운 동무라고."], ["I have been a nightmare to myself, just now--must have had one, I think.", "방금 나 자신에게 악몽이 되어 있었어 — 내 생각엔 악몽을 꾼 게 틀림없어."], ["At odd dull times, nursery tales come up into the memory, unrecognized for what they are.", "가끔 따분하고 음침한 때에는, 옛날 옛적 동화들이 무엇인지도 모를 모습으로 기억 속에 떠오르거든."], ["I believe I have been confounding myself with the bad boy who “didn’t care”, and became food for lions--a grander kind of going to the dogs, I suppose.", "아마 나는 '나는 상관없어' 하다가 사자 밥이 되어 버린 못된 아이와 나 자신을 헷갈렸던 모양이야 — 짐작건대 좀 더 거창하게 신세를 망치는 셈이지."], ["What old women call the horrors, have been creeping over me from head to foot.", "노파들이 '오싹증'이라 부르는 게 머리끝부터 발끝까지 슬금슬금 기어 다녔어."], ["I have been afraid of myself.’", "나는 나 자신이 무서웠던 거야.\""]], [["‘You are afraid of nothing else, I think,’ said I.", "\"자넨 그것 말고는 무서운 게 없는 사람 같은데.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps not, and yet may have enough to be afraid of too,’ he answered.", "\"그럴지도 모르지, 하지만 무서워해야 할 것도 어쩌면 넉넉히 있을 거야.\""], ["‘Well! So it goes by!", "그가 대답했다. \"뭐!"], ["I am not about to be hipped again, David; but I tell you, my good fellow, once more, that it would have been well for me (and for more than me) if I had had a steadfast and judicious father!’", "그렇게 지나가는 거지! 데이비드, 다시 우울해질 생각은 없네. 하지만, 이 친구야, 다시 한번 말해 두건대, 내게 한결같고 사리 분별 밝은 아버지가 있었더라면 나에게는 (그리고 나뿐 아니라 더 많은 이에게도) 좋았을 거야!\""]], [["His face was always full of expression, but I never saw it express such a dark kind of earnestness as when he said these words, with his glance bent on the fire.", "그의 얼굴은 늘 표정이 풍부했지만, 시선을 불에 박은 채 그 말을 할 때처럼 그토록 어두운 진지함을 드러내는 것을 나는 본 적이 없었다."]], [["‘So much for that!’ he said, making as if he tossed something light into the air, with his hand.", "\"그 얘기는 그만하지!\" 그가 손으로 무언가 가벼운 것을 허공에 던져 버리는 시늉을 하며 말했다."], ["“‘Why, being gone, I am a man again,”", "\"'아, 그것이 사라지니 나는 다시 사람이 되었도다.'"], ["like Macbeth.", "맥베스처럼 말이야."], ["And now for dinner!", "자, 이제 저녁을 먹자고!"], ["If I have not (Macbeth-like) broken up the feast with most admired disorder, Daisy.’", "내가 (맥베스처럼) 더없이 놀랄 만한 소란으로 잔치판을 깨 버린 게 아니라면 말이지, 데이지.\""]], [["‘But where are they all, I wonder!’ said I.", "\"한데 다들 어디 갔을까 궁금하군!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘God knows,’ said Steerforth.", "\"누가 알겠나.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘After strolling to the ferry looking for you, I strolled in here and found the place deserted.", "\"자네를 찾으러 나루터까지 어슬렁어슬렁 갔다가, 여기로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어와 보니 집이 텅 비어 있더군."], ["That set me thinking, and you found me thinking.’", "그래서 생각에 잠겼고, 그렇게 생각하고 있는 걸 자네가 찾아낸 거지.\""]], [["The advent of Mrs. Gummidge with a basket, explained how the house had happened to be empty.", "거미지 부인이 바구니를 들고 나타나면서 어찌하여 집이 비게 되었는지가 밝혀졌다."], ["She had hurried out to buy something that was needed, against Mr. Peggotty’s return with the tide; and had left the door open in the meanwhile, lest Ham and little Em’ly, with whom it was an early night, should come home while she was gone.", "부인은 조수와 함께 돌아올 패거티 아저씨를 위해 필요한 것을 사러 부랴부랴 나갔던 것이고, 그 사이에 이른 밤이면 으레 일찍 잠자리에 드는 햄과 어린 에밀리가 자기 없는 틈에 집에 돌아올까 봐 문을 열어 둔 채로 갔던 것이다."], ["Steerforth, after very much improving Mrs. Gummidge’s spirits by a cheerful salutation and a jocose embrace, took my arm, and hurried me away.", "스티어포스는 명랑한 인사와 익살스러운 포옹으로 거미지 부인의 기분을 한껏 북돋아 준 다음, 내 팔을 끼고 서둘러 나를 데리고 나왔다."]], [["He had improved his own spirits, no less than Mrs. Gummidge’s, for they were again at their usual flow, and he was full of vivacious conversation as we went along.", "그는 거미지 부인 못지않게 제 기분도 북돋아 놓은 터라, 다시 평소의 흥이 흘러넘쳤고, 길을 걷는 내내 활기찬 이야기로 넘쳤다."]], [["‘And so,’ he said, gaily, ‘we abandon this buccaneer life tomorrow, do we?’", "\"그래서 말이지,\" 그가 흥겹게 말했다. \"우린 내일 이 해적 생활을 접는 거지, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘So we agreed,’ I returned.", "\"그렇게 정했잖아.\" 내가 받았다."], ["‘And our places by the coach are taken, you know.’", "\"그리고 역마차 자리도 잡아 뒀고, 알다시피.\""]], [["‘Ay!", "\"그래!"], ["there’s no help for it, I suppose,’ said Steerforth.", "어쩔 수 없는 노릇이겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘I have almost forgotten that there is anything to do in the world but to go out tossing on the sea here. I wish there was not.’", "\"이 세상에 여기서 바다 위에 흔들리며 나가는 것 말고도 할 일이 있다는 걸 까맣게 잊어버릴 뻔했어. 차라리 그런 게 없었으면 좋겠군.\""]], [["‘As long as the novelty should last,’ said I, laughing.", "\"새로움이 가시지 않는 동안이나 그렇겠지.\" 내가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘Like enough,’ he returned; ‘though there’s a sarcastic meaning in that observation for an amiable piece of innocence like my young friend. Well!", "\"그럴 법도 하지.\" 그가 받았다. \"하긴 그 말 속엔 내 어린 친구처럼 상냥하고 순진한 사람에게는 비꼬는 뜻이 담겨 있긴 하지만 말이야. 뭐!"], ["I dare say I am a capricious fellow, David.", "내가 변덕쟁이라는 건 나도 인정하네, 데이비드."], ["I know I am; but while the iron is hot, I can strike it vigorously too.", "그건 나도 알아. 하지만 쇠가 달아올라 있는 동안에는, 나도 그걸 힘차게 두들길 수 있다고."], ["I could pass a reasonably good examination already, as a pilot in these waters, I think.’", "지금이라도 이 일대의 물길을 안내하는 도선사로 꽤 괜찮은 시험을 치를 수 있을 것 같아.\""]], [["‘Mr. Peggotty says you are a wonder,’ I returned.", "\"패거티 아저씨는 자네가 놀라운 사람이라고 하던데.\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘A nautical phenomenon, eh?’ laughed Steerforth.", "\"바다의 경이로운 존재란 말이지, 응?\" 스티어포스가 웃었다."]], [["‘Indeed he does, and you know how truly; I know how ardent you are in any pursuit you follow, and how easily you can master it.", "\"정말 그렇게 말하던걸, 게다가 그게 얼마나 맞는 말인지 자네도 알잖아. 자네가 어떤 일을 좇든 얼마나 열성적인지, 또 얼마나 손쉽게 통달해 버리는지 나는 잘 알지."], ["And that amazes me most in you, Steerforth--that you should be contented with such fitful uses of your powers.’", "그래서 자네에게서 가장 놀라운 게 바로 그거야, 스티어포스 — 자네가 그 재능을 그렇게 변덕스럽게 띄엄띄엄 쓰면서도 만족한다는 게 말이야.\""]], [["‘Contented?’ he answered, merrily.", "\"만족한다고?\" 그가 즐겁게 대답했다."], ["‘I am never contented, except with your freshness, my gentle Daisy.", "\"나는 결코 만족하는 법이 없어, 내 상냥한 데이지, 자네의 그 싱그러움만 빼고는 말이지."], ["As to fitfulness, I have never learnt the art of binding myself to any of the wheels on which the Ixions of these days are turning round and round.", "변덕스럽다는 것으로 말하자면, 나는 요즘의 익시온들이 빙글빙글 매여 도는 그 어떤 수레바퀴에도 나 자신을 동여매는 재주를 끝내 배우지 못했네."], ["I missed it somehow in a bad apprenticeship, and now don’t care about it.---You know I have bought a boat down here?’", "어쩌다 잘못된 견습 시절에 그걸 놓쳐 버렸고, 이제는 그런 데 관심도 없어. — 내가 여기서 배 한 척 산 거 알아?\""]], [["‘What an extraordinary fellow you are, Steerforth!’ I exclaimed, stopping--for this was the first I had heard of it.", "\"자넨 참 별난 친구야, 스티어포스!\" 내가 걸음을 멈추며 외쳤다 — 그건 처음 듣는 이야기였으니까."], ["‘When you may never care to come near the place again!’", "\"두 번 다시 이 근방에 오고 싶지 않을지도 모르면서!\""]], [["‘I don’t know that,’ he returned.", "\"그건 또 모르는 일이지.\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘I have taken a fancy to the place.", "\"이곳이 마음에 들었거든. 아무튼,\""], ["At all events,’ walking me briskly on, ‘I have bought a boat that was for sale--a clipper, Mr.", "그가 나를 발랄하게 걷게 하며 말했다."], ["Peggotty says; and so she is--and Mr. Peggotty will be master of her in my absence.’", "\"매물로 나와 있던 배를 한 척 샀어 — 패거티 아저씨 말로는 쾌속선이라더군, 정말 그렇기도 하고 — 그래서 내가 없는 동안엔 패거티 아저씨가 그 배의 선장이 되는 거야.\""]], [["‘Now I understand you, Steerforth!’ said I, exultingly.", "\"이제 알겠어, 스티어포스!\" 내가 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["‘You pretend to have bought it for yourself, but you have really done so to confer a benefit on him.", "\"자넨 자네 쓰려고 산 척하지만, 사실은 아저씨에게 은혜를 베풀려고 그런 거지."], ["I might have known as much at first, knowing you.", "자넬 잘 아니까 처음부터 그쯤은 알아챘어야 했는데."], ["My dear kind Steerforth, how can I tell you what I think of your generosity?’", "내 친절한 스티어포스, 자네의 너그러움을 내가 어떻게 다 말로 표현하겠나?\""]], [["‘Tush!’ he answered, turning red.", "\"쳇!\" 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["‘The less said, the better.’", "\"말은 적을수록 좋아.\""]], [["‘Didn’t I know?’ cried I, ‘didn’t I say that there was not a joy, or sorrow, or any emotion of such honest hearts that was indifferent to you?’", "\"내가 모를 줄 알아?\" 내가 외쳤다. \"그렇게 정직한 마음들의 기쁨이나 슬픔이나 그 어떤 감정도 자네에게는 남의 일이 아니라고, 내가 그러지 않았던가?\""]], [["‘Aye, aye,’ he answered, ‘you told me all that.", "\"그래, 그래.\" 그가 대답했다. \"자네 다 말했어."], ["There let it rest. We have said enough!’", "그 얘긴 그쯤 해 두자고. 충분히 했어!\""]], [["Afraid of offending him by pursuing the subject when he made so light of it, I only pursued it in my thoughts as we went on at even a quicker pace than before.", "그가 그 얘기를 그토록 대수롭지 않게 여기는 마당에 더 파고들어 그의 기분을 상하게 할까 봐, 나는 그저 마음속으로만 그 생각을 이어 갔고, 우리는 아까보다 한층 더 빠른 걸음으로 걸어갔다."]], [["‘She must be newly rigged,’ said Steerforth, ‘and I shall leave Littimer behind to see it done, that I may know she is quite complete.", "\"배에 삭구를 새로 갖춰야 해.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"리티머를 남겨 두고 그 일을 맡길 참이야, 배가 완전히 다 갖춰졌는지 확인하려고."], ["Did I tell you Littimer had come down?’", "리티머가 내려왔다는 얘기 했던가?\""]], [["‘No.’", "\"아니.\""]], [["‘Oh yes! came down this morning, with a letter from my mother.’", "\"아, 그래! 오늘 아침에 내려왔어, 어머니 편지를 가지고.\""]], [["As our looks met, I observed that he was pale even to his lips, though he looked very steadily at me.", "우리 눈길이 마주쳤을 때, 나는 그가 나를 아주 차분하게 바라보면서도 입술까지 창백해져 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["I feared that some difference between him and his mother might have led to his being in the frame of mind in which I had found him at the solitary fireside. I hinted so.", "나는 그와 그의 어머니 사이에 무슨 다툼이 있어서, 그가 그 외로운 난롯가에서 내가 본 그런 심정에 빠졌던 게 아닐까 걱정스러웠다. 그래서 그렇게 넌지시 말해 보았다."]], [["‘Oh no!’ he said, shaking his head, and giving a slight laugh.", "\"아, 천만에!\" 그가 고개를 저으며 가볍게 웃었다."], ["‘Nothing of the sort! Yes.", "\"그런 건 전혀 아니야! 응."], ["He is come down, that man of mine.’", "그자가 내려왔어, 내 그 하인 말이야.\""]], [["‘The same as ever?’ said I.", "\"여전한가?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘The same as ever,’ said Steerforth.", "\"여전하지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Distant and quiet as the North Pole.", "\"북극처럼 데면데면하고 조용하지."], ["He shall see to the boat being fresh named.", "그자가 배 이름을 새로 다는 걸 챙길 거야."], ["She’s the “Stormy Petrel” now.", "지금은 '폭풍의 바다제비'라는 이름이거든."], ["What does Mr. Peggotty care for Stormy Petrels!", "패거티 아저씨가 폭풍의 바다제비 따위를 알 게 뭐야!"], ["I’ll have her christened again.’", "다시 세례를 받게 해 줘야지.\""]], [["‘By what name?’ I asked.", "\"무슨 이름으로?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘The “Little Em’ly”.’", "\"'어린 에밀리.'\""]], [["As he had continued to look steadily at me, I took it as a reminder that he objected to being extolled for his consideration.", "그가 줄곧 나를 차분하게 바라보고 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 그가 자기 배려심을 추켜세우는 것에 거북해한다는 신호로 받아들였다."], ["I could not help showing in my face how much it pleased me, but I said little, and he resumed his usual smile, and seemed relieved.", "나는 그게 얼마나 기쁜지 얼굴에 드러내지 않을 수 없었지만, 말은 거의 하지 않았다. 그러자 그는 다시 평소의 미소를 되찾았고, 마음이 놓인 듯했다."]], [["‘But see here,’ he said, looking before us, ‘where the original little Em’ly comes!", "\"한데 저기 좀 봐.\" 그가 우리 앞을 바라보며 말했다. \"진짜 어린 에밀리가 오는군!"], ["And that fellow with her, eh?", "그리고 함께 오는 저 친구도 있고, 응?"], ["Upon my soul, he’s a true knight. He never leaves her!’", "정말이지, 저 친구는 진짜배기 기사야. 절대로 에밀리 곁을 떠나는 법이 없으니!\""]], [["Ham was a boat-builder in these days, having improved a natural ingenuity in that handicraft, until he had become a skilled workman.", "햄은 요즘 들어 배 목수가 되어 있었는데, 그 일에 타고난 손재주를 갈고닦은 끝에 솜씨 좋은 일꾼이 되어 있었다."], ["He was in his working-dress, and looked rugged enough, but manly withal, and a very fit protector for the blooming little creature at his side.", "그는 작업복 차림이라 꽤나 거칠어 보였지만, 그래도 사내다웠고, 곁에 선 꽃처럼 피어나는 작은 아가씨를 지켜 주기에 더없이 어울리는 사람이었다."], ["Indeed, there was a frankness in his face, an honesty, and an undisguised show of his pride in her, and his love for her, which were, to me, the best of good looks.", "사실 그의 얼굴에는 진솔함과 정직함, 그리고 그녀에 대한 자랑스러움과 사랑을 감추지 않고 드러내는 표정이 있었는데, 내게는 그것이 가장 잘난 외모였다."], ["I thought, as they came towards us, that they were well matched even in that particular.", "그들이 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보면서, 나는 그 점에서도 둘이 참 잘 어울린다고 생각했다."]], [["She withdrew her hand timidly from his arm as we stopped to speak to them, and blushed as she gave it to Steerforth and to me.", "우리가 그들과 이야기를 나누려고 걸음을 멈추자, 그녀는 수줍게 그의 팔에서 손을 거두었고, 스티어포스와 내게 손을 내밀며 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["When they passed on, after we had exchanged a few words, she did not like to replace that hand, but, still appearing timid and constrained, walked by herself.", "몇 마디 주고받은 뒤 그들이 지나쳐 갈 때, 그녀는 그 손을 다시 그의 팔에 얹고 싶어 하지 않으면서, 여전히 수줍고 어색한 기색으로 혼자 떨어져 걸었다."], ["I thought all this very pretty and engaging, and Steerforth seemed to think so too, as we looked after them fading away in the light of a young moon.", "나는 이 모든 것이 무척 어여쁘고 사랑스럽다고 생각했고, 어린 달빛 속으로 그들이 멀어져 가는 모습을 바라보는 스티어포스도 그렇게 여기는 듯했다."]], [["Suddenly there passed us--evidently following them--a young woman whose approach we had not observed, but whose face I saw as she went by, and thought I had a faint remembrance of.", "그때 갑자기 우리 곁을 — 분명 그들을 뒤따르며 — 한 젊은 여자가 지나갔다. 우리는 그녀가 다가오는 것을 미처 보지 못했으나, 지나칠 때 나는 그 얼굴을 보았고, 어딘가 어렴풋이 본 적이 있는 듯하다고 생각했다."], ["She was lightly dressed; looked bold, and haggard, and flaunting, and poor; but seemed, for the time, to have given all that to the wind which was blowing, and to have nothing in her mind but going after them.", "그녀는 차림새가 가벼웠고, 대담하면서도 초췌하고, 야하면서도 가난해 보였다. 하지만 그 순간만큼은 그 모든 것을 불어오는 바람에 다 내맡긴 듯했고, 마음속엔 오직 그들을 뒤쫓는 생각밖에 없는 것 같았다."], ["As the dark distant level, absorbing their figures into itself, left but itself visible between us and the sea and clouds, her figure disappeared in like manner, still no nearer to them than before.", "멀리 어두운 평지가 그들의 모습을 제 속으로 빨아들여, 우리와 바다와 구름 사이에 그저 어둠만 남겨 놓았을 때, 그녀의 모습도 마찬가지로 사라져 버렸으되, 여전히 그들에게 조금도 더 가까워지지는 않은 채였다."]], [["‘That is a black shadow to be following the girl,’ said Steerforth, standing still; ‘what does it mean?’", "\"저 여자가 시커먼 그림자처럼 그 아가씨를 따라가는군.\" 스티어포스가 걸음을 멈추고 서서 말했다. \"무슨 뜻일까?\""]], [["He spoke in a low voice that sounded almost strange to Me.", "그가 어찌나 낮은 목소리로 말했던지, 내게는 거의 낯설게 들렸다."]], [["‘She must have it in her mind to beg of them, I think,’ said I.", "\"저 여자도 그들에게 동냥할 마음을 품고 있나 보지.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘A beggar would be no novelty,’ said Steerforth; ‘but it is a strange thing that the beggar should take that shape tonight.’", "\"거지라면 별일도 아니지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그런데 하필 오늘 밤 그 거지가 저런 모습을 하고 있다는 게 이상하단 말이야.\""]], [["‘Why?’ I asked.", "\"왜?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘For no better reason, truly, than because I was thinking,’ he said, after a pause, ‘of something like it, when it came by.", "\"솔직히 별다른 까닭은 없어, 그저 그게 지나갈 때,\" 그가 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다. \"내가 그것과 비슷한 무언가를 생각하고 있었기 때문이지."], ["Where the Devil did it come from, I wonder!’", "대체 저게 어디서 나타난 걸까, 참!\""]], [["‘From the shadow of this wall, I think,’ said I, as we emerged upon a road on which a wall abutted.", "\"내 생각엔 이 담벼락 그늘에서 나온 것 같아.\" 우리가 담벼락이 맞닿은 길로 나서며 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s gone!’ he returned, looking over his shoulder.", "\"사라졌군!\" 그가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 받았다."], ["‘And all ill go with it.", "\"그 여자랑 같이 온갖 흉한 것도 다 사라져 버려라."], ["Now for our dinner!’", "자, 이제 저녁 먹으러 가자고!\""]], [["But he looked again over his shoulder towards the sea-line glimmering afar off, and yet again.", "하지만 그는 다시 어깨 너머로, 저 멀리 어슴푸레 빛나는 수평선 쪽을 돌아보았고, 또 한 번 돌아보았다."], ["And he wondered about it, in some broken expressions, several times, in the short remainder of our walk; and only seemed to forget it when the light of fire and candle shone upon us, seated warm and merry, at table.", "그리고 우리가 걸어가는 짧은 남은 길 동안에도, 그는 몇 번이나 끊어진 말투로 그 일을 두고 의아해했다. 그리고 따뜻하고 흥겹게 식탁에 둘러앉아 난롯불과 촛불이 우리를 비출 때에야 비로소 그 일을 잊은 듯했다."]], [["Littimer was there, and had his usual effect upon me.", "리티머가 거기 있었고, 그는 늘 그랬듯 나를 주눅 들게 했다."], ["When I said to him that I hoped Mrs. Steerforth and Miss Dartle were well, he answered respectfully (and of course respectably), that they were tolerably well, he thanked me, and had sent their compliments.", "내가 그에게 스티어포스 부인과 다틀 양은 잘 계시느냐고 물었더니, 그는 정중하게 (물론 격식 있게) 두 분 다 그럭저럭 잘 지내신다고, 고맙다고, 그리고 안부를 전하셨다고 대답했다."], ["This was all, and yet he seemed to me to say as plainly as a man could say: ‘You are very young, sir; you are exceedingly young.’", "그게 다였지만, 그래도 그는 사람이 할 수 있는 한 분명하게 이렇게 말하는 것만 같았다. \"도련님은 아주 어리십니다, 손님. 지나치게 어리시지요.\""]], [["We had almost finished dinner, when taking a step or two towards the table, from the corner where he kept watch upon us, or rather upon me, as I felt, he said to his master:", "저녁을 거의 다 먹어 갈 무렵, 우리를 — 아니, 내가 느끼기로는 차라리 나를 — 지켜보던 구석에서 그가 식탁 쪽으로 한두 걸음 다가오더니 제 주인에게 말했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir.", "\"실례합니다, 도련님."], ["Miss Mowcher is down here.’", "마우처 양이 여기 내려와 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Who?’ cried Steerforth, much astonished.", "\"누구?\" 스티어포스가 몹시 놀라 외쳤다."]], [["‘Miss Mowcher, sir.’", "\"마우처 양입니다, 도련님.\""]], [["‘Why, what on earth does she do here?’ said Steerforth.", "\"아니, 도대체 그 여자가 여긴 무슨 일로?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘It appears to be her native part of the country, sir.", "\"이 일대가 그 여자의 고향인 듯합니다, 도련님."], ["She informs me that she makes one of her professional visits here, every year, sir.", "본인 말로는 해마다 여기 한 번씩 일하러 내려온다고 합니다, 도련님."], ["I met her in the street this afternoon, and she wished to know if she might have the honour of waiting on you after dinner, sir.’", "오늘 오후에 길에서 마주쳤는데, 저녁 식사 후에 도련님을 뵙는 영광을 누릴 수 있겠느냐고 묻더군요, 도련님.\""]], [["‘Do you know the Giantess in question, Daisy?’ inquired Steerforth.", "\"자네, 지금 말하는 그 여자 거인을 아나, 데이지?\" 스티어포스가 물었다."]], [["I was obliged to confess--I felt ashamed, even of being at this disadvantage before Littimer--that Miss Mowcher and I were wholly unacquainted.", "나는 — 리티머 앞에서 이런 처지에 놓이는 것조차 부끄러웠지만 — 마우처 양과 나는 전혀 모르는 사이라고 털어놓을 수밖에 없었다."]], [["‘Then you shall know her,’ said Steerforth, ‘for she is one of the seven wonders of the world.", "\"그럼 알게 될 걸세.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그 여자는 세상의 일곱 가지 불가사의 중 하나거든."], ["When Miss Mowcher comes, show her in.’", "마우처 양이 오거든 들여보내게.\""]], [["I felt some curiosity and excitement about this lady, especially as Steerforth burst into a fit of laughing when I referred to her, and positively refused to answer any question of which I made her the subject.", "나는 이 부인에 대해 적잖은 호기심과 흥분을 느꼈는데, 특히 내가 그녀 이야기를 꺼낼 때마다 스티어포스가 웃음보를 터뜨리며 그녀를 화제로 삼은 질문에는 단 하나도 대답하기를 한사코 거부했기 때문이다."], ["I remained, therefore, in a state of considerable expectation until the cloth had been removed some half an hour, and we were sitting over our decanter of wine before the fire, when the door opened, and Littimer, with his habitual serenity quite undisturbed, announced:", "그래서 나는 상당한 기대 속에 잠겨 있었다. 식탁보가 치워지고 반 시간쯤 지나, 우리가 난롯불 앞에서 포도주 병을 앞에 두고 앉아 있을 때 문이 열렸고, 리티머가 평소의 평온함을 조금도 흩뜨리지 않은 채 알렸다."]], [["‘Miss Mowcher!’", "\"마우처 양이십니다!\""]], [["I looked at the doorway and saw nothing.", "나는 문간을 바라보았으나 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["I was still looking at the doorway, thinking that Miss Mowcher was a long while making her appearance, when, to my infinite astonishment, there came waddling round a sofa which stood between me and it, a pursy dwarf, of about forty or forty-five, with a very large head and face, a pair of roguish grey eyes, and such extremely little arms, that, to enable herself to lay a finger archly against her snub nose, as she ogled Steerforth, she was obliged to meet the finger half-way, and lay her nose against it.", "마우처 양이 모습을 드러내는 데 어지간히 오래 걸린다고 생각하며 여전히 문간을 보고 있는데, 놀랍게도 나와 문 사이에 놓인 소파 모퉁이를 돌아 뒤뚱뒤뚱 걸어 나오는 것이 있었다. 마흔이나 마흔다섯쯤 된 통통한 난쟁이였는데, 머리와 얼굴은 아주 컸고, 한 쌍의 장난기 어린 잿빛 눈을 하고 있었으며, 팔은 어찌나 짧던지, 스티어포스에게 추파를 던지며 들창코에 손가락을 짐짓 갖다 대려면 손가락을 절반쯤 마중 보내고 거기에 코를 들이밀어야 할 정도였다."], ["Her chin, which was what is called a double chin, was so fat that it entirely swallowed up the strings of her bonnet, bow and all.", "흔히 이중 턱이라 부르는 그녀의 턱은 어찌나 살집이 두툼했던지 보닛 끈을 매듭이며 뭐며 통째로 삼켜 버렸다."], ["Throat she had none; waist she had none; legs she had none, worth mentioning; for though she was more than full-sized down to where her waist would have been, if she had had any, and though she terminated, as human beings generally do, in a pair of feet, she was so short that she stood at a common-sized chair as at a table, resting a bag she carried on the seat.", "목이라 할 것도 없었고, 허리라 할 것도 없었으며, 다리라 내세울 만한 것도 없었다. 허리가 있었다면 허리쯤 될 자리까지는 풍만하다 못해 넘쳤고, 또 사람들이 대개 그렇듯 두 발로 끝맺기는 했지만, 키가 어찌나 작던지 보통 크기의 의자 앞에 서 있는 모습이 마치 식탁 앞에 선 것 같았고, 들고 온 가방을 그 의자 좌석 위에 올려놓았다."], ["This lady--dressed in an off-hand, easy style; bringing her nose and her forefinger together, with the difficulty I have described; standing with her head necessarily on one side, and, with one of her sharp eyes shut up, making an uncommonly knowing face--after ogling Steerforth for a few moments, broke into a torrent of words.", "이 부인은 — 무심한 듯 편안한 차림새로, 앞서 말한 그 어려움을 무릅쓰고 코와 집게손가락을 맞붙인 채, 머리를 어쩔 수 없이 한쪽으로 기울이고, 날카로운 눈 하나는 감아서, 유난히 알 듯 말 듯한 표정을 지으며 — 잠시 스티어포스에게 추파를 던지더니, 봇물 터지듯 말을 쏟아 내기 시작했다."]], [["‘What!", "\"어머!"], ["My flower!’ she pleasantly began, shaking her large head at him.", "내 꽃송이!\" 그녀가 그에게 큰 머리를 흔들어 보이며 기분 좋게 시작했다."], ["‘You’re there, are you!", "\"거기 있었구나, 응!"], ["Oh, you naughty boy, fie for shame, what do you do so far away from home?", "아이고 이 못된 녀석, 부끄러운 줄 알아야지, 집에서 이리 멀리 떨어진 데서 뭐 하는 거니?"], ["Up to mischief, I’ll be bound.", "틀림없이 무슨 못된 짓 꾸미는 게지."], ["Oh, you’re a downy fellow, Steerforth, so you are, and I’m another, ain’t I? Ha, ha, ha!", "아이고, 넌 참 약삭빠른 녀석이야, 스티어포스, 정말이지. 나도 한 약삭빠름 하지, 안 그래? 하하하!"], ["You’d have betted a hundred pound to five, now, that you wouldn’t have seen me here, wouldn’t you?", "자, 백 파운드 대 오 파운드로 내기를 걸었더라도 여기서 날 만나리라곤 생각도 못 했겠지, 그렇지?"], ["Bless you, man alive, I’m everywhere.", "어이구 이 사람아, 난 어디에나 있다고."], ["I’m here and there, and where not, like the conjurer’s half-crown in the lady’s handkercher.", "여기도 있고 저기도 있고, 안 가는 데가 없어, 마치 마술사의 반 크라운 동전이 부인의 손수건 속에 들어가듯이 말이야."], ["Talking of handkerchers--and talking of ladies--what a comfort you are to your blessed mother, ain’t you, my dear boy, over one of my shoulders, and I don’t say which!’", "손수건 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데 — 그리고 부인들 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데 — 너야말로 네 그 복받은 어머니에게 얼마나 큰 위안이겠니, 안 그래, 내 귀여운 녀석, 내 한쪽 어깨 너머로 보자면 말이야, 어느 쪽인지는 말 안 하련다!\""]], [["Miss Mowcher untied her bonnet, at this passage of her discourse, threw back the strings, and sat down, panting, on a footstool in front of the fire--making a kind of arbour of the dining table, which spread its mahogany shelter above her head.", "마우처 양은 이야기를 이 대목까지 풀어 놓더니, 보닛을 끄르고 끈을 뒤로 젖힌 뒤, 헐떡거리며 난롯불 앞 발받침 위에 주저앉았다 — 머리 위로 마호가니 그늘을 드리운 식탁이 일종의 정자 노릇을 해 주는 모양새였다."]], [["‘Oh my stars and what’s-their-names!’ she went on, clapping a hand on each of her little knees, and glancing shrewdly at me, ‘I’m of too full a habit, that’s the fact, Steerforth.", "\"아이고 맙소사, 별이며 뭐며 다!\" 그녀가 작은 무릎을 한쪽씩 손으로 탁탁 치며, 나를 빈틈없이 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다. \"내가 너무 살이 쪘다는 게 사실이야, 스티어포스."], ["After a flight of stairs, it gives me as much trouble to draw every breath I want, as if it was a bucket of water.", "계단 한 번 오르고 나면, 숨 한 번 들이쉬는 게 마치 물 한 동이를 길어 올리는 것만큼이나 힘들다니까."], ["If you saw me looking out of an upper window, you’d think I was a fine woman, wouldn’t you?’", "만약 내가 위층 창문에서 내다보는 걸 본다면, 날 멋진 미인이라고 여길걸, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘I should think that, wherever I saw you,’ replied Steerforth.", "\"어디서 뵙든 그렇게 여길 텐데요.\" 스티어포스가 받았다."]], [["‘Go along, you dog, do!’ cried the little creature, making a whisk at him with the handkerchief with which she was wiping her face, ‘and don’t be impudent!", "\"저리 가, 이 능청맞은 녀석, 정말!\" 그 작은 사람이, 제 얼굴을 닦던 손수건을 그에게 휙 휘두르며 외쳤다. \"그리고 까불지 좀 마!"], ["But I give you my word and honour I was at Lady Mithers’s last week--THERE’S a woman!", "하지만 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 지난주에 미더스 부인 댁에 갔었거든 — 그 부인이야말로 대단한 여자지!"], ["How SHE wears!--and Mithers himself came into the room where I was waiting for her--THERE’S a man! How HE wears!", "어찌나 잘 가꾸는지! — 그런데 미더스 씨가 본인이, 내가 부인을 기다리던 방으로 들어오지 뭐야 — 그 양반이야말로 대단한 남자지!"], ["and his wig too, for he’s had it these ten years--and he went on at that rate in the complimentary line, that I began to think I should be obliged to ring the bell. Ha! ha! ha!", "어찌나 잘 가꾸는지! 가발까지도, 그게 십 년째 쓰고 있는 건데 말이야 — 그러더니 어찌나 칭찬을 늘어놓는지, 나는 종이라도 울려서 사람을 불러야 하나 싶을 지경이었다니까. 하하하!"], ["He’s a pleasant wretch, but he wants principle.’", "유쾌한 망나니지만, 줏대가 없어.\""]], [["‘What were you doing for Lady Mithers?’ asked Steerforth.", "\"미더스 부인 댁에서는 무슨 일을 한 겁니까?\" 스티어포스가 물었다."]], [["‘That’s tellings, my blessed infant,’ she retorted, tapping her nose again, screwing up her face, and twinkling her eyes like an imp of supernatural intelligence.", "\"그건 비밀이지, 내 복덩이 아기야.\" 그녀가 다시 코를 톡톡 두드리고, 얼굴을 잔뜩 찌푸리고, 초자연적인 지혜를 지닌 꼬마 도깨비처럼 두 눈을 반짝이며 되받았다."], ["‘Never YOU mind!", "\"넌 신경 끄셔!"], ["You’d like to know whether I stop her hair from falling off, or dye it, or touch up her complexion, or improve her eyebrows, wouldn’t you?", "내가 그 부인 머리 빠지는 걸 막아 주는지, 염색을 해 주는지, 안색을 손봐 주는지, 눈썹을 다듬어 주는지 알고 싶은 게지, 안 그래?"], ["And so you shall, my darling--when I tell you! Do you know what my great grandfather’s name was?’", "그럼 알게 해 주마, 내 귀여운 것 — 내가 말해 줄 때 말이야! 내 증조부 이름이 뭐였는지 아니?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Steerforth.", "\"모릅니다.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘It was Walker, my sweet pet,’ replied Miss Mowcher, ‘and he came of a long line of Walkers, that I inherit all the Hookey estates from.’", "\"워커였단다, 내 사랑스러운 녀석.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다. \"워커 집안의 유서 깊은 혈통을 이으신 분이지, 그 덕에 내가 후키 집안의 영지를 죄다 물려받은 거고.\""]], [["I never beheld anything approaching to Miss Mowcher’s wink except Miss Mowcher’s self-possession.", "마우처 양의 눈짓에 견줄 만한 것은 마우처 양의 그 침착함 말고는 본 적이 없다."], ["She had a wonderful way too, when listening to what was said to her, or when waiting for an answer to what she had said herself, of pausing with her head cunningly on one side, and one eye turned up like a magpie’s.", "그녀에게는 또 한 가지 놀라운 버릇이 있었는데, 누가 하는 말을 들을 때나 자기가 한 말에 대한 대답을 기다릴 때면, 까치처럼 한쪽 눈을 위로 치뜨고 머리를 교묘하게 한쪽으로 기울인 채 멈추는 것이었다."], ["Altogether I was lost in amazement, and sat staring at her, quite oblivious, I am afraid, of the laws of politeness.", "아무튼 나는 어찌나 넋이 빠졌던지, 예의범절도 까맣게 잊은 채 그녀를 멀거니 바라보며 앉아 있었던 것 같다."]], [["She had by this time drawn the chair to her side, and was busily engaged in producing from the bag (plunging in her short arm to the shoulder, at every dive) a number of small bottles, sponges, combs, brushes, bits of flannel, little pairs of curling-irons, and other instruments, which she tumbled in a heap upon the chair.", "그러는 사이 그녀는 의자를 자기 곁으로 끌어다 놓고, 가방에서 (한 번 손을 넣을 때마다 짧은 팔을 어깨까지 푹 찔러 넣으며) 작은 병들, 해면, 빗, 솔, 플란넬 조각, 자그마한 고데기 따위 온갖 도구를 꺼내 의자 위에 수북이 쌓느라 분주했다."], ["From this employment she suddenly desisted, and said to Steerforth, much to my confusion:", "그러다 갑자기 그 일을 멈추더니, 나를 몹시 당황스럽게 하는 말을 스티어포스에게 던졌다."]], [["‘Who’s your friend?’", "\"네 친구는 누구냐?\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨예요.\""], ["Copperfield,’ said Steerforth; ‘he wants to know you.’", "스티어포스가 말했다. \"당신과 알고 지내고 싶어 하지요.\""]], [["‘Well, then, he shall!", "\"그럼, 그래야지!"], ["I thought he looked as if he did!’ returned Miss Mowcher, waddling up to me, bag in hand, and laughing on me as she came.", "내 보기에도 그런 것 같더라!\" 마우처 양이 가방을 든 채 뒤뚱뒤뚱 내게로 다가오며, 다가오는 내내 나를 향해 웃으며 받았다."], ["‘Face like a peach!’ standing on tiptoe to pinch my cheek as I sat. ‘Quite tempting!", "\"복숭아 같은 얼굴이네!\" 그녀가 발끝으로 서서, 앉아 있는 내 뺨을 꼬집으며 말했다."], ["I’m very fond of peaches.", "\"정말 군침 도는걸!"], ["Happy to make your acquaintance, Mr. Copperfield, I’m sure.’", "난 복숭아를 아주 좋아하거든. 만나서 정말 반가워요, 코퍼필드 씨.\""]], [["I said that I congratulated myself on having the honour to make hers, and that the happiness was mutual.", "나는 그녀와 알게 되는 영광을 누리게 되어 스스로 자랑스럽고, 그 기쁨은 서로의 것이라고 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, my goodness, how polite we are!’ exclaimed Miss Mowcher, making a preposterous attempt to cover her large face with her morsel of a hand.", "\"어머나 세상에, 우리 어쩜 이리 정중할까!\" 마우처 양이 그 손바닥만 한 손으로 자기 커다란 얼굴을 가리려고 터무니없는 시도를 하며 외쳤다."], ["‘What a world of gammon and spinnage it is, though, ain’t it!’", "\"그래도 세상이란 게 죄다 허울 좋은 헛소리투성이 아니겠어, 안 그래!\""]], [["This was addressed confidentially to both of us, as the morsel of a hand came away from the face, and buried itself, arm and all, in the bag again.", "이 말은 우리 둘 모두에게 은밀히 건네는 것이었는데, 그 손바닥만 한 손이 얼굴에서 떨어지더니 팔째로 다시 가방 속에 파묻혔다."]], [["‘What do you mean, Miss Mowcher?’ said Steerforth.", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 마우처 양?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Ha! ha! ha!", "\"하하하!"], ["What a refreshing set of humbugs we are, to be sure, ain’t we, my sweet child?’ replied that morsel of a woman, feeling in the bag with her head on one side and her eye in the air.", "우린 정말 산뜻하게 한심한 사기꾼들 아니겠니, 안 그래, 내 귀여운 녀석?\" 그 손바닥만 한 여인이 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이고 눈을 허공에 둔 채 가방 속을 더듬으며 대답했다. \"이것 좀 봐!\""], ["‘Look here!’ taking something out.", "그녀가 무언가를 꺼내며 말했다."], ["‘Scraps of the Russian Prince’s nails.", "\"러시아 공작의 손톱 부스러기야."], ["Prince Alphabet turned topsy-turvy, I call him, for his name’s got all the letters in it, higgledy-piggledy.’", "나는 그를 거꾸로 뒤집힌 알파벳 공작이라고 부르지, 이름 속에 글자가 죄다 뒤죽박죽 들어 있거든.\""]], [["‘The Russian Prince is a client of yours, is he?’ said Steerforth.", "\"러시아 공작이 당신 손님이라고요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘I believe you, my pet,’ replied Miss Mowcher.", "\"정말이라니까, 내 귀염둥이.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다."], ["‘I keep his nails in order for him.", "\"그 양반 손톱을 내가 단정하게 다듬어 주거든."], ["Twice a week!", "일주일에 두 번!"], ["Fingers and toes.’", "손가락이며 발가락이며.\""]], [["‘He pays well, I hope?’ said Steerforth.", "\"돈은 후하게 주겠지요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Pays, as he speaks, my dear child--through the nose,’ replied Miss Mowcher.", "\"말하는 거나, 돈 내는 거나, 내 귀여운 아기야 — 둘 다 콧소리로 한다니까.\""], ["‘None of your close shavers the Prince ain’t.", "마우처 양이 대답했다. \"그 공작은 결코 짜게 구는 사람이 아니야."], ["You’d say so, if you saw his moustachios. Red by nature, black by art.’", "그 콧수염을 보면 너도 그렇게 말할걸. 본디 빨갛던 걸 기술로 까맣게 만든 거지.\""]], [["‘By your art, of course,’ said Steerforth.", "\"물론 당신 기술로요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["Miss Mowcher winked assent.", "마우처 양은 그렇다고 눈을 찡긋했다."], ["‘Forced to send for me.", "\"날 부를 수밖에 없었지."], ["Couldn’t help it.", "어쩔 도리가 없었으니까."], ["The climate affected his dye; it did very well in Russia, but it was no go here.", "기후 탓에 염색이 영 안 받더라고. 러시아에선 멀쩡했는데, 여기선 통 안 먹히는 거야."], ["You never saw such a rusty Prince in all your born days as he was.", "그렇게 녹슨 공작은 평생 처음 봤다니까. 꼭 낡은 쇳덩이 같았어!\""], ["Like old iron!’ ‘Is that why you called him a humbug, just now?’ inquired Steerforth.", "\"그래서 방금 그를 사기꾼이라고 부른 건가요?\" 스티어포스가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, you’re a broth of a boy, ain’t you?’ returned Miss Mowcher, shaking her head violently.", "\"아이고, 넌 참 똑똑한 녀석이구나, 안 그래?\" 마우처 양이 머리를 사납게 흔들며 받았다."], ["‘I said, what a set of humbugs we were in general, and I showed you the scraps of the Prince’s nails to prove it.", "\"난 우리가 대체로 다 사기꾼 무리라고 말했고, 그걸 증명하려고 공작의 손톱 부스러기를 보여 준 거야."], ["The Prince’s nails do more for me in private families of the genteel sort, than all my talents put together.", "그 공작의 손톱이 점잖은 양반네 안방에서 내 온갖 재주를 다 합친 것보다 더 큰 노릇을 한다니까."], ["I always carry ‘em about.", "난 늘 그걸 가지고 다녀."], ["They’re the best introduction.", "그게 제일 좋은 소개장이거든."], ["If Miss Mowcher cuts the Prince’s nails, she must be all right.", "마우처 양이 공작의 손톱을 다듬는다면, 틀림없이 믿을 만한 사람이렷다, 이거지."], ["I give ‘em away to the young ladies.", "아가씨들한테 그걸 나눠 주거든."], ["They put ‘em in albums, I believe. Ha! ha! ha!", "그러면 앨범에 끼워 둔다나 봐. 하하하!"], ["Upon my life, “the whole social system” (as the men call it when they make speeches in Parliament) is a system of Prince’s nails!’ said this least of women, trying to fold her short arms, and nodding her large head.", "맹세코, '온 사회 제도'(의원 나리들이 의회에서 연설할 때 쓰는 그 말이지)란 게 죄다 공작의 손톱으로 굴러가는 제도라니까!\" 이 세상에서 가장 작은 여인이 짤막한 두 팔을 끼려고 애쓰며 큰 머리를 끄덕여 보이며 말했다."]], [["Steerforth laughed heartily, and I laughed too.", "스티어포스가 진심으로 웃었고, 나도 따라 웃었다."], ["Miss Mowcher continuing all the time to shake her head (which was very much on one side), and to look into the air with one eye, and to wink with the other.", "마우처 양은 그러는 내내 (몹시 한쪽으로 기울어진) 머리를 계속 흔들면서, 한쪽 눈으로는 허공을 바라보고 다른 한쪽 눈으로는 찡긋거렸다."]], [["‘Well, well!’ she said, smiting her small knees, and rising, ‘this is not business.", "\"자, 자!\" 그녀가 작은 무릎을 탁 치고 일어서며 말했다. \"이러고 있을 게 아니지."], ["Come, Steerforth, let’s explore the polar regions, and have it over.’", "자 스티어포스, 극지방 탐험에 나서서 끝장을 보자고.\""]], [["She then selected two or three of the little instruments, and a little bottle, and asked (to my surprise) if the table would bear.", "그러더니 그녀는 작은 도구 두세 개와 작은 병 하나를 골라 들고는, (놀랍게도) 식탁이 견딜 만하겠느냐고 물었다."], ["On Steerforth’s replying in the affirmative, she pushed a chair against it, and begging the assistance of my hand, mounted up, pretty nimbly, to the top, as if it were a stage.", "스티어포스가 그렇다고 대답하자, 그녀는 식탁에 의자 하나를 밀어 붙이고 내 손을 빌려 달라고 청하더니, 꽤나 잽싸게 식탁 꼭대기로 기어 올라갔는데, 마치 무대에라도 오르는 듯했다."]], [["‘If either of you saw my ankles,’ she said, when she was safely elevated, ‘say so, and I’ll go home and destroy myself!’", "\"둘 중 누구라도 내 발목이 보이거든,\" 그녀가 무사히 높이 올라서고 나서 말했다. \"그렇다고 말해, 그럼 집에 가서 자결해 버릴 테니!\""]], [["‘I did not,’ said Steerforth.", "\"안 보였습니다.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘I did not,’ said I.", "\"안 보였습니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well then,’ cried Miss Mowcher, ‘I’ll consent to live.", "\"그럼 좋아.\" 마우처 양이 외쳤다. \"계속 살기로 하지."], ["Now, ducky, ducky, ducky, come to Mrs. Bond and be killed.’", "자, 아가야, 아가야, 아가야, 본드 부인께 와서 죽임을 당하시지.\""]], [["This was an invitation to Steerforth to place himself under her hands; who, accordingly, sat himself down, with his back to the table, and his laughing face towards me, and submitted his head to her inspection, evidently for no other purpose than our entertainment.", "이것은 스티어포스에게 자기 손에 몸을 맡기라는 권유였다. 그러자 그는 식탁에 등을 돌리고 웃는 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 향한 채 자리에 앉아, 그녀의 검사에 머리를 내맡겼는데, 누가 봐도 그건 그저 우리를 즐겁게 해 주려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없는 짓이었다."], ["To see Miss Mowcher standing over him, looking at his rich profusion of brown hair through a large round magnifying glass, which she took out of her pocket, was a most amazing spectacle.", "마우처 양이 그의 위에 올라서서, 주머니에서 꺼낸 커다랗고 둥근 돋보기로 그의 풍성한 갈색 머리카락을 들여다보는 광경은 더없이 놀라운 구경거리였다."]], [["‘You’re a pretty fellow!’ said Miss Mowcher, after a brief inspection.", "\"넌 참 잘생긴 녀석이야!\" 마우처 양이 잠깐 살펴보고 나서 말했다."], ["‘You’d be as bald as a friar on the top of your head in twelve months, but for me.", "\"내가 없었으면 일 년 안에 정수리가 수도사처럼 훌렁 벗어졌을걸."], ["Just half a minute, my young friend, and we’ll give you a polishing that shall keep your curls on for the next ten years!’", "반 분만 가만있어, 내 어린 친구, 그럼 앞으로 십 년은 그 곱슬머리가 붙어 있게 윤기를 좀 내 주마!\""]], [["With this, she tilted some of the contents of the little bottle on to one of the little bits of flannel, and, again imparting some of the virtues of that preparation to one of the little brushes, began rubbing and scraping away with both on the crown of Steerforth’s head in the busiest manner I ever witnessed, talking all the time.", "이렇게 말하며 그녀는 그 작은 병 속 내용물을 작은 플란넬 조각 하나에 약간 따라 붓고, 또 그 약물의 효험을 작은 솔 하나에 좀 묻혀서, 내가 본 중에 가장 분주한 손놀림으로 두 가지를 다 써 가며 스티어포스의 정수리를 문지르고 긁어 대기 시작했는데, 그러는 내내 쉴 새 없이 떠들었다."]], [["‘There’s Charley Pyegrave, the duke’s son,’ she said.", "\"공작 아드님인 찰리 파이그레이브란 사람이 있거든.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘You know Charley?’ peeping round into his face.", "\"찰리 알지?\" 그러며 그의 얼굴을 빼꼼히 들여다보았다."]], [["‘A little,’ said Steerforth.", "\"조금요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘What a man HE is! THERE’S a whisker!", "\"그 사람이야말로 대단한 남자지! 그 구레나룻이라니!"], ["As to Charley’s legs, if they were only a pair (which they ain’t), they’d defy competition.", "찰리 다리로 말하자면, 그게 짝이 맞기만 하다면 (안 맞지만 말이야) 따라올 자가 없을 거야."], ["Would you believe he tried to do without me--in the Life-Guards, too?’", "그 사람이 나 없이 해 보려고 했다면 믿겠니 — 그것도 근위 기병대에 있으면서?\""]], [["‘Mad!’ said Steerforth.", "\"미쳤군요!\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘It looks like it.", "\"그래 보이지."], ["However, mad or sane, he tried,’ returned Miss Mowcher.", "하지만 미쳤든 멀쩡하든, 해 보긴 했어.\""], ["‘What does he do, but, lo and behold you, he goes into a perfumer’s shop, and wants to buy a bottle of the Madagascar Liquid.’", "마우처 양이 받았다. \"그 사람이 어쨌느냐면, 글쎄, 향수 가게에 들어가서, 마다가스카르 물약을 한 병 사겠다는 거야.\""]], [["‘Charley does?’ said Steerforth.", "\"찰리가요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Charley does.", "\"찰리가."], ["But they haven’t got any of the Madagascar Liquid.’", "그런데 마다가스카르 물약 같은 건 없거든.\""]], [["‘What is it?", "\"그게 뭐죠?"], ["Something to drink?’ asked Steerforth.", "마시는 거예요?\" 스티어포스가 물었다."]], [["‘To drink?’ returned Miss Mowcher, stopping to slap his cheek.", "\"마시는 거?\" 마우처 양이 손을 멈추고 그의 뺨을 찰싹 때리며 받았다."], ["‘To doctor his own moustachios with, you know.", "\"제 콧수염을 손질하는 거 말이야, 알겠니."], ["There was a woman in the shop--elderly female--quite a Griffin--who had never even heard of it by name.", "가게에 여자가 하나 있었는데 — 나이 지긋한 여편네였지 — 영락없는 그리핀이었어 — 그게 무슨 이름인지조차 들어 본 적이 없는 거야."], ["“Begging pardon, sir,”", "'죄송합니다만, 손님,'"], ["said the Griffin to Charley, “it’s not--not--not ROUGE, is it?” “Rouge,”", "하고 그 그리핀이 찰리한테 그러더래, '그게 — 그게 — 그게 연지는 아니지요?'"], ["said Charley to the Griffin. “What the unmentionable to ears polite, do you think I want with rouge?”", "'연지라니,' 찰리가 그 그리핀한테 그랬다지, '점잖은 귀에 입에 담기도 뭣한, 내가 연지를 어디다 쓴다는 거요?'"], ["“No offence, sir,” said the Griffin; “we have it asked for by so many names, I thought it might be.”", "'기분 상하셨다면 죄송합니다, 손님,' 하고 그리핀이 그랬대, '하도 여러 이름으로 찾는 분들이 많아서, 그런가 했지요.'"], ["Now that, my child,’ continued Miss Mowcher, rubbing all the time as busily as ever, ‘is another instance of the refreshing humbug I was speaking of.", "자, 이게 말이지, 내 아가,\" 마우처 양이 여전히 부지런히 문질러 대며 말을 이었다. \"내가 아까 말한 그 산뜻한 사기질의 또 다른 본보기란다."], ["I do something in that way myself--perhaps a good deal--perhaps a little--sharp’s the word, my dear boy--never mind!’", "나도 그쪽으로 좀 하는 게 있거든 — 어쩌면 꽤 많이 — 어쩌면 조금 — 입조심이 상책이야, 내 귀여운 녀석 — 신경 쓰지 마!\""]], [["‘In what way do you mean?", "\"무슨 식으로 말입니까?"], ["In the rouge way?’ said Steerforth.", "연지 식으로요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Put this and that together, my tender pupil,’ returned the wary Mowcher, touching her nose, ‘work it by the rule of Secrets in all trades, and the product will give you the desired result.", "\"이거하고 저거를 한데 맞춰 봐, 내 살가운 제자야.\" 신중한 마우처 양이 자기 코를 만지며 받았다. \"어떤 장사에나 비밀이 있는 법, 그 법칙대로 풀어 보면, 그 답이 네가 바라던 결과를 알려 줄 거야."], ["I say I do a little in that way myself.", "나도 그쪽으로 좀 한다 그랬지."], ["One Dowager, SHE calls it lip-salve.", "어떤 노부인은 그걸 입술 연고라고 부르고."], ["Another, SHE calls it gloves.", "또 어떤 부인은 장갑이라 부르고."], ["Another, SHE calls it tucker-edging.", "또 어떤 부인은 깃 장식이라 부르고."], ["Another, SHE calls it a fan.", "또 어떤 부인은 부채라 부르고."], ["I call it whatever THEY call it.", "나도 그 부인들이 부르는 대로 따라 부르지."], ["I supply it for ‘em, but we keep up the trick so, to one another, and make believe with such a face, that they’d as soon think of laying it on, before a whole drawing-room, as before me.", "그걸 갖다 드리는 건 난데, 우린 서로에게 그렇게 시치미를 떼고, 어찌나 능청스러운 얼굴로 모르는 척하는지, 그 부인들은 객실 사람들 앞에서 그걸 바르느니 차라리 내 앞에서 바르는 게 낫겠다 싶을 정도라니까."], ["And when I wait upon ‘em, they’ll say to me sometimes--WITH IT ON--thick, and no mistake--“How am I looking, Mowcher?", "그러고는 내가 시중을 들 때면, 가끔 나한테 그러는 거야 — 그걸 떡칠해 놓고도, 떡칠해 놓은 게 빤한데 — '내가 어때 보여, 마우처?"], ["Am I pale?” Ha! ha! ha! ha!", "창백해 보이나?' 하하하하!"], ["Isn’t THAT refreshing, my young friend!’", "그거야말로 산뜻하지 않니, 내 어린 친구!\""]], [["I never did in my days behold anything like Mowcher as she stood upon the dining table, intensely enjoying this refreshment, rubbing busily at Steerforth’s head, and winking at me over it.", "식탁 위에 올라서서, 이 산뜻한 즐거움을 한껏 만끽하며, 스티어포스의 머리를 부지런히 문질러 대고, 그 너머로 내게 눈을 찡긋거리던 마우처 양 같은 사람은 내 평생 본 적이 없었다."]], [["‘Ah!’ she said.", "\"아!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Such things are not much in demand hereabouts.", "\"이런 건 이 근방에선 별로 수요가 없어."], ["That sets me off again!", "그러니 또 김이 새지!"], ["I haven’t seen a pretty woman since I’ve been here, jemmy.’", "여기 온 뒤로 예쁜 여자라곤 하나도 못 봤다니까, 얘야.\""]], [["‘No?’ said Steerforth.", "\"그래요?\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Not the ghost of one,’ replied Miss Mowcher.", "\"그림자조차 못 봤어.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다."]], [["‘We could show her the substance of one, I think?’ said Steerforth, addressing his eyes to mine.", "\"우리가 예쁜 여자의 실물을 하나 보여 드릴 수 있을 것 같은데요?\" 스티어포스가 내게 눈길을 던지며 말했다."], ["‘Eh, Daisy?’", "\"그렇지, 데이지?\""]], [["‘Yes, indeed,’ said I.", "\"그럼요, 정말이지.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Aha?’ cried the little creature, glancing sharply at my face, and then peeping round at Steerforth’s. ‘Umph?’", "\"아하?\" 그 작은 사람이 내 얼굴을 날카롭게 흘끔 보고는 스티어포스의 얼굴을 빼꼼히 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"음?\""]], [["The first exclamation sounded like a question put to both of us, and the second like a question put to Steerforth only.", "첫 번째 감탄사는 우리 둘 모두에게 던진 물음 같았고, 두 번째 것은 스티어포스에게만 던진 물음 같았다."], ["She seemed to have found no answer to either, but continued to rub, with her head on one side and her eye turned up, as if she were looking for an answer in the air and were confident of its appearing presently.", "그녀는 어느 쪽에도 답을 못 찾은 듯했으나, 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이고 눈을 위로 치뜬 채, 마치 허공에서 답을 찾기라도 하듯, 그리고 그 답이 곧 나타나리라 자신하기라도 하듯 계속 문질러 댔다."]], [["‘A sister of yours, Mr. Copperfield?’ she cried, after a pause, and still keeping the same look-out.", "\"코퍼필드 씨 누이동생인가요?\" 그녀가 잠시 뒤에, 여전히 똑같은 자세로 살피며 외쳤다."], ["‘Aye, aye?’", "\"응, 응?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Steerforth, before I could reply.", "\"아니요.\" 내가 미처 대답하기도 전에 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Nothing of the sort.", "\"그런 건 전혀 아닙니다."], ["On the contrary, Mr. Copperfield used--or I am much mistaken--to have a great admiration for her.’", "오히려, 코퍼필드 씨가 한때는 — 내가 한참 잘못 안 게 아니라면 — 그 아가씨를 무척이나 흠모했지요.\""]], [["‘Why, hasn’t he now?’ returned Miss Mowcher.", "\"아니, 지금은 안 그래요?\""], ["‘Is he fickle?", "마우처 양이 받았다."], ["Oh, for shame!", "\"변덕쟁이인가요?"], ["Did he sip every flower, and change every hour, until Polly his passion requited?--Is her name Polly?’", "어머, 부끄러운 줄 알아야지! 꽃마다 꿀을 빨고 때마다 마음을 바꾸다가, 폴리가 마침내 그 마음을 받아 준 건가? — 그 아가씨 이름이 폴리예요?\""]], [["The Elfin suddenness with which she pounced upon me with this question, and a searching look, quite disconcerted me for a moment.", "요정 같은 갑작스러움으로 이 질문과 함께 캐묻는 눈길을 던지며 내게 덤벼드는 통에, 나는 한순간 적잖이 당황했다."]], [["‘No, Miss Mowcher,’ I replied.", "\"아니요, 마우처 양.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘Her name is Emily.’", "\"이름은 에밀리입니다.\""]], [["‘Aha?’ she cried exactly as before. ‘Umph?", "\"아하?\" 그녀가 아까와 똑같이 외쳤다. \"음?"], ["What a rattle I am! Mr.", "내가 어찌 이리 수다스러울까!"], ["Copperfield, ain’t I volatile?’", "코퍼필드 씨, 나 참 들떠 있죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["Her tone and look implied something that was not agreeable to me in connexion with the subject.", "그녀의 말투와 눈빛에는 이 화제와 관련해 내게 그리 달갑지 않은 무언가가 담겨 있었다."], ["So I said, in a graver manner than any of us had yet assumed: ‘She is as virtuous as she is pretty.", "그래서 나는, 우리 중 누구도 아직 취한 적 없는 진지한 태도로 말했다. \"그 아가씨는 예쁜 만큼 정숙합니다."], ["She is engaged to be married to a most worthy and deserving man in her own station of life.", "자기 신분에 어울리는, 더없이 훌륭하고 떳떳한 사람과 약혼한 사이고요."], ["I esteem her for her good sense, as much as I admire her for her good looks.’", "나는 그 아가씨의 외모를 흠모하는 만큼이나 그 분별 있음을 존경합니다.\""]], [["‘Well said!’ cried Steerforth.", "\"맞는 말이야!\" 스티어포스가 외쳤다."], ["‘Hear, hear, hear!", "\"옳소, 옳소, 옳소!"], ["Now I’ll quench the curiosity of this little Fatima, my dear Daisy, by leaving her nothing to guess at.", "자, 이 작은 파티마의 호기심을 잠재워 주겠어, 내 사랑하는 데이지, 더는 짐작할 거리를 남기지 않게 말이야."], ["She is at present apprenticed, Miss Mowcher, or articled, or whatever it may be, to Omer and Joram, Haberdashers, Milliners, and so forth, in this town.", "마우처 양, 그 아가씨는 지금 이 마을의 잡화상이자 모자 가게인 오머와 조럼 상회에 견습생으로, 아니 계약 직공으로, 아무튼 뭐라 부르든 그렇게 매여 있습니다."], ["Do you observe?", "알겠습니까?"], ["Omer and Joram.", "오머와 조럼."], ["The promise of which my friend has spoken, is made and entered into with her cousin; Christian name, Ham; surname, Peggotty; occupation, boat-builder; also of this town.", "내 친구가 말한 그 약혼은, 그 아가씨의 사촌과 맺어진 사이입니다. 세례명은 햄, 성은 패거티, 직업은 배 목수, 역시 이 마을 사람이지요."], ["She lives with a relative; Christian name, unknown; surname, Peggotty; occupation, seafaring; also of this town.", "그 아가씨는 한 친척과 함께 사는데, 세례명은 모르고, 성은 패거티, 직업은 뱃사람, 역시 이 마을 사람입니다."], ["She is the prettiest and most engaging little fairy in the world. I admire her--as my friend does--exceedingly.", "세상에서 가장 예쁘고 가장 사랑스러운 작은 요정이지요. 나는 그 아가씨를 — 내 친구가 그러하듯 — 더없이 흠모합니다."], ["If it were not that I might appear to disparage her Intended, which I know my friend would not like, I would add, that to me she seems to be throwing herself away; that I am sure she might do better; and that I swear she was born to be a lady.’", "그 약혼자를 깎아내리는 것처럼 비칠까 봐 — 내 친구가 그걸 싫어할 걸 아니까 — 안 그러는 거지, 그게 아니라면 이렇게 덧붙이고 싶군요. 내 보기엔 그 아가씨가 제 인생을 너무 헐값에 던져 버리는 것 같다고, 분명 더 나은 사람을 만날 수 있을 거라고, 그리고 맹세코 그 아가씨는 귀부인이 되려고 태어난 사람이라고요.\""]], [["Miss Mowcher listened to these words, which were very slowly and distinctly spoken, with her head on one side, and her eye in the air as if she were still looking for that answer.", "마우처 양은 아주 느리고 또렷하게 이어진 이 말을, 머리를 한쪽으로 기울이고 눈을 허공에 둔 채, 여전히 그 답을 찾고 있기라도 하듯 귀 기울여 들었다."], ["When he ceased she became brisk again in an instant, and rattled away with surprising volubility.", "그가 말을 멈추자 그녀는 순식간에 다시 활기를 되찾아, 놀라운 입심으로 떠들어 댔다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["And that’s all about it, is it?’ she exclaimed, trimming his whiskers with a little restless pair of scissors, that went glancing round his head in all directions.", "그래서, 그게 다인가요, 응?\" 작고 쉴 새 없는 가위 한 자루를 그의 머리 둘레로 사방팔방 번뜩이며 구레나룻을 다듬으면서 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["‘Very well: very well!", "\"아주 좋아요, 아주 좋아!"], ["Quite a long story.", "정말 긴 이야기네."], ["Ought to end “and they lived happy ever afterwards”; oughtn’t it? Ah!", "'그리하여 그들은 오래오래 행복하게 살았답니다'로 끝나야 마땅한데, 안 그래요? 아!"], ["What’s that game at forfeits?", "벌금놀이 그게 뭐더라?"], ["I love my love with an E, because she’s enticing; I hate her with an E, because she’s engaged.", "나는 내 사랑을 ㅇ으로 사랑해, 사랑스러우니까. 나는 그녀를 ㅇ으로 미워해, 약혼했으니까."], ["I took her to the sign of the exquisite, and treated her with an elopement, her name’s Emily, and she lives in the east? Ha! ha! ha! Mr. Copperfield, ain’t I volatile?’", "나는 그녀를 '예외'의 간판으로 데려가, '야반도주'로 대접했네, 그녀의 이름은 에밀리, 그녀는 동쪽에 산다네? 하하하! 코퍼필드 씨, 나 참 들떠 있죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["Merely looking at me with extravagant slyness, and not waiting for any reply, she continued, without drawing breath:", "그녀는 그저 짓궂기 그지없는 표정으로 나를 바라볼 뿐, 어떤 대답도 기다리지 않고, 숨도 안 돌리고 말을 이었다."]], [["‘There!", "\"자!"], ["If ever any scapegrace was trimmed and touched up to perfection, you are, Steerforth.", "이 세상 어떤 망나니가 흠잡을 데 없이 다듬어지고 손질됐다면, 그게 바로 너야, 스티어포스."], ["If I understand any noddle in the world, I understand yours.", "내가 이 세상 어떤 골통이든 알아본다면, 네 골통은 훤히 안다고."], ["Do you hear me when I tell you that, my darling?", "내가 그렇게 말할 때 듣고 있니, 내 귀염둥이?"], ["I understand yours,’ peeping down into his face.", "네 골통은 내가 다 안다니까.\" 그러며 그의 얼굴을 빼꼼히 내려다보았다."], ["‘Now you may mizzle, jemmy (as we say at Court), and if Mr.", "\"자 이제 넌 슬슬 꺼져도 좋아, 얘야 (궁정에서 쓰는 말로 하자면 말이야)."], ["Copperfield will take the chair I’ll operate on him.’", "그리고 코퍼필드 씨가 이 의자에 앉으면 내가 손봐 드리지.\""]], [["‘What do you say, Daisy?’ inquired Steerforth, laughing, and resigning his seat.", "\"어쩌겠나, 데이지?\" 스티어포스가 웃으며 제 자리를 내주며 물었다."], ["‘Will you be improved?’", "\"좀 더 나아져 보겠나?\""]], [["‘Thank you, Miss Mowcher, not this evening.’", "\"고맙지만, 마우처 양, 오늘 저녁은 됐습니다.\""]], [["‘Don’t say no,’ returned the little woman, looking at me with the aspect of a connoisseur; ‘a little bit more eyebrow?’", "\"싫다고 하지 말아요.\" 그 작은 여인이 감정사 같은 눈초리로 나를 보며 받았다. \"눈썹만 조금 더 손볼까?\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ I returned, ‘some other time.’", "\"고맙지만,\" 내가 받았다. \"다음에 하지요.\""]], [["‘Have it carried half a quarter of an inch towards the temple,’ said Miss Mowcher.", "\"관자놀이 쪽으로 한 사 분의 일 인치만 끌어 줄게요.\" 마우처 양이 말했다."], ["‘We can do it in a fortnight.’", "\"이 주면 돼요.\""]], [["‘No, I thank you.", "\"아니요, 괜찮습니다."], ["Not at present.’", "지금은 안 하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Go in for a tip,’ she urged. ‘No?", "\"끄트머리만이라도 한번 해 봐요.\" 그녀가 졸랐다."], ["Let’s get the scaffolding up, then, for a pair of whiskers. Come!’", "\"안 돼요? 그럼 구레나룻 한 쌍을 위해 발판부터 세워 볼까요. 자!\""]], [["I could not help blushing as I declined, for I felt we were on my weak point, now. But Miss Mowcher, finding that I was not at present disposed for any decoration within the range of her art, and that I was, for the time being, proof against the blandishments of the small bottle which she held up before one eye to enforce her persuasions, said we would make a beginning on an early day, and requested the aid of my hand to descend from her elevated station.", "거절하면서 나는 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었는데, 우리가 이제 내 약점을 건드리고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 하지만 마우처 양은, 내가 지금으로서는 자기 기술이 미치는 범위 안의 어떤 치장도 받을 마음이 없으며, 또 당장은 그녀가 설득에 힘을 싣느라 한쪽 눈앞에 치켜든 그 작은 병의 꾐에도 끄떡없다는 것을 알아채고는, 가까운 날에 한번 시작해 보자고 말하고는, 그 높은 자리에서 내려오게 손을 좀 빌려 달라고 청했다."], ["Thus assisted, she skipped down with much agility, and began to tie her double chin into her bonnet.", "그렇게 도움을 받아 그녀는 무척 날렵하게 뛰어내리더니, 이중 턱을 보닛 속에 끈으로 동여매기 시작했다."]], [["‘The fee,’ said Steerforth, ‘is--’", "\"보수는,\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"그러니까—\""]], [["‘Five bob,’ replied Miss Mowcher, ‘and dirt cheap, my chicken.", "\"오 실링.\" 마우처 양이 대답했다. \"그야말로 거저나 다름없지, 내 병아리야."], ["Ain’t I volatile, Mr. Copperfield?’", "나 참 들떠 있죠, 코퍼필드 씨?\""]], [["I replied politely: ‘Not at all.’ But I thought she was rather so, when she tossed up his two half-crowns like a goblin pieman, caught them, dropped them in her pocket, and gave it a loud slap.", "나는 공손히 대답했다. \"전혀요.\" 하지만 그녀가 두 닢의 반 크라운 동전을 도깨비 파이 장수처럼 휙 던져 올렸다가 받아서 주머니에 떨어뜨리고 그 주머니를 탁 소리 나게 두드리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그녀가 꽤나 들떠 있구나 싶었다."]], [["‘That’s the Till!’ observed Miss Mowcher, standing at the chair again, and replacing in the bag a miscellaneous collection of little objects she had emptied out of it.", "\"이게 금고지!\" 마우처 양이 다시 의자 곁에 서서, 가방에서 쏟아 냈던 자질구레한 잡동사니를 도로 챙겨 넣으며 말했다. \"내 짐 다 챙겼나?"], ["‘Have I got all my traps? It seems so.", "그런 것 같네. '누군가와 짝지어 주려고'"], ["It won’t do to be like long Ned Beadwood, when they took him to church “to marry him to somebody”, as he says, and left the bride behind. Ha! ha! ha! A wicked rascal, Ned, but droll!", "멀대 같은 네드 비드우드를 교회로 데려갔다가, 본인 말마따나 신부는 두고 와 버렸다던, 그 짝이 나면 안 되니까. 하하하! 못된 망나니지, 네드는, 그래도 우습다니까!"], ["Now, I know I’m going to break your hearts, but I am forced to leave you.", "자, 이제 너희 가슴을 찢어 놓을 줄 알지만, 어쩔 수 없이 떠나야겠다."], ["You must call up all your fortitude, and try to bear it.", "너희도 있는 용기를 다 끌어모아서 견뎌 보렴."], ["Good-bye, Mr. Copperfield!", "잘 있어요, 코퍼필드 씨!"], ["Take care of yourself, jockey of Norfolk!", "몸조심해요, 노퍽의 멋쟁이 기수님!"], ["How I have been rattling on!", "내가 어찌 이리 떠들어 댔을까!"], ["It’s all the fault of you two wretches.", "다 너희 두 망나니 탓이야. 용서해 주마!"], ["I forgive you!", "'밥 스워!'"], ["“Bob swore!”--as the Englishman said for “Good night”, when he first learnt French, and thought it so like English.", "— 어떤 영국 사람이 프랑스어를 처음 배우고는 그게 영어랑 너무 비슷하다고 '굿 나이트' 대신 그렇게 말했다지."], ["“Bob swore,” my ducks!’", "'밥 스워,' 내 귀염둥이들!\""]], [["With the bag slung over her arm, and rattling as she waddled away, she waddled to the door, where she stopped to inquire if she should leave us a lock of her hair.", "가방을 팔에 둘러메고, 뒤뚱뒤뚱 걸어가는 동안 가방을 달그락거리며, 그녀는 문가로 뒤뚱뒤뚱 걸어가더니, 거기서 멈춰 우리에게 자기 머리카락 한 타래를 남기고 갈까 물었다."], ["‘Ain’t I volatile?’ she added, as a commentary on this offer, and, with her finger on her nose, departed.", "\"나 참 들떠 있죠?\" 그녀가 이 제안에 대한 해설이라도 되는 양 덧붙이고는, 코에 손가락을 댄 채 떠나갔다."]], [["Steerforth laughed to that degree, that it was impossible for me to help laughing too; though I am not sure I should have done so, but for this inducement.", "스티어포스가 어찌나 웃어 대던지 나도 따라 웃지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 그렇게 부추기지 않았더라면 내가 웃었을지는 잘 모르겠지만 말이다."], ["When we had had our laugh quite out, which was after some time, he told me that Miss Mowcher had quite an extensive connexion, and made herself useful to a variety of people in a variety of ways.", "우리가 한참 만에 실컷 웃고 나자, 그는 마우처 양이 발이 꽤나 넓어서 갖가지 사람들에게 갖가지 방식으로 쓸모를 보인다고 말해 주었다."], ["Some people trifled with her as a mere oddity, he said; but she was as shrewdly and sharply observant as anyone he knew, and as long-headed as she was short-armed.", "어떤 사람들은 그녀를 그저 별난 구경거리로 여기며 가지고 논다고 했지만, 그녀는 그가 아는 누구 못지않게 빈틈없고 날카롭게 관찰하며, 팔이 짧은 만큼이나 머리가 길다고 했다."], ["He told me that what she had said of being here, and there, and everywhere, was true enough; for she made little darts into the provinces, and seemed to pick up customers everywhere, and to know everybody.", "그녀가 여기에도 있고 저기에도 있고 안 가는 데가 없다고 한 말은 충분히 사실이라고도 했다. 그녀는 지방으로 잽싸게 드나들면서, 어디서나 손님을 끌어모으고 누구든 다 아는 듯하다는 것이었다."], ["I asked him what her disposition was: whether it was at all mischievous, and if her sympathies were generally on the right side of things: but, not succeeding in attracting his attention to these questions after two or three attempts, I forbore or forgot to repeat them.", "나는 그녀의 됨됨이가 어떤지 물었다. 조금이라도 심술궂은 데가 있는지, 그리고 그 동정심이 대체로 옳은 쪽을 향하는지 말이다. 하지만 두세 번 시도해 보아도 그 질문으로 그의 주의를 끄는 데 성공하지 못하자, 나는 다시 묻기를 그만두었거나 잊어버렸다."], ["He told me instead, with much rapidity, a good deal about her skill, and her profits; and about her being a scientific cupper, if I should ever have occasion for her service in that capacity.", "그는 그 대신, 무척 빠른 말투로, 그녀의 솜씨와 벌이에 관한 이야기를, 그리고 혹시라도 내가 부항을 떠야 할 일이 생기면 그녀가 솜씨 좋은 부항사라는 이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았다."]], [["She was the principal theme of our conversation during the evening: and when we parted for the night Steerforth called after me over the banisters, ‘Bob swore!’ as I went downstairs.", "그날 저녁 우리 대화의 주된 화제는 그녀였다. 그리고 밤이 되어 헤어질 때, 내가 아래층으로 내려가는데 스티어포스가 난간 너머로 나를 향해 외쳤다. \"밥 스워!\""]], [["I was surprised, when I came to Mr. Barkis’s house, to find Ham walking up and down in front of it, and still more surprised to learn from him that little Em’ly was inside.", "바키스 씨 집에 이르렀을 때, 나는 햄이 집 앞을 서성이고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐고, 어린 에밀리가 안에 있다는 말을 그에게서 듣고는 더욱 놀랐다."], ["I naturally inquired why he was not there too, instead of pacing the streets by himself?", "나는 당연히, 왜 그도 안에 있지 않고 혼자 길거리를 서성이고 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Why, you see, Mas’r Davy,’ he rejoined, in a hesitating manner, ‘Em’ly, she’s talking to some ‘un in here.’", "\"그게 말이죠, 데이비 도련님,\" 그가 머뭇거리며 받았다. \"에밀리가, 안에서 누구하고 이야기를 나누고 있어서요.\""]], [["‘I should have thought,’ said I, smiling, ‘that that was a reason for your being in here too, Ham.’", "\"내 생각엔 말이야,\" 내가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그거야말로 자네도 안에 들어가 있을 까닭이 될 텐데, 햄.\""]], [["‘Well, Mas’r Davy, in a general way, so ‘t would be,’ he returned; ‘but look’ee here, Mas’r Davy,’ lowering his voice, and speaking very gravely.", "\"뭐, 데이비 도련님, 보통이라면야 그렇겠지요.\" 그가 받았다. \"그런데 이것 좀 보세요, 데이비 도련님,\""], ["‘It’s a young woman, sir--a young woman, that Em’ly knowed once, and doen’t ought to know no more.’", "그가 목소리를 낮추고 아주 무겁게 말했다. \"젊은 여자거든요, 도련님 — 한때 에밀리가 알던 젊은 여자인데, 이젠 더 알고 지내선 안 될 사람이에요.\""]], [["When I heard these words, a light began to fall upon the figure I had seen following them, some hours ago.", "그 말을 들었을 때, 몇 시간 전에 그들을 뒤따라가던 그 모습 위로 한 줄기 빛이 비추기 시작했다."]], [["‘It’s a poor wurem, Mas’r Davy,’ said Ham, ‘as is trod under foot by all the town.", "\"가엾은 여자예요, 데이비 도련님.\" 햄이 말했다. \"온 마을 사람들한테 짓밟히고 사는 여자죠."], ["Up street and down street.", "윗길이든 아랫길이든 어디서나요."], ["The mowld o’ the churchyard don’t hold any that the folk shrink away from, more.’", "교회 묘지의 흙도 그 여자만큼 사람들이 피하는 이는 품지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["‘Did I see her tonight, Ham, on the sand, after we met you?’", "\"오늘 밤 내가 그 여자를 봤나, 햄, 우리가 자넬 만난 뒤에 모래밭에서?\""]], [["‘Keeping us in sight?’ said Ham.", "\"우릴 계속 지켜보던 여자요?\" 햄이 말했다."], ["‘It’s like you did, Mas’r Davy.", "\"아마 보셨을 거예요, 데이비 도련님."], ["Not that I know’d then, she was theer, sir, but along of her creeping soon arterwards under Em’ly’s little winder, when she see the light come, and whispering “Em’ly, Em’ly, for Christ’s sake, have a woman’s heart towards me.", "그땐 저도 그 여자가 거기 있는 줄 몰랐지만요, 도련님. 그런데 곧 뒤이어 그 여자가 에밀리네 작은 창문 아래로 살금살금 기어 와서, 불빛이 비치는 걸 보고는 속삭이더라고요. '에밀리, 에밀리, 제발 부탁이야, 같은 여자로서 내게 마음을 좀 가져 줘."], ["I was once like you!”", "나도 한때는 너 같았어!'"], ["Those was solemn words, Mas’r Davy, fur to hear!’", "듣기에 참 가슴 저민 말이었어요, 데이비 도련님!\""]], [["‘They were indeed, Ham.", "\"정말 그랬겠군, 햄."], ["What did Em’ly do?’ ‘Says Em’ly, “Martha, is it you?", "그래서 에밀리는 어쨌나?\" \"에밀리가 그러더라고요. '마사, 너니?"], ["Oh, Martha, can it be you?”--for they had sat at work together, many a day, at Mr. Omer’s.’", "아, 마사, 정말 너야?' — 둘이 오머 씨 가게에서 여러 날 함께 일했던 사이거든요.\""]], [["‘I recollect her now!’ cried I, recalling one of the two girls I had seen when I first went there.", "\"이제 그 여자가 기억나!\" 내가 외쳤다. 처음 그곳에 갔을 때 보았던 두 처녀 가운데 하나를 떠올리면서."], ["‘I recollect her quite well!’", "\"아주 또렷이 기억나는걸!\""]], [["‘Martha Endell,’ said Ham.", "\"마사 엔델이에요.\""], ["‘Two or three year older than Em’ly, but was at the school with her.’", "햄이 말했다. \"에밀리보다 두세 살 위지만, 같은 학교에 다녔지요.\""]], [["‘I never heard her name,’ said I.", "\"그 여자 이름은 못 들었었네.\""], ["‘I didn’t mean to interrupt you.’", "내가 말했다. \"말을 끊을 생각은 아니었어.\""]], [["‘For the matter o’ that, Mas’r Davy,’ replied Ham, ‘all’s told a’most in them words, “Em’ly, Em’ly, for Christ’s sake, have a woman’s heart towards me.", "\"그건 그렇고요, 데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 받았다. \"그 말 속에 거의 다 들어 있어요."], ["I was once like you!”", "'에밀리, 에밀리, 제발 부탁이야, 같은 여자로서 내게 마음을 좀 가져 줘. 나도 한때는 너 같았어!'"], ["She wanted to speak to Em’ly.", "그 여자가 에밀리하고 이야기를 하고 싶어 했어요. 에밀리는 거기서 이야기를 나눌 수가 없었지요. 그 다정한 외삼촌이 마침 집에 와 있었으니까요."], ["Em’ly couldn’t speak to her theer, for her loving uncle was come home, and he wouldn’t--no, Mas’r Davy,’ said Ham, with great earnestness, ‘he couldn’t, kind-natur’d, tender-hearted as he is, see them two together, side by side, for all the treasures that’s wrecked in the sea.’", "외삼촌은 그 두 사람을 — 아니, 데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 무척 진지하게 말했다. \"그분이 워낙 마음씨 곱고 다정한 분이라, 그 두 사람이 나란히 함께 있는 걸, 바다에 가라앉은 온갖 보물을 다 준다 해도, 차마 못 보실 거예요.\""]], [["I felt how true this was. I knew it, on the instant, quite as well as Ham.", "나는 그것이 얼마나 사실인지 느꼈다. 나는 그 순간, 햄만큼이나 그것을 잘 알았다."]], [["‘So Em’ly writes in pencil on a bit of paper,’ he pursued, ‘and gives it to her out o’ winder to bring here.", "\"그래서 에밀리가 종잇조각에 연필로 글을 적어서,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"창밖으로 그 여자한테 건네주며 여기로 가져가라고 했어요."], ["“Show that,”", "'이걸 보여 드려,'"], ["she says, “to my aunt, Mrs.", "하고요, '우리 외숙모 바키스 부인한테."], ["Barkis, and she’ll set you down by her fire, for the love of me, till uncle is gone out, and I can come.”", "그러면 나를 봐서, 외삼촌이 나가시고 내가 갈 수 있을 때까지, 부인이 너를 난롯가에 앉혀 주실 거야.'"], ["By and by she tells me what I tell you, Mas’r Davy, and asks me to bring her.", "이윽고 그 여자가 제게 도련님께 말씀드린 그 이야기를 하고는, 자기를 여기로 데려다 달라고 부탁하더군요."], ["What can I do?", "제가 어쩌겠어요?"], ["She doen’t ought to know any such, but I can’t deny her, when the tears is on her face.’", "그런 사람을 알아선 안 되지만, 얼굴에 눈물이 흐르는데 어떻게 거절을 하겠어요.\""]], [["He put his hand into the breast of his shaggy jacket, and took out with great care a pretty little purse.", "그는 텁수룩한 윗도리 가슴팍에 손을 넣더니, 무척 조심스럽게 예쁘고 작은 지갑 하나를 꺼냈다."]], [["‘And if I could deny her when the tears was on her face, Mas’r Davy,’ said Ham, tenderly adjusting it on the rough palm of his hand, ‘how could I deny her when she give me this to carry for her--knowing what she brought it for?", "\"얼굴에 눈물이 흐르는데도 제가 거절할 수 있었다 쳐도요, 데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 거친 손바닥 위에 그것을 정성스레 올려놓으며 말했다. \"이걸 자기 대신 맡아 달라고 줬는데 — 무엇 때문에 가져왔는지 빤히 알면서 — 어떻게 거절을 하겠어요?"], ["Such a toy as it is!’ said Ham, thoughtfully looking on it.", "이렇게 앙증맞은 물건을!\" 햄이 그것을 골똘히 들여다보며 말했다."], ["‘With such a little money in it, Em’ly my dear.’", "\"이 안에 그렇게 쥐꼬리만 한 돈을 넣어서 말이에요, 내 사랑 에밀리.\""]], [["I shook him warmly by the hand when he had put it away again--for that was more satisfactory to me than saying anything--and we walked up and down, for a minute or two, in silence.", "그가 그것을 도로 집어넣었을 때 나는 그의 손을 따뜻하게 잡고 흔들었다 — 그게 무슨 말을 하는 것보다 내게는 더 흡족했으니까 — 그리고 우리는 잠시 말없이 한두 차례 오르락내리락 거닐었다."], ["The door opened then, and Peggotty appeared, beckoning to Ham to come in.", "그때 문이 열리고 패거티가 나타나, 햄에게 들어오라고 손짓했다."], ["I would have kept away, but she came after me, entreating me to come in too.", "나는 비켜서 있으려 했지만, 그녀가 내 뒤를 따라와 나도 함께 들어오라고 간곡히 청했다."], ["Even then, I would have avoided the room where they all were, but for its being the neat-tiled kitchen I have mentioned more than once.", "그래도 나는 그들이 다 모여 있는 그 방을 피하고 싶었으나, 그곳이 내가 한두 번 말한 적 있는 그 깔끔한 타일 깔린 부엌이었기에 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["The door opening immediately into it, I found myself among them before I considered whither I was going.", "문이 바로 그 부엌으로 통해 있었던지라, 나는 어디로 가고 있는지 미처 헤아릴 새도 없이 그들 한가운데 들어서 있었다."]], [["The girl--the same I had seen upon the sands--was near the fire.", "그 처녀는 — 내가 모래밭에서 보았던 바로 그 여자는 — 난롯가에 있었다."], ["She was sitting on the ground, with her head and one arm lying on a chair.", "그녀는 바닥에 앉아, 머리와 한쪽 팔을 의자에 기대고 있었다."], ["I fancied, from the disposition of her figure, that Em’ly had but newly risen from the chair, and that the forlorn head might perhaps have been lying on her lap.", "그 몸짓으로 보아, 에밀리가 방금 그 의자에서 일어선 참이고, 그 가엾은 머리가 어쩌면 에밀리의 무릎에 얹혀 있었던 게 아닐까 싶었다."], ["I saw but little of the girl’s face, over which her hair fell loose and scattered, as if she had been disordering it with her own hands; but I saw that she was young, and of a fair complexion.", "그녀의 얼굴은 거의 보이지 않았는데, 마치 제 손으로 헝클어 놓기라도 한 듯 머리카락이 풀어 헤쳐져 그 위로 흘러내려 있었기 때문이다. 하지만 그녀가 젊고, 살결이 흰 사람이라는 것은 알 수 있었다."], ["Peggotty had been crying.", "패거티는 울고 있었다."], ["So had little Em’ly.", "어린 에밀리도 울고 있었다."], ["Not a word was spoken when we first went in; and the Dutch clock by the dresser seemed, in the silence, to tick twice as loud as usual. Em’ly spoke first.", "우리가 처음 들어섰을 때는 아무 말도 오가지 않았고, 그 정적 속에서 찬장 옆 네덜란드제 시계가 평소보다 곱절은 크게 똑딱이는 듯했다. 에밀리가 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Martha wants,’ she said to Ham, ‘to go to London.’", "\"마사가,\" 그녀가 햄에게 말했다. \"런던으로 가고 싶대요.\""]], [["‘Why to London?’ returned Ham.", "\"왜 런던으로?\" 햄이 받았다."]], [["He stood between them, looking on the prostrate girl with a mixture of compassion for her, and of jealousy of her holding any companionship with her whom he loved so well, which I have always remembered distinctly.", "그는 두 사람 사이에 서서, 엎드려 있는 그 처녀를 향한 연민과, 자기가 그토록 사랑하는 사람과 그녀가 어울린다는 것에 대한 시샘이 뒤섞인 눈으로 내려다보고 있었는데, 나는 그 모습을 늘 또렷이 기억해 왔다."], ["They both spoke as if she were ill; in a soft, suppressed tone that was plainly heard, although it hardly rose above a whisper.", "두 사람은 마치 그녀가 병자라도 되는 양, 부드럽고 억눌린 어조로 말했는데, 그 소리는 속삭임을 겨우 넘는 정도였으나 또렷이 들렸다."]], [["‘Better there than here,’ said a third voice aloud--Martha’s, though she did not move.", "\"여기보다는 거기가 나아요.\" 세 번째 목소리가 또렷이 말했다 — 마사였다, 비록 몸은 꿈쩍하지 않았지만."], ["‘No one knows me there.", "\"거기선 아무도 나를 몰라요."], ["Everybody knows me here.’", "여기선 누구나 나를 알고요.\""]], [["‘What will she do there?’ inquired Ham.", "\"거기 가서 뭘 하려고?\" 햄이 물었다."]], [["She lifted up her head, and looked darkly round at him for a moment; then laid it down again, and curved her right arm about her neck, as a woman in a fever, or in an agony of pain from a shot, might twist herself.", "그녀는 고개를 들어 잠시 그를 어둡게 둘러보더니, 다시 고개를 떨구고는, 열병에 걸린 여자나 총에 맞아 괴로움에 몸부림치는 사람처럼 오른팔을 제 목에 휘감았다."]], [["‘She will try to do well,’ said little Em’ly.", "\"제대로 살아 보려는 거예요.\" 어린 에밀리가 말했다."], ["‘You don’t know what she has said to us.", "\"이 사람이 우리한테 뭐라고 했는지 오빠는 몰라요."], ["Does he--do they--aunt?’", "외삼촌은 — 다들 — 외숙모는 알아요?\""]], [["Peggotty shook her head compassionately.", "패거티가 가엾다는 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘I’ll try,’ said Martha, ‘if you’ll help me away.", "\"해 볼 거예요.\" 마사가 말했다. \"여기서 떠나게만 도와준다면요."], ["I never can do worse than I have done here. I may do better.", "여기서 살아온 것보다 더 나쁘게 살 수는 없을 거예요. 어쩌면 더 나아질지도 몰라요. 아!\""], ["Oh!’ with a dreadful shiver, ‘take me out of these streets, where the whole town knows me from a child!’", "그녀가 끔찍하게 몸서리치며 말했다. \"이 거리에서 날 좀 빼내 줘, 어릴 적부터 온 마을이 날 다 아는 이 거리에서!\""]], [["As Em’ly held out her hand to Ham, I saw him put in it a little canvas bag.", "에밀리가 햄에게 손을 내밀자, 나는 그가 그 손에 작은 천 주머니 하나를 쥐여 주는 것을 보았다."], ["She took it, as if she thought it were her purse, and made a step or two forward; but finding her mistake, came back to where he had retired near me, and showed it to him.", "그녀는 그것을 제 지갑인 줄 알았는지 받아 들고 한두 걸음 앞으로 나아갔으나, 잘못 안 것을 깨닫고는, 그가 물러나 내 곁에 서 있던 자리로 돌아와 그것을 그에게 보여 주었다."]], [["‘It’s all yourn, Em’ly,’ I could hear him say.", "\"전부 네 거야, 에밀리.\" 나는 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들을 수 있었다."], ["‘I haven’t nowt in all the wureld that ain’t yourn, my dear.", "\"이 세상에 네 것이 아닌 건 내게 아무것도 없어, 내 사랑."], ["It ain’t of no delight to me, except for you!’", "그 어떤 것도 나한텐 기쁨이 안 돼, 너를 위한 것만 빼고는!\""]], [["The tears rose freshly in her eyes, but she turned away and went to Martha.", "그녀의 눈에 다시 눈물이 차올랐지만, 그녀는 돌아서서 마사에게로 갔다."], ["What she gave her, I don’t know.", "그녀가 마사에게 무엇을 주었는지는 모르겠다."], ["I saw her stooping over her, and putting money in her bosom.", "나는 그녀가 마사 위로 몸을 숙여 그 품속에 돈을 넣어 주는 것을 보았다."], ["She whispered something, as she asked was that enough?", "그녀는 무언가 속삭이며, 이만하면 되겠느냐고 물었다."], ["‘More than enough,’ the other said, and took her hand and kissed it.", "\"넘치고도 남아.\" 상대가 말하며, 그녀의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었다."]], [["Then Martha arose, and gathering her shawl about her, covering her face with it, and weeping aloud, went slowly to the door.", "그러고는 마사가 일어나, 숄을 끌어당겨 몸을 감싸고, 그것으로 얼굴을 가린 채 소리 내어 울며 천천히 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["She stopped a moment before going out, as if she would have uttered something or turned back; but no word passed her lips.", "그녀는 나가기 직전에 잠시 멈춰 서서, 마치 무언가 말하려 하거나 되돌아오려는 듯했으나, 입술 사이로는 한마디도 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["Making the same low, dreary, wretched moaning in her shawl, she went away.", "숄 속에서 그 낮고 음울하고 처량한 흐느낌을 똑같이 흘리며, 그녀는 떠나갔다."]], [["As the door closed, little Em’ly looked at us three in a hurried manner and then hid her face in her hands, and fell to sobbing.", "문이 닫히자, 어린 에밀리는 황급히 우리 셋을 둘러보더니, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느껴 울기 시작했다."]], [["‘Doen’t, Em’ly!’ said Ham, tapping her gently on the shoulder.", "\"그러지 마, 에밀리!\" 햄이 그녀의 어깨를 가만히 토닥이며 말했다."], ["‘Doen’t, my dear!", "\"그러지 마, 내 사랑!"], ["You doen’t ought to cry so, pretty!’", "그렇게 울면 안 돼, 어여쁜 사람!\""]], [["‘Oh, Ham!’ she exclaimed, still weeping pitifully, ‘I am not so good a girl as I ought to be!", "\"아, 햄!\" 그녀가 여전히 가엾게 울며 외쳤다. \"난 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 착한 아이가 못 돼!"], ["I know I have not the thankful heart, sometimes, I ought to have!’", "가끔은 마땅히 가져야 할 만큼 고마운 마음을 못 가질 때가 있다는 걸 나도 알아!\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, you have, I’m sure,’ said Ham.", "\"아냐, 아냐, 넌 그래, 틀림없이.\" 햄이 말했다."]], [["‘No! no! no!’ cried little Em’ly, sobbing, and shaking her head.", "\"아냐! 아냐! 아냐!\" 어린 에밀리가 흐느끼며 고개를 저으며 외쳤다."], ["‘I am not as good a girl as I ought to be.", "\"난 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 착한 아이가 못 돼."], ["Not near!", "어림도 없어!"], ["not near!’ And still she cried, as if her heart would break.", "어림도 없어!\" 그러고도 그녀는 마치 가슴이 미어지기라도 할 듯 계속 울었다."]], [["‘I try your love too much. I know I do!’ she sobbed.", "\"난 오빠 사랑을 너무 시험에 들게 해. 나도 알아!\""], ["‘I’m often cross to you, and changeable with you, when I ought to be far different.", "그녀가 흐느꼈다. \"난 오빠한테 자주 짜증을 내고 변덕을 부려, 한참 다르게 굴어야 마땅한데."], ["You are never so to me.", "오빠는 나한테 절대 그러지 않잖아."], ["Why am I ever so to you, when I should think of nothing but how to be grateful, and to make you happy!’", "난 어쩌자고 오빠한테 그러는 걸까, 오로지 어떻게 고마워하고 어떻게 오빠를 행복하게 해 줄지만 생각해야 마땅한데!\""]], [["‘You always make me so,’ said Ham, ‘my dear!", "\"넌 늘 날 행복하게 해 줘.\" 햄이 말했다. \"내 사랑!"], ["I am happy in the sight of you.", "난 널 보는 것만으로도 행복해."], ["I am happy, all day long, in the thoughts of you.’", "난 하루 종일 네 생각만 해도 행복하다고.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["that’s not enough!’ she cried.", "그걸론 부족해!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["‘That is because you are good; not because I am!", "\"그건 오빠가 착해서지, 내가 착해서가 아냐!"], ["Oh, my dear, it might have been a better fortune for you, if you had been fond of someone else--of someone steadier and much worthier than me, who was all bound up in you, and never vain and changeable like me!’", "아, 내 사랑, 오빠가 다른 누군가를 — 나보다 더 한결같고 훨씬 떳떳한 사람, 오빠한테 온통 마음을 쏟고, 나처럼 허영스럽거나 변덕스럽지 않은 사람을 좋아했더라면, 오빠한테 더 나은 복이 됐을 텐데!\""]], [["‘Poor little tender-heart,’ said Ham, in a low voice.", "\"가엾은 여린 마음.\" 햄이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘Martha has overset her, altogether.’", "\"마사가 이 아이를 완전히 뒤흔들어 놓았어요.\""]], [["‘Please, aunt,’ sobbed Em’ly, ‘come here, and let me lay my head upon you.", "\"제발요, 외숙모.\" 에밀리가 흐느꼈다. \"이리 오셔서 제가 머리를 기대게 해 주세요."], ["Oh, I am very miserable tonight, aunt!", "아, 오늘 밤은 너무 비참해요, 외숙모!"], ["Oh, I am not as good a girl as I ought to be.", "아, 난 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 착한 아이가 못 돼요."], ["I am not, I know!’", "그래요, 나도 알아요!\""]], [["Peggotty had hastened to the chair before the fire.", "패거티는 어느새 난롯불 앞 의자로 서둘러 가 있었다."], ["Em’ly, with her arms around her neck, kneeled by her, looking up most earnestly into her face.", "에밀리는 두 팔로 그녀의 목을 끌어안고 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇은 채, 더없이 간절하게 그 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["‘Oh, pray, aunt, try to help me!", "\"아, 제발요, 외숙모, 저를 좀 도와주세요!"], ["Ham, dear, try to help me! Mr.", "햄, 오빠, 저를 좀 도와줘요!"], ["David, for the sake of old times, do, please, try to help me!", "데이비드 씨, 옛정을 봐서라도, 제발, 저를 좀 도와주세요!"], ["I want to be a better girl than I am.", "저는 지금보다 더 나은 아이가 되고 싶어요."], ["I want to feel a hundred times more thankful than I do.", "지금보다 백 배는 더 고마운 마음을 느끼고 싶어요."], ["I want to feel more, what a blessed thing it is to be the wife of a good man, and to lead a peaceful life. Oh me, oh me!", "착한 사람의 아내가 되어 평온하게 사는 게 얼마나 복된 일인지 더 절실히 느끼고 싶어요. 아 어쩌나, 아 어쩌나!"], ["Oh my heart, my heart!’", "아 내 마음, 내 마음이!\""]], [["She dropped her face on my old nurse’s breast, and, ceasing this supplication, which in its agony and grief was half a woman’s, half a child’s, as all her manner was (being, in that, more natural, and better suited to her beauty, as I thought, than any other manner could have been), wept silently, while my old nurse hushed her like an infant.", "그녀는 내 옛 유모의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고, 그 괴로움과 슬픔이 절반은 여인의 것이요 절반은 아이의 것이던 이 간청을 멈추고는 — 그녀의 모든 태도가 그러했듯이, 그 점에서 (내 생각에는) 다른 어떤 태도보다 더 자연스럽고 그 아름다움에 더 어울렸다 — 소리 없이 울었고, 내 옛 유모는 갓난아기를 달래듯 그녀를 어르고 달랬다."]], [["She got calmer by degrees, and then we soothed her; now talking encouragingly, and now jesting a little with her, until she began to raise her head and speak to us.", "그녀는 차츰 진정되었고, 그러자 우리는 그녀를 다독였다. 때로는 기운을 북돋는 말을 하고, 때로는 가벼운 농담을 건네다 보니, 마침내 그녀가 고개를 들어 우리에게 말을 하기 시작했다."], ["So we got on, until she was able to smile, and then to laugh, and then to sit up, half ashamed; while Peggotty recalled her stray ringlets, dried her eyes, and made her neat again, lest her uncle should wonder, when she got home, why his darling had been crying.", "그렇게 우리는 점점 나아갔고, 마침내 그녀는 미소 짓다가, 이내 웃다가, 좀 부끄러운 듯 일어나 앉을 수 있게 되었다. 그러는 동안 패거티는 그녀의 흐트러진 곱슬머리를 매만지고, 눈물을 닦아 주고, 다시 단정하게 다듬어 주었다. 집에 돌아갔을 때, 외삼촌이 어쩌자고 제 귀염둥이가 울었을까 의아해하지 않도록 말이다."]], [["I saw her do, that night, what I had never seen her do before.", "그날 밤 나는 그녀가 전에는 한 번도 보지 못한 행동을 하는 것을 보았다."], ["I saw her innocently kiss her chosen husband on the cheek, and creep close to his bluff form as if it were her best support.", "나는 그녀가 천진하게 제가 택한 남편의 뺨에 입 맞추고, 그 우람한 몸에 마치 그것이 자기에게 가장 든든한 버팀목이라도 되는 양 바싹 다가서는 것을 보았다."], ["When they went away together, in the waning moonlight, and I looked after them, comparing their departure in my mind with Martha’s, I saw that she held his arm with both her hands, and still kept close to him.", "두 사람이 이지러져 가는 달빛 속을 함께 떠나갈 때, 나는 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보며 마음속으로 그 떠나가는 모습을 마사의 모습과 견주어 보았는데, 그녀가 두 손으로 그의 팔을 붙들고, 여전히 그에게 바싹 붙어 가는 것이 보였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 23. I CORROBORATE Mr. DICK, AND CHOOSE A PROFESSION", "# CHAPTER 23. I CORROBORATE Mr. DICK, AND CHOOSE A PROFESSION"]], [["When I awoke in the morning I thought very much of little Em’ly, and her emotion last night, after Martha had left.", "아침에 잠에서 깨어났을 때 나는 어린 에밀리와, 어젯밤 마사가 떠난 뒤에 그 애가 보였던 격한 마음을 한참 동안 생각했다."], ["I felt as if I had come into the knowledge of those domestic weaknesses and tendernesses in a sacred confidence, and that to disclose them, even to Steerforth, would be wrong.", "마치 내가 어떤 거룩한 믿음 속에서 그 집안의 약함과 다정함을 알게 된 것 같았고, 그것을 누군가에게 — 스티어포스에게라도 — 발설하는 건 잘못된 일이라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["I had no gentler feeling towards anyone than towards the pretty creature who had been my playmate, and whom I have always been persuaded, and shall always be persuaded, to my dying day, I then devotedly loved.", "내 어린 시절의 놀이 동무였던 그 어여쁜 아이만큼 부드러운 마음을 품게 하는 사람은 세상에 없었으며, 그때 내가 그 애를 진심으로 사랑했다고 나는 지금도 믿고 있고, 죽는 날까지 늘 그렇게 믿을 것이다."], ["The repetition to any ears--even to Steerforth’s--of what she had been unable to repress when her heart lay open to me by an accident, I felt would be a rough deed, unworthy of myself, unworthy of the light of our pure childhood, which I always saw encircling her head.", "어떤 우연 덕분에 그 애의 마음이 내 앞에 활짝 열렸을 때 그 애가 차마 억누르지 못하고 쏟아 낸 것을, 누군가의 귀에 — 스티어포스의 귀에라도 — 다시 옮긴다는 건, 나 자신에게도 어울리지 않고 그 애의 머리 둘레를 늘 감싸고 있다고 내가 느끼던 우리 순수한 어린 시절의 빛에도 어울리지 않는, 거친 짓이라는 생각이 들었다."], ["I made a resolution, therefore, to keep it in my own breast; and there it gave her image a new grace.", "그래서 나는 그것을 내 가슴속에만 간직하기로 마음먹었다. 그러자 그 비밀은 내 가슴속에서 그 애의 모습에 새로운 우아함을 더해 주었다."]], [["While we were at breakfast, a letter was delivered to me from my aunt.", "아침을 먹는 동안 고모님이 보낸 편지 한 통이 나에게 전해졌다."], ["As it contained matter on which I thought Steerforth could advise me as well as anyone, and on which I knew I should be delighted to consult him, I resolved to make it a subject of discussion on our journey home.", "그 안에는 누구 못지않게 스티어포스가 조언해 줄 만한 일이 담겨 있었고, 그와 의논하면 분명 기쁠 거라는 것도 알았기에, 나는 집으로 돌아가는 길에 그것을 이야깃거리로 삼기로 했다."], ["For the present we had enough to do, in taking leave of all our friends. Mr.", "당장은 모든 벗들과 작별하느라 할 일이 많았다."], ["Barkis was far from being the last among them, in his regret at our departure; and I believe would even have opened the box again, and sacrificed another guinea, if it would have kept us eight-and-forty hours in Yarmouth.", "바키스 씨는 우리가 떠나는 것을 아쉬워하는 사람들 가운데서도 결코 뒤지지 않았다. 내 생각에 그는 만약 그렇게 해서 우리를 야머스에 마흔여덟 시간만 더 붙잡아 둘 수 있었다면, 그 상자를 다시 열어 또 한 기니를 기꺼이 바쳤을 것이다."], ["Peggotty and all her family were full of grief at our going.", "패거티와 그 가족은 모두 우리가 떠나는 것에 슬픔으로 가득했다."], ["The whole house of Omer and Joram turned out to bid us good-bye; and there were so many seafaring volunteers in attendance on Steerforth, when our portmanteaux went to the coach, that if we had had the baggage of a regiment with us, we should hardly have wanted porters to carry it.", "오머와 조램의 집안 전체가 우리를 배웅하러 나왔고, 우리 여행 가방을 마차로 옮길 때 스티어포스를 거들겠다고 나선 뱃사람 자원자가 어찌나 많던지, 우리가 연대 하나의 짐을 다 짊어지고 있었다 해도 그것을 나를 짐꾼이 모자라지 않았을 것이다."], ["In a word, we departed to the regret and admiration of all concerned, and left a great many people very sorry behind US.", "한마디로, 우리는 관련된 모든 이들의 아쉬움과 흠모를 받으며 떠났고, 우리 뒤에는 무척이나 서운해하는 사람들을 잔뜩 남겨 두었다."]], [["‘Do you stay long here, Littimer?’ said I, as he stood waiting to see the coach start.", "\"리티머, 자네는 여기 오래 머무나?\" 마차가 출발하는 것을 지켜보려고 서서 기다리는 그에게 내가 물었다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ he replied; ‘probably not very long, sir.’", "\"아닙니다, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"아마 그리 오래는 아닐 겁니다, 도련님.\""]], [["‘He can hardly say, just now,’ observed Steerforth, carelessly.", "\"지금 당장은 그도 잘 모를 거야.\" 스티어포스가 무심하게 말했다."], ["‘He knows what he has to do, and he’ll do it.’", "\"자기가 해야 할 일을 알고 있고, 그걸 해낼 테니까.\""]], [["‘That I am sure he will,’ said I.", "\"틀림없이 그러겠지.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Littimer touched his hat in acknowledgement of my good opinion, and I felt about eight years old.", "리티머는 나를 좋게 봐 준 데에 답하듯 모자에 손을 댔는데, 나는 내가 여덟 살쯤 된 어린애가 된 기분이었다."], ["He touched it once more, wishing us a good journey; and we left him standing on the pavement, as respectable a mystery as any pyramid in Egypt.", "그는 우리에게 즐거운 여행을 빌며 다시 한 번 모자에 손을 댔고, 우리는 그를 인도에 세워 둔 채 떠났으니, 그는 이집트의 어느 피라미드 못지않게 점잖은 수수께끼였다."]], [["For some little time we held no conversation, Steerforth being unusually silent, and I being sufficiently engaged in wondering, within myself, when I should see the old places again, and what new changes might happen to me or them in the meanwhile.", "한동안 우리는 아무 대화도 나누지 않았다. 스티어포스는 유난히 말이 없었고, 나는 나대로 그 옛 장소들을 언제 다시 보게 될지, 그동안 나에게나 그곳에 어떤 새로운 변화가 닥칠지 속으로 골똘히 생각하느라 정신이 팔려 있었다."], ["At length Steerforth, becoming gay and talkative in a moment, as he could become anything he liked at any moment, pulled me by the arm:", "마침내 스티어포스가, 언제든 마음만 먹으면 무엇이든 될 수 있는 사람답게 순식간에 명랑하고 수다스러워지더니, 내 팔을 잡아당겼다."]], [["‘Find a voice, David. What about that letter you were speaking of at breakfast?’", "\"입 좀 떼 봐, 데이비드. 아침에 말하던 그 편지는 무슨 얘기야?\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said I, taking it out of my pocket.", "\"아!\" 내가 주머니에서 편지를 꺼내며 말했다."], ["‘It’s from my aunt.’", "\"고모님이 보내신 거야.\""]], [["‘And what does she say, requiring consideration?’", "\"그래, 고려해 볼 만한 게 뭐라고 쓰셨는데?\""]], [["‘Why, she reminds me, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘that I came out on this expedition to look about me, and to think a little.’", "\"그게, 스티어포스, 고모님이 나한테 일깨워 주시는 거야.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 이번 여행을 떠난 건 주위를 둘러보고 좀 생각해 보라는 뜻이었다고 말이야.\""]], [["‘Which, of course, you have done?’", "\"그래서 당연히 둘러보고 생각도 했겠지?\""]], [["‘Indeed I can’t say I have, particularly.", "\"솔직히 딱히 그랬다고는 못 하겠어."], ["To tell you the truth, I am afraid I have forgotten it.’", "사실대로 말하자면, 그걸 까맣게 잊고 있었던 것 같아.\""]], [["‘Well! look about you now, and make up for your negligence,’ said Steerforth.", "\"그럼! 지금이라도 주위를 둘러보고 게으름을 만회하라고.\""], ["‘Look to the right, and you’ll see a flat country, with a good deal of marsh in it; look to the left, and you’ll see the same.", "스티어포스가 말했다. \"오른쪽을 봐, 늪이 잔뜩 깔린 평평한 들판이 보일 거야. 왼쪽을 봐, 똑같은 게 보이지."], ["Look to the front, and you’ll find no difference; look to the rear, and there it is still.’ I laughed, and replied that I saw no suitable profession in the whole prospect; which was perhaps to be attributed to its flatness.", "앞을 봐, 다를 게 없어. 뒤를 봐, 거기도 마찬가지고.\" 나는 웃으면서, 이 풍경을 통틀어 봐도 나한테 어울릴 만한 직업이 하나도 안 보인다고, 아마 이 평평함 탓인 것 같다고 대꾸했다."]], [["‘What says our aunt on the subject?’ inquired Steerforth, glancing at the letter in my hand.", "\"그 문제에 대해선 우리 고모님이 뭐라셔?\" 스티어포스가 내 손에 든 편지를 흘끗 보며 물었다."], ["‘Does she suggest anything?’", "\"뭔가 제안하시는 게 있어?\""]], [["‘Why, yes,’ said I.", "\"응, 있어.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘She asks me, here, if I think I should like to be a proctor?", "\"여기서, 내가 송무대리인이 되면 좋겠다고 생각하는지 물어보셔."], ["What do you think of it?’", "넌 어떻게 생각해?\""]], [["‘Well, I don’t know,’ replied Steerforth, coolly.", "\"글쎄, 잘 모르겠는데.\" 스티어포스가 무덤덤하게 대답했다."], ["‘You may as well do that as anything else, I suppose?’", "\"다른 거나 마찬가지로 그것도 해 볼 만하지 않을까 싶다만?\""]], [["I could not help laughing again, at his balancing all callings and professions so equally; and I told him so.", "그가 모든 직업과 천직을 그렇게 똑같이 저울질하는 통에 나는 또 웃지 않을 수 없었고, 그 말을 그에게 그대로 해 주었다."]], [["‘What is a proctor, Steerforth?’ said I.", "\"송무대리인이 뭐야, 스티어포스?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, he is a sort of monkish attorney,’ replied Steerforth.", "\"그게 말이지, 수도승 같은 변호사라고나 할까.\""], ["‘He is, to some faded courts held in Doctors’ Commons,--a lazy old nook near St. Paul’s Churchyard--what solicitors are to the courts of law and equity.", "스티어포스가 대답했다. \"변호사가 보통법 법정이나 형평법 법정에서 하는 일을, 그 사람은 박사회관에서 열리는 한물간 몇몇 법정에서 하는 거야 — 박사회관이라는 데가 세인트폴 묘지 근처에 있는 게으른 옛 구석탱이지."], ["He is a functionary whose existence, in the natural course of things, would have terminated about two hundred years ago. I can tell you best what he is, by telling you what Doctors’ Commons is.", "자연스러운 이치대로라면 그런 직업은 한 이백 년 전쯤에 사라졌어야 할 거야. 그가 뭐 하는 사람인지는, 박사회관이 뭔지를 말해 주면 가장 잘 알 수 있을 거야."], ["It’s a little out-of-the-way place, where they administer what is called ecclesiastical law, and play all kinds of tricks with obsolete old monsters of acts of Parliament, which three-fourths of the world know nothing about, and the other fourth supposes to have been dug up, in a fossil state, in the days of the Edwards.", "외진 자그마한 곳인데, 거기서 이른바 교회법이라는 걸 다루면서, 세상 사람들 사분의 삼은 들어 본 적도 없고 나머지 사분의 일은 에드워드 왕들 시절에 화석 상태로 발굴된 거라고 여기는, 케케묵은 의회 제정법이라는 낡은 괴물들을 가지고 온갖 재주를 부려."], ["It’s a place that has an ancient monopoly in suits about people’s wills and people’s marriages, and disputes among ships and boats.’", "사람들의 유언이며 혼인에 관한 소송, 그리고 배와 보트끼리의 분쟁을 두고 예부터 독점권을 쥐고 있는 데지.\""]], [["‘Nonsense, Steerforth!’ I exclaimed.", "\"말도 안 돼, 스티어포스!\""], ["‘You don’t mean to say that there is any affinity between nautical matters and ecclesiastical matters?’", "내가 외쳤다. \"설마 뱃일하고 교회 일 사이에 무슨 연관이 있다는 얘기는 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘I don’t, indeed, my dear boy,’ he returned; ‘but I mean to say that they are managed and decided by the same set of people, down in that same Doctors’ Commons.", "\"아무렴, 그건 아니지, 이 친구야.\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"내 말은, 바로 그 박사회관에서 같은 사람들이 그 두 가지를 다 맡아 처리하고 판결한다는 거야."], ["You shall go there one day, and find them blundering through half the nautical terms in Young’s Dictionary, apropos of the “Nancy” having run down the “Sarah Jane”, or Mr. Peggotty and the Yarmouth boatmen having put off in a gale of wind with an anchor and cable to the “Nelson”", "어느 날 자네가 거기 가 보면, '낸시'호가 '세라 제인'호를 들이받았다든가, 아니면 패거티 씨와 야머스 뱃사람들이 폭풍을 뚫고 닻과 밧줄을 들고 조난당한 인도 무역선 '넬슨'호로 출동했다든가 하는 사건을 두고, 그들이 영 사전에 실린 항해 용어의 절반을 더듬더듬 헤매는 꼴을 보게 될 거야."], ["Indiaman in distress; and you shall go there another day, and find them deep in the evidence, pro and con, respecting a clergyman who has misbehaved himself; and you shall find the judge in the nautical case, the advocate in the clergyman’s case, or contrariwise.", "또 어느 날 가 보면, 행실이 나빴던 어느 성직자를 둘러싸고 찬반 양쪽 증언에 푹 빠져 있는 모습을 보게 될 텐데, 항해 사건의 판사가 성직자 사건의 변호인이거나, 아니면 그 반대거나 하단 말이지. 그들은 배우들 같아."], ["They are like actors: now a man’s a judge, and now he is not a judge; now he’s one thing, now he’s another; now he’s something else, change and change about; but it’s always a very pleasant, profitable little affair of private theatricals, presented to an uncommonly select audience.’", "어떤 때는 판사였다가 어떤 때는 판사가 아니고, 어떤 때는 이것이었다가 어떤 때는 저것이고, 또 어떤 때는 전혀 다른 무언가가 돼서 서로 돌아가며 바뀐단 말이야. 하지만 그건 언제나 아주 즐겁고 짭짤한, 유난히 선택받은 소수의 관객 앞에서 펼쳐지는 자그마한 아마추어 연극이지.\""]], [["‘But advocates and proctors are not one and the same?’ said I, a little puzzled.", "\"하지만 변호인하고 송무대리인이 같은 사람은 아니지?\" 내가 조금 어리둥절해서 물었다."], ["‘Are they?’", "\"그렇지?\""]], [["‘No,’ returned Steerforth, ‘the advocates are civilians--men who have taken a doctor’s degree at college--which is the first reason of my knowing anything about it.", "\"아니야.\" 스티어포스가 대답했다. \"변호인은 민법학자들이야 — 대학에서 박사 학위를 딴 사람들이지 — 내가 이런 걸 조금이나마 아는 것도 바로 그 때문이고."], ["The proctors employ the advocates.", "송무대리인이 변호인을 고용하는 거야."], ["Both get very comfortable fees, and altogether they make a mighty snug little party.", "양쪽 다 아주 두둑한 수임료를 챙기니까, 다 합쳐서 보면 굉장히 아늑하고 자그마한 패거리인 셈이지."], ["On the whole, I would recommend you to take to Doctors’ Commons kindly, David.", "전체적으로 보면, 데이비드, 나는 자네가 박사회관에 마음 붙이고 들어가는 걸 권하겠어."], ["They plume themselves on their gentility there, I can tell you, if that’s any satisfaction.’", "위안이 될지 모르겠다만, 거기 사람들은 자기네 점잖음을 무척이나 자랑스러워한다고.\""]], [["I made allowance for Steerforth’s light way of treating the subject, and, considering it with reference to the staid air of gravity and antiquity which I associated with that ‘lazy old nook near St. Paul’s Churchyard’, did not feel indisposed towards my aunt’s suggestion; which she left to my free decision, making no scruple of telling me that it had occurred to her, on her lately visiting her own proctor in Doctors’ Commons for the purpose of settling her will in my favour.", "나는 스티어포스가 이 문제를 가볍게 다루는 투를 감안했고, 또 '세인트폴 묘지 근처의 그 게으른 옛 구석탱이'에서 내가 연상하던 차분하고 진중하며 고풍스러운 분위기와 견주어 보니, 고모님의 제안이 영 싫지만은 않았다. 고모님은 그 결정을 온전히 내 자유에 맡기면서, 얼마 전에 내 앞으로 유언을 정리하려고 박사회관에 있는 당신의 송무대리인을 찾아갔다가 그 생각이 떠올랐노라고 거리낌 없이 말해 주었다."]], [["‘That’s a laudable proceeding on the part of our aunt, at all events,’ said Steerforth, when I mentioned it; ‘and one deserving of all encouragement.", "\"어쨌거나 그건 우리 고모님으로서는 칭찬받을 만한 처사로구먼.\" 내가 그 이야기를 꺼내자 스티어포스가 말했다. \"한껏 북돋아 줄 만한 일이고."], ["Daisy, my advice is that you take kindly to Doctors’ Commons.’", "데이지, 내 충고는 자네가 박사회관에 기꺼이 마음을 붙이라는 거야.\""]], [["I quite made up my mind to do so.", "나는 그렇게 하기로 단단히 마음을 정했다."], ["I then told Steerforth that my aunt was in town awaiting me (as I found from her letter), and that she had taken lodgings for a week at a kind of private hotel at Lincoln’s Inn Fields, where there was a stone staircase, and a convenient door in the roof; my aunt being firmly persuaded that every house in London was going to be burnt down every night.", "그러고 나서 나는 스티어포스에게, (편지를 보고 알았는데) 고모님이 나를 기다리느라 시내에 와 계시며, 링컨스 인 광장에 있는 어느 사설 호텔 같은 곳에 일주일 묵을 방을 잡으셨는데, 거기에는 돌계단과 지붕으로 통하는 편리한 문이 있다고 말해 주었다. 고모님은 런던의 집이란 집은 죄다 밤마다 불에 타 버릴 거라고 굳게 믿는 분이었으니까."]], [["We achieved the rest of our journey pleasantly, sometimes recurring to Doctors’ Commons, and anticipating the distant days when I should be a proctor there, which Steerforth pictured in a variety of humorous and whimsical lights, that made us both merry.", "우리는 이따금 박사회관 이야기로 돌아가, 내가 거기서 송무대리인이 될 먼 훗날을 그려 보며 즐겁게 남은 여정을 마쳤다. 스티어포스가 그 모습을 우스꽝스럽고 별난 갖가지 빛깔로 그려 내는 통에 우리는 둘 다 유쾌해졌다."], ["When we came to our journey’s end, he went home, engaging to call upon me next day but one; and I drove to Lincoln’s Inn Fields, where I found my aunt up, and waiting supper.", "여정의 끝에 다다르자 그는 모레 나를 찾아오기로 약속하고 집으로 갔고, 나는 링컨스 인 광장으로 마차를 몰았다. 거기서 나는 아직 잠자리에 들지 않고 저녁상을 차려 놓고 기다리는 고모님을 만났다."]], [["If I had been round the world since we parted, we could hardly have been better pleased to meet again.", "설령 헤어진 뒤로 내가 세계를 한 바퀴 돌고 왔다 해도 우리가 다시 만나 이보다 더 기뻐할 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["My aunt cried outright as she embraced me; and said, pretending to laugh, that if my poor mother had been alive, that silly little creature would have shed tears, she had no doubt.", "고모님은 나를 끌어안으며 대놓고 울었다. 그러고는 웃는 척하면서, 만약 내 가엾은 어머니가 살아 있었다면 그 어리석은 작은 것이 분명 눈물을 흘렸을 거라고 말했다."]], [["‘So you have left Mr. Dick behind, aunt?’ said I.", "\"그래서 딕 씨는 두고 오신 거예요, 고모님?\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I am sorry for that.", "\"그거 안됐네요."], ["Ah, Janet, how do you do?’", "아, 재닛, 잘 지냈어?\""]], [["As Janet curtsied, hoping I was well, I observed my aunt’s visage lengthen very much.", "재닛이 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 내 안부를 묻는 사이, 나는 고모님의 얼굴이 몹시 길어지는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘I am sorry for it, too,’ said my aunt, rubbing her nose.", "\"나도 그게 안됐다.\" 고모님이 코를 문지르며 말했다."], ["‘I have had no peace of mind, Trot, since I have been here.’ Before I could ask why, she told me.", "\"여기 온 뒤로 마음이 도무지 편치를 않구나, 트롯.\" 내가 왜냐고 물어보기도 전에, 고모님은 이유를 털어놓았다."]], [["‘I am convinced,’ said my aunt, laying her hand with melancholy firmness on the table, ‘that Dick’s character is not a character to keep the donkeys off.", "\"내가 확신하건대.\" 고모님이 우울한 단호함으로 한 손을 식탁 위에 올려놓으며 말했다. \"딕은 당나귀를 쫓아낼 만한 성격이 못 돼."], ["I am confident he wants strength of purpose.", "의지가 모자라는 게 분명해."], ["I ought to have left Janet at home, instead, and then my mind might perhaps have been at ease.", "차라리 재닛을 집에 두고 올걸 그랬어, 그랬으면 내 마음이 좀 편했을지도 모르는데."], ["If ever there was a donkey trespassing on my green,’ said my aunt, with emphasis, ‘there was one this afternoon at four o’clock.", "만약 내 잔디밭에 함부로 들어온 당나귀가 한 마리라도 있었다면,\" 고모님이 힘주어 말했다. \"바로 오늘 오후 네 시에 한 마리가 있었던 거야."], ["A cold feeling came over me from head to foot, and I know it was a donkey!’", "머리끝부터 발끝까지 서늘한 기운이 끼쳐 왔으니, 그게 당나귀라는 걸 난 알아!\""]], [["I tried to comfort her on this point, but she rejected consolation.", "나는 이 문제로 고모님을 달래 보려 했지만, 고모님은 위로를 받아들이지 않았다."]], [["‘It was a donkey,’ said my aunt; ‘and it was the one with the stumpy tail which that Murdering sister of a woman rode, when she came to my house.’ This had been, ever since, the only name my aunt knew for Miss Murdstone. ‘If there is any Donkey in Dover, whose audacity it is harder to me to bear than another’s, that,’ said my aunt, striking the table, ‘is the animal!’", "\"그건 당나귀였어.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"게다가 그 살인자 같은 여편네의 누이가 우리 집에 왔을 때 타고 온, 꼬리가 뭉툭한 바로 그 당나귀였다고.\" 그 후로 줄곧 고모님이 머드스톤 양을 부르는 이름은 이것뿐이었다. \"도버에 있는 당나귀 가운데 그 어떤 놈의 뻔뻔함보다도 내가 더 견디기 힘든 놈이 있다면, 바로,\" 고모님이 식탁을 내리치며 말했다. \"그 짐승이야!\""]], [["Janet ventured to suggest that my aunt might be disturbing herself unnecessarily, and that she believed the donkey in question was then engaged in the sand-and-gravel line of business, and was not available for purposes of trespass. But my aunt wouldn’t hear of it.", "재닛이 조심스레, 마님께서 괜한 일로 속을 끓이시는 것 같다고, 그 당나귀로 말하자면 지금 모래와 자갈을 나르는 일에 매여 있어서 남의 땅을 함부로 침범할 처지가 못 될 거라고 넌지시 말했다. 하지만 고모님은 들으려고도 하지 않았다."]], [["Supper was comfortably served and hot, though my aunt’s rooms were very high up--whether that she might have more stone stairs for her money, or might be nearer to the door in the roof, I don’t know--and consisted of a roast fowl, a steak, and some vegetables, to all of which I did ample justice, and which were all excellent.", "저녁상은 고모님 방이 무척 높은 데 있는데도 — 같은 돈으로 돌계단을 더 많이 누리려는 건지, 아니면 지붕의 문에 더 가까이 있으려는 건지는 모르겠지만 — 따뜻하고 편안하게 차려졌고, 닭구이와 스테이크, 그리고 채소 몇 가지가 있었는데, 나는 그 모든 것을 양껏 먹어 치웠고 죄다 훌륭했다."], ["But my aunt had her own ideas concerning London provision, and ate but little.", "그러나 고모님은 런던 음식에 대해 나름의 견해가 있어서 거의 들지 않았다."]], [["‘I suppose this unfortunate fowl was born and brought up in a cellar,’ said my aunt, ‘and never took the air except on a hackney coach-stand.", "\"이 가엾은 닭은 십중팔구 지하실에서 나고 자랐을 거다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그리고 삯마차 정거장 말고는 한 번도 바깥바람을 쐰 적이 없었겠지."], ["I hope the steak may be beef, but I don’t believe it.", "이 스테이크가 정말 소고기이기를 바라지만, 난 못 믿겠어."], ["Nothing’s genuine in the place, in my opinion, but the dirt.’", "내 생각엔 이곳에서 진짜인 건 더러움뿐이야.\""]], [["‘Don’t you think the fowl may have come out of the country, aunt?’ I hinted.", "\"고모님, 이 닭이 시골에서 왔을 수도 있지 않을까요?\" 내가 슬쩍 운을 띄웠다."]], [["‘Certainly not,’ returned my aunt.", "\"천만에.\" 고모님이 받아쳤다."], ["‘It would be no pleasure to a London tradesman to sell anything which was what he pretended it was.’", "\"런던 장사꾼한테는, 자기가 그렇다고 내세운 그대로의 물건을 파는 일만큼 재미없는 게 없거든.\""]], [["I did not venture to controvert this opinion, but I made a good supper, which it greatly satisfied her to see me do.", "나는 감히 이 견해에 반박하지 못했지만, 저녁만큼은 푸짐하게 먹었고, 내가 그러는 모습을 보고 고모님은 무척이나 흡족해했다."], ["When the table was cleared, Janet assisted her to arrange her hair, to put on her nightcap, which was of a smarter construction than usual (‘in case of fire’, my aunt said), and to fold her gown back over her knees, these being her usual preparations for warming herself before going to bed.", "상이 치워지자 재닛이 고모님을 거들어 머리를 매만지고, 평소보다 더 멋진 모양새의 잠자리 모자를 (고모님 말로는 '불이 날 때를 대비해서'라고 했다) 씌워 드리고, 무릎 위로 치맛자락을 접어 올려 드렸는데, 이것이 잠자리에 들기 전에 몸을 데우려는 고모님의 평소 채비였다."], ["I then made her, according to certain established regulations from which no deviation, however slight, could ever be permitted, a glass of hot wine and water, and a slice of toast cut into long thin strips.", "그러고 나서 나는, 아무리 사소한 일탈도 결코 용납되지 않는 어떤 정해진 규칙에 따라, 고모님께 따뜻한 포도주 물 한 잔과 길고 가늘게 썬 토스트 한 조각을 만들어 드렸다."], ["With these accompaniments we were left alone to finish the evening, my aunt sitting opposite to me drinking her wine and water; soaking her strips of toast in it, one by one, before eating them; and looking benignantly on me, from among the borders of her nightcap.", "이런 것들을 곁들이고 우리 둘만 남아 저녁 시간을 마무리했는데, 고모님은 내 맞은편에 앉아 포도주 물을 마시면서, 토스트 조각을 하나씩 거기에 적셔 먹고는, 잠자리 모자의 테두리 사이로 나를 인자하게 바라보았다."]], [["‘Well, Trot,’ she began, ‘what do you think of the proctor plan?", "\"자, 트롯.\" 고모님이 입을 열었다. \"그 송무대리인 계획을 어떻게 생각하니?"], ["Or have you not begun to think about it yet?’", "아니면 아직 생각해 보지도 않았니?\""]], [["‘I have thought a good deal about it, my dear aunt, and I have talked a good deal about it with Steerforth.", "\"아주 많이 생각해 봤어요, 고모님. 그리고 스티어포스하고도 그 얘기를 많이 나눴고요."], ["I like it very much indeed. I like it exceedingly.’", "정말로 마음에 들어요. 더할 나위 없이 마음에 들어요.\""]], [["‘Come!’ said my aunt.", "\"그래!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘That’s cheering!’", "\"그거 반가운 소리구나!\""]], [["‘I have only one difficulty, aunt.’", "\"다만 한 가지 걸리는 게 있어요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘Say what it is, Trot,’ she returned.", "\"그게 뭔지 말해 보렴, 트롯.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Why, I want to ask, aunt, as this seems, from what I understand, to be a limited profession, whether my entrance into it would not be very expensive?’", "\"그게요, 고모님, 제가 듣기로 이게 한정된 직업인 것 같아서 여쭤보고 싶은데요, 거기 들어가는 데에 돈이 아주 많이 들지 않을까요?\""]], [["‘It will cost,’ returned my aunt, ‘to article you, just a thousand pounds.’", "\"네 견습 계약을 맺는 데에,\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"딱 천 파운드가 들 거다.\""]], [["‘Now, my dear aunt,’ said I, drawing my chair nearer, ‘I am uneasy in my mind about that.", "\"이거 보세요, 고모님.\" 내가 의자를 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["It’s a large sum of money.", "\"그 점이 제 마음을 영 불편하게 해요."], ["You have expended a great deal on my education, and have always been as liberal to me in all things as it was possible to be.", "큰돈이잖아요. 고모님은 제 교육에 이미 큰돈을 쓰셨고, 모든 일에서 늘 더없이 너그럽게 대해 주셨어요."], ["You have been the soul of generosity.", "그야말로 관대함의 화신이셨죠."], ["Surely there are some ways in which I might begin life with hardly any outlay, and yet begin with a good hope of getting on by resolution and exertion.", "분명 거의 돈을 들이지 않고도 인생을 시작할 수 있는 길, 그러면서도 결심과 노력으로 잘 풀려 나갈 좋은 희망을 품고 출발할 길이 있을 거예요."], ["Are you sure that it would not be better to try that course?", "그쪽을 한번 시도해 보는 게 더 낫지 않을까요?"], ["Are you certain that you can afford to part with so much money, and that it is right that it should be so expended? I only ask you, my second mother, to consider.", "그렇게 큰돈을 내놓으셔도 정말 괜찮으신 건지, 그렇게 쓰는 게 옳은 일인지 확신하세요? 그저 부탁드리는 거예요, 제 두 번째 어머니, 한번 생각해 봐 주세요."], ["Are you certain?’", "정말 확신하세요?\""]], [["My aunt finished eating the piece of toast on which she was then engaged, looking me full in the face all the while; and then setting her glass on the chimney-piece, and folding her hands upon her folded skirts, replied as follows:", "고모님은 그때 먹고 있던 토스트 조각을 마저 다 먹으면서, 그러는 내내 내 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다. 그러고는 잔을 벽난로 선반에 내려놓고, 접은 치마 위에 두 손을 포갠 채, 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["‘Trot, my child, if I have any object in life, it is to provide for your being a good, a sensible, and a happy man.", "\"트롯, 얘야, 만약 내 인생에 무슨 목적이 있다면, 그건 네가 착하고, 분별 있고, 행복한 사람이 되도록 뒷바라지하는 거야."], ["I am bent upon it--so is Dick. I should like some people that I know to hear Dick’s conversation on the subject.", "난 거기에 마음을 다 쏟고 있어 — 딕도 마찬가지고. 내가 아는 몇몇 사람들이 이 문제에 관한 딕의 이야기를 들어 봤으면 좋겠구나."], ["Its sagacity is wonderful.", "그 명민함이 놀랍거든."], ["But no one knows the resources of that man’s intellect, except myself!’", "하지만 그 사람 머릿속에 든 자산을 아는 사람은 나 말고는 아무도 없지!\""]], [["She stopped for a moment to take my hand between hers, and went on:", "고모님은 내 손을 두 손 사이에 잡으려고 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이어 갔다."]], [["‘It’s in vain, Trot, to recall the past, unless it works some influence upon the present.", "\"트롯, 지난날을 떠올리는 건, 그것이 지금에 어떤 영향을 끼치지 못한다면 다 헛된 일이야."], ["Perhaps I might have been better friends with your poor father.", "어쩌면 난 네 가엾은 아버지하고 더 좋은 사이로 지낼 수 있었을지도 몰라."], ["Perhaps I might have been better friends with that poor child your mother, even after your sister Betsey Trotwood disappointed me.", "어쩌면 네 누이 베시 트롯우드가 나를 실망시킨 뒤에라도, 네 어머니라는 그 가엾은 아이하고 더 좋은 사이로 지낼 수 있었을지도 모르고."], ["When you came to me, a little runaway boy, all dusty and way-worn, perhaps I thought so. From that time until now, Trot, you have ever been a credit to me and a pride and a pleasure.", "네가 먼지투성이에 길에 지친 채로 달아나 온 어린 사내애가 되어 나를 찾아왔을 때, 어쩌면 난 그런 생각을 했던 것 같다. 그때부터 지금까지, 트롯, 너는 늘 나의 자랑이자 긍지이자 기쁨이었어."], ["I have no other claim upon my means; at least’--here to my surprise she hesitated, and was confused--‘no, I have no other claim upon my means--and you are my adopted child.", "나에겐 내 재산에 대한 다른 권리 주장자가 없단다. 적어도\" — 여기서 놀랍게도 고모님은 머뭇거리며 당황했다 — \"아니, 내 재산에 대한 다른 권리 주장자는 없어 — 그리고 너는 내 양자야."], ["Only be a loving child to me in my age, and bear with my whims and fancies; and you will do more for an old woman whose prime of life was not so happy or conciliating as it might have been, than ever that old woman did for you.’", "그저 늘그막의 나에게 다정한 자식이 되어 주고, 내 변덕과 별난 생각들을 참아 주기만 하렴. 그러면 한창때가 마땅히 그랬어야 할 만큼 행복하지도 다정하지도 못했던 한 늙은 여자에게, 그 늙은 여자가 너에게 해 준 것보다 더 많은 걸 해 주는 셈이 될 거야.\""]], [["It was the first time I had heard my aunt refer to her past history.", "고모님이 자신의 지난 내력을 언급하는 것을 들은 건 그때가 처음이었다."], ["There was a magnanimity in her quiet way of doing so, and of dismissing it, which would have exalted her in my respect and affection, if anything could.", "그것을 조용히 꺼냈다가 이내 떨쳐 버리는 그 태도에는 어떤 너그러운 기품이 있어서, 무엇으로든 더 높일 수 있었다면, 그것은 내 마음속에서 고모님에 대한 존경과 애정을 한층 드높였을 것이다."]], [["‘All is agreed and understood between us, now, Trot,’ said my aunt, ‘and we need talk of this no more.", "\"이제 우리 사이에 모든 게 합의되고 이해됐으니, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이 얘긴 더 할 필요가 없겠다."], ["Give me a kiss, and we’ll go to the Commons after breakfast tomorrow.’", "자, 입 한번 맞춰 주렴. 내일 아침 먹고 나서 같이 박사회관에 가 보자꾸나.\""]], [["We had a long chat by the fire before we went to bed.", "우리는 잠자리에 들기 전에 난롯가에서 오래도록 이야기를 나누었다."], ["I slept in a room on the same floor with my aunt’s, and was a little disturbed in the course of the night by her knocking at my door as often as she was agitated by a distant sound of hackney-coaches or market-carts, and inquiring, ‘if I heard the engines?’ But towards morning she slept better, and suffered me to do so too.", "나는 고모님 방과 같은 층에 있는 방에서 잤는데, 밤중에 멀리서 삯마차나 시장 수레 소리가 들려와 마음이 들썩일 때마다 고모님이 내 방문을 두드리며 \"불자동차 소리 들리지 않니?\" 하고 물어 대는 통에 잠을 좀 설쳤다. 그래도 새벽녘이 되자 고모님은 한결 푹 주무셨고, 나도 그러게 내버려 두었다."]], [["At about mid-day, we set out for the office of Messrs Spenlow and Jorkins, in Doctors’ Commons.", "정오쯤 우리는 박사회관에 있는 스펜로 앤 조킨스 사무소를 향해 길을 나섰다."], ["My aunt, who had this other general opinion in reference to London, that every man she saw was a pickpocket, gave me her purse to carry for her, which had ten guineas in it and some silver.", "고모님은 런던에 관해 또 하나의 일반적인 견해를 품고 있었는데, 눈에 띄는 사람마다 죄다 소매치기라는 것이었다. 그래서 고모님은 십 기니와 은화 몇 닢이 든 지갑을 나더러 대신 들고 다니라고 건네주었다."]], [["We made a pause at the toy shop in Fleet Street, to see the giants of Saint Dunstan’s strike upon the bells--we had timed our going, so as to catch them at it, at twelve o’clock--and then went on towards Ludgate Hill, and St.", "우리는 세인트 던스턴 성당의 거인들이 종을 치는 모습을 보려고 플리트 가의 장난감 가게 앞에 잠시 걸음을 멈추었다 — 그것을 마침 보려고 시간을 맞춰 열두 시에 갔던 것이다 — 그러고는 러드게이트 언덕과 세인트폴 묘지 쪽으로 계속 나아갔다."], ["Paul’s Churchyard. We were crossing to the former place, when I found that my aunt greatly accelerated her speed, and looked frightened.", "우리가 러드게이트 언덕 쪽으로 길을 건너고 있을 때, 나는 고모님이 갑자기 걸음을 부쩍 빨리하면서 겁먹은 표정을 짓는 것을 알아챘다."], ["I observed, at the same time, that a lowering ill-dressed man who had stopped and stared at us in passing, a little before, was coming so close after us as to brush against her.", "동시에, 조금 전 지나가다 멈춰 서서 우리를 빤히 쳐다보던 음침하고 허름한 차림의 사내가 우리 바로 뒤까지 바짝 따라붙어 고모님 몸에 스칠 듯 다가오는 것이 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["‘Trot!", "\"트롯!"], ["My dear Trot!’ cried my aunt, in a terrified whisper, and pressing my arm.", "얘, 트롯!\" 고모님이 겁에 질린 목소리로 속삭이며 내 팔을 꽉 붙들고 외쳤다."], ["‘I don’t know what I am to do.’", "\"어쩌면 좋니, 어쩌면 좋아.\""]], [["‘Don’t be alarmed,’ said I.", "\"놀라지 마세요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘There’s nothing to be afraid of.", "\"겁낼 거 하나도 없어요."], ["Step into a shop, and I’ll soon get rid of this fellow.’", "가게 안으로 들어가세요. 제가 금방 이 작자를 떼어 놓을게요.\""]], [["‘No, no, child!’ she returned.", "\"안 돼, 안 돼, 얘야!\" 고모님이 받아쳤다."], ["‘Don’t speak to him for the world.", "\"절대로 저 사람한테 말 걸지 마라."], ["I entreat, I order you!’", "부탁이다, 명령이야!\""]], [["‘Good Heaven, aunt!’ said I.", "\"맙소사, 고모님!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘He is nothing but a sturdy beggar.’", "\"그냥 억센 거지일 뿐이잖아요.\""]], [["‘You don’t know what he is!’ replied my aunt.", "\"네가 저 사람이 뭐 하는 자인지 몰라서 그래!\" 고모님이 대꾸했다."], ["‘You don’t know who he is!", "\"저 사람이 누군지 모르잖니!"], ["You don’t know what you say!’", "네가 무슨 소리를 하는지도 모르고!\""]], [["We had stopped in an empty door-way, while this was passing, and he had stopped too.", "이렇게 말이 오가는 동안 우리는 텅 빈 어느 문간에 멈춰 서 있었는데, 그 사내도 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Don’t look at him!’ said my aunt, as I turned my head indignantly, ‘but get me a coach, my dear, and wait for me in St.", "\"저 사람 쳐다보지 마라!\" 내가 분이 나서 고개를 돌리자 고모님이 말했다."], ["Paul’s Churchyard.’", "\"그러지 말고, 얘야, 마차나 한 대 잡아 주고, 세인트폴 묘지에서 나를 기다려 다오.\""]], [["‘Wait for you?’ I replied.", "\"고모님을 기다리라고요?\" 내가 되물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ rejoined my aunt.", "\"그래.\" 고모님이 받았다."], ["‘I must go alone.", "\"나 혼자 가야 해."], ["I must go with him.’", "저 사람하고 같이 가야 한다고.\""]], [["‘With him, aunt?", "\"저 사람하고요, 고모님?"], ["This man?’", "저 남자하고요?\""]], [["‘I am in my senses,’ she replied, ‘and I tell you I must.", "\"나 정신 멀쩡하다.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"그러니 가야 한다고 말하는 거야."], ["Get me a coach!’", "마차나 잡아 다오!\""]], [["However much astonished I might be, I was sensible that I had no right to refuse compliance with such a peremptory command.", "내가 아무리 놀랐다 한들, 그토록 단호한 명령을 거역할 권리가 나에게 없다는 것쯤은 알았다."], ["I hurried away a few paces, and called a hackney-chariot which was passing empty.", "나는 몇 걸음 부리나케 달려가, 빈 채로 지나가던 삯마차 한 대를 불러 세웠다."], ["Almost before I could let down the steps, my aunt sprang in, I don’t know how, and the man followed.", "내가 미처 발판을 내리기도 전에, 고모님은 어떻게 했는지 모르게 훌쩍 마차에 올랐고, 그 사내가 뒤따라 올랐다."], ["She waved her hand to me to go away, so earnestly, that, all confounded as I was, I turned from them at once.", "고모님이 어찌나 간절하게 손짓으로 나더러 가라고 하던지, 나는 어리벙벙한 와중에도 곧장 그들에게서 돌아섰다."], ["In doing so, I heard her say to the coachman, ‘Drive anywhere!", "그러다가, 고모님이 마부에게 \"아무 데로나 갑시다! 곧장 달려요!\""], ["Drive straight on!’ and presently the chariot passed me, going up the hill.", "하고 말하는 소리를 들었고, 이내 그 마차가 언덕을 올라가며 내 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["What Mr. Dick had told me, and what I had supposed to be a delusion of his, now came into my mind.", "딕 씨가 나에게 했던 이야기, 그리고 내가 그의 망상이려니 했던 그 이야기가 이제 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["I could not doubt that this person was the person of whom he had made such mysterious mention, though what the nature of his hold upon my aunt could possibly be, I was quite unable to imagine.", "이 사람이 바로 그가 그토록 알쏭달쏭하게 입에 올렸던 그 사람이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 다만 그가 고모님을 옭아쥐고 있는 그 힘의 정체가 도대체 무엇인지는 도무지 짐작조차 할 수 없었다."], ["After half an hour’s cooling in the churchyard, I saw the chariot coming back.", "묘지에서 삼십 분쯤 마음을 가라앉히며 기다리고 있으려니, 그 마차가 돌아오는 것이 보였다."], ["The driver stopped beside me, and my aunt was sitting in it alone.", "마부가 내 곁에 멈춰 섰는데, 고모님은 그 안에 홀로 앉아 있었다."]], [["She had not yet sufficiently recovered from her agitation to be quite prepared for the visit we had to make.", "고모님은 아직 흔들린 마음을 충분히 추스르지 못해, 우리가 가야 할 방문을 치를 채비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["She desired me to get into the chariot, and to tell the coachman to drive slowly up and down a little while.", "고모님은 나더러 마차에 타라고 하고는, 마부에게 잠시 천천히 오르내려 달라고 이르게 했다."], ["She said no more, except, ‘My dear child, never ask me what it was, and don’t refer to it,’ until she had perfectly regained her composure, when she told me she was quite herself now, and we might get out.", "고모님은 \"얘야, 그게 무슨 일이었는지 절대 묻지 말고, 다시는 입에 올리지도 말아라\" 하는 말 외에는 더 이상 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 마음이 완전히 평정을 되찾고 나서야, 이제 다 괜찮아졌으니 내려도 되겠다고 말했다."], ["On her giving me her purse to pay the driver, I found that all the guineas were gone, and only the loose silver remained.", "마부에게 삯을 치르라고 고모님이 지갑을 건넸을 때, 나는 기니가 죄다 사라지고 잔돈 은화만 남아 있는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["Doctors’ Commons was approached by a little low archway.", "박사회관으로 들어가는 길에는 야트막한 작은 아치문 하나가 있었다."], ["Before we had taken many paces down the street beyond it, the noise of the city seemed to melt, as if by magic, into a softened distance.", "그 너머의 거리로 몇 걸음 채 들어서기도 전에, 도시의 소음은 마법에라도 걸린 듯 부드럽게 멀어지며 녹아 사라지는 것 같았다."], ["A few dull courts and narrow ways brought us to the sky-lighted offices of Spenlow and Jorkins; in the vestibule of which temple, accessible to pilgrims without the ceremony of knocking, three or four clerks were at work as copyists.", "흐릿한 안마당 몇 개와 좁은 길들을 지나니 천장으로 빛이 드는 스펜로 앤 조킨스 사무소가 나왔는데, 노크라는 격식 없이도 순례자들이 드나들 수 있는 이 신전의 현관에서는 서기 서너 명이 필사 일을 하고 있었다."], ["One of these, a little dry man, sitting by himself, who wore a stiff brown wig that looked as if it were made of gingerbread, rose to receive my aunt, and show us into Mr. Spenlow’s room.", "그중 한 사람, 진저브레드로 만든 것처럼 보이는 빳빳한 갈색 가발을 쓰고 혼자 떨어져 앉아 있던 자그마하고 메마른 남자가 일어나 고모님을 맞이하고는, 우리를 스펜로 씨의 방으로 안내했다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"스펜로 씨는 지금 법정에 계십니다, 부인.\""], ["Spenlow’s in Court, ma’am,’ said the dry man; ‘it’s an Arches day; but it’s close by, and I’ll send for him directly.’", "그 메마른 남자가 말했다. \"오늘은 아치스 법정이 열리는 날이거든요. 하지만 바로 코앞이라, 곧장 사람을 보내 모셔 오겠습니다.\""]], [["As we were left to look about us while Mr. Spenlow was fetched, I availed myself of the opportunity.", "스펜로 씨를 불러오는 동안 우리는 알아서 주위를 둘러보도록 남겨졌고, 나는 그 기회를 한껏 활용했다."], ["The furniture of the room was old-fashioned and dusty; and the green baize on the top of the writing-table had lost all its colour, and was as withered and pale as an old pauper.", "방의 가구는 구식에다 먼지투성이였고, 필기용 책상 위에 깔린 초록색 모직 천은 빛깔이 다 바래, 늙은 빈민처럼 시들고 핏기 없어 보였다."], ["There were a great many bundles of papers on it, some endorsed as Allegations, and some (to my surprise) as Libels, and some as being in the Consistory Court, and some in the Arches Court, and some in the Prerogative Court, and some in the Admiralty Court, and some in the Delegates’ Court; giving me occasion to wonder much, how many Courts there might be in the gross, and how long it would take to understand them all.", "그 위에는 서류 뭉치가 잔뜩 쌓여 있었는데, 더러는 '주장서'라 적혀 있고, 더러는 (놀랍게도) '명예훼손소장'이라 적혀 있었으며, 더러는 종교법원, 더러는 아치스 법원, 더러는 유언검인법원, 더러는 해사법원, 또 더러는 위임법원에 속하는 것이라 적혀 있어서, 나는 도대체 법정이 통틀어 몇 개나 되며 그것을 죄다 이해하는 데 얼마나 걸릴까 하고 무척이나 궁금해했다."], ["Besides these, there were sundry immense manuscript Books of Evidence taken on affidavit, strongly bound, and tied together in massive sets, a set to each cause, as if every cause were a history in ten or twenty volumes.", "이것들 말고도, 선서 진술로 받은 어마어마한 양의 증거 필사본 여러 권이 단단히 제본된 채, 사건마다 한 묶음씩 묵직하게 끈으로 묶여 있었는데, 마치 사건 하나하나가 열 권 스무 권에 걸친 한 편의 역사이기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["All this looked tolerably expensive, I thought, and gave me an agreeable notion of a proctor’s business.", "이 모든 게 꽤 돈이 들어 보인다는 생각이 들었고, 송무대리인의 일에 대해 흐뭇한 인상을 안겨 주었다."], ["I was casting my eyes with increasing complacency over these and many similar objects, when hasty footsteps were heard in the room outside, and Mr. Spenlow, in a black gown trimmed with white fur, came hurrying in, taking off his hat as he came.", "이것들을 비롯한 여러 비슷한 물건들 위로 점점 흡족한 마음을 담아 눈길을 옮기고 있는데, 바깥방에서 다급한 발소리가 들리더니, 스펜로 씨가 흰 모피로 가장자리를 두른 검은 법복 차림으로 들어오면서 모자를 벗으며 황급히 들어왔다."]], [["He was a little light-haired gentleman, with undeniable boots, and the stiffest of white cravats and shirt-collars.", "그는 머리가 옅고 자그마한 신사였는데, 나무랄 데 없는 장화에, 더없이 빳빳한 흰 넥타이와 셔츠 깃을 두르고 있었다."], ["He was buttoned up, mighty trim and tight, and must have taken a great deal of pains with his whiskers, which were accurately curled.", "그는 위까지 단추를 야무지고 꼭 끼게 채우고 있었고, 정확하게 곱슬곱슬 말려 있는 구레나룻에 어지간히 공을 들였음이 틀림없었다."], ["His gold watch-chain was so massive, that a fancy came across me, that he ought to have a sinewy golden arm, to draw it out with, like those which are put up over the goldbeaters’ shops.", "금시계 줄이 어찌나 묵직하던지, 금박장이 가게 위에 걸려 있는 것 같은 우람한 황금 팔이라도 있어야 그것을 끌어낼 수 있겠다는 엉뚱한 생각이 들 정도였다."], ["He was got up with such care, and was so stiff, that he could hardly bend himself; being obliged, when he glanced at some papers on his desk, after sitting down in his chair, to move his whole body, from the bottom of his spine, like Punch.", "그는 어찌나 정성껏 차려입고 또 어찌나 뻣뻣하던지 몸을 거의 굽히지 못해서, 의자에 앉은 뒤 책상 위 서류를 흘끗 보려 할 때면 펀치 인형처럼 척추 밑동부터 몸 전체를 통째로 움직여야만 했다."]], [["I had previously been presented by my aunt, and had been courteously received. He now said:", "나는 앞서 고모님의 소개를 받았고, 정중한 대접을 받은 터였다. 그가 이제 입을 열었다."]], [["‘And so, Mr. Copperfield, you think of entering into our profession?", "\"그래서, 코퍼필드 군, 자네는 우리 직업에 들어올 생각이로군?"], ["I casually mentioned to Miss Trotwood, when I had the pleasure of an interview with her the other day,’--with another inclination of his body--Punch again--‘that there was a vacancy here. Miss Trotwood was good enough to mention that she had a nephew who was her peculiar care, and for whom she was seeking to provide genteelly in life.", "며칠 전에 트롯우드 양과 면담하는 기쁨을 누렸을 때, 마침 이곳에 빈자리가 하나 있다고 지나가는 말로 언급했더랬지.\" — 다시 한 번 몸을 굽히면서 — 또 펀치 인형이었다 — \"그랬더니 트롯우드 양께서 친절하게도, 당신이 각별히 돌보는 조카가 하나 있는데, 그 아이가 인생을 점잖게 꾸려 나가도록 마련해 주려 한다고 말씀하셨네."], ["That nephew, I believe, I have now the pleasure of’--Punch again.", "그 조카를, 내 생각에, 지금 이렇게 만나는 기쁨을 누리는 것 같은데\""], ["I bowed my acknowledgements, and said, my aunt had mentioned to me that there was that opening, and that I believed I should like it very much.", "— 또 펀치 인형이었다. 나는 고개를 숙여 감사를 표하며, 고모님께 그런 자리가 났다는 이야기를 들었고, 나 역시 그 일이 무척 마음에 들 것 같다고 말했다."], ["That I was strongly inclined to like it, and had taken immediately to the proposal.", "거기에 강하게 끌리는 마음이 들어 그 제안을 곧바로 받아들였노라고."], ["That I could not absolutely pledge myself to like it, until I knew something more about it.", "다만 그 일에 대해 좀 더 알기 전에는 좋아할 거라고 딱 잘라 장담할 수는 없다고."], ["That although it was little else than a matter of form, I presumed I should have an opportunity of trying how I liked it, before I bound myself to it irrevocably.", "비록 형식적인 절차에 지나지 않을지라도, 돌이킬 수 없게 거기에 매이기 전에 그 일이 나에게 맞는지 한번 가늠해 볼 기회가 있으리라 생각한다고 말했다."]], [["‘Oh surely! surely!’ said Mr.", "\"아, 그럼요! 그럼요!\""], ["Spenlow. ‘We always, in this house, propose a month--an initiatory month.", "스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 이곳에서 늘 한 달을 — 시범 삼아 한 달을 두자고 제안한다네."], ["I should be happy, myself, to propose two months--three--an indefinite period, in fact--but I have a partner. Mr. Jorkins.’", "나로서야 두 달을 — 세 달을 — 사실 무기한이라도 기꺼이 제안하고 싶네만, 나에겐 동업자가 있어서 말이야. 조킨스 씨라고.\""]], [["‘And the premium, sir,’ I returned, ‘is a thousand pounds?’", "\"그리고 사례금은요,\" 내가 받았다. \"천 파운드인가요?\""]], [["‘And the premium, Stamp included, is a thousand pounds,’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"그래, 사례금은 인지대를 포함해서 천 파운드일세.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["‘As I have mentioned to Miss Trotwood, I am actuated by no mercenary considerations; few men are less so, I believe; but Mr.", "\"내가 트롯우드 양께도 말씀드렸다시피, 나는 돈을 밝히는 마음에서 움직이는 사람이 아니야."], ["Jorkins has his opinions on these subjects, and I am bound to respect Mr. Jorkins’s opinions. Mr.", "나만큼 그런 사람도 드물 거라고 믿네만, 조킨스 씨는 이런 문제에 자기 나름의 견해가 있고, 나로서는 조킨스 씨의 견해를 존중하지 않을 수가 없거든."], ["Jorkins thinks a thousand pounds too little, in short.’", "한마디로, 조킨스 씨는 천 파운드도 너무 적다고 생각하지.\""]], [["‘I suppose, sir,’ said I, still desiring to spare my aunt, ‘that it is not the custom here, if an articled clerk were particularly useful, and made himself a perfect master of his profession’--I could not help blushing, this looked so like praising myself--‘I suppose it is not the custom, in the later years of his time, to allow him any--’", "\"제 생각에는요,\" 내가 여전히 고모님의 부담을 덜어 드리고 싶은 마음에 말했다. \"만약 견습 서기가 유난히 쓸모가 있어서 자기 일을 완벽하게 통달하게 된다면 — \" 마치 나 자신을 칭찬하는 꼴 같아 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다 — \"견습 기간의 후반부에 가서는 그에게 무언가 — 그런 관례는 여기에 없겠지요?\""]], [["Mr. Spenlow, by a great effort, just lifted his head far enough out of his cravat to shake it, and answered, anticipating the word ‘salary’:", "스펜로 씨는 안간힘을 써서, 넥타이 밖으로 고개를 겨우 가로저을 수 있을 만큼만 빼 들고는, '봉급'이라는 말을 미리 앞질러 대답했다."]], [["‘No. I will not say what consideration I might give to that point myself, Mr.", "\"없네. 만약 내가 매인 데가 없다면 그 점에 대해 내가 어떤 배려를 베풀지는 말하지 않겠네, 코퍼필드 군."], ["Copperfield, if I were unfettered. Mr. Jorkins is immovable.’", "조킨스 씨는 꿈쩍도 안 하니까.\""]], [["I was quite dismayed by the idea of this terrible Jorkins.", "나는 이 무시무시한 조킨스라는 사람이라는 생각에 적잖이 기가 죽었다."], ["But I found out afterwards that he was a mild man of a heavy temperament, whose place in the business was to keep himself in the background, and be constantly exhibited by name as the most obdurate and ruthless of men. If a clerk wanted his salary raised, Mr.", "그러나 나중에 알고 보니, 그는 굼뜬 기질의 온순한 사람으로, 이 사업에서 그가 맡은 역할이란 늘 뒤로 물러나 있으면서 가장 고집 세고 무자비한 사람으로 그 이름만 끊임없이 내세워지는 것이었다. 어느 서기가 봉급을 올려 달라고 하면, 조킨스 씨는 그런 제안 따위는 들으려고도 하지 않는다."], ["Jorkins wouldn’t listen to such a proposition. If a client were slow to settle his bill of costs, Mr.", "어느 의뢰인이 소송 비용 계산서를 늑장 부리며 치르지 않으면, 조킨스 씨가 기어이 받아 내고야 만다."], ["Jorkins was resolved to have it paid; and however painful these things might be (and always were) to the feelings of Mr. Spenlow, Mr. Jorkins would have his bond.", "그리고 이런 일들이 스펜로 씨의 마음에 아무리 괴로웠다 한들 (실제로 늘 괴로웠다), 조킨스 씨는 자기 몫의 빚을 끝내 챙긴다."], ["The heart and hand of the good angel Spenlow would have been always open, but for the restraining demon Jorkins.", "착한 천사 스펜로의 마음과 손은 늘 활짝 열려 있었을 테지만, 그것을 가로막는 악마 조킨스가 있었던 것이다."], ["As I have grown older, I think I have had experience of some other houses doing business on the principle of Spenlow and Jorkins!", "나이가 들면서 보니, 스펜로 앤 조킨스의 방식대로 일을 꾸려 가는 다른 사업체들도 여럿 겪어 본 것 같다!"]], [["It was settled that I should begin my month’s probation as soon as I pleased, and that my aunt need neither remain in town nor return at its expiration, as the articles of agreement, of which I was to be the subject, could easily be sent to her at home for her signature. When we had got so far, Mr.", "내가 원하는 대로 곧 한 달간의 시험 기간을 시작하기로 정해졌고, 내가 당사자가 될 계약서는 고모님 댁으로 손쉽게 부쳐 서명을 받을 수 있으니, 고모님은 시내에 더 머물 필요도, 시험 기간이 끝날 때 다시 올 필요도 없게 되었다. 이야기가 여기까지 이르자, 스펜로 씨는 지금 당장 나를 법정으로 데려가 그곳이 어떤 곳인지 보여 주겠다고 했다."], ["Spenlow offered to take me into Court then and there, and show me what sort of place it was.", "나도 알고 싶은 마음이 충분했기에, 우리는 그러려고 밖으로 나섰고, 고모님은 뒤에 남겨 두었다."], ["As I was willing enough to know, we went out with this object, leaving my aunt behind; who would trust herself, she said, in no such place, and who, I think, regarded all Courts of Law as a sort of powder-mills that might blow up at any time.", "고모님은 그런 곳에는 도무지 발을 들여놓을 마음이 없다고 했는데, 내 생각에 고모님은 모든 법정이라는 것을 언제든 터져 버릴 수 있는 일종의 화약 공장쯤으로 여기는 것 같았다."]], [["Mr. Spenlow conducted me through a paved courtyard formed of grave brick houses, which I inferred, from the Doctors’ names upon the doors, to be the official abiding-places of the learned advocates of whom Steerforth had told me; and into a large dull room, not unlike a chapel to my thinking, on the left hand.", "스펜로 씨는 근엄한 벽돌집들로 둘러싸인 포장된 안마당을 지나 나를 안내했는데, 나는 그 문마다 적힌 박사들의 이름으로 미루어, 거기가 스티어포스가 말해 주었던 그 박식한 변호인들의 공식 거처라고 짐작했다. 그러고는 왼편에 있는, 내가 보기에 예배당과 어딘가 비슷한 크고 음침한 방으로 들어갔다."], ["The upper part of this room was fenced off from the rest; and there, on the two sides of a raised platform of the horse-shoe form, sitting on easy old-fashioned dining-room chairs, were sundry gentlemen in red gowns and grey wigs, whom I found to be the Doctors aforesaid.", "이 방의 윗부분은 나머지 공간과 칸막이로 나뉘어 있었는데, 거기 말굽 모양으로 한 단 높인 단상의 양옆으로, 구식의 안락한 식당 의자에 빨간 법복과 회색 가발 차림의 신사 여럿이 앉아 있었으니, 알고 보니 그들이 바로 앞서 말한 박사들이었다."], ["Blinking over a little desk like a pulpit-desk, in the curve of the horse-shoe, was an old gentleman, whom, if I had seen him in an aviary, I should certainly have taken for an owl, but who, I learned, was the presiding judge.", "말굽의 곡선 부분에서, 설교단 책상 같은 작은 책상 너머로 눈을 끔벅거리고 있던 한 노신사는, 내가 만약 새장 안에서 보았더라면 영락없이 올빼미로 여겼을 테지만, 알고 보니 그가 재판을 주재하는 판사였다."], ["In the space within the horse-shoe, lower than these, that is to say, on about the level of the floor, were sundry other gentlemen, of Mr. Spenlow’s rank, and dressed like him in black gowns with white fur upon them, sitting at a long green table.", "말굽 안쪽의, 이들보다 낮은 공간, 그러니까 거의 바닥 높이쯤 되는 자리에는, 스펜로 씨와 같은 신분에다 그처럼 흰 모피를 두른 검은 법복 차림의 다른 신사 여럿이 길쭉한 초록색 탁자에 앉아 있었다. 내 생각에 그들의 넥타이는 대체로 빳빳했고 표정은 거만해 보였다."], ["Their cravats were in general stiff, I thought, and their looks haughty; but in this last respect I presently conceived I had done them an injustice, for when two or three of them had to rise and answer a question of the presiding dignitary, I never saw anything more sheepish.", "그러나 이 마지막 점에 대해서는 곧 내가 그들을 잘못 본 것이라는 생각이 들었으니, 그들 가운데 두세 명이 일어나 재판장의 질문에 답해야 했을 때, 나는 그보다 더 쭈뼛거리는 모습을 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["The public, represented by a boy with a comforter, and a shabby-genteel man secretly eating crumbs out of his coat pockets, was warming itself at a stove in the centre of the Court.", "목도리를 두른 소년 하나와, 슬그머니 외투 주머니에서 빵 부스러기를 꺼내 먹고 있는 점잖은 척하는 초라한 사내 하나로 대표되는 방청객들은, 법정 한가운데 놓인 난로에 몸을 녹이고 있었다."], ["The languid stillness of the place was only broken by the chirping of this fire and by the voice of one of the Doctors, who was wandering slowly through a perfect library of evidence, and stopping to put up, from time to time, at little roadside inns of argument on the journey.", "그곳의 나른한 고요는 오직 이 난롯불이 타닥타닥 지저귀는 소리와, 증거의 완벽한 도서관을 천천히 헤매고 다니다가 이따금 길가의 자그마한 논거 여인숙에 들러 쉬어 가곤 하던 어느 박사의 목소리에만 깨질 뿐이었다."], ["Altogether, I have never, on any occasion, made one at such a cosey, dosey, old-fashioned, time-forgotten, sleepy-headed little family-party in all my life; and I felt it would be quite a soothing opiate to belong to it in any character--except perhaps as a suitor.", "다 통틀어, 나는 평생 그 어느 자리에서도 그토록 아늑하고, 졸리고, 구식이고, 세월에 잊히고, 꾸벅꾸벅 졸음에 겨운 자그마한 한 가족 모임의 일원이 되어 본 적이 없었다. 그래서 나는 — 어쩌면 소송 당사자로서만 빼고 — 어떤 자격으로든 거기에 끼어든다면 그것이야말로 마음을 달래 주는 아편 같은 일이 되리라는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["Very well satisfied with the dreamy nature of this retreat, I informed Mr. Spenlow that I had seen enough for that time, and we rejoined my aunt; in company with whom I presently departed from the Commons, feeling very young when I went out of Spenlow and Jorkins’s, on account of the clerks poking one another with their pens to point me out.", "이 은신처의 꿈결 같은 분위기에 무척 흡족해진 나는, 오늘은 이만하면 충분히 봤다고 스펜로 씨에게 알리고는, 고모님과 다시 합류했다. 그러고는 이내 고모님과 함께 박사회관을 떠났는데, 스펜로 앤 조킨스 사무소를 나설 때 서기들이 펜으로 서로를 콕콕 찌르며 나를 가리키는 통에 나는 무척 어린애가 된 기분이었다."]], [["We arrived at Lincoln’s Inn Fields without any new adventures, except encountering an unlucky donkey in a costermonger’s cart, who suggested painful associations to my aunt.", "우리는 손수레에 매인 운 나쁜 당나귀 한 마리와 마주친 것 말고는 별다른 새 사건 없이 링컨스 인 광장에 다다랐는데, 그 당나귀는 고모님에게 괴로운 연상을 불러일으켰다."], ["We had another long talk about my plans, when we were safely housed; and as I knew she was anxious to get home, and, between fire, food, and pickpockets, could never be considered at her ease for half-an-hour in London, I urged her not to be uncomfortable on my account, but to leave me to take care of myself.", "무사히 숙소에 들고 나서 우리는 내 계획에 대해 다시 한참 이야기를 나누었다. 나는 고모님이 어서 집에 돌아가고 싶어 한다는 것을, 그리고 불과 음식과 소매치기 사이에서 런던에서는 단 반 시간도 마음 편할 새가 없다는 것을 알았기에, 나 때문에 불편해하지 마시고 나를 알아서 잘 살도록 내버려 두시라고 권했다."]], [["‘I have not been here a week tomorrow, without considering that too, my dear,’ she returned.", "\"여기 온 지 내일이면 일주일인데, 나도 그동안 그 점을 생각 안 해 본 게 아니란다, 얘야.\""], ["‘There is a furnished little set of chambers to be let in the Adelphi, Trot, which ought to suit you to a marvel.’", "고모님이 대꾸했다. \"아델파이에 가구 딸린 자그마한 셋방이 하나 나와 있는데, 트롯, 너한테 기막히게 딱 맞을 거야.\""]], [["With this brief introduction, she produced from her pocket an advertisement, carefully cut out of a newspaper, setting forth that in Buckingham Street in the Adelphi there was to be let furnished, with a view of the river, a singularly desirable, and compact set of chambers, forming a genteel residence for a young gentleman, a member of one of the Inns of Court, or otherwise, with immediate possession.", "이렇게 짤막한 운을 떼고는, 고모님은 신문에서 정성껏 오려 낸 광고 한 장을 주머니에서 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 아델파이의 버킹엄 가에, 강이 내려다보이는, 유난히 탐나고 아담한 셋방이 가구 딸린 채로 나왔으며, 법학원의 회원이거나 그 밖의 젊은 신사에게 점잖은 거처가 될 만하고, 즉시 입주가 가능하다고 적혀 있었다."], ["Terms moderate, and could be taken for a month only, if required.", "조건은 적당하며, 필요하다면 한 달만 빌릴 수도 있다고 했다."]], [["‘Why, this is the very thing, aunt!’ said I, flushed with the possible dignity of living in chambers.", "\"아니, 이거야말로 딱이네요, 고모님!\" 셋방에 산다는 데서 오는 그럴듯한 위엄에 들떠 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Then come,’ replied my aunt, immediately resuming the bonnet she had a minute before laid aside.", "\"그럼 가 보자꾸나.\" 고모님이 조금 전 벗어 두었던 보닛을 곧장 다시 쓰며 대답했다."], ["‘We’ll go and look at ‘em.’", "\"가서 한번 보자고.\""]], [["Away we went.", "우리는 곧장 나섰다."], ["The advertisement directed us to apply to Mrs. Crupp on the premises, and we rung the area bell, which we supposed to communicate with Mrs. Crupp.", "광고에는 건물 안에 있는 크럽 부인에게 문의하라고 적혀 있었기에, 우리는 크럽 부인과 통할 것이라 짐작되는 지하층 초인종을 눌렀다."], ["It was not until we had rung three or four times that we could prevail on Mrs. Crupp to communicate with us, but at last she appeared, being a stout lady with a flounce of flannel petticoat below a nankeen gown.", "서너 번이나 누르고 나서야 겨우 크럽 부인을 우리에게 응해 나오게 할 수 있었는데, 마침내 그녀가 나타났으니, 무명천 가운 아래로 플란넬 페티코트 주름 장식을 드러낸 뚱뚱한 부인이었다."]], [["‘Let us see these chambers of yours, if you please, ma’am,’ said my aunt.", "\"괜찮으시면 그 셋방을 좀 보여 주세요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘For this gentleman?’ said Mrs.", "\"이 신사분이 쓰실 건가요?\""], ["Crupp, feeling in her pocket for her keys.", "크럽 부인이 주머니를 더듬어 열쇠를 찾으며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, for my nephew,’ said my aunt.", "\"그래요, 내 조카가 쓸 거예요.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘And a sweet set they is for sich!’ said Mrs. Crupp.", "\"그런 분께야 더없이 안성맞춤인 방들이죠!\" 크럽 부인이 말했다."]], [["So we went upstairs.", "그래서 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["They were on the top of the house--a great point with my aunt, being near the fire-escape--and consisted of a little half-blind entry where you could see hardly anything, a little stone-blind pantry where you could see nothing at all, a sitting-room, and a bedroom.", "방들은 집 꼭대기에 있었는데 — 화재 비상구가 가깝다는 점에서 이것은 고모님에게 큰 미덕이었다 — 거의 아무것도 보이지 않는 반쯤 캄캄한 작은 입구와, 아예 아무것도 안 보이는 깜깜한 작은 곳간, 그리고 거실 하나와 침실 하나로 이루어져 있었다."], ["The furniture was rather faded, but quite good enough for me; and, sure enough, the river was outside the windows.", "가구는 좀 빛이 바랬지만 나에게는 더없이 좋았고, 과연 창밖에는 강이 흐르고 있었다."]], [["As I was delighted with the place, my aunt and Mrs. Crupp withdrew into the pantry to discuss the terms, while I remained on the sitting-room sofa, hardly daring to think it possible that I could be destined to live in such a noble residence.", "내가 그곳이 무척 마음에 들어 하자, 고모님과 크럽 부인은 조건을 흥정하러 곳간으로 물러갔고, 나는 거실 소파에 남아, 내가 이런 으리으리한 거처에 살 운명이 될 수도 있다는 걸 도무지 감히 믿지 못하고 있었다."], ["After a single combat of some duration they returned, and I saw, to my joy, both in Mrs. Crupp’s countenance and in my aunt’s, that the deed was done.", "한참에 걸친 일대일 격전 끝에 두 사람이 돌아왔는데, 나는 기쁘게도 크럽 부인의 얼굴에서도 고모님의 얼굴에서도, 거래가 성사되었다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘Is it the last occupant’s furniture?’ inquired my aunt.", "\"이게 전에 살던 사람의 가구인가요?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, it is, ma’am,’ said Mrs. Crupp.", "\"네, 그렇답니다, 부인.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘What’s become of him?’ asked my aunt.", "\"그 사람은 어떻게 됐어요?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["Mrs. Crupp was taken with a troublesome cough, in the midst of which she articulated with much difficulty.", "크럽 부인은 성가신 기침에 사로잡혔는데, 그 와중에 무척이나 힘겹게 또박또박 말했다. \"그분은 여기서 병이 나셨어요, 부인."], ["‘He was took ill here, ma’am, and--ugh! ugh! ugh!", "그러고는 — 어흠! 어흠! 어흠! 아이고 저런!"], ["dear me!--and he died!’", "— 돌아가셨답니다!\""]], [["‘Hey!", "\"이런!"], ["What did he die of?’ asked my aunt.", "무엇 때문에 죽었어요?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["‘Well, ma’am, he died of drink,’ said Mrs.", "\"그게요, 부인, 술 때문에 돌아가셨어요.\""], ["Crupp, in confidence. ‘And smoke.’", "크럽 부인이 은밀하게 말했다. \"그리고 담배 때문에요.\""]], [["‘Smoke?", "\"담배라고요?"], ["You don’t mean chimneys?’ said my aunt.", "굴뚝을 말하는 건 아니겠지요?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘No, ma’am,’ returned Mrs. Crupp.", "\"아니에요, 부인.\" 크럽 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["‘Cigars and pipes.’", "\"여송연하고 파이프 담배요.\""]], [["‘That’s not catching, Trot, at any rate,’ remarked my aunt, turning to me.", "\"어쨌든 그건 옮는 건 아니로구나, 트롯.\" 고모님이 나를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘No, indeed,’ said I.", "\"네, 그렇고말고요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["In short, my aunt, seeing how enraptured I was with the premises, took them for a month, with leave to remain for twelve months when that time was out. Mrs.", "요컨대 고모님은 내가 그 집에 얼마나 푹 빠져 있는지를 보고는, 한 달간 빌리되 그 기간이 끝나면 열두 달 더 머물러도 좋다는 조건으로 그 방을 잡았다."], ["Crupp was to find linen, and to cook; every other necessary was already provided; and Mrs.", "크럽 부인이 침구를 마련하고 요리를 해 주기로 했고, 그 밖의 필요한 것들은 이미 다 갖춰져 있었다."], ["Crupp expressly intimated that she should always yearn towards me as a son.", "그리고 크럽 부인은 자기가 늘 나를 친아들처럼 여기겠다고 분명히 내비쳤다."], ["I was to take possession the day after tomorrow, and Mrs. Crupp said, thank Heaven she had now found summun she could care for!", "나는 모레 입주하기로 했고, 크럽 부인은 이제야 마음 쓸 누군가를 찾았으니 하늘에 감사한다고 말했다!"]], [["On our way back, my aunt informed me how she confidently trusted that the life I was now to lead would make me firm and self-reliant, which was all I wanted.", "돌아오는 길에 고모님은, 이제부터 내가 살아갈 삶이 나를 굳건하고 자립적인 사람으로 만들어 주리라 굳게 믿는다고, 그것이야말로 내게 필요한 전부라고 일러 주었다."], ["She repeated this several times next day, in the intervals of our arranging for the transmission of my clothes and books from Mr.", "이튿날 우리가 위크필드 씨 댁에서 내 옷가지와 책들을 부쳐 받을 채비를 하는 틈틈이, 고모님은 이 말을 여러 번 되풀이했다."], ["Wickfield’s; relative to which, and to all my late holiday, I wrote a long letter to Agnes, of which my aunt took charge, as she was to leave on the succeeding day.", "그 일과 지난 휴가에 관한 모든 것을 적어, 나는 아그네스에게 긴 편지를 한 통 썼고, 고모님이 이튿날 떠날 예정이었기에 그 편지는 고모님이 맡아 가기로 했다. 이런 자질구레한 이야기를 더 늘어놓지 않으려면, 이것만 덧붙이면 되겠다."], ["Not to lengthen these particulars, I need only add, that she made a handsome provision for all my possible wants during my month of trial; that Steerforth, to my great disappointment and hers too, did not make his appearance before she went away; that I saw her safely seated in the Dover coach, exulting in the coming discomfiture of the vagrant donkeys, with Janet at her side; and that when the coach was gone, I turned my face to the Adelphi, pondering on the old days when I used to roam about its subterranean arches, and on the happy changes which had brought me to the surface.", "고모님은 내 한 달 시험 기간 동안 있을 수 있는 모든 필요에 대비해 넉넉히 돈을 마련해 주었고, 스티어포스는 나에게도 고모님에게도 무척 실망스럽게도 고모님이 떠나기 전에 끝내 나타나지 않았다. 나는 재닛을 곁에 거느리고, 떠돌이 당나귀들을 곧 혼쭐낼 일에 신이 난 고모님이 도버행 마차에 무사히 자리 잡는 것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 마차가 떠나자, 나는 아델파이 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고서, 한때 그곳 지하 아치 사이를 어슬렁거리며 쏘다니던 옛 시절과, 나를 지상으로 끌어올려 준 그 행복한 변화들을 곰곰이 떠올렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER 24. MY FIRST DISSIPATION", "# CHAPTER 24. MY FIRST DISSIPATION"]], [["It was a wonderfully fine thing to have that lofty castle to myself, and to feel, when I shut my outer door, like Robinson Crusoe, when he had got into his fortification, and pulled his ladder up after him.", "그 높다란 성채를 나 혼자 차지하고서, 바깥문을 닫을 때면 마치 로빈슨 크루소가 자기 요새 안으로 들어가 사다리를 끌어 올린 듯한 기분을 느끼는 것은 더할 나위 없이 멋진 일이었다."], ["It was a wonderfully fine thing to walk about town with the key of my house in my pocket, and to know that I could ask any fellow to come home, and make quite sure of its being inconvenient to nobody, if it were not so to me.", "내 집 열쇠를 주머니에 넣고 시내를 거닐며, 누구에게든 우리 집에 오라고 청할 수 있고, 나만 괜찮다면 그 누구에게도 불편을 끼칠 일이 없으리라는 것을 확신하는 것도 더할 나위 없이 멋진 일이었다."], ["It was a wonderfully fine thing to let myself in and out, and to come and go without a word to anyone, and to ring Mrs. Crupp up, gasping, from the depths of the earth, when I wanted her--and when she was disposed to come.", "누구에게도 한마디 없이 마음대로 드나들고 오가며, 크럽 부인이 필요할 때 땅속 저 깊은 곳에서 헐떡이며 올라오도록 — 부인이 올 마음이 내켰을 때 — 종을 울려 부르는 것도 더할 나위 없이 멋진 일이었다."], ["All this, I say, was wonderfully fine; but I must say, too, that there were times when it was very dreary.", "이 모든 것이, 다시 말하건대, 더할 나위 없이 멋졌다. 그러나 솔직히 말하건대, 때로는 몹시 적막한 순간도 있었다."]], [["It was fine in the morning, particularly in the fine mornings.", "아침이면 멋졌고, 특히 화창한 아침이면 더욱 멋졌다."], ["It looked a very fresh, free life, by daylight: still fresher, and more free, by sunlight.", "햇빛 속에서 보면 이 생활은 무척 신선하고 자유로워 보였다. 햇살이 비치면 더 신선하고 더 자유로워 보였다."], ["But as the day declined, the life seemed to go down too.", "그러나 하루가 저물어 가면 그 생활도 함께 가라앉는 듯했다."], ["I don’t know how it was; it seldom looked well by candle-light.", "어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠으나, 촛불 아래에서는 좀처럼 좋아 보이지 않았다."], ["I wanted somebody to talk to, then.", "그때면 함께 이야기 나눌 누군가가 그리웠다."], ["I missed Agnes.", "애그니스가 그리웠다."], ["I found a tremendous blank, in the place of that smiling repository of my confidence. Mrs.", "내 속내를 털어놓던 그 미소 짓는 보금자리가 있던 자리에 어마어마한 빈터가 생긴 것을 느꼈다."], ["Crupp appeared to be a long way off.", "크럽 부인은 까마득히 멀게만 느껴졌다."], ["I thought about my predecessor, who had died of drink and smoke; and I could have wished he had been so good as to live, and not bother me with his decease.", "술과 담배로 죽었다는 내 전임자를 떠올리며, 그 사람이 좀 살아 있어서 죽음 따위로 나를 성가시게 하지나 말았으면 좋았을 텐데 하고 바라기도 했다."]], [["After two days and nights, I felt as if I had lived there for a year, and yet I was not an hour older, but was quite as much tormented by my own youthfulness as ever.", "이틀 밤낮이 지나자 나는 거기서 일 년은 산 것 같은 기분이 들었으나, 정작 한 시간도 더 나이를 먹지 못한 채, 여전히 내 풋내 나는 젊음에 예전 못지않게 시달리고 있었다."]], [["Steerforth not yet appearing, which induced me to apprehend that he must be ill, I left the Commons early on the third day, and walked out to Highgate. Mrs.", "스티어포스가 여태 나타나지 않자 나는 그가 아픈 게 아닐까 걱정스러워, 사흘째 되는 날 일찌감치 민법박사회관을 나와 하이게이트까지 걸어갔다."], ["Steerforth was very glad to see me, and said that he had gone away with one of his Oxford friends to see another who lived near St. Albans, but that she expected him to return tomorrow.", "스티어포스 부인은 나를 보고 무척 반가워하며, 아들이 옥스퍼드 친구 하나와 함께 세인트올번스 근처에 사는 다른 친구를 보러 떠났는데 내일쯤 돌아올 것 같다고 했다."], ["I was so fond of him, that I felt quite jealous of his Oxford friends.", "나는 그를 어찌나 좋아했던지, 그의 옥스퍼드 친구들이 다 질투가 날 지경이었다."]], [["As she pressed me to stay to dinner, I remained, and I believe we talked about nothing but him all day.", "부인이 저녁까지 들고 가라고 권하기에 나는 머물렀고, 우리는 하루 종일 그의 이야기 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았던 것 같다."], ["I told her how much the people liked him at Yarmouth, and what a delightful companion he had been.", "야머스 사람들이 그를 얼마나 좋아했는지, 그가 얼마나 유쾌한 길동무였는지 나는 부인에게 들려주었다."], ["Miss Dartle was full of hints and mysterious questions, but took a great interest in all our proceedings there, and said, ‘Was it really though?’ and so forth, so often, that she got everything out of me she wanted to know.", "다틀 양은 넌지시 던지는 말과 알쏭달쏭한 질문으로 가득했지만, 우리가 거기서 한 일에 큰 관심을 보이며 \"그게 정말 그랬어요?\" 따위의 말을 어찌나 자주 되풀이하던지, 자기가 알고 싶은 것을 죄다 내게서 캐냈다."], ["Her appearance was exactly what I have described it, when I first saw her; but the society of the two ladies was so agreeable, and came so natural to me, that I felt myself falling a little in love with her.", "그녀의 겉모습은 내가 처음 보았을 때 묘사한 그대로였다. 하지만 두 부인과 함께 어울리는 것이 어찌나 즐겁고 자연스럽던지, 나는 그녀에게 조금씩 마음이 끌리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["I could not help thinking, several times in the course of the evening, and particularly when I walked home at night, what delightful company she would be in Buckingham Street.", "그날 저녁 내내, 특히 밤에 집으로 걸어 돌아오면서, 그녀가 버킹엄 거리에 있다면 얼마나 즐거운 벗이 되어 줄까 하는 생각을 몇 번이고 떨칠 수가 없었다."]], [["I was taking my coffee and roll in the morning, before going to the Commons--and I may observe in this place that it is surprising how much coffee Mrs. Crupp used, and how weak it was, considering--when Steerforth himself walked in, to my unbounded joy.", "이튿날 아침, 민법박사회관으로 가기 전에 커피와 빵을 들고 있었는데 — 그런데 여기서 한마디 짚고 넘어가자면, 크럽 부인이 그토록 많은 커피를 쓰면서 정작 그 커피가 얼마나 묽었는지는 놀라울 따름이었다 — 바로 그때 스티어포스가 직접 걸어 들어와, 나는 한없이 기뻤다."]], [["‘My dear Steerforth,’ cried I, ‘I began to think I should never see you again!’", "\"이보게 스티어포스,\" 내가 외쳤다. \"다시는 자네를 못 보는 줄 알았다네!\""]], [["‘I was carried off, by force of arms,’ said Steerforth, ‘the very next morning after I got home.", "\"집에 도착한 바로 다음 날 아침, 완력으로 끌려갔지 뭔가.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["Why, Daisy, what a rare old bachelor you are here!’", "\"이런, 데이지, 여기서 아주 보기 드문 노총각 생활을 하고 있구먼!\""]], [["I showed him over the establishment, not omitting the pantry, with no little pride, and he commended it highly.", "나는 식료품 저장실까지 빼놓지 않고 적잖은 자랑을 담아 집 안을 두루 보여 주었고, 그는 크게 칭찬했다."], ["‘I tell you what, old boy,’ he added, ‘I shall make quite a town-house of this place, unless you give me notice to quit.’", "\"그래, 친구,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"자네가 나가라고 하지 않는 한, 나는 여기를 아주 내 시내 별장으로 삼을 작정일세.\""]], [["This was a delightful hearing.", "이건 듣던 중 반가운 소리였다."], ["I told him if he waited for that, he would have to wait till doomsday.", "나는 그가 그런 통고를 기다린다면 세상이 끝나는 날까지 기다려야 할 거라고 말했다."]], [["‘But you shall have some breakfast!’ said I, with my hand on the bell-rope, ‘and Mrs.", "\"하지만 아침 좀 들게!\" 종 끈에 손을 얹으며 내가 말했다."], ["Crupp shall make you some fresh coffee, and I’ll toast you some bacon in a bachelor’s Dutch-oven, that I have got here.’", "\"크럽 부인더러 신선한 커피를 새로 끓여 달라고 하고, 여기 마련해 둔 노총각용 더치오븐에 베이컨을 구워 주겠네.\""]], [["‘No, no!’ said Steerforth.", "\"아니, 아냐!\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Don’t ring! I can’t!", "\"종은 울리지 말게!"], ["I am going to breakfast with one of these fellows who is at the Piazza Hotel, in Covent Garden.’", "그럴 수 없어! 코번트가든 피아자 호텔에 묵고 있는 친구 하나하고 아침을 먹기로 했거든.\""]], [["‘But you’ll come back to dinner?’ said I.", "\"그럼 저녁은 돌아와서 먹겠지?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I can’t, upon my life.", "\"정말 그럴 수가 없네."], ["There’s nothing I should like better, but I must remain with these two fellows.", "무엇보다 그러고 싶지만, 그 두 친구하고 같이 있어야 해."], ["We are all three off together tomorrow morning.’", "우리 셋은 내일 아침에 함께 떠나거든.\""]], [["‘Then bring them here to dinner,’ I returned.", "\"그럼 그 친구들을 여기로 데려와서 저녁을 먹게.\""], ["‘Do you think they would come?’", "내가 받았다. \"그들이 와 줄 것 같은가?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["they would come fast enough,’ said Steerforth; ‘but we should inconvenience you.", "오라면 냉큼 오겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다. \"하지만 자네한테 폐가 될 거야."], ["You had better come and dine with us somewhere.’", "자네가 우리한테 와서 어디 적당한 데서 같이 먹는 게 낫겠네.\""]], [["I would not by any means consent to this, for it occurred to me that I really ought to have a little house-warming, and that there never could be a better opportunity.", "나는 결코 그 말에 응할 수 없었다. 마침 집들이를 조촐하게나마 해야겠다는 생각이 떠올랐고, 이보다 더 좋은 기회는 없을 듯했기 때문이다."], ["I had a new pride in my rooms after his approval of them, and burned with a desire to develop their utmost resources.", "그가 내 방을 칭찬해 준 뒤로 나는 내 방에 새삼 자부심이 생겼고, 그 방의 역량을 끝까지 펼쳐 보이고 싶은 마음에 활활 타올랐다."], ["I therefore made him promise positively in the names of his two friends, and we appointed six o’clock as the dinner-hour.", "그래서 나는 그에게 두 친구의 이름까지 걸고 꼭 오겠다는 약속을 받아 냈고, 저녁 식사 시각은 여섯 시로 정했다."]], [["When he was gone, I rang for Mrs. Crupp, and acquainted her with my desperate design. Mrs.", "그가 떠난 뒤, 나는 크럽 부인을 종을 울려 불러서 나의 무모한 계획을 알렸다."], ["Crupp said, in the first place, of course it was well known she couldn’t be expected to wait, but she knew a handy young man, who she thought could be prevailed upon to do it, and whose terms would be five shillings, and what I pleased.", "크럽 부인은 먼저, 자기가 시중을 들리라고 기대해선 안 된다는 것은 다들 익히 아는 바라고 운을 떼더니, 그래도 손재주 있는 젊은이 하나를 알고 있는데 잘만 구슬리면 그 일을 맡길 수 있을 것 같고, 그 사람 삯은 오 실링에 내가 기꺼이 얹어 주는 만큼이면 된다고 했다."], ["I said, certainly we would have him.", "나는 당연히 그를 부르자고 했다."], ["Next Mrs. Crupp said it was clear she couldn’t be in two places at once (which I felt to be reasonable), and that ‘a young gal’ stationed in the pantry with a bedroom candle, there never to desist from washing plates, would be indispensable.", "다음으로 크럽 부인은, 자기가 한꺼번에 두 군데에 있을 수 없는 것은 뻔한 노릇이라며(나도 그게 이치에 맞다고 느꼈다), 침실용 촛불을 든 '젊은 계집애' 하나가 식료품 저장실에 붙박여 끊임없이 접시를 닦아 줘야 꼭 필요할 거라고 했다."], ["I said, what would be the expense of this young female? and Mrs. Crupp said she supposed eighteenpence would neither make me nor break me.", "나는 그 젊은 여자애 비용은 얼마나 드느냐고 물었고, 크럽 부인은 일 실링 육 펜스쯤이면 내 살림을 일으키지도 거덜 내지도 않을 거라고 했다."], ["I said I supposed not; and THAT was settled. Then Mrs.", "나는 그렇겠다고 했고, 그 일은 그렇게 정해졌다. 그러자 크럽 부인이 말했다."], ["Crupp said, Now about the dinner.", "자, 이제 저녁 음식 이야기를 하지요."]], [["It was a remarkable instance of want of forethought on the part of the ironmonger who had made Mrs. Crupp’s kitchen fireplace, that it was capable of cooking nothing but chops and mashed potatoes.", "크럽 부인의 부엌 화덕을 만든 철물장이가 얼마나 앞일을 못 내다봤는지를 보여 주는 두드러진 예가 있었으니, 그 화덕으로는 양고기 토막과 으깬 감자 말고는 아무것도 요리할 수 없었다는 것이다."], ["As to a fish-kittle, Mrs. Crupp said, well!", "생선 냄비로 말하자면, 크럽 부인은, 글쎄요!"], ["would I only come and look at the range?", "그냥 와서 화덕을 한번 보시지 그래요, 라고 했다."], ["She couldn’t say fairer than that. Would I come and look at it?", "그보다 더 정직하게는 말 못 한다고 했다. 와서 보시겠어요?"], ["As I should not have been much the wiser if I HAD looked at it, I declined, and said, ‘Never mind fish.’ But Mrs. Crupp said, Don’t say that; oysters was in, why not them?", "내가 화덕을 본들 별로 더 똑똑해질 것도 없으니, 나는 사양하며 \"생선은 신경 쓰지 마세요.\"라고 했다. 그러나 크럽 부인은, 그런 말씀 마세요, 굴이 제철인데 그건 어떠세요, 라고 했다."], ["So THAT was settled. Mrs.", "그래서 그건 그렇게 정해졌다."], ["Crupp then said what she would recommend would be this.", "이어서 크럽 부인은 자기가 권하고 싶은 건 이런 거라고 했다."], ["A pair of hot roast fowls--from the pastry-cook’s; a dish of stewed beef, with vegetables--from the pastry-cook’s; two little corner things, as a raised pie and a dish of kidneys--from the pastrycook’s; a tart, and (if I liked) a shape of jelly--from the pastrycook’s.", "따뜻하게 구운 닭 한 쌍 — 빵집에서. 채소를 곁들인 쇠고기 스튜 한 접시 — 빵집에서. 자그마한 곁들이 두 가지로 두툼한 파이 하나와 콩팥 요리 한 접시 — 빵집에서."], ["This, Mrs. Crupp said, would leave her at full liberty to concentrate her mind on the potatoes, and to serve up the cheese and celery as she could wish to see it done.", "타르트 하나, 그리고 (내가 원한다면) 틀에 굳힌 젤리 하나 — 빵집에서. 이렇게 하면, 크럽 부인 말로는, 자기가 마음 놓고 감자에만 정신을 쏟을 수 있고, 치즈와 셀러리도 바라는 대로 보기 좋게 차려 낼 수 있다는 것이었다."]], [["I acted on Mrs. Crupp’s opinion, and gave the order at the pastry-cook’s myself.", "나는 크럽 부인의 의견대로 움직여, 빵집에 직접 주문을 넣었다."], ["Walking along the Strand, afterwards, and observing a hard mottled substance in the window of a ham and beef shop, which resembled marble, but was labelled ‘Mock Turtle’, I went in and bought a slab of it, which I have since seen reason to believe would have sufficed for fifteen people.", "그러고서 스트랜드 거리를 걷다가, 햄·쇠고기 가게 진열창에서 대리석처럼 보이는 딱딱하고 얼룩덜룩한 덩어리를 발견했는데 '모조 거북 수프'라는 딱지가 붙어 있어서, 나는 안으로 들어가 그 한 덩이를 샀다. 나중에 가서야 그것이 열다섯 명은 너끈히 먹였을 양이라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["This preparation, Mrs. Crupp, after some difficulty, consented to warm up; and it shrunk so much in a liquid state, that we found it what Steerforth called ‘rather a tight fit’ for four.", "크럽 부인은 다소 옥신각신한 끝에 이 음식을 데워 주기로 했는데, 끓는 동안 어찌나 줄어들던지, 스티어포스가 표현한 대로 네 사람이 먹기에는 '좀 빠듯한' 정도가 되고 말았다."]], [["These preparations happily completed, I bought a little dessert in Covent Garden Market, and gave a rather extensive order at a retail wine-merchant’s in that vicinity.", "이 채비를 다행히 마치고, 나는 코번트가든 시장에서 후식거리를 조금 사고, 그 근처 술 소매상에 제법 푸짐한 주문을 넣었다."], ["When I came home in the afternoon, and saw the bottles drawn up in a square on the pantry floor, they looked so numerous (though there were two missing, which made Mrs. Crupp very uncomfortable), that I was absolutely frightened at them.", "오후에 집에 돌아와, 식료품 저장실 바닥에 네모반듯하게 줄지어 늘어선 술병들을 보았을 때, 그것들이 어찌나 많아 보이던지(두 병이 모자라서 크럽 부인이 무척 거북해했지만) 나는 그 앞에서 그야말로 겁이 났다."]], [["One of Steerforth’s friends was named Grainger, and the other Markham.", "스티어포스의 친구 하나는 이름이 그레인저였고, 다른 하나는 마크햄이었다."], ["They were both very gay and lively fellows; Grainger, something older than Steerforth; Markham, youthful-looking, and I should say not more than twenty.", "둘 다 무척 명랑하고 활기찬 친구들이었다. 그레인저는 스티어포스보다 좀 나이가 위였고, 마크햄은 앳돼 보였는데 스무 살이 넘지 않았지 싶었다."], ["I observed that the latter always spoke of himself indefinitely, as ‘a man’, and seldom or never in the first person singular.", "마크햄은 늘 자기 자신을 '한 사내'라고 두루뭉술하게 일컬으며, 일인칭 단수로는 좀처럼, 아니 거의 말하지 않는다는 것을 나는 눈여겨보았다."]], [["‘A man might get on very well here, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Markham--meaning himself.", "\"이런 데서는 한 사내가 아주 잘 지낼 수 있겠는걸요, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 마크햄이 말했다 — 자기를 두고 하는 말이었다."]], [["‘It’s not a bad situation,’ said I, ‘and the rooms are really commodious.’", "\"그리 나쁜 자리는 아니죠.\" 내가 말했다. \"방도 정말 널찍하고요.\""]], [["‘I hope you have both brought appetites with you?’ said Steerforth.", "\"두 분 다 입맛을 단단히 챙겨 오셨길 바라네.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Upon my honour,’ returned Markham, ‘town seems to sharpen a man’s appetite.", "\"맹세코,\" 마크햄이 받았다. \"도시에 오면 한 사내의 입맛이 날카로워지는 모양입디다."], ["A man is hungry all day long.", "한 사내가 하루 종일 배가 고프지요."], ["A man is perpetually eating.’", "한 사내가 쉴 새 없이 먹어 댄다니까요.\""]], [["Being a little embarrassed at first, and feeling much too young to preside, I made Steerforth take the head of the table when dinner was announced, and seated myself opposite to him.", "처음에는 조금 쑥스러웠고, 또 좌장 노릇을 하기에는 내가 너무 어리게 느껴져서, 저녁이 차려졌다는 소리가 들리자 나는 스티어포스를 상석에 앉히고 그 맞은편에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Everything was very good; we did not spare the wine; and he exerted himself so brilliantly to make the thing pass off well, that there was no pause in our festivity.", "모든 것이 아주 훌륭했다. 우리는 술을 아끼지 않았고, 그는 자리를 흥겹게 이끌려고 어찌나 눈부시게 애를 쓰던지 우리의 잔치에는 끊김이라곤 없었다."], ["I was not quite such good company during dinner as I could have wished to be, for my chair was opposite the door, and my attention was distracted by observing that the handy young man went out of the room very often, and that his shadow always presented itself, immediately afterwards, on the wall of the entry, with a bottle at its mouth.", "정작 나는 저녁 내내 내가 바라던 만큼 좋은 길동무가 되지 못했는데, 내 의자가 문 맞은편이었던 탓에, 손재주 있는 젊은이가 방을 자주 들락거리는 것과, 그러고 나면 그 그림자가 어김없이 현관 벽에 술병을 입에 댄 모습으로 나타나는 것을 지켜보느라 정신이 자꾸 흩어졌기 때문이다."], ["The ‘young gal’ likewise occasioned me some uneasiness: not so much by neglecting to wash the plates, as by breaking them.", "'젊은 계집애' 역시 내게 적잖은 불안을 안겼다. 접시 닦는 일을 게을리해서라기보다, 접시를 깨뜨려서였다."], ["For being of an inquisitive disposition, and unable to confine herself (as her positive instructions were) to the pantry, she was constantly peering in at us, and constantly imagining herself detected; in which belief, she several times retired upon the plates (with which she had carefully paved the floor), and did a great deal of destruction.", "그 애는 호기심 많은 성미라, (분명하게 지시받은 대로) 식료품 저장실에만 들어앉아 있지를 못하고, 끊임없이 우리 쪽을 기웃거리다가, 끊임없이 자기가 들킨 줄로만 여겼다. 그렇게 여길 때마다 그 애는 몇 번이고 접시들 위로 (제 손으로 바닥에 고이 깔아 둔 그 접시들 위로) 물러나는 바람에, 어지간히도 많이 깨먹었다."]], [["These, however, were small drawbacks, and easily forgotten when the cloth was cleared, and the dessert put on the table; at which period of the entertainment the handy young man was discovered to be speechless.", "하지만 이런 것들은 사소한 흠이었고, 식탁보가 치워지고 후식이 식탁에 오르자 쉽사리 잊혔다. 그 잔치가 그 대목에 이르렀을 무렵, 손재주 있는 젊은이는 말을 잃은 상태였음이 드러났다."], ["Giving him private directions to seek the society of Mrs. Crupp, and to remove the ‘young gal’ to the basement also, I abandoned myself to enjoyment.", "나는 그에게 크럽 부인과 어울리고, '젊은 계집애'도 함께 지하실로 데려가라고 넌지시 일러둔 뒤, 흥겨움에 나 자신을 내맡겼다."]], [["I began, by being singularly cheerful and light-hearted; all sorts of half-forgotten things to talk about, came rushing into my mind, and made me hold forth in a most unwonted manner.", "나는 유난히 쾌활하고 들뜬 기분으로 시작했다. 반쯤 잊고 있던 온갖 이야깃거리가 머릿속으로 밀려들어, 나는 평소답지 않게 떠벌리게 되었다."], ["I laughed heartily at my own jokes, and everybody else’s; called Steerforth to order for not passing the wine; made several engagements to go to Oxford; announced that I meant to have a dinner-party exactly like that, once a week, until further notice; and madly took so much snuff out of Grainger’s box, that I was obliged to go into the pantry, and have a private fit of sneezing ten minutes long.", "내 농담에도, 남의 농담에도 호탕하게 웃었다. 스티어포스가 술을 안 돌린다고 나무랐고, 옥스퍼드에 가겠다는 약속을 몇 번이나 했으며, 추후 통고가 있을 때까지 일주일에 한 번씩 꼭 이런 저녁 모임을 갖겠노라 선언했다. 그러고는 그레인저의 코담배갑에서 코담배를 어찌나 미친 듯이 집어 들이켰던지, 식료품 저장실로 들어가 십 분 동안 혼자 재채기를 쏟아 내야 했다."]], [["I went on, by passing the wine faster and faster yet, and continually starting up with a corkscrew to open more wine, long before any was needed. I proposed Steerforth’s health.", "나는 계속해서, 점점 더 빨리 술을 돌렸고, 아직 술이 필요해지기도 한참 전부터 코르크 마개뽑이를 들고 연신 벌떡 일어나 새 병을 땄다. 나는 스티어포스의 건강을 위해 건배를 제안했다."], ["I said he was my dearest friend, the protector of my boyhood, and the companion of my prime.", "그가 내 가장 소중한 벗이요, 내 소년 시절의 보호자요, 내 한창때의 길동무라고 했다."], ["I said I was delighted to propose his health.", "그의 건강을 빌게 되어 기쁘다고 했다."], ["I said I owed him more obligations than I could ever repay, and held him in a higher admiration than I could ever express.", "그에게 도저히 갚을 수 없을 만큼 큰 신세를 졌으며, 결코 다 표현할 수 없을 만큼 그를 우러러본다고 했다. 끝으로 나는 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["I finished by saying, ‘I’ll give you Steerforth!", "\"스티어포스를 위하여! 신의 가호가 있기를! 만세!\""], ["God bless him! Hurrah!’ We gave him three times three, and another, and a good one to finish with.", "우리는 그를 위해 세 번 곱하기 세 번, 거기에 한 번 더, 그리고 마무리로 멋들어진 한 번을 외쳤다."], ["I broke my glass in going round the table to shake hands with him, and I said (in two words)", "나는 그와 악수하려고 식탁을 빙 돌다가 잔을 깨뜨리고는 (단숨에 붙여서) 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Steerforth--you’retheguidingstarofmyexistence.’", "\"스티어포스—자넨내인생의길잡이별일세.\""]], [["I went on, by finding suddenly that somebody was in the middle of a song.", "나는 계속해서, 어느새 누군가가 한창 노래를 부르고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Markham was the singer, and he sang ‘When the heart of a man is depressed with care’.", "노래하는 사람은 마크햄이었고, 그는 \"한 사내의 마음이 근심에 짓눌릴 때\"를 불렀다."], ["He said, when he had sung it, he would give us ‘Woman!’ I took objection to that, and I couldn’t allow it.", "다 부르고 나서 그는 이어서 \"여자!\"를 위해 건배하겠다고 했다. 나는 거기에 이의를 달았고, 도저히 허락할 수 없었다."], ["I said it was not a respectful way of proposing the toast, and I would never permit that toast to be drunk in my house otherwise than as ‘The Ladies!’ I was very high with him, mainly I think because I saw Steerforth and Grainger laughing at me--or at him--or at both of us.", "그건 건배를 청하는 점잖은 방식이 아니며, 내 집에서는 그 건배를 결코 그런 식으로 하게 둘 수 없고 오직 \"숙녀분들을 위하여!\"라고만 해야 한다고 했다. 나는 그에게 자못 거세게 굴었는데, 아무래도 스티어포스와 그레인저가 나를 보고 — 아니면 그를 보고 — 아니면 우리 둘 다를 보고 웃는 것이 눈에 들어왔기 때문이지 싶다."], ["He said a man was not to be dictated to.", "그는 한 사내한테 이래라저래라 명령해선 안 된다고 했다."], ["I said a man was. He said a man was not to be insulted, then.", "나는 한 사내한테 명령해도 된다고 했다. 그러자 그는 한 사내를 모욕해선 안 된다고 했다."], ["I said he was right there--never under my roof, where the Lares were sacred, and the laws of hospitality paramount.", "나는 그건 자네 말이 옳다고 했다 — 내 지붕 아래에서는 결코 안 되지, 여기는 집안 수호신이 신성하고 손님 대접의 법도가 무엇보다 으뜸인 곳이니까."], ["He said it was no derogation from a man’s dignity to confess that I was a devilish good fellow. I instantly proposed his health.", "그는 내가 지독하게 좋은 친구라고 인정하는 것이 한 사내의 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일은 아니라고 했다. 나는 그 자리에서 곧장 그의 건강을 위해 건배를 청했다."]], [["Somebody was smoking.", "누군가가 담배를 피우고 있었다."], ["We were all smoking. I was smoking, and trying to suppress a rising tendency to shudder.", "우리는 모두 담배를 피우고 있었다. 나도 담배를 피우면서, 자꾸만 치미는 진저리를 억누르려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Steerforth had made a speech about me, in the course of which I had been affected almost to tears.", "스티어포스가 나에 대해 연설을 했는데, 그 와중에 나는 거의 눈물이 날 만큼 감동했다."], ["I returned thanks, and hoped the present company would dine with me tomorrow, and the day after--each day at five o’clock, that we might enjoy the pleasures of conversation and society through a long evening.", "나는 감사 인사를 전하며, 부디 이 자리에 모인 분들이 내일도, 모레도 — 매일 다섯 시에 — 나와 저녁을 함께하여, 긴긴 밤 내내 대화와 교제의 즐거움을 누릴 수 있기를 바란다고 했다. 나는 누군가 한 사람을 위해 건배를 청해야겠다는 생각이 들었다."], ["I felt called upon to propose an individual.", "우리 고모님을 위해 건배하겠다고 했다."], ["I would give them my aunt. Miss Betsey Trotwood, the best of her sex!", "베시 트롯우드 양, 그 성별 중에서도 으뜸가는 분을 위하여!"]], [["Somebody was leaning out of my bedroom window, refreshing his forehead against the cool stone of the parapet, and feeling the air upon his face.", "누군가가 내 침실 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고, 난간의 차가운 돌에 이마를 식히며, 얼굴에 닿는 바람을 느끼고 있었다."], ["It was myself.", "그건 나였다."], ["I was addressing myself as ‘Copperfield’, and saying, ‘Why did you try to smoke?", "나는 나 자신을 '코퍼필드'라 부르며 이렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["You might have known you couldn’t do it.’ Now, somebody was unsteadily contemplating his features in the looking-glass.", "\"왜 담배를 피우려 들었어? 못 할 줄 알았으면서.\" 이번에는 누군가가 거울 속 제 얼굴을 비틀거리며 들여다보고 있었다."], ["That was I too.", "그것도 나였다."], ["I was very pale in the looking-glass; my eyes had a vacant appearance; and my hair--only my hair, nothing else--looked drunk.", "거울 속의 나는 몹시 창백했고, 두 눈은 멍한 빛을 띠었으며, 머리카락은 — 오직 머리카락만, 다른 건 아무것도 아니고 — 술 취한 것처럼 보였다."]], [["Somebody said to me, ‘Let us go to the theatre, Copperfield!’ There was no bedroom before me, but again the jingling table covered with glasses; the lamp; Grainger on my right hand, Markham on my left, and Steerforth opposite--all sitting in a mist, and a long way off.", "누군가가 내게 말했다. \"극장에 가세, 코퍼필드!\" 내 앞에는 침실이 아니라, 다시 술잔들이 잔뜩 놓여 짤랑거리는 식탁이 있었다."], ["The theatre? To be sure. The very thing.", "램프, 내 오른쪽의 그레인저, 왼쪽의 마크햄, 그리고 맞은편의 스티어포스 — 다들 안개 속에 까마득히 멀리 앉아 있었다. 극장?"], ["Come along!", "그래, 좋고말고."], ["But they must excuse me if I saw everybody out first, and turned the lamp off--in case of fire.", "그게 딱이지. 가세! 하지만 다들 먼저 내보내고, 램프를 끄고 나서 — 불이 날까 봐 — 가는 건 양해해 주게."]], [["Owing to some confusion in the dark, the door was gone.", "어둠 속에서 무언가 헷갈리는 바람에 문이 사라져 버렸다."], ["I was feeling for it in the window-curtains, when Steerforth, laughing, took me by the arm and led me out.", "창문 커튼 속을 더듬어 문을 찾고 있는데, 스티어포스가 웃으며 내 팔을 잡고 밖으로 이끌어 주었다."], ["We went downstairs, one behind another.", "우리는 한 사람씩 차례로 계단을 내려갔다."], ["Near the bottom, somebody fell, and rolled down.", "거의 바닥에 다다랐을 때, 누군가가 넘어져 데굴데굴 굴러 떨어졌다."], ["Somebody else said it was Copperfield.", "또 다른 누군가가 그건 코퍼필드라고 했다."], ["I was angry at that false report, until, finding myself on my back in the passage, I began to think there might be some foundation for it.", "나는 그 헛소문에 화가 났는데, 정작 내가 복도에서 벌렁 나자빠진 꼴을 깨닫고 나서야, 거기에 일말의 근거가 있을지도 모르겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["A very foggy night, with great rings round the lamps in the streets!", "지독하게 안개 낀 밤, 거리의 가로등마다 커다란 무리가 둘러져 있었다!"], ["There was an indistinct talk of its being wet.", "비가 온다는 둥 하는 어렴풋한 말소리가 들렸다."], ["I considered it frosty.", "나는 서리가 내린 거라고 여겼다."], ["Steerforth dusted me under a lamp-post, and put my hat into shape, which somebody produced from somewhere in a most extraordinary manner, for I hadn’t had it on before.", "스티어포스가 가로등 기둥 아래에서 내 옷을 털어 주고, 어디선가 누군가가 참으로 기묘하게 꺼내 온 내 모자를 매만져 모양을 잡아 주었다 — 나는 아까 모자를 쓰고 있지도 않았으니 말이다."], ["Steerforth then said, ‘You are all right, Copperfield, are you not?’ and I told him, ‘Neverberrer.’", "그러고서 스티어포스가 말했다. \"괜찮은 거지, 코퍼필드, 그렇지?\" 나는 그에게 \"이보다조올수없네.\"라고 했다."]], [["A man, sitting in a pigeon-hole-place, looked out of the fog, and took money from somebody, inquiring if I was one of the gentlemen paid for, and appearing rather doubtful (as I remember in the glimpse I had of him) whether to take the money for me or not.", "비둘기집 같은 칸막이 안에 앉은 한 사내가 안개 너머로 내다보며 누군가에게서 돈을 받더니, 내가 입장료를 대신 내 준 신사들 중 하나냐고 물었고, (얼핏 본 기억으로는) 내 몫의 돈을 받아야 할지 말지 자못 미심쩍어하는 눈치였다."], ["Shortly afterwards, we were very high up in a very hot theatre, looking down into a large pit, that seemed to me to smoke; the people with whom it was crammed were so indistinct.", "곧이어 우리는 어느 몹시 더운 극장의 아주 높다란 곳에 자리해, 내 눈에는 연기가 피어오르는 듯한 커다란 일층 평석을 내려다보고 있었다. 그 자리를 빼곡히 메운 사람들이 어찌나 흐릿하던지."], ["There was a great stage, too, looking very clean and smooth after the streets; and there were people upon it, talking about something or other, but not at all intelligibly.", "커다란 무대도 있었는데, 거리에 비하면 무척 깨끗하고 매끈해 보였다. 그 위에는 사람들이 있어서 무언가에 대해 떠들고 있었으나 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다."], ["There was an abundance of bright lights, and there was music, and there were ladies down in the boxes, and I don’t know what more.", "환한 불빛이 넘쳐 났고, 음악이 흘렀고, 아래쪽 특별석에는 숙녀들이 있었으며, 그 밖에 무엇이 더 있었는지는 모르겠다."], ["The whole building looked to me as if it were learning to swim; it conducted itself in such an unaccountable manner, when I tried to steady it.", "건물 전체가 마치 헤엄을 배우고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 내가 그걸 바로잡아 보려 할 때마다 도무지 설명할 수 없는 방식으로 출렁거렸기 때문이다."]], [["On somebody’s motion, we resolved to go downstairs to the dress-boxes, where the ladies were.", "누군가의 제안에 따라, 우리는 아래층 특별석으로 내려가기로 했다. 거기엔 숙녀들이 있었다."], ["A gentleman lounging, full dressed, on a sofa, with an opera-glass in his hand, passed before my view, and also my own figure at full length in a glass.", "정장을 차려입은 한 신사가 손에 오페라글라스를 들고 소파에 비스듬히 기대 있는 모습이 내 시야를 스쳐 지나갔고, 거울에 비친 내 모습도 온몸이 통째로 지나갔다."], ["Then I was being ushered into one of these boxes, and found myself saying something as I sat down, and people about me crying ‘Silence!’ to somebody, and ladies casting indignant glances at me, and--what!", "그러고 나서 나는 그 특별석 중 하나로 안내되어 들어갔는데, 자리에 앉으며 무슨 말인가를 하고 있는 나 자신을 깨달았고, 주위 사람들이 누군가를 향해 \"조용히!\"라고 외쳤으며, 숙녀들이 나를 향해 분개한 눈길을 던졌고, 그리고 — 아니! 정말로!"], ["yes!--Agnes, sitting on the seat before me, in the same box, with a lady and gentleman beside her, whom I didn’t know.", "— 애그니스가 내 앞자리에, 같은 특별석에, 내가 모르는 숙녀와 신사를 양옆에 두고 앉아 있었다."], ["I see her face now, better than I did then, I dare say, with its indelible look of regret and wonder turned upon me.", "그 얼굴을 나는 지금 그때보다 더 또렷이 본다, 감히 말하건대, 후회와 놀라움이 지워지지 않을 듯이 새겨진 채 나를 향한 그 얼굴을."]], [["‘Agnes!’ I said, thickly, ‘Lorblessmer!", "\"애그니스!\" 내가 혀 꼬부라진 소리로 말했다. \"하느님맙소사!"], ["Agnes!’", "애그니스!\""]], [["‘Hush! Pray!’ she answered, I could not conceive why.", "\"쉿! 제발!\" 그녀가 대답했는데, 나는 왜 그러는지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["‘You disturb the company.", "\"사람들한테 방해가 돼요."], ["Look at the stage!’", "무대를 봐요!\""]], [["I tried, on her injunction, to fix it, and to hear something of what was going on there, but quite in vain.", "그녀의 분부에 따라 나는 무대에 시선을 고정하고 거기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 좀 들어 보려 했으나, 전혀 헛수고였다."], ["I looked at her again by and by, and saw her shrink into her corner, and put her gloved hand to her forehead.", "이윽고 나는 다시 그녀를 보았고, 그녀가 제 자리 구석으로 움츠러들며 장갑 낀 손을 이마에 가져다 대는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Agnes!’ I said.", "\"애그니스!\""], ["‘I’mafraidyou’renorwell.’", "내가 말했다. \"어디아픈거아니에요.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes. Do not mind me, Trotwood,’ she returned.", "\"아니에요, 괜찮아요. 제 걱정은 말아요, 트롯우드.\""], ["‘Listen!", "그녀가 받았다."], ["Are you going away soon?’", "\"들어 봐요! 곧 가실 거죠?\""]], [["‘Amigoarawaysoo?’ I repeated.", "\"내가곧가냐고?\" 내가 되뇌었다."]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"네.\""]], [["I had a stupid intention of replying that I was going to wait, to hand her downstairs.", "나는 그녀를 아래층까지 바래다주려고 기다리겠다고 대답하려는 어리석은 마음을 품었다."], ["I suppose I expressed it, somehow; for after she had looked at me attentively for a little while, she appeared to understand, and replied in a low tone:", "어떻게든 그 뜻을 내비쳤던 모양이다. 그녀가 한동안 나를 찬찬히 들여다보더니, 알아들은 듯 낮은 목소리로 대답했기 때문이다."]], [["‘I know you will do as I ask you, if I tell you I am very earnest in it.", "\"내가 정말 진심으로 부탁한다고 말하면, 당신이 내 말대로 해 주리란 걸 알아요."], ["Go away now, Trotwood, for my sake, and ask your friends to take you home.’", "지금 가요, 트롯우드, 날 봐서라도, 그리고 친구들한테 집까지 데려다 달라고 해요.\""]], [["She had so far improved me, for the time, that though I was angry with her, I felt ashamed, and with a short ‘Goori!’ (which I intended for ‘Good night!’) got up and went away.", "그녀는 그 순간만큼은 나를 어지간히 사람답게 돌려놓아서, 나는 그녀에게 화가 났으면서도 부끄러움을 느꼈고, 짤막하게 \"나잘가요!\"(나는 \"안녕히 주무세요!\"라고 할 작정이었다) 하고는 일어나 자리를 떴다."], ["They followed, and I stepped at once out of the box-door into my bedroom, where only Steerforth was with me, helping me to undress, and where I was by turns telling him that Agnes was my sister, and adjuring him to bring the corkscrew, that I might open another bottle of wine.", "친구들이 뒤따라왔고, 나는 단숨에 특별석 문을 나서서 내 침실로 들어섰는데, 거기엔 스티어포스만이 나와 함께 있으면서 내가 옷 벗는 것을 거들고 있었고, 나는 그에게 애그니스가 내 누이라고 번갈아 일러 가며, 또 한 병 술을 따게 코르크 마개뽑이를 가져오라고 다그치고 있었다."]], [["How somebody, lying in my bed, lay saying and doing all this over again, at cross purposes, in a feverish dream all night--the bed a rocking sea that was never still!", "내 침대에 누운 누군가가, 밤새도록 열에 들뜬 꿈속에서, 앞뒤도 안 맞게, 이 모든 것을 어떻게 거듭 말하고 또 거듭 행하던지 — 침대는 잠시도 잠잠하지 않고 출렁이는 바다였다!"], ["How, as that somebody slowly settled down into myself, did I begin to parch, and feel as if my outer covering of skin were a hard board; my tongue the bottom of an empty kettle, furred with long service, and burning up over a slow fire; the palms of my hands, hot plates of metal which no ice could cool!", "그 누군가가 어떻게 천천히 나 자신으로 가라앉던지, 나는 목이 바싹바싹 타들어 가며, 살갗의 겉껍질은 딱딱한 널빤지가 되고, 혀는 오랜 세월 부려 먹혀 더께가 잔뜩 앉은 빈 주전자 밑바닥이 되어 약한 불 위에서 타들어 가는 듯했고, 두 손바닥은 그 어떤 얼음으로도 식힐 수 없는 뜨겁게 달군 쇳덩이 같았다!"]], [["But the agony of mind, the remorse, and shame I felt when I became conscious next day!", "그러나 이튿날 정신이 들었을 때 느낀 그 마음의 고통, 그 회한, 그 부끄러움이라니!"], ["My horror of having committed a thousand offences I had forgotten, and which nothing could ever expiate--my recollection of that indelible look which Agnes had given me--the torturing impossibility of communicating with her, not knowing, Beast that I was, how she came to be in London, or where she stayed--my disgust of the very sight of the room where the revel had been held--my racking head--the smell of smoke, the sight of glasses, the impossibility of going out, or even getting up! Oh, what a day it was!", "까맣게 잊어버린 데다 무엇으로도 결코 씻어 낼 수 없을 천 가지 잘못을 저질렀다는 끔찍함 — 애그니스가 내게 던졌던 그 지워지지 않을 눈길의 기억 — 짐승만도 못한 내가, 그녀가 어쩌다 런던에 왔는지, 어디에 묵는지조차 알지 못한 채, 그녀와 연락할 길이 도무지 없다는 괴로움 — 그 흥청거림이 벌어졌던 방의 꼴을 보는 것만으로도 치미는 역겨움 — 깨질 듯한 머리 — 담배 냄새, 술잔들의 광경, 밖에 나가기는커녕 일어날 수조차 없는 막막함! 오, 그 얼마나 끔찍한 하루였던가!"]], [["Oh, what an evening, when I sat down by my fire to a basin of mutton broth, dimpled all over with fat, and thought I was going the way of my predecessor, and should succeed to his dismal story as well as to his chambers, and had half a mind to rush express to Dover and reveal all!", "오, 그 얼마나 끔찍한 저녁이었던가. 난롯가에 앉아, 기름이 둥둥 떠 자글거리는 양고기 국물 한 그릇을 앞에 놓고, 내가 전임자의 전철을 밟아 가고 있구나, 그의 방뿐 아니라 그 음울한 신세까지 그대로 물려받겠구나 생각하며, 당장 도버로 달려가 죄다 털어놓고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 들던 그 저녁이!"], ["What an evening, when Mrs.", "그 얼마나 끔찍한 저녁이었던가."], ["Crupp, coming in to take away the broth-basin, produced one kidney on a cheese-plate as the entire remains of yesterday’s feast, and I was really inclined to fall upon her nankeen breast and say, in heartfelt penitence, ‘Oh, Mrs. Crupp, Mrs.", "크럽 부인이 국그릇을 치우러 들어와, 어제 잔치의 잔해라고는 통틀어 치즈 접시에 콩팥 하나만을 달랑 내놓았을 때, 나는 정말이지 그녀의 무명 앞가슴에 매달려, 진심 어린 뉘우침으로 이렇게 말하고 싶을 지경이었다. \"오, 크럽 부인, 크럽 부인, 깨진 고기 따위는 신경 쓰지 마세요!"], ["Crupp, never mind the broken meats!", "저는 너무나 비참합니다!\""], ["I am very miserable!’--only that I doubted, even at that pass, if Mrs. Crupp were quite the sort of woman to confide in!", "— 다만 그 지경에 이르러서도, 크럽 부인이 과연 속내를 털어놓을 만한 그런 부류의 여자일지는 미덥지가 않았을 뿐이다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 25. GOOD AND BAD ANGELS", "# CHAPTER 25. GOOD AND BAD ANGELS"]], [["I was going out at my door on the morning after that deplorable day of headache, sickness, and repentance, with an odd confusion in my mind relative to the date of my dinner-party, as if a body of Titans had taken an enormous lever and pushed the day before yesterday some months back, when I saw a ticket-porter coming upstairs, with a letter in his hand.", "두통과 구역질과 후회로 점철된 그 한심한 날 이튿날 아침, 나는 내가 연 저녁 만찬의 날짜를 두고 머릿속이 묘하게 뒤엉킨 채로 문을 나서던 참이었다. 마치 어떤 거인 무리가 거대한 지렛대를 들고 그저께를 몇 달이나 뒤로 밀쳐 버리기라도 한 것 같은 느낌이었다. 그때 짐꾼 한 사람이 손에 편지를 들고 계단을 올라오는 것이 보였다."], ["He was taking his time about his errand, then; but when he saw me on the top of the staircase, looking at him over the banisters, he swung into a trot, and came up panting as if he had run himself into a state of exhaustion.", "그는 그때까지 심부름을 느긋하게 처리하던 중이었다. 그러나 내가 계단 맨 위에서 난간 너머로 자기를 내려다보는 것을 보자, 그는 종종걸음으로 후다닥 올라오더니, 마치 스스로 녹초가 될 만큼 내달리기라도 한 듯 헐떡이며 다가왔다."]], [["‘T. Copperfield, Esquire,’ said the ticket-porter, touching his hat with his little cane.", "\"코퍼필드 귀하.\" 짐꾼이 작은 지팡이로 모자를 톡 건드리며 말했다."]], [["I could scarcely lay claim to the name: I was so disturbed by the conviction that the letter came from Agnes. However, I told him I was T.", "나는 그 이름의 주인이라고 선뜻 나서기 어려웠다. 그 편지가 아그네스에게서 온 것이라는 확신에 몹시 마음이 흔들렸기 때문이다."], ["Copperfield, Esquire, and he believed it, and gave me the letter, which he said required an answer.", "그래도 나는 내가 코퍼필드 귀하가 맞다고 말했고, 그는 그 말을 믿고 편지를 건네면서 답장이 필요하다고 했다."], ["I shut him out on the landing to wait for the answer, and went into my chambers again, in such a nervous state that I was fain to lay the letter down on my breakfast table, and familiarize myself with the outside of it a little, before I could resolve to break the seal.", "나는 그를 층계참에 내보내 답장을 기다리게 한 다음 다시 방으로 들어왔는데, 어찌나 신경이 곤두섰던지 봉인을 뜯기로 마음먹기 전에 우선 편지를 아침 식탁 위에 내려놓고 겉봉부터 한참 들여다보며 익숙해질 필요가 있었다."]], [["I found, when I did open it, that it was a very kind note, containing no reference to my condition at the theatre.", "마침내 편지를 펼쳐 보니, 그것은 극장에서의 내 꼴에 대해서는 한마디도 적혀 있지 않은 무척 다정한 쪽지였다."], ["All it said was, ‘My dear Trotwood.", "거기에는 이렇게만 적혀 있었다."], ["I am staying at the house of papa’s agent, Mr. Waterbrook, in Ely Place, Holborn.", "\"사랑하는 트롯우드에게. 저는 지금 홀번 일라이 플레이스에 있는 아버지의 대리인 워터브룩 씨 댁에 머물고 있어요."], ["Will you come and see me today, at any time you like to appoint?", "오늘 편할 때 아무 때나 시간을 정해서 저를 보러 와 주실래요?"], ["Ever yours affectionately, AGNES.’", "언제나 다정한 마음으로, 아그네스.\""]], [["It took me such a long time to write an answer at all to my satisfaction, that I don’t know what the ticket-porter can have thought, unless he thought I was learning to write.", "답장을 내 마음에 들게 쓰는 데 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 그 짐꾼이 무슨 생각을 했을지 모르겠다. 내가 글씨 쓰기를 배우는 중이라고 여겼다면 또 모를까."], ["I must have written half-a-dozen answers at least.", "적어도 답장을 대여섯 통은 썼을 것이다."], ["I began one, ‘How can I ever hope, my dear Agnes, to efface from your remembrance the disgusting impression’--there I didn’t like it, and then I tore it up.", "한 통은 이렇게 시작했다. \"사랑하는 아그네스, 내가 어찌 그 역겨운 인상을 당신 기억에서 지우기를 바랄 수—\" 거기서 마음에 들지 않아 찢어 버렸다."], ["I began another, ‘Shakespeare has observed, my dear Agnes, how strange it is that a man should put an enemy into his mouth’--that reminded me of Markham, and it got no farther.", "또 한 통은 이렇게 시작했다. \"사랑하는 아그네스, 셰익스피어가 말하기를, 사람이 제 입에 적을 집어넣는다는 것이 얼마나 이상한 일이냐고—\" 그건 마컴이 떠올라서 더 나아가지 못했다."], ["I even tried poetry.", "심지어 시까지 시도해 보았다."], ["I began one note, in a six-syllable line, ‘Oh, do not remember’--but that associated itself with the fifth of November, and became an absurdity.", "여섯 음절짜리 시행으로 한 통을 시작했다. \"오, 부디 기억하지 마오—\" 그러나 그것이 십일월 오일과 엮이는 바람에 우스꽝스러워지고 말았다."], ["After many attempts, I wrote, ‘My dear Agnes.", "여러 번 애쓴 끝에 나는 이렇게 썼다."], ["Your letter is like you, and what could I say of it that would be higher praise than that? I will come at four o’clock.", "\"사랑하는 아그네스. 당신의 편지는 당신을 꼭 닮았으니, 그보다 더 큰 칭찬이 어디 있어 그것에 대해 무슨 말을 더하겠소?"], ["Affectionately and sorrowfully, T.C.’ With this missive (which I was in twenty minds at once about recalling, as soon as it was out of my hands), the ticket-porter at last departed.", "네 시에 가겠소. 다정하고도 슬픈 마음으로, 트롯우드 코퍼필드.\" 이 편지를 들고 (손에서 떠나기가 무섭게 도로 거두어들이고 싶은 마음이 한꺼번에 스무 갈래나 들었다) 짐꾼은 마침내 떠났다."]], [["If the day were half as tremendous to any other professional gentleman in Doctors’ Commons as it was to me, I sincerely believe he made some expiation for his share in that rotten old ecclesiastical cheese.", "만일 그날이 박사회관의 다른 어느 전문직 신사에게도 나에게 그랬던 것의 절반만큼이라도 끔찍한 하루였다면, 나는 그 사람이 그 케케묵은 교회법 치즈 조각에서 자기가 맡은 몫에 대해 어느 정도 속죄를 했으리라고 진심으로 믿는다."], ["Although I left the office at half past three, and was prowling about the place of appointment within a few minutes afterwards, the appointed time was exceeded by a full quarter of an hour, according to the clock of St. Andrew’s, Holborn, before I could muster up sufficient desperation to pull the private bell-handle let into the left-hand door-post of Mr. Waterbrook’s house.", "나는 세 시 반에 사무실을 나와 몇 분 만에 약속 장소 근처를 어슬렁거렸으나, 홀번 세인트 앤드루 교회 시계로 따져 약속 시각이 꼬박 십오 분이나 지난 뒤에야 비로소, 워터브룩 씨 댁 왼쪽 문기둥에 박힌 사삿집 초인종 손잡이를 잡아당길 만큼 충분한 결심을 끌어모을 수 있었다."]], [["The professional business of Mr. Waterbrook’s establishment was done on the ground-floor, and the genteel business (of which there was a good deal) in the upper part of the building.", "워터브룩 씨 집안의 업무 가운데 사무적인 일은 일 층에서, 점잖은 사교 행사는 (이것도 꽤 많았다) 건물 위층에서 이루어졌다."], ["I was shown into a pretty but rather close drawing-room, and there sat Agnes, netting a purse.", "나는 예쁘지만 다소 갑갑한 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그곳에 아그네스가 앉아 지갑을 뜨고 있었다."]], [["She looked so quiet and good, and reminded me so strongly of my airy fresh school days at Canterbury, and the sodden, smoky, stupid wretch I had been the other night, that, nobody being by, I yielded to my self-reproach and shame, and--in short, made a fool of myself.", "그녀는 어찌나 차분하고 선해 보이던지, 캔터베리에서의 산뜻하고 가벼웠던 학창 시절과, 며칠 전 밤의 후줄근하고 술 냄새에 절어 멍청하던 가련한 내 꼴을 어찌나 강하게 떠올리게 하던지, 곁에 아무도 없는 터라 나는 자책과 수치심에 그만 무너지고 말았다. 한마디로 바보짓을 한 셈이다."], ["I cannot deny that I shed tears.", "눈물을 흘렸다는 것을 부정할 수 없다."], ["To this hour I am undecided whether it was upon the whole the wisest thing I could have done, or the most ridiculous.", "지금 이 순간까지도 나는 그것이 통틀어 내가 할 수 있었던 가장 현명한 일이었는지, 아니면 가장 우스꽝스러운 짓이었는지 결정하지 못했다."]], [["‘If it had been anyone but you, Agnes,’ said I, turning away my head, ‘I should not have minded it half so much.", "\"당신 말고 다른 누가 봤더라면, 아그네스,\" 나는 고개를 돌리며 말했다. \"이렇게까지 마음 쓰이지는 않았을 거예요."], ["But that it should have been you who saw me!", "그런데 하필 그걸 본 사람이 당신이라니!"], ["I almost wish I had been dead, first.’", "차라리 그 전에 죽어 버렸으면 싶을 지경이에요.\""]], [["She put her hand--its touch was like no other hand--upon my arm for a moment; and I felt so befriended and comforted, that I could not help moving it to my lips, and gratefully kissing it.", "그녀는 한순간 손을 내 팔에 얹었는데 — 그 손길은 어떤 다른 손과도 같지 않았다 — 나는 어찌나 위로받고 의지가 되었던지, 그 손을 입술로 가져가 고맙게 입 맞추지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘Sit down,’ said Agnes, cheerfully.", "\"앉으세요.\" 아그네스가 밝게 말했다."], ["‘Don’t be unhappy, Trotwood.", "\"괴로워하지 마세요, 트롯우드."], ["If you cannot confidently trust me, whom will you trust?’", "저를 마음 놓고 믿지 못하면, 대체 누구를 믿겠어요?\""]], [["‘Ah, Agnes!’ I returned.", "\"아, 아그네스!\" 나는 받아쳤다."], ["‘You are my good Angel!’", "\"당신은 나의 선한 천사예요!\""]], [["She smiled rather sadly, I thought, and shook her head.", "그녀는 내가 보기에 다소 슬프게 미소 지으며 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘Yes, Agnes, my good Angel!", "\"그래요, 아그네스, 나의 선한 천사!"], ["Always my good Angel!’", "언제나 나의 선한 천사예요!\""]], [["‘If I were, indeed, Trotwood,’ she returned, ‘there is one thing that I should set my heart on very much.’", "\"제가 정말 그렇다면, 트롯우드,\" 그녀가 받아 말했다. \"제가 꼭 마음에 두고 싶은 일이 하나 있어요.\""]], [["I looked at her inquiringly; but already with a foreknowledge of her meaning.", "나는 묻듯이 그녀를 바라보았다. 하지만 이미 그녀의 뜻을 미리 짐작하고 있었다."]], [["‘On warning you,’ said Agnes, with a steady glance, ‘against your bad Angel.’", "\"당신의 악한 천사를 조심하라고,\" 아그네스가 흔들림 없는 눈길로 말했다. \"당신에게 일러 주는 일이에요.\""]], [["‘My dear Agnes,’ I began, ‘if you mean Steerforth--’", "\"사랑하는 아그네스,\" 나는 입을 열었다. \"스티어포스를 두고 하는 말이라면—\""]], [["‘I do, Trotwood,’ she returned.", "\"맞아요, 트롯우드.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Then, Agnes, you wrong him very much.", "\"그렇다면, 아그네스, 당신은 그를 크게 오해하고 있어요."], ["He my bad Angel, or anyone’s! He, anything but a guide, a support, and a friend to me!", "그가 내 악한 천사라니, 아니 그 누구의 악한 천사라니요! 그는 나에게 길잡이요 버팀목이요 벗일 뿐, 결코 그런 사람이 아니에요!"], ["My dear Agnes!", "사랑하는 아그네스!"], ["Now, is it not unjust, and unlike you, to judge him from what you saw of me the other night?’", "자, 며칠 전 밤 당신이 본 내 꼴만으로 그를 판단하는 건 부당하고, 당신답지도 않은 일 아닌가요?\""]], [["‘I do not judge him from what I saw of you the other night,’ she quietly replied.", "\"며칠 전 밤 제가 본 당신 꼴만으로 그를 판단하는 게 아니에요.\" 그녀가 조용히 대답했다."]], [["‘From what, then?’", "\"그럼 뭘로요?\""]], [["‘From many things--trifles in themselves, but they do not seem to me to be so, when they are put together.", "\"여러 가지로요 — 그 하나하나는 사소한 것들이지만, 한데 모아 놓으면 저에게는 그렇게 사소해 보이지 않아요."], ["I judge him, partly from your account of him, Trotwood, and your character, and the influence he has over you.’", "저는 그를, 한편으로는 당신이 그에 대해 들려준 이야기로, 트롯우드, 그리고 당신의 성품과, 그가 당신에게 미치는 영향으로 판단하는 거예요.\""]], [["There was always something in her modest voice that seemed to touch a chord within me, answering to that sound alone.", "그녀의 조심스러운 목소리에는 언제나 내 안의 어떤 현을, 오직 그 소리에만 응답하는 현을 건드리는 무언가가 있었다."], ["It was always earnest; but when it was very earnest, as it was now, there was a thrill in it that quite subdued me.", "그 목소리는 늘 진지했는데, 지금처럼 몹시 진지할 때면 그 안에 나를 완전히 굴복시키는 떨림이 있었다."], ["I sat looking at her as she cast her eyes down on her work; I sat seeming still to listen to her; and Steerforth, in spite of all my attachment to him, darkened in that tone.", "나는 그녀가 뜨개질에 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 있는 것을 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 나는 여전히 그녀의 말에 귀 기울이는 듯한 모습으로 앉아 있었다. 그리고 스티어포스는, 그에 대한 내 온 애정에도 불구하고, 그 어조 속에서 빛을 잃어 갔다."]], [["‘It is very bold in me,’ said Agnes, looking up again, ‘who have lived in such seclusion, and can know so little of the world, to give you my advice so confidently, or even to have this strong opinion.", "\"이렇게 외따로 살아온 데다 세상을 너무도 모르는 제가, 당신에게 이토록 자신 있게 충고를 한다든지, 심지어 이렇게 강한 의견을 갖는다든지 하는 것은 무척 당돌한 짓이지요.\" 아그네스가 다시 눈을 들며 말했다."], ["But I know in what it is engendered, Trotwood,--in how true a remembrance of our having grown up together, and in how true an interest in all relating to you.", "\"하지만 저는 그것이 어디서 비롯되었는지 알아요, 트롯우드 — 우리가 함께 자라 왔다는 그토록 참된 기억에서, 그리고 당신에 관한 모든 일에 대한 그토록 참된 관심에서 비롯된 거예요."], ["It is that which makes me bold.", "그것이 저를 당돌하게 만들어요."], ["I am certain that what I say is right.", "저는 제 말이 옳다고 확신해요."], ["I am quite sure it is.", "정말이지 확신해요."], ["I feel as if it were someone else speaking to you, and not I, when I caution you that you have made a dangerous friend.’", "당신이 위험한 친구를 사귀었다고 일러 줄 때면, 마치 제가 아니라 다른 누군가가 당신에게 말하고 있는 듯한 느낌마저 들어요.\""]], [["Again I looked at her, again I listened to her after she was silent, and again his image, though it was still fixed in my heart, darkened.", "나는 다시 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀가 입을 다문 뒤에도 다시 그녀의 말에 귀 기울였다. 그리고 그의 모습은, 아직 내 가슴에 단단히 박혀 있었으되, 다시 빛을 잃어 갔다."]], [["‘I am not so unreasonable as to expect,’ said Agnes, resuming her usual tone, after a little while, ‘that you will, or that you can, at once, change any sentiment that has become a conviction to you; least of all a sentiment that is rooted in your trusting disposition.", "\"당신이 이미 굳은 신념이 되어 버린 어떤 감정을, 그것도 당장에 바꾸리라거나 바꿀 수 있으리라고 기대할 만큼 제가 분별없지는 않아요.\" 잠시 뒤 아그네스가 평소 어조로 돌아오며 말했다. \"더구나 당신의 그 사람을 잘 믿는 성품에 뿌리내린 감정이라면 더더욱요."], ["You ought not hastily to do that.", "그걸 성급하게 바꿔서도 안 되고요."], ["I only ask you, Trotwood, if you ever think of me--I mean,’ with a quiet smile, for I was going to interrupt her, and she knew why, ‘as often as you think of me--to think of what I have said.", "저는 그저 부탁하는 거예요, 트롯우드, 혹시라도 저를 생각하거든 — 그러니까,\" 그녀가 조용히 미소 지으며 말했다. 내가 끼어들려 하는 것을, 그리고 그 까닭을 그녀가 알았기 때문이다."], ["Do you forgive me for all this?’", "\"저를 생각할 때마다, 제가 한 말을 떠올려 달라고요. 이 모든 것을 용서해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘I will forgive you, Agnes,’ I replied, ‘when you come to do Steerforth justice, and to like him as well as I do.’", "\"용서할게요, 아그네스.\" 나는 대답했다. \"당신이 스티어포스를 공정하게 대하고, 나만큼 그를 좋아하게 되면요.\""]], [["‘Not until then?’ said Agnes.", "\"그때까지는 안 되나요?\" 아그네스가 말했다."]], [["I saw a passing shadow on her face when I made this mention of him, but she returned my smile, and we were again as unreserved in our mutual confidence as of old.", "내가 그를 입에 올렸을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 스쳐 가는 그림자가 보였다. 그러나 그녀는 내 미소에 미소로 답했고, 우리는 다시 옛날처럼 서로에게 거리낌 없이 속내를 터놓는 사이가 되었다."]], [["‘And when, Agnes,’ said I, ‘will you forgive me the other night?’", "\"그러면 아그네스,\" 내가 말했다. \"며칠 전 밤 일은 언제 용서해 주려고요?\""]], [["‘When I recall it,’ said Agnes.", "\"그 일을 다시 떠올리게 될 때요.\" 아그네스가 말했다."]], [["She would have dismissed the subject so, but I was too full of it to allow that, and insisted on telling her how it happened that I had disgraced myself, and what chain of accidental circumstances had had the theatre for its final link.", "그녀는 그렇게 화제를 접으려 했으나, 나는 그 일이 너무도 마음에 가득 차 그냥 넘길 수 없었다. 그래서 어쩌다 내가 그렇게 망신스러운 꼴이 되었는지, 어떤 우연한 사정들이 사슬처럼 이어져 그 마지막 고리가 극장이 되었는지를 굳이 그녀에게 털어놓았다."], ["It was a great relief to me to do this, and to enlarge on the obligation that I owed to Steerforth for his care of me when I was unable to take care of myself.", "이렇게 이야기하는 것이, 그리고 내가 스스로를 돌보지 못할 때 나를 보살펴 준 스티어포스에게 진 신세를 길게 늘어놓는 것이 나에게는 크나큰 위안이 되었다."]], [["‘You must not forget,’ said Agnes, calmly changing the conversation as soon as I had concluded, ‘that you are always to tell me, not only when you fall into trouble, but when you fall in love.", "\"잊으면 안 돼요.\" 내 이야기가 끝나자마자 아그네스가 차분하게 화제를 바꾸며 말했다. \"곤경에 빠질 때뿐 아니라 사랑에 빠질 때도 저에게 꼭 말해 줘야 한다는 걸요."], ["Who has succeeded to Miss Larkins, Trotwood?’", "라킨스 양 다음 자리는 누가 차지했나요, 트롯우드?\""]], [["‘No one, Agnes.’", "\"아무도 없어요, 아그네스.\""]], [["‘Someone, Trotwood,’ said Agnes, laughing, and holding up her finger.", "\"누군가 있어요, 트롯우드.\" 아그네스가 웃으며 손가락을 들어 보였다."]], [["‘No, Agnes, upon my word!", "\"아니에요, 아그네스, 정말로요!"], ["There is a lady, certainly, at Mrs. Steerforth’s house, who is very clever, and whom I like to talk to--Miss Dartle--but I don’t adore her.’", "스티어포스 부인 댁에 무척 똑똑한 부인이 한 분 있긴 해요. 이야기 나누기를 좋아하는 분이지요 — 다틀 양이라고요 — 하지만 그녀를 흠모하는 건 아니에요.\""]], [["Agnes laughed again at her own penetration, and told me that if I were faithful to her in my confidence she thought she should keep a little register of my violent attachments, with the date, duration, and termination of each, like the table of the reigns of the kings and queens, in the History of England.", "아그네스는 자기 통찰력에 다시 한번 웃으며, 만일 내가 속내를 털어놓는 데 변함이 없다면 내 격렬한 애정 사건들을 하나하나 기록한 작은 명부를 만들어 두어야겠다고, 영국사에 나오는 왕과 여왕의 재위표처럼 각 사건의 날짜와 지속 기간과 종결을 적어 두겠노라고 말했다."], ["Then she asked me if I had seen Uriah.", "그러더니 우라이어를 보았느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Uriah Heep?’ said I. ‘No.", "\"우라이어 힙이요?\" 내가 말했다. \"아뇨."], ["Is he in London?’", "그가 런던에 있어요?\""]], [["‘He comes to the office downstairs, every day,’ returned Agnes.", "\"매일 아래층 사무실로 와요.\" 아그네스가 대답했다."], ["‘He was in London a week before me.", "\"저보다 일주일 먼저 런던에 와 있었어요."], ["I am afraid on disagreeable business, Trotwood.’", "안타깝게도 달갑잖은 일 때문인 듯해요, 트롯우드.\""]], [["‘On some business that makes you uneasy, Agnes, I see,’ said I.", "\"당신을 불안하게 하는 어떤 일 때문이군요, 아그네스. 알겠어요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘What can that be?’", "\"대체 무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["Agnes laid aside her work, and replied, folding her hands upon one another, and looking pensively at me out of those beautiful soft eyes of hers:", "아그네스는 뜨개질감을 옆으로 치우더니, 두 손을 포개고 그 아름답고 부드러운 눈으로 나를 수심에 잠긴 듯 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["‘I believe he is going to enter into partnership with papa.’", "\"그가 아버지와 동업자가 되려는 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘What? Uriah?", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["That mean, fawning fellow, worm himself into such promotion!’ I cried, indignantly.", "우라이어가요? 그 비굴하게 아첨이나 떠는 자가, 그런 자리에까지 슬금슬금 기어 올라온다고요!\" 나는 분개하여 외쳤다."], ["‘Have you made no remonstrance about it, Agnes?", "\"그 일에 아무런 항의도 하지 않았어요, 아그네스?"], ["Consider what a connexion it is likely to be.", "그게 어떤 관계가 될지 생각해 봐요."], ["You must speak out.", "분명하게 말해야 해요."], ["You must not allow your father to take such a mad step.", "아버님이 그런 미친 짓을 하시도록 내버려 두면 안 돼요."], ["You must prevent it, Agnes, while there’s time.’", "막아야 해요, 아그네스, 아직 늦지 않았을 때.\""]], [["Still looking at me, Agnes shook her head while I was speaking, with a faint smile at my warmth: and then replied:", "내가 말하는 동안에도 아그네스는 여전히 나를 바라보며, 내 격앙된 모습에 옅은 미소를 띤 채 고개를 저었다. 그러고는 대답했다."]], [["‘You remember our last conversation about papa?", "\"아버지에 관한 우리의 지난번 대화를 기억하세요?"], ["It was not long after that--not more than two or three days--when he gave me the first intimation of what I tell you.", "그로부터 오래지 않아 — 이삼일도 채 안 되어 — 아버지가 지금 제가 당신에게 말하는 일을 처음으로 넌지시 비치셨어요."], ["It was sad to see him struggling between his desire to represent it to me as a matter of choice on his part, and his inability to conceal that it was forced upon him. I felt very sorry.’", "그것을 마치 당신 스스로 택한 일인 양 저에게 보이고 싶어 하는 마음과, 그것이 떠밀려서 하는 일임을 숨기지 못하는 처지 사이에서 괴로워하시는 모습을 보는 것은 슬펐어요. 저는 무척 마음이 아팠어요.\""]], [["‘Forced upon him, Agnes!", "\"떠밀려서라니, 아그네스!"], ["Who forces it upon him?’", "누가 아버님을 떠미는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Uriah,’ she replied, after a moment’s hesitation, ‘has made himself indispensable to papa.", "\"우라이어가,\" 그녀가 잠시 머뭇거리다 대답했다. \"아버지에게 없어서는 안 될 존재가 되어 버렸어요."], ["He is subtle and watchful.", "그는 교묘하고 빈틈이 없어요."], ["He has mastered papa’s weaknesses, fostered them, and taken advantage of them, until--to say all that I mean in a word, Trotwood,--until papa is afraid of him.’", "아버지의 약점을 속속들이 파악하고, 그것을 키우고, 그것을 이용해서, 마침내 — 제가 하고 싶은 말을 한마디로 다 하자면, 트롯우드 — 마침내 아버지가 그를 두려워하게 만들었어요.\""]], [["There was more that she might have said; more that she knew, or that she suspected; I clearly saw.", "그녀가 더 할 수 있었을 말이, 그녀가 알거나 의심하고 있는 일이 더 있다는 것을 나는 똑똑히 알 수 있었다."], ["I could not give her pain by asking what it was, for I knew that she withheld it from me, to spare her father.", "그것이 무엇이냐고 물어 그녀를 아프게 할 수는 없었다. 그녀가 아버지를 감싸려고 그것을 나에게 숨기고 있다는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["It had long been going on to this, I was sensible: yes, I could not but feel, on the least reflection, that it had been going on to this for a long time.", "일이 오래도록 이 지경으로 흘러왔다는 것을 나는 어렴풋이 깨달았다. 그렇다, 조금만 곱씹어 봐도, 일이 오래전부터 이 지경으로 흘러왔다는 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["I remained silent.", "나는 잠자코 있었다."]], [["‘His ascendancy over papa,’ said Agnes, ‘is very great.", "\"그가 아버지에게 휘두르는 위세가 대단해요.\" 아그네스가 말했다."], ["He professes humility and gratitude--with truth, perhaps: I hope so--but his position is really one of power, and I fear he makes a hard use of his power.’", "\"그는 겸손과 감사를 입에 달고 살아요 — 어쩌면 진심으로요. 그러기를 바라요 — 하지만 그의 처지는 사실상 권력을 쥔 자리이고, 저는 그가 그 권력을 모질게 휘두를까 두려워요.\""]], [["I said he was a hound, which, at the moment, was a great satisfaction to me.", "나는 그가 개 같은 놈이라고 말했는데, 그 순간만큼은 그 말이 나에게 크나큰 만족감을 주었다."]], [["‘At the time I speak of, as the time when papa spoke to me,’ pursued Agnes, ‘he had told papa that he was going away; that he was very sorry, and unwilling to leave, but that he had better prospects. Papa was very much depressed then, and more bowed down by care than ever you or I have seen him; but he seemed relieved by this expedient of the partnership, though at the same time he seemed hurt by it and ashamed of it.’", "\"제가 말하는 그때, 그러니까 아버지가 저에게 그 얘기를 꺼낸 그때,\" 아그네스가 말을 이었다. \"그는 아버지에게 자기가 떠나겠다고 말했대요. 무척 미안하고 떠나기 싫지만, 더 나은 자리가 생겼다고요. 그때 아버지는 몹시 풀이 죽으셨고, 당신이나 제가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 근심에 짓눌려 계셨어요. 그런데 이 동업이라는 방편으로 한시름 놓으신 것 같았어요. 동시에 그것 때문에 상처받고 부끄러워하시는 것도 같았지만요.\""]], [["‘And how did you receive it, Agnes?’", "\"그래서 당신은 그걸 어떻게 받아들였어요, 아그네스?\""]], [["‘I did, Trotwood,’ she replied, ‘what I hope was right.", "\"저는, 트롯우드,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"옳은 일이기를 바라며 그렇게 했어요."], ["Feeling sure that it was necessary for papa’s peace that the sacrifice should be made, I entreated him to make it.", "아버지의 마음의 평화를 위해 그 희생이 꼭 필요하다고 확신했기에, 저는 아버지께 그렇게 하시라고 간청했어요."], ["I said it would lighten the load of his life--I hope it will!--and that it would give me increased opportunities of being his companion.", "그게 아버지 인생의 짐을 덜어 줄 거라고요 — 부디 그러기를 바라요! — 그리고 그러면 제가 아버지의 동무가 되어 드릴 기회도 더 많아질 거라고요. 오, 트롯우드!\""], ["Oh, Trotwood!’ cried Agnes, putting her hands before her face, as her tears started on it, ‘I almost feel as if I had been papa’s enemy, instead of his loving child.", "아그네스가 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 외쳤다. 눈물이 솟구쳐 그 위로 흘렀다. \"저는 아버지의 사랑하는 딸이기는커녕 아버지의 원수였던 게 아닌가 싶기까지 해요."], ["For I know how he has altered, in his devotion to me.", "저를 향한 헌신 속에서 아버지가 어떻게 변해 가셨는지 저는 아니까요."], ["I know how he has narrowed the circle of his sympathies and duties, in the concentration of his whole mind upon me.", "아버지가 온 마음을 저에게만 쏟느라 당신의 동정심과 의무의 테두리를 얼마나 좁혀 버리셨는지 저는 아니까요."], ["I know what a multitude of things he has shut out for my sake, and how his anxious thoughts of me have shadowed his life, and weakened his strength and energy, by turning them always upon one idea.", "저 때문에 아버지가 얼마나 많은 것들을 밀쳐 내 버리셨는지, 그리고 저를 향한 그 노심초사가 어떻게 아버지의 삶에 그늘을 드리우고, 모든 기력을 늘 한 가지 생각에만 돌리게 하여 그 힘과 기운을 어떻게 약하게 만들었는지 저는 아니까요."], ["If I could ever set this right!", "제가 이걸 바로잡을 수만 있다면!"], ["If I could ever work out his restoration, as I have so innocently been the cause of his decline!’", "제가 그토록 철없이 아버지의 쇠락의 원인이 되었듯이, 제가 아버지의 회복을 이뤄 낼 수만 있다면!\""]], [["I had never before seen Agnes cry.", "나는 아그네스가 우는 것을 그전까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["I had seen tears in her eyes when I had brought new honours home from school, and I had seen them there when we last spoke about her father, and I had seen her turn her gentle head aside when we took leave of one another; but I had never seen her grieve like this.", "학교에서 새로운 영예를 안고 집으로 돌아왔을 때 그녀의 눈에 어린 눈물을 본 적은 있었고, 우리가 마지막으로 그녀의 아버지에 관해 이야기했을 때도 그 눈에 눈물이 어린 것을 본 적이 있었으며, 우리가 작별할 때 그녀가 그 다소곳한 고개를 옆으로 돌리는 것을 본 적은 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 이렇게 슬퍼하는 것은 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["It made me so sorry that I could only say, in a foolish, helpless manner, ‘Pray, Agnes, don’t!", "그 모습에 어찌나 마음이 아프던지, 나는 그저 어리석고 속수무책인 투로 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없었다. \"제발, 아그네스, 그러지 마요!"], ["Don’t, my dear sister!’", "그러지 마요, 사랑하는 누이!\""]], [["But Agnes was too superior to me in character and purpose, as I know well now, whatever I might know or not know then, to be long in need of my entreaties.", "그러나 아그네스는 — 그때 내가 무엇을 알았든 몰랐든, 지금은 잘 아는 바이지만 — 성품과 마음가짐에서 나보다 너무도 뛰어난 사람이라, 내 간청을 오래 필요로 하지 않았다."], ["The beautiful, calm manner, which makes her so different in my remembrance from everybody else, came back again, as if a cloud had passed from a serene sky.", "내 기억 속에서 그녀를 다른 누구와도 그토록 다르게 만드는 그 아름답고 차분한 태도가, 마치 맑은 하늘에서 구름 한 점이 지나가듯, 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["‘We are not likely to remain alone much longer,’ said Agnes, ‘and while I have an opportunity, let me earnestly entreat you, Trotwood, to be friendly to Uriah.", "\"우리가 더 오래 단둘이 있을 것 같지는 않으니,\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"기회가 있는 동안에 간곡히 부탁할게요, 트롯우드."], ["Don’t repel him.", "우라이어에게 친절하게 대해 주세요."], ["Don’t resent (as I think you have a general disposition to do) what may be uncongenial to you in him.", "그를 매몰차게 밀어내지 마세요. 그에게 당신과 맞지 않는 점이 있더라도 (당신이 대체로 그러는 성향이 있다고 저는 생각하는데) 그걸 못마땅해하지 마세요."], ["He may not deserve it, for we know no certain ill of him.", "그가 그런 대접을 받아 마땅하지 않을 수도 있어요. 우리는 그에 대해 확실히 나쁜 점을 아는 게 없으니까요."], ["In any case, think first of papa and me!’", "어느 경우든, 무엇보다 먼저 아버지와 저를 생각해 주세요!\""]], [["Agnes had no time to say more, for the room door opened, and Mrs.", "아그네스는 더 말할 겨를이 없었다."], ["Waterbrook, who was a large lady--or who wore a large dress: I don’t exactly know which, for I don’t know which was dress and which was lady--came sailing in.", "방문이 열리며, 덩치가 큰 부인이 — 아니, 큰 드레스를 입은 부인이라고 해야 할까. 어느 쪽이 드레스고 어느 쪽이 부인인지 분간이 안 되니 정확히는 모르겠다 — 미끄러지듯 들어왔기 때문이다."], ["I had a dim recollection of having seen her at the theatre, as if I had seen her in a pale magic lantern; but she appeared to remember me perfectly, and still to suspect me of being in a state of intoxication.", "나는 그녀를 극장에서 본 듯한 어렴풋한 기억이 있었는데, 마치 흐릿한 환등기 속에서 본 것 같은 느낌이었다. 그러나 그녀는 나를 완벽하게 기억하는 듯했고, 여전히 내가 취해 있는 상태라고 의심하는 듯했다."]], [["Finding by degrees, however, that I was sober, and (I hope) that I was a modest young gentleman, Mrs. Waterbrook softened towards me considerably, and inquired, firstly, if I went much into the parks, and secondly, if I went much into society.", "그러나 차츰 내가 멀쩡하다는 것을, 그리고 (그러기를 바라거니와) 내가 점잖은 청년이라는 것을 알게 되자, 워터브룩 부인은 나를 향해 한결 누그러졌다. 그녀는 우선 내가 공원에 자주 나가느냐고, 다음으로 사교계에 자주 드나드느냐고 물었다."], ["On my replying to both these questions in the negative, it occurred to me that I fell again in her good opinion; but she concealed the fact gracefully, and invited me to dinner next day.", "이 두 질문에 다 아니라고 대답하자, 나는 그녀의 호감을 다시 잃은 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그러나 그녀는 그 사실을 우아하게 감추고는, 이튿날 만찬에 나를 초대했다."], ["I accepted the invitation, and took my leave, making a call on Uriah in the office as I went out, and leaving a card for him in his absence.", "나는 초대를 받아들이고 작별을 고했는데, 나가는 길에 사무실로 우라이어를 찾아갔다가 그가 없어서 명함을 한 장 남겨 두었다."]], [["When I went to dinner next day, and on the street door being opened, plunged into a vapour-bath of haunch of mutton, I divined that I was not the only guest, for I immediately identified the ticket-porter in disguise, assisting the family servant, and waiting at the foot of the stairs to carry up my name.", "이튿날 만찬에 갔다가 현관문이 열리는 순간 양 다리 고기에서 피어오르는 김의 한증탕 속으로 곤두박질치면서, 나는 내가 유일한 손님이 아니라는 것을 알아챘다. 변장한 그 짐꾼이 집안 하인을 거들며 계단 아래에서 내 이름을 받아 위층에 알리려고 기다리고 있는 것을 단번에 알아보았기 때문이다."], ["He looked, to the best of his ability, when he asked me for it confidentially, as if he had never seen me before; but well did I know him, and well did he know me.", "그는 은밀히 내 이름을 물으며, 마치 나를 한 번도 본 적이 없다는 듯 제 딴에는 능청을 떨었다. 그러나 나는 그를 똑똑히 알아보았고, 그도 나를 똑똑히 알아보았다."], ["Conscience made cowards of us both.", "양심이 우리 둘 다를 겁쟁이로 만든 셈이었다."]], [["I found Mr. Waterbrook to be a middle-aged gentleman, with a short throat, and a good deal of shirt-collar, who only wanted a black nose to be the portrait of a pug-dog.", "워터브룩 씨는 목이 짧고 셔츠 깃이 유난히 큰 중년 신사였는데, 코끝만 까맣다면 영락없이 퍼그 개의 초상화였다. 그는 나와 알게 된 영광을 누리게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다."], ["He told me he was happy to have the honour of making my acquaintance; and when I had paid my homage to Mrs. Waterbrook, presented me, with much ceremony, to a very awful lady in a black velvet dress, and a great black velvet hat, whom I remember as looking like a near relation of Hamlet’s--say his aunt.", "그리고 내가 워터브룩 부인에게 인사를 올리고 나자, 그는 자못 격식을 차려 나를 검은 벨벳 드레스에 큼직한 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 무척 위압적인 부인에게 소개했는데, 내 기억에 그녀는 햄릿의 가까운 친척, 이를테면 고모처럼 보였다."]], [["Mrs. Henry Spiker was this lady’s name; and her husband was there too: so cold a man, that his head, instead of being grey, seemed to be sprinkled with hoar-frost.", "이 부인의 이름은 헨리 스파이커 부인이었고, 그녀의 남편도 그 자리에 있었다. 어찌나 차가운 사람인지, 그의 머리가 희끗한 게 아니라 마치 서리가 뿌려진 것처럼 보일 지경이었다."], ["Immense deference was shown to the Henry Spikers, male and female; which Agnes told me was on account of Mr. Henry Spiker being solicitor to something or to somebody, I forget what or which, remotely connected with the Treasury.", "헨리 스파이커 내외에게는 남자에게나 여자에게나 엄청난 경의가 표해졌다. 아그네스가 알려 준 바에 따르면, 그것은 헨리 스파이커 씨가 무언가의, 아니 누군가의 변호사라서인데, 그게 무엇인지 누구인지는 잊었지만 어쨌든 재무성과 멀찍이 연줄이 닿아 있기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["I found Uriah Heep among the company, in a suit of black, and in deep humility.", "손님들 가운데 우라이어 힙도 끼어 있었는데, 검은 양복 차림에 깊은 겸손에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He told me, when I shook hands with him, that he was proud to be noticed by me, and that he really felt obliged to me for my condescension.", "내가 그와 악수할 때 그는, 나에게 눈여겨봐 주심을 받아 영광이며, 내가 베풀어 준 황송한 친절에 정말로 고맙다고 말했다."], ["I could have wished he had been less obliged to me, for he hovered about me in his gratitude all the rest of the evening; and whenever I said a word to Agnes, was sure, with his shadowless eyes and cadaverous face, to be looking gauntly down upon us from behind.", "차라리 그가 나에게 덜 고마워했더라면 좋았을 것을, 그날 저녁 내내 그는 감사에 겨워 내 주위를 맴돌았다. 그리고 내가 아그네스에게 한마디라도 할라치면, 영락없이 그 그림자 없는 두 눈과 송장 같은 얼굴로 등 뒤에서 우리를 음산하게 내려다보고 있었다."]], [["There were other guests--all iced for the occasion, as it struck me, like the wine.", "다른 손님들도 있었는데 — 내가 보기엔 죄다 이 자리를 위해 포도주처럼 차갑게 얼려 놓은 것 같았다."], ["But there was one who attracted my attention before he came in, on account of my hearing him announced as Mr.", "그러나 그중 한 사람은 방에 들어오기도 전부터 내 주의를 끌었으니, 트래들스 씨라고 호명되는 소리를 들었기 때문이다!"], ["Traddles! My mind flew back to Salem House; and could it be Tommy, I thought, who used to draw the skeletons!", "내 머릿속은 단숨에 세일럼 하우스로 날아갔다. 혹시 그 해골 그림을 그리던 토미가 아닐까, 하고 나는 생각했다!"]], [["I looked for Mr. Traddles with unusual interest.", "나는 평소와 다른 관심을 품고 트래들스 씨를 찾았다."], ["He was a sober, steady-looking young man of retiring manners, with a comic head of hair, and eyes that were rather wide open; and he got into an obscure corner so soon, that I had some difficulty in making him out.", "그는 점잖고 차분해 보이는, 수줍은 태도의 청년이었는데, 머리카락이 우스꽝스럽게 뻗쳐 있고 두 눈은 다소 휘둥그렇게 뜨여 있었다. 그가 어찌나 잽싸게 후미진 구석으로 들어가 버렸던지, 나는 그를 알아보기가 조금 어려웠다."], ["At length I had a good view of him, and either my vision deceived me, or it was the old unfortunate Tommy.", "마침내 그를 제대로 볼 수 있었는데, 내 눈이 나를 속인 게 아니라면 그는 영락없이 그 가엾던 옛날의 토미였다."]], [["I made my way to Mr. Waterbrook, and said, that I believed I had the pleasure of seeing an old schoolfellow there.", "나는 워터브룩 씨에게 다가가, 옛 학교 친구를 만나는 기쁨을 누리게 된 것 같다고 말했다."]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Mr. Waterbrook, surprised.", "\"그래요!\" 워터브룩 씨가 놀라며 말했다."], ["‘You are too young to have been at school with Mr. Henry Spiker?’", "\"헨리 스파이커 씨와 학교를 같이 다녔다기에는 너무 젊으신데요?\""]], [["‘Oh, I don’t mean him!’ I returned.", "\"오, 그분 말이 아니에요!\""], ["‘I mean the gentleman named Traddles.’", "나는 받아쳤다. \"트래들스라는 분 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Oh! Aye, aye!", "\"오! 아, 아!"], ["Indeed!’ said my host, with much diminished interest.", "그렇군요!\" 주인장은 흥미가 한결 시들해진 투로 말했다."], ["‘Possibly.’", "\"그럴 수도 있겠네요.\""]], [["‘If it’s really the same person,’ said I, glancing towards him, ‘it was at a place called Salem House where we were together, and he was an excellent fellow.’", "\"정말 같은 사람이라면,\" 나는 그를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"우리는 세일럼 하우스라는 곳에서 같이 지냈는데, 그는 정말 좋은 친구였어요.\""]], [["‘Oh yes.", "\"오 그래요."], ["Traddles is a good fellow,’ returned my host nodding his head with an air of toleration.", "트래들스는 좋은 친구지요.\" 주인장은 너그러이 봐준다는 투로 고개를 끄덕이며 받아쳤다."], ["‘Traddles is quite a good fellow.’", "\"트래들스는 꽤 괜찮은 친구예요.\""]], [["‘It’s a curious coincidence,’ said I.", "\"참 묘한 우연이네요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It is really,’ returned my host, ‘quite a coincidence, that Traddles should be here at all: as Traddles was only invited this morning, when the place at table, intended to be occupied by Mrs.", "\"정말이지,\" 주인장이 받아쳤다. \"트래들스가 여기 와 있다는 것부터가 꽤나 우연이지요."], ["Henry Spiker’s brother, became vacant, in consequence of his indisposition.", "트래들스는 오늘 아침에야 초대받았거든요. 헨리 스파이커 부인의 남동생이 앉기로 되어 있던 자리가 그분이 몸이 편찮은 바람에 비게 되어서요."], ["A very gentlemanly man, Mrs. Henry Spiker’s brother, Mr. Copperfield.’", "무척 신사다운 분이지요, 헨리 스파이커 부인의 남동생 말입니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\""]], [["I murmured an assent, which was full of feeling, considering that I knew nothing at all about him; and I inquired what Mr. Traddles was by profession.", "나는 그에 대해 아무것도 모르면서도 자못 정감 어린 동의를 우물거렸다. 그러고는 트래들스 씨가 직업이 무엇이냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Traddles,’ returned Mr.", "\"트래들스는,\" 워터브룩 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["Waterbrook, ‘is a young man reading for the bar. Yes.", "\"법정 변호사가 되려고 공부하는 청년이에요. 네."], ["He is quite a good fellow--nobody’s enemy but his own.’", "꽤 괜찮은 친구지요 — 남에게는 적이 없고 제 자신한테나 적인.\""]], [["‘Is he his own enemy?’ said I, sorry to hear this.", "\"그가 제 자신의 적이라고요?\" 나는 그 말이 안타까워 물었다."]], [["‘Well,’ returned Mr.", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["Waterbrook, pursing up his mouth, and playing with his watch-chain, in a comfortable, prosperous sort of way.", "워터브룩 씨가 입을 오므리고는 편안하고 풍족한 태도로 회중시곗줄을 만지작거리며 받아쳤다."], ["‘I should say he was one of those men who stand in their own light.", "\"내가 보기엔 제 앞길을 제가 막는 부류의 사람이에요."], ["Yes, I should say he would never, for example, be worth five hundred pound.", "네, 가령 그는 결코 오백 파운드의 값어치는 못 될 사람이라고 봅니다."], ["Traddles was recommended to me by a professional friend.", "트래들스는 어느 동업자 친구의 소개로 알게 됐어요."], ["Oh yes. Yes. He has a kind of talent for drawing briefs, and stating a case in writing, plainly.", "오 그래요. 네. 소장을 작성하고, 사건을 글로 또박또박 정리하는 데에는 일종의 재주가 있지요."], ["I am able to throw something in Traddles’s way, in the course of the year; something--for him--considerable. Oh yes. Yes.’", "일 년에 걸쳐 트래들스에게 일거리를 더러 던져 줄 수 있는데, 그에게는 — 꽤 짭짤한 거지요. 오 그래요. 네.\""]], [["I was much impressed by the extremely comfortable and satisfied manner in which Mr. Waterbrook delivered himself of this little word ‘Yes’, every now and then.", "워터브룩 씨가 이따금 이 짤막한 '네'라는 한마디를 내뱉을 때의 그 더없이 편안하고 흡족한 태도에 나는 깊은 인상을 받았다."], ["There was wonderful expression in it. It completely conveyed the idea of a man who had been born, not to say with a silver spoon, but with a scaling-ladder, and had gone on mounting all the heights of life one after another, until now he looked, from the top of the fortifications, with the eye of a philosopher and a patron, on the people down in the trenches.", "그 안에는 놀라운 표현력이 담겨 있었다. 그것은, 은수저까지는 아니더라도 공성 사다리를 입에 물고 태어나, 인생의 온갖 높은 곳을 하나씩 차례로 기어 올라, 이제는 요새 꼭대기에서 철학자이자 후원자의 눈으로 저 아래 참호 속의 사람들을 굽어보는 사람의 모습을 고스란히 전달했다."]], [["My reflections on this theme were still in progress when dinner was announced. Mr.", "이 주제에 대한 내 사색이 아직 진행 중일 때 만찬이 알려졌다."], ["Waterbrook went down with Hamlet’s aunt. Mr.", "워터브룩 씨는 햄릿의 고모를 모시고 내려갔다."], ["Henry Spiker took Mrs. Waterbrook.", "헨리 스파이커 씨는 워터브룩 부인을 데려갔다."], ["Agnes, whom I should have liked to take myself, was given to a simpering fellow with weak legs.", "내가 직접 데려가고 싶었던 아그네스는, 다리가 부실하고 실실 웃기나 하는 사내에게 맡겨졌다."], ["Uriah, Traddles, and I, as the junior part of the company, went down last, how we could.", "우라이어와 트래들스와 나는, 일행 가운데 가장 어린 축이라, 알아서 마지막에 내려갔다."], ["I was not so vexed at losing Agnes as I might have been, since it gave me an opportunity of making myself known to Traddles on the stairs, who greeted me with great fervour; while Uriah writhed with such obtrusive satisfaction and self-abasement, that I could gladly have pitched him over the banisters.", "아그네스를 빼앗긴 것이 그리 분하지만은 않았는데, 덕분에 계단에서 트래들스에게 내가 누구인지 알릴 기회를 얻었기 때문이다. 그는 무척 뜨겁게 나를 맞아 주었다. 한편 우라이어는 어찌나 거슬릴 만큼 흡족해하면서 또 비굴하게 몸을 뒤틀던지, 나는 그를 난간 너머로 기꺼이 내던져 버릴 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["Traddles and I were separated at table, being billeted in two remote corners: he in the glare of a red velvet lady; I, in the gloom of Hamlet’s aunt.", "트래들스와 나는 식탁에서 동떨어진 두 구석에 배치되어 떨어져 앉았다. 그는 붉은 벨벳 차림 부인의 눈부신 광채 속에, 나는 햄릿의 고모가 드리운 음울함 속에 자리 잡았다."], ["The dinner was very long, and the conversation was about the Aristocracy--and Blood. Mrs.", "만찬은 무척 길었고, 대화는 줄곧 귀족 — 그리고 핏줄에 관한 것이었다."], ["Waterbrook repeatedly told us, that if she had a weakness, it was Blood.", "워터브룩 부인은 자기에게 약점이 하나 있다면 그것은 핏줄이라고 거듭 말했다."]], [["It occurred to me several times that we should have got on better, if we had not been quite so genteel.", "우리가 이토록 점잔을 빼지만 않았더라면 자리가 더 잘 풀렸을 텐데, 하는 생각이 여러 번 들었다."], ["We were so exceedingly genteel, that our scope was very limited. A Mr. and Mrs.", "우리는 어찌나 지독하게 점잔을 빼던지, 이야기의 폭이 몹시 좁았다."], ["Gulpidge were of the party, who had something to do at second-hand (at least, Mr. Gulpidge had) with the law business of the Bank; and what with the Bank, and what with the Treasury, we were as exclusive as the Court Circular.", "일행 중에는 걸피지 씨 내외도 있었는데, 그들은 (적어도 걸피지 씨는) 간접적으로 은행의 법률 업무와 무슨 관련이 있었다. 그래서 은행이며 재무성이며 하는 통에, 우리는 궁정 소식란만큼이나 폐쇄적이 되었다."], ["To mend the matter, Hamlet’s aunt had the family failing of indulging in soliloquy, and held forth in a desultory manner, by herself, on every topic that was introduced.", "설상가상으로 햄릿의 고모는 혼잣말을 즐기는 집안 내력이 있어서, 무슨 화제가 나오든 혼자 두서없이 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다."], ["These were few enough, to be sure; but as we always fell back upon Blood, she had as wide a field for abstract speculation as her nephew himself.", "화제야 정말이지 몇 가지뿐이었지만, 우리가 늘 핏줄로 되돌아왔기에, 그녀는 자기 조카 못지않게 추상적인 사색을 펼칠 너른 마당을 누렸다."]], [["We might have been a party of Ogres, the conversation assumed such a sanguine complexion.", "대화가 어찌나 핏빛으로 물들었던지, 우리는 마치 식인귀 무리라도 된 것 같았다."]], [["‘I confess I am of Mrs. Waterbrook’s opinion,’ said Mr.", "\"솔직히 저는 워터브룩 부인과 같은 생각입니다.\""], ["Waterbrook, with his wine-glass at his eye.", "워터브룩 씨가 포도주잔을 눈높이에 들고 말했다."], ["‘Other things are all very well in their way, but give me Blood!’", "\"다른 것들도 다 나름대로 좋지만, 뭐니 뭐니 해도 핏줄이지요!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["There is nothing,’ observed Hamlet’s aunt, ‘so satisfactory to one!", "그만큼 만족스러운 것이 어디 있겠어요!\" 햄릿의 고모가 한마디 했다."], ["There is nothing that is so much one’s beau-ideal of--of all that sort of thing, speaking generally.", "\"대체로 말하자면, 그 모든 것들 가운데 그토록 이상적인 것이 어디 있겠어요."], ["There are some low minds (not many, I am happy to believe, but there are some) that would prefer to do what I should call bow down before idols.", "더러 천한 마음을 가진 사람들이 (다행히 많지는 않지만, 더러는 있지요) 제가 우상 숭배라 부르는 짓을 더 좋아하더군요. 정말이지 우상이라니까요!"], ["Positively Idols! Before service, intellect, and so on.", "공로니 지성이니 하는 것들 앞에 머리를 조아리다니요."], ["But these are intangible points.", "하지만 그런 것들은 손에 잡히지 않는 거예요."], ["Blood is not so.", "핏줄은 그렇지 않아요."], ["We see Blood in a nose, and we know it.", "우리는 코에서 핏줄을 보고, 그것을 알아봅니다."], ["We meet with it in a chin, and we say, “There it is!", "턱에서 그것과 마주치면 이렇게 말하지요. '저기 있구나!"], ["That’s Blood!”", "저게 핏줄이야!'"], ["It is an actual matter of fact. We point it out.", "그건 엄연한 사실이에요. 우리는 그것을 콕 집어낼 수 있어요."], ["It admits of no doubt.’", "거기엔 한 점의 의심도 없지요.\""]], [["The simpering fellow with the weak legs, who had taken Agnes down, stated the question more decisively yet, I thought.", "아그네스를 데려갔던 다리가 부실하고 실실 웃는 사내는, 내가 보기에 그 문제를 한층 더 단호하게 못 박았다."]], [["‘Oh, you know, deuce take it,’ said this gentleman, looking round the board with an imbecile smile, ‘we can’t forego Blood, you know.", "\"오, 그러니까 말이죠, 제기랄,\" 이 신사가 멍청한 미소를 띠며 식탁을 빙 둘러보고 말했다. \"핏줄을 포기할 순 없는 거예요, 안 그래요."], ["We must have Blood, you know.", "핏줄은 있어야 한다고요, 안 그래요."], ["Some young fellows, you know, may be a little behind their station, perhaps, in point of education and behaviour, and may go a little wrong, you know, and get themselves and other people into a variety of fixes--and all that--but deuce take it, it’s delightful to reflect that they’ve got Blood in ‘em!", "어떤 젊은 친구들은, 그러니까, 교육이나 처신 면에서 제 신분에 좀 못 미칠 수도 있고, 좀 삐딱하게 나갈 수도 있고, 제 자신이며 남이며 온갖 곤경에 빠뜨릴 수도 있지요 — 뭐 그런 거 말이에요 — 하지만 제기랄, 그래도 그들 몸속에 핏줄이 흐른다고 생각하면 흐뭇하다니까요!"], ["Myself, I’d rather at any time be knocked down by a man who had got Blood in him, than I’d be picked up by a man who hadn’t!’", "저로 말하면, 핏줄 없는 자한테 일으켜 세워지느니, 차라리 언제든 핏줄 있는 자한테 한 대 얻어맞고 쓰러지는 편이 낫겠어요!\""]], [["This sentiment, as compressing the general question into a nutshell, gave the utmost satisfaction, and brought the gentleman into great notice until the ladies retired.", "이 견해는 그 일반론을 한 알의 호두 껍데기 속에 압축한 것이라, 더없는 만족을 자아냈고, 부인들이 자리를 뜰 때까지 그 신사를 크게 돋보이게 했다."], ["After that, I observed that Mr. Gulpidge and Mr. Henry Spiker, who had hitherto been very distant, entered into a defensive alliance against us, the common enemy, and exchanged a mysterious dialogue across the table for our defeat and overthrow.", "그 뒤로, 그때까지 무척 서먹하게 굴던 걸피지 씨와 헨리 스파이커 씨가 공동의 적인 우리에 맞서 방어 동맹을 맺고는, 우리를 무찌르고 깔아뭉개려는 듯 식탁 너머로 알쏭달쏭한 대화를 주고받는 것이 보였다."]], [["‘That affair of the first bond for four thousand five hundred pounds has not taken the course that was expected, Spiker,’ said Mr. Gulpidge.", "\"사천오백 파운드짜리 첫 번째 채권 건은 예상했던 대로 흘러가지 않았어요, 스파이커.\" 걸피지 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Do you mean the D.", "\"에이 공작 건 말이오?\""], ["of A.’s?’ said Mr. Spiker.", "스파이커 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘The C. of B.’s!’ said Mr.", "\"비 백작 건이오!\""], ["Gulpidge.", "걸피지 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Spiker raised his eyebrows, and looked much concerned.", "스파이커 씨는 눈썹을 치켜올리며 자못 걱정스러운 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘When the question was referred to Lord--I needn’t name him,’ said Mr.", "\"그 문제가 어느 경에게 — 이름은 댈 것 없지만 — 회부되었을 때,\""], ["Gulpidge, checking himself--", "걸피지 씨가 말을 끊으며 말했다 —"]], [["‘I understand,’ said Mr.", "\"알겠소,\" 스파이커 씨가 말했다."], ["Spiker, ‘N.’", "\"엔 말이군요.\""]], [["Mr. Gulpidge darkly nodded--‘was referred to him, his answer was, “Money, or no release.”’", "걸피지 씨가 음울하게 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그분께 회부되었더니, 그분 대답이 '돈을 내든지, 아니면 면제는 없다'였다오.\""]], [["‘Lord bless my soul!’ cried Mr. Spiker.", "\"맙소사, 세상에!\" 스파이커 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["“‘Money, or no release,”’ repeated Mr. Gulpidge, firmly.", "\"'돈을 내든지, 아니면 면제는 없다.'\" 걸피지 씨가 단호하게 되풀이했다."], ["‘The next in reversion--you understand me?’", "\"다음 상속 순위자가 — 무슨 말인지 아시겠소?\""]], [["‘K.,’ said Mr.", "\"케이 말이군요.\""], ["Spiker, with an ominous look.", "스파이커 씨가 불길한 눈빛으로 말했다."]], [["‘--K. then positively refused to sign.", "\"— 그래요, 케이가 서명을 딱 잘라 거절했소."], ["He was attended at Newmarket for that purpose, and he point-blank refused to do it.’", "그 일로 그를 뉴마켓까지 찾아갔는데, 그가 단칼에 거절해 버렸지요.\""]], [["Mr. Spiker was so interested, that he became quite stony.", "스파이커 씨는 어찌나 흥미진진해하던지, 아예 돌처럼 굳어 버렸다."]], [["‘So the matter rests at this hour,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서 지금 이 시각까지 그 문제는 그대로 묶여 있소.\""], ["Gulpidge, throwing himself back in his chair.", "걸피지 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다."], ["‘Our friend Waterbrook will excuse me if I forbear to explain myself generally, on account of the magnitude of the interests involved.’", "\"얽힌 이해관계가 워낙 막중한지라 일반적인 사정을 자세히 밝히기를 삼가는 것을, 우리 친구 워터브룩이 너그러이 이해해 주리라 믿소.\""]], [["Mr. Waterbrook was only too happy, as it appeared to me, to have such interests, and such names, even hinted at, across his table.", "내가 보기에 워터브룩 씨는 자기 식탁에서 그런 이해관계와 그런 이름들이, 비록 넌지시 비치는 정도일망정 오가는 것이 그저 더없이 기뻐 보였다."], ["He assumed an expression of gloomy intelligence (though I am persuaded he knew no more about the discussion than I did), and highly approved of the discretion that had been observed. Mr.", "그는 (이 논의에 대해 나보다 더 아는 게 없으리라고 나는 확신하지만) 음울하게 사정을 다 안다는 표정을 지으며, 그토록 신중함이 지켜진 것을 크게 칭찬했다."], ["Spiker, after the receipt of such a confidence, naturally desired to favour his friend with a confidence of his own; therefore the foregoing dialogue was succeeded by another, in which it was Mr. Gulpidge’s turn to be surprised, and that by another in which the surprise came round to Mr.", "스파이커 씨는 이런 속내를 전해 받았으니, 자연히 제 친구에게 자기 나름의 속내로 보답하고 싶어 했다. 그리하여 앞의 대화에 뒤이어 또 다른 대화가 이어졌는데, 이번에는 걸피지 씨가 놀랄 차례였고, 다시 그에 뒤이은 대화에서는 놀랄 차례가 다시 스파이커 씨에게 돌아갔으며, 그런 식으로 번갈아 가며 주거니 받거니 했다."], ["Spiker’s turn again, and so on, turn and turn about. All this time we, the outsiders, remained oppressed by the tremendous interests involved in the conversation; and our host regarded us with pride, as the victims of a salutary awe and astonishment.", "그러는 내내 우리 바깥사람들은 그 대화에 얽힌 어마어마한 이해관계에 짓눌려 있었다. 그리고 우리 주인장은 우리를 유익한 경외와 경탄의 제물이라도 되는 양 자랑스레 바라보았다."], ["I was very glad indeed to get upstairs to Agnes, and to talk with her in a corner, and to introduce Traddles to her, who was shy, but agreeable, and the same good-natured creature still.", "나는 위층의 아그네스에게로 올라가, 한쪽 구석에서 그녀와 이야기를 나누고, 트래들스를 그녀에게 소개하게 되어 정말로 무척 기뻤다. 트래들스는 수줍어했으나 상냥했고, 여전히 그 사람 좋은 친구 그대로였다."], ["As he was obliged to leave early, on account of going away next morning for a month, I had not nearly so much conversation with him as I could have wished; but we exchanged addresses, and promised ourselves the pleasure of another meeting when he should come back to town.", "그는 이튿날 아침 한 달간 떠나야 하는 탓에 일찍 자리를 떠야 했으므로, 나는 바라던 만큼 그와 이야기를 많이 나누지는 못했다. 그래도 우리는 주소를 주고받았고, 그가 시내로 돌아오면 다시 만나는 즐거움을 누리자고 다짐했다."], ["He was greatly interested to hear that I knew Steerforth, and spoke of him with such warmth that I made him tell Agnes what he thought of him.", "그는 내가 스티어포스를 안다는 것을 듣고 무척 흥미로워하면서 어찌나 따뜻하게 그에 대해 말하던지, 나는 그더러 아그네스에게도 그를 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 보라고 했다."], ["But Agnes only looked at me the while, and very slightly shook her head when only I observed her.", "그러나 아그네스는 그동안 나만 바라보다가, 나만 보고 있을 때 아주 살짝 고개를 저었다."]], [["As she was not among people with whom I believed she could be very much at home, I was almost glad to hear that she was going away within a few days, though I was sorry at the prospect of parting from her again so soon.", "그녀가 그리 마음 편히 어울릴 만한 사람들 속에 있는 것 같지 않았기에, 나는 그녀가 며칠 안에 떠난다는 말을 듣고 거의 다행스럽기까지 했다. 그토록 빨리 다시 그녀와 헤어질 생각에 서운하기는 했지만 말이다."], ["This caused me to remain until all the company were gone.", "이 때문에 나는 손님들이 다 갈 때까지 남아 있었다."], ["Conversing with her, and hearing her sing, was such a delightful reminder to me of my happy life in the grave old house she had made so beautiful, that I could have remained there half the night; but, having no excuse for staying any longer, when the lights of Mr. Waterbrook’s society were all snuffed out, I took my leave very much against my inclination.", "그녀와 이야기를 나누고 그녀의 노래를 듣는 것은, 그녀가 그토록 아름답게 가꿔 놓은 그 고풍스러운 옛집에서 보낸 행복한 시절을 어찌나 즐겁게 떠올려 주던지, 나는 밤이 절반은 새도록 그곳에 머물 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 더 머무를 핑계가 없었기에, 워터브룩 씨 사교 모임의 불빛이 모두 꺼지자, 나는 영 내키지 않는 마음으로 작별을 고했다."], ["I felt then, more than ever, that she was my better Angel; and if I thought of her sweet face and placid smile, as though they had shone on me from some removed being, like an Angel, I hope I thought no harm.", "그때 나는 그 어느 때보다도, 그녀가 나의 더 선한 천사라는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 마치 천사처럼 어딘가 동떨어진 존재로부터 그녀의 다정한 얼굴과 잔잔한 미소가 나를 비춰 주기라도 한 듯 그것들을 떠올렸다 해도, 나쁜 마음은 아니었으리라 믿는다."]], [["I have said that the company were all gone; but I ought to have excepted Uriah, whom I don’t include in that denomination, and who had never ceased to hover near us.", "손님들이 다 갔다고 말했지만, 우라이어만은 빼야 했다. 나는 그를 그 부류에 끼워 주지 않는데, 그는 우리 곁을 맴도는 것을 한순간도 멈추지 않았다."], ["He was close behind me when I went downstairs.", "내가 계단을 내려갈 때 그는 바로 내 등 뒤에 붙어 있었다."], ["He was close beside me, when I walked away from the house, slowly fitting his long skeleton fingers into the still longer fingers of a great Guy Fawkes pair of gloves.", "내가 집을 나서서 걸어갈 때 그는 바로 내 옆에 붙어, 거대한 가이 포크스 장갑의 더 긴 손가락 부분에 자기 해골 같은 긴 손가락을 천천히 끼워 넣고 있었다."]], [["It was in no disposition for Uriah’s company, but in remembrance of the entreaty Agnes had made to me, that I asked him if he would come home to my rooms, and have some coffee.", "우라이어와 함께 있고 싶은 마음이 들어서가 아니라, 아그네스가 나에게 한 간청을 떠올리고서, 나는 그에게 우리 방에 가서 커피라도 들겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, really, Master Copperfield,’ he rejoined--‘I beg your pardon, Mister Copperfield, but the other comes so natural, I don’t like that you should put a constraint upon yourself to ask a numble person like me to your ouse.’", "\"오, 정말이지, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 받아 말했다. \"아, 죄송합니다, 코퍼필드 씨. 그런데 그쪽이 너무 자연스럽게 나와서요. 저같이 미천한 사람을 댁에 초대하려고 도련님이 굳이 억지로 애쓰시는 건 제가 바라지 않아서요.\""]], [["‘There is no constraint in the case,’ said I.", "\"억지로 그러는 게 아니에요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Will you come?’", "\"오겠어요?\""]], [["‘I should like to, very much,’ replied Uriah, with a writhe.", "\"정말 가고 싶습니다.\" 우라이어가 몸을 배배 꼬며 대답했다."]], [["‘Well, then, come along!’ said I.", "\"그럼, 갑시다!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["I could not help being rather short with him, but he appeared not to mind it.", "나는 그에게 다소 퉁명스럽게 굴지 않을 수 없었으나, 그는 개의치 않는 듯했다."], ["We went the nearest way, without conversing much upon the road; and he was so humble in respect of those scarecrow gloves, that he was still putting them on, and seemed to have made no advance in that labour, when we got to my place.", "우리는 길에서 별 대화 없이 가장 가까운 길로 갔다. 그는 그 허수아비 같은 장갑을 끼느라 어찌나 굼뜨던지, 우리가 내 처소에 다다랐을 때까지도 여전히 그것을 끼는 중이었고, 그 일에 진척이라곤 전혀 없어 보였다."]], [["I led him up the dark stairs, to prevent his knocking his head against anything, and really his damp cold hand felt so like a frog in mine, that I was tempted to drop it and run away.", "나는 그가 어디에 머리를 부딪치지 않도록 어두운 계단을 앞장서 안내했는데, 정말이지 그의 축축하고 차가운 손이 내 손안에서 어찌나 개구리처럼 느껴지던지, 그것을 떨어뜨려 버리고 달아나고 싶을 지경이었다."], ["Agnes and hospitality prevailed, however, and I conducted him to my fireside.", "그러나 아그네스와 손님 대접의 도리가 이겨, 나는 그를 내 난롯가로 데려갔다."], ["When I lighted my candles, he fell into meek transports with the room that was revealed to him; and when I heated the coffee in an unassuming block-tin vessel in which Mrs. Crupp delighted to prepare it (chiefly, I believe, because it was not intended for the purpose, being a shaving-pot, and because there was a patent invention of great price mouldering away in the pantry), he professed so much emotion, that I could joyfully have scalded him.", "내가 촛불을 켜자, 그는 눈앞에 드러난 방에 온순한 황홀경에 빠졌다. 그리고 내가 크럽 부인이 즐겨 커피를 끓이던 수수한 양철 통에 (그녀가 그것을 좋아한 건, 내 생각에 주로 그것이 본래 면도용 그릇이라 커피 끓이는 용도가 아니었던 데다, 식료품 저장실에는 비싼 값을 주고 산 특허 발명품이 곰팡이를 피우며 썩어 가고 있었기 때문이다) 커피를 데우자, 그는 어찌나 감격을 늘어놓던지, 나는 그에게 그 끓는 물을 끼얹어 버리고 싶을 만큼 즐거웠을 것이다."]], [["‘Oh, really, Master Copperfield,--I mean Mister Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘to see you waiting upon me is what I never could have expected!", "\"오, 정말이지, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 아니, 코퍼필드 씨,\" 우라이어가 말했다. \"도련님이 저를 시중드시다니, 이건 제가 도저히 기대도 못 한 일입니다요!"], ["But, one way and another, so many things happen to me which I never could have expected, I am sure, in my umble station, that it seems to rain blessings on my ed.", "하지만 이러저러하게, 제 미천한 처지에 도저히 기대도 못 한 일들이 어찌나 많이 생기는지, 정말이지 제 머리 위로 축복이 비처럼 쏟아지는 것만 같습니다요."], ["You have heard something, I des-say, of a change in my expectations, Master Copperfield,--I should say, Mister Copperfield?’", "제 앞날에 변화가 생겼다는 얘기를, 아마 좀 들으셨겠지요, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 아니, 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야겠네요?\""]], [["As he sat on my sofa, with his long knees drawn up under his coffee-cup, his hat and gloves upon the ground close to him, his spoon going softly round and round, his shadowless red eyes, which looked as if they had scorched their lashes off, turned towards me without looking at me, the disagreeable dints I have formerly described in his nostrils coming and going with his breath, and a snaky undulation pervading his frame from his chin to his boots, I decided in my own mind that I disliked him intensely.", "그가 내 소파에 앉아, 긴 무릎을 커피잔 밑으로 끌어 올리고, 모자와 장갑을 발치 가까운 바닥에 내려놓고, 숟가락을 슬슬 휘휘 돌리며, 속눈썹마저 다 태워 버린 듯한 그 그림자 없는 붉은 눈을, 나를 보지도 않으면서 내 쪽으로 돌리고, 앞서 묘사한 바 있는 그 콧구멍의 그 거슬리는 우묵한 자국이 숨결에 따라 폈다 오므라들었다 하고, 턱에서 구두에 이르기까지 뱀처럼 굽이치는 물결이 온몸에 흐르는 모습을 보면서, 나는 마음속으로 그가 지독하게 싫다고 결론지었다."], ["It made me very uncomfortable to have him for a guest, for I was young then, and unused to disguise what I so strongly felt.", "그를 손님으로 두고 있자니 무척 불편했는데, 그때 나는 어렸고, 그토록 강하게 느끼는 감정을 감추는 데 서툴렀기 때문이다."]], [["‘You have heard something, I des-say, of a change in my expectations, Master Copperfield,--I should say, Mister Copperfield?’ observed Uriah.", "\"제 앞날에 변화가 생겼다는 얘기를, 아마 좀 들으셨겠지요, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 아니, 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야겠네요?\" 우라이어가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said I, ‘something.’", "\"네,\" 내가 말했다. \"좀 들었어요.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I thought Miss Agnes would know of it!’ he quietly returned.", "아그네스 아씨가 알고 계실 줄 알았습니다요!\" 그가 조용히 받아쳤다."], ["‘I’m glad to find Miss Agnes knows of it.", "\"아그네스 아씨가 아신다니 기쁘네요."], ["Oh, thank you, Master--Mister Copperfield!’", "오, 고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련 — 코퍼필드 씨!\""]], [["I could have thrown my bootjack at him (it lay ready on the rug), for having entrapped me into the disclosure of anything concerning Agnes, however immaterial.", "나는 그에게 구두 벗기개를 집어 던질 수도 있었을 것이다 (그것이 마침 깔개 위에 놓여 있었다). 아무리 사소한 것일망정 아그네스에 관한 무언가를 발설하도록 나를 옭아 넣었으니 말이다."], ["But I only drank my coffee.", "그러나 나는 그저 커피를 마셨다."]], [["‘What a prophet you have shown yourself, Mister Copperfield!’ pursued Uriah.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 알고 보니 정말 대단한 예언자셨어요!\" 우라이어가 말을 이었다."], ["‘Dear me, what a prophet you have proved yourself to be!", "\"이런, 정말이지 대단한 예언자임이 입증되셨다니까요!"], ["Don’t you remember saying to me once, that perhaps I should be a partner in Mr. Wickfield’s business, and perhaps it might be Wickfield and Heep?", "언젠가 저더러, 어쩌면 제가 위크필드 씨 사업의 동업자가 될지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그게 위크필드 앤드 힙이 될지도 모른다고 하셨던 것, 기억 안 나세요?"], ["You may not recollect it; but when a person is umble, Master Copperfield, a person treasures such things up!’", "도련님은 기억 못 하실지 몰라도, 미천한 사람은 말입니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 그런 말을 가슴에 고이 간직하는 법이지요!\""]], [["‘I recollect talking about it,’ said I, ‘though I certainly did not think it very likely then.’ ‘Oh!", "\"그런 얘기를 했던 건 기억나요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그때는 그게 그리 그럴듯하다고 생각하진 않았지만요.\" \"오!"], ["who would have thought it likely, Mister Copperfield!’ returned Uriah, enthusiastically.", "누가 그게 그럴듯하다고 생각이나 했겠습니까요, 코퍼필드 씨!\" 우라이어가 신이 나서 받아쳤다."], ["‘I am sure I didn’t myself.", "\"정말이지 저 자신도 그러지 못했는걸요."], ["I recollect saying with my own lips that I was much too umble.", "제 입으로 직접, 저는 너무도 미천하다고 말했던 게 기억납니다요."], ["So I considered myself really and truly.’", "그래서 정말이지 진심으로 제 자신을 그렇게 여겼지요.\""]], [["He sat, with that carved grin on his face, looking at the fire, as I looked at him.", "그는 그 새겨 놓은 듯한 히죽거림을 얼굴에 띤 채 앉아 불을 바라보았고, 나는 그를 바라보았다."]], [["‘But the umblest persons, Master Copperfield,’ he presently resumed, ‘may be the instruments of good.", "\"하지만 가장 미천한 사람도, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 이윽고 말을 이었다. \"선한 일의 도구가 될 수 있는 거지요."], ["I am glad to think I have been the instrument of good to Mr. Wickfield, and that I may be more so.", "저는 제가 위크필드 씨에게 선한 일의 도구가 되어 왔다고, 그리고 앞으로 더 그럴 수 있으리라고 생각하니 기쁩니다요."], ["Oh what a worthy man he is, Mister Copperfield, but how imprudent he has been!’", "오, 그분은 얼마나 훌륭한 분인지요, 코퍼필드 씨. 하지만 얼마나 경솔하셨던지요!\""]], [["‘I am sorry to hear it,’ said I.", "\"그 말을 들으니 안타깝네요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["I could not help adding, rather pointedly, ‘on all accounts.’", "나는 다소 콕 집어 이렇게 덧붙이지 않을 수 없었다. \"어느 모로 보나요.\""]], [["‘Decidedly so, Mister Copperfield,’ replied Uriah.", "\"정말 그렇습니다요, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 우라이어가 대답했다."], ["‘On all accounts.", "\"어느 모로 보나요."], ["Miss Agnes’s above all!", "무엇보다 아그네스 아씨를 봐서요!"], ["You don’t remember your own eloquent expressions, Master Copperfield; but I remember how you said one day that everybody must admire her, and how I thanked you for it!", "도련님은 도련님 자신이 했던 그 멋진 말씀을 기억 못 하시겠지만, 저는 도련님이 언젠가 누구라도 그분을 흠모하지 않을 수 없다고 하셨던 것과, 제가 그 말씀에 얼마나 감사드렸는지를 기억합니다요!"], ["You have forgot that, I have no doubt, Master Copperfield?’", "그건 잊으셨겠지요, 틀림없이, 코퍼필드 도련님?\""]], [["‘No,’ said I, drily.", "\"아뇨.\" 내가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."]], [["‘Oh how glad I am you have not!’ exclaimed Uriah.", "\"오, 잊지 않으셨다니 얼마나 기쁜지요!\""], ["‘To think that you should be the first to kindle the sparks of ambition in my umble breast, and that you’ve not forgot it!", "우라이어가 외쳤다. \"제 미천한 가슴에 야망의 불씨를 처음 지펴 주신 분이 도련님이고, 게다가 도련님이 그걸 잊지 않으셨다니요! 오!"], ["Oh!--Would you excuse me asking for a cup more coffee?’", "— 커피 한 잔만 더 청해도 될까요?\""]], [["Something in the emphasis he laid upon the kindling of those sparks, and something in the glance he directed at me as he said it, had made me start as if I had seen him illuminated by a blaze of light.", "그가 그 불씨를 지폈다는 말에 실은 어떤 강조와, 그 말을 하며 나에게 던진 어떤 눈길에, 나는 마치 그가 한 줄기 섬광에 환히 비치는 것을 본 듯 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Recalled by his request, preferred in quite another tone of voice, I did the honours of the shaving-pot; but I did them with an unsteadiness of hand, a sudden sense of being no match for him, and a perplexed suspicious anxiety as to what he might be going to say next, which I felt could not escape his observation.", "전혀 다른 어조로 건넨 그의 청에 정신이 돌아온 나는, 면도용 그릇으로 손님 대접의 도리를 다했다. 그러나 그렇게 하면서도, 손이 떨렸고, 내가 그의 적수가 못 된다는 느낌이 불현듯 들었으며, 그가 다음에 무슨 말을 하려는 것일까 하는 어리둥절하고 미심쩍은 불안에 사로잡혔는데, 이것이 그의 눈을 피할 수 없으리라는 것을 나는 느꼈다."]], [["He said nothing at all.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He stirred his coffee round and round, he sipped it, he felt his chin softly with his grisly hand, he looked at the fire, he looked about the room, he gasped rather than smiled at me, he writhed and undulated about, in his deferential servility, he stirred and sipped again, but he left the renewal of the conversation to me.", "그는 커피를 휘휘 돌리고, 한 모금씩 홀짝이고, 그 으스스한 손으로 턱을 가만히 쓰다듬고, 불을 바라보고, 방 안을 두루 둘러보고, 나를 향해 미소라기보다는 헐떡임에 가까운 표정을 짓고, 그 굽실대는 비굴함 속에서 몸을 배배 꼬며 굽이쳤고, 다시 휘휘 젓고 홀짝였으나, 대화를 다시 시작하는 일은 나에게 떠넘겼다."]], [["‘So, Mr.", "\"그래서, 위크필드 씨가,\" 마침내 내가 말했다."], ["Wickfield,’ said I, at last, ‘who is worth five hundred of you--or me’; for my life, I think, I could not have helped dividing that part of the sentence with an awkward jerk; ‘has been imprudent, has he, Mr. Heep?’", "\"당신 몫의 — 아니 내 몫의 오백 배는 되는 분이,\" (내가 살아 있는 한, 정말이지 그 문장의 그 부분을 어색하게 뚝 끊어 둘로 가르지 않을 수 없었을 것이다) \"경솔하셨다, 그런 거예요, 힙 씨?\""]], [["‘Oh, very imprudent indeed, Master Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, sighing modestly.", "\"오, 정말이지 무척 경솔하셨지요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 우라이어가 겸손하게 한숨을 쉬며 받아쳤다."], ["‘Oh, very much so!", "\"오, 무척이나요!"], ["But I wish you’d call me Uriah, if you please.", "그런데 괜찮으시면 저를 그냥 우라이어라고 불러 주셨으면 합니다요."], ["It’s like old times.’", "그게 옛날 같고 좋아서요.\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"좋아요!"], ["Uriah,’ said I, bolting it out with some difficulty.", "우라이어.\" 나는 다소 힘겹게 그 말을 불쑥 내뱉으며 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you,’ he returned, with fervour.", "\"고맙습니다.\" 그가 뜨겁게 받아쳤다."], ["‘Thank you, Master Copperfield!", "\"고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!"], ["It’s like the blowing of old breezes or the ringing of old bellses to hear YOU say Uriah. I beg your pardon.", "도련님 입에서 우라이어 소리를 들으니, 옛 산들바람이 불어오는 듯, 옛 종소리가 울려 퍼지는 듯합니다요. 죄송합니다."], ["Was I making any observation?’", "제가 무슨 얘기를 하던 중이었지요?\""]], [["‘About Mr. Wickfield,’ I suggested.", "\"위크필드 씨 얘기요.\" 내가 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Yes, truly,’ said Uriah. ‘Ah!", "그래요, 정말이지.\" 우라이어가 말했다. \"아!"], ["Great imprudence, Master Copperfield.", "크나큰 경솔이지요, 코퍼필드 도련님."], ["It’s a topic that I wouldn’t touch upon, to any soul but you.", "도련님 말고는 어느 누구에게도 입에 올리지 않을 화제입니다요."], ["Even to you I can only touch upon it, and no more.", "도련님께도 그저 살짝 건드리고 말 뿐, 그 이상은 안 됩니다요."], ["If anyone else had been in my place during the last few years, by this time he would have had Mr. Wickfield (oh, what a worthy man he is, Master Copperfield, too!) under his thumb.", "지난 몇 년간 제 자리에 다른 누가 있었더라면, 지금쯤 그자는 위크필드 씨를 (오, 그분도 얼마나 훌륭한 분인지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!) 제 엄지손가락 밑에 깔아 놓았을 겁니다요. 엄 — 지손 — 가락 밑에요.\""], ["Un--der--his thumb,’ said Uriah, very slowly, as he stretched out his cruel-looking hand above my table, and pressed his own thumb upon it, until it shook, and shook the room.", "우라이어가 그 잔인해 보이는 손을 내 탁자 위로 쭉 뻗더니, 그 위에 자기 엄지를 꾹 눌러, 그것이 떨리고 또 방이 떨릴 때까지 누르며, 아주 천천히 말했다."]], [["If I had been obliged to look at him with him splay foot on Mr. Wickfield’s head, I think I could scarcely have hated him more.", "만일 그가 위크필드 씨의 머리 위에 그 넓적한 발을 올려놓고 있는 것을 내가 봐야만 했더라도, 나는 그를 이보다 더 미워할 수는 없었으리라 싶다."]], [["‘Oh, dear, yes, Master Copperfield,’ he proceeded, in a soft voice, most remarkably contrasting with the action of his thumb, which did not diminish its hard pressure in the least degree, ‘there’s no doubt of it.", "\"오, 이런, 그렇습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 부드러운 목소리로 말을 이었는데, 그 엄지의 동작과는 너무도 대조적이었다. 그 엄지는 그 모진 압력을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않았다."], ["There would have been loss, disgrace, I don’t know what at all. Mr.", "\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지요. 손실이, 망신이, 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 모릅니다요."], ["Wickfield knows it. I am the umble instrument of umbly serving him, and he puts me on an eminence I hardly could have hoped to reach.", "위크필드 씨도 그걸 아세요. 저는 미천하게 그분을 모시는 미천한 도구일 뿐인데, 그분이 저를 제가 감히 다다르리라고는 바라지도 못했을 높은 자리에 올려 주십니다요."], ["How thankful should I be!’ With his face turned towards me, as he finished, but without looking at me, he took his crooked thumb off the spot where he had planted it, and slowly and thoughtfully scraped his lank jaw with it, as if he were shaving himself.", "제가 얼마나 감사해야 마땅한지요!\" 말을 마칠 무렵 그는 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 돌렸으되 나를 보지는 않은 채, 자기가 박아 두었던 그 자리에서 굽은 엄지를 떼더니, 마치 면도라도 하듯 그 깡마른 턱을 천천히 생각에 잠겨 긁어 댔다."]], [["I recollect well how indignantly my heart beat, as I saw his crafty face, with the appropriately red light of the fire upon it, preparing for something else.", "그 교활한 얼굴이, 그 위에 알맞게 비친 붉은 불빛을 받으며, 무언가 다른 것을 준비하고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 내 심장이 얼마나 분개하여 두근거렸는지 나는 똑똑히 기억한다."]], [["‘Master Copperfield,’ he began--‘but am I keeping you up?’", "\"코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"그런데 제가 도련님을 못 주무시게 붙잡고 있는 건 아닌지요?\""]], [["‘You are not keeping me up.", "\"붙잡고 있는 거 아니에요."], ["I generally go to bed late.’", "나는 대체로 늦게 자니까요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, Master Copperfield!", "\"고맙습니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!"], ["I have risen from my umble station since first you used to address me, it is true; but I am umble still.", "도련님이 처음 저를 부르시던 때 이후로 제가 미천한 처지에서 좀 올라선 건 사실입니다요. 하지만 저는 여전히 미천합니다요."], ["I hope I never shall be otherwise than umble.", "부디 제가 미천하지 않게 되는 일은 결코 없기를 바랍니다요."], ["You will not think the worse of my umbleness, if I make a little confidence to you, Master Copperfield?", "제가 도련님께 자그마한 속내를 털어놓는다 해도, 제 미천함을 더 나쁘게 여기지는 않으시겠지요, 코퍼필드 도련님?"], ["Will you?’", "그러시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Oh no,’ said I, with an effort.", "\"오 그럼요.\" 나는 애써 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you!’ He took out his pocket-handkerchief, and began wiping the palms of his hands.", "\"고맙습니다!\" 그는 손수건을 꺼내 손바닥을 닦기 시작했다. \"아그네스 아씨가요, 코퍼필드 도련님 —\""], ["‘Miss Agnes, Master Copperfield--’ ‘Well, Uriah?’", "\"그래서요, 우라이어?\""]], [["‘Oh, how pleasant to be called Uriah, spontaneously!’ he cried; and gave himself a jerk, like a convulsive fish.", "\"오, 누가 시키지도 않았는데 우라이어라 불러 주시니 어찌나 기쁜지요!\" 그가 외치며, 경련하는 물고기처럼 몸을 한 번 움찔했다."], ["‘You thought her looking very beautiful tonight, Master Copperfield?’", "\"오늘 밤 아씨가 무척 아름다워 보인다고 생각하셨지요, 코퍼필드 도련님?\""]], [["‘I thought her looking as she always does: superior, in all respects, to everyone around her,’ I returned.", "\"늘 그렇듯 보인다고 생각했어요. 모든 면에서, 주위의 누구보다도 뛰어나다고요.\" 내가 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Oh, thank you!", "\"오, 고맙습니다!"], ["It’s so true!’ he cried.", "정말 그렇다니까요!\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh, thank you very much for that!’", "\"오, 그 말씀, 정말 고맙습니다요!\""]], [["‘Not at all,’ I said, loftily.", "\"천만에요.\" 나는 거만하게 말했다."], ["‘There is no reason why you should thank me.’", "\"당신이 나에게 고마워할 까닭이 없어요.\""]], [["‘Why that, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘is, in fact, the confidence that I am going to take the liberty of reposing.", "\"왜냐면 그게, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 우라이어가 말했다. \"실은, 제가 외람되게 털어놓으려는 그 속내거든요."], ["Umble as I am,’ he wiped his hands harder, and looked at them and at the fire by turns, ‘umble as my mother is, and lowly as our poor but honest roof has ever been, the image of Miss Agnes (I don’t mind trusting you with my secret, Master Copperfield, for I have always overflowed towards you since the first moment I had the pleasure of beholding you in a pony-shay) has been in my breast for years.", "제가 미천하지만,\" 그는 손을 더 세게 닦으며 손과 불을 번갈아 바라보았다. \"제 어머니가 미천하지만, 그리고 우리의 가난하나 정직한 지붕이 늘 누추했지만, 아그네스 아씨의 모습이 (제 비밀을 도련님께 맡기는 건 꺼리지 않습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님."], ["Oh, Master Copperfield, with what a pure affection do I love the ground my Agnes walks on!’", "처음 조랑말 마차에서 도련님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 그 순간부터 저는 늘 도련님께 마음이 넘쳐흘렀으니까요) 여러 해 동안 제 가슴속에 자리해 왔습니다요. 오, 코퍼필드 도련님, 제 아그네스가 밟고 다니는 그 땅을 저는 얼마나 순수한 사랑으로 사랑하는지요!\""]], [["I believe I had a delirious idea of seizing the red-hot poker out of the fire, and running him through with it.", "내 생각에 나는 불에서 시뻘겋게 달군 부지깽이를 낚아채 그를 꿰뚫어 버리고 싶은 미친 충동에 사로잡혔던 것 같다."], ["It went from me with a shock, like a ball fired from a rifle: but the image of Agnes, outraged by so much as a thought of this red-headed animal’s, remained in my mind when I looked at him, sitting all awry as if his mean soul griped his body, and made me giddy.", "그것은 마치 소총에서 발사된 총알처럼 충격과 함께 내게서 떠나갔다. 그러나 이 빨강 머리 짐승의 생각 하나만으로도 능욕당한 아그네스의 모습이, 그 비열한 영혼이 그 몸뚱이를 옥죄기라도 한 듯 온통 비뚜름하게 앉아 있는 그를 바라보는 동안 내 마음속에 남아, 나를 어지럽게 했다."], ["He seemed to swell and grow before my eyes; the room seemed full of the echoes of his voice; and the strange feeling (to which, perhaps, no one is quite a stranger) that all this had occurred before, at some indefinite time, and that I knew what he was going to say next, took possession of me.", "그는 내 눈앞에서 부풀어 올라 커지는 듯했고, 방은 그의 목소리의 메아리로 가득 찬 듯했다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 언젠가 정해지지 않은 어느 때에 전에도 벌어진 적이 있으며, 그가 다음에 무슨 말을 할지 내가 안다는 그 기묘한 느낌이 (어쩌면 누구도 전혀 모르지는 않을 그 느낌이) 나를 사로잡았다."]], [["A timely observation of the sense of power that there was in his face, did more to bring back to my remembrance the entreaty of Agnes, in its full force, than any effort I could have made.", "그의 얼굴에 어린 권력의 기색을 때맞춰 알아챈 것이, 내가 할 수 있었던 그 어떤 노력보다도, 아그네스의 간청을 그 온전한 힘으로 내 기억에 되살려 주었다."], ["I asked him, with a better appearance of composure than I could have thought possible a minute before, whether he had made his feelings known to Agnes.", "나는 일 분 전이라면 도저히 불가능하리라 여겼을 만큼 침착한 모양새로, 그에게 자기 감정을 아그네스에게 알린 적이 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Oh no, Master Copperfield!’ he returned; ‘oh dear, no!", "\"오, 아닙니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"오, 이런, 아니지요!"], ["Not to anyone but you.", "도련님 말고는 어느 누구에게도요."], ["You see I am only just emerging from my lowly station.", "보시다시피 저는 이제 막 그 낮은 처지에서 벗어나는 참인걸요."], ["I rest a good deal of hope on her observing how useful I am to her father (for I trust to be very useful to him indeed, Master Copperfield), and how I smooth the way for him, and keep him straight.", "저는 아씨가 제가 자기 아버지에게 얼마나 쓸모 있는지 (정말이지 그분께 무척 쓸모 있는 사람이 되기를 바라거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님), 그리고 제가 어떻게 그분의 앞길을 평탄하게 닦고 그분을 바로 세우는지를 눈여겨봐 주시리라는 데 적잖은 희망을 걸고 있습니다요."], ["She’s so much attached to her father, Master Copperfield (oh, what a lovely thing it is in a daughter!), that I think she may come, on his account, to be kind to me.’", "아씨는 아버지를 무척 따르시니까요, 코퍼필드 도련님 (오, 딸로서 얼마나 사랑스러운 일인지요!), 저는 아씨가 아버지를 봐서 저에게 다정하게 대해 주시게 될지도 모른다고 생각합니다요.\""]], [["I fathomed the depth of the rascal’s whole scheme, and understood why he laid it bare.", "나는 그 악당의 음모 전체의 깊이를 가늠해 보았고, 그가 그것을 까발리는 까닭을 알아챘다."]], [["‘If you’ll have the goodness to keep my secret, Master Copperfield,’ he pursued, ‘and not, in general, to go against me, I shall take it as a particular favour.", "\"제 비밀을 지켜 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시고, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"대체로 저를 거스르지 말아 주신다면, 저는 그것을 각별한 은혜로 알겠습니다요."], ["You wouldn’t wish to make unpleasantness.", "도련님은 불쾌한 일을 만들고 싶지 않으시겠지요."], ["I know what a friendly heart you’ve got; but having only known me on my umble footing (on my umblest I should say, for I am very umble still), you might, unbeknown, go against me rather, with my Agnes.", "도련님이 얼마나 다정한 마음을 가지셨는지 저는 압니다요. 하지만 도련님은 저를 그저 미천한 처지에서만 (가장 미천한 처지에서라고 해야겠네요, 저는 여전히 무척 미천하니까요) 알고 지내셨으니, 어쩌면 저도 모르는 새에 제 아그네스 일로 저를 다소 거스르실지도 모르잖습니까요."], ["I call her mine, you see, Master Copperfield.", "아씨를 두고 제 아그네스라 부르는 거, 보시다시피 그렇습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님."], ["There’s a song that says, “I’d crowns resign, to call her mine!”", "'왕관이라도 내놓으리, 그녀를 내 것이라 부를 수만 있다면!'이라는 노래가 있지요."], ["I hope to do it, one of these days.’", "저도 언젠가 그러기를 바랍니다요.\""]], [["Dear Agnes! So much too loving and too good for anyone that I could think of, was it possible that she was reserved to be the wife of such a wretch as this!", "사랑하는 아그네스! 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 누구에게도 너무나 다정하고 너무나 선한 그녀가, 이런 가련한 놈의 아내가 될 운명으로 점지되었다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일인가!"]], [["‘There’s no hurry at present, you know, Master Copperfield,’ Uriah proceeded, in his slimy way, as I sat gazing at him, with this thought in my mind.", "\"지금 당장 서두를 건 없습니다요, 아시다시피, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 내가 이런 생각을 품고 그를 빤히 바라보며 앉아 있는 동안, 우라이어가 그 끈적끈적한 투로 말을 이었다."], ["‘My Agnes is very young still; and mother and me will have to work our way upwards, and make a good many new arrangements, before it would be quite convenient.", "\"제 아그네스는 아직 무척 어리니까요. 그리고 어머니와 저는 위로 올라가려고 애써야 하고, 일이 완전히 무르익으려면 새로 짜야 할 것들이 한둘이 아니거든요."], ["So I shall have time gradually to make her familiar with my hopes, as opportunities offer.", "그러니 저는 기회 닿는 대로 차츰차츰 아씨가 제 희망에 익숙해지도록 할 시간이 있을 겁니다요."], ["Oh, I’m so much obliged to you for this confidence!", "오, 이렇게 속내를 들어 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다요!"], ["Oh, it’s such a relief, you can’t think, to know that you understand our situation, and are certain (as you wouldn’t wish to make unpleasantness in the family) not to go against me!’", "오, 도련님이 우리 처지를 이해하시고, 게다가 (집안에 불쾌한 일을 만들고 싶지 않으실 테니) 틀림없이 저를 거스르지 않으시리라는 걸 아니, 얼마나 마음이 놓이는지 도련님은 모르실 겁니다요!\""]], [["He took the hand which I dared not withhold, and having given it a damp squeeze, referred to his pale-faced watch.", "그는 내가 감히 거두지 못한 손을 잡아, 축축하게 한 번 꽉 쥐고는, 자기의 핼쑥한 시계 문자판을 들여다보았다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ he said, ‘it’s past one.", "\"이런!\" 그가 말했다. \"한 시가 넘었네요."], ["The moments slip away so, in the confidence of old times, Master Copperfield, that it’s almost half past one!’", "옛정을 나누다 보면 시간이 어찌나 잘도 흘러가는지요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 벌써 거의 한 시 반입니다요!\""]], [["I answered that I had thought it was later.", "나는 더 늦은 줄 알았다고 대답했다."], ["Not that I had really thought so, but because my conversational powers were effectually scattered.", "정말로 그렇게 생각해서가 아니라, 내 대화 능력이 완전히 흩어져 버렸기 때문이었다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ he said, considering.", "\"이런!\" 그가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."], ["‘The ouse that I am stopping at--a sort of a private hotel and boarding ouse, Master Copperfield, near the New River ed--will have gone to bed these two hours.’", "\"제가 묵고 있는 집은 — 일종의 사삿집 호텔 겸 하숙집인데요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 뉴 리버 수원지 근처라 — 두 시간 전에 다 잠자리에 들었을 겁니다요.\""]], [["‘I am sorry,’ I returned, ‘that there is only one bed here, and that I--’", "\"안타깝네요,\" 내가 받아쳤다. \"여기 침대가 하나뿐이라, 내가—\""]], [["‘Oh, don’t think of mentioning beds, Master Copperfield!’ he rejoined ecstatically, drawing up one leg.", "\"오, 침대 얘기는 꺼내지도 마십시오, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 한쪽 다리를 끌어 올리며 황홀하게 받아쳤다."], ["‘But would you have any objections to my laying down before the fire?’", "\"그런데 제가 난로 앞에 누워도 괜찮으실까요?\""]], [["‘If it comes to that,’ I said, ‘pray take my bed, and I’ll lie down before the fire.’", "\"기왕 그렇다면,\" 내가 말했다. \"부디 내 침대를 쓰세요. 나는 난로 앞에 눕겠어요.\""]], [["His repudiation of this offer was almost shrill enough, in the excess of its surprise and humility, to have penetrated to the ears of Mrs.", "이 제안에 대한 그의 거절은, 놀라움과 겸손이 넘쳐흘러, 그때 아마 멀리 떨어진 방에서 잠들어 있었을 크럽 부인의 귀에까지 닿을 만큼 거의 날카로웠다."], ["Crupp, then sleeping, I suppose, in a distant chamber, situated at about the level of low-water mark, soothed in her slumbers by the ticking of an incorrigible clock, to which she always referred me when we had any little difference on the score of punctuality, and which was never less than three-quarters of an hour too slow, and had always been put right in the morning by the best authorities.", "그 방은 대략 간조선 높이쯤에 자리 잡고 있었는데, 부인은 늘 그 고질병 같은 시계의 째깍거림에 잠을 달랬다. 시간 약속 문제로 우리 사이에 자그마한 다툼이 있을 때면 그녀는 늘 그 시계를 들먹였는데, 그것은 언제나 사십오 분 넘게 느렸고, 늘 아침이면 가장 권위 있는 분들이 맞춰 놓는 것이었다."], ["As no arguments I could urge, in my bewildered condition, had the least effect upon his modesty in inducing him to accept my bedroom, I was obliged to make the best arrangements I could, for his repose before the fire.", "어리둥절한 처지에서 내가 아무리 갖은 논리를 들이대도, 내 침실을 받아들이도록 그의 겸손을 움직이는 데 조금도 효과가 없었기에, 나는 그가 난로 앞에서 쉴 수 있도록 할 수 있는 한 최선의 잠자리를 마련해 줄 수밖에 없었다."], ["The mattress of the sofa (which was a great deal too short for his lank figure), the sofa pillows, a blanket, the table-cover, a clean breakfast-cloth, and a great-coat, made him a bed and covering, for which he was more than thankful.", "소파의 매트리스(그의 깡마른 몸뚱이에는 턱없이 짧았다)와 소파 쿠션들, 담요 한 장, 식탁보, 깨끗한 아침 식탁용 천, 그리고 외투 한 벌로 그에게 잠자리와 덮을 것을 만들어 주었더니, 그는 그것에 더없이 고마워했다."], ["Having lent him a night-cap, which he put on at once, and in which he made such an awful figure, that I have never worn one since, I left him to his rest.", "그에게 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자를 하나 빌려주었더니 그는 그것을 곧장 썼는데, 그 꼴이 어찌나 끔찍하던지, 나는 그 후로 두 번 다시 그런 모자를 써 본 적이 없다. 나는 그를 두고 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["I never shall forget that night.", "그날 밤을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다."], ["I never shall forget how I turned and tumbled; how I wearied myself with thinking about Agnes and this creature; how I considered what could I do, and what ought I to do; how I could come to no other conclusion than that the best course for her peace was to do nothing, and to keep to myself what I had heard.", "내가 어떻게 뒤척이고 또 뒤척였는지, 아그네스와 이 작자를 생각하느라 어떻게 스스로를 녹초로 만들었는지, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있을까 또 무엇을 해야 할까를 어떻게 곱씹었는지, 그녀의 마음의 평화를 위한 최선의 길은 아무것도 하지 않고 내가 들은 것을 가슴속에만 묻어 두는 것이라는 결론 말고는 어떤 다른 결론에도 이르지 못했는지를, 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다."], ["If I went to sleep for a few moments, the image of Agnes with her tender eyes, and of her father looking fondly on her, as I had so often seen him look, arose before me with appealing faces, and filled me with vague terrors.", "잠깐이라도 잠이 들면, 그 다정한 눈을 한 아그네스의 모습과, 내가 그토록 자주 보아 온 대로 그녀를 애틋하게 바라보는 그녀 아버지의 모습이, 애원하는 얼굴로 내 앞에 떠올라 막연한 공포로 나를 가득 채웠다."], ["When I awoke, the recollection that Uriah was lying in the next room, sat heavy on me like a waking nightmare; and oppressed me with a leaden dread, as if I had had some meaner quality of devil for a lodger.", "잠에서 깨면, 우라이어가 옆방에 누워 있다는 생각이, 마치 깨어 있는 채로 꾸는 악몽처럼 내 위에 무겁게 내려앉았다. 그리고 마치 좀 더 비루한 종류의 악마를 하숙인으로 들이기라도 한 듯, 납덩이 같은 두려움으로 나를 짓눌렀다."]], [["The poker got into my dozing thoughts besides, and wouldn’t come out.", "게다가 부지깽이까지 내 어렴풋한 꿈속으로 끼어들어 도무지 나가질 않았다."], ["I thought, between sleeping and waking, that it was still red hot, and I had snatched it out of the fire, and run him through the body.", "자는 둥 마는 둥 하는 사이에, 나는 그것이 아직도 시뻘겋게 달아 있어서 내가 그것을 불에서 낚아채 그의 몸뚱이를 꿰뚫어 버렸다고 생각했다."], ["I was so haunted at last by the idea, though I knew there was nothing in it, that I stole into the next room to look at him.", "마침내 그 생각에 어찌나 시달렸던지, 비록 거기엔 아무것도 없다는 것을 알면서도, 나는 그를 보려고 옆방으로 살금살금 들어갔다."], ["There I saw him, lying on his back, with his legs extending to I don’t know where, gurglings taking place in his throat, stoppages in his nose, and his mouth open like a post-office.", "거기서 나는 그가 등을 대고 누워, 두 다리는 어디까지 뻗었는지도 모르게 늘어뜨리고, 목구멍에서 그르렁대는 소리를 내고, 코는 막혔다 뚫렸다 하며, 입은 우체통처럼 떡 벌린 채로 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He was so much worse in reality than in my distempered fancy, that afterwards I was attracted to him in very repulsion, and could not help wandering in and out every half-hour or so, and taking another look at him.", "그는 내 어지러운 상상 속에서보다 실제로 훨씬 더 끔찍했기에, 그 후로 나는 바로 그 혐오감에 이끌려, 삼십 분 남짓마다 들락날락하며 그를 한 번 더 들여다보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Still, the long, long night seemed heavy and hopeless as ever, and no promise of day was in the murky sky.", "그래도 길고 긴 밤은 여전히 무겁고 가망 없게 느껴졌고, 침침한 하늘에는 날이 밝아 올 기미조차 없었다."]], [["When I saw him going downstairs early in the morning (for, thank Heaven! he would not stay to breakfast), it appeared to me as if the night was going away in his person.", "이른 아침 그가 계단을 내려가는 것을 보았을 때 (천만다행으로 그는 아침을 먹으려고 머물지 않았다), 나에게는 마치 밤이 그의 몸을 빌려 떠나가는 것처럼 보였다."], ["When I went out to the Commons, I charged Mrs. Crupp with particular directions to leave the windows open, that my sitting-room might be aired, and purged of his presence.", "박사회관으로 나가면서, 나는 크럽 부인에게 창문을 활짝 열어 두라고, 그래서 내 거실의 공기가 잘 통하고 그의 존재가 말끔히 씻겨 나가게 하라고 각별히 일러두었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 26. I FALL INTO CAPTIVITY", "# CHAPTER 26. I FALL INTO CAPTIVITY"]], [["I saw no more of Uriah Heep, until the day when Agnes left town.", "아그네스가 런던을 떠나는 날까지 나는 유라이어 힙을 다시 보지 못했다."], ["I was at the coach office to take leave of her and see her go; and there was he, returning to Canterbury by the same conveyance.", "나는 역마차 사무소에 나가 그녀와 작별하고 떠나는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그런데 거기 그가 있었다. 같은 마차를 타고 캔터베리로 돌아가는 길이었다."], ["It was some small satisfaction to me to observe his spare, short-waisted, high-shouldered, mulberry-coloured great-coat perched up, in company with an umbrella like a small tent, on the edge of the back seat on the roof, while Agnes was, of course, inside; but what I underwent in my efforts to be friendly with him, while Agnes looked on, perhaps deserved that little recompense.", "그가 입은, 몸에 비해 빈약하고 허리는 짧고 어깨는 솟은 오디색 외투가, 작은 천막 같은 우산과 나란히, 지붕 위 뒷자리 가장자리에 위태롭게 얹혀 있는 것을 보는 것이 그나마 작은 위안이었다. 물론 아그네스는 마차 안에 타고 있었다. 그러나 아그네스가 지켜보는 가운데 그와 다정하게 지내 보려고 애쓰면서 내가 겪은 고통은, 어쩌면 그 정도의 작은 보상을 받을 만했는지도 모른다."], ["At the coach window, as at the dinner-party, he hovered about us without a moment’s intermission, like a great vulture: gorging himself on every syllable that I said to Agnes, or Agnes said to me.", "만찬 자리에서 그랬던 것처럼, 그는 마차 창가에서도 단 한순간도 쉬지 않고 우리 주위를 맴돌았다. 마치 거대한 독수리처럼, 내가 아그네스에게 한 말, 아그네스가 나에게 한 말의 한 음절 한 음절을 게걸스럽게 집어삼키면서."]], [["In the state of trouble into which his disclosure by my fire had thrown me, I had thought very much of the words Agnes had used in reference to the partnership.", "난롯가에서 그가 털어놓은 이야기 때문에 빠져든 그 괴로운 마음 상태에서, 나는 아그네스가 동업을 두고 했던 말을 곱씹고 또 곱씹었다."], ["‘I did what I hope was right.", "\"나는 옳다고 믿는 일을 했어요."], ["Feeling sure that it was necessary for papa’s peace that the sacrifice should be made, I entreated him to make it.’ A miserable foreboding that she would yield to, and sustain herself by, the same feeling in reference to any sacrifice for his sake, had oppressed me ever since.", "아버지의 마음이 편안하시려면 그 희생이 꼭 필요하다는 확신이 들어서, 내가 아버지께 그렇게 해 달라고 간청했어요.\" 그녀가 그를 위한 어떤 희생이든 똑같은 마음으로 받아들이고, 또 그 마음으로 자신을 지탱하리라는 비참한 예감이, 그때부터 줄곧 나를 짓눌렀다."], ["I knew how she loved him. I knew what the devotion of her nature was.", "나는 그녀가 그를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알았다. 그녀의 천성에 깃든 헌신이 어떤 것인지 알았다."], ["I knew from her own lips that she regarded herself as the innocent cause of his errors, and as owing him a great debt she ardently desired to pay.", "그녀가 자신을 아버지가 저지른 잘못의 죄 없는 원인이라 여기고, 자신이 아버지에게 갚아야 할 크나큰 빚을 졌으며 그 빚을 간절히 갚고 싶어 한다는 것을, 나는 그녀 자신의 입으로 들어 알고 있었다."], ["I had no consolation in seeing how different she was from this detestable Rufus with the mulberry-coloured great-coat, for I felt that in the very difference between them, in the self-denial of her pure soul and the sordid baseness of his, the greatest danger lay.", "그녀가 오디색 외투를 걸친 이 가증스러운 루퍼스와 얼마나 다른지 보면서도 나는 조금도 위안을 얻지 못했는데, 바로 그 둘 사이의 차이, 곧 그녀의 순결한 영혼이 보이는 자기희생과 그의 추잡한 비열함 사이에 가장 큰 위험이 도사리고 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["All this, doubtless, he knew thoroughly, and had, in his cunning, considered well.", "이 모든 것을, 그는 틀림없이 속속들이 알고 있었을 테고, 그 교활한 머리로 곰곰이 따져 두었을 것이다."]], [["Yet I was so certain that the prospect of such a sacrifice afar off, must destroy the happiness of Agnes; and I was so sure, from her manner, of its being unseen by her then, and having cast no shadow on her yet; that I could as soon have injured her, as given her any warning of what impended.", "그렇지만 나는 그런 희생의 가능성이 아무리 멀리 있더라도 결국 아그네스의 행복을 무너뜨리고 말리라고 확신했다. 그리고 그녀의 태도로 보아, 그녀가 그때까지는 그것을 보지 못했고, 그것이 아직 그녀에게 아무런 그림자도 드리우지 않았다고 굳게 믿었다. 그러니 닥쳐오는 일을 그녀에게 경고하느니 차라리 그녀를 다치게 하는 편이 나았을 것이다."], ["Thus it was that we parted without explanation: she waving her hand and smiling farewell from the coach window; her evil genius writhing on the roof, as if he had her in his clutches and triumphed.", "그렇게 우리는 아무 설명도 없이 헤어졌다. 그녀는 마차 창문으로 손을 흔들며 미소로 작별을 고했고, 그녀의 악령은 지붕 위에서 몸을 비틀어 댔다. 마치 그녀를 손아귀에 거머쥐고 의기양양해하기라도 하듯이."]], [["I could not get over this farewell glimpse of them for a long time.", "나는 두 사람의 그 작별 장면을 한참 동안 떨쳐 내지 못했다."], ["When Agnes wrote to tell me of her safe arrival, I was as miserable as when I saw her going away.", "아그네스가 무사히 도착했다는 편지를 보내왔을 때도, 나는 그녀가 떠나는 모습을 보았을 때만큼이나 비참했다."], ["Whenever I fell into a thoughtful state, this subject was sure to present itself, and all my uneasiness was sure to be redoubled.", "생각에 잠길 때마다 이 문제가 어김없이 떠올랐고, 내 모든 불안이 어김없이 곱절로 불어났다."], ["Hardly a night passed without my dreaming of it.", "그 꿈을 꾸지 않고 지나가는 밤이 거의 없었다."], ["It became a part of my life, and as inseparable from my life as my own head.", "그것은 내 삶의 일부가 되었고, 내 머리가 내 몸에서 떼어 낼 수 없듯이 내 삶에서 떼어 낼 수 없게 되었다."]], [["I had ample leisure to refine upon my uneasiness: for Steerforth was at Oxford, as he wrote to me, and when I was not at the Commons, I was very much alone.", "나는 내 불안을 더욱 곱씹을 시간이 차고도 넘쳤다. 스티어포스는 편지로 알려 온 대로 옥스퍼드에 가 있었고, 내가 민법박사회관에 나가 있지 않을 때면 나는 거의 혼자였기 때문이다."], ["I believe I had at this time some lurking distrust of Steerforth.", "이 무렵 나는 스티어포스에게 어딘가 숨은 불신을 품고 있었던 것 같다."], ["I wrote to him most affectionately in reply to his, but I think I was glad, upon the whole, that he could not come to London just then.", "그의 편지에 답하며 나는 더없이 다정하게 썼지만, 솔직히 그가 바로 그때 런던에 올 수 없다는 것이 전체적으로는 다행스러웠던 듯하다. 짐작건대 진실은 이러했다."], ["I suspect the truth to be, that the influence of Agnes was upon me, undisturbed by the sight of him; and that it was the more powerful with me, because she had so large a share in my thoughts and interest.", "아그네스의 영향이, 그를 보는 일에 흐트러지지 않은 채로 나를 사로잡고 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그 영향이 나에게 더욱 강하게 작용했던 것은, 그녀가 내 생각과 관심에서 그토록 큰 자리를 차지하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["In the meantime, days and weeks slipped away.", "그러는 사이 나날과 몇 주가 미끄러지듯 흘러갔다."], ["I was articled to Spenlow and Jorkins. I had ninety pounds a year (exclusive of my house-rent and sundry collateral matters) from my aunt.", "나는 스펜로 앤드 조킨스 사무소의 견습 서기로 들어갔다. 고모님에게서 일 년에 구십 파운드(집세와 여러 부수적인 일들은 제외하고)를 받았다."], ["My rooms were engaged for twelve months certain: and though I still found them dreary of an evening, and the evenings long, I could settle down into a state of equable low spirits, and resign myself to coffee; which I seem, on looking back, to have taken by the gallon at about this period of my existence.", "내 방은 적어도 열두 달 치를 계약해 두었다. 비록 여전히 저녁이면 방이 음산했고 저녁 시간이 길게만 느껴졌지만, 나는 어느 정도 한결같은 침울한 상태에 안착할 수 있었고, 커피에 몸을 맡겼다. 돌이켜 보면 내 생애 이 무렵에 나는 커피를 갤런 단위로 마셔 댄 것 같다."], ["At about this time, too, I made three discoveries: first, that Mrs.", "또 이 무렵에 나는 세 가지를 발견했다."], ["Crupp was a martyr to a curious disorder called ‘the spazzums’, which was generally accompanied with inflammation of the nose, and required to be constantly treated with peppermint; secondly, that something peculiar in the temperature of my pantry, made the brandy-bottles burst; thirdly, that I was alone in the world, and much given to record that circumstance in fragments of English versification.", "첫째, 크럽 부인이 '발작'이라는 묘한 병에 시달리는 순교자라는 것인데, 그 병은 으레 코의 염증을 동반했고 끊임없이 박하로 다스려야 했다. 둘째, 내 식료품 저장실의 온도에 무언가 별난 데가 있어서 브랜디 병들이 터진다는 것이었다. 셋째, 내가 이 세상에 홀로라는 것, 그리고 그 사정을 영시의 토막들에 적어 넣기를 무척이나 즐긴다는 것이었다."]], [["On the day when I was articled, no festivity took place, beyond my having sandwiches and sherry into the office for the clerks, and going alone to the theatre at night.", "내가 견습 서기로 들어간 날, 사무소로 서기들에게 줄 샌드위치와 셰리주를 들여보내고, 밤에 혼자 극장에 간 것 말고는 아무런 잔치도 벌어지지 않았다."], ["I went to see The Stranger, as a Doctors’ Commons sort of play, and was so dreadfully cut up, that I hardly knew myself in my own glass when I got home. Mr.", "나는 「이방인」을 보러 갔는데, 민법박사회관 풍의 연극이라 그랬는지 어찌나 끔찍하게 마음이 무너졌던지, 집에 돌아와 거울 속의 나조차 알아보기 힘들 지경이었다."], ["Spenlow remarked, on this occasion, when we concluded our business, that he should have been happy to have seen me at his house at Norwood to celebrate our becoming connected, but for his domestic arrangements being in some disorder, on account of the expected return of his daughter from finishing her education at Paris.", "스펜로 씨는 그날 우리 볼일을 마무리 지으면서, 우리가 인연을 맺게 된 것을 축하하는 뜻으로 노우드에 있는 자기 집에 나를 초대해 보고 싶었으나, 딸이 파리에서 학업을 마치고 돌아올 예정이라 집안일이 다소 어수선한 형편이라고 말했다."], ["But, he intimated that when she came home he should hope to have the pleasure of entertaining me.", "그러나 딸이 집에 오면 나를 대접하는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바란다고 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["I knew that he was a widower with one daughter, and expressed my acknowledgements.", "나는 그가 딸 하나를 둔 홀아비라는 것을 알고 있었기에, 감사의 뜻을 표했다."]], [["Mr. Spenlow was as good as his word.", "스펜로 씨는 약속을 어기지 않았다."], ["In a week or two, he referred to this engagement, and said, that if I would do him the favour to come down next Saturday, and stay till Monday, he would be extremely happy.", "한두 주 뒤에 그는 이 약속을 다시 꺼내며, 다음 토요일에 내려와 월요일까지 묵어 주는 호의를 베풀어 준다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고 말했다."], ["Of course I said I would do him the favour; and he was to drive me down in his phaeton, and to bring me back.", "물론 나는 기꺼이 그 호의를 베풀겠다고 했다. 그가 자기 사두마차에 나를 태워 내려갔다가 다시 데려오기로 했다."]], [["When the day arrived, my very carpet-bag was an object of veneration to the stipendiary clerks, to whom the house at Norwood was a sacred mystery.", "그날이 되자, 내 여행 가방마저 봉급 받는 서기들에게는 경배의 대상이 되었다. 그들에게 노우드의 집은 신성한 수수께끼였다."], ["One of them informed me that he had heard that Mr. Spenlow ate entirely off plate and china; and another hinted at champagne being constantly on draught, after the usual custom of table-beer.", "그중 하나는, 스펜로 씨가 오로지 은식기와 도자기로만 식사를 한다는 말을 들었다고 일러 주었다. 또 하나는, 보통 사람들이 식탁용 맥주를 마시듯 그 집에서는 샴페인이 늘 통째로 따라진다고 슬쩍 흘렸다."], ["The old clerk with the wig, whose name was Mr. Tiffey, had been down on business several times in the course of his career, and had on each occasion penetrated to the breakfast-parlour.", "가발을 쓴 늙은 서기, 이름이 티피 씨인 사람은, 살아오는 동안 볼일로 그 집에 여러 차례 내려갔었고 그때마다 아침 식사용 응접실까지 들어가 본 적이 있다고 했다."], ["He described it as an apartment of the most sumptuous nature, and said that he had drunk brown East India sherry there, of a quality so precious as to make a man wink.", "그는 그곳을 더없이 호화로운 방이라 묘사하면서, 거기서 갈색 동인도 셰리주를 마셨는데 그 품질이 어찌나 귀한지 절로 눈이 찡긋해질 정도였다고 했다."], ["We had an adjourned cause in the Consistory that day--about excommunicating a baker who had been objecting in a vestry to a paving-rate--and as the evidence was just twice the length of Robinson Crusoe, according to a calculation I made, it was rather late in the day before we finished.", "그날 우리는 종교법원에서 연기되었던 사건 하나를 다루었다. 어느 빵집 주인이 교구 회의에서 도로포장세에 이의를 제기한 일로 그를 파문하느냐 마느냐 하는 문제였다. 내가 계산해 보니 그 증거가 「로빈슨 크루소」의 딱 두 배 길이라, 일을 끝마쳤을 때는 이미 날이 꽤 저문 뒤였다."], ["However, we got him excommunicated for six weeks, and sentenced in no end of costs; and then the baker’s proctor, and the judge, and the advocates on both sides (who were all nearly related), went out of town together, and Mr. Spenlow and I drove away in the phaeton.", "어쨌거나 우리는 그를 여섯 주 동안 파문에 처하고 한도 끝도 없는 소송비용을 물도록 선고했다. 그러고 나서 빵집 주인 쪽 소송 대리인과 판사, 그리고 양쪽 변호사들(다들 거의 친척 사이였다)이 함께 시내를 빠져나갔고, 스펜로 씨와 나는 사두마차를 타고 떠났다."]], [["The phaeton was a very handsome affair; the horses arched their necks and lifted up their legs as if they knew they belonged to Doctors’ Commons.", "그 사두마차는 무척이나 멋진 물건이었다. 말들은 마치 자기들이 민법박사회관 소속임을 안다는 듯 목을 둥글게 세우고 다리를 높이 쳐들었다."], ["There was a good deal of competition in the Commons on all points of display, and it turned out some very choice equipages then; though I always have considered, and always shall consider, that in my time the great article of competition there was starch: which I think was worn among the proctors to as great an extent as it is in the nature of man to bear.", "박사회관에서는 모든 겉치레를 두고 경쟁이 상당했고, 그 덕에 더러는 아주 빼어난 마차들도 나타났다. 다만 나는 늘 생각해 왔고 앞으로도 늘 생각하겠지만, 내가 있던 시절 그곳에서 가장 큰 경쟁 품목은 풀 먹인 빳빳함이었다. 내가 보기에 소송 대리인들 사이에서 그것은 인간이 견딜 수 있는 한계까지 입혀지고 있었다."]], [["We were very pleasant, going down, and Mr. Spenlow gave me some hints in reference to my profession.", "내려가는 길 우리는 무척 유쾌했고, 스펜로 씨는 내 직업에 관해 몇 가지 귀띔을 해 주었다."], ["He said it was the genteelest profession in the world, and must on no account be confounded with the profession of a solicitor: being quite another sort of thing, infinitely more exclusive, less mechanical, and more profitable.", "그는 그것이 세상에서 가장 점잖은 직업이며, 결코 사무변호사라는 직업과 혼동해서는 안 된다고 했다. 그것과는 전혀 다른 부류로, 비할 데 없이 더 배타적이고, 덜 기계적이며, 더 돈벌이가 된다는 것이었다."], ["We took things much more easily in the Commons than they could be taken anywhere else, he observed, and that set us, as a privileged class, apart.", "그는 박사회관에서는 다른 어디서보다 훨씬 느긋하게 일을 처리하며, 그것이 우리를 특권 계층으로서 따로 구별 짓는다고 했다."], ["He said it was impossible to conceal the disagreeable fact, that we were chiefly employed by solicitors; but he gave me to understand that they were an inferior race of men, universally looked down upon by all proctors of any pretensions.", "그는 우리가 주로 사무변호사들에게 일을 받는다는 거북한 사실은 감출 수 없다고 했지만, 그들은 열등한 족속이며 어지간한 자부심을 가진 소송 대리인이라면 누구나 깔보는 자들이라는 점을 내게 일러 주었다."]], [["I asked Mr. Spenlow what he considered the best sort of professional business?", "나는 스펜로 씨에게 어떤 종류의 일이 가장 좋은 직업적 사건이라고 보느냐고 물었다."], ["He replied, that a good case of a disputed will, where there was a neat little estate of thirty or forty thousand pounds, was, perhaps, the best of all.", "그는, 삼사만 파운드쯤 되는 아담한 재산이 걸린 유언 분쟁 사건이 아마 그 무엇보다 가장 좋을 것이라고 답했다."], ["In such a case, he said, not only were there very pretty pickings, in the way of arguments at every stage of the proceedings, and mountains upon mountains of evidence on interrogatory and counter-interrogatory (to say nothing of an appeal lying, first to the Delegates, and then to the Lords), but, the costs being pretty sure to come out of the estate at last, both sides went at it in a lively and spirited manner, and expense was no consideration.", "그런 사건에서는, 그가 말하기를, 소송의 단계마다 변론에서 아주 짭짤한 부스러기가 떨어질 뿐 아니라, 신문과 반대신문에서 산더미 위에 또 산더미처럼 증거가 쌓이고(처음에는 대표위원회로, 그다음에는 상원으로 가는 항소는 말할 것도 없고), 게다가 비용은 결국 그 재산에서 나오게 마련이라 양쪽 다 활기차고 신나게 달려들어, 경비 따위는 안중에도 없게 된다는 것이었다."], ["Then, he launched into a general eulogium on the Commons.", "그러고는 박사회관에 대한 전반적인 찬사로 흘러들었다."], ["What was to be particularly admired (he said) in the Commons, was its compactness.", "박사회관에서 특히 감탄할 만한 점은(그가 말하기를) 그 아담함이라고 했다."], ["It was the most conveniently organized place in the world.", "세상에서 가장 편리하게 짜인 곳이라는 것이었다. 그것은 아늑함의 완벽한 화신이었다."], ["It was the complete idea of snugness.", "그것은 호두 껍데기 안에 들어 있었다."], ["It lay in a nutshell.", "예를 들면 이렇다."], ["For example: You brought a divorce case, or a restitution case, into the Consistory.", "당신이 종교법원에 이혼 사건이나 부부 동거 회복 사건을 가져온다."], ["Very good.", "아주 좋다."], ["You tried it in the Consistory.", "당신은 그것을 종교법원에서 심리한다."], ["You made a quiet little round game of it, among a family group, and you played it out at leisure.", "한 가족끼리 둘러앉아 조용한 카드놀이 한판을 벌이듯, 느긋하게 끝까지 즐긴다."], ["Suppose you were not satisfied with the Consistory, what did you do then?", "종교법원이 만족스럽지 않다고 치자, 그러면 당신은 어떻게 하는가?"], ["Why, you went into the Arches.", "그야, 아치스 법원으로 간다."], ["What was the Arches?", "아치스 법원이 무엇인가?"], ["The same court, in the same room, with the same bar, and the same practitioners, but another judge, for there the Consistory judge could plead any court-day as an advocate.", "같은 법정, 같은 방, 같은 법조계, 같은 종사자들인데 판사만 다르다. 거기서는 종교법원 판사도 여느 개정일이면 변호사로 나서서 변론할 수 있기 때문이다."], ["Well, you played your round game out again.", "자, 당신은 그 카드놀이 한판을 또 끝까지 즐긴다."], ["Still you were not satisfied.", "그래도 여전히 만족스럽지 않다."], ["Very good.", "아주 좋다."], ["What did you do then? Why, you went to the Delegates.", "그러면 당신은 어떻게 하는가? 그야, 대표위원회로 간다."], ["Who were the Delegates?", "대표위원들이 누구인가?"], ["Why, the Ecclesiastical Delegates were the advocates without any business, who had looked on at the round game when it was playing in both courts, and had seen the cards shuffled, and cut, and played, and had talked to all the players about it, and now came fresh, as judges, to settle the matter to the satisfaction of everybody!", "그야, 교회 대표위원들이란 일감이 하나도 없는 변호사들로, 두 법정에서 그 카드놀이가 벌어질 때 곁에서 구경하며 패가 섞이고 떼이고 놓이는 것을 죄다 지켜보고, 놀이꾼 모두와 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나눈 자들인데, 이제 판사랍시고 새 얼굴로 나서서 모두가 만족하도록 그 문제를 매듭짓는 것이다!"], ["Discontented people might talk of corruption in the Commons, closeness in the Commons, and the necessity of reforming the Commons, said Mr.", "불만 많은 자들은 박사회관의 부패니, 박사회관의 폐쇄성이니, 박사회관을 개혁해야 할 필요니 떠들어 댈지도 모른다고, 스펜로 씨는 엄숙하게 결론을 내리며 말했다."], ["Spenlow solemnly, in conclusion; but when the price of wheat per bushel had been highest, the Commons had been busiest; and a man might lay his hand upon his heart, and say this to the whole world,--‘Touch the Commons, and down comes the country!’", "그러나 부셸당 밀값이 가장 높았을 때 박사회관도 가장 분주했으니, 사람이라면 누구나 가슴에 손을 얹고 온 세상을 향해 이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것이었다. \"박사회관을 건드려 봐라, 그러면 나라가 무너진다!\""]], [["I listened to all this with attention; and though, I must say, I had my doubts whether the country was quite as much obliged to the Commons as Mr. Spenlow made out, I respectfully deferred to his opinion.", "나는 이 모든 말을 주의 깊게 들었다. 그리고 솔직히, 나라가 과연 스펜로 씨가 말하는 만큼이나 박사회관에 신세를 지고 있는지 의심스럽기는 했지만, 그래도 정중하게 그의 의견을 따랐다."], ["That about the price of wheat per bushel, I modestly felt was too much for my strength, and quite settled the question.", "부셸당 밀값에 관한 그 대목은, 내 처지로는 너무 벅차서 그 문제를 완전히 결판내 버렸다고 겸손히 느꼈다."], ["I have never, to this hour, got the better of that bushel of wheat.", "나는 지금 이 순간까지도 그 밀 한 부셸을 끝내 이겨 내지 못했다."], ["It has reappeared to annihilate me, all through my life, in connexion with all kinds of subjects.", "그것은 평생토록 온갖 종류의 주제와 엮여 나를 짓뭉개려고 거듭 나타났다."], ["I don’t know now, exactly, what it has to do with me, or what right it has to crush me, on an infinite variety of occasions; but whenever I see my old friend the bushel brought in by the head and shoulders (as he always is, I observe), I give up a subject for lost.", "지금도 나는 그것이 정확히 나와 무슨 상관인지, 무슨 권리로 끝없이 다양한 자리에서 나를 짓이기는지 모르겠다. 그러나 내 옛 친구인 그 부셸이 머리채를 잡힌 듯 억지로 끌려 나오는 것을(내가 보기에 늘 그런 식으로 등장한다) 볼 때마다, 나는 그 주제를 가망 없는 것으로 단념해 버린다."]], [["This is a digression.", "이건 곁다리 이야기다."], ["I was not the man to touch the Commons, and bring down the country.", "나는 박사회관을 건드려 나라를 무너뜨릴 위인이 못 되었다."], ["I submissively expressed, by my silence, my acquiescence in all I had heard from my superior in years and knowledge; and we talked about The Stranger and the Drama, and the pairs of horses, until we came to Mr. Spenlow’s gate.", "나는 나보다 나이도 지식도 위인 분에게서 들은 모든 말에 순순히 동의한다는 뜻을 침묵으로 표했다. 그리고 우리는 「이방인」과 연극, 그리고 두 필씩 짝지은 말들에 관해 이야기를 나누다가, 스펜로 씨네 대문 앞에 다다랐다."]], [["There was a lovely garden to Mr. Spenlow’s house; and though that was not the best time of the year for seeing a garden, it was so beautifully kept, that I was quite enchanted.", "스펜로 씨네 집에는 사랑스러운 정원이 딸려 있었다. 정원을 구경하기에 가장 좋은 철은 아니었지만, 어찌나 아름답게 가꿔져 있던지 나는 그만 넋을 잃었다."], ["There was a charming lawn, there were clusters of trees, and there were perspective walks that I could just distinguish in the dark, arched over with trellis-work, on which shrubs and flowers grew in the growing season.", "매혹적인 잔디밭이 있었고, 나무들이 무리 지어 있었으며, 어둠 속에서 겨우 분간할 수 있는 멀어지는 산책로들이 있었는데, 그 위로는 격자 시렁이 아치를 이루고 있어서 자라는 철이면 거기에 관목과 꽃들이 피어날 터였다."], ["‘Here Miss Spenlow walks by herself,’ I thought.", "'여기를 스펜로 양이 홀로 거니는구나.' 나는 생각했다."], ["‘Dear me!’", "'아, 이런!'"]], [["We went into the house, which was cheerfully lighted up, and into a hall where there were all sorts of hats, caps, great-coats, plaids, gloves, whips, and walking-sticks.", "우리는 환하게 불을 밝힌 집으로 들어가, 온갖 모자며 두건이며 외투며 격자무늬 숄이며 장갑이며 채찍이며 지팡이가 놓인 현관으로 들어섰다. \"도라 아가씨는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["‘Where is Miss Dora?’ said Mr. Spenlow to the servant.", "스펜로 씨가 하인에게 물었다. '도라!'"], ["‘Dora!’ I thought.", "나는 생각했다."], ["‘What a beautiful name!’", "'얼마나 아름다운 이름인가!'"]], [["We turned into a room near at hand (I think it was the identical breakfast-room, made memorable by the brown East Indian sherry), and I heard a voice say, ‘Mr.", "우리는 가까운 방으로 들어섰는데(내 생각에 그 갈색 동인도 셰리주로 기억할 만한 바로 그 아침 식사용 방이었다), 한 목소리가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."], ["Copperfield, my daughter Dora, and my daughter Dora’s confidential friend!’ It was, no doubt, Mr. Spenlow’s voice, but I didn’t know it, and I didn’t care whose it was.", "\"코퍼필드 군, 이쪽은 내 딸 도라, 그리고 내 딸 도라의 가장 가까운 벗일세!\" 그것은 분명 스펜로 씨의 목소리였으나, 나는 알아듣지 못했고, 누구 목소리인지 신경도 쓰지 않았다."], ["All was over in a moment.", "모든 것이 한순간에 끝나 버렸다."], ["I had fulfilled my destiny.", "나는 내 운명을 완수했다."], ["I was a captive and a slave.", "나는 사로잡힌 자요, 노예였다."], ["I loved Dora Spenlow to distraction!", "나는 도라 스펜로를 미칠 듯이 사랑하게 되었다!"]], [["She was more than human to me.", "그녀는 나에게 인간 이상의 존재였다."], ["She was a Fairy, a Sylph, I don’t know what she was--anything that no one ever saw, and everything that everybody ever wanted.", "그녀는 요정이었고, 공기의 정령이었고, 무엇인지 나도 모르겠다. 그 누구도 한 번도 본 적 없는 모든 것이자, 누구나 한 번쯤 바라던 모든 것이었다."], ["I was swallowed up in an abyss of love in an instant.", "나는 순식간에 사랑의 심연 속으로 삼켜져 버렸다."], ["There was no pausing on the brink; no looking down, or looking back; I was gone, headlong, before I had sense to say a word to her.", "벼랑 끝에서 멈칫거릴 틈도 없었고, 아래를 내려다보거나 뒤돌아볼 겨를도 없었다. 그녀에게 한마디 건넬 정신을 차리기도 전에, 나는 곤두박질치며 사라져 버렸다."]], [["‘I,’ observed a well-remembered voice, when I had bowed and murmured something, ‘have seen Mr.", "\"저는,\" 잘 기억나는 한 목소리가, 내가 고개 숙여 인사하며 무언가를 중얼거리고 났을 때 말했다."], ["Copperfield before.’", "\"코퍼필드 군을 전에 뵌 적이 있답니다.\""]], [["The speaker was not Dora.", "말한 사람은 도라가 아니었다. 아니었다."], ["No; the confidential friend, Miss Murdstone!", "그 가장 가까운 벗이라는 사람, 머드스톤 양이었다!"]], [["I don’t think I was much astonished.", "나는 그다지 놀라지 않았던 것 같다."], ["To the best of my judgement, no capacity of astonishment was left in me.", "내 짐작으로는, 내 안에 놀랄 능력이라곤 한 톨도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["There was nothing worth mentioning in the material world, but Dora Spenlow, to be astonished about.", "이 물질세계에 도라 스펜로 말고는 놀랄 만한 것이 단 하나도 없었던 것이다. 나는 말했다. \"안녕하세요, 머드스톤 양?"], ["I said, ‘How do you do, Miss Murdstone?", "건강하시길 바랍니다.\" 그녀가 답했다."], ["I hope you are well.’ She answered, ‘Very well.’ I said, ‘How is Mr.", "\"아주 건강해요.\" 나는 말했다. \"머드스톤 씨는 안녕하시고요?\""], ["Murdstone?’ She replied, ‘My brother is robust, I am obliged to you.’", "그녀가 응했다. \"오라버니는 정정하답니다, 고마워요.\""]], [["Mr. Spenlow, who, I suppose, had been surprised to see us recognize each other, then put in his word.", "스펜로 씨는, 짐작건대 우리가 서로를 알아보는 것에 놀란 듯, 이때 한마디 거들었다."]], [["‘I am glad to find,’ he said, ‘Copperfield, that you and Miss Murdstone are already acquainted.’", "\"코퍼필드 군, 자네와 머드스톤 양이 이미 아는 사이라니 반갑군.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield and myself,’ said Miss Murdstone, with severe composure, ‘are connexions.", "\"코퍼필드 군과 저는,\" 머드스톤 양이 매서운 침착함으로 말했다."], ["We were once slightly acquainted.", "\"친척 간이랍니다. 한때 가볍게 알고 지냈지요."], ["It was in his childish days.", "그가 어렸던 시절이었어요."], ["Circumstances have separated us since.", "그 뒤로 사정이 있어 서로 갈라졌고요."], ["I should not have known him.’", "저라면 그를 못 알아봤을 거예요.\""]], [["I replied that I should have known her, anywhere.", "나는 어디서든 그녀만큼은 알아봤을 거라고 답했다."], ["Which was true enough.", "그건 충분히 사실이었다."]], [["‘Miss Murdstone has had the goodness,’ said Mr.", "\"머드스톤 양께서 고맙게도,\" 스펜로 씨가 나에게 말했다."], ["Spenlow to me, ‘to accept the office--if I may so describe it--of my daughter Dora’s confidential friend.", "\"내 딸 도라의 가장 가까운 벗이라는 — 그렇게 불러도 된다면 — 그 소임을 맡아 주셨다네."], ["My daughter Dora having, unhappily, no mother, Miss Murdstone is obliging enough to become her companion and protector.’", "내 딸 도라에게 불행히도 어머니가 없는 터라, 머드스톤 양이 친절하게도 그 애의 동무이자 보호자가 되어 주신 거지.\""]], [["A passing thought occurred to me that Miss Murdstone, like the pocket instrument called a life-preserver, was not so much designed for purposes of protection as of assault.", "문득 이런 생각이 스쳤다. 머드스톤 양은, 호신용 곤봉이라 불리는 호주머니 도구처럼, 보호가 아니라 공격이 목적으로 만들어진 존재가 아닐까."], ["But as I had none but passing thoughts for any subject save Dora, I glanced at her, directly afterwards, and was thinking that I saw, in her prettily pettish manner, that she was not very much inclined to be particularly confidential to her companion and protector, when a bell rang, which Mr.", "그러나 도라 말고는 어떤 주제든 스쳐 가는 생각밖에 들지 않았으므로, 나는 곧장 그녀를 흘끗 바라보았고, 그녀의 그 어여쁘게 토라진 태도에서, 자기 동무이자 보호자에게 그리 속내를 터놓고 싶어 하지는 않는다는 것을 본 듯하다고 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 종이 울렸다."], ["Spenlow said was the first dinner-bell, and so carried me off to dress.", "스펜로 씨가 그것이 첫 번째 만찬 종이라고 하더니, 나를 옷 갈아입으라며 데려갔다."]], [["The idea of dressing one’s self, or doing anything in the way of action, in that state of love, was a little too ridiculous.", "그런 사랑의 상태에서 옷을 갈아입는다거나 무언가 행동이랍시고 한다는 발상 자체가 좀 너무 우스꽝스러웠다."], ["I could only sit down before my fire, biting the key of my carpet-bag, and think of the captivating, girlish, bright-eyed lovely Dora.", "나는 그저 난로 앞에 앉아 여행 가방 열쇠를 깨물며, 그 매혹적이고 소녀답고 눈이 반짝이는 사랑스러운 도라를 생각하는 수밖에 없었다."], ["What a form she had, what a face she had, what a graceful, variable, enchanting manner!", "어쩌면 그런 자태, 어쩌면 그런 얼굴, 어쩌면 그토록 우아하고 변화무쌍하며 사람을 홀리는 태도란 말인가!"]], [["The bell rang again so soon that I made a mere scramble of my dressing, instead of the careful operation I could have wished under the circumstances, and went downstairs.", "종이 어찌나 빨리 다시 울렸던지, 나는 그런 상황에서 마땅히 했어야 할 정성스러운 단장 대신 그저 허둥지둥 옷을 걸치고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["There was some company.", "손님이 몇 있었다."], ["Dora was talking to an old gentleman with a grey head.", "도라는 백발의 노신사와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Grey as he was--and a great-grandfather into the bargain, for he said so--I was madly jealous of him.", "그토록 머리가 셌는데도 — 게다가 증조부라기까지 했다, 본인이 그렇게 말했으니까 — 나는 그가 미칠 듯이 질투가 났다."]], [["What a state of mind I was in!", "나는 어떤 심정이었던가!"], ["I was jealous of everybody.", "나는 모든 사람이 질투가 났다."], ["I couldn’t bear the idea of anybody knowing Mr. Spenlow better than I did.", "누군가가 나보다 스펜로 씨를 더 잘 안다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["It was torturing to me to hear them talk of occurrences in which I had had no share.", "내가 끼지 못했던 일들을 그들이 이야기하는 것을 듣자니 고문이 따로 없었다."], ["When a most amiable person, with a highly polished bald head, asked me across the dinner table, if that were the first occasion of my seeing the grounds, I could have done anything to him that was savage and revengeful.", "더없이 상냥한 한 사람이, 번들번들 윤이 나는 대머리를 한 채, 식탁 너머로 나에게 이 정원을 본 것이 이번이 처음이냐고 물었을 때, 나는 그에게 무엇이든 야만스럽고 앙심에 찬 짓을 저지를 수도 있었다."]], [["I don’t remember who was there, except Dora.", "거기 누가 있었는지는 도라 말고는 기억나지 않는다."], ["I have not the least idea what we had for dinner, besides Dora.", "만찬에 도라 말고 무엇이 나왔는지는 전혀 모르겠다."], ["My impression is, that I dined off Dora, entirely, and sent away half-a-dozen plates untouched. I sat next to her.", "내 인상으로는, 나는 오로지 도라만 먹었고, 여섯 접시쯤은 손도 대지 않은 채 물려 보냈다. 나는 그녀 옆자리에 앉았다."], ["I talked to her. She had the most delightful little voice, the gayest little laugh, the pleasantest and most fascinating little ways, that ever led a lost youth into hopeless slavery.", "나는 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 그녀는 더없이 사랑스러운 작은 목소리에, 더없이 명랑한 작은 웃음에, 길 잃은 젊은이를 가망 없는 노예 신세로 이끈 그 어떤 것보다 더 유쾌하고 매혹적인 작은 몸짓을 지니고 있었다."], ["She was rather diminutive altogether. So much the more precious, I thought.", "그녀는 통틀어 꽤 자그마했다. 그래서 더더욱 귀하게 느껴졌다, 나는 생각했다."]], [["When she went out of the room with Miss Murdstone (no other ladies were of the party), I fell into a reverie, only disturbed by the cruel apprehension that Miss Murdstone would disparage me to her.", "그녀가 머드스톤 양과 함께 방을 나가자(다른 부인은 일행에 없었다), 나는 공상에 잠겼는데, 머드스톤 양이 그녀에게 나를 헐뜯을지도 모른다는 잔인한 불안만이 그 공상을 깨뜨렸다."], ["The amiable creature with the polished head told me a long story, which I think was about gardening.", "번들거리는 머리를 한 그 상냥한 인물이 나에게 긴 이야기를 늘어놓았는데, 아마 정원 가꾸기에 관한 이야기였던 것 같다."], ["I think I heard him say, ‘my gardener’, several times.", "그가 \"우리 집 정원사\"라고 몇 번이나 말하는 것을 들은 듯하다."], ["I seemed to pay the deepest attention to him, but I was wandering in a garden of Eden all the while, with Dora.", "나는 그에게 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울이는 척했지만, 그동안 내내 도라와 함께 에덴동산을 거닐고 있었다."]], [["My apprehensions of being disparaged to the object of my engrossing affection were revived when we went into the drawing-room, by the grim and distant aspect of Miss Murdstone.", "내가 흠뻑 빠진 그 사랑의 대상에게 헐뜯기지나 않을까 하는 불안은, 우리가 응접실로 들어섰을 때 머드스톤 양의 음산하고 쌀쌀맞은 기색을 보고 되살아났다."], ["But I was relieved of them in an unexpected manner.", "그러나 나는 뜻밖의 방식으로 그 불안에서 벗어났다."]], [["‘David Copperfield,’ said Miss Murdstone, beckoning me aside into a window.", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드.\" 머드스톤 양이 나를 창가로 따로 불러내며 말했다."], ["‘A word.’", "\"할 말이 있어요.\""]], [["I confronted Miss Murdstone alone.", "나는 머드스톤 양과 단둘이 마주 섰다."]], [["‘David Copperfield,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘I need not enlarge upon family circumstances.", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드.\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"집안 사정을 구구절절 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠지요."], ["They are not a tempting subject.’ ‘Far from it, ma’am,’ I returned.", "입에 올려 즐거운 화제는 아니니까요.\" \"전혀 그렇지 않습니다, 부인.\" 내가 응했다."]], [["‘Far from it,’ assented Miss Murdstone.", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아요.\" 머드스톤 양이 맞장구쳤다."], ["‘I do not wish to revive the memory of past differences, or of past outrages.", "\"나는 지난날의 불화나 지난날의 모욕을 다시 떠올리고 싶지 않아요."], ["I have received outrages from a person--a female I am sorry to say, for the credit of my sex--who is not to be mentioned without scorn and disgust; and therefore I would rather not mention her.’", "나는 어떤 사람에게서 — 같은 여자로서 부끄럽지만 여자한테서 — 모욕을 당했는데, 그 사람은 경멸과 혐오 없이는 입에 올릴 수도 없는 자라, 차라리 언급하지 않는 편이 낫겠어요.\""]], [["I felt very fiery on my aunt’s account; but I said it would certainly be better, if Miss Murdstone pleased, not to mention her.", "나는 고모님을 생각하니 속에서 불같이 끓어올랐다. 그러나 나는, 머드스톤 양이 괜찮다면 그녀를 언급하지 않는 편이 분명 더 낫겠다고 말했다."], ["I could not hear her disrespectfully mentioned, I added, without expressing my opinion in a decided tone.", "그녀가 함부로 무례하게 언급되는 것을 듣고서는 내 의견을 단호한 어조로 밝히지 않고는 못 배긴다고 덧붙였다."]], [["Miss Murdstone shut her eyes, and disdainfully inclined her head; then, slowly opening her eyes, resumed:", "머드스톤 양은 눈을 감고 경멸하듯 고개를 숙였다. 그러더니 천천히 눈을 뜨고 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["‘David Copperfield, I shall not attempt to disguise the fact, that I formed an unfavourable opinion of you in your childhood.", "\"데이비드 코퍼필드, 사실을 숨길 생각은 없는데, 나는 당신의 어린 시절에 당신에 대해 좋지 않은 견해를 품었어요."], ["It may have been a mistaken one, or you may have ceased to justify it.", "그게 잘못된 견해였을 수도 있고, 당신이 더는 그런 평가를 받을 만하지 않게 되었을 수도 있겠지요."], ["That is not in question between us now.", "그건 지금 우리 사이에서 따질 문제가 아니에요."], ["I belong to a family remarkable, I believe, for some firmness; and I am not the creature of circumstance or change.", "나는 어느 정도 굳건함으로 이름난, 내 믿기로는 그런 집안의 사람이고, 형편이나 변덕에 휘둘리는 사람이 아니에요."], ["I may have my opinion of you. You may have your opinion of me.’", "나는 당신에 대해 내 견해를 품을 수 있고, 당신은 나에 대해 당신 견해를 품을 수 있어요.\""]], [["I inclined my head, in my turn.", "이번에는 내가 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘But it is not necessary,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘that these opinions should come into collision here.", "\"하지만,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"이런 견해들이 여기서 충돌할 필요는 없어요."], ["Under existing circumstances, it is as well on all accounts that they should not.", "지금 처지로는, 여러모로 그러지 않는 편이 나아요."], ["As the chances of life have brought us together again, and may bring us together on other occasions, I would say, let us meet here as distant acquaintances.", "인생의 우연이 우리를 다시 한자리에 모이게 했고, 또 다른 자리에서 모이게 할 수도 있으니, 여기서는 서로 데면데면한 지인으로 만나자고 하고 싶어요."], ["Family circumstances are a sufficient reason for our only meeting on that footing, and it is quite unnecessary that either of us should make the other the subject of remark.", "우리가 그 정도 관계로만 만나기에는 집안 사정이라는 이유 하나로 충분하고, 둘 중 누구도 상대를 입방아의 거리로 삼을 필요는 전혀 없어요."], ["Do you approve of this?’", "이 제안에 찬성해요?\""]], [["‘Miss Murdstone,’ I returned, ‘I think you and Mr.", "\"머드스톤 양.\" 내가 응했다."], ["Murdstone used me very cruelly, and treated my mother with great unkindness.", "\"당신과 머드스톤 씨는 저를 아주 잔인하게 대했고, 제 어머니에게도 몹시 매정하게 굴었다고 생각합니다."], ["I shall always think so, as long as I live.", "살아 있는 한 늘 그렇게 생각할 거예요."], ["But I quite agree in what you propose.’", "하지만 제안하신 것에는 전적으로 동의합니다.\""]], [["Miss Murdstone shut her eyes again, and bent her head.", "머드스톤 양은 다시 눈을 감고 고개를 숙였다."], ["Then, just touching the back of my hand with the tips of her cold, stiff fingers, she walked away, arranging the little fetters on her wrists and round her neck; which seemed to be the same set, in exactly the same state, as when I had seen her last.", "그러고는 차갑고 뻣뻣한 손가락 끝으로 내 손등을 살짝 건드리더니, 손목과 목에 두른 작은 족쇄들을 매만지며 걸어가 버렸다. 그 족쇄들은 내가 마지막으로 보았을 때와 똑같은 한 벌이, 똑같은 상태 그대로인 것 같았다."], ["These reminded me, in reference to Miss Murdstone’s nature, of the fetters over a jail door; suggesting on the outside, to all beholders, what was to be expected within.", "그것들은 머드스톤 양의 천성을 떠올리게 하면서, 마치 감옥 문 위에 걸린 족쇄처럼, 바깥의 모든 구경꾼에게 그 안에서 무엇을 기대해야 할지를 일러 주는 듯했다."]], [["All I know of the rest of the evening is, that I heard the empress of my heart sing enchanted ballads in the French language, generally to the effect that, whatever was the matter, we ought always to dance, Ta ra la, Ta ra la!", "그날 저녁 나머지에 대해 내가 아는 것이라곤 이것뿐이다. 내 마음의 여제가 프랑스어로 황홀한 발라드를 부르는 것을 들었는데, 대개 무슨 일이 있더라도 우리는 늘 춤을 추어야 한다는 내용으로, 타 라 라, 타 라 라!"], ["accompanying herself on a glorified instrument, resembling a guitar. That I was lost in blissful delirium.", "하며, 기타를 닮은 화려한 악기로 직접 반주를 했다는 것. 내가 더없이 행복한 도취에 빠져 넋을 잃었다는 것."], ["That I refused refreshment.", "내가 다과를 거절했다는 것."], ["That my soul recoiled from punch particularly.", "특히 펀치술 앞에서는 내 영혼이 움츠러들었다는 것."], ["That when Miss Murdstone took her into custody and led her away, she smiled and gave me her delicious hand.", "머드스톤 양이 그녀를 거두어 데려갈 때, 그녀가 미소 지으며 나에게 그 감미로운 손을 내밀었다는 것."], ["That I caught a view of myself in a mirror, looking perfectly imbecile and idiotic.", "내가 거울에 비친 내 모습을 흘끗 보았는데, 영락없이 얼간이에 멍청이처럼 보였다는 것."], ["That I retired to bed in a most maudlin state of mind, and got up in a crisis of feeble infatuation.", "내가 더없이 청승맞은 심정으로 잠자리에 들었다가, 가냘픈 도취의 고비 속에서 일어났다는 것."]], [["It was a fine morning, and early, and I thought I would go and take a stroll down one of those wire-arched walks, and indulge my passion by dwelling on her image.", "날씨 좋은 이른 아침이었다. 나는 철 시렁이 아치를 이룬 그 산책로 가운데 하나로 내려가 거닐며, 그녀의 모습을 곱씹는 것으로 내 열정을 마음껏 누려야겠다고 생각했다."], ["On my way through the hall, I encountered her little dog, who was called Jip--short for Gipsy.", "현관을 지나던 길에 나는 그녀의 작은 개와 마주쳤는데, 이름이 집이라고, 집시를 줄인 것이었다."], ["I approached him tenderly, for I loved even him; but he showed his whole set of teeth, got under a chair expressly to snarl, and wouldn’t hear of the least familiarity.", "나는 그 녀석마저 사랑했기에 다정하게 다가갔다. 그러나 녀석은 이빨을 죄다 드러내더니, 일부러 으르렁대려고 의자 밑으로 기어들어 가서는, 조금의 친한 척도 들으려 하지 않았다."]], [["The garden was cool and solitary.", "정원은 서늘하고 호젓했다."], ["I walked about, wondering what my feelings of happiness would be, if I could ever become engaged to this dear wonder.", "나는 이리저리 거닐며, 만약 이 사랑스러운 경이로움과 약혼이라도 하게 된다면 내 행복의 감정이 어떨까 상상해 보았다."], ["As to marriage, and fortune, and all that, I believe I was almost as innocently undesigning then, as when I loved little Em’ly.", "결혼이며 재산이며 그 따위 모든 것에 대해서는, 그때 나는 어린 에밀리를 사랑하던 시절만큼이나 거의 천진하게 아무런 속셈이 없었던 것 같다."], ["To be allowed to call her ‘Dora’, to write to her, to dote upon and worship her, to have reason to think that when she was with other people she was yet mindful of me, seemed to me the summit of human ambition--I am sure it was the summit of mine.", "그녀를 '도라'라고 부를 수 있고, 그녀에게 편지를 쓰고, 그녀에게 푹 빠져 그녀를 숭배하고, 그녀가 다른 사람들과 함께 있을 때조차 나를 마음에 두고 있다고 믿을 만한 근거를 갖는 것, 그것이 나에게는 인간이 품을 수 있는 야망의 정점으로 보였다. 적어도 내 야망의 정점이었던 것은 분명하다."], ["There is no doubt whatever that I was a lackadaisical young spooney; but there was a purity of heart in all this, that prevents my having quite a contemptuous recollection of it, let me laugh as I may.", "내가 맥없이 흐느적대는 풋사랑꾼이었다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그러나 이 모든 것에는 마음의 순수함이 깃들어 있어서, 내가 아무리 그것을 비웃는다 해도, 그 시절을 아주 경멸스럽게 회상하지는 못하게 한다."]], [["I had not been walking long, when I turned a corner, and met her.", "오래 걷지 않았는데, 모퉁이를 돌다가 그녀와 마주쳤다."], ["I tingle again from head to foot as my recollection turns that corner, and my pen shakes in my hand.", "기억이 그 모퉁이를 돌아설 때면 나는 지금도 머리끝부터 발끝까지 짜릿하고, 펜을 쥔 내 손이 떨린다."]], [["‘You--are--out early, Miss Spenlow,’ said I.", "\"이렇게 — 일찍 — 나오셨군요, 스펜로 양.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s so stupid at home,’ she replied, ‘and Miss Murdstone is so absurd!", "\"집안이 어찌나 따분한지요.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"게다가 머드스톤 양은 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지!"], ["She talks such nonsense about its being necessary for the day to be aired, before I come out.", "제가 나오기 전에 하루의 공기가 충분히 데워져야 한다나, 그런 말도 안 되는 소리를 하는 거예요."], ["Aired!’ (She laughed, here, in the most melodious manner.) ‘On a Sunday morning, when I don’t practise, I must do something.", "데워지다니요!\"(그녀는 여기서 더없이 곱고 가락진 웃음을 터뜨렸다.) \"연습을 안 하는 일요일 아침이면 저는 무언가는 해야 하잖아요."], ["So I told papa last night I must come out.", "그래서 어젯밤에 아빠한테 꼭 나가야겠다고 말했어요."], ["Besides, it’s the brightest time of the whole day.", "게다가 지금이 하루 중 가장 환한 때이고요."], ["Don’t you think so?’", "그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\""]], [["I hazarded a bold flight, and said (not without stammering) that it was very bright to me then, though it had been very dark to me a minute before.", "나는 대담하게 날아올라 보기로 하고, 말했다(더듬거리지 않은 것은 아니다). 일 분 전만 해도 나에게는 무척 어두웠는데 지금은 무척 환하다고."]], [["‘Do you mean a compliment?’ said Dora, ‘or that the weather has really changed?’", "\"칭찬을 하시는 거예요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"아니면 정말로 날씨가 바뀌었다는 거예요?\""]], [["I stammered worse than before, in replying that I meant no compliment, but the plain truth; though I was not aware of any change having taken place in the weather.", "나는 아까보다 더 심하게 더듬으며 답하기를, 칭찬이 아니라 있는 그대로의 진실을 말한 것이라고, 다만 날씨에 어떤 변화가 일어났는지는 모르겠다고 했다."], ["It was in the state of my own feelings, I added bashfully: to clench the explanation.", "그건 내 마음의 상태에 일어난 일이라고, 나는 수줍게 덧붙였다. 설명에 쐐기를 박으려고."]], [["I never saw such curls--how could I, for there never were such curls!--as those she shook out to hide her blushes.", "나는 그런 곱슬머리를 본 적이 없다 — 어찌 보았겠는가, 그런 곱슬머리는 세상에 없었으니!"], ["As to the straw hat and blue ribbons which was on the top of the curls, if I could only have hung it up in my room in Buckingham Street, what a priceless possession it would have been!", "— 그녀가 발그레해진 뺨을 가리려고 흔들어 흩뜨리던 그 곱슬머리 같은 것을. 그 곱슬머리 위에 얹힌 밀짚모자와 파란 리본으로 말하자면, 그것을 버킹엄가의 내 방에 걸어 둘 수만 있었다면, 그것은 얼마나 값을 매길 수 없는 보물이었을까!"]], [["‘You have just come home from Paris,’ said I.", "\"파리에서 막 돌아오셨군요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said she.", "\"네.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Have you ever been there?’\n‘No.’", "\"거기 가 보신 적 있어요?\""]], [["‘Oh! I hope you’ll go soon!", "\"없어요.\" \"어머! 곧 꼭 가 보세요!"], ["You would like it so much!’", "정말 좋아하실 거예요!\""]], [["Traces of deep-seated anguish appeared in my countenance.", "깊디깊은 고뇌의 흔적이 내 얼굴에 떠올랐다."], ["That she should hope I would go, that she should think it possible I could go, was insupportable.", "그녀가 내가 가기를 바란다는 것, 내가 갈 수도 있다고 그녀가 생각한다는 것은 견딜 수 없는 일이었다."], ["I depreciated Paris; I depreciated France.", "나는 파리를 깎아내렸다. 나는 프랑스를 깎아내렸다."], ["I said I wouldn’t leave England, under existing circumstances, for any earthly consideration.", "나는 지금 같은 처지에서는 이 세상 그 어떤 것을 준다 해도 잉글랜드를 떠나지 않겠다고 말했다."], ["Nothing should induce me. In short, she was shaking the curls again, when the little dog came running along the walk to our relief.", "그 무엇으로도 나를 꾈 수 없을 거라고. 요컨대, 그녀가 다시 곱슬머리를 흔들고 있을 때, 그 작은 개가 산책로를 따라 달려와 우리를 구해 주었다."]], [["He was mortally jealous of me, and persisted in barking at me.", "녀석은 나를 죽도록 질투해서, 한사코 나를 향해 짖어 댔다."], ["She took him up in her arms--oh my goodness!--and caressed him, but he persisted upon barking still.", "그녀가 녀석을 품에 안아 들고는 — 오, 맙소사! — 어루만졌으나, 녀석은 그래도 한사코 짖어 댔다."], ["He wouldn’t let me touch him, when I tried; and then she beat him.", "내가 만져 보려 하자 녀석은 손도 못 대게 했다. 그러자 그녀가 녀석을 때렸다."], ["It increased my sufferings greatly to see the pats she gave him for punishment on the bridge of his blunt nose, while he winked his eyes, and licked her hand, and still growled within himself like a little double-bass.", "벌을 준답시고 녀석의 뭉툭한 콧등을 톡톡 두드리는 것을 보자니 내 괴로움은 한층 커졌는데, 녀석은 그러는 동안 눈을 끔벅이고 그녀의 손을 핥으면서도, 여전히 속으로 작은 콘트라베이스처럼 으르렁거렸다."], ["At length he was quiet--well he might be with her dimpled chin upon his head!--and we walked away to look at a greenhouse.", "마침내 녀석은 잠잠해졌다 — 그럴 만도 했지, 보조개 팬 그녀의 턱이 녀석의 머리 위에 얹혀 있었으니! — 그리고 우리는 온실을 구경하러 걸어갔다."]], [["‘You are not very intimate with Miss Murdstone, are you?’ said Dora.", "\"머드스톤 양과는 그리 가깝지 않으시죠, 그렇죠?\" 도라가 말했다."], ["--‘My pet.’", "\"— 우리 아가.\""]], [["(The two last words were to the dog.", "(뒤의 두 마디는 개에게 한 말이었다."], ["Oh, if they had only been to me!)", "오, 그것이 나에게 한 말이었더라면!)"]], [["‘No,’ I replied.", "\"아니요.\" 내가 답했다."], ["‘Not at all so.’", "\"전혀 그렇지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘She is a tiresome creature,’ said Dora, pouting.", "\"그분은 정말 지긋지긋한 사람이에요.\""], ["‘I can’t think what papa can have been about, when he chose such a vexatious thing to be my companion.", "도라가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다. \"아빠가 무슨 생각으로 그런 짜증 나는 사람을 골라 제 동무로 삼았는지 모르겠어요."], ["Who wants a protector?", "보호자가 누가 필요하대요?"], ["I am sure I don’t want a protector.", "저는 정말이지 보호자 따위 필요 없어요."], ["Jip can protect me a great deal better than Miss Murdstone,--can’t you, Jip, dear?’", "머드스톤 양보다 집이 저를 훨씬 더 잘 지켜 줄 수 있어요 — 그렇지 않니, 집, 우리 아가?\""]], [["He only winked lazily, when she kissed his ball of a head.", "녀석은 그녀가 공처럼 동그란 머리에 입을 맞추자, 그저 나른하게 눈을 끔벅일 뿐이었다."]], [["‘Papa calls her my confidential friend, but I am sure she is no such thing--is she, Jip?", "\"아빠는 그분을 제 가장 가까운 벗이라고 부르지만, 분명 그런 사람 아니에요 — 그렇지, 집?"], ["We are not going to confide in any such cross people, Jip and I.", "우리는 그런 심술궂은 사람들한테는 속내를 털어놓지 않을 거야, 집이랑 나는."], ["We mean to bestow our confidence where we like, and to find out our own friends, instead of having them found out for us--don’t we, Jip?’", "우리는 우리가 좋아하는 데에 속내를 터놓을 거고, 누가 골라 주는 게 아니라 우리 손으로 친구를 찾아낼 거야 — 그렇지, 집?\""]], [["Jip made a comfortable noise, in answer, a little like a tea-kettle when it sings.", "집은 대답으로 편안한 소리를, 노래하는 주전자가 끓을 때와 좀 비슷한 소리를 냈다."], ["As for me, every word was a new heap of fetters, riveted above the last.", "나로 말하자면, 한마디 한마디가 앞의 것 위에 다시 박히는 새로운 족쇄 무더기였다."]], [["‘It is very hard, because we have not a kind Mama, that we are to have, instead, a sulky, gloomy old thing like Miss Murdstone, always following us about--isn’t it, Jip?", "\"우리한테 다정한 엄마가 없다는 것만으로도 참 가혹한데, 그 대신 머드스톤 양 같은 시무룩하고 음울한 늙은이가 늘 우리를 졸졸 따라다녀야 하다니 — 그렇지 않니, 집?"], ["Never mind, Jip.", "괜찮아, 집."], ["We won’t be confidential, and we’ll make ourselves as happy as we can in spite of her, and we’ll tease her, and not please her--won’t we, Jip?’", "우리는 속내를 안 털어놓을 거고, 그래도 그 사람 때문에 굴하지 않고 우리끼리 최대한 즐겁게 지낼 거고, 그 사람을 약 올리되 비위는 맞춰 주지 않을 거야 — 그렇지, 집?\""]], [["If it had lasted any longer, I think I must have gone down on my knees on the gravel, with the probability before me of grazing them, and of being presently ejected from the premises besides.", "이 말이 조금만 더 이어졌더라면, 나는 자갈밭에 무릎을 꿇고 말았을 것 같다. 무릎이 까질 게 뻔하고, 게다가 곧장 이 집에서 쫓겨날 게 뻔한데도 말이다."], ["But, by good fortune the greenhouse was not far off, and these words brought us to it.", "그러나 다행히도 온실이 멀지 않았고, 이 말이 끝날 즈음 우리는 그 앞에 다다랐다."]], [["It contained quite a show of beautiful geraniums.", "온실 안에는 아름다운 제라늄이 한 무더기 흐드러져 있었다."], ["We loitered along in front of them, and Dora often stopped to admire this one or that one, and I stopped to admire the same one, and Dora, laughing, held the dog up childishly, to smell the flowers; and if we were not all three in Fairyland, certainly I was.", "우리는 그 앞을 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐었고, 도라는 이것저것 감탄하느라 자주 멈춰 섰고, 나도 똑같은 것을 감탄하느라 멈춰 섰고, 도라는 웃으며 개를 어린아이처럼 안아 들어 꽃향기를 맡게 했다. 그리고 우리 셋이 다 요정 나라에 있었던 것은 아닐지라도, 적어도 나는 분명 거기 있었다."], ["The scent of a geranium leaf, at this day, strikes me with a half comical half serious wonder as to what change has come over me in a moment; and then I see a straw hat and blue ribbons, and a quantity of curls, and a little black dog being held up, in two slender arms, against a bank of blossoms and bright leaves.", "오늘날에도 제라늄 잎의 향기를 맡으면, 한순간에 나에게 무슨 변화가 일어났던 것인지 반쯤은 우스꽝스럽고 반쯤은 진지한 경이로움이 나를 덮친다. 그러면 밀짚모자와 파란 리본, 그리고 한 무더기의 곱슬머리가 보이고, 흐드러진 꽃과 환한 잎사귀를 배경으로, 가느다란 두 팔에 안겨 들어 올려진 작고 까만 개가 보인다."]], [["Miss Murdstone had been looking for us.", "머드스톤 양이 우리를 찾고 있었다."], ["She found us here; and presented her uncongenial cheek, the little wrinkles in it filled with hair powder, to Dora to be kissed.", "그녀는 여기서 우리를 찾아냈다. 그러고는 잔주름마다 머릿가루가 들어찬 그 무뚝뚝한 뺨을, 도라에게 입맞춤하라고 내밀었다."], ["Then she took Dora’s arm in hers, and marched us into breakfast as if it were a soldier’s funeral.", "그런 다음 도라의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼더니, 마치 군인의 장례 행렬이라도 되는 듯 우리를 이끌고 아침 식탁으로 행군해 갔다."]], [["How many cups of tea I drank, because Dora made it, I don’t know.", "도라가 차를 따라 준다는 이유로 내가 차를 몇 잔이나 마셨는지는 모르겠다."], ["But, I perfectly remember that I sat swilling tea until my whole nervous system, if I had had any in those days, must have gone by the board.", "그러나 분명히 기억하는 것은, 내가 차를 들이켜고 또 들이켜며 앉아 있었다는 것인데, 그 시절 나에게 신경계라는 게 있었다면 그 전부가 결딴나 버렸을 정도였다."], ["By and by we went to church.", "이윽고 우리는 교회에 갔다."], ["Miss Murdstone was between Dora and me in the pew; but I heard her sing, and the congregation vanished.", "신도석에서 머드스톤 양이 도라와 나 사이에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 나는 그녀의 노랫소리를 들었고, 그러자 신도들이 죄다 사라져 버렸다."], ["A sermon was delivered--about Dora, of course--and I am afraid that is all I know of the service.", "설교가 전해졌는데 — 물론 도라에 관한 것이었다 — 그 예배에 대해 내가 아는 것이라곤 그게 전부인 듯하다."]], [["We had a quiet day.", "우리는 조용한 하루를 보냈다."], ["No company, a walk, a family dinner of four, and an evening of looking over books and pictures; Miss Murdstone with a homily before her, and her eye upon us, keeping guard vigilantly. Ah! little did Mr.", "손님도 없었고, 산책 한 번, 넷이 둘러앉은 가족 만찬, 그리고 책과 그림을 들여다보는 저녁이 있었다. 머드스톤 양은 앞에 설교집을 펼쳐 놓고, 우리에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 빈틈없이 망을 보았다. 아!"], ["Spenlow imagine, when he sat opposite to me after dinner that day, with his pocket-handkerchief over his head, how fervently I was embracing him, in my fancy, as his son-in-law!", "그날 만찬을 마친 뒤 손수건을 머리에 덮어쓴 채 내 맞은편에 앉아 있던 스펜로 씨는, 내가 공상 속에서 사위로서 그를 얼마나 뜨겁게 끌어안고 있었는지 꿈에도 몰랐으리라!"], ["Little did he think, when I took leave of him at night, that he had just given his full consent to my being engaged to Dora, and that I was invoking blessings on his head!", "밤에 그에게 작별을 고할 때, 그는 자신이 방금 내가 도라와 약혼하는 것에 완전한 동의를 해 준 셈이라는 것도, 내가 그의 머리 위로 온갖 축복을 빌고 있다는 것도 까맣게 몰랐으리라!"]], [["We departed early in the morning, for we had a Salvage case coming on in the Admiralty Court, requiring a rather accurate knowledge of the whole science of navigation, in which (as we couldn’t be expected to know much about those matters in the Commons) the judge had entreated two old Trinity Masters, for charity’s sake, to come and help him out.", "우리는 이른 아침에 떠났다. 해사법원에서 해난 구조 사건 하나가 열릴 예정이었는데, 항해라는 학문 전반에 관한 꽤 정확한 지식을 요하는 사건이라(박사회관에서야 우리가 그런 문제에 대해 잘 알 리가 없으니), 판사가 자비를 베푸는 셈 치고 와서 자기를 좀 도와 달라고 늙은 트리니티 선장 두 명에게 간청해 둔 터였다."], ["Dora was at the breakfast-table to make the tea again, however; and I had the melancholy pleasure of taking off my hat to her in the phaeton, as she stood on the door-step with Jip in her arms.", "그래도 도라는 다시 차를 따라 주려고 아침 식탁에 나와 있었다. 그래서 나는 그녀가 집을 품에 안고 문간 계단에 서 있을 때, 사두마차에서 그녀에게 모자를 벗어 인사하는 우울한 기쁨을 누렸다."]], [["What the Admiralty was to me that day; what nonsense I made of our case in my mind, as I listened to it; how I saw ‘DORA’ engraved upon the blade of the silver oar which they lay upon the table, as the emblem of that high jurisdiction; and how I felt when Mr. Spenlow went home without me (I had had an insane hope that he might take me back again), as if I were a mariner myself, and the ship to which I belonged had sailed away and left me on a desert island; I shall make no fruitless effort to describe.", "그날 해사법원이 나에게 어떤 곳이었는지, 사건 이야기를 들으면서 내가 그것을 마음속에서 얼마나 엉터리로 만들어 버렸는지, 그 높은 재판권의 상징으로 그들이 탁자 위에 올려놓은 은제 노의 날에 '도라'라는 글자가 새겨진 것을 어떻게 보았는지, 그리고 스펜로 씨가 나를 빼놓고 혼자 집으로 돌아갔을 때(나는 그가 나를 다시 데려가 줄지도 모른다는 미친 희망을 품고 있었다) 내가 어떤 심정이었는지 — 마치 내가 한낱 뱃사람이고, 내가 속한 배는 떠나 버려 나를 무인도에 남겨 둔 것만 같았다 — 이 모든 것을 나는 굳이 헛되이 묘사하려 들지 않겠다."], ["If that sleepy old court could rouse itself, and present in any visible form the daydreams I have had in it about Dora, it would reveal my truth.", "만약 그 졸음에 겨운 늙은 법정이 제 몸을 추슬러 일으켜, 내가 그 안에서 도라에 대해 품었던 백일몽을 무슨 눈에 보이는 형태로든 보여 줄 수 있다면, 그것은 내 진심을 드러내 보일 것이다."]], [["I don’t mean the dreams that I dreamed on that day alone, but day after day, from week to week, and term to term.", "내가 말하는 건 그날 하루에 꾼 꿈만이 아니라, 날이면 날마다, 주에서 주로, 개정기에서 개정기로 이어진 꿈이다."], ["I went there, not to attend to what was going on, but to think about Dora.", "나는 거기 가되, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 살피려는 게 아니라 도라를 생각하려고 갔다."], ["If ever I bestowed a thought upon the cases, as they dragged their slow length before me, it was only to wonder, in the matrimonial cases (remembering Dora), how it was that married people could ever be otherwise than happy; and, in the Prerogative cases, to consider, if the money in question had been left to me, what were the foremost steps I should immediately have taken in regard to Dora.", "사건들이 내 앞에서 더디고 길게 끌려갈 때 행여 거기에 한 가닥 생각이라도 쏟았다면, 그건 다만 혼인 사건들에서는(도라를 떠올리며) 결혼한 사람들이 어떻게 행복하지 않을 수가 있는지 의아해하느라, 그리고 유언 검인 사건들에서는, 만약 그 문제의 돈이 나에게 남겨졌다면 내가 도라를 위해 곧장 밟았을 첫 단계가 무엇이었을지 따져 보느라 그랬을 뿐이다."], ["Within the first week of my passion, I bought four sumptuous waistcoats--not for myself; I had no pride in them; for Dora--and took to wearing straw-coloured kid gloves in the streets, and laid the foundations of all the corns I have ever had.", "사랑에 빠진 첫 주 안에 나는 화려한 조끼 네 벌을 샀는데 — 나를 위해서가 아니었다, 나는 그것들이 조금도 자랑스럽지 않았으니까. 도라를 위해서였다 — 그리고 거리에서 밀짚색 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 다니기 시작했으며, 평생 나를 괴롭힐 모든 티눈의 주춧돌을 놓았다."], ["If the boots I wore at that period could only be produced and compared with the natural size of my feet, they would show what the state of my heart was, in a most affecting manner.", "그 시절 내가 신던 구두를 지금 꺼내어 내 발의 실제 크기와 견주어 볼 수만 있다면, 그것은 내 마음이 어떤 상태였는지를 더없이 가슴 저리게 보여 줄 것이다."]], [["And yet, wretched cripple as I made myself by this act of homage to Dora, I walked miles upon miles daily in the hope of seeing her.", "그런데도 도라에게 바치는 이 경배의 행위로 내 몸을 가련한 절름발이로 만들어 가면서도, 나는 그녀를 한 번이라도 볼 수 있을까 하는 희망에 날마다 몇 마일이고 몇 마일이고 걸었다."], ["Not only was I soon as well known on the Norwood Road as the postmen on that beat, but I pervaded London likewise.", "나는 머지않아 노우드 가도에서 그 구역을 도는 우편배달부만큼이나 잘 알려진 얼굴이 되었을 뿐 아니라, 런던 곳곳에도 두루 출몰했다."], ["I walked about the streets where the best shops for ladies were, I haunted the Bazaar like an unquiet spirit, I fagged through the Park again and again, long after I was quite knocked up.", "나는 부인용 좋은 가게들이 늘어선 거리를 거닐었고, 안식을 못 찾는 혼령처럼 잡화시장을 떠돌았으며, 완전히 녹초가 되고도 한참 뒤까지 공원을 거듭거듭 헤집고 다녔다."], ["Sometimes, at long intervals and on rare occasions, I saw her.", "더러, 긴 사이를 두고 드물게나마, 나는 그녀를 보았다."], ["Perhaps I saw her glove waved in a carriage window; perhaps I met her, walked with her and Miss Murdstone a little way, and spoke to her.", "어쩌면 마차 창문으로 흔드는 그녀의 장갑을 보았을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그녀를 만나 그녀와 머드스톤 양과 함께 잠깐 걸으며 그녀에게 말을 건넸을 수도 있다."], ["In the latter case I was always very miserable afterwards, to think that I had said nothing to the purpose; or that she had no idea of the extent of my devotion, or that she cared nothing about me.", "뒤의 경우, 나는 나중에 늘 몹시 비참해졌는데, 내가 핵심이 되는 말은 아무것도 못 했다는 생각에, 혹은 내 헌신이 얼마나 깊은지 그녀가 짐작도 못 한다는 생각에, 혹은 그녀가 나에 대해 아무 관심도 없다는 생각에 그랬다."], ["I was always looking out, as may be supposed, for another invitation to Mr. Spenlow’s house.", "짐작할 수 있듯이, 나는 늘 스펜로 씨 댁으로 또 한 번 초대받기를 눈 빠지게 기다렸다."], ["I was always being disappointed, for I got none.", "그리고 늘 실망했다, 한 번도 초대를 받지 못했으니까."]], [["Mrs. Crupp must have been a woman of penetration; for when this attachment was but a few weeks old, and I had not had the courage to write more explicitly even to Agnes, than that I had been to Mr.", "크럽 부인은 통찰력 있는 여인이었음이 틀림없다. 이 사랑이 생겨난 지 고작 몇 주밖에 안 되었고, 아그네스에게조차 스펜로 씨 댁에 다녀왔다는 것 이상으로는 솔직히 쓸 용기가 나지 않아 \"그 집안 식구는,\""], ["Spenlow’s house, ‘whose family,’ I added, ‘consists of one daughter’;--I say Mrs. Crupp must have been a woman of penetration, for, even in that early stage, she found it out.", "하고 내가 덧붙이기를 \"딸 하나로 이루어져 있다\"라고만 적었을 무렵인데도 — 다시 말하지만 크럽 부인은 통찰력 있는 여인이었음이 틀림없다, 그 이른 단계에서조차 그 사실을 알아챘으니 말이다."], ["She came up to me one evening, when I was very low, to ask (she being then afflicted with the disorder I have mentioned) if I could oblige her with a little tincture of cardamums mixed with rhubarb, and flavoured with seven drops of the essence of cloves, which was the best remedy for her complaint;--or, if I had not such a thing by me, with a little brandy, which was the next best.", "어느 날 저녁, 내가 몹시 풀이 죽어 있을 때 그녀가 올라와서(그때 그녀는 앞서 말한 그 병에 시달리고 있었다), 카더먼 팅크처를 대황과 섞고 정향 정유 일곱 방울로 향을 낸 것을 조금 나누어 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었는데, 그것이 자기 병에 가장 좋은 약이라는 것이었다. 혹시 그런 게 없거든, 그다음으로 좋은 브랜디라도 조금 달라고 했다."], ["It was not, she remarked, so palatable to her, but it was the next best.", "그것이 자기 입맛에 그리 맞지는 않지만 그다음으로 좋은 약이라고 했다."], ["As I had never even heard of the first remedy, and always had the second in the closet, I gave Mrs.", "나는 첫 번째 약은 들어 본 적조차 없었고 두 번째 것은 늘 벽장에 두고 있었으므로, 크럽 부인에게 두 번째 것을 한 잔 주었다."], ["Crupp a glass of the second, which (that I might have no suspicion of its being devoted to any improper use) she began to take in my presence.", "그러자 그녀는(그것이 어떤 부적절한 용도로 쓰이는 게 아닌가 하는 의심을 내가 품지 않도록) 내가 보는 앞에서 그것을 마시기 시작했다."]], [["‘Cheer up, sir,’ said Mrs. Crupp.", "\"기운 내세요, 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다."], ["‘I can’t abear to see you so, sir: I’m a mother myself.’", "\"이렇게 풀 죽은 모습을 보고는 못 견디겠어요, 나리. 저도 한 아이의 어미랍니다.\""]], [["I did not quite perceive the application of this fact to myself, but I smiled on Mrs. Crupp, as benignly as was in my power.", "나는 그 사실이 나에게 어떻게 적용되는지 잘 알 수 없었으나, 그래도 최대한 너그럽게 크럽 부인을 향해 미소 지었다."]], [["‘Come, sir,’ said Mrs. Crupp.", "\"자, 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Excuse me. I know what it is, sir.", "\"외람되지만, 저는 그게 뭔지 알아요, 나리."], ["There’s a lady in the case.’", "여기에는 한 아가씨가 얽혀 있는 거지요.\""]], [["‘Mrs. Crupp?’ I returned, reddening.", "\"크럽 부인?\" 내가 얼굴을 붉히며 응했다."]], [["‘Oh, bless you!", "\"오, 어쩜 그러세요!"], ["Keep a good heart, sir!’ said Mrs. Crupp, nodding encouragement.", "마음 굳게 잡수세요, 나리!\" 크럽 부인이 격려하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘Never say die, sir!", "\"절대 포기하지 마세요, 나리!"], ["If She don’t smile upon you, there’s a many as will.", "그 아가씨가 나리한테 미소 짓지 않더라도, 미소 지어 줄 여자야 수두룩하답니다."], ["You are a young gentleman to be smiled on, Mr. Copperfull, and you must learn your walue, sir.’", "나리는 미소를 받을 만한 젊은 신사분이세요, 코퍼풀 나리, 그러니 나리는 자신의 값어치를 깨달으셔야 해요, 나리.\""]], [["Mrs. Crupp always called me Mr.", "크럽 부인은 늘 나를 코퍼풀 나리라고 불렀다."], ["Copperfull: firstly, no doubt, because it was not my name; and secondly, I am inclined to think, in some indistinct association with a washing-day.", "첫째로는 분명 그게 내 이름이 아니어서였고, 둘째로는 내 짐작에, 어렴풋이나마 빨래하는 날과 무언가 연결 지어 그랬던 것 같다."]], [["‘What makes you suppose there is any young lady in the case, Mrs.", "\"무얼 보고 여기에 어떤 아가씨가 얽혀 있다고 짐작하시는 거예요, 크럽 부인?\""], ["Crupp?’ said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfull,’ said Mrs.", "\"코퍼풀 나리.\""], ["Crupp, with a great deal of feeling, ‘I’m a mother myself.’", "크럽 부인이 자못 절절하게 말했다. \"저도 한 아이의 어미랍니다.\""]], [["For some time Mrs. Crupp could only lay her hand upon her nankeen bosom, and fortify herself against returning pain with sips of her medicine.", "한동안 크럽 부인은 그저 한 손을 자기 무명천 가슴에 얹은 채, 약을 홀짝홀짝 마시며 되돌아오는 통증에 대비해 몸을 다잡을 뿐이었다."], ["At length she spoke again.", "마침내 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["‘When the present set were took for you by your dear aunt, Mr.", "\"나리의 자상하신 고모님께서 나리를 위해 지금 이 방을 잡으셨을 때, 코퍼풀 나리.\""], ["Copperfull,’ said Mrs.", "크럽 부인이 말했다."], ["Crupp, ‘my remark were, I had now found summun I could care for.", "\"제가 한 말이 있어요, 이제야 제가 마음 쓸 만한 분을 찾았다고요."], ["“Thank Ev’in!”", "'하느님 감사합니다!'"], ["were the expression, “I have now found summun I can care for!”--You don’t eat enough, sir, nor yet drink.’", "이게 그때 제가 한 말이었어요, '이제야 제가 마음 쓸 만한 분을 찾았네!' — 나리는 잘 잡숫지도 않으시고, 잘 드시지도 않아요.\""]], [["‘Is that what you found your supposition on, Mrs.", "\"바로 그걸 보고 그렇게 짐작하시는 거예요, 크럽 부인?\""], ["Crupp?’ said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"나리.\""], ["Crupp, in a tone approaching to severity, ‘I’ve laundressed other young gentlemen besides yourself.", "크럽 부인이 자못 매서운 어조로 말했다. \"저는 나리 말고도 여러 젊은 신사분들 빨래를 해 드렸답니다."], ["A young gentleman may be over-careful of himself, or he may be under-careful of himself. He may brush his hair too regular, or too un-regular.", "젊은 신사분은 자기 몸을 너무 챙기시기도 하고, 너무 안 챙기시기도 해요. 머리를 너무 규칙적으로 빗기도 하고, 너무 불규칙하게 빗기도 하고요."], ["He may wear his boots much too large for him, or much too small.", "구두를 자기 발에 너무 큰 걸 신기도 하고, 너무 작은 걸 신기도 하지요."], ["That is according as the young gentleman has his original character formed.", "그건 그 젊은 신사분이 타고난 성품이 어떻게 잡혀 있느냐에 따라 다른 거예요."], ["But let him go to which extreme he may, sir, there’s a young lady in both of ‘em.’", "하지만 어느 쪽 끝으로 가시든, 나리, 그 두 가지 다에 한 아가씨가 얽혀 있는 거랍니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Crupp shook her head in such a determined manner, that I had not an inch of vantage-ground left.", "크럽 부인이 어찌나 단호하게 고개를 가로젓던지, 나에게는 발 디딜 한 치의 유리한 자리도 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["‘It was but the gentleman which died here before yourself,’ said Mrs.", "\"바로 나리보다 먼저 이 집에서 돌아가신 그 신사분도요.\" 크럽 부인이 말했다."], ["Crupp, ‘that fell in love--with a barmaid--and had his waistcoats took in directly, though much swelled by drinking.’", "\"사랑에 빠지셨거든요 — 술집 아가씨한테요 — 그러고는 술을 많이 드셔서 한껏 부풀어 올랐는데도 곧장 조끼를 줄이게 하셨답니다.\""]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"크럽 부인.\""], ["Crupp,’ said I, ‘I must beg you not to connect the young lady in my case with a barmaid, or anything of that sort, if you please.’", "내가 말했다. \"부탁이니, 제 경우의 그 아가씨를 술집 아가씨나 그 비슷한 것과 엮지 말아 주세요, 제발.\""]], [["‘Mr. Copperfull,’ returned Mrs.", "\"코퍼풀 나리.\" 크럽 부인이 응했다."], ["Crupp, ‘I’m a mother myself, and not likely.", "\"저도 한 아이의 어미인데, 그럴 리가요."], ["I ask your pardon, sir, if I intrude.", "외람되었다면 용서하세요, 나리."], ["I should never wish to intrude where I were not welcome.", "저는 결코 환영받지 못할 데에 끼어들고 싶지는 않답니다."], ["But you are a young gentleman, Mr. Copperfull, and my adwice to you is, to cheer up, sir, to keep a good heart, and to know your own walue.", "하지만 나리는 젊은 신사분이세요, 코퍼풀 나리, 그러니 제가 드리는 충고는, 기운을 내시고, 나리, 마음을 굳게 잡수시고, 자신의 값어치를 아시라는 거예요."], ["If you was to take to something, sir,’ said Mrs.", "만약 나리가 무언가에 빠져 보신다면, 나리.\""], ["Crupp, ‘if you was to take to skittles, now, which is healthy, you might find it divert your mind, and do you good.’", "크럽 부인이 말했다. \"이를테면 구주희에라도 빠져 보신다면, 그건 몸에도 좋으니, 마음이 풀리고 나리한테도 도움이 될 거예요.\""]], [["With these words, Mrs. Crupp, affecting to be very careful of the brandy--which was all gone--thanked me with a majestic curtsey, and retired.", "이 말과 함께 크럽 부인은 브랜디를 — 다 비워 버렸으면서 — 무척 아끼는 척하며, 위풍당당한 절로 나에게 고마움을 표하고 물러갔다."], ["As her figure disappeared into the gloom of the entry, this counsel certainly presented itself to my mind in the light of a slight liberty on Mrs.", "그녀의 모습이 어둑한 입구 속으로 사라질 때, 이 충고는 분명 크럽 부인 쪽에서 약간 주제넘게 군 처사로 내 마음에 비쳤다."], ["Crupp’s part; but, at the same time, I was content to receive it, in another point of view, as a word to the wise, and a warning in future to keep my secret better.", "그러나 동시에 나는 그것을, 다른 관점에서 보아, 지혜로운 자에게 건네는 한마디이자 앞으로 내 비밀을 좀 더 잘 간수하라는 경고로 기꺼이 받아들였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 27. TOMMY TRADDLES", "# CHAPTER 27. TOMMY TRADDLES"]], [["It may have been in consequence of Mrs. Crupp’s advice, and, perhaps, for no better reason than because there was a certain similarity in the sound of the word skittles and Traddles, that it came into my head, next day, to go and look after Traddles.", "크럽 부인의 충고 때문이었는지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그저 스키틀즈라는 낱말과 트래들스라는 이름의 소리가 어딘가 닮았다는 것보다 더 그럴듯한 이유가 없었는지도 모르지만, 어쨌든 이튿날 나는 트래들스를 찾아가 봐야겠다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐다."], ["The time he had mentioned was more than out, and he lived in a little street near the Veterinary College at Camden Town, which was principally tenanted, as one of our clerks who lived in that direction informed me, by gentlemen students, who bought live donkeys, and made experiments on those quadrupeds in their private apartments.", "그가 말했던 기한은 이미 한참 지나 있었고, 그는 캠던타운의 수의학 대학 근처 작은 거리에 살고 있었다. 그쪽 방면에 사는 우리 사무소 직원 하나가 일러 주기로, 그 거리에는 주로 신사 학생들이 세 들어 사는데, 그들이 살아 있는 당나귀를 사다가 자기 방에서 그 네발짐승을 가지고 실험을 한다고 했다."], ["Having obtained from this clerk a direction to the academic grove in question, I set out, the same afternoon, to visit my old schoolfellow.", "그 직원에게서 문제의 학구적인 동네로 가는 길을 알아낸 뒤, 나는 바로 그날 오후에 옛 학교 친구를 찾아 나섰다."]], [["I found that the street was not as desirable a one as I could have wished it to be, for the sake of Traddles.", "그 거리는, 트래들스를 생각하면, 내가 바랐던 만큼 마음에 드는 곳이 아니었다."], ["The inhabitants appeared to have a propensity to throw any little trifles they were not in want of, into the road: which not only made it rank and sloppy, but untidy too, on account of the cabbage-leaves.", "그곳 주민들은 자기에게 필요 없는 자질구레한 것들을 죄다 길바닥에 내던지는 버릇이 있는 듯했는데, 그래서 거리는 고약한 냄새가 나고 질척거릴 뿐 아니라, 양배추 잎사귀 때문에 지저분하기까지 했다."], ["The refuse was not wholly vegetable either, for I myself saw a shoe, a doubled-up saucepan, a black bonnet, and an umbrella, in various stages of decomposition, as I was looking out for the number I wanted.", "게다가 쓰레기가 죄다 채소만은 아니었으니, 내가 찾는 번지를 두리번거리며 살피는 동안 신발 한 짝, 구겨진 냄비 하나, 검은 보닛 하나, 그리고 우산 하나가 저마다 다른 정도로 썩어 가는 꼴을 내 눈으로 똑똑히 보았다."]], [["The general air of the place reminded me forcibly of the days when I lived with Mr. and Mrs.", "그곳의 전반적인 분위기는 내가 미코버 씨 내외와 함께 살던 시절을 강렬하게 떠올리게 했다."], ["Micawber. An indescribable character of faded gentility that attached to the house I sought, and made it unlike all the other houses in the street--though they were all built on one monotonous pattern, and looked like the early copies of a blundering boy who was learning to make houses, and had not yet got out of his cramped brick-and-mortar pothooks--reminded me still more of Mr.", "내가 찾는 그 집에는 뭐라 형용할 수 없는, 빛바랜 점잖음의 기색이 배어 있어서, 그 거리의 다른 집들과는 사뭇 달라 보였다. 그 거리의 집들은 죄다 단조롭기 짝이 없는 한 가지 본을 따라 지어져, 마치 집 그리기를 배우는 서툰 사내아이가 벽돌과 회반죽으로 어설픈 갈고리 모양을 끄적이던 버릇에서 아직 벗어나지 못한 채 그려 낸 초기 습작들처럼 보였는데 말이다."], ["and Mrs. Micawber. Happening to arrive at the door as it was opened to the afternoon milkman, I was reminded of Mr.", "그 빛바랜 분위기가 나에게 미코버 씨 내외를 더더욱 또렷이 떠올리게 했다."], ["and Mrs. Micawber more forcibly yet.", "마침 오후의 우유 배달부에게 문이 열리는 순간 그 집 앞에 다다른 나는 미코버 씨 내외가 한층 더 강렬하게 떠올랐다."]], [["‘Now,’ said the milkman to a very youthful servant girl.", "\"이봐,\" 우유 배달부가 아주 어린 하녀에게 말했다."], ["‘Has that there little bill of mine been heerd on?’", "\"내 그 외상 장부 말이야, 무슨 소식이라도 들었나?\""]], [["‘Oh, master says he’ll attend to it immediate,’ was the reply.", "\"아, 주인님이 당장 처리하시겠다고 하셨어요.\" 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Because,’ said the milkman, going on as if he had received no answer, and speaking, as I judged from his tone, rather for the edification of somebody within the house, than of the youthful servant--an impression which was strengthened by his manner of glaring down the passage--‘because that there little bill has been running so long, that I begin to believe it’s run away altogether, and never won’t be heerd of.", "\"그게 말이지,\" 우유 배달부는 아무 대답도 못 들은 양 말을 이어 갔는데, 그 말투로 미루어 보아 어린 하녀보다는 집 안에 있는 누군가에게 일러 주려고 하는 말 같았다 — 통로 안쪽을 쏘아보는 그 태도가 그런 인상을 더욱 굳혔다 — \"그게 말이지, 그 외상 장부가 어찌나 오래 굴러가는지, 이제 아주 영영 도망쳐서 다시는 소식도 못 듣게 생긴 것 같단 말씀이야."], ["Now, I’m not a going to stand it, you know!’ said the milkman, still throwing his voice into the house, and glaring down the passage.", "자, 나는 그런 꼴 못 참아, 알겠어!\" 우유 배달부는 여전히 목소리를 집 안으로 던지며, 통로 안쪽을 노려보면서 말했다."]], [["As to his dealing in the mild article of milk, by the by, there never was a greater anomaly.", "말이 난 김에 덧붙이자면, 우유처럼 순한 물건을 파는 사람치고 이보다 더 어울리지 않는 자도 없었다."], ["His deportment would have been fierce in a butcher or a brandy-merchant.", "그 사나운 거동은 푸줏간 주인이나 브랜디 장수에게나 어울렸을 법했다."]], [["The voice of the youthful servant became faint, but she seemed to me, from the action of her lips, again to murmur that it would be attended to immediate.", "어린 하녀의 목소리는 가물가물해졌지만, 그 입술이 움직이는 모양으로 보아 또다시 당장 처리될 거라고 중얼거리는 듯했다."]], [["‘I tell you what,’ said the milkman, looking hard at her for the first time, and taking her by the chin, ‘are you fond of milk?’", "\"내 한마디 하지,\" 우유 배달부가 그제야 처음으로 그 애를 빤히 쏘아보며, 턱을 잡고 말했다. \"너 우유 좋아하냐?\""]], [["‘Yes, I likes it,’ she replied.", "\"네, 좋아해요.\" 그 애가 대답했다. \"좋아.\""], ["‘Good,’ said the milkman.", "우유 배달부가 말했다."], ["‘Then you won’t have none tomorrow.", "\"그럼 내일은 한 방울도 못 먹을 줄 알아."], ["D’ye hear?", "알아들었어?"], ["Not a fragment of milk you won’t have tomorrow.’", "내일은 우유 부스러기 하나도 못 받을 거야.\""]], [["I thought she seemed, upon the whole, relieved by the prospect of having any today.", "내 보기에 그 애는 대체로, 오늘만이라도 우유를 받을 수 있다는 사실에 오히려 안도하는 눈치였다."], ["The milkman, after shaking his head at her darkly, released her chin, and with anything rather than good-will opened his can, and deposited the usual quantity in the family jug.", "우유 배달부는 음침하게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 나서 턱을 놓아 주더니, 호의라고는 도무지 찾아볼 수 없는 태도로 통을 열어 그 집 항아리에 늘 받던 만큼을 부어 넣었다."], ["This done, he went away, muttering, and uttered the cry of his trade next door, in a vindictive shriek.", "그러고는 투덜거리며 떠나갔고, 옆집 앞에서 앙심에 찬 비명 같은 소리로 자기 장사 외치는 소리를 질러 댔다."]], [["‘Does Mr. Traddles live here?’ I then inquired.", "\"트래들스 씨가 여기 사나요?\" 내가 그제야 물었다."]], [["A mysterious voice from the end of the passage replied ‘Yes.’ Upon which the youthful servant replied ‘Yes.’", "통로 끝에서 정체 모를 목소리가 \"네\" 하고 대답했다. 그러자 어린 하녀가 \"네\" 하고 따라 했다."]], [["‘Is he at home?’ said I.", "\"집에 계시나요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["Again the mysterious voice replied in the affirmative, and again the servant echoed it.", "다시 그 정체 모를 목소리가 그렇다고 대답했고, 다시 하녀가 그 말을 메아리처럼 따라 했다."], ["Upon this, I walked in, and in pursuance of the servant’s directions walked upstairs; conscious, as I passed the back parlour-door, that I was surveyed by a mysterious eye, probably belonging to the mysterious voice.", "그래서 나는 안으로 들어가, 하녀가 일러 주는 대로 위층으로 올라갔다. 뒤편 거실 문을 지날 때, 아마도 그 정체 모를 목소리의 임자일 정체 모를 눈 하나가 나를 살피고 있다는 것을 의식하면서."]], [["When I got to the top of the stairs--the house was only a story high above the ground floor--Traddles was on the landing to meet me.", "계단 꼭대기에 다다랐을 때 — 그 집은 일 층 위로 딱 한 층밖에 없었다 — 트래들스가 층계참에서 나를 맞으러 나와 있었다."], ["He was delighted to see me, and gave me welcome, with great heartiness, to his little room.", "그는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐하며, 진심 어린 환대로 자기 작은 방으로 맞아들였다."], ["It was in the front of the house, and extremely neat, though sparely furnished.", "그 방은 집 앞쪽에 있었고, 가구는 단출했지만 무척이나 깔끔했다."], ["It was his only room, I saw; for there was a sofa-bedstead in it, and his blacking-brushes and blacking were among his books--on the top shelf, behind a dictionary.", "보아하니 그가 가진 방은 그것 하나뿐이었으니, 방 안에 소파 겸 침대가 놓여 있었고, 그의 구두약 솔과 구두약은 책들 틈에 — 맨 위 선반 사전 뒤에 — 끼어 있었다."], ["His table was covered with papers, and he was hard at work in an old coat.", "그의 탁자는 서류로 뒤덮여 있었고, 그는 낡은 외투를 걸친 채 한창 일에 매달려 있었다."], ["I looked at nothing, that I know of, but I saw everything, even to the prospect of a church upon his china inkstand, as I sat down--and this, too, was a faculty confirmed in me in the old Micawber times.", "내가 무엇을 유심히 본 것은 아니었지만, 자리에 앉으면서 나는 모든 것을, 그의 도자기 잉크스탠드에 그려진 교회 풍경까지도 다 보았다 — 이 또한 옛 미코버 시절에 내 안에 굳어진 능력이었다."], ["Various ingenious arrangements he had made, for the disguise of his chest of drawers, and the accommodation of his boots, his shaving-glass, and so forth, particularly impressed themselves upon me, as evidences of the same Traddles who used to make models of elephants’ dens in writing-paper to put flies in; and to comfort himself under ill usage, with the memorable works of art I have so often mentioned.", "그가 서랍장을 가리고, 장화와 면도 거울 따위를 갈무리하려고 이리저리 궁리해서 마련한 여러 가지 솜씨가 유독 내 마음에 와닿았으니, 이는 옛날에 편지지로 파리를 넣어 둘 코끼리 굴 모형을 만들고, 모진 대접을 받을 때면 내가 앞서 그토록 자주 이야기한 그 잊지 못할 예술품을 만들며 스스로를 달래던 바로 그 트래들스가 여전하다는 증거였다."]], [["In a corner of the room was something neatly covered up with a large white cloth. I could not make out what that was.", "방 한구석에는 무언가가 커다란 흰 천으로 말끔히 덮여 있었다. 나는 그게 무엇인지 도무지 알아볼 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Traddles,’ said I, shaking hands with him again, after I had sat down, ‘I am delighted to see you.’", "\"트래들스,\" 자리에 앉은 뒤 다시 그와 악수하며 내가 말했다. \"너를 보니 정말 반갑다.\""]], [["‘I am delighted to see YOU, Copperfield,’ he returned.", "\"나야말로 너를 보니 반갑다, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘I am very glad indeed to see you.", "\"정말이지 너를 보니 기뻐."], ["It was because I was thoroughly glad to see you when we met in Ely Place, and was sure you were thoroughly glad to see me, that I gave you this address instead of my address at chambers.’ ‘Oh!", "일리 플레이스에서 우리가 마주쳤을 때, 내가 너를 보고 진심으로 기뻤고, 너도 나를 보고 진심으로 기뻐했다고 확신했기에, 사무실 주소 대신 이 주소를 알려 준 거야.\" \"아!"], ["You have chambers?’ said I.", "너 사무실도 있어?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, I have the fourth of a room and a passage, and the fourth of a clerk,’ returned Traddles.", "\"뭐, 방 하나에 통로 하나를 사 분의 일씩 나눠 쓰고, 사무원도 사 분의 일만 쓰는 셈이지.\" 트래들스가 대답했다."], ["‘Three others and myself unite to have a set of chambers--to look business-like--and we quarter the clerk too.", "\"나 말고 셋이 더 합쳐서 사무실 한 칸을 같이 쓰거든 — 그래야 일하는 티가 나니까 — 사무원도 넷이 나눠 쓴단 말이야."], ["Half-a-crown a week he costs me.’", "나한테는 일주일에 반 크라운씩 드는 셈이지.\""]], [["His old simple character and good temper, and something of his old unlucky fortune also, I thought, smiled at me in the smile with which he made this explanation.", "그가 이 설명을 하며 짓는 그 미소 속에서, 나는 그의 옛날 그대로의 순박한 성품과 너그러운 성미가, 그리고 또 그의 옛날 그대로의 박복한 운수마저 나를 향해 빙긋 웃고 있는 것 같았다."]], [["‘It’s not because I have the least pride, Copperfield, you understand,’ said Traddles, ‘that I don’t usually give my address here.", "\"내가 무슨 자존심 같은 게 있어서, 코퍼필드, 그래서 여기 주소를 잘 안 알려 주는 게 아니란 건 너도 알 거야.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["It’s only on account of those who come to me, who might not like to come here.", "\"그저 나를 찾아오는 사람들 때문이지. 그 사람들이 여기 오는 걸 별로 안 좋아할지도 모르니까."], ["For myself, I am fighting my way on in the world against difficulties, and it would be ridiculous if I made a pretence of doing anything else.’", "나 자신으로 말하자면, 나는 온갖 어려움과 맞서 가며 세상을 헤쳐 나가고 있고, 그렇지 않은 척 시늉을 한다면 그거야말로 우스운 일이지.\""]], [["‘You are reading for the bar, Mr. Waterbrook informed me?’ said I.", "\"법정 변호사가 되려고 공부하고 있다고, 워터브룩 씨가 알려 주던데?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, yes,’ said Traddles, rubbing his hands slowly over one another.", "\"음, 그래.\" 트래들스가 두 손을 천천히 비비며 말했다."], ["‘I am reading for the bar. The fact is, I have just begun to keep my terms, after rather a long delay.", "\"법정 변호사가 되려고 공부 중이야. 사실은, 꽤 오래 미뤄 두었다가 이제 막 학기 출석을 시작했어."], ["It’s some time since I was articled, but the payment of that hundred pounds was a great pull.", "견습 계약을 맺은 지는 한참 됐는데, 그 백 파운드 치르는 게 어찌나 힘들던지."], ["A great pull!’ said Traddles, with a wince, as if he had had a tooth out.", "정말 죽을 맛이었어!\" 트래들스가 마치 이를 뽑기라도 한 듯 움찔하며 말했다."]], [["‘Do you know what I can’t help thinking of, Traddles, as I sit here looking at you?’ I asked him.", "\"트래들스, 여기 앉아서 너를 보고 있으니 자꾸 떠오르는 게 뭔지 알아?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ said he.", "\"아니.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘That sky-blue suit you used to wear.’", "\"네가 입던 그 하늘색 양복.\""]], [["‘Lord, to be sure!’ cried Traddles, laughing.", "\"이런, 그래 맞아!\" 트래들스가 웃으며 외쳤다."], ["‘Tight in the arms and legs, you know? Dear me! Well!", "\"팔이랑 다리가 꽉 끼던 거 말이지? 이런! 참!"], ["Those were happy times, weren’t they?’", "그때가 행복한 시절이었어, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘I think our schoolmaster might have made them happier, without doing any harm to any of us, I acknowledge,’ I returned.", "\"우리 교장 선생님이 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않고도 우리를 좀 더 행복하게 해 줄 수 있었을 거라고 나는 생각하지만 말이야.\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘Perhaps he might,’ said Traddles.", "\"그럴 수도 있었겠지.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘But dear me, there was a good deal of fun going on.", "\"하지만 이런, 그래도 재미난 일이 꽤 많았잖아."], ["Do you remember the nights in the bedroom?", "침실에서 보내던 밤들 기억나?"], ["When we used to have the suppers?", "우리가 야식 먹던 거 말이야."], ["And when you used to tell the stories?", "네가 이야기 들려주던 거? 하하하!"], ["Ha, ha, ha! And do you remember when I got caned for crying about Mr. Mell?", "그리고 내가 멜 선생님 일로 울다가 매 맞은 거 기억나?"], ["Old Creakle!", "늙은 크리클!"], ["I should like to see him again, too!’", "그 양반도 다시 한번 보고 싶다니까!\""]], [["‘He was a brute to you, Traddles,’ said I, indignantly; for his good humour made me feel as if I had seen him beaten but yesterday.", "\"그 사람은 너한테 짐승 같은 짓을 했어, 트래들스.\" 내가 분개해서 말했다. 그가 워낙 좋은 기분으로 말하니, 마치 어제 그가 매 맞는 꼴을 본 것처럼 느껴졌던 것이다."]], [["‘Do you think so?’ returned Traddles. ‘Really?", "\"그렇게 생각해?\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"정말?"], ["Perhaps he was rather.", "하긴 좀 그랬을지도 모르지."], ["But it’s all over, a long while.", "하지만 다 끝난 일이야, 한참 전에."], ["Old Creakle!’", "늙은 크리클!\""]], [["‘You were brought up by an uncle, then?’ said I.", "\"그럼 너는 큰아버지 손에서 자란 거지?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Of course I was!’ said Traddles.", "\"그럼, 그렇고말고!\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘The one I was always going to write to.", "\"내가 늘 편지 쓰겠다고 하던 그 양반."], ["And always didn’t, eh! Ha, ha, ha!", "그러고는 늘 안 썼던 그분 말이야, 응! 하하하!"], ["Yes, I had an uncle then.", "그래, 그때는 큰아버지가 계셨지."], ["He died soon after I left school.’", "내가 학교를 떠나고 얼마 안 돼서 돌아가셨어.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’", "\"저런!\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"응."], ["He was a retired--what do you call it!--draper--cloth-merchant--and had made me his heir.", "은퇴한 — 그걸 뭐라고 부르더라! — 포목상 — 옷감 장수였는데 — 나를 상속자로 삼으셨지."], ["But he didn’t like me when I grew up.’", "그런데 내가 다 크고 나니까 나를 안 좋아하시더라고.\""]], [["‘Do you really mean that?’ said I.", "\"정말 그렇다는 거야?\" 내가 말했다."], ["He was so composed, that I fancied he must have some other meaning.", "그가 어찌나 태연하던지, 무슨 다른 뜻이 있는 게 아닌가 싶었던 것이다."]], [["‘Oh dear, yes, Copperfield!", "\"아이고, 그렇다니까, 코퍼필드!"], ["I mean it,’ replied Traddles.", "정말이야.\" 트래들스가 대답했다."], ["‘It was an unfortunate thing, but he didn’t like me at all.", "\"안된 일이었지만, 그분이 나를 도무지 좋아하지 않으셨어."], ["He said I wasn’t at all what he expected, and so he married his housekeeper.’", "내가 당신 기대와는 전혀 딴판이라고 하시더니, 그래서 가정부랑 결혼해 버리셨지.\""]], [["‘And what did you do?’ I asked.", "\"그래서 너는 어떻게 했는데?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I didn’t do anything in particular,’ said Traddles.", "\"딱히 뭘 한 건 없어.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘I lived with them, waiting to be put out in the world, until his gout unfortunately flew to his stomach--and so he died, and so she married a young man, and so I wasn’t provided for.’", "\"세상에 내보내질 때를 기다리면서 그분들이랑 같이 살았지. 그러다 안타깝게도 그분 통풍이 위장으로 번지는 바람에 — 그래서 돌아가셨고, 그래서 그 부인은 젊은 남자랑 결혼했고, 그래서 나는 아무것도 못 받게 됐어.\""]], [["‘Did you get nothing, Traddles, after all?’", "\"결국 아무것도 못 받았단 말이야, 트래들스?\""]], [["‘Oh dear, yes!’ said Traddles.", "\"아이고, 받긴 받았지!\""], ["‘I got fifty pounds.", "트래들스가 말했다."], ["I had never been brought up to any profession, and at first I was at a loss what to do for myself.", "\"오십 파운드 받았어. 나는 어떤 직업으로도 길러진 적이 없어서, 처음엔 무얼 해야 할지 막막했지."], ["However, I began, with the assistance of the son of a professional man, who had been to Salem House--Yawler, with his nose on one side.", "그래도 살렘 하우스를 다녔던 어느 전문직 양반의 아들 — 야울러라고, 코가 한쪽으로 비뚤어진 녀석 도움으로 시작했어."], ["Do you recollect him?’", "그 녀석 기억나?\""]], [["No.", "아니."], ["He had not been there with me; all the noses were straight in my day.", "그는 나와 같은 시절에 있지 않았다. 내가 다니던 시절에는 코들이 다 곧았다."]], [["‘It don’t matter,’ said Traddles.", "\"뭐 상관없어.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘I began, by means of his assistance, to copy law writings.", "\"그 녀석 도움으로, 나는 법률 서류를 베껴 쓰는 일부터 시작했어."], ["That didn’t answer very well; and then I began to state cases for them, and make abstracts, and that sort of work.", "그게 그리 신통치 않아서, 그 다음엔 사건 개요를 정리해 주고, 발췌문을 만들고, 그런 일을 하기 시작했지."], ["For I am a plodding kind of fellow, Copperfield, and had learnt the way of doing such things pithily. Well!", "나는 우직하게 매달리는 부류라서 말이야, 코퍼필드, 그런 일을 요점만 추려서 하는 법을 익혀 뒀거든. 자!"], ["That put it in my head to enter myself as a law student; and that ran away with all that was left of the fifty pounds.", "그러다 보니 나도 법학생으로 등록해 볼까 하는 생각이 들었고, 그게 오십 파운드 남은 걸 죄다 잡아먹어 버렸어."], ["Yawler recommended me to one or two other offices, however--Mr. Waterbrook’s for one--and I got a good many jobs.", "그래도 야울러가 사무소 한두 군데를 더 소개해 줬는데 — 워터브룩 씨 사무소도 그중 하나야 — 그래서 일거리를 꽤 많이 얻었지."], ["I was fortunate enough, too, to become acquainted with a person in the publishing way, who was getting up an Encyclopaedia, and he set me to work; and, indeed’ (glancing at his table), ‘I am at work for him at this minute.", "게다가 운 좋게도 출판 쪽에 종사하는 사람 하나와 알게 됐는데, 백과사전을 만들던 참이라, 나한테 일을 맡기더라고. 그리고 사실,\" (탁자를 흘끗 보며) \"바로 지금도 그 사람 일을 하고 있는 중이야."], ["I am not a bad compiler, Copperfield,’ said Traddles, preserving the same air of cheerful confidence in all he said, ‘but I have no invention at all; not a particle.", "나는 편찬자로서는 그리 나쁘지 않아, 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 무슨 말을 하든 한결같이 쾌활하고 자신 있는 태도를 잃지 않으며 말했다. \"그런데 창의력이라곤 도통 없어."], ["I suppose there never was a young man with less originality than I have.’", "한 톨도 없지. 나만큼 독창성 없는 젊은이는 세상에 또 없을걸.\""]], [["As Traddles seemed to expect that I should assent to this as a matter of course, I nodded; and he went on, with the same sprightly patience--I can find no better expression--as before.", "트래들스가 이 말에 내가 당연히 맞장구쳐 주기를 바라는 눈치였으므로, 나는 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러자 그는 아까처럼 한결같이 쾌활하면서도 참을성 있는 태도로 — 이보다 더 나은 표현을 못 찾겠다 — 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘So, by little and little, and not living high, I managed to scrape up the hundred pounds at last,’ said Traddles; ‘and thank Heaven that’s paid--though it was--though it certainly was,’ said Traddles, wincing again as if he had had another tooth out, ‘a pull.", "\"그래서, 조금씩 조금씩, 호화롭게 살지 않으면서, 결국 마침내 백 파운드를 긁어모았어.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그리고 다행히 그건 다 갚았지 — 비록 그게 — 정말이지 그게,\""], ["I am living by the sort of work I have mentioned, still, and I hope, one of these days, to get connected with some newspaper: which would almost be the making of my fortune.", "트래들스가 또 이를 뽑은 사람처럼 움찔하며 말했다. \"죽을 맛이었지만 말이야. 나는 지금도 아까 말한 그런 일로 먹고살고 있고, 언젠가는 어느 신문사하고 연줄이 닿았으면 해."], ["Now, Copperfield, you are so exactly what you used to be, with that agreeable face, and it’s so pleasant to see you, that I sha’n’t conceal anything.", "그렇게 되면 거의 출셋길이 트이는 셈이거든. 자, 코퍼필드, 너는 그 사람 좋은 얼굴 그대로, 옛날하고 어찌나 똑같은지, 너를 보고 있는 게 너무 즐거워서 나는 아무것도 숨기지 않을 거야."], ["Therefore you must know that I am engaged.’", "그러니까 너도 알아 둬야 해, 나 약혼했다는 걸.\""]], [["Engaged! Oh, Dora!", "약혼! 아, 도라!"]], [["‘She is a curate’s daughter,’ said Traddles; ‘one of ten, down in Devonshire.", "\"그 사람은 부목사의 딸이야.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"데번셔 시골에 사는, 열 남매 중 하나지. 그래!\""], ["Yes!’ For he saw me glance, involuntarily, at the prospect on the inkstand.", "그가 내 눈이 저도 모르게 잉크스탠드의 그 풍경으로 흘끗 향하는 것을 보았던 것이다."], ["‘That’s the church!", "\"그게 바로 그 교회야!"], ["You come round here to the left, out of this gate,’ tracing his finger along the inkstand, ‘and exactly where I hold this pen, there stands the house--facing, you understand, towards the church.’", "이 문에서 나와서 이쪽 왼쪽으로 돌아오면,\" 손가락으로 잉크스탠드를 짚어 가며, \"내가 지금 이 펜을 쥐고 있는 바로 이 자리에, 그 집이 서 있어 — 그러니까, 교회 쪽을 마주 보고 말이지.\""]], [["The delight with which he entered into these particulars, did not fully present itself to me until afterwards; for my selfish thoughts were making a ground-plan of Mr. Spenlow’s house and garden at the same moment.", "그가 이런 세세한 이야기에 푹 빠져 드는 그 기쁨이 나에게 온전히 와닿은 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다. 바로 그 순간 내 이기적인 생각은 스펜로 씨의 집과 정원의 평면도를 그리고 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘She is such a dear girl!’ said Traddles; ‘a little older than me, but the dearest girl!", "\"그 사람은 정말 사랑스러운 아가씨야!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"나보다 나이가 조금 많지만, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라니까!"], ["I told you I was going out of town?", "내가 시외로 나간다고 했었지?"], ["I have been down there.", "거기 다녀온 거야."], ["I walked there, and I walked back, and I had the most delightful time!", "걸어서 갔다가 걸어서 돌아왔는데, 더없이 즐거운 시간을 보냈어!"], ["I dare say ours is likely to be a rather long engagement, but our motto is “Wait and hope!”", "우리 약혼은 아마 꽤 길어질 것 같지만, 우리 좌우명이 '기다리며 바라자!'거든."], ["We always say that.", "우린 늘 그렇게 말해."], ["“Wait and hope,” we always say.", "'기다리며 바라자', 늘 그렇게 말하지."], ["And she would wait, Copperfield, till she was sixty--any age you can mention--for me!’", "그리고 그 사람은 나를 위해서라면, 코퍼필드, 예순 살이 될 때까지라도 — 네가 댈 수 있는 어떤 나이까지라도 기다려 줄 거야!\""]], [["Traddles rose from his chair, and, with a triumphant smile, put his hand upon the white cloth I had observed.", "트래들스는 의자에서 일어나, 의기양양한 미소를 띠고, 아까 내가 눈여겨보았던 그 흰 천 위에 손을 얹었다."]], [["‘However,’ he said, ‘it’s not that we haven’t made a beginning towards housekeeping.", "\"하지만 말이야,\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 살림 채비를 아예 시작도 안 한 건 아니야."], ["No, no; we have begun.", "아니, 아니지. 우린 시작했어."], ["We must get on by degrees, but we have begun.", "차차 늘려 가야겠지만, 어쨌든 시작은 한 거야. 자,\""], ["Here,’ drawing the cloth off with great pride and care, ‘are two pieces of furniture to commence with.", "무척이나 자랑스럽고 조심스럽게 천을 걷어 내며, \"이게 살림 밑천으로 마련한 가구 두 점이야."], ["This flower-pot and stand, she bought herself.", "이 화분이랑 받침대는 그 사람이 직접 산 거고."], ["You put that in a parlour window,’ said Traddles, falling a little back from it to survey it with the greater admiration, ‘with a plant in it, and--and there you are!", "이걸 거실 창가에 놓고,\" 트래들스가 그것을 더 흐뭇하게 바라보려고 조금 물러서며 말했다. \"거기다 화초를 심으면 — 그럼 끝나는 거야!"], ["This little round table with the marble top (it’s two feet ten in circumference), I bought.", "이 대리석 상판을 얹은 자그마한 둥근 탁자는 (둘레가 두 자 열 치야) 내가 샀지."], ["You want to lay a book down, you know, or somebody comes to see you or your wife, and wants a place to stand a cup of tea upon, and--and there you are again!’ said Traddles.", "책을 한 권 내려놓고 싶다든가, 아니면 누가 너나 네 아내를 보러 와서 찻잔 놓을 자리가 필요하다든가 — 그럼 또 그걸로 끝나는 거지!\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘It’s an admirable piece of workmanship--firm as a rock!’ I praised them both, highly, and Traddles replaced the covering as carefully as he had removed it.", "\"정말 솜씨가 기막힌 물건이야 — 바위처럼 든든하다니까!\" 나는 그 둘 다를 한껏 칭찬했고, 트래들스는 걷어 냈던 것만큼이나 조심스럽게 천을 다시 덮어 두었다."]], [["‘It’s not a great deal towards the furnishing,’ said Traddles, ‘but it’s something.", "\"살림 채비로 치자면 대단한 건 아니지만,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그래도 어디야."], ["The table-cloths, and pillow-cases, and articles of that kind, are what discourage me most, Copperfield.", "식탁보랑 베갯잇이랑, 그런 종류 물건들이 제일 기를 죽인단 말이야, 코퍼필드."], ["So does the ironmongery--candle-boxes, and gridirons, and that sort of necessaries--because those things tell, and mount up.", "철물도 그렇지 — 양초 상자랑 석쇠랑, 그런 살림 필수품들 말이야 — 그런 게 돈이 들어서 쌓이고 쌓이거든. 그래도, '기다리며 바라자!'야."], ["However, “wait and hope!” And I assure you she’s the dearest girl!’", "그리고 정말이지 그 사람은 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라니까!\""]], [["‘I am quite certain of it,’ said I.", "\"나도 그건 아주 확신해.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘In the meantime,’ said Traddles, coming back to his chair; ‘and this is the end of my prosing about myself, I get on as well as I can.", "\"그러는 동안에도,\" 트래들스가 의자로 돌아오며 말했다. \"이게 내 신세 한탄 이야기의 끝이야 — 나는 할 수 있는 한 잘 버텨 나가고 있어."], ["I don’t make much, but I don’t spend much.", "벌이는 많지 않아도, 쓰는 것도 많지 않으니까."], ["In general, I board with the people downstairs, who are very agreeable people indeed.", "보통은 아래층 사람들한테서 하숙을 하는데, 정말이지 더없이 유쾌한 사람들이야."], ["Both Mr. and Mrs. Micawber have seen a good deal of life, and are excellent company.’", "미코버 씨 내외 두 분 다 세상 풍파를 꽤 겪은 분들이라, 함께 있으면 정말 좋아.\""]], [["‘My dear Traddles!’ I quickly exclaimed.", "\"트래들스, 이 친구야!\" 내가 얼른 외쳤다."], ["‘What are you talking about?’", "\"지금 무슨 소리를 하는 거야?\""]], [["Traddles looked at me, as if he wondered what I was talking about.", "트래들스는 내가 무슨 소리를 하는 거냐는 듯 나를 쳐다보았다."]], [["‘Mr. and Mrs.", "\"미코버 씨 내외라니!\""], ["Micawber!’ I repeated.", "내가 되풀이했다."], ["‘Why, I am intimately acquainted with them!’", "\"아니, 나 그 두 분하고 아주 가깝게 아는 사이야!\""]], [["An opportune double knock at the door, which I knew well from old experience in Windsor Terrace, and which nobody but Mr. Micawber could ever have knocked at that door, resolved any doubt in my mind as to their being my old friends.", "마침 절묘하게 현관문을 두 번 두드리는 소리가 났는데, 윈저 테라스 시절의 옛 경험으로 익히 아는 그 소리, 미코버 씨 말고는 그 문을 그렇게 두드릴 사람이 결코 없는 그 소리가, 그분들이 바로 내 옛 친구들이라는 것에 대한 내 마음속 일말의 의심마저 깨끗이 풀어 주었다."], ["I begged Traddles to ask his landlord to walk up.", "나는 트래들스에게 집주인더러 올라오시라고 청해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Traddles accordingly did so, over the banister; and Mr. Micawber, not a bit changed--his tights, his stick, his shirt-collar, and his eye-glass, all the same as ever--came into the room with a genteel and youthful air.", "트래들스는 그 말대로 난간 너머로 그렇게 청했고, 미코버 씨가 — 조금도 변하지 않은 채로, 그 꼭 끼는 바지며 지팡이며 셔츠 깃이며 외알 안경까지 죄다 옛날 그대로인 채로 — 점잖으면서도 젊은이다운 기색으로 방 안으로 들어왔다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Mr. Traddles,’ said Mr.", "\"실례했습니다, 트래들스 씨.\""], ["Micawber, with the old roll in his voice, as he checked himself in humming a soft tune.", "미코버 씨가 부드러운 곡조를 흥얼거리다 멈추며, 옛날 그대로 굴리는 말투로 말했다."], ["‘I was not aware that there was any individual, alien to this tenement, in your sanctum.’", "\"이 거처와 무관한 다른 분이 선생의 보금자리에 와 계신 줄은 미처 몰랐습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber slightly bowed to me, and pulled up his shirt-collar.", "미코버 씨가 나에게 가볍게 고개를 숙이고 셔츠 깃을 추켜올렸다."]], [["‘How do you do, Mr. Micawber?’ said I.", "\"안녕하십니까, 미코버 씨?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Micawber, ‘you are exceedingly obliging.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"정말 황송한 인사로군요."], ["I am in statu quo.’", "저는 현상 그대로올시다.\""]], [["‘And Mrs. Micawber?’ I pursued.", "\"미코버 부인께서는요?\" 내가 이어서 물었다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Micawber, ‘she is also, thank God, in statu quo.’", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그 사람도, 고맙게도, 현상 그대로올시다.\""]], [["‘And the children, Mr. Micawber?’", "\"아이들은요, 미코버 씨?\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Micawber, ‘I rejoice to reply that they are, likewise, in the enjoyment of salubrity.’", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"기쁘게 답하건대, 그 아이들도 마찬가지로 무병의 즐거움을 누리고 있소이다.\""]], [["All this time, Mr. Micawber had not known me in the least, though he had stood face to face with me.", "이러는 내내, 미코버 씨는 나와 얼굴을 마주 대고 서 있으면서도 나를 도무지 알아보지 못했다."], ["But now, seeing me smile, he examined my features with more attention, fell back, cried, ‘Is it possible!", "그러나 이제 내가 빙긋 웃는 것을 보더니, 그는 내 이목구비를 더 유심히 뜯어보고, 뒤로 한 발 물러서며 외쳤다."], ["Have I the pleasure of again beholding Copperfield!’ and shook me by both hands with the utmost fervour.", "\"이럴 수가! 코퍼필드를 다시 뵙는 기쁨을 누리다니!\" 그러고는 더없이 격렬하게 두 손을 잡고 흔들었다."]], [["‘Good Heaven, Mr.", "\"맙소사, 트래들스 씨!\""], ["Traddles!’ said Mr.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘to think that I should find you acquainted with the friend of my youth, the companion of earlier days!", "\"선생이 내 젊은 날의 벗이요, 지난날의 동무를 알고 지내는 사이라는 걸 알게 되다니! 여보!\""], ["My dear!’ calling over the banisters to Mrs. Micawber, while Traddles looked (with reason) not a little amazed at this description of me.", "그가 난간 너머로 미코버 부인을 부르는 동안, 트래들스는 나를 두고 하는 이런 묘사에 (그럴 만도 하게) 적잖이 놀란 표정을 지었다."], ["‘Here is a gentleman in Mr. Traddles’s apartment, whom he wishes to have the pleasure of presenting to you, my love!’", "\"트래들스 씨 방에 한 신사분이 와 계신데, 당신에게 소개해 드리는 기쁨을 누리고 싶다는구려, 여보!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber immediately reappeared, and shook hands with me again.", "미코버 씨는 곧바로 다시 나타나, 나와 또 한 번 악수를 했다."]], [["‘And how is our good friend the Doctor, Copperfield?’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 우리 좋은 벗 박사님은 어떻게 지내시나, 코퍼필드?\""], ["Micawber, ‘and all the circle at Canterbury?’", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 캔터베리의 그 일가분들 모두는?\""]], [["‘I have none but good accounts of them,’ said I.", "\"다들 좋은 소식뿐입니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am most delighted to hear it,’ said Mr. Micawber.", "\"그것 참 더없이 기쁜 소식이로군.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It was at Canterbury where we last met.", "\"우리가 마지막으로 만난 게 캔터베리였지."], ["Within the shadow, I may figuratively say, of that religious edifice immortalized by Chaucer, which was anciently the resort of Pilgrims from the remotest corners of--in short,’ said Mr.", "초서가 불멸로 남긴 그 종교적 건축물의 그늘 아래에서 — 비유적으로 말하자면 말일세 — 그곳은 예부터 가장 머나먼 구석구석에서 온 순례자들의 발길이 모이던 곳이었지 — 요컨대,\""], ["Micawber, ‘in the immediate neighbourhood of the Cathedral.’", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그 대성당 바로 코앞에서 말이오.\""]], [["I replied that it was. Mr.", "나는 그렇다고 대답했다."], ["Micawber continued talking as volubly as he could; but not, I thought, without showing, by some marks of concern in his countenance, that he was sensible of sounds in the next room, as of Mrs. Micawber washing her hands, and hurriedly opening and shutting drawers that were uneasy in their action.", "미코버 씨는 가능한 한 청산유수로 이야기를 이어 갔지만, 내가 보기에는 그 얼굴에 어린 어떤 근심의 기색으로 미루어, 옆방에서 나는 소리에 — 미코버 부인이 손을 씻는 소리며, 뻑뻑하게 잘 안 열리는 서랍들을 황급히 여닫는 소리에 — 신경을 쓰고 있는 듯했다."]], [["‘You find us, Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"보다시피 우리는, 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, with one eye on Traddles, ‘at present established, on what may be designated as a small and unassuming scale; but, you are aware that I have, in the course of my career, surmounted difficulties, and conquered obstacles.", "미코버 씨가 트래들스를 한쪽 눈으로 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"지금은, 소박하고 보잘것없다 할 만한 규모로 자리를 잡고 있다네. 하지만 자네도 알다시피, 나는 내 살아온 여정에서 온갖 어려움을 헤쳐 나오고, 숱한 장애물을 정복해 왔지."], ["You are no stranger to the fact, that there have been periods of my life, when it has been requisite that I should pause, until certain expected events should turn up; when it has been necessary that I should fall back, before making what I trust I shall not be accused of presumption in terming--a spring.", "또한 자네도 익히 아는 사실이지만, 내 인생에는, 어떤 기대하던 일이 떠오를 때까지 잠시 멈춰 서 있어야 할 때가 있었고, 무언가 — 외람되게도 그렇게 부른다고 비난받지 않기를 바라네만 — 도약을 하기에 앞서 한 걸음 물러나야 할 때가 있었네."], ["The present is one of those momentous stages in the life of man.", "지금이 바로 인간의 삶에서 그런 중대한 고비 가운데 하나일세."], ["You find me, fallen back, FOR a spring; and I have every reason to believe that a vigorous leap will shortly be the result.’", "자네는 지금, 도약을 위해 한 걸음 물러나 있는 나를 보고 있는 것이고, 머잖아 힘찬 도약이 그 결실로 나타나리라고 나는 굳게 믿을 만한 온갖 근거를 가지고 있네.\""]], [["I was expressing my satisfaction, when Mrs. Micawber came in; a little more slatternly than she used to be, or so she seemed now, to my unaccustomed eyes, but still with some preparation of herself for company, and with a pair of brown gloves on.", "내가 만족스럽다는 뜻을 표하고 있는데, 미코버 부인이 들어왔다. 예전보다 옷차림이 조금 더 후줄근해진 것 같았다 — 아니면 그저 익숙지 않은 내 눈에 그렇게 보인 것인지도 모르지만 — 그래도 손님 맞을 채비는 어느 정도 해서, 갈색 장갑을 한 켤레 끼고 있었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\""], ["Micawber, leading her towards me, ‘here is a gentleman of the name of Copperfield, who wishes to renew his acquaintance with you.’", "미코버 씨가 그녀를 나에게로 이끌며 말했다. \"여기 코퍼필드라는 이름의 신사분이, 당신과 다시 알고 지내기를 바라고 계신다오.\""]], [["It would have been better, as it turned out, to have led gently up to this announcement, for Mrs.", "결과적으로 보면, 이 소식은 부드럽게 차근차근 꺼내는 편이 나았을 것이다."], ["Micawber, being in a delicate state of health, was overcome by it, and was taken so unwell, that Mr. Micawber was obliged, in great trepidation, to run down to the water-butt in the backyard, and draw a basinful to lave her brow with.", "미코버 부인이 몸이 약한 상태였던 터라 그 말에 그만 정신을 못 차리고 어찌나 몸이 안 좋아졌던지, 미코버 씨가 몹시 허둥대며 뒤뜰 빗물통으로 달려가 대야 가득 물을 떠다가 그녀의 이마를 적셔 주어야 했다."], ["She presently revived, however, and was really pleased to see me.", "그래도 그녀는 곧 정신을 차렸고, 정말로 나를 보고 기뻐했다."], ["We had half-an-hour’s talk, all together; and I asked her about the twins, who, she said, were ‘grown great creatures’; and after Master and Miss Micawber, whom she described as ‘absolute giants’, but they were not produced on that occasion.", "우리는 다 같이 반 시간쯤 이야기를 나누었다. 나는 쌍둥이들 안부를 물었는데, 부인 말로는 그 아이들이 '엄청 큰 녀석들로 자랐다'고 했고, 미코버 도련님과 미코버 아가씨의 안부도 물었더니, 그 둘은 '완전 거인이 됐다'고 했지만, 그날은 정작 모습을 보이지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Micawber was very anxious that I should stay to dinner.", "미코버 씨는 내가 저녁 식사까지 하고 가기를 무척이나 바랐다."], ["I should not have been averse to do so, but that I imagined I detected trouble, and calculation relative to the extent of the cold meat, in Mrs.", "나도 그러는 게 싫지는 않았겠지만, 미코버 부인의 눈빛에서 식은 고기가 얼마나 되나 가늠하며 곤혹스러워하는 기색을 읽은 것 같았다."], ["Micawber’s eye. I therefore pleaded another engagement; and observing that Mrs. Micawber’s spirits were immediately lightened, I resisted all persuasion to forego it.", "그래서 나는 다른 약속이 있다는 핑계를 댔고, 그러자 미코버 부인의 안색이 대번에 환해지는 것을 보고서, 그 약속을 무르라는 온갖 권유에 끝내 넘어가지 않았다."]], [["But I told Traddles, and Mr. and Mrs. Micawber, that before I could think of leaving, they must appoint a day when they would come and dine with me.", "하지만 나는 트래들스와 미코버 씨 내외에게, 떠나기 전에 두 분이 우리 집에 와서 함께 저녁을 들 날을 정해야 한다고 말했다."], ["The occupations to which Traddles stood pledged, rendered it necessary to fix a somewhat distant one; but an appointment was made for the purpose, that suited us all, and then I took my leave.", "트래들스가 약속해 둔 일거리가 있어서 날짜를 좀 멀찍이 잡을 수밖에 없었지만, 어쨌든 우리 모두에게 맞는 날로 약속이 정해졌고, 그러고 나서 나는 작별을 고했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber, under pretence of showing me a nearer way than that by which I had come, accompanied me to the corner of the street; being anxious (he explained to me) to say a few words to an old friend, in confidence.", "미코버 씨는 내가 왔던 길보다 가까운 길을 알려 주겠다는 핑계로 거리 모퉁이까지 나를 바래다주었는데, 옛 친구에게 은밀히 몇 마디 하고 싶어서였다고 (그가 나에게 설명하기를) 했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"이보게, 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, ‘I need hardly tell you that to have beneath our roof, under existing circumstances, a mind like that which gleams--if I may be allowed the expression--which gleams--in your friend Traddles, is an unspeakable comfort.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"지금 같은 처지에, 우리 지붕 아래에 — 이런 표현을 써도 된다면 — 자네 친구 트래들스에게서 반짝이는 그런 정신을, 그런 반짝임을 두고 산다는 게 말로 다 할 수 없는 위안이라는 걸 굳이 자네한테 일러 줄 필요도 없겠지."], ["With a washerwoman, who exposes hard-bake for sale in her parlour-window, dwelling next door, and a Bow-street officer residing over the way, you may imagine that his society is a source of consolation to myself and to Mrs. Micawber.", "거실 창에 단단한 사탕과자를 늘어놓고 파는 세탁부 아낙이 바로 옆집에 살고, 보 가의 경찰관이 길 건너에 사는 처지이니, 그 친구와 어울리는 것이 나와 미코버 부인에게 얼마나 큰 위로가 되는지 자네도 짐작할 수 있을 걸세."], ["I am at present, my dear Copperfield, engaged in the sale of corn upon commission.", "나는 지금, 코퍼필드, 수수료를 받고 곡물을 파는 일에 종사하고 있네."], ["It is not an avocation of a remunerative description--in other words, it does not pay--and some temporary embarrassments of a pecuniary nature have been the consequence.", "그게 그리 돈벌이가 되는 직업은 아니야 — 다시 말해, 벌이가 시원찮다는 게지 — 그래서 일시적으로 금전적인 곤경을 좀 겪고 있는 게 그 결과일세."], ["I am, however, delighted to add that I have now an immediate prospect of something turning up (I am not at liberty to say in what direction), which I trust will enable me to provide, permanently, both for myself and for your friend Traddles, in whom I have an unaffected interest.", "하지만 기쁘게 덧붙이건대, 이제 머잖아 무언가 떠오를 전망이 (어느 방면인지는 말할 자유가 없네만) 눈앞에 있는데, 나는 그것이 나 자신과, 또 내가 진심으로 마음을 쓰고 있는 자네 친구 트래들스를 모두 영구히 건사할 수 있게 해 주리라고 믿네."], ["You may, perhaps, be prepared to hear that Mrs. Micawber is in a state of health which renders it not wholly improbable that an addition may be ultimately made to those pledges of affection which--in short, to the infantine group. Mrs.", "어쩌면 자네는, 미코버 부인이 머잖아 그 애정의 결실들에 또 하나가 보태질 가능성이 — 요컨대 그 어린것들 무리에 하나가 더해질 가능성이 — 영 없지만은 않은 몸 상태라는 소식을 들을 마음의 준비가 되어 있을지도 모르겠군."], ["Micawber’s family have been so good as to express their dissatisfaction at this state of things.", "미코버 부인의 친정 식구들은 친절하게도 이런 형편에 대해 못마땅함을 표해 왔네."], ["I have merely to observe, that I am not aware that it is any business of theirs, and that I repel that exhibition of feeling with scorn, and with defiance!’", "나는 그저, 그게 그들이 참견할 일인지 도무지 모르겠다는 것과, 그런 감정의 발로를 나는 경멸과 항거로 물리친다는 것을 한마디 해 둘 뿐일세!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber then shook hands with me again, and left me.", "그러고 나서 미코버 씨는 나와 또 한 번 악수를 하고, 떠나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 28. Mr. MICAWBER’S GAUNTLET", "# CHAPTER 28. Mr. MICAWBER’S GAUNTLET"]], [["Until the day arrived on which I was to entertain my newly-found old friends, I lived principally on Dora and coffee.", "내가 새로 다시 찾은 옛 친구들을 대접하기로 한 날이 다가오기 전까지, 나는 주로 도라와 커피로 살았다."], ["In my love-lorn condition, my appetite languished; and I was glad of it, for I felt as though it would have been an act of perfidy towards Dora to have a natural relish for my dinner.", "사랑에 빠져 애태우는 처지였던지라 입맛도 시들었는데, 나는 그게 오히려 반가웠다. 저녁밥을 자연스레 맛있게 먹는다는 것이 어쩐지 도라를 향한 배신 행위처럼 느껴졌기 때문이다."], ["The quantity of walking exercise I took, was not in this respect attended with its usual consequence, as the disappointment counteracted the fresh air.", "그렇게 많이 걸어 다녀도 평소처럼 입맛이 돋는 효과는 나타나지 않았는데, 상심이 신선한 바깥 공기를 상쇄해 버린 탓이었다."], ["I have my doubts, too, founded on the acute experience acquired at this period of my life, whether a sound enjoyment of animal food can develop itself freely in any human subject who is always in torment from tight boots.", "게다가 나는 내 인생의 이 시기에 얻은 뼈저린 경험에 비추어, 꽉 끼는 구두 때문에 늘 고통에 시달리는 사람이라면 누구든 고기 음식을 마음껏 즐기는 즐거움을 자유로이 키울 수 있을지 의심스럽다."], ["I think the extremities require to be at peace before the stomach will conduct itself with vigour.", "위장이 힘차게 제 일을 하기 전에는, 먼저 발끝이 평화로워야 하는 법인 것 같다."]], [["On the occasion of this domestic little party, I did not repeat my former extensive preparations.", "이 자그마한 집들이 모임을 위해, 나는 예전처럼 거창하게 준비하는 짓을 되풀이하지 않았다."], ["I merely provided a pair of soles, a small leg of mutton, and a pigeon-pie. Mrs.", "그저 가자미 두 마리, 양고기 다리 살 한 덩이, 그리고 비둘기 파이 하나를 마련했을 뿐이다."], ["Crupp broke out into rebellion on my first bashful hint in reference to the cooking of the fish and joint, and said, with a dignified sense of injury, ‘No! No, sir!", "생선과 고기를 요리하는 일을 두고 내가 처음 머뭇머뭇 운을 떼자, 크루프 부인은 반란을 일으키더니 모욕이라도 당한 양 위엄을 차리며 이렇게 말했다. \"안 돼요!"], ["You will not ask me sich a thing, for you are better acquainted with me than to suppose me capable of doing what I cannot do with ampial satisfaction to my own feelings!’ But, in the end, a compromise was effected; and Mrs.", "안 돼요, 나리! 그런 부탁을 나리가 저한테 하실 리가 없지요. 나리는 절 잘 아시니까, 제 마음이 흡—족하게 만족할 수 없는 일은 제가 못 한다는 걸 아실 텐데요!\""], ["Crupp consented to achieve this feat, on condition that I dined from home for a fortnight afterwards.", "하지만 결국에는 타협이 이루어졌다. 크루프 부인은 그 뒤로 두 주일 동안 내가 바깥에서 저녁을 먹는다는 조건으로 이 위업을 해내기로 승낙했다."]], [["And here I may remark, that what I underwent from Mrs. Crupp, in consequence of the tyranny she established over me, was dreadful.", "여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 크루프 부인이 나를 상대로 폭정을 세운 탓에 내가 겪은 고초는 끔찍했다."], ["I never was so much afraid of anyone.", "나는 그토록 누군가를 무서워한 적이 없었다."], ["We made a compromise of everything.", "우리는 무슨 일이든 타협으로 결판을 냈다."], ["If I hesitated, she was taken with that wonderful disorder which was always lying in ambush in her system, ready, at the shortest notice, to prey upon her vitals.", "내가 조금이라도 머뭇거리면, 그녀는 늘 자기 몸속 어딘가에 매복해 있다가 아주 짧은 통보만으로도 당장 그녀의 급소를 덮칠 채비를 하고 있는 그 놀라운 병에 사로잡혔다."], ["If I rang the bell impatiently, after half-a-dozen unavailing modest pulls, and she appeared at last--which was not by any means to be relied upon--she would appear with a reproachful aspect, sink breathless on a chair near the door, lay her hand upon her nankeen bosom, and become so ill, that I was glad, at any sacrifice of brandy or anything else, to get rid of her.", "점잖게 여섯 번이나 당겨도 소용이 없어 내가 조바심에 못 이겨 초인종 줄을 잡아당기면, 마침내 그녀가 나타나는데 — 그나마도 결코 믿을 수 없는 일이었다 — 그녀는 나무라는 낯빛으로 나타나, 숨을 헐떡이며 문 가까운 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 무명천을 댄 가슴팍에 손을 얹고는, 어찌나 몸져눕는지 나는 브랜디든 뭐든 무엇을 내주는 희생을 치르고서라도 그녀를 떨쳐 버리게 되어 차라리 다행이라 여길 지경이었다."], ["If I objected to having my bed made at five o’clock in the afternoon--which I do still think an uncomfortable arrangement--one motion of her hand towards the same nankeen region of wounded sensibility was enough to make me falter an apology.", "내가 오후 다섯 시에 잠자리를 펴는 것에 반대하기라도 하면 — 지금도 나는 그게 불편한 처사라고 생각한다 — 그 상처받은 감수성이 깃든 무명천 가슴팍 쪽으로 손짓 한 번 까딱하는 것만으로도 나는 더듬더듬 사과를 늘어놓기에 충분했다."], ["In short, I would have done anything in an honourable way rather than give Mrs. Crupp offence; and she was the terror of my life.", "한마디로, 나는 크루프 부인의 비위를 거스르느니 떳떳한 방법이라면 무슨 짓이든 했을 것이다. 그녀는 내 인생의 공포였다."]], [["I bought a second-hand dumb-waiter for this dinner-party, in preference to re-engaging the handy young man; against whom I had conceived a prejudice, in consequence of meeting him in the Strand, one Sunday morning, in a waistcoat remarkably like one of mine, which had been missing since the former occasion.", "나는 이 만찬을 위해 손재주 좋은 그 젊은이를 다시 부르는 대신, 중고 식기 운반대를 하나 샀다. 그 젊은이에게 편견을 품게 된 것은, 어느 일요일 아침 스트랜드 거리에서 그와 마주쳤는데, 그가 내 조끼와 놀랍도록 닮은 조끼를 입고 있었던 탓이다. 그 조끼는 지난번 그 모임 이후로 사라지고 없던 것이었다."], ["The ‘young gal’ was re-engaged; but on the stipulation that she should only bring in the dishes, and then withdraw to the landing-place, beyond the outer door; where a habit of sniffing she had contracted would be lost upon the guests, and where her retiring on the plates would be a physical impossibility.", "'젊은 계집애'는 다시 불렀다. 다만 음식 접시를 들여오기만 하고, 그런 다음에는 바깥문 너머 층계참으로 물러나 있어야 한다는 조건을 달았다. 거기라면 그녀가 버릇처럼 들이는 코를 훌쩍거리는 소리도 손님들에게는 들리지 않을 테고, 접시 위로 코를 빠뜨리는 일도 물리적으로 불가능할 터였다."]], [["Having laid in the materials for a bowl of punch, to be compounded by Mr. Micawber; having provided a bottle of lavender-water, two wax-candles, a paper of mixed pins, and a pincushion, to assist Mrs. Micawber in her toilette at my dressing-table; having also caused the fire in my bedroom to be lighted for Mrs. Micawber’s convenience; and having laid the cloth with my own hands, I awaited the result with composure.", "미코버 씨가 제조할 펀치 한 사발의 재료를 갖춰 두고, 미코버 부인이 내 화장대에서 몸단장을 하는 데 보탬이 되도록 라벤더 화장수 한 병, 밀랍 양초 두 자루, 갖가지 핀을 꽂은 종이 한 장, 그리고 바늘겨레 하나를 마련해 두고, 또 미코버 부인이 편하도록 내 침실 난로에 불도 지피게 하고, 식탁보까지 내 손으로 직접 깔아 둔 다음, 나는 차분한 마음으로 결과를 기다렸다."]], [["At the appointed time, my three visitors arrived together. Mr.", "약속한 시간에, 손님 셋이 함께 도착했다."], ["Micawber with more shirt-collar than usual, and a new ribbon to his eye-glass; Mrs.", "미코버 씨는 평소보다 셔츠 깃을 더 세우고, 외알 안경에는 새 리본을 달고 있었다."], ["Micawber with her cap in a whitey-brown paper parcel; Traddles carrying the parcel, and supporting Mrs. Micawber on his arm.", "미코버 부인은 모자를 누르스름한 갈색 종이 꾸러미에 싸서 들고 있었다. 트래들스가 그 꾸러미를 들고, 한쪽 팔로는 미코버 부인을 부축하고 있었다."], ["They were all delighted with my residence. When I conducted Mrs.", "세 사람 모두 내 거처를 보고 무척 기뻐했다."], ["Micawber to my dressing-table, and she saw the scale on which it was prepared for her, she was in such raptures, that she called Mr. Micawber to come in and look.", "내가 미코버 부인을 화장대로 안내하자, 그녀는 자기를 위해 마련된 그 규모를 보고는 어찌나 황홀해하던지, 미코버 씨를 불러 들어와서 보라고 했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"이보게 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, ‘this is luxurious.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["This is a way of life which reminds me of the period when I was myself in a state of celibacy, and Mrs. Micawber had not yet been solicited to plight her faith at the Hymeneal altar.’", "\"이거 정말 호사로구먼. 이런 생활 방식을 보니, 내가 아직 독신이고 미코버 부인이 혼인의 제단 앞에서 신의를 맹세해 달라는 청을 받기도 전이던 시절이 떠오르네그려.\""]], [["‘He means, solicited by him, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"이이 말은, 자기가 청혼하기 전이라는 뜻이에요, 코퍼필드 씨.\""], ["Micawber, archly.", "미코버 부인이 짐짓 장난스럽게 말했다."], ["‘He cannot answer for others.’", "\"다른 사람들 일까지는 장담 못 하니까요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ returned Mr.", "\"여보,\""], ["Micawber with sudden seriousness, ‘I have no desire to answer for others.", "미코버 씨가 갑자기 진지해지며 받았다. \"나는 다른 사람들 일을 장담할 마음이 조금도 없소."], ["I am too well aware that when, in the inscrutable decrees of Fate, you were reserved for me, it is possible you may have been reserved for one, destined, after a protracted struggle, at length to fall a victim to pecuniary involvements of a complicated nature.", "헤아릴 길 없는 운명의 섭리 속에서 당신이 나를 위해 점지되었을 때, 어쩌면 당신은, 오래도록 발버둥 치다가 끝내 복잡하기 짝이 없는 금전 문제의 희생양으로 쓰러질 운명을 타고난 어떤 사내를 위해 점지되었을지도 모른다는 걸, 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있으니 말이오."], ["I understand your allusion, my love.", "당신이 무얼 빗대어 말하는지 알겠소, 여보."], ["I regret it, but I can bear it.’", "가슴 아프지만, 견딜 수 있소.\""]], [["‘Micawber!’ exclaimed Mrs. Micawber, in tears.", "\"미코버!\" 미코버 부인이 눈물을 글썽이며 외쳤다."], ["‘Have I deserved this!", "\"내가 이런 소릴 들어야 마땅한가요!"], ["I, who never have deserted you; who never WILL desert you, Micawber!’ ‘My love,’ said Mr.", "단 한 번도 당신을 저버린 적 없고, 앞으로도 결코 당신을 저버리지 않을 이 내가요, 미코버!\" \"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 몹시 감동하여 말했다."], ["Micawber, much affected, ‘you will forgive, and our old and tried friend Copperfield will, I am sure, forgive, the momentary laceration of a wounded spirit, made sensitive by a recent collision with the Minion of Power--in other words, with a ribald Turncock attached to the water-works--and will pity, not condemn, its excesses.’", "\"당신도 용서해 주고, 우리의 오래되고 믿음직한 벗 코퍼필드도 분명 용서해 줄 거요. 권력의 앞잡이와 — 다시 말해, 수도국에 딸린 상스러운 수도꼭지 담당자와 — 방금 부딪히는 바람에 예민해진, 상처 입은 영혼이 한순간 찢어져 터진 것을 말이오. 그리고 그 지나침을 나무라지 않고 가엾이 여겨 줄 거요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber then embraced Mrs.", "그러더니 미코버 씨는 미코버 부인을 끌어안고 내 손을 꼭 쥐었다."], ["Micawber, and pressed my hand; leaving me to infer from this broken allusion that his domestic supply of water had been cut off that afternoon, in consequence of default in the payment of the company’s rates.", "나는 이 두서없는 암시로 미루어, 그날 오후 그 집의 수돗물 공급이 끊겼다는 것을, 그것도 수도 회사 요금을 내지 않은 탓에 그리되었다는 것을 짐작할 수 있었다."]], [["To divert his thoughts from this melancholy subject, I informed Mr. Micawber that I relied upon him for a bowl of punch, and led him to the lemons.", "이 우울한 화제에서 그의 생각을 돌리려고, 나는 미코버 씨에게 펀치 한 사발은 당신만 믿겠노라고 일러 주고는 그를 레몬이 있는 데로 데려갔다."], ["His recent despondency, not to say despair, was gone in a moment.", "방금까지의 의기소침함은, 절망이라 해도 좋을 그 기색은, 순식간에 사라졌다."], ["I never saw a man so thoroughly enjoy himself amid the fragrance of lemon-peel and sugar, the odour of burning rum, and the steam of boiling water, as Mr.", "나는 그날 오후의 미코버 씨만큼, 레몬 껍질과 설탕의 향기, 럼주가 끓는 냄새, 펄펄 끓는 물의 김 한가운데서 그토록 흠뻑 즐기는 사람을 본 적이 없다."], ["Micawber did that afternoon. It was wonderful to see his face shining at us out of a thin cloud of these delicate fumes, as he stirred, and mixed, and tasted, and looked as if he were making, instead of punch, a fortune for his family down to the latest posterity.", "그가 휘젓고, 섞고, 맛보면서, 이 섬세한 김의 엷은 구름 사이로 우리를 향해 얼굴을 환히 빛내는 모습은 참으로 볼 만했다. 마치 펀치가 아니라, 먼 후손에 이르기까지 제 식구가 누릴 한밑천을 장만하고 있는 사람 같았다."], ["As to Mrs. Micawber, I don’t know whether it was the effect of the cap, or the lavender-water, or the pins, or the fire, or the wax-candles, but she came out of my room, comparatively speaking, lovely.", "미코버 부인으로 말하자면, 모자 덕분인지, 라벤더 화장수 덕분인지, 핀 덕분인지, 난로 덕분인지, 밀랍 양초 덕분인지는 모르겠으나, 비교적 따져 말하건대 사랑스러운 모습으로 내 방에서 나왔다."], ["And the lark was never gayer than that excellent woman.", "그리고 그 훌륭한 부인은 종달새 못지않게 명랑했다."]], [["I suppose--I never ventured to inquire, but I suppose--that Mrs. Crupp, after frying the soles, was taken ill.", "짐작건대 — 감히 물어본 적은 없으나, 짐작건대 — 크루프 부인은 가자미를 튀긴 뒤에 몸져누운 모양이었다."], ["Because we broke down at that point.", "거기서부터 일이 어그러져 버렸으니까."], ["The leg of mutton came up very red within, and very pale without: besides having a foreign substance of a gritty nature sprinkled over it, as if if had had a fall into the ashes of that remarkable kitchen fireplace.", "양고기 다리 살은 속은 시뻘겋고 겉은 새하얀 채로 올라왔다. 게다가 자글자글한 이물질이 그 위에 뿌려져 있었는데, 마치 그 별난 부엌 난로의 잿더미 속에 한번 굴러떨어지기라도 한 듯했다."], ["But we were not in condition to judge of this fact from the appearance of the gravy, forasmuch as the ‘young gal’ had dropped it all upon the stairs--where it remained, by the by, in a long train, until it was worn out.", "하지만 우리는 그 사실을 고기 국물의 모양새로 판단할 처지가 못 되었다. '젊은 계집애'가 국물을 죄다 계단에 쏟아 버린 탓이었는데 — 그건 그렇고, 그 국물은 그 뒤로도 발에 다 닳아 없어질 때까지 길게 줄을 이루며 거기 그대로 남아 있었다."], ["The pigeon-pie was not bad, but it was a delusive pie: the crust being like a disappointing head, phrenologically speaking: full of lumps and bumps, with nothing particular underneath.", "비둘기 파이는 나쁘지 않았지만, 사람을 속이는 파이였다. 파이 껍질이 골상학으로 따지자면 실망스러운 머리통 같았으니, 울퉁불퉁 혹투성이인데 그 아래에는 이렇다 할 게 아무것도 없었다."], ["In short, the banquet was such a failure that I should have been quite unhappy--about the failure, I mean, for I was always unhappy about Dora--if I had not been relieved by the great good humour of my company, and by a bright suggestion from Mr. Micawber.", "한마디로 이 잔치는 어찌나 실패작이었던지, 손님들의 더없이 좋은 흥과 미코버 씨의 기발한 제안으로 마음을 놓지 못했더라면 나는 무척 불행했을 것이다 — 잔치가 실패한 것 때문에 말이다. 도라 때문에야 나는 늘 불행했으니까."]], [["‘My dear friend Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘accidents will occur in the best-regulated families; and in families not regulated by that pervading influence which sanctifies while it enhances the--a--I would say, in short, by the influence of Woman, in the lofty character of Wife, they may be expected with confidence, and must be borne with philosophy.", "\"사고란 아무리 잘 다스려지는 집안에서도 일어나는 법이지. 그리고 그 두루 미치는 영향력으로 다스려지지 않는 집안에서는 — 그러니까 격조 높은 아내라는 자리에 선 여성의 영향력으로 다스려지지 않는, 그 영향력은 격을 높이는 동시에 신성하게도 하는 것인데 — 그런 집안에서는 사고가 일어나리라고 자신 있게 내다보아야 하고, 또 철학으로써 견뎌야 하는 법이라네."], ["If you will allow me to take the liberty of remarking that there are few comestibles better, in their way, than a Devil, and that I believe, with a little division of labour, we could accomplish a good one if the young person in attendance could produce a gridiron, I would put it to you, that this little misfortune may be easily repaired.’", "외람되이 한마디 하자면, 데블 구이만큼 그 나름으로 훌륭한 음식도 드물다네. 그리고 일을 조금씩 나눠 맡으면, 시중드는 저 젊은 사람이 석쇠 하나만 가져와 준다면 우리가 썩 괜찮은 걸 하나 만들어 낼 수 있으리라 믿네. 내 생각엔, 이 작은 불운쯤은 쉽게 만회할 수 있을 듯하이.\""]], [["There was a gridiron in the pantry, on which my morning rasher of bacon was cooked.", "식료품 저장실에 석쇠가 하나 있었는데, 내 아침 베이컨 한 조각을 굽던 것이었다."], ["We had it in, in a twinkling, and immediately applied ourselves to carrying Mr.", "우리는 눈 깜짝할 새에 그걸 들여와, 곧장 미코버 씨의 착상을 실행에 옮기는 데 매달렸다."], ["Micawber’s idea into effect.", "그가 말한 분업이란 이러했다."], ["The division of labour to which he had referred was this:--Traddles cut the mutton into slices; Mr. Micawber (who could do anything of this sort to perfection) covered them with pepper, mustard, salt, and cayenne; I put them on the gridiron, turned them with a fork, and took them off, under Mr.", "트래들스가 양고기를 저며 썰면, 미코버 씨가 (이런 종류의 일이라면 무엇이든 완벽하게 해내는 사람이었다) 거기에 후추와 겨자와 소금과 카옌 고춧가루를 뿌렸다. 나는 그것을 석쇠에 올리고, 미코버 씨의 지휘에 따라 포크로 뒤집었다가 다시 내렸다."], ["Micawber’s direction; and Mrs. Micawber heated, and continually stirred, some mushroom ketchup in a little saucepan.", "그리고 미코버 부인은 작은 냄비에다 버섯 케첩을 데우며 쉴 새 없이 휘저었다."], ["When we had slices enough done to begin upon, we fell-to, with our sleeves still tucked up at the wrist, more slices sputtering and blazing on the fire, and our attention divided between the mutton on our plates, and the mutton then preparing.", "우선 먹기 시작할 만큼 고기가 충분히 구워지자, 우리는 손목까지 소매를 걷어붙인 채로 달려들었는데, 그러는 동안에도 또 다른 고기 조각들이 불 위에서 지글지글 타오르고 있었고, 우리의 관심은 접시 위의 양고기와 그때 구워지고 있는 양고기 사이로 갈렸다."]], [["What with the novelty of this cookery, the excellence of it, the bustle of it, the frequent starting up to look after it, the frequent sitting down to dispose of it as the crisp slices came off the gridiron hot and hot, the being so busy, so flushed with the fire, so amused, and in the midst of such a tempting noise and savour, we reduced the leg of mutton to the bone.", "이 요리의 신기함, 그 맛깔스러움, 그 부산함, 자꾸만 벌떡 일어나 고기를 살피던 일, 바삭한 고기 조각이 석쇠에서 뜨끈뜨끈 막 구워져 나올 때마다 자꾸만 도로 앉아 그걸 해치우던 일, 그렇게 바쁘고, 불기운에 그렇게 얼굴이 달아오르고, 그렇게 흥겹고, 그토록 군침 도는 소리와 냄새 한가운데 있던 덕분에, 우리는 양고기 다리 살을 뼈만 남기고 다 먹어 치웠다."], ["My own appetite came back miraculously.", "내 입맛도 기적처럼 되돌아왔다."], ["I am ashamed to record it, but I really believe I forgot Dora for a little while.", "이런 걸 적어 두려니 부끄럽지만, 사실 나는 잠시나마 도라를 잊었던 것 같다."], ["I am satisfied that Mr. and Mrs. Micawber could not have enjoyed the feast more, if they had sold a bed to provide it.", "미코버 부부가 이 잔치를 차리려고 침대 하나를 팔았다 한들, 그보다 더 즐기지는 못했으리라 나는 확신한다."], ["Traddles laughed as heartily, almost the whole time, as he ate and worked.", "트래들스는 먹고 일하는 거의 내내 진심으로 호탕하게 웃어 댔다."], ["Indeed we all did, all at once; and I dare say there was never a greater success.", "사실 우리 모두가, 한꺼번에 그랬다. 그러니 이보다 더 큰 성공은 다시없었으리라 감히 말하겠다."]], [["We were at the height of our enjoyment, and were all busily engaged, in our several departments, endeavouring to bring the last batch of slices to a state of perfection that should crown the feast, when I was aware of a strange presence in the room, and my eyes encountered those of the staid Littimer, standing hat in hand before me.", "우리가 즐거움의 절정에 올라, 저마다 맡은 부서에서 잔치를 멋지게 마무리할 마지막 고기 한 무더기를 완벽한 상태로 구워 내려고 바쁘게 매달려 있을 때, 나는 방 안에 낯선 기척이 있음을 깨달았다. 그리고 내 눈은 점잖은 리티머의 눈과 마주쳤는데, 그는 모자를 손에 들고 내 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ I involuntarily asked.", "\"무슨 일인가?\" 나도 모르게 물었다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir, I was directed to come in.", "\"죄송합니다, 나리. 들어오라는 분부를 받았습니다."], ["Is my master not here, sir?’\n‘No.’", "제 주인님이 여기 안 계신지요, 나리?\""]], [["‘Have you not seen him, sir?’", "\"안 계시네.\" \"못 보셨습니까, 나리?\""]], [["‘No; don’t you come from him?’", "\"못 봤네. 주인이 보내서 온 게 아닌가?\""]], [["‘Not immediately so, sir.’", "\"바로 그렇지는 않습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Did he tell you you would find him here?’", "\"주인이 자네더러 여기서 자기를 찾을 거라고 하던가?\""]], [["‘Not exactly so, sir. But I should think he might be here tomorrow, as he has not been here today.’", "\"꼭 그렇지는 않습니다, 나리. 다만 오늘은 여기 안 오셨으니, 내일쯤 여기 오시지 않을까 합니다.\""]], [["‘Is he coming up from Oxford?’", "\"옥스퍼드에서 올라오는 길인가?\""]], [["‘I beg, sir,’ he returned respectfully, ‘that you will be seated, and allow me to do this.’ With which he took the fork from my unresisting hand, and bent over the gridiron, as if his whole attention were concentrated on it.", "\"나리,\" 그가 공손히 대답했다. \"부디 앉으시고, 이건 제가 하도록 해 주십시오.\" 그러면서 그는 내가 순순히 내미는 포크를 손에서 받아 들더니, 마치 온 정신을 거기에 쏟아붓는 사람처럼 석쇠 위로 몸을 숙였다."]], [["We should not have been much discomposed, I dare say, by the appearance of Steerforth himself, but we became in a moment the meekest of the meek before his respectable serving-man. Mr.", "감히 말하건대, 스티어포스 본인이 나타났다 한들 우리는 그리 크게 흐트러지지 않았을 것이다. 그런데 우리는 그의 점잖은 하인 앞에서 순식간에 더없이 온순한 양 떼가 되고 말았다."], ["Micawber, humming a tune, to show that he was quite at ease, subsided into his chair, with the handle of a hastily concealed fork sticking out of the bosom of his coat, as if he had stabbed himself. Mrs.", "미코버 씨는 자기가 아주 편안하다는 걸 보이려고 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 의자에 도로 주저앉았는데, 황급히 감춘 포크 자루가 외투 가슴팍에서 삐죽 튀어나와, 마치 제 몸을 찌르기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["Micawber put on her brown gloves, and assumed a genteel languor.", "미코버 부인은 갈색 장갑을 끼더니 우아하게 나른한 자태를 지어 보였다."], ["Traddles ran his greasy hands through his hair, and stood it bolt upright, and stared in confusion on the table-cloth.", "트래들스는 기름 묻은 손으로 머리카락을 쓸어 올려 빳빳이 곤두세우고는, 어쩔 줄 몰라 식탁보만 멍하니 바라보았다."], ["As for me, I was a mere infant at the head of my own table; and hardly ventured to glance at the respectable phenomenon, who had come from Heaven knows where, to put my establishment to rights.", "나로 말하자면, 내 식탁의 윗자리에 앉은 한낱 갓난애나 다름없었다. 그래서 어디서 왔는지 하느님이나 아실, 내 살림을 바로잡으러 온 그 점잖은 진풍경을 감히 흘끗 쳐다보지도 못했다."]], [["Meanwhile he took the mutton off the gridiron, and gravely handed it round.", "그러는 동안 그는 석쇠에서 고기를 내려 점잖게 돌렸다."], ["We all took some, but our appreciation of it was gone, and we merely made a show of eating it.", "우리는 다들 조금씩 받았지만, 고기를 음미하는 마음은 이미 사라져 버려서, 그저 먹는 시늉만 했다."], ["As we severally pushed away our plates, he noiselessly removed them, and set on the cheese.", "우리가 저마다 접시를 밀어내자, 그는 소리 없이 접시를 치우고 치즈를 내놓았다."], ["He took that off, too, when it was done with; cleared the table; piled everything on the dumb-waiter; gave us our wine-glasses; and, of his own accord, wheeled the dumb-waiter into the pantry.", "치즈도 다 먹고 나자 그것 역시 치웠다. 식탁을 정리하고, 모든 걸 식기 운반대에 차곡차곡 쌓고, 우리에게 포도주 잔을 건네고, 그러고는 제 스스로 식기 운반대를 식료품 저장실로 밀어 넣었다."], ["All this was done in a perfect manner, and he never raised his eyes from what he was about.", "이 모든 일이 완벽하게 이루어졌고, 그는 자기가 하는 일에서 단 한 번도 눈을 들지 않았다."], ["Yet his very elbows, when he had his back towards me, seemed to teem with the expression of his fixed opinion that I was extremely young.", "그런데도 그가 내게 등을 돌리고 있을 때면, 그 팔꿈치마저도 내가 지독히도 어리다는 자신의 확고한 견해를 잔뜩 내뿜는 것만 같았다."]], [["‘Can I do anything more, sir?’", "\"더 해 드릴 일이 있는지요, 나리?\""]], [["I thanked him and said, No; but would he take no dinner himself?", "나는 고맙다며 없다고 했다. 그런데 자네는 저녁을 좀 들지 않겠나?"]], [["‘None, I am obliged to you, sir.’", "\"안 들겠습니다. 감사합니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘Is Mr. Steerforth coming from Oxford?’", "\"스티어포스 씨가 옥스퍼드에서 오는 길인가?\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir?’", "\"뭐라고 하셨는지요, 나리?\""]], [["‘Is Mr. Steerforth coming from Oxford?’", "\"스티어포스 씨가 옥스퍼드에서 오는 길이냐고.\""]], [["‘I should imagine that he might be here tomorrow, sir.", "\"내일쯤 여기 오시지 않을까 짐작합니다, 나리."], ["I rather thought he might have been here today, sir. The mistake is mine, no doubt, sir.’", "오늘 오시려나 했는데요, 나리. 제가 잘못 안 게 틀림없습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘If you should see him first--’ said I.", "\"혹시 자네가 먼저 그분을 보거든—\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘If you’ll excuse me, sir, I don’t think I shall see him first.’", "\"실례지만, 나리, 제가 그분을 먼저 뵐 것 같지는 않습니다.\""]], [["‘In case you do,’ said I, ‘pray say that I am sorry he was not here today, as an old schoolfellow of his was here.’", "\"혹여 보거든,\" 내가 말했다. \"그분의 옛 학교 친구가 여기 왔었는데, 오늘 그분이 안 계셔서 섭섭했다고 좀 전해 주게.\""]], [["‘Indeed, sir!’ and he divided a bow between me and Traddles, with a glance at the latter.", "\"그러십니까, 나리!\" 그러더니 그는 나와 트래들스에게 절을 반씩 나누어 하면서, 트래들스 쪽을 한 번 흘끗 보았다."]], [["He was moving softly to the door, when, in a forlorn hope of saying something naturally--which I never could, to this man--I said:", "그가 사뿐히 문 쪽으로 걸어가는데, 나는 이 사내에게는 도무지 그래 본 적이 없는 일이지만, 무어라도 자연스럽게 한마디 해 보겠다는 가망 없는 바람으로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Oh! Littimer!’\n‘Sir!’", "\"아 참! 리티머!\""]], [["‘Did you remain long at Yarmouth, that time?’", "\"네, 나리!\" \"그때, 야머스에 오래 머물렀나?\""]], [["‘Not particularly so, sir.’", "\"그리 오래는 아니었습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘You saw the boat completed?’", "\"배가 완성된 걸 봤겠지?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.", "\"네, 나리."], ["I remained behind on purpose to see the boat completed.’", "배가 완성되는 걸 보려고 일부러 남아 있었습니다.\""]], [["‘I know!’ He raised his eyes to mine respectfully.", "\"알지!\" 그가 공손히 내 눈으로 눈을 들었다."]], [["‘Mr. Steerforth has not seen it yet, I suppose?’", "\"스티어포스 씨는 아직 그 배를 못 봤겠지?\""]], [["‘I really can’t say, sir.", "\"정말 모르겠습니다, 나리."], ["I think--but I really can’t say, sir.", "제 생각엔 — 하지만 정말 모르겠습니다, 나리."], ["I wish you good night, sir.’", "안녕히 주무십시오, 나리.\""]], [["He comprehended everybody present, in the respectful bow with which he followed these words, and disappeared.", "그는 이 말끝에 이어진 공손한 절 속에 그 자리의 모든 사람을 다 아우르고는, 사라졌다."], ["My visitors seemed to breathe more freely when he was gone; but my own relief was very great, for besides the constraint, arising from that extraordinary sense of being at a disadvantage which I always had in this man’s presence, my conscience had embarrassed me with whispers that I had mistrusted his master, and I could not repress a vague uneasy dread that he might find it out.", "그가 떠나자 손님들은 숨통이 좀 트인 듯했다. 하지만 내가 느낀 안도감은 무척 컸다. 이 사내 앞에만 서면 늘 따라붙던, 내가 어쩐지 불리한 처지에 놓여 있다는 그 묘한 느낌에서 비롯된 거북함도 거북함이려니와, 내가 그의 주인을 의심했다는 사실을 두고 양심이 속닥속닥 나를 곤혹스럽게 했고, 그가 그걸 알아챌지도 모른다는 막연하고 께름칙한 두려움을 도무지 억누를 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["How was it, having so little in reality to conceal, that I always DID feel as if this man were finding me out?", "실은 숨길 것이라곤 거의 없는데도, 어째서 나는 늘 이 사내가 내 속을 꿰뚫어 보는 것만 같았을까?"]], [["Mr. Micawber roused me from this reflection, which was blended with a certain remorseful apprehension of seeing Steerforth himself, by bestowing many encomiums on the absent Littimer as a most respectable fellow, and a thoroughly admirable servant. Mr.", "미코버 씨가 이 상념에서 나를 일깨워 주었는데, 그 상념에는 스티어포스 본인을 만나게 되리라는, 어딘가 뉘우치는 듯한 불안이 뒤섞여 있던 터였다. 그는 자리에 없는 리티머를 두고 더없이 점잖은 친구이자 진정 감탄스러운 하인이라며 칭찬을 잔뜩 늘어놓았다."], ["Micawber, I may remark, had taken his full share of the general bow, and had received it with infinite condescension.", "한마디 덧붙이자면, 미코버 씨도 그 두루 나눈 절을 제 몫만큼 충분히 받았고, 그것도 한없이 너그러운 태도로 받았다."]], [["‘But punch, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 펀치 말일세, 이보게 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, tasting it, ‘like time and tide, waits for no man. Ah!", "미코버 씨가 펀치를 맛보며 말했다. \"이건 세월이며 조수와 같아서, 사람을 기다려 주지 않지. 아!"], ["it is at the present moment in high flavour.", "지금 이 순간이 한창 맛이 무르익었네."], ["My love, will you give me your opinion?’", "여보, 당신 의견을 좀 말해 주겠소?\""]], [["Mrs. Micawber pronounced it excellent.", "미코버 부인은 일품이라고 단언했다."]], [["‘Then I will drink,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 한잔 마셔야겠네.\""], ["Micawber, ‘if my friend Copperfield will permit me to take that social liberty, to the days when my friend Copperfield and myself were younger, and fought our way in the world side by side.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 벗 코퍼필드가 이런 격의 없는 호의를 허락해 준다면, 내 벗 코퍼필드와 내가 더 젊었던 시절, 어깨를 나란히 하고 세상과 싸워 헤쳐 나가던 그 나날을 위해 마시겠네."], ["I may say, of myself and Copperfield, in words we have sung together before now, that", "나와 코퍼필드를 두고, 우리가 전에 함께 부르던 노랫말로 말하자면 이렇다네."]], [["We twa hae run about the braes And pu’d the gowans’ fine", "우리 둘이 언덕을 누비며 뛰놀았네 그리고 고운 데이지꽃을 꺾었네"]], [["--in a figurative point of view--on several occasions.", "— 비유적인 관점에서 — 여러 번 그랬지."], ["I am not exactly aware,’ said Mr.", "데이지꽃이라는 게 정확히 무엇인지,\""], ["Micawber, with the old roll in his voice, and the old indescribable air of saying something genteel, ‘what gowans may be, but I have no doubt that Copperfield and myself would frequently have taken a pull at them, if it had been feasible.’", "미코버 씨가 예의 그 굴리는 목소리로, 무언가 격조 있는 말을 한다는 예의 그 형언할 수 없는 분위기로 말했다. \"잘은 모르겠네만, 그럴 형편만 됐다면 코퍼필드와 내가 그 꽃을 곧잘 꺾어 당겼으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없네그려.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, at the then present moment, took a pull at his punch.", "미코버 씨는 마침 그 순간에 펀치를 한 모금 쭉 들이켰다."], ["So we all did: Traddles evidently lost in wondering at what distant time Mr. Micawber and I could have been comrades in the battle of the world.", "그래서 우리 모두 그렇게 했다. 트래들스는 도대체 얼마나 먼 옛날에 미코버 씨와 내가 세상이라는 싸움터에서 전우가 될 수 있었던 것인지 궁금해하느라 넋이 나간 게 분명했다."]], [["‘Ahem!’ said Mr. Micawber, clearing his throat, and warming with the punch and with the fire.", "\"에헴!\" 미코버 씨가 목청을 가다듬으며 말했는데, 펀치와 난롯불로 몸이 후끈 달아오른 참이었다."], ["‘My dear, another glass?’", "\"여보, 한 잔 더 하겠소?\""]], [["Mrs. Micawber said it must be very little; but we couldn’t allow that, so it was a glassful.", "미코버 부인은 아주 조금만이라고 했지만, 우리는 그걸 허락할 수 없었으니, 결국 한 잔 가득이 되었다."]], [["‘As we are quite confidential here, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리끼리 아주 허물없는 자리니까요, 코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Micawber, sipping her punch, ‘Mr.", "미코버 부인이 펀치를 홀짝이며 말했다."], ["Traddles being a part of our domesticity, I should much like to have your opinion on Mr. Micawber’s prospects.", "\"트래들스 씨도 우리 식구나 다름없으니, 미코버 씨의 앞날에 대해 당신 의견을 꼭 좀 듣고 싶어요."], ["For corn,’ said Mrs.", "곡물 장사로 말하자면,\""], ["Micawber argumentatively, ‘as I have repeatedly said to Mr. Micawber, may be gentlemanly, but it is not remunerative.", "미코버 부인이 따지듯이 말했다. \"제가 미코버 씨한테 거듭 말씀드렸다시피, 신사답기는 할지언정 벌이가 되질 않아요."], ["Commission to the extent of two and ninepence in a fortnight cannot, however limited our ideas, be considered remunerative.’", "두 주일에 고작 이 실링 구 펜스짜리 수수료라니, 우리 살림 규모가 아무리 소박하다 해도, 그걸 벌이라고 할 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\""]], [["We were all agreed upon that.", "우리는 모두 그 말에 동의했다."]], [["‘Then,’ said Mrs.", "\"그래서,\""], ["Micawber, who prided herself on taking a clear view of things, and keeping Mr. Micawber straight by her woman’s wisdom, when he might otherwise go a little crooked, ‘then I ask myself this question.", "미코버 부인이 말을 이었는데, 그녀는 사물을 명료하게 보는 것을, 그리고 미코버 씨가 자칫 좀 비뚤어질 만하면 여자다운 지혜로 바로잡아 주는 것을 자랑으로 여기는 사람이었다."], ["If corn is not to be relied upon, what is?", "\"그래서 저는 제 자신에게 이렇게 물어봐요."], ["Are coals to be relied upon? Not at all.", "곡물을 믿을 수 없다면, 무얼 믿을 수 있나?"], ["We have turned our attention to that experiment, on the suggestion of my family, and we find it fallacious.’", "석탄은 믿을 만한가? 천만에요. 우리 친정 식구들 권유로 그 시도에 관심을 돌려 봤지만, 헛것임을 알았답니다.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, leaning back in his chair with his hands in his pockets, eyed us aside, and nodded his head, as much as to say that the case was very clearly put.", "미코버 씨는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 의자에 등을 기대고 앉아, 우리를 곁눈질하며 고개를 끄덕였는데, 마치 사정이 아주 명료하게 정리되었다는 듯한 시늉이었다."]], [["‘The articles of corn and coals,’ said Mrs.", "\"곡물과 석탄이라는 품목이,\""], ["Micawber, still more argumentatively, ‘being equally out of the question, Mr.", "미코버 부인이 한층 더 따지듯이 말했다."], ["Copperfield, I naturally look round the world, and say, “What is there in which a person of Mr.", "\"똑같이 논외라고 한다면요, 코퍼필드 씨, 저는 자연스레 온 세상을 둘러보며 이렇게 말하지요."], ["Micawber’s talent is likely to succeed?” And I exclude the doing anything on commission, because commission is not a certainty.", "'미코버 씨 같은 재능을 지닌 사람이 성공할 만한 일이 대체 무엇일까?' 그리고 저는 수수료를 받고 하는 일은 다 제외해요."], ["What is best suited to a person of Mr.", "수수료라는 건 확실한 게 아니니까요."], ["Micawber’s peculiar temperament is, I am convinced, a certainty.’", "미코버 씨의 그 독특한 기질을 지닌 사람에게 가장 잘 맞는 것은, 저는 확신하건대, 확실한 일이에요.\""]], [["Traddles and I both expressed, by a feeling murmur, that this great discovery was no doubt true of Mr. Micawber, and that it did him much credit.", "트래들스와 나는 둘 다 절절한 웅얼거림으로, 이 위대한 발견이 미코버 씨에게는 의심할 바 없이 들어맞으며 그것이 그분의 자랑거리라는 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["‘I will not conceal from you, my dear Mr.", "\"숨기지 않고 말씀드리는데요, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘that I have long felt the Brewing business to be particularly adapted to Mr.", "\"저는 양조업이야말로 미코버 씨에게 특히 잘 맞는다고 오래전부터 느껴 왔답니다."], ["Micawber. Look at Barclay and Perkins!", "바클레이 앤드 퍼킨스를 보세요!"], ["Look at Truman, Hanbury, and Buxton!", "트루먼, 핸버리, 앤드 버크스턴을 보세요!"], ["It is on that extensive footing that Mr. Micawber, I know from my own knowledge of him, is calculated to shine; and the profits, I am told, are e-NOR-MOUS!", "미코버 씨가 빛을 발하도록 점지된 곳은, 제가 그이를 잘 아는 바로 미루어, 바로 그렇게 큰 규모랍니다. 그리고 듣자 하니 그 수익이 어—마—어마하다더군요!"], ["But if Mr. Micawber cannot get into those firms--which decline to answer his letters, when he offers his services even in an inferior capacity--what is the use of dwelling upon that idea? None.", "하지만 미코버 씨가 그런 회사에 들어가지 못한다면 — 그이가 한참 낮은 직책으로라도 일하겠다고 제안해도 회사들은 편지에 답조차 안 해요 — 그런 생각에 매달려 봐야 무슨 소용이겠어요?"], ["I may have a conviction that Mr. Micawber’s manners--’", "아무 소용 없지요. 저는 미코버 씨의 몸가짐이—\""]], [["‘Hem!", "\"흠!"], ["Really, my dear,’ interposed Mr. Micawber.", "정말이지, 여보,\" 미코버 씨가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘My love, be silent,’ said Mrs.", "\"여보, 가만히 좀 계세요.\""], ["Micawber, laying her brown glove on his hand. ‘I may have a conviction, Mr.", "미코버 부인이 갈색 장갑을 낀 손을 그의 손 위에 얹으며 말했다."], ["Copperfield, that Mr. Micawber’s manners peculiarly qualify him for the Banking business.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 저는 미코버 씨의 몸가짐이 은행업에 유달리 잘 맞는다고 확신할 수 있어요."], ["I may argue within myself, that if I had a deposit at a banking-house, the manners of Mr. Micawber, as representing that banking-house, would inspire confidence, and must extend the connexion.", "저는 속으로 이렇게 따져 봐요. 만약 내가 어느 은행에 예금을 맡겨 두었다면, 그 은행을 대표하는 미코버 씨의 몸가짐이 신뢰를 자아낼 테고, 틀림없이 거래를 넓혀 줄 거라고요."], ["But if the various banking-houses refuse to avail themselves of Mr. Micawber’s abilities, or receive the offer of them with contumely, what is the use of dwelling upon THAT idea? None.", "하지만 여러 은행이 미코버 씨의 능력을 쓰려 하지 않거나, 그 제안을 모욕으로 받아넘긴다면, 그런 생각에 매달려 봐야 무슨 소용이겠어요? 아무 소용 없지요."], ["As to originating a banking-business, I may know that there are members of my family who, if they chose to place their money in Mr. Micawber’s hands, might found an establishment of that description.", "은행업을 새로 차리는 일로 말하자면, 우리 친정 식구들 중에는 마음만 먹으면 자기 돈을 미코버 씨 손에 맡겨서 그런 사업체를 세워 줄 만한 사람들이 있다는 것도 저는 알아요."], ["But if they do NOT choose to place their money in Mr. Micawber’s hands--which they don’t--what is the use of that?", "하지만 그들이 자기 돈을 미코버 씨 손에 맡길 마음이 없다면 — 실제로 그들은 그럴 마음이 없는데요 — 그게 다 무슨 소용이겠어요?"], ["Again I contend that we are no farther advanced than we were before.’", "저는 다시 한번 주장하건대, 우리는 전혀 한 발짝도 나아가지 못한 거예요.\""]], [["I shook my head, and said, ‘Not a bit.’ Traddles also shook his head, and said, ‘Not a bit.’", "나는 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"조금도요.\" 트래들스도 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"조금도요.\""]], [["‘What do I deduce from this?’ Mrs.", "\"제가 이로부터 무얼 추론하느냐고요?\""], ["Micawber went on to say, still with the same air of putting a case lucidly.", "미코버 부인이, 여전히 사정을 명료하게 펼쳐 보이는 그 태도로 말을 이었다."], ["‘What is the conclusion, my dear Mr. Copperfield, to which I am irresistibly brought?", "\"이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 제가 거역할 수 없이 다다르는 결론이 무엇이겠어요?"], ["Am I wrong in saying, it is clear that we must live?’", "우리가 살아가야 한다는 것이 명백하다고 말한다면, 제가 틀린 건가요?\""]], [["I answered ‘Not at all!’ and Traddles answered ‘Not at all!’ and I found myself afterwards sagely adding, alone, that a person must either live or die.", "나는 \"전혀요!\" 하고 대답했고, 트래들스도 \"전혀요!\" 하고 대답했다. 그러고 나서 나는 혼자, 사람이란 살든지 죽든지 둘 중 하나일 수밖에 없노라고 제법 지혜로운 척 덧붙이고 있었다."]], [["‘Just so,’ returned Mrs.", "\"바로 그거예요.\""], ["Micawber, ‘It is precisely that.", "미코버 부인이 받았다."], ["And the fact is, my dear Mr.", "\"정확히 그거랍니다."], ["Copperfield, that we can not live without something widely different from existing circumstances shortly turning up.", "그런데 사실인즉슨, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 지금 형편과는 크게 다른 무언가가 머지않아 생겨나지 않고서는 우리가 살아갈 수가 없어요."], ["Now I am convinced, myself, and this I have pointed out to Mr. Micawber several times of late, that things cannot be expected to turn up of themselves.", "그런데 저는요, 저 자신은 확신하는데, 그리고 이건 제가 요즘 미코버 씨한테 몇 번이나 짚어 드린 건데요, 일이란 게 저절로 생겨나기를 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이에요."], ["We must, in a measure, assist to turn them up.", "우리가 어느 정도는 그 일이 생겨나도록 거들어야 해요."], ["I may be wrong, but I have formed that opinion.’", "제가 틀렸을지도 모르지만, 저는 그런 의견을 갖게 되었답니다.\""]], [["Both Traddles and I applauded it highly.", "트래들스도 나도 그 말에 열렬히 박수를 보냈다."]], [["‘Very well,’ said Mrs. Micawber.", "\"좋아요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Then what do I recommend?", "\"그럼 제가 무얼 권하느냐고요?"], ["Here is Mr. Micawber with a variety of qualifications--with great talent--’", "여기 미코버 씨가 있어요. 갖가지 자질을 갖추고 — 대단한 재능을 지니고—\""]], [["‘Really, my love,’ said Mr. Micawber.", "\"정말이지, 여보,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Pray, my dear, allow me to conclude.", "\"여보, 제발, 끝까지 말하게 해 주세요."], ["Here is Mr. Micawber, with a variety of qualifications, with great talent--I should say, with genius, but that may be the partiality of a wife--’", "여기 미코버 씨가 있어요. 갖가지 자질을 갖추고, 대단한 재능을 지니고 — 천재라고 말하고 싶지만, 그건 아내의 편애일지도 모르니—\""]], [["Traddles and I both murmured ‘No.’", "트래들스도 나도 둘 다 \"아니요.\" 하고 웅얼거렸다."]], [["‘And here is Mr. Micawber without any suitable position or employment.", "\"그런데 여기 미코버 씨가 마땅한 자리도, 일자리도 없이 있어요."], ["Where does that responsibility rest?", "그 책임이 어디에 있나요?"], ["Clearly on society.", "명백히 사회에 있지요."], ["Then I would make a fact so disgraceful known, and boldly challenge society to set it right.", "그러니 저라면 그렇게 수치스러운 사실을 널리 알리고, 사회를 향해 그걸 바로잡아 보라고 당당히 도전장을 내밀겠어요."], ["It appears to me, my dear Mr.", "제가 보기엔요, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "미코버 부인이 힘주어 말했다."], ["Micawber, forcibly, ‘that what Mr. Micawber has to do, is to throw down the gauntlet to society, and say, in effect, “Show me who will take that up.", "\"미코버 씨가 해야 할 일은, 사회를 향해 결투의 장갑을 내던지고는 사실상 이렇게 말하는 거예요. '누가 이걸 집어 드는지 어디 보자."], ["Let the party immediately step forward.”’", "그럴 사람은 당장 앞으로 나서라.'\""]], [["I ventured to ask Mrs. Micawber how this was to be done.", "나는 미코버 부인에게 그걸 어떻게 하면 되느냐고 조심스레 물어보았다."]], [["‘By advertising,’ said Mrs.", "\"광고를 내는 거지요.\""], ["Micawber--‘in all the papers.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["It appears to me, that what Mr.", "\"모든 신문에다요."], ["Micawber has to do, in justice to himself, in justice to his family, and I will even go so far as to say in justice to society, by which he has been hitherto overlooked, is to advertise in all the papers; to describe himself plainly as so-and-so, with such and such qualifications and to put it thus: “Now employ me, on remunerative terms, and address, post-paid, to W.", "제가 보기엔요, 미코버 씨가 자기 자신에 대한 도리로, 또 자기 식구에 대한 도리로, 심지어 지금껏 그이를 외면해 온 사회에 대한 도리로 말하더라도, 마땅히 해야 할 일은, 모든 신문에 광고를 내는 거예요. 자기가 어떤 사람이고 이러이러한 자질을 갖췄다고 분명히 밝히고는, 이렇게 적는 거지요."], ["M., Post Office, Camden Town.”’", "'지금 당장 저를 합당한 보수로 고용하시고, 우편 요금 선납으로 캠던 타운 우체국 더블유 엠 앞으로 연락 주시오.'\""]], [["‘This idea of Mrs. Micawber’s, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"미코버 부인의 이 착상은 말일세, 이보게 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, making his shirt-collar meet in front of his chin, and glancing at me sideways, ‘is, in fact, the Leap to which I alluded, when I last had the pleasure of seeing you.’", "미코버 씨가 셔츠 깃을 턱 밑에서 맞물리게 세우고 나를 곁눈으로 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"실은, 내가 지난번에 자네를 만나는 기쁨을 누렸을 때 넌지시 말한 그 도약이라네.\""]], [["‘Advertising is rather expensive,’ I remarked, dubiously.", "\"광고는 비용이 꽤 들 텐데요.\" 나는 미심쩍어하며 말했다."]], [["‘Exactly so!’ said Mrs. Micawber, preserving the same logical air.", "\"바로 그거예요!\" 미코버 부인이 여전히 그 논리정연한 태도를 지키며 말했다."], ["‘Quite true, my dear Mr. Copperfield!", "\"지당하신 말씀이에요, 이보세요 코퍼필드 씨!"], ["I have made the identical observation to Mr.", "제가 미코버 씨한테 똑같은 지적을 했답니다."], ["Micawber. It is for that reason especially, that I think Mr. Micawber ought (as I have already said, in justice to himself, in justice to his family, and in justice to society) to raise a certain sum of money--on a bill.’", "특히 그 때문에라도, 저는 미코버 씨가 마땅히 (이미 말씀드렸듯이, 자기 자신에 대한 도리로, 자기 식구에 대한 도리로, 또 사회에 대한 도리로) 어음을 잡혀 일정한 돈을 마련해야 한다고 생각해요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, leaning back in his chair, trifled with his eye-glass and cast his eyes up at the ceiling; but I thought him observant of Traddles, too, who was looking at the fire.", "미코버 씨는 의자에 등을 기댄 채 외알 안경을 만지작거리며 천장으로 눈길을 올려보냈다. 하지만 내 보기에 그는 난롯불을 바라보고 있던 트래들스도 살피고 있는 것 같았다."]], [["‘If no member of my family,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리 친정 식구들 가운데,\""], ["Micawber, ‘is possessed of sufficient natural feeling to negotiate that bill--I believe there is a better business-term to express what I mean--’", "미코버 부인이 말했다. \"그 어음을 처리해 줄 만한 인지상정을 지닌 사람이 하나도 없다면 — 제가 말하려는 걸 표현하는 더 나은 사업 용어가 있을 텐데요—\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, with his eyes still cast up at the ceiling, suggested ‘Discount.’", "미코버 씨가 여전히 천장으로 눈길을 올려둔 채로 \"할인\"이라고 거들었다."]], [["‘To discount that bill,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘then my opinion is, that Mr.", "\"그 어음을 할인해 줄 사람이 하나도 없다면요,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber should go into the City, should take that bill into the Money Market, and should dispose of it for what he can get. If the individuals in the Money Market oblige Mr.", "\"그렇다면 제 의견은 이래요. 미코버 씨가 시티로 가서, 그 어음을 들고 금융 시장에 나가, 받을 수 있는 만큼 받고 처분해야 한다는 거예요."], ["Micawber to sustain a great sacrifice, that is between themselves and their consciences. I view it, steadily, as an investment.", "금융 시장의 사람들이 미코버 씨로 하여금 큰 희생을 치르게 한다면, 그건 그들과 그들 양심 사이의 문제지요."], ["I recommend Mr. Micawber, my dear Mr.", "저는 그걸 한결같이 투자로 봐요."], ["Copperfield, to do the same; to regard it as an investment which is sure of return, and to make up his mind to any sacrifice.’", "이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 저는 미코버 씨에게도 똑같이 하라고 권하고 있어요. 그걸 틀림없이 돌아올 투자로 여기고, 어떤 희생이든 감수할 각오를 하라고요.\""]], [["I felt, but I am sure I don’t know why, that this was self-denying and devoted in Mrs. Micawber, and I uttered a murmur to that effect.", "나는, 왜 그런지는 정말 모르겠으나, 이것이 미코버 부인다운 헌신이자 희생이라고 느꼈고, 그런 뜻으로 한마디 웅얼거렸다."], ["Traddles, who took his tone from me, did likewise, still looking at the fire.", "나를 따라 장단을 맞추던 트래들스도 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며 똑같이 했다."]], [["‘I will not,’ said Mrs.", "\"저는 더 이상,\""], ["Micawber, finishing her punch, and gathering her scarf about her shoulders, preparatory to her withdrawal to my bedroom: ‘I will not protract these remarks on the subject of Mr.", "미코버 부인이 펀치를 다 비우고는, 내 침실로 물러갈 채비로 어깨에 두른 숄을 여미며 말했다. \"미코버 씨의 금전 문제에 관한 이 이야기를 길게 늘이지 않겠어요."], ["Micawber’s pecuniary affairs. At your fireside, my dear Mr. Copperfield, and in the presence of Mr.", "이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 당신의 난롯가에서, 그리고 그리 오랜 벗은 아니어도 우리 식구나 다름없는 트래들스 씨가 계신 자리에서, 저는 미코버 씨에게 권하는 방도를 당신께 알려 드리지 않을 수가 없었어요."], ["Traddles, who, though not so old a friend, is quite one of ourselves, I could not refrain from making you acquainted with the course I advise Mr. Micawber to take.", "이제 미코버 씨가 분발하고 — 한마디 덧붙이자면 — 자기 자신을 내세워야 할 때가 왔다고 저는 느끼고, 또 이것이 그 방도라고 봐요."], ["I feel that the time is arrived when Mr. Micawber should exert himself and--I will add--assert himself, and it appears to me that these are the means.", "제가 한낱 아녀자에 지나지 않는다는 것도, 또 이런 문제를 따지는 데에는 으레 남자의 판단이 더 미덥다고 여겨진다는 것도 잘 알아요. 그래도 저는 잊지 말아야겠지요."], ["I am aware that I am merely a female, and that a masculine judgement is usually considered more competent to the discussion of such questions; still I must not forget that, when I lived at home with my papa and mama, my papa was in the habit of saying, “Emma’s form is fragile, but her grasp of a subject is inferior to none.”", "제가 친정 아빠 엄마와 함께 살던 시절, 우리 아빠는 곧잘 이렇게 말씀하셨어요. '에마는 몸은 약하지만, 사안을 파악하는 능력만큼은 누구한테도 뒤지지 않는다.' 우리 아빠가 너무 편애하셨다는 건 저도 잘 알아요."], ["That my papa was too partial, I well know; but that he was an observer of character in some degree, my duty and my reason equally forbid me to doubt.’", "하지만 아빠가 사람 됨됨이를 어느 정도는 볼 줄 아는 분이셨다는 것은, 제 도리로 보나 제 이성으로 보나 똑같이 의심할 수 없는 일이랍니다.\""]], [["With these words, and resisting our entreaties that she would grace the remaining circulation of the punch with her presence, Mrs.", "이런 말을 남기고는, 펀치가 남아 도는 동안만이라도 자리를 빛내 달라는 우리의 간청을 뿌리치며, 미코버 부인은 내 침실로 물러갔다."], ["Micawber retired to my bedroom. And really I felt that she was a noble woman--the sort of woman who might have been a Roman matron, and done all manner of heroic things, in times of public trouble.", "그리고 나는 정말로 그녀가 고결한 여성이라는 — 나라가 환난을 겪는 때라면 로마의 귀부인이라도 되어 온갖 영웅적인 일을 해냈을 법한 그런 여성이라는 — 느낌이 들었다."]], [["In the fervour of this impression, I congratulated Mr. Micawber on the treasure he possessed.", "이 감회에 사무친 나머지, 나는 미코버 씨에게 그가 지닌 보배를 두고 축하의 말을 건넸다."], ["So did Traddles. Mr.", "트래들스도 그랬다."], ["Micawber extended his hand to each of us in succession, and then covered his face with his pocket-handkerchief, which I think had more snuff upon it than he was aware of.", "미코버 씨는 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 차례로 손을 내밀더니, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가렸는데, 내 생각에 그 손수건에는 그가 미처 모르는 코담배 가루가 잔뜩 묻어 있었다."], ["He then returned to the punch, in the highest state of exhilaration.", "그러고는 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 다시 펀치 쪽으로 돌아왔다."]], [["He was full of eloquence.", "그는 청산유수였다."], ["He gave us to understand that in our children we lived again, and that, under the pressure of pecuniary difficulties, any accession to their number was doubly welcome. He said that Mrs.", "그는 우리에게, 우리는 우리 자식들 속에서 다시 살아가는 것이며, 금전 곤란의 압박을 받는 처지라 해도 식구 수가 하나 느는 것은 갑절로 반가운 일이라는 뜻을 일러 주었다."], ["Micawber had latterly had her doubts on this point, but that he had dispelled them, and reassured her.", "그는 미코버 부인이 근래에 이 점을 두고 의구심을 품었으나, 자기가 그것을 떨쳐 주고 다시 안심시켰노라고 했다."], ["As to her family, they were totally unworthy of her, and their sentiments were utterly indifferent to him, and they might--I quote his own expression--go to the Devil.", "처가 식구들로 말하자면, 그들은 부인에게는 도무지 어울리지 않는 사람들이고, 그들의 심정 따위는 자기에게 조금도 대수롭지 않으니, 그들은 — 그의 표현을 그대로 옮기자면 — 악마한테나 가 버려도 좋다고 했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber then delivered a warm eulogy on Traddles.", "이어서 미코버 씨는 트래들스에 대한 따뜻한 찬사를 늘어놓았다."], ["He said Traddles’s was a character, to the steady virtues of which he (Mr. Micawber) could lay no claim, but which, he thanked Heaven, he could admire.", "그는 트래들스의 인품으로 말하자면, 그 한결같은 미덕은 자기(미코버 씨)로서는 도저히 가졌다고 내세울 수 없는 것이지만, 하늘에 감사하게도 자기가 우러러볼 줄은 안다고 했다."], ["He feelingly alluded to the young lady, unknown, whom Traddles had honoured with his affection, and who had reciprocated that affection by honouring and blessing Traddles with her affection. Mr.", "그는 트래들스가 애정을 바치는 영광을 안긴, 그리고 트래들스에게 애정을 돌려주어 그를 빛내고 축복해 준, 그 알지 못하는 젊은 아가씨를 정겹게 입에 올렸다. 미코버 씨가 그녀를 위해 잔을 들었다."], ["Micawber pledged her.", "나도 그랬다."], ["So did I. Traddles thanked us both, by saying, with a simplicity and honesty I had sense enough to be quite charmed with, ‘I am very much obliged to you indeed.", "트래들스는, 내가 듣고 흠뻑 반할 만큼 소박하고 진솔하게, 이렇게 말하며 우리 둘에게 고마워했다. \"정말이지 두 분께 무척 감사합니다."], ["And I do assure you, she’s the dearest girl!--’", "그리고 장담하건대, 그녀는 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨예요!—\""]], [["Mr. Micawber took an early opportunity, after that, of hinting, with the utmost delicacy and ceremony, at the state of my affections.", "그 뒤에 미코버 씨는 일찌감치 기회를 잡아, 더없이 섬세하고 격식 차린 태도로 내 연정의 상태를 넌지시 건드렸다."], ["Nothing but the serious assurance of his friend Copperfield to the contrary, he observed, could deprive him of the impression that his friend Copperfield loved and was beloved.", "그는, 자기 벗 코퍼필드가 사랑을 하고 또 사랑을 받고 있다는 인상을, 정작 벗 코퍼필드 본인이 그렇지 않다고 진지하게 단언하지 않고서는 결코 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없다고 말했다."], ["After feeling very hot and uncomfortable for some time, and after a good deal of blushing, stammering, and denying, I said, having my glass in my hand, ‘Well!", "한동안 몸이 무척 후끈거리고 거북한 데다, 한참을 얼굴 붉히고 더듬거리며 잡아떼고 난 끝에, 나는 잔을 손에 든 채 말했다."], ["I would give them D.!’ which so excited and gratified Mr.", "\"좋습니다! 그녀의 머리글자 디를 위해 마시겠어요!\""], ["Micawber, that he ran with a glass of punch into my bedroom, in order that Mrs. Micawber might drink D., who drank it with enthusiasm, crying from within, in a shrill voice, ‘Hear, hear!", "이 말에 미코버 씨가 어찌나 흥이 나고 흐뭇해하던지, 미코버 부인도 디를 위해 마실 수 있도록 펀치 한 잔을 들고 내 침실로 달려갔다. 부인은 그것을 열렬히 들이켜고는, 안에서 새된 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["My dear Mr.", "\"옳소, 옳소!"], ["Copperfield, I am delighted. Hear!’ and tapping at the wall, by way of applause.", "이보세요 코퍼필드 씨, 정말 기뻐요. 옳소!\" 그러면서 박수 대신 벽을 톡톡 두드렸다."]], [["Our conversation, afterwards, took a more worldly turn; Mr.", "그 뒤로 우리의 대화는 한결 세속적인 쪽으로 흘렀다."], ["Micawber telling us that he found Camden Town inconvenient, and that the first thing he contemplated doing, when the advertisement should have been the cause of something satisfactory turning up, was to move.", "미코버 씨는 우리에게, 캠던 타운이 불편하더라며, 광고 덕분에 흡족한 무언가가 생겨나거든 가장 먼저 할 작정인 일은 이사라고 했다."], ["He mentioned a terrace at the western end of Oxford Street, fronting Hyde Park, on which he had always had his eye, but which he did not expect to attain immediately, as it would require a large establishment.", "그는 옥스퍼드 거리 서쪽 끝, 하이드 파크를 마주 본 어느 연립주택을 입에 올렸는데, 늘 거기에 눈독을 들이고 있었으나 당장 손에 넣기를 바라지는 않는다고 했다."], ["There would probably be an interval, he explained, in which he should content himself with the upper part of a house, over some respectable place of business--say in Piccadilly,--which would be a cheerful situation for Mrs. Micawber; and where, by throwing out a bow-window, or carrying up the roof another story, or making some little alteration of that sort, they might live, comfortably and reputably, for a few years.", "큰 살림이 필요할 테니 말이다. 그는 설명하기를, 아마 그 중간에 한동안은 어느 번듯한 상점 위층 — 이를테면 피커딜리쯤의 — 윗부분으로 만족하며 지낼 텐데, 거기라면 미코버 부인에게 쾌적한 자리일 테고, 내닫이창을 하나 내거나, 지붕을 한 층 더 올리거나, 그런 식으로 자그마한 손질을 좀 하면, 몇 해쯤은 안락하고 떳떳하게 살 수 있으리라고 했다."], ["Whatever was reserved for him, he expressly said, or wherever his abode might be, we might rely on this--there would always be a room for Traddles, and a knife and fork for me.", "자기에게 무엇이 점지되어 있든, 또 자기 거처가 어디가 되든, 이것만은 우리가 믿어도 좋다고 그는 분명히 못 박았다 — 거기에는 언제나 트래들스를 위한 방 하나와 나를 위한 나이프와 포크 한 벌이 마련되어 있으리라고."], ["We acknowledged his kindness; and he begged us to forgive his having launched into these practical and business-like details, and to excuse it as natural in one who was making entirely new arrangements in life.", "우리는 그의 친절에 고마움을 표했다. 그러자 그는, 이렇게 실속 있고 사무적인 세부 사항을 늘어놓은 것을 용서해 달라며, 인생의 전혀 새로운 채비를 꾸리고 있는 사람으로서는 자연스러운 일이려니 너그러이 봐 달라고 청했다."]], [["Mrs. Micawber, tapping at the wall again to know if tea were ready, broke up this particular phase of our friendly conversation.", "미코버 부인이 차가 준비됐는지 알아보려고 다시 벽을 톡톡 두드리는 바람에, 우리의 정겨운 대화는 이 대목에서 끊겼다."], ["She made tea for us in a most agreeable manner; and, whenever I went near her, in handing about the tea-cups and bread-and-butter, asked me, in a whisper, whether D. was fair, or dark, or whether she was short, or tall: or something of that kind; which I think I liked.", "부인은 더없이 상냥한 솜씨로 우리에게 차를 끓여 주었다. 그리고 내가 찻잔과 버터 바른 빵을 돌리느라 그녀 곁에 갈 때마다, 그녀는 속삭임으로 내게 묻기를, 디는 살결이 흰지 검은지, 키가 작은지 큰지, 뭐 그런 것들을 물었는데, 내 생각에 나는 그게 싫지 않았다."], ["After tea, we discussed a variety of topics before the fire; and Mrs.", "차를 마신 뒤, 우리는 난롯가에서 갖가지 화제를 놓고 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Micawber was good enough to sing us (in a small, thin, flat voice, which I remembered to have considered, when I first knew her, the very table-beer of acoustics) the favourite ballads of ‘The Dashing White Sergeant’, and ‘Little Tafflin’. For both of these songs Mrs.", "그리고 미코버 부인은 고맙게도 우리에게 '늠름한 백의의 하사관'과 '꼬마 태플린'이라는 즐겨 부르는 발라드 두 곡을 (가늘고 얇고 밋밋한 목소리로 불러 주었는데, 나는 그녀를 처음 알았을 적에 그 목소리를 음향계의 맹물 맥주쯤으로 여겼던 기억이 난다) 불러 주었다."], ["Micawber had been famous when she lived at home with her papa and mama. Mr.", "이 두 노래로 미코버 부인은 친정 아빠 엄마와 함께 살던 시절에 이름을 날린 바 있었다."], ["Micawber told us, that when he heard her sing the first one, on the first occasion of his seeing her beneath the parental roof, she had attracted his attention in an extraordinary degree; but that when it came to Little Tafflin, he had resolved to win that woman or perish in the attempt.", "미코버 씨가 우리에게 들려주기를, 친정집 지붕 아래서 그녀를 처음 보았을 때 첫 곡을 부르는 걸 들었는데, 그녀가 자기 마음을 어찌나 유난히도 사로잡던지, 그러다 '꼬마 태플린'에 이르렀을 때는, 저 여인을 손에 넣지 못하면 그 시도 끝에 죽어 버리리라 결심했다고 했다."]], [["It was between ten and eleven o’clock when Mrs. Micawber rose to replace her cap in the whitey-brown paper parcel, and to put on her bonnet. Mr.", "미코버 부인이 일어나 모자를 다시 누르스름한 갈색 종이 꾸러미에 넣고 보닛을 쓴 것은 열 시에서 열한 시 사이였다."], ["Micawber took the opportunity of Traddles putting on his great-coat, to slip a letter into my hand, with a whispered request that I would read it at my leisure.", "미코버 씨는 트래들스가 외투를 걸치는 틈을 타, 내 손에 편지 한 통을 슬쩍 쥐여 주며, 한가할 때 읽어 달라고 속삭여 부탁했다."], ["I also took the opportunity of my holding a candle over the banisters to light them down, when Mr. Micawber was going first, leading Mrs. Micawber, and Traddles was following with the cap, to detain Traddles for a moment on the top of the stairs.", "나도 기회를 잡았는데, 미코버 씨가 먼저 미코버 부인을 이끌고 앞장서고 트래들스가 모자를 들고 뒤따르며 내려갈 때, 내가 난간 너머로 촛불을 비춰 길을 밝혀 주면서, 잠시 트래들스를 계단 꼭대기에 붙들어 둔 것이다."]], [["‘Traddles,’ said I, ‘Mr.", "\"트래들스,\" 내가 말했다."], ["Micawber don’t mean any harm, poor fellow: but, if I were you, I wouldn’t lend him anything.’", "\"미코버 씨는 나쁜 뜻이야 없는 분이지, 가엾은 양반. 하지만 내가 자네라면, 그분한테 아무것도 빌려주지 않겠네.\""]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ returned Traddles, smiling, ‘I haven’t got anything to lend.’", "\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 웃으며 받았다. \"빌려줄 게 있어야 말이지.\""]], [["‘You have got a name, you know,’ said I.", "\"이름은 있잖나.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You call THAT something to lend?’ returned Traddles, with a thoughtful look.", "그걸 빌려줄 것이라고 하는 건가?\" 트래들스가 골똘한 낯빛으로 받았다."]], [["‘Certainly.’", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Traddles.", "\"오!\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘Yes, to be sure!", "\"그래, 그렇고말고!"], ["I am very much obliged to you, Copperfield; but--I am afraid I have lent him that already.’", "정말 고맙네, 코퍼필드. 하지만 — 그건 이미 빌려준 것 같아서 걱정일세.\""]], [["‘For the bill that is to be a certain investment?’ I inquired.", "\"틀림없는 투자가 된다는 그 어음 때문에?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ said Traddles.", "\"아니,\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘Not for that one. This is the first I have heard of that one.", "\"그 어음 때문은 아니야. 그 어음 얘기는 나도 지금 처음 듣는걸."], ["I have been thinking that he will most likely propose that one, on the way home.", "아마 집에 가는 길에 그 어음 얘기를 꺼내실 것 같다는 생각은 들지만."], ["Mine’s another.’", "내 건 다른 거야.\""]], [["‘I hope there will be nothing wrong about it,’ said I.", "\"아무 탈 없으면 좋으련만.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I hope not,’ said Traddles.", "\"탈이 없어야 할 텐데.\""], ["‘I should think not, though, because he told me, only the other day, that it was provided for.", "트래들스가 말했다. \"그래도 탈은 없을 것 같아. 바로 며칠 전에 그분이 나한테 그건 대비가 다 돼 있다고 했거든."], ["That was Mr. Micawber’s expression, “Provided for.”’", "그게 미코버 씨의 표현이었지. '대비가 돼 있다.'\""]], [["Mr. Micawber looking up at this juncture to where we were standing, I had only time to repeat my caution.", "마침 그때 미코버 씨가 우리가 서 있는 쪽으로 고개를 들어 올려다보는 바람에, 나는 내 당부를 한 번 더 되뇔 겨를밖에 없었다."], ["Traddles thanked me, and descended.", "트래들스는 내게 고마워하고는 내려갔다."], ["But I was much afraid, when I observed the good-natured manner in which he went down with the cap in his hand, and gave Mrs. Micawber his arm, that he would be carried into the Money Market neck and heels.", "하지만 그가 손에 모자를 든 채 그 사람 좋은 태도로 내려가, 미코버 부인에게 팔을 내미는 모습을 지켜보고 있자니, 나는 그가 멱살을 잡힌 채 금융 시장으로 통째로 끌려가는 게 아닐까 무척 두려웠다."]], [["I returned to my fireside, and was musing, half gravely and half laughing, on the character of Mr. Micawber and the old relations between us, when I heard a quick step ascending the stairs.", "나는 다시 난롯가로 돌아와, 미코버 씨의 사람됨과 우리 사이의 오랜 인연을 두고 반은 진지하게 반은 우습게 곰곰 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그러다 계단을 빠르게 올라오는 발소리를 들었다."], ["At first, I thought it was Traddles coming back for something Mrs.", "처음에는 미코버 부인이 두고 간 무언가를 가지러 트래들스가 되돌아오는 줄 알았다."], ["Micawber had left behind; but as the step approached, I knew it, and felt my heart beat high, and the blood rush to my face, for it was Steerforth’s.", "하지만 그 발소리가 가까워지자, 나는 그것을 알아채고는 가슴이 쿵쾅쿵쾅 뛰고 얼굴로 피가 확 쏠리는 것을 느꼈으니, 그건 스티어포스의 발소리였던 것이다."]], [["I was never unmindful of Agnes, and she never left that sanctuary in my thoughts--if I may call it so--where I had placed her from the first.", "나는 한시도 애그니스를 마음에서 놓은 적이 없었고, 그녀는 내가 처음부터 그녀를 모셔 둔, 내 마음속 그 성소라고 불러도 좋을 자리를 결코 떠난 적이 없었다."], ["But when he entered, and stood before me with his hand out, the darkness that had fallen on him changed to light, and I felt confounded and ashamed of having doubted one I loved so heartily.", "그러나 그가 들어와 손을 내밀며 내 앞에 서자, 그를 덮고 있던 어둠은 빛으로 바뀌었고, 나는 그토록 진심으로 아끼는 사람을 의심했던 것이 부끄럽고 면구스러웠다. 그렇다고 그녀를 덜 사랑하게 된 것은 아니었다."], ["I loved her none the less; I thought of her as the same benignant, gentle angel in my life; I reproached myself, not her, with having done him an injury; and I would have made him any atonement if I had known what to make, and how to make it.", "나는 여전히 그녀를 내 인생의 한결같이 자애롭고 다정한 천사로 여겼다. 나는 그녀가 아니라 나 자신을, 그에게 못할 짓을 했다고 나무랐다. 그리고 어떻게 보상해야 할지, 또 어떻게 해야 할지를 알았더라면 어떤 보상이든 그에게 해 주었을 것이다."]], [["‘Why, Daisy, old boy, dumb-foundered!’ laughed Steerforth, shaking my hand heartily, and throwing it gaily away.", "\"이런, 데이지, 이 친구야, 넋이 나갔구먼!\" 스티어포스가 내 손을 진심으로 꼭 쥐었다가 흥겹게 휙 놓으며 웃었다."], ["‘Have I detected you in another feast, you Sybarite!", "\"또 잔치를 벌이는 현장을 내가 딱 붙잡았군그래, 이 사치꾼아!"], ["These Doctors’ Commons fellows are the gayest men in town, I believe, and beat us sober Oxford people all to nothing!’ His bright glance went merrily round the room, as he took the seat on the sofa opposite to me, which Mrs. Micawber had recently vacated, and stirred the fire into a blaze.", "이 민법박사회관 친구들이 런던에서 제일가는 한량들이라더니, 우리 술도 안 마시는 옥스퍼드 사람들쯤은 완전히 코를 납작하게 만들어 버리는걸!\" 미코버 부인이 방금 비우고 간, 나와 마주 보는 소파 자리에 앉으며, 그의 환한 눈길이 즐겁게 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌더니, 난롯불을 들쑤셔 활활 타오르게 했다."]], [["‘I was so surprised at first,’ said I, giving him welcome with all the cordiality I felt, ‘that I had hardly breath to greet you with, Steerforth.’", "\"처음엔 어찌나 놀랐던지,\" 나는 진심에서 우러난 온 정성으로 그를 맞으며 말했다. \"자넬 반길 숨조차 거의 안 나오더군, 스티어포스.\""]], [["‘Well, the sight of me is good for sore eyes, as the Scotch say,’ replied Steerforth, ‘and so is the sight of you, Daisy, in full bloom.", "\"뭐, 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나, 내 얼굴을 보면 아픈 눈도 낫는 법이지.\" 스티어포스가 대꾸했다. \"활짝 핀 자네 얼굴을 보는 것도 그렇고 말이야, 데이지."], ["How are you, my Bacchanal?’", "잘 지냈나, 나의 술꾼 양반?\""]], [["‘I am very well,’ said I; ‘and not at all Bacchanalian tonight, though I confess to another party of three.’", "\"아주 잘 지냈네.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 오늘 밤은 도무지 술꾼이 아닐세. 셋이서 또 한바탕 모이긴 했네만.\""]], [["‘All of whom I met in the street, talking loud in your praise,’ returned Steerforth.", "\"내가 길에서 그 셋을 다 만났는데, 자네 칭찬을 큰 소리로 늘어놓더군.\" 스티어포스가 받았다."], ["‘Who’s our friend in the tights?’", "\"그 쫄바지 입은 친구는 누군가?\""]], [["I gave him the best idea I could, in a few words, of Mr.", "나는 미코버 씨에 대해 몇 마디로 최대한 잘 알려 주었다."], ["Micawber. He laughed heartily at my feeble portrait of that gentleman, and said he was a man to know, and he must know him.", "그는 그 신사에 대한 내 어설픈 묘사에 진심으로 호탕하게 웃고는, 알아 둘 만한 사람이니 자기가 꼭 알아야겠다고 했다."], ["‘But who do you suppose our other friend is?’ said I, in my turn.", "\"그런데 다른 친구는 누군지 짐작이 가나?\" 이번에는 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Heaven knows,’ said Steerforth.", "\"낸들 알겠나.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Not a bore, I hope? I thought he looked a little like one.’", "\"따분한 친구는 아니겠지? 내 보기엔 좀 그래 보이던데.\""]], [["‘Traddles!’ I replied, triumphantly.", "\"트래들스야!\" 내가 의기양양하게 대답했다."]], [["‘Who’s he?’ asked Steerforth, in his careless way.", "\"그게 누군데?\" 스티어포스가 예의 그 무심한 투로 물었다."]], [["‘Don’t you remember Traddles? Traddles in our room at Salem House?’", "\"트래들스 기억 안 나? 세일럼 하우스에서 우리랑 한 방 쓰던 트래들스 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["That fellow!’ said Steerforth, beating a lump of coal on the top of the fire, with the poker.", "그 친구!\" 스티어포스가 부지깽이로 난롯불 위의 석탄 덩어리를 툭툭 치며 말했다."], ["‘Is he as soft as ever?", "\"여전히 그렇게 물러 터졌나?"], ["And where the deuce did you pick him up?’", "그런데 도대체 어디서 그 친구를 주워 왔어?\""]], [["I extolled Traddles in reply, as highly as I could; for I felt that Steerforth rather slighted him.", "나는 대답으로 트래들스를 할 수 있는 한 한껏 추켜세웠다. 스티어포스가 그를 좀 깔보는 듯한 느낌이 들었기 때문이다."], ["Steerforth, dismissing the subject with a light nod, and a smile, and the remark that he would be glad to see the old fellow too, for he had always been an odd fish, inquired if I could give him anything to eat?", "스티어포스는 가벼운 끄덕임과 미소로, 그리고 그 친구도 늘 별난 놈이긴 했으니 자기도 만나면 반갑겠다는 말로 그 화제를 털어 버리고는, 내게 뭐 먹을 것 좀 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["During most of this short dialogue, when he had not been speaking in a wild vivacious manner, he had sat idly beating on the lump of coal with the poker.", "이 짤막한 대화 내내, 그는 거칠게 활기찬 투로 말을 하지 않을 때면 부지깽이로 석탄 덩어리를 멍하니 툭툭 치며 앉아 있었다."], ["I observed that he did the same thing while I was getting out the remains of the pigeon-pie, and so forth.", "내가 비둘기 파이 남은 것이며 이것저것을 꺼내는 동안에도 그가 똑같이 그러고 있는 것을 나는 알아챘다."]], [["‘Why, Daisy, here’s a supper for a king!’ he exclaimed, starting out of his silence with a burst, and taking his seat at the table.", "\"이야, 데이지, 이건 임금님이 먹을 만한 저녁상인걸!\" 그가 침묵에서 불쑥 튀어나오듯 외치며 식탁 자리에 앉았다."], ["‘I shall do it justice, for I have come from Yarmouth.’", "\"내 이걸 제대로 대접해 주지. 야머스에서 오는 길이라 말이야.\""]], [["‘I thought you came from Oxford?’ I returned.", "\"옥스퍼드에서 오는 줄 알았는데?\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘Not I,’ said Steerforth.", "\"천만에.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘I have been seafaring--better employed.’", "\"나는 뱃놀이를 하고 있었네 — 그게 더 나은 소일거리지.\""]], [["‘Littimer was here today, to inquire for you,’ I remarked, ‘and I understood him that you were at Oxford; though, now I think of it, he certainly did not say so.’", "\"리티머가 오늘 자넬 찾으러 여기 왔었네.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 그 사람 말을 자네가 옥스퍼드에 있다는 뜻으로 알아들었는데, 지금 가만 생각해 보니 분명 그렇게 말하지는 않았어.\""]], [["‘Littimer is a greater fool than I thought him, to have been inquiring for me at all,’ said Steerforth, jovially pouring out a glass of wine, and drinking to me.", "\"리티머가 도대체 나를 찾으러 다녔다니, 내가 생각했던 것보다 더한 멍청이로군.\" 스티어포스가 유쾌하게 포도주를 한 잔 따라 내게 권하며 말했다."], ["‘As to understanding him, you are a cleverer fellow than most of us, Daisy, if you can do that.’", "\"그 친구 말을 알아듣는 것으로 말하자면, 데이지, 자네가 그걸 해낼 수 있다면 우리 중 누구보다도 영리한 사람일세.\""]], [["‘That’s true, indeed,’ said I, moving my chair to the table.", "\"그건 정말 그래.\" 내가 의자를 식탁 쪽으로 당기며 말했다."], ["‘So you have been at Yarmouth, Steerforth!’ interested to know all about it.", "\"그래, 야머스에 다녀왔다니, 스티어포스!\" 나는 그 일에 대해 죄다 알고 싶어서 물었다."], ["‘Have you been there long?’", "\"거기 오래 있었나?\""]], [["‘No,’ he returned.", "\"아니.\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘An escapade of a week or so.’", "\"한 일주일쯤 훌쩍 다녀온 거야.\""]], [["‘And how are they all?", "\"다들 어떻게 지내나?"], ["Of course, little Emily is not married yet?’", "물론 꼬마 에밀리는 아직 결혼 안 했겠지?\""]], [["‘Not yet.", "\"아직."], ["Going to be, I believe--in so many weeks, or months, or something or other. I have not seen much of ‘em.", "곧 할 모양이야 — 몇 주 뒤든, 몇 달 뒤든, 뭐 그쯤에. 그 사람들이랑 그렇게 많이 어울리진 않았어."], ["By the by’; he laid down his knife and fork, which he had been using with great diligence, and began feeling in his pockets; ‘I have a letter for you.’", "참 그렇지.\" 그는 그토록 부지런히 놀리던 나이프와 포크를 내려놓고는, 주머니를 뒤지기 시작했다. \"자네한테 줄 편지가 있어.\""]], [["‘From whom?’", "\"누구한테서 온 건데?\""]], [["‘Why, from your old nurse,’ he returned, taking some papers out of his breast pocket. “‘J.", "\"아, 자네의 옛 유모한테서지.\" 그가 가슴 주머니에서 종이 몇 장을 꺼내며 받았다."], ["Steerforth, Esquire, debtor, to The Willing Mind”; that’s not it.", "\"'제이 스티어포스 귀하, 더 윌링 마인드에 외상값 빚짐.'"], ["Patience, and we’ll find it presently.", "이건 아니고. 좀 참아 봐, 곧 찾을 테니."], ["Old what’s-his-name’s in a bad way, and it’s about that, I believe.’", "그 뭐냐 하는 노인네가 형편이 안 좋다는데, 그 일에 관한 것 같아.\""]], [["‘Barkis, do you mean?’", "\"바키스 말인가?\""]], [["‘Yes!’ still feeling in his pockets, and looking over their contents: ‘it’s all over with poor Barkis, I am afraid.", "\"맞아!\" 그가 여전히 주머니를 뒤지며 그 안의 것들을 훑어보았다. \"가엾은 바키스도 이젠 다 끝난 것 같아 걱정일세."], ["I saw a little apothecary there--surgeon, or whatever he is--who brought your worship into the world.", "거기서 자그마한 약제사를 — 외과의든 뭐든 — 하나 만났는데, 그 양반이 자네 나리를 이 세상에 받아 낸 사람이더군."], ["He was mighty learned about the case, to me; but the upshot of his opinion was, that the carrier was making his last journey rather fast.---Put your hand into the breast pocket of my great-coat on the chair yonder, and I think you’ll find the letter.", "나한테 그 병에 대해 어찌나 유식하게 떠들던지. 하지만 그 말의 요지인즉슨, 그 짐마차꾼이 마지막 길을 제법 빠르게 가고 있다는 거였어. — 저기 의자에 걸린 내 외투 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣어 보게."], ["Is it there?’", "거기 편지가 있을 거야. 있나?\""]], [["‘Here it is!’ said I.", "\"여기 있네!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘That’s right!’", "\"됐어!\""]], [["It was from Peggotty; something less legible than usual, and brief.", "페거티에게서 온 것이었다. 평소보다 다소 알아보기 어렵고 짤막했다."], ["It informed me of her husband’s hopeless state, and hinted at his being ‘a little nearer’ than heretofore, and consequently more difficult to manage for his own comfort.", "편지는 남편의 가망 없는 상태를 알리면서, 그가 전보다 '조금 가까워졌고', 그래서 그를 편안하게 보살피기가 더 어려워졌다고 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["It said nothing of her weariness and watching, and praised him highly.", "자기가 얼마나 지치고 밤을 새우는지에 대해서는 한마디도 없이, 그를 한껏 칭찬했다."], ["It was written with a plain, unaffected, homely piety that I knew to be genuine, and ended with ‘my duty to my ever darling’--meaning myself.", "그것은 내가 진실하다고 알고 있는, 꾸밈없고 수수하고 정겨운 신앙심으로 쓰여 있었고, '나의 영원한 사랑둥이에게 정성을 다하여'라는 말로 끝맺었다 — 나를 두고 하는 말이었다."]], [["While I deciphered it, Steerforth continued to eat and drink.", "내가 그 편지를 더듬더듬 읽어 가는 동안, 스티어포스는 줄곧 먹고 마셨다."]], [["‘It’s a bad job,’ he said, when I had done; ‘but the sun sets every day, and people die every minute, and we mustn’t be scared by the common lot.", "\"안된 일이군.\" 내가 다 읽고 나자 그가 말했다. \"하지만 해는 날마다 지고, 사람은 시시각각 죽는 법이니, 우리가 누구에게나 닥치는 그 흔한 운명에 겁먹어서는 안 되지."], ["If we failed to hold our own, because that equal foot at all men’s doors was heard knocking somewhere, every object in this world would slip from us. No!", "만인의 문간을 똑같이 두드리는 그 발소리가 어디선가 들려온다고 해서 우리가 제 자리를 지키지 못한다면, 이 세상 모든 것이 우리 손아귀에서 빠져나가고 말 거야. 안 돼!"], ["Ride on!", "계속 달려!"], ["Rough-shod if need be, smooth-shod if that will do, but ride on!", "필요하면 험하게 박차를 가하든, 그걸로 되면 부드럽게 가든, 어쨌든 계속 달려!"], ["Ride on over all obstacles, and win the race!’", "모든 걸림돌을 넘어 계속 달려서, 경주에서 이겨 내!\""]], [["‘And win what race?’ said I.", "\"무슨 경주에서 이긴단 말인가?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘The race that one has started in,’ said he.", "\"자기가 뛰어든 그 경주에서.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Ride on!’", "\"계속 달리는 거지!\""]], [["I noticed, I remember, as he paused, looking at me with his handsome head a little thrown back, and his glass raised in his hand, that, though the freshness of the sea-wind was on his face, and it was ruddy, there were traces in it, made since I last saw it, as if he had applied himself to some habitual strain of the fervent energy which, when roused, was so passionately roused within him.", "기억하건대, 그가 잘생긴 머리를 약간 뒤로 젖히고 손에 잔을 든 채 말을 멈추고 나를 바라보았을 때, 나는 알아챘다. 그의 얼굴에는 바닷바람의 싱그러움이 어려 발그레했지만, 내가 지난번에 보았을 때 이후로 새로 생긴 흔적이 있었다는 것을. 마치 한번 불붙으면 그토록 격정적으로 타오르는 그 뜨거운 열정을, 무슨 버릇처럼 끊임없이 쥐어짜며 매달려 온 사람 같았다."], ["I had it in my thoughts to remonstrate with him upon his desperate way of pursuing any fancy that he took--such as this buffeting of rough seas, and braving of hard weather, for example--when my mind glanced off to the immediate subject of our conversation again, and pursued that instead.", "나는 그가 무슨 변덕이든 한번 꽂히면 그것을 그토록 무모하게 좇는 방식을 — 이를테면 이번처럼 거친 바다에 부대끼고 험한 날씨에 맞서는 짓을 — 두고 한마디 타이를 마음을 먹고 있었다. 그런데 그때 내 마음이 다시 우리 대화의 당장의 화제로 빗겨 가, 그것을 대신 좇아갔다."]], [["‘I tell you what, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘if your high spirits will listen to me--’", "\"내 말 좀 들어 보게, 스티어포스.\" 내가 말했다. \"자네의 그 드높은 기운이 내 말에 좀 귀를 기울여 준다면—\""]], [["‘They are potent spirits, and will do whatever you like,’ he answered, moving from the table to the fireside again.", "\"강력한 기운이지. 자네가 원하는 건 뭐든 들어줄 걸세.\" 그가 식탁에서 다시 난롯가로 옮겨 앉으며 대답했다."]], [["‘Then I tell you what, Steerforth.", "\"그럼 내 말 좀 들어 보게, 스티어포스."], ["I think I will go down and see my old nurse.", "나는 내려가서 내 옛 유모를 만나 볼까 하네."], ["It is not that I can do her any good, or render her any real service; but she is so attached to me that my visit will have as much effect on her, as if I could do both.", "내가 그녀에게 무슨 도움을 줄 수 있다거나, 무슨 실질적인 보탬이 된다는 건 아니야. 다만 그녀가 나를 어찌나 따르는지, 내가 방문하는 것만으로도 내가 그 둘 다를 해 줄 수 있는 것만큼이나 그녀에게 큰 위로가 될 거란 말일세."], ["She will take it so kindly that it will be a comfort and support to her.", "그녀는 그걸 무척 고맙게 여길 테고, 그게 그녀에게 위안이 되고 버팀목이 될 거야."], ["It is no great effort to make, I am sure, for such a friend as she has been to me.", "그녀가 내게 그토록 좋은 벗이 되어 준 걸 생각하면, 분명 이건 그리 대단한 수고도 아니지."], ["Wouldn’t you go a day’s journey, if you were in my place?’", "자네가 내 처지라면 하루 길쯤은 가지 않겠나?\""]], [["His face was thoughtful, and he sat considering a little before he answered, in a low voice, ‘Well! Go.", "그의 얼굴은 생각에 잠겼다. 그는 잠시 골똘히 헤아리다가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. \"그래! 가게."], ["You can do no harm.’", "해 될 거야 없으니.\""]], [["‘You have just come back,’ said I, ‘and it would be in vain to ask you to go with me?’", "\"자네는 막 돌아온 참이니,\" 내가 말했다. \"함께 가자고 청해 봐야 헛일이겠지?\""]], [["‘Quite,’ he returned.", "\"전혀 안 되지.\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘I am for Highgate tonight.", "\"나는 오늘 밤 하이게이트로 갈 참이야."], ["I have not seen my mother this long time, and it lies upon my conscience, for it’s something to be loved as she loves her prodigal son.---Bah!", "어머니를 못 뵌 지 한참 됐고, 그게 양심에 걸리거든. 어머니가 그 탕아 같은 아들을 사랑하시는 만큼 사랑받는다는 건, 그래도 대단한 일이니까. — 에이!"], ["Nonsense!--You mean to go tomorrow, I suppose?’ he said, holding me out at arm’s length, with a hand on each of my shoulders.", "다 부질없는 소리지! — 자네는 내일 갈 작정인 거지?\" 그가 두 손을 내 양어깨에 하나씩 얹고 나를 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓으며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, I think so.’", "\"응, 그럴 생각이야.\""]], [["‘Well, then, don’t go till next day.", "\"그럼, 모레 가게."], ["I wanted you to come and stay a few days with us.", "자네가 와서 우리랑 며칠 묵었으면 했단 말이야."], ["Here I am, on purpose to bid you, and you fly off to Yarmouth!’", "일부러 자넬 부르러 여기 왔는데, 자네는 야머스로 훌쩍 날아가 버리겠다니!\""]], [["‘You are a nice fellow to talk of flying off, Steerforth, who are always running wild on some unknown expedition or other!’", "\"훌쩍 날아간다는 말을 할 처지가 못 되는걸, 스티어포스. 자네야말로 늘 무슨 알 수 없는 원정인지 뭔지를 찾아 거칠게 싸돌아다니지 않나!\""]], [["He looked at me for a moment without speaking, and then rejoined, still holding me as before, and giving me a shake:", "그는 아무 말 없이 잠시 나를 바라보다가, 아까처럼 나를 붙든 채 한 번 흔들며 다시 받았다."]], [["‘Come!", "\"자!"], ["Say the next day, and pass as much of tomorrow as you can with us!", "모레라고 하게. 그리고 내일도 되도록 많은 시간을 우리랑 보내!"], ["Who knows when we may meet again, else? Come!", "안 그러면 우리가 언제 또 만날지 누가 알겠나? 자!"], ["Say the next day!", "모레라고 하게!"], ["I want you to stand between Rosa Dartle and me, and keep us asunder.’", "나는 자네가 로자 다틀과 나 사이에 끼어 우리 둘을 떼어 놔 주면 좋겠어.\""]], [["‘Would you love each other too much, without me?’", "\"내가 없으면 자네 둘이 서로를 너무 사랑하기라도 할까 봐?\""]], [["‘Yes; or hate,’ laughed Steerforth; ‘no matter which. Come!", "\"그래, 아니면 너무 미워하든가.\" 스티어포스가 웃었다. \"어느 쪽이든 상관없어. 자!"], ["Say the next day!’", "모레라고 하게!\""]], [["I said the next day; and he put on his great-coat and lighted his cigar, and set off to walk home. Finding him in this intention, I put on my own great-coat (but did not light my own cigar, having had enough of that for one while) and walked with him as far as the open road: a dull road, then, at night. He was in great spirits all the way; and when we parted, and I looked after him going so gallantly and airily homeward, I thought of his saying, ‘Ride on over all obstacles, and win the race!’ and wished, for the first time, that he had some worthy race to run.", "나는 모레라고 했다. 그러자 그는 외투를 걸치고 여송연에 불을 붙이고는 집을 향해 걸어가려고 나섰다. 그가 이럴 작정인 것을 알고, 나도 내 외투를 걸치고 (다만 내 여송연에는 불을 붙이지 않았는데, 당분간은 그것으로 족할 만큼 피웠던 터였다) 그와 함께 큰길까지 걸었다. 그 무렵 밤에는 음산한 길이었다. 그는 가는 내내 기운이 넘쳤다. 그리고 우리가 헤어진 뒤, 그가 그토록 늠름하고 경쾌하게 집을 향해 가는 뒷모습을 바라보고 있자니, '모든 걸림돌을 넘어 계속 달려서, 경주에서 이겨 내!'라던 그의 말이 떠올랐다. 그리고 나는, 그가 달려 볼 만한 값진 경주가 있었으면 하고, 난생처음 바랐다. 내 방에서 옷을 벗고 있는데, 미코버 씨의 편지가 바닥으로 툭 떨어졌다. 그래서 그 편지가 생각나, 나는 봉인을 뜯고 다음과 같이 읽었다. 그것은 저녁 식사 한 시간 반 전에 쓰인 것이었다. 내가 앞서 말했는지 모르겠으나, 미코버 씨는 무슨 유달리 절망적인 위기에 처할 때면 일종의 법률 투의 말투를 쓰곤 했는데, 그게 자기 신변을 정리하는 일에 맞먹는다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["I was undressing in my own room, when Mr. Micawber’s letter tumbled on the floor. Thus reminded of it, I broke the seal and read as follows.", "'선생 — 감히 친애하는 코퍼필드라고는 부르지 못하겠기에,\n'본인은 짓이겨졌음을 귀하께 알려 드리는 것이 마땅하겠습니다."], ["It was dated an hour and a half before dinner. I am not sure whether I have mentioned that, when Mr.", "자신의 비참한 처지를 귀하께서 너무 일찍 알게 되지 않도록 막아 보려는 가물거리는 노력의 흔적을, 귀하께서는 오늘 본인에게서 보셨을 것입니다."], ["Micawber was at any particularly desperate crisis, he used a sort of legal phraseology, which he seemed to think equivalent to winding up his affairs.", "그러나 희망은 지평선 아래로 가라앉았고, 본인은 짓이겨졌습니다."]], [["‘It is expedient that I should inform you that the undersigned is Crushed. Some flickering efforts to spare you the premature knowledge of his calamitous position, you may observe in him this day; but hope has sunk beneath the horizon, and the undersigned is Crushed.", "'이 서신은 어느 한 사람의 — 그 인품 곁이라고는 차마 말 못 하겠고 — 그 신변 범위 안에서 쓰이고 있습니다. 그자는 만취에 가까운 상태로, 어느 차압업자에게 고용된 사람입니다. 그자는 집세 미납에 따른 압류 명목으로, 이 거처를 법적으로 점유하고 있습니다. 그자의 재산 목록에는, 이 집의 연차 임차인인 본인에게 속한 온갖 종류의 동산과 세간뿐 아니라, 이너 템플 명예 협회 회원인 하숙인 토머스 트래들스 씨에게 속한 물건들까지 들어 있습니다. '지금 (불멸의 어느 작가의 표현을 빌리자면) 본인의 입술로 \"넘겨진\" 그 넘쳐흐르는 잔에, 만에 하나 한 방울의 암울함이라도 모자랐다면, 그것은 다음과 같은 사실에서 채워질 것입니다. 즉, 앞서 말한 토머스 트래들스 씨가 본인에게 호의로 인수해 준 이십삼 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스 반의 어음이 기한을 넘겼는데도, 대비가 돼 있지 않다는 사실에서 말입니다. 또한, 본인에게 매달린 산 입들의 책임이, 자연의 순리에 따라, 가엾은 희생자 하나가 더 늘어남으로써 무거워지리라는 사실에서 말입니다. 그 비참한 출현은 — 어림잡아 — 지금으로부터 여섯 달을 넘기지 않는 기간이 다하는 무렵에 예견될 수 있습니다."]], [["‘The present communication is penned within the personal range (I cannot call it the society) of an individual, in a state closely bordering on intoxication, employed by a broker. That individual is in legal possession of the premises, under a distress for rent. His inventory includes, not only the chattels and effects of every description belonging to the undersigned, as yearly tenant of this habitation, but also those appertaining to Mr. Thomas Traddles, lodger, a member of the Honourable Society of the Inner Temple.", "'이만큼 미리 말씀드렸으니, 티끌과 잿더미가 영원토록 흩뿌려졌다는 말을 덧붙이는 것은 사족이 될 것입니다. '그"]], [["‘If any drop of gloom were wanting in the overflowing cup, which is now “commended” (in the language of an immortal Writer) to the lips of the undersigned, it would be found in the fact, that a friendly acceptance granted to the undersigned, by the before-mentioned Mr. Thomas Traddles, for the sum Of 23l 4s 9 1/2d is over due, and is NOT provided for. Also, in the fact that the living responsibilities clinging to the undersigned will, in the course of nature, be increased by the sum of one more helpless victim; whose miserable appearance may be looked for--in round numbers--at the expiration of a period not exceeding six lunar months from the present date.", "'머\n'리"]], [["‘After premising thus much, it would be a work of supererogation to add, that dust and ashes are for ever scattered", "'위\n'에"]], [["Poor Traddles!", "'윌킨스 미코버.'"], ["I knew enough of Mr.", "가엾은 트래들스!"], ["Micawber by this time, to foresee that he might be expected to recover the blow; but my night’s rest was sorely distressed by thoughts of Traddles, and of the curate’s daughter, who was one of ten, down in Devonshire, and who was such a dear girl, and who would wait for Traddles (ominous praise!) until she was sixty, or any age that could be mentioned.", "이 무렵 나는 미코버 씨를 충분히 알았던 터라, 그가 이 타격에서 회복하리라고 능히 내다볼 수 있었다. 하지만 그날 밤 내 잠자리는 트래들스 생각으로, 그리고 데번셔 시골에 사는 그 부목사의 딸 생각으로 몹시 어지러웠다. 열 남매 중 하나인 그 아가씨는 그토록 사랑스러운 처녀였고, 예순 살이 되도록, 아니 입에 올릴 수 있는 그 어떤 나이가 되도록 트래들스를 기다려 줄 (불길한 칭찬이로다!) 사람이었으니."]], [["# CHAPTER 29. I VISIT STEERFORTH AT HIS HOME, AGAIN", "# CHAPTER 29. I VISIT STEERFORTH AT HIS HOME, AGAIN"]], [["I mentioned to Mr. Spenlow in the morning, that I wanted leave of absence for a short time; and as I was not in the receipt of any salary, and consequently was not obnoxious to the implacable Jorkins, there was no difficulty about it.", "아침에 나는 스펜로 씨에게 잠깐 휴가를 얻고 싶다고 말했다. 나는 봉급을 받는 처지가 아니었고, 따라서 그 무자비한 조킨스의 미움을 살 까닭도 없었으므로, 휴가를 얻는 데는 아무런 어려움이 없었다."], ["I took that opportunity, with my voice sticking in my throat, and my sight failing as I uttered the words, to express my hope that Miss Spenlow was quite well; to which Mr.", "나는 그 기회를 빌려, 목소리가 목구멍에 걸리고 말을 내뱉는 동안 눈앞이 아득해지는 채로, 스펜로 양이 잘 지내시기를 바란다는 뜻을 전했다."], ["Spenlow replied, with no more emotion than if he had been speaking of an ordinary human being, that he was much obliged to me, and she was very well.", "그러자 스펜로 씨는 마치 흔해 빠진 보통 사람을 두고 말하기라도 하는 양 아무런 감정도 없이, 고맙다고, 딸은 아주 잘 지낸다고 대답했다."]], [["We articled clerks, as germs of the patrician order of proctors, were treated with so much consideration, that I was almost my own master at all times.", "우리 견습 서기들은 귀족적인 대리인 계급의 씨앗으로서 어찌나 융숭한 대접을 받았던지, 나는 거의 언제나 내 시간의 주인이나 다름없었다."], ["As I did not care, however, to get to Highgate before one or two o’clock in the day, and as we had another little excommunication case in court that morning, which was called The office of the judge promoted by Tipkins against Bullock for his soul’s correction, I passed an hour or two in attendance on it with Mr.", "그렇지만 나는 한낮 한두 시 전에는 하이게이트에 도착하고 싶지 않았고, 마침 그날 아침 법정에는 또 하나의 자그마한 파문 사건이 걸려 있었는데, 사건명은 '팁킨스가 영혼의 교정을 위해 불록을 상대로 제기하여 판사가 직권으로 다루는 사건'이었다. 그래서 나는 스펜로 씨를 따라 한두 시간을 자못 즐겁게 그 사건에 들이부었다."], ["Spenlow very agreeably. It arose out of a scuffle between two churchwardens, one of whom was alleged to have pushed the other against a pump; the handle of which pump projecting into a school-house, which school-house was under a gable of the church-roof, made the push an ecclesiastical offence.", "사건은 두 교회 관리인 사이의 몸싸움에서 비롯된 것이었는데, 그중 한 사람이 다른 한 사람을 펌프 쪽으로 밀쳤다는 혐의였다. 그 펌프 손잡이가 학교 건물 안으로 비죽 튀어나와 있었고, 그 학교 건물은 교회 지붕의 박공 아래에 있었던 까닭에, 그 밀치는 행위가 교회법상의 위반이 된 것이었다."], ["It was an amusing case; and sent me up to Highgate, on the box of the stage-coach, thinking about the Commons, and what Mr. Spenlow had said about touching the Commons and bringing down the country.", "재미있는 사건이었다. 그 사건 덕분에 나는 역마차 마부석에 올라앉아 하이게이트로 가는 내내, 박사회와, 박사회를 건드리면 나라가 무너진다던 스펜로 씨의 말을 곱씹었다."]], [["Mrs. Steerforth was pleased to see me, and so was Rosa Dartle.", "스티어포스 부인은 나를 보고 반가워했고, 로자 다틀도 그랬다."], ["I was agreeably surprised to find that Littimer was not there, and that we were attended by a modest little parlour-maid, with blue ribbons in her cap, whose eye it was much more pleasant, and much less disconcerting, to catch by accident, than the eye of that respectable man.", "리티머가 그 자리에 없다는 것을 알게 되어 나는 기분 좋게 놀랐는데, 우리 시중을 든 것은 모자에 파란 리본을 단 얌전하고 자그마한 응접실 하녀였다. 그 점잖은 사내의 눈을 우연히 마주치는 것보다, 그 하녀의 눈을 마주치는 편이 훨씬 더 기분 좋고 훨씬 덜 거북했다."], ["But what I particularly observed, before I had been half-an-hour in the house, was the close and attentive watch Miss Dartle kept upon me; and the lurking manner in which she seemed to compare my face with Steerforth’s, and Steerforth’s with mine, and to lie in wait for something to come out between the two.", "그런데 그 집에 들어선 지 반 시간도 채 되기 전에 내가 유독 눈여겨본 것은, 다틀 양이 나를 향해 바짝, 잔뜩 신경을 곤두세우고 지켜본다는 사실이었다. 그녀는 내 얼굴을 스티어포스의 얼굴과, 또 스티어포스의 얼굴을 내 얼굴과 슬그머니 견주어 보면서, 둘 사이에서 무언가 튀어나오기를 도사려 기다리는 듯한 태도를 보였다."], ["So surely as I looked towards her, did I see that eager visage, with its gaunt black eyes and searching brow, intent on mine; or passing suddenly from mine to Steerforth’s; or comprehending both of us at once.", "내가 그녀 쪽으로 눈길을 돌릴 때마다, 그 간절한 얼굴이, 움푹 꺼진 검은 눈과 캐묻는 듯한 이마를 하고서, 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 노리거나, 내 얼굴에서 갑자기 스티어포스의 얼굴로 옮겨 가거나, 우리 둘을 한꺼번에 거머쥐고 있는 것이 어김없이 보였다."], ["In this lynx-like scrutiny she was so far from faltering when she saw I observed it, that at such a time she only fixed her piercing look upon me with a more intent expression still.", "이 스라소니 같은 감시에서, 그녀는 내가 그것을 알아챈 것을 보고서도 결코 움츠러들기는커녕, 오히려 그럴 때면 더욱더 골똘한 표정으로 그 꿰뚫는 시선을 나에게 못 박았다."], ["Blameless as I was, and knew that I was, in reference to any wrong she could possibly suspect me of, I shrunk before her strange eyes, quite unable to endure their hungry lustre.", "그녀가 나를 두고 의심할 만한 어떤 잘못과 관련해서도 나는 떳떳했고, 또 떳떳하다는 것을 스스로 알고 있었음에도, 나는 그 굶주린 광채를 도무지 견디지 못하고 그 기이한 눈앞에서 움츠러들었다."]], [["All day, she seemed to pervade the whole house.", "온종일 그녀는 온 집안을 떠도는 것만 같았다."], ["If I talked to Steerforth in his room, I heard her dress rustle in the little gallery outside.", "내가 스티어포스의 방에서 그와 이야기를 나누면, 바깥 작은 복도에서 그녀의 옷자락 스치는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["When he and I engaged in some of our old exercises on the lawn behind the house, I saw her face pass from window to window, like a wandering light, until it fixed itself in one, and watched us.", "그와 내가 집 뒤 잔디밭에서 옛날에 하던 운동을 좀 해 보려고 하면, 그녀의 얼굴이 떠도는 불빛처럼 이 창에서 저 창으로 옮겨 가다가, 마침내 어느 한 창에 멈춰 서서 우리를 지켜보는 것이 보였다."], ["When we all four went out walking in the afternoon, she closed her thin hand on my arm like a spring, to keep me back, while Steerforth and his mother went on out of hearing: and then spoke to me.", "오후에 우리 넷이서 산책을 나갔을 때는, 스티어포스와 그의 어머니가 듣지 못할 만큼 앞서 걸어 나가자, 그녀가 그 가느다란 손을 용수철처럼 내 팔에 척 감아 나를 붙들어 세우더니, 이렇게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘You have been a long time,’ she said, ‘without coming here.", "\"오랫동안,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여기 발걸음을 안 했더군요."], ["Is your profession really so engaging and interesting as to absorb your whole attention?", "당신 일이 정말로 그렇게나 마음을 사로잡고 흥미진진해서, 온통 정신을 다 빼앗아 가나요?"], ["I ask because I always want to be informed, when I am ignorant.", "내가 모르는 게 있으면 늘 알고 싶어서 묻는 거예요."], ["Is it really, though?’", "정말 그래요?\""]], [["I replied that I liked it well enough, but that I certainly could not claim so much for it.", "나는 그 일이 제법 마음에 들기는 하지만, 그 정도로까지 대단하다고는 도저히 내세울 수 없다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["I am glad to know that, because I always like to be put right when I am wrong,’ said Rosa Dartle.", "그걸 알게 되어 다행이네요. 나는 내가 틀렸을 때 바로잡아 주는 걸 늘 좋아하거든요.\" 로자 다틀이 말했다."], ["‘You mean it is a little dry, perhaps?’", "\"그러니까 좀 무미건조하다는 말씀인가요, 아마?\""]], [["‘Well,’ I replied; ‘perhaps it was a little dry.’", "\"글쎄요,\" 나는 대답했다. \"어쩌면 좀 무미건조하기는 하지요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["and that’s a reason why you want relief and change--excitement and all that?’ said she. ‘Ah!", "그래서 당신은 기분 전환과 변화가, 자극이며 그런 것들이 필요한 거로군요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아아!"], ["very true!", "정말 그렇겠네요!"], ["But isn’t it a little--Eh?--for him; I don’t mean you?’", "그런데 그게 그분한테는 좀, 에—? 당신 말고 말이에요.\""]], [["A quick glance of her eye towards the spot where Steerforth was walking, with his mother leaning on his arm, showed me whom she meant; but beyond that, I was quite lost.", "스티어포스가 어머니를 자기 팔에 기대게 한 채 거닐고 있는 쪽으로 그녀의 눈이 잽싸게 한 번 흘끗 가는 것을 보고, 나는 그녀가 누구를 가리키는지는 알아챘다. 그러나 그 이상은 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수 없었다."], ["And I looked so, I have no doubt.", "내 표정이 그래 보였으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않는다."]], [["‘Don’t it--I don’t say that it does, mind I want to know--don’t it rather engross him?", "\"그게, 꼭 그렇다는 건 아니에요, 잘 들어요, 난 알고 싶은 것뿐이니까, 그게 그분을 좀 너무 사로잡지는 않나요?"], ["Don’t it make him, perhaps, a little more remiss than usual in his visits to his blindly-doting--eh?’ With another quick glance at them, and such a glance at me as seemed to look into my innermost thoughts.", "그것 때문에 그분이, 어쩌면, 자기를 눈멀도록 끔찍이 아끼는 분을 찾아오는 일에 평소보다 좀 더 소홀해지지는 않나요, 에—?\" 그러면서 또 한 번 그들 쪽을 잽싸게 흘끗 보고는, 내 가장 깊은 속마음까지 들여다보려는 듯한 눈길을 나에게 던졌다."]], [["‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘pray do not think--’", "\"다틀 양,\" 나는 대답했다. \"제발 그런 생각은 마—\""]], [["‘I don’t!’ she said.", "\"안 해요!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Oh dear me, don’t suppose that I think anything!", "\"어머나 세상에, 내가 무슨 생각을 한다고 여기지 말아요!"], ["I am not suspicious.", "난 의심하는 게 아니에요."], ["I only ask a question.", "그저 물어볼 뿐이지요."], ["I don’t state any opinion.", "무슨 의견을 내세우는 게 아니라고요."], ["I want to found an opinion on what you tell me.", "당신이 일러 주는 말을 바탕으로 의견을 세워 보려는 거예요."], ["Then, it’s not so? Well!", "그래서, 그렇지 않다는 거지요? 좋아요!"], ["I am very glad to know it.’", "그걸 알게 되어 정말 기쁘네요.\""]], [["‘It certainly is not the fact,’ said I, perplexed, ‘that I am accountable for Steerforth’s having been away from home longer than usual--if he has been: which I really don’t know at this moment, unless I understand it from you. I have not seen him this long while, until last night.’", "\"분명히 사실이 아닙니다.\" 나는 어리둥절해하며 말했다. \"스티어포스가 평소보다 더 오래 집을 비웠다고 해서 그게 제 탓이라는 건요. 정말 그가 그랬다 해도 말이지요. 사실 저는 지금 이 순간까지도 그걸 몰랐어요, 당신한테 듣고서야 알았을 뿐이지요. 어젯밤에 보기 전까지는 한참 동안 그를 본 적이 없거든요.\" \"그래요?\""]], [["‘No?’\n‘Indeed, Miss Dartle, no!’", "\"정말입니다, 다틀 양, 없어요!\""]], [["As she looked full at me, I saw her face grow sharper and paler, and the marks of the old wound lengthen out until it cut through the disfigured lip, and deep into the nether lip, and slanted down the face.", "그녀가 나를 똑바로 쳐다보았을 때, 나는 그녀의 얼굴이 더 날카로워지고 더 창백해지는 것을, 그리고 그 오래된 상처 자국이 길게 늘어나면서 일그러진 윗입술을 가르고 아랫입술 깊숙이 파고들어, 얼굴을 비스듬히 내리긋는 것을 보았다."], ["There was something positively awful to me in this, and in the brightness of her eyes, as she said, looking fixedly at me:", "거기에는, 그리고 그녀가 나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 이렇게 말할 때 그 두 눈에 서린 광채에는, 나로서는 정말이지 끔찍하다 싶은 무언가가 있었다."]], [["‘What is he doing?’", "\"그가 뭘 하고 있죠?\""]], [["I repeated the words, more to myself than her, being so amazed.", "나는 너무 어안이 벙벙한 나머지, 그녀에게라기보다 나 자신에게 하듯 그 말을 따라 되뇌었다."]], [["‘What is he doing?’ she said, with an eagerness that seemed enough to consume her like a fire.", "\"그가 뭘 하고 있느냐고요?\" 그녀가, 불길처럼 제 몸을 태워 버릴 듯한 간절함으로 말했다."], ["‘In what is that man assisting him, who never looks at me without an inscrutable falsehood in his eyes?", "\"나를 볼 때마다 그 눈에 알 수 없는 거짓을 담고 있는 그 사내가, 그를 거들어 도대체 무슨 짓을 꾸미고 있는 거죠?"], ["If you are honourable and faithful, I don’t ask you to betray your friend.", "당신이 명예롭고 신의 있는 사람이라면, 당신더러 친구를 배신하라고는 하지 않아요."], ["I ask you only to tell me, is it anger, is it hatred, is it pride, is it restlessness, is it some wild fancy, is it love, what is it, that is leading him?’", "그저 이것만 말해 줘요. 그를 이끌고 있는 게 노여움인가요, 미움인가요, 자존심인가요, 들뜬 마음인가요, 어떤 거친 망상인가요, 사랑인가요, 도대체 그게 뭐죠?\""]], [["‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘how shall I tell you, so that you will believe me, that I know of nothing in Steerforth different from what there was when I first came here?", "\"다틀 양,\" 나는 대답했다. \"스티어포스에게서 제가 여기 처음 왔을 때와 달라진 점이라곤 아무것도 모른다는 걸, 어떻게 말씀드려야 당신이 믿어 주시겠습니까?"], ["I can think of nothing.", "짚이는 게 아무것도 없어요."], ["I firmly believe there is nothing.", "정말이지 아무것도 없다고 굳게 믿습니다."], ["I hardly understand even what you mean.’", "무슨 말씀을 하시는지조차 거의 알아듣지 못하겠어요.\""]], [["As she still stood looking fixedly at me, a twitching or throbbing, from which I could not dissociate the idea of pain, came into that cruel mark; and lifted up the corner of her lip as if with scorn, or with a pity that despised its object.", "그녀가 여전히 나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 서 있는 사이, 그 잔인한 흉터에, 내가 고통이라는 생각과 도저히 떼어 놓을 수 없는 어떤 씰룩거림 혹은 욱신거림이 떠오르더니, 입술 한쪽 끝을 들어 올렸다. 마치 비웃듯이, 아니면 그 대상을 경멸하는 듯한 연민으로. 그녀는 황급히 거기에 손을 갖다 댔다."], ["She put her hand upon it hurriedly--a hand so thin and delicate, that when I had seen her hold it up before the fire to shade her face, I had compared it in my thoughts to fine porcelain--and saying, in a quick, fierce, passionate way, ‘I swear you to secrecy about this!’ said not a word more.", "어찌나 가늘고 여린 손이었던지, 그녀가 그 손을 들어 올려 난롯불 앞에서 얼굴을 가리는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 속으로 그 손을 고운 자기에 견주었었다. 그러고는 빠르고 사납고 격정에 찬 목소리로 \"이 일에 대해선 비밀로 하겠다고 맹세해요!\" 하고 말하고는, 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Steerforth was particularly happy in her son’s society, and Steerforth was, on this occasion, particularly attentive and respectful to her.", "스티어포스 부인은 아들과 함께 있는 것을 유달리 행복해했고, 스티어포스도 이번에는 어머니에게 유달리 다정하고 정중했다."], ["It was very interesting to me to see them together, not only on account of their mutual affection, but because of the strong personal resemblance between them, and the manner in which what was haughty or impetuous in him was softened by age and sex, in her, to a gracious dignity.", "두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 나에게 무척 흥미로웠는데, 서로를 향한 애정 때문만이 아니라, 둘 사이의 강한 외모의 닮음과, 그에게서 도도하거나 격하게 드러나는 것이 그녀에게서는 나이와 성별 덕분에 부드러워져 기품 있는 위엄으로 누그러진다는 점 때문이었다. 나는 한 번 이상 이런 생각을 했다."], ["I thought, more than once, that it was well no serious cause of division had ever come between them; or two such natures--I ought rather to express it, two such shades of the same nature--might have been harder to reconcile than the two extremest opposites in creation.", "두 사람 사이에 갈라설 만한 심각한 까닭이 한 번도 끼어들지 않은 것은 다행한 일이라고. 그렇지 않았다면 그토록 닮은 두 천성은, 아니 차라리 같은 천성의 두 빛깔이라고 해야겠는데, 세상에서 가장 극단으로 맞선 두 정반대보다도 화해시키기가 더 어려웠을지도 모른다고."], ["The idea did not originate in my own discernment, I am bound to confess, but in a speech of Rosa Dartle’s. She said at dinner:", "솔직히 고백하건대, 그 생각은 내 분별에서 비롯된 것이 아니라 로자 다틀의 한마디에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["‘Oh, but do tell me, though, somebody, because I have been thinking about it all day, and I want to know.’", "그녀는 저녁 식사 자리에서 이렇게 말했다. \"아, 그런데 누구든 좀 말해 줘요. 온종일 그것만 생각하고 있었거든요. 정말 알고 싶어서요.\""]], [["‘You want to know what, Rosa?’ returned Mrs. Steerforth.", "\"무엇을 알고 싶다는 거니, 로자?\" 스티어포스 부인이 받아 말했다."], ["‘Pray, pray, Rosa, do not be mysterious.’", "\"제발, 제발, 로자, 알쏭달쏭하게 굴지 좀 마라.\""]], [["‘Mysterious!’ she cried. ‘Oh! really?", "\"알쏭달쏭하다고요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"어머! 정말요?"], ["Do you consider me so?’", "저를 그렇게 보세요?\""]], [["‘Do I constantly entreat you,’ said Mrs.", "\"내가 늘 너한테 부탁하지 않니,\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다."], ["Steerforth, ‘to speak plainly, in your own natural manner?’", "\"있는 그대로, 네 본래의 모습 그대로 분명하게 말하라고?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["then this is not my natural manner?’ she rejoined.", "그러면 이게 제 본래 모습이 아니라는 건가요?\" 그녀가 되받았다."], ["‘Now you must really bear with me, because I ask for information.", "\"이제 정말 저를 좀 참아 주셔야겠어요. 알고 싶어서 여쭙는 거니까요."], ["We never know ourselves.’", "우리는 우리 자신을 결코 모르잖아요.\""]], [["‘It has become a second nature,’ said Mrs.", "\"그게 어느새 제이의 천성이 되어 버렸지.\" 스티어포스 부인이 조금도 언짢은 기색 없이 말했다. \"하지만 나는 기억한단다."], ["Steerforth, without any displeasure; ‘but I remember,--and so must you, I think,--when your manner was different, Rosa; when it was not so guarded, and was more trustful.’", "너도 기억할 거야, 아마. 네 태도가 지금과 달랐던 때를 말이다, 로자. 그렇게 경계심에 차 있지 않고, 더 마음을 열고 있던 때를.\""]], [["‘I am sure you are right,’ she returned; ‘and so it is that bad habits grow upon one!", "\"정말 맞는 말씀이세요.\" 그녀가 받아 말했다. \"그렇게 나쁜 버릇이 사람한테 차츰 배는 거지요! 정말요?"], ["Really? Less guarded and more trustful?", "덜 경계하고 더 마음을 열고 있었다고요?"], ["How can I, imperceptibly, have changed, I wonder!", "어떻게 나도 모르는 새에 그렇게 변할 수가 있었을까요!"], ["Well, that’s very odd!", "어머, 참 묘하네요!"], ["I must study to regain my former self.’", "옛날의 저를 되찾도록 애써 봐야겠어요.\""]], [["‘I wish you would,’ said Mrs. Steerforth, with a smile.", "\"그래 주면 좋겠구나.\" 스티어포스 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["I really will, you know!’ she answered.", "정말로 그럴게요, 아시겠죠!\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘I will learn frankness from--let me see--from James.’", "\"솔직함을 배워야겠어요, 누구한테냐면, 가만있자, 제임스한테서요.\""]], [["‘You cannot learn frankness, Rosa,’ said Mrs.", "\"솔직함이라면, 로자,\" 스티어포스 부인이 얼른 말했다."], ["Steerforth quickly--for there was always some effect of sarcasm in what Rosa Dartle said, though it was said, as this was, in the most unconscious manner in the world--‘in a better school.’", "로자 다틀의 말에는, 이번처럼 세상에 둘도 없이 무심한 태도로 내뱉어도, 늘 어딘가 빈정대는 느낌이 묻어났기 때문이다. \"그보다 더 좋은 스승은 없겠지.\""]], [["‘That I am sure of,’ she answered, with uncommon fervour.", "\"그건 저도 확신해요.\" 그녀가 보기 드문 열의를 담아 대답했다."], ["‘If I am sure of anything, of course, you know, I am sure of that.’", "\"제가 무언가를 확신한다면, 당연히, 아시다시피, 그건 확신하지요.\""]], [["Mrs. Steerforth appeared to me to regret having been a little nettled; for she presently said, in a kind tone:", "스티어포스 부인은 조금 발끈했던 것을 후회하는 듯 보였다. 이내 다정한 어조로 이렇게 말했으니까."]], [["‘Well, my dear Rosa, we have not heard what it is that you want to be satisfied about?’", "\"자, 로자야, 네가 무엇을 알고 싶어 하는지 우리는 아직 못 들었구나?\""]], [["‘That I want to be satisfied about?’ she replied, with provoking coldness. ‘Oh!", "\"제가 무엇을 알고 싶어 하느냐고요?\" 그녀가 사람 약 올리는 듯한 냉랭함으로 대답했다. \"어머!"], ["It was only whether people, who are like each other in their moral constitution--is that the phrase?’", "그저, 도덕적 기질이 서로 닮은 사람들이, 그게 맞는 표현인가요?\""]], [["‘It’s as good a phrase as another,’ said Steerforth.", "\"다른 어떤 표현 못지않게 좋은 표현이지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you:--whether people, who are like each other in their moral constitution, are in greater danger than people not so circumstanced, supposing any serious cause of variance to arise between them, of being divided angrily and deeply?’", "\"고마워요. 그러니까, 도덕적 기질이 서로 닮은 사람들이, 그렇지 않은 처지의 사람들보다, 둘 사이에 무언가 심각한 다툼의 씨앗이 생겼다고 칠 때, 화를 내며 깊이 갈라설 위험이 더 큰가 하는 거예요.\""]], [["‘I should say yes,’ said Steerforth.", "\"그렇다고 봐야겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."]], [["‘Should you?’ she retorted. ‘Dear me!", "\"그럴까요?\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"어머나!"], ["Supposing then, for instance--any unlikely thing will do for a supposition--that you and your mother were to have a serious quarrel.’", "그럼 가령, 예를 들어, 있을 법하지 않은 일이라야 가정이 되니까, 당신과 어머님이 심각하게 다투신다고 쳐 봐요.\""]], [["‘My dear Rosa,’ interposed Mrs.", "\"얘, 로자야,\""], ["Steerforth, laughing good-naturedly, ‘suggest some other supposition!", "스티어포스 부인이 사람 좋게 웃으며 끼어들었다. \"다른 가정을 좀 들려무나!"], ["James and I know our duty to each other better, I pray Heaven!’", "제임스와 나는 서로에 대한 도리를 그보다는 잘 안단다, 하느님 감사하게도!\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Miss Dartle, nodding her head thoughtfully.", "\"어머!\" 다틀 양이 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘To be sure.", "\"그렇겠지요."], ["That would prevent it?", "그게 다툼을 막아 준다는 거지요?"], ["Why, of course it would. Exactly.", "아니, 물론 그렇겠지요. 바로 그거예요."], ["Now, I am glad I have been so foolish as to put the case, for it is so very good to know that your duty to each other would prevent it!", "자, 제가 이렇게 어리석게 그런 경우를 들어 본 게 다행이네요. 서로에 대한 도리가 그걸 막아 준다는 걸 알게 되어 정말 좋으니까요!"], ["Thank you very much.’", "정말 고마워요.\""]], [["One other little circumstance connected with Miss Dartle I must not omit; for I had reason to remember it thereafter, when all the irremediable past was rendered plain.", "다틀 양과 관련하여 또 하나의 자그마한 일을 빠뜨려서는 안 되겠다. 훗날, 돌이킬 수 없는 지난 일이 모두 환히 드러났을 때, 나는 그것을 떠올릴 까닭이 있었으니까."], ["During the whole of this day, but especially from this period of it, Steerforth exerted himself with his utmost skill, and that was with his utmost ease, to charm this singular creature into a pleasant and pleased companion.", "그날 하루 종일, 그러나 특히 이 무렵부터, 스티어포스는 자신의 온갖 재주를 다해, 그것도 더없이 수월하게 다해, 이 별난 사람을 흐뭇하고 유쾌한 길동무로 홀려 놓으려 애썼다."], ["That he should succeed, was no matter of surprise to me.", "그가 성공하리라는 것은 나에게 조금도 놀라운 일이 아니었다."], ["That she should struggle against the fascinating influence of his delightful art--delightful nature I thought it then--did not surprise me either; for I knew that she was sometimes jaundiced and perverse.", "그녀가 그의 유쾌한 재주, 그때 나로서는 유쾌한 천성이라 여겼던 그 매혹적인 힘에 맞서 버둥대는 것 또한 놀랍지 않았다. 그녀가 이따금 비뚤어지고 심술궂어진다는 것을 나는 알고 있었으니까. 나는 그녀의 얼굴빛과 태도가 천천히 변해 가는 것을 보았다."], ["I saw her features and her manner slowly change; I saw her look at him with growing admiration; I saw her try, more and more faintly, but always angrily, as if she condemned a weakness in herself, to resist the captivating power that he possessed; and finally, I saw her sharp glance soften, and her smile become quite gentle, and I ceased to be afraid of her as I had really been all day, and we all sat about the fire, talking and laughing together, with as little reserve as if we had been children.", "나는 그녀가 점점 더 큰 흠모의 눈길로 그를 바라보는 것을 보았다. 나는 그녀가, 마치 제 안의 나약함을 꾸짖기라도 하듯, 점점 더 힘없이, 그러나 늘 화가 난 듯이, 그가 지닌 그 사람 홀리는 힘에 맞서려 애쓰는 것을 보았다. 그리고 마침내 나는 그녀의 날카로운 눈길이 부드러워지고, 그 미소가 자못 온화해지는 것을 보았으며, 온종일 정말로 품고 있던 그녀에 대한 두려움도 어느새 사라져, 우리 넷은 모두 난롯가에 둘러앉아, 마치 어린아이들처럼 거리낌 없이 이야기하고 웃었다."]], [["Whether it was because we had sat there so long, or because Steerforth was resolved not to lose the advantage he had gained, I do not know; but we did not remain in the dining-room more than five minutes after her departure.", "우리가 그곳에 너무 오래 앉아 있었기 때문인지, 아니면 스티어포스가 애써 얻은 이점을 놓치지 않겠다고 작정한 탓인지는 모르겠으나, 그녀가 자리를 뜬 뒤로 우리가 식당에 남아 있던 시간은 오 분도 되지 않았다. \"그녀가 하프를 켜고 있군.\" 응접실 문 앞에서 스티어포스가 나직이 말했다."], ["‘She is playing her harp,’ said Steerforth, softly, at the drawing-room door, ‘and nobody but my mother has heard her do that, I believe, these three years.’ He said it with a curious smile, which was gone directly; and we went into the room and found her alone.", "\"내가 알기로, 어머니 말고는 지난 삼 년 동안 그녀가 하프 켜는 걸 들은 사람이 아무도 없을 거야.\" 그는 묘한 미소를 띠고 그렇게 말했는데, 그 미소는 이내 사라졌다. 우리가 방으로 들어가 보니 그녀는 혼자 있었다."]], [["‘Don’t get up,’ said Steerforth (which she had already done)’ my dear Rosa, don’t!", "\"일어나지 마요.\" 스티어포스가 말했다, 그녀는 이미 일어선 뒤였다. \"로자, 일어나지 말아요!"], ["Be kind for once, and sing us an Irish song.’", "한 번만 너그럽게, 우리한테 아일랜드 노래 한 곡 불러 줘요.\""]], [["‘What do you care for an Irish song?’ she returned.", "\"당신이 아일랜드 노래 따위에 무슨 관심이 있어요?\" 그녀가 되받았다."]], [["‘Much!’ said Steerforth.", "\"많아요!\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Much more than for any other.", "\"다른 어떤 노래보다 훨씬 더."], ["Here is Daisy, too, loves music from his soul.", "여기 데이지도 마음속 깊이 음악을 사랑한답니다."], ["Sing us an Irish song, Rosa! and let me sit and listen as I used to do.’", "아일랜드 노래 한 곡 불러 줘요, 로자! 그러면 옛날처럼 내가 앉아서 들을게요.\""]], [["He did not touch her, or the chair from which she had risen, but sat himself near the harp.", "그는 그녀에게도, 그녀가 일어선 의자에도 손대지 않고, 하프 가까이에 자리를 잡고 앉았다."], ["She stood beside it for some little while, in a curious way, going through the motion of playing it with her right hand, but not sounding it.", "그녀는 한동안 묘한 모양새로 그 옆에 서서, 오른손으로 하프를 켜는 시늉만 할 뿐 소리는 내지 않았다."], ["At length she sat down, and drew it to her with one sudden action, and played and sang.", "마침내 그녀는 자리에 앉아, 단번에 하프를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기더니, 켜며 노래했다."]], [["I don’t know what it was, in her touch or voice, that made that song the most unearthly I have ever heard in my life, or can imagine.", "그녀의 손길에서였는지 목소리에서였는지, 무엇이 그 노래를, 내 평생 들어 본, 아니 상상할 수 있는 그 어떤 노래보다도 이 세상 것 같지 않게 만들었는지 나는 모른다."], ["There was something fearful in the reality of it.", "그 생생함에는 어딘가 무서운 데가 있었다."], ["It was as if it had never been written, or set to music, but sprung out of passion within her; which found imperfect utterance in the low sounds of her voice, and crouched again when all was still.", "마치 그 노래가 애초에 쓰인 적도, 곡이 붙은 적도 없이, 그녀 안의 격정에서 솟아난 것만 같았다. 그 격정은 나직한 목소리로 어설프게 터져 나왔다가, 모든 것이 잠잠해지자 다시 웅크렸다."], ["I was dumb when she leaned beside the harp again, playing it, but not sounding it, with her right hand.", "그녀가 다시 하프 옆에 몸을 기울이고, 오른손으로 켜는 시늉만 할 뿐 소리는 내지 않았을 때, 나는 말을 잃은 채로 있었다."]], [["A minute more, and this had roused me from my trance:--Steerforth had left his seat, and gone to her, and had put his arm laughingly about her, and had said, ‘Come, Rosa, for the future we will love each other very much!’ And she had struck him, and had thrown him off with the fury of a wild cat, and had burst out of the room.", "그러고 다시 한참이 지나서야, 무언가가 나를 그 황홀경에서 깨워 놓았다. 스티어포스가 자리를 떠나 그녀에게로 가더니, 웃으며 그녀의 몸에 팔을 두르고, \"자, 로자, 앞으로 우리 서로 무척 사랑하기로 해요!\" 하고 말한 것이다. 그러자 그녀는 그를 후려치고는, 들고양이 같은 사나움으로 그를 뿌리치고 방을 박차고 나가 버렸다."]], [["‘What is the matter with Rosa?’ said Mrs. Steerforth, coming in.", "\"로자한테 무슨 일이라도 있니?\" 스티어포스 부인이 들어오며 말했다."]], [["‘She has been an angel, mother,’ returned Steerforth, ‘for a little while; and has run into the opposite extreme, since, by way of compensation.’", "\"잠깐 동안,\" 스티어포스가 대답했다. \"천사 노릇을 했어요, 어머니. 그러고는 보상이라도 하려는 듯 정반대 끝으로 내달은 거지요.\""]], [["‘You should be careful not to irritate her, James.", "\"그 애를 화나게 하지 않도록 조심해야 한다, 제임스."], ["Her temper has been soured, remember, and ought not to be tried.’", "그 애 성미가 비뚤어져 있다는 걸 잊지 말거라. 함부로 건드릴 게 못 돼.\""]], [["Rosa did not come back; and no other mention was made of her, until I went with Steerforth into his room to say Good night.", "로자는 돌아오지 않았다. 내가 잘 자라는 인사를 하러 스티어포스와 함께 그의 방으로 들어갈 때까지, 그녀 이야기는 더 나오지 않았다."], ["Then he laughed about her, and asked me if I had ever seen such a fierce little piece of incomprehensibility.", "그제야 그는 그녀를 두고 웃으며, 그렇게 사납고 자그마한, 도무지 알 수 없는 존재를 본 적이 있느냐고 나에게 물었다."]], [["I expressed as much of my astonishment as was then capable of expression, and asked if he could guess what it was that she had taken so much amiss, so suddenly.", "나는 그때 표현할 수 있는 만큼 놀라움을 나타내며, 그녀가 그토록 갑자기, 그토록 심하게 마음 상해 한 까닭이 무엇인지 짐작할 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, Heaven knows,’ said Steerforth.", "\"아, 그건 하느님이나 아시겠지.\" 스티어포스가 말했다."], ["‘Anything you like--or nothing! I told you she took everything, herself included, to a grindstone, and sharpened it.", "\"네 마음대로 갖다 붙여, 아니면 아무것도 아니거나! 누이는 자기 자신을 포함해서 모든 걸 숫돌에 갈아 날을 세운다고 내가 말했잖아."], ["She is an edge-tool, and requires great care in dealing with.", "누이는 날붙이라서, 다룰 때 여간 조심하지 않으면 안 돼."], ["She is always dangerous. Good night!’", "늘 위험한 사람이야. 잘 자!\""]], [["‘Good night!’ said I, ‘my dear Steerforth!", "\"잘 자!\" 나는 말했다. \"친애하는 스티어포스!"], ["I shall be gone before you wake in the morning. Good night!’", "나는 아침에 네가 깨기 전에 떠날 거야. 잘 자!\""]], [["He was unwilling to let me go; and stood, holding me out, with a hand on each of my shoulders, as he had done in my own room.", "그는 나를 보내기 싫어하며, 내 방에서 그랬듯이, 내 양어깨에 한 손씩 얹고 나를 멀찍이 밀어내듯 붙든 채로 서 있었다."]], [["‘Daisy,’ he said, with a smile--‘for though that’s not the name your godfathers and godmothers gave you, it’s the name I like best to call you by--and I wish, I wish, I wish, you could give it to me!’", "\"데이지,\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"네 대부 대모가 지어 준 이름은 아니지만, 너를 부를 때 내가 제일 좋아하는 이름이지. 그리고 나는 바라, 정말 바라, 진정 바라, 네가 그 이름을 나한테 줄 수 있다면 좋겠어!\""]], [["‘Why so I can, if I choose,’ said I.", "\"왜, 내가 마음만 먹으면 줄 수 있지.\" 나는 말했다."]], [["‘Daisy, if anything should ever separate us, you must think of me at my best, old boy. Come!", "\"데이지, 혹시라도 무엇이 우리를 갈라놓는 일이 생기거든, 너는 나를 내 가장 좋았던 모습으로 떠올려 줘야 해, 친구. 자!"], ["Let us make that bargain.", "우리 그렇게 약속하자."], ["Think of me at my best, if circumstances should ever part us!’", "혹시라도 사정이 우리를 갈라놓거든, 나를 내 가장 좋았던 모습으로 생각해 줘!\""]], [["‘You have no best to me, Steerforth,’ said I, ‘and no worst.", "\"너에게는 나에게 가장 좋은 모습도, 가장 나쁜 모습도 따로 없어, 스티어포스.\""], ["You are always equally loved, and cherished in my heart.’", "나는 말했다. \"너는 언제나 한결같이 사랑받고, 내 마음속에 소중히 간직되어 있으니까.\""]], [["So much compunction for having ever wronged him, even by a shapeless thought, did I feel within me, that the confession of having done so was rising to my lips.", "내가 그를, 비록 형체도 없는 한 가닥 생각으로나마 잘못 의심한 적이 있다는 것에 대한 가책이 어찌나 내 안에 사무쳤던지, 그런 적이 있노라는 고백이 입가에까지 차올랐다. 애그니스의 믿음을 저버리기가 꺼려졌던 것, 그리고 그 믿음을 저버릴 위험 없이 그 이야기를 어떻게 꺼내야 할지 막막했던 것만 아니었다면, 그가 \"신의 가호가 있기를, 데이지, 잘 자!\""], ["But for the reluctance I had to betray the confidence of Agnes, but for my uncertainty how to approach the subject with no risk of doing so, it would have reached them before he said, ‘God bless you, Daisy, and good night!’ In my doubt, it did NOT reach them; and we shook hands, and we parted.", "하고 말하기 전에 그 고백이 입 밖으로 나왔으리라. 그러나 그 망설임 속에서, 그것은 끝내 입 밖으로 나오지 못했다. 그리고 우리는 악수를 나누고 헤어졌다."]], [["I was up with the dull dawn, and, having dressed as quietly as I could, looked into his room.", "나는 희뿌연 새벽녘에 일어나, 되도록 소리 나지 않게 옷을 챙겨 입고서, 그의 방을 들여다보았다."], ["He was fast asleep; lying, easily, with his head upon his arm, as I had often seen him lie at school.", "그는 깊이 잠들어 있었다. 학창 시절 누워 있는 모습을 자주 보았던 그대로, 한쪽 팔을 베고 편안하게 누워서."]], [["The time came in its season, and that was very soon, when I almost wondered that nothing troubled his repose, as I looked at him.", "때가 제철에 이르렀을 때, 그것도 머지않아 이르렀을 때, 나는 그를 바라보며, 어떻게 아무것도 그의 잠을 어지럽히지 않는지 거의 의아할 지경이었다."], ["But he slept--let me think of him so again--as I had often seen him sleep at school; and thus, in this silent hour, I left him.", "하지만 그는 잠들어 있었다. 다시 한번 그렇게 떠올리게 해 다오, 학창 시절 잠든 모습을 자주 보았던 그대로. 그렇게, 이 고요한 시각에, 나는 그를 두고 떠났다."], ["--Never more, oh God forgive you, Steerforth!", "다시는, 오, 하느님 그대를 용서하소서, 스티어포스!"], ["to touch that passive hand in love and friendship.", "다시는 그 무력한 손을 사랑과 우정으로 잡지 못하리라."], ["Never, never more!", "다시는, 영영 다시는!"]], [["# CHAPTER 30. A LOSS", "# CHAPTER 30. A LOSS"]], [["I got down to Yarmouth in the evening, and went to the inn.", "저녁 무렵 야머스에 도착해 여관으로 갔다."], ["I knew that Peggotty’s spare room--my room--was likely to have occupation enough in a little while, if that great Visitor, before whose presence all the living must give place, were not already in the house; so I betook myself to the inn, and dined there, and engaged my bed.", "패거티의 빈방, 곧 내 방이 머지않아 사람들로 북적이게 될 것이 뻔했다. 모든 산 자가 그 앞에서 길을 비켜 줘야 하는 그 위대한 손님이 이미 그 집에 들어와 있지 않다면 말이다. 그래서 나는 여관에 들어 저녁을 먹고 잠자리를 잡아 두었다."]], [["It was ten o’clock when I went out.", "밖으로 나섰을 때는 열 시였다."], ["Many of the shops were shut, and the town was dull.", "가게는 대부분 문을 닫았고, 거리는 적막했다."], ["When I came to Omer and Joram’s, I found the shutters up, but the shop door standing open.", "오머앤조럼 가게에 이르렀을 때 덧문은 닫혀 있었으나 가게 문은 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["As I could obtain a perspective view of Mr. Omer inside, smoking his pipe by the parlour door, I entered, and asked him how he was.", "안쪽 거실 문가에서 파이프를 피우고 있는 오머 씨의 모습이 비스듬히 눈에 들어와, 나는 안으로 들어가 안부를 물었다."]], [["‘Why, bless my life and soul!’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 이런 세상에, 맙소사!\""], ["Omer, ‘how do you find yourself?", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"어떻게 지내셨소?"], ["Take a seat.---Smoke not disagreeable, I hope?’", "자, 앉으시오. 담배 연기, 거북하지 않으면 좋겠는데?\""]], [["‘By no means,’ said I.", "\"전혀요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I like it--in somebody else’s pipe.’", "\"전 담배가 좋아요. 남이 피우는 파이프라면요.\""]], [["‘What, not in your own, eh?’ Mr.", "\"뭐라, 본인 것은 아니라는 말이오, 응?\""], ["Omer returned, laughing.", "오머 씨가 웃으며 받았다."], ["‘All the better, sir.", "\"그게 더 낫지요, 선생."], ["Bad habit for a young man.", "젊은이한테는 나쁜 버릇이니까."], ["Take a seat.", "자, 앉으시오."], ["I smoke, myself, for the asthma.’", "나야 천식 때문에 피우는 거라오.\""]], [["Mr. Omer had made room for me, and placed a chair.", "오머 씨는 나에게 자리를 내주고 의자를 놓아 주었다."], ["He now sat down again very much out of breath, gasping at his pipe as if it contained a supply of that necessary, without which he must perish.", "그러고는 다시 앉았는데 숨이 무척 차서, 파이프 속에 자기 목숨이 달린 무언가가 들어 있어 그게 없으면 죽기라도 하는 양 거기에 대고 헐떡였다."]], [["‘I am sorry to have heard bad news of Mr. Barkis,’ said I.", "\"바키스 씨가 안 좋다는 소식을 들어서 마음이 아픕니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Omer looked at me, with a steady countenance, and shook his head.", "오머 씨는 흔들림 없는 얼굴로 나를 바라보더니 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘Do you know how he is tonight?’ I asked.", "\"오늘 밤 그분 상태가 어떤지 아세요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘The very question I should have put to you, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"그건 내가 선생한테 물었어야 할 질문인데,\" 오머 씨가 받았다."], ["Omer, ‘but on account of delicacy.", "\"체면상 못 묻는 거라오."], ["It’s one of the drawbacks of our line of business. When a party’s ill, we can’t ask how the party is.’", "우리 같은 장사의 곤란한 점이지. 누가 앓고 있으면, 우리는 그 사람이 어떤지 물을 수가 없거든.\""]], [["The difficulty had not occurred to me; though I had had my apprehensions too, when I went in, of hearing the old tune.", "그런 곤란함은 미처 생각해 보지 못했다. 들어올 때 그 익숙한 곡조를 듣게 될까 봐 나도 걱정이 없진 않았지만 말이다."], ["On its being mentioned, I recognized it, however, and said as much.", "그가 입에 올리자 비로소 나도 알아채고, 그렇다고 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, yes, you understand,’ said Mr.", "\"그래요, 그래, 알아들으셨구먼.\""], ["Omer, nodding his head. ‘We dursn’t do it.", "오머 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["Bless you, it would be a shock that the generality of parties mightn’t recover, to say “Omer and Joram’s compliments, and how do you find yourself this morning?”--or this afternoon--as it may be.’", "\"우린 감히 그럴 수가 없소. 글쎄, '오머앤조럼이 안부 여쭙니다, 오늘 아침은 좀 어떠신지요?'—아니면 오후라든가, 형편 따라—이렇게 말했다간, 웬만한 사람은 충격에서 못 헤어날 거요.\""]], [["Mr. Omer and I nodded at each other, and Mr. Omer recruited his wind by the aid of his pipe.", "오머 씨와 나는 서로 고개를 끄덕였고, 오머 씨는 파이프의 힘을 빌려 숨을 추슬렀다."]], [["‘It’s one of the things that cut the trade off from attentions they could often wish to show,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"그게 바로 우리 장사가 마음 쓰고 싶어도 못 쓰게 되는 점 중 하나라오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다. \"내 경우만 봐도 그렇지."], ["‘Take myself. If I have known Barkis a year, to move to as he went by, I have known him forty years.", "바키스를 안 지가, 그가 지나갈 때 알은체할 만큼 사귄 지가 사십 년이오. 그런데도 가서 '좀 어떻소?'"], ["But I can’t go and say, “how is he?”’", "하고 물을 수가 없으니.\""]], [["I felt it was rather hard on Mr. Omer, and I told him so.", "오머 씨에게는 좀 가혹한 일이다 싶어, 그렇게 말해 주었다."]], [["‘I’m not more self-interested, I hope, than another man,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Look at me!", "\"나도 남들보다 더 제 잇속만 챙기는 사람은 아닐 거요.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["My wind may fail me at any moment, and it ain’t likely that, to my own knowledge, I’d be self-interested under such circumstances.", "\"날 좀 보시오! 내 숨이 언제 멎을지 모르는데, 내가 알기로, 이런 처지에 제 잇속을 챙길 리가 있겠소."], ["I say it ain’t likely, in a man who knows his wind will go, when it DOES go, as if a pair of bellows was cut open; and that man a grandfather,’ said Mr. Omer.", "풀무가 두 쪽으로 갈라지듯 숨이 갈 때 가 버린다는 걸 아는 사람이, 그것도 할애비까지 된 사람이 그럴 리가 없다는 말이오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."]], [["I said, ‘Not at all.’", "\"그럼요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It ain’t that I complain of my line of business,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"내가 우리 장사를 두고 불평하는 건 아니라오.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It ain’t that.", "\"그게 아니야."], ["Some good and some bad goes, no doubt, to all callings.", "어느 직업에나 좋은 면도 있고 나쁜 면도 있는 법이지, 두말할 것 없이."], ["What I wish is, that parties was brought up stronger-minded.’", "내가 바라는 건 다만, 사람들이 좀 더 마음 단단하게 자라났으면 하는 거요.\""]], [["Mr. Omer, with a very complacent and amiable face, took several puffs in silence; and then said, resuming his first point:", "오머 씨는 더없이 흡족하고 인자한 얼굴로 말없이 몇 모금 빨아들이더니, 처음 이야기로 돌아와 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Accordingly we’re obleeged, in ascertaining how Barkis goes on, to limit ourselves to Em’ly.", "\"그래서 우리는 바키스의 형편이 어떤지 알아보려면 에밀리한테 묻는 수밖에 없소."], ["She knows what our real objects are, and she don’t have any more alarms or suspicions about us, than if we was so many lambs.", "그 애는 우리의 진짜 속셈이 뭔지 아니까, 우리가 새끼 양 떼이기라도 한 것처럼 우리를 두고 더는 놀라거나 의심하지 않는다오."], ["Minnie and Joram have just stepped down to the house, in fact (she’s there, after hours, helping her aunt a bit), to ask her how he is tonight; and if you was to please to wait till they come back, they’d give you full partic’lers.", "사실 미니랑 조럼이 방금 그 집에 내려갔소(그 애가 일 끝나고 거기 가서 제 고모를 좀 도와주거든). 오늘 밤 그분이 좀 어떤지 물어보러 말이오. 선생이 그 애들 돌아올 때까지 기다려 주시겠다면, 자세한 사정을 다 들려드릴 거요."], ["Will you take something?", "뭐 좀 드시겠소?"], ["A glass of srub and water, now?", "럼주에 물 탄 거 한 잔, 어떻소?"], ["I smoke on srub and water, myself,’ said Mr.", "나야 럼주에 물 타서 마시며 담배를 피운다오.\""], ["Omer, taking up his glass, ‘because it’s considered softening to the passages, by which this troublesome breath of mine gets into action.", "오머 씨가 제 잔을 집어 들며 말했다. \"이 성가신 숨이 드나드는 그 통로를 부드럽게 해 준다고들 해서 말이오."], ["But, Lord bless you,’ said Mr.", "그렇지만, 원 세상에,\""], ["Omer, huskily, ‘it ain’t the passages that’s out of order!", "오머 씨가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"탈이 난 건 통로가 아니라오!"], ["“Give me breath enough,” said I to my daughter Minnie, “and I’ll find passages, my dear.”’", "'나한테 숨만 넉넉히 다오, 그럼 통로는 내가 알아서 찾을 테니, 얘야.' 우리 딸 미니한테 그렇게 말한다오.\""]], [["He really had no breath to spare, and it was very alarming to see him laugh.", "그는 정말로 아낄 숨이 없는 사람이라, 그가 웃는 모습을 보는 것은 여간 조마조마한 일이 아니었다."], ["When he was again in a condition to be talked to, I thanked him for the proffered refreshment, which I declined, as I had just had dinner; and, observing that I would wait, since he was so good as to invite me, until his daughter and his son-in-law came back, I inquired how little Emily was?", "다시 말을 받을 수 있는 상태가 되었을 때, 나는 권해 준 음료에 고맙다고 인사한 뒤 방금 저녁을 먹었다며 사양했다. 그러고는 이렇게 친절히 청해 주시니 따님 내외가 돌아올 때까지 기다리겠다고 하면서, 어린 에밀리는 어떻게 지내는지 물었다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 선생,\""], ["Omer, removing his pipe, that he might rub his chin: ‘I tell you truly, I shall be glad when her marriage has taken place.’", "오머 씨가 턱을 문지르려고 파이프를 떼며 말했다. \"솔직히 말하면, 나는 그 애 혼인이 치러지면 마음이 놓일 것 같소.\""]], [["‘Why so?’ I inquired.", "\"왜 그러시죠?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Well, she’s unsettled at present,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"글쎄, 지금은 그 애가 마음을 못 잡고 있어서 말이오.\""], ["‘It ain’t that she’s not as pretty as ever, for she’s prettier--I do assure you, she is prettier.", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"예전만 못해서가 아니오, 오히려 더 예뻐졌으니까—정말이오, 더 예뻐졌다오."], ["It ain’t that she don’t work as well as ever, for she does. She WAS worth any six, and she IS worth any six.", "일을 예전만큼 못 해서도 아니오, 잘하니까. 예전에도 여섯 사람 몫을 했고, 지금도 여섯 사람 몫을 한다오."], ["But somehow she wants heart. If you understand,’ said Mr.", "그런데 어쩐지 마음이 빠진 것 같단 말이지."], ["Omer, after rubbing his chin again, and smoking a little, ‘what I mean in a general way by the expression, “A long pull, and a strong pull, and a pull altogether, my hearties, hurrah!”", "'자, 길게 한 번, 세게 한 번, 다 같이 한 번, 자 동지들, 만세!' 이런 표현으로 내가 대충 무슨 말을 하려는 건지 알아들으신다면,\" 오머 씨가 다시 턱을 문지르고 담배를 좀 빨고 나서 말했다."], ["I should say to you, that that was--in a general way--what I miss in Em’ly.’", "\"선생한테 이렇게 말하겠소. 그게 바로—대충 말해서—요즘 에밀리한테서 안 보이는 거라고 말이오.\""]], [["Mr. Omer’s face and manner went for so much, that I could conscientiously nod my head, as divining his meaning.", "오머 씨의 얼굴과 몸짓에 워낙 많은 것이 담겨 있어서, 나는 진심으로 그 뜻을 헤아린 듯 고개를 끄덕일 수 있었다."], ["My quickness of apprehension seemed to please him, and he went on: ‘Now I consider this is principally on account of her being in an unsettled state, you see.", "내가 빨리 알아듣는 것이 그를 흐뭇하게 한 모양인지, 그는 말을 이어 갔다. \"한데 내가 보기엔, 이게 주로 그 애가 마음을 못 잡은 상태라서 그런 거란 말이오, 아시겠소."], ["We have talked it over a good deal, her uncle and myself, and her sweetheart and myself, after business; and I consider it is principally on account of her being unsettled.", "일 끝나고 그 애 외삼촌이랑 나랑, 또 그 애 연인이랑 나랑 이 얘기를 꽤나 했다오. 내가 보기엔 주로 그 애가 마음을 못 잡아서야."], ["You must always recollect of Em’ly,’ said Mr.", "에밀리에 대해서는 늘 잊지 말아야 할 게 있소.\""], ["Omer, shaking his head gently, ‘that she’s a most extraordinary affectionate little thing.", "오머 씨가 부드럽게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그 애는 더없이 정 많은 어린것이란 말이오."], ["The proverb says, “You can’t make a silk purse out of a sow’s ear.”", "속담에 '암퇘지 귀로는 비단 지갑을 못 만든다'고 하지요."], ["Well, I don’t know about that.", "글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠소."], ["I rather think you may, if you begin early in life.", "내 생각엔, 어려서부터 시작하면 만들 수 있을 것도 같소."], ["She has made a home out of that old boat, sir, that stone and marble couldn’t beat.’", "그 애는 저 낡은 배를 보금자리로 만들었다오, 선생. 돌이며 대리석으로도 못 따라갈 보금자리를 말이오.\""]], [["‘I am sure she has!’ said I.", "\"정말 그렇게 했지요!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘To see the clinging of that pretty little thing to her uncle,’ said Mr.", "\"그 예쁜 어린것이 외삼촌한테 매달리는 걸 보면 말이오,\""], ["Omer; ‘to see the way she holds on to him, tighter and tighter, and closer and closer, every day, is to see a sight.", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"날마다 점점 더 꼭, 점점 더 바짝 그이한테 매달리는 걸 보면, 그게 정말 볼만한 광경이오."], ["Now, you know, there’s a struggle going on when that’s the case.", "한데 그렇게 되면, 아시다시피 마음속에 싸움이 벌어지는 법이지."], ["Why should it be made a longer one than is needful?’", "그걸 굳이 필요 이상으로 길게 끌 까닭이 어디 있겠소?\""]], [["I listened attentively to the good old fellow, and acquiesced, with all my heart, in what he said.", "나는 이 마음씨 좋은 노인의 말에 귀를 기울이며, 그의 말에 진심으로 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Therefore, I mentioned to them,’ said Mr.", "\"그래서 내가 그 애들한테 이렇게 말했소.\""], ["Omer, in a comfortable, easy-going tone, ‘this.", "오머 씨가 느긋하고 편안한 어조로 말했다."], ["I said, “Now, don’t consider Em’ly nailed down in point of time, at all.", "\"이렇게 말이오. '자, 에밀리를 시간으로 못 박아 두었다고는 절대 생각지들 마라."], ["Make it your own time.", "너희들 형편대로 정하렴."], ["Her services have been more valuable than was supposed; her learning has been quicker than was supposed; Omer and Joram can run their pen through what remains; and she’s free when you wish.", "그 애가 해 준 일은 생각보다 훨씬 값졌고, 그 애가 배운 솜씨도 생각보다 빨랐다. 남은 거야 오머앤조럼이 펜으로 쓱 그어 지워 버리면 되고, 너희가 원할 때 그 애는 자유의 몸이다."], ["If she likes to make any little arrangement, afterwards, in the way of doing any little thing for us at home, very well.", "그 뒤에 그 애가 집에서 우리한테 자잘한 일을 좀 해 주겠다고 약조라도 하고 싶다면, 그것도 좋고."], ["If she don’t, very well still.", "안 한다 해도, 그것도 좋고."], ["We’re no losers, anyhow.” For--don’t you see,’ said Mr.", "어쨌든 우리야 손해 볼 게 없으니.' 왜냐하면—아시겠소,\""], ["Omer, touching me with his pipe, ‘it ain’t likely that a man so short of breath as myself, and a grandfather too, would go and strain points with a little bit of a blue-eyed blossom, like her?’", "오머 씨가 파이프로 나를 툭 건드리며 말했다. \"나처럼 숨이 모자라는 사람이, 그것도 할애비까지 된 사람이, 그 애처럼 자그마한 파란 눈의 꽃봉오리 같은 애를 두고 야박하게 따질 리가 있겠소?\""]], [["‘Not at all, I am certain,’ said I.", "\"전혀 그러실 분이 아니지요, 분명히요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Not at all!", "\"전혀 아니고말고!"], ["You’re right!’ said Mr. Omer.", "옳은 말씀이오!\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Well, sir, her cousin--you know it’s a cousin she’s going to be married to?’", "\"그래, 선생, 그 애 사촌이—그 애가 사촌이랑 혼인한다는 건 아시지요?\""]], [["‘Oh yes,’ I replied.", "\"아, 네.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘I know him well.’", "\"그 사람 잘 알아요.\""]], [["‘Of course you do,’ said Mr. Omer. ‘Well, sir!", "\"하긴 잘 아시겠지.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["Her cousin being, as it appears, in good work, and well to do, thanked me in a very manly sort of manner for this (conducting himself altogether, I must say, in a way that gives me a high opinion of him), and went and took as comfortable a little house as you or I could wish to clap eyes on.", "\"그래, 선생! 그 애 사촌이, 보아하니 일자리도 좋고 살림도 넉넉한데, 이 일을 두고 아주 사내답게 나한테 고마워하더란 말이오(그 사람 행동거지가 전반적으로, 정말이지, 내가 그 사람을 높이 보게 만들었소). 그러더니 가서, 선생이나 나나 한번 보면 탐낼 만한, 아담하고 편안한 집을 한 채 구해 놨소."], ["That little house is now furnished right through, as neat and complete as a doll’s parlour; and but for Barkis’s illness having taken this bad turn, poor fellow, they would have been man and wife--I dare say, by this time.", "그 작은 집이 이제 안팎으로 다 꾸며져서, 인형의 집 거실처럼 말끔하고 빈틈이 없다오. 바키스 병세가 이렇게 나빠지지만 않았어도, 가엾은 양반, 그 애들은 진작에—아마 지금쯤은—부부가 됐을 거요."], ["As it is, there’s a postponement.’", "사정이 이러니, 미뤄진 거지.\""]], [["‘And Emily, Mr. Omer?’ I inquired.", "\"그럼 에밀리는요, 오머 씨?\" 내가 물었다."], ["‘Has she become more settled?’", "\"마음을 좀 더 잡았나요?\""]], [["‘Why that, you know,’ he returned, rubbing his double chin again, ‘can’t naturally be expected.", "\"글쎄 그건, 아시다시피,\" 그가 다시 겹턱을 문지르며 받았다. \"당연히 기대할 수 없는 일이지."], ["The prospect of the change and separation, and all that, is, as one may say, close to her and far away from her, both at once.", "곧 닥칠 변화며 이별이며 그런 게, 말하자면 그 애한테 코앞에 다가온 동시에 아득히 멀게도 느껴지는 거라오."], ["Barkis’s death needn’t put it off much, but his lingering might. Anyway, it’s an uncertain state of matters, you see.’", "바키스가 죽는다고 혼인이 그리 미뤄질 건 없지만, 이렇게 질질 끄는 건 미뤄질 수 있지. 아무튼 형편이 뒤숭숭한 거요, 아시겠소.\""]], [["‘I see,’ said I.", "\"알겠어요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Consequently,’ pursued Mr.", "\"그러니,\" 오머 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["Omer, ‘Em’ly’s still a little down, and a little fluttered; perhaps, upon the whole, she’s more so than she was.", "\"에밀리는 여전히 좀 가라앉아 있고, 좀 안절부절못한다오. 어쩌면 전체로 봐서 예전보다 더하지."], ["Every day she seems to get fonder and fonder of her uncle, and more loth to part from all of us.", "날마다 외삼촌을 점점 더 따르고, 우리 모두랑 헤어지기를 점점 더 싫어하는 것 같소."], ["A kind word from me brings the tears into her eyes; and if you was to see her with my daughter Minnie’s little girl, you’d never forget it.", "내가 다정한 말 한마디만 건네도 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해진다오. 우리 딸 미니의 어린 딸이랑 같이 있는 모습을 보면, 선생도 평생 못 잊을 거요. 아이고 세상에!\""], ["Bless my heart alive!’ said Mr.", "오머 씨가 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 말했다."], ["Omer, pondering, ‘how she loves that child!’", "\"그 애가 그 아기를 얼마나 사랑하는지!\""]], [["Having so favourable an opportunity, it occurred to me to ask Mr. Omer, before our conversation should be interrupted by the return of his daughter and her husband, whether he knew anything of Martha.", "이렇게 좋은 기회가 온 김에, 그의 딸 내외가 돌아와 우리 이야기가 끊기기 전에, 오머 씨에게 마사에 대해 뭔가 아는 게 있는지 물어볼 생각이 들었다."]], [["‘Ah!’ he rejoined, shaking his head, and looking very much dejected.", "\"아!\" 그가 고개를 가로저으며, 몹시 풀이 죽은 얼굴로 받았다."], ["‘No good. A sad story, sir, however you come to know it.", "\"안된 일이오. 슬픈 사연이지요, 선생이 어떻게 알게 됐든 간에."], ["I never thought there was harm in the girl.", "난 그 애한테 나쁜 구석이 있다고는 한 번도 생각 안 했소."], ["I wouldn’t wish to mention it before my daughter Minnie--for she’d take me up directly--but I never did.", "우리 딸 미니 앞에서야 이 얘기를 꺼내고 싶지 않지만—꺼냈다간 당장 나한테 따지고 들 테니까—그래도 난 한 번도 안 그랬소."], ["None of us ever did.’", "우리 중 누구도 안 그랬지.\""]], [["Mr. Omer, hearing his daughter’s footstep before I heard it, touched me with his pipe, and shut up one eye, as a caution.", "오머 씨는 내가 듣기 전에 먼저 딸의 발소리를 듣고는, 파이프로 나를 툭 건드리고 한쪽 눈을 찡긋 감아 조심하라는 신호를 보냈다."], ["She and her husband came in immediately afterwards.", "곧이어 딸 내외가 들어왔다."]], [["Their report was, that Mr. Barkis was ‘as bad as bad could be’; that he was quite unconscious; and that Mr.", "그들이 전한 소식은, 바키스 씨가 '더할 나위 없이 나쁜'"], ["Chillip had mournfully said in the kitchen, on going away just now, that the College of Physicians, the College of Surgeons, and Apothecaries’ Hall, if they were all called in together, couldn’t help him.", "상태라는 것, 의식이 전혀 없다는 것, 그리고 칠립 의사 선생이 방금 떠나면서 부엌에서 침통하게 하던 말, 곧 내과의사회며 외과의사회며 약제사 회관을 다 한꺼번에 불러 모은들 그분을 못 살린다고 했다는 것이었다."], ["He was past both Colleges, Mr. Chillip said, and the Hall could only poison him.", "그분은 두 의사회의 손을 이미 떠났고, 약제사 회관은 그저 그분을 독살하는 일밖에 못 한다고 칠립 의사 선생이 말했다고 했다."]], [["Hearing this, and learning that Mr. Peggotty was there, I determined to go to the house at once.", "이 말을 듣고, 또 패거티 씨가 거기 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 나는 곧장 그 집으로 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["I bade good night to Mr. Omer, and to Mr. and Mrs.", "오머 씨와 조럼 씨 내외에게 잘 자라고 인사하고, 그 집을 향해 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["Joram; and directed my steps thither, with a solemn feeling, which made Mr. Barkis quite a new and different creature.", "엄숙한 심정이 가슴에 차오르면서, 바키스 씨가 전혀 새롭고 다른 존재로 느껴졌다."]], [["My low tap at the door was answered by Mr.", "문을 나직이 두드리자 패거티 씨가 나왔다."], ["Peggotty. He was not so much surprised to see me as I had expected.", "그는 내 예상만큼 나를 보고 놀라지 않았다. 잠시 뒤 내려온 패거티에게서도 그 점을 알아챘다."], ["I remarked this in Peggotty, too, when she came down; and I have seen it since; and I think, in the expectation of that dread surprise, all other changes and surprises dwindle into nothing.", "그 뒤로도 나는 그런 모습을 여러 번 보았는데, 생각건대 그 무시무시한 놀라움을 기다리는 마음 앞에서는 다른 모든 변화와 놀라움이 아무것도 아닌 것으로 쪼그라드는 모양이다."]], [["I shook hands with Mr. Peggotty, and passed into the kitchen, while he softly closed the door.", "나는 패거티 씨와 악수하고 부엌으로 들어갔고, 그는 살그머니 문을 닫았다."], ["Little Emily was sitting by the fire, with her hands before her face.", "어린 에밀리가 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 난롯가에 앉아 있었다."], ["Ham was standing near her.", "함이 그 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["We spoke in whispers; listening, between whiles, for any sound in the room above.", "우리는 소곤소곤 이야기했다. 이따금 말을 멈추고 위층 방에서 무슨 소리가 나는지 귀를 기울이면서."], ["I had not thought of it on the occasion of my last visit, but how strange it was to me, now, to miss Mr. Barkis out of the kitchen!", "지난번 방문 때는 미처 생각지 못했는데, 부엌에서 바키스 씨가 보이지 않는다는 것이 이제 와 어찌나 이상하게 느껴지던지!"]], [["‘This is very kind of you, Mas’r Davy,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"이렇게 와 주시니 정말 고맙소, 데이비 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s oncommon kind,’ said Ham.", "\"보통 친절이 아니지요.\" 함이 말했다."]], [["‘Em’ly, my dear,’ cried Mr. Peggotty.", "\"에밀리, 얘야,\" 패거티 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘See here!", "\"이것 봐라!"], ["Here’s Mas’r Davy come!", "데이비 도련님이 오셨다!"], ["What, cheer up, pretty!", "자, 기운 내거라, 예쁜아!"], ["Not a wured to Mas’r Davy?’", "데이비 도련님께 한마디 안 할 게냐?\""]], [["There was a trembling upon her, that I can see now.", "그 애의 온몸이 떨리고 있었는데, 지금도 그 모습이 눈에 선하다."], ["The coldness of her hand when I touched it, I can feel yet.", "내가 손을 잡았을 때 느껴지던 그 손의 차가움은 지금도 손끝에 남아 있다."], ["Its only sign of animation was to shrink from mine; and then she glided from the chair, and creeping to the other side of her uncle, bowed herself, silently and trembling still, upon his breast.", "그 손이 살아 있다는 유일한 표시는 내 손을 피해 움츠러드는 것뿐이었다. 그러고는 의자에서 스르르 미끄러져 내려와, 외삼촌의 반대편으로 기어가더니, 여전히 말없이 떨면서 그의 가슴에 고개를 묻었다."]], [["‘It’s such a loving art,’ said Mr.", "\"이 애 마음이 어찌나 정이 깊은지,\""], ["Peggotty, smoothing her rich hair with his great hard hand, ‘that it can’t abear the sorrer of this.", "패거티 씨가 크고 거친 손으로 그 애의 탐스러운 머리카락을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"이런 슬픔은 견디질 못한다오."], ["It’s nat’ral in young folk, Mas’r Davy, when they’re new to these here trials, and timid, like my little bird,--it’s nat’ral.’", "젊은것들이야 본디 그런 거지요, 데이비 도련님. 이런 시련을 처음 겪고, 게다가 내 작은 새처럼 겁이 많으면—본디 그런 거라오.\""]], [["She clung the closer to him, but neither lifted up her face, nor spoke a word.", "그 애는 외삼촌에게 더욱 바짝 매달렸으나, 얼굴을 들지도, 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘It’s getting late, my dear,’ said Mr.", "\"밤이 깊었구나, 얘야,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘and here’s Ham come fur to take you home.", "\"함이 너를 집에 데려다주려고 왔단다. 자!"], ["Theer! Go along with t’other loving art!", "이 또 다른 정 깊은 사람이랑 같이 가렴!"], ["What’ Em’ly?", "뭐라고, 에밀리?"], ["Eh, my pretty?’", "응, 예쁜아?\""]], [["The sound of her voice had not reached me, but he bent his head as if he listened to her, and then said:", "그 애의 목소리는 나한테까지 닿지 않았지만, 그는 그 애 말에 귀 기울이듯 고개를 숙이더니 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Let you stay with your uncle?", "\"외삼촌이랑 같이 있겠다고?"], ["Why, you doen’t mean to ask me that!", "아니, 너 설마 나한테 그런 걸 물으려는 게냐!"], ["Stay with your uncle, Moppet?", "외삼촌이랑 있겠다고, 우리 아가?"], ["When your husband that’ll be so soon, is here fur to take you home?", "곧 네 남편이 될 사람이 너를 집에 데려다주러 여기 와 있는데?"], ["Now a person wouldn’t think it, fur to see this little thing alongside a rough-weather chap like me,’ said Mr.", "이 자그마한 애가 나처럼 비바람에 거칠어진 사내 옆에 서 있는 걸 보면 아무도 짐작 못 하겠지만,\""], ["Peggotty, looking round at both of us, with infinite pride; ‘but the sea ain’t more salt in it than she has fondness in her for her uncle--a foolish little Em’ly!’", "패거티 씨가 한없는 자랑스러움을 담아 우리 둘을 둘러보며 말했다. \"바닷물의 소금기보다도 이 애가 제 외삼촌을 향해 품은 정이 더 짙다오—어리석은 우리 에밀리!\""]], [["‘Em’ly’s in the right in that, Mas’r Davy!’ said Ham.", "\"에밀리 말이 맞아요, 데이비 도련님!\" 함이 말했다."], ["‘Lookee here!", "\"여기 봐요!"], ["As Em’ly wishes of it, and as she’s hurried and frightened, like, besides, I’ll leave her till morning. Let me stay too!’", "에밀리가 그러길 원하고, 게다가 마음이 이렇게 뒤숭숭하고 겁에 질려 있으니, 아침까지 그냥 두겠소. 나도 같이 있게 해 주세요!\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니다.\""], ["Peggotty. ‘You doen’t ought--a married man like you--or what’s as good--to take and hull away a day’s work.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"너 같은 가장이—아니, 곧 가장이 될 사람이—하루 일감을 내팽개치면 못쓴다."], ["And you doen’t ought to watch and work both.", "게다가 밤새 지켜보고 일까지 둘 다 하면 못써."], ["That won’t do.", "그건 안 될 일이지."], ["You go home and turn in.", "너는 집에 가서 잠을 청하거라."], ["You ain’t afeerd of Em’ly not being took good care on, I know.’", "에밀리가 보살핌을 잘 못 받을까 봐 걱정하는 건 아니잖니, 내 다 안다.\""]], [["Ham yielded to this persuasion, and took his hat to go.", "함은 이 설득에 따라, 가려고 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["Even when he kissed her--and I never saw him approach her, but I felt that nature had given him the soul of a gentleman--she seemed to cling closer to her uncle, even to the avoidance of her chosen husband.", "그가 그 애에게 입을 맞출 때조차도—그가 그 애에게 다가가는 모습을 볼 때마다, 나는 하늘이 그에게 신사의 마음을 주었음을 느꼈다—그 애는 외삼촌에게 더욱 바짝 매달리며, 제가 택한 남편마저 피하려는 듯했다."], ["I shut the door after him, that it might cause no disturbance of the quiet that prevailed; and when I turned back, I found Mr. Peggotty still talking to her.", "나는 그가 나간 뒤 문을 닫아, 감도는 고요가 흐트러지지 않게 했다. 그러고 돌아서니, 패거티 씨가 여전히 그 애에게 이야기하고 있었다."]], [["‘Now, I’m a going upstairs to tell your aunt as Mas’r Davy’s here, and that’ll cheer her up a bit,’ he said.", "\"자, 나는 위층에 올라가서 네 고모한테 데이비 도련님이 오셨다고 알려야겠다. 그러면 고모도 좀 기운이 날 거야.\""], ["‘Sit ye down by the fire, the while, my dear, and warm those mortal cold hands.", "그가 말했다. \"그동안 너는 난롯가에 앉아 그 얼음장 같은 손을 좀 녹이렴."], ["You doen’t need to be so fearsome, and take on so much. What?", "그렇게까지 무서워하고 안달할 것 없다. 뭐라고?"], ["You’ll go along with me?--Well!", "나랑 같이 가겠다고?—그래!"], ["come along with me--come!", "같이 가자꾸나—오너라!"], ["If her uncle was turned out of house and home, and forced to lay down in a dyke, Mas’r Davy,’ said Mr.", "만약 외삼촌이 집에서 쫓겨나 도랑에 누워야 하는 신세가 된다 해도, 데이비 도련님,\""], ["Peggotty, with no less pride than before, ‘it’s my belief she’d go along with him, now!", "패거티 씨가 아까 못지않은 자랑스러움을 담아 말했다. \"내 생각엔 이 애가 지금 같아선 외삼촌을 따라나설 거요!"], ["But there’ll be someone else, soon,--someone else, soon, Em’ly!’", "그렇지만 머지않아 다른 사람이 생길 게다—머지않아, 다른 사람이, 에밀리야!\""]], [["Afterwards, when I went upstairs, as I passed the door of my little chamber, which was dark, I had an indistinct impression of her being within it, cast down upon the floor.", "그 뒤에 위층으로 올라가다가, 캄캄한 내 작은 방 문 앞을 지날 때, 그 애가 그 안에서 바닥에 엎드려 있는 듯한 어렴풋한 느낌이 들었다."], ["But, whether it was really she, or whether it was a confusion of the shadows in the room, I don’t know now.", "그러나 정말로 그 애였는지, 아니면 방 안 그림자가 어지럽게 뒤엉킨 것이었는지는 지금도 알 수 없다."]], [["I had leisure to think, before the kitchen fire, of pretty little Emily’s dread of death--which, added to what Mr.", "나는 부엌 난롯가에서, 예쁜 어린 에밀리가 죽음을 두려워하는 마음을 곰곰이 생각해 볼 여유가 있었다."], ["Omer had told me, I took to be the cause of her being so unlike herself--and I had leisure, before Peggotty came down, even to think more leniently of the weakness of it: as I sat counting the ticking of the clock, and deepening my sense of the solemn hush around me.", "오머 씨가 들려준 이야기에 그 마음까지 더해 보니, 그것이 그 애가 그토록 제 본모습 같지 않은 까닭이라 짐작되었다. 그리고 패거티가 내려오기 전까지, 나에게는 그 나약함을 좀 더 너그럽게 헤아릴 여유마저 있었다. 시계 똑딱이는 소리를 세고, 주위를 감싼 그 엄숙한 정적의 무게를 곱씹으며 앉아 있는 동안 말이다."], ["Peggotty took me in her arms, and blessed and thanked me over and over again for being such a comfort to her (that was what she said) in her distress.", "패거티는 나를 품에 안고, 이 슬픔 속에서 내가 이토록 큰 위로가 되어 준다고(그렇게 말했다) 거듭거듭 축복하며 고마워했다."], ["She then entreated me to come upstairs, sobbing that Mr. Barkis had always liked me and admired me; that he had often talked of me, before he fell into a stupor; and that she believed, in case of his coming to himself again, he would brighten up at sight of me, if he could brighten up at any earthly thing.", "그러고는 위층으로 올라오라고 간곡히 청하면서, 바키스 씨가 늘 나를 좋아하고 우러러보았다고, 정신을 잃기 전까지 자주 내 이야기를 했다고 흐느꼈다. 또 만약 그이가 다시 정신을 차린다면, 이 세상 무엇에든 환해질 수 있는 사람이라면, 나를 보고 환해질 거라고 믿는다고 했다."]], [["The probability of his ever doing so, appeared to me, when I saw him, to be very small.", "그러나 그를 보았을 때, 그이가 다시 정신을 차릴 가망은 내가 보기에 아주 희박했다."], ["He was lying with his head and shoulders out of bed, in an uncomfortable attitude, half resting on the box which had cost him so much pain and trouble.", "그는 머리와 어깨를 침대 밖으로 내민 채, 불편한 자세로 누워, 그토록 숱한 고통과 애를 먹였던 그 궤짝에 반쯤 몸을 기대고 있었다."], ["I learned, that, when he was past creeping out of bed to open it, and past assuring himself of its safety by means of the divining rod I had seen him use, he had required to have it placed on the chair at the bed-side, where he had ever since embraced it, night and day. His arm lay on it now.", "알고 보니, 그이가 침대에서 기어 나가 그것을 열어 볼 기력도, 내가 그이가 쓰는 걸 본 적 있는 그 점치는 막대기로 궤짝이 무사한지 확인할 기력도 없어지자, 침대 머리맡 의자에 그것을 올려놓아 달라고 했고, 그 뒤로 밤낮없이 그것을 끌어안고 있었다는 것이다. 지금도 그의 팔은 그 위에 얹혀 있었다."], ["Time and the world were slipping from beneath him, but the box was there; and the last words he had uttered were (in an explanatory tone) ‘Old clothes!’", "시간과 세상이 그의 발밑에서 빠져나가고 있었으나, 궤짝만은 그 자리에 있었다. 그리고 그가 마지막으로 내뱉은 말은 (무언가 설명하는 듯한 어조로) \"헌 옷가지야!\"였다."]], [["‘Barkis, my dear!’ said Peggotty, almost cheerfully: bending over him, while her brother and I stood at the bed’s foot.", "\"바키스, 여보!\" 패거티가 거의 명랑하다 싶을 만큼 밝게 말하며 그이 위로 몸을 숙였다. 그동안 그녀의 오빠와 나는 침대 발치에 서 있었다."], ["‘Here’s my dear boy--my dear boy, Master Davy, who brought us together, Barkis!", "\"여기 우리 귀한 도련님이 오셨어요—우리 귀한 도련님, 데이비 도련님이요, 우리 둘을 맺어 준 그분이잖아요, 바키스!"], ["That you sent messages by, you know!", "당신이 도련님 편에 전갈을 보냈잖아요!"], ["Won’t you speak to Master Davy?’", "데이비 도련님께 말 좀 안 할래요?\""]], [["He was as mute and senseless as the box, from which his form derived the only expression it had.", "그는 궤짝처럼 말이 없고 아무것도 느끼지 못했다. 그의 모습이 띤 유일한 표정이라곤 그 궤짝에서 흘러나온 것뿐이었다."]], [["‘He’s a going out with the tide,’ said Mr. Peggotty to me, behind his hand.", "\"썰물 따라 떠나는 거라오.\" 패거티 씨가 손으로 입을 가리고 나에게 말했다."]], [["My eyes were dim and so were Mr.", "내 눈도 흐릿했고 패거티 씨의 눈도 그러했다."], ["Peggotty’s; but I repeated in a whisper, ‘With the tide?’", "그러나 나는 속삭이듯 되물었다. \"썰물 따라요?\""]], [["‘People can’t die, along the coast,’ said Mr.", "\"바닷가 사람들은 죽지를 못한다오,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘except when the tide’s pretty nigh out.", "\"물이 거의 다 빠질 무렵이 아니면 말이오."], ["They can’t be born, unless it’s pretty nigh in--not properly born, till flood.", "태어나지도 못해요, 물이 거의 다 들어찰 무렵이 아니면—제대로 태어나질 못하지, 밀물이 차야 비로소."], ["He’s a going out with the tide.", "그이는 썰물 따라 떠나는 거라오."], ["It’s ebb at half-arter three, slack water half an hour.", "세 시 반에 썰물이 빠지고, 그 뒤 반 시간은 물이 멈춘다오."], ["If he lives till it turns, he’ll hold his own till past the flood, and go out with the next tide.’", "물이 돌아설 때까지 버틴다면, 밀물이 지나도록 견디다가, 다음 썰물 따라 떠날 거요.\""]], [["We remained there, watching him, a long time--hours.", "우리는 거기 머물며 그를 오래도록 지켜보았다—몇 시간을."], ["What mysterious influence my presence had upon him in that state of his senses, I shall not pretend to say; but when he at last began to wander feebly, it is certain he was muttering about driving me to school.", "그런 의식 상태에 빠진 그에게 내 존재가 어떤 알 수 없는 영향을 끼쳤는지는 감히 말하지 못하겠다. 그러나 그가 마침내 가냘프게 헛소리를 시작했을 때, 분명 그는 나를 학교에 데려다준 일을 두고 중얼거리고 있었다."]], [["‘He’s coming to himself,’ said Peggotty.", "\"정신이 돌아오나 봐요.\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty touched me, and whispered with much awe and reverence.", "패거티 씨가 나를 건드리더니, 깊은 경외와 두려움을 담아 속삭였다."], ["‘They are both a-going out fast.’", "\"둘 다 빠르게 떠나가고 있구려.\""]], [["‘Barkis, my dear!’ said Peggotty.", "\"바키스, 여보!\" 패거티가 말했다."]], [["‘C. P. Barkis,’ he cried faintly.", "\"클래라 페거티 바키스.\" 그가 희미하게 외쳤다."], ["‘No better woman anywhere!’", "\"세상에 다시없을 좋은 사람이지!\""]], [["‘Look!", "\"봐요!"], ["Here’s Master Davy!’ said Peggotty.", "여기 데이비 도련님이 계세요!\" 패거티가 말했다."], ["For he now opened his eyes.", "그가 이제 눈을 떴기 때문이다."]], [["I was on the point of asking him if he knew me, when he tried to stretch out his arm, and said to me, distinctly, with a pleasant smile:", "내가 막 그에게 나를 알아보겠느냐고 물으려는 찰나, 그가 팔을 뻗으려 애쓰며, 또렷한 목소리로 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 나에게 말했다."]], [["‘Barkis is willin’!’", "\"바키스는 좋다 이거야!\""]], [["And, it being low water, he went out with the tide.", "그리고 물이 가장 낮게 빠진 그때, 그는 썰물 따라 떠나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 31. A GREATER LOSS", "# CHAPTER 31. A GREATER LOSS"]], [["It was not difficult for me, on Peggotty’s solicitation, to resolve to stay where I was, until after the remains of the poor carrier should have made their last journey to Blunderstone.", "패거티가 간곡히 청하니, 그 가엾은 짐마차꾼의 유해가 블런더스톤으로 마지막 여행을 다녀올 때까지 이곳에 머무르기로 마음먹는 일은 나에게 그리 어렵지 않았다."], ["She had long ago bought, out of her own savings, a little piece of ground in our old churchyard near the grave of ‘her sweet girl’, as she always called my mother; and there they were to rest.", "패거티는 오래전에 자기가 모은 돈을 털어, 우리 옛 교회 묘지에 '자기 귀여운 아가씨'—패거티는 늘 내 어머니를 그렇게 불렀다—의 무덤 가까이 작은 땅 한 뙈기를 사 두었고, 그곳에 두 사람이 함께 잠들게 될 터였다."]], [["In keeping Peggotty company, and doing all I could for her (little enough at the utmost), I was as grateful, I rejoice to think, as even now I could wish myself to have been.", "패거티 곁에서 함께 지내며 내가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 해 주는 동안(아무리 애써 봐야 보잘것없었지만), 나는 지금 돌이켜 보아도 흐뭇할 만큼, 그때 내가 그래 주었기를 바라는 만큼 진심으로 고마운 마음을 다했다고 자부한다."], ["But I am afraid I had a supreme satisfaction, of a personal and professional nature, in taking charge of Mr. Barkis’s will, and expounding its contents.", "그러나 솔직히 고백하건대, 바키스 씨의 유언장을 떠맡아 그 내용을 풀어 설명하는 일에서는, 나 개인의, 그리고 내 직업과 관련된 더할 나위 없는 만족감을 느꼈던 것 같아 부끄럽다."]], [["I may claim the merit of having originated the suggestion that the will should be looked for in the box.", "유언장을 그 상자 안에서 찾아보자고 처음 제안한 공로만큼은 내 것이라 주장해도 좋으리라."], ["After some search, it was found in the box, at the bottom of a horse’s nose-bag; wherein (besides hay) there was discovered an old gold watch, with chain and seals, which Mr.", "한참을 뒤진 끝에 유언장은 상자 안, 말 먹이주머니 밑바닥에서 나왔다. 그 주머니 안에서는 (건초 말고도) 갖가지 물건이 발견되었다."], ["Barkis had worn on his wedding-day, and which had never been seen before or since; a silver tobacco-stopper, in the form of a leg; an imitation lemon, full of minute cups and saucers, which I have some idea Mr. Barkis must have purchased to present to me when I was a child, and afterwards found himself unable to part with; eighty-seven guineas and a half, in guineas and half-guineas; two hundred and ten pounds, in perfectly clean Bank notes; certain receipts for Bank of England stock; an old horseshoe, a bad shilling, a piece of camphor, and an oyster-shell.", "바키스 씨가 결혼식 날 차고는 그 전에도 그 후에도 한 번도 본 적 없던 줄과 봉인이 달린 낡은 금시계, 다리 모양으로 된 은 담배 누름쇠, 자그마한 찻잔과 받침이 가득 든 모조 레몬—이건 내 짐작에 바키스 씨가 내가 어릴 적에 선물로 주려고 사 두었다가 나중에 차마 손에서 놓지 못한 물건이 아니었을까 싶다—, 기니 금화와 반 기니 금화로 된 여든일곱 기니 반, 깔끔하기 그지없는 은행권으로 된 이백십 파운드, 잉글랜드 은행 주식의 영수증 몇 장, 낡은 편자 하나, 가짜 일 실링 동전, 장뇌 한 덩이, 그리고 굴 껍데기 하나가 그것이었다."], ["From the circumstance of the latter article having been much polished, and displaying prismatic colours on the inside, I conclude that Mr. Barkis had some general ideas about pearls, which never resolved themselves into anything definite.", "마지막 굴 껍데기는 잘 닦여 안쪽이 무지갯빛으로 반짝이고 있는 것으로 보아, 나는 바키스 씨가 진주에 관해 어렴풋한 생각을 품고 있었으되 그것이 끝내 무엇으로도 결실을 맺지 못했다고 결론지었다."]], [["For years and years, Mr. Barkis had carried this box, on all his journeys, every day.", "여러 해 동안, 바키스 씨는 길을 나설 때면 날마다 이 상자를 지니고 다녔다."], ["That it might the better escape notice, he had invented a fiction that it belonged to ‘Mr. Blackboy’, and was ‘to be left with Barkis till called for’; a fable he had elaborately written on the lid, in characters now scarcely legible.", "남의 눈에 더 잘 띄지 않도록, 그는 이것이 '블랙보이 씨'의 것이며 '찾으러 올 때까지 바키스에게 맡겨 둔다'는 이야기를 꾸며 냈고, 그 그럴싸한 거짓말을 뚜껑에 정성껏 적어 두었는데, 글자는 이제 거의 알아보기 힘들 정도였다."]], [["He had hoarded, all these years, I found, to good purpose. His property in money amounted to nearly three thousand pounds.", "알고 보니, 그는 이 여러 해 동안 알뜰한 목적으로 돈을 모아 두었던 것이다."], ["Of this he bequeathed the interest of one thousand to Mr.", "그의 돈 재산은 거의 삼천 파운드에 이르렀다."], ["Peggotty for his life; on his decease, the principal to be equally divided between Peggotty, little Emily, and me, or the survivor or survivors of us, share and share alike.", "이 중 천 파운드의 이자는 패거티 씨가 살아 있는 동안 받도록 남겼고, 그가 죽으면 원금은 패거티와 어린 에밀리와 나에게, 혹은 그중 살아남은 사람에게 똑같이 나누어 갖게 했다."], ["All the rest he died possessed of, he bequeathed to Peggotty; whom he left residuary legatee, and sole executrix of that his last will and testament.", "그가 남기고 떠난 나머지 전부는 패거티에게 물려주었고, 패거티를 잔여 재산 상속인이자 그 마지막 유언장의 유일한 유언 집행인으로 지정했다."]], [["I felt myself quite a proctor when I read this document aloud with all possible ceremony, and set forth its provisions, any number of times, to those whom they concerned.", "나는 이 문서를 격식을 한껏 차려 큰 소리로 읽고, 그 조항들을 관계자들에게 몇 번이고 거듭 설명하면서, 내가 어엿한 대리인이라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다."], ["I began to think there was more in the Commons than I had supposed.", "나는 민사 법원이라는 곳이 내가 생각했던 것보다 더 알찬 데라고 여기기 시작했다."], ["I examined the will with the deepest attention, pronounced it perfectly formal in all respects, made a pencil-mark or so in the margin, and thought it rather extraordinary that I knew so much.", "나는 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 유언장을 살핀 뒤, 모든 면에서 완벽하게 형식을 갖추었다고 단언하고, 여백에 연필로 표시를 몇 군데 해 두고는, 내가 이렇게 많이 알고 있다는 것이 자못 놀랍다고 생각했다."]], [["In this abstruse pursuit; in making an account for Peggotty, of all the property into which she had come; in arranging all the affairs in an orderly manner; and in being her referee and adviser on every point, to our joint delight; I passed the week before the funeral.", "이 어려운 일에 매달리면서, 패거티에게 그녀가 물려받은 모든 재산을 정리해 명세를 만들어 주면서, 모든 일을 조리 있게 처리하면서, 그리고 모든 사안에서 그녀의 자문이자 조언자 노릇을 하면서—이건 우리 둘 다에게 기쁜 일이었다—나는 장례식 전 그 한 주를 보냈다."], ["I did not see little Emily in that interval, but they told me she was to be quietly married in a fortnight.", "그동안 어린 에밀리는 보지 못했지만, 보름 뒤에 그 애가 조용히 결혼식을 올릴 거라는 이야기를 들었다."]], [["I did not attend the funeral in character, if I may venture to say so.", "감히 말하자면, 나는 격식을 갖춰 장례식에 참석하지는 않았다."], ["I mean I was not dressed up in a black coat and a streamer, to frighten the birds; but I walked over to Blunderstone early in the morning, and was in the churchyard when it came, attended only by Peggotty and her brother.", "새들을 놀래킬 만한 검은 외투에 상장 띠를 두르고 차려입지 않았다는 뜻이다. 다만 아침 일찍 블런더스톤까지 걸어가서, 운구 행렬이 도착했을 때 교회 묘지에 있었다."], ["The mad gentleman looked on, out of my little window; Mr.", "행렬을 따라온 사람이라고는 패거티와 그 오라비뿐이었다."], ["Chillip’s baby wagged its heavy head, and rolled its goggle eyes, at the clergyman, over its nurse’s shoulder; Mr. Omer breathed short in the background; no one else was there; and it was very quiet.", "그 미친 신사는 내 작은 창문 너머로 내다보고 있었고, 칠립 씨네 아기는 유모의 어깨 너머로 목사를 향해 무거운 머리를 까닥이며 똥그란 눈을 데굴데굴 굴렸으며, 오머 씨는 뒤편에서 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬었다. 그 밖에는 아무도 없었고, 사방이 무척 고요했다."], ["We walked about the churchyard for an hour, after all was over; and pulled some young leaves from the tree above my mother’s grave.", "모든 것이 끝난 뒤, 우리는 한 시간 남짓 교회 묘지를 거닐었고, 내 어머니의 무덤 위에 선 나무에서 어린 잎사귀 몇 장을 따 왔다."]], [["A dread falls on me here.", "여기서 나는 두려움에 사로잡힌다."], ["A cloud is lowering on the distant town, towards which I retraced my solitary steps.", "내가 외로운 걸음을 되짚어 향하던 그 먼 마을 위로 먹구름이 무겁게 드리운다."], ["I fear to approach it.", "나는 거기 다가서기가 두렵다."], ["I cannot bear to think of what did come, upon that memorable night; of what must come again, if I go on.", "그 잊지 못할 밤에 닥쳐온 일을, 그리고 내가 계속 이야기를 이어 간다면 다시금 닥쳐올 그 일을 떠올리는 것을 차마 견딜 수가 없다."]], [["It is no worse, because I write of it.", "내가 그것을 글로 쓴다고 해서 더 나빠지는 것은 아니다."], ["It would be no better, if I stopped my most unwilling hand. It is done.", "더없이 내키지 않는 이 손을 멈춘다고 해서 더 나아지는 것도 아니다. 일은 이미 벌어졌다."], ["Nothing can undo it; nothing can make it otherwise than as it was.", "그 무엇도 되돌릴 수 없고, 그 무엇도 일을 있는 그대로가 아닌 다른 것으로 만들 수 없다."]], [["My old nurse was to go to London with me next day, on the business of the will.", "나의 옛 유모는 이튿날 유언장 문제로 나와 함께 런던에 가기로 되어 있었다."], ["Little Emily was passing that day at Mr. Omer’s.", "어린 에밀리는 그날을 오머 씨네서 보내고 있었다."], ["We were all to meet in the old boathouse that night.", "우리는 그날 밤 그 낡은 배 집에서 다 함께 모이기로 했다."], ["Ham would bring Emily at the usual hour.", "햄이 늘 그렇듯 그 시각에 에밀리를 데려올 터였다."], ["I would walk back at my leisure.", "나는 한가로이 걸어 돌아갈 생각이었다."], ["The brother and sister would return as they had come, and be expecting us, when the day closed in, at the fireside.", "오라비와 누이는 왔던 길로 돌아가, 날이 저물 무렵 난롯가에서 우리를 기다리기로 했다."]], [["I parted from them at the wicket-gate, where visionary Strap had rested with Roderick Random’s knapsack in the days of yore; and, instead of going straight back, walked a little distance on the road to Lowestoft.", "나는 쪽문에서 그들과 헤어졌다. 그 옛날 환영 속의 스트랩이 로데릭 랜덤의 배낭을 메고 쉬어 가던 그 문이었다. 그러고는 곧장 돌아가지 않고, 로스토프트로 가는 길을 따라 조금 걸었다."], ["Then I turned, and walked back towards Yarmouth.", "그런 다음 발길을 돌려 야머스 쪽으로 되짚어 걸었다."], ["I stayed to dine at a decent alehouse, some mile or two from the Ferry I have mentioned before; and thus the day wore away, and it was evening when I reached it.", "앞서 말한 나루터에서 일이 마일쯤 떨어진 그럴듯한 선술집에 들러 저녁을 먹었다. 그렇게 하루가 흘러가, 거기 다다랐을 때는 이미 저녁이었다."], ["Rain was falling heavily by that time, and it was a wild night; but there was a moon behind the clouds, and it was not dark.", "그 무렵 비가 거세게 쏟아지고 있었고 사나운 밤이었다. 그래도 구름 뒤에 달이 있어서, 캄캄하지는 않았다."]], [["I was soon within sight of Mr. Peggotty’s house, and of the light within it shining through the window.", "이내 패거티 씨네 집과 창문 너머로 새어 나오는 그 안의 불빛이 눈에 들어왔다."], ["A little floundering across the sand, which was heavy, brought me to the door, and I went in.", "발이 푹푹 빠지는 무거운 모래밭을 허우적허우적 가로지르자 문 앞에 이르렀고, 나는 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["It looked very comfortable indeed. Mr.", "집 안은 정말이지 더없이 아늑해 보였다."], ["Peggotty had smoked his evening pipe and there were preparations for some supper by and by.", "패거티 씨는 저녁 담배를 한 대 피운 참이었고, 곧 차릴 저녁 식사 채비도 되어 있었다."], ["The fire was bright, the ashes were thrown up, the locker was ready for little Emily in her old place.", "난롯불은 환했고, 재는 위로 그러모아 두었으며, 어린 에밀리의 그 옛 자리에는 사물함이 놓여 있었다."], ["In her own old place sat Peggotty, once more, looking (but for her dress) as if she had never left it.", "패거티는 다시금 자기 옛 자리에 앉아, (옷차림만 빼면) 마치 한 번도 그 자리를 떠난 적 없는 사람처럼 보였다."], ["She had fallen back, already, on the society of the work-box with St. Paul’s upon the lid, the yard-measure in the cottage, and the bit of wax-candle; and there they all were, just as if they had never been disturbed. Mrs.", "그녀는 어느새 뚜껑에 세인트 폴 성당이 그려진 반짇고리며, 오두막이 그려진 줄자며, 밀랍 양초 토막의 벗 노릇하던 시절로 되돌아가 있었다. 그리고 그 물건들은 모두, 한 번도 누가 건드린 적 없다는 듯 그대로 거기 있었다."], ["Gummidge appeared to be fretting a little, in her old corner; and consequently looked quite natural, too.", "거미지 부인은 자기 옛 구석 자리에서 조금 끙끙대며 시름에 잠긴 듯했고, 그래서 마찬가지로 아주 자연스러워 보였다."]], [["‘You’re first of the lot, Mas’r Davy!’ said Mr.", "\"제일 먼저 오셨네요, 데이비 도련님!\""], ["Peggotty with a happy face.", "패거티 씨가 환한 얼굴로 말했다."], ["‘Doen’t keep in that coat, sir, if it’s wet.’", "\"도련님, 젖었으면 그 외투를 계속 입고 계시면 안 됩니다요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, Mr. Peggotty,’ said I, giving him my outer coat to hang up.", "\"고마워요, 패거티 씨.\" 나는 그에게 겉외투를 벗어 걸어 달라고 건네며 말했다."], ["‘It’s quite dry.’", "\"아주 보송보송한걸요.\""]], [["‘So ‘tis!’ said Mr.", "\"그렇네요!\""], ["Peggotty, feeling my shoulders.", "내 어깨를 만져 보며 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘As a chip! Sit ye down, sir.", "\"바싹 마른 나뭇조각 같습니다요! 앉으시지요, 도련님."], ["It ain’t o’ no use saying welcome to you, but you’re welcome, kind and hearty.’", "도련님한테 어서 오시라 말하는 건 다 부질없는 일이지만, 그래도 진심으로, 마음 다해 환영합니다요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, Mr. Peggotty, I am sure of that.", "\"고마워요, 패거티 씨, 그건 잘 알아요."], ["Well, Peggotty!’ said I, giving her a kiss.", "그래, 패거티!\" 나는 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["‘And how are you, old woman?’", "\"어떻게 지냈어요, 우리 할멈?\""]], [["‘Ha, ha!’ laughed Mr.", "\"하, 하!\""], ["Peggotty, sitting down beside us, and rubbing his hands in his sense of relief from recent trouble, and in the genuine heartiness of his nature; ‘there’s not a woman in the wureld, sir--as I tell her--that need to feel more easy in her mind than her!", "패거티 씨가 웃으며 우리 곁에 앉더니, 근래 시름에서 풀려난 안도감과 천성 그대로의 정 깊은 마음에서 두 손을 비볐다. \"도련님, 이 세상에 저 사람보다 마음 편히 지내도 될 여자는 없습니다요—제가 늘 그렇게 말해요."], ["She done her dooty by the departed, and the departed know’d it; and the departed done what was right by her, as she done what was right by the departed;--and--and--and it’s all right!’", "저 사람은 떠난 양반한테 도리를 다했고, 떠난 양반도 그걸 알았지요. 그리고 떠난 양반도 저 사람한테 옳게 해 주었고, 저 사람도 떠난 양반한테 옳게 해 주었으니—그러니—그러니—다 잘된 거예요!\""]], [["Mrs. Gummidge groaned.", "거미지 부인이 끙 신음했다."]], [["‘Cheer up, my pritty mawther!’ said Mr.", "\"기운 내세요, 우리 곱디고운 아주머니!\""], ["Peggotty. (But he shook his head aside at us, evidently sensible of the tendency of the late occurrences to recall the memory of the old one.) ‘Doen’t be down!", "패거티 씨가 말했다. (하지만 그는 우리 쪽으로 고개를 돌려 살래살래 저었는데, 근래 일들이 옛일의 기억을 다시 불러일으키리라는 걸 분명히 알아챈 눈치였다.) \"축 처져 있지 마세요!"], ["Cheer up, for your own self, on’y a little bit, and see if a good deal more doen’t come nat’ral!’", "아주머니 자신을 위해서라도, 딱 조금만 기운을 내 봐요. 그럼 훨씬 더 많은 기운이 절로 따라오는지 안 오는지 두고 보자고요!\""]], [["‘Not to me, Dan’l,’ returned Mrs. Gummidge.", "\"나한텐 안 그래요, 대니얼.\" 거미지 부인이 받아쳤다."], ["‘Nothink’s nat’ral to me but to be lone and lorn.’", "\"나한텐 외롭고 쓸쓸한 것 말고는 뭐 하나 절로 되는 게 없어요.\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\""], ["Peggotty, soothing her sorrows.", "패거티 씨가 그녀의 슬픔을 달래며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, yes, Dan’l!’ said Mrs.", "\"맞아요, 맞다고요, 대니얼!\""], ["Gummidge. ‘I ain’t a person to live with them as has had money left.", "거미지 부인이 말했다. \"난 돈을 물려받은 사람들하고 같이 살 처지가 못 돼요."], ["Things go too contrary with me.", "만사가 나랑은 너무 어긋나거든."], ["I had better be a riddance.’", "차라리 내가 없어지는 게 나아요.\""]], [["‘Why, how should I ever spend it without you?’ said Mr.", "\"아니, 아주머니 없이 내가 그 돈을 어떻게 쓰겠어요?\""], ["Peggotty, with an air of serious remonstrance.", "패거티 씨가 자못 진지하게 나무라는 투로 말했다."], ["‘What are you a talking on?", "\"무슨 그런 말씀을 하세요?"], ["Doen’t I want you more now, than ever I did?’", "이제는 어느 때보다 아주머니가 더 필요하지 않습니까?\""]], [["‘I know’d I was never wanted before!’ cried Mrs.", "\"난 진작부터 누가 날 필요로 한 적이 없는 줄 알고 있었어요!\""], ["Gummidge, with a pitiable whimper, ‘and now I’m told so!", "거미지 부인이 가엾게 흐느끼며 외쳤다. \"그런데 이젠 그 소리를 대놓고 듣는구려!"], ["How could I expect to be wanted, being so lone and lorn, and so contrary!’", "이렇게 외롭고 쓸쓸하고 만사가 어긋나는 내가, 누가 날 필요로 하길 어찌 바라겠어요!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty seemed very much shocked at himself for having made a speech capable of this unfeeling construction, but was prevented from replying, by Peggotty’s pulling his sleeve, and shaking her head.", "패거티 씨는 그토록 매정하게 받아들여질 만한 말을 한 자신에게 몹시 충격을 받은 듯했지만, 패거티가 소맷자락을 잡아당기며 고개를 가로젓는 바람에 대꾸하지 못했다."], ["After looking at Mrs. Gummidge for some moments, in sore distress of mind, he glanced at the Dutch clock, rose, snuffed the candle, and put it in the window.", "그는 깊은 시름에 잠겨 한동안 거미지 부인을 바라보다가, 네덜란드제 시계를 흘끗 보더니, 자리에서 일어나 촛불 심지를 다듬어 창가에 놓았다."]], [["‘Theer!’ said Mr. Peggotty, cheerily.", "\"자!\" 패거티 씨가 기운차게 말했다."], ["‘Theer we are, Missis Gummidge!’ Mrs.", "\"자, 됐습니다요, 거미지 아주머니!\""], ["Gummidge slightly groaned.", "거미지 부인이 살짝 끙 신음했다."], ["‘Lighted up, accordin’ to custom!", "\"늘 하던 대로 불을 밝혔지요!"], ["You’re a wonderin’ what that’s fur, sir!", "도련님은 저게 무엇 때문인지 궁금하시겠지요!"], ["Well, it’s fur our little Em’ly.", "글쎄요, 저건 우리 어린 에밀리를 위해서랍니다."], ["You see, the path ain’t over light or cheerful arter dark; and when I’m here at the hour as she’s a comin’ home, I puts the light in the winder.", "보세요, 어두워지면 그 길이 그리 밝지도 명랑하지도 않거든요. 그래서 그 애가 집에 오는 시각에 제가 여기 있으면, 창가에 불을 놓아둡니다요. 저게 말이지요,\""], ["That, you see,’ said Mr. Peggotty, bending over me with great glee, ‘meets two objects.", "패거티 씨가 흐뭇함에 겨워 내 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 말했다. \"두 가지 뜻이 있어요."], ["She says, says Em’ly, “Theer’s home!”", "에밀리가 그래요, '저기가 우리 집이구나!' 하고요."], ["she says. And likewise, says Em’ly, “My uncle’s theer!”", "또 에밀리가 이렇게도 말하지요. '우리 외삼촌이 저기 계시는구나!' 하고요."], ["Fur if I ain’t theer, I never have no light showed.’", "제가 거기 없으면 불을 안 밝히니까요.\""]], [["‘You’re a baby!’ said Peggotty; very fond of him for it, if she thought so.", "\"외삼촌은 어린애 같아요!\" 패거티가 말했다. 정말로 그렇게 여겼다 해도, 그래서 더 그가 사랑스럽다는 투였다."]], [["‘Well,’ returned Mr.", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["Peggotty, standing with his legs pretty wide apart, and rubbing his hands up and down them in his comfortable satisfaction, as he looked alternately at us and at the fire.", "패거티 씨가 다리를 꽤 넓게 벌리고 서서, 흐뭇한 만족감에 두 손으로 다리를 위아래로 문지르며, 우리와 난롯불을 번갈아 바라보며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그럴지도 모르겠네요."], ["‘I doen’t know but I am. Not, you see, to look at.’", "보세요, 겉으로 봐선 그렇지 않지만요.\""]], [["‘Not azackly,’ observed Peggotty.", "\"딱히 그렇진 않죠.\" 패거티가 한마디 거들었다."]], [["‘No,’ laughed Mr.", "\"그럼요.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘not to look at, but to--to consider on, you know. I doen’t care, bless you!", "패거티 씨가 웃으며 말했다. \"겉으로 봐선 아니지만, 그—속을 들여다보면 말이에요."], ["Now I tell you.", "아이고, 상관없어요!"], ["When I go a looking and looking about that theer pritty house of our Em’ly’s, I’m--I’m Gormed,’ said Mr.", "자, 내 말 좀 들어 보세요. 제가 우리 에밀리의 그 예쁘장한 집을 둘러보고 또 둘러보고 있노라면, 저는—저는, 빌어먹을,\""], ["Peggotty, with sudden emphasis--‘theer!", "패거티 씨가 갑자기 힘주어 말했다. \"자!"], ["I can’t say more--if I doen’t feel as if the littlest things was her, a’most.", "더는 못 하겠네—꼭 그 자그마한 물건 하나하나가 그 애 같은 느낌이 든단 말이지요."], ["I takes ‘em up and I put ‘em down, and I touches of ‘em as delicate as if they was our Em’ly.", "그걸 집어 들었다 내려놨다 하고, 우리 에밀리라도 되는 양 조심조심 어루만져요."], ["So ‘tis with her little bonnets and that.", "그 애 작은 모자며 그런 것들도 마찬가지고요."], ["I couldn’t see one on ‘em rough used a purpose--not fur the whole wureld.", "누가 일부러 그것들을 함부로 다루는 꼴은 못 봅니다요—온 세상을 다 준대도요."], ["There’s a babby fur you, in the form of a great Sea Porkypine!’ said Mr.", "거대한 바다 호저(豪猪) 꼴을 한 어린애가 여기 있구먼!\""], ["Peggotty, relieving his earnestness with a roar of laughter.", "패거티 씨가 한바탕 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃으며 진지함을 털어 냈다."]], [["Peggotty and I both laughed, but not so loud.", "패거티와 나도 함께 웃었지만, 그렇게 크게는 아니었다."]], [["‘It’s my opinion, you see,’ said Mr.", "\"제 생각은 말이지요,\""], ["Peggotty, with a delighted face, after some further rubbing of his legs, ‘as this is along of my havin’ played with her so much, and made believe as we was Turks, and French, and sharks, and every wariety of forinners--bless you, yes; and lions and whales, and I doen’t know what all!--when she warn’t no higher than my knee.", "패거티 씨가 다시 한참 다리를 문지른 뒤, 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 말했다. \"이게 다 제가 그 애랑 그렇게 많이 놀아 준 탓인 것 같아요. 우리가 튀르키예 사람이네, 프랑스 사람이네, 상어네 하고, 별별 외국 것들 흉내를 다 냈거든요—아이고, 그럼요."], ["I’ve got into the way on it, you know. Why, this here candle, now!’ said Mr.", "사자에 고래에, 또 뭐였는지도 모를 온갖 것까지요!—그 애가 내 무릎 높이도 안 됐을 적에 말이에요. 그게 버릇이 돼 버렸나 봅니다요."], ["Peggotty, gleefully holding out his hand towards it, ‘I know wery well that arter she’s married and gone, I shall put that candle theer, just the same as now.", "자, 이 촛불만 봐도 그래요!\" 패거티 씨가 신이 나서 촛불 쪽으로 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"그 애가 시집가서 떠나고 나면, 그래도 저는 저 촛불을 지금처럼 똑같이 거기 놓아둘 게 뻔하단 말이지요."], ["I know wery well that when I’m here o’ nights (and where else should I live, bless your arts, whatever fortun’ I come into!) and she ain’t here or I ain’t theer, I shall put the candle in the winder, and sit afore the fire, pretending I’m expecting of her, like I’m a doing now.", "제가 밤마다 여기 있을 때면 (어떤 복이 굴러들어 온대도, 아이고 참, 제가 여기 말고 어디서 살겠어요!) 그 애가 여기 없거나 제가 거기 없거나 해도, 저는 창가에 촛불을 놓고 난로 앞에 앉아, 지금 이러는 것처럼 그 애를 기다리는 척하고 있을 거예요."], ["THERE’S a babby for you,’ said Mr. Peggotty, with another roar, ‘in the form of a Sea Porkypine!", "거대한 바다 호저 꼴을 한 어린애가 여기 있다니까요!\" 패거티 씨가 또 한바탕 껄껄거리며 말했다."], ["Why, at the present minute, when I see the candle sparkle up, I says to myself, “She’s a looking at it!", "\"글쎄, 지금 이 순간에도, 촛불이 환히 타오르는 걸 보면 저는 속으로 '그 애가 저걸 보고 있구나! 에밀리가 오고 있어!'"], ["Em’ly’s a coming!”", "하고 중얼거린다니까요."], ["THERE’S a babby for you, in the form of a Sea Porkypine!", "거대한 바다 호저 꼴을 한 어린애가 여기 있다고요!"], ["Right for all that,’ said Mr.", "그래도 다 맞는 말이지요.\""], ["Peggotty, stopping in his roar, and smiting his hands together; ‘fur here she is!’", "패거티 씨가 너털웃음을 뚝 그치고 두 손을 탁 마주치며 말했다. \"왜냐면 그 애가 여기 왔거든요!\""]], [["It was only Ham.", "그러나 햄뿐이었다."], ["The night should have turned more wet since I came in, for he had a large sou’wester hat on, slouched over his face.", "내가 들어온 뒤로 밤이 더 사나워진 모양인지, 그는 커다란 방수모를 얼굴 위로 푹 눌러쓰고 있었다."]], [["‘Wheer’s Em’ly?’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"에밀리는 어디 있냐?\" 패거티 씨가 물었다."]], [["Ham made a motion with his head, as if she were outside. Mr.", "햄이 그 애가 밖에 있다는 듯 고갯짓을 했다."], ["Peggotty took the light from the window, trimmed it, put it on the table, and was busily stirring the fire, when Ham, who had not moved, said:", "패거티 씨는 창가에서 촛불을 가져다 심지를 다듬어 탁자에 놓고는, 부지런히 난롯불을 휘젓고 있었다. 그때, 꼼짝도 않고 서 있던 햄이 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy, will you come out a minute, and see what Em’ly and me has got to show you?’", "\"데이비 도련님, 잠깐 밖에 나오셔서, 에밀리하고 제가 도련님께 보여 드릴 게 있는데 한번 봐 주시겠어요?\""]], [["We went out. As I passed him at the door, I saw, to my astonishment and fright, that he was deadly pale.", "우리는 밖으로 나갔다. 문간에서 그를 지나치는 순간, 나는 그가 새파랗게 질려 있는 것을 보고 소스라치게 놀랐다."], ["He pushed me hastily into the open air, and closed the door upon us.", "그는 다급히 나를 한데로 떠밀더니, 우리 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["Only upon us two.", "우리 둘만 남았다."]], [["‘Ham!", "\"햄!"], ["what’s the matter?’", "무슨 일이야?\""]], [["‘Mas’r Davy!--’ Oh, for his broken heart, how dreadfully he wept!", "\"데이비 도련님!—\" 아, 그 찢어진 가슴이여, 그는 어찌나 끔찍하게 흐느끼던지!"]], [["I was paralysed by the sight of such grief.", "나는 그런 비탄의 모습 앞에서 온몸이 굳어 버렸다."], ["I don’t know what I thought, or what I dreaded.", "내가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 무엇을 두려워했는지조차 모르겠다."], ["I could only look at him.", "그저 그를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다."]], [["‘Ham!", "\"햄!"], ["Poor good fellow! For Heaven’s sake, tell me what’s the matter!’", "가엾고 착한 친구! 제발, 무슨 일인지 말해 줘!\""]], [["‘My love, Mas’r Davy--the pride and hope of my art--her that I’d have died for, and would die for now--she’s gone!’", "\"제 사랑이, 데이비 도련님—제 가슴의 자랑이자 희망이—제가 그 애를 위해서라면 죽었을 테고 지금도 죽을 그 애가—떠나 버렸어요!\""]], [["‘Gone!’", "\"떠나다니!\""]], [["‘Em’ly’s run away!", "\"에밀리가 달아나 버렸어요!"], ["Oh, Mas’r Davy, think HOW she’s run away, when I pray my good and gracious God to kill her (her that is so dear above all things) sooner than let her come to ruin and disgrace!’", "아, 데이비 도련님, 그 애가 어떻게 달아났는지 좀 생각해 보세요. 저는 자비롭고 거룩하신 하느님께, 그 애가(세상 그 무엇보다 소중한 그 애가) 파멸과 치욕에 빠지게 하시느니 차라리 그 애를 거두어 가시기를 비는데도요!\""]], [["The face he turned up to the troubled sky, the quivering of his clasped hands, the agony of his figure, remain associated with the lonely waste, in my remembrance, to this hour.", "그가 어지러운 하늘을 향해 쳐든 얼굴, 맞잡은 두 손의 떨림, 그 몸뚱이의 고통은, 내 기억 속에서 지금 이 순간까지도 그 외로운 황무지와 한데 얽혀 떠오른다."], ["It is always night there, and he is the only object in the scene.", "그곳은 늘 밤이고, 그 풍경 속 유일한 존재는 바로 그다."]], [["‘You’re a scholar,’ he said, hurriedly, ‘and know what’s right and best.", "\"도련님은 배운 분이시니까,\" 그가 다급히 말했다. \"뭐가 옳고 뭐가 가장 좋은지 아실 거예요."], ["What am I to say, indoors?", "제가 집 안에 들어가서 뭐라고 말해야 하나요?"], ["How am I ever to break it to him, Mas’r Davy?’", "도련님, 제가 외삼촌한테 이걸 도대체 어떻게 알려야 하지요?\""]], [["I saw the door move, and instinctively tried to hold the latch on the outside, to gain a moment’s time.", "문이 움직이는 것이 보였고, 나는 본능적으로 한순간이라도 시간을 벌려고 바깥쪽에서 빗장을 붙잡으려 했다."], ["It was too late. Mr.", "너무 늦었다."], ["Peggotty thrust forth his face; and never could I forget the change that came upon it when he saw us, if I were to live five hundred years.", "패거티 씨가 얼굴을 쑥 내밀었다. 우리를 본 순간 그 얼굴에 떠오른 변화를, 내가 오백 년을 산다 해도 결코 잊지 못하리라."]], [["I remember a great wail and cry, and the women hanging about him, and we all standing in the room; I with a paper in my hand, which Ham had given me; Mr. Peggotty, with his vest torn open, his hair wild, his face and lips quite white, and blood trickling down his bosom (it had sprung from his mouth, I think), looking fixedly at me.", "큰 통곡과 울부짖음이 있었고, 여자들이 그에게 매달렸으며, 우리 모두 방 안에 서 있던 것이 기억난다. 나는 햄이 건네준 종이 한 장을 손에 쥐고 있었고, 패거티 씨는 조끼가 찢겨 풀어헤쳐진 채, 머리는 산발이고, 얼굴과 입술은 새하얗게 질렸으며, 가슴팍으로는 피가 (입에서 솟구친 것이지 싶다) 뚝뚝 흘러내리는 채로, 나를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘Read it, sir,’ he said, in a low shivering voice.", "\"읽어 주세요, 도련님.\" 그가 떨리는 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘Slow, please.", "\"천천히요, 부디."], ["I doen’t know as I can understand.’", "제가 알아들을 수 있을지 모르겠네요.\""]], [["In the midst of the silence of death, I read thus, from a blotted letter:", "죽음 같은 정적 속에서, 나는 얼룩진 편지를 이렇게 읽어 내려갔다."]], [["‘“When you, who love me so much better than I ever have deserved, even when my mind was innocent, see this, I shall be far away.”’", "\"'제가 마음이 순결했던 시절에도 받을 자격이 없던 저를, 이토록 끔찍이 사랑해 주신 외삼촌이 이 글을 보실 때면, 저는 멀리 떠나 있을 거예요.'\""]], [["‘I shall be fur away,’ he repeated slowly.", "\"멀리 떠나 있겠다.\" 그가 천천히 따라 읊었다. \"잠깐!"], ["‘Stop! Em’ly fur away. Well!’", "에밀리가 멀리 떠나 있다. 그래!\""]], [["‘“When I leave my dear home--my dear home--oh, my dear home!--in the morning,”’", "\"'제가 사랑하는 우리 집—사랑하는 우리 집—아, 사랑하는 우리 집을!—아침에 떠날 때면,'\""]], [["the letter bore date on the previous night:", "편지에는 그 전날 밤 날짜가 적혀 있었다."]], [["‘“--it will be never to come back, unless he brings me back a lady.", "\"'—그건 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않으려는 거예요. 그이가 저를 귀부인으로 만들어 데려오지 않는 한은요."], ["This will be found at night, many hours after, instead of me.", "이 편지는 밤에, 여러 시간이 지난 뒤에야, 저 대신 발견되겠지요."], ["Oh, if you knew how my heart is torn.", "아, 제 가슴이 얼마나 찢어지는지 외삼촌이 아신다면."], ["If even you, that I have wronged so much, that never can forgive me, could only know what I suffer!", "제가 그토록 몹쓸 짓을 한 외삼촌이, 결코 저를 용서하실 수 없는 외삼촌이, 제가 얼마나 괴로운지 알기만이라도 하실 수 있다면!"], ["I am too wicked to write about myself!", "저는 너무 사악해서 제 이야기를 쓸 수도 없어요!"], ["Oh, take comfort in thinking that I am so bad.", "아, 제가 이렇게 못된 애라고 생각하시며 위안 삼으세요."], ["Oh, for mercy’s sake, tell uncle that I never loved him half so dear as now.", "아, 제발, 외삼촌한테 전해 주세요. 제가 지금만큼 외삼촌을 절반만큼도 사랑한 적이 없다고요."], ["Oh, don’t remember how affectionate and kind you have all been to me--don’t remember we were ever to be married--but try to think as if I died when I was little, and was buried somewhere.", "아, 다들 저한테 얼마나 다정하고 친절하셨는지 떠올리지 마세요—우리가 결혼하기로 했던 일도 떠올리지 마세요—그저 제가 어렸을 때 죽어서 어딘가에 묻힌 셈 치고 생각하려 해 보세요."], ["Pray Heaven that I am going away from, have compassion on my uncle!", "제가 떠나가는 곳, 그 하늘에 비나니, 외삼촌을 가엾이 여겨 주시기를!"], ["Tell him that I never loved him half so dear. Be his comfort.", "제가 외삼촌을 절반만큼도 이리 사랑한 적이 없다고 전해 주세요. 외삼촌의 위안이 되어 주세요."], ["Love some good girl that will be what I was once to uncle, and be true to you, and worthy of you, and know no shame but me. God bless all!", "한때 제가 외삼촌께 그러했듯, 그리고 당신께 진실하고, 당신께 어울리며, 저 말고는 부끄러움이라곤 모를, 그런 착한 아가씨를 사랑하세요. 모두에게 하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를!"], ["I’ll pray for all, often, on my knees.", "저는 무릎 꿇고, 모두를 위해 자주 기도할게요."], ["If he don’t bring me back a lady, and I don’t pray for my own self, I’ll pray for all.", "그이가 저를 귀부인으로 만들어 데려오지 않는다면, 저 자신을 위해서는 기도하지 않고, 모두를 위해 기도할게요."], ["My parting love to uncle.", "외삼촌께 이별의 사랑을 보냅니다."], ["My last tears, and my last thanks, for uncle!”’", "제 마지막 눈물과, 제 마지막 감사를, 외삼촌께!'\""]], [["That was all.", "그게 다였다."]], [["He stood, long after I had ceased to read, still looking at me.", "내가 다 읽기를 멈춘 뒤에도 그는 한참을 그대로 서서, 여전히 나를 바라보았다."], ["At length I ventured to take his hand, and to entreat him, as well as I could, to endeavour to get some command of himself.", "마침내 나는 용기를 내어 그의 손을 잡고, 할 수 있는 한 정성껏, 부디 마음을 좀 다잡아 보시라고 간곡히 청했다."], ["He replied, ‘I thankee, sir, I thankee!’ without moving.", "그는 꼼짝도 않은 채 대답했다. \"고맙습니다, 도련님, 고맙습니다요!\""]], [["Ham spoke to him. Mr.", "햄이 그에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Peggotty was so far sensible of HIS affliction, that he wrung his hand; but, otherwise, he remained in the same state, and no one dared to disturb him.", "패거티 씨는 햄의 슬픔만큼은 가까스로 헤아릴 수 있을 만큼은 정신이 있어서, 그의 손을 꼭 그러쥐었다. 그러나 그것 말고는 여전히 똑같은 상태였고, 아무도 감히 그를 건드리지 못했다."]], [["Slowly, at last, he moved his eyes from my face, as if he were waking from a vision, and cast them round the room.", "마침내 그는 환상에서 깨어나기라도 하듯 천천히 내 얼굴에서 눈길을 떼더니, 방 안을 두루 둘러보았다."], ["Then he said, in a low voice:", "그러고는 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Who’s the man?", "\"그놈이 누구냐?"], ["I want to know his name.’", "난 그놈 이름을 알아야겠다.\""]], [["Ham glanced at me, and suddenly I felt a shock that struck me back.", "햄이 나를 흘끗 보았고, 순간 나는 뒤로 나동그라질 만큼 큰 충격에 휩싸였다."]], [["‘There’s a man suspected,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"의심 가는 놈이 있구나.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Who is it?’", "\"그게 누구냐?\""]], [["‘Mas’r Davy!’ implored Ham.", "\"데이비 도련님!\" 햄이 애원했다."], ["‘Go out a bit, and let me tell him what I must.", "\"잠깐 나가 계세요. 제가 외삼촌한테 마땅히 해야 할 말을 하게요."], ["You doen’t ought to hear it, sir.’", "도련님은 이 얘길 들으시면 안 됩니다요.\""]], [["I felt the shock again.", "나는 또다시 충격을 느꼈다."], ["I sank down in a chair, and tried to utter some reply; but my tongue was fettered, and my sight was weak.", "의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 무어라 대답을 해 보려 했지만, 혀가 묶인 듯했고 눈앞이 흐릿했다."]], [["‘I want to know his name!’ I heard said once more.", "\"그놈 이름을 알아야겠다니까!\" 또 한 번 그렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["‘For some time past,’ Ham faltered, ‘there’s been a servant about here, at odd times.", "\"얼마 전부터,\" 햄이 더듬더듬 말했다. \"이 근방에 종놈 하나가 이따금 얼쩡거렸어요."], ["There’s been a gen’lm’n too. Both of ‘em belonged to one another.’", "신사 양반도 한 명 있었고요. 둘이 한패였어요.\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty stood fixed as before, but now looking at him.", "패거티 씨는 아까처럼 그대로 굳은 채 서 있었으나, 이제는 그를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘The servant,’ pursued Ham, ‘was seen along with--our poor girl--last night.", "\"그 종놈이,\" 햄이 말을 이었다. \"어젯밤에—우리 가엾은 그 애랑—함께 있는 걸 누가 봤대요."], ["He’s been in hiding about here, this week or over.", "그놈은 이 일주일 넘게 이 근방에 숨어 있었어요."], ["He was thought to have gone, but he was hiding.", "다들 그놈이 떠난 줄 알았는데, 숨어 있었던 거지요."], ["Doen’t stay, Mas’r Davy, doen’t!’", "가지 마세요, 데이비 도련님, 가지 말아요!\""]], [["I felt Peggotty’s arm round my neck, but I could not have moved if the house had been about to fall upon me.", "패거티의 팔이 내 목을 감는 것이 느껴졌지만, 설령 집이 무너져 내릴 참이었다 해도 나는 꼼짝할 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["‘A strange chay and hosses was outside town, this morning, on the Norwich road, a’most afore the day broke,’ Ham went on.", "\"오늘 아침, 날이 채 밝기도 전에, 노리치 가는 길 마을 어귀에 낯선 사륜마차하고 말들이 서 있었대요.\" 햄이 말을 이었다."], ["‘The servant went to it, and come from it, and went to it again.", "\"그 종놈이 거기로 갔다가, 다시 돌아왔다가, 또 거기로 갔답니다."], ["When he went to it again, Em’ly was nigh him.", "마지막으로 거기 갔을 때, 에밀리가 그놈 곁에 있었고요."], ["The t’other was inside. He’s the man.’", "다른 한 놈은 마차 안에 있었어요. 그놈이 바로 그 작자예요.\""]], [["‘For the Lord’s love,’ said Mr. Peggotty, falling back, and putting out his hand, as if to keep off what he dreaded.", "\"하느님 맙소사.\" 패거티 씨가 뒤로 물러서며, 두려워하는 무언가를 막아 내려는 듯 손을 쑥 내밀며 말했다."], ["‘Doen’t tell me his name’s Steerforth!’", "\"그놈 이름이 스티어포스라고는 말하지 마라!\""]], [["‘Mas’r Davy,’ exclaimed Ham, in a broken voice, ‘it ain’t no fault of yourn--and I am far from laying of it to you--but his name is Steerforth, and he’s a damned villain!’", "\"데이비 도련님,\" 햄이 갈라진 목소리로 외쳤다. \"이건 도련님 잘못이 아니에요—저도 도련님 탓을 할 마음은 추호도 없고요—하지만 그놈 이름이 스티어포스고, 빌어먹을 악당이에요!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty uttered no cry, and shed no tear, and moved no more, until he seemed to wake again, all at once, and pulled down his rough coat from its peg in a corner.", "패거티 씨는 비명도 지르지 않고, 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않고, 더는 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 그러다 별안간 다시 정신이 든 듯, 구석 못에 걸려 있던 자기 거친 외투를 끌어 내렸다."]], [["‘Bear a hand with this!", "\"이것 좀 거들어 다오!"], ["I’m struck of a heap, and can’t do it,’ he said, impatiently.", "정신이 얼떨떨해서 혼자 못 입겠다.\" 그가 조급하게 말했다."], ["‘Bear a hand and help me.", "\"손 좀 빌려 다오. 자!\""], ["Well!’ when somebody had done so.", "누군가 그렇게 해 주자 그가 말했다."], ["‘Now give me that theer hat!’", "\"이제 저기 저 모자를 다오!\""]], [["Ham asked him whither he was going.", "햄이 그에게 어디로 가시려느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘I’m a going to seek my niece.", "\"내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다."], ["I’m a going to seek my Em’ly.", "내 에밀리를 찾으러 갈 거야."], ["I’m a going, first, to stave in that theer boat, and sink it where I would have drownded him, as I’m a living soul, if I had had one thought of what was in him!", "먼저, 저기 저 배부터 박살을 내서, 만약 내가 그놈 속을 한 가닥이라도 짐작했더라면—내 산 목숨을 걸고—그놈을 빠뜨려 죽였을 바로 그 자리에 가라앉혀 버릴 거다! 그놈이 내 앞에 앉아 있을 때,\""], ["As he sat afore me,’ he said, wildly, holding out his clenched right hand, ‘as he sat afore me, face to face, strike me down dead, but I’d have drownded him, and thought it right!--I’m a going to seek my niece.’", "그가 꽉 쥔 오른손을 쑥 내밀며 사납게 말했다. \"그놈이 내 앞에 마주 앉아 있을 때, 내가 거짓말이면 그 자리에서 벼락 맞아 죽어도 좋다, 나는 그놈을 빠뜨려 죽이고도 그게 옳다고 여겼을 거다!—내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다.\""]], [["‘Where?’ cried Ham, interposing himself before the door.", "\"어디로요?\" 햄이 문 앞을 가로막아 서며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Anywhere!", "\"어디든!"], ["I’m a going to seek my niece through the wureld.", "온 세상을 다 뒤져서라도 내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다."], ["I’m a going to find my poor niece in her shame, and bring her back. No one stop me!", "치욕에 빠진 내 가엾은 조카딸을 찾아내서 데려올 거야. 아무도 날 막지 마라!"], ["I tell you I’m a going to seek my niece!’", "분명히 말하건대, 나는 내 조카딸을 찾으러 간다!\""]], [["‘No, no!’ cried Mrs.", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼요!\""], ["Gummidge, coming between them, in a fit of crying.", "거미지 부인이 울음을 터뜨리며 두 사람 사이로 끼어들며 외쳤다."], ["‘No, no, Dan’l, not as you are now.", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼, 대니얼, 지금 그 꼴로는 안 돼요."], ["Seek her in a little while, my lone lorn Dan’l, and that’ll be but right! but not as you are now.", "좀 있다가 그 애를 찾으러 가요, 외롭고 쓸쓸한 우리 대니얼, 그래야 옳지요! 하지만 지금 그 꼴로는 안 돼요. 앉아요."], ["Sit ye down, and give me your forgiveness for having ever been a worrit to you, Dan’l--what have my contraries ever been to this!--and let us speak a word about them times when she was first an orphan, and when Ham was too, and when I was a poor widder woman, and you took me in.", "그리고 내가 그동안 당신한테 골칫거리였던 걸 용서해 줘요, 대니얼—내 그 어긋난 성미가 이 일에 비하면 다 뭐예요!—그리고 그 애가 처음 고아가 됐을 때, 햄도 그랬을 때, 그리고 내가 가난한 과부였을 때 당신이 날 거둬 준 그 시절 이야기나 한마디 나눠요. 그러면 당신의 가엾은 마음도 누그러질 거예요, 대니얼.\" 그녀가 그의 어깨에 머리를 기대며 말했다."], ["It’ll soften your poor heart, Dan’l,’ laying her head upon his shoulder, ‘and you’ll bear your sorrow better; for you know the promise, Dan’l, “As you have done it unto one of the least of these, you have done it unto me”,--and that can never fail under this roof, that’s been our shelter for so many, many year!’", "\"그럼 당신 슬픔도 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 거예요. 당신도 그 약속을 알잖아요, 대니얼. '너희가 여기 지극히 작은 자 하나에게 한 것이 곧 내게 한 것이니라'—그 말씀은 그토록 여러 해 동안 이토록 많은 이들의 보금자리가 되어 준 이 지붕 아래서는 결코 헛되지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["He was quite passive now; and when I heard him crying, the impulse that had been upon me to go down upon my knees, and ask their pardon for the desolation I had caused, and curse Steerforth, yielded to a better feeling.", "이제 그는 모든 것을 내맡긴 듯 잠잠해졌다. 그가 우는 소리를 들었을 때, 내가 일으킨 이 참담함을 무릎 꿇고 빌고 스티어포스를 저주하고 싶었던 충동은 더 나은 감정에 자리를 내주었다."], ["My overcharged heart found the same relief, and I cried too.", "미어터지던 내 가슴도 똑같은 위안을 찾아냈고, 나 또한 울었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 32. THE BEGINNING OF A LONG JOURNEY", "# CHAPTER 32. THE BEGINNING OF A LONG JOURNEY"]], [["What is natural in me, is natural in many other men, I infer, and so I am not afraid to write that I never had loved Steerforth better than when the ties that bound me to him were broken.", "내 안에 자연스러운 것은 다른 많은 사람들 안에서도 자연스러우리라 짐작하니, 나는 두려움 없이 이렇게 쓰겠다. 나를 스티어포스와 묶어 주던 끈이 끊어진 바로 그때만큼 그를 진하게 사랑한 적이 없었다고."], ["In the keen distress of the discovery of his unworthiness, I thought more of all that was brilliant in him, I softened more towards all that was good in him, I did more justice to the qualities that might have made him a man of a noble nature and a great name, than ever I had done in the height of my devotion to him.", "그가 그럴 만한 사람이 못 된다는 걸 알아챘을 때의 그 사무치는 고통 속에서, 나는 그에게 있던 온갖 빛나는 면을 더 많이 떠올렸고, 그에게 있던 온갖 선한 면에 더 부드러워졌으며, 그를 고귀한 천성과 위대한 이름을 가진 사람으로 만들어 주었을 법한 자질들에 대해 그 어느 때보다도, 그에게 가장 헌신적으로 빠져 있던 절정의 순간보다도 더 공정한 평가를 내렸다."], ["Deeply as I felt my own unconscious part in his pollution of an honest home, I believed that if I had been brought face to face with him, I could not have uttered one reproach.", "정직한 한 가정을 그가 더럽히는 데 나도 모르는 사이 한몫 거들었다는 걸 뼈저리게 느꼈으면서도, 만일 그와 얼굴을 맞대는 처지가 되었더라면 나는 단 한마디 질책도 입에 올리지 못했으리라 믿는다."], ["I should have loved him so well still--though he fascinated me no longer--I should have held in so much tenderness the memory of my affection for him, that I think I should have been as weak as a spirit-wounded child, in all but the entertainment of a thought that we could ever be re-united. That thought I never had.", "나는 여전히 그를 그토록 깊이 사랑했을 테니까 — 그가 더는 나를 매혹하지 못했음에도 — 그를 향한 내 애정의 기억을 그토록 애틋하게 품고 있었을 테니까, 우리가 다시 하나가 될 수도 있으리라는 생각만 빼면, 나는 영혼에 상처 입은 어린아이처럼 한없이 나약했으리라 싶다. 그러나 그 생각만은 단 한 번도 품지 않았다."], ["I felt, as he had felt, that all was at an end between us.", "그가 느꼈던 것처럼, 나도 우리 사이의 모든 것이 끝났음을 느꼈다."], ["What his remembrances of me were, I have never known--they were light enough, perhaps, and easily dismissed--but mine of him were as the remembrances of a cherished friend, who was dead.", "나에 대한 그의 기억이 어떤 것이었는지 나는 끝내 알지 못했다 — 아마 가벼웠을 테고, 쉽게 떨쳐 버렸을 것이다 — 그러나 그에 대한 내 기억은, 세상을 떠난 소중한 벗을 추억하는 그것과 같았다."]], [["Yes, Steerforth, long removed from the scenes of this poor history!", "그래, 스티어포스, 이 가엾은 이야기의 무대에서 멀리 떠나 버린 자여!"], ["My sorrow may bear involuntary witness against you at the judgement Throne; but my angry thoughts or my reproaches never will, I know!", "내 슬픔은 심판의 옥좌 앞에서 본의 아니게 너를 증언할지 모르나, 내 노여운 생각이나 질책은 결코 그러지 않으리라는 걸, 나는 안다!"]], [["The news of what had happened soon spread through the town; insomuch that as I passed along the streets next morning, I overheard the people speaking of it at their doors.", "무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 그 소식은 이내 온 동네에 퍼졌다. 어찌나 빠르던지 이튿날 아침 거리를 지나는데 사람들이 제 집 문간에서 그 이야기를 주고받는 것이 귀에 들어올 정도였다."], ["Many were hard upon her, some few were hard upon him, but towards her second father and her lover there was but one sentiment.", "많은 이들은 그 처녀를 모질게 몰아세웠고, 더러 몇몇은 그 사내를 모질게 나무랐으나, 그 처녀의 두 번째 아버지와 그 처녀의 연인을 향해서는 단 하나의 감정뿐이었다."], ["Among all kinds of people a respect for them in their distress prevailed, which was full of gentleness and delicacy.", "온갖 부류의 사람들 사이에 그 두 사람의 비탄을 향한 존중이 가득했는데, 그것은 다정함과 섬세함으로 충만한 것이었다."], ["The seafaring men kept apart, when those two were seen early, walking with slow steps on the beach; and stood in knots, talking compassionately among themselves.", "그 두 사람이 이른 아침에 바닷가를 느릿느릿 걷는 모습이 보이면 뱃사람들은 멀찍이 비켜섰고, 무리 지어 서서 자기들끼리 측은한 마음으로 두런두런 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["It was on the beach, close down by the sea, that I found them.", "내가 두 사람을 찾아낸 곳은 바닷가, 바다와 바짝 맞닿은 곳이었다."], ["It would have been easy to perceive that they had not slept all last night, even if Peggotty had failed to tell me of their still sitting just as I left them, when it was broad day.", "패거티가 미리 일러 주지 않았더라도 — 날이 훤히 밝았을 때 내가 자리를 떴을 때 그대로 두 사람이 아직 앉아 있더라고 말해 주지 않았더라도 — 그들이 간밤에 한숨도 자지 못했음을 알아채기는 쉬웠을 것이다."], ["They looked worn; and I thought Mr. Peggotty’s head was bowed in one night more than in all the years I had known him.", "둘은 지쳐 보였다. 게다가 패거티 씨의 머리는, 내가 그를 알고 지낸 그 모든 세월보다 단 하룻밤에 더 깊이 수그러든 것 같았다."], ["But they were both as grave and steady as the sea itself, then lying beneath a dark sky, waveless--yet with a heavy roll upon it, as if it breathed in its rest--and touched, on the horizon, with a strip of silvery light from the unseen sun.", "그러나 두 사람은 바다 그 자체만큼이나 진중하고 흔들림이 없었는데, 그 바다는 그때 어두운 하늘 아래 물결 한 점 없이 누워 있었으되 — 그러면서도 쉬는 와중에 숨이라도 쉬는 듯 묵직하게 너울거렸고 — 수평선 위로는 보이지 않는 해가 던진 은빛 한 줄기 빛에 어려 있었다."]], [["‘We have had a mort of talk, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"우리는 한참을 얘기했답니다, 도련님.\""], ["Peggotty to me, when we had all three walked a little while in silence, ‘of what we ought and doen’t ought to do.", "우리 셋이 한동안 말없이 걷고 난 뒤 패거티 씨가 나에게 말했다. \"뭘 해야 하고 뭘 하면 안 되는지를요."], ["But we see our course now.’", "그치만 이제 우리가 갈 길이 보입니다.\""]], [["I happened to glance at Ham, then looking out to sea upon the distant light, and a frightful thought came into my mind--not that his face was angry, for it was not; I recall nothing but an expression of stern determination in it--that if ever he encountered Steerforth, he would kill him.", "나는 마침 햄을 흘끗 보았는데, 그는 그때 저 멀리 빛을 향해 바다를 내다보고 있었다. 그러자 끔찍한 생각이 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다 — 그의 얼굴이 노여워서가 아니라, 노여움은 거기 없었으니까. 나는 그 얼굴에서 단호한 결의의 표정 말고는 아무것도 떠올릴 수 없는데 — 만일 언젠가 그가 스티어포스와 마주친다면, 그를 죽이고 말리라는 생각이었다."]], [["‘My dooty here, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"여기서 제가 할 도리는요, 도련님.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘is done.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"다 했습니다."], ["I’m a going to seek my--’ he stopped, and went on in a firmer voice: ‘I’m a going to seek her.", "저는 이제 제 — 을 찾으러 갑니다.\" 그는 말을 멈췄다가, 한결 굳은 목소리로 이어 갔다. \"저는 이제 그 애를 찾으러 갑니다."], ["That’s my dooty evermore.’", "그게 앞으로 영원토록 제가 할 도리예요.\""]], [["He shook his head when I asked him where he would seek her, and inquired if I were going to London tomorrow?", "내가 어디로 그 애를 찾으러 갈 거냐고 묻자 그는 고개를 가로저었고, 내가 내일 런던으로 가느냐고 물었다."], ["I told him I had not gone today, fearing to lose the chance of being of any service to him; but that I was ready to go when he would.", "나는 오늘은 가지 않았다고, 그를 위해 무어라도 도움이 될 기회를 놓칠까 봐 그랬다고 말했다. 하지만 그가 갈 때면 나도 갈 채비가 되어 있다고 했다."]], [["‘I’ll go along with you, sir,’ he rejoined, ‘if you’re agreeable, tomorrow.’", "\"도련님만 좋으시다면 함께 가지요.\" 그가 받아 말했다. \"내일요.\""]], [["We walked again, for a while, in silence.", "우리는 또 한동안 말없이 걸었다."]], [["‘Ham,’ he presently resumed, ‘he’ll hold to his present work, and go and live along with my sister.", "\"햄은요.\" 그가 이윽고 다시 말을 이었다. \"지금 하는 일을 계속하고, 제 누이랑 같이 가서 살 겁니다."], ["The old boat yonder--’", "저기 저 낡은 배는—\""]], [["‘Will you desert the old boat, Mr. Peggotty?’ I gently interposed.", "\"그 낡은 배를 버리실 거예요, 패거티 씨?\" 내가 부드럽게 끼어들었다."]], [["‘My station, Mas’r Davy,’ he returned, ‘ain’t there no longer; and if ever a boat foundered, since there was darkness on the face of the deep, that one’s gone down.", "\"제 자리는요, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이제 거기 없습니다."], ["But no, sir, no; I doen’t mean as it should be deserted.", "그리고 깊은 바다 위에 어둠이 깔린 이래 침몰한 배가 하나라도 있다면, 그 배는 가라앉은 거예요. 하지만 아니요, 도련님, 아닙니다. 그 배를 버려야 한다는 뜻은 아니에요."], ["Fur from that.’", "그런 게 결코 아닙니다.\""]], [["We walked again for a while, as before, until he explained:", "우리는 아까처럼 또 한동안 걸었고, 마침내 그가 설명했다."]], [["‘My wishes is, sir, as it shall look, day and night, winter and summer, as it has always looked, since she fust know’d it.", "\"제 바람은요, 도련님, 그 배가 밤이고 낮이고, 겨울이고 여름이고, 그 애가 처음 그 배를 알았던 그때 모습 그대로 늘 보였으면 하는 겁니다."], ["If ever she should come a wandering back, I wouldn’t have the old place seem to cast her off, you understand, but seem to tempt her to draw nigher to ‘t, and to peep in, maybe, like a ghost, out of the wind and rain, through the old winder, at the old seat by the fire.", "혹여라도 그 애가 헤매다 돌아온다면, 그 옛집이 그 애를 내쳤다고 여기게 하고 싶진 않거든요, 아시겠어요. 오히려 그 애가 가까이 다가오고 싶게, 어쩌면 유령처럼 비바람을 피해 낡은 창문 너머로 난롯가 옛 자리를 살짝 들여다보고 싶게 하고 싶어요."], ["Then, maybe, Mas’r Davy, seein’ none but Missis Gummidge there, she might take heart to creep in, trembling; and might come to be laid down in her old bed, and rest her weary head where it was once so gay.’", "그러면, 어쩌면, 데이비 도련님, 거기 거미지 부인 말고는 아무도 없는 걸 보고서, 그 애가 용기를 내어 떨면서 살그머니 기어들어 올지도 모르지요. 그래서 자기 옛 침대에 누워, 한때 그토록 즐거웠던 그곳에 지친 머리를 뉘고 쉬게 될지도 모르고요.\""]], [["I could not speak to him in reply, though I tried.", "나는 대꾸하려 애썼지만 그에게 한마디도 할 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Every night,’ said Mr.", "\"밤마다요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘as reg’lar as the night comes, the candle must be stood in its old pane of glass, that if ever she should see it, it may seem to say “Come back, my child, come back!”", "\"밤이 찾아오는 만큼 어김없이, 그 옛 유리창에 촛불을 세워 둬야 합니다. 혹여라도 그 애가 그걸 본다면, 그 불빛이 '돌아오너라, 내 아가, 돌아오너라!' 하고 말하는 것처럼 보이게요."], ["If ever there’s a knock, Ham (partic’ler a soft knock), arter dark, at your aunt’s door, doen’t you go nigh it.", "혹여라도 날이 저문 뒤에 누가 자네 고모 댁 문을 두드리거든, 햄아 (특히 살그머니 두드리거든), 자넨 거기 가까이 가지 마라."], ["Let it be her--not you--that sees my fallen child!’", "내 가엾이 타락한 그 애를 보는 건 — 자네가 아니라 — 그 사람이라야 한다!\""]], [["He walked a little in front of us, and kept before us for some minutes.", "그는 우리보다 조금 앞서 걷더니, 몇 분 동안 줄곧 우리 앞에 섰다."], ["During this interval, I glanced at Ham again, and observing the same expression on his face, and his eyes still directed to the distant light, I touched his arm.", "그 사이 나는 다시 햄을 흘끗 보았는데, 그 얼굴에 똑같은 표정이 어려 있고 두 눈은 여전히 저 멀리 빛을 향한 것을 보고서, 그의 팔을 건드렸다."]], [["Twice I called him by his name, in the tone in which I might have tried to rouse a sleeper, before he heeded me.", "그가 나를 알아채기까지 나는 두 번이나 그의 이름을 불렀는데, 잠든 사람을 깨우려 할 때 쓸 법한 그런 어조였다."], ["When I at last inquired on what his thoughts were so bent, he replied:", "마침내 무슨 생각에 그토록 마음을 쏟고 있느냐고 묻자, 그가 대답했다."]], [["‘On what’s afore me, Mas’r Davy; and over yon.’ ‘On the life before you, do you mean?’ He had pointed confusedly out to sea.", "\"제 앞에 놓인 것에요, 데이비 도련님. 그리고 저 너머에요.\" \"자네 앞에 놓인 인생을 말하는 건가?\" 그는 어수선하게 바다 쪽을 가리켰다."]], [["‘Ay, Mas’r Davy.", "\"네, 데이비 도련님."], ["I doen’t rightly know how ‘tis, but from over yon there seemed to me to come--the end of it like,’ looking at me as if he were waking, but with the same determined face.", "어찌 그런지 저도 잘 모르겠는데, 저 너머에서 무언가 오는 것 같았어요 — 그게 끝 같은 거 말이에요.\" 그가 마치 잠에서 깨어나는 사람처럼, 그러나 여전히 단호한 얼굴로 나를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘What end?’ I asked, possessed by my former fear.", "\"무슨 끝?\" 내가 물었다, 아까의 그 두려움에 사로잡힌 채로."]], [["‘I doen’t know,’ he said, thoughtfully; ‘I was calling to mind that the beginning of it all did take place here--and then the end come. But it’s gone! Mas’r Davy,’ he added; answering, as I think, my look; ‘you han’t no call to be afeerd of me: but I’m kiender muddled; I don’t fare to feel no matters,’--which was as much as to say that he was not himself, and quite confounded.", "\"모르겠어요.\" 그가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"이 모든 게 여기서 시작됐다는 걸 떠올리고 있었어요 — 그리고 그다음에 끝이 오는 거지요. 하지만 가 버렸어요! 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 덧붙였다. 내 생각에는, 내 표정에 답하느라 그런 것 같았다. \"저를 무서워하실 거 없어요. 그냥 제가 좀 얼떨떨해서요. 아무것도 제대로 느껴지질 않네요.\" 이는 곧, 제정신이 아니고 완전히 갈피를 못 잡고 있다는 말이나 다름없었다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty stopping for us to join him: we did so, and said no more.", "패거티 씨가 합류하라고 우리를 위해 멈춰 섰고, 우리는 그렇게 했으며, 더는 말을 하지 않았다."], ["The remembrance of this, in connexion with my former thought, however, haunted me at intervals, even until the inexorable end came at its appointed time.", "그러나 이 일에 대한 기억은, 아까의 그 생각과 한데 얽혀, 정해진 때에 거역할 수 없는 그 끝이 찾아오기까지 줄곧 이따금씩 나를 사로잡았다."]], [["We insensibly approached the old boat, and entered. Mrs.", "우리는 나도 모르는 사이에 낡은 배에 다가가 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Gummidge, no longer moping in her especial corner, was busy preparing breakfast.", "거미지 부인은 더 이상 늘 앉던 구석에서 풀이 죽어 있지 않고, 부지런히 아침을 차리고 있었다."], ["She took Mr. Peggotty’s hat, and placed his seat for him, and spoke so comfortably and softly, that I hardly knew her.", "그녀는 패거티 씨의 모자를 받아 들고, 그의 자리를 마련해 주고, 어찌나 따뜻하고 부드럽게 말을 거는지, 나는 그녀를 알아보기조차 힘들었다."]], [["‘Dan’l, my good man,’ said she, ‘you must eat and drink, and keep up your strength, for without it you’ll do nowt.", "\"댄을, 우리 착한 양반.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"먹고 마시고, 기운을 차려야 해요, 안 그러면 아무것도 못 할 테니까."], ["Try, that’s a dear soul!", "좀 들어 봐요, 우리 귀한 양반!"], ["An if I disturb you with my clicketten,’ she meant her chattering, ‘tell me so, Dan’l, and I won’t.’", "그리고 내가 종알종알 떠들어서 거슬리거든,\" 그녀가 말한 것은 자기가 재잘대는 것이었다, \"그렇다고 말해 줘요, 댄을, 그럼 안 그럴게요.\""]], [["When she had served us all, she withdrew to the window, where she sedulously employed herself in repairing some shirts and other clothes belonging to Mr. Peggotty, and neatly folding and packing them in an old oilskin bag, such as sailors carry.", "우리 모두에게 시중을 다 들고 나서, 그녀는 창가로 물러나, 패거티 씨의 셔츠 몇 벌과 다른 옷가지를 손보는 일에 정성껏 매달려, 그것들을 가지런히 접어 뱃사람들이 들고 다니는 그런 낡은 방수포 가방에 챙겨 넣었다."], ["Meanwhile, she continued talking, in the same quiet manner:", "그러는 내내, 그녀는 한결같이 차분한 투로 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["‘All times and seasons, you know, Dan’l,’ said Mrs.", "\"어느 때 어느 철이든, 알지요, 댄을.\" 거미지 부인이 말했다."], ["Gummidge, ‘I shall be allus here, and everythink will look accordin’ to your wishes.", "\"나는 언제고 여기 있을 거고, 모든 게 당신 바람대로 돌아가게 할 거예요."], ["I’m a poor scholar, but I shall write to you, odd times, when you’re away, and send my letters to Mas’r Davy.", "나야 배운 게 변변찮지만, 이따금 당신이 떠나 있을 때 편지를 써서 데이비 도련님 앞으로 부칠게요."], ["Maybe you’ll write to me too, Dan’l, odd times, and tell me how you fare to feel upon your lone lorn journies.’", "어쩌면 당신도 나한테 써 줄지 모르지요, 댄을, 이따금, 그 외롭고 쓸쓸한 길에서 어떤 심정인지를요.\""]], [["‘You’ll be a solitary woman heer, I’m afeerd!’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"당신, 여기서 외톨이 신세가 될까 봐 걱정이오!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no, Dan’l,’ she returned, ‘I shan’t be that.", "\"아니, 아니에요, 댄을.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["Doen’t you mind me.", "\"그렇진 않을 거예요."], ["I shall have enough to do to keep a Beein for you’ (Mrs.", "내 걱정은 마요. 나는 당신을 위해 보금자리를 지키느라 할 일이 넘칠 테니까\""], ["Gummidge meant a home), ‘again you come back--to keep a Beein here for any that may hap to come back, Dan’l.", "(거미지 부인이 말한 것은 집이었다) \"당신이 돌아올 때까지요 — 혹여 돌아올지 모를 누구를 위해서라도 여기 보금자리를 지키느라요, 댄을."], ["In the fine time, I shall set outside the door as I used to do.", "날 좋을 때면 나는 예전처럼 문간에 나가 앉을 거예요."], ["If any should come nigh, they shall see the old widder woman true to ‘em, a long way off.’", "누가 가까이 오거든, 그 늙은 과부가 멀찍이서부터 자기들한테 변함없는 걸 보게 될 거예요.\""]], [["What a change in Mrs. Gummidge in a little time!", "짧은 시간 사이에 거미지 부인은 어찌나 달라졌는지!"], ["She was another woman.", "그녀는 딴사람이었다."], ["She was so devoted, she had such a quick perception of what it would be well to say, and what it would be well to leave unsaid; she was so forgetful of herself, and so regardful of the sorrow about her, that I held her in a sort of veneration.", "어찌나 헌신적인지, 무슨 말을 하면 좋고 무슨 말을 안 하는 게 좋을지 어찌나 재바르게 알아채는지, 자기 자신은 어찌나 잊고 주위의 슬픔은 어찌나 살뜰히 챙기는지, 나는 그녀에게 일종의 경외감마저 품게 되었다."], ["The work she did that day!", "그날 그녀가 해낸 일이라니!"], ["There were many things to be brought up from the beach and stored in the outhouse--as oars, nets, sails, cordage, spars, lobster-pots, bags of ballast, and the like; and though there was abundance of assistance rendered, there being not a pair of working hands on all that shore but would have laboured hard for Mr. Peggotty, and been well paid in being asked to do it, yet she persisted, all day long, in toiling under weights that she was quite unequal to, and fagging to and fro on all sorts of unnecessary errands.", "바닷가에서 헛간으로 끌어 올려 챙겨 둬야 할 것이 많았다 — 노며, 그물이며, 돛이며, 밧줄이며, 활대며, 바닷가재 통발이며, 바닥짐 자루며, 그 비슷한 것들이. 그리고 일손이야 넘치도록 거들었으니, 그 바닷가에 패거티 씨를 위해서라면 기꺼이 힘껏 일할 사람, 그 일을 부탁받는 것만으로도 충분히 보람을 느낄 사람이 한 쌍도 빠짐없었음에도, 그녀는 온종일 자기 힘에 도무지 부치는 무거운 짐을 지고 끙끙대고, 온갖 쓸데없는 심부름으로 이리저리 부산을 떨기를 고집했다."], ["As to deploring her misfortunes, she appeared to have entirely lost the recollection of ever having had any.", "자기 불행을 한탄하는 일로 말하자면, 그녀는 자기에게 그런 게 있었다는 사실 자체를 까맣게 잊어버린 듯했다."], ["She preserved an equable cheerfulness in the midst of her sympathy, which was not the least astonishing part of the change that had come over her.", "그녀는 동정심 한가운데서도 한결같은 명랑함을 잃지 않았는데, 이것이 그녀에게 일어난 변화 가운데서도 결코 덜 놀라운 대목이 아니었다."], ["Querulousness was out of the question.", "푸념은 입에 올릴 일조차 없었다."], ["I did not even observe her voice to falter, or a tear to escape from her eyes, the whole day through, until twilight; when she and I and Mr.", "그날 하루 내내 황혼이 질 때까지 나는 그녀의 목소리가 떨리는 것도, 눈에서 눈물 한 방울 흐르는 것도 보지 못했다."], ["Peggotty being alone together, and he having fallen asleep in perfect exhaustion, she broke into a half-suppressed fit of sobbing and crying, and taking me to the door, said, ‘Ever bless you, Mas’r Davy, be a friend to him, poor dear!’ Then, she immediately ran out of the house to wash her face, in order that she might sit quietly beside him, and be found at work there, when he should awake.", "그러다 그녀와 나, 그리고 패거티 씨만 남게 되었을 때, 그가 완전히 기진맥진해 잠들어 버리자, 그녀는 반쯤 억누른 흐느낌과 울음을 터뜨리며, 나를 문간으로 데려가 말했다. \"부디 복 받으세요, 데이비 도련님, 저 가엾은 양반의 벗이 되어 주세요!\" 그러고는 곧장 집 밖으로 달려 나가 얼굴을 씻었는데, 그래야 조용히 그의 곁에 앉아, 그가 깨어났을 때 거기서 일하는 모습으로 발견될 수 있을 터였다."], ["In short I left her, when I went away at night, the prop and staff of Mr.", "한마디로, 그날 밤 내가 떠날 때, 나는 그녀를 패거티 씨의 비탄을 떠받치는 버팀목이자 지팡이로 남겨 두고 왔다."], ["Peggotty’s affliction; and I could not meditate enough upon the lesson that I read in Mrs. Gummidge, and the new experience she unfolded to me.", "나는 거미지 부인에게서 읽어 낸 그 교훈을, 그녀가 나에게 펼쳐 보여 준 그 새로운 깨달음을 아무리 곱씹어도 모자랐다."]], [["It was between nine and ten o’clock when, strolling in a melancholy manner through the town, I stopped at Mr.", "내가 우울한 걸음으로 동네를 거닐다가 오머 씨의 문 앞에 멈춰 선 것은 아홉 시에서 열 시 사이였다."], ["Omer’s door. Mr. Omer had taken it so much to heart, his daughter told me, that he had been very low and poorly all day, and had gone to bed without his pipe.", "그의 딸이 일러 주기를, 오머 씨는 이 일을 어찌나 가슴 아파하는지 온종일 몹시 풀이 죽고 기운이 없어, 파이프 담배도 피우지 않은 채 잠자리에 들었다고 했다."]], [["‘A deceitful, bad-hearted girl,’ said Mrs.", "\"속이 시커멓고 마음씨 고약한 계집애예요.\""], ["Joram. ‘There was no good in her, ever!’", "조램 부인이 말했다. \"애초에 글러 먹은 애였다고요!\""]], [["‘Don’t say so,’ I returned.", "\"그런 말 마세요.\" 내가 받아 말했다."], ["‘You don’t think so.’", "\"그렇게 생각하지 않으시면서.\""]], [["‘Yes, I do!’ cried Mrs.", "\"네, 그렇게 생각해요!\""], ["Joram, angrily. ‘No, no,’ said I.", "조램 부인이 성을 내며 외쳤다."]], [["Mrs. Joram tossed her head, endeavouring to be very stern and cross; but she could not command her softer self, and began to cry.", "\"아뇨, 아니에요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["I was young, to be sure; but I thought much the better of her for this sympathy, and fancied it became her, as a virtuous wife and mother, very well indeed.", "조램 부인은 어떻게든 아주 엄하고 쌀쌀맞게 굴어 보려고 고개를 홱 젖혔으나, 자신의 부드러운 본심을 다스리지 못하고 울기 시작했다. 나야 분명 어렸지만, 이 동정심 때문에 그녀를 훨씬 더 좋게 보았고, 정숙한 아내이자 어머니로서 그녀에게 그런 모습이 참으로 잘 어울린다고 생각했다."]], [["‘What will she ever do!’ sobbed Minnie.", "\"걔는 이제 어떡한대요!\" 미니가 흐느꼈다."], ["‘Where will she go!", "\"어디로 간대요!"], ["What will become of her!", "걔는 어떻게 되는 거예요!"], ["Oh, how could she be so cruel, to herself and him!’", "아아, 어떻게 저 자신한테도 그이한테도 그렇게 모질 수가 있어요!\""]], [["I remembered the time when Minnie was a young and pretty girl; and I was glad she remembered it too, so feelingly.", "나는 미니가 젊고 예쁜 아가씨였던 시절을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그녀도 그 시절을 그토록 살뜰히 기억하고 있다는 것이 반가웠다."]], [["‘My little Minnie,’ said Mrs.", "\"우리 어린 미니는요.\""], ["Joram, ‘has only just now been got to sleep.", "조램 부인이 말했다. \"이제 막 겨우 잠이 들었어요."], ["Even in her sleep she is sobbing for Em’ly.", "잠결에도 에밀리를 부르며 흐느낀답니다."], ["All day long, little Minnie has cried for her, and asked me, over and over again, whether Em’ly was wicked?", "우리 어린 미니가 온종일 그 애를 찾아 울면서, 에밀리가 못된 애냐고 나한테 묻고 또 물었어요."], ["What can I say to her, when Em’ly tied a ribbon off her own neck round little Minnie’s the last night she was here, and laid her head down on the pillow beside her till she was fast asleep!", "에밀리가 여기 있던 마지막 날 밤에 제 목에 두른 리본을 풀어 우리 어린 미니 목에 둘러 주고, 아이가 곤히 잠들 때까지 베개 옆에 머리를 뉘어 줬는데, 내가 그 애한테 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어요!"], ["The ribbon’s round my little Minnie’s neck now.", "그 리본이 지금도 우리 어린 미니 목에 둘려 있어요."], ["It ought not to be, perhaps, but what can I do?", "아마 그래선 안 되겠지만, 내가 어쩌겠어요?"], ["Em’ly is very bad, but they were fond of one another.", "에밀리가 아주 못된 짓을 했어도, 둘은 서로 끔찍이 아꼈어요."], ["And the child knows nothing!’", "그리고 아이는 아무것도 모른답니다!\""]], [["Mrs. Joram was so unhappy that her husband came out to take care of her.", "조램 부인이 어찌나 비통해하는지 그녀의 남편이 그녀를 돌보려고 나왔다."], ["Leaving them together, I went home to Peggotty’s; more melancholy myself, if possible, than I had been yet.", "두 사람을 함께 두고, 나는 패거티의 집으로 돌아갔다. 가능하다면, 그때까지보다도 더 우울한 심정으로."]], [["That good creature--I mean Peggotty--all untired by her late anxieties and sleepless nights, was at her brother’s, where she meant to stay till morning. An old woman, who had been employed about the house for some weeks past, while Peggotty had been unable to attend to it, was the house’s only other occupant besides myself.", "그 착한 사람은 — 패거티를 두고 하는 말이다 — 근래의 근심과 잠 못 든 밤들로도 조금도 지치지 않은 채, 제 오라비의 집에 가 있었는데, 거기서 아침까지 머물 작정이었다. 패거티가 집안일을 돌볼 겨를이 없던 지난 몇 주 동안 일손으로 들였던 어느 노파가, 나 말고는 그 집의 유일한 식구였다."], ["As I had no occasion for her services, I sent her to bed, by no means against her will, and sat down before the kitchen fire a little while, to think about all this.", "나는 그녀의 시중이 필요하지 않았으므로, 그녀를 잠자리로 보냈는데, 그녀로서도 결코 마다할 일이 아니었다. 그러고는 한동안 부엌 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 이 모든 일을 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["I was blending it with the deathbed of the late Mr. Barkis, and was driving out with the tide towards the distance at which Ham had looked so singularly in the morning, when I was recalled from my wanderings by a knock at the door.", "나는 그것을 고인이 된 바키스 씨의 임종과 한데 뒤섞으며, 아침에 햄이 그토록 야릇하게 바라보던 그 먼 곳을 향해 밀물을 타고 떠밀려 나가고 있었는데, 그때 문 두드리는 소리에 그 헤맴에서 불려 나왔다."], ["There was a knocker upon the door, but it was not that which made the sound.", "문에는 노커가 달려 있었지만, 그 소리를 낸 것은 그게 아니었다."], ["The tap was from a hand, and low down upon the door, as if it were given by a child.", "그 똑똑 소리는 손으로 낸 것이었고, 문 아래쪽에서 났는데, 마치 어린아이가 두드린 것 같았다."]], [["It made me start as much as if it had been the knock of a footman to a person of distinction.", "그 소리는 지체 높은 분을 찾아온 하인의 노크라도 되는 양 나를 흠칫 놀라게 했다."], ["I opened the door; and at first looked down, to my amazement, on nothing but a great umbrella that appeared to be walking about of itself.", "나는 문을 열었다. 그리고 처음에는 놀랍게도, 저 혼자 돌아다니는 듯한 커다란 우산 하나밖에 보이지 않아 시선을 아래로 떨궜다."], ["But presently I discovered underneath it, Miss Mowcher.", "그러나 이내 그 밑에서 모처 양을 발견했다."]], [["I might not have been prepared to give the little creature a very kind reception, if, on her removing the umbrella, which her utmost efforts were unable to shut up, she had shown me the ‘volatile’ expression of face which had made so great an impression on me at our first and last meeting.", "만일 그녀가 아무리 안간힘을 써도 접지 못하던 그 우산을 치웠을 때, 우리가 처음이자 마지막으로 만났을 적에 나에게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼던 그 '변덕스러운' 표정을 지어 보였더라면, 나는 그 자그마한 사람을 그리 살갑게 맞이할 마음이 들지 않았을지도 모른다."], ["But her face, as she turned it up to mine, was so earnest; and when I relieved her of the umbrella (which would have been an inconvenient one for the Irish Giant), she wrung her little hands in such an afflicted manner; that I rather inclined towards her.", "그러나 그녀가 나를 향해 쳐든 얼굴은 어찌나 간절한지, 게다가 내가 그 우산을 (아일랜드 거인이 들기에도 거추장스러웠을 그것을) 대신 받아 들자, 그녀가 어찌나 비통하게 자그마한 두 손을 비틀어 짜는지, 나는 오히려 그녀 쪽으로 마음이 기울었다."]], [["‘Miss Mowcher!’ said I, after glancing up and down the empty street, without distinctly knowing what I expected to see besides; ‘how do you come here?", "\"모처 양!\" 텅 빈 거리를 위아래로 한번 흘끗거리며 — 무얼 더 보게 되리라 기대한 건지 나 자신도 분명히 알지 못한 채 — 내가 말했다."], ["What is the matter?’ She motioned to me with her short right arm, to shut the umbrella for her; and passing me hurriedly, went into the kitchen.", "\"어떻게 여기까지 오셨어요? 무슨 일이에요?\" 그녀는 짤막한 오른팔로 나에게 우산을 접어 달라는 시늉을 했고, 황급히 나를 지나쳐 부엌으로 들어갔다."], ["When I had closed the door, and followed, with the umbrella in my hand, I found her sitting on the corner of the fender--it was a low iron one, with two flat bars at top to stand plates upon--in the shadow of the boiler, swaying herself backwards and forwards, and chafing her hands upon her knees like a person in pain.", "내가 문을 닫고 우산을 손에 든 채 뒤따라가 보니, 그녀는 난로 가리개 모서리에 앉아 있었는데 — 그것은 낮은 무쇠 가리개로, 위에 접시를 올려놓을 수 있게 납작한 살 두 개가 있었다 — 보일러의 그늘 속에서 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들며, 아픈 사람처럼 두 손을 무릎에 대고 비벼 대고 있었다."]], [["Quite alarmed at being the only recipient of this untimely visit, and the only spectator of this portentous behaviour, I exclaimed again, ‘Pray tell me, Miss Mowcher, what is the matter!", "이 난데없는 방문을 받은 유일한 사람이자, 이 불길한 거동을 지켜보는 유일한 구경꾼이 된 것에 적잖이 놀란 나는, 다시 외쳤다. \"제발 말씀해 주세요, 모처 양, 무슨 일이에요!"], ["are you ill?’", "어디 편찮으세요?\""]], [["‘My dear young soul,’ returned Miss Mowcher, squeezing her hands upon her heart one over the other.", "\"내 귀한 젊은이.\" 모처 양이 두 손을 포개어 제 가슴을 꼭 누르며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I am ill here, I am very ill. To think that it should come to this, when I might have known it and perhaps prevented it, if I hadn’t been a thoughtless fool!’", "\"나는 여기가 아파요, 몹시 아프다고요. 일이 이 지경에 이르다니, 내가 그 생각 없는 바보만 아니었더라면 진작 알아채고 어쩌면 막을 수도 있었을 텐데!\""]], [["Again her large bonnet (very disproportionate to the figure) went backwards and forwards, in her swaying of her little body to and fro; while a most gigantic bonnet rocked, in unison with it, upon the wall.", "다시 그녀의 커다란 보닛이 (몸집에 견주면 영 어울리지 않게 큰 것이었다) 자그마한 몸을 이리저리 흔드는 통에 앞뒤로 까딱거렸고, 벽에는 거대하기 짝이 없는 보닛 그림자가 그것과 박자를 맞추어 흔들거렸다."]], [["‘I am surprised,’ I began, ‘to see you so distressed and serious’--when she interrupted me.", "\"놀랍네요.\" 내가 입을 뗐다. \"이렇게 비탄에 잠겨 진지해하시는 걸 보니—\" 그때 그녀가 내 말을 가로막았다."]], [["‘Yes, it’s always so!’ she said.", "\"그래요, 늘 그렇지요!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘They are all surprised, these inconsiderate young people, fairly and full grown, to see any natural feeling in a little thing like me!", "\"다들 놀라요, 이 분별없는 젊은이들은, 어엿이 다 자란 어른들이, 나 같은 자그마한 것한테 무슨 자연스러운 감정이 있는 걸 보면!"], ["They make a plaything of me, use me for their amusement, throw me away when they are tired, and wonder that I feel more than a toy horse or a wooden soldier!", "다들 나를 장난감으로 삼고, 저희 즐거움에 써먹다가, 싫증 나면 내던지고는, 내가 장난감 목마나 나무 병정보다 더 깊이 느낀다고 놀라워하지요! 그래요, 그래, 다 그런 식이에요."], ["Yes, yes, that’s the way. The old way!’", "늘 그래 온 그 방식 말이에요!\""]], [["‘It may be, with others,’ I returned, ‘but I do assure you it is not with me.", "\"다른 사람들은 그럴지 몰라도요.\" 내가 받아 말했다. \"분명히 말씀드리지만 저는 그렇지 않습니다."], ["Perhaps I ought not to be at all surprised to see you as you are now: I know so little of you.", "어쩌면 지금 모처 양의 이런 모습을 보고 제가 조금도 놀라지 말았어야 했는지도 모르지요. 제가 모처 양을 너무 모르니까요."], ["I said, without consideration, what I thought.’", "저는 깊이 생각하지 않고, 그저 떠오른 대로 말했던 거예요.\""]], [["‘What can I do?’ returned the little woman, standing up, and holding out her arms to show herself. ‘See!", "\"내가 뭘 어쩌겠어요?\" 그 자그마한 여인이 일어서서 두 팔을 벌려 제 모습을 보이며 대꾸했다. \"보세요!"], ["What I am, my father was; and my sister is; and my brother is.", "지금 내 모습, 그게 우리 아버지 모습이었고, 우리 언니 모습이고, 우리 오빠 모습이에요."], ["I have worked for sister and brother these many years--hard, Mr.", "나는 이 여러 해 동안 언니와 오빠를 위해 일해 왔어요 — 고되게요, 코퍼필드 씨 — 온종일요."], ["Copperfield--all day.", "나는 살아야 하니까요."], ["I must live. I do no harm.", "나는 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않아요."], ["If there are people so unreflecting or so cruel, as to make a jest of me, what is left for me to do but to make a jest of myself, them, and everything?", "만일 나를 두고 농지거리를 할 만큼 생각 없고 모진 사람들이 있다면, 나한테 남은 길이 나 자신을, 그들을, 세상 모든 걸 농담거리로 삼는 것 말고 또 뭐가 있겠어요?"], ["If I do so, for the time, whose fault is that? Mine?’", "내가 당장이야 그렇게 한다 친들, 그게 누구 잘못이에요? 내 잘못인가요?\""]], [["No.", "아니."], ["Not Miss Mowcher’s, I perceived.", "모처 양의 잘못이 아니라는 걸, 나는 알아챘다."]], [["‘If I had shown myself a sensitive dwarf to your false friend,’ pursued the little woman, shaking her head at me, with reproachful earnestness, ‘how much of his help or good will do you think I should ever have had?", "\"내가 당신의 그 가짜 친구한테 감수성 예민한 난쟁이 행세를 했더라면요.\" 그 자그마한 여인이 나무라듯 진지하게 나를 향해 고개를 가로저으며 말을 이었다. \"그자한테서 도움이나 호의를 얼마나 받았을 성싶어요?"], ["If little Mowcher (who had no hand, young gentleman, in the making of herself) addressed herself to him, or the like of him, because of her misfortunes, when do you suppose her small voice would have been heard?", "만일 어린 모처가 (이봐요 젊은이, 자기를 이렇게 만드는 데는 아무 손도 보탠 적 없는 이 모처가) 제 불행을 빌미로 그자나 그 비슷한 자한테 호소했더라면, 그 가냘픈 목소리가 언제 한 번이라도 귀에 들렸겠어요?"], ["Little Mowcher would have as much need to live, if she was the bitterest and dullest of pigmies; but she couldn’t do it. No.", "어린 모처가 난쟁이 가운데서도 가장 모질고 가장 무딘 난쟁이라 한들 살아야 하는 건 마찬가지지요. 하지만 그런 식으론 살 수가 없어요. 안 되지요."], ["She might whistle for her bread and butter till she died of Air.’", "굶어 죽도록 빵 한 조각 버터 한 조각 달라고 휘파람이나 불어 댈 수밖에요, 바람을 먹다 죽도록 말이에요.\""]], [["Miss Mowcher sat down on the fender again, and took out her handkerchief, and wiped her eyes.", "모처 양은 다시 난로 가리개에 앉아 손수건을 꺼내 눈을 닦았다."]], [["‘Be thankful for me, if you have a kind heart, as I think you have,’ she said, ‘that while I know well what I am, I can be cheerful and endure it all.", "\"당신이 내 생각대로 다정한 마음을 지녔다면, 나를 위해 고마워해 줘요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 내 처지를 빤히 알면서도, 명랑할 수 있고 이 모든 걸 견딜 수 있다는 걸요."], ["I am thankful for myself, at any rate, that I can find my tiny way through the world, without being beholden to anyone; and that in return for all that is thrown at me, in folly or vanity, as I go along, I can throw bubbles back.", "어쨌든 나 자신을 위해서 나는 고마워해요, 누구한테도 신세 지지 않고 이 세상을 내 자그마한 방식대로 헤쳐 나갈 수 있다는 것이요. 그리고 길을 가는 동안 어리석음이나 허영에서 나한테 던져지는 그 모든 것에 대한 답례로, 나는 거품을 되던질 수 있다는 것이요."], ["If I don’t brood over all I want, it is the better for me, and not the worse for anyone.", "내가 원하는 것들을 죄다 끌어안고 곱씹지 않는다면, 그건 나한테 나은 일이고, 누구한테도 나쁠 게 없어요."], ["If I am a plaything for you giants, be gentle with me.’", "내가 당신네 거인들한테 장난감이라면, 부디 나한테 다정하게 굴어 줘요.\""]], [["Miss Mowcher replaced her handkerchief in her pocket, looking at me with very intent expression all the while, and pursued:", "모처 양은 손수건을 다시 주머니에 넣고는, 내내 무척 골똘한 표정으로 나를 바라보며 말을 이었다."]], [["‘I saw you in the street just now.", "\"방금 거리에서 당신을 봤어요."], ["You may suppose I am not able to walk as fast as you, with my short legs and short breath, and I couldn’t overtake you; but I guessed where you came, and came after you.", "짐작하겠지만 나는 짧은 다리와 가쁜 숨으로는 당신만큼 빨리 걷지 못하니, 당신을 따라잡을 수가 없었지요. 하지만 당신이 어디로 가는지 짐작하고서 뒤따라왔어요."], ["I have been here before, today, but the good woman wasn’t at home.’", "오늘 아까도 여기 왔었는데, 그 착한 부인이 집에 없었어요.\""]], [["‘Do you know her?’ I demanded.", "\"그분을 아세요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I know of her, and about her,’ she replied, ‘from Omer and Joram.", "\"그 부인에 대해 알아요, 그 부인 사정도요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["I was there at seven o’clock this morning.", "\"오머랑 조램한테서요. 오늘 아침 일곱 시에 거기 있었어요."], ["Do you remember what Steerforth said to me about this unfortunate girl, that time when I saw you both at the inn?’", "그 여관에서 당신네 두 사람을 봤을 때, 이 불행한 처녀를 두고 스티어포스가 나한테 했던 말 기억해요?\""]], [["The great bonnet on Miss Mowcher’s head, and the greater bonnet on the wall, began to go backwards and forwards again when she asked this question.", "모처 양이 이 질문을 던지자, 그녀 머리 위의 그 커다란 보닛과 벽에 비친 더 커다란 보닛이 다시 앞뒤로 까딱거리기 시작했다."]], [["I remembered very well what she referred to, having had it in my thoughts many times that day. I told her so.", "나는 그녀가 가리키는 것을 아주 잘 기억하고 있었으니, 그날 머릿속에서 여러 번 떠올렸던 터였다. 나는 그렇다고 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["‘May the Father of all Evil confound him,’ said the little woman, holding up her forefinger between me and her sparkling eyes, ‘and ten times more confound that wicked servant; but I believed it was YOU who had a boyish passion for her!’", "\"온갖 악의 아비가 그자를 멸하기를.\" 그 자그마한 여인이 나와 자신의 반짝이는 두 눈 사이로 집게손가락을 곧추세우며 말했다. \"그리고 그 못된 하인놈은 열 배로 더 멸하기를. 그렇지만 나는 그 애한테 풋사랑을 품은 게 바로 당신인 줄 알았다고요!\""]], [["‘I?’ I repeated.", "\"제가요?\" 내가 되물었다."]], [["‘Child, child!", "\"이런, 이런!"], ["In the name of blind ill-fortune,’ cried Miss Mowcher, wringing her hands impatiently, as she went to and fro again upon the fender, ‘why did you praise her so, and blush, and look disturbed?’", "눈먼 악운을 두고 맹세하건대.\" 모처 양이 다시 난로 가리개 위를 이리저리 오가며, 안달이 나서 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 외쳤다. \"그럼 왜 그 애를 그렇게 칭찬하고, 얼굴을 붉히고, 안절부절못하는 표정을 지었던 거예요?\""]], [["I could not conceal from myself that I had done this, though for a reason very different from her supposition.", "내가 그렇게 했다는 사실은, 비록 그녀의 짐작과는 전혀 다른 까닭에서였을지라도, 나 자신에게조차 숨길 수 없었다."]], [["‘What did I know?’ said Miss Mowcher, taking out her handkerchief again, and giving one little stamp on the ground whenever, at short intervals, she applied it to her eyes with both hands at once.", "\"내가 뭘 알았겠어요?\" 모처 양이 다시 손수건을 꺼내, 짧은 사이를 두고 양손으로 한꺼번에 눈가에 갖다 댈 때마다 바닥을 톡톡 한 번씩 구르며 말했다."], ["‘He was crossing you and wheedling you, I saw; and you were soft wax in his hands, I saw.", "\"그자가 당신을 어르고 구슬리는 게 보였어요. 그리고 당신이 그자 손안에서 물렁한 밀랍 같은 것도 보였고요."], ["Had I left the room a minute, when his man told me that “Young Innocence”", "그자의 하인이 나한테 '순진한 도련님'이 (그자가 당신을 그렇게 불렀어요."], ["(so he called you, and you may call him “Old Guilt” all the days of your life) had set his heart upon her, and she was giddy and liked him, but his master was resolved that no harm should come of it--more for your sake than for hers--and that that was their business here?", "그러니 당신은 평생토록 그자를 '늙은 죄인'이라 불러도 돼요) 그 애한테 마음을 뺐는데, 그 애가 들떠서 그를 좋아하지만, 자기 주인은 거기서 아무 탈도 안 나게 하기로 단단히 마음먹었다고 — 그 애보다 당신을 위해서요 — 그래서 그게 여기 온 용건이라고 말했을 때, 내가 그 방을 단 일 분이라도 비웠던가요?"], ["How could I BUT believe him?", "그러니 내가 그자를 안 믿고 어쩌겠어요?"], ["I saw Steerforth soothe and please you by his praise of her!", "스티어포스가 그 애를 칭찬하며 당신을 달래고 흐뭇하게 해 주는 걸 봤다고요!"], ["You were the first to mention her name.", "그 애 이름을 먼저 꺼낸 것도 당신이었어요."], ["You owned to an old admiration of her.", "당신은 오래전부터 그 애를 흠모해 왔다고 인정했고요."], ["You were hot and cold, and red and white, all at once when I spoke to you of her.", "내가 그 애 얘기를 꺼냈을 때 당신은 뜨거웠다 차가웠다, 빨개졌다 하얘졌다 했어요."], ["What could I think--what DID I think--but that you were a young libertine in everything but experience, and had fallen into hands that had experience enough, and could manage you (having the fancy) for your own good? Oh! oh! oh!", "그러니 내가 뭘 생각할 수 있었겠어요 — 내가 실제로 뭘 생각했겠어요 — 당신이 경험만 없다 뿐이지 모든 면에서 젊은 난봉꾼이고, 경험이 차고 넘쳐 (당신한테 그런 욕망이 있으니) 당신 자신을 위해 당신을 주무를 수 있는 손아귀에 빠진 거라고 여기는 것 말고요? 아아! 아아! 아아! 그자들은 내가 진실을 알아챌까 봐 두려워했던 거예요.\" 모처 양이 난로 가리개에서 내려와, 짤막한 두 팔을 비통하게 쳐든 채 부엌을 종종걸음으로 오르내리며 외쳤다."], ["They were afraid of my finding out the truth,’ exclaimed Miss Mowcher, getting off the fender, and trotting up and down the kitchen with her two short arms distressfully lifted up, ‘because I am a sharp little thing--I need be, to get through the world at all!--and they deceived me altogether, and I gave the poor unfortunate girl a letter, which I fully believe was the beginning of her ever speaking to Littimer, who was left behind on purpose!’", "\"내가 영악한 자그마한 것이니까요 — 이 세상을 조금이라도 헤쳐 나가려면 그래야만 하거든요! — 그래서 그자들이 나를 깡그리 속였고, 나는 그 가엾고 불행한 처녀한테 편지를 건넸어요. 그게, 나는 분명히 믿는데, 일부러 남겨졌던 그 리티머한테 그 애가 말을 트게 된 시초였던 거예요!\""]], [["I stood amazed at the revelation of all this perfidy, looking at Miss Mowcher as she walked up and down the kitchen until she was out of breath: when she sat upon the fender again, and, drying her face with her handkerchief, shook her head for a long time, without otherwise moving, and without breaking silence.", "이 모든 배신의 정체가 드러나자 나는 어안이 벙벙해진 채로, 숨이 찰 때까지 부엌을 오르내리는 모처 양을 바라보고 서 있었다. 이윽고 그녀는 다시 난로 가리개에 앉아, 손수건으로 얼굴을 닦으며, 다른 데는 미동도 없이, 침묵도 깨지 않고, 한참을 고개만 가로저었다."]], [["‘My country rounds,’ she added at length, ‘brought me to Norwich, Mr.", "\"내가 시골을 도는 길에.\" 그녀가 마침내 덧붙였다."], ["Copperfield, the night before last.", "\"그저께 밤 노리치에 닿았어요, 코퍼필드 씨."], ["What I happened to find there, about their secret way of coming and going, without you--which was strange--led to my suspecting something wrong.", "거기서 우연히 알게 된 것이 — 그자들이 당신도 없이 남몰래 오가는 그 방식에 대해서요, 그게 이상했거든요 — 무언가 잘못됐다는 의심으로 이어졌어요."], ["I got into the coach from London last night, as it came through Norwich, and was here this morning. Oh, oh, oh!", "어젯밤 런던에서 오는 마차가 노리치를 지날 때 그걸 잡아타고, 오늘 아침 여기 온 거예요. 아아, 아아, 아아!"], ["too late!’", "너무 늦었어!\""]], [["Poor little Mowcher turned so chilly after all her crying and fretting, that she turned round on the fender, putting her poor little wet feet in among the ashes to warm them, and sat looking at the fire, like a large doll.", "가엾은 어린 모처는 그렇게 실컷 울고 안달한 끝에 몸이 어찌나 차가워졌는지, 난로 가리개 위에서 몸을 돌려, 가엾고 자그마한 젖은 두 발을 잿더미 속에 묻어 녹이며, 커다란 인형처럼 앉아 불을 바라보았다."], ["I sat in a chair on the other side of the hearth, lost in unhappy reflections, and looking at the fire too, and sometimes at her.", "나는 난롯가 맞은편 의자에 앉아, 비참한 상념에 잠긴 채, 나 또한 불을 바라보다가 이따금 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["‘I must go,’ she said at last, rising as she spoke.", "\"가 봐야겠어요.\" 그녀가 마침내 말하며 일어섰다."], ["‘It’s late.", "\"늦었네요."], ["You don’t mistrust me?’", "나를 못 믿는 건 아니지요?\""]], [["Meeting her sharp glance, which was as sharp as ever when she asked me, I could not on that short challenge answer no, quite frankly.", "그녀가 나에게 그렇게 물을 때 여전히 날카롭던 그 날카로운 눈빛과 마주치자, 나는 그 짧은 다그침에 정말 솔직하게 아니라고 대답할 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Come!’ said she, accepting the offer of my hand to help her over the fender, and looking wistfully up into my face, ‘you know you wouldn’t mistrust me, if I was a full-sized woman!’", "\"이리 줘요!\" 그녀가 난로 가리개를 넘는 걸 도와주려고 내민 내 손을 잡으며, 그리고 내 얼굴을 애틋하게 올려다보며 말했다. \"당신도 알잖아요, 내가 멀쩡한 키의 여자였다면 나를 못 믿거나 하진 않았을 거라는 걸요!\""]], [["I felt that there was much truth in this; and I felt rather ashamed of myself.", "나는 이 말에 많은 진실이 담겨 있음을 느꼈고, 나 자신이 어쩐지 부끄러워졌다."]], [["‘You are a young man,’ she said, nodding.", "\"당신은 젊은 사람이에요.\" 그녀가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘Take a word of advice, even from three foot nothing.", "\"삼 척도 안 되는 것한테서라도 충고 한마디 새겨들어요."], ["Try not to associate bodily defects with mental, my good friend, except for a solid reason.’", "몸의 결함을 마음의 결함과 결부 짓지 않도록 애써 봐요, 내 좋은 벗, 확실한 까닭이 있는 게 아니라면요.\""]], [["She had got over the fender now, and I had got over my suspicion.", "이제 그녀는 난로 가리개를 넘어선 참이었고, 나는 내 의심을 넘어선 참이었다."], ["I told her that I believed she had given me a faithful account of herself, and that we had both been hapless instruments in designing hands.", "나는 그녀가 자기 자신에 대해 솔직히 다 털어놓았다고 믿는다고, 그리고 우리 둘 다 음흉한 손아귀에 놀아난 불운한 도구였다고 말했다."], ["She thanked me, and said I was a good fellow.", "그녀는 나에게 고마워하며, 나더러 좋은 사람이라고 했다."]], [["‘Now, mind!’ she exclaimed, turning back on her way to the door, and looking shrewdly at me, with her forefinger up again.--‘I have some reason to suspect, from what I have heard--my ears are always open; I can’t afford to spare what powers I have--that they are gone abroad.", "\"자, 명심해요!\" 그녀가 문 쪽으로 가던 길을 되돌아서서, 다시 집게손가락을 쳐든 채 나를 빈틈없이 바라보며 외쳤다. \"내가 들은 바로 미루어 의심할 만한 까닭이 좀 있어요 — 내 귀는 늘 열려 있거든요, 나는 가진 능력을 아껴 둘 처지가 못 되니까요 — 그자들이 외국으로 갔다는 거예요."], ["But if ever they return, if ever any one of them returns, while I am alive, I am more likely than another, going about as I do, to find it out soon.", "하지만 혹여라도 그들이 돌아온다면, 혹여라도 그중 누구 하나라도 내가 살아 있는 동안 돌아온다면, 나처럼 이리저리 돌아다니는 사람이라면 누구보다도 그걸 빨리 알아챌 가능성이 높아요."], ["Whatever I know, you shall know.", "내가 무얼 알게 되든, 당신도 알게 될 거예요."], ["If ever I can do anything to serve the poor betrayed girl, I will do it faithfully, please Heaven!", "혹여라도 내가 그 가엾고 배신당한 처녀를 위해 무어라도 할 수 있다면, 하늘이 허락하는 한 충실히 하겠어요!"], ["And Littimer had better have a bloodhound at his back, than little Mowcher!’", "그리고 리티머로 말하자면, 그자는 어린 모처를 뒤에 두느니 차라리 사냥개를 뒤에 두는 편이 나을 거예요!\""]], [["I placed implicit faith in this last statement, when I marked the look with which it was accompanied.", "이 마지막 말에 곁들여진 그 눈빛을 보았을 때, 나는 그 말을 한 치의 의심도 없이 믿었다."]], [["‘Trust me no more, but trust me no less, than you would trust a full-sized woman,’ said the little creature, touching me appealingly on the wrist.", "\"멀쩡한 키의 여자를 믿을 만큼만 나를 믿어 줘요, 그보다 더도 말고 덜도 말고요.\" 그 자그마한 사람이 호소하듯 내 손목을 건드리며 말했다."], ["‘If ever you see me again, unlike what I am now, and like what I was when you first saw me, observe what company I am in.", "\"혹여라도 당신이 나를 다시 보게 되는데, 그게 지금의 내 모습과는 다르고 당신이 나를 처음 봤을 때의 모습 같거든, 내가 어떤 무리에 끼어 있는지 잘 봐요."], ["Call to mind that I am a very helpless and defenceless little thing.", "내가 아주 무력하고 무방비한 자그마한 것이라는 걸 떠올려 줘요."], ["Think of me at home with my brother like myself and sister like myself, when my day’s work is done.", "하루 일을 마치고 나처럼 자그마한 오빠와 나처럼 자그마한 언니와 함께 집에 있는 나를 생각해 줘요."], ["Perhaps you won’t, then, be very hard upon me, or surprised if I can be distressed and serious.", "그러면 아마 당신도 나를 그리 모질게 대하지 않을 테고, 내가 비탄에 잠겨 진지해할 수 있다는 데 놀라지도 않겠지요."], ["Good night!’", "잘 자요!\""]], [["I gave Miss Mowcher my hand, with a very different opinion of her from that which I had hitherto entertained, and opened the door to let her out.", "나는 지금껏 품어 온 것과는 사뭇 다른 마음으로 모처 양에게 손을 내밀어 주고는, 그녀를 내보내려고 문을 열었다."], ["It was not a trifling business to get the great umbrella up, and properly balanced in her grasp; but at last I successfully accomplished this, and saw it go bobbing down the street through the rain, without the least appearance of having anybody underneath it, except when a heavier fall than usual from some over-charged water-spout sent it toppling over, on one side, and discovered Miss Mowcher struggling violently to get it right.", "그 커다란 우산을 펴서 그녀의 손아귀에 제대로 균형을 잡아 들리게 하는 것은 결코 사소한 일이 아니었다. 하지만 마침내 나는 그 일을 성공적으로 해냈고, 그 우산이 빗속에서 거리를 따라 까딱까딱 멀어져 가는 것을 지켜보았는데, 그 밑에 누가 있다는 기색이라곤 조금도 없었다 — 다만 어느 넘쳐 나는 빗물받이에서 평소보다 더 거센 물줄기가 쏟아져 그 우산을 한쪽으로 휘청 넘어뜨려, 그것을 바로 세우려고 사납게 안간힘을 쓰는 모처 양이 드러날 때만 빼고는."], ["After making one or two sallies to her relief, which were rendered futile by the umbrella’s hopping on again, like an immense bird, before I could reach it, I came in, went to bed, and slept till morning.", "내가 그녀를 거들려고 한두 번 달려 나갔으나, 내가 미처 닿기도 전에 그 우산이 거대한 새처럼 다시 폴짝 뛰어 달아나는 바람에 헛수고가 되었고, 나는 안으로 들어와 잠자리에 들어 아침까지 잤다."]], [["In the morning I was joined by Mr. Peggotty and by my old nurse, and we went at an early hour to the coach office, where Mrs. Gummidge and Ham were waiting to take leave of us.", "아침에 패거티 씨와 내 옛 유모가 나와 합류했고, 우리는 이른 시각에 마차 사무소로 갔는데, 거기서 거미지 부인과 햄이 우리를 배웅하려고 기다리고 있었다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy,’ Ham whispered, drawing me aside, while Mr. Peggotty was stowing his bag among the luggage, ‘his life is quite broke up.", "\"데이비 도련님.\" 패거티 씨가 짐 사이에 자기 가방을 챙겨 넣는 사이, 햄이 나를 한쪽으로 끌며 속삭였다."], ["He doen’t know wheer he’s going; he doen’t know--what’s afore him; he’s bound upon a voyage that’ll last, on and off, all the rest of his days, take my wured for ‘t, unless he finds what he’s a seeking of.", "\"삼촌은 인생이 완전히 무너져 버렸어요. 어디로 가는지도 모르고, 앞에 뭐가 있는지도 몰라요. 삼촌은 이제 길을 떠나는데, 찾고 있는 그 애를 찾지 못한다면, 제 말 믿으세요, 그 길은 끊겼다 이어졌다 하면서 남은 평생토록 이어질 거예요."], ["I am sure you’ll be a friend to him, Mas’r Davy?’", "도련님이 삼촌의 벗이 되어 주실 거지요, 데이비 도련님?\""]], [["‘Trust me, I will indeed,’ said I, shaking hands with Ham earnestly.", "\"믿어 주게, 정말 그러겠네.\" 내가 햄과 진심으로 악수하며 말했다."]], [["‘Thankee.", "\"고마워요."], ["Thankee, very kind, sir. One thing furder.", "정말 고맙습니다, 인정 많으세요, 도련님."], ["I’m in good employ, you know, Mas’r Davy, and I han’t no way now of spending what I gets.", "한 가지만 더요. 도련님도 아시다시피 저는 일자리가 좋고, 이제 버는 걸 쓸 데가 없어요."], ["Money’s of no use to me no more, except to live.", "돈은 이제 사는 데 말고는 저한테 아무 쓸모가 없거든요."], ["If you can lay it out for him, I shall do my work with a better art.", "그걸 삼촌을 위해 좀 써 주실 수 있다면, 저는 더 신명 나게 일할 거예요."], ["Though as to that, sir,’ and he spoke very steadily and mildly, ‘you’re not to think but I shall work at all times, like a man, and act the best that lays in my power!’", "그렇다고 도련님, \" 그가 아주 차분하고 부드럽게 말했다, \"제가 언제든 사내답게 일하지 않거나, 제 힘이 닿는 한 최선을 다하지 않을 거라고는 생각지 마세요!\""]], [["I told him I was well convinced of it; and I hinted that I hoped the time might even come, when he would cease to lead the lonely life he naturally contemplated now.", "나는 그 점을 충분히 믿는다고 그에게 말했다. 그리고 그가 지금 으레 그러려니 여기는 그 외로운 삶을 그만두게 될 날이 언젠가 오기를 바란다고 넌지시 비쳤다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ he said, shaking his head, ‘all that’s past and over with me, sir.", "\"아뇨, 도련님.\" 그가 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그런 건 다 지나갔어요, 끝났습니다, 도련님."], ["No one can never fill the place that’s empty.", "텅 빈 그 자리를 채워 줄 사람은 누구도 없어요."], ["But you’ll bear in mind about the money, as theer’s at all times some laying by for him?’", "그치만 돈 얘기는 마음에 새겨 두세요, 늘 삼촌을 위해 따로 떼어 둘 게 좀 있을 테니까요?\""]], [["Reminding him of the fact, that Mr. Peggotty derived a steady, though certainly a very moderate income from the bequest of his late brother-in-law, I promised to do so.", "패거티 씨가 고인이 된 매부의 유산에서 비록 매우 적은 액수일망정 꾸준한 수입을 받고 있다는 사실을 그에게 일깨워 주며, 나는 그러겠노라 약속했다."], ["We then took leave of each other.", "그러고서 우리는 서로 작별했다."], ["I cannot leave him even now, without remembering with a pang, at once his modest fortitude and his great sorrow.", "나는 지금도 그를, 그 겸손한 꿋꿋함과 그 크나큰 슬픔을 가슴 저미는 마음 없이는 떠올릴 수가 없다."]], [["As to Mrs. Gummidge, if I were to endeavour to describe how she ran down the street by the side of the coach, seeing nothing but Mr. Peggotty on the roof, through the tears she tried to repress, and dashing herself against the people who were coming in the opposite direction, I should enter on a task of some difficulty.", "거미지 부인으로 말하자면, 만일 내가 그녀가 어떻게 마차 옆을 따라 거리를 달려 내려갔는지를 그려 내려 한다면 — 억누르려 애쓰는 눈물 너머로 마차 지붕 위의 패거티 씨밖에는 아무것도 보이지 않은 채, 맞은편에서 오는 사람들에게 자기 몸을 부딪쳐 가며 — 자못 어려운 일에 발을 들이는 셈이 되리라."], ["Therefore I had better leave her sitting on a baker’s door-step, out of breath, with no shape at all remaining in her bonnet, and one of her shoes off, lying on the pavement at a considerable distance.", "그러니 나는 그녀를 어느 빵집 문간 계단에 앉혀 둔 채 떠나는 편이 낫겠다, 숨을 헐떡이며, 보닛은 형체라곤 하나도 남지 않고, 신발 한 짝은 벗겨져 저만치 떨어진 포도(鋪道) 위에 나뒹구는 채로."]], [["When we got to our journey’s end, our first pursuit was to look about for a little lodging for Peggotty, where her brother could have a bed.", "여정의 끝에 다다랐을 때, 우리가 맨 먼저 한 일은 패거티가 묵을 작은 셋방을 알아보는 것이었는데, 그녀의 오라비가 잠잘 침대를 둘 만한 곳이라야 했다."], ["We were so fortunate as to find one, of a very clean and cheap description, over a chandler’s shop, only two streets removed from me.", "우리는 운 좋게도 그런 곳을 하나 찾아냈으니, 아주 깨끗하고 값싼 곳으로, 잡화점 위층이었고, 내 집에서 겨우 두 골목 떨어진 데였다."], ["When we had engaged this domicile, I bought some cold meat at an eating-house, and took my fellow-travellers home to tea; a proceeding, I regret to state, which did not meet with Mrs. Crupp’s approval, but quite the contrary.", "이 거처를 잡고 나서, 나는 음식점에서 차가운 고기를 좀 사다가, 내 길동무들을 집으로 데려와 차를 들었다. 유감스럽게도 이 일은 크럽 부인의 마음에 들지 않았는데, 그러기는커녕 정반대였다."], ["I ought to observe, however, in explanation of that lady’s state of mind, that she was much offended by Peggotty’s tucking up her widow’s gown before she had been ten minutes in the place, and setting to work to dust my bedroom. This Mrs.", "다만 그 부인의 심사를 설명하자면 이 점은 짚어 두어야겠다. 그녀는 패거티가 여기 온 지 십 분도 채 안 되어 과부의 치맛자락을 걷어붙이고 내 침실을 먼지 터는 일에 나선 것에 몹시 기분이 상했던 것이다."], ["Crupp regarded in the light of a liberty, and a liberty, she said, was a thing she never allowed.", "크럽 부인은 이를 주제넘은 짓으로 여겼고, 주제넘은 짓이란 자기가 결코 용납하지 않는 것이라고 말했다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty had made a communication to me on the way to London for which I was not unprepared.", "패거티 씨는 런던으로 오는 길에 나에게 한 가지를 일러 주었는데, 나로서는 마음의 준비가 안 된 것은 아니었다."], ["It was, that he purposed first seeing Mrs. Steerforth.", "그것은 그가 먼저 스티어포스 부인을 만나 볼 작정이라는 거였다."], ["As I felt bound to assist him in this, and also to mediate between them; with the view of sparing the mother’s feelings as much as possible, I wrote to her that night.", "나는 이 일에서 그를 거들고, 또 두 사람 사이를 중재할 의무가 있다고 느꼈으므로 — 그 어머니의 심정을 되도록 다치지 않게 하려는 생각에서 — 그날 밤 그녀에게 편지를 썼다."], ["I told her as mildly as I could what his wrong was, and what my own share in his injury.", "나는 되도록 부드럽게 그가 입은 부당한 일이 무엇이고, 그가 받은 상처에 내가 무슨 몫을 했는지를 말했다."], ["I said he was a man in very common life, but of a most gentle and upright character; and that I ventured to express a hope that she would not refuse to see him in his heavy trouble.", "나는 그가 아주 평범한 처지의 사람이지만, 더없이 온화하고 강직한 성품을 지녔다고 했다. 그리고 외람되나마, 그가 이 무거운 곤경에 처한 지금, 부인이 그를 만나기를 거절하지 않으시기를 바란다는 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["I mentioned two o’clock in the afternoon as the hour of our coming, and I sent the letter myself by the first coach in the morning.", "우리가 갈 시각으로 오후 두 시를 일러두고, 나는 아침 첫 마차 편으로 그 편지를 직접 부쳤다."]], [["At the appointed time, we stood at the door--the door of that house where I had been, a few days since, so happy: where my youthful confidence and warmth of heart had been yielded up so freely: which was closed against me henceforth: which was now a waste, a ruin.", "정해진 시각에, 우리는 그 문 앞에 섰다 — 며칠 전에 내가 그토록 행복했던 그 집의 문, 내 젊은 신뢰와 따뜻한 마음을 그토록 거리낌 없이 내어 주었던 곳, 이제부터는 나에게 닫혀 버린 곳, 이제는 황량한 폐허가 된 곳의 문이었다."]], [["No Littimer appeared.", "리티머는 나타나지 않았다."], ["The pleasanter face which had replaced his, on the occasion of my last visit, answered to our summons, and went before us to the drawing-room. Mrs. Steerforth was sitting there.", "지난번 내 방문 때 그를 대신했던 그 한결 상냥한 얼굴이 우리 부름에 응하여, 우리를 앞장서 응접실로 안내했다. 스티어포스 부인이 거기 앉아 있었다."], ["Rosa Dartle glided, as we went in, from another part of the room and stood behind her chair.", "우리가 들어서자 로자 다틀이 방의 다른 쪽에서 미끄러지듯 다가와 그녀의 의자 뒤에 섰다."]], [["I saw, directly, in his mother’s face, that she knew from himself what he had done.", "나는 그 어머니의 얼굴에서 곧바로, 그녀가 그가 한 일을 그의 입을 통해 알고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["It was very pale; and bore the traces of deeper emotion than my letter alone, weakened by the doubts her fondness would have raised upon it, would have been likely to create.", "그 얼굴은 몹시 창백했고, 내 편지 하나만으로는 — 그것도 그녀의 애정이 거기 일으켰을 의심에 힘이 빠진 그 편지만으로는 — 자아낼 법하지 않은 깊은 동요의 흔적이 어려 있었다."], ["I thought her more like him than ever I had thought her; and I felt, rather than saw, that the resemblance was not lost on my companion.", "나는 그녀가 그 어느 때보다도 그를 닮았다고 생각했다. 그리고 그 닮음이 내 길동무에게도 놓치지 않고 보였다는 것을, 나는 보았다기보다 느꼈다."]], [["She sat upright in her arm-chair, with a stately, immovable, passionless air, that it seemed as if nothing could disturb.", "그녀는 안락의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아, 어떤 것도 흔들 수 없을 듯한, 위엄 있고 미동도 없는 무감한 기색이었다."], ["She looked very steadfastly at Mr. Peggotty when he stood before her; and he looked quite as steadfastly at her.", "패거티 씨가 그녀 앞에 섰을 때 그녀는 그를 아주 또렷이 바라보았고, 그 또한 못지않게 또렷이 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Rosa Dartle’s keen glance comprehended all of us.", "로자 다틀의 날카로운 눈길은 우리 모두를 한눈에 담았다."], ["For some moments not a word was spoken.", "한동안 단 한마디도 오가지 않았다."]], [["She motioned to Mr. Peggotty to be seated.", "그녀는 패거티 씨에게 앉으라는 손짓을 했다."], ["He said, in a low voice, ‘I shouldn’t feel it nat’ral, ma’am, to sit down in this house.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이 집에서 앉는 건 제 마음에 영 자연스럽지가 않습니다, 마님."], ["I’d sooner stand.’ And this was succeeded by another silence, which she broke thus:", "차라리 서 있겠어요.\" 그러자 또 한 차례 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그녀가 그것을 이렇게 깨뜨렸다."]], [["‘I know, with deep regret, what has brought you here.", "\"무엇이 당신을 이리로 데려왔는지, 나는 깊은 유감 속에 알고 있어요."], ["What do you want of me?", "나에게서 무엇을 바라나요?"], ["What do you ask me to do?’", "나더러 무엇을 하라는 거예요?\""]], [["He put his hat under his arm, and feeling in his breast for Emily’s letter, took it out, unfolded it, and gave it to her.", "그는 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 가슴팍을 더듬어 에밀리의 편지를 찾아 꺼내, 그것을 펼쳐 그녀에게 건넸다."], ["‘Please to read that, ma’am.", "\"그걸 읽어 보세요, 마님."], ["That’s my niece’s hand!’", "그게 제 조카딸의 글씨예요!\""]], [["She read it, in the same stately and impassive way,--untouched by its contents, as far as I could see,--and returned it to him.", "그녀는 똑같이 위엄 있고 무덤덤한 태도로 그것을 읽었다 — 내가 보기에는, 그 내용에 조금도 마음 흔들리는 기색 없이 — 그러고는 그것을 그에게 돌려주었다."]], [["‘“Unless he brings me back a lady,”’ said Mr.", "\"'그이가 저를 숙녀로 만들어 데려오지 않는 한.'\""], ["Peggotty, tracing out that part with his finger.", "패거티 씨가 손가락으로 그 대목을 짚어 가며 말했다."], ["‘I come to know, ma’am, whether he will keep his wured?’", "\"저는 그걸 알아보려고 왔습니다, 마님, 그이가 자기 말을 지킬지를요.\""]], [["‘No,’ she returned.", "\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["‘Why not?’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"왜 안 되지요?\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘It is impossible. He would disgrace himself.", "\"불가능하니까요. 그 애는 제 체면을 망치게 될 거예요."], ["You cannot fail to know that she is far below him.’", "그 처녀가 그 애보다 한참 아래라는 걸 모르실 리 없겠지요.\""]], [["‘Raise her up!’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"그 애를 끌어올려 주세요!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘She is uneducated and ignorant.’", "\"그 처녀는 못 배웠고 무지해요.\""]], [["‘Maybe she’s not; maybe she is,’ said Mr.", "\"그럴 수도, 안 그럴 수도 있지요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty. ‘I think not, ma’am; but I’m no judge of them things.", "\"저는 안 그렇다고 봅니다, 마님. 하지만 저야 그런 걸 가릴 줄 모르지요."], ["Teach her better!’", "그 애를 가르쳐 주세요!\""]], [["‘Since you oblige me to speak more plainly, which I am very unwilling to do, her humble connexions would render such a thing impossible, if nothing else did.’", "\"당신이 나로 하여금 더 분명히 말하게 만드는군요, 나로서는 무척 내키지 않는 일인데. 다른 게 아무것도 걸리지 않는다 해도, 그 처녀의 미천한 집안 내력만으로도 그런 일은 불가능해요.\""]], [["‘Hark to this, ma’am,’ he returned, slowly and quietly.", "\"이 말 좀 들어 보세요, 마님.\" 그가 천천히, 그리고 조용히 받아 말했다."], ["‘You know what it is to love your child.", "\"자식을 사랑한다는 게 어떤 건지 마님은 아십니다."], ["So do I.", "저도 그래요."], ["If she was a hundred times my child, I couldn’t love her more.", "그 애가 백 배로 제 자식이라 해도, 지금보다 더 사랑할 순 없을 거예요."], ["You doen’t know what it is to lose your child. I do.", "마님은 자식을 잃는다는 게 어떤 건지 모르세요. 저는 알아요."], ["All the heaps of riches in the wureld would be nowt to me (if they was mine) to buy her back!", "세상의 온갖 재물을 산더미로 쌓아 놓는다 해도 (그게 제 거라 한들) 그 애를 되사 올 수 있다면 저한테는 아무것도 아닐 겁니다!"], ["But, save her from this disgrace, and she shall never be disgraced by us.", "하지만 그 애를 이 치욕에서 구해만 주신다면, 그 애는 결코 우리 때문에 욕을 보는 일이 없을 거예요."], ["Not one of us that she’s growed up among, not one of us that’s lived along with her and had her for their all in all, these many year, will ever look upon her pritty face again.", "그 애가 자라난 우리 가운데 단 한 사람도, 그 애와 함께 살며 이 여러 해 그 애를 전부로 여겨 온 우리 가운데 단 한 사람도, 다시는 그 애의 예쁜 얼굴을 보지 않을 거예요."], ["We’ll be content to let her be; we’ll be content to think of her, far off, as if she was underneath another sun and sky; we’ll be content to trust her to her husband,--to her little children, p’raps,--and bide the time when all of us shall be alike in quality afore our God!’", "우리는 그 애를 그냥 내버려 두는 데 만족하겠어요. 그 애를 멀리, 마치 다른 해와 다른 하늘 아래 있는 것처럼 여기는 데 만족하겠어요. 그 애를 그 애 남편한테 — 어쩌면 그 애의 어린 자식들한테 — 맡기고, 우리 모두가 하느님 앞에서 신분이 다 똑같아질 그때를 기다리는 데 만족하겠어요!\""]], [["The rugged eloquence with which he spoke, was not devoid of all effect.", "그가 말한 그 투박한 웅변은 아무런 효력이 없지는 않았다."], ["She still preserved her proud manner, but there was a touch of softness in her voice, as she answered:", "그녀는 여전히 그 거만한 태도를 지켰으나, 대답할 때 그 목소리에는 한 가닥 부드러움이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["‘I justify nothing. I make no counter-accusations.", "\"나는 아무것도 옳다 하지 않아요. 맞고소를 하지도 않고요."], ["But I am sorry to repeat, it is impossible.", "하지만 거듭 말하게 되어 유감스럽지만, 그건 불가능해요."], ["Such a marriage would irretrievably blight my son’s career, and ruin his prospects.", "그런 결혼은 내 아들의 앞길을 돌이킬 수 없이 망치고, 그 애의 장래를 무너뜨릴 거예요."], ["Nothing is more certain than that it never can take place, and never will.", "그런 일이 결코 일어날 수 없고, 결코 일어나지 않으리라는 것보다 더 확실한 건 없어요."], ["If there is any other compensation--’", "만일 다른 어떤 보상이라도—\""]], [["‘I am looking at the likeness of the face,’ interrupted Mr.", "\"저는 그 얼굴을 빼닮은 모습을 보고 있습니다.\""], ["Peggotty, with a steady but a kindling eye, ‘that has looked at me, in my home, at my fireside, in my boat--wheer not?---smiling and friendly, when it was so treacherous, that I go half wild when I think of it.", "패거티 씨가 흔들림 없으나 점점 타오르는 눈으로 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"제 집에서, 제 난롯가에서, 제 배에서 — 어딘들 안 그랬겠어요 — 저를 바라보던 그 얼굴 말입니다. 그토록 음흉했으면서도 웃으며 다정하던 그 얼굴, 떠올리기만 해도 제가 반쯤 미쳐 버리는 그 얼굴이요."], ["If the likeness of that face don’t turn to burning fire, at the thought of offering money to me for my child’s blight and ruin, it’s as bad. I doen’t know, being a lady’s, but what it’s worse.’", "그 빼닮은 얼굴이, 제 자식을 망치고 무너뜨린 값으로 저한테 돈을 내미는 그 생각에 불길로 타오르지 않는다면, 그건 그것대로 못된 거예요. 잘은 모르겠습니다만, 귀부인의 얼굴이고 보니, 그게 차라리 더 못된 건지도 모르지요.\""]], [["She changed now, in a moment.", "그녀는 이제 한순간에 달라졌다."], ["An angry flush overspread her features; and she said, in an intolerant manner, grasping the arm-chair tightly with her hands:", "노여운 홍조가 그녀의 이목구비를 뒤덮었다. 그리고 그녀는 두 손으로 안락의자를 꽉 움켜쥔 채, 못 참겠다는 투로 말했다."]], [["‘What compensation can you make to ME for opening such a pit between me and my son?", "\"나와 내 아들 사이에 그런 구렁을 벌려 놓은 일에 대해, 당신이 나한테 무슨 보상을 할 수 있다는 거죠?"], ["What is your love to mine?", "당신의 사랑이 내 사랑에 견줄 게 무엇이에요?"], ["What is your separation to ours?’", "당신의 이별이 우리의 이별에 견줄 게 무엇이냐고요?\""]], [["Miss Dartle softly touched her, and bent down her head to whisper, but she would not hear a word.", "다틀 양이 그녀를 살며시 건드리며 무어라 귀엣말을 하려고 고개를 숙였으나, 그녀는 단 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다."]], [["‘No, Rosa, not a word!", "\"안 돼, 로자, 한마디도!"], ["Let the man listen to what I say!", "이 사람더러 내가 하는 말을 듣게 해!"], ["My son, who has been the object of my life, to whom its every thought has been devoted, whom I have gratified from a child in every wish, from whom I have had no separate existence since his birth,--to take up in a moment with a miserable girl, and avoid me!", "내 아들, 내 삶의 목표였던 그 애, 내 모든 생각을 다 바쳐 온 그 애, 어릴 적부터 무슨 소원이든 다 이루어 주었던 그 애, 태어난 이래로 나와 따로 떨어진 존재라곤 없던 그 애가 — 한순간에 어느 비참한 계집애한테 빠져서 나를 피하다니!"], ["To repay my confidence with systematic deception, for her sake, and quit me for her!", "그 애를 위해 빈틈없이 속임수를 써서 내 신뢰를 갚고, 그 애 때문에 나를 버리다니!"], ["To set this wretched fancy, against his mother’s claims upon his duty, love, respect, gratitude--claims that every day and hour of his life should have strengthened into ties that nothing could be proof against! Is this no injury?’", "이 한심한 변덕을, 의무며 사랑이며 존경이며 감사에 대한 어미의 권리에 — 그 애 삶의 하루하루, 한시 한시가 어떤 것도 깨뜨릴 수 없는 끈으로 더 단단히 다져 왔어야 마땅한 그 권리에 맞세우다니! 이게 상처가 아니란 말이에요?\""]], [["Again Rosa Dartle tried to soothe her; again ineffectually.", "다시 로자 다틀이 그녀를 달래려 했으나, 다시 헛일이었다."]], [["‘I say, Rosa, not a word!", "\"내 말했지, 로자, 한마디도!"], ["If he can stake his all upon the lightest object, I can stake my all upon a greater purpose.", "그 애가 더없이 하찮은 것에 제 전부를 걸 수 있다면, 나는 더 큰 목적에 내 전부를 걸 수 있어."], ["Let him go where he will, with the means that my love has secured to him! Does he think to reduce me by long absence?", "내 사랑이 그 애한테 마련해 준 그 수단을 가지고 어디로든 가라지! 그 애가 오래 떨어져 있으면 나를 꺾을 수 있다고 생각하는 건가?"], ["He knows his mother very little if he does.", "그렇다면 제 어미를 너무 모르는 거지."], ["Let him put away his whim now, and he is welcome back.", "지금 그 변덕을 치워 버린다면, 나는 그 애를 기꺼이 다시 맞을 거야."], ["Let him not put her away now, and he never shall come near me, living or dying, while I can raise my hand to make a sign against it, unless, being rid of her for ever, he comes humbly to me and begs for my forgiveness.", "지금 그 계집을 치워 버리지 않는다면, 살아서든 죽어서든, 내가 손을 들어 거부의 표시를 할 수 있는 한, 그 애는 결코 내 곁에 올 수 없어 — 그 계집을 영영 떼어 내고 겸손히 내게 와서 용서를 빌지 않는 한은."], ["This is my right.", "이게 내 권리야."], ["This is the acknowledgement I WILL HAVE.", "이게 내가 반드시 받아 낼 인정이고."], ["This is the separation that there is between us!", "이게 우리 사이에 있는 이별이야! 그래, 이게,\""], ["And is this,’ she added, looking at her visitor with the proud intolerant air with which she had begun, ‘no injury?’", "그녀가 처음에 그랬던 그 거만하고 못 참겠다는 기색으로 손님을 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"상처가 아니란 말이에요?\""]], [["While I heard and saw the mother as she said these words, I seemed to hear and see the son, defying them.", "그 어머니가 이 말들을 내뱉는 것을 듣고 보는 동안, 나는 그 말들에 맞서던 그 아들을 듣고 보는 것만 같았다."], ["All that I had ever seen in him of an unyielding, wilful spirit, I saw in her.", "내가 그에게서 본 적 있는 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 고집스러운 기질을, 나는 죄다 그녀에게서 보았다."], ["All the understanding that I had now of his misdirected energy, became an understanding of her character too, and a perception that it was, in its strongest springs, the same.", "그릇된 데로 쏟아부어진 그의 정력을 이제껏 내가 이해한 그 모든 것이, 그녀의 성격에 대한 이해이기도 하다는 것을, 그리고 그것이 가장 깊은 근원에서는 똑같은 것임을, 나는 깨달았다."]], [["She now observed to me, aloud, resuming her former restraint, that it was useless to hear more, or to say more, and that she begged to put an end to the interview.", "그녀는 이제, 다시 아까의 절제된 태도로 돌아가, 더 듣거나 더 말해 봐야 소용없으니 이 면담을 끝내고 싶다고 나에게 소리 내어 말했다."], ["She rose with an air of dignity to leave the room, when Mr. Peggotty signified that it was needless.", "그녀가 위엄 있는 태도로 방을 나서려고 몸을 일으키는데, 패거티 씨가 그럴 필요 없다는 뜻을 비쳤다."]], [["‘Doen’t fear me being any hindrance to you, I have no more to say, ma’am,’ he remarked, as he moved towards the door.", "\"제가 마님께 무슨 방해가 될까 봐 걱정하실 거 없습니다, 저는 더 할 말이 없으니까요, 마님.\" 그가 문 쪽으로 걸음을 옮기며 말했다."], ["‘I come heer with no hope, and I take away no hope.", "\"저는 아무 희망도 없이 여기 왔고, 아무 희망도 없이 갑니다."], ["I have done what I thowt should be done, but I never looked fur any good to come of my stan’ning where I do.", "저는 마땅히 해야겠다 싶은 걸 했을 뿐, 제가 선 이 자리에서 무슨 좋은 게 나오리라고는 결코 바라지 않았어요."], ["This has been too evil a house fur me and mine, fur me to be in my right senses and expect it.’", "여긴 저와 제 식구한테 너무도 사악한 집이라서요, 제정신으로 그런 걸 바랄 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["With this, we departed; leaving her standing by her elbow-chair, a picture of a noble presence and a handsome face.", "이 말과 함께, 우리는 떠났다. 그녀를, 고귀한 풍채와 아름다운 얼굴을 한 한 폭의 그림처럼 자기 안락의자 곁에 선 채로 남겨 두고서."]], [["We had, on our way out, to cross a paved hall, with glass sides and roof, over which a vine was trained. Its leaves and shoots were green then, and the day being sunny, a pair of glass doors leading to the garden were thrown open.", "나가는 길에 우리는 유리 벽과 유리 지붕으로 된, 포도(鋪道)가 깔린 복도를 가로질러야 했는데, 그 위로는 포도덩굴이 뻗어 있었다. 그 잎과 새순은 그때 푸르렀고, 날이 화창한 까닭에 정원으로 통하는 유리문 한 쌍이 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["Rosa Dartle, entering this way with a noiseless step, when we were close to them, addressed herself to me:", "우리가 그 문에 바짝 다가섰을 때, 로자 다틀이 소리 없는 걸음으로 이쪽으로 들어와, 나에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘You do well,’ she said, ‘indeed, to bring this fellow here!’", "\"잘하셨네요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"정말이지, 이런 작자를 여기 데려오다니!\""]], [["Such a concentration of rage and scorn as darkened her face, and flashed in her jet-black eyes, I could not have thought compressible even into that face.", "분노와 경멸이 어찌나 응축되어 그녀의 얼굴을 어둡게 하고 칠흑 같은 두 눈에서 번득이는지, 나는 그 얼굴에 그토록 많은 것이 압축될 수 있으리라고는 생각조차 못 했을 것이다."], ["The scar made by the hammer was, as usual in this excited state of her features, strongly marked.", "망치에 맞아 생긴 그 흉터는, 그녀의 이목구비가 이렇게 격앙된 상태에서 늘 그렇듯, 도드라지게 드러나 있었다."], ["When the throbbing I had seen before, came into it as I looked at her, she absolutely lifted up her hand, and struck it.", "내가 그녀를 바라보는데 전에 본 적 있는 그 욱신거림이 거기 떠오르자, 그녀는 영락없이 손을 쳐들어 그것을 후려쳤다."]], [["‘This is a fellow,’ she said, ‘to champion and bring here, is he not?", "\"이런 작자를 두둔하고 여기로 데려오다니, 안 그래요?\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["You are a true man!’", "\"당신 참 의리 있는 사람이군요!\""]], [["‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘you are surely not so unjust as to condemn ME!’", "\"다틀 양.\" 내가 받아 말했다. \"설마 저를 탓할 만큼 불공평하시진 않겠지요!\""]], [["‘Why do you bring division between these two mad creatures?’ she returned.", "\"왜 이 미쳐 날뛰는 두 인간 사이에 분란을 끌어들이는 거죠?\""], ["‘Don’t you know that they are both mad with their own self-will and pride?’", "그녀가 되받았다. \"저 둘이 다 제 고집과 자존심으로 미쳐 있는 걸 모르세요?\""]], [["‘Is it my doing?’ I returned.", "\"이게 제가 한 일이라고요?\" 내가 받아 말했다."]], [["‘Is it your doing!’ she retorted.", "\"당신이 한 일이냐고요!\" 그녀가 쏘아붙였다."], ["‘Why do you bring this man here?’", "\"그럼 왜 이 작자를 여기 데려오는 건데요?\""]], [["‘He is a deeply-injured man, Miss Dartle,’ I replied.", "\"이분은 깊이 상처 입은 사람입니다, 다틀 양.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘You may not know it.’", "\"당신은 모르실지 몰라도요.\""]], [["‘I know that James Steerforth,’ she said, with her hand on her bosom, as if to prevent the storm that was raging there, from being loud, ‘has a false, corrupt heart, and is a traitor.", "\"제임스 스티어포스가,\" 그녀가 가슴속에서 휘몰아치는 폭풍이 소리 내지 못하게 막으려는 듯 가슴에 손을 얹고 말했다. \"거짓되고 썩어 빠진 마음을 지닌 배신자라는 건 나도 알아요."], ["But what need I know or care about this fellow, and his common niece?’", "하지만 이 작자랑 이 작자의 천한 조카딸 따위를 내가 알 까닭이, 신경 쓸 까닭이 뭐가 있겠어요?\""]], [["‘Miss Dartle,’ I returned, ‘you deepen the injury.", "\"다틀 양.\" 내가 받아 말했다. \"당신은 상처를 더 깊게 하고 있어요."], ["It is sufficient already. I will only say, at parting, that you do him a great wrong.’", "이미 충분합니다. 떠나면서 이 말만은 하겠어요, 당신은 이분에게 큰 잘못을 저지르고 있다고요.\""]], [["‘I do him no wrong,’ she returned.", "\"나는 이 작자한테 아무 잘못도 안 해요.\""], ["‘They are a depraved, worthless set.", "그녀가 대꾸했다. \"저들은 타락하고 쓸모없는 부류예요."], ["I would have her whipped!’", "나라면 저 계집을 채찍질했을 거라고요!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty passed on, without a word, and went out at the door.", "패거티 씨는 한마디도 없이 지나쳐 문밖으로 나갔다."]], [["‘Oh, shame, Miss Dartle!", "\"아, 부끄러운 줄 아세요, 다틀 양!"], ["shame!’ I said indignantly.", "부끄러운 줄 아세요!\" 내가 분개하며 말했다."], ["‘How can you bear to trample on his undeserved affliction!’", "\"어떻게 까닭 없이 고통받는 이분을 짓밟고도 견딜 수가 있어요!\""]], [["‘I would trample on them all,’ she answered.", "\"나라면 저들을 죄다 짓밟아 줬을 거예요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘I would have his house pulled down.", "\"나라면 그 집을 헐어 버렸을 거라고요."], ["I would have her branded on the face, dressed in rags, and cast out in the streets to starve.", "나라면 그 계집의 얼굴에 낙인을 찍고, 누더기를 입혀, 거리로 내쫓아 굶겨 죽였을 거예요."], ["If I had the power to sit in judgement on her, I would see it done.", "내게 그 계집을 심판할 권한이 있다면, 나는 기어이 그렇게 되는 걸 봤을 거예요."], ["See it done?", "보기만 하겠어요?"], ["I would do it! I detest her.", "내 손으로 했을 거라고요!"], ["If I ever could reproach her with her infamous condition, I would go anywhere to do so. If I could hunt her to her grave, I would.", "나는 그 계집이 진저리 나게 싫어요. 그 계집의 그 수치스러운 처지를 두고 한번 나무랄 수만 있다면, 나는 그러러 어디든 갈 거예요."], ["If there was any word of comfort that would be a solace to her in her dying hour, and only I possessed it, I wouldn’t part with it for Life itself.’", "그 계집을 무덤까지 쫓을 수 있다면, 나는 그러겠어요. 죽어 가는 순간에 그 계집에게 위안이 될 말 한마디가 있고 그걸 오직 나만 가졌다면, 나는 목숨을 준다 해도 그걸 내놓지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["The mere vehemence of her words can convey, I am sensible, but a weak impression of the passion by which she was possessed, and which made itself articulate in her whole figure, though her voice, instead of being raised, was lower than usual.", "그 말의 격렬함만으로는, 나도 잘 알거니와, 그녀를 사로잡은 그 격정을 그저 희미하게나마 전할 수 있을 뿐이다. 비록 그 목소리는 높아지기는커녕 평소보다 낮았으나, 그 격정은 그녀의 온몸을 통해 또렷이 드러나고 있었다."], ["No description I could give of her would do justice to my recollection of her, or to her entire deliverance of herself to her anger.", "내가 그녀를 어떻게 묘사한들 그녀에 대한 내 기억에도, 노여움에 자기 자신을 송두리째 내맡긴 그 모습에도 온당히 값하지 못할 것이다."], ["I have seen passion in many forms, but I have never seen it in such a form as that.", "나는 여러 형태의 격정을 보아 왔으나, 그런 형태의 격정은 단 한 번도 본 적이 없다."]], [["When I joined Mr. Peggotty, he was walking slowly and thoughtfully down the hill.", "내가 패거티 씨와 다시 합류했을 때, 그는 언덕을 천천히, 생각에 잠겨 내려가고 있었다."], ["He told me, as soon as I came up with him, that having now discharged his mind of what he had purposed doing in London, he meant ‘to set out on his travels’, that night.", "그는 내가 따라붙자마자, 런던에서 하려고 마음먹었던 일을 이제 다 덜어 냈으니, 그날 밤 '길을 떠날' 작정이라고 나에게 말했다. 나는 어디로 갈 생각이냐고 물었다."], ["I asked him where he meant to go?", "그는 그저 이렇게만 대답했다."], ["He only answered, ‘I’m a going, sir, to seek my niece.’", "\"저는요, 도련님, 제 조카딸을 찾으러 갑니다.\""]], [["We went back to the little lodging over the chandler’s shop, and there I found an opportunity of repeating to Peggotty what he had said to me.", "우리는 잡화점 위층 그 작은 셋방으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 나는 그가 나에게 한 말을 패거티에게 그대로 전할 기회를 얻었다."], ["She informed me, in return, that he had said the same to her that morning.", "그러자 그녀는, 그가 그날 아침 자기에게도 똑같이 말했다고 일러 주었다."], ["She knew no more than I did, where he was going, but she thought he had some project shaped out in his mind.", "그녀도 그가 어디로 가는지는 나만큼이나 알지 못했으나, 그가 마음속에 무언가 꾸려 놓은 계획이 있는 것 같다고 했다."]], [["I did not like to leave him, under such circumstances, and we all three dined together off a beefsteak pie--which was one of the many good things for which Peggotty was famous--and which was curiously flavoured on this occasion, I recollect well, by a miscellaneous taste of tea, coffee, butter, bacon, cheese, new loaves, firewood, candles, and walnut ketchup, continually ascending from the shop.", "나는 이런 처지의 그를 두고 떠나기가 내키지 않았다. 그래서 우리 셋은 비프스테이크 파이를 놓고 함께 저녁을 들었는데 — 그것은 패거티가 솜씨 좋기로 이름난 여러 음식 가운데 하나였다 — 이번만큼은, 내 또렷이 기억하건대, 가게에서 끊임없이 피어오르는 차며 커피며 버터며 베이컨이며 치즈며 갓 구운 빵이며 장작이며 양초며 호두 케첩의 잡다한 냄새가 묘하게 그 맛에 배어들어 있었다."], ["After dinner we sat for an hour or so near the window, without talking much; and then Mr.", "저녁을 먹고 나서 우리는 한 시간 남짓 창가에 앉아, 별말 없이 있었다."], ["Peggotty got up, and brought his oilskin bag and his stout stick, and laid them on the table.", "그러고는 패거티 씨가 일어나, 자기 방수포 가방과 튼튼한 지팡이를 가져와 탁자 위에 놓았다."]], [["He accepted, from his sister’s stock of ready money, a small sum on account of his legacy; barely enough, I should have thought, to keep him for a month.", "그는 누이가 가진 현금에서 제 유산의 일부로 적은 액수를 받았는데, 내 생각으로는 한 달을 버티기에도 빠듯한 정도였다."], ["He promised to communicate with me, when anything befell him; and he slung his bag about him, took his hat and stick, and bade us both ‘Good-bye!’", "그는 무슨 일이 생기면 나에게 연락하겠노라 약속했다. 그러고는 가방을 어깨에 둘러메고, 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들고, 우리 둘에게 '잘 있게나!' 하고 작별을 고했다."]], [["‘All good attend you, dear old woman,’ he said, embracing Peggotty, ‘and you too, Mas’r Davy!’ shaking hands with me.", "\"온갖 복이 당신을 따르기를, 우리 정다운 누이.\" 그가 패거티를 끌어안으며 말했다. \"도련님도요!\""], ["‘I’m a-going to seek her, fur and wide.", "나와 악수하며 말했다. \"저는 그 애를 찾으러 갑니다, 멀리멀리요."], ["If she should come home while I’m away--but ah, that ain’t like to be!--or if I should bring her back, my meaning is, that she and me shall live and die where no one can’t reproach her.", "제가 떠나 있는 동안 그 애가 집으로 돌아온다면 — 하지만 아아, 그럴 것 같진 않지만! — 아니면 제가 그 애를 데려온다면, 제 뜻은요, 그 애와 제가 아무도 그 애를 나무랄 수 없는 곳에서 살다 죽자는 겁니다."], ["If any hurt should come to me, remember that the last words I left for her was, “My unchanged love is with my darling child, and I forgive her!”’", "혹여라도 저한테 무슨 변고가 생기거든, 제가 그 애한테 남긴 마지막 말이 이거였다는 걸 기억해 주세요. '내 변함없는 사랑은 내 사랑하는 아이와 함께 있으며, 나는 그 애를 용서한다!'\""]], [["He said this solemnly, bare-headed; then, putting on his hat, he went down the stairs, and away.", "그는 모자를 벗은 채 이 말을 엄숙하게 했다. 그러고는 모자를 쓰고, 계단을 내려가, 떠나갔다."], ["We followed to the door.", "우리는 문까지 뒤따라갔다."], ["It was a warm, dusty evening, just the time when, in the great main thoroughfare out of which that by-way turned, there was a temporary lull in the eternal tread of feet upon the pavement, and a strong red sunshine.", "따뜻하고 먼지 자욱한 저녁이었는데, 마침 그 샛길이 갈라져 나온 큰길에서 포도(鋪道) 위를 끊임없이 밟아 대던 발소리가 잠시 멎고, 붉은 햇살이 강하게 내리쬐던 그때였다."], ["He turned, alone, at the corner of our shady street, into a glow of light, in which we lost him.", "그는 홀로, 우리 그늘진 거리 모퉁이에서 돌아, 환한 빛무리 속으로 들어섰고, 우리는 그 안에서 그를 놓쳤다."]], [["Rarely did that hour of the evening come, rarely did I wake at night, rarely did I look up at the moon, or stars, or watch the falling rain, or hear the wind, but I thought of his solitary figure toiling on, poor pilgrim, and recalled the words:", "그 저녁 시각이 찾아올 때면, 밤에 잠이 깰 때면, 달이나 별을 올려다볼 때면, 흩날리는 빗줄기를 바라보거나 바람 소리를 들을 때면, 나는 어김없이 묵묵히 길을 가는 그 외로운 모습을, 그 가엾은 순례자를 떠올렸고, 그 말들을 되새기곤 했다."]], [["‘I’m a going to seek her, fur and wide.", "\"저는 그 애를 찾으러 갑니다, 멀리멀리요."], ["If any hurt should come to me, remember that the last words I left for her was, “My unchanged love is with my darling child, and I forgive her!”’", "혹여라도 저한테 무슨 변고가 생기거든, 제가 그 애한테 남긴 마지막 말이 이거였다는 걸 기억해 주세요. '내 변함없는 사랑은 내 사랑하는 아이와 함께 있으며, 나는 그 애를 용서한다!'\""]], [["# CHAPTER 33. BLISSFUL", "# CHAPTER 33. BLISSFUL"]], [["All this time, I had gone on loving Dora, harder than ever.", "그동안 나는 줄곧 도라를 사랑해 왔는데, 그 사랑은 어느 때보다도 더 격렬했다."], ["Her idea was my refuge in disappointment and distress, and made some amends to me, even for the loss of my friend.", "도라를 떠올리는 것이 실망과 괴로움 속에서 나의 피난처였고, 친구를 잃은 슬픔까지도 어느 정도 달래 주었다."], ["The more I pitied myself, or pitied others, the more I sought for consolation in the image of Dora.", "나 자신이 가엾게 느껴질수록, 또는 남들이 가엾게 느껴질수록, 나는 도라의 모습에서 위안을 더욱 구했다."], ["The greater the accumulation of deceit and trouble in the world, the brighter and the purer shone the star of Dora high above the world.", "세상에 거짓과 근심이 쌓일수록, 그 세상 높은 곳에서 도라라는 별은 더 밝고 더 맑게 빛났다."], ["I don’t think I had any definite idea where Dora came from, or in what degree she was related to a higher order of beings; but I am quite sure I should have scouted the notion of her being simply human, like any other young lady, with indignation and contempt.", "도라가 어디서 왔는지, 또 더 높은 차원의 존재들과 얼마나 가까운 사이인지 내가 무슨 분명한 생각을 품었던 것 같지는 않다. 하지만 도라가 그저 여느 아가씨와 다를 바 없는 한낱 인간일 뿐이라는 말을 누가 했다면, 나는 분개와 경멸로 그 생각을 단번에 내쳤을 것이 분명하다."]], [["If I may so express it, I was steeped in Dora.", "이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 나는 도라에 흠뻑 절어 있었다."], ["I was not merely over head and ears in love with her, but I was saturated through and through.", "그저 머리끝까지 사랑에 빠진 정도가 아니라, 속속들이 흠뻑 배어들어 있었다."], ["Enough love might have been wrung out of me, metaphorically speaking, to drown anybody in; and yet there would have remained enough within me, and all over me, to pervade my entire existence.", "비유로 말하자면, 나에게서 사랑을 짜내면 누구라도 빠뜨려 죽일 만큼 쏟아져 나왔을 것이고, 그러고도 내 안에는, 그리고 내 온몸에는, 나의 존재 전체를 채우고도 남을 만큼 사랑이 남아 있었을 것이다."]], [["The first thing I did, on my own account, when I came back, was to take a night-walk to Norwood, and, like the subject of a venerable riddle of my childhood, to go ‘round and round the house, without ever touching the house’, thinking about Dora.", "런던에 돌아와서 내 마음대로 한 첫 일은, 밤에 노우드까지 걸어가, 어린 시절 들었던 그 점잖은 수수께끼의 주인공처럼, '집을 한 번도 건드리지 않으면서 집 둘레를 빙빙 도는' 것이었다. 물론 도라를 생각하면서 말이다."], ["I believe the theme of this incomprehensible conundrum was the moon.", "이 알쏭달쏭한 수수께끼의 답은 달이었던 것 같다."], ["No matter what it was, I, the moon-struck slave of Dora, perambulated round and round the house and garden for two hours, looking through crevices in the palings, getting my chin by dint of violent exertion above the rusty nails on the top, blowing kisses at the lights in the windows, and romantically calling on the night, at intervals, to shield my Dora--I don’t exactly know what from, I suppose from fire.", "그게 무엇이었든 간에, 도라에게 홀린 노예인 나는, 두 시간 동안 집과 정원 둘레를 빙빙 돌며, 울타리 틈으로 안을 들여다보고, 안간힘을 써 가며 울타리 꼭대기의 녹슨 못 위로 턱을 간신히 올리고, 창문의 불빛에 입맞춤을 날리고, 이따금 낭만에 젖어 밤을 향해 내 도라를 지켜 달라고 외쳤다 — 무엇으로부터 지켜 달라는 건지는 나도 잘 모르겠는데, 아마 불이었지 싶다."], ["Perhaps from mice, to which she had a great objection.", "어쩌면 쥐였을지도 모른다. 도라가 쥐라면 끔찍이 싫어했으니까."]], [["My love was so much in my mind and it was so natural to me to confide in Peggotty, when I found her again by my side of an evening with the old set of industrial implements, busily making the tour of my wardrobe, that I imparted to her, in a sufficiently roundabout way, my great secret.", "내 사랑은 마음속에 가득했고, 어느 날 저녁 패거티가 옛날 그 바느질 도구 한 벌을 들고 다시 내 곁에 앉아 내 옷가지를 바삐 손보고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 패거티에게 속내를 털어놓는 것은 너무도 자연스러운 일이라, 나는 빙빙 둘러대는 식으로 충분히 에둘러서 나의 큰 비밀을 그녀에게 알렸다."], ["Peggotty was strongly interested, but I could not get her into my view of the case at all.", "패거티는 무척 관심을 보였으나, 도무지 나처럼 이 일을 바라보게 만들 수가 없었다."], ["She was audaciously prejudiced in my favour, and quite unable to understand why I should have any misgivings, or be low-spirited about it.", "패거티는 뻔뻔스러울 만큼 내 편으로 치우쳐 있어서, 내가 왜 무슨 불안을 느끼거나 이 일로 풀이 죽어 있어야 하는지 도통 이해하지 못했다."], ["‘The young lady might think herself well off,’ she observed, ‘to have such a beau.", "\"그 아가씨야말로 운이 좋다 여겨야지요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"이런 멋진 신랑감을 두다니요."], ["And as to her Pa,’ she said, ‘what did the gentleman expect, for gracious sake!’", "그리고 그 아가씨 아버지로 말하자면,\" 패거티가 말했다. \"아니, 도련님, 그 양반이 대체 뭘 더 바란답니까!\""]], [["I observed, however, that Mr.", "그렇지만 나는 알아챘다."], ["Spenlow’s proctorial gown and stiff cravat took Peggotty down a little, and inspired her with a greater reverence for the man who was gradually becoming more and more etherealized in my eyes every day, and about whom a reflected radiance seemed to me to beam when he sat erect in Court among his papers, like a little lighthouse in a sea of stationery.", "스펜로 씨의 법무사 가운과 빳빳한 크라바트가 패거티의 기를 조금 꺾어 놓았고, 날이 갈수록 내 눈에 점점 더 신성한 존재로 변해 가던 그 사람에 대한 더 큰 경외심을 패거티에게 불어넣었다는 것을. 그 사람이 법정에서 서류 더미 사이에 꼿꼿이 앉아 있을 때면, 문방구의 바다 위에 떠 있는 작은 등대처럼, 반사된 광채가 그를 둘러싸고 환히 빛나는 것만 같았다."], ["And by the by, it used to be uncommonly strange to me to consider, I remember, as I sat in Court too, how those dim old judges and doctors wouldn’t have cared for Dora, if they had known her; how they wouldn’t have gone out of their senses with rapture, if marriage with Dora had been proposed to them; how Dora might have sung, and played upon that glorified guitar, until she led me to the verge of madness, yet not have tempted one of those slow-goers an inch out of his road!", "그러고 보니, 나 역시 법정에 앉아 있을 때면, 저 침침하고 늙은 판사들과 박사들이, 도라를 알았더라면 도라 따위에는 눈길조차 주지 않았으리라는 것이 — 도라와 결혼하라는 제안을 받았더라도 황홀해서 넋이 나갈 일은 결코 없었으리라는 것이 — 도라가 노래를 부르고 그 거룩한 기타를 켜며 나를 미칠 지경까지 몰아간다 해도, 저 굼뜬 작자들 중 단 한 명도 제 길에서 한 치도 꾀어내지 못했으리라는 것이, 어찌나 유난히 이상하게 느껴졌던지 기억이 난다."]], [["I despised them, to a man. Frozen-out old gardeners in the flower-beds of the heart, I took a personal offence against them all.", "나는 그들을 한 사람도 빠짐없이 경멸했다. 마음이라는 꽃밭에서 얼어 죽은 늙은 정원사들이라 여기며, 나는 그들 모두에게 사사로운 반감을 품었다."], ["The Bench was nothing to me but an insensible blunderer.", "내게 판사석은 한낱 무감각한 얼간이에 지나지 않았다."], ["The Bar had no more tenderness or poetry in it, than the bar of a public-house.", "변호사석에는 선술집 카운터보다도 다정함이나 시정(詩情)이 없었다."]], [["Taking the management of Peggotty’s affairs into my own hands, with no little pride, I proved the will, and came to a settlement with the Legacy Duty-office, and took her to the Bank, and soon got everything into an orderly train.", "나는 적잖은 자부심을 품고 패거티의 일을 내 손으로 도맡아, 유언장을 검인받고, 상속세 사무소와 합의를 보고, 패거티를 은행에 데려가, 머지않아 모든 일을 순조로운 궤도에 올려놓았다."], ["We varied the legal character of these proceedings by going to see some perspiring Wax-work, in Fleet Street (melted, I should hope, these twenty years); and by visiting Miss Linwood’s Exhibition, which I remember as a Mausoleum of needlework, favourable to self-examination and repentance; and by inspecting the Tower of London; and going to the top of St. Paul’s.", "우리는 이런 법률 일의 딱딱함을, 플리트 거리에서 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리는 듯한 밀랍 인형 전시를 보러 가는 것으로 누그러뜨렸고 (그것들은 이 이십 년 사이에 녹아 없어졌기를 바란다), 린우드 양의 전시회를 찾아가는 것으로도 그러했는데, 나는 그곳을 자기반성과 회개에 알맞은 자수(刺繡)의 영묘로 기억한다. 또 런던탑을 둘러보고, 세인트폴 대성당 꼭대기까지 올라가는 것으로도 그렇게 했다."], ["All these wonders afforded Peggotty as much pleasure as she was able to enjoy, under existing circumstances: except, I think, St.", "이 온갖 진기한 구경거리는 지금 처지에서 패거티가 누릴 수 있는 만큼의 즐거움을 안겨 주었는데, 다만 세인트폴 대성당만은 예외였던 것 같다."], ["Paul’s, which, from her long attachment to her work-box, became a rival of the picture on the lid, and was, in some particulars, vanquished, she considered, by that work of art.", "패거티는 제 반짇고리를 오래도록 애지중지해 온 터라, 그 성당이 반짇고리 뚜껑에 그려진 그림과 경쟁 상대가 되고 말았고, 몇 가지 점에서는 그 예술 작품에 패배했다고 패거티는 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Peggotty’s business, which was what we used to call ‘common-form business’ in the Commons (and very light and lucrative the common-form business was), being settled, I took her down to the office one morning to pay her bill. Mr.", "우리가 민사법원에서 흔히 '정식 절차 사건'이라 부르던 패거티의 일이 (그 정식 절차 사건이라는 게 무척 수월하고 돈벌이가 짭짤했다) 마무리되자, 어느 날 아침 나는 패거티를 사무소로 데려가 비용을 치르게 했다."], ["Spenlow had stepped out, old Tiffey said, to get a gentleman sworn for a marriage licence; but as I knew he would be back directly, our place lying close to the Surrogate’s, and to the Vicar-General’s office too, I told Peggotty to wait.", "늙은 티피의 말로는, 스펜로 씨는 어떤 신사에게 혼인 허가증 선서를 받게 하려고 잠깐 자리를 비웠다고 했다. 하지만 우리 사무소가 대리 판사 사무실, 그리고 부주교 사무실과도 가까이 붙어 있어서, 그가 곧 돌아오리라는 것을 알았기에, 나는 패거티에게 기다리라고 일렀다."]], [["We were a little like undertakers, in the Commons, as regarded Probate transactions; generally making it a rule to look more or less cut up, when we had to deal with clients in mourning.", "유언 검인 거래에 관한 한, 우리 민사법원 사람들은 장의사와 좀 비슷한 데가 있었다. 상복을 입은 의뢰인을 상대할 때면 으레 어느 정도 침통한 표정을 짓는 것이 규칙이었다."], ["In a similar feeling of delicacy, we were always blithe and light-hearted with the licence clients.", "그와 비슷한 섬세한 마음씨에서, 허가증 의뢰인을 대할 때는 늘 명랑하고 들떠 있었다."], ["Therefore I hinted to Peggotty that she would find Mr. Spenlow much recovered from the shock of Mr.", "그래서 나는 패거티에게, 스펜로 씨가 바키스 씨의 별세에서 받은 충격을 한결 회복한 모습일 거라고 넌지시 일러 두었다."], ["Barkis’s decease; and indeed he came in like a bridegroom.", "과연 그는 신랑처럼 환하게 들어왔다."]], [["But neither Peggotty nor I had eyes for him, when we saw, in company with him, Mr.", "그러나 그와 함께 들어오는 머드스톤 씨를 보았을 때, 패거티도 나도 그에게는 눈길조차 줄 수 없었다."], ["Murdstone. He was very little changed.", "머드스톤 씨는 거의 변한 데가 없었다."], ["His hair looked as thick, and was certainly as black, as ever; and his glance was as little to be trusted as of old.", "머리숱은 여전히 빽빽해 보였고, 색깔도 분명 예전처럼 새까맸다. 그리고 그 눈초리는 옛날과 다름없이 도무지 믿을 만한 구석이 없었다."]], [["‘Ah, Copperfield?’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"아, 코퍼필드 군?\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You know this gentleman, I believe?’", "\"이분과는 아는 사이지 싶은데, 그렇지?\""]], [["I made my gentleman a distant bow, and Peggotty barely recognized him.", "나는 그 신사에게 데면데면하게 고개를 까딱했고, 패거티는 그를 알은체하는 둥 마는 둥 했다."], ["He was, at first, somewhat disconcerted to meet us two together; but quickly decided what to do, and came up to me.", "그는 처음에는 우리 둘이 함께 있는 것을 보고 다소 당황했으나, 이내 어떻게 할지 마음을 정하고 나에게 다가왔다."]], [["‘I hope,’ he said, ‘that you are doing well?’", "\"잘 지내기를 바라네.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘It can hardly be interesting to you,’ said I.", "\"당신이 관심 가질 만한 일은 아닐 텐데요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Yes, if you wish to know.’", "\"그래요, 굳이 알고 싶으시다면, 잘 지냅니다.\""]], [["We looked at each other, and he addressed himself to Peggotty.", "우리는 서로를 마주 보았고, 그는 패거티에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘And you,’ said he.", "\"그리고 자네는,\""], ["‘I am sorry to observe that you have lost your husband.’", "그가 말했다. \"남편을 잃었다니 안됐군.\""]], [["‘It’s not the first loss I have had in my life, Mr.", "\"제 평생 처음 겪는 상실은 아니에요, 머드스톤 씨.\""], ["Murdstone,’ replied Peggotty, trembling from head to foot. ‘I am glad to hope that there is nobody to blame for this one,--nobody to answer for it.’", "패거티가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 대꾸했다. \"이번 일에는 탓할 사람이 아무도 없다는 게, 책임질 사람이 아무도 없다는 게 다행이라 여겨 기쁠 따름이지요.\""]], [["‘Ha!’ said he; ‘that’s a comfortable reflection.", "\"하!\" 그가 말했다. \"마음 편한 생각이군."], ["You have done your duty?’", "자네는 도리를 다했고?\""]], [["‘I have not worn anybody’s life away,’ said Peggotty, ‘I am thankful to think!", "\"저는 그 누구의 목숨도 시들게 한 적이 없어요.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"그렇게 생각할 수 있으니 고맙지요!"], ["No, Mr. Murdstone, I have not worrited and frightened any sweet creetur to an early grave!’", "아무렴요, 머드스톤 씨, 저는 그 어떤 사랑스러운 사람도 들들 볶고 겁주어 일찍 무덤으로 보낸 적이 없답니다!\""]], [["He eyed her gloomily--remorsefully I thought--for an instant; and said, turning his head towards me, but looking at my feet instead of my face:", "그는 한순간 패거티를 음울하게 — 내 생각에는 뉘우치듯이 — 바라보았다. 그러고는 나를 향해 고개를 돌렸으되, 내 얼굴 대신 내 발을 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘We are not likely to encounter soon again;--a source of satisfaction to us both, no doubt, for such meetings as this can never be agreeable.", "\"우리가 가까운 시일에 다시 마주칠 일은 없겠지. 둘 다에게 다행한 일임은 의심할 여지가 없네. 이런 만남이 유쾌할 리 없으니 말이야."], ["I do not expect that you, who always rebelled against my just authority, exerted for your benefit and reformation, should owe me any good-will now.", "자네의 이익과 교화를 위해 행사한 나의 정당한 권위에 늘 반항하던 자네가, 이제 와서 나에게 무슨 호의를 품으리라고 기대하지는 않네."], ["There is an antipathy between us--’", "우리 사이에는 반감이 있으니까—\""]], [["‘An old one, I believe?’ said I, interrupting him.", "\"오래된 반감이지요?\" 내가 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다."]], [["He smiled, and shot as evil a glance at me as could come from his dark eyes.", "그가 미소를 지었고, 그 검은 눈에서 나올 수 있는 가장 사악한 눈초리를 나에게 쏘아붙였다."]], [["‘It rankled in your baby breast,’ he said.", "\"그건 자네의 어린 가슴속에서 곪았지.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘It embittered the life of your poor mother.", "\"그것이 자네의 가엾은 어머니의 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들었어."], ["You are right.", "자네 말이 맞네."], ["I hope you may do better, yet; I hope you may correct yourself.’", "그래도 자네가 더 나아지기를 바라네. 자네가 스스로를 바로잡기를 바라.\""]], [["Here he ended the dialogue, which had been carried on in a low voice, in a corner of the outer office, by passing into Mr. Spenlow’s room, and saying aloud, in his smoothest manner:", "여기서 그는 바깥 사무실 한구석에서 낮은 목소리로 주고받던 이 대화를 끝내고, 스펜로 씨의 방으로 들어가며, 더없이 매끄러운 말투로 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Gentlemen of Mr. Spenlow’s profession are accustomed to family differences, and know how complicated and difficult they always are!’ With that, he paid the money for his licence; and, receiving it neatly folded from Mr.", "\"스펜로 씨 같은 직업을 가진 분들은 집안의 불화에 이골이 나서, 그런 일이 늘 얼마나 복잡하고 까다로운지 잘 아시지요!\" 그렇게 말하고 그는 허가증 값을 치렀다."], ["Spenlow, together with a shake of the hand, and a polite wish for his happiness and the lady’s, went out of the office.", "그리고 스펜로 씨가 깔끔하게 접어 건네주는 허가증을, 악수와 더불어, 자기 자신과 그 부인의 행복을 비는 정중한 인사와 함께 받아 들고, 사무소를 나갔다."]], [["I might have had more difficulty in constraining myself to be silent under his words, if I had had less difficulty in impressing upon Peggotty (who was only angry on my account, good creature!) that we were not in a place for recrimination, and that I besought her to hold her peace.", "그의 말을 듣고도 잠자코 있도록 나 자신을 다잡는 데 더 애를 먹을 뻔했으나, 다행히 패거티를 (나를 위해서만 화를 내던 착한 사람!) 달래는 데 그만큼 애를 먹지 않았다. 여기는 서로 비난을 주고받을 자리가 아니라고, 부디 잠자코 있어 달라고 나는 패거티에게 통사정했다."], ["She was so unusually roused, that I was glad to compound for an affectionate hug, elicited by this revival in her mind of our old injuries, and to make the best I could of it, before Mr. Spenlow and the clerks.", "패거티가 어찌나 유난히 흥분했던지, 나는 우리가 옛날에 당했던 부당한 일들이 그녀의 마음속에 되살아나면서 터져 나온 다정한 포옹으로 무마하고, 스펜로 씨와 사무원들 앞에서 그것을 최대한 잘 넘어가게 한 것만으로도 다행이라 여겼다."]], [["Mr. Spenlow did not appear to know what the connexion between Mr.", "스펜로 씨는 머드스톤 씨와 나 사이가 어떤 관계인지 모르는 듯했다."], ["Murdstone and myself was; which I was glad of, for I could not bear to acknowledge him, even in my own breast, remembering what I did of the history of my poor mother. Mr.", "나로서는 그게 다행이었는데, 내 가엾은 어머니의 사연을 기억하는 처지에, 그를 친척으로 인정하는 것을 — 나 자신의 가슴속에서조차 — 견딜 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["Spenlow seemed to think, if he thought anything about the matter, that my aunt was the leader of the state party in our family, and that there was a rebel party commanded by somebody else--so I gathered at least from what he said, while we were waiting for Mr. Tiffey to make out Peggotty’s bill of costs.", "스펜로 씨는, 이 문제에 대해 무언가 생각한 게 있다면, 우리 집안에서 우리 고모님이 정부(政府) 측의 지도자이고, 다른 누군가가 이끄는 반란 측이 있다고 여기는 듯했다. 적어도 티피 씨가 패거티의 비용 청구서를 작성하기를 기다리는 동안 그가 한 말로 미루어 나는 그렇게 짐작했다."]], [["‘Miss Trotwood,’ he remarked, ‘is very firm, no doubt, and not likely to give way to opposition.", "\"트롯우드 양은,\" 그가 말했다. \"의심할 여지 없이 무척 단호하시고, 반대에 굴할 분이 아니지."], ["I have an admiration for her character, and I may congratulate you, Copperfield, on being on the right side.", "나는 그분의 성품을 흠모한다네. 그리고 코퍼필드 군, 자네가 옳은 편에 서 있는 것을 축하해도 되겠군."], ["Differences between relations are much to be deplored--but they are extremely general--and the great thing is, to be on the right side’: meaning, I take it, on the side of the moneyed interest.", "친척들 사이의 불화는 참으로 개탄할 일이지만, 그런 일은 지극히 흔하다네. 중요한 건 옳은 편에 서는 것이지.\" 내가 보기에 그 말은 돈줄을 쥔 편에 서라는 뜻이었다."]], [["‘Rather a good marriage this, I believe?’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"이번 혼인은 꽤 좋은 혼사라지, 아마?\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."]], [["I explained that I knew nothing about it.", "나는 그 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모른다고 설명했다."]], [["‘Indeed!’ he said.", "\"그래?\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Speaking from the few words Mr. Murdstone dropped--as a man frequently does on these occasions--and from what Miss Murdstone let fall, I should say it was rather a good marriage.’", "\"머드스톤 씨가 흘린 몇 마디로 미루어 — 사람들이 그런 자리에서 곧잘 그러듯이 말이야 — 그리고 머드스톤 양이 흘린 말로 미루어 보건대, 꽤 좋은 혼사라 하겠더군.\""]], [["‘Do you mean that there is money, sir?’ I asked.", "\"돈이 있다는 말씀이신가요, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다네.\""], ["Spenlow, ‘I understand there’s money.", "스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"내가 알기로는 돈이 있다지."], ["Beauty too, I am told.’", "게다가 미모까지 갖췄다더군.\""]], [["‘Indeed!", "\"그래요!"], ["Is his new wife young?’", "그 새 부인이 젊은가요?\""]], [["‘Just of age,’ said Mr.", "\"이제 막 성년이 됐다네.\""], ["Spenlow. ‘So lately, that I should think they had been waiting for that.’", "스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"어찌나 최근인지, 내 생각엔 그들이 그때까지 기다린 듯싶더군.\""]], [["‘Lord deliver her!’ said Peggotty.", "\"하느님께서 그 사람을 구해 주시길!\""], ["So very emphatically and unexpectedly, that we were all three discomposed; until Tiffey came in with the bill.", "패거티가 말했다. 어찌나 힘주어, 또 뜻밖에 내뱉었던지, 우리 셋 다 당황하고 말았는데, 티피가 청구서를 들고 들어올 때까지 그러했다."]], [["Old Tiffey soon appeared, however, and handed it to Mr. Spenlow, to look over. Mr.", "그러나 늙은 티피는 곧 나타나, 그것을 스펜로 씨에게 건네 살펴보게 했다."], ["Spenlow, settling his chin in his cravat and rubbing it softly, went over the items with a deprecatory air--as if it were all Jorkins’s doing--and handed it back to Tiffey with a bland sigh.", "스펜로 씨는 턱을 크라바트에 파묻고 그것을 부드럽게 문지르며, 마치 그게 죄다 조킨스가 한 짓이라는 듯 못마땅한 기색으로 항목들을 훑어 내려가더니, 부드러운 한숨과 함께 티피에게 도로 건넸다."]], [["‘Yes,’ he said. ‘That’s right.", "\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"맞네."], ["Quite right.", "아주 잘됐어."], ["I should have been extremely happy, Copperfield, to have limited these charges to the actual expenditure out of pocket, but it is an irksome incident in my professional life, that I am not at liberty to consult my own wishes.", "코퍼필드 군, 나는 이 비용을 실제로 내 주머니에서 나간 지출에만 한정할 수 있었더라면 더없이 기뻤겠네만, 내 직업 생활에서 영 성가신 점이 하나 있으니, 내 뜻대로 할 자유가 없다는 거야."], ["I have a partner--Mr. Jorkins.’", "나에게는 동업자가 있거든. 조킨스 씨라고.\""]], [["As he said this with a gentle melancholy, which was the next thing to making no charge at all, I expressed my acknowledgements on Peggotty’s behalf, and paid Tiffey in banknotes.", "그가 이 말을, 아예 한 푼도 청구하지 않는 것이나 다름없는 부드러운 우수에 잠겨 하기에, 나는 패거티를 대신해 고마움을 표하고, 티피에게 지폐로 값을 치렀다."], ["Peggotty then retired to her lodging, and Mr. Spenlow and I went into Court, where we had a divorce-suit coming on, under an ingenious little statute (repealed now, I believe, but in virtue of which I have seen several marriages annulled), of which the merits were these.", "그러고 나서 패거티는 숙소로 물러갔고, 스펜로 씨와 나는 법정으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서 우리는 어떤 기발한 소법령(지금은 폐지되었지 싶으나, 이 법령에 따라 여러 건의 혼인이 무효가 되는 것을 나는 보았다)에 근거한 이혼 소송 한 건을 맡고 있었고, 사건의 요지는 이러했다."], ["The husband, whose name was Thomas Benjamin, had taken out his marriage licence as Thomas only; suppressing the Benjamin, in case he should not find himself as comfortable as he expected.", "토머스 벤저민이라는 이름의 남편이, 혹시 자기가 기대만큼 편치 못할 경우에 대비해 벤저민을 빼고 그저 토머스라는 이름으로만 혼인 허가증을 받았던 것이다."], ["NOT finding himself as comfortable as he expected, or being a little fatigued with his wife, poor fellow, he now came forward, by a friend, after being married a year or two, and declared that his name was Thomas Benjamin, and therefore he was not married at all.", "그러다 기대만큼 편하지 않다는 것을 알았는지, 아니면 가엾게도 아내에게 조금 싫증이 났는지, 그는 결혼한 지 일이 년이 지나, 친구를 내세워 나서서, 자기 이름은 토머스 벤저민이므로 애초에 결혼한 적이 없다고 선언했다."], ["Which the Court confirmed, to his great satisfaction.", "법원은 이를 인정해 주었고, 그는 무척 흡족해했다."]], [["I must say that I had my doubts about the strict justice of this, and was not even frightened out of them by the bushel of wheat which reconciles all anomalies.", "이것이 엄밀한 정의에 들어맞는지 나로서는 미심쩍었다는 것을 밝혀 두어야겠는데, 온갖 변칙을 다 무마해 준다는 밀 한 부셸을 들이대도 그 의심을 떨치지 못했다."], ["But Mr. Spenlow argued the matter with me.", "그러나 스펜로 씨는 이 문제를 두고 나와 논쟁을 벌였다."], ["He said, Look at the world, there was good and evil in that; look at the ecclesiastical law, there was good and evil in THAT. It was all part of a system.", "그가 말하기를, 세상을 보라, 거기에 선과 악이 있다, 교회법을 보라, 거기에도 선과 악이 있다는 것이었다. 그것은 모두 하나의 체계의 일부라고 했다."], ["Very good.", "아주 훌륭하다."], ["There you were!", "그러니 어쩌겠는가!"]], [["I had not the hardihood to suggest to Dora’s father that possibly we might even improve the world a little, if we got up early in the morning, and took off our coats to the work; but I confessed that I thought we might improve the Commons. Mr.", "나는 도라의 아버지에게, 우리가 아침 일찍 일어나 웃옷을 벗어부치고 일에 달려든다면 어쩌면 세상을 조금 더 낫게 만들 수도 있지 않겠느냐고 감히 말할 배짱은 없었다. 다만 민사법원만은 우리가 개선할 수 있을 것 같다고 털어놓았다."], ["Spenlow replied that he would particularly advise me to dismiss that idea from my mind, as not being worthy of my gentlemanly character; but that he would be glad to hear from me of what improvement I thought the Commons susceptible?", "스펜로 씨는 그런 생각은 내 신사다운 품성에 어울리지 않으니 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버리라고 각별히 충고하고 싶다고 대꾸했다. 그러면서도 민사법원의 어떤 점을 개선할 수 있다고 보는지 내 이야기를 기꺼이 들어 주겠다고 했다."]], [["Taking that part of the Commons which happened to be nearest to us--for our man was unmarried by this time, and we were out of Court, and strolling past the Prerogative Office--I submitted that I thought the Prerogative Office rather a queerly managed institution. Mr. Spenlow inquired in what respect?", "마침 우리에게서 제일 가까운 부분의 민사법원을 들어 — 그 무렵엔 그 사내의 혼인이 무효로 처리된 뒤라, 우리는 법정을 나와 유언 검인 사무소를 지나며 거닐고 있었다 — 나는 그 검인 사무소가 운영이 좀 괴상한 기관 같다고 말했다. 스펜로 씨가 어떤 점에서 그러냐고 물었다."], ["I replied, with all due deference to his experience (but with more deference, I am afraid, to his being Dora’s father), that perhaps it was a little nonsensical that the Registry of that Court, containing the original wills of all persons leaving effects within the immense province of Canterbury, for three whole centuries, should be an accidental building, never designed for the purpose, leased by the registrars for their Own private emolument, unsafe, not even ascertained to be fire-proof, choked with the important documents it held, and positively, from the roof to the basement, a mercenary speculation of the registrars, who took great fees from the public, and crammed the public’s wills away anyhow and anywhere, having no other object than to get rid of them cheaply.", "나는 그의 경험에 마땅한 경의를 표하면서 (다만 송구스럽게도, 그가 도라의 아버지라는 점에 더 큰 경의를 표하면서) 대답하기를, 어쩌면 이런 점이 좀 어처구니없는 것 같다고 했다. 그 법원의 등기소는 광대한 캔터베리 관구 안에서 유산을 남긴 모든 사람의 유언장 원본을 무려 삼백 년 동안 보관하고 있는데, 그 건물이라는 게 애초에 그 용도로 설계된 적도 없이 우연히 떠맡은 건물이고, 등기관들이 제 사사로운 잇속을 위해 빌린 것이며, 안전하지도 않고, 화재에 견딘다는 보장조차 없으며, 거기 보관된 중요한 문서들로 발 디딜 틈 없이 빼곡한 데다, 지붕에서 지하실까지 그야말로 등기관들의 돈벌이 투기판이라는 점 말이다. 그들은 대중에게서 막대한 수수료를 챙기면서, 대중의 유언장을 닥치는 대로 아무 데나 쑤셔 넣어, 그저 값싸게 처치해 버리는 것 말고는 다른 목적이 없었다."], ["That, perhaps, it was a little unreasonable that these registrars in the receipt of profits amounting to eight or nine thousand pounds a year (to say nothing of the profits of the deputy registrars, and clerks of seats), should not be obliged to spend a little of that money, in finding a reasonably safe place for the important documents which all classes of people were compelled to hand over to them, whether they would or no.", "또 어쩌면 이런 점도 좀 부당한 것 같다고 했다. 연 수입이 팔구천 파운드에 이르는 (부등기관과 좌석 서기들의 수입은 말할 것도 없고) 이 등기관들이, 모든 계층의 사람들이 좋든 싫든 어쩔 수 없이 넘겨야만 하는 그 중요한 문서들을 위해 그럭저럭 안전한 장소 하나 마련하는 데 그 돈을 조금이라도 쓰도록 의무 지워지지 않는다는 점 말이다. 또 어쩌면 이런 점도 좀 불공평한 것 같다고 했다."], ["That, perhaps, it was a little unjust, that all the great offices in this great office should be magnificent sinecures, while the unfortunate working-clerks in the cold dark room upstairs were the worst rewarded, and the least considered men, doing important services, in London.", "이 거대한 관청 안의 큰 자리들은 죄다 거창한 한직(閑職)인데, 위층의 춥고 어두운 방에서 일하는 가엾은 실무 서기들은 런던에서 가장 푸대접받고 가장 무시당하면서도 중요한 일을 떠맡고 있다는 점 말이다. 또 어쩌면 이런 점도 좀 꼴사나운 것 같다고 했다."], ["That perhaps it was a little indecent that the principal registrar of all, whose duty it was to find the public, constantly resorting to this place, all needful accommodation, should be an enormous sinecurist in virtue of that post (and might be, besides, a clergyman, a pluralist, the holder of a staff in a cathedral, and what not),--while the public was put to the inconvenience of which we had a specimen every afternoon when the office was busy, and which we knew to be quite monstrous.", "이곳을 끊임없이 드나드는 대중에게 필요한 온갖 편의를 제공하는 것이 본분인 수석 등기관이, 그 직책 덕분에 어마어마한 한직을 차지하고 있고 (게다가 성직자에다, 여러 직책을 겸한 사람이며, 대성당의 한 자리를 꿰찬 자에다, 그 밖에도 온갖 직함을 거머쥐었을 수도 있는데), 그러는 동안 대중은 불편을 겪어야만 하는데, 그 본보기를 우리는 사무소가 분주한 오후마다 보았고, 그것이 그야말로 끔찍하다는 것을 우리는 알고 있었다는 점 말이다."], ["That, perhaps, in short, this Prerogative Office of the diocese of Canterbury was altogether such a pestilent job, and such a pernicious absurdity, that but for its being squeezed away in a corner of St. Paul’s Churchyard, which few people knew, it must have been turned completely inside out, and upside down, long ago.", "요컨대 어쩌면 캔터베리 교구의 이 유언 검인 사무소라는 것이, 통째로 그토록 해로운 폐단이요, 그토록 고약한 모순덩어리라, 아는 사람이 거의 없는 세인트폴 교회 묘지 한구석에 처박혀 있지 않았더라면, 진작에 완전히 안팎이 뒤집히고 위아래가 뒤바뀌었어야 마땅하다는 점 말이다."]], [["Mr. Spenlow smiled as I became modestly warm on the subject, and then argued this question with me as he had argued the other.", "내가 이 화제에 점잖게 열을 올리자 스펜로 씨는 미소를 짓더니, 앞서 다른 문제를 두고 그랬던 것처럼 이 문제를 놓고도 나와 논쟁을 벌였다."], ["He said, what was it after all?", "그가 말하기를, 결국 그게 무슨 문제냐는 것이었다."], ["It was a question of feeling.", "그것은 감정의 문제라고 했다."], ["If the public felt that their wills were in safe keeping, and took it for granted that the office was not to be made better, who was the worse for it?", "대중이 제 유언장이 안전하게 보관되고 있다고 느끼고, 그 사무소를 더 낫게 만들 필요가 없다고 당연하게 여긴다면, 그로써 손해 보는 사람이 누가 있느냐고 했다. 아무도 없다."], ["Nobody. Who was the better for it?", "그로써 덕 보는 사람이 누구냐고 했다."], ["All the Sinecurists.", "한직을 차지한 자들 모두다."], ["Very well.", "아주 좋다."], ["Then the good predominated.", "그러니 좋은 쪽이 우세하다는 것이었다."], ["It might not be a perfect system; nothing was perfect; but what he objected to, was, the insertion of the wedge.", "그것이 완벽한 체계는 아닐지 모르나, 완벽한 것은 어디에도 없다고 했다. 다만 그가 반대하는 것은 쐐기를 박아 넣는 일이라고 했다."], ["Under the Prerogative Office, the country had been glorious.", "유언 검인 사무소 아래에서 이 나라는 영광을 누려 왔다는 것이다."], ["Insert the wedge into the Prerogative Office, and the country would cease to be glorious.", "그 사무소에 쐐기를 박아 넣으면, 이 나라는 더 이상 영광스럽지 못하리라고 했다."], ["He considered it the principle of a gentleman to take things as he found them; and he had no doubt the Prerogative Office would last our time.", "그는 매사를 있는 그대로 받아들이는 것이 신사의 원칙이라 여기며, 유언 검인 사무소가 우리 당대까지는 무난히 갈 것을 의심치 않는다고 했다."], ["I deferred to his opinion, though I had great doubts of it myself.", "나는 그의 의견에 따랐으나, 나 자신은 그것을 크게 의심했다."], ["I find he was right, however; for it has not only lasted to the present moment, but has done so in the teeth of a great parliamentary report made (not too willingly) eighteen years ago, when all these objections of mine were set forth in detail, and when the existing stowage for wills was described as equal to the accumulation of only two years and a half more.", "하지만 알고 보니 그가 옳았다. 그 사무소는 지금 이 순간까지 버텨 왔을 뿐 아니라, 십팔 년 전에 (그리 내키지 않는 마음으로) 작성된 어느 굵직한 의회 보고서에도 아랑곳없이 그래 왔으니까. 그 보고서에는 내가 말한 이 모든 반대 의견이 자세히 적혀 있었고, 유언장을 쌓아 둘 현재의 공간은 고작 이 년 반 어치를 더 보관할 정도밖에 안 된다고 적혀 있었다."], ["What they have done with them since; whether they have lost many, or whether they sell any, now and then, to the butter shops; I don’t know.", "그 뒤로 그들이 유언장을 어떻게 했는지, 많이 잃어버렸는지, 아니면 이따금 버터 가게에 팔아넘기는지, 나는 모른다."], ["I am glad mine is not there, and I hope it may not go there, yet awhile.", "내 유언장이 거기 있지 않아서 다행이고, 한동안은 그리로 가지 않기를 바란다."]], [["I have set all this down, in my present blissful chapter, because here it comes into its natural place. Mr.", "내가 이 모든 것을 지금의 이 더없이 행복한 장에 적어 둔 까닭은, 여기가 그것이 자연스레 들어맞을 자리이기 때문이다."], ["Spenlow and I falling into this conversation, prolonged it and our saunter to and fro, until we diverged into general topics.", "스펜로 씨와 나는 이런 대화에 빠져들어, 이리저리 거니는 산책과 더불어 그 이야기를 길게 끌다가, 마침내 두루뭉술한 화제로 넘어갔다."], ["And so it came about, in the end, that Mr. Spenlow told me this day week was Dora’s birthday, and he would be glad if I would come down and join a little picnic on the occasion.", "그렇게 해서 결국, 스펜로 씨가 나에게, 오늘로부터 일주일 뒤가 도라의 생일이니, 그날을 맞아 내가 내려와 자그마한 소풍에 함께해 준다면 기쁘겠다고 말하게 된 것이다. 나는 그 자리에서 넋이 나가 버렸다."], ["I went out of my senses immediately; became a mere driveller next day, on receipt of a little lace-edged sheet of note-paper, ‘Favoured by papa.", "이튿날에는 '아빠가 보내 줌. 잊지 말라고'라고 적힌, 레이스로 가장자리를 두른 작은 편지지 한 장을 받고 그만 얼간이가 되어 버렸다."], ["To remind’; and passed the intervening period in a state of dotage.", "그리고 그날까지의 시간을 망령 든 사람처럼 흘려보냈다."]], [["I think I committed every possible absurdity in the way of preparation for this blessed event.", "이 복된 행사를 준비하느라 나는 가능한 온갖 어리석은 짓을 다 저지른 것 같다."], ["I turn hot when I remember the cravat I bought.", "내가 산 크라바트를 떠올리면 지금도 얼굴이 화끈거린다."], ["My boots might be placed in any collection of instruments of torture.", "내 부츠는 어떤 고문 도구 진열장에 갖다 놓아도 손색이 없을 정도였다."], ["I provided, and sent down by the Norwood coach the night before, a delicate little hamper, amounting in itself, I thought, almost to a declaration.", "나는 전날 밤 노우드행 마차 편으로 자그마하고 고급스러운 바구니 하나를 마련해 내려보냈는데, 내 생각엔 그것만으로도 거의 사랑 고백이나 다름없었다."], ["There were crackers in it with the tenderest mottoes that could be got for money.", "그 안에는 돈으로 구할 수 있는 가장 다정한 글귀가 적힌 봉봉 과자가 들어 있었다."], ["At six in the morning, I was in Covent Garden Market, buying a bouquet for Dora.", "아침 여섯 시에 나는 코번트 가든 시장에 가서 도라에게 줄 꽃다발을 샀다."], ["At ten I was on horseback (I hired a gallant grey, for the occasion), with the bouquet in my hat, to keep it fresh, trotting down to Norwood.", "열 시에는 (그 일을 위해 늠름한 잿빛 말 한 마리를 빌렸다) 그 꽃다발을 싱싱하게 보존하려고 모자에 꽂은 채 말에 올라타, 노우드를 향해 달려 내려갔다."]], [["I suppose that when I saw Dora in the garden and pretended not to see her, and rode past the house pretending to be anxiously looking for it, I committed two small fooleries which other young gentlemen in my circumstances might have committed--because they came so very natural to me.", "정원에 있는 도라를 보고도 못 본 척하면서, 집을 애타게 찾는 척 그 집을 지나쳐 달렸을 때, 나는 내 처지에 놓인 다른 젊은 신사들도 저질렀을 법한 자그마한 어리석음 두 가지를 저질렀던 것 같다. 그것들이 내게는 너무도 자연스럽게 우러나왔으니까."], ["But oh! when I DID find the house, and DID dismount at the garden-gate, and drag those stony-hearted boots across the lawn to Dora sitting on a garden-seat under a lilac tree, what a spectacle she was, upon that beautiful morning, among the butterflies, in a white chip bonnet and a dress of celestial blue!", "그러나 아아! 마침내 그 집을 찾아내, 정원 문 앞에서 말에서 내려, 그 돌처럼 딱딱한 부츠를 끌고 잔디밭을 가로질러, 라일락 나무 아래 정원 의자에 앉아 있는 도라에게 다가갔을 때, 그 아름다운 아침에, 나비들 사이에서, 하얀 챙 없는 보닛을 쓰고 천상의 푸른빛 드레스를 입은 도라는 얼마나 황홀한 광경이었던지!"], ["There was a young lady with her--comparatively stricken in years--almost twenty, I should say.", "도라 곁에는 한 아가씨가 있었는데 — 비교적 나이가 든 편으로 — 거의 스무 살은 됐지 싶었다."], ["Her name was Miss Mills.", "그녀의 이름은 밀스 양이었다."], ["And Dora called her Julia.", "그리고 도라는 그녀를 줄리아라고 불렀다."], ["She was the bosom friend of Dora.", "그녀는 도라의 둘도 없는 벗이었다."], ["Happy Miss Mills!", "행복한 밀스 양 같으니!"]], [["Jip was there, and Jip WOULD bark at me again.", "집은 거기 있었고, 집은 또다시 나를 보고 짖어 댔다."], ["When I presented my bouquet, he gnashed his teeth with jealousy.", "내가 꽃다발을 내밀자, 집은 질투에 이를 갈았다."], ["Well he might.", "그럴 만도 했다."], ["If he had the least idea how I adored his mistress, well he might!", "내가 제 안주인을 얼마나 흠모하는지 조금이라도 눈치챘다면, 그럴 만도 했고말고!"]], [["‘Oh, thank you, Mr. Copperfield!", "\"어머, 고마워요, 코퍼필드 씨!"], ["What dear flowers!’ said Dora.", "정말 예쁜 꽃이네요!\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["I had had an intention of saying (and had been studying the best form of words for three miles) that I thought them beautiful before I saw them so near HER.", "나는 (그리고 삼 마일을 오는 내내 가장 멋진 표현을 궁리한 끝에) 도라 곁에서 이토록 가까이 보기 전부터 그 꽃이 아름답다고 여겼노라고 말할 작정이었다."], ["But I couldn’t manage it.", "하지만 도무지 입이 떨어지지 않았다."], ["She was too bewildering. To see her lay the flowers against her little dimpled chin, was to lose all presence of mind and power of language in a feeble ecstasy.", "도라는 너무도 정신을 아찔하게 만드는 사람이었다. 도라가 그 꽃을 자신의 작고 보조개 진 턱에 갖다 대는 것을 보자, 나는 정신을 차릴 힘도, 말을 할 힘도 죄다 잃고 가냘픈 황홀경에 빠져들었다."], ["I wonder I didn’t say, ‘Kill me, if you have a heart, Miss Mills. Let me die here!’", "\"밀스 양, 자비심이 있다면 저를 죽여 주세요. 여기서 죽게 해 주세요!\"라고 말하지 않은 것이 신기할 따름이다."]], [["Then Dora held my flowers to Jip to smell.", "이윽고 도라는 내 꽃을 집에게 내밀어 냄새를 맡게 했다."], ["Then Jip growled, and wouldn’t smell them.", "그러자 집은 으르렁대며 냄새 맡기를 거부했다."], ["Then Dora laughed, and held them a little closer to Jip, to make him.", "그러자 도라는 웃으면서 꽃을 집에게 조금 더 가까이 들이밀어 억지로 맡게 하려 했다."], ["Then Jip laid hold of a bit of geranium with his teeth, and worried imaginary cats in it.", "그러자 집은 제라늄 한 송이를 이빨로 덥석 물고, 거기에 든 가상의 고양이를 마구 물어뜯어 댔다."], ["Then Dora beat him, and pouted, and said, ‘My poor beautiful flowers!’ as compassionately, I thought, as if Jip had laid hold of me.", "그러자 도라는 집을 때리고는 입을 삐죽이며, \"내 가엾고 예쁜 꽃들!\" 하고 말했는데, 내 생각엔 집이 나를 물어뜯기라도 한 것처럼 안타까워하는 투였다."], ["I wished he had!", "차라리 집이 나를 물어뜯었더라면!"]], [["‘You’ll be so glad to hear, Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 정말 반가운 소식이 있어요.\""], ["Copperfield,’ said Dora, ‘that that cross Miss Murdstone is not here.", "도라가 말했다. \"그 심술궂은 머드스톤 양이 여기 없거든요."], ["She has gone to her brother’s marriage, and will be away at least three weeks.", "자기 오빠 결혼식에 가서, 적어도 삼 주는 비울 거예요."], ["Isn’t that delightful?’", "정말 신나지 않아요?\""]], [["I said I was sure it must be delightful to her, and all that was delightful to her was delightful to me.", "나는 도라에게 그것이 분명 신나는 일일 것이며, 도라에게 신나는 일은 무엇이든 나에게도 신나는 일이라고 말했다."], ["Miss Mills, with an air of superior wisdom and benevolence, smiled upon us.", "밀스 양은 한층 지혜롭고 자애로운 기색으로 우리를 보고 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘She is the most disagreeable thing I ever saw,’ said Dora.", "\"내가 본 사람 중에 제일 못마땅한 사람이에요.\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘You can’t believe how ill-tempered and shocking she is, Julia.’", "\"그 여자가 얼마나 심술궂고 끔찍한지, 줄리아, 너는 못 믿을 거야.\""]], [["‘Yes, I can, my dear!’ said Julia.", "\"아냐, 믿을 수 있어, 얘!\" 줄리아가 말했다."]], [["‘YOU can, perhaps, love,’ returned Dora, with her hand on Julia’s.", "\"넌 어쩌면 믿을 수 있겠지.\" 도라가 줄리아의 손에 제 손을 얹으며 대꾸했다."], ["‘Forgive my not excepting you, my dear, at first.’", "\"처음에 너를 빼놓고 말한 거 용서해 줘, 얘.\""]], [["I learnt, from this, that Miss Mills had had her trials in the course of a chequered existence; and that to these, perhaps, I might refer that wise benignity of manner which I had already noticed.", "이로써 나는, 밀스 양이 파란만장한 인생을 살아오는 동안 갖은 시련을 겪었다는 것과, 어쩌면 내가 이미 눈여겨본 그 지혜로운 자애로움이 그 시련에서 비롯되었으리라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["I found, in the course of the day, that this was the case: Miss Mills having been unhappy in a misplaced affection, and being understood to have retired from the world on her awful stock of experience, but still to take a calm interest in the unblighted hopes and loves of youth.", "그날 하루를 보내는 동안 나는 그것이 사실임을 알았다. 밀스 양은 잘못 둔 정 때문에 불행을 겪은 적이 있었고, 그 무시무시한 경험을 밑천 삼아 세상을 등진 것으로 통했으나, 그러면서도 시들지 않은 젊음의 희망과 사랑에는 여전히 잔잔한 관심을 두고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["But now Mr.", "그런데 이때 스펜로 씨가 집에서 나왔고, 도라가 그에게 달려가 말했다. \"보세요, 아빠, 얼마나 예쁜 꽃이에요!\""], ["Spenlow came out of the house, and Dora went to him, saying, ‘Look, papa, what beautiful flowers!’ And Miss Mills smiled thoughtfully, as who should say, ‘Ye Mayflies, enjoy your brief existence in the bright morning of life!’ And we all walked from the lawn towards the carriage, which was getting ready.", "그러자 밀스 양은 생각에 잠긴 듯 미소 지었는데, 마치 \"그대 하루살이들이여, 인생의 환한 아침에 주어진 짧은 생을 마음껏 누리려무나!\" 하고 말하는 것 같았다. 그리고 우리는 모두 잔디밭에서 마차 쪽으로 걸어갔는데, 마차는 떠날 채비를 갖추고 있었다."]], [["I shall never have such a ride again.", "나는 다시는 그런 마차 길을 누리지 못하리라."], ["I have never had such another.", "그런 길은 그 후로 단 한 번도 없었다."], ["There were only those three, their hamper, my hamper, and the guitar-case, in the phaeton; and, of course, the phaeton was open; and I rode behind it, and Dora sat with her back to the horses, looking towards me.", "사륜마차에는 그 셋과, 그들의 바구니, 내 바구니, 그리고 기타 케이스밖에 없었다. 그리고 물론 그 마차는 덮개 없는 것이었다. 나는 그 뒤를 따라 말을 달렸고, 도라는 말 쪽으로 등을 돌리고 앉아 나를 바라보았다."], ["She kept the bouquet close to her on the cushion, and wouldn’t allow Jip to sit on that side of her at all, for fear he should crush it.", "도라는 꽃다발을 방석 위 제 곁에 바짝 붙여 놓고, 행여 그것이 짓눌릴까 봐 집을 그쪽에는 아예 앉지 못하게 했다."], ["She often carried it in her hand, often refreshed herself with its fragrance.", "도라는 자주 그것을 손에 들었고, 자주 그 향기로 기운을 돋우었다."], ["Our eyes at those times often met; and my great astonishment is that I didn’t go over the head of my gallant grey into the carriage.", "그럴 때면 우리 눈은 자주 마주쳤다. 내가 그 늠름한 잿빛 말 머리를 넘어 마차 안으로 거꾸러지지 않은 것이 그저 놀라울 따름이다."]], [["There was dust, I believe.", "먼지가 일었던 것 같다."], ["There was a good deal of dust, I believe.", "먼지가 꽤 많이 일었던 것 같다."], ["I have a faint impression that Mr. Spenlow remonstrated with me for riding in it; but I knew of none.", "스펜로 씨가 그 속에서 말을 달린다고 나를 나무란 듯한 어렴풋한 기억이 있다. 하지만 나는 아무것도 의식하지 못했다."], ["I was sensible of a mist of love and beauty about Dora, but of nothing else.", "나는 도라를 에워싼 사랑과 아름다움의 안개만 느꼈을 뿐, 다른 것은 아무것도 느끼지 못했다."], ["He stood up sometimes, and asked me what I thought of the prospect.", "그는 이따금 일어서서, 경치를 어떻게 보느냐고 나에게 물었다."], ["I said it was delightful, and I dare say it was; but it was all Dora to me.", "나는 멋지다고 대답했고, 정말 멋졌을 것이다. 하지만 나에게는 모든 것이 도라였다."], ["The sun shone Dora, and the birds sang Dora.", "해는 도라로 빛났고, 새들은 도라를 노래했다."], ["The south wind blew Dora, and the wild flowers in the hedges were all Doras, to a bud. My comfort is, Miss Mills understood me.", "남풍은 도라로 불었고, 산울타리의 들꽃들은 봉오리 하나하나까지 죄다 도라였다. 위안이 되는 것은, 밀스 양이 나를 이해해 주었다는 사실이다."], ["Miss Mills alone could enter into my feelings thoroughly.", "밀스 양만이 내 심정에 온전히 공감해 줄 수 있었다."]], [["I don’t know how long we were going, and to this hour I know as little where we went.", "우리가 얼마나 오래 갔는지 나는 모르고, 지금 이 순간까지도 우리가 어디로 갔는지 도통 알지 못한다."], ["Perhaps it was near Guildford.", "어쩌면 길퍼드 근처였는지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps some Arabian-night magician, opened up the place for the day, and shut it up for ever when we came away.", "어쩌면 어느 아라비안나이트의 마법사가, 그날 하루만 그곳을 열어 놓았다가 우리가 떠나오자 영영 닫아 버린 것인지도 모른다."], ["It was a green spot, on a hill, carpeted with soft turf.", "그곳은 언덕 위의 푸른 자리였고, 부드러운 잔디가 양탄자처럼 깔려 있었다."], ["There were shady trees, and heather, and, as far as the eye could see, a rich landscape.", "그늘진 나무들과 히스가 있었고, 눈길이 닿는 데까지 풍요로운 풍경이 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["It was a trying thing to find people here, waiting for us; and my jealousy, even of the ladies, knew no bounds.", "그곳에서 우리를 기다리는 사람들을 발견한 것은 견디기 힘든 일이었고, 여자들에 대해서까지도 내 질투는 끝을 몰랐다."], ["But all of my own sex--especially one impostor, three or four years my elder, with a red whisker, on which he established an amount of presumption not to be endured--were my mortal foes.", "그러나 내 동성들은 모조리 — 특히 나보다 서너 살 위에, 붉은 구레나룻을 기른 어떤 사기꾼은, 그 구레나룻을 밑천 삼아 도무지 참아 줄 수 없을 만큼 거들먹거렸는데 — 나의 철천지원수였다."]], [["We all unpacked our baskets, and employed ourselves in getting dinner ready.", "우리는 모두 바구니를 풀어, 저녁 식사 채비를 하느라 분주했다."], ["Red Whisker pretended he could make a salad (which I don’t believe), and obtruded himself on public notice.", "붉은 구레나룻은 샐러드를 만들 줄 안다고 떠벌리며 (나는 믿지 않았지만) 뭇사람의 눈에 띄려고 나섰다."], ["Some of the young ladies washed the lettuces for him, and sliced them under his directions. Dora was among these.", "젊은 아가씨 몇몇이 그를 위해 상추를 씻었고, 그의 지시에 따라 그것을 썰었다. 도라도 그들 틈에 끼어 있었다."], ["I felt that fate had pitted me against this man, and one of us must fall.", "운명이 나를 이 사내와 맞붙여 놓았으니, 우리 둘 중 하나는 쓰러질 수밖에 없다는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["Red Whisker made his salad (I wondered how they could eat it.", "붉은 구레나룻이 샐러드를 만들었다 (사람들이 그걸 어떻게 먹는지 신기했다."], ["Nothing should have induced ME to touch it!) and voted himself into the charge of the wine-cellar, which he constructed, being an ingenious beast, in the hollow trunk of a tree.", "나라면 무슨 일이 있어도 손도 대지 않았으리라!). 그러고는 자기가 술 창고를 맡겠다고 자청했는데, 약삭빠른 짐승답게 나무의 텅 빈 둥치 속에다 그 술 창고를 만들었다."], ["By and by, I saw him, with the majority of a lobster on his plate, eating his dinner at the feet of Dora!", "이윽고 나는 그가 접시에 바닷가재의 대부분을 담아 들고, 도라의 발치에서 저녁을 먹는 것을 보았다!"]], [["I have but an indistinct idea of what happened for some time after this baleful object presented itself to my view.", "이 끔찍한 광경이 내 눈앞에 펼쳐진 뒤로 한동안 무슨 일이 있었는지는 어렴풋한 기억밖에 없다."], ["I was very merry, I know; but it was hollow merriment.", "내가 무척 들떠 있었다는 것은 안다. 하지만 그것은 속 빈 들뜸이었다."], ["I attached myself to a young creature in pink, with little eyes, and flirted with her desperately.", "나는 눈이 작은 분홍 옷 차림의 젊은 처자에게 들러붙어, 죽기 살기로 수작을 걸었다."], ["She received my attentions with favour; but whether on my account solely, or because she had any designs on Red Whisker, I can’t say.", "그녀는 내 관심을 호의로 받아들였다. 그러나 그게 오로지 나 때문이었는지, 아니면 그녀가 붉은 구레나룻에게 무슨 속셈이 있어서였는지는 알 수 없다."], ["Dora’s health was drunk.", "도라를 위한 축배가 돌았다."], ["When I drank it, I affected to interrupt my conversation for that purpose, and to resume it immediately afterwards.", "나는 그 축배를 들 때, 일부러 하던 대화를 끊고 잔을 든 다음, 곧바로 다시 대화를 이어 가는 척했다."], ["I caught Dora’s eye as I bowed to her, and I thought it looked appealing.", "도라에게 고개 숙여 인사하다가 도라의 눈길을 붙잡았는데, 무언가 호소하는 듯한 눈빛으로 보였다."], ["But it looked at me over the head of Red Whisker, and I was adamant.", "그러나 그 눈빛은 붉은 구레나룻의 머리 너머로 나를 보고 있었고, 나는 매정하게 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["The young creature in pink had a mother in green; and I rather think the latter separated us from motives of policy.", "분홍 옷의 젊은 처자에게는 초록 옷을 입은 어머니가 있었다. 내 생각엔 그 어머니가 무슨 속셈이 있어 우리를 떼어 놓은 것 같다."], ["Howbeit, there was a general breaking up of the party, while the remnants of the dinner were being put away; and I strolled off by myself among the trees, in a raging and remorseful state.", "어쨌든 저녁 식사의 남은 것을 치우는 사이, 모임이 전체적으로 흩어졌고, 나는 사납고 회한에 찬 심정으로 홀로 나무들 사이를 거닐었다."], ["I was debating whether I should pretend that I was not well, and fly--I don’t know where--upon my gallant grey, when Dora and Miss Mills met me.", "내가 몸이 안 좋은 척하고, 늠름한 잿빛 말을 타고 어딘가로 — 어디로인지는 모르겠지만 — 달아나 버릴까 궁리하고 있을 때, 도라와 밀스 양이 나를 마주쳤다."]], [["‘Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Copperfield,’ said Miss Mills, ‘you are dull.’", "밀스 양이 말했다. \"기운이 없으시네요.\""]], [["I begged her pardon.", "나는 그녀에게 양해를 구했다."], ["Not at all.", "전혀 그렇지 않다고."]], [["‘And Dora,’ said Miss Mills, ‘YOU are dull.’", "\"그리고 도라,\" 밀스 양이 말했다. \"너도 기운이 없구나.\""]], [["Oh dear no!", "어머, 아니에요!"], ["Not in the least.", "조금도 그렇지 않아요."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield and Dora,’ said Miss Mills, with an almost venerable air.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 그리고 도라.\" 밀스 양이 거의 위엄 어린 기색으로 말했다."], ["‘Enough of this.", "\"이쯤 하세요."], ["Do not allow a trivial misunderstanding to wither the blossoms of spring, which, once put forth and blighted, cannot be renewed.", "하찮은 오해 때문에 봄의 꽃송이를 시들게 하지 마세요. 그 꽃송이는 한번 피었다 시들면 다시는 되살릴 수 없으니까요. 저는,\" 밀스 양이 말했다."], ["I speak,’ said Miss Mills, ‘from experience of the past--the remote, irrevocable past.", "\"지난날의 경험에서 우러나 말하는 거예요. 저 멀고도 되돌릴 수 없는 지난날 말이에요."], ["The gushing fountains which sparkle in the sun, must not be stopped in mere caprice; the oasis in the desert of Sahara must not be plucked up idly.’", "햇빛에 반짝이며 솟구치는 샘물을 한낱 변덕으로 막아서는 안 됩니다. 사하라 사막의 오아시스를 함부로 뽑아 버려서도 안 되고요.\""]], [["I hardly knew what I did, I was burning all over to that extraordinary extent; but I took Dora’s little hand and kissed it--and she let me!", "나는 내가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 잘 몰랐다. 온몸이 그렇게나 유난히 활활 타오르고 있었으니까. 하지만 나는 도라의 작은 손을 잡고 거기에 입을 맞추었다 — 그리고 도라는 그러도록 내버려 두었다!"], ["I kissed Miss Mills’s hand; and we all seemed, to my thinking, to go straight up to the seventh heaven.", "나는 밀스 양의 손에도 입을 맞추었다. 그리고 내 생각에, 우리는 모두 곧장 일곱째 하늘로 솟구쳐 오르는 것만 같았다."], ["We did not come down again.", "우리는 다시 내려오지 않았다."], ["We stayed up there all the evening.", "우리는 저녁 내내 그 위에 머물렀다."], ["At first we strayed to and fro among the trees: I with Dora’s shy arm drawn through mine: and Heaven knows, folly as it all was, it would have been a happy fate to have been struck immortal with those foolish feelings, and have stayed among the trees for ever!", "처음에 우리는 나무들 사이를 이리저리 거닐었다. 나는 도라의 수줍은 팔을 내 팔에 끼고서. 그리고 그 모든 것이 어리석은 짓이긴 했어도, 그 어리석은 감정에 사로잡힌 채 불멸의 몸이 되어 영원토록 그 나무들 사이에 머물 수만 있었다면, 정말이지 그것은 행복한 운명이었으리라는 것을, 하느님은 아실 것이다!"]], [["But, much too soon, we heard the others laughing and talking, and calling ‘where’s Dora?’ So we went back, and they wanted Dora to sing.", "그러나 너무도 빨리, 우리는 다른 사람들이 웃고 떠들며 \"도라는 어디 있어?\" 하고 부르는 소리를 들었다. 그래서 우리는 돌아갔고, 사람들은 도라에게 노래를 청했다."], ["Red Whisker would have got the guitar-case out of the carriage, but Dora told him nobody knew where it was, but I.", "붉은 구레나룻이 마차에서 기타 케이스를 꺼내 오려 했으나, 도라는 그것이 어디 있는지 나 말고는 아무도 모른다고 그에게 일러 주었다."], ["So Red Whisker was done for in a moment; and I got it, and I unlocked it, and I took the guitar out, and I sat by her, and I held her handkerchief and gloves, and I drank in every note of her dear voice, and she sang to ME who loved her, and all the others might applaud as much as they liked, but they had nothing to do with it!", "그리하여 붉은 구레나룻은 단번에 끝장났다. 그리고 내가 그것을 가져와, 내가 그것을 열고, 내가 기타를 꺼내, 내가 도라 곁에 앉아, 내가 그녀의 손수건과 장갑을 들어 주고, 내가 그녀의 사랑스러운 목소리의 한 음 한 음을 들이마셨다. 그리고 그녀는 자기를 사랑하는 나에게 노래해 주었으니, 다른 사람들이야 좋을 대로 갈채를 보내든 말든, 그들은 그 노래와 아무 상관이 없었다!"]], [["I was intoxicated with joy.", "나는 기쁨에 취했다."], ["I was afraid it was too happy to be real, and that I should wake in Buckingham Street presently, and hear Mrs.", "그것이 너무 행복해서 도무지 현실 같지가 않아, 머잖아 버킹엄 거리에서 잠을 깨, 크럽 부인이 아침을 차리느라 찻잔을 달그락거리는 소리나 듣게 되는 것은 아닐까 두려웠다. 그러나 도라가 노래했고, 다른 사람들도 노래했고, 밀스 양도 노래했다."], ["Crupp clinking the teacups in getting breakfast ready.", "기억의 동굴 속에 잠든 메아리에 대해 — 마치 자기가 백 살이라도 먹은 양 — 노래했다."], ["But Dora sang, and others sang, and Miss Mills sang--about the slumbering echoes in the caverns of Memory; as if she were a hundred years old--and the evening came on; and we had tea, with the kettle boiling gipsy-fashion; and I was still as happy as ever.", "그리고 저녁이 깔려 왔다. 우리는 집시처럼 주전자를 끓여 차를 마셨다. 그리고 나는 여전히 더없이 행복했다."]], [["I was happier than ever when the party broke up, and the other people, defeated Red Whisker and all, went their several ways, and we went ours through the still evening and the dying light, with sweet scents rising up around us. Mr.", "모임이 파하고, 패배한 붉은 구레나룻을 비롯한 다른 사람들이 저마다 제 갈 길로 가고, 우리는 우리 길로 접어들어, 사방에 달콤한 향기가 피어오르는 고요한 저녁과 스러져 가는 빛 속을 지날 때, 나는 어느 때보다도 더 행복했다."], ["Spenlow being a little drowsy after the champagne--honour to the soil that grew the grape, to the grape that made the wine, to the sun that ripened it, and to the merchant who adulterated it!--and being fast asleep in a corner of the carriage, I rode by the side and talked to Dora.", "스펜로 씨는 샴페인을 마신 뒤라 조금 졸려 했는데 — 그 포도를 길러 낸 토양에, 그 포도로 술을 빚어 낸 포도에, 그것을 익혀 준 햇빛에, 그리고 그것에 물을 탄 상인에게 영광 있으라! — 그래서 마차 한구석에서 곤히 잠들어 버렸기에, 나는 마차 옆에서 말을 달리며 도라와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["She admired my horse and patted him--oh, what a dear little hand it looked upon a horse!--and her shawl would not keep right, and now and then I drew it round her with my arm; and I even fancied that Jip began to see how it was, and to understand that he must make up his mind to be friends with me.", "도라는 내 말을 칭찬하며 토닥여 주었는데 — 아아, 말 위에 얹힌 그 손은 얼마나 사랑스러운 작은 손으로 보이던지! — 그리고 도라의 숄이 자꾸 흘러내리기에, 나는 이따금 팔로 그것을 그녀에게 둘러 주었다. 심지어 나는 집마저도 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 눈치채기 시작해, 마음을 정하고 나와 친해지는 수밖에 없다는 것을 깨닫는구나 하고 상상했다."]], [["That sagacious Miss Mills, too; that amiable, though quite used up, recluse; that little patriarch of something less than twenty, who had done with the world, and mustn’t on any account have the slumbering echoes in the caverns of Memory awakened; what a kind thing she did!", "게다가 저 슬기로운 밀스 양, 비록 진이 다 빠지긴 했어도 그 상냥한 은둔자, 세상과는 이미 인연을 끊어 기억의 동굴 속에 잠든 메아리를 무슨 일이 있어도 깨워서는 안 되는, 스무 살이 채 안 된 그 자그마한 가모장(家母長)은, 얼마나 친절한 일을 해 주었던지!"]], [["‘Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Copperfield,’ said Miss Mills, ‘come to this side of the carriage a moment--if you can spare a moment.", "밀스 양이 말했다. \"잠깐 마차 이쪽으로 와 보시겠어요? 잠깐 시간이 되신다면요."], ["I want to speak to you.’", "드릴 말씀이 있어서요.\""]], [["Behold me, on my gallant grey, bending at the side of Miss Mills, with my hand upon the carriage door!", "자, 보라, 늠름한 잿빛 말에 올라탄 채, 마차 문에 손을 얹고, 밀스 양 곁으로 몸을 숙이고 있는 나를!"]], [["‘Dora is coming to stay with me.", "\"도라가 저와 함께 지내러 올 거예요."], ["She is coming home with me the day after tomorrow.", "모레 저와 함께 집으로 갈 거랍니다."], ["If you would like to call, I am sure papa would be happy to see you.’ What could I do but invoke a silent blessing on Miss Mills’s head, and store Miss Mills’s address in the securest corner of my memory!", "찾아오고 싶으시다면, 분명 아빠도 코퍼필드 씨를 반가워하실 거예요.\" 내가 밀스 양의 머리 위로 말없이 축복을 빌고, 밀스 양의 주소를 내 기억의 가장 안전한 한구석에 새겨 두는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴!"], ["What could I do but tell Miss Mills, with grateful looks and fervent words, how much I appreciated her good offices, and what an inestimable value I set upon her friendship!", "내가 밀스 양에게, 고마운 눈빛과 뜨거운 말로, 그녀의 호의를 얼마나 고맙게 여기는지, 그녀의 우정에 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없는 가치를 두는지 전하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었으랴!"]], [["Then Miss Mills benignantly dismissed me, saying, ‘Go back to Dora!’ and I went; and Dora leaned out of the carriage to talk to me, and we talked all the rest of the way; and I rode my gallant grey so close to the wheel that I grazed his near fore leg against it, and ‘took the bark off’, as his owner told me, ‘to the tune of three pun’ sivin’--which I paid, and thought extremely cheap for so much joy. What time Miss Mills sat looking at the moon, murmuring verses--and recalling, I suppose, the ancient days when she and earth had anything in common.", "그러고 나서 밀스 양은 자애롭게 나를 물러가게 하며, \"도라에게 돌아가세요!\" 하고 말했다. 그래서 나는 갔다. 도라가 마차 밖으로 몸을 내밀어 나에게 말을 걸었고, 우리는 남은 길 내내 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 나는 늠름한 잿빛 말을 바퀴에 어찌나 바짝 붙여 몰았던지, 그놈의 앞다리를 바퀴에 긁혀, 말 주인의 말마따나 \"삼 파운드 칠 실링어치로 살가죽을 벗겨\" 먹고 말았다 — 나는 그 값을 치렀고, 그만한 기쁨에 비하면 더없이 싸다고 여겼다. 그동안 밀스 양은 달을 바라보며, 시구를 읊조리고 앉아 있었다 — 짐작건대, 자기가 이 세상과 무언가 통하던 것이 있던 그 옛날을 떠올리면서."]], [["Norwood was many miles too near, and we reached it many hours too soon; but Mr.", "노우드는 몇 마일이나 너무 가까웠고, 우리는 몇 시간이나 너무 일찍 그곳에 다다랐다."], ["Spenlow came to himself a little short of it, and said, ‘You must come in, Copperfield, and rest!’ and I consenting, we had sandwiches and wine-and-water.", "하지만 스펜로 씨가 그곳에 채 못 미쳐 정신을 조금 차리고는 말했다. \"코퍼필드 군, 들어와서 좀 쉬게나!\" 그리고 내가 응하자, 우리는 샌드위치와 포도주 탄 물을 먹었다."], ["In the light room, Dora blushing looked so lovely, that I could not tear myself away, but sat there staring, in a dream, until the snoring of Mr.", "불빛 환한 방에서, 발그레해진 도라가 어찌나 사랑스러워 보였던지, 나는 도무지 자리를 뜰 수가 없어, 꿈결에 잠긴 듯 멍하니 도라만 바라보며 앉아 있었다."], ["Spenlow inspired me with sufficient consciousness to take my leave.", "그러다 스펜로 씨의 코 고는 소리에 가까스로 정신을 차리고 자리를 떴다."], ["So we parted; I riding all the way to London with the farewell touch of Dora’s hand still light on mine, recalling every incident and word ten thousand times; lying down in my own bed at last, as enraptured a young noodle as ever was carried out of his five wits by love.", "그렇게 우리는 헤어졌다. 나는 도라의 손이 작별로 닿았던 가벼운 감촉을 여전히 내 손에 느끼며, 그날의 사건 하나하나와 말 한마디 한마디를 만 번이고 곱씹으며, 런던까지 내내 말을 달렸다. 그리고 마침내 내 침대에 누웠으니, 사랑에 정신이 홀딱 나가 버린, 일찍이 없던 황홀한 풋내기 얼간이였다."]], [["When I awoke next morning, I was resolute to declare my passion to Dora, and know my fate.", "이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때, 나는 도라에게 내 사랑을 고백하고 내 운명을 알아내리라 굳게 마음먹었다."], ["Happiness or misery was now the question.", "이제는 행복이냐 비참함이냐가 문제였다."], ["There was no other question that I knew of in the world, and only Dora could give the answer to it.", "세상에 내가 아는 다른 문제는 없었고, 오직 도라만이 그 답을 줄 수 있었다."], ["I passed three days in a luxury of wretchedness, torturing myself by putting every conceivable variety of discouraging construction on all that ever had taken place between Dora and me.", "나는 도라와 나 사이에 일찍이 있었던 모든 일에 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 비관적인 해석을 죄다 갖다 붙이며 나 자신을 괴롭히는, 비참함의 사치 속에서 사흘을 보냈다."], ["At last, arrayed for the purpose at a vast expense, I went to Miss Mills’s, fraught with a declaration.", "마침내 나는 그 목적을 위해 엄청난 돈을 들여 차려입고, 사랑 고백을 잔뜩 품은 채 밀스 양의 집으로 갔다."]], [["How many times I went up and down the street, and round the square--painfully aware of being a much better answer to the old riddle than the original one--before I could persuade myself to go up the steps and knock, is no matter now.", "내가 그 계단을 올라가 문을 두드릴 결심이 설 때까지, 그 거리를 몇 번이나 오르내리고 광장 둘레를 몇 번이나 돌았는지는 — 그 옛날 수수께끼의 본래 답보다 내가 훨씬 더 그럴듯한 답이라는 것을 뼈저리게 느끼면서 말이다 — 이제 와서는 아무래도 좋다."], ["Even when, at last, I had knocked, and was waiting at the door, I had some flurried thought of asking if that were Mr. Blackboy’s (in imitation of poor Barkis), begging pardon, and retreating.", "마침내 문을 두드리고 문 앞에서 기다리고 있을 때조차, 나는 (가엾은 바키스를 흉내 내어) 여기가 블랙보이 씨 댁이냐고 물은 뒤, 죄송하다며 물러나 버릴까 하는 허둥대는 생각마저 들었다."], ["But I kept my ground.", "하지만 나는 자리를 지켰다."]], [["Mr. Mills was not at home.", "밀스 씨는 집에 없었다."], ["I did not expect he would be.", "나는 그가 있으리라 기대하지도 않았다."], ["Nobody wanted HIM.", "아무도 그를 찾지 않았다."], ["Miss Mills was at home.", "밀스 양은 집에 있었다."], ["Miss Mills would do.", "밀스 양이면 됐다."]], [["I was shown into a room upstairs, where Miss Mills and Dora were.", "나는 위층의 어느 방으로 안내되었는데, 그곳에 밀스 양과 도라가 있었다."], ["Jip was there.", "집도 거기 있었다."], ["Miss Mills was copying music (I recollect, it was a new song, called ‘Affection’s Dirge’), and Dora was painting flowers.", "밀스 양은 악보를 베껴 쓰고 있었고 (기억하기로, '애정의 만가'라는 새 노래였다), 도라는 꽃을 그리고 있었다."], ["What were my feelings, when I recognized my own flowers; the identical Covent Garden Market purchase!", "그 그림이 내 꽃이라는 것을, 바로 그 코번트 가든 시장에서 산 꽃이라는 것을 알아보았을 때, 내 심정이 어떠했겠는가!"], ["I cannot say that they were very like, or that they particularly resembled any flowers that have ever come under my observation; but I knew from the paper round them which was accurately copied, what the composition was.", "그 그림이 실물과 아주 닮았다거나, 내가 여태껏 본 어떤 꽃과 유난히 비슷했다고는 말할 수 없다. 하지만 그 꽃을 두른 종이가 정확히 베껴진 것을 보고, 나는 그 그림이 무엇을 그린 것인지 알아챘다."]], [["Miss Mills was very glad to see me, and very sorry her papa was not at home: though I thought we all bore that with fortitude.", "밀스 양은 나를 보고 무척 반가워했고, 아빠가 집에 없어 무척 안타까워했다. 하지만 우리 모두 그 사실을 꿋꿋이 잘 견뎌 내고 있다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Miss Mills was conversational for a few minutes, and then, laying down her pen upon ‘Affection’s Dirge’, got up, and left the room.", "밀스 양은 몇 분간 이야기를 나누더니, '애정의 만가' 위에 펜을 내려놓고 일어나, 방을 나갔다."]], [["I began to think I would put it off till tomorrow.", "나는 이 일을 내일로 미룰까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["‘I hope your poor horse was not tired, when he got home at night,’ said Dora, lifting up her beautiful eyes.", "\"가엾은 말이 밤에 집에 갔을 때 지치지 않았기를 바라요.\" 도라가 그 아름다운 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["‘It was a long way for him.’", "\"그 말한테는 먼 길이었을 텐데요.\""]], [["I began to think I would do it today.", "나는 이 일을 오늘 해야겠다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["‘It was a long way for him,’ said I, ‘for he had nothing to uphold him on the journey.’", "\"그 말한테는 먼 길이었지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그 여정에서 자기를 버티게 해 줄 게 아무것도 없었으니까요.\""]], [["‘Wasn’t he fed, poor thing?’ asked Dora.", "\"불쌍하게도, 먹이는 줬나요?\" 도라가 물었다."]], [["I began to think I would put it off till tomorrow.", "나는 이 일을 내일로 미룰까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["‘Ye-yes,’ I said, ‘he was well taken care of.", "\"네, 네.\" 내가 말했다. \"잘 보살핌을 받았어요."], ["I mean he had not the unutterable happiness that I had in being so near you.’", "제 말은, 그 말은 당신 곁에 이토록 가까이 있는, 저 같은 형언할 수 없는 행복은 누리지 못했다는 거예요.\""]], [["Dora bent her head over her drawing and said, after a little while--I had sat, in the interval, in a burning fever, and with my legs in a very rigid state--", "도라는 그림 위로 고개를 숙이더니, 잠시 뒤에 말했다 — 그동안 나는 활활 타오르는 열병에 들뜬 채, 두 다리를 몹시 뻣뻣하게 굳히고 앉아 있었다 —"]], [["‘You didn’t seem to be sensible of that happiness yourself, at one time of the day.’", "\"오늘 낮의 어느 때엔, 그 행복을 정작 본인은 느끼지 못하는 것 같던데요.\""]], [["I saw now that I was in for it, and it must be done on the spot.", "이제 나는 일이 벌어졌고, 당장 그 자리에서 해치워야 한다는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["‘You didn’t care for that happiness in the least,’ said Dora, slightly raising her eyebrows, and shaking her head, ‘when you were sitting by Miss Kitt.’", "\"당신은 그 행복에 조금도 관심이 없었잖아요.\" 도라가 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리며 고개를 가로젓고 말했다. \"키트 양 곁에 앉아 있을 때 말이에요.\""]], [["Kitt, I should observe, was the name of the creature in pink, with the little eyes.", "밝혀 두자면, 키트는 그 눈 작은 분홍 옷 차림의 처자 이름이었다."]], [["‘Though certainly I don’t know why you should,’ said Dora, ‘or why you should call it a happiness at all.", "\"하긴 당신이 왜 그래야 하는지, 또 왜 그걸 행복이라고 부르는지 저야 모르지만요.\" 도라가 말했다."], ["But of course you don’t mean what you say.", "\"하지만 물론 당신은 마음에도 없는 말을 하는 거겠죠."], ["And I am sure no one doubts your being at liberty to do whatever you like.", "그리고 당신이 무엇이든 하고 싶은 대로 할 자유가 있다는 걸 의심하는 사람은 분명 아무도 없을 거예요."], ["Jip, you naughty boy, come here!’", "집, 이 못된 녀석, 이리 와!\""]], [["I don’t know how I did it.", "내가 어떻게 그랬는지 모르겠다."], ["I did it in a moment.", "나는 순식간에 해치웠다."], ["I intercepted Jip.", "나는 집을 가로막았다."], ["I had Dora in my arms.", "나는 도라를 품에 안았다."], ["I was full of eloquence.", "나는 청산유수로 말을 쏟아 냈다."], ["I never stopped for a word.", "나는 단 한 마디도 막힘이 없었다."], ["I told her how I loved her.", "나는 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지 말했다."], ["I told her I should die without her.", "나는 그녀 없이는 죽고 말 거라고 말했다."], ["I told her that I idolized and worshipped her.", "나는 그녀를 신처럼 떠받들고 숭배한다고 말했다."], ["Jip barked madly all the time.", "그동안 집은 미친 듯이 짖어 댔다."]], [["When Dora hung her head and cried, and trembled, my eloquence increased so much the more.", "도라가 고개를 떨구고 울며 떨자, 내 청산유수는 그만큼 더 거세졌다."], ["If she would like me to die for her, she had but to say the word, and I was ready.", "그녀가 자기를 위해 내가 죽기를 바란다면, 한마디만 하면 된다고, 나는 죽을 각오가 되어 있다고 했다."], ["Life without Dora’s love was not a thing to have on any terms.", "도라의 사랑 없는 삶이란 어떤 조건으로도 가질 만한 것이 못 된다고 했다."], ["I couldn’t bear it, and I wouldn’t.", "나는 그것을 견딜 수 없고, 견디지도 않겠노라고 했다."], ["I had loved her every minute, day and night, since I first saw her.", "나는 그녀를 처음 본 순간부터, 밤낮으로, 매 순간 그녀를 사랑해 왔다고 했다."], ["I loved her at that minute to distraction.", "나는 그 순간에도 정신이 나갈 만큼 그녀를 사랑한다고 했다."], ["I should always love her, every minute, to distraction.", "나는 언제까지나, 매 순간, 정신이 나갈 만큼 그녀를 사랑하리라고 했다."], ["Lovers had loved before, and lovers would love again; but no lover had loved, might, could, would, or should ever love, as I loved Dora.", "옛날에도 연인들은 사랑했고, 앞으로도 연인들은 사랑하리라. 그러나 내가 도라를 사랑하듯 사랑한 연인은 없었고, 사랑할 수도, 사랑할 리도, 사랑할 마음도, 사랑해서도 안 되리라고 했다."], ["The more I raved, the more Jip barked.", "내가 더 미쳐 날뛸수록, 집은 더 짖어 댔다."], ["Each of us, in his own way, got more mad every moment.", "우리는 저마다 제 나름의 방식으로, 순간순간 더 미쳐 갔다."]], [["Well, well!", "자, 자!"], ["Dora and I were sitting on the sofa by and by, quiet enough, and Jip was lying in her lap, winking peacefully at me. It was off my mind.", "이윽고 도라와 나는 소파에 나란히 앉아, 제법 차분해져 있었고, 집은 도라의 무릎에 누워 나를 향해 평화롭게 눈을 끔뻑이고 있었다. 짐을 덜었다."], ["I was in a state of perfect rapture.", "나는 더없는 황홀경에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Dora and I were engaged.", "도라와 나는 약혼했다."]], [["I suppose we had some notion that this was to end in marriage.", "우리는 이것이 결혼으로 이어지리라는 막연한 생각쯤은 했던 것 같다."], ["We must have had some, because Dora stipulated that we were never to be married without her papa’s consent.", "분명 했을 것이다. 도라가, 자기 아빠의 동의 없이는 절대 결혼하지 않겠다고 못 박았으니까."], ["But, in our youthful ecstasy, I don’t think that we really looked before us or behind us; or had any aspiration beyond the ignorant present.", "그러나 우리는 젊은 황홀경에 빠져, 정작 앞날을 내다보거나 지난날을 돌아보지는 않았던 것 같다. 철없는 현재 너머의 그 어떤 바람도 품지 않았다."], ["We were to keep our secret from Mr.", "우리는 이 비밀을 스펜로 씨에게 숨기기로 했다."], ["Spenlow; but I am sure the idea never entered my head, then, that there was anything dishonourable in that.", "하지만 분명 그때 나는, 거기에 무슨 떳떳하지 못한 구석이 있다는 생각은 결코 머릿속에 떠올리지 못했다."]], [["Miss Mills was more than usually pensive when Dora, going to find her, brought her back;--I apprehend, because there was a tendency in what had passed to awaken the slumbering echoes in the caverns of Memory.", "도라가 밀스 양을 찾으러 가서 그녀를 데리고 돌아왔을 때, 밀스 양은 여느 때보다 한층 더 수심에 잠겨 있었다 — 짐작건대, 방금 일어난 일에 기억의 동굴 속에 잠든 메아리를 깨우는 구석이 있었기 때문이리라."], ["But she gave us her blessing, and the assurance of her lasting friendship, and spoke to us, generally, as became a Voice from the Cloister.", "그러나 밀스 양은 우리에게 축복을 내려 주고, 변치 않을 우정을 약속해 주었으며, 대체로 수도원에서 들려오는 목소리답게 우리에게 말해 주었다."]], [["What an idle time it was!", "얼마나 한가로운 시절이었던가!"], ["What an insubstantial, happy, foolish time it was!", "얼마나 덧없고, 행복하고, 어리석은 시절이었던가!"]], [["When I measured Dora’s finger for a ring that was to be made of Forget-me-nots, and when the jeweller, to whom I took the measure, found me out, and laughed over his order-book, and charged me anything he liked for the pretty little toy, with its blue stones--so associated in my remembrance with Dora’s hand, that yesterday, when I saw such another, by chance, on the finger of my own daughter, there was a momentary stirring in my heart, like pain!", "물망초로 만들 반지에 맞추려고 내가 도라의 손가락을 쟀을 때, 그리고 그 치수를 가져간 보석상이 내 속을 꿰뚫어 보고 주문 장부를 들여다보며 깔깔 웃고는, 그 파란 보석이 박힌 예쁜 노리개에 제멋대로 값을 매겼을 때 — 그 반지는 내 기억 속에서 도라의 손과 어찌나 깊이 얽혀 있던지, 어제 우연히 내 딸의 손가락에서 그와 똑같은 반지를 보았을 때, 내 가슴속에 한순간, 아픔과도 같은 떨림이 일었다!"]], [["When I walked about, exalted with my secret, and full of my own interest, and felt the dignity of loving Dora, and of being beloved, so much, that if I had walked the air, I could not have been more above the people not so situated, who were creeping on the earth!", "나의 비밀에 들떠, 나 자신에 대한 관심으로 가득 차 거리를 거닐며, 도라를 사랑하는, 또 그토록 사랑받는 그 고귀함을 느꼈을 때 — 어찌나 그러했던지, 설령 내가 허공을 걸었다 한들, 땅바닥을 기어 다니는 그 처지 못한 사람들 위로 그보다 더 높이 솟아오를 수는 없었으리라!"]], [["When we had those meetings in the garden of the square, and sat within the dingy summer-house, so happy, that I love the London sparrows to this hour, for nothing else, and see the plumage of the tropics in their smoky feathers!", "우리가 광장의 정원에서 그 만남을 가지며, 그 칙칙한 정자 안에 앉아 어찌나 행복했던지, 나는 지금 이 순간까지도 다른 까닭은 하나 없이 그저 그 까닭만으로 런던의 참새들을 사랑하고, 그 그을린 깃털 속에서 열대의 깃을 보는 것이다!"], ["When we had our first great quarrel (within a week of our betrothal), and when Dora sent me back the ring, enclosed in a despairing cocked-hat note, wherein she used the terrible expression that ‘our love had begun in folly, and ended in madness!’ which dreadful words occasioned me to tear my hair, and cry that all was over!", "우리가 처음으로 크게 다투었을 때 (약혼한 지 일주일도 안 되어서였다), 그리고 도라가 절망에 찬 삼각 모자 모양으로 접은 쪽지에 반지를 넣어 돌려보냈을 때, 그 쪽지에서 도라는 '우리의 사랑은 어리석음에서 시작되어 광기로 끝났다!'는 그 끔찍한 표현을 썼고, 그 무시무시한 말 때문에 나는 머리를 쥐어뜯으며 모든 게 끝났다고 부르짖었으니!"]], [["When, under cover of the night, I flew to Miss Mills, whom I saw by stealth in a back kitchen where there was a mangle, and implored Miss Mills to interpose between us and avert insanity.", "밤을 틈타 내가 밀스 양에게 달려가, 압착기가 놓인 뒤편 부엌에서 남몰래 그녀를 만나, 우리 사이에 끼어들어 광기를 막아 달라고 애원했을 때."], ["When Miss Mills undertook the office and returned with Dora, exhorting us, from the pulpit of her own bitter youth, to mutual concession, and the avoidance of the Desert of Sahara!", "밀스 양이 그 소임을 떠맡아 도라를 데리고 돌아와, 자신의 쓰라린 젊음이라는 설교단에서, 서로 양보하고 사하라 사막을 피하라고 우리를 타일렀을 때!"]], [["When we cried, and made it up, and were so blest again, that the back kitchen, mangle and all, changed to Love’s own temple, where we arranged a plan of correspondence through Miss Mills, always to comprehend at least one letter on each side every day!", "우리가 울고, 화해하고, 다시 어찌나 큰 축복을 받았던지, 그 뒤편 부엌이, 압착기며 뭐며 죄다, 사랑의 신전으로 바뀌어, 거기서 우리가 밀스 양을 통해 편지를 주고받을 계획을 짰을 때, 날마다 양쪽에서 적어도 한 통씩은 꼭 주고받기로 했을 때!"]], [["What an idle time!", "얼마나 한가로운 시절이었던가!"], ["What an insubstantial, happy, foolish time!", "얼마나 덧없고, 행복하고, 어리석은 시절이었던가!"], ["Of all the times of mine that Time has in his grip, there is none that in one retrospect I can smile at half so much, and think of half so tenderly.", "시간의 손아귀에 쥐어진 내 모든 시절 가운데, 단 한 번 되돌아보아 이만큼 흐뭇하게 미소 지을 수 있고, 이만큼 애틋하게 떠올릴 수 있는 시절은 다시없다."]], [["# CHAPTER 34. MY AUNT ASTONISHES ME", "# CHAPTER 34. MY AUNT ASTONISHES ME"]], [["I wrote to Agnes as soon as Dora and I were engaged.", "도라와 약혼하자마자 나는 곧바로 아그네스에게 편지를 썼다."], ["I wrote her a long letter, in which I tried to make her comprehend how blest I was, and what a darling Dora was.", "긴 편지였는데, 내가 얼마나 큰 축복을 받았는지, 도라가 얼마나 사랑스러운 사람인지를 그녀가 알아주기를 바라며 애써 풀어냈다."], ["I entreated Agnes not to regard this as a thoughtless passion which could ever yield to any other, or had the least resemblance to the boyish fancies that we used to joke about.", "나는 아그네스에게, 이것을 언젠가 다른 무엇에 자리를 내줄 가벼운 열정쯤으로 여기지 말아 달라고, 우리가 예전에 농담거리로 삼곤 했던 풋내기 시절의 들뜬 공상과는 털끝만큼도 닮지 않았다고 간곡히 호소했다."], ["I assured her that its profundity was quite unfathomable, and expressed my belief that nothing like it had ever been known.", "나는 그녀에게 그 깊이가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 깊다고 단언했고, 이런 감정은 일찍이 세상에 알려진 적이 없으리라는 내 믿음을 털어놓았다."]], [["Somehow, as I wrote to Agnes on a fine evening by my open window, and the remembrance of her clear calm eyes and gentle face came stealing over me, it shed such a peaceful influence upon the hurry and agitation in which I had been living lately, and of which my very happiness partook in some degree, that it soothed me into tears.", "어찌 된 일인지, 어느 화창한 저녁 열린 창가에 앉아 아그네스에게 편지를 쓰는 동안, 그 맑고 차분한 눈동자와 부드러운 얼굴에 대한 기억이 슬그머니 내 안으로 스며들면서, 요즘 내가 빠져 살던 그 부산함과 들뜬 마음에 — 내 행복마저도 어느 정도는 거기 물들어 있었는데 — 어찌나 평화로운 기운을 드리워 주던지, 나는 마음이 누그러져 눈물이 났다."], ["I remember that I sat resting my head upon my hand, when the letter was half done, cherishing a general fancy as if Agnes were one of the elements of my natural home.", "편지를 절반쯤 썼을 때, 나는 한 손에 머리를 괴고 앉아, 아그네스가 마치 내 본디 집을 이루는 한 요소이기라도 한 듯한 어렴풋한 환상을 마음속에 품고 있었던 기억이 난다."], ["As if, in the retirement of the house made almost sacred to me by her presence, Dora and I must be happier than anywhere.", "마치 그녀의 존재만으로 거의 신성하게 느껴지는 그 집의 고요함 속에서라면, 도라와 나는 세상 어디에서보다 더 행복할 수밖에 없을 것 같았다."], ["As if, in love, joy, sorrow, hope, or disappointment; in all emotions; my heart turned naturally there, and found its refuge and best friend.", "마치 사랑이든, 기쁨이든, 슬픔이든, 희망이든, 실망이든, 그 어떤 감정 속에서든, 내 마음은 자연스레 그곳으로 향해 거기서 안식처와 가장 좋은 벗을 찾아내는 것만 같았다."]], [["Of Steerforth I said nothing. I only told her there had been sad grief at Yarmouth, on account of Emily’s flight; and that on me it made a double wound, by reason of the circumstances attending it.", "스티어포스에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 다만 에밀리의 도주로 야머스에 깊은 슬픔이 닥쳤다는 것, 그리고 그 일에 얽힌 사정 때문에 나에게는 그것이 이중의 상처가 되었다는 것만 알렸다."], ["I knew how quick she always was to divine the truth, and that she would never be the first to breathe his name.", "나는 그녀가 늘 진실을 얼마나 빨리 알아채는지 알고 있었고, 그녀가 결코 먼저 그 이름을 입에 올리지 않으리라는 것도 알고 있었다."]], [["To this letter, I received an answer by return of post.", "이 편지에 대해, 나는 바로 다음 우편으로 답장을 받았다."], ["As I read it, I seemed to hear Agnes speaking to me.", "그것을 읽노라니, 아그네스가 나에게 말을 거는 소리가 들리는 듯했다."], ["It was like her cordial voice in my ears.", "그 다정한 목소리가 귓가에 울리는 것만 같았다."], ["What can I say more!", "무슨 말을 더 하겠는가!"]], [["While I had been away from home lately, Traddles had called twice or thrice.", "요즘 내가 집을 비운 사이, 트래들스가 두세 번 다녀갔다."], ["Finding Peggotty within, and being informed by Peggotty (who always volunteered that information to whomsoever would receive it), that she was my old nurse, he had established a good-humoured acquaintance with her, and had stayed to have a little chat with her about me.", "그는 안에 패거티가 있는 것을 발견하고, 또 패거티가 (받아 줄 사람만 있으면 누구에게나 자청해서 일러 주던 정보였다) 자기가 내 옛 유모라고 알려 주자, 그녀와 화기애애한 사이가 되어, 나에 대해 잠깐 도란도란 이야기를 나누다 가곤 했다. 패거티 말로는 그랬다."], ["So Peggotty said; but I am afraid the chat was all on her own side, and of immoderate length, as she was very difficult indeed to stop, God bless her! when she had me for her theme.", "하지만 내 짐작에 그 도란도란은 죄다 패거티 혼자만의 것이었고, 또 도가 지나치게 길었을 것이다. 나를 화제로 삼았을 때의 그녀는, 하느님 그녀를 축복하시길, 정말이지 멈추게 하기가 여간 어렵지 않았으니 말이다."]], [["This reminds me, not only that I expected Traddles on a certain afternoon of his own appointing, which was now come, but that Mrs. Crupp had resigned everything appertaining to her office (the salary excepted) until Peggotty should cease to present herself. Mrs.", "이 일로 떠오르는 것은, 트래들스가 직접 정한 어느 날 오후 — 바로 오늘이 그날이었다 — 에 그를 기다리고 있었다는 사실만이 아니라, 크럽 부인이 패거티가 더는 모습을 드러내지 않을 때까지 자기 직무에 딸린 일을 (봉급만 빼고) 죄다 손에서 놓아 버렸다는 사실이었다."], ["Crupp, after holding divers conversations respecting Peggotty, in a very high-pitched voice, on the staircase--with some invisible Familiar it would appear, for corporeally speaking she was quite alone at those times--addressed a letter to me, developing her views.", "크럽 부인은 계단에서 — 겉으로 보아서는 어떤 보이지 않는 정령을 상대로, 그런 때면 육신으로 따져 그녀는 늘 혼자였으므로 — 아주 새된 목소리로 패거티에 관한 여러 대화를 나눈 끝에, 자기 견해를 펼쳐 보이는 편지 한 통을 나에게 보냈다."], ["Beginning it with that statement of universal application, which fitted every occurrence of her life, namely, that she was a mother herself, she went on to inform me that she had once seen very different days, but that at all periods of her existence she had had a constitutional objection to spies, intruders, and informers.", "그 편지는 그녀의 인생 모든 일에 두루 들어맞는, 어디에나 통하는 그 진술로 시작했는데, 곧 자기도 한 사람의 어미라는 것이었다. 이어서 그녀는, 자기도 한때는 지금과 사뭇 다른 시절을 누려 본 적이 있으되, 평생 어느 시기에도 첩자며 침입자며 밀고자라면 천성적으로 질색이었다고 알려 주었다. 이름을 대지는 않겠노라고 그녀는 말했다."], ["She named no names, she said; let them the cap fitted, wear it; but spies, intruders, and informers, especially in widders’ weeds (this clause was underlined), she had ever accustomed herself to look down upon.", "모자가 맞는 자가 있거든 알아서 쓰면 그만이라고. 하지만 첩자며 침입자며 밀고자, 특히 과부의 상복을 걸친 자들이라면 (이 구절에는 밑줄이 그어져 있었다) 자기는 늘 멸시하는 데 이골이 나 있었다고 했다."], ["If a gentleman was the victim of spies, intruders, and informers (but still naming no names), that was his own pleasure.", "만약 어떤 신사가 첩자며 침입자며 밀고자의 희생양이 된다면 (그래도 이름은 대지 않았다), 그건 그 신사의 취향이라는 것이었다."], ["He had a right to please himself; so let him do. All that she, Mrs.", "그에게는 제 좋을 대로 할 권리가 있으니, 마음대로 하시라는 거였다."], ["Crupp, stipulated for, was, that she should not be ‘brought in contract’ with such persons.", "다만 자기, 크럽 부인이 못 박아 두고 싶은 것은, 자기가 그런 자들과 '결탁'당하는 일은 없어야 한다는 것뿐이라고 했다."], ["Therefore she begged to be excused from any further attendance on the top set, until things were as they formerly was, and as they could be wished to be; and further mentioned that her little book would be found upon the breakfast-table every Saturday morning, when she requested an immediate settlement of the same, with the benevolent view of saving trouble ‘and an ill-conwenience’ to all parties.", "그러므로 그녀는, 모든 일이 예전 같아지고 또 마땅히 바랄 만한 상태로 돌아갈 때까지, 꼭대기 층 살림을 더는 돌보지 못함을 양해해 달라고 청했다. 덧붙여, 토요일 아침마다 자기 작은 외상장부가 식탁 위에 놓여 있을 터이니 즉시 정산해 주기를 바란다고, 모든 당사자의 수고와 '불편함'을 덜어 주려는 갸륵한 뜻에서 그리한다고 적었다."]], [["After this, Mrs. Crupp confined herself to making pitfalls on the stairs, principally with pitchers, and endeavouring to delude Peggotty into breaking her legs.", "이런 일이 있은 뒤로, 크럽 부인은 계단에 함정을 파는 일에만, 그것도 주로 물주전자를 동원해 매달리며, 패거티를 꾀어 다리라도 부러뜨려 보려고 갖은 애를 썼다."], ["I found it rather harassing to live in this state of siege, but was too much afraid of Mrs. Crupp to see any way out of it.", "나는 이런 포위 상태로 살아가는 것이 꽤나 괴로웠지만, 크럽 부인이 너무도 무서운 탓에 거기서 벗어날 길을 도무지 찾지 못했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ cried Traddles, punctually appearing at my door, in spite of all these obstacles, ‘how do you do?’", "\"이보게 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 이 온갖 장애물에도 아랑곳없이 정확히 제시간에 내 집 문 앞에 나타난 트래들스가 외쳤다. \"잘 지내나?\""]], [["‘My dear Traddles,’ said I, ‘I am delighted to see you at last, and very sorry I have not been at home before.", "\"이보게 친애하는 트래들스,\" 내가 말했다. \"드디어 자네를 보게 되어 정말 반갑네."], ["But I have been so much engaged--’", "그리고 진작 집에 있지 못해서 정말 미안하이. 그런데 내가 워낙 정신없이 바빴던 터라—\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, I know,’ said Traddles, ‘of course.", "\"그래, 그래, 알지.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"물론이고말고."], ["Yours lives in London, I think.’", "자네 사람은 런던에 산다지, 아마?\""]], [["‘What did you say?’", "\"방금 뭐라고 했나?\""]], [["‘She--excuse me--Miss D., you know,’ said Traddles, colouring in his great delicacy, ‘lives in London, I believe?’", "\"그녀가 — 아 실례하네 — 그러니까 디 양 말일세,\" 트래들스가 그 지극한 조심성으로 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"런던에 산다지, 내가 알기로는?\""]], [["‘Oh yes.", "\"아 그래."], ["Near London.’", "런던 근처라네.\""]], [["‘Mine, perhaps you recollect,’ said Traddles, with a serious look, ‘lives down in Devonshire--one of ten.", "\"내 사람은, 자네도 기억할는지 모르겠네만,\" 트래들스가 자못 진지한 표정으로 말했다. \"데번셔 시골에 산다네 — 열 남매 중 하나지."], ["Consequently, I am not so much engaged as you--in that sense.’", "그러니 나는 자네만큼 바쁘질 못해 — 그런 뜻에서 말일세.\""]], [["‘I wonder you can bear,’ I returned, ‘to see her so seldom.’", "\"그렇게 어쩌다 한 번씩밖에 못 보면서 어떻게 견디나,\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘Hah!’ said Traddles, thoughtfully.", "\"하!\" 트래들스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."], ["‘It does seem a wonder.", "\"그게 참 신기하긴 하지."], ["I suppose it is, Copperfield, because there is no help for it?’", "아마도 그건, 코퍼필드, 달리 어쩔 도리가 없어서일 거야, 그렇지 않겠나?\""]], [["‘I suppose so,’ I replied with a smile, and not without a blush.", "\"그렇겠지.\" 나는 미소 띤 채, 얼굴을 살짝 붉히며 대답했다."], ["‘And because you have so much constancy and patience, Traddles.’", "\"그리고 자네에게 한결같음과 인내심이 그토록 많아서이기도 하겠고, 트래들스.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said Traddles, considering about it, ‘do I strike you in that way, Copperfield?", "\"아이고 이런!\" 트래들스가 그 말을 곱씹으며 말했다. \"내가 자네 눈에 그렇게 보이나, 코퍼필드?"], ["Really I didn’t know that I had. But she is such an extraordinarily dear girl herself, that it’s possible she may have imparted something of those virtues to me.", "정말이지 나한테 그런 게 있는 줄은 몰랐는걸. 하지만 그녀 자신이 워낙 더없이 사랑스러운 처자라서, 어쩌면 그 미덕을 나한테도 조금 옮겨 주었는지 모르지."], ["Now you mention it, Copperfield, I shouldn’t wonder at all.", "자네가 그렇게 말하니, 코퍼필드, 그래도 조금도 이상할 게 없겠어."], ["I assure you she is always forgetting herself, and taking care of the other nine.’", "정말이지 그녀는 늘 자기 자신은 잊은 채 나머지 아홉을 돌본다네.\""]], [["‘Is she the eldest?’ I inquired.", "\"그녀가 맏이인가?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh dear, no,’ said Traddles.", "\"아이고 저런, 아닐세.\""], ["‘The eldest is a Beauty.’", "트래들스가 말했다. \"맏이는 미인이지.\""]], [["He saw, I suppose, that I could not help smiling at the simplicity of this reply; and added, with a smile upon his own ingenuous face:", "내 짐작에 그는, 내가 그 천진한 대답에 웃음을 참지 못하는 것을 본 모양이었다. 그래서 자기 순박한 얼굴에도 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Not, of course, but that my Sophy--pretty name, Copperfield, I always think?’", "\"물론, 내 소피가 — 예쁜 이름이지, 코퍼필드, 난 늘 그렇게 생각한다네 —\""]], [["‘Very pretty!’ said I.", "\"아주 예쁜 이름이야!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Not, of course, but that Sophy is beautiful too in my eyes, and would be one of the dearest girls that ever was, in anybody’s eyes (I should think).", "\"물론, 내 눈에는 소피도 아름답고, 또 (내 생각엔) 누구의 눈에 비추어 봐도 일찍이 둘도 없을 만큼 사랑스러운 처자임에는 틀림없지. 하지만 내가 맏이를 미인이라고 할 때는, 정말이지 그녀가 —\""], ["But when I say the eldest is a Beauty, I mean she really is a--’ he seemed to be describing clouds about himself, with both hands: ‘Splendid, you know,’ said Traddles, energetically.", "그는 두 손으로 제 둘레에 구름이라도 그려 보이는 듯한 시늉을 했다. \"눈부시다, 그런 뜻일세.\" 트래들스가 힘주어 말했다. \"허허!\""], ["‘Indeed!’ said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, I assure you,’ said Traddles, ‘something very uncommon, indeed!", "\"오, 정말이라니까,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"정말이지 보기 드문 미인이야!"], ["Then, you know, being formed for society and admiration, and not being able to enjoy much of it in consequence of their limited means, she naturally gets a little irritable and exacting, sometimes. Sophy puts her in good humour!’", "그런데 알다시피, 사교계와 뭇사람의 찬사를 받도록 태어났으면서도, 형편이 넉넉지 못한 탓에 그런 걸 마음껏 누리지 못하니, 자연히 가끔은 좀 짜증을 내고 까다롭게 굴기도 한다네. 그러면 소피가 그녀의 기분을 풀어 주지!\""]], [["‘Is Sophy the youngest?’ I hazarded.", "\"소피가 막내인가?\" 내가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["‘Oh dear, no!’ said Traddles, stroking his chin.", "\"아이고 저런, 아닐세!\" 트래들스가 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말했다."], ["‘The two youngest are only nine and ten.", "\"막내 둘은 겨우 아홉 살, 열 살이라네."], ["Sophy educates ‘em.’", "소피가 그 애들을 가르치지.\""]], [["‘The second daughter, perhaps?’ I hazarded.", "\"그럼 혹시 둘째인가?\" 내가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ said Traddles.", "\"아니야.\""], ["‘Sarah’s the second.", "트래들스가 말했다."], ["Sarah has something the matter with her spine, poor girl.", "\"둘째는 새러라네. 새러는 가엾게도 척추에 무슨 탈이 있어."], ["The malady will wear out by and by, the doctors say, but in the meantime she has to lie down for a twelvemonth. Sophy nurses her.", "그 병은 차차 나아질 거라고 의사들이 그러긴 하는데, 그동안은 일 년쯤 누워 지내야 한다네. 소피가 그 애를 간병하지."], ["Sophy’s the fourth.’", "소피는 넷째라네.\""]], [["‘Is the mother living?’ I inquired.", "\"어머니는 살아 계신가?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh yes,’ said Traddles, ‘she is alive.", "\"아 그럼,\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["She is a very superior woman indeed, but the damp country is not adapted to her constitution, and--in fact, she has lost the use of her limbs.’", "\"살아 계시지. 정말이지 아주 훌륭한 분이신데, 다만 그 축축한 시골이 체질에 맞질 않아서 — 사실은, 손발을 못 쓰게 되셨다네.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said I.", "\"이런 저런!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Very sad, is it not?’ returned Traddles.", "\"무척 딱한 일이지, 그렇지 않나?\" 트래들스가 받았다."], ["‘But in a merely domestic view it is not so bad as it might be, because Sophy takes her place.", "\"하지만 그저 살림이라는 관점에서만 보면 그렇게까지 나쁘진 않다네. 소피가 그 자리를 대신해 주니까."], ["She is quite as much a mother to her mother, as she is to the other nine.’", "그녀는 어머니에게도 어머니나 다름없고, 나머지 아홉에게도 어머니나 다름없거든.\""]], [["I felt the greatest admiration for the virtues of this young lady; and, honestly with the view of doing my best to prevent the good-nature of Traddles from being imposed upon, to the detriment of their joint prospects in life, inquired how Mr. Micawber was?", "나는 이 젊은 처자의 미덕에 더없는 감탄을 느꼈다. 그리고, 진심으로, 두 사람이 함께 꾸려 갈 인생의 앞날을 해치면서까지 트래들스의 그 선량함이 이용당하는 것을 막아 보려는 마음에서, 미코버 씨는 어떻게 지내는지 물었다."]], [["‘He is quite well, Copperfield, thank you,’ said Traddles.", "\"아주 잘 지내신다네, 코퍼필드, 물어봐 줘서 고맙군.\""], ["‘I am not living with him at present.’\n‘No?’", "트래들스가 말했다. \"지금은 내가 그분과 함께 살지 않아.\""]], [["‘No. You see the truth is,’ said Traddles, in a whisper, ‘he had changed his name to Mortimer, in consequence of his temporary embarrassments; and he don’t come out till after dark--and then in spectacles.", "\"아니라고?\" \"그래. 실은 말일세,\""], ["There was an execution put into our house, for rent. Mrs.", "트래들스가 속삭이듯 말했다. \"그분이 일시적인 곤경 때문에 이름을 모티머로 바꾸셨다네."], ["Micawber was in such a dreadful state that I really couldn’t resist giving my name to that second bill we spoke of here.", "그리고 날이 어두워진 뒤에야 밖에 나오시고 — 그것도 안경을 쓰고 말이야. 우리 집에 집세 때문에 압류가 들어왔거든."], ["You may imagine how delightful it was to my feelings, Copperfield, to see the matter settled with it, and Mrs. Micawber recover her spirits.’", "미코버 부인이 어찌나 끔찍한 상태였던지, 나는 도무지 거절할 수가 없어서 여기서 우리가 이야기했던 그 두 번째 어음에 내 이름을 빌려 주고 말았지. 코퍼필드, 그 일이 그걸로 해결되고 미코버 부인이 기운을 되찾는 걸 보았을 때 내 마음이 얼마나 흐뭇했는지 자네도 짐작이 갈 걸세.\""]], [["‘Hum!’ said I.", "\"흠!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Not that her happiness was of long duration,’ pursued Traddles, ‘for, unfortunately, within a week another execution came in.", "\"그렇다고 그분의 행복이 오래간 건 아니라네,\" 트래들스가 말을 이었다. \"불행히도, 일주일도 안 돼서 또 다른 압류가 들어왔거든."], ["It broke up the establishment.", "그 바람에 살림이 통째로 거덜 났지."], ["I have been living in a furnished apartment since then, and the Mortimers have been very private indeed.", "그 뒤로 나는 가구 딸린 셋방에서 지내고 있고, 모티머 씨네는 정말이지 아주 은밀하게 숨어 지내고 있다네."], ["I hope you won’t think it selfish, Copperfield, if I mention that the broker carried off my little round table with the marble top, and Sophy’s flower-pot and stand?’", "코퍼필드, 내가 이런 말을 한다고 이기적이라 여기진 말아 주게나. 그 거간꾼이 대리석 상판이 달린 내 작은 둥근 탁자며 소피의 화분이랑 받침대까지 죄다 가져가 버렸지 뭔가?\""]], [["‘What a hard thing!’ I exclaimed indignantly.", "\"그것참 너무한 일이로군!\" 나는 분개하여 외쳤다."]], [["‘It was a--it was a pull,’ said Traddles, with his usual wince at that expression.", "\"그건 — 그건 참 곤혹스러운 일이었지.\""], ["‘I don’t mention it reproachfully, however, but with a motive.", "트래들스가 그 표현이 나올 때면 늘 그러듯 움찔하며 말했다."], ["The fact is, Copperfield, I was unable to repurchase them at the time of their seizure; in the first place, because the broker, having an idea that I wanted them, ran the price up to an extravagant extent; and, in the second place, because I--hadn’t any money.", "\"그래도 나는 원망하는 마음에서가 아니라, 다 까닭이 있어서 이 얘기를 꺼내는 걸세. 사실은 말이야, 코퍼필드, 그게 압류당했을 때 나는 그걸 되살 형편이 못 됐다네. 첫째로는, 거간꾼이 내가 그걸 원한다는 낌새를 챘는지 값을 터무니없이 올려 버려서였고, 둘째로는, 내게 — 돈이 한 푼도 없어서였지."], ["Now, I have kept my eye since, upon the broker’s shop,’ said Traddles, with a great enjoyment of his mystery, ‘which is up at the top of Tottenham Court Road, and, at last, today I find them put out for sale.", "그런데 그 뒤로 내가 줄곧 그 거간꾼 가게를 눈여겨봐 왔거든,\" 트래들스가 제 비밀스러운 계획을 무척이나 즐기며 말했다. \"그 가게가 토트넘 코트 로드 꼭대기에 있는데, 마침내 오늘, 그 물건들이 다시 팔려고 내놓인 걸 발견했지 뭔가."], ["I have only noticed them from over the way, because if the broker saw me, bless you, he’d ask any price for them!", "길 건너에서만 슬쩍 봤는데, 거간꾼이 나를 봤다간, 아이고, 부르는 게 값이 될 게 뻔하니까 말일세!"], ["What has occurred to me, having now the money, is, that perhaps you wouldn’t object to ask that good nurse of yours to come with me to the shop--I can show it her from round the corner of the next street--and make the best bargain for them, as if they were for herself, that she can!’", "이제 돈이 생겼으니 내가 떠올린 생각은 이거라네. 혹시 자네 그 착한 유모님에게 부탁해서 나랑 같이 그 가게에 가 달라고 해 줄 수 있겠나 — 다음 골목 모퉁이에서 가게를 가리켜 보여 드릴 테니 — 그래서 마치 자기가 쓸 물건인 양, 되도록 값을 잘 깎아서 사 주실 수 있다면 어떨까 싶어서!\""]], [["The delight with which Traddles propounded this plan to me, and the sense he had of its uncommon artfulness, are among the freshest things in my remembrance.", "트래들스가 이 계획을 나에게 털어놓을 때의 그 기쁨이며, 그 계획이 어지간히 영리하다고 여기던 그 흐뭇함은, 내 기억 속에서 가장 생생하게 남아 있는 것들 가운데 하나다."]], [["I told him that my old nurse would be delighted to assist him, and that we would all three take the field together, but on one condition.", "나는 그에게, 우리 옛 유모가 기꺼이 도와줄 것이고, 우리 셋이서 함께 나서겠노라고 말했다. 다만 한 가지 조건이 있었다."], ["That condition was, that he should make a solemn resolution to grant no more loans of his name, or anything else, to Mr. Micawber.", "그 조건이란, 그가 다시는 자기 이름이든 그 무엇이든 미코버 씨에게 빌려 주지 않겠노라고 엄숙히 결심해야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, ‘I have already done so, because I begin to feel that I have not only been inconsiderate, but that I have been positively unjust to Sophy.", "\"이보게 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"나는 이미 그렇게 결심했다네."], ["My word being passed to myself, there is no longer any apprehension; but I pledge it to you, too, with the greatest readiness.", "내가 그동안 경솔했을 뿐만 아니라, 소피에게 정말이지 부당하게 굴었구나 하는 생각이 들기 시작했거든. 나 자신에게 약속을 했으니 더는 걱정할 일이 없네만, 자네에게도 더없이 기꺼이 그 약속을 맹세하겠네."], ["That first unlucky obligation, I have paid.", "그 첫 번째 운 나쁜 빚은 내가 갚았어."], ["I have no doubt Mr. Micawber would have paid it if he could, but he could not.", "미코버 씨도 갚을 수 있었다면 분명 갚았을 텐데, 그러질 못한 것뿐이지."], ["One thing I ought to mention, which I like very much in Mr. Micawber, Copperfield.", "한 가지 꼭 말해 두고 싶은 게 있는데, 미코버 씨한테는 내가 무척 마음에 드는 점이 있다네, 코퍼필드."], ["It refers to the second obligation, which is not yet due.", "그건 아직 만기가 안 된 두 번째 빚에 관한 건데 말이야."], ["He don’t tell me that it is provided for, but he says it WILL BE.", "그분은 그게 다 마련돼 있다고는 안 하셔. 다만 마련될 것이라고 하시지."], ["Now, I think there is something very fair and honest about that!’", "그런데 내 생각에 거기엔 뭔가 아주 공정하고 정직한 데가 있단 말일세!\""]], [["I was unwilling to damp my good friend’s confidence, and therefore assented.", "나는 이 착한 친구의 믿음에 찬물을 끼얹고 싶지 않았으므로, 그저 맞장구를 쳤다."], ["After a little further conversation, we went round to the chandler’s shop, to enlist Peggotty; Traddles declining to pass the evening with me, both because he endured the liveliest apprehensions that his property would be bought by somebody else before he could re-purchase it, and because it was the evening he always devoted to writing to the dearest girl in the world.", "잠깐 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 우리는 패거티를 끌어들이려고 잡화점으로 향했다. 트래들스는 나와 함께 저녁을 보내기를 사양했는데, 자기가 그 물건들을 되사기도 전에 누가 채 가 버릴까 봐 마음이 잔뜩 조마조마했던 데다가, 그날 저녁은 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 처자에게 편지를 쓰는 날로 늘 정해 두었기 때문이었다."]], [["I never shall forget him peeping round the corner of the street in Tottenham Court Road, while Peggotty was bargaining for the precious articles; or his agitation when she came slowly towards us after vainly offering a price, and was hailed by the relenting broker, and went back again.", "패거티가 그 소중한 물건들 값을 흥정하는 동안, 토트넘 코트 로드의 길모퉁이를 돌아 빠끔히 내다보던 그의 모습을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 또 그녀가 값을 불렀다가 헛물을 켜고 우리 쪽으로 천천히 걸어오는데, 마음이 누그러진 거간꾼이 그녀를 불러 세워 다시 되돌아가던 그때의 그의 안절부절못하던 모습도."], ["The end of the negotiation was, that she bought the property on tolerably easy terms, and Traddles was transported with pleasure.", "흥정의 끝은, 그녀가 그런대로 수월한 값에 그 물건들을 사들였다는 것이었고, 트래들스는 기쁨에 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."]], [["‘I am very much obliged to you, indeed,’ said Traddles, on hearing it was to be sent to where he lived, that night.", "\"정말이지 자네한테 신세를 많이 졌네.\" 그날 밤 그 물건들을 자기가 사는 곳으로 보내 주기로 했다는 말을 듣고 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘If I might ask one other favour, I hope you would not think it absurd, Copperfield?’", "\"혹시 한 가지 부탁을 더 드려도 될까, 코퍼필드, 터무니없다고 여기진 않았으면 좋겠는데?\""]], [["I said beforehand, certainly not.", "나는 미리, 천만에, 결코 그렇지 않다고 말했다."]], [["‘Then if you WOULD be good enough,’ said Traddles to Peggotty, ‘to get the flower-pot now, I think I should like (it being Sophy’s, Copperfield) to carry it home myself!’", "\"그럼 친절을 베풀어,\" 트래들스가 패거티에게 말했다. \"지금 그 화분만 좀 받아 주시겠어요? 제 생각엔 (그게 소피 거라서요, 코퍼필드) 그건 제가 직접 집까지 들고 가고 싶거든요!\""]], [["Peggotty was glad to get it for him, and he overwhelmed her with thanks, and went his way up Tottenham Court Road, carrying the flower-pot affectionately in his arms, with one of the most delighted expressions of countenance I ever saw.", "패거티는 기꺼이 그것을 받아 그에게 건네주었고, 그는 고맙다는 인사를 잔뜩 퍼붓고는, 그 화분을 두 팔에 다정하게 끌어안은 채, 내가 일찍이 본 가운데 가장 기쁜 표정 가운데 하나를 지으며 토트넘 코트 로드를 따라 제 갈 길로 올라갔다."]], [["We then turned back towards my chambers.", "그러고 나서 우리는 내 거처 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["As the shops had charms for Peggotty which I never knew them possess in the same degree for anybody else, I sauntered easily along, amused by her staring in at the windows, and waiting for her as often as she chose.", "패거티에게는 상점들이 어찌나 매력적으로 보였던지, 나는 다른 누구에게서도 그만큼은 본 적이 없을 정도였는데, 나는 어슬렁어슬렁 느긋이 걸으며, 그녀가 진열창을 들여다보며 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨는 모습을 재미있어하고, 그녀가 원할 때마다 몇 번이고 기다려 주었다."], ["We were thus a good while in getting to the Adelphi.", "그렇게 우리는 애덜파이에 이르기까지 꽤 오랜 시간이 걸렸다."]], [["On our way upstairs, I called her attention to the sudden disappearance of Mrs. Crupp’s pitfalls, and also to the prints of recent footsteps.", "계단을 올라가다가, 나는 그녀에게 크럽 부인의 함정들이 갑자기 사라진 것과, 또 방금 누가 다녀간 듯한 발자국 자국들을 알려 주었다."], ["We were both very much surprised, coming higher up, to find my outer door standing open (which I had shut) and to hear voices inside.", "우리는 더 위로 올라가다가, 내가 분명히 닫아 두었던 바깥문이 활짝 열려 있고 안에서 사람 목소리가 들리는 것을 발견하고는 둘 다 무척 놀랐다."]], [["We looked at one another, without knowing what to make of this, and went into the sitting-room. What was my amazement to find, of all people upon earth, my aunt there, and Mr. Dick!", "우리는 영문을 모른 채 서로를 마주 보고는 거실로 들어갔다. 세상 어느 누구도 아닌, 바로 우리 고모님과 딕 씨가 거기 있는 것을 발견했을 때 내 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가!"], ["My aunt sitting on a quantity of luggage, with her two birds before her, and her cat on her knee, like a female Robinson Crusoe, drinking tea. Mr.", "고모님은 한 무더기의 짐 위에 앉아, 앞에 새 두 마리를 두고, 무릎에는 고양이를 올려놓은 채, 마치 여자 로빈슨 크루소처럼 차를 마시고 있었다."], ["Dick leaning thoughtfully on a great kite, such as we had often been out together to fly, with more luggage piled about him!", "딕 씨는, 우리가 함께 곧잘 띄우러 나가곤 했던 그런 커다란 연에 생각에 잠긴 듯 기대선 채, 그 둘레에 더 많은 짐을 잔뜩 쌓아 두고 있었다!"]], [["‘My dear aunt!’ cried I.", "\"고모님!\" 내가 외쳤다."], ["‘Why, what an unexpected pleasure!’", "\"아니, 이게 웬 뜻밖의 기쁨이에요!\""]], [["We cordially embraced; and Mr.", "우리는 다정하게 끌어안았다."], ["Dick and I cordially shook hands; and Mrs.", "그리고 딕 씨와 나도 다정하게 악수를 나누었다."], ["Crupp, who was busy making tea, and could not be too attentive, cordially said she had knowed well as Mr. Copperfull would have his heart in his mouth, when he see his dear relations.", "한창 차를 끓이느라 분주하던 크럽 부인은, 더없이 살뜰하게 굴며, 코퍼풀 씨가 사랑하는 친척을 보면 가슴이 벅차오를 줄 진작 알고 있었노라고 다정스레 말했다."]], [["‘Holloa!’ said my aunt to Peggotty, who quailed before her awful presence.", "\"이봐!\" 고모님이, 그 위압적인 존재 앞에서 잔뜩 움츠러든 패거티에게 말했다."], ["‘How are YOU?’", "\"자넨 어떻게 지내나?\""]], [["‘You remember my aunt, Peggotty?’ said I.", "\"고모님, 패거티 기억하시죠?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘For the love of goodness, child,’ exclaimed my aunt, ‘don’t call the woman by that South Sea Island name!", "\"맙소사, 얘야,\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"그 사람을 그런 남태평양 섬나라 이름으로 부르지 좀 마라!"], ["If she married and got rid of it, which was the best thing she could do, why don’t you give her the benefit of the change?", "결혼해서 그 이름을 떼어 냈다면, 그게 제일 잘한 일인데, 왜 그 바뀐 덕을 못 보게 한단 말이냐?"], ["What’s your name now,--P?’ said my aunt, as a compromise for the obnoxious appellation.", "이제 자네 이름이 뭔가 — 피?\" 고모님은 그 거슬리는 호칭을 절충해 보려고 그렇게 물었다."]], [["‘Barkis, ma’am,’ said Peggotty, with a curtsey.", "\"바키스예요, 마님.\" 패거티가 무릎을 살짝 굽히며 말했다."]], [["‘Well!", "\"옳지!"], ["That’s human,’ said my aunt.", "그게 사람 이름답구먼,\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘It sounds less as if you wanted a missionary.", "\"선교사라도 보내 달라는 것처럼 들리진 않으니 말이야."], ["How d’ye do, Barkis?", "바키스, 어떻게 지내나?"], ["I hope you’re well?’", "잘 지내고 있겠지?\""]], [["Encouraged by these gracious words, and by my aunt’s extending her hand, Barkis came forward, and took the hand, and curtseyed her acknowledgements.", "이 너그러운 말에, 또 고모님이 손을 내미는 데 용기를 얻은 바키스는 앞으로 나아가, 그 손을 잡고, 무릎을 굽혀 고마움을 표했다."]], [["‘We are older than we were, I see,’ said my aunt.", "\"우리 둘 다 예전보다 나이를 먹었구먼,\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘We have only met each other once before, you know.", "\"알다시피 우리가 전에 만난 건 딱 한 번뿐이었지."], ["A nice business we made of it then!", "그때 우리 둘이 참 잘도 일을 그르쳐 놓았더랬지!"], ["Trot, my dear, another cup.’", "트롯, 얘야, 한 잔 더 다오.\""]], [["I handed it dutifully to my aunt, who was in her usual inflexible state of figure; and ventured a remonstrance with her on the subject of her sitting on a box.", "나는 공손히 고모님께 찻잔을 건넸다. 고모님은 여느 때처럼 꼿꼿하기 그지없는 자세였는데, 나는 큰맘 먹고 상자 위에 앉아 계신 일에 대해 한마디 여쭈었다."]], [["‘Let me draw the sofa here, or the easy-chair, aunt,’ said I.", "\"제가 소파를 이리로 끌어 올게요, 아니면 안락의자라도요, 고모님,\""], ["‘Why should you be so uncomfortable?’", "내가 말했다. \"왜 그렇게 불편하게 계세요?\""]], [["‘Thank you, Trot,’ replied my aunt, ‘I prefer to sit upon my property.’ Here my aunt looked hard at Mrs.", "\"고맙다, 트롯,\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"난 내 재산 위에 앉아 있는 게 더 좋다.\""], ["Crupp, and observed, ‘We needn’t trouble you to wait, ma’am.’", "여기서 고모님은 크럽 부인을 매섭게 쏘아보며 한마디 했다. \"더는 수고스럽게 시중들 것 없네, 부인.\""]], [["‘Shall I put a little more tea in the pot afore I go, ma’am?’ said Mrs. Crupp.", "\"가기 전에 찻주전자에 차를 조금 더 넣어 드릴까요, 마님?\" 크럽 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘No, I thank you, ma’am,’ replied my aunt.", "\"아니, 됐네, 부인.\" 고모님이 대답했다."]], [["‘Would you let me fetch another pat of butter, ma’am?’ said Mrs. Crupp.", "\"버터를 한 덩이 더 가져다 드릴까요, 마님?\" 크럽 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Or would you be persuaded to try a new-laid hegg? or should I brile a rasher?", "\"아니면 갓 낳은 달걀이라도 한번 드셔 보시겠어요? 아니면 베이컨이라도 한 점 구워 올릴까요?"], ["Ain’t there nothing I could do for your dear aunt, Mr. Copperfull?’", "코퍼풀 씨, 사랑하는 고모님을 위해 제가 해 드릴 게 정말 아무것도 없을까요?\""]], [["‘Nothing, ma’am,’ returned my aunt.", "\"아무것도 없네, 부인,\" 고모님이 받았다."], ["‘I shall do very well, I thank you.’", "\"나는 아주 잘 지낼 테니, 고맙네.\""]], [["Mrs. Crupp, who had been incessantly smiling to express sweet temper, and incessantly holding her head on one side, to express a general feebleness of constitution, and incessantly rubbing her hands, to express a desire to be of service to all deserving objects, gradually smiled herself, one-sided herself, and rubbed herself, out of the room.", "크럽 부인은, 상냥한 성품을 드러내려고 쉴 새 없이 미소를 짓고, 타고난 허약한 체질을 드러내려고 쉴 새 없이 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고, 도움받을 자격 있는 모든 이에게 이바지하고 싶은 마음을 드러내려고 쉴 새 없이 두 손을 비비더니, 차츰차츰 미소를 짓고, 고개를 갸웃하고, 손을 비비며 방 밖으로 사라져 갔다. \"딕!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Dick!’ said my aunt. ‘You know what I told you about time-servers and wealth-worshippers?’", "\"내가 자네한테 시류에 빌붙는 자들이며 재물을 떠받드는 자들에 대해 한 얘기 기억하지?\""]], [["Mr. Dick--with rather a scared look, as if he had forgotten it--returned a hasty answer in the affirmative.", "딕 씨는 — 그걸 깜빡 잊기라도 한 듯 다소 겁먹은 표정으로 — 그렇다는 대답을 황급히 내놓았다."]], [["‘Mrs. Crupp is one of them,’ said my aunt.", "\"크럽 부인이 바로 그런 자들 중 하나야,\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Barkis, I’ll trouble you to look after the tea, and let me have another cup, for I don’t fancy that woman’s pouring-out!’", "\"바키스, 미안하지만 차 좀 맡아 줘. 그리고 한 잔 더 따라 줘. 저 여자가 따라 주는 건 영 내키지 않으니 말이야!\""]], [["I knew my aunt sufficiently well to know that she had something of importance on her mind, and that there was far more matter in this arrival than a stranger might have supposed.", "나는 고모님을 충분히 잘 알고 있었으므로, 고모님 마음속에 뭔가 중대한 일이 자리 잡고 있다는 것, 그리고 이 갑작스러운 방문에는 모르는 사람이 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 깊은 사연이 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["I noticed how her eye lighted on me, when she thought my attention otherwise occupied; and what a curious process of hesitation appeared to be going on within her, while she preserved her outward stiffness and composure.", "나는, 내 주의가 딴 데 쏠려 있다고 여길 때면 고모님의 눈길이 어떻게 내게로 와 닿는지를 눈여겨보았다. 또 겉으로는 그 꼿꼿함과 침착함을 지키면서도, 그 속에서는 어떤 묘한 망설임의 과정이 진행되고 있는 듯한 것도 알아챘다."], ["I began to reflect whether I had done anything to offend her; and my conscience whispered me that I had not yet told her about Dora.", "나는 혹시 내가 고모님의 비위를 거스를 무슨 일이라도 한 건 아닌가 곰곰이 돌이켜 보기 시작했다. 그러자 양심이 나에게, 아직 고모님께 도라 이야기를 하지 않았다고 속삭였다."], ["Could it by any means be that, I wondered!", "혹시 어쩌다 그것 때문일까, 하고 나는 의아해했다!"]], [["As I knew she would only speak in her own good time, I sat down near her, and spoke to the birds, and played with the cat, and was as easy as I could be.", "나는 고모님이 적당한 때가 되어야만 입을 열리라는 것을 알았으므로, 고모님 곁에 앉아, 새들에게 말을 걸고, 고양이를 어르며, 최대한 마음 편한 척했다."], ["But I was very far from being really easy; and I should still have been so, even if Mr. Dick, leaning over the great kite behind my aunt, had not taken every secret opportunity of shaking his head darkly at me, and pointing at her.", "그러나 나는 진짜로 마음이 편한 것과는 거리가 멀었다. 그리고 고모님 뒤편에서 그 커다란 연에 기대선 딕 씨가, 틈만 나면 몰래 나를 향해 음울하게 고개를 가로젓고 고모님을 손가락으로 가리키지 않았더라도, 나는 여전히 마음이 편치 못했을 것이다."]], [["‘Trot,’ said my aunt at last, when she had finished her tea, and carefully smoothed down her dress, and wiped her lips--‘you needn’t go, Barkis!--Trot, have you got to be firm and self-reliant?’", "\"트롯,\" 마침내 고모님이 차를 다 마시고, 옷자락을 정성스레 매만지고, 입술을 닦은 뒤 말했다. \"자넨 안 가도 되네, 바키스! 트롯, 너는 이제 꿋꿋하고 자기 힘으로 설 줄 알게 되었느냐?\""]], [["‘I hope so, aunt.’", "\"그러길 바라요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘What do you think?’ inquired Miss Betsey.", "\"네 생각엔 어떠냐?\" 베시 양이 물었다."]], [["‘I think so, aunt.’", "\"그런 것 같아요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘Then why, my love,’ said my aunt, looking earnestly at me, ‘why do you think I prefer to sit upon this property of mine tonight?’", "\"그럼 어째서, 얘야,\" 고모님이 나를 진지하게 바라보며 말했다. \"어째서 내가 오늘 밤 이 내 재산 위에 앉아 있기를 더 좋아한다고 보느냐?\""]], [["I shook my head, unable to guess.", "나는 짐작할 수 없어 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘Because,’ said my aunt, ‘it’s all I have.", "\"왜냐하면,\" 고모님이 말했다. \"이게 내가 가진 전부니까."], ["Because I’m ruined, my dear!’", "왜냐하면 내가 망했으니까, 얘야!\""]], [["If the house, and every one of us, had tumbled out into the river together, I could hardly have received a greater shock.", "집이며 우리 모두가 한꺼번에 강물 속으로 굴러떨어졌다 해도, 그보다 더 큰 충격을 받기는 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["‘Dick knows it,’ said my aunt, laying her hand calmly on my shoulder.", "\"딕은 알고 있어,\" 고모님이 내 어깨에 차분히 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["‘I am ruined, my dear Trot!", "\"나는 망했단다, 사랑하는 트롯!"], ["All I have in the world is in this room, except the cottage; and that I have left Janet to let.", "그 오두막만 빼면, 내가 이 세상에서 가진 거라곤 죄다 이 방 안에 있어. 그 오두막은 자넷한테 세를 놓으라고 맡기고 왔지."], ["Barkis, I want to get a bed for this gentleman tonight.", "바키스, 오늘 밤 이 도련님이 잘 침대를 좀 마련해 주면 좋겠네."], ["To save expense, perhaps you can make up something here for myself.", "비용을 아끼게, 나는 여기 아무거나 좀 깔아서 자도 되니까."], ["Anything will do.", "무엇이든 상관없어."], ["It’s only for tonight.", "그저 하룻밤뿐인걸."], ["We’ll talk about this, more, tomorrow.’", "이 일은 내일 더 이야기하자꾸나.\""]], [["I was roused from my amazement, and concern for her--I am sure, for her--by her falling on my neck, for a moment, and crying that she only grieved for me.", "나는 고모님을 향한 놀라움과 걱정에서 — 정말이지, 고모님이 걱정되어서였다 — 정신이 번쩍 들었는데, 고모님이 잠깐 내 목을 끌어안고는, 자기는 오직 나 때문에 슬플 뿐이라고 흐느꼈기 때문이었다."], ["In another moment she suppressed this emotion; and said with an aspect more triumphant than dejected:", "다음 순간 고모님은 그 감정을 억누르고는, 풀이 죽었다기보다는 오히려 의기양양한 낯빛으로 말했다."]], [["‘We must meet reverses boldly, and not suffer them to frighten us, my dear.", "\"우리는 역경을 용감하게 맞서야 한다, 그리고 그것에 겁먹어선 안 돼, 얘야."], ["We must learn to act the play out.", "우리는 이 연극을 끝까지 해내는 법을 배워야 해."], ["We must live misfortune down, Trot!’", "우리는 불행을 이겨 내고 꿋꿋이 살아가야 한다, 트롯!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 35. DEPRESSION", "# CHAPTER 35. DEPRESSION"]], [["As soon as I could recover my presence of mind, which quite deserted me in the first overpowering shock of my aunt’s intelligence, I proposed to Mr.", "고모님의 이야기가 안긴 첫 충격에 정신이 그만 송두리째 달아났다가, 겨우 마음을 추스르자마자 나는 딕 씨에게 양초 가게로 가서 패거티 씨가 얼마 전까지 쓰다 비운 침대를 차지하라고 권했다."], ["Dick to come round to the chandler’s shop, and take possession of the bed which Mr. Peggotty had lately vacated.", "양초 가게는 헝거퍼드 시장에 있었는데, 그 무렵 헝거퍼드 시장은 지금과는 사뭇 다른 곳이었다."], ["The chandler’s shop being in Hungerford Market, and Hungerford Market being a very different place in those days, there was a low wooden colonnade before the door (not very unlike that before the house where the little man and woman used to live, in the old weather-glass), which pleased Mr. Dick mightily.", "가게 문 앞에는 나지막한 나무 회랑이 있었는데(옛날 날씨 알리는 유리 기압계 속에 사는 작은 남자와 여자의 집 앞에 있던 것과 그리 다르지 않았다), 이것이 딕 씨를 무척이나 기쁘게 했다. 이런 구조물 위에서 묵는다는 영광이라면, 감히 말하건대 어지간한 불편쯤은 다 상쇄하고도 남았을 것이다."], ["The glory of lodging over this structure would have compensated him, I dare say, for many inconveniences; but, as there were really few to bear, beyond the compound of flavours I have already mentioned, and perhaps the want of a little more elbow-room, he was perfectly charmed with his accommodation. Mrs. Crupp had indignantly assured him that there wasn’t room to swing a cat there; but, as Mr.", "그러나 사실 견뎌야 할 불편이라곤 앞서 말한 그 뒤섞인 냄새들과, 어쩌면 팔꿈치 놀릴 자리가 조금 더 있었으면 싶은 것 말고는 별로 없었으니, 딕 씨는 자기 숙소에 완전히 반해 버렸다. 크룹 부인은 거기는 고양이를 휘둘러 돌릴 공간조차 없다고 분개하며 장담했지만, 딕 씨가 침대 발치에 앉아 다리를 감싸 쥐고서 나에게 정당하게 지적했듯이 말이다."], ["Dick justly observed to me, sitting down on the foot of the bed, nursing his leg, ‘You know, Trotwood, I don’t want to swing a cat. I never do swing a cat.", "\"이봐요, 트롯우드, 난 고양이를 휘두를 생각이 없어요. 난 고양이를 휘둘러 본 적이 한 번도 없다고요."], ["Therefore, what does that signify to ME!’", "그러니 그게 나한테 무슨 상관이겠어요!\""]], [["I tried to ascertain whether Mr. Dick had any understanding of the causes of this sudden and great change in my aunt’s affairs.", "나는 딕 씨가 고모님의 살림에 일어난 이 갑작스럽고 엄청난 변화의 원인을 조금이라도 알고 있는지 알아보려고 했다."], ["As I might have expected, he had none at all.", "그러나 예상했던 대로, 그는 전혀 알지 못했다."], ["The only account he could give of it was, that my aunt had said to him, the day before yesterday, ‘Now, Dick, are you really and truly the philosopher I take you for?’ That then he had said, Yes, he hoped so. That then my aunt had said, ‘Dick, I am ruined.’ That then he had said, ‘Oh, indeed!’ That then my aunt had praised him highly, which he was glad of.", "그가 들려줄 수 있는 설명이라고는, 그저께 고모님이 그에게 \"이봐요 딕, 당신은 정말이지 내가 생각하는 그런 철학자가 맞아요?\"라고 물었다는 것뿐이었다. 그러자 그가 \"네, 그러길 바라지요\"라고 대답했고, 그러자 고모님이 \"딕, 나 망했어요\"라고 했고, 그러자 그가 \"아, 그래요!\"라고 했고, 그러자 고모님이 그를 한껏 칭찬해 주어서 그가 기뻤다는 것이다."], ["And that then they had come to me, and had had bottled porter and sandwiches on the road.", "그러고 나서 둘이 나에게로 왔는데, 오는 길에 병맥주와 샌드위치를 먹었다는 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Dick was so very complacent, sitting on the foot of the bed, nursing his leg, and telling me this, with his eyes wide open and a surprised smile, that I am sorry to say I was provoked into explaining to him that ruin meant distress, want, and starvation; but I was soon bitterly reproved for this harshness, by seeing his face turn pale, and tears course down his lengthened cheeks, while he fixed upon me a look of such unutterable woe, that it might have softened a far harder heart than mine.", "딕 씨는 침대 발치에 앉아 다리를 감싸 쥔 채, 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 놀란 미소를 지으며 이 이야기를 나에게 들려주는 모습이 어찌나 흡족해 보였던지, 나는 말하기 부끄럽지만 그만 발끈해서 그에게 망했다는 것은 곤궁과 결핍과 굶주림을 뜻한다고 설명하고 말았다. 그러나 나는 이 모진 말에 곧 쓰라린 책망을 받았다."], ["I took infinitely greater pains to cheer him up again than I had taken to depress him; and I soon understood (as I ought to have known at first) that he had been so confident, merely because of his faith in the wisest and most wonderful of women, and his unbounded reliance on my intellectual resources.", "그의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리고 길쭉해진 두 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내리는 것을 보았기 때문인데, 그가 나에게 던진 그 형언할 수 없는 비탄의 눈길은 나보다 훨씬 모진 마음마저 누그러뜨릴 만했다. 나는 그를 풀이 죽게 만들 때보다 다시 기운을 북돋아 주는 데 한없이 더 큰 공을 들였다. 그리고 이내 깨달았는데(처음부터 알았어야 마땅했다), 그가 그토록 태평했던 것은 단지 세상에서 가장 현명하고 가장 놀라운 여인에 대한 믿음과, 내 지적 능력에 대한 무한한 의지 때문이었다."], ["The latter, I believe, he considered a match for any kind of disaster not absolutely mortal.", "후자에 대해서는, 짐작건대 그는 절대적으로 치명적이지만 않다면 어떤 종류의 재앙이든 능히 상대할 수 있는 것으로 여기는 듯했다."]], [["‘What can we do, Trotwood?’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"우리가 어떻게 하면 좋겠어요, 트롯우드?\" 딕 씨가 물었다."], ["‘There’s the Memorial-’", "\"그 청원서가 있잖아요—\""]], [["‘To be sure there is,’ said I.", "\"그렇지요, 물론이에요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘But all we can do just now, Mr. Dick, is to keep a cheerful countenance, and not let my aunt see that we are thinking about it.’", "\"하지만 지금 우리가 할 수 있는 거라곤, 딕 씨, 밝은 낯빛을 지키면서 고모님이 우리가 이 일을 걱정한다는 걸 눈치채지 못하게 하는 거예요.\""]], [["He assented to this in the most earnest manner; and implored me, if I should see him wandering an inch out of the right course, to recall him by some of those superior methods which were always at my command.", "그는 더없이 진지하게 이 말에 동의했고, 혹시라도 내가 보기에 그가 한 치라도 옳은 길에서 벗어나 헤매거든 언제든 내가 부릴 수 있는 그 뛰어난 방법들로 자기를 불러 세워 달라고 간청했다."], ["But I regret to state that the fright I had given him proved too much for his best attempts at concealment.", "그러나 유감스럽게도, 내가 그에게 안긴 두려움은 감추려는 그의 갖은 노력으로도 도무지 감당이 안 되는 것이었다."], ["All the evening his eyes wandered to my aunt’s face, with an expression of the most dismal apprehension, as if he saw her growing thin on the spot.", "저녁 내내 그의 눈은 마치 고모님이 그 자리에서 점점 야위어 가는 것을 보기라도 하는 양, 더없이 음울한 근심을 담고 고모님의 얼굴로 자꾸만 향했다."], ["He was conscious of this, and put a constraint upon his head; but his keeping that immovable, and sitting rolling his eyes like a piece of machinery, did not mend the matter at all.", "그도 이것을 의식하고 머리를 꼼짝 않게 붙들려고 애썼지만, 머리를 그렇게 고정한 채 앉아 기계 장치처럼 눈알만 굴려 대니 사정은 조금도 나아지지 않았다."], ["I saw him look at the loaf at supper (which happened to be a small one), as if nothing else stood between us and famine; and when my aunt insisted on his making his customary repast, I detected him in the act of pocketing fragments of his bread and cheese; I have no doubt for the purpose of reviving us with those savings, when we should have reached an advanced stage of attenuation.", "나는 그가 저녁 식사 때 빵 한 덩이를 (마침 작은 덩이였다) 바라보는 것을 보았는데, 그것 말고는 우리와 굶주림 사이에 아무것도 가로막은 게 없기라도 한 듯한 눈빛이었다. 그리고 고모님이 그에게 늘 하던 대로 식사를 하라고 고집하자, 나는 그가 빵과 치즈 조각을 주머니에 슬쩍 챙겨 넣는 현장을 잡아냈다. 분명 우리가 야윌 대로 야윈 지경에 이르렀을 때 그렇게 아껴 둔 것으로 우리를 되살리려는 속셈이었으리라."]], [["My aunt, on the other hand, was in a composed frame of mind, which was a lesson to all of us--to me, I am sure.", "반면에 고모님은 차분한 마음가짐을 유지하고 있었는데, 이것은 우리 모두에게—적어도 나에게는 분명히—하나의 가르침이었다."], ["She was extremely gracious to Peggotty, except when I inadvertently called her by that name; and, strange as I knew she felt in London, appeared quite at home.", "고모님은 패거티에게 더없이 자상했는데, 다만 내가 무심코 그녀를 그 이름으로 부를 때만은 예외였다. 그리고 런던에서 영 낯설어한다는 것을 내가 알고 있었음에도, 고모님은 아주 마음 편안한 듯이 보였다."], ["She was to have my bed, and I was to lie in the sitting-room, to keep guard over her.", "고모님은 내 침대를 쓰기로 했고, 나는 거실에 누워 고모님을 지키기로 했다."], ["She made a great point of being so near the river, in case of a conflagration; and I suppose really did find some satisfaction in that circumstance.", "고모님은 화재가 났을 경우를 대비해 강가에 이토록 가까이 있다는 것을 대단히 중요하게 여겼고, 짐작건대 정말로 그 점에서 어떤 만족을 느끼는 듯했다."]], [["‘Trot, my dear,’ said my aunt, when she saw me making preparations for compounding her usual night-draught, ‘No!’", "\"트롯, 얘야.\" 내가 고모님이 늘 마시는 잠자리 음료를 타려고 채비하는 것을 보고는 고모님이 말했다. \"안 돼!\" \"아무것도 안 마시게요, 고모님?\""]], [["‘Nothing, aunt?’\n‘Not wine, my dear. Ale.’", "\"포도주는 안 돼, 얘야. 에일로.\""]], [["‘But there is wine here, aunt.", "\"하지만 여기 포도주가 있는걸요, 고모님."], ["And you always have it made of wine.’", "게다가 늘 포도주로 만들어 드시잖아요.\""]], [["‘Keep that, in case of sickness,’ said my aunt.", "\"그건 아플 때를 위해 남겨 둬.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘We mustn’t use it carelessly, Trot. Ale for me.", "\"함부로 써서는 안 돼, 트롯. 나한텐 에일로 줘."], ["Half a pint.’", "반 파인트.\""]], [["I thought Mr. Dick would have fallen, insensible.", "나는 딕 씨가 정신을 잃고 쓰러지는 줄 알았다."], ["My aunt being resolute, I went out and got the ale myself.", "고모님이 단호하니, 나는 직접 나가서 에일을 사 왔다."], ["As it was growing late, Peggotty and Mr. Dick took that opportunity of repairing to the chandler’s shop together.", "밤이 이슥해지고 있던 터라, 패거티와 딕 씨는 이 기회를 틈타 함께 양초 가게로 물러갔다."], ["I parted from him, poor fellow, at the corner of the street, with his great kite at his back, a very monument of human misery.", "나는 가엾은 그와 길모퉁이에서 헤어졌는데, 그는 커다란 연을 등에 둘러멘 채 마치 인간 비참함의 기념비 같았다."]], [["My aunt was walking up and down the room when I returned, crimping the borders of her nightcap with her fingers.", "내가 돌아왔을 때 고모님은 손가락으로 잠자리 모자의 가장자리를 잡아 주름을 잡으며 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다."], ["I warmed the ale and made the toast on the usual infallible principles.", "나는 늘 하던 어김없는 방식대로 에일을 데우고 토스트를 만들었다."], ["When it was ready for her, she was ready for it, with her nightcap on, and the skirt of her gown turned back on her knees.", "그것이 준비되자, 고모님도 잠자리 모자를 쓰고 가운 자락을 무릎 위로 걷어 올린 채 그것을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said my aunt, after taking a spoonful of it; ‘it’s a great deal better than wine.", "\"얘야.\" 한 숟가락 떠먹고 나서 고모님이 말했다. \"이게 포도주보다 훨씬 낫구나."], ["Not half so bilious.’", "속이 절반도 안 거북해.\""]], [["I suppose I looked doubtful, for she added:", "내가 미심쩍은 표정을 지었던 모양인지, 고모님이 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Tut, tut, child.", "\"쯧, 쯧, 얘야."], ["If nothing worse than Ale happens to us, we are well off.’", "우리한테 에일보다 더 나쁜 일이 안 닥친다면, 우린 형편이 좋은 거야.\""]], [["‘I should think so myself, aunt, I am sure,’ said I.", "\"저도 그렇게 생각해요, 고모님, 정말이에요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, then, why DON’T you think so?’ said my aunt.", "\"그럼, 그러면, 왜 그렇게 생각을 안 하는데?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Because you and I are very different people,’ I returned.", "\"고모님하고 저는 아주 다른 사람이니까요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Stuff and nonsense, Trot!’ replied my aunt.", "\"쓸데없는 소리, 트롯!\" 고모님이 받아쳤다."]], [["My aunt went on with a quiet enjoyment, in which there was very little affectation, if any; drinking the warm ale with a tea-spoon, and soaking her strips of toast in it.", "고모님은 가식이라곤 거의, 아니 조금도 없는 조용한 즐거움 속에서 음료를 들었다. 따뜻한 에일을 찻숟가락으로 떠먹으며, 토스트 조각을 그 안에 적셔 가면서."]], [["‘Trot,’ said she, ‘I don’t care for strange faces in general, but I rather like that Barkis of yours, do you know!’", "\"트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"난 대체로 낯선 얼굴을 좋아하지 않는데, 네 그 바키스라는 여자는 어쩐지 마음에 들지 뭐냐, 그거 아니?\""]], [["‘It’s better than a hundred pounds to hear you say so!’ said I.", "\"고모님 입에서 그 말을 듣다니 백 파운드보다 더 값지네요!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s a most extraordinary world,’ observed my aunt, rubbing her nose; ‘how that woman ever got into it with that name, is unaccountable to me.", "\"참 별난 세상이야.\" 고모님이 코를 문지르며 말했다. \"그 여자가 어쩌다 그런 이름을 달고 세상에 나왔는지 도무지 알 수가 없어."], ["It would be much more easy to be born a Jackson, or something of that sort, one would think.’", "차라리 잭슨이나 뭐 그런 이름으로 태어나는 게 훨씬 쉬웠을 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["‘Perhaps she thinks so, too; it’s not her fault,’ said I.", "\"어쩌면 그 여자도 그렇게 생각할지 몰라요. 그게 그 여자 잘못은 아니잖아요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I suppose not,’ returned my aunt, rather grudging the admission; ‘but it’s very aggravating.", "\"그렇겠지.\" 고모님이 다소 마지못해 인정하며 받았다. \"하지만 정말 속 터지는 일이야."], ["However, she’s Barkis now.", "그래도 이제 바키스가 됐으니."], ["That’s some comfort.", "그나마 다행이지."], ["Barkis is uncommonly fond of you, Trot.’", "바키스가 너를 끔찍이도 아끼더구나, 트롯.\""]], [["‘There is nothing she would leave undone to prove it,’ said I.", "\"그걸 증명하기 위해서라면 그 여자가 못 할 일이 없을 거예요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing, I believe,’ returned my aunt.", "\"못 할 일이 없겠지, 정말로.\""], ["‘Here, the poor fool has been begging and praying about handing over some of her money--because she has got too much of it.", "고모님이 받았다. \"여기, 그 가엾은 바보가 자기 돈을 좀 넘겨받아 달라고 빌고 또 빌었지 뭐냐—돈이 너무 많다면서."], ["A simpleton!’", "어리석은 것!\""]], [["My aunt’s tears of pleasure were positively trickling down into the warm ale.", "고모님의 기쁨에 겨운 눈물이 정말이지 따뜻한 에일 속으로 뚝뚝 떨어지고 있었다."]], [["‘She’s the most ridiculous creature that ever was born,’ said my aunt.", "\"그 여자는 세상에 태어난 사람 중에 제일 우스꽝스러운 인간이야.\""], ["‘I knew, from the first moment when I saw her with that poor dear blessed baby of a mother of yours, that she was the most ridiculous of mortals.", "고모님이 말했다. \"네 그 가엾고 사랑스러운 아기 같은 어미와 함께 있는 모습을 처음 본 순간부터 나는 알았어, 그 여자가 인간 중에 제일 우스꽝스럽다는 걸."], ["But there are good points in Barkis!’", "하지만 바키스한테도 좋은 구석이 있어!\""]], [["Affecting to laugh, she got an opportunity of putting her hand to her eyes.", "웃는 척하면서, 고모님은 손을 눈으로 가져갈 기회를 얻었다."], ["Having availed herself of it, she resumed her toast and her discourse together.", "그 기회를 한껏 이용하고 나서, 고모님은 토스트와 이야기를 함께 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Mercy upon us!’ sighed my aunt.", "우리에게 자비를!\" 고모님이 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["‘I know all about it, Trot!", "\"난 다 알아, 트롯!"], ["Barkis and myself had quite a gossip while you were out with Dick.", "네가 딕하고 나가 있는 동안 바키스랑 나랑 한참 수다를 떨었거든."], ["I know all about it. I don’t know where these wretched girls expect to go to, for my part.", "난 다 안다고. 그 가엾은 계집애들이 대체 어디로 가게 될 거라고 생각하는지, 나로서는 알 수가 없어."], ["I wonder they don’t knock out their brains against--against mantelpieces,’ said my aunt; an idea which was probably suggested to her by her contemplation of mine.", "차라리 머리를 박살 내 버리지 않는 게 신기하다니까—저기 벽난로 선반에다가.\" 고모님이 말했는데, 이 발상은 아마도 자기 집 벽난로 선반을 떠올린 데서 비롯된 듯했다."]], [["‘Poor Emily!’ said I.", "\"가엾은 에밀리!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, don’t talk to me about poor,’ returned my aunt.", "\"오, 가엾다는 소리는 내 앞에서 하지 마라.\" 고모님이 받았다."], ["‘She should have thought of that, before she caused so much misery!", "\"그렇게 많은 사람을 불행하게 만들기 전에 진작 그 생각을 했어야지!"], ["Give me a kiss, Trot.", "입 좀 맞춰 다오, 트롯."], ["I am sorry for your early experience.’", "네가 어린 나이에 그런 일을 겪다니 안됐구나.\""]], [["As I bent forward, she put her tumbler on my knee to detain me, and said:", "내가 몸을 앞으로 숙이자, 고모님은 나를 붙들어 두려고 술잔을 내 무릎 위에 올려놓고 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, Trot, Trot!", "\"오, 트롯, 트롯!"], ["And so you fancy yourself in love! Do you?’", "그래서 네가 사랑에 빠졌다고 여기는 거냐? 그래?\""]], [["‘Fancy, aunt!’ I exclaimed, as red as I could be.", "\"여기다니요, 고모님!\" 나는 더 붉어질 수 없을 만큼 새빨개져서 외쳤다."], ["‘I adore her with my whole soul!’", "\"저는 온 영혼을 다해 그녀를 흠모해요!\""]], [["‘Dora, indeed!’ returned my aunt.", "\"도라란 말이지!\" 고모님이 받았다."], ["‘And you mean to say the little thing is very fascinating, I suppose?’", "\"그러니까 그 조그만 것이 아주 매혹적이라고, 그렇게 말하고 싶은 거지?\""]], [["‘My dear aunt,’ I replied, ‘no one can form the least idea what she is!’", "\"고모님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"아무도 그녀가 어떤 사람인지 손톱만큼도 짐작 못 할 거예요!\""]], [["‘Ah! And not silly?’ said my aunt.", "\"아! 그리고 어리석지는 않고?\""], ["‘Silly, aunt!’", "고모님이 말했다."]], [["I seriously believe it had never once entered my head for a single moment, to consider whether she was or not.", "\"어리석다니요, 고모님!\" 진심으로 말하건대, 그녀가 어리석은지 아닌지 따져 볼 생각은 단 한 순간도 내 머릿속에 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["I resented the idea, of course; but I was in a manner struck by it, as a new one altogether.", "물론 나는 그 발상에 발끈했지만, 어쩐지 그것이 아주 새로운 것으로 다가와 적잖이 충격을 받았다."]], [["‘Not light-headed?’ said my aunt.", "\"머리가 가볍지는 않고?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Light-headed, aunt!’ I could only repeat this daring speculation with the same kind of feeling with which I had repeated the preceding question.", "\"머리가 가볍다니요, 고모님!\" 나는 앞서 던진 질문에 느꼈던 것과 똑같은 종류의 심정으로 이 당돌한 억측을 그저 되뇌는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["‘Well, well!’ said my aunt. ‘I only ask.", "\"그래, 그래!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["I don’t depreciate her. Poor little couple!", "\"그냥 물어본 거야. 그 애를 깎아내리려는 게 아니야."], ["And so you think you were formed for one another, and are to go through a party-supper-table kind of life, like two pretty pieces of confectionery, do you, Trot?’", "가엾은 어린 한 쌍! 그래서 너희 둘은 서로를 위해 만들어졌다고, 두 개의 예쁜 과자처럼 잔치 음식상 같은 인생을 살아갈 거라고 생각하는 거지, 트롯?\""]], [["She asked me this so kindly, and with such a gentle air, half playful and half sorrowful, that I was quite touched.", "고모님이 어찌나 다정하게, 절반은 장난스럽고 절반은 슬픈 그 부드러운 태도로 이것을 물었던지, 나는 마음이 자못 뭉클했다."]], [["‘We are young and inexperienced, aunt, I know,’ I replied; ‘and I dare say we say and think a good deal that is rather foolish.", "\"우리가 젊고 미숙하다는 건 저도 알아요, 고모님.\" 내가 대답했다. \"그리고 우리가 다소 어리석은 말과 생각을 꽤 많이 한다는 것도 감히 인정해요."], ["But we love one another truly, I am sure.", "하지만 우리는 진실로 서로를 사랑해요, 분명히요."], ["If I thought Dora could ever love anybody else, or cease to love me; or that I could ever love anybody else, or cease to love her; I don’t know what I should do--go out of my mind, I think!’", "만약 도라가 다른 누구를 사랑하게 된다거나 저를 사랑하기를 멈춘다면, 혹은 제가 다른 누구를 사랑하게 된다거나 그녀를 사랑하기를 멈춘다면—저는 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요—제정신이 아니게 될 거예요, 분명히!\""]], [["‘Ah, Trot!’ said my aunt, shaking her head, and smiling gravely; ‘blind, blind, blind!’", "\"아, 트롯!\" 고모님이 고개를 저으며, 진지하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"눈멀었구나, 눈멀었어, 눈멀었어!\""]], [["‘Someone that I know, Trot,’ my aunt pursued, after a pause, ‘though of a very pliant disposition, has an earnestness of affection in him that reminds me of poor Baby.", "\"내가 아는 누군가는 말이다, 트롯.\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"성품은 무척 유순하지만, 그 안에 가엾은 아기를 떠올리게 하는 진실한 애정이 있어."], ["Earnestness is what that Somebody must look for, to sustain him and improve him, Trot.", "진실함이야말로 그 누군가가 자기를 지탱하고 더 나은 사람으로 만들기 위해 찾아야 할 거야, 트롯."], ["Deep, downright, faithful earnestness.’", "깊고, 곧고, 한결같은 진실함 말이다.\""]], [["‘If you only knew the earnestness of Dora, aunt!’ I cried.", "\"고모님이 도라의 진실함을 아시기만 한다면요!\" 내가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Oh, Trot!’ she said again; ‘blind, blind!’ and without knowing why, I felt a vague unhappy loss or want of something overshadow me like a cloud.", "\"오, 트롯!\" 고모님이 다시 말했다. \"눈멀었어, 눈멀었어!\" 그리고 영문도 모른 채, 나는 무언가를 잃었거나 무언가가 모자란다는 어렴풋하고 불행한 느낌이 구름처럼 나를 뒤덮는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["‘However,’ said my aunt, ‘I don’t want to put two young creatures out of conceit with themselves, or to make them unhappy; so, though it is a girl and boy attachment, and girl and boy attachments very often--mind!", "\"하지만 말이다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"나는 두 젊은것이 자기네 마음을 의심하게 만들거나 불행하게 만들고 싶지는 않아."], ["I don’t say always!--come to nothing, still we’ll be serious about it, and hope for a prosperous issue one of these days.", "그러니, 비록 이것이 풋사랑이고, 풋사랑이란 게 흔히—명심해라! 언제나 그렇다는 건 아니야!—아무 결실 없이 끝나기도 하지만, 그래도 우린 이걸 진지하게 받아들이고, 언젠가 좋은 결말이 나기를 바라기로 하자."], ["There’s time enough for it to come to anything!’", "무언가로 무르익는 데는 시간이 넉넉하니까!\""]], [["This was not upon the whole very comforting to a rapturous lover; but I was glad to have my aunt in my confidence, and I was mindful of her being fatigued.", "이것은 전체적으로 황홀경에 빠진 연인에게 그리 위안이 되는 말은 아니었지만, 나는 고모님이 내 속내를 알아주는 것이 기뻤고, 또 고모님이 지쳐 있다는 것을 염두에 두었다."], ["So I thanked her ardently for this mark of her affection, and for all her other kindnesses towards me; and after a tender good night, she took her nightcap into my bedroom.", "그래서 나는 이런 애정의 표시에 대해, 그리고 나를 향한 다른 모든 친절에 대해 고모님에게 뜨겁게 감사를 드렸다. 그러고는 다정한 작별 인사를 나눈 뒤, 고모님은 잠자리 모자를 들고 내 침실로 들어갔다."]], [["How miserable I was, when I lay down!", "자리에 누웠을 때 나는 얼마나 비참했던가!"], ["How I thought and thought about my being poor, in Mr. Spenlow’s eyes; about my not being what I thought I was, when I proposed to Dora; about the chivalrous necessity of telling Dora what my worldly condition was, and releasing her from her engagement if she thought fit; about how I should contrive to live, during the long term of my articles, when I was earning nothing; about doing something to assist my aunt, and seeing no way of doing anything; about coming down to have no money in my pocket, and to wear a shabby coat, and to be able to carry Dora no little presents, and to ride no gallant greys, and to show myself in no agreeable light!", "나는 얼마나 생각하고 또 생각했던가—내가 스펜로 씨 눈에 가난뱅이라는 것을, 도라에게 청혼했을 때 내가 스스로 생각하던 그런 사람이 아니라는 것을, 내 세속적 처지가 어떠한지를 도라에게 말하고 그녀가 원한다면 약혼에서 풀어 주어야 한다는 그 기사도적인 의무를, 아무것도 벌지 못하는 긴 견습 기간 동안 어떻게 살아갈 방도를 마련할지를, 고모님을 도울 무언가를 해야 하는데 그 무엇도 할 방도가 보이지 않는다는 것을, 주머니에 돈 한 푼 없는 신세로 전락해 초라한 외투를 걸치고, 도라에게 작은 선물 하나 가져다주지 못하고, 늠름한 회색 말을 타지도 못하고, 어디서도 멋진 모습을 보여 주지 못하게 된다는 것을!"], ["Sordid and selfish as I knew it was, and as I tortured myself by knowing that it was, to let my mind run on my own distress so much, I was so devoted to Dora that I could not help it.", "내 자신의 곤경에 마음을 그토록 골몰하는 것이 비루하고 이기적이라는 걸 알면서도, 또 그것이 그렇다는 걸 알기에 스스로를 괴롭히면서도, 나는 도라에게 너무나 헌신적이어서 어찌할 수가 없었다."], ["I knew that it was base in me not to think more of my aunt, and less of myself; but, so far, selfishness was inseparable from Dora, and I could not put Dora on one side for any mortal creature.", "고모님을 더 생각하고 나 자신을 덜 생각하지 않는 것이 비열한 짓임을 알았지만, 지금까지 이기심은 도라와 떼려야 뗄 수 없었고, 나는 그 어떤 사람을 위해서도 도라를 한쪽으로 밀어 둘 수가 없었다."], ["How exceedingly miserable I was, that night!", "그날 밤 나는 얼마나 한없이 비참했던가!"]], [["As to sleep, I had dreams of poverty in all sorts of shapes, but I seemed to dream without the previous ceremony of going to sleep.", "잠으로 말하자면, 나는 온갖 모양의 가난을 꿈꾸었는데, 어쩐지 잠드는 사전 의식도 없이 곧장 꿈을 꾸는 것 같았다."], ["Now I was ragged, wanting to sell Dora matches, six bundles for a halfpenny; now I was at the office in a nightgown and boots, remonstrated with by Mr.", "어느새 나는 누더기를 걸친 채 도라에게 성냥을 팔려 하고 있었는데, 반 페니에 여섯 묶음이었다. 또 어느새 나는 잠옷에 장화 차림으로 사무소에 가 있었는데, 그런 산뜻한 옷차림으로 의뢰인들 앞에 나타났다고 스펜로 씨에게 핀잔을 들었다."], ["Spenlow on appearing before the clients in that airy attire; now I was hungrily picking up the crumbs that fell from old Tiffey’s daily biscuit, regularly eaten when St.", "또 어느새 나는 늙은 티피가 세인트폴 성당이 한 시를 칠 때마다 어김없이 먹는 그 일과의 비스킷에서 떨어지는 부스러기를 허기진 채 주워 먹고 있었다."], ["Paul’s struck one; now I was hopelessly endeavouring to get a licence to marry Dora, having nothing but one of Uriah Heep’s gloves to offer in exchange, which the whole Commons rejected; and still, more or less conscious of my own room, I was always tossing about like a distressed ship in a sea of bed-clothes.", "또 어느새 나는 도라와 결혼할 허가증을 얻으려고 가망 없이 애쓰고 있었는데, 그 대가로 내밀 거라곤 유라이어 힙의 장갑 한 짝뿐이어서 민법 박사회 전체가 거절해 버렸다. 그러면서도 내 방을 어느 정도 의식하고 있는 채로, 나는 이불의 바다에서 조난당한 배처럼 늘 이리저리 뒤척이고 있었다."]], [["My aunt was restless, too, for I frequently heard her walking to and fro.", "고모님도 잠을 이루지 못하고 있었으니, 나는 고모님이 이리저리 거니는 소리를 자주 들었다."], ["Two or three times in the course of the night, attired in a long flannel wrapper in which she looked seven feet high, she appeared, like a disturbed ghost, in my room, and came to the side of the sofa on which I lay.", "밤사이 두세 번, 키가 칠 척은 되어 보이게 만드는 긴 플란넬 실내복을 걸치고서, 고모님은 잠 못 든 유령처럼 내 방에 나타나, 내가 누운 소파 곁으로 다가왔다."], ["On the first occasion I started up in alarm, to learn that she inferred from a particular light in the sky, that Westminster Abbey was on fire; and to be consulted in reference to the probability of its igniting Buckingham Street, in case the wind changed.", "처음에 나는 놀라서 벌떡 일어났는데, 알고 보니 고모님은 하늘에 어린 어떤 특이한 빛을 보고 웨스트민스터 사원에 불이 났다고 짐작한 것이었고, 만약 바람이 바뀐다면 그 불이 버킹엄 거리로 옮겨붙을 가능성에 대해 내 의견을 듣고자 한 것이었다."], ["Lying still, after that, I found that she sat down near me, whispering to herself ‘Poor boy!’ And then it made me twenty times more wretched, to know how unselfishly mindful she was of me, and how selfishly mindful I was of myself.", "그 뒤로 가만히 누워 있자니, 고모님이 내 곁에 앉아 \"가엾은 것!\" 하고 혼잣말로 속삭이는 것을 알게 되었다. 그리고 그때, 고모님이 얼마나 사심 없이 나를 마음에 두는지, 또 내가 얼마나 이기적으로 나 자신만 마음에 두는지를 알게 되니, 나는 스무 배는 더 비참해졌다."]], [["It was difficult to believe that a night so long to me, could be short to anybody else.", "나에게 그토록 길었던 밤이 다른 누군가에게는 짧을 수 있다는 것은 믿기 어려웠다."], ["This consideration set me thinking and thinking of an imaginary party where people were dancing the hours away, until that became a dream too, and I heard the music incessantly playing one tune, and saw Dora incessantly dancing one dance, without taking the least notice of me.", "이 생각을 곱씹다 보니 사람들이 밤이 새도록 춤추며 시간을 흘려보내는 어떤 가상의 잔치를 자꾸자꾸 떠올리게 되었고, 마침내 그것마저 꿈이 되어, 나는 음악이 쉬지 않고 한 곡만을 연주하는 소리를 듣고, 도라가 쉬지 않고 한 춤만을 추면서 나에게는 눈길 한 번 주지 않는 것을 보았다."], ["The man who had been playing the harp all night, was trying in vain to cover it with an ordinary-sized nightcap, when I awoke; or I should rather say, when I left off trying to go to sleep, and saw the sun shining in through the window at last.", "밤새 하프를 켜던 남자가 보통 크기의 잠자리 모자로 그것을 덮어 보려고 헛되이 애쓰고 있을 때 나는 잠에서 깨어났다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하자면, 잠들려는 애를 그만두었을 때, 그리고 마침내 창문으로 햇살이 비쳐 드는 것을 보았을 때라고 해야겠다."]], [["There was an old Roman bath in those days at the bottom of one of the streets out of the Strand--it may be there still--in which I have had many a cold plunge.", "그 무렵 스트랜드에서 갈라져 나온 어느 거리 끝에는 오래된 로마식 목욕탕이 있었는데—아마 지금도 거기 있을 것이다—나는 거기서 찬물에 몸을 던진 적이 여러 번 있었다."], ["Dressing myself as quietly as I could, and leaving Peggotty to look after my aunt, I tumbled head foremost into it, and then went for a walk to Hampstead.", "나는 되도록 조용히 옷을 갈아입고, 고모님은 패거티에게 맡겨 둔 채, 그 안으로 머리부터 곤두박질치듯 뛰어든 다음, 햄프스테드로 산책을 나갔다."], ["I had a hope that this brisk treatment might freshen my wits a little; and I think it did them good, for I soon came to the conclusion that the first step I ought to take was, to try if my articles could be cancelled and the premium recovered.", "나는 이 활기찬 처방이 머리를 조금이나마 맑게 해 주기를 바랐고, 실제로 효과가 있었던 것 같다. 곧 나는 내가 가장 먼저 밟아야 할 단계가, 견습 계약을 해지하고 그 사례금을 돌려받을 수 있는지 알아보는 것이라는 결론에 이르렀기 때문이다."], ["I got some breakfast on the Heath, and walked back to Doctors’ Commons, along the watered roads and through a pleasant smell of summer flowers, growing in gardens and carried into town on hucksters’ heads, intent on this first effort to meet our altered circumstances.", "나는 히스 들판에서 아침을 좀 먹고, 물 뿌려진 길을 따라, 정원에서 자라 행상들의 머리에 이고 시내로 들어오는 여름 꽃들의 향긋한 냄새를 가로질러, 우리의 달라진 형편에 맞서려는 이 첫걸음에 골몰하며 민법 박사회로 걸어 돌아왔다."]], [["I arrived at the office so soon, after all, that I had half an hour’s loitering about the Commons, before old Tiffey, who was always first, appeared with his key.", "결국 너무 일찍 사무소에 도착하는 바람에, 늘 제일 먼저 나오는 늙은 티피가 열쇠를 들고 나타날 때까지 나는 박사회 근처에서 반 시간을 어슬렁거려야 했다."], ["Then I sat down in my shady corner, looking up at the sunlight on the opposite chimney-pots, and thinking about Dora; until Mr. Spenlow came in, crisp and curly.", "그러고 나서 나는 내 그늘진 구석에 앉아, 맞은편 굴뚝 갓에 비친 햇살을 올려다보며 도라를 생각했다. 스펜로 씨가 산뜻하게, 머리를 곱슬곱슬 매만진 채로 들어올 때까지."]], [["‘How are you, Copperfield?’ said he.", "\"잘 지냈나, 코퍼필드?\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Fine morning!’", "\"좋은 아침이군!\""]], [["‘Beautiful morning, sir,’ said I.", "\"아름다운 아침입니다, 선생님.\""], ["‘Could I say a word to you before you go into Court?’", "내가 말했다. \"법정에 들어가시기 전에 잠깐 말씀 좀 드려도 될까요?\""]], [["‘By all means,’ said he.", "\"물론이지.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Come into my room.’", "\"내 방으로 들어오게.\""]], [["I followed him into his room, and he began putting on his gown, and touching himself up before a little glass he had, hanging inside a closet door.", "나는 그를 따라 방으로 들어갔고, 그는 가운을 걸치고 벽장 문 안쪽에 걸어 둔 작은 거울 앞에서 매무새를 다듬기 시작했다."]], [["‘I am sorry to say,’ said I, ‘that I have some rather disheartening intelligence from my aunt.’", "\"유감스럽지만,\" 내가 말했다. \"고모님께 다소 낙담스러운 소식을 들었습니다.\""]], [["‘No!’ said he. ‘Dear me!", "\"저런!\" 그가 말했다. \"이런!"], ["Not paralysis, I hope?’", "설마 중풍은 아니겠지?\""]], [["‘It has no reference to her health, sir,’ I replied.", "\"건강과는 무관한 일입니다, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘She has met with some large losses.", "\"고모님께서 큰 손실을 보셨어요."], ["In fact, she has very little left, indeed.’", "사실, 거의 남은 게 없으세요, 정말로요.\""]], [["‘You as-tound me, Copperfield!’ cried Mr. Spenlow.", "\"이거 놀라운데, 코퍼필드!\" 스펜로 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["I shook my head. ‘Indeed, sir,’ said I, ‘her affairs are so changed, that I wished to ask you whether it would be possible--at a sacrifice on our part of some portion of the premium, of course,’ I put in this, on the spur of the moment, warned by the blank expression of his face--‘to cancel my articles?’", "나는 고개를 저었다. \"정말입니다, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"고모님 형편이 너무나 바뀌어서, 여쭤 보고 싶었습니다. 혹시—물론 저희 쪽에서 사례금의 일부를 포기하는 조건으로요.\" 그의 멍한 표정에 경고를 받아, 나는 순간적으로 이 말을 끼워 넣었다. \"—제 견습 계약을 해지하는 것이 가능할지요?\""]], [["What it cost me to make this proposal, nobody knows.", "이 제안을 꺼내는 데 내가 얼마나 큰 대가를 치렀는지는 아무도 모른다."], ["It was like asking, as a favour, to be sentenced to transportation from Dora.", "그것은 마치 호의를 베풀어 달라며, 도라에게서 추방되는 형을 선고해 달라고 청하는 것과 같았다."]], [["‘To cancel your articles, Copperfield? Cancel?’", "\"자네 견습 계약을 해지한다고, 코퍼필드? 해지?\""]], [["I explained with tolerable firmness, that I really did not know where my means of subsistence were to come from, unless I could earn them for myself.", "나는 제법 단호하게, 스스로 생계를 벌지 못한다면 도무지 어디서 살림 밑천이 나올지 알 수가 없다고 설명했다."], ["I had no fear for the future, I said--and I laid great emphasis on that, as if to imply that I should still be decidedly eligible for a son-in-law one of these days--but, for the present, I was thrown upon my own resources.", "미래에 대해서는 아무 두려움이 없습니다, 라고 나는 말했고—마치 언젠가는 여전히 사윗감으로 손색없는 사람이 되리라는 뜻을 넌지시 비치려는 듯, 그 점을 한껏 강조했다—하지만 당장은 제 힘만으로 버텨야 하는 처지가 되었다고 했다."], ["‘I am extremely sorry to hear this, Copperfield,’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"이런 말을 듣게 되어 대단히 유감일세, 코퍼필드.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Extremely sorry.", "\"대단히 유감이야."], ["It is not usual to cancel articles for any such reason.", "그런 이유로 견습 계약을 해지하는 건 흔한 일이 아닐세."], ["It is not a professional course of proceeding.", "직업적으로 온당한 절차도 아니고."], ["It is not a convenient precedent at all. Far from it.", "편리한 선례라곤 결코 아니야. 천만의 말씀이지."], ["At the same time--’", "그렇지만 동시에—\""]], [["‘You are very good, sir,’ I murmured, anticipating a concession.", "\"정말 자비로우십니다, 선생님.\" 나는 양보를 기대하며 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘Not at all.", "\"천만에."], ["Don’t mention it,’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "그런 말 말게.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["‘At the same time, I was going to say, if it had been my lot to have my hands unfettered--if I had not a partner--Mr. Jorkins--’", "\"그렇지만 동시에, 내가 말하려던 건, 만약 내 두 손이 자유로운 처지였다면—만약 나에게 동업자가 없었다면—조킨스 씨 말일세—\""]], [["My hopes were dashed in a moment, but I made another effort.", "내 희망은 순식간에 산산조각이 났지만, 나는 다시 한번 시도해 보았다."]], [["‘Do you think, sir,’ said I, ‘if I were to mention it to Mr.", "\"선생님, 혹시,\" 내가 말했다."], ["Jorkins--’", "\"제가 조킨스 씨께 그 이야기를 꺼내 본다면—\""]], [["Mr. Spenlow shook his head discouragingly.", "스펜로 씨가 맥 빠지게 고개를 저었다."], ["‘Heaven forbid, Copperfield,’ he replied, ‘that I should do any man an injustice: still less, Mr.", "\"당치도 않네, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답했다. \"내가 누군가에게 부당한 짓을 하다니, 하물며 조킨스 씨에게라니."], ["Jorkins. But I know my partner, Copperfield. Mr.", "하지만 나는 내 동업자를 알아, 코퍼필드."], ["Jorkins is not a man to respond to a proposition of this peculiar nature. Mr.", "조킨스 씨는 이런 특이한 성격의 제안에 응할 사람이 아닐세."], ["Jorkins is very difficult to move from the beaten track. You know what he is!’", "조킨스 씨는 정해진 길에서 한 발짝도 움직이게 하기가 무척 어려운 사람이야. 자네도 그가 어떤 사람인지 알잖나!\""]], [["I am sure I knew nothing about him, except that he had originally been alone in the business, and now lived by himself in a house near Montagu Square, which was fearfully in want of painting; that he came very late of a day, and went away very early; that he never appeared to be consulted about anything; and that he had a dingy little black-hole of his own upstairs, where no business was ever done, and where there was a yellow old cartridge-paper pad upon his desk, unsoiled by ink, and reported to be twenty years of age.", "사실 나는 그에 대해 아는 게 전혀 없었다. 다만 그가 본래 혼자서 이 사업을 했다는 것, 지금은 몬터규 광장 근처의, 페인트칠이 끔찍하게 필요한 집에서 혼자 산다는 것, 날마다 아주 늦게 와서 아주 일찍 간다는 것, 무슨 일이든 그에게 의논하는 법이 없어 보인다는 것, 그리고 위층에 자기만의 침침하고 자그마한 골방을 두고 있는데 거기서는 도무지 아무 일도 처리된 적이 없고, 책상 위에는 잉크 한 방울 묻지 않은, 스무 살은 됐다고 소문난 누런 옛 종이 받침대가 놓여 있다는 것 정도였다."]], [["‘Would you object to my mentioning it to him, sir?’ I asked.", "\"제가 그분께 그 이야기를 꺼내도 괜찮으시겠습니까, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘By no means,’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"무방하지.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["‘But I have some experience of Mr. Jorkins, Copperfield.", "\"하지만 나는 조킨스 씨를 좀 겪어 봤네, 코퍼필드."], ["I wish it were otherwise, for I should be happy to meet your views in any respect.", "그렇지 않았으면 좋겠어, 어떤 식으로든 자네 뜻에 부응할 수 있다면 나야 기쁠 테니까."], ["I cannot have the objection to your mentioning it to Mr. Jorkins, Copperfield, if you think it worth while.’", "자네가 굳이 그럴 만하다고 여긴다면, 조킨스 씨에게 그 이야기를 꺼내는 데 반대하진 않겠네, 코퍼필드.\""]], [["Availing myself of this permission, which was given with a warm shake of the hand, I sat thinking about Dora, and looking at the sunlight stealing from the chimney-pots down the wall of the opposite house, until Mr. Jorkins came.", "이 허락을—따뜻한 악수와 함께 주어진 허락이었다—이용하기로 하고, 나는 앉아서 도라를 생각하며, 맞은편 집 벽을 따라 굴뚝 갓에서 슬며시 내려오는 햇살을 바라보았다. 조킨스 씨가 올 때까지."], ["I then went up to Mr. Jorkins’s room, and evidently astonished Mr. Jorkins very much by making my appearance there.", "그러고 나서 나는 조킨스 씨의 방으로 올라갔는데, 내가 거기 나타난 것이 조킨스 씨를 무척이나 놀라게 한 것이 분명했다."]], [["‘Come in, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"들어오게, 코퍼필드 군.\" 조킨스 씨가 말했다."], ["Jorkins. ‘Come in!’", "\"들어와요!\""]], [["I went in, and sat down; and stated my case to Mr. Jorkins pretty much as I had stated it to Mr. Spenlow. Mr.", "나는 안으로 들어가 앉았고, 스펜로 씨에게 했던 것과 거의 똑같이 내 사정을 조킨스 씨에게 이야기했다."], ["Jorkins was not by any means the awful creature one might have expected, but a large, mild, smooth-faced man of sixty, who took so much snuff that there was a tradition in the Commons that he lived principally on that stimulant, having little room in his system for any other article of diet.", "조킨스 씨는 결코 사람들이 예상할 법한 무시무시한 인물이 아니라, 코담배를 어찌나 많이 피우는지 박사회에는 그가 주로 그 자극제로 연명하며 다른 음식이 들어갈 자리가 별로 없다는 전설까지 있는, 덩치 크고 온화하며 얼굴이 매끈한 예순 살 노인이었다."]], [["‘You have mentioned this to Mr.", "\"이 이야기를 스펜로 씨에게 했겠지?\""], ["Spenlow, I suppose?’ said Mr. Jorkins; when he had heard me, very restlessly, to an end.", "내 말을 무척 안절부절못하며 끝까지 듣고 나서 조킨스 씨가 말했다."]], [["I answered Yes, and told him that Mr. Spenlow had introduced his name.", "나는 그렇다고 대답하고, 스펜로 씨가 그분의 이름을 거론했다고 말했다."]], [["‘He said I should object?’ asked Mr. Jorkins.", "\"내가 반대할 거라고 하던가?\" 조킨스 씨가 물었다."]], [["I was obliged to admit that Mr. Spenlow had considered it probable.", "나는 스펜로 씨가 그럴 가능성이 높다고 여겼음을 인정할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["‘I am sorry to say, Mr. Copperfield, I can’t advance your object,’ said Mr.", "\"유감스럽지만, 코퍼필드 군, 나는 자네의 목적을 도울 수가 없네.\""], ["Jorkins, nervously. ‘The fact is--but I have an appointment at the Bank, if you’ll have the goodness to excuse me.’", "조킨스 씨가 안절부절못하며 말했다. \"사실은—그런데 내가 은행에 약속이 있어서, 양해해 주면 고맙겠네만.\""]], [["With that he rose in a great hurry, and was going out of the room, when I made bold to say that I feared, then, there was no way of arranging the matter?", "그 말과 함께 그는 몹시 서둘러 일어나 방을 나서려 했고, 나는 용기를 내어 그렇다면 이 일을 처리할 방도가 없는 것 같아 두렵다고 말했다."]], [["‘No!’ said Mr.", "\"없네!\""], ["Jorkins, stopping at the door to shake his head.", "조킨스 씨가 문가에 멈춰 서서 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘Oh, no!", "\"아, 없어!"], ["I object, you know,’ which he said very rapidly, and went out.", "내가 반대하니까 말이야.\" 그는 이 말을 아주 빠르게 내뱉고는 나가 버렸다."], ["‘You must be aware, Mr. Copperfield,’ he added, looking restlessly in at the door again, ‘if Mr.", "\"자네도 알겠지만, 코퍼필드 군.\" 그가 다시 문 안으로 안절부절못하며 들여다보면서 덧붙였다."], ["Spenlow objects--’", "\"스펜로 씨가 반대한다면—\""]], [["‘Personally, he does not object, sir,’ said I.", "\"개인적으로는 그분이 반대하지 않으십니다, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Personally!’ repeated Mr. Jorkins, in an impatient manner.", "개인적으로!\" 조킨스 씨가 조바심 난 투로 되뇌었다."], ["‘I assure you there’s an objection, Mr. Copperfield.", "\"분명히 말하건대 반대가 있네, 코퍼필드 군."], ["Hopeless!", "가망 없어!"], ["What you wish to be done, can’t be done.", "자네가 바라는 일은 될 수가 없어."], ["I--I really have got an appointment at the Bank.’ With that he fairly ran away; and to the best of my knowledge, it was three days before he showed himself in the Commons again.", "나—나는 정말 은행에 약속이 있어서.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 그야말로 줄행랑을 쳤다. 그리고 내가 아는 한, 그가 박사회에 다시 모습을 드러낸 것은 사흘이 지나서였다."]], [["Being very anxious to leave no stone unturned, I waited until Mr. Spenlow came in, and then described what had passed; giving him to understand that I was not hopeless of his being able to soften the adamantine Jorkins, if he would undertake the task.", "나는 어떤 수단도 빠뜨리지 않으려는 마음이 간절해서, 스펜로 씨가 들어올 때까지 기다렸다가 그간의 경위를 들려주었다. 그리고 그가 마음만 먹는다면 그 쇠처럼 단단한 조킨스를 누그러뜨릴 수 있으리라는 희망을 버리지 않았다는 뜻을 그에게 내비쳤다."]], [["‘Copperfield,’ returned Mr.", "\"코퍼필드,\""], ["Spenlow, with a gracious smile, ‘you have not known my partner, Mr. Jorkins, as long as I have.", "스펜로 씨가 자비로운 미소를 지으며 받았다. \"자네는 내 동업자 조킨스 씨를 나만큼 오래 알지 못했네."], ["Nothing is farther from my thoughts than to attribute any degree of artifice to Mr.", "조킨스 씨에게 조금이라도 술수가 있다고 돌리는 것만큼 내 생각과 동떨어진 일은 없어."], ["Jorkins. But Mr. Jorkins has a way of stating his objections which often deceives people.", "하지만 조킨스 씨에게는 자기 반대를 늘어놓는 방식이 있는데, 그게 사람들을 종종 속인단 말이지."], ["No, Copperfield!’ shaking his head. ‘Mr.", "안 돼, 코퍼필드!\" 그가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["Jorkins is not to be moved, believe me!’", "\"조킨스 씨는 움직일 수가 없어, 내 말을 믿게!\""]], [["I was completely bewildered between Mr. Spenlow and Mr.", "나는 스펜로 씨와 조킨스 씨 둘 중 누가 정말로 반대하는 동업자인지를 두고 완전히 어리둥절해졌다."], ["Jorkins, as to which of them really was the objecting partner; but I saw with sufficient clearness that there was obduracy somewhere in the firm, and that the recovery of my aunt’s thousand pounds was out of the question.", "그러나 이 회사 어딘가에 완고함이 도사리고 있다는 것, 그리고 고모님의 천 파운드를 되찾는 일은 가망이 없다는 것만은 충분히 또렷이 알 수 있었다."], ["In a state of despondency, which I remember with anything but satisfaction, for I know it still had too much reference to myself (though always in connexion with Dora), I left the office, and went homeward.", "지금 떠올려도 도무지 흡족하지 않은 낙담 속에서—그것이 여전히 (언제나 도라와 결부되어 있긴 했으나) 나 자신에 관한 것이 너무 많았음을 알기에 그렇다—나는 사무소를 나와 집으로 향했다."]], [["I was trying to familiarize my mind with the worst, and to present to myself the arrangements we should have to make for the future in their sternest aspect, when a hackney-chariot coming after me, and stopping at my very feet, occasioned me to look up.", "나는 마음을 최악의 상황에 길들이려고, 그리고 앞으로 우리가 마련해야 할 채비를 가장 가혹한 모습 그대로 그려 보려고 애쓰고 있었다. 그때 삯마차 한 대가 내 뒤를 따라오다가 바로 내 발치에 멈춰 서는 바람에 나는 고개를 들었다."], ["A fair hand was stretched forth to me from the window; and the face I had never seen without a feeling of serenity and happiness, from the moment when it first turned back on the old oak staircase with the great broad balustrade, and when I associated its softened beauty with the stained-glass window in the church, was smiling on me.", "창문에서 고운 손 하나가 나를 향해 내밀어졌다. 그리고 그 얼굴이—커다랗고 널찍한 난간이 달린 그 오래된 떡갈나무 계단에서 처음으로 나를 돌아보던 순간부터, 그리고 그 부드러운 아름다움을 교회의 스테인드글라스 창문과 연결 짓던 순간부터, 평온함과 행복감 없이는 한 번도 본 적이 없는 그 얼굴이—나를 향해 미소 짓고 있었다."]], [["‘Agnes!’ I joyfully exclaimed.", "\"아그네스!\" 나는 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh, my dear Agnes, of all people in the world, what a pleasure to see you!’", "\"오, 사랑하는 아그네스, 세상 모든 사람 중에 너를 보다니, 이 얼마나 기쁜 일이야!\""]], [["‘Is it, indeed?’ she said, in her cordial voice.", "\"정말 그래?\" 그녀가 그 다정한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I want to talk to you so much!’ said I.", "\"네게 이야기하고 싶은 게 정말 많아!\""], ["‘It’s such a lightening of my heart, only to look at you!", "내가 말했다. \"그냥 너를 보는 것만으로도 마음이 이렇게나 가벼워지는걸!"], ["If I had had a conjuror’s cap, there is no one I should have wished for but you!’", "만약 나에게 마법사의 모자가 있었다면, 내가 바랐을 사람은 너 말고는 아무도 없었을 거야!\""]], [["‘What?’ returned Agnes.", "\"뭐라고?\" 아그네스가 받았다."]], [["‘Well!", "\"음!"], ["perhaps Dora first,’ I admitted, with a blush.", "어쩌면 도라가 먼저겠지.\" 나는 얼굴을 붉히며 인정했다."]], [["‘Certainly, Dora first, I hope,’ said Agnes, laughing.", "\"당연히 도라가 먼저라야지, 그러길 바라.\" 아그네스가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘But you next!’ said I.", "\"하지만 그다음은 너야!\""], ["‘Where are you going?’", "내가 말했다. \"어디 가는 길이야?\""]], [["She was going to my rooms to see my aunt.", "그녀는 내 방으로 고모님을 뵈러 가는 길이었다."], ["The day being very fine, she was glad to come out of the chariot, which smelt (I had my head in it all this time) like a stable put under a cucumber-frame.", "날씨가 무척 좋아서, 그녀는 (오이 모종 틀 아래 마구간을 들여놓은 것 같은 냄새가 나는—그동안 내내 나는 거기에 머리를 들이밀고 있었다) 마차에서 나오게 되어 기뻐했다."], ["I dismissed the coachman, and she took my arm, and we walked on together. She was like Hope embodied, to me.", "나는 마부를 돌려보냈고, 그녀가 내 팔을 잡았으며, 우리는 함께 걸어갔다. 그녀는 나에게 마치 희망이 사람의 모습을 입은 것 같았다."], ["How different I felt in one short minute, having Agnes at my side!", "곁에 아그네스를 두니, 단 일 분 사이에 나는 얼마나 달라진 기분이었던가!"]], [["My aunt had written her one of the odd, abrupt notes--very little longer than a Bank note--to which her epistolary efforts were usually limited.", "고모님은 그녀에게 그 별나고 무뚝뚝한 쪽지 가운데 하나를 써 보낸 터였다—고모님의 편지란 으레 은행권 지폐보다 그리 길지 않은 정도가 한계였다."], ["She had stated therein that she had fallen into adversity, and was leaving Dover for good, but had quite made up her mind to it, and was so well that nobody need be uncomfortable about her.", "그 쪽지에서 고모님은 자신이 역경에 빠졌으며 도버를 아주 떠나기로 했지만, 마음은 단단히 먹었고 또 아주 건강하니 아무도 자기 때문에 마음 불편해할 필요 없다고 적었다."], ["Agnes had come to London to see my aunt, between whom and herself there had been a mutual liking these many years: indeed, it dated from the time of my taking up my residence in Mr. Wickfield’s house.", "아그네스는 고모님을 뵈러 런던에 온 것이었는데, 두 사람 사이에는 오랜 세월에 걸친 상호 호감이 있었다. 사실 그것은 내가 위크필드 씨 댁에 머물게 된 시절로 거슬러 올라가는 것이었다."], ["She was not alone, she said.", "그녀는 혼자 온 게 아니라고 했다."], ["Her papa was with her--and Uriah Heep.", "아버지가 함께 왔다고—그리고 유라이어 힙도."]], [["‘And now they are partners,’ said I.", "\"이제 둘이 동업자가 됐지.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Confound him!’", "\"빌어먹을 자식!\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Agnes.", "\"맞아.\" 아그네스가 말했다."], ["‘They have some business here; and I took advantage of their coming, to come too.", "\"여기 볼일이 좀 있어서, 나도 그들이 오는 길에 따라온 거야. 트롯우드, 내 방문이 전적으로 다정하고 사심 없는 거라고만 생각하진 말아 줘."], ["You must not think my visit all friendly and disinterested, Trotwood, for--I am afraid I may be cruelly prejudiced--I do not like to let papa go away alone, with him.’ ‘Does he exercise the same influence over Mr. Wickfield still, Agnes?’", "왜냐하면—내가 너무 모질게 편견을 가진 건지도 모르지만—아버지가 그 사람하고만 단둘이 떠나시게 두는 게 싫거든.\" \"그 자식이 아직도 위크필드 씨에게 똑같이 영향력을 휘두르고 있어, 아그네스?\""]], [["Agnes shook her head.", "아그네스가 고개를 저었다."], ["‘There is such a change at home,’ said she, ‘that you would scarcely know the dear old house.", "\"집안이 너무 많이 바뀌어서,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네가 그 정든 옛집을 알아보기도 어려울 거야."], ["They live with us now.’", "이제 그들이 우리와 함께 살거든.\""]], [["‘They?’ said I.", "\"그들이라니?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Heep and his mother.", "\"힙 씨하고 그 어머니 말이야."], ["He sleeps in your old room,’ said Agnes, looking up into my face.", "그 사람이 네 옛날 방에서 잔단다.\" 아그네스가 내 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘I wish I had the ordering of his dreams,’ said I.", "\"그자의 꿈을 내가 짜 줄 수 있다면 좋으련만.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘He wouldn’t sleep there long.’", "\"거기서 오래 못 잘 텐데.\""]], [["‘I keep my own little room,’ said Agnes, ‘where I used to learn my lessons.", "\"나는 내 작은 방을 그대로 지키고 있어.\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"예전에 공부하던 그 방 말이야."], ["How the time goes! You remember?", "세월이 어쩜 이리도 흐르는지! 기억나?"], ["The little panelled room that opens from the drawing-room?’", "응접실에서 이어지는, 판자를 댄 그 작은 방?\""]], [["‘Remember, Agnes? When I saw you, for the first time, coming out at the door, with your quaint little basket of keys hanging at your side?’", "\"기억나느냐고, 아그네스? 내가 처음으로 너를 봤을 때, 그 정겨운 작은 열쇠 바구니를 허리춤에 늘어뜨린 채 그 문에서 나오던 너를?\""]], [["‘It is just the same,’ said Agnes, smiling.", "\"여전히 그대로야.\" 아그네스가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["‘I am glad you think of it so pleasantly.", "\"네가 그걸 그렇게 기분 좋게 떠올려 주니 기뻐."], ["We were very happy.’", "우린 정말 행복했었지.\""]], [["‘We were, indeed,’ said I.", "\"정말 그랬어.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I keep that room to myself still; but I cannot always desert Mrs. Heep, you know.", "\"나는 그 방을 아직도 나만의 것으로 지키고 있어. 하지만 힙 부인을 늘 외면할 수만은 없잖아. 그래서,\""], ["And so,’ said Agnes, quietly, ‘I feel obliged to bear her company, when I might prefer to be alone.", "아그네스가 조용히 말했다. \"혼자 있고 싶을 때에도 그 부인 곁을 지켜야 한다는 의무감을 느껴."], ["But I have no other reason to complain of her.", "하지만 그것 말고는 그 부인을 두고 불평할 거리가 없어."], ["If she tires me, sometimes, by her praises of her son, it is only natural in a mother. He is a very good son to her.’", "가끔 아들 자랑으로 나를 지치게 하긴 해도, 그건 어머니로서 지극히 당연한 일이지. 그 사람은 그 부인에게 무척 효성스러운 아들이거든.\""]], [["I looked at Agnes when she said these words, without detecting in her any consciousness of Uriah’s design.", "아그네스가 이 말을 할 때 나는 그녀를 바라보았지만, 그녀에게서 유라이어의 속셈을 알아챈 기색은 전혀 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Her mild but earnest eyes met mine with their own beautiful frankness, and there was no change in her gentle face.", "부드럽지만 진지한 그녀의 두 눈은 그 특유의 아름다운 솔직함으로 내 눈을 마주했고, 그 온화한 얼굴에는 아무런 변화가 없었다."]], [["‘The chief evil of their presence in the house,’ said Agnes, ‘is that I cannot be as near papa as I could wish--Uriah Heep being so much between us--and cannot watch over him, if that is not too bold a thing to say, as closely as I would.", "\"그들이 그 집에 있어서 생기는 가장 큰 폐단은,\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"내가 바라는 만큼 아버지 가까이에 있을 수 없다는 거야—유라이어 힙이 우리 사이를 워낙 가로막고 있어서—그리고, 이런 말이 너무 당돌하지 않다면, 내가 바라는 만큼 가까이서 아버지를 보살펴 드릴 수 없다는 거지."], ["But if any fraud or treachery is practising against him, I hope that simple love and truth will be strong in the end.", "하지만 만약 아버지를 상대로 어떤 속임수나 배신이 꾸며지고 있다 해도, 나는 결국 단순한 사랑과 진실이 강하리라고 믿어."], ["I hope that real love and truth are stronger in the end than any evil or misfortune in the world.’", "진정한 사랑과 진실이 이 세상 그 어떤 악이나 불행보다도 끝내 더 강하리라고 믿어.\""]], [["A certain bright smile, which I never saw on any other face, died away, even while I thought how good it was, and how familiar it had once been to me; and she asked me, with a quick change of expression (we were drawing very near my street), if I knew how the reverse in my aunt’s circumstances had been brought about.", "다른 어떤 얼굴에서도 본 적 없는 어떤 환한 미소가, 그것이 얼마나 선한지, 그리고 한때 나에게 얼마나 익숙했던 것인지를 떠올리는 사이에도 어느새 스러져 갔다. 그러고는 그녀가 (우리는 우리 거리에 무척 가까워지고 있었다) 표정을 빠르게 바꾸며, 고모님 형편이 어쩌다 그렇게 뒤바뀌게 됐는지 아느냐고 나에게 물었다."], ["On my replying no, she had not told me yet, Agnes became thoughtful, and I fancied I felt her arm tremble in mine.", "모른다고, 고모님이 아직 나에게 말해 주지 않았다고 내가 대답하자, 아그네스는 생각에 잠겼고, 나는 그녀의 팔이 내 팔에 닿은 채 떨리는 것을 느낀 듯했다."]], [["We found my aunt alone, in a state of some excitement. A difference of opinion had arisen between herself and Mrs.", "우리가 가 보니 고모님은 혼자였는데, 다소 흥분한 상태였다."], ["Crupp, on an abstract question (the propriety of chambers being inhabited by the gentler sex); and my aunt, utterly indifferent to spasms on the part of Mrs.", "고모님과 크룹 부인 사이에 어떤 추상적인 문제(점잖은 여성이 셋방에 거주하는 것이 온당한가 하는 문제)를 두고 의견 차이가 생긴 것이었다."], ["Crupp, had cut the dispute short, by informing that lady that she smelt of my brandy, and that she would trouble her to walk out. Both of these expressions Mrs.", "그래서 고모님은 크룹 부인 쪽의 발작 따위에는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고, 그 부인에게서 내 브랜디 냄새가 난다고, 그러니 미안하지만 좀 나가 주셔야겠다고 일러 줌으로써 논쟁을 단번에 잘라 버렸다."], ["Crupp considered actionable, and had expressed her intention of bringing before a ‘British Judy’--meaning, it was supposed, the bulwark of our national liberties.", "크룹 부인은 이 두 표현을 모두 소송감으로 여겨, '브리티시 주디' 앞에 끌고 가겠다는 뜻을 밝혔는데—짐작건대 우리 국민적 자유의 보루를 두고 하는 말인 듯했다."]], [["My aunt, however, having had time to cool, while Peggotty was out showing Mr. Dick the soldiers at the Horse Guards--and being, besides, greatly pleased to see Agnes--rather plumed herself on the affair than otherwise, and received us with unimpaired good humour.", "그러나 고모님은, 패거티가 딕 씨에게 호스 가즈의 병사들을 구경시켜 주러 나가 있는 사이 마음을 식힐 시간이 있었던 데다가, 아그네스를 보게 되어 무척 기뻤던 터라, 이 일을 도리어 자랑스레 우쭐대며 변함없는 좋은 기분으로 우리를 맞았다."], ["When Agnes laid her bonnet on the table, and sat down beside her, I could not but think, looking on her mild eyes and her radiant forehead, how natural it seemed to have her there; how trustfully, although she was so young and inexperienced, my aunt confided in her; how strong she was, indeed, in simple love and truth.", "아그네스가 모자를 탁자에 내려놓고 고모님 곁에 앉았을 때, 나는 그녀의 온화한 눈과 빛나는 이마를 바라보며 이런 생각을 하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 거기 있는 것이 얼마나 자연스러워 보이는지, 그녀가 그토록 젊고 미숙한데도 고모님이 얼마나 미더이 그녀를 믿는지, 그리고 그녀가 단순한 사랑과 진실로 정말 얼마나 강한지를."]], [["We began to talk about my aunt’s losses, and I told them what I had tried to do that morning.", "우리는 고모님의 손실에 대해 이야기하기 시작했고, 나는 그날 아침 내가 해 보려 했던 일을 그들에게 들려주었다."]], [["‘Which was injudicious, Trot,’ said my aunt, ‘but well meant.", "\"그건 분별없는 짓이었어, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"하지만 뜻은 좋았지."], ["You are a generous boy--I suppose I must say, young man, now--and I am proud of you, my dear.", "너는 너그러운 아이야—이제는 청년이라고 해야 하나 보다—그리고 나는 네가 자랑스럽다, 얘야."], ["So far, so good.", "거기까진 좋아."], ["Now, Trot and Agnes, let us look the case of Betsey Trotwood in the face, and see how it stands.’", "자, 이제 트롯과 아그네스, 우리 베시 트롯우드의 처지를 정면으로 마주 보고, 어떻게 돌아가는지 따져 보자.\""]], [["I observed Agnes turn pale, as she looked very attentively at my aunt.", "나는 아그네스가 고모님을 무척 유심히 바라보면서 얼굴이 새파래지는 것을 보았다."], ["My aunt, patting her cat, looked very attentively at Agnes.", "고모님은 고양이를 쓰다듬으며 아그네스를 무척 유심히 바라보았다."]], [["‘Betsey Trotwood,’ said my aunt, who had always kept her money matters to herself.", "\"베시 트롯우드는,\" 늘 자기 돈 문제는 혼자만 간직해 온 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘--I don’t mean your sister, Trot, my dear, but myself--had a certain property.", "\"—네 누이를 두고 하는 말이 아니라, 얘야, 나 자신을 두고 하는 말이다—얼마간의 재산이 있었어."], ["It don’t matter how much; enough to live on.", "얼마나 되는지는 중요하지 않아. 먹고살 만큼은 됐지."], ["More; for she had saved a little, and added to it.", "그 이상이기도 했고. 조금 모아서 보태기도 했으니까."], ["Betsey funded her property for some time, and then, by the advice of her man of business, laid it out on landed security.", "베시는 한동안 그 재산을 공채에 넣어 뒀다가, 자기 사업 대리인의 조언에 따라 토지 담보에 투자했어. 그건 아주 잘됐고, 이자도 아주 짭짤하게 들어왔지, 베시가 원금을 다 돌려받을 때까지. 내가 지금 베시를 무슨 군함처럼 말하고 있구나. 자!"], ["That did very well, and returned very good interest, till Betsey was paid off.", "그러고 나서 베시는 새로운 투자처를 찾아 둘러봐야 했어. 베시는 이제 자기가 사업 대리인보다 더 현명하다고 생각했지."], ["I am talking of Betsey as if she was a man-of-war. Well! Then, Betsey had to look about her, for a new investment.", "그 무렵엔 그 사람이 예전만큼 유능한 사업가가 아니었거든—아그네스, 네 아버지 얘기다—그래서 자기 손으로 직접 투자하기로 마음먹은 거야. 그래서 자기 돼지들을 끌고,\""], ["She thought she was wiser, now, than her man of business, who was not such a good man of business by this time, as he used to be--I am alluding to your father, Agnes--and she took it into her head to lay it out for herself.", "고모님이 말했다. \"낯선 시장으로 갔는데, 알고 보니 아주 형편없는 시장이었지 뭐냐. 처음엔 광산 쪽에서 잃고, 그다음엔 잠수 쪽에서 잃었어—보물을 건져 올린다나 뭐 그런 톰 티들러 같은 허튼소리였지.\""], ["So she took her pigs,’ said my aunt, ‘to a foreign market; and a very bad market it turned out to be.", "고모님이 코를 문지르며 설명했다. \"그러고는 또 광산 쪽에서 잃고, 마지막으로, 일을 아주 완전히 결딴내려는 듯, 은행 쪽에서 잃었어."], ["First, she lost in the mining way, and then she lost in the diving way--fishing up treasure, or some such Tom Tiddler nonsense,’ explained my aunt, rubbing her nose; ‘and then she lost in the mining way again, and, last of all, to set the thing entirely to rights, she lost in the banking way.", "한동안 그 은행 주식이 얼마나 값나갔는지는 모르겠다.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"백 퍼센트가 그중 제일 낮은 거였던 것 같아."], ["I don’t know what the Bank shares were worth for a little while,’ said my aunt; ‘cent per cent was the lowest of it, I believe; but the Bank was at the other end of the world, and tumbled into space, for what I know; anyhow, it fell to pieces, and never will and never can pay sixpence; and Betsey’s sixpences were all there, and there’s an end of them.", "하지만 그 은행은 세상 저편 끝에 있었고, 내가 아는 한 허공 속으로 곤두박질쳐 버렸어. 어쨌든 산산조각이 나서, 육 펜스도 갚지 못할 거고 갚을 수도 없어. 그리고 베시의 육 펜스들이 죄다 거기 들어 있었으니, 그것들도 그것으로 끝장이야."], ["Least said, soonest mended!’", "말이 적을수록 빨리 아무는 법이지!\""]], [["My aunt concluded this philosophical summary, by fixing her eyes with a kind of triumph on Agnes, whose colour was gradually returning.", "고모님은 이 철학적인 요약을 마무리하며, 아그네스에게 일종의 승리감 어린 눈길을 박았는데, 아그네스의 얼굴빛은 차츰 되돌아오고 있었다."]], [["‘Dear Miss Trotwood, is that all the history?’ said Agnes.", "\"트롯우드 아주머니, 그게 이야기의 전부인가요?\" 아그네스가 말했다."]], [["‘I hope it’s enough, child,’ said my aunt.", "\"이만하면 충분하길 바란다, 얘야.\""], ["‘If there had been more money to lose, it wouldn’t have been all, I dare say.", "고모님이 말했다. \"잃을 돈이 더 있었더라면 이게 전부는 아니었을 거야, 분명히."], ["Betsey would have contrived to throw that after the rest, and make another chapter, I have little doubt.", "베시가 그것마저 나머지 뒤로 던져 버려서 또 한 장을 채웠을 게 틀림없지."], ["But there was no more money, and there’s no more story.’", "하지만 더는 돈이 없었으니, 더는 이야기도 없는 거야.\""]], [["Agnes had listened at first with suspended breath.", "아그네스는 처음에 숨을 죽이고 듣고 있었다."], ["Her colour still came and went, but she breathed more freely.", "얼굴빛이 여전히 들락날락했지만, 그녀의 숨결은 한결 편안해졌다."], ["I thought I knew why. I thought she had had some fear that her unhappy father might be in some way to blame for what had happened.", "나는 그 까닭을 안다고 생각했다. 그녀는 불행한 자기 아버지가 일어난 일에 어떤 식으로든 책임이 있을까 봐 두려워했던 것이라고 나는 생각했다."], ["My aunt took her hand in hers, and laughed.", "고모님이 그녀의 손을 자기 손으로 잡으며 웃었다."]], [["‘Is that all?’ repeated my aunt.", "\"그게 다냐고?\" 고모님이 되뇌었다."], ["‘Why, yes, that’s all, except, “And she lived happy ever afterwards.”", "\"그래, 그렇지, 그게 다야. '그리고 그녀는 그 뒤로도 행복하게 살았답니다'를 빼면 말이지."], ["Perhaps I may add that of Betsey yet, one of these days.", "어쩌면 언젠가 베시에게 그 한마디를 더 붙일 수 있을지도 모르지."], ["Now, Agnes, you have a wise head.", "자, 아그네스, 너는 지혜로운 머리를 가졌어."], ["So have you, Trot, in some things, though I can’t compliment you always’; and here my aunt shook her own at me, with an energy peculiar to herself.", "너도 그래, 트롯, 어떤 일에 있어서는 말이다. 비록 늘 칭찬해 줄 수는 없지만.\" 그러면서 고모님은 자기 특유의 기세로 나를 향해 고개를 흔들어 댔다."], ["‘What’s to be done?", "\"어떻게 하면 좋을까?"], ["Here’s the cottage, taking one time with another, will produce say seventy pounds a year.", "여기 이 오두막이, 이런저런 때를 다 따져 보면, 일 년에 한 칠십 파운드쯤은 나올 거야."], ["I think we may safely put it down at that.", "그쯤으로 잡아 두면 안전할 것 같구나."], ["Well!--That’s all we’ve got,’ said my aunt; with whom it was an idiosyncrasy, as it is with some horses, to stop very short when she appeared to be in a fair way of going on for a long while.", "자!—그게 우리가 가진 전부야.\" 고모님이 말했는데, 어떤 말들이 그렇듯이 고모님도 한참 더 이어 갈 것처럼 보이다가 아주 뚝 멈춰 버리는 별난 버릇이 있었다."]], [["‘Then,’ said my aunt, after a rest, ‘there’s Dick.", "\"그리고,\" 잠시 쉬었다가 고모님이 말했다. \"딕이 있지."], ["He’s good for a hundred a-year, but of course that must be expended on himself.", "그 사람은 일 년에 백 파운드쯤 되지만, 물론 그건 그 사람 자신을 위해 써야 해."], ["I would sooner send him away, though I know I am the only person who appreciates him, than have him, and not spend his money on himself.", "그 사람을 곁에 두고 그 돈을 그 사람한테 안 쓰느니, 차라리 떠나보내는 편이 낫겠어—비록 그 사람을 알아주는 사람이 나밖에 없다는 걸 알지만 말이야."], ["How can Trot and I do best, upon our means?", "우리 형편으로 트롯과 내가 어떻게 하는 게 최선일까?"], ["What do you say, Agnes?’", "어떻게 생각하니, 아그네스?\""]], [["‘I say, aunt,’ I interposed, ‘that I must do something!’", "\"제 생각엔, 고모님,\" 내가 끼어들었다. \"제가 무언가를 해야 해요!\""]], [["‘Go for a soldier, do you mean?’ returned my aunt, alarmed; ‘or go to sea?", "\"군인이 되겠다는 거냐?\" 고모님이 놀라서 받았다. \"아니면 바다로 나가겠다고?"], ["I won’t hear of it.", "그런 소리는 듣지 않겠어."], ["You are to be a proctor.", "너는 변호사가 될 거야."], ["We’re not going to have any knockings on the head in THIS family, if you please, sir.’", "이 집안에서는, 미안하지만, 누구도 머리통이 깨지는 일 따위는 없을 거다, 도련님.\""]], [["I was about to explain that I was not desirous of introducing that mode of provision into the family, when Agnes inquired if my rooms were held for any long term?", "나는 그런 생계 방편을 우리 집안에 들여올 생각은 없다고 설명하려던 참이었는데, 그때 아그네스가 내 방을 오래 계약했느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘You come to the point, my dear,’ said my aunt.", "\"바로 그게 핵심이야, 얘야.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘They are not to be got rid of, for six months at least, unless they could be underlet, and that I don’t believe.", "\"적어도 여섯 달 동안은 처분할 수가 없어. 전대를 줄 수만 있다면 모를까, 그건 안 될 것 같고."], ["The last man died here. Five people out of six would die--of course--of that woman in nankeen with the flannel petticoat.", "여기서 마지막에 살던 사람은 죽어 나갔지. 여섯 중 다섯은 죽을 거야—당연하지—플란넬 속치마에 황색 무명옷 차림인 저 여자 때문에."], ["I have a little ready money; and I agree with you, the best thing we can do, is, to live the term out here, and get a bedroom hard by.’", "나한테 현찰이 조금 있으니, 우리가 할 수 있는 최선은 여기서 계약 기간을 다 채우고 사는 거라는 네 말에 동의한다. 그리고 바로 가까이에 침실을 하나 얻는 거지.\""]], [["I thought it my duty to hint at the discomfort my aunt would sustain, from living in a continual state of guerilla warfare with Mrs.", "나는 크룹 부인과 끊임없는 게릴라전을 벌이며 사는 통에 고모님이 겪을 불편함을 넌지시 일러 드리는 것이 내 도리라고 생각했다."], ["Crupp; but she disposed of that objection summarily by declaring that, on the first demonstration of hostilities, she was prepared to astonish Mrs. Crupp for the whole remainder of her natural life.", "그러나 고모님은, 적대 행위의 첫 기미가 보이는 순간 크룹 부인을 평생 두고두고 까무러치게 만들 채비가 되어 있다고 선언함으로써 그 반대를 단번에 처리해 버렸다."]], [["‘I have been thinking, Trotwood,’ said Agnes, diffidently, ‘that if you had time--’", "\"내가 생각해 봤는데, 트롯우드,\" 아그네스가 조심스레 말했다. \"만약 네가 시간이 있다면—\""]], [["‘I have a good deal of time, Agnes.", "\"나는 시간이 아주 많아, 아그네스."], ["I am always disengaged after four or five o’clock, and I have time early in the morning.", "네다섯 시 이후에는 늘 비어 있고, 이른 아침에도 시간이 있어."], ["In one way and another,’ said I, conscious of reddening a little as I thought of the hours and hours I had devoted to fagging about town, and to and fro upon the Norwood Road, ‘I have abundance of time.’", "이래저래,\" 시내를 쏘다니고 노우드 길을 오가는 데 들인 그 숱한 시간들을 떠올리며 얼굴이 조금 붉어지는 걸 의식한 채 나는 말했다. \"나는 시간이 넘쳐.\""]], [["‘I know you would not mind,’ said Agnes, coming to me, and speaking in a low voice, so full of sweet and hopeful consideration that I hear it now, ‘the duties of a secretary.’", "\"네가 꺼리지 않을 거라고 생각해,\" 아그네스가 나에게 다가오며, 어찌나 달콤하고 희망찬 배려가 가득 담긴 낮은 목소리로 말했던지 지금도 그 소리가 들리는 듯한데, \"비서 일을 말이야.\""]], [["‘Mind, my dear Agnes?’", "\"꺼리다니, 아그네스?\""]], [["‘Because,’ continued Agnes, ‘Doctor Strong has acted on his intention of retiring, and has come to live in London; and he asked papa, I know, if he could recommend him one.", "\"왜냐하면,\" 아그네스가 말을 이었다. \"스트롱 박사님이 은퇴하시려던 뜻을 실행에 옮겨서, 런던에 와서 사시거든."], ["Don’t you think he would rather have his favourite old pupil near him, than anybody else?’", "그리고 아버지에게, 내가 알기로, 적당한 사람을 추천해 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물으셨대. 박사님이 다른 누구보다 자기가 아끼던 옛 제자를 곁에 두고 싶어 하지 않으실까?\""]], [["‘Dear Agnes!’ said I.", "\"사랑하는 아그네스!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘What should I do without you!", "\"네가 없으면 나는 어떻게 해야 할까!"], ["You are always my good angel. I told you so.", "너는 언제나 나의 선한 천사야. 전에도 그렇게 말했잖아."], ["I never think of you in any other light.’", "나는 너를 다른 어떤 모습으로도 떠올린 적이 없어.\""]], [["Agnes answered with her pleasant laugh, that one good Angel (meaning Dora) was enough; and went on to remind me that the Doctor had been used to occupy himself in his study, early in the morning, and in the evening--and that probably my leisure would suit his requirements very well.", "아그네스는 그 환한 웃음으로 대답하기를, 선한 천사 하나면(도라를 두고 하는 말이었다) 충분하다고 했다. 그러고는 박사님이 이른 아침과 저녁에 서재에서 일하시는 습관이 있었으니, 아마 내 한가한 시간이 박사님의 필요와 잘 맞을 거라고 일러 주었다."], ["I was scarcely more delighted with the prospect of earning my own bread, than with the hope of earning it under my old master; in short, acting on the advice of Agnes, I sat down and wrote a letter to the Doctor, stating my object, and appointing to call on him next day at ten in the forenoon.", "나는 내 손으로 빵을 번다는 전망에 기뻤거니와, 그 빵을 옛 스승 밑에서 벌게 되리라는 희망에는 그에 못지않게 기뻤다. 요컨대, 아그네스의 조언에 따라 나는 자리에 앉아 박사님께 편지를 써서 내 목적을 밝히고, 이튿날 오전 열 시에 찾아뵙겠다고 약속을 잡았다."], ["This I addressed to Highgate--for in that place, so memorable to me, he lived--and went and posted, myself, without losing a minute.", "이 편지를 나는 하이게이트로 부쳤는데—나에게 그토록 잊지 못할 그곳에 박사님이 살고 계셨기 때문이다—그리고 일 분도 지체하지 않고 직접 가서 부쳤다."]], [["Wherever Agnes was, some agreeable token of her noiseless presence seemed inseparable from the place.", "아그네스가 있는 곳이라면 어디든, 그녀의 소리 없는 손길이 남긴 어떤 흐뭇한 흔적이 그 자리와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 듯했다."], ["When I came back, I found my aunt’s birds hanging, just as they had hung so long in the parlour window of the cottage; and my easy-chair imitating my aunt’s much easier chair in its position at the open window; and even the round green fan, which my aunt had brought away with her, screwed on to the window-sill.", "내가 돌아와 보니, 고모님의 새들이 오두막의 거실 창가에 그토록 오래 걸려 있던 그대로 걸려 있었고, 내 안락의자는 열린 창가에 놓인 고모님의 훨씬 더 편안한 의자의 자리를 흉내 내고 있었으며, 심지어 고모님이 가지고 온 그 둥글고 초록빛 부채까지 창턱에 단단히 나사로 고정되어 있었다."], ["I knew who had done all this, by its seeming to have quietly done itself; and I should have known in a moment who had arranged my neglected books in the old order of my school days, even if I had supposed Agnes to be miles away, instead of seeing her busy with them, and smiling at the disorder into which they had fallen.", "나는 이 모든 것을 누가 했는지 알아챘는데, 그것이 마치 저절로 조용히 된 것처럼 보였기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 내 학창 시절의 옛 순서대로 정리된, 내가 내팽개쳐 두었던 책들을 보았는데, 설령 아그네스가 거기서 몇 마일은 떨어져 있다고 여겼더라도, 그것들이 빠진 그 어수선함을 보고 미소 짓는 그녀를 직접 보지 않았더라도, 나는 누가 그것들을 정리했는지 한순간에 알아보았을 것이다."]], [["My aunt was quite gracious on the subject of the Thames (it really did look very well with the sun upon it, though not like the sea before the cottage), but she could not relent towards the London smoke, which, she said, ‘peppered everything’.", "고모님은 템스강에 대해서는 무척 너그러웠지만(햇살이 비치니 정말 아주 보기 좋았다. 오두막 앞 바다 같지는 않았지만), 런던의 매연에 대해서는 도무지 마음을 풀지 못하고, 그것이 '모든 것에 후추를 뿌려 놓는다'고 말했다."], ["A complete revolution, in which Peggotty bore a prominent part, was being effected in every corner of my rooms, in regard of this pepper; and I was looking on, thinking how little even Peggotty seemed to do with a good deal of bustle, and how much Agnes did without any bustle at all, when a knock came at the door.", "이 후추를 두고 내 방 구석구석에서는 패거티가 두드러진 역할을 맡은 일대 혁명이 일어나고 있었다. 나는 그 광경을 지켜보면서, 패거티는 부산을 떠는 것에 비해 어쩜 이리도 하는 일이 적어 보이고, 아그네스는 도무지 부산이라곤 떨지 않으면서도 어쩜 이리도 많은 일을 해내는지 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 문에서 노크 소리가 났다."]], [["‘I think,’ said Agnes, turning pale, ‘it’s papa.", "\"아무래도,\" 아그네스가 얼굴이 새파래지며 말했다."], ["He promised me that he would come.’", "\"아버지인 것 같아. 오시겠다고 약속하셨거든.\""]], [["I opened the door, and admitted, not only Mr. Wickfield, but Uriah Heep.", "나는 문을 열어, 위크필드 씨뿐만 아니라 유라이어 힙까지 들어오게 했다."], ["I had not seen Mr. Wickfield for some time.", "나는 위크필드 씨를 한동안 보지 못한 터였다."], ["I was prepared for a great change in him, after what I had heard from Agnes, but his appearance shocked me.", "아그네스에게 들은 이야기가 있어 그가 크게 달라졌으리라 마음의 준비는 했지만, 그의 모습은 나에게 충격을 주었다."]], [["It was not that he looked many years older, though still dressed with the old scrupulous cleanliness; or that there was an unwholesome ruddiness upon his face; or that his eyes were full and bloodshot; or that there was a nervous trembling in his hand, the cause of which I knew, and had for some years seen at work.", "그가 여러 해는 더 늙어 보여서가 아니었다—여전히 예전처럼 꼼꼼하고 정갈하게 차려입긴 했지만. 그의 얼굴에 병색이 도는 불그스레함이 어려 있어서도, 그의 두 눈이 충혈되어 부풀어 있어서도, 그의 손이 신경질적으로 떨려서도 아니었다—그 떨림의 원인을 나는 알고 있었고, 몇 해 전부터 그것이 작용하는 것을 지켜봐 온 터였다."], ["It was not that he had lost his good looks, or his old bearing of a gentleman--for that he had not--but the thing that struck me most, was, that with the evidences of his native superiority still upon him, he should submit himself to that crawling impersonation of meanness, Uriah Heep.", "그가 잘생긴 외모를, 혹은 신사다운 옛 풍모를 잃어서도 아니었다—그것은 잃지 않았으니까. 나에게 가장 충격을 준 것은, 그가 타고난 우월함의 증거를 여전히 지닌 채로, 저 비루함이 기어 다니는 화신, 유라이어 힙에게 자신을 굽실거리며 내맡기고 있다는 사실이었다."], ["The reversal of the two natures, in their relative positions, Uriah’s of power and Mr. Wickfield’s of dependence, was a sight more painful to me than I can express.", "두 천성의 상대적 위치가 뒤바뀐 것—유라이어가 권력의 자리에, 위크필드 씨가 의존의 자리에 선 것—은 내가 표현할 수 없을 만큼 고통스러운 광경이었다."], ["If I had seen an Ape taking command of a Man, I should hardly have thought it a more degrading spectacle.", "만약 원숭이가 사람을 부리는 것을 보았다 한들, 나는 그보다 더 굴욕적인 구경거리라고 여기기 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["He appeared to be only too conscious of it himself.", "그 자신도 그것을 너무나 잘 의식하는 듯했다."], ["When he came in, he stood still; and with his head bowed, as if he felt it.", "그는 들어오면서 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 그것을 느끼기라도 하는 양 고개를 숙였다."], ["This was only for a moment; for Agnes softly said to him, ‘Papa!", "이것은 잠깐뿐이었으니, 아그네스가 부드럽게 그에게 \"아버지!"], ["Here is Miss Trotwood--and Trotwood, whom you have not seen for a long while!’ and then he approached, and constrainedly gave my aunt his hand, and shook hands more cordially with me.", "여기 트롯우드 아주머니가 계세요—그리고 오랫동안 못 보신 트롯우드도요!\"라고 말했기 때문이다. 그러자 그가 다가와, 어색하게 고모님에게 손을 내밀었고, 나와는 좀 더 진심으로 악수했다."], ["In the moment’s pause I speak of, I saw Uriah’s countenance form itself into a most ill-favoured smile.", "내가 말한 그 잠깐의 멈춤 사이에, 나는 유라이어의 얼굴이 더없이 흉측한 미소로 일그러지는 것을 보았다."], ["Agnes saw it too, I think, for she shrank from him.", "아그네스도 그것을 본 것 같았는데, 그녀가 그에게서 움찔 물러섰기 때문이다."]], [["What my aunt saw, or did not see, I defy the science of physiognomy to have made out, without her own consent.", "고모님이 무엇을 보았는지, 혹은 무엇을 보지 못했는지는, 고모님 자신의 동의 없이는 관상학이라는 학문도 알아낼 수 없으리라고 나는 장담한다."], ["I believe there never was anybody with such an imperturbable countenance when she chose.", "고모님이 마음만 먹으면 그토록 흔들림 없는 얼굴을 한 사람은 일찍이 없었으리라고 나는 믿는다."], ["Her face might have been a dead-wall on the occasion in question, for any light it threw upon her thoughts; until she broke silence with her usual abruptness.", "문제의 그 순간, 고모님의 얼굴은 자기 생각에 대해 어떤 빛도 비추지 않는, 막다른 벽 같았을 것이다. 마침내 고모님이 늘 그렇듯 불쑥 침묵을 깨뜨릴 때까지."]], [["‘Well, Wickfield!’ said my aunt; and he looked up at her for the first time.", "\"그래, 위크필드!\" 고모님이 말하자, 그가 처음으로 고모님을 올려다보았다."], ["‘I have been telling your daughter how well I have been disposing of my money for myself, because I couldn’t trust it to you, as you were growing rusty in business matters.", "\"내가 당신 딸한테 얘기하던 참이었어요. 내가 내 돈을 나 혼자 힘으로 얼마나 잘 굴렸는지 말이에요. 당신이 사업 일에 점점 녹슬어 가니, 당신한테 맡길 수가 없어서요."], ["We have been taking counsel together, and getting on very well, all things considered. Agnes is worth the whole firm, in my opinion.’", "우리는 함께 의논을 해 왔고, 이래저래 따져 보면 아주 잘 풀리고 있어요. 내 생각엔 아그네스가 그 회사 전체만큼의 값어치가 있어요.\""]], [["‘If I may umbly make the remark,’ said Uriah Heep, with a writhe, ‘I fully agree with Miss Betsey Trotwood, and should be only too appy if Miss Agnes was a partner.’", "\"외람되이 한 말씀 드리자면,\" 유라이어 힙이 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"저는 베시 트롯우드 양의 말씀에 전적으로 동감입니다요. 아그네스 양이 동업자가 되어 주신다면 그저 더없이 기쁠 따름이겠습니다요.\""]], [["‘You’re a partner yourself, you know,’ returned my aunt, ‘and that’s about enough for you, I expect.", "\"당신 자신이 동업자잖아, 안 그래?\" 고모님이 받았다. \"그쯤이면 당신한테는 충분하고도 남을 것 같은데."], ["How do you find yourself, sir?’", "당신은 어떻게 지내요, 선생?\""]], [["In acknowledgement of this question, addressed to him with extraordinary curtness, Mr. Heep, uncomfortably clutching the blue bag he carried, replied that he was pretty well, he thanked my aunt, and hoped she was the same.", "남달리 무뚝뚝하게 자기에게 던져진 이 질문에 답하느라, 힙 씨는 들고 있던 푸른 가방을 불편하게 움켜쥔 채, 덕분에 그럭저럭 잘 지낸다고, 고모님께 감사하다고, 고모님도 그러하시기를 바란다고 대답했다."]], [["‘And you, Master--I should say, Mister Copperfield,’ pursued Uriah.", "\"그리고 너는 말이다, 도련님—아니, 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야겠군요.\" 유라이어가 말을 이었다. \"잘 지내시는 것 같아 보이는데요!"], ["‘I hope I see you well!", "코퍼필드 씨, 당신을 보게 되어 기쁩니다요, 지금 같은 형편에서도 말이지요.\" 나는 그 말을 믿었다."], ["I am rejoiced to see you, Mister Copperfield, even under present circumstances.’ I believed that; for he seemed to relish them very much.", "그는 그 형편을 무척이나 음미하는 듯 보였으니까. \"지금 같은 형편이야 당신 친구들이 당신께 바라던 바는 아니겠지요, 코퍼필드 씨. 하지만 사람을 만드는 건 돈이 아니에요."], ["‘Present circumstances is not what your friends would wish for you, Mister Copperfield, but it isn’t money makes the man: it’s--I am really unequal with my umble powers to express what it is,’ said Uriah, with a fawning jerk, ‘but it isn’t money!’", "그건—저는 정말이지 제 미천한 능력으로는 그게 뭔지 표현할 수가 없습니다요.\" 유라이어가 알랑거리며 몸을 홱 비틀었다. \"하지만 그건 돈이 아니에요!\""]], [["Here he shook hands with me: not in the common way, but standing at a good distance from me, and lifting my hand up and down like a pump handle, that he was a little afraid of.", "여기서 그는 나와 악수했다. 보통의 방식이 아니라, 나에게서 꽤 멀찍이 떨어져 선 채로, 마치 펌프 손잡이라도 되는 양 내 손을 위아래로 들었다 놓았다 하면서, 그것을 약간 두려워하는 듯이."]], [["‘And how do you think we are looking, Master Copperfield,--I should say, Mister?’ fawned Uriah.", "\"그래, 우리가 어때 보이세요, 코퍼필드 도련님—아니, 씨라고 해야겠군요?\" 유라이어가 알랑거렸다. \"위크필드 씨가 활짝 피어 보이지 않습니까요, 선생?"], ["‘Don’t you find Mr. Wickfield blooming, sir?", "우리 회사에서는 세월이 별 표를 안 내요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 다만 미천한 자들을 일으켜 세운 것만은 예외지요."], ["Years don’t tell much in our firm, Master Copperfield, except in raising up the umble, namely, mother and self--and in developing,’ he added, as an afterthought, ‘the beautiful, namely, Miss Agnes.’", "그러니까 어머니와 저 말입니다요—그리고 발현시킨 것도요.\" 그가 뒤늦게 떠올린 듯 덧붙였다. \"아름다운 분, 그러니까 아그네스 양을요.\""]], [["He jerked himself about, after this compliment, in such an intolerable manner, that my aunt, who had sat looking straight at him, lost all patience.", "이 찬사를 늘어놓은 뒤 그는 어찌나 견디기 힘들게 몸을 홱홱 비틀어 댔던지, 줄곧 그를 똑바로 바라보며 앉아 있던 고모님이 그만 인내심을 죄다 잃고 말았다."]], [["‘Deuce take the man!’ said my aunt, sternly, ‘what’s he about?", "\"이 인간을 어쩌면 좋아!\" 고모님이 매섭게 말했다. \"도대체 뭐 하는 거야?"], ["Don’t be galvanic, sir!’", "전기에 감전된 사람처럼 굴지 말아요, 선생!\""]], [["‘I ask your pardon, Miss Trotwood,’ returned Uriah; ‘I’m aware you’re nervous.’", "\"용서를 빕니다요, 트롯우드 양.\" 유라이어가 받았다. \"당신께서 신경이 예민하시다는 걸 잘 압니다요.\""]], [["‘Go along with you, sir!’ said my aunt, anything but appeased.", "\"썩 비켜요, 선생!\" 고모님이 도무지 가라앉지 않은 채 말했다."], ["‘Don’t presume to say so!", "\"감히 그런 소리 마요!"], ["I am nothing of the sort.", "나는 그런 사람이 전혀 아니에요."], ["If you’re an eel, sir, conduct yourself like one. If you’re a man, control your limbs, sir!", "당신이 뱀장어라면, 선생, 뱀장어답게 굴어요. 당신이 사람이라면, 그 사지를 좀 가만히 두라고요, 선생! 맙소사!\""], ["Good God!’ said my aunt, with great indignation, ‘I am not going to be serpentined and corkscrewed out of my senses!’", "고모님이 크게 분개하며 말했다. \"나는 뱀처럼 꿈틀대고 코르크 마개뽑이처럼 비틀어 대는 통에 제정신을 빼앗기진 않을 거예요!\""]], [["Mr. Heep was rather abashed, as most people might have been, by this explosion; which derived great additional force from the indignant manner in which my aunt afterwards moved in her chair, and shook her head as if she were making snaps or bounces at him.", "힙 씨는 이 폭발에 자못 무안해했는데, 누구라도 그랬을 법했다. 그 뒤에 고모님이 분개한 태도로 의자에서 몸을 들썩이며, 마치 그를 향해 콱 물어뜯거나 덮치려는 듯 고개를 흔들어 댄 것이 그 폭발에 큰 힘을 더했다."], ["But he said to me aside in a meek voice:", "그러나 그는 나에게만 들리게 온순한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I am well aware, Master Copperfield, that Miss Trotwood, though an excellent lady, has a quick temper (indeed I think I had the pleasure of knowing her, when I was a numble clerk, before you did, Master Copperfield), and it’s only natural, I am sure, that it should be made quicker by present circumstances.", "\"저는 잘 압니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 트롯우드 양께서는 훌륭한 분이시지만 성미가 급하시다는 걸요(사실 제가 미천한 사무원이던 시절에, 당신보다 먼저 저분을 알게 되는 기쁨을 누렸던 것 같습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님). 그리고 지금 같은 형편 때문에 그 성미가 더 급해지신 것도 지극히 당연한 일이지요, 분명히요."], ["The wonder is, that it isn’t much worse!", "오히려 훨씬 더 심해지지 않은 게 놀라울 따름이에요!"], ["I only called to say that if there was anything we could do, in present circumstances, mother or self, or Wickfield and Heep,--we should be really glad.", "저는 그저, 지금 같은 형편에서 저희가 할 수 있는 일이 무엇이든 있다면, 어머니든 저든, 혹은 위크필드 앤드 힙이든—정말로 기쁘게 하겠다는 말씀을 드리러 들렀을 뿐입니다요."], ["I may go so far?’ said Uriah, with a sickly smile at his partner.", "이렇게까지 말씀드려도 될까요?\" 유라이어가 자기 동업자를 향해 병약한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘Uriah Heep,’ said Mr.", "\"유라이어 힙은,\""], ["Wickfield, in a monotonous forced way, ‘is active in the business, Trotwood.", "위크필드 씨가 단조롭고 억지스러운 투로 말했다. \"사업에 적극적이라네, 트롯우드."], ["What he says, I quite concur in.", "그가 하는 말에 나는 전적으로 동의해."], ["You know I had an old interest in you.", "자네도 알다시피 나는 자네에게 오랜 애정을 품어 왔지."], ["Apart from that, what Uriah says I quite concur in!’", "그건 그렇다 치고, 유라이어가 하는 말에 나는 전적으로 동의하네!\""]], [["‘Oh, what a reward it is,’ said Uriah, drawing up one leg, at the risk of bringing down upon himself another visitation from my aunt, ‘to be so trusted in!", "\"오, 이 얼마나 큰 보답인지요,\" 고모님에게서 또 한 번 호된 일격을 자초할 위험을 무릅쓰고 한쪽 다리를 끌어올리며 유라이어가 말했다. \"이토록 신임을 받다니요!"], ["But I hope I am able to do something to relieve him from the fatigues of business, Master Copperfield!’", "하지만 저는 그분을 사업의 고단함에서 덜어 드리는 데 무언가 보탬이 될 수 있기를 바랍니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["‘Uriah Heep is a great relief to me,’ said Mr. Wickfield, in the same dull voice.", "\"유라이어 힙은 나에게 큰 위안일세.\" 위크필드 씨가 여전히 맥없는 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘It’s a load off my mind, Trotwood, to have such a partner.’", "\"그런 동업자를 둔 것이, 트롯우드, 내 마음의 짐을 덜어 준다네.\""]], [["The red fox made him say all this, I knew, to exhibit him to me in the light he had indicated on the night when he poisoned my rest.", "그 붉은 여우가 그로 하여금 이 모든 말을 하게 시킨 것임을, 나는 알았다. 그가 내 안식을 망쳐 놓던 그날 밤 넌지시 비쳤던 그 모습대로 위크필드 씨를 나에게 보여 주려는 것이었다."], ["I saw the same ill-favoured smile upon his face again, and saw how he watched me.", "나는 그의 얼굴에 다시 그 흉측한 미소가 떠오르는 것을 보았고, 그가 나를 살피는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘You are not going, papa?’ said Agnes, anxiously.", "\"가시려는 거 아니죠, 아버지?\" 아그네스가 근심스레 말했다."], ["‘Will you not walk back with Trotwood and me?’", "\"트롯우드와 저와 함께 걸어서 돌아가시지 않을래요?\""]], [["He would have looked to Uriah, I believe, before replying, if that worthy had not anticipated him.", "내 생각에 그는 대답하기 전에 유라이어를 쳐다보았을 것이다. 만약 그 잘난 자가 그를 앞지르지 않았더라면."]], [["‘I am bespoke myself,’ said Uriah, ‘on business; otherwise I should have been appy to have kept with my friends.", "\"저는 볼일이 있어 미리 약속이 되어 있습니다요.\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"그게 아니라면 친구분들과 함께하게 되어 기뻤을 텐데요."], ["But I leave my partner to represent the firm.", "하지만 회사를 대표하는 일은 제 동업자에게 맡기겠습니다요."], ["Miss Agnes, ever yours!", "아그네스 양, 언제나 당신의 종입니다요!"], ["I wish you good-day, Master Copperfield, and leave my umble respects for Miss Betsey Trotwood.’", "안녕히 계십시오, 코퍼필드 도련님. 베시 트롯우드 양께 제 미천한 경의를 남기고 갑니다요.\""]], [["With those words, he retired, kissing his great hand, and leering at us like a mask.", "그 말과 함께 그는 자기 커다란 손에 입을 맞추고, 가면처럼 우리를 곁눈질로 흘겨보며 물러갔다."]], [["We sat there, talking about our pleasant old Canterbury days, an hour or two. Mr.", "우리는 거기 앉아, 정겨운 옛 캔터베리 시절을 이야기하며 한두 시간을 보냈다."], ["Wickfield, left to Agnes, soon became more like his former self; though there was a settled depression upon him, which he never shook off.", "아그네스에게 맡겨진 위크필드 씨는 곧 예전의 자기 모습에 한결 가까워졌다. 비록 끝내 떨쳐 내지 못하는 깊이 가라앉은 우울이 그에게 깔려 있긴 했지만."], ["For all that, he brightened; and had an evident pleasure in hearing us recall the little incidents of our old life, many of which he remembered very well.", "그래도 그는 환해졌고, 우리가 옛 시절의 자잘한 일들을 떠올리는 것을 듣는 데 분명한 기쁨을 느꼈는데, 그 가운데 많은 것을 그는 아주 또렷이 기억하고 있었다."], ["He said it was like those times, to be alone with Agnes and me again; and he wished to Heaven they had never changed.", "그는 아그네스와 나와 다시 단둘이—아니, 셋이서—함께 있으니 마치 그 시절 같다고 했고, 그 시절이 차라리 변하지 않았더라면 하고 하늘에 빌었다."], ["I am sure there was an influence in the placid face of Agnes, and in the very touch of her hand upon his arm, that did wonders for him.", "아그네스의 평온한 얼굴에, 그리고 그의 팔에 닿는 그녀의 손길 그 자체에 어떤 힘이 깃들어 있어, 그것이 그에게 놀라운 일을 해냈음을 나는 분명히 안다."]], [["My aunt (who was busy nearly all this while with Peggotty, in the inner room) would not accompany us to the place where they were staying, but insisted on my going; and I went.", "고모님은(그동안 거의 내내 안쪽 방에서 패거티와 함께 부산했다) 그들이 묵는 곳까지 우리와 함께 가려 하지 않았지만, 나에게는 꼭 가라고 우겼다. 그래서 나는 갔다."], ["We dined together.", "우리는 함께 저녁을 먹었다."], ["After dinner, Agnes sat beside him, as of old, and poured out his wine.", "저녁 식사 후, 아그네스는 옛날처럼 그의 곁에 앉아 그의 잔에 포도주를 따라 주었다."], ["He took what she gave him, and no more--like a child--and we all three sat together at a window as the evening gathered in.", "그는 그녀가 주는 만큼만 받았고 그 이상은 받지 않았다—마치 어린아이처럼. 그리고 우리 셋은 저녁이 깃들어 가는 동안 창가에 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["When it was almost dark, he lay down on a sofa, Agnes pillowing his head and bending over him a little while; and when she came back to the window, it was not so dark but I could see tears glittering in her eyes.", "거의 어두워졌을 때, 그는 소파에 누웠고, 아그네스는 그의 머리를 받쳐 주고 잠시 그 위로 몸을 숙였다. 그녀가 창가로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀의 눈에 어린 눈물이 반짝이는 것을 내가 알아볼 수 있을 만큼은 아직 어둡지 않았다."]], [["I pray Heaven that I never may forget the dear girl in her love and truth, at that time of my life; for if I should, I must be drawing near the end, and then I would desire to remember her best!", "내 인생의 그 시절에, 사랑과 진실 속에 있던 그 사랑스러운 소녀를 결코 잊지 않게 해 달라고 나는 하늘에 빈다. 만약 잊는다면, 그것은 내가 종말에 가까워졌다는 뜻일 테고, 그렇다면 나는 그녀를 가장 또렷이 기억하기를 바랄 것이다!"], ["She filled my heart with such good resolutions, strengthened my weakness so, by her example, so directed--I know not how, she was too modest and gentle to advise me in many words--the wandering ardour and unsettled purpose within me, that all the little good I have done, and all the harm I have forborne, I solemnly believe I may refer to her.", "그녀는 그토록 선한 결심들로 내 마음을 채워 주었고, 자신의 본보기로 내 나약함을 그토록 굳세게 해 주었으며, 내 안에서 갈팡질팡하던 열정과 정처 없던 목적을 그토록—어떻게 그랬는지는 모르겠다. 그녀는 너무나 겸손하고 온화해서 여러 말로 충고하는 법이 없었다—이끌어 주었기에, 내가 행한 그 자그마한 선과, 내가 삼간 그 모든 해악을 나는 엄숙히 그녀에게 돌릴 수 있다고 믿는다."]], [["And how she spoke to me of Dora, sitting at the window in the dark; listened to my praises of her; praised again; and round the little fairy-figure shed some glimpses of her own pure light, that made it yet more precious and more innocent to me!", "그리고 어두운 가운데 창가에 앉아, 그녀가 도라에 대해 나에게 어떻게 이야기해 주었던가. 도라를 향한 내 찬사에 귀 기울여 주고, 다시 그녀를 칭찬해 주며, 그 작은 요정 같은 모습을 둘러 자기 순결한 빛을 조금씩 비추어 주어, 그것을 나에게 한층 더 소중하고 한층 더 천진한 것으로 만들어 주었던가!"], ["Oh, Agnes, sister of my boyhood, if I had known then, what I knew long afterwards--!", "오, 아그네스, 내 소년 시절의 누이여, 만약 내가 그때, 훗날에야 알게 된 것을 알았더라면—!"]], [["There was a beggar in the street, when I went down; and as I turned my head towards the window, thinking of her calm seraphic eyes, he made me start by muttering, as if he were an echo of the morning: ‘Blind!", "내가 아래로 내려갈 때 거리에 거지 하나가 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 고요하고 천사 같은 두 눈을 떠올리며 창문 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸을 때, 그가 마치 아침의 메아리이기라도 한 듯 중얼거리는 바람에 나는 흠칫 놀랐다. \"눈멀었어! 눈멀었어!"], ["Blind! Blind!’", "눈멀었어!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 36. ENTHUSIASM", "# CHAPTER 36. ENTHUSIASM"]], [["I began the next day with another dive into the Roman bath, and then started for Highgate.", "이튿날 나는 또 한 번 로마식 욕조에 풍덩 뛰어드는 것으로 하루를 시작하고, 하이게이트로 출발했다."], ["I was not dispirited now.", "이제는 풀이 죽어 있지 않았다."], ["I was not afraid of the shabby coat, and had no yearnings after gallant greys.", "허름한 외투도 두렵지 않았고, 늠름한 잿빛 말을 향한 동경 따위도 없었다."], ["My whole manner of thinking of our late misfortune was changed.", "우리가 최근에 겪은 불운을 생각하는 내 마음가짐 전체가 달라져 있었다."], ["What I had to do, was, to show my aunt that her past goodness to me had not been thrown away on an insensible, ungrateful object.", "내가 해야 할 일은, 고모님이 지금껏 나에게 베푼 은혜가 무정하고 배은망덕한 자에게 헛되이 쏟아부어진 게 아니라는 사실을 보여 드리는 것이었다."], ["What I had to do, was, to turn the painful discipline of my younger days to account, by going to work with a resolute and steady heart.", "내가 해야 할 일은, 굳세고 흔들림 없는 마음으로 일에 뛰어들어, 어린 시절의 그 고통스러운 단련을 밑천으로 삼는 것이었다."], ["What I had to do, was, to take my woodman’s axe in my hand, and clear my own way through the forest of difficulty, by cutting down the trees until I came to Dora. And I went on at a mighty rate, as if it could be done by walking.", "내가 해야 할 일은, 나무꾼의 도끼를 손에 쥐고, 도라에게 이를 때까지 나무를 베어 넘기며 곤경의 숲을 헤치고 나만의 길을 트는 것이었다. 그리고 나는 걷는 것만으로도 그 일이 이루어지기라도 할 듯이, 무서운 속도로 내달았다."]], [["When I found myself on the familiar Highgate road, pursuing such a different errand from that old one of pleasure, with which it was associated, it seemed as if a complete change had come on my whole life. But that did not discourage me.", "낯익은 하이게이트 길에 들어서서, 예전 이 길과 얽혀 있던 그 즐거운 나들이와는 전혀 다른 볼일을 좇아 걸으니, 내 삶 전체에 완전한 변화가 들이닥친 것만 같았다. 그러나 그것이 나를 주눅 들게 하지는 못했다."], ["With the new life, came new purpose, new intention.", "새로운 삶과 함께 새로운 목적, 새로운 다짐이 찾아왔다."], ["Great was the labour; priceless the reward.", "수고는 컸고, 보상은 값을 매길 수 없었다."], ["Dora was the reward, and Dora must be won.", "도라가 그 보상이었고, 도라는 반드시 쟁취해야 했다."]], [["I got into such a transport, that I felt quite sorry my coat was not a little shabby already.", "나는 어찌나 황홀경에 빠져들었던지, 내 외투가 아직 조금이라도 더 허름하지 않은 것이 못내 아쉬울 지경이었다."], ["I wanted to be cutting at those trees in the forest of difficulty, under circumstances that should prove my strength.", "나는 내 힘을 입증해 줄 그런 상황에서 곤경의 숲에 선 저 나무들을 베어 넘기고 싶었다."], ["I had a good mind to ask an old man, in wire spectacles, who was breaking stones upon the road, to lend me his hammer for a little while, and let me begin to beat a path to Dora out of granite.", "길에서 돌을 깨고 있던, 철테 안경을 쓴 노인에게 잠깐만 그 망치를 빌려 달라고, 화강암을 깨뜨려 도라에게 이르는 길을 닦기 시작하게 해 달라고 부탁하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다."], ["I stimulated myself into such a heat, and got so out of breath, that I felt as if I had been earning I don’t know how much.", "나는 스스로를 어찌나 후끈 달아오르게 자극했던지, 그리고 숨을 어찌나 헐떡였던지, 마치 얼마인지도 모를 큰돈을 벌어들이기라도 한 것 같은 기분이 들었다."]], [["In this state, I went into a cottage that I saw was to let, and examined it narrowly,--for I felt it necessary to be practical.", "이런 상태로, 나는 세놓는다는 표시가 붙은 어느 오두막에 들어가, 그곳을 꼼꼼히 살펴보았다 — 실속을 차려야 한다는 생각이 들었기 때문이다."], ["It would do for me and Dora admirably: with a little front garden for Jip to run about in, and bark at the tradespeople through the railings, and a capital room upstairs for my aunt.", "나와 도라가 살기에 더할 나위 없이 좋을 듯했다. 집 짚이 울타리 사이로 장사꾼들을 향해 짖어 대며 뛰어다닐 작은 앞뜰도 있고, 위층에는 고모님이 쓰실 멋진 방도 있었다."], ["I came out again, hotter and faster than ever, and dashed up to Highgate, at such a rate that I was there an hour too early; and, though I had not been, should have been obliged to stroll about to cool myself, before I was at all presentable.", "나는 다시 밖으로 나와, 그 어느 때보다 더 후끈 달아올라 더 빠른 걸음으로, 하이게이트를 향해 어찌나 내달렸던지 약속 시간보다 한 시간이나 일찍 그곳에 닿았다. 설령 그렇게 일찍 닿지 않았더라도, 그나마 봐줄 만한 꼴이 되려면 몸을 식히려고 한동안 어슬렁거려야 했을 것이다."]], [["My first care, after putting myself under this necessary course of preparation, was to find the Doctor’s house.", "이렇게 꼭 필요한 준비 절차를 거친 뒤, 내가 제일 먼저 마음 쓴 일은 박사님의 집을 찾는 것이었다."], ["It was not in that part of Highgate where Mrs. Steerforth lived, but quite on the opposite side of the little town.", "그 집은 스티어포스 부인이 사는 하이게이트의 그 동네가 아니라, 작은 마을 정반대편에 있었다."], ["When I had made this discovery, I went back, in an attraction I could not resist, to a lane by Mrs. Steerforth’s, and looked over the corner of the garden wall.", "그 사실을 알아내고 나서, 나는 도저히 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 끌림에 이끌려, 스티어포스 부인의 집 옆 골목으로 되돌아가, 정원 담장 모서리 너머를 들여다보았다."], ["His room was shut up close.", "그의 방은 굳게 닫혀 있었다."], ["The conservatory doors were standing open, and Rosa Dartle was walking, bareheaded, with a quick, impetuous step, up and down a gravel walk on one side of the lawn.", "온실 문은 활짝 열려 있었고, 로자 다틀이 모자도 쓰지 않은 채, 빠르고 격한 걸음으로 잔디밭 한쪽 자갈길을 오락가락 거닐고 있었다."], ["She gave me the idea of some fierce thing, that was dragging the length of its chain to and fro upon a beaten track, and wearing its heart out.", "그녀는 마치 다져진 길 위에서 제 사슬의 길이만큼 이리저리 끌려다니며 제 가슴을 갉아 닳리는 어떤 사나운 짐승 같다는 느낌을 나에게 주었다."]], [["I came softly away from my place of observation, and avoiding that part of the neighbourhood, and wishing I had not gone near it, strolled about until it was ten o’clock.", "나는 살그머니 망보던 자리에서 물러나, 그 동네를 피해 가며, 괜히 그 근처에 갔다 싶어 후회하면서, 열 시가 될 때까지 어슬렁거렸다."], ["The church with the slender spire, that stands on the top of the hill now, was not there then to tell me the time.", "지금은 언덕 꼭대기에 서서 시각을 알려 주는 가느다란 첨탑의 교회가, 그때는 거기 없었다."], ["An old red-brick mansion, used as a school, was in its place; and a fine old house it must have been to go to school at, as I recollect it.", "그 자리에는 학교로 쓰이던 낡은 붉은 벽돌 저택이 있었는데, 내 기억으로는 학교로 다니기에 멋진 옛 집이었음이 틀림없다."]], [["When I approached the Doctor’s cottage--a pretty old place, on which he seemed to have expended some money, if I might judge from the embellishments and repairs that had the look of being just completed--I saw him walking in the garden at the side, gaiters and all, as if he had never left off walking since the days of my pupilage.", "박사님의 오두막에 다가갔을 때 — 막 끝난 듯한 장식과 손질로 미루어 보건대 그가 적잖이 돈을 들인 듯한, 예쁘고 오래된 곳이었다 — 나는 그가 정원 한쪽에서 각반까지 차고 거니는 모습을 보았는데, 마치 내가 학생이던 시절부터 단 한 번도 거니는 일을 멈춘 적이 없는 것만 같았다."], ["He had his old companions about him, too; for there were plenty of high trees in the neighbourhood, and two or three rooks were on the grass, looking after him, as if they had been written to about him by the Canterbury rooks, and were observing him closely in consequence.", "그에게는 옛 동무들도 곁에 있었다. 근방에 키 큰 나무가 잔뜩 있었던 덕에, 까마귀 두세 마리가 잔디밭에서 그를 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 마치 캔터베리의 까마귀들이 그에 대해 편지로 일러바치기라도 한 듯, 그 결과 그를 유심히 살피고 있는 것 같았다."]], [["Knowing the utter hopelessness of attracting his attention from that distance, I made bold to open the gate, and walk after him, so as to meet him when he should turn round.", "그 거리에서 그의 주의를 끌기란 도무지 가망 없는 일임을 알기에, 나는 용기를 내어 문을 열고 그의 뒤를 따라 걸어가, 그가 몸을 돌릴 때 마주치도록 했다."], ["When he did, and came towards me, he looked at me thoughtfully for a few moments, evidently without thinking about me at all; and then his benevolent face expressed extraordinary pleasure, and he took me by both hands.", "마침내 그가 돌아서서 나에게 다가왔을 때, 그는 몇 순간 나를 골똘히 바라보았으나, 분명 나에 대해서는 아무 생각도 하지 않는 눈치였다. 그러더니 그 인자한 얼굴에 더없는 기쁨이 떠오르며, 그가 두 손으로 내 손을 잡았다."]], [["‘Why, my dear Copperfield,’ said the Doctor, ‘you are a man!", "\"아니, 이런, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\" 박사님이 말했다. \"어엿한 어른이 다 되었구나!"], ["How do you do?", "잘 지냈는가?"], ["I am delighted to see you.", "자네를 보니 정말 기쁘네."], ["My dear Copperfield, how very much you have improved!", "내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 어쩌면 이렇게 훤칠해졌나!"], ["You are quite--yes--dear me!’", "자넨 아주 — 그래 — 어이구, 이런!\""]], [["I hoped he was well, and Mrs. Strong too.", "나는 그가 건강하기를, 그리고 스트롱 부인도 건강하기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["‘Oh dear, yes!’ said the Doctor; ‘Annie’s quite well, and she’ll be delighted to see you.", "\"오, 그럼, 그럼!\" 박사님이 말했다. \"애니도 아주 잘 지내고, 자네를 보면 무척 기뻐할 걸세."], ["You were always her favourite. She said so, last night, when I showed her your letter.", "자넨 늘 그 사람이 제일 아끼던 학생이었지. 어젯밤에 자네 편지를 보여 줬더니 그렇게 말하더군."], ["And--yes, to be sure--you recollect Mr. Jack Maldon, Copperfield?’", "그리고 — 그래, 그렇지 — 자네 잭 몰던 군을 기억하나, 코퍼필드?\""]], [["‘Perfectly, sir.’", "\"완벽하게 기억합니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Of course,’ said the Doctor.", "\"그렇겠지,\" 박사님이 말했다."], ["‘To be sure.", "\"암, 그렇고말고."], ["He’s pretty well, too.’", "그 친구도 잘 지내네.\""]], [["‘Has he come home, sir?’ I inquired.", "\"고국으로 돌아왔나요, 선생님?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘From India?’ said the Doctor. ‘Yes. Mr.", "\"인도에서 말인가?\" 박사님이 말했다. \"그래."], ["Jack Maldon couldn’t bear the climate, my dear. Mrs.", "잭 몰던 군이 그곳 기후를 견디지 못했다네."], ["Markleham--you have not forgotten Mrs. Markleham?’", "마클햄 부인 말이야 — 마클햄 부인을 잊진 않았겠지?\""]], [["Forgotten the Old Soldier!", "그 노병을 잊다니!"], ["And in that short time!", "그 짧은 사이에!"]], [["‘Mrs. Markleham,’ said the Doctor, ‘was quite vexed about him, poor thing; so we have got him at home again; and we have bought him a little Patent place, which agrees with him much better.’ I knew enough of Mr.", "\"마클햄 부인이,\" 박사님이 말했다."], ["Jack Maldon to suspect from this account that it was a place where there was not much to do, and which was pretty well paid.", "\"그 친구 일로 어찌나 속을 끓이던지, 가엾게도. 그래서 우리가 그 친구를 다시 고국으로 불러들였고, 그에게 작은 특허 자리를 하나 마련해 주었다네. 그 친구한테 한결 잘 맞는 자리지.\""], ["The Doctor, walking up and down with his hand on my shoulder, and his kind face turned encouragingly to mine, went on:", "나는 잭 몰던 군을 익히 아는 터라, 이 이야기로 미루어 그것이 할 일은 별로 없으면서 보수는 꽤 두둑한 자리이리라 짐작했다. 박사님은 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 오락가락 거닐면서, 그 다정한 얼굴을 격려하듯 내 쪽으로 돌린 채, 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Now, my dear Copperfield, in reference to this proposal of yours.", "\"자, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 자네의 이 제안 말인데."], ["It’s very gratifying and agreeable to me, I am sure; but don’t you think you could do better?", "나로서야 정말 흐뭇하고 반가운 일이지, 그럼. 하지만 자네, 더 나은 길을 찾을 수 있다고 생각하지 않나?"], ["You achieved distinction, you know, when you were with us.", "자넨 우리와 함께 있을 때 두각을 나타냈잖나."], ["You are qualified for many good things.", "자넨 여러 좋은 일을 할 자질이 있어."], ["You have laid a foundation that any edifice may be raised upon; and is it not a pity that you should devote the spring-time of your life to such a poor pursuit as I can offer?’", "어떤 건물이든 올릴 수 있는 토대를 닦아 두지 않았나. 그런 자네가 인생의 봄날을, 내가 줄 수 있는 이런 변변찮은 일에 바친다는 게 안타깝지 않은가?\""]], [["I became very glowing again, and, expressing myself in a rhapsodical style, I am afraid, urged my request strongly; reminding the Doctor that I had already a profession.", "나는 다시 후끈 달아올라, 두렵게도 황홀한 어조로 내 뜻을 펼치며 내 청을 힘주어 밀어붙였다. 그러면서 나에게는 이미 직업이 있음을 박사님에게 일깨워 드렸다."]], [["‘Well, well,’ said the Doctor, ‘that’s true.", "\"음, 그래, 그래,\" 박사님이 말했다."], ["Certainly, your having a profession, and being actually engaged in studying it, makes a difference.", "\"그건 사실이지. 분명, 자네에게 직업이 있고, 또 실제로 그걸 익히고 있는 중이라면, 사정이 다르지."], ["But, my good young friend, what’s seventy pounds a year?’", "하지만, 내 착한 젊은 친구여, 일 년에 칠십 파운드가 대체 얼마나 된다고?\""]], [["‘It doubles our income, Doctor Strong,’ said I.", "\"우리 수입이 갑절로 늘어납니다, 스트롱 박사님.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ replied the Doctor.", "\"이런, 세상에!\" 박사님이 대답했다."], ["‘To think of that!", "\"그걸 생각하면!"], ["Not that I mean to say it’s rigidly limited to seventy pounds a-year, because I have always contemplated making any young friend I might thus employ, a present too.", "그렇다고 일 년에 딱 칠십 파운드로 빠듯하게 묶어 두자는 뜻은 아니야. 나는 이렇게 데려다 쓰는 젊은 친구에게는 으레 따로 선물도 챙겨 줄 작정이었거든."], ["Undoubtedly,’ said the Doctor, still walking me up and down with his hand on my shoulder.", "틀림없이,\" 박사님은 여전히 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 나를 오락가락 거닐게 하며 말했다."], ["‘I have always taken an annual present into account.’", "\"나는 늘 해마다 줄 선물까지 셈에 넣고 있었네.\""]], [["‘My dear tutor,’ said I (now, really, without any nonsense), ‘to whom I owe more obligations already than I ever can acknowledge--’", "\"제 사랑하는 은사님,\" 내가 말했다 (이제 정말이지 아무 허튼소리도 없이). \"저는 이미 갚을 길 없는 은혜를 선생님께 너무도 많이 졌습니다만—\""]], [["‘No, no,’ interposed the Doctor.", "\"아니, 아니야,\" 박사님이 가로막았다."], ["‘Pardon me!’", "\"그런 말 말게!\""]], [["‘If you will take such time as I have, and that is my mornings and evenings, and can think it worth seventy pounds a year, you will do me such a service as I cannot express.’", "\"제가 낼 수 있는 시간, 그러니까 아침나절과 저녁나절을 받아 주시고, 그것이 일 년에 칠십 파운드의 값어치가 있다고 여겨 주신다면, 저로서는 이루 말할 수 없는 은혜를 베풀어 주시는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Dear me!’ said the Doctor, innocently.", "\"이런, 세상에!\" 박사님이 천진하게 말했다."], ["‘To think that so little should go for so much!", "\"그렇게 적은 걸 그렇게 큰 값에 친다니!"], ["Dear, dear!", "이런, 이런!"], ["And when you can do better, you will?", "그래서 더 나은 길이 생기면, 그리로 가겠나?"], ["On your word, now?’ said the Doctor,--which he had always made a very grave appeal to the honour of us boys.", "자, 약속하지?\" 박사님이 말했다 — 이 말은 그가 늘 우리 소년들의 명예에 자못 엄숙하게 호소하던 방식이었다."]], [["‘On my word, sir!’ I returned, answering in our old school manner.", "\"약속합니다, 선생님!\" 나는 옛 학창 시절의 어투로 대답했다."]], [["‘Then be it so,’ said the Doctor, clapping me on the shoulder, and still keeping his hand there, as we still walked up and down.", "\"그렇다면 그렇게 하세.\" 박사님이 내 어깨를 툭 치며 말했는데, 우리가 여전히 오락가락 거니는 동안에도 그 손은 그대로 거기 얹혀 있었다."]], [["‘And I shall be twenty times happier, sir,’ said I, with a little--I hope innocent--flattery, ‘if my employment is to be on the Dictionary.’", "\"그리고 저는 스무 배는 더 기쁠 겁니다, 선생님,\" 내가 조금 — 부디 악의 없는 — 아첨을 섞어 말했다. \"제 일이 그 사전 작업이라면요.\""]], [["The Doctor stopped, smilingly clapped me on the shoulder again, and exclaimed, with a triumph most delightful to behold, as if I had penetrated to the profoundest depths of mortal sagacity, ‘My dear young friend, you have hit it.", "박사님은 걸음을 멈추더니, 빙긋 웃으며 다시 한번 내 어깨를 툭 치고는, 마치 내가 인간 예지의 가장 깊은 심연까지 꿰뚫어 본 것처럼, 보기에 더없이 흐뭇한 의기양양함으로 외쳤다. \"내 사랑하는 젊은 친구여, 자네가 정곡을 찔렀네."], ["It IS the Dictionary!’", "바로 그 사전일세!\""]], [["How could it be anything else!", "그 밖에 무엇일 수 있었겠는가!"], ["His pockets were as full of it as his head. It was sticking out of him in all directions.", "그의 주머니는 머릿속만큼이나 사전으로 가득 차 있었다. 사방으로 사전이 삐져나와 있었다."], ["He told me that since his retirement from scholastic life, he had been advancing with it wonderfully; and that nothing could suit him better than the proposed arrangements for morning and evening work, as it was his custom to walk about in the daytime with his considering cap on.", "그가 나에게 말하기를, 학교 일에서 물러난 뒤로 사전 작업이 놀랍도록 진척되었으며, 낮에는 으레 생각하는 모자를 쓰고 거니는 게 버릇인 만큼, 아침저녁으로 일하자는 이 제안만큼 그에게 안성맞춤인 것도 없다고 했다."], ["His papers were in a little confusion, in consequence of Mr. Jack Maldon having lately proffered his occasional services as an amanuensis, and not being accustomed to that occupation; but we should soon put right what was amiss, and go on swimmingly.", "잭 몰던 군이 최근 들어 이따금 필경사 노릇을 자청해 주었으나 그 일에 익숙하지 못한 탓에, 그의 원고가 다소 어수선해져 있었다. 하지만 어긋난 부분은 곧 바로잡아, 일이 술술 풀릴 것이라고 했다."], ["Afterwards, when we were fairly at our work, I found Mr. Jack Maldon’s efforts more troublesome to me than I had expected, as he had not confined himself to making numerous mistakes, but had sketched so many soldiers, and ladies’ heads, over the Doctor’s manuscript, that I often became involved in labyrinths of obscurity.", "나중에, 우리가 본격적으로 일에 들어갔을 때, 나는 잭 몰던 군이 해 놓은 일이 예상보다 훨씬 더 골칫거리라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 숱한 실수를 저질렀을 뿐 아니라, 박사님의 원고 위에 군인이며 여인의 머리통을 어찌나 많이 그려 놓았던지, 나는 종종 알 수 없는 미궁 속에 빠져들곤 했던 것이다."]], [["The Doctor was quite happy in the prospect of our going to work together on that wonderful performance, and we settled to begin next morning at seven o’clock.", "박사님은 그 놀라운 대작을 함께 만들어 나가게 되었다는 기대에 무척이나 행복해했고, 우리는 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 시작하기로 정했다."], ["We were to work two hours every morning, and two or three hours every night, except on Saturdays, when I was to rest.", "우리는 매일 아침 두 시간, 매일 밤 두세 시간씩 일하기로 했고, 다만 토요일에는 내가 쉬기로 했다."], ["On Sundays, of course, I was to rest also, and I considered these very easy terms.", "일요일에는 물론 나도 쉴 참이었으니, 나는 이 조건이 무척 수월하다고 여겼다."]], [["Our plans being thus arranged to our mutual satisfaction, the Doctor took me into the house to present me to Mrs.", "이렇게 우리 계획이 서로 흡족하게 짜이고 나자, 박사님은 나를 집 안으로 데려가 스트롱 부인에게 인사시켜 주려 했다."], ["Strong, whom we found in the Doctor’s new study, dusting his books,--a freedom which he never permitted anybody else to take with those sacred favourites.", "우리는 부인이 박사님의 새 서재에서 그의 책에 먼지를 떨고 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그것은 그가 그 거룩한 애장서들에 대해 다른 누구에게도 결코 허락하지 않던 자유였다."]], [["They had postponed their breakfast on my account, and we sat down to table together.", "그들은 나 때문에 아침 식사를 미뤄 두었던 터라, 우리는 함께 식탁에 둘러앉았다."], ["We had not been seated long, when I saw an approaching arrival in Mrs. Strong’s face, before I heard any sound of it.", "자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 나는 무슨 기척이 들려오기도 전에, 스트롱 부인의 얼굴에서 누군가 다가오고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["A gentleman on horseback came to the gate, and leading his horse into the little court, with the bridle over his arm, as if he were quite at home, tied him to a ring in the empty coach-house wall, and came into the breakfast parlour, whip in hand. It was Mr.", "말을 탄 신사 하나가 문 앞에 이르더니, 마치 제집인 양 말고삐를 팔에 건 채 말을 끌고 작은 마당으로 들어와, 텅 빈 마차 보관소 벽의 고리에 말을 매고는, 채찍을 손에 들고 아침 식사를 하는 응접실로 들어왔다. 잭 몰던 군이었다."], ["Jack Maldon; and Mr. Jack Maldon was not at all improved by India, I thought.", "그런데 내 생각에 잭 몰던 군은 인도에 다녀와서도 조금도 나아진 데가 없었다."], ["I was in a state of ferocious virtue, however, as to young men who were not cutting down trees in the forest of difficulty; and my impression must be received with due allowance.", "다만 곤경의 숲에서 나무를 베어 넘기지 않는 젊은이들에 대해서라면 나는 무자비할 만큼 의로움에 들떠 있던 터라, 내 인상은 그만한 에누리를 두고 받아들여야 할 것이다."]], [["‘Mr. Jack!’ said the Doctor.", "\"잭 군!\" 박사님이 말했다."], ["‘Copperfield!’", "\"코퍼필드 군일세!\""]], [["Mr. Jack Maldon shook hands with me; but not very warmly, I believed; and with an air of languid patronage, at which I secretly took great umbrage.", "잭 몰던 군은 나와 악수했으나, 내가 보기에 그리 따뜻하지는 않았다. 게다가 나른하게 생색내는 태도라, 나는 속으로 몹시 못마땅했다."], ["But his languor altogether was quite a wonderful sight; except when he addressed himself to his cousin Annie.", "그런데 그 나른함이라는 게 통틀어 정말 볼 만한 구경거리였다 — 사촌 누이 애니에게 말을 걸 때만 빼고."], ["‘Have you breakfasted this morning, Mr.", "\"오늘 아침은 드셨나, 잭 군?\""], ["Jack?’ said the Doctor.", "박사님이 말했다."]], [["‘I hardly ever take breakfast, sir,’ he replied, with his head thrown back in an easy-chair.", "\"저는 아침은 거의 안 먹습니다, 선생님.\" 그가 안락의자에 머리를 뒤로 젖힌 채 대답했다."], ["‘I find it bores me.’", "\"먹으면 따분하더군요.\""]], [["‘Is there any news today?’ inquired the Doctor.", "\"오늘 무슨 소식이라도 있나?\" 박사님이 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing at all, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"전혀 없습니다, 선생님.\""], ["Maldon. ‘There’s an account about the people being hungry and discontented down in the North, but they are always being hungry and discontented somewhere.’", "몰던 군이 대답했다. \"북부 지방에서 사람들이 굶주리고 불만에 차 있다는 기사가 있긴 합니다만, 그 사람들이야 늘 어디선가 굶주리고 불만에 차 있는걸요.\""]], [["The Doctor looked grave, and said, as though he wished to change the subject, ‘Then there’s no news at all; and no news, they say, is good news.’", "박사님은 자못 심각한 얼굴이 되더니, 화제를 돌리고 싶다는 듯 말했다. \"그렇다면 소식이 전혀 없는 셈이군. 그리고 무소식이 희소식이라고들 하지.\""]], [["‘There’s a long statement in the papers, sir, about a murder,’ observed Mr. Maldon.", "\"신문에 살인 사건에 대한 긴 기사가 실렸습니다, 선생님,\" 몰던 군이 한마디 했다."], ["‘But somebody is always being murdered, and I didn’t read it.’", "\"하지만 누군가는 늘 살해당하기 마련이라, 저는 읽지 않았습니다.\""]], [["A display of indifference to all the actions and passions of mankind was not supposed to be such a distinguished quality at that time, I think, as I have observed it to be considered since.", "인류의 온갖 행동과 격정에 대해 무관심을 드러내는 것이, 그 무렵에는, 그 후로 내가 으레 그렇게 여겨지는 것을 지켜본 만큼 그리 대단한 자질로 통하지는 않았던 것 같다."], ["I have known it very fashionable indeed.", "나는 그것이 정말이지 무척 유행하는 것을 보았다."], ["I have seen it displayed with such success, that I have encountered some fine ladies and gentlemen who might as well have been born caterpillars.", "어찌나 성공적으로 펼쳐지는지, 나는 차라리 애벌레로 태어났더라면 나았을 법한 멋쟁이 신사 숙녀들을 더러 만나기도 했다."], ["Perhaps it impressed me the more then, because it was new to me, but it certainly did not tend to exalt my opinion of, or to strengthen my confidence in, Mr. Jack Maldon.", "어쩌면 그때 나에게는 그것이 새로웠던 까닭에 더욱 인상 깊었는지 모르나, 어쨌든 그것이 잭 몰던 군에 대한 내 평가를 높여 주거나 그에 대한 내 신뢰를 굳혀 주는 데 보탬이 되지 않았던 것만은 분명하다."]], [["‘I came out to inquire whether Annie would like to go to the opera tonight,’ said Mr. Maldon, turning to her.", "\"오늘 밤 애니가 오페라에 가고 싶어 할지 물어보러 들렀어.\" 몰던 군이 그녀에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."], ["‘It’s the last good night there will be, this season; and there’s a singer there, whom she really ought to hear.", "\"이번 시즌에 볼 만한 마지막 밤이거든. 게다가 그곳에 꼭 들어 봐야 할 가수가 한 명 있어."], ["She is perfectly exquisite.", "정말 빼어난 가수야."], ["Besides which, she is so charmingly ugly,’ relapsing into languor.", "게다가 어찌나 매력적으로 못생겼는지,\" 다시 나른함 속으로 가라앉으며."]], [["The Doctor, ever pleased with what was likely to please his young wife, turned to her and said:", "박사님은 늘 젊은 아내를 기쁘게 할 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 흐뭇해하던지라, 부인에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["‘You must go, Annie.", "\"가야지, 애니."], ["You must go.’", "꼭 가야 해.\""]], [["‘I would rather not,’ she said to the Doctor.", "\"전 안 가는 게 낫겠어요.\" 그녀가 박사님에게 말했다."], ["‘I prefer to remain at home.", "\"집에 있고 싶어요."], ["I would much rather remain at home.’", "집에 있는 게 훨씬 좋아요.\""]], [["Without looking at her cousin, she then addressed me, and asked me about Agnes, and whether she should see her, and whether she was not likely to come that day; and was so much disturbed, that I wondered how even the Doctor, buttering his toast, could be blind to what was so obvious.", "그러더니 사촌 오빠는 쳐다보지도 않고 나에게 말을 건네며, 아그네스에 대해, 아그네스를 만나 볼 수 있을지, 아그네스가 오늘 오지 않을지를 물었다. 그러고는 어찌나 심란해 보이던지, 토스트에 버터를 바르고 있는 박사님조차 어떻게 저토록 빤한 것을 보지 못하는지 나로서는 의아할 지경이었다."]], [["But he saw nothing. He told her, good-naturedly, that she was young and ought to be amused and entertained, and must not allow herself to be made dull by a dull old fellow.", "그러나 그는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그는 부인에게 너그럽게 말하기를, 부인은 젊으니 즐기고 놀아야지, 따분한 늙은이 때문에 따분해져서는 안 된다고 했다."], ["Moreover, he said, he wanted to hear her sing all the new singer’s songs to him; and how could she do that well, unless she went?", "더구나 그는 부인이 그 새 가수의 노래를 모조리 자기에게 불러 주는 것을 듣고 싶다고 했다. 그런데 가지 않고서야 어떻게 그걸 제대로 부르겠느냐는 것이었다."], ["So the Doctor persisted in making the engagement for her, and Mr. Jack Maldon was to come back to dinner.", "그리하여 박사님은 한사코 부인을 대신해 약속을 잡았고, 잭 몰던 군은 저녁 식사 때 다시 오기로 했다."], ["This concluded, he went to his Patent place, I suppose; but at all events went away on his horse, looking very idle.", "이렇게 매듭이 지어지자, 그는 짐작건대 자기 특허 자리로 갔을 테지만, 어쨌든 무척이나 빈둥대는 모습으로 말을 타고 떠났다."]], [["I was curious to find out next morning, whether she had been.", "나는 이튿날 아침, 그녀가 갔었는지 궁금해 알아보고 싶었다."], ["She had not, but had sent into London to put her cousin off; and had gone out in the afternoon to see Agnes, and had prevailed upon the Doctor to go with her; and they had walked home by the fields, the Doctor told me, the evening being delightful.", "그녀는 가지 않았고, 사촌 오빠에게 약속을 미루겠다고 런던으로 기별을 보냈으며, 오후에는 아그네스를 만나러 외출했는데, 박사님을 졸라 함께 가게 했다고 한다. 그리고 저녁나절이 무척 좋아서, 둘이 들판을 가로질러 걸어 돌아왔다고 박사님이 나에게 일러 주었다."], ["I wondered then, whether she would have gone if Agnes had not been in town, and whether Agnes had some good influence over her too!", "그때 나는 궁금했다. 아그네스가 시내에 없었더라도 그녀가 갔을까, 그리고 아그네스가 그녀에게도 어떤 좋은 영향을 끼치고 있는 것은 아닐까 하고!"]], [["She did not look very happy, I thought; but it was a good face, or a very false one.", "내 생각에 그녀는 그리 행복해 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그것은 선한 얼굴이거나, 아니면 더없이 거짓된 얼굴이었다."], ["I often glanced at it, for she sat in the window all the time we were at work; and made our breakfast, which we took by snatches as we were employed.", "나는 자주 그녀의 얼굴을 흘끔거렸는데, 우리가 일하는 동안 내내 그녀가 창가에 앉아 있었던 데다, 우리가 일하는 틈틈이 짬짬이 들던 아침 식사도 그녀가 차려 주었기 때문이다."], ["When I left, at nine o’clock, she was kneeling on the ground at the Doctor’s feet, putting on his shoes and gaiters for him.", "아홉 시에 내가 떠날 무렵, 그녀는 박사님 발치에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 그의 신발과 각반을 신겨 주고 있었다."], ["There was a softened shade upon her face, thrown from some green leaves overhanging the open window of the low room; and I thought all the way to Doctors’ Commons, of the night when I had seen it looking at him as he read.", "그 낮은 방의 열린 창문 위로 드리운 푸른 잎사귀에서 던져진 부드러운 그늘이 그녀의 얼굴에 어려 있었다. 그리고 나는 민법 박사회관으로 가는 내내, 예전에 박사님이 책을 읽는 동안 그녀가 그를 바라보던 그 모습을 보았던 그 밤을 떠올렸다."]], [["I was pretty busy now; up at five in the morning, and home at nine or ten at night.", "이제 나는 꽤 바빴다. 아침 다섯 시에 일어나, 밤 아홉 시나 열 시에야 집에 돌아왔다."], ["But I had infinite satisfaction in being so closely engaged, and never walked slowly on any account, and felt enthusiastically that the more I tired myself, the more I was doing to deserve Dora.", "그러나 나는 그토록 빡빡하게 일하는 데서 무한한 만족을 느꼈고, 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 느릿느릿 걷는 법이 없었으며, 내 몸을 지치게 할수록 도라를 가질 자격을 그만큼 더 쌓는 것이라는 열의에 찬 마음이 들었다."], ["I had not revealed myself in my altered character to Dora yet, because she was coming to see Miss Mills in a few days, and I deferred all I had to tell her until then; merely informing her in my letters (all our communications were secretly forwarded through Miss Mills), that I had much to tell her.", "나는 아직 달라진 내 모습을 도라에게 드러내지 않았는데, 며칠 안에 그녀가 밀스 양을 만나러 오기로 되어 있어, 들려줄 이야기는 죄다 그때까지 미뤄 두었기 때문이다. 다만 편지로 (우리의 모든 연락은 밀스 양을 통해 몰래 오갔다) 그녀에게 들려줄 이야기가 많다고만 알려 두었다."], ["In the meantime, I put myself on a short allowance of bear’s grease, wholly abandoned scented soap and lavender water, and sold off three waistcoats at a prodigious sacrifice, as being too luxurious for my stern career.", "그러는 동안, 나는 곰 기름을 아껴 쓰고, 향 비누와 라벤더 물은 아주 끊어 버렸으며, 조끼 세 벌은 내 모진 앞날에 너무 사치스럽다며 엄청난 손해를 무릅쓰고 팔아 치웠다."]], [["Not satisfied with all these proceedings, but burning with impatience to do something more, I went to see Traddles, now lodging up behind the parapet of a house in Castle Street, Holborn. Mr.", "이 모든 일로도 만족하지 못하고, 무언가 더 하고 싶어 안달이 나서, 나는 트래들스를 보러 갔다. 그는 이제 홀번의 캐슬 가에 있는 어느 집 난간 뒤쪽에 세 들어 살고 있었다."], ["Dick, who had been with me to Highgate twice already, and had resumed his companionship with the Doctor, I took with me.", "이미 나와 함께 하이게이트에 두 번이나 다녀왔고 박사님과의 교제도 다시 트게 된 딕 씨를 나는 데리고 갔다."]], [["I took Mr. Dick with me, because, acutely sensitive to my aunt’s reverses, and sincerely believing that no galley-slave or convict worked as I did, he had begun to fret and worry himself out of spirits and appetite, as having nothing useful to do.", "내가 딕 씨를 데려간 까닭은 이러했다. 그는 고모님의 몰락에 몹시 마음 아파했고, 나만큼 일하는 갤리선 노예나 죄수도 없으리라고 진심으로 믿었기에, 자기는 쓸모 있게 할 일이 하나도 없다며 안달복달하다 기운과 입맛마저 잃기 시작한 것이다."], ["In this condition, he felt more incapable of finishing the Memorial than ever; and the harder he worked at it, the oftener that unlucky head of King Charles the First got into it.", "이런 상태에서 그는 그 어느 때보다 진정서를 끝내지 못할 것 같다고 느꼈다. 그리고 거기에 매달려 애쓰면 애쓸수록, 그 불운한 찰스 일세의 머리통이 그만큼 더 자주 끼어들었다."], ["Seriously apprehending that his malady would increase, unless we put some innocent deception upon him and caused him to believe that he was useful, or unless we could put him in the way of being really useful (which would be better), I made up my mind to try if Traddles could help us.", "나는 그의 병이 더 깊어질까 봐 자못 걱정스러웠다. 그가 쓸모 있다고 믿게끔 무해한 속임수라도 쓰든지, 아니면 (이편이 더 나을 텐데) 그를 정말로 쓸모 있게 할 길을 마련해 주든지 하지 않으면 안 될 것 같았다. 그래서 나는 트래들스가 우리를 도와줄 수 있는지 시험해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["Before we went, I wrote Traddles a full statement of all that had happened, and Traddles wrote me back a capital answer, expressive of his sympathy and friendship.", "가기에 앞서 나는 그동안 일어난 일을 모조리 적어 트래들스에게 보냈고, 트래들스는 자신의 연민과 우정을 담은 더없이 훌륭한 답장을 보내왔다."]], [["We found him hard at work with his inkstand and papers, refreshed by the sight of the flower-pot stand and the little round table in a corner of the small apartment.", "우리는 그가 잉크병과 서류를 펼쳐 놓고 열심히 일하고 있는 것을 보았는데, 그 작은 방 한구석의 화분 받침대와 작고 둥근 탁자가 눈에 들어와 마음이 환해졌다."], ["He received us cordially, and made friends with Mr. Dick in a moment. Mr.", "그는 우리를 다정하게 맞아 주었고, 딕 씨와도 단번에 친해졌다."], ["Dick professed an absolute certainty of having seen him before, and we both said, ‘Very likely.’", "딕 씨는 전에 그를 본 적이 분명히 있노라고 우겼고, 우리 둘은 \"그럴 만도 하지요\"라고 맞장구쳐 주었다."]], [["The first subject on which I had to consult Traddles was this,--I had heard that many men distinguished in various pursuits had begun life by reporting the debates in Parliament.", "내가 트래들스와 의논해야 할 첫 번째 주제는 이것이었다. 나는 여러 분야에서 두각을 나타낸 많은 이들이 의회의 토론을 기사로 받아쓰는 일로 인생을 시작했다는 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다."], ["Traddles having mentioned newspapers to me, as one of his hopes, I had put the two things together, and told Traddles in my letter that I wished to know how I could qualify myself for this pursuit.", "트래들스가 자기 희망 중 하나로 신문 일을 나에게 언급한 적이 있던지라, 나는 그 두 가지를 한데 엮어, 편지로 트래들스에게 이 일을 하려면 어떻게 자격을 갖춰야 하는지 알고 싶다고 했다."], ["Traddles now informed me, as the result of his inquiries, that the mere mechanical acquisition necessary, except in rare cases, for thorough excellence in it, that is to say, a perfect and entire command of the mystery of short-hand writing and reading, was about equal in difficulty to the mastery of six languages; and that it might perhaps be attained, by dint of perseverance, in the course of a few years.", "이제 트래들스가 알아본 결과를 일러 주기를, 드문 경우를 빼고는, 그 일에 두루 능통해지는 데 필요한 순전히 기계적인 기술의 습득, 다시 말해 속기로 쓰고 읽는 그 비법을 완벽하고 온전하게 익히는 일이, 그 어려움으로 치자면 여섯 개 언어를 통달하는 것과 맞먹는다고 했다. 그리고 그것은 어쩌면 끈기를 발휘하면 몇 해에 걸쳐 이룰 수 있을지도 모른다고 했다."], ["Traddles reasonably supposed that this would settle the business; but I, only feeling that here indeed were a few tall trees to be hewn down, immediately resolved to work my way on to Dora through this thicket, axe in hand.", "트래들스는 으레 이쯤이면 이 일이 매듭지어지리라 짐작했다. 그러나 나는 여기에야말로 베어 넘겨야 할 키 큰 나무 몇 그루가 있구나 싶은 마음에, 그 즉시 도끼를 손에 쥐고 이 덤불을 헤치며 도라에게로 나아가기로 작정했다."]], [["‘I am very much obliged to you, my dear Traddles!’ said I.", "\"정말 고맙네, 내 사랑하는 트래들스!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I’ll begin tomorrow.’", "\"내일부터 시작하겠네.\""]], [["Traddles looked astonished, as he well might; but he had no notion as yet of my rapturous condition.", "트래들스는 놀란 표정을 지었는데, 그도 그럴 만했다. 하지만 그는 아직 내 황홀한 상태를 까맣게 모르고 있었다."]], [["‘I’ll buy a book,’ said I, ‘with a good scheme of this art in it; I’ll work at it at the Commons, where I haven’t half enough to do; I’ll take down the speeches in our court for practice--Traddles, my dear fellow, I’ll master it!’", "\"이 기술의 좋은 체계가 담긴 책을 한 권 살 거야,\" 내가 말했다. \"할 일이 절반도 안 되는 박사회관에서 그걸 익히겠어. 연습 삼아 우리 법정에서 오가는 진술을 받아 적어 보겠어 — 트래들스, 이 친구야, 내가 기어이 통달하고 말겠어!\""]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Traddles, opening his eyes, ‘I had no idea you were such a determined character, Copperfield!’", "\"이런, 세상에,\" 트래들스가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 말했다. \"자네가 그렇게 굳센 사람인 줄은 미처 몰랐네, 코퍼필드!\""]], [["I don’t know how he should have had, for it was new enough to me.", "그가 어떻게 알았겠는가, 나 자신에게도 새로운 일이었으니."], ["I passed that off, and brought Mr. Dick on the carpet.", "나는 그 말을 슬쩍 흘려 넘기고, 딕 씨 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["‘You see,’ said Mr.", "\"그러니까 말입니다,\""], ["Dick, wistfully, ‘if I could exert myself, Mr. Traddles--if I could beat a drum--or blow anything!’", "딕 씨가 간절하게 말했다. \"제가 힘을 좀 쓸 수 있다면요, 트래들스 씨 — 제가 북을 칠 수만 있다면 — 아니, 무엇이든 불 수만 있다면요!\""]], [["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 양반!"], ["I have little doubt he would have preferred such an employment in his heart to all others.", "나는 그가 마음속으로는 그런 일을 다른 무엇보다 더 좋아했으리라는 것을 거의 의심하지 않는다."], ["Traddles, who would not have smiled for the world, replied composedly:", "무슨 일이 있어도 빙긋 웃지 않았을 트래들스가 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["‘But you are a very good penman, sir.", "\"하지만 선생님은 글씨를 아주 잘 쓰시잖습니까."], ["You told me so, Copperfield?’ ‘Excellent!’ said I.", "자네가 그렇게 말했지, 코퍼필드?\" \"기막히게 잘 쓰시지!\""], ["And indeed he was.", "내가 말했다."], ["He wrote with extraordinary neatness.", "정말 그러했다. 그는 유난히 정갈한 글씨를 썼다."]], [["‘Don’t you think,’ said Traddles, ‘you could copy writings, sir, if I got them for you?’", "\"이렇게 생각해 보시는 건 어떨까요,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"제가 글을 좀 구해다 드리면, 선생님이 그걸 베껴 써 주실 수 있지 않을까요?\""]], [["Mr. Dick looked doubtfully at me.", "딕 씨는 미심쩍은 얼굴로 나를 보았다."], ["‘Eh, Trotwood?’", "\"어떤가, 트롯우드?\""]], [["I shook my head. Mr.", "나는 고개를 가로저었다."], ["Dick shook his, and sighed.", "딕 씨도 고개를 가로저으며 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["‘Tell him about the Memorial,’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"진정서 이야기를 해 주게.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["I explained to Traddles that there was a difficulty in keeping King Charles the First out of Mr.", "나는 딕 씨의 원고에서 찰스 일세를 떼어 놓기가 어렵다는 사정을 트래들스에게 설명해 주었다."], ["Dick’s manuscripts; Mr. Dick in the meanwhile looking very deferentially and seriously at Traddles, and sucking his thumb.", "그러는 동안 딕 씨는 자못 공손하고 진지한 얼굴로 트래들스를 바라보며 엄지손가락을 빨고 있었다."]], [["‘But these writings, you know, that I speak of, are already drawn up and finished,’ said Traddles after a little consideration. ‘Mr.", "\"하지만 제가 말씀드리는 그 글들은, 아시다시피, 이미 다 작성되어 끝난 것들입니다,\" 트래들스가 잠깐 생각하더니 말했다."], ["Dick has nothing to do with them.", "\"딕 씨가 거기에 무얼 보탤 것도 없지요."], ["Wouldn’t that make a difference, Copperfield?", "그러면 사정이 달라지지 않을까, 코퍼필드?"], ["At all events, wouldn’t it be well to try?’", "어쨌거나, 한번 시도해 보는 것이 좋지 않겠나?\""]], [["This gave us new hope. Traddles and I laying our heads together apart, while Mr.", "이 말이 우리에게 새로운 희망을 주었다."], ["Dick anxiously watched us from his chair, we concocted a scheme in virtue of which we got him to work next day, with triumphant success.", "딕 씨가 의자에 앉아 애타게 우리를 지켜보는 동안, 트래들스와 나는 한쪽에서 머리를 맞대고, 그를 이튿날부터 일에 끌어들일 묘책을 짜냈고, 그것은 의기양양한 성공을 거두었다."]], [["On a table by the window in Buckingham Street, we set out the work Traddles procured for him--which was to make, I forget how many copies of a legal document about some right of way--and on another table we spread the last unfinished original of the great Memorial. Our instructions to Mr.", "버킹엄 가의 창가에 놓인 탁자 하나에, 우리는 트래들스가 구해 온 일거리를 펼쳐 놓았다 — 어느 통행권에 관한 법률 문서를, 몇 부였는지는 잊었지만 여러 부 베끼는 일이었다 — 그리고 다른 탁자 하나에는 그 위대한 진정서의 아직 끝맺지 못한 마지막 원본을 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["Dick were that he should copy exactly what he had before him, without the least departure from the original; and that when he felt it necessary to make the slightest allusion to King Charles the First, he should fly to the Memorial. We exhorted him to be resolute in this, and left my aunt to observe him.", "딕 씨에게 우리가 일러 둔 지시는, 눈앞에 놓인 것을 한 치도 원본에서 벗어나지 말고 그대로 베껴 쓰되, 찰스 일세를 조금이라도 끌어들이고 싶은 마음이 들거든 그 즉시 진정서로 달려가라는 것이었다. 우리는 그에게 이것을 굳게 지키라고 거듭 당부하고는, 그를 지켜보는 일은 고모님에게 맡겼다."], ["My aunt reported to us, afterwards, that, at first, he was like a man playing the kettle-drums, and constantly divided his attentions between the two; but that, finding this confuse and fatigue him, and having his copy there, plainly before his eyes, he soon sat at it in an orderly business-like manner, and postponed the Memorial to a more convenient time.", "고모님이 나중에 우리에게 일러 주기를, 처음에 그는 케틀드럼을 치는 사람 같아서, 두 가지 사이를 끊임없이 오가며 갈팡질팡했다고 한다. 그러나 이러다 보니 머리가 복잡해지고 지치는 데다, 베낄 것이 눈앞에 빤히 놓여 있는 터라, 그는 곧 차분하고 일하는 사람답게 자리에 앉아, 진정서는 더 한가한 때로 미뤄 두었다고 한다."], ["In a word, although we took great care that he should have no more to do than was good for him, and although he did not begin with the beginning of a week, he earned by the following Saturday night ten shillings and nine-pence; and never, while I live, shall I forget his going about to all the shops in the neighbourhood to change this treasure into sixpences, or his bringing them to my aunt arranged in the form of a heart upon a waiter, with tears of joy and pride in his eyes.", "한마디로, 비록 우리가 그에게 알맞은 만큼만 일을 시키려고 무척 마음을 썼고, 또 그가 한 주가 시작될 때부터 일을 시작한 것도 아니었지만, 그는 다음 토요일 밤까지 십 실링 구 펜스를 벌었다. 그리고 그가 이 보물을 육 펜스짜리 동전으로 바꾸려고 동네 가게란 가게를 죄다 돌아다니던 일, 그것을 쟁반 위에 하트 모양으로 가지런히 늘어놓고, 두 눈에 기쁨과 자랑의 눈물을 글썽이며 고모님께 가져오던 일을, 나는 살아 있는 동안 결코 잊지 못할 것이다."], ["He was like one under the propitious influence of a charm, from the moment of his being usefully employed; and if there were a happy man in the world, that Saturday night, it was the grateful creature who thought my aunt the most wonderful woman in existence, and me the most wonderful young man.", "그는 쓸모 있게 일하게 된 그 순간부터, 마치 상서로운 부적의 힘 아래 놓인 사람 같았다. 그리고 그 토요일 밤, 세상에 행복한 사람이 있었다면, 그것은 고모님을 세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인으로, 나를 가장 놀라운 청년으로 여기던, 그 고마운 양반이었다."]], [["‘No starving now, Trotwood,’ said Mr.", "\"이제 굶을 일은 없어, 트롯우드,\""], ["Dick, shaking hands with me in a corner.", "딕 씨가 한구석에서 나와 악수하며 말했다."], ["‘I’ll provide for her, Sir!’ and he flourished his ten fingers in the air, as if they were ten banks.", "\"내가 고모님을 부양할 걸세, 자네!\" 그러더니 마치 열 손가락이 열 개의 은행이라도 되는 양, 손가락을 공중에 활짝 펼쳐 흔들었다."]], [["I hardly know which was the better pleased, Traddles or I. ‘It really,’ said Traddles, suddenly, taking a letter out of his pocket, and giving it to me, ‘put Mr.", "트래들스와 나, 둘 중에 누가 더 기뻤는지 나로서는 알기 어렵다. \"이런,\" 트래들스가 갑자기 주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 나에게 건네며 말했다."], ["Micawber quite out of my head!’", "\"미코버 씨를 까맣게 잊고 있었지 뭔가!\""]], [["The letter (Mr. Micawber never missed any possible opportunity of writing a letter) was addressed to me, ‘By the kindness of T.", "그 편지는 (미코버 씨는 편지 쓸 만한 기회라면 단 한 번도 놓치는 법이 없었다) 나에게 보내진 것으로, \"이너 템플 소속 T."], ["Traddles, Esquire, of the Inner Temple.’ It ran thus:--", "트래들스 귀하의 호의를 빌려\"라고 적혀 있었다. 그 내용은 이러했다."]], [["‘You may possibly not be unprepared to receive the intimation that something has turned up. I may have mentioned to you on a former occasion that I was in expectation of such an event.", "\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\n무언가 일이 풀렸다는 소식을 받을 마음의 준비가 어쩌면 그대에게 안 되어 있지는 않으리라 보네. 내가 전에 한번 그런 일을 기대하고 있노라고 그대에게 언급했을 법도 하니."]], [["‘I am about to establish myself in one of the provincial towns of our favoured island (where the society may be described as a happy admixture of the agricultural and the clerical), in immediate connexion with one of the learned professions. Mrs.", "나는 우리 축복받은 섬의 어느 지방 도시(그곳 사회는 농업과 성직의 행복한 어우러짐이라 일컬을 만하네)에, 학식 있는 직업 중 하나와 직접 연을 맺으며 자리를 잡으려 하네. 미코버 부인과 우리 자식들도 나와 함께 갈 걸세."], ["Micawber and our offspring will accompany me. Our ashes, at a future period, will probably be found commingled in the cemetery attached to a venerable pile, for which the spot to which I refer has acquired a reputation, shall I say from China to Peru?", "훗날 우리의 유골은 어느 유서 깊은 건물에 딸린 묘지에서 한데 섞인 채 발견될 것이네. 내가 말하는 그곳은 그 묘지로 하여, 이를테면 중국에서 페루에 이르기까지, 그만한 명성을 얻었다고 해도 좋을 곳이지."]], [["‘In bidding adieu to the modern Babylon, where we have undergone many vicissitudes, I trust not ignobly, Mrs. Micawber and myself cannot disguise from our minds that we part, it may be for years and it may be for ever, with an individual linked by strong associations to the altar of our domestic life.", "수많은 부침을 — 그래도 부디 비루하지는 않게 겪어 낸 — 저 현대의 바빌론에 작별을 고하면서, 미코버 부인과 나는, 우리 가정생활의 제단에 강한 인연으로 얽힌 한 사람과, 여러 해 동안일 수도, 어쩌면 영영일 수도 있는 이별을 한다는 사실을 마음에서 감출 길이 없네."], ["If, on the eve of such a departure, you will accompany our mutual friend, Mr. Thomas Traddles, to our present abode, and there reciprocate the wishes natural to the occasion, you will confer a Boon", "그러한 떠남을 앞둔 저녁에, 그대가 우리 공동의 벗인 토머스 트래들스 씨와 함께 지금 우리가 머무는 거처로 와, 그 자리에 어울리는 소망을 함께 나눠 준다면, 그대는 다음과 같은 자에게 더없는 은혜를 베푸는 것이 되리라."]], [["‘On ‘One ‘Who ‘Is ‘Ever yours, ‘WILKINS MICAWBER.’", "영원히 그대의 것인 이 한 사람, 윌킨스 미코버.\""]], [["I was glad to find that Mr. Micawber had got rid of his dust and ashes, and that something really had turned up at last.", "나는 미코버 씨가 그 잿더미를 털어 내고, 마침내 정말로 무언가 일이 풀렸다는 것을 알게 되어 기뻤다."], ["Learning from Traddles that the invitation referred to the evening then wearing away, I expressed my readiness to do honour to it; and we went off together to the lodging which Mr.", "트래들스에게서 그 초대가 곧 저물어 가는 그날 저녁을 가리키는 것임을 듣고, 나는 기꺼이 그 자리를 빛내 드리겠노라고 했다."], ["Micawber occupied as Mr. Mortimer, and which was situated near the top of the Gray’s Inn Road.", "그리하여 우리는 함께, 미코버 씨가 모티머 씨라는 이름으로 머물고 있는, 그레이즈 인 가 꼭대기 근처에 자리한 그 숙소로 향했다."]], [["The resources of this lodging were so limited, that we found the twins, now some eight or nine years old, reposing in a turn-up bedstead in the family sitting-room, where Mr.", "이 숙소의 형편은 어찌나 빠듯하던지, 우리는 이제 여덟아홉 살쯤 된 쌍둥이가 가족 거실의 접이식 침대에서 잠들어 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Micawber had prepared, in a wash-hand-stand jug, what he called ‘a Brew’ of the agreeable beverage for which he was famous.", "그 거실에서 미코버 씨는 세면대 물병에다, 그가 그것으로 이름난 바로 그 흐뭇한 음료를 자기 말로 \"한 통\""], ["I had the pleasure, on this occasion, of renewing the acquaintance of Master Micawber, whom I found a promising boy of about twelve or thirteen, very subject to that restlessness of limb which is not an unfrequent phenomenon in youths of his age.", "우려내 두었다. 나는 이 자리에서, 미코버 도령과 다시 알고 지내는 기쁨을 누렸는데, 그는 열두세 살쯤 된 장래가 촉망되는 소년으로, 그 또래 젊은이들에게 드물지 않게 나타나는 그 사지의 들썩거림에 무척 시달리고 있었다."], ["I also became once more known to his sister, Miss Micawber, in whom, as Mr. Micawber told us, ‘her mother renewed her youth, like the Phoenix’.", "또한 나는 그의 누이인 미코버 양과도 다시 알게 되었는데, 미코버 씨의 말을 빌리자면, 그 아이에게서는 \"그 어미가 불사조처럼 젊음을 되살리고\" 있었다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, ‘yourself and Mr.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Traddles find us on the brink of migration, and will excuse any little discomforts incidental to that position.’", "\"그대와 트래들스 씨가 보다시피 우리는 이주의 문턱에 있으니, 그러한 처지에 따르기 마련인 자그마한 불편들은 너그러이 봐주시게나.\""]], [["Glancing round as I made a suitable reply, I observed that the family effects were already packed, and that the amount of luggage was by no means overwhelming.", "알맞은 대답을 하며 둘레를 둘러보니, 가족의 살림살이가 이미 다 꾸려져 있고, 짐의 양도 결코 압도적일 만큼은 아님이 눈에 들어왔다."], ["I congratulated Mrs. Micawber on the approaching change.", "나는 미코버 부인에게 다가오는 변화를 축하했다."]], [["‘My dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘of your friendly interest in all our affairs, I am well assured.", "\"우리 집안일 전부에 대한 당신의 다정한 관심을, 저는 잘 믿고 있답니다. 제 친정 식구들이야 이걸 추방이라 여기든 말든 그건 그들 마음이지요."], ["My family may consider it banishment, if they please; but I am a wife and mother, and I never will desert Mr. Micawber.’", "하지만 저는 한 사람의 아내요 어미입니다. 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 저버리지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["Traddles, appealed to by Mrs. Micawber’s eye, feelingly acquiesced.", "미코버 부인의 눈길에 호소받은 트래들스가, 진심 어린 마음으로 그 말에 수긍했다."]], [["‘That,’ said Mrs.", "\"적어도 그것이,\""], ["Micawber, ‘that, at least, is my view, my dear Mr.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Copperfield and Mr. Traddles, of the obligation which I took upon myself when I repeated the irrevocable words, “I, Emma, take thee, Wilkins.”", "\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨, 그리고 트래들스 씨, 제가 '나, 엠마는, 그대 윌킨스를 지아비로 맞노라'라는, 돌이킬 수 없는 그 말을 따라 외었을 때 스스로 짊어진 의무에 대한 제 생각이랍니다."], ["I read the service over with a flat-candle on the previous night, and the conclusion I derived from it was, that I never could desert Mr.", "저는 그 전날 밤 손촛불을 켜 놓고 그 혼인 예식문을 처음부터 끝까지 읽어 보았는데, 거기서 제가 끌어낸 결론은, 저는 결코 미코버 씨를 저버릴 수 없다는 것이었어요. 그리고,\""], ["Micawber. And,’ said Mrs.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘though it is possible I may be mistaken in my view of the ceremony, I never will!’", "\"제가 그 예식의 뜻을 잘못 새겼을 수도 있겠지만, 저는 결단코 그러지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\""], ["Micawber, a little impatiently, ‘I am not conscious that you are expected to do anything of the sort.’", "미코버 씨가 다소 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"당신이 그런 일을 하리라고 누가 기대한다는 건지 나는 영 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘I am aware, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ pursued Mrs.", "\"잘 알고 있어요, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Micawber, ‘that I am now about to cast my lot among strangers; and I am also aware that the various members of my family, to whom Mr.", "미코버 부인이 말을 이었다. \"제가 이제 낯선 사람들 틈에 제 운명을 던지려 한다는 것을요."], ["Micawber has written in the most gentlemanly terms, announcing that fact, have not taken the least notice of Mr. Micawber’s communication.", "또한 미코버 씨가 그 사실을 알리려고 더없이 점잖은 말로 편지를 보낸 제 친정 식구들이, 미코버 씨의 그 글에 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳하지 않았다는 것도요."], ["Indeed I may be superstitious,’ said Mrs. Micawber, ‘but it appears to me that Mr.", "어쩌면 제가 미신을 믿는 것인지도 모르겠습니다만,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber is destined never to receive any answers whatever to the great majority of the communications he writes.", "\"제가 보기에 미코버 씨는 자기가 쓰는 글의 거의 대부분에 대해 그 어떤 답장도 결코 받지 못할 운명인 것만 같아요."], ["I may augur, from the silence of my family, that they object to the resolution I have taken; but I should not allow myself to be swerved from the path of duty, Mr. Copperfield, even by my papa and mama, were they still living.’", "친정 식구들의 침묵으로 미루어, 제가 내린 결심에 그들이 반대하는 거라고 점쳐 볼 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 저는, 코퍼필드 씨, 설령 친정 아버지 어머니가 아직 살아 계신다 한들, 마땅히 가야 할 길에서 결코 벗어나지 않겠어요.\""]], [["I expressed my opinion that this was going in the right direction. ‘It may be a sacrifice,’ said Mrs.", "나는 그것이 옳은 방향으로 가는 것이라는 내 의견을 밝혔다. \"성당이 있는 도시에 스스로를 가두는 것이,\""], ["Micawber, ‘to immure one’s-self in a Cathedral town; but surely, Mr.", "미코버 부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 하나의 희생일 수도 있겠지요."], ["Copperfield, if it is a sacrifice in me, it is much more a sacrifice in a man of Mr. Micawber’s abilities.’", "하지만 코퍼필드 씨, 정녕 그것이 저에게 희생이라면, 미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 분에게는 훨씬 더 큰 희생일 거예요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You are going to a Cathedral town?’ said I.", "성당이 있는 도시로 가시는군요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber, who had been helping us all, out of the wash-hand-stand jug, replied:", "세면대 물병에서 우리 모두에게 음료를 따라 주고 있던 미코버 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘To Canterbury. In fact, my dear Copperfield, I have entered into arrangements, by virtue of which I stand pledged and contracted to our friend Heep, to assist and serve him in the capacity of--and to be--his confidential clerk.’", "\"캔터베리로 가네. 실은, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 나는 어떤 약정을 맺었는데, 그에 따라 우리 벗 힙에게 — 그의 심복 사무원 자격으로 그를 보좌하고 섬기기로 — 맹세하고 계약한 몸이 되었다네.\""]], [["I stared at Mr. Micawber, who greatly enjoyed my surprise.", "나는 미코버 씨를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 그는 내 놀라움을 무척이나 즐겼다."]], [["‘I am bound to state to you,’ he said, with an official air, ‘that the business habits, and the prudent suggestions, of Mrs.", "\"그대에게 꼭 말해 두어야겠는데,\" 그가 격식 차린 태도로 말했다."], ["Micawber, have in a great measure conduced to this result. The gauntlet, to which Mrs.", "\"미코버 부인의 사무적인 습성과 신중한 제안이, 이 결과를 끌어내는 데 크게 한몫했다네."], ["Micawber referred upon a former occasion, being thrown down in the form of an advertisement, was taken up by my friend Heep, and led to a mutual recognition.", "미코버 부인이 전에 한번 말한 그 장갑이 광고의 형태로 던져졌고, 그것을 내 벗 힙이 집어 들면서, 서로를 알아보게 된 것이지. 내 벗 힙으로 말할 것 같으면,\""], ["Of my friend Heep,’ said Mr.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘who is a man of remarkable shrewdness, I desire to speak with all possible respect.", "\"놀랍도록 빈틈없는 사람인데, 나는 그에 대해 가능한 한 온갖 경의를 다해 말하고 싶네."], ["My friend Heep has not fixed the positive remuneration at too high a figure, but he has made a great deal, in the way of extrication from the pressure of pecuniary difficulties, contingent on the value of my services; and on the value of those services I pin my faith.", "내 벗 힙은 확정된 보수를 너무 높은 액수로 정하지는 않았으나, 금전적 곤경의 압박에서 벗어나는 일과 관련해서는, 내 봉사의 값어치에 달린 것으로 많은 것을 약속해 주었네. 그리고 나는 바로 그 봉사의 값어치에 내 믿음을 거네."], ["Such address and intelligence as I chance to possess,’ said Mr.", "나에게 우연히 갖춰진 그만한 수완과 지력이라면,\""], ["Micawber, boastfully disparaging himself, with the old genteel air, ‘will be devoted to my friend Heep’s service.", "미코버 씨가 옛날의 그 점잖은 태도로, 자기를 한껏 낮추는 척 뽐내며 말했다. \"내 벗 힙을 섬기는 데 바쳐질 것이네."], ["I have already some acquaintance with the law--as a defendant on civil process--and I shall immediately apply myself to the Commentaries of one of the most eminent and remarkable of our English jurists.", "나는 이미 법에 대해 어느 정도 아는 바가 있어 — 민사 소송의 피고로서 말이지 — 그러니 곧장 우리 영국에서 가장 저명하고 빼어난 법학자 한 분의 주해서에 매달릴 작정이네."], ["I believe it is unnecessary to add that I allude to Mr. justice Blackstone.’", "내가 말하는 분이 블랙스톤 판사님이라는 건 굳이 덧붙일 필요가 없겠지.\""]], [["These observations, and indeed the greater part of the observations made that evening, were interrupted by Mrs. Micawber’s discovering that Master Micawber was sitting on his boots, or holding his head on with both arms as if he felt it loose, or accidentally kicking Traddles under the table, or shuffling his feet over one another, or producing them at distances from himself apparently outrageous to nature, or lying sideways with his hair among the wine-glasses, or developing his restlessness of limb in some other form incompatible with the general interests of society; and by Master Micawber’s receiving those discoveries in a resentful spirit.", "이런 말들은, 그리고 사실 그날 저녁에 오간 말들의 대부분은, 미코버 도령이 자기 장화 위에 걸터앉거나, 머리가 헐거워지기라도 한 듯 두 팔로 머리를 감싸 쥐거나, 탁자 밑에서 얼떨결에 트래들스를 걷어차거나, 두 발을 서로 비비적대거나, 자연의 섭리를 거스를 만큼 터무니없는 거리에 두 발을 쭉 뻗어 놓거나, 머리카락을 와인 잔 사이에 늘어뜨린 채 옆으로 드러눕거나, 그 밖에 사회의 공익과 영 어울리지 않는 어떤 다른 모양새로 사지의 들썩거림을 드러내는 것을 미코버 부인이 발견할 때마다, 그리고 미코버 도령이 그러한 발견을 못마땅한 기색으로 받아들일 때마다 끊겼다."], ["I sat all the while, amazed by Mr. Micawber’s disclosure, and wondering what it meant; until Mrs.", "나는 그동안 줄곧, 미코버 씨가 털어놓은 이야기에 어안이 벙벙해, 그것이 대체 무슨 뜻인지 곱씹으며 앉아 있었다."], ["Micawber resumed the thread of the discourse, and claimed my attention.", "그러다 미코버 부인이 다시 이야기의 실마리를 이어 가며 내 주의를 끌었다."]], [["‘What I particularly request Mr. Micawber to be careful of, is,’ said Mrs.", "\"제가 미코버 씨에게 각별히 조심하라고 당부하는 것은요,\""], ["Micawber, ‘that he does not, my dear Mr. Copperfield, in applying himself to this subordinate branch of the law, place it out of his power to rise, ultimately, to the top of the tree.", "미코버 부인이 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드 씨, 그이가 법의 이 하급 부문에 매진하느라, 끝내 나무 꼭대기까지 오를 힘을 스스로 잃어버리는 일이 없도록 하라는 거예요."], ["I am convinced that Mr.", "저는 확신해요."], ["Micawber, giving his mind to a profession so adapted to his fertile resources, and his flow of language, must distinguish himself. Now, for example, Mr.", "미코버 씨가 자신의 풍부한 자질과 거침없는 언변에 그토록 잘 맞는 직업에 마음을 쏟는다면, 틀림없이 두각을 나타내리라는 것을요. 자, 예를 들면요, 트래들스 씨,\""], ["Traddles,’ said Mrs. Micawber, assuming a profound air, ‘a judge, or even say a Chancellor.", "미코버 부인이 자못 심오한 태도를 지으며 말했다. \"판사, 아니 대법관이라도 말이에요."], ["Does an individual place himself beyond the pale of those preferments by entering on such an office as Mr. Micawber has accepted?’", "미코버 씨가 받아들인 그런 직책을 맡는다고 해서, 그 사람이 그러한 영전의 울타리 밖으로 밀려나기라도 하나요?\""]], [["‘My dear,’ observed Mr.", "\"여보,\""], ["Micawber--but glancing inquisitively at Traddles, too; ‘we have time enough before us, for the consideration of those questions.’", "미코버 씨가 한마디 했다 — 그러나 트래들스를 향해 캐묻듯 흘끔거리기도 하면서. \"그런 문제들을 따져 볼 시간은 우리 앞에 얼마든지 있소.\""]], [["‘Micawber,’ she returned, ‘no!", "\"미코버,\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"아니에요!"], ["Your mistake in life is, that you do not look forward far enough.", "당신의 인생에서 잘못된 점은, 멀리까지 충분히 내다보지 않는다는 거예요."], ["You are bound, in justice to your family, if not to yourself, to take in at a comprehensive glance the extremest point in the horizon to which your abilities may lead you.’", "당신은, 당신 자신을 위해서는 아니더라도, 적어도 가족에 대한 도리로서, 당신의 능력이 데려다줄 수 있는 지평선의 그 가장 먼 지점까지 한눈에 담아 보아야 마땅해요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber coughed, and drank his punch with an air of exceeding satisfaction--still glancing at Traddles, as if he desired to have his opinion.", "미코버 씨는 기침을 하더니, 더없이 흡족한 태도로 펀치를 마셨다 — 여전히 트래들스를 흘끔거리면서, 마치 그의 의견을 듣고 싶다는 듯이."]], [["‘Why, the plain state of the case, Mrs. Micawber,’ said Traddles, mildly breaking the truth to her.", "\"저, 사정을 있는 그대로 말씀드리자면 말입니다, 미코버 부인,\" 트래들스가 부드럽게 진실을 그녀에게 일러 주며 말했다."], ["‘I mean the real prosaic fact, you know--’", "\"제 말은, 그 진짜 무미건조한 사실 말입니다만—\""]], [["‘Just so,’ said Mrs.", "\"바로 그거예요,\""], ["Micawber, ‘my dear Mr.", "미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Traddles, I wish to be as prosaic and literal as possible on a subject of so much importance.’", "\"내 사랑하는 트래들스 씨, 이렇게 중요한 문제에 대해서라면 저야말로 되도록 무미건조하고 곧이곧대로이고 싶답니다.\""]], [["‘--Is,’ said Traddles, ‘that this branch of the law, even if Mr.", "\"—그러니까,\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["Micawber were a regular solicitor--’", "\"법의 이 부문은, 설령 미코버 씨가 정식 사무 변호사라 하더라도—\""]], [["‘Exactly so,’ returned Mrs. Micawber.", "\"바로 그래요,\" 미코버 부인이 받았다."], ["(‘Wilkins, you are squinting, and will not be able to get your eyes back.’)", "(\"윌킨스, 당신 사팔뜨기가 되겠어요, 눈이 도로 안 돌아올 거예요.\")"]], [["‘--Has nothing,’ pursued Traddles, ‘to do with that.", "\"—그것과는,\" 트래들스가 말을 이었다."], ["Only a barrister is eligible for such preferments; and Mr. Micawber could not be a barrister, without being entered at an inn of court as a student, for five years.’", "\"아무 상관이 없습니다. 그런 영전에 오를 자격이 있는 건 오직 법정 변호사뿐인데, 미코버 씨는 법학원에 학생으로 등록해 오 년을 채우지 않고서는 법정 변호사가 될 수 없거든요.\""]], [["‘Do I follow you?’ said Mrs.", "\"제가 제대로 따라가고 있나요?\""], ["Micawber, with her most affable air of business.", "미코버 부인이 더없이 상냥한 사무적 태도로 말했다."], ["‘Do I understand, my dear Mr. Traddles, that, at the expiration of that period, Mr. Micawber would be eligible as a Judge or Chancellor?’", "\"그러니까 제가 이해하기로는, 내 사랑하는 트래들스 씨, 그 기간이 끝나면 미코버 씨가 판사나 대법관이 될 자격이 생긴다는 거지요?\""]], [["‘He would be ELIGIBLE,’ returned Traddles, with a strong emphasis on that word.", "\"자격은 생기지요.\" 트래들스가 그 말에 힘주어 강조하며 대답했다."]], [["‘Thank you,’ said Mrs. Micawber.", "\"고마워요,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["‘That is quite sufficient.", "\"그것으로 충분해요."], ["If such is the case, and Mr. Micawber forfeits no privilege by entering on these duties, my anxiety is set at rest.", "사정이 그렇고, 미코버 씨가 이 직무를 맡는다고 해서 아무런 특권도 잃지 않는다면, 제 근심은 가라앉은 셈이에요."], ["I speak,’ said Mrs.", "저야 어쩔 수 없이,\""], ["Micawber, ‘as a female, necessarily; but I have always been of opinion that Mr. Micawber possesses what I have heard my papa call, when I lived at home, the judicial mind; and I hope Mr.", "미코버 부인이 말했다. \"한낱 여자로서 말하는 것입니다만, 저는 늘 미코버 씨가, 제가 친정에서 살 때 아버지가 부르시던 말로 하자면 '법관다운 두뇌'를 지녔다고 여겨 왔답니다."], ["Micawber is now entering on a field where that mind will develop itself, and take a commanding station.’", "그리고 이제 미코버 씨가 그 두뇌를 펼쳐 내고 높은 자리를 차지할 수 있는 마당에 들어서기를 바라요.\""]], [["I quite believe that Mr. Micawber saw himself, in his judicial mind’s eye, on the woolsack.", "나는 미코버 씨가 그 법관다운 두뇌의 눈으로, 자기가 대법관의 양모 방석 위에 앉아 있는 모습을 보고 있다는 걸 똑똑히 믿었다."], ["He passed his hand complacently over his bald head, and said with ostentatious resignation:", "그는 만족스러운 듯 자기 대머리를 쓸어 넘기더니, 짐짓 체념한 척하며 말했다."]], [["‘My dear, we will not anticipate the decrees of fortune.", "\"여보, 우리 운명의 판결을 미리 점치지는 맙시다."], ["If I am reserved to wear a wig, I am at least prepared, externally,’ in allusion to his baldness, ‘for that distinction.", "만일 내가 가발을 쓸 운명이라면, 적어도 겉으로는,\" 자기 대머리를 빗대어, \"그 영예를 맞을 채비가 되어 있는 셈이지. 나는,\""], ["I do not,’ said Mr.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘regret my hair, and I may have been deprived of it for a specific purpose. I cannot say.", "\"내 머리카락을 아쉬워하지 않네. 어쩌면 어떤 특별한 목적이 있어 그것을 잃은 건지도 모르지."], ["It is my intention, my dear Copperfield, to educate my son for the Church; I will not deny that I should be happy, on his account, to attain to eminence.’", "그건 나도 알 수 없네. 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 나는 내 아들을 교회를 위해 키울 작정이네. 그 아이를 생각하면, 내가 높은 자리에 오를 수 있다면 행복하겠다는 것을 굳이 감추지는 않겠네.\""]], [["‘For the Church?’ said I, still pondering, between whiles, on Uriah Heep.", "\"교회를 위해서요?\" 내가, 여전히 틈틈이 유라이아 힙을 곰곰이 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다네,\""], ["Micawber. ‘He has a remarkable head-voice, and will commence as a chorister.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"그 아이는 두성이 빼어나니, 성가대원으로 시작할 걸세."], ["Our residence at Canterbury, and our local connexion, will, no doubt, enable him to take advantage of any vacancy that may arise in the Cathedral corps.’", "우리가 캔터베리에 거처를 두고, 그곳에 연줄이 생기면, 틀림없이 성당 성가대에 자리가 나는 대로 그 기회를 잡을 수 있을 게야.\""]], [["On looking at Master Micawber again, I saw that he had a certain expression of face, as if his voice were behind his eyebrows; where it presently appeared to be, on his singing us (as an alternative between that and bed) ‘The Wood-Pecker tapping’.", "미코버 도령을 다시 보니, 어딘지 자기 목소리가 두 눈썹 뒤에 있기라도 한 듯한 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그리고 그 목소리는 곧, 그가 (노래를 부르든지 잠자리에 들든지 둘 중 하나를 택하라기에) \"딱따구리가 또드락또드락\"을 우리에게 불러 줄 때, 정말로 거기 있는 것처럼 보였다."], ["After many compliments on this performance, we fell into some general conversation; and as I was too full of my desperate intentions to keep my altered circumstances to myself, I made them known to Mr. and Mrs.", "이 노래 솜씨에 숱한 칭찬을 늘어놓은 뒤, 우리는 이런저런 이야기에 빠져들었다. 그리고 나는 필사의 각오를 품은 마음이 너무 벅차서 달라진 내 형편을 혼자만 알고 있을 수가 없어, 미코버 씨 부부에게 그것을 털어놓았다."], ["Micawber. I cannot express how extremely delighted they both were, by the idea of my aunt’s being in difficulties; and how comfortable and friendly it made them.", "고모님이 곤경에 처했다는 생각에 두 사람이 얼마나 더없이 기뻐했는지, 그리고 그것이 두 사람을 얼마나 편안하고 다정하게 만들었는지, 나는 이루 말로 다 할 수가 없다."]], [["When we were nearly come to the last round of the punch, I addressed myself to Traddles, and reminded him that we must not separate, without wishing our friends health, happiness, and success in their new career. I begged Mr.", "펀치가 거의 마지막 잔에 이르렀을 때, 나는 트래들스에게로 몸을 돌려, 우리 벗들의 건강과 행복과 새 출발에서의 성공을 빌어 주지 않고서는 헤어져서는 안 된다고 일러 주었다."], ["Micawber to fill us bumpers, and proposed the toast in due form: shaking hands with him across the table, and kissing Mrs. Micawber, to commemorate that eventful occasion.", "나는 미코버 씨에게 잔을 가득 채워 달라고 청하고, 격식을 갖춰 건배를 제안했다. 탁자 너머로 그와 악수하고, 미코버 부인에게는 입을 맞추어, 그 뜻깊은 자리를 기렸다."], ["Traddles imitated me in the first particular, but did not consider himself a sufficiently old friend to venture on the second.", "트래들스는 첫 번째 것은 나를 따라 했으나, 두 번째 것을 감행할 만큼 오랜 벗은 못 된다고 여겼다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"내 사랑하는 코퍼필드,\""], ["Micawber, rising with one of his thumbs in each of his waistcoat pockets, ‘the companion of my youth: if I may be allowed the expression--and my esteemed friend Traddles: if I may be permitted to call him so--will allow me, on the part of Mrs. Micawber, myself, and our offspring, to thank them in the warmest and most uncompromising terms for their good wishes.", "미코버 씨가 조끼 양쪽 주머니에 엄지손가락을 하나씩 찔러 넣은 채 자리에서 일어나며 말했다. \"내 젊은 날의 벗 — 이런 표현을 써도 좋다면 — 그리고 내가 존경하는 벗 트래들스 — 그를 그렇게 불러도 된다면 — 이 두 사람은, 미코버 부인과 나 자신과 우리 자식들을 대신해, 그들의 따뜻한 소망에 대해 더없이 뜨겁고 한 점 에누리 없는 말로 감사드리는 것을 허락해 주리라 믿네."], ["It may be expected that on the eve of a migration which will consign us to a perfectly new existence,’ Mr.", "우리를 전혀 새로운 삶으로 데려갈 이주를 앞둔 저녁에,\" 미코버 씨는 마치 그들이 오십만 마일이라도 떠나는 양 말했다."], ["Micawber spoke as if they were going five hundred thousand miles, ‘I should offer a few valedictory remarks to two such friends as I see before me.", "\"내 눈앞에 보이는 이런 두 벗에게 작별의 말 몇 마디를 건네는 것이 마땅하다고 기대할 법도 하지."], ["But all that I have to say in this way, I have said.", "하지만 그런 식으로 내가 할 말은, 이미 다 했네."], ["Whatever station in society I may attain, through the medium of the learned profession of which I am about to become an unworthy member, I shall endeavour not to disgrace, and Mrs. Micawber will be safe to adorn.", "내가 이제 그 변변찮은 일원이 되려 하는 그 학식 있는 직업을 통해 사회에서 어떤 지위에 오르든, 나는 그것을 욕되게 하지 않으려 애쓸 것이고, 미코버 부인은 틀림없이 그것을 빛내 줄 걸세."], ["Under the temporary pressure of pecuniary liabilities, contracted with a view to their immediate liquidation, but remaining unliquidated through a combination of circumstances, I have been under the necessity of assuming a garb from which my natural instincts recoil--I allude to spectacles--and possessing myself of a cognomen, to which I can establish no legitimate pretensions.", "즉시 갚을 작정으로 졌으나 여러 사정이 겹쳐 끝내 갚지 못한 금전적 부채의 일시적인 압박 아래, 나는 내 타고난 본성이 움츠러드는 차림새를 어쩔 수 없이 걸쳐야 했네 — 내가 말하는 것은 안경일세 — 그리고 정당하게 내세울 근거라고는 하나도 없는 가명을 갖다 쓰기도 했지."], ["All I have to say on that score is, that the cloud has passed from the dreary scene, and the God of Day is once more high upon the mountain tops.", "그 점에 대해 내가 할 말은 오직 이것뿐이네. 그 음울한 풍경에서 먹구름이 걷히고, 낮의 신께서 다시금 산봉우리 높이 떠오르셨다는 것 말일세."], ["On Monday next, on the arrival of the four o’clock afternoon coach at Canterbury, my foot will be on my native heath--my name, Micawber!’", "다가오는 월요일, 오후 네 시 마차가 캔터베리에 닿으면, 내 발은 내 고향 땅을 딛고 설 것이네 — 내 이름은, 미코버일세!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber resumed his seat on the close of these remarks, and drank two glasses of punch in grave succession.", "미코버 씨는 이 말을 맺으며 다시 자리에 앉더니, 엄숙하게 펀치 두 잔을 잇따라 들이켰다."], ["He then said with much solemnity:", "그러고는 자못 장중하게 말했다."]], [["‘One thing more I have to do, before this separation is complete, and that is to perform an act of justice. My friend Mr.", "\"이 이별이 온전히 마무리되기 전에 한 가지 더 해야 할 일이 있으니, 그것은 정의의 행위를 하나 행하는 것이네."], ["Thomas Traddles has, on two several occasions, “put his name”, if I may use a common expression, to bills of exchange for my accommodation.", "내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨는, 두 번에 걸쳐, 흔한 표현을 빌리자면, 나의 편의를 위해 환어음에 '자기 이름을 올려' 주었네."], ["On the first occasion Mr. Thomas Traddles was left--let me say, in short, in the lurch.", "첫 번째 경우, 토머스 트래들스 씨는 — 짧게 말하자면 — 곤경에 내팽개쳐졌네."], ["The fulfilment of the second has not yet arrived.", "두 번째 것의 만기는 아직 돌아오지 않았고."], ["The amount of the first obligation,’ here Mr.", "첫 번째 채무의 액수는,\""], ["Micawber carefully referred to papers, ‘was, I believe, twenty-three, four, nine and a half, of the second, according to my entry of that transaction, eighteen, six, two.", "여기서 미코버 씨는 서류를 꼼꼼히 들춰 보았다. \"내 기억에 이십삼 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스 반이었고, 두 번째 것은, 그 거래에 대한 내 기록에 따르면 십팔 파운드 육 실링 이 펜스였네."], ["These sums, united, make a total, if my calculation is correct, amounting to forty-one, ten, eleven and a half.", "이 두 액수를 합하면, 내 계산이 맞다면, 도합 사십일 파운드 십 실링 십일 펜스 반이 되네."], ["My friend Copperfield will perhaps do me the favour to check that total?’", "내 벗 코퍼필드가 그 합계를 좀 확인해 주는 친절을 베풀어 주겠나?\""]], [["I did so and found it correct.", "나는 그렇게 했고, 맞다는 것을 알았다."]], [["‘To leave this metropolis,’ said Mr.", "\"이 대도시와,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘and my friend Mr. Thomas Traddles, without acquitting myself of the pecuniary part of this obligation, would weigh upon my mind to an insupportable extent.", "\"내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨를 떠나면서, 이 채무의 금전적인 부분을 스스로 청산하지 않는다면, 그것은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 내 마음을 짓누를 걸세."], ["I have, therefore, prepared for my friend Mr. Thomas Traddles, and I now hold in my hand, a document, which accomplishes the desired object.", "그래서 나는 내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨를 위해, 그 바라던 바를 이루어 줄 증서를 한 장 마련했고, 지금 그것을 손에 들고 있네."], ["I beg to hand to my friend Mr. Thomas Traddles my I.O.U. for forty-one, ten, eleven and a half, and I am happy to recover my moral dignity, and to know that I can once more walk erect before my fellow man!’", "내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨에게 사십일 파운드 십 실링 십일 펜스 반에 대한 내 차용증을 건네는 바이며, 나는 내 도덕적 존엄을 되찾아, 다시 한번 동포 앞에서 허리를 곧추세우고 걸을 수 있게 되었음을 기쁘게 여기네!\""]], [["With this introduction (which greatly affected him), Mr. Micawber placed his I.O.U. in the hands of Traddles, and said he wished him well in every relation of life.", "이 서두(이것이 그를 무척이나 감동시켰다)와 함께, 미코버 씨는 자신의 차용증을 트래들스의 손에 쥐여 주며, 인생의 온갖 일에서 그가 잘되기를 바란다고 했다."], ["I am persuaded, not only that this was quite the same to Mr. Micawber as paying the money, but that Traddles himself hardly knew the difference until he had had time to think about it. Mr.", "나는 이것이 미코버 씨에게는 돈을 갚은 것과 조금도 다를 바 없을 뿐 아니라, 트래들스 자신도 곰곰이 생각해 볼 틈이 생기기 전까지는 그 차이를 거의 알아채지 못했으리라고 확신한다."], ["Micawber walked so erect before his fellow man, on the strength of this virtuous action, that his chest looked half as broad again when he lighted us downstairs.", "미코버 씨는 이 갸륵한 행위의 힘을 빌려 동포 앞에서 어찌나 허리를 곧추세우고 걸었던지, 우리에게 불을 비추며 아래층으로 안내할 때는 그의 가슴팍이 절반은 더 떡 벌어져 보일 지경이었다."], ["We parted with great heartiness on both sides; and when I had seen Traddles to his own door, and was going home alone, I thought, among the other odd and contradictory things I mused upon, that, slippery as Mr.", "우리는 양쪽 다 더없이 진심 어린 마음으로 작별했다. 그리고 트래들스를 그의 집 문 앞까지 바래다주고 홀로 집으로 가면서, 나는 골똘히 생각하던 다른 야릇하고 모순된 일들 가운데, 이런 생각이 들었다."], ["Micawber was, I was probably indebted to some compassionate recollection he retained of me as his boy-lodger, for never having been asked by him for money.", "미코버 씨가 그토록 미끄러운 사람이긴 해도, 그가 나에게 단 한 번도 돈을 빌려 달라고 하지 않은 것은, 아마도 나를 그의 어린 하숙생으로 기억하며 품어 온 어떤 가엾이 여기는 마음 덕분이었으리라고."], ["I certainly should not have had the moral courage to refuse it; and I have no doubt he knew that (to his credit be it written), quite as well as I did.", "나라면 그것을 거절할 도덕적 용기가 분명 없었을 것이다. 그리고 그도 그것을 (그의 명예를 위해 적어 두건대) 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었으리라는 것을, 나는 조금도 의심하지 않는다."]], [["# CHAPTER 37. A LITTLE COLD WATER", "# CHAPTER 37. A LITTLE COLD WATER"]], [["My new life had lasted for more than a week, and I was stronger than ever in those tremendous practical resolutions that I felt the crisis required.", "내 새로운 삶이 시작된 지 일주일이 넘었고, 지금 이 위기가 요구한다고 느낀 그 엄청난 실천적 결심들에 있어서 나는 어느 때보다 단단했다."], ["I continued to walk extremely fast, and to have a general idea that I was getting on. I made it a rule to take as much out of myself as I possibly could, in my way of doing everything to which I applied my energies.", "나는 여전히 무서운 속도로 걸어 다녔고, 내가 앞으로 나아가고 있다는 막연한 느낌을 품고 있었다. 무엇이든 내 정력을 쏟아붓는 일이라면, 나 자신을 최대한 쥐어짜 내는 것을 하나의 규칙으로 삼았다."], ["I made a perfect victim of myself.", "나는 나 자신을 완벽한 제물로 바쳤다."], ["I even entertained some idea of putting myself on a vegetable diet, vaguely conceiving that, in becoming a graminivorous animal, I should sacrifice to Dora.", "심지어 채식만 하겠다는 생각까지 잠깐 품었는데, 풀만 먹는 짐승이 되면 도라를 위해 나를 희생하는 셈이 되리라는 막연한 상상에서였다."]], [["As yet, little Dora was quite unconscious of my desperate firmness, otherwise than as my letters darkly shadowed it forth.", "지금까지 어린 도라는 내 굳건한 결의를 거의 알아채지 못했다. 내 편지가 그것을 어렴풋이 그림자처럼 비춰 보인 것 말고는 말이다."], ["But another Saturday came, and on that Saturday evening she was to be at Miss Mills’s; and when Mr.", "그러나 또 한 번 토요일이 돌아왔고, 그 토요일 저녁에 도라는 밀스 양 집에 와 있기로 되어 있었다."], ["Mills had gone to his whist-club (telegraphed to me in the street, by a bird-cage in the drawing-room middle window), I was to go there to tea.", "그리고 밀스 씨가 자기 휘스트 클럽에 가고 나면 (응접실 가운데 창문에 내건 새장으로 길거리에 있는 나에게 신호를 보내기로 했다) 나는 그 집에 차를 마시러 가기로 했다."]], [["By this time, we were quite settled down in Buckingham Street, where Mr. Dick continued his copying in a state of absolute felicity.", "이 무렵 우리는 버킹엄 거리에 완전히 자리를 잡았고, 그곳에서 딕 씨는 더없는 행복에 젖어 필사 일을 이어 갔다."], ["My aunt had obtained a signal victory over Mrs. Crupp, by paying her off, throwing the first pitcher she planted on the stairs out of window, and protecting in person, up and down the staircase, a supernumerary whom she engaged from the outer world.", "고모님은 크럽 부인을 상대로 눈부신 승리를 거두었는데, 밀린 삯을 깨끗이 치러 내보내고, 그녀가 계단에 떡하니 갖다 놓은 첫 번째 물병을 창밖으로 내던지고, 바깥세상에서 새로 고용한 임시 일꾼을 몸소 계단 위아래로 따라다니며 호위한 덕분이었다."], ["These vigorous measures struck such terror to the breast of Mrs. Crupp, that she subsided into her own kitchen, under the impression that my aunt was mad.", "이 강경한 조치들은 크럽 부인의 가슴에 어찌나 큰 공포를 심어 주었던지, 그녀는 고모님이 미쳤다고 단정한 채 제 부엌으로 슬그머니 물러나 버렸다."], ["My aunt being supremely indifferent to Mrs. Crupp’s opinion and everybody else’s, and rather favouring than discouraging the idea, Mrs. Crupp, of late the bold, became within a few days so faint-hearted, that rather than encounter my aunt upon the staircase, she would endeavour to hide her portly form behind doors--leaving visible, however, a wide margin of flannel petticoat--or would shrink into dark corners.", "고모님은 크럽 부인을 비롯한 그 누구의 의견에도 더없이 무관심했고, 그런 인상을 말리기는커녕 오히려 부추기는 쪽이었던 터라, 한때 그토록 대담하던 크럽 부인은 며칠 사이 어찌나 겁쟁이가 되었던지, 계단에서 고모님과 마주치느니 차라리 문 뒤에 제 육중한 몸을 숨기려고 애쓰거나 — 그러면서도 플란넬 속치마 자락이 넓게 비어져 나오는 건 어쩌지 못했다 — 어두운 구석으로 움츠러들곤 했다."], ["This gave my aunt such unspeakable satisfaction, that I believe she took a delight in prowling up and down, with her bonnet insanely perched on the top of her head, at times when Mrs. Crupp was likely to be in the way.", "이것이 고모님에게 어찌나 말할 수 없는 만족을 주었던지, 나는 고모님이 크럽 부인이 어슬렁댈 만한 시간이면 보닛을 머리 꼭대기에 미친 듯이 얹은 채 계단을 오르내리며 어슬렁대는 것을 즐겼다고 믿는다."]], [["My aunt, being uncommonly neat and ingenious, made so many little improvements in our domestic arrangements, that I seemed to be richer instead of poorer.", "고모님은 유난히 깔끔하고 손재주가 좋아서, 우리 집 살림살이에 자질구레한 개선을 어찌나 많이 해 놓았던지, 나는 형편이 더 가난해지기는커녕 오히려 더 부유해진 듯한 느낌이 들 정도였다."], ["Among the rest, she converted the pantry into a dressing-room for me; and purchased and embellished a bedstead for my occupation, which looked as like a bookcase in the daytime as a bedstead could.", "그중에서도 고모님은 식료품 저장실을 내 옷방으로 바꿔 놓았고, 내가 쓸 침대를 하나 사다가 꾸며 주었는데, 낮에는 침대가 책장처럼 보일 수 있는 한도 내에서 영락없는 책장으로 보였다."], ["I was the object of her constant solicitude; and my poor mother herself could not have loved me better, or studied more how to make me happy.", "나는 고모님이 끊임없이 마음 쓰는 대상이었다. 가엾은 어머니라 할지라도 나를 그보다 더 사랑하거나, 나를 행복하게 해 주려고 그보다 더 골몰하지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["Peggotty had considered herself highly privileged in being allowed to participate in these labours; and, although she still retained something of her old sentiment of awe in reference to my aunt, had received so many marks of encouragement and confidence, that they were the best friends possible.", "패거티는 이 일들에 한몫 끼도록 허락받은 것을 큰 특권으로 여겼다. 그리고 고모님에 대한 옛날의 경외심 같은 것을 아직 얼마쯤 간직하고 있긴 했어도, 격려와 신뢰의 표시를 어찌나 많이 받았던지, 둘은 더없이 좋은 친구 사이가 되었다."], ["But the time had now come (I am speaking of the Saturday when I was to take tea at Miss Mills’s) when it was necessary for her to return home, and enter on the discharge of the duties she had undertaken in behalf of Ham.", "그러나 이제 (밀스 양 집에 차를 마시러 가기로 한 그 토요일 이야기다) 패거티가 고향으로 돌아가, 햄을 위해 떠맡은 의무를 이행하기 시작해야 할 때가 왔다. \"그럼 잘 가요, 바키스.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘So good-bye, Barkis,’ said my aunt, ‘and take care of yourself! I am sure I never thought I could be sorry to lose you!’", "\"몸조심하고! 내가 당신을 잃는 게 섭섭하리라곤 정말이지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없는데 말이야!\""]], [["I took Peggotty to the coach office and saw her off.", "나는 패거티를 마차 정거장까지 데려다주고 배웅했다."], ["She cried at parting, and confided her brother to my friendship as Ham had done.", "그녀는 헤어지면서 울었고, 햄이 그랬던 것처럼 제 오빠를 내 우정에 부탁했다."], ["We had heard nothing of him since he went away, that sunny afternoon.", "그가 그 화창한 오후에 떠난 뒤로 우리는 그에 대해 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 터였다."]], [["‘And now, my own dear Davy,’ said Peggotty, ‘if, while you’re a prentice, you should want any money to spend; or if, when you’re out of your time, my dear, you should want any to set you up (and you must do one or other, or both, my darling); who has such a good right to ask leave to lend it you, as my sweet girl’s own old stupid me!’", "\"그리고 이제, 우리 사랑하는 데이비.\" 패거티가 말했다. \"도련님이 견습생으로 있는 동안 쓸 돈이 좀 필요하거나, 아니면 견습이 끝나고 나서 자리를 잡는 데 밑천이 좀 필요하거든 (도련님은 둘 중 하나는, 아니 어쩌면 둘 다 해야 할 거예요, 우리 도련님), 그 돈을 빌려 드리게 해 달라고 부탁할 권리가, 우리 예쁜 아씨가 키운 늙고 어리석은 이 몸만큼 떳떳한 사람이 또 어디 있겠어요!\""]], [["I was not so savagely independent as to say anything in reply, but that if ever I borrowed money of anyone, I would borrow it of her.", "나는 누구에게든 돈을 빌리게 된다면 그건 그녀에게서 빌리겠노라고 대답하는 것 말고는, 야박하게 자존심을 세울 만큼 모질지 못했다."], ["Next to accepting a large sum on the spot, I believe this gave Peggotty more comfort than anything I could have done.", "그 자리에서 큰돈을 덥석 받는 것 다음으로, 이 대답이 내가 해 줄 수 있었던 그 무엇보다도 패거티에게 큰 위안이 되었다고 나는 믿는다."]], [["‘And, my dear!’ whispered Peggotty, ‘tell the pretty little angel that I should so have liked to see her, only for a minute!", "\"그리고, 도련님!\" 패거티가 속삭였다. \"그 예쁜 꼬마 천사한테 전해 줘요, 단 일 분만이라도 그분을 정말 너무너무 보고 싶었다고요!"], ["And tell her that before she marries my boy, I’ll come and make your house so beautiful for you, if you’ll let me!’", "그리고 그분이 우리 도련님과 결혼하기 전에, 도련님이 허락만 해 주신다면, 내가 가서 도련님 집을 아주 아름답게 꾸며 드리겠다고요!\""]], [["I declared that nobody else should touch it; and this gave Peggotty such delight that she went away in good spirits.", "나는 다른 누구의 손도 그 집에 대지 못하게 하겠노라고 단언했다. 이것이 패거티에게 어찌나 큰 기쁨을 주었던지, 그녀는 기분 좋게 떠났다."]], [["I fatigued myself as much as I possibly could in the Commons all day, by a variety of devices, and at the appointed time in the evening repaired to Mr.", "나는 종일 민사 법원에서 갖가지 방법을 동원해 최대한 나 자신을 녹초로 만들었고, 저녁의 약속 시간에 맞춰 밀스 씨네 거리로 향했다."], ["Mills’s street. Mr. Mills, who was a terrible fellow to fall asleep after dinner, had not yet gone out, and there was no bird-cage in the middle window.", "저녁을 먹고 나면 곯아떨어지기로 악명 높은 밀스 씨는 아직 나가지 않은 상태였고, 가운데 창문에는 새장이 걸려 있지 않았다."]], [["He kept me waiting so long, that I fervently hoped the Club would fine him for being late.", "밀스 씨가 어찌나 오래 나를 기다리게 했던지, 나는 클럽이 그에게 지각 벌금을 물리기를 간절히 빌었다."], ["At last he came out; and then I saw my own Dora hang up the bird-cage, and peep into the balcony to look for me, and run in again when she saw I was there, while Jip remained behind, to bark injuriously at an immense butcher’s dog in the street, who could have taken him like a pill.", "마침내 그가 나왔다. 그러자 나의 도라가 새장을 거는 것이 보였고, 발코니로 고개를 내밀어 나를 찾다가, 내가 거기 있는 것을 보고는 다시 안으로 뛰어 들어가는 것이 보였다. 그동안 집은 뒤에 남아, 길거리의 거대한 푸줏간 개를 향해 사납게 짖어 댔는데, 그 개는 집을 알약 삼키듯 한입에 꿀꺽할 수도 있었을 것이다."]], [["Dora came to the drawing-room door to meet me; and Jip came scrambling out, tumbling over his own growls, under the impression that I was a Bandit; and we all three went in, as happy and loving as could be.", "도라가 나를 맞으러 응접실 문으로 나왔다. 집은 제 으르렁거리는 소리에 제풀에 걸려 넘어지며 허둥지둥 기어 나왔는데, 나를 산적쯤으로 여기는 모양이었다. 그렇게 우리 셋은 더없이 행복하고 다정하게 안으로 들어갔다."], ["I soon carried desolation into the bosom of our joys--not that I meant to do it, but that I was so full of the subject--by asking Dora, without the smallest preparation, if she could love a beggar?", "그러나 나는 곧 우리의 기쁨 한가운데에 황폐함을 들이고 말았다 — 일부러 그런 것은 아니고, 그 문제로 머릿속이 가득 차 있었던 탓이다 — 아무런 사전 준비도 없이, 거지를 사랑할 수 있겠느냐고 도라에게 물어버린 것이다."]], [["My pretty, little, startled Dora!", "내 예쁘고 작고 깜짝 놀란 도라!"], ["Her only association with the word was a yellow face and a nightcap, or a pair of crutches, or a wooden leg, or a dog with a decanter-stand in his mouth, or something of that kind; and she stared at me with the most delightful wonder.", "그녀가 그 단어를 두고 떠올릴 수 있는 거라곤 누런 얼굴에 잠자리 모자를 쓴 사람, 아니면 한 쌍의 목발, 나무 의족, 혹은 술병 받침대를 입에 문 개, 그런 것들뿐이었다. 그래서 그녀는 더없이 사랑스러운 어리둥절함으로 나를 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["‘How can you ask me anything so foolish?’ pouted Dora.", "\"어떻게 그런 바보 같은 걸 물어볼 수 있어요?\" 도라가 입을 삐죽였다."], ["‘Love a beggar!’", "\"거지를 사랑하라니!\""]], [["‘Dora, my own dearest!’ said I.", "\"도라, 내 가장 사랑하는 사람!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I am a beggar!’", "\"내가 거지요!\""]], [["‘How can you be such a silly thing,’ replied Dora, slapping my hand, ‘as to sit there, telling such stories?", "\"어떻게 그렇게 어리석을 수가 있어요.\" 도라가 내 손을 찰싹 때리며 대꾸했다. \"거기 앉아서 그런 거짓말이나 하고 있다니."], ["I’ll make Jip bite you!’", "집을 시켜서 물어 버리게 할 거예요!\""]], [["Her childish way was the most delicious way in the world to me, but it was necessary to be explicit, and I solemnly repeated:", "그녀의 그 어린애 같은 태도는 내게 세상에서 가장 달콤한 것이었지만, 나는 분명하게 말할 필요가 있었기에, 엄숙하게 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Dora, my own life, I am your ruined David!’", "\"도라, 내 생명, 나는 파산한 당신의 데이비드요!\""]], [["‘I declare I’ll make Jip bite you!’ said Dora, shaking her curls, ‘if you are so ridiculous.’", "\"정말 집을 시켜서 물어 버리게 할 거예요!\" 도라가 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"그렇게 우스꽝스럽게 굴면요.\""]], [["But I looked so serious, that Dora left off shaking her curls, and laid her trembling little hand upon my shoulder, and first looked scared and anxious, then began to cry.", "그러나 내가 어찌나 심각한 표정을 짓고 있었던지, 도라는 곱슬머리 흔들기를 멈추고, 떨리는 작은 손을 내 어깨에 얹더니, 처음에는 겁먹은 듯 불안한 표정을 짓다가, 이내 울기 시작했다."], ["That was dreadful.", "그것은 끔찍했다."], ["I fell upon my knees before the sofa, caressing her, and imploring her not to rend my heart; but, for some time, poor little Dora did nothing but exclaim Oh dear!", "나는 소파 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀를 어루만지며, 제발 내 가슴을 갈가리 찢지 말아 달라고 애원했다. 그러나 한동안 가엾고 작은 도라는 그저 아이고, 어머나! 아이고, 어머나!"], ["Oh dear! And oh, she was so frightened!", "하고 외치기만 했다. 게다가, 아, 그녀는 너무도 겁에 질려 있었다!"], ["And where was Julia Mills!", "그리고 줄리아 밀스는 어디 있느냐고!"], ["And oh, take her to Julia Mills, and go away, please!", "아, 제발 자기를 줄리아 밀스에게 데려다주고 가 버리라고!"], ["until I was almost beside myself.", "그렇게 외쳐 대는 통에 나는 거의 제정신이 아니었다."]], [["At last, after an agony of supplication and protestation, I got Dora to look at me, with a horrified expression of face, which I gradually soothed until it was only loving, and her soft, pretty cheek was lying against mine. Then I told her, with my arms clasped round her, how I loved her, so dearly, and so dearly; how I felt it right to offer to release her from her engagement, because now I was poor; how I never could bear it, or recover it, if I lost her; how I had no fears of poverty, if she had none, my arm being nerved and my heart inspired by her; how I was already working with a courage such as none but lovers knew; how I had begun to be practical, and look into the future; how a crust well earned was sweeter far than a feast inherited; and much more to the same purpose, which I delivered in a burst of passionate eloquence quite surprising to myself, though I had been thinking about it, day and night, ever since my aunt had astonished me.", "마침내, 간청과 항변의 고통스러운 몸부림 끝에, 나는 도라가 공포에 질린 얼굴로 나를 바라보게 만들었고, 그 표정을 차츰차츰 달래어 마침내 사랑스러운 빛만 남게 했으며, 그녀의 부드럽고 예쁜 뺨이 내 뺨에 기대게 했다. 그러고 나서 나는 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안은 채 그녀에게 말했다. 내가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 너무도 깊이, 너무도 깊이 사랑하는지를. 이제 내가 가난해졌으니 약혼에서 그녀를 풀어 주겠다고 제안하는 것이 옳다고 느꼈음을. 만약 그녀를 잃는다면 나는 결코 그것을 견디거나 회복할 수 없으리라는 것을. 그녀만 두렵지 않다면 나도 가난이 조금도 두렵지 않다는 것을, 그녀가 내 팔에 힘을 주고 내 가슴을 북돋아 주니까. 내가 이미 연인들만이 아는 그런 용기를 가지고 일하고 있다는 것을. 내가 실천적인 사람이 되어 미래를 내다보기 시작했다는 것을. 정직하게 벌어들인 빵 껍질 한 조각이 물려받은 잔칫상보다 훨씬 더 달콤하다는 것을. 그리고 그 밖에도 같은 취지의 많은 말을, 나 자신도 꽤나 놀랄 만큼 격정적인 웅변을 쏟아내며 전했는데, 사실 고모님이 나를 깜짝 놀라게 한 이래로 밤낮없이 그 일을 생각해 온 터였다."]], [["‘Is your heart mine still, dear Dora?’ said I, rapturously, for I knew by her clinging to me that it was.", "\"당신 마음은 아직도 내 것이오, 사랑하는 도라?\" 내가 황홀하게 말했다. 그녀가 내게 매달리는 것으로 그렇다는 걸 알았기 때문이다."]], [["‘Oh, yes!’ cried Dora.", "\"오, 그럼요!\" 도라가 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh, yes, it’s all yours.", "\"오, 그럼요, 전부 당신 거예요."], ["Oh, don’t be dreadful!’", "오, 제발 끔찍하게 굴지 말아요!\""]], [["I dreadful! To Dora!", "내가 끔찍하다니! 도라에게!"]], [["‘Don’t talk about being poor, and working hard!’ said Dora, nestling closer to me.", "\"가난하다느니, 힘들게 일한다느니, 그런 얘기는 하지 말아요!\" 도라가 내게 더 바싹 파고들며 말했다."], ["‘Oh, don’t, don’t!’", "\"오, 제발, 제발!\""]], [["‘My dearest love,’ said I, ‘the crust well-earned--’", "\"내 가장 사랑하는 사람.\" 내가 말했다. \"정직하게 벌어들인 빵 껍질은—\""]], [["‘Oh, yes; but I don’t want to hear any more about crusts!’ said Dora.", "\"오, 그래요. 하지만 빵 껍질 얘기는 더 듣고 싶지 않아요!\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘And Jip must have a mutton-chop every day at twelve, or he’ll die.’", "\"그리고 집은 매일 열두 시에 양고기 갈비를 먹어야 해요, 안 그러면 죽어 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["I was charmed with her childish, winning way.", "나는 그녀의 어린애 같고 사랑스러운 태도에 마음을 빼앗겼다."], ["I fondly explained to Dora that Jip should have his mutton-chop with his accustomed regularity.", "나는 집이 늘 하던 대로 어김없이 양고기 갈비를 먹게 될 거라고 도라에게 다정하게 설명해 주었다."], ["I drew a picture of our frugal home, made independent by my labour--sketching in the little house I had seen at Highgate, and my aunt in her room upstairs.", "나는 내 노동으로 자립한 우리의 검소한 보금자리를 그림처럼 그려 보였다 — 하이게이트에서 본 그 작은 집을, 그리고 위층 방에 있는 고모님을 스케치하면서."]], [["‘I am not dreadful now, Dora?’ said I, tenderly.", "\"이제 나 끔찍하지 않지요, 도라?\" 내가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, no, no!’ cried Dora.", "\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요!\""], ["‘But I hope your aunt will keep in her own room a good deal.", "도라가 외쳤다. \"하지만 당신 고모님은 자기 방에 많이 계셨으면 좋겠어요."], ["And I hope she’s not a scolding old thing!’", "그리고 잔소리하는 늙은이가 아니었으면 좋겠고요!\""]], [["If it were possible for me to love Dora more than ever, I am sure I did.", "내가 도라를 이전보다 더 사랑하는 것이 가능한 일이라면, 분명 나는 그러했다."], ["But I felt she was a little impracticable.", "그러나 나는 그녀가 좀 비현실적이라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["It damped my new-born ardour, to find that ardour so difficult of communication to her.", "갓 피어난 내 열정을 그녀에게 전하는 것이 그토록 어렵다는 사실은 그 열정에 찬물을 끼얹었다."], ["I made another trial. When she was quite herself again, and was curling Jip’s ears, as he lay upon her lap, I became grave, and said:", "나는 한 번 더 시도해 보았다. 그녀가 완전히 제정신을 되찾고, 제 무릎 위에 누운 집의 귀를 돌돌 말고 있을 때, 나는 진지해져서 말했다."]], [["‘My own!", "\"내 사람!"], ["May I mention something?’", "내가 뭔가 한마디 해도 될까요?\""]], [["‘Oh, please don’t be practical!’ said Dora, coaxingly.", "\"오, 제발 실천적으로 굴지 말아요!\" 도라가 어르듯 말했다."], ["‘Because it frightens me so!’", "\"그건 나를 너무 무섭게 하니까요!\""]], [["‘Sweetheart!’ I returned; ‘there is nothing to alarm you in all this.", "\"내 사랑!\" 내가 대꾸했다. \"이 모든 일에 당신을 놀라게 할 건 하나도 없어요."], ["I want you to think of it quite differently.", "나는 당신이 이걸 아주 다르게 생각해 주었으면 해요."], ["I want to make it nerve you, and inspire you, Dora!’", "나는 이것이 당신에게 힘을 주고, 당신을 북돋아 주기를 바라요, 도라!\""]], [["‘Oh, but that’s so shocking!’ cried Dora.", "\"오, 하지만 그건 너무 충격적이에요!\" 도라가 외쳤다."]], [["‘My love, no.", "\"내 사랑, 천만에요."], ["Perseverance and strength of character will enable us to bear much worse things.’ ‘But I haven’t got any strength at all,’ said Dora, shaking her curls.", "끈기와 굳건한 인격이 있으면 우리는 이보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 일도 견딜 수 있어요.\" \"하지만 난 굳건함이라곤 조금도 없는걸요.\" 도라가 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘Have I, Jip?", "\"안 그래, 집?"], ["Oh, do kiss Jip, and be agreeable!’", "오, 어서 집에게 입 맞추고, 좀 상냥하게 굴어요!\""]], [["It was impossible to resist kissing Jip, when she held him up to me for that purpose, putting her own bright, rosy little mouth into kissing form, as she directed the operation, which she insisted should be performed symmetrically, on the centre of his nose.", "그녀가 입맞춤을 받게 하려고 집을 내게 들어 올리고는, 자기 입으로 그 동작을 지휘하면서 제 밝고 발그레한 작은 입을 입맞춤 모양으로 오므렸을 때, 나는 집에게 입 맞추지 않을 도리가 없었다. 그녀는 그 입맞춤이 반드시 집의 코 한가운데에 좌우 대칭으로 행해져야 한다고 우겼다."], ["I did as she bade me--rewarding myself afterwards for my obedience--and she charmed me out of my graver character for I don’t know how long.", "나는 그녀가 시키는 대로 했고 — 그러고 나서는 그 순종에 대해 나 자신에게 보상을 내렸다 — 그녀는 얼마나 오랫동안인지 모를 시간 동안 나를 그 진지한 태도에서 홀려 끌어냈다."]], [["‘But, Dora, my beloved!’ said I, at last resuming it; ‘I was going to mention something.’", "\"하지만, 도라, 내 사랑하는 사람!\" 마침내 나는 다시 그 태도를 되찾으며 말했다. \"내가 뭔가 한마디 하려던 참이었어요.\""]], [["The judge of the Prerogative Court might have fallen in love with her, to see her fold her little hands and hold them up, begging and praying me not to be dreadful any more.", "작은 두 손을 모아 들어 올린 채 제발 더는 끔찍하게 굴지 말아 달라고 빌고 애원하는 그녀의 모습을 보았다면, 유산 검인 법원의 판사라도 그녀에게 반해 버렸을 것이다."]], [["‘Indeed I am not going to be, my darling!’ I assured her.", "\"정말이지 그러지 않을게요, 내 사랑!\" 나는 그녀를 안심시켰다."], ["‘But, Dora, my love, if you will sometimes think,--not despondingly, you know; far from that!--but if you will sometimes think--just to encourage yourself--that you are engaged to a poor man--’", "\"하지만, 도라, 내 사랑, 당신이 가끔씩만이라도 생각해 준다면 — 낙담해서 그러란 건 아니에요. 결코 그런 게 아니라! — 그저 가끔씩만 생각해 준다면 — 그냥 스스로 마음을 다잡기 위해서라도 — 당신이 가난한 남자와 약혼한 사이라는 걸—\""]], [["‘Don’t, don’t!", "\"하지 말아요, 하지 말아요!"], ["Pray don’t!’ cried Dora.", "제발 하지 말아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다."], ["‘It’s so very dreadful!’", "\"그건 너무너무 끔찍해요!\""]], [["‘My soul, not at all!’ said I, cheerfully.", "\"내 영혼, 전혀 그렇지 않아요!\""], ["‘If you will sometimes think of that, and look about now and then at your papa’s housekeeping, and endeavour to acquire a little habit--of accounts, for instance--’", "내가 명랑하게 말했다. \"당신이 가끔씩 그걸 생각하고, 이따금 당신 아버지의 살림살이를 한번 둘러보고, 작은 습관을 — 이를테면 가계부 적는 습관 같은 걸 — 좀 익히려고 애써 준다면—\""]], [["Poor little Dora received this suggestion with something that was half a sob and half a scream.", "가엾고 작은 도라는 이 제안을, 흐느낌인지 비명인지 모를 소리를 반쯤 섞어 받아들였다."]], [["‘--It would be so useful to us afterwards,’ I went on.", "\"—그게 나중에 우리한테 아주 쓸모가 있을 거예요.\" 나는 말을 이어 갔다."], ["‘And if you would promise me to read a little--a little Cookery Book that I would send you, it would be so excellent for both of us.", "\"그리고 만약 당신이 약속해 준다면, 책을 조금 — 내가 보내 줄 작은 요리책을 조금 읽어 주겠다고 약속해 준다면, 그건 우리 둘 모두에게 아주 훌륭한 일이 될 거예요."], ["For our path in life, my Dora,’ said I, warming with the subject, ‘is stony and rugged now, and it rests with us to smooth it.", "우리가 살아갈 인생길은, 도라.\" 나는 그 주제에 점점 열을 올리며 말했다. \"지금은 돌투성이에 험하지만, 그걸 평평하게 다지는 건 우리에게 달려 있어요."], ["We must fight our way onward.", "우린 앞으로 싸우며 나아가야 해요."], ["We must be brave.", "우린 용감해야 해요."], ["There are obstacles to be met, and we must meet, and crush them!’", "만나야 할 장애물들이 있고, 우린 그것들과 맞서서, 짓밟아 부숴 버려야 해요!\""]], [["I was going on at a great rate, with a clenched hand, and a most enthusiastic countenance; but it was quite unnecessary to proceed.", "나는 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 더없이 열정에 찬 얼굴로 굉장한 기세로 말을 이어 가고 있었다. 그러나 더 나아갈 필요는 전혀 없었다. 나는 이미 충분히 말한 것이다."], ["I had said enough. I had done it again.", "또 그 짓을 저지르고 만 것이다."], ["Oh, she was so frightened!", "아, 그녀는 너무도 겁에 질렸다!"], ["Oh, where was Julia Mills!", "아, 줄리아 밀스는 어디 있느냐고!"], ["Oh, take her to Julia Mills, and go away, please!", "아, 제발 자기를 줄리아 밀스에게 데려다주고 가 버리라고!"], ["So that, in short, I was quite distracted, and raved about the drawing-room.", "그리하여, 요컨대 나는 완전히 정신이 나가, 응접실을 미친 듯이 헤매며 소리쳤다."]], [["I thought I had killed her, this time.", "이번엔 내가 그녀를 죽여 버린 줄로만 알았다."], ["I sprinkled water on her face.", "나는 그녀의 얼굴에 물을 뿌렸다."], ["I went down on my knees. I plucked at my hair.", "나는 무릎을 꿇었다. 나는 내 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었다."], ["I denounced myself as a remorseless brute and a ruthless beast.", "나는 나 자신을 무자비한 짐승이요 잔인한 야수라고 비난했다."], ["I implored her forgiveness.", "나는 그녀에게 용서를 빌었다."], ["I besought her to look up.", "나는 제발 눈을 떠 달라고 간청했다."], ["I ravaged Miss Mills’s work-box for a smelling-bottle, and in my agony of mind applied an ivory needle-case instead, and dropped all the needles over Dora.", "나는 냄새 맡는 약병을 찾으려고 밀스 양의 반짇고리를 헤집었는데, 마음의 고통에 사로잡힌 나머지 그 대신 상아 바늘통을 집어 들어, 도라에게 온통 바늘을 쏟아 버렸다."], ["I shook my fists at Jip, who was as frantic as myself.", "나는 나만큼이나 광란에 빠진 집을 향해 두 주먹을 흔들어 댔다."], ["I did every wild extravagance that could be done, and was a long way beyond the end of my wits when Miss Mills came into the room.", "나는 할 수 있는 온갖 미친 짓을 다 했고, 밀스 양이 방으로 들어왔을 때 나는 이미 정신줄을 놓은 지 한참이었다."]], [["‘Who has done this?’ exclaimed Miss Mills, succouring her friend.", "\"이게 누구 짓이에요?\" 밀스 양이 친구를 부축하며 외쳤다."]], [["I replied, ‘I, Miss Mills!", "나는 대답했다. \"나요, 밀스 양!"], ["I have done it!", "내가 한 짓이오!"], ["Behold the destroyer!’--or words to that effect--and hid my face from the light, in the sofa cushion.", "이 파괴자를 보시오!\" — 뭐 그런 취지의 말을 하고는 — 소파 쿠션에 얼굴을 파묻어 빛으로부터 숨겼다."]], [["At first Miss Mills thought it was a quarrel, and that we were verging on the Desert of Sahara; but she soon found out how matters stood, for my dear affectionate little Dora, embracing her, began exclaiming that I was ‘a poor labourer’; and then cried for me, and embraced me, and asked me would I let her give me all her money to keep, and then fell on Miss Mills’s neck, sobbing as if her tender heart were broken.", "처음에 밀스 양은 그것이 다툼이라고, 우리가 사하라 사막의 가장자리에 다다랐다고 생각했다. 그러나 그녀는 곧 사정이 어찌 된 것인지 알아차렸는데, 나의 사랑스럽고 다정한 작은 도라가 그녀를 끌어안고서 내가 \"가난한 노동자\"라고 외쳐 대기 시작했기 때문이다. 그러고는 나를 위해 울고, 나를 끌어안고, 자기 돈을 전부 내게 맡아 달라고 주면 안 되겠느냐고 묻더니, 이내 밀스 양의 목에 매달려, 그 여린 가슴이 무너져 내리기라도 한 듯 흐느꼈다."]], [["Miss Mills must have been born to be a blessing to us.", "밀스 양은 분명 우리에게 축복이 되려고 태어난 사람이었다."], ["She ascertained from me in a few words what it was all about, comforted Dora, and gradually convinced her that I was not a labourer--from my manner of stating the case I believe Dora concluded that I was a navigator, and went balancing myself up and down a plank all day with a wheelbarrow--and so brought us together in peace.", "그녀는 몇 마디로 내게서 무슨 일인지를 알아내고, 도라를 달래고, 차츰차츰 그녀에게 내가 노동자가 아니라는 것을 납득시켰다 — 내가 사정을 설명한 방식 때문에 도라는 내가 뱃사람이 되어, 외바퀴 손수레를 밀며 종일 널빤지 위에서 몸을 휘청휘청 가누고 있는 거라고 결론지은 모양이었다 — 그렇게 우리를 화목하게 다시 이어 주었다."], ["When we were quite composed, and Dora had gone up-stairs to put some rose-water to her eyes, Miss Mills rang for tea.", "우리가 완전히 진정되고, 도라가 두 눈에 장미수를 좀 바르려고 위층으로 올라가자, 밀스 양이 차를 가져오라고 벨을 울렸다."], ["In the ensuing interval, I told Miss Mills that she was evermore my friend, and that my heart must cease to vibrate ere I could forget her sympathy.", "뒤이은 그 짬에, 나는 밀스 양에게 그녀가 영원토록 나의 벗이며, 내 심장이 고동을 멈추기 전에는 그녀의 동정심을 결코 잊지 못할 것이라고 말했다."]], [["I then expounded to Miss Mills what I had endeavoured, so very unsuccessfully, to expound to Dora.", "그러고 나서 나는 도라에게 그토록 처참하게 실패한 채로 설명하려 애썼던 것을 밀스 양에게 풀어놓았다."], ["Miss Mills replied, on general principles, that the Cottage of content was better than the Palace of cold splendour, and that where love was, all was.", "밀스 양은 일반론적으로 대답하기를, 만족이 깃든 오두막이 차디찬 화려함의 궁전보다 낫고, 사랑이 있는 곳에 모든 것이 있다고 했다."]], [["I said to Miss Mills that this was very true, and who should know it better than I, who loved Dora with a love that never mortal had experienced yet?", "나는 밀스 양에게, 그것은 참으로 맞는 말이며, 일찍이 어떤 인간도 겪어 보지 못한 그런 사랑으로 도라를 사랑하는 나만큼 그것을 잘 아는 사람이 또 어디 있겠느냐고 말했다."], ["But on Miss Mills observing, with despondency, that it were well indeed for some hearts if this were so, I explained that I begged leave to restrict the observation to mortals of the masculine gender.", "그러나 밀스 양이 침울하게, 정말이지 어떤 이들의 마음에게는 그것이 사실이라면 다행이겠다고 말하자, 나는 그 말을 남성에 속한 인간들에게로 한정해 주십사 부탁드린다고 설명했다."]], [["I then put it to Miss Mills, to say whether she considered that there was or was not any practical merit in the suggestion I had been anxious to make, concerning the accounts, the housekeeping, and the Cookery", "그러고 나서 나는 밀스 양에게, 내가 그토록 간절히 하려고 했던 그 제안 — 가계부와 살림살이와 요리책에 관한 그 제안에, 과연 실천적인 가치가 있다고 보는지 없다고 보는지를 말해 달라고 청했다."]], [["Miss Mills, after some consideration, thus replied:", "밀스 양은 잠시 생각하더니 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield, I will be plain with you.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 솔직하게 말씀드릴게요."], ["Mental suffering and trial supply, in some natures, the place of years, and I will be as plain with you as if I were a Lady Abbess. No.", "마음의 고통과 시련은, 어떤 사람에게는 세월을 대신해 주는 법이지요. 그러니 저는 마치 수녀원장이라도 된 듯이 솔직하게 말씀드리겠어요. 안 돼요."], ["The suggestion is not appropriate to our Dora.", "그 제안은 우리 도라에게는 어울리지 않아요."], ["Our dearest Dora is a favourite child of nature.", "우리 가장 사랑스러운 도라는 자연이 어여삐 여기는 아이예요."], ["She is a thing of light, and airiness, and joy.", "그 애는 빛이고, 산들거림이고, 기쁨이지요."], ["I am free to confess that if it could be done, it might be well, but--’ And Miss Mills shook her head.", "솔직히 말씀드리면, 그게 가능하기만 하다면야 좋겠지만, 하지만—\" 그러고는 밀스 양이 고개를 절레절레 저었다."]], [["I was encouraged by this closing admission on the part of Miss Mills to ask her, whether, for Dora’s sake, if she had any opportunity of luring her attention to such preparations for an earnest life, she would avail herself of it?", "밀스 양이 끝에 이렇게 인정해 주는 데 용기를 얻어, 나는 그녀에게 물어보았다. 도라를 위해서, 혹시라도 그 애의 마음을 진지한 삶을 위한 그런 준비 쪽으로 살며시 끌어당길 기회가 생긴다면, 그 기회를 놓치지 않고 잡아 주겠느냐고."], ["Miss Mills replied in the affirmative so readily, that I further asked her if she would take charge of the Cookery Book; and, if she ever could insinuate it upon Dora’s acceptance, without frightening her, undertake to do me that crowning service.", "밀스 양이 어찌나 선뜻 그러겠노라 대답하던지, 나는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 요리책을 맡아 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그리고 혹시 도라를 겁주지 않으면서 그 책을 슬며시 받아들이게 할 수만 있다면, 그 더없는 수고를 베풀어 주겠느냐고도."], ["Miss Mills accepted this trust, too; but was not sanguine.", "밀스 양은 이 임무도 받아들였다. 다만 그리 자신만만해하지는 않았다."]], [["And Dora returned, looking such a lovely little creature, that I really doubted whether she ought to be troubled with anything so ordinary.", "이윽고 도라가 돌아왔는데, 어찌나 사랑스러운 작은 생명체로 보이던지, 나는 정말이지 이 아이에게 그런 평범하기 짝이 없는 일로 걱정을 끼쳐도 되는 건지 의심스러워졌다."], ["And she loved me so much, and was so captivating (particularly when she made Jip stand on his hind legs for toast, and when she pretended to hold that nose of his against the hot teapot for punishment because he wouldn’t), that I felt like a sort of Monster who had got into a Fairy’s bower, when I thought of having frightened her, and made her cry.", "게다가 그 애는 나를 어찌나 사랑하던지, 또 어찌나 마음을 사로잡던지 (특히 집을 뒷발로 서게 해서 토스트를 받게 하거나, 그러기를 마다한다고 벌을 준답시고 그 코를 뜨거운 찻주전자에다 갖다 대는 시늉을 할 때면 더더욱 그랬다), 나는 그 애를 겁주고 울게 만들었던 일을 떠올릴 때마다, 마치 요정의 정원에 쳐들어간 괴물이라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["After tea we had the guitar; and Dora sang those same dear old French songs about the impossibility of ever on any account leaving off dancing, La ra la, La ra la, until I felt a much greater Monster than before.", "차를 마신 뒤에는 기타를 꺼냈다. 그리고 도라는 그 정답고 오래된 프랑스 노래들, 무슨 일이 있어도 도무지 춤추기를 멈출 수 없다는 그 노래들을, 라 라 라, 라 라 라, 하고 불렀고, 그럴수록 나는 아까보다 훨씬 더 큰 괴물이 된 기분이었다."]], [["We had only one check to our pleasure, and that happened a little while before I took my leave, when, Miss Mills chancing to make some allusion to tomorrow morning, I unluckily let out that, being obliged to exert myself now, I got up at five o’clock.", "우리의 즐거움에 찬물을 끼얹은 일은 단 한 번뿐이었는데, 그것은 내가 작별을 고하기 조금 전에 벌어졌다. 밀스 양이 어쩌다 내일 아침 이야기를 슬쩍 꺼냈을 때, 나는 불행히도 그만 입을 놀려, 이제는 부지런히 애써야 하는 처지라 다섯 시에 일어난다고 털어놓고 만 것이다."], ["Whether Dora had any idea that I was a Private Watchman, I am unable to say; but it made a great impression on her, and she neither played nor sang any more.", "도라가 나를 야경꾼쯤으로 여겼는지 어땠는지는 알 수 없으나, 어쨌든 그 말은 그 애에게 큰 충격을 주었고, 그 애는 더는 연주도 노래도 하지 않았다."]], [["It was still on her mind when I bade her adieu; and she said to me, in her pretty coaxing way--as if I were a doll, I used to think:", "내가 작별 인사를 할 때까지도 그 일은 그 애의 마음에 걸려 있었다. 그러더니 그 애는, 마치 내가 인형이라도 되는 양 — 나는 늘 그렇게 느끼곤 했다 — 그 어여쁘게 어르는 말투로 나에게 말했다."]], [["‘Now don’t get up at five o’clock, you naughty boy. It’s so nonsensical!’", "\"이제 다섯 시에 일어나지 마요, 못된 사람. 그건 너무 말도 안 돼요!\""]], [["‘My love,’ said I, ‘I have work to do.’", "\"내 사랑,\" 내가 말했다. \"나는 해야 할 일이 있는걸.\""]], [["‘But don’t do it!’ returned Dora.", "\"그럼 하지 마요!\" 도라가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Why should you?’", "\"왜 해야 하는데요?\""]], [["It was impossible to say to that sweet little surprised face, otherwise than lightly and playfully, that we must work to live.", "그 사랑스럽고 작고 놀란 얼굴을 마주하고서는, 가볍고 장난스러운 투가 아니고서는, 우리가 먹고살자면 일을 해야 한다는 말을 도저히 할 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["How ridiculous!’ cried Dora.", "정말 우스워요!\" 도라가 외쳤다."]], [["‘How shall we live without, Dora?’ said I.", "\"일을 안 하면 우리가 어떻게 살아, 도라?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘How?", "\"어떻게요?"], ["Any how!’ said Dora.", "아무렇게나요!\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["She seemed to think she had quite settled the question, and gave me such a triumphant little kiss, direct from her innocent heart, that I would hardly have put her out of conceit with her answer, for a fortune.", "그 애는 자기가 그 문제를 완전히 해결했다고 여기는 모양이었고, 그 천진한 마음에서 곧장 우러나온 의기양양한 작은 입맞춤을 나에게 해 주었으니, 나는 큰 재산을 준다 해도 그 애가 자기 대답에 품은 그 흐뭇함을 깨뜨릴 수가 없었다."]], [["Well!", "그래!"], ["I loved her, and I went on loving her, most absorbingly, entirely, and completely.", "나는 그 애를 사랑했고, 계속해서 사랑했다. 무엇보다도 푹 빠져, 온전히, 남김없이."], ["But going on, too, working pretty hard, and busily keeping red-hot all the irons I now had in the fire, I would sit sometimes of a night, opposite my aunt, thinking how I had frightened Dora that time, and how I could best make my way with a guitar-case through the forest of difficulty, until I used to fancy that my head was turning quite grey.", "하지만 그러면서도 나는 꽤 고되게 일했고, 이제 화로에 올려놓은 쇳덩이를 죄다 벌겋게 달군 채로 바삐 지켰다. 그러다 밤이면 고모님 맞은편에 앉아, 그날 내가 도라를 어떻게 겁주었던가, 그리고 기타 케이스를 들고 이 어려움의 숲을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 헤쳐 나갈 수 있을까를 곰곰이 생각하다가, 급기야 내 머리가 온통 희끗희끗 세어 버리는 것만 같다고 상상하곤 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 38. A DISSOLUTION OF PARTNERSHIP", "# CHAPTER 38. A DISSOLUTION OF PARTNERSHIP"]], [["I did not allow my resolution, with respect to the Parliamentary Debates, to cool.", "의회 토론을 받아쓰겠다는 내 결심은 좀처럼 식지 않았다."], ["It was one of the irons I began to heat immediately, and one of the irons I kept hot, and hammered at, with a perseverance I may honestly admire.", "그것은 내가 곧장 달구기 시작한 쇠붙이 가운데 하나였고, 줄곧 뜨겁게 데워 두고 두드려 댄 쇠붙이였다. 그 끈기만큼은 나 스스로도 솔직히 감탄할 만했다."], ["I bought an approved scheme of the noble art and mystery of stenography (which cost me ten and sixpence); and plunged into a sea of perplexity that brought me, in a few weeks, to the confines of distraction.", "나는 속기라는 고귀한 기술이자 신비의 비법을 정리해 놓은, 권위를 인정받은 교본을 한 권 샀고(십 실링 육 펜스를 치렀다), 그러고는 몇 주 만에 나를 정신 줄을 놓을 지경까지 몰고 간 혼란의 바다에 풍덩 뛰어들었다."], ["The changes that were rung upon dots, which in such a position meant such a thing, and in such another position something else, entirely different; the wonderful vagaries that were played by circles; the unaccountable consequences that resulted from marks like flies’ legs; the tremendous effects of a curve in a wrong place; not only troubled my waking hours, but reappeared before me in my sleep.", "점 하나가 이런 자리에 찍히면 이런 뜻이 되고, 또 저런 자리에 찍히면 전혀 딴판인 다른 뜻이 되는 그 무수한 변주들, 동그라미들이 부리는 기막힌 장난들, 파리 다리처럼 생긴 표시에서 비롯되는 도무지 알 수 없는 결과들, 곡선 하나가 엉뚱한 데 놓였을 때 빚어지는 어마어마한 효과 — 이런 것들이 내 깨어 있는 시간을 괴롭힌 것은 물론이요, 잠든 사이에도 눈앞에 되살아났다."], ["When I had groped my way, blindly, through these difficulties, and had mastered the alphabet, which was an Egyptian Temple in itself, there then appeared a procession of new horrors, called arbitrary characters; the most despotic characters I have ever known; who insisted, for instance, that a thing like the beginning of a cobweb, meant expectation, and that a pen-and-ink sky-rocket, stood for disadvantageous.", "이 난관들을 더듬더듬, 앞이 안 보이는 채로 헤쳐 나가, 그 자체로 이집트 신전이나 다름없던 알파벳을 겨우 익히고 나자, 이번에는 임의 기호라는 새로운 공포의 행렬이 줄지어 나타났다. 내가 여태껏 겪어 본 가장 횡포한 기호들이었다. 이를테면 거미줄 첫머리처럼 생긴 것이 '기대'를 뜻한다고 우기고, 펜과 잉크로 그린 폭죽 같은 것이 '불리한'이라는 뜻이라고 떼를 쓰는 것이었다."], ["When I had fixed these wretches in my mind, I found that they had driven everything else out of it; then, beginning again, I forgot them; while I was picking them up, I dropped the other fragments of the system; in short, it was almost heart-breaking.", "이 몹쓸 것들을 머릿속에 박아 넣고 나니, 이놈들이 다른 모든 것을 머릿속에서 죄다 몰아내 버렸다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 다시 처음부터 시작하면 이놈들을 잊어버렸고, 이놈들을 다시 주워 담는 사이에는 체계의 다른 조각들을 떨어뜨렸다. 한마디로 거의 애간장이 녹을 노릇이었다."]], [["It might have been quite heart-breaking, but for Dora, who was the stay and anchor of my tempest-driven bark.", "도라가 없었다면 정말이지 애간장이 녹았을지도 모른다. 그녀야말로 폭풍에 떠밀리는 내 조각배의 버팀목이자 닻이었다."], ["Every scratch in the scheme was a gnarled oak in the forest of difficulty, and I went on cutting them down, one after another, with such vigour, that in three or four months I was in a condition to make an experiment on one of our crack speakers in the Commons.", "교본에 그어진 획 하나하나가 난관의 숲에 솟은 옹이투성이 떡갈나무였고, 나는 그것들을 하나하나 어찌나 기운차게 베어 넘겼던지, 서너 달 만에 하원의 내로라하는 명연설가 하나를 상대로 실험을 해 볼 만한 상태에 이르렀다."], ["Shall I ever forget how the crack speaker walked off from me before I began, and left my imbecile pencil staggering about the paper as if it were in a fit!", "그 명연설가가 내가 받아쓰기를 시작하기도 전에 저만치 달아나 버리고, 내 얼간이 같은 연필만 발작이라도 일으킨 양 종이 위를 비틀비틀 헤매도록 내버려 둔 그 광경을 내가 어찌 잊으랴!"]], [["This would not do, it was quite clear.", "이래선 안 될 일이었다. 그건 너무나 분명했다."], ["I was flying too high, and should never get on, so.", "나는 너무 높이 날고 있었으니, 이대로는 결코 나아갈 수 없었다."], ["I resorted to Traddles for advice; who suggested that he should dictate speeches to me, at a pace, and with occasional stoppages, adapted to my weakness.", "나는 트래들스에게 조언을 구했다. 그는 내 부족함에 맞춰, 알맞은 속도로, 이따금 멈춰 가며, 자기가 나에게 연설을 불러 주겠노라고 제안했다."], ["Very grateful for this friendly aid, I accepted the proposal; and night after night, almost every night, for a long time, we had a sort of Private Parliament in Buckingham Street, after I came home from the Doctor’s.", "이 우정 어린 도움이 무척이나 고마워 나는 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그리하여 밤이면 밤마다, 거의 매일 밤, 오랫동안, 박사님 댁에서 집으로 돌아온 뒤면 우리는 버킹엄 가에서 일종의 사설 의회를 열었다."]], [["I should like to see such a Parliament anywhere else! My aunt and Mr.", "그런 의회는 세상 어디에 가도 다시 볼 수 없을 것이다!"], ["Dick represented the Government or the Opposition (as the case might be), and Traddles, with the assistance of Enfield’s Speakers, or a volume of parliamentary orations, thundered astonishing invectives against them.", "고모님과 딕 씨가 (그때그때) 정부 측이나 야당 측을 맡았고, 트래들스는 엔필드의 명연설집이나 의회 연설 모음집의 도움을 받아 그들을 향해 놀라운 독설을 천둥처럼 퍼부었다."], ["Standing by the table, with his finger in the page to keep the place, and his right arm flourishing above his head, Traddles, as Mr. Pitt, Mr. Fox, Mr.", "책장 사이에 손가락을 끼워 자리를 표시하고 오른팔을 머리 위로 휘두르며, 트래들스는 피트 씨, 폭스 씨, 셰리든 씨, 버크 씨, 캐슬레이 경, 시드머스 자작, 또는 캐닝 씨가 되어, 스스로를 가장 격렬한 흥분으로 몰아넣고는 고모님과 딕 씨의 방탕과 부패를 향해 가장 신랄한 규탄을 쏟아 냈다."], ["Sheridan, Mr. Burke, Lord Castlereagh, Viscount Sidmouth, or Mr. Canning, would work himself into the most violent heats, and deliver the most withering denunciations of the profligacy and corruption of my aunt and Mr.", "그동안 나는 조금 떨어진 자리에 공책을 무릎에 얹고 앉아, 있는 힘 없는 힘 다 짜내어 그의 말을 죽기 살기로 좇았다."], ["Dick; while I used to sit, at a little distance, with my notebook on my knee, fagging after him with all my might and main. The inconsistency and recklessness of Traddles were not to be exceeded by any real politician.", "트래들스의 일관성 없음과 무모함은 어떤 진짜 정치가도 따라올 수 없을 정도였다. 그는 일주일 안에 온갖 종류의 정책을 다 지지했고, 온갖 색깔의 깃발을 온갖 돛대에 다 못질해 매달았다."], ["He was for any description of policy, in the compass of a week; and nailed all sorts of colours to every denomination of mast.", "고모님은 꿈쩍도 않는 재무장관을 빼다 박은 모습으로, 본문이 요구하는 듯싶을 때면 이따금 \"옳소!\" 또는 \"안 되오!\""], ["My aunt, looking very like an immovable Chancellor of the Exchequer, would occasionally throw in an interruption or two, as ‘Hear!’ or ‘No!’ or ‘Oh!’ when the text seemed to require it: which was always a signal to Mr. Dick (a perfect country gentleman) to follow lustily with the same cry.", "또는 \"오!\" 하고 한두 마디씩 끼어들었는데, 그것은 늘 딕 씨(영락없는 시골 신사였다)에게 같은 외침을 우렁차게 따라 하라는 신호였다."], ["But Mr. Dick got taxed with such things in the course of his Parliamentary career, and was made responsible for such awful consequences, that he became uncomfortable in his mind sometimes.", "하지만 딕 씨는 의정 활동을 하는 동안 그런 일들로 추궁을 당하고, 그토록 끔찍한 결과에 책임을 지게 되는 통에, 더러는 마음이 영 편치 않아졌다."], ["I believe he actually began to be afraid he really had been doing something, tending to the annihilation of the British constitution, and the ruin of the country.", "내가 보기에 그는, 자기가 정말로 무언가 영국 헌정을 무너뜨리고 나라를 망치는 짓을 저지른 게 아닌가 진심으로 두려워하기 시작한 것 같았다."]], [["Often and often we pursued these debates until the clock pointed to midnight, and the candles were burning down.", "우리는 시계가 자정을 가리키고 촛불이 다 타들어 갈 때까지 이런 토론을 거듭하고 또 거듭했다."], ["The result of so much good practice was, that by and by I began to keep pace with Traddles pretty well, and should have been quite triumphant if I had had the least idea what my notes were about.", "이토록 많은 연습이 가져다준 결과는, 머지않아 내가 트래들스를 꽤 잘 따라잡기 시작했다는 것이었으니, 만약 내가 받아쓴 글이 무슨 내용인지 손톱만큼이라도 알아볼 수 있었다면 나는 아주 의기양양했을 것이다."], ["But, as to reading them after I had got them, I might as well have copied the Chinese inscriptions of an immense collection of tea-chests, or the golden characters on all the great red and green bottles in the chemists’ shops!", "그러나 받아쓴 것을 나중에 읽는 일로 말하자면, 차라리 어마어마하게 쌓아 둔 찻상자 더미에 적힌 한문 글귀나, 약방의 크고 빨갛고 푸른 병들에 죄다 박혀 있는 금빛 글자들을 베껴 적은 편이 나았을 것이다!"]], [["There was nothing for it, but to turn back and begin all over again.", "별수 없었다. 되돌아가 처음부터 죄다 다시 시작하는 수밖에."], ["It was very hard, but I turned back, though with a heavy heart, and began laboriously and methodically to plod over the same tedious ground at a snail’s pace; stopping to examine minutely every speck in the way, on all sides, and making the most desperate efforts to know these elusive characters by sight wherever I met them.", "무척 힘들었지만, 나는 무거운 마음을 안고 되돌아갔고, 달팽이 걸음으로 똑같이 지긋지긋한 땅을 부지런히, 차근차근 다시 갈기 시작했다. 길에 놓인 점 하나하나를 사방으로 꼼꼼히 뜯어보려고 멈춰 서고, 이 종잡을 수 없는 기호들을 어디서 마주치든 한눈에 알아보려고 죽을힘을 다해 애썼다."], ["I was always punctual at the office; at the Doctor’s too: and I really did work, as the common expression is, like a cart-horse.", "나는 사무소에 늘 시간을 어김없이 댔고, 박사님 댁에도 그러했다. 그리고 흔히 하는 말마따나, 정말이지 짐말처럼 일했다."], ["One day, when I went to the Commons as usual, I found Mr. Spenlow in the doorway looking extremely grave, and talking to himself.", "어느 날, 여느 때처럼 박사회에 갔을 때, 나는 스펜로 씨가 입구에 서서 몹시 심각한 얼굴로 혼잣말을 하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["As he was in the habit of complaining of pains in his head--he had naturally a short throat, and I do seriously believe he over-starched himself--I was at first alarmed by the idea that he was not quite right in that direction; but he soon relieved my uneasiness.", "그는 평소 머리가 아프다고 푸념하는 버릇이 있었던 데다 — 워낙 목이 짧았고, 내 진심으로 믿건대 풀을 너무 빳빳하게 먹인 탓이었다 — 그래서 나는 처음엔 그가 머리 쪽이 좀 어떻게 된 게 아닌가 하는 생각에 덜컥 놀랐다. 그러나 그는 곧 내 불안을 거두어 주었다."]], [["Instead of returning my ‘Good morning’ with his usual affability, he looked at me in a distant, ceremonious manner, and coldly requested me to accompany him to a certain coffee-house, which, in those days, had a door opening into the Commons, just within the little archway in St. Paul’s Churchyard.", "그는 평소의 살가움으로 내 \"안녕하세요\"에 답하는 대신, 서먹하고 격식 차린 태도로 나를 바라보고는, 어느 커피집까지 함께 가 달라고 차갑게 청했다. 그 시절 그곳은 세인트 폴 묘지의 작은 아치 길 바로 안쪽에서 박사회로 통하는 문이 하나 나 있었다."], ["I complied, in a very uncomfortable state, and with a warm shooting all over me, as if my apprehensions were breaking out into buds.", "나는 몹시 불편한 심정으로, 마치 내 불안이 봉오리를 틔워 터져 나오기라도 하듯 온몸이 뜨겁게 욱신거리는 채로 그를 따랐다."], ["When I allowed him to go on a little before, on account of the narrowness of the way, I observed that he carried his head with a lofty air that was particularly unpromising; and my mind misgave me that he had found out about my darling Dora.", "길이 좁아서 그를 조금 앞서 가게 했을 때, 나는 그가 유난히 불길하게 느껴지는 거만한 자세로 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 걷는 것을 보았다. 그러자 그가 내 사랑하는 도라에 관해 알아챈 것이 아닌가 하는 불안이 마음을 짓눌렀다."]], [["If I had not guessed this, on the way to the coffee-house, I could hardly have failed to know what was the matter when I followed him into an upstairs room, and found Miss Murdstone there, supported by a background of sideboard, on which were several inverted tumblers sustaining lemons, and two of those extraordinary boxes, all corners and flutings, for sticking knives and forks in, which, happily for mankind, are now obsolete.", "설령 커피집으로 가는 길에 이 짐작을 못 했더라도, 그를 따라 이 층 방으로 올라가 거기서 머드스톤 양을 발견했을 때 나는 무슨 일인지 모를 수가 없었을 것이다. 그녀는 찬장을 배경 삼아 떡 버티고 앉아 있었는데, 그 찬장 위에는 거꾸로 엎어 놓은 유리잔 몇 개가 레몬을 떠받치고 있었고, 칼과 포크를 꽂아 두는, 온통 모서리와 골진 무늬뿐인 그 별난 상자가 둘 놓여 있었다 — 인류에게는 다행스럽게도, 이제는 자취를 감춘 물건이다."]], [["Miss Murdstone gave me her chilly finger-nails, and sat severely rigid. Mr.", "머드스톤 양은 차디찬 손톱을 나에게 내밀고는, 무섭도록 뻣뻣하게 앉아 있었다."], ["Spenlow shut the door, motioned me to a chair, and stood on the hearth-rug in front of the fireplace.", "스펜로 씨는 문을 닫고 나에게 의자를 가리킨 다음, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 섰다."]], [["‘Have the goodness to show Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"머드스톤 양, 그 손가방에 든 것을 코퍼필드 씨에게 보여 주시겠습니까.\""], ["Spenlow, what you have in your reticule, Miss Murdstone.’", "스펜로 씨가 말했다."]], [["I believe it was the old identical steel-clasped reticule of my childhood, that shut up like a bite.", "내 어린 시절의 바로 그 강철 잠금쇠 달린 손가방, 무는 입처럼 딱 닫히던 그 가방이 분명했다."], ["Compressing her lips, in sympathy with the snap, Miss Murdstone opened it--opening her mouth a little at the same time--and produced my last letter to Dora, teeming with expressions of devoted affection.", "머드스톤 양은 그 딸깍 소리에 맞춰 입술을 꾹 다물며 가방을 열었고 — 그와 동시에 입도 조금 벌리며 — 도라에게 보낸 내 마지막 편지, 한결같은 애정의 표현이 넘쳐흐르는 그 편지를 꺼냈다."]], [["‘I believe that is your writing, Mr.", "\"이게 당신 글씨가 맞지요, 코퍼필드 씨?\""], ["Copperfield?’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "스펜로 씨가 말했다."]], [["I was very hot, and the voice I heard was very unlike mine, when I said, ‘It is, sir!’", "온몸이 화끈거렸고, 내가 \"맞습니다, 선생님!\" 하고 말했을 때 내 귀에 들린 목소리는 도무지 내 것 같지 않았다."]], [["‘If I am not mistaken,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면,\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["Spenlow, as Miss Murdstone brought a parcel of letters out of her reticule, tied round with the dearest bit of blue ribbon, ‘those are also from your pen, Mr. Copperfield?’", "그때 머드스톤 양이 손가방에서 더없이 정겨운 파란 리본으로 묶은 편지 한 묶음을 꺼냈다. \"이것들도 당신 펜에서 나온 것이겠지요, 코퍼필드 씨?\""]], [["I took them from her with a most desolate sensation; and, glancing at such phrases at the top, as ‘My ever dearest and own Dora,’ ‘My best beloved angel,’ ‘My blessed one for ever,’ and the like, blushed deeply, and inclined my head.", "나는 더없이 쓸쓸한 심정으로 그것들을 그녀에게서 받아 들었다. 그러고는 첫머리에 적힌 \"내 영원히 가장 사랑하는 나만의 도라\", \"내 가장 사랑하는 천사\", \"내 영원히 축복받은 사람\" 따위의 문구를 흘끗 보고는, 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["‘No, thank you!’ said Mr.", "\"아니요, 괜찮습니다!\""], ["Spenlow, coldly, as I mechanically offered them back to him.", "내가 기계적으로 그것들을 도로 건네자 스펜로 씨가 차갑게 말했다."], ["‘I will not deprive you of them.", "\"당신 것을 빼앗지는 않겠소."], ["Miss Murdstone, be so good as to proceed!’", "머드스톤 양, 계속해 주시지요!\""]], [["That gentle creature, after a moment’s thoughtful survey of the carpet, delivered herself with much dry unction as follows.", "그 온순한 인물은 잠시 생각에 잠긴 듯 양탄자를 살펴본 다음, 메마른 거드름을 잔뜩 풍기며 다음과 같이 입을 열었다."]], [["‘I must confess to having entertained my suspicions of Miss Spenlow, in reference to David Copperfield, for some time.", "\"고백하건대, 데이비드 코퍼필드와 관련하여 스펜로 양을 얼마간 의심해 왔습니다."], ["I observed Miss Spenlow and David Copperfield, when they first met; and the impression made upon me then was not agreeable.", "나는 스펜로 양과 데이비드 코퍼필드가 처음 만났을 때 두 사람을 눈여겨보았는데, 그때 받은 인상이 영 마뜩잖았어요."], ["The depravity of the human heart is such--’", "사람 마음이란 어찌나 타락한 것인지—\""]], [["‘You will oblige me, ma’am,’ interrupted Mr.", "\"부인,\" 스펜로 씨가 가로막았다."], ["Spenlow, ‘by confining yourself to facts.’", "\"사실에만 국한해 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\""]], [["Miss Murdstone cast down her eyes, shook her head as if protesting against this unseemly interruption, and with frowning dignity resumed:", "머드스톤 양은 눈을 내리깔고, 이 무례한 끼어듦에 항의라도 하듯 고개를 가로젓더니, 눈살을 찌푸린 위엄을 풍기며 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Since I am to confine myself to facts, I will state them as dryly as I can.", "\"사실에만 국한해야 한다면, 되도록 메마르게 진술하지요."], ["Perhaps that will be considered an acceptable course of proceeding.", "어쩌면 그게 받아들일 만한 진행 방식으로 여겨질 테니까요."], ["I have already said, sir, that I have had my suspicions of Miss Spenlow, in reference to David Copperfield, for some time.", "이미 말씀드렸다시피, 선생님, 나는 데이비드 코퍼필드와 관련하여 스펜로 양을 얼마간 의심해 왔습니다."], ["I have frequently endeavoured to find decisive corroboration of those suspicions, but without effect.", "그 의심을 결정적으로 뒷받침할 증거를 거듭 찾아내려 애썼으나 헛수고였지요."], ["I have therefore forborne to mention them to Miss Spenlow’s father’; looking severely at him--‘knowing how little disposition there usually is in such cases, to acknowledge the conscientious discharge of duty.’", "그래서 스펜로 양의 아버님께는 그 일을 말씀드리지 않고 참아 왔습니다.\" 그녀는 그를 매섭게 쏘아보았다. \"그런 경우 양심껏 의무를 다한 것을 인정해 주려는 마음이 대개 얼마나 박한지 잘 알고 있었으니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Spenlow seemed quite cowed by the gentlemanly sternness of Miss Murdstone’s manner, and deprecated her severity with a conciliatory little wave of his hand.", "스펜로 씨는 머드스톤 양의 신사 같은 준엄한 태도에 잔뜩 기가 죽은 듯, 화해를 청하듯 손을 살짝 내저으며 그녀의 매서움을 누그러뜨려 보려 했다."]], [["‘On my return to Norwood, after the period of absence occasioned by my brother’s marriage,’ pursued Miss Murdstone in a disdainful voice, ‘and on the return of Miss Spenlow from her visit to her friend Miss Mills, I imagined that the manner of Miss Spenlow gave me greater occasion for suspicion than before.", "\"내 오라비의 결혼으로 자리를 비웠다가 노우드로 돌아온 뒤,\" 머드스톤 양은 경멸 어린 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"그리고 스펜로 양이 친구인 밀스 양 댁을 방문하고 돌아온 뒤, 나는 스펜로 양의 태도가 전보다 더 의심할 만한 빌미를 준다고 여겼습니다."], ["Therefore I watched Miss Spenlow closely.’", "그래서 스펜로 양을 면밀히 지켜보았지요.\""]], [["Dear, tender little Dora, so unconscious of this Dragon’s eye!", "사랑스럽고 다정한 어린 도라, 이 용의 눈을 그토록 까맣게 모르고 있었다니!"]], [["‘Still,’ resumed Miss Murdstone, ‘I found no proof until last night.", "\"그래도,\" 머드스톤 양이 다시 말을 이었다."], ["It appeared to me that Miss Spenlow received too many letters from her friend Miss Mills; but Miss Mills being her friend with her father’s full concurrence,’ another telling blow at Mr.", "\"어젯밤까지는 아무 증거도 잡지 못했습니다. 스펜로 양이 친구인 밀스 양에게서 편지를 지나치게 많이 받는다 싶긴 했으나, 밀스 양은 아버님의 전적인 동의 아래 친구가 된 사이였으니,\""], ["Spenlow, ‘it was not for me to interfere.", "또 한 번 스펜로 씨에게 따끔한 일격이었다."], ["If I may not be permitted to allude to the natural depravity of the human heart, at least I may--I must--be permitted, so far to refer to misplaced confidence.’", "\"내가 끼어들 일은 아니었습니다. 인간 마음의 타고난 타락을 언급하는 것이 허락되지 않는다면, 적어도 잘못 둔 신뢰에 관해서만은 — 언급해도 좋겠지요, 아니, 언급해야겠지요.\""]], [["Mr. Spenlow apologetically murmured his assent.", "스펜로 씨는 사과하듯 동의를 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘Last evening after tea,’ pursued Miss Murdstone, ‘I observed the little dog starting, rolling, and growling about the drawing-room, worrying something.", "\"어제저녁 차를 마신 뒤,\" 머드스톤 양이 말을 이었다. \"나는 그 작은 개가 응접실을 이리저리 펄쩍거리고, 뒹굴고, 으르렁대며 무언가를 물어뜯는 것을 보았습니다."], ["I said to Miss Spenlow, “Dora, what is that the dog has in his mouth?", "그래서 스펜로 양에게 말했지요. '도라, 저 개가 입에 문 게 뭐냐?"], ["It’s paper.”", "종이로구나.'"], ["Miss Spenlow immediately put her hand to her frock, gave a sudden cry, and ran to the dog.", "스펜로 양은 곧장 자기 옷자락에 손을 대더니, 별안간 외마디 소리를 지르며 개에게 달려갔어요."], ["I interposed, and said, “Dora, my love, you must permit me.”’", "내가 가로막으며 말했습니다. '도라, 얘야, 나한테 맡기렴.'\""]], [["Oh Jip, miserable Spaniel, this wretchedness, then, was your work!", "오, 지프, 가엾은 스패니얼아, 그렇다면 이 모든 비참함이 다 네 짓이었구나!"]], [["‘Miss Spenlow endeavoured,’ said Miss Murdstone, ‘to bribe me with kisses, work-boxes, and small articles of jewellery--that, of course, I pass over.", "\"스펜로 양은,\" 머드스톤 양이 말했다. \"입맞춤이며 반짇고리며 자잘한 패물 따위로 나를 구슬리려 들었지만 — 그건 물론 그냥 넘어가지요."], ["The little dog retreated under the sofa on my approaching him, and was with great difficulty dislodged by the fire-irons.", "내가 다가가자 그 작은 개는 소파 밑으로 달아났고, 부지깽이를 동원해 가까스로 끌어냈습니다."], ["Even when dislodged, he still kept the letter in his mouth; and on my endeavouring to take it from him, at the imminent risk of being bitten, he kept it between his teeth so pertinaciously as to suffer himself to be held suspended in the air by means of the document.", "끌어냈을 때도 녀석은 여전히 편지를 입에 물고 있었고, 내가 물릴 위험을 무릅쓰고 그것을 빼앗으려 하자, 어찌나 끈질기게 이 사이에 꽉 물고 있던지 그 편지로 제 몸이 공중에 대롱대롱 매달리는 것마저 견디더군요."], ["At length I obtained possession of it.", "마침내 나는 그것을 손에 넣었습니다."], ["After perusing it, I taxed Miss Spenlow with having many such letters in her possession; and ultimately obtained from her the packet which is now in David Copperfield’s hand.’", "그것을 다 읽고 나서 나는 스펜로 양에게 그런 편지를 많이 갖고 있지 않으냐고 다그쳤고, 결국 지금 데이비드 코퍼필드의 손에 들린 그 묶음을 그녀에게서 받아 냈습니다.\""]], [["Here she ceased; and snapping her reticule again, and shutting her mouth, looked as if she might be broken, but could never be bent.", "여기서 그녀는 말을 그쳤다. 그러고는 손가방을 다시 딱 닫고 입을 다문 채, 부러질지언정 결코 휘지는 않으리라는 표정으로 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘You have heard Miss Murdstone,’ said Mr.", "\"머드스톤 양의 말을 들었지요.\""], ["Spenlow, turning to me. ‘I beg to ask, Mr.", "스펜로 씨가 나를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Copperfield, if you have anything to say in reply?’", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 이에 대해 할 말이 있는지 묻고 싶군요.\""]], [["The picture I had before me, of the beautiful little treasure of my heart, sobbing and crying all night--of her being alone, frightened, and wretched, then--of her having so piteously begged and prayed that stony-hearted woman to forgive her--of her having vainly offered her those kisses, work-boxes, and trinkets--of her being in such grievous distress, and all for me--very much impaired the little dignity I had been able to muster.", "내 마음속 아름다운 작은 보물이 밤새도록 흐느끼며 울었을 모습 — 그 무렵 외롭고, 겁에 질리고, 비참했을 모습 — 그 돌처럼 매정한 여자에게 용서해 달라고 그토록 애처롭게 빌고 또 빌었을 모습 — 그 입맞춤이며 반짇고리며 패물을 부질없이 내밀었을 모습 — 그토록 처절한 고통에 빠져 있었을, 그것도 죄다 나 때문에 그러했을 모습이 눈앞에 떠올라, 내가 가까스로 그러모았던 알량한 위엄을 송두리째 무너뜨렸다."], ["I am afraid I was in a tremulous state for a minute or so, though I did my best to disguise it.", "애써 감추려 했지만, 한 일 분쯤 내가 부들부들 떨고 있었던 것 같아 부끄럽다."]], [["‘There is nothing I can say, sir,’ I returned, ‘except that all the blame is mine.", "\"드릴 말씀이 없습니다, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["Dora--’", "\"모든 잘못이 제게 있다는 것 말고는요. 도라가—\""]], [["‘Miss Spenlow, if you please,’ said her father, majestically.", "\"스펜로 양이라고 해 주시지요.\" 그녀의 아버지가 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["‘--was induced and persuaded by me,’ I went on, swallowing that colder designation, ‘to consent to this concealment, and I bitterly regret it.’", "\"—제게 꼬임을 당하고 설득당해,\" 나는 그 차가운 호칭을 꾹 삼키며 말을 이었다. \"이 비밀에 동의한 것이니, 저는 그것을 뼈저리게 후회하고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘You are very much to blame, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"당신은 큰 잘못을 저질렀소, 선생님.\""], ["Spenlow, walking to and fro upon the hearth-rug, and emphasizing what he said with his whole body instead of his head, on account of the stiffness of his cravat and spine.", "스펜로 씨가 벽난로 앞 깔개 위를 왔다 갔다 하며 말했다. 그는 빳빳한 넥타이와 등 때문에 고개 대신 온몸으로 자기 말을 강조했다."], ["‘You have done a stealthy and unbecoming action, Mr. Copperfield.", "\"당신은 떳떳치 못하고 어울리지 않는 짓을 했소, 코퍼필드 씨."], ["When I take a gentleman to my house, no matter whether he is nineteen, twenty-nine, or ninety, I take him there in a spirit of confidence.", "내가 한 신사를 우리 집에 들일 때는, 그가 열아홉이든 스물아홉이든 아흔이든 간에, 신뢰하는 마음으로 들이는 거요."], ["If he abuses my confidence, he commits a dishonourable action, Mr. Copperfield.’", "그가 내 신뢰를 저버린다면, 그건 명예롭지 못한 짓을 저지른 것이오, 코퍼필드 씨.\""]], [["‘I feel it, sir, I assure you,’ I returned.", "\"정말 그렇게 느낍니다, 선생님, 맹세코요.\""], ["‘But I never thought so, before. Sincerely, honestly, indeed, Mr.", "내가 대답했다. \"하지만 이전에는 한 번도 그렇게 생각해 본 적이 없습니다."], ["Spenlow, I never thought so, before. I love Miss Spenlow to that extent--’", "진심으로, 솔직히, 스펜로 씨, 이전에는 정말 그렇게 생각해 본 적이 없어요. 저는 스펜로 양을 그토록 사랑하기에—\""]], [["‘Pooh! nonsense!’ said Mr.", "\"흥! 헛소리!\""], ["Spenlow, reddening.", "스펜로 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["‘Pray don’t tell me to my face that you love my daughter, Mr. Copperfield!’", "\"내 면전에서 내 딸을 사랑한다는 따위 말은 제발 하지 마시오, 코퍼필드 씨!\""]], [["‘Could I defend my conduct if I did not, sir?’ I returned, with all humility.", "\"그렇지 않다면 제가 어찌 제 행동을 변명할 수 있겠습니까, 선생님?\" 나는 한껏 겸손하게 대답했다."]], [["‘Can you defend your conduct if you do, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 당신이 어찌 제 행동을 변명할 수 있겠소, 선생님?\""], ["Spenlow, stopping short upon the hearth-rug.", "스펜로 씨가 깔개 위에서 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다."], ["‘Have you considered your years, and my daughter’s years, Mr. Copperfield?", "\"당신 나이와 내 딸의 나이를 헤아려 보았소, 코퍼필드 씨?"], ["Have you considered what it is to undermine the confidence that should subsist between my daughter and myself?", "내 딸과 나 사이에 있어야 마땅한 신뢰를 허무는 일이 무얼 뜻하는지 헤아려 보았소?"], ["Have you considered my daughter’s station in life, the projects I may contemplate for her advancement, the testamentary intentions I may have with reference to her?", "내 딸의 사회적 지위, 내가 그 애의 앞날을 위해 품을지 모를 계획, 그 애와 관련해 내가 가질지 모를 유언상의 뜻을 헤아려 보았소?"], ["Have you considered anything, Mr. Copperfield?’", "무엇 하나라도 헤아려 본 것이 있소, 코퍼필드 씨?\""]], [["‘Very little, sir, I am afraid;’ I answered, speaking to him as respectfully and sorrowfully as I felt; ‘but pray believe me, I have considered my own worldly position.", "\"별로 헤아려 보지 못한 것 같습니다, 선생님.\" 나는 느끼는 그대로 공손하고 침통하게 그에게 말했다. \"하지만 제발 믿어 주십시오."], ["When I explained it to you, we were already engaged--’", "제 세속적인 처지만큼은 헤아려 보았습니다. 그 처지를 선생님께 설명드렸을 때, 저희는 이미 약혼한 사이였고—\""]], [["‘I BEG,’ said Mr.", "\"제발 부탁이오.\""], ["Spenlow, more like Punch than I had ever seen him, as he energetically struck one hand upon the other--I could not help noticing that even in my despair; ‘that YOU Will NOT talk to me of engagements, Mr. Copperfield!’", "스펜로 씨가 한 손으로 다른 손을 힘껏 내리치며 말했는데, 그 모습이 내가 여태 본 어느 때보다 펀치 인형을 닮아 있어서 — 절망 속에서도 나는 그것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다 — \"나에게 약혼이니 뭐니 하는 말은 하지 마시오, 코퍼필드 씨!\""]], [["The otherwise immovable Miss Murdstone laughed contemptuously in one short syllable.", "좀처럼 꿈쩍 않던 머드스톤 양이 한 음절로 짤막하게 비웃었다."]], [["‘When I explained my altered position to you, sir,’ I began again, substituting a new form of expression for what was so unpalatable to him, ‘this concealment, into which I am so unhappy as to have led Miss Spenlow, had begun.", "\"바뀐 제 처지를 선생님께 설명드렸을 때, 선생님,\" 나는 그가 그토록 못마땅해하는 표현 대신 새 표현으로 바꾸어 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"제가 불행히도 스펜로 양을 끌어들이고 만 이 비밀이 이미 시작된 뒤였습니다."], ["Since I have been in that altered position, I have strained every nerve, I have exerted every energy, to improve it. I am sure I shall improve it in time.", "그 바뀐 처지에 놓인 뒤로, 저는 그것을 나아지게 하려고 신경을 곤두세우고 온 힘을 쏟아부었습니다. 시간이 지나면 반드시 나아지게 만들 자신이 있습니다."], ["Will you grant me time--any length of time?", "제게 시간을 주시지 않겠습니까 — 얼마가 됐든 좋습니다."], ["We are both so young, sir,--’", "저희는 둘 다 이토록 젊으니까요, 선생님—\""]], [["‘You are right,’ interrupted Mr.", "\"옳은 말이오.\""], ["Spenlow, nodding his head a great many times, and frowning very much, ‘you are both very young.", "스펜로 씨가 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이고 잔뜩 눈살을 찌푸리며 가로막았다. \"둘 다 아주 젊지."], ["It’s all nonsense.", "죄다 헛소리요."], ["Let there be an end of the nonsense.", "이 헛소리를 끝냅시다."], ["Take away those letters, and throw them in the fire.", "그 편지들을 가져가서 불속에 던지시오."], ["Give me Miss Spenlow’s letters to throw in the fire; and although our future intercourse must, you are aware, be restricted to the Commons here, we will agree to make no further mention of the past.", "스펜로 양의 편지는 내가 불에 던지게 나에게 주고. 그리고 앞으로 우리 사이의 왕래는, 당신도 알다시피 이곳 박사회로만 제한될 수밖에 없겠지만, 지난 일은 더 이상 들먹이지 않기로 합시다."], ["Come, Mr. Copperfield, you don’t want sense; and this is the sensible course.’", "자, 코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 분별이 모자란 사람이 아니오. 이것이 분별 있는 길이오.\""]], [["No. I couldn’t think of agreeing to it.", "아니다. 나는 거기에 동의할 생각조차 할 수 없었다."], ["I was very sorry, but there was a higher consideration than sense. Love was above all earthly considerations, and I loved Dora to idolatry, and Dora loved me.", "무척 죄송했지만, 분별보다 더 높은 고려가 있었다. 사랑은 이 세상의 그 어떤 고려보다도 위에 있었고, 나는 도라를 우상처럼 떠받들며 사랑했고, 도라도 나를 사랑했다."], ["I didn’t exactly say so; I softened it down as much as I could; but I implied it, and I was resolute upon it.", "딱 잘라 그렇게 말하지는 않았다. 되도록 부드럽게 누그러뜨렸다. 하지만 그런 뜻을 내비쳤고, 그 점에 대해서는 단호했다."], ["I don’t think I made myself very ridiculous, but I know I was resolute.", "내가 아주 우스꽝스러워 보이지는 않았던 것 같지만, 내가 단호했던 것만은 분명하다."]], [["‘Very well, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"좋소, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["Spenlow, ‘I must try my influence with my daughter.’", "\"내 딸에게 내 영향력을 행사해 봐야겠군.\""]], [["Miss Murdstone, by an expressive sound, a long drawn respiration, which was neither a sigh nor a moan, but was like both, gave it as her opinion that he should have done this at first.", "머드스톤 양은 한숨도 신음도 아니면서 그 둘을 다 닮은, 길게 끌어 내쉬는 의미심장한 숨소리로, 진작에 그랬어야 했다는 자기 의견을 드러냈다."]], [["‘I must try,’ said Mr.", "\"내 딸에게,\""], ["Spenlow, confirmed by this support, ‘my influence with my daughter.", "이 지원에 힘을 얻은 스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"내 영향력을 행사해 봐야겠소."], ["Do you decline to take those letters, Mr.", "그 편지들을 가져가지 않겠다는 거요, 코퍼필드 씨?\""], ["Copperfield?’ For I had laid them on the table.", "내가 편지들을 탁자 위에 놓아 두었기 때문이다."]], [["Yes. I told him I hoped he would not think it wrong, but I couldn’t possibly take them from Miss Murdstone.", "그렇다. 나는 그가 잘못이라 여기지 않기를 바라지만, 도무지 머드스톤 양에게서 그것을 받아 갈 수는 없노라고 말했다."]], [["‘Nor from me?’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"나한테서도 못 받겠소?\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."]], [["No, I replied with the profoundest respect; nor from him.", "못 받겠습니다, 하고 나는 더없이 깊은 공손함으로 대답했다. 선생님께서 주셔도 못 받겠습니다."]], [["‘Very well!’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"좋소!\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."]], [["A silence succeeding, I was undecided whether to go or stay.", "침묵이 뒤따랐고, 나는 가야 할지 머물러야 할지 마음을 정하지 못했다."], ["At length I was moving quietly towards the door, with the intention of saying that perhaps I should consult his feelings best by withdrawing: when he said, with his hands in his coat pockets, into which it was as much as he could do to get them; and with what I should call, upon the whole, a decidedly pious air:", "마침내 나는, 어쩌면 물러가는 것이 그의 심정을 가장 잘 헤아리는 길일지 모르겠다고 말할 작정으로 문 쪽을 향해 조용히 발걸음을 옮기고 있었다. 그때 그가 두 손을 — 가까스로 욱여넣을 수 있을 만큼 빠듯한 — 코트 주머니에 찌른 채, 전체로 보아 단연 경건하다고 할 만한 분위기를 풍기며 말했다."]], [["‘You are probably aware, Mr. Copperfield, that I am not altogether destitute of worldly possessions, and that my daughter is my nearest and dearest relative?’", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 당신도 아마 알고 있겠지만, 나에게 세속적 재산이 아주 없는 것은 아니고, 내 딸은 나에게 가장 가깝고 가장 소중한 혈육이오?\""]], [["I hurriedly made him a reply to the effect, that I hoped the error into which I had been betrayed by the desperate nature of my love, did not induce him to think me mercenary too?", "나는 부랴부랴, 절박한 사랑에 휩쓸려 빠지고 만 잘못 때문에 그가 저를 돈이나 노리는 사람으로까지 여기지 않으시길 바란다는 취지로 대답했다."]], [["‘I don’t allude to the matter in that light,’ said Mr. Spenlow.", "\"그런 뜻으로 그 일을 들먹이는 건 아니오.\" 스펜로 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It would be better for yourself, and all of us, if you WERE mercenary, Mr. Copperfield--I mean, if you were more discreet and less influenced by all this youthful nonsense. No.", "\"당신이 돈을 노리는 사람이라면 차라리 당신 자신을 위해서도, 우리 모두를 위해서도 더 나을 거요, 코퍼필드 씨 — 내 말은, 당신이 좀 더 신중하고 이 풋내기 같은 헛소리에 덜 휘둘린다면 말이오."], ["I merely say, with quite another view, you are probably aware I have some property to bequeath to my child?’", "아니, 나는 그저 전혀 다른 관점에서, 내가 내 딸에게 물려줄 재산이 얼마간 있다는 것을 당신도 아마 알고 있으리란 얘기를 하는 거요?\""]], [["I certainly supposed so.", "나는 분명 그러리라 짐작하고 있었다."]], [["‘And you can hardly think,’ said Mr.", "\"그런데 당신도 이곳 박사회에서 날마다 보아 알다시피,\""], ["Spenlow, ‘having experience of what we see, in the Commons here, every day, of the various unaccountable and negligent proceedings of men, in respect of their testamentary arrangements--of all subjects, the one on which perhaps the strangest revelations of human inconsistency are to be met with--but that mine are made?’", "스펜로 씨가 말했다. \"사람들이 유언 처리에 관해 갖가지로 도무지 알 수 없고 또 소홀한 행태를 보이지요 — 인간의 일관성 없음에 관한 가장 기이한 본색이 드러나는 주제를 다 통틀어도 아마 이것만 한 것이 없을 거요 — 그런 마당에, 설마 내 유언만큼은 다 마련되어 있지 않으리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없겠지요?\""]], [["I inclined my head in acquiescence.", "나는 동의한다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘I should not allow,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 결코,\""], ["Spenlow, with an evident increase of pious sentiment, and slowly shaking his head as he poised himself upon his toes and heels alternately, ‘my suitable provision for my child to be influenced by a piece of youthful folly like the present.", "스펜로 씨가 경건한 감정을 부쩍 더하며, 발끝과 발뒤꿈치로 번갈아 몸을 가누고 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"내 딸을 위한 합당한 대비가 지금 같은 풋내기 어리석음 따위에 휘둘리도록 내버려 두지 않을 거요."], ["It is mere folly. Mere nonsense.", "그건 그저 어리석음일 뿐이오."], ["In a little while, it will weigh lighter than any feather.", "한낱 헛소리지. 머지않아 그것은 깃털 하나보다 가벼워질 거요."], ["But I might--I might--if this silly business were not completely relinquished altogether, be induced in some anxious moment to guard her from, and surround her with protections against, the consequences of any foolish step in the way of marriage. Now, Mr.", "하지만 만약 이 어처구니없는 일이 완전히 깨끗이 청산되지 않는다면, 나는 어쩌면 — 어쩌면 — 어느 불안한 순간에, 어리석은 결혼 같은 어떤 잘못된 발걸음이 빚을 결과로부터 그 애를 지키고, 그 애 둘레에 보호막을 둘러야겠다는 마음에 이끌릴지도 모르오."], ["Copperfield, I hope that you will not render it necessary for me to open, even for a quarter of an hour, that closed page in the book of life, and unsettle, even for a quarter of an hour, grave affairs long since composed.’", "자, 코퍼필드 씨, 나는 당신이, 인생이라는 책의 그 덮인 페이지를 단 십오 분이라도 다시 펼쳐 보고, 오래전에 정리된 중대한 일들을 단 십오 분이라도 다시 흔들어 놓을 수밖에 없게끔 만들지 않기를 바라오.\""]], [["There was a serenity, a tranquillity, a calm sunset air about him, which quite affected me.", "그에게는 어떤 고요함, 어떤 평온함, 어떤 잔잔한 저녁놀 같은 기운이 감돌고 있어서, 나는 자못 마음이 움직였다."], ["He was so peaceful and resigned--clearly had his affairs in such perfect train, and so systematically wound up--that he was a man to feel touched in the contemplation of.", "그는 어찌나 평화롭고 체념한 듯하던지 — 자기 일들을 분명 그토록 완벽한 궤도에 올려놓고 그토록 빈틈없이 매듭지어 둔 듯하여 — 가만히 들여다보고 있노라면 절로 가슴이 뭉클해지는 사람이었다."], ["I really think I saw tears rise to his eyes, from the depth of his own feeling of all this.", "이 모든 것에 대한 자기 자신의 깊은 감정에서 그의 눈에 정말로 눈물이 차오르는 것을 본 것만 같다."]], [["But what could I do? I could not deny Dora and my own heart.", "하지만 내가 무얼 할 수 있었겠는가? 나는 도라와 내 마음을 부정할 수 없었다."], ["When he told me I had better take a week to consider of what he had said, how could I say I wouldn’t take a week, yet how could I fail to know that no amount of weeks could influence such love as mine?", "그가 자기 말을 일주일 동안 곰곰이 생각해 보는 게 좋겠다고 했을 때, 일주일은 못 받겠다고 어찌 말할 수 있었으랴. 그러면서도, 몇 주가 됐든 그것이 나 같은 사랑에 영향을 미칠 리 없다는 것을 어찌 모를 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["‘In the meantime, confer with Miss Trotwood, or with any person with any knowledge of life,’ said Mr.", "\"그동안 트롯우드 양이든, 세상 물정 좀 아는 누구든 상의해 보시오.\""], ["Spenlow, adjusting his cravat with both hands.", "스펜로 씨가 두 손으로 넥타이를 매만지며 말했다."], ["‘Take a week, Mr. Copperfield.’", "\"일주일을 갖도록 하시오, 코퍼필드 씨.\""]], [["I submitted; and, with a countenance as expressive as I was able to make it of dejected and despairing constancy, came out of the room.", "나는 따랐다. 그러고는 풀이 죽고 절망에 빠진 변함없는 마음을 할 수 있는 한 또렷이 드러낸 얼굴로 그 방을 나왔다."], ["Miss Murdstone’s heavy eyebrows followed me to the door--I say her eyebrows rather than her eyes, because they were much more important in her face--and she looked so exactly as she used to look, at about that hour of the morning, in our parlour at Blunderstone, that I could have fancied I had been breaking down in my lessons again, and that the dead weight on my mind was that horrible old spelling-book, with oval woodcuts, shaped, to my youthful fancy, like the glasses out of spectacles.", "머드스톤 양의 묵직한 눈썹이 문까지 나를 좇았다 — 그녀의 눈이 아니라 눈썹이라고 말하는 까닭은, 그녀의 얼굴에서는 눈썹이 훨씬 더 중요했기 때문이다 — 그리고 그녀는 블런더스톤의 우리 집 거실에서 아침 그 무렵이면 늘 짓던 그 표정 그대로여서, 나는 마치 또다시 공부를 망쳐 쩔쩔매고 있고, 내 마음을 짓누르는 그 무거운 덩어리가 바로 그 끔찍한 옛 철자 교본 — 어린 내 눈에는 안경알처럼 생긴 타원형 목판화가 박혀 있던 그 책 — 인 듯한 착각마저 들 지경이었다."]], [["When I got to the office, and, shutting out old Tiffey and the rest of them with my hands, sat at my desk, in my own particular nook, thinking of this earthquake that had taken place so unexpectedly, and in the bitterness of my spirit cursing Jip, I fell into such a state of torment about Dora, that I wonder I did not take up my hat and rush insanely to Norwood.", "사무소에 도착해, 두 손으로 늙은 티피 영감과 다른 사람들을 가려 막고, 내 전용 구석 자리 책상에 앉아, 그토록 느닷없이 일어난 이 지진을 곱씹으며 비통한 심정으로 지프를 저주하다가, 나는 도라를 둘러싸고 어찌나 큰 괴로움에 빠져들었던지, 모자를 집어 들고 미친 사람처럼 노우드로 달려가지 않은 것이 신기할 지경이었다."], ["The idea of their frightening her, and making her cry, and of my not being there to comfort her, was so excruciating, that it impelled me to write a wild letter to Mr.", "그들이 그녀를 겁주고 울리는데, 나는 그 곁에서 위로해 줄 수도 없다는 생각이 어찌나 사무치게 괴로웠던지, 나는 그 충동에 떠밀려 스펜로 씨에게 미친 듯한 편지를 썼다."], ["Spenlow, beseeching him not to visit upon her the consequences of my awful destiny.", "부디 내 끔찍한 운명의 결과를 그녀에게 떠넘기지 말아 달라고 애원하는 내용이었다."], ["I implored him to spare her gentle nature--not to crush a fragile flower--and addressed him generally, to the best of my remembrance, as if, instead of being her father, he had been an Ogre, or the Dragon of Wantley.", "그녀의 여린 성품을 부디 아껴 달라고 — 가녀린 꽃을 짓밟지 말아 달라고 — 빌었고, 내 기억으로는, 그가 그녀의 아버지가 아니라 무슨 식인귀나 완틀리의 용이라도 되는 양 그를 통틀어 다루었다."], ["This letter I sealed and laid upon his desk before he returned; and when he came in, I saw him, through the half-opened door of his room, take it up and read it.", "이 편지를 봉해서 그가 돌아오기 전에 그의 책상 위에 놓아 두었고, 그가 들어왔을 때 나는 반쯤 열린 그의 방문 틈으로 그가 그것을 집어 들고 읽는 것을 보았다. * 완틀리의 용: 영국 옛 발라드에 나오는 용으로, 무엇이든 닥치는 대로 집어삼키는 괴물."]], [["He said nothing about it all the morning; but before he went away in the afternoon he called me in, and told me that I need not make myself at all uneasy about his daughter’s happiness.", "그는 오전 내내 그것에 대해 아무 말도 없었다. 그러나 오후에 나가기 전에 나를 불러서는, 자기 딸의 행복에 대해 조금도 불안해할 것 없다고 일러 주었다."], ["He had assured her, he said, that it was all nonsense; and he had nothing more to say to her.", "그는 그녀에게 그 모든 게 다 헛소리라고 단단히 못 박아 두었고, 더 이상 그녀에게 할 말이 없노라고 했다."], ["He believed he was an indulgent father (as indeed he was), and I might spare myself any solicitude on her account.", "자기는 너그러운 아버지라고 믿으며(실제로도 그러했다), 그러니 나는 그녀 때문에 마음 졸일 필요가 전혀 없다고 했다."]], [["‘You may make it necessary, if you are foolish or obstinate, Mr.", "\"당신이 어리석게 굴거나 고집을 부린다면, 코퍼필드 씨,\" 그가 말했다."], ["Copperfield,’ he observed, ‘for me to send my daughter abroad again, for a term; but I have a better opinion of you.", "\"내가 한동안 딸을 다시 외국으로 보내야 할 수밖에 없게 될지도 모르오. 하지만 나는 당신을 더 좋게 보고 있소."], ["I hope you will be wiser than that, in a few days.", "며칠 안에 당신이 그보다는 현명해지기를 바라오."], ["As to Miss Murdstone,’ for I had alluded to her in the letter, ‘I respect that lady’s vigilance, and feel obliged to her; but she has strict charge to avoid the subject.", "머드스톤 양으로 말하자면,\" 내가 편지에서 그녀를 언급했기 때문이다. \"나는 그 부인의 경계심을 존중하고 또 고맙게 여기지만, 그 부인에게는 이 일을 입에 올리지 말라고 엄히 일러 두었소."], ["All I desire, Mr. Copperfield, is, that it should be forgotten.", "내가 바라는 건 오직, 코퍼필드 씨, 이 일이 잊히는 것뿐이오."], ["All you have got to do, Mr. Copperfield, is to forget it.’", "당신이 해야 할 일은 오직, 코퍼필드 씨, 이 일을 잊는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["All!", "오직!"], ["In the note I wrote to Miss Mills, I bitterly quoted this sentiment.", "밀스 양에게 쓴 쪽지에서 나는 이 말을 비통하게 인용했다."], ["All I had to do, I said, with gloomy sarcasm, was to forget Dora. That was all, and what was that!", "내가 해야 할 일은 오직, 하고 나는 음울한 빈정거림을 담아 말했다, 도라를 잊는 것뿐이라고."], ["I entreated Miss Mills to see me, that evening. If it could not be done with Mr.", "그게 다라고, 한데 그게 무슨 말이냐고! 나는 밀스 양에게 그날 저녁 나를 만나 달라고 간청했다."], ["Mills’s sanction and concurrence, I besought a clandestine interview in the back kitchen where the Mangle was.", "만약 밀스 씨의 허락과 동의 아래 그것이 안 된다면, 짤주기가 놓인 뒤편 부엌에서라도 몰래 만나 달라고 애원했다."], ["I informed her that my reason was tottering on its throne, and only she, Miss Mills, could prevent its being deposed.", "나는 그녀에게, 내 이성이 그 옥좌 위에서 휘청거리고 있으며, 오직 그녀, 밀스 양만이 그것이 폐위되는 것을 막을 수 있노라고 알렸다."], ["I signed myself, hers distractedly; and I couldn’t help feeling, while I read this composition over, before sending it by a porter, that it was something in the style of Mr. Micawber.", "나는 미칠 듯한 그녀의 것, 이라고 서명했다. 그리고 이 글을 짐꾼 편에 부치기 전에 다시 읽어 보면서, 어딘가 미코버 씨의 문체를 빼닮았다는 느낌을 떨칠 수가 없었다."]], [["However, I sent it. At night I repaired to Miss Mills’s street, and walked up and down, until I was stealthily fetched in by Miss Mills’s maid, and taken the area way to the back kitchen.", "* 짤주기(맹글): 빨래의 물기를 짜내거나 다림질하듯 펴 주는 옛날의 큰 압착 기구. 그래도 나는 그것을 보냈다."], ["I have since seen reason to believe that there was nothing on earth to prevent my going in at the front door, and being shown up into the drawing-room, except Miss Mills’s love of the romantic and mysterious.", "밤이 되자 나는 밀스 양이 사는 거리로 가서, 밀스 양의 하녀가 슬그머니 나를 데리러 나와 지하 통로를 거쳐 뒤편 부엌으로 안내할 때까지 이리저리 서성였다. 그 뒤로 나는, 밀스 양이 낭만적이고 신비로운 것을 좋아하는 취향만 아니었다면, 내가 정문으로 들어가 응접실로 안내받는 것을 세상 그 무엇도 막지 못했으리라고 믿을 만한 까닭을 알게 되었다."]], [["In the back kitchen, I raved as became me.", "뒤편 부엌에서 나는 내 처지에 어울리게 마구 헛소리를 늘어놓았다."], ["I went there, I suppose, to make a fool of myself, and I am quite sure I did it.", "짐작건대 나는 거기에 나 자신을 바보로 만들러 간 셈이었고, 분명히 그러고도 남았다."], ["Miss Mills had received a hasty note from Dora, telling her that all was discovered, and saying.", "밀스 양은 도라에게서 급히 쓴 쪽지를 받았는데, 모든 게 들통났다는 내용과 함께 \"오, 줄리아, 제발 와 줘, 어서, 어서!\""], ["‘Oh pray come to me, Julia, do, do!’ But Miss Mills, mistrusting the acceptability of her presence to the higher powers, had not yet gone; and we were all benighted in the Desert of Sahara.", "하는 말이 적혀 있었다. 그러나 밀스 양은 자기가 가도 윗분들이 반겨 줄지 미덥지 못해 아직 가지 않은 터였다. 그래서 우리는 다 함께 사하라 사막에 갇혀 어둠 속을 헤매는 신세였다."]], [["Miss Mills had a wonderful flow of words, and liked to pour them out.", "밀스 양은 놀라운 말솜씨를 지녔고, 그것을 쏟아 내기를 즐겼다."], ["I could not help feeling, though she mingled her tears with mine, that she had a dreadful luxury in our afflictions.", "그녀가 내 눈물에 자기 눈물을 보태기는 했지만, 나는 그녀가 우리의 고통 속에서 어딘가 무시무시한 호사를 누리고 있다는 느낌을 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["She petted them, as I may say, and made the most of them.", "말하자면 그녀는 그 고통을 어루만지고, 한껏 부풀려 즐겼다."], ["A deep gulf, she observed, had opened between Dora and me, and Love could only span it with its rainbow.", "그녀는 말하기를, 도라와 나 사이에 깊은 심연이 입을 벌렸고, 사랑만이 그 위에 무지개 다리를 놓을 수 있다고 했다."], ["Love must suffer in this stern world; it ever had been so, it ever would be so.", "사랑은 이 모진 세상에서 마땅히 고통받기 마련이니, 늘 그래 왔고 앞으로도 늘 그러하리라고."], ["No matter, Miss Mills remarked.", "하지만 상관없다고 밀스 양은 말했다."], ["Hearts confined by cobwebs would burst at last, and then Love was avenged.", "거미줄에 갇힌 가슴은 끝내 터지고 말 것이며, 그때 사랑은 원수를 갚는다고."]], [["This was small consolation, but Miss Mills wouldn’t encourage fallacious hopes.", "이건 보잘것없는 위로였지만, 밀스 양은 헛된 희망을 부추기려 들지 않았다."], ["She made me much more wretched than I was before, and I felt (and told her with the deepest gratitude) that she was indeed a friend.", "그녀는 나를 전보다 훨씬 더 비참하게 만들었고, 나는 (그리고 더없이 깊은 고마움을 담아 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다) 그녀가 정말로 벗이라고 느꼈다."], ["We resolved that she should go to Dora the first thing in the morning, and find some means of assuring her, either by looks or words, of my devotion and misery.", "우리는 그녀가 이튿날 아침 제일 먼저 도라에게 가서, 눈빛으로든 말로든 내 헌신과 비참함을 어떻게든 그녀에게 전해 줄 방도를 찾기로 다짐했다."], ["We parted, overwhelmed with grief; and I think Miss Mills enjoyed herself completely.", "우리는 슬픔에 짓눌린 채 헤어졌다. 그리고 내 생각엔, 밀스 양은 더없이 흡족하게 즐긴 듯했다."]], [["I confided all to my aunt when I got home; and in spite of all she could say to me, went to bed despairing.", "집에 돌아와 나는 고모님께 모든 것을 털어놓았다. 고모님이 아무리 타일러도, 나는 절망에 빠진 채 잠자리에 들었다."], ["I got up despairing, and went out despairing.", "절망에 빠진 채 일어나, 절망에 빠진 채 집을 나섰다."], ["It was Saturday morning, and I went straight to the Commons.", "토요일 아침이었고, 나는 곧장 박사회로 갔다."]], [["I was surprised, when I came within sight of our office-door, to see the ticket-porters standing outside talking together, and some half-dozen stragglers gazing at the windows which were shut up.", "우리 사무소 문이 보이는 데까지 왔을 때, 나는 입찰 짐꾼들이 밖에 서서 서로 이야기를 나누고, 대여섯 명의 행인이 닫혀 있는 창문을 멀거니 올려다보고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["I quickened my pace, and, passing among them, wondering at their looks, went hurriedly in.", "나는 걸음을 재촉해, 그들 사이를 헤치고 지나가며 그 표정을 의아해하다가, 허둥지둥 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["The clerks were there, but nobody was doing anything.", "서기들은 거기 있었으나 아무도 일을 하고 있지 않았다."], ["Old Tiffey, for the first time in his life I should think, was sitting on somebody else’s stool, and had not hung up his hat.", "늙은 티피 영감은, 내 생각엔 평생 처음으로, 남의 의자에 걸터앉아 있었고 모자도 걸어 두지 않은 채였다."]], [["‘This is a dreadful calamity, Mr. Copperfield,’ said he, as I entered.", "\"이건 끔찍한 재앙입니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 내가 들어서자 그가 말했다."]], [["‘What is?’ I exclaimed.", "\"뭐가요?\" 내가 소리쳤다."], ["‘What’s the matter?’", "\"무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["‘Don’t you know?’ cried Tiffey, and all the rest of them, coming round me.", "\"모르십니까?\" 티피가 외쳤고, 나머지 사람들도 죄다 내 둘레로 모여들었다."]], [["‘No!’ said I, looking from face to face.", "\"몰라요!\" 내가 이 얼굴 저 얼굴을 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Spenlow,’ said Tiffey.", "\"스펜로 씨가요.\" 티피가 말했다."]], [["‘What about him!’", "\"그분이 어떻게 됐는데요!\""]], [["‘Dead!’ I thought it was the office reeling, and not I, as one of the clerks caught hold of me.", "\"돌아가셨습니다!\" 서기 하나가 나를 붙드는 순간, 나는 흔들리는 것이 내가 아니라 사무소라고 생각했다."], ["They sat me down in a chair, untied my neck-cloth, and brought me some water.", "그들은 나를 의자에 앉히고, 목수건을 풀어 주고, 물을 좀 가져다주었다."], ["I have no idea whether this took any time. ‘Dead?’ said I.", "이 모든 일에 시간이 얼마나 걸렸는지 나는 전혀 모르겠다."]], [["‘He dined in town yesterday, and drove down in the phaeton by himself,’ said Tiffey, ‘having sent his own groom home by the coach, as he sometimes did, you know--’", "\"돌아가셨다고요?\" 내가 말했다. \"어제 시내에서 식사를 하시고, 사륜마차를 손수 몰아 내려가셨답니다.\" 티피가 말했다. \"아시다시피 가끔 그러시듯이, 마부는 역마차 편에 먼저 집으로 돌려보내시고요—\""]], [["‘Well?’", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["‘The phaeton went home without him.", "\"마차가 그분 없이 집에 돌아온 겁니다."], ["The horses stopped at the stable-gate.", "말들이 마구간 문 앞에 멈춰 섰지요."], ["The man went out with a lantern. Nobody in the carriage.’", "마부가 등불을 들고 나가 보니, 마차 안엔 아무도 없었던 거예요.\""]], [["‘Had they run away?’", "\"말들이 달아났던 겁니까?\""]], [["‘They were not hot,’ said Tiffey, putting on his glasses; ‘no hotter, I understand, than they would have been, going down at the usual pace.", "\"열이 올라 있지 않았어요.\" 티피가 안경을 쓰며 말했다. \"내가 듣기로는, 여느 때 속도로 내려갈 때보다 조금도 더 열이 오른 게 아니었답니다."], ["The reins were broken, but they had been dragging on the ground.", "고삐는 끊어져 있었지만 땅에 질질 끌린 흔적이 있었고요."], ["The house was roused up directly, and three of them went out along the road. They found him a mile off.’", "집안이 곧장 발칵 뒤집혀, 셋이 길을 따라 나섰습니다. 그분을 일 마일쯤 떨어진 데서 찾았지요.\""]], [["‘More than a mile off, Mr.", "\"일 마일도 넘었습니다, 티피 씨.\""], ["Tiffey,’ interposed a junior.", "한 젊은 서기가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Was it?", "\"그랬던가?"], ["I believe you are right,’ said Tiffey,--‘more than a mile off--not far from the church--lying partly on the roadside, and partly on the path, upon his face.", "자네 말이 맞을 거야.\" 티피가 말했다. \"일 마일도 넘는 곳에서 — 교회에서 멀지 않은 데서 — 한쪽은 길가에, 한쪽은 보도에 걸친 채, 엎드린 모습으로요."], ["Whether he fell out in a fit, or got out, feeling ill before the fit came on--or even whether he was quite dead then, though there is no doubt he was quite insensible--no one appears to know.", "발작이 일어나 마차에서 떨어진 건지, 아니면 발작이 닥치기 전에 몸이 안 좋아서 내린 건지 — 심지어 그때 이미 완전히 돌아가셨던 건지조차도 — 의식이 전혀 없었던 것은 분명하지만 — 아무도 모르는 것 같습니다."], ["If he breathed, certainly he never spoke. Medical assistance was got as soon as possible, but it was quite useless.’", "설령 숨은 붙어 있었다 해도, 한마디도 못 하신 것만은 확실하고요. 되도록 빨리 의료진을 불렀지만, 다 소용없었답니다.\""]], [["I cannot describe the state of mind into which I was thrown by this intelligence.", "이 소식이 나를 어떤 심정으로 몰아넣었는지 나는 다 말로 옮길 수가 없다."], ["The shock of such an event happening so suddenly, and happening to one with whom I had been in any respect at variance--the appalling vacancy in the room he had occupied so lately, where his chair and table seemed to wait for him, and his handwriting of yesterday was like a ghost--the indefinable impossibility of separating him from the place, and feeling, when the door opened, as if he might come in--the lazy hush and rest there was in the office, and the insatiable relish with which our people talked about it, and other people came in and out all day, and gorged themselves with the subject--this is easily intelligible to anyone.", "그토록 갑작스레, 그것도 어느 면에서든 나와 갈등을 빚었던 사람에게 그런 일이 닥쳤다는 충격 — 그가 바로 얼마 전까지 자리하던 방의 소름 끼치는 텅 빈 자리, 그의 의자와 책상이 그를 기다리는 듯하고, 어제 적어 둔 그의 글씨가 유령처럼 남아 있던 그 방 — 그를 그 자리에서 도무지 떼어 낼 수 없는 듯한 느낌, 그래서 문이 열리면 그가 들어올 것만 같은 느낌 — 사무소에 깃든 나른한 정적과 멈춤, 그리고 우리 사람들이 그 일을 입에 올리며 보이던 채울 줄 모르는 입맛, 온종일 사람들이 드나들며 그 화제를 게걸스레 먹어 치우던 광경 — 이런 것들은 누구나 쉽게 알아들을 것이다."], ["What I cannot describe is, how, in the innermost recesses of my own heart, I had a lurking jealousy even of Death.", "내가 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없는 것은, 내 마음 가장 깊은 구석에서 죽음마저 은근히 질투하고 있었다는 것이다."], ["How I felt as if its might would push me from my ground in Dora’s thoughts.", "그 힘이 도라의 생각 속 내 자리에서 나를 밀어낼 것만 같은 그 느낌."], ["How I was, in a grudging way I have no words for, envious of her grief.", "뭐라 말할 길 없는 옹졸한 방식으로 그녀의 슬픔을 시샘하던 그 마음."], ["How it made me restless to think of her weeping to others, or being consoled by others.", "그녀가 남들 앞에서 운다거나, 남들에게 위로받는다는 생각이 나를 안절부절못하게 만들던 그 심정."], ["How I had a grasping, avaricious wish to shut out everybody from her but myself, and to be all in all to her, at that unseasonable time of all times.", "다른 모든 때를 통틀어 가장 어울리지 않을 그 시점에, 나 말고는 누구도 그녀에게서 떼어 내고 내가 그녀의 전부가 되고 싶다는, 움켜쥐려 들고 탐욕스럽기까지 한 그 바람."]], [["In the trouble of this state of mind--not exclusively my own, I hope, but known to others--I went down to Norwood that night; and finding from one of the servants, when I made my inquiries at the door, that Miss Mills was there, got my aunt to direct a letter to her, which I wrote.", "이런 심정의 혼란 속에서 — 부디 나 혼자만의 것이 아니라 남들도 아는 것이기를 바라지만 — 나는 그날 밤 노우드로 내려갔다. 문간에서 물어보다가 하인 하나에게서 밀스 양이 거기 와 있다는 것을 알고는, 고모님께 부탁해 그녀에게 보내는 편지의 겉봉을 쓰게 하고 안의 글은 내가 썼다."], ["I deplored the untimely death of Mr. Spenlow, most sincerely, and shed tears in doing so.", "나는 스펜로 씨의 때 이른 죽음을 더없이 진심으로 애도했고, 그 글을 쓰면서 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["I entreated her to tell Dora, if Dora were in a state to hear it, that he had spoken to me with the utmost kindness and consideration; and had coupled nothing but tenderness, not a single or reproachful word, with her name.", "나는 그녀에게, 만약 도라가 들을 만한 상태라면, 그가 나에게 더없이 다정하고 사려 깊게 말했으며, 그녀의 이름과 더불어 다정함 말고는 아무것도, 단 한마디 책망도 곁들이지 않았노라고 전해 달라고 간청했다."], ["I know I did this selfishly, to have my name brought before her; but I tried to believe it was an act of justice to his memory.", "내가 이렇게 한 것이, 내 이름을 그녀 앞에 들이밀려는 이기심에서였다는 걸 안다. 그러나 나는 그것이 그를 기리는 정당한 일이라고 믿으려 애썼다."], ["Perhaps I did believe it.", "어쩌면 정말 그렇게 믿었는지도 모른다."]], [["My aunt received a few lines next day in reply; addressed, outside, to her; within, to me.", "이튿날 고모님은 답장으로 몇 줄을 받았다. 겉봉에는 고모님 앞으로, 안에는 나에게 쓴 글이었다. 도라는 슬픔에 짓눌려 있었다."], ["Dora was overcome by grief; and when her friend had asked her should she send her love to me, had only cried, as she was always crying, ‘Oh, dear papa!", "그리고 그 벗이 나에게 사랑을 전할까 하고 물었을 때, 그녀는 늘 그러듯 그저 \"오, 사랑하는 아빠! 오, 가엾은 아빠!\" 하고 울기만 했다고 한다."], ["oh, poor papa!’ But she had not said No, and that I made the most of.", "그러나 그녀는 안 된다고 말하지는 않았으니, 나는 그 점을 최대한 부풀려 새겼다."]], [["Mr. Jorkins, who had been at Norwood since the occurrence, came to the office a few days afterwards.", "그 일이 있은 뒤로 줄곧 노우드에 머물던 조킨스 씨가, 며칠 지나 사무소로 왔다."], ["He and Tiffey were closeted together for some few moments, and then Tiffey looked out at the door and beckoned me in.", "그와 티피는 한동안 둘이서 방에 틀어박혀 있었고, 이윽고 티피가 문밖으로 고개를 내밀어 나를 손짓으로 불러들였다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Mr. Jorkins. ‘Mr.", "\"오!\" 조킨스 씨가 말했다."], ["Tiffey and myself, Mr. Copperfield, are about to examine the desks, the drawers, and other such repositories of the deceased, with the view of sealing up his private papers, and searching for a Will.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 티피 씨와 나는 고인의 책상과 서랍, 그 밖에 그런 보관함들을 살펴보려 합니다. 그분의 개인 서류를 봉인하고 유언장을 찾으려는 거지요."], ["There is no trace of any, elsewhere. It may be as well for you to assist us, if you please.’", "다른 데서는 그런 게 있다는 흔적이 전혀 없으니까요. 괜찮으시다면 당신이 우리를 거들어 주는 것도 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["I had been in agony to obtain some knowledge of the circumstances in which my Dora would be placed--as, in whose guardianship, and so forth--and this was something towards it.", "나는 우리 도라가 어떤 처지에 놓일지 — 누구의 후견을 받게 될지 따위 — 무어라도 알아내려고 애가 타던 참이라, 이것은 그 실마리가 될 만했다."], ["We began the search at once; Mr.", "우리는 곧장 수색을 시작했다."], ["Jorkins unlocking the drawers and desks, and we all taking out the papers.", "조킨스 씨가 서랍과 책상을 열고, 우리 모두가 서류를 꺼냈다."], ["The office-papers we placed on one side, and the private papers (which were not numerous) on the other.", "사무소 서류는 한쪽에, 개인 서류는(수가 많지 않았다) 다른 쪽에 놓았다."], ["We were very grave; and when we came to a stray seal, or pencil-case, or ring, or any little article of that kind which we associated personally with him, we spoke very low.", "우리는 무척 엄숙했다. 그리고 외따로 떨어진 인장이나 연필집, 반지, 또는 그가 떠오르는 그런 자잘한 물건이 나오면 우리는 목소리를 잔뜩 낮추었다."]], [["We had sealed up several packets; and were still going on dustily and quietly, when Mr.", "우리는 몇 묶음을 봉인했고, 여전히 먼지 속에서 조용히 일을 이어 가던 차에, 조킨스 씨가 우리에게 말했다."], ["Jorkins said to us, applying exactly the same words to his late partner as his late partner had applied to him:", "죽은 동업자가 자기에게 쓰던 바로 그 말을, 토씨 하나 안 틀리고 그 죽은 동업자에게 그대로 갖다 붙이면서."]], [["‘Mr. Spenlow was very difficult to move from the beaten track.", "\"스펜로 씨는 정해진 길에서 벗어나게 하기가 무척 어려운 분이었지요."], ["You know what he was! I am disposed to think he had made no will.’", "그분이 어떤 양반이었는지 잘 아시지 않습니까! 내 생각엔 유언장을 안 만드셨지 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, I know he had!’ said I.", "\"오, 만드신 게 분명한데요!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["They both stopped and looked at me.", "두 사람이 다 멈춰 서서 나를 바라보았다."], ["‘On the very day when I last saw him,’ said I, ‘he told me that he had, and that his affairs were long since settled.’", "\"제가 그분을 마지막으로 뵌 바로 그날,\" 내가 말했다. \"그분이 직접 저에게, 유언장을 만들어 두었고 자기 일들은 진작에 정리되어 있다고 말씀하셨습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Jorkins and old Tiffey shook their heads with one accord.", "조킨스 씨와 늙은 티피는 약속이라도 한 듯 한꺼번에 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘That looks unpromising,’ said Tiffey.", "\"그건 영 불길한 얘긴데요.\" 티피가 말했다."]], [["‘Very unpromising,’ said Mr. Jorkins.", "\"아주 불길하지요.\" 조킨스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Surely you don’t doubt--’ I began.", "\"설마 두 분은 의심하시는—\" 내가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘My good Mr.", "\"이보세요, 코퍼필드 씨!\""], ["Copperfield!’ said Tiffey, laying his hand upon my arm, and shutting up both his eyes as he shook his head: ‘if you had been in the Commons as long as I have, you would know that there is no subject on which men are so inconsistent, and so little to be trusted.’", "티피가 내 팔에 손을 얹고, 고개를 가로저으며 두 눈을 다 감은 채 말했다. \"당신도 나만큼 오래 박사회에 있어 봤다면, 사람이 이만큼 일관성 없고 이만큼 믿을 수 없는 주제도 없다는 걸 알게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Why, bless my soul, he made that very remark!’ I replied persistently.", "\"아니, 세상에, 그분이 바로 그 말씀을 하셨다니까요!\" 나는 고집스레 대꾸했다."]], [["‘I should call that almost final,’ observed Tiffey.", "\"그거라면 거의 결론을 내린 거나 다름없군요.\" 티피가 한마디 했다."], ["‘My opinion is--no will.’", "\"내 생각엔 — 유언장은 없습니다.\""]], [["It appeared a wonderful thing to me, but it turned out that there was no will.", "나에게는 놀라운 일로 여겨졌으나, 결국 유언장은 없었던 것으로 드러났다."], ["He had never so much as thought of making one, so far as his papers afforded any evidence; for there was no kind of hint, sketch, or memorandum, of any testamentary intention whatever.", "그가 남긴 서류로 보건대, 그는 그런 것을 만들 생각조차 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다. 유언상의 뜻이라곤 그 어떤 종류의 암시도, 초안도, 메모도 없었으니까."], ["What was scarcely less astonishing to me, was, that his affairs were in a most disordered state.", "그에 못지않게 나를 놀라게 한 것은, 그의 일들이 더없이 엉망인 상태였다는 점이다."], ["It was extremely difficult, I heard, to make out what he owed, or what he had paid, or of what he died possessed.", "그가 빚진 것이 얼마인지, 갚은 것이 얼마인지, 죽을 때 무엇을 지니고 있었는지를 가려내기가 무척 어려웠다고 한다."], ["It was considered likely that for years he could have had no clear opinion on these subjects himself.", "십중팔구 그 자신도 여러 해 동안 이런 문제에 대해 분명한 생각을 가져 본 적이 없었으리라고들 여겼다."], ["By little and little it came out, that, in the competition on all points of appearance and gentility then running high in the Commons, he had spent more than his professional income, which was not a very large one, and had reduced his private means, if they ever had been great (which was exceedingly doubtful), to a very low ebb indeed.", "차츰차츰 드러난 바로는, 당시 박사회에서 겉치레와 점잖음을 두고 모든 면에서 한창 치열하던 경쟁 속에서, 그는 그리 많지도 않은 자기 직업 수입을 넘게 써 버렸고, 설령 한때 넉넉했다 하더라도(이는 몹시 의심스러웠지만) 개인 재산을 정말이지 바닥까지 갉아먹었던 것이다. 노우드에서는 가구와 임차권을 처분하는 경매가 열렸다."], ["There was a sale of the furniture and lease, at Norwood; and Tiffey told me, little thinking how interested I was in the story, that, paying all the just debts of the deceased, and deducting his share of outstanding bad and doubtful debts due to the firm, he wouldn’t give a thousand pounds for all the assets remaining.", "그리고 티피는, 내가 그 이야기에 얼마나 마음이 쓰이는지 까맣게 모른 채, 고인의 정당한 빚을 다 갚고 회사에 들어올 떼이거나 받을지 의심스러운 채권 가운데 그의 몫을 제하고 나면, 남은 자산 전부를 다 합쳐도 천 파운드를 쳐주지 않겠다고 나에게 일러 주었다."]], [["This was at the expiration of about six weeks.", "이것은 약 여섯 주가 지난 무렵의 일이었다."], ["I had suffered tortures all the time; and thought I really must have laid violent hands upon myself, when Miss Mills still reported to me, that my broken-hearted little Dora would say nothing, when I was mentioned, but ‘Oh, poor papa!", "그동안 나는 줄곧 고문에 시달렸다. 그리고 밀스 양이, 가슴이 찢어진 내 어린 도라가 내 이야기가 나오면 그저 \"오, 가엾은 아빠! 오, 사랑하는 아빠!\""], ["Oh, dear papa!’ Also, that she had no other relations than two aunts, maiden sisters of Mr. Spenlow, who lived at Putney, and who had not held any other than chance communication with their brother for many years.", "할 뿐 다른 말은 한마디도 않는다고 여전히 전해 줄 때면, 나는 정말이지 내 손으로 나 자신을 해치고야 말 것만 같았다. 또한 그녀에게는 스펜로 씨의 미혼 누이인 두 고모 말고는 다른 친척이 없는데, 그들은 퍼트니에 살았고 여러 해 동안 자기 오라비와 그저 어쩌다 한 번 연락할 뿐이었다는 것도 전해 들었다."], ["Not that they had ever quarrelled (Miss Mills informed me); but that having been, on the occasion of Dora’s christening, invited to tea, when they considered themselves privileged to be invited to dinner, they had expressed their opinion in writing, that it was ‘better for the happiness of all parties’ that they should stay away.", "둘이 다툰 적이 있어서가 아니라(밀스 양이 일러 주었다), 도라의 세례식 때 자기들은 만찬에 초대받을 자격이 있다고 여겼는데 차나 마시러 오라고 초대받은 일이 있어서, 글로 자기들 의견을 밝히기를, \"모두의 행복을 위해서\" 자기들이 오지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 했다는 것이다."], ["Since which they had gone their road, and their brother had gone his.", "그 후로 그들은 그들 길을 갔고, 그들의 오라비는 그의 길을 갔다."]], [["These two ladies now emerged from their retirement, and proposed to take Dora to live at Putney.", "이 두 부인이 이제 칩거를 깨고 나타나, 도라를 데려가 퍼트니에서 함께 살자고 제안했다."], ["Dora, clinging to them both, and weeping, exclaimed, ‘O yes, aunts!", "도라는 두 사람에게 매달려 울며 외쳤다. \"오, 그래요, 고모님들!"], ["Please take Julia Mills and me and Jip to Putney!’ So they went, very soon after the funeral.", "제발 줄리아 밀스와 저와 지프를 퍼트니로 데려가 주세요!\" 그래서 그들은 장례식이 끝나고 머지않아 떠났다."]], [["How I found time to haunt Putney, I am sure I don’t know; but I contrived, by some means or other, to prowl about the neighbourhood pretty often.", "내가 어떻게 짬을 내어 퍼트니를 맴돌았는지는 나도 정말 모르겠다. 하지만 어떻게든 그 동네를 꽤 자주 어슬렁거리는 데 용케 성공했다."], ["Miss Mills, for the more exact discharge of the duties of friendship, kept a journal; and she used to meet me sometimes, on the Common, and read it, or (if she had not time to do that) lend it to me. How I treasured up the entries, of which I subjoin a sample--!", "밀스 양은 우정의 의무를 더 빈틈없이 다하려고 일기를 썼는데, 더러는 그 공유지에서 나를 만나 그것을 읽어 주거나, (그럴 짬이 없으면) 나에게 빌려주곤 했다. 내가 그 기록들을 얼마나 애지중지 간직했던지 — 여기 그 한 자락을 옮겨 적는다!"]], [["‘Monday.", "\"월요일."], ["My sweet D. still much depressed. Headache.", "내 사랑스러운 도라는 여전히 몹시 풀이 죽어 있음. 두통."], ["Called attention to J. as being beautifully sleek. D.", "지프가 어여쁘게 윤이 난다고 주의를 끌어 줌."], ["fondled J. Associations thus awakened, opened floodgates of sorrow.", "도라가 지프를 어루만짐. 그리하여 깨어난 추억이 슬픔의 봇물을 열어젖힘."], ["Rush of grief admitted.", "비탄이 밀려듦."], ["(Are tears the dewdrops of the heart? J. M.)", "(눈물은 마음의 이슬방울인가? 줄리아 밀스.)"]], [["‘Tuesday. D. weak and nervous.", "\"화요일. 도라는 기운 없고 신경이 곤두서 있음."], ["Beautiful in pallor.", "창백한 모습이 아름다움."], ["(Do we not remark this in moon likewise? J.", "(우리는 달에게서도 이를 보지 않는가?"], ["M.) D., J. M. and J. took airing in carriage. J.", "줄리아 밀스.) 도라와 줄리아 밀스와 지프, 마차로 바람을 쐼."], ["looking out of window, and barking violently at dustman, occasioned smile to overspread features of D.", "지프가 창밖을 내다보며 청소부에게 사납게 짖어 대니, 도라의 얼굴에 미소가 번짐."], ["(Of such slight links is chain of life composed! J. M.)", "(생명의 사슬은 이토록 보잘것없는 고리들로 엮여 있구나! 줄리아 밀스.)"]], [["‘Wednesday. D.", "\"수요일."], ["comparatively cheerful.", "도라는 비교적 명랑함."], ["Sang to her, as congenial melody, “Evening Bells”.", "어울릴 만한 가락이다 싶어 그녀에게 '저녁 종소리'를 불러 줌."], ["Effect not soothing, but reverse. D. inexpressibly affected.", "효과는 달래기는커녕 정반대. 도라가 형언할 수 없이 북받침."], ["Found sobbing afterwards, in own room.", "나중에 제 방에서 흐느끼고 있는 것을 발견."], ["Quoted verses respecting self and young Gazelle.", "자신과 어린 가젤에 관한 시구를 읊어 줌. 헛수고."], ["Ineffectually. Also referred to Patience on Monument. (Qy.", "기념비 위의 인내상도 들먹여 봄. (의문. 왜 하필 기념비 위에?"], ["Why on monument? J. M.)", "줄리아 밀스.)"]], [["‘Thursday. D.", "\"목요일."], ["certainly improved.", "도라는 확실히 나아짐."], ["Better night.", "밤잠을 더 잘 잠."], ["Slight tinge of damask revisiting cheek.", "뺨에 발그레한 다마스크 빛이 살짝 되돌아옴."], ["Resolved to mention name of D. C.", "디 시의 이름을 꺼내 보기로 결심."], ["Introduced same, cautiously, in course of airing. D.", "바람을 쐬는 동안 조심스레 그 이름을 끄집어냄."], ["immediately overcome.", "도라가 즉시 무너짐."], ["“Oh, dear, dear Julia! Oh, I have been a naughty and undutiful child!”", "'오, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 줄리아! 오, 나는 못되고 불효한 딸이었어!'"], ["Soothed and caressed.", "달래고 어루만짐."], ["Drew ideal picture of D. C. on verge of tomb. D.", "무덤 가장자리에 선 디 시의 모습을 상상으로 그려 줌."], ["again overcome. “Oh, what shall I do, what shall I do?", "도라가 다시 무너짐. '오, 어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아?"], ["Oh, take me somewhere!” Much alarmed.", "오, 나를 어디로든 데려가 줘!'"], ["Fainting of D.", "몹시 놀람."], ["and glass of water from public-house. (Poetical affinity.", "도라가 실신해 선술집에서 물 한 잔 얻어 옴. (시적인 인연."], ["Chequered sign on door-post; chequered human life. Alas! J. M.)", "문기둥의 격자무늬 간판, 격자무늬 같은 인생사. 아아! 줄리아 밀스.)"]], [["‘Friday.", "\"금요일."], ["Day of incident. Man appears in kitchen, with blue bag, “for lady’s boots left out to heel”.", "사건의 날. 한 사내가 파란 자루를 들고 부엌에 나타나, '굽을 고치라고 내놓은 아씨 부츠를 가지러 왔다'고 함."], ["Cook replies, “No such orders.” Man argues point.", "요리사가 답하길, '그런 분부 없었다.' 사내가 따짐."], ["Cook withdraws to inquire, leaving man alone with J.", "요리사가 알아보러 물러가며, 사내를 지프와 단둘이 남겨 둠."], ["On Cook’s return, man still argues point, but ultimately goes. J.", "요리사가 돌아오니 사내는 여전히 따지다가 결국 가 버림."], ["missing. D.", "지프가 사라짐."], ["distracted. Information sent to police.", "도라가 제정신이 아님. 경찰에 신고함."], ["Man to be identified by broad nose, and legs like balustrades of bridge.", "사내의 인상착의는 넓적한 코와 다리 난간 같은 두 다리."], ["Search made in every direction. No J. D.", "사방으로 수색. 지프는 없음."], ["weeping bitterly, and inconsolable.", "도라가 슬피 울며 달래지지 않음."], ["Renewed reference to young Gazelle.", "어린 가젤을 다시 들먹임. 적절하나 소용없음."], ["Appropriate, but unavailing.", "저녁 무렵 낯선 소년이 찾아옴."], ["Towards evening, strange boy calls. Brought into parlour.", "거실로 들임. 넓적한 코는 맞으나 난간 같은 다리는 아님."], ["Broad nose, but no balustrades.", "1파운드를 원하며 개를 안다고 함."], ["Says he wants a pound, and knows a dog.", "거듭 채근해도 더는 설명하려 들지 않음."], ["Declines to explain further, though much pressed. Pound being produced by D.", "도라가 1파운드를 내놓자 요리사를 어느 작은 집으로 데려감."], ["takes Cook to little house, where J.", "거기서 지프가 탁자 다리에 홀로 묶여 있었음."], ["alone tied up to leg of table. Joy of D. who dances round J.", "도라의 기쁨, 지프가 저녁을 먹는 동안 그 둘레를 빙빙 돎."], ["while he eats his supper. Emboldened by this happy change, mention D. C.", "이 반가운 변화에 용기를 얻어, 위층에서 디 시 이야기를 꺼냄."], ["upstairs. D. weeps afresh, cries piteously, “Oh, don’t, don’t, don’t!", "도라가 다시 울며 애처롭게 외침. '오, 하지 마, 하지 마, 하지 마!"], ["It is so wicked to think of anything but poor papa!”--embraces J.", "가엾은 아빠 말고 다른 걸 생각하다니, 너무 못된 짓이야!'"], ["and sobs herself to sleep.", "— 지프를 끌어안고 흐느끼다 잠듦."], ["(Must not D. C. confine himself to the broad pinions of Time? J. M.)’", "(디 시는 시간의 너른 날개에 자신을 맡겨야 하지 않을까? 줄리아 밀스.)\""]], [["Miss Mills and her journal were my sole consolation at this period.", "밀스 양과 그녀의 일기는 이 무렵 나의 유일한 위안이었다."], ["To see her, who had seen Dora but a little while before--to trace the initial letter of Dora’s name through her sympathetic pages--to be made more and more miserable by her--were my only comforts.", "얼마 전까지 도라를 보았던 그녀를 만나는 것, 그녀의 공감 어린 책장 사이로 도라 이름의 첫 글자를 더듬어 보는 것, 그녀 때문에 점점 더 비참해지는 것 — 그것이 나의 유일한 위로였다."], ["I felt as if I had been living in a palace of cards, which had tumbled down, leaving only Miss Mills and me among the ruins; I felt as if some grim enchanter had drawn a magic circle round the innocent goddess of my heart, which nothing indeed but those same strong pinions, capable of carrying so many people over so much, would enable me to enter!", "나는 마치 카드로 지은 궁전에서 살아왔는데, 그것이 와르르 무너져 폐허 속에 밀스 양과 나만 남겨진 듯한 느낌이었다. 마치 어떤 험상궂은 마법사가 내 마음속 순결한 여신 둘레에 마법의 원을 그어 놓아서, 그토록 많은 사람을 그토록 먼 거리까지 실어 나를 수 있는 바로 그 억센 날개가 아니고서는 그 무엇으로도 내가 그 안에 들어설 수 없을 것만 같았다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 39. WICKFIELD AND HEEP", "# CHAPTER 39. WICKFIELD AND HEEP"]], [["My aunt, beginning, I imagine, to be made seriously uncomfortable by my prolonged dejection, made a pretence of being anxious that I should go to Dover, to see that all was working well at the cottage, which was let; and to conclude an agreement, with the same tenant, for a longer term of occupation.", "고모님은, 내 짐작에, 오래 끄는 내 우울 때문에 정말로 마음이 불편해지기 시작하셨던 모양인지, 내가 도버에 가서 세를 놓은 그 오두막이 다 잘 돌아가는지 살펴보고, 같은 세입자와 더 오래 사는 조건으로 계약을 마무리짓고 오기를 바라는 척하셨다."], ["Janet was drafted into the service of Mrs. Strong, where I saw her every day.", "재닛은 스트롱 부인의 집에 일하러 보내졌고, 그곳에서 나는 그 애를 날마다 보았다."], ["She had been undecided, on leaving Dover, whether or no to give the finishing touch to that renunciation of mankind in which she had been educated, by marrying a pilot; but she decided against that venture.", "도버를 떠날 무렵 재닛은, 자기가 받아 온 그 인간 거부 교육의 마지막 마무리로 도선사와 결혼할까 말까 망설이고 있었으나, 끝내 그 모험은 하지 않기로 정했다."], ["Not so much for the sake of principle, I believe, as because she happened not to like him.", "내 생각에 그건 무슨 신념 때문이라기보다, 그저 그 남자가 마음에 들지 않았기 때문이었다."]], [["Although it required an effort to leave Miss Mills, I fell rather willingly into my aunt’s pretence, as a means of enabling me to pass a few tranquil hours with Agnes.", "밀스 양과 헤어지는 것은 마음을 다잡아야 하는 일이었지만, 나는 고모님의 그 핑계에 차라리 기꺼이 따랐다. 그래야 아그네스와 더불어 평온한 몇 시간을 보낼 수 있었으니까."], ["I consulted the good Doctor relative to an absence of three days; and the Doctor wishing me to take that relaxation,--he wished me to take more; but my energy could not bear that,--I made up my mind to go.", "나는 사흘쯤 자리를 비우는 일을 두고 마음씨 좋은 박사님과 상의했다. 박사님은 내가 그렇게 쉬는 것을 바라셨고 — 사실은 더 쉬기를 바라셨지만 나는 기운이 그걸 견디지 못했다 — 그래서 나는 가기로 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["As to the Commons, I had no great occasion to be particular about my duties in that quarter.", "민법 박사회 쪽 일로 말하자면, 그 방면에서 내 직무를 굳이 꼼꼼히 챙길 까닭이 별로 없었다."], ["To say the truth, we were getting in no very good odour among the tip-top proctors, and were rapidly sliding down to but a doubtful position. The business had been indifferent under Mr.", "사실대로 말하자면, 우리는 일류 소송 대리인들 사이에서 그리 좋은 평판을 얻지 못하고 있었고, 빠르게 미끄러져 미심쩍은 자리로 떨어지고 있었다."], ["Jorkins, before Mr. Spenlow’s time; and although it had been quickened by the infusion of new blood, and by the display which Mr. Spenlow made, still it was not established on a sufficiently strong basis to bear, without being shaken, such a blow as the sudden loss of its active manager.", "스펜로 씨가 들어오기 전, 조킨스 씨 밑에서도 사업은 시원찮았고, 비록 새 피가 수혈되고 스펜로 씨가 보여 준 화려한 겉치레 덕에 한동안 살아나기는 했어도, 활동적인 운영자가 갑자기 사라지는 그런 타격을 흔들림 없이 견딜 만큼 단단한 토대 위에 서 있지는 못했다."], ["It fell off very much. Mr.", "사업은 크게 기울었다."], ["Jorkins, notwithstanding his reputation in the firm, was an easy-going, incapable sort of man, whose reputation out of doors was not calculated to back it up.", "조킨스 씨는 회사 안에서의 명성에도 불구하고 무사태평하고 무능한 사람이었으며, 바깥에서의 평판은 그 명성을 떠받쳐 줄 만한 것이 못 되었다."], ["I was turned over to him now, and when I saw him take his snuff and let the business go, I regretted my aunt’s thousand pounds more than ever.", "나는 이제 그에게 넘겨졌는데, 그가 코담배나 들이마시며 일을 내버려 두는 꼴을 보고 있노라니, 고모님의 천 파운드가 그 어느 때보다 더 아까웠다."]], [["But this was not the worst of it.", "그러나 이게 최악은 아니었다."], ["There were a number of hangers-on and outsiders about the Commons, who, without being proctors themselves, dabbled in common-form business, and got it done by real proctors, who lent their names in consideration of a share in the spoil;--and there were a good many of these too.", "민법 박사회 언저리에는 빌붙어 사는 자들과 바깥 사람들이 한 무리 있었는데, 이들은 정작 자기가 소송 대리인은 아니면서 일반 서식 업무에 손을 댔고, 떡고물 한몫을 받는 대가로 이름을 빌려 주는 진짜 대리인을 통해 그 일을 처리했다 — 그런 자들도 꽤 많았다."], ["As our house now wanted business on any terms, we joined this noble band; and threw out lures to the hangers-on and outsiders, to bring their business to us.", "우리 회사도 이제는 어떤 조건으로든 일거리가 아쉬웠던 터라, 우리도 이 고결한 무리에 끼어들어, 그 빌붙는 자들과 바깥 사람들에게 미끼를 던져 일감을 우리에게 가져오도록 꾀었다."], ["Marriage licences and small probates were what we all looked for, and what paid us best; and the competition for these ran very high indeed.", "우리 모두가 노리는 것은 혼인 허가증과 자잘한 유언 검인이었고, 그것이 가장 짭짤한 벌이였으니, 그것을 두고 경쟁이 어찌나 치열했던지 말로 다 할 수 없었다."], ["Kidnappers and inveiglers were planted in all the avenues of entrance to the Commons, with instructions to do their utmost to cut off all persons in mourning, and all gentlemen with anything bashful in their appearance, and entice them to the offices in which their respective employers were interested; which instructions were so well observed, that I myself, before I was known by sight, was twice hustled into the premises of our principal opponent.", "민법 박사회로 들어오는 길목마다 호객꾼과 유인꾼이 진을 치고서, 상복 입은 사람이라면 누구든, 또 행색에 조금이라도 수줍은 구석이 있는 신사라면 누구든 가로채어, 제각기 자기 고용주가 관심을 둔 사무소로 꾀어 오라는 지시를 받고 있었다. 그 지시가 어찌나 잘 지켜졌던지, 나조차도 얼굴이 알려지기 전에 두 번이나 우리 회사의 주된 경쟁사 안으로 떠밀려 들어간 적이 있었다."], ["The conflicting interests of these touting gentlemen being of a nature to irritate their feelings, personal collisions took place; and the Commons was even scandalized by our principal inveigler (who had formerly been in the wine trade, and afterwards in the sworn brokery line) walking about for some days with a black eye.", "이렇게 호객하는 신사들의 이해가 서로 부딪치다 보니 감정이 상하는 일이 잦았고, 그러다 몸싸움까지 벌어졌다. 우리 회사의 주된 유인꾼(예전에 포도주 장사를 했고, 그 뒤에는 공인 중개업에 몸담았던 자다)이 며칠 동안 눈에 시퍼런 멍을 달고 돌아다니는 바람에 민법 박사회가 망신을 당하기도 했다."], ["Any one of these scouts used to think nothing of politely assisting an old lady in black out of a vehicle, killing any proctor whom she inquired for, representing his employer as the lawful successor and representative of that proctor, and bearing the old lady off (sometimes greatly affected) to his employer’s office.", "이런 끄나풀들 가운데 누구든, 검은 상복 차림의 노부인을 마차에서 정중히 부축해 내려 준 다음, 노부인이 찾는 소송 대리인을 (말로) 죽여 버리고는, 자기 고용주가 바로 그 대리인의 정당한 후계자이자 대리인이라고 둘러대며, 노부인을 (때로는 몹시 감격해하는 노부인을) 자기 고용주의 사무소로 끌고 가는 일쯤은 아무렇지도 않게 여겼다."], ["Many captives were brought to me in this way.", "이런 식으로 끌려온 포로가 내게도 여럿 넘겨졌다."], ["As to marriage licences, the competition rose to such a pitch, that a shy gentleman in want of one, had nothing to do but submit himself to the first inveigler, or be fought for, and become the prey of the strongest.", "혼인 허가증으로 말하자면, 경쟁이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 허가증이 필요한 수줍은 신사로서는 맨 처음 만난 유인꾼에게 그대로 몸을 맡기거나, 아니면 쟁탈전의 대상이 되어 가장 힘센 자의 먹이가 되는 수밖에 없었다."], ["One of our clerks, who was an outsider, used, in the height of this contest, to sit with his hat on, that he might be ready to rush out and swear before a surrogate any victim who was brought in.", "우리 회사의 한 서기는 바깥 사람이었는데, 이 다툼이 한창일 때면 모자를 쓴 채 앉아 있곤 했다. 누구든 잡혀 들어오면 곧장 뛰쳐나가 대리 판사 앞에서 선서시킬 채비를 해 두려는 것이었다."], ["The system of inveigling continues, I believe, to this day.", "이 유인 수법은 내 생각에 오늘날까지도 이어지고 있다."], ["The last time I was in the Commons, a civil able-bodied person in a white apron pounced out upon me from a doorway, and whispering the word ‘Marriage-licence’ in my ear, was with great difficulty prevented from taking me up in his arms and lifting me into a proctor’s.", "내가 마지막으로 민법 박사회에 갔을 때도, 흰 앞치마를 두른 점잖고 건장한 사내 하나가 문간에서 내게 와락 달려들더니, 귀에 대고 \"혼인 허가증\"이라고 속삭이며, 하마터면 나를 두 팔로 번쩍 들어 어느 소송 대리인의 사무소로 옮겨 갈 뻔한 것을 간신히 막았다."], ["From this digression, let me proceed to Dover.", "이 여담은 이쯤 하고, 도버 이야기로 넘어가자."]], [["I found everything in a satisfactory state at the cottage; and was enabled to gratify my aunt exceedingly by reporting that the tenant inherited her feud, and waged incessant war against donkeys.", "오두막은 모든 것이 흡족한 상태였다. 그리고 나는 새 세입자가 고모님의 그 싸움을 물려받아 당나귀들과 끊임없는 전쟁을 벌이고 있더라고 전하여 고모님을 무척이나 기쁘게 해 드릴 수 있었다."], ["Having settled the little business I had to transact there, and slept there one night, I walked on to Canterbury early in the morning.", "그곳에서 처리할 자잘한 용무를 마치고 하룻밤을 묵은 뒤, 나는 이튿날 이른 아침에 캔터베리로 걸어갔다. 어느덧 다시 겨울이었다."], ["It was now winter again; and the fresh, cold windy day, and the sweeping downland, brightened up my hopes a little.", "상쾌하고 차가운 바람 부는 날씨와, 끝없이 펼쳐진 구릉지가 내 희망을 조금은 환하게 밝혀 주었다."]], [["Coming into Canterbury, I loitered through the old streets with a sober pleasure that calmed my spirits, and eased my heart.", "캔터베리에 들어서면서, 나는 그 오래된 거리를 천천히 거닐었는데, 그 차분한 기쁨이 마음을 가라앉히고 가슴을 어루만져 주었다."], ["There were the old signs, the old names over the shops, the old people serving in them.", "옛 간판들이 그대로 있었고, 가게 위에 걸린 옛 이름들도, 그 안에서 일하는 옛 사람들도 그대로였다."], ["It appeared so long, since I had been a schoolboy there, that I wondered the place was so little changed, until I reflected how little I was changed myself.", "내가 이곳에서 학생으로 지낸 지 어찌나 오래된 것 같던지, 이곳이 이리도 변하지 않았다는 것이 의아할 지경이었다. 그러다 나 자신이 얼마나 변하지 않았는가를 떠올리고서야 고개가 끄덕여졌다."], ["Strange to say, that quiet influence which was inseparable in my mind from Agnes, seemed to pervade even the city where she dwelt.", "묘하게도, 내 마음속에서 아그네스와 떼려야 뗄 수 없던 그 고요한 기운이, 그녀가 사는 이 도시 전체에까지 스며 있는 듯했다."], ["The venerable cathedral towers, and the old jackdaws and rooks whose airy voices made them more retired than perfect silence would have done; the battered gateways, one stuck full with statues, long thrown down, and crumbled away, like the reverential pilgrims who had gazed upon them; the still nooks, where the ivied growth of centuries crept over gabled ends and ruined walls; the ancient houses, the pastoral landscape of field, orchard, and garden; everywhere--on everything--I felt the same serener air, the same calm, thoughtful, softening spirit.", "유서 깊은 대성당의 첨탑들, 그리고 그 아련한 울음소리로 완전한 침묵보다 더 깊은 적막을 자아내던 늙은 갈까마귀와 까마귀들, 오래전 쓰러져 부서진 동상들이 빼곡히 박힌 낡은 성문들 — 그 동상들은 그것을 우러러보던 경건한 순례자들처럼 닳고 허물어져 있었다 — 수백 년 세월의 담쟁이가 박공 끝과 무너진 담벼락을 타고 기어오른 고요한 모퉁이들, 오래된 집들, 들판과 과수원과 정원이 어우러진 목가적인 풍경, 그 어디에서든, 그 모든 것에서, 나는 똑같이 더없이 잔잔한 공기를, 똑같이 고요하고 사색적이며 마음을 누그러뜨리는 기운을 느꼈다."]], [["Arrived at Mr. Wickfield’s house, I found, in the little lower room on the ground floor, where Uriah Heep had been of old accustomed to sit, Mr. Micawber plying his pen with great assiduity.", "위크필드 씨의 집에 다다라, 일 층 아래쪽 작은 방, 예전에 유라이아 힙이 늘 앉아 있던 그 방에 들어서니, 미코버 씨가 더없이 부지런히 펜을 놀리고 있었다."], ["He was dressed in a legal-looking suit of black, and loomed, burly and large, in that small office.", "그는 법조계 사람처럼 보이는 검은 양복을 차려입고서, 그 비좁은 사무실 안에서 우람하고 커다랗게 버티고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Micawber was extremely glad to see me, but a little confused too.", "미코버 씨는 나를 보고 무척이나 반가워했으나, 한편으로는 조금 당황한 기색이기도 했다."], ["He would have conducted me immediately into the presence of Uriah, but I declined.", "그는 곧장 나를 유라이아 앞으로 데려가려 했지만, 나는 사양했다."]], [["‘I know the house of old, you recollect,’ said I, ‘and will find my way upstairs.", "\"이 집은 예전부터 잘 알잖아요, 기억하시죠.\" 내가 말했다. \"위층은 알아서 찾아갈게요."], ["How do you like the law, Mr. Micawber?’", "법률 일은 좀 마음에 드세요, 미코버 씨?\""]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ he replied.", "\"이보게, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘To a man possessed of the higher imaginative powers, the objection to legal studies is the amount of detail which they involve.", "\"더 높은 상상력을 지닌 사람에게, 법률 공부의 거슬리는 점은 그것이 끌어안고 있는 자질구레한 세부 사항의 양이라네."], ["Even in our professional correspondence,’ said Mr.", "우리가 주고받는 업무 서신에서조차,\""], ["Micawber, glancing at some letters he was writing, ‘the mind is not at liberty to soar to any exalted form of expression.", "미코버 씨가 자기가 쓰던 편지 몇 통을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"정신이 어떤 드높은 표현의 경지로 날아오를 자유가 없거든."], ["Still, it is a great pursuit. A great pursuit!’", "그래도 위대한 일이야. 위대한 일이고말고!\""]], [["He then told me that he had become the tenant of Uriah Heep’s old house; and that Mrs. Micawber would be delighted to receive me, once more, under her own roof.", "이어서 그는 자기가 유라이아 힙이 예전에 살던 집의 세입자가 되었다는 것과, 미코버 부인이 자기 집 지붕 아래에서 다시금 나를 맞이하게 되어 무척 기뻐할 거라는 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["‘It is humble,’ said Mr.", "\"누추하지.\""], ["Micawber, ‘--to quote a favourite expression of my friend Heep; but it may prove the stepping-stone to more ambitious domiciliary accommodation.’", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"내 친구 힙이 즐겨 쓰는 표현을 빌리자면 말일세. 하지만 그게 더 야심 찬 거처로 가는 디딤돌이 될지도 모르지.\""]], [["I asked him whether he had reason, so far, to be satisfied with his friend Heep’s treatment of him?", "나는 그에게, 여태까지 친구 힙이 그를 대하는 태도에 만족할 만한 까닭이 있었느냐고 물었다."], ["He got up to ascertain if the door were close shut, before he replied, in a lower voice:", "그는 대답하기에 앞서 일어나 문이 꼭 닫혀 있는지 확인하고 나서, 한층 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield, a man who labours under the pressure of pecuniary embarrassments, is, with the generality of people, at a disadvantage.", "\"이보게 코퍼필드, 금전적 곤경에 짓눌린 사람은, 대다수 사람들 앞에서 불리한 처지에 놓이게 마련이라네."], ["That disadvantage is not diminished, when that pressure necessitates the drawing of stipendiary emoluments, before those emoluments are strictly due and payable.", "그 불리함은, 그 곤경 때문에 봉급이 엄밀히 따져 받을 때가 되기도 전에 미리 끌어다 써야 할 지경이 되면 결코 줄어들지 않지."], ["All I can say is, that my friend Heep has responded to appeals to which I need not more particularly refer, in a manner calculated to redound equally to the honour of his head, and of his heart.’", "내가 말할 수 있는 건 이것뿐이야. 굳이 자세히 밝힐 필요 없는 어떤 부탁들에 대해, 내 친구 힙은 그의 머리와 가슴의 명예에 똑같이 빛이 될 만한 방식으로 응해 주었다는 것 말일세.\""]], [["‘I should not have supposed him to be very free with his money either,’ I observed.", "\"제가 보기엔 그가 돈에 그리 후한 사람 같지도 않던데요.\" 내가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Pardon me!’ said Mr.", "\"천만에!\""], ["Micawber, with an air of constraint, ‘I speak of my friend Heep as I have experience.’", "미코버 씨가 무언가에 얽매인 듯한 태도로 말했다. \"나는 내 친구 힙을, 내가 직접 겪은 대로 말하는 것일세.\""]], [["‘I am glad your experience is so favourable,’ I returned.", "\"그 경험이 그토록 좋은 것이었다니 다행이네요.\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘You are very obliging, my dear Copperfield,’ said Mr. Micawber; and hummed a tune.", "\"아주 친절한 말이군, 이보게 코퍼필드.\" 미코버 씨가 말하더니, 콧노래를 흥얼거렸다."]], [["‘Do you see much of Mr. Wickfield?’ I asked, to change the subject.", "\"위크필드 씨는 자주 보세요?\" 화제를 바꾸려고 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Not much,’ said Mr.", "\"자주는 아니야.\""], ["Micawber, slightingly. ‘Mr.", "미코버 씨가 대수롭지 않게 말했다."], ["Wickfield is, I dare say, a man of very excellent intentions; but he is--in short, he is obsolete.’", "\"위크필드 씨는, 내 보기에, 더없이 훌륭한 뜻을 품은 사람이지. 하지만 그분은 — 한마디로, 한물간 사람이라네.\""]], [["‘I am afraid his partner seeks to make him so,’ said I.", "\"그분의 동업자가 그분을 그렇게 만들려 드는 게 아닌가 싶습니다만.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield!’ returned Mr.", "\"이보게 코퍼필드!\""], ["Micawber, after some uneasy evolutions on his stool, ‘allow me to offer a remark!", "미코버 씨가 의자 위에서 불편하게 몸을 몇 번 뒤척이고 나서 대꾸했다. \"한마디 하게 해 주게나!"], ["I am here, in a capacity of confidence.", "나는 지금 비밀을 다루는 자리에 있네."], ["I am here, in a position of trust.", "나는 지금 신임을 받는 자리에 있어."], ["The discussion of some topics, even with Mrs. Micawber herself (so long the partner of my various vicissitudes, and a woman of a remarkable lucidity of intellect), is, I am led to consider, incompatible with the functions now devolving on me.", "어떤 화제들을 입에 올리는 것은, 설령 미코버 부인 자신과라도 (오랜 세월 내 온갖 부침을 함께해 온 동반자이자, 보기 드물게 명석한 지성을 지닌 여인이지만) 이제 내게 맡겨진 직무와 양립할 수 없는 일이라고 나는 생각하게 되었네. 그러니 외람되지만 한 가지 제안하겠네."], ["I would therefore take the liberty of suggesting that in our friendly intercourse--which I trust will never be disturbed!--we draw a line.", "우리의 우정 어린 교류에서 — 결코 흐트러지는 일이 없기를 바라네! — 우리 한 가닥 선을 긋세나. 이 선의 한쪽에는,\""], ["On one side of this line,’ said Mr.", "미코버 씨가 사무실 자를 책상 위에 대어 그 선을 그려 보이며 말했다."], ["Micawber, representing it on the desk with the office ruler, ‘is the whole range of the human intellect, with a trifling exception; on the other, IS that exception; that is to say, the affairs of Messrs Wickfield and Heep, with all belonging and appertaining thereunto.", "\"사소한 예외 하나만 빼고 인간 지성의 모든 영역이 들어가네. 다른 한쪽에는, 바로 그 예외가 있지. 다시 말해, 위크필드 힙 상회의 사무와, 거기에 딸리고 결부된 모든 것 말일세."], ["I trust I give no offence to the companion of my youth, in submitting this proposition to his cooler judgement?’", "이 제안을 내 젊은 시절 벗의 한결 냉정한 판단에 맡긴다 해서, 그 벗의 기분을 상하게 하는 건 아니리라 믿네만?\""]], [["Though I saw an uneasy change in Mr. Micawber, which sat tightly on him, as if his new duties were a misfit, I felt I had no right to be offended.", "미코버 씨에게 일어난 어색한 변화가, 마치 그 새 직무가 몸에 맞지 않는 옷이라도 되는 양 그를 옥죄고 있는 것이 보였지만, 나는 기분 상해할 권리가 없다고 느꼈다."], ["My telling him so, appeared to relieve him; and he shook hands with me.", "내가 그렇게 말해 주자 그는 마음이 놓이는 듯했고, 나와 악수를 나누었다."]], [["‘I am charmed, Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 매료되었네, 코퍼필드.\""], ["Micawber, ‘let me assure you, with Miss Wickfield.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"확언컨대, 위크필드 양에게 말이야."], ["She is a very superior young lady, of very remarkable attractions, graces, and virtues.", "그분은 더없이 빼어난 아가씨라네. 보기 드문 매력과 우아함과 미덕을 갖추셨지. 내 명예를 걸고,\""], ["Upon my honour,’ said Mr. Micawber, indefinitely kissing his hand and bowing with his genteelest air, ‘I do Homage to Miss Wickfield!", "미코버 씨가 어딘지 막연하게 손에 입을 맞추고 더없이 점잖은 태도로 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"나는 위크필드 양께 경의를 바치네! 흠!\""], ["Hem!’ ‘I am glad of that, at least,’ said I.", "\"그건 적어도 반가운 일이네요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘If you had not assured us, my dear Copperfield, on the occasion of that agreeable afternoon we had the happiness of passing with you, that D. was your favourite letter,’ said Mr.", "\"그 즐거웠던 오후, 우리가 자네와 더불어 행복하게 보냈던 그날, 자네가 D가 가장 좋아하는 글자라고 우리에게 단언하지 않았더라면,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘I should unquestionably have supposed that A. had been so.’", "\"나는 틀림없이 A가 그 글자였으리라 짐작했을 걸세.\""]], [["We have all some experience of a feeling, that comes over us occasionally, of what we are saying and doing having been said and done before, in a remote time--of our having been surrounded, dim ages ago, by the same faces, objects, and circumstances--of our knowing perfectly what will be said next, as if we suddenly remembered it!", "우리 모두는 가끔 어떤 느낌에 사로잡힌다. 지금 우리가 하는 말과 행동이, 까마득한 옛적에 이미 말해지고 행해졌던 것 같은 느낌 — 아득한 옛날에 똑같은 얼굴들, 똑같은 사물들, 똑같은 상황에 둘러싸여 있었던 것 같은 느낌 — 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지를, 마치 문득 그것이 기억나기라도 한 듯 완벽하게 알고 있는 것 같은 느낌 말이다."], ["I never had this mysterious impression more strongly in my life, than before he uttered those words.", "그가 그 말을 입에 올리기 직전만큼, 내 평생 그 신비로운 느낌을 더 강하게 받은 적은 없었다."]], [["I took my leave of Mr. Micawber, for the time, charging him with my best remembrances to all at home.", "나는 일단 미코버 씨와 작별하면서, 집에 있는 모든 이에게 안부를 잘 전해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["As I left him, resuming his stool and his pen, and rolling his head in his stock, to get it into easier writing order, I clearly perceived that there was something interposed between him and me, since he had come into his new functions, which prevented our getting at each other as we used to do, and quite altered the character of our intercourse.", "그가 다시 의자에 앉아 펜을 집어 들고, 더 편하게 글을 쓰려고 빳빳한 깃 속에서 고개를 이리저리 돌리는 모습을 뒤로하고 떠나면서, 나는 그가 새 직무를 맡은 뒤로 그와 나 사이에 무언가가 끼어들어, 예전처럼 서로 가까이 다가서는 것을 막고, 우리 사이의 성격을 완전히 바꿔 버렸음을 똑똑히 알아차렸다."]], [["There was no one in the quaint old drawing-room, though it presented tokens of Mrs.", "예스러운 그 응접실에는 아무도 없었으나, 힙 부인이 어딘가에 있다는 흔적은 남아 있었다."], ["Heep’s whereabouts. I looked into the room still belonging to Agnes, and saw her sitting by the fire, at a pretty old-fashioned desk she had, writing.", "나는 아직 아그네스의 것인 그 방을 들여다보았고, 그녀가 난롯가에서, 자기가 쓰는 아담하고 고풍스러운 책상에 앉아 무언가를 쓰고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["My darkening the light made her look up.", "내가 빛을 가리는 바람에 그녀가 고개를 들었다."], ["What a pleasure to be the cause of that bright change in her attentive face, and the object of that sweet regard and welcome!", "그녀의 그 주의 깊은 얼굴에 환한 변화를 불러일으키는 까닭이 되고, 그 다정한 눈길과 환대의 대상이 된다는 것이 어찌나 기쁘던지!"]], [["‘Ah, Agnes!’ said I, when we were sitting together, side by side; ‘I have missed you so much, lately!’", "\"아, 아그네스!\" 우리가 나란히 곁에 앉았을 때 내가 말했다. \"요즘 네가 어찌나 보고 싶던지!\""]], [["‘Indeed?’ she replied.", "\"정말?\""], ["‘Again! And so soon?’", "그녀가 대답했다. \"또? 그새?\""]], [["I shook my head.", "나는 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘I don’t know how it is, Agnes; I seem to want some faculty of mind that I ought to have.", "\"어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠어, 아그네스. 나는 마땅히 갖춰야 할 어떤 마음의 능력이 모자란 것 같아."], ["You were so much in the habit of thinking for me, in the happy old days here, and I came so naturally to you for counsel and support, that I really think I have missed acquiring it.’", "이곳에서 보낸 그 행복한 옛 시절에, 너는 그렇게 늘 나를 위해 생각해 주는 게 버릇이 됐고, 나도 그렇게 자연스레 네게 와서 조언과 힘을 구했으니, 정말이지 나는 그 능력을 익힐 기회를 놓쳐 버린 게 아닌가 싶어.\""]], [["‘And what is it?’ said Agnes, cheerfully.", "\"그게 뭔데?\" 아그네스가 환하게 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t know what to call it,’ I replied.", "\"뭐라고 불러야 할지 모르겠어.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘I think I am earnest and persevering?’", "\"내가 진지하고 끈기 있다고는 생각하는데?\""]], [["‘I am sure of it,’ said Agnes.", "\"그건 확실해.\" 아그네스가 말했다."]], [["‘And patient, Agnes?’ I inquired, with a little hesitation.", "\"그리고 참을성도 있고, 아그네스?\" 나는 살짝 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Agnes, laughing.", "\"응.\" 아그네스가 웃으며 대답했다."], ["‘Pretty well.’", "\"그럭저럭.\""]], [["‘And yet,’ said I, ‘I get so miserable and worried, and am so unsteady and irresolute in my power of assuring myself, that I know I must want--shall I call it--reliance, of some kind?’", "\"그런데도,\" 내가 말했다. \"나는 이렇게 비참해지고 안달이 나고, 스스로를 다잡는 힘이 이토록 흔들리고 우유부단하니, 분명 뭔가가 모자란 거야 — 뭐랄까 — 일종의 의지할 데가 모자란 거겠지?\""]], [["‘Call it so, if you will,’ said Agnes.", "\"네가 그러고 싶다면 그렇게 불러.\" 아그네스가 말했다."]], [["‘Well!’ I returned.", "\"그래!\" 내가 받았다."], ["‘See here!", "\"이것 봐!"], ["You come to London, I rely on you, and I have an object and a course at once.", "네가 런던에 오면, 나는 너를 의지하고, 곧바로 목표와 나아갈 길이 생겨."], ["I am driven out of it, I come here, and in a moment I feel an altered person.", "그러다 거기서 밀려나, 이곳에 오면, 한순간에 사람이 달라진 것 같아."], ["The circumstances that distressed me are not changed, since I came into this room; but an influence comes over me in that short interval that alters me, oh, how much for the better!", "이 방에 들어왔다고 해서, 나를 괴롭히던 상황이 달라진 건 아니야. 그런데 그 짧은 사이에 어떤 기운이 나를 휘감아 나를 바꿔 놓는단 말이지, 아, 얼마나 좋은 쪽으로 바뀌는지 몰라!"], ["What is it?", "그게 뭘까?"], ["What is your secret, Agnes?’", "네 비결이 뭐야, 아그네스?\""]], [["Her head was bent down, looking at the fire.", "그녀는 고개를 숙인 채 불을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘It’s the old story,’ said I.", "\"늘 똑같은 얘기지.\""], ["‘Don’t laugh, when I say it was always the same in little things as it is in greater ones.", "내가 말했다. \"내가 이런 말을 해도 웃지 마. 작은 일에서나 큰일에서나 늘 한결같았어."], ["My old troubles were nonsense, and now they are serious; but whenever I have gone away from my adopted sister--’", "옛날에 내가 겪던 고민들은 시시한 것이었고, 지금은 심각한 것이야. 하지만 내가 누이에게서 멀어질 때면 언제나—\""]], [["Agnes looked up--with such a Heavenly face!--and gave me her hand, which I kissed.", "아그네스가 고개를 들었다 — 어찌나 천사 같은 얼굴이던지! — 그리고 내게 손을 내밀었고, 나는 그 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘Whenever I have not had you, Agnes, to advise and approve in the beginning, I have seemed to go wild, and to get into all sorts of difficulty.", "\"처음에 네가 조언하고 인정해 주는 일이 없을 때면 언제나, 아그네스, 나는 정신을 못 차리고 온갖 곤경에 빠져드는 것 같았어."], ["When I have come to you, at last (as I have always done), I have come to peace and happiness.", "그러다 끝내 (늘 그래 왔듯이) 네게 오면, 나는 평온과 행복에 이르렀지."], ["I come home, now, like a tired traveller, and find such a blessed sense of rest!’", "나는 지금 지친 나그네처럼 집에 돌아와, 이렇게 복된 안식의 느낌을 누리고 있어!\""]], [["I felt so deeply what I said, it affected me so sincerely, that my voice failed, and I covered my face with my hand, and broke into tears.", "내가 한 말이 어찌나 가슴 깊이 사무치던지, 어찌나 진심으로 마음을 흔들던지, 목소리가 잠겨 버려, 나는 한 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["I write the truth.", "나는 진실을 적고 있다."], ["Whatever contradictions and inconsistencies there were within me, as there are within so many of us; whatever might have been so different, and so much better; whatever I had done, in which I had perversely wandered away from the voice of my own heart; I knew nothing of.", "우리 가운데 그토록 많은 이의 내면이 그러하듯, 내 안에 어떤 모순과 어긋남이 있었든, 무엇이 그토록 다를 수도, 그토록 더 나을 수도 있었든, 내가 비뚤어지게 제 마음의 소리에서 벗어나 헤맨 일이 무엇이었든, 나는 아무것도 알지 못했다."], ["I only knew that I was fervently in earnest, when I felt the rest and peace of having Agnes near me.", "다만, 아그네스가 곁에 있다는 안식과 평온을 느낄 때면, 내가 뜨겁게 진심이었다는 것만은 알았다."]], [["In her placid sisterly manner; with her beaming eyes; with her tender voice; and with that sweet composure, which had long ago made the house that held her quite a sacred place to me; she soon won me from this weakness, and led me on to tell all that had happened since our last meeting.", "그 잔잔한 누이다운 태도로, 그 빛나는 눈으로, 그 다정한 목소리로, 그리고 오래전부터 그녀를 품은 그 집을 내게 더없이 신성한 곳으로 만들어 준 그 사랑스러운 평정으로, 그녀는 곧 나를 이 나약함에서 끌어내, 우리가 마지막으로 만난 뒤로 일어난 모든 일을 털어놓도록 이끌었다."]], [["‘And there is not another word to tell, Agnes,’ said I, when I had made an end of my confidence.", "\"더는 들려줄 게 없어, 아그네스.\" 속내를 다 털어놓고 나서 내가 말했다."], ["‘Now, my reliance is on you.’", "\"이제, 나는 너를 의지할 거야.\""]], [["‘But it must not be on me, Trotwood,’ returned Agnes, with a pleasant smile.", "\"하지만 나를 의지해선 안 돼, 트롯우드.\" 아그네스가 환한 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["‘It must be on someone else.’", "\"다른 누군가를 의지해야지.\""]], [["‘On Dora?’ said I.", "\"도라를?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Assuredly.’", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["‘Why, I have not mentioned, Agnes,’ said I, a little embarrassed, ‘that Dora is rather difficult to--I would not, for the world, say, to rely upon, because she is the soul of purity and truth--but rather difficult to--I hardly know how to express it, really, Agnes.", "\"그게 말이야, 아직 얘기 안 했지, 아그네스.\" 내가 조금 멋쩍어하며 말했다. \"도라는 좀 — 죽어도 의지하기 어렵다는 말은 하고 싶지 않아, 그 애는 순수함과 진실 그 자체니까 — 하지만 좀 뭐랄까 — 정말이지 어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠어, 아그네스."], ["She is a timid little thing, and easily disturbed and frightened.", "그 애는 겁 많은 작은 아이라, 쉽게 마음이 흔들리고 겁을 먹어."], ["Some time ago, before her father’s death, when I thought it right to mention to her--but I’ll tell you, if you will bear with me, how it was.’", "얼마 전에, 그 애 아버지가 돌아가시기 전에, 내가 그 애에게 말해 두는 게 옳다고 여겨서 — 아니, 어떻게 된 일인지 들어 줄 수 있다면 얘기해 줄게.\""]], [["Accordingly, I told Agnes about my declaration of poverty, about the cookery-book, the housekeeping accounts, and all the rest of it.", "그래서 나는 아그네스에게, 내가 가난을 고백했던 일이며, 요리책이며, 살림 장부며, 그 밖의 모든 일을 들려주었다."]], [["‘Oh, Trotwood!’ she remonstrated, with a smile.", "\"오, 트롯우드!\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 나무랐다."], ["‘Just your old headlong way!", "\"딱 너다운 그 성급함이야!"], ["You might have been in earnest in striving to get on in the world, without being so very sudden with a timid, loving, inexperienced girl.", "세상에서 자리 잡으려고 애쓰는 일에 진심일 수는 있겠지만, 그렇게 겁 많고 사랑스럽고 세상 물정 모르는 아이한테 그토록 느닷없이 굴 것까진 없잖아."], ["Poor Dora!’", "가엾은 도라!\""]], [["I never heard such sweet forbearing kindness expressed in a voice, as she expressed in making this reply.", "이 대답을 하면서 그녀가 목소리에 담아낸 것만큼 다정하고 너그러운 친절을, 나는 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다."], ["It was as if I had seen her admiringly and tenderly embracing Dora, and tacitly reproving me, by her considerate protection, for my hot haste in fluttering that little heart.", "마치 그녀가 도라를 감탄과 애정으로 끌어안으면서, 그 작은 마음을 떨리게 한 내 성급함을 그 사려 깊은 보호로써 넌지시 나무라는 모습을 본 것만 같았다."], ["It was as if I had seen Dora, in all her fascinating artlessness, caressing Agnes, and thanking her, and coaxingly appealing against me, and loving me with all her childish innocence.", "마치 도라가 그 매혹적인 천진함을 한껏 드러내며 아그네스를 어루만지고, 고마워하고, 나에게 항의하듯이 애교를 부리면서, 그 어린아이다운 순수함으로 나를 사랑하는 모습을 본 것만 같았다."]], [["I felt so grateful to Agnes, and admired her so!", "나는 아그네스가 어찌나 고맙던지, 또 어찌나 그녀가 존경스럽던지!"], ["I saw those two together, in a bright perspective, such well-associated friends, each adorning the other so much!", "나는 그 두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을, 환한 미래 속에서, 그토록 잘 어울리는 벗으로, 서로가 서로를 그토록 빛내 주는 모습으로 그려 보았다!"]], [["‘What ought I to do then, Agnes?’ I inquired, after looking at the fire a little while.", "\"그럼 난 어떻게 해야 할까, 아그네스?\" 잠시 불을 바라보고 나서 내가 물었다."], ["‘What would it be right to do?’", "\"어떻게 하는 게 옳을까?\""]], [["‘I think,’ said Agnes, ‘that the honourable course to take, would be to write to those two ladies.", "\"내 생각엔,\" 아그네스가 말했다. \"떳떳한 길은, 그 두 분께 편지를 쓰는 거야."], ["Don’t you think that any secret course is an unworthy one?’", "무엇이든 몰래 하는 건 떳떳하지 못한 일이라고 생각하지 않아?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래."], ["If YOU think so,’ said I.", "네가 그렇게 생각한다면.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am poorly qualified to judge of such matters,’ replied Agnes, with a modest hesitation, ‘but I certainly feel--in short, I feel that your being secret and clandestine, is not being like yourself.’", "\"나야 그런 일을 판단할 자격이 모자라지.\" 아그네스가 겸손하게 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"하지만 분명히 느껴 — 한마디로, 네가 그렇게 비밀스럽고 은밀하게 구는 건 너답지 않다는 느낌이 들어.\""]], [["‘Like myself, in the too high opinion you have of me, Agnes, I am afraid,’ said I.", "\"네가 나를 너무 높이 봐 줘서 그런 거지, 아무래도 그런 것 같아, 아그네스.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Like yourself, in the candour of your nature,’ she returned; ‘and therefore I would write to those two ladies.", "\"네 천성의 솔직함을 두고 너답다고 하는 거야.\" 그녀가 받았다. \"그러니 나라면 그 두 분께 편지를 쓰겠어."], ["I would relate, as plainly and as openly as possible, all that has taken place; and I would ask their permission to visit sometimes, at their house.", "일어난 일을 되도록 명백하고 솔직하게 다 털어놓겠어. 그리고 가끔 그 댁을 찾아뵙도록 허락해 달라고 청하겠어."], ["Considering that you are young, and striving for a place in life, I think it would be well to say that you would readily abide by any conditions they might impose upon you.", "네가 아직 젊고, 인생에서 자리를 잡으려고 애쓰는 중인 걸 생각하면, 그분들이 내거는 어떤 조건에도 기꺼이 따르겠다고 말하는 게 좋을 것 같아."], ["I would entreat them not to dismiss your request, without a reference to Dora; and to discuss it with her when they should think the time suitable.", "도라에게 알리지 않은 채로 네 청을 물리치지는 말아 달라고, 그리고 적당한 때가 되었다 싶으면 도라와 의논해 달라고 부탁드리겠어. 너무 격하게 굴거나,\""], ["I would not be too vehement,’ said Agnes, gently, ‘or propose too much.", "아그네스가 부드럽게 말했다. \"지나치게 많은 걸 내세우지는 않겠어."], ["I would trust to my fidelity and perseverance--and to Dora.’", "나라면 내 신의와 끈기를 — 그리고 도라를 믿겠어.\""]], [["‘But if they were to frighten Dora again, Agnes, by speaking to her,’ said I.", "\"하지만 그분들이 도라에게 말을 꺼내서 그 애를 또 겁먹게 한다면, 아그네스.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘And if Dora were to cry, and say nothing about me!’", "\"그래서 도라가 울기만 하고 나에 대해선 아무 말도 안 한다면!\""]], [["‘Is that likely?’ inquired Agnes, with the same sweet consideration in her face.", "\"그럴 것 같아?\" 아그네스가 여전히 그 다정하고 사려 깊은 얼굴로 물었다."]], [["‘God bless her, she is as easily scared as a bird,’ said I.", "\"맙소사, 그 앤 새처럼 잘도 놀라거든.\""], ["‘It might be!", "내가 말했다."], ["Or if the two Miss Spenlows (elderly ladies of that sort are odd characters sometimes) should not be likely persons to address in that way!’", "\"그럴 수도 있다고! 아니면 그 두 스펜로 양이 (그런 부류의 나이 든 부인들은 가끔 별난 데가 있으니까) 그런 식으로 말을 건넬 만한 분들이 아닐 수도 있고!\""]], [["‘I don’t think, Trotwood,’ returned Agnes, raising her soft eyes to mine, ‘I would consider that.", "\"내 생각엔, 트롯우드,\" 아그네스가 그 부드러운 눈을 들어 나를 보며 대답했다. \"그런 건 따지지 않겠어."], ["Perhaps it would be better only to consider whether it is right to do this; and, if it is, to do it.’", "어쩌면 이걸 하는 게 옳은지 아닌지만 따져 보는 게 나을지도 몰라. 그래서 옳다면, 하는 거지.\""]], [["I had no longer any doubt on the subject.", "나는 더는 그 문제로 망설이지 않았다."], ["With a lightened heart, though with a profound sense of the weighty importance of my task, I devoted the whole afternoon to the composition of the draft of this letter; for which great purpose, Agnes relinquished her desk to me.", "마음이 한결 가벼워진 채로, 그러나 내 과업의 막중한 무게를 깊이 느끼며, 나는 그 오후를 통째로 이 편지의 초안을 다듬는 데 바쳤다. 그 대업을 위해 아그네스가 자기 책상을 내게 내주었다."], ["But first I went downstairs to see Mr. Wickfield and Uriah Heep.", "그러나 먼저 나는 아래층으로 내려가 위크필드 씨와 유라이아 힙을 만났다."]], [["I found Uriah in possession of a new, plaster-smelling office, built out in the garden; looking extraordinarily mean, in the midst of a quantity of books and papers.", "유라이아는 정원에 새로 지어 회반죽 냄새가 풀풀 나는 사무실을 차지하고 있었는데, 무더기로 쌓인 책과 서류 한가운데서 유난히 비루해 보였다."], ["He received me in his usual fawning way, and pretended not to have heard of my arrival from Mr.", "그는 여느 때처럼 알랑거리는 태도로 나를 맞이하더니, 미코버 씨에게서 내가 왔다는 말을 못 들은 척했다."], ["Micawber; a pretence I took the liberty of disbelieving.", "그 시치미를 나는 외람되지만 믿지 않기로 했다."], ["He accompanied me into Mr. Wickfield’s room, which was the shadow of its former self--having been divested of a variety of conveniences, for the accommodation of the new partner--and stood before the fire, warming his back, and shaving his chin with his bony hand, while Mr. Wickfield and I exchanged greetings.", "그는 나를 위크필드 씨의 방으로 안내했는데, 그 방은 예전의 그림자에 지나지 않았다 — 새 동업자의 편의를 위해 온갖 편리한 것들이 다 빠져나가 버린 탓이었다 — 그리고 위크필드 씨와 내가 인사를 나누는 동안, 그는 난롯불 앞에 서서 등을 쬐며, 뼈만 앙상한 손으로 제 턱을 쓱쓱 쓸어 댔다."]], [["‘You stay with us, Trotwood, while you remain in Canterbury?’ said Mr. Wickfield, not without a glance at Uriah for his approval.", "\"트롯우드, 캔터베리에 머무는 동안엔 우리 집에 묵겠지?\" 위크필드 씨가 유라이아의 동의를 구하는 듯한 눈길을 흘끗 던지며 말했다."]], [["‘Is there room for me?’ said I.", "\"제가 묵을 방이 있을까요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am sure, Master Copperfield--I should say Mister, but the other comes so natural,’ said Uriah,--‘I would turn out of your old room with pleasure, if it would be agreeable.’", "\"여부가 있겠습니까, 코퍼필드 도련님 — 코퍼필드 씨라고 해야 하는데, 그 호칭이 워낙 입에 배어서요,\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"기꺼이 도련님이 쓰시던 옛 방을 비워 드리지요, 그게 좋으시다면요.\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"아니, 아니야.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Why should you be inconvenienced?", "\"왜 자네가 불편을 겪어야 하나? 다른 방이 있어."], ["There’s another room.", "다른 방이 있다네.\""], ["There’s another room.’ ‘Oh, but you know,’ returned Uriah, with a grin, ‘I should really be delighted!’", "\"오, 하지만 아시다시피,\" 유라이아가 씩 웃으며 받았다. \"저야 정말 기쁠 텐데요!\""]], [["To cut the matter short, I said I would have the other room or none at all; so it was settled that I should have the other room; and, taking my leave of the firm until dinner, I went upstairs again.", "이야기를 짧게 끝내려고, 나는 다른 방을 쓰거나 아니면 아예 묵지 않겠다고 말했다. 그래서 내가 다른 방을 쓰기로 정해졌고, 나는 저녁 식사 때까지 회사 사람들과 작별하고 다시 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["I had hoped to have no other companion than Agnes. But Mrs.", "나는 아그네스 말고는 다른 동무가 없기를 바랐다."], ["Heep had asked permission to bring herself and her knitting near the fire, in that room; on pretence of its having an aspect more favourable for her rheumatics, as the wind then was, than the drawing-room or dining-parlour.", "그러나 힙 부인이, 바람이 그렇게 부는 날에는 그 방이 응접실이나 식당보다 류머티즘에 더 좋은 방향이라는 핑계로, 자기와 뜨개질감을 그 방 난롯가로 가져와도 되겠느냐고 청해 둔 터였다."], ["Though I could almost have consigned her to the mercies of the wind on the topmost pinnacle of the Cathedral, without remorse, I made a virtue of necessity, and gave her a friendly salutation.", "나는 그녀를 양심의 가책 하나 없이 대성당 꼭대기 첨탑 위에 올려놓고 바람의 처분에 맡겨 버리고 싶을 지경이었지만, 어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕으로 삼아 그녀에게 정답게 인사를 건넸다."]], [["‘I’m umbly thankful to you, sir,’ said Mrs.", "\"황송할 따름이에요, 나리.\""], ["Heep, in acknowledgement of my inquiries concerning her health, ‘but I’m only pretty well.", "내가 건강을 묻자 힙 부인이 대답했다. \"하지만 그저 그렇답니다."], ["I haven’t much to boast of.", "자랑할 게 별로 없네요."], ["If I could see my Uriah well settled in life, I couldn’t expect much more I think.", "우리 유라이아가 세상에 자리 잘 잡은 걸 볼 수만 있다면, 더 바랄 게 없을 것 같아요."], ["How do you think my Ury looking, sir?’", "우리 유리가 어때 보여요, 나리?\""]], [["I thought him looking as villainous as ever, and I replied that I saw no change in him.", "내가 보기엔 그는 여느 때처럼 악당 같아 보였다. 그래서 나는 그에게서 아무 변화도 못 보겠다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh, don’t you think he’s changed?’ said Mrs. Heep.", "\"아니, 변한 것 같지 않아요?\" 힙 부인이 말했다."], ["‘There I must umbly beg leave to differ from you.", "\"거기선 황송하지만 생각이 다르다고 말씀드려야겠네요."], ["Don’t you see a thinness in him?’", "좀 야윈 게 안 보여요?\""]], [["‘Not more than usual,’ I replied.", "\"여느 때보다 더하지는 않은데요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Don’t you though!’ said Mrs. Heep.", "\"어머, 그래요!\" 힙 부인이 말했다."], ["‘But you don’t take notice of him with a mother’s eye!’", "\"하지만 나리는 어미의 눈으로 저 애를 보는 게 아니잖아요!\""]], [["His mother’s eye was an evil eye to the rest of the world, I thought as it met mine, howsoever affectionate to him; and I believe she and her son were devoted to one another.", "그 어미의 눈은, 그것이 내 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그에게는 그토록 다정할지언정 나머지 세상에는 사악한 눈이라고 나는 생각했다. 그리고 내 생각에 그녀와 그녀의 아들은 서로에게 헌신적이었다."], ["It passed me, and went on to Agnes.", "그 눈은 나를 지나쳐 아그네스에게로 옮겨 갔다."]], [["‘Don’t YOU see a wasting and a wearing in him, Miss Wickfield?’ inquired Mrs. Heep.", "\"위크필드 양은 저 애가 시들고 쇠해 가는 게 안 보여요?\" 힙 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ said Agnes, quietly pursuing the work on which she was engaged.", "\"아뇨.\" 아그네스가 하던 일을 조용히 이어 가며 말했다."], ["‘You are too solicitous about him.", "\"부인은 저분을 너무 걱정하세요."], ["He is very well.’", "아주 건강하신걸요.\""]], [["Mrs. Heep, with a prodigious sniff, resumed her knitting.", "힙 부인은 엄청나게 코를 훌쩍이고는 다시 뜨개질을 시작했다."]], [["She never left off, or left us for a moment.", "그녀는 한순간도 손을 멈추거나 우리 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["I had arrived early in the day, and we had still three or four hours before dinner; but she sat there, plying her knitting-needles as monotonously as an hour-glass might have poured out its sands.", "나는 이른 시각에 도착했으니, 저녁 식사까지 아직 서너 시간이 남아 있었다. 그런데도 그녀는 거기 앉아, 모래시계가 모래를 쏟아 내듯 단조롭게 뜨개바늘을 놀려 댔다."], ["She sat on one side of the fire; I sat at the desk in front of it; a little beyond me, on the other side, sat Agnes.", "그녀는 난로 한쪽에 앉았고, 나는 그 앞 책상에 앉았으며, 나에게서 조금 떨어진 반대쪽에 아그네스가 앉아 있었다."], ["Whensoever, slowly pondering over my letter, I lifted up my eyes, and meeting the thoughtful face of Agnes, saw it clear, and beam encouragement upon me, with its own angelic expression, I was conscious presently of the evil eye passing me, and going on to her, and coming back to me again, and dropping furtively upon the knitting.", "천천히 내 편지를 곱씹다가 눈을 들어 아그네스의 사색에 잠긴 얼굴과 마주칠 때면, 그 얼굴이 환하게, 그 특유의 천사 같은 표정으로 나를 격려하며 빛나는 것이 보였고, 그러면 곧 나는 그 사악한 눈이 나를 지나쳐 그녀에게로 갔다가 다시 내게 돌아왔다가, 슬그머니 뜨개질감으로 떨어지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["What the knitting was, I don’t know, not being learned in that art; but it looked like a net; and as she worked away with those Chinese chopsticks of knitting-needles, she showed in the firelight like an ill-looking enchantress, baulked as yet by the radiant goodness opposite, but getting ready for a cast of her net by and by.", "그 뜨개질이 무엇이었는지는, 내가 그 기술을 알지 못하니 모르겠으나, 그것은 그물처럼 보였다. 그리고 그녀가 그 중국 젓가락 같은 뜨개바늘을 놀려 대는 모습은, 난롯불 빛 속에서 보기 흉한 마녀처럼 보였다. 맞은편의 환한 선함에 아직은 가로막혀 있으나, 머지않아 그물을 한 번 던질 채비를 하고 있는 마녀 말이다."]], [["At dinner she maintained her watch, with the same unwinking eyes.", "저녁 식사 자리에서도 그녀는 똑같이 깜빡이지 않는 눈으로 감시를 이어 갔다."], ["After dinner, her son took his turn; and when Mr. Wickfield, himself, and I were left alone together, leered at me, and writhed until I could hardly bear it.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자 그녀의 아들이 그 일을 넘겨받았고, 위크필드 씨와 그와 나, 셋만 남게 되자, 그는 나를 곁눈질하고 몸을 비비 꼬아 대는 통에 나는 도저히 견디기 어려울 지경이었다."], ["In the drawing-room, there was the mother knitting and watching again.", "응접실에서는 또 어미가 뜨개질을 하며 지켜보고 있었다."], ["All the time that Agnes sang and played, the mother sat at the piano.", "아그네스가 노래하고 연주하는 내내, 어미는 피아노 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["Once she asked for a particular ballad, which she said her Ury (who was yawning in a great chair) doted on; and at intervals she looked round at him, and reported to Agnes that he was in raptures with the music.", "한번은 그녀가 어떤 발라드를 청하면서, 자기 유리가 (커다란 안락의자에 앉아 하품을 하고 있던 그가) 그 곡을 무척 좋아한다고 했다. 그러고는 틈틈이 그를 돌아보며, 그가 음악에 흠뻑 빠져 있다고 아그네스에게 일러 주었다."], ["But she hardly ever spoke--I question if she ever did--without making some mention of him.", "그러나 그녀는 그를 언급하지 않고는 좀처럼 입을 떼는 법이 없었다 — 아니, 한 번이라도 그런 적이 있었는지 의심스럽다."], ["It was evident to me that this was the duty assigned to her.", "그것이 그녀에게 맡겨진 임무임이 내게는 분명히 보였다."]], [["This lasted until bedtime. To have seen the mother and son, like two great bats hanging over the whole house, and darkening it with their ugly forms, made me so uncomfortable, that I would rather have remained downstairs, knitting and all, than gone to bed.", "이런 일이 잠자리에 들 시각까지 계속되었다. 그 어미와 아들이 두 마리의 커다란 박쥐처럼 온 집안 위에 매달려, 그 흉측한 몰골로 집안을 어둡게 만드는 꼴을 보고 있자니 어찌나 불편하던지, 차라리 뜨개질이고 뭐고 아래층에 그대로 남아 있는 편이 잠자리에 드는 것보다 나을 지경이었다."], ["I hardly got any sleep.", "나는 거의 한숨도 못 잤다."], ["Next day the knitting and watching began again, and lasted all day.", "이튿날에도 뜨개질과 감시가 다시 시작되어 온종일 이어졌다."]], [["I had not an opportunity of speaking to Agnes, for ten minutes. I could barely show her my letter.", "나는 아그네스와 십 분도 이야기할 기회를 얻지 못했다. 가까스로 그녀에게 내 편지를 보여 줄 수 있었을 뿐이다."], ["I proposed to her to walk out with me; but Mrs. Heep repeatedly complaining that she was worse, Agnes charitably remained within, to bear her company.", "나는 그녀에게 함께 산책을 나가자고 제안했지만, 힙 부인이 자꾸만 몸이 더 안 좋다고 하소연하는 통에, 아그네스는 자비를 베풀어 집에 남아 그녀의 말동무가 되어 주었다."], ["Towards the twilight I went out by myself, musing on what I ought to do, and whether I was justified in withholding from Agnes, any longer, what Uriah Heep had told me in London; for that began to trouble me again, very much.", "황혼 무렵 나는 혼자 밖으로 나가, 내가 어떻게 해야 할지, 그리고 유라이아 힙이 런던에서 내게 한 말을 더 이상 아그네스에게 숨기는 것이 과연 옳은지를 곰곰이 생각했다. 그 일이 다시금 나를 몹시 괴롭히기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["I had not walked out far enough to be quite clear of the town, upon the Ramsgate road, where there was a good path, when I was hailed, through the dust, by somebody behind me.", "마을을 완전히 벗어날 만큼 멀리 걷지도 못한 채, 길이 잘 닦인 램스게이트 가도에 들어섰을 때, 뒤에서 누군가가 흙먼지 사이로 나를 불러 세웠다."], ["The shambling figure, and the scanty great-coat, were not to be mistaken.", "그 비척거리는 걸음걸이와 헐렁한 외투는 못 알아볼 수가 없었다. 나는 멈춰 섰고, 유라이아 힙이 다가왔다."], ["I stopped, and Uriah Heep came up.", "\"무슨 일이야?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well?’ said I. ‘How fast you walk!’ said he.", "\"걸음이 어찌나 빠르신지!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘My legs are pretty long, but you’ve given ‘em quite a job.’", "\"제 다리가 꽤 긴 편인데도, 도련님 덕분에 아주 혼쭐이 났네요.\""]], [["‘Where are you going?’ said I.", "\"어디 가는 길이야?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am going with you, Master Copperfield, if you’ll allow me the pleasure of a walk with an old acquaintance.’ Saying this, with a jerk of his body, which might have been either propitiatory or derisive, he fell into step beside me.", "\"도련님하고 같이 가려고요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 옛 지인과 산책하는 즐거움을 허락해 주신다면요.\" 이렇게 말하면서, 그는 비위를 맞추려는 건지 비웃는 건지 모를 몸짓을 한 번 까딱하더니, 내 곁에서 보조를 맞춰 걸었다."]], [["‘Uriah!’ said I, as civilly as I could, after a silence.", "\"유라이아!\" 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 나는 되도록 정중하게 말했다."]], [["‘Master Copperfield!’ said Uriah.", "\"코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이아가 말했다."]], [["‘To tell you the truth (at which you will not be offended), I came Out to walk alone, because I have had so much company.’", "\"솔직히 말하면 (기분 상해하진 말고), 나는 사람들에게 너무 시달려서, 혼자 걸으려고 나온 거야.\""]], [["He looked at me sideways, and said with his hardest grin, ‘You mean mother.’", "그는 나를 곁눈으로 보더니, 그 더없이 짓궂은 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"어머니를 두고 하시는 말씀이군요.\""]], [["‘Why yes, I do,’ said I.", "\"뭐, 그래, 맞아.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But you know we’re so very umble,’ he returned.", "하지만 아시다시피 저희는 워낙 미천한 사람들이잖아요.\""], ["‘And having such a knowledge of our own umbleness, we must really take care that we’re not pushed to the wall by them as isn’t umble.", "그가 받았다. \"그리고 저희 자신의 미천함을 그토록 잘 아는 만큼, 미천하지 않은 자들에게 구석으로 밀려나지 않도록 정말 조심해야 한답니다."], ["All stratagems are fair in love, sir.’", "사랑에서는 어떤 술수든 정당하니까요, 나리.\""]], [["Raising his great hands until they touched his chin, he rubbed them softly, and softly chuckled; looking as like a malevolent baboon, I thought, as anything human could look.", "그는 커다란 두 손을 턱에 닿을 만큼 치켜들어 살살 비비고는, 나직이 낄낄거렸는데, 인간으로서 지을 수 있는 가장 악의에 찬 비비 원숭이의 표정이 아닐까 싶었다."]], [["‘You see,’ he said, still hugging himself in that unpleasant way, and shaking his head at me, ‘you’re quite a dangerous rival, Master Copperfield.", "\"보세요,\" 그는 여전히 그 불쾌한 식으로 제 몸을 끌어안고 내게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"도련님은 꽤나 위험한 연적이거든요, 코퍼필드 도련님."], ["You always was, you know.’", "늘 그러셨죠, 아시다시피.\""]], [["‘Do you set a watch upon Miss Wickfield, and make her home no home, because of me?’ said I.", "\"네가 나 때문에 위크필드 양을 감시하고, 그녀의 집을 집이 아니게 만드는 거냐?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh! Master Copperfield!", "\"오! 코퍼필드 도련님!"], ["Those are very arsh words,’ he replied.", "그거 참 험한 말씀이네요.\" 그가 대답했다."]], [["‘Put my meaning into any words you like,’ said I.", "\"내 뜻을 네가 좋을 대로 어떤 말로 바꿔도 좋아.\""], ["‘You know what it is, Uriah, as well as I do.’", "내가 말했다. \"그게 뭔지 너도 나만큼 잘 알잖아, 유라이아.\""]], [["‘Oh no!", "\"오 아니죠!"], ["You must put it into words,’ he said.", "도련님이 말로 옮기셔야죠.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Oh, really!", "\"오, 정말이지!"], ["I couldn’t myself.’", "저로선 도저히 못 하겠어요.\""]], [["‘Do you suppose,’ said I, constraining myself to be very temperate and quiet with him, on account of Agnes, ‘that I regard Miss Wickfield otherwise than as a very dear sister?’", "\"내가 위크필드 양을 더없이 사랑하는 누이가 아닌 다른 무엇으로 여긴다고 생각하는 거냐?\" 아그네스를 생각해서 그에게 아주 차분하고 조용히 대하려 애쓰며 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, Master Copperfield,’ he replied, ‘you perceive I am not bound to answer that question.", "\"글쎄요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 대답했다. \"보시다시피 저는 그 물음에 답할 의무가 없죠."], ["You may not, you know.", "아닐 수도 있고요, 아시다시피."], ["But then, you see, you may!’", "하지만 또 그런가 하면, 그럴 수도 있는 거고요!\""]], [["Anything to equal the low cunning of his visage, and of his shadowless eyes without the ghost of an eyelash, I never saw.", "그의 표정에 어린, 그리고 속눈썹 그림자 하나 없는 그 그늘 없는 두 눈에 어린 그 비루한 교활함에 견줄 만한 것을, 나는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."]], [["‘Come then!’ said I.", "\"좋아 그럼!\""], ["‘For the sake of Miss Wickfield--’", "내가 말했다. \"위크필드 양을 위해서—\""]], [["‘My Agnes!’ he exclaimed, with a sickly, angular contortion of himself.", "\"제 아그네스 말이죠!\" 그가 병적이고 모난 몸짓으로 제 몸을 뒤틀며 외쳤다."], ["‘Would you be so good as call her Agnes, Master Copperfield!’", "\"그분을 아그네스라고 불러 주시겠어요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["‘For the sake of Agnes Wickfield--Heaven bless her!’", "\"아그네스 위크필드를 위해서 — 하느님의 축복이 그녀에게 있기를!\""]], [["‘Thank you for that blessing, Master Copperfield!’ he interposed.", "\"그 축복 고맙습니다요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘I will tell you what I should, under any other circumstances, as soon have thought of telling to--Jack Ketch.’", "\"내가 어떤 다른 상황이었다면 차라리 잭 케치한테나 털어놓을까 싶은 이야기를 들려주지.\""]], [["‘To who, sir?’ said Uriah, stretching out his neck, and shading his ear with his hand.", "\"누구한테요, 나리?\" 유라이아가 목을 쭉 빼고 손으로 귀를 가리며 말했다."]], [["‘To the hangman,’ I returned.", "\"교수형 집행인 말이야.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘The most unlikely person I could think of,’--though his own face had suggested the allusion quite as a natural sequence.", "\"내가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 어림없는 상대지.\" — 정작 그의 얼굴이야말로 그 비유를 아주 자연스러운 순서로 떠올리게 해 주었지만 말이다."], ["‘I am engaged to another young lady.", "\"나는 다른 아가씨와 약혼했어."], ["I hope that contents you.’", "이걸로 네 마음이 풀리길 바란다.\""]], [["‘Upon your soul?’ said Uriah.", "\"영혼을 걸고요?\" 유라이아가 말했다."]], [["I was about indignantly to give my assertion the confirmation he required, when he caught hold of my hand, and gave it a squeeze.", "내가 발끈하여 그가 요구하는 확언을 해 주려는 찰나, 그가 내 손을 덥석 잡더니 꽉 움켜쥐었다."]], [["‘Oh, Master Copperfield!’ he said.", "\"오, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘If you had only had the condescension to return my confidence when I poured out the fulness of my art, the night I put you so much out of the way by sleeping before your sitting-room fire, I never should have doubted you.", "\"도련님 거실 난롯가에서 잠들어 그토록 폐를 끼쳐 드렸던 그날 밤, 제가 제 속내를 죄다 쏟아 냈을 때 도련님께서도 황송하게 마음을 터놓아 주셨더라면, 저는 결코 도련님을 의심하지 않았을 텐데요."], ["As it is, I’m sure I’ll take off mother directly, and only too appy.", "사정이 이러하니, 어머니는 당장 떼어 놓겠습니다. 그것도 더없이 기쁜 마음으로요."], ["I know you’ll excuse the precautions of affection, won’t you?", "애정에서 비롯된 조심성은 너그러이 봐주시겠죠?"], ["What a pity, Master Copperfield, that you didn’t condescend to return my confidence!", "정말 안타까워요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 도련님이 황송하게도 제게 속내를 터놓아 주지 않으신 게요!"], ["I’m sure I gave you every opportunity.", "저는 분명 도련님께 온갖 기회를 다 드렸는데 말이죠."], ["But you never have condescended to me, as much as I could have wished.", "하지만 도련님은 제가 바라는 만큼 황송하게 저를 대해 주신 적이 없어요."], ["I know you have never liked me, as I have liked you!’", "도련님은 제가 도련님을 좋아한 만큼 저를 좋아하신 적이 한 번도 없다는 걸 저도 알아요!\""]], [["All this time he was squeezing my hand with his damp fishy fingers, while I made every effort I decently could to get it away.", "이러는 내내 그는 그 축축하고 물고기 같은 손가락으로 내 손을 움켜쥐고 있었고, 나는 점잖게나마 손을 빼내려고 온갖 애를 다 썼다."], ["But I was quite unsuccessful.", "그러나 영 헛수고였다."], ["He drew it under the sleeve of his mulberry-coloured great-coat, and I walked on, almost upon compulsion, arm-in-arm with him.", "그는 내 손을 자기 오디색 외투 소매 밑으로 끌어당겼고, 나는 거의 떠밀리다시피 그와 팔짱을 낀 채 걸었다."]], [["‘Shall we turn?’ said Uriah, by and by wheeling me face about towards the town, on which the early moon was now shining, silvering the distant windows.", "\"돌아갈까요?\" 이윽고 유라이아가 나를 마을 쪽으로 휙 돌려세우며 말했다. 마을 위로는 어느새 초저녁 달이 빛나며 멀리 창문들을 은빛으로 물들이고 있었다."]], [["‘Before we leave the subject, you ought to understand,’ said I, breaking a pretty long silence, ‘that I believe Agnes Wickfield to be as far above you, and as far removed from all your aspirations, as that moon herself!’", "\"이 얘기를 그만두기 전에, 이건 분명히 알아 둬야겠다.\" 꽤 긴 침묵을 깨고 내가 말했다. \"내가 보기에 아그네스 위크필드는 너 따위보다 한참 위에 있고, 저 달 자체만큼이나 네 온갖 야심에서 멀리 떨어져 있어!\""]], [["‘Peaceful!", "\"고요하죠!"], ["Ain’t she!’ said Uriah. ‘Very!", "안 그래요!\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"정말요!"], ["Now confess, Master Copperfield, that you haven’t liked me quite as I have liked you.", "자, 인정하세요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 도련님은 제가 도련님을 좋아한 만큼은 저를 좋아하지 않으셨다는 걸요."], ["All along you’ve thought me too umble now, I shouldn’t wonder?’", "처음부터 줄곧 저를 너무 미천하다고 여기셨겠죠, 지금도요, 안 봐도 뻔하네요?\""]], [["‘I am not fond of professions of humility,’ I returned, ‘or professions of anything else.’ ‘There now!’ said Uriah, looking flabby and lead-coloured in the moonlight.", "\"나는 미천함을 늘어놓는 걸 좋아하지 않아.\" 내가 받았다. \"다른 무엇을 늘어놓는 것도 마찬가지고.\""], ["‘Didn’t I know it!", "\"그것 보세요!\""], ["But how little you think of the rightful umbleness of a person in my station, Master Copperfield!", "유라이아가 달빛 속에서 흐물흐물하고 납빛으로 보이며 말했다. \"제가 모를 줄 아셨어요!"], ["Father and me was both brought up at a foundation school for boys; and mother, she was likewise brought up at a public, sort of charitable, establishment.", "하지만 도련님은 저 같은 처지의 사람이 마땅히 지녀야 할 미천함을 얼마나 가볍게 여기시는지요, 코퍼필드 도련님! 아버지와 저는 둘 다 사내아이들을 위한 자선 학교에서 자랐고, 어머니도 그와 비슷한 공립 자선 시설에서 자랐답니다."], ["They taught us all a deal of umbleness--not much else that I know of, from morning to night.", "거기서 사람들은 우리에게 미천함을 잔뜩 가르쳤어요 — 아침부터 밤까지, 제가 아는 한 그 외엔 별로 가르친 게 없었죠."], ["We was to be umble to this person, and umble to that; and to pull off our caps here, and to make bows there; and always to know our place, and abase ourselves before our betters.", "우리는 이 사람에게도 미천해야 하고, 저 사람에게도 미천해야 하고, 여기서 모자를 벗어야 하고, 저기서 절을 해야 하고, 늘 제 분수를 알고 윗사람들 앞에서 굽실거려야 한다고요."], ["And we had such a lot of betters!", "게다가 윗사람도 어찌나 많던지요!"], ["Father got the monitor-medal by being umble. So did I.", "아버지는 미천하게 굴어서 모범생 메달을 받으셨고, 저도 그랬죠."], ["Father got made a sexton by being umble.", "아버지는 미천하게 굴어서 교회지기가 되셨어요."], ["He had the character, among the gentlefolks, of being such a well-behaved man, that they were determined to bring him in. “Be umble, Uriah,”", "점잖은 분들 사이에서 어찌나 행실 바른 사람이라는 평판을 얻으셨던지, 그분들이 기어이 그 자리에 앉히기로 작정하셨던 거예요. '미천하게 굴어라, 유라이아,'"], ["says father to me, “and you’ll get on.", "아버지가 제게 말씀하셨죠. '그러면 출세할 거다."], ["It was what was always being dinned into you and me at school; it’s what goes down best.", "학교에서 너와 나한테 늘 귀에 못이 박이도록 들려준 게 그거 아니냐. 그게 제일 잘 먹히는 거다. 미천하게 굴어라,'"], ["Be umble,” says father, “and you’ll do!”", "아버지가 말씀하셨죠. '그러면 잘될 거야!'"], ["And really it ain’t done bad!’", "그런데 정말이지 그게 나쁘지 않게 먹혔어요!\""]], [["It was the first time it had ever occurred to me, that this detestable cant of false humility might have originated out of the Heep family. I had seen the harvest, but had never thought of the seed.", "거짓 미천함이라는 이 가증스러운 위선이 힙 집안에서 비롯되었을지도 모른다는 생각이, 그때 처음으로 내 머리에 떠올랐다. 나는 그 수확물은 보아 왔으나, 그 씨앗에 대해서는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었던 것이다."]], [["‘When I was quite a young boy,’ said Uriah, ‘I got to know what umbleness did, and I took to it.", "\"제가 아주 어린 소년이었을 때,\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"저는 미천함이 어떤 일을 해내는지 알게 됐고, 거기에 푹 빠졌어요."], ["I ate umble pie with an appetite.", "굴욕의 떡을 입맛 다시며 먹었죠."], ["I stopped at the umble point of my learning, and says I, “Hold hard!”", "저는 제 배움을 미천한 수준에서 딱 멈추고는, '여기서 멈춰!' 하고 다짐했어요."], ["When you offered to teach me Latin, I knew better.", "도련님이 제게 라틴어를 가르쳐 주겠다고 하셨을 때, 저는 더 잘 알았죠."], ["“People like to be above you,” says father, “keep yourself down.”", "'사람들은 자기가 너보다 위에 있는 걸 좋아하는 법이다,' 아버지가 말씀하셨거든요."], ["I am very umble to the present moment, Master Copperfield, but I’ve got a little power!’", "'몸을 낮춰라.' 저는 지금 이 순간까지도 아주 미천하답니다, 코퍼필드 도련님, 하지만 이제 저도 조금은 힘을 갖게 됐어요!\""]], [["And he said all this--I knew, as I saw his face in the moonlight--that I might understand he was resolved to recompense himself by using his power.", "그리고 그가 이 모든 말을 한 까닭은 — 달빛 속에서 그의 얼굴을 보며 나는 알았다 — 그가 자기 힘을 써서 스스로 그 빚을 되갚으리라 작정했음을 내게 알리려는 것이었다."], ["I had never doubted his meanness, his craft and malice; but I fully comprehended now, for the first time, what a base, unrelenting, and revengeful spirit, must have been engendered by this early, and this long, suppression.", "나는 그의 비열함과 교활함과 악의를 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었다. 그러나 이 어린 시절부터의, 이토록 오랜 억압이 얼마나 비루하고 무자비하며 앙심에 찬 영혼을 길러 냈을지를, 이제야 처음으로 온전히 깨달았다."]], [["His account of himself was so far attended with an agreeable result, that it led to his withdrawing his hand in order that he might have another hug of himself under the chin.", "자기 자신에 대한 그의 이 이야기에는 그나마 한 가지 반가운 결과가 따랐으니, 그가 손을 빼내어 다시 한번 제 턱 밑에서 자기 몸을 끌어안게 되었다는 것이다."], ["Once apart from him, I was determined to keep apart; and we walked back, side by side, saying very little more by the way.", "일단 그에게서 떨어지자, 나는 떨어진 채로 있기로 마음먹었다. 우리는 나란히 걸어 돌아오면서, 오는 길에 더는 별말을 하지 않았다."], ["Whether his spirits were elevated by the communication I had made to him, or by his having indulged in this retrospect, I don’t know; but they were raised by some influence.", "내가 그에게 털어놓은 이야기 때문에 그의 기분이 들떴는지, 아니면 그가 이 회상에 흠뻑 잠겨 든 탓인지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그의 기분은 무언가의 영향으로 한껏 들떠 있었다."], ["He talked more at dinner than was usual with him; asked his mother (off duty, from the moment of our re-entering the house) whether he was not growing too old for a bachelor; and once looked at Agnes so, that I would have given all I had, for leave to knock him down.", "그는 저녁 식사 자리에서 평소보다 말이 많았다. 어머니에게 (집에 다시 들어선 순간부터 임무에서 풀려난 그녀에게) 자기가 이제 노총각으로 지내기엔 나이가 너무 든 것 아니냐고 물었고, 한번은 아그네스를 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보던지, 나는 그를 때려눕힐 수만 있다면 가진 것을 죄다 내줘도 좋다고 생각했다."]], [["When we three males were left alone after dinner, he got into a more adventurous state.", "저녁 식사 후 우리 남자 셋만 남게 되자, 그는 한층 대담한 상태가 되었다."], ["He had taken little or no wine; and I presume it was the mere insolence of triumph that was upon him, flushed perhaps by the temptation my presence furnished to its exhibition.", "그는 술을 거의, 아니 전혀 입에 대지 않았다. 그러니 그것은 그저 승리감에 도취된 오만함이었으리라 짐작되는데, 어쩌면 그것을 한껏 드러내 보이고픈 유혹을 내 존재가 부추긴 탓에 더욱 달아올랐는지도 모른다."]], [["I had observed yesterday, that he tried to entice Mr. Wickfield to drink; and, interpreting the look which Agnes had given me as she went out, had limited myself to one glass, and then proposed that we should follow her.", "나는 어제, 그가 위크필드 씨에게 술을 권해 마시게 하려 드는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그래서 아그네스가 나가면서 내게 던진 눈길의 뜻을 헤아려, 나는 한 잔으로 그쳤고, 그러고는 우리도 그녀를 따라가자고 제안했다."], ["I would have done so again today; but Uriah was too quick for me.", "오늘도 그렇게 하고 싶었지만, 유라이아가 나보다 한발 빨랐다."]], [["‘We seldom see our present visitor, sir,’ he said, addressing Mr.", "\"우리 손님을 자주 뵙지도 못하잖습니까, 나리.\""], ["Wickfield, sitting, such a contrast to him, at the end of the table, ‘and I should propose to give him welcome in another glass or two of wine, if you have no objections. Mr.", "그가, 식탁 끝에 그와는 너무도 대조적인 모습으로 앉아 있는 위크필드 씨를 향해 말했다. \"그러니 괜찮으시다면 포도주 한두 잔으로 손님을 환대하면 어떨까 싶은데요."], ["Copperfield, your elth and appiness!’", "코퍼필드 씨, 도련님의 건강과 행복을 위하여!\""]], [["I was obliged to make a show of taking the hand he stretched across to me; and then, with very different emotions, I took the hand of the broken gentleman, his partner.", "나는 그가 식탁 너머로 내민 손을 잡는 시늉이라도 해야 했다. 그러고 나서, 사뭇 다른 감정으로, 나는 그의 동업자인 그 무너진 신사의 손을 잡았다."]], [["‘Come, fellow-partner,’ said Uriah, ‘if I may take the liberty,--now, suppose you give us something or another appropriate to Copperfield!’", "\"자, 동업자 양반,\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"외람되지만 말씀드리자면 — 자, 이번엔 코퍼필드에게 어울릴 만한 무언가로 한 잔 권해 주시지요!\""]], [["I pass over Mr. Wickfield’s proposing my aunt, his proposing Mr. Dick, his proposing Doctors’ Commons, his proposing Uriah, his drinking everything twice; his consciousness of his own weakness, the ineffectual effort that he made against it; the struggle between his shame in Uriah’s deportment, and his desire to conciliate him; the manifest exultation with which Uriah twisted and turned, and held him up before me.", "위크필드 씨가 내 고모님을 위하여, 딕 씨를 위하여, 민법 박사회를 위하여, 유라이아를 위하여 잔을 들자고 제안하며, 모든 것을 두 잔씩 마셔 댄 일이며, 자신의 나약함을 의식하면서도 그것에 맞서 헛되이 안간힘을 쓰던 일이며, 유라이아의 처신에 부끄러워하면서도 그를 달래고 싶어 하던 그 갈등이며, 유라이아가 그를 비틀고 뒤틀어 내 앞에서 조롱거리로 떠받들며 드러내던 그 노골적인 의기양양함은, 그냥 넘어가겠다."], ["It made me sick at heart to see, and my hand recoils from writing it.", "그것을 지켜보는 것만으로도 가슴이 메스꺼웠고, 그것을 적으려니 손이 절로 움츠러든다."]], [["‘Come, fellow-partner!’ said Uriah, at last, ‘I’ll give you another one, and I umbly ask for bumpers, seeing I intend to make it the divinest of her sex.’", "\"자, 동업자 양반!\" 마침내 유라이아가 말했다. \"한 잔 더 올리겠습니다. 그런데 이번엔 잔을 가득 채워 주시기를 황송하게 청합니다. 여인들 중에서도 가장 거룩한 분을 위해 들 작정이니까요.\""]], [["Her father had his empty glass in his hand.", "그녀의 아버지는 빈 잔을 손에 들고 있었다."], ["I saw him set it down, look at the picture she was so like, put his hand to his forehead, and shrink back in his elbow-chair.", "나는 그가 잔을 내려놓고, 그녀를 그토록 빼닮은 초상화를 바라보더니, 손을 이마에 갖다 대고 안락의자 깊숙이 움츠러드는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘I’m an umble individual to give you her elth,’ proceeded Uriah, ‘but I admire--adore her.’", "\"제가 그분의 건강을 위해 잔을 들기엔 미천한 인간이지요.\" 유라이아가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 저는 그분을 흠모합니다 — 아니, 숭배합니다.\""]], [["No physical pain that her father’s grey head could have borne, I think, could have been more terrible to me, than the mental endurance I saw compressed now within both his hands.", "그녀의 아버지의 그 희끗한 머리가 견딜 수 있을 어떤 육체의 고통도, 지금 그가 두 손에 짓눌러 담고 있던 그 마음의 인내보다 더 끔찍하지는 않았으리라."]], [["‘Agnes,’ said Uriah, either not regarding him, or not knowing what the nature of his action was, ‘Agnes Wickfield is, I am safe to say, the divinest of her sex.", "\"아그네스,\" 유라이아가, 그를 안중에 두지 않는 건지 아니면 자기 행동이 어떤 것인지 모르는 건지, 이렇게 말했다. \"아그네스 위크필드는, 단언컨대, 여인들 중에서도 가장 거룩한 분입니다."], ["May I speak out, among friends?", "친구들 사이니 터놓고 말해도 되겠지요?"], ["To be her father is a proud distinction, but to be her usband--’", "그분의 아버지가 된다는 건 자랑스러운 영예이지만, 그분의 남편이 된다는 건—\""]], [["Spare me from ever again hearing such a cry, as that with which her father rose up from the table!", "그녀의 아버지가 식탁에서 벌떡 일어서며 내지른 그 비명을, 다시는 듣지 않게 해 주소서!"], ["‘What’s the matter?’ said Uriah, turning of a deadly colour.", "\"무슨 일이십니까?\" 유라이아가 핏기가 싹 가신 얼굴로 말했다."], ["‘You are not gone mad, after all, Mr. Wickfield, I hope?", "\"설마 결국 미쳐 버리신 건 아니겠죠, 위크필드 씨?"], ["If I say I’ve an ambition to make your Agnes my Agnes, I have as good a right to it as another man.", "제가 댁의 아그네스를 제 아그네스로 만들고 싶은 야심이 있다고 한들, 저도 다른 사내만큼 그럴 권리가 있습니다."], ["I have a better right to it than any other man!’", "아니, 다른 어느 사내보다 더 그럴 권리가 있다고요!\""]], [["I had my arms round Mr. Wickfield, imploring him by everything that I could think of, oftenest of all by his love for Agnes, to calm himself a little.", "나는 두 팔로 위크필드 씨를 끌어안고, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것을 다 들먹이며, 그중에서도 아그네스에 대한 그의 사랑을 가장 많이 들먹이며, 부디 마음을 조금만 가라앉히시라고 애원했다."], ["He was mad for the moment; tearing out his hair, beating his head, trying to force me from him, and to force himself from me, not answering a word, not looking at or seeing anyone; blindly striving for he knew not what, his face all staring and distorted--a frightful spectacle.", "그는 한순간 제정신이 아니었다. 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고, 제 머리를 두드리고, 나를 떼어 내려 하고, 제 몸을 내게서 빼내려 하면서, 한마디도 대답하지 않고, 아무도 쳐다보거나 알아보지 못한 채, 자기도 무엇인지 모를 것을 향해 무작정 안간힘을 썼는데, 그 얼굴은 온통 부릅뜨고 일그러져 — 보기에도 끔찍한 광경이었다."]], [["I conjured him, incoherently, but in the most impassioned manner, not to abandon himself to this wildness, but to hear me.", "나는 두서없이, 그러나 더없이 간절하게, 부디 이 광기에 자신을 내맡기지 마시고 내 말을 들어 달라고 그에게 빌었다."], ["I besought him to think of Agnes, to connect me with Agnes, to recollect how Agnes and I had grown up together, how I honoured her and loved her, how she was his pride and joy.", "아그네스를 생각하시라고, 나를 아그네스와 결부지어 생각하시라고, 아그네스와 내가 어떻게 함께 자라 왔는지, 내가 그녀를 얼마나 존경하고 사랑하는지, 그녀가 그분의 자랑이자 기쁨임을 떠올려 보시라고 애원했다."], ["I tried to bring her idea before him in any form; I even reproached him with not having firmness to spare her the knowledge of such a scene as this.", "나는 어떤 식으로든 그녀의 모습을 그의 앞에 떠올려 주려 애썼다. 심지어 이런 장면을 그녀가 알지 못하도록 막아 줄 만한 굳건함조차 없으시냐고 나무라기까지 했다."], ["I may have effected something, or his wildness may have spent itself; but by degrees he struggled less, and began to look at me--strangely at first, then with recognition in his eyes.", "내가 무언가를 해냈는지, 아니면 그의 광기가 제풀에 다한 것인지 모르겠으나, 차츰 그는 덜 몸부림치더니, 나를 바라보기 시작했다 — 처음엔 낯설게, 그러다 눈에 알아보는 빛을 띠고서."], ["At length he said, ‘I know, Trotwood!", "마침내 그가 말했다. \"안다네, 트롯우드!"], ["My darling child and you--I know!", "내 사랑하는 아이와 자네는 — 다 알아!"], ["But look at him!’", "하지만 저자를 보게!\""]], [["He pointed to Uriah, pale and glowering in a corner, evidently very much out in his calculations, and taken by surprise.", "그가 유라이아를 가리켰다. 그는 한쪽 구석에서 핼쑥하게 노려보고 있었는데, 분명 자기 계산이 한참 빗나가 허를 찔린 기색이 역력했다."]], [["‘Look at my torturer,’ he replied.", "\"내 고문자를 보게.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Before him I have step by step abandoned name and reputation, peace and quiet, house and home.’", "\"저자 앞에서 나는 한 걸음 한 걸음, 이름과 평판을, 평화와 안식을, 집과 가정을 다 내놓았네.\""]], [["‘I have kept your name and reputation for you, and your peace and quiet, and your house and home too,’ said Uriah, with a sulky, hurried, defeated air of compromise.", "\"제가 댁의 이름과 평판을 지켜 드렸고, 댁의 평화와 안식도, 댁의 집과 가정도 지켜 드렸지요.\" 유라이아가 시무룩하고 다급하며 패배한 듯한 타협조로 말했다."], ["‘Don’t be foolish, Mr. Wickfield.", "\"어리석게 굴지 마세요, 위크필드 씨."], ["If I have gone a little beyond what you were prepared for, I can go back, I suppose?", "제가 댁이 마음의 준비를 한 것보다 조금 더 나갔다면, 도로 물릴 수도 있죠, 안 그래요?"], ["There’s no harm done.’", "해될 건 없잖습니까.\""]], [["‘I looked for single motives in everyone,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 누구에게서나 단순한 동기 하나만을 찾으려 했네.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield, ‘and I was satisfied I had bound him to me by motives of interest. But see what he is--oh, see what he is!’", "\"그래서 나는 이익이라는 동기로 저자를 내게 묶어 두었다고 흡족해했지. 하지만 저자가 어떤 놈인지 보게나 — 오, 저자가 어떤 놈인지 좀 보라고!\""]], [["‘You had better stop him, Copperfield, if you can,’ cried Uriah, with his long forefinger pointing towards me.", "\"가능하면 저분 입 좀 막으시는 게 좋을 겁니다, 코퍼필드.\" 유라이아가 그 긴 집게손가락을 내 쪽으로 겨누며 외쳤다."], ["‘He’ll say something presently--mind you!--he’ll be sorry to have said afterwards, and you’ll be sorry to have heard!’", "\"저분이 곧 무슨 말을 할 텐데 — 명심하세요! — 나중에 가서 한 걸 후회할 말을요. 그러면 도련님도 그걸 들은 걸 후회하실 테고요!\""]], [["‘I’ll say anything!’ cried Mr.", "\"무슨 말이든 하고 말겠다!\""], ["Wickfield, with a desperate air.", "위크필드 씨가 절망에 찬 표정으로 외쳤다."], ["‘Why should I not be in all the world’s power if I am in yours?’", "\"내가 자네 손아귀에 있는 마당에, 어찌 온 세상의 손아귀에 들어가지 않을 수 있겠나?\""]], [["‘Mind!", "\"명심하세요!"], ["I tell you!’ said Uriah, continuing to warn me.", "말씀드립니다!\" 유라이아가 내게 계속 경고하며 말했다."], ["‘If you don’t stop his mouth, you’re not his friend!", "\"저분 입을 막지 않으시면, 도련님은 저분의 친구가 아닙니다!"], ["Why shouldn’t you be in all the world’s power, Mr. Wickfield?", "어찌 온 세상의 손아귀에 들어가지 않을 수 있겠느냐고요, 위크필드 씨?"], ["Because you have got a daughter.", "댁에게 딸이 있기 때문이죠."], ["You and me know what we know, don’t we?", "댁과 저는 우리가 아는 걸 알고 있죠, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["Let sleeping dogs lie--who wants to rouse ‘em? I don’t.", "잠자는 개는 그냥 누워 있게 두는 겁니다 — 누가 그놈을 깨우고 싶겠어요? 저는 아니에요."], ["Can’t you see I am as umble as I can be?", "제가 더없이 미천하게 굴고 있는 게 안 보이세요?"], ["I tell you, if I’ve gone too far, I’m sorry.", "말씀드리지만, 제가 너무 멀리 나갔다면, 미안합니다."], ["What would you have, sir?’", "더 무엇을 바라십니까, 나리?\""]], [["‘Oh, Trotwood, Trotwood!’ exclaimed Mr. Wickfield, wringing his hands.", "\"오, 트롯우드, 트롯우드!\" 위크필드 씨가 두 손을 쥐어짜며 외쳤다."], ["‘What I have come down to be, since I first saw you in this house!", "\"내가 이 집에서 자네를 처음 본 뒤로, 이 지경까지 떨어지다니!"], ["I was on my downward way then, but the dreary, dreary road I have traversed since!", "그때도 나는 내리막길에 들어서 있었지만, 그 뒤로 내가 걸어온 그 황량하고 황량한 길이라니!"], ["Weak indulgence has ruined me.", "나약한 탐닉이 나를 망쳤네."], ["Indulgence in remembrance, and indulgence in forgetfulness.", "추억에 빠지는 탐닉, 그리고 망각에 빠지는 탐닉 말일세."], ["My natural grief for my child’s mother turned to disease; my natural love for my child turned to disease.", "내 아이의 어미를 향한 자연스러운 슬픔이 병이 되었고, 내 아이를 향한 자연스러운 사랑이 병이 되었네."], ["I have infected everything I touched.", "나는 내가 손대는 모든 것을 병들게 했어."], ["I have brought misery on what I dearly love, I know--you know!", "내가 끔찍이 사랑하는 것에 비참함을 안겨 주었지, 나도 알아 — 자네도 알지!"], ["I thought it possible that I could truly love one creature in the world, and not love the rest; I thought it possible that I could truly mourn for one creature gone out of the world, and not have some part in the grief of all who mourned.", "나는 세상에서 한 사람만을 진실로 사랑하면서 나머지는 사랑하지 않아도 된다고 여겼네. 세상을 떠난 한 사람만을 진실로 애도하면서, 애도하는 모든 이의 슬픔에 한몫 끼지 않아도 된다고 여겼지."], ["Thus the lessons of my life have been perverted!", "그렇게 내 인생의 가르침이 비뚤어지고 만 거야!"], ["I have preyed on my own morbid coward heart, and it has preyed on me.", "나는 병들고 비겁한 내 마음을 파먹었고, 그 마음이 나를 파먹었네."], ["Sordid in my grief, sordid in my love, sordid in my miserable escape from the darker side of both, oh see the ruin I am, and hate me, shun me!’", "슬픔에서도 비루하고, 사랑에서도 비루하고, 그 둘의 어두운 면에서 비참하게 달아나는 데서도 비루한 인간, 오, 내가 얼마나 폐허가 되었는지 보게나, 그리고 나를 미워하게, 나를 멀리하게!\""]], [["He dropped into a chair, and weakly sobbed.", "그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 힘없이 흐느꼈다."], ["The excitement into which he had been roused was leaving him. Uriah came out of his corner.", "그를 들끓게 했던 흥분이 가시고 있었다. 유라이아가 제 구석에서 걸어 나왔다."]], [["‘I don’t know all I have done, in my fatuity,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 어리석은 나머지 저지른 짓을 나는 다 알지 못하네.\""], ["Wickfield, putting out his hands, as if to deprecate my condemnation.", "위크필드 씨가, 마치 내 비난을 만류하려는 듯 두 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["‘He knows best,’ meaning Uriah Heep, ‘for he has always been at my elbow, whispering me.", "\"저자가 가장 잘 알겠지.\" 유라이아 힙을 가리키는 말이었다. \"저자는 늘 내 곁에 붙어 내게 속삭여 왔으니까."], ["You see the millstone that he is about my neck.", "저자가 내 목에 매달린 맷돌이라는 게 보이지."], ["You find him in my house, you find him in my business.", "자네는 저자를 내 집에서 마주치고, 저자를 내 사업에서 마주치네."], ["You heard him, but a little time ago.", "자네도 방금 전에 저자가 하는 말을 들었지."], ["What need have I to say more!’", "내가 더 무슨 말을 할 필요가 있겠나!\""]], [["‘You haven’t need to say so much, nor half so much, nor anything at all,’ observed Uriah, half defiant, and half fawning.", "\"그렇게 많이, 아니 그 절반도, 아니 아무 말도 하실 필요 없었어요.\" 유라이아가 반쯤은 대들고 반쯤은 알랑거리며 한마디 했다."], ["‘You wouldn’t have took it up so, if it hadn’t been for the wine.", "\"포도주만 아니었으면 그렇게까지 받아들이진 않으셨을 거예요."], ["You’ll think better of it tomorrow, sir.", "내일이면 다시 생각이 달라지실 겁니다, 나리."], ["If I have said too much, or more than I meant, what of it?", "제가 너무 많은 말을, 아니 본심보다 지나친 말을 했다 한들, 그게 어떻다는 겁니까?"], ["I haven’t stood by it!’", "저는 그 말을 고수하지도 않았는걸요!\""]], [["The door opened, and Agnes, gliding in, without a vestige of colour in her face, put her arm round his neck, and steadily said, ‘Papa, you are not well.", "문이 열리고, 아그네스가 얼굴에 핏기 하나 없이 미끄러지듯 들어오더니, 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 차분하게 말했다. \"아빠, 몸이 안 좋으세요."], ["Come with me!’", "저랑 가요!\""]], [["He laid his head upon her shoulder, as if he were oppressed with heavy shame, and went out with her. Her eyes met mine for but an instant, yet I saw how much she knew of what had passed.", "그는 무거운 수치심에 짓눌린 듯 그녀의 어깨에 고개를 기댄 채, 그녀와 함께 나갔다. 그녀의 눈이 잠깐 내 눈과 마주쳤을 뿐인데, 나는 그녀가 방금 벌어진 일을 얼마나 많이 알고 있는지 알 수 있었다."]], [["‘I didn’t expect he’d cut up so rough, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah.", "\"그분이 그렇게 거칠게 나오실 줄은 몰랐네요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 유라이아가 말했다."], ["‘But it’s nothing. I’ll be friends with him tomorrow.", "\"하지만 아무것도 아니에요. 내일이면 그분과 화해할 겁니다."], ["It’s for his good.", "다 그분을 위한 거예요."], ["I’m umbly anxious for his good.’", "저는 황송하게도 그분이 잘되기를 바라거든요.\""]], [["I gave him no answer, and went upstairs into the quiet room where Agnes had so often sat beside me at my books.", "나는 그에게 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 위층의 그 고요한 방으로 올라갔다. 아그네스가 그토록 자주 내 곁에서 함께 책을 보던 그 방이었다."], ["Nobody came near me until late at night.", "밤이 깊도록 아무도 내게 다가오지 않았다."], ["I took up a book, and tried to read.", "나는 책을 집어 들고 읽어 보려 했다."], ["I heard the clocks strike twelve, and was still reading, without knowing what I read, when Agnes touched me.", "시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리가 들렸고, 그래도 나는 내가 무엇을 읽고 있는지도 모른 채 여전히 읽고 있었는데, 그때 아그네스가 나를 건드렸다."]], [["‘You will be going early in the morning, Trotwood!", "\"내일 아침 일찍 떠날 거잖아, 트롯우드!"], ["Let us say good-bye, now!’", "지금 작별 인사를 해 두자!\""]], [["She had been weeping, but her face then was so calm and beautiful!", "그녀는 울고 있었으나, 그때 그녀의 얼굴은 어찌나 차분하고 아름답던지!"]], [["‘Heaven bless you!’ she said, giving me her hand.", "\"하느님의 축복이 함께하길!\" 그녀가 내게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["‘Dearest Agnes!’ I returned, ‘I see you ask me not to speak of tonight--but is there nothing to be done?’", "\"더없이 소중한 아그네스!\" 내가 받았다. \"오늘 밤 일은 입에 올리지 말라고 네가 청하는 걸 알아 — 하지만 정말 아무것도 할 게 없는 거야?\""]], [["‘There is God to trust in!’ she replied.", "\"믿고 의지할 하느님이 계셔!\" 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["‘Can I do nothing--I, who come to you with my poor sorrows?’", "\"내가 아무것도 못 한단 말이야 — 이렇게 변변찮은 슬픔이나 안고 네게 오는 내가?\""]], [["‘And make mine so much lighter,’ she replied.", "\"그래서 내 슬픔을 그토록 가볍게 해 주잖아.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘Dear Trotwood, no!’", "\"사랑하는 트롯우드, 아무것도 하지 마!\""]], [["‘Dear Agnes,’ I said, ‘it is presumptuous for me, who am so poor in all in which you are so rich--goodness, resolution, all noble qualities--to doubt or direct you; but you know how much I love you, and how much I owe you.", "\"사랑하는 아그네스,\" 내가 말했다. \"네가 그토록 풍요롭게 지닌 모든 것에서 — 선함, 결단력, 온갖 고귀한 자질에서 — 그토록 빈곤한 내가 너를 의심하거나 이끌려 든다는 건 주제넘은 일이야."], ["You will never sacrifice yourself to a mistaken sense of duty, Agnes?’", "하지만 너도 알지, 내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 또 네게 얼마나 큰 빚을 졌는지. 너는 잘못된 의무감 때문에 자신을 희생하는 일은 결코 없을 거지, 아그네스?\""]], [["More agitated for a moment than I had ever seen her, she took her hands from me, and moved a step back.", "내가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 한순간 동요하며, 그녀는 내게서 두 손을 빼고 한 걸음 물러섰다."]], [["‘Say you have no such thought, dear Agnes!", "\"그런 생각은 없다고 말해 줘, 사랑하는 아그네스!"], ["Much more than sister!", "누이보다 훨씬 더 소중한 너!"], ["Think of the priceless gift of such a heart as yours, of such a love as yours!’", "너의 그런 마음 같은, 너의 그런 사랑 같은 더없이 값진 선물을 생각해 봐!\""]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["long, long afterwards, I saw that face rise up before me, with its momentary look, not wondering, not accusing, not regretting.", "오랜, 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤, 나는 그 얼굴이 내 앞에 떠오르는 것을 보았다. 그 찰나의 표정, 놀라지도, 탓하지도, 아쉬워하지도 않던 그 표정과 함께."], ["Oh, long, long afterwards, I saw that look subside, as it did now, into the lovely smile, with which she told me she had no fear for herself--I need have none for her--and parted from me by the name of Brother, and was gone!", "오, 오랜, 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤, 나는 그 표정이, 지금처럼, 그 사랑스러운 미소로 잦아드는 것을 보았다. 그 미소로 그녀는, 자신에 대해서는 아무 두려움이 없으니 — 나도 그녀를 두고 두려워할 필요가 없다고 말하고는, 오라버니라는 이름으로 나와 작별하고, 떠나갔다!"]], [["It was dark in the morning, when I got upon the coach at the inn door.", "아침에 여관 문 앞에서 마차에 올랐을 때, 날은 아직 어두웠다."], ["The day was just breaking when we were about to start, and then, as I sat thinking of her, came struggling up the coach side, through the mingled day and night, Uriah’s head.", "막 출발하려 할 무렵에야 동이 트기 시작했는데, 그때 그녀를 생각하며 앉아 있던 내게, 낮과 밤이 뒤섞인 어스름을 뚫고 유라이아의 머리가 마차 옆으로 비척비척 올라왔다."]], [["‘Copperfield!’ said he, in a croaking whisper, as he hung by the iron on the roof, ‘I thought you’d be glad to hear before you went off, that there are no squares broke between us.", "\"코퍼필드!\" 그가 지붕의 쇠막대에 매달린 채, 까마귀 울음 같은 목소리로 속삭이듯 말했다. \"떠나시기 전에 들으면 기뻐하실 것 같아서요."], ["I’ve been into his room already, and we’ve made it all smooth.", "우리 사이엔 깨진 게 하나도 없답니다. 저는 벌써 그분 방에 다녀왔고, 우리는 다 매끄럽게 풀었어요."], ["Why, though I’m umble, I’m useful to him, you know; and he understands his interest when he isn’t in liquor!", "아, 저는 미천할지언정 그분에게 쓸모 있는 사람이거든요, 아시다시피. 그분은 술에 안 취했을 땐 자기 잇속을 잘 챙기시죠!"], ["What an agreeable man he is, after all, Master Copperfield!’", "그분도 알고 보면 어찌나 호감 가는 분인지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["I obliged myself to say that I was glad he had made his apology.", "나는 그가 사과를 했다니 다행이라는 말을 억지로 내뱉었다."]], [["‘Oh, to be sure!’ said Uriah.", "\"오, 그럼요!\" 유라이아가 말했다."], ["‘When a person’s umble, you know, what’s an apology?", "\"사람이 미천하면, 아시다시피, 사과쯤이야 뭐가 대수겠어요? 그저 쉬운 일이죠! 그런데요!"], ["So easy!", "짐작건대,\""], ["I say! I suppose,’ with a jerk, ‘you have sometimes plucked a pear before it was ripe, Master Copperfield?’", "그가 몸을 한 번 까딱하며 말했다. \"도련님도 가끔은 배가 익기도 전에 따 본 적이 있으시겠죠, 코퍼필드 도련님?\""]], [["‘I suppose I have,’ I replied.", "\"그런 적이 있겠지.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘I did that last night,’ said Uriah; ‘but it’ll ripen yet!", "\"저는 어젯밤에 그걸 했어요.\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"하지만 그것도 곧 익을 겁니다!"], ["It only wants attending to. I can wait!’", "그저 손길이 좀 필요할 뿐이죠. 저는 기다릴 수 있거든요!\""]], [["Profuse in his farewells, he got down again as the coachman got up.", "작별 인사를 잔뜩 늘어놓고 나서, 마부가 올라타자 그도 다시 내려갔다."], ["For anything I know, he was eating something to keep the raw morning air out; but he made motions with his mouth as if the pear were ripe already, and he were smacking his lips over it.", "내가 아는 한, 그는 차가운 아침 공기를 막으려고 무언가를 씹고 있었는지도 모른다. 하지만 그는, 마치 그 배가 벌써 익기라도 한 듯, 또 그것을 맛보며 입맛을 다시기라도 하듯 입을 우물거렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER 40. THE WANDERER", "# CHAPTER 40. THE WANDERER"]], [["We had a very serious conversation in Buckingham Street that night, about the domestic occurrences I have detailed in the last chapter.", "그날 밤 우리는 버킹엄 거리에서, 내가 지난 장에 자세히 적은 집안일들을 두고 아주 심각하게 이야기를 나눴다."], ["My aunt was deeply interested in them, and walked up and down the room with her arms folded, for more than two hours afterwards.", "고모님은 그 일에 깊이 마음을 쓰셨고, 그 뒤로 두 시간 넘게 팔짱을 낀 채 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하셨다."], ["Whenever she was particularly discomposed, she always performed one of these pedestrian feats; and the amount of her discomposure might always be estimated by the duration of her walk.", "마음이 유난히 어지러울 때면 고모님은 늘 이런 보행 묘기를 한바탕 펼치셨고, 그 어지러움의 정도는 걸음을 얼마나 오래 잇느냐로 늘 가늠할 수 있었다."], ["On this occasion she was so much disturbed in mind as to find it necessary to open the bedroom door, and make a course for herself, comprising the full extent of the bedrooms from wall to wall; and while Mr.", "이번에는 마음이 어찌나 심란하셨던지 침실 문까지 열어 두고, 침실 벽에서 벽까지 그 폭을 통째로 아우르는 경로를 스스로 마련하실 필요를 느끼셨다."], ["Dick and I sat quietly by the fire, she kept passing in and out, along this measured track, at an unchanging pace, with the regularity of a clock-pendulum.", "그리고 디크 씨와 내가 난롯가에 가만히 앉아 있는 동안, 고모님은 그 잰 듯한 길을 따라 들고 나기를 반복하셨는데, 그 걸음걸이가 시계추처럼 한결같이 규칙적이었다."]], [["When my aunt and I were left to ourselves by Mr. Dick’s going out to bed, I sat down to write my letter to the two old ladies.", "디크 씨가 자러 들어가 고모님과 나만 남게 되자, 나는 두 노부인에게 보낼 편지를 쓰려고 자리에 앉았다."], ["By that time she was tired of walking, and sat by the fire with her dress tucked up as usual.", "그 무렵 고모님은 걷는 데 지쳐, 늘 그러시듯 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고 난롯가에 앉아 계셨다."], ["But instead of sitting in her usual manner, holding her glass upon her knee, she suffered it to stand neglected on the chimney-piece; and, resting her left elbow on her right arm, and her chin on her left hand, looked thoughtfully at me.", "그러나 평소처럼 잔을 무릎에 올려 든 채 앉아 계신 게 아니라, 잔을 벽난로 선반에 그냥 내버려 둔 채로, 왼쪽 팔꿈치를 오른팔에 괴고 턱을 왼손에 받친 채, 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 나를 바라보셨다."], ["As often as I raised my eyes from what I was about, I met hers.", "하던 일에서 눈을 들 때마다 나는 고모님의 눈길과 마주쳤다."], ["‘I am in the lovingest of tempers, my dear,’ she would assure me with a nod, ‘but I am fidgeted and sorry!’", "\"난 더없이 다정한 마음인 거야, 얘야.\" 고모님은 고개를 끄덕이며 나를 안심시키곤 하셨다. \"다만 안절부절못하고 마음이 아플 뿐이지!\""]], [["I had been too busy to observe, until after she was gone to bed, that she had left her night-mixture, as she always called it, untasted on the chimney-piece.", "나는 너무 바빠서, 고모님이 잠자리에 드시고 나서야 비로소 그분이 늘 '밤 약물'이라고 부르시던 그것을 벽난로 선반에 입도 안 댄 채 그냥 두고 가신 것을 알아챘다."], ["She came to her door, with even more than her usual affection of manner, when I knocked to acquaint her with this discovery; but only said, ‘I have not the heart to take it, Trot, tonight,’ and shook her head, and went in again.", "이 발견을 알리려고 내가 문을 두드리자, 고모님은 평소보다 한층 더 다정한 태도로 문 앞에 나오셨다. 그러나 \"오늘 밤은 그걸 마실 마음이 없구나, 트롯.\" 이렇게만 말씀하시고는, 고개를 가로젓더니 다시 안으로 들어가셨다."]], [["She read my letter to the two old ladies, in the morning, and approved of it.", "아침에 고모님은 두 노부인에게 보낼 내 편지를 읽고는 흡족해하셨다."], ["I posted it, and had nothing to do then, but wait, as patiently as I could, for the reply.", "나는 편지를 부쳤고, 그러고 나서는 답장을 되도록 참을성 있게 기다리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었다."], ["I was still in this state of expectation, and had been, for nearly a week; when I left the Doctor’s one snowy night, to walk home.", "나는 여전히 이렇게 기다리는 상태였고, 그러기를 거의 일주일째였는데, 어느 눈 오는 밤 박사님 댁을 나와 집으로 걸어가던 참이었다."]], [["It had been a bitter day, and a cutting north-east wind had blown for some time.", "매섭게 추운 날이었고, 살을 에는 북동풍이 한동안 불어 댔다."], ["The wind had gone down with the light, and so the snow had come on.", "해가 지면서 바람이 잦아들자, 눈이 내리기 시작했다."], ["It was a heavy, settled fall, I recollect, in great flakes; and it lay thick.", "큼직큼직한 송이로, 무겁게 차곡차곡 쌓이는 눈이었다고 기억한다. 눈은 두텁게 깔렸다."], ["The noise of wheels and tread of people were as hushed, as if the streets had been strewn that depth with feathers.", "바퀴 구르는 소리며 사람들 발걸음 소리가, 마치 거리에 그 두께만큼 깃털을 뿌려 놓기라도 한 듯 잦아들었다."]], [["My shortest way home,--and I naturally took the shortest way on such a night--was through St. Martin’s Lane.", "집으로 가는 가장 빠른 길은 — 그런 밤에 나는 당연히 가장 빠른 길을 택했다 — 세인트 마틴스 레인을 가로지르는 길이었다."], ["Now, the church which gives its name to the lane, stood in a less free situation at that time; there being no open space before it, and the lane winding down to the Strand.", "그런데 그 골목길에 이름을 준 교회는 그 무렵 지금보다 트인 곳에 서 있지 못했다. 앞에 빈터가 없었고, 골목은 스트랜드 거리로 굽이쳐 내려갔다."], ["As I passed the steps of the portico, I encountered, at the corner, a woman’s face.", "현관 기둥의 계단을 지나칠 때, 나는 모퉁이에서 어느 여인의 얼굴과 마주쳤다."], ["It looked in mine, passed across the narrow lane, and disappeared. I knew it.", "그 얼굴은 내 얼굴을 들여다보고, 좁은 골목을 가로질러, 사라졌다. 나는 그 얼굴을 알았다."], ["I had seen it somewhere.", "어디선가 본 적이 있었다."], ["But I could not remember where.", "하지만 어디서였는지 떠오르지 않았다."], ["I had some association with it, that struck upon my heart directly; but I was thinking of anything else when it came upon me, and was confused.", "그 얼굴에는 곧장 내 가슴을 친 어떤 연상이 얽혀 있었다. 그러나 그 얼굴이 불쑥 나타났을 때 나는 전혀 다른 생각에 잠겨 있던 터라, 정신이 어지러웠다."]], [["On the steps of the church, there was the stooping figure of a man, who had put down some burden on the smooth snow, to adjust it; my seeing the face, and my seeing him, were simultaneous.", "교회 계단에는 한 남자가 몸을 구부린 채 서 있었는데, 매끈한 눈 위에 무슨 짐을 내려놓고 그것을 고쳐 매던 참이었다. 내가 그 여인의 얼굴을 본 것과 그 남자를 본 것은 동시였다."], ["I don’t think I had stopped in my surprise; but, in any case, as I went on, he rose, turned, and came down towards me.", "놀란 나머지 발걸음을 멈추지는 않았던 것 같다. 하지만 어쨌든 내가 계속 걸어가는 사이에 그 남자가 몸을 일으켜 돌아서더니 내 쪽으로 내려왔다."], ["I stood face to face with Mr. Peggotty!", "나는 패거티 씨와 얼굴을 마주하고 섰다!"]], [["Then I remembered the woman.", "그제야 나는 그 여인을 떠올렸다. 마사였다."], ["It was Martha, to whom Emily had given the money that night in the kitchen. Martha Endell--side by side with whom, he would not have seen his dear niece, Ham had told me, for all the treasures wrecked in the sea.", "그날 밤 부엌에서 에밀리가 돈을 건넸던 바로 그 여인. 마사 엔델 — 햄이 나에게 말하기를, 바다에 가라앉은 온갖 보물을 다 준다 해도 패거티 씨는 사랑하는 조카가 그 여인과 나란히 있는 꼴을 보려 하지 않았을 거라던, 바로 그 여인이었다."]], [["We shook hands heartily.", "우리는 진심으로 악수를 나눴다."], ["At first, neither of us could speak a word.", "처음에는 둘 다 한마디도 꺼내지 못했다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy!’ he said, gripping me tight, ‘it do my art good to see you, sir.", "\"데이비 도련님!\" 그가 나를 꽉 붙잡으며 말했다. \"도련님을 뵈니 가슴이 다 따뜻해집니다요, 나리."], ["Well met, well met!’", "반갑습니다요, 반가워요!\""]], [["‘Well met, my dear old friend!’ said I.", "\"반가워요, 오래된 벗이여!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I had my thowts o’ coming to make inquiration for you, sir, tonight,’ he said, ‘but knowing as your aunt was living along wi’ you--fur I’ve been down yonder--Yarmouth way--I was afeerd it was too late.", "\"오늘 밤에 도련님 안부나 여쭤 볼까 하던 참이었습니다요, 나리.\" 그가 말했다. \"헌데 고모님이 도련님하고 같이 사신다는 걸 알고 보니 — 제가 그 아래쪽에 다녀왔거든요, 야머스 쪽에요 — 너무 늦은 시각이지 싶어 겁이 났습죠."], ["I should have come early in the morning, sir, afore going away.’", "떠나기 전에 아침 일찍 찾아뵐 작정이었습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["‘Again?’ said I.", "\"또 떠나신다고요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ he replied, patiently shaking his head, ‘I’m away tomorrow.’", "\"예, 나리.\" 그가 참을성 있게 고개를 가로저으며 대답했다. \"내일 떠납니다요.\""]], [["‘Where were you going now?’ I asked.", "\"이번에는 어디로 가시던 길이었나요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Well!’ he replied, shaking the snow out of his long hair, ‘I was a-going to turn in somewheers.’", "\"글쎄요!\" 그가 긴 머리카락에서 눈을 털어 내며 대답했다. \"어디든 묵을 데를 찾아 들어가던 참이었습죠.\""]], [["In those days there was a side-entrance to the stable-yard of the Golden Cross, the inn so memorable to me in connexion with his misfortune, nearly opposite to where we stood.", "그 시절에는 우리가 서 있던 곳 바로 맞은편에, 골든 크로스의 마구간 마당으로 통하는 옆문이 하나 있었다. 그의 불운과 얽혀 나에게 잊지 못할 그 여인숙 말이다."], ["I pointed out the gateway, put my arm through his, and we went across.", "나는 그 문간을 가리키고는, 그의 팔에 내 팔을 끼고 함께 길을 건넜다."], ["Two or three public-rooms opened out of the stable-yard; and looking into one of them, and finding it empty, and a good fire burning, I took him in there.", "마구간 마당 쪽으로는 두세 개의 손님방이 열려 있었다. 그중 한 방을 들여다보니 비어 있고 난롯불이 잘 타고 있어서, 나는 그를 그리로 데리고 들어갔다."]], [["When I saw him in the light, I observed, not only that his hair was long and ragged, but that his face was burnt dark by the sun.", "불빛 아래에서 그를 보니, 머리카락이 길고 부스스할 뿐 아니라, 얼굴이 햇볕에 거뭇하게 그을린 게 눈에 띄었다."], ["He was greyer, the lines in his face and forehead were deeper, and he had every appearance of having toiled and wandered through all varieties of weather; but he looked very strong, and like a man upheld by steadfastness of purpose, whom nothing could tire out.", "머리는 더 세었고, 얼굴과 이마의 주름은 더 깊어졌으며, 온갖 날씨를 헤치며 고생하고 떠돌아다닌 티가 역력했다. 하지만 그는 무척 강건해 보였고, 어떤 것에도 지치지 않는, 굳은 결심에 떠받쳐진 사람 같았다."], ["He shook the snow from his hat and clothes, and brushed it away from his face, while I was inwardly making these remarks.", "내가 속으로 이런 생각을 하는 동안, 그는 모자와 옷에서 눈을 털고, 얼굴에서도 눈을 쓸어 냈다."], ["As he sat down opposite to me at a table, with his back to the door by which we had entered, he put out his rough hand again, and grasped mine warmly.", "우리가 들어온 그 문을 등진 채 탁자를 사이에 두고 내 맞은편에 앉으면서, 그는 다시 거친 손을 내밀어 내 손을 따뜻하게 움켜잡았다."]], [["‘I’ll tell you, Mas’r Davy,’ he said,--‘wheer all I’ve been, and what-all we’ve heerd.", "\"말씀드리겠습니다요, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 다녀온 데를 죄다, 그리고 우리가 들은 얘기를 죄다요."], ["I’ve been fur, and we’ve heerd little; but I’ll tell you!’", "멀리도 다녔지만, 들은 건 별로 없습니다요. 그래도 다 말씀드리겠습니다요!\""]], [["I rang the bell for something hot to drink.", "나는 따뜻한 마실 것을 시키려고 종을 울렸다."], ["He would have nothing stronger than ale; and while it was being brought, and being warmed at the fire, he sat thinking.", "그는 에일보다 독한 것은 마다했다. 그것이 나오고 난롯불에 데워지는 동안, 그는 가만히 앉아 생각에 잠겼다."], ["There was a fine, massive gravity in his face, I did not venture to disturb.", "그의 얼굴에는 곱고도 묵직한 진중함이 어려 있어, 나는 감히 그 침묵을 깨뜨리지 못했다."]], [["‘When she was a child,’ he said, lifting up his head soon after we were left alone, ‘she used to talk to me a deal about the sea, and about them coasts where the sea got to be dark blue, and to lay a-shining and a-shining in the sun.", "\"그 애가 어릴 적에는요.\" 우리만 남게 된 지 얼마 안 되어 그가 고개를 들며 말했다. \"저한테 바다 얘기를 참 많이 했습니다요."], ["I thowt, odd times, as her father being drownded made her think on it so much.", "바다가 짙푸르게 변하고, 햇살 아래 반짝반짝 빛나는 그런 바닷가 얘기를요."], ["I doen’t know, you see, but maybe she believed--or hoped--he had drifted out to them parts, where the flowers is always a-blowing, and the country bright.’", "가끔은요, 그 애 아비가 물에 빠져 죽어서 그 애가 그걸 그렇게 골똘히 생각하나 싶었습죠. 모르긴 해도요, 어쩌면 그 애는 믿었던가 — 아니면 바랐던가 봐요 — 아비가 꽃이 늘 흐드러지게 피고 온 땅이 환한 그런 데로 떠밀려 갔다고요.\""]], [["‘It is likely to have been a childish fancy,’ I replied.", "\"어린애다운 공상이었겠지요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘When she was--lost,’ said Mr.", "\"그 애가 — 사라졌을 때요.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘I know’d in my mind, as he would take her to them countries.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"저는 속으로 알았습니다요, 그놈이 그 애를 그런 나라들로 데려가리란 걸요."], ["I know’d in my mind, as he’d have told her wonders of ‘em, and how she was to be a lady theer, and how he got her to listen to him fust, along o’ sech like.", "속으로 알았습죠, 그놈이 그 나라들의 신기한 얘기를 그 애한테 들려주었으리란 걸요. 거기서 그 애가 귀부인이 될 거라는 둥, 그런 식으로 그 애를 꾀어 처음에 제 말에 귀 기울이게 했으리란 걸요."], ["When we see his mother, I know’d quite well as I was right.", "그놈 어미를 만났을 때, 제가 옳았다는 걸 똑똑히 알았습니다요."], ["I went across-channel to France, and landed theer, as if I’d fell down from the sky.’", "저는 해협을 건너 프랑스로 가서, 하늘에서 뚝 떨어지기라도 한 양 그 땅에 내렸습죠.\""]], [["I saw the door move, and the snow drift in.", "문이 움직이고 눈발이 안으로 휘몰려 드는 것이 보였다."], ["I saw it move a little more, and a hand softly interpose to keep it open.", "문이 조금 더 움직이더니, 어떤 손 하나가 살그머니 끼어들어 문을 열어 두는 것이 보였다."]], [["‘I found out an English gen’leman as was in authority,’ said Mr.", "\"권세 있는 자리에 있는 영국 신사 한 분을 알아냈습죠.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘and told him I was a-going to seek my niece.", "\"그분께 제 조카를 찾으러 간다고 말씀드렸습니다요."], ["He got me them papers as I wanted fur to carry me through--I doen’t rightly know how they’re called--and he would have give me money, but that I was thankful to have no need on.", "그분이 제가 길을 다니는 데 필요한 서류들을 마련해 주셨습죠 — 그게 뭐라고 부르는 건지는 제대로 모릅니다만 — 돈도 주려 하셨지만, 다행히 그건 필요가 없어 고맙게 사양했습니다요. 그분이 베풀어 주신 모든 게 정말로 고맙습죠!"], ["I thank him kind, for all he done, I’m sure!", "'내가 자네보다 먼저 편지를 써 두겠네.'"], ["“I’ve wrote afore you,”", "그분이 저한테 말씀하셨습니다요."], ["he says to me, “and I shall speak to many as will come that way, and many will know you, fur distant from here, when you’re a-travelling alone.”", "'그리고 그쪽으로 가는 많은 이들한테 일러둘 테니, 자네가 혼자 멀리 떠돌더라도 여기서 한참 떨어진 곳에서도 많은 사람이 자네를 알아볼 걸세.'"], ["I told him, best as I was able, what my gratitoode was, and went away through France.’", "저는 할 수 있는 한 제 고마움을 다 말씀드리고, 프랑스를 가로질러 떠났습니다요.\""]], [["‘Alone, and on foot?’ said I.", "\"혼자서, 걸어서요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Mostly a-foot,’ he rejoined; ‘sometimes in carts along with people going to market; sometimes in empty coaches.", "\"거의 걸어서요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이따금은 장에 가는 사람들 짐수레에 얹혀서요."], ["Many mile a day a-foot, and often with some poor soldier or another, travelling to see his friends.", "가끔은 빈 마차를 얻어 타고요. 하루에도 몇 마일씩 걷는데, 흔히는 친구들을 만나러 가는 어느 가엾은 병사 하나하고 같이였습죠."], ["I couldn’t talk to him,’ said Mr.", "저는 그 사람한테 말을 붙일 수도 없었고요.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘nor he to me; but we was company for one another, too, along the dusty roads.’", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그 사람도 저한테 그랬습죠. 하지만 그래도 우린 그 흙먼지 길에서 서로한테 길동무가 돼 주었습니다요.\""]], [["I should have known that by his friendly tone.", "그의 다정한 말투만으로도 나는 그랬으리란 걸 알 수 있었다."]], [["‘When I come to any town,’ he pursued, ‘I found the inn, and waited about the yard till someone turned up (someone mostly did) as know’d English.", "\"어느 마을이든 닿으면요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"여인숙을 찾아서, 영어를 아는 누군가가 나타날 때까지(거의 누군가는 나타났습죠) 마당에서 기다렸습니다요."], ["Then I told how that I was on my way to seek my niece, and they told me what manner of gentlefolks was in the house, and I waited to see any as seemed like her, going in or out.", "그러면 제가 조카를 찾으러 가는 길이라고 얘기했고, 그러면 그 사람들이 그 집에 어떤 양반네가 묵고 있는지 일러 주었습죠. 저는 그 애 비슷한 사람이 드나드는지 보려고 기다렸습니다요."], ["When it warn’t Em’ly, I went on agen.", "에밀리가 아니면 저는 다시 길을 떠났습죠."], ["By little and little, when I come to a new village or that, among the poor people, I found they know’d about me.", "차츰차츰, 새 마을이나 그런 데에 닿으면, 가난한 사람들 사이에서 그 사람들이 제 얘기를 안다는 걸 알게 됐습니다요."], ["They would set me down at their cottage doors, and give me what-not fur to eat and drink, and show me where to sleep; and many a woman, Mas’r Davy, as has had a daughter of about Em’ly’s age, I’ve found a-waiting fur me, at Our Saviour’s Cross outside the village, fur to do me sim’lar kindnesses.", "그 사람들은 저를 자기네 오두막 문간에 앉히고, 먹고 마실 것을 이것저것 내주고, 잘 데를 일러 주었습죠. 그리고 데이비 도련님, 에밀리 또래 딸을 둔 여인네들 중에는요, 마을 어귀 구세주 십자가 곁에서 저를 기다리고 있다가 저한테 그런 친절을 베풀어 준 이가 한둘이 아니었습니다요."], ["Some has had daughters as was dead.", "더러는 그 딸이 죽고 없는 이도 있었습죠."], ["And God only knows how good them mothers was to me!’", "그 어미들이 저한테 얼마나 잘해 줬는지는 하느님만 아실 겁니다요!\""]], [["It was Martha at the door.", "문간에 선 것은 마사였다."], ["I saw her haggard, listening face distinctly.", "나는 그 초췌하게 귀 기울이는 얼굴을 또렷이 보았다."], ["My dread was lest he should turn his head, and see her too.", "내가 두려웠던 것은, 그가 고개를 돌려 그 여인까지 보게 되는 일이었다."]], [["‘They would often put their children--particular their little girls,’ said Mr.", "\"그 사람들은 자기네 아이들을 — 특히 어린 딸아이들을요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘upon my knee; and many a time you might have seen me sitting at their doors, when night was coming in, a’most as if they’d been my Darling’s children.", "\"곧잘 제 무릎에 앉혔습니다요. 밤이 내려앉을 무렵 그 집 문간에 앉아 있는 절 도련님도 여러 번 보셨을지도 모릅죠. 꼭 그 아이들이 제 사랑하는 그 애의 자식들이기라도 한 것처럼요."], ["Oh, my Darling!’", "오, 내 사랑하는 그 애!\""]], [["Overpowered by sudden grief, he sobbed aloud.", "갑작스러운 슬픔에 짓눌려 그는 큰 소리로 흐느꼈다."], ["I laid my trembling hand upon the hand he put before his face.", "나는 그가 얼굴 앞에 가져다 댄 그 손 위에 떨리는 내 손을 얹었다."], ["‘Thankee, sir,’ he said, ‘doen’t take no notice.’", "\"고맙습니다요, 나리.\" 그가 말했다. \"신경 쓰지 마십쇼.\""]], [["In a very little while he took his hand away and put it on his breast, and went on with his story.", "이윽고 그는 손을 떼어 가슴에 갖다 대더니, 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다. \"그 사람들은 곧잘 저랑 같이 걸어 주었습니다요.\""], ["‘They often walked with me,’ he said, ‘in the morning, maybe a mile or two upon my road; and when we parted, and I said, “I’m very thankful to you!", "그가 말했다. \"아침이면, 한두 마일쯤 제 길을 같이 가 주었습죠. 그리고 헤어질 때 제가 '정말로 고맙습니다!"], ["God bless you!” they always seemed to understand, and answered pleasant.", "하느님의 축복이 있기를!' 하면, 그 사람들은 늘 알아듣는 듯이 정답게 대꾸해 주었습니다요."], ["At last I come to the sea.", "마침내 저는 바다에 닿았습죠."], ["It warn’t hard, you may suppose, for a seafaring man like me to work his way over to Italy.", "저처럼 뱃일하던 사람한테야, 이탈리아까지 일을 거들며 건너가는 게 어렵지 않았다는 건 짐작하실 겝니다요."], ["When I got theer, I wandered on as I had done afore.", "거기 닿아서는 전에 하던 대로 떠돌았습죠."], ["The people was just as good to me, and I should have gone from town to town, maybe the country through, but that I got news of her being seen among them Swiss mountains yonder.", "사람들은 한결같이 저한테 잘해 줬고요. 저는 이 마을 저 마을, 어쩌면 그 나라를 통째로 누비고 다녔을 겝니다요. 헌데 그 애가 저 스위스 산속에서 목격됐다는 소식을 들었습죠."], ["One as know’d his servant see ‘em there, all three, and told me how they travelled, and where they was.", "그놈 하인을 아는 누군가가 거기서 그 셋을 다 봤다면서, 그들이 어떻게 다니는지, 어디 있는지를 일러 주었습니다요."], ["I made fur them mountains, Mas’r Davy, day and night.", "저는 밤낮으로 그 산들을 향해 갔습죠, 데이비 도련님."], ["Ever so fur as I went, ever so fur the mountains seemed to shift away from me.", "제가 가면 갈수록, 그 산들은 자꾸만 저한테서 멀어지는 것 같았습니다요."], ["But I come up with ‘em, and I crossed ‘em.", "그래도 저는 따라잡았고, 그 산들을 넘었습죠."], ["When I got nigh the place as I had been told of, I began to think within my own self, “What shall I do when I see her?”’", "듣고 온 그 곳 가까이에 이르자, 저는 속으로 이런 생각이 들기 시작했습니다요. '그 애를 보면 난 어떡하지?'\""]], [["The listening face, insensible to the inclement night, still drooped at the door, and the hands begged me--prayed me--not to cast it forth.", "귀 기울이는 그 얼굴은 매서운 밤에도 아랑곳없이 여전히 문간에 드리워 있었고, 두 손은 나에게 그 얼굴을 내쫓지 말아 달라고 빌고 — 또 빌고 — 있었다."]], [["‘I never doubted her,’ said Mr.", "\"저는 한 번도 그 애를 의심하지 않았습니다요.\""], ["Peggotty. ‘No! Not a bit!", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"아뇨!"], ["On’y let her see my face--on’y let her heer my voice--on’y let my stanning still afore her bring to her thoughts the home she had fled away from, and the child she had been--and if she had growed to be a royal lady, she’d have fell down at my feet!", "눈곱만큼도요! 그저 그 애가 제 얼굴을 보기만 한다면 — 그저 그 애가 제 목소리를 듣기만 한다면 — 그저 제가 그 애 앞에 가만히 서 있는 것만으로 그 애가 도망쳐 나온 집과 자기가 한때 어떤 아이였는지를 떠올리기만 한다면요 — 설령 그 애가 왕녀가 다 됐다 한들, 제 발 앞에 엎어졌을 겝니다요!"], ["I know’d it well!", "저는 그걸 잘 알았습죠!"], ["Many a time in my sleep had I heerd her cry out, “Uncle!” and seen her fall like death afore me.", "꿈속에서 저는 그 애가 '삼촌!' 하고 외치는 소리를 여러 번 들었고, 그 애가 제 앞에서 죽은 듯이 쓰러지는 걸 봤습니다요."], ["Many a time in my sleep had I raised her up, and whispered to her, “Em’ly, my dear, I am come fur to bring forgiveness, and to take you home!”’", "꿈속에서 저는 여러 번 그 애를 일으켜 세우고, '에밀리야, 얘야, 내가 용서를 가지고 왔단다, 너를 집으로 데려가려고 왔어!' 하고 속삭였습죠.\""]], [["He stopped and shook his head, and went on with a sigh.", "그는 말을 멈추고 고개를 가로젓더니, 한숨을 쉬며 말을 이었다."]], [["‘He was nowt to me now. Em’ly was all.", "\"이제 그놈은 저한테 아무것도 아니었습니다요."], ["I bought a country dress to put upon her; and I know’d that, once found, she would walk beside me over them stony roads, go where I would, and never, never, leave me more.", "에밀리가 전부였습죠. 저는 그 애한테 입힐 시골 옷을 한 벌 샀습니다요. 그리고 알았습죠, 일단 찾기만 하면 그 애는 제가 어디로 가든 그 돌투성이 길을 제 곁에서 함께 걸을 거고, 두 번 다시는, 두 번 다시는 저를 떠나지 않으리란 걸요."], ["To put that dress upon her, and to cast off what she wore--to take her on my arm again, and wander towards home--to stop sometimes upon the road, and heal her bruised feet and her worse-bruised heart--was all that I thowt of now.", "그 애한테 그 옷을 입히고, 그 애가 걸친 것을 벗겨 던지고 — 그 애를 다시 제 팔에 끼고 집을 향해 걷고 — 이따금 길에서 멈춰서, 그 애의 멍든 발과, 그보다 더 멍든 마음을 어루만져 낫게 하고 — 이제 제가 생각하는 건 그게 다였습니다요."], ["I doen’t believe I should have done so much as look at him. But, Mas’r Davy, it warn’t to be--not yet!", "그놈은 쳐다보지도 않았을 겝니다요. 헌데, 데이비 도련님, 그게 이뤄질 일이 아니었습죠 — 아직은요!"], ["I was too late, and they was gone.", "제가 너무 늦었고, 그들은 떠나 버렸습니다요."], ["Wheer, I couldn’t learn.", "어디로 갔는지는 알 수가 없었습죠."], ["Some said heer, some said theer.", "누구는 여기라 하고, 누구는 저기라 했습니다요."], ["I travelled heer, and I travelled theer, but I found no Em’ly, and I travelled home.’", "저는 여기로도 가 보고, 저기로도 가 봤지만, 에밀리를 찾지 못한 채 집으로 돌아왔습죠.\""]], [["‘How long ago?’ I asked.", "\"얼마 전이었나요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘A matter o’ fower days,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"한 나흘쯤 됐습니다요.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I sighted the old boat arter dark, and the light a-shining in the winder.", "\"날이 어두워진 뒤에 그 낡은 배가 눈에 들어왔고, 창에 불빛이 비치고 있었습죠."], ["When I come nigh and looked in through the glass, I see the faithful creetur Missis Gummidge sittin’ by the fire, as we had fixed upon, alone.", "가까이 가서 유리창 너머로 들여다보니, 그 충직한 거미지 아주머니가 우리가 정해 둔 대로 난롯가에 혼자 앉아 있는 게 보였습니다요."], ["I called out, “Doen’t be afeerd!", "제가 '겁먹지 마세요! 댄이에요!'"], ["It’s Dan’l!” and I went in.", "하고 외치고는 안으로 들어갔습죠."], ["I never could have thowt the old boat would have been so strange!’ From some pocket in his breast, he took out, with a very careful hand a small paper bundle containing two or three letters or little packets, which he laid upon the table.", "그 낡은 배가 그토록 낯설게 느껴질 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐습니다요!\" 그는 가슴팍 어느 주머니에서 아주 조심스러운 손길로 작은 종이 꾸러미 하나를 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 편지인지 작은 봉지인지가 두세 개 들어 있었다. 그는 그것을 탁자 위에 올려놓았다."]], [["‘This fust one come,’ he said, selecting it from the rest, ‘afore I had been gone a week.", "\"이 첫 번째 게 왔습니다요.\" 그가 그중 하나를 골라내며 말했다. \"제가 떠난 지 일주일도 안 돼서요."], ["A fifty pound Bank note, in a sheet of paper, directed to me, and put underneath the door in the night.", "종이 한 장에 싼 오십 파운드짜리 은행권인데, 제 앞으로 부쳐진 채 밤중에 문 밑으로 밀어 넣어져 있었습죠."], ["She tried to hide her writing, but she couldn’t hide it from Me!’", "그 애가 글씨를 감추려고 했지만, 저한테는 못 감췄습니다요!\""]], [["He folded up the note again, with great patience and care, in exactly the same form, and laid it on one side.", "그는 그 지폐를 더없이 참을성 있고 조심스럽게, 처음과 똑같은 모양으로 다시 접어, 한쪽에 내려놓았다."]], [["‘This come to Missis Gummidge,’ he said, opening another, ‘two or three months ago.’ After looking at it for some moments, he gave it to me, and added in a low voice, ‘Be so good as read it, sir.’", "\"이건 거미지 아주머니한테 왔습니다요.\" 그가 또 하나를 펼치며 말했다. \"두세 달 전쯤이었습죠.\" 잠시 그것을 들여다보더니, 그는 그것을 나에게 건네며 나직한 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"수고스러우시겠지만 읽어 주십쇼, 나리.\""]], [["I read as follows:", "나는 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["‘Oh what will you feel when you see this writing, and know it comes from my wicked hand!", "\"오, 이 글씨를 보고, 이게 제 못된 손에서 나왔다는 걸 알면 아주머니는 어떤 심정이실까요!"], ["But try, try--not for my sake, but for uncle’s goodness, try to let your heart soften to me, only for a little little time!", "하지만 부디, 부디 — 저를 봐서가 아니라 삼촌의 어지심을 봐서, 아주 짧디짧은 동안만이라도 저한테 마음을 누그러뜨려 주세요!"], ["Try, pray do, to relent towards a miserable girl, and write down on a bit of paper whether he is well, and what he said about me before you left off ever naming me among yourselves--and whether, of a night, when it is my old time of coming home, you ever see him look as if he thought of one he used to love so dear.", "부디, 제발, 이 비참한 계집애한테 마음을 풀어 주시고, 종잇조각에 적어 주세요. 삼촌이 안녕하신지, 그리고 아주머니들끼리 제 이름을 입에 올리기를 그만두기 전에 삼촌이 저에 대해 뭐라고 하셨는지를요. 그리고 밤에, 제가 옛날에 집으로 돌아오던 그 시각이면, 삼촌이 한때 그토록 애틋하게 사랑하던 사람을 떠올리는 듯한 표정을 지으시는 걸 본 적이 있는지를요."], ["Oh, my heart is breaking when I think about it!", "오, 그걸 생각하면 제 가슴이 미어져요!"], ["I am kneeling down to you, begging and praying you not to be as hard with me as I deserve--as I well, well, know I deserve--but to be so gentle and so good, as to write down something of him, and to send it to me.", "저는 아주머니께 무릎 꿇고, 제가 받아 마땅한 만큼 — 정말로, 정말로 받아 마땅하다는 걸 잘 알아요 — 그만큼 저한테 모질게 굴지는 말아 주십사 빌고 또 빌어요. 부디 그토록 다정하고 그토록 어지셔서, 삼촌에 대해 뭐든 좀 적어 보내 주세요. 저를 '꼬마'라고 부르지 않으셔도 돼요."], ["You need not call me Little, you need not call me by the name I have disgraced; but oh, listen to my agony, and have mercy on me so far as to write me some word of uncle, never, never to be seen in this world by my eyes again!", "제가 더럽힌 그 이름으로 부르지 않으셔도 돼요. 하지만 오, 제 고통에 귀 기울이시고, 삼촌에 대해 한마디라도 적어 주실 만큼만이라도 저한테 자비를 베풀어 주세요. 이 세상에서는 제 눈으로 두 번 다시, 두 번 다시 뵐 수 없는 그 삼촌에 대해서요!"]], [["‘Dear, if your heart is hard towards me--justly hard, I know--but, listen, if it is hard, dear, ask him I have wronged the most--him whose wife I was to have been--before you quite decide against my poor poor prayer!", "사랑하는 아주머니, 만약 아주머니 마음이 저한테 모질다면 — 마땅히 모질겠지요, 알아요 — 하지만, 들어 주세요, 만약 마음이 모질다면, 아주머니, 제가 가장 크게 잘못한 그 사람한테 — 제가 그 아내가 될 뻔했던 그 사람한테 물어봐 주세요."], ["If he should be so compassionate as to say that you might write something for me to read--I think he would, oh, I think he would, if you would only ask him, for he always was so brave and so forgiving--tell him then (but not else), that when I hear the wind blowing at night, I feel as if it was passing angrily from seeing him and uncle, and was going up to God against me.", "제 가엾고 가엾은 이 청을 아주 매정하게 물리쳐 버리기 전에요! 만약 그가 너무도 인정이 많아, 제가 읽을 수 있게 뭔가 적어 줘도 좋다고 한다면 — 그럴 거예요, 오, 그럴 거예요, 아주머니가 그저 물어봐 주기만 한다면요, 그는 늘 그토록 용감하고 그토록 잘 용서하는 사람이었으니까요 — 그러면 그에게 전해 주세요(다만 그가 그러마 할 때만요). 밤에 바람이 부는 소리를 들으면, 그게 마치 그와 삼촌을 보고 성이 나서 휙 스쳐 가는 것 같고, 저를 고발하러 하느님께 올라가는 것 같다고요."], ["Tell him that if I was to die tomorrow (and oh, if I was fit, I would be so glad to die!) I would bless him and uncle with my last words, and pray for his happy home with my last breath!’", "그에게 전해 주세요. 만약 제가 내일 죽는다면(오, 제가 죽을 자격이 있다면, 저는 기꺼이 죽을 텐데요!) 마지막 말로 그와 삼촌을 축복하고, 마지막 숨으로 그의 행복한 가정을 위해 기도하겠다고요!\""]], [["Some money was enclosed in this letter also. Five pounds.", "이 편지에도 돈이 동봉되어 있었다. 오 파운드였다."], ["It was untouched like the previous sum, and he refolded it in the same way. Detailed instructions were added relative to the address of a reply, which, although they betrayed the intervention of several hands, and made it difficult to arrive at any very probable conclusion in reference to her place of concealment, made it at least not unlikely that she had written from that spot where she was stated to have been seen.", "그것 역시 앞서의 돈처럼 손도 안 댄 채였고, 그는 그것을 같은 모양으로 다시 접었다. 답장 주소에 관한 상세한 지시도 덧붙어 있었는데, 그 지시는 여러 사람의 손을 거쳤음을 드러내, 그 애가 숨어 있는 곳에 관해 어떤 그럴듯한 결론에 이르기는 어렵게 만들었지만, 적어도 그 애가 목격됐다고 전해진 바로 그곳에서 편지를 썼을 가능성이 적지 않음을 보여 주었다."]], [["‘What answer was sent?’ I inquired of Mr. Peggotty.", "\"답장은 어떻게 보냈나요?\" 나는 패거티 씨에게 물었다."]], [["‘Missis Gummidge,’ he returned, ‘not being a good scholar, sir, Ham kindly drawed it out, and she made a copy on it.", "\"거미지 아주머니가요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"글재주가 변변치 못한지라, 나리, 햄이 친절하게 초안을 잡아 주고, 아주머니가 그걸 베껴 썼습죠."], ["They told her I was gone to seek her, and what my parting words was.’", "제가 그 애를 찾으러 떠났다는 것과, 제 작별 인사가 뭐였는지를 그 애한테 일러 주었습니다요.\""]], [["‘Is that another letter in your hand?’ said I.", "\"손에 든 그것도 또 편지인가요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s money, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"돈입니다요, 나리.\""], ["Peggotty, unfolding it a little way.", "패거티 씨가 그것을 조금 펼쳐 보이며 말했다."], ["‘Ten pound, you see.", "\"보십쇼, 십 파운드입니다요."], ["And wrote inside, “From a true friend,” like the fust.", "그리고 안쪽에 '한 진정한 벗으로부터'라고 적혀 있습죠, 첫 번째 것처럼요."], ["But the fust was put underneath the door, and this come by the post, day afore yesterday. I’m a-going to seek her at the post-mark.’", "하지만 첫 번째 건 문 밑으로 넣어졌고, 이건 우편으로 왔습니다요, 그제요. 저는 이 소인을 찍은 데로 그 애를 찾으러 갈 작정입니다요.\""]], [["He showed it to me. It was a town on the Upper Rhine.", "그가 그것을 나에게 보여 주었다. 어퍼 라인의 한 도시였다."], ["He had found out, at Yarmouth, some foreign dealers who knew that country, and they had drawn him a rude map on paper, which he could very well understand.", "그는 야머스에서, 그 지방을 아는 외국인 상인 몇몇을 알아냈고, 그들이 종이에 그를 위해 투박한 지도를 그려 주었는데, 그는 그것을 아주 잘 이해할 수 있었다."], ["He laid it between us on the table; and, with his chin resting on one hand, tracked his course upon it with the other.", "그는 그것을 우리 사이의 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓고는, 한 손으로 턱을 괴고 다른 손으로 자기가 갈 길을 짚어 갔다."]], [["I asked him how Ham was?", "나는 햄이 어떻게 지내는지 물었다."], ["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘He works,’ he said, ‘as bold as a man can.", "\"그놈은 일합니다요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["His name’s as good, in all that part, as any man’s is, anywheres in the wureld.", "\"사내대장부가 할 수 있는 만큼 씩씩하게요. 그 일대에서 그놈 이름은 세상 어디 누구 이름만큼이나 좋습죠."], ["Anyone’s hand is ready to help him, you understand, and his is ready to help them.", "누구든 그놈을 거들려고 선뜻 손을 내밀고, 그놈도 그들을 거들려고 선뜻 손을 내밉니다요, 아시겠습니까."], ["He’s never been heerd fur to complain.", "그놈이 불평하는 건 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없습죠."], ["But my sister’s belief is (‘twixt ourselves) as it has cut him deep.’", "하지만 제 누이 생각으로는(우리끼리 얘깁니다만), 그게 그놈을 속 깊이 후벼 팠다는 겁니다요.\""]], [["‘Poor fellow, I can believe it!’", "\"가엾은 친구로군요, 그럴 만도 합니다!\""]], [["‘He ain’t no care, Mas’r Davy,’ said Mr.", "\"그놈은 통 자기 앞가림을 안 합니다요, 데이비 도련님.\""], ["Peggotty in a solemn whisper--‘kinder no care no-how for his life.", "패거티 씨가 엄숙하게 속삭이며 말했다. \"어쩐지 제 목숨 따위는 도무지 아랑곳을 안 해요."], ["When a man’s wanted for rough sarvice in rough weather, he’s theer.", "험한 날씨에 험한 일에 누가 필요하다 하면, 그놈이 거기 있습죠."], ["When there’s hard duty to be done with danger in it, he steps for’ard afore all his mates.", "위험이 따르는 고된 임무가 있으면, 그놈이 동료들을 다 제치고 앞장섭니다요."], ["And yet he’s as gentle as any child.", "그러면서도 그놈은 어린애처럼 순합죠."], ["There ain’t a child in Yarmouth that doen’t know him.’", "야머스에서 그놈을 모르는 아이가 하나도 없습니다요.\""]], [["He gathered up the letters thoughtfully, smoothing them with his hand; put them into their little bundle; and placed it tenderly in his breast again.", "그는 생각에 잠긴 채 편지들을 그러모아, 손으로 매만져 폈다. 그것들을 작은 꾸러미로 묶어, 다시 가슴에 고이 간직했다. 문간에서 그 얼굴은 사라지고 없었다."], ["The face was gone from the door. I still saw the snow drifting in; but nothing else was there.", "나는 여전히 눈발이 안으로 휘몰려 드는 것은 보았으나, 그 밖에는 거기 아무것도 없었다."]], [["‘Well!’ he said, looking to his bag, ‘having seen you tonight, Mas’r Davy (and that doos me good!), I shall away betimes tomorrow morning.", "\"자!\" 그가 자기 짐 보따리를 바라보며 말했다. \"오늘 밤 도련님을 뵈었으니(그게 저한테 큰 위안이 됩니다요!), 저는 내일 아침 일찌감치 떠나겠습니다요."], ["You have seen what I’ve got heer’; putting his hand on where the little packet lay; ‘all that troubles me is, to think that any harm might come to me, afore that money was give back.", "제가 여기 가진 게 뭔지 도련님도 보셨습죠.\" 그는 그 작은 꾸러미가 놓인 가슴팍에 손을 얹었다. \"저를 괴롭히는 건 단 하나, 저 돈을 돌려주기도 전에 저한테 무슨 일이 닥칠까 하는 생각뿐입니다요."], ["If I was to die, and it was lost, or stole, or elseways made away with, and it was never know’d by him but what I’d took it, I believe the t’other wureld wouldn’t hold me!", "만약 제가 죽고, 저게 없어지거나, 도둑맞거나, 다른 식으로 사라져 버려서, 제가 그걸 가로챘다고밖에는 그놈이 알 길이 없게 된다면, 저는 저세상에서도 가만히 있지 못할 겝니다요!"], ["I believe I must come back!’", "분명 다시 돌아오고 말 겝니다요!\""]], [["He rose, and I rose too; we grasped each other by the hand again, before going out.", "그가 일어섰고, 나도 일어섰다. 우리는 밖으로 나가기 전에 다시 한번 서로 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["‘I’d go ten thousand mile,’ he said, ‘I’d go till I dropped dead, to lay that money down afore him.", "\"일만 마일이라도 가겠습니다요.\" 그가 말했다. \"쓰러져 죽을 때까지라도 가서, 저 돈을 그놈 앞에 내려놓겠습니다요."], ["If I do that, and find my Em’ly, I’m content.", "그러고 나서 제 에밀리를 찾는다면, 저는 만족합죠."], ["If I doen’t find her, maybe she’ll come to hear, sometime, as her loving uncle only ended his search for her when he ended his life; and if I know her, even that will turn her home at last!’", "만약 못 찾더라도, 어쩌면 그 애가 언젠가는 듣게 되겠지요. 그 애를 사랑하는 삼촌이 제 목숨을 다하는 그 순간에야 그 애 찾는 일을 그만뒀다는 걸요. 그리고 제가 그 애를 안다면, 바로 그것이 끝내 그 애를 집으로 돌아오게 만들 겝니다요!\""]], [["As he went out into the rigorous night, I saw the lonely figure flit away before us.", "그가 매서운 밤 속으로 나서자, 나는 그 외로운 형체가 우리 앞으로 휙 스쳐 가는 것을 보았다."], ["I turned him hastily on some pretence, and held him in conversation until it was gone.", "나는 무슨 핑계를 대어 황급히 그를 돌려세우고는, 그 형체가 사라질 때까지 그를 붙들고 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["He spoke of a traveller’s house on the Dover Road, where he knew he could find a clean, plain lodging for the night.", "그는 도버 가도에 있는, 그날 밤 깨끗하고 수수한 잠자리를 분명히 구할 수 있다는 어느 나그네 숙소 이야기를 했다."], ["I went with him over Westminster Bridge, and parted from him on the Surrey shore.", "나는 그와 함께 웨스트민스터 다리를 건너, 서리 강가에서 그와 헤어졌다."], ["Everything seemed, to my imagination, to be hushed in reverence for him, as he resumed his solitary journey through the snow.", "그가 눈을 헤치며 외로운 여정을 다시 시작하는 모습에, 내 머릿속에는 온 세상이 그에게 경의를 표하느라 숨죽이는 것만 같았다."]], [["I returned to the inn yard, and, impressed by my remembrance of the face, looked awfully around for it.", "나는 여인숙 마당으로 돌아와, 그 얼굴의 기억에 사로잡힌 채 두려운 마음으로 사방을 둘러보며 그것을 찾았다."], ["It was not there.", "거기에 없었다."], ["The snow had covered our late footprints; my new track was the only one to be seen; and even that began to die away (it snowed so fast) as I looked back over my shoulder.", "눈이 우리가 조금 전 남긴 발자국을 덮어 버려, 새로 찍힌 내 발자국만이 보였다. 그리고 어깨 너머로 돌아보는 사이에, 그것마저도 (눈이 어찌나 세차게 내렸던지) 차츰 지워지기 시작했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 41. DORA’S AUNTS", "# CHAPTER 41. DORA’S AUNTS"]], [["At last, an answer came from the two old ladies. They presented their compliments to Mr.", "마침내 두 노부인에게서 답장이 왔다."], ["Copperfield, and informed him that they had given his letter their best consideration, ‘with a view to the happiness of both parties’--which I thought rather an alarming expression, not only because of the use they had made of it in relation to the family difference before-mentioned, but because I had (and have all my life) observed that conventional phrases are a sort of fireworks, easily let off, and liable to take a great variety of shapes and colours not at all suggested by their original form.", "두 분은 코퍼필드 씨에게 정중한 인사를 전하면서, 그의 편지를 '양쪽 모두의 행복을 위하여' 가장 신중하게 검토했다고 알려 왔다. 이 표현이 나는 어쩐지 섬뜩하게 느껴졌는데, 앞서 말한 집안 불화와 관련해서 두 분이 이 말을 써먹은 적이 있기 때문이기도 하지만, 내가 (그리고 평생토록) 지켜본 바로는 이런 상투적인 문구란 일종의 불꽃놀이 같아서, 쉽게 쏘아 올릴 수 있고, 본래의 모양에서는 전혀 짐작할 수 없는 온갖 형태와 빛깔로 변하기 마련이기 때문이다."], ["The Misses Spenlow added that they begged to forbear expressing, ‘through the medium of correspondence’, an opinion on the subject of Mr.", "스펜로 자매분은 덧붙이기를, 코퍼필드 씨의 편지에 담긴 사안에 대해 '편지라는 수단을 통해서는'"], ["Copperfield’s communication; but that if Mr. Copperfield would do them the favour to call, upon a certain day (accompanied, if he thought proper, by a confidential friend), they would be happy to hold some conversation on the subject.", "의견을 밝히기를 삼가고 싶으나, 코퍼필드 씨가 어느 정해진 날에 (그가 적당하다고 여긴다면 믿을 만한 벗을 한 사람 데리고) 방문하는 호의를 베풀어 준다면, 그 사안을 두고 기꺼이 이야기를 나누겠다고 했다."]], [["To this favour, Mr. Copperfield immediately replied, with his respectful compliments, that he would have the honour of waiting on the Misses Spenlow, at the time appointed; accompanied, in accordance with their kind permission, by his friend Mr. Thomas Traddles of the Inner Temple.", "이 호의에 코퍼필드 씨는 곧장 정중한 인사와 함께 답장을 보내어, 지정된 시각에 스펜로 자매분을 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠으며, 두 분의 친절한 허락에 따라 이너 템플의 토머스 트래들스 씨를 벗으로 데리고 가겠노라고 했다."], ["Having dispatched which missive, Mr. Copperfield fell into a condition of strong nervous agitation; and so remained until the day arrived.", "이 서신을 보내고 나서 코퍼필드 씨는 극심한 신경과민과 불안 상태에 빠졌고, 약속한 날이 올 때까지 그대로였다."]], [["It was a great augmentation of my uneasiness to be bereaved, at this eventful crisis, of the inestimable services of Miss Mills.", "이 중대한 고비에서, 밀스 양의 그 값을 매길 수 없는 도움을 빼앗긴 것은 내 불안을 한층 더 키웠다."], ["But Mr. Mills, who was always doing something or other to annoy me--or I felt as if he were, which was the same thing--had brought his conduct to a climax, by taking it into his head that he would go to India.", "그런데 늘 이런저런 일로 나를 성가시게 굴던 밀스 씨가 — 아니, 적어도 나는 그가 그렇게 군다고 느꼈는데, 그게 그거였다 — 인도로 가겠다는 생각을 머리에 박아 넣음으로써 그 행태를 절정에 이르게 했다."], ["Why should he go to India, except to harass me?", "나를 괴롭히려는 것이 아니라면 도대체 왜 인도로 간단 말인가?"], ["To be sure he had nothing to do with any other part of the world, and had a good deal to do with that part; being entirely in the India trade, whatever that was (I had floating dreams myself concerning golden shawls and elephants’ teeth); having been at Calcutta in his youth; and designing now to go out there again, in the capacity of resident partner. But this was nothing to me.", "하기야 그가 세상의 다른 지역과는 아무런 인연이 없고 오직 그 지역과만 큰 인연이 있긴 했다. 그게 무엇이든 간에 (나 스스로도 황금빛 숄과 코끼리 어금니에 관한 어렴풋한 공상을 품곤 했다) 그는 온전히 인도 무역에 종사하고 있었으며, 젊은 시절 캘커타에 가 본 적이 있고, 이제 다시 그곳으로 나가 주재 동업자 자격으로 일할 작정이었다."], ["However, it was so much to him that for India he was bound, and Julia with him; and Julia went into the country to take leave of her relations; and the house was put into a perfect suit of bills, announcing that it was to be let or sold, and that the furniture (Mangle and all) was to be taken at a valuation.", "하지만 그것은 나와는 아무 상관이 없었다. 그러나 그에게는 어찌나 큰일이었던지 인도로 떠날 결심을 굳혔고, 줄리아도 함께 떠나기로 했다. 그래서 줄리아는 친척들에게 작별 인사를 하러 시골로 내려갔고, 집은 세를 놓거나 판다는, 그리고 가구는 (탈수기까지 죄다) 감정가에 넘긴다는 광고로 온통 도배되었다."], ["So, here was another earthquake of which I became the sport, before I had recovered from the shock of its predecessor!", "그러니 여기 또 하나의 지진이 닥쳐, 앞선 충격에서 미처 회복하기도 전에 나는 그 노리개 신세가 되고 만 것이다!"]], [["I was in several minds how to dress myself on the important day; being divided between my desire to appear to advantage, and my apprehensions of putting on anything that might impair my severely practical character in the eyes of the Misses Spenlow.", "그 중요한 날 어떻게 차려입어야 할지 나는 마음을 여러 갈래로 정하지 못했다. 한껏 돋보이고 싶은 욕심과, 스펜로 자매분의 눈에 내 엄정하고 실속 있는 됨됨이를 깎아내릴 만한 무언가를 걸치지나 않을까 하는 걱정 사이에서 갈팡질팡했던 것이다."], ["I endeavoured to hit a happy medium between these two extremes; my aunt approved the result; and Mr. Dick threw one of his shoes after Traddles and me, for luck, as we went downstairs.", "나는 이 두 극단 사이에서 적절한 중간을 맞춰 보려고 애썼다. 고모님은 그 결과를 마음에 들어 했고, 딕 씨는 트래들스와 내가 아래층으로 내려갈 때 행운을 빈다며 자기 신발 한 짝을 우리 뒤로 던졌다."]], [["Excellent fellow as I knew Traddles to be, and warmly attached to him as I was, I could not help wishing, on that delicate occasion, that he had never contracted the habit of brushing his hair so very upright.", "트래들스가 더없이 훌륭한 친구임을 잘 알고, 그에게 따뜻한 애정을 품고 있으면서도, 그 미묘한 자리에서만큼은 그가 머리카락을 그토록 빳빳하게 세워 빗어 올리는 버릇을 결코 들이지 않았더라면 좋았겠다는 마음을 떨칠 수가 없었다."], ["It gave him a surprised look--not to say a hearth-broomy kind of expression--which, my apprehensions whispered, might be fatal to us.", "그 머리는 그를 깜짝 놀란 사람처럼 — 아니, 벽난로 빗자루 같은 표정이라고 해도 좋을 만큼 — 보이게 만들었는데, 그것이 우리에게 치명적일지도 모른다고 내 불안이 속삭였다."]], [["I took the liberty of mentioning it to Traddles, as we were walking to Putney; and saying that if he WOULD smooth it down a little--", "퍼트니로 걸어가는 동안, 나는 외람되게 그 점을 트래들스에게 꺼내며, 머리카락을 조금만 가라앉혀 준다면 좋겠다고 말했다 —"]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, lifting off his hat, and rubbing his hair all kinds of ways, ‘nothing would give me greater pleasure.", "\"이보게 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 모자를 벗고 머리카락을 온갖 방향으로 문지르며 말했다. \"그렇게만 된다면 나도 더없이 기쁘겠네."], ["But it won’t.’", "하지만 안 돼.\""]], [["‘Won’t be smoothed down?’ said I.", "\"가라앉지를 않는다고?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ said Traddles.", "\"안 돼.\""], ["‘Nothing will induce it.", "트래들스가 말했다."], ["If I was to carry a half-hundred-weight upon it, all the way to Putney, it would be up again the moment the weight was taken off.", "\"무슨 수를 써도 안 가라앉아. 퍼트니까지 내내 그 위에 오십 파운드짜리 추를 얹고 간다 해도, 추를 떼는 순간 도로 곤두서고 말 거야."], ["You have no idea what obstinate hair mine is, Copperfield.", "자네는 내 머리가 얼마나 고집통인지 모를 걸세, 코퍼필드."], ["I am quite a fretful porcupine.’", "나는 완전히 안달복달하는 고슴도치라니까.\""]], [["I was a little disappointed, I must confess, but thoroughly charmed by his good-nature too. I told him how I esteemed his good-nature; and said that his hair must have taken all the obstinacy out of his character, for he had none.", "솔직히 좀 실망스럽긴 했지만, 그의 사람 좋은 천성에 또 흠뻑 반하고 말았다. 나는 그 좋은 천성을 얼마나 높이 사는지 말해 주었고, 그의 머리카락이 그의 성격에서 고집이란 고집은 죄다 빼내 가 버린 모양이라고, 그에게는 고집이라곤 한 톨도 없으니 말이다, 하고 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Oh!’ returned Traddles, laughing.", "\"오!\" 트래들스가 웃으며 받았다."], ["‘I assure you, it’s quite an old story, my unfortunate hair.", "\"정말이지 내 불행한 머리카락은 아주 해묵은 이야기라네."], ["My uncle’s wife couldn’t bear it. She said it exasperated her.", "우리 큰아버지 부인은 이 머리를 참아 내질 못했어. 자길 약 올린다나."], ["It stood very much in my way, too, when I first fell in love with Sophy. Very much!’", "처음 소피에게 반했을 때도 이게 어찌나 걸림돌이 됐던지. 아주 단단히 말일세!\""]], [["‘Did she object to it?’", "\"그 아가씨가 싫어했나?\""]], [["‘SHE didn’t,’ rejoined Traddles; ‘but her eldest sister--the one that’s the Beauty--quite made game of it, I understand.", "\"그녀는 아니었어.\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"다만 그녀 맏언니가 — 미인이라는 그 언니 말이야 — 이걸 완전히 놀림감으로 삼았던 모양이야."], ["In fact, all the sisters laugh at it.’", "사실 자매들이 죄다 이걸 보고 웃거든.\""]], [["‘Agreeable!’ said I.", "\"거참 유쾌하구먼!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Traddles with perfect innocence, ‘it’s a joke for us.", "\"그렇지.\" 트래들스가 더없이 천진하게 받았다. \"우리한테는 웃음거리지."], ["They pretend that Sophy has a lock of it in her desk, and is obliged to shut it in a clasped book, to keep it down.", "자기들끼리 짓궂게 굴기를, 소피가 책상 속에 내 머리카락 한 타래를 넣어 두고, 그게 곤두서지 못하도록 잠금쇠 달린 책에 끼워 두어야 한다고 한다네."], ["We laugh about it.’", "우린 그걸로 웃고 그래.\""]], [["‘By the by, my dear Traddles,’ said I, ‘your experience may suggest something to me.", "\"그건 그렇고, 이보게 트래들스,\" 내가 말했다. \"자네 경험에서 내가 뭔가 얻을 게 있을지도 모르겠네."], ["When you became engaged to the young lady whom you have just mentioned, did you make a regular proposal to her family?", "방금 말한 그 아가씨와 약혼할 때, 자네는 그 집안에 정식으로 청혼을 했었나?"], ["Was there anything like--what we are going through today, for instance?’ I added, nervously.", "이를테면 — 오늘 우리가 겪고 있는 것 같은 그런 일이 있었나?\" 나는 초조하게 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Why,’ replied Traddles, on whose attentive face a thoughtful shade had stolen, ‘it was rather a painful transaction, Copperfield, in my case.", "\"글쎄,\" 트래들스가 받았는데, 그 사려 깊은 얼굴에 어느새 생각에 잠긴 그늘이 드리워 있었다. \"내 경우에는 꽤나 고통스러운 거래였네, 코퍼필드."], ["You see, Sophy being of so much use in the family, none of them could endure the thought of her ever being married.", "알다시피 소피가 집안에서 워낙 쓸모가 많다 보니, 식구들 누구도 그녀가 시집간다는 생각을 견디질 못하는 거야."], ["Indeed, they had quite settled among themselves that she never was to be married, and they called her the old maid.", "사실 자기들끼리 그녀는 절대 시집 안 보낸다고 못 박아 놓고는, 그녀를 노처녀라고 불렀거든."], ["Accordingly, when I mentioned it, with the greatest precaution, to Mrs. Crewler--’", "그래서 내가 더없이 조심조심 크루러 부인에게 그 얘길 꺼냈을 때 —\""]], [["‘The mama?’ said I.", "\"그 어머니 말인가?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘The mama,’ said Traddles--‘Reverend Horace Crewler--when I mentioned it with every possible precaution to Mrs.", "\"그 어머니지.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["Crewler, the effect upon her was such that she gave a scream and became insensible.", "\"호러스 크루러 목사님 사모님 말이야 — 내가 온갖 조심을 다 기울여 크루러 부인에게 그 얘길 꺼냈더니, 그 효과가 어찌나 대단했던지 부인이 비명을 지르며 정신을 잃고 말았네."], ["I couldn’t approach the subject again, for months.’", "그 뒤로 몇 달 동안은 그 얘길 다시 꺼내지도 못했어.\""]], [["‘You did at last?’ said I.", "\"그래도 결국엔 꺼냈나?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, the Reverend Horace did,’ said Traddles.", "\"음, 호러스 목사님이 꺼내셨지.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘He is an excellent man, most exemplary in every way; and he pointed out to her that she ought, as a Christian, to reconcile herself to the sacrifice (especially as it was so uncertain), and to bear no uncharitable feeling towards me.", "\"그분은 더없이 훌륭한 분이고, 모든 면에서 본보기가 되는 분이거든. 그분이 부인에게, 기독교인으로서 마땅히 이 희생을 (더구나 워낙 불확실한 일이니만큼) 받아들이고, 나를 향해 매정한 감정을 품어서는 안 된다고 일깨워 주셨어."], ["As to myself, Copperfield, I give you my word, I felt a perfect bird of prey towards the family.’", "나로 말하자면, 코퍼필드, 맹세코, 그 집안에 대해 완전히 맹금이라도 된 듯한 기분이었다네.\""]], [["‘The sisters took your part, I hope, Traddles?’", "\"자매들은 자네 편을 들어 줬으면 좋았겠는데, 트래들스?\""]], [["‘Why, I can’t say they did,’ he returned.", "\"글쎄, 그랬다고는 못 하겠네.\""], ["‘When we had comparatively reconciled Mrs. Crewler to it, we had to break it to Sarah.", "그가 받았다. \"크루러 부인을 그럭저럭 누그러뜨리고 나니, 이번엔 세라에게 그 얘길 꺼내야 했어."], ["You recollect my mentioning Sarah, as the one that has something the matter with her spine?’", "척추에 뭔가 문제가 있다는 세라 얘길 내가 했던 거, 기억하나?\""]], [["‘Perfectly!’", "\"잘 기억하지!\""]], [["‘She clenched both her hands,’ said Traddles, looking at me in dismay; ‘shut her eyes; turned lead-colour; became perfectly stiff; and took nothing for two days but toast-and-water, administered with a tea-spoon.’", "\"그녀가 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐더군.\" 트래들스가 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 얼굴로 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"그러고는 눈을 감고, 납빛으로 변하더니, 온몸이 빳빳하게 굳어서는, 이틀 동안 찻숟가락으로 떠 넣어 주는 구운 빵과 물 말고는 아무것도 입에 대질 않았어.\""]], [["‘What a very unpleasant girl, Traddles!’ I remarked.", "\"거참 어지간히도 거북한 아가씨로구먼, 트래들스!\" 내가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Oh, I beg your pardon, Copperfield!’ said Traddles.", "\"오, 그건 좀 양해해 주게, 코퍼필드!\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘She is a very charming girl, but she has a great deal of feeling.", "\"아주 사랑스러운 아가씨인데, 다만 감정이 워낙 풍부해서 그래."], ["In fact, they all have. Sophy told me afterwards, that the self-reproach she underwent while she was in attendance upon Sarah, no words could describe.", "사실 그 집 식구들이 다 그렇거든. 나중에 소피가 그러는데, 세라를 간호하면서 자기가 겪은 자책감은 무슨 말로도 표현할 수 없다더군."], ["I know it must have been severe, by my own feelings, Copperfield; which were like a criminal’s.", "내 심정으로 미루어 봐도 그게 분명 모질었으리란 걸 알겠어, 코퍼필드. 마치 죄인 같은 심정이었으니까."], ["After Sarah was restored, we still had to break it to the other eight; and it produced various effects upon them of a most pathetic nature.", "세라가 회복되고 나서도, 우린 나머지 여덟 명에게 또 그 얘길 꺼내야 했고, 그게 저마다에게 더없이 애처로운 갖가지 반응을 불러일으켰지."], ["The two little ones, whom Sophy educates, have only just left off de-testing me.’", "소피가 가르치는 어린 두 동생은 이제 막 나를 끔찍이 싫어하길 그만뒀을 정도야.\""]], [["‘At any rate, they are all reconciled to it now, I hope?’ said I.", "\"어쨌든 이제는 다들 그 일을 받아들였으면 좋겠는데, 그렇지?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Ye-yes, I should say they were, on the whole, resigned to it,’ said Traddles, doubtfully.", "\"그-그래, 대체로 보면 단념했다고는 할 수 있겠지.\" 트래들스가 미심쩍은 듯 말했다."], ["‘The fact is, we avoid mentioning the subject; and my unsettled prospects and indifferent circumstances are a great consolation to them.", "\"사실은, 우린 그 얘길 꺼내는 걸 피하거든. 그리고 내 앞날이 불안하고 형편이 변변찮다는 점이 그들에겐 큰 위안이 되고 있어."], ["There will be a deplorable scene, whenever we are married.", "우리가 결혼할 땐 통탄스러운 광경이 벌어질 거야."], ["It will be much more like a funeral, than a wedding.", "결혼식이라기보다는 차라리 장례식에 훨씬 가깝겠지."], ["And they’ll all hate me for taking her away!’", "그리고 식구들은 죄다 내가 그녀를 데려간다고 나를 미워할 거고!\""]], [["His honest face, as he looked at me with a serio-comic shake of his head, impresses me more in the remembrance than it did in the reality, for I was by this time in a state of such excessive trepidation and wandering of mind, as to be quite unable to fix my attention on anything.", "그가 정색과 우스개를 뒤섞어 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 나를 바라보던 그 정직한 얼굴은, 실제로 마주했을 때보다 돌이켜 떠올리는 지금 더 깊은 인상으로 남아 있다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그때 나는 극도의 떨림과 산만함에 빠진 나머지 무엇 하나에도 도무지 정신을 붙들 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["On our approaching the house where the Misses Spenlow lived, I was at such a discount in respect of my personal looks and presence of mind, that Traddles proposed a gentle stimulant in the form of a glass of ale.", "스펜로 자매분이 사는 집에 다가갈 무렵, 나는 외모며 침착함이며 죄다 형편없이 떨어진 상태여서, 트래들스가 맥주 한 잔이라는 형태의 부드러운 자극제를 권했다."], ["This having been administered at a neighbouring public-house, he conducted me, with tottering steps, to the Misses Spenlow’s door.", "이웃 술집에서 그것을 들이켜고 나자, 그는 휘청거리는 걸음의 나를 부축해 스펜로 자매분 댁 문 앞까지 데려다주었다."]], [["I had a vague sensation of being, as it were, on view, when the maid opened it; and of wavering, somehow, across a hall with a weather-glass in it, into a quiet little drawing-room on the ground-floor, commanding a neat garden. Also of sitting down here, on a sofa, and seeing Traddles’s hair start up, now his hat was removed, like one of those obtrusive little figures made of springs, that fly out of fictitious snuff-boxes when the lid is taken off.", "하녀가 문을 열었을 때, 나는 어쩐지 구경거리로 내걸린 듯한 어렴풋한 느낌이 들었다. 그리고 어찌어찌 청우계가 놓인 현관을 비틀비틀 가로질러, 일 층의 아담한 정원이 내려다보이는 조용하고 자그마한 응접실로 들어선 느낌, 거기 소파에 앉은 느낌, 그리고 모자를 벗자 트래들스의 머리카락이 곤두서는 것을 본 느낌 — 가짜 코담뱃갑 뚜껑을 열면 튀어나오는, 용수철로 만든 그 주제넘은 작은 인형들 가운데 하나처럼."], ["Also of hearing an old-fashioned clock ticking away on the chimney-piece, and trying to make it keep time to the jerking of my heart,--which it wouldn’t.", "그리고 벽난로 선반 위에서 구식 시계가 똑딱이는 소리를 들으며, 그것을 내 심장의 발딱거림에 맞춰 보려 애쓰던 느낌 — 도무지 맞춰지지 않았지만."], ["Also of looking round the room for any sign of Dora, and seeing none.", "그리고 방 안을 둘러보며 도라의 흔적을 찾았으나 아무것도 보이지 않던 느낌."], ["Also of thinking that Jip once barked in the distance, and was instantly choked by somebody.", "그리고 멀리서 집이 한 번 짖었다가 누군가에게 곧장 입이 틀어막힌 듯하다고 생각한 느낌."], ["Ultimately I found myself backing Traddles into the fireplace, and bowing in great confusion to two dry little elderly ladies, dressed in black, and each looking wonderfully like a preparation in chip or tan of the late Mr. Spenlow.", "마침내 나는 트래들스를 벽난로 쪽으로 밀어붙인 채, 검은 옷을 입은 두 분의 자그마하고 메마른 노부인에게 어쩔 줄 모르며 절을 하고 있는 나 자신을 발견했는데, 두 분 모두 작고하신 스펜로 씨를 나무껍질이나 가죽으로 빚어 놓은 표본처럼 신기할 만큼 빼닮아 있었다."]], [["‘Pray,’ said one of the two little ladies, ‘be seated.’", "\"부디,\" 두 자그마한 부인 가운데 한 분이 말했다. \"앉으시지요.\""]], [["When I had done tumbling over Traddles, and had sat upon something which was not a cat--my first seat was--I so far recovered my sight, as to perceive that Mr.", "내가 트래들스를 들이받고 넘어지길 멈추고, 고양이가 아닌 무언가 위에 앉고 — 처음 앉은 자리가 그러했다 — 나서야, 나는 시야를 어느 정도 되찾아 다음을 알아차렸다."], ["Spenlow had evidently been the youngest of the family; that there was a disparity of six or eight years between the two sisters; and that the younger appeared to be the manager of the conference, inasmuch as she had my letter in her hand--so familiar as it looked to me, and yet so odd!--and was referring to it through an eye-glass.", "스펜로 씨는 분명 집안에서 막내였다는 것, 두 자매 사이에는 여섯 살이나 여덟 살쯤 터울이 진다는 것, 그리고 동생 쪽이 이 회담의 진행을 맡은 듯하다는 것이었다. 내 편지를 손에 쥐고 있는 게 바로 그분이었기 때문인데 — 그 편지가 나에게는 어찌나 낯익으면서도 또 어찌나 묘하게 보이던지! — 그분은 외알 안경 너머로 그것을 들여다보고 있었다."], ["They were dressed alike, but this sister wore her dress with a more youthful air than the other; and perhaps had a trifle more frill, or tucker, or brooch, or bracelet, or some little thing of that kind, which made her look more lively.", "두 분은 똑같이 차려입었으나, 이 동생 쪽이 언니보다 좀 더 젊은 분위기로 옷을 걸치고 있었다. 어쩌면 주름 장식이나 깃 장식, 브로치나 팔찌, 또는 그런 류의 소소한 것을 살짝 더 달고 있어서, 한결 생기 있어 보였는지도 모른다."], ["They were both upright in their carriage, formal, precise, composed, and quiet.", "두 분 다 자세가 꼿꼿하고, 격식 차리고, 빈틈없고, 차분하고, 조용했다."], ["The sister who had not my letter, had her arms crossed on her breast, and resting on each other, like an Idol.", "내 편지를 들지 않은 언니 쪽은 두 팔을 가슴 위에 포개어 서로 기대 놓고 있었는데, 마치 우상 같았다."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield, I believe,’ said the sister who had got my letter, addressing herself to Traddles.", "\"코퍼필드 씨가 맞으시지요.\" 내 편지를 든 동생 쪽이 트래들스를 향해 말했다."]], [["This was a frightful beginning.", "이것은 끔찍한 시작이었다."], ["Traddles had to indicate that I was Mr. Copperfield, and I had to lay claim to myself, and they had to divest themselves of a preconceived opinion that Traddles was Mr.", "트래들스는 코퍼필드 씨가 나임을 가리켜 보여야 했고, 나는 내가 나임을 주장해야 했으며, 두 분은 트래들스가 코퍼필드 씨라는 미리 품은 생각을 떨쳐 내야 했으니, 통틀어 우리는 꽤나 딱한 처지에 놓인 셈이었다."], ["Copperfield, and altogether we were in a nice condition.", "그 처지를 더욱 빛내 주려고, 우리는 모두 또렷이 들었다."], ["To improve it, we all distinctly heard Jip give two short barks, and receive another choke.", "집이 짧게 두 번 짖고는 또 한 번 입이 틀어막히는 소리를."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield!’ said the sister with the letter.", "\"코퍼필드 씨!\" 편지를 든 동생 쪽이 말했다."]], [["I did something--bowed, I suppose--and was all attention, when the other sister struck in.", "나는 무언가를 했다 — 짐작건대 절을 했을 것이다 — 그러고는 온통 귀를 기울이고 있는데, 언니 쪽이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘My sister Lavinia,’ said she ‘being conversant with matters of this nature, will state what we consider most calculated to promote the happiness of both parties.’", "\"제 동생 러비니아가,\" 그분이 말했다. \"이런 종류의 일에 밝으니, 양쪽 모두의 행복을 도모하는 데 가장 알맞다고 저희가 여기는 바를 말씀드릴 거예요.\""]], [["I discovered afterwards that Miss Lavinia was an authority in affairs of the heart, by reason of there having anciently existed a certain Mr.", "나는 나중에 러비니아 양이 연애 문제에 일가견이 있는 분이라는 것을 알게 되었는데, 옛날에 짧은 휘스트를 즐기던 어떤 피저 씨라는 사람이 있어 그녀에게 마음을 두었다고 짐작되기 때문이었다."], ["Pidger, who played short whist, and was supposed to have been enamoured of her. My private opinion is, that this was entirely a gratuitous assumption, and that Pidger was altogether innocent of any such sentiments--to which he had never given any sort of expression that I could ever hear of.", "내 개인적인 생각으로는, 이는 순전히 근거 없는 억측이며, 피저는 그런 류의 감정과는 전혀 무관했다 — 내가 들어 본 한, 그는 그런 감정을 그 어떤 식으로도 내비친 적이 없었으니까. 그렇지만 러비니아 양과 클러리사 양은 둘 다 미신처럼 믿고 있었다."], ["Both Miss Lavinia and Miss Clarissa had a superstition, however, that he would have declared his passion, if he had not been cut short in his youth (at about sixty) by over-drinking his constitution, and over-doing an attempt to set it right again by swilling Bath water.", "피저가 젊은 시절에 (예순 살쯤에) 술을 너무 마셔 몸을 망가뜨리고, 그것을 다시 바로잡겠다며 배스 광천수를 들이켜다가 그만 이를 무리하게 한 탓에 일찍 꺾이지만 않았다면, 틀림없이 자기 열정을 고백했으리라고. 두 분은 심지어 그가 남모를 사랑 때문에 죽었다고 은근히 의심하기까지 했다."], ["They had a lurking suspicion even, that he died of secret love; though I must say there was a picture of him in the house with a damask nose, which concealment did not appear to have ever preyed upon.", "그렇지만 솔직히 말하자면, 그 집에는 그가 다홍빛 코를 한 채로 그려진 초상화가 걸려 있었는데, 그 모습으로 보아 사랑을 감추느라 속을 끓인 흔적이라곤 도무지 보이지 않았다."]], [["‘We will not,’ said Miss Lavinia, ‘enter on the past history of this matter.", "\"우리는,\" 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"이 일의 지난 내력은 들추지 않겠어요."], ["Our poor brother Francis’s death has cancelled that.’", "우리 가엾은 오빠 프랜시스의 죽음으로 그건 다 지워졌으니까요.\""]], [["‘We had not,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘been in the habit of frequent association with our brother Francis; but there was no decided division or disunion between us.", "\"우리는,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"오라버니 프랜시스와 자주 왕래하는 사이는 아니었어요."], ["Francis took his road; we took ours.", "하지만 우리 사이에 딱 잘라 갈라서거나 등 돌린 일은 없었지요."], ["We considered it conducive to the happiness of all parties that it should be so.", "프랜시스는 프랜시스의 길을 갔고, 우리는 우리 길을 갔어요. 우리는 그것이 양쪽 모두의 행복에 보탬이 된다고 여겼지요."], ["And it was so.’", "그리고 실제로 그러했어요.\""]], [["Each of the sisters leaned a little forward to speak, shook her head after speaking, and became upright again when silent.", "자매분은 말할 때마다 몸을 조금씩 앞으로 기울였고, 말을 마치면 고개를 흔들었으며, 입을 다물면 다시 꼿꼿해졌다."], ["Miss Clarissa never moved her arms.", "클러리사 양은 결코 팔을 움직이지 않았다."], ["She sometimes played tunes upon them with her fingers--minuets and marches I should think--but never moved them.", "가끔 손가락으로 팔 위에 가락을 두드리기는 했는데 — 짐작건대 미뉴에트며 행진곡 따위를 — 그러면서도 팔만큼은 결코 움직이지 않았다."]], [["‘Our niece’s position, or supposed position, is much changed by our brother Francis’s death,’ said Miss Lavinia; ‘and therefore we consider our brother’s opinions as regarded her position as being changed too.", "\"우리 조카의 처지는, 아니 그러리라 짐작되는 처지는, 오라버니 프랜시스의 죽음으로 크게 달라졌어요.\" 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"그러니 우리는 조카의 처지에 관한 오라버니의 생각도 달라졌다고 봅니다."], ["We have no reason to doubt, Mr. Copperfield, that you are a young gentleman possessed of good qualities and honourable character; or that you have an affection--or are fully persuaded that you have an affection--for our niece.’", "코퍼필드 씨, 우리는 당신이 좋은 자질과 떳떳한 인품을 갖춘 젊은 신사라는 것, 또 우리 조카에게 애정을 품고 있다는 것 — 아니, 애정을 품고 있다고 굳게 믿고 있다는 것 — 을 의심할 까닭이 없어요.\""]], [["I replied, as I usually did whenever I had a chance, that nobody had ever loved anybody else as I loved Dora.", "나는 기회가 생길 때마다 으레 하던 대로, 내가 도라를 사랑하는 만큼 누구를 사랑한 사람은 세상에 없을 거라고 대답했다."], ["Traddles came to my assistance with a confirmatory murmur.", "트래들스가 맞장구치는 중얼거림으로 나를 거들었다."]], [["Miss Lavinia was going on to make some rejoinder, when Miss Clarissa, who appeared to be incessantly beset by a desire to refer to her brother Francis, struck in again:", "러비니아 양이 무언가 대꾸를 이어 가려는데, 줄곧 오라버니 프랜시스 얘기를 꺼내고 싶은 충동에 시달리는 듯한 클러리사 양이 또 끼어들었다."]], [["‘If Dora’s mama,’ she said, ‘when she married our brother Francis, had at once said that there was not room for the family at the dinner-table, it would have been better for the happiness of all parties.’", "\"도라의 어머니가,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"우리 오라버니 프랜시스와 결혼할 때, 진작에 식탁에 가족이 앉을 자리는 없다고 잘라 말했더라면, 양쪽 모두의 행복을 위해 더 나았을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Sister Clarissa,’ said Miss Lavinia.", "\"클러리사 언니,\" 러비니아 양이 말했다."], ["‘Perhaps we needn’t mind that now.’", "\"그 얘긴 이제 굳이 안 해도 되지 않을까요.\""]], [["‘Sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘it belongs to the subject.", "\"러비니아 동생,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다."], ["With your branch of the subject, on which alone you are competent to speak, I should not think of interfering.", "\"그건 이 일에 속하는 얘기야. 네가 말할 자격이 있는 그 부분에 대해서라면 나는 끼어들 생각이 없어."], ["On this branch of the subject I have a voice and an opinion.", "하지만 이 부분에 대해서는 나도 할 말이 있고 내 의견이 있어."], ["It would have been better for the happiness of all parties, if Dora’s mama, when she married our brother Francis, had mentioned plainly what her intentions were.", "도라의 어머니가 우리 오라버니 프랜시스와 결혼할 때, 자기 속셈을 솔직하게 밝혔더라면 양쪽 모두의 행복을 위해 더 나았을 거야."], ["We should then have known what we had to expect.", "그랬다면 우리도 무엇을 기대해야 할지 알았겠지."], ["We should have said “Pray do not invite us, at any time”; and all possibility of misunderstanding would have been avoided.’", "우리도 '아무 때고 저희를 초대하지 말아 주세요'라고 했을 테고, 오해의 소지는 죄다 피할 수 있었을 거야.\""]], [["When Miss Clarissa had shaken her head, Miss Lavinia resumed: again referring to my letter through her eye-glass.", "클러리사 양이 고개를 흔들고 나자, 러비니아 양이 다시 말을 이으며, 또 한 번 외알 안경 너머로 내 편지를 들여다보았다."], ["They both had little bright round twinkling eyes, by the way, which were like birds’ eyes.", "그건 그렇고, 두 분 다 작고 반짝이며 동그랗게 빛나는 눈을 가졌는데, 꼭 새 눈 같았다."], ["They were not unlike birds, altogether; having a sharp, brisk, sudden manner, and a little short, spruce way of adjusting themselves, like canaries.", "두 분은 통틀어 새와 그리 다르지 않았으니, 날카롭고 잽싸고 불쑥불쑥하는 태도며, 카나리아처럼 짧고 말쑥하게 매무새를 가다듬는 모양새가 그러했다."]], [["Miss Lavinia, as I have said, resumed:", "앞서 말했듯이, 러비니아 양이 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["‘You ask permission of my sister Clarissa and myself, Mr. Copperfield, to visit here, as the accepted suitor of our niece.’", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 우리 조카가 받아들인 구혼자 자격으로 이곳을 드나들 수 있게 해 달라고, 제 언니 클러리사와 저에게 허락을 청하셨지요.\""]], [["‘If our brother Francis,’ said Miss Clarissa, breaking out again, if I may call anything so calm a breaking out, ‘wished to surround himself with an atmosphere of Doctors’ Commons, and of Doctors’ Commons only, what right or desire had we to object?", "\"우리 오라버니 프랜시스가,\" 이토록 차분한 것을 두고 폭발이라 부를 수 있다면, 클러리사 양이 또 한 번 폭발하며 말했다. \"법학원 분위기로, 그것도 오직 법학원 분위기로만 자기 둘레를 채우고 싶어 했다면, 우리에게 무슨 권리나 마음이 있어 거기에 토를 달겠어요?"], ["None, I am sure.", "없지요, 분명히."], ["We have ever been far from wishing to obtrude ourselves on anyone. But why not say so?", "우리는 누구에게도 억지로 비집고 들어가길 늘 멀리해 왔어요. 하지만 왜 그렇다고 말하지 않았을까요?"], ["Let our brother Francis and his wife have their society.", "오라버니 프랜시스와 그 부인은 그들의 사교 모임을 가지라지요."], ["Let my sister Lavinia and myself have our society.", "제 동생 러비니아와 저는 우리 사교 모임을 가질 테니까요."], ["We can find it for ourselves, I hope.’", "그쯤은 우리 스스로 찾을 수 있어요, 그러길 바라요.\""]], [["As this appeared to be addressed to Traddles and me, both Traddles and I made some sort of reply.", "이 말이 트래들스와 나에게 한 것 같아서, 트래들스도 나도 무언가 대답 비슷한 것을 했다."], ["Traddles was inaudible.", "트래들스의 말은 들리지 않았다."], ["I think I observed, myself, that it was highly creditable to all concerned.", "나로 말하자면, 그것이 관련된 모두에게 더없이 떳떳한 일이라고 한 것 같다."], ["I don’t in the least know what I meant.", "그게 무슨 뜻이었는지는 나도 도무지 모르겠다."]], [["‘Sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa, having now relieved her mind, ‘you can go on, my dear.’", "\"러비니아 동생,\" 이제 속이 후련해진 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"이어서 하렴, 얘야.\""]], [["Miss Lavinia proceeded:", "러비니아 양이 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield, my sister Clarissa and I have been very careful indeed in considering this letter; and we have not considered it without finally showing it to our niece, and discussing it with our niece.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 제 언니 클러리사와 저는 정말이지 이 편지를 매우 신중하게 검토했어요. 게다가 끝내는 우리 조카에게 보여 주고, 조카와 의논까지 한 끝에야 검토를 마쳤지요."], ["We have no doubt that you think you like her very much.’", "우리는 당신이 그 애를 무척 좋아한다고 여긴다는 걸 의심하지 않아요.\""]], [["‘Think, ma’am,’ I rapturously began, ‘oh!--’", "\"여기다니요, 부인.\" 내가 황홀하게 입을 떼었다. \"오! —\""]], [["But Miss Clarissa giving me a look (just like a sharp canary), as requesting that I would not interrupt the oracle, I begged pardon.", "그러나 클러리사 양이 (꼭 날카로운 카나리아처럼) 나를 쳐다보며, 신탁을 가로막지 말라는 듯이 굴기에, 나는 용서를 빌었다."]], [["‘Affection,’ said Miss Lavinia, glancing at her sister for corroboration, which she gave in the form of a little nod to every clause, ‘mature affection, homage, devotion, does not easily express itself.", "\"애정이란,\" 러비니아 양이 동의를 구하듯 언니를 흘끗 보자, 언니가 구절마다 가볍게 고갯짓으로 동의를 표하는 가운데 말했다. \"무르익은 애정, 흠모, 헌신이란, 쉽사리 제 모습을 드러내지 않아요."], ["Its voice is low.", "그 목소리는 낮지요."], ["It is modest and retiring, it lies in ambush, waits and waits.", "겸손하고 물러나 있으며, 숨어 엎드린 채 기다리고 또 기다려요."], ["Such is the mature fruit.", "무르익은 열매란 그런 거예요."], ["Sometimes a life glides away, and finds it still ripening in the shade.’", "때로는 한평생이 미끄러지듯 흘러가도, 그것이 여전히 그늘 속에서 익어 가고 있음을 보게 되지요.\""]], [["Of course I did not understand then that this was an allusion to her supposed experience of the stricken Pidger; but I saw, from the gravity with which Miss Clarissa nodded her head, that great weight was attached to these words.", "물론 그때는 이것이 그녀가 사랑에 꺾인 피저에 대해 겪었다고 짐작되는 경험을 빗댄 말이라는 것을 알지 못했다. 하지만 클러리사 양이 무겁게 고개를 끄덕이는 그 진지함으로 미루어, 이 말에 큰 무게가 실려 있음은 알아챘다."]], [["‘The light--for I call them, in comparison with such sentiments, the light--inclinations of very young people,’ pursued Miss Lavinia, ‘are dust, compared to rocks.", "\"아주 어린 사람들의 가벼운 — 이런 감정에 견주어 나는 이를 가볍다고 부르겠는데 — 마음의 끌림이란,\" 러비니아 양이 이어 갔다. \"바위에 견주면 먼지나 다름없어요."], ["It is owing to the difficulty of knowing whether they are likely to endure or have any real foundation, that my sister Clarissa and myself have been very undecided how to act, Mr. Copperfield, and Mr.--’", "그것이 과연 오래 갈 만한 것인지, 아니면 무슨 참된 바탕이 있는 것인지 알기가 어렵기에, 제 언니 클러리사와 저는 어떻게 처신해야 할지 무척 망설였어요, 코퍼필드 씨, 그리고 — 씨도요.\""]], [["‘Traddles,’ said my friend, finding himself looked at.", "\"트래들스입니다.\" 자기를 쳐다보는 것을 알아챈 내 친구가 말했다."]], [["‘I beg pardon.", "\"실례했어요."], ["Of the Inner Temple, I believe?’ said Miss Clarissa, again glancing at my letter.", "이너 템플 소속이시지요?\" 클러리사 양이 또 한 번 내 편지를 흘끗 보며 말했다."]], [["Traddles said ‘Exactly so,’ and became pretty red in the face.", "트래들스는 \"바로 그렇습니다\" 하고는, 얼굴이 제법 벌게졌다."]], [["Now, although I had not received any express encouragement as yet, I fancied that I saw in the two little sisters, and particularly in Miss Lavinia, an intensified enjoyment of this new and fruitful subject of domestic interest, a settling down to make the most of it, a disposition to pet it, in which there was a good bright ray of hope.", "자, 비록 나는 아직 그 어떤 분명한 격려도 받지 못했지만, 두 자그마한 자매분에게서, 특히 러비니아 양에게서, 이 새롭고 풍성한 집안 관심거리를 한껏 즐기는 기색, 이를 최대한 우려먹으려고 자리를 잡는 기색, 이를 어루만지며 귀여워하려는 기색을 본 듯했고, 거기에는 밝고 환한 희망의 한 줄기 빛이 깃들어 있었다."], ["I thought I perceived that Miss Lavinia would have uncommon satisfaction in superintending two young lovers, like Dora and me; and that Miss Clarissa would have hardly less satisfaction in seeing her superintend us, and in chiming in with her own particular department of the subject whenever that impulse was strong upon her. This gave me courage to protest most vehemently that I loved Dora better than I could tell, or anyone believe; that all my friends knew how I loved her; that my aunt, Agnes, Traddles, everyone who knew me, knew how I loved her, and how earnest my love had made me.", "내가 보기에 러비니아 양은 도라와 나처럼 어린 두 연인을 감독하는 데 남다른 만족을 느낄 듯했고, 클러리사 양도 동생이 우리를 감독하는 모습을 지켜보며, 또 충동이 강하게 일 때마다 자기만의 그 특별한 영역으로 끼어들며, 그에 못지않은 만족을 느낄 듯했다. 이것이 나에게 용기를 주어, 나는 누구에게 말로 다 할 수도, 누가 곧이들을 수도 없을 만큼 도라를 사랑한다고, 내 벗들이 죄다 내가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지 안다고, 고모님이며 아그네스며 트래들스며 나를 아는 사람이라면 누구나 내가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그 사랑이 나를 얼마나 진실하게 만들었는지 안다고, 더없이 열렬하게 항변했다."], ["For the truth of this, I appealed to Traddles.", "그 진실함을 두고 나는 트래들스에게 증언을 청했다."], ["And Traddles, firing up as if he were plunging into a Parliamentary Debate, really did come out nobly: confirming me in good round terms, and in a plain sensible practical manner, that evidently made a favourable impression.", "그러자 트래들스는 마치 의회 토론에라도 뛰어드는 양 불끈 달아올라, 정말이지 훌륭하게 나섰다. 그는 시원시원하고 묵직한 말로, 또 꾸밈없고 사리에 맞으며 실속 있는 태도로 나를 뒷받침했는데, 그것이 보아하니 좋은 인상을 남겼다."]], [["‘I speak, if I may presume to say so, as one who has some little experience of such things,’ said Traddles, ‘being myself engaged to a young lady--one of ten, down in Devonshire--and seeing no probability, at present, of our engagement coming to a termination.’", "\"외람되나마 말씀드리자면, 저는 이런 일에 약간의 경험이 있는 사람으로서 말씀드리는 겁니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"저 자신도 어떤 아가씨와 약혼한 처지거든요 — 데번셔의 열 자매 가운데 하나인데요 — 그리고 지금으로선 우리 약혼이 깨질 낌새는 전혀 보이지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘You may be able to confirm what I have said, Mr.", "\"제가 한 말을 당신이 뒷받침해 주실 수 있겠군요, 트래들스 씨.\""], ["Traddles,’ observed Miss Lavinia, evidently taking a new interest in him, ‘of the affection that is modest and retiring; that waits and waits?’", "분명히 그에게 새로운 흥미를 느끼며 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"겸손하고 물러나 있으며, 기다리고 또 기다리는 그 애정에 대해서 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Entirely, ma’am,’ said Traddles.", "\"전적으로요, 부인.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["Miss Clarissa looked at Miss Lavinia, and shook her head gravely.", "클러리사 양이 러비니아 양을 바라보며 무겁게 고개를 흔들었다."], ["Miss Lavinia looked consciously at Miss Clarissa, and heaved a little sigh.", "러비니아 양은 무언가 깨달은 듯 클러리사 양을 바라보며 작게 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["‘Sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘take my smelling-bottle.’", "\"러비니아 동생,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"내 냄새 맡는 약병을 받으렴.\""]], [["Miss Lavinia revived herself with a few whiffs of aromatic vinegar--Traddles and I looking on with great solicitude the while; and then went on to say, rather faintly:", "러비니아 양은 향초 식초를 몇 번 들이마시며 기운을 차렸고 — 그동안 트래들스와 나는 무척 근심스럽게 지켜보았다 — 그러고는 다소 가냘프게 말을 이었다."]], [["‘My sister and myself have been in great doubt, Mr. Traddles, what course we ought to take in reference to the likings, or imaginary likings, of such very young people as your friend Mr. Copperfield and our niece.’", "\"제 언니와 저는, 트래들스 씨, 당신 벗 코퍼필드 씨와 우리 조카처럼 아주 어린 사람들의 호감, 아니 호감이라고 상상하는 그 마음에 관해, 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 무척 망설였어요.\""]], [["‘Our brother Francis’s child,’ remarked Miss Clarissa.", "\"우리 오라버니 프랜시스의 자식이지요.\""], ["‘If our brother Francis’s wife had found it convenient in her lifetime (though she had an unquestionable right to act as she thought best) to invite the family to her dinner-table, we might have known our brother Francis’s child better at the present moment.", "클러리사 양이 한마디 했다. \"오라버니 프랜시스의 부인이 생전에 (물론 자기가 가장 옳다고 여기는 대로 행할 두말없는 권리가 있긴 했지만) 우리 가족을 식탁에 부르는 게 편하다고 여겼더라면, 우리도 지금쯤 오라버니 프랜시스의 자식을 더 잘 알았을 거예요."], ["Sister Lavinia, proceed.’", "러비니아 동생, 계속하렴.\""]], [["Miss Lavinia turned my letter, so as to bring the superscription towards herself, and referred through her eye-glass to some orderly-looking notes she had made on that part of it.", "러비니아 양은 내 편지를 돌려 겉봉의 글씨가 자기 쪽을 향하게 한 다음, 외알 안경 너머로 그 부분에 적어 둔 단정해 보이는 메모를 들여다보았다."]], [["‘It seems to us,’ said she, ‘prudent, Mr.", "\"우리가 보기에는,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["Traddles, to bring these feelings to the test of our own observation.", "\"트래들스 씨, 이 감정들을 우리 자신의 관찰이라는 시험대에 올려 보는 것이 신중한 처사인 듯해요."], ["At present we know nothing of them, and are not in a situation to judge how much reality there may be in them.", "지금으로선 우리는 그 감정에 대해 아무것도 모르고, 거기에 얼마만큼의 진실함이 있는지 판단할 처지도 못 되니까요."], ["Therefore we are inclined so far to accede to Mr. Copperfield’s proposal, as to admit his visits here.’", "그래서 우리는 코퍼필드 씨의 청을, 그가 이곳을 드나드는 것을 허락하는 선까지는 받아들일 마음이에요.\""]], [["‘I shall never, dear ladies,’ I exclaimed, relieved of an immense load of apprehension, ‘forget your kindness!’", "\"친애하는 부인들,\" 내가 어마어마한 불안의 짐을 덜고 외쳤다. \"두 분의 친절을 저는 결코 잊지 않겠습니다!\""]], [["‘But,’ pursued Miss Lavinia,--‘but, we would prefer to regard those visits, Mr.", "\"하지만,\" 러비니아 양이 이어 갔다. \"하지만 우리는 그 드나듦을, 트래들스 씨, 지금으로선 우리를 찾아오는 것으로 여기고 싶어요."], ["Traddles, as made, at present, to us. We must guard ourselves from recognizing any positive engagement between Mr. Copperfield and our niece, until we have had an opportunity--’", "우리에게 기회가 생길 때까지는, 코퍼필드 씨와 우리 조카 사이의 그 어떤 분명한 약혼도 인정하지 않도록 우리 스스로 경계해야 하니까요 —\""]], [["‘Until YOU have had an opportunity, sister Lavinia,’ said Miss Clarissa.", "\"네가 기회를 갖게 될 때까지지, 러비니아 동생.\" 클러리사 양이 말했다."]], [["‘Be it so,’ assented Miss Lavinia, with a sigh--‘until I have had an opportunity of observing them.’", "\"그렇다고 하지요.\" 러비니아 양이 한숨과 함께 동의했다. \"내가 그들을 관찰할 기회를 갖게 될 때까지요.\""]], [["‘Copperfield,’ said Traddles, turning to me, ‘you feel, I am sure, that nothing could be more reasonable or considerate.’", "\"코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이보다 더 이치에 맞고 사려 깊은 일은 없으리라는 걸, 자네도 분명 느낄 걸세.\""]], [["‘Nothing!’ cried I.", "\"없고말고!\" 내가 외쳤다."], ["‘I am deeply sensible of it.’", "\"그 점을 저는 뼈저리게 느낍니다.\""]], [["‘In this position of affairs,’ said Miss Lavinia, again referring to her notes, ‘and admitting his visits on this understanding only, we must require from Mr.", "\"이런 사정인 만큼,\" 러비니아 양이 다시 메모를 들여다보며 말했다. \"그리고 오직 이런 양해 아래서만 그의 드나듦을 허락하는 만큼, 우리는 코퍼필드 씨에게 명예를 걸고 분명한 다짐을 받아야 해요."], ["Copperfield a distinct assurance, on his word of honour, that no communication of any kind shall take place between him and our niece without our knowledge.", "우리가 모르는 채로는 그와 우리 조카 사이에 어떤 식의 연락도 오가지 않으리라는 것 말이에요."], ["That no project whatever shall be entertained with regard to our niece, without being first submitted to us--’ ‘To you, sister Lavinia,’ Miss Clarissa interposed.", "우리 조카에 관한 그 어떤 계획도, 먼저 우리에게 알리지 않고서는 도모하지 않으리라는 것도요 —\" \"너에게지, 러비니아 동생.\" 클러리사 양이 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Be it so, Clarissa!’ assented Miss Lavinia resignedly--‘to me--and receiving our concurrence.", "\"그렇다고 하자, 클러리사!\" 러비니아 양이 체념한 듯 동의했다. \"나에게 알리고 — 우리 동의를 얻으리라는 것 말이에요."], ["We must make this a most express and serious stipulation, not to be broken on any account. We wished Mr.", "우리는 이것을 더없이 분명하고 엄중한 조건으로 삼아야 하며, 어떤 경우에도 어겨서는 안 돼요."], ["Copperfield to be accompanied by some confidential friend today,’ with an inclination of her head towards Traddles, who bowed, ‘in order that there might be no doubt or misconception on this subject.", "우리가 오늘 코퍼필드 씨더러 믿을 만한 벗을 한 분 데려오시라고 한 것은,\" 트래들스 쪽으로 고개를 까딱하자 그가 절을 했다. \"이 문제에 관해 의심이나 오해가 없도록 하기 위해서였어요."], ["If Mr. Copperfield, or if you, Mr. Traddles, feel the least scruple, in giving this promise, I beg you to take time to consider it.’", "만약 코퍼필드 씨나, 또는 트래들스 씨, 당신이 이 약속을 하기에 조금이라도 께름칙한 데가 있다면, 부디 시간을 두고 생각해 보시기를 청해요.\""]], [["I exclaimed, in a state of high ecstatic fervour, that not a moment’s consideration could be necessary.", "나는 한껏 황홀한 열기에 휩싸여, 단 한순간도 생각할 필요가 없다고 외쳤다."], ["I bound myself by the required promise, in a most impassioned manner; called upon Traddles to witness it; and denounced myself as the most atrocious of characters if I ever swerved from it in the least degree.", "나는 더없이 열정적인 태도로 요구된 약속에 나 자신을 옭아맸고, 트래들스에게 증인이 되어 달라고 청했으며, 만약 내가 그 약속에서 조금이라도 비껴난다면 나야말로 더없이 흉악한 인간이라고 스스로를 단죄했다."]], [["‘Stay!’ said Miss Lavinia, holding up her hand; ‘we resolved, before we had the pleasure of receiving you two gentlemen, to leave you alone for a quarter of an hour, to consider this point.", "\"잠깐!\" 러비니아 양이 손을 들며 말했다. \"우리는 두 분 신사를 맞이하는 기쁨을 누리기에 앞서, 십오 분 동안 두 분만 남겨 두고 이 점을 곰곰이 생각하시도록 하기로 정해 두었어요."], ["You will allow us to retire.’", "우리가 물러가는 걸 허락해 주시겠지요.\""]], [["It was in vain for me to say that no consideration was necessary.", "생각할 필요가 없다고 말해 봐야 헛일이었다."], ["They persisted in withdrawing for the specified time.", "두 분은 정해진 시간만큼 물러가겠다고 고집을 부렸다."], ["Accordingly, these little birds hopped out with great dignity; leaving me to receive the congratulations of Traddles, and to feel as if I were translated to regions of exquisite happiness.", "그리하여 이 자그마한 새 두 마리는 무척 위엄 있게 폴짝폴짝 나갔고, 나는 남아서 트래들스의 축하를 받으며, 마치 내가 더없이 황홀한 행복의 나라로 옮겨진 듯한 기분에 잠겼다."], ["Exactly at the expiration of the quarter of an hour, they reappeared with no less dignity than they had disappeared.", "정확히 십오 분이 다 지나자, 두 분은 물러갈 때 못지않은 위엄으로 다시 나타났다."], ["They had gone rustling away as if their little dresses were made of autumn-leaves: and they came rustling back, in like manner.", "두 분은 마치 자그마한 옷이 가을 낙엽으로 지어지기라도 한 양 바스락거리며 나갔다가, 똑같이 바스락거리며 돌아온 것이다."]], [["I then bound myself once more to the prescribed conditions.", "그러고서 나는 또 한 번 정해진 조건에 나 자신을 옭아맸다."]], [["‘Sister Clarissa,’ said Miss Lavinia, ‘the rest is with you.’", "\"클러리사 언니,\" 러비니아 양이 말했다. \"나머지는 언니가 맡아요.\""]], [["Miss Clarissa, unfolding her arms for the first time, took the notes and glanced at them.", "클러리사 양은 처음으로 팔짱을 풀고, 메모를 집어 들어 흘끗 보았다."]], [["‘We shall be happy,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘to see Mr.", "\"우리는,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다."], ["Copperfield to dinner, every Sunday, if it should suit his convenience.", "\"코퍼필드 씨가 편하시다면, 일요일마다 저녁 식사에 모시는 게 기쁘겠어요."], ["Our hour is three.’\nI bowed.", "우리 식사 시간은 세 시예요.\""]], [["‘In the course of the week,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘we shall be happy to see Mr.", "나는 절을 했다. \"주중에는,\" 클러리사 양이 말했다."], ["Copperfield to tea. Our hour is half-past six.’", "\"코퍼필드 씨를 차 시간에 모시는 게 기쁘겠어요. 우리 차 시간은 여섯 시 반이고요.\""]], [["I bowed again.", "나는 또 절을 했다."]], [["‘Twice in the week,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘but, as a rule, not oftener.’", "\"일주일에 두 번이에요.\" 클러리사 양이 말했다. \"다만 보통은 그보다 잦지 않게요.\""]], [["I bowed again.", "나는 또 절을 했다."]], [["‘Miss Trotwood,’ said Miss Clarissa, ‘mentioned in Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨 편지에 언급된 트롯우드 양도,\""], ["Copperfield’s letter, will perhaps call upon us.", "클러리사 양이 말했다. \"어쩌면 우리를 찾아 주시겠지요."], ["When visiting is better for the happiness of all parties, we are glad to receive visits, and return them.", "왕래가 양쪽 모두의 행복에 더 나을 때라면, 우리는 기꺼이 방문을 받고 또 답방하지요."], ["When it is better for the happiness of all parties that no visiting should take place, (as in the case of our brother Francis, and his establishment) that is quite different.’", "왕래가 없는 편이 양쪽 모두의 행복에 더 나을 때라면 (우리 오라버니 프랜시스와 그 식솔의 경우처럼) 그건 또 전혀 다른 얘기지만요.\""]], [["I intimated that my aunt would be proud and delighted to make their acquaintance; though I must say I was not quite sure of their getting on very satisfactorily together.", "나는 고모님이 두 분과 사귀게 되어 자랑스럽고 기쁘게 여기시리라는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 다만 솔직히 말해, 두 쪽이 그리 흡족하게 어울려 지낼지는 영 자신이 없었다."], ["The conditions being now closed, I expressed my acknowledgements in the warmest manner; and, taking the hand, first of Miss Clarissa, and then of Miss Lavinia, pressed it, in each case, to my lips.", "이제 조건이 다 매듭지어졌기에, 나는 더없이 따뜻한 태도로 감사의 뜻을 표했다. 그리고 먼저 클러리사 양의 손을, 이어서 러비니아 양의 손을 잡아, 그때마다 그 손을 내 입술에 갖다 댔다."]], [["Miss Lavinia then arose, and begging Mr. Traddles to excuse us for a minute, requested me to follow her.", "그러자 러비니아 양이 일어나서, 트래들스 씨에게 잠깐 양해를 구하고는, 나더러 따라오라고 했다."], ["I obeyed, all in a tremble, and was conducted into another room.", "나는 온통 떨면서 그 말을 따랐고, 다른 방으로 인도되었다."], ["There I found my blessed darling stopping her ears behind the door, with her dear little face against the wall; and Jip in the plate-warmer with his head tied up in a towel.", "거기서 나는 내 축복받은 그대가 귀여운 작은 얼굴을 벽에 댄 채 문 뒤에서 두 귀를 틀어막고 있는 것을 보았다. 그리고 집은 머리를 수건으로 싸맨 채 접시 데우는 통 속에 들어 있었다."]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["How beautiful she was in her black frock, and how she sobbed and cried at first, and wouldn’t come out from behind the door!", "검은 옷을 입은 그녀가 어찌나 아름답던지, 그리고 처음에는 또 어찌나 흐느끼며 울던지, 문 뒤에서 좀체 나오려 들지 않던지!"], ["How fond we were of one another, when she did come out at last; and what a state of bliss I was in, when we took Jip out of the plate-warmer, and restored him to the light, sneezing very much, and were all three reunited!", "마침내 그녀가 나왔을 때 우리가 서로 얼마나 다정했던지. 그리고 우리가 집을 접시 데우는 통에서 꺼내, 연거푸 재채기를 해 대는 그 녀석을 다시 빛 가운데로 돌려놓고, 셋이 다 함께 다시 만났을 때, 나는 또 얼마나 더없는 행복에 잠겨 있었던지!"]], [["‘My dearest Dora!", "\"내 가장 사랑하는 도라!"], ["Now, indeed, my own for ever!’", "이제 정말로, 영원히 나만의 그대로구려!\""]], [["‘Oh, DON’T!’ pleaded Dora. ‘Please!’", "\"오, 그러지 마세요!\" 도라가 애원했다. \"제발!\""]], [["‘Are you not my own for ever, Dora?’", "\"그대는 영원히 나만의 사람이 아니오, 도라?\""]], [["‘Oh yes, of course I am!’ cried Dora, ‘but I am so frightened!’", "\"오 그럼요, 당연히 그렇지요!\" 도라가 외쳤다. \"하지만 난 너무 무서운걸요!\""]], [["‘Frightened, my own?’", "\"무섭다니, 내 사람아?\""]], [["‘Oh yes!", "\"오 그래요!"], ["I don’t like him,’ said Dora.", "난 저분이 싫어요.\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘Why don’t he go?’", "\"저분은 왜 안 가는 거죠?\""]], [["‘Who, my life?’", "\"누구 말이오, 내 생명이여?\""]], [["‘Your friend,’ said Dora.", "\"당신 친구분이요.\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘It isn’t any business of his.", "\"이건 저분이 상관할 일이 아니잖아요."], ["What a stupid he must be!’", "어쩜 저렇게 멍청할까요!\""]], [["‘My love!’ (There never was anything so coaxing as her childish ways.) ‘He is the best creature!’", "\"내 사랑!\" (그녀의 어린애 같은 몸짓만큼 사람을 녹이는 것은 없었다.) \"그는 더없이 좋은 사람이라오!\""]], [["‘Oh, but we don’t want any best creatures!’ pouted Dora.", "\"오, 하지만 우린 그런 더없이 좋은 사람 같은 건 필요 없는걸요!\" 도라가 입을 삐죽였다."]], [["‘My dear,’ I argued, ‘you will soon know him well, and like him of all things.", "\"여보,\" 내가 타일렀다. \"당신도 곧 그를 잘 알게 되면, 무엇보다 그를 좋아하게 될 거요."], ["And here is my aunt coming soon; and you’ll like her of all things too, when you know her.’", "그리고 곧 우리 고모님도 오실 텐데, 고모님도 알게 되면 무엇보다 좋아하게 될 거요.\""]], [["‘No, please don’t bring her!’ said Dora, giving me a horrified little kiss, and folding her hands.", "\"아뇨, 제발 고모님은 데려오지 말아요!\" 도라가 겁에 질린 작은 입맞춤을 나에게 하고는 두 손을 모으며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t.", "\"안 돼요."], ["I know she’s a naughty, mischief-making old thing!", "난 알아요, 그분은 심술궂고, 말썽이나 부리는 늙은이라는 걸!"], ["Don’t let her come here, Doady!’ which was a corruption of David.", "그분을 여기 오게 하지 말아요, 도디!\" 이는 데이비드를 비틀어 부르는 말이었다."]], [["Remonstrance was of no use, then; so I laughed, and admired, and was very much in love and very happy; and she showed me Jip’s new trick of standing on his hind legs in a corner--which he did for about the space of a flash of lightning, and then fell down--and I don’t know how long I should have stayed there, oblivious of Traddles, if Miss Lavinia had not come in to take me away.", "그때는 타일러 봐야 소용없었다. 그래서 나는 그저 웃고, 감탄하고, 사랑에 흠뻑 빠져 더없이 행복했다. 그녀는 집이 구석에서 뒷다리로 서는 새 재주를 보여 주었는데 — 녀석은 번개가 번쩍하는 정도의 짧은 동안 그러다가 폭삭 주저앉았다 — 만약 러비니아 양이 들어와 나를 데려가지 않았더라면, 나는 트래들스도 까맣게 잊은 채 거기 얼마나 오래 머물렀을지 모른다."], ["Miss Lavinia was very fond of Dora (she told me Dora was exactly like what she had been herself at her age--she must have altered a good deal), and she treated Dora just as if she had been a toy.", "러비니아 양은 도라를 무척 귀여워했고 (그녀는 도라가 자기 그 나이 적 모습과 똑 닮았다고 했는데 — 그렇다면 그동안 어지간히 달라진 모양이다), 도라를 꼭 장난감처럼 다루었다."], ["I wanted to persuade Dora to come and see Traddles, but on my proposing it she ran off to her own room and locked herself in; so I went to Traddles without her, and walked away with him on air.", "나는 도라를 구슬려 트래들스를 만나러 가게 하고 싶었으나, 그 말을 꺼내자 그녀는 제 방으로 달아나 문을 잠가 버렸다. 그래서 나는 그녀 없이 트래들스에게 갔고, 그와 함께 둥실둥실 떠가는 기분으로 걸어 나왔다."]], [["‘Nothing could be more satisfactory,’ said Traddles; ‘and they are very agreeable old ladies, I am sure.", "\"이보다 더 흡족할 수가 없네.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그리고 분명 무척 상냥한 노부인들이야."], ["I shouldn’t be at all surprised if you were to be married years before me, Copperfield.’", "자네가 나보다 몇 해는 먼저 결혼한다 해도 나는 조금도 놀라지 않을 걸세, 코퍼필드.\""]], [["‘Does your Sophy play on any instrument, Traddles?’ I inquired, in the pride of my heart.", "\"자네의 소피는 무슨 악기라도 다루나, 트래들스?\" 내가 우쭐한 마음에 물었다."]], [["‘She knows enough of the piano to teach it to her little sisters,’ said Traddles.", "\"피아노라면 어린 동생들에게 가르칠 만큼은 알지.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Does she sing at all?’ I asked.", "\"노래는 좀 하고?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, she sings ballads, sometimes, to freshen up the others a little when they’re out of spirits,’ said Traddles.", "\"음, 가끔 동생들이 풀 죽어 있을 때 기운 좀 북돋워 주려고 발라드를 부르긴 해.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘Nothing scientific.’", "\"전문적인 건 아니고.\""]], [["‘She doesn’t sing to the guitar?’ said I.", "\"기타에 맞춰서 노래하지는 않나?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh dear no!’ said Traddles.", "\"오, 천만에!\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Paint at all?’", "\"그림은 좀 그리나?\""]], [["‘Not at all,’ said Traddles.", "\"전혀.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["I promised Traddles that he should hear Dora sing, and see some of her flower-painting.", "나는 트래들스에게 도라의 노래를 들려주고, 그녀가 그린 꽃 그림도 몇 점 보여 주겠다고 약속했다."], ["He said he should like it very much, and we went home arm in arm in great good humour and delight.", "그는 그러면 무척 좋겠다고 했고, 우리는 팔짱을 끼고 더없이 흐뭇하고 즐거운 기분으로 집에 돌아갔다."], ["I encouraged him to talk about Sophy, on the way; which he did with a loving reliance on her that I very much admired.", "가는 길에 나는 그에게 소피 얘기를 하도록 부추겼고, 그는 그녀에 대한 사랑 어린 믿음으로 그 얘길 했는데, 나는 그게 무척 감탄스러웠다."], ["I compared her in my mind with Dora, with considerable inward satisfaction; but I candidly admitted to myself that she seemed to be an excellent kind of girl for Traddles, too.", "나는 속으로 그녀를 도라와 견주며 적잖은 흐뭇함을 느꼈다. 그러나 솔직히 나 자신에게 인정하기를, 그녀 또한 트래들스에게는 더없이 훌륭한 아가씨인 듯하다고 했다."]], [["Of course my aunt was immediately made acquainted with the successful issue of the conference, and with all that had been said and done in the course of it. She was happy to see me so happy, and promised to call on Dora’s aunts without loss of time.", "물론 고모님께는 곧장 회담이 잘 풀린 결과와, 그 과정에서 오간 말과 일을 죄다 알려 드렸다. 고모님은 내가 그토록 행복해하는 것을 보고 기뻐하셨고, 지체 없이 도라의 고모들을 찾아가겠다고 약속하셨다."], ["But she took such a long walk up and down our rooms that night, while I was writing to Agnes, that I began to think she meant to walk till morning.", "그러나 그날 밤 내가 아그네스에게 편지를 쓰는 동안 고모님이 우리 방을 어찌나 오래 왔다 갔다 거니시던지, 나는 고모님이 아침까지 거닐 작정이신가 하는 생각마저 들었다."]], [["My letter to Agnes was a fervent and grateful one, narrating all the good effects that had resulted from my following her advice.", "아그네스에게 보낸 내 편지는 열렬하고 고마움이 넘치는 것이었는데, 그녀의 조언을 따른 데서 나온 온갖 좋은 결실을 죄다 적었다."], ["She wrote, by return of post, to me.", "그녀는 돌아오는 우편으로 나에게 답장을 했다."], ["Her letter was hopeful, earnest, and cheerful.", "그녀의 편지는 희망차고, 진지하고, 명랑했다."], ["She was always cheerful from that time.", "그녀는 그때부터 늘 명랑했다."]], [["I had my hands more full than ever, now.", "이제 나는 어느 때보다 두 손이 바빠졌다."], ["My daily journeys to Highgate considered, Putney was a long way off; and I naturally wanted to go there as often as I could.", "하이게이트로 날마다 오가는 것을 감안하면, 퍼트니는 먼 길이었다. 그런데도 나는 자연히 가능한 한 자주 그곳에 가고 싶었다."], ["The proposed tea-drinkings being quite impracticable, I compounded with Miss Lavinia for permission to visit every Saturday afternoon, without detriment to my privileged Sundays.", "차를 마시러 오라는 제안은 도무지 지키기 어려운 일이어서, 나는 러비니아 양과 타협해, 내가 특권으로 누리는 일요일을 축내지 않으면서 토요일 오후마다 방문해도 좋다는 허락을 받아 냈다."], ["So, the close of every week was a delicious time for me; and I got through the rest of the week by looking forward to it.", "그리하여 매주의 끝자락은 나에게 더없이 달콤한 시간이었고, 나는 그것을 기다리는 마음으로 나머지 한 주를 견뎌 냈다."]], [["I was wonderfully relieved to find that my aunt and Dora’s aunts rubbed on, all things considered, much more smoothly than I could have expected.", "고모님과 도라의 고모들이, 이모저모 따져 보아도 내가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 매끄럽게 잘 지내는 것을 알고 나는 무척 마음이 놓였다."], ["My aunt made her promised visit within a few days of the conference; and within a few more days, Dora’s aunts called upon her, in due state and form.", "고모님은 회담이 있은 지 며칠 안 되어 약속한 방문을 했고, 다시 며칠이 더 지나 도라의 고모들이 격식과 절차를 갖추어 고모님을 답방했다."], ["Similar but more friendly exchanges took place afterwards, usually at intervals of three or four weeks.", "그 뒤로도 비슷하나 한결 다정한 왕래가 이어졌는데, 대개 서너 주 간격이었다."], ["I know that my aunt distressed Dora’s aunts very much, by utterly setting at naught the dignity of fly-conveyance, and walking out to Putney at extraordinary times, as shortly after breakfast or just before tea; likewise by wearing her bonnet in any manner that happened to be comfortable to her head, without at all deferring to the prejudices of civilization on that subject.", "내가 알기로 고모님은 도라의 고모들을 무척이나 곤혹스럽게 만들었는데, 마차로 오가는 격식을 깡그리 무시하고 아침 식사 직후나 차 시간 직전 같은 엉뚱한 때에 퍼트니까지 걸어 나가는가 하면, 그 문제에 관한 문명의 통념 따위는 조금도 개의치 않고, 그저 머리에 편한 대로 아무렇게나 보닛을 쓰고 다녔기 때문이다."], ["But Dora’s aunts soon agreed to regard my aunt as an eccentric and somewhat masculine lady, with a strong understanding; and although my aunt occasionally ruffled the feathers of Dora’s aunts, by expressing heretical opinions on various points of ceremony, she loved me too well not to sacrifice some of her little peculiarities to the general harmony.", "그래도 도라의 고모들은 이내 고모님을, 분별이 야무진, 좀 별나고 다소 남성적인 부인으로 여기기로 했다. 그리고 고모님이 이따금 갖가지 예법 문제를 두고 이단스러운 의견을 내뱉어 도라의 고모들의 깃털을 곤두서게 하긴 했어도, 고모님은 나를 워낙 끔찍이 사랑한 나머지 전체의 화목을 위해 자신의 자잘한 별난 점 몇 가지쯤은 기꺼이 희생하셨다."]], [["The only member of our small society who positively refused to adapt himself to circumstances, was Jip.", "우리의 작은 무리 가운데 형편에 자기를 맞추기를 한사코 거부한 단 하나의 일원은 집이었다."], ["He never saw my aunt without immediately displaying every tooth in his head, retiring under a chair, and growling incessantly: with now and then a doleful howl, as if she really were too much for his feelings.", "녀석은 고모님만 봤다 하면 곧장 입안의 이란 이는 죄다 드러내 보이고, 의자 밑으로 물러나, 쉴 새 없이 으르렁거렸다. 이따금은 고모님이 정말이지 제 감정으로는 도무지 못 견딜 상대라는 듯 구슬프게 울부짖기까지 했다."], ["All kinds of treatment were tried with him, coaxing, scolding, slapping, bringing him to Buckingham Street (where he instantly dashed at the two cats, to the terror of all beholders); but he never could prevail upon himself to bear my aunt’s society.", "녀석에게는 온갖 방법이 다 동원되었다 — 어르고, 야단치고, 찰싹 때리고, 버킹엄 거리로 데려가 보고 (거기서 녀석은 두 고양이에게 단박에 덤벼들어 보는 사람들을 죄다 질겁하게 만들었다)."], ["He would sometimes think he had got the better of his objection, and be amiable for a few minutes; and then would put up his snub nose, and howl to that extent, that there was nothing for it but to blind him and put him in the plate-warmer.", "그러나 녀석은 도무지 고모님과 함께 있는 것을 견뎌 낼 마음을 먹지 못했다. 가끔은 제 반감을 이겨 낸 줄 알고 몇 분쯤 상냥하게 굴다가도, 이내 그 들창코를 쳐들고 어찌나 울부짖던지, 별수 없이 눈을 가려 접시 데우는 통에 가둘 수밖에 없었다."], ["At length, Dora regularly muffled him in a towel and shut him up there, whenever my aunt was reported at the door.", "결국 도라는 문간에 고모님이 왔다는 기별이 오면, 으레 녀석을 수건으로 싸맨 채 거기 가두어 두었다."]], [["One thing troubled me much, after we had fallen into this quiet train.", "이렇게 조용한 흐름에 접어든 뒤로 나를 무척 괴롭힌 것이 하나 있었다."], ["It was, that Dora seemed by one consent to be regarded like a pretty toy or plaything.", "그것은 도라가 다들 한마음으로 마치 예쁜 장난감이나 노리개처럼 대접받는 것 같다는 점이었다."], ["My aunt, with whom she gradually became familiar, always called her Little Blossom; and the pleasure of Miss Lavinia’s life was to wait upon her, curl her hair, make ornaments for her, and treat her like a pet child.", "차츰 그녀와 허물없어진 고모님은 늘 그녀를 '꼬마 꽃송이'라고 불렀다. 그리고 러비니아 양의 삶의 낙은 그녀의 시중을 들고, 머리를 곱슬곱슬 말아 주고, 장신구를 만들어 주고, 귀여운 아이처럼 다루는 것이었다."], ["What Miss Lavinia did, her sister did as a matter of course.", "러비니아 양이 하는 일이라면 그 언니도 으레 따라 했다. 나에게는 무척 묘한 노릇이었다."], ["It was very odd to me; but they all seemed to treat Dora, in her degree, much as Dora treated Jip in his.", "그런데 다들 도라를 그 나름의 격에 맞춰, 꼭 도라가 집을 그 나름의 격에 맞춰 대하는 것과 똑같이 대하는 것 같았다."]], [["I made up my mind to speak to Dora about this; and one day when we were out walking (for we were licensed by Miss Lavinia, after a while, to go out walking by ourselves), I said to her that I wished she could get them to behave towards her differently.", "나는 이 일을 두고 도라에게 말해 보기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 하루는 둘이 밖으로 산책을 나갔을 때 (얼마간 지나자 러비니아 양이 우리끼리 산책을 나가도 좋다고 허락해 주었다), 나는 그녀에게, 식구들이 그녀를 좀 다르게 대하도록 해 줬으면 좋겠다고 말했다."]], [["‘Because you know, my darling,’ I remonstrated, ‘you are not a child.’", "\"왜냐하면 말이오, 여보,\" 내가 타일렀다. \"당신은 어린애가 아니잖소.\""]], [["‘There!’ said Dora.", "\"저것 봐요!\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘Now you’re going to be cross!’", "\"또 화내려고 그러는 거죠!\""]], [["‘Cross, my love?’", "\"화라니, 내 사랑?\""]], [["‘I am sure they’re very kind to me,’ said Dora, ‘and I am very happy--’", "\"그분들은 분명 나한테 무척 잘해 주는걸요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그리고 난 무척 행복하고요 —\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"그래!"], ["But my dearest life!’ said I, ‘you might be very happy, and yet be treated rationally.’", "하지만 내 가장 소중한 사람이여!\" 내가 말했다. \"당신은 무척 행복하면서도, 또 사리에 맞게 대접받을 수도 있는 거요.\""]], [["Dora gave me a reproachful look--the prettiest look!--and then began to sob, saying, if I didn’t like her, why had I ever wanted so much to be engaged to her? And why didn’t I go away, now, if I couldn’t bear her?", "도라는 나를 나무라는 눈빛으로 보았다 — 더없이 예쁜 눈빛이었다! — 그러더니 흐느끼기 시작하며 말하기를, 자기를 좋아하지 않으면서 왜 그토록 자기와 약혼하고 싶어 했느냐고, 그리고 자기를 견딜 수 없다면 지금이라도 왜 떠나지 않느냐고 했다."]], [["What could I do, but kiss away her tears, and tell her how I doted on her, after that!", "그러니 그 뒤에 내가 그녀의 눈물을 입맞춤으로 닦아 주고, 내가 그녀를 얼마나 끔찍이 사랑하는지 말해 주는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가!"]], [["‘I am sure I am very affectionate,’ said Dora; ‘you oughtn’t to be cruel to me, Doady!’", "\"난 분명 정이 무척 많은 사람이에요.\" 도라가 말했다. \"당신은 나한테 모질게 굴면 안 돼요, 도디!\""]], [["‘Cruel, my precious love!", "\"모질다니, 내 소중한 사랑이여!"], ["As if I would--or could--be cruel to you, for the world!’", "내가 세상 무엇과도 바꾸어 당신에게 모질게 굴 — 아니, 모질게 굴 수나 있겠소!\""]], [["‘Then don’t find fault with me,’ said Dora, making a rosebud of her mouth; ‘and I’ll be good.’", "\"그럼 내 흠을 잡지 말아요.\" 도라가 입을 장미 봉오리처럼 오므리며 말했다. \"그럼 나도 착하게 굴게요.\""]], [["I was charmed by her presently asking me, of her own accord, to give her that cookery-book I had once spoken of, and to show her how to keep accounts as I had once promised I would.", "이윽고 그녀가 제 입으로 먼저, 언젠가 내가 말했던 그 요리책을 갖다 달라고, 그리고 언젠가 약속했던 대로 가계부 적는 법을 알려 달라고 청하기에, 나는 흠뻑 반하고 말았다."], ["I brought the volume with me on my next visit (I got it prettily bound, first, to make it look less dry and more inviting); and as we strolled about the Common, I showed her an old housekeeping-book of my aunt’s, and gave her a set of tablets, and a pretty little pencil-case and box of leads, to practise housekeeping with.", "다음 방문 때 나는 그 책을 가져갔고 (먼저 너무 딱딱해 보이지 않고 좀 더 끌리게끔 예쁘게 제본해 두었다), 우리가 공유지를 거니는 동안 고모님의 낡은 살림 장부를 보여 주었으며, 살림 연습을 하라고 그녀에게 글씨판 한 벌과, 예쁘고 작은 연필집과 심을 담은 갑을 주었다."]], [["But the cookery-book made Dora’s head ache, and the figures made her cry.", "그러나 요리책은 도라를 머리 아프게 했고, 숫자는 그녀를 울게 만들었다."], ["They wouldn’t add up, she said.", "숫자가 도무지 더해지질 않는다나."], ["So she rubbed them out, and drew little nosegays and likenesses of me and Jip, all over the tablets.", "그래서 그녀는 숫자를 지워 버리고, 글씨판 온통에 자그마한 꽃다발이며 나와 집의 모습을 그려 넣었다."]], [["Then I playfully tried verbal instruction in domestic matters, as we walked about on a Saturday afternoon.", "그러고서 나는 토요일 오후에 둘이 거닐며, 살림 문제를 말로 가르쳐 보려고 장난스레 시도했다."], ["Sometimes, for example, when we passed a butcher’s shop, I would say:", "이를테면 가끔 푸줏간을 지날 때면 나는 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["‘Now suppose, my pet, that we were married, and you were going to buy a shoulder of mutton for dinner, would you know how to buy it?’", "\"자 이보, 우리가 결혼했고, 당신이 저녁상에 올릴 양 어깨살을 사러 간다고 쳐 봐요. 그걸 어떻게 사야 할지 알겠소?\""]], [["My pretty little Dora’s face would fall, and she would make her mouth into a bud again, as if she would very much prefer to shut mine with a kiss.", "내 예쁘고 작은 도라의 얼굴은 시무룩해졌고, 그녀는 또 입을 봉오리처럼 오므렸다. 마치 그보다는 입맞춤으로 내 입을 막아 버리는 편이 훨씬 낫다는 듯이."]], [["‘Would you know how to buy it, my darling?’ I would repeat, perhaps, if I were very inflexible.", "\"그걸 어떻게 사야 할지 알겠소, 여보?\" 내가 무척 꿋꿋하게 굴 때면, 아마 이렇게 거듭 물었으리라."]], [["Dora would think a little, and then reply, perhaps, with great triumph:", "도라는 잠시 생각하더니, 이내 의기양양하게 대답하곤 했다."]], [["‘Why, the butcher would know how to sell it, and what need I know?", "\"아니, 그건 푸줏간 주인이 어떻게 팔지 알 텐데, 내가 알 게 뭐예요?"], ["Oh, you silly boy!’", "오, 바보 같은 사람아!\""]], [["So, when I once asked Dora, with an eye to the cookery-book, what she would do, if we were married, and I were to say I should like a nice Irish stew, she replied that she would tell the servant to make it; and then clapped her little hands together across my arm, and laughed in such a charming manner that she was more delightful than ever.", "그래서 한번은 요리책을 염두에 두고 도라에게, 우리가 결혼했는데 내가 맛있는 아일랜드식 스튜가 먹고 싶다고 하면 어떻게 하겠느냐고 물었더니, 그녀는 하녀더러 만들라고 시키겠다고 대답했다. 그러고는 내 팔에 작은 두 손을 마주쳐 손뼉을 치며 어찌나 사랑스럽게 웃던지, 그녀는 여느 때보다 더 매혹스러웠다."]], [["Consequently, the principal use to which the cookery-book was devoted, was being put down in the corner for Jip to stand upon.", "그 결과, 요리책의 주된 쓰임새는 구석에 놓여 집이 그 위에 올라서는 받침이 되는 것이었다."], ["But Dora was so pleased, when she had trained him to stand upon it without offering to come off, and at the same time to hold the pencil-case in his mouth, that I was very glad I had bought it.", "그래도 도라는, 녀석이 내려오려는 기색도 없이 그 위에 올라선 채 동시에 입에 연필집을 물고 있도록 길들여 놓고는 어찌나 흐뭇해하던지, 나는 그 책을 산 것이 무척 잘한 일이다 싶었다."]], [["And we fell back on the guitar-case, and the flower-painting, and the songs about never leaving off dancing, Ta ra la! and were as happy as the week was long.", "그러고서 우리는 다시 기타집과 꽃 그림과, 춤을 절대 멈추지 않겠다는 노래, 타 라 라! 로 돌아갔고, 한 주가 길고 긴 만큼이나 행복했다."], ["I occasionally wished I could venture to hint to Miss Lavinia, that she treated the darling of my heart a little too much like a plaything; and I sometimes awoke, as it were, wondering to find that I had fallen into the general fault, and treated her like a plaything too--but not often.", "나는 이따금, 러비니아 양에게 슬쩍 일러 줄 용기를 낼 수 있다면 좋겠다고 생각했다 — 내 마음의 그대를 좀 지나치게 노리개처럼 대하신다고 말이다. 그리고 가끔은, 나 또한 그 흔한 잘못에 빠져 그녀를 노리개처럼 대해 왔음을 문득 깨닫고는 화들짝 놀라곤 했다 — 그렇게 자주는 아니었지만."]], [["# CHAPTER 42. MISCHIEF", "# CHAPTER 42. MISCHIEF"]], [["I feel as if it were not for me to record, even though this manuscript is intended for no eyes but mine, how hard I worked at that tremendous short-hand, and all improvement appertaining to it, in my sense of responsibility to Dora and her aunts.", "내가 도라와 그 고모들에게 진 책임감 때문에, 저 가공할 속기와 거기에 딸린 온갖 향상에 얼마나 열심히 매달렸는지를 여기 굳이 적는 것은 — 비록 이 원고가 나 말고는 누구의 눈에도 보일 일이 없다 하더라도 — 내가 할 일이 아닌 것만 같다."], ["I will only add, to what I have already written of my perseverance at this time of my life, and of a patient and continuous energy which then began to be matured within me, and which I know to be the strong part of my character, if it have any strength at all, that there, on looking back, I find the source of my success.", "그저 내 인생의 이 시기에 내가 보여 준 끈기에 대하여, 그리고 그때 내 안에서 영글기 시작한, 참을성 있고 꾸준한 기운에 대하여 — 내 성격에 조금이라도 강한 구석이 있다면 바로 이것이 그 강한 부분이라고 나는 안다 — 이미 적어 둔 것에 한 가지만 덧붙이고 싶다. 돌이켜 보면, 거기에서 나는 내 성공의 원천을 찾는다."], ["I have been very fortunate in worldly matters; many men have worked much harder, and not succeeded half so well; but I never could have done what I have done, without the habits of punctuality, order, and diligence, without the determination to concentrate myself on one object at a time, no matter how quickly its successor should come upon its heels, which I then formed.", "나는 세상일에서 무척 운이 좋았다. 나보다 훨씬 더 열심히 일하고도 그 절반만큼도 성공하지 못한 사람이 많다. 하지만 나는 그때 몸에 익힌 시간 엄수와 질서와 부지런함의 습관 없이는, 그리고 한 번에 한 가지 일에만 나를 집중하겠다는 — 그 일의 뒤를 이을 일이 아무리 빨리 발꿈치를 밟아 오더라도 — 그런 결심 없이는, 내가 이룬 일을 결코 이루지 못했을 것이다."], ["Heaven knows I write this, in no spirit of self-laudation. The man who reviews his own life, as I do mine, in going on here, from page to page, had need to have been a good man indeed, if he would be spared the sharp consciousness of many talents neglected, many opportunities wasted, many erratic and perverted feelings constantly at war within his breast, and defeating him.", "하늘이 아시거니와, 나는 결코 제 자랑하는 마음으로 이것을 쓰는 것이 아니다. 나처럼 한 장 한 장 넘어가며 제 인생을 되짚어 보는 사람이라면, 묵혀 둔 숱한 재능과 흘려보낸 숱한 기회와, 가슴속에서 끊임없이 다투며 자신을 무너뜨리던 숱한 변덕스럽고 비뚤어진 감정을 또렷이 의식하지 않고 넘어가려면, 정말로 어지간히 좋은 사람이라야 할 것이다."], ["I do not hold one natural gift, I dare say, that I have not abused.", "감히 말하건대, 나에게는 함부로 굴리지 않은 타고난 재주가 단 하나도 없다."], ["My meaning simply is, that whatever I have tried to do in life, I have tried with all my heart to do well; that whatever I have devoted myself to, I have devoted myself to completely; that in great aims and in small, I have always been thoroughly in earnest.", "내 말뜻은 그저, 인생에서 무엇을 하려고 했든 나는 온 마음을 다해 잘하려고 애썼다는 것, 무엇에 나를 바쳤든 나는 거기에 온전히 나를 바쳤다는 것, 큰 목표에서나 작은 목표에서나 나는 늘 철저히 진심이었다는 것이다."], ["I have never believed it possible that any natural or improved ability can claim immunity from the companionship of the steady, plain, hard-working qualities, and hope to gain its end.", "타고난 재능이든 갈고닦은 재능이든, 그것이 꾸준하고 수수하고 고되게 일하는 자질의 동행을 면제받고도 제 목적을 이룰 수 있다고 나는 한 번도 믿어 본 적이 없다."], ["There is no such thing as such fulfilment on this earth.", "이 세상에 그런 식의 성취란 없다."], ["Some happy talent, and some fortunate opportunity, may form the two sides of the ladder on which some men mount, but the rounds of that ladder must be made of stuff to stand wear and tear; and there is no substitute for thorough-going, ardent, and sincere earnestness.", "어떤 행복한 재능과 어떤 운 좋은 기회가 어떤 사람이 올라가는 사다리의 양쪽 기둥을 이룰 수는 있겠지만, 그 사다리의 가로대만큼은 닳고 부대껴도 견딜 재료로 만들어져야 한다. 그리고 철저하고 뜨겁고 참된 진정성을 대신할 것은 없다."], ["Never to put one hand to anything, on which I could throw my whole self; and never to affect depreciation of my work, whatever it was; I find, now, to have been my golden rules.", "내 온몸을 던질 수 없는 일에는 결코 한 손도 대지 않을 것, 그리고 그것이 무엇이든 내 일을 짐짓 깎아내리는 시늉은 결코 하지 않을 것 — 이것이 내 황금률이었음을 나는 이제 깨닫는다."]], [["How much of the practice I have just reduced to precept, I owe to Agnes, I will not repeat here.", "내가 방금 격언으로 추려 낸 그 실천 가운데 얼마나 많은 것을 애그니스에게 빚지고 있는지는, 여기서 되풀이하지 않겠다."], ["My narrative proceeds to Agnes, with a thankful love.", "내 이야기는 고마운 사랑을 품고 애그니스에게로 나아간다."]], [["She came on a visit of a fortnight to the Doctor’s. Mr.", "애그니스는 보름 동안 박사님 댁에 머물러 다니러 왔다."], ["Wickfield was the Doctor’s old friend, and the Doctor wished to talk with him, and do him good.", "위크필드 씨는 박사님의 오랜 벗이었고, 박사님은 그와 이야기를 나누며 그에게 도움이 되어 주고 싶어 했다."], ["It had been matter of conversation with Agnes when she was last in town, and this visit was the result.", "지난번 애그니스가 시내에 왔을 때 이 일이 화제에 올랐고, 이번 방문이 그 결실이었다."], ["She and her father came together.", "애그니스와 아버지는 함께 왔다."], ["I was not much surprised to hear from her that she had engaged to find a lodging in the neighbourhood for Mrs.", "애그니스가 힙 부인을 위해 동네에 묵을 곳을 구해 주기로 했다는 말을 들었을 때도 나는 그리 놀라지 않았다."], ["Heep, whose rheumatic complaint required change of air, and who would be charmed to have it in such company.", "힙 부인은 류머티즘 탓에 공기를 바꿔 줄 필요가 있었고, 그런 사람들과 함께 지낼 수 있다면 더없이 좋아할 터였다."], ["Neither was I surprised when, on the very next day, Uriah, like a dutiful son, brought his worthy mother to take possession.", "바로 그 이튿날, 효성스러운 아들답게 유라이어가 자기 갸륵한 어머니를 모셔다가 그곳에 자리를 잡게 했을 때도 나는 놀라지 않았다."]], [["‘You see, Master Copperfield,’ said he, as he forced himself upon my company for a turn in the Doctor’s garden, ‘where a person loves, a person is a little jealous--leastways, anxious to keep an eye on the beloved one.’", "\"보세요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 박사님 정원을 한 바퀴 돌자며 억지로 내 곁에 끼어들어 말했다. \"사람이란 게 사랑을 하면 좀 질투가 나는 법이지요 — 적어도 사랑하는 사람한테서 눈을 떼지 않으려고 애를 쓰게 되니까요.\""]], [["‘Of whom are you jealous, now?’ said I.", "\"그래, 지금 누구를 질투하는 건데?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Thanks to you, Master Copperfield,’ he returned, ‘of no one in particular just at present--no male person, at least.’", "\"도련님 덕분에,\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"지금 당장은 딱히 누구랄 것도 없네요 — 적어도 남자는요.\""]], [["‘Do you mean that you are jealous of a female person?’", "“그럼 네가 어떤 여자를 질투한다는 말이냐?”"]], [["He gave me a sidelong glance out of his sinister red eyes, and laughed.", "그는 음흉한 붉은 눈으로 나를 곁눈질하더니 웃었다. \"정말이지, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Really, Master Copperfield,’ he said, ‘--I should say Mister, but I know you’ll excuse the abit I’ve got into--you’re so insinuating, that you draw me like a corkscrew!", "\"— 나리라고 불러야겠지만, 제 몸에 밴 버릇은 너그러이 봐주실 줄 압니다 — 도련님은 어찌나 슬슬 파고드시는지, 코르크 따개처럼 절 끌어내신다니까요! 뭐, 말씀드려도 상관없습니다만,\" 그가 그 물고기 같은 손을 내 손 위에 얹으며 말했다."], ["Well, I don’t mind telling you,’ putting his fish-like hand on mine, ‘I’m not a lady’s man in general, sir, and I never was, with Mrs. Strong.’", "\"저는 본디 여자한테 살가운 부류가 아니에요, 나리. 스트롱 부인한테도 한 번도 그런 적이 없고요.\""]], [["His eyes looked green now, as they watched mine with a rascally cunning.", "이제 그의 눈은 내 눈을 지켜보면서 악당 같은 교활함으로 푸르스름하게 보였다."]], [["‘What do you mean?’ said I.", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, though I am a lawyer, Master Copperfield,’ he replied, with a dry grin, ‘I mean, just at present, what I say.’", "\"아니, 제가 법률가이긴 합니다만, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 그가 메마른 히죽거림을 띠며 대답했다. \"지금 당장은 그저 말 그대로의 뜻이지요.\""]], [["‘And what do you mean by your look?’ I retorted, quietly.", "\"그럼 네 그 눈빛은 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 내가 조용히 되받았다."]], [["‘By my look?", "\"제 눈빛이요?"], ["Dear me, Copperfield, that’s sharp practice!", "아이고 참, 코퍼필드, 그건 너무 매서운 수법인데요!"], ["What do I mean by my look?’", "제 눈빛이 무슨 뜻이냐니요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said I.", "\"그래,\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘By your look.’", "\"네 그 눈빛 말이다.\""]], [["He seemed very much amused, and laughed as heartily as it was in his nature to laugh.", "그는 무척 재미있어하는 듯, 제 천성이 허락하는 한껏 마음을 다해 웃었다."], ["After some scraping of his chin with his hand, he went on to say, with his eyes cast downward--still scraping, very slowly:", "손으로 턱을 몇 번 긁적이고 나서, 그는 눈을 내리깐 채 — 여전히, 아주 느릿느릿 긁으면서 — 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘When I was but an umble clerk, she always looked down upon me.", "\"제가 한낱 미천한 서기였을 적에, 그 부인은 늘 절 깔보았지요."], ["She was for ever having my Agnes backwards and forwards at her ouse, and she was for ever being a friend to you, Master Copperfield; but I was too far beneath her, myself, to be noticed.’", "노상 우리 애그니스를 자기 집으로 이리 오라 저리 가라 불러 대고, 노상 도련님하고는 다정한 벗이 되어 주면서요, 코퍼필드 도련님. 한데 저 자신은 너무 까마득히 낮아서 눈에 들지도 못했답니다.\""]], [["‘Well?’ said I; ‘suppose you were!’", "\"그래서?\" 내가 말했다. \"그랬다 치자!\""]], [["‘--And beneath him too,’ pursued Uriah, very distinctly, and in a meditative tone of voice, as he continued to scrape his chin.", "\"— 그리고 그분한테도 까마득히 낮았지요.\" 유라이어가 턱을 계속 긁으면서, 사뭇 또렷이, 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 듯한 목소리로 이어 갔다."]], [["‘Don’t you know the Doctor better,’ said I, ‘than to suppose him conscious of your existence, when you were not before him?’", "\"네가 박사님 앞에 없을 때 박사님이 네 존재 따위를 의식하고 계셨으리라고 짐작할 만큼,\" 내가 말했다. \"박사님을 그것밖에 모르느냐?\""]], [["He directed his eyes at me in that sidelong glance again, and he made his face very lantern-jawed, for the greater convenience of scraping, as he answered:", "그는 다시 그 곁눈질로 나를 바라보았고, 더 편하게 긁으려는 듯 턱을 길쭉하게 빼며 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh dear, I am not referring to the Doctor!", "\"아이고 저런, 저는 박사님 말씀을 하는 게 아닙니다!"], ["Oh no, poor man!", "아니에요, 가엾으신 양반!"], ["I mean Mr. Maldon!’", "저는 몰던 씨 얘기를 하는 거예요!\""]], [["My heart quite died within me. All my old doubts and apprehensions on that subject, all the Doctor’s happiness and peace, all the mingled possibilities of innocence and compromise, that I could not unravel, I saw, in a moment, at the mercy of this fellow’s twisting.", "내 심장이 가슴속에서 완전히 죽어 버렸다. 그 일에 대한 나의 오랜 의심과 불안, 박사님의 온갖 행복과 평온, 도무지 풀어낼 수 없었던 결백과 묵인의 뒤얽힌 갖가지 가능성, 그 모든 것이 이 작자의 뒤틀어 대는 짓에 휘둘리고 있음을 나는 한순간에 깨달았다."]], [["‘He never could come into the office, without ordering and shoving me about,’ said Uriah.", "\"그 양반은 사무소에 들어올 때마다 꼭 절 부려 먹고 이리저리 떠밀지 않고는 못 배겼어요.\" 유라이어가 말했다."], ["‘One of your fine gentlemen he was!", "\"도련님 식으로 치면 점잖은 신사 나리 가운데 하나였지요!"], ["I was very meek and umble--and I am.", "저야 더없이 온순하고 미천했고요 — 지금도 그렇습니다만."], ["But I didn’t like that sort of thing--and I don’t!’", "한데 저는 그런 짓거리가 싫었어요 — 지금도 싫고요!\""]], [["He left off scraping his chin, and sucked in his cheeks until they seemed to meet inside; keeping his sidelong glance upon me all the while.", "그는 턱 긁기를 멈추고 두 볼을 안으로 빨아들였는데, 그러자 볼이 입 안에서 맞닿을 것만 같았다. 그러면서도 줄곧 곁눈으로 나를 살폈다."]], [["‘She is one of your lovely women, she is,’ he pursued, when he had slowly restored his face to its natural form; ‘and ready to be no friend to such as me, I know.", "\"그 부인은 도련님 식으로 치면 어여쁜 여인네 가운데 하나지요, 정말요.\" 그가 천천히 얼굴을 본디 모양으로 되돌려 놓고 나서 말을 이었다. \"그리고 저 같은 인간한테는 벗이 되어 줄 마음이 조금도 없는 분이고요, 그건 제가 압니다."], ["She’s just the person as would put my Agnes up to higher sort of game.", "우리 애그니스를 더 높은 부류의 놀음판에 끌어들일 만한 바로 그런 분이에요."], ["Now, I ain’t one of your lady’s men, Master Copperfield; but I’ve had eyes in my ed, a pretty long time back.", "자, 저는 여자한테 살가운 부류가 아닙니다, 코퍼필드 도련님. 하지만 저는 꽤 오래전부터 머리에 눈깔이 박혀 있었거든요."], ["We umble ones have got eyes, mostly speaking--and we look out of ‘em.’", "우리 같은 미천한 것들도 대개는 눈이 있고요 — 그 눈으로 살핍니다.\""]], [["I endeavoured to appear unconscious and not disquieted, but, I saw in his face, with poor success.", "나는 아무것도 모르는 척, 마음이 흔들리지 않은 척 보이려고 애썼지만, 그의 얼굴을 보니 그 노력이 영 신통치 못했음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["‘Now, I’m not a-going to let myself be run down, Copperfield,’ he continued, raising that part of his countenance, where his red eyebrows would have been if he had had any, with malignant triumph, ‘and I shall do what I can to put a stop to this friendship.", "\"자, 저는 짓밟히고 가만있을 생각이 없어요, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 붉은 눈썹이 있었다면 그 자리였을 부분을 악의에 찬 의기양양함으로 치켜올리며 이어 갔다. \"그래서 저는 이 우정을 막으려고 제가 할 수 있는 일을 할 겁니다."], ["I don’t approve of it. I don’t mind acknowledging to you that I’ve got rather a grudging disposition, and want to keep off all intruders.", "저는 이걸 못마땅하게 여기거든요. 솔직히 말씀드려, 저는 좀 인색하게 따지는 성미라서, 끼어드는 자들은 죄다 떼어 놓고 싶어 한다는 걸 인정하는 데 거리낌이 없습니다."], ["I ain’t a-going, if I know it, to run the risk of being plotted against.’", "제가 아는 한, 저는 음모에 당할 위험을 무릅쓸 생각이 없어요.\""]], [["‘You are always plotting, and delude yourself into the belief that everybody else is doing the like, I think,’ said I.", "\"내 보기엔, 너는 늘 음모를 꾸미면서, 남들도 다 그러려니 하고 스스로를 속이는 것 같구나.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps so, Master Copperfield,’ he replied.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요, 코퍼필드 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘But I’ve got a motive, as my fellow-partner used to say; and I go at it tooth and nail.", "\"하지만 제 동업자 양반 말마따나, 저한테는 동기가 있고, 저는 그걸 이를 악물고 손톱이 빠지도록 파고듭니다."], ["I mustn’t be put upon, as a numble person, too much.", "저는 미천한 인간이라고 너무 함부로 당해서는 안 되거든요."], ["I can’t allow people in my way.", "제 앞길에 사람들이 가로막고 서게 둘 수는 없어요."], ["Really they must come out of the cart, Master Copperfield!’", "정말이지 그 사람들은 수레에서 내려와야 합니다, 코퍼필드 도련님!\""]], [["‘I don’t understand you,’ said I.", "\"무슨 소린지 모르겠다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t you, though?’ he returned, with one of his jerks.", "\"정말 모르시겠어요?\" 그가 특유의 움찔하는 몸짓을 하며 받았다."], ["‘I’m astonished at that, Master Copperfield, you being usually so quick!", "\"그것참 놀랍네요, 코퍼필드 도련님, 늘 그렇게 빠르신 분이!"], ["I’ll try to be plainer, another time.---Is that Mr. Maldon a-norseback, ringing at the gate, sir?’", "다음번엔 좀 더 알아듣기 쉽게 해 보지요. — 저기 문에서 종을 울리고 있는 게 말 위에 앉은 몰던 씨 아닌가요, 나리?\""]], [["‘It looks like him,’ I replied, as carelessly as I could.", "\"그 사람 같군.\" 나는 되도록 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["Uriah stopped short, put his hands between his great knobs of knees, and doubled himself up with laughter.", "유라이어는 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 두 손을 커다란 혹 같은 무릎 사이에 끼우고 몸을 둘로 접듯 구부려 웃어 댔다."], ["With perfectly silent laughter.", "완벽하게 소리 없는 웃음이었다."], ["Not a sound escaped from him. I was so repelled by his odious behaviour, particularly by this concluding instance, that I turned away without any ceremony; and left him doubled up in the middle of the garden, like a scarecrow in want of support.", "그에게서는 단 한 가지 소리도 새어 나오지 않았다. 나는 그의 역겨운 행태에, 특히 이 마지막 꼴에 어찌나 진저리가 났던지, 인사치레도 없이 돌아서서, 받쳐 줄 것이 없어 휘청거리는 허수아비처럼 정원 한복판에 몸을 접고 선 그를 그대로 두고 와 버렸다."]], [["It was not on that evening; but, as I well remember, on the next evening but one, which was a Sunday; that I took Agnes to see Dora.", "내가 애그니스를 데리고 도라를 보러 간 것은 그날 저녁이 아니라, 내 기억으로는 그다음다음 날 저녁, 그러니까 일요일이었다."], ["I had arranged the visit, beforehand, with Miss Lavinia; and Agnes was expected to tea.", "나는 미리 라비니아 양과 그 방문을 의논해 두었고, 애그니스는 차 마실 시간에 오기로 되어 있었다."]], [["I was in a flutter of pride and anxiety; pride in my dear little betrothed, and anxiety that Agnes should like her.", "나는 자랑스러움과 불안으로 마음이 들떠 있었다. 내 사랑스러운 어린 약혼녀가 자랑스러웠고, 애그니스가 그 애를 마음에 들어 했으면 하는 불안이 있었다."], ["All the way to Putney, Agnes being inside the stage-coach, and I outside, I pictured Dora to myself in every one of the pretty looks I knew so well; now making up my mind that I should like her to look exactly as she looked at such a time, and then doubting whether I should not prefer her looking as she looked at such another time; and almost worrying myself into a fever about it.", "퍼트니로 가는 내내, 애그니스는 역마차 안에 타고 나는 바깥에 탄 채로, 나는 내가 너무도 잘 아는 도라의 그 어여쁜 표정 하나하나를 마음속에 그려 보았다. 어떤 때는 그 애가 꼭 이런 때에 짓던 그 표정 그대로였으면 좋겠다고 마음을 정했다가, 또 어떤 때는 저런 다른 때에 짓던 표정이 더 낫지 않을까 망설였다. 그러다 거의 그 일로 안달이 나서 열병에 걸릴 지경이 되었다."]], [["I was troubled by no doubt of her being very pretty, in any case; but it fell out that I had never seen her look so well.", "어쨌거나 그 애가 무척 예쁘리라는 데는 조금도 의심이 없었다. 그런데 마침 그날 나는 그 애가 그토록 고와 보인 적이 없는 모습을 보게 되었다."], ["She was not in the drawing-room when I presented Agnes to her little aunts, but was shyly keeping out of the way.", "내가 그 작은 고모들에게 애그니스를 인사시킬 때 그 애는 응접실에 없었고, 수줍게 자리를 피하고 있었다."], ["I knew where to look for her, now; and sure enough I found her stopping her ears again, behind the same dull old door.", "이제 나는 그 애를 어디서 찾아야 할지 알았다. 아니나 다를까, 그 애는 여전히 그 칙칙하고 낡은 문 뒤에서 또 두 귀를 틀어막고 있었다."]], [["At first she wouldn’t come at all; and then she pleaded for five minutes by my watch.", "처음에 그 애는 도무지 나오려 하지 않았다. 그러더니 내 시계로 오 분만 더 있게 해 달라고 사정했다."], ["When at length she put her arm through mine, to be taken to the drawing-room, her charming little face was flushed, and had never been so pretty.", "마침내 그 애가 응접실로 데려가 달라며 내 팔에 제 팔을 끼웠을 때, 그 매력적인 작은 얼굴은 발그레 상기되어 그토록 예뻐 보인 적이 없었다."], ["But, when we went into the room, and it turned pale, she was ten thousand times prettier yet.", "그런데 우리가 방에 들어서면서 그 얼굴이 새파랗게 질리자, 그 애는 그보다 만 배는 더 예뻤다."]], [["Dora was afraid of Agnes.", "도라는 애그니스를 두려워했다."], ["She had told me that she knew Agnes was ‘too clever’.", "그 애는 애그니스가 '너무 똑똑하다'는 걸 알고 있다고 나한테 말한 적이 있었다."], ["But when she saw her looking at once so cheerful and so earnest, and so thoughtful, and so good, she gave a faint little cry of pleased surprise, and just put her affectionate arms round Agnes’s neck, and laid her innocent cheek against her face.", "하지만 애그니스가 그토록 환하면서도 그토록 진지하고, 그토록 사려 깊고, 그토록 착하게 자기를 바라보는 모습을 보자, 그 애는 반가운 놀라움에 가냘프게 작은 소리를 내지르고는, 다정한 두 팔을 애그니스의 목에 두르고, 그 순진한 뺨을 애그니스의 얼굴에 갖다 댔다."]], [["I never was so happy. I never was so pleased as when I saw those two sit down together, side by side.", "나는 그토록 행복한 적이 없었다. 그 둘이 나란히 함께 앉는 모습을 보았을 때만큼 기뻤던 적이 없었다."], ["As when I saw my little darling looking up so naturally to those cordial eyes.", "내 어린 연인이 그 다정한 두 눈을 그토록 자연스럽게 올려다보는 모습을 보았을 때만큼."], ["As when I saw the tender, beautiful regard which Agnes cast upon her.", "애그니스가 그 애에게 보내는 그 부드럽고 아름다운 눈길을 보았을 때만큼."]], [["Miss Lavinia and Miss Clarissa partook, in their way, of my joy.", "라비니아 양과 클라리사 양도 그들 나름의 방식으로 내 기쁨을 함께 나누었다."], ["It was the pleasantest tea-table in the world. Miss Clarissa presided.", "세상에서 가장 즐거운 찻상이었다. 클라리사 양이 자리를 주관했다."], ["I cut and handed the sweet seed-cake--the little sisters had a bird-like fondness for picking up seeds and pecking at sugar; Miss Lavinia looked on with benignant patronage, as if our happy love were all her work; and we were perfectly contented with ourselves and one another.", "내가 캐러웨이 씨앗 케이크를 잘라 돌렸다 — 그 작은 자매는 새처럼 씨앗을 쪼아 먹고 설탕을 콕콕 찍어 먹는 걸 좋아했다. 라비니아 양은 마치 우리의 행복한 사랑이 모두 제 솜씨이기라도 한 양 인자하게 후원하는 눈길로 바라보았고, 우리는 우리 자신에게도 서로에게도 더없이 흡족했다."]], [["The gentle cheerfulness of Agnes went to all their hearts.", "애그니스의 부드러운 쾌활함은 그들 모두의 마음을 사로잡았다."], ["Her quiet interest in everything that interested Dora; her manner of making acquaintance with Jip (who responded instantly); her pleasant way, when Dora was ashamed to come over to her usual seat by me; her modest grace and ease, eliciting a crowd of blushing little marks of confidence from Dora; seemed to make our circle quite complete.", "도라가 관심을 갖는 모든 것에 보이는 조용한 관심, 집(곧바로 화답한)과 친해지는 방식, 도라가 부끄러워 늘 앉던 내 옆자리로 건너오지 못할 때 보여 준 그 상냥한 솜씨, 도라에게서 발그레한 신뢰의 작은 표시를 한 무더기나 끌어낸 그 수수한 우아함과 편안함 — 그 모든 것이 우리의 모임을 더없이 완전하게 만들어 주는 듯했다."]], [["‘I am so glad,’ said Dora, after tea, ‘that you like me.", "\"정말 기뻐요.\" 차를 마시고 나서 도라가 말했다. \"절 좋아해 주셔서요."], ["I didn’t think you would; and I want, more than ever, to be liked, now Julia Mills is gone.’", "그러실 줄 몰랐거든요. 그리고 줄리아 밀스도 떠나 버린 지금, 저는 그 어느 때보다도 사랑받고 싶어요.\""]], [["I have omitted to mention it, by the by.", "그러고 보니 이걸 미처 말하지 못했다."], ["Miss Mills had sailed, and Dora and I had gone aboard a great East Indiaman at Gravesend to see her; and we had had preserved ginger, and guava, and other delicacies of that sort for lunch; and we had left Miss Mills weeping on a camp-stool on the quarter-deck, with a large new diary under her arm, in which the original reflections awakened by the contemplation of Ocean were to be recorded under lock and key.", "밀스 양은 배를 타고 떠나 버렸고, 도라와 나는 그레이브젠드까지 가서 커다란 동인도 무역선에 올라 그녀를 배웅했다. 우리는 점심으로 설탕에 절인 생강과 구아바, 그 밖의 그런 진미들을 먹었다. 그리고 우리는 후갑판 위 접의자에 앉아 우는 밀스 양을 두고 왔는데, 그녀는 커다란 새 일기장을 옆구리에 끼고 있었고, 거기에다 대양을 바라보며 떠오른 독창적인 사색들을 자물쇠로 채워 적어 둘 작정이었다."]], [["Agnes said she was afraid I must have given her an unpromising character; but Dora corrected that directly.", "애그니스는 내가 자기를 별 볼 일 없는 사람으로 소개했나 보다며 걱정스러워했지만, 도라가 곧장 바로잡았다."]], [["‘Oh no!’ she said, shaking her curls at me; ‘it was all praise.", "\"어머, 아니에요!\" 그 애가 나를 향해 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다. \"전부 칭찬이었어요."], ["He thinks so much of your opinion, that I was quite afraid of it.’", "이이가 언니 의견을 어찌나 대단하게 여기는지, 저는 그게 다 무서울 정도였다니까요.\""]], [["‘My good opinion cannot strengthen his attachment to some people whom he knows,’ said Agnes, with a smile; ‘it is not worth their having.’", "\"제 좋은 평가가, 그분이 아는 어떤 사람들에 대한 그분의 애정을 더 굳게 해 줄 수는 없어요.\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그 사람들이 받을 만한 가치도 없는 거고요.\""]], [["‘But please let me have it,’ said Dora, in her coaxing way, ‘if you can!’", "\"하지만 부디 저한테는 주세요.\" 도라가 어리광 부리는 투로 말했다. \"주실 수 있다면요!\""]], [["We made merry about Dora’s wanting to be liked, and Dora said I was a goose, and she didn’t like me at any rate, and the short evening flew away on gossamer-wings.", "우리는 도라가 사랑받고 싶어 한다며 즐겁게 웃었고, 도라는 내가 멍청이라고, 어쨌거나 자기는 날 좋아하지 않는다고 했다. 그렇게 짧은 저녁이 거미줄 같은 날개를 달고 훌훌 날아가 버렸다."], ["The time was at hand when the coach was to call for us.", "마차가 우리를 데리러 올 시각이 가까워지고 있었다."], ["I was standing alone before the fire, when Dora came stealing softly in, to give me that usual precious little kiss before I went.", "내가 난로 앞에 혼자 서 있는데, 도라가 내가 떠나기 전에 늘 주던 그 소중한 작은 입맞춤을 해 주려고 살그머니 들어왔다."]], [["‘Don’t you think, if I had had her for a friend a long time ago, Doady,’ said Dora, her bright eyes shining very brightly, and her little right hand idly busying itself with one of the buttons of my coat, ‘I might have been more clever perhaps?’", "\"있잖아요, 도디, 내가 오래전부터 애그니스 언니를 벗으로 두었더라면,\" 도라가 그 반짝이는 눈을 유난히 환하게 빛내며, 작은 오른손으로 내 외투 단추 하나를 무심히 만지작거리며 말했다. \"어쩌면 내가 좀 더 똑똑해졌을까요?\""]], [["‘My love!’ said I, ‘what nonsense!’", "\"내 사랑!\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리야!\""]], [["‘Do you think it is nonsense?’ returned Dora, without looking at me.", "\"그게 말도 안 되는 소리 같아요?\" 도라가 나를 보지도 않고 되물었다."], ["‘Are you sure it is?’", "\"정말 그래요?\" \"당연하지!\""]], [["‘Of course I am!’ ‘I have forgotten,’ said Dora, still turning the button round and round, ‘what relation Agnes is to you, you dear bad boy.’", "\"잊어버렸는데,\" 여전히 단추를 빙글빙글 돌리며 도라가 말했다. \"애그니스 언니가 당신하고 무슨 사이라고 했죠, 이 사랑스럽고 못된 사람?\""]], [["‘No blood-relation,’ I replied; ‘but we were brought up together, like brother and sister.’", "\"피붙이는 아니야.\" 내가 대답했다. \"하지만 우리는 오누이처럼 함께 자랐어.\""]], [["‘I wonder why you ever fell in love with me?’ said Dora, beginning on another button of my coat.", "\"당신은 대체 왜 나 같은 사람하고 사랑에 빠졌을까?\" 도라가 내 외투의 또 다른 단추를 만지작거리기 시작하며 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps because I couldn’t see you, and not love you, Dora!’", "\"어쩌면 너를 보고도 사랑하지 않을 수가 없었기 때문이겠지, 도라!\""]], [["‘Suppose you had never seen me at all,’ said Dora, going to another button.", "\"만약에 당신이 나를 아예 본 적이 없었더라면요.\" 도라가 또 다른 단추로 옮겨 가며 말했다."]], [["‘Suppose we had never been born!’ said I, gaily.", "\"만약에 우리가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면!\" 내가 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["I wondered what she was thinking about, as I glanced in admiring silence at the little soft hand travelling up the row of buttons on my coat, and at the clustering hair that lay against my breast, and at the lashes of her downcast eyes, slightly rising as they followed her idle fingers.", "내 외투 단추 줄을 따라 올라오는 그 작고 보드라운 손과, 내 가슴께에 닿아 늘어진 송이송이 머리채와, 그 무심한 손가락을 따라 살짝 들리는 내리깐 눈의 속눈썹을, 나는 감탄에 잠겨 말없이 바라보며, 그 애가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 궁금해했다."], ["At length her eyes were lifted up to mine, and she stood on tiptoe to give me, more thoughtfully than usual, that precious little kiss--once, twice, three times--and went out of the room.", "마침내 그 애가 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보더니, 발끝으로 서서 평소보다 더 사뭇 생각에 잠긴 듯 그 소중한 작은 입맞춤을 해 주고는 — 한 번, 두 번, 세 번 — 방을 나갔다."]], [["They all came back together within five minutes afterwards, and Dora’s unusual thoughtfulness was quite gone then.", "그러고 나서 오 분도 안 되어 그들 모두가 함께 돌아왔는데, 그때쯤 도라의 그 평소답지 않던 사색은 말끔히 가셔 있었다."], ["She was laughingly resolved to put Jip through the whole of his performances, before the coach came.", "그 애는 마차가 오기 전에 집에게 온갖 재주를 다 부리게 하겠다며 웃음 띤 얼굴로 단단히 마음먹고 있었다."], ["They took some time (not so much on account of their variety, as Jip’s reluctance), and were still unfinished when it was heard at the door.", "그것은 시간이 좀 걸렸는데(재주가 다양해서라기보다는 집이 영 내키지 않아 해서였다), 문간에서 마차 소리가 들렸을 때도 아직 끝나지 않은 채였다."], ["There was a hurried but affectionate parting between Agnes and herself; and Dora was to write to Agnes (who was not to mind her letters being foolish, she said), and Agnes was to write to Dora; and they had a second parting at the coach door, and a third when Dora, in spite of the remonstrances of Miss Lavinia, would come running out once more to remind Agnes at the coach window about writing, and to shake her curls at me on the box.", "애그니스와 그 애 사이에 황급하지만 다정한 작별이 있었다. 도라는 애그니스에게 편지를 쓰기로 했고(자기 편지가 어리석어도 신경 쓰지 말라고 했다), 애그니스는 도라에게 편지를 쓰기로 했다. 그러고 둘은 마차 문 앞에서 두 번째로 작별했고, 도라가 라비니아 양의 만류에도 아랑곳없이 한 번 더 뛰어나와 마차 창 너머로 애그니스에게 편지 쓰는 걸 거듭 당부하고, 마부석에 앉은 나를 향해 곱슬머리를 흔들 때 세 번째로 작별했다."]], [["The stage-coach was to put us down near Covent Garden, where we were to take another stage-coach for Highgate.", "역마차는 우리를 코번트 가든 근처에 내려놓기로 되어 있었고, 거기서 우리는 하이게이트로 가는 다른 역마차로 갈아탈 참이었다."], ["I was impatient for the short walk in the interval, that Agnes might praise Dora to me. Ah!", "나는 그 사이의 짧은 도보길이 못 견디게 기다려졌으니, 애그니스가 나에게 도라를 칭찬해 줄 수 있을 터였기 때문이다."], ["what praise it was! How lovingly and fervently did it commend the pretty creature I had won, with all her artless graces best displayed, to my most gentle care!", "아, 그것은 얼마나 큰 칭찬이었던가! 그 애의 온갖 꾸밈없는 우아함이 가장 잘 드러난 채로, 내가 얻은 그 어여쁜 아이를 내가 더없이 부드럽게 보살피도록, 그 칭찬은 얼마나 사랑스럽고 뜨겁게 당부해 주었던가!"], ["How thoughtfully remind me, yet with no pretence of doing so, of the trust in which I held the orphan child!", "그러면서도 짐짓 그러는 티는 조금도 내지 않으면서, 내가 그 고아 같은 아이를 맡아 지키고 있다는 그 신성한 책임을 얼마나 사려 깊게 일깨워 주었던가!"]], [["Never, never, had I loved Dora so deeply and truly, as I loved her that night.", "나는 그 어느 때도, 그날 밤 도라를 사랑한 것만큼 그토록 깊고 참되게 그 애를 사랑한 적이 없었다."], ["When we had again alighted, and were walking in the starlight along the quiet road that led to the Doctor’s house, I told Agnes it was her doing.", "우리가 다시 마차에서 내려, 박사님 댁으로 이어지는 고요한 길을 별빛 아래 걷고 있을 때, 나는 애그니스에게 이게 다 네 덕분이라고 말했다."]], [["‘When you were sitting by her,’ said I, ‘you seemed to be no less her guardian angel than mine; and you seem so now, Agnes.’", "\"네가 그 애 곁에 앉아 있을 때,\" 내가 말했다. \"너는 내 수호천사이기도 한 만큼이나 그 애의 수호천사이기도 한 것 같았어. 지금도 그렇게 보여, 애그니스.\""]], [["‘A poor angel,’ she returned, ‘but faithful.’", "\"보잘것없는 천사지.\" 애그니스가 받았다. \"하지만 진실한 천사야.\""]], [["The clear tone of her voice, going straight to my heart, made it natural to me to say:", "내 가슴속으로 곧장 파고드는 그 맑은 목소리에, 나는 자연스레 이렇게 말하게 되었다."]], [["‘The cheerfulness that belongs to you, Agnes (and to no one else that ever I have seen), is so restored, I have observed today, that I have begun to hope you are happier at home?’", "\"너한테만 있는 그 쾌활함이 — 내가 여태 본 그 누구에게도 없는 그 쾌활함이 — 오늘 보니 너무도 되살아나 있어서, 나는 네가 집에서 더 행복해진 게 아닐까 하는 희망을 품기 시작했어.\""]], [["‘I am happier in myself,’ she said; ‘I am quite cheerful and light-hearted.’", "\"내 안에서 더 행복해.\" 그 애가 말했다. \"나는 아주 명랑하고 마음이 가벼워.\""]], [["I glanced at the serene face looking upward, and thought it was the stars that made it seem so noble.", "나는 위를 올려다보는 그 평온한 얼굴을 흘끗 보았고, 그 얼굴을 그토록 고귀해 보이게 한 것은 별빛이라고 생각했다."]], [["‘There has been no change at home,’ said Agnes, after a few moments.", "\"집에는 아무 변화도 없었어.\" 잠시 뒤 애그니스가 말했다."]], [["‘No fresh reference,’ said I, ‘to--I wouldn’t distress you, Agnes, but I cannot help asking--to what we spoke of, when we parted last?’", "\"새삼스레 다시 들먹인 일은 없었고?\" 내가 말했다. \"— 너를 괴롭히고 싶진 않지만, 묻지 않을 수가 없구나 — 우리가 지난번 헤어질 때 이야기했던 그 일 말이야.\""]], [["‘No, none,’ she answered.", "\"응, 없었어.\" 그 애가 대답했다."]], [["‘I have thought so much about it.’", "\"나는 그 일을 정말 많이 생각했어.\""]], [["‘You must think less about it.", "\"그 생각은 좀 덜 해야 해."], ["Remember that I confide in simple love and truth at last.", "내가 결국에는 수수한 사랑과 진실을 믿고 의지한다는 걸 기억해 줘."], ["Have no apprehensions for me, Trotwood,’ she added, after a moment; ‘the step you dread my taking, I shall never take.’", "나 때문에 걱정하지 마, 트롯우드.\" 잠시 뒤 그 애가 덧붙였다. \"네가 내가 밟을까 봐 두려워하는 그 발걸음을, 나는 결코 내딛지 않을 거야.\""]], [["Although I think I had never really feared it, in any season of cool reflection, it was an unspeakable relief to me to have this assurance from her own truthful lips. I told her so, earnestly.", "차분히 곰곰 따져 보던 어느 때든 내가 정말로 그것을 두려워한 적은 없었다고 생각하지만, 그래도 그 애의 진실한 입에서 직접 이 다짐을 듣고 나니 말할 수 없는 안도가 밀려왔다. 나는 그 마음을 진심으로 그 애에게 털어놓았다."]], [["‘And when this visit is over,’ said I,--‘for we may not be alone another time,--how long is it likely to be, my dear Agnes, before you come to London again?’", "\"그런데 이 방문이 끝나면,\" 내가 말했다. \"— 다시 단둘이 있을 기회가 없을지도 모르니까 — 애그니스, 네가 다시 런던에 오기까지는 얼마나 걸릴 것 같아?\""]], [["‘Probably a long time,’ she replied; ‘I think it will be best--for papa’s sake--to remain at home.", "\"아마 한참 걸릴 거야.\" 그 애가 대답했다. \"내 생각엔 — 아버지를 위해서는 — 집에 있는 게 가장 좋을 것 같아."], ["We are not likely to meet often, for some time to come; but I shall be a good correspondent of Dora’s, and we shall frequently hear of one another that way.’", "앞으로 한동안은 자주 못 만나겠지. 하지만 나는 도라하고 부지런히 편지를 주고받을 거고, 그렇게 해서 우리는 서로 소식을 자주 듣게 될 거야.\""]], [["We were now within the little courtyard of the Doctor’s cottage.", "우리는 이제 박사님 오두막의 작은 안뜰 안에 들어와 있었다."], ["It was growing late.", "시각이 늦어 가고 있었다."], ["There was a light in the window of Mrs. Strong’s chamber, and Agnes, pointing to it, bade me good night.", "스트롱 부인 방 창문에 불이 켜져 있었고, 애그니스는 그쪽을 가리키며 내게 잘 자라고 인사했다."]], [["‘Do not be troubled,’ she said, giving me her hand, ‘by our misfortunes and anxieties.", "\"우리 불행이나 근심 때문에 마음 쓰지 마.\" 그 애가 내게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["I can be happier in nothing than in your happiness.", "\"나는 네 행복보다 더 큰 행복을 어디서도 못 느껴."], ["If you can ever give me help, rely upon it I will ask you for it.", "혹시라도 네가 나를 도와줄 수 있을 때가 오면, 틀림없이 내가 너한테 부탁할 테니 믿어 줘."], ["God bless you always!’ In her beaming smile, and in these last tones of her cheerful voice, I seemed again to see and hear my little Dora in her company.", "늘 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\" 그 애의 환한 미소 속에서, 그리고 그 명랑한 목소리의 마지막 가락 속에서, 나는 그 애와 함께 있는 내 어린 도라를 다시 보고 듣는 것만 같았다."], ["I stood awhile, looking through the porch at the stars, with a heart full of love and gratitude, and then walked slowly forth.", "나는 사랑과 고마움으로 가슴이 벅찬 채, 한동안 현관 처마 사이로 별들을 바라보며 서 있다가, 천천히 걸어 나왔다."], ["I had engaged a bed at a decent alehouse close by, and was going out at the gate, when, happening to turn my head, I saw a light in the Doctor’s study.", "나는 가까운 점잖은 술집에 잠자리를 잡아 두었고, 막 대문을 나서던 참이었는데, 우연히 고개를 돌렸다가 박사님 서재에 불이 켜진 것을 보았다."], ["A half-reproachful fancy came into my mind, that he had been working at the Dictionary without my help.", "내 도움 없이 박사님이 사전 작업을 하고 계셨던 게 아닐까 하는, 절반쯤 자책 어린 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["With the view of seeing if this were so, and, in any case, of bidding him good night, if he were yet sitting among his books, I turned back, and going softly across the hall, and gently opening the door, looked in.", "정말 그런지 살펴볼 겸, 어쨌든 박사님이 아직 책들 사이에 앉아 계시다면 잘 주무시라는 인사라도 드릴 겸, 나는 발길을 돌려 살그머니 현관을 가로질러 문을 가만히 열고 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["The first person whom I saw, to my surprise, by the sober light of the shaded lamp, was Uriah.", "갓을 씌운 등불의 은은한 빛 속에서 내가 맨 먼저 본 사람은, 놀랍게도 유라이어였다."], ["He was standing close beside it, with one of his skeleton hands over his mouth, and the other resting on the Doctor’s table.", "그는 그 등불 바로 곁에 서서, 해골 같은 한 손을 입에 갖다 대고, 다른 손은 박사님 책상 위에 얹은 채였다."], ["The Doctor sat in his study chair, covering his face with his hands. Mr.", "박사님은 서재 의자에 앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 계셨다."], ["Wickfield, sorely troubled and distressed, was leaning forward, irresolutely touching the Doctor’s arm.", "위크필드 씨는 몹시 괴롭고 비통한 얼굴로, 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채 머뭇거리며 박사님 팔을 만지고 있었다."]], [["For an instant, I supposed that the Doctor was ill.", "순간 나는 박사님이 편찮으신 줄로만 알았다."], ["I hastily advanced a step under that impression, when I met Uriah’s eye, and saw what was the matter.", "그런 생각에 황급히 한 걸음 나아갔다가, 유라이어의 눈과 마주치고서야 무슨 일인지 알아챘다."], ["I would have withdrawn, but the Doctor made a gesture to detain me, and I remained.", "나는 물러나려 했지만, 박사님이 나를 붙잡으려는 몸짓을 하셔서 그대로 남았다."]], [["‘At any rate,’ observed Uriah, with a writhe of his ungainly person, ‘we may keep the door shut.", "\"어쨌든,\" 유라이어가 그 볼썽사나운 몸을 한 번 비틀며 말했다. \"문은 닫아 두는 게 좋겠지요."], ["We needn’t make it known to ALL the town.’", "온 동네에 다 알릴 필요는 없으니까요.\""]], [["Saying which, he went on his toes to the door, which I had left open, and carefully closed it.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 내가 열어 둔 문으로 발끝을 세워 다가가 조심스레 닫았다."], ["He then came back, and took up his former position.", "그러고는 다시 돌아와 아까의 자리에 섰다."], ["There was an obtrusive show of compassionate zeal in his voice and manner, more intolerable--at least to me--than any demeanour he could have assumed.", "그의 목소리와 태도에는 동정하는 척하는 열성이 거슬리게 드러나 있었는데, 그것은 그가 꾸며 보일 수 있는 그 어떤 태도보다도 — 적어도 나에게는 — 더 견디기 어려웠다."]], [["‘I have felt it incumbent upon me, Master Copperfield,’ said Uriah, ‘to point out to Doctor Strong what you and me have already talked about.", "\"저는 마땅히 그래야 할 것 같아서요, 코퍼필드 도련님,\" 유라이어가 말했다. \"도련님과 제가 이미 이야기 나눴던 그 일을 스트롱 박사님께 짚어 드렸습니다."], ["You didn’t exactly understand me, though?’", "그런데 도련님은 제 말뜻을 제대로 못 알아들으셨지요?\""]], [["I gave him a look, but no other answer; and, going to my good old master, said a few words that I meant to be words of comfort and encouragement.", "나는 그를 한 번 노려보았을 뿐 다른 대답은 하지 않았다. 그리고 나의 인자하신 옛 스승님께 다가가, 위로와 격려가 되었으면 하는 마음으로 몇 마디 말씀을 드렸다."], ["He put his hand upon my shoulder, as it had been his custom to do when I was quite a little fellow, but did not lift his grey head.", "박사님은 내가 아주 어린 꼬마였을 적에 늘 그러시던 대로 내 어깨에 손을 얹으셨지만, 그 백발 머리는 들지 않으셨다."]], [["‘As you didn’t understand me, Master Copperfield,’ resumed Uriah in the same officious manner, ‘I may take the liberty of umbly mentioning, being among friends, that I have called Doctor Strong’s attention to the goings-on of Mrs.", "\"도련님이 제 말뜻을 못 알아들으셨으니,\" 유라이어가 똑같이 주제넘은 태도로 말을 이었다. \"이렇게 벗들끼리 모인 자리에서 외람되지만 미천하게나마 말씀드리자면, 제가 스트롱 박사님께 스트롱 부인의 행실에 대해 주의를 환기해 드렸습니다."], ["Strong. It’s much against the grain with me, I assure you, Copperfield, to be concerned in anything so unpleasant; but really, as it is, we’re all mixing ourselves up with what oughtn’t to be.", "정말이지, 코퍼필드, 이렇게 불쾌한 일에 끼어드는 게 저로서는 무척 내키지 않는다는 걸 알아주세요. 하지만 사실 이대로 가다가는 우리 모두가 있어서는 안 될 일에 얽혀 들게 되거든요."], ["That was what my meaning was, sir, when you didn’t understand me.’ I wonder now, when I recall his leer, that I did not collar him, and try to shake the breath out of his body.", "도련님이 제 말뜻을 못 알아들으셨을 때, 제가 하려던 말이 바로 그거였습니다, 나리.\" 지금 그의 곁눈질을 떠올리면, 그때 내가 그의 멱살을 잡고 숨이 끊어지도록 흔들어 대지 않은 것이 의아할 지경이다."]], [["‘I dare say I didn’t make myself very clear,’ he went on, ‘nor you neither.", "\"제가 제 뜻을 그리 분명히 밝히지 못했나 봅니다.\" 그가 말을 이어 갔다."], ["Naturally, we was both of us inclined to give such a subject a wide berth.", "\"도련님도 그러셨고요. 하긴 그런 화제는 우리 둘 다 멀찍이 피하고 싶었을 테지요."], ["Hows’ever, at last I have made up my mind to speak plain; and I have mentioned to Doctor Strong that--did you speak, sir?’", "한데 결국 저는 터놓고 말하기로 마음을 굳혔고, 스트롱 박사님께 말씀드렸습니다 — 뭐라고 하셨나요, 나리?\""]], [["This was to the Doctor, who had moaned.", "이것은 신음 소리를 내신 박사님을 향한 말이었다."], ["The sound might have touched any heart, I thought, but it had no effect upon Uriah’s.", "그 소리는 어떤 사람의 가슴이든 다 울렸을 것 같았지만, 유라이어의 가슴에는 아무 영향도 미치지 못했다."]], [["‘--mentioned to Doctor Strong,’ he proceeded, ‘that anyone may see that Mr.", "\"— 스트롱 박사님께 말씀드렸습니다.\" 그가 계속했다."], ["Maldon, and the lovely and agreeable lady as is Doctor Strong’s wife, are too sweet on one another.", "\"몰던 씨와, 스트롱 박사님 부인이신 그 어여쁘고 상냥한 부인이 서로에게 너무 다정하다는 건 누구든 알아볼 수 있다고요."], ["Really the time is come (we being at present all mixing ourselves up with what oughtn’t to be), when Doctor Strong must be told that this was full as plain to everybody as the sun, before Mr.", "정말이지 이제는 (지금 우리 모두가 있어서는 안 될 일에 얽혀 들고 있으니) 스트롱 박사님께 말씀드려야 할 때입니다, 몰던 씨가 인도로 떠나기 전에 이미 이 일이 햇빛처럼 누구에게나 훤히 보였다는 것을요."], ["Maldon went to India; that Mr. Maldon made excuses to come back, for nothing else; and that he’s always here, for nothing else.", "몰던 씨가 다른 까닭은 전혀 없이 오로지 그것 때문에 핑계를 만들어 돌아왔다는 것을요. 그리고 그가 다른 까닭 없이 오로지 그것 때문에 늘 여기 와 있다는 것을요."], ["When you come in, sir, I was just putting it to my fellow-partner,’ towards whom he turned, ‘to say to Doctor Strong upon his word and honour, whether he’d ever been of this opinion long ago, or not.", "나리께서 들어오셨을 때, 저는 마침 제 동업자 양반한테,\" 그가 그쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"스트롱 박사님께 자기 명예와 양심을 걸고, 오래전부터 정말 이런 의견을 품어 왔는지 아닌지 말씀드려 보라고 청하던 참이었습니다."], ["Come, Mr. Wickfield, sir!", "자, 위크필드 씨, 나리!"], ["Would you be so good as tell us? Yes or no, sir?", "저희한테 말씀해 주시겠습니까? 네입니까, 아니요입니까, 나리?"], ["Come, partner!’", "자, 동업자 양반!\""]], [["‘For God’s sake, my dear Doctor,’ said Mr.", "\"제발 부탁이오, 박사님,\""], ["Wickfield again laying his irresolute hand upon the Doctor’s arm, ‘don’t attach too much weight to any suspicions I may have entertained.’", "위크필드 씨가 다시 그 머뭇거리는 손을 박사님 팔에 얹으며 말했다. \"내가 품었을지도 모를 어떤 의심에도 너무 무게를 두지 마시오.\""]], [["‘There!’ cried Uriah, shaking his head.", "\"저것 보십시오!\" 유라이어가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 외쳤다."], ["‘What a melancholy confirmation: ain’t it? Him!", "\"얼마나 서글픈 확증입니까, 안 그래요? 저 양반이요!"], ["Such an old friend!", "저런 오랜 벗이!"], ["Bless your soul, when I was nothing but a clerk in his office, Copperfield, I’ve seen him twenty times, if I’ve seen him once, quite in a taking about it--quite put out, you know (and very proper in him as a father; I’m sure I can’t blame him), to think that Miss Agnes was mixing herself up with what oughtn’t to be.’", "맙소사, 제가 한낱 저분 사무소 서기에 지나지 않았을 적에도, 코퍼필드, 저는 저분이 이 일로 어쩔 줄 몰라 하시는 걸 한두 번도 아니고 스무 번은 보았어요 — 아주 마음을 졸이셨지요 (그야 아버지로서 지극히 당연한 일이고요. 정말이지 저로서는 저분을 탓할 수 없습니다), 애그니스 아가씨가 있어서는 안 될 일에 얽혀 들고 있다는 생각에요.\""]], [["‘My dear Strong,’ said Mr.", "\"이보게, 스트롱,\""], ["Wickfield in a tremulous voice, ‘my good friend, I needn’t tell you that it has been my vice to look for some one master motive in everybody, and to try all actions by one narrow test.", "위크필드 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"내 좋은 벗이여, 굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 누구한테서나 어떤 으뜸가는 동기를 찾으려 들고, 모든 행동을 하나의 좁은 잣대로 재 보려는 것이 내 못된 버릇이었네."], ["I may have fallen into such doubts as I have had, through this mistake.’", "그런 잘못 때문에 내가 품었던 그런 의심에 빠져들었는지도 모르지.\""]], [["‘You have had doubts, Wickfield,’ said the Doctor, without lifting up his head.", "\"자네는 의심을 품었어, 위크필드.\" 박사님이 고개를 들지 않은 채 말씀하셨다."], ["‘You have had doubts.’", "\"자네는 의심을 품었어.\""]], [["‘Speak up, fellow-partner,’ urged Uriah.", "\"말씀하세요, 동업자 양반.\" 유라이어가 다그쳤다."]], [["‘I had, at one time, certainly,’ said Mr.", "\"한때는, 분명히 그랬소.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield. ‘I--God forgive me--I thought YOU had.’", "\"나는 — 하느님, 용서하소서 — 자네가 그런 줄로만 알았네.\""]], [["‘No, no, no!’ returned the Doctor, in a tone of most pathetic grief.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야!\" 박사님이 더없이 애절한 비탄에 잠긴 목소리로 받으셨다."], ["‘I thought, at one time,’ said Mr.", "\"한때 나는,\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield, ‘that you wished to send Maldon abroad to effect a desirable separation.’", "\"자네가 바람직한 별거를 이루려고 몰던을 외국으로 보내고 싶어 하는 줄로 알았네.\""]], [["‘No, no, no!’ returned the Doctor.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야!\" 박사님이 받으셨다."], ["‘To give Annie pleasure, by making some provision for the companion of her childhood. Nothing else.’", "\"애니에게 기쁨을 주려고, 그 애 어린 시절의 동무에게 무슨 자리라도 마련해 주려던 것뿐이었네. 다른 뜻은 전혀 없었어.\""]], [["‘So I found,’ said Mr. Wickfield.", "\"나도 그런 줄 알게 됐지.\""], ["‘I couldn’t doubt it, when you told me so.", "위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["But I thought--I implore you to remember the narrow construction which has been my besetting sin--that, in a case where there was so much disparity in point of years--’", "\"자네가 그렇게 말해 주니 의심할 수가 없더군. 하지만 나는 생각했네 — 부디 그 좁은 해석이 내 고질병 같은 죄였음을 기억해 주게 — 나이 차가 그토록 많이 나는 경우에는—\""]], [["‘That’s the way to put it, you see, Master Copperfield!’ observed Uriah, with fawning and offensive pity.", "\"보십시오, 바로 저렇게 둘러대는 거지요, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어가 아첨하면서도 모욕적인 연민을 띠며 한마디 했다."]], [["‘--a lady of such youth, and such attractions, however real her respect for you, might have been influenced in marrying, by worldly considerations only.", "\"— 저토록 젊고, 저토록 매력이 넘치는 부인이라면, 자네를 향한 존경이 아무리 참되다 한들, 결혼할 때 오로지 세속적인 잇속만으로 마음이 움직였을 수도 있다고 말이네."], ["I make no allowance for innumerable feelings and circumstances that may have all tended to good.", "두루 좋은 쪽으로 작용했을지도 모를 그 무수한 감정과 사정은 내가 전혀 헤아리지 못했어."], ["For Heaven’s sake remember that!’", "제발 그것만은 기억해 주게!\""]], [["‘How kind he puts it!’ said Uriah, shaking his head.", "\"얼마나 점잖게 둘러대시는지!\" 유라이어가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."]], [["‘Always observing her from one point of view,’ said Mr.", "\"늘 한 가지 관점에서만 그 부인을 바라보았으니.\" 위크필드 씨가 말했다."], ["Wickfield; ‘but by all that is dear to you, my old friend, I entreat you to consider what it was; I am forced to confess now, having no escape-’", "\"하지만 자네에게 소중한 그 모든 것을 걸고, 내 오랜 벗이여, 부디 그게 어떤 것이었는지 헤아려 주게. 이제 나는 빠져나갈 길이 없으니 고백할 수밖에 없네—\""]], [["‘No! There’s no way out of it, Mr.", "\"맞습니다, 거기서 빠져나갈 길은 없지요, 위크필드 씨, 나리,\""], ["Wickfield, sir,’ observed Uriah, ‘when it’s got to this.’", "유라이어가 한마디 했다. \"일이 이 지경에 이른 마당에는요.\""]], [["‘--that I did,’ said Mr.", "\"— 내가 정말로,\""], ["Wickfield, glancing helplessly and distractedly at his partner, ‘that I did doubt her, and think her wanting in her duty to you; and that I did sometimes, if I must say all, feel averse to Agnes being in such a familiar relation towards her, as to see what I saw, or in my diseased theory fancied that I saw.", "위크필드 씨가 어쩔 줄 몰라 정신없이 동업자를 흘끗거리며 말했다. \"정말로 그 부인을 의심했고, 자네에 대한 도리를 다하지 못한다고 여겼다는 것을. 그리고 이왕 다 말해야 한다면, 나는 이따금 애그니스가 그 부인과 그토록 허물없는 사이로 지내는 것이 — 내가 본 것을, 아니면 내 병든 억측으로 보았다고 여긴 것을 그 애가 보게 될까 봐 — 못마땅했다는 것을."], ["I never mentioned this to anyone. I never meant it to be known to anyone.", "나는 이 일을 누구에게도 입 밖에 낸 적이 없네. 그 누구도 알게 할 생각이 없었어."], ["And though it is terrible to you to hear,’ said Mr.", "그리고 이 말을 듣는 자네에게는 끔찍한 일이겠지만,\""], ["Wickfield, quite subdued, ‘if you knew how terrible it is for me to tell, you would feel compassion for me!’", "위크필드 씨가 완전히 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"이 말을 하는 게 나에게 얼마나 끔찍한지를 자네가 안다면, 나를 가엾게 여겨 줄 걸세!\""]], [["The Doctor, in the perfect goodness of his nature, put out his hand. Mr.", "박사님은 그 천성의 더없는 선량함으로 손을 내미셨다."], ["Wickfield held it for a little while in his, with his head bowed down.", "위크필드 씨는 고개를 숙인 채 그 손을 잠시 자기 손에 쥐고 있었다."]], [["‘I am sure,’ said Uriah, writhing himself into the silence like a Conger-eel, ‘that this is a subject full of unpleasantness to everybody.", "\"제 생각엔,\" 유라이어가 갯장어처럼 그 침묵 속으로 몸을 비틀어 들어오며 말했다. \"이게 누구에게나 불쾌함으로 가득한 화제임은 분명합니다."], ["But since we have got so far, I ought to take the liberty of mentioning that Copperfield has noticed it too.’", "하지만 이왕 여기까지 온 마당이니, 외람되지만 코퍼필드도 이걸 눈치채고 있었다는 걸 말씀드려야겠네요.\""]], [["I turned upon him, and asked him how he dared refer to me!", "나는 그를 홱 돌아보며, 어떻게 감히 나를 끌어들이느냐고 따져 물었다!"]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아이고!"], ["it’s very kind of you, Copperfield,’ returned Uriah, undulating all over, ‘and we all know what an amiable character yours is; but you know that the moment I spoke to you the other night, you knew what I meant.", "참 친절하시네요, 코퍼필드,\" 유라이어가 온몸을 일렁이며 받았다. \"도련님 성품이 얼마나 다정한지야 우리 모두 알지요."], ["You know you knew what I meant, Copperfield. Don’t deny it!", "하지만 일전 그날 밤 제가 도련님께 말을 꺼낸 그 순간, 도련님은 제 말뜻을 아셨잖아요. 제 말뜻을 아셨다는 걸 도련님도 아시면서요, 코퍼필드."], ["You deny it with the best intentions; but don’t do it, Copperfield.’", "발뺌하지 마세요! 도련님은 좋은 뜻으로 발뺌하시는 거지만, 그러지 마세요, 코퍼필드.\""]], [["I saw the mild eye of the good old Doctor turned upon me for a moment, and I felt that the confession of my old misgivings and remembrances was too plainly written in my face to be overlooked.", "나는 그 인자하신 옛 박사님의 온화한 눈길이 한순간 나에게로 향하는 것을 보았고, 나의 오랜 의구심과 기억의 고백이 내 얼굴에 너무도 또렷이 쓰여 있어 도저히 지나칠 수 없으리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["It was of no use raging.", "길길이 날뛰어 봐야 소용없었다."], ["I could not undo that.", "나는 그것을 되돌릴 수 없었다."], ["Say what I would, I could not unsay it.", "무슨 말을 한들 이미 한 말을 주워 담을 수는 없었다."]], [["We were silent again, and remained so, until the Doctor rose and walked twice or thrice across the room.", "우리는 다시 입을 다물었고, 박사님이 일어나 방을 두세 번 가로질러 걸으실 때까지 그렇게 있었다."], ["Presently he returned to where his chair stood; and, leaning on the back of it, and occasionally putting his handkerchief to his eyes, with a simple honesty that did him more honour, to my thinking, than any disguise he could have effected, said:", "이윽고 박사님은 의자가 놓인 자리로 돌아오셔서, 그 등받이에 몸을 기대고, 이따금 손수건을 눈가에 갖다 대시면서, 내 생각으로는 그 어떤 가식으로도 얻지 못할 명예를 안겨 주는 그 수수한 정직함으로 말씀하셨다."]], [["‘I have been much to blame.", "\"내 잘못이 컸네."], ["I believe I have been very much to blame.", "정말이지 내 잘못이 무척 컸다고 생각하네."], ["I have exposed one whom I hold in my heart, to trials and aspersions--I call them aspersions, even to have been conceived in anybody’s inmost mind--of which she never, but for me, could have been the object.’", "나는 내 마음속 깊이 품고 있는 사람을 시련과 — 비방이라 부르겠네, 누구의 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에서 떠올랐을 뿐이라 해도 비방이라 부르겠네 — 그런 비방에 내맡겼어. 나만 아니었다면 그 사람은 결코 그런 일의 대상이 될 리 없었을 텐데.\""]], [["Uriah Heep gave a kind of snivel.", "유라이어 힙이 코를 훌쩍이는 듯한 소리를 냈다."], ["I think to express sympathy.", "동정을 나타내려는 것이었으리라."]], [["‘Of which my Annie,’ said the Doctor, ‘never, but for me, could have been the object.", "\"나만 아니었다면, 내 애니는,\" 박사님이 말씀하셨다. \"결코 그런 일의 대상이 될 리 없었을 걸세."], ["Gentlemen, I am old now, as you know; I do not feel, tonight, that I have much to live for.", "여러분, 아시다시피 나는 이제 늙었네. 오늘 밤, 나는 더 살아갈 까닭이 그리 많지 않다는 느낌이 드는구려."], ["But my life--my Life--upon the truth and honour of the dear lady who has been the subject of this conversation!’", "하지만 내 목숨을 — 내 목숨을 걸고 맹세하겠소, 이 대화의 주인공이 된 그 사랑하는 부인의 진실과 명예를 두고서!\""]], [["I do not think that the best embodiment of chivalry, the realization of the handsomest and most romantic figure ever imagined by painter, could have said this, with a more impressive and affecting dignity than the plain old Doctor did.", "기사도 정신을 가장 훌륭히 구현한 인물이라 해도, 화가가 일찍이 상상한 가장 잘생기고 가장 낭만적인 형상을 그대로 빚어낸 인물이라 해도, 이 소박한 노박사님보다 더 인상 깊고 더 가슴을 울리는 위엄으로 이 말을 할 수는 없었으리라."]], [["‘But I am not prepared,’ he went on, ‘to deny--perhaps I may have been, without knowing it, in some degree prepared to admit--that I may have unwittingly ensnared that lady into an unhappy marriage.", "\"하지만 나는 부인할 준비가 되어 있지 않네.\" 박사님이 말을 이으셨다. \"— 아니, 어쩌면 나도 모르게, 어느 정도는 인정할 준비가 되어 있었는지도 모르지 — 내가 본의 아니게 그 부인을 불행한 결혼으로 옭아 넣었을지도 모른다는 것을."], ["I am a man quite unaccustomed to observe; and I cannot but believe that the observation of several people, of different ages and positions, all too plainly tending in one direction (and that so natural), is better than mine.’", "나는 무언가를 살펴 헤아리는 데는 도무지 익숙지 못한 사람이야. 그러니 나이도 처지도 제각각인 여러 사람의 관찰이, 하나같이 너무도 분명히 한 방향을 (그것도 그토록 자연스러운 방향을) 가리키고 있다면, 그게 내 관찰보다 낫다고 믿지 않을 수가 없네.\""]], [["I had often admired, as I have elsewhere described, his benignant manner towards his youthful wife; but the respectful tenderness he manifested in every reference to her on this occasion, and the almost reverential manner in which he put away from him the lightest doubt of her integrity, exalted him, in my eyes, beyond description.", "내가 다른 데서도 적었듯이, 나는 종종 박사님이 그 젊은 부인을 대하는 인자한 태도에 감탄하곤 했다. 그러나 이 자리에서 부인을 언급할 때마다 보이신 그 정중한 다정함, 그리고 부인의 결백에 대한 아주 사소한 의심마저 자기에게서 밀쳐 내시던 그 거의 경건하기까지 한 태도는, 내 눈에 그분을 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 드높여 놓았다."]], [["‘I married that lady,’ said the Doctor, ‘when she was extremely young.", "\"나는 그 부인이 아주 어렸을 때 결혼했네.\" 박사님이 말씀하셨다."], ["I took her to myself when her character was scarcely formed.", "\"그 사람의 인격이 채 영글기도 전에 내 사람으로 맞아들였지."], ["So far as it was developed, it had been my happiness to form it. I knew her father well.", "그것이 자라난 만큼은, 그것을 빚어 가는 게 내 행복이었네. 나는 그 사람의 아버지를 잘 알았어."], ["I knew her well.", "그 사람도 잘 알았고."], ["I had taught her what I could, for the love of all her beautiful and virtuous qualities.", "나는 그 모든 아름답고 어진 성품을 아끼는 마음에서, 내가 할 수 있는 것을 그 사람에게 가르쳐 주었네."], ["If I did her wrong; as I fear I did, in taking advantage (but I never meant it) of her gratitude and her affection; I ask pardon of that lady, in my heart!’", "만약 내가 그 사람의 고마움과 애정을 이용해 (하지만 결코 그럴 뜻은 없었네) 그 사람에게 못할 짓을 했다면, 정말 그랬을까 두렵네만, 나는 마음속으로 그 부인에게 용서를 빌겠네!\""]], [["He walked across the room, and came back to the same place; holding the chair with a grasp that trembled, like his subdued voice, in its earnestness.", "박사님은 방을 가로질러 걸으셨다가 다시 같은 자리로 돌아오셨다. 그 진심 어린 간절함 속에서, 죽인 목소리가 그러하듯 떨리는 손길로 의자를 움켜쥐신 채."]], [["‘I regarded myself as a refuge, for her, from the dangers and vicissitudes of life.", "\"나는 나 자신을, 인생의 위험과 풍파로부터 그 사람을 지켜 줄 피난처로 여겼네."], ["I persuaded myself that, unequal though we were in years, she would live tranquilly and contentedly with me.", "나이는 우리가 비록 어울리지 않아도, 그 사람이 나와 함께 평온하고 만족스럽게 살아가리라고 스스로를 타일렀어."], ["I did not shut out of my consideration the time when I should leave her free, and still young and still beautiful, but with her judgement more matured--no, gentlemen--upon my truth!’", "내가 그 사람을 자유롭게 놓아줄 날을, 그때도 여전히 젊고 여전히 아름답되 분별만큼은 더 무르익은 그 사람을 놓아줄 그날을, 나는 결코 헤아림에서 빼놓지 않았네 — 아니, 여러분 — 내 진실을 걸고서!\""]], [["His homely figure seemed to be lightened up by his fidelity and generosity.", "그 소박한 모습이 그분의 충심과 너그러움으로 환히 빛나는 듯했다."], ["Every word he uttered had a force that no other grace could have imparted to it.", "그분이 내뱉는 한마디 한마디에는, 그 어떤 다른 우아함으로도 실어 줄 수 없었을 힘이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["‘My life with this lady has been very happy.", "\"이 부인과 함께한 내 삶은 무척 행복했네."], ["Until tonight, I have had uninterrupted occasion to bless the day on which I did her great injustice.’", "오늘 밤까지, 나는 그 사람에게 큰 잘못을 저지른 그날을 끊임없이 축복할 까닭이 있었네.\""]], [["His voice, more and more faltering in the utterance of these words, stopped for a few moments; then he went on:", "이 말들을 내뱉으면서 점점 더 떨려 가던 박사님의 목소리가 잠시 멎었다. 그러더니 다시 말을 이으셨다."]], [["‘Once awakened from my dream--I have been a poor dreamer, in one way or other, all my life--I see how natural it is that she should have some regretful feeling towards her old companion and her equal.", "\"내 꿈에서 한번 깨어나고 보니 — 나는 이런저런 방식으로 평생을 가엾은 몽상가로 살아왔네만 — 그 사람이 옛 동무, 자기와 처지가 같은 그 사람에게 어떤 아쉬운 마음을 품는 것이 얼마나 자연스러운 일인지 알겠네."], ["That she does regard him with some innocent regret, with some blameless thoughts of what might have been, but for me, is, I fear, too true.", "그 사람이 그를 어떤 순결한 아쉬움으로, 나만 아니었다면 어땠을까 하는 어떤 죄 없는 생각으로 바라본다는 것은, 안타깝게도 너무도 사실인 듯하네."], ["Much that I have seen, but not noted, has come back upon me with new meaning, during this last trying hour.", "내가 보았으나 마음에 새겨 두지 않았던 많은 것들이, 이 견디기 힘든 지난 한 시간 동안 새로운 뜻을 띠고 나에게 되돌아왔어."], ["But, beyond this, gentlemen, the dear lady’s name never must be coupled with a word, a breath, of doubt.’", "하지만 여러분, 그 이상으로는, 그 사랑하는 부인의 이름이 의심의 말 한마디나 숨결 하나와도 결코 엮여서는 안 되네.\""]], [["For a little while, his eye kindled and his voice was firm; for a little while he was again silent.", "잠시 그분의 눈에 불이 일고 목소리가 단단해졌다. 잠시 그분은 다시 입을 다무셨다."], ["Presently, he proceeded as before:", "이윽고 그분은 아까처럼 말을 이어 가셨다."]], [["‘It only remains for me, to bear the knowledge of the unhappiness I have occasioned, as submissively as I can.", "\"이제 내게 남은 일은, 내가 불러일으킨 그 불행을 아는 채로, 되도록 순순히 견뎌 내는 것뿐이네."], ["It is she who should reproach; not I.", "책망해야 할 사람은 그 사람이지 내가 아니야."], ["To save her from misconstruction, cruel misconstruction, that even my friends have not been able to avoid, becomes my duty.", "그 사람을 오해로부터, 내 벗들조차 피하지 못했던 그 잔인한 오해로부터 지켜 내는 것이 이제 내 의무가 되었네."], ["The more retired we live, the better I shall discharge it.", "우리가 더 조용히 숨어 살수록, 나는 그 의무를 더 잘 다할 수 있겠지."], ["And when the time comes--may it come soon, if it be His merciful pleasure!--when my death shall release her from constraint, I shall close my eyes upon her honoured face, with unbounded confidence and love; and leave her, with no sorrow then, to happier and brighter days.’", "그리고 그때가 오면 — 하느님의 자비로운 뜻이라면, 부디 그때가 어서 오기를! — 내 죽음이 그 사람을 굴레에서 풀어 줄 그때가 오면, 나는 더없는 믿음과 사랑으로 그 명예로운 얼굴을 향해 두 눈을 감겠네. 그리고 그때는 아무 슬픔 없이, 그 사람을 더 행복하고 더 환한 나날로 떠나보내겠네.\""]], [["I could not see him for the tears which his earnestness and goodness, so adorned by, and so adorning, the perfect simplicity of his manner, brought into my eyes.", "그분의 그 간절함과 선량함이, 그 더없이 수수한 태도로 한껏 꾸며지고 또 그 태도를 한껏 빛내면서, 내 두 눈에 차오르게 한 눈물 때문에 나는 그분을 볼 수가 없었다."], ["He had moved to the door, when he added:", "그분은 문 쪽으로 걸어가시면서 이렇게 덧붙이셨다."]], [["‘Gentlemen, I have shown you my heart.", "\"여러분, 나는 여러분에게 내 마음을 보여 드렸네."], ["I am sure you will respect it.", "여러분이 그것을 존중해 주리라 믿네."], ["What we have said tonight is never to be said more.", "오늘 밤 우리가 한 말은 두 번 다시 입에 올려서는 안 되네."], ["Wickfield, give me an old friend’s arm upstairs!’", "위크필드, 오랜 벗의 팔을 빌려 위층까지 좀 부축해 주겠나!\""]], [["Mr. Wickfield hastened to him.", "위크필드 씨가 서둘러 그분께 다가갔다."], ["Without interchanging a word they went slowly out of the room together, Uriah looking after them.", "두 사람은 한마디도 주고받지 않고 함께 천천히 방을 나갔고, 유라이어는 그 뒷모습을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Well, Master Copperfield!’ said Uriah, meekly turning to me.", "\"자, 코퍼필드 도련님!\" 유라이어가 점잖은 척 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이 일이 예상했던 대로 흘러가진 않았네요."], ["‘The thing hasn’t took quite the turn that might have been expected, for the old Scholar--what an excellent man!--is as blind as a brickbat; but this family’s out of the cart, I think!’", "저 늙은 학자 양반이 — 참으로 훌륭한 분이지요! — 벽돌 조각처럼 눈이 멀어 버려서요. 하지만 이 집안은 수레에서 내렸다고 봐야겠지요!\""]], [["I needed but the sound of his voice to be so madly enraged as I never was before, and never have been since.", "그의 목소리를 듣는 것만으로도, 나는 그전에도 없었고 그 뒤로도 없었을 만큼 미친 듯이 격분했다."]], [["‘You villain,’ said I, ‘what do you mean by entrapping me into your schemes?", "\"이 악당아,\" 내가 말했다. \"네 음모에 나를 끌어들이다니 무슨 수작이냐?"], ["How dare you appeal to me just now, you false rascal, as if we had been in discussion together?’", "어떻게 감히 방금 마치 우리가 함께 의논이라도 했던 것처럼 나를 끌어대느냐, 이 거짓말쟁이 악당아?\""]], [["As we stood, front to front, I saw so plainly, in the stealthy exultation of his face, what I already so plainly knew; I mean that he forced his confidence upon me, expressly to make me miserable, and had set a deliberate trap for me in this very matter; that I couldn’t bear it.", "우리가 얼굴을 맞대고 서 있을 때, 나는 그 얼굴에 슬그머니 떠오른 의기양양함에서, 내가 이미 너무도 분명히 알고 있던 것을 너무도 분명히 보았다. 곧, 그가 나를 비참하게 만들 작정으로 일부러 자기 속내를 나에게 들이밀었고, 바로 이 일에서 나에게 계획된 덫을 놓았다는 것을."], ["The whole of his lank cheek was invitingly before me, and I struck it with my open hand with that force that my fingers tingled as if I had burnt them.", "나는 도저히 참을 수가 없었다. 그 길쭉한 볼 전체가 나를 부추기듯 눈앞에 있었고, 나는 손가락이 데기라도 한 듯 얼얼하도록 그 볼을 펼친 손바닥으로 후려쳤다."]], [["He caught the hand in his, and we stood in that connexion, looking at each other.", "그는 그 손을 제 손으로 붙잡았고, 우리는 그렇게 손을 맞잡은 채 서로를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["We stood so, a long time; long enough for me to see the white marks of my fingers die out of the deep red of his cheek, and leave it a deeper red.", "우리는 그렇게 한참을 서 있었다. 그 깊고 붉은 볼에서 내 손가락의 하얀 자국이 사라지고, 그 자리가 더 짙게 붉어지는 것을 내가 볼 수 있을 만큼 오래도록."]], [["‘Copperfield,’ he said at length, in a breathless voice, ‘have you taken leave of your senses?’", "\"코퍼필드,\" 마침내 그가 숨 가쁜 목소리로 말했다. \"정신이 나가셨어요?\""]], [["‘I have taken leave of you,’ said I, wresting my hand away.", "\"너하고는 끝이다.\" 나는 손을 홱 빼내며 말했다."], ["‘You dog, I’ll know no more of you.’", "\"이 개 같은 놈, 너에 대해선 더는 아무것도 알고 싶지 않아.\""]], [["‘Won’t you?’ said he, constrained by the pain of his cheek to put his hand there.", "\"안 그러실걸요?\" 그가 볼이 아파 그쪽에 손을 갖다 대지 않을 수 없어 하며 말했다."], ["‘Perhaps you won’t be able to help it.", "\"어쩌면 그러고 싶어도 못 그러실 거예요."], ["Isn’t this ungrateful of you, now?’", "이건 좀 배은망덕한 짓 아닙니까?\""]], [["‘I have shown you often enough,’ said I, ‘that I despise you.", "\"나는 너를 경멸한다는 걸 그동안 충분히 보여 줬어.\" 내가 말했다."], ["I have shown you now, more plainly, that I do. Why should I dread your doing your worst to all about you?", "\"지금은 더 분명히 보여 줬고. 네가 네 주위 사람들 모두에게 못된 짓을 다 한다 한들 내가 왜 두려워해야 하지?"], ["What else do you ever do?’", "너야 늘 그것 말고 다른 짓을 하는 게 있느냐?\""]], [["He perfectly understood this allusion to the considerations that had hitherto restrained me in my communications with him.", "그는 지금까지 내가 그와 주고받는 말에서 나를 자제하게 했던 그 사정에 대한 이 빗댄 말을 완벽하게 알아들었다."], ["I rather think that neither the blow, nor the allusion, would have escaped me, but for the assurance I had had from Agnes that night. It is no matter.", "그날 밤 애그니스에게서 받은 그 다짐이 아니었다면, 그 따귀도, 그 빗댄 말도 내 입에서 나오지 않았으리라 싶다. 하지만 이제 와 그게 무슨 상관이랴."]], [["There was another long pause.", "또 한 번 긴 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["His eyes, as he looked at me, seemed to take every shade of colour that could make eyes ugly.", "나를 바라보는 그의 두 눈은, 눈을 추하게 만들 수 있는 온갖 빛깔을 다 띠는 듯했다."]], [["‘Copperfield,’ he said, removing his hand from his cheek, ‘you have always gone against me.", "\"코퍼필드,\" 그가 볼에서 손을 떼며 말했다. \"당신은 늘 저한테 맞서 왔어요."], ["I know you always used to be against me at Mr. Wickfield’s.’", "위크필드 씨 댁에서도 늘 저한테 맞서곤 하셨다는 걸 저는 압니다.\""]], [["‘You may think what you like,’ said I, still in a towering rage.", "\"네 멋대로 생각해라.\" 나는 여전히 노여움이 머리끝까지 치민 채 말했다."], ["‘If it is not true, so much the worthier you.’", "\"그게 사실이 아니라면, 그만큼 너는 더 가치 있는 인간이겠지.\""]], [["‘And yet I always liked you, Copperfield!’ he rejoined.", "\"그런데도 저는 늘 당신이 좋았어요, 코퍼필드!\" 그가 받아쳤다."]], [["I deigned to make him no reply; and, taking up my hat, was going out to bed, when he came between me and the door.", "나는 그에게 대꾸할 가치도 못 느꼈다. 그래서 모자를 집어 들고 잠자리로 나가려는데, 그가 나와 문 사이에 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Copperfield,’ he said, ‘there must be two parties to a quarrel.", "\"코퍼필드,\" 그가 말했다. \"싸움이란 두 편이 있어야 하는 법이지요."], ["I won’t be one.’", "저는 그 한 편이 되지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You may go to the devil!’ said I.", "\"꺼져 버려!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Don’t say that!’ he replied.", "\"그런 말 마세요!\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘I know you’ll be sorry afterwards.", "\"나중에 후회하실 거라는 걸 저는 압니다."], ["How can you make yourself so inferior to me, as to show such a bad spirit?", "어떻게 그렇게 못된 심보를 내보이면서 스스로를 저보다 못한 사람으로 깎아내리실 수 있어요?"], ["But I forgive you.’", "하지만 저는 당신을 용서합니다.\""]], [["‘You forgive me!’ I repeated disdainfully.", "\"네가 나를 용서한다고!\" 나는 경멸을 담아 되뇌었다."]], [["‘I do, and you can’t help yourself,’ replied Uriah.", "\"용서합니다, 그리고 당신은 어쩔 도리가 없어요.\""], ["‘To think of your going and attacking me, that have always been a friend to you!", "유라이어가 받았다. \"늘 당신의 벗이었던 저를 가서 후려치다니요!"], ["But there can’t be a quarrel without two parties, and I won’t be one.", "하지만 싸움이란 두 편이 없으면 안 되고, 저는 그 한 편이 되지 않겠습니다."], ["I will be a friend to you, in spite of you.", "저는 당신이 마다해도 당신의 벗이 되겠어요."], ["So now you know what you’ve got to expect.’", "자, 이제 당신이 뭘 각오해야 하는지 아시겠지요.\""]], [["The necessity of carrying on this dialogue (his part in which was very slow; mine very quick) in a low tone, that the house might not be disturbed at an unseasonable hour, did not improve my temper; though my passion was cooling down.", "이 대화를(그의 몫은 무척 느렸고 내 몫은 무척 빨랐다), 이런 때아닌 시각에 집 안 사람들을 깨우지 않도록 낮은 목소리로 이어 가야 한다는 점이 내 화를 가라앉히는 데는 도움이 안 됐다. 그래도 내 격정은 차츰 식어 갔다."], ["Merely telling him that I should expect from him what I always had expected, and had never yet been disappointed in, I opened the door upon him, as if he had been a great walnut put there to be cracked, and went out of the house.", "나는 그저, 내가 늘 너에게서 기대해 온 것을, 그리고 여태 한 번도 어긋난 적 없던 그것을 앞으로도 기대하겠노라고만 말하고는, 그가 마치 까 보라고 거기 놓인 커다란 호두라도 되는 양 문을 열어젖히고 집을 나섰다."], ["But he slept out of the house too, at his mother’s lodging; and before I had gone many hundred yards, came up with me.", "하지만 그도 집 밖, 제 어머니의 셋방에서 잤기에, 내가 몇백 야드도 채 못 가서 그가 따라붙었다."]], [["‘You know, Copperfield,’ he said, in my ear (I did not turn my head), ‘you’re in quite a wrong position’; which I felt to be true, and that made me chafe the more; ‘you can’t make this a brave thing, and you can’t help being forgiven.", "\"있잖아요, 코퍼필드,\" 그가 내 귀에 대고 말했다(나는 고개를 돌리지 않았다). \"당신은 지금 영 그릇된 처지에 놓여 있어요.\""], ["I don’t intend to mention it to mother, nor to any living soul. I’m determined to forgive you.", "나는 그게 사실임을 느꼈고, 그래서 더 약이 올랐다. \"이걸 용감한 일로 꾸밀 수도 없고, 용서받지 않을 도리도 없지요."], ["But I do wonder that you should lift your hand against a person that you knew to be so umble!’", "저는 어머니한테도, 살아 있는 그 누구한테도 이걸 말할 생각이 없습니다. 저는 당신을 용서하기로 단단히 마음먹었어요. 하지만 그토록 미천한 줄 빤히 아는 사람한테 손찌검을 하시다니, 그건 정말 의아하네요!\""]], [["I felt only less mean than he. He knew me better than I knew myself.", "나는 그저 그보다 덜 비열한 인간일 뿐이라고 느꼈다. 그는 나 자신보다도 나를 더 잘 알았다."], ["If he had retorted or openly exasperated me, it would have been a relief and a justification; but he had put me on a slow fire, on which I lay tormented half the night.", "그가 맞받아쳤거나 대놓고 나를 약 올렸더라면, 그건 차라리 한숨 돌릴 일이자 내 행동의 변명거리가 되었을 것이다. 하지만 그는 나를 약한 불 위에 올려놓았고, 나는 그 위에서 밤의 절반을 시달리며 누워 있었다."]], [["In the morning, when I came out, the early church-bell was ringing, and he was walking up and down with his mother.", "아침에 내가 밖으로 나왔을 때, 이른 교회 종이 울리고 있었고, 그는 제 어머니와 함께 거닐고 있었다."], ["He addressed me as if nothing had happened, and I could do no less than reply.", "그는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 나에게 말을 걸었고, 나도 대꾸하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["I had struck him hard enough to give him the toothache, I suppose.", "짐작건대 나는 그가 치통을 앓을 만큼 세게 후려쳤던 모양이다."], ["At all events his face was tied up in a black silk handkerchief, which, with his hat perched on the top of it, was far from improving his appearance.", "어쨌거나 그의 얼굴은 검은 비단 손수건으로 동여매여 있었고, 그 위에 모자를 얹은 꼴은 그의 행색을 나아 보이게 하기는커녕 영 딴판이었다."], ["I heard that he went to a dentist’s in London on the Monday morning, and had a tooth out.", "듣자 하니 그는 월요일 아침에 런던의 어느 치과에 가서 이를 하나 뺐다고 했다."], ["I hope it was a double one.", "부디 그게 어금니였기를 바란다."]], [["The Doctor gave out that he was not quite well; and remained alone, for a considerable part of every day, during the remainder of the visit.", "박사님은 몸이 좀 편찮으시다고 알리고는, 남은 방문 기간 내내 날마다 상당한 시간을 혼자 지내셨다."], ["Agnes and her father had been gone a week, before we resumed our usual work.", "애그니스와 그 아버지가 떠난 지 일주일이 지나서야 우리는 평소의 작업을 다시 시작했다."], ["On the day preceding its resumption, the Doctor gave me with his own hands a folded note not sealed.", "작업을 다시 시작하기 전날, 박사님은 봉인하지 않은 접힌 쪽지를 손수 나에게 건네셨다."], ["It was addressed to myself; and laid an injunction on me, in a few affectionate words, never to refer to the subject of that evening.", "그것은 나에게 보내는 것이었는데, 다정한 몇 마디로, 그날 밤의 그 일은 결코 다시 입에 올리지 말라는 당부를 담고 있었다."], ["I had confided it to my aunt, but to no one else.", "나는 그 일을 고모님께만 털어놓았고, 다른 누구에게도 말하지 않았다."], ["It was not a subject I could discuss with Agnes, and Agnes certainly had not the least suspicion of what had passed.", "그것은 내가 애그니스와 의논할 만한 일이 아니었고, 애그니스 역시 그동안 무슨 일이 있었는지 조금도 눈치채지 못한 게 분명했다."]], [["Neither, I felt convinced, had Mrs. Strong then.", "내가 확신하건대, 그때는 스트롱 부인도 마찬가지였다."], ["Several weeks elapsed before I saw the least change in her.", "부인에게서 아주 작은 변화라도 보이기까지는 몇 주가 흘렀다."], ["It came on slowly, like a cloud when there is no wind.", "그것은 바람 한 점 없을 때 끼는 구름처럼 천천히 다가왔다."], ["At first, she seemed to wonder at the gentle compassion with which the Doctor spoke to her, and at his wish that she should have her mother with her, to relieve the dull monotony of her life.", "처음에 부인은 박사님이 자기에게 말을 건넬 때 보이는 그 부드러운 연민에, 그리고 그 따분하고 단조로운 생활을 덜어 주려고 어머니를 곁에 두면 좋겠다는 박사님의 바람에 어리둥절해하는 듯했다."], ["Often, when we were at work, and she was sitting by, I would see her pausing and looking at him with that memorable face.", "우리가 작업을 하고 부인이 곁에 앉아 있을 때면, 나는 종종 부인이 하던 것을 멈추고 그 잊을 수 없는 얼굴로 박사님을 바라보는 것을 보곤 했다."], ["Afterwards, I sometimes observed her rise, with her eyes full of tears, and go out of the room.", "그 뒤로 나는 이따금 부인이 눈에 눈물이 가득한 채 일어나 방을 나가는 것을 보았다."], ["Gradually, an unhappy shadow fell upon her beauty, and deepened every day. Mrs.", "차츰 불행한 그늘이 그 아름다움 위에 드리워, 날마다 짙어 갔다."], ["Markleham was a regular inmate of the cottage then; but she talked and talked, and saw nothing.", "그 무렵 마클럼 부인은 오두막에 아예 눌러앉아 살았지만, 그녀는 떠들고 또 떠들 뿐, 아무것도 보지 못했다."]], [["As this change stole on Annie, once like sunshine in the Doctor’s house, the Doctor became older in appearance, and more grave; but the sweetness of his temper, the placid kindness of his manner, and his benevolent solicitude for her, if they were capable of any increase, were increased.", "한때 박사님 집안의 햇살 같던 애니에게 이 변화가 슬그머니 스며드는 동안, 박사님은 겉모습이 더 늙어 가고 더 무거워지셨다. 하지만 그분의 그 다정한 성미와 그 잔잔한 친절함과 부인을 향한 그 자애로운 염려는, 만약 더 늘어날 여지가 있었다면 더 늘어나 있었다."], ["I saw him once, early on the morning of her birthday, when she came to sit in the window while we were at work (which she had always done, but now began to do with a timid and uncertain air that I thought very touching), take her forehead between his hands, kiss it, and go hurriedly away, too much moved to remain.", "부인의 생일날 이른 아침, 우리가 작업을 하는 동안 그녀가 창가에 앉으러 왔을 때(늘 해 오던 일이지만, 이제는 내가 보기에 무척 가슴 아프도록 머뭇거리고 머쓱한 기색으로 그러기 시작했다), 나는 박사님이 두 손으로 그녀의 이마를 감싸고 입을 맞추신 뒤, 더는 머물 수 없을 만큼 마음이 북받쳐 황급히 자리를 뜨시는 모습을 한 번 보았다."], ["I saw her stand where he had left her, like a statue; and then bend down her head, and clasp her hands, and weep, I cannot say how sorrowfully.", "나는 그녀가 박사님이 떠나신 자리에 조각상처럼 가만히 서 있다가, 이윽고 고개를 떨구고 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 뭐라 말할 수 없을 만큼 서글피 우는 것을 보았다."]], [["Sometimes, after that, I fancied that she tried to speak even to me, in intervals when we were left alone.", "그 뒤로 이따금, 우리 둘만 남게 되는 틈에 그녀가 나에게라도 무슨 말을 하려 애쓰는 것만 같았다."], ["But she never uttered a word. The Doctor always had some new project for her participating in amusements away from home, with her mother; and Mrs.", "하지만 그녀는 끝내 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않았다. 박사님은 늘 그녀가 어머니와 함께 집을 떠나 무슨 놀이에 끼어들 만한 새 계획을 마련하셨다."], ["Markleham, who was very fond of amusements, and very easily dissatisfied with anything else, entered into them with great good-will, and was loud in her commendations.", "그리고 놀이라면 무척 좋아하고 다른 것에는 어찌나 쉽게 싫증을 내는지 모르는 마클럼 부인은, 더없이 흔쾌히 거기에 끼어들어 큰 소리로 그것들을 칭찬했다."], ["But Annie, in a spiritless unhappy way, only went whither she was led, and seemed to have no care for anything.", "하지만 애니는 기운 없고 불행한 모습으로, 그저 이끄는 대로 따라갈 뿐, 무엇에도 도무지 마음을 두지 않는 듯했다."]], [["I did not know what to think.", "나는 어찌 생각해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["Neither did my aunt; who must have walked, at various times, a hundred miles in her uncertainty.", "고모님도 마찬가지였으니, 분명 그 막막함 속에서 이런저런 때에 도합 백 마일은 걸어 다니셨을 것이다."], ["What was strangest of all was, that the only real relief which seemed to make its way into the secret region of this domestic unhappiness, made its way there in the person of Mr. Dick.", "무엇보다 기이한 것은, 이 가정의 불행이 자리한 그 은밀한 영역으로 비집고 든 단 하나의 진정한 위안이, 다름 아닌 딕 씨라는 사람의 모습으로 그곳에 비집고 들었다는 점이다."]], [["What his thoughts were on the subject, or what his observation was, I am as unable to explain, as I dare say he would have been to assist me in the task.", "이 일에 대해 그의 생각이 어떠했는지, 또는 그의 관찰이 어떠했는지를 나는 도무지 설명할 수가 없는데, 감히 말하건대 그 일을 거들어 달라 했어도 그 자신 역시 그러지 못했을 것이다."], ["But, as I have recorded in the narrative of my school days, his veneration for the Doctor was unbounded; and there is a subtlety of perception in real attachment, even when it is borne towards man by one of the lower animals, which leaves the highest intellect behind.", "하지만 내 학창 시절 이야기에 적어 두었듯이, 박사님을 향한 그의 숭배는 끝이 없었다. 그리고 참된 애정에는 — 그것이 미물 가운데 하나가 사람을 향해 품은 것일 때조차 — 더없이 높은 지성을 저만치 뒤로 따돌리는 어떤 섬세한 통찰이 깃들어 있는 법이다."], ["To this mind of the heart, if I may call it so, in Mr. Dick, some bright ray of the truth shot straight.", "딕 씨 안에 있는 이 마음의 지성이라고 불러도 좋을 것에는, 진실의 어떤 밝은 빛줄기 하나가 곧장 내리꽂혔다."]], [["He had proudly resumed his privilege, in many of his spare hours, of walking up and down the garden with the Doctor; as he had been accustomed to pace up and down The Doctor’s Walk at Canterbury.", "그는 캔터베리에서 박사님 산책로를 오르내리며 거닐던 데 익숙했던 것처럼, 한가한 시간이면 박사님과 함께 정원을 오르내리며 거니는 그 특권을 자랑스레 되찾았다."], ["But matters were no sooner in this state, than he devoted all his spare time (and got up earlier to make it more) to these perambulations.", "그런데 사정이 이렇게 되자마자, 그는 한가한 시간을 죄다(그 시간을 더 늘리려고 더 일찍 일어나기까지 했다) 이 거닒에 바쳤다."], ["If he had never been so happy as when the Doctor read that marvellous performance, the Dictionary, to him; he was now quite miserable unless the Doctor pulled it out of his pocket, and began. When the Doctor and I were engaged, he now fell into the custom of walking up and down with Mrs.", "그가 박사님이 그 놀라운 역작인 사전을 자기에게 읽어 주실 때만큼 행복했던 적이 없었다면, 이제는 박사님이 그것을 주머니에서 꺼내 읽기 시작하지 않으면 영 비참해했다. 박사님과 내가 일에 매달려 있을 때면, 그는 이제 스트롱 부인과 함께 오르내리며 거닐고, 부인이 아끼는 꽃을 다듬거나 화단의 잡초를 뽑는 것을 돕는 버릇이 들었다."], ["Strong, and helping her to trim her favourite flowers, or weed the beds.", "감히 말하건대 그는 한 시간에 열 마디도 채 입에 올리지 않았을 것이다."], ["I dare say he rarely spoke a dozen words in an hour: but his quiet interest, and his wistful face, found immediate response in both their breasts; each knew that the other liked him, and that he loved both; and he became what no one else could be--a link between them.", "하지만 그의 그 조용한 관심과 애틋한 얼굴은 두 사람의 가슴에서 곧바로 화답을 얻었다. 저마다 상대도 그를 좋아한다는 것을, 그리고 그가 둘 다 사랑한다는 것을 알았다. 그리하여 그는 다른 누구도 될 수 없는 무엇이 되었으니 — 두 사람을 잇는 다리가 된 것이다."]], [["When I think of him, with his impenetrably wise face, walking up and down with the Doctor, delighted to be battered by the hard words in the Dictionary; when I think of him carrying huge watering-pots after Annie; kneeling down, in very paws of gloves, at patient microscopic work among the little leaves; expressing as no philosopher could have expressed, in everything he did, a delicate desire to be her friend; showering sympathy, trustfulness, and affection, out of every hole in the watering-pot; when I think of him never wandering in that better mind of his to which unhappiness addressed itself, never bringing the unfortunate King Charles into the garden, never wavering in his grateful service, never diverted from his knowledge that there was something wrong, or from his wish to set it right--I really feel almost ashamed of having known that he was not quite in his wits, taking account of the utmost I have done with mine.", "그가 그 헤아릴 길 없이 지혜로운 얼굴을 하고 박사님과 함께 오르내리며 거닐면서, 사전에 나오는 어려운 말들에 두들겨 맞는 것을 좋아하던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 그가 애니의 뒤를 따라 커다란 물뿌리개를 나르고, 정말이지 장갑이라는 앞발을 낀 채 무릎을 꿇고, 작은 잎사귀들 사이에서 참을성 있게 미세한 일을 하던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 어떤 철학자도 표현하지 못했을 것을, 그가 하는 모든 일에서, 그녀의 벗이 되고 싶다는 그 섬세한 바람으로 표현하던 모습을. 물뿌리개의 구멍이란 구멍에서마다 공감과 믿음과 애정을 쏟아붓던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 그가 불행이 말을 걸어오는 그 더 나은 정신을 잃고 헤매는 일이 결코 없었고, 그 불운한 찰스 왕을 정원에 끌어들이는 일도 결코 없었으며, 그 고마워하는 섬김에서 흔들리는 법도 결코 없었고, 무언가 잘못되어 있다는 자각이나 그것을 바로잡고 싶다는 바람에서 한눈파는 법도 결코 없던 모습을 떠올릴 때. 나는 내 멀쩡한 정신으로 해낸 그 모든 일을 다 따져 보더라도, 그가 정신이 온전치 못한 사람이라는 걸 알고 지냈다는 사실이 정말이지 거의 부끄러워질 지경이다."]], [["‘Nobody but myself, Trot, knows what that man is!’ my aunt would proudly remark, when we conversed about it.", "\"트롯, 저 사람이 어떤 사람인지는 나 말고는 아무도 몰라!\" 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나눌 때면 고모님이 자랑스레 말씀하시곤 했다."], ["‘Dick will distinguish himself yet!’", "\"딕은 언젠가 이름을 떨치고 말 거야!\""]], [["I must refer to one other topic before I close this chapter.", "이 장을 마치기 전에 한 가지 이야기를 더 짚어 두어야겠다."], ["While the visit at the Doctor’s was still in progress, I observed that the postman brought two or three letters every morning for Uriah Heep, who remained at Highgate until the rest went back, it being a leisure time; and that these were always directed in a business-like manner by Mr.", "박사님 댁의 방문이 아직 한창일 무렵, 나는 우편배달부가 아침마다 유라이어 힙 앞으로 두세 통의 편지를 가져오는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그는 한가한 때라 다른 사람들이 돌아간 뒤에도 하이게이트에 남아 있었다."], ["Micawber, who now assumed a round legal hand. I was glad to infer, from these slight premises, that Mr.", "그리고 그 편지들은 늘 미코버 씨가 사무적인 필치로 겉봉을 쓴 것이었는데, 그는 이제 둥글둥글한 법률가 글씨체를 쓰고 있었다."], ["Micawber was doing well; and consequently was much surprised to receive, about this time, the following letter from his amiable wife.", "나는 이 사소한 단서들로 미루어 미코버 씨가 잘 지내고 있나 보다고 짐작하며 반가워했고, 그래서 이 무렵 그의 사랑스러운 아내에게서 다음과 같은 편지를 받았을 때 무척 놀랐다."]], [["‘CANTERBURY, Monday Evening.", "'캔터베리, 월요일 저녁."]], [["‘You will doubtless be surprised, my dear Mr. Copperfield, to receive this communication.", "'친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 이 편지를 받으시고 분명 놀라시겠지요."], ["Still more so, by its contents.", "그 내용에는 더더욱 놀라실 테고요."], ["Still more so, by the stipulation of implicit confidence which I beg to impose.", "그리고 제가 간곡히 부탁드리는 절대적인 비밀 엄수의 조건에는 한층 더 놀라실 겁니다."], ["But my feelings as a wife and mother require relief; and as I do not wish to consult my family (already obnoxious to the feelings of Mr. Micawber), I know no one of whom I can better ask advice than my friend and former lodger.", "하지만 아내로서, 또 어머니로서 제 심정이 풀어 놓을 길을 찾고 있는데, 친정 식구들(이미 미코버 씨의 심기를 거스르는 이들이지요)과 상의하고 싶지는 않으니, 제 벗이자 옛 하숙인인 당신만큼 조언을 구하기에 더 나은 사람을 저는 알지 못합니다."]], [["‘You may be aware, my dear Mr. Copperfield, that between myself and Mr. Micawber (whom I will never desert), there has always been preserved a spirit of mutual confidence. Mr.", "'친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 당신도 아시겠지만, 저와 미코버 씨(저는 결코 그이를 저버리지 않을 거예요) 사이에는 늘 서로를 믿는 마음이 지켜져 왔습니다."], ["Micawber may have occasionally given a bill without consulting me, or he may have misled me as to the period when that obligation would become due.", "미코버 씨가 이따금 저와 상의도 없이 어음을 끊었거나, 그 빚이 만기가 되는 시점을 두고 저를 오해하게 했을 수는 있어요."], ["This has actually happened.", "실제로 그런 일이 있었고요."], ["But, in general, Mr. Micawber has had no secrets from the bosom of affection--I allude to his wife--and has invariably, on our retirement to rest, recalled the events of the day.", "하지만 대체로 미코버 씨는 애정 어린 품, 곧 자기 아내한테 비밀이 없었고, 잠자리에 들 때면 늘 그날 있었던 일들을 돌이켜 들려주곤 했습니다."]], [["‘You will picture to yourself, my dear Mr. Copperfield, what the poignancy of my feelings must be, when I inform you that Mr.", "'친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 미코버 씨가 완전히 변해 버렸다고 말씀드리면, 제 심정이 얼마나 사무치게 쓰라릴지 짐작이 가실 거예요."], ["Micawber is entirely changed.", "그이는 입을 닫아 버렸어요."], ["He is reserved.", "비밀스러워졌고요."], ["He is secret. His life is a mystery to the partner of his joys and sorrows--I again allude to his wife--and if I should assure you that beyond knowing that it is passed from morning to night at the office, I now know less of it than I do of the man in the south, connected with whose mouth the thoughtless children repeat an idle tale respecting cold plum porridge, I should adopt a popular fallacy to express an actual fact.", "기쁨도 슬픔도 나누는 동반자에게 — 다시금 자기 아내를 두고 하는 말입니다만 — 그이의 삶은 하나의 수수께끼가 되었어요. 그리고 그이가 아침부터 밤까지 사무소에서 시간을 보낸다는 것만 알 뿐, 이제 저는 그 삶에 대해, 식어 빠진 자두 죽을 두고 철없는 아이들이 부질없는 옛이야기를 읊어 대는 그 남쪽 나라 사내에 대해 아는 것보다도 더 모른다고 말씀드린다면, 그건 흔히들 빠지는 잘못된 표현을 빌려 어김없는 사실을 전하는 셈이겠지요."]], [["‘But this is not all. Mr.", "'하지만 이게 다가 아니에요."], ["Micawber is morose.", "미코버 씨는 시무룩해졌어요."], ["He is severe.", "모질어졌고요."], ["He is estranged from our eldest son and daughter, he has no pride in his twins, he looks with an eye of coldness even on the unoffending stranger who last became a member of our circle.", "큰아들과 큰딸과는 서먹해졌고, 쌍둥이를 자랑스러워하지도 않으며, 우리 식구가 된 지 얼마 안 되는, 아무 죄도 없는 그 갓난것조차 차가운 눈으로 바라봅니다."], ["The pecuniary means of meeting our expenses, kept down to the utmost farthing, are obtained from him with great difficulty, and even under fearful threats that he will Settle himself (the exact expression); and he inexorably refuses to give any explanation whatever of this distracting policy.", "우리 살림에 드는 돈은, 한 푼까지 바짝 쥐어짜 줄였는데도, 그이한테서 받아 내기가 여간 어려운 게 아니에요. 심지어 자기를 끝장내 버리겠다는(꼭 그 말 그대로예요) 무시무시한 으름장까지 놓는답니다. 그러면서도 이 사람을 미치게 만드는 그 처사에 대해서는 어떤 설명도 한사코 거절하고요."]], [["‘This is hard to bear.", "'이건 견디기 힘든 일이에요."], ["This is heart-breaking.", "가슴이 미어지는 일이고요."], ["If you will advise me, knowing my feeble powers such as they are, how you think it will be best to exert them in a dilemma so unwonted, you will add another friendly obligation to the many you have already rendered me.", "제 변변찮은 힘이 어떤지 아시는 당신이, 이토록 낯선 곤경에서 그 힘을 어떻게 쓰는 것이 가장 좋을지 조언해 주신다면, 이미 제게 베풀어 주신 그 숱한 다정한 은혜에 또 하나의 은혜를 더해 주시는 셈이 될 거예요."], ["With loves from the children, and a smile from the happily-unconscious stranger, I remain, dear Mr. Copperfield,", "아이들의 사랑과, 아무것도 모른 채 행복한 그 갓난것의 미소를 함께 보내며, 저는 그럼 이만 줄이겠습니다, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨,"]], [["‘Your afflicted, ‘EMMA MICAWBER.’", "'시름에 잠긴 당신의 벗, '엠마 미코버 올림.'"]], [["I did not feel justified in giving a wife of Mrs. Micawber’s experience any other recommendation, than that she should try to reclaim Mr.", "미코버 부인 정도의 경륜을 지닌 아내에게 내가 드릴 만한 권고라곤, 참을성과 다정함으로 미코버 씨를 되돌려 보라는 것밖에 (어차피 그녀라면 어떤 경우든 그러리라는 걸 나는 알았다) 달리 떳떳하게 내놓을 게 없었다."], ["Micawber by patience and kindness (as I knew she would in any case); but the letter set me thinking about him very much.", "하지만 이 편지는 나로 하여금 그에 대해 무척 많은 생각에 잠기게 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 43. ANOTHER RETROSPECT", "# CHAPTER 43. ANOTHER RETROSPECT"]], [["Once again, let me pause upon a memorable period of my life.", "내 인생에서 잊지 못할 한 시절 앞에서, 다시 한번 발걸음을 멈추게 해 다오."], ["Let me stand aside, to see the phantoms of those days go by me, accompanying the shadow of myself, in dim procession.", "한쪽으로 비켜서서, 그 시절의 환영들이 내 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 모습을 — 나 자신의 그림자를 거느린 채, 어슴푸레한 행렬을 이루며 — 바라보게 해 다오."]], [["Weeks, months, seasons, pass along.", "몇 주, 몇 달, 계절이 흘러간다."], ["They seem little more than a summer day and a winter evening.", "그것들은 어느 여름날 하루와 어느 겨울 저녁 한때보다 그리 길게 느껴지지도 않는다."], ["Now, the Common where I walk with Dora is all in bloom, a field of bright gold; and now the unseen heather lies in mounds and bunches underneath a covering of snow.", "어느 순간엔 도라와 함께 거니는 공유지가 온통 꽃으로 뒤덮여 눈부신 황금 들판을 이루고, 또 어느 순간엔 보이지 않던 히스가 눈 이불 밑에 둔덕과 무더기를 이루며 누워 있다."], ["In a breath, the river that flows through our Sunday walks is sparkling in the summer sun, is ruffled by the winter wind, or thickened with drifting heaps of ice.", "눈 깜짝할 사이에, 일요일 산책길을 가로질러 흐르는 강물이 여름 햇살에 반짝이다가, 겨울바람에 잔물결을 일으키다가, 떠다니는 얼음덩이로 두꺼워진다."], ["Faster than ever river ran towards the sea, it flashes, darkens, and rolls away.", "그 어떤 강물이 바다를 향해 달려간 것보다도 더 빠르게, 강물은 번득이고, 어두워지고, 굽이쳐 흘러가 버린다."]], [["Not a thread changes, in the house of the two little bird-like ladies.", "새 두 마리처럼 자그마한 두 부인의 집에서는 실오라기 하나 달라지지 않는다."], ["The clock ticks over the fireplace, the weather-glass hangs in the hall.", "시계는 벽난로 위에서 똑딱거리고, 청우계는 복도에 걸려 있다."], ["Neither clock nor weather-glass is ever right; but we believe in both, devoutly.", "시계도 청우계도 한 번도 맞은 적이 없으나, 우리는 둘 다 경건하게 믿는다."]], [["I have come legally to man’s estate.", "나는 법적으로 어엿한 성년이 되었다."], ["I have attained the dignity of twenty-one.", "스물한 살의 위엄에 이르렀다."], ["But this is a sort of dignity that may be thrust upon one.", "하지만 이것은 누군가에게 떠밀려 얹어지는 종류의 위엄이다."], ["Let me think what I have achieved.", "내가 무엇을 이루었는지 한번 생각해 보자."]], [["I have tamed that savage stenographic mystery.", "나는 그 사납던 속기의 비밀을 길들였다."], ["I make a respectable income by it.", "그것으로 그럭저럭 괜찮은 수입을 올린다."], ["I am in high repute for my accomplishment in all pertaining to the art, and am joined with eleven others in reporting the debates in Parliament for a Morning Newspaper.", "그 기술에 관한 모든 분야에서 솜씨를 인정받아 명성이 높고, 다른 열한 사람과 함께 어느 조간신문을 위해 의회의 토론을 받아 적는다."], ["Night after night, I record predictions that never come to pass, professions that are never fulfilled, explanations that are only meant to mystify. I wallow in words.", "밤이면 밤마다, 나는 결코 들어맞지 않는 예언, 결코 지켜지지 않는 공언, 그저 사람들을 헷갈리게 할 목적뿐인 해명을 받아 적는다. 나는 말의 늪에서 뒹군다."], ["Britannia, that unfortunate female, is always before me, like a trussed fowl: skewered through and through with office-pens, and bound hand and foot with red tape.", "그 불운한 여인, 브리타니아는 늘 내 앞에 묶인 닭처럼 놓여 있다. 사무용 펜에 온몸이 줄줄이 꿰뚫리고, 붉은 끈으로 손발이 꽁꽁 묶인 채로."], ["I am sufficiently behind the scenes to know the worth of political life.", "나는 정치판 무대 뒤를 충분히 들여다봐서 정치 인생의 값어치를 안다."], ["I am quite an Infidel about it, and shall never be converted.", "나는 그 점에서는 완전한 불신자이고, 앞으로도 결코 개종하지 않을 것이다."]], [["My dear old Traddles has tried his hand at the same pursuit, but it is not in Traddles’s way.", "내 다정한 옛 친구 트래들스도 같은 일에 손을 대 보았으나, 트래들스의 방식과는 맞지 않았다."], ["He is perfectly good-humoured respecting his failure, and reminds me that he always did consider himself slow.", "그는 자신의 실패를 두고도 더할 나위 없이 너그럽게 받아들이며, 자기는 늘 스스로 굼뜬 사람이라고 여겨 왔노라고 나에게 일깨워 준다."], ["He has occasional employment on the same newspaper, in getting up the facts of dry subjects, to be written about and embellished by more fertile minds.", "그는 같은 신문에 이따금 일거리를 맡는데, 메마른 주제들의 사실을 그러모아 더 풍부한 머리를 가진 사람들이 글로 쓰고 살을 붙이도록 넘기는 일이다."], ["He is called to the bar; and with admirable industry and self-denial has scraped another hundred pounds together, to fee a Conveyancer whose chambers he attends.", "그는 변호사 자격을 얻었고, 감탄스러운 근면과 극기로 또 백 파운드를 그러모아, 자기가 드나드는 법률 사무실의 부동산 양도 전문 변호사에게 수업료로 바쳤다."], ["A great deal of very hot port wine was consumed at his call; and, considering the figure, I should think the Inner Temple must have made a profit by it.", "그의 변호사 임명식에서는 아주 뜨거운 포트와인이 어마어마하게 소비되었으니, 그 액수로 따지면 이너 템플은 그 일로 한몫 챙긴 게 분명하지 싶다."]], [["I have come out in another way.", "나는 또 다른 길로도 세상에 나왔다."], ["I have taken with fear and trembling to authorship.", "나는 두렵고 떨리는 마음으로 글쓰기에 손을 댔다."], ["I wrote a little something, in secret, and sent it to a magazine, and it was published in the magazine.", "남몰래 자그마한 것을 하나 써서 어느 잡지에 보냈더니, 그게 잡지에 실렸다."], ["Since then, I have taken heart to write a good many trifling pieces.", "그 뒤로 나는 용기를 내어 꽤 많은 자질구레한 글을 썼다."], ["Now, I am regularly paid for them.", "이제는 그것들로 어엿이 원고료를 받는다."], ["Altogether, I am well off, when I tell my income on the fingers of my left hand, I pass the third finger and take in the fourth to the middle joint.", "통틀어 보면 나는 살림이 넉넉한 편이라, 내 수입을 왼손 손가락으로 꼽아 헤아릴 때면, 셋째 손가락을 지나 넷째 손가락 가운데 마디까지 이른다."]], [["We have removed, from Buckingham Street, to a pleasant little cottage very near the one I looked at, when my enthusiasm first came on.", "우리는 버킹엄 거리에서, 내가 처음 열병에 들떴을 적에 눈여겨봐 두었던 집과 아주 가까운, 아담하고 정겨운 작은 오두막으로 이사했다."], ["My aunt, however (who has sold the house at Dover, to good advantage), is not going to remain here, but intends removing herself to a still more tiny cottage close at hand.", "그러나 고모님은 (도버의 집을 좋은 값에 파셨다) 여기 머무를 작정은 아니고, 바로 가까이에 있는 더 자그마한 오두막으로 거처를 옮기려 하신다."], ["What does this portend?", "이것이 무엇을 뜻하는가?"], ["My marriage? Yes!", "내 결혼인가? 그렇다!"]], [["Yes! I am going to be married to Dora!", "그렇다! 나는 도라와 결혼하려 한다!"], ["Miss Lavinia and Miss Clarissa have given their consent; and if ever canary birds were in a flutter, they are.", "러비니어 양과 클라리사 양도 승낙해 주었다. 카나리아 두 마리가 호들갑을 떤 적이 있다면, 바로 지금 그 두 사람이 그렇다."], ["Miss Lavinia, self-charged with the superintendence of my darling’s wardrobe, is constantly cutting out brown-paper cuirasses, and differing in opinion from a highly respectable young man, with a long bundle, and a yard measure under his arm.", "러비니어 양은 내 사랑의 혼수 단속을 자청해 맡고서, 끊임없이 갈색 종이로 갑옷 같은 본을 떠 내며, 긴 옷감 꾸러미를 들고 야드 자를 옆구리에 낀 어느 점잖은 젊은이와 의견이 엇갈린다."], ["A dressmaker, always stabbed in the breast with a needle and thread, boards and lodges in the house; and seems to me, eating, drinking, or sleeping, never to take her thimble off.", "가슴에 늘 바늘과 실을 꽂고 다니는 양재사 한 사람이 그 집에 묵으며 일하는데, 내가 보기엔 먹을 때나 마실 때나 잘 때나 골무를 손에서 빼는 법이 없는 것 같다."], ["They make a lay-figure of my dear.", "그들은 내 사랑을 마네킹으로 삼는다."], ["They are always sending for her to come and try something on.", "늘 그녀를 불러다가 이것저것 입혀 본다."], ["We can’t be happy together for five minutes in the evening, but some intrusive female knocks at the door, and says, ‘Oh, if you please, Miss Dora, would you step upstairs!’", "우리는 저녁에 단 오 분도 단둘이 다정히 있질 못하는데, 어김없이 어떤 주제넘은 여자가 문을 두드리고는 이렇게 말하기 때문이다. \"어머, 도라 아가씨, 죄송하지만 위층으로 좀 올라와 주시겠어요!\""]], [["Miss Clarissa and my aunt roam all over London, to find out articles of furniture for Dora and me to look at.", "클라리사 양과 고모님은 도라와 내가 살펴볼 가구를 찾으려고 런던 곳곳을 헤매고 다닌다."], ["It would be better for them to buy the goods at once, without this ceremony of inspection; for, when we go to see a kitchen fender and meat-screen, Dora sees a Chinese house for Jip, with little bells on the top, and prefers that.", "차라리 이런 검사 절차 없이 그냥 물건을 사 버리는 편이 두 분에게 더 나으련만. 왜냐하면 우리가 부엌 난로 가리개와 고기 굽는 가림막을 보러 가면, 도라는 꼭대기에 작은 종들이 달린, 집의 중국식 개집을 보고는 차라리 그게 더 좋다고 하기 때문이다."], ["And it takes a long time to accustom Jip to his new residence, after we have bought it; whenever he goes in or out, he makes all the little bells ring, and is horribly frightened.", "그리고 그걸 사고 나서도 집을 새 거처에 길들이는 데는 한참이 걸린다. 들어가거나 나올 때마다 집은 그 작은 종들을 죄다 울려 대며 무섭다고 질겁한다."]], [["Peggotty comes up to make herself useful, and falls to work immediately.", "패거티가 한몫 거들겠다고 올라와서는 곧장 일에 매달린다."], ["Her department appears to be, to clean everything over and over again.", "그녀가 맡은 분야는 모든 것을 닦고 또 닦는 일인 듯하다."], ["She rubs everything that can be rubbed, until it shines, like her own honest forehead, with perpetual friction.", "닦을 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든, 끊임없는 마찰로 제 정직한 이마처럼 반들반들 빛이 날 때까지 문지른다."], ["And now it is, that I begin to see her solitary brother passing through the dark streets at night, and looking, as he goes, among the wandering faces.", "그리고 바로 이 무렵에, 나는 그녀의 외로운 오라비가 밤이면 어두운 거리를 지나가며, 걸으면서 떠돌이 얼굴들 사이를 살피는 모습을 보기 시작한다."], ["I never speak to him at such an hour.", "그런 시각엔 나는 결코 그에게 말을 걸지 않는다."], ["I know too well, as his grave figure passes onward, what he seeks, and what he dreads.", "그의 무거운 모습이 앞으로 지나갈 때, 나는 그가 무엇을 찾고 무엇을 두려워하는지를 너무도 잘 알기 때문이다."]], [["Why does Traddles look so important when he calls upon me this afternoon in the Commons--where I still occasionally attend, for form’s sake, when I have time?", "오늘 오후 트래들스가 민사 법원으로 — 시간이 날 때면 나도 아직 형식상 가끔 들르는 곳이다 — 나를 찾아왔을 때, 어째서 그토록 거창한 표정을 짓는 걸까?"], ["The realization of my boyish day-dreams is at hand.", "어린 시절 내 백일몽이 실현될 날이 코앞에 닥쳤다."], ["I am going to take out the licence.", "나는 혼인 허가증을 받으러 가는 길이다."]], [["It is a little document to do so much; and Traddles contemplates it, as it lies upon my desk, half in admiration, half in awe.", "이만한 일을 해내기에는 너무도 자그마한 서류다. 그것이 내 책상 위에 놓여 있는 것을, 트래들스는 반은 감탄하며 반은 경외하며 바라본다."], ["There are the names, in the sweet old visionary connexion, David Copperfield and Dora Spenlow; and there, in the corner, is that Parental Institution, the Stamp Office, which is so benignantly interested in the various transactions of human life, looking down upon our Union; and there is the Archbishop of Canterbury invoking a blessing on us in print, and doing it as cheap as could possibly be expected.", "거기엔 그 달콤하고 오래되고 꿈결 같은 인연으로 맺어진 이름, 데이비드 코퍼필드와 도라 스펜로가 적혀 있다. 그리고 한쪽 귀퉁이엔, 인간사의 온갖 거래에 그토록 자애로이 관심을 기울이는 그 부모 같은 기관, 인지국이 우리 두 사람의 결합을 굽어보고 있다. 또한 캔터베리 대주교가 인쇄된 글자로 우리에게 축복을 빌어 주는데, 그것도 도무지 더는 바랄 수 없을 만큼 헐값에 해 주고 있다."]], [["Nevertheless, I am in a dream, a flustered, happy, hurried dream.", "그렇건만 나는 꿈속에 있다. 허둥대고, 행복하고, 정신없는 꿈속에."], ["I can’t believe that it is going to be; and yet I can’t believe but that everyone I pass in the street, must have some kind of perception, that I am to be married the day after tomorrow.", "나는 이 일이 정말로 일어나리라는 것을 믿을 수가 없다. 그러면서도, 길에서 스치는 사람마다 내가 모레 결혼한다는 것을 무언가 어렴풋이 알아챘으리라고 믿지 않을 수가 없다."], ["The Surrogate knows me, when I go down to be sworn; and disposes of me easily, as if there were a Masonic understanding between us.", "선서를 하러 내려갔을 때 대리 판사가 나를 알아보고는, 마치 우리 사이에 비밀결사 같은 묵계라도 있는 듯이 손쉽게 일을 처리해 준다."], ["Traddles is not at all wanted, but is in attendance as my general backer.", "트래들스는 전혀 필요하지 않으나, 내 전반적인 후원자로서 곁에 서 있다."]], [["‘I hope the next time you come here, my dear fellow,’ I say to Traddles, ‘it will be on the same errand for yourself.", "\"다음번에 자네가 여기 올 땐 말이야,\" 나는 트래들스에게 말한다. \"자네 자신을 위한 같은 볼일로 오게 되길 바라네."], ["And I hope it will be soon.’", "그리고 그게 어서 이루어지길 바라.\""]], [["‘Thank you for your good wishes, my dear Copperfield,’ he replies.", "\"좋게 빌어 줘서 고맙네, 코퍼필드.\" 그가 대답한다. \"나도 그러길 바라네."], ["‘I hope so too. It’s a satisfaction to know that she’ll wait for me any length of time, and that she really is the dearest girl--’", "그녀가 아무리 오래 걸려도 나를 기다려 줄 거라는 것, 그리고 그녀가 정말이지 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라는 것을 안다는 게 큰 위안이지—\""]], [["‘When are you to meet her at the coach?’ I ask.", "\"그녀를 역마차 정류장에서 언제 만나기로 했나?\" 내가 묻는다."]], [["‘At seven,’ says Traddles, looking at his plain old silver watch--the very watch he once took a wheel out of, at school, to make a water-mill.", "\"일곱 시에.\" 트래들스가 자기의 수수하고 낡은 은시계를 들여다보며 말한다. 학창 시절에 물레방아를 만들겠다고 톱니바퀴 하나를 빼낸 적이 있던 바로 그 시계다."], ["‘That is about Miss Wickfield’s time, is it not?’", "\"그쯤이면 위크필드 양이 오는 시각이지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["‘A little earlier.", "\"조금 더 이르네."], ["Her time is half past eight.’ ‘I assure you, my dear boy,’ says Traddles, ‘I am almost as pleased as if I were going to be married myself, to think that this event is coming to such a happy termination.", "그녀는 여덟 시 반이야.\" \"정말이지 말이네,\" 트래들스가 말한다."], ["And really the great friendship and consideration of personally associating Sophy with the joyful occasion, and inviting her to be a bridesmaid in conjunction with Miss Wickfield, demands my warmest thanks.", "\"이 일이 이토록 행복하게 마무리될 거라고 생각하니, 마치 내가 결혼하는 것이나 진배없이 기쁘다네. 그리고 정말로, 소피를 이 기쁜 자리에 직접 함께하게 해 주고, 위크필드 양과 더불어 신부 들러리로 청해 준 그 두터운 우정과 배려에는 가장 따뜻한 감사를 드려야 마땅하지."], ["I am extremely sensible of it.’", "나는 그걸 더없이 고맙게 여긴다네.\""]], [["I hear him, and shake hands with him; and we talk, and walk, and dine, and so on; but I don’t believe it.", "나는 그의 말을 들으며 그와 악수한다. 그리고 우리는 이야기를 나누고, 걷고, 저녁을 먹고, 그런 식으로 시간을 보낸다. 그러나 나는 그것이 믿기지 않는다."], ["Nothing is real.", "무엇 하나 실감 나지 않는다."]], [["Sophy arrives at the house of Dora’s aunts, in due course.", "소피가 때맞춰 도라의 고모들 집에 도착한다."], ["She has the most agreeable of faces,--not absolutely beautiful, but extraordinarily pleasant,--and is one of the most genial, unaffected, frank, engaging creatures I have ever seen.", "그녀는 더없이 호감 가는 얼굴을 지녔는데 — 빼어나게 아름답다고는 할 수 없으나, 더할 나위 없이 사랑스럽다 — 내가 여태껏 본 사람 가운데 가장 상냥하고, 꾸밈없고, 솔직하고, 마음을 사로잡는 이들 중 하나다."], ["Traddles presents her to us with great pride; and rubs his hands for ten minutes by the clock, with every individual hair upon his head standing on tiptoe, when I congratulate him in a corner on his choice.", "트래들스는 한껏 자랑스러워하며 그녀를 우리에게 소개한다. 그리고 내가 한쪽 구석에서 그의 선택을 축하해 주자, 그는 머리카락 한 올 한 올이 까치발을 디딘 듯 곤두선 채로 시계로 꼬박 십 분 동안 두 손을 비벼 댄다."]], [["I have brought Agnes from the Canterbury coach, and her cheerful and beautiful face is among us for the second time.", "나는 캔터베리발 역마차에서 애그니스를 데려왔고, 그녀의 밝고 아름다운 얼굴이 두 번째로 우리 사이에 끼어든다."], ["Agnes has a great liking for Traddles, and it is capital to see them meet, and to observe the glory of Traddles as he commends the dearest girl in the world to her acquaintance.", "애그니스는 트래들스를 무척 좋아하는데, 두 사람이 만나는 모습을 보는 것도, 트래들스가 세상에서 둘도 없이 사랑스러운 아가씨를 그녀에게 인사시키며 으스대는 모습을 지켜보는 것도 더할 나위 없이 즐겁다."]], [["Still I don’t believe it.", "그래도 나는 그것이 믿기지 않는다."], ["We have a delightful evening, and are supremely happy; but I don’t believe it yet.", "우리는 더없이 흐뭇한 저녁을 보내며 한껏 행복하지만, 나는 아직도 그것이 믿기지 않는다."], ["I can’t collect myself.", "도무지 정신을 가다듬을 수가 없다."], ["I can’t check off my happiness as it takes place.", "행복이 일어나는 족족 그것을 헤아려 짚어 낼 수가 없다."], ["I feel in a misty and unsettled kind of state; as if I had got up very early in the morning a week or two ago, and had never been to bed since.", "나는 안개에 휩싸인 듯 붕 뜬 상태에 빠져 있다. 마치 한두 주 전 어느 아침에 아주 일찍 일어난 뒤로, 그 후로 한 번도 잠자리에 든 적이 없는 것만 같다."], ["I can’t make out when yesterday was.", "어제가 언제였는지조차 가늠이 안 된다."], ["I seem to have been carrying the licence about, in my pocket, many months.", "그 허가증을 호주머니에 넣고 여러 달째 들고 다닌 것만 같다."]], [["Next day, too, when we all go in a flock to see the house--our house--Dora’s and mine--I am quite unable to regard myself as its master.", "이튿날도 마찬가지로, 우리 모두가 떼를 지어 그 집을 — 우리 집을, 도라와 나의 집을 — 보러 갔을 때, 나는 도무지 나 자신을 그 집의 주인으로 여길 수가 없다."], ["I seem to be there, by permission of somebody else.", "마치 다른 누군가의 허락을 받아 거기 있는 것만 같다."], ["I half expect the real master to come home presently, and say he is glad to see me.", "진짜 주인이 이내 집에 돌아와서는, 나를 보니 반갑다고 말하지나 않을까 하는 생각이 반쯤 든다."], ["Such a beautiful little house as it is, with everything so bright and new; with the flowers on the carpets looking as if freshly gathered, and the green leaves on the paper as if they had just come out; with the spotless muslin curtains, and the blushing rose-coloured furniture, and Dora’s garden hat with the blue ribbon--do I remember, now, how I loved her in such another hat when I first knew her!--already hanging on its little peg; the guitar-case quite at home on its heels in a corner; and everybody tumbling over Jip’s pagoda, which is much too big for the establishment.", "모든 것이 그토록 밝고 새것인, 어쩌면 그리도 아름답고 작은 집인지. 양탄자 위의 꽃들은 방금 꺾어 온 것만 같고, 벽지의 푸른 잎사귀들은 막 돋아난 것만 같고, 티 하나 없는 모슬린 커튼이며, 발그레한 장밋빛 가구며, 파란 리본이 달린 도라의 정원 모자가 — 그녀를 처음 알았을 적에 바로 그런 모자를 쓴 그녀를 내가 얼마나 사랑했던가, 지금도 기억나는구나! — 벌써 자그마한 못에 걸려 있고, 기타 케이스가 한쪽 구석에 발을 딛고 아주 편안히 자리 잡고 있으며, 모두가 이 살림에 비해 너무도 큰 집의 탑 모양 개집에 발이 걸려 넘어진다."], ["Another happy evening, quite as unreal as all the rest of it, and I steal into the usual room before going away.", "또 한 번 행복한 저녁이, 다른 모든 일과 똑같이 도무지 실감 나지 않는 저녁이 흐르고, 떠나기 전에 나는 슬그머니 그 늘 쓰던 방으로 들어선다."], ["Dora is not there.", "도라가 거기 없다."], ["I suppose they have not done trying on yet.", "아직 옷 입혀 보는 일이 안 끝난 모양이다."], ["Miss Lavinia peeps in, and tells me mysteriously that she will not be long.", "러비니어 양이 빼꼼히 들여다보고는, 그녀가 곧 올 거라고 알 듯 말 듯한 투로 일러 준다."], ["She is rather long, notwithstanding; but by and by I hear a rustling at the door, and someone taps.", "그런데도 그녀는 꽤나 오래 걸린다. 하지만 이윽고 나는 문께에서 사각거리는 소리를 듣고, 누군가 똑똑 두드린다."]], [["I say, ‘Come in!’ but someone taps again.", "내가 \"들어와요!\" 하고 말하지만, 누군가 또 똑똑 두드린다."]], [["I go to the door, wondering who it is; there, I meet a pair of bright eyes, and a blushing face; they are Dora’s eyes and face, and Miss Lavinia has dressed her in tomorrow’s dress, bonnet and all, for me to see. I take my little wife to my heart; and Miss Lavinia gives a little scream because I tumble the bonnet, and Dora laughs and cries at once, because I am so pleased; and I believe it less than ever.", "나는 누구일까 궁금해하며 문으로 간다. 거기서 나는 한 쌍의 반짝이는 눈과 발그레한 얼굴을 마주한다. 그것은 도라의 눈과 얼굴이다. 러비니어 양이 나에게 보여 주려고, 모자까지 갖춰서 내일 입을 옷을 도라에게 입혀 놓은 것이다. 나는 내 작은 아내를 가슴에 끌어안는다. 그러자 러비니어 양이 내가 모자를 흐트러뜨렸다고 조그맣게 비명을 지르고, 도라는 내가 너무 기뻐하니까 한꺼번에 웃다가 운다. 그리고 나는 그 어느 때보다도 더 이것이 믿기지 않는다."]], [["‘Do you think it pretty, Doady?’ says Dora.", "\"이거 예뻐요, 도디?\" 도라가 말한다."]], [["Pretty! I should rather think I did.", "예쁘냐고! 두말하면 잔소리다."]], [["‘And are you sure you like me very much?’ says Dora.", "\"그리고 저를 정말 무척 사랑하는 거 맞아요?\" 도라가 말한다."]], [["The topic is fraught with such danger to the bonnet, that Miss Lavinia gives another little scream, and begs me to understand that Dora is only to be looked at, and on no account to be touched.", "이 화제는 모자에 너무도 위험천만한지라, 러비니어 양이 또 한 번 조그맣게 비명을 지르고는, 도라는 오로지 바라보기만 해야 하며 결코 손을 대서는 안 된다는 것을 명심하라고 나에게 신신당부한다. 그래서 도라는 일이 분 동안 흐뭇하리만치 어쩔 줄 몰라하는 채로 서서 찬사를 받는다."], ["So Dora stands in a delightful state of confusion for a minute or two, to be admired; and then takes off her bonnet--looking so natural without it!--and runs away with it in her hand; and comes dancing down again in her own familiar dress, and asks Jip if I have got a beautiful little wife, and whether he’ll forgive her for being married, and kneels down to make him stand upon the cookery-book, for the last time in her single life.", "그러더니 모자를 벗는데 — 그걸 벗으니 어찌나 자연스러워 보이는지! — 그것을 손에 든 채 달아나 버린다. 그러고는 늘 보던 제 평상복 차림으로 다시 춤추듯 내려와, 집에게 내가 아름답고 작은 아내를 얻은 거냐고, 그리고 이제 자기가 결혼한 걸 용서해 주겠느냐고 묻고는, 처녀로 사는 마지막으로 집을 요리책 위에 올라서게 하려고 무릎을 꿇는다."]], [["I go home, more incredulous than ever, to a lodging that I have hard by; and get up very early in the morning, to ride to the Highgate road and fetch my aunt.", "나는 그 어느 때보다도 더 믿기지 않는 심정으로, 바로 가까이에 잡아 둔 하숙집으로 돌아간다. 그리고 아침 일찍 일어나, 하이게이트 길로 말을 달려 고모님을 모시러 간다."]], [["I have never seen my aunt in such state.", "나는 고모님이 그토록 차려입은 모습을 본 적이 없다."], ["She is dressed in lavender-coloured silk, and has a white bonnet on, and is amazing.", "고모님은 라벤더빛 비단옷을 입고 흰 모자를 쓰고 계신데, 보기에 놀랍기 그지없다."], ["Janet has dressed her, and is there to look at me. Peggotty is ready to go to church, intending to behold the ceremony from the gallery. Mr.", "재닛이 그분을 단장해 드리고는, 나를 보려고 거기 서 있다. 패거티는 교회에 갈 채비를 마치고, 회랑에서 예식을 지켜볼 작정이다."], ["Dick, who is to give my darling to me at the altar, has had his hair curled.", "제단 앞에서 내 사랑을 나에게 건네줄 딕 씨는 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 말았다."], ["Traddles, whom I have taken up by appointment at the turnpike, presents a dazzling combination of cream colour and light blue; and both he and Mr. Dick have a general effect about them of being all gloves.", "약속대로 통행료 징수소에서 태워 온 트래들스는, 크림빛과 연파랑이 어우러진 눈부신 차림새를 뽐낸다. 그리고 그와 딕 씨 둘 다, 온통 장갑투성이라는 인상을 전체적으로 풍긴다."]], [["No doubt I see this, because I know it is so; but I am astray, and seem to see nothing.", "물론 나는 이것이 그러하다는 걸 알기에 보는 것이지, 사실 나는 정신이 나가서 아무것도 보이지 않는 것 같다."], ["Nor do I believe anything whatever.", "또한 무엇 하나 도무지 믿기지도 않는다."], ["Still, as we drive along in an open carriage, this fairy marriage is real enough to fill me with a sort of wondering pity for the unfortunate people who have no part in it, but are sweeping out the shops, and going to their daily occupations.", "그래도, 우리가 지붕 없는 마차를 타고 달리는 동안, 이 동화 같은 결혼은 충분히 실감 나서, 거기에 아무 몫도 없이 그저 가게를 쓸어 내고 그날그날의 생업으로 향하는 불운한 사람들에게 묘한 연민 비슷한 것이 차오를 만큼은 된다."]], [["My aunt sits with my hand in hers all the way.", "고모님은 가는 내내 내 손을 당신 손에 쥐고 앉아 계신다."], ["When we stop a little way short of the church, to put down Peggotty, whom we have brought on the box, she gives it a squeeze, and me a kiss.", "우리가 마차 위 바깥자리에 태우고 온 패거티를 내려 주려고 교회 조금 못 미쳐 멈춰 섰을 때, 고모님은 내 손을 꼭 쥐고 나에게 입을 맞춰 주신다."]], [["‘God bless you, Trot!", "\"신의 가호가 있기를, 트롯!"], ["My own boy never could be dearer.", "내 친자식인들 너보다 더 사랑스러울 수는 없을 게다."], ["I think of poor dear Baby this morning.’ ‘So do I.", "오늘 아침엔 가엾고 사랑스러운 그 아기 생각이 나는구나.\" \"저도요."], ["And of all I owe to you, dear aunt.’", "그리고 제가 고모님께 진 모든 신세도요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘Tut, child!’ says my aunt; and gives her hand in overflowing cordiality to Traddles, who then gives his to Mr.", "\"쯧, 얘야!\" 고모님이 말씀하시며, 넘쳐흐르는 다정함으로 트래들스에게 손을 내미신다."], ["Dick, who then gives his to me, who then gives mine to Traddles, and then we come to the church door.", "그러자 트래들스가 딕 씨에게 손을 내밀고, 딕 씨가 나에게 손을 내밀고, 내가 다시 트래들스에게 손을 내미는데, 그러다 보니 우리는 교회 문에 다다른다."]], [["The church is calm enough, I am sure; but it might be a steam-power loom in full action, for any sedative effect it has on me.", "교회는 분명 충분히 고요하다. 하지만 그것이 나를 가라앉히는 효과로 따지자면, 차라리 한창 돌아가는 증기 동력 직조기라 해도 좋을 정도다."], ["I am too far gone for that.", "나는 그러기엔 이미 너무 멀리 가 버렸다."]], [["The rest is all a more or less incoherent dream.", "나머지는 죄다, 어느 정도 갈피를 못 잡는 꿈이다."]], [["A dream of their coming in with Dora; of the pew-opener arranging us, like a drill-sergeant, before the altar rails; of my wondering, even then, why pew-openers must always be the most disagreeable females procurable, and whether there is any religious dread of a disastrous infection of good-humour which renders it indispensable to set those vessels of vinegar upon the road to Heaven.", "그들이 도라와 함께 들어오는 꿈. 신도석 안내인이 우리를 훈련 교관처럼 제단 난간 앞에 정렬시키는 꿈. 그러면서도 나는, 그 와중에도, 어째서 신도석 안내인이란 늘 구할 수 있는 한 가장 못마땅한 여인들이어야 하는지, 혹시 좋은 기분이 옮아 재앙처럼 번지는 것을 막으려는 어떤 종교적 두려움이 있어서, 천국으로 가는 길에 그런 식초 단지 같은 여인들을 세워 두는 것이 꼭 필요하다 여기는 건 아닌지 궁금해하는 꿈."]], [["Of the clergyman and clerk appearing; of a few boatmen and some other people strolling in; of an ancient mariner behind me, strongly flavouring the church with rum; of the service beginning in a deep voice, and our all being very attentive.", "목사와 서기가 나타나는 꿈. 뱃사공 몇과 다른 사람 몇이 어슬렁어슬렁 들어오는 꿈. 내 뒤에 있던 늙은 뱃사람 하나가 럼주 냄새를 교회 안에 진하게 풍기는 꿈. 예식이 굵직한 목소리로 시작되고, 우리 모두가 아주 정중히 귀를 기울이는 꿈."]], [["Of Miss Lavinia, who acts as a semi-auxiliary bridesmaid, being the first to cry, and of her doing homage (as I take it) to the memory of Pidger, in sobs; of Miss Clarissa applying a smelling-bottle; of Agnes taking care of Dora; of my aunt endeavouring to represent herself as a model of sternness, with tears rolling down her face; of little Dora trembling very much, and making her responses in faint whispers.", "준보조 신부 들러리 노릇을 하는 러비니어 양이 맨 먼저 우는 꿈. 그리고 그녀가 (내가 보기엔) 흐느낌으로 피저의 추억에 경의를 표하는 꿈. 클라리사 양이 냄새 맡는 병을 갖다 대는 꿈. 애그니스가 도라를 보살피는 꿈. 고모님이 얼굴에 눈물을 줄줄 흘리면서도 자신을 근엄함의 본보기로 꾸며 보이려 애쓰는 꿈. 자그마한 도라가 몹시 떨면서, 가냘픈 속삭임으로 서약의 응답을 하는 꿈."]], [["Of our kneeling down together, side by side; of Dora’s trembling less and less, but always clasping Agnes by the hand; of the service being got through, quietly and gravely; of our all looking at each other in an April state of smiles and tears, when it is over; of my young wife being hysterical in the vestry, and crying for her poor papa, her dear papa.", "우리가 나란히 함께 무릎을 꿇는 꿈. 도라가 점점 덜 떨면서도, 줄곧 애그니스의 손을 꼭 붙잡고 있는 꿈. 예식이 조용하고 엄숙하게 치러지는 꿈. 그것이 끝나자 우리 모두가 사월처럼 웃음과 눈물이 뒤섞인 채로 서로를 바라보는 꿈. 내 젊은 아내가 제의실에서 신경이 북받쳐, 자기의 가엾은 아빠를, 사랑하는 아빠를 부르며 우는 꿈."]], [["Of her soon cheering up again, and our signing the register all round.", "그녀가 이내 다시 기운을 차리고, 우리 모두가 둘러서서 혼인 명부에 서명하는 꿈."], ["Of my going into the gallery for Peggotty to bring her to sign it; of Peggotty’s hugging me in a corner, and telling me she saw my own dear mother married; of its being over, and our going away.", "내가 패거티를 데려와 서명하게 하려고 회랑으로 올라가는 꿈. 패거티가 한쪽 구석에서 나를 끌어안고, 내 사랑하는 어머니가 결혼하던 모습을 자기가 지켜봤노라고 일러 주는 꿈. 그것이 다 끝나고, 우리가 떠나는 꿈."]], [["Of my walking so proudly and lovingly down the aisle with my sweet wife upon my arm, through a mist of half-seen people, pulpits, monuments, pews, fonts, organs, and church windows, in which there flutter faint airs of association with my childish church at home, so long ago.", "내가 사랑스러운 아내를 팔에 끼고서, 반쯤만 보이는 사람들과 설교단들, 기념비들, 신도석들, 세례반들, 오르간들, 그리고 교회 창문들이 자아내는 안개 사이를, 그토록 자랑스럽고 다정하게 통로를 걸어 내려가는 꿈. 그 안개 속에는 그 옛날 고향의 어린 시절 교회와 얽힌 어렴풋한 회상의 기운이 가물가물 떠다니는 것이었다."]], [["Of their whispering, as we pass, what a youthful couple we are, and what a pretty little wife she is.", "우리가 지나갈 때 사람들이, 어쩌면 저리도 젊은 한 쌍인지, 또 어쩌면 저리도 어여쁘고 작은 아내인지 하고 속삭이는 꿈."], ["Of our all being so merry and talkative in the carriage going back.", "돌아가는 마차 안에서 우리 모두가 그토록 흥겹고 수다스러운 꿈."], ["Of Sophy telling us that when she saw Traddles (whom I had entrusted with the licence) asked for it, she almost fainted, having been convinced that he would contrive to lose it, or to have his pocket picked.", "소피가, 트래들스에게 (내가 그에게 허가증을 맡겼다) 그것을 내놓으라 했을 때, 그가 어떻게든 그걸 잃어버리거나 소매치기를 당할 거라고 굳게 믿었던지라 까무러칠 뻔했다고 우리에게 일러 주는 꿈."], ["Of Agnes laughing gaily; and of Dora being so fond of Agnes that she will not be separated from her, but still keeps her hand.", "애그니스가 명랑하게 웃는 꿈. 그리고 도라가 애그니스를 어찌나 좋아하는지, 그녀와 떨어지려 하지 않고 여태 그 손을 꼭 잡고 있는 꿈."]], [["Of there being a breakfast, with abundance of things, pretty and substantial, to eat and drink, whereof I partake, as I should do in any other dream, without the least perception of their flavour; eating and drinking, as I may say, nothing but love and marriage, and no more believing in the viands than in anything else.", "먹고 마실, 예쁘고도 든든한 것들이 잔뜩 차려진 아침 잔치가 있는 꿈. 나도 거기에 한몫 끼는데, 다른 어떤 꿈에서나 그러하듯, 그 맛을 조금도 느끼지 못한 채로 먹고 마신다. 말하자면, 나는 사랑과 결혼 말고는 아무것도 먹고 마시지 않으며, 그 음식들도 다른 무엇과 마찬가지로 도무지 믿기지 않는 것이다."]], [["Of my making a speech in the same dreamy fashion, without having an idea of what I want to say, beyond such as may be comprehended in the full conviction that I haven’t said it.", "내가 똑같이 꿈결 같은 투로 연설을 하는 꿈. 내가 무슨 말을 하고 싶은지 도무지 모르겠고, 다만 내가 그 말을 아직 하지 않았다는 확신 속에 담길 만한 것밖에는 떠오르지 않는다."], ["Of our being very sociably and simply happy (always in a dream though); and of Jip’s having wedding cake, and its not agreeing with him afterwards.", "우리가 아주 정답고 소박하게 행복한 (그래도 늘 꿈속에서지만) 꿈. 집이 결혼 케이크를 먹고, 그게 나중에 탈을 일으키는 꿈."]], [["Of the pair of hired post-horses being ready, and of Dora’s going away to change her dress.", "세를 낸 역마 두 마리가 채비를 마치고, 도라가 옷을 갈아입으러 가는 꿈."], ["Of my aunt and Miss Clarissa remaining with us; and our walking in the garden; and my aunt, who has made quite a speech at breakfast touching Dora’s aunts, being mightily amused with herself, but a little proud of it too.", "고모님과 클라리사 양이 우리와 함께 남고, 우리가 정원을 거니는 꿈. 그리고 아침 잔치에서 도라의 고모들을 두고 제법 일장 연설을 한 고모님이, 스스로 무척 흐뭇해하면서도 한편으로 조금은 우쭐해하는 꿈."]], [["Of Dora’s being ready, and of Miss Lavinia’s hovering about her, loth to lose the pretty toy that has given her so much pleasant occupation.", "도라가 채비를 마치고, 러비니어 양이 그토록 즐거운 소일거리를 안겨 주던 그 어여쁜 장난감을 잃기 아쉬워 그녀 곁을 맴도는 꿈."], ["Of Dora’s making a long series of surprised discoveries that she has forgotten all sorts of little things; and of everybody’s running everywhere to fetch them.", "도라가 온갖 자질구레한 것들을 깜박 잊었다는 사실을 잇따라 놀라며 새로 깨닫고, 모두가 그것을 가지러 사방으로 뛰어다니는 꿈."]], [["Of their all closing about Dora, when at last she begins to say good-bye, looking, with their bright colours and ribbons, like a bed of flowers.", "마침내 도라가 작별 인사를 하기 시작하자, 다들 그녀를 빙 둘러싸는데, 그 화사한 빛깔과 리본들이 마치 한 무더기 꽃밭처럼 보이는 꿈."], ["Of my darling being almost smothered among the flowers, and coming out, laughing and crying both together, to my jealous arms.", "내 사랑이 그 꽃들 사이에서 거의 파묻힐 뻔하다가, 웃음과 울음을 한꺼번에 터뜨리며 질투에 찬 내 품으로 빠져나오는 꿈."]], [["Of my wanting to carry Jip (who is to go along with us), and Dora’s saying no, that she must carry him, or else he’ll think she don’t like him any more, now she is married, and will break his heart.", "내가 (우리와 함께 갈) 집을 안고 가려 하자, 도라가 안 된다고, 자기가 그를 안고 가야 한다고, 안 그러면 이제 자기가 결혼했으니 더는 자기를 좋아하지 않는다고 그가 여겨 마음이 찢어질 거라고 말하는 꿈."], ["Of our going, arm in arm, and Dora stopping and looking back, and saying, ‘If I have ever been cross or ungrateful to anybody, don’t remember it!’ and bursting into tears.", "우리가 팔짱을 끼고 걸어가는데, 도라가 멈춰 서서 뒤돌아보며, \"혹시 제가 누구한테든 심술궂거나 고마운 줄 모르게 군 적이 있더라도, 부디 기억하지 말아 줘요!\" 하고 말하고는 울음을 터뜨리는 꿈."]], [["Of her waving her little hand, and our going away once more.", "그녀가 작은 손을 흔들고, 우리가 또 한 번 떠나가는 꿈."], ["Of her once more stopping, and looking back, and hurrying to Agnes, and giving Agnes, above all the others, her last kisses and farewells.", "그녀가 또 한 번 멈춰 서서 뒤돌아보고는, 애그니스에게로 달려가, 다른 누구보다도 애그니스에게 마지막 입맞춤과 작별 인사를 건네는 꿈."]], [["We drive away together, and I awake from the dream.", "우리는 함께 마차를 타고 달려가고, 나는 꿈에서 깨어난다."], ["I believe it at last.", "나는 마침내 그것을 믿는다."], ["It is my dear, dear, little wife beside me, whom I love so well!", "내 곁에 있는 것은 내가 그토록 사랑하는, 사랑스럽고 사랑스러운 내 작은 아내다!"]], [["‘Are you happy now, you foolish boy?’ says Dora, ‘and sure you don’t repent?’", "\"이제 행복해요, 이 바보 같은 양반?\" 도라가 말한다. \"그리고 후회하지 않는 거 맞죠?\""]], [["I have stood aside to see the phantoms of those days go by me.", "나는 그 시절의 환영들이 내 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 모습을 보려고 한쪽으로 비켜서 있었다."], ["They are gone, and I resume the journey of my story.", "그것들은 지나가 버렸고, 나는 다시 내 이야기의 여정을 이어 간다."]], [["# CHAPTER 44. OUR HOUSEKEEPING", "# CHAPTER 44. OUR HOUSEKEEPING"]], [["It was a strange condition of things, the honeymoon being over, and the bridesmaids gone home, when I found myself sitting down in my own small house with Dora; quite thrown out of employment, as I may say, in respect of the delicious old occupation of making love.", "신혼여행도 끝나고 신부 들러리들도 다 집으로 돌아간 뒤, 도라와 함께 우리 집 작은 거실에 마주 앉아 있는 나 자신을 발견했을 때, 그것은 참으로 묘한 상황이었다. 말하자면 나는, 그 달콤하고도 오래된 일거리 — 사랑을 하는 일 — 에 관한 한 졸지에 실업자가 된 셈이었다."]], [["It seemed such an extraordinary thing to have Dora always there.", "도라가 늘 거기 있다는 것은 이루 말할 수 없이 신기한 일이었다."], ["It was so unaccountable not to be obliged to go out to see her, not to have any occasion to be tormenting myself about her, not to have to write to her, not to be scheming and devising opportunities of being alone with her.", "그녀를 보러 일부러 나가지 않아도 된다는 것, 그녀 때문에 마음 졸이며 나를 들볶을 까닭이 없다는 것, 그녀에게 편지를 쓸 일이 없다는 것, 그녀와 단둘이 있을 기회를 궁리하고 꾸밀 필요가 없다는 것이 도무지 믿기지 않았다."], ["Sometimes of an evening, when I looked up from my writing, and saw her seated opposite, I would lean back in my chair, and think how queer it was that there we were, alone together as a matter of course--nobody’s business any more--all the romance of our engagement put away upon a shelf, to rust--no one to please but one another--one another to please, for life.", "가끔 저녁 무렵, 글을 쓰다가 고개를 들어 맞은편에 앉은 그녀를 보면, 나는 의자에 등을 기대고 이런 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 우리가 이렇게 단둘이, 당연한 듯 함께 있다니 — 더는 누구의 참견거리도 아니고 — 우리 약혼 시절의 그 모든 낭만은 선반에 얹혀 녹이나 슬도록 치워졌으며 — 기쁘게 해 줄 사람이라곤 서로밖에 없고 — 평생토록 서로를 기쁘게 해 줄 뿐이라니, 참으로 이상한 노릇이다, 하고."]], [["When there was a debate, and I was kept out very late, it seemed so strange to me, as I was walking home, to think that Dora was at home!", "토론회가 열려 아주 늦게까지 붙들려 있다가, 집으로 걸어 돌아오면서 도라가 집에 있다는 생각을 떠올리는 것은 어찌나 묘하던지!"], ["It was such a wonderful thing, at first, to have her coming softly down to talk to me as I ate my supper.", "처음에는, 내가 저녁을 먹는 동안 그녀가 살그머니 내려와 나에게 말을 거는 것이 얼마나 신기한 일이었던가."], ["It was such a stupendous thing to know for certain that she put her hair in papers.", "그녀가 머리를 종이로 말아 둔다는 것을 확실히 알게 된 것은 또 얼마나 놀라운 일이었던가."], ["It was altogether such an astonishing event to see her do it!", "그녀가 실제로 그렇게 하는 모습을 보는 것은 정말이지 통째로 경이로운 사건이었다!"]], [["I doubt whether two young birds could have known less about keeping house, than I and my pretty Dora did.", "나와 내 예쁜 도라보다 살림에 대해 더 모르는 어린 새 두 마리가 과연 있었을까 싶다. 물론 우리에게는 하녀가 있었다."], ["We had a servant, of course.", "그 여자가 우리 살림을 맡아 주었다."], ["She kept house for us. I have still a latent belief that she must have been Mrs. Crupp’s daughter in disguise, we had such an awful time of it with Mary Anne.", "나는 아직도, 그 여자가 사실은 크럽 부인의 딸이 변장하고 온 게 아닐까 하는 속내를 은근히 품고 있는데, 메리 앤과 함께 우리가 겪은 고생이 그만큼 끔찍했기 때문이다."]], [["Her name was Paragon.", "그 여자의 이름은 패러건이었다."], ["Her nature was represented to us, when we engaged her, as being feebly expressed in her name.", "우리가 그 여자를 고용할 때, 그 천성은 이름으로 다 담아내기엔 모자랄 정도라는 식으로 소개를 받았다."], ["She had a written character, as large as a proclamation; and, according to this document, could do everything of a domestic nature that ever I heard of, and a great many things that I never did hear of.", "그 여자에게는 포고문만큼이나 큼지막한 추천서가 있었는데, 그 문서에 따르자면 내가 들어 본 적 있는 온갖 집안일은 물론, 내가 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 일까지 죄다 해낼 수 있다고 적혀 있었다."], ["She was a woman in the prime of life; of a severe countenance; and subject (particularly in the arms) to a sort of perpetual measles or fiery rash.", "그 여자는 한창나이의 여인으로, 표정이 험상궂었고, (특히 두 팔에) 일종의 영구적인 홍역이나 시뻘건 발진을 달고 사는 사람이었다."], ["She had a cousin in the Life-Guards, with such long legs that he looked like the afternoon shadow of somebody else.", "그 여자에게는 근위 기병대에 사촌이 하나 있었는데, 다리가 어찌나 길던지 마치 다른 누군가의 오후 그림자처럼 보였다."], ["His shell-jacket was as much too little for him as he was too big for the premises.", "그의 짧은 군복 상의가 그에게 터무니없이 작았던 만큼, 그도 우리 집에 비해 터무니없이 컸다."], ["He made the cottage smaller than it need have been, by being so very much out of proportion to it.", "그는 워낙 집과 비례가 맞지 않아서, 있어야 할 것보다 우리 오두막을 더 좁아 보이게 만들었다."], ["Besides which, the walls were not thick, and, whenever he passed the evening at our house, we always knew of it by hearing one continual growl in the kitchen.", "게다가 벽이 두껍지 않은 탓에, 그가 우리 집에서 저녁을 보낼 때면 우리는 부엌에서 끊임없이 울리는 으르렁 소리만 듣고도 늘 그가 와 있음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["Our treasure was warranted sober and honest.", "우리의 보물 같은 하녀는 술도 안 마시고 정직하다고 보증되어 있었다."], ["I am therefore willing to believe that she was in a fit when we found her under the boiler; and that the deficient tea-spoons were attributable to the dustman.", "그러니 나는 기꺼이 이렇게 믿어 두련다. 우리가 그 여자를 보일러 밑에서 발견했을 때 그것은 발작을 일으킨 것이었으며, 모자란 찻숟가락들은 쓰레기 치우는 인부 탓이었다고 말이다."]], [["But she preyed upon our minds dreadfully.", "그러나 그 여자는 우리 마음을 무섭게 갉아먹었다."], ["We felt our inexperience, and were unable to help ourselves.", "우리는 우리 자신의 미숙함을 절감했으되, 어찌해 볼 도리가 없었다."], ["We should have been at her mercy, if she had had any; but she was a remorseless woman, and had none.", "그 여자에게 조금이라도 자비심이 있었다면 우리는 그 자비에 매달렸을 테지만, 그 여자는 인정사정없는 사람이라 그런 것이 전혀 없었다."], ["She was the cause of our first little quarrel.", "그 여자가 바로 우리 부부 첫 말다툼의 빌미였다."]], [["‘My dearest life,’ I said one day to Dora, ‘do you think Mary Anne has any idea of time?’", "\"여보, 내 사랑.\" 어느 날 나는 도라에게 말했다. \"메리 앤이 시간 개념이라는 게 있기는 한 것 같소?\""]], [["‘Why, Doady?’ inquired Dora, looking up, innocently, from her drawing.", "\"왜요, 도디?\" 도라가 그림을 그리다 천진하게 고개를 들며 물었다."]], [["‘My love, because it’s five, and we were to have dined at four.’", "\"여보, 다섯 시인데 우리는 네 시에 밥을 먹기로 했으니까 그렇지.\""]], [["Dora glanced wistfully at the clock, and hinted that she thought it was too fast.", "도라는 시계를 아쉬운 듯 흘끗 보더니, 시계가 너무 빠른 것 같다고 넌지시 말했다."]], [["‘On the contrary, my love,’ said I, referring to my watch, ‘it’s a few minutes too slow.’", "\"천만에, 여보.\" 나는 회중시계를 들여다보며 말했다. \"오히려 몇 분 느린걸.\""]], [["My little wife came and sat upon my knee, to coax me to be quiet, and drew a line with her pencil down the middle of my nose; but I couldn’t dine off that, though it was very agreeable.", "내 작은 아내는 다가와 내 무릎에 앉아, 그만 조용히 하라고 나를 어르며, 연필로 내 코 한복판에 줄을 죽 그었다. 하지만 그게 아무리 기분 좋은 일이라 해도, 그걸 밥으로 삼아 먹을 수는 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["‘Don’t you think, my dear,’ said I, ‘it would be better for you to remonstrate with Mary Anne?’", "\"여보, 그러지 말고.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 메리 앤에게 한마디 따끔하게 일러두는 편이 낫지 않겠소?\""]], [["‘Oh no, please!", "\"어머, 안 돼요, 제발!"], ["I couldn’t, Doady!’ said Dora.", "난 못 해요, 도디!\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["‘Why not, my love?’ I gently asked.", "\"왜 못 하오, 여보?\" 나는 부드럽게 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, because I am such a little goose,’ said Dora, ‘and she knows I am!’", "\"아유, 난 이렇게 어리바리한 바보인데,\" 도라가 말했다. \"그 애도 내가 그런 걸 다 안단 말이에요!\""]], [["I thought this sentiment so incompatible with the establishment of any system of check on Mary Anne, that I frowned a little.", "나는 이런 생각이 메리 앤에 대한 어떤 통제 체계를 세우는 일과는 도무지 어울리지 않는다고 여겨, 살짝 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["‘Oh, what ugly wrinkles in my bad boy’s forehead!’ said Dora, and still being on my knee, she traced them with her pencil; putting it to her rosy lips to make it mark blacker, and working at my forehead with a quaint little mockery of being industrious, that quite delighted me in spite of myself.", "\"어머, 우리 나쁜 도련님 이마에 보기 싫은 주름이 잡혔네!\" 도라가 말했다. 그러고는 여전히 내 무릎에 앉은 채로, 연필로 그 주름을 따라 그렸다. 더 검게 그려지라고 연필을 제 발그레한 입술에 갖다 댄 다음, 부지런한 척 익살스럽게 내 이마를 매만지는데, 나도 모르게 그만 기분이 한껏 좋아졌다."]], [["‘There’s a good child,’ said Dora, ‘it makes its face so much prettier to laugh.’ ‘But, my love,’ said I.", "\"아유, 착하지.\" 도라가 말했다. \"웃으니까 얼굴이 훨씬 예쁘잖아.\" \"하지만, 여보.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no!", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼! 제발!\""], ["please!’ cried Dora, with a kiss, ‘don’t be a naughty Blue Beard!", "도라가 입을 맞추며 외쳤다. \"심술궂은 푸른 수염은 되지 말아요!"], ["Don’t be serious!’", "진지해지지 말아요!\""]], [["‘My precious wife,’ said I, ‘we must be serious sometimes. Come!", "* 푸른 수염: 아내를 여럿 죽인 프랑스 동화 속 잔혹한 남편. \"내 소중한 아내여.\""], ["Sit down on this chair, close beside me!", "내가 말했다. \"사람이 때로는 진지해져야 하는 법이오. 자!"], ["Give me the pencil! There!", "이 의자에, 내 바로 옆에 앉아요!"], ["Now let us talk sensibly.", "연필 이리 줘요! 자!"], ["You know, dear’; what a little hand it was to hold, and what a tiny wedding-ring it was to see!", "이제 우리 사리에 맞게 얘기해 봅시다. 알다시피, 여보\" — 손을 잡으니 어찌나 작은 손이며, 결혼반지는 또 어찌나 앙증맞던지!"], ["‘You know, my love, it is not exactly comfortable to have to go out without one’s dinner.", "— \"알다시피, 여보, 밥도 못 먹고 일하러 나가야 한다는 건 그리 편한 노릇이 아니잖소."], ["Now, is it?’", "자, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["‘N-n-no!’ replied Dora, faintly.", "\"아—아—니요!\" 도라가 가냘프게 대답했다."]], [["‘My love, how you tremble!’", "\"여보, 왜 그리 떠는 거요!\""]], [["‘Because I KNOW you’re going to scold me,’ exclaimed Dora, in a piteous voice.", "\"당신이 날 야단치려는 걸 다 아니까요.\" 도라가 가엾은 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["‘My sweet, I am only going to reason.’", "\"내 사랑, 나는 그저 차근차근 따져 보려는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["‘Oh, but reasoning is worse than scolding!’ exclaimed Dora, in despair.", "\"어머, 차근차근 따지는 게 야단치는 것보다 더 나빠요!\" 도라가 절망하며 외쳤다."], ["‘I didn’t marry to be reasoned with.", "\"난 따짐을 당하려고 결혼한 게 아니에요."], ["If you meant to reason with such a poor little thing as I am, you ought to have told me so, you cruel boy!’", "나처럼 가엾고 작은 애를 붙잡고 따질 생각이었다면, 미리 말해 줬어야죠, 이 매정한 도련님!\""]], [["I tried to pacify Dora, but she turned away her face, and shook her curls from side to side, and said, ‘You cruel, cruel boy!’ so many times, that I really did not exactly know what to do: so I took a few turns up and down the room in my uncertainty, and came back again.", "나는 도라를 달래 보려 했지만, 그녀는 얼굴을 돌리고 곱슬머리를 이리저리 흔들며 \"매정해, 매정한 도련님!\" 하고 어찌나 여러 번 되뇌던지, 나는 정말 어찌해야 좋을지 알 수가 없었다. 그래서 어쩔 줄 모른 채 방 안을 몇 번 왔다 갔다 하다가 다시 그녀에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["‘Dora, my darling!’", "\"도라, 내 사랑!\""]], [["‘No, I am not your darling.", "\"아니, 난 당신 사랑이 아니에요."], ["Because you must be sorry that you married me, or else you wouldn’t reason with me!’ returned Dora.", "당신은 나랑 결혼한 걸 분명 후회하고 있으니까요. 안 그러면 나랑 따지려 들지 않았을 거예요!\" 도라가 받아쳤다."]], [["I felt so injured by the inconsequential nature of this charge, that it gave me courage to be grave.", "나는 이렇게 앞뒤 안 맞는 비난에 어찌나 억울하던지, 도리어 그 억울함이 진지해질 용기를 주었다."]], [["‘Now, my own Dora,’ said I, ‘you are very childish, and are talking nonsense.", "\"자, 나의 도라.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신은 너무 어린애 같고, 말도 안 되는 소리를 하고 있소."], ["You must remember, I am sure, that I was obliged to go out yesterday when dinner was half over; and that, the day before, I was made quite unwell by being obliged to eat underdone veal in a hurry; today, I don’t dine at all--and I am afraid to say how long we waited for breakfast--and then the water didn’t boil.", "분명히 기억할 거요, 어제 나는 밥을 반쯤 먹다 말고 나가야 했고, 그저께는 덜 익은 송아지 고기를 허겁지겁 먹는 바람에 속이 영 안 좋았다는 걸. 오늘은 아예 밥을 못 먹고 있고 — 아침 먹으려고 얼마나 기다렸는지는 입에 담기도 무서울 지경이오 — 게다가 물도 끓질 않았소."], ["I don’t mean to reproach you, my dear, but this is not comfortable.’", "당신을 나무라려는 게 아니오, 여보. 하지만 이건 편한 살림이 아니란 말이오.\""]], [["‘Oh, you cruel, cruel boy, to say I am a disagreeable wife!’ cried Dora.", "\"어머, 매정해, 매정한 도련님, 내가 마뜩잖은 아내라는 거잖아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Now, my dear Dora, you must know that I never said that!’", "\"자, 나의 도라, 내가 그런 말은 절대 안 한 걸 당신도 알 거 아니오!\""]], [["‘You said, I wasn’t comfortable!’ cried Dora.", "\"내가 편하지 않다고 했잖아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다."], ["‘I said the housekeeping was not comfortable!’", "\"나는 살림이 편하지 않다고 했소!\""]], [["‘It’s exactly the same thing!’ cried Dora.", "\"그게 그거잖아요!\" 도라가 외쳤다."], ["And she evidently thought so, for she wept most grievously.", "그리고 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 여기는 게 분명했다. 너무도 서럽게 울었으니까."]], [["I took another turn across the room, full of love for my pretty wife, and distracted by self-accusatory inclinations to knock my head against the door.", "나는 예쁜 아내에 대한 사랑으로 가슴이 벅찬 동시에, 차라리 문에다 머리를 박아 버리고 싶은 자책감에 정신이 산란한 채로, 또 한 번 방을 가로질러 서성였다."], ["I sat down again, and said:", "그러고는 다시 앉아 말했다."]], [["‘I am not blaming you, Dora.", "\"당신을 탓하는 게 아니오, 도라."], ["We have both a great deal to learn.", "우리 둘 다 배워야 할 게 산더미요."], ["I am only trying to show you, my dear, that you must--you really must’ (I was resolved not to give this up)--‘accustom yourself to look after Mary Anne.", "나는 그저, 여보, 당신이 — 정말로 당신이\" (나는 이것만은 포기하지 않기로 작정했다) — \"메리 앤을 단속하는 데 익숙해져야 한다는 걸 보여 주려는 것뿐이오."], ["Likewise to act a little for yourself, and me.’", "그리고 당신 자신을 위해서도, 나를 위해서도, 조금은 알아서 처신해야 한다는 것을.\""]], [["‘I wonder, I do, at your making such ungrateful speeches,’ sobbed Dora.", "\"어쩜, 정말이지, 당신이 그렇게 배은망덕한 소릴 하다니 놀라워요.\""], ["‘When you know that the other day, when you said you would like a little bit of fish, I went out myself, miles and miles, and ordered it, to surprise you.’", "도라가 흐느꼈다. \"지난번에 당신이 생선이 좀 먹고 싶다고 했을 때, 내가 깜짝 놀라게 해 주려고 몇십 리나 직접 나가서 주문해 온 걸 다 알면서!\""]], [["‘And it was very kind of you, my own darling,’ said I.", "\"그건 정말 고마운 일이었소, 내 사랑.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I felt it so much that I wouldn’t on any account have even mentioned that you bought a Salmon--which was too much for two. Or that it cost one pound six--which was more than we can afford.’", "\"어찌나 고맙던지, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 당신이 연어를 사 왔다는 말은 꺼내지도 않으려 했소 — 둘이 먹기엔 너무 컸다는 것도, 값이 일 파운드 육 실링이나 했다는 것도 — 그건 우리 형편에 너무 과한 돈이었지만 말이오.\""]], [["‘You enjoyed it very much,’ sobbed Dora.", "\"당신은 아주 맛있게 먹었잖아요.\" 도라가 흐느꼈다."], ["‘And you said I was a Mouse.’", "\"그리고 날 보고 생쥐라고 했잖아요.\""]], [["‘And I’ll say so again, my love,’ I returned, ‘a thousand times!’", "\"또 그렇게 부르고말고, 내 사랑.\" 나는 대답했다. \"천 번이라도!\""]], [["But I had wounded Dora’s soft little heart, and she was not to be comforted.", "그러나 나는 이미 도라의 여리고 작은 마음에 상처를 입혔고, 그녀는 도무지 달랠 수가 없었다."], ["She was so pathetic in her sobbing and bewailing, that I felt as if I had said I don’t know what to hurt her.", "그녀가 흐느끼며 슬퍼하는 모습이 어찌나 가엾던지, 나는 마치 무슨 끔찍한 말을 해서 그녀를 아프게 한 것만 같았다."], ["I was obliged to hurry away; I was kept out late; and I felt all night such pangs of remorse as made me miserable.", "나는 황급히 집을 나서야 했고, 늦게까지 밖에 붙들려 있었으며, 밤새도록 비참할 만큼 가책에 시달렸다."], ["I had the conscience of an assassin, and was haunted by a vague sense of enormous wickedness.", "나는 살인자 같은 죄책감을 품은 채, 어마어마한 죄악을 저질렀다는 어렴풋한 느낌에 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["It was two or three hours past midnight when I got home.", "집에 돌아온 것은 자정을 두세 시간 넘긴 무렵이었다."], ["I found my aunt, in our house, sitting up for me.", "우리 집에서 고모님이 나를 기다리며 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Is anything the matter, aunt?’ said I, alarmed.", "\"무슨 일 있어요, 고모님?\" 나는 깜짝 놀라 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing, Trot,’ she replied.", "\"아무 일 없다, 트롯.\""], ["‘Sit down, sit down.", "고모님이 대답했다."], ["Little Blossom has been rather out of spirits, and I have been keeping her company.", "\"앉아라, 앉아. 우리 작은 꽃송이가 기운이 좀 없길래 곁에 있어 줬을 뿐이야."], ["That’s all.’", "그게 다란다.\""]], [["I leaned my head upon my hand; and felt more sorry and downcast, as I sat looking at the fire, than I could have supposed possible so soon after the fulfilment of my brightest hopes.", "나는 손으로 머리를 괴었다. 그리고 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있자니, 내 가장 빛나던 소망을 이룬 지 이토록 얼마 되지도 않아 이렇게나 서글프고 풀이 죽으리라고는 미처 짐작도 못 했던 만큼, 마음이 한층 무겁고 가라앉았다."], ["As I sat thinking, I happened to meet my aunt’s eyes, which were resting on my face.", "그렇게 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있다가, 문득 내 얼굴에 머물러 있던 고모님의 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["There was an anxious expression in them, but it cleared directly.", "그 눈에는 근심 어린 빛이 서려 있었으나, 이내 말끔히 가셨다."]], [["‘I assure you, aunt,’ said I, ‘I have been quite unhappy myself all night, to think of Dora’s being so.", "\"정말이에요, 고모님.\" 내가 말했다. \"도라가 저렇게 속상해하는 걸 생각하니 저도 밤새 영 마음이 안 좋았어요."], ["But I had no other intention than to speak to her tenderly and lovingly about our home-affairs.’", "하지만 저는 그저 우리 집안일에 대해 다정하고 살갑게 얘기하려던 것뿐이었어요.\""]], [["My aunt nodded encouragement.", "고모님이 격려하듯 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘You must have patience, Trot,’ said she.", "\"인내심을 가져야 한다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Of course.", "\"물론이죠."], ["Heaven knows I don’t mean to be unreasonable, aunt!’", "하늘이 아시겠지만, 저는 억지를 부릴 생각은 없어요, 고모님!\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said my aunt.", "\"그래, 그래.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘But Little Blossom is a very tender little blossom, and the wind must be gentle with her.’", "\"하지만 우리 작은 꽃송이는 아주 여린 꽃송이라, 바람도 살살 불어 줘야 하는 법이란다.\""]], [["I thanked my good aunt, in my heart, for her tenderness towards my wife; and I was sure that she knew I did.", "나는 내 아내를 향한 고모님의 다정함에 마음속으로 고마워했다. 그리고 내가 그렇게 고마워하는 것을 고모님도 알고 계심을 확신했다."]], [["‘Don’t you think, aunt,’ said I, after some further contemplation of the fire, ‘that you could advise and counsel Dora a little, for our mutual advantage, now and then?’", "\"고모님은 어떻게 생각하세요.\" 나는 한참 더 난롯불을 바라보다가 말했다. \"이따금 우리 둘을 위해서, 도라에게 살짝 충고하고 조언해 주실 수 있지 않을까요?\""]], [["‘Trot,’ returned my aunt, with some emotion, ‘no!", "\"트롯.\" 고모님이 다소 감정이 북받친 듯 대답했다. \"아니다!"], ["Don’t ask me such a thing.’", "나에게 그런 부탁은 하지 마라.\""]], [["Her tone was so very earnest that I raised my eyes in surprise.", "그 어조가 어찌나 간곡하던지, 나는 놀라 눈을 들었다."]], [["‘I look back on my life, child,’ said my aunt, ‘and I think of some who are in their graves, with whom I might have been on kinder terms.", "\"내 살아온 날들을 돌아보면 말이다, 얘야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"지금은 무덤 속에 있는 사람들, 내가 좀 더 다정하게 대할 수도 있었던 사람들이 떠오른단다."], ["If I judged harshly of other people’s mistakes in marriage, it may have been because I had bitter reason to judge harshly of my own.", "내가 남들의 결혼 실수를 모질게 판단했다면, 그건 내 자신의 결혼을 모질게 판단할 쓰라린 까닭이 있었기 때문일지도 모르지."], ["Let that pass.", "그건 그렇다 치자."], ["I have been a grumpy, frumpy, wayward sort of a woman, a good many years.", "나는 오랜 세월 동안 무뚝뚝하고, 칙칙하고, 제멋대로인 여자로 살아왔다."], ["I am still, and I always shall be.", "지금도 그렇고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거야."], ["But you and I have done one another some good, Trot,--at all events, you have done me good, my dear; and division must not come between us, at this time of day.’", "하지만 너와 나는 서로에게 좋은 일을 해 왔어, 트롯 — 적어도 너는 나에게 좋은 일을 해 줬다, 얘야. 그러니 이제 와서 우리 사이에 틈이 생겨서는 안 돼.\""]], [["‘Division between us!’ cried I.", "\"우리 사이에 틈이라뇨!\" 내가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Child, child!’ said my aunt, smoothing her dress, ‘how soon it might come between us, or how unhappy I might make our Little Blossom, if I meddled in anything, a prophet couldn’t say.", "\"얘야, 얘야!\" 고모님이 옷자락을 매만지며 말했다. \"내가 뭐든 끼어들었다가는, 우리 사이에 얼마나 빨리 틈이 벌어질지, 또 내가 우리 작은 꽃송이를 얼마나 불행하게 만들지, 예언자라도 알 수 없는 일이야."], ["I want our pet to like me, and be as gay as a butterfly.", "나는 우리 귀여운 것이 나를 좋아하고, 나비처럼 명랑하기를 바란단다."], ["Remember your own home, in that second marriage; and never do both me and her the injury you have hinted at!’", "그 두 번째 결혼 시절의 네 집을 떠올려 보렴. 그리고 방금 네가 넌지시 말한 것 같은 그런 해코지를, 나에게도 그 애에게도 절대로 하지 마라!\""]], [["I comprehended, at once, that my aunt was right; and I comprehended the full extent of her generous feeling towards my dear wife.", "나는 단번에 고모님 말씀이 옳음을 깨달았다. 그리고 사랑하는 내 아내를 향한 고모님의 그 너그러운 마음이 얼마나 깊은지를 온전히 헤아렸다."]], [["‘These are early days, Trot,’ she pursued, ‘and Rome was not built in a day, nor in a year.", "\"아직은 이른 날들이야, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말을 이었다. \"로마도 하루아침에 세워진 게 아니고, 일 년 만에 세워진 것도 아니지."], ["You have chosen freely for yourself’; a cloud passed over her face for a moment, I thought; ‘and you have chosen a very pretty and a very affectionate creature.", "너는 네 손으로 자유로이 선택했고\" — 한순간 고모님 얼굴에 구름 한 점이 스쳐 가는 것 같았다 — \"아주 예쁘고 아주 정 많은 사람을 골랐어."], ["It will be your duty, and it will be your pleasure too--of course I know that; I am not delivering a lecture--to estimate her (as you chose her) by the qualities she has, and not by the qualities she may not have.", "그 애를 (네가 그렇게 골랐듯이) 그 애에게 있는 자질로 가늠하고, 없을지도 모를 자질로 가늠하지 않는 것이 네 의무이자 — 또한 네 기쁨이기도 할 거야 — 물론 그건 나도 안다. 잔소리를 늘어놓으려는 게 아니야."], ["The latter you must develop in her, if you can.", "없는 자질이라면, 네가 할 수 있다면 그 애 안에서 키워 줘야지."], ["And if you cannot, child,’ here my aunt rubbed her nose, ‘you must just accustom yourself to do without ‘em.", "그리고 만약 그러지 못한다면, 얘야,\" 여기서 고모님은 코를 문질렀다. \"그저 그것 없이 지내는 데 익숙해지는 수밖에 없어."], ["But remember, my dear, your future is between you two.", "하지만 명심해라, 얘야, 너희 둘의 앞날은 너희 둘 사이의 일이다."], ["No one can assist you; you are to work it out for yourselves.", "아무도 너희를 도울 수 없어. 너희 스스로 헤쳐 나가야 해."], ["This is marriage, Trot; and Heaven bless you both, in it, for a pair of babes in the wood as you are!’", "이게 결혼이라는 거야, 트롯. 그러니 부디 하늘이 너희 두 사람을 축복하시기를, 숲속의 두 어린 아기 같은 너희를!\""]], [["My aunt said this in a sprightly way, and gave me a kiss to ratify the blessing.", "고모님은 발랄하게 그렇게 말씀하시고는, 그 축복을 확인하듯 내게 입을 맞추셨다."]], [["‘Now,’ said she, ‘light my little lantern, and see me into my bandbox by the garden path’; for there was a communication between our cottages in that direction. ‘Give Betsey Trotwood’s love to Blossom, when you come back; and whatever you do, Trot, never dream of setting Betsey up as a scarecrow, for if I ever saw her in the glass, she’s quite grim enough and gaunt enough in her private capacity!’", "\"자.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"내 작은 등불을 켜다오. 그리고 정원 길로 해서 이 보잘것없는 처소까지 나를 바래다주렴.\" 그쪽 방향으로 우리 두 오두막 사이에 통하는 길이 나 있었다. \"돌아오거든 베시 트롯우드의 사랑을 꽃송이에게 전하고. 그리고 무슨 일이 있어도, 트롯, 이 베시를 허수아비로 세울 생각일랑 꿈에도 하지 마라. 거울 속에서 본 적이 있다만, 그 애는 혼자만으로도 충분히 험상궂고 충분히 깡말랐으니까!\""]], [["With this my aunt tied her head up in a handkerchief, with which she was accustomed to make a bundle of it on such occasions; and I escorted her home.", "이 말과 함께 고모님은 손수건으로 머리를 동여맸는데, 이런 때면 늘 그렇게 머리를 보따리처럼 싸매곤 했다. 그리고 나는 고모님을 댁까지 모셔다드렸다."], ["As she stood in her garden, holding up her little lantern to light me back, I thought her observation of me had an anxious air again; but I was too much occupied in pondering on what she had said, and too much impressed--for the first time, in reality--by the conviction that Dora and I had indeed to work out our future for ourselves, and that no one could assist us, to take much notice of it.", "고모님이 당신 정원에 서서, 돌아가는 내 길을 비추려고 작은 등불을 치켜든 채 서 있을 때, 나를 바라보는 그 눈길에 또다시 근심이 어린 듯했다. 그러나 나는 고모님이 하신 말씀을 곱씹느라 너무도 골몰해 있었고, 도라와 내가 정말로 우리 둘의 앞날을 우리 스스로 헤쳐 나가야 하며 아무도 우리를 도울 수 없다는 — 처음으로, 진실로 — 그 확신에 너무도 깊이 사로잡혀 있던 터라, 그 눈길을 그리 눈여겨보지 못했다."]], [["Dora came stealing down in her little slippers, to meet me, now that I was alone; and cried upon my shoulder, and said I had been hard-hearted and she had been naughty; and I said much the same thing in effect, I believe; and we made it up, and agreed that our first little difference was to be our last, and that we were never to have another if we lived a hundred years.", "이제 나 혼자 있게 되자, 도라가 작은 슬리퍼를 신고 살그머니 내려와 나를 맞이했다. 그녀는 내 어깨에 기대어 울며, 내가 매정했고 자기가 못되게 굴었다고 말했다. 나도 사실상 거의 같은 말을 했던 것 같다. 그리고 우리는 화해했고, 이 첫 작은 다툼을 마지막으로 삼기로, 백 년을 살더라도 두 번 다시 그런 일은 없도록 하기로 약속했다."]], [["The next domestic trial we went through, was the Ordeal of Servants.", "우리가 다음으로 겪은 가정의 시련은 바로 하인 시험이었다."], ["Mary Anne’s cousin deserted into our coal-hole, and was brought out, to our great amazement, by a piquet of his companions in arms, who took him away handcuffed in a procession that covered our front-garden with ignominy.", "메리 앤의 사촌이 우리 집 석탄 광으로 탈영해 들어왔는데, 우리가 기겁할 만큼 놀랍게도, 그의 동료 병사들로 이루어진 분견대가 그를 끌어내어 수갑을 채운 채 줄지어 데려가는 바람에, 우리 앞뜰이 온통 망신살에 뒤덮였다."], ["This nerved me to get rid of Mary Anne, who went so mildly, on receipt of wages, that I was surprised, until I found out about the tea-spoons, and also about the little sums she had borrowed in my name of the tradespeople without authority.", "이 일에 힘입어 나는 메리 앤을 내보낼 용기를 냈다. 그 여자는 품삯을 받고는 어찌나 순순히 나가던지 나는 의아했는데, 나중에 찻숟가락 일이며, 또 내 허락도 없이 내 이름을 대고 상인들에게서 빌려 쓴 자잘한 돈의 내막을 알고 나서야 그 까닭을 깨달았다."], ["After an interval of Mrs. Kidgerbury--the oldest inhabitant of Kentish Town, I believe, who went out charing, but was too feeble to execute her conceptions of that art--we found another treasure, who was one of the most amiable of women, but who generally made a point of falling either up or down the kitchen stairs with the tray, and almost plunged into the parlour, as into a bath, with the tea-things.", "그 뒤로 키저베리 부인 — 켄티시 타운에서 가장 나이 든 주민으로 알고 있는데, 가정부 일을 다녔으나 그 솜씨에 대한 자신의 구상을 실행하기엔 너무 노쇠한 분이었다 — 이 잠시 거쳐 간 다음, 우리는 또 다른 보물을 들였다. 그녀는 더없이 상냥한 여인이었으나, 으레 쟁반을 든 채 부엌 계단을 오르내리며 굴러떨어지는 것을 일과로 삼았고, 찻그릇을 든 채로는 마치 목욕통에 뛰어들 듯 거실로 곤두박질치다시피 했다."], ["The ravages committed by this unfortunate, rendering her dismissal necessary, she was succeeded (with intervals of Mrs. Kidgerbury) by a long line of Incapables; terminating in a young person of genteel appearance, who went to Greenwich Fair in Dora’s bonnet.", "이 가엾은 여인이 저지른 손실이 너무 막심해 결국 내보낼 수밖에 없었고, 그 뒤로 (사이사이 키저베리 부인을 끼워 가며) 무능한 사람들이 줄줄이 거쳐 갔으며, 그 끝은 점잖아 보이는 어느 젊은 여자였는데, 그녀는 도라의 보닛을 쓰고 그리니치 장터에 놀러 갔다."], ["After whom I remember nothing but an average equality of failure.", "그 뒤로는, 고만고만하게 골고루 실패했다는 것 말고는 아무것도 기억나는 게 없다."]], [["Everybody we had anything to do with seemed to cheat us.", "우리와 거래하는 사람은 죄다 우리를 속여먹는 것 같았다."], ["Our appearance in a shop was a signal for the damaged goods to be brought out immediately.", "우리가 가게에 나타나는 것은 곧장 상한 물건을 꺼내 오라는 신호였다."], ["If we bought a lobster, it was full of water.", "바닷가재를 사면 속이 물로 가득 차 있었다."], ["All our meat turned out to be tough, and there was hardly any crust to our loaves.", "고기란 고기는 죄다 질겼고, 빵 덩어리에는 껍질이라 할 만한 게 거의 없었다."], ["In search of the principle on which joints ought to be roasted, to be roasted enough, and not too much, I myself referred to the Cookery Book, and found it there established as the allowance of a quarter of an hour to every pound, and say a quarter over. But the principle always failed us by some curious fatality, and we never could hit any medium between redness and cinders.", "고깃덩이를 어떤 원칙으로 구워야 알맞게, 너무 과하지 않게 익는지 알아보려고, 나는 직접 요리책을 들춰 보았는데, 거기에는 일 파운드당 십오 분씩, 거기에 십오 분쯤 더 잡으면 된다고 정해져 있었다. 그러나 이 원칙은 어떤 묘한 운명의 장난인지 늘 우리에게 통하지 않았고, 우리는 시뻘건 날고기와 새카만 숯덩이 사이의 그 어떤 중간치도 끝내 맞추지 못했다."]], [["I had reason to believe that in accomplishing these failures we incurred a far greater expense than if we had achieved a series of triumphs.", "이런 실패들을 이루어 내느라 우리가 도리어 줄줄이 성공을 거두는 것보다 훨씬 큰 비용을 치렀다고 믿을 만한 이유가 나에게는 있었다."], ["It appeared to me, on looking over the tradesmen’s books, as if we might have kept the basement storey paved with butter, such was the extensive scale of our consumption of that article.", "상인들의 장부를 들여다보면, 마치 우리가 지하층 바닥을 버터로 깔아 놓고 지낸 게 아닌가 싶을 정도로, 그 물건의 소비량이 어마어마했다."], ["I don’t know whether the Excise returns of the period may have exhibited any increase in the demand for pepper; but if our performances did not affect the market, I should say several families must have left off using it.", "그 무렵의 소비세 통계에 후추 수요의 증가가 잡혔는지는 모르겠으나, 우리의 행태가 시장에 영향을 주지 않았다면, 분명 여러 집안이 후추 쓰기를 그만두었으리라고 나는 말하겠다."], ["And the most wonderful fact of all was, that we never had anything in the house.", "그리고 무엇보다도 놀라운 사실은, 집 안에 도무지 남아나는 게 없었다는 것이다."]], [["As to the washerwoman pawning the clothes, and coming in a state of penitent intoxication to apologize, I suppose that might have happened several times to anybody.", "빨래하는 여자가 옷가지를 전당포에 잡혀 놓고는, 뉘우치며 거나하게 취한 꼴로 찾아와 사과하던 일로 말할 것 같으면, 누구에게나 몇 번쯤은 일어날 법한 일이려니 싶다."], ["Also the chimney on fire, the parish engine, and perjury on the part of the Beadle.", "굴뚝에 불이 난 것이며, 교구 소방펌프가 출동한 것이며, 교회지기가 위증을 한 것도 마찬가지다."], ["But I apprehend that we were personally fortunate in engaging a servant with a taste for cordials, who swelled our running account for porter at the public-house by such inexplicable items as ‘quartern rum shrub (Mrs. C.)’; ‘Half-quartern gin and cloves (Mrs.", "그러나 짐작건대 우리는 특히나 운이 좋아서, 술을 즐기는 취향을 지닌 하녀를 들였는데, 그 여자는 술집 외상 장부에 도무지 알 수 없는 항목들로 우리 빚을 불려 놓았다. 이를테면 '럼 슈러브 사분의 일 통 (C 부인)', '진과 정향 팔분의 일 통 (C 부인)', '럼과 박하 한 잔 (C 부인)'"], ["C.)’; ‘Glass rum and peppermint (Mrs. C.)’--the parentheses always referring to Dora, who was supposed, it appeared on explanation, to have imbibed the whole of these refreshments.", "따위였다 — 이 괄호는 늘 도라를 가리켰는데, 나중에 해명을 들어 보니, 이 음료들을 죄다 도라가 들이켠 것으로 되어 있다는 것이었다."]], [["One of our first feats in the housekeeping way was a little dinner to Traddles.", "우리가 살림에서 처음으로 해낸 큰일 가운데 하나는 트래들스를 초대한 조촐한 만찬이었다."], ["I met him in town, and asked him to walk out with me that afternoon.", "나는 시내에서 그와 마주쳐, 그날 오후 함께 우리 집까지 걷자고 청했다."], ["He readily consenting, I wrote to Dora, saying I would bring him home.", "그가 흔쾌히 응하기에, 나는 도라에게 그를 데려가겠다고 편지를 써 보냈다."], ["It was pleasant weather, and on the road we made my domestic happiness the theme of conversation.", "날씨가 좋았고, 가는 길에 우리는 내 가정의 행복을 화제로 삼아 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Traddles was very full of it; and said, that, picturing himself with such a home, and Sophy waiting and preparing for him, he could think of nothing wanting to complete his bliss.", "트래들스는 그 이야기로 가슴이 잔뜩 부풀어, 그런 집에서 소피가 자기를 기다리며 채비하는 모습을 그려 보니, 자기 행복을 완성하는 데 더 바랄 게 아무것도 없겠다고 말했다."]], [["I could not have wished for a prettier little wife at the opposite end of the table, but I certainly could have wished, when we sat down, for a little more room.", "식탁 맞은편에 더 예쁜 작은 아내가 앉아 있기를 바랄 수는 없었지만, 막상 자리에 앉고 보니 공간이 조금만 더 있었으면 하는 바람은 분명히 들었다."], ["I did not know how it was, but though there were only two of us, we were at once always cramped for room, and yet had always room enough to lose everything in.", "어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠으나, 우리는 단둘뿐인데도 늘 자리가 비좁았고, 그러면서도 물건을 죄다 잃어버릴 만큼은 자리가 넉넉했다."], ["I suspect it may have been because nothing had a place of its own, except Jip’s pagoda, which invariably blocked up the main thoroughfare.", "짐작건대 그것은, 큰길목을 어김없이 가로막는 집의 작은 탑 모양 개집을 빼고는, 무엇 하나 제자리라는 게 없었기 때문인 듯하다."], ["On the present occasion, Traddles was so hemmed in by the pagoda and the guitar-case, and Dora’s flower-painting, and my writing-table, that I had serious doubts of the possibility of his using his knife and fork; but he protested, with his own good-humour, ‘Oceans of room, Copperfield!", "이날도 트래들스는 그 탑 모양 개집과 기타 가방과 도라의 꽃 그림과 내 책상에 어찌나 둘러싸여 있던지, 나는 그가 과연 나이프와 포크를 쓸 수나 있을지 진지하게 걱정스러웠다. 그러나 그는 특유의 너그러움으로 손사래를 쳤다. \"자리는 바다처럼 넓다네, 코퍼필드!"], ["I assure you, Oceans!’", "정말이지, 바다처럼 넓어!\""]], [["There was another thing I could have wished, namely, that Jip had never been encouraged to walk about the tablecloth during dinner.", "또 한 가지 바라는 게 있었으니, 그것은 식사 도중 집이 식탁보 위를 돌아다니도록 부추기는 일이 애당초 없었으면 하는 것이었다."], ["I began to think there was something disorderly in his being there at all, even if he had not been in the habit of putting his foot in the salt or the melted butter.", "나는 그 녀석이 거기 올라와 있다는 것 자체가, 설령 발을 소금이나 녹인 버터에 담그는 버릇이 없었더라도, 어딘가 어수선한 일이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["On this occasion he seemed to think he was introduced expressly to keep Traddles at bay; and he barked at my old friend, and made short runs at his plate, with such undaunted pertinacity, that he may be said to have engrossed the conversation.", "이날 그 녀석은 자기가 트래들스를 막아 내려고 일부러 불려 나온 줄로 여기는 듯했다. 녀석은 내 옛 친구를 향해 짖어 대고, 그의 접시를 향해 짧게 돌진하기를 어찌나 겁 없이 끈질기게 되풀이하던지, 가히 그 녀석이 대화를 독차지했다고 해도 될 정도였다."]], [["However, as I knew how tender-hearted my dear Dora was, and how sensitive she would be to any slight upon her favourite, I hinted no objection.", "그러나 사랑하는 도라가 얼마나 여린 마음의 소유자인지, 또 자기가 아끼는 녀석을 조금이라도 푸대접하면 얼마나 예민하게 굴지를 잘 알았기에, 나는 일절 싫은 내색을 비치지 않았다."], ["For similar reasons I made no allusion to the skirmishing plates upon the floor; or to the disreputable appearance of the castors, which were all at sixes and sevens, and looked drunk; or to the further blockade of Traddles by wandering vegetable dishes and jugs.", "같은 까닭으로, 나는 바닥에 흩어져 옥신각신하는 접시들에 대해서도, 죄다 뒤죽박죽으로 술 취한 듯 보이는 양념병 받침의 꼴사나운 행색에 대해서도, 떠돌아다니는 채소 접시와 주전자들 때문에 트래들스가 한층 더 포위당한 사정에 대해서도 입도 뻥긋하지 않았다."], ["I could not help wondering in my own mind, as I contemplated the boiled leg of mutton before me, previous to carving it, how it came to pass that our joints of meat were of such extraordinary shapes--and whether our butcher contracted for all the deformed sheep that came into the world; but I kept my reflections to myself.", "나는 눈앞에 놓인 삶은 양 다리를 썰기에 앞서 가만히 바라보면서, 우리 집 고깃덩이가 어쩜 이리도 별난 모양인지 — 우리 정육점 주인이 세상에 태어나는 기형 양들을 죄다 도맡아 계약하는 게 아닌가 — 속으로 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 그 생각만큼은 혼자 묻어 두었다."]], [["‘My love,’ said I to Dora, ‘what have you got in that dish?’", "\"여보.\" 나는 도라에게 말했다. \"그 접시에는 뭐가 들었소?\""]], [["I could not imagine why Dora had been making tempting little faces at me, as if she wanted to kiss me.", "도라가 마치 나에게 입맞춤이라도 하고 싶은 듯, 귀엽게 얼굴을 찡긋거리며 나를 꼬드기던 까닭을 나는 도무지 알 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Oysters, dear,’ said Dora, timidly.", "\"굴이에요, 여보.\" 도라가 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["‘Was that YOUR thought?’ said I, delighted.", "\"그게 당신 생각이었소?\" 나는 반색하며 말했다."]], [["‘Ye-yes, Doady,’ said Dora.", "\"네—네, 도디.\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["‘There never was a happier one!’ I exclaimed, laying down the carving-knife and fork.", "\"그보다 더 좋은 생각은 없었을 거요!\" 나는 고기 써는 칼과 포크를 내려놓으며 외쳤다."], ["‘There is nothing Traddles likes so much!’", "\"트래들스가 세상에서 제일 좋아하는 게 바로 굴이거든!\""]], [["‘Ye-yes, Doady,’ said Dora, ‘and so I bought a beautiful little barrel of them, and the man said they were very good.", "\"네—네, 도디.\" 도라가 말했다. \"그래서 아주 예쁜 작은 통으로 하나 샀어요."], ["But I--I am afraid there’s something the matter with them.", "가게 아저씨가 아주 좋은 거라고 했고요. 그런데 나—나, 뭔가 잘못된 것 같아 걱정이에요."], ["They don’t seem right.’ Here Dora shook her head, and diamonds twinkled in her eyes.", "영 이상해 보여서요.\" 여기서 도라가 고개를 절레절레 흔드니, 그 눈에서 다이아몬드 같은 눈물이 반짝였다."]], [["‘They are only opened in both shells,’ said I.", "\"두 껍데기가 다 붙어 있을 뿐이오.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Take the top one off, my love.’", "\"위쪽 껍데기만 떼어 내요, 여보.\""]], [["‘But it won’t come off!’ said Dora, trying very hard, and looking very much distressed.", "\"그런데 안 떨어져요!\" 도라가 안간힘을 쓰면서, 몹시 난처한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["‘Do you know, Copperfield,’ said Traddles, cheerfully examining the dish, ‘I think it is in consequence--they are capital oysters, but I think it is in consequence--of their never having been opened.’", "\"있잖나, 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 명랑하게 접시를 살펴보며 말했다. \"이게 다 그 때문인 것 같은데 — 아주 훌륭한 굴이긴 한데, 내 생각엔 다 그 때문인 것 같아 — 이게 한 번도 까지지 않은 탓인 것 같네.\""]], [["They never had been opened; and we had no oyster-knives--and couldn’t have used them if we had; so we looked at the oysters and ate the mutton.", "그 굴들은 한 번도 까진 적이 없었다. 게다가 우리에게는 굴 까는 칼도 없었고 — 설령 있었더라도 쓸 줄 몰랐을 것이다. 그래서 우리는 굴은 바라만 보고 양고기를 먹었다."], ["At least we ate as much of it as was done, and made up with capers.", "적어도 익은 만큼은 먹고, 모자란 데는 케이퍼로 메웠다."], ["If I had permitted him, I am satisfied that Traddles would have made a perfect savage of himself, and eaten a plateful of raw meat, to express enjoyment of the repast; but I would hear of no such immolation on the altar of friendship, and we had a course of bacon instead; there happening, by good fortune, to be cold bacon in the larder.", "내가 말리지 않았더라면, 트래들스는 분명 완전한 야만인 흉내를 내며, 이 만찬이 얼마나 즐거운지를 표하느라 날고기 한 접시를 통째로 먹어 치웠을 것이다. 그러나 나는 우정의 제단에 그런 희생을 바치는 일은 결코 듣지 않으려 했고, 대신 베이컨을 한 접시 내놓았다. 다행스럽게도 식료품 저장실에 식은 베이컨이 마침 남아 있었던 것이다."]], [["My poor little wife was in such affliction when she thought I should be annoyed, and in such a state of joy when she found I was not, that the discomfiture I had subdued, very soon vanished, and we passed a happy evening; Dora sitting with her arm on my chair while Traddles and I discussed a glass of wine, and taking every opportunity of whispering in my ear that it was so good of me not to be a cruel, cross old boy.", "내 가엾은 작은 아내는 내가 화가 났을까 봐 어찌나 애를 태우다가, 그렇지 않다는 걸 알고는 또 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 내가 가까스로 억눌렀던 그 언짢음마저 이내 씻은 듯 사라지고, 우리는 즐거운 저녁을 보냈다. 트래들스와 내가 포도주를 한 잔씩 기울이는 동안, 도라는 내 의자에 팔을 얹고 곁에 앉아, 틈만 나면 내 귀에 대고 매정하고 심술궂은 늙은이가 되지 않아 줘서 정말 고맙다고 속삭였다."], ["By and by she made tea for us; which it was so pretty to see her do, as if she was busying herself with a set of doll’s tea-things, that I was not particular about the quality of the beverage.", "이윽고 그녀가 우리에게 차를 끓여 주었는데, 마치 인형 놀이용 찻그릇 한 벌을 가지고 부지런을 떠는 것처럼 그 모습이 어찌나 예쁘던지, 나는 차의 맛 따위에는 까다롭게 굴 생각이 들지 않았다."], ["Then Traddles and I played a game or two at cribbage; and Dora singing to the guitar the while, it seemed to me as if our courtship and marriage were a tender dream of mine, and the night when I first listened to her voice were not yet over.", "그런 다음 트래들스와 나는 크리비지를 한두 판 했다. 그러는 동안 도라가 기타에 맞춰 노래를 부르니, 나에게는 우리의 연애와 결혼이 마치 내 다정한 꿈만 같고, 처음으로 그녀의 목소리에 귀 기울였던 그 밤이 아직 끝나지 않은 것만 같았다."]], [["When Traddles went away, and I came back into the parlour from seeing him out, my wife planted her chair close to mine, and sat down by my side.", "* 크리비지: 점수판에 못을 꽂아 가며 셈하는 영국식 카드놀이. 트래들스가 돌아가고, 그를 배웅한 뒤 거실로 들어오니, 아내가 제 의자를 내 의자 바로 옆에 끌어다 놓고 곁에 앉았다."], ["‘I am very sorry,’ she said.", "\"정말 미안해요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Will you try to teach me, Doady?’", "\"도디, 나에게 가르쳐 줄래요?\""]], [["‘I must teach myself first, Dora,’ said I.", "\"먼저 내가 나 자신부터 가르쳐야겠소, 도라.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I am as bad as you, love.’", "\"나도 당신만큼이나 형편없으니까, 여보.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But you can learn,’ she returned; ‘and you are a clever, clever man!’", "하지만 당신은 배울 수 있잖아요.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다. \"당신은 영리하고, 영리한 사람이니까요!\""]], [["‘Nonsense, mouse!’ said I.", "\"말도 안 되는 소리, 생쥐야!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I wish,’ resumed my wife, after a long silence, ‘that I could have gone down into the country for a whole year, and lived with Agnes!’", "\"나는 말이에요.\" 한참 입을 다물고 있던 아내가 다시 입을 열었다. \"꼬박 일 년쯤 시골로 내려가, 아그네스와 함께 살았더라면 좋았을 것 같아요!\""]], [["Her hands were clasped upon my shoulder, and her chin rested on them, and her blue eyes looked quietly into mine.", "그녀는 두 손을 내 어깨 위에 모으고 그 위에 턱을 괸 채, 푸른 눈으로 가만히 내 눈을 들여다보고 있었다."]], [["‘Why so?’ I asked.", "\"왜 그렇소?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I think she might have improved me, and I think I might have learned from her,’ said Dora.", "\"그분이라면 나를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 줬을 것 같고, 나도 그분에게서 뭔가 배웠을 것 같아서요.\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["‘All in good time, my love.", "\"다 때가 되면 될 일이오, 여보."], ["Agnes has had her father to take care of for these many years, you should remember.", "아그네스는 이 여러 해 동안 아버지를 돌봐야 했다는 걸 잊지 마요."], ["Even when she was quite a child, she was the Agnes whom we know,’ said I.", "아주 어린아이였을 적에도, 그녀는 이미 우리가 아는 그 아그네스였소.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Will you call me a name I want you to call me?’ inquired Dora, without moving.", "\"내가 당신더러 불러 달라고 하는 이름으로, 날 불러 줄래요?\" 도라가 꼼짝도 않고 물었다."]], [["‘What is it?’ I asked with a smile.", "\"그게 뭔데?\" 나는 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["‘It’s a stupid name,’ she said, shaking her curls for a moment.", "\"바보 같은 이름이에요.\" 그녀가 잠시 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘Child-wife.’", "\"어린 아내.\""]], [["I laughingly asked my child-wife what her fancy was in desiring to be so called.", "나는 웃으며, 어린 아내에게 어쩌다 그렇게 불리고 싶은 마음이 들었느냐고 물었다."], ["She answered without moving, otherwise than as the arm I twined about her may have brought her blue eyes nearer to me:", "그녀는 내가 둘러 감은 팔에 이끌려 푸른 눈이 내게 좀 더 가까이 다가온 것 말고는 꼼짝도 않은 채 대답했다."]], [["‘I don’t mean, you silly fellow, that you should use the name instead of Dora.", "\"바보 같으니, 내 말은 당신이 도라 대신 그 이름을 쓰라는 게 아니에요."], ["I only mean that you should think of me that way.", "그저 나를 그렇게 생각해 달라는 거예요."], ["When you are going to be angry with me, say to yourself, “it’s only my child-wife!”", "나에게 화가 나려고 할 때면, 속으로 이렇게 말해요. '그저 내 어린 아내일 뿐이야!'"], ["When I am very disappointing, say, “I knew, a long time ago, that she would make but a child-wife!”", "내가 당신을 몹시 실망시킬 때면, 이렇게 말하고요. '저 사람이 한낱 어린 아내밖에 못 될 거라는 건 진작에 알고 있었지!'"], ["When you miss what I should like to be, and I think can never be, say, “still my foolish child-wife loves me!”", "내가 마땅히 되어야 할, 하지만 결코 될 수 없을 것 같은 그 모습이 아쉬울 때면, 이렇게 말해요. '그래도 내 어리석은 어린 아내는 나를 사랑하지!'"], ["For indeed I do.’", "정말로 사랑하니까요.\""]], [["I had not been serious with her; having no idea until now, that she was serious herself.", "나는 그때까지 그녀에게 진지하게 굴지 않았다. 지금 이 순간까지 그녀 자신이 진지하다는 걸 미처 몰랐던 것이다."], ["But her affectionate nature was so happy in what I now said to her with my whole heart, that her face became a laughing one before her glittering eyes were dry.", "그러나 이제 내가 진심을 다해 건넨 말에 그녀의 다정한 천성이 어찌나 행복해하던지, 반짝이는 두 눈의 눈물이 채 마르기도 전에 얼굴에는 웃음이 번졌다."], ["She was soon my child-wife indeed; sitting down on the floor outside the Chinese House, ringing all the little bells one after another, to punish Jip for his recent bad behaviour; while Jip lay blinking in the doorway with his head out, even too lazy to be teased.", "그녀는 이내 정말로 내 어린 아내가 되어, 중국식 작은 집 밖 바닥에 주저앉아, 요전번 못된 짓에 대한 벌로 작은 종을 하나하나 차례로 흔들어 댔다. 그러는 동안 집은 머리만 내민 채 문간에 엎드려 눈을 끔벅거렸는데, 너무 게을러서 약 올림을 당하기조차 귀찮은 모양이었다."]], [["This appeal of Dora’s made a strong impression on me.", "도라의 이 호소는 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 나는 지금 글로 옮기는 그 시절을 돌아본다."], ["I look back on the time I write of; I invoke the innocent figure that I dearly loved, to come out from the mists and shadows of the past, and turn its gentle head towards me once again; and I can still declare that this one little speech was constantly in my memory.", "내가 그토록 사랑했던 그 천진한 모습이 과거의 안개와 그늘 밖으로 걸어 나와, 그 다정한 고개를 다시 한번 내게로 돌려 주기를 불러 본다. 그리고 나는 지금도 이렇게 말할 수 있다. 그 한마디 작은 호소가 늘 내 기억 속에 머물러 있었노라고."], ["I may not have used it to the best account; I was young and inexperienced; but I never turned a deaf ear to its artless pleading.", "어쩌면 나는 그것을 가장 잘 활용하지는 못했을지도 모른다. 나는 젊고 미숙했으니까. 그러나 나는 그 꾸밈없는 간청에 단 한 번도 귀를 닫지 않았다."]], [["Dora told me, shortly afterwards, that she was going to be a wonderful housekeeper.", "도라는 그 직후 나에게, 자기가 훌륭한 살림꾼이 되겠노라고 말했다."], ["Accordingly, she polished the tablets, pointed the pencil, bought an immense account-book, carefully stitched up with a needle and thread all the leaves of the Cookery Book which Jip had torn, and made quite a desperate little attempt ‘to be good’, as she called it.", "그리하여 그녀는 석판을 닦고, 연필을 깎고, 어마어마하게 큰 가계부를 사고, 집이 찢어 놓은 요리책의 책장을 죄다 바늘과 실로 정성껏 꿰매고, '착해지려고' — 그녀가 그렇게 부르던 대로 — 자못 필사적인 작은 시도를 벌였다."], ["But the figures had the old obstinate propensity--they WOULD NOT add up.", "그러나 숫자들은 예의 그 고집스러운 성미를 부려 — 도무지 더해지질 않았다."], ["When she had entered two or three laborious items in the account-book, Jip would walk over the page, wagging his tail, and smear them all out.", "그녀가 가계부에 힘들여 항목을 두세 개 적어 넣고 나면, 집이 그 위를 걸어 다니며 꼬리를 흔들어 죄다 문질러 지워 버렸다."], ["Her own little right-hand middle finger got steeped to the very bone in ink; and I think that was the only decided result obtained.", "그녀 자신의 작은 오른손 가운뎃손가락만 뼛속까지 잉크에 절었으니, 내 생각엔 그것만이 유일하게 확실히 얻어 낸 결과였다."]], [["Sometimes, of an evening, when I was at home and at work--for I wrote a good deal now, and was beginning in a small way to be known as a writer--I would lay down my pen, and watch my child-wife trying to be good.", "가끔 저녁이면, 내가 집에서 일을 하고 있을 때 — 나는 그 무렵 글을 꽤 많이 썼고, 조금씩 작가로 알려지기 시작하던 참이었다 — 나는 펜을 내려놓고 착해지려 애쓰는 내 어린 아내를 가만히 지켜보곤 했다."], ["First of all, she would bring out the immense account-book, and lay it down upon the table, with a deep sigh.", "우선 그녀는 그 어마어마하게 큰 가계부를 꺼내 와, 깊은 한숨과 함께 책상에 내려놓았다."], ["Then she would open it at the place where Jip had made it illegible last night, and call Jip up, to look at his misdeeds.", "그러고는 어젯밤 집이 알아볼 수 없게 만들어 놓은 자리를 펼치고, 집을 불러내 제 잘못을 들여다보게 했다."], ["This would occasion a diversion in Jip’s favour, and some inking of his nose, perhaps, as a penalty.", "이 일은 으레 집에게 유리하게 흐르는 딴청으로 번졌고, 벌이랍시고 그 녀석 코에 잉크칠이나 좀 하는 것으로 끝나곤 했다."], ["Then she would tell Jip to lie down on the table instantly, ‘like a lion’--which was one of his tricks, though I cannot say the likeness was striking--and, if he were in an obedient humour, he would obey.", "그런 다음 그녀는 집에게 당장 책상 위에 '사자처럼' 엎드리라고 일렀는데 — 그건 그 녀석의 재주 가운데 하나였으나, 사자와 닮았다고는 도저히 말 못 하겠다 — 녀석이 순한 기분이면 시키는 대로 따랐다."], ["Then she would take up a pen, and begin to write, and find a hair in it.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 펜을 집어 들고 쓰기 시작하다가, 펜에 머리카락이 끼어 있는 걸 발견했다."], ["Then she would take up another pen, and begin to write, and find that it spluttered.", "그러면 다른 펜을 집어 들고 쓰기 시작하다가, 펜이 잉크를 튀긴다는 걸 알았다."], ["Then she would take up another pen, and begin to write, and say in a low voice, ‘Oh, it’s a talking pen, and will disturb Doady!’ And then she would give it up as a bad job, and put the account-book away, after pretending to crush the lion with it.", "그러면 또 다른 펜을 집어 들고 쓰기 시작하다가, 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"아유, 이건 말하는 펜이라 도디를 방해하겠어!\" 그러고는 가망 없는 일이라며 손을 놓고, 그 가계부로 사자를 짓누르는 시늉을 한 뒤 치워 버렸다."]], [["Or, if she were in a very sedate and serious state of mind, she would sit down with the tablets, and a little basket of bills and other documents, which looked more like curl-papers than anything else, and endeavour to get some result out of them.", "아니면, 그녀가 아주 차분하고 진지한 마음 상태일 때면, 석판과, 무엇보다도 머리 마는 종이 뭉치처럼 보이는 청구서며 온갖 서류가 담긴 작은 바구니를 앞에 놓고 앉아, 거기서 어떻게든 결과를 뽑아내려 애썼다."], ["After severely comparing one with another, and making entries on the tablets, and blotting them out, and counting all the fingers of her left hand over and over again, backwards and forwards, she would be so vexed and discouraged, and would look so unhappy, that it gave me pain to see her bright face clouded--and for me!--and I would go softly to her, and say:", "이것저것 매섭게 견주어 보고, 석판에 적었다가 다시 지우고, 왼손 손가락을 앞으로 뒤로 몇 번이고 되풀이해 세어 보다가, 어찌나 약이 오르고 풀이 죽던지, 또 그 표정이 어찌나 서글퍼 보이던지, 그 환한 얼굴이 — 그것도 나 때문에! — 흐려지는 것을 보고 있자면 나는 가슴이 아팠다. 그래서 나는 살그머니 그녀에게 다가가 말하곤 했다."]], [["‘What’s the matter, Dora?’", "\"왜 그래, 도라?\""]], [["Dora would look up hopelessly, and reply, ‘They won’t come right.", "도라는 절망한 듯 고개를 들고 대답했다. \"이게 도무지 맞아떨어지질 않아요."], ["They make my head ache so.", "머리가 어찌나 아픈지."], ["And they won’t do anything I want!’", "그리고 내가 바라는 건 하나도 안 돼요!\""]], [["Then I would say, ‘Now let us try together.", "그러면 나는 말했다. \"자, 우리 같이 해 보자."], ["Let me show you, Dora.’", "내가 보여 줄게, 도라.\""]], [["Then I would commence a practical demonstration, to which Dora would pay profound attention, perhaps for five minutes; when she would begin to be dreadfully tired, and would lighten the subject by curling my hair, or trying the effect of my face with my shirt-collar turned down.", "그러고는 실제로 시범을 보이기 시작하면, 도라는 깊이 귀를 기울였는데 — 어쩌면 오 분쯤은 그랬다. 그러다 그녀는 끔찍하게 지치기 시작해, 내 머리카락을 배배 꼬거나, 내 셔츠 깃을 접어 내려 내 얼굴이 어떻게 보이는지 시험하면서 그 따분한 화제를 가볍게 풀어 버렸다."], ["If I tacitly checked this playfulness, and persisted, she would look so scared and disconsolate, as she became more and more bewildered, that the remembrance of her natural gaiety when I first strayed into her path, and of her being my child-wife, would come reproachfully upon me; and I would lay the pencil down, and call for the guitar.", "내가 잠자코 그 장난을 막고 끈질기게 계속하면, 그녀는 점점 더 갈피를 못 잡으며 어찌나 겁먹고 풀 죽은 표정이 되던지, 내가 처음 그녀의 길로 들어섰을 때의 그 타고난 명랑함이며 그녀가 내 어린 아내라는 사실이 나를 꾸짖듯 떠올랐다. 그러면 나는 연필을 내려놓고 기타를 가져오라고 했다."]], [["I had a great deal of work to do, and had many anxieties, but the same considerations made me keep them to myself.", "나는 할 일이 산더미였고 근심도 많았지만, 바로 그런 마음 씀씀이 때문에 그것들을 혼자 묻어 두었다."], ["I am far from sure, now, that it was right to do this, but I did it for my child-wife’s sake.", "그렇게 한 것이 옳았는지 지금도 도무지 확신이 서지 않으나, 나는 내 어린 아내를 위해 그렇게 했다."], ["I search my breast, and I commit its secrets, if I know them, without any reservation to this paper.", "나는 내 가슴속을 더듬어, 그 안의 비밀을 — 내가 그것을 안다면 — 한 점 숨김없이 이 종이에 털어놓는다."], ["The old unhappy loss or want of something had, I am conscious, some place in my heart; but not to the embitterment of my life.", "무언가를 예전에 잃었거나 가지지 못했다는 그 오래된 서글픔이, 내 마음 한구석에 얼마쯤 자리 잡고 있었음을 나도 안다."], ["When I walked alone in the fine weather, and thought of the summer days when all the air had been filled with my boyish enchantment, I did miss something of the realization of my dreams; but I thought it was a softened glory of the Past, which nothing could have thrown upon the present time.", "그러나 그것이 내 삶을 쓰디쓰게 만들 정도는 아니었다. 화창한 날 홀로 거닐며, 온 대기가 소년 시절의 황홀로 가득 차 있던 그 여름날들을 떠올릴 때면, 내 꿈이 다 이루어지지는 못했다는 아쉬움이 조금은 들었다. 그러나 나는 그것이, 그 무엇도 지금 이 순간 위에 드리울 수 없는, 과거의 한결 부드러워진 영광이려니 여겼다."], ["I did feel, sometimes, for a little while, that I could have wished my wife had been my counsellor; had had more character and purpose, to sustain me and improve me by; had been endowed with power to fill up the void which somewhere seemed to be about me; but I felt as if this were an unearthly consummation of my happiness, that never had been meant to be, and never could have been.", "가끔은 잠깐씩, 내 아내가 나의 조언자였더라면, 나를 떠받치고 더 나은 사람으로 이끌어 줄 만한 더 굳건한 인품과 목표를 지녔더라면, 내 주위 어딘가에 있는 듯한 그 빈자리를 채워 줄 힘을 타고났더라면 하고 바라는 마음이 일기도 했다. 그러나 나는 이것이 내 행복의 천상 같은 완성이며, 애당초 그렇게 될 운명이 아니었고 또 결코 그렇게 될 수도 없었던 일이라고 느꼈다."]], [["I was a boyish husband as to years.", "나는 나이로 따지면 소년 같은 남편이었다."], ["I had known the softening influence of no other sorrows or experiences than those recorded in these leaves.", "나는 이 책장에 적힌 것 말고는 그 어떤 다른 슬픔이나 경험의, 사람을 단련시키는 영향을 알지 못했다."], ["If I did any wrong, as I may have done much, I did it in mistaken love, and in my want of wisdom.", "내가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀다면 — 어쩌면 많이 저질렀을 텐데 — 그것은 잘못된 사랑에서, 그리고 지혜가 모자란 탓에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["I write the exact truth.", "나는 있는 그대로의 진실을 적는다."], ["It would avail me nothing to extenuate it now.", "이제 와서 그것을 변명한들 나에게 아무 도움도 되지 않을 것이다."]], [["Thus it was that I took upon myself the toils and cares of our life, and had no partner in them.", "그리하여 나는 우리 삶의 수고와 근심을 혼자 짊어졌고, 거기에 동반자는 없었다."], ["We lived much as before, in reference to our scrambling household arrangements; but I had got used to those, and Dora I was pleased to see was seldom vexed now.", "우리는 그 엉망진창인 살림살이에 관한 한 예전과 다름없이 살았다. 그러나 나는 그런 데 이미 익숙해졌고, 도라가 이제는 좀처럼 속을 끓이지 않는 것을 보니 흐뭇했다."], ["She was bright and cheerful in the old childish way, loved me dearly, and was happy with her old trifles.", "그녀는 예의 그 어린아이 같은 모습으로 환하고 명랑했으며, 나를 끔찍이 사랑했고, 그 오래된 자질구레한 것들로도 행복해했다."]], [["When the debates were heavy--I mean as to length, not quality, for in the last respect they were not often otherwise--and I went home late, Dora would never rest when she heard my footsteps, but would always come downstairs to meet me.", "토론회가 길어질 때면 — 길이로 말하는 것이지 내용으로 말하는 게 아니다, 내용으로 따지면 길지 않은 적이 드물었으니까 — 그리고 내가 집에 늦게 돌아오면, 도라는 내 발소리를 듣고는 결코 가만히 쉬는 법 없이 언제나 아래층으로 내려와 나를 맞이했다."], ["When my evenings were unoccupied by the pursuit for which I had qualified myself with so much pains, and I was engaged in writing at home, she would sit quietly near me, however late the hour, and be so mute, that I would often think she had dropped asleep.", "그토록 애써 자격을 갖춘 그 일거리에 매이지 않은 저녁이면, 그리고 내가 집에서 글쓰기에 몰두해 있으면, 그녀는 아무리 밤이 깊어도 내 곁에 조용히 앉아, 어찌나 입을 다물고 있던지 나는 그녀가 잠든 줄로 여기기 일쑤였다."], ["But generally, when I raised my head, I saw her blue eyes looking at me with the quiet attention of which I have already spoken.", "그러나 대개 내가 고개를 들면, 앞서 말한 그 고요한 눈길로 나를 바라보고 있는 그녀의 푸른 눈이 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["‘Oh, what a weary boy!’ said Dora one night, when I met her eyes as I was shutting up my desk.", "\"아유, 어쩜 이리 지친 도련님이람!\" 어느 날 밤 책상을 정리하다 그녀의 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 도라가 말했다."]], [["‘What a weary girl!’ said I.", "\"어쩜 이리 지친 아가씨람!\""], ["‘That’s more to the purpose.", "내가 말했다. \"그게 더 맞는 말이지."], ["You must go to bed another time, my love.", "다음부터는 먼저 자러 가야 해, 여보."], ["It’s far too late for you.’", "당신한테는 너무 늦은 시각이야.\""]], [["‘No, don’t send me to bed!’ pleaded Dora, coming to my side.", "\"안 돼요, 날 자러 보내지 말아요!\" 도라가 내 곁으로 다가오며 애원했다."], ["‘Pray, don’t do that!’", "\"제발, 그러지 말아요!\""]], [["‘Dora!’ To my amazement she was sobbing on my neck.", "\"도라!\" 놀랍게도 그녀는 내 목에 기대어 흐느끼고 있었다."], ["‘Not well, my dear!", "\"몸이 안 좋아, 여보!"], ["not happy!’", "행복하지 않은 거야!\""]], [["‘Yes!", "\"아니에요!"], ["quite well, and very happy!’ said Dora.", "아주 건강하고, 아주 행복해요!\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘But say you’ll let me stop, and see you write.’", "\"하지만 내가 여기 머물면서 당신이 글 쓰는 걸 지켜봐도 된다고 말해 줘요.\""]], [["‘Why, what a sight for such bright eyes at midnight!’ I replied.", "\"아니, 이 한밤중에 그토록 반짝이는 눈으로 볼 게 뭐 그리 있다고!\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Are they bright, though?’ returned Dora, laughing.", "\"그래도 이 눈이 반짝이긴 해요?\" 도라가 웃으며 받았다."], ["‘I’m so glad they’re bright.’ ‘Little Vanity!’ said I.", "\"반짝인다니 정말 기뻐요.\" \"허영덩어리 같으니!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["But it was not vanity; it was only harmless delight in my admiration.", "그러나 그것은 허영이 아니었다. 그저 내가 감탄해 주는 것에 대한 순수한 기쁨일 뿐이었다."], ["I knew that very well, before she told me so.", "그녀가 말해 주기 전에도 나는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["‘If you think them pretty, say I may always stop, and see you write!’ said Dora.", "\"이 눈이 예쁘다고 생각하면, 내가 늘 여기 머물면서 당신 글 쓰는 걸 봐도 된다고 해 줘요!\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘Do you think them pretty?’", "\"이 눈이 예쁘다고 생각해요?\""]], [["‘Very pretty.’", "\"아주 예쁘지.\""]], [["‘Then let me always stop and see you write.’", "\"그럼 늘 여기 머물면서 당신 글 쓰는 걸 보게 해 줘요.\""]], [["‘I am afraid that won’t improve their brightness, Dora.’", "\"그래서야 이 눈이 더 반짝여지지는 않을 것 같은데, 도라.\""]], [["‘Yes, it will! Because, you clever boy, you’ll not forget me then, while you are full of silent fancies.", "\"아니에요, 더 반짝일 거예요! 왜냐면, 이 영리한 도련님아, 당신이 말없는 공상에 푹 빠져 있을 때에도 그러면 나를 잊지 않을 테니까요."], ["Will you mind it, if I say something very, very silly?---more than usual?’ inquired Dora, peeping over my shoulder into my face.", "내가 아주, 아주 바보 같은 소리를 — 평소보다 더 바보 같은 소리를 — 해도 괜찮겠어요?\" 도라가 내 어깨 너머로 내 얼굴을 빠끔히 들여다보며 물었다."]], [["‘What wonderful thing is that?’ said I.", "\"그 놀라운 게 뭔데?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Please let me hold the pens,’ said Dora.", "\"펜을 들고 있게 해 줘요.\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘I want to have something to do with all those many hours when you are so industrious.", "\"당신이 그토록 부지런히 일하는 그 숱한 시간 동안 나도 뭔가 할 일이 있었으면 좋겠어요."], ["May I hold the pens?’", "펜을 들고 있어도 돼요?\""]], [["The remembrance of her pretty joy when I said yes, brings tears into my eyes.", "내가 그러라고 했을 때 그녀가 보이던 그 예쁜 기쁨을 떠올리면 눈에 눈물이 고인다."], ["The next time I sat down to write, and regularly afterwards, she sat in her old place, with a spare bundle of pens at her side.", "다음번 글을 쓰려고 앉았을 때부터, 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧, 그녀는 예의 그 자리에 여분의 펜 한 묶음을 곁에 두고 앉았다."], ["Her triumph in this connexion with my work, and her delight when I wanted a new pen--which I very often feigned to do--suggested to me a new way of pleasing my child-wife.", "내 일과 이렇게 맺어진 것을 그녀가 어찌나 자랑스러워하던지, 또 내가 새 펜이 필요하다고 할 때면 — 나는 아주 자주 그런 척했다 — 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 그 모습에 나는 내 어린 아내를 기쁘게 해 줄 새로운 방법을 떠올렸다."], ["I occasionally made a pretence of wanting a page or two of manuscript copied.", "나는 이따금 원고를 한두 장 베껴 써 줘야 한다는 시늉을 했다."], ["Then Dora was in her glory.", "그러면 도라는 의기양양해졌다."], ["The preparations she made for this great work, the aprons she put on, the bibs she borrowed from the kitchen to keep off the ink, the time she took, the innumerable stoppages she made to have a laugh with Jip as if he understood it all, her conviction that her work was incomplete unless she signed her name at the end, and the way in which she would bring it to me, like a school-copy, and then, when I praised it, clasp me round the neck, are touching recollections to me, simple as they might appear to other men.", "이 대단한 작업을 위해 그녀가 차리던 채비며, 둘러쓰던 앞치마며, 잉크를 막으려고 부엌에서 빌려 오던 턱받이며, 들이던 시간이며, 마치 집이 다 알아듣기라도 하는 양 그 녀석과 한바탕 웃느라 수도 없이 멈추던 일이며, 끝에 제 이름을 서명하지 않으면 그 작업이 완성되지 않은 거라고 굳게 믿던 것이며, 그것을 마치 학교 습자장처럼 나에게 가져오던 모습이며, 그러고는 내가 칭찬해 주면 내 목을 끌어안던 그 모습은, 다른 사람들에게는 아무리 시시해 보일지언정 나에게는 가슴 뭉클한 추억이다."]], [["She took possession of the keys soon after this, and went jingling about the house with the whole bunch in a little basket, tied to her slender waist.", "그녀는 그 직후 열쇠들을 인수하여, 가느다란 허리에 묶은 작은 바구니에 그 꾸러미를 통째로 담고 짤랑짤랑 집 안을 돌아다녔다."], ["I seldom found that the places to which they belonged were locked, or that they were of any use except as a plaything for Jip--but Dora was pleased, and that pleased me.", "그 열쇠들이 맞는 곳이 잠겨 있는 일은 좀처럼 없었고, 집의 장난감 구실 말고는 무슨 쓸모가 있는 것도 아니었다 — 그러나 도라가 좋아하니, 그것이 나도 좋았다."], ["She was quite satisfied that a good deal was effected by this make-belief of housekeeping; and was as merry as if we had been keeping a baby-house, for a joke.", "그녀는 이 살림 소꿉놀이로 꽤 많은 일이 이루어진다고 흡족해했으며, 마치 우리가 장난삼아 인형 집 살림을 차린 것처럼 즐거워했다."]], [["So we went on.", "그렇게 우리는 살아갔다."], ["Dora was hardly less affectionate to my aunt than to me, and often told her of the time when she was afraid she was ‘a cross old thing’.", "도라는 고모님에게도 나에게 못지않게 살갑게 굴었고, 자기가 고모님을 '심술궂은 늙은이'로 여기며 무서워하던 시절 이야기를 자주 들려주곤 했다. 나는 고모님이 누군가에게 그토록 한결같이 마음을 푸는 것을 본 적이 없었다. 고모님은 집에게 살갑게 굴었다 — 집은 한 번도 응해 주지 않았는데도."], ["I never saw my aunt unbend more systematically to anyone.", "날이면 날마다 기타 소리에 귀를 기울였다 — 음악에는 취미가 없었을 텐데도."], ["She courted Jip, though Jip never responded; listened, day after day, to the guitar, though I am afraid she had no taste for music; never attacked the Incapables, though the temptation must have been severe; went wonderful distances on foot to purchase, as surprises, any trifles that she found out Dora wanted; and never came in by the garden, and missed her from the room, but she would call out, at the foot of the stairs, in a voice that sounded cheerfully all over the house:", "무능한 하인들을 한 번도 몰아붙이지 않았다 — 그 유혹이 분명 어지간히 컸을 텐데도. 도라가 갖고 싶어 한다는 걸 알아낸 것이라면 무엇이든 깜짝 선물로 사 주려고 먼 길을 걸어 다녔다. 그리고 정원 쪽으로 들어왔다가 방에 도라가 없으면, 어김없이 계단 아래에서, 온 집 안에 명랑하게 울려 퍼지는 목소리로 이렇게 외치곤 했다."]], [["‘Where’s Little Blossom?’", "\"우리 작은 꽃송이는 어디 있나?\""]], [["# CHAPTER 45. MR. DICK FULFILS MY AUNT’S PREDICTIONS", "# CHAPTER 45. MR. DICK FULFILS MY AUNT’S PREDICTIONS"]], [["It was some time now, since I had left the Doctor.", "내가 박사 선생님 곁을 떠난 지도 이제 제법 시간이 흘렀다."], ["Living in his neighbourhood, I saw him frequently; and we all went to his house on two or three occasions to dinner or tea.", "그 동네에 살다 보니 박사 선생님을 자주 뵈었고, 우리는 두세 번쯤 그 댁에 가서 저녁을 먹거나 차를 마셨다."], ["The Old Soldier was in permanent quarters under the Doctor’s roof.", "늙은 군인 아주머니는 박사 선생님 댁 지붕 아래 아주 눌러앉아 있었다."], ["She was exactly the same as ever, and the same immortal butterflies hovered over her cap.", "아주머니는 예전과 조금도 다름없었고, 그 모자 위로는 죽지 않는 나비들이 여전히 너울거렸다."]], [["Like some other mothers, whom I have known in the course of my life, Mrs. Markleham was far more fond of pleasure than her daughter was.", "내가 살아오면서 알게 된 다른 몇몇 어머니들처럼, 마크럼 부인도 자기 딸보다 훨씬 더 노는 것을 좋아했다."], ["She required a great deal of amusement, and, like a deep old soldier, pretended, in consulting her own inclinations, to be devoting herself to her child.", "아주머니에게는 즐길 거리가 무척이나 많이 필요했는데, 닳고 닳은 늙은 군인답게, 자기가 좋아하는 일을 찾아 즐기면서도 마치 딸을 위해 헌신하는 척했다."], ["The Doctor’s desire that Annie should be entertained, was therefore particularly acceptable to this excellent parent; who expressed unqualified approval of his discretion.", "그러니 애니를 즐겁게 해 주고 싶다는 박사 선생님의 바람은 이 훌륭한 어머니에게 특히나 반가운 것이었고, 아주머니는 박사 선생님의 사려 깊음을 두말없이 칭찬해 마지않았다."]], [["I have no doubt, indeed, that she probed the Doctor’s wound without knowing it.", "사실 나는, 아주머니가 자기도 모르게 박사 선생님의 상처를 후벼 파고 있었다고 믿어 의심치 않는다."], ["Meaning nothing but a certain matured frivolity and selfishness, not always inseparable from full-blown years, I think she confirmed him in his fear that he was a constraint upon his young wife, and that there was no congeniality of feeling between them, by so strongly commending his design of lightening the load of her life.", "별다른 뜻 없이 그저, 나이가 무르익으면 으레 따라붙기 마련인 어떤 농익은 경박함과 이기심을 품었을 뿐이지만, 아주머니가 아내의 삶의 짐을 덜어 주려는 박사 선생님의 계획을 그토록 열렬히 추켜세움으로써, 자기가 어린 아내에게 거추장스러운 존재라는 두려움, 그리고 둘 사이에 마음이 통하는 구석이 없다는 두려움을 박사 선생님에게 더욱 굳혀 준 것이라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["‘My dear soul,’ she said to him one day when I was present, ‘you know there is no doubt it would be a little pokey for Annie to be always shut up here.’", "\"여보게,\" 어느 날 내가 그 자리에 있을 때 아주머니가 박사 선생님에게 말했다. \"애니가 늘 여기만 틀어박혀 있으면 좀 갑갑할 거라는 건, 자네도 의심할 여지가 없지 않은가.\""]], [["The Doctor nodded his benevolent head.", "박사 선생님은 인자한 머리를 끄덕였다."], ["‘When she comes to her mother’s age,’ said Mrs.", "\"그 애가 제 어미 나이쯤 되면,\""], ["Markleham, with a flourish of her fan, ‘then it’ll be another thing.", "마크럼 부인이 부채를 한 번 펄럭이며 말했다. \"그땐 또 다른 얘기지."], ["You might put ME into a Jail, with genteel society and a rubber, and I should never care to come out.", "나야 점잖은 사람들이랑 카드놀이만 할 수 있다면 감옥에 처넣어도 나오고 싶은 생각이 안 들 거야."], ["But I am not Annie, you know; and Annie is not her mother.’", "하지만 나는 애니가 아니잖나, 알다시피. 그리고 애니는 제 어미가 아니고.\""]], [["‘Surely, surely,’ said the Doctor.", "\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고요.\" 박사 선생님이 말했다."]], [["‘You are the best of creatures--no, I beg your pardon!’ for the Doctor made a gesture of deprecation, ‘I must say before your face, as I always say behind your back, you are the best of creatures; but of course you don’t--now do you?---enter into the same pursuits and fancies as Annie?’", "\"자네는 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 사람이야 — 아니, 미안하네!\" 박사 선생님이 손사래를 쳤기 때문이다. \"내 늘 자네 뒤에서 하는 말을 자네 앞에서도 해야겠는데, 자네는 세상에 둘도 없이 착한 사람이야. 하지만 자네는 당연히 — 안 그런가? — 애니와 똑같은 취미나 흥취에 빠져들진 못하잖나?\""]], [["‘No,’ said the Doctor, in a sorrowful tone.", "\"못하지요.\" 박사 선생님이 서글픈 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘No, of course not,’ retorted the Old Soldier.", "\"그럼, 당연히 못하지.\" 늙은 군인이 받아쳤다."], ["‘Take your Dictionary, for example.", "\"예를 들어 자네 그 사전을 보게나."], ["What a useful work a Dictionary is!", "사전이라는 게 얼마나 쓸모 있는 책인가!"], ["What a necessary work! The meanings of words!", "얼마나 꼭 필요한 책이냐고! 단어의 뜻이라니!"], ["Without Doctor Johnson, or somebody of that sort, we might have been at this present moment calling an Italian-iron, a bedstead.", "존슨 박사인지 그 비슷한 누군가가 없었더라면, 우린 지금 이 순간에도 다리미를 침대 틀이라고 부르고 있었을지도 몰라."], ["But we can’t expect a Dictionary--especially when it’s making--to interest Annie, can we?’", "하지만 사전이 — 더구나 한창 만드는 중인 사전이 — 애니의 흥미를 끌 거라고 기대할 순 없잖나, 안 그래?\""]], [["The Doctor shook his head.", "박사 선생님은 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘And that’s why I so much approve,’ said Mrs. Markleham, tapping him on the shoulder with her shut-up fan, ‘of your thoughtfulness.", "\"그래서 내가 자네의 그 깊은 마음씀을 그토록 갸륵하게 여긴다는 거야.\" 마크럼 부인이 접은 부채로 박사 선생님의 어깨를 톡톡 치며 말했다."], ["It shows that you don’t expect, as many elderly people do expect, old heads on young shoulders.", "\"이건 자네가, 나이 든 사람들이 흔히 그러듯, 젊은 어깨에 늙은 머리가 얹혀 있길 바라지 않는다는 뜻이지."], ["You have studied Annie’s character, and you understand it. That’s what I find so charming!’", "자네는 애니의 성품을 잘 살폈고, 그걸 이해하고 있어. 그게 바로 내가 그렇게나 멋지다고 여기는 점이라네!\""]], [["Even the calm and patient face of Doctor Strong expressed some little sense of pain, I thought, under the infliction of these compliments.", "이런 칭찬 세례를 받자, 침착하고 참을성 많은 스트롱 박사 선생님의 얼굴에마저 어렴풋한 고통의 빛이 스치는 것 같았다."]], [["‘Therefore, my dear Doctor,’ said the Old Soldier, giving him several affectionate taps, ‘you may command me, at all times and seasons.", "\"그러니까, 친애하는 박사 선생,\" 늙은 군인이 박사 선생님을 다정하게 몇 번 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"언제 어느 때든 나를 마음껏 부려도 좋네."], ["Now, do understand that I am entirely at your service.", "자, 내가 자네를 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠다는 걸 알아 두게."], ["I am ready to go with Annie to operas, concerts, exhibitions, all kinds of places; and you shall never find that I am tired.", "나는 애니를 데리고 오페라며 음악회며 전시회며, 온갖 데를 다 따라나설 준비가 돼 있어. 그리고 자네는 내가 지치는 꼴을 결코 못 볼 걸세."], ["Duty, my dear Doctor, before every consideration in the universe!’", "의무라네, 친애하는 박사 선생, 세상 그 무엇보다 의무가 먼저야!\""]], [["She was as good as her word.", "아주머니는 자기 말을 그대로 지켰다."], ["She was one of those people who can bear a great deal of pleasure, and she never flinched in her perseverance in the cause.", "아주머니는 엄청난 즐거움도 거뜬히 견뎌 내는 부류의 사람이었고, 그 대의를 향한 끈기에서 한 번도 물러서는 법이 없었다."], ["She seldom got hold of the newspaper (which she settled herself down in the softest chair in the house to read through an eye-glass, every day, for two hours), but she found out something that she was certain Annie would like to see.", "신문(아주머니는 날마다 두 시간씩, 집에서 제일 푹신한 의자에 떡하니 자리 잡고 앉아 안경 너머로 그것을 처음부터 끝까지 읽었다)을 손에 쥐었다 하면, 애니가 분명 보고 싶어 할 것이 틀림없는 무언가를 어김없이 찾아냈다."], ["It was in vain for Annie to protest that she was weary of such things.", "애니가 그런 일에는 진력이 났다고 아무리 항변해도 소용없었다."], ["Her mother’s remonstrance always was, ‘Now, my dear Annie, I am sure you know better; and I must tell you, my love, that you are not making a proper return for the kindness of Doctor Strong.’", "어머니의 잔소리는 늘 한결같았다. \"자, 우리 애니, 너도 잘 알 텐데. 그리고 얘야, 내 말해 두는데, 너는 스트롱 박사의 친절에 합당한 보답을 못 하고 있는 거야.\""]], [["This was usually said in the Doctor’s presence, and appeared to me to constitute Annie’s principal inducement for withdrawing her objections when she made any.", "이 말은 대개 박사 선생님이 있는 자리에서 나왔고, 애니가 무언가 반대를 했다가도 그것을 거두어들이게 만드는 주된 빌미가 되는 것 같았다."], ["But in general she resigned herself to her mother, and went where the Old Soldier would.", "하지만 대개 애니는 어머니에게 순순히 따랐고, 늙은 군인이 가자는 데로 갔다."]], [["It rarely happened now that Mr. Maldon accompanied them.", "이제는 몰던 씨가 그들과 함께 가는 일이 거의 없었다."], ["Sometimes my aunt and Dora were invited to do so, and accepted the invitation.", "가끔은 고모님과 도라가 함께 가자는 청을 받아 그 초대에 응하기도 했다."], ["Sometimes Dora only was asked.", "가끔은 도라만 부르기도 했다."], ["The time had been, when I should have been uneasy in her going; but reflection on what had passed that former night in the Doctor’s study, had made a change in my mistrust.", "한때는 도라가 거기 가는 것이 마음 불편했던 적도 있었다. 그러나 예전 그날 밤 박사 선생님의 서재에서 있었던 일을 곱씹어 본 뒤로, 내 미심쩍은 마음에도 변화가 생겼다."], ["I believed that the Doctor was right, and I had no worse suspicions.", "나는 박사 선생님이 옳다고 믿었고, 더 나쁜 의심 따위는 품지 않았다."]], [["My aunt rubbed her nose sometimes when she happened to be alone with me, and said she couldn’t make it out; she wished they were happier; she didn’t think our military friend (so she always called the Old Soldier) mended the matter at all.", "고모님은 가끔 나와 단둘이 있게 되면 코를 문지르며, 도무지 영문을 모르겠다고 말했다. 그분들이 좀 더 행복했으면 좋겠다고, 우리의 그 군인 양반(고모님은 늙은 군인을 늘 그렇게 불렀다)이 사태를 조금도 나아지게 하는 것 같지 않다고 했다."], ["My aunt further expressed her opinion, ‘that if our military friend would cut off those butterflies, and give ‘em to the chimney-sweepers for May-day, it would look like the beginning of something sensible on her part.’", "고모님은 한술 더 떠 이런 의견을 내놓았다. \"우리 군인 양반이 그 나비들을 싹둑 잘라서, 오월제 때 굴뚝 청소부들한테나 줘 버린다면, 그게 그 양반 쪽에서 뭔가 분별 있는 일을 시작하는 신호처럼 보일 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["But her abiding reliance was on Mr. Dick.", "하지만 고모님이 한결같이 기대를 거는 사람은 딕 씨였다."], ["That man had evidently an idea in his head, she said; and if he could only once pen it up into a corner, which was his great difficulty, he would distinguish himself in some extraordinary manner.", "저 사람 머릿속에는 분명 어떤 생각이 들어 있다고, 고모님은 말했다. 다만 그 생각을 어느 한 구석에 단단히 몰아넣을 수만 있다면 — 그게 저 사람한테는 큰 난관이었지만 — 어떤 비범한 방식으로 두각을 나타낼 거라는 것이었다."]], [["Unconscious of this prediction, Mr. Dick continued to occupy precisely the same ground in reference to the Doctor and to Mrs.", "이 예언을 까맣게 모른 채, 딕 씨는 박사 선생님과 스트롱 부인에 관해서라면 정확히 똑같은 자리를 계속 지키고 있었다."], ["Strong. He seemed neither to advance nor to recede.", "앞으로 나아가지도, 뒤로 물러서지도 않는 것 같았다."], ["He appeared to have settled into his original foundation, like a building; and I must confess that my faith in his ever Moving, was not much greater than if he had been a building.", "마치 건물처럼 애초의 토대 위에 그대로 눌러앉은 듯했다. 그리고 솔직히 고백하건대, 딕 씨가 언젠가 움직일 거라는 내 믿음은, 그가 건물이었을 경우와 별반 다를 게 없었다."]], [["But one night, when I had been married some months, Mr.", "그런데 어느 날 밤, 내가 결혼한 지 몇 달쯤 됐을 무렵, 딕 씨가 응접실로 머리를 쑥 들이밀었다."], ["Dick put his head into the parlour, where I was writing alone (Dora having gone out with my aunt to take tea with the two little birds), and said, with a significant cough:", "나는 거기서 혼자 글을 쓰고 있었다(도라는 고모님과 함께 그 두 마리 작은 새와 차를 마시러 나가고 없었다). 딕 씨는 의미심장하게 기침을 한 번 하고는 말했다."]], [["‘You couldn’t speak to me without inconveniencing yourself, Trotwood, I am afraid?’", "\"트롯우드, 자네한테 폐를 끼치지 않고 잠깐 얘기 좀 할 수 있을까 모르겠네?\""]], [["‘Certainly, Mr.", "\"그럼요, 딕 씨.\""], ["Dick,’ said I; ‘come in!’", "내가 말했다. \"들어오세요!\""]], [["‘Trotwood,’ said Mr.", "\"트롯우드.\""], ["Dick, laying his finger on the side of his nose, after he had shaken hands with me.", "딕 씨가 나와 악수를 하고 나서, 손가락을 콧등에 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["‘Before I sit down, I wish to make an observation.", "\"앉기 전에 한마디 짚고 넘어가고 싶은 게 있네."], ["You know your aunt?’", "자네, 자네 고모님 알지?\""]], [["‘A little,’ I replied.", "\"조금은요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘She is the most wonderful woman in the world, sir!’", "\"그분은 이 세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인일세!\""]], [["After the delivery of this communication, which he shot out of himself as if he were loaded with it, Mr. Dick sat down with greater gravity than usual, and looked at me.", "자기 안에 그 말이 잔뜩 장전돼 있기라도 했던 것처럼 그것을 탕 쏘아 내고는, 딕 씨는 여느 때보다 더 진지하게 자리에 앉아 나를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Now, boy,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 얘야.\""], ["Dick, ‘I am going to put a question to you.’", "딕 씨가 말했다. \"자네한테 질문 하나를 던지겠네.\""]], [["‘As many as you please,’ said I.", "\"얼마든지 하세요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘What do you consider me, sir?’ asked Mr. Dick, folding his arms.", "\"자네는 나를 뭐라고 생각하나?\" 딕 씨가 팔짱을 끼며 물었다."]], [["‘A dear old friend,’ said I.", "\"정다운 오랜 친구죠.\""], ["‘Thank you, Trotwood,’ returned Mr.", "내가 말했다. \"고맙네, 트롯우드.\""], ["Dick, laughing, and reaching across in high glee to shake hands with me.", "딕 씨가 웃으며, 무척 기뻐서 몸을 쭉 뻗어 나와 악수를 하려고 손을 내밀며 대답했다."], ["‘But I mean, boy,’ resuming his gravity, ‘what do you consider me in this respect?’ touching his forehead.", "\"하지만 내 말은 말이야, 얘야,\" 다시 진지함을 되찾으며 자기 이마를 짚었다. \"이쪽으로 봐서 자네는 나를 뭐라고 생각하느냔 말이야.\""]], [["I was puzzled how to answer, but he helped me with a word.", "나는 어떻게 대답해야 할지 난감했지만, 그가 한마디로 거들어 주었다."]], [["‘Weak?’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"모자라다고?\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Well,’ I replied, dubiously.", "\"글쎄요.\" 내가 우물쭈물 대답했다."], ["‘Rather so.’", "\"좀 그런 편이죠.\""]], [["‘Exactly!’ cried Mr.", "\"바로 그거야!\""], ["Dick, who seemed quite enchanted by my reply.", "딕 씨가 외쳤는데, 내 대답에 아주 홀딱 반한 듯했다."], ["‘That is, Trotwood, when they took some of the trouble out of you-know-who’s head, and put it you know where, there was a--’ Mr.", "\"그러니까, 트롯우드, 그 누군지 알지, 그 사람 머릿속에서 골칫거리를 좀 끄집어내서, 자네도 알다시피 그걸 어디다 집어넣었을 때 말이야, 거기엔 — \""], ["Dick made his two hands revolve very fast about each other a great number of times, and then brought them into collision, and rolled them over and over one another, to express confusion.", "딕 씨는 두 손을 서로 맞대고 아주 빠르게 여러 번 빙글빙글 돌리더니, 와락 부딪쳐서는, 뒤죽박죽 엉킨 모양을 나타내려는 듯 서로 위로 아래로 마구 비벼 댔다."], ["‘There was that sort of thing done to me somehow. Eh?’", "\"어쩐 일인지 나한테도 그런 비슷한 일이 일어났던 거야. 응?\""]], [["I nodded at him, and he nodded back again.", "내가 그를 향해 고개를 끄덕이자, 그도 마주 끄덕였다."]], [["‘In short, boy,’ said Mr.", "\"한마디로 말해서, 얘야,\""], ["Dick, dropping his voice to a whisper, ‘I am simple.’", "딕 씨가 목소리를 속삭이듯 낮추며 말했다. \"나는 단순한 사람이야.\""]], [["I would have qualified that conclusion, but he stopped me.", "나는 그 결론을 좀 누그러뜨려 보려 했지만, 그가 나를 막았다."]], [["‘Yes, I am!", "\"맞아, 단순해!"], ["She pretends I am not.", "그분은 안 그런 척하지."], ["She won’t hear of it; but I am. I know I am.", "그분은 그 말을 들으려고도 안 해. 하지만 나는 단순한 사람이야."], ["If she hadn’t stood my friend, sir, I should have been shut up, to lead a dismal life these many years.", "내가 잘 안다고. 그분이 내 편이 되어 주지 않았더라면, 나는 갇혀서 이 오랜 세월을 침울하게 살았을 걸세."], ["But I’ll provide for her!", "하지만 내가 그분을 보살펴 드릴 거야!"], ["I never spend the copying money.", "나는 필사해서 번 돈은 한 푼도 안 써."], ["I put it in a box.", "그걸 상자에 넣어 두지."], ["I have made a will.", "유언장도 만들어 놨어."], ["I’ll leave it all to her. She shall be rich--noble!’", "그걸 죄다 그분께 남길 거야. 그분은 부자가 될 걸세 — 귀족처럼 떵떵거리며 살 거라고!\""]], [["Mr. Dick took out his pocket-handkerchief, and wiped his eyes.", "딕 씨는 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 눈가를 닦았다."], ["He then folded it up with great care, pressed it smooth between his two hands, put it in his pocket, and seemed to put my aunt away with it.", "그러고는 그것을 무척 정성스레 접어, 두 손바닥 사이에 끼워 반반하게 누른 다음, 주머니에 집어넣었다. 마치 고모님을 그 손수건과 함께 갈무리해 넣는 것 같았다."]], [["‘Now you are a scholar, Trotwood,’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"자, 자네는 학자야, 트롯우드.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You are a fine scholar.", "\"자네는 훌륭한 학자야."], ["You know what a learned man, what a great man, the Doctor is.", "박사 선생님이 얼마나 학식 깊은 분인지, 얼마나 위대한 분인지 자네는 알지."], ["You know what honour he has always done me.", "그분이 늘 나를 얼마나 높이 대접해 주셨는지도 알 거야."], ["Not proud in his wisdom.", "자기 지혜를 뽐내지 않으셔."], ["Humble, humble--condescending even to poor Dick, who is simple and knows nothing.", "겸손하고, 또 겸손해서 — 단순하고 아무것도 모르는 가엾은 딕한테까지도 몸을 낮추신다고."], ["I have sent his name up, on a scrap of paper, to the kite, along the string, when it has been in the sky, among the larks.", "연이 하늘 위에, 종달새들 사이에 떠 있을 때, 나는 종잇조각에 그분 이름을 적어서 연줄을 따라 위로 올려 보냈지."], ["The kite has been glad to receive it, sir, and the sky has been brighter with it.’", "연이 그걸 기꺼이 받아 갔다네, 트롯우드. 그러니 하늘이 그 덕에 더 환해졌지.\""]], [["I delighted him by saying, most heartily, that the Doctor was deserving of our best respect and highest esteem.", "박사 선생님이 우리의 가장 깊은 존경과 가장 높은 흠모를 받을 만한 분이라고 내가 진심을 다해 말하자, 그는 무척 기뻐했다."]], [["‘And his beautiful wife is a star,’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"그리고 그분의 아름다운 부인은 별이야.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["‘A shining star.", "\"반짝이는 별이지."], ["I have seen her shine, sir. But,’ bringing his chair nearer, and laying one hand upon my knee--‘clouds, sir--clouds.’", "나는 그분이 빛나는 걸 봤어, 트롯우드. 하지만,\" 의자를 더 가까이 끌어당기고, 한 손을 내 무릎에 얹으며 — \"먹구름이야, 트롯우드 — 먹구름.\""]], [["I answered the solicitude which his face expressed, by conveying the same expression into my own, and shaking my head.", "나는 그의 얼굴에 어린 근심에 답하듯, 똑같은 표정을 내 얼굴에 담고 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘What clouds?’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"무슨 먹구름이지?\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["He looked so wistfully into my face, and was so anxious to understand, that I took great pains to answer him slowly and distinctly, as I might have entered on an explanation to a child.", "그가 어찌나 애타게 내 얼굴을 들여다보며 알고 싶어 안달하던지, 나는 마치 아이에게 무언가를 설명해 주듯, 천천히 또박또박 대답하려고 무진 애를 썼다."]], [["‘There is some unfortunate division between them,’ I replied.", "\"두 분 사이에 무언가 불행한 골이 패었어요.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘Some unhappy cause of separation.", "\"무언가 안타까운 이유로 마음이 멀어진 거죠."], ["A secret.", "비밀이에요."], ["It may be inseparable from the discrepancy in their years.", "어쩌면 두 분의 나이 차이와 떼어 놓을 수 없는 일인지도 모르고요."], ["It may have grown up out of almost nothing.’", "어쩌면 거의 아무것도 아닌 데서 자라난 것인지도 모르죠.\""]], [["Mr. Dick, who had told off every sentence with a thoughtful nod, paused when I had done, and sat considering, with his eyes upon my face, and his hand upon my knee.", "문장 하나하나를 사려 깊게 끄덕여 가며 헤아리던 딕 씨는, 내가 말을 마치자 잠시 멈추더니, 눈은 내 얼굴에, 손은 내 무릎에 둔 채로 앉아 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["‘Doctor not angry with her, Trotwood?’ he said, after some time.", "\"박사 선생님이 부인한테 화가 나신 게 아니지, 트롯우드?\" 한참 있다가 그가 말했다."]], [["‘No. Devoted to her.’", "\"아니에요. 부인께 헌신하고 계시죠.\""]], [["‘Then, I have got it, boy!’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"그럼, 알아냈어, 얘야!\" 딕 씨가 말했다."]], [["The sudden exultation with which he slapped me on the knee, and leaned back in his chair, with his eyebrows lifted up as high as he could possibly lift them, made me think him farther out of his wits than ever.", "그가 느닷없이 의기양양해져서 내 무릎을 탁 치고는, 눈썹을 올릴 수 있는 데까지 한껏 치켜올린 채 의자에 등을 기대는 바람에, 나는 그가 그 어느 때보다 더 정신이 나갔다고 생각했다."], ["He became as suddenly grave again, and leaning forward as before, said--first respectfully taking out his pocket-handkerchief, as if it really did represent my aunt:", "그러더니 또 갑작스레 진지해져서, 아까처럼 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 — 먼저 손수건을, 그것이 정말로 고모님을 대신하기라도 하는 듯 공손히 꺼내고는 — 말했다."]], [["‘Most wonderful woman in the world, Trotwood.", "\"세상에서 가장 놀라운 여인이야, 트롯우드."], ["Why has she done nothing to set things right?’", "그런데 그분은 왜 일을 바로잡으려고 아무것도 안 했을까?\""]], [["‘Too delicate and difficult a subject for such interference,’ I replied.", "\"그렇게 끼어들기엔 너무 미묘하고 어려운 일이니까요.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Fine scholar,’ said Mr.", "\"훌륭한 학자야.\""], ["Dick, touching me with his finger.", "딕 씨가 손가락으로 나를 가리키며 말했다."], ["‘Why has HE done nothing?’", "\"그럼 그분은 왜 아무것도 안 했지?\""]], [["‘For the same reason,’ I returned.", "\"같은 이유에서죠.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘Then, I have got it, boy!’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"그럼, 알아냈어, 얘야!\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["And he stood up before me, more exultingly than before, nodding his head, and striking himself repeatedly upon the breast, until one might have supposed that he had nearly nodded and struck all the breath out of his body.", "그러고는 아까보다 더 의기양양하게 내 앞에 우뚝 서서, 고개를 끄덕이고 제 가슴을 거듭 쳐 댔는데, 어찌나 끄덕이고 쳐 대던지 몸에서 숨이 다 빠져나갈 지경이 아닌가 싶을 정도였다."]], [["‘A poor fellow with a craze, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"무언가에 홀린 가엾은 사람이,\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["Dick, ‘a simpleton, a weak-minded person--present company, you know!’ striking himself again, ‘may do what wonderful people may not do.", "\"얼간이가, 마음이 모자란 사람이 — 바로 여기 있는 이 사람 말이야!\" 다시 제 가슴을 치며 — \"놀라운 사람들이 못 하는 일을 해낼 수도 있는 법이지."], ["I’ll bring them together, boy.", "내가 두 분을 화해시킬 거야, 얘야."], ["I’ll try.", "내가 해 볼게."], ["They’ll not blame me.", "두 분은 나를 탓하지 않을 거야."], ["They’ll not object to me.", "두 분은 나한테 시비를 걸지 않을 거야."], ["They’ll not mind what I do, if it’s wrong.", "내가 무슨 일을 하든, 그게 틀린 일이라 해도 두 분은 개의치 않을 거라고."], ["I’m only Mr. Dick.", "나는 그저 딕 씨일 뿐이니까."], ["And who minds Dick?", "그리고 누가 딕 따위를 마음에 두겠어?"], ["Dick’s nobody! Whoo!’ He blew a slight, contemptuous breath, as if he blew himself away.", "딕은 아무것도 아닌데! 후우!\" 그는 자기 자신을 훅 불어 날려 버리기라도 하듯, 가볍게, 깔보듯 입김을 한 번 내뿜었다."]], [["It was fortunate he had proceeded so far with his mystery, for we heard the coach stop at the little garden gate, which brought my aunt and Dora home.", "그가 자기 비밀스러운 계획을 거기까지 풀어놓은 것이 다행이었으니, 마차가 작은 정원 문 앞에 멈추는 소리가 들리며 고모님과 도라가 집으로 돌아왔기 때문이다."]], [["‘Not a word, boy!’ he pursued in a whisper; ‘leave all the blame with Dick--simple Dick--mad Dick.", "\"한마디도 하지 마, 얘야!\" 그가 속삭이며 말을 이었다. \"모든 탓은 딕한테 다 떠넘기게 — 단순한 딕, 미친 딕 말이야."], ["I have been thinking, sir, for some time, that I was getting it, and now I have got it. After what you have said to me, I am sure I have got it.", "얼마 전부터 내가 그걸 알아 가고 있다는 느낌이 들었는데, 이제 알아냈어. 자네가 나한테 해 준 말을 듣고 나니, 정말 알아낸 게 틀림없어. 좋아!\""], ["All right!’ Not another word did Mr. Dick utter on the subject; but he made a very telegraph of himself for the next half-hour (to the great disturbance of my aunt’s mind), to enjoin inviolable secrecy on me.", "딕 씨는 그 일에 관해 더는 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않았다. 다만 그 후 삼십 분 동안(고모님의 마음을 어지간히도 어지럽혀 가며) 나에게 절대적인 함구령을 내리려고 제 몸을 영락없는 전신기로 만들어 댔다."]], [["To my surprise, I heard no more about it for some two or three weeks, though I was sufficiently interested in the result of his endeavours; descrying a strange gleam of good sense--I say nothing of good feeling, for that he always exhibited--in the conclusion to which he had come.", "뜻밖에도, 나는 그 일에 관해 두세 주쯤 아무 말도 듣지 못했다. 그의 노력의 결과가 무척이나 궁금했는데도 말이다. 그가 다다른 결론에서, 나는 묘하게 번득이는 분별력을 알아챘다 — 따뜻한 마음씨야 그가 늘 보여 주던 것이니 굳이 말할 것도 없고."], ["At last I began to believe, that, in the flighty and unsettled state of his mind, he had either forgotten his intention or abandoned it.", "마침내 나는, 그토록 들떠 있고 진득하지 못한 그의 마음 상태로 보아, 그가 자기 뜻을 잊어버렸거나 아니면 단념해 버렸으리라고 믿기 시작했다."]], [["One fair evening, when Dora was not inclined to go out, my aunt and I strolled up to the Doctor’s cottage.", "어느 청명한 저녁, 도라가 나가기 싫어하기에, 고모님과 나는 박사 선생님 댁까지 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 올라갔다."], ["It was autumn, when there were no debates to vex the evening air; and I remember how the leaves smelt like our garden at Blunderstone as we trod them under foot, and how the old, unhappy feeling, seemed to go by, on the sighing wind.", "때는 가을이라, 저녁 공기를 어지럽힐 토론도 없었다. 그리고 나는 기억한다, 발밑에 밟히는 낙엽에서 블런더스톤의 우리 집 정원 같은 냄새가 풍기던 것을, 그리고 그 오래된 불행했던 느낌이 한숨짓는 바람결을 타고 스쳐 지나가는 듯하던 것을."]], [["It was twilight when we reached the cottage. Mrs.", "우리가 그 댁에 다다랐을 때는 땅거미가 내리고 있었다."], ["Strong was just coming out of the garden, where Mr. Dick yet lingered, busy with his knife, helping the gardener to point some stakes.", "스트롱 부인은 마침 정원에서 나오던 참이었는데, 거기서는 딕 씨가 아직 칼을 들고 분주히, 정원사가 말뚝을 깎아 뾰족하게 만드는 일을 거들며 어정거리고 있었다."], ["The Doctor was engaged with someone in his study; but the visitor would be gone directly, Mrs. Strong said, and begged us to remain and see him.", "박사 선생님은 서재에서 누군가와 일을 보는 중이었지만, 손님이 곧 갈 테니 남아서 만나고 가시라고 스트롱 부인이 청했다."], ["We went into the drawing-room with her, and sat down by the darkening window.", "우리는 부인을 따라 응접실로 들어가, 어두워져 가는 창가에 앉았다."], ["There was never any ceremony about the visits of such old friends and neighbours as we were.", "우리처럼 오랜 벗이자 이웃인 사람들이 오갈 때 격식 따위는 차린 적이 없었다."]], [["We had not sat here many minutes, when Mrs. Markleham, who usually contrived to be in a fuss about something, came bustling in, with her newspaper in her hand, and said, out of breath, ‘My goodness gracious, Annie, why didn’t you tell me there was someone in the Study!’", "여기 앉은 지 몇 분 되지 않아, 늘 무언가로 한바탕 부산을 떨곤 하던 마크럼 부인이, 신문을 손에 들고 헐레벌떡 들어오더니 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"어머나 세상에, 애니야, 서재에 손님이 와 있다는 걸 왜 나한테 말 안 했니!\""]], [["‘My dear mama,’ she quietly returned, ‘how could I know that you desired the information?’", "\"엄마.\" 애니가 차분히 대답했다. \"엄마가 그걸 알고 싶어 하실 줄 제가 어떻게 알았겠어요?\""]], [["‘Desired the information!’ said Mrs. Markleham, sinking on the sofa.", "\"알고 싶어 했냐고!\" 마크럼 부인이 소파에 털썩 주저앉으며 말했다."], ["‘I never had such a turn in all my life!’", "\"내 평생 그렇게 놀란 적이 없다!\""]], [["‘Have you been to the Study, then, mama?’ asked Annie.", "\"그럼 서재에 들어가 보셨어요, 엄마?\" 애니가 물었다."]], [["‘BEEN to the Study, my dear!’ she returned emphatically.", "\"들어가 봤다마다, 얘야!\" 부인이 힘주어 대답했다."], ["‘Indeed I have!", "\"정말로 들어가 봤지!"], ["I came upon the amiable creature--if you’ll imagine my feelings, Miss Trotwood and David--in the act of making his will.’", "그 사랑스러운 양반이 — 트롯우드 양과 데이비드, 내 심정을 좀 헤아려 주게나 — 유언장을 쓰고 있는 현장을 딱 맞닥뜨렸지 뭐냐.\""]], [["Her daughter looked round from the window quickly.", "부인의 딸이 창가에서 홱 돌아보았다."]], [["‘In the act, my dear Annie,’ repeated Mrs.", "\"바로 그 현장을, 우리 애니야,\""], ["Markleham, spreading the newspaper on her lap like a table-cloth, and patting her hands upon it, ‘of making his last Will and Testament.", "마크럼 부인이 신문을 식탁보처럼 무릎에 쫙 펼치고 그 위를 손으로 토닥거리며 거듭 말했다. \"유언장을, 그 마지막 유언과 유증을 작성하고 있는 현장을 말이야."], ["The foresight and affection of the dear!", "그 사랑스러운 양반의 앞날을 내다보는 마음과 깊은 정이라니!"], ["I must tell you how it was. I really must, in justice to the darling--for he is nothing less!--tell you how it was.", "어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 내 너한테 꼭 얘기해야겠다. 그 양반을 위해서라도 정말이지 얘기해야겠어 — 그이는 더없이 사랑스러운 사람이니까 말이야!"], ["Perhaps you know, Miss Trotwood, that there is never a candle lighted in this house, until one’s eyes are literally falling out of one’s head with being stretched to read the paper.", "— 어쩌다 그랬는지 들어 보렴. 트롯우드 양도 아마 알 거야, 이 집에선 신문을 읽느라 눈알이 머리에서 빠져나올 지경이 되도록 죽어라 애써야 비로소 촛불 한 자루 켜 준다는 걸."], ["And that there is not a chair in this house, in which a paper can be what I call, read, except one in the Study.", "그리고 이 집엔 서재에 있는 의자 하나 빼고는, 내가 말하는 그 '읽는다'는 걸 할 만한 의자가 없다는 것도."], ["This took me to the Study, where I saw a light.", "그래서 서재로 갔더니, 불빛이 보이지 뭐냐."], ["I opened the door.", "문을 열었지."], ["In company with the dear Doctor were two professional people, evidently connected with the law, and they were all three standing at the table: the darling Doctor pen in hand.", "그 사랑스러운 박사 양반과 함께 보아하니 법 쪽 일을 하는 사람 둘이 있었고, 세 사람이 다 탁자 앞에 서 있었어. 그 사랑스러운 박사 양반은 펜을 손에 쥐고 말이야."], ["“This simply expresses then,”", "'그러니까 이 문서는,'"], ["said the Doctor--Annie, my love, attend to the very words--“this simply expresses then, gentlemen, the confidence I have in Mrs. Strong, and gives her all unconditionally?”", "박사 양반이 말하더라 — 애니야, 얘야, 그 말 한마디 한마디를 잘 새겨들으렴 — '그러니까 이 문서는 말입니다, 여러분, 내가 스트롱 부인에게 품은 신뢰를 그저 명시할 뿐이며, 그녀에게 모든 것을 아무 조건 없이 넘긴다는 뜻이지요?' 그 법 쪽 사람 하나가 대답하더라."], ["One of the professional people replied, “And gives her all unconditionally.”", "'그리고 그녀에게 모든 것을 아무 조건 없이 넘긴다는 뜻입니다.'"], ["Upon that, with the natural feelings of a mother, I said, “Good God, I beg your pardon!”", "그 말에, 어미의 본능적인 심정으로, 내가 '아이고 맙소사, 실례했습니다!'"], ["fell over the door-step, and came away through the little back passage where the pantry is.’", "하고는, 문턱에 발이 걸려 휘청거리며 식료품 저장실이 있는 그 작은 뒷복도로 빠져나왔단다.\""]], [["Mrs. Strong opened the window, and went out into the verandah, where she stood leaning against a pillar.", "스트롱 부인이 창문을 열고 베란다로 나가, 거기 기둥에 기대어 섰다."]], [["‘But now isn’t it, Miss Trotwood, isn’t it, David, invigorating,’ said Mrs.", "\"하지만, 트롯우드 양, 그리고 데이비드, 이거야말로 기운이 솟는 일 아니냐,\""], ["Markleham, mechanically following her with her eyes, ‘to find a man at Doctor Strong’s time of life, with the strength of mind to do this kind of thing?", "마크럼 부인이 무심결에 눈으로 딸을 좇으며 말했다. \"스트롱 박사 정도 연배에, 이런 일을 해낼 만한 굳은 마음을 지닌 사람을 보다니 말이야."], ["It only shows how right I was.", "이건 내 말이 옳았다는 걸 보여 주는 거야."], ["I said to Annie, when Doctor Strong paid a very flattering visit to myself, and made her the subject of a declaration and an offer, I said, “My dear, there is no doubt whatever, in my opinion, with reference to a suitable provision for you, that Doctor Strong will do more than he binds himself to do.”’", "스트롱 박사가 나한테 더없이 영광스러운 방문을 해서 애니를 두고 사랑을 고백하고 청혼을 했을 때, 내가 애니한테 그랬거든. '얘야, 너한테 마땅한 살림 밑천이라는 점에서 보자면, 스트롱 박사는 자기가 약속하는 것보다 더 많은 걸 해 줄 거다, 내 보기엔 그건 추호도 의심할 여지가 없어.'\""]], [["Here the bell rang, and we heard the sound of the visitors’ feet as they went out.", "이때 초인종이 울리고, 손님들이 나가는 발소리가 들렸다."]], [["‘It’s all over, no doubt,’ said the Old Soldier, after listening; ‘the dear creature has signed, sealed, and delivered, and his mind’s at rest.", "\"다 끝난 게 틀림없어.\" 늙은 군인이 귀를 기울이고 나서 말했다. \"그 사랑스러운 양반이 서명하고 봉인하고 넘겨줬으니, 이제 마음이 놓이겠지."], ["Well it may be! What a mind!", "그럴 만도 하지! 참으로 대단한 마음이야!"], ["Annie, my love, I am going to the Study with my paper, for I am a poor creature without news.", "애니야, 얘야, 나는 신문을 들고 서재로 가야겠다. 소식거리가 없으면 영 못 견디는 가엾은 사람이라서 말이야."], ["Miss Trotwood, David, pray come and see the Doctor.’", "트롯우드 양, 데이비드, 어서들 와서 박사 양반을 만나 보게나.\""]], [["I was conscious of Mr. Dick’s standing in the shadow of the room, shutting up his knife, when we accompanied her to the Study; and of my aunt’s rubbing her nose violently, by the way, as a mild vent for her intolerance of our military friend; but who got first into the Study, or how Mrs.", "우리가 부인을 따라 서재로 갈 때, 나는 딕 씨가 방의 그늘진 구석에 서서 칼을 접고 있는 것을 알아챘다. 또 우리 군인 양반을 향한 못마땅함을 점잖게 푸느라, 고모님이 가는 길에 코를 사납게 문질러 대는 것도 알아챘다."], ["Markleham settled herself in a moment in her easy-chair, or how my aunt and I came to be left together near the door (unless her eyes were quicker than mine, and she held me back), I have forgotten, if I ever knew.", "그러나 누가 먼저 서재에 들어갔는지, 마크럼 부인이 어떻게 순식간에 안락의자에 자리를 잡았는지, 또 어쩌다가 고모님과 내가 문가에 단둘이 남게 됐는지(고모님의 눈이 내 눈보다 빨라서 나를 붙들어 세운 것이 아니라면 말이다)는, 설령 한때 알았다 한들 지금은 다 잊어버렸다."], ["But this I know,--that we saw the Doctor before he saw us, sitting at his table, among the folio volumes in which he delighted, resting his head calmly on his hand.", "하지만 이것만은 안다. 우리가 박사 선생님보다 먼저 그분을 보았다는 것을. 그분이 자기가 좋아하던 2절판 책들 사이, 탁자 앞에 앉아, 손에 머리를 차분히 괴고 있던 것을."], ["That, in the same moment, we saw Mrs. Strong glide in, pale and trembling.", "바로 그 순간, 스트롱 부인이 창백하게 떨며 미끄러지듯 들어오는 것을 우리가 보았다는 것을."], ["That Mr. Dick supported her on his arm.", "딕 씨가 자기 팔로 부인을 부축하고 있었다는 것을."], ["That he laid his other hand upon the Doctor’s arm, causing him to look up with an abstracted air.", "그가 다른 한 손을 박사 선생님의 팔에 얹자, 박사 선생님이 멍한 얼굴로 고개를 들었다는 것을."], ["That, as the Doctor moved his head, his wife dropped down on one knee at his feet, and, with her hands imploringly lifted, fixed upon his face the memorable look I had never forgotten.", "박사 선생님이 머리를 움직이자, 그 아내가 그분 발치에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 두 손을 애원하듯 치켜든 채, 내가 결코 잊은 적 없는 그 잊지 못할 눈빛으로 그분의 얼굴을 지그시 응시했다는 것을."], ["That at this sight Mrs. Markleham dropped the newspaper, and stared more like a figure-head intended for a ship to be called The Astonishment, than anything else I can think of.", "이 광경을 보고 마크럼 부인이 신문을 떨어뜨리더니, 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 무엇보다도, 마치 '경악호'라는 이름이 붙을 배를 위해 만든 뱃머리 조각상처럼 멀거니 바라보았다는 것을."]], [["The gentleness of the Doctor’s manner and surprise, the dignity that mingled with the supplicating attitude of his wife, the amiable concern of Mr. Dick, and the earnestness with which my aunt said to herself, ‘That man mad!’ (triumphantly expressive of the misery from which she had saved him)--I see and hear, rather than remember, as I write about it.", "박사 선생님의 그 부드러운 태도와 놀란 기색, 그 아내의 애원하는 자세에 어린 위엄, 딕 씨의 다정한 근심, 그리고 고모님이 \"저 사람 미쳤어!\"라고 혼잣말하던 그 진지함(그가 저 사람을 구해 냈다는 그 비참한 처지를 의기양양하게 드러내던 말)을 — 이 일을 글로 적는 지금, 나는 그것을 기억한다기보다 차라리 보고 듣는 것만 같다."]], [["‘Doctor!’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"박사 선생님!\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What is it that’s amiss? Look here!’", "\"뭐가 잘못된 겁니까? 여기 좀 보세요!\""]], [["‘Annie!’ cried the Doctor.", "\"애니!\" 박사 선생님이 외쳤다."], ["‘Not at my feet, my dear!’", "\"내 발치에 무릎 꿇지 마오, 여보!\""]], [["‘Yes!’ she said.", "\"아니에요!\""], ["‘I beg and pray that no one will leave the room!", "부인이 말했다. \"제발 부탁이니 아무도 이 방을 떠나지 말아 주세요!"], ["Oh, my husband and father, break this long silence.", "오, 내 남편이자 아버지여, 이 기나긴 침묵을 깨뜨려 주세요."], ["Let us both know what it is that has come between us!’", "우리 사이를 갈라놓은 것이 무엇인지, 우리 둘 다 알게 해 주세요!\""]], [["Mrs.", "이때쯤 말할 기운을 되찾고, 가문의 자존심과 어미로서의 분노로 잔뜩 부풀어 오른 듯한 마크럼 부인이 여기서 소리쳤다."], ["Markleham, by this time recovering the power of speech, and seeming to swell with family pride and motherly indignation, here exclaimed, ‘Annie, get up immediately, and don’t disgrace everybody belonging to you by humbling yourself like that, unless you wish to see me go out of my mind on the spot!’", "\"애니, 당장 일어나라. 그렇게 제 몸을 낮춰서 네 식구들 모두를 욕보이지 마. 당장 이 자리에서 내가 정신 줄 놓는 꼴을 보고 싶은 게 아니라면 말이야!\""]], [["‘Mama!’ returned Annie.", "\"엄마!\" 애니가 대답했다."], ["‘Waste no words on me, for my appeal is to my husband, and even you are nothing here.’", "\"저한테 말을 낭비하지 마세요. 제 호소는 제 남편을 향한 거예요. 그러니 여기서는 엄마조차도 아무것도 아니에요.\""]], [["‘Nothing!’ exclaimed Mrs. Markleham.", "\"아무것도 아니라고!\" 마크럼 부인이 외쳤다."], ["‘Me, nothing! The child has taken leave of her senses.", "\"내가, 아무것도 아니라니! 저 애가 제정신이 아니구나."], ["Please to get me a glass of water!’", "누구 나한테 물 한 잔만 가져다주게!\""]], [["I was too attentive to the Doctor and his wife, to give any heed to this request; and it made no impression on anybody else; so Mrs.", "나는 박사 선생님과 그 아내에게 온통 마음을 빼앗겨, 이 요청에 조금도 신경 쓰지 못했다. 그리고 다른 누구에게도 그 말은 아무 인상을 주지 못했다."], ["Markleham panted, stared, and fanned herself.", "그래서 마크럼 부인은 헐떡이고, 멀거니 바라보고, 부채질만 했다."]], [["‘Annie!’ said the Doctor, tenderly taking her in his hands. ‘My dear!", "\"애니!\" 박사 선생님이 다정하게 그녀의 두 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"여보!"], ["If any unavoidable change has come, in the sequence of time, upon our married life, you are not to blame.", "세월이 흐르는 가운데 우리 부부의 삶에 어쩔 수 없는 변화가 찾아온 것이라면, 그건 당신 탓이 아니오."], ["The fault is mine, and only mine.", "잘못은 내게 있소, 오직 내게만."], ["There is no change in my affection, admiration, and respect.", "당신을 향한 내 애정과 흠모와 존경에는 아무런 변함이 없소."], ["I wish to make you happy.", "나는 당신을 행복하게 해 주고 싶소."], ["I truly love and honour you.", "나는 진심으로 당신을 사랑하고 받드오."], ["Rise, Annie, pray!’", "일어나오, 애니, 제발!\""]], [["But she did not rise. After looking at him for a little while, she sank down closer to him, laid her arm across his knee, and dropping her head upon it, said:", "그러나 그녀는 일어나지 않았다. 잠시 그를 바라보던 그녀는, 그에게 더 바짝 몸을 낮추어, 한쪽 팔을 그의 무릎 위에 걸치고, 그 위에 머리를 떨군 채 말했다."]], [["‘If I have any friend here, who can speak one word for me, or for my husband in this matter; if I have any friend here, who can give a voice to any suspicion that my heart has sometimes whispered to me; if I have any friend here, who honours my husband, or has ever cared for me, and has anything within his knowledge, no matter what it is, that may help to mediate between us, I implore that friend to speak!’", "\"이 일에서 저를 위해, 혹은 제 남편을 위해 한마디라도 해 줄 벗이 여기 있다면, 제 마음이 이따금 저에게 속삭이던 어떤 의심에 목소리를 실어 줄 벗이 여기 있다면, 제 남편을 존경하거나 저를 한 번이라도 아껴 준 적이 있고, 우리 사이를 중재하는 데 도움이 될 만한 것을, 그것이 무엇이든, 알고 있는 벗이 여기 있다면, 부디 그 벗에게 간청합니다, 말씀해 주세요!\""]], [["There was a profound silence.", "깊은 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["After a few moments of painful hesitation, I broke the silence.", "괴로운 머뭇거림이 잠시 이어진 끝에, 내가 그 침묵을 깨뜨렸다."]], [["‘Mrs.", "\"스트롱 부인.\""], ["Strong,’ I said, ‘there is something within my knowledge, which I have been earnestly entreated by Doctor Strong to conceal, and have concealed until tonight.", "내가 말했다. \"제가 알고 있는 것이 하나 있습니다. 그것을 감춰 달라고 스트롱 박사 선생님께서 간곡히 부탁하셔서, 오늘 밤까지 감추고 있었습니다."], ["But, I believe the time has come when it would be mistaken faith and delicacy to conceal it any longer, and when your appeal absolves me from his injunction.’", "하지만 이제는, 그것을 더 감추는 것이 잘못된 믿음이자 잘못된 배려가 될 때가 왔다고 저는 믿습니다. 그리고 부인의 호소가 저를 그분의 당부에서 풀어 주는군요.\""]], [["She turned her face towards me for a moment, and I knew that I was right.", "부인이 잠시 내 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸고, 나는 내가 옳았음을 알았다."], ["I could not have resisted its entreaty, if the assurance that it gave me had been less convincing.", "그 얼굴이 내게 준 확신이 설령 덜 미더운 것이었다 해도, 나는 그 간청을 도저히 뿌리치지 못했을 것이다."]], [["‘Our future peace,’ she said, ‘may be in your hands.", "\"우리의 앞날의 평화가,\" 부인이 말했다. \"당신 손에 달려 있을지도 모릅니다."], ["I trust it confidently to your not suppressing anything.", "당신이 무엇 하나 숨기지 않으리라 믿고, 그것을 당신께 온전히 맡깁니다."], ["I know beforehand that nothing you, or anyone, can tell me, will show my husband’s noble heart in any other light than one.", "당신이든 그 누구든 제게 무슨 말을 해도, 제 남편의 고귀한 마음을 오직 한 가지 빛으로밖에는 비추지 못하리라는 걸 저는 미리 알고 있습니다."], ["Howsoever it may seem to you to touch me, disregard that.", "그것이 저를 어떻게 건드리는 것처럼 당신께 보이든, 그건 개의치 마세요."], ["I will speak for myself, before him, and before God afterwards.’", "제 일은 제가 직접, 남편 앞에서, 그리고 그다음엔 하느님 앞에서 말하겠습니다.\""]], [["Thus earnestly besought, I made no reference to the Doctor for his permission, but, without any other compromise of the truth than a little softening of the coarseness of Uriah Heep, related plainly what had passed in that same room that night.", "이렇듯 간절히 청을 받았기에, 나는 박사 선생님에게 허락을 구하지도 않고, 유라이아 힙의 그 추악함을 약간 누그러뜨린 것 말고는 진실을 조금도 굽히지 않은 채, 바로 그 방에서 그날 밤 있었던 일을 있는 그대로 이야기했다."], ["The staring of Mrs. Markleham during the whole narration, and the shrill, sharp interjections with which she occasionally interrupted it, defy description.", "이야기가 이어지는 내내 마크럼 부인이 멀거니 바라보던 모습과, 가끔 그것을 끊고 끼어들던 새되고 날카로운 탄성은 이루 다 말로 할 수가 없다."]], [["When I had finished, Annie remained, for some few moments, silent, with her head bent down, as I have described.", "내가 이야기를 마쳤을 때, 애니는 앞서 말한 대로 고개를 숙인 채로 잠시 말없이 가만있었다."], ["Then, she took the Doctor’s hand (he was sitting in the same attitude as when we had entered the room), and pressed it to her breast, and kissed it. Mr.", "이윽고 그녀는 박사 선생님의 손을(그분은 우리가 방에 들어왔을 때와 똑같은 자세로 앉아 있었다) 잡아, 자기 가슴에 꼭 눌러 대고 입을 맞추었다."], ["Dick softly raised her; and she stood, when she began to speak, leaning on him, and looking down upon her husband--from whom she never turned her eyes.", "딕 씨가 그녀를 살그머니 일으켜 세웠다. 그녀는 말을 시작할 때, 그에게 기대어 선 채, 남편을 내려다보았다 — 한 번도 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채로."]], [["‘All that has ever been in my mind, since I was married,’ she said in a low, submissive, tender voice, ‘I will lay bare before you.", "\"결혼한 뒤로 제 마음속에 있던 모든 것을,\" 그녀가 낮고, 순종적이고, 다정한 목소리로 말했다. \"당신 앞에 낱낱이 풀어놓겠어요."], ["I could not live and have one reservation, knowing what I know now.’", "지금 제가 아는 것을 안 이상, 단 하나라도 숨긴 채로는 도저히 살아갈 수가 없어요.\""]], [["‘Nay, Annie,’ said the Doctor, mildly, ‘I have never doubted you, my child.", "\"아니오, 애니.\" 박사 선생님이 부드럽게 말했다. \"나는 당신을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없소, 내 아이여."], ["There is no need; indeed there is no need, my dear.’", "그럴 필요 없소. 정말로 그럴 필요 없소, 여보.\""]], [["‘There is great need,’ she answered, in the same way, ‘that I should open my whole heart before the soul of generosity and truth, whom, year by year, and day by day, I have loved and venerated more and more, as Heaven knows!’", "\"꼭 그래야 해요.\" 그녀가 같은 투로 대답했다. \"해마다, 날마다 점점 더 사랑하고 우러러 온, 너그러움과 진실의 화신이신 그분 앞에 제 마음을 통째로 열어 보여야 해요. 하늘이 아실 거예요!\""]], [["‘Really,’ interrupted Mrs.", "\"정말이지,\" 마크럼 부인이 끼어들었다."], ["Markleham, ‘if I have any discretion at all--’", "\"나한테 분별이라는 게 조금이라도 있다면 —\""]], [["(‘Which you haven’t, you Marplot,’ observed my aunt, in an indignant whisper.) --‘I must be permitted to observe that it cannot be requisite to enter into these details.’", "(\"자네한텐 그런 게 없어, 이 훼방꾼아.\" 고모님이 분개한 목소리로 속삭이듯 말했다.) \" — 이런 시시콜콜한 얘기까지 다 늘어놓을 필요는 없다고 한마디 해야겠다.\""]], [["‘No one but my husband can judge of that, mama,’ said Annie without removing her eyes from his face, ‘and he will hear me.", "\"그건 제 남편 말고는 아무도 판단할 수 없어요, 엄마.\" 애니가 남편의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. \"그리고 남편은 제 말을 들어 줄 거예요."], ["If I say anything to give you pain, mama, forgive me.", "제가 무슨 말을 해서 엄마 마음을 아프게 한다면, 용서하세요."], ["I have borne pain first, often and long, myself.’", "저는 그 아픔을, 누구보다 먼저, 자주, 그리고 오래 견뎌 왔으니까요.\""]], [["‘Upon my word!’ gasped Mrs. Markleham.", "\"세상에, 별꼴이야!\" 마크럼 부인이 숨을 헐떡였다."]], [["‘When I was very young,’ said Annie, ‘quite a little child, my first associations with knowledge of any kind were inseparable from a patient friend and teacher--the friend of my dead father--who was always dear to me.", "\"제가 아주 어렸을 때,\" 애니가 말했다. \"그야말로 어린아이였을 때, 어떤 종류든 앎이라는 것과 처음 맺은 인연은, 한 분의 인내심 깊은 벗이자 스승과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것이었어요 — 돌아가신 제 아버지의 벗이자, 제겐 늘 소중한 분이셨죠."], ["I can remember nothing that I know, without remembering him.", "제가 아는 것 가운데, 그분을 떠올리지 않고 떠올릴 수 있는 건 하나도 없어요."], ["He stored my mind with its first treasures, and stamped his character upon them all.", "그분은 제 마음에 처음으로 보물을 채워 주셨고, 그 모든 것에 자신의 인품을 새겨 넣으셨어요."], ["They never could have been, I think, as good as they have been to me, if I had taken them from any other hands.’", "만약 제가 그것을 다른 누구의 손에서 받았더라면, 지금처럼 제게 좋은 것이 되지는 못했을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Makes her mother nothing!’ exclaimed Mrs. Markleham.", "\"제 어미는 아무것도 아니라는 거구나!\" 마크럼 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Not so mama,’ said Annie; ‘but I make him what he was.", "\"그게 아니에요, 엄마.\" 애니가 말했다. \"저는 그분이 어떤 분이었는지를 있는 그대로 말씀드리는 거예요."], ["I must do that.", "꼭 그래야만 해요."], ["As I grew up, he occupied the same place still.", "제가 자라는 동안에도 그분은 여전히 같은 자리에 계셨어요."], ["I was proud of his interest: deeply, fondly, gratefully attached to him.", "저는 그분의 관심이 자랑스러웠어요. 깊이, 살뜰하게, 고마운 마음으로 그분께 매여 있었죠."], ["I looked up to him, I can hardly describe how--as a father, as a guide, as one whose praise was different from all other praise, as one in whom I could have trusted and confided, if I had doubted all the world.", "저는 그분을 우러러보았어요, 뭐라 표현하기도 어렵지만 — 아버지처럼, 길잡이처럼, 그분의 칭찬은 다른 어떤 칭찬과도 다른, 온 세상을 다 의심하게 되더라도 그분만은 믿고 마음을 털어놓을 수 있는 그런 분으로요."], ["You know, mama, how young and inexperienced I was, when you presented him before me, of a sudden, as a lover.’", "엄마도 아시죠, 엄마가 갑자기 그분을 제 앞에 연인으로 내세웠을 때, 제가 얼마나 어리고 세상 물정 모르는 아이였는지를요.\""]], [["‘I have mentioned the fact, fifty times at least, to everybody here!’ said Mrs. Markleham.", "\"내 그 얘기는 여기 있는 누구한테든 적어도 쉰 번은 했다!\" 마크럼 부인이 말했다."]], [["(‘Then hold your tongue, for the Lord’s sake, and don’t mention it any more!’ muttered my aunt.)", "(\"그럼 제발 좀 입 다물고, 더는 그 얘기 꺼내지 말게나!\" 고모님이 중얼거렸다.)"]], [["‘It was so great a change: so great a loss, I felt it, at first,’ said Annie, still preserving the same look and tone, ‘that I was agitated and distressed.", "\"그건 너무도 큰 변화였고, 너무도 큰 상실이라, 처음엔 그렇게 느껴졌어요.\" 애니가 여전히 같은 눈빛과 같은 목소리를 지키며 말했다. \"그래서 마음이 흔들리고 괴로웠죠."], ["I was but a girl; and when so great a change came in the character in which I had so long looked up to him, I think I was sorry.", "저는 그저 어린 소녀였으니까요. 그토록 오랫동안 우러러보던 그분의 자리가 그렇게 크게 바뀌니, 제 생각엔 서글펐던 것 같아요. 하지만 그 무엇도 그분을 예전의 그분으로 되돌려 놓을 순 없었어요."], ["But nothing could have made him what he used to be again; and I was proud that he should think me so worthy, and we were married.’ ‘--At Saint Alphage, Canterbury,’ observed Mrs.", "그리고 그분이 저를 그토록 합당한 사람으로 여겨 주신다는 게 자랑스러웠죠. 그래서 우리는 결혼했어요.\" \" — 캔터베리의 세인트 앨피지 성당에서 말이야.\""], ["Markleham.", "마크럼 부인이 한마디 거들었다."]], [["(‘Confound the woman!’ said my aunt, ‘she WON’T be quiet!’)", "(\"저 여편네 진짜!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"도무지 조용히 있질 못하는군!\")"]], [["‘I never thought,’ proceeded Annie, with a heightened colour, ‘of any worldly gain that my husband would bring to me.", "\"저는 한 번도,\" 애니가 얼굴빛을 붉히며 말을 이었다. \"제 남편이 제게 가져다줄 세속적인 이득 따위를 생각해 본 적이 없어요."], ["My young heart had no room in its homage for any such poor reference.", "제 어린 마음은 그분을 향한 흠모로 가득 차서, 그런 천박한 셈속이 끼어들 틈이 없었어요."], ["Mama, forgive me when I say that it was you who first presented to my mind the thought that anyone could wrong me, and wrong him, by such a cruel suspicion.’", "엄마, 이 말을 하는 저를 용서하세요. 그렇게 잔인한 의심으로 누군가가 저를, 그리고 그분을 욕보일 수 있다는 생각을 제 머릿속에 처음 심어 준 건 바로 엄마였어요.\""]], [["‘Me!’ cried Mrs. Markleham.", "\"내가!\" 마크럼 부인이 소리쳤다."]], [["(‘Ah!", "(\"아, 그래!"], ["You, to be sure!’ observed my aunt, ‘and you can’t fan it away, my military friend!’)", "바로 자네지!\" 고모님이 한마디 했다. \"그건 부채질로도 못 날려 보내, 우리 군인 양반!\")"]], [["‘It was the first unhappiness of my new life,’ said Annie.", "\"그게 제 새 삶의 첫 번째 불행이었어요.\" 애니가 말했다."], ["‘It was the first occasion of every unhappy moment I have known.", "\"제가 겪은 모든 불행한 순간의 첫 빌미였죠. 그런 순간들이 요즘 들어 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 많아졌어요."], ["These moments have been more, of late, than I can count; but not--my generous husband!--not for the reason you suppose; for in my heart there is not a thought, a recollection, or a hope, that any power could separate from you!’", "하지만 — 너그러우신 제 남편이여! — 당신이 짐작하시는 그런 이유 때문은 아니에요. 제 마음속에는, 그 어떤 힘으로도 당신과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 생각도, 추억도, 희망도 단 하나뿐이니까요!\""]], [["She raised her eyes, and clasped her hands, and looked as beautiful and true, I thought, as any Spirit.", "그녀는 눈을 들어 두 손을 맞잡았는데, 내가 보기엔 그 어떤 천사 못지않게 아름답고 진실해 보였다."], ["The Doctor looked on her, henceforth, as steadfastly as she on him.", "박사 선생님은 이제부터 줄곧, 그녀가 자기를 바라보던 그 한결같음으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Mama is blameless,’ she went on, ‘of having ever urged you for herself, and she is blameless in intention every way, I am sure,--but when I saw how many importunate claims were pressed upon you in my name; how you were traded on in my name; how generous you were, and how Mr.", "\"엄마에게는 아무 잘못이 없어요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"엄마가 자신을 위해 당신을 졸랐던 적은 한 번도 없으니까요."], ["Wickfield, who had your welfare very much at heart, resented it; the first sense of my exposure to the mean suspicion that my tenderness was bought--and sold to you, of all men on earth--fell upon me like unmerited disgrace, in which I forced you to participate.", "그리고 그 의도에 있어서는 어느 모로 보나 엄마에게 아무 잘못이 없다고 저는 확신해요 — 하지만 제 이름으로 그토록 많은 끈질긴 요구가 당신께 밀려드는 것을, 제 이름이 그렇게 흥정거리가 되는 것을, 당신이 얼마나 너그러우신지를, 그리고 당신의 안녕을 무척이나 마음 쓰던 위크필드 씨가 그것을 얼마나 못마땅해하던지를 보았을 때 — 제 다정함이 돈에 팔려, 그것도 세상 모든 사람 가운데 하필 당신께 팔려 넘어갔다는 그 비열한 의심에 제가 내던져졌다는 첫 깨달음이, 마치 까닭 없는 치욕처럼 제게 덮쳐 왔어요."], ["I cannot tell you what it was--mama cannot imagine what it was--to have this dread and trouble always on my mind, yet know in my own soul that on my marriage-day I crowned the love and honour of my life!’", "그리고 저는 당신마저 그 치욕에 끌어들이고 만 거예요. 이 두려움과 괴로움을 늘 마음에 짊어진 채, 그러면서도 제 영혼 깊이로는, 결혼하던 그날 제가 제 삶의 사랑과 명예에 면류관을 씌웠다는 걸 알고 있던 것이 — 어떤 심정이었는지 당신께 말로 다 할 수가 없어요. 엄마는 짐작조차 못 할 거예요.\""]], [["‘A specimen of the thanks one gets,’ cried Mrs.", "\"가족을 보살펴 준 대가로 받는 고마움이 고작 이런 거구나!\""], ["Markleham, in tears, ‘for taking care of one’s family! I wish I was a Turk!’", "마크럼 부인이 눈물을 흘리며 외쳤다. \"차라리 내가 터키 사람이었으면 좋겠다!\""]], [["(‘I wish you were, with all my heart--and in your native country!’ said my aunt.)", "(\"나야말로 진심으로 그러길 바라네 — 그것도 자네 그 나라에 가서 말이야!\" 고모님이 말했다.)"]], [["‘It was at that time that mama was most solicitous about my Cousin Maldon.", "\"바로 그 무렵 엄마는 제 사촌 몰던 일로 가장 안달복달하셨어요. 저는 그를 좋아했었죠.\""], ["I had liked him’: she spoke softly, but without any hesitation: ‘very much.", "그녀는 부드럽게, 그러나 조금의 머뭇거림도 없이 말했다. \"아주 많이요."], ["We had been little lovers once.", "우리는 한때 어린 연인 사이였어요."], ["If circumstances had not happened otherwise, I might have come to persuade myself that I really loved him, and might have married him, and been most wretched.", "사정이 다르게 흘러가지 않았더라면, 저는 그를 정말로 사랑한다고 스스로를 설득하고 말았을지도 몰라요. 그래서 그와 결혼해 더없이 비참하게 살았을지도 모르죠."], ["There can be no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’", "결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없으니까요.\""]], [["I pondered on those words, even while I was studiously attending to what followed, as if they had some particular interest, or some strange application that I could not divine.", "나는 그 뒤에 이어지는 말에 골똘히 귀 기울이면서도, 마치 그 말들에 무언가 각별한 뜻이, 아니면 내가 헤아릴 수 없는 어떤 기묘한 적용이 담겨 있기라도 한 듯, 그 말을 곱씹었다."], ["‘There can be no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose’--‘no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’", "'결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없다' — '결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없다.'"]], [["‘There is nothing,’ said Annie, ‘that we have in common.", "\"우리 사이엔,\" 애니가 말했다. \"함께 나눌 만한 게 아무것도 없어요."], ["I have long found that there is nothing.", "아무것도 없다는 걸 안 지 오래됐죠."], ["If I were thankful to my husband for no more, instead of for so much, I should be thankful to him for having saved me from the first mistaken impulse of my undisciplined heart.’", "설령 제가 그토록 많은 게 아니라 그저 이 한 가지만으로 제 남편께 감사한다 해도, 저는 그분께 감사할 거예요 — 다스려지지 않은 제 마음의 그 첫 잘못된 충동에서 저를 구해 주신 것을 말이에요.\""]], [["She stood quite still, before the Doctor, and spoke with an earnestness that thrilled me.", "그녀는 박사 선생님 앞에 꼼짝도 않고 서서, 나를 전율케 하는 간절함으로 말했다."], ["Yet her voice was just as quiet as before.", "그러면서도 그 목소리는 아까와 똑같이 잔잔했다."]], [["‘When he was waiting to be the object of your munificence, so freely bestowed for my sake, and when I was unhappy in the mercenary shape I was made to wear, I thought it would have become him better to have worked his own way on.", "\"그가, 저를 위해 그토록 아낌없이 베풀어 주신 당신의 후한 베풂의 대상이 되기를 기다리고 있었을 때, 그리고 제가 억지로 뒤집어쓴 그 돈에 팔린 모습 때문에 괴로워하고 있었을 때, 저는 그가 차라리 제 힘으로 살길을 헤쳐 나갔더라면 더 떳떳했으리라고 생각했어요."], ["I thought that if I had been he, I would have tried to do it, at the cost of almost any hardship.", "제가 그였다면, 거의 어떤 고생을 무릅쓰고서라도 그렇게 해 보려 했을 거라고요."], ["But I thought no worse of him, until the night of his departure for India.", "하지만 그가 인도로 떠나던 그날 밤 전까지는, 저는 그를 그보다 더 나쁘게 여기지는 않았어요."], ["That night I knew he had a false and thankless heart.", "그날 밤, 저는 그가 거짓되고 배은망덕한 마음을 지녔다는 걸 알았어요."], ["I saw a double meaning, then, in Mr. Wickfield’s scrutiny of me.", "그때 저는 위크필드 씨가 저를 살피던 그 눈길에 숨은 이중의 뜻을 보았어요."], ["I perceived, for the first time, the dark suspicion that shadowed my life.’", "그리고 처음으로, 제 삶에 그늘을 드리운 그 어두운 의심을 알아차렸죠.\""]], [["‘Suspicion, Annie!’ said the Doctor.", "\"의심이라니, 애니!\" 박사 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘No, no, no!’", "\"아니, 아니, 아니오!\""]], [["‘In your mind there was none, I know, my husband!’ she returned.", "\"당신 마음속엔 그런 게 없었다는 거, 저도 알아요, 여보!\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘And when I came to you, that night, to lay down all my load of shame and grief, and knew that I had to tell that, underneath your roof, one of my own kindred, to whom you had been a benefactor, for the love of me, had spoken to me words that should have found no utterance, even if I had been the weak and mercenary wretch he thought me--my mind revolted from the taint the very tale conveyed.", "\"그날 밤 제가 당신께 가서 제 모든 부끄러움과 슬픔의 짐을 내려놓으려 했을 때, 그리고 당신의 지붕 아래서, 당신이 저를 사랑하는 마음에 은혜를 베풀어 주신 제 친척 가운데 하나가, 설령 제가 그가 여기던 그 나약하고 돈에 눈먼 계집이었다 한들 결코 입에 담아선 안 될 말을 제게 했다는 걸 털어놓아야 한다는 걸 알았을 때 — 그 이야기를 입에 올리는 것만으로도 묻어나는 그 더러움에 제 마음이 진저리를 쳤어요."], ["It died upon my lips, and from that hour till now has never passed them.’", "그 말은 제 입술에서 죽어 버렸고, 그 순간부터 지금까지 단 한 번도 그 입술을 넘은 적이 없어요.\""]], [["Mrs. Markleham, with a short groan, leaned back in her easy-chair; and retired behind her fan, as if she were never coming out any more.", "마크럼 부인이 짤막한 신음을 내뱉으며 안락의자에 등을 기대고는, 다시는 나오지 않을 사람처럼 부채 뒤로 몸을 숨겼다."]], [["‘I have never, but in your presence, interchanged a word with him from that time; then, only when it has been necessary for the avoidance of this explanation.", "\"그때부터 저는, 당신이 있는 자리가 아니고서는, 그와 단 한마디도 주고받은 적이 없어요. 그것도 오직 이런 해명을 피하기 위해 어쩔 수 없을 때뿐이었죠."], ["Years have passed since he knew, from me, what his situation here was.", "그가 이곳에서 자기 처지가 어떤 것인지를 제 입을 통해 알게 된 지도 여러 해가 흘렀어요."], ["The kindnesses you have secretly done for his advancement, and then disclosed to me, for my surprise and pleasure, have been, you will believe, but aggravations of the unhappiness and burden of my secret.’", "당신이 그의 출세를 위해 남몰래 베풀어 주신 친절들, 그러다 저를 놀라게 하고 기쁘게 해 주려고 제게 털어놓으신 그 친절들이, 짐작하시겠지만, 제 비밀의 불행과 무게를 더 무겁게 했을 뿐이었어요.\""]], [["She sunk down gently at the Doctor’s feet, though he did his utmost to prevent her; and said, looking up, tearfully, into his face:", "박사 선생님이 한사코 막으려 했지만, 그녀는 그분 발치에 살며시 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 눈물 어린 눈으로 그분의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Do not speak to me yet!", "\"아직 제게 아무 말씀 마세요!"], ["Let me say a little more!", "조금만 더 말하게 해 주세요!"], ["Right or wrong, if this were to be done again, I think I should do just the same.", "옳든 그르든, 만약 이 일을 다시 하게 된다 해도, 저는 똑같이 했을 거라고 생각해요."], ["You never can know what it was to be devoted to you, with those old associations; to find that anyone could be so hard as to suppose that the truth of my heart was bartered away, and to be surrounded by appearances confirming that belief.", "그 오랜 정과 함께 당신께 헌신한다는 게 어떤 것인지, 누군가가 그토록 모질게도 제 마음의 진실이 흥정거리로 팔려 넘어갔다고 짐작할 수 있다는 걸 알게 되는 게, 그리고 그 믿음을 굳혀 주는 겉모습들에 둘러싸이는 게 어떤 것인지, 당신은 결코 모르실 거예요."], ["I was very young, and had no adviser.", "저는 너무 어렸고, 의논할 사람도 없었어요."], ["Between mama and me, in all relating to you, there was a wide division.", "당신에 관한 모든 일에서, 엄마와 저 사이엔 넓은 골이 있었죠."], ["If I shrunk into myself, hiding the disrespect I had undergone, it was because I honoured you so much, and so much wished that you should honour me!’", "제가 겪은 그 멸시를 감추며 제 안으로 움츠러들었다면, 그건 제가 당신을 그토록 존경했고, 당신도 저를 존경해 주시기를 그토록 바랐기 때문이에요!\""]], [["‘Annie, my pure heart!’ said the Doctor, ‘my dear girl!’", "\"애니, 내 순결한 마음이여!\" 박사 선생님이 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 아이여!\""]], [["‘A little more!", "\"조금만 더요!"], ["a very few words more!", "정말 몇 마디만 더요!"], ["I used to think there were so many whom you might have married, who would not have brought such charge and trouble on you, and who would have made your home a worthier home.", "저는 늘 생각했어요, 당신이 결혼할 수 있었던 수많은 여자가 있었을 텐데, 그 여자들이라면 당신께 이런 짐과 골칫거리를 안기지 않고, 당신의 집을 더 떳떳한 보금자리로 만들어 주었을 텐데, 하고요."], ["I used to be afraid that I had better have remained your pupil, and almost your child.", "저는 차라리 당신의 제자로, 아니 거의 당신의 아이로 남아 있는 편이 나았으리라고 두려워하곤 했어요."], ["I used to fear that I was so unsuited to your learning and wisdom.", "제가 당신의 학식과 지혜에 너무도 어울리지 않는 사람이라고 늘 겁이 났죠."], ["If all this made me shrink within myself (as indeed it did), when I had that to tell, it was still because I honoured you so much, and hoped that you might one day honour me.’", "이 모든 것이 저를 제 안으로 움츠러들게 했다면(정말 그랬어요), 그 털어놓아야 할 것을 품고 있었을 때조차, 그건 여전히 제가 당신을 그토록 존경했고, 언젠가 당신도 저를 존경해 주시기를 바랐기 때문이에요.\""]], [["‘That day has shone this long time, Annie,’ said the Doctor, ‘and can have but one long night, my dear.’", "\"그날은 이미 오래전부터 환히 빛나고 있었소, 애니.\" 박사 선생님이 말했다. \"그리고 그 뒤에 올 긴 밤은 오직 하나뿐일 거요, 여보.\""]], [["‘Another word!", "\"한마디만 더요!"], ["I afterwards meant--steadfastly meant, and purposed to myself--to bear the whole weight of knowing the unworthiness of one to whom you had been so good.", "그 뒤로 저는 마음먹었어요 — 굳게 마음먹고, 스스로에게 다짐했어요 — 당신이 그토록 잘해 주신 사람의 그 못난 됨됨이를 안다는 그 무게를, 통째로 혼자 짊어지겠다고요."], ["And now a last word, dearest and best of friends!", "그리고 이제 마지막 한마디예요, 더없이 소중하고 가장 좋은 벗이여!"], ["The cause of the late change in you, which I have seen with so much pain and sorrow, and have sometimes referred to my old apprehension--at other times to lingering suppositions nearer to the truth--has been made clear tonight; and by an accident I have also come to know, tonight, the full measure of your noble trust in me, even under that mistake.", "제가 그토록 큰 아픔과 슬픔으로 지켜보았고, 때로는 제 오랜 불안 탓으로, 또 때로는 진실에 더 가까운, 마음속에 맴도는 어떤 짐작 탓으로 돌리곤 했던, 요즘 당신께 일어난 그 변화의 까닭이 오늘 밤 밝혀졌어요. 그리고 어쩌다 보니 저는 오늘 밤, 그 오해 속에서조차 당신이 저를 얼마나 고귀하게 믿어 주셨는지 그 깊이를 온전히 알게 됐어요."], ["I do not hope that any love and duty I may render in return, will ever make me worthy of your priceless confidence; but with all this knowledge fresh upon me, I can lift my eyes to this dear face, revered as a father’s, loved as a husband’s, sacred to me in my childhood as a friend’s, and solemnly declare that in my lightest thought I have never wronged you; never wavered in the love and the fidelity I owe you!’", "제가 그 보답으로 바칠 어떤 사랑과 정성도, 저를 당신의 그 값을 매길 수 없는 믿음에 합당한 사람으로 만들어 주리라고는 바라지 않아요. 하지만 이 모든 깨달음이 제 안에 생생한 지금, 저는 아버지처럼 우러르고, 남편처럼 사랑하고, 어린 시절엔 벗으로서 거룩하게 여겼던 이 소중한 얼굴을 향해 눈을 들어, 엄숙히 선언할 수 있어요. 저는 그 가장 가벼운 생각 속에서조차 당신을 한 번도 저버린 적이 없으며, 당신께 마땅히 바쳐야 할 사랑과 정절에서 단 한 번도 흔들린 적이 없다고요!\""]], [["She had her arms around the Doctor’s neck, and he leant his head down over her, mingling his grey hair with her dark brown tresses.", "그녀는 두 팔로 박사 선생님의 목을 감싸 안았고, 그분은 그 위로 고개를 숙여, 자신의 백발을 그녀의 짙은 갈색 머리채에 뒤섞었다."]], [["‘Oh, hold me to your heart, my husband!", "\"오, 저를 당신 가슴에 꼭 안아 주세요, 여보!"], ["Never cast me out!", "저를 결코 내치지 마세요!"], ["Do not think or speak of disparity between us, for there is none, except in all my many imperfections.", "우리 사이에 어긋남이 있다는 생각도, 말도 하지 마세요. 그런 건 없으니까요. 제 그 많은 부족함 말고는요."], ["Every succeeding year I have known this better, as I have esteemed you more and more.", "한 해 한 해 지날수록 저는 이 사실을 더 잘 알게 됐어요, 당신을 점점 더 우러러보게 되면서요."], ["Oh, take me to your heart, my husband, for my love was founded on a rock, and it endures!’", "오, 저를 당신 가슴에 안아 주세요, 여보. 제 사랑은 반석 위에 세워졌고, 그래서 영원하니까요!\""]], [["In the silence that ensued, my aunt walked gravely up to Mr. Dick, without at all hurrying herself, and gave him a hug and a sounding kiss.", "뒤이은 침묵 속에, 고모님은 조금도 서두르는 기색 없이 진중하게 딕 씨에게 걸어가더니, 그를 와락 끌어안고 쪽 소리 나게 입을 맞추었다."], ["And it was very fortunate, with a view to his credit, that she did so; for I am confident that I detected him at that moment in the act of making preparations to stand on one leg, as an appropriate expression of delight.", "그렇게 한 것은 딕 씨의 체면을 봐서 참으로 다행한 일이었으니, 바로 그 순간 그가 기쁨을 적절히 드러내는 표현으로 한쪽 다리로 서려고 채비하는 것을 내가 분명히 보았다고 확신하기 때문이다."]], [["‘You are a very remarkable man, Dick!’ said my aunt, with an air of unqualified approbation; ‘and never pretend to be anything else, for I know better!’", "\"자네는 정말 비범한 사람일세, 딕!\" 고모님이 두말없는 칭찬의 기색으로 말했다. \"그러니 절대 다른 사람인 척하지 말게, 내가 더 잘 아니까!\""]], [["With that, my aunt pulled him by the sleeve, and nodded to me; and we three stole quietly out of the room, and came away.", "그 말과 함께, 고모님은 딕 씨의 소매를 잡아끌며 나에게 고갯짓을 했다. 그리하여 우리 셋은 조용히 방에서 살그머니 빠져나와 그 자리를 떠났다."]], [["‘That’s a settler for our military friend, at any rate,’ said my aunt, on the way home.", "\"어쨌거나 우리 군인 양반한테는 이걸로 끝장이 난 셈이지.\" 고모님이 집으로 돌아오는 길에 말했다."], ["‘I should sleep the better for that, if there was nothing else to be glad of!’", "\"다른 기뻐할 일이 아무것도 없다 해도, 이것만으로도 오늘 밤은 발 뻗고 잘 수 있겠어!\""]], [["‘She was quite overcome, I am afraid,’ said Mr.", "\"그분이 무척이나 북받쳐 하시던데요.\""], ["Dick, with great commiseration.", "딕 씨가 깊이 가엾어하며 말했다."]], [["‘What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Did you ever see a crocodile overcome?’ inquired my aunt.", "자네 악어가 북받쳐 하는 거 본 적 있나?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["‘I don’t think I ever saw a crocodile,’ returned Mr. Dick, mildly.", "\"제가 악어를 본 적이 있는지 모르겠는데요.\" 딕 씨가 점잖게 대답했다."]], [["‘There never would have been anything the matter, if it hadn’t been for that old Animal,’ said my aunt, with strong emphasis.", "\"그 늙은 짐승만 아니었으면 애당초 아무 탈도 없었을 거야.\" 고모님이 힘주어 말했다."], ["‘It’s very much to be wished that some mothers would leave their daughters alone after marriage, and not be so violently affectionate.", "\"어떤 어미들은 딸이 결혼하고 나면 좀 내버려 두고, 그렇게 극성맞게 끼고돌지 않았으면 정말 좋겠어."], ["They seem to think the only return that can be made them for bringing an unfortunate young woman into the world--God bless my soul, as if she asked to be brought, or wanted to come!--is full liberty to worry her out of it again.", "그런 어미들은 가엾은 젊은 여자 하나를 세상에 내보내 준 대가로 받을 수 있는 유일한 보답이 — 아이고 맙소사, 그 애가 세상에 내보내 달라고 졸랐거나, 나오고 싶어 안달이라도 했던 것처럼 말이지! — 그 애를 다시 들들 볶아서 세상 밖으로 몰아낼 완전한 권리라고 여기는 모양이야."], ["What are you thinking of, Trot?’", "무슨 생각을 그렇게 하나, 트롯?\""]], [["I was thinking of all that had been said.", "나는 오갔던 그 모든 말을 생각하고 있었다."], ["My mind was still running on some of the expressions used.", "내 머릿속에는 거기서 쓰인 몇몇 표현이 여전히 맴돌고 있었다. '결혼에서 마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 큰 어긋남은 없다.' '다스려지지 않은 마음의 그 첫 잘못된 충동.'"], ["‘There can be no disparity in marriage like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’ ‘The first mistaken impulse of an undisciplined heart.’ ‘My love was founded on a rock.’ But we were at home; and the trodden leaves were lying under-foot, and the autumn wind was blowing.", "'제 사랑은 반석 위에 세워졌어요.' 그러나 우리는 어느새 집에 다다랐다. 밟힌 낙엽이 발밑에 깔려 있었고, 가을바람이 불고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 46. INTELLIGENCE", "# CHAPTER 46. INTELLIGENCE"]], [["I must have been married, if I may trust to my imperfect memory for dates, about a year or so, when one evening, as I was returning from a solitary walk, thinking of the book I was then writing--for my success had steadily increased with my steady application, and I was engaged at that time upon my first work of fiction--I came past Mrs.", "결혼한 지 한 일 년쯤 지났을 무렵이었던 것 같다. 날짜에 관한 한 내 기억은 영 미덥지 못하니 그쯤으로 믿어 둘 수밖에 없다. 어느 저녁, 나는 혼자 산책을 마치고 돌아오던 길에, 그 무렵 쓰고 있던 책을 골똘히 생각하고 있었다."], ["Steerforth’s house. I had often passed it before, during my residence in that neighbourhood, though never when I could choose another road.", "꾸준히 매달린 만큼 내 성공도 차곡차곡 쌓여 갔고, 나는 그때 첫 소설을 붙들고 있었다. 그러다 스티어포스 부인의 집 앞을 지나게 되었다."], ["Howbeit, it did sometimes happen that it was not easy to find another, without making a long circuit; and so I had passed that way, upon the whole, pretty often.", "그 동네에 사는 동안 나는 그 집 앞을 자주 지나다녔는데, 다른 길을 고를 수 있을 때는 한 번도 그쪽으로 가지 않았다. 그러나 멀리 빙 돌지 않고서는 다른 길을 찾기가 쉽지 않을 때도 더러 있어서, 결국 따지고 보면 그 길을 꽤 자주 지난 셈이었다."]], [["I had never done more than glance at the house, as I went by with a quickened step.", "나는 걸음을 빨리하며 그 집을 흘끗 보는 것 말고는 한 번도 더 눈여겨본 적이 없었다."], ["It had been uniformly gloomy and dull.", "그 집은 한결같이 음침하고 칙칙했다."], ["None of the best rooms abutted on the road; and the narrow, heavily-framed old-fashioned windows, never cheerful under any circumstances, looked very dismal, close shut, and with their blinds always drawn down.", "가장 좋은 방들은 어느 것도 길에 면해 있지 않았고, 좁고 묵직한 틀을 두른 구식 창문들은 어떤 상황에서도 명랑해 보인 적이 없었으며, 늘 꽉 닫힌 채 차양을 내리고 있어 몹시 을씨년스러웠다."], ["There was a covered way across a little paved court, to an entrance that was never used; and there was one round staircase window, at odds with all the rest, and the only one unshaded by a blind, which had the same unoccupied blank look.", "작은 포석 깔린 안뜰을 가로질러 한 번도 쓰인 적 없는 출입구로 이어지는 지붕 덮인 통로가 있었다. 그리고 다른 것들과는 어긋나게, 차양에 가려지지 않은 유일한 둥근 계단 창이 하나 있었는데, 그것 역시 똑같이 비어 있는 듯한 멍한 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["I do not remember that I ever saw a light in all the house.", "그 집 전체에서 불빛을 본 기억이 나는 없다."], ["If I had been a casual passer-by, I should have probably supposed that some childless person lay dead in it.", "만약 내가 우연히 지나가는 행인이었다면, 그 안에 자식 없는 누군가가 죽어 누워 있다고 짐작했을 것이다."], ["If I had happily possessed no knowledge of the place, and had seen it often in that changeless state, I should have pleased my fancy with many ingenious speculations, I dare say.", "다행히 그 집을 전혀 모르는 채로 변함없는 그 모습을 자주 보았다면, 나는 분명 온갖 기발한 상상을 떠올리며 공상을 즐겼을 것이다."]], [["As it was, I thought as little of it as I might. But my mind could not go by it and leave it, as my body did; and it usually awakened a long train of meditations.", "그러나 사정이 사정인 만큼, 나는 되도록 그 집을 떠올리지 않으려 했다. 하지만 내 몸은 그 집을 지나쳐 버릴 수 있어도, 내 마음은 그러지 못했다."], ["Coming before me, on this particular evening that I mention, mingled with the childish recollections and later fancies, the ghosts of half-formed hopes, the broken shadows of disappointments dimly seen and understood, the blending of experience and imagination, incidental to the occupation with which my thoughts had been busy, it was more than commonly suggestive.", "그 집은 으레 길고 긴 상념의 행렬을 깨워 놓았다. 내가 말하는 바로 그날 저녁에는, 어린 시절의 기억과 훗날의 공상, 채 영글지도 못한 희망의 유령들, 어렴풋이 보이고 어렴풋이 헤아려지는 실망의 부서진 그림자들, 그리고 그 무렵 내 생각을 분주하게 만들던 일에 따라붙기 마련인 경험과 상상의 뒤섞임이 한데 어우러져 떠올랐고, 그래서 그것은 여느 때보다 더 많은 것을 일깨웠다."], ["I fell into a brown study as I walked on, and a voice at my side made me start.", "나는 걷는 내내 깊은 명상에 빠져들었고, 옆에서 들려온 목소리에 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["It was a woman’s voice, too.", "게다가 그것은 여자 목소리였다."], ["I was not long in recollecting Mrs. Steerforth’s little parlour-maid, who had formerly worn blue ribbons in her cap.", "나는 곧 그것이 스티어포스 부인의 어린 응접실 하녀임을 떠올렸다. 예전에는 모자에 파란 리본을 달고 다니던 아이였다."], ["She had taken them out now, to adapt herself, I suppose, to the altered character of the house; and wore but one or two disconsolate bows of sober brown.", "이제는 그 리본을 떼어 냈는데, 짐작건대 달라진 집안 분위기에 맞추려는 것이었으리라. 지금은 칙칙한 갈색의 처량한 나비매듭 한두 개만 달고 있었다."]], [["‘If you please, sir, would you have the goodness to walk in, and speak to Miss Dartle?’", "\"실례합니다, 선생님. 죄송하지만 안으로 드셔서 다틀 아가씨와 말씀 좀 나누어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Has Miss Dartle sent you for me?’ I inquired.", "\"다틀 양이 나를 부르라고 너를 보냈느냐?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Not tonight, sir, but it’s just the same.", "\"오늘 밤은 아니에요, 선생님. 하지만 마찬가지예요."], ["Miss Dartle saw you pass a night or two ago; and I was to sit at work on the staircase, and when I saw you pass again, to ask you to step in and speak to her.’", "다틀 아가씨가 하루 이틀 전에 선생님이 지나가시는 걸 보셨거든요. 그래서 저더러 계단에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있다가, 선생님이 또 지나가시거든 안으로 모시고 와서 말씀을 나누시게 하라고 하셨어요.\""]], [["I turned back, and inquired of my conductor, as we went along, how Mrs. Steerforth was.", "나는 발길을 돌렸고, 가는 동안 안내하는 아이에게 스티어포스 부인은 어떠시냐고 물었다."], ["She said her lady was but poorly, and kept her own room a good deal.", "아이는 마님이 편찮으셔서 방에만 많이 계신다고 했다."]], [["When we arrived at the house, I was directed to Miss Dartle in the garden, and left to make my presence known to her myself.", "집에 다다르자 나는 정원에 있는 다틀 양에게로 안내되었고, 내가 왔음을 직접 알리도록 그 자리에 남겨졌다."], ["She was sitting on a seat at one end of a kind of terrace, overlooking the great city.", "그녀는 일종의 테라스 한쪽 끝에 놓인 자리에 앉아, 저 아래 거대한 도시를 내려다보고 있었다."], ["It was a sombre evening, with a lurid light in the sky; and as I saw the prospect scowling in the distance, with here and there some larger object starting up into the sullen glare, I fancied it was no inapt companion to the memory of this fierce woman.", "음울한 저녁이었고, 하늘에는 불그죽죽한 빛이 어려 있었다. 저 멀리 풍경이 잔뜩 찌푸린 듯 보이고, 그 침침한 노을 속에 여기저기 큼직한 윤곽들이 불쑥불쑥 솟아오르는 것을 바라보면서, 나는 그것이 이 사나운 여인을 떠올리기에 그리 어울리지 않는 동반자는 아니라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["She saw me as I advanced, and rose for a moment to receive me.", "그녀는 내가 다가오는 것을 보고 잠깐 일어서서 나를 맞았다."], ["I thought her, then, still more colourless and thin than when I had seen her last; the flashing eyes still brighter, and the scar still plainer.", "그때 나는 그녀가 지난번에 보았을 때보다 한층 더 핏기 없고 야위었다고 느꼈다. 번뜩이는 두 눈은 여전히 더 형형했고, 흉터는 여전히 더 또렷했다."]], [["Our meeting was not cordial.", "우리의 만남은 다정하지 않았다."], ["We had parted angrily on the last occasion; and there was an air of disdain about her, which she took no pains to conceal.", "지난번에 우리는 성을 내며 헤어졌다. 그녀에게는 굳이 감추려 들지 않는 경멸의 기색이 어려 있었다."]], [["‘I am told you wish to speak to me, Miss Dartle,’ said I, standing near her, with my hand upon the back of the seat, and declining her gesture of invitation to sit down.", "\"저더러 할 말이 있으시다고 들었습니다, 다틀 양.\" 내가 그녀 곁에 서서, 한 손을 자리 등받이에 얹은 채, 앉으라는 손짓을 사양하며 말했다."]], [["‘If you please,’ said she.", "\"그러시지요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Pray has this girl been found?’", "\"그래, 그 계집은 찾았나요?\" \"못 찾았습니다.\""]], [["‘No.’\n‘And yet she has run away!’", "\"그런데도 도망을 쳤다니!\""]], [["I saw her thin lips working while she looked at me, as if they were eager to load her with reproaches.", "나를 바라보는 그녀의 얇은 입술이 달싹이는 것이 보였다. 마치 그 입술이 그 아이에게 비난을 한껏 퍼붓고 싶어 안달이 난 것 같았다."]], [["‘Run away?’ I repeated.", "\"도망을 쳤다고요?\" 내가 되물었다."]], [["‘Yes!", "\"그래요!"], ["From him,’ she said, with a laugh.", "그 사람한테서요.\" 그녀가 웃음을 띠며 말했다."], ["‘If she is not found, perhaps she never will be found.", "\"못 찾는다면, 어쩌면 영영 못 찾을지도 모르죠."], ["She may be dead!’", "죽었을지도 모르고요!\""]], [["The vaunting cruelty with which she met my glance, I never saw expressed in any other face that ever I have seen.", "내 눈길을 받아 내며 으스대듯 드러내던 그 잔인함은, 내가 이제껏 본 그 어떤 얼굴에서도 그런 식으로 드러나는 것을 본 적이 없었다."]], [["‘To wish her dead,’ said I, ‘may be the kindest wish that one of her own sex could bestow upon her.", "\"그 아이가 죽기를 바라는 것은,\" 내가 말했다. \"같은 여자가 그 아이에게 베풀 수 있는 가장 다정한 소망일지도 모르겠군요."], ["I am glad that time has softened you so much, Miss Dartle.’", "세월이 당신을 그토록 누그러뜨려 놓아서 다행입니다, 다틀 양.\""]], [["She condescended to make no reply, but, turning on me with another scornful laugh, said:", "그녀는 굳이 대꾸하는 수고를 마다하고, 또 한 번 비웃음을 흘리며 나를 돌아보고는 말했다."]], [["‘The friends of this excellent and much-injured young lady are friends of yours.", "\"이 훌륭하고 크게 상처 입은 아가씨의 벗들이 당신의 벗들이시죠."], ["You are their champion, and assert their rights.", "당신은 그들의 투사이고, 그들의 권리를 주장하시고요."], ["Do you wish to know what is known of her?’", "그 계집에 대해 알려진 것을 알고 싶으세요?\" \"그렇습니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said I.", "그녀는 험상궂은 미소를 지으며 일어나, 가까이에 있던, 잔디밭과 채소밭을 가르는 호랑가시나무 울타리 쪽으로 몇 걸음 다가가더니, 더 큰 목소리로 말했다."], ["She rose with an ill-favoured smile, and taking a few steps towards a wall of holly that was near at hand, dividing the lawn from a kitchen-garden, said, in a louder voice, ‘Come here!’--as if she were calling to some unclean beast.", "\"이리 와!\" 마치 무슨 더러운 짐승이라도 부르는 듯한 투였다."]], [["‘You will restrain any demonstrative championship or vengeance in this place, of course, Mr.", "\"물론 이 자리에서 과시하듯 투사 노릇을 하거나 복수를 벌이는 일은 삼가시겠지요, 코퍼필드 씨?\""], ["Copperfield?’ said she, looking over her shoulder at me with the same expression.", "그녀가 어깨너머로 나를 돌아보며 아까와 똑같은 표정으로 말했다."]], [["I inclined my head, without knowing what she meant; and she said, ‘Come here!’ again; and returned, followed by the respectable Mr.", "나는 그녀가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지도 모른 채 고개를 숙였다. 그녀는 다시 \"이리 와!\" 하고 부르더니, 점잖은 리티머 씨를 거느리고 돌아왔다."], ["Littimer, who, with undiminished respectability, made me a bow, and took up his position behind her.", "리티머 씨는 여전히 점잖음을 조금도 잃지 않은 채 나에게 인사를 하고는 그녀의 뒤에 가서 섰다."], ["The air of wicked grace: of triumph, in which, strange to say, there was yet something feminine and alluring: with which she reclined upon the seat between us, and looked at me, was worthy of a cruel Princess in a Legend.", "그녀가 우리 사이의 자리에 비스듬히 기대어 나를 바라보던 그 사악한 우아함, 의기양양함의 기색에는, 이상하게도 어딘가 여성적이고 매혹적인 무엇이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 전설 속 잔인한 공주에게나 어울릴 법한 것이었다."]], [["‘Now,’ said she, imperiously, without glancing at him, and touching the old wound as it throbbed: perhaps, in this instance, with pleasure rather than pain.", "\"자.\" 그녀가 그를 흘끗도 보지 않은 채 위압적으로 말하며, 욱신거리는 그 오래된 상처를 어루만졌다. 어쩌면 이번만큼은 고통이 아니라 쾌감으로 그랬는지도 모른다."], ["‘Tell Mr. Copperfield about the flight.’", "\"코퍼필드 씨에게 그 도망 이야기를 들려 드려.\""]], [["‘Mr. James and myself, ma’am--’", "\"제임스 도련님과 저는, 마님—\""]], [["‘Don’t address yourself to me!’ she interrupted with a frown.", "\"나한테 말 걸지 마!\" 그녀가 눈살을 찌푸리며 말을 끊었다."]], [["‘Mr. James and myself, sir--’", "\"제임스 도련님과 저는, 선생님—\""]], [["‘Nor to me, if you please,’ said I.", "\"저한테도 말 걸지 마시지요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Littimer, without being at all discomposed, signified by a slight obeisance, that anything that was most agreeable to us was most agreeable to him; and began again.", "리티머 씨는 조금도 흐트러지지 않고, 가볍게 머리를 조아려 우리에게 가장 흡족한 것이라면 무엇이든 자기에게도 가장 흡족하다는 뜻을 내비치고는, 다시 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["‘Mr. James and myself have been abroad with the young woman, ever since she left Yarmouth under Mr. James’s protection.", "\"제임스 도련님과 저는 그 젊은 여자가 야머스를 떠나 도련님의 보호 아래 들어온 뒤로 줄곧 함께 외국에 나가 있었습니다."], ["We have been in a variety of places, and seen a deal of foreign country.", "여러 곳을 다녔고, 이국땅을 많이 구경했습니다."], ["We have been in France, Switzerland, Italy, in fact, almost all parts.’", "프랑스, 스위스, 이탈리아, 사실상 거의 모든 곳을 다 다녔습니다.\""]], [["He looked at the back of the seat, as if he were addressing himself to that; and softly played upon it with his hands, as if he were striking chords upon a dumb piano.", "그는 마치 그 자리 등받이를 향해 말하기라도 하듯 그쪽을 바라보았고, 마치 소리 안 나는 피아노 건반을 짚어 화음을 내듯, 두 손으로 그 등받이를 부드럽게 두드렸다."]], [["‘Mr. James took quite uncommonly to the young woman; and was more settled, for a length of time, than I have known him to be since I have been in his service.", "\"제임스 도련님은 그 젊은 여자에게 자못 유별나게 빠져들었습니다. 그래서 한동안은, 제가 도련님을 모신 이래로 본 적이 없을 만큼 마음을 잡고 차분하게 지내셨습니다."], ["The young woman was very improvable, and spoke the languages; and wouldn’t have been known for the same country-person.", "그 젊은 여자는 무척 가르치기 좋은 사람이라 외국어도 배워 익혔고, 도무지 같은 시골 출신이라고는 알아볼 수 없을 정도였습니다."], ["I noticed that she was much admired wherever we went.’", "가는 곳마다 많은 사람의 찬사를 받는 것을 저도 눈여겨보았습니다.\""]], [["Miss Dartle put her hand upon her side.", "다틀 양이 한 손을 옆구리에 갖다 댔다."], ["I saw him steal a glance at her, and slightly smile to himself.", "나는 그가 그녀를 슬쩍 곁눈질하며 혼자 살짝 미소 짓는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Very much admired, indeed, the young woman was.", "\"정말로, 그 젊은 여자는 대단한 찬사를 받았습니다."], ["What with her dress; what with the air and sun; what with being made so much of; what with this, that, and the other; her merits really attracted general notice.’", "옷차림이며, 바닷바람과 햇볕이며, 그토록 떠받들어졌던 것이며, 이것저것 온갖 사정이 어우러진 덕에, 그 여자의 매력이 정말이지 뭇사람의 눈길을 끌었던 겁니다.\""]], [["He made a short pause.", "그는 잠깐 말을 멈췄다."], ["Her eyes wandered restlessly over the distant prospect, and she bit her nether lip to stop that busy mouth.", "그녀의 두 눈은 먼 풍경 위를 불안하게 헤맸고, 그녀는 그 부산한 입을 멈추려는 듯 아랫입술을 깨물었다."]], [["Taking his hands from the seat, and placing one of them within the other, as he settled himself on one leg, Mr. Littimer proceeded, with his eyes cast down, and his respectable head a little advanced, and a little on one side:", "리티머 씨는 등받이에서 두 손을 거두어 한 손을 다른 손 안에 포개고, 한쪽 다리에 몸을 싣고 자세를 고치고는, 눈을 내리깐 채, 점잖은 머리를 조금 앞으로, 또 조금 옆으로 기울이며 말을 이었다."]], [["‘The young woman went on in this manner for some time, being occasionally low in her spirits, until I think she began to weary Mr. James by giving way to her low spirits and tempers of that kind; and things were not so comfortable. Mr.", "\"그 젊은 여자는 한동안 이런 식으로 지냈는데, 이따금 기분이 가라앉곤 했습니다. 그러다 제 생각에 그 가라앉은 기분이며 그런 종류의 변덕을 자꾸 부려 대는 통에 제임스 도련님이 슬슬 싫증을 내기 시작하셨고, 사정이 그리 편치 않게 되었습니다."], ["James he began to be restless again.", "도련님은 다시 안절부절못하기 시작하셨고요."], ["The more restless he got, the worse she got; and I must say, for myself, that I had a very difficult time of it indeed between the two.", "도련님이 안절부절못할수록, 그 여자는 더 나빠졌습니다. 그래서 제 처지로 말하자면, 두 사람 사이에서 참으로 어려운 시간을 보냈다고 말씀드릴 수밖에 없습니다."], ["Still matters were patched up here, and made good there, over and over again; and altogether lasted, I am sure, for a longer time than anybody could have expected.’", "그래도 여기서 한 번 꿰매고, 저기서 한 번 메우고, 그렇게 거듭하면서, 통틀어 누구도 예상치 못했을 만큼 오래 끌었던 것은 분명합니다.\""]], [["Recalling her eyes from the distance, she looked at me again now, with her former air. Mr.", "그녀는 먼 데에서 눈길을 거두어 다시 나를 바라보았는데, 아까의 그 기색 그대로였다."], ["Littimer, clearing his throat behind his hand with a respectable short cough, changed legs, and went on:", "리티머 씨는 손으로 입을 가리고 점잖게 짧은 헛기침으로 목을 가다듬더니, 다리를 바꿔 짚고 말을 이었다."]], [["‘At last, when there had been, upon the whole, a good many words and reproaches, Mr.", "\"마침내, 통틀어 말다툼과 비난이 꽤 오갔던 어느 날 아침, 제임스 도련님은 우리가 별장을 두고 있던 나폴리 근방에서 떠나셨습니다(그 젊은 여자가 바다를 무척 좋아했거든요). 그러고는 하루 이틀 안에 돌아오겠다는 핑계를 대고, 관련된 모두의 행복을 위해서 도련님이—\" 여기서 또 한 번 짧은 기침으로 말이 끊겼다."], ["James he set off one morning, from the neighbourhood of Naples, where we had a villa (the young woman being very partial to the sea), and, under pretence of coming back in a day or so, left it in charge with me to break it out, that, for the general happiness of all concerned, he was’--here an interruption of the short cough--‘gone. But Mr.", "\"—떠나셨다는 것을 제가 알려 주는 일을 떠맡기셨습니다. 하지만 제임스 도련님이 참으로 더없이 떳떳하게 처신하셨다는 점은 말씀드려야겠습니다."], ["James, I must say, certainly did behave extremely honourable; for he proposed that the young woman should marry a very respectable person, who was fully prepared to overlook the past, and who was, at least, as good as anybody the young woman could have aspired to in a regular way: her connexions being very common.’", "도련님은 그 젊은 여자가 아주 점잖은 사람과 결혼하면 어떻겠느냐고 제안하셨으니까요. 그 사람은 지난 일을 다 눈감아 줄 마음의 준비가 충분히 되어 있었고, 적어도 그 젊은 여자가 정상적인 길로 바랄 수 있었던 그 누구 못지않게 괜찮은 사람이었습니다. 그 여자의 집안이 워낙 미천했으니까요.\""]], [["He changed legs again, and wetted his lips.", "그는 다시 다리를 바꿔 짚고 입술을 축였다."], ["I was convinced that the scoundrel spoke of himself, and I saw my conviction reflected in Miss Dartle’s face.", "나는 그 악당이 자기 자신을 두고 하는 말이라고 확신했고, 다틀 양의 얼굴에 비친 표정에서 내 확신이 그대로 반영된 것을 보았다."]], [["‘This I also had it in charge to communicate.", "\"이것 또한 제가 전하도록 맡은 일이었습니다."], ["I was willing to do anything to relieve Mr. James from his difficulty, and to restore harmony between himself and an affectionate parent, who has undergone so much on his account.", "저는 제임스 도련님을 그 곤경에서 벗어나게 해 드리고, 도련님과, 도련님 때문에 그토록 많은 고생을 하신 자애로우신 어머님 사이의 화목을 되찾아 드리기 위해서라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 하려 했습니다."], ["Therefore I undertook the commission.", "그래서 그 일을 떠맡았던 겁니다."], ["The young woman’s violence when she came to, after I broke the fact of his departure, was beyond all expectations.", "도련님이 떠나셨다는 사실을 제가 알리자, 정신을 차린 그 젊은 여자가 부린 행패는 도무지 상상을 뛰어넘는 것이었습니다."], ["She was quite mad, and had to be held by force; or, if she couldn’t have got to a knife, or got to the sea, she’d have beaten her head against the marble floor.’", "완전히 미쳐 날뛰어서 억지로 붙들어야 했고, 그러지 않으면 칼이라도 잡거나 바다로 뛰어들었을 테고, 그러지도 못하면 대리석 바닥에 제 머리를 짓찧었을 겁니다.\""]], [["Miss Dartle, leaning back upon the seat, with a light of exultation in her face, seemed almost to caress the sounds this fellow had uttered.", "다틀 양은 자리에 등을 기대고 얼굴에 환희의 빛을 띤 채, 이자가 내뱉은 말들을 거의 어루만지는 듯했다."]], [["‘But when I came to the second part of what had been entrusted to me,’ said Mr.", "\"하지만 제가 맡은 일의 두 번째 부분으로 넘어가자,\""], ["Littimer, rubbing his hands uneasily, ‘which anybody might have supposed would have been, at all events, appreciated as a kind intention, then the young woman came out in her true colours.", "리티머 씨가 두 손을 불편하게 비비며 말했다. \"누구든 그래도 그것만큼은 적어도 친절한 뜻으로 여겨 고마워하리라 짐작했을 그 대목으로 넘어가자, 그 젊은 여자는 본색을 드러냈습니다."], ["A more outrageous person I never did see.", "그토록 막돼먹은 사람은 저도 본 적이 없습니다."], ["Her conduct was surprisingly bad.", "그 행실은 놀라울 만큼 못됐습니다."], ["She had no more gratitude, no more feeling, no more patience, no more reason in her, than a stock or a stone. If I hadn’t been upon my guard, I am convinced she would have had my blood.’", "그 여자에게는 나무토막이나 돌멩이만큼의 고마움도, 정도, 참을성도, 분별도 없었습니다. 제가 미리 경계하고 있지 않았다면, 그 여자가 제 피를 보고야 말았으리라고 저는 확신합니다.\""]], [["‘I think the better of her for it,’ said I, indignantly.", "\"그래서 나는 그 아이가 더 마음에 드는군요.\" 내가 분개하여 말했다."]], [["Mr. Littimer bent his head, as much as to say, ‘Indeed, sir?", "리티머 씨는 \"그러십니까, 선생님? 하지만 선생님은 아직 젊으시지요!\""], ["But you’re young!’ and resumed his narrative.", "하고 말하기라도 하듯 고개를 숙이고는, 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["‘It was necessary, in short, for a time, to take away everything nigh her, that she could do herself, or anybody else, an injury with, and to shut her up close.", "\"요컨대 한동안은, 그 여자 가까이에서 제 몸이든 남의 몸이든 해칠 수 있는 모든 것을 치워 버리고, 그 여자를 꽉 가둬 둘 필요가 있었습니다."], ["Notwithstanding which, she got out in the night; forced the lattice of a window, that I had nailed up myself; dropped on a vine that was trailed below; and never has been seen or heard of, to my knowledge, since.’", "그런데도 그 여자는 밤중에 빠져나갔습니다. 제가 직접 못질을 해 두었던 창문 격자를 비틀어 부수고, 그 아래로 뻗어 있던 포도 덩굴을 타고 내려가서는, 제가 알기로 그 뒤로 다시는 보이지도, 소식이 들리지도 않았습니다.\""]], [["‘She is dead, perhaps,’ said Miss Dartle, with a smile, as if she could have spurned the body of the ruined girl.", "\"어쩌면 죽었겠지.\" 다틀 양이 미소를 띠며 말했는데, 마치 그 망가진 여자의 시신을 발로 걷어찰 수도 있다는 듯한 투였다."]], [["‘She may have drowned herself, miss,’ returned Mr.", "\"물에 빠져 죽었을지도 모릅니다, 아가씨.\""], ["Littimer, catching at an excuse for addressing himself to somebody.", "리티머 씨가 누군가에게 말을 걸 구실을 붙잡으며 대답했다."], ["‘It’s very possible.", "\"충분히 그럴 만합니다."], ["Or, she may have had assistance from the boatmen, and the boatmen’s wives and children.", "아니면 뱃사람들이나 그 아낙들, 아이들의 도움을 받았을 수도 있고요."], ["Being given to low company, she was very much in the habit of talking to them on the beach, Miss Dartle, and sitting by their boats.", "그 여자는 천한 무리들과 어울리기를 좋아해서, 해변에서 그들과 이야기하고 그들의 배 곁에 앉아 있는 일이 다반사였습니다, 다틀 아가씨."], ["I have known her do it, when Mr. James has been away, whole days. Mr.", "제임스 도련님이 자리를 비우면 하루 종일 그러는 것을 제가 본 적도 있습니다."], ["James was far from pleased to find out, once, that she had told the children she was a boatman’s daughter, and that in her own country, long ago, she had roamed about the beach, like them.’", "한번은 그 여자가 아이들에게 자기가 뱃사람의 딸이며, 옛날 제 고향에서는 자기도 그 애들처럼 해변을 쏘다녔노라고 말한 것을 알고 도련님이 영 못마땅해하셨던 일도 있고요.\""]], [["Oh, Emily!", "오, 에밀리!"], ["Unhappy beauty!", "가엾은 미인이여!"], ["What a picture rose before me of her sitting on the far-off shore, among the children like herself when she was innocent, listening to little voices such as might have called her Mother had she been a poor man’s wife; and to the great voice of the sea, with its eternal ‘Never more!’", "그녀가 저 머나먼 바닷가에, 자기가 순결하던 시절의 자기와 똑같은 아이들 틈에 앉아, 가난한 사내의 아내였더라면 자기를 어머니라 불렀을 법한 그 작은 목소리들에, 그리고 영원토록 \"두 번 다시는!\" 하고 외치는 바다의 그 거대한 목소리에 귀 기울이는 그림이, 내 눈앞에 얼마나 또렷이 떠올랐던가!"]], [["‘When it was clear that nothing could be done, Miss Dartle--’", "\"더 이상 어찌해 볼 도리가 없다는 게 분명해지자, 다틀 아가씨—\""]], [["‘Did I tell you not to speak to me?’ she said, with stern contempt.", "\"내가 나한테 말 걸지 말랬잖아?\" 그녀가 매서운 경멸을 담아 말했다."]], [["‘You spoke to me, miss,’ he replied.", "\"아가씨가 저한테 말을 거셨잖습니까, 아가씨.\""], ["‘I beg your pardon.", "그가 대답했다. \"용서하십시오."], ["But it is my service to obey.’", "하지만 따르는 것이 제 직분이라서요.\""]], [["‘Do your service,’ she returned.", "\"네 직분이나 해.\" 그녀가 되받았다."], ["‘Finish your story, and go!’", "\"이야기나 끝내고, 가!\""]], [["‘When it was clear,’ he said, with infinite respectability and an obedient bow, ‘that she was not to be found, I went to Mr.", "\"더 이상 어찌해 볼 도리가 없다는 게 분명해지자,\" 그가 한없는 점잖음과 순종하는 인사로 말했다."], ["James, at the place where it had been agreed that I should write to him, and informed him of what had occurred.", "\"그 여자를 도무지 찾을 수 없다는 게 분명해지자, 저는 도련님께 편지를 보내기로 약속해 둔 곳으로 가서, 그간 있었던 일을 알렸습니다."], ["Words passed between us in consequence, and I felt it due to my character to leave him.", "그 일로 우리 사이에 말이 오갔고, 저는 제 인격을 지키기 위해서라도 도련님 곁을 떠나는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈습니다."], ["I could bear, and I have borne, a great deal from Mr.", "저는 제임스 도련님에게서 많은 것을 견딜 수 있었고, 또 견뎌 왔습니다."], ["James; but he insulted me too far. He hurt me.", "하지만 도련님은 저를 너무 심하게 모욕하셨습니다."], ["Knowing the unfortunate difference between himself and his mother, and what her anxiety of mind was likely to be, I took the liberty of coming home to England, and relating--’", "저를 상처 입히셨어요. 저는 도련님과 어머님 사이의 불행한 불화를, 또 그로 인해 어머님 마음이 얼마나 애를 태우실지를 알았기에, 외람되이 영국 본국으로 돌아와 알리는—\""]], [["‘For money which I paid him,’ said Miss Dartle to me.", "\"내가 그에게 돈을 치렀고요.\" 다틀 양이 나에게 말했다."]], [["‘Just so, ma’am--and relating what I knew.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 마님—제가 아는 것을 알리는 일을 했습니다."], ["I am not aware,’ said Mr.", "그 밖에 더 드릴 말씀은,\""], ["Littimer, after a moment’s reflection, ‘that there is anything else.", "리티머 씨가 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다. \"없는 것 같습니다."], ["I am at present out of employment, and should be happy to meet with a respectable situation.’", "저는 지금 일자리가 없는 처지이니, 점잖은 자리를 하나 만날 수 있다면 기쁘겠습니다.\""]], [["Miss Dartle glanced at me, as though she would inquire if there were anything that I desired to ask.", "다틀 양은 내가 묻고 싶은 것이 있는지 알아보려는 듯 나를 흘끗 보았다."], ["As there was something which had occurred to my mind, I said in reply:", "마침 내 마음에 떠오른 것이 있어, 나는 이렇게 대답 삼아 말했다."]], [["‘I could wish to know from this--creature,’ I could not bring myself to utter any more conciliatory word, ‘whether they intercepted a letter that was written to her from home, or whether he supposes that she received it.’", "\"나는 이—작자에게서 알고 싶은 게 있소.\" 나는 도저히 그보다 누그러진 말은 입에 올릴 수가 없었다. \"이 사람들이 집에서 그 아이에게 보낸 편지를 가로챈 것인지, 아니면 그 아이가 그 편지를 받았다고 이 사람이 짐작하는지를 말이오.\""]], [["He remained calm and silent, with his eyes fixed on the ground, and the tip of every finger of his right hand delicately poised against the tip of every finger of his left.", "그는 차분하고 잠잠하게, 눈길을 땅에 고정한 채, 오른손 손가락 끝마다 왼손 손가락 끝을 하나하나 섬세하게 맞댄 채 서 있었다."]], [["Miss Dartle turned her head disdainfully towards him.", "다틀 양이 멸시하듯 고개를 그에게로 돌렸다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, miss,’ he said, awakening from his abstraction, ‘but, however submissive to you, I have my position, though a servant. Mr.", "\"용서하십시오, 아가씨.\" 그가 멍한 상태에서 깨어나며 말했다. \"아무리 아가씨께 순종한다 해도, 저는 하인일망정 제 처지가 있습니다."], ["Copperfield and you, miss, are different people.", "코퍼필드 씨와 아가씨는 서로 다른 분이시고요."], ["If Mr. Copperfield wishes to know anything from me, I take the liberty of reminding Mr. Copperfield that he can put a question to me.", "코퍼필드 씨가 저에게서 무언가를 알고 싶으시다면, 외람되지만 코퍼필드 씨께서 저에게 직접 물으실 수 있다는 점을 일깨워 드리는 바입니다."], ["I have a character to maintain.’", "저에게는 지켜야 할 인격이 있으니까요.\""]], [["After a momentary struggle with myself, I turned my eyes upon him, and said, ‘You have heard my question.", "나는 잠깐 나 자신과 씨름한 끝에, 그에게 눈길을 돌리고 말했다. \"내 질문은 자네도 들었네."], ["Consider it addressed to yourself, if you choose.", "원한다면 그게 자네에게 던진 것이라고 여기게."], ["What answer do you make?’", "자네는 어떻게 대답하겠나?\""]], [["‘Sir,’ he rejoined, with an occasional separation and reunion of those delicate tips, ‘my answer must be qualified; because, to betray Mr.", "\"선생님.\" 그가 그 섬세한 손가락 끝을 이따금 떼었다 다시 붙이며 대답했다. \"제 대답에는 단서를 달 수밖에 없습니다."], ["James’s confidence to his mother, and to betray it to you, are two different actions.", "제임스 도련님의 신임을 그 어머님께 저버리는 것과, 선생님께 저버리는 것은 서로 다른 행동이니까요."], ["It is not probable, I consider, that Mr. James would encourage the receipt of letters likely to increase low spirits and unpleasantness; but further than that, sir, I should wish to avoid going.’", "제 생각에, 제임스 도련님이 기분을 가라앉히고 불쾌함을 더할 만한 편지를 받게끔 부추기셨을 리는 없습니다. 하지만 그 이상은, 선생님, 더 들어가는 것을 삼가고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘Is that all?’ inquired Miss Dartle of me.", "\"그게 다인가요?\" 다틀 양이 나에게 물었다."]], [["I indicated that I had nothing more to say.", "나는 더 할 말이 없다는 뜻을 내비쳤다. \"다만,\""], ["‘Except,’ I added, as I saw him moving off, ‘that I understand this fellow’s part in the wicked story, and that, as I shall make it known to the honest man who has been her father from her childhood, I would recommend him to avoid going too much into public.’", "그가 자리를 뜨려는 것을 보고 내가 덧붙였다. \"이 못된 이야기에서 이 작자가 맡은 역할을 나는 똑똑히 알고 있고, 그 아이를 어릴 적부터 아버지처럼 돌봐 온 그 정직한 분에게 이 사실을 알릴 작정이니, 이 사람은 사람들 앞에 너무 나돌아 다니지 않는 게 좋을 거라고 일러 두겠소.\""]], [["He had stopped the moment I began, and had listened with his usual repose of manner.", "내가 말을 꺼내자 그는 곧장 멈춰 서서, 여느 때처럼 침착한 태도로 귀 기울였다."]], [["‘Thank you, sir.", "\"감사합니다, 선생님."], ["But you’ll excuse me if I say, sir, that there are neither slaves nor slave-drivers in this country, and that people are not allowed to take the law into their own hands.", "하지만 선생님, 외람되지만 한 말씀 드리자면, 이 나라에는 노예도 노예 부리는 자도 없으며, 사람이 제멋대로 법을 손에 쥐는 일은 허락되지 않습니다."], ["If they do, it is more to their own peril, I believe, than to other people’s.", "만약 그런 짓을 한다면, 제 생각에 그것은 남보다 자기 자신에게 더 위태로운 일이지요."], ["Consequently speaking, I am not at all afraid of going wherever I may wish, sir.’", "그러니 결과적으로 말씀드리자면, 저는 제가 가고 싶은 곳이라면 어디든 가는 데 전혀 두려움이 없습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["With that, he made a polite bow; and, with another to Miss Dartle, went away through the arch in the wall of holly by which he had come.", "그러고는 그가 정중하게 인사를 하고, 다틀 양에게도 또 한 번 인사를 하고는, 그가 들어왔던 호랑가시나무 울타리의 아치문을 통해 가 버렸다."], ["Miss Dartle and I regarded each other for a little while in silence; her manner being exactly what it was, when she had produced the man.", "다틀 양과 나는 잠시 말없이 서로를 바라보았다. 그녀의 태도는 그자를 불러냈을 때와 조금도 다르지 않았다."]], [["‘He says besides,’ she observed, with a slow curling of her lip, ‘that his master, as he hears, is coasting Spain; and this done, is away to gratify his seafaring tastes till he is weary.", "\"그가 덧붙이기로는,\" 그녀가 입술을 천천히 비틀며 말했다. \"자기가 듣기로 자기 주인이 스페인 연안을 항해 중이고, 이 일이 끝나면 물릴 때까지 뱃놀이 취미를 마음껏 채우러 떠난다고 하더군요."], ["But this is of no interest to you.", "하지만 이건 당신에게 아무 흥미도 없는 얘기겠죠."], ["Between these two proud persons, mother and son, there is a wider breach than before, and little hope of its healing, for they are one at heart, and time makes each more obstinate and imperious.", "이 거만한 두 사람, 어머니와 아들 사이에는 전보다 더 깊은 골이 패었고, 그 골이 메워질 가망도 거의 없어요. 둘은 본바탕이 똑같고, 세월이 갈수록 서로 더 고집스럽고 더 안하무인이 되어 가니까요."], ["Neither is this of any interest to you; but it introduces what I wish to say. This devil whom you make an angel of.", "이것도 당신한테는 아무 흥미 없는 얘기겠지만, 이게 내가 하려는 말의 실마리예요. 당신이 천사로 떠받드는 이 악마, 그러니까 그가 갯벌 진창에서 건져 올린 이 천한 계집 말이에요.\""], ["I mean this low girl whom he picked out of the tide-mud,’ with her black eyes full upon me, and her passionate finger up, ‘may be alive,--for I believe some common things are hard to die.", "그녀가 검은 두 눈을 나에게 가득 쏟고 격정에 찬 손가락을 치켜들며 말을 이었다. \"어쩌면 살아 있을지도 모르죠. 천한 것들은 좀처럼 죽지 않는 법이라고 나는 믿거든요."], ["If she is, you will desire to have a pearl of such price found and taken care of.", "만약 살아 있다면, 당신은 그토록 값진 진주가 발견되어 잘 간수되기를 바라겠죠."], ["We desire that, too; that he may not by any chance be made her prey again.", "우리도 그걸 바라요. 그가 어쩌다 다시 그 계집의 먹잇감이 되지 않도록 말이에요."], ["So far, we are united in one interest; and that is why I, who would do her any mischief that so coarse a wretch is capable of feeling, have sent for you to hear what you have heard.’", "거기까지는, 우리가 한 가지 이해로 뭉친 셈이죠. 그래서 그토록 천박한 인간이 느낄 수 있는 온갖 해코지라도 그 계집에게 퍼붓고 싶은 나 같은 사람이, 당신을 불러서 당신이 들은 얘기를 듣게 한 거예요.\""]], [["I saw, by the change in her face, that someone was advancing behind me. It was Mrs.", "나는 그녀의 얼굴빛이 변하는 것을 보고, 누군가 내 뒤에서 다가오고 있음을 알았다. 스티어포스 부인이었다."], ["Steerforth, who gave me her hand more coldly than of yore, and with an augmentation of her former stateliness of manner, but still, I perceived--and I was touched by it--with an ineffaceable remembrance of my old love for her son.", "그녀는 예전보다 더 쌀쌀하게, 또 그 옛날의 위엄을 한층 더 부풀린 태도로 나에게 손을 내밀었다. 하지만 그러면서도 나는—그리고 그 점에 마음이 뭉클했는데—그녀가 자기 아들을 향한 내 옛 사랑을 결코 지울 수 없는 기억으로 간직하고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["She was greatly altered.", "그녀는 몹시 변해 있었다."], ["Her fine figure was far less upright, her handsome face was deeply marked, and her hair was almost white.", "그 곱던 자태는 한참 덜 꼿꼿했고, 잘생긴 얼굴에는 깊은 주름이 패었으며, 머리는 거의 하얗게 세어 있었다."], ["But when she sat down on the seat, she was a handsome lady still; and well I knew the bright eye with its lofty look, that had been a light in my very dreams at school.", "그러나 자리에 앉았을 때, 그녀는 여전히 아름다운 부인이었다. 그 고고한 눈빛이 깃든 형형한 눈을, 학창 시절 내 꿈속에서도 한 줄기 빛이 되어 주던 그 눈을, 나는 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["‘Is Mr. Copperfield informed of everything, Rosa?’", "\"코퍼필드 씨가 모든 걸 들었느냐, 로자?\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘And has he heard Littimer himself?’", "\"리티머가 직접 하는 얘기도 들었고?\""]], [["‘Yes; I have told him why you wished it.’ ‘You are a good girl.", "\"네. 어머님이 왜 그러길 바라셨는지도 제가 말씀드렸어요.\" \"착하구나."], ["I have had some slight correspondence with your former friend, sir,’ addressing me, ‘but it has not restored his sense of duty or natural obligation.", "선생, 나는 자네의 옛 벗과 약간의 서신을 주고받은 적이 있네.\" 그녀가 나에게 말했다. \"하지만 그것으로 그 애의 의무감이나 천륜에 대한 도리가 되살아나지는 않았네."], ["Therefore I have no other object in this, than what Rosa has mentioned.", "그러니 이 일에서 내가 바라는 것은 로자가 말한 것 외에는 아무것도 없네."], ["If, by the course which may relieve the mind of the decent man you brought here (for whom I am sorry--I can say no more), my son may be saved from again falling into the snares of a designing enemy, well!’", "만약, 자네가 여기 데려온 그 점잖은 사람의(나는 그 사람이 안됐다고 생각하네—그 이상은 말할 수 없지만) 마음을 덜어 줄 어떤 방법을 통해서, 내 아들이 음흉한 적의 덫에 또다시 빠지는 일을 면할 수 있다면, 그것으로 좋네!\""]], [["She drew herself up, and sat looking straight before her, far away.", "그녀는 몸을 곧추세우고, 저 멀리 앞만을 똑바로 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Madam,’ I said respectfully, ‘I understand.", "\"부인.\" 내가 정중하게 말했다. \"잘 알겠습니다."], ["I assure you I am in no danger of putting any strained construction on your motives.", "단언컨대 저는 부인의 뜻을 비뚤어지게 해석할 마음이 조금도 없습니다."], ["But I must say, even to you, having known this injured family from childhood, that if you suppose the girl, so deeply wronged, has not been cruelly deluded, and would not rather die a hundred deaths than take a cup of water from your son’s hand now, you cherish a terrible mistake.’", "하지만 이 상처 입은 가족을 어릴 적부터 알아 온 사람으로서, 부인께라도 이 말씀만은 드려야겠습니다. 만약 그토록 깊이 짓밟힌 그 아이가 잔인하게 속아 넘어간 것이 아니라고, 그리고 지금 부인의 아드님 손에서 물 한 잔 받아 마시느니 차라리 백 번을 죽으려 하지 않을 거라고 여기신다면, 부인은 무서운 착각을 품고 계신 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Well, Rosa, well!’ said Mrs.", "\"됐다, 로자, 됐어!\""], ["Steerforth, as the other was about to interpose, ‘it is no matter.", "다른 여자가 끼어들려 하자 스티어포스 부인이 말했다. \"아무래도 좋다."], ["Let it be.", "그냥 두어라."], ["You are married, sir, I am told?’", "선생, 자네 결혼했다고 들었네만?\""]], [["I answered that I had been some time married.", "나는 결혼한 지 얼마 되었다고 대답했다."]], [["‘And are doing well?", "\"잘 지내고 있고?"], ["I hear little in the quiet life I lead, but I understand you are beginning to be famous.’", "내가 조용히 사는 터라 듣는 것이 거의 없네만, 자네 이름이 점점 알려지기 시작했다고 알고 있네.\""]], [["‘I have been very fortunate,’ I said, ‘and find my name connected with some praise.’", "\"운이 무척 좋았습니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"제 이름이 약간의 칭찬과 함께 오르내리고 있더군요.\""]], [["‘You have no mother?’--in a softened voice. ‘No.’", "\"어머니는 안 계신가?\" 누그러진 목소리로 물었다."]], [["‘It is a pity,’ she returned.", "\"네, 안 계십니다.\" \"안됐구먼.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["‘She would have been proud of you.", "\"어머니가 계셨다면 자네를 자랑스러워하셨을 텐데."], ["Good night!’", "잘 가게!\""]], [["I took the hand she held out with a dignified, unbending air, and it was as calm in mine as if her breast had been at peace.", "나는 그녀가 위엄 있고 굽힐 줄 모르는 태도로 내미는 손을 잡았는데, 그 손은 마치 그녀의 가슴속이 평온하기라도 한 듯 내 손안에서 차분했다."], ["Her pride could still its very pulses, it appeared, and draw the placid veil before her face, through which she sat looking straight before her on the far distance.", "그녀의 자존심은 그 맥박마저도 잠재울 수 있는 듯했고, 그 얼굴 앞에 잔잔한 장막을 드리워, 그녀는 그 너머로 저 멀리 앞만을 똑바로 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["As I moved away from them along the terrace, I could not help observing how steadily they both sat gazing on the prospect, and how it thickened and closed around them.", "내가 테라스를 따라 그들에게서 멀어져 가는 동안, 나는 두 사람이 얼마나 미동도 없이 앉아 그 풍경을 응시하고 있는지, 그리고 그 풍경이 얼마나 그들 둘레로 짙어지며 죄어드는지를 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Here and there, some early lamps were seen to twinkle in the distant city; and in the eastern quarter of the sky the lurid light still hovered.", "저 멀리 도시에서는 여기저기 이른 등불이 깜박이는 것이 보였고, 동쪽 하늘에는 그 불그죽죽한 빛이 여전히 어른거렸다."], ["But, from the greater part of the broad valley interposed, a mist was rising like a sea, which, mingling with the darkness, made it seem as if the gathering waters would encompass them.", "그러나 그 사이에 끼어든 너른 골짜기의 대부분에서는 안개가 바다처럼 피어올랐고, 그것이 어둠과 뒤섞여 마치 차오르는 물이 그들을 에워싸려는 것처럼 보였다."], ["I have reason to remember this, and think of it with awe; for before I looked upon those two again, a stormy sea had risen to their feet.", "나에게는 이것을 기억하고, 두려운 마음으로 떠올릴 까닭이 있다. 내가 그 두 사람을 다시 보기 전에, 사나운 바다가 그들의 발치까지 차올랐으니까."]], [["Reflecting on what had been thus told me, I felt it right that it should be communicated to Mr. Peggotty.", "이렇게 전해 들은 이야기를 곰곰이 되새기면서, 나는 그것을 패거티 씨에게 알리는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈다."], ["On the following evening I went into London in quest of him.", "이튿날 저녁, 나는 그를 찾아 런던으로 들어갔다."], ["He was always wandering about from place to place, with his one object of recovering his niece before him; but was more in London than elsewhere.", "그는 조카딸을 되찾겠다는 그 하나의 목표를 눈앞에 두고 늘 이곳저곳을 떠돌았지만, 다른 어느 곳보다 런던에 머무는 일이 많았다."], ["Often and often, now, had I seen him in the dead of night passing along the streets, searching, among the few who loitered out of doors at those untimely hours, for what he dreaded to find.", "이제 나는 깊은 밤에 그가 거리를 따라 지나가며, 그런 야심한 시각에 바깥을 어슬렁거리는 몇 안 되는 사람들 틈에서 차마 마주치기 두려운 그것을 찾아 헤매는 모습을 자주, 또 자주 보아 왔다."]], [["He kept a lodging over the little chandler’s shop in Hungerford Market, which I have had occasion to mention more than once, and from which he first went forth upon his errand of mercy. Hither I directed my walk.", "그는 헝거퍼드 시장의 작은 잡화점 위층에 셋방을 얻어 지내고 있었다. 이 가게는 내가 한두 번 이야기한 적이 있는데, 그가 그 자비로운 임무를 위해 처음 길을 나섰던 곳이다."], ["On making inquiry for him, I learned from the people of the house that he had not gone out yet, and I should find him in his room upstairs.", "나는 그쪽으로 발길을 잡았다. 그를 찾으니, 집안사람들에게서 그가 아직 나가지 않았으며 위층 방에 있을 거라는 말을 들었다."]], [["He was sitting reading by a window in which he kept a few plants.", "그는 화초 몇 그루를 둔 창가에서 책을 읽으며 앉아 있었다."], ["The room was very neat and orderly.", "방은 무척 단정하고 가지런했다."], ["I saw in a moment that it was always kept prepared for her reception, and that he never went out but he thought it possible he might bring her home.", "나는 한눈에, 이 방이 늘 그녀를 맞이할 채비를 갖춘 채로 정돈되어 있으며, 그가 밖에 나갈 때마다 어쩌면 그녀를 집으로 데려올 수 있을지도 모른다고 생각하며 나선다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He had not heard my tap at the door, and only raised his eyes when I laid my hand upon his shoulder.", "그는 내가 문을 두드리는 소리도 듣지 못한 채, 내가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고서야 비로소 눈을 들었다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy!", "\"데이비 도련님!"], ["Thankee, sir!", "고맙습니다, 도련님!"], ["thankee hearty, for this visit! Sit ye down.", "이렇게 찾아 주시다니 진심으로 고맙습니다요! 앉으세요."], ["You’re kindly welcome, sir!’", "어서 오세요, 도련님!\""]], [["‘Mr. Peggotty,’ said I, taking the chair he handed me, ‘don’t expect much!", "\"패거티 씨.\" 그가 건네주는 의자에 앉으며 내가 말했다."], ["I have heard some news.’\n‘Of Em’ly!’", "\"너무 큰 기대는 마세요! 소식을 좀 들었습니다.\""]], [["He put his hand, in a nervous manner, on his mouth, and turned pale, as he fixed his eyes on mine.", "\"에밀리 소식인가요!\" 그는 초조하게 손을 입에 갖다 대고, 얼굴이 하얗게 질리며 두 눈을 내 눈에 박았다."]], [["‘It gives no clue to where she is; but she is not with him.’", "\"그 애가 어디 있는지는 알 수 없습니다. 하지만 그 사람과 함께 있지는 않아요.\""]], [["He sat down, looking intently at me, and listened in profound silence to all I had to tell.", "그는 자리에 앉아 나를 골똘히 바라보며, 내가 들려줄 이야기를 깊은 침묵 속에 귀 기울여 들었다."], ["I well remember the sense of dignity, beauty even, with which the patient gravity of his face impressed me, when, having gradually removed his eyes from mine, he sat looking downward, leaning his forehead on his hand.", "그가 차츰 내 눈에서 눈길을 거두어 아래를 내려다보며 이마를 손에 괴고 앉았을 때, 그 얼굴의 참을성 어린 무게가 나에게 안겨 준 위엄, 아니 아름다움마저도 나는 또렷이 기억한다."], ["He offered no interruption, but remained throughout perfectly still.", "그는 한 번도 말을 끊지 않고, 처음부터 끝까지 완전히 미동도 없이 있었다."], ["He seemed to pursue her figure through the narrative, and to let every other shape go by him, as if it were nothing.", "그는 그 이야기 속에서 오로지 그녀의 모습만을 좇으며, 다른 모든 형상은 아무것도 아니라는 듯 그냥 흘려보내는 것 같았다."]], [["When I had done, he shaded his face, and continued silent. I looked out of the window for a little while, and occupied myself with the plants.", "내가 이야기를 마치자, 그는 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 여전히 입을 다물고 있었다. 나는 잠시 창밖을 내다보며 화초들에 마음을 두었다."]], [["‘How do you fare to feel about it, Mas’r Davy?’ he inquired at length.", "\"이 일을 어떻게 받아들이시나요, 데이비 도련님?\" 마침내 그가 물었다."]], [["‘I think that she is living,’ I replied.", "\"제 생각엔 그 애가 살아 있는 것 같습니다.\" 내가 대답했다."]], [["‘I doen’t know.", "\"저는 모르겠습니다요."], ["Maybe the first shock was too rough, and in the wildness of her art--!", "어쩌면 처음 받은 충격이 너무 모질어서, 미친 듯이 어쩔 줄 모르다가—!"], ["That there blue water as she used to speak on.", "그 애가 늘 입에 올리던 그 푸른 바닷물 말이에요."], ["Could she have thowt o’ that so many year, because it was to be her grave!’", "그게 제 무덤이 될 거라서, 그 애가 그토록 여러 해 그 생각을 했던 걸까요!\""]], [["He said this, musing, in a low, frightened voice; and walked across the little room.", "그는 낮고 겁먹은 목소리로 이렇게 혼잣말하듯 중얼거리며, 그 작은 방을 가로질러 걸었다."]], [["‘And yet,’ he added, ‘Mas’r Davy, I have felt so sure as she was living--I have know’d, awake and sleeping, as it was so trew that I should find her--I have been so led on by it, and held up by it--that I doen’t believe I can have been deceived. No!", "\"그렇지만요,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"데이비 도련님, 저는 그 애가 살아 있다고 너무도 굳게 믿어 왔습니다요—깨어 있을 때나 잠들었을 때나, 제가 그 애를 찾아내리라는 게 너무도 참된 일이라고 알고 있었습니다요—그 믿음에 이끌리고, 그 믿음에 버텨 왔습니다요—그러니 제가 속았을 리가 없다고 믿습니다요. 아니에요!"], ["Em’ly’s alive!’", "에밀리는 살아 있어요!\""]], [["He put his hand down firmly on the table, and set his sunburnt face into a resolute expression.", "그는 손을 탁자 위에 굳게 내려놓고, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 단호한 표정으로 다잡았다."]], [["‘My niece, Em’ly, is alive, sir!’ he said, steadfastly.", "\"제 조카딸 에밀리는 살아 있습니다요, 도련님!\" 그가 흔들림 없이 말했다."], ["‘I doen’t know wheer it comes from, or how ‘tis, but I am told as she’s alive!’", "\"그게 어디서 오는 건지, 어떻게 그런 건지는 모르겠습니다요만, 그 애가 살아 있다고, 누가 저한테 일러 주는 것만 같습니다요!\""]], [["He looked almost like a man inspired, as he said it.", "그는 이 말을 하면서 거의 영감에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 보였다."], ["I waited for a few moments, until he could give me his undivided attention; and then proceeded to explain the precaution, that, it had occurred to me last night, it would be wise to take.", "나는 그가 온전히 나에게 마음을 기울일 수 있을 때까지 잠시 기다렸다가, 어젯밤 내 머릿속에 떠오른, 미리 해 두면 좋을 듯한 조치를 설명하기 시작했다."]], [["‘Now, my dear friend--‘I began.", "\"자, 나의 소중한 벗이여—\" 내가 입을 뗐다."]], [["‘Thankee, thankee, kind sir,’ he said, grasping my hand in both of his.", "\"고맙습니다요, 고맙습니다요, 친절하신 도련님.\" 그가 두 손으로 내 손을 꼭 움켜쥐며 말했다."]], [["‘If she should make her way to London, which is likely--for where could she lose herself so readily as in this vast city; and what would she wish to do, but lose and hide herself, if she does not go home?--’", "\"만약 그 애가 런던으로 길을 잡는다면 말입니다—그럴 법도 하지요. 이 거대한 도시만큼 제 몸을 쉽게 숨겨 버릴 수 있는 데가 어디 있겠습니까. 게다가 집으로 돌아가지 않을 거라면, 그 애가 바라는 거라곤 제 몸을 잃어버리고 숨기는 것 말고 또 뭐가 있겠습니까—\""]], [["‘And she won’t go home,’ he interposed, shaking his head mournfully.", "\"그 애는 집에 안 갈 겁니다요.\" 그가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 끼어들었다."], ["‘If she had left of her own accord, she might; not as It was, sir.’", "\"제 발로 떠난 거였다면 모를까, 사정이 그렇질 못하니까요, 도련님.\""]], [["‘If she should come here,’ said I, ‘I believe there is one person, here, more likely to discover her than any other in the world.", "\"만약 그 애가 여기로 온다면,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 세상 그 누구보다 그 애를 찾아낼 가능성이 큰 사람이 한 명, 여기 있다고 저는 믿습니다."], ["Do you remember--hear what I say, with fortitude--think of your great object!--do you remember Martha?’", "기억하세요—제 말을 굳게 들으세요—당신의 그 큰 목표를 떠올리세요!—마사를 기억하시나요?\""]], [["‘Of our town?’", "\"우리 고장의 그 애요?\""]], [["I needed no other answer than his face.", "그의 얼굴 말고는 다른 대답이 필요 없었다."]], [["‘Do you know that she is in London?’", "\"그 애가 런던에 있는 걸 아세요?\""]], [["‘I have seen her in the streets,’ he answered, with a shiver.", "\"길거리에서 본 적이 있습니다요.\" 그가 몸서리를 치며 대답했다."]], [["‘But you don’t know,’ said I, ‘that Emily was charitable to her, with Ham’s help, long before she fled from home.", "\"하지만 모르실 겁니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"에밀리가 집을 떠나기 한참 전부터, 햄의 도움을 받아 그 애에게 자비를 베풀었다는 걸요."], ["Nor, that, when we met one night, and spoke together in the room yonder, over the way, she listened at the door.’", "또 우리가 어느 날 밤 만나서, 길 건너 저쪽 방에서 함께 이야기를 나눌 때, 그 애가 문간에서 엿들었다는 것도요.\""]], [["‘Mas’r Davy!’ he replied in astonishment.", "\"데이비 도련님!\" 그가 깜짝 놀라 대꾸했다."], ["‘That night when it snew so hard?’", "\"그날 밤, 눈이 그토록 모질게 내리던 그날 밤이요?\""]], [["‘That night.", "\"바로 그날 밤이에요."], ["I have never seen her since.", "저는 그 뒤로 그 애를 한 번도 보지 못했어요."], ["I went back, after parting from you, to speak to her, but she was gone.", "당신과 헤어진 뒤에 그 애에게 말을 걸려고 되돌아갔지만, 그 애는 이미 사라지고 없었어요."], ["I was unwilling to mention her to you then, and I am now; but she is the person of whom I speak, and with whom I think we should communicate.", "그때는 그 애 이야기를 당신께 꺼내고 싶지 않았고, 지금도 그렇습니다. 하지만 제가 말하는 사람이 바로 그 애고, 우리가 그 애와 연락을 해 봐야 한다고 생각합니다."], ["Do you understand?’", "무슨 말인지 아시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Too well, sir,’ he replied.", "\"너무 잘 알겠습니다요, 도련님.\""], ["We had sunk our voices, almost to a whisper, and continued to speak in that tone.", "그가 대답했다. 우리는 거의 속삭임에 가깝게 목소리를 낮추었고, 그 어조로 계속 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["‘You say you have seen her.", "\"그 애를 본 적이 있다고 하셨죠."], ["Do you think that you could find her?", "그 애를 찾을 수 있을 것 같으세요?"], ["I could only hope to do so by chance.’", "저로서는 그저 운에 기대는 수밖에 없거든요.\""]], [["‘I think, Mas’r Davy, I know wheer to look.’", "\"제 생각엔, 데이비 도련님, 어디를 찾아봐야 할지 알 것 같습니다요.\""]], [["‘It is dark.", "\"날이 어둡네요."], ["Being together, shall we go out now, and try to find her tonight?’", "이왕 함께 있으니, 지금 나가서 오늘 밤에 그 애를 찾아볼까요?\""]], [["He assented, and prepared to accompany me.", "그는 그러자고 하고, 나를 따라나설 채비를 했다."], ["Without appearing to observe what he was doing, I saw how carefully he adjusted the little room, put a candle ready and the means of lighting it, arranged the bed, and finally took out of a drawer one of her dresses (I remember to have seen her wear it), neatly folded with some other garments, and a bonnet, which he placed upon a chair.", "나는 그가 무얼 하는지 눈여겨보지 않는 척했지만, 그가 얼마나 정성스레 그 작은 방을 매만지는지를 보았다. 그는 초 한 자루와 불 붙일 도구를 챙겨 두고, 침대를 매만지고, 마지막으로 서랍에서 그녀의 옷 한 벌(그녀가 그 옷을 입은 것을 본 기억이 난다)을 다른 옷가지 몇 점과 함께 곱게 개어진 채로 꺼내고, 모자도 하나 꺼내어 의자 위에 놓아두었다."], ["He made no allusion to these clothes, neither did I.", "그는 이 옷들에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 나도 그랬다."], ["There they had been waiting for her, many and many a night, no doubt.", "그것들은 분명, 수없이 많은 밤 동안 그곳에서 그녀를 기다려 왔으리라."]], [["‘The time was, Mas’r Davy,’ he said, as we came downstairs, ‘when I thowt this girl, Martha, a’most like the dirt underneath my Em’ly’s feet.", "\"한때는요, 데이비 도련님,\" 우리가 계단을 내려오면서 그가 말했다. \"저는 이 마사라는 애를 우리 에밀리 발밑의 흙먼지나 진배없다고 여겼습니다요."], ["God forgive me, theer’s a difference now!’", "하느님, 용서하소서, 이제는 사정이 달라졌습니다요!\""]], [["As we went along, partly to hold him in conversation, and partly to satisfy myself, I asked him about Ham.", "길을 가는 동안, 한편으로는 그가 계속 말을 잇게 하려고, 한편으로는 내 마음을 좀 놓으려고, 나는 그에게 햄의 안부를 물었다."], ["He said, almost in the same words as formerly, that Ham was just the same, ‘wearing away his life with kiender no care nohow for ‘t; but never murmuring, and liked by all’.", "그는 예전과 거의 똑같은 말로, 햄은 그대로라고, \"제 한 몸 도무지 돌볼 생각도 없이 그렇게 목숨을 깎아 먹으면서도, 한 번도 투덜대는 법 없이 모두에게 사랑받는다\"고 대답했다."]], [["I asked him what he thought Ham’s state of mind was, in reference to the cause of their misfortunes?", "나는 그에게, 그들이 겪은 불행의 원인에 대해 햄이 어떤 심정일 것 같으냐고 물었다."], ["Whether he believed it was dangerous?", "그것이 위태롭다고 여기는지?"], ["What he supposed, for example, Ham would do, if he and Steerforth ever should encounter?", "이를테면, 햄이 스티어포스와 마주치기라도 하면 어찌할 것 같으냐고?"]], [["‘I doen’t know, sir,’ he replied.", "\"저도 모르겠습니다요, 도련님.\""], ["‘I have thowt of it oftentimes, but I can’t awize myself of it, no matters.’", "그가 대답했다. \"여러 번 생각해 봤습니다요만, 도무지 짐작이 가질 않습니다요, 아무리 해도요.\""]], [["I recalled to his remembrance the morning after her departure, when we were all three on the beach.", "나는 그녀가 떠난 이튿날 아침, 우리 셋이 모두 해변에 나가 있던 일을 그에게 일깨워 주었다."], ["‘Do you recollect,’ said I, ‘a certain wild way in which he looked out to sea, and spoke about “the end of it”?’", "\"기억하세요?\" 내가 말했다. \"그 애가 사나운 눈빛으로 바다를 내다보며 '그게 끝장날 날'에 대해 말하던 거요?\""]], [["‘Sure I do!’ said he.", "\"그럼요, 기억하고말고요!\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘What do you suppose he meant?’", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이었던 것 같으세요?\""]], [["‘Mas’r Davy,’ he replied, ‘I’ve put the question to myself a mort o’ times, and never found no answer.", "\"데이비 도련님,\" 그가 대답했다. \"저도 그 물음을 제 자신한테 숱하게 던져 봤습니다요만, 도무지 답을 찾지 못했습니다요."], ["And theer’s one curious thing--that, though he is so pleasant, I wouldn’t fare to feel comfortable to try and get his mind upon ‘t.", "그리고 한 가지 묘한 게 있는데요—그 애가 그토록 싹싹한데도, 저는 차마 그 애 속을 떠보려는 마음이 영 편치가 않습니다요."], ["He never said a wured to me as warn’t as dootiful as dootiful could be, and it ain’t likely as he’d begin to speak any other ways now; but it’s fur from being fleet water in his mind, where them thowts lays. It’s deep, sir, and I can’t see down.’", "그 애는 저한테 더없이 고분고분한 말 말고는 한 마디도 한 적이 없고, 이제 와 딴 식으로 말을 꺼낼 것 같지도 않습니다요만, 그 생각들이 가라앉아 있는 그 애 마음속은 결코 얕은 물이 아닙니다요. 깊어요, 도련님, 저는 그 바닥이 보이질 않습니다요.\""]], [["‘You are right,’ said I, ‘and that has sometimes made me anxious.’", "\"맞는 말씀입니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 가끔 저도 불안하게 만들더군요.\""]], [["‘And me too, Mas’r Davy,’ he rejoined.", "\"저도 그렇습니다요, 데이비 도련님.\""], ["‘Even more so, I do assure you, than his ventersome ways, though both belongs to the alteration in him.", "그가 대꾸했다. \"장담컨대, 그 애가 무모하게 구는 것보다 그게 더 그렇습니다요. 둘 다 그 애가 변한 탓이긴 하지만요."], ["I doen’t know as he’d do violence under any circumstances, but I hope as them two may be kep asunders.’", "그 애가 어떤 상황에서든 폭력을 휘두를 사람이라고는 생각지 않습니다요만, 그래도 저는 그 둘이 서로 떨어져 있게 되길 바랍니다요.\""]], [["We had come, through Temple Bar, into the city.", "우리는 템플 바를 지나 시내로 들어와 있었다."], ["Conversing no more now, and walking at my side, he yielded himself up to the one aim of his devoted life, and went on, with that hushed concentration of his faculties which would have made his figure solitary in a multitude.", "이제는 더 말을 나누지 않고, 그는 내 곁에서 걸으며 그 헌신적인 삶의 하나뿐인 목표에 온전히 자기 자신을 내맡긴 채, 무리 속에서도 그의 모습을 외따로 보이게 했을 그 고요한 정신의 집중으로 걸어갔다."], ["We were not far from Blackfriars Bridge, when he turned his head and pointed to a solitary female figure flitting along the opposite side of the street.", "우리가 블랙프라이어스 다리에서 멀지 않은 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그가 고개를 돌려 길 건너편을 스치듯 지나가는 외로운 여인의 모습을 가리켰다."], ["I knew it, readily, to be the figure that we sought.", "나는 그것이 우리가 찾던 바로 그 사람임을 단번에 알아보았다."]], [["We crossed the road, and were pressing on towards her, when it occurred to me that she might be more disposed to feel a woman’s interest in the lost girl, if we spoke to her in a quieter place, aloof from the crowd, and where we should be less observed.", "우리는 길을 건너 그녀를 향해 다가가고 있었는데, 문득 이런 생각이 들었다. 사람들에게서 떨어진 더 조용한 곳에서, 남의 눈에 덜 띌 만한 데서 말을 건넨다면, 그녀가 그 잃어버린 아이에게 같은 여자로서 더 마음을 쓰게 될지도 모른다는 것이었다."], ["I advised my companion, therefore, that we should not address her yet, but follow her; consulting in this, likewise, an indistinct desire I had, to know where she went.", "그래서 나는 동행에게 아직 그녀에게 말을 걸지 말고 따라가자고 일렀다. 여기에는 그녀가 어디로 가는지 알고 싶다는, 나 자신도 또렷이 알 수 없는 바람도 한몫했다."]], [["He acquiescing, we followed at a distance: never losing sight of her, but never caring to come very near, as she frequently looked about.", "그가 동의해서, 우리는 거리를 두고 뒤를 밟았다. 결코 그녀를 시야에서 놓치지 않으면서도, 그녀가 자주 두리번거리는 통에 너무 가까이 다가가지는 않으려 애썼다."], ["Once, she stopped to listen to a band of music; and then we stopped too.", "한번은 그녀가 악단의 연주를 들으려고 멈춰 섰고, 그러자 우리도 멈춰 섰다."]], [["She went on a long way.", "그녀는 한참을 걸어갔다."], ["Still we went on.", "우리도 계속 걸어갔다."], ["It was evident, from the manner in which she held her course, that she was going to some fixed destination; and this, and her keeping in the busy streets, and I suppose the strange fascination in the secrecy and mystery of so following anyone, made me adhere to my first purpose.", "그녀가 길을 잡아 가는 모양새로 보아, 어딘가 정해진 목적지로 향하고 있는 것이 분명했다. 이것과, 그녀가 번잡한 거리를 벗어나지 않는다는 것, 그리고 짐작건대 누군가를 그렇게 몰래 뒤쫓는 일에 깃든 야릇한 매혹이 어우러져, 나는 처음의 작정을 그대로 밀고 나갔다."], ["At length she turned into a dull, dark street, where the noise and crowd were lost; and I said, ‘We may speak to her now’; and, mending our pace, we went after her.", "마침내 그녀는 소음과 인파가 사라진 칙칙하고 어두운 거리로 접어들었다. 그래서 나는 \"이제 말을 걸어도 되겠어요\" 하고 말했고, 우리는 걸음을 다그쳐 그녀의 뒤를 쫓았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 47. MARTHA", "# CHAPTER 47. MARTHA"]], [["We were now down in Westminster.", "우리는 이제 웨스트민스터까지 내려와 있었다."], ["We had turned back to follow her, having encountered her coming towards us; and Westminster Abbey was the point at which she passed from the lights and noise of the leading streets.", "그녀가 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 것과 마주친 터라, 우리는 발길을 돌려 뒤를 밟았다. 웨스트민스터 사원은 그녀가 큰길의 불빛과 소음에서 벗어나는 지점이었다."], ["She proceeded so quickly, when she got free of the two currents of passengers setting towards and from the bridge, that, between this and the advance she had of us when she struck off, we were in the narrow water-side street by Millbank before we came up with her.", "그녀는 다리 쪽으로 향하는 인파와 다리에서 빠져나오는 인파, 그 두 물결에서 풀려나자 어찌나 빨리 걸음을 옮기던지, 갈림길에서 방향을 틀며 벌어진 거리까지 더해져, 우리가 그녀를 따라잡았을 무렵에는 이미 밀뱅크 옆 좁은 강가 골목에 들어서 있었다."], ["At that moment she crossed the road, as if to avoid the footsteps that she heard so close behind; and, without looking back, passed on even more rapidly.", "바로 그 순간, 그녀는 등 뒤로 바짝 따라붙는 발소리를 피하려는 듯 길을 가로질렀고, 뒤도 돌아보지 않은 채 한층 더 빠르게 걸어갔다."]], [["A glimpse of the river through a dull gateway, where some waggons were housed for the night, seemed to arrest my feet.", "수레 몇 대가 밤을 나려고 들어와 있던 칙칙한 출입문 너머로 강물이 언뜻 비치자, 내 발은 그 자리에 멈춰 서는 것 같았다."], ["I touched my companion without speaking, and we both forbore to cross after her, and both followed on that opposite side of the way; keeping as quietly as we could in the shadow of the houses, but keeping very near her.", "나는 말없이 동행을 건드렸고, 우리 둘 다 그녀를 따라 길을 건너지 않은 채, 길 맞은편을 따라 걸었다. 집들의 그늘 속에 최대한 조용히 몸을 숨기면서도, 그녀와의 거리는 아주 바짝 좁히고서."]], [["There was, and is when I write, at the end of that low-lying street, a dilapidated little wooden building, probably an obsolete old ferry-house. Its position is just at that point where the street ceases, and the road begins to lie between a row of houses and the river.", "내가 이 글을 쓰는 지금도 그렇지만, 그 낮게 깔린 골목 끝에는 다 허물어져 가는 작은 나무 건물이 하나 있었는데, 아마도 더는 쓰지 않는 낡은 나루터집이었을 것이다. 그 위치는 바로 골목이 끝나고, 길이 늘어선 집들과 강 사이로 뻗기 시작하는 지점이었다."], ["As soon as she came here, and saw the water, she stopped as if she had come to her destination; and presently went slowly along by the brink of the river, looking intently at it.", "그녀는 이곳에 다다라 물을 보자마자, 목적지에 도착하기라도 한 듯 멈춰 섰다. 그러더니 이내 강가를 따라 천천히 걸으며 물을 뚫어지게 들여다보았다."]], [["All the way here, I had supposed that she was going to some house; indeed, I had vaguely entertained the hope that the house might be in some way associated with the lost girl.", "여기까지 오는 내내, 나는 그녀가 어느 집으로 향하는 줄로만 알았다. 사실, 그 집이 어떤 식으로든 잃어버린 그 아이와 이어져 있을지 모른다는 막연한 희망까지 품고 있었다."], ["But that one dark glimpse of the river, through the gateway, had instinctively prepared me for her going no farther.", "그러나 출입문 너머로 어둑하게 한 번 비친 그 강물이, 그녀가 더는 가지 않으리라는 것을 본능적으로 일러 주었다."]], [["The neighbourhood was a dreary one at that time; as oppressive, sad, and solitary by night, as any about London.", "그 일대는 그 무렵 황량하기 그지없었다. 밤이면 런던 어느 동네 못지않게 짓누르듯 답답하고, 슬프고, 외로운 곳이었다."], ["There were neither wharves nor houses on the melancholy waste of road near the great blank Prison.", "텅 빈 거대한 감옥 가까이로 음울하게 버려진 길에는 부두도 집도 없었다."], ["A sluggish ditch deposited its mud at the prison walls.", "느릿한 도랑은 감옥 담벼락에다 진흙을 게워 냈다."], ["Coarse grass and rank weeds straggled over all the marshy land in the vicinity.", "거친 풀과 무성한 잡초가 그 부근의 질척한 땅을 온통 뒤덮으며 어지러이 뻗어 있었다."], ["In one part, carcases of houses, inauspiciously begun and never finished, rotted away.", "한쪽에서는, 불길하게 짓기 시작했다가 끝내 완성되지 못한 집들의 잔해가 썩어 가고 있었다."], ["In another, the ground was cumbered with rusty iron monsters of steam-boilers, wheels, cranks, pipes, furnaces, paddles, anchors, diving-bells, windmill-sails, and I know not what strange objects, accumulated by some speculator, and grovelling in the dust, underneath which--having sunk into the soil of their own weight in wet weather--they had the appearance of vainly trying to hide themselves.", "또 다른 쪽에서는, 어느 투기꾼이 긁어모아 쌓아 놓은 녹슨 쇠붙이 괴물들 — 증기 보일러, 바퀴, 크랭크, 파이프, 화로, 외륜, 닻, 잠수종, 풍차 날개, 그 밖에 내가 무엇인지도 모를 온갖 기괴한 물건들 — 이 땅을 어지럽히며 흙먼지 속에 나뒹굴고 있었는데, 비 오는 날이면 제 무게로 흙 속에 가라앉아, 마치 헛되이 제 몸을 감추려는 듯한 꼴이었다."], ["The clash and glare of sundry fiery Works upon the river-side, arose by night to disturb everything except the heavy and unbroken smoke that poured out of their chimneys.", "강가에 늘어선 갖가지 불 뿜는 공장들의 쨍그랑 소리와 번쩍이는 불빛은, 밤이면 굴뚝에서 쏟아져 나오는 무겁고 끊이지 않는 연기만 빼고 모든 것을 어지럽혔다."], ["Slimy gaps and causeways, winding among old wooden piles, with a sickly substance clinging to the latter, like green hair, and the rags of last year’s handbills offering rewards for drowned men fluttering above high-water mark, led down through the ooze and slush to the ebb-tide.", "미끈거리는 빈틈과 둑길이 낡은 나무 말뚝 사이로 굽이쳤는데, 그 말뚝에는 초록빛 머리카락 같은 병든 물질이 들러붙어 있었고, 물에 빠져 죽은 사람을 찾는다고 현상금을 내건 작년 전단의 너덜너덜한 조각들이 만조선 위에서 펄럭였다. 그 길은 진창과 질척한 수렁을 지나 썰물 진 물가로 내려갔다."], ["There was a story that one of the pits dug for the dead in the time of the Great Plague was hereabout; and a blighting influence seemed to have proceeded from it over the whole place.", "대역병이 돌던 시절 죽은 자들을 묻으려고 파 놓은 구덩이 가운데 하나가 이 근처에 있었다는 이야기가 전해 왔다. 그리고 그곳에서부터 무언가 시들게 하는 기운이 온 동네로 퍼져 나온 듯했다."], ["Or else it looked as if it had gradually decomposed into that nightmare condition, out of the overflowings of the polluted stream.", "아니면, 더럽혀진 강물이 넘쳐흘러, 이 일대가 차츰 그런 악몽 같은 꼴로 썩어 들어간 것처럼 보이기도 했다."]], [["As if she were a part of the refuse it had cast out, and left to corruption and decay, the girl we had followed strayed down to the river’s brink, and stood in the midst of this night-picture, lonely and still, looking at the water.", "마치 그 강이 토해 내어 썩고 문드러지도록 내버린 쓰레기의 한 조각이기라도 하듯, 우리가 뒤쫓아 온 그 아이는 강가로 비척비척 내려가, 이 밤 풍경 한복판에 외롭고도 가만히 선 채 물을 바라보았다."]], [["There were some boats and barges astrand in the mud, and these enabled us to come within a few yards of her without being seen.", "진흙에 좌초된 보트와 거룻배 몇 척이 있었던 덕분에, 우리는 들키지 않고 그녀에게서 몇 야드 안까지 다가갈 수 있었다."], ["I then signed to Mr. Peggotty to remain where he was, and emerged from their shade to speak to her.", "나는 패거티 씨에게 그 자리에 그대로 있으라고 손짓하고, 그늘에서 빠져나와 그녀에게 말을 걸었다."], ["I did not approach her solitary figure without trembling; for this gloomy end to her determined walk, and the way in which she stood, almost within the cavernous shadow of the iron bridge, looking at the lights crookedly reflected in the strong tide, inspired a dread within me.", "나는 그 외로운 모습에 다가가면서 떨지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 작정하고 걸어온 길의 그 음울한 끝, 그리고 쇠다리의 동굴 같은 그늘 거의 안쪽에 서서, 거센 물살에 비뚜름하게 비친 불빛을 바라보던 그 모습이, 내 안에 두려움을 불러일으켰기 때문이다."]], [["I think she was talking to herself. I am sure, although absorbed in gazing at the water, that her shawl was off her shoulders, and that she was muffling her hands in it, in an unsettled and bewildered way, more like the action of a sleep-walker than a waking person.", "내 생각에 그녀는 혼잣말을 하고 있었던 것 같다. 물을 들여다보는 데 온통 정신이 팔려 있었으나, 분명 어깨에서 숄이 흘러내린 채였고, 그 숄로 두 손을 어수선하고 얼떨떨하게 감싸고 있었는데, 깨어 있는 사람이라기보다 몽유병 환자의 몸짓에 가까웠다."], ["I know, and never can forget, that there was that in her wild manner which gave me no assurance but that she would sink before my eyes, until I had her arm within my grasp.", "나는 안다, 그리고 결코 잊지 못한다 — 내가 그녀의 팔을 움켜잡기 전까지, 그 사나운 몸짓에는 그녀가 바로 내 눈앞에서 물속으로 가라앉지 않으리라는 보장이 조금도 없었다는 것을."]], [["At the same moment I said ‘Martha!’", "바로 그 순간 나는 \"마사!\" 하고 불렀다."]], [["She uttered a terrified scream, and struggled with me with such strength that I doubt if I could have held her alone.", "그녀는 겁에 질려 비명을 지르며, 어찌나 거센 힘으로 나에게 맞서던지, 나 혼자였다면 과연 붙잡을 수 있었을지 의심스러울 정도였다."], ["But a stronger hand than mine was laid upon her; and when she raised her frightened eyes and saw whose it was, she made but one more effort and dropped down between us.", "그러나 내 손보다 더 센 손이 그녀에게 얹혔다. 그녀가 겁먹은 눈을 들어 그것이 누구의 손인지 알아본 순간, 그녀는 단 한 번 더 몸부림치다가 우리 둘 사이로 풀썩 주저앉았다."], ["We carried her away from the water to where there were some dry stones, and there laid her down, crying and moaning.", "우리는 그녀를 물가에서 마른 돌이 있는 데로 옮겨, 거기 눕혔는데, 그녀는 울며 신음했다."], ["In a little while she sat among the stones, holding her wretched head with both her hands.", "잠시 후 그녀는 두 손으로 비참한 머리를 감싼 채 돌 틈에 주저앉았다."]], [["‘Oh, the river!’ she cried passionately.", "\"아, 강물!\" 그녀가 격렬하게 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh, the river!’", "\"아, 저 강물!\""]], [["‘Hush, hush!’ said I.", "\"쉿, 쉿!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Calm yourself.’", "\"마음을 가라앉혀요.\""]], [["But she still repeated the same words, continually exclaiming, ‘Oh, the river!’ over and over again.", "그러나 그녀는 여전히 같은 말만 되풀이하며, \"아, 저 강물!\"이라고 자꾸만 외쳐 댔다."]], [["‘I know it’s like me!’ she exclaimed.", "\"저게 나 같다는 걸 알아요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["‘I know that I belong to it.", "\"내가 저기에 속한다는 걸 알아요."], ["I know that it’s the natural company of such as I am!", "저게 나 같은 것들의 마땅한 짝이라는 걸 안다고요!"], ["It comes from country places, where there was once no harm in it--and it creeps through the dismal streets, defiled and miserable--and it goes away, like my life, to a great sea, that is always troubled--and I feel that I must go with it!’ I have never known what despair was, except in the tone of those words.", "저것도 시골에서 흘러나와요, 한때는 아무런 해도 없던 곳에서 — 그러다 음침한 거리를 기어 다니며, 더럽혀지고 비참해지고 — 그러다 내 인생처럼, 늘 뒤숭숭한 큰 바다로 흘러가요 — 그래서 나도 저것과 함께 가야만 한다는 느낌이 들어요!\" 나는 그 말투에서만큼 절망이라는 것이 무엇인지를 절절히 안 적이 없다."]], [["‘I can’t keep away from it.", "\"저것에서 벗어날 수가 없어요."], ["I can’t forget it.", "잊을 수가 없어요."], ["It haunts me day and night.", "밤낮으로 나를 따라다녀요."], ["It’s the only thing in all the world that I am fit for, or that’s fit for me.", "이 세상에서 내가 어울릴 만한 단 하나, 또는 나에게 어울리는 단 하나예요."], ["Oh, the dreadful river!’", "아, 저 무서운 강물!\""]], [["The thought passed through my mind that in the face of my companion, as he looked upon her without speech or motion, I might have read his niece’s history, if I had known nothing of it.", "말없이 미동도 없이 그녀를 바라보는 내 동행의 얼굴에서, 만약 내가 그 조카딸의 사연을 아무것도 몰랐더라도 그 사연을 능히 읽어 낼 수 있었으리라는 생각이 내 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["I never saw, in any painting or reality, horror and compassion so impressively blended.", "나는 그림에서든 현실에서든, 공포와 연민이 그토록 인상 깊게 뒤섞인 모습을 본 적이 없었다."], ["He shook as if he would have fallen; and his hand--I touched it with my own, for his appearance alarmed me--was deadly cold.", "그는 곧 쓰러질 듯 떨었다. 그리고 그의 손은 — 그 모습이 걱정스러워 내 손으로 만져 보았는데 — 죽은 사람처럼 차가웠다."]], [["‘She is in a state of frenzy,’ I whispered to him.", "\"지금 제정신이 아니에요.\" 나는 그에게 속삭였다."], ["‘She will speak differently in a little time.’", "\"조금만 지나면 다른 말을 할 거예요.\""]], [["I don’t know what he would have said in answer.", "그가 무어라 대답하려 했는지는 모르겠다."], ["He made some motion with his mouth, and seemed to think he had spoken; but he had only pointed to her with his outstretched hand.", "그는 입을 무어라 달싹이며, 자기가 말을 한 줄로 여기는 듯했으나, 실은 뻗은 손으로 그녀를 가리켰을 뿐이었다."]], [["A new burst of crying came upon her now, in which she once more hid her face among the stones, and lay before us, a prostrate image of humiliation and ruin.", "이제 그녀에게 또 한 차례 울음이 터져 나왔고, 그녀는 다시 한번 돌 틈에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 굴욕과 파멸의 엎드린 형상으로 우리 앞에 누웠다."], ["Knowing that this state must pass, before we could speak to her with any hope, I ventured to restrain him when he would have raised her, and we stood by in silence until she became more tranquil.", "이 상태가 지나가기 전에는 어떤 희망을 품고 그녀에게 말을 건넬 수 없음을 알기에, 나는 그가 그녀를 일으키려 할 때 감히 그를 말렸고, 우리는 그녀가 좀 더 잠잠해질 때까지 말없이 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["‘Martha,’ said I then, leaning down, and helping her to rise--she seemed to want to rise as if with the intention of going away, but she was weak, and leaned against a boat.", "\"마사.\" 그러고 나서 나는 몸을 숙여 그녀가 일어서도록 도우며 말했다. 그녀는 어디론가 가려는 듯 일어서고 싶어 하는 것 같았으나, 몸이 약해서 보트에 기댔다."], ["‘Do you know who this is, who is with me?’", "\"지금 나와 함께 있는 이분이 누구인지 알겠어요?\""]], [["She said faintly, ‘Yes.’", "그녀는 가냘프게 \"네.\" 하고 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you know that we have followed you a long way tonight?’", "\"오늘 밤 우리가 먼 길을 당신 뒤를 밟아 왔다는 걸 알아요?\""]], [["She shook her head.", "그녀는 고개를 저었다."], ["She looked neither at him nor at me, but stood in a humble attitude, holding her bonnet and shawl in one hand, without appearing conscious of them, and pressing the other, clenched, against her forehead.", "그녀는 그도 나도 보지 않고, 겸허한 자세로 서서, 한 손에는 보닛과 숄을 쥐었으되 그것들을 의식하지도 못하는 듯했고, 다른 한 손은 주먹을 쥐어 이마를 짓누르고 있었다."]], [["‘Are you composed enough,’ said I, ‘to speak on the subject which so interested you--I hope Heaven may remember it!--that snowy night?’", "\"당신이 그토록 마음을 쓰던 그 일에 대해 — 부디 하늘이 그것을 기억해 주기를! — 그 눈 내리던 밤의 그 일에 대해 이야기를 나눌 만큼 마음이 가라앉았나요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["Her sobs broke out afresh, and she murmured some inarticulate thanks to me for not having driven her away from the door.", "그녀는 다시 흐느끼기 시작하더니, 자기를 문 앞에서 내쫓지 않은 데 대해 나에게 무어라 알아들을 수 없는 감사의 말을 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘I want to say nothing for myself,’ she said, after a few moments.", "\"제 자신을 위해서는 아무 말도 하고 싶지 않아요.\" 잠시 후 그녀가 말했다. \"전 나쁜 년이에요, 전 버려진 몸이고요."], ["‘I am bad, I am lost.", "저에겐 아무 희망도 없어요."], ["I have no hope at all.", "하지만 저분께 전해 주세요, 선생님.\" 그녀는 그에게서 움츠러들며 물러나 있었다."], ["But tell him, sir,’ she had shrunk away from him, ‘if you don’t feel too hard to me to do it, that I never was in any way the cause of his misfortune.’ ‘It has never been attributed to you,’ I returned, earnestly responding to her earnestness.", "\"저를 너무 모질게 여기시지 않는다면요, 제가 어떤 식으로도 저분의 불행의 원인이 된 적은 결코 없다고요.\" \"그건 한 번도 당신 탓으로 돌려진 적이 없어요.\" 나는 그녀의 간절함에 진심으로 응답하며 대답했다."]], [["‘It was you, if I don’t deceive myself,’ she said, in a broken voice, ‘that came into the kitchen, the night she took such pity on me; was so gentle to me; didn’t shrink away from me like all the rest, and gave me such kind help!", "\"제가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그 애가 저를 그토록 가엾이 여겨 주던 그날 밤 부엌으로 들어오셨던 분이 바로 선생님이었지요.\" 그녀가 갈라진 목소리로 말했다. \"그 애는 제게 어찌나 다정했던지요."], ["Was it you, sir?’", "남들처럼 저를 보고 움츠러들지도 않고, 그토록 친절히 도와줬어요! 그게 선생님이셨나요?\""]], [["‘It was,’ said I.", "\"그래요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I should have been in the river long ago,’ she said, glancing at it with a terrible expression, ‘if any wrong to her had been upon my mind.", "\"그 애에게 제가 무슨 잘못이라도 저질렀다는 마음의 짐이 있었다면,\" 그녀는 무시무시한 표정으로 강물을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"전 진작에 저 강물 속에 있었을 거예요."], ["I never could have kept out of it a single winter’s night, if I had not been free of any share in that!’", "거기에 제가 한 자락이라도 끼어 있었다면, 단 하룻밤 겨울밤도 강물에서 멀리 떨어져 견디지 못했을 거예요!\""]], [["‘The cause of her flight is too well understood,’ I said.", "\"그 애가 달아난 까닭은 너무도 잘 알려져 있어요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘You are innocent of any part in it, we thoroughly believe,--we know.’", "\"당신은 거기에 아무런 잘못이 없다고, 우린 진심으로 믿어요 — 우린 알아요.\""]], [["‘Oh, I might have been much the better for her, if I had had a better heart!’ exclaimed the girl, with most forlorn regret; ‘for she was always good to me!", "\"아, 제 마음이 조금만 더 착했더라면, 전 그 애에게 훨씬 더 좋은 사람이 될 수도 있었을 텐데!\" 그녀가 더없이 처량한 회한에 잠겨 외쳤다. \"그 애는 늘 제게 잘해 줬으니까요!"], ["She never spoke a word to me but what was pleasant and right.", "그 애는 저에게 즐겁고 옳은 말이 아니면 단 한마디도 한 적이 없어요."], ["Is it likely I would try to make her what I am myself, knowing what I am myself, so well?", "제가 어떤 인간인지 그토록 잘 알면서, 제가 그 애를 저 같은 인간으로 만들려고 들었을 리가 있겠어요?"], ["When I lost everything that makes life dear, the worst of all my thoughts was that I was parted for ever from her!’", "인생을 값지게 해 주는 모든 것을 잃었을 때, 제 온갖 생각 가운데 가장 끔찍한 것은 제가 그 애와 영영 갈라섰다는 거였어요!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty, standing with one hand on the gunwale of the boat, and his eyes cast down, put his disengaged hand before his face.", "패거티 씨는 한 손을 보트 뱃전에 얹고 눈을 내리깐 채 서 있다가, 비어 있던 다른 손을 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔다."]], [["‘And when I heard what had happened before that snowy night, from some belonging to our town,’ cried Martha, ‘the bitterest thought in all my mind was, that the people would remember she once kept company with me, and would say I had corrupted her!", "\"그리고 그 눈 내리던 밤보다 앞서 무슨 일이 있었는지를 우리 고장 사람한테서 들었을 때,\" 마사가 외쳤다. \"제 마음에 가장 쓰라린 생각은, 사람들이 그 애가 한때 저와 어울려 다녔던 걸 기억해 내고는, 제가 그 애를 타락시켰다고 떠들어 댈 거라는 거였어요!"], ["When, Heaven knows, I would have died to have brought back her good name!’", "하늘이 알아요, 전 그 애의 깨끗한 이름을 되돌릴 수만 있다면 죽기라도 했을 거예요!\""]], [["Long unused to any self-control, the piercing agony of her remorse and grief was terrible.", "오랫동안 자제라는 것을 해 본 적이 없는 터라, 회한과 비탄의 그 찌르는 듯한 고뇌는 보기에 처참했다."]], [["‘To have died, would not have been much--what can I say?---I would have lived!’ she cried.", "\"죽는 거야 별것도 아니었겠죠 — 뭐라고 해야 할까요? — 전 살아 냈을 거예요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["‘I would have lived to be old, in the wretched streets--and to wander about, avoided, in the dark--and to see the day break on the ghastly line of houses, and remember how the same sun used to shine into my room, and wake me once--I would have done even that, to save her!’", "\"전 그 비참한 거리에서 늙도록 살아 냈을 거예요 — 어둠 속에서 사람들의 외면을 받으며 떠돌고 — 그 으스스하게 늘어선 집들 위로 동이 트는 걸 보면서, 한때 그 똑같은 해가 내 방으로 비쳐 들어 나를 깨워 주던 걸 떠올리며 — 전 그 애를 구할 수만 있다면 그런 일조차 했을 거예요!\""]], [["Sinking on the stones, she took some in each hand, and clenched them up, as if she would have ground them.", "그녀는 돌 위로 주저앉아, 양손에 돌을 한 줌씩 움켜쥐고는, 그것을 갈아 으스러뜨리기라도 할 듯 꽉 쥐었다."], ["She writhed into some new posture constantly: stiffening her arms, twisting them before her face, as though to shut out from her eyes the little light there was, and drooping her head, as if it were heavy with insupportable recollections.", "그녀는 끊임없이 새로운 자세로 몸을 비틀었다. 두 팔을 뻣뻣하게 세우고, 그것을 얼굴 앞에서 배배 꼬았는데, 마치 그나마 거기 있는 작은 빛마저 두 눈에서 가리려는 듯했고, 머리를 떨구었는데, 마치 견딜 수 없는 기억으로 그것이 무겁기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["‘What shall I ever do!’ she said, fighting thus with her despair.", "\"전 대체 어떻게 해야 할까요!\" 그녀가 그렇게 절망과 싸우며 말했다."], ["‘How can I go on as I am, a solitary curse to myself, a living disgrace to everyone I come near!’ Suddenly she turned to my companion.", "\"이대로 어떻게 살아가나요, 제 자신에게는 외로운 저주이고, 제가 가까이 가는 모두에게는 살아 있는 치욕인데요!\" 갑자기 그녀가 내 동행에게 돌아섰다."], ["‘Stamp upon me, kill me!", "\"저를 짓밟으세요, 저를 죽이세요!"], ["When she was your pride, you would have thought I had done her harm if I had brushed against her in the street.", "그 애가 당신의 자랑이던 시절엔, 제가 길에서 그 애를 스치기만 했어도 그 애를 더럽혔다고 여기셨을 거예요."], ["You can’t believe--why should you?---a syllable that comes out of my lips.", "당신은 제 입에서 나오는 단 한마디도 믿지 못하시겠죠 — 어찌 믿으시겠어요?"], ["It would be a burning shame upon you, even now, if she and I exchanged a word.", "지금이라도 그 애와 제가 말 한마디라도 주고받는다면, 그건 당신에게 불타는 수치일 거예요."], ["I don’t complain.", "전 불평하지 않아요."], ["I don’t say she and I are alike--I know there is a long, long way between us.", "그 애와 제가 같다고 말하는 것도 아니에요 — 우리 사이엔 멀고도 먼 거리가 있다는 걸 알아요."], ["I only say, with all my guilt and wretchedness upon my head, that I am grateful to her from my soul, and love her.", "다만 이 한 가지, 제 머리 위에 온갖 죄와 비참함을 다 인 채로 말할게요, 전 영혼을 다해 그 애에게 고마워하고, 그 애를 사랑해요."], ["Oh, don’t think that all the power I had of loving anything is quite worn out!", "아, 무언가를 사랑할 제 능력이 죄다 닳아 없어졌다고는 여기지 말아 주세요! 온 세상이 그러듯 저를 내던지세요."], ["Throw me away, as all the world does. Kill me for being what I am, and having ever known her; but don’t think that of me!’", "제가 이런 인간이라는 것 때문에, 한때 그 애를 알았다는 것 때문에 저를 죽이세요. 하지만 저에 대해 그런 생각만은 하지 말아 주세요!\""]], [["He looked upon her, while she made this supplication, in a wild distracted manner; and, when she was silent, gently raised her.", "그녀가 이렇게 애원하는 동안, 그는 사납고 넋 나간 모습으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그리고 그녀가 입을 다물자, 그는 부드럽게 그녀를 일으켜 세웠다."]], [["‘Martha,’ said Mr.", "\"마사,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘God forbid as I should judge you.", "\"내가 너를 심판하다니 하늘이 막을 일이지."], ["Forbid as I, of all men, should do that, my girl!", "다른 사람도 아닌 이 내가 그런 짓을 하다니 하늘이 막을 일이고말고, 얘야!"], ["You doen’t know half the change that’s come, in course of time, upon me, when you think it likely.", "네가 그럴 법하다고 여기지만, 시간이 흐르는 동안 나한테 닥친 변화가 어떤 건지 넌 그 절반도 몰라. 그래!\""], ["Well!’ he paused a moment, then went on.", "그는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 다시 이었다."], ["‘You doen’t understand how ‘tis that this here gentleman and me has wished to speak to you.", "\"여기 이 신사 양반하고 내가 너한테 말을 걸고 싶어 한 게 어찌 된 영문인지 넌 모를 게다."], ["You doen’t understand what ‘tis we has afore us. Listen now!’", "우리 앞에 무엇이 놓여 있는지 넌 모를 게야. 자, 들어 봐라!\""]], [["His influence upon her was complete.", "그가 그녀에게 미치는 영향력은 완벽했다."], ["She stood, shrinkingly, before him, as if she were afraid to meet his eyes; but her passionate sorrow was quite hushed and mute.", "그녀는 그의 눈을 마주하기가 두렵기라도 한 듯 움츠러든 채 그 앞에 섰다. 그러나 그 격정에 찬 슬픔만은 아주 잠잠해지고 입을 다물었다."]], [["‘If you heerd,’ said Mr.", "\"네가,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘owt of what passed between Mas’r Davy and me, th’ night when it snew so hard, you know as I have been--wheer not--fur to seek my dear niece.", "\"그 눈이 몹시 퍼붓던 밤, 데이비 도련님하고 나 사이에 오간 얘기를 조금이라도 들었다면, 내가 그동안 — 안 가 본 데 없이 — 내 사랑하는 조카딸을 찾아다녔다는 걸 알 게다."], ["My dear niece,’ he repeated steadily.", "내 사랑하는 조카딸 말이다.\" 그가 굳건하게 되풀이했다."], ["‘Fur she’s more dear to me now, Martha, than she was dear afore.’", "\"전보다 지금이 그 애가 나한테 더 소중하니까 말이다, 마사.\""]], [["She put her hands before her face; but otherwise remained quiet.", "그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 그러나 그 밖에는 가만히 있었다."]], [["‘I have heerd her tell,’ said Mr.", "\"내가 그 애한테 들었다.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘as you was early left fatherless and motherless, with no friend fur to take, in a rough seafaring-way, their place.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"네가 일찍이 아비도 어미도 잃고, 거친 뱃사람 식으로나마 그 빈자리를 채워 줄 벗 하나 없었다고 말이다."], ["Maybe you can guess that if you’d had such a friend, you’d have got into a way of being fond of him in course of time, and that my niece was kiender daughter-like to me.’", "너한테 그런 벗이 하나 있었더라면, 시간이 흐르면서 그 사람을 따르게 됐으리라는 걸 너도 짐작할 게다. 그리고 내 조카딸은 나한테 딸이나 다름없었다는 것도.\""]], [["As she was silently trembling, he put her shawl carefully about her, taking it up from the ground for that purpose.", "그녀가 말없이 떨고 있자, 그는 바닥에서 숄을 집어 들어 정성스레 그녀의 어깨에 둘러 주었다."]], [["‘Whereby,’ said he, ‘I know, both as she would go to the wureld’s furdest end with me, if she could once see me again; and that she would fly to the wureld’s furdest end to keep off seeing me. For though she ain’t no call to doubt my love, and doen’t--and doen’t,’ he repeated, with a quiet assurance of the truth of what he said, ‘there’s shame steps in, and keeps betwixt us.’", "\"그러니,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 안다 — 그 애가 나를 다시 한번 볼 수만 있다면 나하고 함께 이 세상 끝까지라도 가리라는 것을. 그리고 나를 보지 않으려고 이 세상 끝까지라도 달아나리라는 것도. 그 애가 내 사랑을 의심할 까닭이야 없고, 의심하지도 않지만 — 의심하지 않지만,\" 그는 자기 말이 옳다는 조용한 확신으로 되풀이했다. \"수치심이 끼어들어, 우리 사이를 가로막고 있으니 말이다.\""]], [["I read, in every word of his plain impressive way of delivering himself, new evidence of his having thought of this one topic, in every feature it presented.", "나는 그가 자신을 풀어내는 그 꾸밈없고 묵직한 말 한마디 한마디에서, 그가 이 한 가지 주제를, 그것이 드러내는 온갖 면면을 빠짐없이 곱씹어 왔다는 새로운 증거를 읽었다."]], [["‘According to our reckoning,’ he proceeded, ‘Mas’r Davy’s here, and mine, she is like, one day, to make her own poor solitary course to London.", "\"우리 짐작으로는,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"여기 데이비 도련님하고 내 짐작으로는, 그 애가 언젠가 외롭고 가엾은 제 발길을 런던으로 향할 것 같다."], ["We believe--Mas’r Davy, me, and all of us--that you are as innocent of everything that has befell her, as the unborn child.", "우린 믿는다 — 데이비 도련님도, 나도, 우리 모두가 — 네가 그 애한테 닥친 모든 일에 대해, 아직 태어나지 않은 아기만큼이나 아무 죄가 없다는 걸."], ["You’ve spoke of her being pleasant, kind, and gentle to you. Bless her, I knew she was!", "너는 그 애가 너한테 다정하고, 친절하고, 상냥했다고 말했지. 그 애에게 복을 빈다, 난 그 애가 그런 줄 알고 있었어!"], ["I knew she always was, to all.", "그 애가 누구에게나 늘 그랬다는 걸 난 알고 있었다."], ["You’re thankful to her, and you love her.", "너는 그 애한테 고마워하고, 그 애를 사랑하지."], ["Help us all you can to find her, and may Heaven reward you!’", "그 애를 찾도록 우리를 힘껏 도와다오, 그러면 하늘이 너에게 갚아 주실 게다!\""]], [["She looked at him hastily, and for the first time, as if she were doubtful of what he had said.", "그녀는 그가 한 말을 미심쩍어하기라도 하는 듯, 황급히, 그리고 처음으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Will you trust me?’ she asked, in a low voice of astonishment.", "\"저를 믿어 주신다고요?\" 그녀가 놀라움에 잠긴 낮은 목소리로 물었다."]], [["‘Full and free!’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"온전히, 거리낌 없이!\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘To speak to her, if I should ever find her; shelter her, if I have any shelter to divide with her; and then, without her knowledge, come to you, and bring you to her?’ she asked hurriedly.", "\"제가 그 애를 찾게 되거든 말을 걸고, 제게 나눠 줄 거처가 조금이라도 있거든 그 애를 거두고, 그런 다음 그 애 모르게 당신께 와서 당신을 그 애에게 데려다드리라는 거지요?\" 그녀가 다급히 물었다."]], [["We both replied together, ‘Yes!’", "우리는 함께 대답했다. \"그래요!\""]], [["She lifted up her eyes, and solemnly declared that she would devote herself to this task, fervently and faithfully.", "그녀는 눈을 들어, 이 일에 자신을 바치겠노라고, 뜨겁고 충실하게 그리하겠노라고 엄숙히 다짐했다."], ["That she would never waver in it, never be diverted from it, never relinquish it, while there was any chance of hope.", "한 가닥 희망이라도 남아 있는 한, 결코 흔들리지 않고, 결코 곁길로 새지 않고, 결코 손을 놓지 않겠노라고."], ["If she were not true to it, might the object she now had in life, which bound her to something devoid of evil, in its passing away from her, leave her more forlorn and more despairing, if that were possible, than she had been upon the river’s brink that night; and then might all help, human and Divine, renounce her evermore!", "만약 그 다짐에 충실하지 못한다면, 지금 자기를 악이 깃들지 않은 무언가에 붙들어 매 주는 이 삶의 목표가 자기에게서 떠나갈 때, 자기를 그날 밤 강가에 섰을 때보다 — 그게 가능하다면 — 더 처량하고 더 절망스럽게 내버려 두기를. 그리하여 사람의 도움이든 하늘의 도움이든 모든 도움이 자기를 영영 저버리기를."]], [["She did not raise her voice above her breath, or address us, but said this to the night sky; then stood profoundly quiet, looking at the gloomy water.", "그녀는 숨소리보다 높게 목소리를 올리지도 않았고, 우리에게 말을 건네지도 않았으며, 이 말을 밤하늘을 향해 했다. 그러고는 음울한 강물을 바라보며 더없이 고요하게 서 있었다."]], [["We judged it expedient, now, to tell her all we knew; which I recounted at length.", "우리는 이제 우리가 아는 것을 모두 그녀에게 말해 주는 것이 좋겠다고 판단했고, 나는 그것을 길게 들려주었다."], ["She listened with great attention, and with a face that often changed, but had the same purpose in all its varying expressions.", "그녀는 잔뜩 귀를 기울여 들었는데, 얼굴은 자주 바뀌었으되, 그 온갖 변하는 표정 속에는 한결같은 결의가 깃들어 있었다."], ["Her eyes occasionally filled with tears, but those she repressed.", "그녀의 두 눈에는 이따금 눈물이 차올랐으나, 그 눈물을 억눌렀다."], ["It seemed as if her spirit were quite altered, and she could not be too quiet.", "마치 그 영혼이 송두리째 달라지기라도 한 듯, 그녀는 아무리 차분해도 지나치지 않다는 듯했다."]], [["She asked, when all was told, where we were to be communicated with, if occasion should arise.", "이야기가 다 끝나자 그녀는, 만일 일이 생기면 우리와 어디로 연락하면 되느냐고 물었다."], ["Under a dull lamp in the road, I wrote our two addresses on a leaf of my pocket-book, which I tore out and gave to her, and which she put in her poor bosom.", "길가 흐릿한 등불 아래에서, 나는 수첩 한 장에 우리 두 사람의 주소를 적어, 그것을 찢어 그녀에게 주었고, 그녀는 그것을 가엾은 가슴팍에 넣었다."], ["I asked her where she lived herself.", "나는 그녀 자신은 어디 사느냐고 물었다."], ["She said, after a pause, in no place long. It were better not to know.", "그녀는 잠시 뜸을 들이더니, 한곳에 오래 머무는 법이 없다고 했다. 모르는 편이 낫다고."]], [["Mr. Peggotty suggesting to me, in a whisper, what had already occurred to myself, I took out my purse; but I could not prevail upon her to accept any money, nor could I exact any promise from her that she would do so at another time.", "패거티 씨가 나에게 속삭여 일러 준 것은, 이미 나도 떠올린 바였다. 나는 지갑을 꺼냈으나, 그녀에게 어떤 돈도 받게 할 수가 없었고, 다음에라도 받겠다는 약속조차 받아 낼 수가 없었다."], ["I represented to her that Mr. Peggotty could not be called, for one in his condition, poor; and that the idea of her engaging in this search, while depending on her own resources, shocked us both.", "나는 그녀에게, 패거티 씨가 처지로 보아 결코 가난하다고는 할 수 없는 사람이라는 것, 그리고 그녀가 제 한 몸 살림에 기대어 이 수색에 나선다는 생각이 우리 둘 다를 충격에 빠뜨린다는 것을 일러 주었다."], ["She continued steadfast.", "그녀는 한결같이 버텼다."], ["In this particular, his influence upon her was equally powerless with mine.", "이 한 가지에서만큼은, 그가 그녀에게 미치는 영향력도 나의 것과 똑같이 아무런 힘을 쓰지 못했다."], ["She gratefully thanked him but remained inexorable.", "그녀는 그에게 고마워하면서도 꿈쩍하지 않았다."]], [["‘There may be work to be got,’ she said.", "\"일거리야 구하면 되죠.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘I’ll try.’", "\"해 볼게요.\""]], [["‘At least take some assistance,’ I returned, ‘until you have tried.’", "\"적어도 해 보기 전까지만이라도, 도움을 좀 받아요.\" 내가 받아 말했다."]], [["‘I could not do what I have promised, for money,’ she replied.", "\"돈을 받고서는 제가 약속한 일을 할 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘I could not take it, if I was starving.", "\"굶어 죽는다 해도 그 돈은 받지 못해요."], ["To give me money would be to take away your trust, to take away the object that you have given me, to take away the only certain thing that saves me from the river.’", "저에게 돈을 주신다는 건, 당신의 믿음을 도로 거두는 것이고, 당신이 저에게 주신 그 목표를 도로 빼앗는 것이며, 저를 강물에서 건져 주는 단 하나의 확실한 것을 도로 앗아 가는 거예요.\""]], [["‘In the name of the great judge,’ said I, ‘before whom you and all of us must stand at His dread time, dismiss that terrible idea!", "\"우리 모두가 그분의 무서운 때에 그 앞에 서야 할, 그 위대한 심판자의 이름으로,\" 내가 말했다. \"그 끔찍한 생각을 떨쳐 버려요!"], ["We can all do some good, if we will.’", "우리 모두는 마음만 먹으면 무언가 좋은 일을 할 수 있어요.\""]], [["She trembled, and her lip shook, and her face was paler, as she answered:", "그녀는 떨었고, 입술을 떨었으며, 대답할 때는 얼굴이 더 창백해졌다."]], [["‘It has been put into your hearts, perhaps, to save a wretched creature for repentance.", "\"어쩌면 가엾은 한 인간을 회개하도록 구원하려는 마음이 당신들 가슴에 심어진 건지도 모르죠."], ["I am afraid to think so; it seems too bold.", "그렇게 생각하기가 두려워요. 너무 분에 넘치는 것 같아서요."], ["If any good should come of me, I might begin to hope; for nothing but harm has ever come of my deeds yet.", "만약 저한테서 조금이라도 좋은 일이 나온다면, 저도 희망을 품기 시작할지 몰라요. 지금껏 제 행실에서 나온 건 해악밖에 없었으니까요."], ["I am to be trusted, for the first time in a long while, with my miserable life, on account of what you have given me to try for.", "당신들이 저에게 애써 보라고 무언가를 맡겨 주신 덕분에, 오랜만에 처음으로, 이 비참한 목숨을 두고 누군가의 믿음을 받게 됐어요."], ["I know no more, and I can say no more.’", "그 이상은 저도 모르고, 더 할 말도 없어요.\""]], [["Again she repressed the tears that had begun to flow; and, putting out her trembling hand, and touching Mr. Peggotty, as if there was some healing virtue in him, went away along the desolate road.", "그녀는 다시, 흐르기 시작한 눈물을 억눌렀다. 그러고는 떨리는 손을 내밀어, 마치 그에게 무슨 치유의 힘이라도 깃들어 있는 듯 패거티 씨를 한 번 만지더니, 황량한 길을 따라 떠나갔다."], ["She had been ill, probably for a long time. I observed, upon that closer opportunity of observation, that she was worn and haggard, and that her sunken eyes expressed privation and endurance.", "그녀는 병을 앓았던 모양인데, 아마 오래 그랬을 것이다. 좀 더 가까이서 살펴볼 기회에, 나는 그녀가 야위고 초췌하며, 푹 꺼진 두 눈이 궁핍과 인고를 말해 주고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["We followed her at a short distance, our way lying in the same direction, until we came back into the lighted and populous streets.", "우리는 얼마쯤 거리를 두고 그녀를 뒤따랐는데, 길이 같은 방향이었던 까닭이다. 그렇게 환하고 사람 많은 거리로 다시 돌아올 때까지."], ["I had such implicit confidence in her declaration, that I then put it to Mr. Peggotty, whether it would not seem, in the onset, like distrusting her, to follow her any farther.", "나는 그녀의 다짐을 어찌나 굳게 믿었던지, 그 시점에 패거티 씨에게, 그녀를 더 따라가는 것은 처음부터 그녀를 못 미더워하는 것처럼 보이지 않겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He being of the same mind, and equally reliant on her, we suffered her to take her own road, and took ours, which was towards Highgate.", "그도 같은 생각이었고, 똑같이 그녀를 믿었으므로, 우리는 그녀가 제 갈 길을 가도록 두고, 우리는 우리 길로, 하이게이트 쪽으로 향했다."], ["He accompanied me a good part of the way; and when we parted, with a prayer for the success of this fresh effort, there was a new and thoughtful compassion in him that I was at no loss to interpret.", "그는 한참을 나와 함께 걸어 주었다. 그리고 이 새로운 시도가 잘되기를 비는 기도와 함께 우리가 헤어질 때, 그에게는 내가 어렵잖게 읽어 낼 수 있는, 새롭고도 깊은 연민이 어려 있었다."]], [["It was midnight when I arrived at home.", "집에 다다랐을 때는 한밤중이었다."], ["I had reached my own gate, and was standing listening for the deep bell of St.", "나는 우리 집 대문께에 이르러, 세인트폴 대성당의 묵직한 종소리에 귀를 기울이며 서 있었다."], ["Paul’s, the sound of which I thought had been borne towards me among the multitude of striking clocks, when I was rather surprised to see that the door of my aunt’s cottage was open, and that a faint light in the entry was shining out across the road.", "사방에서 울리는 무수한 시계 종소리 틈에 그 소리가 섞여 나에게 실려 온 것 같았기 때문이다. 그런데 고모님 오두막의 문이 열려 있고, 현관의 희미한 불빛이 길 건너까지 새어 나오는 것을 보고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["Thinking that my aunt might have relapsed into one of her old alarms, and might be watching the progress of some imaginary conflagration in the distance, I went to speak to her.", "고모님이 또 옛날의 그 불안 가운데 하나에 빠져들어, 멀리서 일어나는 가상의 화재가 번져 가는 것을 지켜보고 계신 건 아닐까 싶어, 나는 고모님께 말씀을 드리러 갔다."], ["It was with very great surprise that I saw a man standing in her little garden.", "그러다 한 남자가 고모님의 작은 정원에 서 있는 것을 보고 나는 무척이나 놀랐다."]], [["He had a glass and bottle in his hand, and was in the act of drinking.", "그는 손에 잔과 병을 들고 술을 마시는 참이었다."], ["I stopped short, among the thick foliage outside, for the moon was up now, though obscured; and I recognized the man whom I had once supposed to be a delusion of Mr.", "나는 바깥의 우거진 잎사귀들 틈에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 비록 가려져 있긴 했어도 이제 달이 떠 있었던 까닭이다. 그리고 나는 그 남자를 알아보았다."], ["Dick’s, and had once encountered with my aunt in the streets of the city.", "한때 딕 씨의 헛것이려니 여겼던, 그리고 한 번은 시내 거리에서 고모님과 함께 마주쳤던 바로 그 남자였다."]], [["He was eating as well as drinking, and seemed to eat with a hungry appetite.", "그는 술뿐 아니라 음식도 먹고 있었는데, 허기진 듯 게걸스럽게 먹는 듯했다."], ["He seemed curious regarding the cottage, too, as if it were the first time he had seen it.", "그는 또 오두막을 두고 마치 처음 보는 것처럼 신기해하는 눈치였다."], ["After stooping to put the bottle on the ground, he looked up at the windows, and looked about; though with a covert and impatient air, as if he was anxious to be gone.", "그는 몸을 숙여 병을 바닥에 내려놓고는, 창문들을 올려다보고 사방을 둘러보았다. 그러면서도 어딘가 은밀하고 조바심 나는 기색이, 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶어 안달하는 듯했다."]], [["The light in the passage was obscured for a moment, and my aunt came out.", "복도의 불빛이 잠깐 가려지더니, 고모님이 나오셨다."], ["She was agitated, and told some money into his hand. I heard it chink.", "고모님은 마음이 동요한 채로, 그의 손에 돈을 세어 쥐여 주었다. 나는 그 짤랑거리는 소리를 들었다."]], [["‘What’s the use of this?’ he demanded.", "\"이까짓 게 무슨 소용이오?\" 그가 따져 물었다."]], [["‘I can spare no more,’ returned my aunt.", "\"더는 줄 게 없어요.\" 고모님이 대답했다."]], [["‘Then I can’t go,’ said he. ‘Here!", "\"그럼 못 가겠소.\" 그가 말했다. \"자!"], ["You may take it back!’", "도로 가져가시오!\""]], [["‘You bad man,’ returned my aunt, with great emotion; ‘how can you use me so?", "\"못된 사람 같으니.\" 고모님이 크게 격해진 채로 받아쳤다. \"어떻게 나를 이렇게 부려먹을 수가 있어요?"], ["But why do I ask?", "한데 내가 왜 이런 걸 묻는담?"], ["It is because you know how weak I am!", "그건 당신이 내가 얼마나 약한지를 알고 있기 때문이지!"], ["What have I to do, to free myself for ever of your visits, but to abandon you to your deserts?’", "당신의 발걸음에서 영영 자유로워지려면, 당신을 마땅한 업보에 내버려 두는 것 말고 내가 무얼 할 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["‘And why don’t you abandon me to my deserts?’ said he.", "\"그럼 왜 나를 마땅한 업보에 내버려 두지 않는 거요?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘You ask me why!’ returned my aunt.", "\"나한테 왜냐고 묻다니!\" 고모님이 받아쳤다."], ["‘What a heart you must have!’", "\"당신은 대체 무슨 심보를 가진 사람이람!\""]], [["He stood moodily rattling the money, and shaking his head, until at length he said:", "그는 침울하게 돈을 짤랑거리며 고개를 가로젓고 서 있다가, 마침내 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Is this all you mean to give me, then?’", "\"그럼, 나한테 줄 게 이게 다란 말이오?\""]], [["‘It is all I CAN give you,’ said my aunt.", "\"이게 내가 줄 수 있는 전부예요.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘You know I have had losses, and am poorer than I used to be.", "\"내가 손해를 봐서 예전보다 가난해졌다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요."], ["I have told you so.", "그렇게 말해 줬고요."], ["Having got it, why do you give me the pain of looking at you for another moment, and seeing what you have become?’", "그걸 받았으면, 어째서 나한테 당신을 한순간이라도 더 바라보며 당신이 어떤 꼴이 됐는지 보아야 하는 고통을 주는 거예요?\""]], [["‘I have become shabby enough, if you mean that,’ he said.", "\"그 말이라면, 내 꼴이 어지간히 초라해지긴 했지.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘I lead the life of an owl.’", "\"올빼미 같은 신세로 살고 있으니.\""]], [["‘You stripped me of the greater part of all I ever had,’ said my aunt.", "\"당신은 내가 가진 거의 모든 걸 나한테서 벗겨 갔어요.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘You closed my heart against the whole world, years and years.", "\"당신은 몇 해고 몇 해고 내 마음을 온 세상에 닫아걸게 만들었어요."], ["You treated me falsely, ungratefully, and cruelly.", "당신은 나를 거짓되게, 배은망덕하게, 모질게 대했어요."], ["Go, and repent of it.", "가요, 그리고 뉘우쳐요."], ["Don’t add new injuries to the long, long list of injuries you have done me!’", "당신이 나한테 저지른 길고도 긴 잘못의 목록에다 새로운 잘못을 더 보태지 말고요!\""]], [["‘Aye!’ he returned.", "\"하!\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘It’s all very fine--Well!", "\"다 그럴듯한 소리지 — 그래!"], ["I must do the best I can, for the present, I suppose.’", "당분간은 내 할 수 있는 대로 해 봐야겠지, 뭐.\""]], [["In spite of himself, he appeared abashed by my aunt’s indignant tears, and came slouching out of the garden.", "자기도 모르게, 그는 고모님의 분에 찬 눈물에 머쓱해진 듯, 어슬렁어슬렁 정원에서 나왔다."], ["Taking two or three quick steps, as if I had just come up, I met him at the gate, and went in as he came out.", "나는 마치 막 도착하기라도 한 양 두세 걸음 빠르게 내디뎌, 대문께에서 그와 마주쳤고, 그가 나오는 사이에 안으로 들어갔다."], ["We eyed one another narrowly in passing, and with no favour.", "우리는 스쳐 지나며 서로를 빈틈없이, 그리고 조금도 호의 없이 훑어보았다."]], [["‘Aunt,’ said I, hurriedly.", "\"고모님,\" 내가 다급히 말했다."], ["‘This man alarming you again!", "\"이 남자가 또 고모님을 겁주고 있어요!"], ["Let me speak to him. Who is he?’", "제가 저 사람한테 말 좀 하게 해 주세요. 누구예요?\""]], [["‘Child,’ returned my aunt, taking my arm, ‘come in, and don’t speak to me for ten minutes.’", "\"얘야,\" 고모님이 내 팔을 붙들며 대답했다. \"들어가자, 그리고 십 분 동안은 나한테 말 걸지 말고.\""]], [["We sat down in her little parlour.", "우리는 고모님의 작은 거실에 앉았다."], ["My aunt retired behind the round green fan of former days, which was screwed on the back of a chair, and occasionally wiped her eyes, for about a quarter of an hour.", "고모님은 옛날에 쓰던 둥근 초록빛 부채 뒤로 물러나 앉았는데, 그 부채는 의자 등받이에 나사로 고정되어 있었다. 고모님은 한 십오 분쯤 이따금 눈물을 훔쳤다."], ["Then she came out, and took a seat beside me.", "그러더니 부채 뒤에서 나와, 내 곁에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["‘Trot,’ said my aunt, calmly, ‘it’s my husband.’", "\"트롯,\" 고모님이 차분하게 말했다. \"그 사람은 내 남편이란다.\""]], [["‘Your husband, aunt?", "\"고모님 남편이라고요?"], ["I thought he had been dead!’", "전 돌아가신 줄로만 알았는데요!\""]], [["‘Dead to me,’ returned my aunt, ‘but living.’", "\"나한테는 죽은 사람이지.\" 고모님이 대답했다. \"하지만 살아 있단다.\""]], [["I sat in silent amazement.", "나는 말없이 놀란 채로 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Betsey Trotwood don’t look a likely subject for the tender passion,’ said my aunt, composedly, ‘but the time was, Trot, when she believed in that man most entirely.", "\"베시 트롯우드가 그런 애틋한 정 같은 것에 어울릴 만한 위인으로는 안 보이겠지.\" 고모님이 침착하게 말했다. \"하지만 트롯, 한때는 이 베시 트롯우드가 그 남자를 더없이 송두리째 믿던 시절이 있었단다."], ["When she loved him, Trot, right well.", "그를 사랑하던 시절이 있었지, 트롯, 아주 깊이."], ["When there was no proof of attachment and affection that she would not have given him.", "그를 위해서라면 내주지 못할 애정과 정성의 증표가 없던 시절이 있었어."], ["He repaid her by breaking her fortune, and nearly breaking her heart.", "그는 그 보답으로 내 재산을 거덜 내고, 내 마음마저 거의 부숴 놓았지."], ["So she put all that sort of sentiment, once and for ever, in a grave, and filled it up, and flattened it down.’", "그래서 나는 그런 종류의 감정이란 감정은 죄다, 단 한 번에 영영, 무덤에 묻고, 흙을 메우고, 평평하게 다져 버렸단다.\""]], [["‘My dear, good aunt!’", "\"고모님, 사랑하는 고모님!\""]], [["‘I left him,’ my aunt proceeded, laying her hand as usual on the back of mine, ‘generously.", "\"나는 그를 떠났다.\" 고모님이 늘 하던 대로 자기 손을 내 손등에 얹으며 말을 이었다. \"너그럽게 떠나 줬어."], ["I may say at this distance of time, Trot, that I left him generously.", "이제 이렇게 세월이 흐른 마당이니 말할 수 있다만, 트롯, 나는 그를 너그럽게 떠났단다."], ["He had been so cruel to me, that I might have effected a separation on easy terms for myself; but I did not.", "그는 나한테 어찌나 모질게 굴었던지, 나로서는 손쉬운 조건으로 갈라설 수도 있었지. 하지만 나는 그러지 않았어."], ["He soon made ducks and drakes of what I gave him, sank lower and lower, married another woman, I believe, became an adventurer, a gambler, and a cheat.", "그는 내가 준 것을 금세 물 위로 던진 조약돌처럼 흥청망청 날려 버리고, 점점 더 밑바닥으로 가라앉아, 내 짐작엔 딴 여자랑 결혼했고, 협잡꾼에 노름꾼에 사기꾼이 됐단다. 지금 그가 어떤 꼴인지는 너도 보았지."], ["What he is now, you see.", "하지만 내가 그와 결혼할 무렵엔 풍채 좋은 남자였단다.\""], ["But he was a fine-looking man when I married him,’ said my aunt, with an echo of her old pride and admiration in her tone; ‘and I believed him--I was a fool!--to be the soul of honour!’", "고모님이 옛 자부심과 흠모의 메아리를 그 말투에 실어 말했다. \"그리고 나는 그를 믿었지 — 내가 바보였어! — 명예의 화신쯤으로 말이다!\""]], [["She gave my hand a squeeze, and shook her head.", "고모님은 내 손을 한번 꼭 쥐고는, 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘He is nothing to me now, Trot--less than nothing.", "\"그는 이제 나한테 아무것도 아니다, 트롯 — 아무것도 아닌 것보다도 못해."], ["But, sooner than have him punished for his offences (as he would be if he prowled about in this country), I give him more money than I can afford, at intervals when he reappears, to go away.", "하지만 그가 자기 잘못으로 벌을 받느니 (이 나라에서 어슬렁거리고 다니다가는 그렇게 될 테니), 그가 다시 나타날 때마다 그가 떠나도록 내 형편에 넘치는 돈을 쥐여 준단다. 그와 결혼할 때 나는 바보였지."], ["I was a fool when I married him; and I am so far an incurable fool on that subject, that, for the sake of what I once believed him to be, I wouldn’t have even this shadow of my idle fancy hardly dealt with. For I was in earnest, Trot, if ever a woman was.’", "그리고 그 일에 관한 한 나는 어찌나 구제 불능의 바보인지, 한때 내가 그를 어떤 사람이라고 믿었던가, 그 기억을 봐서라도, 이 부질없는 환상의 그림자나마 모질게 다뤄지는 꼴은 차마 못 보겠더구나. 나는 진심이었으니까, 트롯, 어느 여자가 진심이었던 적이 있다면 바로 나만큼은 말이다.\""]], [["My aunt dismissed the matter with a heavy sigh, and smoothed her dress.", "고모님은 무거운 한숨과 함께 이 이야기를 매듭짓고, 옷매무새를 가다듬었다."]], [["‘There, my dear!’ she said.", "\"자, 얘야!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Now you know the beginning, middle, and end, and all about it.", "\"이제 너도 처음과 가운데와 끝까지, 그 모든 사연을 다 알게 됐구나."], ["We won’t mention the subject to one another any more; neither, of course, will you mention it to anybody else.", "우리 둘은 이 이야기를 두 번 다시 서로 입에 올리지 말자꾸나. 너도 물론 다른 누구한테든 이 얘기를 꺼내지 말고."], ["This is my grumpy, frumpy story, and we’ll keep it to ourselves, Trot!’", "이건 나의 부루퉁하고 케케묵은 이야기이니, 우리끼리만 간직하자, 트롯!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 48. DOMESTIC", "# CHAPTER 48. DOMESTIC"]], [["I laboured hard at my book, without allowing it to interfere with the punctual discharge of my newspaper duties; and it came out and was very successful.", "나는 신문 일을 한 치 어긋남 없이 제때 해내면서도 거기에 방해받지 않도록 내 책에 매달려 부지런히 썼다. 그렇게 책이 세상에 나왔고, 큰 성공을 거두었다."], ["I was not stunned by the praise which sounded in my ears, notwithstanding that I was keenly alive to it, and thought better of my own performance, I have little doubt, than anybody else did.", "귀에 쟁쟁하게 울리는 칭찬에 나는 정신을 잃을 만큼 들뜨지는 않았다. 물론 그 칭찬에 예민하게 마음이 가 닿기는 했고, 내 작품을 두고 누구보다도 내가 더 높이 평가했으리라는 것은 거의 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["It has always been in my observation of human nature, that a man who has any good reason to believe in himself never flourishes himself before the faces of other people in order that they may believe in him.", "사람의 본성을 지켜보면서 내가 늘 깨달은 바가 있는데, 자기를 믿을 만한 그럴듯한 근거를 가진 사람은 결코 남들이 자기를 믿게 하려고 그들 면전에서 제 자랑을 떠벌리지 않는다는 것이다."], ["For this reason, I retained my modesty in very self-respect; and the more praise I got, the more I tried to deserve.", "그래서 나는 바로 그 자존심 덕분에 겸손함을 지켰고, 칭찬을 받으면 받을수록 그 칭찬에 더 어울리는 사람이 되려고 애썼다."]], [["It is not my purpose, in this record, though in all other essentials it is my written memory, to pursue the history of my own fictions.", "이 기록은 다른 모든 본질적인 면에서는 내 기억을 글로 옮긴 것이지만, 내가 쓴 소설들의 내력을 좇는 것이 그 목적은 아니다."], ["They express themselves, and I leave them to themselves.", "소설은 스스로 제 모습을 드러내니, 나는 그것들을 그것들에게 맡겨 둔다."], ["When I refer to them, incidentally, it is only as a part of my progress.", "내가 이따금 그것들을 언급한다면, 그것은 오로지 내 성장 과정의 한 부분으로서일 뿐이다."]], [["Having some foundation for believing, by this time, that nature and accident had made me an author, I pursued my vocation with confidence.", "이 무렵 천성과 우연이 나를 작가로 만들어 놓았다고 믿을 만한 근거가 어느 정도 생겼기에, 나는 자신감을 품고 내 천직을 좇았다."], ["Without such assurance I should certainly have left it alone, and bestowed my energy on some other endeavour.", "그런 확신이 없었더라면 나는 분명 그 길을 내버려 두고 다른 일에 내 힘을 쏟았을 것이다."], ["I should have tried to find out what nature and accident really had made me, and to be that, and nothing else.", "천성과 우연이 나를 정말로 무엇으로 만들어 놓았는지를 알아내려 애썼을 테고, 다른 무엇도 아닌 바로 그것이 되려 했을 것이다."], ["I had been writing, in the newspaper and elsewhere, so prosperously, that when my new success was achieved, I considered myself reasonably entitled to escape from the dreary debates.", "나는 신문에도, 다른 데에도 글을 써서 꽤 잘 풀려 가고 있었으니, 이번 새로운 성공을 거두고 나자 그 따분한 토론에서 벗어날 자격쯤은 마땅히 있다고 여겼다."], ["One joyful night, therefore, I noted down the music of the parliamentary bagpipes for the last time, and I have never heard it since; though I still recognize the old drone in the newspapers, without any substantial variation (except, perhaps, that there is more of it), all the livelong session.", "그래서 어느 기쁜 밤, 나는 마지막으로 의회의 백파이프 소리를 받아 적었고, 그 뒤로는 그 소리를 다시는 듣지 않았다. 그래도 회기 내내 신문 속에서 그 낡은 웅웅거림을 여전히 알아보기는 하는데, 이렇다 할 변화 하나 없이 (아마 그 양이 더 늘어났다는 것 말고는) 그대로다."]], [["I now write of the time when I had been married, I suppose, about a year and a half.", "지금 내가 쓰고 있는 것은, 내가 결혼한 지 한 일 년 반쯤 되었을 무렵의 이야기다."], ["After several varieties of experiment, we had given up the housekeeping as a bad job.", "이런저런 방식으로 여러 번 실험해 본 끝에, 우리는 살림이란 것을 글러 먹은 일로 여기고 포기해 버렸다."], ["The house kept itself, and we kept a page.", "집은 알아서 굴러갔고, 우리는 사환 아이 하나를 두었다."], ["The principal function of this retainer was to quarrel with the cook; in which respect he was a perfect Whittington, without his cat, or the remotest chance of being made Lord Mayor.", "이 하인의 주된 임무는 요리사와 다투는 것이었는데, 그 점에서 그는 영락없는 휘팅턴이었다 — 다만 고양이도 없고, 런던 시장이 될 가망이라곤 손톱만큼도 없는 휘팅턴이었지만."]], [["He appears to me to have lived in a hail of saucepan-lids. His whole existence was a scuffle.", "* 휘팅턴: 고양이 덕에 출세하여 런던 시장이 되었다는 전설 속 인물 딕 휘팅턴을 가리킴."], ["He would shriek for help on the most improper occasions,--as when we had a little dinner-party, or a few friends in the evening,--and would come tumbling out of the kitchen, with iron missiles flying after him.", "내가 보기에 그 아이는 냄비 뚜껑이 우박처럼 쏟아지는 속에서 사는 듯했다. 그의 삶 전체가 한바탕 드잡이였다. 그는 영 어울리지 않는 순간마다 — 가령 우리가 조촐한 만찬 자리를 벌이거나 저녁에 친구 몇이 와 있을 때 — 살려 달라고 비명을 질러 댔고, 쇠붙이가 뒤따라 날아오는 와중에 부엌에서 굴러 나오곤 했다."], ["We wanted to get rid of him, but he was very much attached to us, and wouldn’t go.", "우리는 그를 떼어 내고 싶었지만, 그는 우리에게 어찌나 정이 깊었던지 도무지 떠나려 하지 않았다."], ["He was a tearful boy, and broke into such deplorable lamentations, when a cessation of our connexion was hinted at, that we were obliged to keep him.", "그는 눈물이 헤픈 아이였고, 관계를 끊자는 기미만 비쳐도 어찌나 가엾게 통곡을 터뜨리던지, 우리는 그를 데리고 있을 수밖에 없었다."], ["He had no mother--no anything in the way of a relative, that I could discover, except a sister, who fled to America the moment we had taken him off her hands; and he became quartered on us like a horrible young changeling.", "그에게는 어머니도 없었고 — 내가 찾아낸 한, 친척이라 할 만한 것이 아무것도 없었다 — 누이 하나만 있었는데, 그 누이는 우리가 동생을 떠맡자마자 미국으로 줄행랑을 쳐 버렸다. 그래서 그는 끔찍한 어린 도깨비처럼 우리 집에 눌러앉게 되었다."], ["He had a lively perception of his own unfortunate state, and was always rubbing his eyes with the sleeve of his jacket, or stooping to blow his nose on the extreme corner of a little pocket-handkerchief, which he never would take completely out of his pocket, but always economized and secreted.", "그는 제 불운한 처지를 생생하게 알아차리고 있어서, 늘 윗도리 소맷자락으로 눈을 비비거나, 작은 손수건의 맨 끄트머리 한 귀퉁이에 코를 풀려고 몸을 숙이곤 했는데, 그 손수건은 절대 호주머니에서 통째로 꺼내는 법이 없이 늘 아껴 가며 감춰 두었다."]], [["This unlucky page, engaged in an evil hour at six pounds ten per annum, was a source of continual trouble to me.", "이 불운한 사환 아이는, 불길한 시각에 연봉 육 파운드 십 실링으로 고용된 터였는데, 나에게는 끊임없는 골칫거리의 근원이었다."], ["I watched him as he grew--and he grew like scarlet beans--with painful apprehensions of the time when he would begin to shave; even of the days when he would be bald or grey.", "나는 그가 자라는 것을 — 그는 강낭콩처럼 쑥쑥 자랐다 — 그가 면도를 시작할 날을, 심지어 머리가 벗어지거나 희끗희끗해질 날을 떠올리며 괴로운 불안에 사로잡혀 지켜보았다."], ["I saw no prospect of ever getting rid of him; and, projecting myself into the future, used to think what an inconvenience he would be when he was an old man.", "그를 떼어 낼 가망은 도무지 보이지 않았다. 그래서 나는 미래로 나 자신을 던져 보며, 그가 노인이 되면 얼마나 골칫거리일까 곰곰이 생각하곤 했다."]], [["I never expected anything less, than this unfortunate’s manner of getting me out of my difficulty.", "이 불행한 녀석이 내 곤경을 풀어 줄 방식을, 나는 꿈에도 짐작하지 못했다."], ["He stole Dora’s watch, which, like everything else belonging to us, had no particular place of its own; and, converting it into money, spent the produce (he was always a weak-minded boy) in incessantly riding up and down between London and Uxbridge outside the coach.", "그는 도라의 시계를 훔쳤는데, 그 시계는 우리 것이 다 그렇듯 따로 정해진 자리가 없던 물건이었다. 그는 그것을 돈으로 바꿔, 그 돈을 (그는 늘 줏대 없는 아이였으니) 런던과 억스브리지 사이를 마차 바깥자리에 앉아 쉴 새 없이 오르내리는 데 다 써 버렸다."], ["He was taken to Bow Street, as well as I remember, on the completion of his fifteenth journey; when four-and-sixpence, and a second-hand fife which he couldn’t play, were found upon his person.", "내 기억으로 그는 열다섯 번째 여행을 마쳤을 때 보 스트리트로 끌려갔는데, 그때 그의 몸에서 사 실링 육 펜스와, 불 줄도 모르는 중고 피리 하나가 나왔다."]], [["The surprise and its consequences would have been much less disagreeable to me if he had not been penitent.", "만약 그가 뉘우치지만 않았더라면 그 사건과 그 뒷일은 나에게 훨씬 덜 거북했을 것이다."], ["But he was very penitent indeed, and in a peculiar way--not in the lump, but by instalments.", "하지만 그는 정말이지 깊이 뉘우쳤고, 그것도 묘한 방식으로 — 한꺼번에가 아니라 할부로 뉘우쳤다."], ["For example: the day after that on which I was obliged to appear against him, he made certain revelations touching a hamper in the cellar, which we believed to be full of wine, but which had nothing in it except bottles and corks.", "이를테면 이런 식이었다. 내가 그를 상대로 법정에 서야 했던 다음 날, 그는 지하실의 광주리 하나에 관해 어떤 폭로를 했는데, 우리는 그것이 포도주로 가득 찬 줄로만 알고 있었으나 안에는 빈 병과 코르크 마개밖에 없었다."], ["We supposed he had now eased his mind, and told the worst he knew of the cook; but, a day or two afterwards, his conscience sustained a new twinge, and he disclosed how she had a little girl, who, early every morning, took away our bread; and also how he himself had been suborned to maintain the milkman in coals.", "우리는 그가 이제 속을 다 털어놓고, 요리사에 대해 아는 가장 나쁜 일까지 다 일러바쳤거니 했다. 그러나 하루 이틀쯤 지나자 그의 양심이 새로이 뜨끔해졌는지, 요리사에게 어린 딸이 하나 있어 그 애가 매일 새벽 우리 빵을 가져갔다는 것과, 또 자기 자신은 우유 배달부에게 석탄을 대 주도록 매수당했다는 사실을 폭로했다."], ["In two or three days more, I was informed by the authorities of his having led to the discovery of sirloins of beef among the kitchen-stuff, and sheets in the rag-bag.", "다시 이삼 일이 더 지나자, 나는 그가 부엌 잡동사니 속에서 소 등심 덩어리가, 그리고 누더기 자루 속에서 침대 시트가 발견되도록 단서를 댔다는 소식을 당국으로부터 전해 들었다."], ["A little while afterwards, he broke out in an entirely new direction, and confessed to a knowledge of burglarious intentions as to our premises, on the part of the pot-boy, who was immediately taken up.", "그러고 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 전혀 새로운 방향으로 터져 나와, 술집 심부름꾼 녀석이 우리 집에 강도질을 들 뜻을 품고 있더라는 것을 자기가 알고 있노라 자백했고, 그 녀석은 즉시 붙잡혀 갔다."], ["I got to be so ashamed of being such a victim, that I would have given him any money to hold his tongue, or would have offered a round bribe for his being permitted to run away.", "나는 이렇게 봉변을 당하는 처지가 너무도 부끄러워, 그가 입을 다물어만 준다면 얼마든 돈을 주었을 것이고, 그를 도망치게 놓아주는 대가로라면 두둑한 뇌물이라도 내놓았을 것이다."], ["It was an aggravating circumstance in the case that he had no idea of this, but conceived that he was making me amends in every new discovery: not to say, heaping obligations on my head.", "사건에서 더 약 오르는 대목은, 그가 이런 내 심정은 짐작도 못 한 채, 새로운 폭로를 할 때마다 자기가 나에게 빚을 갚는 셈이라고 — 더 나아가 나에게 은혜를 잔뜩 베푸는 셈이라고 여겼다는 것이다."]], [["At last I ran away myself, whenever I saw an emissary of the police approaching with some new intelligence; and lived a stealthy life until he was tried and ordered to be transported.", "마침내 나는, 새로운 정보를 들고 경찰 사자가 다가오는 게 보일 때마다, 나 자신이 줄행랑을 쳤다. 그래서 그가 재판을 받고 유형에 처해질 때까지 숨어 사는 신세로 지냈다."], ["Even then he couldn’t be quiet, but was always writing us letters; and wanted so much to see Dora before he went away, that Dora went to visit him, and fainted when she found herself inside the iron bars.", "그러고 나서도 그는 가만히 있지 못하고 줄곧 우리에게 편지를 써 보냈다. 떠나기 전에 도라를 몹시도 보고 싶어 한 나머지, 도라가 면회를 갔다가, 쇠창살 안에 들어선 순간 그만 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."], ["In short, I had no peace of my life until he was expatriated, and made (as I afterwards heard) a shepherd of, ‘up the country’ somewhere; I have no geographical idea where.", "한마디로 나는 그가 국외로 추방되어, (나중에 들으니) 어딘가 '내륙 깊숙한 곳'에서 양치기가 될 때까지 단 하루도 마음 편할 날이 없었다. 그곳이 지리상 어디인지는 나도 전혀 모른다."]], [["All this led me into some serious reflections, and presented our mistakes in a new aspect; as I could not help communicating to Dora one evening, in spite of my tenderness for her.", "이 모든 일은 나를 어떤 진지한 성찰로 이끌었고, 우리의 잘못을 새로운 측면에서 보여 주었다. 어느 날 저녁, 도라를 향한 애틋한 마음에도 불구하고 나는 끝내 그것을 그녀에게 털어놓지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘My love,’ said I, ‘it is very painful to me to think that our want of system and management, involves not only ourselves (which we have got used to), but other people.’", "\"여보,\" 내가 말했다. \"우리에게 체계도 관리도 없는 탓에 우리 자신만이 아니라 (그건 이제 우리도 익숙해졌지) 다른 사람들까지 끌어들인다고 생각하면 정말 마음이 아파.\""]], [["‘You have been silent for a long time, and now you are going to be cross!’ said Dora.", "\"당신 한참을 잠자코 있더니, 이제 또 짜증을 내려는 거죠!\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["‘No, my dear, indeed!", "\"아니야, 여보, 정말 아니야!"], ["Let me explain to you what I mean.’", "내 말이 무슨 뜻인지 설명하게 해 줘.\""]], [["‘I think I don’t want to know,’ said Dora.", "\"난 알고 싶지 않은 것 같아요.\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["‘But I want you to know, my love.", "\"하지만 난 당신이 알았으면 해, 여보."], ["Put Jip down.’", "집을 내려놓아.\""]], [["Dora put his nose to mine, and said ‘Boh!’ to drive my seriousness away; but, not succeeding, ordered him into his Pagoda, and sat looking at me, with her hands folded, and a most resigned little expression of countenance.", "도라는 집의 코를 내 코에 갖다 대고는 \"워!\" 하고 외쳐 내 진지함을 쫓아 버리려 했다. 그러나 뜻대로 되지 않자, 집을 탑 모양 개집으로 들여보내고는, 두 손을 포갠 채 더없이 체념한 듯한 자그마한 표정으로 나를 빤히 바라보며 앉았다."]], [["‘The fact is, my dear,’ I began, ‘there is contagion in us.", "\"실은 말이야, 여보,\" 내가 입을 뗐다. \"우리한테는 전염성이 있어."], ["We infect everyone about us.’", "우리 주위 사람들을 죄다 물들인단 말이야.\""]], [["I might have gone on in this figurative manner, if Dora’s face had not admonished me that she was wondering with all her might whether I was going to propose any new kind of vaccination, or other medical remedy, for this unwholesome state of ours.", "만약 도라의 얼굴이 나에게 일러 주지 않았더라면, 나는 이런 비유적인 투로 계속 늘어놓았을지도 모른다. 도라는 온 힘을 다해 궁리하고 있었던 것이다 — 내가 이 우리의 못된 상태를 두고 무슨 새로운 종류의 예방접종이나 다른 의학적 치료법을 제안하려는 게 아닐까 하고."], ["Therefore I checked myself, and made my meaning plainer.", "그래서 나는 말을 멈추고, 내 뜻을 좀 더 알아듣기 쉽게 풀었다."]], [["‘It is not merely, my pet,’ said I, ‘that we lose money and comfort, and even temper sometimes, by not learning to be more careful; but that we incur the serious responsibility of spoiling everyone who comes into our service, or has any dealings with us.", "\"내 사랑, 그저 우리가 좀 더 조심하기를 배우지 못한 탓에 돈과 안락을, 때로는 마음의 평화까지 잃는다는 것만이 아니야,\" 내가 말했다. \"우리 밑에서 일하거나 우리와 거래하는 사람을 죄다 망쳐 놓는 중대한 책임까지 떠안게 된다는 거야."], ["I begin to be afraid that the fault is not entirely on one side, but that these people all turn out ill because we don’t turn out very well ourselves.’", "잘못이 어느 한쪽에만 있는 게 아니라, 이 사람들이 다 못되게 변해 가는 건 우리 자신이 그리 잘하지 못하기 때문이 아닐까, 슬슬 그런 게 두려워지기 시작했어.\""]], [["‘Oh, what an accusation,’ exclaimed Dora, opening her eyes wide; ‘to say that you ever saw me take gold watches! Oh!’", "\"어머나, 무슨 그런 누명을 씌워요,\" 도라가 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 외쳤다. \"내가 금시계 훔치는 걸 본 적이라도 있다는 듯이! 어머나!\""]], [["‘My dearest,’ I remonstrated, ‘don’t talk preposterous nonsense!", "\"이봐, 여보,\" 나는 타일렀다. \"말도 안 되는 소리 하지 마!"], ["Who has made the least allusion to gold watches?’", "누가 금시계 얘길 눈곱만큼이라도 꺼냈다고 그래?\""]], [["‘You did,’ returned Dora.", "\"당신이 그랬잖아요.\" 도라가 받아쳤다."], ["‘You know you did.", "\"그랬다는 거 알면서."], ["You said I hadn’t turned out well, and compared me to him.’", "내가 잘하지 못했다고 하면서, 나를 그 애한테 빗댔잖아요.\""]], [["‘To whom?’ I asked.", "\"누구한테?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘To the page,’ sobbed Dora.", "\"그 사환 아이한테요.\""], ["‘Oh, you cruel fellow, to compare your affectionate wife to a transported page!", "도라가 흐느꼈다. \"어쩜 그렇게 잔인해요, 당신을 사랑하는 아내를 유배된 사환 아이한테 빗대다니!"], ["Why didn’t you tell me your opinion of me before we were married?", "왜 결혼하기 전에 나에 대한 당신 속내를 말해 주지 않았어요?"], ["Why didn’t you say, you hard-hearted thing, that you were convinced I was worse than a transported page?", "왜 말 안 했어요, 이 매정한 양반아, 내가 유배된 사환 아이보다 더 못한 사람이라고 확신한다고?"], ["Oh, what a dreadful opinion to have of me!", "어쩜 나를 그렇게 끔찍하게 생각해요!"], ["Oh, my goodness!’", "아아, 세상에!\""]], [["‘Now, Dora, my love,’ I returned, gently trying to remove the handkerchief she pressed to her eyes, ‘this is not only very ridiculous of you, but very wrong.", "\"자, 도라, 여보,\" 나는 그녀가 눈에 갖다 댄 손수건을 살며시 치우려 애쓰며 말했다. \"이건 당신답지 않게 우스꽝스러울 뿐 아니라 아주 잘못된 생각이야."], ["In the first place, it’s not true.’", "무엇보다도 그건 사실이 아니야.\""]], [["‘You always said he was a story-teller,’ sobbed Dora.", "\"당신은 늘 그 애가 거짓말쟁이라고 했잖아요.\" 도라가 흐느꼈다."], ["‘And now you say the same of me!", "\"그런데 이제 나한테도 똑같은 말을 하다니!"], ["Oh, what shall I do!", "아아, 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["What shall I do!’", "어쩌면 좋아!\""]], [["‘My darling girl,’ I retorted, ‘I really must entreat you to be reasonable, and listen to what I did say, and do say.", "\"내 사랑하는 여보,\" 나는 되받았다. \"정말이지 부탁이니 분별 있게 좀 굴고, 내가 한 말, 지금 하는 말을 잘 들어 봐."], ["My dear Dora, unless we learn to do our duty to those whom we employ, they will never learn to do their duty to us.", "도라, 우리가 부리는 사람들에게 우리 도리를 다하는 법을 배우지 않으면, 그들도 결코 우리에게 제 도리를 다하는 법을 배우지 못할 거야."], ["I am afraid we present opportunities to people to do wrong, that never ought to be presented.", "우리가 사람들에게 결코 줘서는 안 될 잘못을 저지를 기회를 주고 있는 게 아닐까 두려워."], ["Even if we were as lax as we are, in all our arrangements, by choice--which we are not--even if we liked it, and found it agreeable to be so--which we don’t--I am persuaded we should have no right to go on in this way.", "설령 우리가 이 모든 살림살이에서 지금처럼 느슨한 게 우리 선택이라 해도 — 사실 그렇지도 않지만 — 설령 우리가 그걸 좋아하고 그렇게 사는 게 즐겁다 해도 — 그렇지도 않지만 — 그래도 우리에게 이런 식으로 계속할 권리는 없다고 나는 확신해."], ["We are positively corrupting people.", "우리는 분명히 사람들을 타락시키고 있어."], ["We are bound to think of that.", "우린 그걸 생각해야 해."], ["I can’t help thinking of it, Dora.", "나는 그 생각을 떨칠 수가 없어, 도라."], ["It is a reflection I am unable to dismiss, and it sometimes makes me very uneasy.", "도무지 떨쳐 버릴 수 없는 생각이고, 때로는 그게 나를 몹시 불안하게 만들어."], ["There, dear, that’s all.", "자, 여보, 그게 다야."], ["Come now.", "이제 그만."], ["Don’t be foolish!’", "바보같이 굴지 마!\""]], [["Dora would not allow me, for a long time, to remove the handkerchief.", "도라는 한참 동안 내가 손수건을 치우도록 놔두지 않았다."], ["She sat sobbing and murmuring behind it, that, if I was uneasy, why had I ever been married?", "그녀는 손수건 뒤에서 흐느끼며 중얼거리기를, 그렇게 불안하다면 애초에 왜 결혼을 했느냐고 했다."], ["Why hadn’t I said, even the day before we went to church, that I knew I should be uneasy, and I would rather not?", "왜 교회에 가기 바로 전날에라도, 내가 불안할 걸 알고 있으니 차라리 결혼하지 않는 게 낫겠다고 말하지 않았느냐고 했다."], ["If I couldn’t bear her, why didn’t I send her away to her aunts at Putney, or to Julia Mills in India?", "나를 견뎌 낼 수 없다면 왜 자기를 퍼트니의 고모들에게나, 아니면 인도의 줄리아 밀스에게 보내 버리지 않느냐고도 했다."], ["Julia would be glad to see her, and would not call her a transported page; Julia never had called her anything of the sort.", "줄리아라면 자기를 기쁘게 맞아 줄 것이고, 유배된 사환 아이라고 부르지도 않을 거라고 — 줄리아는 한 번도 자기를 그딴 식으로 부른 적이 없다고 했다."], ["In short, Dora was so afflicted, and so afflicted me by being in that condition, that I felt it was of no use repeating this kind of effort, though never so mildly, and I must take some other course.", "한마디로 도라는 어찌나 슬퍼하던지, 또 그 슬퍼하는 모습으로 나를 어찌나 슬프게 하던지, 나는 아무리 부드럽게 한들 이런 식의 시도를 되풀이해 봐야 소용없겠다는 것을, 무언가 다른 길을 택해야겠다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["What other course was left to take?", "남은 다른 길이 무엇이었던가?"], ["To ‘form her mind’? This was a common phrase of words which had a fair and promising sound, and I resolved to form Dora’s mind.", "그녀의 '마음을 빚어' 보는 것? 이것은 그럴듯하고 희망찬 울림을 가진 흔한 말이었기에, 나는 도라의 마음을 빚어 보기로 결심했다."]], [["I began immediately.", "나는 곧장 시작했다."], ["When Dora was very childish, and I would have infinitely preferred to humour her, I tried to be grave--and disconcerted her, and myself too.", "도라가 한껏 어린애처럼 굴어서 그저 비위를 맞춰 주는 게 한없이 더 좋을 때에도, 나는 짐짓 근엄해지려 애썼다 — 그래서 그녀를 당황하게 했고, 나 자신마저 당황하게 했다."], ["I talked to her on the subjects which occupied my thoughts; and I read Shakespeare to her--and fatigued her to the last degree.", "나는 내 머릿속을 채우고 있는 주제들에 관해 그녀에게 이야기했고, 셰익스피어를 읽어 주었다 — 그래서 그녀를 지칠 대로 지치게 만들었다."], ["I accustomed myself to giving her, as it were quite casually, little scraps of useful information, or sound opinion--and she started from them when I let them off, as if they had been crackers.", "나는 짐짓 아주 무심한 척 그녀에게 쓸모 있는 정보 부스러기나 건전한 의견을 자잘하게 던져 주는 버릇을 들였다 — 그러면 내가 그것을 터뜨릴 때마다 그녀는 마치 폭죽이라도 터진 듯 화들짝 놀라며 물러섰다."], ["No matter how incidentally or naturally I endeavoured to form my little wife’s mind, I could not help seeing that she always had an instinctive perception of what I was about, and became a prey to the keenest apprehensions.", "아무리 우연인 척, 자연스러운 척 내 어린 아내의 마음을 빚어 보려 애써도, 그녀가 늘 본능적으로 내 속셈을 알아채고는 더없이 날카로운 불안에 사로잡힌다는 것을 나는 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["In particular, it was clear to me, that she thought Shakespeare a terrible fellow. The formation went on very slowly.", "특히 그녀가 셰익스피어를 끔찍한 사람으로 여긴다는 것만은 나에게 분명했다. 마음을 빚는 일은 더디기 짝이 없게 진행되었다."]], [["I pressed Traddles into the service without his knowledge; and whenever he came to see us, exploded my mines upon him for the edification of Dora at second hand.", "나는 트래들스를 본인도 모르게 이 일에 끌어들여, 그가 우리를 보러 올 때마다 도라를 간접적으로 교화할 셈으로 그를 향해 내 지뢰를 터뜨렸다."], ["The amount of practical wisdom I bestowed upon Traddles in this manner was immense, and of the best quality; but it had no other effect upon Dora than to depress her spirits, and make her always nervous with the dread that it would be her turn next.", "이런 식으로 내가 트래들스에게 베푼 실용적 지혜의 양은 엄청났고, 질도 최상급이었다. 그러나 그것이 도라에게 미친 영향이라곤, 그녀의 기분을 가라앉히고, 다음은 자기 차례일 거라는 두려움에 늘 안절부절못하게 만든 것뿐이었다."], ["I found myself in the condition of a schoolmaster, a trap, a pitfall; of always playing spider to Dora’s fly, and always pouncing out of my hole to her infinite disturbance.", "나는 어느새 내가 학교 선생, 덫, 함정 같은 존재가 되어, 도라라는 파리를 노리는 거미 노릇을 하며, 늘 구멍에서 불쑥 튀어나와 그녀를 한없이 어지럽히고 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["Still, looking forward through this intermediate stage, to the time when there should be a perfect sympathy between Dora and me, and when I should have ‘formed her mind’ to my entire satisfaction, I persevered, even for months.", "그래도 나는 이 중간 단계 너머로, 도라와 나 사이에 완벽한 공감이 이루어지고, 내가 그녀의 마음을 내 마음에 흡족하도록 '빚어' 놓을 그날을 내다보며, 몇 달이나 끈기 있게 버텼다."], ["Finding at last, however, that, although I had been all this time a very porcupine or hedgehog, bristling all over with determination, I had effected nothing, it began to occur to me that perhaps Dora’s mind was already formed.", "그러나 마침내 깨닫고 보니, 그 오랜 시간 내내 나는 결의로 온몸의 가시를 잔뜩 곤두세운 호저나 고슴도치 같은 꼴이었으면서도 이뤄 놓은 것이 하나도 없었다. 그러자 어쩌면 도라의 마음은 이미 다 빚어져 있는 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["On further consideration this appeared so likely, that I abandoned my scheme, which had had a more promising appearance in words than in action; resolving henceforth to be satisfied with my child-wife, and to try to change her into nothing else by any process.", "곰곰이 더 따져 보니 그럴 법한 일로 여겨졌기에, 나는 그 계획을 — 행동으로 옮길 때보다 말로 할 때 훨씬 그럴듯해 보였던 그 계획을 — 단념했다. 이제부터는 내 어린 아내에 만족하고, 어떤 방법으로도 그녀를 다른 무엇으로 바꾸려 들지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["I was heartily tired of being sagacious and prudent by myself, and of seeing my darling under restraint; so I bought a pretty pair of ear-rings for her, and a collar for Jip, and went home one day to make myself agreeable.", "혼자서 슬기롭고 신중한 척하는 데에도, 내 사랑하는 사람이 옴짝달싹 못 하는 모습을 지켜보는 데에도 진절머리가 났다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 위해 예쁜 귀걸이 한 쌍을, 집을 위해 목걸이 하나를 사 들고, 어느 날 집으로 돌아가 다정한 사람이 되기로 했다."]], [["Dora was delighted with the little presents, and kissed me joyfully; but there was a shadow between us, however slight, and I had made up my mind that it should not be there.", "도라는 그 자그마한 선물에 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르며 흥겹게 나에게 입을 맞췄다. 그러나 우리 사이에는, 아무리 희미할지언정 그늘이 하나 있었고, 나는 그것이 거기 있어서는 안 된다고 단단히 마음을 굳혔다."], ["If there must be such a shadow anywhere, I would keep it for the future in my own breast.", "어디엔가 그런 그늘이 꼭 있어야만 한다면, 나는 앞으로 그것을 내 가슴속에만 간직하리라."]], [["I sat down by my wife on the sofa, and put the ear-rings in her ears; and then I told her that I feared we had not been quite as good company lately, as we used to be, and that the fault was mine.", "나는 소파에 앉은 아내 곁에 앉아, 귀걸이를 그녀의 귀에 달아 주었다. 그러고 나서 요즘 우리가 예전만큼 좋은 동무가 되지 못한 것 같아 걱정인데, 그게 다 내 잘못이라고 말했다."], ["Which I sincerely felt, and which indeed it was.", "진심으로 그렇게 느꼈고, 또 사실이 그러했다."]], [["‘The truth is, Dora, my life,’ I said; ‘I have been trying to be wise.’", "\"실은 말이야, 도라, 내 사랑,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 슬기로운 사람이 되어 보려고 애써 왔어.\""]], [["‘And to make me wise too,’ said Dora, timidly.", "\"그리고 나까지 슬기롭게 만들려고요.\" 도라가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["‘Haven’t you, Doady?’", "\"그렇죠, 두디?\""]], [["I nodded assent to the pretty inquiry of the raised eyebrows, and kissed the parted lips.", "치켜올린 두 눈썹의 그 예쁜 물음에, 나는 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 답하고는, 살짝 벌어진 입술에 입을 맞췄다."]], [["‘It’s of not a bit of use,’ said Dora, shaking her head, until the ear-rings rang again.", "\"그래 봐야 손톱만큼도 소용없어요.\" 도라가 고개를 절레절레 흔드는 바람에 귀걸이가 다시 짤랑거렸다."], ["‘You know what a little thing I am, and what I wanted you to call me from the first.", "\"내가 얼마나 하찮은 사람인지, 처음부터 당신이 나를 뭐라고 불러 주길 바랐는지 잘 알잖아요."], ["If you can’t do so, I am afraid you’ll never like me. Are you sure you don’t think, sometimes, it would have been better to have--’", "그렇게 해 주지 못한다면, 당신은 영영 나를 좋아하지 못할까 봐 걱정이에요. 정말로 가끔, 차라리 이러는 편이 나았을 거라고 생각하지 않아요 —\""]], [["‘Done what, my dear?’ For she made no effort to proceed.", "\"뭘 하는 편이, 여보?\" 그녀가 더는 말을 잇지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["‘Nothing!’ said Dora. ‘Nothing?’ I repeated.", "\"아무것도 아니에요!\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["She put her arms round my neck, and laughed, and called herself by her favourite name of a goose, and hid her face on my shoulder in such a profusion of curls that it was quite a task to clear them away and see it.", "\"아무것도 아니라고?\" 내가 되물었다. 그녀는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고는 웃음을 터뜨리며, 자기가 좋아하는 별명대로 스스로를 거위라고 부르고는, 곱슬머리를 어찌나 풍성하게 늘어뜨려 내 어깨에 얼굴을 묻던지, 그것을 헤치고 얼굴을 보는 것이 여간 큰일이 아니었다."]], [["‘Don’t I think it would have been better to have done nothing, than to have tried to form my little wife’s mind?’ said I, laughing at myself.", "\"내 어린 아내의 마음을 빚으려 애쓰느니, 차라리 아무것도 하지 않는 편이 나았을 거라고 생각하지 않느냐고?\" 나는 나 자신을 비웃으며 말했다."], ["‘Is that the question?", "\"그게 묻고 싶은 거야?"], ["Yes, indeed, I do.’", "그래, 정말이지 그래.\""]], [["‘Is that what you have been trying?’ cried Dora.", "\"그게 당신이 애써 온 일이었어요?\" 도라가 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh what a shocking boy!’", "\"어머, 못된 사람 같으니!\""]], [["‘But I shall never try any more,’ said I.", "\"하지만 다시는 그러지 않을 거야.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘For I love her dearly as she is.’", "\"나는 지금 그대로의 그녀를 끔찍이 사랑하니까.\""]], [["‘Without a story--really?’ inquired Dora, creeping closer to me.", "\"거짓말 빼고 — 정말로요?\" 도라가 나에게 더 바짝 파고들며 물었다."]], [["‘Why should I seek to change,’ said I, ‘what has been so precious to me for so long!", "\"오랫동안 나에게 그토록 소중했던 것을,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 왜 바꾸려 들겠어!"], ["You never can show better than as your own natural self, my sweet Dora; and we’ll try no conceited experiments, but go back to our old way, and be happy.’", "너는 타고난 네 모습일 때보다 더 멋질 수가 없어, 사랑스러운 도라. 우리는 이제 잘난 척하는 실험 따위는 그만두고, 옛날 우리 방식으로 돌아가 행복하게 지내자.\""]], [["‘And be happy!’ returned Dora. ‘Yes! All day!", "\"행복하게 지내요!\" 도라가 받아쳤다. \"그래요!"], ["And you won’t mind things going a tiny morsel wrong, sometimes?’", "하루 종일! 그리고 가끔 일이 아주 쪼끔 틀어져도 신경 쓰지 않을 거죠?\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said I.", "\"안 쓰지, 안 써.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘We must do the best we can.’", "\"우린 그저 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다하면 돼.\""]], [["‘And you won’t tell me, any more, that we make other people bad,’ coaxed Dora; ‘will you?", "\"그리고 우리가 다른 사람들을 못되게 만든다는 말은 다시 안 할 거죠,\" 도라가 어리광을 부렸다. \"그렇죠?"], ["Because you know it’s so dreadfully cross!’", "그건 정말이지 너무 끔찍하게 짜증 나는 소리란 말이에요!\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said I.", "\"안 해, 안 해.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It’s better for me to be stupid than uncomfortable, isn’t it?’ said Dora.", "\"내가 불편한 것보다는 어리석은 편이 더 낫죠, 그렇죠?\" 도라가 말했다."]], [["‘Better to be naturally Dora than anything else in the world.’", "\"세상 그 무엇보다, 타고난 도라 그대로인 편이 낫지.\""]], [["‘In the world!", "\"세상이라니!"], ["Ah, Doady, it’s a large place!’", "아, 두디, 세상은 넓은 곳이잖아요!\""]], [["She shook her head, turned her delighted bright eyes up to mine, kissed me, broke into a merry laugh, and sprang away to put on Jip’s new collar.", "그녀는 고개를 흔들고는, 기쁨에 빛나는 두 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보며, 나에게 입을 맞추고, 까르르 즐겁게 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 집의 새 목걸이를 채워 주려고 깡충 뛰어가 버렸다."]], [["So ended my last attempt to make any change in Dora.", "이렇게 도라를 조금이라도 바꿔 보려던 나의 마지막 시도는 막을 내렸다."], ["I had been unhappy in trying it; I could not endure my own solitary wisdom; I could not reconcile it with her former appeal to me as my child-wife.", "그것을 시도하면서 나는 불행했다. 나 홀로 슬기로운 척하는 것을 견딜 수 없었고, 또 그것을 나의 어린 아내로서 일찍이 나에게 매달리던 그녀의 모습과 도무지 조화시킬 수 없었다."], ["I resolved to do what I could, in a quiet way, to improve our proceedings myself, but I foresaw that my utmost would be very little, or I must degenerate into the spider again, and be for ever lying in wait.", "나는 조용한 방식으로 우리 살림을 나 스스로 나아지게 할 수 있는 만큼 해 보기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 내가 아무리 애를 써 봐야 그 결과는 보잘것없으리라는 것을, 그러지 않으려면 다시 거미로 전락해 영영 잠복해 기다리는 신세가 되리라는 것을 나는 내다보았다."]], [["And the shadow I have mentioned, that was not to be between us any more, but was to rest wholly on my own heart?", "그리고 내가 앞서 말한 그늘, 다시는 우리 사이에 두지 않고 온전히 내 가슴 위에만 얹어 두기로 한 그 그늘은 어찌 되었던가?"], ["How did that fall?", "그것은 어떻게 드리워졌던가?"]], [["The old unhappy feeling pervaded my life.", "그 오래된 불행한 느낌이 내 삶에 스며들었다."], ["It was deepened, if it were changed at all; but it was as undefined as ever, and addressed me like a strain of sorrowful music faintly heard in the night.", "변했다 해도 그저 더 깊어졌을 뿐이었고, 여전히 종잡을 수 없는 채로, 마치 밤중에 어렴풋이 들려오는 한 가락 구슬픈 음악처럼 나에게 말을 걸어왔다."], ["I loved my wife dearly, and I was happy; but the happiness I had vaguely anticipated, once, was not the happiness I enjoyed, and there was always something wanting.", "나는 아내를 끔찍이 사랑했고, 행복했다. 그러나 한때 내가 어렴풋이 그려 보았던 그 행복은 내가 누리는 행복이 아니었으며, 늘 무언가 모자란 데가 있었다."]], [["In fulfilment of the compact I have made with myself, to reflect my mind on this paper, I again examine it, closely, and bring its secrets to the light.", "내 마음을 이 종이 위에 비추어 내겠다고 나 자신과 맺은 약속을 지키고자, 나는 다시 그것을 면밀히 들여다보며 그 비밀을 빛 가운데로 끄집어낸다."], ["What I missed, I still regarded--I always regarded--as something that had been a dream of my youthful fancy; that was incapable of realization; that I was now discovering to be so, with some natural pain, as all men did.", "내가 아쉬워했던 것을, 나는 여전히 — 늘 — 젊은 날의 환상이 빚어낸 한낱 꿈이었다고 여겼다. 결코 이루어질 수 없는 것, 모든 사람이 그러하듯 어느 정도 자연스러운 아픔과 더불어 그것이 그러하다는 것을 이제 깨달아 가는 중이라고 말이다."], ["But that it would have been better for me if my wife could have helped me more, and shared the many thoughts in which I had no partner; and that this might have been; I knew.", "그러나 내 아내가 나를 더 도와줄 수 있었더라면, 나에게 동무가 없던 그 숱한 생각들을 함께 나눠 줄 수 있었더라면 나에게 더 나았으리라는 것, 또 그럴 수도 있었으리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었다."]], [["Between these two irreconcilable conclusions: the one, that what I felt was general and unavoidable; the other, that it was particular to me, and might have been different: I balanced curiously, with no distinct sense of their opposition to each other.", "서로 화해할 수 없는 이 두 결론 사이에서 — 하나는 내가 느끼는 것이 누구에게나 흔한, 피할 수 없는 일이라는 것, 다른 하나는 그것이 나에게만 고유한 일이며 달라질 수도 있었으리라는 것 — 나는 둘이 서로 어긋난다는 또렷한 자각도 없이 묘하게 갈팡질팡했다."], ["When I thought of the airy dreams of youth that are incapable of realization, I thought of the better state preceding manhood that I had outgrown; and then the contented days with Agnes, in the dear old house, arose before me, like spectres of the dead, that might have some renewal in another world, but never more could be reanimated here.", "이루어질 수 없는 젊은 날의 부질없는 꿈을 떠올리면, 어느새 자라나 벗어 버린, 어른이 되기 직전의 더 나은 시절이 떠올랐다. 그러면 정든 옛집에서 아그네스와 함께 보낸 그 흡족하던 날들이, 죽은 자들의 망령처럼 내 앞에 솟아올랐다 — 다른 세상에서라면 어떤 식으로든 되살아날지 모르나, 이승에서는 다시는 소생할 수 없는 망령처럼."]], [["Sometimes, the speculation came into my thoughts, What might have happened, or what would have happened, if Dora and I had never known each other?", "이따금 이런 생각이 머릿속에 떠오르곤 했다. 도라와 내가 서로를 영영 몰랐더라면 어떤 일이 벌어졌을까, 혹은 벌어졌을까?"], ["But she was so incorporated with my existence, that it was the idlest of all fancies, and would soon rise out of my reach and sight, like gossamer floating in the air.", "그러나 그녀는 내 존재와 너무도 한 몸으로 엮여 있어서, 그것은 온갖 부질없는 공상 가운데서도 가장 부질없는 것이었고, 이내 허공에 떠다니는 거미줄처럼 내 손길과 시야 너머로 솟아올라 사라져 버리곤 했다."]], [["I always loved her.", "나는 언제나 그녀를 사랑했다."], ["What I am describing, slumbered, and half awoke, and slept again, in the innermost recesses of my mind.", "내가 여기 적고 있는 이 느낌은 내 마음 가장 깊은 구석에서 잠들었다가, 반쯤 깨어났다가, 다시 잠들곤 했다."], ["There was no evidence of it in me; I know of no influence it had in anything I said or did.", "그것이 내 안에 있다는 흔적은 겉으로 드러나지 않았으며, 그것이 내가 한 말이나 행동에 무슨 영향을 끼쳤는지 나는 알지 못한다."], ["I bore the weight of all our little cares, and all my projects; Dora held the pens; and we both felt that our shares were adjusted as the case required.", "우리의 자잘한 근심거리며 내 모든 계획의 무게는 모두 내가 짊어졌고, 도라는 펜을 들었다. 그리고 우리 둘 다 우리 몫이 형편에 맞게 잘 나누어졌다고 느꼈다."], ["She was truly fond of me, and proud of me; and when Agnes wrote a few earnest words in her letters to Dora, of the pride and interest with which my old friends heard of my growing reputation, and read my book as if they heard me speaking its contents, Dora read them out to me with tears of joy in her bright eyes, and said I was a dear old clever, famous boy.", "그녀는 진심으로 나를 좋아했고, 나를 자랑스러워했다. 아그네스가 도라에게 보내는 편지에 몇 마디 진심 어린 말을 적어 — 나의 옛 친구들이 나날이 높아 가는 내 명성을 전해 듣고, 또 내 책을 마치 내가 그 내용을 직접 말해 주기라도 하는 양 읽으면서 품은 그 자랑스러움과 관심을 적어 — 보내면, 도라는 빛나는 두 눈에 기쁨의 눈물을 글썽이며 그것을 나에게 소리 내어 읽어 주고는, 나더러 정답고 똑똑하고 유명한 양반이라고 했다."]], [["‘The first mistaken impulse of an undisciplined heart.’ Those words of Mrs.", "\"단련되지 못한 마음이 처음으로 잘못 내디딘 충동.\""], ["Strong’s were constantly recurring to me, at this time; were almost always present to my mind.", "스트롱 부인의 그 말이 이 무렵 나에게 끊임없이 되살아났다. 거의 늘 내 마음속에 자리하고 있었다."], ["I awoke with them, often, in the night; I remember to have even read them, in dreams, inscribed upon the walls of houses.", "나는 밤중에 종종 그 말과 함께 잠에서 깼고, 심지어 꿈속에서 그 말이 집 벽에 새겨진 것을 읽은 기억까지 난다."], ["For I knew, now, that my own heart was undisciplined when it first loved Dora; and that if it had been disciplined, it never could have felt, when we were married, what it had felt in its secret experience.", "이제 나는 알았기 때문이다 — 내 마음이 처음 도라를 사랑했을 때 그 마음은 단련되지 못한 것이었고, 만약 단련된 마음이었더라면 결혼한 뒤에, 그 은밀한 경험 속에서 느꼈던 것을 결코 느끼지 못했으리라는 것을."]], [["‘There can be no disparity in marriage, like unsuitability of mind and purpose.’ Those words I remembered too.", "\"마음과 뜻이 맞지 않는 것만큼 결혼에 큰 어긋남은 없다.\" 그 말도 나는 기억했다."], ["I had endeavoured to adapt Dora to myself, and found it impracticable.", "나는 도라를 나에게 맞추려 애써 보았으나, 그것이 불가능하다는 것을 알았다."], ["It remained for me to adapt myself to Dora; to share with her what I could, and be happy; to bear on my own shoulders what I must, and be happy still.", "이제 남은 것은 나를 도라에게 맞추는 일이었다 — 내가 나눌 수 있는 것을 그녀와 나누며 행복해하고, 내가 짊어져야 할 것을 내 어깨에 지고서도 여전히 행복해하는 일이었다."], ["This was the discipline to which I tried to bring my heart, when I began to think.", "이것이 내가 생각이란 것을 하기 시작했을 때 내 마음을 데려가려 한 단련이었다."], ["It made my second year much happier than my first; and, what was better still, made Dora’s life all sunshine.", "그것은 나의 둘째 해를 첫해보다 훨씬 행복하게 만들어 주었고, 더더욱 좋았던 것은, 도라의 삶을 온통 햇살로 채워 주었다는 점이다."]], [["But, as that year wore on, Dora was not strong.", "그러나 그해가 저물어 갈수록 도라는 몸이 튼튼하지 못했다."], ["I had hoped that lighter hands than mine would help to mould her character, and that a baby-smile upon her breast might change my child-wife to a woman. It was not to be.", "나는 나보다 더 부드러운 손길이 그녀의 성품을 빚는 일을 도와주기를, 그녀의 가슴에 안긴 아기의 미소가 나의 어린 아내를 한 여인으로 바꾸어 주기를 바랐다. 그러나 그렇게 될 운명이 아니었다."], ["The spirit fluttered for a moment on the threshold of its little prison, and, unconscious of captivity, took wing.", "그 어린 영혼은 자그마한 감옥의 문턱에서 한순간 파닥이더니, 갇혀 있었다는 것조차 모른 채 날개를 펴고 날아가 버렸다."]], [["‘When I can run about again, as I used to do, aunt,’ said Dora, ‘I shall make Jip race.", "\"내가 다시 예전처럼 뛰어다닐 수 있게 되면, 고모님,\" 도라가 말했다. \"집을 막 달리게 할 거예요."], ["He is getting quite slow and lazy.’", "요즘 영 느려 터지고 게을러졌거든요.\""]], [["‘I suspect, my dear,’ said my aunt quietly working by her side, ‘he has a worse disorder than that.", "\"내 보기엔 말이다, 얘야,\" 고모님이 그녀 곁에서 조용히 바느질을 하며 말했다. \"저 녀석은 그것보다 더 고약한 병이 든 게야."], ["Age, Dora.’", "나이라는 병이지, 도라.\""]], [["‘Do you think he is old?’ said Dora, astonished.", "\"저 애가 늙었다고 생각하세요?\" 도라가 놀라 물었다."], ["‘Oh, how strange it seems that Jip should be old!’", "\"어머, 집이 늙었다니, 어쩜 그렇게 이상한 일이 다 있어요!\""]], [["‘It’s a complaint we are all liable to, Little One, as we get on in life,’ said my aunt, cheerfully; ‘I don’t feel more free from it than I used to be, I assure you.’", "\"그건 우리 모두가 살아가면서 걸리게 마련인 병이란다, 우리 아가,\" 고모님이 명랑하게 말했다. \"나도 예전보다 그 병에서 더 자유로워졌다고는 말 못 하지, 정말로.\""]], [["‘But Jip,’ said Dora, looking at him with compassion, ‘even little Jip!", "\"하지만 집이,\" 도라가 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"우리 조그만 집이까지!"], ["Oh, poor fellow!’", "아아, 가엾어라!\""]], [["‘I dare say he’ll last a long time yet, Blossom,’ said my aunt, patting Dora on the cheek, as she leaned out of her couch to look at Jip, who responded by standing on his hind legs, and baulking himself in various asthmatic attempts to scramble up by the head and shoulders.", "\"내 보기엔 아직 한참 더 살 것 같구나, 우리 꽃송이,\" 고모님이 도라의 뺨을 토닥이며 말했다. 도라는 집을 보려고 긴 의자에서 몸을 내밀고 있었는데, 집은 거기에 응답하느라 뒷다리로 일어서서, 머리와 어깨로 기어오르려고 천식 걸린 듯 헐떡이며 이런저런 시도를 하다가 번번이 제풀에 주저앉았다."], ["‘He must have a piece of flannel in his house this winter, and I shouldn’t wonder if he came out quite fresh again, with the flowers in the spring.", "\"올겨울엔 저 녀석 집에 플란넬 천 한 조각을 깔아 줘야겠다. 그러면 봄에 꽃들이 필 무렵엔 다시 아주 팔팔해져서 나온대도 놀랍지 않을 게야."], ["Bless the little dog!’ exclaimed my aunt, ‘if he had as many lives as a cat, and was on the point of losing ‘em all, he’d bark at me with his last breath, I believe!’", "저 작은 강아지 녀석!\" 고모님이 외쳤다. \"고양이만큼 목숨이 여럿이고 그 목숨을 죄다 잃기 직전이라 해도, 마지막 숨을 내쉬면서까지 나한테 짖어 댈 녀석이라니까, 정말로!\""]], [["Dora had helped him up on the sofa; where he really was defying my aunt to such a furious extent, that he couldn’t keep straight, but barked himself sideways.", "도라가 집을 소파 위로 끌어 올려 주었는데, 거기서 집은 정말이지 어찌나 사납게 고모님에게 대들던지, 똑바로 서 있지도 못하고 옆으로 비틀거리며 짖어 댔다."], ["The more my aunt looked at him, the more he reproached her; for she had lately taken to spectacles, and for some inscrutable reason he considered the glasses personal.", "고모님이 그를 바라보면 바라볼수록 그는 더욱 그녀를 나무랐다. 고모님이 요즘 들어 안경을 쓰기 시작했는데, 무슨 알 수 없는 까닭에서인지 그는 그 안경알을 자기에 대한 모욕으로 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Dora made him lie down by her, with a good deal of persuasion; and when he was quiet, drew one of his long ears through and through her hand, repeating thoughtfully, ‘Even little Jip!", "도라는 한참을 구슬려 가며 집을 자기 곁에 눕혔다. 그가 잠잠해지자, 그녀는 그의 긴 귀 한쪽을 손으로 자꾸자꾸 쓸어내리며, 골똘히 되뇌었다. \"우리 조그만 집이까지!"], ["Oh, poor fellow!’", "아아, 가엾어라!\""]], [["‘His lungs are good enough,’ said my aunt, gaily, ‘and his dislikes are not at all feeble.", "\"폐는 멀쩡하구먼,\" 고모님이 쾌활하게 말했다. \"싫어하는 것 미워하는 기운도 전혀 시들지 않았고."], ["He has a good many years before him, no doubt.", "틀림없이 앞으로 여러 해는 더 살 게다."], ["But if you want a dog to race with, Little Blossom, he has lived too well for that, and I’ll give you one.’", "하지만 함께 달릴 강아지가 갖고 싶다면, 우리 작은 꽃송이야, 저 녀석은 너무 호강하며 살아서 그건 안 되겠으니, 내가 한 마리 구해 주마.\""]], [["‘Thank you, aunt,’ said Dora, faintly.", "\"고마워요, 고모님,\" 도라가 가냘프게 말했다."], ["‘But don’t, please!’", "\"하지만 제발 그러지 마세요!\""]], [["‘No?’ said my aunt, taking off her spectacles.", "\"안 된다고?\" 고모님이 안경을 벗으며 말했다."]], [["‘I couldn’t have any other dog but Jip,’ said Dora.", "\"저는 집 말고 다른 강아지는 못 키워요.\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘It would be so unkind to Jip!", "\"그건 집한테 너무 못된 일이잖아요!"], ["Besides, I couldn’t be such friends with any other dog but Jip; because he wouldn’t have known me before I was married, and wouldn’t have barked at Doady when he first came to our house.", "게다가 저는 집 말고 다른 강아지하곤 그렇게 친구가 될 수 없어요. 다른 강아지는 제가 결혼하기 전의 저를 알지 못했을 테고, 두디가 우리 집에 처음 왔을 때 짖어 주지도 않았을 테니까요."], ["I couldn’t care for any other dog but Jip, I am afraid, aunt.’", "집 말고 다른 강아지는 도무지 마음이 안 갈 것 같아요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘To be sure!’ said my aunt, patting her cheek again.", "\"그렇고말고!\" 고모님이 다시 그녀의 뺨을 토닥이며 말했다."], ["‘You are right.’", "\"네 말이 옳다.\""]], [["‘You are not offended,’ said Dora.", "\"화나신 거 아니죠.\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘Are you?’", "\"그렇죠?\""]], [["‘Why, what a sensitive pet it is!’ cried my aunt, bending over her affectionately.", "\"아니, 이렇게 여린 우리 아가가 다 있나!\" 고모님이 다정하게 그녀에게 몸을 숙이며 외쳤다."], ["‘To think that I could be offended!’", "\"내가 화를 내다니, 그런 생각을 다 하다니!\""]], [["‘No, no, I didn’t really think so,’ returned Dora; ‘but I am a little tired, and it made me silly for a moment--I am always a silly little thing, you know, but it made me more silly--to talk about Jip.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요, 정말로 그렇게 생각한 건 아니에요.\" 도라가 받아쳤다. \"그저 조금 피곤해서, 잠깐 바보같이 군 거예요 — 저야 늘 바보 같은 조그만 사람이지만, 집 얘기를 하니까 더 바보가 된 거예요."], ["He has known me in all that has happened to me, haven’t you, Jip?", "집은 저한테 일어난 모든 일을 다 겪으면서 저를 알아 왔잖아요, 그렇지, 집?"], ["And I couldn’t bear to slight him, because he was a little altered--could I, Jip?’", "그런데 저 애가 좀 변했다고 해서 저 애를 푸대접한다는 건 견딜 수가 없어요 — 그렇지, 집?\""]], [["Jip nestled closer to his mistress, and lazily licked her hand.", "집은 제 주인 곁에 더 바짝 파고들어, 나른하게 그녀의 손을 핥았다."]], [["‘You are not so old, Jip, are you, that you’ll leave your mistress yet?’ said Dora.", "\"너 그렇게 늙진 않았지, 집, 그렇지? 벌써 주인을 두고 떠나 버릴 만큼은 아니지?\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘We may keep one another company a little longer!’", "\"우리 조금 더 오래 서로 동무가 되어 줄 수 있겠지!\""]], [["My pretty Dora!", "내 어여쁜 도라!"], ["When she came down to dinner on the ensuing Sunday, and was so glad to see old Traddles (who always dined with us on Sunday), we thought she would be ‘running about as she used to do’, in a few days.", "이튿날 일요일에 그녀가 저녁 식사 자리에 내려와, 그 늙은 트래들스를 (그는 일요일마다 늘 우리와 함께 식사했다) 보고 어찌나 기뻐하던지, 우리는 그녀가 며칠만 지나면 '예전처럼 뛰어다니게' 되리라 여겼다."], ["But they said, wait a few days more; and then, wait a few days more; and still she neither ran nor walked.", "그러나 며칠만 더 기다려 보라고들 했고, 그러고는 또 며칠만 더 기다려 보라고들 했다. 그래도 그녀는 뛰지도 걷지도 못했다."], ["She looked very pretty, and was very merry; but the little feet that used to be so nimble when they danced round Jip, were dull and motionless.", "그녀는 무척 예뻐 보였고, 무척 명랑했다. 그러나 집의 둘레를 돌며 춤출 때면 그토록 날래던 그 자그마한 두 발은, 이제 둔하게 멈춰 있었다."]], [["I began to carry her downstairs every morning, and upstairs every night.", "나는 아침마다 그녀를 안아 아래층으로 데리고 내려가고, 밤마다 안아 위층으로 데리고 올라가기 시작했다."], ["She would clasp me round the neck and laugh, the while, as if I did it for a wager.", "그러는 동안 그녀는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고는, 내가 무슨 내기라도 걸고 하는 양 웃어 댔다."], ["Jip would bark and caper round us, and go on before, and look back on the landing, breathing short, to see that we were coming.", "집은 우리 둘레를 짖으며 깡충깡충 뛰다가 앞장서 나아가서는, 우리가 따라오는지 보려고 층계참에서 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 뒤를 돌아보곤 했다."], ["My aunt, the best and most cheerful of nurses, would trudge after us, a moving mass of shawls and pillows. Mr.", "고모님은, 더없이 훌륭하고 더없이 쾌활한 간병인답게, 숄과 베개를 한 무더기 움직이며 우리 뒤를 터덜터덜 따라왔다."], ["Dick would not have relinquished his post of candle-bearer to anyone alive.", "딕 씨는 촛불 드는 자리를 이 세상 그 누구에게도 결코 내주려 하지 않았다."], ["Traddles would be often at the bottom of the staircase, looking on, and taking charge of sportive messages from Dora to the dearest girl in the world.", "트래들스는 곧잘 계단 아래에 서서 그 광경을 지켜보며, 도라가 세상에서 제일 사랑하는 아가씨에게 보내는 장난스러운 전갈을 맡아 주곤 했다."], ["We made quite a gay procession of it, and my child-wife was the gayest there.", "우리는 그것을 자못 흥겨운 행렬로 만들었고, 그 가운데서 나의 어린 아내가 가장 흥겨운 사람이었다."]], [["But, sometimes, when I took her up, and felt that she was lighter in my arms, a dead blank feeling came upon me, as if I were approaching to some frozen region yet unseen, that numbed my life.", "그러나 이따금, 그녀를 안아 올리면서 두 팔에 안긴 그녀가 더 가벼워졌다는 것을 느낄 때면, 죽은 듯한 텅 빈 느낌이 나를 덮쳐 왔다. 마치 아직 보이지 않는 어떤 얼어붙은 땅에 다가가고 있어, 그것이 내 삶을 마비시키는 것만 같았다."], ["I avoided the recognition of this feeling by any name, or by any communing with myself; until one night, when it was very strong upon me, and my aunt had left her with a parting cry of ‘Good night, Little Blossom,’ I sat down at my desk alone, and tried to think, Oh what a fatal name it was, and how the blossom withered in its bloom upon the tree!", "나는 그 느낌을 어떤 이름으로 부르거나 나 자신과 마주 보고 따져 보기를 애써 피했다. 그러던 어느 날 밤, 그 느낌이 몹시도 강하게 나를 짓누르고, 고모님이 \"잘 자라, 우리 작은 꽃송이\"라는 작별 인사를 남기고 그녀 곁을 떠났을 때, 나는 홀로 책상 앞에 앉아 생각해 보려 했다. 아아, 그것은 얼마나 불길한 이름이었던가, 그리고 그 꽃송이는 가지에 핀 채로 어찌 그리도 시들어 갔던가!"]], [["# CHAPTER 49. I AM INVOLVED IN MYSTERY", "# CHAPTER 49. I AM INVOLVED IN MYSTERY"]], [["I received one morning by the post, the following letter, dated Canterbury, and addressed to me at Doctor’s Commons; which I read with some surprise:", "내가 어느 날 아침 우편으로 받은 편지는 다음과 같았는데, 캔터베리에서 부친 것으로, 박사회관에 있는 나에게로 온 것이었다. 나는 다소 놀라며 이 편지를 읽었다."]], [["‘Circumstances beyond my individual control have, for a considerable lapse of time, effected a severance of that intimacy which, in the limited opportunities conceded to me in the midst of my professional duties, of contemplating the scenes and events of the past, tinged by the prismatic hues of memory, has ever afforded me, as it ever must continue to afford, gratifying emotions of no common description.", "\"친애하는 선생께,\n저 개인의 힘으로는 어찌할 수 없는 사정들이, 상당한 세월에 걸쳐, 그 친밀함을 끊어 놓았으니, 이 친밀함이란 직무에 매인 와중에 저에게 허락된 한정된 기회 가운데, 기억의 프리즘 빛깔로 물든 지난날의 정경과 사건들을 돌이켜 볼 적마다 저에게 늘 안겨 주었던 것이요, 앞으로도 늘 안겨 줄 수밖에 없는 것으로, 결코 흔치 않은 종류의 흐뭇한 정서를 일으켜 주는 것입니다."], ["This fact, my dear sir, combined with the distinguished elevation to which your talents have raised you, deters me from presuming to aspire to the liberty of addressing the companion of my youth, by the familiar appellation of Copperfield!", "친애하는 선생, 이 사실에다가, 선생의 재능이 선생을 끌어올려 놓은 그 빼어난 지위까지 더해지고 보니, 저는 감히 제 젊은 시절의 벗을 코퍼필드라는 그 정겨운 호칭으로 부르고자 하는 자유를 함부로 넘보지 못하게 됩니다!"], ["It is sufficient to know that the name to which I do myself the honour to refer, will ever be treasured among the muniments of our house (I allude to the archives connected with our former lodgers, preserved by Mrs. Micawber), with sentiments of personal esteem amounting to affection.", "제가 외람되이 입에 올리는 그 이름이, 우리 집안의 문서고 가운데 (저는 우리 옛 하숙인들과 관련하여 미코버 부인이 보존하고 있는 기록을 가리키는 것입니다) 애정에 다다른 개인적 존경의 정과 더불어 언제까지나 소중히 간직되리라는 것을 아는 것만으로 충분합니다."]], [["‘It is not for one, situated, through his original errors and a fortuitous combination of unpropitious events, as is the foundered Bark (if he may be allowed to assume so maritime a denomination), who now takes up the pen to address you--it is not, I repeat, for one so circumstanced, to adopt the language of compliment, or of congratulation.", "자신의 본래 과오와, 상서롭지 못한 사건들의 우연한 결합으로 말미암아, 좌초한 배(이토록 바다 냄새 짙은 명칭을 제 몸에 갖다 붙이는 것을 허락해 주신다면 말입니다)와 같은 처지에 놓인 한 사람이, 지금 펜을 들어 선생께 글을 올리거니와—거듭 말씀드리거니와, 이런 처지에 놓인 사람으로서, 찬사의 언어나 축하의 언어를 늘어놓는 것은 가당치 않은 일입니다."], ["That he leaves to abler and to purer hands.", "그런 것은 더 유능하고 더 정결한 손에 맡기겠습니다."]], [["‘If your more important avocations should admit of your ever tracing these imperfect characters thus far--which may be, or may not be, as circumstances arise--you will naturally inquire by what object am I influenced, then, in inditing the present missive?", "선생의 더 중요한 일과가 혹시라도 이 못난 글자들을 여기까지 더듬어 내려오는 것을 허락한다면—이는 사정이 어찌 되느냐에 따라 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠습니다만—선생은 자연스레, 그렇다면 대체 무슨 목적으로 제가 이 글월을 적고 있는지 물으실 것입니다."], ["Allow me to say that I fully defer to the reasonable character of that inquiry, and proceed to develop it; premising that it is not an object of a pecuniary nature.", "그 물음이 지당하다는 데에 제가 온전히 따른다는 것을 말씀드리며, 그것을 풀어 나가겠습니다. 다만 그것이 금전과 관련된 목적은 아니라는 것을 먼저 밝혀 둡니다."]], [["‘Without more directly referring to any latent ability that may possibly exist on my part, of wielding the thunderbolt, or directing the devouring and avenging flame in any quarter, I may be permitted to observe, in passing, that my brightest visions are for ever dispelled--that my peace is shattered and my power of enjoyment destroyed--that my heart is no longer in the right place--and that I no more walk erect before my fellow man.", "제 안에 혹시라도 존재할지 모르는, 벼락을 휘두르거나 집어삼키며 복수하는 화염을 어느 방향으로든 향하게 할 잠재된 능력을 더 직접적으로 언급하지는 않더라도, 지나가는 말로 이 정도는 말씀드리는 것이 허락되겠지요. 저의 가장 빛나던 환상은 영영 흩어져 버렸고—저의 평화는 산산이 깨졌으며 즐길 수 있는 힘은 무너졌고—저의 심장은 더는 제자리에 있지 않으며—저는 더 이상 동포 앞에서 떳떳이 허리를 펴고 걷지 못한다는 것입니다."], ["The canker is in the flower.", "꽃 속에 좀이 들었습니다."], ["The cup is bitter to the brim.", "잔은 가장자리까지 쓰디씁니다."], ["The worm is at his work, and will soon dispose of his victim. The sooner the better.", "벌레가 제 일을 하고 있으니, 머지않아 그 제물을 처리해 버릴 것입니다."], ["But I will not digress.", "빠르면 빠를수록 좋겠지요."], ["‘Placed in a mental position of peculiar painfulness, beyond the assuaging reach even of Mrs. Micawber’s influence, though exercised in the tripartite character of woman, wife, and mother, it is my intention to fly from myself for a short period, and devote a respite of eight-and-forty hours to revisiting some metropolitan scenes of past enjoyment.", "그러나 옆길로 새지는 않겠습니다. 미코버 부인의 영향력으로도—그것이 여인이자 아내이자 어머니라는 삼중의 역할로 발휘된다 한들—누그러뜨릴 수 없는 곳에 이를 만큼 유난히 고통스러운 정신적 처지에 놓인 저는, 잠시 저 자신에게서 달아나, 사십팔 시간의 말미를 내어, 지난날 즐거움을 누리던 대도시의 어떤 정경들을 다시 찾아보고자 합니다."], ["Among other havens of domestic tranquillity and peace of mind, my feet will naturally tend towards the King’s Bench Prison.", "가정의 평온과 마음의 안식이 깃든 여러 안식처 가운데서도, 제 발길은 자연스레 킹스벤치 감옥을 향할 것입니다."], ["In stating that I shall be (D. V.) on the outside of the south wall of that place of incarceration on civil process, the day after tomorrow, at seven in the evening, precisely, my object in this epistolary communication is accomplished.", "모레 저녁 일곱 시 정각에, 민사 절차로 사람을 가두는 그곳의 남쪽 담장 바깥에 제가 (하느님의 뜻이라면) 있으리라는 것을 말씀드림으로써, 이 서신을 통한 전달의 목적은 이루어진 셈입니다."]], [["‘I do not feel warranted in soliciting my former friend Mr. Copperfield, or my former friend Mr. Thomas Traddles of the Inner Temple, if that gentleman is still existent and forthcoming, to condescend to meet me, and renew (so far as may be) our past relations of the olden time.", "저는 옛 벗인 코퍼필드 씨께, 또는 옛 벗인 이너템플의 토머스 트래들스 씨께—그분이 아직 살아 계셔서 만날 수 있다면 말입니다만—황송하게도 저를 만나 주시어, (가능한 한) 지난 옛 시절의 관계를 다시 이어 주십사 청할 자격이 제게 있다고는 느끼지 않습니다."], ["I confine myself to throwing out the observation, that, at the hour and place I have indicated, may be found such ruined vestiges as yet", "다만 제가 일러 드린 그 시각과 장소에 가면, 다음과 같이 남아 있는, 폐허가 된 자취를 보실 수 있으리라는 한마디를 던져 둘 따름입니다."]], [["‘Remain, ‘Of ‘A ‘Fallen Tower, ‘WILKINS MICAWBER.", "'무너진 '탑의 '잔해로 '남아 있는, '윌킨스 미코버."]], [["‘P.S.", "추신."], ["It may be advisable to superadd to the above, the statement that Mrs. Micawber is not in confidential possession of my intentions.’", "위 내용에다, 미코버 부인은 저의 의도를 은밀히 알고 있지 않다는 사실을 덧붙여 두는 것이 좋을 듯합니다.\""]], [["I read the letter over several times.", "나는 그 편지를 몇 번이고 거듭 읽었다."], ["Making due allowance for Mr. Micawber’s lofty style of composition, and for the extraordinary relish with which he sat down and wrote long letters on all possible and impossible occasions, I still believed that something important lay hidden at the bottom of this roundabout communication.", "미코버 씨 특유의 거창한 문장 솜씨를 충분히 감안하고, 또 그가 가능한 경우든 불가능한 경우든 가리지 않고 책상에 앉아 긴 편지를 써 내려가며 느끼는 그 유별난 흥취를 헤아려 보더라도, 나는 여전히 이 빙빙 둘러대는 글의 밑바닥에 뭔가 중요한 것이 숨어 있다고 믿었다."], ["I put it down, to think about it; and took it up again, to read it once more; and was still pursuing it, when Traddles found me in the height of my perplexity.", "나는 편지를 내려놓고 곰곰이 생각해 보았다가, 다시 집어 들어 한 번 더 읽었다. 그렇게 여전히 그 뜻을 좇고 있던 참에, 내가 한창 어쩔 줄 몰라 하고 있을 때 트래들스가 나를 찾아왔다."]], [["‘My dear fellow,’ said I, ‘I never was better pleased to see you.", "\"이보게,\" 내가 말했다. \"자네를 만나서 이보다 더 반가운 적이 없었네."], ["You come to give me the benefit of your sober judgement at a most opportune time.", "정말이지 더없이 알맞은 때에 자네의 그 차분한 판단을 빌려주러 와 주었군."], ["I have received a very singular letter, Traddles, from Mr. Micawber.’", "트래들스, 내가 미코버 씨한테서 아주 묘한 편지를 한 통 받았다네.\""]], [["‘No?’ cried Traddles.", "\"설마?\""], ["‘You don’t say so?", "트래들스가 소리쳤다."], ["And I have received one from Mrs. Micawber!’", "\"정말인가? 그런데 나도 미코버 부인한테서 한 통 받았다네!\""]], [["With that, Traddles, who was flushed with walking, and whose hair, under the combined effects of exercise and excitement, stood on end as if he saw a cheerful ghost, produced his letter and made an exchange with me. I watched him into the heart of Mr.", "그러면서 트래들스는—걸어오느라 얼굴이 발갛게 달아오르고, 운동과 흥분이 한데 어우러진 탓에 머리카락이 마치 유쾌한 유령이라도 본 것처럼 곤두선 채로—제 편지를 꺼내어 나와 맞바꾸었다."], ["Micawber’s letter, and returned the elevation of eyebrows with which he said “‘Wielding the thunderbolt, or directing the devouring and avenging flame!”", "나는 그가 미코버 씨의 편지 한복판으로 빠져드는 것을 지켜보다가, \"'벼락을 휘두르거나, 집어삼키며 복수하는 화염을 어느 방향으로든 향하게 할!'"], ["Bless me, Copperfield!’--and then entered on the perusal of Mrs. Micawber’s epistle.", "이런 세상에, 코퍼필드!\"라고 말하며 치켜올리는 그 눈썹에 나도 눈썹을 치켜올려 화답한 다음, 미코버 부인의 서신을 읽어 내려가기 시작했다."]], [["It ran thus:", "편지는 이러했다."]], [["‘My best regards to Mr. Thomas Traddles, and if he should still remember one who formerly had the happiness of being well acquainted with him, may I beg a few moments of his leisure time?", "\"토머스 트래들스 씨께 깊은 안부를 전하오며, 만약 그분께서 지난날 그분과 잘 알고 지내는 행복을 누렸던 한 사람을 아직 기억하신다면, 그분의 한가한 시간 가운데 잠시만 얻을 수 있을까요?"], ["I assure Mr. T. T. that I would not intrude upon his kindness, were I in any other position than on the confines of distraction.", "제가 만약 거의 정신이 나가기 직전의 처지가 아니었다면, T. T. 씨의 친절을 이렇게 함부로 침범하지 않았으리라는 것을 분명히 말씀드립니다."]], [["‘Though harrowing to myself to mention, the alienation of Mr. Micawber (formerly so domesticated) from his wife and family, is the cause of my addressing my unhappy appeal to Mr. Traddles, and soliciting his best indulgence. Mr. T.", "저로서는 입에 올리기조차 가슴 아픈 일이지만, (전에는 그토록 가정적이던) 미코버 씨가 아내와 가족에게서 멀어져 버린 것이, 제가 이 불행한 호소를 트래들스 씨께 올리며 부디 너그러이 헤아려 주십사 매달리게 된 까닭입니다."], ["can form no adequate idea of the change in Mr. Micawber’s conduct, of his wildness, of his violence.", "미코버 씨의 행동이 어떻게 달라졌는지, 그 거칠어짐과 그 난폭함을 T. 씨께서는 짐작조차 못 하실 것입니다."], ["It has gradually augmented, until it assumes the appearance of aberration of intellect.", "그것이 차츰차츰 심해지더니, 이제는 정신이 어긋나 버린 듯한 모습을 띠게 되었습니다."], ["Scarcely a day passes, I assure Mr. Traddles, on which some paroxysm does not take place. Mr. T.", "트래들스 씨께 분명히 말씀드리거니와, 어떤 발작이 일어나지 않고 지나가는 날이 거의 하루도 없습니다."], ["will not require me to depict my feelings, when I inform him that I have become accustomed to hear Mr.", "미코버 씨가 자기 자신을 악마에게 팔아넘겼다고 우겨 대는 소리를 듣는 것이 이제는 익숙해졌다고 말씀드리면, T."], ["Micawber assert that he has sold himself to the D. Mystery and secrecy have long been his principal characteristic, have long replaced unlimited confidence.", "씨께서는 그때 제 심정이 어떠한지 굳이 그려 보라고는 하지 않으실 것입니다. 비밀과 은밀함이 오래도록 그이의 주된 특성이 되어, 오래도록 그 무한하던 신뢰를 밀어내 버렸습니다."], ["The slightest provocation, even being asked if there is anything he would prefer for dinner, causes him to express a wish for a separation.", "아주 사소한 자극에도—심지어 저녁으로 무얼 드시고 싶으냐고 묻는 것만으로도—그이는 헤어지자는 소망을 입에 올립니다."], ["Last night, on being childishly solicited for twopence, to buy ‘lemon-stunners’--a local sweetmeat--he presented an oyster-knife at the twins!", "어젯밤에는, '레몬스터너'라는 이 고장 사탕을 사겠다고 쌍둥이가 어린애답게 이 펜스만 달라고 졸랐더니, 그이는 쌍둥이에게 굴 까는 칼을 들이댔습니다!"]], [["‘I entreat Mr. Traddles to bear with me in entering into these details.", "이런 시시콜콜한 사정을 늘어놓는 저를 트래들스 씨께서 부디 참고 견디어 주시기를 간청합니다."], ["Without them, Mr. T.", "이런 것들이 없으면, T."], ["would indeed find it difficult to form the faintest conception of my heart-rending situation.", "씨께서는 정말이지 가슴이 찢어지는 듯한 제 처지를 어렴풋이나마 짐작하기조차 어려우실 테니까요."]], [["‘May I now venture to confide to Mr. T.", "이제 제 편지의 본뜻을 감히 T."], ["the purport of my letter?", "씨께 털어놓아도 될까요?"], ["Will he now allow me to throw myself on his friendly consideration? Oh yes, for I know his heart!", "이제 그분의 다정한 헤아림에 저를 내맡겨도 될까요? 아, 그럼요, 저는 그분의 마음씨를 잘 아니까요!"]], [["‘The quick eye of affection is not easily blinded, when of the female sex. Mr.", "애정에서 비롯된 날카로운 눈은, 더구나 여자의 것이라면, 쉽사리 멀지 않습니다."], ["Micawber is going to London.", "미코버 씨는 런던으로 가려 합니다."], ["Though he studiously concealed his hand, this morning before breakfast, in writing the direction-card which he attached to the little brown valise of happier days, the eagle-glance of matrimonial anxiety detected, d, o, n, distinctly traced.", "오늘 아침 식사 전에 그이가 더 행복했던 시절에 쓰던 작은 갈색 여행 가방에 붙일 행선지 쪽지를 쓰면서 자기 글씨를 애써 가리려 했지만, 부부간의 근심에서 비롯된 매서운 매의 눈은 거기에 'd, o, n'이 또렷이 적혀 있는 것을 알아챘습니다."], ["The West-End destination of the coach, is the Golden Cross.", "그 마차의 웨스트엔드 종착지는 골든크로스입니다. 감히 T."], ["Dare I fervently implore Mr. T. to see my misguided husband, and to reason with him?", "씨께 제 길 잃은 남편을 만나, 그이를 타일러 주십사 간절히 빌어도 될까요? 감히 T."], ["Dare I ask Mr. T. to endeavour to step in between Mr. Micawber and his agonized family?", "씨께 미코버 씨와 그 괴로워하는 가족 사이에 끼어들어 주십사 청해도 될까요?"], ["Oh no, for that would be too much!", "아, 아니요, 그건 너무 지나친 부탁이겠지요!"]], [["‘If Mr. Copperfield should yet remember one unknown to fame, will Mr. T.", "만약 코퍼필드 씨가 세상에 이름조차 알려지지 않은 한 사람을 아직 기억하신다면, T."], ["take charge of my unalterable regards and similar entreaties?", "씨께서 부디 저의 변함없는 안부와 그와 같은 간청을 그분께 전해 주시겠는지요?"], ["In any case, he will have the benevolence to consider this communication strictly private, and on no account whatever to be alluded to, however distantly, in the presence of Mr.", "어떤 경우든, 그분께서는 이 글을 철저히 비밀로 여기시어, 미코버 씨가 있는 자리에서는 아무리 에둘러서라도 결코 입에 올리지 않으시는 자비를 베풀어 주시리라 믿습니다. 만약 T."], ["Micawber. If Mr. T. should ever reply to it (which I cannot but feel to be most improbable), a letter addressed to M.", "씨께서 이 편지에 답을 보내 주신다면 (그럴 가능성은 거의 없다고 느끼지 않을 수 없습니다만), 캔터베리 우체국, M. E."], ["E., Post Office, Canterbury, will be fraught with less painful consequences than any addressed immediately to one, who subscribes herself, in extreme distress,", "앞으로 부치시는 편지가, 극심한 비탄에 잠겨 이렇게 서명하는 사람에게 곧장 부치는 것보다는 덜 고통스러운 결과를 가져올 것입니다."]], [["‘Mr. Thomas Traddles’s respectful friend and suppliant,", "토머스 트래들스 씨를 공경하는 벗이자 탄원자,\n'에마 미코버.\""]], [["‘What do you think of that letter?’ said Traddles, casting his eyes upon me, when I had read it twice.", "\"그 편지를 어떻게 생각하나?\" 내가 두 번 읽고 나자 트래들스가 나에게 눈길을 던지며 말했다."]], [["‘What do you think of the other?’ said I.", "\"자넨 저쪽 편지를 어떻게 생각하나?\" 내가 말했다."], ["For he was still reading it with knitted brows.", "그는 여전히 이마를 찌푸린 채 그것을 읽고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["‘I think that the two together, Copperfield,’ replied Traddles, ‘mean more than Mr. and Mrs.", "\"내 생각엔 말이야, 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 대답했다."], ["Micawber usually mean in their correspondence--but I don’t know what. They are both written in good faith, I have no doubt, and without any collusion.", "\"이 둘을 합쳐 보면, 미코버 씨 내외가 평소 편지에 담는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 뜻이 있는 것 같아—하지만 그게 뭔지는 모르겠네. 둘 다 진심으로 쓴 거고, 서로 짜고 쓴 게 아닌 건 의심할 여지가 없어."], ["Poor thing!’ he was now alluding to Mrs.", "가엾기도 하지!\""], ["Micawber’s letter, and we were standing side by side comparing the two; ‘it will be a charity to write to her, at all events, and tell her that we will not fail to see Mr. Micawber.’", "그는 이제 미코버 부인의 편지를 두고 하는 말이었는데, 우리는 나란히 서서 두 편지를 견주어 보고 있었다. \"어쨌든 부인에게 답장을 써서, 우리가 틀림없이 미코버 씨를 만나겠다고 알려 주는 게 자비로운 일이겠지.\""]], [["I acceded to this the more readily, because I now reproached myself with having treated her former letter rather lightly.", "나는 더욱 선뜻 이 말에 동의했는데, 이제 와서 부인의 지난번 편지를 다소 가볍게 넘겨 버린 것이 스스로 부끄러웠기 때문이다."], ["It had set me thinking a good deal at the time, as I have mentioned in its place; but my absorption in my own affairs, my experience of the family, and my hearing nothing more, had gradually ended in my dismissing the subject.", "앞서 제때 말했듯이 그 편지는 그때 나에게 적잖이 생각거리를 던져 주었으나, 내 일에 골몰해 있던 데다, 그 집안에 대한 경험과, 그 뒤로 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 탓에, 차츰 그 일을 마음에서 떨쳐 버리고 만 것이었다."], ["I had often thought of the Micawbers, but chiefly to wonder what ‘pecuniary liabilities’ they were establishing in Canterbury, and to recall how shy Mr. Micawber was of me when he became clerk to Uriah Heep.", "나는 미코버 씨네를 자주 떠올리긴 했지만, 주로 그들이 캔터베리에서 어떤 '금전적 채무'를 쌓아 가고 있을까 궁금해하거나, 미코버 씨가 유라이아 히프의 서기가 되었을 때 나를 얼마나 어색하게 대했는지를 되새기는 정도였다."]], [["However, I now wrote a comforting letter to Mrs. Micawber, in our joint names, and we both signed it.", "어쨌든 나는 이제 미코버 부인에게 위로의 편지를 우리 두 사람 이름으로 써서, 둘 다 서명했다."], ["As we walked into town to post it, Traddles and I held a long conference, and launched into a number of speculations, which I need not repeat.", "그것을 부치러 시내로 걸어 들어가면서, 트래들스와 나는 오랫동안 이야기를 나누고 여러 가지 짐작을 늘어놓았는데, 여기서 굳이 다시 옮길 필요는 없겠다."], ["We took my aunt into our counsels in the afternoon; but our only decided conclusion was, that we would be very punctual in keeping Mr. Micawber’s appointment.", "오후에는 고모님까지 의논에 끌어들였지만, 우리가 내린 단 하나의 확실한 결론은 미코버 씨와의 약속을 아주 정확하게 지키자는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Although we appeared at the stipulated place a quarter of an hour before the time, we found Mr. Micawber already there.", "우리는 약속한 장소에 정해진 시각보다 십오 분이나 일찍 나타났는데도, 미코버 씨가 이미 그곳에 와 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He was standing with his arms folded, over against the wall, looking at the spikes on the top, with a sentimental expression, as if they were the interlacing boughs of trees that had shaded him in his youth.", "그는 팔짱을 끼고 담장을 마주한 채 서서, 그 꼭대기의 쇠창살을 바라보고 있었는데, 마치 그것이 젊은 시절 자기에게 그늘을 드리워 주던 나무들의 얽힌 가지이기라도 한 양 감상에 젖은 표정이었다."]], [["When we accosted him, his manner was something more confused, and something less genteel, than of yore.", "우리가 다가가 인사를 건네자, 그의 태도는 예전보다 어딘가 더 어수선하고, 어딘가 덜 점잖았다."], ["He had relinquished his legal suit of black for the purposes of this excursion, and wore the old surtout and tights, but not quite with the old air.", "그는 이번 나들이를 위해 법률가다운 검은 정장을 벗어 두고, 옛날의 그 프록코트와 꼭 끼는 바지를 입고 있었으나, 옛 풍채까지 완전히 갖춘 것은 아니었다."], ["He gradually picked up more and more of it as we conversed with him; but, his very eye-glass seemed to hang less easily, and his shirt-collar, though still of the old formidable dimensions, rather drooped.", "우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안 그는 차츰 그 풍채를 더 많이 되찾아 갔지만, 그의 외알 안경조차 예전만큼 편안하게 매달려 있지 못한 듯했고, 셔츠 깃은 여전히 그 위풍당당한 크기였으되 어쩐지 풀이 죽어 늘어져 있었다."]], [["‘Gentlemen!’ said Mr. Micawber, after the first salutations, ‘you are friends in need, and friends indeed.", "\"두 분!\" 처음 인사를 주고받은 뒤 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Allow me to offer my inquiries with reference to the physical welfare of Mrs.", "\"두 분은 어려울 때 찾아 주신 벗이요, 참된 벗입니다."], ["Copperfield in esse, and Mrs. Traddles in posse,--presuming, that is to say, that my friend Mr. Traddles is not yet united to the object of his affections, for weal and for woe.’", "실제로 존재하는 코퍼필드 부인과, 장차 존재할 트래들스 부인의 신체적 안녕에 관하여 안부를 여쭙는 것을 허락해 주시지요—다시 말해, 제 벗 트래들스 씨가 기쁠 때나 슬플 때나 함께할 그 연모의 대상과 아직 맺어지지 않았다는 것을 전제로 말입니다만.\""]], [["We acknowledged his politeness, and made suitable replies.", "우리는 그의 정중함에 사례하고 적당한 대답을 했다."], ["He then directed our attention to the wall, and was beginning, ‘I assure you, gentlemen,’ when I ventured to object to that ceremonious form of address, and to beg that he would speak to us in the old way.", "그러자 그는 우리의 시선을 담장 쪽으로 돌리며, \"단언컨대, 두 분,\" 하고 운을 떼기 시작했는데, 내가 그 격식 차린 말투에 이의를 제기하며, 부디 옛날처럼 우리에게 말씀해 주십사 청했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ he returned, pressing my hand, ‘your cordiality overpowers me.", "\"코퍼필드 군,\" 그가 내 손을 꼭 쥐며 대답했다. \"자네의 그 따뜻함에 나는 압도되네."], ["This reception of a shattered fragment of the Temple once called Man--if I may be permitted so to express myself--bespeaks a heart that is an honour to our common nature.", "한때 인간이라 불리던 사원의 부서진 한 조각을—이렇게 표현하는 것이 허락된다면 말이네만—이토록 맞아 주는 것은, 우리 공통의 본성에 명예가 되는 마음씨를 일러 주는 것일세."], ["I was about to observe that I again behold the serene spot where some of the happiest hours of my existence fleeted by.’", "내가 막 말하려던 것은, 내 삶에서 가장 행복했던 시간 가운데 얼마가 덧없이 흘러갔던 그 고요한 자리를 다시 보고 있다는 것이었네.\""]], [["‘Made so, I am sure, by Mrs. Micawber,’ said I.", "\"틀림없이 미코버 부인 덕분에 그렇게 행복했겠지요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I hope she is well?’", "\"부인은 잘 지내시는지요?\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ returned Mr.", "\"고맙네.\""], ["Micawber, whose face clouded at this reference, ‘she is but so-so.", "이 말에 얼굴이 흐려지며 미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"그저 그렇다네."], ["And this,’ said Mr. Micawber, nodding his head sorrowfully, ‘is the Bench!", "그리고 여기가 말일세,\" 미코버 씨가 슬프게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["Where, for the first time in many revolving years, the overwhelming pressure of pecuniary liabilities was not proclaimed, from day to day, by importune voices declining to vacate the passage; where there was no knocker on the door for any creditor to appeal to; where personal service of process was not required, and detainees were merely lodged at the gate!", "\"바로 벤치 감옥이라네! 여러 해가 돌고 도는 가운데 처음으로, 금전적 채무라는 그 짓누르는 압박이 날마다 통로를 비키지 않으려 들며 졸라 대는 목소리들로 떠들썩하게 알려지지 않았던 곳이지. 어떤 채권자가 매달려 두드릴 문고리도 없었고, 사람을 직접 붙들어 절차를 밟을 필요도 없었으며, 수감자들은 그저 문간에 거두어 들여졌을 뿐인 곳이라네! 두 분,\""], ["Gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘when the shadow of that iron-work on the summit of the brick structure has been reflected on the gravel of the Parade, I have seen my children thread the mazes of the intricate pattern, avoiding the dark marks.", "\"저 벽돌 건물 꼭대기의 쇠창살 그림자가 산책로의 자갈밭 위에 드리워졌을 때, 나는 내 아이들이 그 복잡한 무늬의 미로를 누비며 어두운 자국을 피해 다니는 것을 보았다네. 나는 이곳의 돌 하나하나에 다 익숙했지."], ["I have been familiar with every stone in the place. If I betray weakness, you will know how to excuse me.’", "내가 약한 모습을 보이더라도, 두 분은 나를 어떻게 너그러이 봐주어야 할지 아실 걸세.\""]], [["‘We have all got on in life since then, Mr. Micawber,’ said I.", "\"그 시절 이후로 우리 모두 인생에서 앞으로 나아갔지요, 미코버 씨.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield,’ returned Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 군,\" 미코버 씨가 쓰디쓰게 대답했다."], ["Micawber, bitterly, ‘when I was an inmate of that retreat I could look my fellow-man in the face, and punch his head if he offended me.", "\"내가 저 안식처의 수감자였을 적에는, 동포의 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 볼 수 있었고, 그자가 나를 욕보이면 그 머리통을 한 대 쥐어박을 수도 있었네."], ["My fellow-man and myself are no longer on those glorious terms!’", "동포와 나는 이제 더 이상 그런 영광스러운 사이가 아닐세!\""]], [["Turning from the building in a downcast manner, Mr. Micawber accepted my proffered arm on one side, and the proffered arm of Traddles on the other, and walked away between us.", "미코버 씨는 풀이 죽은 모습으로 그 건물에서 돌아서더니, 한쪽으로는 내가 내민 팔을, 다른 한쪽으로는 트래들스가 내민 팔을 받아 끼고, 우리 둘 사이에 끼여 걸어갔다."]], [["‘There are some landmarks,’ observed Mr.", "\"무덤으로 가는 길에는 말일세,\""], ["Micawber, looking fondly back over his shoulder, ‘on the road to the tomb, which, but for the impiety of the aspiration, a man would wish never to have passed.", "미코버 씨가 어깨 너머로 애틋하게 돌아보며 말했다. \"그런 소망을 품는 것이 불경한 일만 아니라면, 차라리 결코 지나오지 않았더라면 싶은 이정표들이 더러 있다네."], ["Such is the Bench in my chequered career.’", "내 파란만장한 인생에서 저 벤치 감옥이 바로 그런 것이지.\""]], [["‘Oh, you are in low spirits, Mr. Micawber,’ said Traddles.", "\"아, 기운이 많이 가라앉으셨군요, 미코버 씨.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘I am, sir,’ interposed Mr. Micawber.", "\"그렇다네, 선생.\" 미코버 씨가 끼어들며 말했다."]], [["‘I hope,’ said Traddles, ‘it is not because you have conceived a dislike to the law--for I am a lawyer myself, you know.’", "\"바라건대,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"법이 싫어지셔서 그런 건 아니길 바랍니다—아시다시피 저 자신도 법률가니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber answered not a word.", "미코버 씨는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다."]], [["‘How is our friend Heep, Mr. Micawber?’ said I, after a silence.", "\"우리 친구 히프는 어떻게 지냅니까, 미코버 씨?\" 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 내가 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ returned Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 군,\""], ["Micawber, bursting into a state of much excitement, and turning pale, ‘if you ask after my employer as YOUR friend, I am sorry for it; if you ask after him as MY friend, I sardonically smile at it.", "미코버 씨가 잔뜩 흥분한 상태로 터져 나오며 새파랗게 질려 대답했다. \"자네가 내 고용주를 자네의 친구로 여겨 안부를 묻는 거라면, 나는 그게 안타깝네. 그를 나의 친구로 여겨 묻는 거라면, 나는 거기에 비웃음을 흘릴 뿐일세."], ["In whatever capacity you ask after my employer, I beg, without offence to you, to limit my reply to this--that whatever his state of health may be, his appearance is foxy: not to say diabolical.", "어떤 자격으로 내 고용주의 안부를 묻든 간에, 자네에게 무례가 되지 않기를 바라며, 내 대답은 이것으로 그치겠네—그자의 건강 상태가 어떠하든, 그자의 몰골은 여우 같다는 것이지. 악마 같다고는 차마 말하지 않더라도 말일세."], ["You will allow me, as a private individual, to decline pursuing a subject which has lashed me to the utmost verge of desperation in my professional capacity.’", "사사로운 한 개인으로서, 나는 직무상의 자격으로 나를 절망의 끝자락까지 몰아붙인 이 화제를 더 이상 좇지 않기를 자네가 허락해 주리라 믿네.\""]], [["I expressed my regret for having innocently touched upon a theme that roused him so much.", "나는 그를 이토록 들끓게 만든 화제를 무심코 건드린 것을 미안하다고 했다. \"여쭤도 될까요,\""], ["‘May I ask,’ said I, ‘without any hazard of repeating the mistake, how my old friends Mr. and Miss Wickfield are?’", "내가 말했다. \"같은 실수를 되풀이할 위험 없이 말입니다만, 제 옛 친구분이신 위크필드 씨와 위크필드 양은 어떻게 지내시는지요?\""]], [["‘Miss Wickfield,’ said Mr.", "\"위크필드 양은,\""], ["Micawber, now turning red, ‘is, as she always is, a pattern, and a bright example.", "이번에는 얼굴이 벌게지며 미코버 씨가 말했다. \"늘 그렇듯이, 본보기이자 빛나는 귀감일세."], ["My dear Copperfield, she is the only starry spot in a miserable existence.", "코퍼필드 군, 그 아가씨는 이 비참한 삶 속에서 단 하나뿐인 별빛 같은 자리라네."], ["My respect for that young lady, my admiration of her character, my devotion to her for her love and truth, and goodness!--Take me,’ said Mr.", "그 젊은 아가씨에 대한 나의 존경과, 그 인품에 대한 나의 흠모와, 그 사랑과 진실과 선량함에 대한 나의 헌신이라니!—나를,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘down a turning, for, upon my soul, in my present state of mind I am not equal to this!’", "\"어디 골목으로 좀 데려가 주게, 정말이지 지금 내 심정으로는 도저히 이걸 감당할 수가 없네!\""]], [["We wheeled him off into a narrow street, where he took out his pocket-handkerchief, and stood with his back to a wall.", "우리는 그를 좁은 골목으로 데리고 들어갔고, 거기서 그는 주머니 손수건을 꺼내 들고 담장에 등을 기댄 채 섰다."], ["If I looked as gravely at him as Traddles did, he must have found our company by no means inspiriting.", "만약 내가 트래들스만큼이나 심각한 표정으로 그를 바라보았다면, 그는 우리와 함께 있는 것이 조금도 기운 나는 일이 아니라고 여겼으리라."]], [["‘It is my fate,’ said Mr.", "\"이것이 내 운명일세,\""], ["Micawber, unfeignedly sobbing, but doing even that, with a shadow of the old expression of doing something genteel; ‘it is my fate, gentlemen, that the finer feelings of our nature have become reproaches to me.", "미코버 씨가 진심으로 흐느끼며 말했는데, 그러면서도 어딘가 점잖게 처신한다는 옛 표정의 그림자를 여전히 띠고 있었다. \"두 분, 우리 본성의 더 고운 감정들이 도리어 나에게 책망이 되어 버린 것이 내 운명이라네."], ["My homage to Miss Wickfield, is a flight of arrows in my bosom.", "위크필드 양에 대한 나의 경의가, 내 가슴속에서는 화살 떼가 되어 박힌다네."], ["You had better leave me, if you please, to walk the earth as a vagabond.", "부디 괜찮다면, 나를 그냥 내버려 두고 떠도는 부랑자처럼 이 땅을 떠돌게 해 주게."], ["The worm will settle my business in double-quick time.’", "벌레가 머지않아 내 일을 후딱 끝내 줄 테니.\""]], [["Without attending to this invocation, we stood by, until he put up his pocket-handkerchief, pulled up his shirt-collar, and, to delude any person in the neighbourhood who might have been observing him, hummed a tune with his hat very much on one side.", "우리는 이 절절한 호소에는 아랑곳하지 않고, 그가 주머니 손수건을 도로 집어넣고, 셔츠 깃을 추켜올리고, 혹시 그를 지켜보고 있을지 모를 주변 사람들을 속이려고 모자를 잔뜩 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓴 채 콧노래를 흥얼거릴 때까지 곁에 서 있었다."], ["I then mentioned--not knowing what might be lost if we lost sight of him yet--that it would give me great pleasure to introduce him to my aunt, if he would ride out to Highgate, where a bed was at his service.", "그러고 나서 나는—그를 아직 놓쳐 버리면 무엇을 잃게 될지 몰랐기에—그가 하이게이트까지 마차를 타고 와 준다면, 내 고모님께 그를 소개해 드리는 것이 나로서는 큰 기쁨이겠고, 잠자리도 마련되어 있다고 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["‘You shall make us a glass of your own punch, Mr.", "\"미코버 씨, 직접 펀치를 한 잔 타 주시고,\""], ["Micawber,’ said I, ‘and forget whatever you have on your mind, in pleasanter reminiscences.’", "내가 말했다. \"마음에 담고 계신 것이 무엇이든 더 즐거운 추억거리 속에서 잊어버리시지요.\""]], [["‘Or, if confiding anything to friends will be more likely to relieve you, you shall impart it to us, Mr. Micawber,’ said Traddles, prudently.", "\"아니면, 벗들에게 무언가를 털어놓는 편이 마음을 더 가볍게 해 드릴 것 같으면, 그것을 저희에게 말씀해 주셔도 됩니다, 미코버 씨.\" 트래들스가 신중하게 말했다."]], [["‘Gentlemen,’ returned Mr.", "\"두 분,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다."], ["Micawber, ‘do with me as you will!", "\"마음대로 나를 다루시게!"], ["I am a straw upon the surface of the deep, and am tossed in all directions by the elephants--I beg your pardon; I should have said the elements.’", "나는 깊은 바다 위에 떠 있는 한 가닥 지푸라기요, 코끼리들에게 사방으로 내던져지는 신세라네—실례했네, 원소들이라고 했어야 하는데 말이지.\""]], [["We walked on, arm-in-arm, again; found the coach in the act of starting; and arrived at Highgate without encountering any difficulties by the way.", "우리는 다시 팔짱을 끼고 걸어갔고, 마침 막 떠나려는 마차를 발견했으며, 가는 길에 아무런 어려움도 겪지 않고 하이게이트에 다다랐다."], ["I was very uneasy and very uncertain in my mind what to say or do for the best--so was Traddles, evidently. Mr.", "나는 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 하는 것이 가장 나을지 마음이 몹시 불안하고 갈피를 잡지 못했는데—트래들스도 보아하니 마찬가지였다."], ["Micawber was for the most part plunged into deep gloom.", "미코버 씨는 대체로 깊은 우울 속에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He occasionally made an attempt to smarten himself, and hum the fag-end of a tune; but his relapses into profound melancholy were only made the more impressive by the mockery of a hat exceedingly on one side, and a shirt-collar pulled up to his eyes.", "그는 이따금 기운을 차려 보려 애쓰며 노래 한 자락의 끝부분을 흥얼거리기도 했으나, 모자를 잔뜩 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓰고 셔츠 깃을 눈까지 추켜올린 우스꽝스러운 꼴 탓에, 다시 깊은 우수에 빠져드는 그 모습은 더욱 사무치게 다가올 뿐이었다."]], [["We went to my aunt’s house rather than to mine, because of Dora’s not being well.", "우리는 도라가 몸이 좋지 않은 탓에, 내 집이 아니라 고모님 댁으로 갔다."], ["My aunt presented herself on being sent for, and welcomed Mr. Micawber with gracious cordiality. Mr.", "고모님은 사람을 보내 부르자 모습을 드러내, 미코버 씨를 더없이 정중하고 따뜻하게 맞아 주었다."], ["Micawber kissed her hand, retired to the window, and pulling out his pocket-handkerchief, had a mental wrestle with himself.", "미코버 씨는 고모님의 손등에 입을 맞추고는 창가로 물러나, 주머니 손수건을 꺼내 들고 속으로 자기 자신과 한바탕 씨름을 벌였다."]], [["Mr. Dick was at home.", "딕 씨도 집에 있었다."], ["He was by nature so exceedingly compassionate of anyone who seemed to be ill at ease, and was so quick to find any such person out, that he shook hands with Mr. Micawber, at least half-a-dozen times in five minutes.", "그는 천성이 워낙에 마음이 편치 않아 보이는 사람이라면 누구에게나 더없이 가엾어하는 데다, 그런 사람을 워낙 잽싸게 알아채는 터라, 오 분 사이에 적어도 대여섯 번은 미코버 씨와 악수를 했다."], ["To Mr. Micawber, in his trouble, this warmth, on the part of a stranger, was so extremely touching, that he could only say, on the occasion of each successive shake, ‘My dear sir, you overpower me!’ Which gratified Mr.", "곤경에 빠진 미코버 씨에게는, 낯선 사람이 보여 주는 이런 따뜻함이 어찌나 사무치게 다가왔던지, 그는 악수를 할 때마다 그저 \"선생, 저를 압도하시는군요!\" 하고 말할 따름이었다."], ["Dick so much, that he went at it again with greater vigour than before.", "그 말에 딕 씨는 어찌나 흐뭇했던지, 전보다 더 힘차게 또다시 악수를 해 댔다."]], [["‘The friendliness of this gentleman,’ said Mr.", "\"이 신사분의 다정함이 말입니다,\""], ["Micawber to my aunt, ‘if you will allow me, ma’am, to cull a figure of speech from the vocabulary of our coarser national sports--floors me.", "미코버 씨가 고모님께 말했다. \"부인, 우리 좀 더 거친 국민적 운동 경기의 어휘에서 비유 하나를 따오는 것을 허락해 주신다면—저를 그야말로 때려눕히는군요."], ["To a man who is struggling with a complicated burden of perplexity and disquiet, such a reception is trying, I assure you.’", "복잡하게 뒤엉킨 곤혹과 불안의 짐과 씨름하고 있는 사람에게, 이런 환대는 정말이지 견디기 힘든 시련입니다.\""]], [["‘My friend Mr.", "\"내 친구 딕 씨는,\""], ["Dick,’ replied my aunt proudly, ‘is not a common man.’", "고모님이 자랑스럽게 대답했다. \"여느 사람이 아니랍니다.\""]], [["‘That I am convinced of,’ said Mr.", "\"그 점은 저도 확신합니다.\""], ["Micawber. ‘My dear sir!’ for Mr.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"선생!\""], ["Dick was shaking hands with him again; ‘I am deeply sensible of your cordiality!’", "딕 씨가 또다시 그와 악수를 하고 있었기 때문이다. \"선생의 그 따뜻함을 저는 깊이 느낍니다!\""]], [["‘How do you find yourself?’ said Mr. Dick, with an anxious look.", "\"몸은 좀 어떠십니까?\" 딕 씨가 근심스러운 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["‘Indifferent, my dear sir,’ returned Mr. Micawber, sighing.", "\"그저 그렇습니다, 선생.\" 미코버 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 대답했다."]], [["‘You must keep up your spirits,’ said Mr.", "\"기운을 내셔야 합니다.\" 딕 씨가 말했다."], ["Dick, ‘and make yourself as comfortable as possible.’", "\"그리고 되도록 편안하게 지내셔야지요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber was quite overcome by these friendly words, and by finding Mr. Dick’s hand again within his own.", "미코버 씨는 이 다정한 말과, 딕 씨의 손이 또다시 자기 손안에 들어와 있는 것에 그만 완전히 무너지고 말았다."], ["‘It has been my lot,’ he observed, ‘to meet, in the diversified panorama of human existence, with an occasional oasis, but never with one so green, so gushing, as the present!’", "\"다채로운 인생의 파노라마 속에서,\" 그가 말했다. \"이따금 오아시스를 만나는 것이 제 운명이었습니다만, 지금 이것처럼 이토록 푸르고 이토록 샘솟는 오아시스는 결코 만난 적이 없습니다!\""]], [["At another time I should have been amused by this; but I felt that we were all constrained and uneasy, and I watched Mr. Micawber so anxiously, in his vacillations between an evident disposition to reveal something, and a counter-disposition to reveal nothing, that I was in a perfect fever.", "다른 때 같았으면 나는 이 말에 재미있어했을 것이다. 그러나 나는 우리 모두가 거북하고 불안하다는 것을 느끼고 있었고, 무언가를 털어놓고 싶어 하는 기색과 아무것도 털어놓지 않으려는 기색 사이에서 갈팡질팡하는 미코버 씨를 어찌나 애가 타게 지켜보았던지, 그야말로 안절부절못하는 지경이었다."], ["Traddles, sitting on the edge of his chair, with his eyes wide open, and his hair more emphatically erect than ever, stared by turns at the ground and at Mr. Micawber, without so much as attempting to put in a word.", "트래들스는 의자 끄트머리에 걸터앉아, 두 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고, 머리카락은 어느 때보다 더 도드라지게 곤두선 채, 바닥과 미코버 씨를 번갈아 빤히 바라볼 뿐, 한마디 끼어들 엄두조차 내지 못했다."], ["My aunt, though I saw that her shrewdest observation was concentrated on her new guest, had more useful possession of her wits than either of us; for she held him in conversation, and made it necessary for him to talk, whether he liked it or not.", "고모님은, 내가 보기에 그 빈틈없는 관찰력을 온통 새 손님에게 쏟고 있었으되, 우리 둘 누구보다도 정신을 더 야무지게 부려, 그를 대화 속에 붙들어 두고는, 그가 좋든 싫든 말을 하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다."]], [["‘You are a very old friend of my nephew’s, Mr. Micawber,’ said my aunt.", "\"미코버 씨, 댁은 제 조카의 아주 오랜 벗이시지요.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘I wish I had had the pleasure of seeing you before.’", "\"진작에 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸더라면 좋았을 것을요.\""]], [["‘Madam,’ returned Mr.", "\"부인,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다."], ["Micawber, ‘I wish I had had the honour of knowing you at an earlier period. I was not always the wreck you at present behold.’", "\"저야말로 좀 더 일찍 부인을 알게 되는 영광을 누렸더라면 싶습니다. 저도 지금 부인께서 보시는 이런 난파선 같은 꼴이 늘 그러했던 것은 아니랍니다.\""]], [["‘I hope Mrs. Micawber and your family are well, sir,’ said my aunt.", "\"미코버 부인과 가족분들도 안녕하시기를 바랍니다, 선생.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber inclined his head.", "미코버 씨가 고개를 숙였다."], ["‘They are as well, ma’am,’ he desperately observed after a pause, ‘as Aliens and Outcasts can ever hope to be.’", "\"그들도 잘 지냅니다, 부인,\" 그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 자포자기한 듯 말했다. \"이방인이자 버림받은 자들이 바랄 수 있는 만큼은요.\""]], [["‘Lord bless you, sir!’ exclaimed my aunt, in her abrupt way.", "\"맙소사, 선생!\" 고모님이 그 특유의 퉁명스러운 투로 소리쳤다."], ["‘What are you talking about?’", "\"대체 무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\""]], [["‘The subsistence of my family, ma’am,’ returned Mr.", "\"제 가족의 생계가 말입니다, 부인,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다."], ["Micawber, ‘trembles in the balance.", "\"저울 위에서 위태롭게 흔들리고 있습니다."], ["My employer--’", "제 고용주가—\""]], [["Here Mr. Micawber provokingly left off; and began to peel the lemons that had been under my directions set before him, together with all the other appliances he used in making punch.", "여기서 미코버 씨는 약 올리듯 말을 뚝 끊더니, 내가 일러 준 대로 그의 앞에 차려 놓은 레몬들을, 펀치를 만들 때 쓰는 다른 온갖 도구들과 함께 껍질을 벗기기 시작했다."]], [["‘Your employer, you know,’ said Mr. Dick, jogging his arm as a gentle reminder.", "\"고용주가 말이지요, 그래서요.\" 딕 씨가 살며시 일깨워 주려는 듯 그의 팔을 툭 치며 말했다."]], [["‘My good sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"이보십시오, 선생,\""], ["Micawber, ‘you recall me, I am obliged to you.’ They shook hands again.", "미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"일깨워 주시니, 고맙습니다.\""], ["‘My employer, ma’am--Mr.", "두 사람은 또다시 악수를 했다."], ["Heep--once did me the favour to observe to me, that if I were not in the receipt of the stipendiary emoluments appertaining to my engagement with him, I should probably be a mountebank about the country, swallowing a sword-blade, and eating the devouring element.", "\"제 고용주가 말입니다, 부인—히프 씨가—언젠가 저에게 이런 말을 하는 은혜를 베풀었지요. 만약 제가 그와의 계약에 딸린 봉급이라는 보수를 받지 못했더라면, 저는 아마도 시골을 떠도는 약장수가 되어, 칼날을 삼키고 집어삼키는 불을 먹고 있었을 거라고 말입니다."], ["For anything that I can perceive to the contrary, it is still probable that my children may be reduced to seek a livelihood by personal contortion, while Mrs. Micawber abets their unnatural feats by playing the barrel-organ.’", "제가 보기에 사정이 달라질 것 같지도 않으니, 여전히 제 아이들은 몸을 뒤트는 곡예로 입에 풀칠하는 신세로 전락하고, 미코버 부인은 손풍금을 켜서 그 부자연스러운 재주를 거들게 될 가능성이 큽니다.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, with a random but expressive flourish of his knife, signified that these performances might be expected to take place after he was no more; then resumed his peeling with a desperate air.", "미코버 씨는 칼을 아무렇게나, 그러나 의미심장하게 한 번 휘둘러, 이런 공연들이 자기가 죽고 난 뒤에 벌어지리라 예상된다는 것을 내비치더니, 다시 자포자기한 기색으로 껍질을 벗기기 시작했다."]], [["My aunt leaned her elbow on the little round table that she usually kept beside her, and eyed him attentively.", "고모님은 늘 곁에 두는 작은 둥근 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 그를 유심히 바라보았다."], ["Notwithstanding the aversion with which I regarded the idea of entrapping him into any disclosure he was not prepared to make voluntarily, I should have taken him up at this point, but for the strange proceedings in which I saw him engaged; whereof his putting the lemon-peel into the kettle, the sugar into the snuffer-tray, the spirit into the empty jug, and confidently attempting to pour boiling water out of a candlestick, were among the most remarkable.", "그가 자진해서 할 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않은 어떤 폭로를 함정에 빠뜨려 끌어내는 것을 나는 못마땅하게 여겼지만, 그럼에도 이 대목에서 그를 붙들고 캐물었을 것이다—내가 본, 그가 벌이고 있던 그 괴상한 짓들만 아니었다면 말이다. 그중에서도 가장 눈에 띄는 것을 꼽자면, 레몬 껍질을 주전자에 넣고, 설탕을 양초 심지 자르는 접시에 붓고, 독한 술을 빈 물병에 따르고, 촛대에서 끓는 물을 따르려고 천연덕스럽게 시도하는 것 따위였다."], ["I saw that a crisis was at hand, and it came.", "나는 어떤 고비가 코앞에 닥쳤음을 알아챘고, 과연 그것이 닥쳤다."], ["He clattered all his means and implements together, rose from his chair, pulled out his pocket-handkerchief, and burst into tears.", "그는 자기 재료와 도구를 죄다 와장창 한데 부딪치게 한 뒤, 의자에서 일어나, 주머니 손수건을 꺼내 들고는, 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 군,\""], ["Micawber, behind his handkerchief, ‘this is an occupation, of all others, requiring an untroubled mind, and self-respect.", "미코버 씨가 손수건 뒤에서 말했다. \"이 일은, 무엇보다도, 평온한 마음과 자존심을 필요로 하는 일이라네."], ["I cannot perform it.", "나는 이걸 해낼 수가 없네."], ["It is out of the question.’", "도저히 어림없는 일이야.\""]], [["‘Mr. Micawber,’ said I, ‘what is the matter?", "\"미코버 씨,\" 내가 말했다."], ["Pray speak out.", "\"무슨 일입니까?"], ["You are among friends.’", "부디 속 시원히 말씀해 주세요. 여기는 다 벗들뿐이지 않습니까.\""]], [["‘Among friends, sir!’ repeated Mr.", "\"벗들뿐이라고, 선생!\" 미코버 씨가 되뇌었다."], ["Micawber; and all he had reserved came breaking out of him.", "그러자 그가 그동안 담아 두었던 모든 것이 봇물 터지듯 그에게서 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["‘Good heavens, it is principally because I AM among friends that my state of mind is what it is.", "\"맙소사, 내 마음 상태가 이 지경이 된 것은, 무엇보다도 바로 내가 벗들 사이에 있기 때문이라네. 무슨 일이냐고, 두 분?"], ["What is the matter, gentlemen?", "무슨 일이 아니냐고 물어야 하지 않겠나?"], ["What is NOT the matter?", "악행이 바로 그 일일세."], ["Villainy is the matter; baseness is the matter; deception, fraud, conspiracy, are the matter; and the name of the whole atrocious mass is--HEEP!’", "비열함이 바로 그 일이야. 기만과 사기와 음모가 바로 그 일이지. 그리고 그 흉악한 무더기 전체의 이름이 바로—히프일세!\""]], [["My aunt clapped her hands, and we all started up as if we were possessed.", "고모님은 손뼉을 쳤고, 우리는 모두 무엇에 홀린 사람들처럼 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["‘The struggle is over!’ said Mr.", "\"이제 씨름은 끝났네!\""], ["Micawber violently gesticulating with his pocket-handkerchief, and fairly striking out from time to time with both arms, as if he were swimming under superhuman difficulties. ‘I will lead this life no longer.", "미코버 씨가 주머니 손수건을 거칠게 휘두르며, 마치 인간의 힘을 넘어서는 난관 속에서 헤엄을 치기라도 하는 양 이따금 두 팔을 활짝 내뻗으며 말했다. \"나는 더 이상 이런 삶을 이어 가지 않겠네."], ["I am a wretched being, cut off from everything that makes life tolerable.", "나는 삶을 견딜 만하게 해 주는 모든 것에서 동떨어진 비참한 존재일세."], ["I have been under a Taboo in that infernal scoundrel’s service.", "나는 그 지옥 같은 악당의 종살이 속에서 금기에 묶여 있었네."], ["Give me back my wife, give me back my family, substitute Micawber for the petty wretch who walks about in the boots at present on my feet, and call upon me to swallow a sword tomorrow, and I’ll do it.", "내 아내를 돌려주게, 내 가족을 돌려주게, 지금 내 발에 신긴 장화를 신고 돌아다니는 그 하찮은 놈 대신 미코버를 되돌려 놓아 주게. 그리고 내일 당장 칼을 삼키라 명해 보게, 그러면 나는 해 보이겠네."], ["With an appetite!’", "입맛까지 다시며 말일세!\""]], [["I never saw a man so hot in my life. I tried to calm him, that we might come to something rational; but he got hotter and hotter, and wouldn’t hear a word.", "나는 평생 그토록 펄펄 끓어오르는 사람을 본 적이 없다. 우리가 뭔가 이치에 닿는 데까지 이르도록 그를 진정시키려 애썼지만, 그는 점점 더 뜨거워질 뿐 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다."]], [["‘I’ll put my hand in no man’s hand,’ said Mr.", "\"나는 그 누구의 손도 잡지 않겠네.\""], ["Micawber, gasping, puffing, and sobbing, to that degree that he was like a man fighting with cold water, ‘until I have--blown to fragments--the--a--detestable--serpent--HEEP!", "미코버 씨가 숨을 헐떡이고 씩씩거리며, 또 찬물과 싸우는 사람처럼 그렇게 흐느끼며 말했다. \"내가—그—그 가증스러운—뱀—히프를—산산조각으로—날려 버리기 전까지는!"], ["I’ll partake of no one’s hospitality, until I have--a--moved Mount Vesuvius--to eruption--on--a--the abandoned rascal--HEEP!", "나는 그 누구의 환대도 받지 않겠네—내가—그—저버림받은 악당—히프—위에—베수비오 화산을—폭발시키기—전까지는!"], ["Refreshment--a--underneath this roof--particularly punch--would--a--choke me--unless--I had--previously--choked the eyes--out of the head--a--of--interminable cheat, and liar--HEEP!", "이 지붕 아래에서—먹을 것이라니—특히 펀치라니—그건—나를—목 막히게 할—걸세—내가—먼저—그—끝도 없는 사기꾼이자 거짓말쟁이—히프의—머리통에서—눈알을—후벼 파 버리지—않는 한은!"], ["I--a--I’ll know nobody--and--a--say nothing--and--a--live nowhere--until I have crushed--to--a--undiscoverable atoms--the--transcendent and immortal hypocrite and perjurer--HEEP!’", "나는—그—그 누구도 알은체하지 않고—아무 말도 하지 않고—그 어디에도 머물지 않겠네—내가—그 비할 데 없고 죽지도 않을 위선자이자 위증자—히프를—찾아낼 수 없는—티끌로—짓이겨 버리기—전까지는!\""]], [["I really had some fear of Mr. Micawber’s dying on the spot.", "나는 정말이지 미코버 씨가 그 자리에서 죽어 버리지나 않을까 어느 정도 겁이 났다."], ["The manner in which he struggled through these inarticulate sentences, and, whenever he found himself getting near the name of Heep, fought his way on to it, dashed at it in a fainting state, and brought it out with a vehemence little less than marvellous, was frightful; but now, when he sank into a chair, steaming, and looked at us, with every possible colour in his face that had no business there, and an endless procession of lumps following one another in hot haste up his throat, whence they seemed to shoot into his forehead, he had the appearance of being in the last extremity.", "그가 이 횡설수설하는 문장들을 헤치고 나아가는 모양새며, 히프라는 이름 가까이에 이를 때마다 그 이름을 향해 기를 쓰고 나아가, 까무러칠 듯한 상태로 거기에 달려들어, 거의 경탄스럽다 할 만큼의 격렬함으로 그것을 토해 내는 그 꼴은 무시무시했다. 그러나 이제, 그가 김을 펄펄 내뿜으며 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 있어야 할 자리에 있을 리 없는 온갖 빛깔이 다 떠오른 얼굴로 우리를 바라보았을 때, 그리고 끝없이 줄줄이 이어지는 덩어리들이 다급하게 그의 목구멍을 타고 올라와 거기서 이마로 튀어 오르는 듯했을 때, 그는 그야말로 마지막 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 몰골이었다."], ["I would have gone to his assistance, but he waved me off, and wouldn’t hear a word.", "나는 그를 도우러 가려 했지만, 그는 나를 손짓으로 물리치며 한마디도 들으려 하지 않았다."]], [["‘No, Copperfield!--No communication--a--until--Miss Wickfield--a--redress from wrongs inflicted by consummate scoundrel--HEEP!’ (I am quite convinced he could not have uttered three words, but for the amazing energy with which this word inspired him when he felt it coming.) ‘Inviolable secret--a--from the whole world--a--no exceptions--this day week--a--at breakfast-time--a--everybody present--including aunt--a--and extremely friendly gentleman--to be at the hotel at Canterbury--a--where--Mrs. Micawber and myself--Auld Lang Syne in chorus--and--a--will expose intolerable ruffian--HEEP!", "\"안 되네, 코퍼필드!—아무 말도—그—위크필드 양이—그 천하의 악당—히프가—입힌—상처에서—바로잡힐—때까지는!\" (이 말이 다가오는 것을 느낄 때 그 안에 불어넣어 주던 그 놀라운 기운이 아니었다면, 그는 세 마디도 잇지 못했으리라고 나는 확신한다.) \"온 세상에—그—깨뜨릴 수 없는 비밀로—그—예외 없이—이날로부터 일주일 뒤—그—아침 식사 때—그—모두가 모인 자리에서—고모님까지 포함해서—그리고 더없이 다정한 신사분까지—캔터베리의 그 호텔로 와 주게—그—거기서—미코버 부인과 내가—다 함께 옛 노래를 부르고—그리고—그—그 못 봐줄 악당을—히프를—까발리겠네!"], ["No more to say--a--or listen to persuasion--go immediately--not capable--a--bear society--upon the track of devoted and doomed traitor--HEEP!’", "더 할 말도—그—없고—설득도 듣지 않겠네—당장 가야겠네—견딜 수 없네—그—사람들 곁에 있는 것을—그 충성스럽고 파멸할 운명의 배신자—히프의—뒤를 쫓아서!\""]], [["With this last repetition of the magic word that had kept him going at all, and in which he surpassed all his previous efforts, Mr.", "그를 여태껏 버티게 해 준 그 마법의 단어를, 이전의 그 모든 안간힘을 능가하는 기세로 마지막으로 한 번 더 되뇌고는, 미코버 씨는 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Micawber rushed out of the house; leaving us in a state of excitement, hope, and wonder, that reduced us to a condition little better than his own.", "우리를 흥분과 희망과 경탄의 도가니 속에 남겨 둔 채로 말인데, 그 바람에 우리도 그와 별반 다를 바 없는 꼴이 되고 말았다."], ["But even then his passion for writing letters was too strong to be resisted; for while we were yet in the height of our excitement, hope, and wonder, the following pastoral note was brought to me from a neighbouring tavern, at which he had called to write it:--", "그러나 그런 와중에도 편지 쓰기를 향한 그의 열정은 도저히 억누를 수 없을 만큼 강해서, 우리가 아직 흥분과 희망과 경탄의 한가운데에 있는 동안, 그가 들러 적은 다음과 같은 목가적인 쪽지가 이웃 선술집에서 나에게 전해졌다."]], [["‘Most secret and confidential. ‘MY DEAR SIR,", "'극비이자 친전. \"친애하는 선생께,"]], [["‘I beg to be allowed to convey, through you, my apologies to your excellent aunt for my late excitement.", "저의 아까 그 흥분에 대하여, 선생을 통하여, 선생의 훌륭하신 고모님께 사죄의 말씀을 전할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 청합니다."], ["An explosion of a smouldering volcano long suppressed, was the result of an internal contest more easily conceived than described.", "오래도록 억눌려 있던 화산이 속에서 부글거리다 터져 나온 것은, 말로 그려 내기보다 마음으로 헤아리기가 더 쉬운 내면의 다툼이 빚어낸 결과였습니다."]], [["‘I trust I rendered tolerably intelligible my appointment for the morning of this day week, at the house of public entertainment at Canterbury, where Mrs. Micawber and myself had once the honour of uniting our voices to yours, in the well-known strain of the Immortal exciseman nurtured beyond the Tweed.", "이날로부터 일주일 뒤 아침, 캔터베리의 그 대중 접객소에서의 약속을 제가 그런대로 알아들으실 만하게 전했기를 바랍니다. 그곳은 일찍이 미코버 부인과 제가, 트위드강 너머에서 자라난 저 불멸의 세리를 노래한 그 널리 알려진 가락에 선생의 목소리와 우리의 목소리를 한데 모으는 영광을 누렸던 자리이지요."]], [["‘The duty done, and act of reparation performed, which can alone enable me to contemplate my fellow mortal, I shall be known no more.", "저로 하여금 비로소 동포를 마주 볼 수 있게 해 줄 그 의무를 다하고, 그 속죄의 행위를 이루고 나면, 저는 더 이상 세상에 알려지지 않을 것입니다."], ["I shall simply require to be deposited in that place of universal resort, where", "저는 그저, 만인이 모여드는 그곳에 묻히기만을 바랄 따름입니다. 그곳은,"]], [["Each in his narrow cell for ever laid, The rude forefathers of the hamlet sleep,", "저마다 좁은 방 안에 영영 누운 채로, 마을의 투박한 옛 조상들이 잠든 곳이니,\n'—다음과 같은 단출한 비명과 함께 말입니다."]], [["‘--With the plain Inscription,", "'윌킨스 미코버.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 50. Mr. PEGGOTTY’S DREAM COMES TRUE", "# CHAPTER 50. Mr. PEGGOTTY’S DREAM COMES TRUE"]], [["By this time, some months had passed since our interview on the bank of the river with Martha.", "그러는 사이 몇 달이 흘렀다. 강둑에서 마사를 만난 그날 이후로 말이다."], ["I had never seen her since, but she had communicated with Mr. Peggotty on several occasions.", "그 후로 나는 마사를 한 번도 본 적이 없었지만, 마사는 여러 번 패거티 아저씨와 연락을 주고받았다."], ["Nothing had come of her zealous intervention; nor could I infer, from what he told me, that any clue had been obtained, for a moment, to Emily’s fate.", "그 열성적인 개입에서 얻은 것은 아무것도 없었다. 아저씨의 이야기로 미루어 보아도, 단 한순간이나마 에밀리의 행방을 짚어 줄 실마리를 붙잡았다고는 보이지 않았다."], ["I confess that I began to despair of her recovery, and gradually to sink deeper and deeper into the belief that she was dead.", "솔직히 고백하면, 나는 에밀리를 되찾으리란 희망을 차츰 잃기 시작했고, 그 애가 이미 죽었으리라는 생각 속으로 갈수록 깊이 가라앉아 갔다."]], [["His conviction remained unchanged.", "그러나 아저씨의 확신은 조금도 흔들리지 않았다."], ["So far as I know--and I believe his honest heart was transparent to me--he never wavered again, in his solemn certainty of finding her.", "내가 아는 한 — 그분의 정직한 마음은 나에게 훤히 들여다보였다고 믿는다 — 그분은 에밀리를 찾아내고야 말겠다는 엄숙한 확신 속에서 두 번 다시 흔들리지 않았다."], ["His patience never tired.", "그분의 인내는 결코 지치는 법이 없었다."], ["And, although I trembled for the agony it might one day be to him to have his strong assurance shivered at a blow, there was something so religious in it, so affectingly expressive of its anchor being in the purest depths of his fine nature, that the respect and honour in which I held him were exalted every day.", "언젠가 그 굳건한 믿음이 단 한 번의 타격으로 산산이 부서지는 날이 오면 그것이 그분에게 얼마나 큰 고통일까 싶어 나는 마음이 떨렸지만, 그 믿음에는 어딘가 신성한 데가 있었고, 그분의 고결한 천성의 가장 맑은 깊은 곳에 닻을 내리고 있음을 가슴 저미게 드러내고 있었다. 그래서 내가 그분에게 품은 존경과 흠모는 날마다 더 높아만 갔다."]], [["His was not a lazy trustfulness that hoped, and did no more.", "그분의 믿음은 그저 바라기만 하고 그 이상은 하지 않는 게으른 믿음이 아니었다."], ["He had been a man of sturdy action all his life, and he knew that in all things wherein he wanted help he must do his own part faithfully, and help himself.", "그분은 평생을 억센 행동으로 살아온 사람이었고, 도움이 필요한 모든 일에서 자기 몫을 성실하게 해내고 스스로를 도와야 한다는 것을 알았다."], ["I have known him set out in the night, on a misgiving that the light might not be, by some accident, in the window of the old boat, and walk to Yarmouth.", "옛 배에 불이 켜져 있지 않을지도 모른다는 어쩌다 든 불안 때문에, 그분이 한밤중에 길을 나서 야머스까지 걸어간 적도 있었다."], ["I have known him, on reading something in the newspaper that might apply to her, take up his stick, and go forth on a journey of three--or four-score miles.", "신문에서 에밀리에게 해당될 법한 무언가를 읽고는 지팡이를 집어 들고 칠팔십 마일이나 되는 길을 떠나는 것도 보았다."], ["He made his way by sea to Naples, and back, after hearing the narrative to which Miss Dartle had assisted me.", "다틀 양이 들려준 이야기를 듣고 난 뒤에는 배를 타고 나폴리까지 갔다가 돌아오기도 했다."], ["All his journeys were ruggedly performed; for he was always steadfast in a purpose of saving money for Emily’s sake, when she should be found.", "그 모든 여정을 그분은 거칠게, 고생스럽게 치러 냈다. 에밀리를 찾는 날을 위해 돈을 아끼겠다는 마음을 늘 굳게 지켰기 때문이다."], ["In all this long pursuit, I never heard him repine; I never heard him say he was fatigued, or out of heart.", "이 길고 긴 추적 내내, 나는 그분이 한탄하는 소리를 들은 적이 없었다. 그분이 지쳤다거나 풀이 죽었다고 말하는 것도 들은 적이 없었다."]], [["Dora had often seen him since our marriage, and was quite fond of him.", "도라는 우리가 결혼한 뒤로 그분을 여러 번 만났고, 무척 좋아했다."], ["I fancy his figure before me now, standing near her sofa, with his rough cap in his hand, and the blue eyes of my child-wife raised, with a timid wonder, to his face.", "지금도 그분의 모습이 눈앞에 선하다. 거친 모자를 손에 쥔 채 도라의 소파 곁에 서 있고, 나의 어린 아내가 푸른 두 눈을 들어 수줍은 경이로움을 담아 그분의 얼굴을 올려다보던 모습 말이다."], ["Sometimes of an evening, about twilight, when he came to talk with me, I would induce him to smoke his pipe in the garden, as we slowly paced to and fro together; and then, the picture of his deserted home, and the comfortable air it used to have in my childish eyes of an evening when the fire was burning, and the wind moaning round it, came most vividly into my mind.", "저녁 무렵, 황혼 즈음에 그분이 나와 이야기를 나누러 오면, 나는 종종 그분을 꾀어 정원에서 함께 천천히 거닐며 파이프 담배를 피우게 하곤 했다. 그러면 텅 비어 버린 그분의 옛집, 어린 시절 내 눈에 비쳤던 그 집의 아늑한 정취 — 난롯불이 타오르고 바람이 집 둘레에서 신음하던 어느 저녁의 그 풍경이 더없이 생생하게 마음속에 떠올랐다."]], [["One evening, at this hour, he told me that he had found Martha waiting near his lodging on the preceding night when he came out, and that she had asked him not to leave London on any account, until he should have seen her again.", "어느 저녁, 바로 그 시각에, 그분은 어젯밤 거처에서 나왔을 때 마사가 근처에서 기다리고 있더라고, 그리고 자기를 다시 만나기 전까지는 무슨 일이 있어도 런던을 떠나지 말아 달라고 부탁하더라고 말했다."]], [["‘Did she tell you why?’ I inquired.", "\"이유는 말했나요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I asked her, Mas’r Davy,’ he replied, ‘but it is but few words as she ever says, and she on’y got my promise and so went away.’", "\"물어봤지유, 데이비 도련님.\" 그분이 대답했다. \"그치만 그 애는 워낙 말이 없어서, 그저 내 약속만 받고는 가 버립디다.\""]], [["‘Did she say when you might expect to see her again?’ I demanded.", "\"언제쯤 다시 만나게 될지는 말했고요?\" 내가 캐물었다."]], [["‘No, Mas’r Davy,’ he returned, drawing his hand thoughtfully down his face.", "\"아니유, 데이비 도련님.\" 그분은 손을 얼굴 위로 생각에 잠긴 듯 쓸어내리며 대답했다."], ["‘I asked that too; but it was more (she said) than she could tell.’", "\"그것도 물어봤지유. 그치만 그건 (그 애 말로는) 자기도 알 수 없는 거라고 합디다.\""]], [["As I had long forborne to encourage him with hopes that hung on threads, I made no other comment on this information than that I supposed he would see her soon.", "가는 실에 매달린 희망으로 그분을 부추기는 일을 나는 오래전에 그만두었던 터라, 이 소식에 대해서도 곧 마사를 만나게 되리라 본다는 말 외에는 아무런 토를 달지 않았다."], ["Such speculations as it engendered within me I kept to myself, and those were faint enough.", "그것이 내 안에 불러일으킨 갖은 추측은 나 혼자만 간직했고, 그나마도 무척 희미한 것이었다."]], [["I was walking alone in the garden, one evening, about a fortnight afterwards.", "그로부터 보름쯤 지난 어느 저녁, 나는 정원을 홀로 거닐고 있었다."], ["I remember that evening well.", "나는 그 저녁을 또렷이 기억한다."], ["It was the second in Mr. Micawber’s week of suspense.", "미코버 씨의 그 마음 졸이던 한 주 가운데 둘째 날이었다."], ["There had been rain all day, and there was a damp feeling in the air.", "종일 비가 내렸고, 공기에는 축축한 기운이 감돌았다."], ["The leaves were thick upon the trees, and heavy with wet; but the rain had ceased, though the sky was still dark; and the hopeful birds were singing cheerfully.", "나뭇잎은 무성하게 우거진 채 물기를 머금어 묵직했다. 그러나 비는 그쳤고, 하늘은 여전히 어두웠으며, 희망에 찬 새들은 명랑하게 지저귀고 있었다."], ["As I walked to and fro in the garden, and the twilight began to close around me, their little voices were hushed; and that peculiar silence which belongs to such an evening in the country when the lightest trees are quite still, save for the occasional droppings from their boughs, prevailed.", "내가 정원을 이리저리 거니는 동안 황혼이 내 둘레로 내려앉기 시작하자 새들의 작은 노랫소리도 잦아들었다. 그리고 시골의 그런 저녁에만 깃드는 그 독특한 고요가 사방을 채웠다. 이따금 가지에서 물방울이 똑똑 떨어지는 것 말고는, 가장 여린 나무들조차 미동도 없었다."]], [["There was a little green perspective of trellis-work and ivy at the side of our cottage, through which I could see, from the garden where I was walking, into the road before the house.", "우리 오두막 옆으로는 격자 울타리와 담쟁이덩굴이 이루는 자그마한 초록의 길이 나 있었는데, 내가 거닐던 정원에서 그 사이로 집 앞 길이 내다보였다."], ["I happened to turn my eyes towards this place, as I was thinking of many things; and I saw a figure beyond, dressed in a plain cloak.", "이런저런 생각에 잠겨 있다가, 마침 나는 그쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다. 그러자 그 너머에 수수한 망토를 두른 사람 하나가 서 있는 것이 보였다."], ["It was bending eagerly towards me, and beckoning.", "그 사람은 나를 향해 간절히 몸을 기울이며 손짓하고 있었다."]], [["‘Martha!’ said I, going to it.", "\"마사!\" 내가 그쪽으로 다가가며 말했다."]], [["‘Can you come with me?’ she inquired, in an agitated whisper.", "\"저랑 같이 가 주실 수 있어요?\" 마사가 흥분한 듯 떨리는 목소리로 속삭이듯 물었다."], ["‘I have been to him, and he is not at home.", "\"아저씨 댁에 갔는데 집에 안 계셨어요."], ["I wrote down where he was to come, and left it on his table with my own hand.", "어디로 오시면 되는지 제 손으로 적어서 탁자에 두고 왔어요. 곧 돌아오신다더군요."], ["They said he would not be out long.", "아저씨께 전할 소식이 있어요."], ["I have tidings for him. Can you come directly?’", "지금 바로 가 주실 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["My answer was, to pass out at the gate immediately.", "내 대답은, 곧장 대문 밖으로 나서는 것이었다."], ["She made a hasty gesture with her hand, as if to entreat my patience and my silence, and turned towards London, whence, as her dress betokened, she had come expeditiously on foot.", "마사는 손으로 황급히 몸짓을 했는데, 마치 참아 달라고, 조용히 해 달라고 사정하는 듯했다. 그러고는 런던 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 차림새로 보아 거기서부터 부지런히 걸어온 모양이었다."]], [["I asked her if that were not our destination?", "나는 그곳이 우리가 갈 곳이냐고 물었다."], ["On her motioning Yes, with the same hasty gesture as before, I stopped an empty coach that was coming by, and we got into it.", "마사가 조금 전과 똑같이 황급한 몸짓으로 그렇다고 끄덕이자, 나는 마침 지나가던 빈 마차를 세웠고, 우리는 그 안에 올라탔다."], ["When I asked her where the coachman was to drive, she answered, ‘Anywhere near Golden Square!", "마부에게 어디로 몰면 되느냐고 묻자, 마사는 대답했다. \"골든 광장 근처 아무 데나요! 그리고 빨리요!\""], ["And quick!’--then shrunk into a corner, with one trembling hand before her face, and the other making the former gesture, as if she could not bear a voice.", "그러더니 한쪽 구석으로 움츠러들어, 떨리는 한 손을 얼굴 앞에 대고, 다른 손으로는 아까와 같은 몸짓을 했다. 마치 어떤 목소리도 견딜 수 없다는 듯이."]], [["Now much disturbed, and dazzled with conflicting gleams of hope and dread, I looked at her for some explanation.", "나는 이제 몹시 마음이 어지러워, 희망과 두려움이 엇갈려 번쩍이는 빛에 눈이 부신 채로, 무슨 설명이라도 듣고 싶어 마사를 바라보았다."], ["But seeing how strongly she desired to remain quiet, and feeling that it was my own natural inclination too, at such a time, I did not attempt to break the silence.", "그러나 마사가 조용히 있기를 얼마나 간절히 바라는지를 보았고, 또 이런 때에는 그것이 나 자신의 본능이기도 하다고 느꼈기에, 나는 굳이 그 침묵을 깨려 들지 않았다."], ["We proceeded without a word being spoken.", "우리는 한마디도 나누지 않은 채 나아갔다."], ["Sometimes she glanced out of the window, as though she thought we were going slowly, though indeed we were going fast; but otherwise remained exactly as at first.", "이따금 마사는 창밖을 흘끗거렸는데, 마치 우리가 더디게 가고 있다고 여기는 듯했지만, 실은 빠르게 가고 있었다. 그것 말고는 처음 모습 그대로였다."]], [["We alighted at one of the entrances to the Square she had mentioned, where I directed the coach to wait, not knowing but that we might have some occasion for it.", "우리는 마사가 말한 광장의 어느 입구에서 내렸다. 나는 마차에게 거기서 기다리라고 일렀는데, 혹시 다시 쓸 일이 있을지 몰라서였다."], ["She laid her hand on my arm, and hurried me on to one of the sombre streets, of which there are several in that part, where the houses were once fair dwellings in the occupation of single families, but have, and had, long degenerated into poor lodgings let off in rooms.", "마사는 내 팔에 손을 얹고는, 그 동네에 몇 군데 있는 음침한 거리 가운데 한 곳으로 나를 서둘러 데려갔다. 그 거리의 집들은 한때 한 가족씩 들어 살던 번듯한 살림집이었으나, 지금은, 그리고 오래전부터, 방마다 세를 놓는 초라한 셋집으로 영락해 버린 곳이었다."], ["Entering at the open door of one of these, and releasing my arm, she beckoned me to follow her up the common staircase, which was like a tributary channel to the street.", "그 가운데 한 집의 열린 문으로 들어서더니, 마사는 내 팔을 놓고는 공동 계단을 따라 자기를 뒤따르라고 손짓했다. 그 계단은 마치 큰 거리로 흘러드는 샛물줄기 같았다."]], [["The house swarmed with inmates.", "집 안은 세든 사람들로 바글바글했다."], ["As we went up, doors of rooms were opened and people’s heads put out; and we passed other people on the stairs, who were coming down.", "우리가 올라가는 동안 방문들이 열리고 사람들이 고개를 내밀었다. 계단에서는 내려오는 다른 사람들과 마주쳤다."], ["In glancing up from the outside, before we entered, I had seen women and children lolling at the windows over flower-pots; and we seemed to have attracted their curiosity, for these were principally the observers who looked out of their doors.", "안으로 들어서기 전, 바깥에서 올려다보았을 때 나는 여자들과 아이들이 화분 너머로 창가에 기대어 있는 것을 보았다. 우리가 그들의 호기심을 끈 모양이었는지, 문밖을 내다보던 이들이 대개 그 사람들이었다."], ["It was a broad panelled staircase, with massive balustrades of some dark wood; cornices above the doors, ornamented with carved fruit and flowers; and broad seats in the windows.", "널찍한 판자벽 계단에는 어떤 검은 나무로 만든 묵직한 난간이 달려 있었고, 문 위에는 깎아 새긴 과일과 꽃으로 꾸민 처마 장식이 있었으며, 창가에는 넓은 자리가 마련되어 있었다."], ["But all these tokens of past grandeur were miserably decayed and dirty; rot, damp, and age, had weakened the flooring, which in many places was unsound and even unsafe.", "그러나 지난날의 영화를 보여 주는 이 모든 자취는 비참하게 썩고 더럽혀져 있었다. 부패와 습기와 세월이 마룻바닥을 약하게 만들어 놓아, 곳곳이 부실하고 심지어 위태롭기까지 했다."], ["Some attempts had been made, I noticed, to infuse new blood into this dwindling frame, by repairing the costly old wood-work here and there with common deal; but it was like the marriage of a reduced old noble to a plebeian pauper, and each party to the ill-assorted union shrunk away from the other.", "이 쇠락해 가는 골조에 새 피를 불어넣어 보려는 시도가 있었던 듯, 군데군데 값비싼 옛 목공품을 흔한 전나무 판자로 때워 놓은 것이 눈에 띄었다. 그러나 그것은 마치 몰락한 늙은 귀족과 천민 거지의 결혼 같아서, 어울리지 않는 이 짝의 두 편이 서로에게서 움츠러들고 있었다."], ["Several of the back windows on the staircase had been darkened or wholly blocked up.", "계단의 뒤편 창문 가운데 몇몇은 어둡게 가려졌거나 아예 막혀 있었다."], ["In those that remained, there was scarcely any glass; and, through the crumbling frames by which the bad air seemed always to come in, and never to go out, I saw, through other glassless windows, into other houses in a similar condition, and looked giddily down into a wretched yard, which was the common dust-heap of the mansion.", "남아 있는 창에는 유리라곤 거의 없었다. 그리고 탁한 공기가 늘 들어오기만 하고 나가지는 못하는 듯한 그 부스러진 창틀 사이로, 나는 또 다른 유리 없는 창들 너머 비슷한 처지의 다른 집들을 보았고, 어지러운 눈으로 비참한 안마당을 내려다보았다. 그곳은 이 저택의 공동 쓰레기 더미였다."]], [["We proceeded to the top-storey of the house.", "우리는 집 꼭대기 층까지 올라갔다."], ["Two or three times, by the way, I thought I observed in the indistinct light the skirts of a female figure going up before us.", "도중에 두세 번, 나는 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 우리 앞서 올라가는 어떤 여자의 치맛자락을 본 듯했다."], ["As we turned to ascend the last flight of stairs between us and the roof, we caught a full view of this figure pausing for a moment, at a door.", "우리와 지붕 사이의 마지막 계단을 오르려고 몸을 돌렸을 때, 우리는 그 사람이 어느 문 앞에서 잠시 멈춰 서 있는 모습을 온전히 보게 되었다."], ["Then it turned the handle, and went in.", "이윽고 그 사람은 손잡이를 돌리고 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘What’s this!’ said Martha, in a whisper.", "\"이게 뭐죠!\" 마사가 속삭이듯 말했다."], ["‘She has gone into my room.", "\"저 여자가 제 방으로 들어갔어요."], ["I don’t know her!’", "저는 모르는 사람인데!\""]], [["I knew her.", "나는 그 여자를 알았다."], ["I had recognized her with amazement, for Miss Dartle.", "나는 놀라움 속에서 그 여자를 알아보았다. 다틀 양이었다."]], [["I said something to the effect that it was a lady whom I had seen before, in a few words, to my conductress; and had scarcely done so, when we heard her voice in the room, though not, from where we stood, what she was saying.", "나는 짤막한 몇 마디로, 전에 본 적 있는 부인이라는 뜻의 말을 안내자에게 건넸다. 그 말이 채 끝나기도 전에, 우리는 그 방 안에서 다틀 양의 목소리를 들었다. 다만 우리가 선 자리에서는 무슨 말을 하는지까지는 들리지 않았다."], ["Martha, with an astonished look, repeated her former action, and softly led me up the stairs; and then, by a little back-door which seemed to have no lock, and which she pushed open with a touch, into a small empty garret with a low sloping roof, little better than a cupboard.", "마사는 놀란 얼굴로 아까의 그 몸짓을 되풀이하며, 나를 살며시 계단 위로 이끌었다. 그러고는 자물쇠가 없는 듯한 작은 뒷문을 손끝으로 밀어 열고, 천장이 낮게 비스듬히 기운, 벽장이나 다름없는 작고 텅 빈 다락방으로 나를 데려갔다."], ["Between this, and the room she had called hers, there was a small door of communication, standing partly open.", "이 방과, 마사가 자기 방이라고 한 그 방 사이에는 자그마한 통하는 문이 하나 있었는데, 반쯤 열린 채였다."], ["Here we stopped, breathless with our ascent, and she placed her hand lightly on my lips.", "우리는 여기서 멈췄다. 올라오느라 숨이 가빴다. 마사는 내 입술에 손을 가볍게 댔다."], ["I could only see, of the room beyond, that it was pretty large; that there was a bed in it; and that there were some common pictures of ships upon the walls.", "건너편 방에서 내가 볼 수 있는 것이라곤, 방이 제법 넓다는 것, 침대가 하나 있다는 것, 그리고 벽에 흔한 배 그림 몇 점이 걸려 있다는 것뿐이었다."], ["I could not see Miss Dartle, or the person whom we had heard her address.", "다틀 양도, 그녀가 말을 건네는 상대도 나는 볼 수 없었다."], ["Certainly, my companion could not, for my position was the best.", "내 동행은 더더욱 볼 수 없었으니, 자리는 내 쪽이 가장 나았던 것이다."], ["A dead silence prevailed for some moments.", "한동안 죽은 듯한 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Martha kept one hand on my lips, and raised the other in a listening attitude.", "마사는 한 손을 내 입술에 댄 채, 다른 손을 들어 귀 기울이는 자세를 취했다."]], [["‘It matters little to me her not being at home,’ said Rosa Dartle haughtily, ‘I know nothing of her.", "\"그 애가 집에 없든 말든 나에겐 별일 아니지.\" 로사 다틀이 거만하게 말했다. \"난 그 애 따위는 모르니까."], ["It is you I come to see.’", "내가 보러 온 건 너야.\""]], [["‘Me?’ replied a soft voice.", "\"저요?\" 부드러운 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["At the sound of it, a thrill went through my frame.", "그 목소리가 들리는 순간, 전율이 내 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다."], ["For it was Emily’s!", "에밀리의 목소리였던 것이다!"]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Miss Dartle, ‘I have come to look at you. What?", "\"그래.\" 다틀 양이 받았다. \"널 보러 왔어. 뭐?"], ["You are not ashamed of the face that has done so much?’", "그렇게 많은 일을 저지른 그 낯짝이 부끄럽지도 않은 모양이지?\""]], [["The resolute and unrelenting hatred of her tone, its cold stern sharpness, and its mastered rage, presented her before me, as if I had seen her standing in the light.", "그 말투에 담긴 단호하고 가차 없는 증오, 그 차갑고 매서운 날카로움, 그리고 억눌린 분노가, 마치 환한 빛 속에 선 그녀를 직접 본 것처럼 내 눈앞에 그녀를 그려 냈다."], ["I saw the flashing black eyes, and the passion-wasted figure; and I saw the scar, with its white track cutting through her lips, quivering and throbbing as she spoke.", "나는 번뜩이는 검은 두 눈과 격정에 야윈 몸을 보았다. 그리고 그 흉터를, 입술을 가르며 지나가는 하얀 자국을 보았다. 그녀가 말할 때마다 그것이 떨리고 꿈틀거렸다."]], [["‘I have come to see,’ she said, ‘James Steerforth’s fancy; the girl who ran away with him, and is the town-talk of the commonest people of her native place; the bold, flaunting, practised companion of persons like James Steerforth.", "\"난 보러 왔어.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제임스 스티어포스가 노리갯감으로 삼은 계집을."], ["I want to know what such a thing is like.’", "그놈과 함께 달아나, 제 고향에서 가장 천한 자들의 입방아에 오르내리는 계집을. 제임스 스티어포스 같은 사내들의 뻔뻔하고 요란하고 닳고 닳은 동무를. 난 그런 물건이 어떻게 생겨 먹었는지 알고 싶었어.\""]], [["There was a rustle, as if the unhappy girl, on whom she heaped these taunts, ran towards the door, and the speaker swiftly interposed herself before it.", "옷자락 스치는 소리가 났다. 마치 이 야유 세례를 받던 불행한 처녀가 문 쪽으로 달려가려는 듯했고, 말하던 여자가 잽싸게 그 앞을 가로막은 듯했다."], ["It was succeeded by a moment’s pause.", "그러고는 잠깐 정적이 이어졌다."]], [["When Miss Dartle spoke again, it was through her set teeth, and with a stamp upon the ground.", "다틀 양이 다시 입을 열었을 때, 그것은 앙다문 잇새로 나오는 소리였고, 발로 바닥을 쾅 구르는 소리가 함께였다."]], [["‘Stay there!’ she said, ‘or I’ll proclaim you to the house, and the whole street!", "\"거기 가만있어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 이 집 사람들한테, 온 거리에 네 정체를 떠벌릴 테니까!"], ["If you try to evade me, I’ll stop you, if it’s by the hair, and raise the very stones against you!’", "날 피해 달아나려 들면, 머리채를 잡아서라도 붙들고, 길바닥의 돌멩이까지 일으켜 너에게 들이댈 거야!\""]], [["A frightened murmur was the only reply that reached my ears.", "겁에 질린 웅얼거림만이 내 귀에 닿은 유일한 대답이었다."], ["A silence succeeded. I did not know what to do.", "정적이 뒤따랐다. 나는 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Much as I desired to put an end to the interview, I felt that I had no right to present myself; that it was for Mr. Peggotty alone to see her and recover her.", "이 만남을 끝내고 싶은 마음이 아무리 간절해도, 나에게는 나설 권리가 없다고 느꼈다. 에밀리를 만나 되찾는 일은 오직 패거티 아저씨만의 몫이었다."], ["Would he never come?", "아저씨는 영영 안 오시는 걸까?"], ["I thought impatiently.", "나는 애가 타서 생각했다."]], [["‘So!’ said Rosa Dartle, with a contemptuous laugh, ‘I see her at last!", "\"그래!\" 로사 다틀이 경멸 어린 웃음을 흘리며 말했다. \"이제야 그 애 꼴을 보는구나!"], ["Why, he was a poor creature to be taken by that delicate mock-modesty, and that hanging head!’", "흥, 그 가냘프고 짐짓 꾸민 정숙함과 푹 수그린 그 머리에 넘어가다니, 그놈도 참 못난 위인이었어!\""]], [["‘Oh, for Heaven’s sake, spare me!’ exclaimed Emily.", "\"오, 제발, 저를 좀 봐주세요!\" 에밀리가 외쳤다."], ["‘Whoever you are, you know my pitiable story, and for Heaven’s sake spare me, if you would be spared yourself!’", "\"당신이 누구든, 제 가엾은 사연을 아시잖아요. 제발, 당신도 봐줌을 받고 싶으시거든, 저를 좀 봐주세요!\""]], [["‘If I would be spared!’ returned the other fiercely; ‘what is there in common between US, do you think!’", "\"내가 봐줌을 받고 싶다고!\" 상대가 사납게 받아쳤다. \"너와 나 사이에 도대체 무슨 공통점이 있다고 생각하는 거지!\""]], [["‘Nothing but our sex,’ said Emily, with a burst of tears.", "\"우리가 같은 여자라는 것밖에는요.\" 에밀리가 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다."]], [["‘And that,’ said Rosa Dartle, ‘is so strong a claim, preferred by one so infamous, that if I had any feeling in my breast but scorn and abhorrence of you, it would freeze it up.", "\"바로 그게,\" 로사 다틀이 말했다. \"너처럼 추악한 것이 내세우기엔 너무도 강력한 권리라서, 만약 내 가슴에 너에 대한 멸시와 혐오 말고 다른 감정이 조금이라도 있었다면, 그 권리가 그걸 꽁꽁 얼려 버렸을 거다."], ["Our sex! You are an honour to our sex!’", "우리가 같은 여자라니! 너는 우리 여자들의 자랑거리로구나!\""]], [["‘I have deserved this,’ said Emily, ‘but it’s dreadful!", "\"이런 대접을 받아 마땅하다는 거 알아요.\" 에밀리가 말했다. \"그렇지만 너무 끔찍해요!"], ["Dear, dear lady, think what I have suffered, and how I am fallen!", "부인, 부인, 제가 얼마나 괴로워했는지, 제가 얼마나 나락에 떨어졌는지 헤아려 주세요!"], ["Oh, Martha, come back!", "오, 마사, 돌아와!"], ["Oh, home, home!’", "오, 집으로, 집으로!\""]], [["Miss Dartle placed herself in a chair, within view of the door, and looked downward, as if Emily were crouching on the floor before her.", "다틀 양은 문이 보이는 위치의 의자에 앉아, 마치 에밀리가 자기 앞 바닥에 웅크리고 있기라도 한 듯 아래를 내려다보았다."], ["Being now between me and the light, I could see her curled lip, and her cruel eyes intently fixed on one place, with a greedy triumph.", "이제 그녀가 나와 빛 사이에 자리해 있어서, 나는 비웃듯 말려 올라간 그녀의 입술과, 한곳에 매섭게 고정된 잔인한 두 눈을, 탐욕스러운 승리감을 띤 그 눈을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["‘Listen to what I say!’ she said; ‘and reserve your false arts for your dupes.", "\"내 말 잘 들어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 그 거짓 수작은 네 어수룩한 봉들한테나 써먹어."], ["Do you hope to move me by your tears?", "눈물 따위로 날 움직일 수 있을 것 같아?"], ["No more than you could charm me by your smiles, you purchased slave.’", "네 웃음으로 날 호릴 수 없는 것만큼이나 어림없는 짓이야, 이 돈에 팔려 온 노예야.\""]], [["‘Oh, have some mercy on me!’ cried Emily.", "\"오, 저에게 자비를 좀 베풀어 주세요!\" 에밀리가 부르짖었다."], ["‘Show me some compassion, or I shall die mad!’", "\"저에게 동정을 좀 보여 주세요. 안 그러면 저는 미쳐서 죽고 말 거예요!\""]], [["‘It would be no great penance,’ said Rosa Dartle, ‘for your crimes.", "\"그건 네 죄에 비하면 대수로운 벌도 아니지.\" 로사 다틀이 말했다."], ["Do you know what you have done?", "\"네가 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 알기나 해?"], ["Do you ever think of the home you have laid waste?’", "네가 쑥대밭으로 만들어 놓은 그 집을 한 번이라도 떠올려 보긴 해?\""]], [["‘Oh, is there ever night or day, when I don’t think of it!’ cried Emily; and now I could just see her, on her knees, with her head thrown back, her pale face looking upward, her hands wildly clasped and held out, and her hair streaming about her.", "\"오, 그걸 떠올리지 않는 밤이나 낮이 단 한순간이라도 있는 줄 아세요!\" 에밀리가 부르짖었다. 이제 나는 겨우 그 애를 볼 수 있었다."], ["‘Has there ever been a single minute, waking or sleeping, when it hasn’t been before me, just as it used to be in the lost days when I turned my back upon it for ever and for ever! Oh, home, home!", "무릎을 꿇은 채 머리를 뒤로 젖히고, 창백한 얼굴을 위로 향하고, 두 손을 미친 듯이 맞잡아 앞으로 내밀고, 머리카락은 사방으로 흘러내려 있었다. \"깨어 있을 때나 잠들었을 때나, 그게 내 눈앞에 떠오르지 않은 순간이 단 일 분이라도 있었던 줄 아세요?"], ["Oh dear, dear uncle, if you ever could have known the agony your love would cause me when I fell away from good, you never would have shown it to me so constant, much as you felt it; but would have been angry to me, at least once in my life, that I might have had some comfort!", "영영, 영영 그 집에 등을 돌리던 그 잃어버린 날들의 모습 그대로 말이에요! 오, 집으로, 집으로! 오, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아저씨, 제가 선한 길에서 떨어져 나갔을 때 아저씨의 사랑이 저에게 얼마나 큰 고통을 줄지 진작 아셨더라면, 아무리 그 사랑이 깊더라도 저에게 그토록 변함없이 베풀지는 않으셨을 거예요."], ["I have none, none, no comfort upon earth, for all of them were always fond of me!’ She dropped on her face, before the imperious figure in the chair, with an imploring effort to clasp the skirt of her dress.", "오히려 살면서 단 한 번이라도 저에게 화를 내 주셨더라면, 저는 조금이나마 위안을 얻었을 텐데! 저에겐 위안이라곤 없어요, 하나도, 이 세상에 위안이라곤 하나도 없어요, 다들 늘 저를 그토록 아껴 주셨으니까요!\" 그 애는 의자에 앉은 도도한 사람 앞에 엎드려, 그 치맛자락이라도 붙들어 보려고 애원하듯 안간힘을 썼다."]], [["Rosa Dartle sat looking down upon her, as inflexible as a figure of brass.", "로사 다틀은 놋쇠로 빚은 형상처럼 꿈쩍도 않고 그 애를 내려다보고 앉아 있었다."], ["Her lips were tightly compressed, as if she knew that she must keep a strong constraint upon herself--I write what I sincerely believe--or she would be tempted to strike the beautiful form with her foot.", "그녀의 입술은 꽉 다물려 있었는데, 마치 자기 자신을 단단히 억눌러야 한다는 것을 — 나는 진심으로 믿는 바를 쓰는 것이다 — 알고 있는 듯했다. 안 그러면 그 아름다운 자태를 발로 차 버리고 싶은 충동에 사로잡힐 것이기 때문이었다."], ["I saw her, distinctly, and the whole power of her face and character seemed forced into that expression.---Would he never come?", "나는 그녀를 똑똑히 보았다. 그녀의 얼굴과 성격의 모든 힘이 그 표정 하나에 짜내어진 듯했다. 아저씨는 영영 안 오시는 걸까?"]], [["‘The miserable vanity of these earth-worms!’ she said, when she had so far controlled the angry heavings of her breast, that she could trust herself to speak.", "\"이 땅벌레들의 가련한 허영이라니!\" 그녀는 가슴속에 끓어오르는 분노를 어느 정도 다스려 말을 꺼낼 만큼 자제력을 되찾자 이렇게 말했다. \"네 집이라고!"], ["‘YOUR home! Do you imagine that I bestow a thought on it, or suppose you could do any harm to that low place, which money would not pay for, and handsomely?", "내가 그런 데 신경이나 쓸 줄 알아? 돈이면, 그것도 넉넉히 치르면 메우지 못할 손해를 네가 그 천한 곳에 끼칠 수 있다고 생각해?"], ["YOUR home! You were a part of the trade of your home, and were bought and sold like any other vendible thing your people dealt in.’", "네 집이라고! 너는 네 집의 장사 밑천 가운데 하나였고, 네 집안사람들이 다루던 다른 팔 물건처럼 사고팔린 거야.\""]], [["‘Oh, not that!’ cried Emily.", "\"오, 그건 아니에요!\" 에밀리가 부르짖었다."], ["‘Say anything of me; but don’t visit my disgrace and shame, more than I have done, on folks who are as honourable as you!", "\"저에 대해선 무슨 말이든 하세요. 하지만 제 치욕과 수치를 제가 이미 짊어진 것보다 더, 당신 못지않게 떳떳한 사람들에게 들씌우지는 말아 주세요!"], ["Have some respect for them, as you are a lady, if you have no mercy for me.’", "당신이 귀부인이라면 그분들을 존중해 주세요. 저에게 베풀 자비가 없으시더라도요.\""]], [["‘I speak,’ she said, not deigning to take any heed of this appeal, and drawing away her dress from the contamination of Emily’s touch, ‘I speak of HIS home--where I live.", "\"내가 말하는 건,\" 그녀는 이 호소엔 눈길 한 번 줄 가치도 없다는 듯, 에밀리의 손길이 닿아 더럽혀질세라 제 옷자락을 끌어당기며 말했다. \"내가 말하는 건 그자의 집이야."], ["Here,’ she said, stretching out her hand with her contemptuous laugh, and looking down upon the prostrate girl, ‘is a worthy cause of division between lady-mother and gentleman-son; of grief in a house where she wouldn’t have been admitted as a kitchen-girl; of anger, and repining, and reproach.", "내가 사는 곳 말이야. 자,\" 그녀는 경멸 어린 웃음을 띠고 손을 뻗으며, 엎드린 처녀를 내려다보며 말했다. \"여기 귀부인 어머니와 신사 아들 사이를 갈라놓을 만한 훌륭한 빌미가 있구나."], ["This piece of pollution, picked up from the water-side, to be made much of for an hour, and then tossed back to her original place!’", "너 같은 건 부엌데기로도 들이지 않았을 그 집에 슬픔을 안기고, 분노와 한탄과 비난을 불러일으킨 빌미가. 물가에서 주워 올린 이 더러운 오물 조각이, 한때 귀하게 떠받들어지다가, 다시 제자리로 내동댕이쳐진 거야!\""]], [["‘No! no!’ cried Emily, clasping her hands together.", "\"아니에요! 아니에요!\""], ["‘When he first came into my way--that the day had never dawned upon me, and he had met me being carried to my grave!--I had been brought up as virtuous as you or any lady, and was going to be the wife of as good a man as you or any lady in the world can ever marry.", "에밀리가 두 손을 맞잡으며 부르짖었다. \"그이가 처음 제 앞에 나타났을 때 — 차라리 그날이 밝지 않았더라면, 그이가 무덤으로 실려 가는 저를 만났더라면! — 저는 당신이나 어느 귀부인 못지않게 정숙하게 자랐고, 당신이나 세상 어느 귀부인이 맞을 수 있는 그 누구 못지않게 착한 사내의 아내가 될 참이었어요."], ["If you live in his home and know him, you know, perhaps, what his power with a weak, vain girl might be.", "당신이 그이의 집에 살며 그이를 안다면, 그이가 약하고 허영심 많은 처녀에게 어떤 힘을 휘두를 수 있는지도 아마 아시겠지요. 저는 변명하려는 게 아니에요."], ["I don’t defend myself, but I know well, and he knows well, or he will know when he comes to die, and his mind is troubled with it, that he used all his power to deceive me, and that I believed him, trusted him, and loved him!’", "하지만 저는 잘 알아요, 그이도 잘 알아요. 아니면 죽음을 맞이할 때, 마음이 그 일로 괴로워질 때 알게 되겠지요. 그이가 저를 속이려고 온갖 수를 다 썼다는 것을, 그리고 제가 그이를 믿고, 의지하고, 사랑했다는 것을요!\""]], [["Rosa Dartle sprang up from her seat; recoiled; and in recoiling struck at her, with a face of such malignity, so darkened and disfigured by passion, that I had almost thrown myself between them.", "로사 다틀은 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 뒷걸음질을 쳤다. 그러더니 뒷걸음질하면서 에밀리를 후려쳤는데, 그 얼굴이 어찌나 악의에 차고, 격정으로 일그러지고 시커멓게 변했던지, 나는 하마터면 두 사람 사이로 몸을 던질 뻔했다."], ["The blow, which had no aim, fell upon the air.", "겨냥 없이 휘두른 그 손길은 허공을 갈랐을 뿐이었다."], ["As she now stood panting, looking at her with the utmost detestation that she was capable of expressing, and trembling from head to foot with rage and scorn, I thought I had never seen such a sight, and never could see such another.", "이제 그녀는 헐떡이며 서서, 자기가 드러낼 수 있는 최고의 혐오를 담아 에밀리를 노려보았고, 분노와 멸시로 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있었다. 나는 그런 광경은 일찍이 본 적이 없었고, 앞으로도 두 번 다시 볼 수 없으리라 생각했다."]], [["‘YOU love him?", "\"네가 그놈을 사랑한다고? 네가?\""], ["You?’ she cried, with her clenched hand, quivering as if it only wanted a weapon to stab the object of her wrath.", "그녀가 부르짖었다. 움켜쥔 손이, 마치 그 분노의 대상을 찌를 무기 하나만 있으면 된다는 듯 부들부들 떨렸다."]], [["Emily had shrunk out of my view.", "에밀리는 내 시야 밖으로 움츠러들어 있었다."], ["There was no reply.", "아무 대답도 없었다."]], [["‘And tell that to ME,’ she added, ‘with your shameful lips?", "\"그걸 나한테 지껄여?\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그 수치스러운 입으로?"], ["Why don’t they whip these creatures?", "이런 것들에게 어째서 채찍질을 안 하는 거지?"], ["If I could order it to be done, I would have this girl whipped to death.’", "내가 명령을 내릴 수만 있다면, 이 계집을 채찍으로 때려죽이게 할 텐데.\""]], [["And so she would, I have no doubt.", "그러고도 남았으리라, 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["I would not have trusted her with the rack itself, while that furious look lasted.", "그 사나운 표정이 가시지 않는 한, 나는 그녀에게 고문대조차 맡기지 않았을 것이다."], ["She slowly, very slowly, broke into a laugh, and pointed at Emily with her hand, as if she were a sight of shame for gods and men.", "그녀는 천천히, 아주 천천히 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 마치 에밀리가 신들과 사람들 앞에 내놓인 수치의 구경거리라도 되는 양 손으로 그 애를 가리켰다."]], [["‘SHE love!’ she said.", "\"저 계집이 사랑을 한다고!\""], ["‘THAT carrion!", "그녀가 말했다."], ["And he ever cared for her, she’d tell me. Ha, ha!", "\"저 썩은 고깃덩이가! 그리고 그놈이 자기를 아꼈었다고 나한테 말하다니. 하, 하!"], ["The liars that these traders are!’", "이런 장사치들이란 거짓말쟁이가 따로 없다니까!\""]], [["Her mockery was worse than her undisguised rage.", "그녀의 조롱은 노골적인 분노보다 더 끔찍했다."], ["Of the two, I would have much preferred to be the object of the latter.", "둘 중 하나를 고르라면, 나는 차라리 그 분노의 대상이 되는 편이 훨씬 나았을 것이다."], ["But, when she suffered it to break loose, it was only for a moment. She had chained it up again, and however it might tear her within, she subdued it to herself.", "그러나 그녀가 그 조롱을 풀어 놓은 것은 한순간뿐이었다. 그녀는 그것을 다시 사슬로 묶어 두었고, 그것이 속을 아무리 갈가리 찢어 놓을지언정, 기어이 제 안에 가두어 다스렸다."]], [["‘I came here, you pure fountain of love,’ she said, ‘to see--as I began by telling you--what such a thing as you was like.", "\"내가 여기 온 건, 이 순결한 사랑의 샘물 같은 것아,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"처음에 말했듯이, 너 같은 물건이 어떻게 생겨 먹었는지 보러 온 거야."], ["I was curious.", "궁금했거든."], ["I am satisfied.", "이제 만족스러워."], ["Also to tell you, that you had best seek that home of yours, with all speed, and hide your head among those excellent people who are expecting you, and whom your money will console.", "또 한 가지 일러 둘 게 있어서야. 너는 어서 빨리 그 집으로 찾아가, 너를 기다리는, 네 돈이면 위로받을 그 갸륵한 사람들 틈에 머리를 처박는 게 상책이라는 걸."], ["When it’s all gone, you can believe, and trust, and love again, you know!", "돈이 다 떨어지면, 또 믿고, 의지하고, 사랑하면 되는 거 아니겠어, 그치!"], ["I thought you a broken toy that had lasted its time; a worthless spangle that was tarnished, and thrown away.", "난 너를 제 수명을 다 산 부서진 노리개, 빛바래 내던져진 하찮은 번쩍이쯤으로 여겼어."], ["But, finding you true gold, a very lady, and an ill-used innocent, with a fresh heart full of love and trustfulness--which you look like, and is quite consistent with your story!--I have something more to say.", "그런데 알고 보니 네가 진짜 금이고, 어엿한 귀부인이고, 사랑과 신뢰로 가득 찬 싱그러운 마음을 지닌, 모진 대접을 받은 순진한 아이였다니 — 네 꼬락서니가 딱 그렇고, 네 사연하고도 아주 잘 들어맞는구나! — 그러니 한마디 더 해 둬야겠어. 잘 들어."], ["Attend to it; for what I say I’ll do.", "난 말한 건 반드시 하니까."], ["Do you hear me, you fairy spirit?", "듣고 있어, 이 요정 같은 것아?"], ["What I say, I mean to do!’", "내가 말한 건, 기어이 할 작정이야!\""]], [["Her rage got the better of her again, for a moment; but it passed over her face like a spasm, and left her smiling.", "분노가 또 한 번 그녀를 사로잡았지만, 그것은 발작처럼 그녀의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나가고, 그녀를 미소 짓는 채로 남겨 두었다."]], [["‘Hide yourself,’ she pursued, ‘if not at home, somewhere.", "\"몸을 숨겨.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그 집이 아니거든 어디든."], ["Let it be somewhere beyond reach; in some obscure life--or, better still, in some obscure death.", "손 닿지 않는 어딘가에 있어. 어디 이름 없는 삶 속에 — 아니, 그보다도 이름 없는 죽음 속에 있으면 더 좋고."], ["I wonder, if your loving heart will not break, you have found no way of helping it to be still!", "네 그 사랑 넘치는 마음이 터져 버리지 않을 거라면, 그걸 잠재울 방도를 아직도 못 찾았다니 놀랍구나!"], ["I have heard of such means sometimes.", "나는 그런 방도가 더러 있다고 들었어."], ["I believe they may be easily found.’", "쉽게 찾을 수 있을걸.\""]], [["A low crying, on the part of Emily, interrupted her here.", "여기서 에밀리의 나직한 울음소리가 그녀의 말을 가로막았다."], ["She stopped, and listened to it as if it were music.", "그녀는 말을 멈추고, 그것이 음악이라도 되는 양 귀를 기울였다."]], [["‘I am of a strange nature, perhaps,’ Rosa Dartle went on; ‘but I can’t breathe freely in the air you breathe.", "\"내가 좀 별난 성미인지도 모르지.\" 로사 다틀이 말을 이었다. \"하지만 난 네가 들이쉬는 공기 속에선 숨을 편히 쉴 수가 없어. 메스꺼워."], ["I find it sickly. Therefore, I will have it cleared; I will have it purified of you.", "그러니 난 그 공기를 깨끗이 치울 거야. 너를 말끔히 몰아내 버리겠어."], ["If you live here tomorrow, I’ll have your story and your character proclaimed on the common stair.", "내일도 네가 여기 살고 있으면, 이 공동 계단에 네 사연과 네 정체를 떠벌릴 테다."], ["There are decent women in the house, I am told; and it is a pity such a light as you should be among them, and concealed.", "이 집엔 점잖은 여자들이 산다더군. 너 같은 빛이 그들 틈에 섞여 숨어 있다니 안타까운 노릇이지."], ["If, leaving here, you seek any refuge in this town in any character but your true one (which you are welcome to bear, without molestation from me), the same service shall be done you, if I hear of your retreat.", "여기를 떠나, 이 도시 어디에서든 네 본래의 정체 말고 다른 행세로 몸을 숨기려 든다면 (본래의 정체로라면야 내가 방해 않을 테니 얼마든지 그러렴), 네 은신처를 알게 되는 즉시 똑같이 해 주마."], ["Being assisted by a gentleman who not long ago aspired to the favour of your hand, I am sanguine as to that.’", "얼마 전까지 네 손을 잡아 보려 안달하던 어느 신사 양반의 도움을 받고 있으니, 그쯤은 거뜬할 거야.\""]], [["Would he never, never come?", "아저씨는 영영, 영영 안 오시는 걸까?"], ["How long was I to bear this?", "이걸 얼마나 더 견뎌야 하나? 얼마나 더 견딜 수 있을까?"], ["How long could I bear it?", "\"오, 어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아!\""], ["‘Oh me, oh me!’ exclaimed the wretched Emily, in a tone that might have touched the hardest heart, I should have thought; but there was no relenting in Rosa Dartle’s smile.", "가엾은 에밀리가 부르짖었다. 그 목소리는 아무리 모진 마음이라도 움직일 만했다고, 나는 생각했다. 그러나 로사 다틀의 미소에는 누그러짐이라곤 없었다."], ["‘What, what, shall I do!’", "\"어쩌면, 어쩌면 좋아, 난 어찌해야 하나!\""]], [["‘Do?’ returned the other.", "\"어찌하다니?\" 상대가 받았다."], ["‘Live happy in your own reflections!", "\"네 그 추억 속에서 행복하게 살려무나!"], ["Consecrate your existence to the recollection of James Steerforth’s tenderness--he would have made you his serving-man’s wife, would he not?---or to feeling grateful to the upright and deserving creature who would have taken you as his gift.", "제임스 스티어포스의 다정함을 되새기는 데 네 평생을 바치든가 — 그자가 너를 제 하인의 아내로 만들어 줬을 거 아니냐, 안 그래? — 아니면 너를 제 선물로 받아 주려던 그 올곧고 갸륵한 위인에게 고마워하는 데 바치든가."], ["Or, if those proud remembrances, and the consciousness of your own virtues, and the honourable position to which they have raised you in the eyes of everything that wears the human shape, will not sustain you, marry that good man, and be happy in his condescension.", "그것도 아니면, 그 자랑스러운 추억들과, 네 미덕에 대한 자부심과, 그 미덕이 사람 꼴을 한 만물의 눈에 너를 끌어올려 준 그 명예로운 자리가 너를 지탱해 주지 못한다면, 그 착한 사내와 결혼해서 그자가 너에게 베풀어 주는 은덕 속에서 행복하렴."], ["If this will not do either, die!", "이것도 저것도 안 되거든, 죽어 버려!"], ["There are doorways and dust-heaps for such deaths, and such despair--find one, and take your flight to Heaven!’", "그런 죽음을 위해, 그런 절망을 위해 마련된 문간이며 쓰레기 더미가 있으니 — 하나 골라잡아, 천국으로 날아가 버리렴!\""]], [["I heard a distant foot upon the stairs.", "멀리 계단 위에서 발소리가 들렸다. 나는 그것을 알았다."], ["I knew it, I was certain. It was his, thank God!", "나는 확신했다. 아저씨의 발소리였다, 하느님 감사합니다!"]], [["She moved slowly from before the door when she said this, and passed out of my sight.", "그녀는 이 말을 하면서 천천히 문 앞에서 비켜나더니, 내 시야 밖으로 사라졌다."]], [["‘But mark!’ she added, slowly and sternly, opening the other door to go away, ‘I am resolved, for reasons that I have and hatreds that I entertain, to cast you out, unless you withdraw from my reach altogether, or drop your pretty mask.", "\"하지만 명심해!\" 그녀가 나가려고 다른 쪽 문을 열며, 천천히 그리고 매섭게 덧붙였다. \"나는, 나에게 있는 이유들과 내가 품은 증오 때문에, 네가 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 아주 물러나거나 그 예쁜 가면을 벗어 버리지 않는 한, 너를 기어이 내쫓기로 작정했다."], ["This is what I had to say; and what I say, I mean to do!’", "이게 내가 하려던 말이야. 그리고 내가 말한 건, 기어이 할 작정이고!\""]], [["The foot upon the stairs came nearer--nearer--passed her as she went down--rushed into the room!", "계단 위의 발소리가 가까이 — 더 가까이 — 내려가는 그녀를 스쳐 지나 — 방 안으로 와락 들이닥쳤다!"]], [["‘Uncle!’", "\"아저씨!\""]], [["A fearful cry followed the word.", "그 말에 뒤이어 무시무시한 외침이 터져 나왔다."], ["I paused a moment, and looking in, saw him supporting her insensible figure in his arms.", "나는 잠시 멈칫했다가 안을 들여다보았고, 아저씨가 정신을 잃은 그 애의 몸을 두 팔로 받쳐 안고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He gazed for a few seconds in the face; then stooped to kiss it--oh, how tenderly!--and drew a handkerchief before it.", "아저씨는 몇 초간 그 얼굴을 들여다보더니, 몸을 숙여 입을 맞추고는 — 오, 얼마나 다정하게! — 손수건을 그 얼굴 위에 덮었다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy,’ he said, in a low tremulous voice, when it was covered, ‘I thank my Heav’nly Father as my dream’s come true!", "\"데이비 도련님,\" 얼굴이 가려지자, 아저씨가 낮고 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"내 꿈이 이뤄진 걸 하늘에 계신 아버지께 감사드립니다!"], ["I thank Him hearty for having guided of me, in His own ways, to my darling!’", "당신의 뜻대로 나를 내 귀한 것에게로 인도해 주신 걸 진심으로 감사드려유!\""]], [["With those words he took her up in his arms; and, with the veiled face lying on his bosom, and addressed towards his own, carried her, motionless and unconscious, down the stairs.", "그 말과 함께 아저씨는 그 애를 두 팔로 들어 안았다. 그리고 가려진 얼굴을 제 가슴에 뉘어 자기 쪽으로 향하게 한 채, 미동도 없이 정신을 잃은 그 애를 안고 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 51. THE BEGINNING OF A LONGER JOURNEY", "# CHAPTER 51. THE BEGINNING OF A LONGER JOURNEY"]], [["It was yet early in the morning of the following day, when, as I was walking in my garden with my aunt (who took little other exercise now, being so much in attendance on my dear Dora), I was told that Mr. Peggotty desired to speak with me.", "이튿날 아침 아직 이른 시각에, 나는 정원에서 고모님과 함께 거닐고 있었는데 — 고모님은 사랑하는 도라를 워낙 극진히 돌보느라 요즈음은 그 밖의 운동이라곤 거의 하지 않으셨다 — 그때 패거티 씨가 나와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다는 전갈을 받았다."], ["He came into the garden to meet me half-way, on my going towards the gate; and bared his head, as it was always his custom to do when he saw my aunt, for whom he had a high respect.", "내가 대문 쪽으로 걸어가자 그는 정원으로 들어와 중간쯤에서 나를 맞았다. 그러고는, 자기가 깊이 존경하는 고모님을 볼 때면 늘 그러듯이 모자를 벗어 머리를 드러냈다."], ["I had been telling her all that had happened overnight.", "나는 간밤에 일어난 일을 죄다 고모님께 말씀드려 둔 터였다."], ["Without saying a word, she walked up with a cordial face, shook hands with him, and patted him on the arm.", "고모님은 한마디 말도 없이 정겨운 낯빛으로 다가와 그와 악수를 나누고 그의 팔을 토닥였다."], ["It was so expressively done, that she had no need to say a word. Mr.", "어찌나 뜻이 또렷이 담겼던지, 굳이 입을 열 필요조차 없었다."], ["Peggotty understood her quite as well as if she had said a thousand.", "패거티 씨는 고모님이 천 마디를 한 것이나 다름없이 그 마음을 똑똑히 헤아렸다."]], [["‘I’ll go in now, Trot,’ said my aunt, ‘and look after Little Blossom, who will be getting up presently.’", "\"난 이제 안으로 들어가 봐야겠다, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"곧 일어날 테니 우리 어린 꽃송이를 보살펴야지.\""]], [["‘Not along of my being heer, ma’am, I hope?’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"제가 여기 와 있다고 그러시는 건 아니지요, 마님?\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Unless my wits is gone a bahd’s neezing’--by which Mr.", "\"제 정신이 오늘 아침 새둥지 뒤지러 나간 게 아니라면\""], ["Peggotty meant to say, bird’s-nesting--‘this morning, ‘tis along of me as you’re a-going to quit us?’", "— 패거티 씨가 하려던 말은 새둥지 뒤지기, 곧 정신이 딴 데 팔렸다는 뜻이었다 — \"혹시 저 때문에 자리를 비키시려는 거 아닙니까?\""]], [["‘You have something to say, my good friend,’ returned my aunt, ‘and will do better without me.’", "\"자네가 할 얘기가 있을 텐데, 나의 좋은 벗이여.\" 고모님이 받았다. \"내가 없는 편이 더 편할 거야.\""]], [["‘By your leave, ma’am,’ returned Mr.", "\"실례지만, 마님.\" 패거티 씨가 대답했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘I should take it kind, pervising you doen’t mind my clicketten, if you’d bide heer.’", "\"제 수다를 마다하지 않으신다면, 여기 그냥 계셔 주시는 게 저는 고맙겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Would you?’ said my aunt, with short good-nature.", "\"그래?\" 고모님이 짤막하게 너그러운 투로 말했다."], ["‘Then I am sure I will!’", "\"그렇다면야 당연히 있어야지!\""]], [["So, she drew her arm through Mr. Peggotty’s, and walked with him to a leafy little summer-house there was at the bottom of the garden, where she sat down on a bench, and I beside her.", "그래서 고모님은 패거티 씨의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고, 정원 끝에 있는 잎이 우거진 작은 정자로 함께 걸어갔다. 거기서 고모님은 긴 의자에 앉았고, 나는 그 곁에 앉았다."], ["There was a seat for Mr. Peggotty too, but he preferred to stand, leaning his hand on the small rustic table.", "패거티 씨를 위한 자리도 있었지만 그는 작은 시골풍 탁자에 손을 짚고 서 있기를 택했다."], ["As he stood, looking at his cap for a little while before beginning to speak, I could not help observing what power and force of character his sinewy hand expressed, and what a good and trusty companion it was to his honest brow and iron-grey hair.", "그가 입을 떼기 전에 잠시 자기 모자를 내려다보며 서 있는 동안, 나는 그의 억센 손이 얼마나 강한 힘과 굳센 성품을 드러내는지, 또 그 손이 정직한 이마와 쇠처럼 희끗한 머리칼에 얼마나 미덥고 든든한 동무인지를 새삼 눈여겨보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘I took my dear child away last night,’ Mr.", "\"어젯밤에 제가 그 가엾은 자식을 데려왔습니다.\""], ["Peggotty began, as he raised his eyes to ours, ‘to my lodging, wheer I have a long time been expecting of her and preparing fur her.", "패거티 씨가 우리에게 눈을 들며 이야기를 시작했다. \"오랫동안 그 애를 기다리고 그 애를 위해 채비를 해 둔 제 거처로요."], ["It was hours afore she knowed me right; and when she did, she kneeled down at my feet, and kiender said to me, as if it was her prayers, how it all come to be.", "그 애가 저를 제대로 알아보기까지 몇 시간이나 걸렸습니다. 그러다 알아보고는, 제 발치에 무릎을 꿇고서, 기도라도 올리듯이, 어쩌다 일이 이 지경까지 왔는지를 저한테 들려주었지요."], ["You may believe me, when I heerd her voice, as I had heerd at home so playful--and see her humbled, as it might be in the dust our Saviour wrote in with his blessed hand--I felt a wownd go to my ‘art, in the midst of all its thankfulness.’", "믿어 주십시오, 집에서 그토록 명랑하게 듣던 그 애 목소리를 다시 듣고 — 또 우리 구주께서 복된 손으로 글을 쓰시던 그 흙먼지 속에 엎드리듯 비참하게 무릎 꿇은 그 애를 보고 — 저는 온통 감사한 마음 한가운데서도 가슴을 찌르는 아픔을 느꼈습니다.\""]], [["He drew his sleeve across his face, without any pretence of concealing why; and then cleared his voice.", "그는 왜 그러는지 숨기려는 기색도 없이 소맷자락으로 얼굴을 쓱 훔치고는 목청을 가다듬었다."]], [["‘It warn’t for long as I felt that; for she was found.", "\"하지만 그런 마음이 오래가지는 않았습니다. 그 애를 되찾았으니까요."], ["I had on’y to think as she was found, and it was gone.", "그저 그 애를 되찾았다는 생각만 하면 그 아픔은 사라졌지요."], ["I doen’t know why I do so much as mention of it now, I’m sure.", "사실 제가 지금 왜 이런 얘기까지 꺼내는지 모르겠습니다."], ["I didn’t have it in my mind a minute ago, to say a word about myself; but it come up so nat’ral, that I yielded to it afore I was aweer.’", "조금 전까지만 해도 제 얘기를 할 마음은 없었거든요. 그런데 너무 자연스레 솟구쳐서, 저도 모르게 그만 거기에 끌려가 버렸네요.\""]], [["‘You are a self-denying soul,’ said my aunt, ‘and will have your reward.’", "\"자넨 자기를 비울 줄 아는 사람이야.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그러니 마땅한 보답을 받을 게야.\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty, with the shadows of the leaves playing athwart his face, made a surprised inclination of the head towards my aunt, as an acknowledgement of her good opinion; then took up the thread he had relinquished.", "패거티 씨는 나뭇잎 그림자가 얼굴을 가로질러 어른거리는 가운데, 고모님의 좋은 평가에 화답하듯 놀란 듯이 고개를 한 번 숙여 보이더니, 놓았던 이야기의 실마리를 다시 잡았다."]], [["‘When my Em’ly took flight,’ he said, in stern wrath for the moment, ‘from the house wheer she was made a prisoner by that theer spotted snake as Mas’r Davy see,--and his story’s trew, and may GOD confound him!--she took flight in the night.", "\"제 에밀리가 도망쳐 나온 건,\" 그가 잠깐 매서운 분노에 사로잡혀 말했다. \"데이비 도련님이 본 그 얼룩무늬 뱀 같은 놈한테 갇혀 있던 그 집에서였습니다 — 그 도련님 얘기는 죄다 사실이고, 부디 하느님이 그놈을 벌하시기를!"], ["It was a dark night, with a many stars a-shining. She was wild.", "— 그 애는 밤에 도망쳤습니다. 별이 잔뜩 빛나던 어두운 밤이었지요."], ["She ran along the sea beach, believing the old boat was theer; and calling out to us to turn away our faces, for she was a-coming by.", "그 애는 제정신이 아니었습니다. 옛날 배가 거기 있으리라 믿고 바닷가를 따라 내달리면서, 자기가 지나가니 우리더러 얼굴을 돌리라고 소리쳤다더군요."], ["She heerd herself a-crying out, like as if it was another person; and cut herself on them sharp-pinted stones and rocks, and felt it no more than if she had been rock herself.", "자기 울부짖는 소리가 마치 딴사람 목소리처럼 들렸다고요. 뾰족한 돌과 바위에 살갗을 베이면서도, 제 몸이 바위라도 된 양 아무것도 못 느꼈답니다."], ["Ever so fur she run, and there was fire afore her eyes, and roarings in her ears.", "그렇게 한참을 달리는데 눈앞엔 불길이 일렁이고 귓속엔 굉음이 울렸다지요."], ["Of a sudden--or so she thowt, you unnerstand--the day broke, wet and windy, and she was lying b’low a heap of stone upon the shore, and a woman was a-speaking to her, saying, in the language of that country, what was it as had gone so much amiss?’", "그러다 갑자기 — 그 애 느낌으로는 그랬다는 겁니다, 아시겠지요 — 비 내리고 바람 부는 날이 밝았고, 보니 자기는 바닷가 돌무더기 밑에 누워 있고, 웬 여인네가 그 고장 말로 자기한테 무슨 일이 그리 크게 잘못된 거냐고 묻고 있더랍니다.\""]], [["He saw everything he related. It passed before him, as he spoke, so vividly, that, in the intensity of his earnestness, he presented what he described to me, with greater distinctness than I can express.", "그는 자기가 이야기하는 모든 장면을 눈으로 보고 있었다. 말하는 동안 그것이 그의 앞을 어찌나 생생하게 스쳐 지나갔던지, 그 절절한 진정 속에서 그가 묘사하는 광경이 내가 글로 표현할 수 있는 것보다 더 또렷하게 내게 와닿았다."], ["I can hardly believe, writing now long afterwards, but that I was actually present in these scenes; they are impressed upon me with such an astonishing air of fidelity.", "한참 세월이 흐른 지금 이렇게 글을 쓰면서도, 그때 내가 정말로 그 장면 속에 함께 있었던 게 아닌가 하는 생각을 떨치기 어렵다. 그만큼 그 광경들은 놀라우리만치 생생하게 내게 새겨져 있다."]], [["‘As Em’ly’s eyes--which was heavy--see this woman better,’ Mr.", "\"무거워 감기던 에밀리의 두 눈이 그 여인네를 좀 더 또렷이 보게 되자,\""], ["Peggotty went on, ‘she know’d as she was one of them as she had often talked to on the beach.", "패거티 씨가 이어 갔다. \"그 애는 자기가 바닷가에서 자주 말을 섞던 사람들 가운데 하나라는 걸 알아봤습니다."], ["Fur, though she had run (as I have said) ever so fur in the night, she had oftentimes wandered long ways, partly afoot, partly in boats and carriages, and know’d all that country, ‘long the coast, miles and miles.", "아까도 말씀드렸듯이 그 애가 그 밤에 한참을 달리긴 했지만, 그 전에도 여러 차례 멀리까지 헤매고 다녔거든요. 어떤 때는 걸어서, 어떤 때는 배나 마차로요. 그래서 해안을 따라 수십 리에 걸쳐 그 고장 일대를 다 꿰고 있었답니다."], ["She hadn’t no children of her own, this woman, being a young wife; but she was a-looking to have one afore long.", "이 여인네는 젊은 새댁이라 제 자식은 아직 없었지만, 머지않아 하나 낳을 참이었다지요."], ["And may my prayers go up to Heaven that ‘twill be a happiness to her, and a comfort, and a honour, all her life!", "부디 그 아이가 평생토록 그 여인에게 기쁨이 되고, 위안이 되고, 자랑이 되기를 하늘에 비나이다!"], ["May it love her and be dootiful to her, in her old age; helpful of her at the last; a Angel to her heer, and heerafter!’", "그 여인이 늙었을 때 자식이 어미를 사랑하고 효성스럽게 모시며, 마지막 가는 길까지 보살펴 드리기를. 이승에서도 저승에서도 그 여인에게 천사가 되어 주기를!\""]], [["‘Amen!’ said my aunt.", "\"아멘!\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘She had been summat timorous and down,’ said Mr.", "\"그 여인네는 좀 수줍고 풀 죽은 성미라서,\""], ["Peggotty, ‘and had sat, at first, a little way off, at her spinning, or such work as it was, when Em’ly talked to the children.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"처음에 에밀리가 아이들하고 얘기를 나눌 때면, 좀 떨어진 데 앉아 물레질이며 무슨 일이며를 하고 있었답니다."], ["But Em’ly had took notice of her, and had gone and spoke to her; and as the young woman was partial to the children herself, they had soon made friends.", "그런데 에밀리가 그 여인네를 눈여겨보고는 다가가 말을 걸었지요. 그 젊은 여인네도 아이들을 무척 좋아하던 터라, 둘은 금세 친해졌습니다."], ["Sermuchser, that when Em’ly went that way, she always giv Em’ly flowers.", "어찌나 친해졌는지, 에밀리가 그 길로 지날 때면 그 여인네는 늘 에밀리한테 꽃을 주었답니다."], ["This was her as now asked what it was that had gone so much amiss.", "무슨 일이 그리 크게 잘못된 거냐고 이제 물어 온 사람이 바로 이 여인네였습니다."], ["Em’ly told her, and she--took her home. She did indeed.", "에밀리가 사정을 말하자, 그 여인네는 — 그 애를 집으로 데려갔습니다. 정말로요."], ["She took her home,’ said Mr.", "집으로 데려갔어요.\""], ["Peggotty, covering his face.", "패거티 씨가 얼굴을 감싸며 말했다."]], [["He was more affected by this act of kindness, than I had ever seen him affected by anything since the night she went away.", "그는 에밀리가 떠나던 그 밤 이후로 내가 보아 온 어떤 일보다도 이 친절한 행동에 더 깊이 마음이 흔들린 듯했다."], ["My aunt and I did not attempt to disturb him.", "고모님과 나는 그를 방해하려 들지 않았다."]], [["‘It was a little cottage, you may suppose,’ he said, presently, ‘but she found space for Em’ly in it,--her husband was away at sea,--and she kep it secret, and prevailed upon such neighbours as she had (they was not many near) to keep it secret too.", "\"짐작하시겠지만 그건 자그마한 오두막이었지요.\" 그가 이윽고 말했다. \"그래도 그 여인네는 그 안에 에밀리가 머물 자리를 마련해 줬습니다 — 남편은 바다에 나가 있었거든요 — 그리고 그 일을 비밀로 했고, 가까이 사는 이웃이라야 몇 안 됐지만 그 사람들한테도 입조심을 시켰지요."], ["Em’ly was took bad with fever, and, what is very strange to me is,--maybe ‘tis not so strange to scholars,--the language of that country went out of her head, and she could only speak her own, that no one unnerstood.", "에밀리는 열병이 들어 몹시 앓았는데, 저로서는 참 이상한 게 — 학식 있는 분들한테야 그리 이상한 일이 아닐는지도 모르지만 — 그 고장 말이 머릿속에서 싹 사라져 버리고, 제 고향 말밖에 못 하게 됐다는 겁니다."], ["She recollects, as if she had dreamed it, that she lay there always a-talking her own tongue, always believing as the old boat was round the next pint in the bay, and begging and imploring of ‘em to send theer and tell how she was dying, and bring back a message of forgiveness, if it was on’y a wured.", "아무도 못 알아듣는 말이었지요. 그 애는 꿈결처럼 어렴풋이 기억한답니다. 거기 누워 줄곧 제 고향 말로 떠들면서, 옛날 배가 만의 바로 다음 곶 근처에 있으리라 믿고, 사람을 보내 자기가 죽어 간다고 전해 달라고, 단 한마디라도 좋으니 용서한다는 말을 받아다 달라고 빌고 애원했다고요."], ["A’most the whole time, she thowt,--now, that him as I made mention on just now was lurking for her unnerneath the winder; now that him as had brought her to this was in the room,--and cried to the good young woman not to give her up, and know’d, at the same time, that she couldn’t unnerstand, and dreaded that she must be took away.", "거의 내내, 그 애 생각으로는 — 어떤 때는 제가 방금 말씀드린 그놈이 창문 밑에 숨어 자기를 노린다 싶고, 어떤 때는 자기를 이 지경으로 만든 놈이 방 안에 있다 싶었답니다. 그래서 그 착한 젊은 여인네한테 자기를 내치지 말라고 울며 매달리면서도, 동시에 그 여인이 알아듣지 못한다는 걸 알았고, 자기가 끌려가게 될까 봐 두려워했다지요."], ["Likewise the fire was afore her eyes, and the roarings in her ears; and theer was no today, nor yesterday, nor yet tomorrow; but everything in her life as ever had been, or as ever could be, and everything as never had been, and as never could be, was a crowding on her all at once, and nothing clear nor welcome, and yet she sang and laughed about it!", "눈앞엔 여전히 불길이 일렁이고 귓속엔 굉음이 울렸으며, 오늘도 어제도 내일도 없이, 살면서 있었던 일, 있을 수 있었던 일, 한 번도 없었던 일, 결코 있을 수 없었던 일이 죄다 한꺼번에 들이닥쳐서, 또렷한 것도 반가운 것도 하나 없었답니다. 그런데도 그 애는 그걸 두고 노래하고 웃었다는 거예요!"], ["How long this lasted, I doen’t know; but then theer come a sleep; and in that sleep, from being a many times stronger than her own self, she fell into the weakness of the littlest child.’", "이게 얼마나 오래갔는지는 모르겠습니다. 그러다 잠이 찾아왔고, 그 잠 속에서, 제 본래 모습보다 몇 곱절 억셌던 그 애가 가장 어린 갓난아이만큼이나 가녀린 상태로 떨어졌답니다.\""]], [["Here he stopped, as if for relief from the terrors of his own description.", "여기서 그는 자기가 들려준 이야기의 두려움에서 한숨 돌리려는 듯 말을 멈추었다."], ["After being silent for a few moments, he pursued his story.", "잠깐 입을 다물고 있다가, 그는 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["‘It was a pleasant arternoon when she awoke; and so quiet, that there warn’t a sound but the rippling of that blue sea without a tide, upon the shore.", "\"그 애가 깨어났을 때는 화창한 오후였답니다. 어찌나 고요한지, 밀물도 썰물도 없는 그 푸른 바다가 기슭에 찰랑이는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었다지요."], ["It was her belief, at first, that she was at home upon a Sunday morning; but the vine leaves as she see at the winder, and the hills beyond, warn’t home, and contradicted of her.", "처음엔 자기가 집에서 어느 일요일 아침을 맞은 줄 알았답니다. 그런데 창가에 보이는 포도 덩굴잎이며 그 너머 언덕들이 집이 아니어서, 그게 아니라고 일러 주더래요. 그러다 그 벗이 들어와 침대 곁을 지켜 주었고, 그제야 그 애는 옛날 배가 만의 다음 곶 근처에 있는 게 아니라 멀리 떨어져 있다는 걸 깨달았답니다."], ["Then, come in her friend to watch alongside of her bed; and then she know’d as the old boat warn’t round that next pint in the bay no more, but was fur off; and know’d where she was, and why; and broke out a-crying on that good young woman’s bosom, wheer I hope her baby is a-lying now, a-cheering of her with its pretty eyes!’", "자기가 어디 있는지, 왜 거기 있는지도요. 그러고는 그 착한 젊은 여인네 품에 안겨 울음을 터뜨렸지요. 부디 지금쯤 그 여인의 아기가 그 품에 누워, 예쁜 두 눈으로 어미를 기쁘게 해 주고 있기를!\""]], [["He could not speak of this good friend of Emily’s without a flow of tears.", "그는 눈물을 쏟지 않고는 에밀리의 이 착한 벗을 입에 올릴 수가 없었다."], ["It was in vain to try.", "참아 보려 했지만 헛수고였다."], ["He broke down again, endeavouring to bless her!", "그 여인을 축복하려 애쓰다가 그는 또다시 무너지고 말았다."]], [["‘That done my Em’ly good,’ he resumed, after such emotion as I could not behold without sharing in; and as to my aunt, she wept with all her heart; ‘that done Em’ly good, and she begun to mend.", "\"그 덕분에 우리 에밀리가 나아졌습니다.\" 나로서는 함께 마음 아파하지 않고는 차마 볼 수 없는 격정에 잠겼다가 그가 다시 입을 열었다. 고모님으로 말하자면, 진심을 다해 울고 있었다."], ["But, the language of that country was quite gone from her, and she was forced to make signs.", "\"그 덕에 에밀리가 나아졌고, 차차 회복되기 시작했습니다."], ["So she went on, getting better from day to day, slow, but sure, and trying to learn the names of common things--names as she seemed never to have heerd in all her life--till one evening come, when she was a-setting at her window, looking at a little girl at play upon the beach.", "하지만 그 고장 말은 머릿속에서 깡그리 사라져 버려서, 그 애는 손짓으로 뜻을 전할 수밖에 없었지요. 그렇게 날이 갈수록 더디지만 분명하게 좋아져 가면서, 흔한 물건들의 이름을 — 난생처음 들어 보는 듯한 이름들을 — 익히려고 애썼답니다. 그러던 어느 저녁, 그 애가 창가에 앉아 바닷가에서 노는 어린 계집아이를 바라보고 있었지요."], ["And of a sudden this child held out her hand, and said, what would be in English, “Fisherman’s daughter, here’s a shell!”--for you are to unnerstand that they used at first to call her “Pretty lady”, as the general way in that country is, and that she had taught ‘em to call her “Fisherman’s daughter”", "그런데 그 아이가 별안간 손을 내밀며, 영어로 옮기면 이런 말을 했답니다. '어부의 딸님, 여기 조개 있어요!' — 아셔야 할 게, 거기서는 처음에 그 고장 풍습대로 에밀리를 '예쁜 아씨'라고 불렀는데, 그 애가 대신 '어부의 딸'이라 불러 달라고 가르쳐 줬거든요."], ["instead. The child says of a sudden, “Fisherman’s daughter, here’s a shell!”", "그 아이가 느닷없이 '어부의 딸님, 여기 조개 있어요!'"], ["Then Em’ly unnerstands her; and she answers, bursting out a-crying; and it all comes back!", "하니까, 그제야 에밀리가 그 말을 알아들었고, 울음을 터뜨리며 대꾸했답니다. 그러자 모든 게 한꺼번에 되살아났다는 거예요!\""]], [["‘When Em’ly got strong again,’ said Mr.", "\"에밀리가 다시 기운을 차린 뒤,\""], ["Peggotty, after another short interval of silence, ‘she cast about to leave that good young creetur, and get to her own country.", "또 한 차례 짧은 침묵 끝에 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"그 애는 그 착한 젊은이를 떠나 제 나라로 돌아갈 궁리를 했습니다."], ["The husband was come home, then; and the two together put her aboard a small trader bound to Leghorn, and from that to France.", "그때쯤 남편이 집에 돌아와 있었지요. 부부가 함께 그 애를 레그혼으로 가는 작은 무역선에 태워 줬고, 거기서 다시 프랑스로 건너가게 했답니다."], ["She had a little money, but it was less than little as they would take for all they done.", "그 애한테 돈이 좀 있긴 했지만, 그 사람들이 베푼 것에 견주면 그들이 받은 건 쥐꼬리만큼도 못 됐지요."], ["I’m a’most glad on it, though they was so poor! What they done, is laid up wheer neither moth or rust doth corrupt, and wheer thieves do not break through nor steal.", "그 사람들이 그토록 가난한데도, 저는 차라리 그게 다행이다 싶습니다! 그들이 베푼 것은 좀먹지도 녹슬지도 않고, 도둑이 뚫고 들어와 훔쳐 가지도 못하는 곳에 고이 쌓여 있을 테니까요."], ["Mas’r Davy, it’ll outlast all the treasure in the wureld.", "데이비 도련님, 그건 이 세상 어떤 보물보다 오래갈 겁니다."]], [["‘Em’ly got to France, and took service to wait on travelling ladies at a inn in the port.", "에밀리는 프랑스에 닿았고, 항구의 어느 여관에서 여행하는 부인들의 시중을 드는 일자리를 얻었습니다."], ["Theer, theer come, one day, that snake. --Let him never come nigh me.", "그런데 거기로 어느 날 그 뱀 같은 놈이 찾아왔지요 — 그놈이 절대 제 곁에 얼씬도 하지 않기를."], ["I doen’t know what hurt I might do him!--Soon as she see him, without him seeing her, all her fear and wildness returned upon her, and she fled afore the very breath he draw’d.", "안 그러면 제가 무슨 짓을 저지를지 저도 모릅니다! — 그놈이 자기를 보기도 전에 그놈을 본 순간, 그 애는 온갖 두려움과 광기가 되살아나서, 그놈이 내쉬는 숨결 앞에서 도망쳐 버렸습니다."], ["She come to England, and was set ashore at Dover.", "그렇게 영국으로 건너와 도버에 내렸지요."]], [["‘I doen’t know,’ said Mr.", "언제부터 그 애 가슴이 무너지기 시작했는지는,\""], ["Peggotty, ‘for sure, when her ‘art begun to fail her; but all the way to England she had thowt to come to her dear home.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"저도 똑똑히는 모르겠습니다. 다만 영국으로 오는 내내 그 애는 그리운 제 집으로 돌아올 작정이었답니다."], ["Soon as she got to England she turned her face tow’rds it.", "영국에 닿자마자 집 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸지요."], ["But, fear of not being forgiv, fear of being pinted at, fear of some of us being dead along of her, fear of many things, turned her from it, kiender by force, upon the road: “Uncle, uncle,”", "그런데 용서받지 못할까 봐 두렵고, 손가락질당할까 봐 두렵고, 우리 중 누군가가 자기 때문에 죽었을까 봐 두렵고, 그 밖에 온갖 게 두려워서, 거의 떠밀리다시피 가던 길에서 발길을 돌리고 말았답니다. '아저씨, 아저씨'"], ["she says to me, “the fear of not being worthy to do what my torn and bleeding breast so longed to do, was the most fright’ning fear of all!", "하고 그 애가 저한테 그러더군요. '찢기고 피 흘리는 제 가슴이 그토록 간절히 하고 싶어 하던 일을 할 자격이 없을까 봐, 그게 무엇보다 무서운 두려움이었어요!"], ["I turned back, when my ‘art was full of prayers that I might crawl to the old door-step, in the night, kiss it, lay my wicked face upon it, and theer be found dead in the morning.”", "밤중에 그 옛날 문턱까지 기어가서 거기에 입 맞추고, 죄 많은 제 얼굴을 거기 묻고, 아침이면 거기서 죽은 채 발견되게 해 달라고 기도로 가슴이 미어지던 바로 그때, 저는 발길을 돌리고 말았어요.'"]], [["‘She come,’ said Mr.", "그 애는,\""], ["Peggotty, dropping his voice to an awe-stricken whisper, ‘to London.", "패거티 씨가 두려움에 사로잡힌 속삭임으로 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"런던으로 갔습니다."], ["She--as had never seen it in her life--alone--without a penny--young--so pretty--come to London.", "그 애가 — 평생 한 번도 본 적 없는 그곳으로 — 혼자서 — 한 푼도 없이 — 어리고 — 그토록 예쁜 채로 — 런던으로 갔습니다."], ["A’most the moment as she lighted heer, all so desolate, she found (as she believed) a friend; a decent woman as spoke to her about the needle-work as she had been brought up to do, about finding plenty of it fur her, about a lodging fur the night, and making secret inquiration concerning of me and all at home, tomorrow.", "그 막막한 곳에 발을 디딘 거의 그 순간, 그 애는 (자기 딴엔) 벗을 하나 만났다고 믿었지요. 점잖아 보이는 여자가 다가와, 그 애가 어려서부터 익힌 바느질 얘기며, 그 일감을 넉넉히 구해 주겠다는 얘기며, 하룻밤 묵을 곳 얘기며, 이튿날 저와 집안 식구들에 대해 몰래 알아봐 주겠다는 얘기를 했답니다. 제 자식이,\""], ["When my child,’ he said aloud, and with an energy of gratitude that shook him from head to foot, ‘stood upon the brink of more than I can say or think on--Martha, trew to her promise, saved her.’", "그가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨릴 만큼 벅찬 감사를 담아 소리 높여 말했다. \"제가 말로도 생각으로도 다 담을 수 없는 그 무엇의 벼랑 끝에 서 있을 때 — 마사가, 제 약속을 지킨 마사가, 그 애를 구해 냈습니다.\""]], [["I could not repress a cry of joy.", "나는 기쁨의 외침을 참을 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy!’ said he, gripping my hand in that strong hand of his, ‘it was you as first made mention of her to me.", "\"데이비 도련님!\" 그가 그 억센 손으로 내 손을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"마사 얘기를 저한테 처음 꺼내 주신 게 도련님이었지요."], ["I thankee, sir!", "고맙습니다, 도련님!"], ["She was arnest.", "마사는 진심이었습니다."], ["She had know’d of her bitter knowledge wheer to watch and what to do.", "자기가 쓰라리게 겪어 알게 된 덕에, 어디를 지켜봐야 하고 무얼 해야 하는지를 알고 있었지요."], ["She had done it. And the Lord was above all!", "그리고 그대로 해냈습니다. 그 위에 주님이 계셨고요!"], ["She come, white and hurried, upon Em’ly in her sleep.", "마사는 핏기 없이 황급한 얼굴로, 잠든 에밀리에게 다가갔답니다."], ["She says to her, “Rise up from worse than death, and come with me!”", "그러고는 '죽음보다 더한 데서 일어나, 나랑 같이 가자!' 하고 말했지요."], ["Them belonging to the house would have stopped her, but they might as soon have stopped the sea.", "그 집 사람들이 그 애를 막으려 들었지만, 차라리 바다를 막는 게 빨랐을 겁니다. '내게서 물러서.'"], ["“Stand away from me,”", "마사가 말했답니다."], ["she says, “I am a ghost that calls her from beside her open grave!” She told Em’ly she had seen me, and know’d I loved her, and forgive her.", "'나는 파헤쳐진 무덤 곁에서 이 애를 불러내는 유령이야!' 마사는 에밀리한테 제가 그 애를 봤다고, 제가 그 애를 사랑하고 용서한다는 걸 안다고 말해 줬습니다."], ["She wrapped her, hasty, in her clothes. She took her, faint and trembling, on her arm.", "그러고는 서둘러 그 애한테 옷을 둘러 입히고, 정신을 잃고 떨고 있는 그 애를 제 팔에 안아 들었지요."], ["She heeded no more what they said, than if she had had no ears.", "그 사람들이 뭐라고 하든, 귀가 없는 사람처럼 아랑곳하지 않았답니다."], ["She walked among ‘em with my child, minding only her; and brought her safe out, in the dead of the night, from that black pit of ruin!", "오직 제 자식만 살피며 그 사람들 틈을 걸어 나와, 그 캄캄한 파멸의 구덩이에서 깊은 밤중에 그 애를 무사히 끌어냈습니다!"]], [["‘She attended on Em’ly,’ said Mr.", "마사는 에밀리를 보살폈습니다.\""], ["Peggotty, who had released my hand, and put his own hand on his heaving chest; ‘she attended to my Em’ly, lying wearied out, and wandering betwixt whiles, till late next day.", "내 손을 놓고 자기 손을 들썩이는 가슴에 얹으며 패거티 씨가 말했다. \"지쳐 쓰러져, 이따금 헛소리를 하며 누워 있는 우리 에밀리를 이튿날 늦게까지 곁에서 돌봐 줬지요."], ["Then she went in search of me; then in search of you, Mas’r Davy.", "그런 다음 저를 찾아 나섰고, 또 도련님을 찾아 나섰습니다."], ["She didn’t tell Em’ly what she come out fur, lest her ‘art should fail, and she should think of hiding of herself.", "마사는 자기가 왜 나왔는지를 에밀리한테 말하지 않았답니다. 그 애 가슴이 또 무너져서 어디 숨어 버릴 생각을 할까 봐서요."], ["How the cruel lady know’d of her being theer, I can’t say.", "그 모진 여자가 어떻게 그 애가 거기 있는 걸 알았는지는 저도 모릅니다."], ["Whether him as I have spoke so much of, chanced to see ‘em going theer, or whether (which is most like, to my thinking) he had heerd it from the woman, I doen’t greatly ask myself. My niece is found.", "제가 그토록 말씀드린 그놈이 그들이 거기 들어가는 걸 우연히 본 건지, 아니면 (제 생각엔 이쪽이 더 그럴 법한데) 그 여자한테서 들은 건지, 저는 그리 따져 묻지 않습니다. 제 조카는 되찾았으니까요."]], [["‘All night long,’ said Mr.", "밤새도록,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘we have been together, Em’ly and me.", "\"에밀리하고 저는 함께 있었습니다."], ["‘Tis little (considering the time) as she has said, in wureds, through them broken-hearted tears; ‘tis less as I have seen of her dear face, as grow’d into a woman’s at my hearth.", "(그 시간을 생각하면) 가슴 찢어지는 눈물 사이로 그 애가 말로 한 건 얼마 안 됩니다. 제 화롯가에서 어엿한 여인으로 자라난 그 애의 그리운 얼굴도 그만큼밖에 못 봤지요."], ["But, all night long, her arms has been about my neck; and her head has laid heer; and we knows full well, as we can put our trust in one another, ever more.’", "하지만 밤새도록 그 애 두 팔이 제 목을 감싸고, 그 애 머리가 여기 기대 있었습니다. 그리고 우리는 이제 영영 서로를 믿고 의지할 수 있다는 걸 똑똑히 압니다.\""]], [["He ceased to speak, and his hand upon the table rested there in perfect repose, with a resolution in it that might have conquered lions.", "그는 말을 멈추었고, 탁자에 얹은 그의 손은 사자라도 굴복시킬 만한 결의를 담은 채 완벽한 고요 속에 놓여 있었다."]], [["‘It was a gleam of light upon me, Trot,’ said my aunt, drying her eyes, ‘when I formed the resolution of being godmother to your sister Betsey Trotwood, who disappointed me; but, next to that, hardly anything would have given me greater pleasure, than to be godmother to that good young creature’s baby!’", "\"나에게 한 줄기 빛이 비쳤더랬지, 트롯.\" 고모님이 눈물을 닦으며 말했다. \"내가 네 누이동생 베시 트롯우드의 대모가 되겠다고 마음먹었을 때 말이다. 그 애는 끝내 나를 실망시켰지만. 그런데 그다음으로는, 저 착한 젊은이의 아기의 대모가 되는 일만큼 나를 기쁘게 할 일이 거의 없겠구나!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty nodded his understanding of my aunt’s feelings, but could not trust himself with any verbal reference to the subject of her commendation.", "패거티 씨는 고모님의 마음을 알아들었다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였지만, 고모님이 칭찬한 그 여인에 대해서는 차마 말로 한마디도 꺼낼 자신이 없었다."], ["We all remained silent, and occupied with our own reflections (my aunt drying her eyes, and now sobbing convulsively, and now laughing and calling herself a fool); until I spoke.", "우리는 모두 입을 다문 채 저마다 제 상념에 잠겨 있었다 (고모님은 눈물을 닦다가, 발작하듯 흐느끼다가, 또 웃음을 터뜨리며 자기를 바보라 부르고 있었다). 그러다 내가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘You have quite made up your mind,’ said I to Mr. Peggotty, ‘as to the future, good friend?", "\"앞날에 대해서는 마음을 완전히 정하셨겠지요, 나의 좋은 벗이여?\" 내가 패거티 씨에게 말했다."], ["I need scarcely ask you.’", "\"굳이 여쭤볼 것도 없겠지만요.\""]], [["‘Quite, Mas’r Davy,’ he returned; ‘and told Em’ly.", "\"완전히 정했습니다, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"에밀리한테도 말해 뒀고요."], ["Theer’s mighty countries, fur from heer.", "여기서 멀리 떨어진, 드넓은 나라들이 있지 않습니까."], ["Our future life lays over the sea.’", "우리 앞날은 저 바다 건너에 있습니다.\""]], [["‘They will emigrate together, aunt,’ said I.", "\"두 사람이 함께 이민을 가려나 봐요, 고모님.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes!’ said Mr.", "\"그렇습니다!\""], ["Peggotty, with a hopeful smile.", "패거티 씨가 희망 어린 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["‘No one can’t reproach my darling in Australia.", "\"오스트레일리아에서는 아무도 제 귀한 자식을 손가락질하지 못합니다."], ["We will begin a new life over theer!’", "우린 거기서 새 삶을 시작할 겁니다!\""]], [["I asked him if he yet proposed to himself any time for going away.", "나는 떠날 시기를 어느 정도 정해 두었느냐고 그에게 물었다."]], [["‘I was down at the Docks early this morning, sir,’ he returned, ‘to get information concerning of them ships.", "\"오늘 아침 일찍 부두에 나가 봤습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다. \"배편을 알아보려고요."], ["In about six weeks or two months from now, there’ll be one sailing--I see her this morning--went aboard--and we shall take our passage in her.’", "지금부터 한 여섯 주, 아니면 두 달쯤 뒤에 떠나는 배가 한 척 있더군요 — 오늘 아침에 그 배를 봤습니다 — 올라가 봤고요 — 우린 그 배로 떠날 작정입니다.\""]], [["‘Quite alone?’ I asked.", "\"단둘이서요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Aye, Mas’r Davy!’ he returned.", "\"네, 데이비 도련님!\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘My sister, you see, she’s that fond of you and yourn, and that accustomed to think on’y of her own country, that it wouldn’t be hardly fair to let her go.", "\"보시다시피 제 누이는 도련님하고 도련님 식구들을 그토록 끔찍이 아끼는 데다, 제 나라 생각밖에 할 줄 모르는 사람이라, 데려간다는 게 도무지 도리가 아니지요."], ["Besides which, theer’s one she has in charge, Mas’r Davy, as doen’t ought to be forgot.’", "게다가 누이가 맡아 돌봐야 할 사람이 하나 있지 않습니까, 데이비 도련님. 그 사람을 잊어선 안 되지요.\""]], [["‘Poor Ham!’ said I.", "\"가엾은 햄!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘My good sister takes care of his house, you see, ma’am, and he takes kindly to her,’ Mr.", "\"보시다시피 제 착한 누이가 그 애 집을 돌봐 주는데, 그 애도 누이를 잘 따른답니다, 마님.\""], ["Peggotty explained for my aunt’s better information.", "고모님이 더 잘 알아들으시도록 패거티 씨가 설명했다."], ["‘He’ll set and talk to her, with a calm spirit, wen it’s like he couldn’t bring himself to open his lips to another.", "\"그 애가 다른 사람한테는 도무지 입을 떼지 못할 것 같을 때도, 누이한테는 차분한 마음으로 앉아서 이런저런 얘기를 한답니다."], ["Poor fellow!’ said Mr.", "가엾은 녀석!\""], ["Peggotty, shaking his head, ‘theer’s not so much left him, that he could spare the little as he has!’", "패거티 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그 애한테 남은 게 얼마 없으니, 그나마 가진 그 얼마를 내줄 형편도 못 되지요!\""]], [["‘And Mrs. Gummidge?’ said I.", "\"거미지 부인은요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, I’ve had a mort of consideration, I do tell you,’ returned Mr.", "\"음, 정말이지 거미지 부인 일을 두고 제가 어지간히 고민을 했습니다.\""], ["Peggotty, with a perplexed look which gradually cleared as he went on, ‘concerning of Missis Gummidge.", "패거티 씨가 곤혹스러운 낯빛으로 대답했는데, 그 낯빛은 이야기를 이어 갈수록 차츰 풀렸다."], ["You see, wen Missis Gummidge falls a-thinking of the old ‘un, she an’t what you may call good company.", "\"보시다시피, 거미지 부인이 그 영감 생각에 빠지면, 영 함께 있을 만한 사람이 못 됩니다."], ["Betwixt you and me, Mas’r Davy--and you, ma’am--wen Mrs. Gummidge takes to wimicking,’--our old country word for crying,--‘she’s liable to be considered to be, by them as didn’t know the old ‘un, peevish-like.", "우리끼리 얘긴데요, 데이비 도련님 — 그리고 마님 — 거미지 부인이 훌쩍훌쩍 울기 시작하면,\" — 운다는 뜻의 우리 고향 옛말이다 — \"그 영감을 모르는 사람들 눈에는 짜증 부리는 거로 보이기 십상이지요."], ["Now I DID know the old ‘un,’ said Mr.", "저야 그 영감을 알았고,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘and I know’d his merits, so I unnerstan’ her; but ‘tan’t entirely so, you see, with others--nat’rally can’t be!’", "\"그 영감의 좋은 점도 알았으니까 부인을 이해합니다만, 다른 사람들이야 다 그럴 수는 없지요 — 당연히 그럴 수가 없잖습니까!\""]], [["My aunt and I both acquiesced.", "고모님과 나는 둘 다 그렇겠다고 수긍했다."]], [["‘Wheerby,’ said Mr.", "\"그러니,\""], ["Peggotty, ‘my sister might--I doen’t say she would, but might--find Missis Gummidge give her a leetle trouble now-and-again.", "패거티 씨가 말했다. \"제 누이가 어쩌면 — 꼭 그러리란 건 아니지만, 어쩌면 — 거미지 부인 때문에 이따금 자잘한 골치를 앓을지도 모릅니다."], ["Theerfur ‘tan’t my intentions to moor Missis Gummidge ‘long with them, but to find a Beein’ fur her wheer she can fisherate for herself.’ (A Beein’ signifies, in that dialect, a home, and to fisherate is to provide.) ‘Fur which purpose,’ said Mr.", "그래서 거미지 부인을 그들과 한데 묶어 둘 생각은 없고, 부인 혼자서 제 살림을 꾸려 갈 만한 '보금자리'를 마련해 줄 작정입니다.\" ('보금자리'는 그 사투리로 집을 뜻하고, '살림을 꾸린다'는 건 생계를 댄다는 뜻이다.) \"그러자면,\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘I means to make her a ‘lowance afore I go, as’ll leave her pretty comfort’ble.", "\"제가 떠나기 전에 부인한테 생활비를 떼어 줘서, 그럭저럭 편히 지내게 해 줄 작정입니다."], ["She’s the faithfullest of creeturs.", "부인은 더없이 신실한 사람이지요."], ["‘Tan’t to be expected, of course, at her time of life, and being lone and lorn, as the good old Mawther is to be knocked about aboardship, and in the woods and wilds of a new and fur-away country.", "물론 그 나이에, 외롭고 의지가지없는 처지에, 그 착한 노인네더러 배 위에서 이리저리 부대끼고, 멀고 낯선 나라의 숲이며 황야를 헤매라고 할 수야 없지요."], ["So that’s what I’m a-going to do with her.’", "그래서 부인은 그렇게 할 작정입니다.\""]], [["He forgot nobody. He thought of everybody’s claims and strivings, but his own.", "그는 누구 하나 잊지 않았다. 그는 자기 자신을 빼고는 모두의 처지와 노고를 헤아렸다."]], [["‘Em’ly,’ he continued, ‘will keep along with me--poor child, she’s sore in need of peace and rest!--until such time as we goes upon our voyage.", "\"에밀리는,\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"저랑 함께 지낼 겁니다 — 가엾은 것, 평안과 안식이 절실히 필요하니까요!"], ["She’ll work at them clothes, as must be made; and I hope her troubles will begin to seem longer ago than they was, wen she finds herself once more by her rough but loving uncle.’", "— 우리가 항해를 떠날 때까지요. 그 애는 만들어야 할 그 옷들을 지을 테고요. 그러다 보면, 거칠지만 사랑 깊은 제 아저씨 곁에 다시 있게 되면서, 그 애의 괴로움이 실제보다 더 먼 옛일처럼 느껴지기 시작하기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["My aunt nodded confirmation of this hope, and imparted great satisfaction to Mr. Peggotty.", "고모님은 그 바람에 동감한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였고, 패거티 씨는 크게 흐뭇해했다."]], [["‘Theer’s one thing furder, Mas’r Davy,’ said he, putting his hand in his breast-pocket, and gravely taking out the little paper bundle I had seen before, which he unrolled on the table.", "\"한 가지 더 있습니다, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣어, 전에 본 적 있는 그 작은 종이 꾸러미를 점잖게 꺼내 탁자 위에 펼치며 말했다."], ["‘Theer’s these here banknotes--fifty pound, and ten.", "\"여기 이 지폐들 말입니다 — 오십 파운드하고 십 파운드요."], ["To them I wish to add the money as she come away with.", "여기에다 그 애가 떠날 때 가져갔던 돈을 보태고 싶습니다."], ["I’ve asked her about that (but not saying why), and have added of it up.", "그 애한테 그 일을 물어봐서 (왜 묻는지는 말하지 않고요) 합산해 뒀지요."], ["I an’t a scholar. Would you be so kind as see how ‘tis?’", "저야 배운 게 없으니, 죄송하지만 이게 맞는지 좀 봐 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["He handed me, apologetically for his scholarship, a piece of paper, and observed me while I looked it over.", "그는 자기 학식 없음을 사과하듯 종이 한 장을 내게 건네고는, 내가 그것을 살펴보는 동안 나를 지켜보았다."], ["It was quite right.", "셈은 정확했다."]], [["‘Thankee, sir,’ he said, taking it back.", "\"고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 그것을 도로 받으며 말했다."], ["‘This money, if you doen’t see objections, Mas’r Davy, I shall put up jest afore I go, in a cover directed to him; and put that up in another, directed to his mother.", "\"이 돈을, 도련님이 보시기에 별 탈이 없다면, 데이비 도련님, 제가 떠나기 바로 전에 그놈 앞으로 봉투에 넣어 두렵니다. 그리고 그걸 다시 그놈 어미 앞으로 된 다른 봉투에 넣어 두고요."], ["I shall tell her, in no more wureds than I speak to you, what it’s the price on; and that I’m gone, and past receiving of it back.’", "그 어미한테는, 지금 도련님께 말씀드리는 만큼만 짤막하게, 이게 무엇의 대가인지를 적어 두겠습니다. 그리고 저는 이미 떠나서, 돌려받을 수 없게 됐다고요.\""]], [["I told him that I thought it would be right to do so--that I was thoroughly convinced it would be, since he felt it to be right.", "나는 그렇게 하는 것이 옳을 것 같다고 — 그가 그것을 옳다고 느끼는 만큼, 옳으리라 확신한다고 말해 주었다."]], [["‘I said that theer was on’y one thing furder,’ he proceeded with a grave smile, when he had made up his little bundle again, and put it in his pocket; ‘but theer was two.", "\"한 가지 더 있다고 말씀드렸지요.\" 그가 작은 꾸러미를 다시 묶어 주머니에 넣고 나서, 점잖은 미소를 띠고 말을 이었다. \"그런데 사실 두 가지였습니다."], ["I warn’t sure in my mind, wen I come out this morning, as I could go and break to Ham, of my own self, what had so thankfully happened.", "오늘 아침 집을 나설 때만 해도, 그토록 감사한 일이 일어났다는 걸 제가 직접 햄한테 가서 전할 수 있을지 마음이 영 자신이 없었습니다."], ["So I writ a letter while I was out, and put it in the post-office, telling of ‘em how all was as ‘tis; and that I should come down tomorrow to unload my mind of what little needs a-doing of down theer, and, most-like, take my farewell leave of Yarmouth.’", "그래서 나가 있는 동안 편지를 한 통 써서 우체국에 넣어 뒀지요. 일이 다 이렇게 됐다고, 그리고 내일 거기로 내려가 처리해야 할 자잘한 일들을 마무리하고, 어쩌면 야머스에 작별 인사를 하게 될 거라고요.\""]], [["‘And do you wish me to go with you?’ said I, seeing that he left something unsaid.", "\"그럼 저더러 같이 가 달라는 말씀인가요?\" 그가 무언가 말하지 않은 게 있음을 알아채고 내가 말했다."]], [["‘If you could do me that kind favour, Mas’r Davy,’ he replied.", "\"그렇게 친절을 베풀어 주실 수 있다면요, 데이비 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘I know the sight on you would cheer ‘em up a bit.’", "\"도련님 얼굴을 보면 그 사람들 기운이 좀 날 것 같아서요.\""]], [["My little Dora being in good spirits, and very desirous that I should go--as I found on talking it over with her--I readily pledged myself to accompany him in accordance with his wish.", "내 어린 도라가 기분이 좋은 데다, 내가 다녀오기를 무척 바라는 것을 — 그 일을 함께 의논해 보고 알았는데 — 알았기에, 나는 그의 바람대로 기꺼이 동행하기로 약속했다."], ["Next morning, consequently, we were on the Yarmouth coach, and again travelling over the old ground.", "그리하여 이튿날 아침, 우리는 야머스행 역마차에 올라, 다시 그 옛 길을 따라 달려갔다."]], [["As we passed along the familiar street at night--Mr. Peggotty, in despite of all my remonstrances, carrying my bag--I glanced into Omer and Joram’s shop, and saw my old friend Mr. Omer there, smoking his pipe.", "밤에 그 낯익은 거리를 지나는데 — 패거티 씨는 내가 아무리 말려도 기어이 내 가방을 들었다 — 나는 오머와 조럼의 가게 안을 흘끗 들여다보았고, 거기서 옛 친구 오머 씨가 파이프를 피우고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["I felt reluctant to be present, when Mr. Peggotty first met his sister and Ham; and made Mr. Omer my excuse for lingering behind.", "패거티 씨가 누이와 햄을 처음 만나는 자리에 내가 끼어 있기가 영 마음에 걸려, 오머 씨를 핑계 삼아 뒤에 좀 처졌다."]], [["‘How is Mr. Omer, after this long time?’ said I, going in.", "\"이렇게 오랜만인데, 오머 씨는 어떻게 지내세요?\" 안으로 들어서며 내가 말했다."]], [["He fanned away the smoke of his pipe, that he might get a better view of me, and soon recognized me with great delight.", "그는 나를 더 잘 보려고 파이프 연기를 손으로 휘저어 흩고는, 이내 나를 알아보고 무척 반가워했다."]], [["‘I should get up, sir, to acknowledge such an honour as this visit,’ said he, ‘only my limbs are rather out of sorts, and I am wheeled about.", "\"이런 영광스러운 방문에 마땅히 일어나서 인사를 드려야겠지만, 손님.\" 그가 말했다. \"다리가 영 시원찮아서, 이렇게 굴려 다니는 신세랍니다."], ["With the exception of my limbs and my breath, howsoever, I am as hearty as a man can be, I’m thankful to say.’", "그래도 다리하고 숨 말고는, 사람이 누릴 수 있는 만큼 정정하다고, 고맙게도 그렇게 말씀드릴 수 있지요.\""]], [["I congratulated him on his contented looks and his good spirits, and saw, now, that his easy-chair went on wheels.", "나는 그의 흡족한 표정과 좋은 기분을 두고 축하의 말을 건넸는데, 그제야 그의 안락의자에 바퀴가 달려 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘It’s an ingenious thing, ain’t it?’ he inquired, following the direction of my glance, and polishing the elbow with his arm.", "\"기발한 물건이지요, 안 그래요?\" 그가 내 시선이 향하는 데를 따라가며, 팔로 팔걸이를 쓱쓱 닦으며 물었다."], ["‘It runs as light as a feather, and tracks as true as a mail-coach.", "\"깃털처럼 가볍게 굴러가고, 역마차만큼이나 곧게 나아간답니다."], ["Bless you, my little Minnie--my grand-daughter you know, Minnie’s child--puts her little strength against the back, gives it a shove, and away we go, as clever and merry as ever you see anything!", "아이고, 우리 어린 미니가 — 내 손녀딸 말이오, 미니의 자식이지요 — 그 작은 힘으로 등받이를 밀면, 휙 하고 나아가는데, 그렇게 영리하고 신나는 광경은 좀처럼 보기 힘들 거요!"], ["And I tell you what--it’s a most uncommon chair to smoke a pipe in.’", "게다가 말이오 — 파이프 피우기에 이만큼 좋은 의자가 또 없답니다.\""]], [["I never saw such a good old fellow to make the best of a thing, and find out the enjoyment of it, as Mr. Omer.", "나는 오머 씨처럼 무슨 일이든 좋게 받아들이고 거기서 즐거움을 찾아내는 착한 노인을 본 적이 없다."], ["He was as radiant, as if his chair, his asthma, and the failure of his limbs, were the various branches of a great invention for enhancing the luxury of a pipe.", "그는 마치 자기 의자며, 천식이며, 시원찮은 다리가 죄다 파이프의 즐거움을 한층 돋우기 위한 어느 위대한 발명의 갈래들이라도 되는 양 환하게 빛나고 있었다."]], [["‘I see more of the world, I can assure you,’ said Mr.", "\"내 장담하는데, 이 의자에 앉아서 세상을 더 많이 본다오.\""], ["Omer, ‘in this chair, than ever I see out of it.", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"이 의자 밖에서 보던 것보다 말이오."], ["You’d be surprised at the number of people that looks in of a day to have a chat.", "하루에도 얼마나 많은 사람이 들여다보며 한담을 나누고 가는지, 놀라실 거요."], ["You really would!", "정말이라니까!"], ["There’s twice as much in the newspaper, since I’ve taken to this chair, as there used to be.", "이 의자를 들이고 나서는 신문에 실린 게 예전보다 두 배는 많아 보인답니다."], ["As to general reading, dear me, what a lot of it I do get through!", "일반 독서로 말하자면, 아이고, 어찌나 많이 읽어 치우는지!"], ["That’s what I feel so strong, you know!", "내가 그게 정말 다행이다 싶은 거요, 알겠소?"], ["If it had been my eyes, what should I have done?", "만약 눈이 탈이 났더라면 어쩔 뻔했겠소?"], ["If it had been my ears, what should I have done?", "귀가 탈이 났더라면 또 어쩔 뻔했고?"], ["Being my limbs, what does it signify?", "그게 다리니까, 그게 무슨 대수겠소?"], ["Why, my limbs only made my breath shorter when I used ‘em.", "아니, 다리야 쓸 때면 그저 숨이나 더 가쁘게 했을 뿐인걸."], ["And now, if I want to go out into the street or down to the sands, I’ve only got to call Dick, Joram’s youngest ‘prentice, and away I go in my own carriage, like the Lord Mayor of London.’", "그리고 이제 거리에 나가거나 모래밭에 내려가고 싶으면, 조럼네 막내 도제 딕을 부르기만 하면 돼요. 그러면 런던 시장님처럼 내 마차를 타고 휙 나간다오.\""]], [["He half suffocated himself with laughing here.", "여기서 그는 웃다가 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이 되었다."]], [["‘Lord bless you!’ said Mr.", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Omer, resuming his pipe, ‘a man must take the fat with the lean; that’s what he must make up his mind to, in this life.", "오머 씨가 다시 파이프를 물며 말했다. \"사람이란 기름진 데와 살코기를 함께 받아들여야 하는 법이오. 이 세상살이에서 사람이 마음먹어야 할 게 바로 그거지요."], ["Joram does a fine business.", "조럼이 장사를 아주 잘한답니다."], ["Ex-cellent business!’", "기막히게 잘해요!\""]], [["‘I am very glad to hear it,’ said I.", "\"그것참 반가운 소식이네요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I knew you would be,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"그럴 줄 알았소.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘And Joram and Minnie are like Valentines.", "\"게다가 조럼하고 미니는 한 쌍의 발렌타인 같다오."], ["What more can a man expect?", "사람이 그 이상 무얼 바라겠소?"], ["What’s his limbs to that!’", "그에 비하면 다리 따위가 뭐 대수겠소!\""]], [["His supreme contempt for his own limbs, as he sat smoking, was one of the pleasantest oddities I have ever encountered.", "파이프를 피우며 앉아 자기 다리를 그토록 깔보는 그 모습은, 내가 여태껏 만나 본 가장 흐뭇한 별남 가운데 하나였다."]], [["‘And since I’ve took to general reading, you’ve took to general writing, eh, sir?’ said Mr.", "\"그래, 내가 일반 독서에 빠진 사이, 손님은 일반 저술에 빠지셨구려, 안 그래요?\""], ["Omer, surveying me admiringly. ‘What a lovely work that was of yours!", "오머 씨가 나를 흠모하듯 훑어보며 말했다. \"참으로 사랑스러운 작품이었소!"], ["What expressions in it!", "그 속에 담긴 표현들이라니!"], ["I read it every word--every word.", "한 글자도 빠짐없이 읽었다오 — 한 글자도요."], ["And as to feeling sleepy! Not at all!’", "그런데 졸음이 오기는커녕! 전혀 안 왔지 뭐요!\""]], [["I laughingly expressed my satisfaction, but I must confess that I thought this association of ideas significant.", "나는 웃으며 흡족하다고 말했지만, 솔직히 그가 이 두 가지를 한데 묶어 말하는 게 의미심장하게 느껴졌다."]], [["‘I give you my word and honour, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"내 명예를 걸고 말하는데, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["Omer, ‘that when I lay that book upon the table, and look at it outside; compact in three separate and indiwidual wollumes--one, two, three; I am as proud as Punch to think that I once had the honour of being connected with your family.", "\"그 책을 탁자 위에 올려놓고 겉을 바라볼 때면, 따로따로 세 권으로 야무지게 묶인 그 책을 — 하나, 둘, 셋 — 한때 내가 손님 댁과 연을 맺는 영광을 누렸구나 생각하면, 어찌나 우쭐한지 모른다오."], ["And dear me, it’s a long time ago, now, ain’t it?", "아이고, 이제 그것도 한참 된 일이지요, 안 그래요?"], ["Over at Blunderstone.", "저 블런더스톤에서 말이오."], ["With a pretty little party laid along with the other party.", "어여쁜 작은 손님 한 분을 또 다른 손님 곁에 나란히 모셨던 그때 말입니다."], ["And you quite a small party then, yourself. Dear, dear!’", "그리고 그때는 손님도 아주 작은 손님이셨지요. 아이고, 저런, 저런!\""]], [["I changed the subject by referring to Emily.", "나는 에밀리 이야기를 꺼내며 화제를 돌렸다."], ["After assuring him that I did not forget how interested he had always been in her, and how kindly he had always treated her, I gave him a general account of her restoration to her uncle by the aid of Martha; which I knew would please the old man.", "그가 늘 에밀리에게 얼마나 마음을 써 왔는지, 또 얼마나 다정하게 대해 왔는지를 내가 잊지 않고 있다고 일러 준 뒤, 마사의 도움으로 에밀리가 아저씨 품으로 돌아오게 된 자초지종을 대강 들려주었다. 이 노인이 기뻐하리라는 걸 알았기 때문이다."], ["He listened with the utmost attention, and said, feelingly, when I had done:", "그는 더없이 골똘히 귀를 기울이더니, 내 말이 끝나자 감정에 겨워 말했다."]], [["‘I am rejoiced at it, sir!", "\"정말 기쁘구려, 손님!"], ["It’s the best news I have heard for many a day.", "요 며칠 들은 소식 중에 가장 반가운 소식이오."], ["Dear, dear, dear! And what’s going to be undertook for that unfortunate young woman, Martha, now?’", "아이고, 저런, 저런! 그런데 그 불쌍한 처녀, 마사를 위해서는 이제 무얼 해 줄 작정이오?\""]], [["‘You touch a point that my thoughts have been dwelling on since yesterday,’ said I, ‘but on which I can give you no information yet, Mr. Omer. Mr.", "\"어제부터 제 마음에 줄곧 걸려 있던 대목을 짚으시네요.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 아직은 그 일에 대해 알려 드릴 게 없습니다, 오머 씨."], ["Peggotty has not alluded to it, and I have a delicacy in doing so.", "패거티 씨가 그 얘기를 꺼내지 않았는데, 제가 먼저 입에 올리기가 조심스러워서요."], ["I am sure he has not forgotten it.", "분명 그분이 그 일을 잊지는 않았을 겁니다."], ["He forgets nothing that is disinterested and good.’", "사심 없고 선한 일이라면 그분은 무엇 하나 잊는 법이 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘Because you know,’ said Mr.", "\"그게 말이오,\""], ["Omer, taking himself up, where he had left off, ‘whatever is done, I should wish to be a member of.", "오머 씨가 끊었던 말을 다시 이어 받으며 말했다. \"무슨 일을 하든, 나도 한몫 끼고 싶구려."], ["Put me down for anything you may consider right, and let me know.", "손님이 보기에 옳다 싶은 일이라면 내 몫으로 얼마든 적어 두고, 알려 주시오."], ["I never could think the girl all bad, and I am glad to find she’s not.", "나는 도무지 그 처녀가 죄다 못된 애라고는 생각할 수 없었는데, 그렇지 않다는 걸 알게 되어 기쁘오."], ["So will my daughter Minnie be.", "내 딸 미니도 그럴 거요."], ["Young women are contradictory creatures in some things--her mother was just the same as her--but their hearts are soft and kind.", "젊은 여자들이란 어떤 일에선 영 종잡을 수 없는 데가 있어요 — 그 애 어미가 꼭 그랬지요 — 그래도 마음만은 곱고 따뜻하답니다."], ["It’s all show with Minnie, about Martha. Why she should consider it necessary to make any show, I don’t undertake to tell you.", "마사 일에 대해서는 미니가 죄다 겉치레로 그러는 거요. 어째서 그 애가 겉치레할 필요를 느끼는지는 나도 뭐라 말 못 하겠소."], ["But it’s all show, bless you.", "하지만 그게 다 겉치레라니까요, 아무렴."], ["She’d do her any kindness in private.", "남몰래는 무슨 친절이든 베풀 애지요."], ["So, put me down for whatever you may consider right, will you be so good? and drop me a line where to forward it.", "그러니 손님이 보기에 옳다 싶은 만큼 내 몫으로 적어 주시겠소? 그리고 어디로 보내면 될지 몇 자 적어 보내 주시오. 아이고,\""], ["Dear me!’ said Mr.", "오머 씨가 말했다."], ["Omer, ‘when a man is drawing on to a time of life, where the two ends of life meet; when he finds himself, however hearty he is, being wheeled about for the second time, in a speeches of go-cart; he should be over-rejoiced to do a kindness if he can.", "\"사람이란 게 삶의 두 끝이 맞닿는 그런 나이에 이르러서, 제아무리 정정하다 한들 두 번째로 일종의 유모차에 실려 굴려 다니는 신세가 되면 말이오, 친절을 베풀 수 있을 때 베푸는 걸 더없이 기뻐해야 하는 법이오."], ["He wants plenty. And I don’t speak of myself, particular,’ said Mr.", "그런 게 잔뜩 필요하지요. 그리고 딱히 내 얘기만 하는 건 아니라오.\""], ["Omer, ‘because, sir, the way I look at it is, that we are all drawing on to the bottom of the hill, whatever age we are, on account of time never standing still for a single moment.", "오머 씨가 말했다. \"왜냐하면, 손님, 내가 보기엔, 우리는 나이가 몇이든 다 함께 언덕 밑바닥을 향해 내려가고 있는 거요. 시간이란 게 단 한순간도 멈춰 서는 법이 없으니까."], ["So let us always do a kindness, and be over-rejoiced. To be sure!’", "그러니 우리 늘 친절을 베풀고, 더없이 기뻐합시다. 암, 그렇고말고요!\""]], [["He knocked the ashes out of his pipe, and put it on a ledge in the back of his chair, expressly made for its reception.", "그는 파이프에서 재를 톡톡 털어 내더니, 의자 등받이에 그것을 받쳐 두라고 일부러 만들어 둔 받침에 파이프를 올려놓았다."]], [["‘There’s Em’ly’s cousin, him that she was to have been married to,’ said Mr.", "\"에밀리의 사촌 말이오, 그 애와 결혼하기로 돼 있던 그 사람 말입니다.\""], ["Omer, rubbing his hands feebly, ‘as fine a fellow as there is in Yarmouth!", "오머 씨가 힘없이 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"야머스에서 둘도 없이 훌륭한 청년이지요!"], ["He’ll come and talk or read to me, in the evening, for an hour together sometimes.", "저녁이면 가끔 와서 한 시간씩 나한테 이야기도 해 주고 글도 읽어 준답니다."], ["That’s a kindness, I should call it! All his life’s a kindness.’", "그게 친절이라는 거지요, 암! 그 사람 한평생이 다 친절이라오.\""]], [["‘I am going to see him now,’ said I.", "\"마침 그분을 만나러 가는 길입니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Are you?’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"그래요?\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Tell him I was hearty, and sent my respects.", "\"내가 정정하더라고, 안부 전한다고 말해 주시오."], ["Minnie and Joram’s at a ball.", "미니하고 조럼은 무도회에 갔다오."], ["They would be as proud to see you as I am, if they was at home.", "집에 있었으면 나만큼이나 손님을 보고 자랑스러워했을 텐데."], ["Minnie won’t hardly go out at all, you see, “on account of father”, as she says.", "미니는 통 바깥출입을 안 하려 든답니다, '아버지 때문에'라면서 말이오."], ["So I swore tonight, that if she didn’t go, I’d go to bed at six. In consequence of which,’ Mr.", "그래서 오늘 밤엔 내가 으름장을 놨지요, 네가 안 나가면 나는 여섯 시에 자 버리겠다고. 그 덕분에,\""], ["Omer shook himself and his chair with laughter at the success of his device, ‘she and Joram’s at a ball.’", "오머 씨가 제 꾀가 통한 게 우스워 제 몸과 의자를 들썩이며 웃었다. \"미니하고 조럼이 무도회에 간 거라오.\""]], [["I shook hands with him, and wished him good night.", "나는 그와 악수를 나누고 안녕히 주무시라고 인사했다."]], [["‘Half a minute, sir,’ said Mr. Omer.", "\"잠깐만요, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘If you was to go without seeing my little elephant, you’d lose the best of sights. You never see such a sight!", "\"내 어린 코끼리를 안 보고 가시면, 둘도 없는 구경거리를 놓치는 거요. 그런 구경거리는 본 적 없을걸! 미니!\""], ["Minnie!’ A musical little voice answered, from somewhere upstairs, ‘I am coming, grandfather!’ and a pretty little girl with long, flaxen, curling hair, soon came running into the shop.", "위층 어디선가 작고 낭랑한 목소리가 대답했다. \"가요, 할아버지!\" 그러더니 길고 곱슬곱슬한 아마빛 머리칼을 늘어뜨린 예쁜 여자아이가 이내 가게로 달려 들어왔다."]], [["‘This is my little elephant, sir,’ said Mr. Omer, fondling the child.", "\"이게 내 어린 코끼리랍니다, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 아이를 어루만지며 말했다."], ["‘Siamese breed, sir.", "\"샴 종이지요, 손님."], ["Now, little elephant!’", "자, 어린 코끼리야!\""]], [["The little elephant set the door of the parlour open, enabling me to see that, in these latter days, it was converted into a bedroom for Mr.", "어린 코끼리가 거실 문을 활짝 열어 주는 바람에, 나는 요즈음 그 방이 위층으로 옮기기 힘든 오머 씨를 위한 침실로 바뀌었다는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Omer who could not be easily conveyed upstairs; and then hid her pretty forehead, and tumbled her long hair, against the back of Mr. Omer’s chair.", "그러고는 아이는 그 예쁜 이마를 감추듯, 긴 머리칼을 헝클어뜨리며 오머 씨의 의자 등받이에 머리를 들이밀었다."]], [["‘The elephant butts, you know, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"코끼리란 게 말이오, 손님.\" 오머 씨가 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다."], ["Omer, winking, ‘when he goes at a object.", "\"목표물을 향해 갈 때면 머리로 들이받는답니다."], ["Once, elephant. Twice. Three times!’", "하나, 코끼리야. 둘. 셋!\""]], [["At this signal, the little elephant, with a dexterity that was next to marvellous in so small an animal, whisked the chair round with Mr. Omer in it, and rattled it off, pell-mell, into the parlour, without touching the door-post: Mr.", "이 신호에 어린 코끼리는, 그 작은 짐승치고는 거의 놀랍다 할 만한 솜씨로, 오머 씨를 태운 채 의자를 홱 돌리더니, 문설주에 한 번 부딪치는 일도 없이 와르르 거실 안으로 밀고 들어갔다."], ["Omer indescribably enjoying the performance, and looking back at me on the road as if it were the triumphant issue of his life’s exertions.", "오머 씨는 그 묘기를 말할 수 없이 즐기며, 마치 그것이 제 한평생 노고의 의기양양한 결실이기라도 한 양, 나아가는 길에 나를 돌아보았다."]], [["After a stroll about the town I went to Ham’s house.", "마을을 한 바퀴 거닐고 나서 나는 햄의 집으로 갔다."], ["Peggotty had now removed here for good; and had let her own house to the successor of Mr. Barkis in the carrying business, who had paid her very well for the good-will, cart, and horse.", "패거티는 이제 아주 이리로 옮겨 와 살고 있었다. 그리고 자기 집은 운송업에서 바키스 씨의 뒤를 이은 사람에게 세놓았는데, 그 사람은 영업권과 짐수레와 말값을 그녀에게 후하게 쳐주었다."], ["I believe the very same slow horse that Mr. Barkis drove was still at work.", "내 생각에는 바키스 씨가 몰던 바로 그 굼뜬 말이 아직도 일하고 있는 듯했다."]], [["I found them in the neat kitchen, accompanied by Mrs. Gummidge, who had been fetched from the old boat by Mr. Peggotty himself.", "나는 그들이 깔끔한 부엌에 있는 것을 보았는데, 패거티 씨가 손수 옛날 배에서 데려온 거미지 부인도 함께 있었다."], ["I doubt if she could have been induced to desert her post, by anyone else.", "다른 누가 권했더라면 부인이 제 자리를 버리고 따라나섰을 성싶지 않다."], ["He had evidently told them all. Both Peggotty and Mrs.", "그는 식구들에게 모든 것을 다 일러둔 게 분명했다."], ["Gummidge had their aprons to their eyes, and Ham had just stepped out ‘to take a turn on the beach’.", "패거티와 거미지 부인은 둘 다 앞치마를 눈에 대고 있었고, 햄은 막 '바닷가나 한 바퀴 돌고 오겠다'며 나간 참이었다."], ["He presently came home, very glad to see me; and I hope they were all the better for my being there.", "그는 이내 집으로 돌아와 나를 보고 무척 반가워했다. 그리고 내가 거기 있어 준 덕에 식구들 모두가 조금이나마 마음이 나아졌기를 바란다."], ["We spoke, with some approach to cheerfulness, of Mr. Peggotty’s growing rich in a new country, and of the wonders he would describe in his letters.", "우리는 어느 정도 명랑한 기색을 띠며, 패거티 씨가 새 나라에서 부자가 될 일이며, 그가 편지에 적어 보낼 갖가지 신기한 이야기들에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."], ["We said nothing of Emily by name, but distantly referred to her more than once.", "우리는 에밀리의 이름을 입에 올리지는 않았지만, 한 번 이상 에둘러 그 애를 언급했다."], ["Ham was the serenest of the party.", "그 자리에서 가장 평온한 사람은 햄이었다."]], [["But, Peggotty told me, when she lighted me to a little chamber where the Crocodile book was lying ready for me on the table, that he always was the same.", "하지만 패거티가, 탁자 위에 악어 책이 나를 위해 준비된 채 놓여 있는 작은 방으로 촛불을 들고 나를 안내하면서 들려준 말로는, 그는 늘 그 모양이라고 했다."], ["She believed (she told me, crying) that he was broken-hearted; though he was as full of courage as of sweetness, and worked harder and better than any boat-builder in any yard in all that part.", "그녀는 (울먹이며 내게 말하기를) 그가 가슴이 무너진 거라고 믿었다. 그는 다정한 만큼이나 용기가 넘쳤고, 그 일대 어느 조선소의 어느 배 목수보다 더 부지런하고 솜씨 좋게 일했지만 말이다."], ["There were times, she said, of an evening, when he talked of their old life in the boat-house; and then he mentioned Emily as a child.", "저녁이면 가끔, 그가 그 배 집에서 보낸 옛 시절 이야기를 꺼낼 때가 있다고 그녀는 말했다. 그럴 때면 그는 에밀리를 어린아이로 입에 올렸다."], ["But, he never mentioned her as a woman.", "하지만 결코 그 애를 한 여인으로는 입에 올리지 않았다."]], [["I thought I had read in his face that he would like to speak to me alone.", "나는 그의 얼굴에서, 그가 나와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶어 한다는 것을 읽었다고 생각했다."], ["I therefore resolved to put myself in his way next evening, as he came home from his work.", "그래서 이튿날 저녁, 그가 일을 마치고 돌아올 때 그의 길목에 서 있기로 마음먹었다."], ["Having settled this with myself, I fell asleep.", "이렇게 혼자 결심하고 나서 나는 잠이 들었다."], ["That night, for the first time in all those many nights, the candle was taken out of the window, Mr. Peggotty swung in his old hammock in the old boat, and the wind murmured with the old sound round his head.", "그날 밤, 그 숱한 밤을 통틀어 처음으로, 창가의 촛불이 치워졌고, 패거티 씨는 옛날 배 안에서 그 옛 해먹에 누워 흔들렸으며, 바람은 그 옛 소리로 그의 머리맡을 맴돌며 속삭였다."]], [["All next day, he was occupied in disposing of his fishing-boat and tackle; in packing up, and sending to London by waggon, such of his little domestic possessions as he thought would be useful to him; and in parting with the rest, or bestowing them on Mrs.", "이튿날 그는 온종일 고깃배와 어구를 처분하고, 자기한테 쓸모 있겠다 싶은 자잘한 살림살이를 챙겨 짐수레 편으로 런던에 부치고, 나머지는 떠나보내거나 거미지 부인에게 나눠 주는 일로 바빴다."], ["Gummidge. She was with him all day.", "부인은 온종일 그의 곁에 있었다."], ["As I had a sorrowful wish to see the old place once more, before it was locked up, I engaged to meet them there in the evening.", "나는 그 옛 장소가 자물쇠로 잠기기 전에 한 번 더 보고 싶은 서글픈 마음에, 저녁에 거기서 그들과 만나기로 약속했다."], ["But I so arranged it, as that I should meet Ham first.", "다만 햄을 먼저 만날 수 있도록 일정을 짰다."]], [["It was easy to come in his way, as I knew where he worked.", "그가 어디서 일하는지 알았으니 그의 길목에 서기는 어렵지 않았다."], ["I met him at a retired part of the sands, which I knew he would cross, and turned back with him, that he might have leisure to speak to me if he really wished.", "나는 그가 가로지를 것을 아는 한적한 모래밭 어귀에서 그를 만나, 그가 정말로 원한다면 나와 한가로이 이야기할 수 있도록 그와 함께 발길을 돌렸다."], ["I had not mistaken the expression of his face.", "그의 얼굴에 떠오른 기색을 내가 잘못 읽은 게 아니었다."], ["We had walked but a little way together, when he said, without looking at me:", "우리가 함께 얼마 걷지 않았을 때, 그가 나를 보지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy, have you seen her?’", "\"데이비 도련님, 그 애를 보셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Only for a moment, when she was in a swoon,’ I softly answered.", "\"잠깐, 그 애가 혼절해 있을 때 봤을 뿐이야.\" 내가 나직이 대답했다."]], [["We walked a little farther, and he said:", "우리는 조금 더 걸었고, 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy, shall you see her, d’ye think?’", "\"데이비 도련님, 도련님은 그 애를 보실 것 같으세요?\""]], [["‘It would be too painful to her, perhaps,’ said I.", "\"아마 그 애한테는 너무 괴로운 일일 거야.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I have thowt of that,’ he replied.", "\"저도 그 생각을 했습니다.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘So ‘twould, sir, so ‘twould.’", "\"그렇겠지요, 도련님, 그렇겠지요.\""]], [["‘But, Ham,’ said I, gently, ‘if there is anything that I could write to her, for you, in case I could not tell it; if there is anything you would wish to make known to her through me; I should consider it a sacred trust.’", "\"하지만 햄,\" 내가 부드럽게 말했다. \"혹시 내가 그 애한테 직접 전할 수 없을 경우, 자네를 대신해 글로 적어 보낼 만한 게 있다면, 또 나를 통해 그 애한테 알리고 싶은 게 있다면, 내가 그걸 거룩한 부탁으로 여기고 맡겠네.\""]], [["‘I am sure on’t.", "\"틀림없이 그러시겠지요."], ["I thankee, sir, most kind! I think theer is something I could wish said or wrote.’", "고맙습니다, 도련님, 참으로 친절하십니다! 말로든 글로든 전하고 싶은 게 하나 있는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘What is it?’", "\"그게 뭔가?\""]], [["We walked a little farther in silence, and then he spoke.", "우리는 말없이 조금 더 걸었고, 이윽고 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘’Tan’t that I forgive her.", "\"제가 그 애를 용서한다는 게 아닙니다."], ["‘Tan’t that so much. ‘Tis more as I beg of her to forgive me, for having pressed my affections upon her.", "그건 그리 대수로운 게 아니에요. 그보다는, 제 마음을 그 애한테 억지로 들이민 걸 용서해 달라고 그 애한테 비는 겁니다."], ["Odd times, I think that if I hadn’t had her promise fur to marry me, sir, she was that trustful of me, in a friendly way, that she’d have told me what was struggling in her mind, and would have counselled with me, and I might have saved her.’", "이따금 이런 생각이 들거든요. 제가 그 애한테서 결혼하겠다는 약속을 받아 내지만 않았더라도, 도련님, 그 애가 저를 벗으로서 그토록 믿고 따랐으니, 제 마음속에서 무엇이 그리 들끓고 있는지 저한테 털어놓고 의논해 줬을 테고, 그랬다면 제가 그 애를 구할 수도 있었을 텐데 하고요.\""]], [["I pressed his hand.", "나는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. \"그게 다인가?\""], ["‘Is that all?’ ‘Theer’s yet a something else,’ he returned, ‘if I can say it, Mas’r Davy.’", "\"아직 하나 더 있습니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"제가 말로 할 수만 있다면요, 데이비 도련님.\""]], [["We walked on, farther than we had walked yet, before he spoke again.", "우리는 여태 걸은 것보다 더 멀리 걷고서야, 그가 다시 입을 열었다."], ["He was not crying when he made the pauses I shall express by lines.", "내가 줄표로 나타낼 멈춤의 순간에 그가 울고 있던 것은 아니었다."], ["He was merely collecting himself to speak very plainly.", "그는 그저 아주 또박또박 말하려고 마음을 추스르고 있었을 뿐이다."]], [["‘I loved her--and I love the mem’ry of her--too deep--to be able to lead her to believe of my own self as I’m a happy man.", "\"저는 그 애를 사랑했고 — 그 애에 대한 기억을 사랑합니다 — 너무 깊이요 — 그래서 제 스스로 행복한 사람인 척 그 애가 믿게 만들 수가 없습니다. 저는 오직 — 그 애를 잊어야만 — 행복할 수 있을 텐데 — 제가 그랬다는 말을 그 애가 듣게 되는 건 도저히 견딜 수 없을 것 같습니다."], ["I could only be happy--by forgetting of her--and I’m afeerd I couldn’t hardly bear as she should be told I done that.", "하지만 그토록 학식이 깊으신 도련님이, 무슨 말을 좀 생각해 내셔서, 제가 크게 상처받지 않았다고 그 애가 믿게 해 주실 수 있다면요. 여전히 그 애를 사랑하고, 그 애를 위해 슬퍼하고 있다고요."], ["But if you, being so full of learning, Mas’r Davy, could think of anything to say as might bring her to believe I wasn’t greatly hurt: still loving of her, and mourning for her: anything as might bring her to believe as I was not tired of my life, and yet was hoping fur to see her without blame, wheer the wicked cease from troubling and the weary are at rest--anything as would ease her sorrowful mind, and yet not make her think as I could ever marry, or as ‘twas possible that anyone could ever be to me what she was--I should ask of you to say that--with my prayers for her--that was so dear.’", "제가 삶에 지친 게 아니라, 다만 사악한 자들이 더는 괴롭히지 못하고 지친 자들이 안식을 얻는 그곳에서, 아무 허물 없이 그 애를 다시 만나기를 바라고 있다고 그 애가 믿게 할 만한 말 무엇이든요. 그 애의 슬픈 마음을 달래 주면서도, 제가 언젠가 결혼할 수 있으리라거나, 그 누구라도 그 애가 제게 그러했던 만큼이 될 수 있으리라고는 그 애가 생각지 않게 할 만한 말 무엇이든요. 그런 말을 그 애한테 전해 주십사고 — 그토록 소중했던 그 애를 위한 제 기도와 함께 — 부탁드리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["I pressed his manly hand again, and told him I would charge myself to do this as well as I could.", "나는 그의 사내다운 손을 다시 한번 꼭 쥐고, 내가 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 그 일을 맡겠노라고 말했다."]], [["‘I thankee, sir,’ he answered.", "\"고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘’Twas kind of you to meet me.", "\"이렇게 저를 만나러 와 주셔서 고맙습니다."], ["‘Twas kind of you to bear him company down.", "아저씨와 함께 내려와 주셔서 고맙고요."], ["Mas’r Davy, I unnerstan’ very well, though my aunt will come to Lon’on afore they sail, and they’ll unite once more, that I am not like to see him agen.", "데이비 도련님, 저는 잘 압니다. 비록 제 고모님이 그들이 떠나기 전에 런던으로 가서 다시 한번 한자리에 모이게 되겠지만, 저는 아저씨를 다시 못 볼 것 같다는 걸요."], ["I fare to feel sure on’t.", "그리될 것 같다는 게 똑똑히 느껴집니다."], ["We doen’t say so, but so ‘twill be, and better so.", "우리끼리 그렇게 말하지는 않습니다만, 그리될 거고, 또 그게 낫지요."], ["The last you see on him--the very last--will you give him the lovingest duty and thanks of the orphan, as he was ever more than a father to?’", "도련님이 아저씨를 마지막으로 보실 때 — 그 마지막 순간에 — 아저씨한테 전해 주시겠습니까? 아저씨가 늘 친아버지 이상으로 대해 주신 이 고아의, 더없이 사랑 깊은 효성과 감사를요?\""]], [["This I also promised, faithfully.", "이것도 나는 꼭 그러겠노라고 약속했다."]], [["‘I thankee agen, sir,’ he said, heartily shaking hands.", "\"다시 한번 고맙습니다, 도련님.\" 그가 진심으로 악수를 나누며 말했다."], ["‘I know wheer you’re a-going. Good-bye!’", "\"도련님이 어디로 가시는지 압니다. 안녕히 가십시오!\""]], [["With a slight wave of his hand, as though to explain to me that he could not enter the old place, he turned away.", "마치 자기는 그 옛 장소에 들어갈 수 없노라고 내게 일러 주기라도 하듯 가볍게 손을 한 번 흔들고는, 그는 발길을 돌렸다."], ["As I looked after his figure, crossing the waste in the moonlight, I saw him turn his face towards a strip of silvery light upon the sea, and pass on, looking at it, until he was a shadow in the distance.", "달빛 아래 황량한 벌판을 가로질러 가는 그의 모습을 바라보고 있노라니, 그가 바다 위에 비친 은빛 띠 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 그것을 바라보며 걸어가다가, 이윽고 저 멀리 한 점 그림자가 되는 것이 보였다."]], [["The door of the boat-house stood open when I approached; and, on entering, I found it emptied of all its furniture, saving one of the old lockers, on which Mrs. Gummidge, with a basket on her knee, was seated, looking at Mr.", "내가 다가갔을 때 그 배 집의 문은 열려 있었다. 안으로 들어서니, 낡은 궤짝 하나만 빼고 가구가 죄다 치워져 있었는데, 그 궤짝 위에 거미지 부인이 무릎에 바구니를 얹은 채 앉아 패거티 씨를 바라보고 있었다."], ["Peggotty. He leaned his elbow on the rough chimney-piece, and gazed upon a few expiring embers in the grate; but he raised his head, hopefully, on my coming in, and spoke in a cheery manner.", "그는 거친 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 난로 안에서 사위어 가는 몇 점의 잉걸불을 물끄러미 들여다보고 있었다. 그러다 내가 들어서자 희망에 찬 얼굴로 고개를 들고, 명랑한 투로 말했다."]], [["‘Come, according to promise, to bid farewell to ‘t, eh, Mas’r Davy?’ he said, taking up the candle.", "\"약속대로, 여기에 작별 인사를 하러 오셨군요, 그렇지요, 데이비 도련님?\" 그가 촛불을 집어 들며 말했다."], ["‘Bare enough, now, an’t it?’ ‘Indeed you have made good use of the time,’ said I.", "\"이제 텅 비었지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" \"정말 시간을 알차게 쓰셨네요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, we have not been idle, sir. Missis Gummidge has worked like a--I doen’t know what Missis Gummidge an’t worked like,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼요, 저희가 놀고 있지는 않았지요, 도련님. 거미지 부인이 마치 — 거미지 부인이 마치 뭐처럼 일했다고 해야 할지 모르겠네요.\""], ["Peggotty, looking at her, at a loss for a sufficiently approving simile.", "충분히 흡족할 만한 비유가 떠오르지 않아 막막한 듯, 패거티 씨가 부인을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Gummidge, leaning on her basket, made no observation.", "거미지 부인은 바구니에 몸을 기댄 채 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Theer’s the very locker that you used to sit on, ‘long with Em’ly!’ said Mr.", "\"바로 저게 도련님이 에밀리하고 같이 앉곤 하시던 그 궤짝입니다!\""], ["Peggotty, in a whisper.", "패거티 씨가 속삭이듯 말했다."], ["‘I’m a-going to carry it away with me, last of all.", "\"저건 맨 마지막에 제가 가지고 떠날 작정이에요."], ["And heer’s your old little bedroom, see, Mas’r Davy!", "그리고 여기가 도련님의 그 작은 침실이었지요, 보세요, 데이비 도련님!"], ["A’most as bleak tonight, as ‘art could wish!’", "오늘 밤은 마음이 바랄 만큼이나 휑하군요!\""]], [["In truth, the wind, though it was low, had a solemn sound, and crept around the deserted house with a whispered wailing that was very mournful.", "정말이지 바람은 잔잔했어도 어딘가 엄숙한 소리를 냈고, 버려진 집 둘레를 속삭이듯 흐느끼며 맴도는 그 소리가 무척이나 구슬펐다."], ["Everything was gone, down to the little mirror with the oyster-shell frame.", "굴 껍데기 테를 두른 작은 거울까지, 모든 것이 다 사라지고 없었다."], ["I thought of myself, lying here, when that first great change was being wrought at home.", "나는 집에 그 첫 번째 큰 변화가 일어나던 무렵, 이곳에 누워 있던 내 모습을 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of the blue-eyed child who had enchanted me.", "나를 홀딱 반하게 했던 그 파란 눈의 아이를 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of Steerforth: and a foolish, fearful fancy came upon me of his being near at hand, and liable to be met at any turn.", "그리고 스티어포스를 떠올렸다. 그러자 그가 바로 가까이에 있어, 어느 모퉁이에서든 마주칠지 모른다는, 어리석고도 두려운 망상이 나를 사로잡았다."]], [["‘’Tis like to be long,’ said Mr.", "\"한참 걸릴 것 같습니다.\""], ["Peggotty, in a low voice, ‘afore the boat finds new tenants.", "패거티 씨가 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"이 배가 새 임자를 만나기까지는요."], ["They look upon ‘t, down heer, as being unfortunate now!’", "이 동네 사람들은 이제 이 배를 불길하게 여기거든요!\""]], [["‘Does it belong to anybody in the neighbourhood?’ I asked.", "\"이 동네 누가 주인인가요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘To a mast-maker up town,’ said Mr. Peggotty.", "\"읍내의 돛대 만드는 사람입니다.\" 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I’m a-going to give the key to him tonight.’", "\"오늘 밤 그 사람한테 열쇠를 넘겨줄 작정이에요.\""]], [["We looked into the other little room, and came back to Mrs. Gummidge, sitting on the locker, whom Mr.", "우리는 다른 작은 방을 들여다보고는, 궤짝에 앉아 있는 거미지 부인에게로 돌아왔다."], ["Peggotty, putting the light on the chimney-piece, requested to rise, that he might carry it outside the door before extinguishing the candle.", "패거티 씨가 촛불을 벽난로 선반에 올려놓고는, 촛불을 끄기 전에 궤짝을 문밖으로 내가려고 부인에게 일어나 달라고 청했다."]], [["‘Dan’l,’ said Mrs.", "\"댄..\""], ["Gummidge, suddenly deserting her basket, and clinging to his arm ‘my dear Dan’l, the parting words I speak in this house is, I mustn’t be left behind.", "거미지 부인이 별안간 바구니를 내팽개치고 그의 팔에 매달리며 말했다. \"내 소중한 댄, 내가 이 집에서 하는 작별 인사는 이거예요. 나를 두고 가면 안 돼요."], ["Doen’t ye think of leaving me behind, Dan’l!", "날 뒤에 남겨 두고 갈 생각일랑 말아요, 댄!"], ["Oh, doen’t ye ever do it!’", "아이고, 절대 그러지 말아요!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty, taken aback, looked from Mrs. Gummidge to me, and from me to Mrs. Gummidge, as if he had been awakened from a sleep.", "패거티 씨는 화들짝 놀라, 잠에서 막 깨어난 사람처럼, 거미지 부인을 보았다가 나를 보고, 다시 나를 보았다가 거미지 부인을 보았다."]], [["‘Doen’t ye, dearest Dan’l, doen’t ye!’ cried Mrs.", "\"그러지 말아요, 더없이 소중한 댄, 그러지 말아요!\""], ["Gummidge, fervently.", "거미지 부인이 간절하게 외쳤다."], ["‘Take me ‘long with you, Dan’l, take me ‘long with you and Em’ly!", "\"나를 데려가 줘요, 댄, 나도 당신하고 에밀리하고 같이 데려가 줘요!"], ["I’ll be your servant, constant and trew.", "변함없이 진실되게 당신네 하인이 될게요."], ["If there’s slaves in them parts where you’re a-going, I’ll be bound to you for one, and happy, but doen’t ye leave me behind, Dan’l, that’s a deary dear!’", "당신이 가는 그 고장에 노예가 있다면, 기꺼이 당신한테 매인 노예 한 사람이 되어도 좋아요. 그래도 행복할 거예요. 그러니 나를 두고 가지 말아요, 댄, 그래 줘요 제발!\""]], [["‘My good soul,’ said Mr.", "\"이 착한 사람아.\""], ["Peggotty, shaking his head, ‘you doen’t know what a long voyage, and what a hard life ‘tis!’ ‘Yes, I do, Dan’l!", "패거티 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그게 얼마나 긴 항해인지, 또 얼마나 고된 삶인지 모르고 하는 소리야!\" \"아니, 알아요, 댄!"], ["I can guess!’ cried Mrs. Gummidge.", "짐작할 수 있다고요!\" 거미지 부인이 외쳤다."], ["‘But my parting words under this roof is, I shall go into the house and die, if I am not took.", "\"하지만 이 지붕 아래서 하는 내 작별 인사는 이거예요. 나를 데려가지 않으면, 나는 구빈원에 들어가 죽을 거예요."], ["I can dig, Dan’l. I can work.", "나는 땅도 팔 수 있고, 댄."], ["I can live hard.", "일도 할 수 있어요."], ["I can be loving and patient now--more than you think, Dan’l, if you’ll on’y try me.", "고생도 견딜 수 있고요. 이젠 다정하고 참을성 있게 굴 수도 있어요 — 당신 생각보다 훨씬요, 댄, 한 번만 시험해 보면 알 거예요."], ["I wouldn’t touch the ‘lowance, not if I was dying of want, Dan’l Peggotty; but I’ll go with you and Em’ly, if you’ll on’y let me, to the world’s end!", "굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 그 생활비엔 손도 안 댈 거예요, 댄 패거티. 하지만 한 번만 허락해 준다면, 나는 당신하고 에밀리를 따라 세상 끝까지라도 갈 거예요! 나도 알아요."], ["I know how ‘tis; I know you think that I am lone and lorn; but, deary love, ‘tan’t so no more!", "내가 외롭고 의지가지없는 신세라고 당신이 여기는 걸 알아요. 하지만 소중한 당신, 이젠 더는 그렇지 않아요!"], ["I ain’t sat here, so long, a-watching, and a-thinking of your trials, without some good being done me.", "내가 여기 이토록 오래 앉아, 지켜보면서, 당신의 시련을 곱씹는 동안 나한테도 좋은 게 좀 생긴 거라고요."], ["Mas’r Davy, speak to him for me!", "데이비 도련님, 저 대신 좀 말해 주세요!"], ["I knows his ways, and Em’ly’s, and I knows their sorrows, and can be a comfort to ‘em, some odd times, and labour for ‘em allus!", "저는 저 사람 성미도, 에밀리 성미도 알고, 두 사람의 슬픔도 알아서, 이따금 그들에게 위안이 되어 줄 수도 있고, 언제까지나 그들을 위해 애쓸 수도 있어요!"], ["Dan’l, deary Dan’l, let me go ‘long with you!’", "댄, 소중한 댄, 나도 당신하고 같이 가게 해 줘요!\""]], [["And Mrs. Gummidge took his hand, and kissed it with a homely pathos and affection, in a homely rapture of devotion and gratitude, that he well deserved.", "그러고서 거미지 부인은 그의 손을 잡아, 그가 마땅히 받을 만한, 헌신과 감사가 어린 소박한 황홀경 속에서, 투박하면서도 애틋한 정과 가련함을 담아 그 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["We brought the locker out, extinguished the candle, fastened the door on the outside, and left the old boat close shut up, a dark speck in the cloudy night.", "우리는 궤짝을 밖으로 내가고, 촛불을 끄고, 문을 바깥에서 단단히 잠근 다음, 그 옛 배를 꼭 닫아 두고 떠났다. 흐린 밤하늘 아래 한 점 검은 얼룩으로."], ["Next day, when we were returning to London outside the coach, Mrs. Gummidge and her basket were on the seat behind, and Mrs. Gummidge was happy.", "이튿날, 우리가 역마차 바깥자리에 앉아 런던으로 돌아가는데, 거미지 부인과 부인의 바구니는 뒷자리에 실려 있었고, 거미지 부인은 행복했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 52. I ASSIST AT AN EXPLOSION", "# CHAPTER 52. I ASSIST AT AN EXPLOSION"]], [["When the time Mr. Micawber had appointed so mysteriously, was within four-and-twenty hours of being come, my aunt and I consulted how we should proceed; for my aunt was very unwilling to leave Dora. Ah!", "미코버 씨가 그토록 알쏭달쏭하게 정해 둔 날이 이제 스물네 시간도 채 남지 않았을 무렵, 고모님과 나는 어떻게 일을 처리할지 의논했다. 고모님이 도라 곁을 떠나기를 몹시 꺼렸기 때문이다. 아!"], ["how easily I carried Dora up and down stairs, now!", "이제 나는 얼마나 가뿐하게 도라를 안고 계단을 오르내렸던가!"]], [["We were disposed, notwithstanding Mr. Micawber’s stipulation for my aunt’s attendance, to arrange that she should stay at home, and be represented by Mr.", "미코버 씨가 고모님이 꼭 참석해야 한다고 못 박았는데도, 우리는 고모님은 집에 남고 디크 씨와 내가 대신 가도록 일을 꾸미려는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["Dick and me. In short, we had resolved to take this course, when Dora again unsettled us by declaring that she never would forgive herself, and never would forgive her bad boy, if my aunt remained behind, on any pretence.", "한마디로, 우리는 그렇게 하기로 마음을 정했는데, 그러자 도라가 또다시 우리를 흔들어 놓았다. 무슨 핑계를 대든 고모님이 뒤에 남는다면, 자기는 평생 자기 자신을 용서하지 못할 거고, 자기 나쁜 남편도 결코 용서하지 못할 거라고 선언한 것이다."]], [["‘I won’t speak to you,’ said Dora, shaking her curls at my aunt.", "\"고모님하고는 말도 안 할 거예요.\" 도라가 고모님을 향해 곱슬머리를 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘I’ll be disagreeable!", "\"심술쟁이가 될 거예요!"], ["I’ll make Jip bark at you all day.", "집프가 하루 종일 고모님한테 짖게 만들 거예요."], ["I shall be sure that you really are a cross old thing, if you don’t go!’", "안 가시면, 고모님이 정말로 못된 늙은이라고 단단히 믿어 버릴 거라고요!\""]], [["‘Tut, Blossom!’ laughed my aunt.", "\"쯧, 우리 꽃송이!\" 고모님이 웃었다."], ["‘You know you can’t do without me!’", "\"넌 내가 없으면 못 견딘다는 거, 잘 알면서!\""]], [["‘Yes, I can,’ said Dora.", "\"아니요, 견딜 수 있어요.\""], ["‘You are no use to me at all. You never run up and down stairs for me, all day long.", "도라가 말했다. \"고모님은 저한테 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없어요."], ["You never sit and tell me stories about Doady, when his shoes were worn out, and he was covered with dust--oh, what a poor little mite of a fellow!", "하루 종일 저 때문에 계단을 오르내려 주지도 않잖아요. 곁에 앉아서, 도디가 신발이 다 닳고 먼지투성이가 됐을 적 이야기를 들려주지도 않고요 — 아, 그 가엾고 조그만 꼬마!"], ["You never do anything at all to please me, do you, dear?’ Dora made haste to kiss my aunt, and say, ‘Yes, you do!", "고모님은 저를 기쁘게 해 줄 일은 하나도 안 하잖아요, 안 그래요, 고모님?\" 도라는 고모님이 그 말을 진담으로 받아들일까 봐, 부랴부랴 고모님에게 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["I’m only joking!’-lest my aunt should think she really meant it.", "\"맞아요, 다 해 주시죠! 농담이에요!\""]], [["‘But, aunt,’ said Dora, coaxingly, ‘now listen.", "\"하지만, 고모님.\" 도라가 어리광 부리듯 말했다."], ["You must go.", "\"이제 들어 보세요."], ["I shall tease you, ‘till you let me have my own way about it.", "꼭 가셔야 해요. 제 뜻대로 될 때까지 고모님을 졸라 댈 거예요."], ["I shall lead my naughty boy such a life, if he don’t make you go.", "우리 못된 남편이 고모님을 안 가게 하면, 그 사람 인생을 아주 들들 볶을 거예요."], ["I shall make myself so disagreeable--and so will Jip!", "제가 얼마나 심술궂게 굴 텐데요 — 집프도 그럴 거고요!"], ["You’ll wish you had gone, like a good thing, for ever and ever so long, if you don’t go.", "안 가시면, 착하게 다녀올걸 하고 두고두고, 정말 오래오래 후회하실 거예요. 게다가,\""], ["Besides,’ said Dora, putting back her hair, and looking wonderingly at my aunt and me, ‘why shouldn’t you both go?", "도라가 머리를 뒤로 넘기며, 고모님과 나를 의아한 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"왜 두 분이 다 가시면 안 되는 거죠?"], ["I am not very ill indeed. Am I?’", "저 정말로 그렇게 아프지 않아요. 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘Why, what a question!’ cried my aunt.", "\"아니, 그게 무슨 소리니!\" 고모님이 외쳤다."]], [["‘What a fancy!’ said I.", "\"무슨 그런 생각을!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes!", "\"네!"], ["I know I am a silly little thing!’ said Dora, slowly looking from one of us to the other, and then putting up her pretty lips to kiss us as she lay upon her couch.", "제가 어리석은 꼬마라는 거 알아요!\" 도라가 우리 둘을 천천히 번갈아 보다가, 소파에 누운 채 우리에게 입 맞추려고 예쁜 입술을 내밀며 말했다. \"그럼, 두 분 다 가셔야 해요."], ["‘Well, then, you must both go, or I shall not believe you; and then I shall cry!’", "안 그러면 저는 두 분을 안 믿을 거고, 그러면 울어 버릴 거예요!\""]], [["I saw, in my aunt’s face, that she began to give way now, and Dora brightened again, as she saw it too.", "고모님의 얼굴에서, 이제 마음이 누그러지기 시작하는 것이 보였고, 도라도 그것을 알아채자 다시 환해졌다."]], [["‘You’ll come back with so much to tell me, that it’ll take at least a week to make me understand!’ said Dora.", "\"두 분이 돌아오시면 들려줄 이야기가 어찌나 많을지, 제가 알아듣는 데만도 적어도 일주일은 걸릴 거예요!\" 도라가 말했다."], ["‘Because I know I shan’t understand, for a length of time, if there’s any business in it.", "\"왜냐면 거기에 무슨 사무적인 일이 끼어 있으면, 한참 동안 못 알아들을 게 뻔하거든요."], ["And there’s sure to be some business in it!", "그리고 분명 무슨 사무적인 일이 끼어 있을 거고요!"], ["If there’s anything to add up, besides, I don’t know when I shall make it out; and my bad boy will look so miserable all the time. There!", "게다가 셈할 게 있기라도 하면, 언제 풀어낼지 모르겠고요. 그동안 우리 나쁜 남편은 내내 어찌나 비참한 얼굴을 하고 있을 텐데요. 자!"], ["Now you’ll go, won’t you?", "이제 가실 거죠, 네?"], ["You’ll only be gone one night, and Jip will take care of me while you are gone.", "딱 하룻밤만 비우시는 거고, 비우신 동안엔 집프가 저를 지켜 줄 거예요."], ["Doady will carry me upstairs before you go, and I won’t come down again till you come back; and you shall take Agnes a dreadfully scolding letter from me, because she has never been to see us!’", "도디가 가시기 전에 저를 위층으로 안아다 줄 거고, 두 분이 돌아오실 때까지 저는 다시 안 내려올 거예요. 그리고 아그네스한테 제가 보내는 무섭게 야단치는 편지를 가져다주세요. 한 번도 우리 보러 안 왔으니까요!\""]], [["We agreed, without any more consultation, that we would both go, and that Dora was a little Impostor, who feigned to be rather unwell, because she liked to be petted.", "우리는 더 의논할 것도 없이, 둘 다 가기로, 그리고 도라는 어리광 받는 게 좋아서 좀 아픈 척하는 작은 사기꾼이라고 의견을 모았다."], ["She was greatly pleased, and very merry; and we four, that is to say, my aunt, Mr. Dick, Traddles, and I, went down to Canterbury by the Dover mail that night.", "도라는 무척 기뻐하며 한껏 들떴다. 그리고 우리 넷, 그러니까 고모님, 디크 씨, 트래들스, 나는 그날 밤 도버행 우편 마차를 타고 캔터베리로 내려갔다."]], [["At the hotel where Mr. Micawber had requested us to await him, which we got into, with some trouble, in the middle of the night, I found a letter, importing that he would appear in the morning punctually at half past nine.", "미코버 씨가 자기를 기다려 달라고 청한 호텔에 한밤중에 어렵사리 들어가서, 나는 편지 한 통을 발견했다. 그가 아침 아홉 시 반 정각에 나타나겠다는 내용이었다."], ["After which, we went shivering, at that uncomfortable hour, to our respective beds, through various close passages; which smelt as if they had been steeped, for ages, in a solution of soup and stables.", "그러고 나서 우리는 그 거북한 시각에, 묵은 수프와 마구간 냄새가 우러난 용액에 오랜 세월 절여진 듯한 비좁은 통로들을 거쳐, 부들부들 떨며 각자의 침대로 갔다."]], [["Early in the morning, I sauntered through the dear old tranquil streets, and again mingled with the shadows of the venerable gateways and churches.", "이른 아침, 나는 정겹고 오래된 고요한 거리를 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐며, 다시 한번 유서 깊은 성문과 교회의 그림자 속으로 스며들었다. 까마귀들이 대성당 첨탑 둘레를 빙빙 떠다녔다."], ["The rooks were sailing about the cathedral towers; and the towers themselves, overlooking many a long unaltered mile of the rich country and its pleasant streams, were cutting the bright morning air, as if there were no such thing as change on earth.", "그 첨탑들은 변함없이 길게 뻗은 기름진 들판과 그 정겨운 시내를 굽어보며, 이 땅에 변화라는 것은 도무지 없다는 듯 환한 아침 공기를 가르고 있었다. 그러나 종소리가 울리자, 그 소리는 만물의 변화를 서글피 일러 주었다. 종 자신의 늙음을, 그리고 내 어여쁜 도라의 젊음을."], ["Yet the bells, when they sounded, told me sorrowfully of change in everything; told me of their own age, and my pretty Dora’s youth; and of the many, never old, who had lived and loved and died, while the reverberations of the bells had hummed through the rusty armour of the Black Prince hanging up within, and, motes upon the deep of Time, had lost themselves in air, as circles do in water.", "그리고 종소리의 울림이, 안쪽에 걸린 흑태자의 녹슨 갑옷을 뚫고 웅웅거리다가, 시간의 깊은 물 위에 떠도는 티끌이 되어, 물에 번지는 동그라미처럼 허공으로 사라지는 동안, 살고 사랑하고 죽어 간, 결코 늙지 않는 수많은 이들을 일러 주었다. * 흑태자: 14세기 잉글랜드 왕 에드워드 3세의 맏아들 에드워드를 가리키는 별칭. 검은 갑옷을 입었다 하여 그렇게 불렸다."]], [["I looked at the old house from the corner of the street, but did not go nearer to it, lest, being observed, I might unwittingly do any harm to the design I had come to aid.", "나는 길모퉁이에서 그 오래된 집을 바라보았으나, 더 가까이 가지는 않았다. 들켜서, 내가 도우러 온 계획에 본의 아니게 해를 끼칠까 봐서였다."], ["The early sun was striking edgewise on its gables and lattice-windows, touching them with gold; and some beams of its old peace seemed to touch my heart.", "이른 아침 햇살이 그 집 박공과 격자창에 비스듬히 내려앉아 황금빛으로 어루만지고 있었다. 그 오래된 평화의 빛줄기 몇 가닥이 내 마음마저 어루만지는 듯했다."]], [["I strolled into the country for an hour or so, and then returned by the main street, which in the interval had shaken off its last night’s sleep.", "나는 한 시간 남짓 들판을 거닐다가, 그새 간밤의 잠을 떨쳐 낸 큰길을 따라 돌아왔다."], ["Among those who were stirring in the shops, I saw my ancient enemy the butcher, now advanced to top-boots and a baby, and in business for himself.", "가게에서 부산을 떠는 사람들 가운데, 나의 옛 원수인 푸줏간 사내가 보였다. 이제는 목 긴 장화를 신고 갓난아기까지 거느린, 제 가게를 차린 어엿한 주인이었다."], ["He was nursing the baby, and appeared to be a benignant member of society.", "그는 아기를 어르고 있었는데, 사회의 자애로운 일원처럼 보였다."]], [["We all became very anxious and impatient, when we sat down to breakfast.", "우리는 아침을 먹으려고 자리에 앉았을 때 다들 몹시 초조하고 안달이 났다."], ["As it approached nearer and nearer to half past nine o’clock, our restless expectation of Mr. Micawber increased.", "아홉 시 반이 점점 가까워질수록, 미코버 씨를 기다리는 우리의 안절부절못함도 커져 갔다."], ["At last we made no more pretence of attending to the meal, which, except with Mr. Dick, had been a mere form from the first; but my aunt walked up and down the room.", "마침내 우리는 식사에 마음을 쓰는 척조차 그만두었는데, 디크 씨를 빼고는 처음부터 그저 형식에 불과한 식사였다. 고모님은 방 안을 서성였다."], ["Traddles sat upon the sofa affecting to read the paper with his eyes on the ceiling; and I looked out of the window to give early notice of Mr.", "트래들스는 소파에 앉아 두 눈은 천장에 둔 채 신문을 읽는 척했고, 나는 창밖을 내다보며 미코버 씨가 오는지 미리 알리려 했다."], ["Micawber’s coming. Nor had I long to watch, for, at the first chime of the half hour, he appeared in the street.", "오래 지켜볼 것도 없었다. 그 반 시각을 알리는 첫 종소리가 울리자, 그가 거리에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Here he is,’ said I, ‘and not in his legal attire!’", "\"저기 오네요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그런데 법률가 차림이 아니에요!\""]], [["My aunt tied the strings of her bonnet (she had come down to breakfast in it), and put on her shawl, as if she were ready for anything that was resolute and uncompromising.", "고모님은 보닛 끈을 묶고(아침 먹으러 내려올 때부터 쓰고 있었다), 마치 단호하고 한 치도 물러섬 없는 일이라면 무엇이든 맞설 채비를 한 사람처럼 숄을 둘렀다."], ["Traddles buttoned his coat with a determined air. Mr.", "트래들스는 결연한 기색으로 코트 단추를 채웠다."], ["Dick, disturbed by these formidable appearances, but feeling it necessary to imitate them, pulled his hat, with both hands, as firmly over his ears as he possibly could; and instantly took it off again, to welcome Mr. Micawber.", "디크 씨는 이 가공할 차림새들에 마음이 어수선했지만, 자기도 흉내 내야겠다고 느꼈는지, 두 손으로 모자를 잡아 있는 힘껏 단단히 귀까지 푹 눌러썼다가, 미코버 씨를 맞이하려고 곧바로 다시 벗었다."]], [["‘Gentlemen, and madam,’ said Mr.", "\"신사 여러분, 그리고 부인.\""], ["Micawber, ‘good morning!", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"안녕하십니까!"], ["My dear sir,’ to Mr.", "친애하는 선생님,\""], ["Dick, who shook hands with him violently, ‘you are extremely good.’", "그와 격렬하게 악수를 나눈 디크 씨를 향해. \"정말 친절하십니다.\""]], [["‘Have you breakfasted?’ said Mr. Dick.", "\"아침은 드셨소?\" 디크 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Have a chop!’", "\"갈비 한 점 드시오!\""]], [["‘Not for the world, my good sir!’ cried Mr.", "\"천만에요, 친절하신 선생님!\""], ["Micawber, stopping him on his way to the bell; ‘appetite and myself, Mr. Dixon, have long been strangers.’", "미코버 씨가 종을 울리러 가는 그를 가로막으며 외쳤다. \"식욕과 저는, 딕슨 씨, 오래전부터 서로 모르는 사이가 되었답니다.\""]], [["Mr. Dixon was so well pleased with his new name, and appeared to think it so obliging in Mr. Micawber to confer it upon him, that he shook hands with him again, and laughed rather childishly.", "딕슨 씨는 새 이름이 어찌나 마음에 들었는지, 또 미코버 씨가 그 이름을 붙여 준 것이 어찌나 고마운 일로 여겨졌는지, 그와 또 한 번 악수를 하며 다소 어린아이처럼 웃었다."]], [["‘Dick,’ said my aunt, ‘attention!’", "\"디크.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"정신 차려요!\""]], [["Mr. Dick recovered himself, with a blush.", "디크 씨는 얼굴을 붉히며 제정신을 차렸다."]], [["‘Now, sir,’ said my aunt to Mr.", "\"자, 그럼.\""], ["Micawber, as she put on her gloves, ‘we are ready for Mount Vesuvius, or anything else, as soon as YOU please.’", "고모님이 장갑을 끼며 미코버 씨에게 말했다. \"당신이 원하시면 언제든, 우리는 베수비오 화산이든 뭐든 맞설 채비가 되어 있어요.\""]], [["‘Madam,’ returned Mr.", "\"부인.\""], ["Micawber, ‘I trust you will shortly witness an eruption. Mr.", "미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"곧 분화를 목격하시리라 믿습니다."], ["Traddles, I have your permission, I believe, to mention here that we have been in communication together?’", "트래들스 씨, 우리가 함께 연락을 주고받아 왔다는 사실을 여기서 밝혀도 좋다는 허락을 주신 걸로 압니다만?\""]], [["‘It is undoubtedly the fact, Copperfield,’ said Traddles, to whom I looked in surprise. ‘Mr.", "\"틀림없는 사실이라네, 코퍼필드.\" 내가 놀라서 쳐다보자 트래들스가 말했다."], ["Micawber has consulted me in reference to what he has in contemplation; and I have advised him to the best of my judgement.’", "\"미코버 씨가 자기가 구상하는 일에 관해 내게 상의했고, 나는 내 판단껏 최선을 다해 조언했네.\""]], [["‘Unless I deceive myself, Mr. Traddles,’ pursued Mr.", "\"제 생각이 틀리지 않았다면, 트래들스 씨.\""], ["Micawber, ‘what I contemplate is a disclosure of an important nature.’", "미코버 씨가 말을 이었다. \"제가 구상하는 것은 중대한 성격의 폭로입니다.\""]], [["‘Highly so,’ said Traddles.", "\"지극히 그렇지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps, under such circumstances, madam and gentlemen,’ said Mr.", "\"그렇다면, 부인 그리고 신사 여러분.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘you will do me the favour to submit yourselves, for the moment, to the direction of one who, however unworthy to be regarded in any other light but as a Waif and Stray upon the shore of human nature, is still your fellow-man, though crushed out of his original form by individual errors, and the accumulative force of a combination of circumstances?’", "\"이런 상황에서, 부디 잠시만 한 사람의 지시에 따라 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까? 그자는 인간 본성의 물가에 떠밀려 온 표류물이자 부랑자로 여겨지는 것 말고는 달리 봐줄 구석이 없는 자이지만, 그래도 개인적 과오와 갖가지 상황이 겹쳐 누적된 힘에 짓눌려 본래의 모습이 으스러졌을 뿐, 여전히 여러분과 같은 인간입니다.\""]], [["‘We have perfect confidence in you, Mr.", "\"우리는 당신을 온전히 믿습니다, 미코버 씨.\""], ["Micawber,’ said I, ‘and will do what you please.’", "내가 말했다. \"당신이 하라는 대로 하지요.\""]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield,’ returned Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨.\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다."], ["Micawber, ‘your confidence is not, at the existing juncture, ill-bestowed.", "\"현 시점에서 당신의 믿음은 헛되이 베풀어진 것이 아닙니다."], ["I would beg to be allowed a start of five minutes by the clock; and then to receive the present company, inquiring for Miss Wickfield, at the office of Wickfield and Heep, whose Stipendiary I am.’", "부디 시계로 오 분만 먼저 출발하도록 허락해 주십시오. 그런 다음, 제가 봉급쟁이로 일하는 위크필드 앤드 힙 사무소로, 위크필드 양을 찾아 오신 여러분을 맞이하겠습니다.\""]], [["My aunt and I looked at Traddles, who nodded his approval.", "고모님과 나는 트래들스를 쳐다보았고, 그는 고개를 끄덕여 찬성했다."]], [["‘I have no more,’ observed Mr. Micawber, ‘to say at present.’", "\"지금으로서는 더 드릴 말씀이 없습니다.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."]], [["With which, to my infinite surprise, he included us all in a comprehensive bow, and disappeared; his manner being extremely distant, and his face extremely pale.", "그러고는, 나로서는 한없이 놀랍게도, 그가 우리 모두를 아우르는 넓은 절을 하더니 사라져 버렸다. 그 태도는 더없이 서먹했고, 얼굴은 더없이 창백했다."]], [["Traddles only smiled, and shook his head (with his hair standing upright on the top of it), when I looked to him for an explanation; so I took out my watch, and, as a last resource, counted off the five minutes.", "내가 설명을 바라며 쳐다보자 트래들스는 그저 빙긋 웃으며 (머리카락이 정수리에서 빳빳하게 곤두선 채) 고개를 가로저을 뿐이었다. 그래서 나는 시계를 꺼내, 마지막 방편으로 오 분을 세었다."], ["My aunt, with her own watch in her hand, did the like.", "고모님도 손에 자기 시계를 들고 똑같이 했다."], ["When the time was expired, Traddles gave her his arm; and we all went out together to the old house, without saying one word on the way.", "시간이 다 되자, 트래들스가 고모님에게 팔을 내밀었고, 우리는 가는 내내 한마디도 하지 않고 다 함께 그 오래된 집으로 갔다."]], [["We found Mr. Micawber at his desk, in the turret office on the ground floor, either writing, or pretending to write, hard.", "미코버 씨는 일 층 탑루 사무실의 책상에 앉아, 글을 쓰거나 쓰는 척하느라 여념이 없었다."], ["The large office-ruler was stuck into his waistcoat, and was not so well concealed but that a foot or more of that instrument protruded from his bosom, like a new kind of shirt-frill.", "큼직한 사무용 자가 그의 조끼에 꽂혀 있었는데, 잘 숨기지 못해서, 그 자가 한 자 남짓 가슴팍에서 삐죽 튀어나와, 무슨 새로운 종류의 셔츠 주름 장식처럼 보였다."]], [["As it appeared to me that I was expected to speak, I said aloud:", "내가 말을 꺼내야 할 것 같아서, 나는 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["‘How do you do, Mr. Micawber?’", "\"안녕하세요, 미코버 씨?\""]], [["‘Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨.\" 미코버 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["Micawber, gravely, ‘I hope I see you well?’", "\"건강하신 모습이길 바랍니다만?\""]], [["‘Is Miss Wickfield at home?’ said I.", "\"위크필드 양은 댁에 계신가요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Wickfield is unwell in bed, sir, of a rheumatic fever,’ he returned; ‘but Miss Wickfield, I have no doubt, will be happy to see old friends.", "\"위크필드 씨는 류머티즘열로 자리에 누워 편찮으십니다, 선생님.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["Will you walk in, sir?’", "\"하지만 위크필드 양은 옛 친구분들을 분명 반갑게 맞으시리라 믿습니다. 들어오시겠습니까, 선생님?\""]], [["He preceded us to the dining-room--the first room I had entered in that house--and flinging open the door of Mr. Wickfield’s former office, said, in a sonorous voice:", "그는 우리를 앞서 식당으로 데려갔다 — 내가 이 집에서 맨 처음 들어가 본 방이었다 — 그러고는 위크필드 씨가 예전에 쓰던 사무실 문을 활짝 열어젖히며, 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Miss Trotwood, Mr. David Copperfield, Mr. Thomas Traddles, and Mr. Dixon!’", "\"트롯우드 양, 데이비드 코퍼필드 씨, 토머스 트래들스 씨, 그리고 딕슨 씨!\""]], [["I had not seen Uriah Heep since the time of the blow.", "나는 그 일격 이후로 유라이아 힙을 본 적이 없었다."], ["Our visit astonished him, evidently; not the less, I dare say, because it astonished ourselves.", "우리의 방문이 그를 놀라게 한 것이 역력했다. 우리 자신도 놀랐던 만큼이나 더더욱 그랬으리라 싶다."], ["He did not gather his eyebrows together, for he had none worth mentioning; but he frowned to that degree that he almost closed his small eyes, while the hurried raising of his grisly hand to his chin betrayed some trepidation or surprise.", "그는 눈썹을 한데 모으지 않았는데, 모을 만한 눈썹이랄 게 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 작은 두 눈이 거의 감기다시피 할 만큼 눈살을 찌푸렸고, 흉측한 손을 황급히 턱으로 가져가는 모습이 어떤 당혹이나 놀람을 드러냈다."], ["This was only when we were in the act of entering his room, and when I caught a glance at him over my aunt’s shoulder.", "이건 우리가 그의 방으로 막 들어서는 찰나, 내가 고모님 어깨 너머로 그를 흘끗 보았을 때뿐이었다."], ["A moment afterwards, he was as fawning and as humble as ever.", "잠시 후, 그는 여느 때처럼 알랑거리고 비굴해졌다."]], [["‘Well, I am sure,’ he said.", "\"어이쿠, 이거 참.\""], ["‘This is indeed an unexpected pleasure!", "그가 말했다. \"이거 정말 뜻밖의 기쁨이로군요!"], ["To have, as I may say, all friends round St. Paul’s at once, is a treat unlooked for! Mr.", "말하자면, 세인트 폴 둘레의 벗들을 한꺼번에 다 모시다니, 바라지도 못한 호사올시다!"], ["Copperfield, I hope I see you well, and--if I may umbly express myself so--friendly towards them as is ever your friends, whether or not. Mrs.", "코퍼필드 씨, 건강하신 모습이길 바라고요 — 외람되이 이렇게 표현해도 된다면 — 당신의 벗 되시는 분들에게, 그렇든 아니든, 늘 다정하신 모습이길 바랍니다."], ["Copperfield, sir, I hope she’s getting on. We have been made quite uneasy by the poor accounts we have had of her state, lately, I do assure you.’", "코퍼필드 부인은요, 선생님, 차도가 있으시길 바랍니다. 요즘 부인의 상태가 좋지 않다는 소식에 우리도 정말이지 어찌나 마음을 졸였는지요.\""]], [["I felt ashamed to let him take my hand, but I did not know yet what else to do.", "그에게 내 손을 잡히는 게 부끄러웠지만, 달리 어쩌면 좋을지 알 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Things are changed in this office, Miss Trotwood, since I was an umble clerk, and held your pony; ain’t they?’ said Uriah, with his sickliest smile.", "\"제가 보잘것없는 서기로 트롯우드 양의 조랑말을 붙들고 있던 때랑은, 이 사무실도 변했지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 유라이아가 더없이 역겨운 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["‘But I am not changed, Miss Trotwood.’", "\"하지만 저는 안 변했습니다, 트롯우드 양.\""]], [["‘Well, sir,’ returned my aunt, ‘to tell you the truth, I think you are pretty constant to the promise of your youth; if that’s any satisfaction to you.’", "\"글쎄요, 선생.\" 고모님이 대꾸했다. \"솔직히 말하자면, 당신은 어린 시절의 그 싹수에 꽤나 한결같다고 생각하는데. 그게 당신에게 무슨 위안이 된다면 말이오.\""]], [["‘Thank you, Miss Trotwood,’ said Uriah, writhing in his ungainly manner, ‘for your good opinion!", "\"고맙습니다, 트롯우드 양.\" 유라이아가 볼썽사납게 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"좋게 봐주셔서요!"], ["Micawber, tell ‘em to let Miss Agnes know--and mother.", "미코버, 아그네스 아씨한테 알리라고 일러 — 그리고 어머니한테도."], ["Mother will be quite in a state, when she sees the present company!’ said Uriah, setting chairs.", "어머니는 이분들을 보면 정신이 다 나가실 거요!\" 유라이아가 의자를 갖다 놓으며 말했다."]], [["‘You are not busy, Mr.", "\"바쁘지 않으시오, 힙 씨?\""], ["Heep?’ said Traddles, whose eye the cunning red eye accidentally caught, as it at once scrutinized and evaded us.", "트래들스가 말했다. 우리를 동시에 살피면서도 피하던 그 교활하고 붉은 눈이, 트래들스의 눈과 우연히 마주쳤다."]], [["‘No, Mr. Traddles,’ replied Uriah, resuming his official seat, and squeezing his bony hands, laid palm to palm between his bony knees.", "\"아니요, 트래들스 씨.\" 유라이아가 공무용 자리로 돌아가 앉으며, 뼈만 앙상한 두 손을 손바닥끼리 맞붙여 앙상한 두 무릎 사이에 끼우고 쥐어짜며 대답했다."], ["‘Not so much so as I could wish.", "\"바랐던 만큼은 아니지요."], ["But lawyers, sharks, and leeches, are not easily satisfied, you know!", "하지만 변호사며, 상어며, 거머리는 좀처럼 만족을 모르는 법 아닙니까!"], ["Not but what myself and Micawber have our hands pretty full, in general, on account of Mr.", "그렇다고 저랑 미코버 손이 한가하다는 건 아닙니다, 대체로 양손에 일이 그득하지요."], ["Wickfield’s being hardly fit for any occupation, sir.", "위크필드 씨가 무슨 일이든 도무지 감당하기 어려우신 탓이라서요, 선생님."], ["But it’s a pleasure as well as a duty, I am sure, to work for him.", "하지만 그분을 위해 일하는 건 의무이자 즐거움이올시다, 분명히요."], ["You’ve not been intimate with Mr. Wickfield, I think, Mr. Traddles?", "트래들스 씨는 위크필드 씨와 가깝게 지내신 적이 없으시지요?"], ["I believe I’ve only had the honour of seeing you once myself?’", "제가 직접 뵌 영광은 딱 한 번뿐인 걸로 압니다만?\""]], [["‘No, I have not been intimate with Mr.", "\"네, 위크필드 씨와 가깝게 지낸 적은 없습니다.\""], ["Wickfield,’ returned Traddles; ‘or I might perhaps have waited on you long ago, Mr. Heep.’", "트래들스가 대답했다. \"그랬더라면 진작에 당신을 찾아뵈었을지도 모르지요, 힙 씨.\""]], [["There was something in the tone of this reply, which made Uriah look at the speaker again, with a very sinister and suspicious expression.", "이 대답의 어조에는 무언가가 있어서, 유라이아는 매우 음흉하고 의심스러운 표정으로 그 말을 한 사람을 다시 쳐다보았다."], ["But, seeing only Traddles, with his good-natured face, simple manner, and hair on end, he dismissed it as he replied, with a jerk of his whole body, but especially his throat:", "그러나 사람 좋은 얼굴에, 꾸밈없는 태도에, 곤두선 머리카락을 한 트래들스밖에 보이지 않자, 그는 그 의심을 떨쳐 버리며, 온몸을, 특히 목구멍을 한 번 홱 비틀고는 대답했다."]], [["‘I am sorry for that, Mr.", "\"안타깝군요, 트래들스 씨."], ["Traddles. You would have admired him as much as we all do.", "그분을 가까이 두셨다면 우리 모두처럼 흠모하셨을 텐데요."], ["His little failings would only have endeared him to you the more.", "그분의 사소한 결점들이 오히려 더 정겹게 느껴지셨을 거고요."], ["But if you would like to hear my fellow-partner eloquently spoken of, I should refer you to Copperfield.", "하지만 제 동업자를 멋들어지게 칭송하는 걸 들어 보고 싶으시거든, 코퍼필드한테 물어보시라고 권하지요."], ["The family is a subject he’s very strong upon, if you never heard him.’", "그 집안 이야기라면 저 친구가 아주 자신 있어 하는 화제거든요, 들어 보신 적 없으시다면 말입니다.\""]], [["I was prevented from disclaiming the compliment (if I should have done so, in any case), by the entrance of Agnes, now ushered in by Mr. Micawber.", "내가 그 치하를(어쨌든 그래야 했다면) 부인할 새도 없이, 미코버 씨가 안내하는 아그네스가 들어왔다."], ["She was not quite so self-possessed as usual, I thought; and had evidently undergone anxiety and fatigue.", "그녀는 평소만큼 차분하지는 못한 것 같았고, 불안과 피로를 겪은 것이 역력했다."], ["But her earnest cordiality, and her quiet beauty, shone with the gentler lustre for it.", "그러나 그녀의 진심 어린 다정함과 고요한 아름다움은, 그 때문에 더 부드러운 광채로 빛났다."]], [["I saw Uriah watch her while she greeted us; and he reminded me of an ugly and rebellious genie watching a good spirit.", "유라이아가 그녀가 우리에게 인사하는 동안 그녀를 지켜보는 것이 보였다. 그는 선한 정령을 노려보는 추악하고 반항적인 마귀를 떠올리게 했다."], ["In the meanwhile, some slight sign passed between Mr. Micawber and Traddles; and Traddles, unobserved except by me, went out.", "그러는 사이, 미코버 씨와 트래들스 사이에 어떤 가벼운 신호가 오갔고, 나 말고는 아무도 못 본 채, 트래들스가 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["‘Don’t wait, Micawber,’ said Uriah.", "\"기다리지 마, 미코버.\" 유라이아가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber, with his hand upon the ruler in his breast, stood erect before the door, most unmistakably contemplating one of his fellow-men, and that man his employer.", "미코버 씨는 가슴팍의 자에 한 손을 얹은 채 문 앞에 꼿꼿이 서서, 자기와 같은 인간 하나를, 더구나 그 인간이 자기 고용주인 자를, 더없이 또렷하게 응시하고 있었다."]], [["‘What are you waiting for?’ said Uriah. ‘Micawber!", "\"뭘 기다리는 거야?\" 유라이아가 말했다. \"미코버!"], ["did you hear me tell you not to wait?’", "기다리지 말라고 한 말 못 들었어?\""]], [["‘Yes!’ replied the immovable Mr. Micawber.", "\"들었지!\" 꿈쩍도 않는 미코버 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘Then why DO you wait?’ said Uriah.", "\"그럼 왜 기다리는 거야?\" 유라이아가 말했다."]], [["‘Because I--in short, choose,’ replied Mr.", "\"왜냐하면 나는 — 한마디로, 그러기로 택했으니까.\""], ["Micawber, with a burst.", "미코버 씨가 버럭 대답했다."]], [["Uriah’s cheeks lost colour, and an unwholesome paleness, still faintly tinged by his pervading red, overspread them. He looked at Mr.", "유라이아의 두 뺨에서 핏기가 가셨고, 온몸에 깔린 붉은 기가 아직 희미하게 어린, 병색 짙은 창백함이 그 뺨을 뒤덮었다."], ["Micawber attentively, with his whole face breathing short and quick in every feature.", "그는 미코버 씨를 유심히 쳐다보았는데, 얼굴의 모든 근육이 가쁘고 빠르게 숨을 쉬었다."]], [["‘You are a dissipated fellow, as all the world knows,’ he said, with an effort at a smile, ‘and I am afraid you’ll oblige me to get rid of you.", "\"넌 세상이 다 아는 방탕한 놈이야.\" 그가 억지로 웃음을 지어 보이며 말했다. \"그러니 너 때문에 어쩔 수 없이 널 내쫓게 될까 봐 걱정이로구나. 썩 꺼져!"], ["Go along! I’ll talk to you presently.’", "이따가 너랑 얘기하지.\""]], [["‘If there is a scoundrel on this earth,’ said Mr.", "\"이 땅 위에, 내가 이미 너무 많은 말을 섞은 악당이 하나 있다면,\""], ["Micawber, suddenly breaking out again with the utmost vehemence, ‘with whom I have already talked too much, that scoundrel’s name is--HEEP!’", "미코버 씨가 별안간 다시, 더없이 격렬하게 터져 나오며 말했다. \"그 악당의 이름은 바로 — 힙이다!\""]], [["Uriah fell back, as if he had been struck or stung.", "유라이아는 얻어맞거나 쏘이기라도 한 듯 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["Looking slowly round upon us with the darkest and wickedest expression that his face could wear, he said, in a lower voice:", "그러더니 그 얼굴이 지을 수 있는 가장 어둡고 사악한 표정으로 우리를 천천히 둘러보며, 한층 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Oho! This is a conspiracy!", "\"오호! 이거 음모로구나!"], ["You have met here by appointment!", "너희가 미리 짜고 여기서 만난 거야!"], ["You are playing Booty with my clerk, are you, Copperfield?", "내 서기랑 짜고 한통속으로 노는 거지, 그렇지, 코퍼필드?"], ["Now, take care.", "자, 조심해."], ["You’ll make nothing of this. We understand each other, you and me.", "이래 봤자 아무것도 얻지 못할 테니까. 너랑 나는 서로를 잘 알아."], ["There’s no love between us.", "우리 사이엔 정이라곤 없지."], ["You were always a puppy with a proud stomach, from your first coming here; and you envy me my rise, do you?", "넌 여기 처음 발 들인 순간부터 늘 콧대 높은 강아지였고, 내가 출세한 걸 시기하지, 안 그래?"], ["None of your plots against me; I’ll counterplot you!", "나한테 음모 따위 꾸미지 마. 내가 맞불을 놓을 테니!"], ["Micawber, you be off.", "미코버, 넌 꺼져."], ["I’ll talk to you presently.’", "이따 너랑 얘기하지.\""]], [["‘Mr.", "\"미코버 씨.\""], ["Micawber,’ said I, ‘there is a sudden change in this fellow, in more respects than the extraordinary one of his speaking the truth in one particular, which assures me that he is brought to bay.", "내가 말했다. \"이 작자에게 갑작스러운 변화가 일어났습니다. 한 가지 점에서 진실을 말했다는 그 유별난 변화 말고도 여러 면에서요."], ["Deal with him as he deserves!’", "그건 이자가 막다른 골목에 몰렸다는 증거입니다. 받아 마땅한 대로 다뤄 주십시오!\""]], [["‘You are a precious set of people, ain’t you?’ said Uriah, in the same low voice, and breaking out into a clammy heat, which he wiped from his forehead, with his long lean hand, ‘to buy over my clerk, who is the very scum of society,--as you yourself were, Copperfield, you know it, before anyone had charity on you,--to defame me with his lies?", "\"너희들 참 별난 작자들이로구나, 안 그래?\" 유라이아가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로, 이마에 송골송골 맺힌 끈끈한 땀을 길고 비쩍 마른 손으로 닦아 내며 말했다. \"사회의 쓰레기나 다름없는 내 서기를 매수해서 — 너 자신도 그랬잖아, 코퍼필드, 너도 알지, 누가 너한테 자비를 베풀기 전까진 말이야 — 거짓말로 날 헐뜯다니?"], ["Miss Trotwood, you had better stop this; or I’ll stop your husband shorter than will be pleasant to you.", "트롯우드 양, 이 짓 그만두는 게 좋을 거요. 안 그러면 당신 남편 일을 당신 마음에 안 들 만큼 짧게 끝장내 줄 테니까."], ["I won’t know your story professionally, for nothing, old lady!", "내가 당신 사연을 직업상 공짜로 알게 된 줄 아쇼, 노부인!"], ["Miss Wickfield, if you have any love for your father, you had better not join that gang.", "위크필드 양, 아버지를 조금이라도 사랑한다면, 저 패거리에 끼지 않는 게 좋을 거요."], ["I’ll ruin him, if you do.", "그러면 내가 그분을 파멸시킬 테니까."], ["Now, come!", "자, 어디!"], ["I have got some of you under the harrow.", "너희 중 몇몇은 이미 내 써레 밑에 깔려 있어."], ["Think twice, before it goes over you.", "그게 너희 위로 지나가기 전에 두 번 생각해."], ["Think twice, you, Micawber, if you don’t want to be crushed.", "두 번 생각하라고, 너, 미코버, 으스러지기 싫거든."], ["I recommend you to take yourself off, and be talked to presently, you fool!", "내 충고하건대 썩 꺼져서 이따 이야기나 듣지 그래, 이 멍청아!"], ["while there’s time to retreat.", "물러설 시간 있을 때 말이야."], ["Where’s mother?’ he said, suddenly appearing to notice, with alarm, the absence of Traddles, and pulling down the bell-rope.", "어머니는 어딨지?\" 그가 트래들스가 없어진 것을 별안간 알아챈 듯 화들짝 놀라며, 종 줄을 잡아당기며 말했다."], ["‘Fine doings in a person’s own house!’", "\"남의 집에서 별짓을 다 하는군!\""]], [["‘Mrs. Heep is here, sir,’ said Traddles, returning with that worthy mother of a worthy son.", "\"힙 부인은 여기 계십니다, 선생.\" 트래들스가 그 갸륵한 아들의 갸륵한 어머니를 데리고 돌아오며 말했다."], ["‘I have taken the liberty of making myself known to her.’", "\"외람되이 제가 부인께 제 소개를 해 두었습니다.\""]], [["‘Who are you to make yourself known?’ retorted Uriah.", "\"네가 뭔데 네 소개를 해?\" 유라이아가 쏘아붙였다."], ["‘And what do you want here?’", "\"그리고 여기서 뭘 원하는 거야?\""]], [["‘I am the agent and friend of Mr. Wickfield, sir,’ said Traddles, in a composed and business-like way.", "\"저는 위크필드 씨의 대리인이자 친구입니다, 선생.\" 트래들스가 차분하고 사무적인 투로 말했다."], ["‘And I have a power of attorney from him in my pocket, to act for him in all matters.’", "\"그리고 그분이 모든 일에서 그분을 대신해 처리하라고 주신 위임장이 제 주머니에 있습니다.\""]], [["‘The old ass has drunk himself into a state of dotage,’ said Uriah, turning uglier than before, ‘and it has been got from him by fraud!’", "\"그 늙은 멍청이가 술에 절어 노망이 든 거지.\" 유라이아가 아까보다 더 추악해진 얼굴로 말했다. \"그걸 사기로 받아 낸 거야!\""]], [["‘Something has been got from him by fraud, I know,’ returned Traddles quietly; ‘and so do you, Mr. Heep.", "\"무언가를 사기로 받아 냈지요, 나도 압니다.\" 트래들스가 조용히 대꾸했다. \"당신도 알고요, 힙 씨."], ["We will refer that question, if you please, to Mr. Micawber.’", "괜찮으시다면 그 문제는 미코버 씨에게 맡기지요.\""]], [["‘Ury--!’ Mrs.", "\"유리—!\""], ["Heep began, with an anxious gesture.", "힙 부인이 불안한 몸짓으로 입을 열었다."]], [["‘YOU hold your tongue, mother,’ he returned; ‘least said, soonest mended.’", "\"어머니는 입 다물어요.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"말이 적을수록 탈도 적은 법이니까.\""]], [["‘But, my Ury--’", "\"하지만, 우리 유리—\""]], [["‘Will you hold your tongue, mother, and leave it to me?’", "\"입 다물고 나한테 맡기라니까요, 어머니?\""]], [["Though I had long known that his servility was false, and all his pretences knavish and hollow, I had had no adequate conception of the extent of his hypocrisy, until I now saw him with his mask off.", "그의 비굴함이 거짓이고, 그의 모든 가식이 음흉하고 속이 빈 것임은 진작부터 알고 있었지만, 이제 가면을 벗어 던진 그를 보고서야, 나는 그 위선의 깊이를 제대로 헤아렸다."], ["The suddenness with which he dropped it, when he perceived that it was useless to him; the malice, insolence, and hatred, he revealed; the leer with which he exulted, even at this moment, in the evil he had done--all this time being desperate too, and at his wits’ end for the means of getting the better of us--though perfectly consistent with the experience I had of him, at first took even me by surprise, who had known him so long, and disliked him so heartily.", "가면이 더는 쓸모없음을 알아챈 순간 그것을 내던지는 그 갑작스러움, 그가 드러낸 악의와 오만과 증오, 이 순간에조차 자기가 저지른 악행에 의기양양해하며 짓던 곁눈질 — 게다가 이 내내 그는 절박했고, 우리를 이길 방도를 찾느라 막다른 골목에 몰려 있기도 했다 — 이 모든 것이 내가 그에게서 겪은 경험과 완벽하게 들어맞았는데도, 그토록 오래 그를 알아 왔고 그토록 진심으로 싫어한 나마저, 처음엔 깜짝 놀라고 말았다."]], [["I say nothing of the look he conferred on me, as he stood eyeing us, one after another; for I had always understood that he hated me, and I remembered the marks of my hand upon his cheek.", "그가 우리를 하나하나 노려보며 서 있을 때 나에게 던진 그 눈초리에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠다. 그가 나를 미워한다는 건 늘 알고 있었고, 그의 뺨에 남은 내 손자국도 기억하고 있었으니까."], ["But when his eyes passed on to Agnes, and I saw the rage with which he felt his power over her slipping away, and the exhibition, in their disappointment, of the odious passions that had led him to aspire to one whose virtues he could never appreciate or care for, I was shocked by the mere thought of her having lived, an hour, within sight of such a man.", "그러나 그의 눈길이 아그네스에게 옮겨 갔을 때, 그녀를 휘두르던 자기 힘이 손가락 사이로 빠져나가는 것을 느끼며 그가 품은 분노, 그리고 그 좌절 속에서, 그가 결코 진가를 알아보지도 마음 쓰지도 못할 미덕을 지닌 사람을 탐내게 만든 그 가증스러운 욕정의 표출 — 그것을 본 나는, 그녀가 그런 사내의 눈길이 닿는 곳에서 단 한 시간이라도 살아왔다는 생각만으로도 몸서리가 쳐졌다."]], [["After some rubbing of the lower part of his face, and some looking at us with those bad eyes, over his grisly fingers, he made one more address to me, half whining, and half abusive.", "얼굴 아랫부분을 한참 문지르고, 흉측한 손가락들 너머로 그 못된 눈으로 우리를 한참 쳐다보더니, 그는 나에게 다시 한번, 반은 징징대고 반은 욕설을 퍼붓는 투로 말을 걸었다."]], [["‘You think it justifiable, do you, Copperfield, you who pride yourself so much on your honour and all the rest of it, to sneak about my place, eaves-dropping with my clerk?", "\"이게 정당하다고 생각하는 거지, 어, 코퍼필드, 그렇게 명예니 뭐니 하는 걸 그토록 자랑스러워하는 네가, 내 집을 살금살금 기웃거리며 내 서기랑 엿듣고 다니는 게? 이게 나였다면 놀라지도 않았을 거야."], ["If it had been ME, I shouldn’t have wondered; for I don’t make myself out a gentleman (though I never was in the streets either, as you were, according to Micawber), but being you!--And you’re not afraid of doing this, either?", "난 나 자신을 신사라고 내세우지도 않으니까(하긴 미코버 말로는 너처럼 거리를 떠돈 적은 한 번도 없지만). 그런데 다른 사람도 아닌 네가! 게다가 이런 짓을 하면서 무섭지도 않아?"], ["You don’t think at all of what I shall do, in return; or of getting yourself into trouble for conspiracy and so forth? Very well.", "내가 그 대가로 무슨 짓을 할지는 조금도 생각 안 해? 음모니 뭐니 해서 네 발등에 불 떨어질 일은? 좋아."], ["We shall see! Mr.", "두고 보자고!"], ["What’s-your-name, you were going to refer some question to Micawber. There’s your referee.", "이름이 뭐였더라, 당신, 어떤 문제를 미코버한테 맡긴다고 했지. 자, 저기 당신네 중재인 있잖아."], ["Why don’t you make him speak?", "어디 한번 말을 시켜 보시지?"], ["He has learnt his lesson, I see.’", "보아하니 자기 대본은 다 외운 모양인데.\""]], [["Seeing that what he said had no effect on me or any of us, he sat on the edge of his table with his hands in his pockets, and one of his splay feet twisted round the other leg, waiting doggedly for what might follow.", "자기 말이 나에게도, 우리 누구에게도 아무 효과가 없는 걸 보고서, 그는 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 한쪽 넓적한 발을 다른 다리에 비비 꼰 채, 책상 모서리에 걸터앉아, 다음에 무슨 일이 닥칠지 끈질기게 기다렸다."]], [["Mr. Micawber, whose impetuosity I had restrained thus far with the greatest difficulty, and who had repeatedly interposed with the first syllable of SCOUN-drel! without getting to the second, now burst forward, drew the ruler from his breast (apparently as a defensive weapon), and produced from his pocket a foolscap document, folded in the form of a large letter.", "미코버 씨는, 그때까지 내가 그의 격정을 천신만고 끝에 억눌러 온 터였고, 두 번째 음절까지는 가지도 못한 채 \"악다—악당!\"의 첫 음절만 거듭 끼워 넣던 참이었는데, 이제 앞으로 와락 뛰쳐나오며 가슴팍에서 자를 빼 들고(아무래도 방어용 무기로 삼으려는 듯했다), 주머니에서 큼직한 편지 모양으로 접힌 대형 문서 한 장을 꺼냈다."], ["Opening this packet, with his old flourish, and glancing at the contents, as if he cherished an artistic admiration of their style of composition, he began to read as follows:", "옛날 그 멋들어진 손짓으로 이 꾸러미를 펼쳐, 마치 그 문장의 작법에 예술적 감탄이라도 품은 듯 내용을 한번 훑어보더니, 이렇게 읽어 내려가기 시작했다."]], [["‘“Dear Miss Trotwood and gentlemen--“’", "\"'친애하는 트롯우드 양, 그리고 신사 여러분—'\""]], [["‘Bless and save the man!’ exclaimed my aunt in a low voice.", "\"맙소사, 저 양반 좀 봐!\" 고모님이 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["‘He’d write letters by the ream, if it was a capital offence!’", "\"사형감인 일이라도, 편지는 한 연(連)씩 써 댈 양반이라니까!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, without hearing her, went on.", "미코버 씨는 그 말을 듣지 못한 채 계속했다."]], [["‘“In appearing before you to denounce probably the most consummate Villain that has ever existed,”’ Mr.", "\"'아마도 일찍이 존재한 가장 완벽한 악당을 고발하고자 여러분 앞에 나서면서,'\""], ["Micawber, without looking off the letter, pointed the ruler, like a ghostly truncheon, at Uriah Heep, ‘“I ask no consideration for myself.", "미코버 씨는 편지에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 유령의 곤봉처럼 자로 유라이아 힙을 가리켰다. \"'저 자신을 위한 배려는 일절 청하지 않습니다."], ["The victim, from my cradle, of pecuniary liabilities to which I have been unable to respond, I have ever been the sport and toy of debasing circumstances.", "요람에 있을 적부터, 갚을 길 없는 금전 채무의 희생양이었던 저는, 늘 비천한 처지의 노리개이자 장난감이었습니다."], ["Ignominy, Want, Despair, and Madness, have, collectively or separately, been the attendants of my career.”’", "치욕, 궁핍, 절망, 광기가, 한꺼번에 혹은 따로따로, 제 인생 행로의 길동무였습니다.'\""]], [["The relish with which Mr. Micawber described himself as a prey to these dismal calamities, was only to be equalled by the emphasis with which he read his letter; and the kind of homage he rendered to it with a roll of his head, when he thought he had hit a sentence very hard indeed.", "미코버 씨가 자신을 이 음울한 재난들의 먹잇감이라고 묘사하는 그 감칠맛은, 편지를 읽어 내려가는 그 힘찬 어조에나 견줄 만했다. 그리고 정말 어느 문장을 제대로 한 방 먹였다 싶을 때, 머리를 한 번 끄덕여 그 문장에 바치는 일종의 경의에나 비길 만했다."]], [["‘“In an accumulation of Ignominy, Want, Despair, and Madness, I entered the office--or, as our lively neighbour the Gaul would term it, the Bureau--of the Firm, nominally conducted under the appellation of Wickfield and--HEEP, but in reality, wielded by--HEEP alone.", "\"'치욕과 궁핍과 절망과 광기가 켜켜이 쌓인 가운데, 저는 명목상으로는 위크필드 앤드 — 힙이라는 명칭으로 운영되나, 실제로는 — 힙 혼자 좌지우지하는 그 회사의 사무소 — 우리의 발랄한 이웃 갈리아 사람이라면 뷔로라 부를 — 에 들어갔습니다."], ["HEEP, and only HEEP, is the mainspring of that machine.", "힙, 오로지 힙만이, 그 기계의 태엽입니다."], ["HEEP, and only HEEP, is the Forger and the Cheat.”’", "힙, 오로지 힙만이, 위조범이자 사기꾼입니다.'\""]], [["Uriah, more blue than white at these words, made a dart at the letter, as if to tear it in pieces. Mr.", "이 말에 흰빛보다 푸른빛이 더 도는 얼굴이 된 유라이아가, 편지를 갈기갈기 찢으려는 듯 와락 달려들었다."], ["Micawber, with a perfect miracle of dexterity or luck, caught his advancing knuckles with the ruler, and disabled his right hand. It dropped at the wrist, as if it were broken.", "미코버 씨는 솜씨인지 운인지 완벽한 기적을 부려, 다가오는 그의 손마디를 자로 후려쳐 오른손을 못 쓰게 만들었다. 그 손은 손목에서 뚝 꺾여, 부러지기라도 한 듯 축 늘어졌다."], ["The blow sounded as if it had fallen on wood.", "그 일격은 나무토막을 후려친 듯한 소리를 냈다."]], [["‘The Devil take you!’ said Uriah, writhing in a new way with pain.", "\"이 빌어먹을 놈!\" 유라이아가 통증에 새로운 몸짓으로 비틀거리며 말했다."], ["‘I’ll be even with you.’", "\"이 빚은 꼭 갚아 주마.\""]], [["‘Approach me again, you--you--you HEEP of infamy,’ gasped Mr.", "\"한 번만 더 나한테 다가와 봐, 이 — 이 — 이 치욕덩어리 힙아.\" 미코버 씨가 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘and if your head is human, I’ll break it.", "\"네 머리통이 사람의 머리통이거든, 내가 그걸 박살 내 주마."], ["Come on, come on!’", "덤벼, 덤벼 봐!\""]], [["I think I never saw anything more ridiculous--I was sensible of it, even at the time--than Mr. Micawber making broad-sword guards with the ruler, and crying, ‘Come on!’ while Traddles and I pushed him back into a corner, from which, as often as we got him into it, he persisted in emerging again.", "미코버 씨가 자로 큰칼 막기 자세를 취하며 \"덤벼!\" 하고 외치고, 트래들스와 내가 그를 구석으로 밀어 넣어도 그가 처넣어 줄 때마다 한사코 다시 뛰쳐나오던 그 광경보다 더 우스꽝스러운 건 평생 본 적이 없는 것 같다 — 그 순간조차 나는 그게 우습다는 걸 느끼고 있었다."]], [["His enemy, muttering to himself, after wringing his wounded hand for sometime, slowly drew off his neck-kerchief and bound it up; then held it in his other hand, and sat upon his table with his sullen face looking down.", "그의 적은 한참 동안 다친 손을 부여잡고 혼잣말로 중얼거리더니, 천천히 목도리를 풀어 손을 싸맸다. 그러고는 그것을 성한 손에 쥔 채, 뚱한 얼굴을 떨군 채로 책상에 걸터앉았다."]], [["Mr. Micawber, when he was sufficiently cool, proceeded with his letter.", "미코버 씨는 충분히 가라앉자 편지를 계속 읽어 나갔다."]], [["‘“The stipendiary emoluments in consideration of which I entered into the service of--HEEP,”’ always pausing before that word and uttering it with astonishing vigour, ‘“were not defined, beyond the pittance of twenty-two shillings and six per week.", "\"'제가 — 힙의 일을 맡는 대가로 받기로 한 봉급은,'\" 그 단어 앞에서는 늘 한 박자 쉬고, 놀랄 만큼 힘차게 그 말을 내뱉었다. \"'주급 이십이 실링 육 펜스라는 쥐꼬리만 한 푼돈 말고는 정해진 바가 없었습니다."], ["The rest was left contingent on the value of my professional exertions; in other and more expressive words, on the baseness of my nature, the cupidity of my motives, the poverty of my family, the general moral (or rather immoral) resemblance between myself and--HEEP.", "나머지는 제 직무 수행의 가치에 달려 있었습니다. 달리, 더 적나라하게 말하자면, 제 천성의 비열함, 제 동기의 탐욕, 제 가족의 가난, 그리고 저와 — 힙 사이의 전반적인 도덕적(아니, 차라리 부도덕적) 유사성에 달려 있었습니다."], ["Need I say, that it soon became necessary for me to solicit from--HEEP--pecuniary advances towards the support of Mrs. Micawber, and our blighted but rising family?", "미코버 부인과, 시들었으나 자라나는 우리 가족을 부양하기 위해, 제가 곧 — 힙에게 — 돈을 미리 빌려 달라고 간청해야 하는 처지가 되었음을 굳이 말할 필요가 있을까요?"], ["Need I say that this necessity had been foreseen by--HEEP? That those advances were secured by I.O.U.’s and other similar acknowledgements, known to the legal institutions of this country?", "이런 처지가 — 힙에 의해 — 미리 예견된 것이었음을 말할 필요가 있을까요? 그 가불금이 이 나라의 법률 제도가 인정하는 차용증서며 그 밖의 비슷한 증서로 담보되었음을?"], ["And that I thus became immeshed in the web he had spun for my reception?”’", "그리하여 제가, 그가 저를 잡으려고 쳐 둔 거미줄에 얽혀 들었음을?'\""]], [["Mr. Micawber’s enjoyment of his epistolary powers, in describing this unfortunate state of things, really seemed to outweigh any pain or anxiety that the reality could have caused him.", "이 불행한 처지를 묘사하면서 미코버 씨가 자기 서간 솜씨를 즐기는 모양새는, 정작 그 현실이 그에게 안겨 주었을 어떤 고통이나 불안마저 능가하는 듯했다."], ["He read on:", "그는 읽어 나갔다."]], [["‘“Then it was that--HEEP--began to favour me with just so much of his confidence, as was necessary to the discharge of his infernal business.", "\"'바로 그때부터 — 힙은 — 자기의 그 사악한 사업을 처리하는 데 꼭 필요한 만큼만 제게 속내를 털어놓는 호의를 베풀기 시작했습니다."], ["Then it was that I began, if I may so Shakespearianly express myself, to dwindle, peak, and pine.", "바로 그때부터 저는, 셰익스피어식으로 표현하자면, 시들고 야위고 말라 가기 시작했습니다."], ["I found that my services were constantly called into requisition for the falsification of business, and the mystification of an individual whom I will designate as Mr. W.", "제 직무가 끊임없이 동원되는 데가, 사무를 위조하고, 제가 W 씨라 칭하려는 어떤 인물을 미혹하는 일임을 알게 되었습니다."], ["That Mr. W. was imposed upon, kept in ignorance, and deluded, in every possible way; yet, that all this while, the ruffian--HEEP--was professing unbounded gratitude to, and unbounded friendship for, that much-abused gentleman.", "그 W 씨는 온갖 수단으로 기만당하고, 무지 속에 갇히고, 농락당했습니다. 그러면서도 내내, 그 악당 — 힙은 — 그토록 모진 학대를 당한 그 신사에게 한없는 감사와 한없는 우정을 공언하고 있었습니다."], ["This was bad enough; but, as the philosophic Dane observes, with that universal applicability which distinguishes the illustrious ornament of the Elizabethan Era, worse remains behind!”’", "이것만으로도 충분히 고약했지만, 그 철학자다운 덴마크인이, 엘리자베스 시대의 그 빛나는 보배를 돋보이게 하는 보편적 적용성으로써 말했듯, 더 고약한 것이 남아 있습니다!'\""]], [["Mr. Micawber was so very much struck by this happy rounding off with a quotation, that he indulged himself, and us, with a second reading of the sentence, under pretence of having lost his place.", "미코버 씨는 이렇게 인용으로 말끔하게 마무리 지은 것에 어찌나 흡족했는지, 읽던 자리를 놓쳤다는 핑계로, 자신과 우리에게 그 문장을 두 번째로 낭독하는 호사를 누렸다."]], [["‘“It is not my intention,”’ he continued reading on, ‘“to enter on a detailed list, within the compass of the present epistle (though it is ready elsewhere), of the various malpractices of a minor nature, affecting the individual whom I have denominated Mr.", "\"'본 서신의 범위 안에서,'\" 그가 계속 읽어 나갔다. \"'제가 W 씨라 명명한 인물에게 영향을 끼친, 그리고 제가 암묵적으로 동조한 당사자였던 갖가지 소소한 비행을(다른 곳에 준비되어 있긴 합니다만) 상세히 나열할 생각은 없습니다."], ["W., to which I have been a tacitly consenting party. My object, when the contest within myself between stipend and no stipend, baker and no baker, existence and non-existence, ceased, was to take advantage of my opportunities to discover and expose the major malpractices committed, to that gentleman’s grievous wrong and injury, by--HEEP.", "봉급이냐 무봉급이냐, 빵집이냐 무빵집이냐, 생존이냐 비생존이냐 하는 제 안의 갈등이 끝났을 때, 제 목적은, 그 기회를 활용해, — 힙이 — 그 신사에게 끼친 심대한 잘못과 손해를 저지른 중대한 비행을 밝혀내고 폭로하는 것이었습니다."], ["Stimulated by the silent monitor within, and by a no less touching and appealing monitor without--to whom I will briefly refer as Miss W.--I entered on a not unlaborious task of clandestine investigation, protracted--now, to the best of my knowledge, information, and belief, over a period exceeding twelve calendar months.”’", "안에서 조용히 타이르는 충고자와, 그에 못지않게 마음을 울리며 호소하는 바깥의 충고자 — 간략히 W 양이라 칭하겠습니다 — 에게 자극받아, 저는 결코 수월치 않은 은밀한 조사라는 과업에 착수하여, 제가 알고 정보를 입수하고 믿는 바로는, 열두 달이 넘는 기간에 걸쳐 그것을 이어 왔습니다.'\""]], [["He read this passage as if it were from an Act of Parliament; and appeared majestically refreshed by the sound of the words.", "그는 이 대목을 마치 의회 법령이라도 읽듯이 읽었고, 그 말들의 울림에 위풍당당하게 활기를 되찾는 듯했다."]], [["‘“My charges against--HEEP,”’ he read on, glancing at him, and drawing the ruler into a convenient position under his left arm, in case of need, ‘“are as follows.”’", "\"'— 힙에 대한 제 고발은,'\" 그가 만일을 대비해 자를 왼팔 밑 편한 위치로 끌어당기고 그를 흘끗 보며 읽어 나갔다. \"'다음과 같습니다.'\""]], [["We all held our breath, I think.", "우리 모두가 숨을 죽였던 것 같다."], ["I am sure Uriah held his.", "유라이아도 분명 숨을 죽였다."]], [["‘“First,”’ said Mr.", "\"'첫째,'\""], ["Micawber, ‘“When Mr.", "미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["W.’s faculties and memory for business became, through causes into which it is not necessary or expedient for me to enter, weakened and confused,--HEEP--designedly perplexed and complicated the whole of the official transactions.", "\"'W 씨의 사무 능력과 기억력이, 제가 굳이 들어가 따질 필요도 마땅함도 없는 까닭으로 인해, 약해지고 흐려졌을 때, — 힙은 — 모든 공식 거래를 의도적으로 헝클어뜨리고 복잡하게 만들었습니다."], ["When Mr. W. was least fit to enter on business,--HEEP was always at hand to force him to enter on it. He obtained Mr.", "W 씨가 사무에 임하기에 가장 부적합할 때면, — 힙은 — 늘 곁에 붙어 그를 억지로 사무에 임하게 했습니다."], ["W.’s signature under such circumstances to documents of importance, representing them to be other documents of no importance.", "그는 그런 상황에서 W 씨의 서명을 중요한 문서에 받아 냈는데, 그것들을 아무 중요치 않은 다른 문서라고 둘러댔습니다."], ["He induced Mr. W. to empower him to draw out, thus, one particular sum of trust-money, amounting to twelve six fourteen, two and nine, and employed it to meet pretended business charges and deficiencies which were either already provided for, or had never really existed.", "그는 W 씨를 꾀어, 이런 식으로 십이 파운드 육 실링 십사, 이 펜스 구에 이르는 특정 신탁 자금 한 건을 인출할 권한을 자기에게 주도록 했고, 그것을 이미 처리되었거나 실은 존재한 적도 없는 가짜 사무 경비와 결손을 메우는 데 썼습니다."], ["He gave this proceeding, throughout, the appearance of having originated in Mr. W.’s own dishonest intention, and of having been accomplished by Mr. W.’s own dishonest act; and has used it, ever since, to torture and constrain him.”’", "그는 이 모든 과정에, 그것이 W 씨 자신의 부정한 의도에서 비롯되어 W 씨 자신의 부정한 행위로 이루어진 것 같은 외양을 입혔고, 그 이후로 줄곧 그것을 빌미로 그를 괴롭히고 옭아맸습니다.'\""]], [["‘You shall prove this, you Copperfield!’ said Uriah, with a threatening shake of the head.", "\"이걸 증명해 봐, 이 코퍼필드야!\" 유라이아가 머리를 위협적으로 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘All in good time!’", "\"다 때가 되면 알게 될 거야!\""]], [["‘Ask--HEEP--Mr. Traddles, who lived in his house after him,’ said Mr.", "\"— 힙에게 물어보세요, 트래들스 씨, 위크필드 씨 다음에 그 집에 살았던 사람 아닙니까.\""], ["Micawber, breaking off from the letter; ‘will you?’", "미코버 씨가 편지에서 잠시 손을 떼며 말했다. \"물어보시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘The fool himself--and lives there now,’ said Uriah, disdainfully.", "\"그 멍청이가 직접 살았지 — 지금도 거기 살고.\" 유라이아가 깔보듯 말했다."]], [["‘Ask--HEEP--if he ever kept a pocket-book in that house,’ said Mr.", "\"— 힙에게 물어보세요, 그 집에서 수첩을 가지고 있던 적이 있는지.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber; ‘will you?’", "\"물어보시겠습니까?\""]], [["I saw Uriah’s lank hand stop, involuntarily, in the scraping of his chin.", "유라이아의 비쩍 마른 손이 턱을 긁다 말고 저도 모르게 멈추는 것이 보였다."]], [["‘Or ask him,’ said Mr.", "\"아니면 물어보세요.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘if he ever burnt one there.", "\"그가 그 집에서 수첩을 태운 적이 있는지를요."], ["If he says yes, and asks you where the ashes are, refer him to Wilkins Micawber, and he will hear of something not at all to his advantage!’", "만일 그렇다고 하면서 그 재가 어디 있느냐고 묻거든, 윌킨스 미코버에게 알아보라고 하세요. 그러면 자기한테 전혀 이로울 게 없는 소식을 듣게 될 테니까요!\""]], [["The triumphant flourish with which Mr. Micawber delivered himself of these words, had a powerful effect in alarming the mother; who cried out, in much agitation:", "미코버 씨가 이 말을 의기양양하게 멋들어진 손짓으로 내뱉자, 그 어머니가 잔뜩 겁먹는 강력한 효과가 났다. 어머니는 몹시 동요하며 부르짖었다."]], [["‘Ury, Ury! Be umble, and make terms, my dear!’", "\"유리, 유리! 비굴하게 굽혀서 타협하렴, 얘야!\""]], [["‘Mother!’ he retorted, ‘will you keep quiet?", "\"어머니!\" 그가 쏘아붙였다. \"가만히 좀 있을래요?"], ["You’re in a fright, and don’t know what you say or mean.", "겁에 질려서 자기가 무슨 말을, 무슨 뜻으로 하는지도 모르면서. 비굴하게라니!\""], ["Umble!’ he repeated, looking at me, with a snarl; ‘I’ve umbled some of ‘em for a pretty long time back, umble as I was!’", "그가 나를 쳐다보며 으르렁대듯 되풀이했다. \"내가 저들 중 몇몇은 진작부터 한참 동안 비굴하게 굽실거리게 만들어 왔어, 나 자신은 비굴했어도 말이지!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, genteelly adjusting his chin in his cravat, presently proceeded with his composition.", "미코버 씨는 점잖게 턱을 크라바트에 추슬러 넣고는, 곧 자기 작문을 이어 나갔다."]], [["‘“Second.", "\"'둘째."], ["HEEP has, on several occasions, to the best of my knowledge, information, and belief--“’", "힙은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 제가 알고 정보를 입수하고 믿는 바로는—'\""]], [["‘But that won’t do,’ muttered Uriah, relieved.", "\"하지만 그건 안 통할 거야.\" 유라이아가 안도하며 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Mother, you keep quiet.’", "\"어머니, 가만히 좀 있어요.\""]], [["‘We will endeavour to provide something that WILL do, and do for you finally, sir, very shortly,’ replied Mr. Micawber.", "\"통하고도, 끝내 당신을 끝장낼 무언가를 마련하도록 애써 보지요, 선생, 머지않아서요.\" 미코버 씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘“Second.", "\"'둘째."], ["HEEP has, on several occasions, to the best of my knowledge, information, and belief, systematically forged, to various entries, books, and documents, the signature of Mr.", "힙은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 제가 알고 정보를 입수하고 믿는 바로는, 여러 장부 기재와 책자와 문서에 W 씨의 서명을 조직적으로 위조했습니다."], ["W.; and has distinctly done so in one instance, capable of proof by me.", "그리고 한 건에 있어서는 명백히 그리하였으며, 그것은 제가 증명할 수 있습니다."], ["To wit, in manner following, that is to say:”’", "즉, 다음과 같은 방식으로, 다시 말하면 이러합니다.'\""]], [["Again, Mr. Micawber had a relish in this formal piling up of words, which, however ludicrously displayed in his case, was, I must say, not at all peculiar to him.", "또다시 미코버 씨는 이렇게 말을 격식 있게 켜켜이 쌓아 올리는 데서 감칠맛을 느꼈는데, 그것은 그의 경우엔 우스꽝스럽게 드러났을망정, 말하건대 결코 그만의 별난 버릇은 아니었다."], ["I have observed it, in the course of my life, in numbers of men.", "나는 살아오면서 숱한 사람들에게서 그것을 보아 왔다."], ["It seems to me to be a general rule.", "그건 일반적인 법칙인 듯싶다."], ["In the taking of legal oaths, for instance, deponents seem to enjoy themselves mightily when they come to several good words in succession, for the expression of one idea; as, that they utterly detest, abominate, and abjure, or so forth; and the old anathemas were made relishing on the same principle.", "이를테면 법정 선서를 할 때, 진술인들은 하나의 뜻을 표현하려고 멋진 말 여러 개를 잇달아 늘어놓는 대목에 이르면 무척이나 즐거워하는 것 같다. 자기는 무엇무엇을 철저히 혐오하고, 증오하고, 배격한다는 식으로 말이다. 옛날의 저주문들도 같은 원리로 감칠맛이 났다."], ["We talk about the tyranny of words, but we like to tyrannize over them too; we are fond of having a large superfluous establishment of words to wait upon us on great occasions; we think it looks important, and sounds well.", "우리는 말의 횡포를 운운하지만, 우리 자신도 말을 횡포하게 부려 먹기를 좋아한다. 우리는 큰 행사가 있을 때 우리 시중을 들 군더더기 말들을 잔뜩 거느리기를 즐긴다. 그래야 중해 보이고 듣기에도 그럴싸하다고 여기는 것이다."], ["As we are not particular about the meaning of our liveries on state occasions, if they be but fine and numerous enough, so, the meaning or necessity of our words is a secondary consideration, if there be but a great parade of them.", "우리가 의전 행사 때 입는 제복의 의미에 그리 까다롭지 않고 그저 화려하고 수만 많으면 되듯이, 말의 의미나 필요성도, 그 말이 그저 거창하게 줄지어 늘어서기만 한다면, 부차적인 고려 사항일 뿐이다."], ["And as individuals get into trouble by making too great a show of liveries, or as slaves when they are too numerous rise against their masters, so I think I could mention a nation that has got into many great difficulties, and will get into many greater, from maintaining too large a retinue of words.", "그리고 개인이 제복을 너무 거창하게 차려입어 곤경에 빠지듯, 또는 노예가 수가 너무 많아지면 주인에게 들고일어나듯, 너무 많은 말의 종자(從者)를 거느린 탓에 숱한 큰 곤경에 빠졌고 또 앞으로 더 큰 곤경에 빠질 어느 나라를 나는 댈 수 있을 것 같다."]], [["Mr. Micawber read on, almost smacking his lips:", "미코버 씨는 거의 입맛을 다시며 읽어 나갔다."]], [["‘“To wit, in manner following, that is to say. Mr. W.", "\"'즉, 다음과 같은 방식으로, 다시 말하면 이러합니다."], ["being infirm, and it being within the bounds of probability that his decease might lead to some discoveries, and to the downfall of--HEEP’S--power over the W. family,--as I, Wilkins Micawber, the undersigned, assume--unless the filial affection of his daughter could be secretly influenced from allowing any investigation of the partnership affairs to be ever made, the said--HEEP--deemed it expedient to have a bond ready by him, as from Mr.", "W 씨가 병약하여, 그의 사망이 어떤 사실의 발각으로 이어지고, W 가문에 대한 — 힙의 — 지배력의 몰락으로 이어질 개연성의 범위 안에 있었으므로 — 즉, 저 윌킨스 미코버, 아래에 서명한 본인이 추정하건대 — 그의 딸의 효성을 은밀히 부추겨 동업 사무에 대한 일체의 조사가 영영 이뤄지지 못하게 막지 않는 한 — 상기 — 힙은 — W 씨가 발행한 것처럼 꾸민 차용 증서 한 장을, 앞서 언급한 십이 파운드 육 실링 십사, 이 펜스 구에 이자를 더한 금액으로, 곁에 마련해 두는 것이 상책이라 여겼습니다."], ["W., for the before-mentioned sum of twelve six fourteen, two and nine, with interest, stated therein to have been advanced by--HEEP--to Mr. W. to save Mr. W.", "그 증서에는 그 돈이 W 씨의 불명예를 막기 위해 — 힙이 — W 씨에게 빌려준 것이라 적혀 있으나, 실은 그 돈을 그가 빌려준 적이 결코 없으며, 게다가 진작에 채워 넣어진 것입니다."], ["from dishonour; though really the sum was never advanced by him, and has long been replaced. The signatures to this instrument purporting to be executed by Mr. W.", "이 증서에 W 씨가 작성하고 윌킨스 미코버가 입회 서명한 것처럼 되어 있는 서명들은 — 힙이 — 위조한 것입니다."], ["and attested by Wilkins Micawber, are forgeries by--HEEP. I have, in my possession, in his hand and pocket-book, several similar imitations of Mr.", "저는 그의 필체와 수첩에서, W 씨 서명을 비슷하게 흉내 낸 것 여러 점을 제 손에 가지고 있는데, 여기저기 불에 그슬렸으나 누구든 알아볼 수 있을 정도입니다."], ["W.’s signature, here and there defaced by fire, but legible to anyone. I never attested any such document.", "저는 그러한 문서에 입회 서명한 적이 결코 없습니다. 그리고 저는 그 증서 자체를 제 손에 가지고 있습니다.'\""], ["And I have the document itself, in my possession.”’ Uriah Heep, with a start, took out of his pocket a bunch of keys, and opened a certain drawer; then, suddenly bethought himself of what he was about, and turned again towards us, without looking in it.", "유라이아 힙이 흠칫하며 주머니에서 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내 어느 서랍을 열다가, 별안간 자기가 뭘 하고 있는지 깨닫고는, 들여다보지도 않은 채 다시 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["‘“And I have the document,”’ Mr.", "\"'그리고 저는 그 증서를,'\""], ["Micawber read again, looking about as if it were the text of a sermon, ‘“in my possession,--that is to say, I had, early this morning, when this was written, but have since relinquished it to Mr. Traddles.”’", "미코버 씨가 마치 설교의 본문이라도 되는 양 두리번거리며 다시 읽었다. \"'제 손에 가지고 있습니다 — 그러니까, 이 글을 쓴 오늘 이른 아침까지는 가지고 있었으나, 그 후 트래들스 씨에게 넘겼습니다.'\""]], [["‘It is quite true,’ assented Traddles.", "\"틀림없는 사실입니다.\" 트래들스가 동의했다."]], [["‘Ury, Ury!’ cried the mother, ‘be umble and make terms.", "\"유리, 유리!\" 어머니가 외쳤다. \"비굴하게 굽혀서 타협하렴."], ["I know my son will be umble, gentlemen, if you’ll give him time to think. Mr.", "우리 아들은 분명 비굴하게 굽힐 거예요, 여러분, 생각할 시간만 주신다면요."], ["Copperfield, I’m sure you know that he was always very umble, sir!’", "코퍼필드 씨, 저 애가 늘 아주 비굴했다는 거, 분명 아시잖아요, 선생님!\""]], [["It was singular to see how the mother still held to the old trick, when the son had abandoned it as useless.", "아들은 쓸모없다고 내던진 그 낡은 수법에, 어머니는 여전히 매달리고 있었으니, 참으로 묘한 일이었다."]], [["‘Mother,’ he said, with an impatient bite at the handkerchief in which his hand was wrapped, ‘you had better take and fire a loaded gun at me.’", "\"어머니.\" 그가 손을 싸맨 손수건을 짜증스럽게 한 번 깨물며 말했다. \"차라리 장전한 총이나 가져다 나한테 쏘는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["‘But I love you, Ury,’ cried Mrs. Heep.", "\"하지만 난 널 사랑한단다, 유리.\" 힙 부인이 외쳤다."], ["And I have no doubt she did; or that he loved her, however strange it may appear; though, to be sure, they were a congenial couple.", "나는 그녀가 정말로 그를 사랑했음을, 또 아무리 이상해 보일지언정 그가 그녀를 사랑했음을 의심치 않는다. 하긴 둘은 천생연분인 한 쌍이긴 했다."], ["‘And I can’t bear to hear you provoking the gentlemen, and endangering of yourself more.", "\"그리고 난 네가 신사분들을 자극하면서 더 큰 위험에 빠지는 걸 차마 못 듣겠구나."], ["I told the gentleman at first, when he told me upstairs it was come to light, that I would answer for your being umble, and making amends.", "저 신사분이 위층에서 일이 들통났다고 말해 줬을 때, 내가 처음부터 그랬어. 네가 비굴하게 굽혀서 잘못을 바로잡을 거라고 내가 보증한다고."], ["Oh, see how umble I am, gentlemen, and don’t mind him!’", "아, 보세요, 제가 이렇게 비굴하잖아요, 여러분, 저 애 말은 신경 쓰지 마세요!\""]], [["‘Why, there’s Copperfield, mother,’ he angrily retorted, pointing his lean finger at me, against whom all his animosity was levelled, as the prime mover in the discovery; and I did not undeceive him; ‘there’s Copperfield, would have given you a hundred pound to say less than you’ve blurted out!’", "\"아니, 저기 코퍼필드 있잖아요, 어머니.\" 그가 자기의 모든 적의가 향한 나에게, 이 발각의 주모자인 나에게 비쩍 마른 손가락을 겨누며 성나게 되받았다. 나는 그 오해를 굳이 풀어 주지 않았다. \"저기 코퍼필드 말이에요, 어머니가 함부로 지껄인 것보다 말을 좀 줄이라고 어머니한테 백 파운드라도 줬을 사람이라고요!\""]], [["‘I can’t help it, Ury,’ cried his mother.", "\"어쩔 수 없잖니, 유리.\" 어머니가 외쳤다."], ["‘I can’t see you running into danger, through carrying your head so high.", "\"네가 그렇게 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 위험 속으로 달려드는 꼴을 두고 볼 수가 없구나."], ["Better be umble, as you always was.’", "늘 그래 왔듯이 비굴하게 굽히는 게 낫지.\""]], [["He remained for a little, biting the handkerchief, and then said to me with a scowl:", "그는 잠시 동안 손수건을 깨물고 있다가, 나를 향해 인상을 쓰며 말했다."]], [["‘What more have you got to bring forward?", "\"더 내놓을 게 뭐가 있어?"], ["If anything, go on with it.", "있으면 어디 계속해 봐."], ["What do you look at me for?’", "왜 날 쳐다보는 거야?\""]], [["Mr. Micawber promptly resumed his letter, glad to revert to a performance with which he was so highly satisfied.", "미코버 씨는 곧장 편지를 다시 집어 들었다. 그토록 흡족해하던 공연으로 되돌아가게 되어 반가운 기색이었다."]], [["‘“Third. And last.", "\"'셋째."], ["I am now in a condition to show, by--HEEP’S--false books, and--HEEP’S--real memoranda, beginning with the partially destroyed pocket-book (which I was unable to comprehend, at the time of its accidental discovery by Mrs. Micawber, on our taking possession of our present abode, in the locker or bin devoted to the reception of the ashes calcined on our domestic hearth), that the weaknesses, the faults, the very virtues, the parental affections, and the sense of honour, of the unhappy Mr. W.", "그리고 마지막. 저는 이제, — 힙의 — 거짓 장부와 — 힙의 — 진짜 비망록을 통해, 그것도 일부 훼손된 그 수첩(미코버 부인이 우리가 현재의 거처를 차지하면서, 집 안 난로에서 태운 재를 받는 통, 즉 함에서 우연히 발견했을 당시에는 제가 그 내용을 이해할 수 없었습니다)으로 시작하여, 다음을 증명할 수 있는 처지에 있습니다."], ["have been for years acted on by, and warped to the base purposes of--HEEP. That Mr. W. has been for years deluded and plundered, in every conceivable manner, to the pecuniary aggrandisement of the avaricious, false, and grasping--HEEP.", "즉, 불행한 W 씨의 약점과 결점과, 심지어 미덕과, 부모로서의 애정과, 명예심까지가, 수년에 걸쳐 — 힙에 의해 — 농락당하고 그의 비열한 목적에 맞게 비틀렸다는 것을."], ["That the engrossing object of--HEEP--was, next to gain, to subdue Mr. and Miss W. (of his ulterior views in reference to the latter I say nothing) entirely to himself.", "W 씨가 수년에 걸쳐, 상상할 수 있는 온갖 방식으로 기만당하고 약탈당하여, 그 탐욕스럽고 거짓되고 게걸스러운 — 힙의 — 금전적 치부에 이바지했다는 것을."], ["That his last act, completed but a few months since, was to induce Mr. W. to execute a relinquishment of his share in the partnership, and even a bill of sale on the very furniture of his house, in consideration of a certain annuity, to be well and truly paid by--HEEP--on the four common quarter-days in each and every year.", "— 힙의 — 골몰한 목적이, 이득 다음으로, W 씨와 W 양을(후자에 관한 그의 더 음흉한 속셈에 대해서는 아무 말도 않겠습니다) 완전히 자기 손아귀에 넣는 것이었다는 것을. 불과 몇 달 전에 완수된 그의 마지막 행각이, W 씨를 꾀어 동업 지분 포기 증서를, 심지어 자기 집 가구 자체에 대한 매도 증서까지 작성하게 한 것이었다는 것을."], ["That these meshes; beginning with alarming and falsified accounts of the estate of which Mr. W. is the receiver, at a period when Mr. W.", "— 힙이 — 매년 사분기 결산일 네 차례에 어김없이 또박또박 지급하기로 한 일정 연금을 대가로 말입니다."], ["had launched into imprudent and ill-judged speculations, and may not have had the money, for which he was morally and legally responsible, in hand; going on with pretended borrowings of money at enormous interest, really coming from--HEEP--and by--HEEP--fraudulently obtained or withheld from Mr. W. himself, on pretence of such speculations or otherwise; perpetuated by a miscellaneous catalogue of unscrupulous chicaneries--gradually thickened, until the unhappy Mr. W. could see no world beyond.", "이 그물이 — W 씨가 관리인으로 있는 재산에 관한, 그가 무모하고 그릇된 투기에 손을 대어 도덕적으로나 법적으로나 책임져야 할 돈이 수중에 없었을지도 모를 시기의, 놀라우면서도 거짓으로 꾸며진 회계 보고에서 시작하여 — 실은 — 힙에게서 — 나온 돈을, 또는 그러한 투기 따위를 핑계로 — 힙이 — 사기로 가로채거나 W 씨 본인에게서 떼어먹은 돈을, 엄청난 이자를 붙여 빌려주는 척하는 행위로 이어지고 — 양심 없는 온갖 협잡의 잡다한 목록으로 영속화되어 — 점점 두꺼워진 끝에, 불행한 W 씨는 그 너머의 세상을 볼 수 없게 되었습니다."], ["Bankrupt, as he believed, alike in circumstances, in all other hope, and in honour, his sole reliance was upon the monster in the garb of man,”’--Mr.", "처지로 보나, 다른 모든 희망으로 보나, 명예로 보나, 스스로 믿기에 파산한 그가, 유일하게 의지한 것은 사람의 탈을 쓴 괴물이었으니,'\""], ["Micawber made a good deal of this, as a new turn of expression,--‘“who, by making himself necessary to him, had achieved his destruction.", "— 미코버 씨는 이 새로운 표현의 묘미를 한껏 살렸다 — \"'그자는 자기를 그에게 없어서는 안 될 존재로 만들어, 그의 파멸을 이룬 것입니다."], ["All this I undertake to show. Probably much more!”’", "이 모든 것을 제가 증명하겠습니다. 어쩌면 훨씬 더 많은 것을요!'\""]], [["I whispered a few words to Agnes, who was weeping, half joyfully, half sorrowfully, at my side; and there was a movement among us, as if Mr. Micawber had finished.", "나는 내 곁에서 반은 기쁨에, 반은 슬픔에 잠겨 울고 있던 아그네스에게 몇 마디를 속삭였다. 미코버 씨가 다 끝낸 줄 알고, 우리 사이에 무언가 움직임이 일었다."], ["He said, with exceeding gravity, ‘Pardon me,’ and proceeded, with a mixture of the lowest spirits and the most intense enjoyment, to the peroration of his letter.", "그러자 그가 더없이 엄숙하게 \"양해해 주십시오.\" 하고는, 더없이 가라앉은 기분과 더없이 강렬한 즐거움이 뒤섞인 채로, 편지의 맺음말로 나아갔다."]], [["‘“I have now concluded.", "\"'이제 저는 글을 맺습니다."], ["It merely remains for me to substantiate these accusations; and then, with my ill-starred family, to disappear from the landscape on which we appear to be an encumbrance. That is soon done.", "제게 남은 일이라곤 이 고발들을 입증하는 것, 그러고는 박복한 제 식구와 더불어, 우리가 한낱 짐이 되는 듯한 이 풍경에서 사라지는 것뿐입니다. 그건 곧 이루어질 일입니다."], ["It may be reasonably inferred that our baby will first expire of inanition, as being the frailest member of our circle; and that our twins will follow next in order. So be it!", "우리 갓난아기가, 우리 식구 가운데 가장 연약한 일원인 만큼, 먼저 영양실조로 숨질 것이고, 다음 차례로 우리 쌍둥이가 뒤따르리라고 어렵잖이 짐작할 수 있습니다. 그렇게 되라죠!"], ["For myself, my Canterbury Pilgrimage has done much; imprisonment on civil process, and want, will soon do more.", "저로 말하자면, 저의 캔터베리 순례가 이미 많은 일을 했고, 민사 소송에 따른 투옥과 궁핍이 머지않아 더 많은 일을 할 것입니다."], ["I trust that the labour and hazard of an investigation--of which the smallest results have been slowly pieced together, in the pressure of arduous avocations, under grinding penurious apprehensions, at rise of morn, at dewy eve, in the shadows of night, under the watchful eye of one whom it were superfluous to call Demon--combined with the struggle of parental Poverty to turn it, when completed, to the right account, may be as the sprinkling of a few drops of sweet water on my funeral pyre. I ask no more.", "부디, 이 조사의 — 더없이 보잘것없는 성과들을, 고된 생업의 압박 속에, 사람을 갉아먹는 곤궁한 두려움 아래, 동틀 녘에, 이슬 맺힌 저녁에, 밤의 그늘 속에서, 굳이 악마라 부를 것도 없는 자의 감시하는 눈길 아래, 더디게나마 짜 맞춰 온 그 노고와 위험이 — 그것이 완성되었을 때 그것을 옳은 쓸모로 돌리려는 부모 된 가난의 분투와 어우러져 — 제 화장용 장작더미에 달콤한 물 몇 방울을 뿌려 주는 격이 되기를 바랍니다. 저는 그 이상은 바라지 않습니다."], ["Let it be, in justice, merely said of me, as of a gallant and eminent naval Hero, with whom I have no pretensions to cope, that what I have done, I did, in despite of mercenary and selfish objects,", "부디, 제가 감히 견줄 엄두도 못 낼 어느 용감하고 걸출한 해군 영웅에 대해 그러했듯이, 저에 대해서도 다만 공정하게, 제가 한 일은, 돈벌이나 사사로운 목적을 마다하고 했노라고 말해 주시기를 바랄 뿐입니다."]], [["For England, home, and Beauty.", "영국과 가정과 미녀를 위하여."]], [["‘“Remaining always, &c. &c., WILKINS MICAWBER.”’", "언제나 변함없이, 운운, 운운, 윌킨스 미코버.'\""]], [["Much affected, but still intensely enjoying himself, Mr. Micawber folded up his letter, and handed it with a bow to my aunt, as something she might like to keep.", "미코버 씨는 몹시 감격하면서도 여전히 강렬하게 자기 흥에 겨워, 편지를 접어, 고모님이 간직하고 싶어 할 만한 물건이라는 듯 절을 하며 건넸다."]], [["There was, as I had noticed on my first visit long ago, an iron safe in the room. The key was in it.", "오래전 처음 방문했을 때 보았듯이, 그 방에는 철제 금고가 하나 있었다. 열쇠는 거기 꽂혀 있었다."], ["A hasty suspicion seemed to strike Uriah; and, with a glance at Mr. Micawber, he went to it, and threw the doors clanking open.", "어떤 다급한 의심이 유라이아를 후려친 듯했다. 그는 미코버 씨를 한 번 흘끗 보더니, 그쪽으로 가서 철컹 소리가 나게 문을 와락 열어젖혔다."], ["It was empty.", "텅 비어 있었다."]], [["‘Where are the books?’ he cried, with a frightful face.", "\"장부들 어디 갔어?\" 그가 끔찍한 얼굴로 외쳤다."], ["‘Some thief has stolen the books!’", "\"어떤 도둑놈이 장부를 훔쳐 갔어!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber tapped himself with the ruler.", "미코버 씨가 자로 자기 몸을 톡톡 쳤다."], ["‘I did, when I got the key from you as usual--but a little earlier--and opened it this morning.’", "\"내가 가져갔지, 늘 하던 대로 네게서 열쇠를 받았을 때 — 다만 조금 일찍 받아서 — 오늘 아침에 열었지.\""]], [["‘Don’t be uneasy,’ said Traddles.", "\"불안해할 것 없습니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘They have come into my possession.", "\"그것들은 제 손에 들어왔습니다."], ["I will take care of them, under the authority I mentioned.’", "제가 말씀드린 권한에 따라 잘 보관하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You receive stolen goods, do you?’ cried Uriah.", "\"장물을 받아 챙기는 거야, 어?\" 유라이아가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Under such circumstances,’ answered Traddles, ‘yes.’", "\"이런 상황에서는,\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["What was my astonishment when I beheld my aunt, who had been profoundly quiet and attentive, make a dart at Uriah Heep, and seize him by the collar with both hands!", "그토록 깊이 침묵하며 주의 깊게 지켜보던 고모님이, 유라이아 힙에게 와락 달려들어 두 손으로 그의 멱살을 움켜쥐는 것을 보았을 때, 내 놀라움이 어떠했겠는가!"]], [["‘You know what I want?’ said my aunt.", "\"내가 뭘 원하는지 알지?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘A strait-waistcoat,’ said he.", "\"구속복이겠지요.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘No. My property!’ returned my aunt.", "* 구속복: 정신 이상자나 난폭한 사람이 팔을 못 쓰게 묶는 옷. \"아니."], ["‘Agnes, my dear, as long as I believed it had been really made away with by your father, I wouldn’t--and, my dear, I didn’t, even to Trot, as he knows--breathe a syllable of its having been placed here for investment.", "내 재산이오!\" 고모님이 되받았다. \"아그네스야, 얘야, 그게 정말로 네 아버지 손에 날아간 줄로 믿고 있는 동안에는, 나는 그것이 투자용으로 여기 맡겨졌다는 걸 한마디도 — 얘야, 트롯에게조차 한마디도 안 했다, 저 애도 알다시피 — 입 밖에 내지 않았다."], ["But, now I know this fellow’s answerable for it, and I’ll have it!", "하지만 이제 이 작자한테 그 책임이 있다는 걸 알았으니, 그걸 받아 내고 말겠다!"], ["Trot, come and take it away from him!’", "트롯, 이리 와서 저자한테서 그걸 빼앗아라!\""]], [["Whether my aunt supposed, for the moment, that he kept her property in his neck-kerchief, I am sure I don’t know; but she certainly pulled at it as if she thought so.", "고모님이 그 순간, 그가 자기 재산을 목도리 속에 감춰 두기라도 한 줄로 여겼는지는 정말이지 알 수 없으나, 어쨌든 정말 그런 줄 아는 사람처럼 그것을 잡아당긴 것만은 분명했다."], ["I hastened to put myself between them, and to assure her that we would all take care that he should make the utmost restitution of everything he had wrongly got.", "나는 얼른 둘 사이에 끼어들어, 그가 부당하게 손에 넣은 모든 것을 한 푼도 빠짐없이 토해 내도록 우리 모두가 단단히 챙기겠노라고 고모님을 안심시켰다."], ["This, and a few moments’ reflection, pacified her; but she was not at all disconcerted by what she had done (though I cannot say as much for her bonnet) and resumed her seat composedly.", "이 말과, 잠깐의 생각 끝에 고모님은 진정되었다. 그러나 고모님은 자기가 한 일에 조금도 당황하지 않았고(보닛에 대해서는 그렇게 말 못 하겠지만), 침착하게 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["During the last few minutes, Mrs. Heep had been clamouring to her son to be ‘umble’; and had been going down on her knees to all of us in succession, and making the wildest promises.", "지난 몇 분 동안, 힙 부인은 아들에게 \"비굴하게 굽히라\"고 아우성쳤고, 우리 모두에게 차례차례 무릎을 꿇으며 더없이 터무니없는 약속들을 늘어놓고 있었다."], ["Her son sat her down in his chair; and, standing sulkily by her, holding her arm with his hand, but not rudely, said to me, with a ferocious look:", "아들은 어머니를 자기 의자에 앉히고, 곁에 뚱하니 서서, 거칠지는 않게 한 손으로 어머니 팔을 붙든 채, 험악한 눈초리로 나에게 말했다."]], [["‘What do you want done?’", "\"뭘 어떻게 했으면 좋겠어?\""]], [["‘I will tell you what must be done,’ said Traddles.", "\"무엇을 해야 하는지 내가 말해 주지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Has that Copperfield no tongue?’ muttered Uriah, ‘I would do a good deal for you if you could tell me, without lying, that somebody had cut it out.’", "\"저 코퍼필드는 혀가 없나?\" 유라이아가 중얼거렸다. \"누가 저놈 혀를 잘라 갔다고 거짓말 안 하고 말해 줄 수만 있다면, 내가 당신을 위해 어지간한 건 다 해 주겠는데.\""]], [["‘My Uriah means to be umble!’ cried his mother.", "\"우리 유라이아는 비굴하게 굽힐 작정이에요!\" 어머니가 외쳤다."], ["‘Don’t mind what he says, good gentlemen!’", "\"저 애 말은 신경 쓰지 마세요, 친절하신 여러분!\""]], [["‘What must be done,’ said Traddles, ‘is this.", "\"해야 할 것은,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"이것입니다."], ["First, the deed of relinquishment, that we have heard of, must be given over to me now--here.’", "첫째, 우리가 들은 그 지분 포기 증서를, 지금 — 여기서 — 나에게 넘겨야 합니다.\""]], [["‘Suppose I haven’t got it,’ he interrupted.", "\"내가 그걸 안 가지고 있다면?\" 그가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘But you have,’ said Traddles; ‘therefore, you know, we won’t suppose so.’ And I cannot help avowing that this was the first occasion on which I really did justice to the clear head, and the plain, patient, practical good sense, of my old schoolfellow.", "\"하지만 당신은 가지고 있지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그러니, 알다시피, 그렇게 가정하진 않겠습니다.\""], ["‘Then,’ said Traddles, ‘you must prepare to disgorge all that your rapacity has become possessed of, and to make restoration to the last farthing.", "그리고 내 옛 학교 친구의 명석한 머리와, 꾸밈없고 끈기 있고 실속 있는 양식(良識)을 내가 진정으로 제대로 평가한 건 이때가 처음이었음을 고백하지 않을 수 없다. \"그다음,\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["All the partnership books and papers must remain in our possession; all your books and papers; all money accounts and securities, of both kinds.", "\"당신은 그 탐욕으로 손에 넣은 모든 것을 토해 내고, 마지막 한 푼까지 되돌려 줄 채비를 해야 합니다. 모든 동업 장부와 서류는 우리 손에 남겨 둬야 하고, 당신의 모든 장부와 서류, 두 종류의 모든 금전 계정과 증권도 마찬가지입니다."], ["In short, everything here.’", "한마디로, 여기 있는 모든 것을요.\""]], [["‘Must it? I don’t know that,’ said Uriah.", "\"꼭 그래야 하나? 난 그건 모르겠는데.\""], ["‘I must have time to think about that.’", "유라이아가 말했다. \"그건 생각할 시간이 좀 필요해.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ replied Traddles; ‘but, in the meanwhile, and until everything is done to our satisfaction, we shall maintain possession of these things; and beg you--in short, compel you--to keep to your own room, and hold no communication with anyone.’", "\"물론입니다.\" 트래들스가 대답했다. \"하지만 그동안, 모든 것이 우리가 만족할 만큼 처리될 때까지, 우리는 이 물건들을 우리 손에 두겠습니다. 그리고 당신에게 청하건대 — 한마디로, 강제하건대 — 당신 방에만 머물며 누구와도 연락하지 마십시오.\""]], [["‘I won’t do it!’ said Uriah, with an oath.", "\"안 그럴 거야!\" 유라이아가 욕설을 내뱉으며 말했다."]], [["‘Maidstone jail is a safer place of detention,’ observed Traddles; ‘and though the law may be longer in righting us, and may not be able to right us so completely as you can, there is no doubt of its punishing YOU.", "\"메이드스톤 감옥이 더 안전한 구금 장소지요.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그리고 법이 우리의 권리를 바로잡는 데는 더 오래 걸릴지도 모르고, 당신만큼 완벽하게 바로잡아 주지 못할지도 모르지만, 당신을 벌하리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없습니다."], ["Dear me, you know that quite as well as I!", "어허, 그건 당신도 나만큼 잘 알지 않습니까!"], ["Copperfield, will you go round to the Guildhall, and bring a couple of officers?’", "코퍼필드, 길드홀로 가서 관리 두 사람만 데려와 주겠나?\""]], [["Here, Mrs. Heep broke out again, crying on her knees to Agnes to interfere in their behalf, exclaiming that he was very humble, and it was all true, and if he didn’t do what we wanted, she would, and much more to the same purpose; being half frantic with fears for her darling.", "여기서 힙 부인이 다시 터져 나와, 무릎을 꿇고 아그네스에게 자기네 편을 들어 달라고 울며 매달렸다. 아들은 아주 비굴하고, 다 사실이며, 아들이 우리가 원하는 대로 하지 않으면 자기가 하겠다는 둥, 같은 취지의 말을 잔뜩 외쳐 댔다."], ["To inquire what he might have done, if he had had any boldness, would be like inquiring what a mongrel cur might do, if it had the spirit of a tiger.", "사랑하는 아들에 대한 두려움으로 반쯤 제정신이 아니었던 것이다. 그에게 조금이라도 배짱이 있었다면 무슨 짓을 했을까 묻는 것은, 똥개에게 호랑이의 기상이 있었다면 무슨 짓을 했을까 묻는 것과 같으리라."], ["He was a coward, from head to foot; and showed his dastardly nature through his sullenness and mortification, as much as at any time of his mean life.", "그는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 겁쟁이였다. 그리고 그 비루한 인생의 어느 때 못지않게, 뚱한 표정과 굴욕감을 통해 자신의 비겁한 본성을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Stop!’ he growled to me; and wiped his hot face with his hand.", "\"그만!\" 그가 나에게 으르렁대며, 화끈거리는 얼굴을 손으로 닦았다."], ["‘Mother, hold your noise. Well!", "\"어머니, 시끄러운 소리 좀 그쳐요. 좋아!"], ["Let ‘em have that deed.", "저 증서 가져가라고 해."], ["Go and fetch it!’", "가서 가져와요!\""]], [["‘Do you help her, Mr.", "\"부인을 좀 도와드리시지요, 디크 씨.\""], ["Dick,’ said Traddles, ‘if you please.’", "트래들스가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["Proud of his commission, and understanding it, Mr. Dick accompanied her as a shepherd’s dog might accompany a sheep.", "자기 소임을 자랑스러워하며, 또 그것을 잘 알아듣고서, 디크 씨는 양치기 개가 양을 따라가듯 그녀를 따라갔다."], ["But, Mrs. Heep gave him little trouble; for she not only returned with the deed, but with the box in which it was, where we found a banker’s book and some other papers that were afterwards serviceable.", "하지만 힙 부인은 그를 별로 애먹이지 않았으니, 증서를 가지고 돌아왔을 뿐 아니라, 그것이 든 상자까지 가져왔는데, 거기서 우리는 은행 통장과, 나중에 쓸모 있게 된 다른 서류 몇 가지를 발견했다."]], [["‘Good!’ said Traddles, when this was brought. ‘Now, Mr.", "\"좋습니다!\" 이것이 들어오자 트래들스가 말했다."], ["Heep, you can retire to think: particularly observing, if you please, that I declare to you, on the part of all present, that there is only one thing to be done; that it is what I have explained; and that it must be done without delay.’", "\"자, 힙 씨, 이제 방으로 물러가서 생각하셔도 됩니다. 특히, 부디 잘 새겨 두세요. 여기 있는 모두를 대신하여 내가 당신에게 선언하건대, 해야 할 일은 단 한 가지뿐이고, 그것은 내가 설명한 그대로이며, 지체 없이 이뤄져야 한다는 것을요.\""]], [["Uriah, without lifting his eyes from the ground, shuffled across the room with his hand to his chin, and pausing at the door, said:", "유라이아는 땅바닥에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 손을 턱에 댄 채로 방을 가로질러 발을 질질 끌며 가다가, 문 앞에서 멈춰 서서 말했다."]], [["‘Copperfield, I have always hated you.", "\"코퍼필드, 난 늘 널 미워해 왔어."], ["You’ve always been an upstart, and you’ve always been against me.’", "넌 늘 벼락출세한 놈이었고, 늘 나한테 맞서 왔지.\""]], [["‘As I think I told you once before,’ said I, ‘it is you who have been, in your greed and cunning, against all the world.", "\"전에도 한 번 말했던 것 같은데,\" 내가 말했다. \"탐욕과 교활함으로 온 세상에 맞서 온 건 바로 너야."], ["It may be profitable to you to reflect, in future, that there never were greed and cunning in the world yet, that did not do too much, and overreach themselves.", "앞으로 이걸 곰곰이 새겨 두면 너한테 득이 될지도 모르지. 도가 지나쳐 제 꾀에 제가 넘어가지 않은 탐욕과 교활함은 이 세상에 일찍이 없었다는 걸 말이야."], ["It is as certain as death.’", "그건 죽음만큼이나 확실한 일이지.\""]], [["‘Or as certain as they used to teach at school (the same school where I picked up so much umbleness), from nine o’clock to eleven, that labour was a curse; and from eleven o’clock to one, that it was a blessing and a cheerfulness, and a dignity, and I don’t know what all, eh?’ said he with a sneer.", "\"아니면, 학교에서(내가 그 숱한 비굴함을 주워 배운 바로 그 학교지) 가르치던 것만큼이나 확실하겠지. 아홉 시부터 열한 시까진 노동이 저주라고 가르치고, 열한 시부터 한 시까진 그게 축복이고 즐거움이고 위엄이고 뭐고 다 된다고 가르치던 거 말이야, 어?\" 그가 비웃으며 말했다."], ["‘You preach, about as consistent as they did.", "\"넌 그자들만큼이나 앞뒤 안 맞게 설교를 늘어놓는군."], ["Won’t umbleness go down?", "비굴함이 안 통한다고?"], ["I shouldn’t have got round my gentleman fellow-partner without it, I think.", "내 생각엔, 그게 없었으면 나의 그 신사 동업자를 구워삶지 못했을걸."], ["--Micawber, you old bully, I’ll pay YOU!’", "— 미코버, 이 늙은 깡패야, 너한텐 꼭 갚아 주마!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, supremely defiant of him and his extended finger, and making a great deal of his chest until he had slunk out at the door, then addressed himself to me, and proffered me the satisfaction of ‘witnessing the re-establishment of mutual confidence between himself and Mrs.", "미코버 씨는 그와 그의 쭉 뻗은 손가락 따위 더없이 당당하게 무시하며, 그가 문밖으로 슬그머니 빠져나갈 때까지 가슴팍을 한껏 부풀려 보이고는, 나에게로 몸을 돌려, \"자기와 미코버 부인 사이에 상호 신뢰가 회복되는 것을 목격하는\" 만족을 베풀어 주겠노라고 했다."], ["Micawber’. After which, he invited the company generally to the contemplation of that affecting spectacle.", "그러고 나서 그는 좌중 전체에게, 그 가슴 뭉클한 광경을 함께 음미해 보자고 청했다."]], [["‘The veil that has long been interposed between Mrs. Micawber and myself, is now withdrawn,’ said Mr.", "\"오랫동안 미코버 부인과 저 사이에 드리워져 있던 장막이, 이제 걷혔습니다.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber; ‘and my children and the Author of their Being can once more come in contact on equal terms.’", "\"그리하여 제 아이들과 그들의 창조주가 다시 한번 대등한 처지에서 마주할 수 있게 되었습니다.\""]], [["As we were all very grateful to him, and all desirous to show that we were, as well as the hurry and disorder of our spirits would permit, I dare say we should all have gone, but that it was necessary for Agnes to return to her father, as yet unable to bear more than the dawn of hope; and for someone else to hold Uriah in safe keeping.", "우리 모두 그에게 무척 고마웠고, 어수선하고 어지러운 마음이 허락하는 한, 다들 그 고마움을 보이고 싶어 했으니, 아마 다 함께 갔을 것이다. 다만 아그네스가, 아직 희망의 동틀 녘 이상은 감당하지 못하는 아버지에게 돌아가야 했고, 또 누군가는 유라이아를 안전하게 붙잡아 두어야 했다."], ["So, Traddles remained for the latter purpose, to be presently relieved by Mr.", "그래서 트래들스가 뒤엣일을 위해 남았다가 곧 디크 씨와 교대하기로 했다."], ["Dick; and Mr. Dick, my aunt, and I, went home with Mr. Micawber.", "그리고 디크 씨와 고모님과 나는 미코버 씨와 함께 집으로 갔다."], ["As I parted hurriedly from the dear girl to whom I owed so much, and thought from what she had been saved, perhaps, that morning--her better resolution notwithstanding--I felt devoutly thankful for the miseries of my younger days which had brought me to the knowledge of Mr. Micawber.", "내가 그토록 많은 신세를 진 그 사랑스러운 처녀와 황급히 헤어지면서, 그녀가 아마도 그날 아침 — 그녀의 더 굳건한 결심에도 불구하고 — 무엇으로부터 구원받았는지를 생각하니, 나를 미코버 씨와 알게 해 준 내 어린 시절의 그 모진 불행들에 진심으로 감사한 마음이 들었다."]], [["His house was not far off; and as the street door opened into the sitting-room, and he bolted in with a precipitation quite his own, we found ourselves at once in the bosom of the family. Mr.", "그의 집은 멀지 않았다. 그리고 길로 난 문이 곧장 거실로 통해 있어서, 그가 자기만의 그 다급한 기세로 안으로 와락 뛰어들자, 우리는 단숨에 그 가족의 품 한복판에 서 있게 되었다."], ["Micawber exclaiming, ‘Emma!", "미코버 씨가 \"에마!"], ["my life!’ rushed into Mrs. Micawber’s arms. Mrs.", "내 생명이여!\" 하고 외치며 미코버 부인의 품으로 달려들었다."], ["Micawber shrieked, and folded Mr. Micawber in her embrace.", "미코버 부인은 비명을 지르며 미코버 씨를 끌어안았다."], ["Miss Micawber, nursing the unconscious stranger of Mrs. Micawber’s last letter to me, was sensibly affected.", "미코버 부인이 나에게 보낸 지난번 편지 속의 그 모르는 손님을 어르고 있던 미코버 양은, 적잖이 감격했다."], ["The stranger leaped. The twins testified their joy by several inconvenient but innocent demonstrations.", "그 모르는 손님은 깡충깡충 뛰었다. 쌍둥이는 거추장스럽지만 천진한 갖가지 몸짓으로 기쁨을 드러냈다."], ["Master Micawber, whose disposition appeared to have been soured by early disappointment, and whose aspect had become morose, yielded to his better feelings, and blubbered.", "일찌감치 좌절에 마음이 비뚤어진 듯하고 표정도 시무룩해진 미코버 도령은, 더 나은 감정에 굴복하여 엉엉 울었다."]], [["‘Emma!’ said Mr. Micawber.", "\"에마!\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["‘The cloud is past from my mind.", "\"내 마음에서 먹구름이 걷혔소."], ["Mutual confidence, so long preserved between us once, is restored, to know no further interruption.", "한때 우리 사이에 그토록 오래 지켜졌던 상호 신뢰가 회복되었으니, 다시는 끊기는 일이 없을 거요."], ["Now, welcome poverty!’ cried Mr. Micawber, shedding tears.", "자, 가난이여, 어서 오라!\" 미코버 씨가 눈물을 흘리며 외쳤다."], ["‘Welcome misery, welcome houselessness, welcome hunger, rags, tempest, and beggary!", "\"비참함이여 어서 오라, 집 없음이여 어서 오라, 굶주림과 누더기와 비바람과 거지 신세여 어서 오라!"], ["Mutual confidence will sustain us to the end!’", "상호 신뢰가 끝까지 우리를 떠받쳐 주리니!\""]], [["With these expressions, Mr. Micawber placed Mrs.", "이런 말들과 함께, 미코버 씨는 미코버 부인을 의자에 앉히고, 온 식구를 빙 돌아가며 끌어안았다."], ["Micawber in a chair, and embraced the family all round; welcoming a variety of bleak prospects, which appeared, to the best of my judgement, to be anything but welcome to them; and calling upon them to come out into Canterbury and sing a chorus, as nothing else was left for their support.", "그리고 내가 보기엔 도무지 반가울 게 없을 갖가지 암담한 앞날을 반긴 다음, 식구들에게 캔터베리 거리로 나가 합창이나 하자고 청했다. 달리 그들을 부지할 방도가 없다는 것이었다."]], [["But Mrs. Micawber having, in the strength of her emotions, fainted away, the first thing to be done, even before the chorus could be considered complete, was to recover her.", "그러나 미코버 부인이 벅찬 감정에 그만 까무러쳐 버려서, 합창을 갖출 생각을 하기에 앞서, 제일 먼저 할 일은 그녀를 깨우는 것이었다. 고모님과 미코버 씨가 그 일을 했다."], ["This my aunt and Mr. Micawber did; and then my aunt was introduced, and Mrs. Micawber recognized me.", "그러고 나서 고모님이 소개되었고, 미코버 부인은 나를 알아보았다."]], [["‘Excuse me, dear Mr.", "\"용서하세요, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨.\""], ["Copperfield,’ said the poor lady, giving me her hand, ‘but I am not strong; and the removal of the late misunderstanding between Mr. Micawber and myself was at first too much for me.’", "가엾은 부인이 나에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제가 몸이 튼튼치 못한 데다, 미코버 씨와 저 사이의 지난번 오해가 풀리고 나니 처음엔 너무 벅찼답니다.\""]], [["‘Is this all your family, ma’am?’ said my aunt.", "\"이게 댁의 식구가 다인가요, 부인?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘There are no more at present,’ returned Mrs. Micawber.", "\"지금으로서는 더는 없습니다.\" 미코버 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Good gracious, I didn’t mean that, ma’am,’ said my aunt.", "\"아이고, 그 말이 아니라요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘I mean, are all these yours?’", "\"내 말은, 이 아이들이 다 댁의 자식이냐는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Madam,’ replied Mr.", "\"부인.\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다."], ["Micawber, ‘it is a true bill.’", "\"틀림없는 사실입니다.\""]], [["‘And that eldest young gentleman, now,’ said my aunt, musing, ‘what has he been brought up to?’", "\"그런데 저 맏아드님 말인데요,\" 고모님이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"무얼 시키며 키우셨나요?\""]], [["‘It was my hope when I came here,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 여기 왔을 때 품었던 희망은,\""], ["Micawber, ‘to have got Wilkins into the Church: or perhaps I shall express my meaning more strictly, if I say the Choir.", "미코버 씨가 말했다. \"윌킨스를 교회에 들여보내는 것이었습니다. 아니, 좀 더 엄밀히 말하자면, 성가대에 넣는 것이라 해야겠군요."], ["But there was no vacancy for a tenor in the venerable Pile for which this city is so justly eminent; and he has--in short, he has contracted a habit of singing in public-houses, rather than in sacred edifices.’", "그러나 이 도시가 마땅히 그토록 이름난 그 유서 깊은 대성당에는 테너 자리가 비어 있지 않았습니다. 그래서 이 아이는 — 한마디로, 성스러운 건물보다는 술집에서 노래하는 버릇이 들고 말았습니다.\""]], [["‘But he means well,’ said Mrs.", "\"하지만 저 애는 마음씨가 착해요.\""], ["Micawber, tenderly.", "미코버 부인이 다정하게 말했다."]], [["‘I dare say, my love,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"그렇겠지, 여보.\" 미코버 씨가 맞장구쳤다."], ["Micawber, ‘that he means particularly well; but I have not yet found that he carries out his meaning, in any given direction whatsoever.’", "\"저 애가 유독 마음씨가 착하다는 거야 그렇겠지만, 저 애가 그 착한 마음을 어떤 방향으로든 실천에 옮기는 건 아직 본 적이 없구려.\""]], [["Master Micawber’s moroseness of aspect returned upon him again, and he demanded, with some temper, what he was to do?", "미코버 도령의 시무룩한 표정이 다시 돌아왔고, 그는 다소 발끈하며, 자기더러 어쩌라는 거냐고 따져 물었다."], ["Whether he had been born a carpenter, or a coach-painter, any more than he had been born a bird?", "자기가 새로 태어난 게 아니듯, 목수나 마차 도장공으로 태어나기라도 했느냐고?"], ["Whether he could go into the next street, and open a chemist’s shop?", "옆 거리로 가서 약방을 차릴 수 있느냐고?"], ["Whether he could rush to the next assizes, and proclaim himself a lawyer?", "다음 순회 재판소로 달려가 자기가 변호사라고 선언할 수 있느냐고?"], ["Whether he could come out by force at the opera, and succeed by violence?", "오페라 극장에 힘으로 밀고 들어가 폭력으로 성공할 수 있느냐고?"], ["Whether he could do anything, without being brought up to something?", "무언가를 익히도록 키워지지 않고서야, 무슨 일을 할 수 있느냐고?"]], [["My aunt mused a little while, and then said:", "고모님은 잠시 곰곰이 생각하더니 말했다."]], [["‘Mr. Micawber, I wonder you have never turned your thoughts to emigration.’", "\"미코버 씨, 어찌 한 번도 이민에 생각을 돌려 보지 않으셨는지 모르겠군요.\""]], [["‘Madam,’ returned Mr.", "\"부인.\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다."], ["Micawber, ‘it was the dream of my youth, and the fallacious aspiration of my riper years.’ I am thoroughly persuaded, by the by, that he had never thought of it in his life.", "\"그건 제 젊은 시절의 꿈이었고, 좀 더 무르익은 나이의 헛된 갈망이었습니다.\" 그건 그렇고, 그가 평생 그런 걸 생각해 본 적이 없다는 걸 나는 확신한다."]], [["‘Aye?’ said my aunt, with a glance at me.", "\"그래요?\" 고모님이 나를 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["‘Why, what a thing it would be for yourselves and your family, Mr. and Mrs. Micawber, if you were to emigrate now.’", "\"아니, 두 분과 댁 식구에게, 지금 이민을 떠나신다면 그게 얼마나 좋은 일이겠어요, 미코버 부부.\""]], [["‘Capital, madam, capital,’ urged Mr. Micawber, gloomily.", "\"자본이지요, 부인, 자본이요.\" 미코버 씨가 침울하게 역설했다."]], [["‘That is the principal, I may say the only difficulty, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ assented his wife.", "\"그게 가장 큰, 아니 유일한 어려움이랍니다, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨.\" 그의 아내가 동의했다."]], [["‘Capital?’ cried my aunt.", "\"자본이라뇨?\" 고모님이 외쳤다."], ["‘But you are doing us a great service--have done us a great service, I may say, for surely much will come out of the fire--and what could we do for you, that would be half so good as to find the capital?’", "\"하지만 당신들은 우리에게 큰 신세를 — 큰 신세를 지웠다고 해야겠지요, 분명 이 불길에서 많은 게 나올 테니까 — 그러니 우리가 자본을 마련해 주는 것의 절반만큼이라도 좋은 일을, 당신들을 위해 무엇을 더 해 줄 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["‘I could not receive it as a gift,’ said Mr.", "\"그걸 거저 받을 수는 없습니다.\""], ["Micawber, full of fire and animation, ‘but if a sufficient sum could be advanced, say at five per cent interest, per annum, upon my personal liability--say my notes of hand, at twelve, eighteen, and twenty-four months, respectively, to allow time for something to turn up--’", "미코버 씨가 열의와 활기에 가득 차 말했다. \"하지만 충분한 액수를, 이를테면 연 오 퍼센트 이자로, 제 개인 책임 아래 — 이를테면 무슨 일이 생길 시간을 벌도록 각각 십이 개월, 십팔 개월, 이십사 개월짜리 어음으로 — 미리 빌릴 수만 있다면—\""]], [["‘Could be? Can be and shall be, on your own terms,’ returned my aunt, ‘if you say the word.", "\"빌릴 수 있느냐고요? 당신이 원하는 조건대로 빌릴 수 있고말고요, 빌려 드리지요.\""], ["Think of this now, both of you.", "고모님이 대답했다. \"말씀만 하시면요."], ["Here are some people David knows, going out to Australia shortly.", "자, 이제 두 분 다 이걸 생각해 보세요. 데이비드가 아는 사람 몇이 머잖아 오스트레일리아로 떠나요."], ["If you decide to go, why shouldn’t you go in the same ship?", "두 분이 가기로 정하시면, 왜 같은 배로 못 가겠어요?"], ["You may help each other.", "서로 도울 수도 있고요."], ["Think of this now, Mr. and Mrs. Micawber.", "자, 이제 이걸 생각해 보세요, 미코버 부부."], ["Take your time, and weigh it well.’", "시간을 갖고, 잘 따져 보세요.\""]], [["‘There is but one question, my dear ma’am, I could wish to ask,’ said Mrs. Micawber.", "\"꼭 한 가지, 친애하는 부인, 여쭙고 싶은 게 있는데요.\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["‘The climate, I believe, is healthy?’", "\"기후가, 제가 알기로는, 건강에 좋다지요?\""]], [["‘Finest in the world!’ said my aunt.", "\"세상에서 제일 좋답니다!\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Just so,’ returned Mrs. Micawber.", "\"바로 그거예요.\""], ["‘Then my question arises.", "미코버 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["Now, are the circumstances of the country such, that a man of Mr. Micawber’s abilities would have a fair chance of rising in the social scale?", "\"그럼 제 질문이 나옵니다. 자, 그 나라 사정이, 미코버 씨 같은 능력을 지닌 사람이 사회적 지위에서 출세할 기회를 공정하게 누릴 만한 곳인가요?"], ["I will not say, at present, might he aspire to be Governor, or anything of that sort; but would there be a reasonable opening for his talents to develop themselves--that would be amply sufficient--and find their own expansion?’", "지금 당장, 그이가 총독이라든가 그런 자리를 넘볼 수 있겠느냐고는 묻지 않겠습니다. 하지만 그이의 재능이 — 그거면 충분하고도 남으니까요 — 스스로 펼쳐지고, 제 나름의 발전을 이룰, 합당한 길이 열려 있을까요?\""]], [["‘No better opening anywhere,’ said my aunt, ‘for a man who conducts himself well, and is industrious.’", "\"어디에도 그보다 나은 길은 없지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"처신을 바르게 하고 부지런한 사람한테는요.\""]], [["‘For a man who conducts himself well,’ repeated Mrs.", "\"처신을 바르게 하고,\""], ["Micawber, with her clearest business manner, ‘and is industrious. Precisely.", "미코버 부인이 더없이 또렷한 사무적 태도로 되풀이했다. \"부지런한 사람한테요."], ["It is evident to me that Australia is the legitimate sphere of action for Mr. Micawber!’", "바로 그거예요. 오스트레일리아가 미코버 씨의 정당한 활동 무대라는 게 제겐 분명해졌어요!\""]], [["‘I entertain the conviction, my dear madam,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 확신하건대, 친애하는 부인.\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘that it is, under existing circumstances, the land, the only land, for myself and family; and that something of an extraordinary nature will turn up on that shore.", "\"그곳이, 지금 형편으로는, 저와 제 식구를 위한 땅, 유일한 땅이며, 그 해안에서 무언가 비범한 일이 생길 것입니다. 그곳은 — 비교적 말해서 — 거리도 멀지 않지요."], ["It is no distance--comparatively speaking; and though consideration is due to the kindness of your proposal, I assure you that is a mere matter of form.’", "그리고 부인의 제안에 담긴 친절에는 마땅히 감사드려야겠지만, 분명히 말씀드리건대 그 부분은 그저 형식에 불과합니다.\""]], [["Shall I ever forget how, in a moment, he was the most sanguine of men, looking on to fortune; or how Mrs.", "그가 어떻게 단숨에, 행운을 내다보는 더없이 낙천적인 사람이 되었는지를 내가 어찌 잊을 수 있으랴."], ["Micawber presently discoursed about the habits of the kangaroo!", "또 미코버 부인이 곧이어 캥거루의 습성에 대해 늘어놓던 그 이야기를 어찌 잊으랴!"], ["Shall I ever recall that street of Canterbury on a market-day, without recalling him, as he walked back with us; expressing, in the hardy roving manner he assumed, the unsettled habits of a temporary sojourner in the land; and looking at the bullocks, as they came by, with the eye of an Australian farmer!", "장날의 그 캔터베리 거리를 떠올리면서, 우리와 함께 걸어 돌아오던 그를 떠올리지 않을 도리가 어디 있으랴. 그는 짐짓 취한 대담하고 떠돌이다운 태도로, 그 땅에 잠시 머무는 나그네의 정처 없는 버릇을 드러내 보였고, 황소들이 지나가자 오스트레일리아 농부의 눈길로 그것들을 바라보았던 것이다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 53. ANOTHER RETROSPECT", "# CHAPTER 53. ANOTHER RETROSPECT"]], [["I must pause yet once again.", "다시 한번 멈춰야겠다."], ["O, my child-wife, there is a figure in the moving crowd before my memory, quiet and still, saying in its innocent love and childish beauty, Stop to think of me--turn to look upon the Little Blossom, as it flutters to the ground!", "오, 나의 어린 아내여, 내 기억 앞을 흐르는 무리 속에 한 모습이 있어, 고요하고 잔잔하게, 그 순결한 사랑과 어린아이 같은 아름다움으로 이렇게 말한다. 멈춰서 나를 생각해 줘요 — 작은 꽃송이가 땅으로 팔랑팔랑 떨어지는 모습을 돌아봐 줘요!"]], [["I do. All else grows dim, and fades away.", "나는 그렇게 한다. 다른 모든 것은 흐릿해지고, 스러져 사라진다."], ["I am again with Dora, in our cottage.", "나는 다시 도라와 함께, 우리 오두막에 있다."], ["I do not know how long she has been ill.", "그녀가 앓은 지 얼마나 됐는지 나는 모른다."], ["I am so used to it in feeling, that I cannot count the time.", "마음으로는 그것이 너무도 익숙해져서, 그 시간을 헤아릴 수가 없다."], ["It is not really long, in weeks or months; but, in my usage and experience, it is a weary, weary while.", "주나 달로 따지면 정말로 긴 시간은 아니다. 하지만 내 처지와 경험으로는, 지치고 또 지치는 긴 세월이다."]], [["They have left off telling me to ‘wait a few days more’.", "이제 사람들은 나에게 \"며칠만 더 기다려 보라\"는 말을 그만두었다."], ["I have begun to fear, remotely, that the day may never shine, when I shall see my child-wife running in the sunlight with her old friend Jip.", "나는 어렴풋이, 내 어린 아내가 옛 친구 집과 함께 햇살 속을 달리는 모습을 다시 보게 될 그날이 영영 밝지 않을지도 모른다는 두려움을 품기 시작했다."]], [["He is, as it were suddenly, grown very old.", "집은, 말하자면 갑작스레, 무척 늙어 버렸다. 어쩌면 그 녀석은 제 주인에게서 자기를 활기차게 하고 젊어지게 했던 무언가를 잃은 것인지도 모른다."], ["It may be that he misses in his mistress, something that enlivened him and made him younger; but he mopes, and his sight is weak, and his limbs are feeble, and my aunt is sorry that he objects to her no more, but creeps near her as he lies on Dora’s bed--she sitting at the bedside--and mildly licks her hand.", "하지만 녀석은 풀이 죽어 시들시들하고, 눈은 침침해졌고, 다리에는 힘이 없다. 그리고 고모님은 녀석이 더 이상 자기에게 대들지 않는 것을 안타까워한다. 녀석은 도라의 침대 위에 누운 채로 — 고모님은 침대 곁에 앉아 있는데 — 고모님 가까이 슬금슬금 다가가, 그 손을 부드럽게 핥는다."]], [["Dora lies smiling on us, and is beautiful, and utters no hasty or complaining word.", "도라는 우리를 바라보며 미소 띤 채 누워 있고, 아름답고, 성급하거나 불평하는 말은 한마디도 내뱉지 않는다."], ["She says that we are very good to her; that her dear old careful boy is tiring himself out, she knows; that my aunt has no sleep, yet is always wakeful, active, and kind.", "그녀는 우리가 자기에게 무척 잘해 준다고 말한다. 자상하던 옛 소년이 스스로를 지치게 만들고 있다는 걸 안다고, 고모님은 한숨도 못 자면서도 늘 깨어 있고, 부지런하고, 다정하다고 말한다."], ["Sometimes, the little bird-like ladies come to see her; and then we talk about our wedding-day, and all that happy time.", "가끔, 그 작은 새 같은 부인들이 그녀를 보러 온다. 그러면 우리는 우리 결혼식 날과, 그 행복했던 시절을 이야기한다."]], [["What a strange rest and pause in my life there seems to be--and in all life, within doors and without--when I sit in the quiet, shaded, orderly room, with the blue eyes of my child-wife turned towards me, and her little fingers twining round my hand!", "조용하고, 그늘지고, 가지런한 방에 앉아, 내 어린 아내의 푸른 두 눈이 나를 향하고, 그 작은 손가락들이 내 손을 감싸 휘감을 때 — 내 삶에, 아니 안팎의 온 삶에, 이 얼마나 이상한 안식과 멈춤이 깃든 것 같은지! 나는 이렇게 수없이 많은 시간을 앉아 있다."], ["Many and many an hour I sit thus; but, of all those times, three times come the freshest on my mind.", "하지만 그 모든 시간 가운데, 세 번이 내 마음속에 가장 생생하게 떠오른다."]], [["It is morning; and Dora, made so trim by my aunt’s hands, shows me how her pretty hair will curl upon the pillow yet, an how long and bright it is, and how she likes to have it loosely gathered in that net she wears.", "아침이다. 고모님의 손길로 곱게 단장한 도라가, 자기 예쁜 머리카락이 아직도 베개 위에서 어떻게 곱슬곱슬 말리는지, 또 얼마나 길고 윤기 나는지, 그리고 자기가 쓰고 있는 그 망에 그것을 느슨하게 모아 두는 걸 얼마나 좋아하는지를 나에게 보여 준다."]], [["‘Not that I am vain of it, now, you mocking boy,’ she says, when I smile; ‘but because you used to say you thought it so beautiful; and because, when I first began to think about you, I used to peep in the glass, and wonder whether you would like very much to have a lock of it.", "\"이제 이걸 뽐내려는 게 아니에요, 짓궂은 양반.\" 내가 미소 짓자 그녀가 말한다. \"당신이 이게 참 아름답다고 생각한다고 늘 말했으니까요."], ["Oh what a foolish fellow you were, Doady, when I gave you one!’", "그리고 내가 당신을 처음 마음에 두기 시작했을 때, 거울을 들여다보면서 당신이 이 머리카락 한 타래를 갖고 싶어 무척 좋아할까 궁금해하곤 했으니까요. 아아, 내가 한 타래 줬을 때 당신은 얼마나 바보 같았는지 몰라요, 두디!\""]], [["‘That was on the day when you were painting the flowers I had given you, Dora, and when I told you how much in love I was.’", "\"그건 당신이, 내가 준 꽃을 그리고 있던 날이었지, 도라. 그리고 내가 당신에게 얼마나 사랑에 빠졌는지를 털어놓던 날이었고.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아아!"], ["but I didn’t like to tell you,’ says Dora, ‘then, how I had cried over them, because I believed you really liked me!", "하지만 그땐 차마 말하기 싫었어요.\" 도라가 말한다. \"내가 그 꽃을 보며 얼마나 울었는지를요."], ["When I can run about again as I used to do, Doady, let us go and see those places where we were such a silly couple, shall we?", "당신이 정말로 나를 좋아한다고 믿었으니까요! 내가 예전처럼 다시 뛰어다닐 수 있게 되면, 두디, 우리가 그렇게 바보 같은 한 쌍이었던 그 장소들을 보러 가요, 그래 줄래요?"], ["And take some of the old walks?", "그리고 옛날에 걷던 길도 좀 걷고요?"], ["And not forget poor papa?’", "그리고 가엾은 아빠도 잊지 말고요?\""]], [["‘Yes, we will, and have some happy days.", "\"그래, 그러자, 그리고 행복한 날들을 보내자."], ["So you must make haste to get well, my dear.’", "그러니 어서 빨리 나아야 해, 여보.\""]], [["‘Oh, I shall soon do that!", "\"오, 곧 그렇게 될 거예요!"], ["I am so much better, you don’t know!’", "나 정말 훨씬 좋아졌어요, 당신은 모를 거예요!\""]], [["It is evening; and I sit in the same chair, by the same bed, with the same face turned towards me.", "저녁이다. 나는 같은 의자에, 같은 침대 곁에 앉아 있고, 같은 얼굴이 나를 향하고 있다."], ["We have been silent, and there is a smile upon her face.", "우리는 말없이 있었고, 그녀의 얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있다."], ["I have ceased to carry my light burden up and down stairs now.", "이제 나는 내 가벼운 짐을 안고 계단을 오르내리지 않는다."], ["She lies here all the day.", "그녀는 온종일 여기 누워 있다."]], [["‘Doady!’", "\"두디!\""]], [["‘My dear Dora!’", "\"내 사랑 도라!\""]], [["‘You won’t think what I am going to say, unreasonable, after what you told me, such a little while ago, of Mr. Wickfield’s not being well?", "\"얼마 전에 당신이 위크필드 씨가 편찮으시다고 했잖아요, 그 얘기 들은 뒤에 내가 하려는 말을 당신이 터무니없다고 여기지 않을 거죠?"], ["I want to see Agnes.", "나, 애그니스를 보고 싶어요."], ["Very much I want to see her.’", "무척이나 그녀를 보고 싶어요.\""]], [["‘I will write to her, my dear.’", "\"그녀에게 편지를 쓰마, 여보.\""]], [["‘Will you?’", "\"그래 줄래요?\""]], [["‘Directly.’", "\"당장에.\""]], [["‘What a good, kind boy!", "\"참 착하고 다정한 양반이에요!"], ["Doady, take me on your arm.", "두디, 나를 당신 팔에 기대게 해 줘요."], ["Indeed, my dear, it’s not a whim.", "정말이에요, 여보, 이건 변덕이 아니에요."], ["It’s not a foolish fancy.", "바보 같은 공상도 아니고요."], ["I want, very much indeed, to see her!’", "나, 정말이지 무척이나, 그녀를 보고 싶어요!\""]], [["‘I am certain of it. I have only to tell her so, and she is sure to come.’", "\"나도 알아. 내가 그렇게 전하기만 하면, 그녀는 틀림없이 올 거야.\""]], [["‘You are very lonely when you go downstairs, now?’ Dora whispers, with her arm about my neck.", "\"이제 아래층에 내려가면 무척 외롭죠?\" 도라가 내 목에 팔을 두른 채 속삭인다."]], [["‘How can I be otherwise, my own love, when I see your empty chair?’", "\"어찌 안 그러겠어, 내 사랑, 당신의 빈 의자를 보는데?\""]], [["‘My empty chair!’ She clings to me for a little while, in silence.", "\"내 빈 의자!\" 그녀는 말없이 한동안 나에게 매달려 있다."], ["‘And you really miss me, Doady?’ looking up, and brightly smiling.", "\"그러면 당신은 정말로 내가 그리워요, 두디?\" 나를 올려다보며, 환하게 미소 지으며."], ["‘Even poor, giddy, stupid me?’", "\"이 가엾고, 덜렁대고, 멍청한 나조차도요?\""]], [["‘My heart, who is there upon earth that I could miss so much?’", "\"내 마음, 이 세상에 내가 그토록 그리워할 사람이 또 어디 있겠어?\""]], [["‘Oh, husband!", "\"오, 여보!"], ["I am so glad, yet so sorry!’ creeping closer to me, and folding me in both her arms.", "나 너무 기뻐요, 그런데 또 너무 슬퍼요!\" 나에게 더 바짝 파고들며, 두 팔로 나를 감싸 안으며."], ["She laughs and sobs, and then is quiet, and quite happy.", "그녀는 웃다가 흐느끼고, 그러다 잠잠해지더니, 아주 행복해한다."]], [["‘Quite!’ she says.", "\"아주요!\" 그녀가 말한다."], ["‘Only give Agnes my dear love, and tell her that I want very, very, much to see her; and I have nothing left to wish for.’", "\"그저 애그니스에게 내 깊은 사랑을 전해 주고, 내가 정말, 정말로 그녀를 무척 보고 싶어 한다고 말해 줘요. 그러면 나는 더 바랄 게 아무것도 없어요.\""]], [["‘Except to get well again, Dora.’", "\"다시 건강해지는 것만 빼면 말이지, 도라.\""]], [["‘Ah, Doady!", "\"아아, 두디!"], ["Sometimes I think--you know I always was a silly little thing!--that that will never be!’", "가끔 이런 생각이 들어요 — 알잖아요, 난 늘 어리석은 어린것이었으니까! — 그런 날은 영영 오지 않을 거라는 생각이요!\""]], [["‘Don’t say so, Dora!", "\"그런 말 마, 도라!"], ["Dearest love, don’t think so!’", "가장 사랑하는 사람, 그렇게 생각하지 마!\""]], [["‘I won’t, if I can help it, Doady.", "\"안 할게요, 그럴 수만 있다면요, 두디."], ["But I am very happy; though my dear boy is so lonely by himself, before his child-wife’s empty chair!’", "하지만 나는 무척 행복해요. 내 사랑하는 양반이 어린 아내의 빈 의자 앞에서 혼자 그토록 외롭더라도요!\""]], [["It is night; and I am with her still.", "밤이다. 나는 여전히 그녀와 함께 있다."], ["Agnes has arrived; has been among us for a whole day and an evening.", "애그니스가 도착해, 꼬박 하루 낮과 한 저녁을 우리 곁에 머물렀다."], ["She, my aunt, and I, have sat with Dora since the morning, all together.", "그녀와 고모님과 나는 아침부터 다 함께 도라 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["We have not talked much, but Dora has been perfectly contented and cheerful. We are now alone.", "우리는 말을 많이 하지 않았지만, 도라는 더없이 흡족하고 명랑했다. 이제 우리는 단둘이다."]], [["Do I know, now, that my child-wife will soon leave me?", "이제 나는, 내 어린 아내가 머지않아 나를 떠나리라는 것을 알고 있는 걸까?"], ["They have told me so; they have told me nothing new to my thoughts--but I am far from sure that I have taken that truth to heart.", "사람들은 나에게 그렇게 말했다. 내 생각에 이미 없던 사실을 그들이 새로 알려 준 것은 아니다 — 하지만 내가 그 진실을 마음 깊이 받아들였는지는 도무지 확신이 서지 않는다."], ["I cannot master it. I have withdrawn by myself, many times today, to weep.", "나는 그것을 도저히 감당할 수가 없다. 오늘 나는 여러 번 혼자 물러나 울었다."], ["I have remembered Who wept for a parting between the living and the dead.", "산 자와 죽은 자의 이별 앞에서 눈물 흘리셨던 그분을 나는 떠올렸다."], ["I have bethought me of all that gracious and compassionate history.", "그 자비롭고 가엾이 여기는 모든 이야기를 곱씹어 보았다."], ["I have tried to resign myself, and to console myself; and that, I hope, I may have done imperfectly; but what I cannot firmly settle in my mind is, that the end will absolutely come.", "나는 체념하려, 스스로를 달래려 애썼다. 그리고 그것은, 바라건대, 어설프게나마 해냈는지도 모른다. 하지만 내 마음속에 굳게 자리잡힐 수 없는 단 하나는, 끝이 정녕 오고야 만다는 사실이다."], ["I hold her hand in mine, I hold her heart in mine, I see her love for me, alive in all its strength.", "나는 그녀의 손을 내 손에 쥐고, 그녀의 마음을 내 마음에 품고, 나를 향한 그녀의 사랑이 온 힘을 다해 살아 있음을 본다."], ["I cannot shut out a pale lingering shadow of belief that she will be spared.", "나는 그녀가 살아남으리라는 창백하게 어른거리는 믿음의 그림자를 차마 떨쳐 낼 수가 없다."]], [["‘I am going to speak to you, Doady.", "\"당신에게 할 말이 있어요, 두디."], ["I am going to say something I have often thought of saying, lately.", "요즘 자주 하고 싶었던 말을 하려고요. 괜찮죠?\""], ["You won’t mind?’ with a gentle look.", "부드러운 눈길로."]], [["‘Mind, my darling?’", "\"괜찮다니, 내 사랑?\""]], [["‘Because I don’t know what you will think, or what you may have thought sometimes.", "\"왜냐하면 당신이 어떻게 생각할지, 아니면 가끔 어떻게 생각했을지 모르겠으니까요."], ["Perhaps you have often thought the same.", "어쩌면 당신도 똑같은 생각을 자주 했을지 몰라요."], ["Doady, dear, I am afraid I was too young.’", "두디, 여보, 나는 내가 너무 어렸던 게 아닌가 싶어요.\""]], [["I lay my face upon the pillow by her, and she looks into my eyes, and speaks very softly.", "나는 그녀 곁 베개에 얼굴을 묻고, 그녀는 내 두 눈을 들여다보며, 아주 부드럽게 말한다."], ["Gradually, as she goes on, I feel, with a stricken heart, that she is speaking of herself as past.", "그녀가 말을 이어 갈수록, 나는 가슴이 찢어지는 마음으로, 그녀가 자기 자신을 이미 지나간 사람처럼 이야기하고 있음을 느낀다."]], [["‘I am afraid, dear, I was too young. I don’t mean in years only, but in experience, and thoughts, and everything.", "\"나는, 여보, 내가 너무 어렸던 게 아닌가 싶어요. 나이만 두고 하는 말이 아니에요, 경험이며, 생각이며, 모든 것이요."], ["I was such a silly little creature!", "나는 정말 어리석은 어린것이었어요!"], ["I am afraid it would have been better, if we had only loved each other as a boy and girl, and forgotten it.", "우리가 그저 소년 소녀처럼 서로 사랑하다가 잊어버렸더라면 더 나았을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어요."], ["I have begun to think I was not fit to be a wife.’", "나는 아내가 될 만한 사람이 아니었다는 생각이 들기 시작했어요.\""]], [["I try to stay my tears, and to reply, ‘Oh, Dora, love, as fit as I to be a husband!’", "나는 눈물을 참으려 애쓰며 대답한다. \"오, 도라, 내 사랑, 당신은 내가 남편 될 만큼은 충분했어!\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ with the old shake of her curls.", "\"모르겠어요.\" 옛날처럼 곱슬머리를 살래살래 흔들며."], ["‘Perhaps!", "\"어쩌면요!"], ["But if I had been more fit to be married I might have made you more so, too. Besides, you are very clever, and I never was.’", "하지만 내가 결혼할 자질이 더 있었더라면, 당신도 더 그렇게 만들었을지 몰라요. 게다가 당신은 무척 영리한 사람인데, 나는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었으니까요.\""]], [["‘We have been very happy, my sweet Dora.’", "\"우리는 무척 행복했잖아, 사랑스러운 도라.\""]], [["‘I was very happy, very.", "\"나는 무척 행복했어요, 정말로요."], ["But, as years went on, my dear boy would have wearied of his child-wife.", "하지만 세월이 흐르면, 내 사랑하는 양반은 어린 아내에게 싫증이 났을 거예요."], ["She would have been less and less a companion for him.", "그녀는 점점 더 그에게 어울리는 짝이 못 되었을 테고요."], ["He would have been more and more sensible of what was wanting in his home.", "그는 자기 가정에 부족한 것이 무엇인지 점점 더 절실히 느꼈을 거예요."], ["She wouldn’t have improved.", "그녀는 나아지지 않았을 테고요."], ["It is better as it is.’", "이대로가 더 나아요.\""]], [["‘Oh, Dora, dearest, dearest, do not speak to me so.", "\"오, 도라, 가장 사랑하는, 가장 사랑하는 사람, 나에게 그렇게 말하지 마."], ["Every word seems a reproach!’", "한마디 한마디가 다 꾸짖음 같아!\""]], [["‘No, not a syllable!’ she answers, kissing me.", "\"아니에요, 한 음절도 그렇지 않아요!\" 그녀가 나에게 입 맞추며 대답한다."], ["‘Oh, my dear, you never deserved it, and I loved you far too well to say a reproachful word to you, in earnest--it was all the merit I had, except being pretty--or you thought me so.", "\"오, 여보, 당신은 그런 말을 들을 까닭이 한 번도 없었어요. 그리고 나는 당신을 너무 깊이 사랑해서, 진심으로 당신에게 꾸짖는 말 한마디 할 수 없었어요 — 그게 내가 가진 유일한 미덕이었죠, 예쁜 것 말고는요 — 아니면 당신이 나를 그렇게 여겨 줬던 거고요."], ["Is it lonely, down-stairs, Doady?’", "아래층은 외롭죠, 두디?\""]], [["‘Very! Very!’", "\"무척! 무척!\""]], [["‘Don’t cry!", "\"울지 마요!"], ["Is my chair there?’", "내 의자는 거기 있어요?\""]], [["‘In its old place.’", "\"그 옛날 자리에 있지.\""]], [["‘Oh, how my poor boy cries! Hush, hush!", "\"오, 내 가엾은 양반이 어쩜 저렇게 우는지! 쉿, 쉿!"], ["Now, make me one promise.", "자, 나에게 한 가지만 약속해 줘요."], ["I want to speak to Agnes.", "나, 애그니스와 이야기하고 싶어요."], ["When you go downstairs, tell Agnes so, and send her up to me; and while I speak to her, let no one come--not even aunt.", "아래층에 내려가거든, 애그니스에게 그렇게 말하고, 그녀를 나에게 올려보내 줘요. 그리고 내가 그녀와 이야기하는 동안에는, 아무도 오지 않게 해 줘요 — 고모님조차도요."], ["I want to speak to Agnes by herself.", "나는 애그니스와 단둘이서 이야기하고 싶어요."], ["I want to speak to Agnes, quite alone.’", "애그니스와, 아주 단둘이서요.\""]], [["I promise that she shall, immediately; but I cannot leave her, for my grief.", "나는 그렇게 하겠노라고, 당장 그러겠노라고 약속한다. 하지만 슬픔에 겨워 그녀 곁을 떠날 수가 없다."]], [["‘I said that it was better as it is!’ she whispers, as she holds me in her arms.", "\"이대로가 더 낫다고 내가 말했죠!\" 그녀가 두 팔로 나를 끌어안은 채 속삭인다."], ["‘Oh, Doady, after more years, you never could have loved your child-wife better than you do; and, after more years, she would so have tried and disappointed you, that you might not have been able to love her half so well!", "\"오, 두디, 세월이 더 흘렀더라도, 당신은 지금보다 어린 아내를 더 사랑할 수는 없었을 거예요. 그리고 세월이 더 흘렀더라면, 그녀는 당신을 너무도 애먹이고 실망시켜서, 당신이 그녀를 지금의 절반만큼도 사랑할 수 없었을지 몰라요!"], ["I know I was too young and foolish. It is much better as it is!’", "나는 내가 너무 어리고 어리석었다는 걸 알아요. 이대로가 훨씬 나아요!\""]], [["Agnes is downstairs, when I go into the parlour; and I give her the message.", "내가 거실로 들어가니 애그니스가 아래층에 있다. 나는 그녀에게 그 말을 전한다."], ["She disappears, leaving me alone with Jip.", "그녀는 나를 집과 단둘이 남겨 둔 채 사라진다."]], [["His Chinese house is by the fire; and he lies within it, on his bed of flannel, querulously trying to sleep.", "집의 중국식 개집이 난로 곁에 있고, 녀석은 그 안에서 플란넬 잠자리에 누워, 보채듯 잠들려 애쓰고 있다."], ["The bright moon is high and clear.", "밝은 달이 높이, 맑게 떠 있다."], ["As I look out on the night, my tears fall fast, and my undisciplined heart is chastened heavily--heavily.", "밤을 내다보는 사이, 내 눈물은 줄줄 흘러내리고, 다스리지 못한 내 마음은 무겁게, 무겁게 매를 맞는다."]], [["I sit down by the fire, thinking with a blind remorse of all those secret feelings I have nourished since my marriage.", "나는 난롯가에 앉아, 결혼 이후로 내가 키워 온 그 모든 은밀한 감정들을 눈먼 회한과 함께 곱씹는다."], ["I think of every little trifle between me and Dora, and feel the truth, that trifles make the sum of life.", "나와 도라 사이의 사소한 일 하나하나를 떠올리며, 그 사소한 것들이 모여 삶의 총합을 이룬다는 진실을 느낀다."], ["Ever rising from the sea of my remembrance, is the image of the dear child as I knew her first, graced by my young love, and by her own, with every fascination wherein such love is rich.", "내 기억의 바다에서 끝없이 떠오르는 것은, 내가 처음 알았던 그 사랑스러운 아이의 모습이다. 내 젊은 사랑과 그녀 자신의 사랑으로 곱게 빛나고, 그러한 사랑이 풍성히 머금은 온갖 매력으로 단장한 모습이다."], ["Would it, indeed, have been better if we had loved each other as a boy and a girl, and forgotten it? Undisciplined heart, reply!", "정녕, 우리가 소년 소녀처럼 서로 사랑하다가 잊어버렸더라면 더 나았을까? 다스리지 못한 마음이여, 대답하라!"]], [["How the time wears, I know not; until I am recalled by my child-wife’s old companion.", "시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지 나는 모른다. 내 어린 아내의 옛 동무가 나를 깨워 부를 때까지는."], ["More restless than he was, he crawls out of his house, and looks at me, and wanders to the door, and whines to go upstairs.", "녀석은 아까보다 더 안절부절못하며, 제 집에서 기어 나와 나를 바라보다가, 문 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어가, 위층으로 올라가고 싶다고 낑낑댄다."]], [["‘Not tonight, Jip!", "\"오늘 밤은 안 돼, 집!"], ["Not tonight!’", "오늘 밤은 안 돼!\""]], [["He comes very slowly back to me, licks my hand, and lifts his dim eyes to my face.", "녀석은 아주 천천히 나에게 돌아와, 내 손을 핥고, 침침한 두 눈을 들어 내 얼굴을 본다."]], [["‘Oh, Jip!", "\"오, 집!"], ["It may be, never again!’", "어쩌면, 다시는 못 보겠지!\""]], [["He lies down at my feet, stretches himself out as if to sleep, and with a plaintive cry, is dead.", "녀석은 내 발치에 엎드려, 잠이라도 들려는 듯 몸을 쭉 뻗더니, 구슬프게 한 번 끙 소리를 내고는, 숨을 거둔다."]], [["‘Oh, Agnes!", "\"오, 애그니스!"], ["Look, look, here!’ --That face, so full of pity, and of grief, that rain of tears, that awful mute appeal to me, that solemn hand upraised towards Heaven!", "봐요, 봐요, 여기를!\" — 연민과 슬픔으로 가득 찬 그 얼굴, 그 빗물처럼 쏟아지는 눈물, 나를 향한 그 말 없는 끔찍한 호소, 하늘을 향해 엄숙히 치켜든 그 손!"]], [["‘Agnes?’", "\"애그니스?\""]], [["It is over.", "다 끝났다."], ["Darkness comes before my eyes; and, for a time, all things are blotted out of my remembrance.", "어둠이 내 두 눈 앞으로 밀려든다. 그리고 한동안, 모든 것이 내 기억에서 지워진다."]], [["# CHAPTER 54. Mr. MICAWBER’S TRANSACTIONS", "# CHAPTER 54. Mr. MICAWBER’S TRANSACTIONS"]], [["This is not the time at which I am to enter on the state of my mind beneath its load of sorrow.", "지금은 슬픔의 무게에 짓눌린 내 마음의 상태를 풀어놓을 자리가 아니다."], ["I came to think that the Future was walled up before me, that the energy and action of my life were at an end, that I never could find any refuge but in the grave.", "나는 미래가 내 앞에 벽처럼 가로막혀 버렸다고, 내 삶의 활력과 의욕이 끝장나 버렸다고, 무덤 말고는 어디서도 안식을 찾을 수 없으리라고 생각하기에 이르렀다."], ["I came to think so, I say, but not in the first shock of my grief.", "그렇게 생각하기에 이르렀다고 나는 말하지만, 그것은 슬픔의 첫 충격 속에서가 아니었다."], ["It slowly grew to that.", "그런 마음은 천천히 자라났다."], ["If the events I go on to relate, had not thickened around me, in the beginning to confuse, and in the end to augment, my affliction, it is possible (though I think not probable), that I might have fallen at once into this condition.", "내가 이제부터 이야기할 사건들이 내 주위로 빽빽이 몰려들어, 처음에는 내 고통을 헝클어 놓고 끝내는 그것을 부풀려 놓지 않았더라면, 나는 어쩌면 (그럴 법하지는 않다고 보지만) 곧장 그런 상태로 떨어졌을지도 모른다."], ["As it was, an interval occurred before I fully knew my own distress; an interval, in which I even supposed that its sharpest pangs were past; and when my mind could soothe itself by resting on all that was most innocent and beautiful, in the tender story that was closed for ever.", "하지만 실제로는, 내가 내 비탄을 온전히 깨닫기까지 한동안 틈이 있었다. 그 틈 동안 나는 그 비탄의 가장 날카로운 아픔이 이미 지나갔다고 여기기까지 했다. 영원히 막을 내린 그 애틋한 이야기 속에서 가장 순결하고 아름다운 모든 것에 마음을 기대어 스스로를 달랠 수 있던 틈이었다."]], [["When it was first proposed that I should go abroad, or how it came to be agreed among us that I was to seek the restoration of my peace in change and travel, I do not, even now, distinctly know.", "내가 외국으로 떠나야 한다는 이야기가 처음 나온 것이 언제였는지, 변화와 여행 속에서 마음의 평화를 되찾아야 한다는 것이 우리 사이에서 어떻게 합의되었는지, 나는 지금도 또렷이 모른다."], ["The spirit of Agnes so pervaded all we thought, and said, and did, in that time of sorrow, that I assume I may refer the project to her influence.", "그 슬픔의 시간에 우리가 생각하고, 말하고, 행한 모든 것에 아그네스의 마음이 어찌나 두루 스며 있었던지, 나는 그 계획을 그녀의 영향에 돌려도 좋으리라고 짐작한다."], ["But her influence was so quiet that I know no more.", "그러나 그녀의 영향은 어찌나 조용했던지, 나는 그 이상은 알지 못한다."]], [["And now, indeed, I began to think that in my old association of her with the stained-glass window in the church, a prophetic foreshadowing of what she would be to me, in the calamity that was to happen in the fullness of time, had found a way into my mind.", "그리고 이제, 정말로, 나는 이런 생각을 하기 시작했다. 오래전 내가 그녀를 교회의 스테인드글라스 창과 한데 묶어 떠올리던 그 일이, 때가 무르익어 닥칠 그 참사 속에서 그녀가 나에게 어떤 존재가 될지를 예언처럼 미리 비춰 준 것이, 어느새 내 마음속으로 길을 찾아 들어왔던 거라고."], ["In all that sorrow, from the moment, never to be forgotten, when she stood before me with her upraised hand, she was like a sacred presence in my lonely house.", "그 모든 슬픔 속에서, 그녀가 손을 치켜든 채 내 앞에 서 있던, 결코 잊지 못할 그 순간부터, 그녀는 내 외로운 집 안의 성스러운 존재 같았다."], ["When the Angel of Death alighted there, my child-wife fell asleep--they told me so when I could bear to hear it--on her bosom, with a smile.", "죽음의 천사가 그곳에 내려앉았을 때, 내 어린 아내는 미소를 머금은 채 그녀의 가슴에 안겨 잠들었다 — 내가 그 말을 견딜 수 있게 되었을 때 사람들이 그렇게 일러 주었다."], ["From my swoon, I first awoke to a consciousness of her compassionate tears, her words of hope and peace, her gentle face bending down as from a purer region nearer Heaven, over my undisciplined heart, and softening its pain.", "혼절했다가 처음 깨어났을 때 내가 의식한 것은, 그녀의 가엾어하는 눈물이었고, 희망과 평안을 담은 그녀의 말이었으며, 천국에 더 가까운 더 맑은 세계에서 내려오기라도 한 듯 다스려지지 않은 내 마음 위로 굽어, 그 아픔을 부드럽게 어루만지는 그녀의 다정한 얼굴이었다."]], [["Let me go on.", "이야기를 계속하자."]], [["I was to go abroad. That seemed to have been determined among us from the first.", "나는 외국으로 떠나기로 되어 있었다. 그것은 처음부터 우리 사이에서 정해져 있었던 듯하다."], ["The ground now covering all that could perish of my departed wife, I waited only for what Mr. Micawber called the ‘final pulverization of Heep’; and for the departure of the emigrants.", "세상을 떠난 내 아내의 스러질 수 있는 모든 것을 이제 흙이 덮고 있는 지금, 나는 미코버 씨가 '히프의 최종적 분쇄'라고 부르던 일과 이주민들의 출발만을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["At the request of Traddles, most affectionate and devoted of friends in my trouble, we returned to Canterbury: I mean my aunt, Agnes, and I.", "내 곤경의 시절에 가장 살뜰하고 헌신적인 벗이었던 트래들스의 청에 따라, 우리는 캔터베리로 돌아갔다. 고모님과 아그네스와 나, 이렇게 말이다."], ["We proceeded by appointment straight to Mr. Micawber’s house; where, and at Mr. Wickfield’s, my friend had been labouring ever since our explosive meeting.", "우리는 약속대로 곧장 미코버 씨의 집으로 갔는데, 우리가 폭탄 같은 자리를 함께한 이래로 내 벗은 그 집과 위크필드 씨 댁에서 줄곧 일을 해 오고 있었다."], ["When poor Mrs. Micawber saw me come in, in my black clothes, she was sensibly affected.", "가엾은 미코버 부인은 내가 검은 상복을 입고 들어서는 것을 보자 눈에 띄게 마음이 흔들렸다."], ["There was a great deal of good in Mrs. Micawber’s heart, which had not been dunned out of it in all those many years.", "미코버 부인의 마음속에는 그 숱한 세월의 빚 독촉에도 닳아 없어지지 않은 착한 구석이 꽤 많이 남아 있었다."]], [["‘Well, Mr. and Mrs.", "\"자, 미코버 씨, 미코버 부인.\""], ["Micawber,’ was my aunt’s first salutation after we were seated.", "우리가 자리에 앉은 뒤 고모님이 처음 건넨 인사였다."], ["‘Pray, have you thought about that emigration proposal of mine?’", "\"내가 말한 그 이주 제안, 생각들은 좀 해 보셨나요?\""]], [["‘My dear madam,’ returned Mr.", "\"부인,\" 미코버 씨가 대답했다."], ["Micawber, ‘perhaps I cannot better express the conclusion at which Mrs. Micawber, your humble servant, and I may add our children, have jointly and severally arrived, than by borrowing the language of an illustrious poet, to reply that our Boat is on the shore, and our Bark is on the sea.’", "\"미코버 부인과 부인의 보잘것없는 종, 그리고 거기에 우리 아이들까지 더하여, 다 함께 그리고 저마다 다다른 결론을 표현하자면, 저로서는 어느 이름난 시인의 말씨를 빌려, 우리의 거룻배는 물가에 있고 우리의 큰 배는 바다 위에 있다고 답하는 것보다 더 나은 방도를 찾지 못하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘That’s right,’ said my aunt.", "\"바로 그거예요.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘I augur all sort of good from your sensible decision.’", "\"그 현명한 결정에서 나는 온갖 좋은 일을 점쳐 봅니다.\""]], [["‘Madam, you do us a great deal of honour,’ he rejoined.", "\"부인, 저희에게 큰 영광을 베푸시는군요.\" 그가 받았다."], ["He then referred to a memorandum.", "그러더니 그는 비망록을 들여다보았다."], ["‘With respect to the pecuniary assistance enabling us to launch our frail canoe on the ocean of enterprise, I have reconsidered that important business-point; and would beg to propose my notes of hand--drawn, it is needless to stipulate, on stamps of the amounts respectively required by the various Acts of Parliament applying to such securities--at eighteen, twenty-four, and thirty months.", "\"사업이라는 대양에 우리의 가냘픈 카누를 띄우게 해 줄 금전적 도움에 관해서는, 그 중대한 사업상의 요점을 다시 생각해 보았습니다. 그리하여 제 약속어음을 — 그런 증권에 적용되는 여러 의회법이 저마다 요구하는 액수의 인지에 끊어 발행하는 것은 굳이 따로 정할 것도 없는 일입니다만 — 십팔 개월, 이십사 개월, 삼십 개월짜리로 내놓을 것을 제안드리고자 합니다."], ["The proposition I originally submitted, was twelve, eighteen, and twenty-four; but I am apprehensive that such an arrangement might not allow sufficient time for the requisite amount of--Something--to turn up.", "제가 애초에 제출한 안은 십이 개월, 십팔 개월, 이십사 개월이었습니다만, 그런 식으로 짜면 무언가가 — 무언가가 — 충분히 생겨날 만큼의 시간이 모자랄까 봐 염려가 됩니다. 우리는,\""], ["We might not,’ said Mr. Micawber, looking round the room as if it represented several hundred acres of highly cultivated land, ‘on the first responsibility becoming due, have been successful in our harvest, or we might not have got our harvest in.", "미코버 씨가, 마치 그 방이 잘 일군 수백 에이커의 땅이라도 되는 양 둘러보며 말했다. \"첫 채무가 만기에 이르렀을 때, 추수에 성공하지 못했을 수도, 또는 거둔 것을 미처 들이지 못했을 수도 있습니다."], ["Labour, I believe, is sometimes difficult to obtain in that portion of our colonial possessions where it will be our lot to combat with the teeming soil.’", "우리가 비옥하게 들끓는 흙과 씨름하게 될 운명에 처할, 우리 식민 영토의 그 지역에서는, 일손을 구하기가 이따금 어렵다고 들었습니다.\""]], [["‘Arrange it in any way you please, sir,’ said my aunt.", "\"좋으신 대로 정하세요, 선생님.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Madam,’ he replied, ‘Mrs.", "\"부인,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["Micawber and myself are deeply sensible of the very considerate kindness of our friends and patrons.", "\"미코버 부인과 저는 우리의 벗이자 후원자 되시는 분들의 그토록 사려 깊은 친절을 깊이 느끼고 있습니다."], ["What I wish is, to be perfectly business-like, and perfectly punctual.", "제가 바라는 바는, 더없이 사업가답고, 더없이 정확하게 처신하는 것입니다."], ["Turning over, as we are about to turn over, an entirely new leaf; and falling back, as we are now in the act of falling back, for a Spring of no common magnitude; it is important to my sense of self-respect, besides being an example to my son, that these arrangements should be concluded as between man and man.’", "이제 막 우리가 펼치려는 것처럼 완전히 새로운 페이지를 넘기면서, 또 지금 우리가 하고 있는 것처럼 예사롭지 않은 큰 도약을 위해 한껏 뒤로 물러서면서, 이 약정들이 사내와 사내 사이의 일로서 마무리되는 것은, 제 자존심의 측면에서도 중요하거니와 제 아들에게도 본보기가 됩니다.\""]], [["I don’t know that Mr.", "미코버 씨가 이 마지막 구절에 어떤 의미를 담았는지 나는 모르겠다."], ["Micawber attached any meaning to this last phrase; I don’t know that anybody ever does, or did; but he appeared to relish it uncommonly, and repeated, with an impressive cough, ‘as between man and man’.", "누가 지금이나 예전이나 거기에 의미를 담은 적이 있는지도 모르겠다. 그러나 그는 그 말을 유난히 음미하는 듯했고, 인상적인 헛기침과 함께 \"사내와 사내 사이의 일로서\"라고 되뇌었다."]], [["‘I propose,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 제안하는 것은,\" 미코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘Bills--a convenience to the mercantile world, for which, I believe, we are originally indebted to the Jews, who appear to me to have had a devilish deal too much to do with them ever since--because they are negotiable.", "\"어음입니다. 상업계에 편리한 물건이지요. 제가 알기로 우리가 본디 이것을 빚진 상대는 유대인들인데, 그들은 그 후로 줄곧 어음과 지나치게 깊이 얽혀 온 듯합니다 — 어음은 양도가 가능하니까요."], ["But if a Bond, or any other description of security, would be preferred, I should be happy to execute any such instrument.", "하지만 채무 증서든 그 밖의 어떤 형태의 담보든 그쪽을 더 원하신다면, 저는 기꺼이 그런 증서를 작성하겠습니다."], ["As between man and man.’", "사내와 사내 사이의 일로서요.\""]], [["My aunt observed, that in a case where both parties were willing to agree to anything, she took it for granted there would be no difficulty in settling this point. Mr. Micawber was of her opinion.", "고모님은, 양쪽 다 무엇에든 기꺼이 동의하려는 마당이니, 이 점을 매듭짓는 데 어려움이 없으리라 여긴다고 말했다. 미코버 씨도 같은 생각이었다."]], [["‘In reference to our domestic preparations, madam,’ said Mr.", "\"우리의 가정적 준비에 관해서 말씀드리자면, 부인,\""], ["Micawber, with some pride, ‘for meeting the destiny to which we are now understood to be self-devoted, I beg to report them.", "미코버 씨가 약간 자랑스러운 기색으로 말했다. \"이제 우리가 스스로 바쳐 나아가기로 한 운명을 맞이하기 위한 준비를 보고드리겠습니다."], ["My eldest daughter attends at five every morning in a neighbouring establishment, to acquire the process--if process it may be called--of milking cows.", "제 맏딸은 소젖 짜는 과정을 — 그것을 과정이라 부를 수 있다면요 — 익히려고 매일 아침 다섯 시에 이웃의 한 시설에 나가 있습니다."], ["My younger children are instructed to observe, as closely as circumstances will permit, the habits of the pigs and poultry maintained in the poorer parts of this city: a pursuit from which they have, on two occasions, been brought home, within an inch of being run over.", "어린 자식들은 이 도시의 더 가난한 구역에서 기르는 돼지며 가금의 습성을, 사정이 허락하는 한 가까이서 관찰하라고 일러 두었는데, 그 일로 두 차례나 자칫 수레에 깔릴 뻔한 채로 집에 실려 온 적이 있습니다."], ["I have myself directed some attention, during the past week, to the art of baking; and my son Wilkins has issued forth with a walking-stick and driven cattle, when permitted, by the rugged hirelings who had them in charge, to render any voluntary service in that direction--which I regret to say, for the credit of our nature, was not often; he being generally warned, with imprecations, to desist.’", "저 자신은 지난 한 주 동안 빵 굽는 기술에 얼마간 주의를 기울였고, 제 아들 윌킨스는 지팡이를 들고 나가 소를 몰았습니다 — 소를 맡은 거친 일꾼들이 그쪽으로 자진해서 봉사하는 것을 허락해 줄 때 말이지요. 다만 인간 본성의 명예를 위해 유감스럽게 말씀드리건대, 그런 허락은 자주 있지 않았습니다. 대개는 욕설과 함께 그만두라는 경고를 받았으니까요.\""]], [["‘All very right indeed,’ said my aunt, encouragingly. ‘Mrs.", "\"정말 다 잘하셨네요.\" 고모님이 북돋우듯 말했다."], ["Micawber has been busy, too, I have no doubt.’", "\"미코버 부인도 분명 바쁘셨겠지요.\""]], [["‘My dear madam,’ returned Mrs. Micawber, with her business-like air.", "\"부인,\" 미코버 부인이 사업가다운 태도로 받았다."], ["‘I am free to confess that I have not been actively engaged in pursuits immediately connected with cultivation or with stock, though well aware that both will claim my attention on a foreign shore.", "\"솔직히 고백하건대, 저는 경작이나 가축에 곧바로 닿는 일에는 적극적으로 매달리지 못했습니다. 낯선 해안에 가면 그 두 가지가 다 제 손길을 요구하리라는 것은 잘 알고 있습니다만 말입니다."], ["Such opportunities as I have been enabled to alienate from my domestic duties, I have devoted to corresponding at some length with my family.", "가정의 본분에서 떼어 낼 수 있었던 짬은, 저는 친정 식구들에게 제법 긴 편지를 띄우는 데 바쳤습니다."], ["For I own it seems to me, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "솔직히 제게는 이렇게 여겨지거든요, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Micawber, who always fell back on me, I suppose from old habit, to whomsoever else she might address her discourse at starting, ‘that the time is come when the past should be buried in oblivion; when my family should take Mr.", "미코버 부인은, 처음에는 누구를 향해 이야기를 시작하든, 아마도 오랜 버릇에서 늘 나에게로 되돌아오곤 했는데, 이렇게 말했다."], ["Micawber by the hand, and Mr. Micawber should take my family by the hand; when the lion should lie down with the lamb, and my family be on terms with Mr. Micawber.’", "\"지난 일은 망각 속에 묻어야 할 때가, 우리 친정이 미코버 씨의 손을 잡고 미코버 씨가 우리 친정의 손을 잡아야 할 때가, 사자가 양과 함께 누워야 할 때가, 그리고 우리 친정이 미코버 씨와 화목해져야 할 때가 왔다고 말입니다.\""]], [["I said I thought so too.", "나도 그렇게 생각한다고 말했다."]], [["‘This, at least, is the light, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ pursued Mrs.", "\"적어도, 친애하는 코퍼필드 씨, 이것이 제가 이 문제를 바라보는 관점입니다.\""], ["Micawber, ‘in which I view the subject.", "미코버 부인이 이어 갔다."], ["When I lived at home with my papa and mama, my papa was accustomed to ask, when any point was under discussion in our limited circle, “In what light does my Emma view the subject?”", "\"제가 친정에서 아빠 엄마와 함께 살 때, 우리의 좁은 식구들 사이에서 무슨 안건이 논의될라치면 아빠는 으레 이렇게 물으셨답니다. '우리 에마는 이 문제를 어떤 관점에서 보느냐?'"], ["That my papa was too partial, I know; still, on such a point as the frigid coldness which has ever subsisted between Mr. Micawber and my family, I necessarily have formed an opinion, delusive though it may be.’", "우리 아빠가 저를 지나치게 편애하셨다는 건 저도 압니다. 그래도 미코버 씨와 우리 친정 사이에 줄곧 이어져 온 그 얼음장 같은 냉기 같은 문제라면, 비록 잘못된 것일지언정 저로서는 어쩔 수 없이 의견 하나는 갖게 마련이지요.\""]], [["‘No doubt.", "\"그럼요."], ["Of course you have, ma’am,’ said my aunt.", "당연히 그러시겠지요, 부인.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Precisely so,’ assented Mrs. Micawber.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 미코버 부인이 맞장구쳤다."], ["‘Now, I may be wrong in my conclusions; it is very likely that I am, but my individual impression is, that the gulf between my family and Mr. Micawber may be traced to an apprehension, on the part of my family, that Mr.", "\"이제, 제 결론이 틀렸을 수도 있습니다. 틀렸을 가능성이 아주 큽니다만, 제 개인적인 인상으로는, 우리 친정과 미코버 씨 사이의 그 깊은 골은, 미코버 씨가 금전적 융통을 요구할 것이라는 우리 친정 쪽의 우려에서 비롯된 것으로 더듬어 갈 수 있다고 봅니다."], ["Micawber would require pecuniary accommodation.", "저는 이렇게 생각하지 않을 수가 없어요,\""], ["I cannot help thinking,’ said Mrs.", "미코버 부인이 깊은 통찰의 기색으로 말했다."], ["Micawber, with an air of deep sagacity, ‘that there are members of my family who have been apprehensive that Mr.", "\"우리 친정 식구 가운데에는, 미코버 씨가 자기들 이름을 청해 올까 봐 걱정해 온 이들이 있다고 말입니다."], ["Micawber would solicit them for their names.---I do not mean to be conferred in Baptism upon our children, but to be inscribed on Bills of Exchange, and negotiated in the Money Market.’", "우리 아이들에게 세례 때 붙여 달라는 것이 아니라, 환어음에 적어 넣어 금융 시장에서 양도하자는 그런 이름 말이지요.\""]], [["The look of penetration with which Mrs.", "미코버 부인이 마치 여태 아무도 거기에 생각이 미친 적이 없다는 듯 이 발견을 알릴 때 짓던 그 꿰뚫는 듯한 표정은, 고모님을 적잖이 놀라게 한 모양이었다."], ["Micawber announced this discovery, as if no one had ever thought of it before, seemed rather to astonish my aunt; who abruptly replied, ‘Well, ma’am, upon the whole, I shouldn’t wonder if you were right!’", "고모님은 불쑥 대꾸했다. \"글쎄요, 부인, 따지고 보면, 부인 말씀이 옳다 해도 이상할 게 없겠네요!\""]], [["‘Mr. Micawber being now on the eve of casting off the pecuniary shackles that have so long enthralled him,’ said Mrs.", "\"미코버 씨는 이제 막, 그토록 오래 그를 옭아매 온 금전의 족쇄를 벗어 던지고,\" 미코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘and of commencing a new career in a country where there is sufficient range for his abilities,--which, in my opinion, is exceedingly important; Mr. Micawber’s abilities peculiarly requiring space,--it seems to me that my family should signalize the occasion by coming forward.", "\"그의 능력을 펼칠 충분한 활동 폭이 있는 나라에서 — 제가 보기에 이건 대단히 중요한 일입니다, 미코버 씨의 능력은 유난히 넓은 공간을 필요로 하니까요 — 새로운 길을 시작하려는 참입니다. 그러니 제게는, 우리 친정이 앞으로 나서서 이 일을 기려야 마땅하게 여겨집니다."], ["What I could wish to see, would be a meeting between Mr. Micawber and my family at a festive entertainment, to be given at my family’s expense; where Mr.", "제가 보고 싶은 것은, 우리 친정의 비용으로 베푸는 흥겨운 잔치 자리에서 미코버 씨와 우리 친정이 만나는 모습입니다."], ["Micawber’s health and prosperity being proposed, by some leading member of my family, Mr. Micawber might have an opportunity of developing his views.’", "그 자리에서 우리 친정의 어느 이름난 어른이 미코버 씨의 건강과 번영을 위해 축배를 들면, 미코버 씨도 자기 포부를 펼쳐 보일 기회를 얻을 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\" 미코버 씨가 약간 발끈하여 말했다."], ["Micawber, with some heat, ‘it may be better for me to state distinctly, at once, that if I were to develop my views to that assembled group, they would possibly be found of an offensive nature: my impression being that your family are, in the aggregate, impertinent Snobs; and, in detail, unmitigated Ruffians.’", "\"이참에 분명히 밝혀 두는 편이 나을 듯하오. 만약 내가 그 모인 무리 앞에서 내 포부를 펼친다면, 그건 십중팔구 듣기에 거북한 것이 되고 말 거요. 내 인상으로 당신네 친정 식구들은, 통틀어 말하면 건방진 속물들이고, 하나하나 따지면 누그러질 줄 모르는 악당들이니까.\""]], [["‘Micawber,’ said Mrs.", "\"미코버,\""], ["Micawber, shaking her head, ‘no!", "미코버 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["You have never understood them, and they have never understood you.’", "\"아니에요! 당신은 한 번도 그들을 이해한 적이 없고, 그들도 한 번도 당신을 이해한 적이 없어요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber coughed.", "미코버 씨가 헛기침을 했다."]], [["‘They have never understood you, Micawber,’ said his wife.", "\"그들은 한 번도 당신을 이해한 적이 없어요, 미코버.\" 그의 아내가 말했다."], ["‘They may be incapable of it.", "\"그럴 능력이 없는 건지도 모르지요."], ["If so, that is their misfortune.", "만약 그렇다면, 그건 그들의 불행이에요."], ["I can pity their misfortune.’", "나는 그들의 불행을 가엾이 여길 수 있어요.\""]], [["‘I am extremely sorry, my dear Emma,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 미안하오, 사랑하는 에마,\""], ["Micawber, relenting, ‘to have been betrayed into any expressions that might, even remotely, have the appearance of being strong expressions.", "미코버 씨가 누그러지며 말했다. \"조금이라도, 아주 멀게나마, 거친 말로 보일 만한 표현에 휩쓸리고 만 것 말이오."], ["All I would say is, that I can go abroad without your family coming forward to favour me,--in short, with a parting Shove of their cold shoulders; and that, upon the whole, I would rather leave England with such impetus as I possess, than derive any acceleration of it from that quarter.", "내가 말하고 싶은 건 단지, 당신네 친정이 나서서 호의를 베풀어 주지 않더라도 — 한마디로 작별 삼아 차갑게 어깨를 한 번 떠밀어 주는 것만으로도 — 나는 외국으로 떠날 수 있다는 것, 그리고 따지고 보면, 그쪽에서 가속을 좀 얻느니 차라리 내가 가진 만큼의 힘으로 영국을 떠나는 편이 낫겠다는 것이오."], ["At the same time, my dear, if they should condescend to reply to your communications--which our joint experience renders most improbable--far be it from me to be a barrier to your wishes.’", "그래도, 여보, 만약 그들이 황송하게도 당신 편지에 답장이라도 보내 준다면 — 우리 둘의 경험으로 보아 그럴 가능성은 거의 없습니다만 — 당신의 바람을 가로막을 마음은 결코 없소.\""]], [["The matter being thus amicably settled, Mr. Micawber gave Mrs.", "이렇게 문제가 화기애애하게 마무리되자, 미코버 씨는 미코버 부인에게 팔을 내밀고, 트래들스 앞 탁자에 쌓인 책과 서류 더미를 한번 흘끗 보더니, 우리끼리 있게 자리를 비켜 주겠다고 말했다."], ["Micawber his arm, and glancing at the heap of books and papers lying before Traddles on the table, said they would leave us to ourselves; which they ceremoniously did.", "그러고는 둘이 격식을 차려 물러갔다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, leaning back in his chair when they were gone, and looking at me with an affection that made his eyes red, and his hair all kinds of shapes, ‘I don’t make any excuse for troubling you with business, because I know you are deeply interested in it, and it may divert your thoughts.", "\"친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 그들이 가 버린 뒤 트래들스가 의자에 등을 기대고, 눈이 벌게지고 머리카락이 온갖 모양으로 헝클어질 만큼 애정 어린 눈으로 나를 보며 말했다. \"사업 이야기로 자네를 번거롭게 하는 걸 변명하진 않겠네."], ["My dear boy, I hope you are not worn out?’", "자네가 여기에 깊이 마음을 두고 있는 줄 알고, 또 이게 자네 생각을 다른 데로 돌려 줄 수도 있으니까. 이보게, 자네 너무 지친 건 아니지?\""]], [["‘I am quite myself,’ said I, after a pause.", "\"이젠 괜찮아.\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 내가 말했다."], ["‘We have more cause to think of my aunt than of anyone. You know how much she has done.’", "\"누구보다도 우리 고모님을 생각해야지. 고모님이 얼마나 애써 주셨는지 자네도 알잖나.\""]], [["‘Surely, surely,’ answered Traddles.", "\"그럼, 그렇고말고.\" 트래들스가 대답했다."], ["‘Who can forget it!’", "\"누가 그걸 잊을 수 있겠나!\""]], [["‘But even that is not all,’ said I.", "\"하지만 그것도 다가 아니라네.\""], ["‘During the last fortnight, some new trouble has vexed her; and she has been in and out of London every day.", "내가 말했다. \"지난 보름 동안 무슨 새로운 근심이 고모님을 괴롭혔는지, 날마다 런던을 들락날락하셨어."], ["Several times she has gone out early, and been absent until evening.", "몇 번은 이른 아침에 나가셔서 저녁까지 돌아오지 않으셨고."], ["Last night, Traddles, with this journey before her, it was almost midnight before she came home.", "어젯밤엔, 트래들스, 이 여행을 앞두고도, 거의 자정이 다 되어서야 집에 돌아오셨다네."], ["You know what her consideration for others is.", "자네도 고모님이 남을 얼마나 배려하시는지 알잖나."], ["She will not tell me what has happened to distress her.’", "무슨 일로 그토록 마음을 졸이시는지 나한테 말씀을 안 하셔.\""]], [["My aunt, very pale, and with deep lines in her face, sat immovable until I had finished; when some stray tears found their way to her cheeks, and she put her hand on mine.", "고모님은 몹시 창백한 얼굴에 깊은 주름을 새긴 채, 내 말이 끝날 때까지 꼼짝없이 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 흐르는 눈물 몇 방울이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 고모님은 내 손 위에 자기 손을 얹었다."]], [["‘It’s nothing, Trot; it’s nothing.", "\"아무것도 아니다, 트롯. 아무것도 아니야."], ["There will be no more of it.", "다시는 그런 일 없을 게다."], ["You shall know by and by.", "차차 알게 될 거야."], ["Now Agnes, my dear, let us attend to these affairs.’", "자 아그네스, 얘야, 우리 이 일들이나 마저 보자꾸나.\""]], [["‘I must do Mr. Micawber the justice to say,’ Traddles began, ‘that although he would appear not to have worked to any good account for himself, he is a most untiring man when he works for other people.", "\"미코버 씨에게는 공정하게 이 말은 해 둬야겠는데,\" 트래들스가 입을 열었다."], ["I never saw such a fellow. If he always goes on in the same way, he must be, virtually, about two hundred years old, at present.", "\"자기 자신을 위해서는 별 신통한 성과를 못 내는 사람처럼 보여도, 남을 위해 일할 때는 그야말로 지칠 줄을 모르는 사람일세. 그런 친구는 본 적이 없어."], ["The heat into which he has been continually putting himself; and the distracted and impetuous manner in which he has been diving, day and night, among papers and books; to say nothing of the immense number of letters he has written me between this house and Mr. Wickfield’s, and often across the table when he has been sitting opposite, and might much more easily have spoken; is quite extraordinary.’", "늘 이런 식으로 살아온 거라면, 그 양반은 지금쯤 사실상 이백 살은 됐어야 할 거야. 줄곧 스스로를 들끓게 몰아넣던 그 열기며, 밤낮없이 서류와 책 더미 속을 정신없이 다급하게 파고들던 그 태도며, 게다가 이 집과 위크필드 씨 댁 사이를 오가며 나한테 써 보낸 그 엄청난 양의 편지며 — 맞은편에 앉아 있어서 훨씬 쉽게 말로 하면 될 때도 곧잘 탁자 너머로 편지를 써 보내곤 했지 — 정말이지 보통 일이 아니야.\""]], [["‘Letters!’ cried my aunt.", "\"편지라니!\" 고모님이 외쳤다."], ["‘I believe he dreams in letters!’", "\"그 양반은 꿈도 편지로 꾸는 모양이지!\""]], [["‘There’s Mr. Dick, too,’ said Traddles, ‘has been doing wonders!", "\"디크 씨도 말이야,\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["As soon as he was released from overlooking Uriah Heep, whom he kept in such charge as I never saw exceeded, he began to devote himself to Mr. Wickfield.", "\"기적 같은 일을 해냈다네! 유라이어 히프 감시하는 일에서 — 내가 본 것 중 그보다 더한 감시는 없을 만큼 단단히 붙잡고 있었지 — 풀려나자마자, 위크필드 씨에게 온몸을 바쳐 매달리기 시작했어."], ["And really his anxiety to be of use in the investigations we have been making, and his real usefulness in extracting, and copying, and fetching, and carrying, have been quite stimulating to us.’", "그리고 우리가 벌이던 조사에 정말이지 도움이 되고자 하던 그 열성이며, 서류를 뽑아내고, 베끼고, 가져오고, 나르는 데서 보여 준 그 실속 있는 쓸모란, 우리한테 여간 힘이 되지 않았다네.\""]], [["‘Dick is a very remarkable man,’ exclaimed my aunt; ‘and I always said he was.", "\"디크는 아주 비범한 사람이야.\" 고모님이 소리쳤다. \"내가 늘 그렇게 말했지."], ["Trot, you know it.’", "트롯, 너도 알잖니.\""]], [["‘I am happy to say, Miss Wickfield,’ pursued Traddles, at once with great delicacy and with great earnestness, ‘that in your absence Mr.", "\"기쁘게 말씀드릴 게 있습니다, 위크필드 양.\" 트래들스가 더없이 섬세하면서도 더없이 진지하게 이어 갔다. \"그쪽이 자리를 비운 동안 위크필드 씨가 상당히 좋아지셨습니다."], ["Wickfield has considerably improved. Relieved of the incubus that had fastened upon him for so long a time, and of the dreadful apprehensions under which he had lived, he is hardly the same person.", "그토록 오래 그를 옥죄어 온 그 짐과, 그가 그 아래서 살아온 그 무서운 두려움에서 벗어나, 지금은 거의 딴사람 같습니다. 때로는, 특정한 사업상의 사안에 기억과 주의를 모으는 그 손상된 능력마저 상당히 회복되어서, 그가 없었다면 정말 어려웠을, 어쩌면 가망 없었을 몇몇 일들을 또렷이 밝히는 데 우리를 도와주셨습니다."], ["At times, even his impaired power of concentrating his memory and attention on particular points of business, has recovered itself very much; and he has been able to assist us in making some things clear, that we should have found very difficult indeed, if not hopeless, without him.", "하지만 제가 해야 할 일은 결론에 이르는 것이지요. 결론이야 충분히 짧으니까요. 제가 눈여겨본 그 모든 희망찬 정황들을 시시콜콜 늘어놓는 게 아니라요."], ["But what I have to do is to come to results; which are short enough; not to gossip on all the hopeful circumstances I have observed, or I shall never have done.’ His natural manner and agreeable simplicity made it transparent that he said this to put us in good heart, and to enable Agnes to hear her father mentioned with greater confidence; but it was not the less pleasant for that.", "안 그러면 이야기가 끝이 없을 테니까요.\" 그의 천연덕스러운 태도와 사람 좋은 소박함을 보면, 그가 이 말을 하는 것은 우리에게 용기를 북돋워 주고 아그네스가 자기 아버지 이야기를 좀 더 마음 놓고 들을 수 있게 하려는 것임이 훤히 들여다보였다. 그렇다고 해서 그 말이 덜 흐뭇하지는 않았다."]], [["‘Now, let me see,’ said Traddles, looking among the papers on the table.", "\"자, 어디 보자.\" 탁자 위 서류들을 뒤지며 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘Having counted our funds, and reduced to order a great mass of unintentional confusion in the first place, and of wilful confusion and falsification in the second, we take it to be clear that Mr. Wickfield might now wind up his business, and his agency-trust, and exhibit no deficiency or defalcation whatever.’", "\"우선 우리 손에 있는 자금을 헤아리고, 첫째로는 본의 아닌 혼란의, 둘째로는 고의적인 혼란과 위조의 그 어마어마한 덩어리를 정리하고 나니, 이제는 위크필드 씨가 자기 사업과 대리 위탁을 깨끗이 청산하고도 어떠한 부족이나 횡령도 없음을 분명히 보일 수 있다고 보입니다.\""]], [["‘Oh, thank Heaven!’ cried Agnes, fervently.", "\"아, 하느님 감사합니다!\" 아그네스가 간절하게 외쳤다."]], [["‘But,’ said Traddles, ‘the surplus that would be left as his means of support--and I suppose the house to be sold, even in saying this--would be so small, not exceeding in all probability some hundreds of pounds, that perhaps, Miss Wickfield, it would be best to consider whether he might not retain his agency of the estate to which he has so long been receiver.", "\"하지만,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그의 생계 밑천으로 남게 될 잉여금은 — 이렇게 말하는 마당에 집은 팔린다고 잡고서 하는 말입니다만 — 너무도 적어서, 아마 다 합해도 몇백 파운드를 넘지 못할 겁니다."], ["His friends might advise him, you know; now he is free.", "그러니, 위크필드 양, 어쩌면 그분이 그토록 오래 수입 관리인을 맡아 온 그 토지의 대리권만큼은 그대로 지니시는 게 어떨지 생각해 보는 게 상책일지도 모르겠습니다. 이제 자유로운 몸이니, 친구들이 조언을 드릴 수도 있겠지요."], ["You yourself, Miss Wickfield--Copperfield--I--’", "위크필드 양 본인이라든가 — 코퍼필드라든가 — 저라든가 —\""]], [["‘I have considered it, Trotwood,’ said Agnes, looking to me, ‘and I feel that it ought not to be, and must not be; even on the recommendation of a friend to whom I am so grateful, and owe so much.’", "\"저도 그건 생각해 봤어요, 트롯우드.\" 아그네스가 나를 보며 말했다. \"그리고 제가 느끼기에 그건 안 될 일이고, 그래서는 안 됩니다. 제가 이토록 고맙고 이토록 많은 신세를 진 친구분의 권유라 하더라도요.\""]], [["‘I will not say that I recommend it,’ observed Traddles.", "\"권한다고는 말하지 않겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘I think it right to suggest it. No more.’", "\"그저 한번 짚어 드리는 것이 옳다고 여겼을 뿐입니다. 그 이상은 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘I am happy to hear you say so,’ answered Agnes, steadily, ‘for it gives me hope, almost assurance, that we think alike.", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 기쁩니다.\" 아그네스가 차분하게 대답했다. \"우리가 같은 생각이라는 희망을, 거의 확신을 주시니까요."], ["Dear Mr. Traddles and dear Trotwood, papa once free with honour, what could I wish for!", "친애하는 트래들스 씨, 그리고 친애하는 트롯우드, 아빠가 명예를 지키며 한번 자유로워지신다면, 제가 무얼 더 바라겠어요!"], ["I have always aspired, if I could have released him from the toils in which he was held, to render back some little portion of the love and care I owe him, and to devote my life to him.", "저는 늘 갈망해 왔어요, 아빠를 옭아매던 그 굴레에서 벗어나게 해 드릴 수만 있다면, 제가 아빠께 진 사랑과 보살핌을 아주 조금이라도 되돌려 드리고, 제 삶을 아빠께 바치겠다고요."], ["It has been, for years, the utmost height of my hopes.", "여러 해 동안 그것이 제 바람의 가장 높은 꼭대기였어요."], ["To take our future on myself, will be the next great happiness--the next to his release from all trust and responsibility--that I can know.’", "우리의 앞날을 제가 떠맡는 것이, 다음으로 큰 행복이 될 거예요 — 아빠가 모든 위탁과 책임에서 벗어나시는 그 일에 버금가는, 제가 알 수 있는 그다음 행복 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Have you thought how, Agnes?’", "\"어떻게 할지는 생각해 봤니, 아그네스?\""]], [["‘Often! I am not afraid, dear Trotwood.", "\"여러 번요! 두렵지 않아요, 친애하는 트롯우드."], ["I am certain of success.", "저는 잘되리라 확신해요."], ["So many people know me here, and think kindly of me, that I am certain.", "여기엔 저를 알고 저를 따뜻하게 여겨 주는 사람이 아주 많아서, 저는 확신해요."], ["Don’t mistrust me.", "저를 못 미더워하지 마세요."], ["Our wants are not many.", "우리한테 필요한 건 많지 않아요."], ["If I rent the dear old house, and keep a school, I shall be useful and happy.’", "정든 옛집을 세 얻어 학교를 열면, 저는 쓸모도 있고 행복도 할 거예요.\""]], [["The calm fervour of her cheerful voice brought back so vividly, first the dear old house itself, and then my solitary home, that my heart was too full for speech. Traddles pretended for a little while to be busily looking among the papers.", "쾌활한 그녀의 목소리에 담긴 그 잔잔한 열정이, 먼저 정든 옛집 그 자체를, 그다음에는 나의 외로운 집을 어찌나 생생히 되살려 놓던지, 내 가슴은 벅차올라 말이 나오지 않았다. 트래들스는 한동안 서류들을 바쁘게 뒤지는 척했다."]], [["‘Next, Miss Trotwood,’ said Traddles, ‘that property of yours.’", "\"다음은, 트롯우드 양,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그쪽의 그 재산 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Well, sir,’ sighed my aunt.", "\"그래요, 선생님.\""], ["‘All I have got to say about it is, that if it’s gone, I can bear it; and if it’s not gone, I shall be glad to get it back.’", "고모님이 한숨지었다. \"그 일로 내가 할 말이라곤, 사라진 거라면 견디겠고, 안 사라진 거라면 되찾으면 기쁘겠다는 것뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘It was originally, I think, eight thousand pounds, Consols?’ said Traddles.", "\"그건 본디, 제 생각엔, 콘솔 공채로 팔천 파운드였지요?\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Right!’ replied my aunt.", "\"맞아요!\" 고모님이 대답했다."]], [["‘I can’t account for more than five,’ said Traddles, with an air of perplexity.", "\"제가 따져 본 바로는 오천밖에 행방을 못 짚겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 어리둥절한 기색으로 말했다."]], [["‘--thousand, do you mean?’ inquired my aunt, with uncommon composure, ‘or pounds?’", "\"— 천이라는 말인가요,\" 고모님이 유달리 침착하게 물었다. \"아니면 파운드?\""]], [["‘Five thousand pounds,’ said Traddles.", "\"오천 파운드입니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘It was all there was,’ returned my aunt.", "\"그게 있던 전부예요.\" 고모님이 받았다."], ["‘I sold three, myself.", "\"삼천은 내가 직접 팔았지요."], ["One, I paid for your articles, Trot, my dear; and the other two I have by me.", "천은 네 변호사 수습 계약비로 냈고, 트롯, 얘야. 나머지 이천은 내가 갖고 있어요."], ["When I lost the rest, I thought it wise to say nothing about that sum, but to keep it secretly for a rainy day.", "나머지를 잃었을 때, 나는 그 돈 얘기는 입 밖에 안 내고 만일에 대비해 몰래 간직해 두는 게 현명하겠다 싶었지."], ["I wanted to see how you would come out of the trial, Trot; and you came out nobly--persevering, self-reliant, self-denying! So did Dick.", "트롯, 나는 네가 그 시련을 어떻게 헤쳐 나오는지 보고 싶었어. 그런데 너는 보란 듯이 헤쳐 나왔지 — 끈기 있고, 제 힘으로 서고, 제 욕심을 누르면서!"], ["Don’t speak to me, for I find my nerves a little shaken!’", "디크도 그랬고. 나한테 말 걸지 마라, 신경이 좀 흔들리는구나!\""]], [["Nobody would have thought so, to see her sitting upright, with her arms folded; but she had wonderful self-command.", "팔짱을 낀 채 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 모습만 보면 아무도 그렇게 생각지 못했을 테지만, 고모님은 놀라운 자제력을 지니고 있었다."]], [["‘Then I am delighted to say,’ cried Traddles, beaming with joy, ‘that we have recovered the whole money!’", "\"그렇다면 기쁘게 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 기쁨으로 환하게 빛나며 외쳤다. \"우리가 그 돈을 몽땅 되찾았습니다!\""]], [["‘Don’t congratulate me, anybody!’ exclaimed my aunt.", "\"아무도 나한테 축하 같은 거 하지 마요!\" 고모님이 외쳤다."], ["‘How so, sir?’", "\"어떻게 된 거죠, 선생님?\""]], [["‘You believed it had been misappropriated by Mr. Wickfield?’ said Traddles.", "\"그쪽은 그 돈을 위크필드 씨가 잘못 빼돌렸다고 믿으셨지요?\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Of course I did,’ said my aunt, ‘and was therefore easily silenced.", "\"당연히 그렇게 믿었지요.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그래서 쉽게 입을 다물었던 거고."], ["Agnes, not a word!’", "아그네스, 한마디도 하지 마라!\""]], [["‘And indeed,’ said Traddles, ‘it was sold, by virtue of the power of management he held from you; but I needn’t say by whom sold, or on whose actual signature.", "\"그런데 사실은,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그 돈은 그분이 그쪽에게서 받은 관리 권한에 의거해 팔린 게 맞습니다."], ["It was afterwards pretended to Mr. Wickfield, by that rascal,--and proved, too, by figures,--that he had possessed himself of the money (on general instructions, he said) to keep other deficiencies and difficulties from the light. Mr.", "다만 누구 손으로 팔렸는지, 누구의 실제 서명으로 팔렸는지는 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요. 나중에 그 악당이 위크필드 씨에게는, 다른 부족분과 곤란을 드러나지 않게 덮으려고 (포괄적인 지시를 받았다면서) 자기가 그 돈을 챙긴 것이라고 둘러댔고, 숫자로 그렇게 증명까지 해 보였습니다."], ["Wickfield, being so weak and helpless in his hands as to pay you, afterwards, several sums of interest on a pretended principal which he knew did not exist, made himself, unhappily, a party to the fraud.’", "위크필드 씨는 그자의 손아귀에서 어찌나 무력하고 어쩔 줄 몰랐던지, 나중에는 자기도 없는 줄 뻔히 아는 가짜 원금에 대한 이자 명목으로 그쪽에게 몇 차례 돈을 치렀고, 그렇게 불행히도 스스로 그 사기의 한패가 되고 말았습니다.\""]], [["‘And at last took the blame upon himself,’ added my aunt; ‘and wrote me a mad letter, charging himself with robbery, and wrong unheard of. Upon which I paid him a visit early one morning, called for a candle, burnt the letter, and told him if he ever could right me and himself, to do it; and if he couldn’t, to keep his own counsel for his daughter’s sake.---If anybody speaks to me, I’ll leave the house!’", "\"그러고는 끝내 죄를 제가 다 뒤집어썼지요.\" 고모님이 덧붙였다. \"그러고는 나한테 정신 나간 편지를 써 보냈어요. 자기가 강도질을 했네, 듣도 보도 못한 못된 짓을 했네 하면서 죄를 죄다 제게 씌우더군요. 그래서 나는 어느 날 이른 아침 그를 찾아가, 촛불을 가져오라 해서 그 편지를 태워 버리고, 그에게 일렀지요. 언젠가 나와 자신을 바로잡을 수 있거든 그렇게 하고, 못 하겠거든 딸을 위해서라도 입을 다물고 있으라고요. — 누구든 나한테 말 걸면, 나는 이 집을 나가 버리겠어요!\""]], [["We all remained quiet; Agnes covering her face.", "우리는 모두 조용히 있었다. 아그네스는 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["‘Well, my dear friend,’ said my aunt, after a pause, ‘and you have really extorted the money back from him?’", "\"자, 친애하는 친구,\" 잠시 뒤 고모님이 말했다. \"그래서 정말로 그자에게서 그 돈을 도로 짜냈다는 건가요?\""]], [["‘Why, the fact is,’ returned Traddles, ‘Mr.", "\"실은 말입니다,\" 트래들스가 대답했다."], ["Micawber had so completely hemmed him in, and was always ready with so many new points if an old one failed, that he could not escape from us.", "\"미코버 씨가 그자를 어찌나 빈틈없이 몰아붙였던지, 그리고 옛 논점 하나가 막히면 늘 새 논점을 여럿 들고 채비하고 있어서, 그자가 우리한테서 빠져나갈 수가 없었습니다."], ["A most remarkable circumstance is, that I really don’t think he grasped this sum even so much for the gratification of his avarice, which was inordinate, as in the hatred he felt for Copperfield. He said so to me, plainly.", "가장 놀라운 사실은, 그자가 이 돈을 거머쥔 것이, 도를 넘어선 그자의 탐욕을 채우려는 것보다도, 사실은 코퍼필드에 대한 증오 때문이었다고 저는 정말로 봅니다. 그자가 저한테 대놓고 그렇게 말했어요."], ["He said he would even have spent as much, to baulk or injure Copperfield.’", "코퍼필드를 방해하거나 해코지할 수만 있다면 이만한 돈쯤 기꺼이 썼을 거라고 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Ha!’ said my aunt, knitting her brows thoughtfully, and glancing at Agnes.", "\"하!\" 고모님이 생각에 잠겨 눈살을 찌푸리며 아그네스를 흘끗 보았다."], ["‘And what’s become of him?’", "\"그래서 그자는 어찌 됐나요?\""]], [["‘I don’t know.", "\"모릅니다."], ["He left here,’ said Traddles, ‘with his mother, who had been clamouring, and beseeching, and disclosing, the whole time.", "여기를 떠났습니다.\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"내내 악을 쓰고, 애원하고, 죄다 까발리던 제 어미와 함께요."], ["They went away by one of the London night coaches, and I know no more about him; except that his malevolence to me at parting was audacious.", "런던행 야간 마차편으로 떠났는데, 그자에 대해 더는 아는 게 없습니다. 다만 헤어질 때 저한테 보인 그 악의만큼은 대단했습니다."], ["He seemed to consider himself hardly less indebted to me, than to Mr. Micawber; which I consider (as I told him) quite a compliment.’", "그자는 자기가 저한테 진 빚이 미코버 씨에게 진 것에 못지않다고 여기는 모양이었는데, 저는 (그자한테도 그렇게 말했지만) 그걸 그야말로 칭찬으로 받아들입니다.\""]], [["‘Do you suppose he has any money, Traddles?’ I asked.", "\"그자한테 돈이 좀 있을 것 같나, 트래들스?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh dear, yes, I should think so,’ he replied, shaking his head, seriously.", "\"아 이런, 그럼, 있고말고지.\" 그가 진지하게 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["‘I should say he must have pocketed a good deal, in one way or other.", "\"이런저런 방법으로 꽤 챙겨 넣었을 거라고 봐."], ["But, I think you would find, Copperfield, if you had an opportunity of observing his course, that money would never keep that man out of mischief.", "하지만 말이야, 코퍼필드, 자네한테 그자의 행적을 지켜볼 기회가 있다면 알게 될 거야. 돈이 있다고 그런 인간이 못된 짓을 안 할 리는 없다는 걸."], ["He is such an incarnate hypocrite, that whatever object he pursues, he must pursue crookedly.", "그자는 워낙 위선이 뼛속까지 사무친 인간이라, 무슨 목적을 좇든 비뚤어진 길로밖에 못 좇아."], ["It’s his only compensation for the outward restraints he puts upon himself.", "그게 스스로에게 씌운 겉치레 굴레에 대한 그자의 유일한 보상이거든."], ["Always creeping along the ground to some small end or other, he will always magnify every object in the way; and consequently will hate and suspect everybody that comes, in the most innocent manner, between him and it.", "늘 어떤 시시한 목적을 향해 땅바닥을 기어 다니면서, 그자는 길목에 놓인 모든 것을 잔뜩 부풀려 보고, 그래서 아무리 무심하게라도 자기와 그것 사이에 끼어드는 사람은 누구든 미워하고 의심하지."], ["So the crooked courses will become crookeder, at any moment, for the least reason, or for none.", "그러니 그 비뚤어진 행로는, 사소한 이유로든 아무 이유 없이든, 언제든 더 비뚤어지게 마련이야."], ["It’s only necessary to consider his history here,’ said Traddles, ‘to know that.’", "그걸 알려면,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"여기서 그자가 걸어온 내력만 짚어 봐도 충분하지.\""]], [["‘He’s a monster of meanness!’ said my aunt.", "\"비열함이 괴물 같은 자야!\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Really I don’t know about that,’ observed Traddles thoughtfully.", "\"글쎄요, 저는 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."], ["‘Many people can be very mean, when they give their minds to it.’", "\"마음만 먹으면 누구나 꽤 비열해질 수 있으니까요.\""]], [["‘And now, touching Mr. Micawber,’ said my aunt.", "\"자, 이제 미코버 씨 이야기로 넘어가서.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Well, really,’ said Traddles, cheerfully, ‘I must, once more, give Mr.", "\"그게, 정말이지,\" 트래들스가 명랑하게 말했다."], ["Micawber high praise. But for his having been so patient and persevering for so long a time, we never could have hoped to do anything worth speaking of.", "\"저는 다시 한번 미코버 씨를 한껏 칭찬해 드려야겠습니다. 그분이 그토록 오래 그렇게 참고 견디지 않았더라면, 우리는 입에 올릴 만한 일은 아무것도 해내지 못했을 겁니다."], ["And I think we ought to consider that Mr. Micawber did right, for right’s sake, when we reflect what terms he might have made with Uriah Heep himself, for his silence.’", "그리고 미코버 씨가 입을 다물어 주는 대가로 유라이어 히프 본인과 어떤 거래를 맺을 수도 있었을지를 헤아려 보면, 그분이 오로지 옳음을 위해 옳게 처신했다는 점은 우리가 인정해야 한다고 봅니다.\""]], [["‘I think so too,’ said I.", "\"나도 그렇게 생각해요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Now, what would you give him?’ inquired my aunt.", "\"자, 그분께 얼마나 드리실 건가요?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Before you come to that,’ said Traddles, a little disconcerted, ‘I am afraid I thought it discreet to omit (not being able to carry everything before me) two points, in making this lawless adjustment--for it’s perfectly lawless from beginning to end--of a difficult affair.", "거기에 이르기 전에 말입니다,\" 트래들스가 약간 머쓱해하며 말했다. \"이 어려운 일을 이렇게 법에 어긋나게 매듭짓느라 — 처음부터 끝까지 완전히 법에 어긋나는 일이지요 — 두 가지를 (제 뜻대로 죄다 밀어붙일 수가 없어서) 빼놓는 게 신중하겠다 싶었던 것 같습니다."], ["Those I.O.U.’s, and so forth, which Mr. Micawber gave him for the advances he had--’", "미코버 씨가 받은 선금에 대해 그자에게 써 준 그 차용증서 따위 말입니다 —\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"그건!"], ["They must be paid,’ said my aunt.", "갚아야지요.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, but I don’t know when they may be proceeded on, or where they are,’ rejoined Traddles, opening his eyes; ‘and I anticipate, that, between this time and his departure, Mr.", "\"네, 하지만 언제 그게 청구될지, 또 그게 어디 있는지를 모르겠습니다.\" 트래들스가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 받았다."], ["Micawber will be constantly arrested, or taken in execution.’", "\"그리고 제 짐작으로는, 지금부터 출발할 때까지 미코버 씨는 줄곧 체포되거나 강제 집행을 당할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Then he must be constantly set free again, and taken out of execution,’ said my aunt.", "\"그러면 줄곧 다시 풀어 주고, 강제 집행에서 빼내 와야지요.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘What’s the amount altogether?’", "\"다 합해서 얼마인가요?\""]], [["‘Why, Mr.", "\"그게, 미코버 씨가 그 거래들을 — 본인은 거래라고 부릅니다만 — 거창한 격식을 차려 장부에 적어 두었더군요.\""], ["Micawber has entered the transactions--he calls them transactions--with great form, in a book,’ rejoined Traddles, smiling; ‘and he makes the amount a hundred and three pounds, five.’", "트래들스가 빙그레 웃으며 받았다. \"그분 계산으로는 그 액수가 백삼 파운드 오 실링입니다.\""]], [["‘Now, what shall we give him, that sum included?’ said my aunt.", "\"자, 그럼 그 액수까지 포함해서 그분께 얼마를 드릴까요?\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Agnes, my dear, you and I can talk about division of it afterwards.", "\"아그네스, 얘야, 그걸 어떻게 나눌지는 너랑 나랑 나중에 얘기하자."], ["What should it be?", "얼마면 좋을까요?"], ["Five hundred pounds?’", "오백 파운드?\""]], [["Upon this, Traddles and I both struck in at once.", "이 말에 트래들스와 나는 둘 다 동시에 끼어들었다."], ["We both recommended a small sum in money, and the payment, without stipulation to Mr. Micawber, of the Uriah claims as they came in.", "우리는 둘 다 현금으로는 소액을 드리고, 유라이어 쪽 청구가 들어오는 대로 미코버 씨에게 따로 조건을 달지 않고 갚아 주자고 권했다."], ["We proposed that the family should have their passage and their outfit, and a hundred pounds; and that Mr.", "우리는 그 가족에게 뱃삯과 채비 비용, 그리고 백 파운드를 마련해 주자고 제안했다."], ["Micawber’s arrangement for the repayment of the advances should be gravely entered into, as it might be wholesome for him to suppose himself under that responsibility. To this, I added the suggestion, that I should give some explanation of his character and history to Mr.", "또한 선금 상환에 관한 미코버 씨의 약정은 진지하게 받아들여 두자고 했는데, 그 책임 아래 있다고 스스로 여기는 편이 그에게 이로울 수도 있기 때문이었다."], ["Peggotty, who I knew could be relied on; and that to Mr. Peggotty should be quietly entrusted the discretion of advancing another hundred. I further proposed to interest Mr.", "여기에 나는, 믿을 만한 사람임을 내가 아는 패거티 씨에게 그의 성품과 내력을 어느 정도 설명해 두고, 다시 백 파운드를 더 융통해 줄지 여부는 패거티 씨의 재량에 조용히 맡기자는 제안을 보탰다."], ["Micawber in Mr. Peggotty, by confiding so much of Mr. Peggotty’s story to him as I might feel justified in relating, or might think expedient; and to endeavour to bring each of them to bear upon the other, for the common advantage.", "나아가 나는, 정당하다고 느껴지거나 적절하다고 여겨지는 만큼만 패거티 씨의 사연을 미코버 씨에게 털어놓음으로써 미코버 씨가 패거티 씨에게 관심을 갖게 하고, 두 사람이 서로에게 보탬이 되도록 이어 주려 애써 보자고 제안했다."], ["We all entered warmly into these views; and I may mention at once, that the principals themselves did so, shortly afterwards, with perfect good will and harmony.", "우리는 모두 이 견해에 흔쾌히 뜻을 모았다. 그리고 곧장 한마디 덧붙이자면, 당사자들 본인도 얼마 뒤 더없이 좋은 뜻과 화목함으로 거기에 동의했다."]], [["Seeing that Traddles now glanced anxiously at my aunt again, I reminded him of the second and last point to which he had adverted.", "트래들스가 다시 고모님 쪽을 불안하게 흘끗거리는 것을 보고, 나는 그가 앞서 넌지시 말한 그 두 번째이자 마지막 사안을 그에게 일깨워 주었다."]], [["‘You and your aunt will excuse me, Copperfield, if I touch upon a painful theme, as I greatly fear I shall,’ said Traddles, hesitating; ‘but I think it necessary to bring it to your recollection.", "\"자네와 자네 고모님께서 양해해 주시게, 코퍼필드. 내가 무척 두려워하는 대로 아무래도 아픈 화제를 건드리게 될 것 같으니 말이야.\" 트래들스가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["On the day of Mr. Micawber’s memorable denunciation a threatening allusion was made by Uriah Heep to your aunt’s--husband.’", "\"하지만 이건 기억에 되살려 드리는 게 필요하다고 봐. 미코버 씨가 그 잊지 못할 폭로를 하던 날, 유라이어 히프가 자네 고모님의 — 남편을 두고 위협조의 말을 흘렸거든.\""]], [["My aunt, retaining her stiff position, and apparent composure, assented with a nod.", "고모님은 그 뻣뻣한 자세와 겉으로의 침착함을 그대로 지킨 채, 고개를 끄덕여 그렇다고 했다."]], [["‘Perhaps,’ observed Traddles, ‘it was mere purposeless impertinence?’", "\"어쩌면,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"그건 그저 아무 뜻 없는 무례였을지도 모르지요?\""]], [["‘No,’ returned my aunt.", "\"아니에요.\" 고모님이 받았다."]], [["‘There was--pardon me--really such a person, and at all in his power?’ hinted Traddles.", "\"정말로 — 실례입니다만 — 그런 사람이 있고, 그것도 그자가 얼마간 손아귀에 쥐고 있는 사람이었다는 건가요?\" 트래들스가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, my good friend,’ said my aunt.", "\"그래요, 친애하는 친구.\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["Traddles, with a perceptible lengthening of his face, explained that he had not been able to approach this subject; that it had shared the fate of Mr. Micawber’s liabilities, in not being comprehended in the terms he had made; that we were no longer of any authority with Uriah Heep; and that if he could do us, or any of us, any injury or annoyance, no doubt he would.", "트래들스는 눈에 띄게 얼굴이 길어진 채, 자기로서는 이 문제에 손을 댈 수가 없었다고 설명했다. 그것은 미코버 씨의 채무와 같은 운명을 겪어, 그가 맺은 조건 안에 포함되지 못했고, 우리는 이제 유라이어 히프에 대해 아무런 권한도 없으며, 그자가 우리에게, 또는 우리 중 누구에게든 해코지나 골칫거리를 끼칠 수 있다면 틀림없이 그렇게 하리라는 것이었다."]], [["My aunt remained quiet; until again some stray tears found their way to her cheeks.", "고모님은 조용히 있었다. 그러다 또다시 흐르는 눈물 몇 방울이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["‘You are quite right,’ she said.", "\"당신 말이 옳아요.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘It was very thoughtful to mention it.’", "\"그 얘기를 꺼내 준 건 참 사려 깊은 일이었어요.\""]], [["‘Can I--or Copperfield--do anything?’ asked Traddles, gently.", "\"제가 — 아니면 코퍼필드가 — 무얼 좀 할 수 있을까요?\" 트래들스가 부드럽게 물었다."]], [["‘Nothing,’ said my aunt.", "\"아무것도 없어요.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘I thank you many times.", "\"정말 여러 번 고마워요."], ["Trot, my dear, a vain threat!", "트롯, 얘야, 헛된 협박이란다!"], ["Let us have Mr. and Mrs. Micawber back.", "미코버 씨 부부를 다시 들어오게 하자."], ["And don’t any of you speak to me!’ With that she smoothed her dress, and sat, with her upright carriage, looking at the door.", "그리고 너희들 누구도 나한테 말 걸지 마라!\" 그러면서 고모님은 옷매무새를 매만지고, 꼿꼿한 자세로 앉아 문 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Well, Mr. and Mrs.", "\"자, 미코버 씨, 미코버 부인!\""], ["Micawber!’ said my aunt, when they entered.", "그들이 들어서자 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘We have been discussing your emigration, with many apologies to you for keeping you out of the room so long; and I’ll tell you what arrangements we propose.’", "\"우리가 두 분의 이주 문제를 의논하고 있었답니다. 두 분을 그토록 오래 방 밖에 붙들어 둔 것은 거듭 사과드려요. 우리가 제안하는 약정이 무엇인지 말씀드리지요.\""]], [["These she explained to the unbounded satisfaction of the family,--children and all being then present,--and so much to the awakening of Mr.", "고모님이 그것을 풀어 설명하니, 그 가족은 — 그때는 아이들까지 다 함께 있었는데 — 더할 나위 없이 흡족해했다."], ["Micawber’s punctual habits in the opening stage of all bill transactions, that he could not be dissuaded from immediately rushing out, in the highest spirits, to buy the stamps for his notes of hand.", "그리고 그것은 모든 어음 거래의 첫 단계에 나타나는 미코버 씨의 그 어김없는 버릇을 어찌나 일깨웠던지, 그가 그 자리에서 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 약속어음에 붙일 인지를 사러 뛰쳐나가는 것을 도무지 말릴 수가 없었다."], ["But, his joy received a sudden check; for within five minutes, he returned in the custody of a sheriff’s officer, informing us, in a flood of tears, that all was lost.", "그러나 그의 기쁨은 별안간 제동이 걸렸다. 오 분도 채 안 되어 그가 보안관 관리에게 붙들린 채 돌아와, 눈물을 쏟으며 모든 게 끝장났다고 우리에게 알렸기 때문이다."], ["We, being quite prepared for this event, which was of course a proceeding of Uriah Heep’s, soon paid the money; and in five minutes more Mr. Micawber was seated at the table, filling up the stamps with an expression of perfect joy, which only that congenial employment, or the making of punch, could impart in full completeness to his shining face.", "우리는 이 일에 — 물론 유라이어 히프의 짓이었다 — 단단히 대비하고 있던 터라 곧 돈을 치렀고, 다시 오 분 뒤 미코버 씨는 탁자에 앉아 더없는 기쁨의 표정으로 인지를 채워 넣고 있었다. 그것은 오직 그렇게 마음에 꼭 드는 일, 또는 펀치를 만드는 일만이 그의 빛나는 얼굴에 온전히 가득 채워 줄 수 있는 표정이었다."], ["To see him at work on the stamps, with the relish of an artist, touching them like pictures, looking at them sideways, taking weighty notes of dates and amounts in his pocket-book, and contemplating them when finished, with a high sense of their precious value, was a sight indeed.", "그가 예술가의 흥취로 인지에 매달려, 그것을 그림처럼 어루만지고, 곁눈질로 들여다보고, 날짜며 액수를 수첩에 묵직하게 적어 두고, 다 채운 뒤에는 그 귀한 값어치를 한껏 음미하며 들여다보는 모습은, 그야말로 볼만한 광경이었다."]], [["‘Now, the best thing you can do, sir, if you’ll allow me to advise you,’ said my aunt, after silently observing him, ‘is to abjure that occupation for evermore.’", "\"자, 선생님, 제가 한마디 권해도 좋다면, 선생님이 하실 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은요,\" 고모님이 그를 잠자코 지켜보다가 말했다. \"그 일을 다시는 영영 입에 올리지 않겠다고 맹세하는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Madam,’ replied Mr.", "\"부인,\""], ["Micawber, ‘it is my intention to register such a vow on the virgin page of the future. Mrs.", "미코버 씨가 대답했다. \"그런 맹세를 미래의 처녀처럼 깨끗한 페이지에 새겨 넣을 작정입니다."], ["Micawber will attest it.", "미코버 부인이 증언해 줄 것입니다."], ["I trust,’ said Mr.", "저는 믿습니다,\""], ["Micawber, solemnly, ‘that my son Wilkins will ever bear in mind, that he had infinitely better put his fist in the fire, than use it to handle the serpents that have poisoned the life-blood of his unhappy parent!’ Deeply affected, and changed in a moment to the image of despair, Mr.", "미코버 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"제 아들 윌킨스가, 불행한 제 아비의 피를 독으로 물들인 그 뱀들을 손으로 다루느니 차라리 제 주먹을 불 속에 넣는 편이 한없이 낫다는 것을, 두고두고 마음에 새기기를 말입니다!\""], ["Micawber regarded the serpents with a look of gloomy abhorrence (in which his late admiration of them was not quite subdued), folded them up and put them in his pocket.", "깊은 감회에 젖어 한순간에 절망의 화신으로 변한 미코버 씨는, 그 뱀들을 음울한 혐오의 눈길로 (그 안에는 방금 전 그가 그것들에 보내던 감탄이 채 가시지 않은 채였다) 바라보더니, 그것들을 접어 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["This closed the proceedings of the evening.", "이로써 그날 저녁의 일은 마무리되었다."], ["We were weary with sorrow and fatigue, and my aunt and I were to return to London on the morrow.", "우리는 슬픔과 피로에 지쳐 있었고, 고모님과 나는 이튿날 런던으로 돌아가기로 되어 있었다."], ["It was arranged that the Micawbers should follow us, after effecting a sale of their goods to a broker; that Mr. Wickfield’s affairs should be brought to a settlement, with all convenient speed, under the direction of Traddles; and that Agnes should also come to London, pending those arrangements.", "미코버 씨 가족은 가재도구를 중개상에게 팔아넘긴 뒤 우리를 뒤따라오기로 했고, 위크필드 씨의 일은 트래들스의 지휘 아래 되도록 빨리 매듭짓기로 했으며, 그 정리가 진행되는 동안 아그네스도 런던으로 오기로 했다."], ["We passed the night at the old house, which, freed from the presence of the Heeps, seemed purged of a disease; and I lay in my old room, like a shipwrecked wanderer come home.", "우리는 그 옛집에서 하룻밤을 보냈는데, 히프 모자가 사라지고 나니 마치 병이 씻겨 나간 듯했다. 그리고 나는 난파해 떠돌다 집에 돌아온 나그네처럼 내 옛 방에 누웠다."]], [["We went back next day to my aunt’s house--not to mine--and when she and I sat alone, as of old, before going to bed, she said:", "이튿날 우리는 — 내 집이 아니라 — 고모님 댁으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 옛날처럼 잠자리에 들기 전 고모님과 단둘이 앉았을 때, 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Trot, do you really wish to know what I have had upon my mind lately?’", "\"트롯, 요즘 내 마음을 짓누르던 게 무엇인지 정말로 알고 싶으냐?\""]], [["‘Indeed I do, aunt. If there ever was a time when I felt unwilling that you should have a sorrow or anxiety which I could not share, it is now.’", "\"정말 알고 싶어요, 고모님. 제가 함께 나눌 수 없는 슬픔이나 근심을 고모님이 안고 계신 게 못마땅하게 느껴진 때가 있었다면, 바로 지금이에요.\""]], [["‘You have had sorrow enough, child,’ said my aunt, affectionately, ‘without the addition of my little miseries.", "\"넌 이미 슬픔을 겪을 만큼 겪었잖니, 얘야.\" 고모님이 다정하게 말했다. \"거기에 내 자잘한 근심까지 보태지 않더라도 말이다."], ["I could have no other motive, Trot, in keeping anything from you.’", "트롯, 너한테 뭔가를 숨긴 데에 다른 뜻이 있었을 리 없어.\""]], [["‘I know that well,’ said I.", "\"저도 그건 잘 알아요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘But tell me now.’", "\"그러니 이제 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["‘Would you ride with me a little way tomorrow morning?’ asked my aunt.", "\"내일 아침에 나랑 잠깐 마차 타고 같이 나가 주겠니?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["‘Of course.’", "\"물론이죠.\""]], [["‘At nine,’ said she.", "\"아홉 시에.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘I’ll tell you then, my dear.’", "\"그때 말해 주마, 얘야.\""]], [["At nine, accordingly, we went out in a little chariot, and drove to London.", "그리하여 아홉 시에 우리는 작은 사륜마차를 타고 나가 런던으로 향했다."], ["We drove a long way through the streets, until we came to one of the large hospitals.", "우리는 거리를 한참 달려, 큰 병원 가운데 하나에 이르렀다."], ["Standing hard by the building was a plain hearse.", "그 건물 바로 곁에 수수한 영구차 한 대가 서 있었다."], ["The driver recognized my aunt, and, in obedience to a motion of her hand at the window, drove slowly off; we following.", "마부가 고모님을 알아보고는, 창가에서 고모님이 손짓하자 그에 따라 천천히 차를 몰기 시작했고, 우리는 그 뒤를 따라갔다."]], [["‘You understand it now, Trot,’ said my aunt.", "\"이제 알겠지, 트롯.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘He is gone!’", "\"그 사람이 갔단다!\""]], [["‘Did he die in the hospital?’", "\"병원에서 돌아가셨어요?\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["She sat immovable beside me; but, again I saw the stray tears on her face.", "고모님은 내 곁에 꼼짝없이 앉아 있었다. 그러나 나는 또다시 그 얼굴에 흐르는 눈물을 보았다."]], [["‘He was there once before,’ said my aunt presently.", "\"전에도 한 번 거기 있었지.\" 이윽고 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘He was ailing a long time--a shattered, broken man, these many years.", "\"오래 앓았어 — 부서지고 망가진 사람이었지, 이 숱한 세월 동안."], ["When he knew his state in this last illness, he asked them to send for me.", "이번 마지막 병에서 제 처지를 깨닫고는, 나를 불러 달라고 하더구나."], ["He was sorry then.", "그때는 미안해했어."], ["Very sorry.’", "무척 미안해했지.\""]], [["‘You went, I know, aunt.’", "\"고모님이 가셨던 거군요, 알겠어요.\""]], [["‘I went.", "\"갔지."], ["I was with him a good deal afterwards.’", "그 뒤로 그 사람 곁에 꽤 오래 있어 주었단다.\""]], [["‘He died the night before we went to Canterbury?’ said I.", "\"우리가 캔터베리로 떠나기 전날 밤에 돌아가신 건가요?\" 내가 말했다."], ["My aunt nodded.", "고모님이 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["‘No one can harm him now,’ she said.", "\"이제 아무도 그 사람을 해칠 수 없어.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘It was a vain threat.’", "\"그건 헛된 협박이었지.\""]], [["We drove away, out of town, to the churchyard at Hornsey.", "우리는 시내를 벗어나 혼지의 교회 묘지로 마차를 몰았다."], ["‘Better here than in the streets,’ said my aunt.", "\"거리보다는 여기가 낫지.\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘He was born here.’", "\"그 사람은 여기서 태어났거든.\""]], [["We alighted; and followed the plain coffin to a corner I remember well, where the service was read consigning it to the dust.", "우리는 마차에서 내렸다. 그러고는 그 수수한 관을 따라, 내가 잘 기억하는 한 구석으로 갔고, 거기서 관을 흙에 맡기는 예배가 낭독되었다."]], [["‘Six-and-thirty years ago, this day, my dear,’ said my aunt, as we walked back to the chariot, ‘I was married.", "\"오늘로 삼십육 년 전이로구나, 얘야.\" 우리가 마차로 걸어 돌아가는 동안 고모님이 말했다. \"내가 결혼한 날이."], ["God forgive us all!’ We took our seats in silence; and so she sat beside me for a long time, holding my hand.", "하느님, 우리 모두를 용서하소서!\" 우리는 말없이 자리에 앉았다. 그렇게 고모님은 내 손을 잡은 채 오랫동안 내 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["At length she suddenly burst into tears, and said:", "마침내 고모님은 별안간 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다."]], [["‘He was a fine-looking man when I married him, Trot--and he was sadly changed!’", "\"내가 그 사람과 결혼할 때만 해도 그이는 잘생긴 남자였단다, 트롯 — 그런데 어찌나 딱하게 변했던지!\""]], [["It did not last long.", "그것은 오래가지 않았다."], ["After the relief of tears, she soon became composed, and even cheerful.", "눈물로 한바탕 마음을 덜어 내고 나자, 고모님은 곧 평정을 되찾았고, 심지어 명랑해지기까지 했다."], ["Her nerves were a little shaken, she said, or she would not have given way to it. God forgive us all!", "신경이 좀 흔들렸던 거라고, 안 그랬으면 그렇게 무너지지는 않았을 거라고 고모님은 말했다. 하느님, 우리 모두를 용서하소서!"]], [["So we rode back to her little cottage at Highgate, where we found the following short note, which had arrived by that morning’s post from Mr. Micawber:", "그렇게 우리는 하이게이트의 고모님 작은 오두막으로 마차를 몰아 돌아왔는데, 거기서 미코버 씨가 그날 아침 우편으로 부쳐 온 다음의 짧은 쪽지를 받았다."]], [["‘Canterbury,", "'캔터베리,"]], [["‘Friday.", "'금요일."]], [["‘My dear Madam, and Copperfield,", "'친애하는 부인, 그리고 코퍼필드 군에게,"]], [["‘The fair land of promise lately looming on the horizon is again enveloped in impenetrable mists, and for ever withdrawn from the eyes of a drifting wretch whose Doom is sealed!", "'요사이 지평선 위로 어렴풋이 떠오르던 그 아름다운 약속의 땅이 다시금 뚫을 수 없는 안개에 휩싸여, 운명이 봉인된 한 표류하는 가련한 자의 눈앞에서 영영 거두어졌소!"]], [["‘Another writ has been issued (in His Majesty’s High Court of King’s Bench at Westminster), in another cause of HEEP V. MICAWBER, and the defendant in that cause is the prey of the sheriff having legal jurisdiction in this bailiwick.", "'또 한 장의 영장이 (웨스트민스터의 국왕 폐하 고등 왕좌 재판소에서) 또 한 건의 소송, 즉 히프 대 미코버 사건으로 발부되었고, 그 사건의 피고는 이 관할 구역에 법적 권한을 지닌 보안관의 먹잇감이 되었소. '이제 그날이요, 이제 그 시각이라,"]], [["‘Now’s the day, and now’s the hour, See the front of battle lower, See approach proud EDWARD’S power-- Chains and slavery!", "싸움의 진영이 음산하게 다가오니,\n보라, 오만한 에드워드의 군세가 밀려온다 —"]], [["‘Consigned to which, and to a speedy end (for mental torture is not supportable beyond a certain point, and that point I feel I have attained), my course is run. Bless you, bless you!", "쇠사슬과 노예살이를! '그것에, 그리고 머지않은 종말에 넘겨진 채 (정신의 고문이란 어느 한계를 넘어서는 견딜 수 없는 법인데, 그 한계에 내가 다다랐음을 나는 느끼오) 나의 행로는 끝이 났소."], ["Some future traveller, visiting, from motives of curiosity, not unmingled, let us hope, with sympathy, the place of confinement allotted to debtors in this city, may, and I trust will, Ponder, as he traces on its wall, inscribed with a rusty nail,", "부디 평안하시기를, 평안하시기를! 훗날 어느 길손이, 호기심에서, 부디 동정이 섞이지 않았기를 바라지는 않겠소만, 이 도시에서 채무자들에게 배정된 그 갇힌 자리를 찾았을 때, 녹슨 못으로 벽에 새겨진 글자를 더듬으며, 부디 곰곰이 헤아려 주기를 나는 믿소,"]], [["‘The obscure initials,", "'그 흐릿한 머리글자,"]], [["‘P.S. I re-open this to say that our common friend, Mr.", "'W. M. 을. '추신."], ["Thomas Traddles (who has not yet left us, and is looking extremely well), has paid the debt and costs, in the noble name of Miss Trotwood; and that myself and family are at the height of earthly bliss.’", "우리 공동의 벗인 토머스 트래들스 씨가 (그는 아직 우리 곁을 떠나지 않았고, 더없이 좋아 보이오) 트롯우드 양의 고귀한 이름으로 빚과 비용을 갚아 주었음을 알리고자 이 편지를 다시 펴오. 그리하여 나와 내 가족은 이 세상 더없는 행복의 꼭대기에 올라 있소.'"]], [["# CHAPTER 55. TEMPEST", "# CHAPTER 55. TEMPEST"]], [["I now approach an event in my life, so indelible, so awful, so bound by an infinite variety of ties to all that has preceded it, in these pages, that, from the beginning of my narrative, I have seen it growing larger and larger as I advanced, like a great tower in a plain, and throwing its fore-cast shadow even on the incidents of my childish days.", "이제 나는 내 인생의 한 사건에 다가선다. 너무도 또렷하게 새겨졌고, 너무도 끔찍하며, 이 책에서 그 앞에 펼쳐진 모든 일과 무한히 다양한 끈으로 얽혀 있어서, 이야기를 시작한 순간부터 글을 써 나가는 동안 그것이 점점 더 크게 자라나는 것을 나는 보아 왔다. 마치 평원 위에 우뚝 선 거대한 탑처럼, 그것은 내 어린 시절의 자잘한 일들에까지 미리 그림자를 드리웠다."]], [["For years after it occurred, I dreamed of it often.", "그 일이 일어난 뒤로 여러 해 동안 나는 그것을 자주 꿈에 보았다."], ["I have started up so vividly impressed by it, that its fury has yet seemed raging in my quiet room, in the still night.", "어찌나 생생하게 그 인상에 사로잡혀 화들짝 깨어났던지, 고요한 밤 내 조용한 방 안에서 그 광란이 여태 휘몰아치는 듯했다."], ["I dream of it sometimes, though at lengthened and uncertain intervals, to this hour.", "지금 이 시각까지도, 비록 길고 들쭉날쭉한 간격을 두고서나마, 나는 이따금 그것을 꿈꾼다."], ["I have an association between it and a stormy wind, or the lightest mention of a sea-shore, as strong as any of which my mind is conscious.", "그것과 사나운 바람, 또는 바닷가를 스치듯 입에 올리는 가장 가벼운 한마디 사이에는 어떤 연결고리가 있는데, 내 마음이 의식하는 그 어떤 연결보다도 강하다."], ["As plainly as I behold what happened, I will try to write it down.", "그 일이 일어난 광경을 내가 똑똑히 보는 만큼, 그대로 적어 보려 한다."], ["I do not recall it, but see it done; for it happens again before me.", "나는 그것을 기억해 내는 게 아니라, 일어나는 그대로 본다. 그것이 내 눈앞에서 다시 벌어지고 있으니까."]], [["The time drawing on rapidly for the sailing of the emigrant-ship, my good old nurse (almost broken-hearted for me, when we first met) came up to London.", "이민선이 출항할 날이 빠르게 다가오자, 마음씨 고운 늙은 유모가 (우리가 처음 다시 만났을 때 나를 보고 거의 가슴이 찢어질 듯했던 사람이다) 런던으로 올라왔다."], ["I was constantly with her, and her brother, and the Micawbers (they being very much together); but Emily I never saw.", "나는 그녀와, 그녀의 오빠와, 미코버 식구들과 (그들은 늘 함께 어울려 다녔다) 줄곧 같이 지냈다. 그러나 에밀리만큼은 한 번도 보지 못했다."]], [["One evening when the time was close at hand, I was alone with Peggotty and her brother.", "날짜가 코앞에 닥친 어느 날 저녁, 나는 패거티와 그녀의 오빠와만 단둘이 아니 셋이 있었다."], ["Our conversation turned on Ham.", "우리 이야기는 햄에게로 흘러갔다."], ["She described to us how tenderly he had taken leave of her, and how manfully and quietly he had borne himself.", "그녀는 햄이 얼마나 다정하게 작별 인사를 건넸는지, 또 얼마나 사내답고 조용하게 처신했는지를 우리에게 들려주었다."], ["Most of all, of late, when she believed he was most tried.", "무엇보다, 가장 마음이 시달릴 거라고 그녀가 믿었던 요즈음에 더더욱 그랬다고 했다."], ["It was a subject of which the affectionate creature never tired; and our interest in hearing the many examples which she, who was so much with him, had to relate, was equal to hers in relating them.", "그 다정한 여인은 이 이야깃거리에 결코 싫증 내는 법이 없었다. 그리고 햄과 그토록 가까이 지내 온 그녀가 들려주는 숱한 사례를 듣는 우리의 관심도, 그것을 들려주는 그녀의 마음만큼이나 컸다."]], [["My aunt and I were at that time vacating the two cottages at Highgate; I intending to go abroad, and she to return to her house at Dover.", "그 무렵 고모님과 나는 하이게이트의 오두막 두 채를 비우는 중이었다. 나는 외국으로 떠날 작정이었고, 고모님은 도버의 집으로 돌아갈 참이었다."], ["We had a temporary lodging in Covent Garden.", "우리는 코번트 가든에 임시 거처를 두고 있었다."], ["As I walked home to it, after this evening’s conversation, reflecting on what had passed between Ham and myself when I was last at Yarmouth, I wavered in the original purpose I had formed, of leaving a letter for Emily when I should take leave of her uncle on board the ship, and thought it would be better to write to her now.", "그날 저녁 이야기를 마치고 그곳으로 걸어 돌아오면서, 내가 마지막으로 야머스에 갔을 때 햄과 나 사이에 오갔던 말을 곰곰이 떠올리다가, 처음에 세웠던 계획 — 배 위에서 그녀의 외삼촌과 작별할 때 에밀리에게 편지 한 통을 남기겠다는 — 이 마음에서 흔들렸고, 지금 그녀에게 써 보내는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 들었다."], ["She might desire, I thought, after receiving my communication, to send some parting word by me to her unhappy lover.", "내 글을 받고 나면 그녀가 불행한 연인에게 작별의 한마디라도 나를 통해 전하고 싶어 할지 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["I ought to give her the opportunity.", "나는 그 기회를 그녀에게 주어야 마땅했다."]], [["I therefore sat down in my room, before going to bed, and wrote to her.", "그래서 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 내 방에 앉아 그녀에게 편지를 썼다."], ["I told her that I had seen him, and that he had requested me to tell her what I have already written in its place in these sheets.", "나는 햄을 만났다는 것, 그리고 햄이 이 종이 위 제자리에 이미 적어 둔 그 말을 그녀에게 전해 달라고 내게 부탁했다는 것을 말했다."], ["I faithfully repeated it.", "나는 그 말을 고스란히 되풀이했다."], ["I had no need to enlarge upon it, if I had had the right.", "설령 내게 그럴 권리가 있었더라도, 거기에 군말을 보탤 필요는 없었다."], ["Its deep fidelity and goodness were not to be adorned by me or any man.", "그 깊은 진심과 선량함은 나든 그 누구든 꾸밀 수 있는 것이 아니었다."], ["I left it out, to be sent round in the morning; with a line to Mr. Peggotty, requesting him to give it to her; and went to bed at daybreak.", "나는 아침에 부쳐지도록 편지를 따로 빼 두었고, 패거티 씨에게 그것을 그녀에게 전해 달라고 청하는 짧은 글도 함께 적었다. 그러고는 동틀 무렵에야 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["I was weaker than I knew then; and, not falling asleep until the sun was up, lay late, and unrefreshed, next day.", "그때 나는 내가 아는 것보다 더 쇠약해져 있었다. 해가 떠오를 때까지 잠들지 못한 탓에, 이튿날은 늦도록 누워 있었고 잠을 자고도 개운치 않았다."], ["I was roused by the silent presence of my aunt at my bedside.", "나는 머리맡에 말없이 와 서 있는 고모님의 기척에 잠에서 깼다."], ["I felt it in my sleep, as I suppose we all do feel such things.", "잠결에 그것을 느꼈는데, 우리 모두 그런 것은 그렇게 느끼는 모양이다."]], [["‘Trot, my dear,’ she said, when I opened my eyes, ‘I couldn’t make up my mind to disturb you. Mr.", "\"트롯, 얘야,\" 내가 눈을 뜨자 고모님이 말했다. \"도무지 너를 깨울 마음이 안 나더구나."], ["Peggotty is here; shall he come up?’", "패거티 씨가 와 계신다. 올라오라고 할까?\""]], [["I replied yes, and he soon appeared.", "나는 그러라고 대답했고, 그는 곧 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy,’ he said, when we had shaken hands, ‘I giv Em’ly your letter, sir, and she writ this heer; and begged of me fur to ask you to read it, and if you see no hurt in’t, to be so kind as take charge on’t.’", "\"데이비 도련님,\" 우리가 악수를 나눈 뒤 그가 말했다. \"에밀리한테 도련님 편지를 줬구먼유. 그랬더니 그 애가 이걸 써서는, 도련님더러 읽어 봐 주십사 부탁하더라구유. 그래서 거기 거리낄 게 없거든, 부디 맡아 주십사 하던데유.\""]], [["‘Have you read it?’ said I.", "\"읽어 봤어요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["He nodded sorrowfully.", "그는 슬프게 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["I opened it, and read as follows:", "나는 편지를 펼쳐서 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["‘I have got your message.", "\"보내 주신 말씀 받았어요."], ["Oh, what can I write, to thank you for your good and blessed kindness to me!", "아, 제게 베풀어 주신 그 선하고 복된 친절을 무어라 글로 적어 고마움을 전할 수 있을까요!"]], [["‘I have put the words close to my heart.", "저는 그 말씀을 가슴에 꼭 품었어요."], ["I shall keep them till I die.", "죽을 때까지 간직할 거예요."], ["They are sharp thorns, but they are such comfort.", "날카로운 가시 같지만, 그래도 그렇게 위안이 돼요."], ["I have prayed over them, oh, I have prayed so much.", "저는 그 말씀을 두고 기도했어요, 아, 정말 많이도 기도했어요."], ["When I find what you are, and what uncle is, I think what God must be, and can cry to him.", "당신이 어떤 분인지, 외삼촌이 어떤 분인지를 헤아릴 때면, 하느님이 어떤 분이실지를 떠올리며 그분께 울며 매달릴 수 있게 돼요."]], [["‘Good-bye for ever.", "영원히 안녕히."], ["Now, my dear, my friend, good-bye for ever in this world.", "이제, 내 소중한 분, 내 벗이여, 이 세상에서 영원히 안녕히."], ["In another world, if I am forgiven, I may wake a child and come to you.", "다른 세상에서, 혹 제가 용서받는다면, 어린아이로 깨어나 당신께 갈 수 있을지도 몰라요."], ["All thanks and blessings.", "온갖 고마움과 축복을 빌어요."], ["Farewell, evermore.’", "영원토록, 안녕히.\""]], [["This, blotted with tears, was the letter.", "눈물로 얼룩진 이것이 그 편지였다."]], [["‘May I tell her as you doen’t see no hurt in’t, and as you’ll be so kind as take charge on’t, Mas’r Davy?’ said Mr.", "\"도련님이 거기 거리낄 게 없다고 하시고, 또 부디 맡아 주시겠다고, 그 애한테 전해도 될깝쇼, 데이비 도련님?\" 내가 다 읽고 나자 패거티 씨가 말했다."], ["Peggotty, when I had read it.", "\"두말하면 잔소리지요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Unquestionably,’ said I--‘but I am thinking--’", "\"그런데 — 한 가지 생각하는 게 있어요 —\""]], [["‘Yes, Mas’r Davy?’", "\"네, 데이비 도련님?\""]], [["‘I am thinking,’ said I, ‘that I’ll go down again to Yarmouth.", "\"생각하는데,\" 내가 말했다. \"다시 한 번 야머스에 내려갔다 와야겠어요."], ["There’s time, and to spare, for me to go and come back before the ship sails.", "배가 떠나기 전에 다녀올 시간은 넉넉하고도 남아요."], ["My mind is constantly running on him, in his solitude; to put this letter of her writing in his hand at this time, and to enable you to tell her, in the moment of parting, that he has got it, will be a kindness to both of them.", "외톨이로 지내는 그 사람 생각이 자꾸만 머릿속을 떠나지 않아요. 그녀가 직접 쓴 이 편지를 바로 지금 그의 손에 쥐여 주고, 또 헤어지는 그 순간에 그녀에게 그가 편지를 받았노라고 당신이 전해 줄 수 있게 한다면, 그건 두 사람 모두에게 베푸는 친절일 거예요."], ["I solemnly accepted his commission, dear good fellow, and cannot discharge it too completely.", "나는 그의 부탁을 엄숙히 받아들였어요, 마음씨 고운 그 친구의 부탁을요. 그러니 아무리 빈틈없이 해내도 지나치지 않아요."], ["The journey is nothing to me.", "여행쯤은 내게 아무것도 아니에요."], ["I am restless, and shall be better in motion.", "나는 마음이 안절부절못하니, 몸을 움직이는 편이 차라리 나을 거예요."], ["I’ll go down tonight.’", "오늘 밤에 내려가겠어요.\""]], [["Though he anxiously endeavoured to dissuade me, I saw that he was of my mind; and this, if I had required to be confirmed in my intention, would have had the effect.", "그는 애써 나를 말리려 들었으나, 나는 그도 속으로는 나와 같은 마음임을 알았다. 그리고 이것은, 만일 내가 결심을 굳힐 확인이 필요했다면, 바로 그런 효과를 냈을 것이다."], ["He went round to the coach office, at my request, and took the box-seat for me on the mail.", "그는 내 부탁대로 역마차 사무소에 다녀와서, 우편마차 마부석 옆자리를 잡아 주었다."], ["In the evening I started, by that conveyance, down the road I had traversed under so many vicissitudes.", "저녁에 나는 그 마차를 타고, 그토록 많은 부침을 겪으며 오갔던 그 길을 따라 출발했다."]], [["‘Don’t you think that,’ I asked the coachman, in the first stage out of London, ‘a very remarkable sky?", "\"이게 말이오,\" 런던을 벗어난 첫 정거장에서 내가 마부에게 물었다. \"참 별난 하늘 아니오?"], ["I don’t remember to have seen one like it.’", "이런 하늘은 본 기억이 없는데.\""]], [["‘Nor I--not equal to it,’ he replied.", "\"나도 그렇소 — 이만한 건 처음이오.\""], ["‘That’s wind, sir.", "그가 대답했다."], ["There’ll be mischief done at sea, I expect, before long.’", "\"바람이오, 손님. 머지않아 바다에서 사달이 날 게요, 내 짐작엔.\""]], [["It was a murky confusion--here and there blotted with a colour like the colour of the smoke from damp fuel--of flying clouds, tossed up into most remarkable heaps, suggesting greater heights in the clouds than there were depths below them to the bottom of the deepest hollows in the earth, through which the wild moon seemed to plunge headlong, as if, in a dread disturbance of the laws of nature, she had lost her way and were frightened.", "그것은 음울한 뒤죽박죽이었다 — 군데군데 축축한 땔감에서 피어오르는 연기 빛깔로 얼룩진 — 날아다니는 구름이 기막히게 거대한 더미로 솟구쳐, 땅속 가장 깊은 골짜기 바닥까지의 깊이보다 구름 속의 높이가 더 까마득해 보였고, 그 사이로 거친 달이 곤두박질치듯 빠져들었다. 마치 자연의 법칙이 두려이 흐트러진 가운데 길을 잃고 겁에 질리기라도 한 듯이."], ["There had been a wind all day; and it was rising then, with an extraordinary great sound.", "하루 종일 바람이 불었는데, 그때 더 거세지면서 유난히 큰 소리를 내고 있었다."], ["In another hour it had much increased, and the sky was more overcast, and blew hard.", "한 시간이 더 지나자 바람은 한결 사나워졌고, 하늘은 더욱 흐려졌으며, 세차게 불어 댔다."]], [["But, as the night advanced, the clouds closing in and densely over-spreading the whole sky, then very dark, it came on to blow, harder and harder.", "그러나 밤이 깊어 갈수록 구름이 한데 모여들어 하늘 전체를 빽빽이 뒤덮자, 그때는 칠흑같이 어두웠고, 바람은 점점 더, 점점 더 사납게 몰아치기 시작했다."], ["It still increased, until our horses could scarcely face the wind.", "그것은 줄곧 거세져, 우리 말들이 좀처럼 바람을 마주하지 못할 지경에 이르렀다."], ["Many times, in the dark part of the night (it was then late in September, when the nights were not short), the leaders turned about, or came to a dead stop; and we were often in serious apprehension that the coach would be blown over.", "밤의 캄캄한 무렵 (때는 구월 하순이라 밤이 짧지 않았다)에 여러 번 선두의 말들이 몸을 돌리거나 딱 멈춰 섰고, 우리는 마차가 뒤집히지나 않을까 자주 심각하게 두려워했다."], ["Sweeping gusts of rain came up before this storm, like showers of steel; and, at those times, when there was any shelter of trees or lee walls to be got, we were fain to stop, in a sheer impossibility of continuing the struggle.", "휘몰아치는 빗줄기가 이 폭풍 앞에 들이닥쳤는데, 마치 강철 화살이 소나기처럼 쏟아지는 듯했다. 그럴 때, 나무나 바람막이 담 따위 몸을 피할 데가 조금이라도 있으면, 도무지 그 싸움을 더 이어 갈 수 없어 우리는 멈춰 서기를 청했다."]], [["When the day broke, it blew harder and harder.", "날이 밝자 바람은 점점 더, 점점 더 거세게 불었다."], ["I had been in Yarmouth when the seamen said it blew great guns, but I had never known the like of this, or anything approaching to it.", "나는 야머스에 있을 때 뱃사람들이 대포처럼 바람이 분다고 하는 말을 들은 적이 있었으나, 이런 바람은, 아니 이에 견줄 만한 무엇도 일찍이 알지 못했다."], ["We came to Ipswich--very late, having had to fight every inch of ground since we were ten miles out of London; and found a cluster of people in the market-place, who had risen from their beds in the night, fearful of falling chimneys.", "우리는 입스위치에 다다랐다 — 런던에서 십 마일 벗어난 뒤로 한 치 한 치 길을 싸워 가며 와야 했던 탓에 몹시 늦은 시각이었다 — 그리고 굴뚝이 무너질까 두려워 밤중에 잠자리에서 일어난 사람들이 장터에 한 무리 모여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Some of these, congregating about the inn-yard while we changed horses, told us of great sheets of lead having been ripped off a high church-tower, and flung into a by-street, which they then blocked up.", "그중 몇몇은 우리가 말을 바꾸는 동안 여관 마당에 모여서, 높은 교회 탑에서 커다란 납판이 뜯겨 나가 골목길로 내동댕이쳐져 그 길이 막혀 버렸다는 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["Others had to tell of country people, coming in from neighbouring villages, who had seen great trees lying torn out of the earth, and whole ricks scattered about the roads and fields.", "또 어떤 이들은, 이웃 마을에서 들어온 시골 사람들이 큰 나무들이 뿌리째 뽑혀 나뒹구는 모습이며, 낟가리가 통째로 길과 들판에 흩뿌려진 광경을 보았다고 전했다."], ["Still, there was no abatement in the storm, but it blew harder.", "그래도 폭풍은 누그러질 줄 모르고, 오히려 더 사납게 불어 댔다."]], [["As we struggled on, nearer and nearer to the sea, from which this mighty wind was blowing dead on shore, its force became more and more terrific.", "우리가 안간힘을 다해 바다 쪽으로, 이 거대한 바람이 곧장 뭍을 향해 불어오는 그 바다 쪽으로 점점 더 다가갈수록, 그 위력은 점점 더 무시무시해졌다."], ["Long before we saw the sea, its spray was on our lips, and showered salt rain upon us.", "바다가 보이기 한참 전부터 그 물보라가 우리 입술에 닿았고, 짠 비를 우리에게 뿌려 댔다."], ["The water was out, over miles and miles of the flat country adjacent to Yarmouth; and every sheet and puddle lashed its banks, and had its stress of little breakers setting heavily towards us.", "야머스에 잇닿은 평평한 시골 땅 수 마일, 수 마일에 걸쳐 물이 넘쳐 났다. 그리고 못이며 웅덩이마다 제 둑을 후려쳤고, 우리 쪽으로 묵직하게 밀려드는 작은 물결을 세차게 일으켰다."], ["When we came within sight of the sea, the waves on the horizon, caught at intervals above the rolling abyss, were like glimpses of another shore with towers and buildings.", "바다가 시야에 들어왔을 때, 출렁이는 심연 위로 이따금 솟구쳐 지평선에 걸린 파도는, 탑과 건물이 늘어선 또 다른 해안을 언뜻언뜻 보는 것 같았다."], ["When at last we got into the town, the people came out to their doors, all aslant, and with streaming hair, making a wonder of the mail that had come through such a night.", "마침내 우리가 마을 안으로 들어서자, 사람들이 죄다 비스듬히 몸을 기울이고 머리카락을 흩날리며 문간으로 나와서, 이런 밤을 뚫고 들어온 우편마차를 신기해했다."]], [["I put up at the old inn, and went down to look at the sea; staggering along the street, which was strewn with sand and seaweed, and with flying blotches of sea-foam; afraid of falling slates and tiles; and holding by people I met, at angry corners.", "나는 그 옛 여관에 들었다가, 바다를 보러 내려갔다. 모래와 해초와 흩날리는 물거품 얼룩으로 어지러운 거리를 비틀비틀 걸으며, 떨어지는 슬레이트와 기와가 무서워, 바람이 사납게 휘몰아치는 모퉁이에서는 마주치는 사람들을 붙잡으면서."], ["Coming near the beach, I saw, not only the boatmen, but half the people of the town, lurking behind buildings; some, now and then braving the fury of the storm to look away to sea, and blown sheer out of their course in trying to get zigzag back.", "해변 가까이 다가가니, 뱃사람들뿐 아니라 마을 사람들 절반이 건물 뒤에 숨어 있는 것이 보였다. 더러는 이따금 폭풍의 광란을 무릅쓰고 바다 쪽을 내다보았다가, 갈지자로 되돌아오려다 제 방향에서 완전히 밀려나기도 했다."]], [["Joining these groups, I found bewailing women whose husbands were away in herring or oyster boats, which there was too much reason to think might have foundered before they could run in anywhere for safety.", "이 무리에 끼어들고 보니, 청어잡이 배나 굴잡이 배를 타고 나간 남편을 둔 여인들이 통곡하고 있었다. 그 배들이 어디 안전한 데로 미처 피하기도 전에 가라앉았으리라고 여길 까닭이 너무도 많았기 때문이다. 사람들 틈에는 머리가 희끗희끗한 늙은 뱃사람들도 있어서, 바다에서 하늘로 눈을 옮기며 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 서로 무어라 중얼거렸다."], ["Grizzled old sailors were among the people, shaking their heads, as they looked from water to sky, and muttering to one another; ship-owners, excited and uneasy; children, huddling together, and peering into older faces; even stout mariners, disturbed and anxious, levelling their glasses at the sea from behind places of shelter, as if they were surveying an enemy.", "들뜨고 불안한 선주들도 있었다. 아이들은 한데 옹송그려서, 어른들 얼굴을 빤히 올려다보았다. 심지어 건장한 뱃사람들조차 동요하고 불안해하며, 몸을 피한 자리 뒤에서 마치 적을 살피기라도 하듯 바다를 향해 망원경을 겨누었다."]], [["The tremendous sea itself, when I could find sufficient pause to look at it, in the agitation of the blinding wind, the flying stones and sand, and the awful noise, confounded me.", "눈을 멀게 하는 바람과, 날아드는 돌과 모래와, 그 끔찍한 굉음의 소용돌이 속에서, 잠깐이나마 틈을 내어 바라볼 수 있었던 그 거대한 바다 자체가 나를 아연실색하게 만들었다. 높다란 물의 벽이 굽이쳐 밀려와, 그 절정에서 부서져 포말이 되어 무너질 때면, 그중 가장 작은 것 하나라도 마을을 통째로 삼켜 버릴 것만 같았다."], ["As the high watery walls came rolling in, and, at their highest, tumbled into surf, they looked as if the least would engulf the town.", "물러가는 파도가 쉰 목소리로 으르렁대며 휩쓸려 나갈 때면, 마치 땅을 파헤치려는 속셈이라도 있는 듯 해변에 깊은 동굴을 파내는 것 같았다."], ["As the receding wave swept back with a hoarse roar, it seemed to scoop out deep caves in the beach, as if its purpose were to undermine the earth.", "머리가 허연 너울 몇몇이 우레처럼 밀려들다가 뭍에 닿기도 전에 산산조각 부서질 때면, 방금까지 한 덩어리였던 그 파편 하나하나가 제 분노의 온 위력에 사로잡힌 듯, 또 다른 괴물의 몸뚱이로 합쳐지려고 내달렸다."], ["When some white-headed billows thundered on, and dashed themselves to pieces before they reached the land, every fragment of the late whole seemed possessed by the full might of its wrath, rushing to be gathered to the composition of another monster.", "굽이치는 언덕이 골짜기로 바뀌고, 굽이치는 골짜기는 (이따금 외로운 폭풍새 한 마리가 그 사이를 스쳐 갔다) 언덕으로 치솟았다. 물의 덩어리가 우르릉 소리를 내며 해변을 부르르 떨게 하고 뒤흔들었다. 모든 형상이 생겨나기 무섭게 어지러이 굴러가, 제 모습과 자리를 바꾸고, 또 다른 모습과 자리를 후려쳐 쫓아냈다."], ["Undulating hills were changed to valleys, undulating valleys (with a solitary storm-bird sometimes skimming through them) were lifted up to hills; masses of water shivered and shook the beach with a booming sound; every shape tumultuously rolled on, as soon as made, to change its shape and place, and beat another shape and place away; the ideal shore on the horizon, with its towers and buildings, rose and fell; the clouds fell fast and thick; I seemed to see a rending and upheaving of all nature.", "지평선 위 탑과 건물이 늘어선 그 환상의 해안이 솟았다 가라앉았다 했다. 구름은 빠르고 두텁게 내리덮였다. 나는 온 자연이 찢기고 솟구치는 광경을 보는 것만 같았다."]], [["Not finding Ham among the people whom this memorable wind--for it is still remembered down there, as the greatest ever known to blow upon that coast--had brought together, I made my way to his house.", "이 잊지 못할 바람이 — 그곳에서는 여태 그 해안에 불어닥친 가장 거센 바람으로 기억되고 있다 — 한데 불러 모은 사람들 틈에서 햄을 찾지 못하자, 나는 그의 집으로 가는 길을 더듬었다."], ["It was shut; and as no one answered to my knocking, I went, by back ways and by-lanes, to the yard where he worked.", "집은 잠겨 있었고, 두드려도 아무도 대답하지 않기에, 나는 뒷길과 골목을 돌아 그가 일하던 작업장으로 갔다."], ["I learned, there, that he had gone to Lowestoft, to meet some sudden exigency of ship-repairing in which his skill was required; but that he would be back tomorrow morning, in good time.", "거기서 나는 그가 자기 솜씨가 필요한 어떤 급한 배 수리 일이 생겨 그것을 처리하러 로스토프트로 갔으나, 이튿날 아침 넉넉한 시간에 돌아올 거라는 말을 들었다."]], [["I went back to the inn; and when I had washed and dressed, and tried to sleep, but in vain, it was five o’clock in the afternoon.", "나는 여관으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 씻고 옷을 갈아입고 잠을 청해 보았으나 헛수고였고, 그러는 사이 오후 다섯 시가 되었다."], ["I had not sat five minutes by the coffee-room fire, when the waiter, coming to stir it, as an excuse for talking, told me that two colliers had gone down, with all hands, a few miles away; and that some other ships had been seen labouring hard in the Roads, and trying, in great distress, to keep off shore.", "휴게실 난롯가에 앉은 지 오 분도 안 되어, 불을 쑤시러 온 웨이터가 말을 붙일 핑계 삼아, 몇 마일 떨어진 곳에서 석탄 운반선 두 척이 선원 전원과 함께 가라앉았다고 했다. 그리고 다른 배 몇 척도 만 어귀에서 안간힘을 쓰며, 안절부절못하는 가운데 뭍에서 떨어지려고 애쓰는 것이 목격되었다고 했다."], ["Mercy on them, and on all poor sailors, said he, if we had another night like the last!", "어젯밤 같은 밤이 또 닥치면, 그들에게, 그리고 가엾은 뱃사람들 모두에게 부디 자비가 있기를, 하고 그가 말했다."]], [["I was very much depressed in spirits; very solitary; and felt an uneasiness in Ham’s not being there, disproportionate to the occasion.", "나는 마음이 몹시 가라앉았다. 무척이나 외로웠다. 그리고 햄이 거기 없다는 사실에, 상황에 어울리지 않을 만큼 큰 불안을 느꼈다."], ["I was seriously affected, without knowing how much, by late events; and my long exposure to the fierce wind had confused me.", "나는 얼마나 깊이인지도 모른 채 최근의 일들에 심각하게 흔들려 있었고, 사나운 바람에 오래 노출된 탓에 머릿속이 어수선했다."], ["There was that jumble in my thoughts and recollections, that I had lost the clear arrangement of time and distance.", "내 생각과 기억이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽이었던지, 시간과 거리를 또렷이 가늠하던 능력을 잃어버렸다."], ["Thus, if I had gone out into the town, I should not have been surprised, I think, to encounter someone who I knew must be then in London.", "그래서 만일 내가 마을로 나갔다면, 그 순간 분명 런던에 있을 게 틀림없다고 알고 있는 누군가와 마주쳤더라도, 내 생각엔, 그리 놀라지 않았을 듯하다."], ["So to speak, there was in these respects a curious inattention in my mind.", "말하자면 이런 점에서 내 마음에는 묘한 부주의가 깃들어 있었다."], ["Yet it was busy, too, with all the remembrances the place naturally awakened; and they were particularly distinct and vivid.", "그러면서도 그 마음은, 그 장소가 자연히 일깨우는 온갖 추억으로 분주하기도 했다. 그리고 그 추억들은 유난히 또렷하고 생생했다."]], [["In this state, the waiter’s dismal intelligence about the ships immediately connected itself, without any effort of my volition, with my uneasiness about Ham.", "이런 상태에서, 배들에 관한 웨이터의 음울한 소식은, 내 의지의 어떤 노력도 없이, 곧장 햄에 대한 내 불안과 이어졌다."], ["I was persuaded that I had an apprehension of his returning from Lowestoft by sea, and being lost.", "나는 그가 로스토프트에서 바닷길로 돌아오다가 목숨을 잃을지 모른다는 예감에 사로잡혔다는 확신이 들었다."], ["This grew so strong with me, that I resolved to go back to the yard before I took my dinner, and ask the boat-builder if he thought his attempting to return by sea at all likely?", "이것이 내 안에서 어찌나 강하게 자라났던지, 나는 저녁을 들기 전에 작업장으로 다시 가서, 그가 바닷길로 돌아오려 할 가능성이 조금이라도 있다고 보는지 배 만드는 사람에게 물어봐야겠다고 마음먹었다."], ["If he gave me the least reason to think so, I would go over to Lowestoft and prevent it by bringing him with me.", "만일 그가 그럴 법하다고 여길 만한 빌미를 조금이라도 준다면, 나는 로스토프트로 건너가 그를 데리고 와서 그 일을 막겠다고 다짐했다."]], [["I hastily ordered my dinner, and went back to the yard.", "나는 서둘러 저녁을 시켜 놓고, 작업장으로 되돌아갔다."], ["I was none too soon; for the boat-builder, with a lantern in his hand, was locking the yard-gate.", "늦지 않게 갔으니 다행이었다. 배 만드는 사람이 손에 등불을 들고 작업장 문을 잠그던 참이었으니까."], ["He quite laughed when I asked him the question, and said there was no fear; no man in his senses, or out of them, would put off in such a gale of wind, least of all Ham Peggotty, who had been born to seafaring.", "그는 내가 그 질문을 던지자 한껏 웃어 보이며, 걱정할 것 없다고 했다. 제정신인 사람이든 아니든, 이런 폭풍 같은 바람 속에 배를 띄울 사람은 없다고, 더더구나 천성이 뱃사람으로 태어난 햄 패거티라면 어림없다고 했다."]], [["So sensible of this, beforehand, that I had really felt ashamed of doing what I was nevertheless impelled to do, I went back to the inn. If such a wind could rise, I think it was rising.", "이 점을 미리 잘 알고 있었던 터라, 그러면서도 떠밀리듯 한 그 짓이 정말이지 부끄럽게 느껴진 채로, 나는 여관으로 돌아왔다. 그런 바람이 더 거세질 수 있다면, 내 생각엔 정말로 거세지고 있었다."], ["The howl and roar, the rattling of the doors and windows, the rumbling in the chimneys, the apparent rocking of the very house that sheltered me, and the prodigious tumult of the sea, were more fearful than in the morning.", "울부짖고 으르렁대는 소리, 문과 창문이 덜컹대는 소리, 굴뚝 속에서 우르릉대는 소리, 나를 품어 주는 바로 그 집이 통째로 흔들리는 듯한 느낌, 그리고 바다의 어마어마한 아우성이 아침보다 한층 더 무시무시했다."], ["But there was now a great darkness besides; and that invested the storm with new terrors, real and fanciful.", "그러나 이제는 거기에 더해 짙은 어둠까지 깔려서, 그것이 폭풍에 새로운 공포를, 실재하는 공포와 허깨비 같은 공포를 함께 입혀 놓았다."]], [["I could not eat, I could not sit still, I could not continue steadfast to anything.", "먹을 수도, 가만히 앉아 있을 수도, 무엇 하나에 진득이 매달릴 수도 없었다."], ["Something within me, faintly answering to the storm without, tossed up the depths of my memory and made a tumult in them.", "내 안의 무언가가 바깥의 폭풍에 희미하게 화답하며, 내 기억의 밑바닥을 들쑤셔 그 속에 소란을 일으켰다."], ["Yet, in all the hurry of my thoughts, wild running with the thundering sea,--the storm, and my uneasiness regarding Ham were always in the fore-ground.", "그런데도, 우르릉대는 바다와 함께 거칠게 내달리는 내 모든 생각의 혼란 속에서 — 그 폭풍과, 햄에 대한 내 불안이 언제나 맨 앞에 자리하고 있었다."]], [["My dinner went away almost untasted, and I tried to refresh myself with a glass or two of wine.", "내 저녁상은 거의 손도 대지 않은 채 물려졌고, 나는 포도주 한두 잔으로 기운을 차려 보려 했다."], ["In vain. I fell into a dull slumber before the fire, without losing my consciousness, either of the uproar out of doors, or of the place in which I was.", "헛일이었다. 나는 난롯가에서 멍하니 선잠에 빠져들었으나, 바깥의 아우성도, 내가 있는 곳도 의식을 놓치지는 않았다."], ["Both became overshadowed by a new and indefinable horror; and when I awoke--or rather when I shook off the lethargy that bound me in my chair--my whole frame thrilled with objectless and unintelligible fear.", "그 둘 다 새롭고 형언할 수 없는 공포에 가려지더니, 내가 깨어났을 때 — 아니, 의자에 나를 옭아매던 그 무기력을 떨쳐 냈을 때 — 온몸이 까닭도 알 수 없고 정체도 모를 두려움에 부르르 떨렸다."]], [["I walked to and fro, tried to read an old gazetteer, listened to the awful noises: looked at faces, scenes, and figures in the fire.", "나는 이리저리 서성거렸고, 낡은 지명 사전을 읽어 보려 했으며, 그 끔찍한 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 불 속에서 얼굴들과 광경과 형체들을 들여다보았다."], ["At length, the steady ticking of the undisturbed clock on the wall tormented me to that degree that I resolved to go to bed.", "마침내, 벽에 걸린 시계가 아무 동요 없이 째깍거리는 그 한결같은 소리가 어찌나 나를 괴롭히던지, 나는 잠자리에 들기로 마음먹었다."]], [["It was reassuring, on such a night, to be told that some of the inn-servants had agreed together to sit up until morning.", "이런 밤에, 여관 하인 몇이 의논 끝에 아침까지 함께 깨어 있기로 했다는 말을 들으니 마음이 놓였다."], ["I went to bed, exceedingly weary and heavy; but, on my lying down, all such sensations vanished, as if by magic, and I was broad awake, with every sense refined.", "나는 몹시 지치고 무거운 몸으로 잠자리에 들었으나, 막상 눕고 나니 그런 느낌이 마법처럼 죄다 사라지고, 나는 모든 감각이 또렷이 곤두선 채로 말똥말똥 깨어 있었다."]], [["For hours I lay there, listening to the wind and water; imagining, now, that I heard shrieks out at sea; now, that I distinctly heard the firing of signal guns; and now, the fall of houses in the town.", "몇 시간이고 나는 그렇게 누워 바람과 물소리에 귀를 기울였다. 어느 순간엔 바다 저편에서 비명이 들리는 듯했고, 또 어느 순간엔 신호용 대포 쏘는 소리가 또렷이 들리는 듯했으며, 또 어느 순간엔 마을의 집들이 무너지는 소리가 들리는 듯했다."], ["I got up, several times, and looked out; but could see nothing, except the reflection in the window-panes of the faint candle I had left burning, and of my own haggard face looking in at me from the black void.", "나는 몇 번이나 일어나 밖을 내다보았으나, 켜 둔 채로 둔 희미한 촛불이 창유리에 비친 모습과, 캄캄한 허공에서 나를 들여다보는 내 자신의 초췌한 얼굴 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."]], [["At length, my restlessness attained to such a pitch, that I hurried on my clothes, and went downstairs.", "마침내 내 안절부절못함이 어찌나 극에 달했던지, 나는 황급히 옷을 걸치고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["In the large kitchen, where I dimly saw bacon and ropes of onions hanging from the beams, the watchers were clustered together, in various attitudes, about a table, purposely moved away from the great chimney, and brought near the door.", "들보에 베이컨과 양파 두름이 걸려 있는 것이 어슴푸레 보이는 그 널찍한 부엌에서, 야경을 서는 사람들이 큰 굴뚝에서 일부러 떨어뜨려 문 가까이 끌어다 놓은 탁자를 둘러싸고 갖가지 자세로 옹기종기 모여 있었다."], ["A pretty girl, who had her ears stopped with her apron, and her eyes upon the door, screamed when I appeared, supposing me to be a spirit; but the others had more presence of mind, and were glad of an addition to their company.", "앞치마로 귀를 틀어막고 두 눈은 문에 못 박은 한 예쁜 처녀가, 내가 나타나자 유령인 줄 알고 비명을 질렀다. 그러나 나머지 사람들은 좀 더 침착해서, 일행이 하나 늘어난 것을 반겼다."], ["One man, referring to the topic they had been discussing, asked me whether I thought the souls of the collier-crews who had gone down, were out in the storm?", "한 사내가 그들이 나누던 화제를 들먹이며, 가라앉은 석탄 운반선 선원들의 영혼이 이 폭풍 속에 떠돌고 있다고 생각하는지 내게 물었다."]], [["I remained there, I dare say, two hours.", "나는 거기서, 짐작건대, 두 시간쯤 머물렀다."], ["Once, I opened the yard-gate, and looked into the empty street.", "한 번은 작업장 문을 열고 텅 빈 거리를 내다보았다."], ["The sand, the sea-weed, and the flakes of foam, were driving by; and I was obliged to call for assistance before I could shut the gate again, and make it fast against the wind.", "모래와 해초와 거품 조각들이 휘몰려 지나갔다. 그리고 나는 도움을 청한 뒤에야 다시 문을 닫고 바람에 맞서 단단히 잠글 수 있었다."]], [["There was a dark gloom in my solitary chamber, when I at length returned to it; but I was tired now, and, getting into bed again, fell--off a tower and down a precipice--into the depths of sleep.", "마침내 내 외딴 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 그 방엔 어두운 적막이 감돌았다. 그러나 이제는 지친 터라, 다시 잠자리에 들자, 탑에서 — 그리고 벼랑 아래로 — 떨어지듯 깊은 잠 속으로 빠져들었다."], ["I have an impression that for a long time, though I dreamed of being elsewhere and in a variety of scenes, it was always blowing in my dream.", "오랜 시간 동안, 다른 곳, 갖가지 장면 속에 있는 꿈을 꾸면서도, 꿈속에선 늘 바람이 불고 있었다는 느낌이 든다."], ["At length, I lost that feeble hold upon reality, and was engaged with two dear friends, but who they were I don’t know, at the siege of some town in a roar of cannonading.", "마침내 나는 현실에 매달린 그 가냘픈 끈마저 놓쳤고, 두 소중한 벗과 — 그러나 그들이 누구였는지는 모르겠다 — 함께, 우르릉대는 포격 소리 속에서 어느 마을을 포위 공격하는 데 가담해 있었다."]], [["The thunder of the cannon was so loud and incessant, that I could not hear something I much desired to hear, until I made a great exertion and awoke.", "대포의 우레가 어찌나 크고 끊임없던지, 나는 그토록 듣고 싶었던 무언가를 도무지 들을 수가 없다가, 안간힘을 다 쓰고서야 잠에서 깼다."], ["It was broad day--eight or nine o’clock; the storm raging, in lieu of the batteries; and someone knocking and calling at my door.", "환한 대낮이었다 — 여덟아홉 시쯤. 포대 대신 폭풍이 휘몰아치고 있었고, 누군가 내 방문을 두드리며 부르고 있었다."]], [["‘What is the matter?’ I cried.", "\"무슨 일이오?\" 내가 외쳤다."]], [["‘A wreck!", "\"난파선이오!"], ["Close by!’", "바로 코앞에!\""]], [["I sprung out of bed, and asked, what wreck?", "나는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 무슨 난파선이냐고 물었다."]], [["‘A schooner, from Spain or Portugal, laden with fruit and wine.", "\"스페인인지 포르투갈인지에서 온 스쿠너요, 과일이랑 포도주를 싣고요."], ["Make haste, sir, if you want to see her!", "서두르시오, 손님, 보고 싶으시거든!"], ["It’s thought, down on the beach, she’ll go to pieces every moment.’", "해변에선 다들 저 배가 당장이라도 산산조각 날 거라고 하오.\""]], [["The excited voice went clamouring along the staircase; and I wrapped myself in my clothes as quickly as I could, and ran into the street.", "흥분한 목소리가 계단을 따라 떠들썩하게 멀어졌다. 나는 되도록 빨리 옷을 둘러 입고, 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["Numbers of people were there before me, all running in one direction, to the beach.", "벌써 숱한 사람들이 나보다 앞서서, 모두 한 방향으로, 해변을 향해 달려가고 있었다."], ["I ran the same way, outstripping a good many, and soon came facing the wild sea.", "나도 같은 길로 달려 꽤 많은 사람을 앞질렀고, 이내 사나운 바다를 마주하게 되었다."]], [["The wind might by this time have lulled a little, though not more sensibly than if the cannonading I had dreamed of, had been diminished by the silencing of half-a-dozen guns out of hundreds.", "이 무렵 바람은 어쩌면 조금 잦아들었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은, 내가 꿈에서 들었던 포격 소리가 수백 문 가운데 예닐곱 문이 잠잠해진 정도로 줄어든 것이나 다름없을 만큼 미미했다."], ["But the sea, having upon it the additional agitation of the whole night, was infinitely more terrific than when I had seen it last.", "그러나 바다는, 밤새도록 더 들끓은 것이 얹혀, 내가 마지막으로 보았을 때보다 한없이 더 무시무시했다."], ["Every appearance it had then presented, bore the expression of being swelled; and the height to which the breakers rose, and, looking over one another, bore one another down, and rolled in, in interminable hosts, was most appalling.", "그때 보였던 모든 모습이 죄다 부풀어 오른 표정을 띠고 있었다. 파도가 솟구치는 높이며, 서로를 넘겨다보며 서로를 짓누르고 끝없는 무리를 이루어 굽이쳐 밀려드는 그 광경은 더없이 소름 끼쳤다."], ["In the difficulty of hearing anything but wind and waves, and in the crowd, and the unspeakable confusion, and my first breathless efforts to stand against the weather, I was so confused that I looked out to sea for the wreck, and saw nothing but the foaming heads of the great waves.", "바람과 파도 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않는 데다, 인파와, 형언할 수 없는 혼란과, 날씨에 맞서 버티려는 내 첫 숨 가쁜 안간힘 속에서, 나는 어찌나 정신이 없던지 난파선을 찾아 바다를 내다보았으나 거대한 파도의 포말 진 꼭대기 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["A half-dressed boatman, standing next me, pointed with his bare arm (a tattoo’d arrow on it, pointing in the same direction) to the left. Then, O great Heaven, I saw it, close in upon us!", "내 옆에 선, 옷도 채 못 갖춰 입은 한 뱃사람이 (문신으로 새긴 화살이 같은 방향을 가리키고 있는) 맨팔로 왼쪽을 가리켰다. 그제야, 아, 위대하신 하느님, 나는 그것을 보았다, 우리 코앞에 바짝 다가와 있는 것을!"]], [["One mast was broken short off, six or eight feet from the deck, and lay over the side, entangled in a maze of sail and rigging; and all that ruin, as the ship rolled and beat--which she did without a moment’s pause, and with a violence quite inconceivable--beat the side as if it would stave it in.", "돛대 하나가 갑판에서 육칠 피트쯤 되는 자리에서 뚝 부러져 뱃전 너머로 늘어진 채, 돛과 밧줄이 뒤엉킨 미로 속에 얽혀 있었다. 배가 흔들리고 부딪칠 때마다 — 한순간도 쉬지 않고, 도무지 상상조차 못 할 만큼 사납게 그랬다 — 그 부서진 잔해가 뱃전을 뚫고 들어올 듯이 후려쳤다."], ["Some efforts were even then being made, to cut this portion of the wreck away; for, as the ship, which was broadside on, turned towards us in her rolling, I plainly descried her people at work with axes, especially one active figure with long curling hair, conspicuous among the rest.", "그때 이미 이 난파의 한 부분을 잘라 내려는 안간힘이 펼쳐지고 있었다. 옆구리를 드러낸 채 흔들리던 배가 우리 쪽으로 돌아설 때, 나는 사람들이 도끼를 들고 일하는 모습을 또렷이 알아보았는데, 그중에서도 긴 곱슬머리를 한 활기찬 한 사람이 나머지 가운데 유독 눈에 띄었다."], ["But a great cry, which was audible even above the wind and water, rose from the shore at this moment; the sea, sweeping over the rolling wreck, made a clean breach, and carried men, spars, casks, planks, bulwarks, heaps of such toys, into the boiling surge.", "그러나 바로 그 순간, 바람과 물소리 너머로도 들릴 만큼 큰 비명이 해변에서 터져 나왔다. 바다가 흔들리는 난파선 위를 휩쓸며 깨끗이 한 차례 휘몰아쳐, 사람들과 활대와 통과 널빤지와 뱃전 난간과, 그런 장난감 같은 것들을 한 무더기로 들끓는 물결 속으로 쓸어 갔다."]], [["The second mast was yet standing, with the rags of a rent sail, and a wild confusion of broken cordage flapping to and fro.", "두 번째 돛대는 아직 서 있었는데, 찢긴 돛 누더기와, 끊어진 밧줄들이 이리저리 거칠게 펄럭이며 어지러이 뒤엉켜 있었다."], ["The ship had struck once, the same boatman hoarsely said in my ear, and then lifted in and struck again.", "배가 한 차례 부딪쳤다가, 그러고는 밀려 올라가 다시 부딪쳤다고, 아까 그 뱃사람이 내 귀에 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["I understood him to add that she was parting amidships, and I could readily suppose so, for the rolling and beating were too tremendous for any human work to suffer long.", "나는 그가 배가 한가운데서 두 동강 나고 있다고 덧붙인 것으로 알아들었고, 그럴 만하다고 쉬이 짐작했다. 그 흔들림과 부딪침이 어떤 사람의 솜씨로도 오래 견디기엔 너무도 어마어마했으니까."], ["As he spoke, there was another great cry of pity from the beach; four men arose with the wreck out of the deep, clinging to the rigging of the remaining mast; uppermost, the active figure with the curling hair.", "그가 말하는 사이, 해변에서 또 한 차례 큰 연민의 비명이 일었다. 네 사람이 난파선과 함께 심연에서 솟아올라, 남은 돛대의 밧줄에 매달려 있었다. 그 맨 꼭대기에는, 곱슬머리를 한 활기찬 그 사람이 있었다."]], [["There was a bell on board; and as the ship rolled and dashed, like a desperate creature driven mad, now showing us the whole sweep of her deck, as she turned on her beam-ends towards the shore, now nothing but her keel, as she sprung wildly over and turned towards the sea, the bell rang; and its sound, the knell of those unhappy men, was borne towards us on the wind.", "배에는 종이 하나 달려 있었다. 그리고 배가 미쳐 날뛰는 절망에 빠진 짐승처럼 흔들리고 부딪칠 때마다 — 옆으로 거의 자빠지듯 뭍을 향해 돌아설 때면 갑판 전체를 우리에게 다 드러내 보였다가, 사납게 솟구쳐 넘어가 바다를 향해 돌아설 때면 용골 말고는 아무것도 안 보였다 — 그 종이 울렸다. 그리고 그 소리는, 저 불행한 사람들의 조종(弔鐘) 소리는, 바람을 타고 우리 쪽으로 실려 왔다."], ["Again we lost her, and again she rose.", "우리는 또 한 번 배를 놓쳤고, 배는 또 한 번 솟아올랐다."], ["Two men were gone. The agony on the shore increased.", "두 사람이 사라지고 없었다. 해변의 비통함이 더해 갔다."], ["Men groaned, and clasped their hands; women shrieked, and turned away their faces.", "사내들은 신음하며 두 손을 맞잡았고, 여인들은 비명을 지르며 얼굴을 돌렸다."], ["Some ran wildly up and down along the beach, crying for help where no help could be.", "더러는 도움이 닿을 수 없는 곳에서 도와 달라 외치며 해변을 따라 미친 듯이 오르내렸다."], ["I found myself one of these, frantically imploring a knot of sailors whom I knew, not to let those two lost creatures perish before our eyes.", "나도 그중 하나가 되어, 내가 아는 한 무리의 뱃사람들에게, 저 길 잃은 두 사람이 우리 눈앞에서 죽어 가게 두지 말아 달라고 미친 듯이 애원하고 있었다."]], [["They were making out to me, in an agitated way--I don’t know how, for the little I could hear I was scarcely composed enough to understand--that the lifeboat had been bravely manned an hour ago, and could do nothing; and that as no man would be so desperate as to attempt to wade off with a rope, and establish a communication with the shore, there was nothing left to try; when I noticed that some new sensation moved the people on the beach, and saw them part, and Ham come breaking through them to the front.", "그들은 동요한 기색으로 내게 무어라 알려 주려 했다 — 나는 그게 어떤 말이었는지 모르겠다, 겨우 들리는 토막마저 알아들을 만큼 마음이 가라앉아 있지 못했으니까 — 한 시간 전에 구명정이 용감하게 사람들을 태워 나갔으나 아무것도 할 수 없었다는 것, 그리고 밧줄 하나 끌고 물속을 헤치며 나아가 뭍과 연락을 잇겠다고 나설 만큼 무모한 사람은 아무도 없으니, 이제 더 해 볼 것이 남지 않았다는 것이었다. 그때 나는 해변의 사람들을 어떤 새로운 감정이 동요시키는 것을 알아챘고, 그들이 갈라서며, 햄이 그 사이를 헤치고 앞으로 나오는 것을 보았다."]], [["I ran to him--as well as I know, to repeat my appeal for help.", "나는 그에게 달려갔다 — 내가 아는 한, 도와 달라는 호소를 되풀이하려고."], ["But, distracted though I was, by a sight so new to me and terrible, the determination in his face, and his look out to sea--exactly the same look as I remembered in connexion with the morning after Emily’s flight--awoke me to a knowledge of his danger.", "그러나 너무도 낯설고 끔찍한 광경에 넋이 나가 있던 와중에도, 그의 얼굴에 어린 결심과, 바다를 내다보는 그 눈빛이 — 에밀리가 달아난 이튿날 아침과 관련해 내가 기억하던 바로 그 눈빛이 — 그가 처한 위험을 깨닫게 했다."], ["I held him back with both arms; and implored the men with whom I had been speaking, not to listen to him, not to do murder, not to let him stir from off that sand!", "나는 두 팔로 그를 붙들고, 방금까지 이야기를 나누던 그 사람들에게 부디 그의 말을 듣지 말라고, 살인을 저지르지 말라고, 그를 저 모래에서 한 발짝도 움직이게 하지 말라고 애원했다."]], [["Another cry arose on shore; and looking to the wreck, we saw the cruel sail, with blow on blow, beat off the lower of the two men, and fly up in triumph round the active figure left alone upon the mast.", "해변에서 또 한 차례 비명이 일었다. 난파선을 보니, 그 모진 돛이 후려치고 또 후려쳐, 두 사람 중 아래쪽 사람을 쳐서 떨어뜨리고, 의기양양하게 솟구쳐 돛대에 홀로 남은 그 활기찬 사람 둘레로 펄럭였다."]], [["Against such a sight, and against such determination as that of the calmly desperate man who was already accustomed to lead half the people present, I might as hopefully have entreated the wind.", "그런 광경 앞에서, 그리고 이미 거기 모인 사람 절반을 이끄는 데 익숙한, 차분하게 절망한 그 사람의 결심 앞에서, 차라리 나는 바람에게 호소하는 편이 더 가망 있었을 것이다. \"데이비 도련님,\""], ["‘Mas’r Davy,’ he said, cheerily grasping me by both hands, ‘if my time is come, ‘tis come.", "그가 쾌활하게 내 두 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"지가 갈 때가 됐으면, 된 거지유. 안 됐으면, 기다릴 거구유."], ["If ‘tan’t, I’ll bide it. Lord above bless you, and bless all!", "위에 계신 주님이 도련님께 복 주시고, 모두에게 복 주시기를!"], ["Mates, make me ready!", "친구들, 채비해 주게!"], ["I’m a-going off!’", "나 나갈 테니!\""]], [["I was swept away, but not unkindly, to some distance, where the people around me made me stay; urging, as I confusedly perceived, that he was bent on going, with help or without, and that I should endanger the precautions for his safety by troubling those with whom they rested.", "나는 떠밀려 나갔으나, 모질지는 않게, 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳으로 밀려나, 둘러싼 사람들이 나를 거기 붙들어 두었다. 내가 어렴풋이 알아듣기로, 그가 도움을 받든 안 받든 기어이 나갈 작정이니, 그의 안전을 위한 채비를 맡은 이들을 내가 성가시게 하면 그 채비를 위태롭게 할 뿐이라는 것이었다."], ["I don’t know what I answered, or what they rejoined; but I saw hurry on the beach, and men running with ropes from a capstan that was there, and penetrating into a circle of figures that hid him from me.", "내가 무어라 대답했는지, 그들이 무어라 받아쳤는지 나는 모르겠다. 그러나 나는 해변에서 사람들이 분주히 움직이는 것을, 거기 놓인 양묘기에서 밧줄을 들고 사내들이 달려가, 그를 내게서 가려 버린 사람들의 동그라미 속으로 파고드는 것을 보았다."], ["Then, I saw him standing alone, in a seaman’s frock and trousers: a rope in his hand, or slung to his wrist: another round his body: and several of the best men holding, at a little distance, to the latter, which he laid out himself, slack upon the shore, at his feet.", "그러고는 그가 홀로 서 있는 것을 보았다, 뱃사람의 작업복과 바지 차림으로. 밧줄 한 가닥을 손에 쥐었거나 손목에 감고, 또 한 가닥은 몸에 둘렀으며, 가장 솜씨 좋은 사람 몇이 조금 떨어진 데서 그 뒤쪽 밧줄을 붙들었는데, 그 밧줄은 그가 직접 발치 모래밭에 느슨히 풀어 놓은 것이었다."]], [["The wreck, even to my unpractised eye, was breaking up.", "내 서투른 눈에도, 난파선은 부서져 가고 있었다."], ["I saw that she was parting in the middle, and that the life of the solitary man upon the mast hung by a thread.", "나는 배가 한가운데서 갈라지고 있다는 것, 그리고 돛대에 홀로 매달린 그 사람의 목숨이 실낱에 걸려 있다는 것을 알아보았다."], ["Still, he clung to it.", "그래도 그는 거기 매달려 있었다."], ["He had a singular red cap on,--not like a sailor’s cap, but of a finer colour; and as the few yielding planks between him and destruction rolled and bulged, and his anticipative death-knell rung, he was seen by all of us to wave it.", "그는 색다른 빨간 모자를 쓰고 있었는데 — 뱃사람의 모자 같지는 않고, 좀 더 고운 빛깔이었다 — 그를 파멸에서 갈라놓은 몇 안 되는 휘청대는 널빤지가 출렁이며 불룩 솟고, 미리 울리는 그의 조종 소리가 울려 퍼지는 가운데, 우리 모두는 그가 그 모자를 흔드는 것을 보았다."], ["I saw him do it now, and thought I was going distracted, when his action brought an old remembrance to my mind of a once dear friend.", "나는 지금 그가 그러는 것을 보았고, 그 몸짓이 한때 소중했던 한 벗에 대한 옛 기억을 내 마음에 불러일으켰을 때, 나는 정신이 나가 버리는 줄 알았다."]], [["Ham watched the sea, standing alone, with the silence of suspended breath behind him, and the storm before, until there was a great retiring wave, when, with a backward glance at those who held the rope which was made fast round his body, he dashed in after it, and in a moment was buffeting with the water; rising with the hills, falling with the valleys, lost beneath the foam; then drawn again to land.", "햄은 홀로 서서, 등 뒤로는 숨죽인 침묵을, 앞으로는 폭풍을 둔 채, 바다를 지켜보았다. 그러다 크게 물러나는 파도가 일자, 그는 제 몸에 단단히 둘러맨 밧줄을 붙든 사람들을 한 번 흘끗 돌아보고는, 그 파도를 따라 뛰어들었다. 그러고는 순식간에 물과 맞붙어 싸웠다."], ["They hauled in hastily.", "언덕과 함께 솟구치고, 골짜기와 함께 떨어지고, 포말 아래로 사라졌다가, 다시 뭍으로 당겨졌다. 사람들은 황급히 밧줄을 끌어당겼다."]], [["He was hurt.", "그는 다쳐 있었다."], ["I saw blood on his face, from where I stood; but he took no thought of that.", "내가 선 자리에서도 그의 얼굴에 피가 보였다. 그러나 그는 그런 것엔 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["He seemed hurriedly to give them some directions for leaving him more free--or so I judged from the motion of his arm--and was gone as before.", "그는 자기를 좀 더 자유롭게 풀어 달라고 사람들에게 황급히 무어라 지시하는 듯했고 — 적어도 그의 팔짓으로 보아 그랬다 — 이내 아까처럼 떠나갔다."]], [["And now he made for the wreck, rising with the hills, falling with the valleys, lost beneath the rugged foam, borne in towards the shore, borne on towards the ship, striving hard and valiantly.", "그리고 이제 그는 난파선을 향해 나아갔다. 언덕과 함께 솟구치고, 골짜기와 함께 떨어지고, 거친 포말 아래로 사라지고, 뭍 쪽으로 떠밀렸다가, 배 쪽으로 떠밀려 가며, 안간힘을 다해 용감하게 헤쳐 나갔다."], ["The distance was nothing, but the power of the sea and wind made the strife deadly.", "거리는 아무것도 아니었으나, 바다와 바람의 위력이 그 싸움을 목숨을 건 것으로 만들었다."], ["At length he neared the wreck.", "마침내 그는 난파선에 다가갔다."], ["He was so near, that with one more of his vigorous strokes he would be clinging to it,--when a high, green, vast hill-side of water, moving on shoreward, from beyond the ship, he seemed to leap up into it with a mighty bound, and the ship was gone!", "어찌나 가까웠던지, 그 힘찬 손짓 한 번만 더하면 거기 매달릴 참이었는데 — 배 너머에서 뭍을 향해 밀려오던, 높다랗고 시퍼런, 거대한 산비탈 같은 물이, 그가 거기로 힘껏 솟구쳐 뛰어든 듯하더니, 배가 사라져 버렸다!"]], [["Some eddying fragments I saw in the sea, as if a mere cask had been broken, in running to the spot where they were hauling in.", "밧줄을 끌어당기던 자리로 달려가다가, 나는 바다에 소용돌이치는 파편 몇 조각을 보았다. 마치 통 하나가 부서지기라도 한 것처럼."], ["Consternation was in every face. They drew him to my very feet--insensible--dead.", "모두의 얼굴에 경악이 어렸다. 사람들이 그를 바로 내 발치로 끌어왔다 — 정신을 잃은 채 — 숨이 끊긴 채."], ["He was carried to the nearest house; and, no one preventing me now, I remained near him, busy, while every means of restoration were tried; but he had been beaten to death by the great wave, and his generous heart was stilled for ever.", "그는 가장 가까운 집으로 옮겨졌다. 이제 아무도 나를 막지 않았기에, 나는 그의 곁에 머물며, 온갖 소생술을 펼치는 동안 분주히 거들었다. 그러나 그는 그 거대한 파도에 맞아 죽은 것이었고, 그의 너그러운 심장은 영영 멎어 버렸다."]], [["As I sat beside the bed, when hope was abandoned and all was done, a fisherman, who had known me when Emily and I were children, and ever since, whispered my name at the door.", "희망이 버려지고 모든 것이 끝났을 때, 내가 침대 곁에 앉아 있자니, 에밀리와 내가 어린아이였던 시절부터, 그리고 그 후로 줄곧 나를 알아 온 한 어부가 문간에서 내 이름을 속삭였다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said he, with tears starting to his weather-beaten face, which, with his trembling lips, was ashy pale, ‘will you come over yonder?’", "\"손님,\" 그가 말했다. 풍상에 찌든 그의 얼굴에 눈물이 솟구쳤고, 그 얼굴은 떨리는 입술과 더불어 잿빛으로 새파랬다. \"저쪽으로 좀 와 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["The old remembrance that had been recalled to me, was in his look.", "내게 되살아났던 그 옛 기억이, 그의 눈빛 속에 있었다."], ["I asked him, terror-stricken, leaning on the arm he held out to support me:", "나는 공포에 질려, 그가 나를 부축하려고 내민 팔에 기대며 물었다."]], [["‘Has a body come ashore?’", "\"시신이 떠밀려 왔소?\""]], [["He said, ‘Yes.’", "그가 말했다. \"예.\""]], [["‘Do I know it?’ I asked then.", "\"내가 아는 사람이오?\" 그제야 내가 물었다."]], [["He answered nothing.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["But he led me to the shore.", "다만 그는 나를 해변으로 이끌었다."], ["And on that part of it where she and I had looked for shells, two children--on that part of it where some lighter fragments of the old boat, blown down last night, had been scattered by the wind--among the ruins of the home he had wronged--I saw him lying with his head upon his arm, as I had often seen him lie at school.", "그리고 그녀와 내가, 두 아이가 조개껍데기를 줍던 그 해변의 한 자리에서 — 어젯밤 무너져 내린 그 낡은 배의 좀 더 가벼운 파편들이 바람에 흩뿌려진 그 자리에서 — 그가 저버렸던 그 보금자리의 잔해 가운데서 — 나는 그가, 학교에서 그가 누워 있는 모습을 내가 자주 보았던 그대로, 한쪽 팔에 머리를 베고 누워 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 56. THE NEW WOUND, AND THE OLD", "# CHAPTER 56. THE NEW WOUND, AND THE OLD"]], [["No need, O Steerforth, to have said, when we last spoke together, in that hour which I so little deemed to be our parting-hour--no need to have said, ‘Think of me at my best!’ I had done that ever; and could I change now, looking on this sight!", "아, 스티어포스여, 우리가 마지막으로 함께 이야기를 나누던 그 시각, 그것이 우리의 작별 시각이리라고는 꿈에도 몰랐던 그때, 굳이 그런 말을 할 필요는 없었다. \"나를 가장 좋은 모습으로 기억해 줘!\"라고 말할 필요는 없었다. 나는 늘 그렇게 해 왔으니까. 그리고 이 광경을 마주한 지금, 내가 그 마음을 바꿀 수 있겠는가!"]], [["They brought a hand-bier, and laid him on it, and covered him with a flag, and took him up and bore him on towards the houses. All the men who carried him had known him, and gone sailing with him, and seen him merry and bold.", "사람들이 손으로 드는 들것을 가져와 그를 거기 눕히고 깃발로 덮은 다음, 들어 올려 집들이 있는 쪽으로 옮겼다. 그를 옮기는 사람들은 모두 그를 알았고, 그와 함께 배를 타고 바다로 나가 본 적이 있었으며, 그가 쾌활하고 대담한 모습을 보아 온 이들이었다."], ["They carried him through the wild roar, a hush in the midst of all the tumult; and took him to the cottage where Death was already.", "그들은 사나운 포효 한가운데를, 그 모든 소동 속의 정적 한 토막을 가르며 그를 옮겼다. 그러고는 이미 죽음이 와 있는 그 오두막으로 그를 데려갔다."]], [["But when they set the bier down on the threshold, they looked at one another, and at me, and whispered. I knew why.", "하지만 들것을 문지방에 내려놓았을 때, 사람들은 서로를, 그리고 나를 바라보며 수군거렸다. 나는 그 까닭을 알았다."], ["They felt as if it were not right to lay him down in the same quiet room.", "그를 똑같이 고요한 방 안에 함께 눕히는 것이 옳지 않다고 느낀 것이었다."]], [["We went into the town, and took our burden to the inn.", "우리는 읍내로 들어가, 그 짐을 여관으로 옮겼다."], ["So soon as I could at all collect my thoughts, I sent for Joram, and begged him to provide me a conveyance in which it could be got to London in the night.", "마음을 조금이나마 가다듬을 수 있게 되자마자, 나는 조램을 불러, 밤사이에 그를 런던으로 옮길 탈것을 마련해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["I knew that the care of it, and the hard duty of preparing his mother to receive it, could only rest with me; and I was anxious to discharge that duty as faithfully as I could.", "그를 보살피는 일과, 그의 어머니에게 그를 맞을 마음의 준비를 시키는 그 모진 임무가 오직 나에게만 맡겨질 수밖에 없다는 것을 알았다. 그리고 나는 그 임무를 할 수 있는 한 충실히 해내고 싶었다."]], [["I chose the night for the journey, that there might be less curiosity when I left the town.", "나는 읍내를 떠날 때 사람들의 호기심이 덜할까 싶어 밤을 골라 길을 나섰다."], ["But, although it was nearly midnight when I came out of the yard in a chaise, followed by what I had in charge, there were many people waiting.", "그러나 내가 내 임무를 실은 것을 뒤따르게 하고 마차에 올라 마당을 빠져나온 것이 거의 자정 무렵이었는데도, 많은 사람들이 기다리고 있었다."], ["At intervals, along the town, and even a little way out upon the road, I saw more: but at length only the bleak night and the open country were around me, and the ashes of my youthful friendship.", "읍내를 따라 군데군데, 심지어 길을 조금 벗어난 곳에서도 더 많은 이들이 보였다. 그러나 마침내 내 둘레에는 황량한 밤과 텅 빈 들판, 그리고 내 젊은 날 우정의 재만이 남았다."]], [["Upon a mellow autumn day, about noon, when the ground was perfumed by fallen leaves, and many more, in beautiful tints of yellow, red, and brown, yet hung upon the trees, through which the sun was shining, I arrived at Highgate.", "낙엽이 향기를 풍기고, 노랗고 붉고 갈색인 아름다운 빛깔의 잎들이 아직도 나무에 잔뜩 매달려 그 사이로 햇살이 비치던, 무르익은 가을날 정오 무렵, 나는 하이게이트에 다다랐다."], ["I walked the last mile, thinking as I went along of what I had to do; and left the carriage that had followed me all through the night, awaiting orders to advance.", "마지막 일 마일은 걸으면서, 내가 해야 할 일을 곰곰이 생각했다. 밤새 나를 뒤따라온 마차는 앞으로 나아가라는 분부를 기다리도록 남겨 두었다."]], [["The house, when I came up to it, looked just the same.", "가까이 다가가서 보니, 그 집은 예전과 꼭 같았다."], ["Not a blind was raised; no sign of life was in the dull paved court, with its covered way leading to the disused door.", "덧창은 하나도 올라가 있지 않았고, 쓰지 않는 문으로 이어지는 지붕 덮인 통로가 딸린 음침한 포석 깔린 안마당에는 생기라곤 아무런 기색도 없었다."], ["The wind had quite gone down, and nothing moved.", "바람은 완전히 잦아들었고, 아무것도 움직이지 않았다."]], [["I had not, at first, the courage to ring at the gate; and when I did ring, my errand seemed to me to be expressed in the very sound of the bell.", "나는 처음에는 대문 종을 울릴 용기가 나지 않았다. 그리고 막상 종을 울렸을 때, 그 종소리 자체에 내 용건이 고스란히 담겨 있는 것만 같았다."], ["The little parlour-maid came out, with the key in her hand; and looking earnestly at me as she unlocked the gate, said:", "자그마한 응접실 하녀가 손에 열쇠를 들고 나왔는데, 대문을 따면서 나를 골똘히 바라보더니 말했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir. Are you ill?’", "\"실례합니다만, 나리. 어디 편찮으세요?\""]], [["‘I have been much agitated, and am fatigued.’", "\"마음이 몹시 흔들렸고, 또 지쳤다네.\""]], [["‘Is anything the matter, sir?---Mr. James?--’ ‘Hush!’ said I.", "\"무슨 일이라도 있나요, 나리?— 제임스 도련님이—\" \"쉿!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Yes, something has happened, that I have to break to Mrs. Steerforth.", "\"그래, 일이 좀 생겼어. 스티어포스 부인께 알려 드려야 하는 일이라네."], ["She is at home?’", "댁에 계시는가?\""]], [["The girl anxiously replied that her mistress was very seldom out now, even in a carriage; that she kept her room; that she saw no company, but would see me.", "하녀는 조심스럽게 대답하기를, 마님께서는 요즘 마차를 타고 나가시는 일조차 거의 없고, 방 안에만 계시며, 손님도 만나지 않으시지만 저는 만나 주실 거라고 했다."], ["Her mistress was up, she said, and Miss Dartle was with her.", "마님은 일어나 계시고, 다틀 양이 곁에 있다고 했다."], ["What message should she take upstairs?", "위층에 어떤 말씀을 전해 올릴까요?"]], [["Giving her a strict charge to be careful of her manner, and only to carry in my card and say I waited, I sat down in the drawing-room (which we had now reached) until she should come back.", "나는 하녀에게 행동거지를 각별히 조심하고, 그저 내 명함만 들여보내고 내가 기다린다고만 전하라고 단단히 일러 둔 다음, 하녀가 돌아올 때까지 객실에(우리는 어느새 그곳에 이르러 있었다) 앉아 있었다."], ["Its former pleasant air of occupation was gone, and the shutters were half closed.", "예전의 그 정겨운, 사람 손길이 깃든 분위기는 사라졌고, 덧문은 반쯤 닫혀 있었다."], ["The harp had not been used for many and many a day. His picture, as a boy, was there.", "하프는 오래오래 쓰지 않은 채였다. 소년 시절의 그의 초상화가 거기 있었다."], ["The cabinet in which his mother had kept his letters was there.", "그의 어머니가 그의 편지를 간직해 둔 장식장이 거기 있었다."], ["I wondered if she ever read them now; if she would ever read them more!", "나는 부인이 지금도 그 편지들을 읽는지, 앞으로 또 읽기나 할지 궁금했다."]], [["The house was so still that I heard the girl’s light step upstairs.", "집 안이 어찌나 고요한지 위층에서 하녀의 가벼운 발걸음 소리가 다 들렸다."], ["On her return, she brought a message, to the effect that Mrs. Steerforth was an invalid and could not come down; but that if I would excuse her being in her chamber, she would be glad to see me.", "하녀는 내려와서 전갈을 가져왔는데, 스티어포스 부인은 몸이 성치 않아 내려올 수 없으나, 침실에 있는 것을 양해해 주신다면 기꺼이 저를 만나겠다는 뜻이었다."], ["In a few moments I stood before her.", "잠시 후 나는 부인 앞에 섰다."]], [["She was in his room; not in her own.", "부인은 자기 방이 아니라 그의 방에 있었다."], ["I felt, of course, that she had taken to occupy it, in remembrance of him; and that the many tokens of his old sports and accomplishments, by which she was surrounded, remained there, just as he had left them, for the same reason.", "나는 물론 부인이 그를 추억하느라 그 방을 차지하고 지내게 된 것이며, 그녀를 둘러싼 그의 옛 놀이며 재주를 보여 주는 그 숱한 흔적들이 그가 남겨 둔 그대로 거기 남아 있는 것도 같은 까닭에서임을 알 수 있었다."], ["She murmured, however, even in her reception of me, that she was out of her own chamber because its aspect was unsuited to her infirmity; and with her stately look repelled the least suspicion of the truth.", "그러나 부인은 나를 맞으면서도, 자기 침실은 분위기가 병약한 몸에 맞지 않아 거기서 나와 있는 것이라고 중얼거렸고, 그 위엄 어린 표정으로 진실에 대한 일말의 의심마저 물리쳤다."]], [["At her chair, as usual, was Rosa Dartle.", "부인의 의자 곁에는 여느 때처럼 로자 다틀이 있었다."], ["From the first moment of her dark eyes resting on me, I saw she knew I was the bearer of evil tidings.", "그 검은 두 눈이 나에게 머무는 첫 순간부터, 나는 그녀가 내가 흉한 소식을 전하러 온 사람임을 알아챘다는 것을 알았다."], ["The scar sprung into view that instant.", "바로 그 순간 흉터가 도드라졌다."], ["She withdrew herself a step behind the chair, to keep her own face out of Mrs. Steerforth’s observation; and scrutinized me with a piercing gaze that never faltered, never shrunk.", "그녀는 자기 얼굴이 스티어포스 부인의 눈에 들지 않도록 의자 뒤로 한 걸음 물러서더니, 결코 흔들리지도, 움츠러들지도 않는 꿰뚫는 듯한 시선으로 나를 살폈다."]], [["‘I am sorry to observe you are in mourning, sir,’ said Mrs. Steerforth.", "\"상복을 입으신 걸 보니 안타깝군요, 나리.\" 스티어포스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘I am unhappily a widower,’ said I.", "\"불행히도 저는 홀아비가 되었습니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘You are very young to know so great a loss,’ she returned.", "\"그토록 큰 상실을 겪기엔 아주 젊으신데요.\" 부인이 받았다."], ["‘I am grieved to hear it.", "\"참 안된 일입니다."], ["I am grieved to hear it. I hope Time will be good to you.’", "참 안된 일이에요. 부디 세월이 위로가 되어 주길 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘I hope Time,’ said I, looking at her, ‘will be good to all of us.", "\"부디 세월이,\" 부인을 바라보며 내가 말했다. \"우리 모두에게 위로가 되어 주길 바랍니다."], ["Dear Mrs. Steerforth, we must all trust to that, in our heaviest misfortunes.’", "친애하는 스티어포스 부인, 우리는 가장 무거운 불행을 겪을 때 모두 그것에 기댈 수밖에 없지요.\""]], [["The earnestness of my manner, and the tears in my eyes, alarmed her.", "내 태도의 간곡함과 두 눈에 고인 눈물이 부인을 놀라게 했다."], ["The whole course of her thoughts appeared to stop, and change.", "부인의 온갖 생각이 흐름을 멈추고 방향을 바꾸는 듯했다."]], [["I tried to command my voice in gently saying his name, but it trembled.", "나는 그의 이름을 부드럽게 입에 올리며 목소리를 다잡으려 했으나, 목소리가 떨렸다."], ["She repeated it to herself, two or three times, in a low tone.", "부인은 그 이름을 낮은 소리로 두세 번 혼자 되뇌었다."], ["Then, addressing me, she said, with enforced calmness:", "그러더니 나를 향해, 애써 차분함을 갖추고 말했다."]], [["‘My son is ill.’", "\"내 아들이 아픕니다.\""]], [["‘You have seen him?’", "\"그 애를 보았소?\""]], [["‘I have.’", "\"보았습니다.\""]], [["‘Are you reconciled?’", "\"화해는 했고?\""]], [["I could not say Yes, I could not say No.", "나는 그렇다고도 할 수 없었고, 아니라고도 할 수 없었다."], ["She slightly turned her head towards the spot where Rosa Dartle had been standing at her elbow, and in that moment I said, by the motion of my lips, to Rosa, ‘Dead!’", "부인은 로자 다틀이 자기 팔꿈치 곁에 서 있던 자리 쪽으로 고개를 살짝 돌렸고, 바로 그 순간 나는 입술만 움직여 로자에게 말했다. \"죽었어요!\""]], [["That Mrs. Steerforth might not be induced to look behind her, and read, plainly written, what she was not yet prepared to know, I met her look quickly; but I had seen Rosa Dartle throw her hands up in the air with vehemence of despair and horror, and then clasp them on her face.", "스티어포스 부인이 뒤를 돌아보다가, 아직 받아들일 준비가 안 된 사실이 또렷이 적힌 것을 읽어 버리는 일이 없도록, 나는 재빨리 부인의 시선을 마주했다. 그러나 나는 로자 다틀이 절망과 공포에 사무쳐 두 손을 허공으로 격렬하게 치켜드는 것을, 이어 그 두 손을 제 얼굴에 갖다 대는 것을 이미 보고 말았다."]], [["The handsome lady--so like, oh so like!--regarded me with a fixed look, and put her hand to her forehead.", "그 잘생긴 부인은—어찌나 닮았는지, 아아, 어찌나 닮았는지!—나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 한 손을 이마에 갖다 댔다."], ["I besought her to be calm, and prepare herself to bear what I had to tell; but I should rather have entreated her to weep, for she sat like a stone figure.", "나는 부인에게 부디 진정하고, 내가 전할 이야기를 견딜 마음의 준비를 하라고 간청했다. 그러나 차라리 울라고 애원하는 편이 나았을 것이다. 부인은 돌로 깎은 형상처럼 앉아 있었으니까."]], [["‘When I was last here,’ I faltered, ‘Miss Dartle told me he was sailing here and there.", "\"제가 지난번 여기 왔을 때,\" 나는 더듬거렸다. \"다틀 양이 그가 여기저기 배를 타고 다닌다고 했지요."], ["The night before last was a dreadful one at sea.", "그저께 밤 바다는 끔찍했습니다."], ["If he were at sea that night, and near a dangerous coast, as it is said he was; and if the vessel that was seen should really be the ship which--’", "만약 그가 그날 밤 바다에 있었고, 사람들 말처럼 위험한 해안 가까이에 있었다면, 그리고 사람들이 본 그 배가 정말로 그 배라면—\""]], [["‘Rosa!’ said Mrs.", "\"로자!\""], ["Steerforth, ‘come to me!’", "스티어포스 부인이 말했다. \"이리 오너라!\""]], [["She came, but with no sympathy or gentleness.", "로자는 다가왔으나, 거기엔 어떤 연민도 다정함도 없었다."], ["Her eyes gleamed like fire as she confronted his mother, and broke into a frightful laugh.", "그를 낳은 어머니와 마주하는 그녀의 두 눈은 불처럼 번득였고, 이내 그녀는 소름 끼치는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["‘Now,’ she said, ‘is your pride appeased, you madwoman?", "\"자,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이제 당신 자존심은 풀렸나요, 미친 노인네?"], ["Now has he made atonement to you--with his life! Do you hear?---His life!’", "이제 그가 당신에게 속죄했나요—제 목숨으로! 듣고 있어요?— 그 애의 목숨으로!\""]], [["Mrs. Steerforth, fallen back stiffly in her chair, and making no sound but a moan, cast her eyes upon her with a wide stare.", "스티어포스 부인은 의자에 빳빳이 기댄 채 쓰러지듯 주저앉아, 신음 말고는 아무 소리도 내지 못하고, 두 눈을 크게 뜬 채 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Aye!’ cried Rosa, smiting herself passionately on the breast, ‘look at me!", "\"그래요!\" 로자가 격렬하게 제 가슴을 치며 외쳤다. \"날 봐요!"], ["Moan, and groan, and look at me!", "신음하고, 끙끙대면서 날 보라고요!"], ["Look here!’ striking the scar, ‘at your dead child’s handiwork!’", "여길 봐요!\" 흉터를 치며 외쳤다. \"당신 죽은 아들의 솜씨를!\""]], [["The moan the mother uttered, from time to time, went to My heart.", "어머니가 이따금 토해 내는 그 신음이 내 가슴을 후벼 팠다."], ["Always the same.", "늘 똑같았다."], ["Always inarticulate and stifled.", "늘 말이 되지 못하고 짓눌린 소리였다."], ["Always accompanied with an incapable motion of the head, but with no change of face.", "늘 머리를 가누지 못하고 움직이는 몸짓이 따랐으나, 얼굴빛은 전혀 변하지 않았다."], ["Always proceeding from a rigid mouth and closed teeth, as if the jaw were locked and the face frozen up in pain.", "늘 굳어 버린 입과 다문 잇새에서 새어 나왔으니, 마치 턱이 잠겨 버리고 얼굴이 고통 속에 얼어붙은 듯했다."]], [["‘Do you remember when he did this?’ she proceeded.", "\"그가 이런 짓을 한 게 언제였는지 기억나요?\""], ["‘Do you remember when, in his inheritance of your nature, and in your pampering of his pride and passion, he did this, and disfigured me for life?", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그가 당신 천성을 물려받고, 당신이 그의 자만과 격정을 떠받들어 키운 탓에, 이런 짓을 저질러 나를 평생 흉하게 만든 게 언제였는지 기억나요?"], ["Look at me, marked until I die with his high displeasure; and moan and groan for what you made him!’", "날 봐요, 죽는 날까지 그의 분노로 낙인찍힌 이 몸을. 당신이 그를 그렇게 만들어 놓고선 신음하고 끙끙대요!\""]], [["‘Miss Dartle,’ I entreated her.", "\"다틀 양.\" 내가 그녀에게 애원했다."], ["‘For Heaven’s sake--’", "\"제발 부탁이니—\""]], [["‘I WILL speak!’ she said, turning on me with her lightning eyes.", "\"나는 말하고 말 거예요!\" 그녀가 번갯불 같은 눈으로 나를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["‘Be silent, you!", "\"당신은 입 다물어요!"], ["Look at me, I say, proud mother of a proud, false son!", "날 보라고요, 거만하고 거짓된 아들을 둔 거만한 어머니!"], ["Moan for your nurture of him, moan for your corruption of him, moan for your loss of him, moan for mine!’", "그를 길러 낸 것을 신음하고, 그를 타락시킨 것을 신음하고, 그를 잃은 것을 신음하고, 내가 그를 잃은 것을 신음해요!\""]], [["She clenched her hand, and trembled through her spare, worn figure, as if her passion were killing her by inches.", "그녀는 주먹을 불끈 쥐었고, 격정이 그 야위고 지친 몸을 조금씩 갉아먹어 가기라도 하듯 온몸을 부르르 떨었다."]], [["‘You, resent his self-will!’ she exclaimed.", "\"당신이, 그 애의 제멋대로인 고집에 분개한다고요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["‘You, injured by his haughty temper!", "\"당신이, 그 애의 오만한 성미에 상처받았다고요!"], ["You, who opposed to both, when your hair was grey, the qualities which made both when you gave him birth!", "머리가 희끗해진 당신이, 그 두 가지를 못마땅해하면서, 정작 그 애를 낳으면서 그 둘을 만들어 준 그 천성을 들이밀고 있는 거예요!"], ["YOU, who from his cradle reared him to be what he was, and stunted what he should have been!", "요람에서부터 그를 길러 지금의 그로 만들어 놓고, 마땅히 되어야 했을 모습을 짓밟아 버린 게 바로 당신이라고요!"], ["Are you rewarded, now, for your years of trouble?’", "그래, 이제 그 오랜 세월의 수고에 대한 보답을 받은 건가요?\""]], [["‘Oh, Miss Dartle, shame! Oh cruel!’", "\"오, 다틀 양, 부끄러운 줄 아세요! 오, 너무 잔인하군요!\""]], [["‘I tell you,’ she returned, ‘I WILL speak to her.", "\"내 말하지만,\" 그녀가 받았다. \"나는 이 사람한테 말하고 말 거예요."], ["No power on earth should stop me, while I was standing here!", "내가 여기 서 있는 한, 이 세상 그 어떤 힘도 나를 막지 못해요!"], ["Have I been silent all these years, and shall I not speak now?", "내가 이 숱한 세월을 입 다물고 살았는데, 지금 와서 말도 못 한단 말인가요?"], ["I loved him better than you ever loved him!’ turning on her fiercely.", "나는 당신이 그를 사랑한 것보다 더 그를 사랑했어요!\" 부인을 사납게 돌아보며 외쳤다."], ["‘I could have loved him, and asked no return.", "\"나는 그를 사랑하면서도 아무런 보답도 바라지 않을 수 있었어요."], ["If I had been his wife, I could have been the slave of his caprices for a word of love a year.", "내가 그의 아내였더라면, 일 년에 사랑한다는 한마디를 듣자고 그의 변덕에 노예가 되었을 거예요."], ["I should have been. Who knows it better than I?", "그랬을 거라고요. 그걸 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 어디 있겠어요?"], ["You were exacting, proud, punctilious, selfish.", "당신은 사람을 들들 볶고, 거만하고, 까다롭고, 이기적이었어요."], ["My love would have been devoted--would have trod your paltry whimpering under foot!’", "내 사랑은 헌신적이었을 거예요—당신의 그 하잘것없는 칭얼거림 따위는 발밑에 짓밟아 버렸을 거라고요!\""]], [["With flashing eyes, she stamped upon the ground as if she actually did it.", "번득이는 두 눈으로, 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 하기라도 하는 양 땅을 쾅쾅 굴렀다."]], [["‘Look here!’ she said, striking the scar again, with a relentless hand.", "\"여길 봐요!\" 그녀가 가차 없는 손길로 다시 흉터를 치며 말했다."], ["‘When he grew into the better understanding of what he had done, he saw it, and repented of it!", "\"그가 자기가 저지른 짓을 더 잘 헤아릴 만큼 자라자, 그걸 깨닫고 뉘우쳤어요!"], ["I could sing to him, and talk to him, and show the ardour that I felt in all he did, and attain with labour to such knowledge as most interested him; and I attracted him.", "나는 그에게 노래를 불러 주고, 이야기를 나누고, 그가 하는 모든 일에 내가 품은 열정을 보여 주고, 그가 가장 흥미로워하는 것이라면 무엇이든 애써 익혀 그만큼의 지식을 갖추었어요. 그렇게 나는 그를 사로잡았어요."], ["When he was freshest and truest, he loved me. Yes, he did!", "그가 가장 싱그럽고 가장 진실했던 시절에, 그는 나를 사랑했어요."], ["Many a time, when you were put off with a slight word, he has taken Me to his heart!’", "그래요, 정말이에요! 당신이 무심한 한마디로 내쳐지던 그 숱한 순간에, 그는 나를 제 가슴에 품었어요!\""]], [["She said it with a taunting pride in the midst of her frenzy--for it was little less--yet with an eager remembrance of it, in which the smouldering embers of a gentler feeling kindled for the moment.", "그녀는 그 광기—실로 광기에 가까웠다—의 한복판에서, 사람을 비웃는 듯한 자긍심을 담아 그렇게 말했으나, 한편으로는 그것을 애틋이 떠올리는 기색이 있었으니, 그 안에서 한결 부드러운 감정의 사그라들던 불씨가 한순간 되살아났다."]], [["‘I descended--as I might have known I should, but that he fascinated me with his boyish courtship--into a doll, a trifle for the occupation of an idle hour, to be dropped, and taken up, and trifled with, as the inconstant humour took him.", "\"나는 추락했어요—진작 알았어야 했지만, 그 풋풋한 구애에 홀려 버린 탓이었지요—인형 하나로, 한가한 시간을 때울 노리개 하나로 추락했어요. 그의 변덕스러운 기분이 일 때마다 내던져졌다가 다시 집어 들려지고, 또 가지고 놀려지는 노리개로요."], ["When he grew weary, I grew weary.", "그가 싫증을 내자, 나도 싫증이 났어요."], ["As his fancy died out, I would no more have tried to strengthen any power I had, than I would have married him on his being forced to take me for his wife.", "그의 마음이 식어 가자, 나는 내가 쥔 어떤 힘도 더 키워 보려 하지 않았으니, 그가 어쩔 수 없이 나를 아내로 맞아야 할 처지가 됐다 한들 그와 결혼하려 들지 않았을 것과 마찬가지였어요."], ["We fell away from one another without a word.", "우리는 말 한마디 없이 서로에게서 멀어졌어요."], ["Perhaps you saw it, and were not sorry.", "어쩌면 당신도 그걸 보고서 안타까워하지 않았겠지요."], ["Since then, I have been a mere disfigured piece of furniture between you both; having no eyes, no ears, no feelings, no remembrances. Moan?", "그 후로 나는 당신네 두 사람 사이에 놓인, 그저 흉하게 망가진 가구 한 점이었어요. 눈도 없고, 귀도 없고, 감정도 없고, 추억도 없는. 신음한다고요?"], ["Moan for what you made him; not for your love.", "당신이 그를 만들어 놓은 걸 신음해요. 당신의 사랑 때문이 아니라."], ["I tell you that the time was, when I loved him better than you ever did!’", "다시 말하지만, 한때는 내가 당신이 그를 사랑한 것보다 더 그를 사랑한 시절이 있었어요!\""]], [["She stood with her bright angry eyes confronting the wide stare, and the set face; and softened no more, when the moaning was repeated, than if the face had been a picture.", "그녀는 번뜩이는 성난 두 눈으로 그 크게 뜬 멍한 시선과 굳어 버린 얼굴을 마주하고 섰다. 그리고 신음이 되풀이될 때에도, 마치 그 얼굴이 한 폭의 그림이기라도 한 듯 조금도 누그러지지 않았다."]], [["‘Miss Dartle,’ said I, ‘if you can be so obdurate as not to feel for this afflicted mother--’", "\"다틀 양.\" 내가 말했다. \"이 비탄에 잠긴 어머니를 가엾이 여기지 못할 만큼 그토록 모질 수 있다면—\""]], [["‘Who feels for me?’ she sharply retorted.", "\"나를 가엾이 여겨 주는 사람은 누구죠?\" 그녀가 날카롭게 받아쳤다."], ["‘She has sown this.", "\"이건 저 여자가 뿌린 씨예요."], ["Let her moan for the harvest that she reaps today!’", "오늘 거두는 수확을 두고 저 여자더러 실컷 신음하라고 해요!\""]], [["‘And if his faults--’ I began.", "\"그리고 그에게 허물이 있었다 해도—\" 내가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Faults!’ she cried, bursting into passionate tears.", "\"허물이라고요!\" 그녀가 격정에 찬 눈물을 쏟으며 외쳤다."], ["‘Who dares malign him?", "\"누가 감히 그를 헐뜯어요?"], ["He had a soul worth millions of the friends to whom he stooped!’", "그는 자기가 굽히고 들어가 사귀던 그런 친구들 수백만 명을 합친 것보다 더 값진 영혼을 지녔어요!\""]], [["‘No one can have loved him better, no one can hold him in dearer remembrance than I,’ I replied.", "\"나만큼 그를 사랑한 사람도, 나만큼 그를 애틋이 기억하는 사람도 없을 겁니다.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["‘I meant to say, if you have no compassion for his mother; or if his faults--you have been bitter on them--’", "\"내가 하려던 말은, 만약 당신이 그의 어머니를 가엾이 여기지 못한다면, 아니면 그의 허물이—당신은 그 허물을 두고 모질게 말해 왔지요—\""]], [["‘It’s false,’ she cried, tearing her black hair; ‘I loved him!’", "\"거짓말이에요.\" 그녀가 검은 머리카락을 쥐어뜯으며 외쳤다. \"나는 그를 사랑했어요!\""]], [["‘--if his faults cannot,’ I went on, ‘be banished from your remembrance, in such an hour; look at that figure, even as one you have never seen before, and render it some help!’", "\"—만약 그의 허물을,\" 내가 말을 이었다. \"이런 순간에 당신의 기억에서 떨쳐 낼 수 없다면, 저 모습을, 한 번도 본 적 없는 사람을 대하듯 바라보고서라도, 부디 얼마간 도움의 손길을 베풀어 주세요!\""]], [["All this time, the figure was unchanged, and looked unchangeable.", "이러는 내내, 그 모습은 변함이 없었고, 변할 수도 없어 보였다."], ["Motionless, rigid, staring; moaning in the same dumb way from time to time, with the same helpless motion of the head; but giving no other sign of life.", "미동도 없이, 굳은 채로, 멍하니 응시하면서, 이따금 똑같이 말 없는 신음을 흘리며, 똑같이 머리를 가누지 못하고 움직였으나, 그 밖에 살아 있다는 다른 기색은 보이지 않았다."], ["Miss Dartle suddenly kneeled down before it, and began to loosen the dress.", "다틀 양이 갑자기 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 옷매무새를 풀기 시작했다."]], [["‘A curse upon you!’ she said, looking round at me, with a mingled expression of rage and grief.", "\"당신한테 저주가 내리길!\" 그녀가 분노와 비탄이 뒤섞인 표정으로 나를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["‘It was in an evil hour that you ever came here! A curse upon you! Go!’", "\"당신이 여기 온 건 불길한 순간이었어요! 당신한테 저주가 내리길! 가요!\""]], [["After passing out of the room, I hurried back to ring the bell, the sooner to alarm the servants.", "방을 나선 뒤, 나는 하인들에게 한시바삐 알리려고 종을 울리러 서둘러 되돌아갔다."], ["She had then taken the impassive figure in her arms, and, still upon her knees, was weeping over it, kissing it, calling to it, rocking it to and fro upon her bosom like a child, and trying every tender means to rouse the dormant senses.", "그때 그녀는 그 미동도 없는 형상을 두 팔로 끌어안고, 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채로, 그 위로 흐느끼며 입을 맞추고, 부르고, 어린아이를 어르듯 제 가슴에 안고 앞뒤로 흔들면서, 잠든 감각을 일깨우려고 온갖 다정한 수단을 다 써 보고 있었다."], ["No longer afraid of leaving her, I noiselessly turned back again; and alarmed the house as I went out.", "이제는 그녀를 두고 떠나는 것이 두렵지 않았기에, 나는 소리 없이 발길을 돌렸다. 그리고 나가면서 온 집안에 알렸다."]], [["Later in the day, I returned, and we laid him in his mother’s room.", "그날 늦게, 나는 다시 돌아갔고, 우리는 그를 그의 어머니 방에 눕혔다."], ["She was just the same, they told me; Miss Dartle never left her; doctors were in attendance, many things had been tried; but she lay like a statue, except for the low sound now and then.", "부인은 여전히 그대로라고 사람들이 일러 주었다. 다틀 양은 한시도 곁을 떠나지 않았고, 의사들이 와서 시중을 들었으며, 온갖 방도를 다 써 보았으나, 부인은 이따금 내는 낮은 소리 말고는 조각상처럼 누워 있었다."]], [["I went through the dreary house, and darkened the windows.", "나는 음울한 집 안을 돌아다니며 창마다 빛을 가렸다."], ["The windows of the chamber where he lay, I darkened last.", "그가 누워 있는 방의 창은 맨 마지막에 가렸다."], ["I lifted up the leaden hand, and held it to my heart; and all the world seemed death and silence, broken only by his mother’s moaning.", "나는 납덩이처럼 차가운 그 손을 들어 올려 내 가슴에 갖다 댔다. 그러자 온 세상이 죽음과 침묵뿐인 듯했고, 오직 그의 어머니가 흘리는 신음 소리만이 그 침묵을 깨뜨릴 따름이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 57. THE EMIGRANTS", "# CHAPTER 57. THE EMIGRANTS"]], [["One thing more, I had to do, before yielding myself to the shock of these emotions.", "이런 감정의 충격에 나 자신을 내맡기기 전에, 해야 할 일이 하나 더 있었다."], ["It was, to conceal what had occurred, from those who were going away; and to dismiss them on their voyage in happy ignorance.", "그것은 떠나는 사람들에게 일어난 일을 숨기는 것, 그래서 그들이 아무것도 모르는 행복 속에서 항해를 떠나게 하는 것이었다."], ["In this, no time was to be lost.", "여기에는 한시도 지체할 틈이 없었다."]], [["I took Mr. Micawber aside that same night, and confided to him the task of standing between Mr. Peggotty and intelligence of the late catastrophe.", "나는 바로 그날 밤 마이코버 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가, 패거티 씨와 얼마 전의 참사에 관한 소식 사이를 가로막는 일을 그에게 맡겼다."], ["He zealously undertook to do so, and to intercept any newspaper through which it might, without such precautions, reach him.", "그는 그 일을 기꺼이 떠맡았고, 그런 조심성이 없으면 패거티 씨에게 소식이 닿을지도 모를 신문이란 신문은 죄다 중간에서 가로채겠다고 했다."]], [["‘If it penetrates to him, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"혹시라도 그 소식이 그분께 뚫고 들어간다면 말입니다, 선생.\""], ["Micawber, striking himself on the breast, ‘it shall first pass through this body!’", "마이코버 씨가 제 가슴을 탁 치며 말했다. \"그건 먼저 이 몸뚱이를 지나가야 할 겁니다!\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, I must observe, in his adaptation of himself to a new state of society, had acquired a bold buccaneering air, not absolutely lawless, but defensive and prompt.", "내가 짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 마이코버 씨가 새로운 사회 형편에 자기를 맞추는 과정에서 대담한 해적 같은 풍모를 얻었다는 점이다. 완전히 무법자라고는 할 수 없으되, 방어적이면서도 민첩한 기색이었다."], ["One might have supposed him a child of the wilderness, long accustomed to live out of the confines of civilization, and about to return to his native wilds.", "누가 보아도 그가 황야의 자식으로, 문명의 울타리 밖에서 사는 데 오래 익숙해진 사람이라, 이제 곧 제 고향의 거친 들판으로 돌아가려는 참이라고 여겼을 법했다."]], [["He had provided himself, among other things, with a complete suit of oilskin, and a straw hat with a very low crown, pitched or caulked on the outside.", "그는 여러 가지 채비 가운데에서도 기름먹인 방수복 한 벌을 빠짐없이 갖추고, 챙이 아주 낮고 겉에 역청이나 뱃밥을 발라 메운 밀짚모자 하나를 마련해 두었다."], ["In this rough clothing, with a common mariner’s telescope under his arm, and a shrewd trick of casting up his eye at the sky as looking out for dirty weather, he was far more nautical, after his manner, than Mr. Peggotty.", "이 거친 옷차림에, 흔한 뱃사람의 망원경을 팔에 끼고, 궂은 날씨를 살피기라도 하듯 하늘을 향해 눈을 치켜뜨는 약삭빠른 시늉까지 더하니, 그는 그 나름의 방식으로 패거티 씨보다 훨씬 더 뱃사람다웠다."], ["His whole family, if I may so express it, were cleared for action.", "그의 온 식구가, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 전투 준비를 마친 상태였다."], ["I found Mrs. Micawber in the closest and most uncompromising of bonnets, made fast under the chin; and in a shawl which tied her up (as I had been tied up, when my aunt first received me) like a bundle, and was secured behind at the waist, in a strong knot.", "마이코버 부인은 더없이 꽉 끼고 한 치의 타협도 없는 보닛을 턱 밑에서 단단히 묶어 쓰고, 숄로는 (고모님이 나를 처음 거두었을 때 나를 동여맸던 것처럼) 몸을 보따리처럼 칭칭 동여매어 등 뒤 허리께에서 단단한 매듭으로 여민 모습이었다."], ["Miss Micawber I found made snug for stormy weather, in the same manner; with nothing superfluous about her.", "마이코버 양도 같은 방식으로 폭풍우에 대비해 빈틈없이 채비를 갖추었고, 몸에 거추장스러운 것이라곤 하나도 없었다."], ["Master Micawber was hardly visible in a Guernsey shirt, and the shaggiest suit of slops I ever saw; and the children were done up, like preserved meats, in impervious cases. Both Mr.", "마이코버 도련님은 건지 셔츠와 내가 본 것 중 가장 텁수룩한 선원 작업복에 파묻혀 거의 보이지도 않았다. 어린아이들은 통조림 고기처럼 물 한 방울 새지 않는 껍데기에 꽁꽁 싸여 있었다."], ["Micawber and his eldest son wore their sleeves loosely turned back at the wrists, as being ready to lend a hand in any direction, and to ‘tumble up’, or sing out, ‘Yeo--Heave--Yeo!’ on the shortest notice.", "마이코버 씨와 그 맏아들은 둘 다 소맷부리를 손목까지 헐겁게 걷어 올렸는데, 어느 방향으로든 일손을 보탤 채비가 되어 있는 모양새요, 신호만 떨어지면 곧장 \"갑판으로!\" 하고 뛰어오르거나 \"어기—여차—어기!\" 하고 소리를 지를 태세였다."]], [["Thus Traddles and I found them at nightfall, assembled on the wooden steps, at that time known as Hungerford Stairs, watching the departure of a boat with some of their property on board.", "이렇게 차린 그들을, 트래들스와 나는 해 질 녘에, 그 무렵 헝거포드 계단이라 불리던 나무 층계 위에 모여, 자기네 짐 일부를 실은 배가 떠나가는 모습을 지켜보고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["I had told Traddles of the terrible event, and it had greatly shocked him; but there could be no doubt of the kindness of keeping it a secret, and he had come to help me in this last service.", "나는 트래들스에게 그 끔찍한 사건을 이미 일러두었고, 그는 큰 충격을 받았다. 그러나 그것을 비밀로 묻어 두는 것이 마땅하다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었고, 그래서 그는 이 마지막 수고를 거들러 와 준 것이었다."], ["It was here that I took Mr. Micawber aside, and received his promise.", "마이코버 씨를 한쪽으로 데려가 그의 약속을 받아 낸 것이 바로 이곳이었다."]], [["The Micawber family were lodged in a little, dirty, tumble-down public-house, which in those days was close to the stairs, and whose protruding wooden rooms overhung the river.", "마이코버 식구는 그 무렵 계단 바로 곁에 있던, 작고 더럽고 곧 허물어질 듯한 선술집에 묵고 있었는데, 그 집의 비죽 튀어나온 나무 방들은 강물 위로 매달려 있었다."], ["The family, as emigrants, being objects of some interest in and about Hungerford, attracted so many beholders, that we were glad to take refuge in their room.", "이민을 떠나는 식구로서 헝거포드 일대에서 적잖은 구경거리가 된 그들은 어찌나 많은 구경꾼을 끌어모았던지, 우리는 그들의 방으로 피신하게 되어 차라리 다행이었다."], ["It was one of the wooden chambers upstairs, with the tide flowing underneath.", "위층 나무 방 가운데 하나였는데, 그 아래로 밀물이 흐르고 있었다."], ["My aunt and Agnes were there, busily making some little extra comforts, in the way of dress, for the children.", "고모님과 애그니스가 거기 있으면서, 아이들을 위해 옷가지 쪽으로 조금이나마 더 살뜰한 채비를 부지런히 마련하고 있었다."], ["Peggotty was quietly assisting, with the old insensible work-box, yard-measure, and bit of wax-candle before her, that had now outlived so much.", "패거티는 그동안 숱한 세월을 살아남은 그 무덤덤한 낡은 반짇고리며 줄자며 밀초 토막을 앞에 놓고, 조용히 거들고 있었다."]], [["It was not easy to answer her inquiries; still less to whisper Mr.", "그녀의 물음에 답하기란 쉽지 않았다."], ["Peggotty, when Mr. Micawber brought him in, that I had given the letter, and all was well.", "마이코버 씨가 패거티 씨를 데리고 들어왔을 때, 내가 편지를 전했고 만사가 잘되었다고 그에게 귓속말하기란 더욱 어려웠다."], ["But I did both, and made them happy.", "그러나 나는 두 가지를 다 해냈고, 그들을 기쁘게 했다."], ["If I showed any trace of what I felt, my own sorrows were sufficient to account for it.", "혹시라도 내 심정의 흔적이 조금이라도 드러났다면, 내 자신의 슬픔만으로도 그것을 충분히 둘러댈 수 있었으리라."]], [["‘And when does the ship sail, Mr. Micawber?’ asked my aunt.", "\"그래, 배는 언제 떠나나요, 마이코버 씨?\" 고모님이 물었다."]], [["Mr. Micawber considered it necessary to prepare either my aunt or his wife, by degrees, and said, sooner than he had expected yesterday.", "마이코버 씨는 고모님이든 자기 아내든 조금씩 마음의 준비를 시켜 두어야겠다고 여겼는지, 어제 짐작했던 것보다 더 빨리 떠난다고 말했다."]], [["‘The boat brought you word, I suppose?’ said my aunt.", "\"보트가 소식을 전해 줬나 보군요?\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘It did, ma’am,’ he returned.", "\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 그가 대답했다."]], [["‘Well?’ said my aunt.", "\"그래서요?\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘And she sails--’", "\"그러니까 배가 떠나는 게—\""]], [["‘Madam,’ he replied, ‘I am informed that we must positively be on board before seven tomorrow morning.’", "\"부인,\" 그가 대답했다. \"내일 아침 일곱 시 전에는 반드시 승선해 있어야 한다는 통지를 받았습니다.\""]], [["‘Heyday!’ said my aunt, ‘that’s soon.", "\"아이고머니나!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그거 빠르군."], ["Is it a sea-going fact, Mr. Peggotty?’ ‘’Tis so, ma’am.", "그게 항해상 확실한 사실인가요, 패거티 씨?\" \"그렇습죠, 부인."], ["She’ll drop down the river with that theer tide.", "배는 저 밀물을 타고 강을 따라 내려갈 겝니다."], ["If Mas’r Davy and my sister comes aboard at Gravesen’, arternoon o’ next day, they’ll see the last on us.’", "데이비 도련님하고 지 누이가 그담 날 오후에 그레이브젠드에서 배에 올라타면, 떠나는 우리 마지막 모습을 보게 될 거예유.\""]], [["‘And that we shall do,’ said I, ‘be sure!’", "\"그렇게 하고말고요,\" 내가 말했다. \"꼭 그러겠습니다!\""]], [["‘Until then, and until we are at sea,’ observed Mr.", "\"그때까지, 그리고 우리가 바다로 나갈 때까지,\""], ["Micawber, with a glance of intelligence at me, ‘Mr.", "마이코버 씨가 나에게 알겠다는 눈짓을 보내며 말했다."], ["Peggotty and myself will constantly keep a double look-out together, on our goods and chattels.", "\"패거티 씨와 나는 우리 살림 세간을 함께 빈틈없이 이중으로 지켜볼 작정입니다."], ["Emma, my love,’ said Mr.", "에마, 여보,\""], ["Micawber, clearing his throat in his magnificent way, ‘my friend Mr.", "마이코버 씨가 그 특유의 당당한 방식으로 헛기침을 하며 말했다."], ["Thomas Traddles is so obliging as to solicit, in my ear, that he should have the privilege of ordering the ingredients necessary to the composition of a moderate portion of that Beverage which is peculiarly associated, in our minds, with the Roast Beef of Old England.", "\"내 벗 토머스 트래들스 씨가 어찌나 친절하신지, 우리 마음속에서 옛 잉글랜드의 구운 쇠고기와 유독 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 얽혀 있는 그 음료를 적당량 빚는 데 필요한 재료를 주문할 특권을 자기에게 달라고 내 귀에 청해 왔구려."], ["I allude to--in short, Punch. Under ordinary circumstances, I should scruple to entreat the indulgence of Miss Trotwood and Miss Wickfield, but-’", "내가 말하는 것은—한마디로, 펀치라오. 평상시라면 트롯우드 양과 위크필드 양의 너그러움을 구하기를 망설였겠지만—\""]], [["‘I can only say for myself,’ said my aunt, ‘that I will drink all happiness and success to you, Mr.", "\"제 입장에서만 말씀드리자면,\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["Micawber, with the utmost pleasure.’", "\"더없는 기쁨으로 당신들 모두의 행복과 성공을 빌며 마시겠어요, 마이코버 씨.\""]], [["‘And I too!’ said Agnes, with a smile.", "\"저도요!\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Micawber immediately descended to the bar, where he appeared to be quite at home; and in due time returned with a steaming jug.", "마이코버 씨는 곧장 술청으로 내려갔는데, 거기서는 마치 제집처럼 편안해 보였다. 그리고 때맞춰 김이 모락모락 나는 주전자를 들고 돌아왔다."], ["I could not but observe that he had been peeling the lemons with his own clasp-knife, which, as became the knife of a practical settler, was about a foot long; and which he wiped, not wholly without ostentation, on the sleeve of his coat. Mrs.", "그가 제 접칼로 손수 레몬 껍질을 벗기고 있었다는 것을 나는 알아차리지 않을 수 없었는데, 실전에 나선 개척자의 칼답게 그 길이가 한 자쯤 되었고, 그는 그것을 다소 으스대는 기색이 없지 않게 제 외투 소매에다 쓱쓱 닦았다."], ["Micawber and the two elder members of the family I now found to be provided with similar formidable instruments, while every child had its own wooden spoon attached to its body by a strong line. In a similar anticipation of life afloat, and in the Bush, Mr.", "마이코버 부인과 그 집안의 손위 두 사람도 비슷하게 어마어마한 연장을 갖추고 있었으며, 어린아이들은 저마다 나무 숟가락을 튼튼한 끈으로 제 몸에 매달고 있었다."], ["Micawber, instead of helping Mrs. Micawber and his eldest son and daughter to punch, in wine-glasses, which he might easily have done, for there was a shelf-full in the room, served it out to them in a series of villainous little tin pots; and I never saw him enjoy anything so much as drinking out of his own particular pint pot, and putting it in his pocket at the close of the evening.", "이렇게 배 위 생활과 오지 생활을 미리 내다보는 마음에서, 마이코버 씨는 방 안 선반 가득 술잔이 있었으니 마음만 먹으면 손쉽게 그랬을 텐데도, 마이코버 부인과 맏아들과 맏딸에게 술잔에 펀치를 따라 주는 대신, 흉측하기 짝이 없는 작은 양철 단지들에 차례로 따라 돌렸다. 그리고 그가 제 전용 한 파인트짜리 단지로 펀치를 마시고, 그날 밤이 다 끝날 무렵 그것을 제 주머니에 챙겨 넣을 때만큼 무언가를 그렇게 즐기는 모습을 나는 본 적이 없다."]], [["‘The luxuries of the old country,’ said Mr.", "\"옛 고국의 사치품들은,\""], ["Micawber, with an intense satisfaction in their renouncement, ‘we abandon.", "마이코버 씨가 그것들을 내던지는 데서 절절한 만족감을 느끼며 말했다."], ["The denizens of the forest cannot, of course, expect to participate in the refinements of the land of the Free.’", "\"우리가 버립니다. 숲속의 주민들이야 자유의 땅이 누리는 세련됨에 끼어들기를 어찌 바라겠습니까.\""]], [["Here, a boy came in to say that Mr. Micawber was wanted downstairs.", "이때 사내아이 하나가 들어와 마이코버 씨를 아래층에서 찾는다고 했다."]], [["‘I have a presentiment,’ said Mrs.", "\"예감이 들어요,\""], ["Micawber, setting down her tin pot, ‘that it is a member of my family!’", "마이코버 부인이 양철 단지를 내려놓으며 말했다. \"우리 집안 식구일 거예요!\""]], [["‘If so, my dear,’ observed Mr.", "\"만약 그렇다면, 여보,\""], ["Micawber, with his usual suddenness of warmth on that subject, ‘as the member of your family--whoever he, she, or it, may be--has kept us waiting for a considerable period, perhaps the Member may now wait MY convenience.’", "마이코버 씨가 그 화제에 대해 늘 그러듯 별안간 발끈하며 말했다. \"당신 집안 식구라는 자가—그게 남자든 여자든 무엇이든 간에—꽤 오랫동안 우릴 기다리게 했으니, 이제 그 식구분께서는 내 편할 때까지 좀 기다리셔도 되겠구려.\""]], [["‘Micawber,’ said his wife, in a low tone, ‘at such a time as this--’", "\"마이코버,\" 그의 아내가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이런 때에—\""]], [["‘“It is not meet,”’ said Mr. Micawber, rising, ‘“that every nice offence should bear its comment!”", "\"'사소한 허물마다 일일이 따지고 드는 것은 온당치 못하도다!'\" 마이코버 씨가 일어서며 말했다."], ["Emma, I stand reproved.’", "\"에마, 내가 책망을 받아 마땅하오.\""]], [["‘The loss, Micawber,’ observed his wife, ‘has been my family’s, not yours.", "\"손해를 본 건, 마이코버,\" 그의 아내가 말했다. \"우리 집안이지, 당신이 아니에요."], ["If my family are at length sensible of the deprivation to which their own conduct has, in the past, exposed them, and now desire to extend the hand of fellowship, let it not be repulsed.’", "우리 집안 식구가 마침내 지난날 제 처신 탓에 스스로 자초한 그 결핍을 깨닫고, 이제 화해의 손길을 내밀고자 한다면, 그것을 매정하게 뿌리치지는 말아 주세요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ he returned, ‘so be it!’", "\"여보,\" 그가 대답했다. \"그럼 그러리다!\""]], [["‘If not for their sakes; for mine, Micawber,’ said his wife.", "\"그들을 위해서가 아니라면, 나를 위해서라도요, 마이코버.\" 그의 아내가 말했다."]], [["‘Emma,’ he returned, ‘that view of the question is, at such a moment, irresistible.", "\"에마,\" 그가 대답했다. \"이런 순간에는, 그렇게 보면 거역할 도리가 없구려."], ["I cannot, even now, distinctly pledge myself to fall upon your family’s neck; but the member of your family, who is now in attendance, shall have no genial warmth frozen by me.’", "지금조차도 당신 집안의 목을 부둥켜안겠노라고 똑똑히 맹세하지는 못하겠으나, 지금 와 있는 당신 집안 식구만큼은 그 따뜻한 정이 나 때문에 얼어붙는 일은 없도록 하리다.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber withdrew, and was absent some little time; in the course of which Mrs.", "마이코버 씨는 자리를 떴고, 잠시 동안 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Micawber was not wholly free from an apprehension that words might have arisen between him and the Member.", "그러는 사이 마이코버 부인은 그와 그 식구 사이에 말다툼이 벌어지지나 않았을까 하는 걱정에서 영 자유롭지 못했다."], ["At length the same boy reappeared, and presented me with a note written in pencil, and headed, in a legal manner, ‘Heep v. Micawber’.", "마침내 아까 그 사내아이가 다시 나타나, 연필로 쓰고 법률 문서처럼 '힙 대 마이코버'라는 표제를 단 쪽지 하나를 나에게 건넸다."], ["From this document, I learned that Mr. Micawber being again arrested, ‘Was in a final paroxysm of despair; and that he begged me to send him his knife and pint pot, by bearer, as they might prove serviceable during the brief remainder of his existence, in jail.", "이 문서로 미루어 나는, 마이코버 씨가 또 체포되어 '절망의 마지막 발작에 빠졌으며, 부디 칼과 한 파인트짜리 단지를 이 심부름꾼 편에 보내 달라, 감옥에서 자기 생애의 짧은 나머지 동안 요긴하게 쓰일지도 모른다'고 청했음을 알게 되었다."], ["He also requested, as a last act of friendship, that I would see his family to the Parish Workhouse, and forget that such a Being ever lived.", "그는 또한 마지막 우정의 표시로, 자기 식구를 교구 구빈원으로 데려다주고, 그런 인간이 일찍이 살았었다는 사실을 잊어 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["Of course I answered this note by going down with the boy to pay the money, where I found Mr. Micawber sitting in a corner, looking darkly at the Sheriff ‘s Officer who had effected the capture.", "물론 나는 그 사내아이와 함께 내려가 돈을 치름으로써 이 쪽지에 답했고, 거기서 마이코버 씨가 한쪽 구석에 앉아, 자기를 붙잡아 들인 보안관 집행관을 음울하게 노려보고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["On his release, he embraced me with the utmost fervour; and made an entry of the transaction in his pocket-book--being very particular, I recollect, about a halfpenny I inadvertently omitted from my statement of the total.", "풀려나자 그는 더없이 격렬하게 나를 껴안았고, 그 거래를 제 수첩에 적어 넣었는데—내 기억으로는, 내가 총액을 셈하면서 깜빡 빠뜨린 반 페니 하나를 두고 무척이나 꼼꼼하게 굴었다."]], [["This momentous pocket-book was a timely reminder to him of another transaction.", "이 중대한 수첩은 그에게 또 다른 거래 하나를 때맞춰 일깨워 주었다."], ["On our return to the room upstairs (where he accounted for his absence by saying that it had been occasioned by circumstances over which he had no control), he took out of it a large sheet of paper, folded small, and quite covered with long sums, carefully worked.", "위층 방으로 돌아오자 (거기서 그는 자기가 자리를 비운 것을 두고 자기로서는 어쩔 수 없는 사정 탓이었다고 둘러댔다), 그는 수첩에서 작게 접힌 큼지막한 종이 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 정성껏 풀어낸 긴 계산식들이 빼곡히 적혀 있었다."], ["From the glimpse I had of them, I should say that I never saw such sums out of a school ciphering-book.", "흘끗 본 것만으로도 나는, 학교 산수 공책 바깥에서 그런 셈을 본 적이 없다고 말할 수 있을 정도였다."], ["These, it seemed, were calculations of compound interest on what he called ‘the principal amount of forty-one, ten, eleven and a half’, for various periods.", "알고 보니 그것들은 그가 '원금 사십일 파운드 십 실링 십일 펜스 반'이라 부르는 금액에 대해 여러 기간에 걸쳐 복리를 계산한 것이었다."], ["After a careful consideration of these, and an elaborate estimate of his resources, he had come to the conclusion to select that sum which represented the amount with compound interest to two years, fifteen calendar months, and fourteen days, from that date.", "이것들을 꼼꼼히 따져 보고, 자기 재산을 정교하게 어림한 끝에, 그는 그날부터 헤아려 이태 하고도 열다섯 달 하고도 열나흘에 이르는 복리를 더한 금액을 나타내는 그 합계를 고르기로 결론을 내렸다."], ["For this he had drawn a note-of-hand with great neatness, which he handed over to Traddles on the spot, a discharge of his debt in full (as between man and man), with many acknowledgements.", "이것을 위해 그는 더없이 깔끔하게 약속어음을 작성했고, 그 자리에서 그것을 트래들스에게 넘겨주면서 (사람 대 사람으로서) 자기 빚을 전액 청산했노라고, 거듭 고마움을 표하며 말했다."]], [["‘I have still a presentiment,’ said Mrs.", "\"아직도 예감이 들어요,\""], ["Micawber, pensively shaking her head, ‘that my family will appear on board, before we finally depart.’", "마이코버 부인이 시름에 잠겨 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"우리가 마지막으로 떠나기 전에 우리 집안 식구가 배 위에 나타날 거라고요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber evidently had his presentiment on the subject too, but he put it in his tin pot and swallowed it.", "마이코버 씨도 그 일에 대해 분명 제 나름의 예감이 있었으나, 그것을 양철 단지에 담아 꿀꺽 삼켜 버렸다."]], [["‘If you have any opportunity of sending letters home, on your passage, Mrs.", "\"항해 도중에 본국으로 편지를 보낼 기회가 조금이라도 생기거든, 마이코버 부인,\""], ["Micawber,’ said my aunt, ‘you must let us hear from you, you know.’", "고모님이 말했다. \"꼭 소식을 전해 주셔야 해요, 아시겠지요.\""]], [["‘My dear Miss Trotwood,’ she replied, ‘I shall only be too happy to think that anyone expects to hear from us.", "\"고마우신 트롯우드 양,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"누군가 우리 소식을 기다린다고 생각하는 것만으로도 더없이 기쁠 따름이에요."], ["I shall not fail to correspond. Mr.", "빠짐없이 편지를 올리겠어요."], ["Copperfield, I trust, as an old and familiar friend, will not object to receive occasional intelligence, himself, from one who knew him when the twins were yet unconscious?’", "코퍼필드 씨도, 오래되고 허물없는 벗으로서, 쌍둥이가 아직 철도 들기 전부터 자기를 알던 사람한테서 이따금 소식 받는 것을 마다하지 않으시리라 믿어요.\""]], [["I said that I should hope to hear, whenever she had an opportunity of writing.", "나는 그녀에게 편지 쓸 기회가 생길 때마다 소식 듣기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["‘Please Heaven, there will be many such opportunities,’ said Mr. Micawber.", "\"하느님의 뜻이라면, 그런 기회는 숱하게 있을 겁니다.\" 마이코버 씨가 말했다."], ["‘The ocean, in these times, is a perfect fleet of ships; and we can hardly fail to encounter many, in running over.", "\"요즘 바다는 그야말로 배들의 함대지요. 건너가는 동안 숱한 배와 마주치지 않을 도리가 없습니다."], ["It is merely crossing,’ said Mr.", "그저 한번 건너는 것일 뿐이지요.\""], ["Micawber, trifling with his eye-glass, ‘merely crossing.", "마이코버 씨가 외알 안경을 만지작거리며 말했다. \"그저 한번 건너는 것일 뿐."], ["The distance is quite imaginary.’", "그 거리란 순전히 상상 속에나 있는 것입니다.\""]], [["I think, now, how odd it was, but how wonderfully like Mr. Micawber, that, when he went from London to Canterbury, he should have talked as if he were going to the farthest limits of the earth; and, when he went from England to Australia, as if he were going for a little trip across the channel.", "지금 와서 생각하면 참 묘한 일이지만, 또 그것이 얼마나 마이코버 씨다운지 모르겠다. 그는 런던에서 캔터베리로 갈 때는 세상 끝 가장 먼 데까지 떠나는 사람처럼 말하더니, 막상 잉글랜드에서 오스트레일리아로 가게 되자 마치 해협 건너로 잠깐 나들이라도 가는 듯이 말했으니 말이다."]], [["‘On the voyage, I shall endeavour,’ said Mr.", "\"항해하는 동안 나는 애써서,\""], ["Micawber, ‘occasionally to spin them a yarn; and the melody of my son Wilkins will, I trust, be acceptable at the galley-fire. When Mrs.", "마이코버 씨가 말했다. \"이따금 그들에게 옛이야기를 풀어 줄 것이고, 내 아들 윌킨스의 노래도 부디 취사장 화롯가에서 환영받기를 바랍니다."], ["Micawber has her sea-legs on--an expression in which I hope there is no conventional impropriety--she will give them, I dare say, “Little Tafflin”.", "마이코버 부인도 뱃멀미를 떨치고 발이 갑판에 익으면—이 표현에 관습상 무례함이 없기를 바랍니다만—틀림없이 그들에게 '꼬마 태플린'을 불러 줄 겁니다."], ["Porpoises and dolphins, I believe, will be frequently observed athwart our Bows; and, either on the starboard or the larboard quarter, objects of interest will be continually descried.", "쇠돌고래며 돌고래가 우리 뱃머리를 가로질러 자주 보이리라 믿고, 우현이든 좌현이든 가릴 것 없이 흥미로운 것들이 끊임없이 눈에 띄겠지요."], ["In short,’ said Mr.", "한마디로,\""], ["Micawber, with the old genteel air, ‘the probability is, all will be found so exciting, alow and aloft, that when the lookout, stationed in the main-top, cries Land-oh!", "마이코버 씨가 그 옛 점잖은 풍모로 말했다. \"갑판 위아래로 죄다 어찌나 흥미진진할지, 큰돛대 망루에 올라간 망꾼이 '육지다!'"], ["we shall be very considerably astonished!’", "하고 외칠 때쯤이면, 우리는 어지간히 깜짝 놀라 있을 겁니다!\""]], [["With that he flourished off the contents of his little tin pot, as if he had made the voyage, and had passed a first-class examination before the highest naval authorities.", "그러면서 그는 마치 이미 그 항해를 다 마치고 최고위 해군 당국 앞에서 일등 시험에 합격이라도 한 양, 제 작은 양철 단지의 내용물을 호기롭게 들이켰다."]], [["‘What I chiefly hope, my dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"제가 무엇보다 바라는 것은요, 고마우신 코퍼필드 씨,\""], ["Micawber, ‘is, that in some branches of our family we may live again in the old country.", "마이코버 부인이 말했다. \"우리 집안의 어느 가지에서든 옛 고국에서 다시 살아가게 되는 거예요."], ["Do not frown, Micawber!", "인상 쓰지 말아요, 마이코버!"], ["I do not now refer to my own family, but to our children’s children.", "지금은 내 친정 식구 얘기가 아니라, 우리 손주들의 손주 얘기를 하는 거예요."], ["However vigorous the sapling,’ said Mrs.", "어린 묘목이 아무리 기운차게 자란다 해도,\""], ["Micawber, shaking her head, ‘I cannot forget the parent-tree; and when our race attains to eminence and fortune, I own I should wish that fortune to flow into the coffers of Britannia.’", "마이코버 부인이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"나는 그 모태가 된 나무를 잊을 수가 없어요. 우리 후손이 높은 지위와 부를 이루게 되면, 솔직히 나는 그 재산이 브리타니아의 금고로 흘러들기를 바라요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr.", "\"여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘Britannia must take her chance.", "\"브리타니아는 제 운에 맡겨야지요."], ["I am bound to say that she has never done much for me, and that I have no particular wish upon the subject.’", "내 분명히 말하건대, 그 나라가 나를 위해 해 준 게 별로 없으니, 나로서는 그 일에 딱히 바라는 바도 없소.\""]], [["‘Micawber,’ returned Mrs.", "\"마이코버,\" 마이코버 부인이 받아쳤다."], ["Micawber, ‘there, you are wrong.", "\"그 점에서는, 당신이 틀렸어요."], ["You are going out, Micawber, to this distant clime, to strengthen, not to weaken, the connexion between yourself and Albion.’", "당신은, 마이코버, 이 머나먼 땅으로 나가는 거예요. 당신 자신과 앨비언 사이의 인연을 약화시키러 가는 게 아니라, 강화하러 가는 거라고요.\""]], [["‘The connexion in question, my love,’ rejoined Mr.", "\"문제의 그 인연이란, 여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 받아 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘has not laid me, I repeat, under that load of personal obligation, that I am at all sensitive as to the formation of another connexion.’", "\"거듭 말하지만, 또 다른 인연을 맺는 일에 내가 조금이라도 예민해질 만큼, 나에게 그런 개인적 신세의 짐을 지운 적이 없소.\""]], [["‘Micawber,’ returned Mrs. Micawber.", "\"마이코버,\" 마이코버 부인이 받아쳤다."], ["‘There, I again say, you are wrong.", "\"그 점에서, 다시 말하지만, 당신이 틀렸어요."], ["You do not know your power, Micawber.", "당신은 당신의 힘을 모르고 있어요, 마이코버."], ["It is that which will strengthen, even in this step you are about to take, the connexion between yourself and Albion.’", "바로 그 힘이야말로, 지금 당신이 내디디려는 이 한 걸음에서조차, 당신 자신과 앨비언 사이의 인연을 더욱 굳건히 다져 줄 거예요.\""]], [["Mr. Micawber sat in his elbow-chair, with his eyebrows raised; half receiving and half repudiating Mrs. Micawber’s views as they were stated, but very sensible of their foresight.", "마이코버 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 눈썹을 치켜올린 채, 마이코버 부인이 펼쳐 놓는 견해를 반은 받아들이고 반은 물리치면서도, 그 선견지명만큼은 절절히 느끼고 있었다."]], [["‘My dear Mr. Copperfield,’ said Mrs.", "\"고마우신 코퍼필드 씨,\" 마이코버 부인이 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘I wish Mr. Micawber to feel his position.", "\"나는 마이코버 씨가 자기 위치를 자각하기를 바라요."], ["It appears to me highly important that Mr. Micawber should, from the hour of his embarkation, feel his position.", "마이코버 씨가 배에 오르는 그 순간부터 자기 위치를 자각하는 것이, 내가 보기엔 더없이 중요해요."], ["Your old knowledge of me, my dear Mr. Copperfield, will have told you that I have not the sanguine disposition of Mr. Micawber.", "나에 대한 당신의 오랜 앎이, 고마우신 코퍼필드 씨, 내가 마이코버 씨처럼 낙천적인 기질이 아니라는 걸 일러 주었을 거예요."], ["My disposition is, if I may say so, eminently practical.", "내 기질은, 이렇게 말해도 된다면, 지극히 현실적이지요."], ["I know that this is a long voyage.", "나는 이것이 긴 항해라는 걸 알아요."], ["I know that it will involve many privations and inconveniences.", "숱한 결핍과 불편이 따르리라는 것도 알아요."], ["I cannot shut my eyes to those facts.", "그런 사실들에 눈감을 수는 없지요."], ["But I also know what Mr. Micawber is.", "하지만 나는 또한 마이코버 씨가 어떤 사람인지도 알아요."], ["I know the latent power of Mr. Micawber.", "마이코버 씨 안에 잠재된 힘을 알지요."], ["And therefore I consider it vitally important that Mr. Micawber should feel his position.’", "그러니 나는 마이코버 씨가 자기 위치를 자각하는 것이 무엇보다 중요하다고 여기는 거예요.\""]], [["‘My love,’ he observed, ‘perhaps you will allow me to remark that it is barely possible that I DO feel my position at the present moment.’", "\"여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"어쩌면 한마디 해도 될까 하는데, 지금 이 순간 내가 정말로 내 위치를 자각하고 있을 가능성도 영 없지는 않다오.\""]], [["‘I think not, Micawber,’ she rejoined.", "\"내 생각엔 아니에요, 마이코버.\""], ["‘Not fully. My dear Mr.", "그녀가 받아 말했다."], ["Copperfield, Mr.", "\"온전히는 아니지요."], ["Micawber’s is not a common case. Mr. Micawber is going to a distant country expressly in order that he may be fully understood and appreciated for the first time.", "고마우신 코퍼필드 씨, 마이코버 씨의 경우는 흔한 경우가 아니에요. 마이코버 씨가 먼 나라로 가는 건, 다름 아니라 난생처음으로 온전히 이해받고 인정받기 위해서지요."], ["I wish Mr. Micawber to take his stand upon that vessel’s prow, and firmly say, “This country I am come to conquer!", "나는 마이코버 씨가 저 배의 뱃머리에 떡 버티고 서서, 단호하게 이렇게 말하길 바라요. '내가 정복하러 온 나라가 바로 이곳이다!"], ["Have you honours?", "너에게 영예가 있느냐?"], ["Have you riches?", "너에게 부가 있느냐?"], ["Have you posts of profitable pecuniary emolument?", "너에게 짭짤한 금전적 보수가 나오는 자리가 있느냐?"], ["Let them be brought forward. They are mine!”’", "죄다 가져오너라. 그건 다 내 것이다!'\""]], [["Mr. Micawber, glancing at us all, seemed to think there was a good deal in this idea.", "마이코버 씨는 우리 모두를 흘끗 둘러보며, 이 생각에 제법 일리가 있다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["‘I wish Mr. Micawber, if I make myself understood,’ said Mrs.", "\"내 말뜻이 통한다면, 나는 마이코버 씨가,\" 마이코버 부인이 논리를 따지는 어조로 말했다."], ["Micawber, in her argumentative tone, ‘to be the Caesar of his own fortunes. That, my dear Mr.", "\"제 운명의 카이사르가 되길 바라요. 그것이, 고마우신 코퍼필드 씨, 내가 보기엔 그분의 참된 위치예요."], ["Copperfield, appears to me to be his true position. From the first moment of this voyage, I wish Mr.", "이 항해의 첫 순간부터, 나는 마이코버 씨가 저 배의 뱃머리에 서서 이렇게 말하길 바라요."], ["Micawber to stand upon that vessel’s prow and say, “Enough of delay: enough of disappointment: enough of limited means.", "'지체는 그만하면 됐다. 실망도 그만하면 됐다. 쪼들리는 형편도 그만하면 됐다."], ["That was in the old country.", "그건 옛 고국에서의 일이었다."], ["This is the new.", "여기는 새 나라다."], ["Produce your reparation.", "너의 보상을 내놓아라."], ["Bring it forward!”’", "어서 가져오너라!'\""]], [["Mr. Micawber folded his arms in a resolute manner, as if he were then stationed on the figure-head.", "마이코버 씨는 마치 그때 이미 뱃머리 선수상 위에 떡 버티고 서 있기라도 한 듯, 단호하게 두 팔을 팔짱 꼈다."]], [["‘And doing that,’ said Mrs.", "\"그리고 그렇게 하면,\""], ["Micawber, ‘--feeling his position--am I not right in saying that Mr. Micawber will strengthen, and not weaken, his connexion with Britain?", "마이코버 부인이 말했다. \"자기 위치를 자각하면 말이에요—마이코버 씨가 영국과의 인연을 약화시키는 게 아니라 강화하리라고 말한다면, 내가 틀린 건가요?"], ["An important public character arising in that hemisphere, shall I be told that its influence will not be felt at home?", "저 반구에서 중요한 공인이 한 사람 솟아오르는데, 그 영향력이 본국에서 느껴지지 않으리라고 나에게 말할 수 있을까요?"], ["Can I be so weak as to imagine that Mr. Micawber, wielding the rod of talent and of power in Australia, will be nothing in England?", "오스트레일리아에서 재능과 권력의 지팡이를 휘두르는 마이코버 씨가 잉글랜드에서는 아무것도 아니리라고 내가 어리석게 상상이나 할 수 있을까요?"], ["I am but a woman; but I should be unworthy of myself and of my papa, if I were guilty of such absurd weakness.’", "나야 한낱 여자에 불과하지만, 그런 터무니없는 나약함에 빠진다면, 나는 나 자신과 우리 아버지께 부끄러운 사람이 되고 말 거예요.\""]], [["Mrs. Micawber’s conviction that her arguments were unanswerable, gave a moral elevation to her tone which I think I had never heard in it before.", "자기 논리가 반박할 수 없는 것이라는 마이코버 부인의 확신은, 그녀의 어조에 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 듯한 도덕적 드높음을 실어 주었다."]], [["‘And therefore it is,’ said Mrs.", "\"그러니 더더욱,\""], ["Micawber, ‘that I the more wish, that, at a future period, we may live again on the parent soil. Mr.", "마이코버 부인이 말했다. \"나는 언젠가 우리가 다시 그 모태의 땅에서 살게 되기를 바라는 거예요."], ["Micawber may be--I cannot disguise from myself that the probability is, Mr. Micawber will be--a page of History; and he ought then to be represented in the country which gave him birth, and did NOT give him employment!’", "마이코버 씨는 어쩌면—나 자신조차 숨길 수 없는 게, 십중팔구 마이코버 씨는—역사의 한 페이지가 될 거예요. 그렇다면 그분은 마땅히, 자기를 낳아 놓고도 일자리는 주지 않았던 그 나라에서 기려져야 마땅하지요!\""]], [["‘My love,’ observed Mr.", "\"여보,\" 마이코버 씨가 말했다."], ["Micawber, ‘it is impossible for me not to be touched by your affection.", "\"당신의 그 애정에 내가 감동하지 않을 도리가 없구려."], ["I am always willing to defer to your good sense.", "나는 언제나 당신의 분별에 기꺼이 따른다오."], ["What will be--will be.", "될 일은—될 것이오."], ["Heaven forbid that I should grudge my native country any portion of the wealth that may be accumulated by our descendants!’", "우리 후손이 쌓아 올릴 부의 한 자락이라도 내 고국에 돌아가는 것을, 내가 어찌 인색하게 아까워하겠소!\""]], [["‘That’s well,’ said my aunt, nodding towards Mr.", "\"그거 좋군요,\" 고모님이 패거티 씨를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["Peggotty, ‘and I drink my love to you all, and every blessing and success attend you!’", "\"그럼 나는 당신들 모두를 위해 내 마음을 담아 마시겠어요. 온갖 복과 성공이 당신들과 함께하기를!\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty put down the two children he had been nursing, one on each knee, to join Mr. and Mrs.", "패거티 씨는 양쪽 무릎에 하나씩 어르고 있던 두 아이를 내려놓고는, 마이코버 부부와 함께 답례로 우리 모두를 위해 잔을 들었다."], ["Micawber in drinking to all of us in return; and when he and the Micawbers cordially shook hands as comrades, and his brown face brightened with a smile, I felt that he would make his way, establish a good name, and be beloved, go where he would.", "그가 마이코버네 사람들과 동지처럼 다정하게 손을 맞잡고, 그 구릿빛 얼굴이 미소로 환하게 빛날 때, 나는 그가 어디로 가든 제 길을 헤쳐 나가고, 좋은 평판을 얻고, 사랑받으리라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Even the children were instructed, each to dip a wooden spoon into Mr. Micawber’s pot, and pledge us in its contents.", "어린아이들조차 저마다 나무 숟가락을 마이코버 씨의 단지에 담갔다가 그 내용물로 우리를 위해 건배하도록 일러 받았다."], ["When this was done, my aunt and Agnes rose, and parted from the emigrants.", "이것이 끝나자, 고모님과 애그니스가 일어서서 이민자들과 작별을 나누었다."], ["It was a sorrowful farewell.", "슬픈 작별이었다. 모두가 울고 있었다."], ["They were all crying; the children hung about Agnes to the last; and we left poor Mrs. Micawber in a very distressed condition, sobbing and weeping by a dim candle, that must have made the room look, from the river, like a miserable light-house.", "아이들은 끝까지 애그니스에게 매달렸다. 그리고 우리는 가엾은 마이코버 부인을 더없이 비통한 상태로 남겨 둔 채 떠났는데, 그녀는 가물거리는 촛불 곁에서 흐느끼며 울고 있었으니, 강에서 바라보면 그 방이 마치 처량한 등대처럼 보였으리라."]], [["I went down again next morning to see that they were away.", "이튿날 아침 나는 그들이 잘 떠나는지 보러 다시 내려갔다."], ["They had departed, in a boat, as early as five o’clock.", "그들은 새벽 다섯 시도 되기 전에 보트를 타고 떠난 뒤였다."], ["It was a wonderful instance to me of the gap such partings make, that although my association of them with the tumble-down public-house and the wooden stairs dated only from last night, both seemed dreary and deserted, now that they were gone.", "그런 이별이 만들어 내는 빈자리가 어떤 것인지, 그것은 나에게 놀라운 본보기였다. 내가 그들을 그 허물어져 가는 선술집이며 나무 층계와 연관 지은 것은 겨우 어젯밤부터였는데도, 그들이 떠나고 나니 둘 다 황량하고 버림받은 것처럼 보였으니 말이다."]], [["In the afternoon of the next day, my old nurse and I went down to Gravesend.", "그 다음 날 오후, 옛 유모와 나는 그레이브젠드로 내려갔다."], ["We found the ship in the river, surrounded by a crowd of boats; a favourable wind blowing; the signal for sailing at her mast-head.", "우리는 그 배가 강 위에, 무수한 보트에 둘러싸여 떠 있는 것을 발견했다. 순풍이 불고 있었고, 돛대 꼭대기에는 출항 신호가 걸려 있었다."], ["I hired a boat directly, and we put off to her; and getting through the little vortex of confusion of which she was the centre, went on board.", "나는 곧장 보트 한 척을 빌려 배를 향해 저어 갔고, 그 배를 중심으로 소용돌이치는 자그마한 혼잡을 헤치고 나아가 갑판에 올랐다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty was waiting for us on deck. He told me that Mr.", "패거티 씨가 갑판에서 우리를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Micawber had just now been arrested again (and for the last time) at the suit of Heep, and that, in compliance with a request I had made to him, he had paid the money, which I repaid him.", "그는 마이코버 씨가 조금 전 힙의 소송으로 또 (그리고 마지막으로) 체포되었으나, 내가 그에게 부탁해 둔 대로 자기가 그 돈을 치렀다고 일러 주었고, 나는 그 돈을 다시 갚아 주었다."], ["He then took us down between decks; and there, any lingering fears I had of his having heard any rumours of what had happened, were dispelled by Mr. Micawber’s coming out of the gloom, taking his arm with an air of friendship and protection, and telling me that they had scarcely been asunder for a moment, since the night before last.", "그러고 나서 그는 우리를 갑판 아래로 데려갔다. 거기서, 그가 혹시 무슨 일이 벌어졌다는 소문을 들은 것은 아닐까 하고 내가 아직 품고 있던 미련 같은 두려움은, 마이코버 씨가 어둠 속에서 나와 다정하고도 감싸 주는 태도로 그의 팔을 붙잡고는, 그저께 밤 이래로 둘이 한순간도 떨어진 적이 거의 없다고 나에게 일러 주는 바람에 말끔히 가셨다."]], [["It was such a strange scene to me, and so confined and dark, that, at first, I could make out hardly anything; but, by degrees, it cleared, as my eyes became more accustomed to the gloom, and I seemed to stand in a picture by OSTADE.", "나에게는 너무도 낯선 광경이요, 어찌나 비좁고 어두운지, 처음에는 거의 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다. 그러나 차츰차츰, 내 눈이 그 어스름에 익숙해지면서 시야가 또렷해졌고, 나는 마치 오스타데의 그림 속에 서 있는 듯했다."], ["Among the great beams, bulks, and ringbolts of the ship, and the emigrant-berths, and chests, and bundles, and barrels, and heaps of miscellaneous baggage--‘lighted up, here and there, by dangling lanterns; and elsewhere by the yellow daylight straying down a windsail or a hatchway--were crowded groups of people, making new friendships, taking leave of one another, talking, laughing, crying, eating and drinking; some, already settled down into the possession of their few feet of space, with their little households arranged, and tiny children established on stools, or in dwarf elbow-chairs; others, despairing of a resting-place, and wandering disconsolately.", "배의 큰 들보며 선체며 고리못 사이로, 이민자들의 침상이며 궤짝이며 보따리며 통이며 잡다한 짐 더미가 — 여기저기 매달린 등불이, 또 다른 곳에서는 통풍통이나 승강구로 새어 드는 누런 햇살이 비추는 가운데 — 빽빽이 들어찬 사람들 무리가, 새로 사귀고, 서로 작별하고, 이야기하고, 웃고, 울고, 먹고 마시고 있었다. 어떤 이들은 벌써 제 몫인 몇 자(尺)의 공간을 차지하고 자리를 잡아, 단출한 살림을 정돈해 놓고 어린아이들을 등받이 없는 의자나 자그마한 안락의자에 앉혀 두었으며, 또 어떤 이들은 쉴 자리를 찾지 못해 낙담한 채 정처 없이 헤매고 있었다."], ["From babies who had but a week or two of life behind them, to crooked old men and women who seemed to have but a week or two of life before them; and from ploughmen bodily carrying out soil of England on their boots, to smiths taking away samples of its soot and smoke upon their skins; every age and occupation appeared to be crammed into the narrow compass of the ‘tween decks.", "살아온 날이 겨우 한두 주밖에 안 되는 갓난아기에서부터, 앞으로 남은 날이 한두 주밖에 안 돼 보이는 등 굽은 노인들까지, 또 잉글랜드의 흙을 제 장화에 그대로 묻혀 나르는 농부에서부터, 그 나라의 검댕과 연기를 제 살갗에 견본으로 묻혀 가는 대장장이까지, 온갖 나이와 직업이 그 비좁은 중갑판의 한 뼘 공간에 빼곡히 들어차 있는 듯했다."]], [["As my eye glanced round this place, I thought I saw sitting, by an open port, with one of the Micawber children near her, a figure like Emily’s; it first attracted my attention, by another figure parting from it with a kiss; and as it glided calmly away through the disorder, reminding me of--Agnes!", "이곳을 죽 둘러보던 내 눈에, 열린 현창 곁에 마이코버네 아이 하나를 가까이 두고 앉아 있는, 에밀리를 닮은 형체 하나가 보이는 듯했다. 처음 내 눈길을 끈 것은, 또 다른 형체가 그 사람에게 입맞춤하며 작별하는 모습이었다."], ["But in the rapid motion and confusion, and in the unsettlement of my own thoughts, I lost it again; and only knew that the time was come when all visitors were being warned to leave the ship; that my nurse was crying on a chest beside me; and that Mrs.", "그 형체가 그 어수선함 사이를 차분히 미끄러져 멀어져 가는 모습이 나에게 — 애그니스를 떠올리게 했다! 그러나 그 빠른 움직임과 혼란 속에서, 또 나 자신의 어수선한 마음속에서, 나는 그것을 다시 놓쳐 버렸다."], ["Gummidge, assisted by some younger stooping woman in black, was busily arranging Mr. Peggotty’s goods.", "그러고는 그저, 방문객은 모두 배에서 내리라는 경고가 떨어진 그때가 되었다는 것, 내 유모가 내 곁 궤짝에 앉아 울고 있다는 것, 그리고 거미지 부인이 검은 옷을 입고 허리를 굽힌 어떤 젊은 여인의 도움을 받아, 패거티 씨의 짐을 부지런히 정돈하고 있다는 것만 알 따름이었다."]], [["‘Is there any last wured, Mas’r Davy?’ said he.", "\"마지막으로 헐 말씀이 있으세유, 데이비 도련님?\""], ["‘Is there any one forgotten thing afore we parts?’", "그가 말했다. \"갈라서기 전에 잊어뿐 게 하나라도 있남유?\""]], [["‘One thing!’ said I. ‘Martha!’", "\"하나 있어요!\" 내가 말했다. \"마사!\""]], [["He touched the younger woman I have mentioned on the shoulder, and Martha stood before me.", "그는 내가 말한 그 젊은 여인의 어깨를 건드렸고, 마사가 내 앞에 섰다."]], [["‘Heaven bless you, you good man!’ cried I.", "\"하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를, 당신은 참 좋은 분이에요!\""], ["‘You take her with you!’", "내가 외쳤다. \"마사를 데려가시는군요!\""]], [["She answered for him, with a burst of tears.", "마사가 왈칵 눈물을 터뜨리며 그를 대신해 대답했다."], ["I could speak no more at that time, but I wrung his hand; and if ever I have loved and honoured any man, I loved and honoured that man in my soul.", "그때 나는 더 말을 잇지 못한 채 그의 손을 꽉 부여잡았다. 내가 일찍이 어떤 사람을 사랑하고 우러른 적이 있다면, 나는 영혼 깊은 곳에서 그 사람을 사랑하고 우러렀다."]], [["The ship was clearing fast of strangers.", "배에서는 낯선 이들이 빠르게 빠져나가고 있었다."], ["The greatest trial that I had, remained.", "나에게 닥칠 가장 혹독한 시련이 아직 남아 있었다."], ["I told him what the noble spirit that was gone, had given me in charge to say at parting.", "나는 그에게, 이제 세상을 떠난 그 고결한 영혼이 작별 인사로 전해 달라며 나에게 맡긴 말을 일러 주었다."], ["It moved him deeply.", "그것은 그를 깊이 뒤흔들었다."], ["But when he charged me, in return, with many messages of affection and regret for those deaf ears, he moved me more.", "그러나 그가 답례로, 이제는 듣지 못하는 그 귀들을 향한 숱한 애정과 회한의 말을 나에게 맡겼을 때, 그는 나를 더욱 뒤흔들어 놓았다."]], [["The time was come. I embraced him, took my weeping nurse upon my arm, and hurried away.", "때가 되었다. 나는 그를 껴안고, 울고 있는 유모를 내 팔에 부축한 채 서둘러 그곳을 떠났다."], ["On deck, I took leave of poor Mrs. Micawber.", "갑판에서 나는 가엾은 마이코버 부인과 작별했다."], ["She was looking distractedly about for her family, even then; and her last words to me were, that she never would desert Mr. Micawber.", "그녀는 그 순간에도 정신없이 제 식구를 두리번두리번 찾고 있었고, 나에게 남긴 마지막 말은 자기는 결코 마이코버 씨를 버리지 않으리라는 것이었다."]], [["We went over the side into our boat, and lay at a little distance, to see the ship wafted on her course.", "우리는 뱃전을 넘어 우리 보트로 내려가, 조금 떨어진 곳에 머무르며 그 배가 항로를 따라 실려 가는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["It was then calm, radiant sunset.", "그때 바다는 잔잔했고, 노을은 눈부셨다."], ["She lay between us, and the red light; and every taper line and spar was visible against the glow.", "배는 우리와 그 붉은 빛 사이에 떠 있었고, 가느다란 밧줄과 활대 하나하나까지 그 타오르는 노을을 배경으로 또렷이 보였다."], ["A sight at once so beautiful, so mournful, and so hopeful, as the glorious ship, lying, still, on the flushed water, with all the life on board her crowded at the bulwarks, and there clustering, for a moment, bare-headed and silent, I never saw.", "그 영광스러운 배가, 발그레 물든 물 위에 고요히 떠서, 배 안의 모든 생명이 뱃전에 빽빽이 몰려나와 한순간 모자를 벗고 말없이 모여 선 그 광경만큼, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 애달프고, 그토록 희망에 찬 광경을, 나는 한 번도 본 적이 없다."]], [["Silent, only for a moment.", "말없는 것도 한순간뿐이었다."], ["As the sails rose to the wind, and the ship began to move, there broke from all the boats three resounding cheers, which those on board took up, and echoed back, and which were echoed and re-echoed.", "돛이 바람을 받아 솟구치고 배가 움직이기 시작하자, 모든 보트에서 세 번의 우렁찬 환호가 터져 나왔고, 배 위의 사람들이 그 환호를 받아 메아리쳐 되돌렸으며, 그것이 다시 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다."], ["My heart burst out when I heard the sound, and beheld the waving of the hats and handkerchiefs--and then I saw her!", "그 소리를 듣고, 모자와 손수건이 물결치듯 흔들리는 것을 바라보았을 때, 내 가슴은 터질 듯했다—그리고 그때 나는 그녀를 보았다!"]], [["Then I saw her, at her uncle’s side, and trembling on his shoulder.", "그때 나는 그녀를, 그 삼촌의 곁에서, 그의 어깨에 기대어 떨고 있는 그녀를 보았다."], ["He pointed to us with an eager hand; and she saw us, and waved her last good-bye to me.", "그가 간절한 손짓으로 우리를 가리켰고, 그녀는 우리를 보고는 나에게 마지막 작별의 손짓을 보냈다."], ["Aye, Emily, beautiful and drooping, cling to him with the utmost trust of thy bruised heart; for he has clung to thee, with all the might of his great love!", "그래, 에밀리야, 아름답고도 시들어 가는 너야, 멍든 네 가슴이 품을 수 있는 더없는 믿음으로 그에게 매달리렴. 그 또한 제 크나큰 사랑의 온 힘을 다해 너에게 매달려 왔으니!"]], [["Surrounded by the rosy light, and standing high upon the deck, apart together, she clinging to him, and he holding her, they solemnly passed away.", "장밋빛 노을에 둘러싸여, 갑판 높은 곳에 둘이 따로 떨어져 서서, 그녀는 그에게 매달리고 그는 그녀를 감싸 안은 채, 두 사람은 엄숙히 멀어져 갔다."], ["The night had fallen on the Kentish hills when we were rowed ashore--and fallen darkly upon me.", "우리가 노 저어 뭍에 닿았을 때, 켄트의 언덕들 위로 밤이 내려앉아 있었다—그리고 내 위로도 어둑하게 내려앉았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 58. ABSENCE", "# CHAPTER 58. ABSENCE"]], [["It was a long and gloomy night that gathered on me, haunted by the ghosts of many hopes, of many dear remembrances, many errors, many unavailing sorrows and regrets.", "내 위로 길고 음울한 밤이 내려앉았다. 수많은 희망과 수많은 그리운 추억, 수많은 잘못과 수많은 부질없는 슬픔과 후회의 유령들이 그 밤을 떠돌았다."]], [["I went away from England; not knowing, even then, how great the shock was, that I had to bear.", "나는 영국을 떠났다. 그때까지도 내가 견뎌야 할 충격이 얼마나 큰지 알지 못한 채."], ["I left all who were dear to me, and went away; and believed that I had borne it, and it was past.", "나는 내게 소중한 모든 이를 두고 떠났다. 그러면서 나는 그 충격을 이미 견뎌 냈고, 그것은 지나간 일이라고 믿었다."], ["As a man upon a field of battle will receive a mortal hurt, and scarcely know that he is struck, so I, when I was left alone with my undisciplined heart, had no conception of the wound with which it had to strive.", "전장에서 치명상을 입고도 자기가 맞았는지조차 거의 알아채지 못하는 병사처럼, 나는 길들지 않은 내 마음과 홀로 남았을 때, 그 마음이 맞서 싸워야 할 상처를 전혀 가늠하지 못했다."]], [["The knowledge came upon me, not quickly, but little by little, and grain by grain.", "그 깨달음은 빠르게가 아니라 조금씩, 한 톨 한 톨 쌓이며 내게 다가왔다."], ["The desolate feeling with which I went abroad, deepened and widened hourly.", "외국으로 떠나며 품었던 황량한 느낌은 시간이 갈수록 깊어지고 넓어졌다."], ["At first it was a heavy sense of loss and sorrow, wherein I could distinguish little else.", "처음에는 그저 묵직한 상실감과 슬픔뿐이어서, 그 안에서 다른 것은 거의 구별할 수가 없었다."], ["By imperceptible degrees, it became a hopeless consciousness of all that I had lost--love, friendship, interest; of all that had been shattered--my first trust, my first affection, the whole airy castle of my life; of all that remained--a ruined blank and waste, lying wide around me, unbroken, to the dark horizon.", "그러다 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 더디게, 그것은 내가 잃어버린 모든 것 — 사랑, 우정, 관심 — 에 대한, 그리고 산산이 부서진 모든 것 — 나의 첫 신뢰, 나의 첫 애정, 내 인생의 그 허공에 떠 있던 성채 전부 — 에 대한, 그리고 남은 모든 것 — 폐허가 된 텅 빈 황무지, 내 둘레로 끊김 없이 어두운 지평선까지 너르게 펼쳐진 그것 — 에 대한 가망 없는 자각으로 변해 갔다."]], [["If my grief were selfish, I did not know it to be so.", "내 슬픔이 이기적인 것이었다 해도, 나는 그런 줄을 몰랐다."], ["I mourned for my child-wife, taken from her blooming world, so young.", "나는 그 활짝 핀 세상에서 너무도 어린 나이에 떠나간 내 어린 아내를 애도했다."], ["I mourned for him who might have won the love and admiration of thousands, as he had won mine long ago.", "오래전 내 사랑과 흠모를 얻었듯, 수천 명의 사랑과 흠모를 얻을 수도 있었을 그를 애도했다."], ["I mourned for the broken heart that had found rest in the stormy sea; and for the wandering remnants of the simple home, where I had heard the night-wind blowing, when I was a child.", "사나운 바다에서 안식을 찾은 그 부서진 마음을 애도했다. 그리고 내가 어렸을 적 밤바람이 부는 소리를 들었던 그 소박한 집의, 이제는 흩어져 떠도는 잔해들을 애도했다."]], [["From the accumulated sadness into which I fell, I had at length no hope of ever issuing again.", "내가 빠져든 그 차곡차곡 쌓인 슬픔에서, 마침내 나는 다시는 헤어날 가망이 없었다."], ["I roamed from place to place, carrying my burden with me everywhere.", "나는 이곳저곳을 떠돌았고, 어디를 가든 내 짐을 짊어지고 다녔다."], ["I felt its whole weight now; and I drooped beneath it, and I said in my heart that it could never be lightened.", "이제 나는 그 무게를 고스란히 느꼈다. 그 아래 짓눌려 고개를 떨군 채, 이 짐은 결코 가벼워질 수 없으리라고 마음속으로 되뇌었다."]], [["When this despondency was at its worst, I believed that I should die.", "이 의기소침이 가장 심했을 때, 나는 내가 죽을 것이라고 믿었다."], ["Sometimes, I thought that I would like to die at home; and actually turned back on my road, that I might get there soon.", "어떤 때는 고향에서 죽고 싶다는 생각이 들어, 빨리 그곳에 닿으려고 실제로 가던 길을 되돌리기도 했다."], ["At other times, I passed on farther away,--from city to city, seeking I know not what, and trying to leave I know not what behind.", "또 어떤 때는 더 멀리 나아가, 무엇을 찾는지도 모른 채 도시에서 도시로 옮겨 다니며, 무엇을 뒤에 두고 떠나려는지도 모른 채 떠나려 애썼다."]], [["It is not in my power to retrace, one by one, all the weary phases of distress of mind through which I passed.", "내가 지나온 마음의 그 모든 고단한 고통의 국면을 하나하나 되짚는 것은 내 힘으로 할 수 없는 일이다."], ["There are some dreams that can only be imperfectly and vaguely described; and when I oblige myself to look back on this time of my life, I seem to be recalling such a dream.", "어떤 꿈은 불완전하고 어렴풋하게밖에 그려 낼 수 없는 법인데, 내 인생의 이 시기를 억지로라도 돌아보려 하면, 나는 꼭 그런 꿈을 떠올리는 것만 같다."], ["I see myself passing on among the novelties of foreign towns, palaces, cathedrals, temples, pictures, castles, tombs, fantastic streets--the old abiding places of History and Fancy--as a dreamer might; bearing my painful load through all, and hardly conscious of the objects as they fade before me.", "나는 이국의 낯선 도시들, 궁전과 대성당과 신전, 그림과 성채와 무덤, 환상적인 거리들 — 역사와 환상이 오래 깃들어 온 그 자리들 — 사이를 지나가는 내 모습을 마치 꿈꾸는 사람처럼 본다. 그 모든 것 사이로 내 고통스러운 짐을 짊어지고, 눈앞에서 스러져 가는 사물들을 거의 의식하지도 못한 채 지나가는 모습을."], ["Listlessness to everything, but brooding sorrow, was the night that fell on my undisciplined heart.", "깊은 슬픔에 잠기는 것 말고는 모든 것에 무심한 상태, 그것이 길들지 않은 내 마음 위에 내려앉은 밤이었다."], ["Let me look up from it--as at last I did, thank Heaven!--and from its long, sad, wretched dream, to dawn.", "거기서 눈을 들어 — 마침내 그렇게 했으니, 하늘에 감사하리라! — 그 길고 슬프고 비참한 꿈에서 새벽으로 눈을 들게 해 다오."]], [["For many months I travelled with this ever-darkening cloud upon my mind.", "여러 달 동안 나는 점점 더 어두워지는 그 구름을 마음에 인 채 여행했다."], ["Some blind reasons that I had for not returning home--reasons then struggling within me, vainly, for more distinct expression--kept me on my pilgrimage.", "고향으로 돌아가지 않으려는 어떤 어렴풋한 이유들이 — 그때 내 안에서 좀 더 또렷한 표현을 얻으려고 헛되이 몸부림치던 그 이유들이 — 나를 순례의 길에 묶어 두었다."], ["Sometimes, I had proceeded restlessly from place to place, stopping nowhere; sometimes, I had lingered long in one spot.", "어떤 때는 어디에도 멈추지 못하고 안절부절 이곳저곳을 옮겨 다녔고, 어떤 때는 한 자리에 오래 머물기도 했다."], ["I had had no purpose, no sustaining soul within me, anywhere.", "나에게는 어디서도 아무런 목적도, 나를 떠받쳐 줄 영혼도 없었다."]], [["I was in Switzerland.", "나는 스위스에 있었다."], ["I had come out of Italy, over one of the great passes of the Alps, and had since wandered with a guide among the by-ways of the mountains. If those awful solitudes had spoken to my heart, I did not know it.", "이탈리아에서 나와, 알프스의 큰 고개 하나를 넘어온 뒤로, 안내인 하나를 데리고 산속 샛길을 떠돌고 있었다. 그 두려운 적막이 내 마음에 무언가 말을 건넸다 한들, 나는 그것을 알아채지 못했다."], ["I had found sublimity and wonder in the dread heights and precipices, in the roaring torrents, and the wastes of ice and snow; but as yet, they had taught me nothing else.", "나는 무시무시한 높이와 벼랑에서, 으르렁대는 급류에서, 얼음과 눈의 황무지에서 숭고함과 경이를 보았다. 하지만 그때까지 그것들은 내게 다른 것은 아무것도 가르쳐 주지 못했다."]], [["I came, one evening before sunset, down into a valley, where I was to rest.", "어느 날 저녁, 해 지기 전에 나는 쉬어 갈 어느 골짜기로 내려갔다."], ["In the course of my descent to it, by the winding track along the mountain-side, from which I saw it shining far below, I think some long-unwonted sense of beauty and tranquillity, some softening influence awakened by its peace, moved faintly in my breast.", "산허리를 따라 굽이굽이 난 길을 타고 그곳으로 내려가는 동안, 저 멀리 아래에서 골짜기가 반짝이는 것을 보았는데, 오랫동안 느껴 보지 못했던 어떤 아름다움과 고요의 감각이, 그 평화가 일깨운 어떤 누그러뜨리는 기운이, 내 가슴속에서 희미하게 움직였던 것 같다."], ["I remember pausing once, with a kind of sorrow that was not all oppressive, not quite despairing.", "나는 한 번 걸음을 멈췄던 것을 기억한다. 짓누르기만 하지도, 아주 절망스럽기만 하지도 않은 어떤 슬픔과 함께."], ["I remember almost hoping that some better change was possible within me.", "내 안에서 무언가 더 나은 변화가 가능할지도 모른다고 거의 바라기까지 했던 것을 기억한다."]], [["I came into the valley, as the evening sun was shining on the remote heights of snow, that closed it in, like eternal clouds.", "저녁 해가 골짜기를 영원한 구름처럼 에워싼 저 먼 눈 덮인 봉우리를 비추고 있을 때, 나는 그 골짜기로 들어섰다."], ["The bases of the mountains forming the gorge in which the little village lay, were richly green; and high above this gentler vegetation, grew forests of dark fir, cleaving the wintry snow-drift, wedge-like, and stemming the avalanche.", "작은 마을이 자리한 협곡을 이루는 산자락은 풍성한 초록으로 덮여 있었고, 그 부드러운 초목 위로 높이는 검은 전나무 숲이 자라, 겨울 눈더미를 쐐기처럼 가르며 눈사태를 막아서고 있었다."], ["Above these, were range upon range of craggy steeps, grey rock, bright ice, and smooth verdure-specks of pasture, all gradually blending with the crowning snow.", "그 위로는 험준한 비탈이 줄줄이 이어졌으니, 잿빛 바위와 빛나는 얼음, 그리고 매끄러운 초록빛 점점이 박힌 목초지가, 모두 그 꼭대기를 덮은 눈과 차츰 어우러졌다."], ["Dotted here and there on the mountain’s-side, each tiny dot a home, were lonely wooden cottages, so dwarfed by the towering heights that they appeared too small for toys.", "산허리 여기저기 점점이, 그 작은 점 하나하나가 하나의 집인데, 외딴 통나무 오두막들이 박혀 있었다. 우뚝 솟은 높이에 짓눌려 어찌나 작아 보이던지, 장난감이라기에도 너무 작아 보였다."], ["So did even the clustered village in the valley, with its wooden bridge across the stream, where the stream tumbled over broken rocks, and roared away among the trees.", "골짜기에 옹기종기 모인 마을도 마찬가지였으니, 그 마을에는 시냇물을 가로지르는 나무다리가 놓여 있었고, 시냇물은 부서진 바위 위로 굴러떨어지며 나무들 사이로 으르렁대며 흘러갔다."], ["In the quiet air, there was a sound of distant singing--shepherd voices; but, as one bright evening cloud floated midway along the mountain’s-side, I could almost have believed it came from there, and was not earthly music.", "고요한 공기 속에 멀리서 노랫소리가 들려왔다 — 양치기들의 목소리였다. 그러나 밝은 저녁 구름 한 조각이 산허리 중턱을 떠가고 있어서, 나는 그 노래가 저기서 들려오는 것이며 이 세상의 음악이 아니라고 거의 믿을 뻔했다."], ["All at once, in this serenity, great Nature spoke to me; and soothed me to lay down my weary head upon the grass, and weep as I had not wept yet, since Dora died!", "별안간, 이 고요 속에서, 위대한 자연이 내게 말을 건넸다. 그리고 내 지친 머리를 풀밭에 누이고, 도라가 죽은 뒤로 아직 한 번도 울어 보지 못한 만큼 울도록 나를 달래 주었다!"]], [["I had found a packet of letters awaiting me but a few minutes before, and had strolled out of the village to read them while my supper was making ready.", "나는 불과 몇 분 전에 나를 기다리고 있던 편지 꾸러미 하나를 받은 참이었고, 저녁이 차려지는 동안 그것을 읽으려고 마을 밖으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나온 것이었다."], ["Other packets had missed me, and I had received none for a long time.", "다른 꾸러미들은 나를 비껴갔고, 나는 오랫동안 한 통도 받지 못했었다."], ["Beyond a line or two, to say that I was well, and had arrived at such a place, I had not had fortitude or constancy to write a letter since I left home.", "나는 잘 있다, 어디어디에 도착했다 하는 한두 줄을 넘기고는, 집을 떠난 뒤로 편지 한 통 쓸 만한 꿋꿋함도 한결같음도 없었다."]], [["The packet was in my hand.", "그 꾸러미가 내 손에 있었다."], ["I opened it, and read the writing of Agnes.", "나는 그것을 열어, 애그니스의 글씨를 읽었다."]], [["She was happy and useful, was prospering as she had hoped.", "그녀는 행복하고 쓸모 있는 사람으로, 바라던 대로 잘 지내고 있었다."], ["That was all she told me of herself.", "자기 자신에 대해 내게 한 말은 그것이 전부였다."], ["The rest referred to me.", "나머지는 모두 나에 관한 것이었다."]], [["She gave me no advice; she urged no duty on me; she only told me, in her own fervent manner, what her trust in me was.", "그녀는 내게 아무 충고도 하지 않았다. 어떤 의무도 강요하지 않았다. 다만 그녀 특유의 그 뜨거운 태도로, 나를 향한 자기 믿음이 어떤 것인지를 들려주었을 뿐이다."], ["She knew (she said) how such a nature as mine would turn affliction to good.", "그녀는 (이렇게 말했다) 나 같은 천성이 어떻게 고난을 좋은 것으로 바꿔 놓을지 안다고 했다."], ["She knew how trial and emotion would exalt and strengthen it.", "시련과 격정이 어떻게 그 천성을 드높이고 굳세게 할지 안다고 했다."], ["She was sure that in my every purpose I should gain a firmer and a higher tendency, through the grief I had undergone.", "내가 겪은 슬픔을 통해, 내 모든 결심이 더 단단하고 더 높은 방향을 얻게 될 것이라고 그녀는 확신했다."], ["She, who so gloried in my fame, and so looked forward to its augmentation, well knew that I would labour on.", "내 명성을 그토록 자랑스러워하고 그것이 더 커지기를 그토록 고대하던 그녀는, 내가 계속 정진하리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["She knew that in me, sorrow could not be weakness, but must be strength.", "내 안에서는 슬픔이 나약함이 될 수 없으며 도리어 힘이 될 수밖에 없음을 그녀는 알았다."], ["As the endurance of my childish days had done its part to make me what I was, so greater calamities would nerve me on, to be yet better than I was; and so, as they had taught me, would I teach others.", "어린 시절의 인내가 지금의 나를 만드는 데 제 몫을 했듯, 더 큰 재앙들은 나를 다그쳐 지금의 나보다 한층 더 나은 사람으로 만들 것이며, 그리하여 그 재앙들이 나를 가르쳤듯, 나도 남을 가르치게 되리라고 했다."], ["She commended me to God, who had taken my innocent darling to His rest; and in her sisterly affection cherished me always, and was always at my side go where I would; proud of what I had done, but infinitely prouder yet of what I was reserved to do.", "그녀는 나의 순결한 사랑을 데려가 안식에 들게 하신 하느님께 나를 의탁했다. 그리고 그 누이 같은 정으로 언제나 나를 아꼈고, 내가 어디로 가든 늘 내 곁에 있었으며, 내가 이미 이룬 것을 자랑스러워하되, 내가 앞으로 이루도록 남겨진 것을 한없이 더 자랑스러워했다."]], [["I put the letter in my breast, and thought what had I been an hour ago!", "나는 그 편지를 가슴에 품고, 한 시간 전의 나는 어떠했던가 생각했다!"], ["When I heard the voices die away, and saw the quiet evening cloud grow dim, and all the colours in the valley fade, and the golden snow upon the mountain-tops become a remote part of the pale night sky, yet felt that the night was passing from my mind, and all its shadows clearing, there was no name for the love I bore her, dearer to me, henceforward, than ever until then.", "노랫소리가 잦아드는 것을 듣고, 고요한 저녁 구름이 흐려지는 것을, 골짜기의 온갖 빛깔이 스러지는 것을, 산봉우리의 황금빛 눈이 창백한 밤하늘의 아득한 한 부분이 되어 가는 것을 보면서도, 밤이 내 마음에서 물러가고 그 모든 그림자가 걷히는 것을 느꼈을 때, 그때 내가 그녀에게 품은 사랑은 — 그 순간부터 그 어느 때보다 내게 소중해진 그 사랑은 — 이름 붙일 길이 없었다."]], [["I read her letter many times.", "나는 그녀의 편지를 여러 번 읽었다."], ["I wrote to her before I slept.", "잠들기 전에 그녀에게 편지를 썼다."], ["I told her that I had been in sore need of her help; that without her I was not, and I never had been, what she thought me; but that she inspired me to be that, and I would try.", "나는 그녀의 도움이 절실히 필요했노라고, 그녀가 없으면 나는 그녀가 여기는 그런 사람이 아니며 한 번도 그런 적이 없었노라고, 하지만 그녀가 내게 그런 사람이 되라고 일깨워 주니, 나도 애써 보겠노라고 적었다."]], [["I did try. In three months more, a year would have passed since the beginning of my sorrow.", "나는 정말로 애썼다. 석 달이 더 지나면, 내 슬픔이 시작된 지 일 년이 되는 셈이었다."], ["I determined to make no resolutions until the expiration of those three months, but to try.", "나는 그 석 달이 다 지나기 전까지는 아무 결심도 세우지 않되, 그저 애쓰기로 마음먹었다."], ["I lived in that valley, and its neighbourhood, all the time.", "그동안 내내 나는 그 골짜기와 그 언저리에서 지냈다."]], [["The three months gone, I resolved to remain away from home for some time longer; to settle myself for the present in Switzerland, which was growing dear to me in the remembrance of that evening; to resume my pen; to work.", "그 석 달이 지나자, 나는 얼마간 더 고향을 떠나 있기로 결심했다. 그 저녁의 기억 속에서 점점 더 소중해지고 있던 스위스에 당분간 자리를 잡기로, 다시 펜을 들기로, 일하기로 결심했다."]], [["I resorted humbly whither Agnes had commended me; I sought out Nature, never sought in vain; and I admitted to my breast the human interest I had lately shrunk from.", "나는 애그니스가 일러 준 그곳으로 겸허히 발길을 돌렸다. 나는 자연을 찾았고, 결코 헛되이 찾지 않았다. 그리고 얼마 전까지 움츠러들며 피했던 사람에 대한 관심을 다시 내 가슴에 받아들였다."], ["It was not long, before I had almost as many friends in the valley as in Yarmouth: and when I left it, before the winter set in, for Geneva, and came back in the spring, their cordial greetings had a homely sound to me, although they were not conveyed in English words.", "오래지 않아 나는 야머스에서만큼이나 많은 벗을 그 골짜기에 두게 되었다. 겨울이 닥치기 전에 그곳을 떠나 제네바로 갔다가 봄에 다시 돌아왔을 때, 비록 영어로 전해진 것은 아니었으나 그들의 따뜻한 인사는 내게 정겨운 고향의 소리로 들렸다."]], [["I worked early and late, patiently and hard.", "나는 이른 아침부터 밤늦게까지, 참을성 있게, 부지런히 일했다."], ["I wrote a Story, with a purpose growing, not remotely, out of my experience, and sent it to Traddles, and he arranged for its publication very advantageously for me; and the tidings of my growing reputation began to reach me from travellers whom I encountered by chance.", "나는 내 경험에서 — 멀지 않은 곳에서 — 자라난 어떤 목적을 담아 이야기 하나를 썼고, 그것을 트래들에게 보냈다. 그러자 그가 나에게 아주 유리한 조건으로 그 책의 출간을 주선해 주었다. 그리고 우연히 마주친 여행객들에게서, 점점 커져 가는 내 명성의 소식이 들려오기 시작했다."], ["After some rest and change, I fell to work, in my old ardent way, on a new fancy, which took strong possession of me.", "얼마간 쉬며 기분을 바꾼 뒤, 나는 다시 옛날의 그 열정적인 방식으로, 나를 강하게 사로잡은 새로운 구상에 매달려 일했다."], ["As I advanced in the execution of this task, I felt it more and more, and roused my utmost energies to do it well.", "이 작업을 밀고 나아갈수록 나는 그것을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈고, 잘 해내려고 온 힘을 끌어모았다."], ["This was my third work of fiction.", "이것이 나의 세 번째 소설이었다."], ["It was not half written, when, in an interval of rest, I thought of returning home.", "절반도 채 쓰지 못했을 때, 쉬는 틈에 나는 고향으로 돌아가는 일을 생각하게 되었다."]], [["For a long time, though studying and working patiently, I had accustomed myself to robust exercise.", "오랫동안 나는 참을성 있게 공부하고 일하면서도, 한편으로 몸을 튼튼히 하는 운동을 몸에 익혀 왔다."], ["My health, severely impaired when I left England, was quite restored.", "영국을 떠날 무렵 심하게 상했던 건강은 완전히 회복되었다."], ["I had seen much. I had been in many countries, and I hope I had improved my store of knowledge.", "나는 많은 것을 보았다. 여러 나라를 다녔고, 내 지식의 곳간도 한결 늘렸기를 바란다."]], [["I have now recalled all that I think it needful to recall here, of this term of absence--with one reservation.", "이제 나는 이 떠나 있던 기간에 관해, 여기서 떠올릴 필요가 있다고 여기는 것을 모두 떠올렸다 — 한 가지만 빼고."], ["I have made it, thus far, with no purpose of suppressing any of my thoughts; for, as I have elsewhere said, this narrative is my written memory.", "여기까지 나는 내 생각을 어느 하나도 숨길 뜻 없이 적어 왔다. 다른 데서도 말했듯, 이 이야기는 글로 적은 내 기억이니까."], ["I have desired to keep the most secret current of my mind apart, and to the last.", "나는 내 마음의 가장 은밀한 물줄기만큼은 따로 떼어 두었다가, 맨 마지막에 풀어내고 싶었다."], ["I enter on it now.", "이제 그것을 적는다."], ["I cannot so completely penetrate the mystery of my own heart, as to know when I began to think that I might have set its earliest and brightest hopes on Agnes.", "나는 내 마음의 신비를 완전히 꿰뚫어 볼 수가 없어서, 내가 언제부터 가장 이르고 가장 빛나던 내 희망을 애그니스에게 걸었을지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작했는지를 알지 못한다."], ["I cannot say at what stage of my grief it first became associated with the reflection, that, in my wayward boyhood, I had thrown away the treasure of her love.", "내 슬픔의 어느 단계에서, 제멋대로였던 소년 시절에 내가 그녀의 사랑이라는 보물을 내던져 버렸다는 그 생각과 처음 맞물렸는지도 말할 수 없다."], ["I believe I may have heard some whisper of that distant thought, in the old unhappy loss or want of something never to be realized, of which I had been sensible.", "어쩌면 나는 그 아득한 생각의 어떤 속삭임을, 일찍이 내가 어렴풋이 느껴 왔던 그 오래된 불행한 상실 속에서, 끝내 이룰 수 없는 무언가에 대한 결핍 속에서 이미 들었던 것인지도 모른다."], ["But the thought came into my mind as a new reproach and new regret, when I was left so sad and lonely in the world.", "하지만 그 생각이 새로운 자책과 새로운 후회가 되어 내 마음으로 밀려든 것은, 내가 이 세상에 그토록 슬프고 외롭게 남겨졌을 때였다."]], [["If, at that time, I had been much with her, I should, in the weakness of my desolation, have betrayed this.", "만약 그 무렵 내가 그녀와 자주 함께 있었더라면, 나는 내 황량함이 가져온 나약함에 못 이겨 이 마음을 들키고 말았을 것이다."], ["It was what I remotely dreaded when I was first impelled to stay away from England.", "처음 영국을 떠나 있어야겠다는 충동에 사로잡혔을 때, 내가 어렴풋이 두려워한 것이 바로 그것이었다."], ["I could not have borne to lose the smallest portion of her sisterly affection; yet, in that betrayal, I should have set a constraint between us hitherto unknown.", "나는 그녀의 누이 같은 정을 손톱만큼이라도 잃는 것을 견딜 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 마음을 들켰다면, 나는 우리 사이에 여태껏 없던 어떤 거북함을 심어 놓고 말았을 것이다."]], [["I could not forget that the feeling with which she now regarded me had grown up in my own free choice and course.", "나는 잊을 수가 없었다. 지금 그녀가 나를 바라보는 그 감정이, 다름 아닌 나 스스로의 자유로운 선택과 행로 속에서 자라난 것임을."], ["That if she had ever loved me with another love--and I sometimes thought the time was when she might have done so--I had cast it away.", "만약 그녀가 한때 나를 다른 종류의 사랑으로 사랑한 적이 있었다 해도 — 그리고 나는 때때로 그녀가 그랬을 법한 시절이 있었으리라 생각했다 — 그것을 내던져 버린 것은 나였다는 것을."], ["It was nothing, now, that I had accustomed myself to think of her, when we were both mere children, as one who was far removed from my wild fancies.", "우리 둘이 그저 어린아이였을 적에, 그녀를 내 거친 공상과는 한참 동떨어진 사람으로 여기는 데 익숙해졌었다는 것은, 이제 와 아무것도 아니었다."], ["I had bestowed my passionate tenderness upon another object; and what I might have done, I had not done; and what Agnes was to me, I and her own noble heart had made her.", "나는 내 뜨거운 애정을 다른 대상에게 쏟아부었다. 그리고 내가 할 수도 있었을 일을, 나는 하지 않았다. 애그니스가 내게 어떤 사람인가는, 나와 그녀 자신의 고결한 마음이 함께 만들어 낸 것이었다."]], [["In the beginning of the change that gradually worked in me, when I tried to get a better understanding of myself and be a better man, I did glance, through some indefinite probation, to a period when I might possibly hope to cancel the mistaken past, and to be so blessed as to marry her.", "내 안에서 차츰차츰 일어나던 그 변화의 처음 무렵, 나 자신을 좀 더 잘 이해하고 좀 더 나은 사람이 되려고 애쓰던 때에, 나는 어떤 막연한 시험의 시기를 거쳐, 잘못으로 얼룩진 과거를 지워 버리고 그녀와 결혼하는 복을 누릴 수 있을지도 모를 어느 날을 흘끗 그려 보기는 했다."], ["But, as time wore on, this shadowy prospect faded, and departed from me.", "그러나 세월이 흐를수록 이 어렴풋한 전망은 빛이 바래더니, 내게서 떠나갔다."], ["If she had ever loved me, then, I should hold her the more sacred; remembering the confidences I had reposed in her, her knowledge of my errant heart, the sacrifice she must have made to be my friend and sister, and the victory she had won.", "만약 그녀가 그때 나를 사랑한 적이 있었다면, 나는 그녀를 더욱더 신성하게 여겨야 마땅했다. 내가 그녀에게 털어놓았던 속내들을, 내 방황하는 마음을 그녀가 알고 있었다는 것을, 내 벗이자 누이가 되기 위해 그녀가 치렀을 희생을, 그리고 그녀가 거둔 그 승리를 떠올리면서."], ["If she had never loved me, could I believe that she would love me now?", "그녀가 나를 한 번도 사랑한 적이 없었다면, 그녀가 이제 와 나를 사랑하리라고 어찌 믿을 수 있겠는가?"]], [["I had always felt my weakness, in comparison with her constancy and fortitude; and now I felt it more and more.", "나는 그녀의 한결같음과 꿋꿋함에 견주어 늘 내 나약함을 느껴 왔는데, 이제는 그것을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈다."], ["Whatever I might have been to her, or she to me, if I had been more worthy of her long ago, I was not now, and she was not.", "오래전 내가 그녀에게 더 어울리는 사람이었더라면 내가 그녀에게, 또 그녀가 내게 어떤 사이가 되었을지 모르나, 지금의 나는 그렇지 못했고, 그녀도 그렇지 않았다."], ["The time was past.", "그 시절은 지나가 버렸다."], ["I had let it go by, and had deservedly lost her.", "나는 그것을 흘려보냈고, 마땅한 대가로 그녀를 잃은 것이다."]], [["That I suffered much in these contentions, that they filled me with unhappiness and remorse, and yet that I had a sustaining sense that it was required of me, in right and honour, to keep away from myself, with shame, the thought of turning to the dear girl in the withering of my hopes, from whom I had frivolously turned when they were bright and fresh--which consideration was at the root of every thought I had concerning her--is all equally true.", "이런 갈등 속에서 내가 무척이나 괴로웠다는 것, 그것들이 나를 불행과 회한으로 가득 채웠다는 것, 그러면서도 내게는 나를 떠받쳐 주는 어떤 감각이 있었다는 것 — 곧 희망이 밝고 싱그러웠을 때 내가 경솔히 등 돌렸던 그 사랑스러운 여인에게, 그 희망이 시들어 버린 지금 다시 손을 내밀려는 생각만큼은 부끄러움과 함께 나 자신에게서 멀리 떼어 놓는 것이 옳고 명예로운 도리로서 내게 요구된다는 그 감각 말이다. 그리고 이 헤아림이야말로 그녀에 관한 내 모든 생각의 뿌리에 자리하고 있었다는 것 — 이 모두가 한결같이 사실이다."], ["I made no effort to conceal from myself, now, that I loved her, that I was devoted to her; but I brought the assurance home to myself, that it was now too late, and that our long-subsisting relation must be undisturbed.", "나는 이제 내가 그녀를 사랑한다는 것을, 그녀에게 온 마음을 바치고 있다는 것을 나 자신에게 감추려 들지 않았다. 다만 이제는 너무 늦었으며, 오래도록 이어져 온 우리 사이는 흔들림 없이 그대로 두어야 한다는 확신을 내 가슴에 단단히 새겼다."]], [["I had thought, much and often, of my Dora’s shadowing out to me what might have happened, in those years that were destined not to try us; I had considered how the things that never happen, are often as much realities to us, in their effects, as those that are accomplished.", "나는 내 도라가, 우리를 시험하지 않도록 운명 지어진 그 세월 속에 무슨 일이 일어났을 수 있었을지를 내게 어렴풋이 그려 보였던 것을, 자주 그리고 깊이 곱씹었다. 결코 일어나지 않는 일도, 그 미치는 영향에서는 이루어진 일 못지않게 우리에게 생생한 현실인 경우가 많다는 것을 나는 헤아려 보았다."], ["The very years she spoke of, were realities now, for my correction; and would have been, one day, a little later perhaps, though we had parted in our earliest folly.", "그녀가 말한 바로 그 세월이, 이제 나를 바로잡아 주는 현실이 되어 있었다. 그리고 우리가 가장 어리석던 시절에 갈라섰다 하더라도, 언젠가는, 어쩌면 조금 더 늦게나마, 역시 현실이 되었을 것이다."], ["I endeavoured to convert what might have been between myself and Agnes, into a means of making me more self-denying, more resolved, more conscious of myself, and my defects and errors.", "나는 나와 애그니스 사이에 있었을 수도 있었던 것을, 나를 더 자제할 줄 알고, 더 굳건하며, 나 자신과 내 결점과 잘못을 더 또렷이 의식하는 사람으로 만드는 수단으로 바꾸어 보려 애썼다."], ["Thus, through the reflection that it might have been, I arrived at the conviction that it could never be.", "그리하여 그랬을 수도 있었으리라는 그 헤아림을 거쳐, 나는 그것이 결코 이루어질 수 없다는 확신에 다다랐다."]], [["These, with their perplexities and inconsistencies, were the shifting quicksands of my mind, from the time of my departure to the time of my return home, three years afterwards.", "이런 것들이, 그 온갖 혼란과 모순과 더불어, 내가 떠나던 때로부터 삼 년 뒤 고향으로 돌아오던 때까지, 내 마음속에서 끊임없이 자리를 옮기는 모래수렁이었다."], ["Three years had elapsed since the sailing of the emigrant ship; when, at that same hour of sunset, and in the same place, I stood on the deck of the packet vessel that brought me home, looking on the rosy water where I had seen the image of that ship reflected.", "그 이민선이 떠난 지 삼 년이 흘렀다. 바로 그 해 질 무렵, 바로 그 자리에서, 나는 나를 고향으로 실어다 주는 우편선 갑판에 서서, 일찍이 그 배의 그림자가 비치는 것을 보았던 그 장밋빛 물결을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["Three years. Long in the aggregate, though short as they went by.", "삼 년. 다 모아 놓고 보면 길었으되, 지나가는 동안에는 짧기만 했다."], ["And home was very dear to me, and Agnes too--but she was not mine--she was never to be mine.", "그리고 고향은 내게 무척이나 소중했고, 애그니스 또한 그러했다 — 하지만 그녀는 내 사람이 아니었다 — 그녀는 결코 내 사람이 될 수 없었다."], ["She might have been, but that was past!", "그럴 수도 있었으련만, 그것은 이미 지나간 일이었다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 59. RETURN", "# CHAPTER 59. RETURN"]], [["I landed in London on a wintry autumn evening.", "내가 런던에 발을 디딘 것은 어느 겨울 같은 가을 저녁이었다."], ["It was dark and raining, and I saw more fog and mud in a minute than I had seen in a year.", "사방은 어두웠고 비가 내렸는데, 나는 일 분 만에 일 년 동안 본 것보다 더 많은 안개와 진창을 보았다."], ["I walked from the Custom House to the Monument before I found a coach; and although the very house-fronts, looking on the swollen gutters, were like old friends to me, I could not but admit that they were very dingy friends.", "세관에서 기념비까지 걸어가고 나서야 마차 한 대를 잡았다. 불어난 도랑을 굽어보는 그 집들의 정면조차 나에게는 오랜 벗처럼 다가왔지만, 그것들이 무척이나 꾀죄죄한 벗이라는 사실만큼은 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["I have often remarked--I suppose everybody has--that one’s going away from a familiar place, would seem to be the signal for change in it.", "나는 자주 느껴 왔다 — 다들 그러리라 싶은데 — 익숙한 곳을 떠난다는 것이 그곳에 변화가 닥치라는 신호이기라도 한 것 같다고."], ["As I looked out of the coach window, and observed that an old house on Fish-street Hill, which had stood untouched by painter, carpenter, or bricklayer, for a century, had been pulled down in my absence; and that a neighbouring street, of time-honoured insalubrity and inconvenience, was being drained and widened; I half expected to find St. Paul’s Cathedral looking older.", "마차 창밖을 내다보다가, 백 년 동안 페인트공이나 목수나 벽돌공의 손길 한 번 닿지 않고 버텨 온 피시스트리트힐의 어느 낡은 집이 내가 없는 사이 헐려 버린 것을 알아챘을 때, 그리고 예부터 불결하고 불편하기로 유명하던 이웃 거리에 지금 배수로가 놓이고 길이 넓혀지고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 세인트폴 대성당마저도 더 늙어 보이지 않을까 반쯤 기대하게 되었다."]], [["For some changes in the fortunes of my friends, I was prepared. My aunt had long been re-established at Dover, and Traddles had begun to get into some little practice at the Bar, in the very first term after my departure.", "내 친구들의 처지에 일어난 몇 가지 변화에 대해서는 마음의 준비가 되어 있었다. 고모님은 오래전에 도버에 다시 자리를 잡으셨고, 트래들스는 내가 떠난 바로 그다음 학기부터 변호사로서 자그마한 사건들을 맡기 시작했다."], ["He had chambers in Gray’s Inn, now; and had told me, in his last letters, that he was not without hopes of being soon united to the dearest girl in the world.", "그는 이제 그레이스인에 사무실을 두고 있었고, 마지막 편지에서 머지않아 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨와 맺어질 희망이 없지 않다고 나에게 알려 왔다."]], [["They expected me home before Christmas; but had no idea of my returning so soon. I had purposely misled them, that I might have the pleasure of taking them by surprise.", "그들은 내가 크리스마스 전에는 집에 돌아오리라 기대하고 있었지만, 이렇게 빨리 돌아올 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다. 그들을 깜짝 놀라게 하는 기쁨을 누리려고 일부러 그들을 속였던 것이다."], ["And yet, I was perverse enough to feel a chill and disappointment in receiving no welcome, and rattling, alone and silent, through the misty streets.", "그런데도 나는 어찌나 심사가 뒤틀렸던지, 아무런 환영도 받지 못한 채 안개 낀 거리를 홀로 말없이 덜컹덜컹 지나가는 것이 못내 서운하고 쓸쓸하게 느껴졌다."]], [["The well-known shops, however, with their cheerful lights, did something for me; and when I alighted at the door of the Gray’s Inn Coffee-house, I had recovered my spirits.", "그래도 명랑한 불빛을 단 낯익은 가게들이 나를 어느 정도 위로해 주었고, 그레이스인 커피하우스 문 앞에서 내려섰을 무렵에는 기운을 되찾고 있었다."], ["It recalled, at first, that so-different time when I had put up at the Golden Cross, and reminded me of the changes that had come to pass since then; but that was natural.", "그곳은 처음에 내가 골든크로스에 묵었던 그토록 다른 시절을 떠올리게 했고, 그 뒤로 일어난 변화들을 일깨워 주었다. 하지만 그것은 자연스러운 일이었다."]], [["‘Do you know where Mr. Traddles lives in the Inn?’ I asked the waiter, as I warmed myself by the coffee-room fire.", "\"인 안에서 트래들스 씨가 어디 사시는지 아나?\" 나는 커피룸 난롯가에서 몸을 녹이며 종업원에게 물었다."]], [["‘Holborn Court, sir.", "\"홀번코트입니다, 손님."], ["Number two.’", "이 호예요.\""]], [["‘Mr. Traddles has a rising reputation among the lawyers, I believe?’ said I.", "\"트래들스 씨는 변호사들 사이에서 명성이 오르고 있는 분이지, 아마?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ returned the waiter, ‘probably he has, sir; but I am not aware of it myself.’", "\"글쎄요, 손님,\" 종업원이 대답했다. \"아마 그러실 겁니다, 손님. 하지만 저는 잘 모르겠는데요.\""]], [["This waiter, who was middle-aged and spare, looked for help to a waiter of more authority--a stout, potential old man, with a double chin, in black breeches and stockings, who came out of a place like a churchwarden’s pew, at the end of the coffee-room, where he kept company with a cash-box, a Directory, a Law-list, and other books and papers.", "이 종업원은 중년에 깡마른 사람이었는데, 더 권위 있는 종업원에게 도움을 구하듯 눈길을 보냈다. 그쪽은 살집이 두둑하고 위풍당당한 노인으로, 턱이 겹으로 접히고 검은 반바지에 양말을 신고 있었으며, 커피룸 끝에 있는 교회 관리인 좌석 같은 자리에서 나왔다. 그곳에서 그는 현금 상자며 인명부며 변호사 명부며 그 밖의 책과 서류들과 더불어 지내고 있었다."]], [["‘Mr. Traddles,’ said the spare waiter.", "\"트래들스 씨 말입니다.\" 깡마른 종업원이 말했다."], ["‘Number two in the Court.’", "\"코트 이 호요.\""]], [["The potential waiter waved him away, and turned, gravely, to me.", "위풍당당한 종업원은 손을 저어 그를 물리고는, 점잖게 나에게로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["‘I was inquiring,’ said I, ‘whether Mr.", "\"내가 물어본 것은,\" 나는 말했다."], ["Traddles, at number two in the Court, has not a rising reputation among the lawyers?’", "\"코트 이 호에 사는 트래들스 씨가 변호사들 사이에서 명성이 오르고 있지 않으냐는 거였네.\""]], [["‘Never heard his name,’ said the waiter, in a rich husky voice.", "\"그분 이름은 들어 본 적이 없습니다.\" 종업원이 굵직하고 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["I felt quite apologetic for Traddles.", "나는 트래들스를 대신해 사과라도 하고 싶은 심정이었다."]], [["‘He’s a young man, sure?’ said the portentous waiter, fixing his eyes severely on me.", "\"젊은 분이지요, 분명?\" 그 위엄 어린 종업원이 나에게 엄한 눈길을 박으며 말했다."], ["‘How long has he been in the Inn?’", "\"인에 들어온 지는 얼마나 됐습니까?\""]], [["‘Not above three years,’ said I.", "\"삼 년 안쪽일세.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["The waiter, who I supposed had lived in his churchwarden’s pew for forty years, could not pursue such an insignificant subject.", "종업원은 — 짐작건대 그 교회 관리인 좌석에서 사십 년은 살았을 법한 사람이었다 — 그토록 시시한 화제는 더 이어 갈 마음이 없는 듯했다."], ["He asked me what I would have for dinner?", "그는 저녁으로 무엇을 들겠느냐고 나에게 물었다."]], [["I felt I was in England again, and really was quite cast down on Traddles’s account.", "나는 내가 다시 영국에 돌아왔음을 실감했고, 트래들스의 처지를 생각하니 정말이지 풀이 죽었다."], ["There seemed to be no hope for him.", "그에게는 도무지 희망이 없어 보였다."], ["I meekly ordered a bit of fish and a steak, and stood before the fire musing on his obscurity.", "나는 얌전하게 생선 한 토막과 스테이크 하나를 주문하고는, 난로 앞에 서서 그의 무명세에 잠겨 골똘히 생각했다."]], [["As I followed the chief waiter with my eyes, I could not help thinking that the garden in which he had gradually blown to be the flower he was, was an arduous place to rise in.", "수석 종업원을 눈으로 좇으면서, 나는 그가 차츰차츰 자라 마침내 지금과 같은 꽃이 된 그 정원이라는 곳이 출세하기에 어지간히 힘든 곳이구나 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["It had such a prescriptive, stiff-necked, long-established, solemn, elderly air.", "그곳에는 그토록 인습에 얽매이고, 고집스럽고, 오래 자리 잡고, 엄숙하고, 늙수그레한 기운이 감돌았다. 나는 방 안을 둘러보았다."], ["I glanced about the room, which had had its sanded floor sanded, no doubt, in exactly the same manner when the chief waiter was a boy--if he ever was a boy, which appeared improbable; and at the shining tables, where I saw myself reflected, in unruffled depths of old mahogany; and at the lamps, without a flaw in their trimming or cleaning; and at the comfortable green curtains, with their pure brass rods, snugly enclosing the boxes; and at the two large coal fires, brightly burning; and at the rows of decanters, burly as if with the consciousness of pipes of expensive old port wine below; and both England, and the law, appeared to me to be very difficult indeed to be taken by storm.", "모래를 깐 바닥은, 의심할 바 없이, 수석 종업원이 소년이던 시절에도 — 그에게 소년 시절이라는 게 있었다면 말이지만, 그럴 성싶지는 않았다 — 정확히 똑같은 방식으로 모래가 깔려 있었으리라. 또 번들거리는 탁자들에는 잔물결 하나 없는 낡은 마호가니의 깊은 속에 내 모습이 비쳤고, 다듬고 닦은 데 흠 하나 없는 등불들이며, 깔끔한 놋쇠 봉으로 칸막이 좌석을 아늑하게 두른 편안한 초록빛 커튼들이며, 환하게 타오르는 두 개의 큼직한 석탄 난로며, 아래에 값비싼 묵은 포트와인 통이 있다는 사실을 의식하기라도 한 듯 떡 벌어진 술병들이 줄지어 늘어서 있었다. 그러니 영국도, 법조계도, 정말이지 단숨에 들이쳐 점령하기는 무척 어렵겠구나 싶었다."], ["I went up to my bedroom to change my wet clothes; and the vast extent of that old wainscoted apartment (which was over the archway leading to the Inn, I remember), and the sedate immensity of the four-post bedstead, and the indomitable gravity of the chests of drawers, all seemed to unite in sternly frowning on the fortunes of Traddles, or on any such daring youth.", "나는 젖은 옷을 갈아입으려고 내 침실로 올라갔다. 그 낡은 판벽으로 둘러싸인 널따란 방은 — 인으로 통하는 아치 길 위에 있었던 걸로 기억한다 — 그 어마어마한 넓이며, 진중하고 거대한 네 기둥 침대며, 굽힐 줄 모르는 위엄을 풍기는 서랍장들이, 죄다 한통속이 되어 트래들스의 앞날을, 아니 그런 당돌한 젊은이라면 누구든 그 앞날을 엄하게 노려보고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["I came down again to my dinner; and even the slow comfort of the meal, and the orderly silence of the place--which was bare of guests, the Long Vacation not yet being over--were eloquent on the audacity of Traddles, and his small hopes of a livelihood for twenty years to come.", "나는 다시 내려와 저녁을 들었다. 그런데 느릿느릿 흐르는 식사의 안락함과, 그 자리의 정연한 침묵마저도 — 장기 휴정기가 아직 끝나지 않아 손님이라곤 없었다 — 트래들스의 무모함과 앞으로 이십 년간 입에 풀칠이나마 할 가망이 거의 없음을 웅변하는 듯했다."]], [["I had seen nothing like this since I went away, and it quite dashed my hopes for my friend.", "나는 떠난 이래 이런 광경을 본 적이 없었으니, 그것은 내 친구에 대한 희망을 완전히 꺾어 놓았다."], ["The chief waiter had had enough of me.", "수석 종업원은 나에게 진력이 난 모양이었다."], ["He came near me no more; but devoted himself to an old gentleman in long gaiters, to meet whom a pint of special port seemed to come out of the cellar of its own accord, for he gave no order.", "그는 더는 내 곁에 오지 않고, 긴 각반을 찬 어느 노신사에게 전념했다. 그 노신사를 맞으려고 특별한 포트와인 한 파인트가 마치 제 발로 술 창고에서 걸어 나온 듯했는데, 그가 따로 주문을 하지도 않았기 때문이다."], ["The second waiter informed me, in a whisper, that this old gentleman was a retired conveyancer living in the Square, and worth a mint of money, which it was expected he would leave to his laundress’s daughter; likewise that it was rumoured that he had a service of plate in a bureau, all tarnished with lying by, though more than one spoon and a fork had never yet been beheld in his chambers by mortal vision.", "두 번째 종업원이 나에게 속삭이듯 알려 주기를, 이 노신사는 광장에 사는 은퇴한 부동산 양도 전문 변호사로, 엄청난 돈을 가졌으며 그것을 자기 빨래하는 여자의 딸에게 물려줄 거라는 소문이 있다고 했다. 또한 그는 책상 서랍 안에 은식기 한 벌을 두었는데 오래 처박아 두어 죄다 녹이 슬었다는 소문도 있으나, 정작 그의 방에서 숟가락 한 개나 포크 한 개라도 사람 눈으로 본 사람은 한 명도 없다는 것이었다."], ["By this time, I quite gave Traddles up for lost; and settled in my own mind that there was no hope for him.", "이쯤 되니 나는 트래들스를 아주 가망 없는 사람으로 단념했고, 내 마음속으로는 그에게 희망이 없다고 못 박아 버렸다."]], [["Being very anxious to see the dear old fellow, nevertheless, I dispatched my dinner, in a manner not at all calculated to raise me in the opinion of the chief waiter, and hurried out by the back way.", "그렇지만 그 정다운 옛 벗이 몹시 보고 싶어서, 나는 수석 종업원의 눈에 결코 좋게 비치지 않을 모양새로 저녁을 후딱 해치우고는 뒷문으로 서둘러 빠져나왔다."], ["Number two in the Court was soon reached; and an inscription on the door-post informing me that Mr. Traddles occupied a set of chambers on the top storey, I ascended the staircase.", "코트 이 호는 금세 닿았다. 문기둥에 새겨진 글귀가 트래들스 씨는 맨 위층에 방을 쓰고 있노라고 일러 주기에, 나는 계단을 올라갔다."], ["A crazy old staircase I found it to be, feebly lighted on each landing by a club-headed little oil wick, dying away in a little dungeon of dirty glass.", "그것은 망가져 가는 낡은 계단으로, 층계참마다 곤봉 머리처럼 생긴 작은 기름 심지가 더러운 유리로 만든 작은 감방 속에서 가물가물 죽어 가며 희미하게 비추고 있었다."]], [["In the course of my stumbling upstairs, I fancied I heard a pleasant sound of laughter; and not the laughter of an attorney or barrister, or attorney’s clerk or barrister’s clerk, but of two or three merry girls.", "비틀비틀 계단을 오르는 동안, 나는 유쾌한 웃음소리가 들리는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그것은 사무 변호사나 법정 변호사의 웃음도, 사무 변호사의 서기나 법정 변호사의 서기의 웃음도 아닌, 두세 명의 명랑한 아가씨들의 웃음이었다."], ["Happening, however, as I stopped to listen, to put my foot in a hole where the Honourable Society of Gray’s Inn had left a plank deficient, I fell down with some noise, and when I recovered my footing all was silent.", "그런데 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이다가, 마침 그레이스인의 존경하는 협회가 널빤지 한 장을 빠뜨려 놓은 구멍에 발을 디디는 바람에, 제법 요란한 소리를 내며 넘어지고 말았다. 다시 발을 추슬렀을 때는 사방이 고요했다."]], [["Groping my way more carefully, for the rest of the journey, my heart beat high when I found the outer door, which had Mr.", "남은 길은 더 조심조심 더듬어 가는데, '트래들스 씨'라고 칠해진 바깥문이 열려 있는 것을 발견하자 내 심장은 쿵쿵 뛰었다."], ["TRADDLES painted on it, open.", "나는 문을 두드렸다."], ["I knocked. A considerable scuffling within ensued, but nothing else.", "안에서 한바탕 부산하게 들썩이는 소리가 났을 뿐 다른 기척은 없었다."], ["I therefore knocked again.", "그래서 나는 다시 두드렸다."]], [["A small sharp-looking lad, half-footboy and half-clerk, who was very much out of breath, but who looked at me as if he defied me to prove it legally, presented himself.", "자그마하고 약삭빨라 보이는 사내아이가 — 반은 사환이요 반은 서기인 듯한 — 나타났는데, 숨을 몹시 헐떡이면서도 그 사실을 법으로 입증해 보라는 듯 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["‘Is Mr. Traddles within?’ I said.", "\"트래들스 씨 안에 계시느냐?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir, but he’s engaged.’", "\"네, 손님. 하지만 일하시는 중인데요.\""]], [["‘I want to see him.’", "\"내가 그분을 좀 만나야겠다.\""]], [["After a moment’s survey of me, the sharp-looking lad decided to let me in; and opening the door wider for that purpose, admitted me, first, into a little closet of a hall, and next into a little sitting-room; where I came into the presence of my old friend (also out of breath), seated at a table, and bending over papers.", "잠깐 나를 살펴보더니, 약삭빨라 보이는 그 아이는 나를 들여보내기로 마음먹은 모양이었다. 그러려고 문을 더 활짝 열고는, 먼저 옷장만 한 작은 현관으로, 다음에는 자그마한 거실로 나를 들였다. 그곳에서 나는 옛 벗과 마주했는데(그 역시 숨을 헐떡이고 있었다), 그는 탁자에 앉아 서류 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었다."]], [["‘Good God!’ cried Traddles, looking up.", "\"세상에!\" 트래들스가 고개를 들며 소리쳤다."], ["‘It’s Copperfield!’ and rushed into my arms, where I held him tight.", "\"코퍼필드잖아!\" 그러고는 내 품으로 달려들었고, 나는 그를 꼭 끌어안았다."]], [["‘All well, my dear Traddles?’", "\"다들 잘 지내나, 친애하는 트래들스?\""]], [["‘All well, my dear, dear Copperfield, and nothing but good news!’", "\"다들 잘 지내네, 친애하고 친애하는 코퍼필드, 좋은 소식뿐이라네!\""]], [["We cried with pleasure, both of us.", "우리는 둘 다 기뻐서 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["‘My dear fellow,’ said Traddles, rumpling his hair in his excitement, which was a most unnecessary operation, ‘my dearest Copperfield, my long-lost and most welcome friend, how glad I am to see you!", "\"이 사람아,\" 트래들스가 흥분해서 머리카락을 헝클어뜨리며 말했는데, 그것은 더없이 쓸데없는 짓이었다. \"내 가장 친애하는 코퍼필드, 오래도록 잃었던, 더없이 반가운 친구여, 자네를 보니 얼마나 기쁜지!"], ["How brown you are! How glad I am!", "어쩜 이리 까맣게 탔나! 정말 기뻐!"], ["Upon my life and honour, I never was so rejoiced, my beloved Copperfield, never!’", "내 목숨과 명예를 걸고, 나는 이토록 기뻐한 적이 없네, 내 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 결코 없었어!\""]], [["I was equally at a loss to express my emotions.", "나도 내 감정을 표현할 길이 없기는 마찬가지였다."], ["I was quite unable to speak, at first.", "처음에는 도무지 입이 떨어지지 않았다."]], [["‘My dear fellow!’ said Traddles.", "\"이 사람아!\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘And grown so famous!", "\"게다가 이렇게 유명해지다니!"], ["My glorious Copperfield!", "내 자랑스러운 코퍼필드!"], ["Good gracious me, WHEN did you come, WHERE have you come from, WHAT have you been doing?’", "어이쿠 세상에, 도대체 언제 왔고, 어디서 왔으며, 그동안 무얼 하고 지냈나?\""]], [["Never pausing for an answer to anything he said, Traddles, who had clapped me into an easy-chair by the fire, all this time impetuously stirred the fire with one hand, and pulled at my neck-kerchief with the other, under some wild delusion that it was a great-coat.", "자기가 하는 말 어느 것에도 대답을 기다릴 새 없이, 트래들스는 나를 난롯가 안락의자에 털썩 앉혀 놓고는, 줄곧 한 손으로는 부젓가락을 들고 격렬하게 불을 쑤셔 댔고, 다른 손으로는 그것이 무슨 외투라도 되는 양 황당한 착각에 빠져 내 목도리를 잡아당겼다."], ["Without putting down the poker, he now hugged me again; and I hugged him; and, both laughing, and both wiping our eyes, we both sat down, and shook hands across the hearth.", "그는 부젓가락도 내려놓지 않은 채 다시 나를 끌어안았고, 나도 그를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 둘 다 웃다가, 둘 다 눈물을 훔치다가, 둘 다 자리에 앉아 난로를 사이에 두고 악수를 나누었다."]], [["‘To think,’ said Traddles, ‘that you should have been so nearly coming home as you must have been, my dear old boy, and not at the ceremony!’", "\"생각해 보게,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"자네가 분명 집에 거의 다 와 가고 있었을 텐데, 이 친애하는 옛 친구야, 그 예식에는 오지 못했다니!\""]], [["‘What ceremony, my dear Traddles?’", "\"무슨 예식 말인가, 친애하는 트래들스?\""]], [["‘Good gracious me!’ cried Traddles, opening his eyes in his old way.", "\"어이쿠 세상에!\" 트래들스가 예의 그 버릇대로 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 소리쳤다."], ["‘Didn’t you get my last letter?’", "\"내 지난번 편지를 못 받았나?\""]], [["‘Certainly not, if it referred to any ceremony.’", "\"무슨 예식 얘기가 적혀 있었다면, 분명 못 받았네.\""]], [["‘Why, my dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, sticking his hair upright with both hands, and then putting his hands on my knees, ‘I am married!’", "\"아니, 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 두 손으로 머리카락을 곧추세웠다가 그 손을 내 무릎에 얹으며 말했다. \"나 결혼했다네!\""]], [["‘Married!’ I cried joyfully.", "\"결혼!\" 나는 기뻐서 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Lord bless me, yes!’ said Traddles--‘by the Reverend Horace--to Sophy--down in Devonshire.", "\"이런 세상에, 그렇다네!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"호러스 목사님 주례로 — 소피와 — 저 아래 데번셔에서 말이야."], ["Why, my dear boy, she’s behind the window curtain!", "아니, 이 친구야, 그 사람 지금 창문 커튼 뒤에 있다네!"], ["Look here!’", "자 보게!\""]], [["To my amazement, the dearest girl in the world came at that same instant, laughing and blushing, from her place of concealment.", "놀랍게도, 바로 그 순간 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 아가씨가 웃으며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 숨어 있던 자리에서 나왔다."], ["And a more cheerful, amiable, honest, happy, bright-looking bride, I believe (as I could not help saying on the spot) the world never saw.", "그렇게 명랑하고, 상냥하고, 정직하고, 행복하고, 환하게 빛나는 신부를 세상은 본 적이 없으리라고 나는 믿는다(그 자리에서 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다)."], ["I kissed her as an old acquaintance should, and wished them joy with all my might of heart.", "나는 오랜 벗이 마땅히 그러하듯 그녀에게 입을 맞추었고, 진심을 다해 두 사람의 행복을 빌었다."]], [["‘Dear me,’ said Traddles, ‘what a delightful re-union this is!", "\"이런,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"이 얼마나 즐거운 재회인가!"], ["You are so extremely brown, my dear Copperfield!", "자넨 정말 끔찍이도 까맣게 탔네그려, 친애하는 코퍼필드!"], ["God bless my soul, how happy I am!’", "아이고 맙소사, 난 어찌나 행복한지!\""]], [["‘And so am I,’ said I.", "\"나도 그렇다네.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘And I am sure I am!’ said the blushing and laughing Sophy.", "\"저야말로 정말 행복한걸요!\" 얼굴을 붉히며 웃던 소피가 말했다."]], [["‘We are all as happy as possible!’ said Traddles.", "\"우리는 다들 더할 나위 없이 행복해!\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘Even the girls are happy.", "\"그 애들마저도 행복하다네."], ["Dear me, I declare I forgot them!’", "이런, 내가 그 애들을 깜빡했구먼!\""]], [["‘Forgot?’ said I.", "\"깜빡?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘The girls,’ said Traddles.", "\"그 애들 말이야.\""], ["‘Sophy’s sisters.", "트래들스가 말했다."], ["They are staying with us.", "\"소피의 자매들이지."], ["They have come to have a peep at London.", "우리 집에 와서 묵고 있다네. 런던 구경을 좀 하러 왔어."], ["The fact is, when--was it you that tumbled upstairs, Copperfield?’", "사실은, 그러니까 — 코퍼필드, 계단에서 넘어진 게 자넨가?\""]], [["‘It was,’ said I, laughing.", "\"맞네.\" 내가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["‘Well then, when you tumbled upstairs,’ said Traddles, ‘I was romping with the girls.", "\"그래, 그러면, 자네가 계단에서 넘어졌을 때,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"나는 그 애들이랑 한창 뛰놀고 있었다네."], ["In point of fact, we were playing at Puss in the Corner.", "실은 우리가 '구석 술래잡기'를 하고 있었어."], ["But as that wouldn’t do in Westminster Hall, and as it wouldn’t look quite professional if they were seen by a client, they decamped.", "그런데 그게 웨스트민스터홀에선 어림없는 짓이고, 의뢰인 눈에라도 띄면 도무지 변호사답게 보이지 않을 테니, 그 애들이 부랴부랴 달아났지."], ["And they are now--listening, I have no doubt,’ said Traddles, glancing at the door of another room.", "그래서 지금은 — 틀림없이 엿듣고 있을 걸세.\" 트래들스가 다른 방 문 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말했다."]], [["‘I am sorry,’ said I, laughing afresh, ‘to have occasioned such a dispersion.’", "\"내가 미안하군.\" 나는 다시 웃으며 말했다. \"그렇게 흩어지게 만들었으니.\""]], [["‘Upon my word,’ rejoined Traddles, greatly delighted, ‘if you had seen them running away, and running back again, after you had knocked, to pick up the combs they had dropped out of their hair, and going on in the maddest manner, you wouldn’t have said so.", "\"맹세코,\" 트래들스가 무척 기뻐하며 받았다. \"자네가 노크한 뒤에 그 애들이 달아났다가, 머리에서 떨어진 빗을 주우러 다시 뛰어 돌아오고, 그렇게 정신없이 법석을 떠는 꼴을 봤더라면, 그런 말은 못 했을 걸세."], ["My love, will you fetch the girls?’", "여보, 그 애들 좀 데려와 주겠소?\""]], [["Sophy tripped away, and we heard her received in the adjoining room with a peal of laughter.", "소피가 종종걸음으로 가자, 옆방에서 그녀를 한바탕 웃음소리로 맞이하는 것이 들렸다."]], [["‘Really musical, isn’t it, my dear Copperfield?’ said Traddles.", "\"정말 음악 같지 않은가, 친애하는 코퍼필드?\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘It’s very agreeable to hear.", "\"듣기에 참 좋아."], ["It quite lights up these old rooms.", "이 낡은 방들을 환하게 밝혀 준다네."], ["To an unfortunate bachelor of a fellow who has lived alone all his life, you know, it’s positively delicious.", "평생을 홀로 살아온 가엾은 노총각에게는, 알다시피, 그야말로 더없이 감미롭지."], ["It’s charming.", "매혹적이야."], ["Poor things, they have had a great loss in Sophy--who, I do assure you, Copperfield is, and ever was, the dearest girl!--and it gratifies me beyond expression to find them in such good spirits.", "가엾은 것들, 그 애들은 소피를 잃어 큰 상실을 겪었거든 — 장담컨대, 코퍼필드, 소피는 지금도, 예나 지금이나 변함없이, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라네! — 그러니 그 애들이 저렇게 기운찬 모습을 보니 이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇하다네."], ["The society of girls is a very delightful thing, Copperfield.", "아가씨들이랑 함께 지내는 건 참으로 즐거운 일이야, 코퍼필드."], ["It’s not professional, but it’s very delightful.’", "변호사답지는 않지만, 무척 즐거워.\""]], [["Observing that he slightly faltered, and comprehending that in the goodness of his heart he was fearful of giving me some pain by what he had said, I expressed my concurrence with a heartiness that evidently relieved and pleased him greatly.", "그가 살짝 말을 더듬는 것을 보고, 또 마음 착한 그가 방금 한 말로 나에게 어떤 아픔을 줄까 봐 두려워하고 있음을 알아챈 나는, 그의 말에 진심으로 맞장구쳐 주었다. 그것이 그를 눈에 띄게 안심시키고 무척 기쁘게 한 것이 분명했다."]], [["‘But then,’ said Traddles, ‘our domestic arrangements are, to say the truth, quite unprofessional altogether, my dear Copperfield.", "\"하지만 그건 그렇고,\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"사실대로 말하자면, 우리 살림살이는 통째로 변호사답지가 못하다네, 친애하는 코퍼필드."], ["Even Sophy’s being here, is unprofessional.", "소피가 여기 있는 것조차 변호사답지 않지."], ["And we have no other place of abode.", "그래도 우리에겐 달리 거처가 없어."], ["We have put to sea in a cockboat, but we are quite prepared to rough it.", "우린 조각배를 타고 바다로 나선 셈이지만, 거친 풍파를 견딜 각오는 단단히 되어 있다네."], ["And Sophy’s an extraordinary manager!", "게다가 소피는 살림 솜씨가 비상한 사람이야!"], ["You’ll be surprised how those girls are stowed away.", "저 애들을 어떻게 다 욱여넣었는지 알면 자넨 깜짝 놀랄 걸세."], ["I am sure I hardly know how it’s done!’", "정말이지 어떻게 그게 가능한지 나도 잘 모르겠다니까!\""]], [["‘Are many of the young ladies with you?’ I inquired.", "\"아가씨들이 여럿 와 있나?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘The eldest, the Beauty is here,’ said Traddles, in a low confidential voice, ‘Caroline.", "\"맏이인, 그 미인이 와 있다네.\" 트래들스가 나직이 속내를 털어놓는 목소리로 말했다."], ["And Sarah’s here--the one I mentioned to you as having something the matter with her spine, you know.", "\"캐럴라인이지. 그리고 세라도 와 있어 — 알다시피 척추에 무슨 탈이 있다고 자네한테 말했던 그 애 말이야."], ["Immensely better!", "훨씬 좋아졌다네!"], ["And the two youngest that Sophy educated are with us. And Louisa’s here.’", "그리고 소피가 가르친 막내 둘도 함께 있어. 그리고 루이자도 와 있고.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ cried I.", "\"저런!\" 내가 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Traddles.", "\"그렇다네.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘Now the whole set--I mean the chambers--is only three rooms; but Sophy arranges for the girls in the most wonderful way, and they sleep as comfortably as possible.", "\"그런데 이 방 전체가 — 그러니까 이 사무실 말일세 — 고작 방 세 칸뿐인데, 소피가 그 애들 자리를 기막히게 잘 마련해 줘서, 다들 더없이 편안하게 잔다네."], ["Three in that room,’ said Traddles, pointing.", "저 방에 셋.\" 트래들스가 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다."], ["‘Two in that.’", "\"저 방에 둘이지.\""]], [["I could not help glancing round, in search of the accommodation remaining for Mr. and Mrs.", "나는 트래들스 부부에게 남은 자리는 어디인가 싶어 둘러보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Traddles. Traddles understood me.", "트래들스는 내 마음을 알아챘다."]], [["‘Well!’ said Traddles, ‘we are prepared to rough it, as I said just now, and we did improvise a bed last week, upon the floor here.", "\"음!\" 트래들스가 말했다. \"방금 말했듯이 우린 거친 풍파를 견딜 각오가 돼 있어서, 지난주에는 여기 바닥에 임시 잠자리를 마련했다네."], ["But there’s a little room in the roof--a very nice room, when you’re up there--which Sophy papered herself, to surprise me; and that’s our room at present.", "그래도 지붕 밑에 작은 방이 하나 있어 — 올라가 보면 참 아담한 방이지 — 소피가 나를 놀래 주려고 손수 도배를 했다네. 그게 지금 우리 방이야."], ["It’s a capital little gipsy sort of place.", "집시 소굴 같은 그럴듯한 작은 방이지."], ["There’s quite a view from it.’", "거기서는 전망도 제법이라네.\""]], [["‘And you are happily married at last, my dear Traddles!’ said I.", "\"드디어 자네가 행복한 결혼을 이루었구먼, 친애하는 트래들스!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘How rejoiced I am!’", "\"내가 얼마나 기쁜지!\""]], [["‘Thank you, my dear Copperfield,’ said Traddles, as we shook hands once more.", "\"고맙네, 친애하는 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 다시 한번 악수를 나누며 말했다."], ["‘Yes, I am as happy as it’s possible to be.", "\"그래, 나는 더없이 행복하다네."], ["There’s your old friend, you see,’ said Traddles, nodding triumphantly at the flower-pot and stand; ‘and there’s the table with the marble top!", "자네 보게, 저기 자네의 옛 친구가 있지.\" 트래들스가 화분과 받침대를 향해 의기양양하게 고갯짓하며 말했다. \"그리고 저기 대리석 상판을 얹은 탁자도 있고!"], ["All the other furniture is plain and serviceable, you perceive.", "나머지 가구들은 죄다 수수하고 실용적이라는 걸 알 수 있을 걸세."], ["And as to plate, Lord bless you, we haven’t so much as a tea-spoon.’", "그리고 은식기로 말하자면, 어이쿠, 우린 찻숟가락 하나도 없다네.\""]], [["‘All to be earned?’ said I, cheerfully.", "\"앞으로 다 벌어들일 거란 말이지?\" 내가 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["‘Exactly so,’ replied Traddles, ‘all to be earned.", "\"바로 그렇다네.\" 트래들스가 대답했다."], ["Of course we have something in the shape of tea-spoons, because we stir our tea.", "\"다 벌어들일 거야. 물론 찻숟가락 비슷한 건 있긴 하지. 차를 저어야 하니까."], ["But they’re Britannia metal.’", "하지만 그건 백랍 합금이라네.\""]], [["‘The silver will be the brighter when it comes,’ said I.", "\"은은 나중에 들어올 때 더 빛나는 법이지.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘The very thing we say!’ cried Traddles.", "\"바로 우리가 하는 말이 그거라네!\""], ["‘You see, my dear Copperfield,’ falling again into the low confidential tone, ‘after I had delivered my argument in DOE dem.", "트래들스가 소리쳤다. \"그게 말이야, 친애하는 코퍼필드,\" 그가 다시 나직하게 속내를 털어놓는 어조로 말했다."], ["JIPES versus WIGZIELL, which did me great service with the profession, I went down into Devonshire, and had some serious conversation in private with the Reverend Horace.", "\"내가 '도 데임. 자입스 대 위그지엘' 사건에서 변론을 펼친 뒤에 — 그게 변호사들 사이에서 나한테 큰 도움이 됐는데 — 데번셔로 내려가서, 호러스 목사님과 단둘이 진지한 이야기를 나눴다네."], ["I dwelt upon the fact that Sophy--who I do assure you, Copperfield, is the dearest girl!--’", "나는 소피가 — 장담컨대, 코퍼필드, 더없이 사랑스러운 아가씨라네! —\""]], [["‘I am certain she is!’ said I.", "\"틀림없이 그럴 거야!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘She is, indeed!’ rejoined Traddles.", "\"정말 그렇다네!\" 트래들스가 받았다."], ["‘But I am afraid I am wandering from the subject.", "\"그런데 내가 본론에서 벗어나고 있는 것 같군."], ["Did I mention the Reverend Horace?’", "내가 호러스 목사님 얘기를 했던가?\""]], [["‘You said that you dwelt upon the fact--’", "\"이러이러한 사실에 힘주어 말했다고 했네 —\""]], [["‘True!", "\"맞아!"], ["Upon the fact that Sophy and I had been engaged for a long period, and that Sophy, with the permission of her parents, was more than content to take me--in short,’ said Traddles, with his old frank smile, ‘on our present Britannia-metal footing.", "소피와 내가 오랫동안 약혼한 사이였다는 사실, 그리고 소피가 부모님 허락을 얻어 나를 기꺼이 받아들이려 했다는 사실에 힘주어 말했지 — 한마디로,\" 트래들스가 예의 그 솔직한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"지금의 이 백랍 합금 살림 처지에서 말일세. 좋아."], ["Very well. I then proposed to the Reverend Horace--who is a most excellent clergyman, Copperfield, and ought to be a Bishop; or at least ought to have enough to live upon, without pinching himself--that if I could turn the corner, say of two hundred and fifty pounds, in one year; and could see my way pretty clearly to that, or something better, next year; and could plainly furnish a little place like this, besides; then, and in that case, Sophy and I should be united.", "그래서 나는 호러스 목사님께 제안했다네 — 그분은 더없이 훌륭한 성직자이시거든, 코퍼필드, 주교가 되셔야 마땅한 분이지. 적어도 쪼들리지 않고 살아갈 만큼은 가지셔야 할 분이야 — 만약 내가 일 년 안에, 그러니까 이백오십 파운드라는 고비를 넘길 수 있고, 이듬해에는 그만큼이나 그보다 나은 형편으로 갈 길이 제법 환히 보이며, 게다가 이런 작은 거처 하나쯤은 그럭저럭 꾸밀 수 있다면, 그렇다면 그때, 그런 경우에는 소피와 내가 맺어지게 해 달라고 말이야."], ["I took the liberty of representing that we had been patient for a good many years; and that the circumstance of Sophy’s being extraordinarily useful at home, ought not to operate with her affectionate parents, against her establishment in life--don’t you see?’", "나는 외람되게도 우리가 여러 해 동안 참고 기다려 왔다는 점을 말씀드렸고, 또 소피가 집안에서 유난히 쓸모 있는 사람이라는 사정이 그 다정한 부모님으로 하여금 소피가 제 인생을 꾸리는 것을 막는 구실이 되어선 안 된다고 했다네 — 무슨 말인지 알겠나?\""]], [["‘Certainly it ought not,’ said I.", "\"당연히 그래선 안 되지.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am glad you think so, Copperfield,’ rejoined Traddles, ‘because, without any imputation on the Reverend Horace, I do think parents, and brothers, and so forth, are sometimes rather selfish in such cases. Well!", "\"자네가 그리 생각해 주니 기쁘네, 코퍼필드.\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"호러스 목사님을 탓하려는 건 결코 아니지만, 나는 정말이지 그런 경우에 부모며 형제며 하는 사람들이 때때로 좀 이기적인 데가 있다고 생각하거든. 아무튼!"], ["I also pointed out, that my most earnest desire was, to be useful to the family; and that if I got on in the world, and anything should happen to him--I refer to the Reverend Horace--’", "나는 또한 내 가장 간절한 소망은 그 집안에 보탬이 되는 것이라고, 그리고 내가 세상에서 자리를 잡고 만에 하나 그분께 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면 — 호러스 목사님 말일세 —\""]], [["‘I understand,’ said I.", "\"무슨 뜻인지 알겠네.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘--Or to Mrs. Crewler--it would be the utmost gratification of my wishes, to be a parent to the girls.", "\"— 아니면 크루얼러 부인께 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면, 그 아가씨들에게 아비 노릇을 해 주는 것이야말로 내 소원을 더없이 만족시키는 일이 되리라고 말씀드렸다네."], ["He replied in a most admirable manner, exceedingly flattering to my feelings, and undertook to obtain the consent of Mrs. Crewler to this arrangement.", "그분은 더없이 훌륭한 태도로, 내 마음을 무척이나 흐뭇하게 해 주는 답을 주셨고, 이 일에 크루얼러 부인의 승낙을 받아 내겠노라 약속하셨지."], ["They had a dreadful time of it with her.", "그분들은 부인 때문에 무척 끔찍한 시간을 보냈다네."], ["It mounted from her legs into her chest, and then into her head--’\n‘What mounted?’ I asked.", "그게 부인의 다리에서 시작해 가슴으로, 다음엔 머리로 치밀어 올랐거든 —\""]], [["‘Her grief,’ replied Traddles, with a serious look.", "\"무엇이 치밀어 올랐단 말인가?\" 내가 물었다."], ["‘Her feelings generally.", "\"부인의 슬픔 말이야.\""], ["As I mentioned on a former occasion, she is a very superior woman, but has lost the use of her limbs.", "트래들스가 자못 진지한 낯빛으로 대답했다. \"부인의 감정 전반이지. 전에 한번 말했듯이, 부인은 무척 뛰어난 분이지만 사지를 못 쓰신다네."], ["Whatever occurs to harass her, usually settles in her legs; but on this occasion it mounted to the chest, and then to the head, and, in short, pervaded the whole system in a most alarming manner.", "부인을 괴롭히는 일이 생기면, 그게 보통은 다리에 자리를 잡는데, 이번에는 가슴으로 올라갔다가, 다음엔 머리로 올라가서, 한마디로 온몸으로 더없이 끔찍하게 퍼져 나갔지."], ["However, they brought her through it by unremitting and affectionate attention; and we were married yesterday six weeks.", "그래도 그분들이 쉼 없는 정성과 사랑으로 부인을 거기서 끌어내 주셔서, 우리는 어제로 육 주 전에 결혼식을 올렸다네."], ["You have no idea what a Monster I felt, Copperfield, when I saw the whole family crying and fainting away in every direction! Mrs.", "코퍼필드, 온 가족이 사방에서 울고불고 정신을 잃는 꼴을 보았을 때 내가 얼마나 괴물 같은 심정이었는지 자넨 짐작도 못 할 걸세!"], ["Crewler couldn’t see me before we left--couldn’t forgive me, then, for depriving her of her child--but she is a good creature, and has done so since.", "크루얼러 부인은 우리가 떠나기 전에는 나를 차마 보지 못하셨다네 — 그땐 자기 아이를 빼앗아 갔다고 나를 용서하지 못하셨지 — 하지만 마음씨가 고운 분이라 그 뒤로는 용서해 주셨어."], ["I had a delightful letter from her, only this morning.’", "바로 오늘 아침에도 부인께 더없이 정다운 편지를 받았다네.\""]], [["‘And in short, my dear friend,’ said I, ‘you feel as blest as you deserve to feel!’", "\"그러니 한마디로, 친애하는 친구여,\" 내가 말했다. \"자넨 누릴 자격이 있는 만큼 행복을 느끼고 있는 게로군!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["That’s your partiality!’ laughed Traddles.", "그건 자네가 날 편애해서 그런 거지!\" 트래들스가 웃었다."], ["‘But, indeed, I am in a most enviable state.", "\"하지만, 정말이지, 나는 더없이 부러운 처지에 있다네."], ["I work hard, and read Law insatiably.", "열심히 일하고, 게걸스럽게 법을 공부하지."], ["I get up at five every morning, and don’t mind it at all.", "매일 아침 다섯 시에 일어나는데, 조금도 힘들지 않아."], ["I hide the girls in the daytime, and make merry with them in the evening.", "낮에는 그 애들을 감춰 두고, 저녁에는 함께 즐겁게 보낸다네."], ["And I assure you I am quite sorry that they are going home on Tuesday, which is the day before the first day of Michaelmas Term.", "그리고 정말이지 그 애들이 화요일에 집으로 돌아간다니 못내 아쉬워 — 그날이 미가엘 축일 학기 첫날 바로 전날이거든."], ["But here,’ said Traddles, breaking off in his confidence, and speaking aloud, ‘ARE the girls! Mr.", "그런데 마침,\" 트래들스가 속내 털어놓던 이야기를 멈추고 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그 애들이 왔구먼!"], ["Copperfield, Miss Crewler--Miss Sarah--Miss Louisa--Margaret and Lucy!’", "코퍼필드 씨, 이쪽은 크루얼러 양 — 세라 양 — 루이자 양 — 그리고 마거릿과 루시라네!\""]], [["They were a perfect nest of roses; they looked so wholesome and fresh.", "그들은 그야말로 장미 한 둥지였다. 어찌나 싱그럽고 발랄해 보이던지."], ["They were all pretty, and Miss Caroline was very handsome; but there was a loving, cheerful, fireside quality in Sophy’s bright looks, which was better than that, and which assured me that my friend had chosen well.", "다들 예뻤고, 캐럴라인 양은 무척 아름다웠다. 하지만 소피의 환한 얼굴에는 그보다 더 나은, 사랑스럽고 명랑하며 난롯가의 온기 같은 품성이 깃들어 있어, 내 친구가 잘 골랐음을 나에게 확신시켜 주었다."], ["We all sat round the fire; while the sharp boy, who I now divined had lost his breath in putting the papers out, cleared them away again, and produced the tea-things.", "우리는 다 함께 난로 둘레에 둘러앉았다. 그동안 그 약삭빠른 사내아이는 — 이제 보니 서류를 치우느라 숨이 찼던 모양이다 — 서류를 다시 치우고는 찻그릇을 내왔다."], ["After that, he retired for the night, shutting the outer door upon us with a bang. Mrs.", "그러고 나서 그 아이는 우리 등 뒤로 바깥문을 쾅 닫으며 잠자리에 들러 물러갔다."], ["Traddles, with perfect pleasure and composure beaming from her household eyes, having made the tea, then quietly made the toast as she sat in a corner by the fire.", "트래들스 부인은 살림하는 사람 특유의 눈빛에서 더없는 기쁨과 차분함을 뿜어내며 차를 끓이고는, 난롯가 한구석에 앉아 조용히 토스트를 구웠다."]], [["She had seen Agnes, she told me while she was toasting.", "토스트를 구우면서 부인이 나에게 들려준 바로는, 애그니스를 만났다고 했다."], ["‘Tom’ had taken her down into Kent for a wedding trip, and there she had seen my aunt, too; and both my aunt and Agnes were well, and they had all talked of nothing but me.", "'톰'이 신혼여행으로 부인을 켄트로 데려갔는데, 거기서 우리 고모님도 만났다는 것이었다. 고모님도 애그니스도 다들 잘 지내고 있고, 모두가 내 얘기밖에 하지 않았다고 했다."], ["‘Tom’ had never had me out of his thoughts, she really believed, all the time I had been away.", "'톰'은 내가 떠나 있던 그 오랜 세월 내내 단 한 번도 나를 마음에서 놓은 적이 없었다고, 부인은 정말로 그렇게 믿고 있었다."], ["‘Tom’ was the authority for everything.", "'톰'은 무슨 일에서든 권위 그 자체였다."], ["‘Tom’ was evidently the idol of her life; never to be shaken on his pedestal by any commotion; always to be believed in, and done homage to with the whole faith of her heart, come what might.", "'톰'은 분명 그녀의 삶의 우상이었다. 어떤 소동에도 그 받침대 위에서 흔들리는 일이 없고, 무슨 일이 닥치든 늘 믿음의 대상이며, 온 마음의 신앙을 다해 떠받드는 존재였다."]], [["The deference which both she and Traddles showed towards the Beauty, pleased me very much.", "그녀와 트래들스가 그 미인에게 보이는 깍듯함은 나를 무척 흐뭇하게 했다."], ["I don’t know that I thought it very reasonable; but I thought it very delightful, and essentially a part of their character.", "그게 썩 합당하다고 생각했는지는 모르겠지만, 무척 흐뭇한 일이라고, 그리고 본질적으로 그들 성품의 일부라고 여겼다."], ["If Traddles ever for an instant missed the tea-spoons that were still to be won, I have no doubt it was when he handed the Beauty her tea.", "트래들스가 아직 벌어들이지 못한 그 찻숟가락들을 한순간이라도 아쉬워한 적이 있다면, 그것은 틀림없이 그 미인에게 차를 건넬 때였으리라."], ["If his sweet-tempered wife could have got up any self-assertion against anyone, I am satisfied it could only have been because she was the Beauty’s sister.", "그의 마음씨 고운 아내가 누군가를 상대로 자기주장을 내세운 적이 있다면, 그것은 오직 그녀가 그 미인의 언니였기 때문이었으리라 나는 확신한다."], ["A few slight indications of a rather petted and capricious manner, which I observed in the Beauty, were manifestly considered, by Traddles and his wife, as her birthright and natural endowment.", "그 미인에게서 내가 본, 다소 응석받이에 변덕스러운 태도의 자그마한 기색들을 트래들스 부부는 명백히 그녀의 타고난 권리이자 천부의 자질로 여겼다."], ["If she had been born a Queen Bee, and they labouring Bees, they could not have been more satisfied of that.", "그녀가 여왕벌로 태어나고 그들이 일벌이었다 한들, 그보다 더 만족스럽게 받아들이지는 못했으리라."]], [["But their self-forgetfulness charmed me.", "하지만 자기를 잊은 그들의 모습이 나를 매료시켰다."], ["Their pride in these girls, and their submission of themselves to all their whims, was the pleasantest little testimony to their own worth I could have desired to see.", "이 아가씨들에 대한 그들의 자랑스러움, 그리고 그 모든 변덕에 자신들을 내맡기는 그들의 모습은, 내가 보고 싶었던 그들 자신의 가치에 대한 더없이 흐뭇하고 자그마한 증거였다."], ["If Traddles were addressed as ‘a darling’, once in the course of that evening; and besought to bring something here, or carry something there, or take something up, or put something down, or find something, or fetch something, he was so addressed, by one or other of his sisters-in-law, at least twelve times in an hour.", "그날 저녁 내내 트래들스가 '여보'라는 소리를 한 번이라도 들었다면, 그리고 이것 좀 가져오라, 저것 좀 갖다 놓아라, 이것 좀 집어 들어라, 저것 좀 내려놓아라, 무엇 좀 찾아라, 무엇 좀 가져오라는 부탁을 받았다면, 그는 처제들 가운데 누군가에게 한 시간에 적어도 열두 번은 그런 부탁을 받았다."], ["Neither could they do anything without Sophy.", "또한 그들은 소피 없이는 아무것도 하지 못했다."], ["Somebody’s hair fell down, and nobody but Sophy could put it up.", "누군가의 머리가 흘러내리면, 그것을 올려 줄 사람은 소피밖에 없었다."], ["Somebody forgot how a particular tune went, and nobody but Sophy could hum that tune right.", "누군가가 어떤 곡조가 어떻게 가는지 잊어버리면, 그 곡조를 바르게 흥얼거려 줄 사람은 소피밖에 없었다."], ["Somebody wanted to recall the name of a place in Devonshire, and only Sophy knew it.", "누군가가 데번셔의 어떤 지명을 떠올리고 싶어 하면, 그것을 아는 사람은 소피뿐이었다."], ["Something was wanted to be written home, and Sophy alone could be trusted to write before breakfast in the morning.", "집에 편지를 써 보낼 일이 생기면, 아침 식사 전에 미덥게 그 편지를 쓸 수 있는 사람은 소피 한 사람뿐이었다."], ["Somebody broke down in a piece of knitting, and no one but Sophy was able to put the defaulter in the right direction.", "누군가가 뜨개질을 하다 막히면, 그 막힌 데를 바로잡아 줄 사람은 소피밖에 없었다."], ["They were entire mistresses of the place, and Sophy and Traddles waited on them.", "그들은 이곳의 완전한 안주인들이었고, 소피와 트래들스는 그들의 시중을 들었다."], ["How many children Sophy could have taken care of in her time, I can’t imagine; but she seemed to be famous for knowing every sort of song that ever was addressed to a child in the English tongue; and she sang dozens to order with the clearest little voice in the world, one after another (every sister issuing directions for a different tune, and the Beauty generally striking in last), so that I was quite fascinated.", "소피가 한창때 몇 명의 아이를 돌보았을지 나로서는 짐작도 못 하겠지만, 영어로 아이에게 불러 준 온갖 노래를 죄다 안다는 점에서 유명한 사람인 듯했다. 그녀는 세상에서 가장 맑고 자그마한 목소리로 주문받은 노래를 수십 곡씩 잇달아 불러 주었으니(자매마다 저마다 다른 곡을 시켰고, 미인은 대개 맨 마지막에 끼어들었다), 나는 그만 완전히 매혹되고 말았다."], ["The best of all was, that, in the midst of their exactions, all the sisters had a great tenderness and respect both for Sophy and Traddles.", "무엇보다도 좋았던 것은, 그렇게 요구를 늘어놓는 와중에도 자매들 모두가 소피와 트래들스 두 사람에게 깊은 다정함과 존경을 품고 있다는 점이었다."], ["I am sure, when I took my leave, and Traddles was coming out to walk with me to the coffee-house, I thought I had never seen an obstinate head of hair, or any other head of hair, rolling about in such a shower of kisses.", "정말이지, 내가 작별을 고하고 트래들스가 나를 커피하우스까지 바래다주려고 나설 때, 나는 그토록 고집스러운 머리카락이, 아니 그 어떤 머리카락이든, 그렇게 쏟아지는 입맞춤의 소나기 속에서 마구 뒤엉키는 것을 본 적이 없다고 생각했다."]], [["Altogether, it was a scene I could not help dwelling on with pleasure, for a long time after I got back and had wished Traddles good night.", "대체로 그것은, 돌아와서 트래들스에게 잘 자라고 인사한 뒤에도 오래도록 즐거이 곱씹지 않을 수 없는 광경이었다."], ["If I had beheld a thousand roses blowing in a top set of chambers, in that withered Gray’s Inn, they could not have brightened it half so much.", "그 시들어 버린 그레이스인의 맨 위층 사무실에서 천 송이 장미가 피어나는 것을 보았다 한들, 그처럼 절반만큼이라도 그곳을 환하게 밝혀 주지는 못했으리라."], ["The idea of those Devonshire girls, among the dry law-stationers and the attorneys’ offices; and of the tea and toast, and children’s songs, in that grim atmosphere of pounce and parchment, red-tape, dusty wafers, ink-jars, brief and draft paper, law reports, writs, declarations, and bills of costs; seemed almost as pleasantly fanciful as if I had dreamed that the Sultan’s famous family had been admitted on the roll of attorneys, and had brought the talking bird, the singing tree, and the golden water into Gray’s Inn Hall.", "메마른 법률 문구점들과 사무 변호사 사무실들 틈에 자리한 그 데번셔 아가씨들이라는 발상, 그리고 가루분과 양피지, 붉은 끈, 먼지 앉은 봉인지, 잉크병, 소장과 초안 용지, 판례집, 영장, 소장, 비용 청구서가 빚어내는 그 음산한 분위기 속의 차와 토스트와 동요라는 발상은, 마치 술탄의 그 유명한 가족이 사무 변호사 명부에 올라 말하는 새와 노래하는 나무와 황금빛 물을 그레이스인 홀로 가져왔다고 내가 꿈꾼 것이나 다름없이 유쾌한 공상으로 다가왔다."], ["Somehow, I found that I had taken leave of Traddles for the night, and come back to the coffee-house, with a great change in my despondency about him.", "어쩐 일인지, 나는 트래들스에게 밤 인사를 하고 커피하우스로 돌아온 무렵에는, 그에 대한 내 낙담이 크게 달라져 있는 것을 깨달았다."], ["I began to think he would get on, in spite of all the many orders of chief waiters in England.", "나는 영국의 그 숱한 수석 종업원들이 무어라 분부하든, 그가 잘 풀려 나가리라는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["Drawing a chair before one of the coffee-room fires to think about him at my leisure, I gradually fell from the consideration of his happiness to tracing prospects in the live-coals, and to thinking, as they broke and changed, of the principal vicissitudes and separations that had marked my life.", "느긋하게 그를 생각해 보려고 커피룸 난로 가운데 하나 앞으로 의자를 끌어다 놓고 보니, 나는 차츰 그의 행복을 헤아리던 데서 벗어나, 벌겋게 타는 석탄 속에서 앞날을 더듬어 보고, 또 그것들이 부서지고 모양을 바꾸는 모습을 따라 내 인생에 새겨진 주요한 부침과 이별들을 떠올리게 되었다."], ["I had not seen a coal fire, since I had left England three years ago: though many a wood fire had I watched, as it crumbled into hoary ashes, and mingled with the feathery heap upon the hearth, which not inaptly figured to me, in my despondency, my own dead hopes.", "삼 년 전 영국을 떠난 뒤로 나는 석탄 불을 본 적이 없었다. 비록 장작 불이라면 숱하게 지켜봐 왔지만 말이다. 그것이 허옇게 재로 부스러져 난로 위 깃털 같은 잿더미에 섞이는 모습을, 낙담에 빠진 나에게 그것은 내 죽어 버린 희망의 모습을 자못 어울리게 그려 보이곤 했다."]], [["I could think of the past now, gravely, but not bitterly; and could contemplate the future in a brave spirit.", "이제 나는 지난날을 떠올릴 수 있었다. 침울하게나마, 그러나 쓰라리지는 않게. 그리고 씩씩한 마음으로 앞날을 헤아릴 수 있었다."], ["Home, in its best sense, was for me no more.", "가장 참된 의미에서의 가정은, 이제 나에게 더는 없었다."], ["She in whom I might have inspired a dearer love, I had taught to be my sister.", "내가 더 애틋한 사랑을 불어넣어 줄 수도 있었을 그 사람을, 나는 내 누이가 되도록 가르쳐 버렸다."], ["She would marry, and would have new claimants on her tenderness; and in doing it, would never know the love for her that had grown up in my heart.", "그녀는 결혼할 테고, 그 다정함을 새로이 차지할 사람들이 생길 테며, 그러는 동안에도 내 가슴속에 자라난 그녀를 향한 사랑은 영영 알지 못하리라."], ["It was right that I should pay the forfeit of my headlong passion. What I reaped, I had sown.", "내가 무모한 열정의 대가를 치르는 것은 마땅한 일이었다. 내가 뿌린 것을, 내가 거두는 것이었다."]], [["I was thinking.", "나는 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["And had I truly disciplined my heart to this, and could I resolutely bear it, and calmly hold the place in her home which she had calmly held in mine,--when I found my eyes resting on a countenance that might have arisen out of the fire, in its association with my early remembrances.", "그리고 내가 정말로 이 일에 마음을 단단히 길들였는지, 굳세게 이를 견뎌 낼 수 있을지, 그녀가 내 가정에서 차분히 차지해 온 자리를 나도 차분히 그녀의 가정에서 지켜 낼 수 있을지 헤아리고 있는데 — 문득 내 눈길이 어느 얼굴에 멎어 있는 것을 깨달았다. 그 얼굴은 어린 시절의 기억과 얽혀 있어, 마치 불 속에서 떠오른 것만 같았다."]], [["Little Mr. Chillip the Doctor, to whose good offices I was indebted in the very first chapter of this history, sat reading a newspaper in the shadow of an opposite corner.", "이 이야기의 맨 첫 장에서 내가 그 친절한 도움을 입었던 자그마한 칠립 의사 선생님이, 맞은편 구석의 그늘 속에 앉아 신문을 읽고 있었다. 이 무렵 그는 나이가 제법 들어 있었다."], ["He was tolerably stricken in years by this time; but, being a mild, meek, calm little man, had worn so easily, that I thought he looked at that moment just as he might have looked when he sat in our parlour, waiting for me to be born.", "하지만 온화하고 유순하며 차분한 자그마한 사람이라 그런지 어찌나 곱게 늙었던지, 그 순간 그의 모습은 우리 집 거실에 앉아 내가 태어나기를 기다리던 시절의 모습 그대로인 것 같았다."]], [["Mr. Chillip had left Blunderstone six or seven years ago, and I had never seen him since.", "칠립 선생님은 육칠 년 전에 블런더스톤을 떠났고, 나는 그 뒤로 그를 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["He sat placidly perusing the newspaper, with his little head on one side, and a glass of warm sherry negus at his elbow.", "그는 자그마한 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울인 채, 팔꿈치 옆에 따끈한 셰리주 니거스 한 잔을 두고 평온히 신문을 들여다보고 있었다."], ["He was so extremely conciliatory in his manner that he seemed to apologize to the very newspaper for taking the liberty of reading it.", "그의 태도가 어찌나 더없이 고분고분하던지, 신문을 읽는 외람됨을 두고 그 신문에게마저 사과하는 듯이 보였다."]], [["I walked up to where he was sitting, and said, ‘How do you do, Mr.", "나는 그가 앉아 있는 데로 걸어가, \"안녕하십니까, 칠립 선생님?\""], ["Chillip?’", "하고 말했다."]], [["He was greatly fluttered by this unexpected address from a stranger, and replied, in his slow way, ‘I thank you, sir, you are very good.", "낯선 사람의 이 뜻밖의 말 걸기에 그는 몹시 허둥대더니, 예의 그 느릿한 말투로 대답했다. \"감사합니다, 손님, 참 친절하시군요. 고맙습니다, 손님."], ["Thank you, sir. I hope YOU are well.’", "손님께서도 안녕하시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘You don’t remember me?’ said I.", "\"저를 기억 못 하시는군요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 손님,\""], ["Chillip, smiling very meekly, and shaking his head as he surveyed me, ‘I have a kind of an impression that something in your countenance is familiar to me, sir; but I couldn’t lay my hand upon your name, really.’", "칠립 선생님이 더없이 유순하게 웃으며, 나를 살피느라 고개를 절레절레 흔들면서 대답했다. \"어쩐지 손님 얼굴에 낯익은 무언가가 있는 듯한 느낌이 들기는 하는데요, 손님. 도무지 성함이 떠오르질 않는군요, 정말로.\""]], [["‘And yet you knew it, long before I knew it myself,’ I returned.", "\"하지만 선생님은 제가 제 이름을 알기 한참 전부터 그걸 알고 계셨답니다.\" 내가 받았다."]], [["‘Did I indeed, sir?’ said Mr. Chillip.", "\"제가 정말 그랬습니까, 손님?\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다."], ["‘Is it possible that I had the honour, sir, of officiating when--?’", "\"혹시 제가, 손님, 그러니까 — 그때 거들어 드리는 영광을 누렸던 건 아닐까요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said I.", "\"맞습니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Dear me!’ cried Mr. Chillip.", "\"이런 세상에!\" 칠립 선생님이 소리쳤다."], ["‘But no doubt you are a good deal changed since then, sir?’", "\"하지만 그때 이후로 손님은 무척 많이 변하셨겠지요, 손님?\""]], [["‘Probably,’ said I.", "\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ observed Mr.", "\"저, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다."], ["Chillip, ‘I hope you’ll excuse me, if I am compelled to ask the favour of your name?’", "\"제가 어쩔 수 없이 성함을 여쭙는 무례를 범하더라도 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["On my telling him my name, he was really moved.", "내 이름을 일러 주자, 그는 정말로 감동했다."], ["He quite shook hands with me--which was a violent proceeding for him, his usual course being to slide a tepid little fish-slice, an inch or two in advance of his hip, and evince the greatest discomposure when anybody grappled with it.", "그는 나와 한바탕 악수까지 했는데 — 그에게는 격렬한 행동이었다. 그가 늘 하는 식이라면 자기 엉덩이께에서 한두 치쯤 앞으로 미적지근한 생선용 칼 같은 손을 슬며시 내밀고는, 누구든 그것을 덥석 잡으면 더없이 당황하는 기색을 보이는 것이었으니까."], ["Even now, he put his hand in his coat-pocket as soon as he could disengage it, and seemed relieved when he had got it safe back.", "지금도 그는 손을 빼낼 수 있게 되자마자 외투 주머니에 손을 집어넣고, 그 손을 무사히 도로 거둬들이고 나서야 안심하는 듯했다."]], [["‘Dear me, sir!’ said Mr.", "\"이런, 손님!\""], ["Chillip, surveying me with his head on one side. ‘And it’s Mr.", "칠립 선생님이 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울인 채 나를 살피며 말했다."], ["Copperfield, is it? Well, sir, I think I should have known you, if I had taken the liberty of looking more closely at you.", "\"그러니까 코퍼필드 씨로군요, 그렇지요? 저런, 손님, 외람되게도 좀 더 가까이 들여다봤더라면 알아봤을 텐데요."], ["There’s a strong resemblance between you and your poor father, sir.’", "손님은 돌아가신 부친과 무척 닮으셨거든요, 손님.\""]], [["‘I never had the happiness of seeing my father,’ I observed.", "\"저는 아버지를 뵙는 기쁨을 한 번도 누리지 못했답니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Very true, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"지당하신 말씀입니다, 손님.\""], ["Chillip, in a soothing tone.", "칠립 선생님이 달래는 듯한 어조로 말했다."], ["‘And very much to be deplored it was, on all accounts!", "\"그리고 그것은, 여러모로, 무척이나 애석한 일이었지요!"], ["We are not ignorant, sir,’ said Mr.", "저희도, 손님,\""], ["Chillip, slowly shaking his little head again, ‘down in our part of the country, of your fame.", "칠립 선생님이 다시 자그마한 머리를 천천히 흔들며 말했다. \"저희 시골 동네에서도 손님의 명성을 모르지 않습니다."], ["There must be great excitement here, sir,’ said Mr.", "여기는 무척 흥분되는 곳이겠지요, 손님.\""], ["Chillip, tapping himself on the forehead with his forefinger.", "칠립 선생님이 집게손가락으로 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다."], ["‘You must find it a trying occupation, sir!’", "\"참으로 고된 일이라 여기실 게 틀림없습니다, 손님!\""]], [["‘What is your part of the country now?’ I asked, seating myself near him.", "\"요즘은 어느 동네에 계십니까?\" 나는 그의 곁에 자리를 잡으며 물었다."]], [["‘I am established within a few miles of Bury St.", "\"베리세인트에드먼즈에서 몇 마일 안쪽에 자리 잡았습니다, 손님.\""], ["Edmund’s, sir,’ said Mr.", "칠립 선생님이 말했다."], ["Chillip. ‘Mrs. Chillip, coming into a little property in that neighbourhood, under her father’s will, I bought a practice down there, in which you will be glad to hear I am doing well.", "\"집사람이 그 동네에서 부친 유언에 따라 자그마한 재산을 물려받게 되어서, 제가 그 아래쪽에서 병원을 하나 사들였지요. 손님께서 들으시면 기뻐하실 텐데, 거기서 잘 지내고 있답니다."], ["My daughter is growing quite a tall lass now, sir,’ said Mr.", "제 딸아이는 이제 제법 키 큰 아가씨로 자라고 있답니다, 손님.\""], ["Chillip, giving his little head another little shake.", "칠립 선생님이 자그마한 머리를 또 한 번 자그맣게 흔들며 말했다."], ["‘Her mother let down two tucks in her frocks only last week. Such is time, you see, sir!’", "\"바로 지난주에도 애 엄마가 그 애 원피스 단을 두 단이나 내려 주었지요. 세월이란 게 그런 거지요, 손님, 안 그렇습니까!\""]], [["As the little man put his now empty glass to his lips, when he made this reflection, I proposed to him to have it refilled, and I would keep him company with another.", "이렇게 술회하면서 그 자그마한 사람이 이제 빈 잔을 입에 갖다 대기에, 나는 그에게 잔을 다시 채우자고 권하면서 나도 한 잔 더 들어 같이 어울리겠노라 했다."], ["‘Well, sir,’ he returned, in his slow way, ‘it’s more than I am accustomed to; but I can’t deny myself the pleasure of your conversation.", "\"글쎄요, 손님,\" 그가 예의 그 느릿한 말투로 받았다. \"제 주량을 넘는 일입니다만, 손님과 이야기 나누는 즐거움을 차마 마다하지 못하겠군요."], ["It seems but yesterday that I had the honour of attending you in the measles.", "손님께서 홍역을 앓으실 때 제가 돌봐 드리는 영광을 누린 게 바로 어제 일만 같습니다."], ["You came through them charmingly, sir!’", "손님은 홍역을 멋지게 이겨 내셨지요, 손님!\""]], [["I acknowledged this compliment, and ordered the negus, which was soon produced.", "나는 이 칭찬을 받아들이고는 니거스를 주문했고, 그것은 곧 나왔다. \"정말 보기 드문 호사로군요!\""], ["‘Quite an uncommon dissipation!’ said Mr.", "칠립 선생님이 잔을 저으며 말했다."], ["Chillip, stirring it, ‘but I can’t resist so extraordinary an occasion. You have no family, sir?’", "\"하지만 이렇게 각별한 자리를 거역할 수가 없어서요. 손님은 가족이 없으신가요, 손님?\""]], [["I shook my head.", "나는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘I was aware that you sustained a bereavement, sir, some time ago,’ said Mr.", "\"손님께서 얼마 전에 상을 당하셨다는 건 알고 있었습니다, 손님.\""], ["Chillip. ‘I heard it from your father-in-law’s sister.", "칠립 선생님이 말했다. \"손님의 장인 누이에게서 들었지요."], ["Very decided character there, sir?’", "거참 보통내기가 아닌 분이더군요, 손님?\""]], [["‘Why, yes,’ said I, ‘decided enough.", "\"음, 그렇지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"보통내기가 아니고말고요."], ["Where did you see her, Mr. Chillip?’", "그 사람을 어디서 보셨습니까, 칠립 선생님?\""]], [["‘Are you not aware, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"손님께선 모르시나요, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 더없이 평온한 미소를 지으며 받았다."], ["Chillip, with his placidest smile, ‘that your father-in-law is again a neighbour of mine?’", "\"손님의 장인이 다시 제 이웃이 됐다는 걸요?\" \"몰랐습니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ said I. ‘He is indeed, sir!’ said Mr.", "\"정말 그렇답니다, 손님!\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다."], ["Chillip. ‘Married a young lady of that part, with a very good little property, poor thing.---And this action of the brain now, sir?", "\"그 동네 젊은 아가씨와 결혼했는데, 자그마한 재산을 제법 가진 사람이었지요, 가엾게도. — 그런데 손님, 지금 이 두뇌 활동 말입니다만, 손님은 이게 피곤하게 느껴지지 않으십니까?\""], ["Don’t you find it fatigue you?’ said Mr. Chillip, looking at me like an admiring Robin.", "칠립 선생님이 감탄에 젖은 울새처럼 나를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["I waived that question, and returned to the Murdstones.", "나는 그 질문을 흘려 넘기고 머드스톤 남매 얘기로 돌아갔다."], ["‘I was aware of his being married again.", "\"그 사람이 재혼한 건 저도 알고 있었습니다."], ["Do you attend the family?’ I asked.", "그 집안을 진찰하십니까?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Not regularly. I have been called in,’ he replied.", "\"규칙적으로는 아닙니다. 불려 간 적은 있지요.\""], ["‘Strong phrenological developments of the organ of firmness, in Mr. Murdstone and his sister, sir.’", "그가 대답했다. \"머드스톤 씨와 그 누이는 골상학으로 따지면 완고함을 관장하는 부위가 무척 발달해 있더군요, 손님.\""]], [["I replied with such an expressive look, that Mr. Chillip was emboldened by that, and the negus together, to give his head several short shakes, and thoughtfully exclaim, ‘Ah, dear me!", "내가 어찌나 의미심장한 표정으로 대꾸했던지, 칠립 선생님은 그 표정과 니거스에 힘입어 머리를 몇 차례 짧게 흔들고는 생각에 잠긴 듯 외쳤다. \"아아, 이런!"], ["We remember old times, Mr. Copperfield!’", "우리가 옛 시절을 기억하는군요, 코퍼필드 씨!\""]], [["‘And the brother and sister are pursuing their old course, are they?’ said I.", "\"그 남매는 여전히 옛 행실대로 살고 있나 보군요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir,’ replied Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 손님,\""], ["Chillip, ‘a medical man, being so much in families, ought to have neither eyes nor ears for anything but his profession.", "칠립 선생님이 대답했다. \"의사라는 사람은 워낙 여러 집안을 드나드니, 제 직업 말고는 눈도 귀도 가져선 안 되겠지요."], ["Still, I must say, they are very severe, sir: both as to this life and the next.’", "그래도 한마디 해야겠는데, 그분들은 무척 가혹합니다, 손님. 이승에 대해서나 저승에 대해서나 둘 다요.\""]], [["‘The next will be regulated without much reference to them, I dare say,’ I returned: ‘what are they doing as to this?’", "\"저승은 그들과 별 상관없이 다스려질 테니 걱정 없을 겁니다.\" 내가 받았다. \"이승에 대해선 그들이 어떻게 하고 있습니까?\""]], [["Mr. Chillip shook his head, stirred his negus, and sipped it.", "칠립 선생님은 고개를 흔들고, 니거스를 젓더니, 한 모금 홀짝였다."]], [["‘She was a charming woman, sir!’ he observed in a plaintive manner.", "\"매력적인 부인이었답니다, 손님!\" 그가 애처로운 투로 말했다."]], [["‘The present Mrs. Murdstone?’", "\"지금의 머드스톤 부인 말입니까?\""]], [["‘A charming woman indeed, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"정말 매력적인 부인이었지요, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다."], ["Chillip; ‘as amiable, I am sure, as it was possible to be! Mrs.", "\"더없이 상냥한, 그야말로 상냥할 수 있는 만큼 상냥한 분이었습니다!"], ["Chillip’s opinion is, that her spirit has been entirely broken since her marriage, and that she is all but melancholy mad.", "우리 집사람 생각으론, 그 부인의 기개가 결혼한 뒤로 완전히 꺾여 버려서, 거의 우울증에 미칠 지경이 됐다는군요. 그리고 여자들이란,\""], ["And the ladies,’ observed Mr.", "칠립 선생님이 조심스레 말했다."], ["Chillip, timorously, ‘are great observers, sir.’", "\"관찰력이 대단하지요, 손님.\""]], [["‘I suppose she was to be subdued and broken to their detestable mould, Heaven help her!’ said I.", "\"그 가증스러운 틀에 맞춰 그 부인을 억누르고 꺾어 버리려 했겠지요, 하느님 도우소서!\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘And she has been.’", "\"그리고 그렇게 돼 버린 거고요.\""]], [["‘Well, sir, there were violent quarrels at first, I assure you,’ said Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 손님, 처음에는 격렬한 다툼이 있었답니다, 정말로요.\""], ["Chillip; ‘but she is quite a shadow now.", "칠립 선생님이 말했다."], ["Would it be considered forward if I was to say to you, sir, in confidence, that since the sister came to help, the brother and sister between them have nearly reduced her to a state of imbecility?’", "\"하지만 이제 그 부인은 완전히 그림자가 됐지요. 외람된 말씀일지 모르나, 손님, 은밀히 말씀드리자면, 그 누이가 거들러 온 뒤로 오누이가 둘이서 그 부인을 거의 백치 상태로 만들어 버렸답니다.\""]], [["I told him I could easily believe it.", "나는 그 말을 쉬이 믿을 수 있다고 그에게 말했다."]], [["‘I have no hesitation in saying,’ said Mr.", "\"저는 서슴없이 말씀드릴 수 있습니다, 손님,\""], ["Chillip, fortifying himself with another sip of negus, ‘between you and me, sir, that her mother died of it--or that tyranny, gloom, and worry have made Mrs. Murdstone nearly imbecile.", "칠립 선생님이 니거스를 또 한 모금 들이켜 기운을 차리며 말했다. \"이건 손님과 저 둘만의 얘기입니다만, 그 부인의 어머니는 그 일로 돌아가신 거나 다름없다고요 — 아니, 그 횡포와 우울과 시달림이 머드스톤 부인을 거의 백치로 만들어 버렸다고 말입니다."], ["She was a lively young woman, sir, before marriage, and their gloom and austerity destroyed her.", "결혼 전에는 발랄한 젊은 여자였지요, 손님. 그런데 그들의 우울함과 엄혹함이 그 여자를 망가뜨린 겁니다."], ["They go about with her, now, more like her keepers than her husband and sister-in-law.", "이제 그들은 그 부인을 데리고 다니는 게, 남편과 시누이라기보단 마치 간수 같답니다."], ["That was Mrs. Chillip’s remark to me, only last week.", "그게 바로 지난주에 우리 집사람이 저한테 한 말이지요."], ["And I assure you, sir, the ladies are great observers. Mrs.", "그리고 정말이지, 손님, 여자들은 관찰력이 대단하답니다."], ["Chillip herself is a great observer!’", "우리 집사람부터가 대단한 관찰자고요!\""]], [["‘Does he gloomily profess to be (I am ashamed to use the word in such association) religious still?’ I inquired.", "\"그자는 아직도 음울하게 자기가 (이런 데 그 말을 갖다 붙이려니 부끄럽습니다만) 신앙심 깊다고 떠벌리고 다닙니까?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘You anticipate, sir,’ said Mr. Chillip, his eyelids getting quite red with the unwonted stimulus in which he was indulging.", "\"손님께서 앞질러 말씀하시는군요, 손님.\" 칠립 선생님이 평소답지 않게 들이켠 자극에 눈꺼풀이 제법 벌게진 채 말했다."], ["‘One of Mrs. Chillip’s most impressive remarks. Mrs.", "\"우리 집사람이 한 가장 인상 깊은 말 가운데 하나거든요. 우리 집사람이,\""], ["Chillip,’ he proceeded, in the calmest and slowest manner, ‘quite electrified me, by pointing out that Mr. Murdstone sets up an image of himself, and calls it the Divine Nature.", "그가 더없이 차분하고 느릿한 투로 이었다. \"머드스톤 씨가 자기 자신의 형상을 세워 놓고 그걸 신성이라 부른다고 짚어 줬을 때, 저는 정말 깜짝 놀랐답니다."], ["You might have knocked me down on the flat of my back, sir, with the feather of a pen, I assure you, when Mrs. Chillip said so.", "우리 집사람이 그렇게 말했을 때, 손님, 정말이지 깃펜 깃털로 살짝 밀기만 해도 제가 벌렁 나자빠졌을 겁니다."], ["The ladies are great observers, sir?’", "여자들은 관찰력이 대단하지요, 손님?\""]], [["‘Intuitively,’ said I, to his extreme delight.", "\"본능적으로 그렇지요.\" 내가 말했고, 그는 더없이 기뻐했다."]], [["‘I am very happy to receive such support in my opinion, sir,’ he rejoined.", "\"제 견해에 그런 지지를 받게 되어 무척 기쁩니다, 손님.\" 그가 받았다."], ["‘It is not often that I venture to give a non-medical opinion, I assure you. Mr.", "\"정말이지, 제가 의학과 무관한 견해를 감히 내놓는 일은 흔치 않거든요."], ["Murdstone delivers public addresses sometimes, and it is said,--in short, sir, it is said by Mrs. Chillip,--that the darker tyrant he has lately been, the more ferocious is his doctrine.’", "머드스톤 씨는 가끔 대중 연설을 하는데, 사람들 말이 — 한마디로, 손님, 우리 집사람 말로는 — 그자가 요즘 더 음흉한 폭군이 될수록, 그자의 교리는 더 흉포해진다는군요.\""]], [["‘I believe Mrs. Chillip to be perfectly right,’ said I.", "\"우리 집사람 말이 더없이 옳다고 봅니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Mrs. Chillip does go so far as to say,’ pursued the meekest of little men, much encouraged, ‘that what such people miscall their religion, is a vent for their bad humours and arrogance.", "\"우리 집사람은 이런 말까지 한답니다,\" 더없이 유순한 그 자그마한 사람이 한껏 기운을 얻어 이었다."], ["And do you know I must say, sir,’ he continued, mildly laying his head on one side, ‘that I DON’T find authority for Mr.", "\"그런 자들이 자기네 신앙이라고 잘못 일컫는 것은, 사실은 제 못된 심사와 오만을 쏟아 내는 배출구일 뿐이라고요. 그리고 그거 아십니까, 손님,\" 그가 부드럽게 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울이며 이었다."], ["and Miss Murdstone in the New Testament?’", "\"저는 신약 성서 어디에서도 머드스톤 씨와 머드스톤 양에 대한 근거를 찾지 못하겠더군요.\""]], [["‘I never found it either!’ said I.", "\"저도 한 번도 못 찾았습니다!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘In the meantime, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"한편으로, 손님,\" 칠립 선생님이 말했다."], ["Chillip, ‘they are much disliked; and as they are very free in consigning everybody who dislikes them to perdition, we really have a good deal of perdition going on in our neighbourhood! However, as Mrs.", "\"그들은 무척 미움을 받고 있답니다. 그런데 그들은 자기네를 싫어하는 사람은 누구든 거리낌 없이 지옥에 떨어지라고 단죄하니, 정말이지 우리 동네에선 지옥행이 어지간히 흥하고 있는 셈이지요!"], ["Chillip says, sir, they undergo a continual punishment; for they are turned inward, to feed upon their own hearts, and their own hearts are very bad feeding.", "하지만 우리 집사람 말마따나, 손님, 그들은 끊임없는 벌을 받고 있는 겁니다. 안으로만 향해 제 마음을 파먹고 사는데, 그 마음이라는 게 영 못된 먹이거든요."], ["Now, sir, about that brain of yours, if you’ll excuse my returning to it.", "그런데 손님, 그 두뇌 말씀입니다만, 다시 거기로 돌아가는 걸 양해해 주신다면요."], ["Don’t you expose it to a good deal of excitement, sir?’", "손님은 그걸 너무 많은 흥분에 내맡기시는 것 아닙니까, 손님?\""]], [["I found it not difficult, in the excitement of Mr. Chillip’s own brain, under his potations of negus, to divert his attention from this topic to his own affairs, on which, for the next half-hour, he was quite loquacious; giving me to understand, among other pieces of information, that he was then at the Gray’s Inn Coffee-house to lay his professional evidence before a Commission of Lunacy, touching the state of mind of a patient who had become deranged from excessive drinking.", "니거스를 들이켠 칠립 선생님 자신의 두뇌가 흥분한 틈을 타, 나는 어렵잖게 그의 관심을 이 화제에서 그 자신의 일로 돌려놓았다. 그러자 그는 이후 삼십 분 동안 제법 수다스러워졌는데, 다른 여러 소식 가운데서도, 자기가 지금 그레이스인 커피하우스에 와 있는 것은 정신 질환 위원회에 의학 소견을 제출하기 위해서이며, 그 환자는 과음으로 정신이 이상해진 사람이라는 것을 나에게 알려 주었다."], ["‘And I assure you, sir,’ he said, ‘I am extremely nervous on such occasions.", "\"그리고 정말이지, 손님,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그런 자리에선 무척 떨린답니다."], ["I could not support being what is called Bullied, sir. It would quite unman me.", "이른바 윽박지름을 당하는 건 도저히 못 견디겠어요, 손님. 그러면 완전히 기가 꺾여 버리거든요."], ["Do you know it was some time before I recovered the conduct of that alarming lady, on the night of your birth, Mr. Copperfield?’", "그거 아십니까, 손님이 태어나신 날 밤, 제가 그 무시무시한 부인을 다루던 기개를 되찾는 데 한참이나 걸렸답니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\""]], [["I told him that I was going down to my aunt, the Dragon of that night, early in the morning; and that she was one of the most tender-hearted and excellent of women, as he would know full well if he knew her better.", "나는 그날 밤의 그 용에게로, 바로 우리 고모님께로 내일 아침 일찍 내려갈 참이며, 고모님을 좀 더 잘 알게 되면 그도 익히 알게 되겠지만, 고모님은 더없이 다정하고 훌륭한 부인 가운데 한 분이라고 그에게 말해 주었다."], ["The mere notion of the possibility of his ever seeing her again, appeared to terrify him.", "그가 언젠가 고모님을 다시 볼지도 모른다는 생각만으로도 그는 겁에 질리는 듯했다."], ["He replied with a small pale smile, ‘Is she so, indeed, sir?", "그는 핏기 없는 자그마한 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["Really?’ and almost immediately called for a candle, and went to bed, as if he were not quite safe anywhere else.", "\"정말 그러신가요, 손님? 정말요?\" 그러더니 거의 곧장 촛불을 청해서는, 다른 데서는 도무지 안심이 안 된다는 듯 잠자리에 들었다."], ["He did not actually stagger under the negus; but I should think his placid little pulse must have made two or three more beats in a minute, than it had done since the great night of my aunt’s disappointment, when she struck at him with her bonnet.", "그가 니거스에 정말로 비틀거린 것은 아니었다. 하지만 짐작건대, 고모님이 보닛으로 그를 후려쳤던 그 크나큰 실망의 밤 이래로, 그의 평온하고 자그마한 맥박이 일 분에 두세 번쯤은 더 뛰었으리라."]], [["Thoroughly tired, I went to bed too, at midnight; passed the next day on the Dover coach; burst safe and sound into my aunt’s old parlour while she was at tea (she wore spectacles now); and was received by her, and Mr.", "나도 녹초가 되어 자정에 잠자리에 들었다. 이튿날은 도버행 마차에서 보냈고, 고모님이 차를 들고 계실 때(이젠 안경을 쓰고 계셨다) 그 옛 거실로 무사히 멀쩡하게 뛰어들었다."], ["Dick, and dear old Peggotty, who acted as housekeeper, with open arms and tears of joy.", "그러자 고모님과 딕 씨, 그리고 가정부 노릇을 하고 있던 정다운 패거티가 두 팔을 활짝 벌리고 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 나를 맞아 주었다."], ["My aunt was mightily amused, when we began to talk composedly, by my account of my meeting with Mr. Chillip, and of his holding her in such dread remembrance; and both she and Peggotty had a great deal to say about my poor mother’s second husband, and ‘that murdering woman of a sister’,--on whom I think no pain or penalty would have induced my aunt to bestow any Christian or Proper Name, or any other designation.", "우리가 차분히 이야기를 나누기 시작했을 때, 고모님은 내가 칠립 선생님과 마주친 이야기며 그가 고모님을 그토록 두려운 기억으로 간직하고 있다는 이야기에 무척 즐거워하셨다. 그리고 고모님도 패거티도, 내 가엾은 어머니의 두 번째 남편이며 '그 살인자 같은 누이년'에 대해 할 말이 무척 많았다 — 그 누이로 말하자면, 그 어떤 고통이나 형벌을 들이댄들 고모님이 결코 기독교식 이름이든 제대로 된 이름이든, 아니 그 어떤 호칭이든 붙여 줄 리 없었을 거라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["# CHAPTER 60. AGNES", "# CHAPTER 60. AGNES"]], [["My aunt and I, when we were left alone, talked far into the night.", "이모와 나는 단둘이 남게 되자 밤이 깊도록 이야기를 나누었다."], ["How the emigrants never wrote home, otherwise than cheerfully and hopefully; how Mr. Micawber had actually remitted divers small sums of money, on account of those ‘pecuniary liabilities’, in reference to which he had been so business-like as between man and man; how Janet, returning into my aunt’s service when she came back to Dover, had finally carried out her renunciation of mankind by entering into wedlock with a thriving tavern-keeper; and how my aunt had finally set her seal on the same great principle, by aiding and abetting the bride, and crowning the marriage-ceremony with her presence; were among our topics--already more or less familiar to me through the letters I had had. Mr.", "이민을 떠난 사람들이 본국으로 편지를 보낼 때면 한결같이 밝고 희망찬 내용만 적어 보냈다는 것, 미코버 씨가 사람과 사람 사이의 거래답게 그토록 격식을 차리며 입에 올리던 그 '금전상의 채무' 명목으로 실제로 자잘한 액수의 돈을 여러 차례 송금해 왔다는 것, 도버로 돌아온 제닛이 다시 이모의 시중을 들다가 마침내 잘나가는 선술집 주인과 혼인함으로써 사내라는 족속을 영영 끊겠다던 선언을 실행에 옮겼다는 것, 그리고 이모 또한 그 신부를 거들고 부추기며 결혼식에 직접 참석해 식을 빛내 줌으로써 그 거룩한 신조에 마지막으로 자기 도장을 찍었다는 것 — 이런 것들이 우리가 나눈 화제였는데, 그동안 받은 편지를 통해 나는 이미 어지간히 알고 있던 이야기였다."], ["Dick, as usual, was not forgotten.", "늘 그렇듯 딕 씨도 빠지지 않았다."], ["My aunt informed me how he incessantly occupied himself in copying everything he could lay his hands on, and kept King Charles the First at a respectful distance by that semblance of employment; how it was one of the main joys and rewards of her life that he was free and happy, instead of pining in monotonous restraint; and how (as a novel general conclusion) nobody but she could ever fully know what he was.", "이모는 딕 씨가 손에 잡히는 것이라면 무엇이든 끊임없이 베껴 적는 일에 몰두하면서, 그 일하는 시늉으로 찰스 일세 왕을 점잖은 거리 밖에 붙들어 둔다는 것, 단조로운 속박 속에서 시들어 가는 대신 자유롭고 행복하게 지내는 것이 이모의 삶에서 가장 큰 기쁨이자 보람 가운데 하나라는 것, 그리고 (새삼스러운 결론이지만) 딕 씨가 어떤 사람인지를 온전히 알아볼 수 있는 사람은 이모 자신밖에 없다는 것을 알려 주었다."]], [["‘And when, Trot,’ said my aunt, patting the back of my hand, as we sat in our old way before the fire, ‘when are you going over to Canterbury?’", "\"그런데 말이다, 트롯,\" 우리가 옛날처럼 난롯가에 앉아 있는데 이모가 내 손등을 토닥이며 말했다. \"넌 언제 캔터베리에 가 볼 작정이냐?\""]], [["‘I shall get a horse, and ride over tomorrow morning, aunt, unless you will go with me?’", "\"말을 한 마리 빌려서 내일 아침에 타고 가 보려고요, 이모. 이모가 저랑 같이 가실 게 아니라면요.\""]], [["‘No!’ said my aunt, in her short abrupt way.", "\"싫다!\" 이모가 특유의 짧고 퉁명스러운 투로 말했다."], ["‘I mean to stay where I am.’", "\"난 그냥 여기 있을란다.\""]], [["Then, I should ride, I said.", "그러면 말을 타고 가겠노라고 나는 말했다."], ["I could not have come through Canterbury today without stopping, if I had been coming to anyone but her.", "이모가 아닌 다른 사람을 만나러 가는 길이었다면 오늘 캔터베리를 지나면서 그냥 지나칠 수는 없었을 것이라고 했다."]], [["She was pleased, but answered, ‘Tut, Trot; MY old bones would have kept till tomorrow!’ and softly patted my hand again, as I sat looking thoughtfully at the fire.", "이모는 흐뭇해하면서도 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"쯧, 트롯. 이 늙은 뼈다귀가 내일까지야 못 버티겠니!\" 그러고는 내가 생각에 잠긴 채 난롯불을 바라보고 앉아 있는 동안 다시 한번 내 손을 가만히 토닥여 주었다."]], [["Thoughtfully, for I could not be here once more, and so near Agnes, without the revival of those regrets with which I had so long been occupied.", "생각에 잠겼다고 한 것은, 내가 이곳에 다시 와서 애그니스와 이토록 가까이 있으면서, 그토록 오래 마음을 짓눌러 온 그 회한이 되살아나지 않을 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["Softened regrets they might be, teaching me what I had failed to learn when my younger life was all before me, but not the less regrets.", "어린 시절이 송두리째 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있을 때 미처 배우지 못했던 것을 뒤늦게 깨우쳐 주는, 한결 누그러진 회한이었을지 모르나, 그래도 회한은 회한이었다. \"아, 트롯,\""], ["‘Oh, Trot,’ I seemed to hear my aunt say once more; and I understood her better now--‘Blind, blind, blind!’", "이모가 다시 한번 내게 말하는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 이제는 그 뜻을 전보다 더 잘 알 수 있었다 — \"눈먼 것아, 눈먼 것아, 눈먼 것아!\""]], [["We both kept silence for some minutes.", "우리 둘은 몇 분 동안 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["When I raised my eyes, I found that she was steadily observant of me.", "내가 눈을 들었을 때, 이모가 나를 가만히 지켜보고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Perhaps she had followed the current of my mind; for it seemed to me an easy one to track now, wilful as it had been once.", "어쩌면 이모는 내 마음의 흐름을 좇고 있었는지도 모른다. 한때는 그토록 제멋대로였던 그 마음도 이제는 따라가기 쉬워 보였으니 말이다."]], [["‘You will find her father a white-haired old man,’ said my aunt, ‘though a better man in all other respects--a reclaimed man.", "\"가 보면 그 애 아버지가 백발노인이 되어 있을 게다.\" 이모가 말했다. \"그 밖의 모든 점에서는 더 나은 사람이 됐지만 말이야 — 새사람이 됐지."], ["Neither will you find him measuring all human interests, and joys, and sorrows, with his one poor little inch-rule now.", "그리고 이제는 인간의 온갖 관심사며 기쁨이며 슬픔을, 그 가엾고 작은 한 치짜리 자로 죄다 재 보려 들지도 않을 거다."], ["Trust me, child, such things must shrink very much, before they can be measured off in that way.’", "내 말 믿으렴, 얘야, 그런 것들을 그런 식으로 잴 수 있으려면 아주 많이 쪼그라들어야 하는 법이거든.\""]], [["‘Indeed they must,’ said I.", "\"정말 그렇겠지요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘You will find her,’ pursued my aunt, ‘as good, as beautiful, as earnest, as disinterested, as she has always been.", "\"그 애를 보면,\" 이모가 말을 이었다. \"언제나 그래 왔듯이 착하고, 아름답고, 진실하고, 사심 없는 모습 그대로일 게다."], ["If I knew higher praise, Trot, I would bestow it on her.’", "트롯, 내가 그보다 더 높은 칭찬을 알았더라면, 그 애한테 다 갖다 바쳤을 거야.\""]], [["There was no higher praise for her; no higher reproach for me.", "그 애에게 그보다 더 높은 칭찬은 없었고, 나에게 그보다 더 큰 질책도 없었다."], ["Oh, how had I strayed so far away!", "아, 나는 어쩌다가 그렇게 멀리 헤매고 다녔던가!"]], [["‘If she trains the young girls whom she has about her, to be like herself,’ said my aunt, earnest even to the filling of her eyes with tears, ‘Heaven knows, her life will be well employed!", "\"제 곁에 두고 가르치는 어린 계집애들을 자기처럼 길러 낸다면,\" 이모가 어찌나 간절한지 눈에 눈물까지 그렁그렁한 채로 말했다. \"하늘은 아실 게다, 그 애의 삶이 참으로 보람되게 쓰이리라는 걸!"], ["Useful and happy, as she said that day!", "그 애가 그날 말했던 대로, 쓸모 있고 행복하게 말이야!"], ["How could she be otherwise than useful and happy!’", "그 애가 쓸모 있고 행복하지 않을 도리가 어디 있겠니!\""]], [["‘Has Agnes any--’ I was thinking aloud, rather than speaking.", "\"애그니스한테 혹시—\" 나는 말을 한다기보다 소리 내어 생각하고 있었다."]], [["‘Well? Hey?", "\"왜? 응?"], ["Any what?’ said my aunt, sharply.", "혹시 뭐?\" 이모가 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["‘Any lover,’ said I.", "\"연인이라도 있나요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘A score,’ cried my aunt, with a kind of indignant pride.", "\"한 무더기지.\" 이모가 일종의 분개 어린 자랑스러움을 담아 외쳤다."], ["‘She might have married twenty times, my dear, since you have been gone!’", "\"네가 떠나 있는 동안에, 얘야, 그 애는 스무 번도 더 시집갈 수 있었을 거다!\""]], [["‘No doubt,’ said I.", "\"그렇겠지요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘No doubt.", "\"그렇고말고요."], ["But has she any lover who is worthy of her? Agnes could care for no other.’", "하지만 그 애한테 어울릴 만한 연인이 있느냐는 거예요. 애그니스라면 그만 못한 사람한테는 마음을 줄 수 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["My aunt sat musing for a little while, with her chin upon her hand. Slowly raising her eyes to mine, she said:", "이모는 턱을 손에 괸 채 잠시 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다. 그러더니 천천히 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보며 말했다."]], [["‘I suspect she has an attachment, Trot.’", "\"내 짐작엔 그 애가 마음을 둔 사람이 있는 것 같다, 트롯.\""]], [["‘A prosperous one?’ said I.", "\"잘되어 가는 사이인가요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Trot,’ returned my aunt gravely, ‘I can’t say.", "\"트롯,\" 이모가 무겁게 대꾸했다. \"나도 모르겠다."], ["I have no right to tell you even so much. She has never confided it to me, but I suspect it.’", "사실 이만큼 말할 자격도 내겐 없어. 그 애가 내게 털어놓은 적은 한 번도 없지만, 그저 짐작이 그렇다는 거야.\""]], [["She looked so attentively and anxiously at me (I even saw her tremble), that I felt now, more than ever, that she had followed my late thoughts.", "이모는 너무도 골똘하고 근심스럽게 나를 바라보아서 (나는 이모가 떠는 것까지 보았다), 이모가 조금 전 내 속마음을 좇고 있었다는 것을 이제 그 어느 때보다도 절실히 느꼈다."], ["I summoned all the resolutions I had made, in all those many days and nights, and all those many conflicts of my heart.", "나는 그 숱한 낮과 밤을 거치며, 그 숱한 마음속 갈등을 거치며 다져 온 결심을 모조리 끌어모았다."]], [["‘If it should be so,’ I began, ‘and I hope it is-’", "\"만약 정말 그렇다면,\" 내가 입을 떼었다. \"그리고 저는 그렇기를 바라는데—\""]], [["‘I don’t know that it is,’ said my aunt curtly.", "\"정말 그런지 어떤지 난 모른다.\" 이모가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["‘You must not be ruled by my suspicions.", "\"내 짐작에 휘둘리면 안 돼."], ["You must keep them secret.", "이건 너만 알고 있어야 한다."], ["They are very slight, perhaps.", "어쩌면 아주 어렴풋한 짐작일 뿐이야."], ["I have no right to speak.’", "나는 입을 놀릴 자격이 없어.\""]], [["‘If it should be so,’ I repeated, ‘Agnes will tell me at her own good time.", "\"만약 정말 그렇다면,\" 나는 거듭 말했다. \"애그니스가 때가 되면 제게 말해 줄 거예요."], ["A sister to whom I have confided so much, aunt, will not be reluctant to confide in me.’", "제가 그토록 많은 걸 털어놓은 누이라면, 이모, 저한테 마음을 털어놓기를 꺼리지는 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["My aunt withdrew her eyes from mine, as slowly as she had turned them upon me; and covered them thoughtfully with her hand.", "이모는 나에게 눈길을 돌렸던 때처럼 천천히 내게서 눈을 거두더니, 생각에 잠긴 채 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다."], ["By and by she put her other hand on my shoulder; and so we both sat, looking into the past, without saying another word, until we parted for the night.", "이윽고 다른 손을 내 어깨에 얹었다. 그렇게 우리 둘은 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않은 채, 그날 밤 헤어질 때까지 지난날을 들여다보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["I rode away, early in the morning, for the scene of my old school-days.", "나는 이른 아침에 말을 타고 옛 학창 시절의 무대로 떠났다."], ["I cannot say that I was yet quite happy, in the hope that I was gaining a victory over myself; even in the prospect of so soon looking on her face again.", "나 자신을 이겨 내고 있다는 희망 속에서도, 그렇게 곧 다시 그 애의 얼굴을 보게 되리라는 기대 앞에서도, 아직 마음이 온전히 행복하다고는 말할 수 없었다."]], [["The well-remembered ground was soon traversed, and I came into the quiet streets, where every stone was a boy’s book to me.", "낯익은 길은 금세 지나쳤고, 나는 돌멩이 하나하나가 내겐 한 소년의 이야기책이던 그 고요한 거리로 들어섰다."], ["I went on foot to the old house, and went away with a heart too full to enter.", "나는 걸어서 그 옛집으로 갔다가, 들어서기엔 가슴이 너무 벅차 발길을 돌렸다."], ["I returned; and looking, as I passed, through the low window of the turret-room where first Uriah Heep, and afterwards Mr. Micawber, had been wont to sit, saw that it was a little parlour now, and that there was no office.", "다시 돌아와, 지나는 길에 그 작은 탑 모양 방의 낮은 창문 너머를 들여다보았는데 — 처음에는 유라이아 힙이, 나중에는 미코버 씨가 늘 앉아 있던 그 방이었다 — 이제는 작은 거실이 되어 있고 사무실은 없었다."], ["Otherwise the staid old house was, as to its cleanliness and order, still just as it had been when I first saw it.", "그 밖에는, 차분하고 오래된 그 집이 깨끗하고 정돈된 모습 그대로, 내가 처음 보았을 때와 조금도 다름이 없었다."], ["I requested the new maid who admitted me, to tell Miss Wickfield that a gentleman who waited on her from a friend abroad, was there; and I was shown up the grave old staircase (cautioned of the steps I knew so well), into the unchanged drawing-room.", "나는 문을 열어 준 새 하녀에게, 외국에 있는 한 친구의 부탁으로 위크필드 양을 뵈러 온 신사가 와 있다고 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 그러자 나는 그 점잖고 오래된 계단을 따라 (내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 계단 단을 조심하라는 당부와 함께) 조금도 변하지 않은 응접실로 안내받았다."], ["The books that Agnes and I had read together, were on their shelves; and the desk where I had laboured at my lessons, many a night, stood yet at the same old corner of the table.", "애그니스와 내가 함께 읽던 책들이 그 선반에 그대로 꽂혀 있었고, 내가 여러 밤 동안 공부에 매달리던 책상도 여전히 탁자의 그 옛 모서리에 놓여 있었다."], ["All the little changes that had crept in when the Heeps were there, were changed again.", "힙 부자가 있던 동안 슬그머니 끼어들었던 사소한 변화들은 죄다 다시 제자리로 돌아와 있었다."], ["Everything was as it used to be, in the happy time.", "모든 것이 그 행복하던 시절 그대로였다."]], [["I stood in a window, and looked across the ancient street at the opposite houses, recalling how I had watched them on wet afternoons, when I first came there; and how I had used to speculate about the people who appeared at any of the windows, and had followed them with my eyes up and down stairs, while women went clicking along the pavement in pattens, and the dull rain fell in slanting lines, and poured out of the water-spout yonder, and flowed into the road.", "나는 창가에 서서, 오래된 거리 건너편 집들을 바라보며, 처음 이곳에 왔을 때 비 내리는 오후면 그 집들을 지켜보던 일을 떠올렸다. 어느 창문에든 누군가 나타나면 그 사람에 대해 이런저런 짐작을 해 보고, 그들이 계단을 오르내리는 모습을 눈으로 좇곤 했던 일을. 그러는 동안 여자들은 나막신을 딸깍거리며 보도를 걸어갔고, 흐린 비는 빗금을 그으며 내려, 저쪽 빗물받이 홈통에서 쏟아져 길바닥으로 흘러내렸다."], ["The feeling with which I used to watch the tramps, as they came into the town on those wet evenings, at dusk, and limped past, with their bundles drooping over their shoulders at the ends of sticks, came freshly back to me; fraught, as then, with the smell of damp earth, and wet leaves and briar, and the sensation of the very airs that blew upon me in my own toilsome journey.", "그 비 내리는 저녁이면, 땅거미가 질 무렵 떠돌이들이 마을로 들어와 막대 끝에 보따리를 매달아 어깨에 늘어뜨린 채 절뚝거리며 지나가는 모습을 지켜보던 그 느낌이, 그때처럼 축축한 흙냄새와 젖은 나뭇잎과 들장미 냄새를 가득 품고서, 그리고 내 고단한 여정길에 내 몸을 스치던 바로 그 바람의 감촉을 품고서, 생생하게 되살아났다."]], [["The opening of the little door in the panelled wall made me start and turn.", "판자벽에 난 작은 문이 열리는 바람에 나는 흠칫 놀라 돌아섰다."], ["Her beautiful serene eyes met mine as she came towards me.", "그 애가 내게로 다가오는데, 아름답고 잔잔한 두 눈이 내 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["She stopped and laid her hand upon her bosom, and I caught her in my arms.", "그 애는 걸음을 멈추고 한 손을 가슴에 얹었고, 나는 두 팔로 그 애를 끌어안았다."]], [["‘Agnes!", "\"애그니스!"], ["my dear girl! I have come too suddenly upon you.’", "사랑하는 내 누이! 너무 갑작스럽게 들이닥쳤구나.\""]], [["‘No, no! I am so rejoiced to see you, Trotwood!’", "\"아니야, 아니야! 널 보니 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라, 트롯우드!\""]], [["‘Dear Agnes, the happiness it is to me, to see you once again!’", "\"사랑하는 애그니스, 너를 다시 보게 되다니 내겐 정말 큰 행복이야!\""]], [["I folded her to my heart, and, for a little while, we were both silent.", "나는 그 애를 가슴에 끌어안았고, 우리 둘은 잠시 말이 없었다."], ["Presently we sat down, side by side; and her angel-face was turned upon me with the welcome I had dreamed of, waking and sleeping, for whole years.", "이윽고 우리는 나란히 자리에 앉았다. 천사 같은 그 얼굴이, 깨어 있을 때나 잠들어 있을 때나 몇 해 동안 꿈꿔 온 바로 그 환대를 담고 나를 향했다."]], [["She was so true, she was so beautiful, she was so good,--I owed her so much gratitude, she was so dear to me, that I could find no utterance for what I felt.", "그 애는 너무도 진실했고, 너무도 아름다웠고, 너무도 착했다 — 나는 그 애에게 그토록 많은 빚을 지고 있었고, 그 애가 내겐 너무도 소중해서, 내가 느끼는 것을 무어라 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다."], ["I tried to bless her, tried to thank her, tried to tell her (as I had often done in letters) what an influence she had upon me; but all my efforts were in vain.", "나는 그 애에게 축복을 빌어 보려 했고, 고마움을 전해 보려 했고, (편지로 자주 그랬듯이) 그 애가 내게 어떤 영향을 끼쳐 왔는지 말해 보려 했다. 그러나 내 온갖 애씀은 헛수고였다."], ["My love and joy were dumb.", "내 사랑도 내 기쁨도 입을 떼지 못했다."]], [["With her own sweet tranquillity, she calmed my agitation; led me back to the time of our parting; spoke to me of Emily, whom she had visited, in secret, many times; spoke to me tenderly of Dora’s grave.", "그 애는 특유의 다정한 차분함으로 내 동요를 가라앉혔다. 우리가 헤어지던 때로 나를 데려가, 여러 차례 남몰래 찾아가 만났다는 에밀리 이야기를 들려주었고, 도라의 무덤 이야기를 다정하게 꺼냈다."], ["With the unerring instinct of her noble heart, she touched the chords of my memory so softly and harmoniously, that not one jarred within me; I could listen to the sorrowful, distant music, and desire to shrink from nothing it awoke.", "그 고귀한 마음의 빗나감 없는 직감으로, 그 애는 내 기억의 현(絃)을 어찌나 부드럽고 조화롭게 어루만지던지, 내 안에서 거슬리게 울리는 음이 하나도 없었다. 나는 그 슬프고도 아득한 음악에 귀 기울이며, 그것이 불러일으키는 어떤 것에서도 움츠러들고 싶지 않았다."], ["How could I, when, blended with it all, was her dear self, the better angel of my life?", "그 모든 것에 어우러진 것이 바로 그 애 자신, 내 삶의 더 나은 천사였으니, 내가 어찌 움츠러들 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["‘And you, Agnes,’ I said, by and by.", "\"그런데 너는, 애그니스,\" 이윽고 내가 말했다."], ["‘Tell me of yourself.", "\"네 이야기를 좀 들려줘."], ["You have hardly ever told me of your own life, in all this lapse of time!’", "이렇게 오랜 세월이 흐르는 동안, 너는 네 삶에 대해선 거의 한마디도 안 했잖아!\""]], [["‘What should I tell?’ she answered, with her radiant smile.", "\"무슨 이야기를 하면 될까?\" 그 애가 환한 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["‘Papa is well.", "\"아버지는 잘 지내셔."], ["You see us here, quiet in our own home; our anxieties set at rest, our home restored to us; and knowing that, dear Trotwood, you know all.’", "보다시피 우리는 이렇게 우리 집에서 조용히 지내고 있어. 근심거리도 다 가라앉았고, 우리 집도 우리에게 되돌아왔지. 그것만 알면, 사랑하는 트롯우드, 넌 모든 걸 다 아는 셈이야.\""]], [["‘All, Agnes?’ said I.", "\"모든 걸 다, 애그니스?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["She looked at me, with some fluttering wonder in her face.", "그 애는 얼굴에 조금 떨리는 의아함을 띤 채 나를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Is there nothing else, Sister?’ I said.", "\"다른 건 정말 없어, 누이?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Her colour, which had just now faded, returned, and faded again.", "조금 전 가셨던 그 애의 핏기가 다시 돌아왔다가, 다시 가셨다."], ["She smiled; with a quiet sadness, I thought; and shook her head.", "그 애는 미소를 지었는데, 내 보기엔 어딘가 잔잔한 슬픔이 어린 미소였다. 그러고는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["I had sought to lead her to what my aunt had hinted at; for, sharply painful to me as it must be to receive that confidence, I was to discipline my heart, and do my duty to her.", "나는 이모가 넌지시 비친 그것으로 그 애를 이끌어 보려 했다. 그런 속내를 듣는 것이 내겐 견디기 힘들 만큼 아픈 일일지언정, 나는 내 마음을 다스리고 그 애에게 마땅한 도리를 다해야 했으니까."], ["I saw, however, that she was uneasy, and I let it pass.", "그러나 그 애가 불편해하는 것을 보고, 나는 그냥 넘어갔다."]], [["‘You have much to do, dear Agnes?’", "\"할 일이 많지, 사랑하는 애그니스?\""]], [["‘With my school?’ said she, looking up again, in all her bright composure.", "\"내 학교 말이니?\" 그 애가 환한 차분함을 그대로 간직한 채 다시 고개를 들며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"응."], ["It is laborious, is it not?’", "고된 일이잖아, 안 그래?\""]], [["‘The labour is so pleasant,’ she returned, ‘that it is scarcely grateful in me to call it by that name.’", "\"그 일이 너무 즐거워서,\" 그 애가 대답했다. \"고되다는 말로 부르는 게 오히려 고마움을 모르는 짓 같아.\""]], [["‘Nothing good is difficult to you,’ said I.", "\"너에게 좋은 일치고 어려운 일은 없으니까.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Her colour came and went once more; and once more, as she bent her head, I saw the same sad smile.", "그 애의 핏기가 다시 한번 돌았다 가셨다. 그리고 그 애가 고개를 숙이는데, 나는 또다시 그 슬픈 미소를 보았다."]], [["‘You will wait and see papa,’ said Agnes, cheerfully, ‘and pass the day with us?", "\"기다렸다가 아버지를 만나 봐.\" 애그니스가 밝게 말했다. \"그리고 우리랑 하루 보내고."], ["Perhaps you will sleep in your own room? We always call it yours.’", "어쩌면 네 방에서 자고 가도 되고? 우린 늘 그 방을 네 방이라고 불러.\""]], [["I could not do that, having promised to ride back to my aunt’s at night; but I would pass the day there, joyfully.", "밤이 되면 이모 댁으로 말을 타고 돌아가겠다고 약속한 터라 그럴 수는 없었다. 그러나 하루를 거기서 보내는 것은 기꺼이 그러겠노라고 했다."]], [["‘I must be a prisoner for a little while,’ said Agnes, ‘but here are the old books, Trotwood, and the old music.’", "\"난 잠시 갇힌 몸 신세가 되어야 해.\" 애그니스가 말했다. \"하지만 여기 옛 책들이 있잖아, 트롯우드, 옛 악보도 있고.\""]], [["‘Even the old flowers are here,’ said I, looking round; ‘or the old kinds.’", "\"옛 꽃들까지 다 여기 있네.\" 나는 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. \"아니면 그 옛날과 같은 종류든지.\""]], [["‘I have found a pleasure,’ returned Agnes, smiling, ‘while you have been absent, in keeping everything as it used to be when we were children.", "\"내 즐거움을 하나 찾았어.\" 애그니스가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"네가 떠나 있는 동안, 우리가 어린아이였을 적 그대로 모든 걸 간직해 두는 일 말이야."], ["For we were very happy then, I think.’", "그때 우린 참 행복했던 것 같거든.\""]], [["‘Heaven knows we were!’ said I.", "\"하늘이 아실 거야, 우리가 그랬다는 걸!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘And every little thing that has reminded me of my brother,’ said Agnes, with her cordial eyes turned cheerfully upon me, ‘has been a welcome companion.", "\"그리고 내 오라비를 떠올리게 하는 사소한 것 하나하나가,\" 애그니스가 다정한 두 눈을 환하게 내게로 돌리며 말했다. \"반가운 벗이 되어 주었어."], ["Even this,’ showing me the basket-trifle, full of keys, still hanging at her side, ‘seems to jingle a kind of old tune!’", "이것까지도 말이야.\" 그러면서 여전히 옆구리에 매달려 있는, 열쇠가 가득한 작은 바구니를 내게 보여 주었다. \"어쩐지 옛 가락 같은 걸 짤랑짤랑 울리는 것 같지 뭐야!\""]], [["She smiled again, and went out at the door by which she had come.", "그 애는 다시 미소 짓더니, 들어왔던 그 문으로 나갔다."]], [["It was for me to guard this sisterly affection with religious care.", "이 누이로서의 정을 종교처럼 정성껏 지키는 것이 내가 할 일이었다."], ["It was all that I had left myself, and it was a treasure.", "그것은 내가 나 자신에게 남겨 둔 전부였고, 하나의 보물이었다."], ["If I once shook the foundations of the sacred confidence and usage, in virtue of which it was given to me, it was lost, and could never be recovered. I set this steadily before myself.", "그것이 내게 주어지게 한 그 거룩한 신뢰와 관계의 기틀을 내가 한 번이라도 흔든다면, 그것은 잃어버린 것이 되어 영영 되찾을 수 없으리라. 나는 이것을 흔들림 없이 내 앞에 세워 두었다."], ["The better I loved her, the more it behoved me never to forget it.", "그 애를 사랑하면 할수록, 나는 더더욱 이것을 잊지 말아야 했다."]], [["I walked through the streets; and, once more seeing my old adversary the butcher--now a constable, with his staff hanging up in the shop--went down to look at the place where I had fought him; and there meditated on Miss Shepherd and the eldest Miss Larkins, and all the idle loves and likings, and dislikings, of that time.", "나는 거리를 걸었다. 그러다가 내 옛 원수였던 푸줏간 사내를 다시 보았는데 — 이제는 경관이 되어, 가게에 곤봉을 걸어 두고 있었다 — 그와 한판 붙었던 곳으로 내려가 보았다. 거기서 셰퍼드 양과 큰 라킨스 양, 그리고 그 시절의 부질없던 온갖 사랑과 호감과 반감을 곰곰이 떠올렸다."], ["Nothing seemed to have survived that time but Agnes; and she, ever a star above me, was brighter and higher.", "그 시절에서 살아남은 것은 애그니스밖에 없는 듯했다. 언제나 내 머리 위의 별이던 그 애는, 더 밝고 더 높이 떠 있었다."]], [["When I returned, Mr. Wickfield had come home, from a garden he had, a couple of miles or so out of town, where he now employed himself almost every day.", "내가 돌아왔을 때, 위크필드 씨는 마을에서 한두 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 있는 자기 정원에서 집으로 돌아와 있었다. 그는 요즘 거의 매일 그 정원 일에 몰두하고 있었다."], ["I found him as my aunt had described him.", "나는 그가 이모가 묘사한 그대로의 모습임을 알았다."], ["We sat down to dinner, with some half-dozen little girls; and he seemed but the shadow of his handsome picture on the wall.", "우리는 예닐곱 명쯤 되는 어린 계집애들과 함께 식탁에 둘러앉았다. 그는 벽에 걸린 그 잘생긴 초상화의 그림자에 지나지 않아 보였다."]], [["The tranquillity and peace belonging, of old, to that quiet ground in my memory, pervaded it again.", "내 기억 속 그 고요한 터에 예전부터 깃들어 있던 잔잔함과 평화가, 다시 그곳을 가득 채웠다."], ["When dinner was done, Mr. Wickfield taking no wine, and I desiring none, we went up-stairs; where Agnes and her little charges sang and played, and worked.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자, 위크필드 씨는 술을 들지 않았고 나도 마시고 싶지 않아서, 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다. 그곳에서 애그니스와 그 애가 돌보는 어린 계집애들이 노래하고 연주하고 바느질을 했다."], ["After tea the children left us; and we three sat together, talking of the bygone days.", "차를 마신 뒤 아이들은 우리 곁을 떠났고, 우리 셋은 함께 앉아 지나간 날들에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["‘My part in them,’ said Mr.", "\"그 시절 내가 맡은 몫에는,\""], ["Wickfield, shaking his white head, ‘has much matter for regret--for deep regret, and deep contrition, Trotwood, you well know.", "위크필드 씨가 백발을 가로저으며 말했다. \"후회할 거리가 참 많네 — 깊은 후회, 깊은 뉘우침이 말이야, 트롯우드, 자네도 잘 알다시피."], ["But I would not cancel it, if it were in my power.’", "하지만 그걸 내 힘으로 지울 수 있다 해도, 나는 지우지 않으련다.\""]], [["I could readily believe that, looking at the face beside him.", "그 곁에 있는 얼굴을 바라보니, 그 말이 쉬이 믿어졌다."]], [["‘I should cancel with it,’ he pursued, ‘such patience and devotion, such fidelity, such a child’s love, as I must not forget, no!", "\"그것을 지운다면,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그토록 한결같은 인내와 헌신, 그토록 변함없는 충실함, 그토록 지극한 자식의 사랑까지 함께 지우는 셈이 될 테니 말이야."], ["even to forget myself.’", "그건 잊어서는 안 되지, 안 되고말고! 내 자신을 잊는 한이 있더라도.\""]], [["‘I understand you, sir,’ I softly said.", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 알겠습니다, 어르신.\" 나는 부드럽게 말했다."], ["‘I hold it--I have always held it--in veneration.’", "\"저는 그것을 — 언제나 그것을 — 우러르고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘But no one knows, not even you,’ he returned, ‘how much she has done, how much she has undergone, how hard she has striven.", "\"하지만 아무도 모른다네, 자네조차도,\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"저 애가 얼마나 많은 일을 해냈는지, 얼마나 많은 걸 견뎌 냈는지, 얼마나 모질게 애써 왔는지를 말이야."], ["Dear Agnes!’", "사랑하는 애그니스!\""]], [["She had put her hand entreatingly on his arm, to stop him; and was very, very pale.", "그 애는 그를 멈추게 하려고 애원하듯 그의 팔에 손을 얹고 있었다. 그리고 새파랗게, 너무도 새파랗게 질려 있었다."]], [["‘Well, well!’ he said with a sigh, dismissing, as I then saw, some trial she had borne, or was yet to bear, in connexion with what my aunt had told me. ‘Well!", "\"그래, 그래!\" 그가 한숨을 내쉬며 말했는데, 그제야 나는 알았다, 그가 이모에게 들은 그 일과 관련해 그 애가 이미 견뎌 냈거나 아직 견뎌야 할 어떤 시련을 떨쳐 버리고 있다는 것을. \"그래!"], ["I have never told you, Trotwood, of her mother. Has anyone?’", "트롯우드, 나는 자네한테 저 애 어미 이야기를 한 적이 없지. 누가 한 적이 있던가?\""]], [["‘Never, sir.’", "\"없습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["‘It’s not much--though it was much to suffer.", "\"별것 아니야 — 비록 그 고통은 컸다만."], ["She married me in opposition to her father’s wish, and he renounced her.", "그 사람은 자기 아버지의 뜻을 거스르고 나와 결혼했고, 아버지는 그 사람을 내쳤다네."], ["She prayed him to forgive her, before my Agnes came into this world.", "그 사람은 내 애그니스가 이 세상에 나오기 전에, 아버지에게 용서를 빌었어."], ["He was a very hard man, and her mother had long been dead.", "그는 무척 모진 사람이었고, 그 사람의 어머니는 진작에 세상을 떠난 뒤였지."], ["He repulsed her.", "그는 딸을 뿌리쳤어."], ["He broke her heart.’", "딸의 가슴을 찢어 놓았지.\""]], [["Agnes leaned upon his shoulder, and stole her arm about his neck.", "애그니스는 그의 어깨에 기대어, 가만히 한 팔을 그의 목에 둘렀다."]], [["‘She had an affectionate and gentle heart,’ he said; ‘and it was broken.", "\"그 사람은 정 많고 다정한 마음을 지녔는데,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 마음이 찢어진 거야."], ["I knew its tender nature very well.", "나는 그 여린 천성을 아주 잘 알고 있었네."], ["No one could, if I did not.", "내가 모른다면 아무도 알 수 없었지."], ["She loved me dearly, but was never happy.", "그 사람은 나를 끔찍이 사랑했지만, 한 번도 행복하지 못했어."], ["She was always labouring, in secret, under this distress; and being delicate and downcast at the time of his last repulse--for it was not the first, by many--pined away and died.", "늘 남몰래 이 괴로움에 시달렸지. 그러다가 아버지의 그 마지막 뿌리침을 당할 무렵 — 그게 처음도 아니었어, 몇 번이나 거듭된 일이었지 — 몸이 약하고 마음이 짓눌린 채로, 시들시들 야위어 가다가 세상을 떠났다네."], ["She left me Agnes, two weeks old; and the grey hair that you recollect me with, when you first came.’ He kissed Agnes on her cheek.", "그 사람은 두 주밖에 안 된 애그니스를 내게 남겼고, 자네가 처음 왔을 때 보았던 그 흰머리도 함께 남겼지.\" 그는 애그니스의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘My love for my dear child was a diseased love, but my mind was all unhealthy then.", "\"사랑하는 내 자식을 향한 내 사랑은 병든 사랑이었지만, 그 무렵 내 정신은 온통 병들어 있었으니까."], ["I say no more of that.", "그 이야긴 더 하지 않으마."], ["I am not speaking of myself, Trotwood, but of her mother, and of her.", "트롯우드, 나는 내 이야기를 하는 게 아니라, 저 애 어미와 저 애의 이야기를 하는 거야."], ["If I give you any clue to what I am, or to what I have been, you will unravel it, I know.", "내가 어떤 사람인지, 또는 어떤 사람이었는지에 대해 자네한테 실마리 하나만 던져 주면, 자네는 그걸 풀어낼 게야, 나는 알고 있어."], ["What Agnes is, I need not say.", "애그니스가 어떤 아이인지는 굳이 말할 것도 없지."], ["I have always read something of her poor mother’s story, in her character; and so I tell it you tonight, when we three are again together, after such great changes.", "나는 늘 저 애의 됨됨이에서 그 가엾은 어미의 사연을 어느 정도 읽어 왔다네. 그래서 이렇게 큰 변화들을 다 겪고 우리 셋이 다시 함께 모인 오늘 밤, 자네에게 그 이야기를 들려주는 걸세."], ["I have told it all.’", "이제 다 말했네.\""]], [["His bowed head, and her angel-face and filial duty, derived a more pathetic meaning from it than they had had before.", "수그린 그의 머리와, 그 애의 천사 같은 얼굴과 자식의 효성은, 그 이야기로 말미암아 예전에는 없던 한층 더 애틋한 의미를 띠게 되었다."], ["If I had wanted anything by which to mark this night of our re-union, I should have found it in this.", "우리가 재회한 이 밤을 표시할 무언가가 내게 필요했다면, 나는 바로 이 안에서 그것을 찾았을 것이다."]], [["Agnes rose up from her father’s side, before long; and going softly to her piano, played some of the old airs to which we had often listened in that place.", "이윽고 애그니스는 아버지 곁에서 일어나, 가만히 자기 피아노로 가더니, 그곳에서 우리가 자주 귀 기울이곤 했던 옛 곡들을 몇 곡 연주했다."]], [["‘Have you any intention of going away again?’ Agnes asked me, as I was standing by.", "\"또 어디 멀리 떠날 생각이 있는 거야?\" 내가 곁에 서 있는데 애그니스가 물었다."]], [["‘What does my sister say to that?’", "\"내 누이는 그 일을 어떻게 생각하는데?\""]], [["‘I hope not.’", "\"안 그러길 바라.\""]], [["‘Then I have no such intention, Agnes.’", "\"그렇다면 나도 그럴 생각은 없어, 애그니스.\""]], [["‘I think you ought not, Trotwood, since you ask me,’ she said, mildly.", "\"네가 물으니 하는 말인데, 너는 떠나지 말아야 한다고 생각해, 트롯우드.\" 그 애가 부드럽게 말했다. \"날로 높아지는 네 명성과 성공은 네가 좋은 일을 할 힘을 더 크게 해 주잖아."], ["‘Your growing reputation and success enlarge your power of doing good; and if I could spare my brother,’ with her eyes upon me, ‘perhaps the time could not.’", "그리고 설령 내가 내 오라비를 떠나보낼 수 있다 해도,\" 두 눈을 내게 둔 채로 말했다. \"아마 세상은 너를 떠나보낼 수 없을 거야.\""]], [["‘What I am, you have made me, Agnes.", "\"지금의 나라는 사람은 네가 만든 거야, 애그니스."], ["You should know best.’", "그러니 네가 제일 잘 알겠지.\""]], [["‘I made you, Trotwood?’", "\"내가 너를 만들었다고, 트롯우드?\""]], [["‘Yes!", "\"그래!"], ["Agnes, my dear girl!’ I said, bending over her.", "애그니스, 사랑하는 내 누이!\" 나는 그 애에게 몸을 숙이며 말했다."], ["‘I tried to tell you, when we met today, something that has been in my thoughts since Dora died.", "\"오늘 만났을 때 너에게 하려던 말이 있어, 도라가 죽은 뒤로 줄곧 내 마음속에 자리 잡고 있던 거야."], ["You remember, when you came down to me in our little room--pointing upward, Agnes?’", "기억하니, 네가 우리 작은 방으로 내게 내려와서 — 위를 가리키던 거, 애그니스?\""]], [["‘Oh, Trotwood!’ she returned, her eyes filled with tears.", "\"아, 트롯우드!\" 그 애가 두 눈에 눈물을 가득 담은 채 대답했다."], ["‘So loving, so confiding, and so young!", "\"그토록 다정하고, 그토록 믿음직스럽고, 그토록 어렸지!"], ["Can I ever forget?’", "내가 그걸 어떻게 잊을 수 있겠어?\""]], [["‘As you were then, my sister, I have often thought since, you have ever been to me.", "\"그때의 네 모습 그대로, 나는 그 뒤로 자주 생각해 왔는데, 너는 내게 늘 그런 존재였어."], ["Ever pointing upward, Agnes; ever leading me to something better; ever directing me to higher things!’", "언제나 위를 가리키며, 애그니스. 언제나 나를 더 나은 것으로 이끌며. 언제나 나를 더 높은 것으로 향하게 하며!\""]], [["She only shook her head; through her tears I saw the same sad quiet smile.", "그 애는 그저 고개를 가로저었다. 그 애의 눈물 너머로 나는 또다시 그 슬프고 잔잔한 미소를 보았다."]], [["‘And I am so grateful to you for it, Agnes, so bound to you, that there is no name for the affection of my heart.", "\"그리고 나는 그 일이 너무 고마워, 애그니스, 너에게 너무 매여 있어서, 내 마음속 이 정에는 붙일 이름조차 없어."], ["I want you to know, yet don’t know how to tell you, that all my life long I shall look up to you, and be guided by you, as I have been through the darkness that is past.", "나는 네가 알아주었으면 해, 어떻게 말해야 할지는 모르겠지만, 내 평생토록 나는 너를 우러를 거라는 걸, 그리고 지나간 그 어둠 속을 헤쳐 오는 동안 그래 왔듯이 너의 인도를 받을 거라는 걸 말이야."], ["Whatever betides, whatever new ties you may form, whatever changes may come between us, I shall always look to you, and love you, as I do now, and have always done.", "무슨 일이 닥치든, 네가 어떤 새로운 인연을 맺든, 우리 사이에 어떤 변화가 끼어들든, 나는 지금처럼, 그리고 늘 그래 왔듯이, 언제나 너를 바라보고 너를 사랑할 거야."], ["You will always be my solace and resource, as you have always been.", "너는 늘 그래 왔듯이 언제나 내 위안이자 의지처가 되어 줄 거야."], ["Until I die, my dearest sister, I shall see you always before me, pointing upward!’", "내가 죽는 날까지, 사랑하는 내 누이여, 나는 언제나 내 앞에서 위를 가리키는 너를 보게 될 거야!\""]], [["She put her hand in mine, and told me she was proud of me, and of what I said; although I praised her very far beyond her worth.", "그 애는 내 손에 자기 손을 얹고는, 내가 그 애를 그 진가보다 한참 지나치게 추켜세우긴 했지만, 그래도 내가 자랑스럽고 내가 한 말이 자랑스럽다고 했다."], ["Then she went on softly playing, but without removing her eyes from me.", "그러고는 나에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 다시 가만히 연주를 이어 갔다."], ["‘Do you know, what I have heard tonight, Agnes,’ said I, ‘strangely seems to be a part of the feeling with which I regarded you when I saw you first--with which I sat beside you in my rough school-days?’", "\"있잖아, 애그니스, 오늘 밤 내가 들은 이야기 말이야,\" 내가 말했다. \"이상하게도 내가 너를 처음 보았을 때 너를 바라보던 그 느낌의 한 자락인 것 같아 — 내가 그 거친 학창 시절에 네 곁에 앉아 있던 그때의 느낌 말이야.\""]], [["‘You knew I had no mother,’ she replied with a smile, ‘and felt kindly towards me.’", "\"내게 어머니가 없다는 걸 네가 알고 있었으니까,\" 그 애가 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"나한테 다정한 마음이 든 거지.\""]], [["‘More than that, Agnes, I knew, almost as if I had known this story, that there was something inexplicably gentle and softened, surrounding you; something that might have been sorrowful in someone else (as I can now understand it was), but was not so in you.’", "\"그것만이 아니야, 애그니스. 나는 거의 이 사연을 알고 있기라도 했던 것처럼, 너를 둘러싼 무언가 알 수 없이 부드럽고 누그러진 기운이 있다는 걸 알았어. 다른 사람에게라면 슬픔이 되었을 무언가가 (이제 그것이 슬픔이었다는 걸 알겠지만), 너에게서는 슬픔이 아니던 그런 것 말이야.\""]], [["She softly played on, looking at me still.", "그 애는 여전히 나를 바라보며 가만히 연주를 이어 갔다."]], [["‘Will you laugh at my cherishing such fancies, Agnes?’", "\"내가 이런 상상을 품고 있다고 비웃을 거니, 애그니스?\""]], [["‘No!’", "\"아니!\""]], [["‘Or at my saying that I really believe I felt, even then, that you could be faithfully affectionate against all discouragement, and never cease to be so, until you ceased to live?---Will you laugh at such a dream?’", "\"아니면 내가, 그때조차도 정말로 이렇게 느꼈다고 말하면 어떨까. 너라면 어떤 좌절 속에서도 변함없이 다정할 수 있고, 네 목숨이 다하는 그 순간까지 결코 그러기를 그치지 않으리라고 말이야 — 그런 꿈같은 소릴 비웃을 거니?\""]], [["‘Oh, no!", "\"아, 아니야!"], ["Oh, no!’", "아, 아니야!\""]], [["For an instant, a distressful shadow crossed her face; but, even in the start it gave me, it was gone; and she was playing on, and looking at me with her own calm smile.", "한순간, 괴로움이 어린 그림자가 그 애의 얼굴을 스쳤다. 그러나 그것이 내게 안긴 놀라움이 채 가시기도 전에 그림자는 사라졌고, 그 애는 다시 연주를 이어 가며 특유의 그 차분한 미소로 나를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["As I rode back in the lonely night, the wind going by me like a restless memory, I thought of this, and feared she was not happy.", "내가 적막한 밤길을 말을 타고 돌아오는 동안, 바람이 가만있지 못하는 기억처럼 내 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다. 나는 이 일을 곱씹으며, 그 애가 행복하지 않은 게 아닐까 두려워했다."], ["I was not happy; but, thus far, I had faithfully set the seal upon the Past, and, thinking of her, pointing upward, thought of her as pointing to that sky above me, where, in the mystery to come, I might yet love her with a love unknown on earth, and tell her what the strife had been within me when I loved her here.", "나도 행복하지 않았다. 그러나 여기까지, 나는 지난날에 충실히 도장을 찍어 마무리해 두었으며, 위를 가리키는 그 애를 떠올리며, 내 머리 위 저 하늘을 가리키는 모습으로 그 애를 그렸다. 다가올 그 신비 속에서, 나는 언젠가 이 땅에서는 알 수 없는 사랑으로 그 애를 사랑하고, 이곳에서 그 애를 사랑했을 때 내 안에서 어떤 싸움이 벌어졌는지를 그 애에게 말해 줄 수 있으리라."]], [["# CHAPTER 61. I AM SHOWN TWO INTERESTING PENITENTS", "# CHAPTER 61. I AM SHOWN TWO INTERESTING PENITENTS"]], [["For a time--at all events until my book should be completed, which would be the work of several months--I took up my abode in my aunt’s house at Dover; and there, sitting in the window from which I had looked out at the moon upon the sea, when that roof first gave me shelter, I quietly pursued my task.", "한동안 — 어쨌든 내 책이 완성될 때까지, 그것은 여러 달이 걸릴 일이었으나 — 나는 도버에 있는 고모님 댁에 거처를 정했다. 그리고 그 지붕이 처음으로 나를 품어 주었을 때 바다 위의 달을 내다보던 바로 그 창가에 앉아, 나는 조용히 내 일을 이어 나갔다."]], [["In pursuance of my intention of referring to my own fictions only when their course should incidentally connect itself with the progress of my story, I do not enter on the aspirations, the delights, anxieties, and triumphs of my art.", "내 소설은 그 흐름이 어쩌다 내 이야기의 진행과 맞물릴 때에만 언급하기로 한 뜻에 따라, 나는 내 예술의 포부며 환희, 불안, 승리에는 발을 들이지 않겠다."], ["That I truly devoted myself to it with my strongest earnestness, and bestowed upon it every energy of my soul, I have already said.", "내가 진정 온 마음을 다해 거기에 헌신했고, 내 영혼의 모든 기력을 거기에 쏟았다는 것은 이미 말했다."], ["If the books I have written be of any worth, they will supply the rest.", "내가 쓴 책들에 조금이라도 값어치가 있다면, 그 나머지는 그 책들이 채워 줄 것이다."], ["I shall otherwise have written to poor purpose, and the rest will be of interest to no one.", "그렇지 않다면 나는 헛되이 쓴 셈일 테고, 그 나머지는 아무에게도 흥미를 끌지 못할 것이다."]], [["Occasionally, I went to London; to lose myself in the swarm of life there, or to consult with Traddles on some business point.", "이따금 나는 런던에 갔다. 그곳 삶의 무리 속에 나를 묻으러 가거나, 아니면 어떤 사무적인 일을 트래들스와 의논하러 갔다."], ["He had managed for me, in my absence, with the soundest judgement; and my worldly affairs were prospering.", "그는 내가 자리를 비운 동안 더없이 건전한 판단으로 내 일을 처리해 주었고, 내 세상살이는 잘 풀려 가고 있었다."], ["As my notoriety began to bring upon me an enormous quantity of letters from people of whom I had no knowledge--chiefly about nothing, and extremely difficult to answer--I agreed with Traddles to have my name painted up on his door.", "내 이름이 알려지기 시작하면서 내가 전혀 모르는 사람들로부터 어마어마한 양의 편지가 쏟아져 들어오는데 — 거의 다 별것 아닌 일에다, 답하기는 몹시 까다로운 것들이었다 — 나는 트래들스와 합의해 그의 사무실 문에 내 이름을 써 붙이기로 했다."], ["There, the devoted postman on that beat delivered bushels of letters for me; and there, at intervals, I laboured through them, like a Home Secretary of State without the salary.", "거기로 그 구역을 도는 헌신적인 우편배달부가 내 앞으로 온 편지를 부셸 단위로 날라다 주었고, 거기서 나는 틈틈이, 봉급 없는 내무대신처럼 그 편지들과 씨름했다."]], [["Among this correspondence, there dropped in, every now and then, an obliging proposal from one of the numerous outsiders always lurking about the Commons, to practise under cover of my name (if I would take the necessary steps remaining to make a proctor of myself), and pay me a percentage on the profits.", "이런 편지들 가운데 이따금 한 통씩, 민사 법원 언저리를 늘 어슬렁거리는 수많은 부외자(部外者) 가운데 하나가 보내온 친절한 제안이 끼어 있곤 했다. 내가 변호사가 되기 위해 남은 절차만 밟아 준다면 내 이름을 간판 삼아 일을 하고, 그 수익의 일정 비율을 나에게 떼어 주겠다는 것이었다."], ["But I declined these offers; being already aware that there were plenty of such covert practitioners in existence, and considering the Commons quite bad enough, without my doing anything to make it worse.", "그러나 나는 이런 제안들을 거절했다. 이미 그런 식의 그림자 영업자들이 수두룩하다는 것을 알고 있었던 데다가, 민사 법원은 내가 굳이 더 망쳐 놓지 않아도 이미 충분히 형편없다고 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["The girls had gone home, when my name burst into bloom on Traddles’s door; and the sharp boy looked, all day, as if he had never heard of Sophy, shut up in a back room, glancing down from her work into a sooty little strip of garden with a pump in it.", "내 이름이 트래들스의 문에 활짝 피어났을 무렵, 그 집 처녀들은 이미 고향으로 돌아간 뒤였다. 약삭빠른 사환 아이는 온종일, 마치 소피라는 사람은 들어 본 적도 없다는 듯한 낯빛으로 굴었는데, 정작 소피는 뒷방에 틀어박혀 바느질을 하다가 이따금 펌프 하나가 놓인 그을음투성이 손바닥만 한 정원을 내려다보곤 했다."], ["But there I always found her, the same bright housewife; often humming her Devonshire ballads when no strange foot was coming up the stairs, and blunting the sharp boy in his official closet with melody.", "하지만 나는 거기서 늘 그녀를 보았다. 한결같이 밝은 살림꾼인 그녀를. 그녀는 낯선 발소리가 계단을 올라오지 않을 때면 곧잘 데번셔 민요를 흥얼거렸고, 그 가락으로 약삭빠른 사환 아이가 제 골방에서 부려 대는 날카로운 기세를 무디게 만들었다."]], [["I wondered, at first, why I so often found Sophy writing in a copy-book; and why she always shut it up when I appeared, and hurried it into the table-drawer.", "처음에 나는 왜 소피가 그렇게 자주 습자책에 글씨를 쓰고 있는지, 그리고 내가 나타나면 왜 늘 그것을 덮어 부랴부랴 탁자 서랍 속으로 밀어 넣는지 궁금했다."], ["But the secret soon came out.", "그러나 그 비밀은 곧 드러났다."], ["One day, Traddles (who had just come home through the drizzling sleet from Court) took a paper out of his desk, and asked me what I thought of that handwriting?", "어느 날 트래들스가 (진눈깨비를 맞으며 법정에서 막 돌아온 참이었다) 책상에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내더니, 이 필체를 어떻게 보느냐고 나에게 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, DON’T, Tom!’ cried Sophy, who was warming his slippers before the fire.", "\"어머, 안 돼요, 톰!\" 난롯불 앞에서 그의 슬리퍼를 데우고 있던 소피가 외쳤다."]], [["‘My dear,’ returned Tom, in a delighted state, ‘why not?", "\"여보,\" 톰이 기뻐 어쩔 줄 모르며 받았다."], ["What do you say to that writing, Copperfield?’", "\"왜 안 돼? 이 글씨 어떤가, 코퍼필드?\""]], [["‘It’s extraordinarily legal and formal,’ said I.", "\"굉장히 법률가답고 격식 있는데.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘I don’t think I ever saw such a stiff hand.’", "\"이렇게 딱딱한 필체는 본 적이 없는 것 같아.\""]], [["‘Not like a lady’s hand, is it?’ said Traddles.", "\"여자 손글씨 같지는 않지, 그렇지?\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘A lady’s!’ I repeated.", "\"여자라니!\" 나는 되받았다."], ["‘Bricks and mortar are more like a lady’s hand!’", "\"벽돌하고 회반죽이 차라리 여자 손글씨에 더 가깝겠네!\""]], [["Traddles broke into a rapturous laugh, and informed me that it was Sophy’s writing; that Sophy had vowed and declared he would need a copying-clerk soon, and she would be that clerk; that she had acquired this hand from a pattern; and that she could throw off--I forget how many folios an hour.", "트래들스는 황홀하게 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 그게 소피의 글씨라고 알려 주었다. 소피가 머잖아 그에게 필사 서기가 필요해질 거라고, 그러면 자기가 그 서기가 되겠노라고 굳게 맹세하고 다짐했다는 것, 그래서 본보기를 따라 이 필체를 익혔다는 것, 그리고 시간당 — 몇 절(folio)인지는 잊었지만 — 그만큼을 척척 베껴 낼 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["Sophy was very much confused by my being told all this, and said that when ‘Tom’ was made a judge he wouldn’t be so ready to proclaim it.", "소피는 이 모든 이야기를 내가 듣게 되자 몹시 부끄러워하면서, '톰'이 판사가 되면 이렇게 떠벌리지는 못할 거라고 말했다."], ["Which ‘Tom’ denied; averring that he should always be equally proud of it, under all circumstances.", "그러자 '톰'은 그것을 부인하며, 어떤 처지에 놓이든 늘 한결같이 그것을 자랑스러워할 거라고 단언했다."]], [["‘What a thoroughly good and charming wife she is, my dear Traddles!’ said I, when she had gone away, laughing.", "\"정말이지 더없이 착하고 사랑스러운 아내야, 트래들스.\" 그녀가 웃으며 자리를 뜬 뒤에 내가 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Copperfield,’ returned Traddles, ‘she is, without any exception, the dearest girl!", "\"이보게, 코퍼필드,\" 트래들스가 받았다. \"그녀는 둘도 없이, 세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 여자야!"], ["The way she manages this place; her punctuality, domestic knowledge, economy, and order; her cheerfulness, Copperfield!’", "이 집을 꾸려 가는 솜씨며, 그 빈틈없음, 살림 지식, 알뜰함, 그리고 정돈하는 솜씨 하며, 그 명랑함이라니, 코퍼필드!\""]], [["‘Indeed, you have reason to commend her!’ I returned.", "\"정말이지 자네가 그녀를 칭찬할 만하군!\" 내가 받았다."], ["‘You are a happy fellow.", "\"자네는 행복한 사람이야."], ["I believe you make yourselves, and each other, two of the happiest people in the world.’", "내 믿기로 자네들은 스스로를, 또 서로를, 세상에서 가장 행복한 두 사람으로 만들고 있어.\""]], [["‘I am sure we ARE two of the happiest people,’ returned Traddles.", "\"우리가 가장 행복한 두 사람인 건 분명해.\" 트래들스가 받았다."], ["‘I admit that, at all events.", "\"그건 어쨌든 나도 인정하네."], ["Bless my soul, when I see her getting up by candle-light on these dark mornings, busying herself in the day’s arrangements, going out to market before the clerks come into the Inn, caring for no weather, devising the most capital little dinners out of the plainest materials, making puddings and pies, keeping everything in its right place, always so neat and ornamental herself, sitting up at night with me if it’s ever so late, sweet-tempered and encouraging always, and all for me, I positively sometimes can’t believe it, Copperfield!’", "아이고, 이 어두컴컴한 아침마다 그녀가 촛불을 켜고 일어나 하루 일을 챙기느라 분주히 움직이고, 법학원 사무원들이 출근하기도 전에 장을 보러 나가고, 날씨 따위는 아랑곳 않고, 가장 수수한 재료로 더없이 근사한 자그마한 저녁을 궁리해 내고, 푸딩이며 파이를 만들고, 모든 것을 제자리에 두고, 늘 그렇게 단정하고 곱게 차려입고, 아무리 늦은 밤이라도 나와 함께 깨어 앉아 있고, 언제나 다정하고 북돋아 주며, 그 모든 게 다 나를 위한 것이라니, 정말이지 가끔은 믿기지가 않아, 코퍼필드!\""]], [["He was tender of the very slippers she had been warming, as he put them on, and stretched his feet enjoyingly upon the fender.", "그는 그녀가 데워 놓았던 바로 그 슬리퍼마저 애틋하게 여기며 발을 꿰더니, 흐뭇하게 두 발을 난로 가리개 위로 쭉 뻗었다."]], [["‘I positively sometimes can’t believe it,’ said Traddles.", "\"정말이지 가끔은 믿기지가 않아.\" 트래들스가 말했다."], ["‘Then our pleasures! Dear me, they are inexpensive, but they are quite wonderful!", "\"게다가 우리 낙(樂)이라니! 아이고, 돈은 거의 안 드는데 그게 어찌나 근사한지!"], ["When we are at home here, of an evening, and shut the outer door, and draw those curtains--which she made--where could we be more snug?", "우리가 저녁에 이렇게 집에 있으면서 바깥문을 닫고, 저 커튼을 — 저것도 그녀가 만든 거야 — 치면, 이보다 더 아늑한 데가 어디 있겠나?"], ["When it’s fine, and we go out for a walk in the evening, the streets abound in enjoyment for us.", "날이 좋아서 저녁에 산책하러 나가면, 거리는 우리에게 즐길 거리로 넘쳐 나. 우리는 보석상의 번쩍이는 진열창을 들여다보지."], ["We look into the glittering windows of the jewellers’ shops; and I show Sophy which of the diamond-eyed serpents, coiled up on white satin rising grounds, I would give her if I could afford it; and Sophy shows me which of the gold watches that are capped and jewelled and engine-turned, and possessed of the horizontal lever-escape-movement, and all sorts of things, she would buy for me if she could afford it; and we pick out the spoons and forks, fish-slices, butter-knives, and sugar-tongs, we should both prefer if we could both afford it; and really we go away as if we had got them!", "그러면 나는 소피에게, 형편만 된다면 사 주고 싶은 다이아몬드 눈을 박은 뱀 장신구가 — 하얀 새틴 받침 위에 똬리를 튼 것들 가운데 — 어느 것인지 가리켜 보여. 그러면 소피는 나에게, 자기가 형편만 된다면 사 주고 싶은 금시계가 — 뚜껑이 달리고 보석이 박히고 무늬가 새겨진 데다 수평 레버 탈진기에 온갖 장치가 든 것들 가운데 — 어느 것인지 가리켜 보여 주지. 그러고는 둘 다 형편만 된다면 둘 다 갖고 싶은 숟가락이며 포크, 생선 뒤집개, 버터 칼, 설탕 집게를 골라."], ["Then, when we stroll into the squares, and great streets, and see a house to let, sometimes we look up at it, and say, how would THAT do, if I was made a judge?", "그러다 정말로, 마치 그걸 다 손에 넣기라도 한 것처럼 흐뭇하게 자리를 떠! 그러다 광장이며 큰길로 어슬렁어슬렁 걷다가 세놓는다는 집이 보이면, 가끔 그걸 올려다보면서, 내가 판사가 된다면 저 집은 어떨까, 하고 말하지."], ["And we parcel it out--such a room for us, such rooms for the girls, and so forth; until we settle to our satisfaction that it would do, or it wouldn’t do, as the case may be.", "그러고는 그 집을 이리저리 나눠 보는 거야 — 우리 방은 이 방, 처녀들 방은 저 방, 그런 식으로 — 그러다 마침내 흡족하게 결론을 내려. 경우에 따라, 저 집이면 되겠다, 아니면 안 되겠다, 하고 말이야."], ["Sometimes, we go at half-price to the pit of the theatre--the very smell of which is cheap, in my opinion, at the money--and there we thoroughly enjoy the play: which Sophy believes every word of, and so do I.", "가끔은 반값에 극장 일등석 아래 평토간에 들어가는데 — 내 생각에는 그 냄새만으로도 그 돈값을 하고도 남아 — 거기서 우리는 연극을 마음껏 즐기지. 소피는 그 한마디 한마디를 다 곧이곧대로 믿고, 나도 그래."], ["In walking home, perhaps we buy a little bit of something at a cook’s-shop, or a little lobster at the fishmongers, and bring it here, and make a splendid supper, chatting about what we have seen.", "걸어서 집에 돌아오는 길에는, 어쩌면 음식점에서 뭘 조금 사거나 생선 가게에서 작은 바닷가재를 한 마리 사 와서, 여기서 근사한 야식을 차려 놓고, 우리가 본 것을 두고 도란도란 이야기를 나누지."], ["Now, you know, Copperfield, if I was Lord Chancellor, we couldn’t do this!’", "자, 보게, 코퍼필드, 내가 대법관이라면 이런 건 할 수가 없잖나!\""]], [["‘You would do something, whatever you were, my dear Traddles,’ thought I, ‘that would be pleasant and amiable.", "'자네가 무엇이 되든, 트래들스, 자네는 분명 무언가 즐겁고 정겨운 일을 해낼 사람이지.' 나는 속으로 생각했다."], ["And by the way,’ I said aloud, ‘I suppose you never draw any skeletons now?’", "\"그건 그렇고,\" 나는 소리 내어 말했다. \"이제 해골 그림 같은 건 안 그리겠지?\""]], [["‘Really,’ replied Traddles, laughing, and reddening, ‘I can’t wholly deny that I do, my dear Copperfield.", "\"실은,\" 트래들스가 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고 답했다. \"안 그린다고는 차마 못 하겠네, 코퍼필드."], ["For being in one of the back rows of the King’s Bench the other day, with a pen in my hand, the fancy came into my head to try how I had preserved that accomplishment.", "일전에 왕좌 재판소 뒷줄 어디쯤에 펜을 손에 쥐고 앉아 있다가, 그 솜씨를 아직 간직하고 있는지 한번 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 불쑥 들었거든."], ["And I am afraid there’s a skeleton--in a wig--on the ledge of the desk.’", "그래서 안됐지만, 책상 가장자리에 해골이 하나 — 가발을 쓴 채로 — 남아 있다네.\""]], [["After we had both laughed heartily, Traddles wound up by looking with a smile at the fire, and saying, in his forgiving way, ‘Old Creakle!’", "우리 둘 다 실컷 웃고 난 뒤, 트래들스는 난롯불을 미소 띤 눈으로 바라보며, 그 너그러운 말투로 이렇게 마무리했다. \"크리클 영감!\""]], [["‘I have a letter from that old--Rascal here,’ said I.", "\"여기 그 늙은 — 악당한테서 온 편지가 있어.\" 내가 말했다."], ["For I never was less disposed to forgive him the way he used to batter Traddles, than when I saw Traddles so ready to forgive him himself.", "트래들스가 그토록 선뜻 제 손으로 그를 용서하려는 것을 보니, 그가 트래들스를 그렇게 두들겨 패던 일을 용서할 마음이 그 어느 때보다도 들지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["‘From Creakle the schoolmaster?’ exclaimed Traddles. ‘No!’", "\"교장 크리클한테서?\" 트래들스가 외쳤다. \"설마!\""]], [["‘Among the persons who are attracted to me in my rising fame and fortune,’ said I, looking over my letters, ‘and who discover that they were always much attached to me, is the self-same Creakle.", "\"내 명성과 재산이 일어서면서 나에게 끌려드는 사람들, 그러니까 자기들은 늘 나를 무척 아꼈노라고 새삼 발견해 내는 사람들 가운데,\" 나는 편지를 훑어보며 말했다. \"바로 그 크리클이 끼어 있어."], ["He is not a schoolmaster now, Traddles. He is retired.", "그는 이제 교장이 아니야, 트래들스. 은퇴했어."], ["He is a Middlesex Magistrate.’", "미들섹스 치안판사가 됐지.\""]], [["I thought Traddles might be surprised to hear it, but he was not so at all.", "트래들스가 그 말에 놀랄 줄 알았는데, 그는 전혀 놀라지 않았다."]], [["‘How do you suppose he comes to be a Middlesex Magistrate?’ said I.", "\"그가 어쩌다 미들섹스 치안판사가 됐다고 생각해?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh dear me!’ replied Traddles, ‘it would be very difficult to answer that question.", "\"아이고 이 사람아!\" 트래들스가 답했다. \"그 물음에 답하기는 아주 어렵겠는걸."], ["Perhaps he voted for somebody, or lent money to somebody, or bought something of somebody, or otherwise obliged somebody, or jobbed for somebody, who knew somebody who got the lieutenant of the county to nominate him for the commission.’", "어쩌면 누군가에게 표를 줬거나, 누군가에게 돈을 빌려줬거나, 누군가한테 뭘 사 줬거나, 그게 아니면 누군가에게 신세를 갚게 해 줬거나, 누군가를 위해 뒷일을 봐줬겠지. 그 누군가가, 주(州) 부지사한테 그를 치안위원으로 천거하게 만든 또 다른 누군가를 알고 있었을 테고.\""]], [["‘On the commission he is, at any rate,’ said I.", "\"어쨌든 그가 치안위원직에 있는 건 사실이야.\""], ["‘And he writes to me here, that he will be glad to show me, in operation, the only true system of prison discipline; the only unchallengeable way of making sincere and lasting converts and penitents--which, you know, is by solitary confinement.", "내가 말했다. \"그가 여기 나에게 편지를 써 보냈는데, 단 하나뿐인 참된 감옥 규율 체계가 어떻게 돌아가는지를 기꺼이 나에게 보여 주고 싶다는군. 진실되고 영원한 회심자와 참회자를 만들어 내는, 누구도 토를 달 수 없는 유일한 방법 말이야 — 자네도 알다시피, 그건 독방 감금이지."], ["What do you say?’", "어떻게 생각해?\""]], [["‘To the system?’ inquired Traddles, looking grave.", "\"그 체계 말인가?\" 트래들스가 정색하며 물었다."]], [["‘No. To my accepting the offer, and your going with me?’", "\"아니. 내가 그 제안을 받아들이고, 자네가 나와 함께 가는 거 말이야.\""]], [["‘I don’t object,’ said Traddles.", "\"반대하지 않네.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Then I’ll write to say so.", "\"그럼 그렇게 하겠다고 답장을 쓰지."], ["You remember (to say nothing of our treatment) this same Creakle turning his son out of doors, I suppose, and the life he used to lead his wife and daughter?’", "자네도 기억하겠지 (우리가 받은 대접은 차치하고라도) 바로 그 크리클이 제 아들을 문밖으로 내쫓던 일, 그리고 제 아내와 딸을 어떤 식으로 살게 했는지를?\""]], [["‘Perfectly,’ said Traddles.", "\"똑똑히 기억하지.\" 트래들스가 말했다."]], [["‘Yet, if you’ll read his letter, you’ll find he is the tenderest of men to prisoners convicted of the whole calendar of felonies,’ said I; ‘though I can’t find that his tenderness extends to any other class of created beings.’", "\"그런데도 그의 편지를 읽어 보면, 그가 온갖 중죄 목록에 다 걸려 유죄 선고를 받은 죄수들에게는 더없이 인정 많은 사람이라는 걸 알게 될 거야.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 그 인정이 다른 어떤 부류의 피조물에게까지 미치는 흔적은 도무지 찾을 수가 없더군.\""]], [["Traddles shrugged his shoulders, and was not at all surprised.", "트래들스는 어깨를 으쓱했을 뿐, 조금도 놀라지 않았다."], ["I had not expected him to be, and was not surprised myself; or my observation of similar practical satires would have been but scanty.", "나는 그가 놀라리라 기대하지도 않았고, 나 자신도 놀라지 않았다. 그러지 않았다면 그런 식의 실생활 풍자를 내가 그리 많이 보아 오지 못했다는 뜻이 되었으리라."], ["We arranged the time of our visit, and I wrote accordingly to Mr. Creakle that evening.", "우리는 방문 시간을 잡았고, 나는 그날 저녁 그에 맞춰 크리클 씨에게 편지를 썼다."]], [["On the appointed day--I think it was the next day, but no matter--Traddles and I repaired to the prison where Mr. Creakle was powerful.", "정해진 날 — 아마 그 이튿날이었던 것 같은데, 아무래도 상관없다 — 트래들스와 나는 크리클 씨가 권세를 부리는 그 감옥으로 갔다."], ["It was an immense and solid building, erected at a vast expense.", "어마어마한 돈을 들여 지은, 거대하고 육중한 건물이었다."], ["I could not help thinking, as we approached the gate, what an uproar would have been made in the country, if any deluded man had proposed to spend one half the money it had cost, on the erection of an industrial school for the young, or a house of refuge for the deserving old.", "정문으로 다가가면서 나는 이런 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었다. 만약 어떤 얼빠진 사람이 이 건물에 든 돈의 절반만이라도 어린이를 위한 실업 학교나 마땅히 도움받을 자격이 있는 노인을 위한 보호소를 짓는 데 쓰자고 제안했다면, 이 나라에서 얼마나 큰 소동이 일었을까."]], [["In an office that might have been on the ground-floor of the Tower of Babel, it was so massively constructed, we were presented to our old schoolmaster; who was one of a group, composed of two or three of the busier sort of magistrates, and some visitors they had brought.", "바벨탑의 일 층쯤에나 있었을 법한, 그만큼 육중하게 지어진 사무실에서, 우리는 옛 교장 앞에 안내되었다. 그는 두세 명쯤 되는 좀 더 활동적인 부류의 치안판사들과, 그들이 데려온 방문객 몇 명으로 이루어진 무리 가운데 한 사람이었다."], ["He received me, like a man who had formed my mind in bygone years, and had always loved me tenderly.", "그는 지나간 세월 동안 내 정신을 빚어 왔고 늘 나를 애틋이 사랑해 온 사람인 양 나를 맞이했다."], ["On my introducing Traddles, Mr. Creakle expressed, in like manner, but in an inferior degree, that he had always been Traddles’s guide, philosopher, and friend.", "내가 트래들스를 소개하자, 크리클 씨는 같은 식으로, 다만 한 등급 덜한 정도로, 자기가 늘 트래들스의 길잡이요 철학자요 벗이었노라는 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["Our venerable instructor was a great deal older, and not improved in appearance.", "우리의 존경스러운 스승은 한참 더 늙었고, 외모는 나아진 데가 없었다."], ["His face was as fiery as ever; his eyes were as small, and rather deeper set.", "얼굴은 여전히 시뻘겋고, 두 눈은 여전히 작은 데다 전보다 좀 더 움푹 들어가 있었다."], ["The scanty, wet-looking grey hair, by which I remembered him, was almost gone; and the thick veins in his bald head were none the more agreeable to look at.", "내가 기억하는, 그 듬성듬성하고 축축해 보이던 잿빛 머리카락은 거의 다 사라졌고, 벗어진 머리에 굵게 도드라진 핏줄들은 보기에 조금도 나아진 데가 없었다."]], [["After some conversation among these gentlemen, from which I might have supposed that there was nothing in the world to be legitimately taken into account but the supreme comfort of prisoners, at any expense, and nothing on the wide earth to be done outside prison-doors, we began our inspection.", "이 신사들 사이에 오간 대화로 미루어 보자면, 이 세상에서 정당하게 따져 봐야 할 일이라고는 어떤 비용을 치르더라도 죄수들에게 더없는 안락을 베푸는 것뿐이고, 이 드넓은 땅 위에서 감옥 문 바깥에서 해야 할 일은 아무것도 없는 듯했다. 그런 대화가 오간 뒤에 우리는 시찰을 시작했다."], ["It being then just dinner-time, we went, first into the great kitchen, where every prisoner’s dinner was in course of being set out separately (to be handed to him in his cell), with the regularity and precision of clock-work.", "마침 저녁 식사 때여서, 우리는 먼저 커다란 부엌으로 들어갔다. 그곳에서는 죄수마다 (저마다 제 감방으로 가져다줄) 저녁이 따로따로, 시계 장치 같은 규칙성과 정확성으로 차려지고 있었다. 나는 트래들스에게 가만히 말했다."], ["I said aside, to Traddles, that I wondered whether it occurred to anybody, that there was a striking contrast between these plentiful repasts of choice quality, and the dinners, not to say of paupers, but of soldiers, sailors, labourers, the great bulk of the honest, working community; of whom not one man in five hundred ever dined half so well.", "이렇게 고급스러운 푸짐한 식사와, 빈민은 말할 것도 없고 군인이며 뱃사람, 막일꾼, 다시 말해 정직하게 일하는 공동체의 대다수가 먹는 식사 사이에 뚜렷한 대조가 있다는 걸 누구라도 떠올리기는 할까, 그들 가운데 이 절반만큼이라도 잘 먹는 사람은 오백 명에 하나도 없는데, 하고. 그러나 나는 그 '체계'가 잘 먹는 것을 요구한다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["But I learned that the ‘system’ required high living; and, in short, to dispose of the system, once for all, I found that on that head and on all others, ‘the system’ put an end to all doubts, and disposed of all anomalies.", "한마디로, 이 체계 이야기를 한 번에 끝내 두자면, 나는 이 문제에서나 다른 모든 문제에서나 '그 체계'가 모든 의심을 잠재우고 모든 모순을 처리해 버린다는 것을 알았다."], ["Nobody appeared to have the least idea that there was any other system, but THE system, to be considered.", "누구도 '그 체계' 말고 따져 봐야 할 다른 체계가 있다는 생각은 손톱만큼도 품고 있지 않은 듯했다."]], [["As we were going through some of the magnificent passages, I inquired of Mr. Creakle and his friends what were supposed to be the main advantages of this all-governing and universally over-riding system?", "웅장한 복도 몇 군데를 지나면서, 나는 크리클 씨와 그의 벗들에게, 이 모든 것을 다스리고 무엇이든 짓누르는 이 체계의 주된 이점이 무엇이라고들 여기느냐고 물었다."], ["I found them to be the perfect isolation of prisoners--so that no one man in confinement there, knew anything about another; and the reduction of prisoners to a wholesome state of mind, leading to sincere contrition and repentance.", "알고 보니 그것은 죄수들을 완벽히 격리하는 것 — 그래서 그곳에 갇힌 사람은 누구도 다른 사람에 대해 아무것도 알지 못하게 하는 것 — 과, 죄수들을 건전한 마음 상태로 끌어내려 진실된 통회와 참회로 이끄는 것이었다."]], [["Now, it struck me, when we began to visit individuals in their cells, and to traverse the passages in which those cells were, and to have the manner of the going to chapel and so forth, explained to us, that there was a strong probability of the prisoners knowing a good deal about each other, and of their carrying on a pretty complete system of intercourse.", "그런데 우리가 개개인의 감방을 방문하기 시작하고, 그 감방들이 늘어선 복도를 지나며, 예배당에 가는 방식이며 그런 것들을 설명 듣게 되었을 때, 나에게는 죄수들이 서로에 대해 꽤 많은 것을 알고 있을 가능성이, 그리고 그들끼리 제법 완벽한 교류 체계를 꾸려 가고 있을 가능성이 다분해 보였다."], ["This, at the time I write, has been proved, I believe, to be the case; but, as it would have been flat blasphemy against the system to have hinted such a doubt then, I looked out for the penitence as diligently as I could.", "이 글을 쓰는 지금, 그것이 사실이었음이 입증된 것으로 나는 알고 있다. 그러나 그때 그런 의심을 조금이라도 내비치는 것은 이 체계에 대한 노골적인 모독이었을 테니, 나는 그 대신 참회를 되도록 부지런히 찾아보았다."]], [["And here again, I had great misgivings.", "그리고 여기서도 나는 크게 미심쩍었다."], ["I found as prevalent a fashion in the form of the penitence, as I had left outside in the forms of the coats and waistcoats in the windows of the tailors’ shops.", "나는 참회의 형식에서, 양복점 진열창에 걸린 외투며 조끼의 형식에서 내가 바깥에 두고 온 것만큼이나 판에 박힌 유행을 발견했다."], ["I found a vast amount of profession, varying very little in character: varying very little (which I thought exceedingly suspicious), even in words.", "나는 엄청난 양의 공언(公言)을 발견했는데, 그 성격이 좀처럼 변하지 않았다. 심지어 (이것이 나로서는 몹시 수상쩍었는데) 그 말투조차 거의 변하지 않았다."], ["I found a great many foxes, disparaging whole vineyards of inaccessible grapes; but I found very few foxes whom I would have trusted within reach of a bunch.", "나는 손닿지 않는 곳의 포도밭을 통째로 깎아내리는 여우를 숱하게 발견했지만, 막상 한 송이가 손 닿는 자리에 있을 때 믿을 만한 여우는 거의 보지 못했다."], ["Above all, I found that the most professing men were the greatest objects of interest; and that their conceit, their vanity, their want of excitement, and their love of deception (which many of them possessed to an almost incredible extent, as their histories showed), all prompted to these professions, and were all gratified by them.", "무엇보다도 나는, 가장 떠벌리는 자들이 가장 큰 관심의 대상이 된다는 것을 발견했다. 그리고 그들의 자만심, 허영심, 자극에 대한 갈증, 그리고 속임수를 즐기는 성향이 (그들 가운데 다수는 그 내력이 보여 주듯 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼 이런 성향을 지니고 있었다) 하나같이 이런 공언을 부추겼고, 또 그 공언으로 죄다 충족되었다."]], [["However, I heard so repeatedly, in the course of our goings to and fro, of a certain Number Twenty Seven, who was the Favourite, and who really appeared to be a Model Prisoner, that I resolved to suspend my judgement until I should see Twenty Seven.", "그렇긴 해도, 우리가 이리저리 오가는 동안 어떤 27번에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 거듭 들었던지 — 그가 총애받는 자였고, 정말이지 모범 죄수로 보였다 — 나는 27번을 직접 볼 때까지는 판단을 미루기로 했다."], ["Twenty Eight, I understood, was also a bright particular star; but it was his misfortune to have his glory a little dimmed by the extraordinary lustre of Twenty Seven.", "28번도, 내가 들은 바로는, 유난히 빛나는 별이었으나, 27번의 비범한 광채에 그 영광이 다소 빛바래는 것이 그의 불운이었다."], ["I heard so much of Twenty Seven, of his pious admonitions to everybody around him, and of the beautiful letters he constantly wrote to his mother (whom he seemed to consider in a very bad way), that I became quite impatient to see him.", "나는 27번에 관해 어찌나 많은 이야기를 들었던지 — 그가 주위 모든 사람에게 건네는 경건한 훈계며, 그가 어머니에게 (그가 보기에 매우 위태로운 상태에 있는 듯한 어머니에게) 끊임없이 써 보낸다는 그 아름다운 편지들 이야기를 — 그를 보고 싶어 안달이 날 지경이었다."]], [["I had to restrain my impatience for some time, on account of Twenty Seven being reserved for a concluding effect.", "27번이 마지막을 장식할 효과로 따로 남겨져 있었던 탓에, 나는 한동안 안달을 억눌러야 했다."], ["But, at last, we came to the door of his cell; and Mr.", "그러나 마침내 우리는 그의 감방 문 앞에 이르렀다."], ["Creakle, looking through a little hole in it, reported to us, in a state of the greatest admiration, that he was reading a Hymn Book.", "크리클 씨가 그 문에 난 작은 구멍으로 들여다보더니, 더없는 감탄에 휩싸여, 그가 찬송가집을 읽고 있노라고 우리에게 알렸다."]], [["There was such a rush of heads immediately, to see Number Twenty Seven reading his Hymn Book, that the little hole was blocked up, six or seven heads deep.", "27번이 찬송가집을 읽는 것을 보려고 즉각 머리들이 어찌나 우르르 몰려들었던지, 그 작은 구멍이 여섯이고 일곱이고 겹친 머리들로 막혀 버렸다."], ["To remedy this inconvenience, and give us an opportunity of conversing with Twenty Seven in all his purity, Mr. Creakle directed the door of the cell to be unlocked, and Twenty Seven to be invited out into the passage.", "이 불편을 덜어 주고, 우리가 27번과 그 온전한 순결함 속에서 이야기를 나눌 기회를 주려고, 크리클 씨는 감방 문을 열게 하고 27번을 복도로 나오라고 청하게 했다."], ["This was done; and whom should Traddles and I then behold, to our amazement, in this converted Number Twenty Seven, but Uriah Heep!", "그렇게 하니 — 트래들스와 나는, 회심했다는 이 27번에게서, 놀랍게도 누구를 보았겠는가, 다름 아닌 유라이아 힙이었다!"]], [["He knew us directly; and said, as he came out--with the old writhe,--", "그는 곧바로 우리를 알아보았고, 옛날 그 몸을 비비 꼬는 몸짓 그대로 밖으로 나오며 말했다."]], [["‘How do you do, Mr. Copperfield?", "\"안녕하십니까, 코퍼필드 씨?"], ["How do you do, Mr. Traddles?’", "안녕하십니까, 트래들스 씨?\""]], [["This recognition caused a general admiration in the party.", "이렇게 알아보는 것에 일행은 다들 감탄했다."], ["I rather thought that everyone was struck by his not being proud, and taking notice of us.", "내가 보기에는 그가 거만을 떨지 않고 우리를 알은체했다는 데에 다들 감동한 듯했다."]], [["‘Well, Twenty Seven,’ said Mr. Creakle, mournfully admiring him.", "\"그래, 27번,\" 크리클 씨가 애잔하게 그를 우러러보며 말했다."], ["‘How do you find yourself today?’", "\"오늘은 자네 상태가 어떤가?\""]], [["‘I am very umble, sir!’ replied Uriah Heep.", "\"저는 아주 미천합니다, 나리!\" 유라이아 힙이 답했다."]], [["‘You are always so, Twenty Seven,’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"자네는 늘 그렇지, 27번.\" 크리클 씨가 말했다."]], [["Here, another gentleman asked, with extreme anxiety: ‘Are you quite comfortable?’", "여기서 또 다른 신사가 지극한 근심에 잠겨 물었다. \"정말 편안한가?\""]], [["‘Yes, I thank you, sir!’ said Uriah Heep, looking in that direction.", "\"네, 고맙습니다, 나리!\" 유라이아 힙이 그쪽을 바라보며 말했다."], ["‘Far more comfortable here, than ever I was outside.", "\"바깥에 있을 때보다 여기가 훨씬 더 편안합니다."], ["I see my follies, now, sir. That’s what makes me comfortable.’", "이제 제 어리석음이 보입니다, 나리. 그래서 제가 편안한 겁니다.\""]], [["Several gentlemen were much affected; and a third questioner, forcing himself to the front, inquired with extreme feeling: ‘How do you find the beef?’", "여러 신사가 크게 감동했다. 그리고 세 번째 질문자가 앞으로 비집고 나오더니, 북받치는 감정으로 물었다. \"쇠고기는 어떤가?\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir,’ replied Uriah, glancing in the new direction of this voice, ‘it was tougher yesterday than I could wish; but it’s my duty to bear.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 유라이아가 이 목소리가 들려온 새로운 방향을 흘끔 보며 답했다. \"어제 것은 제가 바라는 것보다 좀 질겼습니다만, 견디는 것이 제 도리지요."], ["I have committed follies, gentlemen,’ said Uriah, looking round with a meek smile, ‘and I ought to bear the consequences without repining.’ A murmur, partly of gratification at Twenty Seven’s celestial state of mind, and partly of indignation against the Contractor who had given him any cause of complaint (a note of which was immediately made by Mr.", "저는 어리석음을 저질렀습니다, 여러분.\" 유라이아가 온순한 미소를 띠고 좌중을 둘러보며 말했다. \"그러니 불평 없이 그 대가를 치러야 마땅합니다.\""], ["Creakle), having subsided, Twenty Seven stood in the midst of us, as if he felt himself the principal object of merit in a highly meritorious museum. That we, the neophytes, might have an excess of light shining upon us all at once, orders were given to let out Twenty Eight.", "27번의 천상에 닿은 마음 상태에 대한 흡족함과, 그에게 불평거리를 안겨 준 납품업자에 대한 분개가 (크리클 씨는 그것을 즉시 메모했다) 반반씩 섞인 웅성거림이 가라앉자, 27번은 우리 한가운데 서 있었는데, 마치 자기가 더없이 공덕 높은 박물관에서 으뜸가는 공덕의 전시물이라도 된 듯한 기색이었다. 풋내기인 우리에게 한꺼번에 넘치는 빛이 쏟아질 수 있도록, 28번을 내보내라는 명령이 떨어졌다."]], [["I had been so much astonished already, that I only felt a kind of resigned wonder when Mr. Littimer walked forth, reading a good book!", "나는 이미 어찌나 놀란 상태였던지, 리티머 씨가 좋은 책을 읽으며 걸어 나왔을 때에도 그저 일종의 체념 어린 경이로움만 느꼈을 뿐이었다!"]], [["‘Twenty Eight,’ said a gentleman in spectacles, who had not yet spoken, ‘you complained last week, my good fellow, of the cocoa.", "\"28번,\" 그때까지 한마디도 하지 않았던 안경 쓴 신사가 말했다. \"여보게, 자네가 지난주에 코코아를 두고 불평했는데."], ["How has it been since?’", "그 뒤로는 어떤가?\""]], [["‘I thank you, sir,’ said Mr.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리.\""], ["Littimer, ‘it has been better made.", "리티머 씨가 말했다."], ["If I might take the liberty of saying so, sir, I don’t think the milk which is boiled with it is quite genuine; but I am aware, sir, that there is a great adulteration of milk, in London, and that the article in a pure state is difficult to be obtained.’", "\"더 잘 타게 됐습니다. 외람되이 말씀드리자면, 나리, 거기 넣고 끓이는 우유가 그리 진짜는 아닌 듯합니다만, 런던에서는 우유에 섞음질이 심하고, 순수한 상태의 물건을 구하기가 어렵다는 것을 저도 알고 있습니다, 나리.\""]], [["It appeared to me that the gentleman in spectacles backed his Twenty Eight against Mr.", "내가 보기에 안경 쓴 신사는 자기네 28번을 크리클 씨의 27번에 맞서 내세우는 듯했다."], ["Creakle’s Twenty Seven, for each of them took his own man in hand.", "둘 다 제 사람을 손수 떠맡고 있었으니 말이다."]], [["‘What is your state of mind, Twenty Eight?’ said the questioner in spectacles.", "\"자네 마음 상태는 어떤가, 28번?\" 안경 쓴 질문자가 말했다."]], [["‘I thank you, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 리티머 씨가 답했다."], ["Littimer; ‘I see my follies now, sir.", "\"이제 제 어리석음이 보입니다, 나리."], ["I am a good deal troubled when I think of the sins of my former companions, sir; but I trust they may find forgiveness.’", "저는 옛 동무들의 죄를 떠올리면 마음이 무척 괴롭습니다, 나리. 그러나 그들도 용서받을 수 있기를 믿습니다.\""]], [["‘You are quite happy yourself?’ said the questioner, nodding encouragement.", "\"자네 자신은 아주 행복한가?\" 질문자가 북돋듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["‘I am much obliged to you, sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"황송합니다, 나리.\" 리티머 씨가 답했다."], ["Littimer. ‘Perfectly so.’", "\"더없이 그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘Is there anything at all on your mind now?’ said the questioner.", "\"지금 마음에 걸리는 것이 조금이라도 있는가?\" 질문자가 말했다."], ["‘If so, mention it, Twenty Eight.’", "\"있다면 말해 보게, 28번.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr.", "\"나리,\""], ["Littimer, without looking up, ‘if my eyes have not deceived me, there is a gentleman present who was acquainted with me in my former life.", "리티머 씨가 고개는 들지 않은 채 말했다. \"제 눈이 저를 속인 게 아니라면, 이 자리에 제 지난 삶에서 저를 알던 신사 한 분이 계십니다."], ["It may be profitable to that gentleman to know, sir, that I attribute my past follies, entirely to having lived a thoughtless life in the service of young men; and to having allowed myself to be led by them into weaknesses, which I had not the strength to resist.", "그분이 아셔 두면 유익할 듯합니다, 나리. 제 지난 어리석음은 오로지 젊은 분들을 모시며 분별없는 삶을 산 데서, 그리고 그분들에게 이끌려, 제 힘으로는 떨쳐 내지 못한 약점에 빠져든 데서 비롯되었다는 것을요."], ["I hope that gentleman will take warning, sir, and will not be offended at my freedom. It is for his good.", "그 신사분이 이를 경계로 삼으시기를, 그리고 저의 거리낌 없는 말에 노여워하지 마시기를 바랍니다, 나리. 다 그분을 위한 것이니까요."], ["I am conscious of my own past follies.", "저는 제 지난 어리석음을 자각하고 있습니다."], ["I hope he may repent of all the wickedness and sin to which he has been a party.’", "그분도 자기가 가담했던 그 모든 사악함과 죄를 뉘우치시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["I observed that several gentlemen were shading their eyes, each with one hand, as if they had just come into church.", "여러 신사가 저마다 한 손으로 두 눈을 가리는 것이 내 눈에 들어왔는데, 마치 방금 교회에 들어선 사람들 같았다."]], [["‘This does you credit, Twenty Eight,’ returned the questioner.", "\"그건 자네에게 영예로운 일일세, 28번.\" 질문자가 답했다."], ["‘I should have expected it of you.", "\"자네라면 그러리라 기대했지."], ["Is there anything else?’", "그 밖에 또 있는가?\""]], [["‘Sir,’ returned Mr.", "\"나리,\""], ["Littimer, slightly lifting up his eyebrows, but not his eyes, ‘there was a young woman who fell into dissolute courses, that I endeavoured to save, sir, but could not rescue.", "리티머 씨가 눈썹은 살짝 치켜올렸으되 눈은 들지 않은 채 답했다. \"방탕한 길로 빠져든 어떤 젊은 여자가 있었습니다. 제가 구하려 애썼으나 끝내 건져 내지 못했지요, 나리."], ["I beg that gentleman, if he has it in his power, to inform that young woman from me that I forgive her her bad conduct towards myself, and that I call her to repentance--if he will be so good.’", "그 신사분이, 혹 그럴 힘이 있으시다면, 그 젊은 여자에게 제 말을 전해 주시기를 청합니다. 저에게 보인 그 못된 행실을 제가 용서하노라고, 그리고 그녀에게 회개를 촉구한다고요 — 그분께서 그리 너그러이 해 주신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I have no doubt, Twenty Eight,’ returned the questioner, ‘that the gentleman you refer to feels very strongly--as we all must--what you have so properly said.", "\"그대가 가리키는 그 신사분도,\" 질문자가 답했다. \"자네가 그토록 온당하게 한 말에 우리 모두가 그렇듯 깊이 느끼는 바가 있으리라는 데에 나는 의심이 없네, 28번."], ["We will not detain you.’", "자네를 더 붙들어 두지 않겠네.\""]], [["‘I thank you, sir,’ said Mr. Littimer.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리.\" 리티머 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Gentlemen, I wish you a good day, and hoping you and your families will also see your wickedness, and amend!’", "\"여러분, 좋은 하루 되시기를 빕니다. 그리고 여러분과 여러분의 가족도 부디 자신의 사악함을 깨닫고 고치시기를 바랍니다!\""]], [["With this, Number Twenty Eight retired, after a glance between him and Uriah; as if they were not altogether unknown to each other, through some medium of communication; and a murmur went round the group, as his door shut upon him, that he was a most respectable man, and a beautiful case.", "이 말과 함께 28번은, 유라이아와 한 번 눈길을 주고받은 뒤에 물러갔다 — 마치 둘이 어떤 소통의 매개를 통해 서로 아주 모르는 사이는 아니라는 듯이. 그리고 그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히자, 그가 더없이 훌륭한 사람이요 참으로 아름다운 사례라는 웅성거림이 무리 사이로 퍼져 나갔다."]], [["‘Now, Twenty Seven,’ said Mr.", "\"자, 27번,\""], ["Creakle, entering on a clear stage with his man, ‘is there anything that anyone can do for you?", "크리클 씨가 자기 사람을 데리고 텅 빈 무대에 올라서듯 말했다. \"누가 자네를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이 무엇이라도 있는가?"], ["If so, mention it.’", "있다면 말해 보게.\""]], [["‘I would umbly ask, sir,’ returned Uriah, with a jerk of his malevolent head, ‘for leave to write again to mother.’", "\"미천하게나마 청하옵건대, 나리,\" 유라이아가 그 악의에 찬 머리를 홱 비틀며 답했다. \"어머니에게 다시 편지를 쓰게 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["‘It shall certainly be granted,’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"틀림없이 허락하겠네.\" 크리클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you, sir!", "\"고맙습니다, 나리!"], ["I am anxious about mother. I am afraid she ain’t safe.’", "저는 어머니가 걱정됩니다. 어머니가 무사하지 못할까 봐 두렵습니다.\""]], [["Somebody incautiously asked, what from?", "누군가가 경솔하게 무엇으로부터 무사하지 못하냐고 물었다."], ["But there was a scandalized whisper of ‘Hush!’", "그러나 \"쉿!\" 하는, 못마땅해하는 속삭임이 일었다."]], [["‘Immortally safe, sir,’ returned Uriah, writhing in the direction of the voice.", "\"영혼의 구원을 두고 드리는 말씀입니다, 나리.\" 유라이아가 그 목소리 쪽으로 몸을 비틀며 답했다."], ["‘I should wish mother to be got into my state.", "\"어머니도 제 상태에 이르렀으면 합니다."], ["I never should have been got into my present state if I hadn’t come here.", "제가 여기 오지 않았더라면 지금 이 상태에 결코 이르지 못했을 겁니다."], ["I wish mother had come here. It would be better for everybody, if they got took up, and was brought here.’", "어머니도 여기 왔더라면 좋았을 텐데요. 다들 붙잡혀서 여기로 끌려오면 모두에게 더 나을 텐데요.\""]], [["This sentiment gave unbounded satisfaction--greater satisfaction, I think, than anything that had passed yet.", "이 견해는 그지없는 만족을 안겨 주었다 — 내 생각에는, 지금껏 오간 그 무엇보다도 큰 만족을."]], [["‘Before I come here,’ said Uriah, stealing a look at us, as if he would have blighted the outer world to which we belonged, if he could, ‘I was given to follies; but now I am sensible of my follies.", "\"제가 여기 오기 전에는,\" 유라이아가, 할 수만 있다면 우리가 속한 그 바깥세상을 통째로 시들게 해 버릴 듯한 눈초리로 우리를 흘끔 훔쳐보며 말했다. \"저는 어리석음에 빠져 있었습니다."], ["There’s a deal of sin outside. There’s a deal of sin in mother.", "그러나 이제 제 어리석음을 깨닫습니다. 바깥에는 죄가 가득합니다."], ["There’s nothing but sin everywhere--except here.’", "어머니 안에도 죄가 가득합니다. 여기 말고는 어디나 온통 죄밖에 없습니다.\""]], [["‘You are quite changed?’ said Mr. Creakle.", "\"완전히 달라졌나?\" 크리클 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh dear, yes, sir!’ cried this hopeful penitent.", "\"아이고, 그럼요, 나리!\" 이 가망 넘치는 참회자가 외쳤다."]], [["‘You wouldn’t relapse, if you were going out?’ asked somebody else.", "\"바깥으로 나간다면 다시 빠져들지는 않겠지?\" 또 다른 누군가가 물었다."]], [["‘Oh de-ar no, sir!’", "\"아이고, 처-언만에요, 나리!\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Mr.", "\"좋아!\""], ["Creakle, ‘this is very gratifying.", "크리클 씨가 말했다. \"이거 참 흐뭇하군."], ["You have addressed Mr. Copperfield, Twenty Seven.", "자네가 코퍼필드 씨에게 말을 건넸는데, 27번."], ["Do you wish to say anything further to him?’", "그에게 더 하고 싶은 말이 있는가?\""]], [["‘You knew me, a long time before I came here and was changed, Mr.", "\"코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 제가 여기 와서 달라지기 한참 전부터 저를 아셨지요.\""], ["Copperfield,’ said Uriah, looking at me; and a more villainous look I never saw, even on his visage.", "유라이아가 나를 바라보며 말했는데, 그토록 악랄한 눈빛은, 그의 얼굴에서조차도, 나는 본 적이 없었다."], ["‘You knew me when, in spite of my follies, I was umble among them that was proud, and meek among them that was violent--you was violent to me yourself, Mr. Copperfield.", "\"당신은 제가, 어리석음에도 불구하고, 거만한 자들 사이에서 미천하고 사나운 자들 사이에서 온순했던 그 시절의 저를 아셨지요 — 코퍼필드 씨, 당신은 저에게 사나우셨습니다."], ["Once, you struck me a blow in the face, you know.’", "한번은 제 얼굴을 후려치셨지요, 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["General commiseration.", "좌중에 동정이 일었다."], ["Several indignant glances directed at me.", "분개한 눈길 여럿이 나를 향했다."]], [["‘But I forgive you, Mr. Copperfield,’ said Uriah, making his forgiving nature the subject of a most impious and awful parallel, which I shall not record.", "\"하지만 저는 당신을 용서합니다, 코퍼필드 씨.\" 유라이아가, 자기의 용서하는 천성을 더없이 불경하고 끔찍한 비유의 소재로 삼으며 말했는데, 그 비유는 여기 옮기지 않겠다."], ["‘I forgive everybody.", "\"저는 모두를 용서합니다."], ["It would ill become me to bear malice.", "앙심을 품는 것은 저에게 어울리지 않으니까요."], ["I freely forgive you, and I hope you’ll curb your passions in future. I hope Mr. W.", "저는 기꺼이 당신을 용서합니다. 그리고 앞으로는 당신이 그 격정을 다스리시기를 바랍니다."], ["will repent, and Miss W., and all of that sinful lot.", "위필드 씨도, 위필드 양도, 그 죄 많은 무리 모두도 뉘우치기를 바랍니다."], ["You’ve been visited with affliction, and I hope it may do you good; but you’d better have come here. Mr. W.", "당신은 시련을 겪으셨고, 그것이 당신에게 약이 되기를 바랍니다. 하지만 차라리 여기 오셨더라면 더 나았을 겁니다."], ["had better have come here, and Miss W. too. The best wish I could give you, Mr.", "위필드 씨도 여기 오셨더라면 더 나았을 테고, 위필드 양도 마찬가지였을 겁니다."], ["Copperfield, and give all of you gentlemen, is, that you could be took up and brought here.", "코퍼필드 씨, 제가 당신께, 그리고 여러분 모두께 빌어 드릴 수 있는 가장 좋은 소원은, 여러분이 붙잡혀 여기로 끌려오는 것입니다."], ["When I think of my past follies, and my present state, I am sure it would be best for you.", "제 지난 어리석음과 지금의 제 상태를 떠올리면, 그것이 여러분께 가장 좋으리라는 것을 저는 확신합니다."], ["I pity all who ain’t brought here!’", "저는 여기 끌려오지 못한 모든 이가 가엾습니다!\""]], [["He sneaked back into his cell, amidst a little chorus of approbation; and both Traddles and I experienced a great relief when he was locked in.", "그는 자그마한 찬사의 합창 속에서 슬그머니 제 감방으로 기어 들어갔다. 그가 안에 갇히자 트래들스도 나도 크게 마음이 놓였다."]], [["It was a characteristic feature in this repentance, that I was fain to ask what these two men had done, to be there at all.", "이 참회에서 한 가지 특징적인 면은, 이 두 사람이 도대체 무슨 짓을 저질러 여기 와 있는지 내가 굳이 물어봐야 했다는 점이다."], ["That appeared to be the last thing about which they had anything to say.", "그것이야말로 그들이 입에 올릴 말이 가장 없는 일인 듯했다."], ["I addressed myself to one of the two warders, who, I suspected from certain latent indications in their faces, knew pretty well what all this stir was worth.", "나는 두 간수 가운데 한 사람에게 물어보았다. 그들의 얼굴에 어린 어떤 은밀한 기색으로 미루어, 나는 그들이 이 모든 소동이 무슨 값어치인지를 꽤 잘 알고 있으리라 짐작했다."]], [["‘Do you know,’ said I, as we walked along the passage, ‘what felony was Number Twenty Seven’s last “folly”?’", "\"혹시 아십니까,\" 우리가 복도를 따라 걸으며 내가 물었다. \"27번의 마지막 '어리석음'이 무슨 중죄였는지?\""]], [["The answer was that it was a Bank case.", "대답인즉 은행 사건이라는 것이었다."]], [["‘A fraud on the Bank of England?’ I asked.", "\"잉글랜드 은행을 상대로 한 사기였습니까?\""], ["‘Yes, sir.", "내가 물었다."], ["Fraud, forgery, and conspiracy.", "\"그렇습니다, 나리. 사기에 위조에 공모였지요."], ["He and some others.", "그자와 몇몇이서 말입니다."], ["He set the others on.", "그자가 나머지를 끌어들였고요."], ["It was a deep plot for a large sum.", "거액을 노린 치밀한 음모였습니다."], ["Sentence, transportation for life.", "형량은 종신 유형(流刑)이고요."], ["Twenty Seven was the knowingest bird of the lot, and had very nearly kept himself safe; but not quite.", "27번은 그 무리 중에서도 가장 약은 새여서, 하마터면 제 몸은 안전하게 빼낼 뻔했습니다만, 끝내 그러지는 못했지요."], ["The Bank was just able to put salt upon his tail--and only just.’", "은행이 아슬아슬하게 그자의 꼬리를 붙잡았거든요 — 정말 간발의 차로요.\""]], [["‘Do you know Twenty Eight’s offence?’", "\"28번의 죄는 아십니까?\""]], [["‘Twenty Eight,’ returned my informant, speaking throughout in a low tone, and looking over his shoulder as we walked along the passage, to guard himself from being overheard, in such an unlawful reference to these Immaculates, by Creakle and the rest; ‘Twenty Eight (also transportation) got a place, and robbed a young master of a matter of two hundred and fifty pounds in money and valuables, the night before they were going abroad.", "\"28번은요,\" 내 정보원이 내내 낮은 목소리로 말하며, 이 흠 없는 성자들에 대한 이런 불경한 언급을 크리클 일당에게 들키지 않으려고 복도를 걷는 내내 어깨 너머를 살피며 답했다. \"28번도 (역시 유형입니다) 어느 자리에 들어가서, 외국으로 떠나기 전날 밤에 젊은 주인의 돈과 귀중품을 한 이백오십 파운드어치쯤 털었습니다."], ["I particularly recollect his case, from his being took by a dwarf.’", "저는 이 사건이 특히 기억에 남습니다, 그자가 난쟁이한테 붙잡혔거든요.\""]], [["‘A what?’", "\"뭐한테요?\""]], [["‘A little woman.", "\"자그마한 여자한테요."], ["I have forgot her name?’", "이름은 잊어버렸습니다만?\""]], [["‘Not Mowcher?’", "\"마우처 아닙니까?\""]], [["‘That’s it!", "\"바로 그겁니다!"], ["He had eluded pursuit, and was going to America in a flaxen wig, and whiskers, and such a complete disguise as never you see in all your born days; when the little woman, being in Southampton, met him walking along the street--picked him out with her sharp eye in a moment--ran betwixt his legs to upset him--and held on to him like grim Death.’", "그자가 추격을 따돌리고 아마빛 가발에 구레나룻까지 붙이고, 평생 본 적 없을 만큼 완벽한 변장을 하고 미국으로 가려던 참이었지요. 그런데 그 자그마한 여자가 마침 사우샘프턴에 와 있다가 길을 걸어가는 그자와 마주쳤습니다 — 그 날카로운 눈으로 단박에 그자를 가려내고 — 그자의 두 다리 사이로 뛰어들어 넘어뜨리고는 — 죽음의 신처럼 악착같이 그자를 붙들고 늘어졌지요.\""]], [["‘Excellent Miss Mowcher!’ cried I.", "\"훌륭한 마우처 양!\" 내가 외쳤다."]], [["‘You’d have said so, if you had seen her, standing on a chair in the witness-box at the trial, as I did,’ said my friend.", "\"나리도 보셨더라면 그렇게 말씀하셨을 겁니다. 제가 그랬듯이, 재판에서 그 여자가 증인석 의자에 올라서 있는 걸 보셨더라면요.\""], ["‘He cut her face right open, and pounded her in the most brutal manner, when she took him; but she never loosed her hold till he was locked up.", "내 벗이 말했다. \"그자는 붙잡힐 때 그 여자의 얼굴을 쫙 갈라놓고 더없이 잔인하게 두들겨 팼습니다만, 여자는 그자가 갇힐 때까지 결코 잡은 손을 놓지 않았습니다."], ["She held so tight to him, in fact, that the officers were obliged to take ‘em both together.", "어찌나 단단히 붙들고 늘어졌던지, 사실 경관들이 둘을 함께 데려갈 수밖에 없었지요."], ["She gave her evidence in the gamest way, and was highly complimented by the Bench, and cheered right home to her lodgings.", "그 여자는 더없이 당찬 태도로 증언을 했고, 재판관들에게 크게 칭찬받았으며, 제 숙소까지 환호를 받으며 돌아갔습니다."], ["She said in Court that she’d have took him single-handed (on account of what she knew concerning him), if he had been Samson.", "그 여자는 법정에서, 그자가 삼손이라 해도 (그자에 대해 자기가 아는 것이 있으니) 혼자서라도 붙잡았을 거라고 말했습니다."], ["And it’s my belief she would!’", "그리고 제 믿기로는 정말 그랬을 겁니다!\""]], [["It was mine too, and I highly respected Miss Mowcher for it.", "나도 같은 생각이었고, 그 점에서 나는 마우처 양을 깊이 우러르게 되었다."]], [["We had now seen all there was to see.", "이제 우리는 볼 것은 다 본 셈이었다."], ["It would have been in vain to represent to such a man as the Worshipful Mr. Creakle, that Twenty Seven and Twenty Eight were perfectly consistent and unchanged; that exactly what they were then, they had always been; that the hypocritical knaves were just the subjects to make that sort of profession in such a place; that they knew its market-value at least as well as we did, in the immediate service it would do them when they were expatriated; in a word, that it was a rotten, hollow, painfully suggestive piece of business altogether.", "존경하올 크리클 씨 같은 사람에게, 27번과 28번이 완벽히 한결같고 조금도 달라지지 않았다는 것을, 그러니까 그때의 그들 모습이 곧 늘 그들의 모습이었다는 것을, 그 위선적인 악당들이야말로 이런 곳에서 그런 식의 공언을 하기에 딱 알맞은 자들이라는 것을, 그들이 그 공언의 시장 가치를 — 유형에 처해질 때 그것이 곧바로 자기들에게 가져다줄 이득을 — 우리 못지않게 잘 알고 있다는 것을, 한마디로 이 모든 것이 썩어 빠지고 속 빈, 가슴 아플 만큼 시사적인 짓거리라는 것을 일러 줘 봤자 헛수고였으리라."], ["We left them to their system and themselves, and went home wondering.", "우리는 그들을 그들의 체계와 그들 자신에게 내맡기고, 어리둥절한 채로 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["‘Perhaps it’s a good thing, Traddles,’ said I, ‘to have an unsound Hobby ridden hard; for it’s the sooner ridden to death.’", "\"어쩌면 잘된 일인지도 몰라, 트래들스.\" 내가 말했다. \"엉터리 취미일수록 호되게 몰아붙이는 게 말이야. 그래야 더 빨리 죽도록 내달려 끝나 버릴 테니.\""]], [["‘I hope so,’ replied Traddles.", "\"그러길 바라네.\" 트래들스가 답했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 62. A LIGHT SHINES ON MY WAY", "# CHAPTER 62. A LIGHT SHINES ON MY WAY"]], [["The year came round to Christmas-time, and I had been at home above two months.", "내 인생이 크리스마스 무렵으로 한 바퀴 돌아왔다. 집에 돌아온 지 두 달이 넘었다."], ["I had seen Agnes frequently.", "그동안 나는 애그니스를 자주 만났다."], ["However loud the general voice might be in giving me encouragement, and however fervent the emotions and endeavours to which it roused me, I heard her lightest word of praise as I heard nothing else.", "사람들이 아무리 큰 목소리로 나를 격려하고, 그 격려가 내 안에 아무리 뜨거운 감정과 노력을 불러일으켰다 한들, 그녀가 던지는 가장 가벼운 칭찬 한마디는 그 무엇과도 다르게 내 귀에 들어왔다."]], [["At least once a week, and sometimes oftener, I rode over there, and passed the evening.", "적어도 일주일에 한 번, 어떤 때는 그보다 더 자주, 나는 그곳까지 말을 타고 가서 저녁 시간을 보냈다. 보통은 밤에 말을 타고 돌아왔다."], ["I usually rode back at night; for the old unhappy sense was always hovering about me now--most sorrowfully when I left her--and I was glad to be up and out, rather than wandering over the past in weary wakefulness or miserable dreams.", "그 오래된 서글픈 감정이 이제 늘 내 곁을 맴돌았기 때문이다. 그녀와 헤어질 때면 가장 사무치게 밀려왔다. 그래서 나는 지친 채 잠 못 이루며 지난날을 헤매거나 비참한 꿈에 시달리기보다는, 차라리 몸을 일으켜 밖으로 나서는 편이 마음 편했다."], ["I wore away the longest part of many wild sad nights, in those rides; reviving, as I went, the thoughts that had occupied me in my long absence.", "거칠고 슬픈 숱한 밤들의 가장 긴 자락을 나는 그 말 위에서 흘려보냈다. 가는 길에, 오래 떨어져 있는 동안 나를 사로잡았던 생각들을 되살려 가면서."]], [["Or, if I were to say rather that I listened to the echoes of those thoughts, I should better express the truth. They spoke to me from afar off.", "아니, 그 생각들의 메아리에 귀를 기울였다고 말하는 편이 진실을 더 잘 담아낼 것이다. 그 생각들은 아득히 먼 곳에서 내게 말을 건넸다."], ["I had put them at a distance, and accepted my inevitable place.", "나는 그것들을 멀찍이 밀어 두고, 내게 주어진 피할 수 없는 자리를 받아들였다."], ["When I read to Agnes what I wrote; when I saw her listening face; moved her to smiles or tears; and heard her cordial voice so earnest on the shadowy events of that imaginative world in which I lived; I thought what a fate mine might have been--but only thought so, as I had thought after I was married to Dora, what I could have wished my wife to be.", "내가 쓴 글을 애그니스에게 읽어 줄 때, 귀 기울이는 그녀의 얼굴을 바라볼 때, 그녀를 웃기거나 울릴 때, 그리고 내가 살아가는 그 상상의 세계에서 벌어지는 어슴푸레한 사건들을 두고 그녀가 어찌나 진지하게 따뜻한 목소리로 말하는지 들을 때면, 내 운명이 어떠했을 수도 있었을까 하고 생각했다. 그러나 그저 생각만 했을 뿐이다. 도라와 결혼한 뒤에, 내 아내가 어떤 사람이었으면 좋았을까 하고 생각만 했던 것처럼."]], [["My duty to Agnes, who loved me with a love, which, if I disquieted, I wronged most selfishly and poorly, and could never restore; my matured assurance that I, who had worked out my own destiny, and won what I had impetuously set my heart on, had no right to murmur, and must bear; comprised what I felt and what I had learned. But I loved her: and now it even became some consolation to me, vaguely to conceive a distant day when I might blamelessly avow it; when all this should be over; when I could say ‘Agnes, so it was when I came home; and now I am old, and I never have loved since!’", "애그니스를 향한 내 도리, 그녀는 그토록 깊은 사랑으로 나를 사랑했는데, 만약 내가 그 마음을 어지럽힌다면 그것은 더없이 이기적이고 옹졸하게 그녀를 짓밟는 일이 될 테고, 그 상처는 결코 되돌릴 수 없으리라. 내 운명은 내 손으로 빚어 낸 것이고, 한때 앞뒤 없이 마음을 쏟아 손에 넣은 것을 얻은 처지이니, 나에게는 투덜댈 권리가 없으며 견뎌야 한다는 무르익은 확신, 이것이 내가 느낀 것이자 배운 것의 전부였다. 그러나 나는 그녀를 사랑했다. 그리고 이제는 언젠가 먼 훗날, 떳떳하게 그 사랑을 고백할 수 있는 날을 어렴풋이 그려 보는 것조차 내게 얼마쯤 위안이 되었다. 이 모든 일이 다 지나가고, 내가 이렇게 말할 수 있는 날을. \"애그니스, 내가 집에 돌아왔을 때 이미 이랬소. 그리고 이제 나는 늙었고, 그 후로 단 한 번도 다른 사람을 사랑한 적이 없소!\""]], [["She did not once show me any change in herself.", "그녀는 단 한 번도 자신이 달라졌다는 기색을 내게 보이지 않았다."], ["What she always had been to me, she still was; wholly unaltered.", "늘 내게 그러했던 모습 그대로, 그녀는 여전히 그러했다. 조금도 변하지 않은 채로."]], [["Between my aunt and me there had been something, in this connexion, since the night of my return, which I cannot call a restraint, or an avoidance of the subject, so much as an implied understanding that we thought of it together, but did not shape our thoughts into words.", "내가 돌아온 그날 밤 이후로, 이 일과 관련해서 고모님과 나 사이에는 무언가가 있었다. 그것을 무슨 거리낌이라거나 화제를 피하는 것이라고 부르기보다는, 우리가 그 일을 함께 떠올리되 그 생각을 말로 빚어내지는 않는다는 무언의 양해라고 하는 편이 맞겠다."], ["When, according to our old custom, we sat before the fire at night, we often fell into this train; as naturally, and as consciously to each other, as if we had unreservedly said so.", "우리는 옛 버릇대로 밤마다 난롯가에 앉았는데, 그럴 때면 종종 그 흐름에 빠져들곤 했다. 마치 거리낌 없이 입 밖에 낸 것처럼, 자연스럽게, 그리고 서로가 또렷이 알아차리면서."], ["But we preserved an unbroken silence.", "그러나 우리는 깨지지 않는 침묵을 지켰다."], ["I believed that she had read, or partly read, my thoughts that night; and that she fully comprehended why I gave mine no more distinct expression.", "나는 믿었다. 그녀가 그날 밤 내 생각을 읽었거나 어느 정도 읽었으리라고, 그리고 내가 왜 내 생각을 더 또렷이 드러내지 않는지를 그녀가 온전히 헤아리고 있으리라고."]], [["This Christmas-time being come, and Agnes having reposed no new confidence in me, a doubt that had several times arisen in my mind--whether she could have that perception of the true state of my breast, which restrained her with the apprehension of giving me pain--began to oppress me heavily.", "이렇게 크리스마스 무렵이 다가왔는데도 애그니스가 새로이 내게 속내를 털어놓지 않자, 여러 번 내 마음속에 떠올랐던 의심 하나가 무겁게 나를 짓누르기 시작했다. 그녀가 내 가슴속 참모습을 꿰뚫어 보고 있어서, 내게 고통을 줄까 봐 두려운 마음에 스스로를 억누르고 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 의심이었다."], ["If that were so, my sacrifice was nothing; my plainest obligation to her unfulfilled; and every poor action I had shrunk from, I was hourly doing.", "만약 그렇다면, 내 희생은 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀에 대한 가장 명백한 내 도리를 다하지 못한 셈이고, 내가 움츠러들며 피해 온 그 옹졸한 짓거리를 시시각각 저지르고 있는 셈이었다."], ["I resolved to set this right beyond all doubt;--if such a barrier were between us, to break it down at once with a determined hand.", "나는 이 일을 한 점 의심도 없이 바로잡기로 결심했다. 우리 사이에 그런 장벽이 가로놓여 있다면, 단호한 손으로 당장 그것을 허물어 버리기로."]], [["It was--what lasting reason have I to remember it!--a cold, harsh, winter day.", "그날은, 내가 이 일을 두고두고 기억할 까닭이 얼마나 깊은지! 춥고 매서운 겨울날이었다."], ["There had been snow, some hours before; and it lay, not deep, but hard-frozen on the ground.", "몇 시간 전에 눈이 내렸고, 깊이 쌓이지는 않았으나 땅 위에 꽝꽝 얼어붙어 있었다."], ["Out at sea, beyond my window, the wind blew ruggedly from the north.", "창밖 저 멀리 바다 위로는 북녘에서 바람이 거칠게 불어왔다."], ["I had been thinking of it, sweeping over those mountain wastes of snow in Switzerland, then inaccessible to any human foot; and had been speculating which was the lonelier, those solitary regions, or a deserted ocean.", "나는 그 바람이 스위스의 저 황량한 설산 위를, 그 무렵에는 사람의 발길이 닿을 수 없는 그곳을 휩쓸어 가는 모습을 떠올리고 있었다. 그리고 어느 쪽이 더 외로울까 곰곰이 따져 보고 있었다. 그 호젓한 산자락일까, 아니면 인적 없는 바다일까."]], [["‘Riding today, Trot?’ said my aunt, putting her head in at the door.", "\"오늘도 말 타고 가니, 트롯?\" 고모님이 문틈으로 고개를 들이밀며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said I, ‘I am going over to Canterbury.", "\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"캔터베리에 갈 거예요."], ["It’s a good day for a ride.’", "말 타기 좋은 날이네요.\""]], [["‘I hope your horse may think so too,’ said my aunt; ‘but at present he is holding down his head and his ears, standing before the door there, as if he thought his stable preferable.’", "\"네 말도 그렇게 생각하면 좋으련만.\" 고모님이 말했다. \"지금은 저기 문 앞에 서서 고개랑 귀를 푹 떨구고 있는 게, 제 마구간이 더 낫다고 여기는 모양이던데.\""]], [["My aunt, I may observe, allowed my horse on the forbidden ground, but had not at all relented towards the donkeys.", "덧붙이자면, 고모님은 내 말이 그 금지된 땅에 들어서는 것은 봐주었으나, 당나귀들에 대해서만큼은 조금도 누그러지지 않았다."]], [["‘He will be fresh enough, presently!’ said I.", "\"곧 기운이 펄펄 날 거예요!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘The ride will do his master good, at all events,’ observed my aunt, glancing at the papers on my table.", "\"어쨌거나 그 주인한테는 말 타는 게 약이 되겠지.\" 고모님이 내 책상 위의 종이들을 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["‘Ah, child, you pass a good many hours here!", "\"아이고, 얘야, 너 여기서 참 많은 시간을 보내는구나!"], ["I never thought, when I used to read books, what work it was to write them.’", "내가 한창 책을 읽던 시절에는, 그걸 쓰는 게 이리 고된 일인 줄은 미처 몰랐다.\""]], [["‘It’s work enough to read them, sometimes,’ I returned.", "\"읽는 것도 어떤 때는 어지간히 고된 일인걸요.\" 내가 받았다."], ["‘As to the writing, it has its own charms, aunt.’", "\"쓰는 거로 말하자면, 그 나름의 매력이 있어요, 고모님.\""]], [["‘Ah! I see!’ said my aunt.", "\"아하! 알겠다!\" 고모님이 말했다."], ["‘Ambition, love of approbation, sympathy, and much more, I suppose?", "\"야망에다, 인정받고 싶은 마음에다, 공감에다, 뭐 그런 것들이 한참 더 있겠지?"], ["Well: go along with you!’", "그래, 어서 가 보거라!\""]], [["‘Do you know anything more,’ said I, standing composedly before her--she had patted me on the shoulder, and sat down in my chair--‘of that attachment of Agnes?’", "\"혹시 더 아시는 게 있으세요.\" 내가 그녀 앞에 차분히 서서 말했다. 고모님은 내 어깨를 토닥이고는 내 의자에 앉아 있었다. \"애그니스의 그 사람 얘기 말이에요.\""]], [["She looked up in my face a little while, before replying:", "고모님은 대답하기에 앞서 잠시 내 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["‘I think I do, Trot.’", "\"있는 것 같다, 트롯.\""]], [["‘Are you confirmed in your impression?’ I inquired.", "\"짐작하신 게 굳어지셨어요?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I think I am, Trot.’", "\"그런 것 같구나, 트롯.\""]], [["She looked so steadfastly at me: with a kind of doubt, or pity, or suspense in her affection: that I summoned the stronger determination to show her a perfectly cheerful face.", "고모님은 어찌나 한결같이 나를 바라보던지, 그 애정 속에 어쩐지 의구심인지 연민인지 조마조마함 같은 것이 어려 있어서, 나는 그녀에게 더없이 환한 얼굴을 보여 주려고 더 굳은 결심을 끌어모았다."]], [["‘And what is more, Trot--’ said my aunt.", "\"게다가 말이다, 트롯—\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes!’", "\"네!\""]], [["‘I think Agnes is going to be married.’", "\"애그니스가 곧 결혼할 것 같구나.\""]], [["‘God bless her!’ said I, cheerfully.", "\"하느님의 축복이 그 애에게 깃들기를!\" 내가 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["‘God bless her!’ said my aunt, ‘and her husband too!’", "\"그 애에게 축복을!\" 고모님이 말했다. \"그리고 그 신랑에게도!\""]], [["I echoed it, parted from my aunt, and went lightly downstairs, mounted, and rode away.", "나는 그 말을 따라 외고는, 고모님과 작별하고 가벼운 걸음으로 아래층에 내려와 말에 올라 떠났다."], ["There was greater reason than before to do what I had resolved to do.", "내가 마음먹은 일을 해야 할 까닭이 전보다 더 커진 것이다."]], [["How well I recollect the wintry ride! The frozen particles of ice, brushed from the blades of grass by the wind, and borne across my face; the hard clatter of the horse’s hoofs, beating a tune upon the ground; the stiff-tilled soil; the snowdrift, lightly eddying in the chalk-pit as the breeze ruffled it; the smoking team with the waggon of old hay, stopping to breathe on the hill-top, and shaking their bells musically; the whitened slopes and sweeps of Down-land lying against the dark sky, as if they were drawn on a huge slate!", "그 겨울날의 말타기를 나는 어찌 이리 또렷이 기억하는지! 바람에 풀잎에서 떨려 나와 내 얼굴을 가로질러 날아드는 얼음 알갱이들, 땅 위에 가락을 두드리는 말발굽의 딱딱한 또각또각 소리, 빳빳하게 갈아엎은 흙, 산들바람이 휘저을 때마다 백악 채굴터에서 가볍게 소용돌이치는 눈더미, 마른 묵은 풀을 실은 짐마차를 끌고 언덕 꼭대기에서 숨을 고르며 방울을 가락 맞춰 흔들어 대던 김 모락모락 나는 말 떼, 그리고 거대한 석판 위에 그려 놓기라도 한 듯 어두운 하늘을 등지고 누운 다운랜드의 허옇게 덮인 비탈과 굽이들!"]], [["I found Agnes alone.", "애그니스는 혼자 있었다."], ["The little girls had gone to their own homes now, and she was alone by the fire, reading.", "어린 여자아이들은 이제 다들 제 집으로 돌아갔고, 그녀는 난롯가에 홀로 앉아 책을 읽고 있었다."], ["She put down her book on seeing me come in; and having welcomed me as usual, took her work-basket and sat in one of the old-fashioned windows.", "내가 들어서는 것을 보자 그녀는 책을 내려놓았다. 그러고는 여느 때처럼 나를 맞이한 뒤, 반짇고리를 들고 와 그 고풍스러운 창문 가운데 하나에 앉았다."]], [["I sat beside her on the window-seat, and we talked of what I was doing, and when it would be done, and of the progress I had made since my last visit.", "나는 창가 자리에 그녀 곁에 앉았고, 우리는 내가 지금 하고 있는 일이며, 그게 언제쯤 끝날지며, 지난번에 들른 뒤로 내가 얼마나 진척을 보았는지를 이야기했다."], ["Agnes was very cheerful; and laughingly predicted that I should soon become too famous to be talked to, on such subjects.", "애그니스는 무척 명랑했다. 그러더니 웃으며, 내가 곧 너무 유명해져서 이런 이야기는 나누기 어려워질 거라고 점쳤다."]], [["‘So I make the most of the present time, you see,’ said Agnes, ‘and talk to you while I may.’", "\"그러니까 보다시피 난 지금 이 순간을 한껏 누리는 거예요.\" 애그니스가 말했다. \"그럴 수 있을 때 당신이랑 이야기를 나누면서요.\""]], [["As I looked at her beautiful face, observant of her work, she raised her mild clear eyes, and saw that I was looking at her.", "내가 그녀의 아름다운 얼굴을 바라보며 그녀가 일감에 정신을 쏟는 모습을 지켜보고 있는데, 그녀가 그 부드럽고 맑은 눈을 들어 올리더니, 내가 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘You are thoughtful today, Trotwood!’", "\"오늘 무슨 생각에 잠겨 있군요, 트롯우드!\""]], [["‘Agnes, shall I tell you what about?", "\"애그니스, 무슨 생각인지 말해 줄까요?"], ["I came to tell you.’", "그 얘기를 하려고 왔어요.\""]], [["She put aside her work, as she was used to do when we were seriously discussing anything; and gave me her whole attention.", "그녀는 우리가 무언가 진지하게 의논할 때면 늘 그러했듯이 일감을 옆으로 치워 놓고, 온 마음을 다해 내게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["‘My dear Agnes, do you doubt my being true to you?’", "\"사랑하는 애그니스, 내가 당신에게 진실하다는 걸 의심하나요?\""]], [["‘No!’ she answered, with a look of astonishment.", "\"아니요!\" 그녀가 놀란 눈빛으로 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you doubt my being what I always have been to you?’", "\"내가 당신에게 늘 그랬던 그 모습 그대로라는 걸 의심하나요?\""]], [["‘No!’ she answered, as before.", "\"아니요!\" 그녀가 아까처럼 대답했다."]], [["‘Do you remember that I tried to tell you, when I came home, what a debt of gratitude I owed you, dearest Agnes, and how fervently I felt towards you?’", "\"내가 집에 돌아왔을 때, 당신에게 진 그 감사의 빚이 얼마나 깊은지, 그리고 당신을 향한 내 마음이 얼마나 뜨거운지 말하려 애썼던 거, 기억해요, 더없이 소중한 애그니스?\""]], [["‘I remember it,’ she said, gently, ‘very well.’", "\"기억해요.\" 그녀가 부드럽게 말했다. \"아주 잘 기억해요.\""]], [["‘You have a secret,’ said I.", "\"당신에겐 비밀이 하나 있죠.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘Let me share it, Agnes.’", "\"그 비밀을 나도 나누게 해 줘요, 애그니스.\""]], [["She cast down her eyes, and trembled.", "그녀는 눈을 내리깔며 떨었다."]], [["‘I could hardly fail to know, even if I had not heard--but from other lips than yours, Agnes, which seems strange--that there is someone upon whom you have bestowed the treasure of your love.", "\"내가 알아채지 못할 리가 없었어요. 설령 듣지 못했더라도요. 그런데 당신 입이 아닌 다른 사람의 입으로 들었다는 게 이상하긴 하지만요, 애그니스, 당신이 사랑이라는 보물을 바친 누군가가 있다는 걸요."], ["Do not shut me out of what concerns your happiness so nearly!", "당신의 행복과 그토록 깊이 맞닿은 일에서 나를 밀어내지 말아요!"], ["If you can trust me, as you say you can, and as I know you may, let me be your friend, your brother, in this matter, of all others!’", "당신이 말한 대로, 그리고 내가 알기에도 그래도 되는 대로, 정말 나를 믿어 준다면, 다른 무엇보다 바로 이 일에서, 내가 당신의 벗이 되고 오라비가 되게 해 줘요!\""]], [["With an appealing, almost a reproachful, glance, she rose from the window; and hurrying across the room as if without knowing where, put her hands before her face, and burst into such tears as smote me to the heart.", "호소하는 듯한, 거의 나무라는 듯한 눈빛으로 그녀는 창가에서 일어섰다. 그러고는 어디로 가는지도 모르는 사람처럼 황급히 방을 가로지르더니, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고, 내 가슴을 후벼 파는 그런 눈물을 터뜨렸다."]], [["And yet they awakened something in me, bringing promise to my heart.", "그런데도 그 눈물은 내 안의 무언가를 깨워, 내 가슴에 어떤 약속을 가져다주었다."], ["Without my knowing why, these tears allied themselves with the quietly sad smile which was so fixed in my remembrance, and shook me more with hope than fear or sorrow.", "왜인지도 모른 채, 그 눈물은 내 기억 속에 그토록 또렷이 박혀 있는 그 조용하고 서글픈 미소와 한데 어우러졌고, 두려움이나 슬픔보다는 희망으로 나를 더 크게 뒤흔들었다."]], [["‘Agnes! Sister!", "\"애그니스! 누이!"], ["Dearest! What have I done?’", "더없이 소중한 사람! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거죠?\""]], [["‘Let me go away, Trotwood.", "\"나가게 해 줘요, 트롯우드."], ["I am not well.", "몸이 좀 안 좋아요."], ["I am not myself.", "제정신이 아니에요."], ["I will speak to you by and by--another time.", "나중에 얘기할게요. 다른 때에요."], ["I will write to you.", "편지로 쓸게요."], ["Don’t speak to me now. Don’t! don’t!’", "지금은 내게 말 걸지 말아요. 제발! 제발!\""]], [["I sought to recollect what she had said, when I had spoken to her on that former night, of her affection needing no return.", "나는 지난날 그 밤에 그녀에게 말을 걸었을 때, 자기 사랑은 보답을 바라지 않는다고 그녀가 했던 말을 떠올려 보려 했다."], ["It seemed a very world that I must search through in a moment.", "한순간에 샅샅이 뒤져야 할 세상이 통째로 펼쳐진 것만 같았다."], ["‘Agnes, I cannot bear to see you so, and think that I have been the cause.", "\"애그니스, 당신이 이러는 걸 차마 볼 수가 없어요. 더구나 그게 나 때문이라고 생각하니."], ["My dearest girl, dearer to me than anything in life, if you are unhappy, let me share your unhappiness.", "더없이 소중한 사람, 내게 이 세상 그 무엇보다 소중한 사람, 당신이 불행하다면 그 불행을 나도 나누게 해 줘요."], ["If you are in need of help or counsel, let me try to give it to you.", "당신에게 도움이나 조언이 필요하다면, 내가 그걸 주도록 애쓰게 해 줘요."], ["If you have indeed a burden on your heart, let me try to lighten it.", "정말 당신 가슴에 무거운 짐이 있다면, 내가 그걸 덜어 주도록 애쓰게 해 줘요."], ["For whom do I live now, Agnes, if it is not for you!’", "이제 내가 누구를 위해 사나요, 애그니스, 당신을 위해서가 아니라면!\""]], [["‘Oh, spare me!", "\"오, 나를 좀 봐줘요!"], ["I am not myself!", "제정신이 아니에요!"], ["Another time!’ was all I could distinguish.", "다른 때에요!\" 내가 알아들을 수 있는 말은 그게 전부였다."]], [["Was it a selfish error that was leading me away?", "나를 잘못된 길로 이끄는 것은 이기적인 착각일까?"], ["Or, having once a clue to hope, was there something opening to me that I had not dared to think of?", "아니면 한번 희망의 실마리를 붙잡고 나니, 감히 떠올려 본 적도 없던 무언가가 내게 열리고 있는 것일까?"]], [["‘I must say more.", "\"더 말해야겠어요."], ["I cannot let you leave me so!", "당신을 이렇게 그냥 보낼 수는 없어요!"], ["For Heaven’s sake, Agnes, let us not mistake each other after all these years, and all that has come and gone with them!", "제발, 애그니스, 이 숱한 세월이 흐른 끝에, 그 세월과 함께 오고 간 그 모든 일을 겪은 끝에, 우리 서로를 오해하지는 맙시다!"], ["I must speak plainly.", "솔직히 말해야겠어요."], ["If you have any lingering thought that I could envy the happiness you will confer; that I could not resign you to a dearer protector, of your own choosing; that I could not, from my removed place, be a contented witness of your joy; dismiss it, for I don’t deserve it!", "혹시라도 당신 마음에, 당신이 베풀 그 행복을 내가 시샘하리라는 생각이, 당신 스스로 고른 더 소중한 보호자에게 당신을 내어주지 못하리라는 생각이, 한발 물러선 내 자리에서 당신의 기쁨을 흐뭇하게 지켜보지 못하리라는 생각이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 떨쳐 버려요. 난 그런 의심을 받을 사람이 아니니까요!"], ["I have not suffered quite in vain.", "나는 그저 헛되이 괴로워한 게 아니에요."], ["You have not taught me quite in vain.", "당신도 그저 헛되이 나를 가르친 게 아니에요."], ["There is no alloy of self in what I feel for you.’", "당신을 향한 내 마음에는 사사로운 이기심이 한 점도 섞여 있지 않아요.\""]], [["She was quiet now.", "이제 그녀는 잠잠해졌다."], ["In a little time, she turned her pale face towards me, and said in a low voice, broken here and there, but very clear:", "잠시 뒤, 그녀는 창백한 얼굴을 내게로 돌리더니, 군데군데 끊기긴 했으나 아주 또렷한 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I owe it to your pure friendship for me, Trotwood--which, indeed, I do not doubt--to tell you, you are mistaken.", "\"나를 향한 당신의 순수한 우정에 대한 도리로 말하는 거예요, 트롯우드. 그 우정은, 정말이지, 나도 의심하지 않아요."], ["I can do no more.", "당신이 잘못 알고 있다고요."], ["If I have sometimes, in the course of years, wanted help and counsel, they have come to me.", "그 이상은 말할 수 없어요. 세월이 흐르는 동안 더러 도움과 조언이 필요했을 때, 그것들은 내게 찾아와 주었어요."], ["If I have sometimes been unhappy, the feeling has passed away.", "더러 불행했을 때, 그 감정은 지나가 버렸어요."], ["If I have ever had a burden on my heart, it has been lightened for me.", "가슴에 무거운 짐을 진 적이 있다 해도, 누군가 그걸 덜어 주었어요."], ["If I have any secret, it is--no new one; and is--not what you suppose.", "내게 비밀이 있다면, 그건 새로운 게 아니에요. 그리고 당신이 짐작하는 그런 것도 아니에요."], ["I cannot reveal it, or divide it.", "나는 그걸 드러낼 수도, 나눌 수도 없어요."], ["It has long been mine, and must remain mine.’", "그건 오랫동안 내 것이었고, 앞으로도 내 것으로 남아야 해요.\""]], [["‘Agnes! Stay! A moment!’", "\"애그니스! 가지 말아요! 잠깐만!\""]], [["She was going away, but I detained her.", "그녀가 떠나려 했으나 나는 그녀를 붙들었다."], ["I clasped my arm about her waist.", "나는 한 팔로 그녀의 허리를 감았다."], ["‘In the course of years!’ ‘It is not a new one!’ New thoughts and hopes were whirling through my mind, and all the colours of my life were changing.", "'세월이 흐르는 동안!' '그건 새로운 게 아니에요!' 새로운 생각과 희망이 내 마음속을 소용돌이쳤고, 내 인생의 온갖 빛깔이 바뀌어 가고 있었다."]], [["‘Dearest Agnes!", "\"더없이 소중한 애그니스!"], ["Whom I so respect and honour--whom I so devotedly love!", "내가 그토록 우러르고 떠받드는 사람, 그토록 한결같이 사랑하는 사람!"], ["When I came here today, I thought that nothing could have wrested this confession from me.", "오늘 이곳에 올 때만 해도, 나는 그 무엇도 이 고백을 내게서 끄집어낼 수 없으리라 여겼어요."], ["I thought I could have kept it in my bosom all our lives, till we were old.", "우리가 늙을 때까지, 평생을 이 마음을 가슴속에만 묻어 둘 수 있으리라 여겼어요."], ["But, Agnes, if I have indeed any new-born hope that I may ever call you something more than Sister, widely different from Sister!--’", "하지만, 애그니스, 만약 내게 정말로, 언젠가 당신을 누이보다 더한, 누이와는 한참 다른 무언가로 부를 수 있으리라는 갓 태어난 희망이 조금이라도 있다면—\""]], [["Her tears fell fast; but they were not like those she had lately shed, and I saw my hope brighten in them.", "그녀의 눈물이 후두두 떨어졌다. 그러나 그것은 조금 전에 흘리던 눈물과는 달랐고, 나는 그 눈물 속에서 내 희망이 환히 밝아 오는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Agnes!", "\"애그니스!"], ["Ever my guide, and best support! If you had been more mindful of yourself, and less of me, when we grew up here together, I think my heedless fancy never would have wandered from you.", "늘 나를 이끌어 준 길잡이이자 더없이 든든한 버팀목! 우리가 여기서 함께 자라던 시절에, 당신이 나를 덜 챙기고 자신을 좀 더 챙겼더라면, 내 그 철없는 마음은 결코 당신에게서 떠돌지 않았을 거예요."], ["But you were so much better than I, so necessary to me in every boyish hope and disappointment, that to have you to confide in, and rely upon in everything, became a second nature, supplanting for the time the first and greater one of loving you as I do!’", "하지만 당신은 나보다 너무도 나은 사람이었고, 소년 시절의 온갖 희망과 좌절 속에서 내게 너무도 없어서는 안 될 사람이어서, 당신을 의지하고 매사에 당신에게 기대는 일이 어느새 제2의 천성이 되어 버렸어요. 한동안은 지금 내가 당신을 사랑하는 그 첫째가는, 더 큰 천성을 밀어내 버릴 만큼요!\""]], [["Still weeping, but not sadly--joyfully!", "여전히 울고 있었으나, 슬픈 울음이 아니었다. 기쁨의 눈물이었다!"], ["And clasped in my arms as she had never been, as I had thought she never was to be!", "그리고 내 품에 안겨 있었다. 한 번도 안겨 본 적 없던 모습으로, 결코 안길 일이 없으리라 여겼던 모습으로!"]], [["‘When I loved Dora--fondly, Agnes, as you know--’", "\"내가 도라를 사랑했을 때, 애그니스, 당신도 알다시피 애틋하게 사랑했을 때—\""]], [["‘Yes!’ she cried, earnestly.", "\"네!\" 그녀가 간절하게 외쳤다."], ["‘I am glad to know it!’", "\"그걸 알게 되어 기뻐요!\""]], [["‘When I loved her--even then, my love would have been incomplete, without your sympathy. I had it, and it was perfected.", "\"내가 그녀를 사랑했을 때, 그때조차도, 당신의 공감이 없었다면 내 사랑은 온전치 못했을 거예요. 나는 그 공감을 받았고, 그래서 내 사랑은 완전해졌어요."], ["And when I lost her, Agnes, what should I have been without you, still!’", "그리고 그녀를 잃었을 때, 애그니스, 당신이 없었다면 나는 또 어찌 되었을까요!\""]], [["Closer in my arms, nearer to my heart, her trembling hand upon my shoulder, her sweet eyes shining through her tears, on mine!", "내 품에 더 바싹, 내 가슴에 더 가까이, 그녀의 떨리는 손이 내 어깨에 얹히고, 눈물 너머로 빛나는 그 사랑스러운 눈이 내 눈을 마주했다!"]], [["‘I went away, dear Agnes, loving you.", "\"나는 당신을 사랑하며 떠났어요, 사랑하는 애그니스."], ["I stayed away, loving you.", "당신을 사랑하며 멀리 머물렀어요."], ["I returned home, loving you!’", "당신을 사랑하며 집으로 돌아왔어요!\""]], [["And now, I tried to tell her of the struggle I had had, and the conclusion I had come to.", "그리고 이제 나는 내가 겪어 온 갈등과, 내가 다다른 결론을 그녀에게 들려주려 애썼다."], ["I tried to lay my mind before her, truly, and entirely.", "내 마음을 그녀 앞에 참되게, 그리고 송두리째 펼쳐 보이려 애썼다."], ["I tried to show her how I had hoped I had come into the better knowledge of myself and of her; how I had resigned myself to what that better knowledge brought; and how I had come there, even that day, in my fidelity to this.", "내가 어떻게 나 자신과 그녀를 더 잘 알게 되었기를 바랐는지, 그 더 깊은 깨달음이 가져다준 것에 어떻게 스스로를 내맡겼는지, 그리고 바로 그날 이곳에 올 때조차 그 깨달음에 충실하고자 어떻게 왔는지를 보여 주려 애썼다."], ["If she did so love me (I said) that she could take me for her husband, she could do so, on no deserving of mine, except upon the truth of my love for her, and the trouble in which it had ripened to be what it was; and hence it was that I revealed it.", "만약 그녀가 정말로 나를 사랑하여 나를 남편으로 맞이할 수 있다면(내가 말했다), 그것은 내가 무슨 자격이 있어서가 아니라, 오직 그녀를 향한 내 사랑의 진실함과, 그 사랑이 지금의 모습으로 무르익기까지 겪은 시련 덕분일 뿐이며, 바로 그래서 내가 이 마음을 드러내는 것이라고."], ["And O, Agnes, even out of thy true eyes, in that same time, the spirit of my child-wife looked upon me, saying it was well; and winning me, through thee, to tenderest recollections of the Blossom that had withered in its bloom!", "그리고 오, 애그니스, 그 순간 바로 그대의 진실한 눈 속에서도, 내 아이 같던 아내의 영혼이 나를 굽어보며 잘했다고 말하고 있었으니, 그대를 통하여, 채 피어 보지도 못한 채 시들어 버린 그 꽃송이에 대한 더없이 애틋한 추억으로 나를 이끌고 있었다!"]], [["‘I am so blest, Trotwood--my heart is so overcharged--but there is one thing I must say.’\n‘Dearest, what?’", "\"나는 너무나 복받은 사람이에요, 트롯우드. 가슴이 벅차서 터질 것만 같아요. 그런데 꼭 해야 할 말이 하나 있어요.\""]], [["She laid her gentle hands upon my shoulders, and looked calmly in my face.", "\"더없이 소중한 사람, 무슨 말인가요?\" 그녀는 그 부드러운 두 손을 내 어깨에 얹고, 차분히 내 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["‘Do you know, yet, what it is?’", "\"이게 무슨 말인지, 이제 알겠어요?\""]], [["‘I am afraid to speculate on what it is. Tell me, my dear.’", "\"무슨 말일지 감히 짐작이 안 돼요. 말해 줘요, 사랑하는 사람.\""]], [["‘I have loved you all my life!’", "\"나는 평생토록 당신을 사랑해 왔어요!\""]], [["O, we were happy, we were happy!", "오, 우리는 행복했다, 우리는 행복했다!"], ["Our tears were not for the trials (hers so much the greater) through which we had come to be thus, but for the rapture of being thus, never to be divided more!", "우리의 눈물은 우리가 이렇게 되기까지 헤쳐 온 시련(그녀의 시련이 훨씬 더 컸다) 때문이 아니라, 이렇게 되어, 다시는 갈라서지 않으리라는 그 황홀함 때문이었다!"]], [["We walked, that winter evening, in the fields together; and the blessed calm within us seemed to be partaken by the frosty air.", "그 겨울 저녁, 우리는 들판을 함께 거닐었다. 우리 안에 깃든 그 복된 고요를 서리 어린 공기마저 함께 나누는 것만 같았다."], ["The early stars began to shine while we were lingering on, and looking up to them, we thanked our GOD for having guided us to this tranquillity.", "우리가 그렇게 거니는 동안 초저녁 별들이 빛나기 시작했고, 그 별들을 올려다보며 우리는 이 평온함으로 우리를 이끌어 주신 하느님께 감사를 드렸다."]], [["We stood together in the same old-fashioned window at night, when the moon was shining; Agnes with her quiet eyes raised up to it; I following her glance.", "밤이 되어 달이 빛날 때, 우리는 그 고풍스러운 옛 창가에 함께 섰다. 애그니스는 그 고요한 눈을 들어 달을 올려다보았고, 나는 그녀의 시선을 따라갔다."], ["Long miles of road then opened out before my mind; and, toiling on, I saw a ragged way-worn boy, forsaken and neglected, who should come to call even the heart now beating against mine, his own.", "그때 내 마음속에는 아득히 먼 길이 펼쳐졌다. 그 길을 힘겹게 걸어가는, 누덕누덕 해진 옷에 여독에 찌든 한 소년이 보였다. 버림받고 내팽개쳐진 그 소년이, 언젠가 지금 내 가슴에 맞닿아 뛰고 있는 이 심장마저 제 것이라 부르게 될 터였다."]], [["It was nearly dinner-time next day when we appeared before my aunt.", "이튿날 우리가 고모님 앞에 나타났을 때는 거의 저녁 먹을 무렵이었다."], ["She was up in my study, Peggotty said: which it was her pride to keep in readiness and order for me.", "고모님은 위층 내 서재에 있다고 패거티가 말했다. 그 서재를 나를 위해 늘 준비해 두고 가지런히 정돈해 두는 것이 패거티의 자랑거리였다."], ["We found her, in her spectacles, sitting by the fire.", "우리가 가 보니 고모님은 안경을 쓴 채 난롯가에 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Goodness me!’ said my aunt, peering through the dusk, ‘who’s this you’re bringing home?’", "\"아이고 세상에!\" 고모님이 어스름 속을 들여다보며 말했다. \"집에 데려온 이 사람은 누구냐?\""]], [["‘Agnes,’ said I.", "\"애그니스예요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["As we had arranged to say nothing at first, my aunt was not a little discomfited.", "처음에는 아무 말도 하지 않기로 미리 짜 둔 터라, 고모님은 적잖이 당황했다."], ["She darted a hopeful glance at me, when I said ‘Agnes’; but seeing that I looked as usual, she took off her spectacles in despair, and rubbed her nose with them.", "내가 \"애그니스\"라고 말하자 고모님은 내게 기대 어린 눈길을 휙 던졌다. 그러나 내가 여느 때와 다름없어 보이자, 절망한 듯 안경을 벗어 그것으로 코를 문질렀다."]], [["She greeted Agnes heartily, nevertheless; and we were soon in the lighted parlour downstairs, at dinner.", "그래도 고모님은 애그니스를 정답게 맞이했다. 우리는 곧 아래층의 불 켜진 거실에서 저녁을 들었다."], ["My aunt put on her spectacles twice or thrice, to take another look at me, but as often took them off again, disappointed, and rubbed her nose with them.", "고모님은 나를 다시 한번 살펴보려고 두세 번 안경을 썼다가, 그때마다 실망한 듯 다시 벗어 그것으로 코를 문질렀다."], ["Much to the discomfiture of Mr. Dick, who knew this to be a bad symptom.", "이를 좋지 않은 징조로 여기던 딕 씨가 적잖이 마음을 졸였다."]], [["‘By the by, aunt,’ said I, after dinner; ‘I have been speaking to Agnes about what you told me.’", "\"참, 그런데 고모님.\" 저녁을 들고 나서 내가 말했다. \"고모님이 말씀해 주신 그 얘기를 애그니스한테 했어요.\""]], [["‘Then, Trot,’ said my aunt, turning scarlet, ‘you did wrong, and broke your promise.’", "\"그렇담, 트롯.\" 고모님이 새빨개지며 말했다. \"넌 잘못한 거다. 약속을 어겼어.\""]], [["‘You are not angry, aunt, I trust?", "\"화내신 건 아니죠, 고모님?"], ["I am sure you won’t be, when you learn that Agnes is not unhappy in any attachment.’", "안 그러실 거예요. 애그니스가 그 어떤 사람을 향한 마음 때문에 불행한 게 아니라는 걸 아시면요.\""]], [["‘Stuff and nonsense!’ said my aunt.", "\"무슨 헛소리, 말도 안 되는 소리!\" 고모님이 말했다."]], [["As my aunt appeared to be annoyed, I thought the best way was to cut her annoyance short.", "고모님이 짜증 난 듯 보여서, 나는 그 짜증을 단번에 잘라 버리는 게 상책이겠다 싶었다."], ["I took Agnes in my arm to the back of her chair, and we both leaned over her.", "나는 애그니스를 한 팔로 안아 고모님 의자 뒤로 데려갔고, 우리 둘은 함께 고모님 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["My aunt, with one clap of her hands, and one look through her spectacles, immediately went into hysterics, for the first and only time in all my knowledge of her.", "고모님은 두 손을 한 번 탁 마주치고 안경 너머로 한 번 바라보더니, 그 자리에서 발작하듯 까무러쳤다. 내가 아는 한 처음이자 단 한 번뿐인 일이었다."]], [["The hysterics called up Peggotty.", "그 발작에 패거티가 달려왔다."], ["The moment my aunt was restored, she flew at Peggotty, and calling her a silly old creature, hugged her with all her might.", "고모님은 정신을 차리자마자 패거티에게 달려들어, 이 어리석은 늙은이라고 부르면서 있는 힘껏 끌어안았다."], ["After that, she hugged Mr. Dick (who was highly honoured, but a good deal surprised); and after that, told them why.", "그러고 나서 딕 씨를 끌어안았는데(딕 씨는 무척 영광스러워하면서도 적잖이 놀랐다), 그다음에야 두 사람에게 까닭을 일러 주었다."], ["Then, we were all happy together.", "그리하여 우리는 다 함께 행복했다."]], [["I could not discover whether my aunt, in her last short conversation with me, had fallen on a pious fraud, or had really mistaken the state of my mind.", "지난번 나와 짧게 나눈 그 대화에서 고모님이 갸륵한 거짓말을 둘러댄 것인지, 아니면 정말로 내 마음 상태를 잘못 알았던 것인지는 끝내 알아낼 수 없었다."], ["It was quite enough, she said, that she had told me Agnes was going to be married; and that I now knew better than anyone how true it was.", "애그니스가 곧 결혼할 거라고 내게 말해 준 것이면 충분하다고 고모님은 말했다. 그리고 그게 얼마나 들어맞는 말이었는지는 이제 내가 누구보다 잘 알지 않느냐고."]], [["We were married within a fortnight.", "우리는 두 주가 채 못 되어 결혼했다."], ["Traddles and Sophy, and Doctor and Mrs. Strong, were the only guests at our quiet wedding.", "트래들스와 소피, 그리고 스트롱 박사 부부가 우리의 조촐한 결혼식에 온 손님의 전부였다."], ["We left them full of joy; and drove away together.", "우리는 그들을 기쁨에 겨운 채로 두고, 함께 마차를 타고 떠났다."], ["Clasped in my embrace, I held the source of every worthy aspiration I had ever had; the centre of myself, the circle of my life, my own, my wife; my love of whom was founded on a rock!", "내 품에 꼭 안긴 채로, 나는 내가 품어 온 모든 값진 열망의 샘을 안고 있었다. 나 자신의 중심이요, 내 인생의 둘레요, 내 사람이요, 내 아내인 그녀를. 그녀를 향한 내 사랑은 반석 위에 세워진 것이었다!"]], [["‘Dearest husband!’ said Agnes.", "\"더없이 소중한 남편!\" 애그니스가 말했다."], ["‘Now that I may call you by that name, I have one thing more to tell you.’", "\"이제 당신을 그 이름으로 부를 수 있게 되었으니, 한 가지 더 말할 게 있어요.\""]], [["‘Let me hear it, love.’", "\"들려줘요, 사랑하는 사람.\""]], [["‘It grows out of the night when Dora died. She sent you for me.’", "\"도라가 세상을 떠나던 그 밤에서 비롯된 거예요. 그녀가 나를 데려오라고 당신을 보냈잖아요.\""]], [["‘She did.’", "\"그랬죠.\""]], [["‘She told me that she left me something.", "\"그녀가 내게 무언가를 남긴다고 말했어요."], ["Can you think what it was?’", "그게 뭐였을지 짐작이 가요?\""]], [["I believed I could.", "나는 짐작할 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["I drew the wife who had so long loved me, closer to my side.", "나는 그토록 오래 나를 사랑해 온 아내를 내 곁으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼다."]], [["‘She told me that she made a last request to me, and left me a last charge.’", "\"그녀가 내게 마지막 부탁을 하고, 마지막 당부를 남긴다고 했어요.\""]], [["‘And it was--’", "\"그게—\""]], [["‘That only I would occupy this vacant place.’", "\"오직 나만이 이 빈자리를 채워 달라는 거였어요.\""]], [["And Agnes laid her head upon my breast, and wept; and I wept with her, though we were so happy.", "그러고 나서 애그니스는 내 가슴에 머리를 묻고 울었다. 우리는 그토록 행복했건만, 나도 그녀와 함께 울었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 63. A VISITOR", "# CHAPTER 63. A VISITOR"]], [["What I have purposed to record is nearly finished; but there is yet an incident conspicuous in my memory, on which it often rests with delight, and without which one thread in the web I have spun would have a ravelled end.", "내가 적어 두기로 마음먹은 이야기는 거의 다 끝나 간다. 그러나 아직 내 기억 속에 또렷이 남아, 떠올릴 때마다 즐거운 마음으로 머무르게 되는 한 사건이 남아 있다. 이것이 없다면 내가 짜 온 이야기의 그물에서 한 가닥 실이 풀린 채 끝을 맺게 될 것이다."]], [["I had advanced in fame and fortune, my domestic joy was perfect, I had been married ten happy years.", "나는 명성과 재산에서 앞으로 나아갔고, 가정의 기쁨은 더없이 완전했으며, 행복한 결혼 생활을 십 년이나 누렸다."], ["Agnes and I were sitting by the fire, in our house in London, one night in spring, and three of our children were playing in the room, when I was told that a stranger wished to see me.", "어느 봄날 밤, 애그니스와 나는 런던 우리 집 난롯가에 앉아 있었고, 우리 아이들 셋이 방 안에서 놀고 있었는데, 그때 누군가 낯선 사람이 나를 만나고 싶어 한다는 전갈이 왔다."]], [["He had been asked if he came on business, and had answered No; he had come for the pleasure of seeing me, and had come a long way.", "볼일이 있어 온 것이냐고 물었더니 아니라고 대답했단다. 그저 나를 보는 즐거움 때문에 왔으며, 먼 길을 왔다고 했다."], ["He was an old man, my servant said, and looked like a farmer.", "하인의 말로는, 나이 든 노인인데 농부처럼 보인다고 했다."]], [["As this sounded mysterious to the children, and moreover was like the beginning of a favourite story Agnes used to tell them, introductory to the arrival of a wicked old Fairy in a cloak who hated everybody, it produced some commotion.", "이 말이 아이들에게는 어쩐지 알쏭달쏭하게 들린 데다, 애그니스가 늘 들려주던, 모두를 미워하는 망토 두른 심술궂은 늙은 요정이 등장하는 이야기의 첫머리와 비슷했던 터라, 작은 소동이 일었다."], ["One of our boys laid his head in his mother’s lap to be out of harm’s way, and little Agnes (our eldest child) left her doll in a chair to represent her, and thrust out her little heap of golden curls from between the window-curtains, to see what happened next.", "우리 아들 하나는 화를 피하려는 듯 어머니 무릎에 머리를 파묻었고, 어린 애그니스(우리 맏이다)는 제 인형을 의자에 앉혀 자기 대신 세워 두고는, 다음에 무슨 일이 일어날지 보려고 황금빛 곱슬머리 뭉치를 창문 커튼 사이로 쑥 내밀었다."]], [["‘Let him come in here!’ said I.", "\"이리로 모셔 와라!\" 내가 말했다."]], [["There soon appeared, pausing in the dark doorway as he entered, a hale, grey-haired old man.", "이윽고 들어서면서 어두운 문간에 잠시 멈춰 선, 정정하고 머리가 희끗희끗한 노인이 나타났다."], ["Little Agnes, attracted by his looks, had run to bring him in, and I had not yet clearly seen his face, when my wife, starting up, cried out to me, in a pleased and agitated voice, that it was Mr. Peggotty!", "어린 애그니스가 그 모습에 끌려 그를 모셔 오려고 달려갔는데, 내가 미처 그의 얼굴을 또렷이 보기도 전에, 아내가 벌떡 일어나 기쁘면서도 들뜬 목소리로 외쳤다. 패거티 아저씨라고!"]], [["It WAS Mr. Peggotty.", "정말 패거티 아저씨였다."], ["An old man now, but in a ruddy, hearty, strong old age.", "이제는 노인이 되었으나, 혈색 좋고 다부지며 정정한 노년이었다."], ["When our first emotion was over, and he sat before the fire with the children on his knees, and the blaze shining on his face, he looked, to me, as vigorous and robust, withal as handsome, an old man, as ever I had seen.", "처음의 벅찬 감정이 가라앉고, 그가 아이들을 무릎에 앉힌 채 난롯불을 받으며 앉았을 때, 내 눈에 비친 그는 내가 여태껏 본 그 어떤 노인보다도 기운차고 튼튼했으며, 게다가 멋졌다."]], [["‘Mas’r Davy,’ said he.", "\"데이비 도련님.\" 그가 말했다."], ["And the old name in the old tone fell so naturally on my ear!", "옛 이름이 옛 말투에 실려 어찌나 자연스럽게 내 귀에 와닿던지!"], ["‘Mas’r Davy, ‘tis a joyful hour as I see you, once more, ‘long with your own trew wife!’", "\"데이비 도련님, 이렇게 다시 도련님을, 그것도 도련님의 어여쁜 마님과 함께 뵈니 참으로 기쁜 순간입니다요!\""]], [["‘A joyful hour indeed, old friend!’ cried I.", "\"정말로 기쁜 순간이오, 오랜 벗이여!\" 내가 외쳤다."]], [["‘And these heer pretty ones,’ said Mr.", "\"그리고 여기 이 예쁜 것들 좀 보십쇼.\""], ["Peggotty. ‘To look at these heer flowers!", "패거티 아저씨가 말했다. \"여기 이 꽃송이들을 좀 보세요!"], ["Why, Mas’r Davy, you was but the heighth of the littlest of these, when I first see you!", "아니, 데이비 도련님, 제가 처음 도련님을 뵈었을 때 도련님은 여기 이 제일 작은 아이만 한 키였잖습니까!"], ["When Em’ly warn’t no bigger, and our poor lad were BUT a lad!’", "에밀리도 그만큼밖에 안 컸고, 우리 가엾은 그 녀석도 한낱 어린애였을 때 말입니다요!\""]], [["‘Time has changed me more than it has changed you since then,’ said I.", "\"그때 이후로 세월은 아저씨보다 나를 더 많이 바꿔 놓았소.\" 내가 말했다."], ["‘But let these dear rogues go to bed; and as no house in England but this must hold you, tell me where to send for your luggage (is the old black bag among it, that went so far, I wonder!), and then, over a glass of Yarmouth grog, we will have the tidings of ten years!’", "\"그건 그렇고, 이 사랑스러운 개구쟁이들은 어서 재웁시다. 그리고 잉글랜드에서 이 집 말고는 어디서도 아저씨를 묵게 할 수 없으니, 짐을 어디로 가지러 보내면 될지 말해 주시오(그 먼 길을 함께 다닌 낡은 검정 가방도 그중에 있는지 궁금하구려!). 그런 다음 야머스식 그로그주 한잔 기울이면서 십 년 치 소식을 들어 봅시다!\""]], [["‘Are you alone?’ asked Agnes.", "\"혼자 오셨어요?\" 애그니스가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, ma’am,’ he said, kissing her hand, ‘quite alone.’", "\"예, 마님.\" 그가 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"아주 혼자입니다요.\""]], [["We sat him between us, not knowing how to give him welcome enough; and as I began to listen to his old familiar voice, I could have fancied he was still pursuing his long journey in search of his darling niece.", "우리는 그를 어떻게 맞이해야 흡족할지 몰라 둘 사이에 앉혔다. 그 정겹고 익숙한 목소리에 다시 귀 기울이기 시작하자, 나는 그가 아직도 사랑하는 조카딸을 찾아 머나먼 여정을 헤매고 있는 것만 같은 착각이 들었다."]], [["‘It’s a mort of water,’ said Mr.", "\"건너오기엔 어마어마한 물길이지요.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘fur to come across, and on’y stay a matter of fower weeks.", "패거티 아저씨가 말했다. \"그것도 고작 사 주쯤 머물려고 말입니다."], ["But water (‘specially when ‘tis salt) comes nat’ral to me; and friends is dear, and I am heer.", "하지만 물이란(특히 짠물은) 저한테는 천성처럼 익숙한 거고, 벗은 소중하니, 그래서 제가 여기 온 겁니다요."], ["--Which is verse,’ said Mr.", "―이거 운율이 맞네요.\""], ["Peggotty, surprised to find it out, ‘though I hadn’t such intentions.’", "패거티 아저씨는 스스로 그걸 깨닫고 놀라며 말했다. \"그럴 작정은 아니었는데도 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Are you going back those many thousand miles, so soon?’ asked Agnes.", "\"그 수천 마일 길을 그렇게 빨리 되돌아가신다고요?\" 애그니스가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, ma’am,’ he returned.", "\"예, 마님.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘I giv the promise to Em’ly, afore I come away.", "\"떠나오기 전에 에밀리한테 약속했거든요."], ["You see, I doen’t grow younger as the years comes round, and if I hadn’t sailed as ‘twas, most like I shouldn’t never have done ‘t.", "보세요, 해가 거듭될수록 저도 젊어지는 건 아니잖습니까. 그러니 그때 배를 타지 않았더라면 아마 영영 못 했을 겁니다."], ["And it’s allus been on my mind, as I must come and see Mas’r Davy and your own sweet blooming self, in your wedded happiness, afore I got to be too old.’", "그리고 늘 마음에 걸렸던 게, 제가 너무 늙어 버리기 전에 데이비 도련님과 마님의 어여쁘고 활짝 피어난 모습을, 그 결혼의 행복 속에서 한번 뵈러 와야겠다는 거였지요.\""]], [["He looked at us, as if he could never feast his eyes on us sufficiently.", "그는 우리를 바라보았는데, 아무리 봐도 그 눈이 우리를 다 담지 못해 못내 아쉬운 듯했다."], ["Agnes laughingly put back some scattered locks of his grey hair, that he might see us better.", "애그니스가 웃으며 그가 우리를 더 잘 볼 수 있도록 흐트러진 희끗한 머리칼 몇 가닥을 쓸어 넘겨 주었다."]], [["‘And now tell us,’ said I, ‘everything relating to your fortunes.’", "\"자, 이제 들려주세요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그동안 어떻게 지내셨는지 죄다요.\""]], [["‘Our fortuns, Mas’r Davy,’ he rejoined, ‘is soon told.", "\"저희가 어떻게 지냈는지는 말입니다, 데이비 도련님,\" 그가 받았다."], ["We haven’t fared nohows, but fared to thrive.", "\"금방 이야기됩니다. 저흰 그럭저럭이 아니라, 잘됐어요."], ["We’ve allus thrived.", "늘 번창했지요."], ["We’ve worked as we ought to ‘t, and maybe we lived a leetle hard at first or so, but we have allus thrived.", "마땅히 일해야 할 만큼 일했고, 처음 얼마간은 좀 고되게 살았을지 몰라도, 늘 번창했습니다."], ["What with sheep-farming, and what with stock-farming, and what with one thing and what with t’other, we are as well to do, as well could be.", "양 치는 일이며, 가축 치는 일이며, 이런저런 일이며 해서, 더 바랄 데 없이 잘 지내고 있어요."], ["Theer’s been kiender a blessing fell upon us,’ said Mr.", "어쩐지 저희한테 복이 내린 것 같습디다.\""], ["Peggotty, reverentially inclining his head, ‘and we’ve done nowt but prosper.", "패거티 아저씨가 경건하게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"저흰 그저 번창하기만 했답니다."], ["That is, in the long run.", "그러니까, 길게 보면요."], ["If not yesterday, why then today. If not today, why then tomorrow.’", "어제가 아니면 오늘, 오늘이 아니면 내일, 그렇게요.\""]], [["‘And Emily?’ said Agnes and I, both together.", "\"그래서 에밀리는요?\" 애그니스와 내가 동시에 물었다."]], [["‘Em’ly,’ said he, ‘arter you left her, ma’am--and I never heerd her saying of her prayers at night, t’other side the canvas screen, when we was settled in the Bush, but what I heerd your name--and arter she and me lost sight of Mas’r Davy, that theer shining sundown--was that low, at first, that, if she had know’d then what Mas’r Davy kep from us so kind and thowtful, ‘tis my opinion she’d have drooped away.", "\"에밀리는요,\" 그가 말했다. \"마님이 그 애 곁을 떠나신 뒤로―저희가 오지에 자리를 잡고 나서, 밤마다 칸막이 천 너머에서 그 애가 기도하는 소리를 들을 때면 늘 마님 이름이 들렸지요―그리고 그 애와 제가, 그 눈부신 노을 속에서 데이비 도련님을 마지막으로 뵌 뒤로는, 처음엔 어찌나 풀이 죽었던지, 만약 그 애가 데이비 도련님이 그토록 자상하고 사려 깊게 저희한테 숨겨 주신 일을 그때 알았더라면, 제 생각엔 그대로 시들어 버렸을 겁니다요."], ["But theer was some poor folks aboard as had illness among ‘em, and she took care of them; and theer was the children in our company, and she took care of them; and so she got to be busy, and to be doing good, and that helped her.’", "그런데 배 안에 병든 가난한 사람들이 좀 있어서 그 애가 그들을 돌봤고, 또 우리 일행 중에 아이들이 있어서 그들도 돌봤지요. 그렇게 바빠지고 좋은 일을 하다 보니, 그게 그 애한테 힘이 됐답니다.\""]], [["‘When did she first hear of it?’ I asked.", "\"그 애가 그걸 처음 안 게 언제였소?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I kep it from her arter I heerd on ‘t,’ said Mr.", "\"제가 그 소식을 듣고 나서도 그 애한텐 숨겼지요.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘going on nigh a year.", "패거티 아저씨가 말했다."], ["We was living then in a solitary place, but among the beautifullest trees, and with the roses a-covering our Beein to the roof.", "\"거의 한 해 가까이요. 그 무렵 저흰 외딴곳에 살았는데, 더없이 아름다운 나무들에 둘러싸여 있었고, 장미가 우리 오두막 지붕까지 덮고 있었지요."], ["Theer come along one day, when I was out a-working on the land, a traveller from our own Norfolk or Suffolk in England (I doen’t rightly mind which), and of course we took him in, and giv him to eat and drink, and made him welcome.", "어느 날 제가 밭에 나가 일하고 있는데, 잉글랜드 우리 노퍽인가 서퍽인가(어느 쪽인지는 똑똑히 기억이 안 납니다)에서 온 나그네 하나가 지나가지 뭡니까. 물론 저흰 그를 들이고 먹고 마실 것을 주며 반갑게 맞이했지요."], ["We all do that, all the colony over.", "그곳 이주민들은 다들 그렇게 합니다요, 식민지 어디서나요."], ["He’d got an old newspaper with him, and some other account in print of the storm.", "그 사람이 낡은 신문 한 장을 가지고 있었는데, 거기에 그 폭풍에 관한 다른 기사도 활자로 실려 있었어요."], ["That’s how she know’d it.", "그렇게 그 애가 알게 된 겁니다."], ["When I came home at night, I found she know’d it.’", "제가 밤에 집에 돌아오니, 그 애가 이미 알고 있더군요.\""]], [["He dropped his voice as he said these words, and the gravity I so well remembered overspread his face.", "이 말을 하면서 그는 목소리를 낮췄고, 내가 그토록 잘 기억하는 그 엄숙함이 그의 얼굴에 번졌다."]], [["‘Did it change her much?’ we asked.", "\"그 일로 그 애가 많이 변했나요?\" 우리가 물었다."]], [["‘Aye, for a good long time,’ he said, shaking his head; ‘if not to this present hour.", "\"예, 한참 동안요.\" 그가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"어쩌면 지금 이 순간까지도요."], ["But I think the solitoode done her good.", "하지만 제 생각엔 그 외로움이 그 애한테 약이 된 것 같습니다요."], ["And she had a deal to mind in the way of poultry and the like, and minded of it, and come through.", "그리고 닭 치는 일이며 그런 잔일거리가 많아서, 거기에 마음을 쏟다 보니 이겨 낸 거지요. 궁금하네요.\""], ["I wonder,’ he said thoughtfully, ‘if you could see my Em’ly now, Mas’r Davy, whether you’d know her!’", "그가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"지금 제 에밀리를 보시면, 데이비 도련님, 도련님이 그 애를 알아보실 수 있을지 말입니다!\""]], [["‘Is she so altered?’ I inquired.", "\"그렇게나 변했소?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘I doen’t know.", "\"저야 모르지요."], ["I see her ev’ry day, and doen’t know; But, odd-times, I have thowt so.", "날마다 보니 모르겠어요. 그런데 가끔은 그런 생각이 들기도 합니다."], ["A slight figure,’ said Mr.", "가냘픈 몸매에,\""], ["Peggotty, looking at the fire, ‘kiender worn; soft, sorrowful, blue eyes; a delicate face; a pritty head, leaning a little down; a quiet voice and way--timid a’most.", "패거티 아저씨가 난롯불을 바라보며 말했다. \"어쩐지 수척하고, 부드럽고 슬픔 어린 파란 눈에, 가녀린 얼굴, 살짝 숙인 예쁜 머리, 조용한 목소리와 몸가짐―거의 수줍은 듯한."], ["That’s Em’ly!’", "그게 에밀리입니다요!\""]], [["We silently observed him as he sat, still looking at the fire.", "우리는 그가 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며 앉아 있는 모습을 말없이 지켜보았다."]], [["‘Some thinks,’ he said, ‘as her affection was ill-bestowed; some, as her marriage was broken off by death.", "\"더러는 그러더군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애 정이 그릇된 사람한테 잘못 갔다고요."], ["No one knows how ‘tis.", "또 더러는 그 애 혼사가 죽음으로 깨졌다고 하고요."], ["She might have married well, a mort of times, “but, uncle,”", "어찌 된 영문인지는 아무도 모르지요. 그 애는 좋은 혼처에 시집갈 기회가 수없이 많았지만, '아저씨,'"], ["she says to me, “that’s gone for ever.”", "그 애가 저한테 그래요, '그건 영영 지나가 버린 일이에요' 하고요."], ["Cheerful along with me; retired when others is by; fond of going any distance fur to teach a child, or fur to tend a sick person, or fur to do some kindness tow’rds a young girl’s wedding (and she’s done a many, but has never seen one); fondly loving of her uncle; patient; liked by young and old; sowt out by all that has any trouble.", "저랑 있을 땐 명랑하다가도, 남이 곁에 있으면 뒤로 물러나지요. 멀리까지라도 마다 않고 가서 아이를 가르치거나, 병자를 보살피거나, 어느 처녀의 혼사를 거들어 주는 친절을 베푸는 걸 좋아하고요(그렇게 거든 혼사가 숱하지만, 정작 제 혼사는 한 번도 못 봤지요). 아저씨를 끔찍이 사랑하고, 참을성 있고, 젊은이든 늙은이든 다 그 애를 좋아하고, 무슨 어려움이 있는 사람이면 죄다 그 애를 찾는답니다."], ["That’s Em’ly!’", "그게 에밀리입니다요!\""]], [["He drew his hand across his face, and with a half-suppressed sigh looked up from the fire.", "그가 손으로 얼굴을 쓸어내리더니, 반쯤 삼킨 한숨을 내쉬며 난롯불에서 눈을 들었다."]], [["‘Is Martha with you yet?’ I asked.", "\"마사는 아직 함께 지내오?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Martha,’ he replied, ‘got married, Mas’r Davy, in the second year.", "\"마사는요,\" 그가 대답했다. \"둘째 해에 결혼했습니다, 데이비 도련님."], ["A young man, a farm-labourer, as come by us on his way to market with his mas’r’s drays--a journey of over five hundred mile, theer and back--made offers fur to take her fur his wife (wives is very scarce theer), and then to set up fur their two selves in the Bush.", "어느 젊은이가, 농장 일꾼이었는데, 제 주인의 짐마차를 끌고 장에 가는 길에 우리 곁을 지나갔지요―갔다 오는 데 오백 마일이 넘는 길이었어요―그 젊은이가 그 애한테 아내가 되어 달라고 청혼을 하더니(그곳엔 아내 될 여자가 아주 귀하거든요), 둘이서 오지에 따로 살림을 차리겠다고 했지요."], ["She spoke to me fur to tell him her trew story.", "그 애가 저한테, 그 사람한테 자기 지난 이야기를 다 일러 달라더군요."], ["I did. They was married, and they live fower hundred mile away from any voices but their own and the singing birds.’", "제가 그리했지요. 둘은 결혼했고, 지금은 사백 마일 안짝에 자기들 목소리와 지저귀는 새소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않는 곳에서 산답니다.\""]], [["‘Mrs. Gummidge?’ I suggested.", "\"거미지 아주머니는요?\" 내가 운을 떼었다."]], [["It was a pleasant key to touch, for Mr.", "이건 건드리기 좋은 화제였다."], ["Peggotty suddenly burst into a roar of laughter, and rubbed his hands up and down his legs, as he had been accustomed to do when he enjoyed himself in the long-shipwrecked boat.", "패거티 아저씨가 별안간 너털웃음을 터뜨리며, 그 오래전 난파선 보트에서 신이 날 때면 으레 그러던 버릇대로 두 손으로 다리를 위아래로 문질러 댔으니까."]], [["‘Would you believe it!’ he said.", "\"이걸 믿으시겠습니까!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Why, someun even made offer fur to marry her!", "\"아니, 글쎄, 그 아주머니한테까지 청혼하는 사람이 다 있었지 뭡니까!"], ["If a ship’s cook that was turning settler, Mas’r Davy, didn’t make offers fur to marry Missis Gummidge, I’m Gormed--and I can’t say no fairer than that!’", "식민지에 정착하려던 어느 배 요리사가 거미지 아주머니한테 청혼을 했더라니까요, 데이비 도련님, 이게 거짓말이면 제가 사람이 아닙니다요―이보다 더 정직하게는 말 못 하겠습니다!\""]], [["I never saw Agnes laugh so. This sudden ecstasy on the part of Mr.", "나는 애그니스가 그토록 웃는 모습을 본 적이 없다."], ["Peggotty was so delightful to her, that she could not leave off laughing; and the more she laughed the more she made me laugh, and the greater Mr. Peggotty’s ecstasy became, and the more he rubbed his legs.", "패거티 아저씨가 별안간 보인 이 황홀한 신명이 그녀에게 어찌나 즐거웠던지, 그녀는 도무지 웃음을 멈추질 못했다. 그녀가 웃으면 웃을수록 나도 따라 웃게 됐고, 그럴수록 패거티 아저씨의 신명은 더 커져서, 그는 더욱더 다리를 문질러 댔다."]], [["‘And what did Mrs. Gummidge say?’ I asked, when I was grave enough.", "\"그래서 거미지 아주머니가 뭐라고 합디까?\" 가까스로 웃음을 가다듬고서 내가 물었다."]], [["‘If you’ll believe me,’ returned Mr.", "\"믿으실는지 모르겠습니다만,\""], ["Peggotty, ‘Missis Gummidge, ‘stead of saying “thank you, I’m much obleeged to you, I ain’t a-going fur to change my condition at my time of life,”", "패거티 아저씨가 대답했다. \"거미지 아주머니가, '고맙수다, 정말 황송하우, 하지만 이 나이에 내 처지를 바꿀 생각은 없수'"], ["up’d with a bucket as was standing by, and laid it over that theer ship’s cook’s head ‘till he sung out fur help, and I went in and reskied of him.’", "하고 말하는 대신에, 곁에 놓여 있던 양동이를 집어 들더니 그 배 요리사 머리에 폭 씌워 버리지 뭡니까. 그 사람이 살려 달라고 고래고래 소리를 지르길래, 제가 들어가서 구해 줬지요.\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty burst into a great roar of laughter, and Agnes and I both kept him company.", "패거티 아저씨가 너털웃음을 터뜨렸고, 애그니스와 나도 함께 어울렸다."]], [["‘But I must say this, for the good creetur,’ he resumed, wiping his face, when we were quite exhausted; ‘she has been all she said she’d be to us, and more.", "\"그래도 이 착한 사람을 위해 이건 꼭 말해야겠습니다.\" 우리가 다 웃다 지쳤을 때, 그가 얼굴을 훔치며 다시 말을 이었다. \"그 아주머니는 저희한테 해 주겠다고 한 건 죄다, 아니 그 이상으로 해 줬어요."], ["She’s the willingest, the trewest, the honestest-helping woman, Mas’r Davy, as ever draw’d the breath of life.", "데이비 도련님, 그 아주머니는 이 세상에 숨 쉬고 사는 그 누구보다도 기꺼이 돕고, 더없이 미덥고, 더없이 진실되게 거드는 여자입니다요."], ["I have never know’d her to be lone and lorn, for a single minute, not even when the colony was all afore us, and we was new to it.", "식민지가 온통 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있고 저희가 거기 처음 발 디뎠을 때조차도, 저는 그 아주머니가 단 일 분이라도 '외롭고 쓸쓸하다'고 하는 걸 본 적이 없어요."], ["And thinking of the old ‘un is a thing she never done, I do assure you, since she left England!’", "그리고 '영감님' 생각일랑은, 정말이지, 잉글랜드를 떠난 뒤로는 한 번도 한 적이 없다니까요!\""]], [["‘Now, last, not least, Mr. Micawber,’ said I.", "\"자, 마지막으로, 그렇다고 결코 덜 중요한 건 아닌데, 미코버 씨는 어떻소?\""], ["‘He has paid off every obligation he incurred here--even to Traddles’s bill, you remember my dear Agnes--and therefore we may take it for granted that he is doing well.", "내가 말했다. \"그분은 여기서 진 빚을 죄다 갚았소―심지어 트래들스한테 진 어음까지도요, 내 사랑 애그니스, 당신도 기억하지요―그러니 그분이 잘 지내고 있으리라고 봐도 무방하겠지요."], ["But what is the latest news of him?’", "그래, 그분의 가장 최근 소식은 어떻소?\""]], [["Mr. Peggotty, with a smile, put his hand in his breast-pocket, and produced a flat-folded, paper parcel, from which he took out, with much care, a little odd-looking newspaper.", "패거티 아저씨가 빙긋 웃으며 가슴팍 안주머니에 손을 넣더니, 납작하게 접은 종이 꾸러미를 꺼냈다. 그러고는 거기서 아주 조심스럽게 야릇하게 생긴 작은 신문 한 장을 끄집어냈다."]], [["‘You are to understan’, Mas’r Davy,’ said he, ‘as we have left the Bush now, being so well to do; and have gone right away round to Port Middlebay Harbour, wheer theer’s what we call a town.’", "\"아셔야 할 게 있는데요, 데이비 도련님,\" 그가 말했다. \"저희가 형편이 워낙 좋아져서 이제 오지를 떠나, 곧장 돌아 나와 포트 미들베이 항구로 갔답니다. 거기는 저희가 '읍내'라고 부를 만한 데가 있거든요.\""]], [["‘Mr. Micawber was in the Bush near you?’ said I.", "\"미코버 씨가 아저씨네 근처 오지에 있었소?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["‘Bless you, yes,’ said Mr.", "\"그럼요, 그렇고말고요.\""], ["Peggotty, ‘and turned to with a will.", "패거티 아저씨가 말했다. \"그것도 아주 발 벗고 나섰지요."], ["I never wish to meet a better gen’l’man for turning to with a will.", "그분만큼 발 벗고 나서서 일하는 신사는 다시없을 겁니다."], ["I’ve seen that theer bald head of his a perspiring in the sun, Mas’r Davy, till I a’most thowt it would have melted away.", "데이비 도련님, 저는 그분의 그 벗어진 머리가 땡볕에 어찌나 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리던지, 그러다 녹아내리지나 않을까 싶었다니까요."], ["And now he’s a Magistrate.’", "그런데 이제 그분이 치안 판사랍니다.\""]], [["‘A Magistrate, eh?’ said I.", "\"치안 판사라고요, 정말?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Peggotty pointed to a certain paragraph in the newspaper, where I read aloud as follows, from the Port Middlebay Times:", "패거티 아저씨가 신문의 어느 한 단락을 가리켰고, 나는 거기 〈포트 미들베이 타임스〉에서 다음과 같이 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["‘The public dinner to our distinguished fellow-colonist and townsman, WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, Port Middlebay District Magistrate, came off yesterday in the large room of the Hotel, which was crowded to suffocation.", "\"우리의 저명한 동향 이주민이자 읍민이신, 포트 미들베이 지구 치안 판사 윌킨스 미코버 님을 위한 공개 만찬이 어제 호텔 대연회실에서 성대히 열렸으며, 연회실은 발 디딜 틈 없이 가득 찼다."], ["It is estimated that not fewer than forty-seven persons must have been accommodated with dinner at one time, exclusive of the company in the passage and on the stairs.", "복도와 계단에 늘어선 사람들을 제외하고도, 한자리에서 만찬을 든 인원이 무려 마흔일곱 명을 밑돌지 않았으리라 추산된다."], ["The beauty, fashion, and exclusiveness of Port Middlebay, flocked to do honour to one so deservedly esteemed, so highly talented, and so widely popular.", "포트 미들베이의 미모와 멋과 명사들이, 이토록 마땅히 존경받고, 이토록 높은 재능을 지녔으며, 이토록 두루 사랑받는 분께 경의를 표하고자 구름같이 모여들었다."], ["Doctor Mell (of Colonial Salem-House Grammar School, Port Middlebay) presided, and on his right sat the distinguished guest.", "멜 박사(포트 미들베이 식민지 세일럼하우스 문법 학교)께서 좌장을 맡으셨고, 그 오른편에 저명한 귀빈이 앉으셨다."], ["After the removal of the cloth, and the singing of Non Nobis (beautifully executed, and in which we were at no loss to distinguish the bell-like notes of that gifted amateur, WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, JUNIOR), the usual loyal and patriotic toasts were severally given and rapturously received.", "상보를 거두고 〈논 노비스〉 합창(아름답게 연주되었으며, 그 가운데서 재능 넘치는 아마추어 윌킨스 미코버 이세 님의 종소리 같은 음색을 분간하기란 어렵지 않았다)이 끝난 뒤, 으레 올리는 충성과 애국의 건배가 차례로 제창되어 열렬한 환호 속에 받아들여졌다."], ["Doctor Mell, in a speech replete with feeling, then proposed “Our distinguished Guest, the ornament of our town.", "이어 멜 박사께서 감정이 북받치는 연설로 이렇게 건배를 제안하셨다. '우리의 저명한 귀빈, 우리 읍의 자랑이신 분."], ["May he never leave us but to better himself, and may his success among us be such as to render his bettering himself impossible!”", "부디 더 나은 자리로 영전하시는 일 말고는 결코 우리 곁을 떠나지 마시며, 우리 가운데서 거두신 성공이 너무도 커서 더는 영전하실 수조차 없게 되기를!'"], ["The cheering with which the toast was received defies description.", "이 건배가 받은 환호는 이루 다 말로 옮길 수 없다."], ["Again and again it rose and fell, like the waves of ocean.", "그것은 마치 대양의 물결처럼 거듭거듭 솟구쳤다 잦아들었다."], ["At length all was hushed, and WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, presented himself to return thanks.", "마침내 모두가 잠잠해지자, 윌킨스 미코버 님께서 답사를 하시려 자리에서 일어나셨다."], ["Far be it from us, in the present comparatively imperfect state of the resources of our establishment, to endeavour to follow our distinguished townsman through the smoothly-flowing periods of his polished and highly-ornate address!", "우리 신문사의 자원이 비교적 아직 미비한 지금 형편으로는, 우리의 저명한 읍민이 펼치신 그 세련되고 더없이 화려한 답사의 유려하게 흐르는 문장들을 일일이 좇으려는 시도는 부디 삼가고자 한다!"], ["Suffice it to observe, that it was a masterpiece of eloquence; and that those passages in which he more particularly traced his own successful career to its source, and warned the younger portion of his auditory from the shoals of ever incurring pecuniary liabilities which they were unable to liquidate, brought a tear into the manliest eye present. The remaining toasts were DOCTOR MELL; Mrs.", "다만 이것이 웅변의 걸작이었다는 것, 그리고 특히 당신 자신의 성공적인 이력을 그 근원까지 거슬러 짚으시며, 청중 가운데 젊은 축에게 결코 갚을 수 없는 금전적 채무라는 암초에 빠지지 말라고 경계하신 대목들이, 그 자리에 있던 가장 사내다운 이의 눈에도 눈물이 맺히게 했다는 것만 일러 두면 족하리라."], ["MICAWBER (who gracefully bowed her acknowledgements from the side-door, where a galaxy of beauty was elevated on chairs, at once to witness and adorn the gratifying scene), Mrs. RIDGER BEGS (late Miss Micawber); Mrs. MELL; WILKINS MICAWBER, ESQUIRE, JUNIOR (who convulsed the assembly by humorously remarking that he found himself unable to return thanks in a speech, but would do so, with their permission, in a song); Mrs.", "나머지 건배는 멜 박사, 미코버 부인(미모의 여인들이 한 무리 의자 위에 올라서서 이 흐뭇한 광경을 지켜보는 동시에 그 자리를 빛내고 있던 옆문에서, 우아하게 고개 숙여 답례하셨다), 리저 베그스 부인(전 미코버 양), 멜 부인, 윌킨스 미코버 이세 님(연설로는 답례를 드릴 수 없으니, 허락해 주신다면 노래로 대신하겠노라고 익살스레 말하여 좌중을 웃음바다로 만드셨다), 미코버 부인 일가(모국에서 익히 알려진 분들임은 새삼 말할 필요도 없다) 등등을 위해 올려졌다."], ["MICAWBER’S FAMILY (well known, it is needless to remark, in the mother-country), &c. &c. &c.", "행사가 끝나자 식탁들이 마치 마법이라도 부린 듯 치워져 무도장이 마련되었다."], ["At the conclusion of the proceedings the tables were cleared as if by art-magic for dancing. Among the votaries of TERPSICHORE, who disported themselves until Sol gave warning for departure, Wilkins Micawber, Esquire, Junior, and the lovely and accomplished Miss Helena, fourth daughter of Doctor Mell, were particularly remarkable.’", "해님이 떠날 시간임을 알릴 때까지 흥겹게 춤판을 벌인 무도의 여신 테르프시코레의 숭배자들 가운데, 윌킨스 미코버 이세 님과 멜 박사의 넷째 따님이신 사랑스럽고 교양 넘치는 헬레나 양이 유독 돋보였다.\""]], [["I was looking back to the name of Doctor Mell, pleased to have discovered, in these happier circumstances, Mr. Mell, formerly poor pinched usher to my Middlesex magistrate, when Mr.", "나는 멜 박사라는 이름으로 눈길을 되돌리고 있었다. 한때 내 미들섹스 치안 판사 밑에서 박봉에 시달리던 가엾은 보조 교사였던 멜 선생이, 이렇게 더 행복한 처지에 놓인 것을 알게 되어 기뻤기 때문이다."], ["Peggotty pointing to another part of the paper, my eyes rested on my own name, and I read thus:", "그때 패거티 아저씨가 신문의 다른 부분을 가리켰고, 내 눈은 내 이름 위에 가 멈췄으며, 나는 다음과 같이 읽었다. \"데이비드 코퍼필드 님께,"]], [["‘My Dear Sir,", "저명한 작가이신 그분께."]], [["‘Years have elapsed, since I had an opportunity of ocularly perusing the lineaments, now familiar to the imaginations of a considerable portion of the civilized world.", "친애하는 선생께,\n문명 세계의 상당수 사람들이 이제 그 모습을 마음속에 익히 그려 보는, 그 이목구비를 제가 두 눈으로 직접 뜯어볼 기회를 가져 본 지도 여러 해가 흘렀습니다."]], [["‘But, my dear Sir, though estranged (by the force of circumstances over which I have had no control) from the personal society of the friend and companion of my youth, I have not been unmindful of his soaring flight. Nor have I been debarred,", "그러나 친애하는 선생, 비록 제가 어찌할 수 없는 여러 사정의 힘에 떠밀려 젊은 시절의 벗이자 동무와 직접 어울릴 기회로부터 멀어지긴 했으되, 저는 그가 드높이 날아오르는 모습을 한시도 잊은 적이 없습니다. 또한 저는,"]], [["Though seas between us braid ha’ roared,", "비록 우리 사이에 바닷물이 굽이쳐 포효할지라도,"]], [["(BURNS) from participating in the intellectual feasts he has spread before us.", "(번스) 그가 우리 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 지적 향연에 동참하는 일에서 가로막힌 적도 없었습니다."]], [["‘I cannot, therefore, allow of the departure from this place of an individual whom we mutually respect and esteem, without, my dear Sir, taking this public opportunity of thanking you, on my own behalf, and, I may undertake to add, on that of the whole of the Inhabitants of Port Middlebay, for the gratification of which you are the ministering agent.", "그러므로 저는, 우리가 서로 존경하고 흠모하는 한 인물이 이곳을 떠나는 마당에, 친애하는 선생, 이 공개적인 기회를 빌려 선생께 감사를 드리지 않을 수 없습니다. 저 자신을 대신하여, 그리고 감히 덧붙이건대 포트 미들베이 주민 전체를 대신하여, 선생이 그 기쁨을 베풀어 주시는 일꾼이신 데 대하여 말입니다."]], [["‘Go on, my dear Sir!", "계속 나아가소서, 친애하는 선생!"], ["You are not unknown here, you are not unappreciated.", "선생은 이곳에서 알려지지 않은 분이 아니며, 인정받지 못하는 분도 아닙니다."], ["Though “remote”, we are neither “unfriended”, “melancholy”, nor (I may add) “slow”.", "비록 '멀리 떨어져' 있으나, 우리는 '벗 없는' 처지도, '우울한'"], ["Go on, my dear Sir, in your Eagle course!", "처지도, (감히 덧붙이건대) '굼뜬' 처지도 아닙니다."], ["The inhabitants of Port Middlebay may at least aspire to watch it, with delight, with entertainment, with instruction!", "계속 나아가소서, 친애하는 선생, 그 독수리의 항로를 따라! 포트 미들베이 주민들은 적어도 그 비상을 우러러보기를 갈망할 수 있으니, 기쁨으로, 즐거움으로, 가르침으로 말입니다!"]], [["‘Among the eyes elevated towards you from this portion of the globe, will ever be found, while it has light and life,", "이 지구의 한구석에서 선생을 향해 우러러 들린 눈들 가운데에는, 그 눈이 빛과 생명을 지니는 한, 언제까지나\n윌킨스 미코버,"]], [["‘The ‘Eye ‘Appertaining to", "치안 판사에게\n속한"]], [["‘WILKINS MICAWBER, ‘Magistrate.’", "눈이\n있을 것입니다.\""]], [["I found, on glancing at the remaining contents of the newspaper, that Mr. Micawber was a diligent and esteemed correspondent of that journal.", "신문의 나머지 내용을 훑어보다가, 나는 미코버 씨가 그 신문의 부지런하고 존경받는 기고가라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["There was another letter from him in the same paper, touching a bridge; there was an advertisement of a collection of similar letters by him, to be shortly republished, in a neat volume, ‘with considerable additions’; and, unless I am very much mistaken, the Leading Article was his also.", "같은 신문에 다리를 다룬 그의 또 다른 편지가 실려 있었고, 머지않아 '상당한 보충을 더하여' 산뜻한 한 권으로 재출간될 예정인 그의 비슷한 편지 모음집 광고도 있었으며, 내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면 사설마저 그의 글이었다."]], [["We talked much of Mr. Micawber, on many other evenings while Mr. Peggotty remained with us.", "패거티 아저씨가 우리와 함께 머무는 동안, 우리는 여러 다른 저녁에도 미코버 씨 이야기를 많이 나누었다."], ["He lived with us during the whole term of his stay,--which, I think, was something less than a month,--and his sister and my aunt came to London to see him.", "그는 머무는 내내 우리와 함께 지냈는데―내 생각엔 한 달이 채 안 되었던 것 같다―그의 누이와 내 고모님도 그를 보러 런던으로 왔다."], ["Agnes and I parted from him aboard-ship, when he sailed; and we shall never part from him more, on earth.", "애그니스와 나는 그가 배를 타고 떠날 때 선상에서 그와 작별했다. 그리고 우리는 이 세상에서 다시는 그와 헤어지지 않을 것이다."]], [["But before he left, he went with me to Yarmouth, to see a little tablet I had put up in the churchyard to the memory of Ham.", "그러나 떠나기 전에, 그는 나와 함께 야머스로 가서, 내가 햄을 기리려고 교회 묘지에 세워 둔 작은 비석을 보았다."], ["While I was copying the plain inscription for him at his request, I saw him stoop, and gather a tuft of grass from the grave and a little earth.", "그의 청에 따라 그 수수한 비문을 베껴 적고 있는데, 나는 그가 몸을 숙여 무덤에서 풀 한 줌과 흙 한 줌을 뜨는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘For Em’ly,’ he said, as he put it in his breast.", "\"에밀리한테 주려고요.\" 그가 그것을 가슴팍에 품으며 말했다."], ["‘I promised, Mas’r Davy.’", "\"약속했거든요, 데이비 도련님.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 64. A LAST RETROSPECT", "# CHAPTER 64. A LAST RETROSPECT"]], [["And now my written story ends.", "이제 내가 쓴 이야기는 끝난다."], ["I look back, once more--for the last time--before I close these leaves.", "나는 다시 한번 — 마지막으로 — 이 책장을 덮기 전에 뒤를 돌아본다."]], [["I see myself, with Agnes at my side, journeying along the road of life.", "나는 애그니스를 곁에 두고 인생의 길을 걸어가는 나 자신을 본다."], ["I see our children and our friends around us; and I hear the roar of many voices, not indifferent to me as I travel on.", "우리를 둘러싼 우리 아이들과 우리 친구들이 보인다. 그리고 길을 걸어가는 나에게 결코 무심하지 않은 수많은 목소리의 울림이 들린다."]], [["What faces are the most distinct to me in the fleeting crowd?", "스쳐 지나가는 무리 가운데 나에게 가장 또렷한 얼굴은 누구일까? 보라, 바로 이 얼굴들이다."], ["Lo, these; all turning to me as I ask my thoughts the question!", "내가 내 생각에게 그 물음을 던지는 순간, 모두가 나를 향해 돌아선다!"]], [["Here is my aunt, in stronger spectacles, an old woman of four-score years and more, but upright yet, and a steady walker of six miles at a stretch in winter weather.", "여기 우리 고모님이 계신다. 도수 높은 안경을 끼고, 여든 살이 넘은 노부인이 되었으되, 여전히 허리가 꼿꼿하고, 한겨울 날씨에도 한 번에 육 마일을 거뜬히 걷는 든든한 보행자다."]], [["Always with her, here comes Peggotty, my good old nurse, likewise in spectacles, accustomed to do needle-work at night very close to the lamp, but never sitting down to it without a bit of wax candle, a yard-measure in a little house, and a work-box with a picture of St. Paul’s upon the lid.", "언제나 고모님과 함께, 여기 패거티가 온다. 내 착하고 나이 든 유모도 마찬가지로 안경을 끼고, 밤이면 등불에 바짝 붙어 바느질하는 버릇이 들었는데, 그러면서도 밀랍 양초 한 토막과, 작은 집 모양 자와, 뚜껑에 성 바울 대성당 그림이 그려진 반짇고리 없이는 결코 자리에 앉는 법이 없다."]], [["The cheeks and arms of Peggotty, so hard and red in my childish days, when I wondered why the birds didn’t peck her in preference to apples, are shrivelled now; and her eyes, that used to darken their whole neighbourhood in her face, are fainter (though they glitter still); but her rough forefinger, which I once associated with a pocket nutmeg-grater, is just the same, and when I see my least child catching at it as it totters from my aunt to her, I think of our little parlour at home, when I could scarcely walk.", "어린 시절 내가 새들이 사과 대신 왜 저 뺨과 팔을 쪼지 않을까 궁금해했을 만큼 단단하고 붉던 패거티의 뺨과 팔은 이제 쪼글쪼글해졌다. 그리고 한때 얼굴에서 그 둘레를 온통 어둡게 물들이던 두 눈도 빛이 흐려졌다(그래도 여전히 반짝거리기는 한다). 하지만 옛날에 내가 호주머니 속 육두구 강판을 떠올리게 했던 그 거친 집게손가락만큼은 조금도 변하지 않았다."], ["My aunt’s old disappointment is set right, now.", "우리 막내가 고모님에게서 패거티에게로 비틀비틀 걸어가며 그 손가락을 붙잡으려고 하는 모습을 볼 때면, 나는 내가 겨우 걷기 시작했던 시절, 우리 집 그 작은 거실을 떠올린다."], ["She is godmother to a real living Betsey Trotwood; and Dora (the next in order) says she spoils her.", "고모님의 오랜 실망은 이제 바로잡혔다. 고모님은 살아 숨 쉬는 진짜 베시 트롯우드의 대모가 되었으니까. 그리고 (그다음 차례인) 도라는 고모님이 그 아이를 버릇없게 키운다고 말한다."]], [["There is something bulky in Peggotty’s pocket.", "패거티의 호주머니에는 뭔가 불룩한 것이 들어 있다."], ["It is nothing smaller than the Crocodile Book, which is in rather a dilapidated condition by this time, with divers of the leaves torn and stitched across, but which Peggotty exhibits to the children as a precious relic.", "그것은 다름 아닌 악어 책인데, 이제는 책장이 여기저기 찢기고 다시 꿰매져 제법 너덜너덜해진 상태지만, 패거티는 그것을 귀한 유물처럼 아이들에게 보여 준다."], ["I find it very curious to see my own infant face, looking up at me from the Crocodile stories; and to be reminded by it of my old acquaintance Brooks of Sheffield.", "악어 이야기 속에서 나를 올려다보는 내 어린 시절의 얼굴을 보는 일도, 그것을 통해 내 오랜 지인인 셰필드의 브룩스 씨를 떠올리게 되는 일도, 나에게는 참으로 신기하게 느껴진다."]], [["Among my boys, this summer holiday time, I see an old man making giant kites, and gazing at them in the air, with a delight for which there are no words.", "이번 여름 방학 동안, 내 아들들 틈에서, 나는 거대한 연을 만들어 그것이 공중에 뜬 것을 바라보며, 말로 다할 수 없는 기쁨에 젖어 있는 한 노인을 본다. 그는 황홀하게 나를 맞이하더니, 연신 고개를 끄덕이고 눈을 찡긋거리며 속삭인다."], ["He greets me rapturously, and whispers, with many nods and winks, ‘Trotwood, you will be glad to hear that I shall finish the Memorial when I have nothing else to do, and that your aunt’s the most extraordinary woman in the world, sir!’", "\"트롯우드, 자네 들으면 기뻐할 걸세. 달리 할 일이 없을 때 진정서를 마저 끝낼 작정이라네. 그리고 자네 고모님은 세상에서 가장 비범한 여인이시지, 선생!\""]], [["Who is this bent lady, supporting herself by a stick, and showing me a countenance in which there are some traces of old pride and beauty, feebly contending with a querulous, imbecile, fretful wandering of the mind?", "지팡이에 의지해 몸을 가누고, 옛날의 자존심과 아름다움이 어렴풋이 남은 얼굴을, 투덜대고 멍하니 안달하며 떠도는 마음과 힘없이 싸우게 하는, 저 허리 굽은 부인은 누구일까? 그녀는 어느 정원에 있다."], ["She is in a garden; and near her stands a sharp, dark, withered woman, with a white scar on her lip. Let me hear what they say.", "그리고 그 곁에는 입술에 하얀 흉터가 있는, 날카롭고 가무잡잡하며 시든 여인이 서 있다. 두 사람이 무슨 말을 나누는지 들어 보자."]], [["‘Rosa, I have forgotten this gentleman’s name.’", "\"로자, 이 신사분 이름을 깜빡했구나.\""]], [["Rosa bends over her, and calls to her, ‘Mr. Copperfield.’", "로자가 그녀에게 몸을 굽혀 외친다. \"코퍼필드 씨예요.\""]], [["‘I am glad to see you, sir.", "\"만나서 반가워요, 선생."], ["I am sorry to observe you are in mourning. I hope Time will be good to you.’", "상복을 입으셨군요, 안됐어요. 부디 세월이 당신에게 너그럽기를 바라요.\""]], [["Her impatient attendant scolds her, tells her I am not in mourning, bids her look again, tries to rouse her.", "성마른 시중꾼이 그녀를 나무라며, 내가 상복을 입은 게 아니라고 일러 주고, 다시 보라고 채근하며, 정신을 차리게 하려 애쓴다."]], [["‘You have seen my son, sir,’ says the elder lady.", "\"내 아들을 만나 봤지요, 선생.\" 노부인이 말한다."], ["‘Are you reconciled?’", "\"둘이 화해했나요?\""]], [["Looking fixedly at me, she puts her hand to her forehead, and moans.", "그녀는 나를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 한 손을 이마에 갖다 대고 신음한다."], ["Suddenly, she cries, in a terrible voice, ‘Rosa, come to me.", "그러더니 갑자기 끔찍한 목소리로 외친다. \"로자, 이리 와. 그 애가 죽었어!\""], ["He is dead!’ Rosa kneeling at her feet, by turns caresses her, and quarrels with her; now fiercely telling her, ‘I loved him better than you ever did!’--now soothing her to sleep on her breast, like a sick child.", "로자는 그녀의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고, 어르다가 다투기를 번갈아 한다. 어느 때는 사납게, \"내가 당신보다 그분을 더 사랑했어요!\"라고 쏘아붙이고 — 또 어느 때는 아픈 아이를 달래듯 자기 가슴에 기대어 잠들게 어른다. 나는 그렇게 두 사람을 두고 떠난다."], ["Thus I leave them; thus I always find them; thus they wear their time away, from year to year.", "언제 가도 두 사람은 그 모습 그대로다. 그렇게 두 사람은 해가 가고 또 가도록 세월을 흘려보낸다."]], [["What ship comes sailing home from India, and what English lady is this, married to a growling old Scotch Croesus with great flaps of ears?", "인도에서 본국을 향해 돛을 올리고 돌아오는 저 배는 무슨 배이며, 커다란 귀가 축 늘어진, 으르렁대는 늙은 스코틀랜드 부자에게 시집간 저 영국 부인은 누구일까?"], ["Can this be Julia Mills?", "설마 줄리아 밀스일까?"]], [["Indeed it is Julia Mills, peevish and fine, with a black man to carry cards and letters to her on a golden salver, and a copper-coloured woman in linen, with a bright handkerchief round her head, to serve her Tiffin in her dressing-room.", "과연 줄리아 밀스다. 짜증스럽고 우아하게 차려입고, 황금 쟁반에 명함과 편지를 받쳐 나르는 흑인 하인과, 머리에 화사한 손수건을 두르고 아마포 옷을 걸친 채 화장실에서 점심 식사를 시중드는 구릿빛 여인을 거느리고 있다."], ["But Julia keeps no diary in these days; never sings Affection’s Dirge; eternally quarrels with the old Scotch Croesus, who is a sort of yellow bear with a tanned hide.", "그러나 줄리아는 요즘 일기를 쓰지 않는다. '애정의 만가'도 결코 부르지 않는다. 햇볕에 그은 가죽을 뒤집어쓴 누런 곰 같은 그 늙은 스코틀랜드 부자와 끊임없이 다툰다."], ["Julia is steeped in money to the throat, and talks and thinks of nothing else.", "줄리아는 목까지 돈에 푹 잠겨, 오직 돈 이야기만 하고 돈 생각만 한다."], ["I liked her better in the Desert of Sahara.", "나는 사하라 사막 시절의 그녀가 더 좋았다."]], [["Or perhaps this IS the Desert of Sahara!", "어쩌면 이곳이 바로 사하라 사막일지도 모른다!"], ["For, though Julia has a stately house, and mighty company, and sumptuous dinners every day, I see no green growth near her; nothing that can ever come to fruit or flower.", "줄리아에게 으리으리한 저택과, 막강한 손님들과, 날마다 호화로운 만찬이 있다 한들, 나는 그녀 곁에서 푸르게 자라는 것을 보지 못하니까. 열매를 맺거나 꽃을 피울 만한 것이라곤 하나도 없다."], ["What Julia calls ‘society’, I see; among it Mr.", "줄리아가 '사교계'라 부르는 것이 내 눈에 들어온다."], ["Jack Maldon, from his Patent Place, sneering at the hand that gave it him, and speaking to me of the Doctor as ‘so charmingly antique’.", "그 가운데에는 자기 특권직에 올라앉아 그 자리를 마련해 준 손을 비웃으며, 나에게 박사님을 두고 \"참으로 매력적으로 고풍스럽다\"고 말하는 잭 몰던 씨가 있다."], ["But when society is the name for such hollow gentlemen and ladies, Julia, and when its breeding is professed indifference to everything that can advance or can retard mankind, I think we must have lost ourselves in that same Desert of Sahara, and had better find the way out.", "그러나 줄리아여, 사교계라는 것이 그처럼 속 빈 신사 숙녀를 가리키는 이름이고, 그 교양이라는 것이 인류를 앞으로 나아가게 하거나 뒤로 물러서게 할 그 어떤 것에도 짐짓 무관심한 태도를 뜻한다면, 우리는 바로 그 사하라 사막에서 길을 잃은 게 틀림없으니, 어서 빠져나갈 길을 찾는 편이 나으리라."]], [["And lo, the Doctor, always our good friend, labouring at his Dictionary (somewhere about the letter D), and happy in his home and wife.", "그리고 보라, 박사님이 계신다. 언제나 우리의 좋은 벗인 그분은, 사전 편찬에 매달려(어디쯤인가 디(D) 자 언저리에 와 있다) 자기 집과 아내 속에서 행복하다."], ["Also the Old Soldier, on a considerably reduced footing, and by no means so influential as in days of yore!", "그리고 옛 군인 부인도 계신데, 형편이 상당히 쪼그라들었고, 지난날과 달리 이제는 결코 그리 큰 영향력을 부리지 못한다!"]], [["Working at his chambers in the Temple, with a busy aspect, and his hair (where he is not bald) made more rebellious than ever by the constant friction of his lawyer’s-wig, I come, in a later time, upon my dear old Traddles.", "법조 거리의 사무실에서 분주한 모습으로 일하고, (대머리가 아닌 부분의) 머리카락은 법조인 가발에 끊임없이 쓸려 그 어느 때보다 더 제멋대로 뻗친 채인, 내 정다운 옛 친구 트래들스를 나는 훗날 다시 만난다."], ["His table is covered with thick piles of papers; and I say, as I look around me:", "그의 책상은 두툼한 서류 더미로 뒤덮여 있다. 나는 둘레를 둘러보며 말한다."]], [["‘If Sophy were your clerk, now, Traddles, she would have enough to do!’", "\"이보게 트래들스, 지금 소피가 자네 서기로 일했다면, 손이 모자라 쩔쩔맸겠는걸!\""]], [["‘You may say that, my dear Copperfield!", "\"그렇게 말해도 되겠네, 사랑하는 코퍼필드!"], ["But those were capital days, too, in Holborn Court!", "하지만 홀번 코트 시절도 더없이 좋은 날들이었지!"], ["Were they not?’", "안 그런가?\""]], [["‘When she told you you would be a judge?", "\"소피가 자네더러 언젠가 판사가 될 거라고 말하던 그 시절 말인가?"], ["But it was not the town talk then!’", "하지만 그땐 온 동네에 소문난 얘기는 아니었지!\""]], [["‘At all events,’ says Traddles, ‘if I ever am one--’ ‘Why, you know you will be.’", "\"어쨌든,\" 트래들스가 말한다. \"내가 정말 판사가 된다면—\" \"이런, 자네도 알잖나, 틀림없이 될 거라는 걸.\""]], [["‘Well, my dear Copperfield, WHEN I am one, I shall tell the story, as I said I would.’", "\"좋아, 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 내가 판사가 되면, 전에 그러겠다고 했듯이, 그 이야기를 들려주겠네.\""]], [["We walk away, arm in arm.", "우리는 팔짱을 끼고 걸어간다."], ["I am going to have a family dinner with Traddles.", "나는 트래들스 집에서 가족 만찬을 함께 할 참이다."], ["It is Sophy’s birthday; and, on our road, Traddles discourses to me of the good fortune he has enjoyed.", "오늘은 소피의 생일이다. 그리고 가는 길에 트래들스는 자기가 누려 온 행운에 대해 나에게 이야기를 풀어놓는다."]], [["‘I really have been able, my dear Copperfield, to do all that I had most at heart.", "\"정말이지, 사랑하는 코퍼필드, 나는 내가 마음속에 가장 간절히 바라던 일을 다 이룰 수 있었다네."], ["There’s the Reverend Horace promoted to that living at four hundred and fifty pounds a year; there are our two boys receiving the very best education, and distinguishing themselves as steady scholars and good fellows; there are three of the girls married very comfortably; there are three more living with us; there are three more keeping house for the Reverend Horace since Mrs.", "호러스 목사님은 연봉 사백오십 파운드짜리 그 성직록 자리로 승진하셨고, 우리 집 두 아들은 더없이 훌륭한 교육을 받으며 착실한 학생이자 좋은 청년으로 두각을 나타내고 있고, 딸아이 셋은 아주 편안하게 시집을 갔고, 셋은 우리와 함께 살고, 또 셋은 크루얼러 부인이 돌아가신 뒤로 호러스 목사님 댁 살림을 맡고 있다네."], ["Crewler’s decease; and all of them happy.’", "그리고 모두가 행복하지.\""]], [["‘Except--’ I suggest.", "\"다만—\" 내가 슬쩍 운을 뗀다."]], [["‘Except the Beauty,’ says Traddles. ‘Yes.", "\"미인 아가씨만 빼고 말이지.\" 트래들스가 말한다. \"그래."], ["It was very unfortunate that she should marry such a vagabond.", "그 애가 그런 떠돌이 같은 자와 결혼한 건 참으로 불운한 일이었어."], ["But there was a certain dash and glare about him that caught her.", "하지만 그자에게는 그 애를 사로잡은 어떤 호기로움과 번지르르함이 있었지."], ["However, now we have got her safe at our house, and got rid of him, we must cheer her up again.’", "그래도 이제는 그 애를 무사히 우리 집으로 데려왔고, 그자도 떨쳐 냈으니, 다시 기운을 북돋워 줘야지.\""]], [["Traddles’s house is one of the very houses--or it easily may have been--which he and Sophy used to parcel out, in their evening walks.", "트래들스의 집은, 저녁 산책길에 그와 소피가 이러쿵저러쿵 나눠 가지곤 하던 바로 그런 집 가운데 하나다 — 아니, 어쩌면 정말 그 집일 수도 있다."], ["It is a large house; but Traddles keeps his papers in his dressing-room and his boots with his papers; and he and Sophy squeeze themselves into upper rooms, reserving the best bedrooms for the Beauty and the girls.", "큰 집이다. 그러나 트래들스는 서류를 자기 화장실에 두고, 장화도 그 서류와 함께 둔다. 그리고 그와 소피는 제일 좋은 침실들을 미인 아가씨와 딸아이들에게 내주고, 자기들은 위층 방으로 비집고 들어간다."], ["There is no room to spare in the house; for more of ‘the girls’ are here, and always are here, by some accident or other, than I know how to count.", "이 집에는 남는 방이 없다. 이런저런 까닭으로 내가 셀 수 없을 만큼 많은 '딸아이들'이 여기 와 있고, 늘 여기 있기 때문이다."], ["Here, when we go in, is a crowd of them, running down to the door, and handing Traddles about to be kissed, until he is out of breath.", "우리가 안으로 들어서면, 한 무리의 아이들이 문간으로 우르르 달려 나와, 트래들스가 숨이 찰 때까지 이리저리 끌고 다니며 입을 맞추게 한다."], ["Here, established in perpetuity, is the poor Beauty, a widow with a little girl; here, at dinner on Sophy’s birthday, are the three married girls with their three husbands, and one of the husband’s brothers, and another husband’s cousin, and another husband’s sister, who appears to me to be engaged to the cousin.", "여기, 영영 눌러앉은 가엾은 미인 아가씨가 있는데, 어린 딸을 둔 과부다. 여기, 소피의 생일 만찬 자리에는, 시집간 딸 셋이 저마다 남편을 데리고 와 있고, 그 남편 가운데 하나의 동생과, 또 다른 남편의 사촌과, 또 다른 남편의 누이가 있는데, 그 누이는 아무래도 그 사촌과 약혼한 사이인 듯하다."], ["Traddles, exactly the same simple, unaffected fellow as he ever was, sits at the foot of the large table like a Patriarch; and Sophy beams upon him, from the head, across a cheerful space that is certainly not glittering with Britannia metal.", "트래들스는, 옛날 그대로 한결같이 소박하고 꾸밈없는 사람이라, 큰 식탁 끄트머리에 족장처럼 앉아 있다. 그리고 소피는 식탁 윗자리에서, 결코 영국제 합금 그릇 따위로 번쩍이지는 않는 그 정겨운 공간 너머로 그를 향해 환하게 웃음을 보낸다. * 영국제 합금: 주석에 안티몬과 구리를 섞어 만든 값싼 식기용 금속."]], [["And now, as I close my task, subduing my desire to linger yet, these faces fade away.", "이제 나는 내 일을 마무리하며, 아직도 더 머물고 싶은 마음을 억누른다. 그러자 이 얼굴들은 차츰 희미해진다."], ["But one face, shining on me like a Heavenly light by which I see all other objects, is above them and beyond them all.", "그러나 한 얼굴만은, 내가 다른 모든 것을 보게 해 주는 천상의 빛처럼 나를 비추며, 그 모든 얼굴 위에, 그 모든 얼굴 너머에 있다."], ["And that remains.", "그리고 그 얼굴은 남는다."]], [["I turn my head, and see it, in its beautiful serenity, beside me.", "나는 고개를 돌려, 그 아름다운 평온 속에 깃든 그 얼굴이 내 곁에 있는 것을 본다."]], [["My lamp burns low, and I have written far into the night; but the dear presence, without which I were nothing, bears me company.", "내 등불은 가물가물 타들어 가고, 나는 밤이 깊도록 글을 써 왔다. 그러나 그 사람 없이는 내가 아무것도 아닐, 그 정다운 존재가 내 곁을 지켜 준다."]], [["O Agnes, O my soul, so may thy face be by me when I close my life indeed; so may I, when realities are melting from me, like the shadows which I now dismiss, still find thee near me, pointing upward!", "오 애그니스, 오 내 영혼이여, 내가 정말로 내 삶을 마감하는 그날에도 부디 그대의 얼굴이 내 곁에 있기를. 지금 내가 떨쳐 보내는 저 그림자들처럼 현실이 내게서 스러져 갈 때에도, 부디 내가 위를 가리키며 내 곁에 선 그대를 여전히 찾을 수 있기를!"]]];
